if(typeof BODIES_ORIG!=="undefined") BODIES_ORIG["gb-1399"]="Happy families are all alike; every unhappy family is unhappy in its own way.\n\nEverything was in confusion in the Oblonskys’ house. The wife had discovered that the husband was carrying on an intrigue with a French girl, who had been a governess in their family, and she had announced to her husband that she could not go on living in the same house with him. This position of affairs had now lasted three days, and not only the husband and wife themselves, but all the members of their family and household, were painfully conscious of it. Every person in the house felt that there was no sense in their living together, and that the stray people brought together by chance in any inn had more in common with one another than they, the members of the family and household of the Oblonskys. The wife did not leave her own room, the husband had not been at home for three days. The children ran wild all over the house; the English governess quarreled with the housekeeper, and wrote to a friend asking her to look out for a new situation for her; the man-cook had walked off the day before just at dinner time; the kitchen-maid, and the coachman had given warning.\n\nThree days after the quarrel, Prince Stepan Arkadyevitch Oblonsky—Stiva, as he was called in the fashionable world—woke up at his usual hour, that is, at eight o’clock in the morning, not in his wife’s bedroom, but on the leather-covered sofa in his study. He turned over his stout, well-cared-for person on the springy sofa, as though he would sink into a long sleep again; he vigorously embraced the pillow on the other side and buried his face in it; but all at once he jumped up, sat up on the sofa, and opened his eyes.\n\n“Yes, yes, how was it now?” he thought, going over his dream. “Now, how was it? To be sure! Alabin was giving a dinner at Darmstadt; no, not Darmstadt, but something American. Yes, but then, Darmstadt was in America. Yes, Alabin was giving a dinner on glass tables, and the tables sang, Il mio tesoro—not Il mio tesoro though, but something better, and there were some sort of little decanters on the table, and they were women, too,” he remembered.\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch’s eyes twinkled gaily, and he pondered with a smile. “Yes, it was nice, very nice. There was a great deal more that was delightful, only there’s no putting it into words, or even expressing it in one’s thoughts awake.” And noticing a gleam of light peeping in beside one of the serge curtains, he cheerfully dropped his feet over the edge of the sofa, and felt about with them for his slippers, a present on his last birthday, worked for him by his wife on gold-colored morocco. And, as he had done every day for the last nine years, he stretched out his hand, without getting up, towards the place where his dressing-gown always hung in his bedroom. And thereupon he suddenly remembered that he was not sleeping in his wife’s room, but in his study, and why: the smile vanished from his face, he knitted his brows.\n\n“Ah, ah, ah! Oo!...” he muttered, recalling everything that had happened. And again every detail of his quarrel with his wife was present to his imagination, all the hopelessness of his position, and worst of all, his own fault.\n\n“Yes, she won’t forgive me, and she can’t forgive me. And the most awful thing about it is that it’s all my fault—all my fault, though I’m not to blame. That’s the point of the whole situation,” he reflected. “Oh, oh, oh!” he kept repeating in despair, as he remembered the acutely painful sensations caused him by this quarrel.\n\nMost unpleasant of all was the first minute when, on coming, happy and good-humored, from the theater, with a huge pear in his hand for his wife, he had not found his wife in the drawing-room, to his surprise had not found her in the study either, and saw her at last in her bedroom with the unlucky letter that revealed everything in her hand.\n\nShe, his Dolly, forever fussing and worrying over household details, and limited in her ideas, as he considered, was sitting perfectly still with the letter in her hand, looking at him with an expression of horror, despair, and indignation.\n\n“What’s this? this?” she asked, pointing to the letter.\n\nAnd at this recollection, Stepan Arkadyevitch, as is so often the case, was not so much annoyed at the fact itself as at the way in which he had met his wife’s words.\n\nThere happened to him at that instant what does happen to people when they are unexpectedly caught in something very disgraceful. He did not succeed in adapting his face to the position in which he was placed towards his wife by the discovery of his fault. Instead of being hurt, denying, defending himself, begging forgiveness, instead of remaining indifferent even—anything would have been better than what he did do—his face utterly involuntarily (reflex spinal action, reflected Stepan Arkadyevitch, who was fond of physiology)—utterly involuntarily assumed its habitual, good-humored, and therefore idiotic smile.\n\nThis idiotic smile he could not forgive himself. Catching sight of that smile, Dolly shuddered as though at physical pain, broke out with her characteristic heat into a flood of cruel words, and rushed out of the room. Since then she had refused to see her husband.\n\n“It’s that idiotic smile that’s to blame for it all,” thought Stepan Arkadyevitch.\n\n“But what’s to be done? What’s to be done?” he said to himself in despair, and found no answer.\n\n# Chapter 2\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch was a truthful man in his relations with himself. He was incapable of deceiving himself and persuading himself that he repented of his conduct. He could not at this date repent of the fact that he, a handsome, susceptible man of thirty-four, was not in love with his wife, the mother of five living and two dead children, and only a year younger than himself. All he repented of was that he had not succeeded better in hiding it from his wife. But he felt all the difficulty of his position and was sorry for his wife, his children, and himself. Possibly he might have managed to conceal his sins better from his wife if he had anticipated that the knowledge of them would have had such an effect on her. He had never clearly thought out the subject, but he had vaguely conceived that his wife must long ago have suspected him of being unfaithful to her, and shut her eyes to the fact. He had even supposed that she, a worn-out woman no longer young or good-looking, and in no way remarkable or interesting, merely a good mother, ought from a sense of fairness to take an indulgent view. It had turned out quite the other way.\n\n“Oh, it’s awful! oh dear, oh dear! awful!” Stepan Arkadyevitch kept repeating to himself, and he could think of nothing to be done. “And how well things were going up till now! how well we got on! She was contented and happy in her children; I never interfered with her in anything; I let her manage the children and the house just as she liked. It’s true it’s bad her having been a governess in our house. That’s bad! There’s something common, vulgar, in flirting with one’s governess. But what a governess!” (He vividly recalled the roguish black eyes of Mlle. Roland and her smile.) “But after all, while she was in the house, I kept myself in hand. And the worst of it all is that she’s already ... it seems as if ill-luck would have it so! Oh, oh! But what, what is to be done?”\n\nThere was no solution, but that universal solution which life gives to all questions, even the most complex and insoluble. That answer is: one must live in the needs of the day—that is, forget oneself. To forget himself in sleep was impossible now, at least till nighttime; he could not go back now to the music sung by the decanter-women; so he must forget himself in the dream of daily life.\n\n“Then we shall see,” Stepan Arkadyevitch said to himself, and getting up he put on a gray dressing-gown lined with blue silk, tied the tassels in a knot, and, drawing a deep breath of air into his broad, bare chest, he walked to the window with his usual confident step, turning out his feet that carried his full frame so easily. He pulled up the blind and rang the bell loudly. It was at once answered by the appearance of an old friend, his valet, Matvey, carrying his clothes, his boots, and a telegram. Matvey was followed by the barber with all the necessaries for shaving.\n\n“Are there any papers from the office?” asked Stepan Arkadyevitch, taking the telegram and seating himself at the looking-glass.\n\n“On the table,” replied Matvey, glancing with inquiring sympathy at his master; and, after a short pause, he added with a sly smile, “They’ve sent from the carriage-jobbers.”\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch made no reply, he merely glanced at Matvey in the looking-glass. In the glance, in which their eyes met in the looking-glass, it was clear that they understood one another. Stepan Arkadyevitch’s eyes asked: “Why do you tell me that? don’t you know?”\n\nMatvey put his hands in his jacket pockets, thrust out one leg, and gazed silently, good-humoredly, with a faint smile, at his master.\n\n“I told them to come on Sunday, and till then not to trouble you or themselves for nothing,” he said. He had obviously prepared the sentence beforehand.\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch saw Matvey wanted to make a joke and attract attention to himself. Tearing open the telegram, he read it through, guessing at the words, misspelt as they always are in telegrams, and his face brightened.\n\n“Matvey, my sister Anna Arkadyevna will be here tomorrow,” he said, checking for a minute the sleek, plump hand of the barber, cutting a pink path through his long, curly whiskers.\n\n“Thank God!” said Matvey, showing by this response that he, like his master, realized the significance of this arrival—that is, that Anna Arkadyevna, the sister he was so fond of, might bring about a reconciliation between husband and wife.\n\n“Alone, or with her husband?” inquired Matvey.\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch could not answer, as the barber was at work on his upper lip, and he raised one finger. Matvey nodded at the looking-glass.\n\n“Alone. Is the room to be got ready upstairs?”\n\n“Inform Darya Alexandrovna: where she orders.”\n\n“Darya Alexandrovna?” Matvey repeated, as though in doubt.\n\n“Yes, inform her. Here, take the telegram; give it to her, and then do what she tells you.”\n\n“You want to try it on,” Matvey understood, but he only said, “Yes, sir.”\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch was already washed and combed and ready to be dressed, when Matvey, stepping deliberately in his creaky boots, came back into the room with the telegram in his hand. The barber had gone.\n\n“Darya Alexandrovna told me to inform you that she is going away. Let him do—that is you—as he likes,” he said, laughing only with his eyes, and putting his hands in his pockets, he watched his master with his head on one side. Stepan Arkadyevitch was silent a minute. Then a good-humored and rather pitiful smile showed itself on his handsome face.\n\n“Eh, Matvey?” he said, shaking his head.\n\n“It’s all right, sir; she will come round,” said Matvey.\n\n“Come round?”\n\n“Yes, sir.”\n\n“Do you think so? Who’s there?” asked Stepan Arkadyevitch, hearing the rustle of a woman’s dress at the door.\n\n“It’s I,” said a firm, pleasant, woman’s voice, and the stern, pockmarked face of Matrona Philimonovna, the nurse, was thrust in at the doorway.\n\n“Well, what is it, Matrona?” queried Stepan Arkadyevitch, going up to her at the door.\n\nAlthough Stepan Arkadyevitch was completely in the wrong as regards his wife, and was conscious of this himself, almost everyone in the house (even the nurse, Darya Alexandrovna’s chief ally) was on his side.\n\n“Well, what now?” he asked disconsolately.\n\n“Go to her, sir; own your fault again. Maybe God will aid you. She is suffering so, it’s sad to see her; and besides, everything in the house is topsy-turvy. You must have pity, sir, on the children. Beg her forgiveness, sir. There’s no help for it! One must take the consequences....”\n\n“But she won’t see me.”\n“You do your part. God is merciful; pray to God, sir, pray to God.”\n\n“Come, that’ll do, you can go,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, blushing suddenly. “Well now, do dress me.” He turned to Matvey and threw off his dressing-gown decisively.\n\nMatvey was already holding up the shirt like a horse’s collar, and, blowing off some invisible speck, he slipped it with obvious pleasure over the well-groomed body of his master.\n\n# Chapter 3\n\nWhen he was dressed, Stepan Arkadyevitch sprinkled some scent on himself, pulled down his shirt-cuffs, distributed into his pockets his cigarettes, pocketbook, matches, and watch with its double chain and seals, and shaking out his handkerchief, feeling himself clean, fragrant, healthy, and physically at ease, in spite of his unhappiness, he walked with a slight swing on each leg into the dining-room, where coffee was already waiting for him, and beside the coffee, letters and papers from the office.\n\nHe read the letters. One was very unpleasant, from a merchant who was buying a forest on his wife’s property. To sell this forest was absolutely essential; but at present, until he was reconciled with his wife, the subject could not be discussed. The most unpleasant thing of all was that his pecuniary interests should in this way enter into the question of his reconciliation with his wife. And the idea that he might be led on by his interests, that he might seek a reconciliation with his wife on account of the sale of the forest—that idea hurt him.\n\nWhen he had finished his letters, Stepan Arkadyevitch moved the office-papers close to him, rapidly looked through two pieces of business, made a few notes with a big pencil, and pushing away the papers, turned to his coffee. As he sipped his coffee, he opened a still damp morning paper, and began reading it.\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch took in and read a liberal paper, not an extreme one, but one advocating the views held by the majority. And in spite of the fact that science, art, and politics had no special interest for him, he firmly held those views on all these subjects which were held by the majority and by his paper, and he only changed them when the majority changed them—or, more strictly speaking, he did not change them, but they imperceptibly changed of themselves within him.\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch had not chosen his political opinions or his views; these political opinions and views had come to him of themselves, just as he did not choose the shapes of his hat and coat, but simply took those that were being worn. And for him, living in a certain society—owing to the need, ordinarily developed at years of discretion, for some degree of mental activity—to have views was just as indispensable as to have a hat. If there was a reason for his preferring liberal to conservative views, which were held also by many of his circle, it arose not from his considering liberalism more rational, but from its being in closer accordance with his manner of life. The liberal party said that in Russia everything is wrong, and certainly Stepan Arkadyevitch had many debts and was decidedly short of money. The liberal party said that marriage is an institution quite out of date, and that it needs reconstruction; and family life certainly afforded Stepan Arkadyevitch little gratification, and forced him into lying and hypocrisy, which was so repulsive to his nature. The liberal party said, or rather allowed it to be understood, that religion is only a curb to keep in check the barbarous classes of the people; and Stepan Arkadyevitch could not get through even a short service without his legs aching from standing up, and could never make out what was the object of all the terrible and high-flown language about another world when life might be so very amusing in this world. And with all this, Stepan Arkadyevitch, who liked a joke, was fond of puzzling a plain man by saying that if he prided himself on his origin, he ought not to stop at Rurik and disown the first founder of his family—the monkey. And so Liberalism had become a habit of Stepan Arkadyevitch’s, and he liked his newspaper, as he did his cigar after dinner, for the slight fog it diffused in his brain. He read the leading article, in which it was maintained that it was quite senseless in our day to raise an outcry that radicalism was threatening to swallow up all conservative elements, and that the government ought to take measures to crush the revolutionary hydra; that, on the contrary, “in our opinion the danger lies not in that fantastic revolutionary hydra, but in the obstinacy of traditionalism clogging progress,” etc., etc. He read another article, too, a financial one, which alluded to Bentham and Mill, and dropped some innuendoes reflecting on the ministry. With his characteristic quickwittedness he caught the drift of each innuendo, divined whence it came, at whom and on what ground it was aimed, and that afforded him, as it always did, a certain satisfaction. But today that satisfaction was embittered by Matrona Philimonovna’s advice and the unsatisfactory state of the household. He read, too, that Count Beist was rumored to have left for Wiesbaden, and that one need have no more gray hair, and of the sale of a light carriage, and of a young person seeking a situation; but these items of information did not give him, as usual, a quiet, ironical gratification. Having finished the paper, a second cup of coffee and a roll and butter, he got up, shaking the crumbs of the roll off his waistcoat; and, squaring his broad chest, he smiled joyously: not because there was anything particularly agreeable in his mind—the joyous smile was evoked by a good digestion.\n\nBut this joyous smile at once recalled everything to him, and he grew thoughtful.\n\nTwo childish voices (Stepan Arkadyevitch recognized the voices of Grisha, his youngest boy, and Tanya, his eldest girl) were heard outside the door. They were carrying something, and dropped it.\n\n“I told you not to sit passengers on the roof,” said the little girl in English; “there, pick them up!”\n\n“Everything’s in confusion,” thought Stepan Arkadyevitch; “there are the children running about by themselves.” And going to the door, he called them. They threw down the box, that represented a train, and came in to their father.\n\nThe little girl, her father’s favorite, ran up boldly, embraced him, and hung laughingly on his neck, enjoying as she always did the smell of scent that came from his whiskers. At last the little girl kissed his face, which was flushed from his stooping posture and beaming with tenderness, loosed her hands, and was about to run away again; but her father held her back.\n\n“How is mamma?” he asked, passing his hand over his daughter’s smooth, soft little neck. “Good morning,” he said, smiling to the boy, who had come up to greet him. He was conscious that he loved the boy less, and always tried to be fair; but the boy felt it, and did not respond with a smile to his father’s chilly smile.\n\n“Mamma? She is up,” answered the girl.\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch sighed. “That means that she’s not slept again all night,” he thought.\n\n“Well, is she cheerful?”\n\nThe little girl knew that there was a quarrel between her father and mother, and that her mother could not be cheerful, and that her father must be aware of this, and that he was pretending when he asked about it so lightly. And she blushed for her father. He at once perceived it, and blushed too.\n\n“I don’t know,” she said. “She did not say we must do our lessons, but she said we were to go for a walk with Miss Hoole to grandmamma’s.”\n\n“Well, go, Tanya, my darling. Oh, wait a minute, though,” he said, still holding her and stroking her soft little hand.\n\nHe took off the mantelpiece, where he had put it yesterday, a little box of sweets, and gave her two, picking out her favorites, a chocolate and a fondant.\n\n“For Grisha?” said the little girl, pointing to the chocolate.\n\n“Yes, yes.” And still stroking her little shoulder, he kissed her on the roots of her hair and neck, and let her go.\n\n“The carriage is ready,” said Matvey; “but there’s someone to see you with a petition.”\n\n“Been here long?” asked Stepan Arkadyevitch.\n\n“Half an hour.”\n\n“How many times have I told you to tell me at once?”\n\n“One must let you drink your coffee in peace, at least,” said Matvey, in the affectionately gruff tone with which it was impossible to be angry.\n\n“Well, show the person up at once,” said Oblonsky, frowning with vexation.\n\nThe petitioner, the widow of a staff captain Kalinin, came with a request impossible and unreasonable; but Stepan Arkadyevitch, as he generally did, made her sit down, heard her to the end attentively without interrupting her, and gave her detailed advice as to how and to whom to apply, and even wrote her, in his large, sprawling, good and legible hand, a confident and fluent little note to a personage who might be of use to her. Having got rid of the staff captain’s widow, Stepan Arkadyevitch took his hat and stopped to recollect whether he had forgotten anything. It appeared that he had forgotten nothing except what he wanted to forget—his wife.\n\n“Ah, yes!” He bowed his head, and his handsome face assumed a harassed expression. “To go, or not to go!” he said to himself; and an inner voice told him he must not go, that nothing could come of it but falsity; that to amend, to set right their relations was impossible, because it was impossible to make her attractive again and able to inspire love, or to make him an old man, not susceptible to love. Except deceit and lying nothing could come of it now; and deceit and lying were opposed to his nature.\n\n“It must be some time, though: it can’t go on like this,” he said, trying to give himself courage. He squared his chest, took out a cigarette, took two whiffs at it, flung it into a mother-of-pearl ashtray, and with rapid steps walked through the drawing-room, and opened the other door into his wife’s bedroom.\n\n# Chapter 4\n\nDarya Alexandrovna, in a dressing jacket, and with her now scanty, once luxuriant and beautiful hair fastened up with hairpins on the nape of her neck, with a sunken, thin face and large, startled eyes, which looked prominent from the thinness of her face, was standing among a litter of all sorts of things scattered all over the room, before an open bureau, from which she was taking something. Hearing her husband’s steps, she stopped, looking towards the door, and trying assiduously to give her features a severe and contemptuous expression. She felt she was afraid of him, and afraid of the coming interview. She was just attempting to do what she had attempted to do ten times already in these last three days—to sort out the children’s things and her own, so as to take them to her mother’s—and again she could not bring herself to do this; but now again, as each time before, she kept saying to herself, “that things cannot go on like this, that she must take some step” to punish him, put him to shame, avenge on him some little part at least of the suffering he had caused her. She still continued to tell herself that she should leave him, but she was conscious that this was impossible; it was impossible because she could not get out of the habit of regarding him as her husband and loving him. Besides this, she realized that if even here in her own house she could hardly manage to look after her five children properly, they would be still worse off where she was going with them all. As it was, even in the course of these three days, the youngest was unwell from being given unwholesome soup, and the others had almost gone without their dinner the day before. She was conscious that it was impossible to go away; but, cheating herself, she went on all the same sorting out her things and pretending she was going.\n\nSeeing her husband, she dropped her hands into the drawer of the bureau as though looking for something, and only looked round at him when he had come quite up to her. But her face, to which she tried to give a severe and resolute expression, betrayed bewilderment and suffering.\n\n“Dolly!” he said in a subdued and timid voice. He bent his head towards his shoulder and tried to look pitiful and humble, but for all that he was radiant with freshness and health. In a rapid glance she scanned his figure that beamed with health and freshness. “Yes, he is happy and content!” she thought; “while I.... And that disgusting good nature, which everyone likes him for and praises—I hate that good nature of his,” she thought. Her mouth stiffened, the muscles of the cheek contracted on the right side of her pale, nervous face.\n\n“What do you want?” she said in a rapid, deep, unnatural voice.\n\n“Dolly!” he repeated, with a quiver in his voice. “Anna is coming today.”\n\n“Well, what is that to me? I can’t see her!” she cried.\n\n“But you must, really, Dolly....”\n\n“Go away, go away, go away!” she shrieked, not looking at him, as though this shriek were called up by physical pain.\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch could be calm when he thought of his wife, he could hope that she would come round, as Matvey expressed it, and could quietly go on reading his paper and drinking his coffee; but when he saw her tortured, suffering face, heard the tone of her voice, submissive to fate and full of despair, there was a catch in his breath and a lump in his throat, and his eyes began to shine with tears.\n\n“My God! what have I done? Dolly! For God’s sake!... You know....” He could not go on; there was a sob in his throat.\n\nShe shut the bureau with a slam, and glanced at him.\n\n“Dolly, what can I say?... One thing: forgive.... Remember, cannot nine years of my life atone for an instant....”\n\nShe dropped her eyes and listened, expecting what he would say, as it were beseeching him in some way or other to make her believe differently.\n\n“—instant of passion?” he said, and would have gone on, but at that word, as at a pang of physical pain, her lips stiffened again, and again the muscles of her right cheek worked.\n\n“Go away, go out of the room!” she shrieked still more shrilly, “and don’t talk to me of your passion and your loathsomeness.”\n\nShe tried to go out, but tottered, and clung to the back of a chair to support herself. His face relaxed, his lips swelled, his eyes were swimming with tears.\n\n“Dolly!” he said, sobbing now; “for mercy’s sake, think of the children; they are not to blame! I am to blame, and punish me, make me expiate my fault. Anything I can do, I am ready to do anything! I am to blame, no words can express how much I am to blame! But, Dolly, forgive me!”\n\nShe sat down. He listened to her hard, heavy breathing, and he was unutterably sorry for her. She tried several times to begin to speak, but could not. He waited.\n\n“You remember the children, Stiva, to play with them; but I remember them, and know that this means their ruin,” she said—obviously one of the phrases she had more than once repeated to herself in the course of the last few days.\n\nShe had called him “Stiva,” and he glanced at her with gratitude, and moved to take her hand, but she drew back from him with aversion.\n\n“I think of the children, and for that reason I would do anything in the world to save them, but I don’t myself know how to save them. By taking them away from their father, or by leaving them with a vicious father—yes, a vicious father.... Tell me, after what ... has happened, can we live together? Is that possible? Tell me, eh, is it possible?” she repeated, raising her voice, “after my husband, the father of my children, enters into a love affair with his own children’s governess?”\n\n“But what could I do? what could I do?” he kept saying in a pitiful voice, not knowing what he was saying, as his head sank lower and lower.\n\n“You are loathsome to me, repulsive!” she shrieked, getting more and more heated. “Your tears mean nothing! You have never loved me; you have neither heart nor honorable feeling! You are hateful to me, disgusting, a stranger—yes, a complete stranger!” With pain and wrath she uttered the word so terrible to herself—stranger.\n\nHe looked at her, and the fury expressed in her face alarmed and amazed him. He did not understand how his pity for her exasperated her. She saw in him sympathy for her, but not love. “No, she hates me. She will not forgive me,” he thought.\n\n“It is awful! awful!” he said.\n\nAt that moment in the next room a child began to cry; probably it had fallen down. Darya Alexandrovna listened, and her face suddenly softened.\n\nShe seemed to be pulling herself together for a few seconds, as though she did not know where she was, and what she was doing, and getting up rapidly, she moved towards the door.\n\n“Well, she loves my child,” he thought, noticing the change of her face at the child’s cry, “my child: how can she hate me?”\n\n“Dolly, one word more,” he said, following her.\n\n“If you come near me, I will call in the servants, the children! They may all know you are a scoundrel! I am going away at once, and you may live here with your mistress!”\n\nAnd she went out, slamming the door.\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch sighed, wiped his face, and with a subdued tread walked out of the room. “Matvey says she will come round; but how? I don’t see the least chance of it. Ah, oh, how horrible it is! And how vulgarly she shouted,” he said to himself, remembering her shriek and the words—“scoundrel” and “mistress.” “And very likely the maids were listening! Horribly vulgar! horrible!” Stepan Arkadyevitch stood a few seconds alone, wiped his face, squared his chest, and walked out of the room.\n\nIt was Friday, and in the dining-room the German watchmaker was winding up the clock. Stepan Arkadyevitch remembered his joke about this punctual, bald watchmaker, “that the German was wound up for a whole lifetime himself, to wind up watches,” and he smiled. Stepan Arkadyevitch was fond of a joke: “And maybe she will come round! That’s a good expression, ‘come round,’” he thought. “I must repeat that.”\n\n“Matvey!” he shouted. “Arrange everything with Darya in the sitting room for Anna Arkadyevna,” he said to Matvey when he came in.\n\n“Yes, sir.”\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch put on his fur coat and went out onto the steps.\n\n“You won’t dine at home?” said Matvey, seeing him off.\n\n“That’s as it happens. But here’s for the housekeeping,” he said, taking ten roubles from his pocketbook. “That’ll be enough.”\n\n“Enough or not enough, we must make it do,” said Matvey, slamming the carriage door and stepping back onto the steps.\n\nDarya Alexandrovna meanwhile having pacified the child, and knowing from the sound of the carriage that he had gone off, went back again to her bedroom. It was her solitary refuge from the household cares which crowded upon her directly she went out from it. Even now, in the short time she had been in the nursery, the English governess and Matrona Philimonovna had succeeded in putting several questions to her, which did not admit of delay, and which only she could answer: “What were the children to put on for their walk? Should they have any milk? Should not a new cook be sent for?”\n\n“Ah, let me alone, let me alone!” she said, and going back to her bedroom she sat down in the same place as she had sat when talking to her husband, clasping tightly her thin hands with the rings that slipped down on her bony fingers, and fell to going over in her memory all the conversation. “He has gone! But has he broken it off with her?” she thought. “Can it be he sees her? Why didn’t I ask him! No, no, reconciliation is impossible. Even if we remain in the same house, we are strangers—strangers forever!” She repeated again with special significance the word so dreadful to her. “And how I loved him! my God, how I loved him!... How I loved him! And now don’t I love him? Don’t I love him more than before? The most horrible thing is,” she began, but did not finish her thought, because Matrona Philimonovna put her head in at the door.\n\n“Let us send for my brother,” she said; “he can get a dinner anyway, or we shall have the children getting nothing to eat till six again, like yesterday.”\n\n“Very well, I will come directly and see about it. But did you send for some new milk?”\n\nAnd Darya Alexandrovna plunged into the duties of the day, and drowned her grief in them for a time.\n\n# Chapter 5\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch had learned easily at school, thanks to his excellent abilities, but he had been idle and mischievous, and therefore was one of the lowest in his class. But in spite of his habitually dissipated mode of life, his inferior grade in the service, and his comparative youth, he occupied the honorable and lucrative position of president of one of the government boards at Moscow. This post he had received through his sister Anna’s husband, Alexey Alexandrovitch Karenin, who held one of the most important positions in the ministry to whose department the Moscow office belonged. But if Karenin had not got his brother-in-law this berth, then through a hundred other personages—brothers, sisters, cousins, uncles, and aunts—Stiva Oblonsky would have received this post, or some other similar one, together with the salary of six thousand absolutely needful for him, as his affairs, in spite of his wife’s considerable property, were in an embarrassed condition.\n\nHalf Moscow and Petersburg were friends and relations of Stepan Arkadyevitch. He was born in the midst of those who had been and are the powerful ones of this world. One-third of the men in the government, the older men, had been friends of his father’s, and had known him in petticoats; another third were his intimate chums, and the remainder were friendly acquaintances. Consequently the distributors of earthly blessings in the shape of places, rents, shares, and such, were all his friends, and could not overlook one of their own set; and Oblonsky had no need to make any special exertion to get a lucrative post. He had only not to refuse things, not to show jealousy, not to be quarrelsome or take offense, all of which from his characteristic good nature he never did. It would have struck him as absurd if he had been told that he would not get a position with the salary he required, especially as he expected nothing out of the way; he only wanted what the men of his own age and standing did get, and he was no worse qualified for performing duties of the kind than any other man.\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch was not merely liked by all who knew him for his good humor, but for his bright disposition, and his unquestionable honesty. In him, in his handsome, radiant figure, his sparkling eyes, black hair and eyebrows, and the white and red of his face, there was something which produced a physical effect of kindliness and good humor on the people who met him. “Aha! Stiva! Oblonsky! Here he is!” was almost always said with a smile of delight on meeting him. Even though it happened at times that after a conversation with him it seemed that nothing particularly delightful had happened, the next day, and the next, everyone was just as delighted at meeting him again.\n\nAfter filling for three years the post of president of one of the government boards at Moscow, Stepan Arkadyevitch had won the respect, as well as the liking, of his fellow-officials, subordinates, and superiors, and all who had had business with him. The principal qualities in Stepan Arkadyevitch which had gained him this universal respect in the service consisted, in the first place, of his extreme indulgence for others, founded on a consciousness of his own shortcomings; secondly, of his perfect liberalism—not the liberalism he read of in the papers, but the liberalism that was in his blood, in virtue of which he treated all men perfectly equally and exactly the same, whatever their fortune or calling might be; and thirdly—the most important point—his complete indifference to the business in which he was engaged, in consequence of which he was never carried away, and never made mistakes.\n\nOn reaching the offices of the board, Stepan Arkadyevitch, escorted by a deferential porter with a portfolio, went into his little private room, put on his uniform, and went into the boardroom. The clerks and copyists all rose, greeting him with good-humored deference. Stepan Arkadyevitch moved quickly, as ever, to his place, shook hands with his colleagues, and sat down. He made a joke or two, and talked just as much as was consistent with due decorum, and began work. No one knew better than Stepan Arkadyevitch how to hit on the exact line between freedom, simplicity, and official stiffness necessary for the agreeable conduct of business. A secretary, with the good-humored deference common to everyone in Stepan Arkadyevitch’s office, came up with papers, and began to speak in the familiar and easy tone which had been introduced by Stepan Arkadyevitch.\n\n“We have succeeded in getting the information from the government department of Penza. Here, would you care?...”\n\n“You’ve got them at last?” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, laying his finger on the paper. “Now, gentlemen....”\n\nAnd the sitting of the board began.\n\n“If they knew,” he thought, bending his head with a significant air as he listened to the report, “what a guilty little boy their president was half an hour ago.” And his eyes were laughing during the reading of the report. Till two o’clock the sitting would go on without a break, and at two o’clock there would be an interval and luncheon.\n\nIt was not yet two, when the large glass doors of the boardroom suddenly opened and someone came in.\n\nAll the officials sitting on the further side under the portrait of the Tsar and the eagle, delighted at any distraction, looked round at the door; but the doorkeeper standing at the door at once drove out the intruder, and closed the glass door after him.\n\nWhen the case had been read through, Stepan Arkadyevitch got up and stretched, and by way of tribute to the liberalism of the times took out a cigarette in the boardroom and went into his private room. Two of the members of the board, the old veteran in the service, Nikitin, and the Kammerjunker Grinevitch, went in with him.\n\n“We shall have time to finish after lunch,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch.\n\n“To be sure we shall!” said Nikitin.\n\n“A pretty sharp fellow this Fomin must be,” said Grinevitch of one of the persons taking part in the case they were examining.\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch frowned at Grinevitch’s words, giving him thereby to understand that it was improper to pass judgment prematurely, and made him no reply.\n\n“Who was that came in?” he asked the doorkeeper.\n\n“Someone, your excellency, crept in without permission directly my back was turned. He was asking for you. I told him: when the members come out, then....”\n\n“Where is he?”\n\n“Maybe he’s gone into the passage, but here he comes anyway. That is he,” said the doorkeeper, pointing to a strongly built, broad-shouldered man with a curly beard, who, without taking off his sheepskin cap, was running lightly and rapidly up the worn steps of the stone staircase. One of the members going down—a lean official with a portfolio—stood out of his way and looked disapprovingly at the legs of the stranger, then glanced inquiringly at Oblonsky.\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch was standing at the top of the stairs. His good-naturedly beaming face above the embroidered collar of his uniform beamed more than ever when he recognized the man coming up.\n\n“Why, it’s actually you, Levin, at last!” he said with a friendly mocking smile, scanning Levin as he approached. “How is it you have deigned to look me up in this den?” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, and not content with shaking hands, he kissed his friend. “Have you been here long?”\n\n“I have just come, and very much wanted to see you,” said Levin, looking shyly and at the same time angrily and uneasily around.\n\n“Well, let’s go into my room,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, who knew his friend’s sensitive and irritable shyness, and, taking his arm, he drew him along, as though guiding him through dangers.\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch was on familiar terms with almost all his acquaintances, and called almost all of them by their Christian names: old men of sixty, boys of twenty, actors, ministers, merchants, and adjutant-generals, so that many of his intimate chums were to be found at the extreme ends of the social ladder, and would have been very much surprised to learn that they had, through the medium of Oblonsky, something in common. He was the familiar friend of everyone with whom he took a glass of champagne, and he took a glass of champagne with everyone, and when in consequence he met any of his disreputable chums, as he used in joke to call many of his friends, in the presence of his subordinates, he well knew how, with his characteristic tact, to diminish the disagreeable impression made on them. Levin was not a disreputable chum, but Oblonsky, with his ready tact, felt that Levin fancied he might not care to show his intimacy with him before his subordinates, and so he made haste to take him off into his room.\n\nLevin was almost of the same age as Oblonsky; their intimacy did not rest merely on champagne. Levin had been the friend and companion of his early youth. They were fond of one another in spite of the difference of their characters and tastes, as friends are fond of one another who have been together in early youth. But in spite of this, each of them—as is often the way with men who have selected careers of different kinds—though in discussion he would even justify the other’s career, in his heart despised it. It seemed to each of them that the life he led himself was the only real life, and the life led by his friend was a mere phantasm. Oblonsky could not restrain a slight mocking smile at the sight of Levin. How often he had seen him come up to Moscow from the country where he was doing something, but what precisely Stepan Arkadyevitch could never quite make out, and indeed he took no interest in the matter. Levin arrived in Moscow always excited and in a hurry, rather ill at ease and irritated by his own want of ease, and for the most part with a perfectly new, unexpected view of things. Stepan Arkadyevitch laughed at this, and liked it. In the same way Levin in his heart despised the town mode of life of his friend, and his official duties, which he laughed at, and regarded as trifling. But the difference was that Oblonsky, as he was doing the same as everyone did, laughed complacently and good-humoredly, while Levin laughed without complacency and sometimes angrily.\n\n“We have long been expecting you,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, going into his room and letting Levin’s hand go as though to show that here all danger was over. “I am very, very glad to see you,” he went on. “Well, how are you? Eh? When did you come?”\n\nLevin was silent, looking at the unknown faces of Oblonsky’s two companions, and especially at the hand of the elegant Grinevitch, which had such long white fingers, such long yellow filbert-shaped nails, and such huge shining studs on the shirt-cuff, that apparently they absorbed all his attention, and allowed him no freedom of thought. Oblonsky noticed this at once, and smiled.\n\n“Ah, to be sure, let me introduce you,” he said. “My colleagues: Philip Ivanitch Nikitin, Mihail Stanislavitch Grinevitch”—and turning to Levin—“a district councilor, a modern district councilman, a gymnast who lifts thirteen stone with one hand, a cattle-breeder and sportsman, and my friend, Konstantin Dmitrievitch Levin, the brother of Sergey Ivanovitch Koznishev.”\n\n“Delighted,” said the veteran.\n\n“I have the honor of knowing your brother, Sergey Ivanovitch,” said Grinevitch, holding out his slender hand with its long nails.\n\nLevin frowned, shook hands coldly, and at once turned to Oblonsky. Though he had a great respect for his half-brother, an author well known to all Russia, he could not endure it when people treated him not as Konstantin Levin, but as the brother of the celebrated Koznishev.\n\n“No, I am no longer a district councilor. I have quarreled with them all, and don’t go to the meetings any more,” he said, turning to Oblonsky.\n\n“You’ve been quick about it!” said Oblonsky with a smile. “But how? why?”\n\n“It’s a long story. I will tell you some time,” said Levin, but he began telling him at once. “Well, to put it shortly, I was convinced that nothing was really done by the district councils, or ever could be,” he began, as though someone had just insulted him. “On one side it’s a plaything; they play at being a parliament, and I’m neither young enough nor old enough to find amusement in playthings; and on the other side” (he stammered) “it’s a means for the coterie of the district to make money. Formerly they had wardships, courts of justice, now they have the district council—not in the form of bribes, but in the form of unearned salary,” he said, as hotly as though someone of those present had opposed his opinion.\n\n“Aha! You’re in a new phase again, I see—a conservative,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch. “However, we can go into that later.”\n\n“Yes, later. But I wanted to see you,” said Levin, looking with hatred at Grinevitch’s hand.\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch gave a scarcely perceptible smile.\n\n“How was it you used to say you would never wear European dress again?” he said, scanning his new suit, obviously cut by a French tailor. “Ah! I see: a new phase.”\n\nLevin suddenly blushed, not as grown men blush, slightly, without being themselves aware of it, but as boys blush, feeling that they are ridiculous through their shyness, and consequently ashamed of it and blushing still more, almost to the point of tears. And it was so strange to see this sensible, manly face in such a childish plight, that Oblonsky left off looking at him.\n\n“Oh, where shall we meet? You know I want very much to talk to you,” said Levin.\n\nOblonsky seemed to ponder.\n\n“I’ll tell you what: let’s go to Gurin’s to lunch, and there we can talk. I am free till three.”\n\n“No,” answered Levin, after an instant’s thought, “I have got to go on somewhere else.”\n\n“All right, then, let’s dine together.”\n\n“Dine together? But I have nothing very particular, only a few words to say, and a question I want to ask you, and we can have a talk afterwards.”\n\n“Well, say the few words, then, at once, and we’ll gossip after dinner.”\n\n“Well, it’s this,” said Levin; “but it’s of no importance, though.”\n\nHis face all at once took an expression of anger from the effort he was making to surmount his shyness.\n\n“What are the Shtcherbatskys doing? Everything as it used to be?” he said.\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch, who had long known that Levin was in love with his sister-in-law, Kitty, gave a hardly perceptible smile, and his eyes sparkled merrily.\n\n“You said a few words, but I can’t answer in a few words, because.... Excuse me a minute....”\n\nA secretary came in, with respectful familiarity and the modest consciousness, characteristic of every secretary, of superiority to his chief in the knowledge of their business; he went up to Oblonsky with some papers, and began, under pretense of asking a question, to explain some objection. Stepan Arkadyevitch, without hearing him out, laid his hand genially on the secretary’s sleeve.\n\n“No, you do as I told you,” he said, softening his words with a smile, and with a brief explanation of his view of the matter he turned away from the papers, and said: “So do it that way, if you please, Zahar Nikititch.”\n\nThe secretary retired in confusion. During the consultation with the secretary Levin had completely recovered from his embarrassment. He was standing with his elbows on the back of a chair, and on his face was a look of ironical attention.\n\n“I don’t understand it, I don’t understand it,” he said.\n\n“What don’t you understand?” said Oblonsky, smiling as brightly as ever, and picking up a cigarette. He expected some queer outburst from Levin.\n\n“I don’t understand what you are doing,” said Levin, shrugging his shoulders. “How can you do it seriously?”\n\n“Why not?”\n\n“Why, because there’s nothing in it.”\n\n“You think so, but we’re overwhelmed with work.”\n\n“On paper. But, there, you’ve a gift for it,” added Levin.\n\n“That’s to say, you think there’s a lack of something in me?”\n\n“Perhaps so,” said Levin. “But all the same I admire your grandeur, and am proud that I’ve a friend in such a great person. You’ve not answered my question, though,” he went on, with a desperate effort looking Oblonsky straight in the face.\n\n“Oh, that’s all very well. You wait a bit, and you’ll come to this yourself. It’s very nice for you to have over six thousand acres in the Karazinsky district, and such muscles, and the freshness of a girl of twelve; still you’ll be one of us one day. Yes, as to your question, there is no change, but it’s a pity you’ve been away so long.”\n\n“Oh, why so?” Levin queried, panic-stricken.\n\n“Oh, nothing,” responded Oblonsky. “We’ll talk it over. But what’s brought you up to town?”\n\n“Oh, we’ll talk about that, too, later on,” said Levin, reddening again up to his ears.\n\n“All right. I see,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch. “I should ask you to come to us, you know, but my wife’s not quite the thing. But I tell you what; if you want to see them, they’re sure now to be at the Zoological Gardens from four to five. Kitty skates. You drive along there, and I’ll come and fetch you, and we’ll go and dine somewhere together.”\n\n“Capital. So good-bye till then.”\n\n“Now mind, you’ll forget, I know you, or rush off home to the country!” Stepan Arkadyevitch called out laughing.\n“No, truly!”\n\nAnd Levin went out of the room, only when he was in the doorway remembering that he had forgotten to take leave of Oblonsky’s colleagues.\n\n“That gentleman must be a man of great energy,” said Grinevitch, when Levin had gone away.\n\n“Yes, my dear boy,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, nodding his head, “he’s a lucky fellow! Over six thousand acres in the Karazinsky district; everything before him; and what youth and vigor! Not like some of us.”\n\n“You have a great deal to complain of, haven’t you, Stepan Arkadyevitch?”\n\n“Ah, yes, I’m in a poor way, a bad way,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch with a heavy sigh.\n\n# Chapter 6\n\nWhen Oblonsky asked Levin what had brought him to town, Levin blushed, and was furious with himself for blushing, because he could not answer, “I have come to make your sister-in-law an offer,” though that was precisely what he had come for.\n\nThe families of the Levins and the Shtcherbatskys were old, noble Moscow families, and had always been on intimate and friendly terms. This intimacy had grown still closer during Levin’s student days. He had both prepared for the university with the young Prince Shtcherbatsky, the brother of Kitty and Dolly, and had entered at the same time with him. In those days Levin used often to be in the Shtcherbatskys’ house, and he was in love with the Shtcherbatsky household. Strange as it may appear, it was with the household, the family, that Konstantin Levin was in love, especially with the feminine half of the household. Levin did not remember his own mother, and his only sister was older than he was, so that it was in the Shtcherbatskys’ house that he saw for the first time that inner life of an old, noble, cultivated, and honorable family of which he had been deprived by the death of his father and mother. All the members of that family, especially the feminine half, were pictured by him, as it were, wrapped about with a mysterious poetical veil, and he not only perceived no defects whatever in them, but under the poetical veil that shrouded them he assumed the existence of the loftiest sentiments and every possible perfection. Why it was the three young ladies had one day to speak French, and the next English; why it was that at certain hours they played by turns on the piano, the sounds of which were audible in their brother’s room above, where the students used to work; why they were visited by those professors of French literature, of music, of drawing, of dancing; why at certain hours all the three young ladies, with Mademoiselle Linon, drove in the coach to the Tversky boulevard, dressed in their satin cloaks, Dolly in a long one, Natalia in a half-long one, and Kitty in one so short that her shapely legs in tightly-drawn red stockings were visible to all beholders; why it was they had to walk about the Tversky boulevard escorted by a footman with a gold cockade in his hat—all this and much more that was done in their mysterious world he did not understand, but he was sure that everything that was done there was very good, and he was in love precisely with the mystery of the proceedings.\n\nIn his student days he had all but been in love with the eldest, Dolly, but she was soon married to Oblonsky. Then he began being in love with the second. He felt, as it were, that he had to be in love with one of the sisters, only he could not quite make out which. But Natalia, too, had hardly made her appearance in the world when she married the diplomat Lvov. Kitty was still a child when Levin left the university. Young Shtcherbatsky went into the navy, was drowned in the Baltic, and Levin’s relations with the Shtcherbatskys, in spite of his friendship with Oblonsky, became less intimate. But when early in the winter of this year Levin came to Moscow, after a year in the country, and saw the Shtcherbatskys, he realized which of the three sisters he was indeed destined to love.\n\nOne would have thought that nothing could be simpler than for him, a man of good family, rather rich than poor, and thirty-two years old, to make the young Princess Shtcherbatskaya an offer of marriage; in all likelihood he would at once have been looked upon as a good match. But Levin was in love, and so it seemed to him that Kitty was so perfect in every respect that she was a creature far above everything earthly; and that he was a creature so low and so earthly that it could not even be conceived that other people and she herself could regard him as worthy of her.\n\nAfter spending two months in Moscow in a state of enchantment, seeing Kitty almost every day in society, into which he went so as to meet her, he abruptly decided that it could not be, and went back to the country.\n\nLevin’s conviction that it could not be was founded on the idea that in the eyes of her family he was a disadvantageous and worthless match for the charming Kitty, and that Kitty herself could not love him. In her family’s eyes he had no ordinary, definite career and position in society, while his contemporaries by this time, when he was thirty-two, were already, one a colonel, and another a professor, another director of a bank and railways, or president of a board like Oblonsky. But he (he knew very well how he must appear to others) was a country gentleman, occupied in breeding cattle, shooting game, and building barns; in other words, a fellow of no ability, who had not turned out well, and who was doing just what, according to the ideas of the world, is done by people fit for nothing else.\n\nThe mysterious, enchanting Kitty herself could not love such an ugly person as he conceived himself to be, and, above all, such an ordinary, in no way striking person. Moreover, his attitude to Kitty in the past—the attitude of a grown-up person to a child, arising from his friendship with her brother—seemed to him yet another obstacle to love. An ugly, good-natured man, as he considered himself, might, he supposed, be liked as a friend; but to be loved with such a love as that with which he loved Kitty, one would need to be a handsome and, still more, a distinguished man.\n\nHe had heard that women often did care for ugly and ordinary men, but he did not believe it, for he judged by himself, and he could not himself have loved any but beautiful, mysterious, and exceptional women.\n\nBut after spending two months alone in the country, he was convinced that this was not one of those passions of which he had had experience in his early youth; that this feeling gave him not an instant’s rest; that he could not live without deciding the question, would she or would she not be his wife, and that his despair had arisen only from his own imaginings, that he had no sort of proof that he would be rejected. And he had now come to Moscow with a firm determination to make an offer, and get married if he were accepted. Or ... he could not conceive what would become of him if he were rejected.\n\n# Chapter 7\n\nOn arriving in Moscow by a morning train, Levin had put up at the house of his elder half-brother, Koznishev. After changing his clothes he went down to his brother’s study, intending to talk to him at once about the object of his visit, and to ask his advice; but his brother was not alone. With him there was a well-known professor of philosophy, who had come from Harkov expressly to clear up a difference that had arisen between them on a very important philosophical question. The professor was carrying on a hot crusade against materialists. Sergey Koznishev had been following this crusade with interest, and after reading the professor’s last article, he had written him a letter stating his objections. He accused the professor of making too great concessions to the materialists. And the professor had promptly appeared to argue the matter out. The point in discussion was the question then in vogue: Is there a line to be drawn between psychological and physiological phenomena in man? and if so, where?\n\nSergey Ivanovitch met his brother with the smile of chilly friendliness he always had for everyone, and introducing him to the professor, went on with the conversation.\n\nA little man in spectacles, with a narrow forehead, tore himself from the discussion for an instant to greet Levin, and then went on talking without paying any further attention to him. Levin sat down to wait till the professor should go, but he soon began to get interested in the subject under discussion.\n\nLevin had come across the magazine articles about which they were disputing, and had read them, interested in them as a development of the first principles of science, familiar to him as a natural science student at the university. But he had never connected these scientific deductions as to the origin of man as an animal, as to reflex action, biology, and sociology, with those questions as to the meaning of life and death to himself, which had of late been more and more often in his mind.\n\nAs he listened to his brother’s argument with the professor, he noticed that they connected these scientific questions with those spiritual problems, that at times they almost touched on the latter; but every time they were close upon what seemed to him the chief point, they promptly beat a hasty retreat, and plunged again into a sea of subtle distinctions, reservations, quotations, allusions, and appeals to authorities, and it was with difficulty that he understood what they were talking about.\n\n“I cannot admit it,” said Sergey Ivanovitch, with his habitual clearness, precision of expression, and elegance of phrase. “I cannot in any case agree with Keiss that my whole conception of the external world has been derived from perceptions. The most fundamental idea, the idea of existence, has not been received by me through sensation; indeed, there is no special sense-organ for the transmission of such an idea.”\n\n“Yes, but they—Wurt, and Knaust, and Pripasov—would answer that your consciousness of existence is derived from the conjunction of all your sensations, that that consciousness of existence is the result of your sensations. Wurt, indeed, says plainly that, assuming there are no sensations, it follows that there is no idea of existence.”\n\n“I maintain the contrary,” began Sergey Ivanovitch.\n\nBut here it seemed to Levin that just as they were close upon the real point of the matter, they were again retreating, and he made up his mind to put a question to the professor.\n\n“According to that, if my senses are annihilated, if my body is dead, I can have no existence of any sort?” he queried.\n\nThe professor, in annoyance, and, as it were, mental suffering at the interruption, looked round at the strange inquirer, more like a bargeman than a philosopher, and turned his eyes upon Sergey Ivanovitch, as though to ask: What’s one to say to him? But Sergey Ivanovitch, who had been talking with far less heat and one-sidedness than the professor, and who had sufficient breadth of mind to answer the professor, and at the same time to comprehend the simple and natural point of view from which the question was put, smiled and said:\n\n“That question we have no right to answer as yet.”\n\n“We have not the requisite data,” chimed in the professor, and he went back to his argument. “No,” he said; “I would point out the fact that if, as Pripasov directly asserts, perception is based on sensation, then we are bound to distinguish sharply between these two conceptions.”\n\nLevin listened no more, and simply waited for the professor to go.\n\n# Chapter 8\n\nWhen the professor had gone, Sergey Ivanovitch turned to his brother.\n\n“Delighted that you’ve come. For some time, is it? How’s your farming getting on?”\n\nLevin knew that his elder brother took little interest in farming, and only put the question in deference to him, and so he only told him about the sale of his wheat and money matters.\n\nLevin had meant to tell his brother of his determination to get married, and to ask his advice; he had indeed firmly resolved to do so. But after seeing his brother, listening to his conversation with the professor, hearing afterwards the unconsciously patronizing tone in which his brother questioned him about agricultural matters (their mother’s property had not been divided, and Levin took charge of both their shares), Levin felt that he could not for some reason begin to talk to him of his intention of marrying. He felt that his brother would not look at it as he would have wished him to.\n\n“Well, how is your district council doing?” asked Sergey Ivanovitch, who was greatly interested in these local boards and attached great importance to them.\n\n“I really don’t know.”\n\n“What! Why, surely you’re a member of the board?”\n\n“No, I’m not a member now; I’ve resigned,” answered Levin, “and I no longer attend the meetings.”\n\n“What a pity!” commented Sergey Ivanovitch, frowning.\n\nLevin in self-defense began to describe what took place in the meetings in his district.\n\n“That’s how it always is!” Sergey Ivanovitch interrupted him. “We Russians are always like that. Perhaps it’s our strong point, really, the faculty of seeing our own shortcomings; but we overdo it, we comfort ourselves with irony which we always have on the tip of our tongues. All I say is, give such rights as our local self-government to any other European people—why, the Germans or the English would have worked their way to freedom from them, while we simply turn them into ridicule.”\n\n“But how can it be helped?” said Levin penitently. “It was my last effort. And I did try with all my soul. I can’t. I’m no good at it.”\n\n“It’s not that you’re no good at it,” said Sergey Ivanovitch; “it is that you don’t look at it as you should.”\n\n“Perhaps not,” Levin answered dejectedly.\n\n“Oh! do you know brother Nikolay’s turned up again?”\n\nThis brother Nikolay was the elder brother of Konstantin Levin, and half-brother of Sergey Ivanovitch; a man utterly ruined, who had dissipated the greater part of his fortune, was living in the strangest and lowest company, and had quarreled with his brothers.\n\n“What did you say?” Levin cried with horror. “How do you know?”\n\n“Prokofy saw him in the street.”\n\n“Here in Moscow? Where is he? Do you know?” Levin got up from his chair, as though on the point of starting off at once.\n\n“I am sorry I told you,” said Sergey Ivanovitch, shaking his head at his younger brother’s excitement. “I sent to find out where he is living, and sent him his IOU to Trubin, which I paid. This is the answer he sent me.”\n\nAnd Sergey Ivanovitch took a note from under a paper-weight and handed it to his brother.\n\nLevin read in the queer, familiar handwriting: “I humbly beg you to leave me in peace. That’s the only favor I ask of my gracious brothers.—Nikolay Levin.”\n\nLevin read it, and without raising his head stood with the note in his hands opposite Sergey Ivanovitch.\n\nThere was a struggle in his heart between the desire to forget his unhappy brother for the time, and the consciousness that it would be base to do so.\n\n“He obviously wants to offend me,” pursued Sergey Ivanovitch; “but he cannot offend me, and I should have wished with all my heart to assist him, but I know it’s impossible to do that.”\n\n“Yes, yes,” repeated Levin. “I understand and appreciate your attitude to him; but I shall go and see him.”\n\n“If you want to, do; but I shouldn’t advise it,” said Sergey Ivanovitch. “As regards myself, I have no fear of your doing so; he will not make you quarrel with me; but for your own sake, I should say you would do better not to go. You can’t do him any good; still, do as you please.”\n\n“Very likely I can’t do any good, but I feel—especially at such a moment—but that’s another thing—I feel I could not be at peace.”\n\n“Well, that I don’t understand,” said Sergey Ivanovitch. “One thing I do understand,” he added; “it’s a lesson in humility. I have come to look very differently and more charitably on what is called infamous since brother Nikolay has become what he is ... you know what he did....”\n\n“Oh, it’s awful, awful!” repeated Levin.\n\nAfter obtaining his brother’s address from Sergey Ivanovitch’s footman, Levin was on the point of setting off at once to see him, but on second thought he decided to put off his visit till the evening. The first thing to do to set his heart at rest was to accomplish what he had come to Moscow for. From his brother’s Levin went to Oblonsky’s office, and on getting news of the Shtcherbatskys from him, he drove to the place where he had been told he might find Kitty.\n\n# Chapter 9\n\nAt four o’clock, conscious of his throbbing heart, Levin stepped out of a hired sledge at the Zoological Gardens, and turned along the path to the frozen mounds and the skating ground, knowing that he would certainly find her there, as he had seen the Shtcherbatskys’ carriage at the entrance.\n\nIt was a bright, frosty day. Rows of carriages, sledges, drivers, and policemen were standing in the approach. Crowds of well-dressed people, with hats bright in the sun, swarmed about the entrance and along the well-swept little paths between the little houses adorned with carving in the Russian style. The old curly birches of the gardens, all their twigs laden with snow, looked as though freshly decked in sacred vestments.\n\nHe walked along the path towards the skating-ground, and kept saying to himself—“You mustn’t be excited, you must be calm. What’s the matter with you? What do you want? Be quiet, stupid,” he conjured his heart. And the more he tried to compose himself, the more breathless he found himself. An acquaintance met him and called him by his name, but Levin did not even recognize him. He went towards the mounds, whence came the clank of the chains of sledges as they slipped down or were dragged up, the rumble of the sliding sledges, and the sounds of merry voices. He walked on a few steps, and the skating-ground lay open before his eyes, and at once, amidst all the skaters, he knew her.\n\nHe knew she was there by the rapture and the terror that seized on his heart. She was standing talking to a lady at the opposite end of the ground. There was apparently nothing striking either in her dress or her attitude. But for Levin she was as easy to find in that crowd as a rose among nettles. Everything was made bright by her. She was the smile that shed light on all round her. “Is it possible I can go over there on the ice, go up to her?” he thought. The place where she stood seemed to him a holy shrine, unapproachable, and there was one moment when he was almost retreating, so overwhelmed was he with terror. He had to make an effort to master himself, and to remind himself that people of all sorts were moving about her, and that he too might come there to skate. He walked down, for a long while avoiding looking at her as at the sun, but seeing her, as one does the sun, without looking.\n\nOn that day of the week and at that time of day people of one set, all acquainted with one another, used to meet on the ice. There were crack skaters there, showing off their skill, and learners clinging to chairs with timid, awkward movements, boys, and elderly people skating with hygienic motives. They seemed to Levin an elect band of blissful beings because they were here, near her. All the skaters, it seemed, with perfect self-possession, skated towards her, skated by her, even spoke to her, and were happy, quite apart from her, enjoying the capital ice and the fine weather.\n\nNikolay Shtcherbatsky, Kitty’s cousin, in a short jacket and tight trousers, was sitting on a garden seat with his skates on. Seeing Levin, he shouted to him:\n\n“Ah, the first skater in Russia! Been here long? First-rate ice—do put your skates on.”\n\n“I haven’t got my skates,” Levin answered, marveling at this boldness and ease in her presence, and not for one second losing sight of her, though he did not look at her. He felt as though the sun were coming near him. She was in a corner, and turning out her slender feet in their high boots with obvious timidity, she skated towards him. A boy in Russian dress, desperately waving his arms and bowed down to the ground, overtook her. She skated a little uncertainly; taking her hands out of the little muff that hung on a cord, she held them ready for emergency, and looking towards Levin, whom she had recognized, she smiled at him, and at her own fears. When she had got round the turn, she gave herself a push off with one foot, and skated straight up to Shtcherbatsky. Clutching at his arm, she nodded smiling to Levin. She was more splendid than he had imagined her.\n\nWhen he thought of her, he could call up a vivid picture of her to himself, especially the charm of that little fair head, so freely set on the shapely girlish shoulders, and so full of childish brightness and good humor. The childishness of her expression, together with the delicate beauty of her figure, made up her special charm, and that he fully realized. But what always struck him in her as something unlooked for, was the expression of her eyes, soft, serene, and truthful, and above all, her smile, which always transported Levin to an enchanted world, where he felt himself softened and tender, as he remembered himself in some days of his early childhood.\n\n“Have you been here long?” she said, giving him her hand. “Thank you,” she added, as he picked up the handkerchief that had fallen out of her muff.\n\n“I? I’ve not long ... yesterday ... I mean today ... I arrived,” answered Levin, in his emotion not at once understanding her question. “I was meaning to come and see you,” he said; and then, recollecting with what intention he was trying to see her, he was promptly overcome with confusion and blushed.\n\n“I didn’t know you could skate, and skate so well.”\n\nShe looked at him earnestly, as though wishing to make out the cause of his confusion.\n\n“Your praise is worth having. The tradition is kept up here that you are the best of skaters,” she said, with her little black-gloved hand brushing a grain of hoarfrost off her muff.\n\n“Yes, I used once to skate with passion; I wanted to reach perfection.”\n\n“You do everything with passion, I think,” she said smiling. “I should so like to see how you skate. Put on skates, and let us skate together.”\n\n“Skate together! Can that be possible?” thought Levin, gazing at her.\n\n“I’ll put them on directly,” he said.\n\nAnd he went off to get skates.\n\n“It’s a long while since we’ve seen you here, sir,” said the attendant, supporting his foot, and screwing on the heel of the skate. “Except you, there’s none of the gentlemen first-rate skaters. Will that be all right?” said he, tightening the strap.\n\n“Oh, yes, yes; make haste, please,” answered Levin, with difficulty restraining the smile of rapture which would overspread his face. “Yes,” he thought, “this now is life, this is happiness! Together, she said; let us skate together! Speak to her now? But that’s just why I’m afraid to speak—because I’m happy now, happy in hope, anyway.... And then?... But I must! I must! I must! Away with weakness!”\n\nLevin rose to his feet, took off his overcoat, and scurrying over the rough ice round the hut, came out on the smooth ice and skated without effort, as it were, by simple exercise of will, increasing and slackening speed and turning his course. He approached with timidity, but again her smile reassured him.\n\nShe gave him her hand, and they set off side by side, going faster and faster, and the more rapidly they moved the more tightly she grasped his hand.\n\n“With you I should soon learn; I somehow feel confidence in you,” she said to him.\n\n“And I have confidence in myself when you are leaning on me,” he said, but was at once panic-stricken at what he had said, and blushed. And indeed, no sooner had he uttered these words, when all at once, like the sun going behind a cloud, her face lost all its friendliness, and Levin detected the familiar change in her expression that denoted the working of thought; a crease showed on her smooth brow.\n\n“Is there anything troubling you?—though I’ve no right to ask such a question,” he added hurriedly.\n\n“Oh, why so?... No, I have nothing to trouble me,” she responded coldly; and she added immediately: “You haven’t seen Mlle. Linon, have you?”\n\n“Not yet.”\n\n“Go and speak to her, she likes you so much.”\n\n“What’s wrong? I have offended her. Lord help me!” thought Levin, and he flew towards the old Frenchwoman with the gray ringlets, who was sitting on a bench. Smiling and showing her false teeth, she greeted him as an old friend.\n\n“Yes, you see we’re growing up,” she said to him, glancing towards Kitty, “and growing old. Tiny bear has grown big now!” pursued the Frenchwoman, laughing, and she reminded him of his joke about the three young ladies whom he had compared to the three bears in the English nursery tale. “Do you remember that’s what you used to call them?”\n\nHe remembered absolutely nothing, but she had been laughing at the joke for ten years now, and was fond of it.\n\n“Now, go and skate, go and skate. Our Kitty has learned to skate nicely, hasn’t she?”\n\nWhen Levin darted up to Kitty her face was no longer stern; her eyes looked at him with the same sincerity and friendliness, but Levin fancied that in her friendliness there was a certain note of deliberate composure. And he felt depressed. After talking a little of her old governess and her peculiarities, she questioned him about his life.\n\n“Surely you must be dull in the country in the winter, aren’t you?” she said.\n\n“No, I’m not dull, I am very busy,” he said, feeling that she was holding him in check by her composed tone, which he would not have the force to break through, just as it had been at the beginning of the winter.\n\n“Are you going to stay in town long?” Kitty questioned him.\n\n“I don’t know,” he answered, not thinking of what he was saying. The thought that if he were held in check by her tone of quiet friendliness he would end by going back again without deciding anything came into his mind, and he resolved to make a struggle against it.\n\n“How is it you don’t know?”\n\n“I don’t know. It depends upon you,” he said, and was immediately horror-stricken at his own words.\n\nWhether it was that she had heard his words, or that she did not want to hear them, she made a sort of stumble, twice struck out, and hurriedly skated away from him. She skated up to Mlle. Linon, said something to her, and went towards the pavilion where the ladies took off their skates.\n\n“My God! what have I done! Merciful God! help me, guide me,” said Levin, praying inwardly, and at the same time, feeling a need of violent exercise, he skated about describing inner and outer circles.\n\nAt that moment one of the young men, the best of the skaters of the day, came out of the coffee-house in his skates, with a cigarette in his mouth. Taking a run, he dashed down the steps in his skates, crashing and bounding up and down. He flew down, and without even changing the position of his hands, skated away over the ice.\n\n“Ah, that’s a new trick!” said Levin, and he promptly ran up to the top to do this new trick.\n\n“Don’t break your neck! it needs practice!” Nikolay Shtcherbatsky shouted after him.\n\nLevin went to the steps, took a run from above as best he could, and dashed down, preserving his balance in this unwonted movement with his hands. On the last step he stumbled, but barely touching the ice with his hand, with a violent effort recovered himself, and skated off, laughing.\n\n“How splendid, how nice he is!” Kitty was thinking at that time, as she came out of the pavilion with Mlle. Linon, and looked towards him with a smile of quiet affection, as though he were a favorite brother. “And can it be my fault, can I have done anything wrong? They talk of flirtation. I know it’s not he that I love; but still I am happy with him, and he’s so jolly. Only, why did he say that?...” she mused.\n\nCatching sight of Kitty going away, and her mother meeting her at the steps, Levin, flushed from his rapid exercise, stood still and pondered a minute. He took off his skates, and overtook the mother and daughter at the entrance of the gardens.\n\n“Delighted to see you,” said Princess Shtcherbatskaya. “On Thursdays we are home, as always.”\n\n“Today, then?”\n\n“We shall be pleased to see you,” the princess said stiffly.\n\nThis stiffness hurt Kitty, and she could not resist the desire to smooth over her mother’s coldness. She turned her head, and with a smile said:\n\n“Good-bye till this evening.”\n\nAt that moment Stepan Arkadyevitch, his hat cocked on one side, with beaming face and eyes, strode into the garden like a conquering hero. But as he approached his mother-in-law, he responded in a mournful and crestfallen tone to her inquiries about Dolly’s health. After a little subdued and dejected conversation with his mother-in-law, he threw out his chest again, and put his arm in Levin’s.\n\n“Well, shall we set off?” he asked. “I’ve been thinking about you all this time, and I’m very, very glad you’ve come,” he said, looking him in the face with a significant air.\n\n“Yes, come along,” answered Levin in ecstasy, hearing unceasingly the sound of that voice saying, “Good-bye till this evening,” and seeing the smile with which it was said.\n\n“To the England or the Hermitage?”\n\n“I don’t mind which.”\n\n“All right, then, the England,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, selecting that restaurant because he owed more there than at the Hermitage, and consequently considered it mean to avoid it. “Have you got a sledge? That’s first-rate, for I sent my carriage home.”\n\nThe friends hardly spoke all the way. Levin was wondering what that change in Kitty’s expression had meant, and alternately assuring himself that there was hope, and falling into despair, seeing clearly that his hopes were insane, and yet all the while he felt himself quite another man, utterly unlike what he had been before her smile and those words, “Good-bye till this evening.”\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch was absorbed during the drive in composing the menu of the dinner.\n\n“You like turbot, don’t you?” he said to Levin as they were arriving.\n\n“Eh?” responded Levin. “Turbot? Yes, I’m awfully fond of turbot.”\n\n# Chapter 10\n\nWhen Levin went into the restaurant with Oblonsky, he could not help noticing a certain peculiarity of expression, as it were, a restrained radiance, about the face and whole figure of Stepan Arkadyevitch. Oblonsky took off his overcoat, and with his hat over one ear walked into the dining-room, giving directions to the Tatar waiters, who were clustered about him in evening coats, bearing napkins. Bowing to right and left to the people he met, and here as everywhere joyously greeting acquaintances, he went up to the sideboard for a preliminary appetizer of fish and vodka, and said to the painted Frenchwoman decked in ribbons, lace, and ringlets, behind the counter, something so amusing that even that Frenchwoman was moved to genuine laughter. Levin for his part refrained from taking any vodka simply because he felt such a loathing of that Frenchwoman, all made up, it seemed, of false hair, poudre de riz, and vinaigre de toilette. He made haste to move away from her, as from a dirty place. His whole soul was filled with memories of Kitty, and there was a smile of triumph and happiness shining in his eyes.\n\n“This way, your excellency, please. Your excellency won’t be disturbed here,” said a particularly pertinacious, white-headed old Tatar with immense hips and coat-tails gaping widely behind. “Walk in, your excellency,” he said to Levin; by way of showing his respect to Stepan Arkadyevitch, being attentive to his guest as well.\n\nInstantly flinging a fresh cloth over the round table under the bronze chandelier, though it already had a table cloth on it, he pushed up velvet chairs, and came to a standstill before Stepan Arkadyevitch with a napkin and a bill of fare in his hands, awaiting his commands.\n\n“If you prefer it, your excellency, a private room will be free directly; Prince Golistin with a lady. Fresh oysters have come in.”\n\n“Ah! oysters.”\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch became thoughtful.\n\n“How if we were to change our program, Levin?” he said, keeping his finger on the bill of fare. And his face expressed serious hesitation. “Are the oysters good? Mind now.”\n\n“They’re Flensburg, your excellency. We’ve no Ostend.”\n\n“Flensburg will do, but are they fresh?”\n\n“Only arrived yesterday.”\n\n“Well, then, how if we were to begin with oysters, and so change the whole program? Eh?”\n\n“It’s all the same to me. I should like cabbage soup and porridge better than anything; but of course there’s nothing like that here.”\n\n“Porridge à la Russe, your honor would like?” said the Tatar, bending down to Levin, like a nurse speaking to a child.\n\n“No, joking apart, whatever you choose is sure to be good. I’ve been skating, and I’m hungry. And don’t imagine,” he added, detecting a look of dissatisfaction on Oblonsky’s face, “that I shan’t appreciate your choice. I am fond of good things.”\n\n“I should hope so! After all, it’s one of the pleasures of life,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch. “Well, then, my friend, you give us two—or better say three—dozen oysters, clear soup with vegetables....”\n\n“Printanière,” prompted the Tatar. But Stepan Arkadyevitch apparently did not care to allow him the satisfaction of giving the French names of the dishes.\n\n“With vegetables in it, you know. Then turbot with thick sauce, then ... roast beef; and mind it’s good. Yes, and capons, perhaps, and then sweets.”\n\nThe Tatar, recollecting that it was Stepan Arkadyevitch’s way not to call the dishes by the names in the French bill of fare, did not repeat them after him, but could not resist rehearsing the whole menu to himself according to the bill:—“Soupe printanière, turbot, sauce Beaumarchais, poulard à l’estragon, macédoine de fruits ... etc.,” and then instantly, as though worked by springs, laying down one bound bill of fare, he took up another, the list of wines, and submitted it to Stepan Arkadyevitch.\n\n“What shall we drink?”\n\n“What you like, only not too much. Champagne,” said Levin.\n\n“What! to start with? You’re right though, I dare say. Do you like the white seal?”\n\n“Cachet blanc,” prompted the Tatar.\n\n“Very well, then, give us that brand with the oysters, and then we’ll see.”\n\n“Yes, sir. And what table wine?”\n\n“You can give us Nuits. Oh, no, better the classic Chablis.”\n\n“Yes, sir. And your cheese, your excellency?”\n\n“Oh, yes, Parmesan. Or would you like another?”\n\n“No, it’s all the same to me,” said Levin, unable to suppress a smile.\n\nAnd the Tatar ran off with flying coat-tails, and in five minutes darted in with a dish of opened oysters on mother-of-pearl shells, and a bottle between his fingers.\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch crushed the starchy napkin, tucked it into his waistcoat, and settling his arms comfortably, started on the oysters.\n\n“Not bad,” he said, stripping the oysters from the pearly shell with a silver fork, and swallowing them one after another. “Not bad,” he repeated, turning his dewy, brilliant eyes from Levin to the Tatar.\n\nLevin ate the oysters indeed, though white bread and cheese would have pleased him better. But he was admiring Oblonsky. Even the Tatar, uncorking the bottle and pouring the sparkling wine into the delicate glasses, glanced at Stepan Arkadyevitch, and settled his white cravat with a perceptible smile of satisfaction.\n\n“You don’t care much for oysters, do you?” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, emptying his wine-glass, “or you’re worried about something. Eh?”\n\nHe wanted Levin to be in good spirits. But it was not that Levin was not in good spirits; he was ill at ease. With what he had in his soul, he felt sore and uncomfortable in the restaurant, in the midst of private rooms where men were dining with ladies, in all this fuss and bustle; the surroundings of bronzes, looking-glasses, gas, and waiters—all of it was offensive to him. He was afraid of sullying what his soul was brimful of.\n\n“I? Yes, I am; but besides, all this bothers me,” he said. “You can’t conceive how queer it all seems to a country person like me, as queer as that gentleman’s nails I saw at your place....”\n\n“Yes, I saw how much interested you were in poor Grinevitch’s nails,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, laughing.\n\n“It’s too much for me,” responded Levin. “Do try, now, and put yourself in my place, take the point of view of a country person. We in the country try to bring our hands into such a state as will be most convenient for working with. So we cut our nails; sometimes we turn up our sleeves. And here people purposely let their nails grow as long as they will, and link on small saucers by way of studs, so that they can do nothing with their hands.”\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch smiled gaily.\n\n“Oh, yes, that’s just a sign that he has no need to do coarse work. His work is with the mind....”\n\n“Maybe. But still it’s queer to me, just as at this moment it seems queer to me that we country folks try to get our meals over as soon as we can, so as to be ready for our work, while here are we trying to drag out our meal as long as possible, and with that object eating oysters....”\n\n“Why, of course,” objected Stepan Arkadyevitch. “But that’s just the aim of civilization—to make everything a source of enjoyment.”\n\n“Well, if that’s its aim, I’d rather be a savage.”\n\n“And so you are a savage. All you Levins are savages.”\n\nLevin sighed. He remembered his brother Nikolay, and felt ashamed and sore, and he scowled; but Oblonsky began speaking of a subject which at once drew his attention.\n\n“Oh, I say, are you going tonight to our people, the Shtcherbatskys’, I mean?” he said, his eyes sparkling significantly as he pushed away the empty rough shells, and drew the cheese towards him.\n\n“Yes, I shall certainly go,” replied Levin; “though I fancied the princess was not very warm in her invitation.”\n\n“What nonsense! That’s her manner.... Come, boy, the soup!... That’s her manner—grande dame,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch. “I’m coming, too, but I have to go to the Countess Bonina’s rehearsal. Come, isn’t it true that you’re a savage? How do you explain the sudden way in which you vanished from Moscow? The Shtcherbatskys were continually asking me about you, as though I ought to know. The only thing I know is that you always do what no one else does.”\n\n“Yes,” said Levin, slowly and with emotion, “you’re right. I am a savage. Only, my savageness is not in having gone away, but in coming now. Now I have come....”\n\n“Oh, what a lucky fellow you are!” broke in Stepan Arkadyevitch, looking into Levin’s eyes.\n\n“Why?”\n\nAnd by his eyes I know a youth in love,’”\ndeclaimed Stepan Arkadyevitch. “Everything is before you.”\n\n“Why, is it over for you already?”\n\n“No; not over exactly, but the future is yours, and the present is mine, and the present—well, it’s not all that it might be.”\n\n“How so?”\n\n“Oh, things go wrong. But I don’t want to talk of myself, and besides I can’t explain it all,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch. “Well, why have you come to Moscow, then?... Hi! take away!” he called to the Tatar.\n\n“You guess?” responded Levin, his eyes like deep wells of light fixed on Stepan Arkadyevitch.\n\n“I guess, but I can’t be the first to talk about it. You can see by that whether I guess right or wrong,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, gazing at Levin with a subtle smile.\n\n“Well, and what have you to say to me?” said Levin in a quivering voice, feeling that all the muscles of his face were quivering too. “How do you look at the question?”\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch slowly emptied his glass of Chablis, never taking his eyes off Levin.\n\n“I?” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, “there’s nothing I desire so much as that—nothing! It would be the best thing that could be.”\n\n“But you’re not making a mistake? You know what we’re speaking of?” said Levin, piercing him with his eyes. “You think it’s possible?”\n\n“I think it’s possible. Why not possible?”\n\n“No! do you really think it’s possible? No, tell me all you think! Oh, but if ... if refusal’s in store for me!... Indeed I feel sure....”\n\n“Why should you think that?” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, smiling at his excitement.\n\n“It seems so to me sometimes. That will be awful for me, and for her too.”\n\n“Oh, well, anyway there’s nothing awful in it for a girl. Every girl’s proud of an offer.”\n\n“Yes, every girl, but not she.”\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch smiled. He so well knew that feeling of Levin’s, that for him all the girls in the world were divided into two classes: one class—all the girls in the world except her, and those girls with all sorts of human weaknesses, and very ordinary girls: the other class—she alone, having no weaknesses of any sort and higher than all humanity.\n\n“Stay, take some sauce,” he said, holding back Levin’s hand as it pushed away the sauce.\n\nLevin obediently helped himself to sauce, but would not let Stepan Arkadyevitch go on with his dinner.\n\n“No, stop a minute, stop a minute,” he said. “You must understand that it’s a question of life and death for me. I have never spoken to anyone of this. And there’s no one I could speak of it to, except you. You know we’re utterly unlike each other, different tastes and views and everything; but I know you’re fond of me and understand me, and that’s why I like you awfully. But for God’s sake, be quite straightforward with me.”\n\n“I tell you what I think,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, smiling. “But I’ll say more: my wife is a wonderful woman....” Stepan Arkadyevitch sighed, remembering his position with his wife, and, after a moment’s silence, resumed—“She has a gift of foreseeing things. She sees right through people; but that’s not all; she knows what will come to pass, especially in the way of marriages. She foretold, for instance, that Princess Shahovskaya would marry Brenteln. No one would believe it, but it came to pass. And she’s on your side.”\n\n“How do you mean?”\n\n“It’s not only that she likes you—she says that Kitty is certain to be your wife.”\n\nAt these words Levin’s face suddenly lighted up with a smile, a smile not far from tears of emotion.\n\n“She says that!” cried Levin. “I always said she was exquisite, your wife. There, that’s enough, enough said about it,” he said, getting up from his seat.\n\n“All right, but do sit down.”\n\nBut Levin could not sit down. He walked with his firm tread twice up and down the little cage of a room, blinked his eyelids that his tears might not fall, and only then sat down to the table.\n\n“You must understand,” said he, “it’s not love. I’ve been in love, but it’s not that. It’s not my feeling, but a sort of force outside me has taken possession of me. I went away, you see, because I made up my mind that it could never be, you understand, as a happiness that does not come on earth; but I’ve struggled with myself, I see there’s no living without it. And it must be settled.”\n\n“What did you go away for?”\n\n“Ah, stop a minute! Ah, the thoughts that come crowding on one! The questions one must ask oneself! Listen. You can’t imagine what you’ve done for me by what you said. I’m so happy that I’ve become positively hateful; I’ve forgotten everything. I heard today that my brother Nikolay ... you know, he’s here ... I had even forgotten him. It seems to me that he’s happy too. It’s a sort of madness. But one thing’s awful.... Here, you’ve been married, you know the feeling ... it’s awful that we—old—with a past ... not of love, but of sins ... are brought all at once so near to a creature pure and innocent; it’s loathsome, and that’s why one can’t help feeling oneself unworthy.”\n\n“Oh, well, you’ve not many sins on your conscience.”\n\n“Alas! all the same,” said Levin, “when with loathing I go over my life, I shudder and curse and bitterly regret it.... Yes.”\n\n“What would you have? The world’s made so,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch.\n\n“The one comfort is like that prayer, which I always liked: ‘Forgive me not according to my unworthiness, but according to Thy loving-kindness.’ That’s the only way she can forgive me.”\n\nLevin emptied his glass, and they were silent for a while.\n\n“There’s one other thing I ought to tell you. Do you know Vronsky?” Stepan Arkadyevitch asked Levin.\n\n“No, I don’t. Why do you ask?”\n\n“Give us another bottle,” Stepan Arkadyevitch directed the Tatar, who was filling up their glasses and fidgeting round them just when he was not wanted.\n\n“Why you ought to know Vronsky is that he’s one of your rivals.”\n\n“Who’s Vronsky?” said Levin, and his face was suddenly transformed from the look of childlike ecstasy which Oblonsky had just been admiring to an angry and unpleasant expression.\n\n“Vronsky is one of the sons of Count Kirill Ivanovitch Vronsky, and one of the finest specimens of the gilded youth of Petersburg. I made his acquaintance in Tver when I was there on official business, and he came there for the levy of recruits. Fearfully rich, handsome, great connections, an aide-de-camp, and with all that a very nice, good-natured fellow. But he’s more than simply a good-natured fellow, as I’ve found out here—he’s a cultivated man, too, and very intelligent; he’s a man who’ll make his mark.”\n\nLevin scowled and was dumb.\n\n“Well, he turned up here soon after you’d gone, and as I can see, he’s over head and ears in love with Kitty, and you know that her mother....”\n\n“Excuse me, but I know nothing,” said Levin, frowning gloomily. And immediately he recollected his brother Nikolay and how hateful he was to have been able to forget him.\n\n“You wait a bit, wait a bit,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, smiling and touching his hand. “I’ve told you what I know, and I repeat that in this delicate and tender matter, as far as one can conjecture, I believe the chances are in your favor.”\n\nLevin dropped back in his chair; his face was pale.\n\n“But I would advise you to settle the thing as soon as may be,” pursued Oblonsky, filling up his glass.\n\n“No, thanks, I can’t drink any more,” said Levin, pushing away his glass. “I shall be drunk.... Come, tell me how are you getting on?” he went on, obviously anxious to change the conversation.\n\n“One word more: in any case I advise you to settle the question soon. Tonight I don’t advise you to speak,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch. “Go round tomorrow morning, make an offer in due form, and God bless you....”\n\n“Oh, do you still think of coming to me for some shooting? Come next spring, do,” said Levin.\n\nNow his whole soul was full of remorse that he had begun this conversation with Stepan Arkadyevitch. A feeling such as his was profaned by talk of the rivalry of some Petersburg officer, of the suppositions and the counsels of Stepan Arkadyevitch.\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch smiled. He knew what was passing in Levin’s soul.\n\n“I’ll come some day,” he said. “But women, my boy, they’re the pivot everything turns upon. Things are in a bad way with me, very bad. And it’s all through women. Tell me frankly now,” he pursued, picking up a cigar and keeping one hand on his glass; “give me your advice.”\n\n“Why, what is it?”\n\n“I’ll tell you. Suppose you’re married, you love your wife, but you’re fascinated by another woman....”\n\n“Excuse me, but I’m absolutely unable to comprehend how ... just as I can’t comprehend how I could now, after my dinner, go straight to a baker’s shop and steal a roll.”\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch’s eyes sparkled more than usual.\n\n“Why not? A roll will sometimes smell so good one can’t resist it.”\n\n“Himmlisch ist’s, wenn ich bezwungen\nMeine irdische Begier;\n\nAber doch wenn’s nich gelungen\nHatt’ ich auch recht hübsch Plaisir!”\n\nAs he said this, Stepan Arkadyevitch smiled subtly. Levin, too, could not help smiling.\n\n“Yes, but joking apart,” resumed Stepan Arkadyevitch, “you must understand that the woman is a sweet, gentle loving creature, poor and lonely, and has sacrificed everything. Now, when the thing’s done, don’t you see, can one possibly cast her off? Even supposing one parts from her, so as not to break up one’s family life, still, can one help feeling for her, setting her on her feet, softening her lot?”\n\n“Well, you must excuse me there. You know to me all women are divided into two classes ... at least no ... truer to say: there are women and there are ... I’ve never seen exquisite fallen beings, and I never shall see them, but such creatures as that painted Frenchwoman at the counter with the ringlets are vermin to my mind, and all fallen women are the same.”\n\n“But the Magdalen?”\n\n“Ah, drop that! Christ would never have said those words if He had known how they would be abused. Of all the Gospel those words are the only ones remembered. However, I’m not saying so much what I think, as what I feel. I have a loathing for fallen women. You’re afraid of spiders, and I of these vermin. Most likely you’ve not made a study of spiders and don’t know their character; and so it is with me.”\n\n“It’s very well for you to talk like that; it’s very much like that gentleman in Dickens who used to fling all difficult questions over his right shoulder. But to deny the facts is no answer. What’s to be done—you tell me that, what’s to be done? Your wife gets older, while you’re full of life. Before you’ve time to look round, you feel that you can’t love your wife with love, however much you may esteem her. And then all at once love turns up, and you’re done for, done for,” Stepan Arkadyevitch said with weary despair.\n\nLevin half smiled.\n\n“Yes, you’re done for,” resumed Oblonsky. “But what’s to be done?”\n\n“Don’t steal rolls.”\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch laughed outright.\n\n“Oh, moralist! But you must understand, there are two women; one insists only on her rights, and those rights are your love, which you can’t give her; and the other sacrifices everything for you and asks for nothing. What are you to do? How are you to act? There’s a fearful tragedy in it.”\n\n“If you care for my profession of faith as regards that, I’ll tell you that I don’t believe there was any tragedy about it. And this is why. To my mind, love ... both the sorts of love, which you remember Plato defines in his Banquet, served as the test of men. Some men only understand one sort, and some only the other. And those who only know the non-platonic love have no need to talk of tragedy. In such love there can be no sort of tragedy. ‘I’m much obliged for the gratification, my humble respects’—that’s all the tragedy. And in platonic love there can be no tragedy, because in that love all is clear and pure, because....”\n\nAt that instant Levin recollected his own sins and the inner conflict he had lived through. And he added unexpectedly:\n\n“But perhaps you are right. Very likely ... I don’t know, I don’t know.”\n\n“It’s this, don’t you see,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, “you’re very much all of a piece. That’s your strong point and your failing. You have a character that’s all of a piece, and you want the whole of life to be of a piece too—but that’s not how it is. You despise public official work because you want the reality to be invariably corresponding all the while with the aim—and that’s not how it is. You want a man’s work, too, always to have a defined aim, and love and family life always to be undivided—and that’s not how it is. All the variety, all the charm, all the beauty of life is made up of light and shadow.”\n\nLevin sighed and made no reply. He was thinking of his own affairs, and did not hear Oblonsky.\n\nAnd suddenly both of them felt that though they were friends, though they had been dining and drinking together, which should have drawn them closer, yet each was thinking only of his own affairs, and they had nothing to do with one another. Oblonsky had more than once experienced this extreme sense of aloofness, instead of intimacy, coming on after dinner, and he knew what to do in such cases.\n\n“Bill!” he called, and he went into the next room where he promptly came across an aide-de-camp of his acquaintance and dropped into conversation with him about an actress and her protector. And at once in the conversation with the aide-de-camp Oblonsky had a sense of relaxation and relief after the conversation with Levin, which always put him to too great a mental and spiritual strain.\n\nWhen the Tatar appeared with a bill for twenty-six roubles and odd kopecks, besides a tip for himself, Levin, who would another time have been horrified, like anyone from the country, at his share of fourteen roubles, did not notice it, paid, and set off homewards to dress and go to the Shtcherbatskys’ there to decide his fate.\n\n# Chapter 12\n\nThe young Princess Kitty Shtcherbatskaya was eighteen. It was the first winter that she had been out in the world. Her success in society had been greater than that of either of her elder sisters, and greater even than her mother had anticipated. To say nothing of the young men who danced at the Moscow balls being almost all in love with Kitty, two serious suitors had already this first winter made their appearance: Levin, and immediately after his departure, Count Vronsky.\n\nLevin’s appearance at the beginning of the winter, his frequent visits, and evident love for Kitty, had led to the first serious conversations between Kitty’s parents as to her future, and to disputes between them. The prince was on Levin’s side; he said he wished for nothing better for Kitty. The princess for her part, going round the question in the manner peculiar to women, maintained that Kitty was too young, that Levin had done nothing to prove that he had serious intentions, that Kitty felt no great attraction to him, and other side issues; but she did not state the principal point, which was that she looked for a better match for her daughter, and that Levin was not to her liking, and she did not understand him. When Levin had abruptly departed, the princess was delighted, and said to her husband triumphantly: “You see I was right.” When Vronsky appeared on the scene, she was still more delighted, confirmed in her opinion that Kitty was to make not simply a good, but a brilliant match.\n\nIn the mother’s eyes there could be no comparison between Vronsky and Levin. She disliked in Levin his strange and uncompromising opinions and his shyness in society, founded, as she supposed, on his pride and his queer sort of life, as she considered it, absorbed in cattle and peasants. She did not very much like it that he, who was in love with her daughter, had kept coming to the house for six weeks, as though he were waiting for something, inspecting, as though he were afraid he might be doing them too great an honor by making an offer, and did not realize that a man, who continually visits at a house where there is a young unmarried girl, is bound to make his intentions clear. And suddenly, without doing so, he disappeared. “It’s as well he’s not attractive enough for Kitty to have fallen in love with him,” thought the mother.\n\nVronsky satisfied all the mother’s desires. Very wealthy, clever, of aristocratic family, on the highroad to a brilliant career in the army and at court, and a fascinating man. Nothing better could be wished for.\n\nVronsky openly flirted with Kitty at balls, danced with her, and came continually to the house, consequently there could be no doubt of the seriousness of his intentions. But, in spite of that, the mother had spent the whole of that winter in a state of terrible anxiety and agitation.\n\nPrincess Shtcherbatskaya had herself been married thirty years ago, her aunt arranging the match. Her husband, about whom everything was well known beforehand, had come, looked at his future bride, and been looked at. The matchmaking aunt had ascertained and communicated their mutual impression. That impression had been favorable. Afterwards, on a day fixed beforehand, the expected offer was made to her parents, and accepted. All had passed very simply and easily. So it seemed, at least, to the princess. But over her own daughters she had felt how far from simple and easy is the business, apparently so commonplace, of marrying off one’s daughters. The panics that had been lived through, the thoughts that had been brooded over, the money that had been wasted, and the disputes with her husband over marrying the two elder girls, Darya and Natalia! Now, since the youngest had come out, she was going through the same terrors, the same doubts, and still more violent quarrels with her husband than she had over the elder girls. The old prince, like all fathers indeed, was exceedingly punctilious on the score of the honor and reputation of his daughters. He was irrationally jealous over his daughters, especially over Kitty, who was his favorite. At every turn he had scenes with the princess for compromising her daughter. The princess had grown accustomed to this already with her other daughters, but now she felt that there was more ground for the prince’s touchiness. She saw that of late years much was changed in the manners of society, that a mother’s duties had become still more difficult. She saw that girls of Kitty’s age formed some sort of clubs, went to some sort of lectures, mixed freely in men’s society; drove about the streets alone, many of them did not curtsey, and, what was the most important thing, all the girls were firmly convinced that to choose their husbands was their own affair, and not their parents’. “Marriages aren’t made nowadays as they used to be,” was thought and said by all these young girls, and even by their elders. But how marriages were made now, the princess could not learn from anyone. The French fashion—of the parents arranging their children’s future—was not accepted; it was condemned. The English fashion of the complete independence of girls was also not accepted, and not possible in Russian society. The Russian fashion of matchmaking by the offices of intermediate persons was for some reason considered unseemly; it was ridiculed by everyone, and by the princess herself. But how girls were to be married, and how parents were to marry them, no one knew. Everyone with whom the princess had chanced to discuss the matter said the same thing: “Mercy on us, it’s high time in our day to cast off all that old-fashioned business. It’s the young people have to marry; and not their parents; and so we ought to leave the young people to arrange it as they choose.” It was very easy for anyone to say that who had no daughters, but the princess realized that in the process of getting to know each other, her daughter might fall in love, and fall in love with someone who did not care to marry her or who was quite unfit to be her husband. And, however much it was instilled into the princess that in our times young people ought to arrange their lives for themselves, she was unable to believe it, just as she would have been unable to believe that, at any time whatever, the most suitable playthings for children five years old ought to be loaded pistols. And so the princess was more uneasy over Kitty than she had been over her elder sisters.\n\nNow she was afraid that Vronsky might confine himself to simply flirting with her daughter. She saw that her daughter was in love with him, but tried to comfort herself with the thought that he was an honorable man, and would not do this. But at the same time she knew how easy it is, with the freedom of manners of today, to turn a girl’s head, and how lightly men generally regard such a crime. The week before, Kitty had told her mother of a conversation she had with Vronsky during a mazurka. This conversation had partly reassured the princess; but perfectly at ease she could not be. Vronsky had told Kitty that both he and his brother were so used to obeying their mother that they never made up their minds to any important undertaking without consulting her. “And just now, I am impatiently awaiting my mother’s arrival from Petersburg, as peculiarly fortunate,” he told her.\n\nKitty had repeated this without attaching any significance to the words. But her mother saw them in a different light. She knew that the old lady was expected from day to day, that she would be pleased at her son’s choice, and she felt it strange that he should not make his offer through fear of vexing his mother. However, she was so anxious for the marriage itself, and still more for relief from her fears, that she believed it was so. Bitter as it was for the princess to see the unhappiness of her eldest daughter, Dolly, on the point of leaving her husband, her anxiety over the decision of her youngest daughter’s fate engrossed all her feelings. Today, with Levin’s reappearance, a fresh source of anxiety arose. She was afraid that her daughter, who had at one time, as she fancied, a feeling for Levin, might, from extreme sense of honor, refuse Vronsky, and that Levin’s arrival might generally complicate and delay the affair so near being concluded.\n\n“Why, has he been here long?” the princess asked about Levin, as they returned home.\n\n“He came today, mamma.”\n\n“There’s one thing I want to say....” began the princess, and from her serious and alert face, Kitty guessed what it would be.\n\n“Mamma,” she said, flushing hotly and turning quickly to her, “please, please don’t say anything about that. I know, I know all about it.”\n\nShe wished for what her mother wished for, but the motives of her mother’s wishes wounded her.\n\n“I only want to say that to raise hopes....”\n\n“Mamma, darling, for goodness’ sake, don’t talk about it. It’s so horrible to talk about it.”\n\n“I won’t,” said her mother, seeing the tears in her daughter’s eyes; “but one thing, my love; you promised me you would have no secrets from me. You won’t?”\n\n“Never, mamma, none,” answered Kitty, flushing a little, and looking her mother straight in the face, “but there’s no use in my telling you anything, and I ... I ... if I wanted to, I don’t know what to say or how.... I don’t know....”\n\n“No, she could not tell an untruth with those eyes,” thought the mother, smiling at her agitation and happiness. The princess smiled that what was taking place just now in her soul seemed to the poor child so immense and so important.\n\n# Chapter 13\n\nAfter dinner, and till the beginning of the evening, Kitty was feeling a sensation akin to the sensation of a young man before a battle. Her heart throbbed violently, and her thoughts would not rest on anything.\n\nShe felt that this evening, when they would both meet for the first time, would be a turning point in her life. And she was continually picturing them to herself, at one moment each separately, and then both together. When she mused on the past, she dwelt with pleasure, with tenderness, on the memories of her relations with Levin. The memories of childhood and of Levin’s friendship with her dead brother gave a special poetic charm to her relations with him. His love for her, of which she felt certain, was flattering and delightful to her; and it was pleasant for her to think of Levin. In her memories of Vronsky there always entered a certain element of awkwardness, though he was in the highest degree well-bred and at ease, as though there were some false note—not in Vronsky, he was very simple and nice, but in herself, while with Levin she felt perfectly simple and clear. But, on the other hand, directly she thought of the future with Vronsky, there arose before her a perspective of brilliant happiness; with Levin the future seemed misty.\n\nWhen she went upstairs to dress, and looked into the looking-glass, she noticed with joy that it was one of her good days, and that she was in complete possession of all her forces,—she needed this so for what lay before her: she was conscious of external composure and free grace in her movements.\n\nAt half-past seven she had only just gone down into the drawing-room, when the footman announced, “Konstantin Dmitrievitch Levin.” The princess was still in her room, and the prince had not come in. “So it is to be,” thought Kitty, and all the blood seemed to rush to her heart. She was horrified at her paleness, as she glanced into the looking-glass. At that moment she knew beyond doubt that he had come early on purpose to find her alone and to make her an offer. And only then for the first time the whole thing presented itself in a new, different aspect; only then she realized that the question did not affect her only—with whom she would be happy, and whom she loved—but that she would have that moment to wound a man whom she liked. And to wound him cruelly. What for? Because he, dear fellow, loved her, was in love with her. But there was no help for it, so it must be, so it would have to be.\n\n“My God! shall I myself really have to say it to him?” she thought. “Can I tell him I don’t love him? That will be a lie. What am I to say to him? That I love someone else? No, that’s impossible. I’m going away, I’m going away.”\n\nShe had reached the door, when she heard his step. “No! it’s not honest. What have I to be afraid of? I have done nothing wrong. What is to be, will be! I’ll tell the truth. And with him one can’t be ill at ease. Here he is,” she said to herself, seeing his powerful, shy figure, with his shining eyes fixed on her. She looked straight into his face, as though imploring him to spare her, and gave her hand.\n\n“It’s not time yet; I think I’m too early,” he said glancing round the empty drawing-room. When he saw that his expectations were realized, that there was nothing to prevent him from speaking, his face became gloomy.\n\n“Oh, no,” said Kitty, and sat down at the table.\n\n“But this was just what I wanted, to find you alone,” he began, not sitting down, and not looking at her, so as not to lose courage.\n\n“Mamma will be down directly. She was very much tired.... Yesterday....”\n\nShe talked on, not knowing what her lips were uttering, and not taking her supplicating and caressing eyes off him.\n\nHe glanced at her; she blushed, and ceased speaking.\n\n“I told you I did not know whether I should be here long ... that it depended on you....”\n\nShe dropped her head lower and lower, not knowing herself what answer she should make to what was coming.\n\n“That it depended on you,” he repeated. “I meant to say ... I meant to say ... I came for this ... to be my wife!” he brought out, not knowing what he was saying; but feeling that the most terrible thing was said, he stopped short and looked at her....\n\nShe was breathing heavily, not looking at him. She was feeling ecstasy. Her soul was flooded with happiness. She had never anticipated that the utterance of love would produce such a powerful effect on her. But it lasted only an instant. She remembered Vronsky. She lifted her clear, truthful eyes, and seeing his desperate face, she answered hastily:\n\n“That cannot be ... forgive me.”\n\nA moment ago, and how close she had been to him, of what importance in his life! And how aloof and remote from him she had become now!\n\n“It was bound to be so,” he said, not looking at her.\n\nHe bowed, and was meaning to retreat.\n\n# Chapter 14\n\nBut at that very moment the princess came in. There was a look of horror on her face when she saw them alone, and their disturbed faces. Levin bowed to her, and said nothing. Kitty did not speak nor lift her eyes. “Thank God, she has refused him,” thought the mother, and her face lighted up with the habitual smile with which she greeted her guests on Thursdays. She sat down and began questioning Levin about his life in the country. He sat down again, waiting for other visitors to arrive, in order to retreat unnoticed.\n\nFive minutes later there came in a friend of Kitty’s, married the preceding winter, Countess Nordston.\n\nShe was a thin, sallow, sickly, and nervous woman, with brilliant black eyes. She was fond of Kitty, and her affection for her showed itself, as the affection of married women for girls always does, in the desire to make a match for Kitty after her own ideal of married happiness; she wanted her to marry Vronsky. Levin she had often met at the Shtcherbatskys’ early in the winter, and she had always disliked him. Her invariable and favorite pursuit, when they met, consisted in making fun of him.\n\n“I do like it when he looks down at me from the height of his grandeur, or breaks off his learned conversation with me because I’m a fool, or is condescending to me. I like that so; to see him condescending! I am so glad he can’t bear me,” she used to say of him.\n\nShe was right, for Levin actually could not bear her, and despised her for what she was proud of and regarded as a fine characteristic—her nervousness, her delicate contempt and indifference for everything coarse and earthly.\n\nThe Countess Nordston and Levin got into that relation with one another not seldom seen in society, when two persons, who remain externally on friendly terms, despise each other to such a degree that they cannot even take each other seriously, and cannot even be offended by each other.\n\nThe Countess Nordston pounced upon Levin at once.\n\n“Ah, Konstantin Dmitrievitch! So you’ve come back to our corrupt Babylon,” she said, giving him her tiny, yellow hand, and recalling what he had chanced to say early in the winter, that Moscow was a Babylon. “Come, is Babylon reformed, or have you degenerated?” she added, glancing with a simper at Kitty.\n\n“It’s very flattering for me, countess, that you remember my words so well,” responded Levin, who had succeeded in recovering his composure, and at once from habit dropped into his tone of joking hostility to the Countess Nordston. “They must certainly make a great impression on you.”\n\n“Oh, I should think so! I always note them all down. Well, Kitty, have you been skating again?...”\n\nAnd she began talking to Kitty. Awkward as it was for Levin to withdraw now, it would still have been easier for him to perpetrate this awkwardness than to remain all the evening and see Kitty, who glanced at him now and then and avoided his eyes. He was on the point of getting up, when the princess, noticing that he was silent, addressed him.\n\n“Shall you be long in Moscow? You’re busy with the district council, though, aren’t you, and can’t be away for long?”\n\n“No, princess, I’m no longer a member of the council,” he said. “I have come up for a few days.”\n\n“There’s something the matter with him,” thought Countess Nordston, glancing at his stern, serious face. “He isn’t in his old argumentative mood. But I’ll draw him out. I do love making a fool of him before Kitty, and I’ll do it.”\n\n“Konstantin Dmitrievitch,” she said to him, “do explain to me, please, what’s the meaning of it. You know all about such things. At home in our village of Kaluga all the peasants and all the women have drunk up all they possessed, and now they can’t pay us any rent. What’s the meaning of that? You always praise the peasants so.”\n\nAt that instant another lady came into the room, and Levin got up.\n\n“Excuse me, countess, but I really know nothing about it, and can’t tell you anything,” he said, and looked round at the officer who came in behind the lady.\n\n“That must be Vronsky,” thought Levin, and, to be sure of it, glanced at Kitty. She had already had time to look at Vronsky, and looked round at Levin. And simply from the look in her eyes, that grew unconsciously brighter, Levin knew that she loved that man, knew it as surely as if she had told him so in words. But what sort of a man was he? Now, whether for good or for ill, Levin could not choose but remain; he must find out what the man was like whom she loved.\n\nThere are people who, on meeting a successful rival, no matter in what, are at once disposed to turn their backs on everything good in him, and to see only what is bad. There are people, on the other hand, who desire above all to find in that lucky rival the qualities by which he has outstripped them, and seek with a throbbing ache at heart only what is good. Levin belonged to the second class. But he had no difficulty in finding what was good and attractive in Vronsky. It was apparent at the first glance. Vronsky was a squarely built, dark man, not very tall, with a good-humored, handsome, and exceedingly calm and resolute face. Everything about his face and figure, from his short-cropped black hair and freshly shaven chin down to his loosely fitting, brand-new uniform, was simple and at the same time elegant. Making way for the lady who had come in, Vronsky went up to the princess and then to Kitty.\n\nAs he approached her, his beautiful eyes shone with a specially tender light, and with a faint, happy, and modestly triumphant smile (so it seemed to Levin), bowing carefully and respectfully over her, he held out his small broad hand to her.\n\nGreeting and saying a few words to everyone, he sat down without once glancing at Levin, who had never taken his eyes off him.\n\n“Let me introduce you,” said the princess, indicating Levin. “Konstantin Dmitrievitch Levin, Count Alexey Kirillovitch Vronsky.”\n\nVronsky got up and, looking cordially at Levin, shook hands with him.\n\n“I believe I was to have dined with you this winter,” he said, smiling his simple and open smile; “but you had unexpectedly left for the country.”\n\n“Konstantin Dmitrievitch despises and hates town and us townspeople,” said Countess Nordston.\n\n“My words must make a deep impression on you, since you remember them so well,” said Levin, and, suddenly conscious that he had said just the same thing before, he reddened.\n\nVronsky looked at Levin and Countess Nordston, and smiled.\n\n“Are you always in the country?” he inquired. “I should think it must be dull in the winter.”\n\n“It’s not dull if one has work to do; besides, one’s not dull by oneself,” Levin replied abruptly.\n\n“I am fond of the country,” said Vronsky, noticing, and affecting not to notice, Levin’s tone.\n\n“But I hope, count, you would not consent to live in the country always,” said Countess Nordston.\n\n“I don’t know; I have never tried for long. I experienced a queer feeling once,” he went on. “I never longed so for the country, Russian country, with bast shoes and peasants, as when I was spending a winter with my mother in Nice. Nice itself is dull enough, you know. And indeed, Naples and Sorrento are only pleasant for a short time. And it’s just there that Russia comes back to me most vividly, and especially the country. It’s as though....”\n\nHe talked on, addressing both Kitty and Levin, turning his serene, friendly eyes from one to the other, and saying obviously just what came into his head.\n\nNoticing that Countess Nordston wanted to say something, he stopped short without finishing what he had begun, and listened attentively to her.\n\nThe conversation did not flag for an instant, so that the princess, who always kept in reserve, in case a subject should be lacking, two heavy guns—the relative advantages of classical and of modern education, and universal military service—had not to move out either of them, while Countess Nordston had not a chance of chaffing Levin.\n\nLevin wanted to, and could not, take part in the general conversation; saying to himself every instant, “Now go,” he still did not go, as though waiting for something.\n\nThe conversation fell upon table-turning and spirits, and Countess Nordston, who believed in spiritualism, began to describe the marvels she had seen.\n\n“Ah, countess, you really must take me, for pity’s sake do take me to see them! I have never seen anything extraordinary, though I am always on the lookout for it everywhere,” said Vronsky, smiling.\n\n“Very well, next Saturday,” answered Countess Nordston. “But you, Konstantin Dmitrievitch, do you believe in it?” she asked Levin.\n\n“Why do you ask me? You know what I shall say.”\n\n“But I want to hear your opinion.”\n\n“My opinion,” answered Levin, “is only that this table-turning simply proves that educated society—so called—is no higher than the peasants. They believe in the evil eye, and in witchcraft and omens, while we....”\n\n“Oh, then you don’t believe in it?”\n\n“I can’t believe in it, countess.”\n\n“But if I’ve seen it myself?”\n\n“The peasant women too tell us they have seen goblins.”\n\n“Then you think I tell a lie?”\n\nAnd she laughed a mirthless laugh.\n\n“Oh, no, Masha, Konstantin Dmitrievitch said he could not believe in it,” said Kitty, blushing for Levin, and Levin saw this, and, still more exasperated, would have answered, but Vronsky with his bright frank smile rushed to the support of the conversation, which was threatening to become disagreeable.\n\n“You do not admit the conceivability at all?” he queried. “But why not? We admit the existence of electricity, of which we know nothing. Why should there not be some new force, still unknown to us, which....”\n\n“When electricity was discovered,” Levin interrupted hurriedly, “it was only the phenomenon that was discovered, and it was unknown from what it proceeded and what were its effects, and ages passed before its applications were conceived. But the spiritualists have begun with tables writing for them, and spirits appearing to them, and have only later started saying that it is an unknown force.”\n\nVronsky listened attentively to Levin, as he always did listen, obviously interested in his words.\n\n“Yes, but the spiritualists say we don’t know at present what this force is, but there is a force, and these are the conditions in which it acts. Let the scientific men find out what the force consists in. No, I don’t see why there should not be a new force, if it....”\n\n“Why, because with electricity,” Levin interrupted again, “every time you rub tar against wool, a recognized phenomenon is manifested, but in this case it does not happen every time, and so it follows it is not a natural phenomenon.”\n\nFeeling probably that the conversation was taking a tone too serious for a drawing-room, Vronsky made no rejoinder, but by way of trying to change the conversation, he smiled brightly, and turned to the ladies.\n\n“Do let us try at once, countess,” he said; but Levin would finish saying what he thought.\n\n“I think,” he went on, “that this attempt of the spiritualists to explain their marvels as some sort of new natural force is most futile. They boldly talk of spiritual force, and then try to subject it to material experiment.”\n\nEveryone was waiting for him to finish, and he felt it.\n\n“And I think you would be a first-rate medium,” said Countess Nordston; “there’s something enthusiastic in you.”\n\nLevin opened his mouth, was about to say something, reddened, and said nothing.\n\n“Do let us try table-turning at once, please,” said Vronsky. “Princess, will you allow it?”\n\nAnd Vronsky stood up, looking for a little table.\n\nKitty got up to fetch a table, and as she passed, her eyes met Levin’s. She felt for him with her whole heart, the more because she was pitying him for suffering of which she was herself the cause. “If you can forgive me, forgive me,” said her eyes, “I am so happy.”\n\n“I hate them all, and you, and myself,” his eyes responded, and he took up his hat. But he was not destined to escape. Just as they were arranging themselves round the table, and Levin was on the point of retiring, the old prince came in, and after greeting the ladies, addressed Levin.\n\n“Ah!” he began joyously. “Been here long, my boy? I didn’t even know you were in town. Very glad to see you.” The old prince embraced Levin, and talking to him did not observe Vronsky, who had risen, and was serenely waiting till the prince should turn to him.\n\nKitty felt how distasteful her father’s warmth was to Levin after what had happened. She saw, too, how coldly her father responded at last to Vronsky’s bow, and how Vronsky looked with amiable perplexity at her father, as though trying and failing to understand how and why anyone could be hostilely disposed towards him, and she flushed.\n\n“Prince, let us have Konstantin Dmitrievitch,” said Countess Nordston; “we want to try an experiment.”\n\n“What experiment? Table-turning? Well, you must excuse me, ladies and gentlemen, but to my mind it is better fun to play the ring game,” said the old prince, looking at Vronsky, and guessing that it had been his suggestion. “There’s some sense in that, anyway.”\n\nVronsky looked wonderingly at the prince with his resolute eyes, and, with a faint smile, began immediately talking to Countess Nordston of the great ball that was to come off next week.\n\n“I hope you will be there?” he said to Kitty. As soon as the old prince turned away from him, Levin went out unnoticed, and the last impression he carried away with him of that evening was the smiling, happy face of Kitty answering Vronsky’s inquiry about the ball.\n\n# Chapter 15\n\nAt the end of the evening Kitty told her mother of her conversation with Levin, and in spite of all the pity she felt for Levin, she was glad at the thought that she had received an offer. She had no doubt that she had acted rightly. But after she had gone to bed, for a long while she could not sleep. One impression pursued her relentlessly. It was Levin’s face, with his scowling brows, and his kind eyes looking out in dark dejection below them, as he stood listening to her father, and glancing at her and at Vronsky. And she felt so sorry for him that tears came into her eyes. But immediately she thought of the man for whom she had given him up. She vividly recalled his manly, resolute face, his noble self-possession, and the good nature conspicuous in everything towards everyone. She remembered the love for her of the man she loved, and once more all was gladness in her soul, and she lay on the pillow, smiling with happiness. “I’m sorry, I’m sorry; but what could I do? It’s not my fault,” she said to herself; but an inner voice told her something else. Whether she felt remorse at having won Levin’s love, or at having refused him, she did not know. But her happiness was poisoned by doubts. “Lord, have pity on us; Lord, have pity on us; Lord, have pity on us!” she repeated to herself, till she fell asleep.\n\nMeanwhile there took place below, in the prince’s little library, one of the scenes so often repeated between the parents on account of their favorite daughter.\n\n“What? I’ll tell you what!” shouted the prince, waving his arms, and at once wrapping his squirrel-lined dressing-gown round him again. “That you’ve no pride, no dignity; that you’re disgracing, ruining your daughter by this vulgar, stupid matchmaking!”\n\n“But, really, for mercy’s sake, prince, what have I done?” said the princess, almost crying.\n\nShe, pleased and happy after her conversation with her daughter, had gone to the prince to say good-night as usual, and though she had no intention of telling him of Levin’s offer and Kitty’s refusal, still she hinted to her husband that she fancied things were practically settled with Vronsky, and that he would declare himself so soon as his mother arrived. And thereupon, at those words, the prince had all at once flown into a passion, and began to use unseemly language.\n\n“What have you done? I’ll tell you what. First of all, you’re trying to catch an eligible gentleman, and all Moscow will be talking of it, and with good reason. If you have evening parties, invite everyone, don’t pick out the possible suitors. Invite all the young bucks. Engage a piano player, and let them dance, and not as you do things nowadays, hunting up good matches. It makes me sick, sick to see it, and you’ve gone on till you’ve turned the poor wench’s head. Levin’s a thousand times the better man. As for this little Petersburg swell, they’re turned out by machinery, all on one pattern, and all precious rubbish. But if he were a prince of the blood, my daughter need not run after anyone.”\n\n“But what have I done?”\n\n“Why, you’ve....” The prince was crying wrathfully.\n\n“I know if one were to listen to you,” interrupted the princess, “we should never marry our daughter. If it’s to be so, we’d better go into the country.”\n\n“Well, and we had better.”\n\n“But do wait a minute. Do I try and catch them? I don’t try to catch them in the least. A young man, and a very nice one, has fallen in love with her, and she, I fancy....”\n\n“Oh, yes, you fancy! And how if she really is in love, and he’s no more thinking of marriage than I am!... Oh, that I should live to see it! Ah! spiritualism! Ah! Nice! Ah! the ball!” And the prince, imagining that he was mimicking his wife, made a mincing curtsey at each word. “And this is how we’re preparing wretchedness for Kitty; and she’s really got the notion into her head....”\n\n“But what makes you suppose so?”\n\n“I don’t suppose; I know. We have eyes for such things, though women-folk haven’t. I see a man who has serious intentions, that’s Levin: and I see a peacock, like this feather-head, who’s only amusing himself.”\n\n“Oh, well, when once you get an idea into your head!...”\n\n“Well, you’ll remember my words, but too late, just as with Dolly.”\n\n“Well, well, we won’t talk of it,” the princess stopped him, recollecting her unlucky Dolly.\n\n“By all means, and good-night!”\n\nAnd signing each other with the cross, the husband and wife parted with a kiss, feeling that they each remained of their own opinion.\n\nThe princess had at first been quite certain that that evening had settled Kitty’s future, and that there could be no doubt of Vronsky’s intentions, but her husband’s words had disturbed her. And returning to her own room, in terror before the unknown future, she, too, like Kitty, repeated several times in her heart, “Lord, have pity; Lord, have pity; Lord, have pity.”\n\n# Chapter 16\n\nVronsky had never had a real home life. His mother had been in her youth a brilliant society woman, who had had during her married life, and still more afterwards, many love affairs notorious in the whole fashionable world. His father he scarcely remembered, and he had been educated in the Corps of Pages.\n\nLeaving the school very young as a brilliant officer, he had at once got into the circle of wealthy Petersburg army men. Although he did go more or less into Petersburg society, his love affairs had always hitherto been outside it.\n\nIn Moscow he had for the first time felt, after his luxurious and coarse life at Petersburg, all the charm of intimacy with a sweet and innocent girl of his own rank, who cared for him. It never even entered his head that there could be any harm in his relations with Kitty. At balls he danced principally with her. He was a constant visitor at their house. He talked to her as people commonly do talk in society—all sorts of nonsense, but nonsense to which he could not help attaching a special meaning in her case. Although he said nothing to her that he could not have said before everybody, he felt that she was becoming more and more dependent upon him, and the more he felt this, the better he liked it, and the tenderer was his feeling for her. He did not know that his mode of behavior in relation to Kitty had a definite character, that it is courting young girls with no intention of marriage, and that such courting is one of the evil actions common among brilliant young men such as he was. It seemed to him that he was the first who had discovered this pleasure, and he was enjoying his discovery.\n\nIf he could have heard what her parents were saying that evening, if he could have put himself at the point of view of the family and have heard that Kitty would be unhappy if he did not marry her, he would have been greatly astonished, and would not have believed it. He could not believe that what gave such great and delicate pleasure to him, and above all to her, could be wrong. Still less could he have believed that he ought to marry.\n\nMarriage had never presented itself to him as a possibility. He not only disliked family life, but a family, and especially a husband was, in accordance with the views general in the bachelor world in which he lived, conceived as something alien, repellant, and, above all, ridiculous.\n\nBut though Vronsky had not the least suspicion what the parents were saying, he felt on coming away from the Shtcherbatskys’ that the secret spiritual bond which existed between him and Kitty had grown so much stronger that evening that some step must be taken. But what step could and ought to be taken he could not imagine.\n\n“What is so exquisite,” he thought, as he returned from the Shtcherbatskys’, carrying away with him, as he always did, a delicious feeling of purity and freshness, arising partly from the fact that he had not been smoking for a whole evening, and with it a new feeling of tenderness at her love for him—“what is so exquisite is that not a word has been said by me or by her, but we understand each other so well in this unseen language of looks and tones, that this evening more clearly than ever she told me she loves me. And how secretly, simply, and most of all, how trustfully! I feel myself better, purer. I feel that I have a heart, and that there is a great deal of good in me. Those sweet, loving eyes! When she said: ‘Indeed I do....’\n\n“Well, what then? Oh, nothing. It’s good for me, and good for her.” And he began wondering where to finish the evening.\n\nHe passed in review of the places he might go to. “Club? a game of bezique, champagne with Ignatov? No, I’m not going. Château des Fleurs; there I shall find Oblonsky, songs, the cancan. No, I’m sick of it. That’s why I like the Shtcherbatskys’, that I’m growing better. I’ll go home.” He went straight to his room at Dussots’ Hotel, ordered supper, and then undressed, and as soon as his head touched the pillow, fell into a sound sleep.\n\n# Chapter 17\n\nNext day at eleven o’clock in the morning Vronsky drove to the station of the Petersburg railway to meet his mother, and the first person he came across on the great flight of steps was Oblonsky, who was expecting his sister by the same train.\n\n“Ah! your excellency!” cried Oblonsky, “whom are you meeting?”\n\n“My mother,” Vronsky responded, smiling, as everyone did who met Oblonsky. He shook hands with him, and together they ascended the steps. “She is to be here from Petersburg today.”\n\n“I was looking out for you till two o’clock last night. Where did you go after the Shtcherbatskys’?”\n\n“Home,” answered Vronsky. “I must own I felt so well content yesterday after the Shtcherbatskys’ that I didn’t care to go anywhere.”\n\n“I know a gallant steed by tokens sure,\n\nAnd by his eyes I know a youth in love,”\n\ndeclaimed Stepan Arkadyevitch, just as he had done before to Levin.\n\nVronsky smiled with a look that seemed to say that he did not deny it, but he promptly changed the subject.\n\n“And whom are you meeting?” he asked.\n\n“I? I’ve come to meet a pretty woman,” said Oblonsky.\n\n“You don’t say so!”\n“Honi soit qui mal y pense! My sister Anna.”\n\n“Ah! that’s Madame Karenina,” said Vronsky.\n\n“You know her, no doubt?”\n\n“I think I do. Or perhaps not ... I really am not sure,” Vronsky answered heedlessly, with a vague recollection of something stiff and tedious evoked by the name Karenina.\n\n“But Alexey Alexandrovitch, my celebrated brother-in-law, you surely must know. All the world knows him.”\n\n“I know him by reputation and by sight. I know that he’s clever, learned, religious somewhat.... But you know that’s not ... not in my line,” said Vronsky in English.\n\n“Yes, he’s a very remarkable man; rather a conservative, but a splendid man,” observed Stepan Arkadyevitch, “a splendid man.”\n\n“Oh, well, so much the better for him,” said Vronsky smiling. “Oh, you’ve come,” he said, addressing a tall old footman of his mother’s, standing at the door; “come here.”\n\nBesides the charm Oblonsky had in general for everyone, Vronsky had felt of late specially drawn to him by the fact that in his imagination he was associated with Kitty.\n\n“Well, what do you say? Shall we give a supper on Sunday for the diva?” he said to him with a smile, taking his arm.\n\n“Of course. I’m collecting subscriptions. Oh, did you make the acquaintance of my friend Levin?” asked Stepan Arkadyevitch.\n\n“Yes; but he left rather early.”\n\n“He’s a capital fellow,” pursued Oblonsky. “Isn’t he?”\n\n“I don’t know why it is,” responded Vronsky, “in all Moscow people—present company of course excepted,” he put in jestingly, “there’s something uncompromising. They are all on the defensive, lose their tempers, as though they all want to make one feel something....”\n\n“Yes, that’s true, it is so,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, laughing good-humoredly.\n\n“Will the train soon be in?” Vronsky asked a railway official.\n\n“The train’s signaled,” answered the man.\n\nThe approach of the train was more and more evident by the preparatory bustle in the station, the rush of porters, the movement of policemen and attendants, and people meeting the train. Through the frosty vapor could be seen workmen in short sheepskins and soft felt boots crossing the rails of the curving line. The hiss of the boiler could be heard on the distant rails, and the rumble of something heavy.\n\n“No,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, who felt a great inclination to tell Vronsky of Levin’s intentions in regard to Kitty. “No, you’ve not got a true impression of Levin. He’s a very nervous man, and is sometimes out of humor, it’s true, but then he is often very nice. He’s such a true, honest nature, and a heart of gold. But yesterday there were special reasons,” pursued Stepan Arkadyevitch, with a meaning smile, totally oblivious of the genuine sympathy he had felt the day before for his friend, and feeling the same sympathy now, only for Vronsky. “Yes, there were reasons why he could not help being either particularly happy or particularly unhappy.”\n\nVronsky stood still and asked directly: “How so? Do you mean he made your belle-sœur an offer yesterday?”\n\n“Maybe,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch. “I fancied something of the sort yesterday. Yes, if he went away early, and was out of humor too, it must mean it.... He’s been so long in love, and I’m very sorry for him.”\n\n“So that’s it! I should imagine, though, she might reckon on a better match,” said Vronsky, drawing himself up and walking about again, “though I don’t know him, of course,” he added. “Yes, that is a hateful position! That’s why most fellows prefer to have to do with Klaras. If you don’t succeed with them it only proves that you’ve not enough cash, but in this case one’s dignity’s at stake. But here’s the train.”\n\nThe engine had already whistled in the distance. A few instants later the platform was quivering, and with puffs of steam hanging low in the air from the frost, the engine rolled up, with the lever of the middle wheel rhythmically moving up and down, and the stooping figure of the engine-driver covered with frost. Behind the tender, setting the platform more and more slowly swaying, came the luggage van with a dog whining in it. At last the passenger carriages rolled in, oscillating before coming to a standstill.\n\nA smart guard jumped out, giving a whistle, and after him one by one the impatient passengers began to get down: an officer of the guards, holding himself erect, and looking severely about him; a nimble little merchant with a satchel, smiling gaily; a peasant with a sack over his shoulder.\n\nVronsky, standing beside Oblonsky, watched the carriages and the passengers, totally oblivious of his mother. What he had just heard about Kitty excited and delighted him. Unconsciously he arched his chest, and his eyes flashed. He felt himself a conqueror.\n\n“Countess Vronskaya is in that compartment,” said the smart guard, going up to Vronsky.\n\nThe guard’s words roused him, and forced him to think of his mother and his approaching meeting with her. He did not in his heart respect his mother, and without acknowledging it to himself, he did not love her, though in accordance with the ideas of the set in which he lived, and with his own education, he could not have conceived of any behavior to his mother not in the highest degree respectful and obedient, and the more externally obedient and respectful his behavior, the less in his heart he respected and loved her.\n\n# Chapter 18\n\nVronsky followed the guard to the carriage, and at the door of the compartment he stopped short to make room for a lady who was getting out.\n\nWith the insight of a man of the world, from one glance at this lady’s appearance Vronsky classified her as belonging to the best society. He begged pardon, and was getting into the carriage, but felt he must glance at her once more; not that she was very beautiful, not on account of the elegance and modest grace which were apparent in her whole figure, but because in the expression of her charming face, as she passed close by him, there was something peculiarly caressing and soft. As he looked round, she too turned her head. Her shining gray eyes, that looked dark from the thick lashes, rested with friendly attention on his face, as though she were recognizing him, and then promptly turned away to the passing crowd, as though seeking someone. In that brief look Vronsky had time to notice the suppressed eagerness which played over her face, and flitted between the brilliant eyes and the faint smile that curved her red lips. It was as though her nature were so brimming over with something that against her will it showed itself now in the flash of her eyes, and now in her smile. Deliberately she shrouded the light in her eyes, but it shone against her will in the faintly perceptible smile.\n\nVronsky stepped into the carriage. His mother, a dried-up old lady with black eyes and ringlets, screwed up her eyes, scanning her son, and smiled slightly with her thin lips. Getting up from the seat and handing her maid a bag, she gave her little wrinkled hand to her son to kiss, and lifting his head from her hand, kissed him on the cheek.\n\n“You got my telegram? Quite well? Thank God.”\n\n“You had a good journey?” said her son, sitting down beside her, and involuntarily listening to a woman’s voice outside the door. He knew it was the voice of the lady he had met at the door.\n\n“All the same I don’t agree with you,” said the lady’s voice.\n\n“It’s the Petersburg view, madame.”\n\n“Not Petersburg, but simply feminine,” she responded.\n\n“Well, well, allow me to kiss your hand.”\n\n“Good-bye, Ivan Petrovitch. And could you see if my brother is here, and send him to me?” said the lady in the doorway, and stepped back again into the compartment.\n\n“Well, have you found your brother?” said Countess Vronskaya, addressing the lady.\n\nVronsky understood now that this was Madame Karenina.\n\n“Your brother is here,” he said, standing up. “Excuse me, I did not know you, and, indeed, our acquaintance was so slight,” said Vronsky, bowing, “that no doubt you do not remember me.”\n\n“Oh, no,” said she, “I should have known you because your mother and I have been talking, I think, of nothing but you all the way.” As she spoke she let the eagerness that would insist on coming out show itself in her smile. “And still no sign of my brother.”\n\n“Do call him, Alexey,” said the old countess. Vronsky stepped out onto the platform and shouted:\n\n“Oblonsky! Here!”\n\nMadame Karenina, however, did not wait for her brother, but catching sight of him she stepped out with her light, resolute step. And as soon as her brother had reached her, with a gesture that struck Vronsky by its decision and its grace, she flung her left arm around his neck, drew him rapidly to her, and kissed him warmly. Vronsky gazed, never taking his eyes from her, and smiled, he could not have said why. But recollecting that his mother was waiting for him, he went back again into the carriage.\n\n“She’s very sweet, isn’t she?” said the countess of Madame Karenina. “Her husband put her with me, and I was delighted to have her. We’ve been talking all the way. And so you, I hear ... vous filez le parfait amour. Tant mieux, mon cher, tant mieux.”\n\n“I don’t know what you are referring to, maman,” he answered coldly. “Come, maman, let us go.”\n\nMadame Karenina entered the carriage again to say good-bye to the countess.\n\n“Well, countess, you have met your son, and I my brother,” she said. “And all my gossip is exhausted. I should have nothing more to tell you.”\n\n“Oh, no,” said the countess, taking her hand. “I could go all around the world with you and never be dull. You are one of those delightful women in whose company it’s sweet to be silent as well as to talk. Now please don’t fret over your son; you can’t expect never to be parted.”\n\nMadame Karenina stood quite still, holding herself very erect, and her eyes were smiling.\n\n“Anna Arkadyevna,” the countess said in explanation to her son, “has a little son eight years old, I believe, and she has never been parted from him before, and she keeps fretting over leaving him.”\n\n“Yes, the countess and I have been talking all the time, I of my son and she of hers,” said Madame Karenina, and again a smile lighted up her face, a caressing smile intended for him.\n\n“I am afraid that you must have been dreadfully bored,” he said, promptly catching the ball of coquetry she had flung him. But apparently she did not care to pursue the conversation in that strain, and she turned to the old countess.\n\n“Thank you so much. The time has passed so quickly. Good-bye, countess.”\n\n“Good-bye, my love,” answered the countess. “Let me have a kiss of your pretty face. I speak plainly, at my age, and I tell you simply that I’ve lost my heart to you.”\n\nStereotyped as the phrase was, Madame Karenina obviously believed it and was delighted by it. She flushed, bent down slightly, and put her cheek to the countess’s lips, drew herself up again, and with the same smile fluttering between her lips and her eyes, she gave her hand to Vronsky. He pressed the little hand she gave him, and was delighted, as though at something special, by the energetic squeeze with which she freely and vigorously shook his hand. She went out with the rapid step which bore her rather fully-developed figure with such strange lightness.\n\n“Very charming,” said the countess.\n\nThat was just what her son was thinking. His eyes followed her till her graceful figure was out of sight, and then the smile remained on his face. He saw out of the window how she went up to her brother, put her arm in his, and began telling him something eagerly, obviously something that had nothing to do with him, Vronsky, and at that he felt annoyed.\n\n“Well, maman, are you perfectly well?” he repeated, turning to his mother.\n\n“Everything has been delightful. Alexander has been very good, and Marie has grown very pretty. She’s very interesting.”\n\nAnd she began telling him again of what interested her most—the christening of her grandson, for which she had been staying in Petersburg, and the special favor shown her elder son by the Tsar.\n\n“Here’s Lavrenty,” said Vronsky, looking out of the window; “now we can go, if you like.”\n\nThe old butler, who had traveled with the countess, came to the carriage to announce that everything was ready, and the countess got up to go.\n\n“Come; there’s not such a crowd now,” said Vronsky.\n\nThe maid took a handbag and the lap dog, the butler and a porter the other baggage. Vronsky gave his mother his arm; but just as they were getting out of the carriage several men ran suddenly by with panic-stricken faces. The station-master, too, ran by in his extraordinary colored cap. Obviously something unusual had happened. The crowd who had left the train were running back again.\n\n“What?... What?... Where?... Flung himself!... Crushed!...” was heard among the crowd. Stepan Arkadyevitch, with his sister on his arm, turned back. They too looked scared, and stopped at the carriage door to avoid the crowd.\n\nThe ladies got in, while Vronsky and Stepan Arkadyevitch followed the crowd to find out details of the disaster.\n\nA guard, either drunk or too much muffled up in the bitter frost, had not heard the train moving back, and had been crushed.\n\nBefore Vronsky and Oblonsky came back the ladies heard the facts from the butler.\n\nOblonsky and Vronsky had both seen the mutilated corpse. Oblonsky was evidently upset. He frowned and seemed ready to cry.\n\n“Ah, how awful! Ah, Anna, if you had seen it! Ah, how awful!” he said.\n\nVronsky did not speak; his handsome face was serious, but perfectly composed.\n\n“Oh, if you had seen it, countess,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch. “And his wife was there.... It was awful to see her!... She flung herself on the body. They say he was the only support of an immense family. How awful!”\n\n“Couldn’t one do anything for her?” said Madame Karenina in an agitated whisper.\n\nVronsky glanced at her, and immediately got out of the carriage.\n\n“I’ll be back directly, maman,” he remarked, turning round in the doorway.\n\nWhen he came back a few minutes later, Stepan Arkadyevitch was already in conversation with the countess about the new singer, while the countess was impatiently looking towards the door, waiting for her son.\n\n“Now let us be off,” said Vronsky, coming in. They went out together. Vronsky was in front with his mother. Behind walked Madame Karenina with her brother. Just as they were going out of the station the station-master overtook Vronsky.\n\n“You gave my assistant two hundred roubles. Would you kindly explain for whose benefit you intend them?”\n\n“For the widow,” said Vronsky, shrugging his shoulders. “I should have thought there was no need to ask.”\n\n“You gave that?” cried Oblonsky, behind, and, pressing his sister’s hand, he added: “Very nice, very nice! Isn’t he a splendid fellow? Good-bye, countess.”\n\nAnd he and his sister stood still, looking for her maid.\n\nWhen they went out the Vronsky’s carriage had already driven away. People coming in were still talking of what happened.\n\n“What a horrible death!” said a gentleman, passing by. “They say he was cut in two pieces.”\n\n“On the contrary, I think it’s the easiest—instantaneous,” observed another.\n\n“How is it they don’t take proper precautions?” said a third.\n\nMadame Karenina seated herself in the carriage, and Stepan Arkadyevitch saw with surprise that her lips were quivering, and she was with difficulty restraining her tears.\n\n“What is it, Anna?” he asked, when they had driven a few hundred yards.\n\n“It’s an omen of evil,” she said.\n\n“What nonsense!” said Stepan Arkadyevitch. “You’ve come, that’s the chief thing. You can’t conceive how I’m resting my hopes on you.”\n\n“Have you known Vronsky long?” she asked.\n\n“Yes. You know we’re hoping he will marry Kitty.”\n\n“Yes?” said Anna softly. “Come now, let us talk of you,” she added, tossing her head, as though she would physically shake off something superfluous oppressing her. “Let us talk of your affairs. I got your letter, and here I am.”\n\n“Yes, all my hopes are in you,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch.\n\n“Well, tell me all about it.”\n\nAnd Stepan Arkadyevitch began to tell his story.\n\nOn reaching home Oblonsky helped his sister out, sighed, pressed her hand, and set off to his office.\n\n# Chapter 19\n\nWhen Anna went into the room, Dolly was sitting in the little drawing-room with a white-headed fat little boy, already like his father, giving him a lesson in French reading. As the boy read, he kept twisting and trying to tear off a button that was nearly off his jacket. His mother had several times taken his hand from it, but the fat little hand went back to the button again. His mother pulled the button off and put it in her pocket.\n\n“Keep your hands still, Grisha,” she said, and she took up her work, a coverlet she had long been making. She always set to work on it at depressed moments, and now she knitted at it nervously, twitching her fingers and counting the stitches. Though she had sent word the day before to her husband that it was nothing to her whether his sister came or not, she had made everything ready for her arrival, and was expecting her sister-in-law with emotion.\n\nDolly was crushed by her sorrow, utterly swallowed up by it. Still she did not forget that Anna, her sister-in-law, was the wife of one of the most important personages in Petersburg, and was a Petersburg grande dame. And, thanks to this circumstance, she did not carry out her threat to her husband—that is to say, she remembered that her sister-in-law was coming. “And, after all, Anna is in no wise to blame,” thought Dolly. “I know nothing of her except the very best, and I have seen nothing but kindness and affection from her towards myself.” It was true that as far as she could recall her impressions at Petersburg at the Karenins’, she did not like their household itself; there was something artificial in the whole framework of their family life. “But why should I not receive her? If only she doesn’t take it into her head to console me!” thought Dolly. “All consolation and counsel and Christian forgiveness, all that I have thought over a thousand times, and it’s all no use.”\n\nAll these days Dolly had been alone with her children. She did not want to talk of her sorrow, but with that sorrow in her heart she could not talk of outside matters. She knew that in one way or another she would tell Anna everything, and she was alternately glad at the thought of speaking freely, and angry at the necessity of speaking of her humiliation with her, his sister, and of hearing her ready-made phrases of good advice and comfort. She had been on the lookout for her, glancing at her watch every minute, and, as so often happens, let slip just that minute when her visitor arrived, so that she did not hear the bell.\n\nCatching a sound of skirts and light steps at the door, she looked round, and her care-worn face unconsciously expressed not gladness, but wonder. She got up and embraced her sister-in-law.\n\n“What, here already!” she said as she kissed her.\n\n“Dolly, how glad I am to see you!”\n\n“I am glad, too,” said Dolly, faintly smiling, and trying by the expression of Anna’s face to find out whether she knew. “Most likely she knows,” she thought, noticing the sympathy in Anna’s face. “Well, come along, I’ll take you to your room,” she went on, trying to defer as long as possible the moment of confidences.\n\n“Is this Grisha? Heavens, how he’s grown!” said Anna; and kissing him, never taking her eyes off Dolly, she stood still and flushed a little. “No, please, let us stay here.”\n\nShe took off her kerchief and her hat, and catching it in a lock of her black hair, which was a mass of curls, she tossed her head and shook her hair down.\n\n“You are radiant with health and happiness!” said Dolly, almost with envy.\n\n“I?... Yes,” said Anna. “Merciful heavens, Tanya! You’re the same age as my Seryozha,” she added, addressing the little girl as she ran in. She took her in her arms and kissed her. “Delightful child, delightful! Show me them all.”\n\nShe mentioned them, not only remembering the names, but the years, months, characters, illnesses of all the children, and Dolly could not but appreciate that.\n\n“Very well, we will go to them,” she said. “It’s a pity Vassya’s asleep.”\n\nAfter seeing the children, they sat down, alone now, in the drawing-room, to coffee. Anna took the tray, and then pushed it away from her.\n\n“Dolly,” she said, “he has told me.”\n\nDolly looked coldly at Anna; she was waiting now for phrases of conventional sympathy, but Anna said nothing of the sort.\n\n“Dolly, dear,” she said, “I don’t want to speak for him to you, nor to try to comfort you; that’s impossible. But, darling, I’m simply sorry, sorry from my heart for you!”\n\nUnder the thick lashes of her shining eyes tears suddenly glittered. She moved nearer to her sister-in-law and took her hand in her vigorous little hand. Dolly did not shrink away, but her face did not lose its frigid expression. She said:\n\n“To comfort me’s impossible. Everything’s lost after what has happened, everything’s over!”\n\nAnd directly she had said this, her face suddenly softened. Anna lifted the wasted, thin hand of Dolly, kissed it and said:\n\n“But, Dolly, what’s to be done, what’s to be done? How is it best to act in this awful position—that’s what you must think of.”\n\n“All’s over, and there’s nothing more,” said Dolly. “And the worst of all is, you see, that I can’t cast him off: there are the children, I am tied. And I can’t live with him! it’s a torture to me to see him.”\n\n“Dolly, darling, he has spoken to me, but I want to hear it from you: tell me about it.”\n\nDolly looked at her inquiringly.\n\nSympathy and love unfeigned were visible on Anna’s face.\n\n“Very well,” she said all at once. “But I will tell you it from the beginning. You know how I was married. With the education mamma gave us I was more than innocent, I was stupid. I knew nothing. I know they say men tell their wives of their former lives, but Stiva”—she corrected herself—“Stepan Arkadyevitch told me nothing. You’ll hardly believe it, but till now I imagined that I was the only woman he had known. So I lived eight years. You must understand that I was so far from suspecting infidelity, I regarded it as impossible, and then—try to imagine it—with such ideas, to find out suddenly all the horror, all the loathsomeness.... You must try and understand me. To be fully convinced of one’s happiness, and all at once....” continued Dolly, holding back her sobs, “to get a letter ... his letter to his mistress, my governess. No, it’s too awful!” She hastily pulled out her handkerchief and hid her face in it. “I can understand being carried away by feeling,” she went on after a brief silence, “but deliberately, slyly deceiving me ... and with whom?... To go on being my husband together with her ... it’s awful! You can’t understand....”\n\n“Oh, yes, I understand! I understand! Dolly, dearest, I do understand,” said Anna, pressing her hand.\n\n“And do you imagine he realizes all the awfulness of my position?” Dolly resumed. “Not the slightest! He’s happy and contented.”\n\n“Oh, no!” Anna interposed quickly. “He’s to be pitied, he’s weighed down by remorse....”\n\n“Is he capable of remorse?” Dolly interrupted, gazing intently into her sister-in-law’s face.\n\n“Yes. I know him. I could not look at him without feeling sorry for him. We both know him. He’s good-hearted, but he’s proud, and now he’s so humiliated. What touched me most....” (and here Anna guessed what would touch Dolly most) “he’s tortured by two things: that he’s ashamed for the children’s sake, and that, loving you—yes, yes, loving you beyond everything on earth,” she hurriedly interrupted Dolly, who would have answered—“he has hurt you, pierced you to the heart. ‘No, no, she cannot forgive me,’ he keeps saying.”\n\nDolly looked dreamily away beyond her sister-in-law as she listened to her words.\n\n“Yes, I can see that his position is awful; it’s worse for the guilty than the innocent,” she said, “if he feels that all the misery comes from his fault. But how am I to forgive him, how am I to be his wife again after her? For me to live with him now would be torture, just because I love my past love for him....”\n\nAnd sobs cut short her words. But as though of set design, each time she was softened she began to speak again of what exasperated her.\n\n“She’s young, you see, she’s pretty,” she went on. “Do you know, Anna, my youth and my beauty are gone, taken by whom? By him and his children. I have worked for him, and all I had has gone in his service, and now of course any fresh, vulgar creature has more charm for him. No doubt they talked of me together, or, worse still, they were silent. Do you understand?”\n\nAgain her eyes glowed with hatred.\n\n“And after that he will tell me.... What! can I believe him? Never! No, everything is over, everything that once made my comfort, the reward of my work, and my sufferings.... Would you believe it, I was teaching Grisha just now: once this was a joy to me, now it is a torture. What have I to strive and toil for? Why are the children here? What’s so awful is that all at once my heart’s turned, and instead of love and tenderness, I have nothing but hatred for him; yes, hatred. I could kill him.”\n\n“Darling Dolly, I understand, but don’t torture yourself. You are so distressed, so overwrought, that you look at many things mistakenly.”\n\nDolly grew calmer, and for two minutes both were silent.\n\n“What’s to be done? Think for me, Anna, help me. I have thought over everything, and I see nothing.”\n\nAnna could think of nothing, but her heart responded instantly to each word, to each change of expression of her sister-in-law.\n\n“One thing I would say,” began Anna. “I am his sister, I know his character, that faculty of forgetting everything, everything” (she waved her hand before her forehead), “that faculty for being completely carried away, but for completely repenting too. He cannot believe it, he cannot comprehend now how he can have acted as he did.”\n\n“No; he understands, he understood!” Dolly broke in. “But I ... you are forgetting me ... does it make it easier for me?”\n\n“Wait a minute. When he told me, I will own I did not realize all the awfulness of your position. I saw nothing but him, and that the family was broken up. I felt sorry for him, but after talking to you, I see it, as a woman, quite differently. I see your agony, and I can’t tell you how sorry I am for you! But, Dolly, darling, I fully realize your sufferings, only there is one thing I don’t know; I don’t know ... I don’t know how much love there is still in your heart for him. That you know—whether there is enough for you to be able to forgive him. If there is, forgive him!”\n\n“No,” Dolly was beginning, but Anna cut her short, kissing her hand once more.\n\n“I know more of the world than you do,” she said. “I know how men like Stiva look at it. You speak of his talking of you with her. That never happened. Such men are unfaithful, but their home and wife are sacred to them. Somehow or other these women are still looked on with contempt by them, and do not touch on their feeling for their family. They draw a sort of line that can’t be crossed between them and their families. I don’t understand it, but it is so.”\n\n“Yes, but he has kissed her....”\n\n“Dolly, hush, darling. I saw Stiva when he was in love with you. I remember the time when he came to me and cried, talking of you, and all the poetry and loftiness of his feeling for you, and I know that the longer he has lived with you the loftier you have been in his eyes. You know we have sometimes laughed at him for putting in at every word: ‘Dolly’s a marvelous woman.’ You have always been a divinity for him, and you are that still, and this has not been an infidelity of the heart....”\n\n“But if it is repeated?”\n\n“It cannot be, as I understand it....”\n\n“Yes, but could you forgive it?”\n\n“I don’t know, I can’t judge.... Yes, I can,” said Anna, thinking a moment; and grasping the position in her thought and weighing it in her inner balance, she added: “Yes, I can, I can, I can. Yes, I could forgive it. I could not be the same, no; but I could forgive it, and forgive it as though it had never been, never been at all....”\n\n“Oh, of course,” Dolly interposed quickly, as though saying what she had more than once thought, “else it would not be forgiveness. If one forgives, it must be completely, completely. Come, let us go; I’ll take you to your room,” she said, getting up, and on the way she embraced Anna. “My dear, how glad I am you came. It has made things better, ever so much better.”\n\n# Chapter 20\n\nThe whole of that day Anna spent at home, that’s to say at the Oblonskys’, and received no one, though some of her acquaintances had already heard of her arrival, and came to call the same day. Anna spent the whole morning with Dolly and the children. She merely sent a brief note to her brother to tell him that he must not fail to dine at home. “Come, God is merciful,” she wrote.\n\nOblonsky did dine at home: the conversation was general, and his wife, speaking to him, addressed him as “Stiva,” as she had not done before. In the relations of the husband and wife the same estrangement still remained, but there was no talk now of separation, and Stepan Arkadyevitch saw the possibility of explanation and reconciliation.\n\nImmediately after dinner Kitty came in. She knew Anna Arkadyevna, but only very slightly, and she came now to her sister’s with some trepidation, at the prospect of meeting this fashionable Petersburg lady, whom everyone spoke so highly of. But she made a favorable impression on Anna Arkadyevna—she saw that at once. Anna was unmistakably admiring her loveliness and her youth: before Kitty knew where she was she found herself not merely under Anna’s sway, but in love with her, as young girls do fall in love with older and married women. Anna was not like a fashionable lady, nor the mother of a boy of eight years old. In the elasticity of her movements, the freshness and the unflagging eagerness which persisted in her face, and broke out in her smile and her glance, she would rather have passed for a girl of twenty, had it not been for a serious and at times mournful look in her eyes, which struck and attracted Kitty. Kitty felt that Anna was perfectly simple and was concealing nothing, but that she had another higher world of interests inaccessible to her, complex and poetic.\n\nAfter dinner, when Dolly went away to her own room, Anna rose quickly and went up to her brother, who was just lighting a cigar.\n\n“Stiva,” she said to him, winking gaily, crossing him and glancing towards the door, “go, and God help you.”\n\nHe threw down the cigar, understanding her, and departed through the doorway.\n\nWhen Stepan Arkadyevitch had disappeared, she went back to the sofa where she had been sitting, surrounded by the children. Either because the children saw that their mother was fond of this aunt, or that they felt a special charm in her themselves, the two elder ones, and the younger following their lead, as children so often do, had clung about their new aunt since before dinner, and would not leave her side. And it had become a sort of game among them to sit as close as possible to their aunt, to touch her, hold her little hand, kiss it, play with her ring, or even touch the flounce of her skirt.\n\n“Come, come, as we were sitting before,” said Anna Arkadyevna, sitting down in her place.\n\nAnd again Grisha poked his little face under her arm, and nestled with his head on her gown, beaming with pride and happiness.\n\n“And when is your next ball?” she asked Kitty.\n\n“Next week, and a splendid ball. One of those balls where one always enjoys oneself.”\n\n“Why, are there balls where one always enjoys oneself?” Anna said, with tender irony.\n\n“It’s strange, but there are. At the Bobrishtchevs’ one always enjoys oneself, and at the Nikitins’ too, while at the Mezhkovs’ it’s always dull. Haven’t you noticed it?”\n\n“No, my dear, for me there are no balls now where one enjoys oneself,” said Anna, and Kitty detected in her eyes that mysterious world which was not open to her. “For me there are some less dull and tiresome.”\n\n“How can you be dull at a ball?”\n\n“Why should not I be dull at a ball?” inquired Anna.\n\nKitty perceived that Anna knew what answer would follow.\n\n“Because you always look nicer than anyone.”\n\nAnna had the faculty of blushing. She blushed a little, and said:\n\n“In the first place it’s never so; and secondly, if it were, what difference would it make to me?”\n\n“Are you coming to this ball?” asked Kitty.\n\n“I imagine it won’t be possible to avoid going. Here, take it,” she said to Tanya, who was pulling the loosely-fitting ring off her white, slender-tipped finger.\n\n“I shall be so glad if you go. I should so like to see you at a ball.”\n\n“Anyway, if I do go, I shall comfort myself with the thought that it’s a pleasure to you ... Grisha, don’t pull my hair. It’s untidy enough without that,” she said, putting up a straying lock, which Grisha had been playing with.\n\n“I imagine you at the ball in lilac.”\n\n“And why in lilac precisely?” asked Anna, smiling. “Now, children, run along, run along. Do you hear? Miss Hoole is calling you to tea,” she said, tearing the children from her, and sending them off to the dining-room.\n\n“I know why you press me to come to the ball. You expect a great deal of this ball, and you want everyone to be there to take part in it.”\n\n“How do you know? Yes.”\n\n“Oh! what a happy time you are at,” pursued Anna. “I remember, and I know that blue haze like the mist on the mountains in Switzerland. That mist which covers everything in that blissful time when childhood is just ending, and out of that vast circle, happy and gay, there is a path growing narrower and narrower, and it is delightful and alarming to enter the ballroom, bright and splendid as it is.... Who has not been through it?”\n\nKitty smiled without speaking. “But how did she go through it? How I should like to know all her love story!” thought Kitty, recalling the unromantic appearance of Alexey Alexandrovitch, her husband.\n\n“I know something. Stiva told me, and I congratulate you. I liked him so much,” Anna continued. “I met Vronsky at the railway station.”\n\n“Oh, was he there?” asked Kitty, blushing. “What was it Stiva told you?”\n\n“Stiva gossiped about it all. And I should be so glad ... I traveled yesterday with Vronsky’s mother,” she went on; “and his mother talked without a pause of him, he’s her favorite. I know mothers are partial, but....”\n\n“What did his mother tell you?”\n\n“Oh, a great deal! And I know that he’s her favorite; still one can see how chivalrous he is.... Well, for instance, she told me that he had wanted to give up all his property to his brother, that he had done something extraordinary when he was quite a child, saved a woman out of the water. He’s a hero, in fact,” said Anna, smiling and recollecting the two hundred roubles he had given at the station.\n\nBut she did not tell Kitty about the two hundred roubles. For some reason it was disagreeable to her to think of it. She felt that there was something that had to do with her in it, and something that ought not to have been.\n\n“She pressed me very much to go and see her,” Anna went on; “and I shall be glad to go to see her tomorrow. Stiva is staying a long while in Dolly’s room, thank God,” Anna added, changing the subject, and getting up, Kitty fancied, displeased with something.\n\n“No, I’m first! No, I!” screamed the children, who had finished tea, running up to their Aunt Anna.\n\n“All together,” said Anna, and she ran laughing to meet them, and embraced and swung round all the throng of swarming children, shrieking with delight.\n\n# Chapter 21\n\nDolly came out of her room to the tea of the grown-up people. Stepan Arkadyevitch did not come out. He must have left his wife’s room by the other door.\n\n“I am afraid you’ll be cold upstairs,” observed Dolly, addressing Anna; “I want to move you downstairs, and we shall be nearer.”\n\n“Oh, please, don’t trouble about me,” answered Anna, looking intently into Dolly’s face, trying to make out whether there had been a reconciliation or not.\n\n“It will be lighter for you here,” answered her sister-in-law.\n\n“I assure you that I sleep everywhere, and always like a marmot.”\n\n“What’s the question?” inquired Stepan Arkadyevitch, coming out of his room and addressing his wife.\n\nFrom his tone both Kitty and Anna knew that a reconciliation had taken place.\n\n“I want to move Anna downstairs, but we must hang up blinds. No one knows how to do it; I must see to it myself,” answered Dolly addressing him.\n\n“God knows whether they are fully reconciled,” thought Anna, hearing her tone, cold and composed.\n\n“Oh, nonsense, Dolly, always making difficulties,” answered her husband. “Come, I’ll do it all, if you like....”\n\n“Yes, they must be reconciled,” thought Anna.\n\n“I know how you do everything,” answered Dolly. “You tell Matvey to do what can’t be done, and go away yourself, leaving him to make a muddle of everything,” and her habitual, mocking smile curved the corners of Dolly’s lips as she spoke.\n\n“Full, full reconciliation, full,” thought Anna; “thank God!” and rejoicing that she was the cause of it, she went up to Dolly and kissed her.\n\n“Not at all. Why do you always look down on me and Matvey?” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, smiling hardly perceptibly, and addressing his wife.\n\nThe whole evening Dolly was, as always, a little mocking in her tone to her husband, while Stepan Arkadyevitch was happy and cheerful, but not so as to seem as though, having been forgiven, he had forgotten his offense.\n\nAt half-past nine o’clock a particularly joyful and pleasant family conversation over the tea-table at the Oblonskys’ was broken up by an apparently simple incident. But this simple incident for some reason struck everyone as strange. Talking about common acquaintances in Petersburg, Anna got up quickly.\n\n“She is in my album,” she said; “and, by the way, I’ll show you my Seryozha,” she added, with a mother’s smile of pride.\n\nTowards ten o’clock, when she usually said good-night to her son, and often before going to a ball put him to bed herself, she felt depressed at being so far from him; and whatever she was talking about, she kept coming back in thought to her curly-headed Seryozha. She longed to look at his photograph and talk of him. Seizing the first pretext, she got up, and with her light, resolute step went for her album. The stairs up to her room came out on the landing of the great warm main staircase.\n\nJust as she was leaving the drawing-room, a ring was heard in the hall.\n\n“Who can that be?” said Dolly.\n\n“It’s early for me to be fetched, and for anyone else it’s late,” observed Kitty.\n\n“Sure to be someone with papers for me,” put in Stepan Arkadyevitch. When Anna was passing the top of the staircase, a servant was running up to announce the visitor, while the visitor himself was standing under a lamp. Anna glancing down at once recognized Vronsky, and a strange feeling of pleasure and at the same time of dread of something stirred in her heart. He was standing still, not taking off his coat, pulling something out of his pocket. At the instant when she was just facing the stairs, he raised his eyes, caught sight of her, and into the expression of his face there passed a shade of embarrassment and dismay. With a slight inclination of her head she passed, hearing behind her Stepan Arkadyevitch’s loud voice calling him to come up, and the quiet, soft, and composed voice of Vronsky refusing.\n\nWhen Anna returned with the album, he was already gone, and Stepan Arkadyevitch was telling them that he had called to inquire about the dinner they were giving next day to a celebrity who had just arrived. “And nothing would induce him to come up. What a queer fellow he is!” added Stepan Arkadyevitch.\n\nKitty blushed. She thought that she was the only person who knew why he had come, and why he would not come up. “He has been at home,” she thought, “and didn’t find me, and thought I should be here, but he did not come up because he thought it late, and Anna’s here.”\n\nAll of them looked at each other, saying nothing, and began to look at Anna’s album.\n\nThere was nothing either exceptional or strange in a man’s calling at half-past nine on a friend to inquire details of a proposed dinner party and not coming in, but it seemed strange to all of them. Above all, it seemed strange and not right to Anna.\n\n# Chapter 22\n\nThe ball was only just beginning as Kitty and her mother walked up the great staircase, flooded with light, and lined with flowers and footmen in powder and red coats. From the rooms came a constant, steady hum, as from a hive, and the rustle of movement; and while on the landing between trees they gave last touches to their hair and dresses before the mirror, they heard from the ballroom the careful, distinct notes of the fiddles of the orchestra beginning the first waltz. A little old man in civilian dress, arranging his gray curls before another mirror, and diffusing an odor of scent, stumbled against them on the stairs, and stood aside, evidently admiring Kitty, whom he did not know. A beardless youth, one of those society youths whom the old Prince Shtcherbatsky called “young bucks,” in an exceedingly open waistcoat, straightening his white tie as he went, bowed to them, and after running by, came back to ask Kitty for a quadrille. As the first quadrille had already been given to Vronsky, she had to promise this youth the second. An officer, buttoning his glove, stood aside in the doorway, and stroking his mustache, admired rosy Kitty.\n\nAlthough her dress, her coiffure, and all the preparations for the ball had cost Kitty great trouble and consideration, at this moment she walked into the ballroom in her elaborate tulle dress over a pink slip as easily and simply as though all the rosettes and lace, all the minute details of her attire, had not cost her or her family a moment’s attention, as though she had been born in that tulle and lace, with her hair done up high on her head, and a rose and two leaves on the top of it.\n\nWhen, just before entering the ballroom, the princess, her mother, tried to turn right side out of the ribbon of her sash, Kitty had drawn back a little. She felt that everything must be right of itself, and graceful, and nothing could need setting straight.\n\nIt was one of Kitty’s best days. Her dress was not uncomfortable anywhere; her lace berthe did not droop anywhere; her rosettes were not crushed nor torn off; her pink slippers with high, hollowed-out heels did not pinch, but gladdened her feet; and the thick rolls of fair chignon kept up on her head as if they were her own hair. All the three buttons buttoned up without tearing on the long glove that covered her hand without concealing its lines. The black velvet of her locket nestled with special softness round her neck. That velvet was delicious; at home, looking at her neck in the looking-glass, Kitty had felt that that velvet was speaking. About all the rest there might be a doubt, but the velvet was delicious. Kitty smiled here too, at the ball, when she glanced at it in the glass. Her bare shoulders and arms gave Kitty a sense of chill marble, a feeling she particularly liked. Her eyes sparkled, and her rosy lips could not keep from smiling from the consciousness of her own attractiveness. She had scarcely entered the ballroom and reached the throng of ladies, all tulle, ribbons, lace, and flowers, waiting to be asked to dance—Kitty was never one of that throng—when she was asked for a waltz, and asked by the best partner, the first star in the hierarchy of the ballroom, a renowned director of dances, a married man, handsome and well-built, Yegorushka Korsunsky. He had only just left the Countess Bonina, with whom he had danced the first half of the waltz, and, scanning his kingdom—that is to say, a few couples who had started dancing—he caught sight of Kitty, entering, and flew up to her with that peculiar, easy amble which is confined to directors of balls. Without even asking her if she cared to dance, he put out his arm to encircle her slender waist. She looked round for someone to give her fan to, and their hostess, smiling to her, took it.\n\n“How nice you’ve come in good time,” he said to her, embracing her waist; “such a bad habit to be late.” Bending her left hand, she laid it on his shoulder, and her little feet in their pink slippers began swiftly, lightly, and rhythmically moving over the slippery floor in time to the music.\n\n“It’s a rest to waltz with you,” he said to her, as they fell into the first slow steps of the waltz. “It’s exquisite—such lightness, precision.” He said to her the same thing he said to almost all his partners whom he knew well.\n\nShe smiled at his praise, and continued to look about the room over his shoulder. She was not like a girl at her first ball, for whom all faces in the ballroom melt into one vision of fairyland. And she was not a girl who had gone the stale round of balls till every face in the ballroom was familiar and tiresome. But she was in the middle stage between these two; she was excited, and at the same time she had sufficient self-possession to be able to observe. In the left corner of the ballroom she saw the cream of society gathered together. There—incredibly naked—was the beauty Lidi, Korsunsky’s wife; there was the lady of the house; there shone the bald head of Krivin, always to be found where the best people were. In that direction gazed the young men, not venturing to approach. There, too, she descried Stiva, and there she saw the exquisite figure and head of Anna in a black velvet gown. And he was there. Kitty had not seen him since the evening she refused Levin. With her long-sighted eyes, she knew him at once, and was even aware that he was looking at her.\n\n“Another turn, eh? You’re not tired?” said Korsunsky, a little out of breath.\n\n“No, thank you!”\n\n“Where shall I take you?”\n\n“Madame Karenina’s here, I think ... take me to her.”\n\n“Wherever you command.”\n\nAnd Korsunsky began waltzing with measured steps straight towards the group in the left corner, continually saying, “Pardon, mesdames, pardon, pardon, mesdames”; and steering his course through the sea of lace, tulle, and ribbon, and not disarranging a feather, he turned his partner sharply round, so that her slim ankles, in light transparent stockings, were exposed to view, and her train floated out in fan shape and covered Krivin’s knees. Korsunsky bowed, set straight his open shirt front, and gave her his arm to conduct her to Anna Arkadyevna. Kitty, flushed, took her train from Krivin’s knees, and, a little giddy, looked round, seeking Anna. Anna was not in lilac, as Kitty had so urgently wished, but in a black, low-cut, velvet gown, showing her full throat and shoulders, that looked as though carved in old ivory, and her rounded arms, with tiny, slender wrists. The whole gown was trimmed with Venetian guipure. On her head, among her black hair—her own, with no false additions—was a little wreath of pansies, and a bouquet of the same in the black ribbon of her sash among white lace. Her coiffure was not striking. All that was noticeable was the little wilful tendrils of her curly hair that would always break free about her neck and temples. Round her well-cut, strong neck was a thread of pearls.\n\nKitty had been seeing Anna every day; she adored her, and had pictured her invariably in lilac. But now seeing her in black, she felt that she had not fully seen her charm. She saw her now as someone quite new and surprising to her. Now she understood that Anna could not have been in lilac, and that her charm was just that she always stood out against her attire, that her dress could never be noticeable on her. And her black dress, with its sumptuous lace, was not noticeable on her; it was only the frame, and all that was seen was she—simple, natural, elegant, and at the same time gay and eager.\n\nShe was standing holding herself, as always, very erect, and when Kitty drew near the group she was speaking to the master of the house, her head slightly turned towards him.\n\n“No, I don’t throw stones,” she was saying, in answer to something, “though I can’t understand it,” she went on, shrugging her shoulders, and she turned at once with a soft smile of protection towards Kitty. With a flying, feminine glance she scanned her attire, and made a movement of her head, hardly perceptible, but understood by Kitty, signifying approval of her dress and her looks. “You came into the room dancing,” she added.\n\n“This is one of my most faithful supporters,” said Korsunsky, bowing to Anna Arkadyevna, whom he had not yet seen. “The princess helps to make balls happy and successful. Anna Arkadyevna, a waltz?” he said, bending down to her.\n\n“Why, have you met?” inquired their host.\n\n“Is there anyone we have not met? My wife and I are like white wolves—everyone knows us,” answered Korsunsky. “A waltz, Anna Arkadyevna?”\n\n“I don’t dance when it’s possible not to dance,” she said.\n\n“But tonight it’s impossible,” answered Korsunsky.\n\nAt that instant Vronsky came up.\n\n“Well, since it’s impossible tonight, let us start,” she said, not noticing Vronsky’s bow, and she hastily put her hand on Korsunsky’s shoulder.\n\n“What is she vexed with him about?” thought Kitty, discerning that Anna had intentionally not responded to Vronsky’s bow. Vronsky went up to Kitty reminding her of the first quadrille, and expressing his regret that he had not seen her all this time. Kitty gazed in admiration at Anna waltzing, and listened to him. She expected him to ask her for a waltz, but he did not, and she glanced wonderingly at him. He flushed slightly, and hurriedly asked her to waltz, but he had only just put his arm round her waist and taken the first step when the music suddenly stopped. Kitty looked into his face, which was so close to her own, and long afterwards—for several years after—that look, full of love, to which he made no response, cut her to the heart with an agony of shame.\n\n“Pardon! pardon! Waltz! waltz!” shouted Korsunsky from the other side of the room, and seizing the first young lady he came across he began dancing himself.\n\n# Chapter 23\n\nVronsky and Kitty waltzed several times round the room. After the first waltz Kitty went to her mother, and she had hardly time to say a few words to Countess Nordston when Vronsky came up again for the first quadrille. During the quadrille nothing of any significance was said: there was disjointed talk between them of the Korsunskys, husband and wife, whom he described very amusingly, as delightful children at forty, and of the future town theater; and only once the conversation touched her to the quick, when he asked her about Levin, whether he was here, and added that he liked him so much. But Kitty did not expect much from the quadrille. She looked forward with a thrill at her heart to the mazurka. She fancied that in the mazurka everything must be decided. The fact that he did not during the quadrille ask her for the mazurka did not trouble her. She felt sure she would dance the mazurka with him as she had done at former balls, and refused five young men, saying she was engaged for the mazurka. The whole ball up to the last quadrille was for Kitty an enchanted vision of delightful colors, sounds, and motions. She only sat down when she felt too tired and begged for a rest. But as she was dancing the last quadrille with one of the tiresome young men whom she could not refuse, she chanced to be vis-à-vis with Vronsky and Anna. She had not been near Anna again since the beginning of the evening, and now again she saw her suddenly quite new and surprising. She saw in her the signs of that excitement of success she knew so well in herself; she saw that she was intoxicated with the delighted admiration she was exciting. She knew that feeling and knew its signs, and saw them in Anna; saw the quivering, flashing light in her eyes, and the smile of happiness and excitement unconsciously playing on her lips, and the deliberate grace, precision, and lightness of her movements.\n\n“Who?” she asked herself. “All or one?” And not assisting the harassed young man she was dancing with in the conversation, the thread of which he had lost and could not pick up again, she obeyed with external liveliness the peremptory shouts of Korsunsky starting them all into the grand rond, and then into the chaîne, and at the same time she kept watch with a growing pang at her heart. “No, it’s not the admiration of the crowd has intoxicated her, but the adoration of one. And that one? can it be he?” Every time he spoke to Anna the joyous light flashed into her eyes, and the smile of happiness curved her red lips. She seemed to make an effort to control herself, to try not to show these signs of delight, but they came out on her face of themselves. “But what of him?” Kitty looked at him and was filled with terror. What was pictured so clearly to Kitty in the mirror of Anna’s face she saw in him. What had become of his always self-possessed resolute manner, and the carelessly serene expression of his face? Now every time he turned to her, he bent his head, as though he would have fallen at her feet, and in his eyes there was nothing but humble submission and dread. “I would not offend you,” his eyes seemed every time to be saying, “but I want to save myself, and I don’t know how.” On his face was a look such as Kitty had never seen before.\n\nThey were speaking of common acquaintances, keeping up the most trivial conversation, but to Kitty it seemed that every word they said was determining their fate and hers. And strange it was that they were actually talking of how absurd Ivan Ivanovitch was with his French, and how the Eletsky girl might have made a better match, yet these words had all the while consequence for them, and they were feeling just as Kitty did. The whole ball, the whole world, everything seemed lost in fog in Kitty’s soul. Nothing but the stern discipline of her bringing-up supported her and forced her to do what was expected of her, that is, to dance, to answer questions, to talk, even to smile. But before the mazurka, when they were beginning to rearrange the chairs and a few couples moved out of the smaller rooms into the big room, a moment of despair and horror came for Kitty. She had refused five partners, and now she was not dancing the mazurka. She had not even a hope of being asked for it, because she was so successful in society that the idea would never occur to anyone that she had remained disengaged till now. She would have to tell her mother she felt ill and go home, but she had not the strength to do this. She felt crushed. She went to the furthest end of the little drawing-room and sank into a low chair. Her light, transparent skirts rose like a cloud about her slender waist; one bare, thin, soft, girlish arm, hanging listlessly, was lost in the folds of her pink tunic; in the other she held her fan, and with rapid, short strokes fanned her burning face. But while she looked like a butterfly, clinging to a blade of grass, and just about to open its rainbow wings for fresh flight, her heart ached with a horrible despair.\n\n“But perhaps I am wrong, perhaps it was not so?” And again she recalled all she had seen.\n\n“Kitty, what is it?” said Countess Nordston, stepping noiselessly over the carpet towards her. “I don’t understand it.”\n\nKitty’s lower lip began to quiver; she got up quickly.\n\n“Kitty, you’re not dancing the mazurka?”\n\n“No, no,” said Kitty in a voice shaking with tears.\n\n“He asked her for the mazurka before me,” said Countess Nordston, knowing Kitty would understand who were “he” and “her.” “She said: ‘Why, aren’t you going to dance it with Princess Shtcherbatskaya?’”\n\n“Oh, I don’t care!” answered Kitty.\n\nNo one but she herself understood her position; no one knew that she had just refused the man whom perhaps she loved, and refused him because she had put her faith in another.\n\nCountess Nordston found Korsunsky, with whom she was to dance the mazurka, and told him to ask Kitty.\n\nKitty danced in the first couple, and luckily for her she had not to talk, because Korsunsky was all the time running about directing the figure. Vronsky and Anna sat almost opposite her. She saw them with her long-sighted eyes, and saw them, too, close by, when they met in the figures, and the more she saw of them the more convinced was she that her unhappiness was complete. She saw that they felt themselves alone in that crowded room. And on Vronsky’s face, always so firm and independent, she saw that look that had struck her, of bewilderment and humble submissiveness, like the expression of an intelligent dog when it has done wrong.\n\nAnna smiled, and her smile was reflected by him. She grew thoughtful, and he became serious. Some supernatural force drew Kitty’s eyes to Anna’s face. She was fascinating in her simple black dress, fascinating were her round arms with their bracelets, fascinating was her firm neck with its thread of pearls, fascinating the straying curls of her loose hair, fascinating the graceful, light movements of her little feet and hands, fascinating was that lovely face in its eagerness, but there was something terrible and cruel in her fascination.\n\nKitty admired her more than ever, and more and more acute was her suffering. Kitty felt overwhelmed, and her face showed it. When Vronsky saw her, coming across her in the mazurka, he did not at once recognize her, she was so changed.\n\n“Delightful ball!” he said to her, for the sake of saying something.\n\n“Yes,” she answered.\n\nIn the middle of the mazurka, repeating a complicated figure, newly invented by Korsunsky, Anna came forward into the center of the circle, chose two gentlemen, and summoned a lady and Kitty. Kitty gazed at her in dismay as she went up. Anna looked at her with drooping eyelids, and smiled, pressing her hand. But, noticing that Kitty only responded to her smile by a look of despair and amazement, she turned away from her, and began gaily talking to the other lady.\n\n“Yes, there is something uncanny, devilish and fascinating in her,” Kitty said to herself.\n\nAnna did not mean to stay to supper, but the master of the house began to press her to do so.\n\n“Nonsense, Anna Arkadyevna,” said Korsunsky, drawing her bare arm under the sleeve of his dress coat, “I’ve such an idea for a cotillion! Un bijou!”\n\nAnd he moved gradually on, trying to draw her along with him. Their host smiled approvingly.\n\n“No, I am not going to stay,” answered Anna, smiling, but in spite of her smile, both Korsunsky and the master of the house saw from her resolute tone that she would not stay.\n\n“No; why, as it is, I have danced more at your ball in Moscow than I have all the winter in Petersburg,” said Anna, looking round at Vronsky, who stood near her. “I must rest a little before my journey.”\n\n“Are you certainly going tomorrow then?” asked Vronsky.\n\n“Yes, I suppose so,” answered Anna, as it were wondering at the boldness of his question; but the irrepressible, quivering brilliance of her eyes and her smile set him on fire as she said it.\n\nAnna Arkadyevna did not stay to supper, but went home.\n\n# Chapter 24\n\n“Yes, there is something in me hateful, repulsive,” thought Levin, as he came away from the Shtcherbatskys’, and walked in the direction of his brother’s lodgings. “And I don’t get on with other people. Pride, they say. No, I have no pride. If I had any pride, I should not have put myself in such a position.” And he pictured to himself Vronsky, happy, good-natured, clever, and self-possessed, certainly never placed in the awful position in which he had been that evening. “Yes, she was bound to choose him. So it had to be, and I cannot complain of anyone or anything. I am myself to blame. What right had I to imagine she would care to join her life to mine? Who am I and what am I? A nobody, not wanted by anyone, nor of use to anybody.” And he recalled his brother Nikolay, and dwelt with pleasure on the thought of him. “Isn’t he right that everything in the world is base and loathsome? And are we fair in our judgment of brother Nikolay? Of course, from the point of view of Prokofy, seeing him in a torn cloak and tipsy, he’s a despicable person. But I know him differently. I know his soul, and know that we are like him. And I, instead of going to seek him out, went out to dinner, and came here.” Levin walked up to a lamppost, read his brother’s address, which was in his pocketbook, and called a sledge. All the long way to his brother’s, Levin vividly recalled all the facts familiar to him of his brother Nikolay’s life. He remembered how his brother, while at the university, and for a year afterwards, had, in spite of the jeers of his companions, lived like a monk, strictly observing all religious rites, services, and fasts, and avoiding every sort of pleasure, especially women. And afterwards, how he had all at once broken out: he had associated with the most horrible people, and rushed into the most senseless debauchery. He remembered later the scandal over a boy, whom he had taken from the country to bring up, and, in a fit of rage, had so violently beaten that proceedings were brought against him for unlawfully wounding. Then he recalled the scandal with a sharper, to whom he had lost money, and given a promissory note, and against whom he had himself lodged a complaint, asserting that he had cheated him. (This was the money Sergey Ivanovitch had paid.) Then he remembered how he had spent a night in the lockup for disorderly conduct in the street. He remembered the shameful proceedings he had tried to get up against his brother Sergey Ivanovitch, accusing him of not having paid him his share of his mother’s fortune, and the last scandal, when he had gone to a western province in an official capacity, and there had got into trouble for assaulting a village elder.... It was all horribly disgusting, yet to Levin it appeared not at all in the same disgusting light as it inevitably would to those who did not know Nikolay, did not know all his story, did not know his heart.\n\nLevin remembered that when Nikolay had been in the devout stage, the period of fasts and monks and church services, when he was seeking in religion a support and a curb for his passionate temperament, everyone, far from encouraging him, had jeered at him, and he, too, with the others. They had teased him, called him Noah, and monk; and, when he had broken out, no one had helped him, but everyone had turned away from him with horror and disgust.\n\nLevin felt that, in spite of all the ugliness of his life, his brother Nikolay, in his soul, in the very depths of his soul, was no more in the wrong than the people who despised him. He was not to blame for having been born with his unbridled temperament and his somehow limited intelligence. But he had always wanted to be good. “I will tell him everything, without reserve, and I will make him speak without reserve, too, and I’ll show him that I love him, and so understand him,” Levin resolved to himself, as, towards eleven o’clock, he reached the hotel of which he had the address.\n\n“At the top, 12 and 13,” the porter answered Levin’s inquiry.\n\n“At home?”\n\n“Sure to be at home.”\n\nThe door of No. 12 was half open, and there came out into the streak of light thick fumes of cheap, poor tobacco, and the sound of a voice, unknown to Levin; but he knew at once that his brother was there; he heard his cough.\n\nAs he went in the door, the unknown voice was saying:\n\n“It all depends with how much judgment and knowledge the thing’s done.”\n\nKonstantin Levin looked in at the door, and saw that the speaker was a young man with an immense shock of hair, wearing a Russian jerkin, and that a pockmarked woman in a woolen gown, without collar or cuffs, was sitting on the sofa. His brother was not to be seen. Konstantin felt a sharp pang at his heart at the thought of the strange company in which his brother spent his life. No one had heard him, and Konstantin, taking off his galoshes, listened to what the gentleman in the jerkin was saying. He was speaking of some enterprise.\n\n“Well, the devil flay them, the privileged classes,” his brother’s voice responded, with a cough. “Masha! get us some supper and some wine if there’s any left; or else go and get some.”\n\nThe woman rose, came out from behind the screen, and saw Konstantin.\n\n“There’s some gentleman, Nikolay Dmitrievitch,” she said.\n\n“Whom do you want?” said the voice of Nikolay Levin, angrily.\n\n“It’s I,” answered Konstantin Levin, coming forward into the light.\n\n“Who’s I?” Nikolay’s voice said again, still more angrily. He could be heard getting up hurriedly, stumbling against something, and Levin saw, facing him in the doorway, the big, scared eyes, and the huge, thin, stooping figure of his brother, so familiar, and yet astonishing in its weirdness and sickliness.\n\nHe was even thinner than three years before, when Konstantin Levin had seen him last. He was wearing a short coat, and his hands and big bones seemed huger than ever. His hair had grown thinner, the same straight mustaches hid his lips, the same eyes gazed strangely and naïvely at his visitor.\n\n“Ah, Kostya!” he exclaimed suddenly, recognizing his brother, and his eyes lit up with joy. But the same second he looked round at the young man, and gave the nervous jerk of his head and neck that Konstantin knew so well, as if his neckband hurt him; and a quite different expression, wild, suffering, and cruel, rested on his emaciated face.\n\n“I wrote to you and Sergey Ivanovitch both that I don’t know you and don’t want to know you. What is it you want?”\n\nHe was not at all the same as Konstantin had been fancying him. The worst and most tiresome part of his character, what made all relations with him so difficult, had been forgotten by Konstantin Levin when he thought of him, and now, when he saw his face, and especially that nervous twitching of his head, he remembered it all.\n\n“I didn’t want to see you for anything,” he answered timidly. “I’ve simply come to see you.”\n\nHis brother’s timidity obviously softened Nikolay. His lips twitched.\n\n“Oh, so that’s it?” he said. “Well, come in; sit down. Like some supper? Masha, bring supper for three. No, stop a minute. Do you know who this is?” he said, addressing his brother, and indicating the gentleman in the jerkin: “This is Mr. Kritsky, my friend from Kiev, a very remarkable man. He’s persecuted by the police, of course, because he’s not a scoundrel.”\n\nAnd he looked round in the way he always did at everyone in the room. Seeing that the woman standing in the doorway was moving to go, he shouted to her, “Wait a minute, I said.” And with the inability to express himself, the incoherence that Konstantin knew so well, he began, with another look round at everyone, to tell his brother Kritsky’s story: how he had been expelled from the university for starting a benefit society for the poor students and Sunday schools; and how he had afterwards been a teacher in a peasant school, and how he had been driven out of that too, and had afterwards been condemned for something.\n\n“You’re of the Kiev university?” said Konstantin Levin to Kritsky, to break the awkward silence that followed.\n\n“Yes, I was of Kiev,” Kritsky replied angrily, his face darkening.\n\n“And this woman,” Nikolay Levin interrupted him, pointing to her, “is the partner of my life, Marya Nikolaevna. I took her out of a bad house,” and he jerked his neck saying this; “but I love her and respect her, and anyone who wants to know me,” he added, raising his voice and knitting his brows, “I beg to love her and respect her. She’s just the same as my wife, just the same. So now you know whom you’ve to do with. And if you think you’re lowering yourself, well, here’s the floor, there’s the door.”\n\nAnd again his eyes traveled inquiringly over all of them.\n\n“Why I should be lowering myself, I don’t understand.”\n\n“Then, Masha, tell them to bring supper; three portions, spirits and wine.... No, wait a minute.... No, it doesn’t matter.... Go along.”\n\n# Chapter 25\n\n“So you see,” pursued Nikolay Levin, painfully wrinkling his forehead and twitching.\n\nIt was obviously difficult for him to think of what to say and do.\n\n“Here, do you see?”... He pointed to some sort of iron bars, fastened together with strings, lying in a corner of the room. “Do you see that? That’s the beginning of a new thing we’re going into. It’s a productive association....”\n\nKonstantin scarcely heard him. He looked into his sickly, consumptive face, and he was more and more sorry for him, and he could not force himself to listen to what his brother was telling him about the association. He saw that this association was a mere anchor to save him from self-contempt. Nikolay Levin went on talking:\n\n“You know that capital oppresses the laborer. The laborers with us, the peasants, bear all the burden of labor, and are so placed that however much they work they can’t escape from their position of beasts of burden. All the profits of labor, on which they might improve their position, and gain leisure for themselves, and after that education, all the surplus values are taken from them by the capitalists. And society’s so constituted that the harder they work, the greater the profit of the merchants and landowners, while they stay beasts of burden to the end. And that state of things must be changed,” he finished up, and he looked questioningly at his brother.\n\n“Yes, of course,” said Konstantin, looking at the patch of red that had come out on his brother’s projecting cheekbones.\n\n“And so we’re founding a locksmiths’ association, where all the production and profit and the chief instruments of production will be in common.”\n\n“Where is the association to be?” asked Konstantin Levin.\n\n“In the village of Vozdrem, Kazan government.”\n\n“But why in a village? In the villages, I think, there is plenty of work as it is. Why a locksmiths’ association in a village?”\n\n“Why? Because the peasants are just as much slaves as they ever were, and that’s why you and Sergey Ivanovitch don’t like people to try and get them out of their slavery,” said Nikolay Levin, exasperated by the objection.\n\nKonstantin Levin sighed, looking meanwhile about the cheerless and dirty room. This sigh seemed to exasperate Nikolay still more.\n\n“I know your and Sergey Ivanovitch’s aristocratic views. I know that he applies all the power of his intellect to justify existing evils.”\n\n“No; and what do you talk of Sergey Ivanovitch for?” said Levin, smiling.\n\n“Sergey Ivanovitch? I’ll tell you what for!” Nikolay Levin shrieked suddenly at the name of Sergey Ivanovitch. “I’ll tell you what for.... But what’s the use of talking? There’s only one thing.... What did you come to me for? You look down on this, and you’re welcome to,—and go away, in God’s name go away!” he shrieked, getting up from his chair. “And go away, and go away!”\n\n“I don’t look down on it at all,” said Konstantin Levin timidly. “I don’t even dispute it.”\n\nAt that instant Marya Nikolaevna came back. Nikolay Levin looked round angrily at her. She went quickly to him, and whispered something.\n\n“I’m not well; I’ve grown irritable,” said Nikolay Levin, getting calmer and breathing painfully; “and then you talk to me of Sergey Ivanovitch and his article. It’s such rubbish, such lying, such self-deception. What can a man write of justice who knows nothing of it? Have you read his article?” he asked Kritsky, sitting down again at the table, and moving back off half of it the scattered cigarettes, so as to clear a space.\n\n“I’ve not read it,” Kritsky responded gloomily, obviously not desiring to enter into the conversation.\n\n“Why not?” said Nikolay Levin, now turning with exasperation upon Kritsky.\n\n“Because I didn’t see the use of wasting my time over it.”\n\n“Oh, but excuse me, how did you know it would be wasting your time? That article’s too deep for many people—that’s to say it’s over their heads. But with me, it’s another thing; I see through his ideas, and I know where its weakness lies.”\n\nEveryone was mute. Kritsky got up deliberately and reached his cap.\n\n“Won’t you have supper? All right, good-bye! Come round tomorrow with the locksmith.”\n\nKritsky had hardly gone out when Nikolay Levin smiled and winked.\n\n“He’s no good either,” he said. “I see, of course....”\n\nBut at that instant Kritsky, at the door, called him....\n\n“What do you want now?” he said, and went out to him in the passage. Left alone with Marya Nikolaevna, Levin turned to her.\n\n“Have you been long with my brother?” he said to her.\n\n“Yes, more than a year. Nikolay Dmitrievitch’s health has become very poor. Nikolay Dmitrievitch drinks a great deal,” she said.\n\n“That is ... how does he drink?”\n\n“Drinks vodka, and it’s bad for him.”\n\n“And a great deal?” whispered Levin.\n\n“Yes,” she said, looking timidly towards the doorway, where Nikolay Levin had reappeared.\n\n“What were you talking about?” he said, knitting his brows, and turning his scared eyes from one to the other. “What was it?”\n\n“Oh, nothing,” Konstantin answered in confusion.\n\n“Oh, if you don’t want to say, don’t. Only it’s no good your talking to her. She’s a wench, and you’re a gentleman,” he said with a jerk of the neck. “You understand everything, I see, and have taken stock of everything, and look with commiseration on my shortcomings,” he began again, raising his voice.\n\n“Nikolay Dmitrievitch, Nikolay Dmitrievitch,” whispered Marya Nikolaevna, again going up to him.\n\n“Oh, very well, very well!... But where’s the supper? Ah, here it is,” he said, seeing a waiter with a tray. “Here, set it here,” he added angrily, and promptly seizing the vodka, he poured out a glassful and drank it greedily. “Like a drink?” he turned to his brother, and at once became better humored.\n\n“Well, enough of Sergey Ivanovitch. I’m glad to see you, anyway. After all’s said and done, we’re not strangers. Come, have a drink. Tell me what you’re doing,” he went on, greedily munching a piece of bread, and pouring out another glassful. “How are you living?”\n\n“I live alone in the country, as I used to. I’m busy looking after the land,” answered Konstantin, watching with horror the greediness with which his brother ate and drank, and trying to conceal that he noticed it.\n\n“Why don’t you get married?”\n\n“It hasn’t happened so,” Konstantin answered, reddening a little.\n\n“Why not? For me now ... everything’s at an end! I’ve made a mess of my life. But this I’ve said, and I say still, that if my share had been given me when I needed it, my whole life would have been different.”\n\nKonstantin made haste to change the conversation.\n\n“Do you know your little Vanya’s with me, a clerk in the countinghouse at Pokrovskoe.”\n\nNikolay jerked his neck, and sank into thought.\n\n“Yes, tell me what’s going on at Pokrovskoe. Is the house standing still, and the birch trees, and our schoolroom? And Philip the gardener, is he living? How I remember the arbor and the seat! Now mind and don’t alter anything in the house, but make haste and get married, and make everything as it used to be again. Then I’ll come and see you, if your wife is nice.”\n\n“But come to me now,” said Levin. “How nicely we would arrange it!”\n\n“I’d come and see you if I were sure I should not find Sergey Ivanovitch.”\n\n“You wouldn’t find him there. I live quite independently of him.”\n\n“Yes, but say what you like, you will have to choose between me and him,” he said, looking timidly into his brother’s face.\n\nThis timidity touched Konstantin.\n\n“If you want to hear my confession of faith on the subject, I tell you that in your quarrel with Sergey Ivanovitch I take neither side. You’re both wrong. You’re more wrong externally, and he inwardly.”\n\n“Ah, ah! You see that, you see that!” Nikolay shouted joyfully.\n\n“But I personally value friendly relations with you more because....”\n\n“Why, why?”\n\nKonstantin could not say that he valued it more because Nikolay was unhappy, and needed affection. But Nikolay knew that this was just what he meant to say, and scowling he took up the vodka again.\n\n“Enough, Nikolay Dmitrievitch!” said Marya Nikolaevna, stretching out her plump, bare arm towards the decanter.\n\n“Let it be! Don’t insist! I’ll beat you!” he shouted.\n\nMarya Nikolaevna smiled a sweet and good-humored smile, which was at once reflected on Nikolay’s face, and she took the bottle.\n\n“And do you suppose she understands nothing?” said Nikolay. “She understands it all better than any of us. Isn’t it true there’s something good and sweet in her?”\n\n“Were you never before in Moscow?” Konstantin said to her, for the sake of saying something.\n\n“Only you mustn’t be polite and stiff with her. It frightens her. No one ever spoke to her so but the justices of the peace who tried her for trying to get out of a house of ill-fame. Mercy on us, the senselessness in the world!” he cried suddenly. “These new institutions, these justices of the peace, rural councils, what hideousness it all is!”\n\nAnd he began to enlarge on his encounters with the new institutions.\n\nKonstantin Levin heard him, and the disbelief in the sense of all public institutions, which he shared with him, and often expressed, was distasteful to him now from his brother’s lips.\n\n“In another world we shall understand it all,” he said lightly.\n\n“In another world! Ah, I don’t like that other world! I don’t like it,” he said, letting his scared eyes rest on his brother’s eyes. “Here one would think that to get out of all the baseness and the mess, one’s own and other people’s, would be a good thing, and yet I’m afraid of death, awfully afraid of death.” He shuddered. “But do drink something. Would you like some champagne? Or shall we go somewhere? Let’s go to the Gypsies! Do you know I have got so fond of the Gypsies and Russian songs.”\n\nHis speech had begun to falter, and he passed abruptly from one subject to another. Konstantin with the help of Masha persuaded him not to go out anywhere, and got him to bed hopelessly drunk.\n\nMasha promised to write to Konstantin in case of need, and to persuade Nikolay Levin to go and stay with his brother.\n\n# Chapter 26\n\nIn the morning Konstantin Levin left Moscow, and towards evening he reached home. On the journey in the train he talked to his neighbors about politics and the new railways, and, just as in Moscow, he was overcome by a sense of confusion of ideas, dissatisfaction with himself, shame of something or other. But when he got out at his own station, when he saw his one-eyed coachman, Ignat, with the collar of his coat turned up; when, in the dim light reflected by the station fires, he saw his own sledge, his own horses with their tails tied up, in their harness trimmed with rings and tassels; when the coachman Ignat, as he put in his luggage, told him the village news, that the contractor had arrived, and that Pava had calved,—he felt that little by little the confusion was clearing up, and the shame and self-dissatisfaction were passing away. He felt this at the mere sight of Ignat and the horses; but when he had put on the sheepskin brought for him, had sat down wrapped up in the sledge, and had driven off pondering on the work that lay before him in the village, and staring at the side-horse, that had been his saddle-horse, past his prime now, but a spirited beast from the Don, he began to see what had happened to him in quite a different light. He felt himself, and did not want to be anyone else. All he wanted now was to be better than before. In the first place he resolved that from that day he would give up hoping for any extraordinary happiness, such as marriage must have given him, and consequently he would not so disdain what he really had. Secondly, he would never again let himself give way to low passion, the memory of which had so tortured him when he had been making up his mind to make an offer. Then remembering his brother Nikolay, he resolved to himself that he would never allow himself to forget him, that he would follow him up, and not lose sight of him, so as to be ready to help when things should go ill with him. And that would be soon, he felt. Then, too, his brother’s talk of communism, which he had treated so lightly at the time, now made him think. He considered a revolution in economic conditions nonsense. But he always felt the injustice of his own abundance in comparison with the poverty of the peasants, and now he determined that so as to feel quite in the right, though he had worked hard and lived by no means luxuriously before, he would now work still harder, and would allow himself even less luxury. And all this seemed to him so easy a conquest over himself that he spent the whole drive in the pleasantest daydreams. With a resolute feeling of hope in a new, better life, he reached home before nine o’clock at night.\n\nThe snow of the little quadrangle before the house was lit up by a light in the bedroom windows of his old nurse, Agafea Mihalovna, who performed the duties of housekeeper in his house. She was not yet asleep. Kouzma, waked up by her, came sidling sleepily out onto the steps. A setter bitch, Laska, ran out too, almost upsetting Kouzma, and whining, turned round about Levin’s knees, jumping up and longing, but not daring, to put her forepaws on his chest.\n\n“You’re soon back again, sir,” said Agafea Mihalovna.\n\n“I got tired of it, Agafea Mihalovna. With friends, one is well; but at home, one is better,” he answered, and went into his study.\n\nThe study was slowly lit up as the candle was brought in. The familiar details came out: the stag’s horns, the bookshelves, the looking-glass, the stove with its ventilator, which had long wanted mending, his father’s sofa, a large table, on the table an open book, a broken ashtray, a manuscript book with his handwriting. As he saw all this, there came over him for an instant a doubt of the possibility of arranging the new life, of which he had been dreaming on the road. All these traces of his life seemed to clutch him, and to say to him: “No, you’re not going to get away from us, and you’re not going to be different, but you’re going to be the same as you’ve always been; with doubts, everlasting dissatisfaction with yourself, vain efforts to amend, and falls, and everlasting expectation, of a happiness which you won’t get, and which isn’t possible for you.”\n\nThis the things said to him, but another voice in his heart was telling him that he must not fall under the sway of the past, and that one can do anything with oneself. And hearing that voice, he went into the corner where stood his two heavy dumbbells, and began brandishing them like a gymnast, trying to restore his confident temper. There was a creak of steps at the door. He hastily put down the dumbbells.\n\nThe bailiff came in, and said everything, thank God, was doing well; but informed him that the buckwheat in the new drying machine had been a little scorched. This piece of news irritated Levin. The new drying machine had been constructed and partly invented by Levin. The bailiff had always been against the drying machine, and now it was with suppressed triumph that he announced that the buckwheat had been scorched. Levin was firmly convinced that if the buckwheat had been scorched, it was only because the precautions had not been taken, for which he had hundreds of times given orders. He was annoyed, and reprimanded the bailiff. But there had been an important and joyful event: Pava, his best cow, an expensive beast, bought at a show, had calved.\n\n“Kouzma, give me my sheepskin. And you tell them to take a lantern. I’ll come and look at her,” he said to the bailiff.\n\nThe cowhouse for the more valuable cows was just behind the house. Walking across the yard, passing a snowdrift by the lilac tree, he went into the cowhouse. There was the warm, steamy smell of dung when the frozen door was opened, and the cows, astonished at the unfamiliar light of the lantern, stirred on the fresh straw. He caught a glimpse of the broad, smooth, black and piebald back of Hollandka. Berkoot, the bull, was lying down with his ring in his lip, and seemed about to get up, but thought better of it, and only gave two snorts as they passed by him. Pava, a perfect beauty, huge as a hippopotamus, with her back turned to them, prevented their seeing the calf, as she sniffed her all over.\n\nLevin went into the pen, looked Pava over, and lifted the red and spotted calf onto her long, tottering legs. Pava, uneasy, began lowing, but when Levin put the calf close to her she was soothed, and, sighing heavily, began licking her with her rough tongue. The calf, fumbling, poked her nose under her mother’s udder, and stiffened her tail out straight.\n\n“Here, bring the light, Fyodor, this way,” said Levin, examining the calf. “Like the mother! though the color takes after the father; but that’s nothing. Very good. Long and broad in the haunch. Vassily Fedorovitch, isn’t she splendid?” he said to the bailiff, quite forgiving him for the buckwheat under the influence of his delight in the calf.\n\n“How could she fail to be? Oh, Semyon the contractor came the day after you left. You must settle with him, Konstantin Dmitrievitch,” said the bailiff. “I did inform you about the machine.”\n\nThis question was enough to take Levin back to all the details of his work on the estate, which was on a large scale, and complicated. He went straight from the cowhouse to the counting house, and after a little conversation with the bailiff and Semyon the contractor, he went back to the house and straight upstairs to the drawing-room.\n\n# Chapter 27\n\nThe house was big and old-fashioned, and Levin, though he lived alone, had the whole house heated and used. He knew that this was stupid, he knew that it was positively not right, and contrary to his present new plans, but this house was a whole world to Levin. It was the world in which his father and mother had lived and died. They had lived just the life that to Levin seemed the ideal of perfection, and that he had dreamed of beginning with his wife, his family.\n\nLevin scarcely remembered his mother. His conception of her was for him a sacred memory, and his future wife was bound to be in his imagination a repetition of that exquisite, holy ideal of a woman that his mother had been.\n\nHe was so far from conceiving of love for woman apart from marriage that he positively pictured to himself first the family, and only secondarily the woman who would give him a family. His ideas of marriage were, consequently, quite unlike those of the great majority of his acquaintances, for whom getting married was one of the numerous facts of social life. For Levin it was the chief affair of life, on which its whole happiness turned. And now he had to give up that.\n\nWhen he had gone into the little drawing-room, where he always had tea, and had settled himself in his armchair with a book, and Agafea Mihalovna had brought him tea, and with her usual, “Well, I’ll stay a while, sir,” had taken a chair in the window, he felt that, however strange it might be, he had not parted from his daydreams, and that he could not live without them. Whether with her, or with another, still it would be. He was reading a book, and thinking of what he was reading, and stopping to listen to Agafea Mihalovna, who gossiped away without flagging, and yet with all that, all sorts of pictures of family life and work in the future rose disconnectedly before his imagination. He felt that in the depth of his soul something had been put in its place, settled down, and laid to rest.\n\nHe heard Agafea Mihalovna talking of how Prohor had forgotten his duty to God, and with the money Levin had given him to buy a horse, had been drinking without stopping, and had beaten his wife till he’d half killed her. He listened, and read his book, and recalled the whole train of ideas suggested by his reading. It was Tyndall’s Treatise on Heat. He recalled his own criticisms of Tyndall of his complacent satisfaction in the cleverness of his experiments, and for his lack of philosophic insight. And suddenly there floated into his mind the joyful thought: “In two years’ time I shall have two Dutch cows; Pava herself will perhaps still be alive, a dozen young daughters of Berkoot and the three others—how lovely!”\n\nHe took up his book again. “Very good, electricity and heat are the same thing; but is it possible to substitute the one quantity for the other in the equation for the solution of any problem? No. Well, then what of it? The connection between all the forces of nature is felt instinctively.... It’s particulary nice if Pava’s daughter should be a red-spotted cow, and all the herd will take after her, and the other three, too! Splendid! To go out with my wife and visitors to meet the herd.... My wife says, ‘Kostya and I looked after that calf like a child.’ ‘How can it interest you so much?’ says a visitor. ‘Everything that interests him, interests me.’ But who will she be?” And he remembered what had happened at Moscow.... “Well, there’s nothing to be done.... It’s not my fault. But now everything shall go on in a new way. It’s nonsense to pretend that life won’t let one, that the past won’t let one. One must struggle to live better, much better.”... He raised his head, and fell to dreaming. Old Laska, who had not yet fully digested her delight at his return, and had run out into the yard to bark, came back wagging her tail, and crept up to him, bringing in the scent of fresh air, put her head under his hand, and whined plaintively, asking to be stroked.\n\n“There, who’d have thought it?” said Agafea Mihalovna. “The dog now ... why, she understands that her master’s come home, and that he’s low-spirited.”\n\n“Why low-spirited?”\n\n“Do you suppose I don’t see it, sir? It’s high time I should know the gentry. Why, I’ve grown up from a little thing with them. It’s nothing, sir, so long as there’s health and a clear conscience.”\n\nLevin looked intently at her, surprised at how well she knew his thought.\n\n“Shall I fetch you another cup?” said she, and taking his cup she went out.\n\nLaska kept poking her head under his hand. He stroked her, and she promptly curled up at his feet, laying her head on a hindpaw. And in token of all now being well and satisfactory, she opened her mouth a little, smacked her lips, and settling her sticky lips more comfortably about her old teeth, she sank into blissful repose. Levin watched all her movements attentively.\n\n“That’s what I’ll do,” he said to himself; “that’s what I’ll do! Nothing’s amiss.... All’s well.”\n\n# Chapter 28\n\nAfter the ball, early next morning, Anna Arkadyevna sent her husband a telegram that she was leaving Moscow the same day.\n\n“No, I must go, I must go”; she explained to her sister-in-law the change in her plans in a tone that suggested that she had to remember so many things that there was no enumerating them: “no, it had really better be today!”\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch was not dining at home, but he promised to come and see his sister off at seven o’clock.\n\nKitty, too, did not come, sending a note that she had a headache. Dolly and Anna dined alone with the children and the English governess. Whether it was that the children were fickle, or that they had acute senses, and felt that Anna was quite different that day from what she had been when they had taken such a fancy to her, that she was not now interested in them,—but they had abruptly dropped their play with their aunt, and their love for her, and were quite indifferent that she was going away. Anna was absorbed the whole morning in preparations for her departure. She wrote notes to her Moscow acquaintances, put down her accounts, and packed. Altogether Dolly fancied she was not in a placid state of mind, but in that worried mood, which Dolly knew well with herself, and which does not come without cause, and for the most part covers dissatisfaction with self. After dinner, Anna went up to her room to dress, and Dolly followed her.\n\n“How queer you are today!” Dolly said to her.\n\n“I? Do you think so? I’m not queer, but I’m nasty. I am like that sometimes. I keep feeling as if I could cry. It’s very stupid, but it’ll pass off,” said Anna quickly, and she bent her flushed face over a tiny bag in which she was packing a nightcap and some cambric handkerchiefs. Her eyes were particularly bright, and were continually swimming with tears. “In the same way I didn’t want to leave Petersburg, and now I don’t want to go away from here.”\n\n“You came here and did a good deed,” said Dolly, looking intently at her.\n\nAnna looked at her with eyes wet with tears.\n\n“Don’t say that, Dolly. I’ve done nothing, and could do nothing. I often wonder why people are all in league to spoil me. What have I done, and what could I do? In your heart there was found love enough to forgive....”\n\n“If it had not been for you, God knows what would have happened! How happy you are, Anna!” said Dolly. “Everything is clear and good in your heart.”\n\n“Every heart has its own skeletons, as the English say.”\n\n“You have no sort of skeleton, have you? Everything is so clear in you.”\n\n“I have!” said Anna suddenly, and, unexpectedly after her tears, a sly, ironical smile curved her lips.\n\n“Come, he’s amusing, anyway, your skeleton, and not depressing,” said Dolly, smiling.\n\n“No, he’s depressing. Do you know why I’m going today instead of tomorrow? It’s a confession that weighs on me; I want to make it to you,” said Anna, letting herself drop definitely into an armchair, and looking straight into Dolly’s face.\n\nAnd to her surprise Dolly saw that Anna was blushing up to her ears, up to the curly black ringlets on her neck.\n\n“Yes,” Anna went on. “Do you know why Kitty didn’t come to dinner? She’s jealous of me. I have spoiled ... I’ve been the cause of that ball being a torture to her instead of a pleasure. But truly, truly, it’s not my fault, or only my fault a little bit,” she said, daintily drawling the words “a little bit.”\n\n“Oh, how like Stiva you said that!” said Dolly, laughing.\n\nAnna was hurt.\n\n“Oh no, oh no! I’m not Stiva,” she said, knitting her brows. “That’s why I’m telling you, just because I could never let myself doubt myself for an instant,” said Anna.\n\nBut at the very moment she was uttering the words, she felt that they were not true. She was not merely doubting herself, she felt emotion at the thought of Vronsky, and was going away sooner than she had meant, simply to avoid meeting him.\n\n“Yes, Stiva told me you danced the mazurka with him, and that he....”\n\n“You can’t imagine how absurdly it all came about. I only meant to be matchmaking, and all at once it turned out quite differently. Possibly against my own will....”\n\nShe crimsoned and stopped.\n\n“Oh, they feel it directly?” said Dolly.\n\n“But I should be in despair if there were anything serious in it on his side,” Anna interrupted her. “And I am certain it will all be forgotten, and Kitty will leave off hating me.”\n\n“All the same, Anna, to tell you the truth, I’m not very anxious for this marriage for Kitty. And it’s better it should come to nothing, if he, Vronsky, is capable of falling in love with you in a single day.”\n\n“Oh, heavens, that would be too silly!” said Anna, and again a deep flush of pleasure came out on her face, when she heard the idea, that absorbed her, put into words. “And so here I am going away, having made an enemy of Kitty, whom I liked so much! Ah, how sweet she is! But you’ll make it right, Dolly? Eh?”\n\nDolly could scarcely suppress a smile. She loved Anna, but she enjoyed seeing that she too had her weaknesses.\n\n“An enemy? That can’t be.”\n\n“I did so want you all to care for me, as I do for you, and now I care for you more than ever,” said Anna, with tears in her eyes. “Ah, how silly I am today!”\n\nShe passed her handkerchief over her face and began dressing.\n\nAt the very moment of starting Stepan Arkadyevitch arrived, late, rosy and good-humored, smelling of wine and cigars.\n\nAnna’s emotionalism infected Dolly, and when she embraced her sister-in-law for the last time, she whispered: “Remember, Anna, what you’ve done for me—I shall never forget. And remember that I love you, and shall always love you as my dearest friend!”\n\n“I don’t know why,” said Anna, kissing her and hiding her tears.\n\n“You understood me, and you understand. Good-bye, my darling!”\n\n# Chapter 29\n\n“Come, it’s all over, and thank God!” was the first thought that came to Anna Arkadyevna, when she had said good-bye for the last time to her brother, who had stood blocking up the entrance to the carriage till the third bell rang. She sat down on her lounge beside Annushka, and looked about her in the twilight of the sleeping-carriage. “Thank God! tomorrow I shall see Seryozha and Alexey Alexandrovitch, and my life will go on in the old way, all nice and as usual.”\n\nStill in the same anxious frame of mind, as she had been all that day, Anna took pleasure in arranging herself for the journey with great care. With her little deft hands she opened and shut her little red bag, took out a cushion, laid it on her knees, and carefully wrapping up her feet, settled herself comfortably. An invalid lady had already lain down to sleep. Two other ladies began talking to Anna, and a stout elderly lady tucked up her feet, and made observations about the heating of the train. Anna answered a few words, but not foreseeing any entertainment from the conversation, she asked Annushka to get a lamp, hooked it onto the arm of her seat, and took from her bag a paper-knife and an English novel. At first her reading made no progress. The fuss and bustle were disturbing; then when the train had started, she could not help listening to the noises; then the snow beating on the left window and sticking to the pane, and the sight of the muffled guard passing by, covered with snow on one side, and the conversations about the terrible snowstorm raging outside, distracted her attention. Farther on, it was continually the same again and again: the same shaking and rattling, the same snow on the window, the same rapid transitions from steaming heat to cold, and back again to heat, the same passing glimpses of the same figures in the twilight, and the same voices, and Anna began to read and to understand what she read. Annushka was already dozing, the red bag on her lap, clutched by her broad hands, in gloves, of which one was torn. Anna Arkadyevna read and understood, but it was distasteful to her to read, that is, to follow the reflection of other people’s lives. She had too great a desire to live herself. If she read that the heroine of the novel was nursing a sick man, she longed to move with noiseless steps about the room of a sick man; if she read of a member of Parliament making a speech, she longed to be delivering the speech; if she read of how Lady Mary had ridden after the hounds, and had provoked her sister-in-law, and had surprised everyone by her boldness, she too wished to be doing the same. But there was no chance of doing anything; and twisting the smooth paper-knife in her little hands, she forced herself to read.\n\nThe hero of the novel was already almost reaching his English happiness, a baronetcy and an estate, and Anna was feeling a desire to go with him to the estate, when she suddenly felt that he ought to feel ashamed, and that she was ashamed of the same thing. But what had he to be ashamed of? “What have I to be ashamed of?” she asked herself in injured surprise. She laid down the book and sank against the back of the chair, tightly gripping the paper-cutter in both hands. There was nothing. She went over all her Moscow recollections. All were good, pleasant. She remembered the ball, remembered Vronsky and his face of slavish adoration, remembered all her conduct with him: there was nothing shameful. And for all that, at the same point in her memories, the feeling of shame was intensified, as though some inner voice, just at the point when she thought of Vronsky, were saying to her, “Warm, very warm, hot.” “Well, what is it?” she said to herself resolutely, shifting her seat in the lounge. “What does it mean? Am I afraid to look it straight in the face? Why, what is it? Can it be that between me and this officer boy there exist, or can exist, any other relations than such as are common with every acquaintance?” She laughed contemptuously and took up her book again; but now she was definitely unable to follow what she read. She passed the paper-knife over the window pane, then laid its smooth, cool surface to her cheek, and almost laughed aloud at the feeling of delight that all at once without cause came over her. She felt as though her nerves were strings being strained tighter and tighter on some sort of screwing peg. She felt her eyes opening wider and wider, her fingers and toes twitching nervously, something within oppressing her breathing, while all shapes and sounds seemed in the uncertain half-light to strike her with unaccustomed vividness. Moments of doubt were continually coming upon her, when she was uncertain whether the train were going forwards or backwards, or were standing still altogether; whether it were Annushka at her side or a stranger. “What’s that on the arm of the chair, a fur cloak or some beast? And what am I myself? Myself or some other woman?” She was afraid of giving way to this delirium. But something drew her towards it, and she could yield to it or resist it at will. She got up to rouse herself, and slipped off her plaid and the cape of her warm dress. For a moment she regained her self-possession, and realized that the thin peasant who had come in wearing a long overcoat, with buttons missing from it, was the stoveheater, that he was looking at the thermometer, that it was the wind and snow bursting in after him at the door; but then everything grew blurred again.... That peasant with the long waist seemed to be gnawing something on the wall, the old lady began stretching her legs the whole length of the carriage, and filling it with a black cloud; then there was a fearful shrieking and banging, as though someone were being torn to pieces; then there was a blinding dazzle of red fire before her eyes and a wall seemed to rise up and hide everything. Anna felt as though she were sinking down. But it was not terrible, but delightful. The voice of a man muffled up and covered with snow shouted something in her ear. She got up and pulled herself together; she realized that they had reached a station and that this was the guard. She asked Annushka to hand her the cape she had taken off and her shawl, put them on and moved towards the door.\n\n“Do you wish to get out?” asked Annushka.\n\n“Yes, I want a little air. It’s very hot in here.” And she opened the door. The driving snow and the wind rushed to meet her and struggled with her over the door. But she enjoyed the struggle.\n\nShe opened the door and went out. The wind seemed as though lying in wait for her; with gleeful whistle it tried to snatch her up and bear her off, but she clung to the cold door post, and holding her skirt got down onto the platform and under the shelter of the carriages. The wind had been powerful on the steps, but on the platform, under the lee of the carriages, there was a lull. With enjoyment she drew deep breaths of the frozen, snowy air, and standing near the carriage looked about the platform and the lighted station.\n\n# Chapter 30\n\nThe raging tempest rushed whistling between the wheels of the carriages, about the scaffolding, and round the corner of the station. The carriages, posts, people, everything that was to be seen was covered with snow on one side, and was getting more and more thickly covered. For a moment there would come a lull in the storm, but then it would swoop down again with such onslaughts that it seemed impossible to stand against it. Meanwhile men ran to and fro, talking merrily together, their steps crackling on the platform as they continually opened and closed the big doors. The bent shadow of a man glided by at her feet, and she heard sounds of a hammer upon iron. “Hand over that telegram!” came an angry voice out of the stormy darkness on the other side. “This way! No. 28!” several different voices shouted again, and muffled figures ran by covered with snow. Two gentlemen with lighted cigarettes passed by her. She drew one more deep breath of the fresh air, and had just put her hand out of her muff to take hold of the door post and get back into the carriage, when another man in a military overcoat, quite close beside her, stepped between her and the flickering light of the lamp post. She looked round, and the same instant recognized Vronsky’s face. Putting his hand to the peak of his cap, he bowed to her and asked, Was there anything she wanted? Could he be of any service to her? She gazed rather a long while at him without answering, and, in spite of the shadow in which he was standing, she saw, or fancied she saw, both the expression of his face and his eyes. It was again that expression of reverential ecstasy which had so worked upon her the day before. More than once she had told herself during the past few days, and again only a few moments before, that Vronsky was for her only one of the hundreds of young men, forever exactly the same, that are met everywhere, that she would never allow herself to bestow a thought upon him. But now at the first instant of meeting him, she was seized by a feeling of joyful pride. She had no need to ask why he had come. She knew as certainly as if he had told her that he was here to be where she was.\n\n“I didn’t know you were going. What are you coming for?” she said, letting fall the hand with which she had grasped the door post. And irrepressible delight and eagerness shone in her face.\n\n“What am I coming for?” he repeated, looking straight into her eyes. “You know that I have come to be where you are,” he said; “I can’t help it.”\n\nAt that moment the wind, as it were, surmounting all obstacles, sent the snow flying from the carriage roofs, and clanked some sheet of iron it had torn off, while the hoarse whistle of the engine roared in front, plaintively and gloomily. All the awfulness of the storm seemed to her more splendid now. He had said what her soul longed to hear, though she feared it with her reason. She made no answer, and in her face he saw conflict.\n\n“Forgive me, if you dislike what I said,” he said humbly.\n\nHe had spoken courteously, deferentially, yet so firmly, so stubbornly, that for a long while she could make no answer.\n\n“It’s wrong, what you say, and I beg you, if you’re a good man, to forget what you’ve said, as I forget it,” she said at last.\n\n“Not one word, not one gesture of yours shall I, could I, ever forget....”\n\n“Enough, enough!” she cried trying assiduously to give a stern expression to her face, into which he was gazing greedily. And clutching at the cold door post, she clambered up the steps and got rapidly into the corridor of the carriage. But in the little corridor she paused, going over in her imagination what had happened. Though she could not recall her own words or his, she realized instinctively that the momentary conversation had brought them fearfully closer; and she was panic-stricken and blissful at it. After standing still a few seconds, she went into the carriage and sat down in her place. The overstrained condition which had tormented her before did not only come back, but was intensified, and reached such a pitch that she was afraid every minute that something would snap within her from the excessive tension. She did not sleep all night. But in that nervous tension, and in the visions that filled her imagination, there was nothing disagreeable or gloomy: on the contrary there was something blissful, glowing, and exhilarating. Towards morning Anna sank into a doze, sitting in her place, and when she waked it was daylight and the train was near Petersburg. At once thoughts of home, of husband and of son, and the details of that day and the following came upon her.\n\nAt Petersburg, as soon as the train stopped and she got out, the first person that attracted her attention was her husband. “Oh, mercy! why do his ears look like that?” she thought, looking at his frigid and imposing figure, and especially the ears that struck her at the moment as propping up the brim of his round hat. Catching sight of her, he came to meet her, his lips falling into their habitual sarcastic smile, and his big, tired eyes looking straight at her. An unpleasant sensation gripped at her heart when she met his obstinate and weary glance, as though she had expected to see him different. She was especially struck by the feeling of dissatisfaction with herself that she experienced on meeting him. That feeling was an intimate, familiar feeling, like a consciousness of hypocrisy, which she experienced in her relations with her husband. But hitherto she had not taken note of the feeling, now she was clearly and painfully aware of it.\n\n“Yes, as you see, your tender spouse, as devoted as the first year after marriage, burned with impatience to see you,” he said in his deliberate, high-pitched voice, and in that tone which he almost always took with her, a tone of jeering at anyone who should say in earnest what he said.\n\n“Is Seryozha quite well?” she asked.\n\n“And is this all the reward,” said he, “for my ardor? He’s quite well....”\n\n# Chapter 31\n\nVronsky had not even tried to sleep all that night. He sat in his armchair, looking straight before him or scanning the people who got in and out. If he had indeed on previous occasions struck and impressed people who did not know him by his air of unhesitating composure, he seemed now more haughty and self-possessed than ever. He looked at people as if they were things. A nervous young man, a clerk in a law court, sitting opposite him, hated him for that look. The young man asked him for a light, and entered into conversation with him, and even pushed against him, to make him feel that he was not a thing, but a person. But Vronsky gazed at him exactly as he did at the lamp, and the young man made a wry face, feeling that he was losing his self-possession under the oppression of this refusal to recognize him as a person.\n\nVronsky saw nothing and no one. He felt himself a king, not because he believed that he had made an impression on Anna—he did not yet believe that,—but because the impression she had made on him gave him happiness and pride.\n\nWhat would come of it all he did not know, he did not even think. He felt that all his forces, hitherto dissipated, wasted, were centered on one thing, and bent with fearful energy on one blissful goal. And he was happy at it. He knew only that he had told her the truth, that he had come where she was, that all the happiness of his life, the only meaning in life for him, now lay in seeing and hearing her. And when he got out of the carriage at Bologova to get some seltzer water, and caught sight of Anna, involuntarily his first word had told her just what he thought. And he was glad he had told her it, that she knew it now and was thinking of it. He did not sleep all night. When he was back in the carriage, he kept unceasingly going over every position in which he had seen her, every word she had uttered, and before his fancy, making his heart faint with emotion, floated pictures of a possible future.\n\nWhen he got out of the train at Petersburg, he felt after his sleepless night as keen and fresh as after a cold bath. He paused near his compartment, waiting for her to get out. “Once more,” he said to himself, smiling unconsciously, “once more I shall see her walk, her face; she will say something, turn her head, glance, smile, maybe.” But before he caught sight of her, he saw her husband, whom the station-master was deferentially escorting through the crowd. “Ah, yes! The husband.” Only now for the first time did Vronsky realize clearly the fact that there was a person attached to her, a husband. He knew that she had a husband, but had hardly believed in his existence, and only now fully believed in him, with his head and shoulders, and his legs clad in black trousers; especially when he saw this husband calmly take her arm with a sense of property.\n\nSeeing Alexey Alexandrovitch with his Petersburg face and severely self-confident figure, in his round hat, with his rather prominent spine, he believed in him, and was aware of a disagreeable sensation, such as a man might feel tortured by thirst, who, on reaching a spring, should find a dog, a sheep, or a pig, who has drunk of it and muddied the water. Alexey Alexandrovitch’s manner of walking, with a swing of the hips and flat feet, particularly annoyed Vronsky. He could recognize in no one but himself an indubitable right to love her. But she was still the same, and the sight of her affected him the same way, physically reviving him, stirring him, and filling his soul with rapture. He told his German valet, who ran up to him from the second class, to take his things and go on, and he himself went up to her. He saw the first meeting between the husband and wife, and noted with a lover’s insight the signs of slight reserve with which she spoke to her husband. “No, she does not love him and cannot love him,” he decided to himself.\n\nAt the moment when he was approaching Anna Arkadyevna he noticed too with joy that she was conscious of his being near, and looked round, and seeing him, turned again to her husband.\n\n“Have you passed a good night?” he asked, bowing to her and her husband together, and leaving it up to Alexey Alexandrovitch to accept the bow on his own account, and to recognize it or not, as he might see fit.\n\n“Thank you, very good,” she answered.\n\nHer face looked weary, and there was not that play of eagerness in it, peeping out in her smile and her eyes; but for a single instant, as she glanced at him, there was a flash of something in her eyes, and although the flash died away at once, he was happy for that moment. She glanced at her husband to find out whether he knew Vronsky. Alexey Alexandrovitch looked at Vronsky with displeasure, vaguely recalling who this was. Vronsky’s composure and self-confidence here struck, like a scythe against a stone, upon the cold self-confidence of Alexey Alexandrovitch.\n\n“Count Vronsky,” said Anna.\n\n“Ah! We are acquainted, I believe,” said Alexey Alexandrovitch indifferently, giving his hand.\n\n“You set off with the mother and you return with the son,” he said, articulating each syllable, as though each were a separate favor he was bestowing.\n\n“You’re back from leave, I suppose?” he said, and without waiting for a reply, he turned to his wife in his jesting tone: “Well, were a great many tears shed at Moscow at parting?”\n\nBy addressing his wife like this he gave Vronsky to understand that he wished to be left alone, and, turning slightly towards him, he touched his hat; but Vronsky turned to Anna Arkadyevna.\n\n“I hope I may have the honor of calling on you,” he said.\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch glanced with his weary eyes at Vronsky.\n\n“Delighted,” he said coldly. “On Mondays we’re at home. Most fortunate,” he said to his wife, dismissing Vronsky altogether, “that I should just have half an hour to meet you, so that I can prove my devotion,” he went on in the same jesting tone.\n\n“You lay too much stress on your devotion for me to value it much,” she responded in the same jesting tone, involuntarily listening to the sound of Vronsky’s steps behind them. “But what has it to do with me?” she said to herself, and she began asking her husband how Seryozha had got on without her.\n\n“Oh, capitally! Mariette says he has been very good, And ... I must disappoint you ... but he has not missed you as your husband has. But once more merci, my dear, for giving me a day. Our dear Samovar will be delighted.” (He used to call the Countess Lidia Ivanovna, well known in society, a samovar, because she was always bubbling over with excitement.) “She has been continually asking after you. And, do you know, if I may venture to advise you, you should go and see her today. You know how she takes everything to heart. Just now, with all her own cares, she’s anxious about the Oblonskys being brought together.”\n\nThe Countess Lidia Ivanovna was a friend of her husband’s, and the center of that one of the coteries of the Petersburg world with which Anna was, through her husband, in the closest relations.\n\n“But you know I wrote to her?”\n\n“Still she’ll want to hear details. Go and see her, if you’re not too tired, my dear. Well, Kondraty will take you in the carriage, while I go to my committee. I shall not be alone at dinner again,” Alexey Alexandrovitch went on, no longer in a sarcastic tone. “You wouldn’t believe how I’ve missed....” And with a long pressure of her hand and a meaning smile, he put her in her carriage.\n\n# Chapter 32\n\nThe first person to meet Anna at home was her son. He dashed down the stairs to her, in spite of the governess’s call, and with desperate joy shrieked: “Mother! mother!” Running up to her, he hung on her neck.\n\n“I told you it was mother!” he shouted to the governess. “I knew!”\n\nAnd her son, like her husband, aroused in Anna a feeling akin to disappointment. She had imagined him better than he was in reality. She had to let herself drop down to the reality to enjoy him as he really was. But even as he was, he was charming, with his fair curls, his blue eyes, and his plump, graceful little legs in tightly pulled-up stockings. Anna experienced almost physical pleasure in the sensation of his nearness, and his caresses, and moral soothing, when she met his simple, confiding, and loving glance, and heard his naïve questions. Anna took out the presents Dolly’s children had sent him, and told her son what sort of little girl was Tanya at Moscow, and how Tanya could read, and even taught the other children.\n\n“Why, am I not so nice as she?” asked Seryozha.\n\n“To me you’re nicer than anyone in the world.”\n\n“I know that,” said Seryozha, smiling.\n\nAnna had not had time to drink her coffee when the Countess Lidia Ivanovna was announced. The Countess Lidia Ivanovna was a tall, stout woman, with an unhealthily sallow face and splendid, pensive black eyes. Anna liked her, but today she seemed to be seeing her for the first time with all her defects.\n\n“Well, my dear, so you took the olive branch?” inquired Countess Lidia Ivanovna, as soon as she came into the room.\n\n“Yes, it’s all over, but it was all much less serious than we had supposed,” answered Anna. “My belle-sœur is in general too hasty.”\n\nBut Countess Lidia Ivanovna, though she was interested in everything that did not concern her, had a habit of never listening to what interested her; she interrupted Anna:\n\n“Yes, there’s plenty of sorrow and evil in the world. I am so worried today.”\n\n“Oh, why?” asked Anna, trying to suppress a smile.\n\n“I’m beginning to be weary of fruitlessly championing the truth, and sometimes I’m quite unhinged by it. The Society of the Little Sisters” (this was a religiously-patriotic, philanthropic institution) “was going splendidly, but with these gentlemen it’s impossible to do anything,” added Countess Lidia Ivanovna in a tone of ironical submission to destiny. “They pounce on the idea, and distort it, and then work it out so pettily and unworthily. Two or three people, your husband among them, understand all the importance of the thing, but the others simply drag it down. Yesterday Pravdin wrote to me....”\n\nPravdin was a well-known Panslavist abroad, and Countess Lidia Ivanovna described the purport of his letter.\n\nThen the countess told her of more disagreements and intrigues against the work of the unification of the churches, and departed in haste, as she had that day to be at the meeting of some society and also at the Slavonic committee.\n\n“It was all the same before, of course; but why was it I didn’t notice it before?” Anna asked herself. “Or has she been very much irritated today? It’s really ludicrous; her object is doing good; she a Christian, yet she’s always angry; and she always has enemies, and always enemies in the name of Christianity and doing good.”\n\nAfter Countess Lidia Ivanovna another friend came, the wife of a chief secretary, who told her all the news of the town. At three o’clock she too went away, promising to come to dinner. Alexey Alexandrovitch was at the ministry. Anna, left alone, spent the time till dinner in assisting at her son’s dinner (he dined apart from his parents) and in putting her things in order, and in reading and answering the notes and letters which had accumulated on her table.\n\nThe feeling of causeless shame, which she had felt on the journey, and her excitement, too, had completely vanished. In the habitual conditions of her life she felt again resolute and irreproachable.\n\nShe recalled with wonder her state of mind on the previous day. “What was it? Nothing. Vronsky said something silly, which it was easy to put a stop to, and I answered as I ought to have done. To speak of it to my husband would be unnecessary and out of the question. To speak of it would be to attach importance to what has no importance.” She remembered how she had told her husband of what was almost a declaration made her at Petersburg by a young man, one of her husband’s subordinates, and how Alexey Alexandrovitch had answered that every woman living in the world was exposed to such incidents, but that he had the fullest confidence in her tact, and could never lower her and himself by jealousy. “So then there’s no reason to speak of it? And indeed, thank God, there’s nothing to speak of,” she told herself.\n\n# Chapter 33\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch came back from the meeting of the ministers at four o’clock, but as often happened, he had not time to come in to her. He went into his study to see the people waiting for him with petitions, and to sign some papers brought him by his chief secretary. At dinner time (there were always a few people dining with the Karenins) there arrived an old lady, a cousin of Alexey Alexandrovitch, the chief secretary of the department and his wife, and a young man who had been recommended to Alexey Alexandrovitch for the service. Anna went into the drawing-room to receive these guests. Precisely at five o’clock, before the bronze Peter the First clock had struck the fifth stroke, Alexey Alexandrovitch came in, wearing a white tie and evening coat with two stars, as he had to go out directly after dinner. Every minute of Alexey Alexandrovitch’s life was portioned out and occupied. And to make time to get through all that lay before him every day, he adhered to the strictest punctuality. “Unhasting and unresting,” was his motto. He came into the dining hall, greeted everyone, and hurriedly sat down, smiling to his wife.\n\n“Yes, my solitude is over. You wouldn’t believe how uncomfortable” (he laid stress on the word uncomfortable) “it is to dine alone.”\n\nAt dinner he talked a little to his wife about Moscow matters, and, with a sarcastic smile, asked her after Stepan Arkadyevitch; but the conversation was for the most part general, dealing with Petersburg official and public news. After dinner he spent half an hour with his guests, and again, with a smile, pressed his wife’s hand, withdrew, and drove off to the council. Anna did not go out that evening either to the Princess Betsy Tverskaya, who, hearing of her return, had invited her, nor to the theater, where she had a box for that evening. She did not go out principally because the dress she had reckoned upon was not ready. Altogether, Anna, on turning, after the departure of her guests, to the consideration of her attire, was very much annoyed. She was generally a mistress of the art of dressing well without great expense, and before leaving Moscow she had given her dressmaker three dresses to transform. The dresses had to be altered so that they could not be recognized, and they ought to have been ready three days before. It appeared that two dresses had not been done at all, while the other one had not been altered as Anna had intended. The dressmaker came to explain, declaring that it would be better as she had done it, and Anna was so furious that she felt ashamed when she thought of it afterwards. To regain her serenity completely she went into the nursery, and spent the whole evening with her son, put him to bed herself, signed him with the cross, and tucked him up. She was glad she had not gone out anywhere, and had spent the evening so well. She felt so light-hearted and serene, she saw so clearly that all that had seemed to her so important on her railway journey was only one of the common trivial incidents of fashionable life, and that she had no reason to feel ashamed before anyone else or before herself. Anna sat down at the hearth with an English novel and waited for her husband. Exactly at half-past nine she heard his ring, and he came into the room.\n\n“Here you are at last!” she observed, holding out her hand to him.\n\nHe kissed her hand and sat down beside her.\n\n“Altogether then, I see your visit was a success,” he said to her.\n\n“Oh, yes,” she said, and she began telling him about everything from the beginning: her journey with Countess Vronskaya, her arrival, the accident at the station. Then she described the pity she had felt, first for her brother, and afterwards for Dolly.\n\n“I imagine one cannot exonerate such a man from blame, though he is your brother,” said Alexey Alexandrovitch severely.\n\nAnna smiled. She knew that he said that simply to show that family considerations could not prevent him from expressing his genuine opinion. She knew that characteristic in her husband, and liked it.\n\n“I am glad it has all ended so satisfactorily, and that you are back again,” he went on. “Come, what do they say about the new act I have got passed in the council?”\n\nAnna had heard nothing of this act, and she felt conscience-stricken at having been able so readily to forget what was to him of such importance.\n\n“Here, on the other hand, it has made a great sensation,” he said, with a complacent smile.\n\nShe saw that Alexey Alexandrovitch wanted to tell her something pleasant to him about it, and she brought him by questions to telling it. With the same complacent smile he told her of the ovations he had received in consequence of the act he had passed.\n\n“I was very, very glad. It shows that at last a reasonable and steady view of the matter is becoming prevalent among us.”\n\nHaving drunk his second cup of tea with cream, and bread, Alexey Alexandrovitch got up, and was going towards his study.\n\n“And you’ve not been anywhere this evening? You’ve been dull, I expect?” he said.\n\n“Oh, no!” she answered, getting up after him and accompanying him across the room to his study. “What are you reading now?” she asked.\n\n“Just now I’m reading Duc de Lille, Poésie des Enfers,” he answered. “A very remarkable book.”\n\nAnna smiled, as people smile at the weaknesses of those they love, and, putting her hand under his, she escorted him to the door of the study. She knew his habit, that had grown into a necessity, of reading in the evening. She knew, too, that in spite of his official duties, which swallowed up almost the whole of his time, he considered it his duty to keep up with everything of note that appeared in the intellectual world. She knew, too, that he was really interested in books dealing with politics, philosophy, and theology, that art was utterly foreign to his nature; but, in spite of this, or rather, in consequence of it, Alexey Alexandrovitch never passed over anything in the world of art, but made it his duty to read everything. She knew that in politics, in philosophy, in theology, Alexey Alexandrovitch often had doubts, and made investigations; but on questions of art and poetry, and, above all, of music, of which he was totally devoid of understanding, he had the most distinct and decided opinions. He was fond of talking about Shakespeare, Raphael, Beethoven, of the significance of new schools of poetry and music, all of which were classified by him with very conspicuous consistency.\n\n“Well, God be with you,” she said at the door of the study, where a shaded candle and a decanter of water were already put by his armchair. “And I’ll write to Moscow.”\n\nHe pressed her hand, and again kissed it.\n\n“All the same he’s a good man; truthful, good-hearted, and remarkable in his own line,” Anna said to herself going back to her room, as though she were defending him to someone who had attacked him and said that one could not love him. “But why is it his ears stick out so strangely? Or has he had his hair cut?”\n\nPrecisely at twelve o’clock, when Anna was still sitting at her writing-table, finishing a letter to Dolly, she heard the sound of measured steps in slippers, and Alexey Alexandrovitch, freshly washed and combed, with a book under his arm, came in to her.\n\n“It’s time, it’s time,” said he, with a meaning smile, and he went into their bedroom.\n\n“And what right had he to look at him like that?” thought Anna, recalling Vronsky’s glance at Alexey Alexandrovitch.\n\nUndressing, she went into the bedroom; but her face had none of the eagerness which, during her stay in Moscow, had fairly flashed from her eyes and her smile; on the contrary, now the fire seemed quenched in her, hidden somewhere far away.\n\n# Chapter 34\n\nWhen Vronsky went to Moscow from Petersburg, he had left his large set of rooms in Morskaia to his friend and favorite comrade Petritsky.\n\nPetritsky was a young lieutenant, not particularly well-connected, and not merely not wealthy, but always hopelessly in debt. Towards evening he was always drunk, and he had often been locked up after all sorts of ludicrous and disgraceful scandals, but he was a favorite both of his comrades and his superior officers. On arriving at twelve o’clock from the station at his flat, Vronsky saw, at the outer door, a hired carriage familiar to him. While still outside his own door, as he rang, he heard masculine laughter, the lisp of a feminine voice, and Petritsky’s voice. “If that’s one of the villains, don’t let him in!” Vronsky told the servant not to announce him, and slipped quietly into the first room. Baroness Shilton, a friend of Petritsky’s, with a rosy little face and flaxen hair, resplendent in a lilac satin gown, and filling the whole room, like a canary, with her Parisian chatter, sat at the round table making coffee. Petritsky, in his overcoat, and the cavalry captain Kamerovsky, in full uniform, probably just come from duty, were sitting each side of her.\n\n“Bravo! Vronsky!” shouted Petritsky, jumping up, scraping his chair. “Our host himself! Baroness, some coffee for him out of the new coffee pot. Why, we didn’t expect you! Hope you’re satisfied with the ornament of your study,” he said, indicating the baroness. “You know each other, of course?”\n\n“I should think so,” said Vronsky, with a bright smile, pressing the baroness’s little hand. “What next! I’m an old friend.”\n\n“You’re home after a journey,” said the baroness, “so I’m flying. Oh, I’ll be off this minute, if I’m in the way.”\n\n“You’re home, wherever you are, baroness,” said Vronsky. “How do you do, Kamerovsky?” he added, coldly shaking hands with Kamerovsky.\n\n“There, you never know how to say such pretty things,” said the baroness, turning to Petritsky.\n\n“No; what’s that for? After dinner I say things quite as good.”\n\n“After dinner there’s no credit in them? Well, then, I’ll make you some coffee, so go and wash and get ready,” said the baroness, sitting down again, and anxiously turning the screw in the new coffee pot. “Pierre, give me the coffee,” she said, addressing Petritsky, whom she called Pierre as a contraction of his surname, making no secret of her relations with him. “I’ll put it in.”\n\n“You’ll spoil it!”\n\n“No, I won’t spoil it! Well, and your wife?” said the baroness suddenly, interrupting Vronsky’s conversation with his comrade. “We’ve been marrying you here. Have you brought your wife?”\n\n“No, baroness. I was born a Bohemian, and a Bohemian I shall die.”\n\n“So much the better, so much the better. Shake hands on it.”\n\nAnd the baroness, detaining Vronsky, began telling him, with many jokes, about her last new plans of life, asking his advice.\n\n“He persists in refusing to give me a divorce! Well, what am I to do?” (He was her husband.) “Now I want to begin a suit against him. What do you advise? Kamerovsky, look after the coffee; it’s boiling over. You see, I’m engrossed with business! I want a lawsuit, because I must have my property. Do you understand the folly of it, that on the pretext of my being unfaithful to him,” she said contemptuously, “he wants to get the benefit of my fortune.”\n\nVronsky heard with pleasure this light-hearted prattle of a pretty woman, agreed with her, gave her half-joking counsel, and altogether dropped at once into the tone habitual to him in talking to such women. In his Petersburg world all people were divided into utterly opposed classes. One, the lower class, vulgar, stupid, and, above all, ridiculous people, who believe that one husband ought to live with the one wife whom he has lawfully married; that a girl should be innocent, a woman modest, and a man manly, self-controlled, and strong; that one ought to bring up one’s children, earn one’s bread, and pay one’s debts; and various similar absurdities. This was the class of old-fashioned and ridiculous people. But there was another class of people, the real people. To this class they all belonged, and in it the great thing was to be elegant, generous, plucky, gay, to abandon oneself without a blush to every passion, and to laugh at everything else.\n\nFor the first moment only, Vronsky was startled after the impression of a quite different world that he had brought with him from Moscow. But immediately as though slipping his feet into old slippers, he dropped back into the light-hearted, pleasant world he had always lived in.\n\nThe coffee was never really made, but spluttered over everyone, and boiled away, doing just what was required of it—that is, providing much cause for much noise and laughter, and spoiling a costly rug and the baroness’s gown.\n\n“Well now, good-bye, or you’ll never get washed, and I shall have on my conscience the worst sin a gentleman can commit. So you would advise a knife to his throat?”\n\n“To be sure, and manage that your hand may not be far from his lips. He’ll kiss your hand, and all will end satisfactorily,” answered Vronsky.\n\n“So at the Français!” and, with a rustle of her skirts, she vanished.\n\nKamerovsky got up too, and Vronsky, not waiting for him to go, shook hands and went off to his dressing-room.\n\nWhile he was washing, Petritsky described to him in brief outlines his position, as far as it had changed since Vronsky had left Petersburg. No money at all. His father said he wouldn’t give him any and pay his debts. His tailor was trying to get him locked up, and another fellow, too, was threatening to get him locked up. The colonel of the regiment had announced that if these scandals did not cease he would have to leave. As for the baroness, he was sick to death of her, especially since she’d taken to offering continually to lend him money. But he had found a girl—he’d show her to Vronsky—a marvel, exquisite, in the strict Oriental style, “genre of the slave Rebecca, don’t you know.” He’d had a row, too, with Berkoshov, and was going to send seconds to him, but of course it would come to nothing. Altogether everything was supremely amusing and jolly. And, not letting his comrade enter into further details of his position, Petritsky proceeded to tell him all the interesting news. As he listened to Petritsky’s familiar stories in the familiar setting of the rooms he had spent the last three years in, Vronsky felt a delightful sense of coming back to the careless Petersburg life that he was used to.\n\n“Impossible!” he cried, letting down the pedal of the washing basin in which he had been sousing his healthy red neck. “Impossible!” he cried, at the news that Laura had flung over Fertinghof and had made up to Mileev. “And is he as stupid and pleased as ever? Well, and how’s Buzulukov?”\n\n“Oh, there is a tale about Buzulukov—simply lovely!” cried Petritsky. “You know his weakness for balls, and he never misses a single court ball. He went to a big ball in a new helmet. Have you seen the new helmets? Very nice, lighter. Well, so he’s standing.... No, I say, do listen.”\n\n“I am listening,” answered Vronsky, rubbing himself with a rough towel.\n\n“Up comes the Grand Duchess with some ambassador or other, and, as ill-luck would have it, she begins talking to him about the new helmets. The Grand Duchess positively wanted to show the new helmet to the ambassador. They see our friend standing there.” (Petritsky mimicked how he was standing with the helmet.) “The Grand Duchess asked him to give her the helmet; he doesn’t give it to her. What do you think of that? Well, everyone’s winking at him, nodding, frowning—give it to her, do! He doesn’t give it to her. He’s mute as a fish. Only picture it!... Well, the ... what’s his name, whatever he was ... tries to take the helmet from him ... he won’t give it up!... He pulls it from him, and hands it to the Grand Duchess. ‘Here, your Highness,’ says he, ‘is the new helmet.’ She turned the helmet the other side up, And—just picture it!—plop went a pear and sweetmeats out of it, two pounds of sweetmeats!... He’d been storing them up, the darling!”\n\nVronsky burst into roars of laughter. And long afterwards, when he was talking of other things, he broke out into his healthy laugh, showing his strong, close rows of teeth, when he thought of the helmet.\n\nHaving heard all the news, Vronsky, with the assistance of his valet, got into his uniform, and went off to report himself. He intended, when he had done that, to drive to his brother’s and to Betsy’s and to pay several visits with a view to beginning to go into that society where he might meet Madame Karenina. As he always did in Petersburg, he left home not meaning to return till late at night.\n\n# PART TWO Chapter 1\n\nAt the end of the winter, in the Shtcherbatskys’ house, a consultation was being held, which was to pronounce on the state of Kitty’s health and the measures to be taken to restore her failing strength. She had been ill, and as spring came on she grew worse. The family doctor gave her cod liver oil, then iron, then nitrate of silver, but as the first and the second and the third were alike in doing no good, and as his advice when spring came was to go abroad, a celebrated physician was called in. The celebrated physician, a very handsome man, still youngish, asked to examine the patient. He maintained, with peculiar satisfaction, it seemed, that maiden modesty is a mere relic of barbarism, and that nothing could be more natural than for a man still youngish to handle a young girl naked. He thought it natural because he did it every day, and felt and thought, as it seemed to him, no harm as he did it and consequently he considered modesty in the girl not merely as a relic of barbarism, but also as an insult to himself.\n\nThere was nothing for it but to submit, since, although all the doctors had studied in the same school, had read the same books, and learned the same science, and though some people said this celebrated doctor was a bad doctor, in the princess’s household and circle it was for some reason accepted that this celebrated doctor alone had some special knowledge, and that he alone could save Kitty. After a careful examination and sounding of the bewildered patient, dazed with shame, the celebrated doctor, having scrupulously washed his hands, was standing in the drawing-room talking to the prince. The prince frowned and coughed, listening to the doctor. As a man who had seen something of life, and neither a fool nor an invalid, he had no faith in medicine, and in his heart was furious at the whole farce, specially as he was perhaps the only one who fully comprehended the cause of Kitty’s illness. “Conceited blockhead!” he thought, as he listened to the celebrated doctor’s chatter about his daughter’s symptoms. The doctor was meantime with difficulty restraining the expression of his contempt for this old gentleman, and with difficulty condescending to the level of his intelligence. He perceived that it was no good talking to the old man, and that the principal person in the house was the mother. Before her he decided to scatter his pearls. At that instant the princess came into the drawing-room with the family doctor. The prince withdrew, trying not to show how ridiculous he thought the whole performance. The princess was distracted, and did not know what to do. She felt she had sinned against Kitty.\n\n“Well, doctor, decide our fate,” said the princess. “Tell me everything.”\n\n“Is there hope?” she meant to say, but her lips quivered, and she could not utter the question. “Well, doctor?”\n\n“Immediately, princess. I will talk it over with my colleague, and then I will have the honor of laying my opinion before you.”\n\n“So we had better leave you?”\n“As you please.”\n\nThe princess went out with a sigh.\n\nWhen the doctors were left alone, the family doctor began timidly explaining his opinion, that there was a commencement of tuberculous trouble, but ... and so on. The celebrated doctor listened to him, and in the middle of his sentence looked at his big gold watch.\n\n“Yes,” said he. “But....”\n\nThe family doctor respectfully ceased in the middle of his observations.\n\n“The commencement of the tuberculous process we are not, as you are aware, able to define; till there are cavities, there is nothing definite. But we may suspect it. And there are indications; malnutrition, nervous excitability, and so on. The question stands thus: in presence of indications of tuberculous process, what is to be done to maintain nutrition?”\n\n“But, you know, there are always moral, spiritual causes at the back in these cases,” the family doctor permitted himself to interpolate with a subtle smile.\n\n“Yes, that’s an understood thing,” responded the celebrated physician, again glancing at his watch. “Beg pardon, is the Yausky bridge done yet, or shall I have to drive around?” he asked. “Ah! it is. Oh, well, then I can do it in twenty minutes. So we were saying the problem may be put thus: to maintain nutrition and to give tone to the nerves. The one is in close connection with the other, one must attack both sides at once.”\n\n“And how about a tour abroad?” asked the family doctor.\n\n“I’ve no liking for foreign tours. And take note: if there is an early stage of tuberculous process, of which we cannot be certain, a foreign tour will be of no use. What is wanted is means of improving nutrition, and not for lowering it.” And the celebrated doctor expounded his plan of treatment with Soden waters, a remedy obviously prescribed primarily on the ground that they could do no harm.\n\nThe family doctor listened attentively and respectfully.\n\n“But in favor of foreign travel I would urge the change of habits, the removal from conditions calling up reminiscences. And then the mother wishes it,” he added.\n\n“Ah! Well, in that case, to be sure, let them go. Only, those German quacks are mischievous.... They ought to be persuaded.... Well, let them go then.”\n\nHe glanced once more at his watch.\n\n“Oh! time’s up already,” And he went to the door. The celebrated doctor announced to the princess (a feeling of what was due from him dictated his doing so) that he ought to see the patient once more.\n\n“What! another examination!” cried the mother, with horror.\n\n“Oh, no, only a few details, princess.”\n\n“Come this way.”\n\nAnd the mother, accompanied by the doctor, went into the drawing-room to Kitty. Wasted and flushed, with a peculiar glitter in her eyes, left there by the agony of shame she had been put through, Kitty stood in the middle of the room. When the doctor came in she flushed crimson, and her eyes filled with tears. All her illness and treatment struck her as a thing so stupid, ludicrous even! Doctoring her seemed to her as absurd as putting together the pieces of a broken vase. Her heart was broken. Why would they try to cure her with pills and powders? But she could not grieve her mother, especially as her mother considered herself to blame.\n\n“May I trouble you to sit down, princess?” the celebrated doctor said to her.\n\nHe sat down with a smile, facing her, felt her pulse, and again began asking her tiresome questions. She answered him, and all at once got up, furious.\n\n“Excuse me, doctor, but there is really no object in this. This is the third time you’ve asked me the same thing.”\n\nThe celebrated doctor did not take offense.\n\n“Nervous irritability,” he said to the princess, when Kitty had left the room. “However, I had finished....”\n\nAnd the doctor began scientifically explaining to the princess, as an exceptionally intelligent woman, the condition of the young princess, and concluded by insisting on the drinking of the waters, which were certainly harmless. At the question: Should they go abroad? the doctor plunged into deep meditation, as though resolving a weighty problem. Finally his decision was pronounced: they were to go abroad, but to put no faith in foreign quacks, and to apply to him in any need.\n\nIt seemed as though some piece of good fortune had come to pass after the doctor had gone. The mother was much more cheerful when she went back to her daughter, and Kitty pretended to be more cheerful. She had often, almost always, to be pretending now.\n\n“Really, I’m quite well, mamma. But if you want to go abroad, let’s go!” she said, and trying to appear interested in the proposed tour, she began talking of the preparations for the journey.\n\n# Chapter 2\n\nSoon after the doctor, Dolly had arrived. She knew that there was to be a consultation that day, and though she was only just up after her confinement (she had another baby, a little girl, born at the end of the winter), though she had trouble and anxiety enough of her own, she had left her tiny baby and a sick child, to come and hear Kitty’s fate, which was to be decided that day.\n\n“Well, well?” she said, coming into the drawing-room, without taking off her hat. “You’re all in good spirits. Good news, then?”\n\nThey tried to tell her what the doctor had said, but it appeared that though the doctor had talked distinctly enough and at great length, it was utterly impossible to report what he had said. The only point of interest was that it was settled they should go abroad.\n\nDolly could not help sighing. Her dearest friend, her sister, was going away. And her life was not a cheerful one. Her relations with Stepan Arkadyevitch after their reconciliation had become humiliating. The union Anna had cemented turned out to be of no solid character, and family harmony was breaking down again at the same point. There had been nothing definite, but Stepan Arkadyevitch was hardly ever at home; money, too, was hardly ever forthcoming, and Dolly was continually tortured by suspicions of infidelity, which she tried to dismiss, dreading the agonies of jealousy she had been through already. The first onslaught of jealousy, once lived through, could never come back again, and even the discovery of infidelities could never now affect her as it had the first time. Such a discovery now would only mean breaking up family habits, and she let herself be deceived, despising him and still more herself, for the weakness. Besides this, the care of her large family was a constant worry to her: first, the nursing of her young baby did not go well, then the nurse had gone away, now one of the children had fallen ill.\n\n“Well, how are all of you?” asked her mother.\n\n“Ah, mamma, we have plenty of troubles of our own. Lili is ill, and I’m afraid it’s scarlatina. I have come here now to hear about Kitty, and then I shall shut myself up entirely, if—God forbid—it should be scarlatina.”\n\nThe old prince too had come in from his study after the doctor’s departure, and after presenting his cheek to Dolly, and saying a few words to her, he turned to his wife:\n\n“How have you settled it? you’re going? Well, and what do you mean to do with me?”\n\n“I suppose you had better stay here, Alexander,” said his wife.\n\n“That’s as you like.”\n\n“Mamma, why shouldn’t father come with us?” said Kitty. “It would be nicer for him and for us too.”\n\nThe old prince got up and stroked Kitty’s hair. She lifted her head and looked at him with a forced smile. It always seemed to her that he understood her better than anyone in the family, though he did not say much about her. Being the youngest, she was her father’s favorite, and she fancied that his love gave him insight. When now her glance met his blue kindly eyes looking intently at her, it seemed to her that he saw right through her, and understood all that was not good that was passing within her. Reddening, she stretched out towards him expecting a kiss, but he only patted her hair and said:\n\n“These stupid chignons! There’s no getting at the real daughter. One simply strokes the bristles of dead women. Well, Dolinka,” he turned to his elder daughter, “what’s your young buck about, hey?”\n\n“Nothing, father,” answered Dolly, understanding that her husband was meant. “He’s always out; I scarcely ever see him,” she could not resist adding with a sarcastic smile.\n\n“Why, hasn’t he gone into the country yet—to see about selling that forest?”\n\n“No, he’s still getting ready for the journey.”\n\n“Oh, that’s it!” said the prince. “And so am I to be getting ready for a journey too? At your service,” he said to his wife, sitting down. “And I tell you what, Katia,” he went on to his younger daughter, “you must wake up one fine day and say to yourself: Why, I’m quite well, and merry, and going out again with father for an early morning walk in the frost. Hey?”\n\nWhat her father said seemed simple enough, yet at these words Kitty became confused and overcome like a detected criminal. “Yes, he sees it all, he understands it all, and in these words he’s telling me that though I’m ashamed, I must get over my shame.” She could not pluck up spirit to make any answer. She tried to begin, and all at once burst into tears, and rushed out of the room.\n\n“See what comes of your jokes!” the princess pounced down on her husband. “You’re always....” she began a string of reproaches.\n\nThe prince listened to the princess’s scolding rather a long while without speaking, but his face was more and more frowning.\n\n“She’s so much to be pitied, poor child, so much to be pitied, and you don’t feel how it hurts her to hear the slightest reference to the cause of it. Ah! to be so mistaken in people!” said the princess, and by the change in her tone both Dolly and the prince knew she was speaking of Vronsky. “I don’t know why there aren’t laws against such base, dishonorable people.”\n\n“Ah, I can’t bear to hear you!” said the prince gloomily, getting up from his low chair, and seeming anxious to get away, yet stopping in the doorway. “There are laws, madam, and since you’ve challenged me to it, I’ll tell you who’s to blame for it all: you and you, you and nobody else. Laws against such young gallants there have always been, and there still are! Yes, if there has been nothing that ought not to have been, old as I am, I’d have called him out to the barrier, the young dandy. Yes, and now you physic her and call in these quacks.”\n\nThe prince apparently had plenty more to say, but as soon as the princess heard his tone she subsided at once, and became penitent, as she always did on serious occasions.\n\n“Alexander, Alexander,” she whispered, moving to him and beginning to weep.\n\nAs soon as she began to cry the prince too calmed down. He went up to her.\n\n“There, that’s enough, that’s enough! You’re wretched too, I know. It can’t be helped. There’s no great harm done. God is merciful ... thanks....” he said, not knowing what he was saying, as he responded to the tearful kiss of the princess that he felt on his hand. And the prince went out of the room.\n\nBefore this, as soon as Kitty went out of the room in tears, Dolly, with her motherly, family instincts, had promptly perceived that here a woman’s work lay before her, and she prepared to do it. She took off her hat, and, morally speaking, tucked up her sleeves and prepared for action. While her mother was attacking her father, she tried to restrain her mother, so far as filial reverence would allow. During the prince’s outburst she was silent; she felt ashamed for her mother, and tender towards her father for so quickly being kind again. But when her father left them she made ready for what was the chief thing needful—to go to Kitty and console her.\n\n“I’d been meaning to tell you something for a long while, mamma: did you know that Levin meant to make Kitty an offer when he was here the last time? He told Stiva so.”\n\n“Well, what then? I don’t understand....”\n\n“So did Kitty perhaps refuse him?... She didn’t tell you so?”\n\n“No, she has said nothing to me either of one or the other; she’s too proud. But I know it’s all on account of the other.”\n\n“Yes, but suppose she has refused Levin, and she wouldn’t have refused him if it hadn’t been for the other, I know. And then, he has deceived her so horribly.”\n\nIt was too terrible for the princess to think how she had sinned against her daughter, and she broke out angrily.\n\n“Oh, I really don’t understand! Nowadays they will all go their own way, and mothers haven’t a word to say in anything, and then....”\n\n“Mamma, I’ll go up to her.”\n\n“Well, do. Did I tell you not to?” said her mother.\n\n# Chapter 3\n\nWhen she went into Kitty’s little room, a pretty, pink little room, full of knick-knacks in vieux saxe, as fresh, and pink, and white, and gay as Kitty herself had been two months ago, Dolly remembered how they had decorated the room the year before together, with what love and gaiety. Her heart turned cold when she saw Kitty sitting on a low chair near the door, her eyes fixed immovably on a corner of the rug. Kitty glanced at her sister, and the cold, rather ill-tempered expression of her face did not change.\n\n“I’m just going now, and I shall have to keep in and you won’t be able to come to see me,” said Dolly, sitting down beside her. “I want to talk to you.”\n\n“What about?” Kitty asked swiftly, lifting her head in dismay.\n\n“What should it be, but your trouble?”\n\n“I have no trouble.”\n\n“Nonsense, Kitty. Do you suppose I could help knowing? I know all about it. And believe me, it’s of so little consequence.... We’ve all been through it.”\n\nKitty did not speak, and her face had a stern expression.\n\n“He’s not worth your grieving over him,” pursued Darya Alexandrovna, coming straight to the point.\n\n“No, because he has treated me with contempt,” said Kitty, in a breaking voice. “Don’t talk of it! Please, don’t talk of it!”\n\n“But who can have told you so? No one has said that. I’m certain he was in love with you, and would still be in love with you, if it hadn’t....”\n\n“Oh, the most awful thing of all for me is this sympathizing!” shrieked Kitty, suddenly flying into a passion. She turned round on her chair, flushed crimson, and rapidly moving her fingers, pinched the clasp of her belt first with one hand and then with the other. Dolly knew this trick her sister had of clenching her hands when she was much excited; she knew, too, that in moments of excitement Kitty was capable of forgetting herself and saying a great deal too much, and Dolly would have soothed her, but it was too late.\n\n“What, what is it you want to make me feel, eh?” said Kitty quickly. “That I’ve been in love with a man who didn’t care a straw for me, and that I’m dying of love for him? And this is said to me by my own sister, who imagines that ... that ... that she’s sympathizing with me!... I don’t want these condolences and humbug!”\n\n“Kitty, you’re unjust.”\n\n“Why are you tormenting me?”\n\n“But I ... quite the contrary ... I see you’re unhappy....”\n\nBut Kitty in her fury did not hear her.\n\n“I’ve nothing to grieve over and be comforted about. I am too proud ever to allow myself to care for a man who does not love me.”\n\n“Yes, I don’t say so either.... Only one thing. Tell me the truth,” said Darya Alexandrovna, taking her by the hand: “tell me, did Levin speak to you?...”\n\nThe mention of Levin’s name seemed to deprive Kitty of the last vestige of self-control. She leaped up from her chair, and flinging her clasp on the ground, she gesticulated rapidly with her hands and said:\n\n“Why bring Levin in too? I can’t understand what you want to torment me for. I’ve told you, and I say it again, that I have some pride, and never, never would I do as you’re doing—go back to a man who’s deceived you, who has cared for another woman. I can’t understand it! You may, but I can’t!”\n\nAnd saying these words she glanced at her sister, and seeing that Dolly sat silent, her head mournfully bowed, Kitty, instead of running out of the room as she had meant to do, sat down near the door, and hid her face in her handkerchief.\n\nThe silence lasted for two minutes: Dolly was thinking of herself. That humiliation of which she was always conscious came back to her with a peculiar bitterness when her sister reminded her of it. She had not looked for such cruelty in her sister, and she was angry with her. But suddenly she heard the rustle of a skirt, and with it the sound of heart-rending, smothered sobbing, and felt arms about her neck. Kitty was on her knees before her.\n\n“Dolinka, I am so, so wretched!” she whispered penitently. And the sweet face covered with tears hid itself in Darya Alexandrovna’s skirt.\n\nAs though tears were the indispensable oil, without which the machinery of mutual confidence could not run smoothly between the two sisters, the sisters after their tears talked, not of what was uppermost in their minds, but, though they talked of outside matters, they understood each other. Kitty knew that the words she had uttered in anger about her husband’s infidelity and her humiliating position had cut her poor sister to the heart, but that she had forgiven her. Dolly for her part knew all she had wanted to find out. She felt certain that her surmises were correct; that Kitty’s misery, her inconsolable misery, was due precisely to the fact that Levin had made her an offer and she had refused him, and Vronsky had deceived her, and that she was fully prepared to love Levin and to detest Vronsky. Kitty said not a word of that; she talked of nothing but her spiritual condition.\n\n“I have nothing to make me miserable,” she said, getting calmer; “but can you understand that everything has become hateful, loathsome, coarse to me, and I myself most of all? You can’t imagine what loathsome thoughts I have about everything.”\n\n“Why, whatever loathsome thoughts can you have?” asked Dolly, smiling.\n\n“The most utterly loathsome and coarse: I can’t tell you. It’s not unhappiness, or low spirits, but much worse. As though everything that was good in me was all hidden away, and nothing was left but the most loathsome. Come, how am I to tell you?” she went on, seeing the puzzled look in her sister’s eyes. “Father began saying something to me just now.... It seems to me he thinks all I want is to be married. Mother takes me to a ball: it seems to me she only takes me to get me married off as soon as may be, and be rid of me. I know it’s not the truth, but I can’t drive away such thoughts. Eligible suitors, as they call them—I can’t bear to see them. It seems to me they’re taking stock of me and summing me up. In old days to go anywhere in a ball dress was a simple joy to me, I admired myself; now I feel ashamed and awkward. And then! The doctor.... Then....” Kitty hesitated; she wanted to say further that ever since this change had taken place in her, Stepan Arkadyevitch had become insufferably repulsive to her, and that she could not see him without the grossest and most hideous conceptions rising before her imagination.\n\n“Oh, well, everything presents itself to me, in the coarsest, most loathsome light,” she went on. “That’s my illness. Perhaps it will pass off.”\n\n“But you mustn’t think about it.”\n\n“I can’t help it. I’m never happy except with the children at your house.”\n\n“What a pity you can’t be with me!”\n\n“Oh, yes, I’m coming. I’ve had scarlatina, and I’ll persuade mamma to let me.”\n\nKitty insisted on having her way, and went to stay at her sister’s and nursed the children all through the scarlatina, for scarlatina it turned out to be. The two sisters brought all the six children successfully through it, but Kitty was no better in health, and in Lent the Shtcherbatskys went abroad.\n\n# Chapter 4\n\nThe highest Petersburg society is essentially one: in it everyone knows everyone else, everyone even visits everyone else. But this great set has its subdivisions. Anna Arkadyevna Karenina had friends and close ties in three different circles of this highest society. One circle was her husband’s government official set, consisting of his colleagues and subordinates, brought together in the most various and capricious manner, and belonging to different social strata. Anna found it difficult now to recall the feeling of almost awe-stricken reverence which she had at first entertained for these persons. Now she knew all of them as people know one another in a country town; she knew their habits and weaknesses, and where the shoe pinched each one of them. She knew their relations with one another and with the head authorities, knew who was for whom, and how each one maintained his position, and where they agreed and disagreed. But the circle of political, masculine interests had never interested her, in spite of countess Lidia Ivanovna’s influence, and she avoided it.\n\nAnother little set with which Anna was in close relations was the one by means of which Alexey Alexandrovitch had made his career. The center of this circle was the Countess Lidia Ivanovna. It was a set made up of elderly, ugly, benevolent, and godly women, and clever, learned, and ambitious men. One of the clever people belonging to the set had called it “the conscience of Petersburg society.” Alexey Alexandrovitch had the highest esteem for this circle, and Anna with her special gift for getting on with everyone, had in the early days of her life in Petersburg made friends in this circle also. Now, since her return from Moscow, she had come to feel this set insufferable. It seemed to her that both she and all of them were insincere, and she felt so bored and ill at ease in that world that she went to see the Countess Lidia Ivanovna as little as possible.\n\nThe third circle with which Anna had ties was preeminently the fashionable world—the world of balls, of dinners, of sumptuous dresses, the world that hung on to the court with one hand, so as to avoid sinking to the level of the demi-monde. For the demi-monde the members of that fashionable world believed that they despised, though their tastes were not merely similar, but in fact identical. Her connection with this circle was kept up through Princess Betsy Tverskaya, her cousin’s wife, who had an income of a hundred and twenty thousand roubles, and who had taken a great fancy to Anna ever since she first came out, showed her much attention, and drew her into her set, making fun of Countess Lidia Ivanovna’s coterie.\n\n“When I’m old and ugly I’ll be the same,” Betsy used to say; “but for a pretty young woman like you it’s early days for that house of charity.”\n\nAnna had at first avoided as far as she could Princess Tverskaya’s world, because it necessitated an expenditure beyond her means, and besides in her heart she preferred the first circle. But since her visit to Moscow she had done quite the contrary. She avoided her serious-minded friends, and went out into the fashionable world. There she met Vronsky, and experienced an agitating joy at those meetings. She met Vronsky specially often at Betsy’s for Betsy was a Vronsky by birth and his cousin. Vronsky was everywhere where he had any chance of meeting Anna, and speaking to her, when he could, of his love. She gave him no encouragement, but every time she met him there surged up in her heart that same feeling of quickened life that had come upon her that day in the railway carriage when she saw him for the first time. She was conscious herself that her delight sparkled in her eyes and curved her lips into a smile, and she could not quench the expression of this delight.\n\nAt first Anna sincerely believed that she was displeased with him for daring to pursue her. Soon after her return from Moscow, on arriving at a soirée where she had expected to meet him, and not finding him there, she realized distinctly from the rush of disappointment that she had been deceiving herself, and that this pursuit was not merely not distasteful to her, but that it made the whole interest of her life.\n\nThe celebrated singer was singing for the second time, and all the fashionable world was in the theater. Vronsky, seeing his cousin from his stall in the front row, did not wait till the entr’acte, but went to her box.\n\n“Why didn’t you come to dinner?” she said to him. “I marvel at the second sight of lovers,” she added with a smile, so that no one but he could hear; “she wasn’t there. But come after the opera.”\n\nVronsky looked inquiringly at her. She nodded. He thanked her by a smile, and sat down beside her.\n\n“But how I remember your jeers!” continued Princess Betsy, who took a peculiar pleasure in following up this passion to a successful issue. “What’s become of all that? You’re caught, my dear boy.”\n\n“That’s my one desire, to be caught,” answered Vronsky, with his serene, good-humored smile. “If I complain of anything it’s only that I’m not caught enough, to tell the truth. I begin to lose hope.”\n\n“Why, whatever hope can you have?” said Betsy, offended on behalf of her friend. “Entendons nous....” But in her eyes there were gleams of light that betrayed that she understood perfectly and precisely as he did what hope he might have.\n\n“None whatever,” said Vronsky, laughing and showing his even rows of teeth. “Excuse me,” he added, taking an opera-glass out of her hand, and proceeding to scrutinize, over her bare shoulder, the row of boxes facing them. “I’m afraid I’m becoming ridiculous.”\n\nHe was very well aware that he ran no risk of being ridiculous in the eyes of Betsy or any other fashionable people. He was very well aware that in their eyes the position of an unsuccessful lover of a girl, or of any woman free to marry, might be ridiculous. But the position of a man pursuing a married woman, and, regardless of everything, staking his life on drawing her into adultery, has something fine and grand about it, and can never be ridiculous; and so it was with a proud and gay smile under his mustaches that he lowered the opera-glass and looked at his cousin.\n\n“But why was it you didn’t come to dinner?” she said, admiring him.\n\n“I must tell you about that. I was busily employed, and doing what, do you suppose? I’ll give you a hundred guesses, a thousand ... you’d never guess. I’ve been reconciling a husband with a man who’d insulted his wife. Yes, really!”\n\n“Well, did you succeed?”\n\n“Almost.”\n\n“You really must tell me about it,” she said, getting up. “Come to me in the next entr’acte.”\n\n“I can’t; I’m going to the French theater.”\n\n“From Nilsson?” Betsy queried in horror, though she could not herself have distinguished Nilsson’s voice from any chorus girl’s.\n\n“Can’t help it. I’ve an appointment there, all to do with my mission of peace.”\n\n“‘Blessed are the peacemakers; theirs is the kingdom of heaven,’” said Betsy, vaguely recollecting she had heard some similar saying from someone. “Very well, then, sit down, and tell me what it’s all about.”\n\nAnd she sat down again.\n\n# Chapter 5\n\n“This is rather indiscreet, but it’s so good it’s an awful temptation to tell the story,” said Vronsky, looking at her with his laughing eyes. “I’m not going to mention any names.”\n\n“But I shall guess, so much the better.”\n\n“Well, listen: two festive young men were driving—”\n\n“Officers of your regiment, of course?”\n\n“I didn’t say they were officers,—two young men who had been lunching.”\n\n“In other words, drinking.”\n\n“Possibly. They were driving on their way to dinner with a friend in the most festive state of mind. And they beheld a pretty woman in a hired sledge; she overtakes them, looks round at them, and, so they fancy anyway, nods to them and laughs. They, of course, follow her. They gallop at full speed. To their amazement, the fair one alights at the entrance of the very house to which they were going. The fair one darts upstairs to the top story. They get a glimpse of red lips under a short veil, and exquisite little feet.”\n\n“You describe it with such feeling that I fancy you must be one of the two.”\n\n“And after what you said, just now! Well, the young men go in to their comrade’s; he was giving a farewell dinner. There they certainly did drink a little too much, as one always does at farewell dinners. And at dinner they inquire who lives at the top in that house. No one knows; only their host’s valet, in answer to their inquiry whether any ‘young ladies’ are living on the top floor, answered that there were a great many of them about there. After dinner the two young men go into their host’s study, and write a letter to the unknown fair one. They compose an ardent epistle, a declaration in fact, and they carry the letter upstairs themselves, so as to elucidate whatever might appear not perfectly intelligible in the letter.”\n\n“Why are you telling me these horrible stories? Well?”\n\n“They ring. A maid-servant opens the door, they hand her the letter, and assure the maid that they’re both so in love that they’ll die on the spot at the door. The maid, stupefied, carries in their messages. All at once a gentleman appears with whiskers like sausages, as red as a lobster, announces that there is no one living in the flat except his wife, and sends them both about their business.”\n\n“How do you know he had whiskers like sausages, as you say?”\n\n“Ah, you shall hear. I’ve just been to make peace between them.”\n\n“Well, and what then?”\n\n“That’s the most interesting part of the story. It appears that it’s a happy couple, a government clerk and his lady. The government clerk lodges a complaint, and I became a mediator, and such a mediator!... I assure you Talleyrand couldn’t hold a candle to me.”\n\n“Why, where was the difficulty?”\n\n“Ah, you shall hear.... We apologize in due form: we are in despair, we entreat forgiveness for the unfortunate misunderstanding. The government clerk with the sausages begins to melt, but he, too, desires to express his sentiments, and as soon as ever he begins to express them, he begins to get hot and say nasty things, and again I’m obliged to trot out all my diplomatic talents. I allowed that their conduct was bad, but I urged him to take into consideration their heedlessness, their youth; then, too, the young men had only just been lunching together. ‘You understand. They regret it deeply, and beg you to overlook their misbehavior.’ The government clerk was softened once more. ‘I consent, count, and am ready to overlook it; but you perceive that my wife—my wife’s a respectable woman—has been exposed to the persecution, and insults, and effrontery of young upstarts, scoundrels....’ And you must understand, the young upstarts are present all the while, and I have to keep the peace between them. Again I call out all my diplomacy, and again as soon as the thing was about at an end, our friend the government clerk gets hot and red, and his sausages stand on end with wrath, and once more I launch out into diplomatic wiles.”\n\n“Ah, he must tell you this story!” said Betsy, laughing, to a lady who came into her box. “He has been making me laugh so.”\n\n“Well, bonne chance!” she added, giving Vronsky one finger of the hand in which she held her fan, and with a shrug of her shoulders she twitched down the bodice of her gown that had worked up, so as to be duly naked as she moved forward towards the footlights into the light of the gas, and the sight of all eyes.\n\nVronsky drove to the French theater, where he really had to see the colonel of his regiment, who never missed a single performance there. He wanted to see him, to report on the result of his mediation, which had occupied and amused him for the last three days. Petritsky, whom he liked, was implicated in the affair, and the other culprit was a capital fellow and first-rate comrade, who had lately joined the regiment, the young Prince Kedrov. And what was most important, the interests of the regiment were involved in it too.\n\nBoth the young men were in Vronsky’s company. The colonel of the regiment was waited upon by the government clerk, Venden, with a complaint against his officers, who had insulted his wife. His young wife, so Venden told the story—he had been married half a year—was at church with her mother, and suddenly overcome by indisposition, arising from her interesting condition, she could not remain standing, she drove home in the first sledge, a smart-looking one, she came across. On the spot the officers set off in pursuit of her; she was alarmed, and feeling still more unwell, ran up the staircase home. Venden himself, on returning from his office, heard a ring at their bell and voices, went out, and seeing the intoxicated officers with a letter, he had turned them out. He asked for exemplary punishment.\n\n“Yes, it’s all very well,” said the colonel to Vronsky, whom he had invited to come and see him. “Petritsky’s becoming impossible. Not a week goes by without some scandal. This government clerk won’t let it drop, he’ll go on with the thing.”\n\nVronsky saw all the thanklessness of the business, and that there could be no question of a duel in it, that everything must be done to soften the government clerk, and hush the matter up. The colonel had called in Vronsky just because he knew him to be an honorable and intelligent man, and, more than all, a man who cared for the honor of the regiment. They talked it over, and decided that Petritsky and Kedrov must go with Vronsky to Venden’s to apologize. The colonel and Vronsky were both fully aware that Vronsky’s name and rank would be sure to contribute greatly to the softening of the injured husband’s feelings.\n\nAnd these two influences were not in fact without effect; though the result remained, as Vronsky had described, uncertain.\n\nOn reaching the French theater, Vronsky retired to the foyer with the colonel, and reported to him his success, or non-success. The colonel, thinking it all over, made up his mind not to pursue the matter further, but then for his own satisfaction proceeded to cross-examine Vronsky about his interview; and it was a long while before he could restrain his laughter, as Vronsky described how the government clerk, after subsiding for a while, would suddenly flare up again, as he recalled the details, and how Vronsky, at the last half word of conciliation, skillfully manœuvered a retreat, shoving Petritsky out before him.\n\n“It’s a disgraceful story, but killing. Kedrov really can’t fight the gentleman! Was he so awfully hot?” he commented, laughing. “But what do you say to Claire today? She’s marvelous,” he went on, speaking of a new French actress. “However often you see her, every day she’s different. It’s only the French who can do that.”\n\n# Chapter 6\n\nPrincess Betsy drove home from the theater, without waiting for the end of the last act. She had only just time to go into her dressing-room, sprinkle her long, pale face with powder, rub it, set her dress to rights, and order tea in the big drawing-room, when one after another carriages drove up to her huge house in Bolshaia Morskaia. Her guests stepped out at the wide entrance, and the stout porter, who used to read the newspapers in the mornings behind the glass door, to the edification of the passers-by, noiselessly opened the immense door, letting the visitors pass by him into the house.\n\nAlmost at the same instant the hostess, with freshly arranged coiffure and freshened face, walked in at one door and her guests at the other door of the drawing-room, a large room with dark walls, downy rugs, and a brightly lighted table, gleaming with the light of candles, white cloth, silver samovar, and transparent china tea-things.\n\nThe hostess sat down at the table and took off her gloves. Chairs were set with the aid of footmen, moving almost imperceptibly about the room; the party settled itself, divided into two groups: one round the samovar near the hostess, the other at the opposite end of the drawing-room, round the handsome wife of an ambassador, in black velvet, with sharply defined black eyebrows. In both groups conversation wavered, as it always does, for the first few minutes, broken up by meetings, greetings, offers of tea, and as it were, feeling about for something to rest upon.\n\n“She’s exceptionally good as an actress; one can see she’s studied Kaulbach,” said a diplomatic attaché in the group round the ambassador’s wife. “Did you notice how she fell down?...”\n\n“Oh, please, don’t let us talk about Nilsson! No one can possibly say anything new about her,” said a fat, red-faced, flaxen-headed lady, without eyebrows and chignon, wearing an old silk dress. This was Princess Myakaya, noted for her simplicity and the roughness of her manners, and nicknamed enfant terrible. Princess Myakaya, sitting in the middle between the two groups, and listening to both, took part in the conversation first of one and then of the other. “Three people have used that very phrase about Kaulbach to me today already, just as though they had made a compact about it. And I can’t see why they liked that remark so.”\n\nThe conversation was cut short by this observation, and a new subject had to be thought of again.\n\n“Do tell me something amusing but not spiteful,” said the ambassador’s wife, a great proficient in the art of that elegant conversation called by the English small talk. She addressed the attaché, who was at a loss now what to begin upon.\n\n“They say that that’s a difficult task, that nothing’s amusing that isn’t spiteful,” he began with a smile. “But I’ll try. Get me a subject. It all lies in the subject. If a subject’s given me, it’s easy to spin something round it. I often think that the celebrated talkers of the last century would have found it difficult to talk cleverly now. Everything clever is so stale....”\n\n“That has been said long ago,” the ambassador’s wife interrupted him, laughing.\n\nThe conversation began amiably, but just because it was too amiable, it came to a stop again. They had to have recourse to the sure, never-failing topic—gossip.\n\n“Don’t you think there’s something Louis Quinze about Tushkevitch?” he said, glancing towards a handsome, fair-haired young man, standing at the table.\n\n“Oh, yes! He’s in the same style as the drawing-room and that’s why it is he’s so often here.”\n\nThis conversation was maintained, since it rested on allusions to what could not be talked of in that room—that is to say, of the relations of Tushkevitch with their hostess.\n\nRound the samovar and the hostess the conversation had been meanwhile vacillating in just the same way between three inevitable topics: the latest piece of public news, the theater, and scandal. It, too, came finally to rest on the last topic, that is, ill-natured gossip.\n\n“Have you heard the Maltishtcheva woman—the mother, not the daughter—has ordered a costume in diable rose color?”\n\n“Nonsense! No, that’s too lovely!”\n\n“I wonder that with her sense—for she’s not a fool, you know—that she doesn’t see how funny she is.”\n\nEveryone had something to say in censure or ridicule of the luckless Madame Maltishtcheva, and the conversation crackled merrily, like a burning faggot-stack.\n\nThe husband of Princess Betsy, a good-natured fat man, an ardent collector of engravings, hearing that his wife had visitors, came into the drawing-room before going to his club. Stepping noiselessly over the thick rugs, he went up to Princess Myakaya.\n\n“How did you like Nilsson?” he asked.\n\n“Oh, how can you steal upon anyone like that! How you startled me!” she responded. “Please don’t talk to me about the opera; you know nothing about music. I’d better meet you on your own ground, and talk about your majolica and engravings. Come now, what treasure have you been buying lately at the old curiosity shops?”\n\n“Would you like me to show you? But you don’t understand such things.”\n\n“Oh, do show me! I’ve been learning about them at those—what’s their names?... the bankers ... they’ve some splendid engravings. They showed them to us.”\n\n“Why, have you been at the Schützburgs?” asked the hostess from the samovar.\n\n“Yes, ma chère. They asked my husband and me to dinner, and told us the sauce at that dinner cost a hundred pounds,” Princess Myakaya said, speaking loudly, and conscious everyone was listening; “and very nasty sauce it was, some green mess. We had to ask them, and I made them sauce for eighteen pence, and everybody was very much pleased with it. I can’t run to hundred-pound sauces.”\n\n“She’s unique!” said the lady of the house.\n\n“Marvelous!” said someone.\n\nThe sensation produced by Princess Myakaya’s speeches was always unique, and the secret of the sensation she produced lay in the fact that though she spoke not always appropriately, as now, she said simple things with some sense in them. In the society in which she lived such plain statements produced the effect of the wittiest epigram. Princess Myakaya could never see why it had that effect, but she knew it had, and took advantage of it.\n\nAs everyone had been listening while Princess Myakaya spoke, and so the conversation around the ambassador’s wife had dropped, Princess Betsy tried to bring the whole party together, and turned to the ambassador’s wife.\n\n“Will you really not have tea? You should come over here by us.”\n\n“No, we’re very happy here,” the ambassador’s wife responded with a smile, and she went on with the conversation that had been begun.\n\nIt was a very agreeable conversation. They were criticizing the Karenins, husband and wife.\n\n“Anna is quite changed since her stay in Moscow. There’s something strange about her,” said her friend.\n\n“The great change is that she brought back with her the shadow of Alexey Vronsky,” said the ambassador’s wife.\n\n“Well, what of it? There’s a fable of Grimm’s about a man without a shadow, a man who’s lost his shadow. And that’s his punishment for something. I never could understand how it was a punishment. But a woman must dislike being without a shadow.”\n\n“Yes, but women with a shadow usually come to a bad end,” said Anna’s friend.\n\n“Bad luck to your tongue!” said Princess Myakaya suddenly. “Madame Karenina’s a splendid woman. I don’t like her husband, but I like her very much.”\n\n“Why don’t you like her husband? He’s such a remarkable man,” said the ambassador’s wife. “My husband says there are few statesmen like him in Europe.”\n\n“And my husband tells me just the same, but I don’t believe it,” said Princess Myakaya. “If our husbands didn’t talk to us, we should see the facts as they are. Alexey Alexandrovitch, to my thinking, is simply a fool. I say it in a whisper ... but doesn’t it really make everything clear? Before, when I was told to consider him clever, I kept looking for his ability, and thought myself a fool for not seeing it; but directly I said, he’s a fool, though only in a whisper, everything’s explained, isn’t it?”\n\n“How spiteful you are today!”\n\n“Not a bit. I’d no other way out of it. One of the two had to be a fool. And, well, you know one can’t say that of oneself.”\n\n“‘No one is satisfied with his fortune, and everyone is satisfied with his wit.’” The attaché repeated the French saying.\n\n“That’s just it, just it,” Princess Myakaya turned to him. “But the point is that I won’t abandon Anna to your mercies. She’s so nice, so charming. How can she help it if they’re all in love with her, and follow her about like shadows?”\n\n“Oh, I had no idea of blaming her for it,” Anna’s friend said in self-defense.\n\n“If no one follows us about like a shadow, that’s no proof that we’ve any right to blame her.”\n\nAnd having duly disposed of Anna’s friend, the Princess Myakaya got up, and together with the ambassador’s wife, joined the group at the table, where the conversation was dealing with the king of Prussia.\n\n“What wicked gossip were you talking over there?” asked Betsy.\n\n“About the Karenins. The princess gave us a sketch of Alexey Alexandrovitch,” said the ambassador’s wife with a smile, as she sat down at the table.\n\n“Pity we didn’t hear it!” said Princess Betsy, glancing towards the door. “Ah, here you are at last!” she said, turning with a smile to Vronsky, as he came in.\n\nVronsky was not merely acquainted with all the persons whom he was meeting here; he saw them all every day; and so he came in with the quiet manner with which one enters a room full of people from whom one has only just parted.\n\n“Where do I come from?” he said, in answer to a question from the ambassador’s wife. “Well, there’s no help for it, I must confess. From the opera bouffe. I do believe I’ve seen it a hundred times, and always with fresh enjoyment. It’s exquisite! I know it’s disgraceful, but I go to sleep at the opera, and I sit out the opera bouffe to the last minute, and enjoy it. This evening....”\n\nHe mentioned a French actress, and was going to tell something about her; but the ambassador’s wife, with playful horror, cut him short.\n\n“Please don’t tell us about that horror.”\n\n“All right, I won’t especially as everyone knows those horrors.”\n\n“And we should all go to see them if it were accepted as the correct thing, like the opera,” chimed in Princess Myakaya.\n\n# Chapter 7\n\nSteps were heard at the door, and Princess Betsy, knowing it was Madame Karenina, glanced at Vronsky. He was looking towards the door, and his face wore a strange new expression. Joyfully, intently, and at the same time timidly, he gazed at the approaching figure, and slowly he rose to his feet. Anna walked into the drawing-room. Holding herself extremely erect, as always, looking straight before her, and moving with her swift, resolute, and light step, that distinguished her from all other society women, she crossed the short space to her hostess, shook hands with her, smiled, and with the same smile looked around at Vronsky. Vronsky bowed low and pushed a chair up for her.\n\nShe acknowledged this only by a slight nod, flushed a little, and frowned. But immediately, while rapidly greeting her acquaintances, and shaking the hands proffered to her, she addressed Princess Betsy:\n\n“I have been at Countess Lidia’s, and meant to have come here earlier, but I stayed on. Sir John was there. He’s very interesting.”\n\n“Oh, that’s this missionary?”\n\n“Yes; he told us about the life in India, most interesting things.”\n\nThe conversation, interrupted by her coming in, flickered up again like the light of a lamp being blown out.\n\n“Sir John! Yes, Sir John; I’ve seen him. He speaks well. The Vlassieva girl’s quite in love with him.”\n\n“And is it true the younger Vlassieva girl’s to marry Topov?”\n\n“Yes, they say it’s quite a settled thing.”\n\n“I wonder at the parents! They say it’s a marriage for love.”\n\n“For love? What antediluvian notions you have! Can one talk of love in these days?” said the ambassador’s wife.\n\n“What’s to be done? It’s a foolish old fashion that’s kept up still,” said Vronsky.\n\n“So much the worse for those who keep up the fashion. The only happy marriages I know are marriages of prudence.”\n\n“Yes, but then how often the happiness of these prudent marriages flies away like dust just because that passion turns up that they have refused to recognize,” said Vronsky.\n\n“But by marriages of prudence we mean those in which both parties have sown their wild oats already. That’s like scarlatina—one has to go through it and get it over.”\n\n“Then they ought to find out how to vaccinate for love, like smallpox.”\n\n“I was in love in my young days with a deacon,” said the Princess Myakaya. “I don’t know that it did me any good.”\n\n“No; I imagine, joking apart, that to know love, one must make mistakes and then correct them,” said Princess Betsy.\n\n“Even after marriage?” said the ambassador’s wife playfully.\n\n“‘It’s never too late to mend.’” The attaché repeated the English proverb.\n\n“Just so,” Betsy agreed; “one must make mistakes and correct them. What do you think about it?” she turned to Anna, who, with a faintly perceptible resolute smile on her lips, was listening in silence to the conversation.\n\n“I think,” said Anna, playing with the glove she had taken off, “I think ... of so many men, so many minds, certainly so many hearts, so many kinds of love.”\n\nVronsky was gazing at Anna, and with a fainting heart waiting for what she would say. He sighed as after a danger escaped when she uttered these words.\n\nAnna suddenly turned to him.\n\n“Oh, I have had a letter from Moscow. They write me that Kitty Shtcherbatskaya’s very ill.”\n\n“Really?” said Vronsky, knitting his brows.\n\nAnna looked sternly at him.\n\n“That doesn’t interest you?”\n\n“On the contrary, it does, very much. What was it exactly they told you, if I may know?” he questioned.\n\nAnna got up and went to Betsy.\n\n“Give me a cup of tea,” she said, standing at her table.\n\nWhile Betsy was pouring out the tea, Vronsky went up to Anna.\n\n“What is it they write to you?” he repeated.\n\n“I often think men have no understanding of what’s not honorable though they’re always talking of it,” said Anna, without answering him. “I’ve wanted to tell you so a long while,” she added, and moving a few steps away, she sat down at a table in a corner covered with albums.\n\n“I don’t quite understand the meaning of your words,” he said, handing her the cup.\n\nShe glanced towards the sofa beside her, and he instantly sat down.\n\n“Yes, I have been wanting to tell you,” she said, not looking at him. “You behaved wrongly, very wrongly.”\n\n“Do you suppose I don’t know that I’ve acted wrongly? But who was the cause of my doing so?”\n\n“What do you say that to me for?” she said, glancing severely at him.\n\n“You know what for,” he answered boldly and joyfully, meeting her glance and not dropping his eyes.\n\nNot he, but she, was confused.\n\n“That only shows you have no heart,” she said. But her eyes said that she knew he had a heart, and that was why she was afraid of him.\n\n“What you spoke of just now was a mistake, and not love.”\n\n“Remember that I have forbidden you to utter that word, that hateful word,” said Anna, with a shudder. But at once she felt that by that very word “forbidden” she had shown that she acknowledged certain rights over him, and by that very fact was encouraging him to speak of love. “I have long meant to tell you this,” she went on, looking resolutely into his eyes, and hot all over from the burning flush on her cheeks. “I’ve come on purpose this evening, knowing I should meet you. I have come to tell you that this must end. I have never blushed before anyone, and you force me to feel to blame for something.”\n\nHe looked at her and was struck by a new spiritual beauty in her face.\n\n“What do you wish of me?” he said simply and seriously.\n\n“I want you to go to Moscow and ask for Kitty’s forgiveness,” she said.\n\n“You don’t wish that?” he said.\n\nHe saw she was saying what she forced herself to say, not what she wanted to say.\n\n“If you love me, as you say,” she whispered, “do so that I may be at peace.”\n\nHis face grew radiant.\n\n“Don’t you know that you’re all my life to me? But I know no peace, and I can’t give it to you; all myself—and love ... yes. I can’t think of you and myself apart. You and I are one to me. And I see no chance before us of peace for me or for you. I see a chance of despair, of wretchedness ... or I see a chance of bliss, what bliss!... Can it be there’s no chance of it?” he murmured with his lips; but she heard.\n\nShe strained every effort of her mind to say what ought to be said. But instead of that she let her eyes rest on him, full of love, and made no answer.\n\n“It’s come!” he thought in ecstasy. “When I was beginning to despair, and it seemed there would be no end—it’s come! She loves me! She owns it!”\n\n“Then do this for me: never say such things to me, and let us be friends,” she said in words; but her eyes spoke quite differently.\n\n“Friends we shall never be, you know that yourself. Whether we shall be the happiest or the wretchedest of people—that’s in your hands.”\n\nShe would have said something, but he interrupted her.\n\n“I ask one thing only: I ask for the right to hope, to suffer as I do. But if even that cannot be, command me to disappear, and I disappear. You shall not see me if my presence is distasteful to you.”\n\n“I don’t want to drive you away.”\n\n“Only don’t change anything, leave everything as it is,” he said in a shaky voice. “Here’s your husband.”\n\nAt that instant Alexey Alexandrovitch did in fact walk into the room with his calm, awkward gait.\n\nGlancing at his wife and Vronsky, he went up to the lady of the house, and sitting down for a cup of tea, began talking in his deliberate, always audible voice, in his habitual tone of banter, ridiculing someone.\n\n“Your Rambouillet is in full conclave,” he said, looking round at all the party; “the graces and the muses.”\n\nBut Princess Betsy could not endure that tone of his—“sneering,” as she called it, using the English word, and like a skillful hostess she at once brought him into a serious conversation on the subject of universal conscription. Alexey Alexandrovitch was immediately interested in the subject, and began seriously defending the new imperial decree against Princess Betsy, who had attacked it.\n\nVronsky and Anna still sat at the little table.\n\n“This is getting indecorous,” whispered one lady, with an expressive glance at Madame Karenina, Vronsky, and her husband.\n\n“What did I tell you?” said Anna’s friend.\n\nBut not only those ladies, almost everyone in the room, even the Princess Myakaya and Betsy herself, looked several times in the direction of the two who had withdrawn from the general circle, as though that were a disturbing fact. Alexey Alexandrovitch was the only person who did not once look in that direction, and was not diverted from the interesting discussion he had entered upon.\n\nNoticing the disagreeable impression that was being made on everyone, Princess Betsy slipped someone else into her place to listen to Alexey Alexandrovitch, and went up to Anna.\n\n“I’m always amazed at the clearness and precision of your husband’s language,” she said. “The most transcendental ideas seem to be within my grasp when he’s speaking.”\n\n“Oh, yes!” said Anna, radiant with a smile of happiness, and not understanding a word of what Betsy had said. She crossed over to the big table and took part in the general conversation.\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch, after staying half an hour, went up to his wife and suggested that they should go home together. But she answered, not looking at him, that she was staying to supper. Alexey Alexandrovitch made his bows and withdrew.\n\nThe fat old Tatar, Madame Karenina’s coachman, was with difficulty holding one of her pair of grays, chilled with the cold and rearing at the entrance. A footman stood opening the carriage door. The hall-porter stood holding open the great door of the house. Anna Arkadyevna, with her quick little hand, was unfastening the lace of her sleeve, caught in the hook of her fur cloak, and with bent head listening to the words Vronsky murmured as he escorted her down.\n\n“You’ve said nothing, of course, and I ask nothing,” he was saying; “but you know that friendship’s not what I want: that there’s only one happiness in life for me, that word that you dislike so ... yes, love!...”\n\n“Love,” she repeated slowly, in an inner voice, and suddenly, at the very instant she unhooked the lace, she added, “Why I don’t like the word is that it means too much to me, far more than you can understand,” and she glanced into his face. “Au revoir!”\n\nShe gave him her hand, and with her rapid, springy step she passed by the porter and vanished into the carriage.\n\nHer glance, the touch of her hand, set him aflame. He kissed the palm of his hand where she had touched it, and went home, happy in the sense that he had got nearer to the attainment of his aims that evening than during the last two months.\n\n# Chapter 8\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch had seen nothing striking or improper in the fact that his wife was sitting with Vronsky at a table apart, in eager conversation with him about something. But he noticed that to the rest of the party this appeared something striking and improper, and for that reason it seemed to him too to be improper. He made up his mind that he must speak of it to his wife.\n\nOn reaching home Alexey Alexandrovitch went to his study, as he usually did, seated himself in his low chair, opened a book on the Papacy at the place where he had laid the paper-knife in it, and read till one o’clock, just as he usually did. But from time to time he rubbed his high forehead and shook his head, as though to drive away something. At his usual time he got up and made his toilet for the night. Anna Arkadyevna had not yet come in. With a book under his arm he went upstairs. But this evening, instead of his usual thoughts and meditations upon official details, his thoughts were absorbed by his wife and something disagreeable connected with her. Contrary to his usual habit, he did not get into bed, but fell to walking up and down the rooms with his hands clasped behind his back. He could not go to bed, feeling that it was absolutely needful for him first to think thoroughly over the position that had just arisen.\n\nWhen Alexey Alexandrovitch had made up his mind that he must talk to his wife about it, it had seemed a very easy and simple matter. But now, when he began to think over the question that had just presented itself, it seemed to him very complicated and difficult.\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch was not jealous. Jealousy according to his notions was an insult to one’s wife, and one ought to have confidence in one’s wife. Why one ought to have confidence—that is to say, complete conviction that his young wife would always love him—he did not ask himself. But he had no experience of lack of confidence, because he had confidence in her, and told himself that he ought to have it. Now, though his conviction that jealousy was a shameful feeling and that one ought to feel confidence, had not broken down, he felt that he was standing face to face with something illogical and irrational, and did not know what was to be done. Alexey Alexandrovitch was standing face to face with life, with the possibility of his wife’s loving someone other than himself, and this seemed to him very irrational and incomprehensible because it was life itself. All his life Alexey Alexandrovitch had lived and worked in official spheres, having to do with the reflection of life. And every time he had stumbled against life itself he had shrunk away from it. Now he experienced a feeling akin to that of a man who, while calmly crossing a precipice by a bridge, should suddenly discover that the bridge is broken, and that there is a chasm below. That chasm was life itself, the bridge that artificial life in which Alexey Alexandrovitch had lived. For the first time the question presented itself to him of the possibility of his wife’s loving someone else, and he was horrified at it.\n\nHe did not undress, but walked up and down with his regular tread over the resounding parquet of the dining-room, where one lamp was burning, over the carpet of the dark drawing-room, in which the light was reflected on the big new portrait of himself hanging over the sofa, and across her boudoir, where two candles burned, lighting up the portraits of her parents and woman friends, and the pretty knick-knacks of her writing-table, that he knew so well. He walked across her boudoir to the bedroom door, and turned back again. At each turn in his walk, especially at the parquet of the lighted dining-room, he halted and said to himself, “Yes, this I must decide and put a stop to; I must express my view of it and my decision.” And he turned back again. “But express what—what decision?” he said to himself in the drawing-room, and he found no reply. “But after all,” he asked himself before turning into the boudoir, “what has occurred? Nothing. She was talking a long while with him. But what of that? Surely women in society can talk to whom they please. And then, jealousy means lowering both myself and her,” he told himself as he went into her boudoir; but this dictum, which had always had such weight with him before, had now no weight and no meaning at all. And from the bedroom door he turned back again; but as he entered the dark drawing-room some inner voice told him that it was not so, and that if others noticed it that showed that there was something. And he said to himself again in the dining-room, “Yes, I must decide and put a stop to it, and express my view of it....” And again at the turn in the drawing-room he asked himself, “Decide how?” And again he asked himself, “What had occurred?” and answered, “Nothing,” and recollected that jealousy was a feeling insulting to his wife; but again in the drawing-room he was convinced that something had happened. His thoughts, like his body, went round a complete circle, without coming upon anything new. He noticed this, rubbed his forehead, and sat down in her boudoir.\n\nThere, looking at her table, with the malachite blotting case lying at the top and an unfinished letter, his thoughts suddenly changed. He began to think of her, of what she was thinking and feeling. For the first time he pictured vividly to himself her personal life, her ideas, her desires, and the idea that she could and should have a separate life of her own seemed to him so alarming that he made haste to dispel it. It was the chasm which he was afraid to peep into. To put himself in thought and feeling in another person’s place was a spiritual exercise not natural to Alexey Alexandrovitch. He looked on this spiritual exercise as a harmful and dangerous abuse of the fancy.\n\n“And the worst of it all,” thought he, “is that just now, at the very moment when my great work is approaching completion” (he was thinking of the project he was bringing forward at the time), “when I stand in need of all my mental peace and all my energies, just now this stupid worry should fall foul of me. But what’s to be done? I’m not one of those men who submit to uneasiness and worry without having the force of character to face them.\n\n“I must think it over, come to a decision, and put it out of my mind,” he said aloud.\n\n“The question of her feelings, of what has passed and may be passing in her soul, that’s not my affair; that’s the affair of her conscience, and falls under the head of religion,” he said to himself, feeling consolation in the sense that he had found to which division of regulating principles this new circumstance could be properly referred.\n\n“And so,” Alexey Alexandrovitch said to himself, “questions as to her feelings, and so on, are questions for her conscience, with which I can have nothing to do. My duty is clearly defined. As the head of the family, I am a person bound in duty to guide her, and consequently, in part the person responsible; I am bound to point out the danger I perceive, to warn her, even to use my authority. I ought to speak plainly to her.” And everything that he would say tonight to his wife took clear shape in Alexey Alexandrovitch’s head. Thinking over what he would say, he somewhat regretted that he should have to use his time and mental powers for domestic consumption, with so little to show for it, but, in spite of that, the form and contents of the speech before him shaped itself as clearly and distinctly in his head as a ministerial report.\n\n“I must say and express fully the following points: first, exposition of the value to be attached to public opinion and to decorum; secondly, exposition of religious significance of marriage; thirdly, if need be, reference to the calamity possibly ensuing to our son; fourthly, reference to the unhappiness likely to result to herself.” And, interlacing his fingers, Alexey Alexandrovitch stretched them, and the joints of the fingers cracked. This trick, a bad habit, the cracking of his fingers, always soothed him, and gave precision to his thoughts, so needful to him at this juncture.\n\nThere was the sound of a carriage driving up to the front door. Alexey Alexandrovitch halted in the middle of the room.\n\nA woman’s step was heard mounting the stairs. Alexey Alexandrovitch, ready for his speech, stood compressing his crossed fingers, waiting to see if the crack would not come again. One joint cracked.\n\nAlready, from the sound of light steps on the stairs, he was aware that she was close, and though he was satisfied with his speech, he felt frightened of the explanation confronting him....\n\n# Chapter 9\n\nAnna came in with hanging head, playing with the tassels of her hood. Her face was brilliant and glowing; but this glow was not one of brightness; it suggested the fearful glow of a conflagration in the midst of a dark night. On seeing her husband, Anna raised her head and smiled, as though she had just waked up.\n\n“You’re not in bed? What a wonder!” she said, letting fall her hood, and without stopping, she went on into the dressing-room. “It’s late, Alexey Alexandrovitch,” she said, when she had gone through the doorway.\n\n“Anna, it’s necessary for me to have a talk with you.”\n\n“With me?” she said, wonderingly. She came out from behind the door of the dressing-room, and looked at him. “Why, what is it? What about?” she asked, sitting down. “Well, let’s talk, if it’s so necessary. But it would be better to get to sleep.”\n\nAnna said what came to her lips, and marveled, hearing herself, at her own capacity for lying. How simple and natural were her words, and how likely that she was simply sleepy! She felt herself clad in an impenetrable armor of falsehood. She felt that some unseen force had come to her aid and was supporting her.\n\n“Anna, I must warn you,” he began.\n\n“Warn me?” she said. “Of what?”\n\nShe looked at him so simply, so brightly, that anyone who did not know her as her husband knew her could not have noticed anything unnatural, either in the sound or the sense of her words. But to him, knowing her, knowing that whenever he went to bed five minutes later than usual, she noticed it, and asked him the reason; to him, knowing that every joy, every pleasure and pain that she felt she communicated to him at once; to him, now to see that she did not care to notice his state of mind, that she did not care to say a word about herself, meant a great deal. He saw that the inmost recesses of her soul, that had always hitherto lain open before him, were closed against him. More than that, he saw from her tone that she was not even perturbed at that, but as it were said straight out to him: “Yes, it’s shut up, and so it must be, and will be in future.” Now he experienced a feeling such as a man might have, returning home and finding his own house locked up. “But perhaps the key may yet be found,” thought Alexey Alexandrovitch.\n\n“I want to warn you,” he said in a low voice, “that through thoughtlessness and lack of caution you may cause yourself to be talked about in society. Your too animated conversation this evening with Count Vronsky” (he enunciated the name firmly and with deliberate emphasis) “attracted attention.”\n\nHe talked and looked at her laughing eyes, which frightened him now with their impenetrable look, and, as he talked, he felt all the uselessness and idleness of his words.\n\n“You’re always like that,” she answered, as though completely misapprehending him, and of all he had said only taking in the last phrase. “One time you don’t like my being dull, and another time you don’t like my being lively. I wasn’t dull. Does that offend you?”\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch shivered, and bent his hands to make the joints crack.\n\n“Oh, please, don’t do that, I do so dislike it,” she said.\n\n“Anna, is this you?” said Alexey Alexandrovitch, quietly making an effort over himself, and restraining the motion of his fingers.\n\n“But what is it all about?” she said, with such genuine and droll wonder. “What do you want of me?”\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch paused, and rubbed his forehead and his eyes. He saw that instead of doing as he had intended—that is to say, warning his wife against a mistake in the eyes of the world—he had unconsciously become agitated over what was the affair of her conscience, and was struggling against the barrier he fancied between them.\n\n“This is what I meant to say to you,” he went on coldly and composedly, “and I beg you to listen to it. I consider jealousy, as you know, a humiliating and degrading feeling, and I shall never allow myself to be influenced by it; but there are certain rules of decorum which cannot be disregarded with impunity. This evening it was not I observed it, but judging by the impression made on the company, everyone observed that your conduct and deportment were not altogether what could be desired.”\n\n“I positively don’t understand,” said Anna, shrugging her shoulders—“He doesn’t care,” she thought. “But other people noticed it, and that’s what upsets him.”—“You’re not well, Alexey Alexandrovitch,” she added, and she got up, and would have gone towards the door; but he moved forward as though he would stop her.\n\nHis face was ugly and forbidding, as Anna had never seen him. She stopped, and bending her head back and on one side, began with her rapid hand taking out her hairpins.\n\n“Well, I’m listening to what’s to come,” she said, calmly and ironically; “and indeed I listen with interest, for I should like to understand what’s the matter.”\n\nShe spoke, and marveled at the confident, calm, and natural tone in which she was speaking, and the choice of the words she used.\n\n“To enter into all the details of your feelings I have no right, and besides, I regard that as useless and even harmful,” began Alexey Alexandrovitch. “Ferreting in one’s soul, one often ferrets out something that might have lain there unnoticed. Your feelings are an affair of your own conscience; but I am in duty bound to you, to myself, and to God, to point out to you your duties. Our life has been joined, not by man, but by God. That union can only be severed by a crime, and a crime of that nature brings its own chastisement.”\n\n“I don’t understand a word. And, oh dear! how sleepy I am, unluckily,” she said, rapidly passing her hand through her hair, feeling for the remaining hairpins.\n\n“Anna, for God’s sake don’t speak like that!” he said gently. “Perhaps I am mistaken, but believe me, what I say, I say as much for myself as for you. I am your husband, and I love you.”\n\nFor an instant her face fell, and the mocking gleam in her eyes died away; but the word love threw her into revolt again. She thought: “Love? Can he love? If he hadn’t heard there was such a thing as love, he would never have used the word. He doesn’t even know what love is.”\n\n“Alexey Alexandrovitch, really I don’t understand,” she said. “Define what it is you find....”\n\n“Pardon, let me say all I have to say. I love you. But I am not speaking of myself; the most important persons in this matter are our son and yourself. It may very well be, I repeat, that my words seem to you utterly unnecessary and out of place; it may be that they are called forth by my mistaken impression. In that case, I beg you to forgive me. But if you are conscious yourself of even the smallest foundation for them, then I beg you to think a little, and if your heart prompts you, to speak out to me....”\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch was unconsciously saying something utterly unlike what he had prepared.\n\n“I have nothing to say. And besides,” she said hurriedly, with difficulty repressing a smile, “it’s really time to be in bed.”\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch sighed, and, without saying more, went into the bedroom.\n\nWhen she came into the bedroom, he was already in bed. His lips were sternly compressed, and his eyes looked away from her. Anna got into her bed, and lay expecting every minute that he would begin to speak to her again. She both feared his speaking and wished for it. But he was silent. She waited for a long while without moving, and had forgotten about him. She thought of that other; she pictured him, and felt how her heart was flooded with emotion and guilty delight at the thought of him. Suddenly she heard an even, tranquil snore. For the first instant Alexey Alexandrovitch seemed, as it were, appalled at his own snoring, and ceased; but after an interval of two breathings the snore sounded again, with a new tranquil rhythm.\n\n“It’s late, it’s late,” she whispered with a smile. A long while she lay, not moving, with open eyes, whose brilliance she almost fancied she could herself see in the darkness.\n\n# Chapter 10\n\nFrom that time a new life began for Alexey Alexandrovitch and for his wife. Nothing special happened. Anna went out into society, as she had always done, was particularly often at Princess Betsy’s, and met Vronsky everywhere. Alexey Alexandrovitch saw this, but could do nothing. All his efforts to draw her into open discussion she confronted with a barrier which he could not penetrate, made up of a sort of amused perplexity. Outwardly everything was the same, but their inner relations were completely changed. Alexey Alexandrovitch, a man of great power in the world of politics, felt himself helpless in this. Like an ox with head bent, submissively he awaited the blow which he felt was lifted over him. Every time he began to think about it, he felt that he must try once more, that by kindness, tenderness, and persuasion there was still hope of saving her, of bringing her back to herself, and every day he made ready to talk to her. But every time he began talking to her, he felt that the spirit of evil and deceit, which had taken possession of her, had possession of him too, and he talked to her in a tone quite unlike that in which he had meant to talk. Involuntarily he talked to her in his habitual tone of jeering at anyone who should say what he was saying. And in that tone it was impossible to say what needed to be said to her.\n\n# Chapter 11\n\nThat which for Vronsky had been almost a whole year the one absorbing desire of his life, replacing all his old desires; that which for Anna had been an impossible, terrible, and even for that reason more entrancing dream of bliss, that desire had been fulfilled. He stood before her, pale, his lower jaw quivering, and besought her to be calm, not knowing how or why.\n\n“Anna! Anna!” he said with a choking voice, “Anna, for pity’s sake!...”\n\nBut the louder he spoke, the lower she dropped her once proud and gay, now shame-stricken head, and she bowed down and sank from the sofa where she was sitting, down on the floor, at his feet; she would have fallen on the carpet if he had not held her.\n\n“My God! Forgive me!” she said, sobbing, pressing his hands to her bosom.\n\nShe felt so sinful, so guilty, that nothing was left her but to humiliate herself and beg forgiveness; and as now there was no one in her life but him, to him she addressed her prayer for forgiveness. Looking at him, she had a physical sense of her humiliation, and she could say nothing more. He felt what a murderer must feel, when he sees the body he has robbed of life. That body, robbed by him of life, was their love, the first stage of their love. There was something awful and revolting in the memory of what had been bought at this fearful price of shame. Shame at their spiritual nakedness crushed her and infected him. But in spite of all the murderer’s horror before the body of his victim, he must hack it to pieces, hide the body, must use what he has gained by his murder.\n\nAnd with fury, as it were with passion, the murderer falls on the body, and drags it and hacks at it; so he covered her face and shoulders with kisses. She held his hand, and did not stir. “Yes, these kisses—that is what has been bought by this shame. Yes, and one hand, which will always be mine—the hand of my accomplice.” She lifted up that hand and kissed it. He sank on his knees and tried to see her face; but she hid it, and said nothing. At last, as though making an effort over herself, she got up and pushed him away. Her face was still as beautiful, but it was only the more pitiful for that.\n\n“All is over,” she said; “I have nothing but you. Remember that.”\n\n“I can never forget what is my whole life. For one instant of this happiness....”\n\n“Happiness!” she said with horror and loathing and her horror unconsciously infected him. “For pity’s sake, not a word, not a word more.”\n\nShe rose quickly and moved away from him.\n\n“Not a word more,” she repeated, and with a look of chill despair, incomprehensible to him, she parted from him. She felt that at that moment she could not put into words the sense of shame, of rapture, and of horror at this stepping into a new life, and she did not want to speak of it, to vulgarize this feeling by inappropriate words. But later too, and the next day and the third day, she still found no words in which she could express the complexity of her feelings; indeed, she could not even find thoughts in which she could clearly think out all that was in her soul.\n\nShe said to herself: “No, just now I can’t think of it, later on, when I am calmer.” But this calm for thought never came; every time the thought rose of what she had done and what would happen to her, and what she ought to do, a horror came over her and she drove those thoughts away.\n\n“Later, later,” she said—“when I am calmer.”\n\nBut in dreams, when she had no control over her thoughts, her position presented itself to her in all its hideous nakedness. One dream haunted her almost every night. She dreamed that both were her husbands at once, that both were lavishing caresses on her. Alexey Alexandrovitch was weeping, kissing her hands, and saying, “How happy we are now!” And Alexey Vronsky was there too, and he too was her husband. And she was marveling that it had once seemed impossible to her, was explaining to them, laughing, that this was ever so much simpler, and that now both of them were happy and contented. But this dream weighed on her like a nightmare, and she awoke from it in terror.\n\n# Chapter 12\n\nIn the early days after his return from Moscow, whenever Levin shuddered and grew red, remembering the disgrace of his rejection, he said to himself: “This was just how I used to shudder and blush, thinking myself utterly lost, when I was plucked in physics and did not get my remove; and how I thought myself utterly ruined after I had mismanaged that affair of my sister’s that was entrusted to me. And yet, now that years have passed, I recall it and wonder that it could distress me so much. It will be the same thing too with this trouble. Time will go by and I shall not mind about this either.”\n\nBut three months had passed and he had not left off minding about it; and it was as painful for him to think of it as it had been those first days. He could not be at peace because after dreaming so long of family life, and feeling himself so ripe for it, he was still not married, and was further than ever from marriage. He was painfully conscious himself, as were all about him, that at his years it is not well for man to be alone. He remembered how before starting for Moscow he had once said to his cowman Nikolay, a simple-hearted peasant, whom he liked talking to: “Well, Nikolay! I mean to get married,” and how Nikolay had promptly answered, as of a matter on which there could be no possible doubt: “And high time too, Konstantin Dmitrievitch.” But marriage had now become further off than ever. The place was taken, and whenever he tried to imagine any of the girls he knew in that place, he felt that it was utterly impossible. Moreover, the recollection of the rejection and the part he had played in the affair tortured him with shame. However often he told himself that he was in no wise to blame in it, that recollection, like other humiliating reminiscences of a similar kind, made him twinge and blush. There had been in his past, as in every man’s, actions, recognized by him as bad, for which his conscience ought to have tormented him; but the memory of these evil actions was far from causing him so much suffering as those trivial but humiliating reminiscences. These wounds never healed. And with these memories was now ranged his rejection and the pitiful position in which he must have appeared to others that evening. But time and work did their part. Bitter memories were more and more covered up by the incidents—paltry in his eyes, but really important—of his country life. Every week he thought less often of Kitty. He was impatiently looking forward to the news that she was married, or just going to be married, hoping that such news would, like having a tooth out, completely cure him.\n\nMeanwhile spring came on, beautiful and kindly, without the delays and treacheries of spring,—one of those rare springs in which plants, beasts, and man rejoice alike. This lovely spring roused Levin still more, and strengthened him in his resolution of renouncing all his past and building up his lonely life firmly and independently. Though many of the plans with which he had returned to the country had not been carried out, still his most important resolution—that of purity—had been kept by him. He was free from that shame, which had usually harassed him after a fall; and he could look everyone straight in the face. In February he had received a letter from Marya Nikolaevna telling him that his brother Nikolay’s health was getting worse, but that he would not take advice, and in consequence of this letter Levin went to Moscow to his brother’s and succeeded in persuading him to see a doctor and to go to a watering-place abroad. He succeeded so well in persuading his brother, and in lending him money for the journey without irritating him, that he was satisfied with himself in that matter. In addition to his farming, which called for special attention in spring, and in addition to reading, Levin had begun that winter a work on agriculture, the plan of which turned on taking into account the character of the laborer on the land as one of the unalterable data of the question, like the climate and the soil, and consequently deducing all the principles of scientific culture, not simply from the data of soil and climate, but from the data of soil, climate, and a certain unalterable character of the laborer. Thus, in spite of his solitude, or in consequence of his solitude, his life was exceedingly full. Only rarely he suffered from an unsatisfied desire to communicate his stray ideas to someone besides Agafea Mihalovna. With her indeed he not infrequently fell into discussion upon physics, the theory of agriculture, and especially philosophy; philosophy was Agafea Mihalovna’s favorite subject.\n\nSpring was slow in unfolding. For the last few weeks it had been steadily fine frosty weather. In the daytime it thawed in the sun, but at night there were even seven degrees of frost. There was such a frozen surface on the snow that they drove the wagons anywhere off the roads. Easter came in the snow. Then all of a sudden, on Easter Monday, a warm wind sprang up, storm clouds swooped down, and for three days and three nights the warm, driving rain fell in streams. On Thursday the wind dropped, and a thick gray fog brooded over the land as though hiding the mysteries of the transformations that were being wrought in nature. Behind the fog there was the flowing of water, the cracking and floating of ice, the swift rush of turbid, foaming torrents; and on the following Monday, in the evening, the fog parted, the storm clouds split up into little curling crests of cloud, the sky cleared, and the real spring had come. In the morning the sun rose brilliant and quickly wore away the thin layer of ice that covered the water, and all the warm air was quivering with the steam that rose up from the quickened earth. The old grass looked greener, and the young grass thrust up its tiny blades; the buds of the guelder-rose and of the currant and the sticky birch-buds were swollen with sap, and an exploring bee was humming about the golden blossoms that studded the willow. Larks trilled unseen above the velvety green fields and the ice-covered stubble-land; peewits wailed over the low lands and marshes flooded by the pools; cranes and wild geese flew high across the sky uttering their spring calls. The cattle, bald in patches where the new hair had not grown yet, lowed in the pastures; the bowlegged lambs frisked round their bleating mothers. Nimble children ran about the drying paths, covered with the prints of bare feet. There was a merry chatter of peasant women over their linen at the pond, and the ring of axes in the yard, where the peasants were repairing ploughs and harrows. The real spring had come.\n\n# Chapter 13\n\nLevin put on his big boots, and, for the first time, a cloth jacket, instead of his fur cloak, and went out to look after his farm, stepping over streams of water that flashed in the sunshine and dazzled his eyes, and treading one minute on ice and the next into sticky mud.\n\nSpring is the time of plans and projects. And, as he came out into the farmyard, Levin, like a tree in spring that knows not what form will be taken by the young shoots and twigs imprisoned in its swelling buds, hardly knew what undertakings he was going to begin upon now in the farm work that was so dear to him. But he felt that he was full of the most splendid plans and projects. First of all he went to the cattle. The cows had been let out into their paddock, and their smooth sides were already shining with their new, sleek, spring coats; they basked in the sunshine and lowed to go to the meadow. Levin gazed admiringly at the cows he knew so intimately to the minutest detail of their condition, and gave orders for them to be driven out into the meadow, and the calves to be let into the paddock. The herdsman ran gaily to get ready for the meadow. The cowherd girls, picking up their petticoats, ran splashing through the mud with bare legs, still white, not yet brown from the sun, waving brush wood in their hands, chasing the calves that frolicked in the mirth of spring.\n\nAfter admiring the young ones of that year, who were particularly fine—the early calves were the size of a peasant’s cow, and Pava’s daughter, at three months old, was as big as a yearling—Levin gave orders for a trough to be brought out and for them to be fed in the paddock. But it appeared that as the paddock had not been used during the winter, the hurdles made in the autumn for it were broken. He sent for the carpenter, who, according to his orders, ought to have been at work at the thrashing machine. But it appeared that the carpenter was repairing the harrows, which ought to have been repaired before Lent. This was very annoying to Levin. It was annoying to come upon that everlasting slovenliness in the farm work against which he had been striving with all his might for so many years. The hurdles, as he ascertained, being not wanted in winter, had been carried to the cart-horses’ stable; and there broken, as they were of light construction, only meant for feeding calves. Moreover, it was apparent also that the harrows and all the agricultural implements, which he had directed to be looked over and repaired in the winter, for which very purpose he had hired three carpenters, had not been put into repair, and the harrows were being repaired when they ought to have been harrowing the field. Levin sent for his bailiff, but immediately went off himself to look for him. The bailiff, beaming all over, like everyone that day, in a sheepskin bordered with astrachan, came out of the barn, twisting a bit of straw in his hands.\n\n“Why isn’t the carpenter at the thrashing machine?”\n\n“Oh, I meant to tell you yesterday, the harrows want repairing. Here it’s time they got to work in the fields.”\n\n“But what were they doing in the winter, then?”\n\n“But what did you want the carpenter for?”\n\n“Where are the hurdles for the calves’ paddock?”\n\n“I ordered them to be got ready. What would you have with those peasants!” said the bailiff, with a wave of his hand.\n\n“It’s not those peasants but this bailiff!” said Levin, getting angry. “Why, what do I keep you for?” he cried. But, bethinking himself that this would not help matters, he stopped short in the middle of a sentence, and merely sighed. “Well, what do you say? Can sowing begin?” he asked, after a pause.\n\n“Behind Turkin tomorrow or the next day they might begin.”\n\n“And the clover?”\n\n“I’ve sent Vassily and Mishka; they’re sowing. Only I don’t know if they’ll manage to get through; it’s so slushy.”\n\n“How many acres?”\n\n“Why not sow all?” cried Levin.\n\nThat they were only sowing the clover on fifteen acres, not on all the forty-five, was still more annoying to him. Clover, as he knew, both from books and from his own experience, never did well except when it was sown as early as possible, almost in the snow. And yet Levin could never get this done.\n\n“There’s no one to send. What would you have with such a set of peasants? Three haven’t turned up. And there’s Semyon....”\n\n“Well, you should have taken some men from the thatching.”\n\n“And so I have, as it is.”\n\n“Where are the peasants, then?”\n\n“Five are making compôte” (which meant compost), “four are shifting the oats for fear of a touch of mildew, Konstantin Dmitrievitch.”\n\nLevin knew very well that “a touch of mildew” meant that his English seed oats were already ruined. Again they had not done as he had ordered.\n\n“Why, but I told you during Lent to put in pipes,” he cried.\n\n“Don’t put yourself out; we shall get it all done in time.”\n\nLevin waved his hand angrily, went into the granary to glance at the oats, and then to the stable. The oats were not yet spoiled. But the peasants were carrying the oats in spades when they might simply let them slide down into the lower granary; and arranging for this to be done, and taking two workmen from there for sowing clover, Levin got over his vexation with the bailiff. Indeed, it was such a lovely day that one could not be angry.\n\n“Ignat!” he called to the coachman, who, with his sleeves tucked up, was washing the carriage wheels, “saddle me....”\n\n“Well, let it be Kolpik.”\n\n“Yes, sir.”\n\nWhile they were saddling his horse, Levin again called up the bailiff, who was hanging about in sight, to make it up with him, and began talking to him about the spring operations before them, and his plans for the farm.\n\nThe wagons were to begin carting manure earlier, so as to get all done before the early mowing. And the ploughing of the further land to go on without a break so as to let it ripen lying fallow. And the mowing to be all done by hired labor, not on half-profits. The bailiff listened attentively, and obviously made an effort to approve of his employer’s projects. But still he had that look Levin knew so well that always irritated him, a look of hopelessness and despondency. That look said: “That’s all very well, but as God wills.”\n\nNothing mortified Levin so much as that tone. But it was the tone common to all the bailiffs he had ever had. They had all taken up that attitude to his plans, and so now he was not angered by it, but mortified, and felt all the more roused to struggle against this, as it seemed, elemental force continually ranged against him, for which he could find no other expression than “as God wills.”\n\n“If we can manage it, Konstantin Dmitrievitch,” said the bailiff.\n\n“Why ever shouldn’t you manage it?”\n\n“We positively must have another fifteen laborers. And they don’t turn up. There were some here today asking seventy roubles for the summer.”\n\nLevin was silent. Again he was brought face to face with that opposing force. He knew that however much they tried, they could not hire more than forty—thirty-seven perhaps or thirty-eight—laborers for a reasonable sum. Some forty had been taken on, and there were no more. But still he could not help struggling against it.\n\n“Send to Sury, to Tchefirovka; if they don’t come we must look for them.”\n\n“Oh, I’ll send, to be sure,” said Vassily Fedorovitch despondently. “But there are the horses, too, they’re not good for much.”\n\n“We’ll get some more. I know, of course,” Levin added laughing, “you always want to do with as little and as poor quality as possible; but this year I’m not going to let you have things your own way. I’ll see to everything myself.”\n\n“Why, I don’t think you take much rest as it is. It cheers us up to work under the master’s eye....”\n\n“So they’re sowing clover behind the Birch Dale? I’ll go and have a look at them,” he said, getting on to the little bay cob, Kolpik, who was led up by the coachman.\n\n“You can’t get across the streams, Konstantin Dmitrievitch,” the coachman shouted.\n\n“All right, I’ll go by the forest.”\n\nAnd Levin rode through the slush of the farmyard to the gate and out into the open country, his good little horse, after his long inactivity, stepping out gallantly, snorting over the pools, and asking, as it were, for guidance. If Levin had felt happy before in the cattle pens and farmyard, he felt happier yet in the open country. Swaying rhythmically with the ambling paces of his good little cob, drinking in the warm yet fresh scent of the snow and the air, as he rode through his forest over the crumbling, wasted snow, still left in parts, and covered with dissolving tracks, he rejoiced over every tree, with the moss reviving on its bark and the buds swelling on its shoots. When he came out of the forest, in the immense plain before him, his grass fields stretched in an unbroken carpet of green, without one bare place or swamp, only spotted here and there in the hollows with patches of melting snow. He was not put out of temper even by the sight of the peasants’ horses and colts trampling down his young grass (he told a peasant he met to drive them out), nor by the sarcastic and stupid reply of the peasant Ipat, whom he met on the way, and asked, “Well, Ipat, shall we soon be sowing?” “We must get the ploughing done first, Konstantin Dmitrievitch,” answered Ipat. The further he rode, the happier he became, and plans for the land rose to his mind each better than the last; to plant all his fields with hedges along the southern borders, so that the snow should not lie under them; to divide them up into six fields of arable and three of pasture and hay; to build a cattle yard at the further end of the estate, and to dig a pond and to construct movable pens for the cattle as a means of manuring the land. And then eight hundred acres of wheat, three hundred of potatoes, and four hundred of clover, and not one acre exhausted.\n\nAbsorbed in such dreams, carefully keeping his horse by the hedges, so as not to trample his young crops, he rode up to the laborers who had been sent to sow clover. A cart with the seed in it was standing, not at the edge, but in the middle of the crop, and the winter corn had been torn up by the wheels and trampled by the horse. Both the laborers were sitting in the hedge, probably smoking a pipe together. The earth in the cart, with which the seed was mixed, was not crushed to powder, but crusted together or adhering in clods. Seeing the master, the laborer, Vassily, went towards the cart, while Mishka set to work sowing. This was not as it should be, but with the laborers Levin seldom lost his temper. When Vassily came up, Levin told him to lead the horse to the hedge.\n\n“It’s all right, sir, it’ll spring up again,” responded Vassily.\n\n“Please don’t argue,” said Levin, “but do as you’re told.”\n\n“Yes, sir,” answered Vassily, and he took the horse’s head. “What a sowing, Konstantin Dmitrievitch,” he said, hesitating; “first rate. Only it’s a work to get about! You drag a ton of earth on your shoes.”\n\n“Why is it you have earth that’s not sifted?” said Levin.\n\n“Well, we crumble it up,” answered Vassily, taking up some seed and rolling the earth in his palms.\n\nVassily was not to blame for their having filled up his cart with unsifted earth, but still it was annoying.\n\nLevin had more than once already tried a way he knew for stifling his anger, and turning all that seemed dark right again, and he tried that way now. He watched how Mishka strode along, swinging the huge clods of earth that clung to each foot; and getting off his horse, he took the sieve from Vassily and started sowing himself.\n\n“Where did you stop?”\n\nVassily pointed to the mark with his foot, and Levin went forward as best he could, scattering the seed on the land. Walking was as difficult as on a bog, and by the time Levin had ended the row he was in a great heat, and he stopped and gave up the sieve to Vassily.\n\n“Well, master, when summer’s here, mind you don’t scold me for these rows,” said Vassily.\n\n“Eh?” said Levin cheerily, already feeling the effect of his method.\n\n“Why, you’ll see in the summer time. It’ll look different. Look you where I sowed last spring. How I did work at it! I do my best, Konstantin Dmitrievitch, d’ye see, as I would for my own father. I don’t like bad work myself, nor would I let another man do it. What’s good for the master’s good for us too. To look out yonder now,” said Vassily, pointing, “it does one’s heart good.”\n\n“It’s a lovely spring, Vassily.”\n\n“Why, it’s a spring such as the old men don’t remember the like of. I was up home; an old man up there has sown wheat too, about an acre of it. He was saying you wouldn’t know it from rye.”\n\n“Have you been sowing wheat long?”\n\n“Why, sir, it was you taught us the year before last. You gave me two measures. We sold about eight bushels and sowed a rood.”\n\n“Well, mind you crumble up the clods,” said Levin, going towards his horse, “and keep an eye on Mishka. And if there’s a good crop you shall have half a rouble for every acre.”\n\n“Humbly thankful. We are very well content, sir, as it is.”\n\nLevin got on his horse and rode towards the field where was last year’s clover, and the one which was ploughed ready for the spring corn.\n\nThe crop of clover coming up in the stubble was magnificent. It had survived everything, and stood up vividly green through the broken stalks of last year’s wheat. The horse sank in up to the pasterns, and he drew each hoof with a sucking sound out of the half-thawed ground. Over the ploughland riding was utterly impossible; the horse could only keep a foothold where there was ice, and in the thawing furrows he sank deep in at each step. The ploughland was in splendid condition; in a couple of days it would be fit for harrowing and sowing. Everything was capital, everything was cheering. Levin rode back across the streams, hoping the water would have gone down. And he did in fact get across, and startled two ducks. “There must be snipe too,” he thought, and just as he reached the turning homewards he met the forest keeper, who confirmed his theory about the snipe.\n\nLevin went home at a trot, so as to have time to eat his dinner and get his gun ready for the evening.\n\n# Chapter 14\n\nAs he rode up to the house in the happiest frame of mind, Levin heard the bell ring at the side of the principal entrance of the house.\n\n“Yes, that’s someone from the railway station,” he thought, “just the time to be here from the Moscow train ... Who could it be? What if it’s brother Nikolay? He did say: ‘Maybe I’ll go to the waters, or maybe I’ll come down to you.’” He felt dismayed and vexed for the first minute, that his brother Nikolay’s presence should come to disturb his happy mood of spring. But he felt ashamed of the feeling, and at once he opened, as it were, the arms of his soul, and with a softened feeling of joy and expectation, now he hoped with all his heart that it was his brother. He pricked up his horse, and riding out from behind the acacias he saw a hired three-horse sledge from the railway station, and a gentleman in a fur coat. It was not his brother. “Oh, if it were only some nice person one could talk to a little!” he thought.\n\n“Ah,” cried Levin joyfully, flinging up both his hands. “Here’s a delightful visitor! Ah, how glad I am to see you!” he shouted, recognizing Stepan Arkadyevitch.\n\n“I shall find out for certain whether she’s married, or when she’s going to be married,” he thought. And on that delicious spring day he felt that the thought of her did not hurt him at all.\n\n“Well, you didn’t expect me, eh?” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, getting out of the sledge, splashed with mud on the bridge of his nose, on his cheek, and on his eyebrows, but radiant with health and good spirits. “I’ve come to see you in the first place,” he said, embracing and kissing him, “to have some stand-shooting second, and to sell the forest at Ergushovo third.”\n\n“Delightful! What a spring we’re having! How ever did you get along in a sledge?”\n\n“In a cart it would have been worse still, Konstantin Dmitrievitch,” answered the driver, who knew him.\n\n“Well, I’m very, very glad to see you,” said Levin, with a genuine smile of childlike delight.\n\nLevin led his friend to the room set apart for visitors, where Stepan Arkadyevitch’s things were carried also—a bag, a gun in a case, a satchel for cigars. Leaving him there to wash and change his clothes, Levin went off to the counting house to speak about the ploughing and clover. Agafea Mihalovna, always very anxious for the credit of the house, met him in the hall with inquiries about dinner.\n\n“Do just as you like, only let it be as soon as possible,” he said, and went to the bailiff.\n\nWhen he came back, Stepan Arkadyevitch, washed and combed, came out of his room with a beaming smile, and they went upstairs together.\n\n“Well, I am glad I managed to get away to you! Now I shall understand what the mysterious business is that you are always absorbed in here. No, really, I envy you. What a house, how nice it all is! So bright, so cheerful!” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, forgetting that it was not always spring and fine weather like that day. “And your nurse is simply charming! A pretty maid in an apron might be even more agreeable, perhaps; but for your severe monastic style it does very well.”\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch told him many interesting pieces of news; especially interesting to Levin was the news that his brother, Sergey Ivanovitch, was intending to pay him a visit in the summer.\n\nNot one word did Stepan Arkadyevitch say in reference to Kitty and the Shtcherbatskys; he merely gave him greetings from his wife. Levin was grateful to him for his delicacy and was very glad of his visitor. As always happened with him during his solitude, a mass of ideas and feelings had been accumulating within him, which he could not communicate to those about him. And now he poured out upon Stepan Arkadyevitch his poetic joy in the spring, and his failures and plans for the land, and his thoughts and criticisms on the books he had been reading, and the idea of his own book, the basis of which really was, though he was unaware of it himself, a criticism of all the old books on agriculture. Stepan Arkadyevitch, always charming, understanding everything at the slightest reference, was particularly charming on this visit, and Levin noticed in him a special tenderness, as it were, and a new tone of respect that flattered him.\n\nThe efforts of Agafea Mihalovna and the cook, that the dinner should be particularly good, only ended in the two famished friends attacking the preliminary course, eating a great deal of bread and butter, salt goose and salted mushrooms, and in Levin’s finally ordering the soup to be served without the accompaniment of little pies, with which the cook had particularly meant to impress their visitor. But though Stepan Arkadyevitch was accustomed to very different dinners, he thought everything excellent: the herb brandy, and the bread, and the butter, and above all the salt goose and the mushrooms, and the nettle soup, and the chicken in white sauce, and the white Crimean wine—everything was superb and delicious.\n\n“Splendid, splendid!” he said, lighting a fat cigar after the roast. “I feel as if, coming to you, I had landed on a peaceful shore after the noise and jolting of a steamer. And so you maintain that the laborer himself is an element to be studied and to regulate the choice of methods in agriculture. Of course, I’m an ignorant outsider; but I should fancy theory and its application will have its influence on the laborer too.”\n\n“Yes, but wait a bit. I’m not talking of political economy, I’m talking of the science of agriculture. It ought to be like the natural sciences, and to observe given phenomena and the laborer in his economic, ethnographical....”\n\nAt that instant Agafea Mihalovna came in with jam.\n\n“Oh, Agafea Mihalovna,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, kissing the tips of his plump fingers, “what salt goose, what herb brandy!... What do you think, isn’t it time to start, Kostya?” he added.\n\nLevin looked out of the window at the sun sinking behind the bare tree-tops of the forest.\n\n“Yes, it’s time,” he said. “Kouzma, get ready the trap,” and he ran downstairs.\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch, going down, carefully took the canvas cover off his varnished gun case with his own hands, and opening it, began to get ready his expensive new-fashioned gun. Kouzma, who already scented a big tip, never left Stepan Arkadyevitch’s side, and put on him both his stockings and boots, a task which Stepan Arkadyevitch readily left him.\n\n“Kostya, give orders that if the merchant Ryabinin comes ... I told him to come today, he’s to be brought in and to wait for me....”\n\n“Why, do you mean to say you’re selling the forest to Ryabinin?”\n\n“Yes. Do you know him?”\n\n“To be sure I do. I have had to do business with him, ‘positively and conclusively.’”\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch laughed. “Positively and conclusively” were the merchant’s favorite words.\n\n“Yes, it’s wonderfully funny the way he talks. She knows where her master’s going!” he added, patting Laska, who hung about Levin, whining and licking his hands, his boots, and his gun.\n\nThe trap was already at the steps when they went out.\n\n“I told them to bring the trap round; or would you rather walk?”\n\n“No, we’d better drive,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, getting into the trap. He sat down, tucked the tiger-skin rug round him, and lighted a cigar. “How is it you don’t smoke? A cigar is a sort of thing, not exactly a pleasure, but the crown and outward sign of pleasure. Come, this is life! How splendid it is! This is how I should like to live!”\n\n“Why, who prevents you?” said Levin, smiling.\n\n“No, you’re a lucky man! You’ve got everything you like. You like horses—and you have them; dogs—you have them; shooting—you have it; farming—you have it.”\n\n“Perhaps because I rejoice in what I have, and don’t fret for what I haven’t,” said Levin, thinking of Kitty.\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch comprehended, looked at him, but said nothing.\n\nLevin was grateful to Oblonsky for noticing, with his never-failing tact, that he dreaded conversation about the Shtcherbatskys, and so saying nothing about them. But now Levin was longing to find out what was tormenting him so, yet he had not the courage to begin.\n\n“Come, tell me how things are going with you,” said Levin, bethinking himself that it was not nice of him to think only of himself.\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch’s eyes sparkled merrily.\n\n“You don’t admit, I know, that one can be fond of new rolls when one has had one’s rations of bread—to your mind it’s a crime; but I don’t count life as life without love,” he said, taking Levin’s question his own way. “What am I to do? I’m made that way. And really, one does so little harm to anyone, and gives oneself so much pleasure....”\n\n“What! is there something new, then?” queried Levin.\n\n“Yes, my boy, there is! There, do you see, you know the type of Ossian’s women.... Women, such as one sees in dreams.... Well, these women are sometimes to be met in reality ... and these women are terrible. Woman, don’t you know, is such a subject that however much you study it, it’s always perfectly new.”\n\n“Well, then, it would be better not to study it.”\n\n“No. Some mathematician has said that enjoyment lies in the search for truth, not in the finding it.”\n\nLevin listened in silence, and in spite of all the efforts he made, he could not in the least enter into the feelings of his friend and understand his sentiments and the charm of studying such women.\n\n# Chapter 15\n\nThe place fixed on for the stand-shooting was not far above a stream in a little aspen copse. On reaching the copse, Levin got out of the trap and led Oblonsky to a corner of a mossy, swampy glade, already quite free from snow. He went back himself to a double birch tree on the other side, and leaning his gun on the fork of a dead lower branch, he took off his full overcoat, fastened his belt again, and worked his arms to see if they were free.\n\nGray old Laska, who had followed them, sat down warily opposite him and pricked up her ears. The sun was setting behind a thick forest, and in the glow of sunset the birch trees, dotted about in the aspen copse, stood out clearly with their hanging twigs, and their buds swollen almost to bursting.\n\nFrom the thickest parts of the copse, where the snow still remained, came the faint sound of narrow winding threads of water running away. Tiny birds twittered, and now and then fluttered from tree to tree.\n\nIn the pauses of complete stillness there came the rustle of last year’s leaves, stirred by the thawing of the earth and the growth of the grass.\n\n“Imagine! One can hear and see the grass growing!” Levin said to himself, noticing a wet, slate-colored aspen leaf moving beside a blade of young grass. He stood, listened, and gazed sometimes down at the wet mossy ground, sometimes at Laska listening all alert, sometimes at the sea of bare tree tops that stretched on the slope below him, sometimes at the darkening sky, covered with white streaks of cloud.\n\nA hawk flew high over a forest far away with slow sweep of its wings; another flew with exactly the same motion in the same direction and vanished. The birds twittered more and more loudly and busily in the thicket. An owl hooted not far off, and Laska, starting, stepped cautiously a few steps forward, and putting her head on one side, began to listen intently. Beyond the stream was heard the cuckoo. Twice she uttered her usual cuckoo call, and then gave a hoarse, hurried call and broke down.\n\n“Imagine! the cuckoo already!” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, coming out from behind a bush.\n\n“Yes, I hear it,” answered Levin, reluctantly breaking the stillness with his voice, which sounded disagreeable to himself. “Now it’s coming!”\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch’s figure again went behind the bush, and Levin saw nothing but the bright flash of a match, followed by the red glow and blue smoke of a cigarette.\n\n“Tchk! tchk!” came the snapping sound of Stepan Arkadyevitch cocking his gun.\n\n“What’s that cry?” asked Oblonsky, drawing Levin’s attention to a prolonged cry, as though a colt were whinnying in a high voice, in play.\n\n“Oh, don’t you know it? That’s the hare. But enough talking! Listen, it’s flying!” almost shrieked Levin, cocking his gun.\n\nThey heard a shrill whistle in the distance, and in the exact time, so well known to the sportsman, two seconds later—another, a third, and after the third whistle the hoarse, guttural cry could be heard.\n\nLevin looked about him to right and to left, and there, just facing him against the dusky blue sky above the confused mass of tender shoots of the aspens, he saw the flying bird. It was flying straight towards him; the guttural cry, like the even tearing of some strong stuff, sounded close to his ear; the long beak and neck of the bird could be seen, and at the very instant when Levin was taking aim, behind the bush where Oblonsky stood, there was a flash of red lightning: the bird dropped like an arrow, and darted upwards again. Again came the red flash and the sound of a blow, and fluttering its wings as though trying to keep up in the air, the bird halted, stopped still an instant, and fell with a heavy splash on the slushy ground.\n\n“Can I have missed it?” shouted Stepan Arkadyevitch, who could not see for the smoke.\n\n“Here it is!” said Levin, pointing to Laska, who with one ear raised, wagging the end of her shaggy tail, came slowly back as though she would prolong the pleasure, and as it were smiling, brought the dead bird to her master. “Well, I’m glad you were successful,” said Levin, who, at the same time, had a sense of envy that he had not succeeded in shooting the snipe.\n\n“It was a bad shot from the right barrel,” responded Stepan Arkadyevitch, loading his gun. “Sh... it’s flying!”\n\nThe shrill whistles rapidly following one another were heard again. Two snipe, playing and chasing one another, and only whistling, not crying, flew straight at the very heads of the sportsmen. There was the report of four shots, and like swallows the snipe turned swift somersaults in the air and vanished from sight.\n\nThe stand-shooting was capital. Stepan Arkadyevitch shot two more birds and Levin two, of which one was not found. It began to get dark. Venus, bright and silvery, shone with her soft light low down in the west behind the birch trees, and high up in the east twinkled the red lights of Arcturus. Over his head Levin made out the stars of the Great Bear and lost them again. The snipe had ceased flying; but Levin resolved to stay a little longer, till Venus, which he saw below a branch of birch, should be above it, and the stars of the Great Bear should be perfectly plain. Venus had risen above the branch, and the ear of the Great Bear with its shaft was now all plainly visible against the dark blue sky, yet still he waited.\n\n“Isn’t it time to go home?” said Stepan Arkadyevitch.\n\nIt was quite still now in the copse, and not a bird was stirring.\n\n“Let’s stay a little while,” answered Levin.\n\n“As you like.”\n\nThey were standing now about fifteen paces from one another.\n\n“Stiva!” said Levin unexpectedly; “how is it you don’t tell me whether your sister-in-law’s married yet, or when she’s going to be?”\n\nLevin felt so resolute and serene that no answer, he fancied, could affect him. But he had never dreamed of what Stepan Arkadyevitch replied.\n\n“She’s never thought of being married, and isn’t thinking of it; but she’s very ill, and the doctors have sent her abroad. They’re positively afraid she may not live.”\n\n“What!” cried Levin. “Very ill? What is wrong with her? How has she...?”\n\nWhile they were saying this, Laska, with ears pricked up, was looking upwards at the sky, and reproachfully at them.\n\n“They have chosen a time to talk,” she was thinking. “It’s on the wing.... Here it is, yes, it is. They’ll miss it,” thought Laska.\n\nBut at that very instant both suddenly heard a shrill whistle which, as it were, smote on their ears, and both suddenly seized their guns and two flashes gleamed, and two bangs sounded at the very same instant. The snipe flying high above instantly folded its wings and fell into a thicket, bending down the delicate shoots.\n\n“Splendid! Together!” cried Levin, and he ran with Laska into the thicket to look for the snipe.\n\n“Oh, yes, what was it that was unpleasant?” he wondered. “Yes, Kitty’s ill.... Well, it can’t be helped; I’m very sorry,” he thought.\n\n“She’s found it! Isn’t she a clever thing?” he said, taking the warm bird from Laska’s mouth and packing it into the almost full game bag. “I’ve got it, Stiva!” he shouted.\n\n# Chapter 16\n\nOn the way home Levin asked all details of Kitty’s illness and the Shtcherbatskys’ plans, and though he would have been ashamed to admit it, he was pleased at what he heard. He was pleased that there was still hope, and still more pleased that she should be suffering who had made him suffer so much. But when Stepan Arkadyevitch began to speak of the causes of Kitty’s illness, and mentioned Vronsky’s name, Levin cut him short.\n\n“I have no right whatever to know family matters, and, to tell the truth, no interest in them either.”\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch smiled hardly perceptibly, catching the instantaneous change he knew so well in Levin’s face, which had become as gloomy as it had been bright a minute before.\n\n“Have you quite settled about the forest with Ryabinin?” asked Levin.\n\n“Yes, it’s settled. The price is magnificent; thirty-eight thousand. Eight straight away, and the rest in six years. I’ve been bothering about it for ever so long. No one would give more.”\n\n“Then you’ve as good as given away your forest for nothing,” said Levin gloomily.\n\n“How do you mean for nothing?” said Stepan Arkadyevitch with a good-humored smile, knowing that nothing would be right in Levin’s eyes now.\n\n“Because the forest is worth at least a hundred and fifty roubles the acre,” answered Levin.\n\n“Oh, these farmers!” said Stepan Arkadyevitch playfully. “Your tone of contempt for us poor townsfolk!... But when it comes to business, we do it better than anyone. I assure you I have reckoned it all out,” he said, “and the forest is fetching a very good price—so much so that I’m afraid of this fellow’s crying off, in fact. You know it’s not ‘timber,’” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, hoping by this distinction to convince Levin completely of the unfairness of his doubts. “And it won’t run to more than twenty-five yards of fagots per acre, and he’s giving me at the rate of seventy roubles the acre.”\n\nLevin smiled contemptuously. “I know,” he thought, “that fashion not only in him, but in all city people, who, after being twice in ten years in the country, pick up two or three phrases and use them in season and out of season, firmly persuaded that they know all about it. ‘Timber, run to so many yards the acre.’ He says those words without understanding them himself.”\n\n“I wouldn’t attempt to teach you what you write about in your office,” said he, “and if need arose, I should come to you to ask about it. But you’re so positive you know all the lore of the forest. It’s difficult. Have you counted the trees?”\n\n“How count the trees?” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, laughing, still trying to draw his friend out of his ill-temper. “Count the sands of the sea, number the stars. Some higher power might do it.”\n\n“Oh, well, the higher power of Ryabinin can. Not a single merchant ever buys a forest without counting the trees, unless they get it given them for nothing, as you’re doing now. I know your forest. I go there every year shooting, and your forest’s worth a hundred and fifty roubles an acre paid down, while he’s giving you sixty by installments. So that in fact you’re making him a present of thirty thousand.”\n\n“Come, don’t let your imagination run away with you,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch piteously. “Why was it none would give it, then?”\n\n“Why, because he has an understanding with the merchants; he’s bought them off. I’ve had to do with all of them; I know them. They’re not merchants, you know: they’re speculators. He wouldn’t look at a bargain that gave him ten, fifteen per cent. profit, but holds back to buy a rouble’s worth for twenty kopecks.”\n\n“Well, enough of it! You’re out of temper.”\n\n“Not the least,” said Levin gloomily, as they drove up to the house.\n\nAt the steps there stood a trap tightly covered with iron and leather, with a sleek horse tightly harnessed with broad collar-straps. In the trap sat the chubby, tightly belted clerk who served Ryabinin as coachman. Ryabinin himself was already in the house, and met the friends in the hall. Ryabinin was a tall, thinnish, middle-aged man, with mustache and a projecting clean-shaven chin, and prominent muddy-looking eyes. He was dressed in a long-skirted blue coat, with buttons below the waist at the back, and wore high boots wrinkled over the ankles and straight over the calf, with big galoshes drawn over them. He rubbed his face with his handkerchief, and wrapping round him his coat, which sat extremely well as it was, he greeted them with a smile, holding out his hand to Stepan Arkadyevitch, as though he wanted to catch something.\n\n“So here you are,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, giving him his hand. “That’s capital.”\n\n“I did not venture to disregard your excellency’s commands, though the road was extremely bad. I positively walked the whole way, but I am here at my time. Konstantin Dmitrievitch, my respects”; he turned to Levin, trying to seize his hand too. But Levin, scowling, made as though he did not notice his hand, and took out the snipe. “Your honors have been diverting yourselves with the chase? What kind of bird may it be, pray?” added Ryabinin, looking contemptuously at the snipe: “a great delicacy, I suppose.” And he shook his head disapprovingly, as though he had grave doubts whether this game were worth the candle.\n\n“Would you like to go into my study?” Levin said in French to Stepan Arkadyevitch, scowling morosely. “Go into my study; you can talk there.”\n\n“Quite so, where you please,” said Ryabinin with contemptuous dignity, as though wishing to make it felt that others might be in difficulties as to how to behave, but that he could never be in any difficulty about anything.\n\nOn entering the study Ryabinin looked about, as his habit was, as though seeking the holy picture, but when he had found it, he did not cross himself. He scanned the bookcases and bookshelves, and with the same dubious air with which he had regarded the snipe, he smiled contemptuously and shook his head disapprovingly, as though by no means willing to allow that this game were worth the candle.\n\n“Well, have you brought the money?” asked Oblonsky. “Sit down.”\n\n“Oh, don’t trouble about the money. I’ve come to see you to talk it over.”\n\n“What is there to talk over? But do sit down.”\n\n“I don’t mind if I do,” said Ryabinin, sitting down and leaning his elbows on the back of his chair in a position of the intensest discomfort to himself. “You must knock it down a bit, prince. It would be too bad. The money is ready conclusively to the last farthing. As to paying the money down, there’ll be no hitch there.”\n\nLevin, who had meanwhile been putting his gun away in the cupboard, was just going out of the door, but catching the merchant’s words, he stopped.\n\n“Why, you’ve got the forest for nothing as it is,” he said. “He came to me too late, or I’d have fixed the price for him.”\n\nRyabinin got up, and in silence, with a smile, he looked Levin down and up.\n\n“Very close about money is Konstantin Dmitrievitch,” he said with a smile, turning to Stepan Arkadyevitch; “there’s positively no dealing with him. I was bargaining for some wheat of him, and a pretty price I offered too.”\n\n“Why should I give you my goods for nothing? I didn’t pick it up on the ground, nor steal it either.”\n\n“Mercy on us! nowadays there’s no chance at all of stealing. With the open courts and everything done in style, nowadays there’s no question of stealing. We are just talking things over like gentlemen. His excellency’s asking too much for the forest. I can’t make both ends meet over it. I must ask for a little concession.”\n\n“But is the thing settled between you or not? If it’s settled, it’s useless haggling; but if it’s not,” said Levin, “I’ll buy the forest.”\n\nThe smile vanished at once from Ryabinin’s face. A hawklike, greedy, cruel expression was left upon it. With rapid, bony fingers he unbuttoned his coat, revealing a shirt, bronze waistcoat buttons, and a watch chain, and quickly pulled out a fat old pocketbook.\n\n“Here you are, the forest is mine,” he said, crossing himself quickly, and holding out his hand. “Take the money; it’s my forest. That’s Ryabinin’s way of doing business; he doesn’t haggle over every half-penny,” he added, scowling and waving the pocketbook.\n\n“I wouldn’t be in a hurry if I were you,” said Levin.\n\n“Come, really,” said Oblonsky in surprise. “I’ve given my word, you know.”\n\nLevin went out of the room, slamming the door. Ryabinin looked towards the door and shook his head with a smile.\n\n“It’s all youthfulness—positively nothing but boyishness. Why, I’m buying it, upon my honor, simply, believe me, for the glory of it, that Ryabinin, and no one else, should have bought the copse of Oblonsky. And as to the profits, why, I must make what God gives. In God’s name. If you would kindly sign the title-deed....”\n\nWithin an hour the merchant, stroking his big overcoat neatly down, and hooking up his jacket, with the agreement in his pocket, seated himself in his tightly covered trap, and drove homewards.\n\n“Ugh, these gentlefolks!” he said to the clerk. “They—they’re a nice lot!”\n\n“That’s so,” responded the clerk, handing him the reins and buttoning the leather apron. “But I can congratulate you on the purchase, Mihail Ignatitch?”\n\n“Well, well....”\n\n# Chapter 17\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch went upstairs with his pocket bulging with notes, which the merchant had paid him for three months in advance. The business of the forest was over, the money in his pocket; their shooting had been excellent, and Stepan Arkadyevitch was in the happiest frame of mind, and so he felt specially anxious to dissipate the ill-humor that had come upon Levin. He wanted to finish the day at supper as pleasantly as it had been begun.\n\nLevin certainly was out of humor, and in spite of all his desire to be affectionate and cordial to his charming visitor, he could not control his mood. The intoxication of the news that Kitty was not married had gradually begun to work upon him.\n\nKitty was not married, but ill, and ill from love for a man who had slighted her. This slight, as it were, rebounded upon him. Vronsky had slighted her, and she had slighted him, Levin. Consequently Vronsky had the right to despise Levin, and therefore he was his enemy. But all this Levin did not think out. He vaguely felt that there was something in it insulting to him, and he was not angry now at what had disturbed him, but he fell foul of everything that presented itself. The stupid sale of the forest, the fraud practiced upon Oblonsky and concluded in his house, exasperated him.\n\n“Well, finished?” he said, meeting Stepan Arkadyevitch upstairs. “Would you like supper?”\n\n“Well, I wouldn’t say no to it. What an appetite I get in the country! Wonderful! Why didn’t you offer Ryabinin something?”\n\n“Oh, damn him!”\n\n“Still, how you do treat him!” said Oblonsky. “You didn’t even shake hands with him. Why not shake hands with him?”\n\n“Because I don’t shake hands with a waiter, and a waiter’s a hundred times better than he is.”\n\n“What a reactionist you are, really! What about the amalgamation of classes?” said Oblonsky.\n\n“Anyone who likes amalgamating is welcome to it, but it sickens me.”\n\n“You’re a regular reactionist, I see.”\n\n“Really, I have never considered what I am. I am Konstantin Levin, and nothing else.”\n\n“And Konstantin Levin very much out of temper,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, smiling.\n\n“Yes, I am out of temper, and do you know why? Because—excuse me—of your stupid sale....”\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch frowned good-humoredly, like one who feels himself teased and attacked for no fault of his own.\n\n“Come, enough about it!” he said. “When did anybody ever sell anything without being told immediately after the sale, ‘It was worth much more’? But when one wants to sell, no one will give anything.... No, I see you’ve a grudge against that unlucky Ryabinin.”\n\n“Maybe I have. And do you know why? You’ll say again that I’m a reactionist, or some other terrible word; but all the same it does annoy and anger me to see on all sides the impoverishing of the nobility to which I belong, and, in spite of the amalgamation of classes, I’m glad to belong. And their impoverishment is not due to extravagance—that would be nothing; living in good style—that’s the proper thing for noblemen; it’s only the nobles who know how to do it. Now the peasants about us buy land, and I don’t mind that. The gentleman does nothing, while the peasant works and supplants the idle man. That’s as it ought to be. And I’m very glad for the peasant. But I do mind seeing the process of impoverishment from a sort of—I don’t know what to call it—innocence. Here a Polish speculator bought for half its value a magnificent estate from a young lady who lives in Nice. And there a merchant will get three acres of land, worth ten roubles, as security for the loan of one rouble. Here, for no kind of reason, you’ve made that rascal a present of thirty thousand roubles.”\n\n“Well, what should I have done? Counted every tree?”\n\n“Of course, they must be counted. You didn’t count them, but Ryabinin did. Ryabinin’s children will have means of livelihood and education, while yours maybe will not!”\n\n“Well, you must excuse me, but there’s something mean in this counting. We have our business and they have theirs, and they must make their profit. Anyway, the thing’s done, and there’s an end of it. And here come some poached eggs, my favorite dish. And Agafea Mihalovna will give us that marvelous herb-brandy....”\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch sat down at the table and began joking with Agafea Mihalovna, assuring her that it was long since he had tasted such a dinner and such a supper.\n\n“Well, you do praise it, anyway,” said Agafea Mihalovna, “but Konstantin Dmitrievitch, give him what you will—a crust of bread—he’ll eat it and walk away.”\n\nThough Levin tried to control himself, he was gloomy and silent. He wanted to put one question to Stepan Arkadyevitch, but he could not bring himself to the point, and could not find the words or the moment in which to put it. Stepan Arkadyevitch had gone down to his room, undressed, again washed, and attired in a nightshirt with goffered frills, he had got into bed, but Levin still lingered in his room, talking of various trifling matters, and not daring to ask what he wanted to know.\n\n“How wonderfully they make this soap,” he said gazing at a piece of soap he was handling, which Agafea Mihalovna had put ready for the visitor but Oblonsky had not used. “Only look; why, it’s a work of art.”\n\n“Yes, everything’s brought to such a pitch of perfection nowadays,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, with a moist and blissful yawn. “The theater, for instance, and the entertainments ... a—a—a!” he yawned. “The electric light everywhere ... a—a—a!”\n\n“Yes, the electric light,” said Levin. “Yes. Oh, and where’s Vronsky now?” he asked suddenly, laying down the soap.\n\n“Vronsky?” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, checking his yawn; “he’s in Petersburg. He left soon after you did, and he’s not once been in Moscow since. And do you know, Kostya, I’ll tell you the truth,” he went on, leaning his elbow on the table, and propping on his hand his handsome ruddy face, in which his moist, good-natured, sleepy eyes shone like stars. “It’s your own fault. You took fright at the sight of your rival. But, as I told you at the time, I couldn’t say which had the better chance. Why didn’t you fight it out? I told you at the time that....” He yawned inwardly, without opening his mouth.\n\n“Does he know, or doesn’t he, that I did make an offer?” Levin wondered, gazing at him. “Yes, there’s something humbugging, diplomatic in his face,” and feeling he was blushing, he looked Stepan Arkadyevitch straight in the face without speaking.\n\n“If there was anything on her side at the time, it was nothing but a superficial attraction,” pursued Oblonsky. “His being such a perfect aristocrat, don’t you know, and his future position in society, had an influence not with her, but with her mother.”\n\nLevin scowled. The humiliation of his rejection stung him to the heart, as though it were a fresh wound he had only just received. But he was at home, and the walls of home are a support.\n\n“Stay, stay,” he began, interrupting Oblonsky. “You talk of his being an aristocrat. But allow me to ask what it consists in, that aristocracy of Vronsky or of anybody else, beside which I can be looked down upon? You consider Vronsky an aristocrat, but I don’t. A man whose father crawled up from nothing at all by intrigue, and whose mother—God knows whom she wasn’t mixed up with.... No, excuse me, but I consider myself aristocratic, and people like me, who can point back in the past to three or four honorable generations of their family, of the highest degree of breeding (talent and intellect, of course that’s another matter), and have never curried favor with anyone, never depended on anyone for anything, like my father and my grandfather. And I know many such. You think it mean of me to count the trees in my forest, while you make Ryabinin a present of thirty thousand; but you get rents from your lands and I don’t know what, while I don’t and so I prize what’s come to me from my ancestors or been won by hard work.... We are aristocrats, and not those who can only exist by favor of the powerful of this world, and who can be bought for twopence halfpenny.”\n\n“Well, but whom are you attacking? I agree with you,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, sincerely and genially; though he was aware that in the class of those who could be bought for twopence halfpenny Levin was reckoning him too. Levin’s warmth gave him genuine pleasure. “Whom are you attacking? Though a good deal is not true that you say about Vronsky, but I won’t talk about that. I tell you straight out, if I were you, I should go back with me to Moscow, and....”\n\n“No; I don’t know whether you know it or not, but I don’t care. And I tell you—I did make an offer and was rejected, and Katerina Alexandrovna is nothing now to me but a painful and humiliating reminiscence.”\n\n“What ever for? What nonsense!”\n\n“But we won’t talk about it. Please forgive me, if I’ve been nasty,” said Levin. Now that he had opened his heart, he became as he had been in the morning. “You’re not angry with me, Stiva? Please don’t be angry,” he said, and smiling, he took his hand.\n\n“Of course not; not a bit, and no reason to be. I’m glad we’ve spoken openly. And do you know, stand-shooting in the morning is unusually good—why not go? I couldn’t sleep the night anyway, but I might go straight from shooting to the station.”\n\n“Capital.”\n\n# Chapter 18\n\nAlthough all Vronsky’s inner life was absorbed in his passion, his external life unalterably and inevitably followed along the old accustomed lines of his social and regimental ties and interests. The interests of his regiment took an important place in Vronsky’s life, both because he was fond of the regiment, and because the regiment was fond of him. They were not only fond of Vronsky in his regiment, they respected him too, and were proud of him; proud that this man, with his immense wealth, his brilliant education and abilities, and the path open before him to every kind of success, distinction, and ambition, had disregarded all that, and of all the interests of life had the interests of his regiment and his comrades nearest to his heart. Vronsky was aware of his comrades’ view of him, and in addition to his liking for the life, he felt bound to keep up that reputation.\n\nIt need not be said that he did not speak of his love to any of his comrades, nor did he betray his secret even in the wildest drinking bouts (though indeed he was never so drunk as to lose all control of himself). And he shut up any of his thoughtless comrades who attempted to allude to his connection. But in spite of that, his love was known to all the town; everyone guessed with more or less confidence at his relations with Madame Karenina. The majority of the younger men envied him for just what was the most irksome factor in his love—the exalted position of Karenin, and the consequent publicity of their connection in society.\n\nThe greater number of the young women, who envied Anna and had long been weary of hearing her called virtuous, rejoiced at the fulfillment of their predictions, and were only waiting for a decisive turn in public opinion to fall upon her with all the weight of their scorn. They were already making ready their handfuls of mud to fling at her when the right moment arrived. The greater number of the middle-aged people and certain great personages were displeased at the prospect of the impending scandal in society.\n\nVronsky’s mother, on hearing of his connection, was at first pleased at it, because nothing to her mind gave such a finishing touch to a brilliant young man as a liaison in the highest society; she was pleased, too, that Madame Karenina, who had so taken her fancy, and had talked so much of her son, was, after all, just like all other pretty and well-bred women,—at least according to the Countess Vronskaya’s ideas. But she had heard of late that her son had refused a position offered him of great importance to his career, simply in order to remain in the regiment, where he could be constantly seeing Madame Karenina. She learned that great personages were displeased with him on this account, and she changed her opinion. She was vexed, too, that from all she could learn of this connection it was not that brilliant, graceful, worldly liaison which she would have welcomed, but a sort of Wertherish, desperate passion, so she was told, which might well lead him into imprudence. She had not seen him since his abrupt departure from Moscow, and she sent her elder son to bid him come to see her.\n\nThis elder son, too, was displeased with his younger brother. He did not distinguish what sort of love his might be, big or little, passionate or passionless, lasting or passing (he kept a ballet girl himself, though he was the father of a family, so he was lenient in these matters), but he knew that this love affair was viewed with displeasure by those whom it was necessary to please, and therefore he did not approve of his brother’s conduct.\n\nBesides the service and society, Vronsky had another great interest—horses; he was passionately fond of horses.\n\nThat year races and a steeplechase had been arranged for the officers. Vronsky had put his name down, bought a thoroughbred English mare, and in spite of his love affair, he was looking forward to the races with intense, though reserved, excitement....\n\nThese two passions did not interfere with one another. On the contrary, he needed occupation and distraction quite apart from his love, so as to recruit and rest himself from the violent emotions that agitated him.\n\n# Chapter 19\n\nOn the day of the races at Krasnoe Selo, Vronsky had come earlier than usual to eat beefsteak in the common messroom of the regiment. He had no need to be strict with himself, as he had very quickly been brought down to the required light weight; but still he had to avoid gaining flesh, and so he eschewed farinaceous and sweet dishes. He sat with his coat unbuttoned over a white waistcoat, resting both elbows on the table, and while waiting for the steak he had ordered he looked at a French novel that lay open on his plate. He was only looking at the book to avoid conversation with the officers coming in and out; he was thinking.\n\nHe was thinking of Anna’s promise to see him that day after the races. But he had not seen her for three days, and as her husband had just returned from abroad, he did not know whether she would be able to meet him today or not, and he did not know how to find out. He had had his last interview with her at his cousin Betsy’s summer villa. He visited the Karenins’ summer villa as rarely as possible. Now he wanted to go there, and he pondered the question how to do it.\n\n“Of course I shall say Betsy has sent me to ask whether she’s coming to the races. Of course, I’ll go,” he decided, lifting his head from the book. And as he vividly pictured the happiness of seeing her, his face lighted up.\n\n“Send to my house, and tell them to have out the carriage and three horses as quick as they can,” he said to the servant, who handed him the steak on a hot silver dish, and moving the dish up he began eating.\n\nFrom the billiard room next door came the sound of balls knocking, of talk and laughter. Two officers appeared at the entrance-door: one, a young fellow, with a feeble, delicate face, who had lately joined the regiment from the Corps of Pages; the other, a plump, elderly officer, with a bracelet on his wrist, and little eyes, lost in fat.\n\nVronsky glanced at them, frowned, and looking down at his book as though he had not noticed them, he proceeded to eat and read at the same time.\n\n“What? Fortifying yourself for your work?” said the plump officer, sitting down beside him.\n\n“As you see,” responded Vronsky, knitting his brows, wiping his mouth, and not looking at the officer.\n\n“So you’re not afraid of getting fat?” said the latter, turning a chair round for the young officer.\n\n“What?” said Vronsky angrily, making a wry face of disgust, and showing his even teeth.\n\n“You’re not afraid of getting fat?”\n\n“Waiter, sherry!” said Vronsky, without replying, and moving the book to the other side of him, he went on reading.\n\nThe plump officer took up the list of wines and turned to the young officer.\n\n“You choose what we’re to drink,” he said, handing him the card, and looking at him.\n\n“Rhine wine, please,” said the young officer, stealing a timid glance at Vronsky, and trying to pull his scarcely visible mustache. Seeing that Vronsky did not turn round, the young officer got up.\n\n“Let’s go into the billiard room,” he said.\n\nThe plump officer rose submissively, and they moved towards the door.\n\nAt that moment there walked into the room the tall and well-built Captain Yashvin. Nodding with an air of lofty contempt to the two officers, he went up to Vronsky.\n\n“Ah! here he is!” he cried, bringing his big hand down heavily on his epaulet. Vronsky looked round angrily, but his face lighted up immediately with his characteristic expression of genial and manly serenity.\n\n“That’s it, Alexey,” said the captain, in his loud baritone. “You must just eat a mouthful, now, and drink only one tiny glass.”\n\n“Oh, I’m not hungry.”\n\n“There go the inseparables,” Yashvin dropped, glancing sarcastically at the two officers who were at that instant leaving the room. And he bent his long legs, swathed in tight riding breeches, and sat down in the chair, too low for him, so that his knees were cramped up in a sharp angle.\n\n“Why didn’t you turn up at the Red Theater yesterday? Numerova wasn’t at all bad. Where were you?”\n\n“I was late at the Tverskoys’,” said Vronsky.\n\n“Ah!” responded Yashvin.\n\nYashvin, a gambler and a rake, a man not merely without moral principles, but of immoral principles, Yashvin was Vronsky’s greatest friend in the regiment. Vronsky liked him both for his exceptional physical strength, which he showed for the most part by being able to drink like a fish, and do without sleep without being in the slightest degree affected by it; and for his great strength of character, which he showed in his relations with his comrades and superior officers, commanding both fear and respect, and also at cards, when he would play for tens of thousands and however much he might have drunk, always with such skill and decision that he was reckoned the best player in the English Club. Vronsky respected and liked Yashvin particularly because he felt Yashvin liked him, not for his name and his money, but for himself. And of all men he was the only one with whom Vronsky would have liked to speak of his love. He felt that Yashvin, in spite of his apparent contempt for every sort of feeling, was the only man who could, so he fancied, comprehend the intense passion which now filled his whole life. Moreover, he felt certain that Yashvin, as it was, took no delight in gossip and scandal, and interpreted his feeling rightly, that is to say, knew and believed that this passion was not a jest, not a pastime, but something more serious and important.\n\nVronsky had never spoken to him of his passion, but he was aware that he knew all about it, and that he put the right interpretation on it, and he was glad to see that in his eyes.\n\n“Ah! yes,” he said, to the announcement that Vronsky had been at the Tverskoys’; and his black eyes shining, he plucked at his left mustache, and began twisting it into his mouth, a bad habit he had.\n\n“Well, and what did you do yesterday? Win anything?” asked Vronsky.\n\n“Eight thousand. But three don’t count; he won’t pay up.”\n\n“Oh, then you can afford to lose over me,” said Vronsky, laughing. (Yashvin had bet heavily on Vronsky in the races.)\n\n“No chance of my losing. Mahotin’s the only one that’s risky.”\n\nAnd the conversation passed to forecasts of the coming race, the only thing Vronsky could think of just now.\n\n“Come along, I’ve finished,” said Vronsky, and getting up he went to the door. Yashvin got up too, stretching his long legs and his long back.\n\n“It’s too early for me to dine, but I must have a drink. I’ll come along directly. Hi, wine!” he shouted, in his rich voice, that always rang out so loudly at drill, and set the windows shaking now.\n\n“No, all right,” he shouted again immediately after. “You’re going home, so I’ll go with you.”\n\nAnd he walked out with Vronsky.\n\n# Chapter 20\n\nVronsky was staying in a roomy, clean, Finnish hut, divided into two by a partition. Petritsky lived with him in camp too. Petritsky was asleep when Vronsky and Yashvin came into the hut.\n\n“Get up, don’t go on sleeping,” said Yashvin, going behind the partition and giving Petritsky, who was lying with ruffled hair and with his nose in the pillow, a prod on the shoulder.\n\nPetritsky jumped up suddenly onto his knees and looked round.\n\n“Your brother’s been here,” he said to Vronsky. “He waked me up, damn him, and said he’d look in again.” And pulling up the rug he flung himself back on the pillow. “Oh, do shut up, Yashvin!” he said, getting furious with Yashvin, who was pulling the rug off him. “Shut up!” He turned over and opened his eyes. “You’d better tell me what to drink; such a nasty taste in my mouth, that....”\n\n“Brandy’s better than anything,” boomed Yashvin. “Tereshtchenko! brandy for your master and cucumbers,” he shouted, obviously taking pleasure in the sound of his own voice.\n\n“Brandy, do you think? Eh?” queried Petritsky, blinking and rubbing his eyes. “And you’ll drink something? All right then, we’ll have a drink together! Vronsky, have a drink?” said Petritsky, getting up and wrapping the tiger-skin rug round him. He went to the door of the partition wall, raised his hands, and hummed in French, “There was a king in Thule.” “Vronsky, will you have a drink?”\n\n“Go along,” said Vronsky, putting on the coat his valet handed to him.\n\n“Where are you off to?” asked Yashvin. “Oh, here are your three horses,” he added, seeing the carriage drive up.\n\n“To the stables, and I’ve got to see Bryansky, too, about the horses,” said Vronsky.\n\nVronsky had as a fact promised to call at Bryansky’s, some eight miles from Peterhof, and to bring him some money owing for some horses; and he hoped to have time to get that in too. But his comrades were at once aware that he was not only going there.\n\nPetritsky, still humming, winked and made a pout with his lips, as though he would say: “Oh, yes, we know your Bryansky.”\n\n“Mind you’re not late!” was Yashvin’s only comment; and to change the conversation: “How’s my roan? is he doing all right?” he inquired, looking out of the window at the middle one of the three horses, which he had sold Vronsky.\n\n“Stop!” cried Petritsky to Vronsky as he was just going out. “Your brother left a letter and a note for you. Wait a bit; where are they?”\n\nVronsky stopped.\n\n“Well, where are they?”\n\n“Where are they? That’s just the question!” said Petritsky solemnly, moving his forefinger upwards from his nose.\n\n“Come, tell me; this is silly!” said Vronsky smiling.\n\n“I have not lighted the fire. Here somewhere about.”\n\n“Come, enough fooling! Where is the letter?”\n\n“No, I’ve forgotten really. Or was it a dream? Wait a bit, wait a bit! But what’s the use of getting in a rage. If you’d drunk four bottles yesterday as I did you’d forget where you were lying. Wait a bit, I’ll remember!”\n\nPetritsky went behind the partition and lay down on his bed.\n\n“Wait a bit! This was how I was lying, and this was how he was standing. Yes—yes—yes.... Here it is!”—and Petritsky pulled a letter out from under the mattress, where he had hidden it.\n\nVronsky took the letter and his brother’s note. It was the letter he was expecting—from his mother, reproaching him for not having been to see her—and the note was from his brother to say that he must have a little talk with him. Vronsky knew that it was all about the same thing. “What business is it of theirs!” thought Vronsky, and crumpling up the letters he thrust them between the buttons of his coat so as to read them carefully on the road. In the porch of the hut he was met by two officers; one of his regiment and one of another.\n\nVronsky’s quarters were always a meeting place for all the officers.\n\n“Where are you off to?”\n\n“I must go to Peterhof.”\n\n“Has the mare come from Tsarskoe?”\n\n“Yes, but I’ve not seen her yet.”\n\n“They say Mahotin’s Gladiator’s lame.”\n\n“Nonsense! But however are you going to race in this mud?” said the other.\n\n“Here are my saviors!” cried Petritsky, seeing them come in. Before him stood the orderly with a tray of brandy and salted cucumbers. “Here’s Yashvin ordering me to drink a pick-me-up.”\n\n“Well, you did give it to us yesterday,” said one of those who had come in; “you didn’t let us get a wink of sleep all night.”\n\n“Oh, didn’t we make a pretty finish!” said Petritsky. “Volkov climbed onto the roof and began telling us how sad he was. I said: ‘Let’s have music, the funeral march!’ He fairly dropped asleep on the roof over the funeral march.”\n\n“Drink it up; you positively must drink the brandy, and then seltzer water and a lot of lemon,” said Yashvin, standing over Petritsky like a mother making a child take medicine, “and then a little champagne—just a small bottle.”\n\n“Come, there’s some sense in that. Stop a bit, Vronsky. We’ll all have a drink.”\n\n“No; good-bye all of you. I’m not going to drink today.”\n\n“Why, are you gaining weight? All right, then we must have it alone. Give us the seltzer water and lemon.”\n\n“Vronsky!” shouted someone when he was already outside.\n\n“Well?”\n\n“You’d better get your hair cut, it’ll weigh you down, especially at the top.”\n\nVronsky was in fact beginning, prematurely, to get a little bald. He laughed gaily, showing his even teeth, and pulling his cap over the thin place, went out and got into his carriage.\n\n“To the stables!” he said, and was just pulling out the letters to read them through, but he thought better of it, and put off reading them so as not to distract his attention before looking at the mare. “Later!”\n\n# Chapter 21\n\nThe temporary stable, a wooden shed, had been put up close to the race course, and there his mare was to have been taken the previous day. He had not yet seen her there.\n\nDuring the last few days he had not ridden her out for exercise himself, but had put her in the charge of the trainer, and so now he positively did not know in what condition his mare had arrived yesterday and was today. He had scarcely got out of his carriage when his groom, the so-called “stable boy,” recognizing the carriage some way off, called the trainer. A dry-looking Englishman, in high boots and a short jacket, clean-shaven, except for a tuft below his chin, came to meet him, walking with the uncouth gait of jockey, turning his elbows out and swaying from side to side.\n\n“Well, how’s Frou-Frou?” Vronsky asked in English.\n\n“All right, sir,” the Englishman’s voice responded somewhere in the inside of his throat. “Better not go in,” he added, touching his hat. “I’ve put a muzzle on her, and the mare’s fidgety. Better not go in, it’ll excite the mare.”\n\n“No, I’m going in. I want to look at her.”\n\n“Come along, then,” said the Englishman, frowning, and speaking with his mouth shut, and, with swinging elbows, he went on in front with his disjointed gait.\n\nThey went into the little yard in front of the shed. A stable boy, spruce and smart in his holiday attire, met them with a broom in his hand, and followed them. In the shed there were five horses in their separate stalls, and Vronsky knew that his chief rival, Gladiator, a very tall chestnut horse, had been brought there, and must be standing among them. Even more than his mare, Vronsky longed to see Gladiator, whom he had never seen. But he knew that by the etiquette of the race course it was not merely impossible for him to see the horse, but improper even to ask questions about him. Just as he was passing along the passage, the boy opened the door into the second horse-box on the left, and Vronsky caught a glimpse of a big chestnut horse with white legs. He knew that this was Gladiator, but, with the feeling of a man turning away from the sight of another man’s open letter, he turned round and went into Frou-Frou’s stall.\n\n“The horse is here belonging to Mak... Mak... I never can say the name,” said the Englishman, over his shoulder, pointing his big finger and dirty nail towards Gladiator’s stall.\n\n“Mahotin? Yes, he’s my most serious rival,” said Vronsky.\n\n“If you were riding him,” said the Englishman, “I’d bet on you.”\n\n“Frou-Frou’s more nervous; he’s stronger,” said Vronsky, smiling at the compliment to his riding.\n\n“In a steeplechase it all depends on riding and on pluck,” said the Englishman.\n\nOf pluck—that is, energy and courage—Vronsky did not merely feel that he had enough; what was of far more importance, he was firmly convinced that no one in the world could have more of this “pluck” than he had.\n\n“Don’t you think I want more thinning down?”\n\n“Oh, no,” answered the Englishman. “Please, don’t speak loud. The mare’s fidgety,” he added, nodding towards the horse-box, before which they were standing, and from which came the sound of restless stamping in the straw.\n\nHe opened the door, and Vronsky went into the horse-box, dimly lighted by one little window. In the horse-box stood a dark bay mare, with a muzzle on, picking at the fresh straw with her hoofs. Looking round him in the twilight of the horse-box, Vronsky unconsciously took in once more in a comprehensive glance all the points of his favorite mare. Frou-Frou was a beast of medium size, not altogether free from reproach, from a breeder’s point of view. She was small-boned all over; though her chest was extremely prominent in front, it was narrow. Her hind-quarters were a little drooping, and in her fore-legs, and still more in her hind-legs, there was a noticeable curvature. The muscles of both hind- and fore-legs were not very thick; but across her shoulders the mare was exceptionally broad, a peculiarity specially striking now that she was lean from training. The bones of her legs below the knees looked no thicker than a finger from in front, but were extraordinarily thick seen from the side. She looked altogether, except across the shoulders, as it were, pinched in at the sides and pressed out in depth. But she had in the highest degree the quality that makes all defects forgotten: that quality was blood, the blood that tells, as the English expression has it. The muscles stood up sharply under the network of sinews, covered with the delicate, mobile skin, soft as satin, and they were hard as bone. Her clean-cut head, with prominent, bright, spirited eyes, broadened out at the open nostrils, that showed the red blood in the cartilage within. About all her figure, and especially her head, there was a certain expression of energy, and, at the same time, of softness. She was one of those creatures which seem only not to speak because the mechanism of their mouth does not allow them to.\n\nTo Vronsky, at any rate, it seemed that she understood all he felt at that moment, looking at her.\n\nDirectly Vronsky went towards her, she drew in a deep breath, and, turning back her prominent eye till the white looked bloodshot, she started at the approaching figures from the opposite side, shaking her muzzle, and shifting lightly from one leg to the other.\n\n“There, you see how fidgety she is,” said the Englishman.\n\n“There, darling! There!” said Vronsky, going up to the mare and speaking soothingly to her.\n\nBut the nearer he came, the more excited she grew. Only when he stood by her head, she was suddenly quieter, while the muscles quivered under her soft, delicate coat. Vronsky patted her strong neck, straightened over her sharp withers a stray lock of her mane that had fallen on the other side, and moved his face near her dilated nostrils, transparent as a bat’s wing. She drew a loud breath and snorted out through her tense nostrils, started, pricked up her sharp ear, and put out her strong, black lip towards Vronsky, as though she would nip hold of his sleeve. But remembering the muzzle, she shook it and again began restlessly stamping one after the other her shapely legs.\n\n“Quiet, darling, quiet!” he said, patting her again over her hind-quarters; and with a glad sense that his mare was in the best possible condition, he went out of the horse-box.\n\nThe mare’s excitement had infected Vronsky. He felt that his heart was throbbing, and that he, too, like the mare, longed to move, to bite; it was both dreadful and delicious.\n\n“Well, I rely on you, then,” he said to the Englishman; “half-past six on the ground.”\n\n“All right,” said the Englishman. “Oh, where are you going, my lord?” he asked suddenly, using the title “my lord,” which he had scarcely ever used before.\n\nVronsky in amazement raised his head, and stared, as he knew how to stare, not into the Englishman’s eyes, but at his forehead, astounded at the impertinence of his question. But realizing that in asking this the Englishman had been looking at him not as an employer, but as a jockey, he answered:\n\n“I’ve got to go to Bryansky’s; I shall be home within an hour.”\n\n“How often I’m asked that question today!” he said to himself, and he blushed, a thing which rarely happened to him. The Englishman looked gravely at him; and, as though he, too, knew where Vronsky was going, he added:\n\n“The great thing’s to keep quiet before a race,” said he; “don’t get out of temper or upset about anything.”\n\n“All right,” answered Vronsky, smiling; and jumping into his carriage, he told the man to drive to Peterhof.\n\nBefore he had driven many paces away, the dark clouds that had been threatening rain all day broke, and there was a heavy downpour of rain.\n\n“What a pity!” thought Vronsky, putting up the roof of the carriage. “It was muddy before, now it will be a perfect swamp.” As he sat in solitude in the closed carriage, he took out his mother’s letter and his brother’s note, and read them through.\n\nYes, it was the same thing over and over again. Everyone, his mother, his brother, everyone thought fit to interfere in the affairs of his heart. This interference aroused in him a feeling of angry hatred—a feeling he had rarely known before. “What business is it of theirs? Why does everybody feel called upon to concern himself about me? And why do they worry me so? Just because they see that this is something they can’t understand. If it were a common, vulgar, worldly intrigue, they would have left me alone. They feel that this is something different, that this is not a mere pastime, that this woman is dearer to me than life. And this is incomprehensible, and that’s why it annoys them. Whatever our destiny is or may be, we have made it ourselves, and we do not complain of it,” he said, in the word we linking himself with Anna. “No, they must needs teach us how to live. They haven’t an idea of what happiness is; they don’t know that without our love, for us there is neither happiness nor unhappiness—no life at all,” he thought.\n\nHe was angry with all of them for their interference just because he felt in his soul that they, all these people, were right. He felt that the love that bound him to Anna was not a momentary impulse, which would pass, as worldly intrigues do pass, leaving no other traces in the life of either but pleasant or unpleasant memories. He felt all the torture of his own and her position, all the difficulty there was for them, conspicuous as they were in the eye of all the world, in concealing their love, in lying and deceiving; and in lying, deceiving, feigning, and continually thinking of others, when the passion that united them was so intense that they were both oblivious of everything else but their love.\n\nHe vividly recalled all the constantly recurring instances of inevitable necessity for lying and deceit, which were so against his natural bent. He recalled particularly vividly the shame he had more than once detected in her at this necessity for lying and deceit. And he experienced the strange feeling that had sometimes come upon him since his secret love for Anna. This was a feeling of loathing for something—whether for Alexey Alexandrovitch, or for himself, or for the whole world, he could not have said. But he always drove away this strange feeling. Now, too, he shook it off and continued the thread of his thoughts.\n\n“Yes, she was unhappy before, but proud and at peace; and now she cannot be at peace and feel secure in her dignity, though she does not show it. Yes, we must put an end to it,” he decided.\n\nAnd for the first time the idea clearly presented itself that it was essential to put an end to this false position, and the sooner the better. “Throw up everything, she and I, and hide ourselves somewhere alone with our love,” he said to himself.\n\n# Chapter 22\n\nThe rain did not last long, and by the time Vronsky arrived, his shaft-horse trotting at full speed and dragging the trace-horses galloping through the mud, with their reins hanging loose, the sun had peeped out again, the roofs of the summer villas and the old limetrees in the gardens on both sides of the principal streets sparkled with wet brilliance, and from the twigs came a pleasant drip and from the roofs rushing streams of water. He thought no more of the shower spoiling the race course, but was rejoicing now that—thanks to the rain—he would be sure to find her at home and alone, as he knew that Alexey Alexandrovitch, who had lately returned from a foreign watering place, had not moved from Petersburg.\n\nHoping to find her alone, Vronsky alighted, as he always did, to avoid attracting attention, before crossing the bridge, and walked to the house. He did not go up the steps to the street door, but went into the court.\n\n“Has your master come?” he asked a gardener.\n\n“No, sir. The mistress is at home. But will you please go to the front door; there are servants there,” the gardener answered. “They’ll open the door.”\n\n“No, I’ll go in from the garden.”\n\nAnd feeling satisfied that she was alone, and wanting to take her by surprise, since he had not promised to be there today, and she would certainly not expect him to come before the races, he walked, holding his sword and stepping cautiously over the sandy path, bordered with flowers, to the terrace that looked out upon the garden. Vronsky forgot now all that he had thought on the way of the hardships and difficulties of their position. He thought of nothing but that he would see her directly, not in imagination, but living, all of her, as she was in reality. He was just going in, stepping on his whole foot so as not to creak, up the worn steps of the terrace, when he suddenly remembered what he always forgot, and what caused the most torturing side of his relations with her, her son with his questioning—hostile, as he fancied—eyes.\n\nThis boy was more often than anyone else a check upon their freedom. When he was present, both Vronsky and Anna did not merely avoid speaking of anything that they could not have repeated before everyone; they did not even allow themselves to refer by hints to anything the boy did not understand. They had made no agreement about this, it had settled itself. They would have felt it wounding themselves to deceive the child. In his presence they talked like acquaintances. But in spite of this caution, Vronsky often saw the child’s intent, bewildered glance fixed upon him, and a strange shyness, uncertainty, at one time friendliness, at another, coldness and reserve, in the boy’s manner to him; as though the child felt that between this man and his mother there existed some important bond, the significance of which he could not understand.\n\nAs a fact, the boy did feel that he could not understand this relation, and he tried painfully, and was not able to make clear to himself what feeling he ought to have for this man. With a child’s keen instinct for every manifestation of feeling, he saw distinctly that his father, his governess, his nurse,—all did not merely dislike Vronsky, but looked on him with horror and aversion, though they never said anything about him, while his mother looked on him as her greatest friend.\n\n“What does it mean? Who is he? How ought I to love him? If I don’t know, it’s my fault; either I’m stupid or a naughty boy,” thought the child. And this was what caused his dubious, inquiring, sometimes hostile, expression, and the shyness and uncertainty which Vronsky found so irksome. This child’s presence always and infallibly called up in Vronsky that strange feeling of inexplicable loathing which he had experienced of late. This child’s presence called up both in Vronsky and in Anna a feeling akin to the feeling of a sailor who sees by the compass that the direction in which he is swiftly moving is far from the right one, but that to arrest his motion is not in his power, that every instant is carrying him further and further away, and that to admit to himself his deviation from the right direction is the same as admitting his certain ruin.\n\nThis child, with his innocent outlook upon life, was the compass that showed them the point to which they had departed from what they knew, but did not want to know.\n\nThis time Seryozha was not at home, and she was completely alone. She was sitting on the terrace waiting for the return of her son, who had gone out for his walk and been caught in the rain. She had sent a manservant and a maid out to look for him. Dressed in a white gown, deeply embroidered, she was sitting in a corner of the terrace behind some flowers, and did not hear him. Bending her curly black head, she pressed her forehead against a cool watering pot that stood on the parapet, and both her lovely hands, with the rings he knew so well, clasped the pot. The beauty of her whole figure, her head, her neck, her hands, struck Vronsky every time as something new and unexpected. He stood still, gazing at her in ecstasy. But, directly he would have made a step to come nearer to her, she was aware of his presence, pushed away the watering pot, and turned her flushed face towards him.\n\n“What’s the matter? You are ill?” he said to her in French, going up to her. He would have run to her, but remembering that there might be spectators, he looked round towards the balcony door, and reddened a little, as he always reddened, feeling that he had to be afraid and be on his guard.\n\n“No, I’m quite well,” she said, getting up and pressing his outstretched hand tightly. “I did not expect ... thee.”\n\n“Mercy! what cold hands!” he said.\n\n“You startled me,” she said. “I’m alone, and expecting Seryozha; he’s out for a walk; they’ll come in from this side.”\n\nBut, in spite of her efforts to be calm, her lips were quivering.\n\n“Forgive me for coming, but I couldn’t pass the day without seeing you,” he went on, speaking French, as he always did to avoid using the stiff Russian plural form, so impossibly frigid between them, and the dangerously intimate singular.\n\n“Forgive you? I’m so glad!”\n\n“But you’re ill or worried,” he went on, not letting go her hands and bending over her. “What were you thinking of?”\n\n“Always the same thing,” she said, with a smile.\n\nShe spoke the truth. If ever at any moment she had been asked what she was thinking of, she could have answered truly: of the same thing, of her happiness and her unhappiness. She was thinking, just when he came upon her, of this: why was it, she wondered, that to others, to Betsy (she knew of her secret connection with Tushkevitch) it was all easy, while to her it was such torture? Today this thought gained special poignancy from certain other considerations. She asked him about the races. He answered her questions, and, seeing that she was agitated, trying to calm her, he began telling her in the simplest tone the details of his preparations for the races.\n\n“Tell him or not tell him?” she thought, looking into his quiet, affectionate eyes. “He is so happy, so absorbed in his races that he won’t understand as he ought, he won’t understand all the gravity of this fact to us.”\n\n“But you haven’t told me what you were thinking of when I came in,” he said, interrupting his narrative; “please tell me!”\n\nShe did not answer, and, bending her head a little, she looked inquiringly at him from under her brows, her eyes shining under their long lashes. Her hand shook as it played with a leaf she had picked. He saw it, and his face expressed that utter subjection, that slavish devotion, which had done so much to win her.\n\n“I see something has happened. Do you suppose I can be at peace, knowing you have a trouble I am not sharing? Tell me, for God’s sake,” he repeated imploringly.\n\n“Yes, I shan’t be able to forgive him if he does not realize all the gravity of it. Better not tell; why put him to the proof?” she thought, still staring at him in the same way, and feeling the hand that held the leaf was trembling more and more.\n\n“For God’s sake!” he repeated, taking her hand.\n\n“Shall I tell you?”\n\n“Yes, yes, yes....”\n\n“I’m with child,” she said, softly and deliberately. The leaf in her hand shook more violently, but she did not take her eyes off him, watching how he would take it. He turned white, would have said something, but stopped; he dropped her hand, and his head sank on his breast. “Yes, he realizes all the gravity of it,” she thought, and gratefully she pressed his hand.\n\nBut she was mistaken in thinking he realized the gravity of the fact as she, a woman, realized it. On hearing it, he felt come upon him with tenfold intensity that strange feeling of loathing of someone. But at the same time, he felt that the turning-point he had been longing for had come now; that it was impossible to go on concealing things from her husband, and it was inevitable in one way or another that they should soon put an end to their unnatural position. But, besides that, her emotion physically affected him in the same way. He looked at her with a look of submissive tenderness, kissed her hand, got up, and, in silence, paced up and down the terrace.\n\n“Yes,” he said, going up to her resolutely. “Neither you nor I have looked on our relations as a passing amusement, and now our fate is sealed. It is absolutely necessary to put an end”—he looked round as he spoke—“to the deception in which we are living.”\n\n“Put an end? How put an end, Alexey?” she said softly.\n\nShe was calmer now, and her face lighted up with a tender smile.\n\n“Leave your husband and make our life one.”\n\n“It is one as it is,” she answered, scarcely audibly.\n\n“Yes, but altogether; altogether.”\n\n“But how, Alexey, tell me how?” she said in melancholy mockery at the hopelessness of her own position. “Is there any way out of such a position? Am I not the wife of my husband?”\n\n“There is a way out of every position. We must take our line,” he said. “Anything’s better than the position in which you’re living. Of course, I see how you torture yourself over everything—the world and your son and your husband.”\n\n“Oh, not over my husband,” she said, with a quiet smile. “I don’t know him, I don’t think of him. He doesn’t exist.”\n\n“You’re not speaking sincerely. I know you. You worry about him too.”\n\n“Oh, he doesn’t even know,” she said, and suddenly a hot flush came over her face; her cheeks, her brow, her neck crimsoned, and tears of shame came into her eyes. “But we won’t talk of him.”\n\n# Chapter 23\n\nVronsky had several times already, though not so resolutely as now, tried to bring her to consider their position, and every time he had been confronted by the same superficiality and triviality with which she met his appeal now. It was as though there were something in this which she could not or would not face, as though directly she began to speak of this, she, the real Anna, retreated somehow into herself, and another strange and unaccountable woman came out, whom he did not love, and whom he feared, and who was in opposition to him. But today he was resolved to have it out.\n\n“Whether he knows or not,” said Vronsky, in his usual quiet and resolute tone, “that’s nothing to do with us. We cannot ... you cannot stay like this, especially now.”\n\n“What’s to be done, according to you?” she asked with the same frivolous irony. She who had so feared he would take her condition too lightly was now vexed with him for deducing from it the necessity of taking some step.\n\n“Tell him everything, and leave him.”\n\n“Very well, let us suppose I do that,” she said. “Do you know what the result of that would be? I can tell you it all beforehand,” and a wicked light gleamed in her eyes, that had been so soft a minute before. “‘Eh, you love another man, and have entered into criminal intrigues with him?’” (Mimicking her husband, she threw an emphasis on the word “criminal,” as Alexey Alexandrovitch did.) “‘I warned you of the results in the religious, the civil, and the domestic relation. You have not listened to me. Now I cannot let you disgrace my name,—’” “and my son,” she had meant to say, but about her son she could not jest,—“‘disgrace my name, and’—and more in the same style,” she added. “In general terms, he’ll say in his official manner, and with all distinctness and precision, that he cannot let me go, but will take all measures in his power to prevent scandal. And he will calmly and punctually act in accordance with his words. That’s what will happen. He’s not a man, but a machine, and a spiteful machine when he’s angry,” she added, recalling Alexey Alexandrovitch as she spoke, with all the peculiarities of his figure and manner of speaking, and reckoning against him every defect she could find in him, softening nothing for the great wrong she herself was doing him.\n\n“But, Anna,” said Vronsky, in a soft and persuasive voice, trying to soothe her, “we absolutely must, anyway, tell him, and then be guided by the line he takes.”\n\n“What, run away?”\n\n“And why not run away? I don’t see how we can keep on like this. And not for my sake—I see that you suffer.”\n\n“Yes, run away, and become your mistress,” she said angrily.\n\n“Anna,” he said, with reproachful tenderness.\n\n“Yes,” she went on, “become your mistress, and complete the ruin of....”\n\nAgain she would have said “my son,” but she could not utter that word.\n\nVronsky could not understand how she, with her strong and truthful nature, could endure this state of deceit, and not long to get out of it. But he did not suspect that the chief cause of it was the word—son, which she could not bring herself to pronounce. When she thought of her son, and his future attitude to his mother, who had abandoned his father, she felt such terror at what she had done, that she could not face it; but, like a woman, could only try to comfort herself with lying assurances that everything would remain as it always had been, and that it was possible to forget the fearful question of how it would be with her son.\n\n“I beg you, I entreat you,” she said suddenly, taking his hand, and speaking in quite a different tone, sincere and tender, “never speak to me of that!”\n\n“But, Anna....”\n\n“Never. Leave it to me. I know all the baseness, all the horror of my position; but it’s not so easy to arrange as you think. And leave it to me, and do what I say. Never speak to me of it. Do you promise me?... No, no, promise!...”\n\n“I promise everything, but I can’t be at peace, especially after what you have told me. I can’t be at peace, when you can’t be at peace....”\n\n“I?” she repeated. “Yes, I am worried sometimes; but that will pass, if you will never talk about this. When you talk about it—it’s only then it worries me.”\n\n“I don’t understand,” he said.\n\n“I know,” she interrupted him, “how hard it is for your truthful nature to lie, and I grieve for you. I often think that you have ruined your whole life for me.”\n\n“I was just thinking the very same thing,” he said; “how could you sacrifice everything for my sake? I can’t forgive myself that you’re unhappy!”\n\n“I unhappy?” she said, coming closer to him, and looking at him with an ecstatic smile of love. “I am like a hungry man who has been given food. He may be cold, and dressed in rags, and ashamed, but he is not unhappy. I unhappy? No, this is my unhappiness....”\n\nShe could hear the sound of her son’s voice coming towards them, and glancing swiftly round the terrace, she got up impulsively. Her eyes glowed with the fire he knew so well; with a rapid movement she raised her lovely hands, covered with rings, took his head, looked a long look into his face, and, putting up her face with smiling, parted lips, swiftly kissed his mouth and both eyes, and pushed him away. She would have gone, but he held her back.\n\n“When?” he murmured in a whisper, gazing in ecstasy at her.\n\n“Tonight, at one o’clock,” she whispered, and, with a heavy sigh, she walked with her light, swift step to meet her son.\n\nSeryozha had been caught by the rain in the big garden, and he and his nurse had taken shelter in an arbor.\n\n“Well, au revoir,” she said to Vronsky. “I must soon be getting ready for the races. Betsy promised to fetch me.”\n\nVronsky, looking at his watch, went away hurriedly.\n\n# Chapter 24\n\nWhen Vronsky looked at his watch on the Karenins’ balcony, he was so greatly agitated and lost in his thoughts that he saw the figures on the watch’s face, but could not take in what time it was. He came out on to the highroad and walked, picking his way carefully through the mud, to his carriage. He was so completely absorbed in his feeling for Anna, that he did not even think what o’clock it was, and whether he had time to go to Bryansky’s. He had left him, as often happens, only the external faculty of memory, that points out each step one has to take, one after the other. He went up to his coachman, who was dozing on the box in the shadow, already lengthening, of a thick limetree; he admired the shifting clouds of midges circling over the hot horses, and, waking the coachman, he jumped into the carriage, and told him to drive to Bryansky’s. It was only after driving nearly five miles that he had sufficiently recovered himself to look at his watch, and realize that it was half-past five, and he was late.\n\nThere were several races fixed for that day: the Mounted Guards’ race, then the officers’ mile-and-a-half race, then the three-mile race, and then the race for which he was entered. He could still be in time for his race, but if he went to Bryansky’s he could only just be in time, and he would arrive when the whole of the court would be in their places. That would be a pity. But he had promised Bryansky to come, and so he decided to drive on, telling the coachman not to spare the horses.\n\nHe reached Bryansky’s, spent five minutes there, and galloped back. This rapid drive calmed him. All that was painful in his relations with Anna, all the feeling of indefiniteness left by their conversation, had slipped out of his mind. He was thinking now with pleasure and excitement of the race, of his being anyhow, in time, and now and then the thought of the blissful interview awaiting him that night flashed across his imagination like a flaming light.\n\nThe excitement of the approaching race gained upon him as he drove further and further into the atmosphere of the races, overtaking carriages driving up from the summer villas or out of Petersburg.\n\nAt his quarters no one was left at home; all were at the races, and his valet was looking out for him at the gate. While he was changing his clothes, his valet told him that the second race had begun already, that a lot of gentlemen had been to ask for him, and a boy had twice run up from the stables. Dressing without hurry (he never hurried himself, and never lost his self-possession), Vronsky drove to the sheds. From the sheds he could see a perfect sea of carriages, and people on foot, soldiers surrounding the race course, and pavilions swarming with people. The second race was apparently going on, for just as he went into the sheds he heard a bell ringing. Going towards the stable, he met the white-legged chestnut, Mahotin’s Gladiator, being led to the race-course in a blue forage horsecloth, with what looked like huge ears edged with blue.\n\n“Where’s Cord?” he asked the stable-boy.\n\n“In the stable, putting on the saddle.”\n\nIn the open horse-box stood Frou-Frou, saddled ready. They were just going to lead her out.\n\n“I’m not too late?”\n\n“All right! All right!” said the Englishman; “don’t upset yourself!”\n\nVronsky once more took in in one glance the exquisite lines of his favorite mare; who was quivering all over, and with an effort he tore himself from the sight of her, and went out of the stable. He went towards the pavilions at the most favorable moment for escaping attention. The mile-and-a-half race was just finishing, and all eyes were fixed on the horse-guard in front and the light hussar behind, urging their horses on with a last effort close to the winning post. From the center and outside of the ring all were crowding to the winning post, and a group of soldiers and officers of the horse-guards were shouting loudly their delight at the expected triumph of their officer and comrade. Vronsky moved into the middle of the crowd unnoticed, almost at the very moment when the bell rang at the finish of the race, and the tall, mudspattered horse-guard who came in first, bending over the saddle, let go the reins of his panting gray horse that looked dark with sweat.\n\nThe horse, stiffening out its legs, with an effort stopped its rapid course, and the officer of the horse-guards looked round him like a man waking up from a heavy sleep, and just managed to smile. A crowd of friends and outsiders pressed round him.\n\nVronsky intentionally avoided that select crowd of the upper world, which was moving and talking with discreet freedom before the pavilions. He knew that Madame Karenina was there, and Betsy, and his brother’s wife, and he purposely did not go near them for fear of something distracting his attention. But he was continually met and stopped by acquaintances, who told him about the previous races, and kept asking him why he was so late.\n\nAt the time when the racers had to go to the pavilion to receive the prizes, and all attention was directed to that point, Vronsky’s elder brother, Alexander, a colonel with heavy fringed epaulets, came up to him. He was not tall, though as broadly built as Alexey, and handsomer and rosier than he; he had a red nose, and an open, drunken-looking face.\n\n“Did you get my note?” he said. “There’s never any finding you.”\n\nAlexander Vronsky, in spite of the dissolute life, and in especial the drunken habits, for which he was notorious, was quite one of the court circle.\n\nNow, as he talked to his brother of a matter bound to be exceedingly disagreeable to him, knowing that the eyes of many people might be fixed upon him, he kept a smiling countenance, as though he were jesting with his brother about something of little moment.\n\n“I got it, and I really can’t make out what you are worrying yourself about,” said Alexey.\n\n“I’m worrying myself because the remark has just been made to me that you weren’t here, and that you were seen in Peterhof on Monday.”\n\n“There are matters which only concern those directly interested in them, and the matter you are so worried about is....”\n\n“Yes, but if so, you may as well cut the service....”\n\n“I beg you not to meddle, and that’s all I have to say.”\n\nAlexey Vronsky’s frowning face turned white, and his prominent lower jaw quivered, which happened rarely with him. Being a man of very warm heart, he was seldom angry; but when he was angry, and when his chin quivered, then, as Alexander Vronsky knew, he was dangerous. Alexander Vronsky smiled gaily.\n\n“I only wanted to give you Mother’s letter. Answer it, and don’t worry about anything just before the race. Bonne chance,” he added, smiling and he moved away from him. But after him another friendly greeting brought Vronsky to a standstill.\n\n“So you won’t recognize your friends! How are you, mon cher?” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, as conspicuously brilliant in the midst of all the Petersburg brilliance as he was in Moscow, his face rosy, and his whiskers sleek and glossy. “I came up yesterday, and I’m delighted that I shall see your triumph. When shall we meet?”\n\n“Come tomorrow to the messroom,” said Vronsky, and squeezing him by the sleeve of his coat, with apologies, he moved away to the center of the race course, where the horses were being led for the great steeplechase.\n\nThe horses who had run in the last race were being led home, steaming and exhausted, by the stable-boys, and one after another the fresh horses for the coming race made their appearance, for the most part English racers, wearing horsecloths, and looking with their drawn-up bellies like strange, huge birds. On the right was led in Frou-Frou, lean and beautiful, lifting up her elastic, rather long pasterns, as though moved by springs. Not far from her they were taking the rug off the lop-eared Gladiator. The strong, exquisite, perfectly correct lines of the stallion, with his superb hind-quarters and excessively short pasterns almost over his hoofs, attracted Vronsky’s attention in spite of himself. He would have gone up to his mare, but he was again detained by an acquaintance.\n\n“Oh, there’s Karenin!” said the acquaintance with whom he was chatting. “He’s looking for his wife, and she’s in the middle of the pavilion. Didn’t you see her?”\n\n“No,” answered Vronsky, and without even glancing round towards the pavilion where his friend was pointing out Madame Karenina, he went up to his mare.\n\nVronsky had not had time to look at the saddle, about which he had to give some direction, when the competitors were summoned to the pavilion to receive their numbers and places in the row at starting. Seventeen officers, looking serious and severe, many with pale faces, met together in the pavilion and drew the numbers. Vronsky drew the number seven. The cry was heard: “Mount!”\n\nFeeling that with the others riding in the race, he was the center upon which all eyes were fastened, Vronsky walked up to his mare in that state of nervous tension in which he usually became deliberate and composed in his movements. Cord, in honor of the races, had put on his best clothes, a black coat buttoned up, a stiffly starched collar, which propped up his cheeks, a round black hat, and top boots. He was calm and dignified as ever, and was with his own hands holding Frou-Frou by both reins, standing straight in front of her. Frou-Frou was still trembling as though in a fever. Her eye, full of fire, glanced sideways at Vronsky. Vronsky slipped his finger under the saddle-girth. The mare glanced aslant at him, drew up her lip, and twitched her ear. The Englishman puckered up his lips, intending to indicate a smile that anyone should verify his saddling.\n\n“Get up; you won’t feel so excited.”\n\nVronsky looked round for the last time at his rivals. He knew that he would not see them during the race. Two were already riding forward to the point from which they were to start. Galtsin, a friend of Vronsky’s and one of his more formidable rivals, was moving round a bay horse that would not let him mount. A little light hussar in tight riding breeches rode off at a gallop, crouched up like a cat on the saddle, in imitation of English jockeys. Prince Kuzovlev sat with a white face on his thoroughbred mare from the Grabovsky stud, while an English groom led her by the bridle. Vronsky and all his comrades knew Kuzovlev and his peculiarity of “weak nerves” and terrible vanity. They knew that he was afraid of everything, afraid of riding a spirited horse. But now, just because it was terrible, because people broke their necks, and there was a doctor standing at each obstacle, and an ambulance with a cross on it, and a sister of mercy, he had made up his mind to take part in the race. Their eyes met, and Vronsky gave him a friendly and encouraging nod. Only one he did not see, his chief rival, Mahotin on Gladiator.\n\n“Don’t be in a hurry,” said Cord to Vronsky, “and remember one thing: don’t hold her in at the fences, and don’t urge her on; let her go as she likes.”\n\n“All right, all right,” said Vronsky, taking the reins.\n\n“If you can, lead the race; but don’t lose heart till the last minute, even if you’re behind.”\n\nBefore the mare had time to move, Vronsky stepped with an agile, vigorous movement into the steel-toothed stirrup, and lightly and firmly seated himself on the creaking leather of the saddle. Getting his right foot in the stirrup, he smoothed the double reins, as he always did, between his fingers, and Cord let go.\n\nAs though she did not know which foot to put first, Frou-Frou started, dragging at the reins with her long neck, and as though she were on springs, shaking her rider from side to side. Cord quickened his step, following him. The excited mare, trying to shake off her rider first on one side and then the other, pulled at the reins, and Vronsky tried in vain with voice and hand to soothe her.\n\nThey were just reaching the dammed-up stream on their way to the starting point. Several of the riders were in front and several behind, when suddenly Vronsky heard the sound of a horse galloping in the mud behind him, and he was overtaken by Mahotin on his white-legged, lop-eared Gladiator. Mahotin smiled, showing his long teeth, but Vronsky looked angrily at him. He did not like him, and regarded him now as his most formidable rival. He was angry with him for galloping past and exciting his mare. Frou-Frou started into a gallop, her left foot forward, made two bounds, and fretting at the tightened reins, passed into a jolting trot, bumping her rider up and down. Cord, too, scowled, and followed Vronsky almost at a trot.\n\n# Chapter 25\n\nThere were seventeen officers in all riding in this race. The race course was a large three-mile ring of the form of an ellipse in front of the pavilion. On this course nine obstacles had been arranged: the stream, a big and solid barrier five feet high, just before the pavilion, a dry ditch, a ditch full of water, a precipitous slope, an Irish barricade (one of the most difficult obstacles, consisting of a mound fenced with brushwood, beyond which was a ditch out of sight for the horses, so that the horse had to clear both obstacles or might be killed); then two more ditches filled with water, and one dry one; and the end of the race was just facing the pavilion. But the race began not in the ring, but two hundred yards away from it, and in that part of the course was the first obstacle, a dammed-up stream, seven feet in breadth, which the racers could leap or wade through as they preferred.\n\nThree times they were ranged ready to start, but each time some horse thrust itself out of line, and they had to begin again. The umpire who was starting them, Colonel Sestrin, was beginning to lose his temper, when at last for the fourth time he shouted “Away!” and the racers started.\n\nEvery eye, every opera-glass, was turned on the brightly colored group of riders at the moment they were in line to start.\n\n“They’re off! They’re starting!” was heard on all sides after the hush of expectation.\n\nAnd little groups and solitary figures among the public began running from place to place to get a better view. In the very first minute the close group of horsemen drew out, and it could be seen that they were approaching the stream in twos and threes and one behind another. To the spectators it seemed as though they had all started simultaneously, but to the racers there were seconds of difference that had great value to them.\n\nFrou-Frou, excited and over-nervous, had lost the first moment, and several horses had started before her, but before reaching the stream, Vronsky, who was holding in the mare with all his force as she tugged at the bridle, easily overtook three, and there were left in front of him Mahotin’s chestnut Gladiator, whose hind-quarters were moving lightly and rhythmically up and down exactly in front of Vronsky, and in front of all, the dainty mare Diana bearing Kuzovlev more dead than alive.\n\nFor the first instant Vronsky was not master either of himself or his mare. Up to the first obstacle, the stream, he could not guide the motions of his mare.\n\nGladiator and Diana came up to it together and almost at the same instant; simultaneously they rose above the stream and flew across to the other side; Frou-Frou darted after them, as if flying; but at the very moment when Vronsky felt himself in the air, he suddenly saw almost under his mare’s hoofs Kuzovlev, who was floundering with Diana on the further side of the stream. (Kuzovlev had let go the reins as he took the leap, and the mare had sent him flying over her head.) Those details Vronsky learned later; at the moment all he saw was that just under him, where Frou-Frou must alight, Diana’s legs or head might be in the way. But Frou-Frou drew up her legs and back in the very act of leaping, like a falling cat, and, clearing the other mare, alighted beyond her.\n\n“O the darling!” thought Vronsky.\n\nAfter crossing the stream Vronsky had complete control of his mare, and began holding her in, intending to cross the great barrier behind Mahotin, and to try to overtake him in the clear ground of about five hundred yards that followed it.\n\nThe great barrier stood just in front of the imperial pavilion. The Tsar and the whole court and crowds of people were all gazing at them—at him, and Mahotin a length ahead of him, as they drew near the “devil,” as the solid barrier was called. Vronsky was aware of those eyes fastened upon him from all sides, but he saw nothing except the ears and neck of his own mare, the ground racing to meet him, and the back and white legs of Gladiator beating time swiftly before him, and keeping always the same distance ahead. Gladiator rose, with no sound of knocking against anything. With a wave of his short tail he disappeared from Vronsky’s sight.\n\n“Bravo!” cried a voice.\n\nAt the same instant, under Vronsky’s eyes, right before him flashed the palings of the barrier. Without the slightest change in her action his mare flew over it; the palings vanished, and he heard only a crash behind him. The mare, excited by Gladiator’s keeping ahead, had risen too soon before the barrier, and grazed it with her hind hoofs. But her pace never changed, and Vronsky, feeling a spatter of mud in his face, realized that he was once more the same distance from Gladiator. Once more he perceived in front of him the same back and short tail, and again the same swiftly moving white legs that got no further away.\n\nAt the very moment when Vronsky thought that now was the time to overtake Mahotin, Frou-Frou herself, understanding his thoughts, without any incitement on his part, gained ground considerably, and began getting alongside of Mahotin on the most favorable side, close to the inner cord. Mahotin would not let her pass that side. Vronsky had hardly formed the thought that he could perhaps pass on the outer side, when Frou-Frou shifted her pace and began overtaking him on the other side. Frou-Frou’s shoulder, beginning by now to be dark with sweat, was even with Gladiator’s back. For a few lengths they moved evenly. But before the obstacle they were approaching, Vronsky began working at the reins, anxious to avoid having to take the outer circle, and swiftly passed Mahotin just upon the declivity. He caught a glimpse of his mud-stained face as he flashed by. He even fancied that he smiled. Vronsky passed Mahotin, but he was immediately aware of him close upon him, and he never ceased hearing the even-thudding hoofs and the rapid and still quite fresh breathing of Gladiator.\n\nThe next two obstacles, the water course and the barrier, were easily crossed, but Vronsky began to hear the snorting and thud of Gladiator closer upon him. He urged on his mare, and to his delight felt that she easily quickened her pace, and the thud of Gladiator’s hoofs was again heard at the same distance away.\n\nVronsky was at the head of the race, just as he wanted to be and as Cord had advised, and now he felt sure of being the winner. His excitement, his delight, and his tenderness for Frou-Frou grew keener and keener. He longed to look round again, but he did not dare do this, and tried to be cool and not to urge on his mare so to keep the same reserve of force in her as he felt that Gladiator still kept. There remained only one obstacle, the most difficult; if he could cross it ahead of the others he would come in first. He was flying towards the Irish barricade, Frou-Frou and he both together saw the barricade in the distance, and both the man and the mare had a moment’s hesitation. He saw the uncertainty in the mare’s ears and lifted the whip, but at the same time felt that his fears were groundless; the mare knew what was wanted. She quickened her pace and rose smoothly, just as he had fancied she would, and as she left the ground gave herself up to the force of her rush, which carried her far beyond the ditch; and with the same rhythm, without effort, with the same leg forward, Frou-Frou fell back into her pace again.\n\n“Bravo, Vronsky!” he heard shouts from a knot of men—he knew they were his friends in the regiment—who were standing at the obstacle. He could not fail to recognize Yashvin’s voice though he did not see him.\n\n“O my sweet!” he said inwardly to Frou-Frou, as he listened for what was happening behind. “He’s cleared it!” he thought, catching the thud of Gladiator’s hoofs behind him. There remained only the last ditch, filled with water and five feet wide. Vronsky did not even look at it, but anxious to get in a long way first began sawing away at the reins, lifting the mare’s head and letting it go in time with her paces. He felt that the mare was at her very last reserve of strength; not her neck and shoulders merely were wet, but the sweat was standing in drops on her mane, her head, her sharp ears, and her breath came in short, sharp gasps. But he knew that she had strength left more than enough for the remaining five hundred yards. It was only from feeling himself nearer the ground and from the peculiar smoothness of his motion that Vronsky knew how greatly the mare had quickened her pace. She flew over the ditch as though not noticing it. She flew over it like a bird; but at the same instant Vronsky, to his horror, felt that he had failed to keep up with the mare’s pace, that he had, he did not know how, made a fearful, unpardonable mistake, in recovering his seat in the saddle. All at once his position had shifted and he knew that something awful had happened. He could not yet make out what had happened, when the white legs of a chestnut horse flashed by close to him, and Mahotin passed at a swift gallop. Vronsky was touching the ground with one foot, and his mare was sinking on that foot. He just had time to free his leg when she fell on one side, gasping painfully, and, making vain efforts to rise with her delicate, soaking neck, she fluttered on the ground at his feet like a shot bird. The clumsy movement made by Vronsky had broken her back. But that he only knew much later. At that moment he knew only that Mahotin had flown swiftly by, while he stood staggering alone on the muddy, motionless ground, and Frou-Frou lay gasping before him, bending her head back and gazing at him with her exquisite eyes. Still unable to realize what had happened, Vronsky tugged at his mare’s reins. Again she struggled all over like a fish, and her shoulders setting the saddle heaving, she rose on her front legs but unable to lift her back, she quivered all over and again fell on her side. With a face hideous with passion, his lower jaw trembling, and his cheeks white, Vronsky kicked her with his heel in the stomach and again fell to tugging at the rein. She did not stir, but thrusting her nose into the ground, she simply gazed at her master with her speaking eyes.\n\n“A—a—a!” groaned Vronsky, clutching at his head. “Ah! what have I done!” he cried. “The race lost! And my fault! shameful, unpardonable! And the poor darling, ruined mare! Ah! what have I done!”\n\nA crowd of men, a doctor and his assistant, the officers of his regiment, ran up to him. To his misery he felt that he was whole and unhurt. The mare had broken her back, and it was decided to shoot her. Vronsky could not answer questions, could not speak to anyone. He turned, and without picking up his cap that had fallen off, walked away from the race course, not knowing where he was going. He felt utterly wretched. For the first time in his life he knew the bitterest sort of misfortune, misfortune beyond remedy, and caused by his own fault.\n\nYashvin overtook him with his cap, and led him home, and half an hour later Vronsky had regained his self-possession. But the memory of that race remained for long in his heart, the cruelest and bitterest memory of his life.\n\n# Chapter 26\n\nThe external relations of Alexey Alexandrovitch and his wife had remained unchanged. The sole difference lay in the fact that he was more busily occupied than ever. As in former years, at the beginning of the spring he had gone to a foreign watering-place for the sake of his health, deranged by the winter’s work that every year grew heavier. And just as always he returned in July and at once fell to work as usual with increased energy. As usual, too, his wife had moved for the summer to a villa out of town, while he remained in Petersburg. From the date of their conversation after the party at Princess Tverskaya’s he had never spoken again to Anna of his suspicions and his jealousies, and that habitual tone of his bantering mimicry was the most convenient tone possible for his present attitude to his wife. He was a little colder to his wife. He simply seemed to be slightly displeased with her for that first midnight conversation, which she had repelled. In his attitude to her there was a shade of vexation, but nothing more. “You would not be open with me,” he seemed to say, mentally addressing her; “so much the worse for you. Now you may beg as you please, but I won’t be open with you. So much the worse for you!” he said mentally, like a man who, after vainly attempting to extinguish a fire, should fly in a rage with his vain efforts and say, “Oh, very well then! you shall burn for this!” This man, so subtle and astute in official life, did not realize all the senselessness of such an attitude to his wife. He did not realize it, because it was too terrible to him to realize his actual position, and he shut down and locked and sealed up in his heart that secret place where lay hid his feelings towards his family, that is, his wife and son. He who had been such a careful father, had from the end of that winter become peculiarly frigid to his son, and adopted to him just the same bantering tone he used with his wife. “Aha, young man!” was the greeting with which he met him.\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch asserted and believed that he had never in any previous year had so much official business as that year. But he was not aware that he sought work for himself that year, that this was one of the means for keeping shut that secret place where lay hid his feelings towards his wife and son and his thoughts about them, which became more terrible the longer they lay there. If anyone had had the right to ask Alexey Alexandrovitch what he thought of his wife’s behavior, the mild and peaceable Alexey Alexandrovitch would have made no answer, but he would have been greatly angered with any man who should question him on that subject. For this reason there positively came into Alexey Alexandrovitch’s face a look of haughtiness and severity whenever anyone inquired after his wife’s health. Alexey Alexandrovitch did not want to think at all about his wife’s behavior, and he actually succeeded in not thinking about it at all.\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch’s permanent summer villa was in Peterhof, and the Countess Lidia Ivanovna used as a rule to spend the summer there, close to Anna, and constantly seeing her. That year Countess Lidia Ivanovna declined to settle in Peterhof, was not once at Anna Arkadyevna’s, and in conversation with Alexey Alexandrovitch hinted at the unsuitability of Anna’s close intimacy with Betsy and Vronsky. Alexey Alexandrovitch sternly cut her short, roundly declaring his wife to be above suspicion, and from that time began to avoid Countess Lidia Ivanovna. He did not want to see, and did not see, that many people in society cast dubious glances on his wife; he did not want to understand, and did not understand, why his wife had so particularly insisted on staying at Tsarskoe, where Betsy was staying, and not far from the camp of Vronsky’s regiment. He did not allow himself to think about it, and he did not think about it; but all the same though he never admitted it to himself, and had no proofs, not even suspicious evidence, in the bottom of his heart he knew beyond all doubt that he was a deceived husband, and he was profoundly miserable about it.\n\nHow often during those eight years of happy life with his wife Alexey Alexandrovitch had looked at other men’s faithless wives and other deceived husbands and asked himself: “How can people descend to that? how is it they don’t put an end to such a hideous position?” But now, when the misfortune had come upon himself, he was so far from thinking of putting an end to the position that he would not recognize it at all, would not recognize it just because it was too awful, too unnatural.\n\nSince his return from abroad Alexey Alexandrovitch had twice been at their country villa. Once he dined there, another time he spent the evening there with a party of friends, but he had not once stayed the night there, as it had been his habit to do in previous years.\n\nThe day of the races had been a very busy day for Alexey Alexandrovitch; but when mentally sketching out the day in the morning, he made up his mind to go to their country house to see his wife immediately after dinner, and from there to the races, which all the Court were to witness, and at which he was bound to be present. He was going to see his wife, because he had determined to see her once a week to keep up appearances. And besides, on that day, as it was the fifteenth, he had to give his wife some money for her expenses, according to their usual arrangement.\n\nWith his habitual control over his thoughts, though he thought all this about his wife, he did not let his thoughts stray further in regard to her.\n\nThat morning was a very full one for Alexey Alexandrovitch. The evening before, Countess Lidia Ivanovna had sent him a pamphlet by a celebrated traveler in China, who was staying in Petersburg, and with it she enclosed a note begging him to see the traveler himself, as he was an extremely interesting person from various points of view, and likely to be useful. Alexey Alexandrovitch had not had time to read the pamphlet through in the evening, and finished it in the morning. Then people began arriving with petitions, and there came the reports, interviews, appointments, dismissals, apportionment of rewards, pensions, grants, notes, the workaday round, as Alexey Alexandrovitch called it, that always took up so much time. Then there was private business of his own, a visit from the doctor and the steward who managed his property. The steward did not take up much time. He simply gave Alexey Alexandrovitch the money he needed together with a brief statement of the position of his affairs, which was not altogether satisfactory, as it had happened that during that year, owing to increased expenses, more had been paid out than usual, and there was a deficit. But the doctor, a celebrated Petersburg doctor, who was an intimate acquaintance of Alexey Alexandrovitch, took up a great deal of time. Alexey Alexandrovitch had not expected him that day, and was surprised at his visit, and still more so when the doctor questioned him very carefully about his health, listened to his breathing, and tapped at his liver. Alexey Alexandrovitch did not know that his friend Lidia Ivanovna, noticing that he was not as well as usual that year, had begged the doctor to go and examine him. “Do this for my sake,” the Countess Lidia Ivanovna had said to him.\n\n“I will do it for the sake of Russia, countess,” replied the doctor.\n\n“A priceless man!” said the Countess Lidia Ivanovna.\n\nThe doctor was extremely dissatisfied with Alexey Alexandrovitch. He found the liver considerably enlarged, and the digestive powers weakened, while the course of mineral waters had been quite without effect. He prescribed more physical exercise as far as possible, and as far as possible less mental strain, and above all no worry—in other words, just what was as much out of Alexey Alexandrovitch’s power as abstaining from breathing. Then he withdrew, leaving in Alexey Alexandrovitch an unpleasant sense that something was wrong with him, and that there was no chance of curing it.\n\nAs he was coming away, the doctor chanced to meet on the staircase an acquaintance of his, Sludin, who was secretary of Alexey Alexandrovitch’s department. They had been comrades at the university, and though they rarely met, they thought highly of each other and were excellent friends, and so there was no one to whom the doctor would have given his opinion of a patient so freely as to Sludin.\n\n“How glad I am you’ve been seeing him!” said Sludin. “He’s not well, and I fancy.... Well, what do you think of him?”\n\n“I’ll tell you,” said the doctor, beckoning over Sludin’s head to his coachman to bring the carriage round. “It’s just this,” said the doctor, taking a finger of his kid glove in his white hands and pulling it, “if you don’t strain the strings, and then try to break them, you’ll find it a difficult job; but strain a string to its very utmost, and the mere weight of one finger on the strained string will snap it. And with his close assiduity, his conscientious devotion to his work, he’s strained to the utmost; and there’s some outside burden weighing on him, and not a light one,” concluded the doctor, raising his eyebrows significantly. “Will you be at the races?” he added, as he sank into his seat in the carriage.\n\n“Yes, yes, to be sure; it does waste a lot of time,” the doctor responded vaguely to some reply of Sludin’s he had not caught.\n\nDirectly after the doctor, who had taken up so much time, came the celebrated traveler, and Alexey Alexandrovitch, by means of the pamphlet he had only just finished reading and his previous acquaintance with the subject, impressed the traveler by the depth of his knowledge of the subject and the breadth and enlightenment of his view of it.\n\nAt the same time as the traveler there was announced a provincial marshal of nobility on a visit to Petersburg, with whom Alexey Alexandrovitch had to have some conversation. After his departure, he had to finish the daily routine of business with his secretary, and then he still had to drive round to call on a certain great personage on a matter of grave and serious import. Alexey Alexandrovitch only just managed to be back by five o’clock, his dinner-hour, and after dining with his secretary, he invited him to drive with him to his country villa and to the races.\n\nThough he did not acknowledge it to himself, Alexey Alexandrovitch always tried nowadays to secure the presence of a third person in his interviews with his wife.\n\n# Chapter 27\n\nAnna was upstairs, standing before the looking-glass, and, with Annushka’s assistance, pinning the last ribbon on her gown when she heard carriage wheels crunching the gravel at the entrance.\n\n“It’s too early for Betsy,” she thought, and glancing out of the window she caught sight of the carriage and the black hat of Alexey Alexandrovitch, and the ears that she knew so well sticking up each side of it. “How unlucky! Can he be going to stay the night?” she wondered, and the thought of all that might come of such a chance struck her as so awful and terrible that, without dwelling on it for a moment, she went down to meet him with a bright and radiant face; and conscious of the presence of that spirit of falsehood and deceit in herself that she had come to know of late, she abandoned herself to that spirit and began talking, hardly knowing what she was saying.\n\n“Ah, how nice of you!” she said, giving her husband her hand, and greeting Sludin, who was like one of the family, with a smile. “You’re staying the night, I hope?” was the first word the spirit of falsehood prompted her to utter; “and now we’ll go together. Only it’s a pity I’ve promised Betsy. She’s coming for me.”\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch knit his brows at Betsy’s name.\n\n“Oh, I’m not going to separate the inseparables,” he said in his usual bantering tone. “I’m going with Mihail Vassilievitch. I’m ordered exercise by the doctors too. I’ll walk, and fancy myself at the springs again.”\n\n“There’s no hurry,” said Anna. “Would you like tea?”\nShe rang.\n\n“Bring in tea, and tell Seryozha that Alexey Alexandrovitch is here. Well, tell me, how have you been? Mihail Vassilievitch, you’ve not been to see me before. Look how lovely it is out on the terrace,” she said, turning first to one and then to the other.\n\nShe spoke very simply and naturally, but too much and too fast. She was the more aware of this from noticing in the inquisitive look Mihail Vassilievitch turned on her that he was, as it were, keeping watch on her.\n\nMihail Vassilievitch promptly went out on the terrace.\n\nShe sat down beside her husband.\n\n“You don’t look quite well,” she said.\n\n“Yes,” he said; “the doctor’s been with me today and wasted an hour of my time. I feel that someone of our friends must have sent him: my health’s so precious, it seems.”\n\n“No; what did he say?”\n\nShe questioned him about his health and what he had been doing, and tried to persuade him to take a rest and come out to her.\n\nAll this she said brightly, rapidly, and with a peculiar brilliance in her eyes. But Alexey Alexandrovitch did not now attach any special significance to this tone of hers. He heard only her words and gave them only the direct sense they bore. And he answered simply, though jestingly. There was nothing remarkable in all this conversation, but never after could Anna recall this brief scene without an agonizing pang of shame.\n\nSeryozha came in preceded by his governess. If Alexey Alexandrovitch had allowed himself to observe he would have noticed the timid and bewildered eyes with which Seryozha glanced first at his father and then at his mother. But he would not see anything, and he did not see it.\n\n“Ah, the young man! He’s grown. Really, he’s getting quite a man. How are you, young man?”\n\nAnd he gave his hand to the scared child. Seryozha had been shy of his father before, and now, ever since Alexey Alexandrovitch had taken to calling him young man, and since that insoluble question had occurred to him whether Vronsky were a friend or a foe, he avoided his father. He looked round towards his mother as though seeking shelter. It was only with his mother that he was at ease. Meanwhile, Alexey Alexandrovitch was holding his son by the shoulder while he was speaking to the governess, and Seryozha was so miserably uncomfortable that Anna saw he was on the point of tears.\n\nAnna, who had flushed a little the instant her son came in, noticing that Seryozha was uncomfortable, got up hurriedly, took Alexey Alexandrovitch’s hand from her son’s shoulder, and kissing the boy, led him out onto the terrace, and quickly came back.\n\n“It’s time to start, though,” said she, glancing at her watch. “How is it Betsy doesn’t come?...”\n\n“Yes,” said Alexey Alexandrovitch, and getting up, he folded his hands and cracked his fingers. “I’ve come to bring you some money, too, for nightingales, we know, can’t live on fairy tales,” he said. “You want it, I expect?”\n\n“No, I don’t ... yes, I do,” she said, not looking at him, and crimsoning to the roots of her hair. “But you’ll come back here after the races, I suppose?”\n\n“Oh, yes!” answered Alexey Alexandrovitch. “And here’s the glory of Peterhof, Princess Tverskaya,” he added, looking out of the window at the elegant English carriage with the tiny seats placed extremely high. “What elegance! Charming! Well, let us be starting too, then.”\n\nPrincess Tverskaya did not get out of her carriage, but her groom, in high boots, a cape, and black hat, darted out at the entrance.\n\n“I’m going; good-bye!” said Anna, and kissing her son, she went up to Alexey Alexandrovitch and held out her hand to him. “It was ever so nice of you to come.”\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch kissed her hand.\n\n“Well, au revoir, then! You’ll come back for some tea; that’s delightful!” she said, and went out, gay and radiant. But as soon as she no longer saw him, she was aware of the spot on her hand that his lips had touched, and she shuddered with repulsion.\n\n# Chapter 28\n\nWhen Alexey Alexandrovitch reached the race-course, Anna was already sitting in the pavilion beside Betsy, in that pavilion where all the highest society had gathered. She caught sight of her husband in the distance. Two men, her husband and her lover, were the two centers of her existence, and unaided by her external senses she was aware of their nearness. She was aware of her husband approaching a long way off, and she could not help following him in the surging crowd in the midst of which he was moving. She watched his progress towards the pavilion, saw him now responding condescendingly to an ingratiating bow, now exchanging friendly, nonchalant greetings with his equals, now assiduously trying to catch the eye of some great one of this world, and taking off his big round hat that squeezed the tips of his ears. All these ways of his she knew, and all were hateful to her. “Nothing but ambition, nothing but the desire to get on, that’s all there is in his soul,” she thought; “as for these lofty ideals, love of culture, religion, they are only so many tools for getting on.”\n\nFrom his glances towards the ladies’ pavilion (he was staring straight at her, but did not distinguish his wife in the sea of muslin, ribbons, feathers, parasols and flowers) she saw that he was looking for her, but she purposely avoided noticing him.\n\n“Alexey Alexandrovitch!” Princess Betsy called to him; “I’m sure you don’t see your wife: here she is.”\n\nHe smiled his chilly smile.\n\n“There’s so much splendor here that one’s eyes are dazzled,” he said, and he went into the pavilion. He smiled to his wife as a man should smile on meeting his wife after only just parting from her, and greeted the princess and other acquaintances, giving to each what was due—that is to say, jesting with the ladies and dealing out friendly greetings among the men. Below, near the pavilion, was standing an adjutant-general of whom Alexey Alexandrovitch had a high opinion, noted for his intelligence and culture. Alexey Alexandrovitch entered into conversation with him.\n\nThere was an interval between the races, and so nothing hindered conversation. The adjutant-general expressed his disapproval of races. Alexey Alexandrovitch replied defending them. Anna heard his high, measured tones, not losing one word, and every word struck her as false, and stabbed her ears with pain.\n\nWhen the three-mile steeplechase was beginning, she bent forward and gazed with fixed eyes at Vronsky as he went up to his horse and mounted, and at the same time she heard that loathsome, never-ceasing voice of her husband. She was in an agony of terror for Vronsky, but a still greater agony was the never-ceasing, as it seemed to her, stream of her husband’s shrill voice with its familiar intonations.\n\n“I’m a wicked woman, a lost woman,” she thought; “but I don’t like lying, I can’t endure falsehood, while as for him (her husband) it’s the breath of his life—falsehood. He knows all about it, he sees it all; what does he care if he can talk so calmly? If he were to kill me, if he were to kill Vronsky, I might respect him. No, all he wants is falsehood and propriety,” Anna said to herself, not considering exactly what it was she wanted of her husband, and how she would have liked to see him behave. She did not understand either that Alexey Alexandrovitch’s peculiar loquacity that day, so exasperating to her, was merely the expression of his inward distress and uneasiness. As a child that has been hurt skips about, putting all his muscles into movement to drown the pain, in the same way Alexey Alexandrovitch needed mental exercise to drown the thoughts of his wife that in her presence and in Vronsky’s, and with the continual iteration of his name, would force themselves on his attention. And it was as natural for him to talk well and cleverly, as it is natural for a child to skip about. He was saying:\n\n“Danger in the races of officers, of cavalry men, is an essential element in the race. If England can point to the most brilliant feats of cavalry in military history, it is simply owing to the fact that she has historically developed this force both in beasts and in men. Sport has, in my opinion, a great value, and as is always the case, we see nothing but what is most superficial.”\n\n“It’s not superficial,” said Princess Tverskaya. “One of the officers, they say, has broken two ribs.”\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch smiled his smile, which uncovered his teeth, but revealed nothing more.\n\n“We’ll admit, princess, that that’s not superficial,” he said, “but internal. But that’s not the point,” and he turned again to the general with whom he was talking seriously; “we mustn’t forget that those who are taking part in the race are military men, who have chosen that career, and one must allow that every calling has its disagreeable side. It forms an integral part of the duties of an officer. Low sports, such as prize-fighting or Spanish bull-fights, are a sign of barbarity. But specialized trials of skill are a sign of development.”\n\n“No, I shan’t come another time; it’s too upsetting,” said Princess Betsy. “Isn’t it, Anna?”\n\n“It is upsetting, but one can’t tear oneself away,” said another lady. “If I’d been a Roman woman I should never have missed a single circus.”\n\nAnna said nothing, and keeping her opera-glass up, gazed always at the same spot.\n\nAt that moment a tall general walked through the pavilion. Breaking off what he was saying, Alexey Alexandrovitch got up hurriedly, though with dignity, and bowed low to the general.\n\n“You’re not racing?” the officer asked, chaffing him.\n\n“My race is a harder one,” Alexey Alexandrovitch responded deferentially.\n\nAnd though the answer meant nothing, the general looked as though he had heard a witty remark from a witty man, and fully relished la pointe de la sauce.\n\n“There are two aspects,” Alexey Alexandrovitch resumed: “those who take part and those who look on; and love for such spectacles is an unmistakable proof of a low degree of development in the spectator, I admit, but....”\n\n“Princess, bets!” sounded Stepan Arkadyevitch’s voice from below, addressing Betsy. “Who’s your favorite?”\n\n“Anna and I are for Kuzovlev,” replied Betsy.\n\n“I’m for Vronsky. A pair of gloves?”\n\n“Done!”\n\n“But it is a pretty sight, isn’t it?”\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch paused while there was talking about him, but he began again directly.\n\n“I admit that manly sports do not....” he was continuing.\n\nBut at that moment the racers started, and all conversation ceased. Alexey Alexandrovitch too was silent, and everyone stood up and turned towards the stream. Alexey Alexandrovitch took no interest in the race, and so he did not watch the racers, but fell listlessly to scanning the spectators with his weary eyes. His eyes rested upon Anna.\n\nHer face was white and set. She was obviously seeing nothing and no one but one man. Her hand had convulsively clutched her fan, and she held her breath. He looked at her and hastily turned away, scrutinizing other faces.\n\n“But here’s this lady too, and others very much moved as well; it’s very natural,” Alexey Alexandrovitch told himself. He tried not to look at her, but unconsciously his eyes were drawn to her. He examined that face again, trying not to read what was so plainly written on it, and against his own will, with horror read on it what he did not want to know.\n\nThe first fall—Kuzovlev’s, at the stream—agitated everyone, but Alexey Alexandrovitch saw distinctly on Anna’s pale, triumphant face that the man she was watching had not fallen. When, after Mahotin and Vronsky had cleared the worst barrier, the next officer had been thrown straight on his head at it and fatally injured, and a shudder of horror passed over the whole public, Alexey Alexandrovitch saw that Anna did not even notice it, and had some difficulty in realizing what they were talking of about her. But more and more often, and with greater persistence, he watched her. Anna, wholly engrossed as she was with the race, became aware of her husband’s cold eyes fixed upon her from one side.\n\nShe glanced round for an instant, looked inquiringly at him, and with a slight frown turned away again.\n\n“Ah, I don’t care!” she seemed to say to him, and she did not once glance at him again.\n\nThe race was an unlucky one, and of the seventeen officers who rode in it more than half were thrown and hurt. Towards the end of the race everyone was in a state of agitation, which was intensified by the fact that the Tsar was displeased.\n\n# Chapter 29\n\nEveryone was loudly expressing disapprobation, everyone was repeating a phrase someone had uttered—“The lions and gladiators will be the next thing,” and everyone was feeling horrified; so that when Vronsky fell to the ground, and Anna moaned aloud, there was nothing very out of the way in it. But afterwards a change came over Anna’s face which really was beyond decorum. She utterly lost her head. She began fluttering like a caged bird, at one moment would have got up and moved away, at the next turned to Betsy.\n\n“Let us go, let us go!” she said.\n\nBut Betsy did not hear her. She was bending down, talking to a general who had come up to her.\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch went up to Anna and courteously offered her his arm.\n\n“Let us go, if you like,” he said in French, but Anna was listening to the general and did not notice her husband.\n\n“He’s broken his leg too, so they say,” the general was saying. “This is beyond everything.”\n\nWithout answering her husband, Anna lifted her opera-glass and gazed towards the place where Vronsky had fallen; but it was so far off, and there was such a crowd of people about it, that she could make out nothing. She laid down the opera-glass, and would have moved away, but at that moment an officer galloped up and made some announcement to the Tsar. Anna craned forward, listening.\n\n“Stiva! Stiva!” she cried to her brother.\n\nBut her brother did not hear her. Again she would have moved away.\n\n“Once more I offer you my arm if you want to be going,” said Alexey Alexandrovitch, reaching towards her hand.\n\nShe drew back from him with aversion, and without looking in his face answered:\n\n“No, no, let me be, I’ll stay.”\n\nShe saw now that from the place of Vronsky’s accident an officer was running across the course towards the pavilion. Betsy waved her handkerchief to him. The officer brought the news that the rider was not killed, but the horse had broken its back.\n\nOn hearing this Anna sat down hurriedly, and hid her face in her fan. Alexey Alexandrovitch saw that she was weeping, and could not control her tears, nor even the sobs that were shaking her bosom. Alexey Alexandrovitch stood so as to screen her, giving her time to recover herself.\n\n“For the third time I offer you my arm,” he said to her after a little time, turning to her. Anna gazed at him and did not know what to say. Princess Betsy came to her rescue.\n\n“No, Alexey Alexandrovitch; I brought Anna and I promised to take her home,” put in Betsy.\n\n“Excuse me, princess,” he said, smiling courteously but looking her very firmly in the face, “but I see that Anna’s not very well, and I wish her to come home with me.”\n\nAnna looked about her in a frightened way, got up submissively, and laid her hand on her husband’s arm.\n\n“I’ll send to him and find out, and let you know,” Betsy whispered to her.\n\nAs they left the pavilion, Alexey Alexandrovitch, as always, talked to those he met, and Anna had, as always, to talk and answer; but she was utterly beside herself, and moved hanging on her husband’s arm as though in a dream.\n\n“Is he killed or not? Is it true? Will he come or not? Shall I see him today?” she was thinking.\n\nShe took her seat in her husband’s carriage in silence, and in silence drove out of the crowd of carriages. In spite of all he had seen, Alexey Alexandrovitch still did not allow himself to consider his wife’s real condition. He merely saw the outward symptoms. He saw that she was behaving unbecomingly, and considered it his duty to tell her so. But it was very difficult for him not to say more, to tell her nothing but that. He opened his mouth to tell her she had behaved unbecomingly, but he could not help saying something utterly different.\n\n“What an inclination we all have, though, for these cruel spectacles,” he said. “I observe....”\n\n“Eh? I don’t understand,” said Anna contemptuously.\n\nHe was offended, and at once began to say what he had meant to say.\n\n“I am obliged to tell you,” he began.\n\n“So now we are to have it out,” she thought, and she felt frightened.\n\n“I am obliged to tell you that your behavior has been unbecoming today,” he said to her in French.\n\n“In what way has my behavior been unbecoming?” she said aloud, turning her head swiftly and looking him straight in the face, not with the bright expression that seemed covering something, but with a look of determination, under which she concealed with difficulty the dismay she was feeling.\n\n“Mind,” he said, pointing to the open window opposite the coachman.\n\nHe got up and pulled up the window.\n\n“What did you consider unbecoming?” she repeated.\n\n“The despair you were unable to conceal at the accident to one of the riders.”\n\nHe waited for her to answer, but she was silent, looking straight before her.\n\n“I have already begged you so to conduct yourself in society that even malicious tongues can find nothing to say against you. There was a time when I spoke of your inward attitude, but I am not speaking of that now. Now I speak only of your external attitude. You have behaved improperly, and I would wish it not to occur again.”\n\nShe did not hear half of what he was saying; she felt panic-stricken before him, and was thinking whether it was true that Vronsky was not killed. Was it of him they were speaking when they said the rider was unhurt, but the horse had broken its back? She merely smiled with a pretense of irony when he finished, and made no reply, because she had not heard what he said. Alexey Alexandrovitch had begun to speak boldly, but as he realized plainly what he was speaking of, the dismay she was feeling infected him too. He saw the smile, and a strange misapprehension came over him.\n\n“She is smiling at my suspicions. Yes, she will tell me directly what she told me before; that there is no foundation for my suspicions, that it’s absurd.”\n\nAt that moment, when the revelation of everything was hanging over him, there was nothing he expected so much as that she would answer mockingly as before that his suspicions were absurd and utterly groundless. So terrible to him was what he knew that now he was ready to believe anything. But the expression of her face, scared and gloomy, did not now promise even deception.\n\n“Possibly I was mistaken,” said he. “If so, I beg your pardon.”\n\n“No, you were not mistaken,” she said deliberately, looking desperately into his cold face. “You were not mistaken. I was, and I could not help being in despair. I hear you, but I am thinking of him. I love him, I am his mistress; I can’t bear you; I’m afraid of you, and I hate you.... You can do what you like to me.”\n\nAnd dropping back into the corner of the carriage, she broke into sobs, hiding her face in her hands. Alexey Alexandrovitch did not stir, and kept looking straight before him. But his whole face suddenly bore the solemn rigidity of the dead, and his expression did not change during the whole time of the drive home. On reaching the house he turned his head to her, still with the same expression.\n\n“Very well! But I expect a strict observance of the external forms of propriety till such time”—his voice shook—“as I may take measures to secure my honor and communicate them to you.”\n\nHe got out first and helped her to get out. Before the servants he pressed her hand, took his seat in the carriage, and drove back to Petersburg. Immediately afterwards a footman came from Princess Betsy and brought Anna a note.\n\n“I sent to Alexey to find out how he is, and he writes me he is quite well and unhurt, but in despair.”\n\n“So he will be here,” she thought. “What a good thing I told him all!”\n\nShe glanced at her watch. She had still three hours to wait, and the memories of their last meeting set her blood in flame.\n\n“My God, how light it is! It’s dreadful, but I do love to see his face, and I do love this fantastic light.... My husband! Oh! yes.... Well, thank God! everything’s over with him.”\n\n# Chapter 30\n\nIn the little German watering-place to which the Shtcherbatskys had betaken themselves, as in all places indeed where people are gathered together, the usual process, as it were, of the crystallization of society went on, assigning to each member of that society a definite and unalterable place. Just as the particle of water in frost, definitely and unalterably, takes the special form of the crystal of snow, so each new person that arrived at the springs was at once placed in his special place.\n\nFürst Shtcherbatsky, sammt Gemahlin und Tochter, by the apartments they took, and from their name and from the friends they made, were immediately crystallized into a definite place marked out for them.\n\nThere was visiting the watering-place that year a real German Fürstin, in consequence of which the crystallizing process went on more vigorously than ever. Princess Shtcherbatskaya wished, above everything, to present her daughter to this German princess, and the day after their arrival she duly performed this rite. Kitty made a low and graceful curtsey in the very simple, that is to say, very elegant frock that had been ordered her from Paris. The German princess said, “I hope the roses will soon come back to this pretty little face,” and for the Shtcherbatskys certain definite lines of existence were at once laid down from which there was no departing. The Shtcherbatskys made the acquaintance too of the family of an English Lady Somebody, and of a German countess and her son, wounded in the last war, and of a learned Swede, and of M. Canut and his sister. But yet inevitably the Shtcherbatskys were thrown most into the society of a Moscow lady, Marya Yevgenyevna Rtishtcheva and her daughter, whom Kitty disliked, because she had fallen ill, like herself, over a love affair, and a Moscow colonel, whom Kitty had known from childhood, and always seen in uniform and epaulets, and who now, with his little eyes and his open neck and flowered cravat, was uncommonly ridiculous and tedious, because there was no getting rid of him. When all this was so firmly established, Kitty began to be very much bored, especially as the prince went away to Carlsbad and she was left alone with her mother. She took no interest in the people she knew, feeling that nothing fresh would come of them. Her chief mental interest in the watering-place consisted in watching and making theories about the people she did not know. It was characteristic of Kitty that she always imagined everything in people in the most favorable light possible, especially so in those she did not know. And now as she made surmises as to who people were, what were their relations to one another, and what they were like, Kitty endowed them with the most marvelous and noble characters, and found confirmation of her idea in her observations.\n\nOf these people the one that attracted her most was a Russian girl who had come to the watering-place with an invalid Russian lady, Madame Stahl, as everyone called her. Madame Stahl belonged to the highest society, but she was so ill that she could not walk, and only on exceptionally fine days made her appearance at the springs in an invalid carriage. But it was not so much from ill-health as from pride—so Princess Shtcherbatskaya interpreted it—that Madame Stahl had not made the acquaintance of anyone among the Russians there. The Russian girl looked after Madame Stahl, and besides that, she was, as Kitty observed, on friendly terms with all the invalids who were seriously ill, and there were many of them at the springs, and looked after them in the most natural way. This Russian girl was not, as Kitty gathered, related to Madame Stahl, nor was she a paid attendant. Madame Stahl called her Varenka, and other people called her “Mademoiselle Varenka.” Apart from the interest Kitty took in this girl’s relations with Madame Stahl and with other unknown persons, Kitty, as often happened, felt an inexplicable attraction to Mademoiselle Varenka, and was aware when their eyes met that she too liked her.\n\nOf Mademoiselle Varenka one would not say that she had passed her first youth, but she was, as it were, a creature without youth; she might have been taken for nineteen or for thirty. If her features were criticized separately, she was handsome rather than plain, in spite of the sickly hue of her face. She would have been a good figure, too, if it had not been for her extreme thinness and the size of her head, which was too large for her medium height. But she was not likely to be attractive to men. She was like a fine flower, already past its bloom and without fragrance, though the petals were still unwithered. Moreover, she would have been unattractive to men also from the lack of just what Kitty had too much of—of the suppressed fire of vitality, and the consciousness of her own attractiveness.\n\nShe always seemed absorbed in work about which there could be no doubt, and so it seemed she could not take interest in anything outside it. It was just this contrast with her own position that was for Kitty the great attraction of Mademoiselle Varenka. Kitty felt that in her, in her manner of life, she would find an example of what she was now so painfully seeking: interest in life, a dignity in life—apart from the worldly relations of girls with men, which so revolted Kitty, and appeared to her now as a shameful hawking about of goods in search of a purchaser. The more attentively Kitty watched her unknown friend, the more convinced she was this girl was the perfect creature she fancied her, and the more eagerly she wished to make her acquaintance.\n\nThe two girls used to meet several times a day, and every time they met, Kitty’s eyes said: “Who are you? What are you? Are you really the exquisite creature I imagine you to be? But for goodness’ sake don’t suppose,” her eyes added, “that I would force my acquaintance on you, I simply admire you and like you.” “I like you too, and you’re very, very sweet. And I should like you better still, if I had time,” answered the eyes of the unknown girl. Kitty saw indeed, that she was always busy. Either she was taking the children of a Russian family home from the springs, or fetching a shawl for a sick lady, and wrapping her up in it, or trying to interest an irritable invalid, or selecting and buying cakes for tea for someone.\n\nSoon after the arrival of the Shtcherbatskys there appeared in the morning crowd at the springs two persons who attracted universal and unfavorable attention. These were a tall man with a stooping figure, and huge hands, in an old coat too short for him, with black, simple, and yet terrible eyes, and a pockmarked, kind-looking woman, very badly and tastelessly dressed. Recognizing these persons as Russians, Kitty had already in her imagination begun constructing a delightful and touching romance about them. But the princess, having ascertained from the visitors’ list that this was Nikolay Levin and Marya Nikolaevna, explained to Kitty what a bad man this Levin was, and all her fancies about these two people vanished. Not so much from what her mother told her, as from the fact that it was Konstantin’s brother, this pair suddenly seemed to Kitty intensely unpleasant. This Levin, with his continual twitching of his head, aroused in her now an irrepressible feeling of disgust.\n\nIt seemed to her that his big, terrible eyes, which persistently pursued her, expressed a feeling of hatred and contempt, and she tried to avoid meeting him.\n\n# Chapter 31\n\nIt was a wet day; it had been raining all the morning, and the invalids, with their parasols, had flocked into the arcades.\n\nKitty was walking there with her mother and the Moscow colonel, smart and jaunty in his European coat, bought ready-made at Frankfort. They were walking on one side of the arcade, trying to avoid Levin, who was walking on the other side. Varenka, in her dark dress, in a black hat with a turn-down brim, was walking up and down the whole length of the arcade with a blind Frenchwoman, and, every time she met Kitty, they exchanged friendly glances.\n\n“Mamma, couldn’t I speak to her?” said Kitty, watching her unknown friend, and noticing that she was going up to the spring, and that they might come there together.\n\n“Oh, if you want to so much, I’ll find out about her first and make her acquaintance myself,” answered her mother. “What do you see in her out of the way? A companion, she must be. If you like, I’ll make acquaintance with Madame Stahl; I used to know her belle-sœur,” added the princess, lifting her head haughtily.\n\nKitty knew that the princess was offended that Madame Stahl had seemed to avoid making her acquaintance. Kitty did not insist.\n\n“How wonderfully sweet she is!” she said, gazing at Varenka just as she handed a glass to the Frenchwoman. “Look how natural and sweet it all is.”\n\n“It’s so funny to see your engouements,” said the princess. “No, we’d better go back,” she added, noticing Levin coming towards them with his companion and a German doctor, to whom he was talking very noisily and angrily.\n\nThey turned to go back, when suddenly they heard, not noisy talk, but shouting. Levin, stopping short, was shouting at the doctor, and the doctor, too, was excited. A crowd gathered about them. The princess and Kitty beat a hasty retreat, while the colonel joined the crowd to find out what was the matter.\n\nA few minutes later the colonel overtook them.\n\n“What was it?” inquired the princess.\n\n“Scandalous and disgraceful!” answered the colonel. “The one thing to be dreaded is meeting Russians abroad. That tall gentleman was abusing the doctor, flinging all sorts of insults at him because he wasn’t treating him quite as he liked, and he began waving his stick at him. It’s simply a scandal!”\n\n“Oh, how unpleasant!” said the princess. “Well, and how did it end?”\n\n“Luckily at that point that ... the one in the mushroom hat ... intervened. A Russian lady, I think she is,” said the colonel.\n\n“Mademoiselle Varenka?” asked Kitty.\n\n“Yes, yes. She came to the rescue before anyone; she took the man by the arm and led him away.”\n\n“There, mamma,” said Kitty; “you wonder that I’m enthusiastic about her.”\n\nThe next day, as she watched her unknown friend, Kitty noticed that Mademoiselle Varenka was already on the same terms with Levin and his companion as with her other protégés. She went up to them, entered into conversation with them, and served as interpreter for the woman, who could not speak any foreign language.\n\nKitty began to entreat her mother still more urgently to let her make friends with Varenka. And, disagreeable as it was to the princess to seem to take the first step in wishing to make the acquaintance of Madame Stahl, who thought fit to give herself airs, she made inquiries about Varenka, and, having ascertained particulars about her tending to prove that there could be no harm though little good in the acquaintance, she herself approached Varenka and made acquaintance with her.\n\nChoosing a time when her daughter had gone to the spring, while Varenka had stopped outside the baker’s, the princess went up to her.\n\n“Allow me to make your acquaintance,” she said, with her dignified smile. “My daughter has lost her heart to you,” she said. “Possibly you do not know me. I am....”\n\n“That feeling is more than reciprocal, princess,” Varenka answered hurriedly.\n\n“What a good deed you did yesterday to our poor compatriot!” said the princess.\n\nVarenka flushed a little. “I don’t remember. I don’t think I did anything,” she said.\n\n“Why, you saved that Levin from disagreeable consequences.”\n\n“Yes, sa compagne called me, and I tried to pacify him, he’s very ill, and was dissatisfied with the doctor. I’m used to looking after such invalids.”\n\n“Yes, I’ve heard you live at Mentone with your aunt—I think—Madame Stahl: I used to know her belle-sœur.”\n\n“No, she’s not my aunt. I call her mamma, but I am not related to her; I was brought up by her,” answered Varenka, flushing a little again.\n\nThis was so simply said, and so sweet was the truthful and candid expression of her face, that the princess saw why Kitty had taken such a fancy to Varenka.\n\n“Well, and what’s this Levin going to do?” asked the princess.\n\n“He’s going away,” answered Varenka.\n\nAt that instant Kitty came up from the spring beaming with delight that her mother had become acquainted with her unknown friend.\n\n“Well, see, Kitty, your intense desire to make friends with Mademoiselle....”\n\n“Varenka,” Varenka put in smiling, “that’s what everyone calls me.”\n\nKitty blushed with pleasure, and slowly, without speaking, pressed her new friend’s hand, which did not respond to her pressure, but lay motionless in her hand. The hand did not respond to her pressure, but the face of Mademoiselle Varenka glowed with a soft, glad, though rather mournful smile, that showed large but handsome teeth.\n\n“I have long wished for this too,” she said.\n\n“But you are so busy.”\n\n“Oh, no, I’m not at all busy,” answered Varenka, but at that moment she had to leave her new friends because two little Russian girls, children of an invalid, ran up to her.\n\n“Varenka, mamma’s calling!” they cried.\n\nAnd Varenka went after them.\n\n# Chapter 32\n\nThe particulars which the princess had learned in regard to Varenka’s past and her relations with Madame Stahl were as follows:\n\nMadame Stahl, of whom some people said that she had worried her husband out of his life, while others said it was he who had made her wretched by his immoral behavior, had always been a woman of weak health and enthusiastic temperament. When, after her separation from her husband, she gave birth to her only child, the child had died almost immediately, and the family of Madame Stahl, knowing her sensibility, and fearing the news would kill her, had substituted another child, a baby born the same night and in the same house in Petersburg, the daughter of the chief cook of the Imperial Household. This was Varenka. Madame Stahl learned later on that Varenka was not her own child, but she went on bringing her up, especially as very soon afterwards Varenka had not a relation of her own living. Madame Stahl had now been living more than ten years continuously abroad, in the south, never leaving her couch. And some people said that Madame Stahl had made her social position as a philanthropic, highly religious woman; other people said she really was at heart the highly ethical being, living for nothing but the good of her fellow creatures, which she represented herself to be. No one knew what her faith was—Catholic, Protestant, or Orthodox. But one fact was indubitable—she was in amicable relations with the highest dignitaries of all the churches and sects.\n\nVarenka lived with her all the while abroad, and everyone who knew Madame Stahl knew and liked Mademoiselle Varenka, as everyone called her.\n\nHaving learned all these facts, the princess found nothing to object to in her daughter’s intimacy with Varenka, more especially as Varenka’s breeding and education were of the best—she spoke French and English extremely well—and what was of the most weight, brought a message from Madame Stahl expressing her regret that she was prevented by her ill health from making the acquaintance of the princess.\n\nAfter getting to know Varenka, Kitty became more and more fascinated by her friend, and every day she discovered new virtues in her.\n\nThe princess, hearing that Varenka had a good voice, asked her to come and sing to them in the evening.\n\n“Kitty plays, and we have a piano; not a good one, it’s true, but you will give us so much pleasure,” said the princess with her affected smile, which Kitty disliked particularly just then, because she noticed that Varenka had no inclination to sing. Varenka came, however, in the evening and brought a roll of music with her. The princess had invited Marya Yevgenyevna and her daughter and the colonel.\n\nVarenka seemed quite unaffected by there being persons present she did not know, and she went directly to the piano. She could not accompany herself, but she could sing music at sight very well. Kitty, who played well, accompanied her.\n\n“You have an extraordinary talent,” the princess said to her after Varenka had sung the first song extremely well.\n\nMarya Yevgenyevna and her daughter expressed their thanks and admiration.\n\n“Look,” said the colonel, looking out of the window, “what an audience has collected to listen to you.” There actually was quite a considerable crowd under the windows.\n\n“I am very glad it gives you pleasure,” Varenka answered simply.\n\nKitty looked with pride at her friend. She was enchanted by her talent, and her voice, and her face, but most of all by her manner, by the way Varenka obviously thought nothing of her singing and was quite unmoved by their praises. She seemed only to be asking: “Am I to sing again, or is that enough?”\n\n“If it had been I,” thought Kitty, “how proud I should have been! How delighted I should have been to see that crowd under the windows! But she’s utterly unmoved by it. Her only motive is to avoid refusing and to please mamma. What is there in her? What is it gives her the power to look down on everything, to be calm independently of everything? How I should like to know it and to learn it of her!” thought Kitty, gazing into her serene face. The princess asked Varenka to sing again, and Varenka sang another song, also smoothly, distinctly, and well, standing erect at the piano and beating time on it with her thin, dark-skinned hand.\n\nThe next song in the book was an Italian one. Kitty played the opening bars, and looked round at Varenka.\n\n“Let’s skip that,” said Varenka, flushing a little. Kitty let her eyes rest on Varenka’s face, with a look of dismay and inquiry.\n\n“Very well, the next one,” she said hurriedly, turning over the pages, and at once feeling that there was something connected with the song.\n\n“No,” answered Varenka with a smile, laying her hand on the music, “no, let’s have that one.” And she sang it just as quietly, as coolly, and as well as the others.\n\nWhen she had finished, they all thanked her again, and went off to tea. Kitty and Varenka went out into the little garden that adjoined the house.\n\n“Am I right, that you have some reminiscences connected with that song?” said Kitty. “Don’t tell me,” she added hastily, “only say if I’m right.”\n\n“No, why not? I’ll tell you simply,” said Varenka, and, without waiting for a reply, she went on: “Yes, it brings up memories, once painful ones. I cared for someone once, and I used to sing him that song.”\n\nKitty with big, wide-open eyes gazed silently, sympathetically at Varenka.\n\n“I cared for him, and he cared for me; but his mother did not wish it, and he married another girl. He’s living now not far from us, and I see him sometimes. You didn’t think I had a love story too,” she said, and there was a faint gleam in her handsome face of that fire which Kitty felt must once have glowed all over her.\n\n“I didn’t think so? Why, if I were a man, I could never care for anyone else after knowing you. Only I can’t understand how he could, to please his mother, forget you and make you unhappy; he had no heart.”\n\n“Oh, no, he’s a very good man, and I’m not unhappy; quite the contrary, I’m very happy. Well, so we shan’t be singing any more now,” she added, turning towards the house.\n\n“How good you are! how good you are!” cried Kitty, and stopping her, she kissed her. “If I could only be even a little like you!”\n\n“Why should you be like anyone? You’re nice as you are,” said Varenka, smiling her gentle, weary smile.\n\n“No, I’m not nice at all. Come, tell me.... Stop a minute, let’s sit down,” said Kitty, making her sit down again beside her. “Tell me, isn’t it humiliating to think that a man has disdained your love, that he hasn’t cared for it?...”\n\n“But he didn’t disdain it; I believe he cared for me, but he was a dutiful son....”\n\n“Yes, but if it hadn’t been on account of his mother, if it had been his own doing?...” said Kitty, feeling she was giving away her secret, and that her face, burning with the flush of shame, had betrayed her already.\n\n“In that case he would have done wrong, and I should not have regretted him,” answered Varenka, evidently realizing that they were now talking not of her, but of Kitty.\n\n“But the humiliation,” said Kitty, “the humiliation one can never forget, can never forget,” she said, remembering her look at the last ball during the pause in the music.\n\n“Where is the humiliation? Why, you did nothing wrong?”\n\n“Worse than wrong—shameful.”\n\nVarenka shook her head and laid her hand on Kitty’s hand.\n\n“Why, what is there shameful?” she said. “You didn’t tell a man, who didn’t care for you, that you loved him, did you?”\n\n“Of course not; I never said a word, but he knew it. No, no, there are looks, there are ways; I can’t forget it, if I live a hundred years.”\n\n“Why so? I don’t understand. The whole point is whether you love him now or not,” said Varenka, who called everything by its name.\n\n“I hate him; I can’t forgive myself.”\n\n“Why, what for?”\n\n“The shame, the humiliation!”\n\n“Oh! if everyone were as sensitive as you are!” said Varenka. “There isn’t a girl who hasn’t been through the same. And it’s all so unimportant.”\n\n“Why, what is important?” said Kitty, looking into her face with inquisitive wonder.\n\n“Oh, there’s so much that’s important,” said Varenka, smiling.\n\n“Why, what?”\n\n“Oh, so much that’s more important,” answered Varenka, not knowing what to say. But at that instant they heard the princess’s voice from the window. “Kitty, it’s cold! Either get a shawl, or come indoors.”\n\n“It really is time to go in!” said Varenka, getting up. “I have to go on to Madame Berthe’s; she asked me to.”\n\nKitty held her by the hand, and with passionate curiosity and entreaty her eyes asked her: “What is it, what is this of such importance that gives you such tranquillity? You know, tell me!” But Varenka did not even know what Kitty’s eyes were asking her. She merely thought that she had to go to see Madame Berthe too that evening, and to make haste home in time for maman’s tea at twelve o’clock. She went indoors, collected her music, and saying good-bye to everyone, was about to go.\n\n“Allow me to see you home,” said the colonel.\n\n“Yes, how can you go alone at night like this?” chimed in the princess. “Anyway, I’ll send Parasha.”\n\nKitty saw that Varenka could hardly restrain a smile at the idea that she needed an escort.\n\n“No, I always go about alone and nothing ever happens to me,” she said, taking her hat. And kissing Kitty once more, without saying what was important, she stepped out courageously with the music under her arm and vanished into the twilight of the summer night, bearing away with her her secret of what was important and what gave her the calm and dignity so much to be envied.\n\n# Chapter 33\n\nKitty made the acquaintance of Madame Stahl too, and this acquaintance, together with her friendship with Varenka, did not merely exercise a great influence on her, it also comforted her in her mental distress. She found this comfort through a completely new world being opened to her by means of this acquaintance, a world having nothing in common with her past, an exalted, noble world, from the height of which she could contemplate her past calmly. It was revealed to her that besides the instinctive life to which Kitty had given herself up hitherto there was a spiritual life. This life was disclosed in religion, but a religion having nothing in common with that one which Kitty had known from childhood, and which found expression in litanies and all-night services at the Widow’s Home, where one might meet one’s friends, and in learning by heart Slavonic texts with the priest. This was a lofty, mysterious religion connected with a whole series of noble thoughts and feelings, which one could do more than merely believe because one was told to, which one could love.\n\nKitty found all this out not from words. Madame Stahl talked to Kitty as to a charming child that one looks on with pleasure as on the memory of one’s youth, and only once she said in passing that in all human sorrows nothing gives comfort but love and faith, and that in the sight of Christ’s compassion for us no sorrow is trifling—and immediately talked of other things. But in every gesture of Madame Stahl, in every word, in every heavenly—as Kitty called it—look, and above all in the whole story of her life, which she heard from Varenka, Kitty recognized that something “that was important,” of which, till then, she had known nothing.\n\nYet, elevated as Madame Stahl’s character was, touching as was her story, and exalted and moving as was her speech, Kitty could not help detecting in her some traits which perplexed her. She noticed that when questioning her about her family, Madame Stahl had smiled contemptuously, which was not in accord with Christian meekness. She noticed, too, that when she had found a Catholic priest with her, Madame Stahl had studiously kept her face in the shadow of the lamp-shade and had smiled in a peculiar way. Trivial as these two observations were, they perplexed her, and she had her doubts as to Madame Stahl. But on the other hand Varenka, alone in the world, without friends or relations, with a melancholy disappointment in the past, desiring nothing, regretting nothing, was just that perfection of which Kitty dared hardly dream. In Varenka she realized that one has but to forget oneself and love others, and one will be calm, happy, and noble. And that was what Kitty longed to be. Seeing now clearly what was the most important, Kitty was not satisfied with being enthusiastic over it; she at once gave herself up with her whole soul to the new life that was opening to her. From Varenka’s accounts of the doings of Madame Stahl and other people whom she mentioned, Kitty had already constructed the plan of her own future life. She would, like Madame Stahl’s niece, Aline, of whom Varenka had talked to her a great deal, seek out those who were in trouble, wherever she might be living, help them as far as she could, give them the Gospel, read the Gospel to the sick, to criminals, to the dying. The idea of reading the Gospel to criminals, as Aline did, particularly fascinated Kitty. But all these were secret dreams, of which Kitty did not talk either to her mother or to Varenka.\n\nWhile awaiting the time for carrying out her plans on a large scale, however, Kitty, even then at the springs, where there were so many people ill and unhappy, readily found a chance for practicing her new principles in imitation of Varenka.\n\nAt first the princess noticed nothing but that Kitty was much under the influence of her engouement, as she called it, for Madame Stahl, and still more for Varenka. She saw that Kitty did not merely imitate Varenka in her conduct, but unconsciously imitated her in her manner of walking, of talking, of blinking her eyes. But later on the princess noticed that, apart from this adoration, some kind of serious spiritual change was taking place in her daughter.\n\nThe princess saw that in the evenings Kitty read a French testament that Madame Stahl had given her—a thing she had never done before; that she avoided society acquaintances and associated with the sick people who were under Varenka’s protection, and especially one poor family, that of a sick painter, Petrov. Kitty was unmistakably proud of playing the part of a sister of mercy in that family. All this was well enough, and the princess had nothing to say against it, especially as Petrov’s wife was a perfectly nice sort of woman, and that the German princess, noticing Kitty’s devotion, praised her, calling her an angel of consolation. All this would have been very well, if there had been no exaggeration. But the princess saw that her daughter was rushing into extremes, and so indeed she told her.\n\n“Il ne faut jamais rien outrer,” she said to her.\n\nHer daughter made her no reply, only in her heart she thought that one could not talk about exaggeration where Christianity was concerned. What exaggeration could there be in the practice of a doctrine wherein one was bidden to turn the other cheek when one was smitten, and give one’s cloak if one’s coat were taken? But the princess disliked this exaggeration, and disliked even more the fact that she felt her daughter did not care to show her all her heart. Kitty did in fact conceal her new views and feelings from her mother. She concealed them not because she did not respect or did not love her mother, but simply because she was her mother. She would have revealed them to anyone sooner than to her mother.\n\n“How is it Anna Pavlovna’s not been to see us for so long?” the princess said one day of Madame Petrova. “I’ve asked her, but she seems put out about something.”\n\n“No, I’ve not noticed it, maman,” said Kitty, flushing hotly.\n\n“Is it long since you went to see them?”\n\n“We’re meaning to make an expedition to the mountains tomorrow,” answered Kitty.\n\n“Well, you can go,” answered the princess, gazing at her daughter’s embarrassed face and trying to guess the cause of her embarrassment.\n\nThat day Varenka came to dinner and told them that Anna Pavlovna had changed her mind and given up the expedition for the morrow. And the princess noticed again that Kitty reddened.\n\n“Kitty, haven’t you had some misunderstanding with the Petrovs?” said the princess, when they were left alone. “Why has she given up sending the children and coming to see us?”\n\nKitty answered that nothing had happened between them, and that she could not tell why Anna Pavlovna seemed displeased with her. Kitty answered perfectly truly. She did not know the reason Anna Pavlovna had changed to her, but she guessed it. She guessed at something which she could not tell her mother, which she did not put into words to herself. It was one of those things which one knows but which one can never speak of even to oneself, so terrible and shameful would it be to be mistaken.\n\nAgain and again she went over in her memory all her relations with the family. She remembered the simple delight expressed on the round, good-humored face of Anna Pavlovna at their meetings; she remembered their secret confabulations about the invalid, their plots to draw him away from the work which was forbidden him, and to get him out-of-doors; the devotion of the youngest boy, who used to call her “my Kitty,” and would not go to bed without her. How nice it all was! Then she recalled the thin, terribly thin figure of Petrov, with his long neck, in his brown coat, his scant, curly hair, his questioning blue eyes that were so terrible to Kitty at first, and his painful attempts to seem hearty and lively in her presence. She recalled the efforts she had made at first to overcome the repugnance she felt for him, as for all consumptive people, and the pains it had cost her to think of things to say to him. She recalled the timid, softened look with which he gazed at her, and the strange feeling of compassion and awkwardness, and later of a sense of her own goodness, which she had felt at it. How nice it all was! But all that was at first. Now, a few days ago, everything was suddenly spoiled. Anna Pavlovna had met Kitty with affected cordiality, and had kept continual watch on her and on her husband.\n\nCould that touching pleasure he showed when she came near be the cause of Anna Pavlovna’s coolness?\n\n“Yes,” she mused, “there was something unnatural about Anna Pavlovna, and utterly unlike her good nature, when she said angrily the day before yesterday: ‘There, he will keep waiting for you; he wouldn’t drink his coffee without you, though he’s grown so dreadfully weak.’”\n\n“Yes, perhaps, too, she didn’t like it when I gave him the rug. It was all so simple, but he took it so awkwardly, and was so long thanking me, that I felt awkward too. And then that portrait of me he did so well. And most of all that look of confusion and tenderness! Yes, yes, that’s it!” Kitty repeated to herself with horror. “No, it can’t be, it oughtn’t to be! He’s so much to be pitied!” she said to herself directly after.\n\nThis doubt poisoned the charm of her new life.\n\n# Chapter 34\n\nBefore the end of the course of drinking the waters, Prince Shtcherbatsky, who had gone on from Carlsbad to Baden and Kissingen to Russian friends—to get a breath of Russian air, as he said—came back to his wife and daughter.\n\nThe views of the prince and of the princess on life abroad were completely opposed. The princess thought everything delightful, and in spite of her established position in Russian society, she tried abroad to be like a European fashionable lady, which she was not—for the simple reason that she was a typical Russian gentlewoman; and so she was affected, which did not altogether suit her. The prince, on the contrary, thought everything foreign detestable, got sick of European life, kept to his Russian habits, and purposely tried to show himself abroad less European than he was in reality.\n\nThe prince returned thinner, with the skin hanging in loose bags on his cheeks, but in the most cheerful frame of mind. His good humor was even greater when he saw Kitty completely recovered. The news of Kitty’s friendship with Madame Stahl and Varenka, and the reports the princess gave him of some kind of change she had noticed in Kitty, troubled the prince and aroused his habitual feeling of jealousy of everything that drew his daughter away from him, and a dread that his daughter might have got out of the reach of his influence into regions inaccessible to him. But these unpleasant matters were all drowned in the sea of kindliness and good humor which was always within him, and more so than ever since his course of Carlsbad waters.\n\nThe day after his arrival the prince, in his long overcoat, with his Russian wrinkles and baggy cheeks propped up by a starched collar, set off with his daughter to the spring in the greatest good humor.\n\nIt was a lovely morning: the bright, cheerful houses with their little gardens, the sight of the red-faced, red-armed, beer-drinking German waitresses, working away merrily, did the heart good. But the nearer they got to the springs the oftener they met sick people; and their appearance seemed more pitiable than ever among the everyday conditions of prosperous German life. Kitty was no longer struck by this contrast. The bright sun, the brilliant green of the foliage, the strains of the music were for her the natural setting of all these familiar faces, with their changes to greater emaciation or to convalescence, for which she watched. But to the prince the brightness and gaiety of the June morning, and the sound of the orchestra playing a gay waltz then in fashion, and above all, the appearance of the healthy attendants, seemed something unseemly and monstrous, in conjunction with these slowly moving, dying figures gathered together from all parts of Europe. In spite of his feeling of pride and, as it were, of the return of youth, with his favorite daughter on his arm, he felt awkward, and almost ashamed of his vigorous step and his sturdy, stout limbs. He felt almost like a man not dressed in a crowd.\n\n“Present me to your new friends,” he said to his daughter, squeezing her hand with his elbow. “I like even your horrid Soden for making you so well again. Only it’s melancholy, very melancholy here. Who’s that?”\n\nKitty mentioned the names of all the people they met, with some of whom she was acquainted and some not. At the entrance of the garden they met the blind lady, Madame Berthe, with her guide, and the prince was delighted to see the old Frenchwoman’s face light up when she heard Kitty’s voice. She at once began talking to him with French exaggerated politeness, applauding him for having such a delightful daughter, extolling Kitty to the skies before her face, and calling her a treasure, a pearl, and a consoling angel.\n\n“Well, she’s the second angel, then,” said the prince, smiling. “she calls Mademoiselle Varenka angel number one.”\n\n“Oh! Mademoiselle Varenka, she’s a real angel, allez,” Madame Berthe assented.\n\nIn the arcade they met Varenka herself. She was walking rapidly towards them carrying an elegant red bag.\n\n“Here is papa come,” Kitty said to her.\n\nVarenka made—simply and naturally as she did everything—a movement between a bow and a curtsey, and immediately began talking to the prince, without shyness, naturally, as she talked to everyone.\n\n“Of course I know you; I know you very well,” the prince said to her with a smile, in which Kitty detected with joy that her father liked her friend. “Where are you off to in such haste?”\n\n“Maman’s here,” she said, turning to Kitty. “She has not slept all night, and the doctor advised her to go out. I’m taking her her work.”\n\n“So that’s angel number one?” said the prince when Varenka had gone on.\n\nKitty saw that her father had meant to make fun of Varenka, but that he could not do it because he liked her.\n\n“Come, so we shall see all your friends,” he went on, “even Madame Stahl, if she deigns to recognize me.”\n\n“Why, did you know her, papa?” Kitty asked apprehensively, catching the gleam of irony that kindled in the prince’s eyes at the mention of Madame Stahl.\n\n“I used to know her husband, and her too a little, before she’d joined the Pietists.”\n\n“What is a Pietist, papa?” asked Kitty, dismayed to find that what she prized so highly in Madame Stahl had a name.\n\n“I don’t quite know myself. I only know that she thanks God for everything, for every misfortune, and thanks God too that her husband died. And that’s rather droll, as they didn’t get on together.”\n\n“Who’s that? What a piteous face!” he asked, noticing a sick man of medium height sitting on a bench, wearing a brown overcoat and white trousers that fell in strange folds about his long, fleshless legs. This man lifted his straw hat, showed his scanty curly hair and high forehead, painfully reddened by the pressure of the hat.\n\n“That’s Petrov, an artist,” answered Kitty, blushing. “And that’s his wife,” she added, indicating Anna Pavlovna, who, as though on purpose, at the very instant they approached walked away after a child that had run off along a path.\n\n“Poor fellow! and what a nice face he has!” said the prince. “Why don’t you go up to him? He wanted to speak to you.”\n\n“Well, let us go, then,” said Kitty, turning round resolutely. “How are you feeling today?” she asked Petrov.\n\nPetrov got up, leaning on his stick, and looked shyly at the prince.\n\n“This is my daughter,” said the prince. “Let me introduce myself.”\n\nThe painter bowed and smiled, showing his strangely dazzling white teeth.\n\n“We expected you yesterday, princess,” he said to Kitty. He staggered as he said this, and then repeated the motion, trying to make it seem as if it had been intentional.\n\n“I meant to come, but Varenka said that Anna Pavlovna sent word you were not going.”\n\n“Not going!” said Petrov, blushing, and immediately beginning to cough, and his eyes sought his wife. “Anita! Anita!” he said loudly, and the swollen veins stood out like cords on his thin white neck.\n\nAnna Pavlovna came up.\n\n“So you sent word to the princess that we weren’t going!” he whispered to her angrily, losing his voice.\n\n“Good morning, princess,” said Anna Pavlovna, with an assumed smile utterly unlike her former manner. “Very glad to make your acquaintance,” she said to the prince. “You’ve long been expected, prince.”\n\n“What did you send word to the princess that we weren’t going for?” the artist whispered hoarsely once more, still more angrily, obviously exasperated that his voice failed him so that he could not give his words the expression he would have liked to.\n\n“Oh, mercy on us! I thought we weren’t going,” his wife answered crossly.\n\n“What, when....” He coughed and waved his hand. The prince took off his hat and moved away with his daughter.\n\n“Ah! ah!” he sighed deeply. “Oh, poor things!”\n\n“Yes, papa,” answered Kitty. “And you must know they’ve three children, no servant, and scarcely any means. He gets something from the Academy,” she went on briskly, trying to drown the distress that the queer change in Anna Pavlovna’s manner to her had aroused in her.\n\n“Oh, here’s Madame Stahl,” said Kitty, indicating an invalid carriage, where, propped on pillows, something in gray and blue was lying under a sunshade. This was Madame Stahl. Behind her stood the gloomy, healthy-looking German workman who pushed the carriage. Close by was standing a flaxen-headed Swedish count, whom Kitty knew by name. Several invalids were lingering near the low carriage, staring at the lady as though she were some curiosity.\n\nThe prince went up to her, and Kitty detected that disconcerting gleam of irony in his eyes. He went up to Madame Stahl, and addressed her with extreme courtesy and affability in that excellent French that so few speak nowadays.\n\n“I don’t know if you remember me, but I must recall myself to thank you for your kindness to my daughter,” he said, taking off his hat and not putting it on again.\n\n“Prince Alexander Shtcherbatsky,” said Madame Stahl, lifting upon him her heavenly eyes, in which Kitty discerned a look of annoyance. “Delighted! I have taken a great fancy to your daughter.”\n\n“You are still in weak health?”\n\n“Yes; I’m used to it,” said Madame Stahl, and she introduced the prince to the Swedish count.\n\n“You are scarcely changed at all,” the prince said to her. “It’s ten or eleven years since I had the honor of seeing you.”\n\n“Yes; God sends the cross and sends the strength to bear it. Often one wonders what is the goal of this life?... The other side!” she said angrily to Varenka, who had rearranged the rug over her feet not to her satisfaction.\n\n“To do good, probably,” said the prince with a twinkle in his eye.\n\n“That is not for us to judge,” said Madame Stahl, perceiving the shade of expression on the prince’s face. “So you will send me that book, dear count? I’m very grateful to you,” she said to the young Swede.\n\n“Ah!” cried the prince, catching sight of the Moscow colonel standing near, and with a bow to Madame Stahl he walked away with his daughter and the Moscow colonel, who joined them.\n\n“That’s our aristocracy, prince!” the Moscow colonel said with ironical intention. He cherished a grudge against Madame Stahl for not making his acquaintance.\n\n“She’s just the same,” replied the prince.\n\n“Did you know her before her illness, prince—that’s to say before she took to her bed?”\n\n“Yes. She took to her bed before my eyes,” said the prince.\n\n“They say it’s ten years since she has stood on her feet.”\n\n“She doesn’t stand up because her legs are too short. She’s a very bad figure.”\n\n“Papa, it’s not possible!” cried Kitty.\n\n“That’s what wicked tongues say, my darling. And your Varenka catches it too,” he added. “Oh, these invalid ladies!”\n\n“Oh, no, papa!” Kitty objected warmly. “Varenka worships her. And then she does so much good! Ask anyone! Everyone knows her and Aline Stahl.”\n\n“Perhaps so,” said the prince, squeezing her hand with his elbow; “but it’s better when one does good so that you may ask everyone and no one knows.”\n\nKitty did not answer, not because she had nothing to say, but because she did not care to reveal her secret thoughts even to her father. But, strange to say, although she had so made up her mind not to be influenced by her father’s views, not to let him into her inmost sanctuary, she felt that the heavenly image of Madame Stahl, which she had carried for a whole month in her heart, had vanished, never to return, just as the fantastic figure made up of some clothes thrown down at random vanishes when one sees that it is only some garment lying there. All that was left was a woman with short legs, who lay down because she had a bad figure, and worried patient Varenka for not arranging her rug to her liking. And by no effort of the imagination could Kitty bring back the former Madame Stahl.\n\n# Chapter 35\n\nThe prince communicated his good humor to his own family and his friends, and even to the German landlord in whose rooms the Shtcherbatskys were staying.\n\nOn coming back with Kitty from the springs, the prince, who had asked the colonel, and Marya Yevgenyevna, and Varenka all to come and have coffee with them, gave orders for a table and chairs to be taken into the garden under the chestnut tree, and lunch to be laid there. The landlord and the servants, too, grew brisker under the influence of his good spirits. They knew his open-handedness; and half an hour later the invalid doctor from Hamburg, who lived on the top floor, looked enviously out of the window at the merry party of healthy Russians assembled under the chestnut tree. In the trembling circles of shadow cast by the leaves, at a table, covered with a white cloth, and set with coffeepot, bread-and-butter, cheese, and cold game, sat the princess in a high cap with lilac ribbons, distributing cups and bread-and-butter. At the other end sat the prince, eating heartily, and talking loudly and merrily. The prince had spread out near him his purchases, carved boxes, and knick-knacks, paper-knives of all sorts, of which he bought a heap at every watering-place, and bestowed them upon everyone, including Lieschen, the servant girl, and the landlord, with whom he jested in his comically bad German, assuring him that it was not the water had cured Kitty, but his splendid cookery, especially his plum soup. The princess laughed at her husband for his Russian ways, but she was more lively and good-humored than she had been all the while she had been at the waters. The colonel smiled, as he always did, at the prince’s jokes, but as far as regards Europe, of which he believed himself to be making a careful study, he took the princess’s side. The simple-hearted Marya Yevgenyevna simply roared with laughter at everything absurd the prince said, and his jokes made Varenka helpless with feeble but infectious laughter, which was something Kitty had never seen before.\n\nKitty was glad of all this, but she could not be light-hearted. She could not solve the problem her father had unconsciously set her by his good-humored view of her friends, and of the life that had so attracted her. To this doubt there was joined the change in her relations with the Petrovs, which had been so conspicuously and unpleasantly marked that morning. Everyone was good-humored, but Kitty could not feel good-humored, and this increased her distress. She felt a feeling such as she had known in childhood, when she had been shut in her room as a punishment, and had heard her sisters’ merry laughter outside.\n\n“Well, but what did you buy this mass of things for?” said the princess, smiling, and handing her husband a cup of coffee.\n\n“One goes for a walk, one looks in a shop, and they ask you to buy. ‘Erlaucht, Durchlaucht?’ Directly they say ‘Durchlaucht,’ I can’t hold out. I lose ten thalers.”\n\n“It’s simply from boredom,” said the princess.\n\n“Of course it is. Such boredom, my dear, that one doesn’t know what to do with oneself.”\n\n“How can you be bored, prince? There’s so much that’s interesting now in Germany,” said Marya Yevgenyevna.\n\n“But I know everything that’s interesting: the plum soup I know, and the pea sausages I know. I know everything.”\n\n“No, you may say what you like, prince, there’s the interest of their institutions,” said the colonel.\n\n“But what is there interesting about it? They’re all as pleased as brass halfpence. They’ve conquered everybody, and why am I to be pleased at that? I haven’t conquered anyone; and I’m obliged to take off my own boots, yes, and put them away too; in the morning, get up and dress at once, and go to the dining-room to drink bad tea! How different it is at home! You get up in no haste, you get cross, grumble a little, and come round again. You’ve time to think things over, and no hurry.”\n\n“But time’s money, you forget that,” said the colonel.\n\n“Time, indeed, that depends! Why, there’s time one would give a month of for sixpence, and time you wouldn’t give half an hour of for any money. Isn’t that so, Katinka? What is it? why are you so depressed?”\n\n“I’m not depressed.”\n\n“Where are you off to? Stay a little longer,” he said to Varenka.\n\n“I must be going home,” said Varenka, getting up, and again she went off into a giggle. When she had recovered, she said good-bye, and went into the house to get her hat.\n\nKitty followed her. Even Varenka struck her as different. She was not worse, but different from what she had fancied her before.\n\n“Oh, dear! it’s a long while since I’ve laughed so much!” said Varenka, gathering up her parasol and her bag. “How nice he is, your father!”\n\nKitty did not speak.\n\n“When shall I see you again?” asked Varenka.\n\n“Mamma meant to go and see the Petrovs. Won’t you be there?” said Kitty, to try Varenka.\n\n“Yes,” answered Varenka. “They’re getting ready to go away, so I promised to help them pack.”\n\n“Well, I’ll come too, then.”\n\n“No, why should you?”\n\n“Why not? why not? why not?” said Kitty, opening her eyes wide, and clutching at Varenka’s parasol, so as not to let her go. “No, wait a minute; why not?”\n\n“Oh, nothing; your father has come, and besides, they will feel awkward at your helping.”\n\n“No, tell me why you don’t want me to be often at the Petrovs’. You don’t want me to—why not?”\n\n“I didn’t say that,” said Varenka quietly.\n\n“No, please tell me!”\n\n“Tell you everything?” asked Varenka.\n\n“Everything, everything!” Kitty assented.\n\n“Well, there’s really nothing of any consequence; only that Mihail Alexeyevitch” (that was the artist’s name) “had meant to leave earlier, and now he doesn’t want to go away,” said Varenka, smiling.\n\n“Well, well!” Kitty urged impatiently, looking darkly at Varenka.\n\n“Well, and for some reason Anna Pavlovna told him that he didn’t want to go because you are here. Of course, that was nonsense; but there was a dispute over it—over you. You know how irritable these sick people are.”\n\nKitty, scowling more than ever, kept silent, and Varenka went on speaking alone, trying to soften or soothe her, and seeing a storm coming—she did not know whether of tears or of words.\n\n“So you’d better not go.... You understand; you won’t be offended?...”\n\n“And it serves me right! And it serves me right!” Kitty cried quickly, snatching the parasol out of Varenka’s hand, and looking past her friend’s face.\n\nVarenka felt inclined to smile, looking at her childish fury, but she was afraid of wounding her.\n\n“How does it serve you right? I don’t understand,” she said.\n\n“It serves me right, because it was all sham; because it was all done on purpose, and not from the heart. What business had I to interfere with outsiders? And so it’s come about that I’m a cause of quarrel, and that I’ve done what nobody asked me to do. Because it was all a sham! a sham! a sham!...”\n\n“A sham! with what object?” said Varenka gently.\n\n“Oh, it’s so idiotic! so hateful! There was no need whatever for me.... Nothing but sham!” she said, opening and shutting the parasol.\n\n“But with what object?”\n\n“To seem better to people, to myself, to God; to deceive everyone. No! now I won’t descend to that. I’ll be bad; but anyway not a liar, a cheat.”\n\n“But who is a cheat?” said Varenka reproachfully. “You speak as if....”\n\nBut Kitty was in one of her gusts of fury, and she would not let her finish.\n\n“I don’t talk about you, not about you at all. You’re perfection. Yes, yes, I know you’re all perfection; but what am I to do if I’m bad? This would never have been if I weren’t bad. So let me be what I am. I won’t be a sham. What have I to do with Anna Pavlovna? Let them go their way, and me go mine. I can’t be different.... And yet it’s not that, it’s not that.”\n\n“What is not that?” asked Varenka in bewilderment.\n\n“Everything. I can’t act except from the heart, and you act from principle. I liked you simply, but you most likely only wanted to save me, to improve me.”\n\n“You are unjust,” said Varenka.\n\n“But I’m not speaking of other people, I’m speaking of myself.”\n\n“Kitty,” they heard her mother’s voice, “come here, show papa your necklace.”\n\nKitty, with a haughty air, without making peace with her friend, took the necklace in a little box from the table and went to her mother.\n\n“What’s the matter? Why are you so red?” her mother and father said to her with one voice.\n\n“Nothing,” she answered. “I’ll be back directly,” and she ran back.\n\n“She’s still here,” she thought. “What am I to say to her? Oh, dear! what have I done, what have I said? Why was I rude to her? What am I to do? What am I to say to her?” thought Kitty, and she stopped in the doorway.\n\nVarenka in her hat and with the parasol in her hands was sitting at the table examining the spring which Kitty had broken. She lifted her head.\n\n“Varenka, forgive me, do forgive me,” whispered Kitty, going up to her. “I don’t remember what I said. I....”\n\n“I really didn’t mean to hurt you,” said Varenka, smiling.\n\nPeace was made. But with her father’s coming all the world in which she had been living was transformed for Kitty. She did not give up everything she had learned, but she became aware that she had deceived herself in supposing she could be what she wanted to be. Her eyes were, it seemed, opened; she felt all the difficulty of maintaining herself without hypocrisy and self-conceit on the pinnacle to which she had wished to mount. Moreover, she became aware of all the dreariness of the world of sorrow, of sick and dying people, in which she had been living. The efforts she had made to like it seemed to her intolerable, and she felt a longing to get back quickly into the fresh air, to Russia, to Ergushovo, where, as she knew from letters, her sister Dolly had already gone with her children.\n\nBut her affection for Varenka did not wane. As she said good-bye, Kitty begged her to come to them in Russia.\n\n“I’ll come when you get married,” said Varenka.\n\n“I shall never marry.”\n\n“Well, then, I shall never come.”\n\n“Well, then, I shall be married simply for that. Mind now, remember your promise,” said Kitty.\n\nThe doctor’s prediction was fulfilled. Kitty returned home to Russia cured. She was not so gay and thoughtless as before, but she was serene. Her Moscow troubles had become a memory to her.\n\n# PART THREE Chapter 1\n\nSergey Ivanovitch Koznishev wanted a rest from mental work, and instead of going abroad as he usually did, he came towards the end of May to stay in the country with his brother. In his judgment the best sort of life was a country life. He had come now to enjoy such a life at his brother’s. Konstantin Levin was very glad to have him, especially as he did not expect his brother Nikolay that summer. But in spite of his affection and respect for Sergey Ivanovitch, Konstantin Levin was uncomfortable with his brother in the country. It made him uncomfortable, and it positively annoyed him to see his brother’s attitude to the country. To Konstantin Levin the country was the background of life, that is of pleasures, endeavors, labor. To Sergey Ivanovitch the country meant on one hand rest from work, on the other a valuable antidote to the corrupt influences of town, which he took with satisfaction and a sense of its utility. To Konstantin Levin the country was good first because it afforded a field for labor, of the usefulness of which there could be no doubt. To Sergey Ivanovitch the country was particularly good, because there it was possible and fitting to do nothing. Moreover, Sergey Ivanovitch’s attitude to the peasants rather piqued Konstantin. Sergey Ivanovitch used to say that he knew and liked the peasantry, and he often talked to the peasants, which he knew how to do without affectation or condescension, and from every such conversation he would deduce general conclusions in favor of the peasantry and in confirmation of his knowing them. Konstantin Levin did not like such an attitude to the peasants. To Konstantin the peasant was simply the chief partner in their common labor, and in spite of all the respect and the love, almost like that of kinship, he had for the peasant—sucked in probably, as he said himself, with the milk of his peasant nurse—still as a fellow-worker with him, while sometimes enthusiastic over the vigor, gentleness, and justice of these men, he was very often, when their common labors called for other qualities, exasperated with the peasant for his carelessness, lack of method, drunkenness, and lying. If he had been asked whether he liked or didn’t like the peasants, Konstantin Levin would have been absolutely at a loss what to reply. He liked and did not like the peasants, just as he liked and did not like men in general. Of course, being a good-hearted man, he liked men rather than he disliked them, and so too with the peasants. But like or dislike “the people” as something apart he could not, not only because he lived with “the people,” and all his interests were bound up with theirs, but also because he regarded himself as a part of “the people,” did not see any special qualities or failings distinguishing himself and “the people,” and could not contrast himself with them. Moreover, although he had lived so long in the closest relations with the peasants, as farmer and arbitrator, and what was more, as adviser (the peasants trusted him, and for thirty miles round they would come to ask his advice), he had no definite views of “the people,” and would have been as much at a loss to answer the question whether he knew “the people” as the question whether he liked them. For him to say he knew the peasantry would have been the same as to say he knew men. He was continually watching and getting to know people of all sorts, and among them peasants, whom he regarded as good and interesting people, and he was continually observing new points in them, altering his former views of them and forming new ones. With Sergey Ivanovitch it was quite the contrary. Just as he liked and praised a country life in comparison with the life he did not like, so too he liked the peasantry in contradistinction to the class of men he did not like, and so too he knew the peasantry as something distinct from and opposed to men generally. In his methodical brain there were distinctly formulated certain aspects of peasant life, deduced partly from that life itself, but chiefly from contrast with other modes of life. He never changed his opinion of the peasantry and his sympathetic attitude towards them.\n\nIn the discussions that arose between the brothers on their views of the peasantry, Sergey Ivanovitch always got the better of his brother, precisely because Sergey Ivanovitch had definite ideas about the peasant—his character, his qualities, and his tastes. Konstantin Levin had no definite and unalterable idea on the subject, and so in their arguments Konstantin was readily convicted of contradicting himself.\n\nIn Sergey Ivanovitch’s eyes his younger brother was a capital fellow, with his heart in the right place (as he expressed it in French), but with a mind which, though fairly quick, was too much influenced by the impressions of the moment, and consequently filled with contradictions. With all the condescension of an elder brother he sometimes explained to him the true import of things, but he derived little satisfaction from arguing with him because he got the better of him too easily.\n\nKonstantin Levin regarded his brother as a man of immense intellect and culture, as generous in the highest sense of the word, and possessed of a special faculty for working for the public good. But in the depths of his heart, the older he became, and the more intimately he knew his brother, the more and more frequently the thought struck him that this faculty of working for the public good, of which he felt himself utterly devoid, was possibly not so much a quality as a lack of something—not a lack of good, honest, noble desires and tastes, but a lack of vital force, of what is called heart, of that impulse which drives a man to choose someone out of the innumerable paths of life, and to care only for that one. The better he knew his brother, the more he noticed that Sergey Ivanovitch, and many other people who worked for the public welfare, were not led by an impulse of the heart to care for the public good, but reasoned from intellectual considerations that it was a right thing to take interest in public affairs, and consequently took interest in them. Levin was confirmed in this generalization by observing that his brother did not take questions affecting the public welfare or the question of the immortality of the soul a bit more to heart than he did chess problems, or the ingenious construction of a new machine.\n\nBesides this, Konstantin Levin was not at his ease with his brother, because in summer in the country Levin was continually busy with work on the land, and the long summer day was not long enough for him to get through all he had to do, while Sergey Ivanovitch was taking a holiday. But though he was taking a holiday now, that is to say, he was doing no writing, he was so used to intellectual activity that he liked to put into concise and eloquent shape the ideas that occurred to him, and liked to have someone to listen to him. His most usual and natural listener was his brother. And so in spite of the friendliness and directness of their relations, Konstantin felt an awkwardness in leaving him alone. Sergey Ivanovitch liked to stretch himself on the grass in the sun, and to lie so, basking and chatting lazily.\n\n“You wouldn’t believe,” he would say to his brother, “what a pleasure this rural laziness is to me. Not an idea in one’s brain, as empty as a drum!”\n\nBut Konstantin Levin found it dull sitting and listening to him, especially when he knew that while he was away they would be carting dung onto the fields not ploughed ready for it, and heaping it all up anyhow; and would not screw the shares in the ploughs, but would let them come off and then say that the new ploughs were a silly invention, and there was nothing like the old Andreevna plough, and so on.\n\n“Come, you’ve done enough trudging about in the heat,” Sergey Ivanovitch would say to him.\n\n“No, I must just run round to the counting-house for a minute,” Levin would answer, and he would run off to the fields.\n\n# Chapter 2\n\nEarly in June it happened that Agafea Mihalovna, the old nurse and housekeeper, in carrying to the cellar a jar of mushrooms she had just pickled, slipped, fell, and sprained her wrist. The district doctor, a talkative young medical student, who had just finished his studies, came to see her. He examined the wrist, said it was not broken, was delighted at a chance of talking to the celebrated Sergey Ivanovitch Koznishev, and to show his advanced views of things told him all the scandal of the district, complaining of the poor state into which the district council had fallen. Sergey Ivanovitch listened attentively, asked him questions, and, roused by a new listener, he talked fluently, uttered a few keen and weighty observations, respectfully appreciated by the young doctor, and was soon in that eager frame of mind his brother knew so well, which always, with him, followed a brilliant and eager conversation. After the departure of the doctor, he wanted to go with a fishing rod to the river. Sergey Ivanovitch was fond of angling, and was, it seemed, proud of being able to care for such a stupid occupation.\n\nKonstantin Levin, whose presence was needed in the plough land and meadows, had come to take his brother in the trap.\n\nIt was that time of the year, the turning-point of summer, when the crops of the present year are a certainty, when one begins to think of the sowing for next year, and the mowing is at hand; when the rye is all in ear, though its ears are still light, not yet full, and it waves in gray-green billows in the wind; when the green oats, with tufts of yellow grass scattered here and there among it, droop irregularly over the late-sown fields; when the early buckwheat is already out and hiding the ground; when the fallow lands, trodden hard as stone by the cattle, are half ploughed over, with paths left untouched by the plough; when from the dry dung-heaps carted onto the fields there comes at sunset a smell of manure mixed with meadow-sweet, and on the low-lying lands the riverside meadows are a thick sea of grass waiting for the mowing, with blackened heaps of the stalks of sorrel among it.\n\nIt was the time when there comes a brief pause in the toil of the fields before the beginning of the labors of harvest—every year recurring, every year straining every nerve of the peasants. The crop was a splendid one, and bright, hot summer days had set in with short, dewy nights.\n\nThe brothers had to drive through the woods to reach the meadows. Sergey Ivanovitch was all the while admiring the beauty of the woods, which were a tangled mass of leaves, pointing out to his brother now an old lime tree on the point of flowering, dark on the shady side, and brightly spotted with yellow stipules, now the young shoots of this year’s saplings brilliant with emerald. Konstantin Levin did not like talking and hearing about the beauty of nature. Words for him took away the beauty of what he saw. He assented to what his brother said, but he could not help beginning to think of other things. When they came out of the woods, all his attention was engrossed by the view of the fallow land on the upland, in parts yellow with grass, in parts trampled and checkered with furrows, in parts dotted with ridges of dung, and in parts even ploughed. A string of carts was moving across it. Levin counted the carts, and was pleased that all that were wanted had been brought, and at the sight of the meadows his thoughts passed to the mowing. He always felt something special moving him to the quick at the hay-making. On reaching the meadow Levin stopped the horse.\n\nThe morning dew was still lying on the thick undergrowth of the grass, and that he might not get his feet wet, Sergey Ivanovitch asked his brother to drive him in the trap up to the willow tree from which the carp was caught. Sorry as Konstantin Levin was to crush down his mowing grass, he drove him into the meadow. The high grass softly turned about the wheels and the horse’s legs, leaving its seeds clinging to the wet axles and spokes of the wheels. His brother seated himself under a bush, arranging his tackle, while Levin led the horse away, fastened him up, and walked into the vast gray-green sea of grass unstirred by the wind. The silky grass with its ripe seeds came almost to his waist in the dampest spots.\n\nCrossing the meadow, Konstantin Levin came out onto the road, and met an old man with a swollen eye, carrying a skep on his shoulder.\n\n“What? taken a stray swarm, Fomitch?” he asked.\n\n“No, indeed, Konstantin Dmitrich! All we can do to keep our own! This is the second swarm that has flown away.... Luckily the lads caught them. They were ploughing your field. They unyoked the horses and galloped after them.”\n\n“Well, what do you say, Fomitch—start mowing or wait a bit?”\n\n“Eh, well. Our way’s to wait till St. Peter’s Day. But you always mow sooner. Well, to be sure, please God, the hay’s good. There’ll be plenty for the beasts.”\n\n“What do you think about the weather?”\n\n“That’s in God’s hands. Maybe it will be fine.”\n\nLevin went up to his brother.\n\nSergey Ivanovitch had caught nothing, but he was not bored, and seemed in the most cheerful frame of mind. Levin saw that, stimulated by his conversation with the doctor, he wanted to talk. Levin, on the other hand, would have liked to get home as soon as possible to give orders about getting together the mowers for next day, and to set at rest his doubts about the mowing, which greatly absorbed him.\n\n“Well, let’s be going,” he said.\n\n“Why be in such a hurry? Let’s stay a little. But how wet you are! Even though one catches nothing, it’s nice. That’s the best thing about every part of sport, that one has to do with nature. How exquisite this steely water is!” said Sergey Ivanovitch. “These riverside banks always remind me of the riddle—do you know it? ‘The grass says to the water: we quiver and we quiver.’”\n\n“I don’t know the riddle,” answered Levin wearily.\n\n# Chapter 3\n\n“Do you know, I’ve been thinking about you,” said Sergey Ivanovitch. “It’s beyond everything what’s being done in the district, according to what this doctor tells me. He’s a very intelligent fellow. And as I’ve told you before, I tell you again: it’s not right for you not to go to the meetings, and altogether to keep out of the district business. If decent people won’t go into it, of course it’s bound to go all wrong. We pay the money, and it all goes in salaries, and there are no schools, nor district nurses, nor midwives, nor drugstores—nothing.”\n\n“Well, I did try, you know,” Levin said slowly and unwillingly. “I can’t! and so there’s no help for it.”\n\n“But why can’t you? I must own I can’t make it out. Indifference, incapacity—I won’t admit; surely it’s not simply laziness?”\n\n“None of those things. I’ve tried, and I see I can do nothing,” said Levin.\n\nHe had hardly grasped what his brother was saying. Looking towards the plough land across the river, he made out something black, but he could not distinguish whether it was a horse or the bailiff on horseback.\n\n“Why is it you can do nothing? You made an attempt and didn’t succeed, as you think, and you give in. How can you have so little self-respect?”\n\n“Self-respect!” said Levin, stung to the quick by his brother’s words; “I don’t understand. If they’d told me at college that other people understood the integral calculus, and I didn’t, then pride would have come in. But in this case one wants first to be convinced that one has certain qualifications for this sort of business, and especially that all this business is of great importance.”\n\n“What! do you mean to say it’s not of importance?” said Sergey Ivanovitch, stung to the quick too at his brother’s considering anything of no importance that interested him, and still more at his obviously paying little attention to what he was saying.\n\n“I don’t think it important; it does not take hold of me, I can’t help it,” answered Levin, making out that what he saw was the bailiff, and that the bailiff seemed to be letting the peasants go off the ploughed land. They were turning the plough over. “Can they have finished ploughing?” he wondered.\n\n“Come, really though,” said the elder brother, with a frown on his handsome, clever face, “there’s a limit to everything. It’s very well to be original and genuine, and to dislike everything conventional—I know all about that; but really, what you’re saying either has no meaning, or it has a very wrong meaning. How can you think it a matter of no importance whether the peasant, whom you love as you assert....”\n\n“I never did assert it,” thought Konstantin Levin.\n\n“...dies without help? The ignorant peasant-women starve the children, and the people stagnate in darkness, and are helpless in the hands of every village clerk, while you have at your disposal a means of helping them, and don’t help them because to your mind it’s of no importance.”\n\nAnd Sergey Ivanovitch put before him the alternative: either you are so undeveloped that you can’t see all that you can do, or you won’t sacrifice your ease, your vanity, or whatever it is, to do it.\n\nKonstantin Levin felt that there was no course open to him but to submit, or to confess to a lack of zeal for the public good. And this mortified him and hurt his feelings.\n\n“It’s both,” he said resolutely: “I don’t see that it was possible....”\n\n“What! was it impossible, if the money were properly laid out, to provide medical aid?”\n\n“Impossible, as it seems to me.... For the three thousand square miles of our district, what with our thaws, and the storms, and the work in the fields, I don’t see how it is possible to provide medical aid all over. And besides, I don’t believe in medicine.”\n\n“Oh, well, that’s unfair ... I can quote to you thousands of instances.... But the schools, anyway.”\n\n“Why have schools?”\n\n“What do you mean? Can there be two opinions of the advantage of education? If it’s a good thing for you, it’s a good thing for everyone.”\n\nKonstantin Levin felt himself morally pinned against a wall, and so he got hot, and unconsciously blurted out the chief cause of his indifference to public business.\n\n“Perhaps it may all be very good; but why should I worry myself about establishing dispensaries which I shall never make use of, and schools to which I shall never send my children, to which even the peasants don’t want to send their children, and to which I’ve no very firm faith that they ought to send them?” said he.\n\nSergey Ivanovitch was for a minute surprised at this unexpected view of the subject; but he promptly made a new plan of attack. He was silent for a little, drew out a hook, threw it in again, and turned to his brother smiling.\n\n“Come, now.... In the first place, the dispensary is needed. We ourselves sent for the district doctor for Agafea Mihalovna.”\n\n“Oh, well, but I fancy her wrist will never be straight again.”\n\n“That remains to be proved.... Next, the peasant who can read and write is as a workman of more use and value to you.”\n\n“No, you can ask anyone you like,” Konstantin Levin answered with decision, “the man that can read and write is much inferior as a workman. And mending the highroads is an impossibility; and as soon as they put up bridges they’re stolen.”\n\n“Still, that’s not the point,” said Sergey Ivanovitch, frowning. He disliked contradiction, and still more, arguments that were continually skipping from one thing to another, introducing new and disconnected points, so that there was no knowing to which to reply. “Do you admit that education is a benefit for the people?”\n\n“Yes, I admit it,” said Levin without thinking, and he was conscious immediately that he had said what he did not think. He felt that if he admitted that, it would be proved that he had been talking meaningless rubbish. How it would be proved he could not tell, but he knew that this would inevitably be logically proved to him, and he awaited the proofs.\n\nThe argument turned out to be far simpler than he had expected.\n\n“If you admit that it is a benefit,” said Sergey Ivanovitch, “then, as an honest man, you cannot help caring about it and sympathizing with the movement, and so wishing to work for it.”\n\n“But I still do not admit this movement to be just,” said Konstantin Levin, reddening a little.\n\n“What! But you said just now....”\n\n“That’s to say, I don’t admit it’s being either good or possible.”\n\n“That you can’t tell without making the trial.”\n\n“Well, supposing that’s so,” said Levin, though he did not suppose so at all, “supposing that is so, still I don’t see, all the same, what I’m to worry myself about it for.”\n\n“How so?”\n“No; since we are talking, explain it to me from the philosophical point of view,” said Levin.\n\n“I can’t see where philosophy comes in,” said Sergey Ivanovitch, in a tone, Levin fancied, as though he did not admit his brother’s right to talk about philosophy. And that irritated Levin.\n\n“I’ll tell you, then,” he said with heat, “I imagine the mainspring of all our actions is, after all, self-interest. Now in the local institutions I, as a nobleman, see nothing that could conduce to my prosperity, and the roads are not better and could not be better; my horses carry me well enough over bad ones. Doctors and dispensaries are no use to me. An arbitrator of disputes is no use to me. I never appeal to him, and never shall appeal to him. The schools are no good to me, but positively harmful, as I told you. For me the district institutions simply mean the liability to pay fourpence halfpenny for every three acres, to drive into the town, sleep with bugs, and listen to all sorts of idiocy and loathsomeness, and self-interest offers me no inducement.”\n\n“Excuse me,” Sergey Ivanovitch interposed with a smile, “self-interest did not induce us to work for the emancipation of the serfs, but we did work for it.”\n\n“No!” Konstantin Levin broke in with still greater heat; “the emancipation of the serfs was a different matter. There self-interest did come in. One longed to throw off that yoke that crushed us, all decent people among us. But to be a town councilor and discuss how many dustmen are needed, and how chimneys shall be constructed in the town in which I don’t live—to serve on a jury and try a peasant who’s stolen a flitch of bacon, and listen for six hours at a stretch to all sorts of jabber from the counsel for the defense and the prosecution, and the president cross-examining my old half-witted Alioshka, ‘Do you admit, prisoner in the dock, the fact of the removal of the bacon?’ ‘Eh?’”\n\nKonstantin Levin had warmed to his subject, and began mimicking the president and the half-witted Alioshka: it seemed to him that it was all to the point.\n\nBut Sergey Ivanovitch shrugged his shoulders.\n\n“Well, what do you mean to say, then?”\n\n“I simply mean to say that those rights that touch me ... my interest, I shall always defend to the best of my ability; that when they made raids on us students, and the police read our letters, I was ready to defend those rights to the utmost, to defend my rights to education and freedom. I can understand compulsory military service, which affects my children, my brothers, and myself, I am ready to deliberate on what concerns me; but deliberating on how to spend forty thousand roubles of district council money, or judging the half-witted Alioshka—I don’t understand, and I can’t do it.”\n\nKonstantin Levin spoke as though the floodgates of his speech had burst open. Sergey Ivanovitch smiled.\n\n“But tomorrow it’ll be your turn to be tried; would it have suited your tastes better to be tried in the old criminal tribunal?”\n\n“I’m not going to be tried. I shan’t murder anybody, and I’ve no need of it. Well, I tell you what,” he went on, flying off again to a subject quite beside the point, “our district self-government and all the rest of it—it’s just like the birch branches we stick in the ground on Trinity Day, for instance, to look like a copse which has grown up of itself in Europe, and I can’t gush over these birch branches and believe in them.”\n\nSergey Ivanovitch merely shrugged his shoulders, as though to express his wonder how the birch branches had come into their argument at that point, though he did really understand at once what his brother meant.\n\n“Excuse me, but you know one really can’t argue in that way,” he observed.\n\nBut Konstantin Levin wanted to justify himself for the failing, of which he was conscious, of lack of zeal for the public welfare, and he went on.\n\n“I imagine,” he said, “that no sort of activity is likely to be lasting if it is not founded on self-interest, that’s a universal principle, a philosophical principle,” he said, repeating the word “philosophical” with determination, as though wishing to show that he had as much right as anyone else to talk of philosophy.\n\nSergey Ivanovitch smiled. “He too has a philosophy of his own at the service of his natural tendencies,” he thought.\n\n“Come, you’d better let philosophy alone,” he said. “The chief problem of the philosophy of all ages consists just in finding the indispensable connection which exists between individual and social interests. But that’s not to the point; what is to the point is a correction I must make in your comparison. The birches are not simply stuck in, but some are sown and some are planted, and one must deal carefully with them. It’s only those peoples that have an intuitive sense of what’s of importance and significance in their institutions, and know how to value them, that have a future before them—it’s only those peoples that one can truly call historical.”\n\nAnd Sergey Ivanovitch carried the subject into the regions of philosophical history where Konstantin Levin could not follow him, and showed him all the incorrectness of his view.\n\n“As for your dislike of it, excuse my saying so, that’s simply our Russian sloth and old serf-owner’s ways, and I’m convinced that in you it’s a temporary error and will pass.”\n\nKonstantin was silent. He felt himself vanquished on all sides, but he felt at the same time that what he wanted to say was unintelligible to his brother. Only he could not make up his mind whether it was unintelligible because he was not capable of expressing his meaning clearly, or because his brother would not or could not understand him. But he did not pursue the speculation, and without replying, he fell to musing on a quite different and personal matter.\n\nSergey Ivanovitch wound up the last line, untied the horse, and they drove off.\n\n# Chapter 4\n\nThe personal matter that absorbed Levin during his conversation with his brother was this. Once in a previous year he had gone to look at the mowing, and being made very angry by the bailiff he had recourse to his favorite means for regaining his temper,—he took a scythe from a peasant and began mowing.\n\nHe liked the work so much that he had several times tried his hand at mowing since. He had cut the whole of the meadow in front of his house, and this year ever since the early spring he had cherished a plan for mowing for whole days together with the peasants. Ever since his brother’s arrival, he had been in doubt whether to mow or not. He was loath to leave his brother alone all day long, and he was afraid his brother would laugh at him about it. But as he drove into the meadow, and recalled the sensations of mowing, he came near deciding that he would go mowing. After the irritating discussion with his brother, he pondered over this intention again.\n\n“I must have physical exercise, or my temper’ll certainly be ruined,” he thought, and he determined he would go mowing, however awkward he might feel about it with his brother or the peasants.\n\nTowards evening Konstantin Levin went to his counting house, gave directions as to the work to be done, and sent about the village to summon the mowers for the morrow, to cut the hay in Kalinov meadow, the largest and best of his grass lands.\n\n“And send my scythe, please, to Tit, for him to set it, and bring it round tomorrow. I shall maybe do some mowing myself too,” he said, trying not to be embarrassed.\n\nThe bailiff smiled and said: “Yes, sir.”\n\nAt tea the same evening Levin said to his brother:\n\n“I fancy the fine weather will last. Tomorrow I shall start mowing.”\n\n“I’m so fond of that form of field labor,” said Sergey Ivanovitch.\n\n“I’m awfully fond of it. I sometimes mow myself with the peasants, and tomorrow I want to try mowing the whole day.”\n\nSergey Ivanovitch lifted his head, and looked with interest at his brother.\n\n“How do you mean? Just like one of the peasants, all day long?”\n\n“Yes, it’s very pleasant,” said Levin.\n\n“It’s splendid as exercise, only you’ll hardly be able to stand it,” said Sergey Ivanovitch, without a shade of irony.\n\n“I’ve tried it. It’s hard work at first, but you get into it. I dare say I shall manage to keep it up....”\n\n“Really! what an idea! But tell me, how do the peasants look at it? I suppose they laugh in their sleeves at their master’s being such a queer fish?”\n\n“No, I don’t think so; but it’s so delightful, and at the same time such hard work, that one has no time to think about it.”\n\n“But how will you do about dining with them? To send you a bottle of Lafitte and roast turkey out there would be a little awkward.”\n\n“No, I’ll simply come home at the time of their noonday rest.”\n\nNext morning Konstantin Levin got up earlier than usual, but he was detained giving directions on the farm, and when he reached the mowing grass the mowers were already at their second row.\n\nFrom the uplands he could get a view of the shaded cut part of the meadow below, with its grayish ridges of cut grass, and the black heaps of coats, taken off by the mowers at the place from which they had started cutting.\n\nGradually, as he rode towards the meadow, the peasants came into sight, some in coats, some in their shirts mowing, one behind another in a long string, swinging their scythes differently. He counted forty-two of them.\n\nThey were mowing slowly over the uneven, low-lying parts of the meadow, where there had been an old dam. Levin recognized some of his own men. Here was old Yermil in a very long white smock, bending forward to swing a scythe; there was a young fellow, Vaska, who had been a coachman of Levin’s, taking every row with a wide sweep. Here, too, was Tit, Levin’s preceptor in the art of mowing, a thin little peasant. He was in front of all, and cut his wide row without bending, as though playing with the scythe.\n\nLevin got off his mare, and fastening her up by the roadside went to meet Tit, who took a second scythe out of a bush and gave it to him.\n\n“It’s ready, sir; it’s like a razor, cuts of itself,” said Tit, taking off his cap with a smile and giving him the scythe.\n\nLevin took the scythe, and began trying it. As they finished their rows, the mowers, hot and good-humored, came out into the road one after another, and, laughing a little, greeted the master. They all stared at him, but no one made any remark, till a tall old man, with a wrinkled, beardless face, wearing a short sheepskin jacket, came out into the road and accosted him.\n\n“Look’ee now, master, once take hold of the rope there’s no letting it go!” he said, and Levin heard smothered laughter among the mowers.\n\n“I’ll try not to let it go,” he said, taking his stand behind Tit, and waiting for the time to begin.\n\n“Mind’ee,” repeated the old man.\n\nTit made room, and Levin started behind him. The grass was short close to the road, and Levin, who had not done any mowing for a long while, and was disconcerted by the eyes fastened upon him, cut badly for the first moments, though he swung his scythe vigorously. Behind him he heard voices:\n\n“It’s not set right; handle’s too high; see how he has to stoop to it,” said one.\n\n“Press more on the heel,” said another.\n\n“Never mind, he’ll get on all right,” the old man resumed.\n\n“He’s made a start.... You swing it too wide, you’ll tire yourself out.... The master, sure, does his best for himself! But see the grass missed out! For such work us fellows would catch it!”\n\nThe grass became softer, and Levin, listening without answering, followed Tit, trying to do the best he could. They moved a hundred paces. Tit kept moving on, without stopping, not showing the slightest weariness, but Levin was already beginning to be afraid he would not be able to keep it up: he was so tired.\n\nHe felt as he swung his scythe that he was at the very end of his strength, and was making up his mind to ask Tit to stop. But at that very moment Tit stopped of his own accord, and stooping down picked up some grass, rubbed his scythe, and began whetting it. Levin straightened himself, and drawing a deep breath looked round. Behind him came a peasant, and he too was evidently tired, for he stopped at once without waiting to mow up to Levin, and began whetting his scythe. Tit sharpened his scythe and Levin’s, and they went on. The next time it was just the same. Tit moved on with sweep after sweep of his scythe, not stopping nor showing signs of weariness. Levin followed him, trying not to get left behind, and he found it harder and harder: the moment came when he felt he had no strength left, but at that very moment Tit stopped and whetted the scythes.\n\nSo they mowed the first row. And this long row seemed particularly hard work to Levin; but when the end was reached and Tit, shouldering his scythe, began with deliberate stride returning on the tracks left by his heels in the cut grass, and Levin walked back in the same way over the space he had cut, in spite of the sweat that ran in streams over his face and fell in drops down his nose, and drenched his back as though he had been soaked in water, he felt very happy. What delighted him particularly was that now he knew he would be able to hold out.\n\nHis pleasure was only disturbed by his row not being well cut. “I will swing less with my arm and more with my whole body,” he thought, comparing Tit’s row, which looked as if it had been cut with a line, with his own unevenly and irregularly lying grass.\n\nThe first row, as Levin noticed, Tit had mowed specially quickly, probably wishing to put his master to the test, and the row happened to be a long one. The next rows were easier, but still Levin had to strain every nerve not to drop behind the peasants.\n\nHe thought of nothing, wished for nothing, but not to be left behind the peasants, and to do his work as well as possible. He heard nothing but the swish of scythes, and saw before him Tit’s upright figure mowing away, the crescent-shaped curve of the cut grass, the grass and flower heads slowly and rhythmically falling before the blade of his scythe, and ahead of him the end of the row, where would come the rest.\n\nSuddenly, in the midst of his toil, without understanding what it was or whence it came, he felt a pleasant sensation of chill on his hot, moist shoulders. He glanced at the sky in the interval for whetting the scythes. A heavy, lowering storm cloud had blown up, and big raindrops were falling. Some of the peasants went to their coats and put them on; others—just like Levin himself—merely shrugged their shoulders, enjoying the pleasant coolness of it.\n\nAnother row, and yet another row, followed—long rows and short rows, with good grass and with poor grass. Levin lost all sense of time, and could not have told whether it was late or early now. A change began to come over his work, which gave him immense satisfaction. In the midst of his toil there were moments during which he forgot what he was doing, and it came all easy to him, and at those same moments his row was almost as smooth and well cut as Tit’s. But so soon as he recollected what he was doing, and began trying to do better, he was at once conscious of all the difficulty of his task, and the row was badly mown.\n\nOn finishing yet another row he would have gone back to the top of the meadow again to begin the next, but Tit stopped, and going up to the old man said something in a low voice to him. They both looked at the sun. “What are they talking about, and why doesn’t he go back?” thought Levin, not guessing that the peasants had been mowing no less than four hours without stopping, and it was time for their lunch.\n\n“Lunch, sir,” said the old man.\n\n“Is it really time? That’s right; lunch, then.”\n\nLevin gave his scythe to Tit, and together with the peasants, who were crossing the long stretch of mown grass, slightly sprinkled with rain, to get their bread from the heap of coats, he went towards his house. Only then he suddenly awoke to the fact that he had been wrong about the weather and the rain was drenching his hay.\n\n“The hay will be spoiled,” he said.\n\n“Not a bit of it, sir; mow in the rain, and you’ll rake in fine weather!” said the old man.\n\nLevin untied his horse and rode home to his coffee. Sergey Ivanovitch was only just getting up. When he had drunk his coffee, Levin rode back again to the mowing before Sergey Ivanovitch had had time to dress and come down to the dining-room.\n\n# Chapter 5\n\nAfter lunch Levin was not in the same place in the string of mowers as before, but stood between the old man who had accosted him jocosely, and now invited him to be his neighbor, and a young peasant, who had only been married in the autumn, and who was mowing this summer for the first time.\n\nThe old man, holding himself erect, moved in front, with his feet turned out, taking long, regular strides, and with a precise and regular action which seemed to cost him no more effort than swinging one’s arms in walking, as though it were in play, he laid down the high, even row of grass. It was as though it were not he but the sharp scythe of itself swishing through the juicy grass.\n\nBehind Levin came the lad Mishka. His pretty, boyish face, with a twist of fresh grass bound round his hair, was all working with effort; but whenever anyone looked at him he smiled. He would clearly have died sooner than own it was hard work for him.\n\nLevin kept between them. In the very heat of the day the mowing did not seem such hard work to him. The perspiration with which he was drenched cooled him, while the sun, that burned his back, his head, and his arms, bare to the elbow, gave a vigor and dogged energy to his labor; and more and more often now came those moments of unconsciousness, when it was possible not to think what one was doing. The scythe cut of itself. These were happy moments. Still more delightful were the moments when they reached the stream where the rows ended, and the old man rubbed his scythe with the wet, thick grass, rinsed its blade in the fresh water of the stream, ladled out a little in a tin dipper, and offered Levin a drink.\n\n“What do you say to my home-brew, eh? Good, eh?” said he, winking.\n\nAnd truly Levin had never drunk any liquor so good as this warm water with green bits floating in it, and a taste of rust from the tin dipper. And immediately after this came the delicious, slow saunter, with his hand on the scythe, during which he could wipe away the streaming sweat, take deep breaths of air, and look about at the long string of mowers and at what was happening around in the forest and the country.\n\nThe longer Levin mowed, the oftener he felt the moments of unconsciousness in which it seemed not his hands that swung the scythe, but the scythe mowing of itself, a body full of life and consciousness of its own, and as though by magic, without thinking of it, the work turned out regular and well-finished of itself. These were the most blissful moments.\n\nIt was only hard work when he had to break off the motion, which had become unconscious, and to think; when he had to mow round a hillock or a tuft of sorrel. The old man did this easily. When a hillock came he changed his action, and at one time with the heel, and at another with the tip of his scythe, clipped the hillock round both sides with short strokes. And while he did this he kept looking about and watching what came into his view: at one moment he picked a wild berry and ate it or offered it to Levin, then he flung away a twig with the blade of the scythe, then he looked at a quail’s nest, from which the bird flew just under the scythe, or caught a snake that crossed his path, and lifting it on the scythe as though on a fork showed it to Levin and threw it away.\n\nFor both Levin and the young peasant behind him, such changes of position were difficult. Both of them, repeating over and over again the same strained movement, were in a perfect frenzy of toil, and were incapable of shifting their position and at the same time watching what was before them.\n\nLevin did not notice how time was passing. If he had been asked how long he had been working he would have said half an hour—and it was getting on for dinner time. As they were walking back over the cut grass, the old man called Levin’s attention to the little girls and boys who were coming from different directions, hardly visible through the long grass, and along the road towards the mowers, carrying sacks of bread dragging at their little hands and pitchers of the sour rye-beer, with cloths wrapped round them.\n\n“Look’ee, the little emmets crawling!” he said, pointing to them, and he shaded his eyes with his hand to look at the sun. They mowed two more rows; the old man stopped.\n\n“Come, master, dinner time!” he said briskly. And on reaching the stream the mowers moved off across the lines of cut grass towards their pile of coats, where the children who had brought their dinners were sitting waiting for them. The peasants gathered into groups—those further away under a cart, those nearer under a willow bush.\n\nLevin sat down by them; he felt disinclined to go away.\n\nAll constraint with the master had disappeared long ago. The peasants got ready for dinner. Some washed, the young lads bathed in the stream, others made a place comfortable for a rest, untied their sacks of bread, and uncovered the pitchers of rye-beer. The old man crumbled up some bread in a cup, stirred it with the handle of a spoon, poured water on it from the dipper, broke up some more bread, and having seasoned it with salt, he turned to the east to say his prayer.\n\n“Come, master, taste my sop,” said he, kneeling down before the cup.\n\nThe sop was so good that Levin gave up the idea of going home. He dined with the old man, and talked to him about his family affairs, taking the keenest interest in them, and told him about his own affairs and all the circumstances that could be of interest to the old man. He felt much nearer to him than to his brother, and could not help smiling at the affection he felt for this man. When the old man got up again, said his prayer, and lay down under a bush, putting some grass under his head for a pillow, Levin did the same, and in spite of the clinging flies that were so persistent in the sunshine, and the midges that tickled his hot face and body, he fell asleep at once and only waked when the sun had passed to the other side of the bush and reached him. The old man had been awake a long while, and was sitting up whetting the scythes of the younger lads.\n\nLevin looked about him and hardly recognized the place, everything was so changed. The immense stretch of meadow had been mown and was sparkling with a peculiar fresh brilliance, with its lines of already sweet-smelling grass in the slanting rays of the evening sun. And the bushes about the river had been cut down, and the river itself, not visible before, now gleaming like steel in its bends, and the moving, ascending, peasants, and the sharp wall of grass of the unmown part of the meadow, and the hawks hovering over the stripped meadow—all was perfectly new. Raising himself, Levin began considering how much had been cut and how much more could still be done that day.\n\nThe work done was exceptionally much for forty-two men. They had cut the whole of the big meadow, which had, in the years of serf labor, taken thirty scythes two days to mow. Only the corners remained to do, where the rows were short. But Levin felt a longing to get as much mowing done that day as possible, and was vexed with the sun sinking so quickly in the sky. He felt no weariness; all he wanted was to get his work done more and more quickly and as much done as possible.\n\n“Could you cut Mashkin Upland too?—what do you think?” he said to the old man.\n\n“As God wills, the sun’s not high. A little vodka for the lads?”\n\nAt the afternoon rest, when they were sitting down again, and those who smoked had lighted their pipes, the old man told the men that “Mashkin Upland’s to be cut—there’ll be some vodka.”\n\n“Why not cut it? Come on, Tit! We’ll look sharp! We can eat at night. Come on!” cried voices, and eating up their bread, the mowers went back to work.\n\n“Come, lads, keep it up!” said Tit, and ran on ahead almost at a trot.\n\n“Get along, get along!” said the old man, hurrying after him and easily overtaking him, “I’ll mow you down, look out!”\n\nAnd young and old mowed away, as though they were racing with one another. But however fast they worked, they did not spoil the grass, and the rows were laid just as neatly and exactly. The little piece left uncut in the corner was mown in five minutes. The last of the mowers were just ending their rows while the foremost snatched up their coats onto their shoulders, and crossed the road towards Mashkin Upland.\n\nThe sun was already sinking into the trees when they went with their jingling dippers into the wooded ravine of Mashkin Upland. The grass was up to their waists in the middle of the hollow, soft, tender, and feathery, spotted here and there among the trees with wild heart’s-ease.\n\nAfter a brief consultation—whether to take the rows lengthwise or diagonally—Prohor Yermilin, also a renowned mower, a huge, black-haired peasant, went on ahead. He went up to the top, turned back again and started mowing, and they all proceeded to form in line behind him, going downhill through the hollow and uphill right up to the edge of the forest. The sun sank behind the forest. The dew was falling by now; the mowers were in the sun only on the hillside, but below, where a mist was rising, and on the opposite side, they mowed into the fresh, dewy shade. The work went rapidly. The grass cut with a juicy sound, and was at once laid in high, fragrant rows. The mowers from all sides, brought closer together in the short row, kept urging one another on to the sound of jingling dippers and clanging scythes, and the hiss of the whetstones sharpening them, and good-humored shouts.\n\nLevin still kept between the young peasant and the old man. The old man, who had put on his short sheepskin jacket, was just as good-humored, jocose, and free in his movements. Among the trees they were continually cutting with their scythes the so-called “birch mushrooms,” swollen fat in the succulent grass. But the old man bent down every time he came across a mushroom, picked it up and put it in his bosom. “Another present for my old woman,” he said as he did so.\n\nEasy as it was to mow the wet, soft grass, it was hard work going up and down the steep sides of the ravine. But this did not trouble the old man. Swinging his scythe just as ever, and moving his feet in their big, plaited shoes with firm, little steps, he climbed slowly up the steep place, and though his breeches hanging out below his smock, and his whole frame trembled with effort, he did not miss one blade of grass or one mushroom on his way, and kept making jokes with the peasants and Levin. Levin walked after him and often thought he must fall, as he climbed with a scythe up a steep cliff where it would have been hard work to clamber without anything. But he climbed up and did what he had to do. He felt as though some external force were moving him.\n\n# Chapter 6\n\nMashkin Upland was mown, the last row finished, the peasants had put on their coats and were gaily trudging home. Levin got on his horse and, parting regretfully from the peasants, rode homewards. On the hillside he looked back; he could not see them in the mist that had risen from the valley; he could only hear rough, good-humored voices, laughter, and the sound of clanking scythes.\n\nSergey Ivanovitch had long ago finished dinner, and was drinking iced lemon and water in his own room, looking through the reviews and papers which he had only just received by post, when Levin rushed into the room, talking merrily, with his wet and matted hair sticking to his forehead, and his back and chest grimed and moist.\n\n“We mowed the whole meadow! Oh, it is nice, delicious! And how have you been getting on?” said Levin, completely forgetting the disagreeable conversation of the previous day.\n\n“Mercy! what do you look like!” said Sergey Ivanovitch, for the first moment looking round with some dissatisfaction. “And the door, do shut the door!” he cried. “You must have let in a dozen at least.”\n\nSergey Ivanovitch could not endure flies, and in his own room he never opened the window except at night, and carefully kept the door shut.\n\n“Not one, on my honor. But if I have, I’ll catch them. You wouldn’t believe what a pleasure it is! How have you spent the day?”\n\n“Very well. But have you really been mowing the whole day? I expect you’re as hungry as a wolf. Kouzma has got everything ready for you.”\n\n“No, I don’t feel hungry even. I had something to eat there. But I’ll go and wash.”\n\n“Yes, go along, go along, and I’ll come to you directly,” said Sergey Ivanovitch, shaking his head as he looked at his brother. “Go along, make haste,” he added smiling, and gathering up his books, he prepared to go too. He, too, felt suddenly good-humored and disinclined to leave his brother’s side. “But what did you do while it was raining?”\n\n“Rain? Why, there was scarcely a drop. I’ll come directly. So you had a nice day too? That’s first-rate.” And Levin went off to change his clothes.\n\nFive minutes later the brothers met in the dining-room. Although it seemed to Levin that he was not hungry, and he sat down to dinner simply so as not to hurt Kouzma’s feelings, yet when he began to eat the dinner struck him as extraordinarily good. Sergey Ivanovitch watched him with a smile.\n\n“Oh, by the way, there’s a letter for you,” said he. “Kouzma, bring it down, please. And mind you shut the doors.”\n\nThe letter was from Oblonsky. Levin read it aloud. Oblonsky wrote to him from Petersburg: “I have had a letter from Dolly; she’s at Ergushovo, and everything seems going wrong there. Do ride over and see her, please; help her with advice; you know all about it. She will be so glad to see you. She’s quite alone, poor thing. My mother-in-law and all of them are still abroad.”\n\n“That’s capital! I will certainly ride over to her,” said Levin. “Or we’ll go together. She’s such a splendid woman, isn’t she?”\n\n“They’re not far from here, then?”\n\n“Twenty-five miles. Or perhaps it is thirty. But a capital road. Capital, we’ll drive over.”\n\n“I shall be delighted,” said Sergey Ivanovitch, still smiling. The sight of his younger brother’s appearance had immediately put him in a good humor.\n\n“Well, you have an appetite!” he said, looking at his dark-red, sunburnt face and neck bent over the plate.\n\n“Splendid! You can’t imagine what an effectual remedy it is for every sort of foolishness. I want to enrich medicine with a new word: Arbeitskur.”\n\n“Well, but you don’t need it, I should fancy.”\n\n“No, but for all sorts of nervous invalids.”\n\n“Yes, it ought to be tried. I had meant to come to the mowing to look at you, but it was so unbearably hot that I got no further than the forest. I sat there a little, and went on by the forest to the village, met your old nurse, and sounded her as to the peasants’ view of you. As far as I can make out, they don’t approve of this. She said: ‘It’s not a gentleman’s work.’ Altogether, I fancy that in the people’s ideas there are very clear and definite notions of certain, as they call it, ‘gentlemanly’ lines of action. And they don’t sanction the gentry’s moving outside bounds clearly laid down in their ideas.”\n\n“Maybe so; but anyway it’s a pleasure such as I have never known in my life. And there’s no harm in it, you know. Is there?” answered Levin. “I can’t help it if they don’t like it. Though I do believe it’s all right. Eh?”\n\n“Altogether,” pursued Sergey Ivanovitch, “you’re satisfied with your day?”\n\n“Quite satisfied. We cut the whole meadow. And such a splendid old man I made friends with there! You can’t fancy how delightful he was!”\n\n“Well, so you’re content with your day. And so am I. First, I solved two chess problems, and one a very pretty one—a pawn opening. I’ll show it you. And then—I thought over our conversation yesterday.”\n\n“Eh! our conversation yesterday?” said Levin, blissfully dropping his eyelids and drawing deep breaths after finishing his dinner, and absolutely incapable of recalling what their conversation yesterday was about.\n\n“I think you are partly right. Our difference of opinion amounts to this, that you make the mainspring self-interest, while I suppose that interest in the common weal is bound to exist in every man of a certain degree of advancement. Possibly you are right too, that action founded on material interest would be more desirable. You are altogether, as the French say, too primesautière a nature; you must have intense, energetic action, or nothing.”\n\nLevin listened to his brother and did not understand a single word, and did not want to understand. He was only afraid his brother might ask him some question which would make it evident he had not heard.\n\n“So that’s what I think it is, my dear boy,” said Sergey Ivanovitch, touching him on the shoulder.\n\n“Yes, of course. But, do you know? I won’t stand up for my view,” answered Levin, with a guilty, childlike smile. “Whatever was it I was disputing about?” he wondered. “Of course, I’m right, and he’s right, and it’s all first-rate. Only I must go round to the counting house and see to things.” He got up, stretching and smiling. Sergey Ivanovitch smiled too.\n\n“If you want to go out, let’s go together,” he said, disinclined to be parted from his brother, who seemed positively breathing out freshness and energy. “Come, we’ll go to the counting house, if you have to go there.”\n\n“Oh, heavens!” shouted Levin, so loudly that Sergey Ivanovitch was quite frightened.\n\n“What, what is the matter?”\n\n“How’s Agafea Mihalovna’s hand?” said Levin, slapping himself on the head. “I’d positively forgotten her even.”\n\n“It’s much better.”\n\n“Well, anyway I’ll run down to her. Before you’ve time to get your hat on, I’ll be back.”\n\nAnd he ran downstairs, clattering with his heels like a spring-rattle.\n\n# Chapter 7\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch had gone to Petersburg to perform the most natural and essential official duty—so familiar to everyone in the government service, though incomprehensible to outsiders—that duty, but for which one could hardly be in government service, of reminding the ministry of his existence—and having, for the due performance of this rite, taken all the available cash from home, was gaily and agreeably spending his days at the races and in the summer villas. Meanwhile Dolly and the children had moved into the country, to cut down expenses as much as possible. She had gone to Ergushovo, the estate that had been her dowry, and the one where in spring the forest had been sold. It was nearly forty miles from Levin’s Pokrovskoe. The big, old house at Ergushovo had been pulled down long ago, and the old prince had had the lodge done up and built on to. Twenty years before, when Dolly was a child, the lodge had been roomy and comfortable, though, like all lodges, it stood sideways to the entrance avenue, and faced the south. But by now this lodge was old and dilapidated. When Stepan Arkadyevitch had gone down in the spring to sell the forest, Dolly had begged him to look over the house and order what repairs might be needed. Stepan Arkadyevitch, like all unfaithful husbands indeed, was very solicitous for his wife’s comfort, and he had himself looked over the house, and given instructions about everything that he considered necessary. What he considered necessary was to cover all the furniture with cretonne, to put up curtains, to weed the garden, to make a little bridge on the pond, and to plant flowers. But he forgot many other essential matters, the want of which greatly distressed Darya Alexandrovna later on.\n\nIn spite of Stepan Arkadyevitch’s efforts to be an attentive father and husband, he never could keep in his mind that he had a wife and children. He had bachelor tastes, and it was in accordance with them that he shaped his life. On his return to Moscow he informed his wife with pride that everything was ready, that the house would be a little paradise, and that he advised her most certainly to go. His wife’s staying away in the country was very agreeable to Stepan Arkadyevitch from every point of view: it did the children good, it decreased expenses, and it left him more at liberty. Darya Alexandrovna regarded staying in the country for the summer as essential for the children, especially for the little girl, who had not succeeded in regaining her strength after the scarlatina, and also as a means of escaping the petty humiliations, the little bills owing to the wood-merchant, the fishmonger, the shoemaker, which made her miserable. Besides this, she was pleased to go away to the country because she was dreaming of getting her sister Kitty to stay with her there. Kitty was to be back from abroad in the middle of the summer, and bathing had been prescribed for her. Kitty wrote that no prospect was so alluring as to spend the summer with Dolly at Ergushovo, full of childish associations for both of them.\n\nThe first days of her existence in the country were very hard for Dolly. She used to stay in the country as a child, and the impression she had retained of it was that the country was a refuge from all the unpleasantness of the town, that life there, though not luxurious—Dolly could easily make up her mind to that—was cheap and comfortable; that there was plenty of everything, everything was cheap, everything could be got, and children were happy. But now coming to the country as the head of a family, she perceived that it was all utterly unlike what she had fancied.\n\nThe day after their arrival there was a heavy fall of rain, and in the night the water came through in the corridor and in the nursery, so that the beds had to be carried into the drawing-room. There was no kitchen maid to be found; of the nine cows, it appeared from the words of the cowherd-woman that some were about to calve, others had just calved, others were old, and others again hard-uddered; there was not butter nor milk enough even for the children. There were no eggs. They could get no fowls; old, purplish, stringy cocks were all they had for roasting and boiling. Impossible to get women to scrub the floors—all were potato-hoeing. Driving was out of the question, because one of the horses was restive, and bolted in the shafts. There was no place where they could bathe; the whole of the river-bank was trampled by the cattle and open to the road; even walks were impossible, for the cattle strayed into the garden through a gap in the hedge, and there was one terrible bull, who bellowed, and therefore might be expected to gore somebody. There were no proper cupboards for their clothes; what cupboards there were either would not close at all, or burst open whenever anyone passed by them. There were no pots and pans; there was no copper in the washhouse, nor even an ironing-board in the maids’ room.\n\nFinding instead of peace and rest all these, from her point of view, fearful calamities, Darya Alexandrovna was at first in despair. She exerted herself to the utmost, felt the hopelessness of the position, and was every instant suppressing the tears that started into her eyes. The bailiff, a retired quartermaster, whom Stepan Arkadyevitch had taken a fancy to and had appointed bailiff on account of his handsome and respectful appearance as a hall-porter, showed no sympathy for Darya Alexandrovna’s woes. He said respectfully, “nothing can be done, the peasants are such a wretched lot,” and did nothing to help her.\n\nThe position seemed hopeless. But in the Oblonskys’ household, as in all families indeed, there was one inconspicuous but most valuable and useful person, Marya Philimonovna. She soothed her mistress, assured her that everything would come round (it was her expression, and Matvey had borrowed it from her), and without fuss or hurry proceeded to set to work herself. She had immediately made friends with the bailiff’s wife, and on the very first day she drank tea with her and the bailiff under the acacias, and reviewed all the circumstances of the position. Very soon Marya Philimonovna had established her club, so to say, under the acacias, and there it was, in this club, consisting of the bailiff’s wife, the village elder, and the counting-house clerk, that the difficulties of existence were gradually smoothed away, and in a week’s time everything actually had come round. The roof was mended, a kitchen maid was found—a crony of the village elder’s—hens were bought, the cows began giving milk, the garden hedge was stopped up with stakes, the carpenter made a mangle, hooks were put in the cupboards, and they ceased to burst open spontaneously, and an ironing-board covered with army cloth was placed across from the arm of a chair to the chest of drawers, and there was a smell of flatirons in the maids’ room.\n\n“Just see, now, and you were quite in despair,” said Marya Philimonovna, pointing to the ironing-board. They even rigged up a bathing-shed of straw hurdles. Lily began to bathe, and Darya Alexandrovna began to realize, if only in part, her expectations, if not of a peaceful, at least of a comfortable, life in the country. Peaceful with six children Darya Alexandrovna could not be. One would fall ill, another might easily become so, a third would be without something necessary, a fourth would show symptoms of a bad disposition, and so on. Rare indeed were the brief periods of peace. But these cares and anxieties were for Darya Alexandrovna the sole happiness possible. Had it not been for them, she would have been left alone to brood over her husband who did not love her. And besides, hard though it was for the mother to bear the dread of illness, the illnesses themselves, and the grief of seeing signs of evil propensities in her children—the children themselves were even now repaying her in small joys for her sufferings. Those joys were so small that they passed unnoticed, like gold in sand, and at bad moments she could see nothing but the pain, nothing but sand; but there were good moments too when she saw nothing but the joy, nothing but gold.\n\nNow in the solitude of the country, she began to be more and more frequently aware of those joys. Often, looking at them, she would make every possible effort to persuade herself that she was mistaken, that she as a mother was partial to her children. All the same, she could not help saying to herself that she had charming children, all six of them in different ways, but a set of children such as is not often to be met with, and she was happy in them, and proud of them.\n\n# Chapter 8\n\nTowards the end of May, when everything had been more or less satisfactorily arranged, she received her husband’s answer to her complaints of the disorganized state of things in the country. He wrote begging her forgiveness for not having thought of everything before, and promised to come down at the first chance. This chance did not present itself, and till the beginning of June Darya Alexandrovna stayed alone in the country.\n\nOn the Sunday in St. Peter’s week Darya Alexandrovna drove to mass for all her children to take the sacrament. Darya Alexandrovna in her intimate, philosophical talks with her sister, her mother, and her friends very often astonished them by the freedom of her views in regard to religion. She had a strange religion of transmigration of souls all her own, in which she had firm faith, troubling herself little about the dogmas of the Church. But in her family she was strict in carrying out all that was required by the Church—and not merely in order to set an example, but with all her heart in it. The fact that the children had not been at the sacrament for nearly a year worried her extremely, and with the full approval and sympathy of Marya Philimonovna she decided that this should take place now in the summer.\n\nFor several days before, Darya Alexandrovna was busily deliberating on how to dress all the children. Frocks were made or altered and washed, seams and flounces were let out, buttons were sewn on, and ribbons got ready. One dress, Tanya’s, which the English governess had undertaken, cost Darya Alexandrovna much loss of temper. The English governess in altering it had made the seams in the wrong place, had taken up the sleeves too much, and altogether spoilt the dress. It was so narrow on Tanya’s shoulders that it was quite painful to look at her. But Marya Philimonovna had the happy thought of putting in gussets, and adding a little shoulder-cape. The dress was set right, but there was nearly a quarrel with the English governess. On the morning, however, all was happily arranged, and towards ten o’clock—the time at which they had asked the priest to wait for them for the mass—the children in their new dresses, with beaming faces, stood on the step before the carriage waiting for their mother.\n\nTo the carriage, instead of the restive Raven, they had harnessed, thanks to the representations of Marya Philimonovna, the bailiff’s horse, Brownie, and Darya Alexandrovna, delayed by anxiety over her own attire, came out and got in, dressed in a white muslin gown.\n\nDarya Alexandrovna had done her hair, and dressed with care and excitement. In the old days she had dressed for her own sake to look pretty and be admired. Later on, as she got older, dress became more and more distasteful to her. She saw that she was losing her good looks. But now she began to feel pleasure and interest in dress again. Now she did not dress for her own sake, not for the sake of her own beauty, but simply that as the mother of those exquisite creatures she might not spoil the general effect. And looking at herself for the last time in the looking-glass she was satisfied with herself. She looked nice. Not nice as she would have wished to look nice in old days at a ball, but nice for the object which she now had in view.\n\nIn the church there was no one but the peasants, the servants and their women-folk. But Darya Alexandrovna saw, or fancied she saw, the sensation produced by her children and her. The children were not only beautiful to look at in their smart little dresses, but they were charming in the way they behaved. Aliosha, it is true, did not stand quite correctly; he kept turning round, trying to look at his little jacket from behind; but all the same he was wonderfully sweet. Tanya behaved like a grown-up person, and looked after the little ones. And the smallest, Lily, was bewitching in her naïve astonishment at everything, and it was difficult not to smile when, after taking the sacrament, she said in English, “Please, some more.”\n\nOn the way home the children felt that something solemn had happened, and were very sedate.\n\nEverything went happily at home too; but at lunch Grisha began whistling, and, what was worse, was disobedient to the English governess, and was forbidden to have any tart. Darya Alexandrovna would not have let things go so far on such a day had she been present; but she had to support the English governess’s authority, and she upheld her decision that Grisha should have no tart. This rather spoiled the general good humor. Grisha cried, declaring that Nikolinka had whistled too, and he was not punished, and that he wasn’t crying for the tart—he didn’t care—but at being unjustly treated. This was really too tragic, and Darya Alexandrovna made up her mind to persuade the English governess to forgive Grisha, and she went to speak to her. But on the way, as she passed the drawing-room, she beheld a scene, filling her heart with such pleasure that the tears came into her eyes, and she forgave the delinquent herself.\n\nThe culprit was sitting at the window in the corner of the drawing-room; beside him was standing Tanya with a plate. On the pretext of wanting to give some dinner to her dolls, she had asked the governess’s permission to take her share of tart to the nursery, and had taken it instead to her brother. While still weeping over the injustice of his punishment, he was eating the tart, and kept saying through his sobs, “Eat yourself; let’s eat it together ... together.”\n\nTanya had at first been under the influence of her pity for Grisha, then of a sense of her noble action, and tears were standing in her eyes too; but she did not refuse, and ate her share.\n\nOn catching sight of their mother they were dismayed, but, looking into her face, they saw they were not doing wrong. They burst out laughing, and, with their mouths full of tart, they began wiping their smiling lips with their hands, and smearing their radiant faces all over with tears and jam.\n\n“Mercy! Your new white frock! Tanya! Grisha!” said their mother, trying to save the frock, but with tears in her eyes, smiling a blissful, rapturous smile.\n\nThe new frocks were taken off, and orders were given for the little girls to have their blouses put on, and the boys their old jackets, and the wagonette to be harnessed; with Brownie, to the bailiff’s annoyance, again in the shafts, to drive out for mushroom picking and bathing. A roar of delighted shrieks arose in the nursery, and never ceased till they had set off for the bathing-place.\n\nThey gathered a whole basketful of mushrooms; even Lily found a birch mushroom. It had always happened before that Miss Hoole found them and pointed them out to her; but this time she found a big one quite of herself, and there was a general scream of delight, “Lily has found a mushroom!”\n\nThen they reached the river, put the horses under the birch trees, and went to the bathing-place. The coachman, Terenty, fastened the horses, who kept whisking away the flies, to a tree, and, treading down the grass, lay down in the shade of a birch and smoked his shag, while the never-ceasing shrieks of delight of the children floated across to him from the bathing-place.\n\nThough it was hard work to look after all the children and restrain their wild pranks, though it was difficult too to keep in one’s head and not mix up all the stockings, little breeches, and shoes for the different legs, and to undo and to do up again all the tapes and buttons, Darya Alexandrovna, who had always liked bathing herself, and believed it to be very good for the children, enjoyed nothing so much as bathing with all the children. To go over all those fat little legs, pulling on their stockings, to take in her arms and dip those little naked bodies, and to hear their screams of delight and alarm, to see the breathless faces with wide-open, scared, and happy eyes of all her splashing cherubs, was a great pleasure to her.\n\nWhen half the children had been dressed, some peasant women in holiday dress, out picking herbs, came up to the bathing-shed and stopped shyly. Marya Philimonovna called one of them and handed her a sheet and a shirt that had dropped into the water for her to dry them, and Darya Alexandrovna began to talk to the women. At first they laughed behind their hands and did not understand her questions, but soon they grew bolder and began to talk, winning Darya Alexandrovna’s heart at once by the genuine admiration of the children that they showed.\n\n“My, what a beauty! as white as sugar,” said one, admiring Tanitchka, and shaking her head; “but thin....”\n\n“Yes, she has been ill.”\n\n“And so they’ve been bathing you too,” said another to the baby.\n\n“No; he’s only three months old,” answered Darya Alexandrovna with pride.\n\n“You don’t say so!”\n\n“And have you any children?”\n\n“I’ve had four; I’ve two living—a boy and a girl. I weaned her last carnival.”\n\n“How old is she?”\n\n“Why, two years old.”\n\n“Why did you nurse her so long?”\n\n“It’s our custom; for three fasts....”\n\nAnd the conversation became most interesting to Darya Alexandrovna. What sort of time did she have? What was the matter with the boy? Where was her husband? Did it often happen?\n\nDarya Alexandrovna felt disinclined to leave the peasant women, so interesting to her was their conversation, so completely identical were all their interests. What pleased her most of all was that she saw clearly what all the women admired more than anything was her having so many children, and such fine ones. The peasant women even made Darya Alexandrovna laugh, and offended the English governess, because she was the cause of the laughter she did not understand. One of the younger women kept staring at the Englishwoman, who was dressing after all the rest, and when she put on her third petticoat she could not refrain from the remark, “My, she keeps putting on and putting on, and she’ll never have done!” she said, and they all went off into roars.\n\n# Chapter 9\n\nOn the drive home, as Darya Alexandrovna, with all her children round her, their heads still wet from their bath, and a kerchief tied over her own head, was getting near the house, the coachman said, “There’s some gentleman coming: the master of Pokrovskoe, I do believe.”\n\nDarya Alexandrovna peeped out in front, and was delighted when she recognized in the gray hat and gray coat the familiar figure of Levin walking to meet them. She was glad to see him at any time, but at this moment she was specially glad he should see her in all her glory. No one was better able to appreciate her grandeur than Levin.\n\nSeeing her, he found himself face to face with one of the pictures of his daydream of family life.\n\n“You’re like a hen with your chickens, Darya Alexandrovna.”\n\n“Ah, how glad I am to see you!” she said, holding out her hand to him.\n\n“Glad to see me, but you didn’t let me know. My brother’s staying with me. I got a note from Stiva that you were here.”\n\n“From Stiva?” Darya Alexandrovna asked with surprise.\n\n“Yes; he writes that you are here, and that he thinks you might allow me to be of use to you,” said Levin, and as he said it he became suddenly embarrassed, and, stopping abruptly, he walked on in silence by the wagonette, snapping off the buds of the lime trees and nibbling them. He was embarrassed through a sense that Darya Alexandrovna would be annoyed by receiving from an outsider help that should by rights have come from her own husband. Darya Alexandrovna certainly did not like this little way of Stepan Arkadyevitch’s of foisting his domestic duties on others. And she was at once aware that Levin was aware of this. It was just for this fineness of perception, for this delicacy, that Darya Alexandrovna liked Levin.\n\n“I know, of course,” said Levin, “that that simply means that you would like to see me, and I’m exceedingly glad. Though I can fancy that, used to town housekeeping as you are, you must feel in the wilds here, and if there’s anything wanted, I’m altogether at your disposal.”\n\n“Oh, no!” said Dolly. “At first things were rather uncomfortable, but now we’ve settled everything capitally—thanks to my old nurse,” she said, indicating Marya Philimonovna, who, seeing that they were speaking of her, smiled brightly and cordially to Levin. She knew him, and knew that he would be a good match for her young lady, and was very keen to see the matter settled.\n\n“Won’t you get in, sir, we’ll make room this side!” she said to him.\n\n“No, I’ll walk. Children, who’d like to race the horses with me?” The children knew Levin very little, and could not remember when they had seen him, but they experienced in regard to him none of that strange feeling of shyness and hostility which children so often experience towards hypocritical, grown-up people, and for which they are so often and miserably punished. Hypocrisy in anything whatever may deceive the cleverest and most penetrating man, but the least wide-awake of children recognizes it, and is revolted by it, however ingeniously it may be disguised. Whatever faults Levin had, there was not a trace of hypocrisy in him, and so the children showed him the same friendliness that they saw in their mother’s face. On his invitation, the two elder ones at once jumped out to him and ran with him as simply as they would have done with their nurse or Miss Hoole or their mother. Lily, too, began begging to go to him, and her mother handed her to him; he sat her on his shoulder and ran along with her.\n\n“Don’t be afraid, don’t be afraid, Darya Alexandrovna!” he said, smiling good-humoredly to the mother; “there’s no chance of my hurting or dropping her.”\n\nAnd, looking at his strong, agile, assiduously careful and needlessly wary movements, the mother felt her mind at rest, and smiled gaily and approvingly as she watched him.\n\nHere, in the country, with children, and with Darya Alexandrovna, with whom he was in sympathy, Levin was in a mood not infrequent with him, of childlike light-heartedness that she particularly liked in him. As he ran with the children, he taught them gymnastic feats, set Miss Hoole laughing with his queer English accent, and talked to Darya Alexandrovna of his pursuits in the country.\n\nAfter dinner, Darya Alexandrovna, sitting alone with him on the balcony, began to speak of Kitty.\n\n“You know, Kitty’s coming here, and is going to spend the summer with me.”\n\n“Really,” he said, flushing, and at once, to change the conversation, he said: “Then I’ll send you two cows, shall I? If you insist on a bill you shall pay me five roubles a month; but it’s really too bad of you.”\n\n“No, thank you. We can manage very well now.”\n\n“Oh, well, then, I’ll have a look at your cows, and if you’ll allow me, I’ll give directions about their food. Everything depends on their food.”\n\nAnd Levin, to turn the conversation, explained to Darya Alexandrovna the theory of cow-keeping, based on the principle that the cow is simply a machine for the transformation of food into milk, and so on.\n\nHe talked of this, and passionately longed to hear more of Kitty, and, at the same time, was afraid of hearing it. He dreaded the breaking up of the inward peace he had gained with such effort.\n\n“Yes, but still all this has to be looked after, and who is there to look after it?” Darya Alexandrovna responded, without interest.\n\nShe had by now got her household matters so satisfactorily arranged, thanks to Marya Philimonovna, that she was disinclined to make any change in them; besides, she had no faith in Levin’s knowledge of farming. General principles, as to the cow being a machine for the production of milk, she looked on with suspicion. It seemed to her that such principles could only be a hindrance in farm management. It all seemed to her a far simpler matter: all that was needed, as Marya Philimonovna had explained, was to give Brindle and Whitebreast more food and drink, and not to let the cook carry all the kitchen slops to the laundry maid’s cow. That was clear. But general propositions as to feeding on meal and on grass were doubtful and obscure. And, what was most important, she wanted to talk about Kitty.\n\n# Chapter 10\n\n“Kitty writes to me that there’s nothing she longs for so much as quiet and solitude,” Dolly said after the silence that had followed.\n\n“And how is she—better?” Levin asked in agitation.\n\n“Thank God, she’s quite well again. I never believed her lungs were affected.”\n\n“Oh, I’m very glad!” said Levin, and Dolly fancied she saw something touching, helpless, in his face as he said this and looked silently into her face.\n\n“Let me ask you, Konstantin Dmitrievitch,” said Darya Alexandrovna, smiling her kindly and rather mocking smile, “why is it you are angry with Kitty?”\n\n“I? I’m not angry with her,” said Levin.\n\n“Yes, you are angry. Why was it you did not come to see us nor them when you were in Moscow?”\n\n“Darya Alexandrovna,” he said, blushing up to the roots of his hair, “I wonder really that with your kind heart you don’t feel this. How it is you feel no pity for me, if nothing else, when you know....”\n\n“What do I know?”\n\n“You know I made an offer and that I was refused,” said Levin, and all the tenderness he had been feeling for Kitty a minute before was replaced by a feeling of anger for the slight he had suffered.\n\n“What makes you suppose I know?”\n\n“Because everybody knows it....”\n\n“That’s just where you are mistaken; I did not know it, though I had guessed it was so.”\n\n“Well, now you know it.”\n\n“All I knew was that something had happened that made her dreadfully miserable, and that she begged me never to speak of it. And if she would not tell me, she would certainly not speak of it to anyone else. But what did pass between you? Tell me.”\n\n“I have told you.”\n\n“When was it?”\n\n“When I was at their house the last time.”\n\n“Do you know that,” said Darya Alexandrovna, “I am awfully, awfully sorry for her. You suffer only from pride....”\n\n“Perhaps so,” said Levin, “but....”\n\nShe interrupted him.\n\n“But she, poor girl ... I am awfully, awfully sorry for her. Now I see it all.”\n\n“Well, Darya Alexandrovna, you must excuse me,” he said, getting up. “Good-bye, Darya Alexandrovna, till we meet again.”\n\n“No, wait a minute,” she said, clutching him by the sleeve. “Wait a minute, sit down.”\n\n“Please, please, don’t let us talk of this,” he said, sitting down, and at the same time feeling rise up and stir within his heart a hope he had believed to be buried.\n\n“If I did not like you,” she said, and tears came into her eyes; “if I did not know you, as I do know you....”\n\nThe feeling that had seemed dead revived more and more, rose up and took possession of Levin’s heart.\n\n“Yes, I understand it all now,” said Darya Alexandrovna. “You can’t understand it; for you men, who are free and make your own choice, it’s always clear whom you love. But a girl’s in a position of suspense, with all a woman’s or maiden’s modesty, a girl who sees you men from afar, who takes everything on trust,—a girl may have, and often has, such a feeling that she cannot tell what to say.”\n\n“Yes, if the heart does not speak....”\n\n“No, the heart does speak; but just consider: you men have views about a girl, you come to the house, you make friends, you criticize, you wait to see if you have found what you love, and then, when you are sure you love her, you make an offer....”\n\n“Well, that’s not quite it.”\n\n“Anyway you make an offer, when your love is ripe or when the balance has completely turned between the two you are choosing from. But a girl is not asked. She is expected to make her choice, and yet she cannot choose, she can only answer ‘yes’ or ‘no.’”\n\n“Yes, to choose between me and Vronsky,” thought Levin, and the dead thing that had come to life within him died again, and only weighed on his heart and set it aching.\n\n“Darya Alexandrovna,” he said, “that’s how one chooses a new dress or some purchase or other, not love. The choice has been made, and so much the better.... And there can be no repeating it.”\n\n“Ah, pride, pride!” said Darya Alexandrovna, as though despising him for the baseness of this feeling in comparison with that other feeling which only women know. “At the time when you made Kitty an offer she was just in a position in which she could not answer. She was in doubt. Doubt between you and Vronsky. Him she was seeing every day, and you she had not seen for a long while. Supposing she had been older ... I, for instance, in her place could have felt no doubt. I always disliked him, and so it has turned out.”\n\nLevin recalled Kitty’s answer. She had said: “No, that cannot be....”\n\n“Darya Alexandrovna,” he said dryly, “I appreciate your confidence in me; I believe you are making a mistake. But whether I am right or wrong, that pride you so despise makes any thought of Katerina Alexandrovna out of the question for me,—you understand, utterly out of the question.”\n\n“I will only say one thing more: you know that I am speaking of my sister, whom I love as I love my own children. I don’t say she cared for you, all I meant to say is that her refusal at that moment proves nothing.”\n\n“I don’t know!” said Levin, jumping up. “If you only knew how you are hurting me. It’s just as if a child of yours were dead, and they were to say to you: He would have been like this and like that, and he might have lived, and how happy you would have been in him. But he’s dead, dead, dead!...”\n\n“How absurd you are!” said Darya Alexandrovna, looking with mournful tenderness at Levin’s excitement. “Yes, I see it all more and more clearly,” she went on musingly. “So you won’t come to see us, then, when Kitty’s here?”\n\n“No, I shan’t come. Of course I won’t avoid meeting Katerina Alexandrovna, but as far as I can, I will try to save her the annoyance of my presence.”\n\n“You are very, very absurd,” repeated Darya Alexandrovna, looking with tenderness into his face. “Very well then, let it be as though we had not spoken of this. What have you come for, Tanya?” she said in French to the little girl who had come in.\n\n“Where’s my spade, mamma?”\n\n“I speak French, and you must too.”\n\nThe little girl tried to say it in French, but could not remember the French for spade; the mother prompted her, and then told her in French where to look for the spade. And this made a disagreeable impression on Levin.\n\nEverything in Darya Alexandrovna’s house and children struck him now as by no means so charming as a little while before. “And what does she talk French with the children for?” he thought; “how unnatural and false it is! And the children feel it so: Learning French and unlearning sincerity,” he thought to himself, unaware that Darya Alexandrovna had thought all that over twenty times already, and yet, even at the cost of some loss of sincerity, believed it necessary to teach her children French in that way.\n\n“But why are you going? Do stay a little.”\n\nLevin stayed to tea; but his good-humor had vanished, and he felt ill at ease.\n\nAfter tea he went out into the hall to order his horses to be put in, and, when he came back, he found Darya Alexandrovna greatly disturbed, with a troubled face, and tears in her eyes. While Levin had been outside, an incident had occurred which had utterly shattered all the happiness she had been feeling that day, and her pride in her children. Grisha and Tanya had been fighting over a ball. Darya Alexandrovna, hearing a scream in the nursery, ran in and saw a terrible sight. Tanya was pulling Grisha’s hair, while he, with a face hideous with rage, was beating her with his fists wherever he could get at her. Something snapped in Darya Alexandrovna’s heart when she saw this. It was as if darkness had swooped down upon her life; she felt that these children of hers, that she was so proud of, were not merely most ordinary, but positively bad, ill-bred children, with coarse, brutal propensities—wicked children.\n\nShe could not talk or think of anything else, and she could not speak to Levin of her misery.\n\nLevin saw she was unhappy and tried to comfort her, saying that it showed nothing bad, that all children fight; but, even as he said it, he was thinking in his heart: “No, I won’t be artificial and talk French with my children; but my children won’t be like that. All one has to do is not spoil children, not to distort their nature, and they’ll be delightful. No, my children won’t be like that.”\n\nHe said good-bye and drove away, and she did not try to keep him.\n\n# Chapter 11\n\nIn the middle of July the elder of the village on Levin’s sister’s estate, about fifteen miles from Pokrovskoe, came to Levin to report on how things were going there and on the hay. The chief source of income on his sister’s estate was from the riverside meadows. In former years the hay had been bought by the peasants for twenty roubles the three acres. When Levin took over the management of the estate, he thought on examining the grasslands that they were worth more, and he fixed the price at twenty-five roubles the three acres. The peasants would not give that price, and, as Levin suspected, kept off other purchasers. Then Levin had driven over himself, and arranged to have the grass cut, partly by hired labor, partly at a payment of a certain proportion of the crop. His own peasants put every hindrance they could in the way of this new arrangement, but it was carried out, and the first year the meadows had yielded a profit almost double. The previous year—which was the third year—the peasants had maintained the same opposition to the arrangement, and the hay had been cut on the same system. This year the peasants were doing all the mowing for a third of the hay crop, and the village elder had come now to announce that the hay had been cut, and that, fearing rain, they had invited the counting-house clerk over, had divided the crop in his presence, and had raked together eleven stacks as the owner’s share. From the vague answers to his question how much hay had been cut on the principal meadow, from the hurry of the village elder who had made the division, not asking leave, from the whole tone of the peasant, Levin perceived that there was something wrong in the division of the hay, and made up his mind to drive over himself to look into the matter.\n\nArriving for dinner at the village, and leaving his horse at the cottage of an old friend of his, the husband of his brother’s wet-nurse, Levin went to see the old man in his bee-house, wanting to find out from him the truth about the hay. Parmenitch, a talkative, comely old man, gave Levin a very warm welcome, showed him all he was doing, told him everything about his bees and the swarms of that year; but gave vague and unwilling answers to Levin’s inquiries about the mowing. This confirmed Levin still more in his suspicions. He went to the hay fields and examined the stacks. The haystacks could not possibly contain fifty wagon-loads each, and to convict the peasants Levin ordered the wagons that had carried the hay to be brought up directly, to lift one stack, and carry it into the barn. There turned out to be only thirty-two loads in the stack. In spite of the village elder’s assertions about the compressibility of hay, and its having settled down in the stacks, and his swearing that everything had been done in the fear of God, Levin stuck to his point that the hay had been divided without his orders, and that, therefore, he would not accept that hay as fifty loads to a stack. After a prolonged dispute the matter was decided by the peasants taking these eleven stacks, reckoning them as fifty loads each. The arguments and the division of the haycocks lasted the whole afternoon. When the last of the hay had been divided, Levin, intrusting the superintendence of the rest to the counting-house clerk, sat down on a haycock marked off by a stake of willow, and looked admiringly at the meadow swarming with peasants.\n\nIn front of him, in the bend of the river beyond the marsh, moved a bright-colored line of peasant women, and the scattered hay was being rapidly formed into gray winding rows over the pale green stubble. After the women came the men with pitchforks, and from the gray rows there were growing up broad, high, soft haycocks. To the left, carts were rumbling over the meadow that had been already cleared, and one after another the haycocks vanished, flung up in huge forkfuls, and in their place there were rising heavy cartloads of fragrant hay hanging over the horses’ hind-quarters.\n\n“What weather for haying! What hay it’ll be!” said an old man, squatting down beside Levin. “It’s tea, not hay! It’s like scattering grain to the ducks, the way they pick it up!” he added, pointing to the growing haycocks. “Since dinner time they’ve carried a good half of it.”\n\n“The last load, eh?” he shouted to a young peasant, who drove by, standing in the front of an empty cart, shaking the cord reins.\n\n“The last, dad!” the lad shouted back, pulling in the horse, and, smiling, he looked round at a bright, rosy-cheeked peasant girl who sat in the cart smiling too, and drove on.\n\n“Who’s that? Your son?” asked Levin.\n\n“My baby,” said the old man with a tender smile.\n\n“What a fine fellow!”\n\n“The lad’s all right.”\n\n“Married already?”\n\n“Yes, it’s two years last St. Philip’s day.”\n\n“Any children?”\n\n“Children indeed! Why, for over a year he was innocent as a babe himself, and bashful too,” answered the old man. “Well, the hay! It’s as fragrant as tea!” he repeated, wishing to change the subject.\n\nLevin looked more attentively at Ivan Parmenov and his wife. They were loading a haycock onto the cart not far from him. Ivan Parmenov was standing on the cart, taking, laying in place, and stamping down the huge bundles of hay, which his pretty young wife deftly handed up to him, at first in armfuls, and then on the pitchfork. The young wife worked easily, merrily, and dexterously. The close-packed hay did not once break away off her fork. First she gathered it together, stuck the fork into it, then with a rapid, supple movement leaned the whole weight of her body on it, and at once with a bend of her back under the red belt she drew herself up, and arching her full bosom under the white smock, with a smart turn swung the fork in her arms, and flung the bundle of hay high onto the cart. Ivan, obviously doing his best to save her every minute of unnecessary labor, made haste, opening his arms to clutch the bundle and lay it in the cart. As she raked together what was left of the hay, the young wife shook off the bits of hay that had fallen on her neck, and straightening the red kerchief that had dropped forward over her white brow, not browned like her face by the sun, she crept under the cart to tie up the load. Ivan directed her how to fasten the cord to the cross-piece, and at something she said he laughed aloud. In the expressions of both faces was to be seen vigorous, young, freshly awakened love.\n\n# Chapter 12\n\nThe load was tied on. Ivan jumped down and took the quiet, sleek horse by the bridle. The young wife flung the rake up on the load, and with a bold step, swinging her arms, she went to join the women, who were forming a ring for the haymakers’ dance. Ivan drove off to the road and fell into line with the other loaded carts. The peasant women, with their rakes on their shoulders, gay with bright flowers, and chattering with ringing, merry voices, walked behind the hay cart. One wild untrained female voice broke into a song, and sang it alone through a verse, and then the same verse was taken up and repeated by half a hundred strong healthy voices, of all sorts, coarse and fine, singing in unison.\n\nThe women, all singing, began to come close to Levin, and he felt as though a storm were swooping down upon him with a thunder of merriment. The storm swooped down, enveloped him and the haycock on which he was lying, and the other haycocks, and the wagon-loads, and the whole meadow and distant fields all seemed to be shaking and singing to the measures of this wild merry song with its shouts and whistles and clapping. Levin felt envious of this health and mirthfulness; he longed to take part in the expression of this joy of life. But he could do nothing, and had to lie and look on and listen. When the peasants, with their singing, had vanished out of sight and hearing, a weary feeling of despondency at his own isolation, his physical inactivity, his alienation from this world, came over Levin.\n\nSome of the very peasants who had been most active in wrangling with him over the hay, some whom he had treated with contumely, and who had tried to cheat him, those very peasants had greeted him good-humoredly, and evidently had not, were incapable of having any feeling of rancor against him, any regret, any recollection even of having tried to deceive him. All that was drowned in a sea of merry common labor. God gave the day, God gave the strength. And the day and the strength were consecrated to labor, and that labor was its own reward. For whom the labor? What would be its fruits? These were idle considerations—beside the point.\n\nOften Levin had admired this life, often he had a sense of envy of the men who led this life; but today for the first time, especially under the influence of what he had seen in the attitude of Ivan Parmenov to his young wife, the idea presented itself definitely to his mind that it was in his power to exchange the dreary, artificial, idle, and individualistic life he was leading for this laborious, pure, and socially delightful life.\n\nThe old man who had been sitting beside him had long ago gone home; the people had all separated. Those who lived near had gone home, while those who came from far were gathered into a group for supper, and to spend the night in the meadow. Levin, unobserved by the peasants, still lay on the haycock, and still looked on and listened and mused. The peasants who remained for the night in the meadow scarcely slept all the short summer night. At first there was the sound of merry talk and laughing all together over the supper, then singing again and laughter.\n\nAll the long day of toil had left no trace in them but lightness of heart. Before the early dawn all was hushed. Nothing was to be heard but the night sounds of the frogs that never ceased in the marsh, and the horses snorting in the mist that rose over the meadow before the morning. Rousing himself, Levin got up from the haycock, and looking at the stars, he saw that the night was over.\n\n“Well, what am I going to do? How am I to set about it?” he said to himself, trying to express to himself all the thoughts and feelings he had passed through in that brief night. All the thoughts and feelings he had passed through fell into three separate trains of thought. One was the renunciation of his old life, of his utterly useless education. This renunciation gave him satisfaction, and was easy and simple. Another series of thoughts and mental images related to the life he longed to live now. The simplicity, the purity, the sanity of this life he felt clearly, and he was convinced he would find in it the content, the peace, and the dignity, of the lack of which he was so miserably conscious. But a third series of ideas turned upon the question how to effect this transition from the old life to the new. And there nothing took clear shape for him. “Have a wife? Have work and the necessity of work? Leave Pokrovskoe? Buy land? Become a member of a peasant community? Marry a peasant girl? How am I to set about it?” he asked himself again, and could not find an answer. “I haven’t slept all night, though, and I can’t think it out clearly,” he said to himself. “I’ll work it out later. One thing’s certain, this night has decided my fate. All my old dreams of home life were absurd, not the real thing,” he told himself. “It’s all ever so much simpler and better....”\n\n“How beautiful!” he thought, looking at the strange, as it were, mother-of-pearl shell of white fleecy cloudlets resting right over his head in the middle of the sky. “How exquisite it all is in this exquisite night! And when was there time for that cloud-shell to form? Just now I looked at the sky, and there was nothing in it—only two white streaks. Yes, and so imperceptibly too my views of life changed!”\n\nHe went out of the meadow and walked along the highroad towards the village. A slight wind arose, and the sky looked gray and sullen. The gloomy moment had come that usually precedes the dawn, the full triumph of light over darkness.\n\nShrinking from the cold, Levin walked rapidly, looking at the ground. “What’s that? Someone coming,” he thought, catching the tinkle of bells, and lifting his head. Forty paces from him a carriage with four horses harnessed abreast was driving towards him along the grassy road on which he was walking. The shaft-horses were tilted against the shafts by the ruts, but the dexterous driver sitting on the box held the shaft over the ruts, so that the wheels ran on the smooth part of the road.\n\nThis was all Levin noticed, and without wondering who it could be, he gazed absently at the coach.\n\nIn the coach was an old lady dozing in one corner, and at the window, evidently only just awake, sat a young girl holding in both hands the ribbons of a white cap. With a face full of light and thought, full of a subtle, complex inner life, that was remote from Levin, she was gazing beyond him at the glow of the sunrise.\n\nAt the very instant when this apparition was vanishing, the truthful eyes glanced at him. She recognized him, and her face lighted up with wondering delight.\n\nHe could not be mistaken. There were no other eyes like those in the world. There was only one creature in the world that could concentrate for him all the brightness and meaning of life. It was she. It was Kitty. He understood that she was driving to Ergushovo from the railway station. And everything that had been stirring Levin during that sleepless night, all the resolutions he had made, all vanished at once. He recalled with horror his dreams of marrying a peasant girl. There only, in the carriage that had crossed over to the other side of the road, and was rapidly disappearing, there only could he find the solution of the riddle of his life, which had weighed so agonizingly upon him of late.\n\nShe did not look out again. The sound of the carriage-springs was no longer audible, the bells could scarcely be heard. The barking of dogs showed the carriage had reached the village, and all that was left was the empty fields all round, the village in front, and he himself isolated and apart from it all, wandering lonely along the deserted highroad.\n\nHe glanced at the sky, expecting to find there the cloud shell he had been admiring and taking as the symbol of the ideas and feelings of that night. There was nothing in the sky in the least like a shell. There, in the remote heights above, a mysterious change had been accomplished. There was no trace of shell, and there was stretched over fully half the sky an even cover of tiny and ever tinier cloudlets. The sky had grown blue and bright; and with the same softness, but with the same remoteness, it met his questioning gaze.\n\n“No,” he said to himself, “however good that life of simplicity and toil may be, I cannot go back to it. I love her.”\n\n# Chapter 13\n\nNone but those who were most intimate with Alexey Alexandrovitch knew that, while on the surface the coldest and most reasonable of men, he had one weakness quite opposed to the general trend of his character. Alexey Alexandrovitch could not hear or see a child or woman crying without being moved. The sight of tears threw him into a state of nervous agitation, and he utterly lost all power of reflection. The chief secretary of his department and his private secretary were aware of this, and used to warn women who came with petitions on no account to give way to tears, if they did not want to ruin their chances. “He will get angry, and will not listen to you,” they used to say. And as a fact, in such cases the emotional disturbance set up in Alexey Alexandrovitch by the sight of tears found expression in hasty anger. “I can do nothing. Kindly leave the room!” he would commonly cry in such cases.\n\nWhen returning from the races Anna had informed him of her relations with Vronsky, and immediately afterwards had burst into tears, hiding her face in her hands, Alexey Alexandrovitch, for all the fury aroused in him against her, was aware at the same time of a rush of that emotional disturbance always produced in him by tears. Conscious of it, and conscious that any expression of his feelings at that minute would be out of keeping with the position, he tried to suppress every manifestation of life in himself, and so neither stirred nor looked at her. This was what had caused that strange expression of deathlike rigidity in his face which had so impressed Anna.\n\nWhen they reached the house he helped her to get out of the carriage, and making an effort to master himself, took leave of her with his usual urbanity, and uttered that phrase that bound him to nothing; he said that tomorrow he would let her know his decision.\n\nHis wife’s words, confirming his worst suspicions, had sent a cruel pang to the heart of Alexey Alexandrovitch. That pang was intensified by the strange feeling of physical pity for her set up by her tears. But when he was all alone in the carriage Alexey Alexandrovitch, to his surprise and delight, felt complete relief both from this pity and from the doubts and agonies of jealousy.\n\nHe experienced the sensations of a man who has had a tooth out after suffering long from toothache. After a fearful agony and a sense of something huge, bigger than the head itself, being torn out of his jaw, the sufferer, hardly able to believe in his own good luck, feels all at once that what has so long poisoned his existence and enchained his attention, exists no longer, and that he can live and think again, and take interest in other things besides his tooth. This feeling Alexey Alexandrovitch was experiencing. The agony had been strange and terrible, but now it was over; he felt that he could live again and think of something other than his wife.\n\n“No honor, no heart, no religion; a corrupt woman. I always knew it and always saw it, though I tried to deceive myself to spare her,” he said to himself. And it actually seemed to him that he always had seen it: he recalled incidents of their past life, in which he had never seen anything wrong before—now these incidents proved clearly that she had always been a corrupt woman. “I made a mistake in linking my life to hers; but there was nothing wrong in my mistake, and so I cannot be unhappy. It’s not I that am to blame,” he told himself, “but she. But I have nothing to do with her. She does not exist for me....”\n\nEverything relating to her and her son, towards whom his sentiments were as much changed as towards her, ceased to interest him. The only thing that interested him now was the question of in what way he could best, with most propriety and comfort for himself, and thus with most justice, extricate himself from the mud with which she had spattered him in her fall, and then proceed along his path of active, honorable, and useful existence.\n\n“I cannot be made unhappy by the fact that a contemptible woman has committed a crime. I have only to find the best way out of the difficult position in which she has placed me. And I shall find it,” he said to himself, frowning more and more. “I’m not the first nor the last.” And to say nothing of historical instances dating from the “Fair Helen” of Menelaus, recently revived in the memory of all, a whole list of contemporary examples of husbands with unfaithful wives in the highest society rose before Alexey Alexandrovitch’s imagination. “Daryalov, Poltavsky, Prince Karibanov, Count Paskudin, Dram.... Yes, even Dram, such an honest, capable fellow ... Semyonov, Tchagin, Sigonin,” Alexey Alexandrovitch remembered. “Admitting that a certain quite irrational ridicule falls to the lot of these men, yet I never saw anything but a misfortune in it, and always felt sympathy for it,” Alexey Alexandrovitch said to himself, though indeed this was not the fact, and he had never felt sympathy for misfortunes of that kind, but the more frequently he had heard of instances of unfaithful wives betraying their husbands, the more highly he had thought of himself. “It is a misfortune which may befall anyone. And this misfortune has befallen me. The only thing to be done is to make the best of the position.”\n\nAnd he began passing in review the methods of proceeding of men who had been in the same position that he was in.\n\n“Daryalov fought a duel....”\n\nThe duel had particularly fascinated the thoughts of Alexey Alexandrovitch in his youth, just because he was physically a coward, and was himself well aware of the fact. Alexey Alexandrovitch could not without horror contemplate the idea of a pistol aimed at himself, and had never made use of any weapon in his life. This horror had in his youth set him pondering on dueling, and picturing himself in a position in which he would have to expose his life to danger. Having attained success and an established position in the world, he had long ago forgotten this feeling; but the habitual bent of feeling reasserted itself, and dread of his own cowardice proved even now so strong that Alexey Alexandrovitch spent a long while thinking over the question of dueling in all its aspects, and hugging the idea of a duel, though he was fully aware beforehand that he would never under any circumstances fight one.\n\n“There’s no doubt our society is still so barbarous (it’s not the same in England) that very many”—and among these were those whose opinion Alexey Alexandrovitch particularly valued—“look favorably on the duel; but what result is attained by it? Suppose I call him out,” Alexey Alexandrovitch went on to himself, and vividly picturing the night he would spend after the challenge, and the pistol aimed at him, he shuddered, and knew that he never would do it—“suppose I call him out. Suppose I am taught,” he went on musing, “to shoot; I press the trigger,” he said to himself, closing his eyes, “and it turns out I have killed him,” Alexey Alexandrovitch said to himself, and he shook his head as though to dispel such silly ideas. “What sense is there in murdering a man in order to define one’s relation to a guilty wife and son? I should still just as much have to decide what I ought to do with her. But what is more probable and what would doubtless occur—I should be killed or wounded. I, the innocent person, should be the victim—killed or wounded. It’s even more senseless. But apart from that, a challenge to fight would be an act hardly honest on my side. Don’t I know perfectly well that my friends would never allow me to fight a duel—would never allow the life of a statesman, needed by Russia, to be exposed to danger? Knowing perfectly well beforehand that the matter would never come to real danger, it would amount to my simply trying to gain a certain sham reputation by such a challenge. That would be dishonest, that would be false, that would be deceiving myself and others. A duel is quite irrational, and no one expects it of me. My aim is simply to safeguard my reputation, which is essential for the uninterrupted pursuit of my public duties.” Official duties, which had always been of great consequence in Alexey Alexandrovitch’s eyes, seemed of special importance to his mind at this moment. Considering and rejecting the duel, Alexey Alexandrovitch turned to divorce—another solution selected by several of the husbands he remembered. Passing in mental review all the instances he knew of divorces (there were plenty of them in the very highest society with which he was very familiar), Alexey Alexandrovitch could not find a single example in which the object of divorce was that which he had in view. In all these instances the husband had practically ceded or sold his unfaithful wife, and the very party which, being in fault, had not the right to contract a fresh marriage, had formed counterfeit, pseudo-matrimonial ties with a self-styled husband. In his own case, Alexey Alexandrovitch saw that a legal divorce, that is to say, one in which only the guilty wife would be repudiated, was impossible of attainment. He saw that the complex conditions of the life they led made the coarse proofs of his wife’s guilt, required by the law, out of the question; he saw that a certain refinement in that life would not admit of such proofs being brought forward, even if he had them, and that to bring forward such proofs would damage him in the public estimation more than it would her.\n\nAn attempt at divorce could lead to nothing but a public scandal, which would be a perfect godsend to his enemies for calumny and attacks on his high position in society. His chief object, to define the position with the least amount of disturbance possible, would not be attained by divorce either. Moreover, in the event of divorce, or even of an attempt to obtain a divorce, it was obvious that the wife broke off all relations with the husband and threw in her lot with the lover. And in spite of the complete, as he supposed, contempt and indifference he now felt for his wife, at the bottom of his heart Alexey Alexandrovitch still had one feeling left in regard to her—a disinclination to see her free to throw in her lot with Vronsky, so that her crime would be to her advantage. The mere notion of this so exasperated Alexey Alexandrovitch, that directly it rose to his mind he groaned with inward agony, and got up and changed his place in the carriage, and for a long while after, he sat with scowling brows, wrapping his numbed and bony legs in the fleecy rug.\n\n“Apart from formal divorce, One might still do like Karibanov, Paskudin, and that good fellow Dram—that is, separate from one’s wife,” he went on thinking, when he had regained his composure. But this step too presented the same drawback of public scandal as a divorce, and what was more, a separation, quite as much as a regular divorce, flung his wife into the arms of Vronsky. “No, it’s out of the question, out of the question!” he said again, twisting his rug about him again. “I cannot be unhappy, but neither she nor he ought to be happy.”\n\nThe feeling of jealousy, which had tortured him during the period of uncertainty, had passed away at the instant when the tooth had been with agony extracted by his wife’s words. But that feeling had been replaced by another, the desire, not merely that she should not be triumphant, but that she should get due punishment for her crime. He did not acknowledge this feeling, but at the bottom of his heart he longed for her to suffer for having destroyed his peace of mind—his honor. And going once again over the conditions inseparable from a duel, a divorce, a separation, and once again rejecting them, Alexey Alexandrovitch felt convinced that there was only one solution,—to keep her with him, concealing what had happened from the world, and using every measure in his power to break off the intrigue, and still more—though this he did not admit to himself—to punish her. “I must inform her of my conclusion, that thinking over the terrible position in which she has placed her family, all other solutions will be worse for both sides than an external status quo, and that such I agree to retain, on the strict condition of obedience on her part to my wishes, that is to say, cessation of all intercourse with her lover.” When this decision had been finally adopted, another weighty consideration occurred to Alexey Alexandrovitch in support of it. “By such a course only shall I be acting in accordance with the dictates of religion,” he told himself. “In adopting this course, I am not casting off a guilty wife, but giving her a chance of amendment; and, indeed, difficult as the task will be to me, I shall devote part of my energies to her reformation and salvation.”\n\nThough Alexey Alexandrovitch was perfectly aware that he could not exert any moral influence over his wife, that such an attempt at reformation could lead to nothing but falsity; though in passing through these difficult moments he had not once thought of seeking guidance in religion, yet now, when his conclusion corresponded, as it seemed to him, with the requirements of religion, this religious sanction to his decision gave him complete satisfaction, and to some extent restored his peace of mind. He was pleased to think that, even in such an important crisis in life, no one would be able to say that he had not acted in accordance with the principles of that religion whose banner he had always held aloft amid the general coolness and indifference. As he pondered over subsequent developments, Alexey Alexandrovitch did not see, indeed, why his relations with his wife should not remain practically the same as before. No doubt, she could never regain his esteem, but there was not, and there could not be, any sort of reason that his existence should be troubled, and that he should suffer because she was a bad and faithless wife. “Yes, time will pass; time, which arranges all things, and the old relations will be reestablished,” Alexey Alexandrovitch told himself; “so far reestablished, that is, that I shall not be sensible of a break in the continuity of my life. She is bound to be unhappy, but I am not to blame, and so I cannot be unhappy.”\n\n# Chapter 14\n\nAs he neared Petersburg, Alexey Alexandrovitch not only adhered entirely to his decision, but was even composing in his head the letter he would write to his wife. Going into the porter’s room, Alexey Alexandrovitch glanced at the letters and papers brought from his office, and directed that they should be brought to him in his study.\n\n“The horses can be taken out and I will see no one,” he said in answer to the porter, with a certain pleasure, indicative of his agreeable frame of mind, emphasizing the words, “see no one.”\n\nIn his study Alexey Alexandrovitch walked up and down twice, and stopped at an immense writing-table, on which six candles had already been lighted by the valet who had preceded him. He cracked his knuckles and sat down, sorting out his writing appurtenances. Putting his elbows on the table, he bent his head on one side, thought a minute, and began to write, without pausing for a second. He wrote without using any form of address to her, and wrote in French, making use of the plural “vous,” which has not the same note of coldness as the corresponding Russian form.\n\n“At our last conversation, I notified you of my intention to communicate to you my decision in regard to the subject of that conversation. Having carefully considered everything, I am writing now with the object of fulfilling that promise. My decision is as follows. Whatever your conduct may have been, I do not consider myself justified in breaking the ties in which we are bound by a Higher Power. The family cannot be broken up by a whim, a caprice, or even by the sin of one of the partners in the marriage, and our life must go on as it has done in the past. This is essential for me, for you, and for our son. I am fully persuaded that you have repented and do repent of what has called forth the present letter, and that you will cooperate with me in eradicating the cause of our estrangement, and forgetting the past. In the contrary event, you can conjecture what awaits you and your son. All this I hope to discuss more in detail in a personal interview. As the season is drawing to a close, I would beg you to return to Petersburg as quickly as possible, not later than Tuesday. All necessary preparations shall be made for your arrival here. I beg you to note that I attach particular significance to compliance with this request.\n\nA. Karenin\n\n“P.S.—I enclose the money which may be needed for your expenses.”\n\nHe read the letter through and felt pleased with it, and especially that he had remembered to enclose money: there was not a harsh word, not a reproach in it, nor was there undue indulgence. Most of all, it was a golden bridge for return. Folding the letter and smoothing it with a massive ivory knife, and putting it in an envelope with the money, he rang the bell with the gratification it always afforded him to use the well arranged appointments of his writing-table.\n\n“Give this to the courier to be delivered to Anna Arkadyevna tomorrow at the summer villa,” he said, getting up.\n\n“Certainly, your excellency; tea to be served in the study?”\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch ordered tea to be brought to the study, and playing with the massive paper-knife, he moved to his easy chair, near which there had been placed ready for him a lamp and the French work on Egyptian hieroglyphics that he had begun. Over the easy chair there hung in a gold frame an oval portrait of Anna, a fine painting by a celebrated artist. Alexey Alexandrovitch glanced at it. The unfathomable eyes gazed ironically and insolently at him. Insufferably insolent and challenging was the effect in Alexey Alexandrovitch’s eyes of the black lace about the head, admirably touched in by the painter, the black hair and handsome white hand with one finger lifted, covered with rings. After looking at the portrait for a minute, Alexey Alexandrovitch shuddered so that his lips quivered and he uttered the sound “brrr,” and turned away. He made haste to sit down in his easy chair and opened the book. He tried to read, but he could not revive the very vivid interest he had felt before in Egyptian hieroglyphics. He looked at the book and thought of something else. He thought not of his wife, but of a complication that had arisen in his official life, which at the time constituted the chief interest of it. He felt that he had penetrated more deeply than ever before into this intricate affair, and that he had originated a leading idea—he could say it without self-flattery—calculated to clear up the whole business, to strengthen him in his official career, to discomfit his enemies, and thereby to be of the greatest benefit to the government. Directly the servant had set the tea and left the room, Alexey Alexandrovitch got up and went to the writing-table. Moving into the middle of the table a portfolio of papers, with a scarcely perceptible smile of self-satisfaction, he took a pencil from a rack and plunged into the perusal of a complex report relating to the present complication. The complication was of this nature: Alexey Alexandrovitch’s characteristic quality as a politician, that special individual qualification that every rising functionary possesses, the qualification that with his unflagging ambition, his reserve, his honesty, and with his self-confidence had made his career, was his contempt for red tape, his cutting down of correspondence, his direct contact, wherever possible, with the living fact, and his economy. It happened that the famous Commission of the 2nd of June had set on foot an inquiry into the irrigation of lands in the Zaraisky province, which fell under Alexey Alexandrovitch’s department, and was a glaring example of fruitless expenditure and paper reforms. Alexey Alexandrovitch was aware of the truth of this. The irrigation of these lands in the Zaraisky province had been initiated by the predecessor of Alexey Alexandrovitch’s predecessor. And vast sums of money had actually been spent and were still being spent on this business, and utterly unproductively, and the whole business could obviously lead to nothing whatever. Alexey Alexandrovitch had perceived this at once on entering office, and would have liked to lay hands on the Board of Irrigation. But at first, when he did not yet feel secure in his position, he knew it would affect too many interests, and would be injudicious. Later on he had been engrossed in other questions, and had simply forgotten the Board of Irrigation. It went of itself, like all such boards, by the mere force of inertia. (Many people gained their livelihood by the Board of Irrigation, especially one highly conscientious and musical family: all the daughters played on stringed instruments, and Alexey Alexandrovitch knew the family and had stood godfather to one of the elder daughters.) The raising of this question by a hostile department was in Alexey Alexandrovitch’s opinion a dishonorable proceeding, seeing that in every department there were things similar and worse, which no one inquired into, for well-known reasons of official etiquette. However, now that the glove had been thrown down to him, he had boldly picked it up and demanded the appointment of a special commission to investigate and verify the working of the Board of Irrigation of the lands in the Zaraisky province. But in compensation he gave no quarter to the enemy either. He demanded the appointment of another special commission to inquire into the question of the Native Tribes Organization Committee. The question of the Native Tribes had been brought up incidentally in the Commission of the 2nd of June, and had been pressed forward actively by Alexey Alexandrovitch as one admitting of no delay on account of the deplorable condition of the native tribes. In the commission this question had been a ground of contention between several departments. The department hostile to Alexey Alexandrovitch proved that the condition of the native tribes was exceedingly flourishing, that the proposed reconstruction might be the ruin of their prosperity, and that if there were anything wrong, it arose mainly from the failure on the part of Alexey Alexandrovitch’s department to carry out the measures prescribed by law. Now Alexey Alexandrovitch intended to demand: First, that a new commission should be formed which should be empowered to investigate the condition of the native tribes on the spot; secondly, if it should appear that the condition of the native tribes actually was such as it appeared to be from the official documents in the hands of the committee, that another new scientific commission should be appointed to investigate the deplorable condition of the native tribes from the—(1) political, (2) administrative, (3) economic, (4) ethnographical, (5) material, and (6) religious points of view; thirdly, that evidence should be required from the rival department of the measures that had been taken during the last ten years by that department for averting the disastrous conditions in which the native tribes were now placed; and fourthly and finally, that that department explain why it had, as appeared from the evidence before the committee, from No. 17,015 and 18,038, from December 5, 1863, and June 7, 1864, acted in direct contravention of the intent of the law T... Act 18, and the note to Act 36. A flash of eagerness suffused the face of Alexey Alexandrovitch as he rapidly wrote out a synopsis of these ideas for his own benefit. Having filled a sheet of paper, he got up, rang, and sent a note to the chief secretary of his department to look up certain necessary facts for him. Getting up and walking about the room, he glanced again at the portrait, frowned, and smiled contemptuously. After reading a little more of the book on Egyptian hieroglyphics, and renewing his interest in it, Alexey Alexandrovitch went to bed at eleven o’clock, and recollecting as he lay in bed the incident with his wife, he saw it now in by no means such a gloomy light.\n\n# Chapter 15\n\nThough Anna had obstinately and with exasperation contradicted Vronsky when he told her their position was impossible, at the bottom of her heart she regarded her own position as false and dishonorable, and she longed with her whole soul to change it. On the way home from the races she had told her husband the truth in a moment of excitement, and in spite of the agony she had suffered in doing so, she was glad of it. After her husband had left her, she told herself that she was glad, that now everything was made clear, and at least there would be no more lying and deception. It seemed to her beyond doubt that her position was now made clear forever. It might be bad, this new position, but it would be clear; there would be no indefiniteness or falsehood about it. The pain she had caused herself and her husband in uttering those words would be rewarded now by everything being made clear, she thought. That evening she saw Vronsky, but she did not tell him of what had passed between her and her husband, though, to make the position definite, it was necessary to tell him.\n\nWhen she woke up next morning the first thing that rose to her mind was what she had said to her husband, and those words seemed to her so awful that she could not conceive now how she could have brought herself to utter those strange, coarse words, and could not imagine what would come of it. But the words were spoken, and Alexey Alexandrovitch had gone away without saying anything. “I saw Vronsky and did not tell him. At the very instant he was going away I would have turned him back and told him, but I changed my mind, because it was strange that I had not told him the first minute. Why was it I wanted to tell him and did not tell him?” And in answer to this question a burning blush of shame spread over her face. She knew what had kept her from it, she knew that she had been ashamed. Her position, which had seemed to her simplified the night before, suddenly struck her now as not only not simple, but as absolutely hopeless. She felt terrified at the disgrace, of which she had not ever thought before. Directly she thought of what her husband would do, the most terrible ideas came to her mind. She had a vision of being turned out of the house, of her shame being proclaimed to all the world. She asked herself where she should go when she was turned out of the house, and she could not find an answer.\n\nWhen she thought of Vronsky, it seemed to her that he did not love her, that he was already beginning to be tired of her, that she could not offer herself to him, and she felt bitter against him for it. It seemed to her that the words that she had spoken to her husband, and had continually repeated in her imagination, she had said to everyone, and everyone had heard them. She could not bring herself to look those of her own household in the face. She could not bring herself to call her maid, and still less go downstairs and see her son and his governess.\n\nThe maid, who had been listening at her door for a long while, came into her room of her own accord. Anna glanced inquiringly into her face, and blushed with a scared look. The maid begged her pardon for coming in, saying that she had fancied the bell rang. She brought her clothes and a note. The note was from Betsy. Betsy reminded her that Liza Merkalova and Baroness Shtoltz were coming to play croquet with her that morning with their adorers, Kaluzhsky and old Stremov. “Come, if only as a study in morals. I shall expect you,” she finished.\n\nAnna read the note and heaved a deep sigh.\n\n“Nothing, I need nothing,” she said to Annushka, who was rearranging the bottles and brushes on the dressing table. “You can go. I’ll dress at once and come down. I need nothing.”\n\nAnnushka went out, but Anna did not begin dressing, and sat in the same position, her head and hands hanging listlessly, and every now and then she shivered all over, seemed as though she would make some gesture, utter some word, and sank back into lifelessness again. She repeated continually, “My God! my God!” But neither “God” nor “my” had any meaning to her. The idea of seeking help in her difficulty in religion was as remote from her as seeking help from Alexey Alexandrovitch himself, although she had never had doubts of the faith in which she had been brought up. She knew that the support of religion was possible only upon condition of renouncing what made up for her the whole meaning of life. She was not simply miserable, she began to feel alarm at the new spiritual condition, never experienced before, in which she found herself. She felt as though everything were beginning to be double in her soul, just as objects sometimes appear double to over-tired eyes. She hardly knew at times what it was she feared, and what she hoped for. Whether she feared or desired what had happened, or what was going to happen, and exactly what she longed for, she could not have said.\n\n“Ah, what am I doing!” she said to herself, feeling a sudden thrill of pain in both sides of her head. When she came to herself, she saw that she was holding her hair in both hands, each side of her temples, and pulling it. She jumped up, and began walking about.\n\n“The coffee is ready, and mademoiselle and Seryozha are waiting,” said Annushka, coming back again and finding Anna in the same position.\n\n“Seryozha? What about Seryozha?” Anna asked, with sudden eagerness, recollecting her son’s existence for the first time that morning.\n\n“He’s been naughty, I think,” answered Annushka with a smile.\n\n“In what way?”\n\n“Some peaches were lying on the table in the corner room. I think he slipped in and ate one of them on the sly.”\n\nThe recollection of her son suddenly roused Anna from the helpless condition in which she found herself. She recalled the partly sincere, though greatly exaggerated, rôle of the mother living for her child, which she had taken up of late years, and she felt with joy that in the plight in which she found herself she had a support, quite apart from her relation to her husband or to Vronsky. This support was her son. In whatever position she might be placed, she could not lose her son. Her husband might put her to shame and turn her out, Vronsky might grow cold to her and go on living his own life apart (she thought of him again with bitterness and reproach); she could not leave her son. She had an aim in life. And she must act; act to secure this relation to her son, so that he might not be taken from her. Quickly indeed, as quickly as possible, she must take action before he was taken from her. She must take her son and go away. Here was the one thing she had to do now. She needed consolation. She must be calm, and get out of this insufferable position. The thought of immediate action binding her to her son, of going away somewhere with him, gave her this consolation.\n\nShe dressed quickly, went downstairs, and with resolute steps walked into the drawing-room, where she found, as usual, waiting for her, the coffee, Seryozha, and his governess. Seryozha, all in white, with his back and head bent, was standing at a table under a looking-glass, and with an expression of intense concentration which she knew well, and in which he resembled his father, he was doing something to the flowers he carried.\n\nThe governess had a particularly severe expression. Seryozha screamed shrilly, as he often did, “Ah, mamma!” and stopped, hesitating whether to go to greet his mother and put down the flowers, or to finish making the wreath and go with the flowers.\n\nThe governess, after saying good-morning, began a long and detailed account of Seryozha’s naughtiness, but Anna did not hear her; she was considering whether she would take her with her or not. “No, I won’t take her,” she decided. “I’ll go alone with my child.”\n\n“Yes, it’s very wrong,” said Anna, and taking her son by the shoulder she looked at him, not severely, but with a timid glance that bewildered and delighted the boy, and she kissed him. “Leave him to me,” she said to the astonished governess, and not letting go of her son, she sat down at the table, where coffee was set ready for her.\n\n“Mamma! I ... I ... didn’t....” he said, trying to make out from her expression what was in store for him in regard to the peaches.\n\n“Seryozha,” she said, as soon as the governess had left the room, “that was wrong, but you’ll never do it again, will you?... You love me?”\n\nShe felt that the tears were coming into her eyes. “Can I help loving him?” she said to herself, looking deeply into his scared and at the same time delighted eyes. “And can he ever join his father in punishing me? Is it possible he will not feel for me?” Tears were already flowing down her face, and to hide them she got up abruptly and almost ran out on to the terrace.\n\nAfter the thunder showers of the last few days, cold, bright weather had set in. The air was cold in the bright sun that filtered through the freshly washed leaves.\n\nShe shivered, both from the cold and from the inward horror which had clutched her with fresh force in the open air.\n\n“Run along, run along to Mariette,” she said to Seryozha, who had followed her out, and she began walking up and down on the straw matting of the terrace. “Can it be that they won’t forgive me, won’t understand how it all couldn’t be helped?” she said to herself.\n\nStanding still, and looking at the tops of the aspen trees waving in the wind, with their freshly washed, brightly shining leaves in the cold sunshine, she knew that they would not forgive her, that everyone and everything would be merciless to her now as was that sky, that green. And again she felt that everything was split in two in her soul. “I mustn’t, mustn’t think,” she said to herself. “I must get ready. To go where? When? Whom to take with me? Yes, to Moscow by the evening train. Annushka and Seryozha, and only the most necessary things. But first I must write to them both.” She went quickly indoors into her boudoir, sat down at the table, and wrote to her husband:—“After what has happened, I cannot remain any longer in your house. I am going away, and taking my son with me. I don’t know the law, and so I don’t know with which of the parents the son should remain; but I take him with me because I cannot live without him. Be generous, leave him to me.”\n\nUp to this point she wrote rapidly and naturally, but the appeal to his generosity, a quality she did not recognize in him, and the necessity of winding up the letter with something touching, pulled her up. “Of my fault and my remorse I cannot speak, because....”\n\nShe stopped again, finding no connection in her ideas. “No,” she said to herself, “there’s no need of anything,” and tearing up the letter, she wrote it again, leaving out the allusion to generosity, and sealed it up.\n\nAnother letter had to be written to Vronsky. “I have told my husband,” she wrote, and she sat a long while unable to write more. It was so coarse, so unfeminine. “And what more am I to write to him?” she said to herself. Again a flush of shame spread over her face; she recalled his composure, and a feeling of anger against him impelled her to tear the sheet with the phrase she had written into tiny bits. “No need of anything,” she said to herself, and closing her blotting-case she went upstairs, told the governess and the servants that she was going that day to Moscow, and at once set to work to pack up her things.\n\n# Chapter 16\n\nAll the rooms of the summer villa were full of porters, gardeners, and footmen going to and fro carrying out things. Cupboards and chests were open; twice they had sent to the shop for cord; pieces of newspaper were tossing about on the floor. Two trunks, some bags and strapped-up rugs, had been carried down into the hall. The carriage and two hired cabs were waiting at the steps. Anna, forgetting her inward agitation in the work of packing, was standing at a table in her boudoir, packing her traveling bag, when Annushka called her attention to the rattle of some carriage driving up. Anna looked out of the window and saw Alexey Alexandrovitch’s courier on the steps, ringing at the front door bell.\n\n“Run and find out what it is,” she said, and with a calm sense of being prepared for anything, she sat down in a low chair, folding her hands on her knees. A footman brought in a thick packet directed in Alexey Alexandrovitch’s hand.\n\n“The courier has orders to wait for an answer,” he said.\n\n“Very well,” she said, and as soon as he had left the room she tore open the letter with trembling fingers. A roll of unfolded notes done up in a wrapper fell out of it. She disengaged the letter and began reading it at the end. “Preparations shall be made for your arrival here ... I attach particular significance to compliance....” she read. She ran on, then back, read it all through, and once more read the letter all through again from the beginning. When she had finished, she felt that she was cold all over, and that a fearful calamity, such as she had not expected, had burst upon her.\n\nIn the morning she had regretted that she had spoken to her husband, and wished for nothing so much as that those words could be unspoken. And here this letter regarded them as unspoken, and gave her what she had wanted. But now this letter seemed to her more awful than anything she had been able to conceive.\n\n“He’s right!” she said; “of course, he’s always right; he’s a Christian, he’s generous! Yes, vile, base creature! And no one understands it except me, and no one ever will; and I can’t explain it. They say he’s so religious, so high-principled, so upright, so clever; but they don’t see what I’ve seen. They don’t know how he has crushed my life for eight years, crushed everything that was living in me—he has not once even thought that I’m a live woman who must have love. They don’t know how at every step he’s humiliated me, and been just as pleased with himself. Haven’t I striven, striven with all my strength, to find something to give meaning to my life? Haven’t I struggled to love him, to love my son when I could not love my husband? But the time came when I knew that I couldn’t cheat myself any longer, that I was alive, that I was not to blame, that God has made me so that I must love and live. And now what does he do? If he’d killed me, if he’d killed him, I could have borne anything, I could have forgiven anything; but, no, he.... How was it I didn’t guess what he would do? He’s doing just what’s characteristic of his mean character. He’ll keep himself in the right, while me, in my ruin, he’ll drive still lower to worse ruin yet....”\n\nShe recalled the words from the letter. “You can conjecture what awaits you and your son....” “That’s a threat to take away my child, and most likely by their stupid law he can. But I know very well why he says it. He doesn’t believe even in my love for my child, or he despises it (just as he always used to ridicule it). He despises that feeling in me, but he knows that I won’t abandon my child, that I can’t abandon my child, that there could be no life for me without my child, even with him whom I love; but that if I abandoned my child and ran away from him, I should be acting like the most infamous, basest of women. He knows that, and knows that I am incapable of doing that.”\n\nShe recalled another sentence in the letter. “Our life must go on as it has done in the past....” “That life was miserable enough in the old days; it has been awful of late. What will it be now? And he knows all that; he knows that I can’t repent that I breathe, that I love; he knows that it can lead to nothing but lying and deceit; but he wants to go on torturing me. I know him; I know that he’s at home and is happy in deceit, like a fish swimming in the water. No, I won’t give him that happiness. I’ll break through the spiderweb of lies in which he wants to catch me, come what may. Anything’s better than lying and deceit.”\n\n“But how? My God! my God! Was ever a woman so miserable as I am?...”\n\n“No; I will break through it, I will break through it!” she cried, jumping up and keeping back her tears. And she went to the writing-table to write him another letter. But at the bottom of her heart she felt that she was not strong enough to break through anything, that she was not strong enough to get out of her old position, however false and dishonorable it might be.\n\nShe sat down at the writing-table, but instead of writing she clasped her hands on the table, and, laying her head on them, burst into tears, with sobs and heaving breast like a child crying. She was weeping that her dream of her position being made clear and definite had been annihilated forever. She knew beforehand that everything would go on in the old way, and far worse, indeed, than in the old way. She felt that the position in the world that she enjoyed, and that had seemed to her of so little consequence in the morning, that this position was precious to her, that she would not have the strength to exchange it for the shameful position of a woman who has abandoned husband and child to join her lover; that however much she might struggle, she could not be stronger than herself. She would never know freedom in love, but would remain forever a guilty wife, with the menace of detection hanging over her at every instant; deceiving her husband for the sake of a shameful connection with a man living apart and away from her, whose life she could never share. She knew that this was how it would be, and at the same time it was so awful that she could not even conceive what it would end in. And she cried without restraint, as children cry when they are punished.\n\nThe sound of the footman’s steps forced her to rouse herself, and, hiding her face from him, she pretended to be writing.\n\n“The courier asks if there’s an answer,” the footman announced.\n\n“An answer? Yes,” said Anna. “Let him wait. I’ll ring.”\n\n“What can I write?” she thought. “What can I decide upon alone? What do I know? What do I want? What is there I care for?” Again she felt that her soul was beginning to be split in two. She was terrified again at this feeling, and clutched at the first pretext for doing something which might divert her thoughts from herself. “I ought to see Alexey” (so she called Vronsky in her thoughts); “no one but he can tell me what I ought to do. I’ll go to Betsy’s, perhaps I shall see him there,” she said to herself, completely forgetting that when she had told him the day before that she was not going to Princess Tverskaya’s, he had said that in that case he should not go either. She went up to the table, wrote to her husband, “I have received your letter.—A.”; and, ringing the bell, gave it to the footman.\n\n“We are not going,” she said to Annushka, as she came in.\n\n“Not going at all?”\n\n“No; don’t unpack till tomorrow, and let the carriage wait. I’m going to the princess’s.”\n\n“Which dress am I to get ready?”\n\n# Chapter 17\n\nThe croquet party to which the Princess Tverskaya had invited Anna was to consist of two ladies and their adorers. These two ladies were the chief representatives of a select new Petersburg circle, nicknamed, in imitation of some imitation, les sept merveilles du monde. These ladies belonged to a circle which, though of the highest society, was utterly hostile to that in which Anna moved. Moreover, Stremov, one of the most influential people in Petersburg, and the elderly admirer of Liza Merkalova, was Alexey Alexandrovitch’s enemy in the political world. From all these considerations Anna had not meant to go, and the hints in Princess Tverskaya’s note referred to her refusal. But now Anna was eager to go, in the hope of seeing Vronsky.\n\nAnna arrived at Princess Tverskaya’s earlier than the other guests.\n\nAt the same moment as she entered, Vronsky’s footman, with side-whiskers combed out like a Kammerjunker, went in too. He stopped at the door, and, taking off his cap, let her pass. Anna recognized him, and only then recalled that Vronsky had told her the day before that he would not come. Most likely he was sending a note to say so.\n\nAs she took off her outer garment in the hall, she heard the footman, pronouncing his “r’s” even like a Kammerjunker, say, “From the count for the princess,” and hand the note.\n\nShe longed to question him as to where his master was. She longed to turn back and send him a letter to come and see her, or to go herself to see him. But neither the first nor the second nor the third course was possible. Already she heard bells ringing to announce her arrival ahead of her, and Princess Tverskaya’s footman was standing at the open door waiting for her to go forward into the inner rooms.\n\n“The princess is in the garden; they will inform her immediately. Would you be pleased to walk into the garden?” announced another footman in another room.\n\nThe position of uncertainty, of indecision, was still the same as at home—worse, in fact, since it was impossible to take any step, impossible to see Vronsky, and she had to remain here among outsiders, in company so uncongenial to her present mood. But she was wearing a dress that she knew suited her. She was not alone; all around was that luxurious setting of idleness that she was used to, and she felt less wretched than at home. She was not forced to think what she was to do. Everything would be done of itself. On meeting Betsy coming towards her in a white gown that struck her by its elegance, Anna smiled at her just as she always did. Princess Tverskaya was walking with Tushkevitch and a young lady, a relation, who, to the great joy of her parents in the provinces, was spending the summer with the fashionable princess.\n\nThere was probably something unusual about Anna, for Betsy noticed it at once.\n\n“I slept badly,” answered Anna, looking intently at the footman who came to meet them, and, as she supposed, brought Vronsky’s note.\n\n“How glad I am you’ve come!” said Betsy. “I’m tired, and was just longing to have some tea before they come. You might go”—she turned to Tushkevitch—“with Masha, and try the croquet ground over there where they’ve been cutting it. We shall have time to talk a little over tea; we’ll have a cozy chat, eh?” she said in English to Anna, with a smile, pressing the hand with which she held a parasol.\n\n“Yes, especially as I can’t stay very long with you. I’m forced to go on to old Madame Vrede. I’ve been promising to go for a century,” said Anna, to whom lying, alien as it was to her nature, had become not merely simple and natural in society, but a positive source of satisfaction. Why she said this, which she had not thought of a second before, she could not have explained. She had said it simply from the reflection that as Vronsky would not be here, she had better secure her own freedom, and try to see him somehow. But why she had spoken of old Madame Vrede, whom she had to go and see, as she had to see many other people, she could not have explained; and yet, as it afterwards turned out, had she contrived the most cunning devices to meet Vronsky, she could have thought of nothing better.\n\n“No. I’m not going to let you go for anything,” answered Betsy, looking intently into Anna’s face. “Really, if I were not fond of you, I should feel offended. One would think you were afraid my society would compromise you. Tea in the little dining-room, please,” she said, half closing her eyes, as she always did when addressing the footman.\n\nTaking the note from him, she read it.\n\n“Alexey’s playing us false,” she said in French; “he writes that he can’t come,” she added in a tone as simple and natural as though it could never enter her head that Vronsky could mean anything more to Anna than a game of croquet. Anna knew that Betsy knew everything, but, hearing how she spoke of Vronsky before her, she almost felt persuaded for a minute that she knew nothing.\n\n“Ah!” said Anna indifferently, as though not greatly interested in the matter, and she went on smiling: “How can you or your friends compromise anyone?”\n\nThis playing with words, this hiding of a secret, had a great fascination for Anna, as, indeed, it has for all women. And it was not the necessity of concealment, not the aim with which the concealment was contrived, but the process of concealment itself which attracted her.\n\n“I can’t be more Catholic than the Pope,” she said. “Stremov and Liza Merkalova, why, they’re the cream of the cream of society. Besides, they’re received everywhere, and I”—she laid special stress on the I—“have never been strict and intolerant. It’s simply that I haven’t the time.”\n\n“No; you don’t care, perhaps, to meet Stremov? Let him and Alexey Alexandrovitch tilt at each other in the committee—that’s no affair of ours. But in the world, he’s the most amiable man I know, and a devoted croquet player. You shall see. And, in spite of his absurd position as Liza’s lovesick swain at his age, you ought to see how he carries off the absurd position. He’s very nice. Sappho Shtoltz you don’t know? Oh, that’s a new type, quite new.”\n\nBetsy said all this, and, at the same time, from her good-humored, shrewd glance, Anna felt that she partly guessed her plight, and was hatching something for her benefit. They were in the little boudoir.\n\n“I must write to Alexey though,” and Betsy sat down to the table, scribbled a few lines, and put the note in an envelope.\n\n“I’m telling him to come to dinner. I’ve one lady extra to dinner with me, and no man to take her in. Look what I’ve said, will that persuade him? Excuse me, I must leave you for a minute. Would you seal it up, please, and send it off?” she said from the door; “I have to give some directions.”\n\nWithout a moment’s thought, Anna sat down to the table with Betsy’s letter, and, without reading it, wrote below: “It’s essential for me to see you. Come to the Vrede garden. I shall be there at six o’clock.” She sealed it up, and, Betsy coming back, in her presence handed the note to be taken.\n\nAt tea, which was brought them on a little tea-table in the cool little drawing-room, the cozy chat promised by Princess Tverskaya before the arrival of her visitors really did come off between the two women. They criticized the people they were expecting, and the conversation fell upon Liza Merkalova.\n\n“She’s very sweet, and I always liked her,” said Anna.\n\n“You ought to like her. She raves about you. Yesterday she came up to me after the races and was in despair at not finding you. She says you’re a real heroine of romance, and that if she were a man she would do all sorts of mad things for your sake. Stremov says she does that as it is.”\n\n“But do tell me, please, I never could make it out,” said Anna, after being silent for some time, speaking in a tone that showed she was not asking an idle question, but that what she was asking was of more importance to her than it should have been; “do tell me, please, what are her relations with Prince Kaluzhsky, Mishka, as he’s called? I’ve met them so little. What does it mean?”\n\nBetsy smiled with her eyes, and looked intently at Anna.\n\n“It’s a new manner,” she said. “They’ve all adopted that manner. They’ve flung their caps over the windmills. But there are ways and ways of flinging them.”\n\n“Yes, but what are her relations precisely with Kaluzhsky?”\n\nBetsy broke into unexpectedly mirthful and irrepressible laughter, a thing which rarely happened with her.\n\n“You’re encroaching on Princess Myakaya’s special domain now. That’s the question of an enfant terrible,” and Betsy obviously tried to restrain herself, but could not, and went off into peals of that infectious laughter that people laugh who do not laugh often. “You’d better ask them,” she brought out, between tears of laughter.\n\n“No; you laugh,” said Anna, laughing too in spite of herself, “but I never could understand it. I can’t understand the husband’s rôle in it.”\n\n“The husband? Liza Merkalova’s husband carries her shawl, and is always ready to be of use. But anything more than that in reality, no one cares to inquire. You know in decent society one doesn’t talk or think even of certain details of the toilet. That’s how it is with this.”\n\n“Will you be at Madame Rolandak’s fête?” asked Anna, to change the conversation.\n\n“I don’t think so,” answered Betsy, and, without looking at her friend, she began filling the little transparent cups with fragrant tea. Putting a cup before Anna, she took out a cigarette, and, fitting it into a silver holder, she lighted it.\n\n“It’s like this, you see: I’m in a fortunate position,” she began, quite serious now, as she took up her cup. “I understand you, and I understand Liza. Liza now is one of those naïve natures that, like children, don’t know what’s good and what’s bad. Anyway, she didn’t comprehend it when she was very young. And now she’s aware that the lack of comprehension suits her. Now, perhaps, she doesn’t know on purpose,” said Betsy, with a subtle smile. “But, anyway, it suits her. The very same thing, don’t you see, may be looked at tragically, and turned into a misery, or it may be looked at simply and even humorously. Possibly you are inclined to look at things too tragically.”\n\n“How I should like to know other people just as I know myself!” said Anna, seriously and dreamily. “Am I worse than other people, or better? I think I’m worse.”\n\n“Enfant terrible, enfant terrible!” repeated Betsy. “But here they are.”\n\n# Chapter 18\n\nThey heard the sound of steps and a man’s voice, then a woman’s voice and laughter, and immediately thereafter there walked in the expected guests: Sappho Shtoltz, and a young man beaming with excess of health, the so-called Vaska. It was evident that ample supplies of beefsteak, truffles, and Burgundy never failed to reach him at the fitting hour. Vaska bowed to the two ladies, and glanced at them, but only for one second. He walked after Sappho into the drawing-room, and followed her about as though he were chained to her, keeping his sparkling eyes fixed on her as though he wanted to eat her. Sappho Shtoltz was a blonde beauty with black eyes. She walked with smart little steps in high-heeled shoes, and shook hands with the ladies vigorously like a man.\n\nAnna had never met this new star of fashion, and was struck by her beauty, the exaggerated extreme to which her dress was carried, and the boldness of her manners. On her head there was such a superstructure of soft, golden hair—her own and false mixed—that her head was equal in size to the elegantly rounded bust, of which so much was exposed in front. The impulsive abruptness of her movements was such that at every step the lines of her knees and the upper part of her legs were distinctly marked under her dress, and the question involuntarily rose to the mind where in the undulating, piled-up mountain of material at the back the real body of the woman, so small and slender, so naked in front, and so hidden behind and below, really came to an end.\n\nBetsy made haste to introduce her to Anna.\n\n“Only fancy, we all but ran over two soldiers,” she began telling them at once, using her eyes, smiling and twitching away her tail, which she flung back at one stroke all on one side. “I drove here with Vaska.... Ah, to be sure, you don’t know each other.” And mentioning his surname she introduced the young man, and reddening a little, broke into a ringing laugh at her mistake—that is, at her having called him Vaska to a stranger. Vaska bowed once more to Anna, but he said nothing to her. He addressed Sappho: “You’ve lost your bet. We got here first. Pay up,” said he, smiling.\n\nSappho laughed still more festively.\n\n“Not just now,” said she.\n\n“Oh, all right, I’ll have it later.”\n\n“Very well, very well. Oh, yes.” She turned suddenly to Princess Betsy: “I am a nice person ... I positively forgot it ... I’ve brought you a visitor. And here he comes.” The unexpected young visitor, whom Sappho had invited, and whom she had forgotten, was, however, a personage of such consequence that, in spite of his youth, both the ladies rose on his entrance.\n\nHe was a new admirer of Sappho’s. He now dogged her footsteps, like Vaska.\n\nSoon after Prince Kaluzhsky arrived, and Liza Merkalova with Stremov. Liza Merkalova was a thin brunette, with an Oriental, languid type of face, and—as everyone used to say—exquisite enigmatic eyes. The tone of her dark dress (Anna immediately observed and appreciated the fact) was in perfect harmony with her style of beauty. Liza was as soft and enervated as Sappho was smart and abrupt.\n\nBut to Anna’s taste Liza was far more attractive. Betsy had said to Anna that she had adopted the pose of an innocent child, but when Anna saw her, she felt that this was not the truth. She really was both innocent and corrupt, but a sweet and passive woman. It is true that her tone was the same as Sappho’s; that like Sappho, she had two men, one young and one old, tacked onto her, and devouring her with their eyes. But there was something in her higher than what surrounded her. There was in her the glow of the real diamond among glass imitations. This glow shone out in her exquisite, truly enigmatic eyes. The weary, and at the same time passionate, glance of those eyes, encircled by dark rings, impressed one by its perfect sincerity. Everyone looking into those eyes fancied he knew her wholly, and knowing her, could not but love her. At the sight of Anna, her whole face lighted up at once with a smile of delight.\n\n“Ah, how glad I am to see you!” she said, going up to her. “Yesterday at the races all I wanted was to get to you, but you’d gone away. I did so want to see you, yesterday especially. Wasn’t it awful?” she said, looking at Anna with eyes that seemed to lay bare all her soul.\n\n“Yes; I had no idea it would be so thrilling,” said Anna, blushing.\n\nThe company got up at this moment to go into the garden.\n\n“I’m not going,” said Liza, smiling and settling herself close to Anna. “You won’t go either, will you? Who wants to play croquet?”\n\n“Oh, I like it,” said Anna.\n\n“There, how do you manage never to be bored by things? It’s delightful to look at you. You’re alive, but I’m bored.”\n\n“How can you be bored? Why, you live in the liveliest set in Petersburg,” said Anna.\n\n“Possibly the people who are not of our set are even more bored; but we—I certainly—are not happy, but awfully, awfully bored.”\n\nSappho smoking a cigarette went off into the garden with the two young men. Betsy and Stremov remained at the tea-table.\n\n“What, bored!” said Betsy. “Sappho says they did enjoy themselves tremendously at your house last night.”\n\n“Ah, how dreary it all was!” said Liza Merkalova. “We all drove back to my place after the races. And always the same people, always the same. Always the same thing. We lounged about on sofas all the evening. What is there to enjoy in that? No; do tell me how you manage never to be bored?” she said, addressing Anna again. “One has but to look at you and one sees, here’s a woman who may be happy or unhappy, but isn’t bored. Tell me how you do it?”\n\n“I do nothing,” answered Anna, blushing at these searching questions.\n\n“That’s the best way,” Stremov put in. Stremov was a man of fifty, partly gray, but still vigorous-looking, very ugly, but with a characteristic and intelligent face. Liza Merkalova was his wife’s niece, and he spent all his leisure hours with her. On meeting Anna Karenina, as he was Alexey Alexandrovitch’s enemy in the government, he tried, like a shrewd man and a man of the world, to be particularly cordial with her, the wife of his enemy.\n\n“‘Nothing,’” he put in with a subtle smile, “that’s the very best way. I told you long ago,” he said, turning to Liza Merkalova, “that if you don’t want to be bored, you mustn’t think you’re going to be bored. It’s just as you mustn’t be afraid of not being able to fall asleep, if you’re afraid of sleeplessness. That’s just what Anna Arkadyevna has just said.”\n\n“I should be very glad if I had said it, for it’s not only clever but true,” said Anna, smiling.\n\n“No, do tell me why it is one can’t go to sleep, and one can’t help being bored?”\n\n“To sleep well one ought to work, and to enjoy oneself one ought to work too.”\n\n“What am I to work for when my work is no use to anybody? And I can’t and won’t knowingly make a pretense about it.”\n\n“You’re incorrigible,” said Stremov, not looking at her, and he spoke again to Anna. As he rarely met Anna, he could say nothing but commonplaces to her, but he said those commonplaces as to when she was returning to Petersburg, and how fond Countess Lidia Ivanovna was of her, with an expression which suggested that he longed with his whole soul to please her and show his regard for her and even more than that.\n\nTushkevitch came in, announcing that the party were awaiting the other players to begin croquet.\n\n“No, don’t go away, please don’t,” pleaded Liza Merkalova, hearing that Anna was going. Stremov joined in her entreaties.\n\n“It’s too violent a transition,” he said, “to go from such company to old Madame Vrede. And besides, you will only give her a chance for talking scandal, while here you arouse none but such different feelings of the highest and most opposite kind,” he said to her.\n\nAnna pondered for an instant in uncertainty. This shrewd man’s flattering words, the naïve, childlike affection shown her by Liza Merkalova, and all the social atmosphere she was used to,—it was all so easy, and what was in store for her was so difficult, that she was for a minute in uncertainty whether to remain, whether to put off a little longer the painful moment of explanation. But remembering what was in store for her alone at home, if she did not come to some decision, remembering that gesture—terrible even in memory—when she had clutched her hair in both hands—she said good-bye and went away.\n\n# Chapter 19\n\nIn spite of Vronsky’s apparently frivolous life in society, he was a man who hated irregularity. In early youth in the Corps of Pages, he had experienced the humiliation of a refusal, when he had tried, being in difficulties, to borrow money, and since then he had never once put himself in the same position again.\n\nIn order to keep his affairs in some sort of order, he used about five times a year (more or less frequently, according to circumstances) to shut himself up alone and put all his affairs into definite shape. This he used to call his day of reckoning or faire la lessive.\n\nOn waking up the day after the races, Vronsky put on a white linen coat, and without shaving or taking his bath, he distributed about the table moneys, bills, and letters, and set to work. Petritsky, who knew he was ill-tempered on such occasions, on waking up and seeing his comrade at the writing-table, quietly dressed and went out without getting in his way.\n\nEvery man who knows to the minutest details all the complexity of the conditions surrounding him, cannot help imagining that the complexity of these conditions, and the difficulty of making them clear, is something exceptional and personal, peculiar to himself, and never supposes that others are surrounded by just as complicated an array of personal affairs as he is. So indeed it seemed to Vronsky. And not without inward pride, and not without reason, he thought that any other man would long ago have been in difficulties, would have been forced to some dishonorable course, if he had found himself in such a difficult position. But Vronsky felt that now especially it was essential for him to clear up and define his position if he were to avoid getting into difficulties.\n\nWhat Vronsky attacked first as being the easiest was his pecuniary position. Writing out on note paper in his minute hand all that he owed, he added up the amount and found that his debts amounted to seventeen thousand and some odd hundreds, which he left out for the sake of clearness. Reckoning up his money and his bank book, he found that he had left one thousand eight hundred roubles, and nothing coming in before the New Year. Reckoning over again his list of debts, Vronsky copied it, dividing it into three classes. In the first class he put the debts which he would have to pay at once, or for which he must in any case have the money ready so that on demand for payment there could not be a moment’s delay in paying. Such debts amounted to about four thousand: one thousand five hundred for a horse, and two thousand five hundred as surety for a young comrade, Venovsky, who had lost that sum to a cardsharper in Vronsky’s presence. Vronsky had wanted to pay the money at the time (he had that amount then), but Venovsky and Yashvin had insisted that they would pay and not Vronsky, who had not played. That was so far well, but Vronsky knew that in this dirty business, though his only share in it was undertaking by word of mouth to be surety for Venovsky, it was absolutely necessary for him to have the two thousand five hundred roubles so as to be able to fling it at the swindler, and have no more words with him. And so for this first and most important division he must have four thousand roubles. The second class—eight thousand roubles—consisted of less important debts. These were principally accounts owing in connection with his race horses, to the purveyor of oats and hay, the English saddler, and so on. He would have to pay some two thousand roubles on these debts too, in order to be quite free from anxiety. The last class of debts—to shops, to hotels, to his tailor—were such as need not be considered. So that he needed at least six thousand roubles for current expenses, and he only had one thousand eight hundred. For a man with one hundred thousand roubles of revenue, which was what everyone fixed as Vronsky’s income, such debts, one would suppose, could hardly be embarrassing; but the fact was that he was far from having one hundred thousand. His father’s immense property, which alone yielded a yearly income of two hundred thousand, was left undivided between the brothers. At the time when the elder brother, with a mass of debts, married Princess Varya Tchirkova, the daughter of a Decembrist without any fortune whatever, Alexey had given up to his elder brother almost the whole income from his father’s estate, reserving for himself only twenty-five thousand a year from it. Alexey had said at the time to his brother that that sum would be sufficient for him until he married, which he probably never would do. And his brother, who was in command of one of the most expensive regiments, and was only just married, could not decline the gift. His mother, who had her own separate property, had allowed Alexey every year twenty thousand in addition to the twenty-five thousand he had reserved, and Alexey had spent it all. Of late his mother, incensed with him on account of his love affair and his leaving Moscow, had given up sending him the money. And in consequence of this, Vronsky, who had been in the habit of living on the scale of forty-five thousand a year, having only received twenty thousand that year, found himself now in difficulties. To get out of these difficulties, he could not apply to his mother for money. Her last letter, which he had received the day before, had particularly exasperated him by the hints in it that she was quite ready to help him to succeed in the world and in the army, but not to lead a life which was a scandal to all good society. His mother’s attempt to buy him stung him to the quick and made him feel colder than ever to her. But he could not draw back from the generous word when it was once uttered, even though he felt now, vaguely foreseeing certain eventualities in his intrigue with Madame Karenina, that this generous word had been spoken thoughtlessly, and that even though he were not married he might need all the hundred thousand of income. But it was impossible to draw back. He had only to recall his brother’s wife, to remember how that sweet, delightful Varya sought, at every convenient opportunity, to remind him that she remembered his generosity and appreciated it, to grasp the impossibility of taking back his gift. It was as impossible as beating a woman, stealing, or lying. One thing only could and ought to be done, and Vronsky determined upon it without an instant’s hesitation: to borrow money from a money-lender, ten thousand roubles, a proceeding which presented no difficulty, to cut down his expenses generally, and to sell his race horses. Resolving on this, he promptly wrote a note to Rolandak, who had more than once sent to him with offers to buy horses from him. Then he sent for the Englishman and the money-lender, and divided what money he had according to the accounts he intended to pay. Having finished this business, he wrote a cold and cutting answer to his mother. Then he took out of his notebook three notes of Anna’s, read them again, burned them, and remembering their conversation on the previous day, he sank into meditation.\n\n# Chapter 20\n\nVronsky’s life was particularly happy in that he had a code of principles, which defined with unfailing certitude what he ought and what he ought not to do. This code of principles covered only a very small circle of contingencies, but then the principles were never doubtful, and Vronsky, as he never went outside that circle, had never had a moment’s hesitation about doing what he ought to do. These principles laid down as invariable rules: that one must pay a cardsharper, but need not pay a tailor; that one must never tell a lie to a man, but one may to a woman; that one must never cheat anyone, but one may a husband; that one must never pardon an insult, but one may give one and so on. These principles were possibly not reasonable and not good, but they were of unfailing certainty, and so long as he adhered to them, Vronsky felt that his heart was at peace and he could hold his head up. Only quite lately in regard to his relations with Anna, Vronsky had begun to feel that his code of principles did not fully cover all possible contingencies, and to foresee in the future difficulties and perplexities for which he could find no guiding clue.\n\nHis present relation to Anna and to her husband was to his mind clear and simple. It was clearly and precisely defined in the code of principles by which he was guided.\n\nShe was an honorable woman who had bestowed her love upon him, and he loved her, and therefore she was in his eyes a woman who had a right to the same, or even more, respect than a lawful wife. He would have had his hand chopped off before he would have allowed himself by a word, by a hint, to humiliate her, or even to fall short of the fullest respect a woman could look for.\n\nHis attitude to society, too, was clear. Everyone might know, might suspect it, but no one might dare to speak of it. If any did so, he was ready to force all who might speak to be silent and to respect the non-existent honor of the woman he loved.\n\nHis attitude to the husband was the clearest of all. From the moment that Anna loved Vronsky, he had regarded his own right over her as the one thing unassailable. Her husband was simply a superfluous and tiresome person. No doubt he was in a pitiable position, but how could that be helped? The one thing the husband had a right to was to demand satisfaction with a weapon in his hand, and Vronsky was prepared for this at any minute.\n\nBut of late new inner relations had arisen between him and her, which frightened Vronsky by their indefiniteness. Only the day before she had told him that she was with child. And he felt that this fact and what she expected of him called for something not fully defined in that code of principles by which he had hitherto steered his course in life. And he had been indeed caught unawares, and at the first moment when she spoke to him of her position, his heart had prompted him to beg her to leave her husband. He had said that, but now thinking things over he saw clearly that it would be better to manage to avoid that; and at the same time, as he told himself so, he was afraid whether it was not wrong.\n\n“If I told her to leave her husband, that must mean uniting her life with mine; am I prepared for that? How can I take her away now, when I have no money? Supposing I could arrange.... But how can I take her away while I’m in the service? If I say that—I ought to be prepared to do it, that is, I ought to have the money and to retire from the army.”\n\nAnd he grew thoughtful. The question whether to retire from the service or not brought him to the other and perhaps the chief though hidden interest of his life, of which none knew but he.\n\nAmbition was the old dream of his youth and childhood, a dream which he did not confess even to himself, though it was so strong that now this passion was even doing battle with his love. His first steps in the world and in the service had been successful, but two years before he had made a great mistake. Anxious to show his independence and to advance, he had refused a post that had been offered him, hoping that this refusal would heighten his value; but it turned out that he had been too bold, and he was passed over. And having, whether he liked or not, taken up for himself the position of an independent man, he carried it off with great tact and good sense, behaving as though he bore no grudge against anyone, did not regard himself as injured in any way, and cared for nothing but to be left alone since he was enjoying himself. In reality he had ceased to enjoy himself as long ago as the year before, when he went away to Moscow. He felt that this independent attitude of a man who might have done anything, but cared to do nothing, was already beginning to pall, that many people were beginning to fancy that he was not really capable of anything but being a straightforward, good-natured fellow. His connection with Madame Karenina, by creating so much sensation and attracting general attention, had given him a fresh distinction which soothed his gnawing worm of ambition for a while, but a week before that worm had been roused up again with fresh force. The friend of his childhood, a man of the same set, of the same coterie, his comrade in the Corps of Pages, Serpuhovskoy, who had left school with him and had been his rival in class, in gymnastics, in their scrapes and their dreams of glory, had come back a few days before from Central Asia, where he had gained two steps up in rank, and an order rarely bestowed upon generals so young.\n\nAs soon as he arrived in Petersburg, people began to talk about him as a newly risen star of the first magnitude. A schoolfellow of Vronsky’s and of the same age, he was a general and was expecting a command, which might have influence on the course of political events; while Vronsky, independent and brilliant and beloved by a charming woman though he was, was simply a cavalry captain who was readily allowed to be as independent as ever he liked. “Of course I don’t envy Serpuhovskoy and never could envy him; but his advancement shows me that one has only to watch one’s opportunity, and the career of a man like me may be very rapidly made. Three years ago he was in just the same position as I am. If I retire, I burn my ships. If I remain in the army, I lose nothing. She said herself she did not wish to change her position. And with her love I cannot feel envious of Serpuhovskoy.” And slowly twirling his mustaches, he got up from the table and walked about the room. His eyes shone particularly brightly, and he felt in that confident, calm, and happy frame of mind which always came after he had thoroughly faced his position. Everything was straight and clear, just as after former days of reckoning. He shaved, took a cold bath, dressed and went out.\n\n# Chapter 21\n\n“We’ve come to fetch you. Your lessive lasted a good time today,” said Petritsky. “Well, is it over?”\n\n“It is over,” answered Vronsky, smiling with his eyes only, and twirling the tips of his mustaches as circumspectly as though after the perfect order into which his affairs had been brought any over-bold or rapid movement might disturb it.\n\n“You’re always just as if you’d come out of a bath after it,” said Petritsky. “I’ve come from Gritsky’s” (that was what they called the colonel); “they’re expecting you.”\n\nVronsky, without answering, looked at his comrade, thinking of something else.\n\n“Yes; is that music at his place?” he said, listening to the familiar sounds of polkas and waltzes floating across to him. “What’s the fête?”\n\n“Serpuhovskoy’s come.”\n\n“Aha!” said Vronsky, “why, I didn’t know.”\n\nThe smile in his eyes gleamed more brightly than ever.\n\nHaving once made up his mind that he was happy in his love, that he sacrificed his ambition to it—having anyway taken up this position, Vronsky was incapable of feeling either envious of Serpuhovskoy or hurt with him for not coming first to him when he came to the regiment. Serpuhovskoy was a good friend, and he was delighted he had come.\n\n“Ah, I’m very glad!”\n\nThe colonel, Demin, had taken a large country house. The whole party were in the wide lower balcony. In the courtyard the first objects that met Vronsky’s eyes were a band of singers in white linen coats, standing near a barrel of vodka, and the robust, good-humored figure of the colonel surrounded by officers. He had gone out as far as the first step of the balcony and was loudly shouting across the band that played Offenbach’s quadrille, waving his arms and giving some orders to a few soldiers standing on one side. A group of soldiers, a quartermaster, and several subalterns came up to the balcony with Vronsky. The colonel returned to the table, went out again onto the steps with a tumbler in his hand, and proposed the toast, “To the health of our former comrade, the gallant general, Prince Serpuhovskoy. Hurrah!”\n\nThe colonel was followed by Serpuhovskoy, who came out onto the steps smiling, with a glass in his hand.\n\n“You always get younger, Bondarenko,” he said to the rosy-cheeked, smart-looking quartermaster standing just before him, still youngish looking though doing his second term of service.\n\nIt was three years since Vronsky had seen Serpuhovskoy. He looked more robust, had let his whiskers grow, but was still the same graceful creature, whose face and figure were even more striking from their softness and nobility than their beauty. The only change Vronsky detected in him was that subdued, continual radiance of beaming content which settles on the faces of men who are successful and are sure of the recognition of their success by everyone. Vronsky knew that radiant air, and immediately observed it in Serpuhovskoy.\n\nAs Serpuhovskoy came down the steps he saw Vronsky. A smile of pleasure lighted up his face. He tossed his head upwards and waved the glass in his hand, greeting Vronsky, and showing him by the gesture that he could not come to him before the quartermaster, who stood craning forward his lips ready to be kissed.\n\n“Here he is!” shouted the colonel. “Yashvin told me you were in one of your gloomy tempers.”\n\nSerpuhovskoy kissed the moist, fresh lips of the gallant-looking quartermaster, and wiping his mouth with his handkerchief, went up to Vronsky.\n\n“How glad I am!” he said, squeezing his hand and drawing him on one side.\n\n“You look after him,” the colonel shouted to Yashvin, pointing to Vronsky; and he went down below to the soldiers.\n\n“Why weren’t you at the races yesterday? I expected to see you there,” said Vronsky, scrutinizing Serpuhovskoy.\n\n“I did go, but late. I beg your pardon,” he added, and he turned to the adjutant: “Please have this divided from me, each man as much as it runs to.” And he hurriedly took notes for three hundred roubles from his pocketbook, blushing a little.\n\n“Vronsky! Have anything to eat or drink?” asked Yashvin. “Hi, something for the count to eat! Ah, here it is: have a glass!”\n\nThe fête at the colonel’s lasted a long while. There was a great deal of drinking. They tossed Serpuhovskoy in the air and caught him again several times. Then they did the same to the colonel. Then, to the accompaniment of the band, the colonel himself danced with Petritsky. Then the colonel, who began to show signs of feebleness, sat down on a bench in the courtyard and began demonstrating to Yashvin the superiority of Russia over Prussia, especially in cavalry attack, and there was a lull in the revelry for a moment. Serpuhovskoy went into the house to the bathroom to wash his hands and found Vronsky there; Vronsky was drenching his head with water. He had taken off his coat and put his sunburnt, hairy neck under the tap, and was rubbing it and his head with his hands. When he had finished, Vronsky sat down by Serpuhovskoy. They both sat down in the bathroom on a lounge, and a conversation began which was very interesting to both of them.\n\n“I’ve always been hearing about you through my wife,” said Serpuhovskoy. “I’m glad you’ve been seeing her pretty often.”\n\n“She’s friendly with Varya, and they’re the only women in Petersburg I care about seeing,” answered Vronsky, smiling. He smiled because he foresaw the topic the conversation would turn on, and he was glad of it.\n\n“The only ones?” Serpuhovskoy queried, smiling.\n\n“Yes; and I heard news of you, but not only through your wife,” said Vronsky, checking his hint by a stern expression of face. “I was greatly delighted to hear of your success, but not a bit surprised. I expected even more.”\n\nSerpuhovskoy smiled. Such an opinion of him was obviously agreeable to him, and he did not think it necessary to conceal it.\n\n“Well, I on the contrary expected less—I’ll own frankly. But I’m glad, very glad. I’m ambitious; that’s my weakness, and I confess to it.”\n\n“Perhaps you wouldn’t confess to it if you hadn’t been successful,” said Vronsky.\n\n“I don’t suppose so,” said Serpuhovskoy, smiling again. “I won’t say life wouldn’t be worth living without it, but it would be dull. Of course I may be mistaken, but I fancy I have a certain capacity for the line I’ve chosen, and that power of any sort in my hands, if it is to be, will be better than in the hands of a good many people I know,” said Serpuhovskoy, with beaming consciousness of success; “and so the nearer I get to it, the better pleased I am.”\n\n“Perhaps that is true for you, but not for everyone. I used to think so too, but here I live and think life worth living not only for that.”\n\n“There it’s out! here it comes!” said Serpuhovskoy, laughing. “Ever since I heard about you, about your refusal, I began.... Of course, I approved of what you did. But there are ways of doing everything. And I think your action was good in itself, but you didn’t do it quite in the way you ought to have done.”\n\n“What’s done can’t be undone, and you know I never go back on what I’ve done. And besides, I’m very well off.”\n\n“Very well off—for the time. But you’re not satisfied with that. I wouldn’t say this to your brother. He’s a nice child, like our host here. There he goes!” he added, listening to the roar of “hurrah!”—“and he’s happy, but that does not satisfy you.”\n\n“I didn’t say it did satisfy me.”\n\n“Yes, but that’s not the only thing. Such men as you are wanted.”\n\n“By whom?”\n\n“By whom? By society, by Russia. Russia needs men; she needs a party, or else everything goes and will go to the dogs.”\n\n“How do you mean? Bertenev’s party against the Russian communists?”\n\n“No,” said Serpuhovskoy, frowning with vexation at being suspected of such an absurdity. “Tout ça est une blague. That’s always been and always will be. There are no communists. But intriguing people have to invent a noxious, dangerous party. It’s an old trick. No, what’s wanted is a powerful party of independent men like you and me.”\n\n“But why so?” Vronsky mentioned a few men who were in power. “Why aren’t they independent men?”\n\n“Simply because they have not, or have not had from birth, an independent fortune; they’ve not had a name, they’ve not been close to the sun and center as we have. They can be bought either by money or by favor. And they have to find a support for themselves in inventing a policy. And they bring forward some notion, some policy that they don’t believe in, that does harm; and the whole policy is really only a means to a government house and so much income. Cela n’est pas plus fin que ça, when you get a peep at their cards. I may be inferior to them, stupider perhaps, though I don’t see why I should be inferior to them. But you and I have one important advantage over them for certain, in being more difficult to buy. And such men are more needed than ever.”\n\nVronsky listened attentively, but he was not so much interested by the meaning of the words as by the attitude of Serpuhovskoy who was already contemplating a struggle with the existing powers, and already had his likes and dislikes in that higher world, while his own interest in the governing world did not go beyond the interests of his regiment. Vronsky felt, too, how powerful Serpuhovskoy might become through his unmistakable faculty for thinking things out and for taking things in, through his intelligence and gift of words, so rarely met with in the world in which he moved. And, ashamed as he was of the feeling, he felt envious.\n\n“Still I haven’t the one thing of most importance for that,” he answered; “I haven’t the desire for power. I had it once, but it’s gone.”\n\n“Excuse me, that’s not true,” said Serpuhovskoy, smiling.\n\n“Yes, it is true, it is true ... now!” Vronsky added, to be truthful.\n\n“Yes, it’s true now, that’s another thing; but that now won’t last forever.”\n\n“Perhaps,” answered Vronsky.\n\n“You say perhaps,” Serpuhovskoy went on, as though guessing his thoughts, “but I say for certain. And that’s what I wanted to see you for. Your action was just what it should have been. I see that, but you ought not to keep it up. I only ask you to give me carte blanche. I’m not going to offer you my protection ... though, indeed, why shouldn’t I protect you?—you’ve protected me often enough! I should hope our friendship rises above all that sort of thing. Yes,” he said, smiling to him as tenderly as a woman, “give me carte blanche, retire from the regiment, and I’ll draw you upwards imperceptibly.”\n\n“But you must understand that I want nothing,” said Vronsky, “except that all should be as it is.”\n\nSerpuhovskoy got up and stood facing him.\n\n“You say that all should be as it is. I understand what that means. But listen: we’re the same age, you’ve known a greater number of women perhaps than I have.” Serpohovskoy’s smile and gestures told Vronsky that he mustn’t be afraid, that he would be tender and careful in touching the sore place. “But I’m married, and believe me, in getting to know thoroughly one’s wife, if one loves her, as someone has said, one gets to know all women better than if one knew thousands of them.”\n\n“We’re coming directly!” Vronsky shouted to an officer, who looked into the room and called them to the colonel.\n\nVronsky was longing now to hear to the end and know what Serpuhovskey would say to him.\n\n“And here’s my opinion for you. Women are the chief stumbling block in a man’s career. It’s hard to love a woman and do anything. There’s only one way of having love conveniently without its being a hindrance—that’s marriage. How, how am I to tell you what I mean?” said Serpuhovskoy, who liked similes. “Wait a minute, wait a minute! Yes, just as you can only carry a fardeau and do something with your hands, when the fardeau is tied on your back, and that’s marriage. And that’s what I felt when I was married. My hands were suddenly set free. But to drag that fardeau about with you without marriage, your hands will always be so full that you can do nothing. Look at Mazankov, at Krupov. They’ve ruined their careers for the sake of women.”\n\n“What women!” said Vronsky, recalling the Frenchwoman and the actress with whom the two men he had mentioned were connected.\n\n“The firmer the woman’s footing in society, the worse it is. That’s much the same as—not merely carrying the fardeau in your arms—but tearing it away from someone else.”\n\n“You have never loved,” Vronsky said softly, looking straight before him and thinking of Anna.\n\n“Perhaps. But you remember what I’ve said to you. And another thing, women are all more materialistic than men. We make something immense out of love, but they are always terre-à-terre.”\n\n“Directly, directly!” he cried to a footman who came in. But the footman had not come to call them again, as he supposed. The footman brought Vronsky a note.\n\n“A man brought it from Princess Tverskaya.”\n\nVronsky opened the letter, and flushed crimson.\n\n“My head’s begun to ache; I’m going home,” he said to Serpuhovskoy.\n\n“Oh, good-bye then. You give me carte blanche!”\n\n“We’ll talk about it later on; I’ll look you up in Petersburg.”\n\n# Chapter 22\n\nIt was six o’clock already, and so, in order to be there quickly, and at the same time not to drive with his own horses, known to everyone, Vronsky got into Yashvin’s hired fly, and told the driver to drive as quickly as possible. It was a roomy, old-fashioned fly, with seats for four. He sat in one corner, stretched his legs out on the front seat, and sank into meditation.\n\nA vague sense of the order into which his affairs had been brought, a vague recollection of the friendliness and flattery of Serpuhovskoy, who had considered him a man that was needed, and most of all, the anticipation of the interview before him—all blended into a general, joyous sense of life. This feeling was so strong that he could not help smiling. He dropped his legs, crossed one leg over the other knee, and taking it in his hand, felt the springy muscle of the calf, where it had been grazed the day before by his fall, and leaning back he drew several deep breaths.\n\n“I’m happy, very happy!” he said to himself. He had often before had this sense of physical joy in his own body, but he had never felt so fond of himself, of his own body, as at that moment. He enjoyed the slight ache in his strong leg, he enjoyed the muscular sensation of movement in his chest as he breathed. The bright, cold August day, which had made Anna feel so hopeless, seemed to him keenly stimulating, and refreshed his face and neck that still tingled from the cold water. The scent of brilliantine on his whiskers struck him as particularly pleasant in the fresh air. Everything he saw from the carriage window, everything in that cold pure air, in the pale light of the sunset, was as fresh, and gay, and strong as he was himself: the roofs of the houses shining in the rays of the setting sun, the sharp outlines of fences and angles of buildings, the figures of passers-by, the carriages that met him now and then, the motionless green of the trees and grass, the fields with evenly drawn furrows of potatoes, and the slanting shadows that fell from the houses, and trees, and bushes, and even from the rows of potatoes—everything was bright like a pretty landscape just finished and freshly varnished.\n\n“Get on, get on!” he said to the driver, putting his head out of the window, and pulling a three-rouble note out of his pocket he handed it to the man as he looked round. The driver’s hand fumbled with something at the lamp, the whip cracked, and the carriage rolled rapidly along the smooth highroad.\n\n“I want nothing, nothing but this happiness,” he thought, staring at the bone button of the bell in the space between the windows, and picturing to himself Anna just as he had seen her last time. “And as I go on, I love her more and more. Here’s the garden of the Vrede Villa. Whereabouts will she be? Where? How? Why did she fix on this place to meet me, and why does she write in Betsy’s letter?” he thought, wondering now for the first time at it. But there was now no time for wonder. He called to the driver to stop before reaching the avenue, and opening the door, jumped out of the carriage as it was moving, and went into the avenue that led up to the house. There was no one in the avenue; but looking round to the right he caught sight of her. Her face was hidden by a veil, but he drank in with glad eyes the special movement in walking, peculiar to her alone, the slope of the shoulders, and the setting of the head, and at once a sort of electric shock ran all over him. With fresh force, he felt conscious of himself from the springy motions of his legs to the movements of his lungs as he breathed, and something set his lips twitching.\n\nJoining him, she pressed his hand tightly.\n\n“You’re not angry that I sent for you? I absolutely had to see you,” she said; and the serious and set line of her lips, which he saw under the veil, transformed his mood at once.\n\n“I angry! But how have you come, where from?”\n\n“Never mind,” she said, laying her hand on his, “come along, I must talk to you.”\n\nHe saw that something had happened, and that the interview would not be a joyous one. In her presence he had no will of his own: without knowing the grounds of her distress, he already felt the same distress unconsciously passing over him.\n\n“What is it? what?” he asked her, squeezing her hand with his elbow, and trying to read her thoughts in her face.\n\nShe walked on a few steps in silence, gathering up her courage; then suddenly she stopped.\n\n“I did not tell you yesterday,” she began, breathing quickly and painfully, “that coming home with Alexey Alexandrovitch I told him everything ... told him I could not be his wife, that ... and told him everything.”\n\nHe heard her, unconsciously bending his whole figure down to her as though hoping in this way to soften the hardness of her position for her. But directly she had said this he suddenly drew himself up, and a proud and hard expression came over his face.\n\n“Yes, yes, that’s better, a thousand times better! I know how painful it was,” he said. But she was not listening to his words, she was reading his thoughts from the expression of his face. She could not guess that that expression arose from the first idea that presented itself to Vronsky—that a duel was now inevitable. The idea of a duel had never crossed her mind, and so she put a different interpretation on this passing expression of hardness.\n\nWhen she got her husband’s letter, she knew then at the bottom of her heart that everything would go on in the old way, that she would not have the strength of will to forego her position, to abandon her son, and to join her lover. The morning spent at Princess Tverskaya’s had confirmed her still more in this. But this interview was still of the utmost gravity for her. She hoped that this interview would transform her position, and save her. If on hearing this news he were to say to her resolutely, passionately, without an instant’s wavering: “Throw up everything and come with me!” she would give up her son and go away with him. But this news had not produced what she had expected in him; he simply seemed as though he were resenting some affront.\n\n“It was not in the least painful to me. It happened of itself,” she said irritably; “and see....” she pulled her husband’s letter out of her glove.\n\n“I understand, I understand,” he interrupted her, taking the letter, but not reading it, and trying to soothe her. “The one thing I longed for, the one thing I prayed for, was to cut short this position, so as to devote my life to your happiness.”\n\n“Why do you tell me that?” she said. “Do you suppose I can doubt it? If I doubted....”\n\n“Who’s that coming?” said Vronsky suddenly, pointing to two ladies walking towards them. “Perhaps they know us!” and he hurriedly turned off, drawing her after him into a side path.\n\n“Oh, I don’t care!” she said. Her lips were quivering. And he fancied that her eyes looked with strange fury at him from under the veil. “I tell you that’s not the point—I can’t doubt that; but see what he writes to me. Read it.” She stood still again.\n\nAgain, just as at the first moment of hearing of her rupture with her husband, Vronsky, on reading the letter, was unconsciously carried away by the natural sensation aroused in him by his own relation to the betrayed husband. Now while he held his letter in his hands, he could not help picturing the challenge, which he would most likely find at home today or tomorrow, and the duel itself, in which, with the same cold and haughty expression that his face was assuming at this moment he would await the injured husband’s shot, after having himself fired into the air. And at that instant there flashed across his mind the thought of what Serpuhovskoy had just said to him, and what he had himself been thinking in the morning—that it was better not to bind himself—and he knew that this thought he could not tell her.\n\nHaving read the letter, he raised his eyes to her, and there was no determination in them. She saw at once that he had been thinking about it before by himself. She knew that whatever he might say to her, he would not say all he thought. And she knew that her last hope had failed her. This was not what she had been reckoning on.\n\n“You see the sort of man he is,” she said, with a shaking voice; “he....”\n\n“Forgive me, but I rejoice at it,” Vronsky interrupted. “For God’s sake, let me finish!” he added, his eyes imploring her to give him time to explain his words. “I rejoice, because things cannot, cannot possibly remain as he supposes.”\n\n“Why can’t they?” Anna said, restraining her tears, and obviously attaching no sort of consequence to what he said. She felt that her fate was sealed.\n\nVronsky meant that after the duel—inevitable, he thought—things could not go on as before, but he said something different.\n\n“It can’t go on. I hope that now you will leave him. I hope”—he was confused, and reddened—“that you will let me arrange and plan our life. Tomorrow....” he was beginning.\n\nShe did not let him go on.\n\n“But my child!” she shrieked. “You see what he writes! I should have to leave him, and I can’t and won’t do that.”\n\n“But, for God’s sake, which is better?—leave your child, or keep up this degrading position?”\n\n“To whom is it degrading?”\n\n“To all, and most of all to you.”\n\n“You say degrading ... don’t say that. Those words have no meaning for me,” she said in a shaking voice. She did not want him now to say what was untrue. She had nothing left her but his love, and she wanted to love him. “Don’t you understand that from the day I loved you everything has changed for me? For me there is one thing, and one thing only—your love. If that’s mine, I feel so exalted, so strong, that nothing can be humiliating to me. I am proud of my position, because ... proud of being ... proud....” She could not say what she was proud of. Tears of shame and despair choked her utterance. She stood still and sobbed.\n\nHe felt, too, something swelling in his throat and twitching in his nose, and for the first time in his life he felt on the point of weeping. He could not have said exactly what it was touched him so. He felt sorry for her, and he felt he could not help her, and with that he knew that he was to blame for her wretchedness, and that he had done something wrong.\n\n“Is not a divorce possible?” he said feebly. She shook her head, not answering. “Couldn’t you take your son, and still leave him?”\n\n“Yes; but it all depends on him. Now I must go to him,” she said shortly. Her presentiment that all would again go on in the old way had not deceived her.\n\n“On Tuesday I shall be in Petersburg, and everything can be settled.”\n\n“Yes,” she said. “But don’t let us talk any more of it.”\n\nAnna’s carriage, which she had sent away, and ordered to come back to the little gate of the Vrede garden, drove up. Anna said good-bye to Vronsky, and drove home.\n\n# Chapter 23\n\nOn Monday there was the usual sitting of the Commission of the 2nd of June. Alexey Alexandrovitch walked into the hall where the sitting was held, greeted the members and the president, as usual, and sat down in his place, putting his hand on the papers laid ready before him. Among these papers lay the necessary evidence and a rough outline of the speech he intended to make. But he did not really need these documents. He remembered every point, and did not think it necessary to go over in his memory what he would say. He knew that when the time came, and when he saw his enemy facing him, and studiously endeavoring to assume an expression of indifference, his speech would flow of itself better than he could prepare it now. He felt that the import of his speech was of such magnitude that every word of it would have weight. Meantime, as he listened to the usual report, he had the most innocent and inoffensive air. No one, looking at his white hands, with their swollen veins and long fingers, so softly stroking the edges of the white paper that lay before him, and at the air of weariness with which his head drooped on one side, would have suspected that in a few minutes a torrent of words would flow from his lips that would arouse a fearful storm, set the members shouting and attacking one another, and force the president to call for order. When the report was over, Alexey Alexandrovitch announced in his subdued, delicate voice that he had several points to bring before the meeting in regard to the Commission for the Reorganization of the Native Tribes. All attention was turned upon him. Alexey Alexandrovitch cleared his throat, and not looking at his opponent, but selecting, as he always did while he was delivering his speeches, the first person sitting opposite him, an inoffensive little old man, who never had an opinion of any sort in the Commission, began to expound his views. When he reached the point about the fundamental and radical law, his opponent jumped up and began to protest. Stremov, who was also a member of the Commission, and also stung to the quick, began defending himself, and altogether a stormy sitting followed; but Alexey Alexandrovitch triumphed, and his motion was carried, three new commissions were appointed, and the next day in a certain Petersburg circle nothing else was talked of but this sitting. Alexey Alexandrovitch’s success had been even greater than he had anticipated.\n\nNext morning, Tuesday, Alexey Alexandrovitch, on waking up, recollected with pleasure his triumph of the previous day, and he could not help smiling, though he tried to appear indifferent, when the chief secretary of his department, anxious to flatter him, informed him of the rumors that had reached him concerning what had happened in the Commission.\n\nAbsorbed in business with the chief secretary, Alexey Alexandrovitch had completely forgotten that it was Tuesday, the day fixed by him for the return of Anna Arkadyevna, and he was surprised and received a shock of annoyance when a servant came in to inform him of her arrival.\n\nAnna had arrived in Petersburg early in the morning; the carriage had been sent to meet her in accordance with her telegram, and so Alexey Alexandrovitch might have known of her arrival. But when she arrived, he did not meet her. She was told that he had not yet gone out, but was busy with his secretary. She sent word to her husband that she had come, went to her own room, and occupied herself in sorting out her things, expecting he would come to her. But an hour passed; he did not come. She went into the dining-room on the pretext of giving some directions, and spoke loudly on purpose, expecting him to come out there; but he did not come, though she heard him go to the door of his study as he parted from the chief secretary. She knew that he usually went out quickly to his office, and she wanted to see him before that, so that their attitude to one another might be defined.\n\nShe walked across the drawing-room and went resolutely to him. When she went into his study he was in official uniform, obviously ready to go out, sitting at a little table on which he rested his elbows, looking dejectedly before him. She saw him before he saw her, and she saw that he was thinking of her.\n\nOn seeing her, he would have risen, but changed his mind, then his face flushed hotly—a thing Anna had never seen before, and he got up quickly and went to meet her, looking not at her eyes, but above them at her forehead and hair. He went up to her, took her by the hand, and asked her to sit down.\n\n“I am very glad you have come,” he said, sitting down beside her, and obviously wishing to say something, he stuttered. Several times he tried to begin to speak, but stopped. In spite of the fact that, preparing herself for meeting him, she had schooled herself to despise and reproach him, she did not know what to say to him, and she felt sorry for him. And so the silence lasted for some time. “Is Seryozha quite well?” he said, and not waiting for an answer, he added: “I shan’t be dining at home today, and I have got to go out directly.”\n\n“I had thought of going to Moscow,” she said.\n\n“No, you did quite, quite right to come,” he said, and was silent again.\n\nSeeing that he was powerless to begin the conversation, she began herself.\n\n“Alexey Alexandrovitch,” she said, looking at him and not dropping her eyes under his persistent gaze at her hair, “I’m a guilty woman, I’m a bad woman, but I am the same as I was, as I told you then, and I have come to tell you that I can change nothing.”\n\n“I have asked you no question about that,” he said, all at once, resolutely and with hatred looking her straight in the face; “that was as I had supposed.” Under the influence of anger he apparently regained complete possession of all his faculties. “But as I told you then, and have written to you,” he said in a thin, shrill voice, “I repeat now, that I am not bound to know this. I ignore it. Not all wives are so kind as you, to be in such a hurry to communicate such agreeable news to their husbands.” He laid special emphasis on the word “agreeable.” “I shall ignore it so long as the world knows nothing of it, so long as my name is not disgraced. And so I simply inform you that our relations must be just as they have always been, and that only in the event of your compromising me I shall be obliged to take steps to secure my honor.”\n\n“But our relations cannot be the same as always,” Anna began in a timid voice, looking at him with dismay.\n\nWhen she saw once more those composed gestures, heard that shrill, childish, and sarcastic voice, her aversion for him extinguished her pity for him, and she felt only afraid, but at all costs she wanted to make clear her position.\n\n“I cannot be your wife while I....” she began.\n\nHe laughed a cold and malignant laugh.\n\n“The manner of life you have chosen is reflected, I suppose, in your ideas. I have too much respect or contempt, or both ... I respect your past and despise your present ... that I was far from the interpretation you put on my words.”\n\nAnna sighed and bowed her head.\n\n“Though indeed I fail to comprehend how, with the independence you show,” he went on, getting hot, “—announcing your infidelity to your husband and seeing nothing reprehensible in it, apparently—you can see anything reprehensible in performing a wife’s duties in relation to your husband.”\n\n“Alexey Alexandrovitch! What is it you want of me?”\n\n“I want you not to meet that man here, and to conduct yourself so that neither the world nor the servants can reproach you ... not to see him. That’s not much, I think. And in return you will enjoy all the privileges of a faithful wife without fulfilling her duties. That’s all I have to say to you. Now it’s time for me to go. I’m not dining at home.” He got up and moved towards the door.\n\nAnna got up too. Bowing in silence, he let her pass before him.\n\n# Chapter 24\n\nThe night spent by Levin on the haycock did not pass without result for him. The way in which he had been managing his land revolted him and had lost all attraction for him. In spite of the magnificent harvest, never had there been, or, at least, never it seemed to him, had there been so many hindrances and so many quarrels between him and the peasants as that year, and the origin of these failures and this hostility was now perfectly comprehensible to him. The delight he had experienced in the work itself, and the consequent greater intimacy with the peasants, the envy he felt of them, of their life, the desire to adopt that life, which had been to him that night not a dream but an intention, the execution of which he had thought out in detail—all this had so transformed his view of the farming of the land as he had managed it, that he could not take his former interest in it, and could not help seeing that unpleasant relation between him and the workpeople which was the foundation of it all. The herd of improved cows such as Pava, the whole land ploughed over and enriched, the nine level fields surrounded with hedges, the two hundred and forty acres heavily manured, the seed sown in drills, and all the rest of it—it was all splendid if only the work had been done for themselves, or for themselves and comrades—people in sympathy with them. But he saw clearly now (his work on a book of agriculture, in which the chief element in husbandry was to have been the laborer, greatly assisted him in this) that the sort of farming he was carrying on was nothing but a cruel and stubborn struggle between him and the laborers, in which there was on one side—his side—a continual intense effort to change everything to a pattern he considered better; on the other side, the natural order of things. And in this struggle he saw that with immense expenditure of force on his side, and with no effort or even intention on the other side, all that was attained was that the work did not go to the liking of either side, and that splendid tools, splendid cattle and land were spoiled with no good to anyone. Worst of all, the energy expended on this work was not simply wasted. He could not help feeling now, since the meaning of this system had become clear to him, that the aim of his energy was a most unworthy one. In reality, what was the struggle about? He was struggling for every farthing of his share (and he could not help it, for he had only to relax his efforts, and he would not have had the money to pay his laborers’ wages), while they were only struggling to be able to do their work easily and agreeably, that is to say, as they were used to doing it. It was for his interests that every laborer should work as hard as possible, and that while doing so he should keep his wits about him, so as to try not to break the winnowing machines, the horse rakes, the thrashing machines, that he should attend to what he was doing. What the laborer wanted was to work as pleasantly as possible, with rests, and above all, carelessly and heedlessly, without thinking. That summer Levin saw this at every step. He sent the men to mow some clover for hay, picking out the worst patches where the clover was overgrown with grass and weeds and of no use for seed; again and again they mowed the best acres of clover, justifying themselves by the pretense that the bailiff had told them to, and trying to pacify him with the assurance that it would be splendid hay; but he knew that it was owing to those acres being so much easier to mow. He sent out a hay machine for pitching the hay—it was broken at the first row because it was dull work for a peasant to sit on the seat in front with the great wings waving above him. And he was told, “Don’t trouble, your honor, sure, the womenfolks will pitch it quick enough.” The ploughs were practically useless, because it never occurred to the laborer to raise the share when he turned the plough, and forcing it round, he strained the horses and tore up the ground, and Levin was begged not to mind about it. The horses were allowed to stray into the wheat because not a single laborer would consent to be night-watchman, and in spite of orders to the contrary, the laborers insisted on taking turns for night duty, and Ivan, after working all day long, fell asleep, and was very penitent for his fault, saying, “Do what you will to me, your honor.”\n\nThey killed three of the best calves by letting them into the clover aftermath without care as to their drinking, and nothing would make the men believe that they had been blown out by the clover, but they told him, by way of consolation, that one of his neighbors had lost a hundred and twelve head of cattle in three days. All this happened, not because anyone felt ill-will to Levin or his farm; on the contrary, he knew that they liked him, thought him a simple gentleman (their highest praise); but it happened simply because all they wanted was to work merrily and carelessly, and his interests were not only remote and incomprehensible to them, but fatally opposed to their most just claims. Long before, Levin had felt dissatisfaction with his own position in regard to the land. He saw where his boat leaked, but he did not look for the leak, perhaps purposely deceiving himself. (Nothing would be left him if he lost faith in it.) But now he could deceive himself no longer. The farming of the land, as he was managing it, had become not merely unattractive but revolting to him, and he could take no further interest in it.\n\nTo this now was joined the presence, only twenty-five miles off, of Kitty Shtcherbatskaya, whom he longed to see and could not see. Darya Alexandrovna Oblonskaya had invited him, when he was over there, to come; to come with the object of renewing his offer to her sister, who would, so she gave him to understand, accept him now. Levin himself had felt on seeing Kitty Shtcherbatskaya that he had never ceased to love her; but he could not go over to the Oblonskys’, knowing she was there. The fact that he had made her an offer, and she had refused him, had placed an insuperable barrier between her and him. “I can’t ask her to be my wife merely because she can’t be the wife of the man she wanted to marry,” he said to himself. The thought of this made him cold and hostile to her. “I should not be able to speak to her without a feeling of reproach; I could not look at her without resentment; and she will only hate me all the more, as she’s bound to. And besides, how can I now, after what Darya Alexandrovna told me, go to see them? Can I help showing that I know what she told me? And me to go magnanimously to forgive her, and have pity on her! Me go through a performance before her of forgiving, and deigning to bestow my love on her!... What induced Darya Alexandrovna to tell me that? By chance I might have seen her, then everything would have happened of itself; but, as it is, it’s out of the question, out of the question!”\n\nDarya Alexandrovna sent him a letter, asking him for a side-saddle for Kitty’s use. “I’m told you have a side-saddle,” she wrote to him; “I hope you will bring it over yourself.”\n\nThis was more than he could stand. How could a woman of any intelligence, of any delicacy, put her sister in such a humiliating position! He wrote ten notes, and tore them all up, and sent the saddle without any reply. To write that he would go was impossible, because he could not go; to write that he could not come because something prevented him, or that he would be away, that was still worse. He sent the saddle without an answer, and with a sense of having done something shameful; he handed over all the now revolting business of the estate to the bailiff, and set off next day to a remote district to see his friend Sviazhsky, who had splendid marshes for grouse in his neighborhood, and had lately written to ask him to keep a long-standing promise to stay with him. The grouse-marsh, in the Surovsky district, had long tempted Levin, but he had continually put off this visit on account of his work on the estate. Now he was glad to get away from the neighborhood of the Shtcherbatskys, and still more from his farm work, especially on a shooting expedition, which always in trouble served as the best consolation.\n\n# Chapter 25\n\nIn the Surovsky district there was no railway nor service of post horses, and Levin drove there with his own horses in his big, old-fashioned carriage.\n\nHe stopped halfway at a well-to-do peasant’s to feed his horses. A bald, well-preserved old man, with a broad, red beard, gray on his cheeks, opened the gate, squeezing against the gatepost to let the three horses pass. Directing the coachman to a place under the shed in the big, clean, tidy yard, with charred, old-fashioned ploughs in it, the old man asked Levin to come into the parlor. A cleanly dressed young woman, with clogs on her bare feet, was scrubbing the floor in the new outer room. She was frightened of the dog, that ran in after Levin, and uttered a shriek, but began laughing at her own fright at once when she was told the dog would not hurt her. Pointing Levin with her bare arm to the door into the parlor, she bent down again, hiding her handsome face, and went on scrubbing.\n\n“Would you like the samovar?” she asked.\n\n“Yes, please.”\n\nThe parlor was a big room, with a Dutch stove, and a screen dividing it into two. Under the holy pictures stood a table painted in patterns, a bench, and two chairs. Near the entrance was a dresser full of crockery. The shutters were closed, there were few flies, and it was so clean that Levin was anxious that Laska, who had been running along the road and bathing in puddles, should not muddy the floor, and ordered her to a place in the corner by the door. After looking round the parlor, Levin went out in the back yard. The good-looking young woman in clogs, swinging the empty pails on the yoke, ran on before him to the well for water.\n\n“Look sharp, my girl!” the old man shouted after her, good-humoredly, and he went up to Levin. “Well, sir, are you going to Nikolay Ivanovitch Sviazhsky? His honor comes to us too,” he began, chatting, leaning his elbows on the railing of the steps. In the middle of the old man’s account of his acquaintance with Sviazhsky, the gates creaked again, and laborers came into the yard from the fields, with wooden ploughs and harrows. The horses harnessed to the ploughs and harrows were sleek and fat. The laborers were obviously of the household: two were young men in cotton shirts and caps, the two others were hired laborers in homespun shirts, one an old man, the other a young fellow. Moving off from the steps, the old man went up to the horses and began unharnessing them.\n\n“What have they been ploughing?” asked Levin.\n\n“Ploughing up the potatoes. We rent a bit of land too. Fedot, don’t let out the gelding, but take it to the trough, and we’ll put the other in harness.”\n\n“Oh, father, the ploughshares I ordered, has he brought them along?” asked the big, healthy-looking fellow, obviously the old man’s son.\n\n“There ... in the outer room,” answered the old man, bundling together the harness he had taken off, and flinging it on the ground. “You can put them on, while they have dinner.”\n\nThe good-looking young woman came into the outer room with the full pails dragging at her shoulders. More women came on the scene from somewhere, young and handsome, middle-aged, old and ugly, with children and without children.\n\nThe samovar was beginning to sing; the laborers and the family, having disposed of the horses, came in to dinner. Levin, getting his provisions out of his carriage, invited the old man to take tea with him.\n\n“Well, I have had some today already,” said the old man, obviously accepting the invitation with pleasure. “But just a glass for company.”\n\nOver their tea Levin heard all about the old man’s farming. Ten years before, the old man had rented three hundred acres from the lady who owned them, and a year ago he had bought them and rented another three hundred from a neighboring landowner. A small part of the land—the worst part—he let out for rent, while a hundred acres of arable land he cultivated himself with his family and two hired laborers. The old man complained that things were doing badly. But Levin saw that he simply did so from a feeling of propriety, and that his farm was in a flourishing condition. If it had been unsuccessful he would not have bought land at thirty-five roubles the acre, he would not have married his three sons and a nephew, he would not have rebuilt twice after fires, and each time on a larger scale. In spite of the old man’s complaints, it was evident that he was proud, and justly proud, of his prosperity, proud of his sons, his nephew, his sons’ wives, his horses and his cows, and especially of the fact that he was keeping all this farming going. From his conversation with the old man, Levin thought he was not averse to new methods either. He had planted a great many potatoes, and his potatoes, as Levin had seen driving past, were already past flowering and beginning to die down, while Levin’s were only just coming into flower. He earthed up his potatoes with a modern plough borrowed from a neighboring landowner. He sowed wheat. The trifling fact that, thinning out his rye, the old man used the rye he thinned out for his horses, specially struck Levin. How many times had Levin seen this splendid fodder wasted, and tried to get it saved; but always it had turned out to be impossible. The peasant got this done, and he could not say enough in praise of it as food for the beasts.\n\n“What have the wenches to do? They carry it out in bundles to the roadside, and the cart brings it away.”\n\n“Well, we landowners can’t manage well with our laborers,” said Levin, handing him a glass of tea.\n\n“Thank you,” said the old man, and he took the glass, but refused sugar, pointing to a lump he had left. “They’re simple destruction,” said he. “Look at Sviazhsky’s, for instance. We know what the land’s like—first-rate, yet there’s not much of a crop to boast of. It’s not looked after enough—that’s all it is!”\n\n“But you work your land with hired laborers?”\n\n“We’re all peasants together. We go into everything ourselves. If a man’s no use, he can go, and we can manage by ourselves.”\n\n“Father, Finogen wants some tar,” said the young woman in the clogs, coming in.\n\n“Yes, yes, that’s how it is, sir!” said the old man, getting up, and crossing himself deliberately, he thanked Levin and went out.\n\nWhen Levin went into the kitchen to call his coachman he saw the whole family at dinner. The women were standing up waiting on them. The young, sturdy-looking son was telling something funny with his mouth full of pudding, and they were all laughing, the woman in the clogs, who was pouring cabbage soup into a bowl, laughing most merrily of all.\n\nVery probably the good-looking face of the young woman in the clogs had a good deal to do with the impression of well-being this peasant household made upon Levin, but the impression was so strong that Levin could never get rid of it. And all the way from the old peasant’s to Sviazhsky’s he kept recalling this peasant farm as though there were something in this impression that demanded his special attention.\n\n# Chapter 26\n\nSviazhsky was the marshal of his district. He was five years older than Levin, and had long been married. His sister-in-law, a young girl Levin liked very much, lived in his house; and Levin knew that Sviazhsky and his wife would have greatly liked to marry the girl to him. He knew this with certainty, as so-called eligible young men always know it, though he could never have brought himself to speak of it to anyone; and he knew too that, although he wanted to get married, and although by every token this very attractive girl would make an excellent wife, he could no more have married her, even if he had not been in love with Kitty Shtcherbatskaya, than he could have flown up to the sky. And this knowledge poisoned the pleasure he had hoped to find in the visit to Sviazhsky.\n\nOn getting Sviazhsky’s letter with the invitation for shooting, Levin had immediately thought of this; but in spite of it he had made up his mind that Sviazhsky’s having such views for him was simply his own groundless supposition, and so he would go, all the same. Besides, at the bottom of his heart he had a desire to try himself, put himself to the test in regard to this girl. The Sviazhskys’ home-life was exceedingly pleasant, and Sviazhsky himself, the best type of man taking part in local affairs that Levin knew, was very interesting to him.\n\nSviazhsky was one of those people, always a source of wonder to Levin, whose convictions, very logical though never original, go one way by themselves, while their life, exceedingly definite and firm in its direction, goes its way quite apart and almost always in direct contradiction to their convictions. Sviazhsky was an extremely advanced man. He despised the nobility, and believed the mass of the nobility to be secretly in favor of serfdom, and only concealing their views from cowardice. He regarded Russia as a ruined country, rather after the style of Turkey, and the government of Russia as so bad that he never permitted himself to criticize its doings seriously, and yet he was a functionary of that government and a model marshal of nobility, and when he drove about he always wore the cockade of office and the cap with the red band. He considered human life only tolerable abroad, and went abroad to stay at every opportunity, and at the same time he carried on a complex and improved system of agriculture in Russia, and with extreme interest followed everything and knew everything that was being done in Russia. He considered the Russian peasant as occupying a stage of development intermediate between the ape and the man, and at the same time in the local assemblies no one was readier to shake hands with the peasants and listen to their opinion. He believed neither in God nor the devil, but was much concerned about the question of the improvement of the clergy and the maintenance of their revenues, and took special trouble to keep up the church in his village.\n\nOn the woman question he was on the side of the extreme advocates of complete liberty for women, and especially their right to labor. But he lived with his wife on such terms that their affectionate childless home life was the admiration of everyone, and arranged his wife’s life so that she did nothing and could do nothing but share her husband’s efforts that her time should pass as happily and as agreeably as possible.\n\nIf it had not been a characteristic of Levin’s to put the most favorable interpretation on people, Sviazhsky’s character would have presented no doubt or difficulty to him: he would have said to himself, “a fool or a knave,” and everything would have seemed clear. But he could not say “a fool,” because Sviazhsky was unmistakably clever, and moreover, a highly cultivated man, who was exceptionally modest over his culture. There was not a subject he knew nothing of. But he did not display his knowledge except when he was compelled to do so. Still less could Levin say that he was a knave, as Sviazhsky was unmistakably an honest, good-hearted, sensible man, who worked good-humoredly, keenly, and perseveringly at his work; he was held in high honor by everyone about him, and certainly he had never consciously done, and was indeed incapable of doing, anything base.\n\nLevin tried to understand him, and could not understand him, and looked at him and his life as at a living enigma.\n\nLevin and he were very friendly, and so Levin used to venture to sound Sviazhsky, to try to get at the very foundation of his view of life; but it was always in vain. Every time Levin tried to penetrate beyond the outer chambers of Sviazhsky’s mind, which were hospitably open to all, he noticed that Sviazhsky was slightly disconcerted; faint signs of alarm were visible in his eyes, as though he were afraid Levin would understand him, and he would give him a kindly, good-humored repulse.\n\nJust now, since his disenchantment with farming, Levin was particularly glad to stay with Sviazhsky. Apart from the fact that the sight of this happy and affectionate couple, so pleased with themselves and everyone else, and their well-ordered home had always a cheering effect on Levin, he felt a longing, now that he was so dissatisfied with his own life, to get at that secret in Sviazhsky that gave him such clearness, definiteness, and good courage in life. Moreover, Levin knew that at Sviazhsky’s he should meet the landowners of the neighborhood, and it was particularly interesting for him just now to hear and take part in those rural conversations concerning crops, laborers’ wages, and so on, which, he was aware, are conventionally regarded as something very low, but which seemed to him just now to constitute the one subject of importance. “It was not, perhaps, of importance in the days of serfdom, and it may not be of importance in England. In both cases the conditions of agriculture are firmly established; but among us now, when everything has been turned upside down and is only just taking shape, the question what form these conditions will take is the one question of importance in Russia,” thought Levin.\n\nThe shooting turned out to be worse than Levin had expected. The marsh was dry and there were no grouse at all. He walked about the whole day and only brought back three birds, but to make up for that—he brought back, as he always did from shooting, an excellent appetite, excellent spirits, and that keen, intellectual mood which with him always accompanied violent physical exertion. And while out shooting, when he seemed to be thinking of nothing at all, suddenly the old man and his family kept coming back to his mind, and the impression of them seemed to claim not merely his attention, but the solution of some question connected with them.\n\nIn the evening at tea, two landowners who had come about some business connected with a wardship were of the party, and the interesting conversation Levin had been looking forward to sprang up.\n\nLevin was sitting beside his hostess at the tea table, and was obliged to keep up a conversation with her and her sister, who was sitting opposite him. Madame Sviazhskaya was a round-faced, fair-haired, rather short woman, all smiles and dimples. Levin tried through her to get a solution of the weighty enigma her husband presented to his mind; but he had not complete freedom of ideas, because he was in an agony of embarrassment. This agony of embarrassment was due to the fact that the sister-in-law was sitting opposite to him, in a dress, specially put on, as he fancied, for his benefit, cut particularly open, in the shape of a trapeze, on her white bosom. This quadrangular opening, in spite of the bosom’s being very white, or just because it was very white, deprived Levin of the full use of his faculties. He imagined, probably mistakenly, that this low-necked bodice had been made on his account, and felt that he had no right to look at it, and tried not to look at it; but he felt that he was to blame for the very fact of the low-necked bodice having been made. It seemed to Levin that he had deceived someone, that he ought to explain something, but that to explain it was impossible, and for that reason he was continually blushing, was ill at ease and awkward. His awkwardness infected the pretty sister-in-law too. But their hostess appeared not to observe this, and kept purposely drawing her into the conversation.\n\n“You say,” she said, pursuing the subject that had been started, “that my husband cannot be interested in what’s Russian. It’s quite the contrary; he is always in cheerful spirits abroad, but not as he is here. Here, he feels in his proper place. He has so much to do, and he has the faculty of interesting himself in everything. Oh, you’ve not been to see our school, have you?”\n\n“I’ve seen it.... The little house covered with ivy, isn’t it?”\n\n“Yes; that’s Nastia’s work,” she said, indicating her sister.\n\n“You teach in it yourself?” asked Levin, trying to look above the open neck, but feeling that wherever he looked in that direction he should see it.\n\n“Yes; I used to teach in it myself, and do teach still, but we have a first-rate schoolmistress now. And we’ve started gymnastic exercises.”\n\n“No, thank you, I won’t have any more tea,” said Levin, and conscious of doing a rude thing, but incapable of continuing the conversation, he got up, blushing. “I hear a very interesting conversation,” he added, and walked to the other end of the table, where Sviazhsky was sitting with the two gentlemen of the neighborhood. Sviazhsky was sitting sideways, with one elbow on the table, and a cup in one hand, while with the other hand he gathered up his beard, held it to his nose and let it drop again, as though he were smelling it. His brilliant black eyes were looking straight at the excited country gentleman with gray whiskers, and apparently he derived amusement from his remarks. The gentleman was complaining of the peasants. It was evident to Levin that Sviazhsky knew an answer to this gentleman’s complaints, which would at once demolish his whole contention, but that in his position he could not give utterance to this answer, and listened, not without pleasure, to the landowner’s comic speeches.\n\nThe gentleman with the gray whiskers was obviously an inveterate adherent of serfdom and a devoted agriculturist, who had lived all his life in the country. Levin saw proofs of this in his dress, in the old-fashioned threadbare coat, obviously not his everyday attire, in his shrewd, deep-set eyes, in his idiomatic, fluent Russian, in the imperious tone that had become habitual from long use, and in the resolute gestures of his large, red, sunburnt hands, with an old betrothal ring on the little finger.\n\n# Chapter 27\n\n“If I’d only the heart to throw up what’s been set going ... such a lot of trouble wasted ... I’d turn my back on the whole business, sell up, go off like Nikolay Ivanovitch ... to hear La Belle Hélène,” said the landowner, a pleasant smile lighting up his shrewd old face.\n\n“But you see you don’t throw it up,” said Nikolay Ivanovitch Sviazhsky; “so there must be something gained.”\n\n“The only gain is that I live in my own house, neither bought nor hired. Besides, one keeps hoping the people will learn sense. Though, instead of that, you’d never believe it—the drunkenness, the immorality! They keep chopping and changing their bits of land. Not a sight of a horse or a cow. The peasant’s dying of hunger, but just go and take him on as a laborer, he’ll do his best to do you a mischief, and then bring you up before the justice of the peace.”\n\n“But then you make complaints to the justice too,” said Sviazhsky.\n\n“I lodge complaints? Not for anything in the world! Such a talking, and such a to-do, that one would have cause to regret it. At the works, for instance, they pocketed the advance-money and made off. What did the justice do? Why, acquitted them. Nothing keeps them in order but their own communal court and their village elder. He’ll flog them in the good old style! But for that there’d be nothing for it but to give it all up and run away.”\n\nObviously the landowner was chaffing Sviazhsky, who, far from resenting it, was apparently amused by it.\n\n“But you see we manage our land without such extreme measures,” said he, smiling: “Levin and I and this gentleman.”\n\nHe indicated the other landowner.\n\n“Yes, the thing’s done at Mihail Petrovitch’s, but ask him how it’s done. Do you call that a rational system?” said the landowner, obviously rather proud of the word “rational.”\n\n“My system’s very simple,” said Mihail Petrovitch, “thank God. All my management rests on getting the money ready for the autumn taxes, and the peasants come to me, ‘Father, master, help us!’ Well, the peasants are all one’s neighbors; one feels for them. So one advances them a third, but one says: ‘Remember, lads, I have helped you, and you must help me when I need it—whether it’s the sowing of the oats, or the haycutting, or the harvest’; and well, one agrees, so much for each taxpayer—though there are dishonest ones among them too, it’s true.”\n\nLevin, who had long been familiar with these patriarchal methods, exchanged glances with Sviazhsky and interrupted Mihail Petrovitch, turning again to the gentleman with the gray whiskers.\n\n“Then what do you think?” he asked; “what system is one to adopt nowadays?”\n\n“Why, manage like Mihail Petrovitch, or let the land for half the crop or for rent to the peasants; that one can do—only that’s just how the general prosperity of the country is being ruined. Where the land with serf-labor and good management gave a yield of nine to one, on the half-crop system it yields three to one. Russia has been ruined by the emancipation!”\n\nSviazhsky looked with smiling eyes at Levin, and even made a faint gesture of irony to him; but Levin did not think the landowner’s words absurd, he understood them better than he did Sviazhsky. A great deal more of what the gentleman with the gray whiskers said to show in what way Russia was ruined by the emancipation struck him indeed as very true, new to him, and quite incontestable. The landowner unmistakably spoke his own individual thought—a thing that very rarely happens—and a thought to which he had been brought not by a desire of finding some exercise for an idle brain, but a thought which had grown up out of the conditions of his life, which he had brooded over in the solitude of his village, and had considered in every aspect.\n\n“The point is, don’t you see, that progress of every sort is only made by the use of authority,” he said, evidently wishing to show he was not without culture. “Take the reforms of Peter, of Catherine, of Alexander. Take European history. And progress in agriculture more than anything else—the potato, for instance, that was introduced among us by force. The wooden plough too wasn’t always used. It was introduced maybe in the days before the Empire, but it was probably brought in by force. Now, in our own day, we landowners in the serf times used various improvements in our husbandry: drying machines and thrashing machines, and carting manure and all the modern implements—all that we brought into use by our authority, and the peasants opposed it at first, and ended by imitating us. Now, by the abolition of serfdom we have been deprived of our authority; and so our husbandry, where it had been raised to a high level, is bound to sink to the most savage primitive condition. That’s how I see it.”\n\n“But why so? If it’s rational, you’ll be able to keep up the same system with hired labor,” said Sviazhsky.\n\n“We’ve no power over them. With whom am I going to work the system, allow me to ask?”\n\n“There it is—the labor force—the chief element in agriculture,” thought Levin.\n\n“With laborers.”\n\n“The laborers won’t work well, and won’t work with good implements. Our laborer can do nothing but get drunk like a pig, and when he’s drunk he ruins everything you give him. He makes the horses ill with too much water, cuts good harness, barters the tires of the wheels for drink, drops bits of iron into the thrashing machine, so as to break it. He loathes the sight of anything that’s not after his fashion. And that’s how it is the whole level of husbandry has fallen. Lands gone out of cultivation, overgrown with weeds, or divided among the peasants, and where millions of bushels were raised you get a hundred thousand; the wealth of the country has decreased. If the same thing had been done, but with care that....”\n\nAnd he proceeded to unfold his own scheme of emancipation by means of which these drawbacks might have been avoided.\n\nThis did not interest Levin, but when he had finished, Levin went back to his first position, and, addressing Sviazhsky, and trying to draw him into expressing his serious opinion:—\n\n“That the standard of culture is falling, and that with our present relations to the peasants there is no possibility of farming on a rational system to yield a profit—that’s perfectly true,” said he.\n\n“I don’t believe it,” Sviazhsky replied quite seriously; “all I see is that we don’t know how to cultivate the land, and that our system of agriculture in the serf days was by no means too high, but too low. We have no machines, no good stock, no efficient supervision; we don’t even know how to keep accounts. Ask any landowner; he won’t be able to tell you what crop’s profitable, and what’s not.”\n\n“Italian bookkeeping,” said the gentleman of the gray whiskers ironically. “You may keep your books as you like, but if they spoil everything for you, there won’t be any profit.”\n\n“Why do they spoil things? A poor thrashing machine, or your Russian presser, they will break, but my steam press they don’t break. A wretched Russian nag they’ll ruin, but keep good dray-horses—they won’t ruin them. And so it is all round. We must raise our farming to a higher level.”\n\n“Oh, if one only had the means to do it, Nikolay Ivanovitch! It’s all very well for you; but for me, with a son to keep at the university, lads to be educated at the high school—how am I going to buy these dray-horses?”\n\n“Well, that’s what the land banks are for.”\n\n“To get what’s left me sold by auction? No, thank you.”\n\n“I don’t agree that it’s necessary or possible to raise the level of agriculture still higher,” said Levin. “I devote myself to it, and I have means, but I can do nothing. As to the banks, I don’t know to whom they’re any good. For my part, anyway, whatever I’ve spent money on in the way of husbandry, it has been a loss: stock—a loss, machinery—a loss.”\n\n“That’s true enough,” the gentleman with the gray whiskers chimed in, positively laughing with satisfaction.\n\n“And I’m not the only one,” pursued Levin. “I mix with all the neighboring landowners, who are cultivating their land on a rational system; they all, with rare exceptions, are doing so at a loss. Come, tell us how does your land do—does it pay?” said Levin, and at once in Sviazhsky’s eyes he detected that fleeting expression of alarm which he had noticed whenever he had tried to penetrate beyond the outer chambers of Sviazhsky’s mind.\n\nMoreover, this question on Levin’s part was not quite in good faith. Madame Sviazhskaya had just told him at tea that they had that summer invited a German expert in bookkeeping from Moscow, who for a consideration of five hundred roubles had investigated the management of their property, and found that it was costing them a loss of three thousand odd roubles. She did not remember the precise sum, but it appeared that the German had worked it out to the fraction of a farthing.\n\nThe gray-whiskered landowner smiled at the mention of the profits of Sviazhsky’s farming, obviously aware how much gain his neighbor and marshal was likely to be making.\n\n“Possibly it does not pay,” answered Sviazhsky. “That merely proves either that I’m a bad manager, or that I’ve sunk my capital for the increase of my rents.”\n\n“Oh, rent!” Levin cried with horror. “Rent there may be in Europe, where land has been improved by the labor put into it, but with us all the land is deteriorating from the labor put into it—in other words they’re working it out; so there’s no question of rent.”\n\n“How no rent? It’s a law.”\n\n“Then we’re outside the law; rent explains nothing for us, but simply muddles us. No, tell me how there can be a theory of rent?...”\n\n“Will you have some junket? Masha, pass us some junket or raspberries.” He turned to his wife. “Extraordinarily late the raspberries are lasting this year.”\n\nAnd in the happiest frame of mind Sviazhsky got up and walked off, apparently supposing the conversation to have ended at the very point when to Levin it seemed that it was only just beginning.\n\nHaving lost his antagonist, Levin continued the conversation with the gray-whiskered landowner, trying to prove to him that all the difficulty arises from the fact that we don’t find out the peculiarities and habits of our laborer; but the landowner, like all men who think independently and in isolation, was slow in taking in any other person’s idea, and particularly partial to his own. He stuck to it that the Russian peasant is a swine and likes swinishness, and that to get him out of his swinishness one must have authority, and there is none; one must have the stick, and we have become so liberal that we have all of a sudden replaced the stick that served us for a thousand years by lawyers and model prisons, where the worthless, stinking peasant is fed on good soup and has a fixed allowance of cubic feet of air.\n\n“What makes you think,” said Levin, trying to get back to the question, “that it’s impossible to find some relation to the laborer in which the labor would become productive?”\n\n“That never could be so with the Russian peasantry; we’ve no power over them,” answered the landowner.\n\n“How can new conditions be found?” said Sviazhsky. Having eaten some junket and lighted a cigarette, he came back to the discussion. “All possible relations to the labor force have been defined and studied,” he said. “The relic of barbarism, the primitive commune with each guarantee for all, will disappear of itself; serfdom has been abolished—there remains nothing but free labor, and its forms are fixed and ready made, and must be adopted. Permanent hands, day-laborers, rammers—you can’t get out of those forms.”\n\n“But Europe is dissatisfied with these forms.”\n\n“Dissatisfied, and seeking new ones. And will find them, in all probability.”\n\n“That’s just what I was meaning,” answered Levin. “Why shouldn’t we seek them for ourselves?”\n\n“Because it would be just like inventing afresh the means for constructing railways. They are ready, invented.”\n\n“But if they don’t do for us, if they’re stupid?” said Levin.\n\nAnd again he detected the expression of alarm in the eyes of Sviazhsky.\n\n“Oh, yes; we’ll bury the world under our caps! We’ve found the secret Europe was seeking for! I’ve heard all that; but, excuse me, do you know all that’s been done in Europe on the question of the organization of labor?”\n“No, very little.”\n\n“That question is now absorbing the best minds in Europe. The Schulze-Delitsch movement.... And then all this enormous literature of the labor question, the most liberal Lassalle movement ... the Mulhausen experiment? That’s a fact by now, as you’re probably aware.”\n\n“I have some idea of it, but very vague.”\n\n“No, you only say that; no doubt you know all about it as well as I do. I’m not a professor of sociology, of course, but it interested me, and really, if it interests you, you ought to study it.”\n\n“But what conclusion have they come to?”\n\n“Excuse me....”\n\nThe two neighbors had risen, and Sviazhsky, once more checking Levin in his inconvenient habit of peeping into what was beyond the outer chambers of his mind, went to see his guests out.\n\n# Chapter 28\n\nLevin was insufferably bored that evening with the ladies; he was stirred as he had never been before by the idea that the dissatisfaction he was feeling with his system of managing his land was not an exceptional case, but the general condition of things in Russia; that the organization of some relation of the laborers to the soil in which they would work, as with the peasant he had met half-way to the Sviazhskys’, was not a dream, but a problem which must be solved. And it seemed to him that the problem could be solved, and that he ought to try and solve it.\n\nAfter saying good-night to the ladies, and promising to stay the whole of the next day, so as to make an expedition on horseback with them to see an interesting ruin in the crown forest, Levin went, before going to bed, into his host’s study to get the books on the labor question that Sviazhsky had offered him. Sviazhsky’s study was a huge room, surrounded by bookcases and with two tables in it—one a massive writing-table, standing in the middle of the room, and the other a round table, covered with recent numbers of reviews and journals in different languages, ranged like the rays of a star round the lamp. On the writing-table was a stand of drawers marked with gold lettering, and full of papers of various sorts.\n\nSviazhsky took out the books, and sat down in a rocking-chair.\n\n“What are you looking at there?” he said to Levin, who was standing at the round table looking through the reviews.\n\n“Oh, yes, there’s a very interesting article here,” said Sviazhsky of the review Levin was holding in his hand. “It appears,” he went on, with eager interest, “that Friedrich was not, after all, the person chiefly responsible for the partition of Poland. It is proved....”\n\nAnd with his characteristic clearness, he summed up those new, very important, and interesting revelations. Although Levin was engrossed at the moment by his ideas about the problem of the land, he wondered, as he heard Sviazhsky: “What is there inside of him? And why, why is he interested in the partition of Poland?” When Sviazhsky had finished, Levin could not help asking: “Well, and what then?” But there was nothing to follow. It was simply interesting that it had been proved to be so and so. But Sviazhsky did not explain, and saw no need to explain why it was interesting to him.\n\n“Yes, but I was very much interested by your irritable neighbor,” said Levin, sighing. “He’s a clever fellow, and said a lot that was true.”\n\n“Oh, get along with you! An inveterate supporter of serfdom at heart, like all of them!” said Sviazhsky.\n\n“Whose marshal you are.”\n\n“Yes, only I marshal them in the other direction,” said Sviazhsky, laughing.\n\n“I’ll tell you what interests me very much,” said Levin. “He’s right that our system, that’s to say of rational farming, doesn’t answer, that the only thing that answers is the money-lender system, like that meek-looking gentleman’s, or else the very simplest.... Whose fault is it?”\n\n“Our own, of course. Besides, it’s not true that it doesn’t answer. It answers with Vassiltchikov.”\n\n“A factory....”\n\n“But I really don’t know what it is you are surprised at. The people are at such a low stage of rational and moral development, that it’s obvious they’re bound to oppose everything that’s strange to them. In Europe, a rational system answers because the people are educated; it follows that we must educate the people—that’s all.”\n\n“But how are we to educate the people?”\n\n“To educate the people three things are needed: schools, and schools, and schools.”\n\n“But you said yourself the people are at such a low stage of material development: what help are schools for that?”\n\n“Do you know, you remind me of the story of the advice given to the sick man—You should try purgative medicine. Taken: worse. Try leeches. Tried them: worse. Well, then, there’s nothing left but to pray to God. Tried it: worse. That’s just how it is with us. I say political economy; you say—worse. I say socialism: worse. Education: worse.”\n\n“But how do schools help matters?”\n\n“They give the peasant fresh wants.”\n\n“Well, that’s a thing I’ve never understood,” Levin replied with heat. “In what way are schools going to help the people to improve their material position? You say schools, education, will give them fresh wants. So much the worse, since they won’t be capable of satisfying them. And in what way a knowledge of addition and subtraction and the catechism is going to improve their material condition, I never could make out. The day before yesterday, I met a peasant woman in the evening with a little baby, and asked her where she was going. She said she was going to the wise woman; her boy had screaming fits, so she was taking him to be doctored. I asked, ‘Why, how does the wise woman cure screaming fits?’ ‘She puts the child on the hen-roost and repeats some charm....’”\n\n“Well, you’re saying it yourself! What’s wanted to prevent her taking her child to the hen-roost to cure it of screaming fits is just....” Sviazhsky said, smiling good-humoredly.\n\n“Oh, no!” said Levin with annoyance; “that method of doctoring I merely meant as a simile for doctoring the people with schools. The people are poor and ignorant—that we see as surely as the peasant woman sees the baby is ill because it screams. But in what way this trouble of poverty and ignorance is to be cured by schools is as incomprehensible as how the hen-roost affects the screaming. What has to be cured is what makes him poor.”\n\n“Well, in that, at least, you’re in agreement with Spencer, whom you dislike so much. He says, too, that education may be the consequence of greater prosperity and comfort, of more frequent washing, as he says, but not of being able to read and write....”\n\n“Well, then, I’m very glad—or the contrary, very sorry, that I’m in agreement with Spencer; only I’ve known it a long while. Schools can do no good; what will do good is an economic organization in which the people will become richer, will have more leisure—and then there will be schools.”\n\n“Still, all over Europe now schools are obligatory.”\n\n“And how far do you agree with Spencer yourself about it?” asked Levin.\n\nBut there was a gleam of alarm in Sviazhsky’s eyes, and he said smiling:\n\n“No; that screaming story is positively capital! Did you really hear it yourself?”\n\nLevin saw that he was not to discover the connection between this man’s life and his thoughts. Obviously he did not care in the least what his reasoning led him to; all he wanted was the process of reasoning. And he did not like it when the process of reasoning brought him into a blind alley. That was the only thing he disliked, and avoided by changing the conversation to something agreeable and amusing.\n\nAll the impressions of the day, beginning with the impression made by the old peasant, which served, as it were, as the fundamental basis of all the conceptions and ideas of the day, threw Levin into violent excitement. This dear good Sviazhsky, keeping a stock of ideas simply for social purposes, and obviously having some other principles hidden from Levin, while with the crowd, whose name is legion, he guided public opinion by ideas he did not share; that irascible country gentleman, perfectly correct in the conclusions that he had been worried into by life, but wrong in his exasperation against a whole class, and that the best class in Russia; his own dissatisfaction with the work he had been doing, and the vague hope of finding a remedy for all this—all was blended in a sense of inward turmoil, and anticipation of some solution near at hand.\n\nLeft alone in the room assigned him, lying on a spring mattress that yielded unexpectedly at every movement of his arm or his leg, Levin did not fall asleep for a long while. Not one conversation with Sviazhsky, though he had said a great deal that was clever, had interested Levin; but the conclusions of the irascible landowner required consideration. Levin could not help recalling every word he had said, and in imagination amending his own replies.\n\n“Yes, I ought to have said to him: You say that our husbandry does not answer because the peasant hates improvements, and that they must be forced on him by authority. If no system of husbandry answered at all without these improvements, you would be quite right. But the only system that does answer is where laborer is working in accordance with his habits, just as on the old peasant’s land half-way here. Your and our general dissatisfaction with the system shows that either we are to blame or the laborers. We have gone our way—the European way—a long while, without asking ourselves about the qualities of our labor force. Let us try to look upon the labor force not as an abstract force, but as the Russian peasant with his instincts, and we shall arrange our system of culture in accordance with that. Imagine, I ought to have said to him, that you have the same system as the old peasant has, that you have found means of making your laborers take an interest in the success of the work, and have found the happy mean in the way of improvements which they will admit, and you will, without exhausting the soil, get twice or three times the yield you got before. Divide it in halves, give half as the share of labor, the surplus left you will be greater, and the share of labor will be greater too. And to do this one must lower the standard of husbandry and interest the laborers in its success. How to do this?—that’s a matter of detail; but undoubtedly it can be done.”\n\nThis idea threw Levin into a great excitement. He did not sleep half the night, thinking over in detail the putting of his idea into practice. He had not intended to go away next day, but he now determined to go home early in the morning. Besides, the sister-in-law with her low-necked bodice aroused in him a feeling akin to shame and remorse for some utterly base action. Most important of all—he must get back without delay: he would have to make haste to put his new project to the peasants before the sowing of the winter wheat, so that the sowing might be undertaken on a new basis. He had made up his mind to revolutionize his whole system.\n\n# Chapter 29\n\nThe carrying out of Levin’s plan presented many difficulties; but he struggled on, doing his utmost, and attained a result which, though not what he desired, was enough to enable him, without self-deception, to believe that the attempt was worth the trouble. One of the chief difficulties was that the process of cultivating the land was in full swing, that it was impossible to stop everything and begin it all again from the beginning, and the machine had to be mended while in motion.\n\nWhen on the evening that he arrived home he informed the bailiff of his plans, the latter with visible pleasure agreed with what he said so long as he was pointing out that all that had been done up to that time was stupid and useless. The bailiff said that he had said so a long while ago, but no heed had been paid him. But as for the proposal made by Levin—to take a part as shareholder with his laborers in each agricultural undertaking—at this the bailiff simply expressed a profound despondency, and offered no definite opinion, but began immediately talking of the urgent necessity of carrying the remaining sheaves of rye the next day, and of sending the men out for the second ploughing, so that Levin felt that this was not the time for discussing it.\n\nOn beginning to talk to the peasants about it, and making a proposition to cede them the land on new terms, he came into collision with the same great difficulty that they were so much absorbed by the current work of the day, that they had not time to consider the advantages and disadvantages of the proposed scheme.\n\nThe simple-hearted Ivan, the cowherd, seemed completely to grasp Levin’s proposal—that he should with his family take a share of the profits of the cattle-yard—and he was in complete sympathy with the plan. But when Levin hinted at the future advantages, Ivan’s face expressed alarm and regret that he could not hear all he had to say, and he made haste to find himself some task that would admit of no delay: he either snatched up the fork to pitch the hay out of the pens, or ran to get water or to clear out the dung.\n\nAnother difficulty lay in the invincible disbelief of the peasant that a landowner’s object could be anything else than a desire to squeeze all he could out of them. They were firmly convinced that his real aim (whatever he might say to them) would always be in what he did not say to them. And they themselves, in giving their opinion, said a great deal but never said what was their real object. Moreover (Levin felt that the irascible landowner had been right) the peasants made their first and unalterable condition of any agreement whatever that they should not be forced to any new methods of tillage of any kind, nor to use new implements. They agreed that the modern plough ploughed better, that the scarifier did the work more quickly, but they found thousands of reasons that made it out of the question for them to use either of them; and though he had accepted the conviction that he would have to lower the standard of cultivation, he felt sorry to give up improved methods, the advantages of which were so obvious. But in spite of all these difficulties he got his way, and by autumn the system was working, or at least so it seemed to him.\n\nAt first Levin had thought of giving up the whole farming of the land just as it was to the peasants, the laborers, and the bailiff on new conditions of partnership; but he was very soon convinced that this was impossible, and determined to divide it up. The cattle-yard, the garden, hay fields, and arable land, divided into several parts, had to be made into separate lots. The simple-hearted cowherd, Ivan, who, Levin fancied, understood the matter better than any of them, collecting together a gang of workers to help him, principally of his own family, became a partner in the cattle-yard. A distant part of the estate, a tract of waste land that had lain fallow for eight years, was with the help of the clever carpenter, Fyodor Ryezunov, taken by six families of peasants on new conditions of partnership, and the peasant Shuraev took the management of all the vegetable gardens on the same terms. The remainder of the land was still worked on the old system, but these three associated partnerships were the first step to a new organization of the whole, and they completely took up Levin’s time.\n\nIt is true that in the cattle-yard things went no better than before, and Ivan strenuously opposed warm housing for the cows and butter made of fresh cream, affirming that cows require less food if kept cold, and that butter is more profitable made from sour cream, and he asked for wages just as under the old system, and took not the slightest interest in the fact that the money he received was not wages but an advance out of his future share in the profits.\n\nIt is true that Fyodor Ryezunov’s company did not plough over the ground twice before sowing, as had been agreed, justifying themselves on the plea that the time was too short. It is true that the peasants of the same company, though they had agreed to work the land on new conditions, always spoke of the land, not as held in partnership, but as rented for half the crop, and more than once the peasants and Ryezunov himself said to Levin, “If you would take a rent for the land, it would save you trouble, and we should be more free.” Moreover the same peasants kept putting off, on various excuses, the building of a cattleyard and barn on the land as agreed upon, and delayed doing it till the winter.\n\nIt is true that Shuraev would have liked to let out the kitchen gardens he had undertaken in small lots to the peasants. He evidently quite misunderstood, and apparently intentionally misunderstood, the conditions upon which the land had been given to him.\n\nOften, too, talking to the peasants and explaining to them all the advantages of the plan, Levin felt that the peasants heard nothing but the sound of his voice, and were firmly resolved, whatever he might say, not to let themselves be taken in. He felt this especially when he talked to the cleverest of the peasants, Ryezunov, and detected the gleam in Ryezunov’s eyes which showed so plainly both ironical amusement at Levin, and the firm conviction that, if anyone were to be taken in, it would not be he, Ryezunov. But in spite of all this Levin thought the system worked, and that by keeping accounts strictly and insisting on his own way, he would prove to them in the future the advantages of the arrangement, and then the system would go of itself.\n\nThese matters, together with the management of the land still left on his hands, and the indoor work over his book, so engrossed Levin the whole summer that he scarcely ever went out shooting. At the end of August he heard that the Oblonskys had gone away to Moscow, from their servant who brought back the side-saddle. He felt that in not answering Darya Alexandrovna’s letter he had by his rudeness, of which he could not think without a flush of shame, burned his ships, and that he would never go and see them again. He had been just as rude with the Sviazhskys, leaving them without saying good-bye. But he would never go to see them again either. He did not care about that now. The business of reorganizing the farming of his land absorbed him as completely as though there would never be anything else in his life. He read the books lent him by Sviazhsky, and copying out what he had not got, he read both the economic and socialistic books on the subject, but, as he had anticipated, found nothing bearing on the scheme he had undertaken. In the books on political economy—in Mill, for instance, whom he studied first with great ardor, hoping every minute to find an answer to the questions that were engrossing him—he found laws deduced from the condition of land culture in Europe; but he did not see why these laws, which did not apply in Russia, must be general. He saw just the same thing in the socialistic books: either they were the beautiful but impracticable fantasies which had fascinated him when he was a student, or they were attempts at improving, rectifying the economic position in which Europe was placed, with which the system of land tenure in Russia had nothing in common. Political economy told him that the laws by which the wealth of Europe had been developed, and was developing, were universal and unvarying. Socialism told him that development along these lines leads to ruin. And neither of them gave an answer, or even a hint, in reply to the question what he, Levin, and all the Russian peasants and landowners, were to do with their millions of hands and millions of acres, to make them as productive as possible for the common weal.\n\nHaving once taken the subject up, he read conscientiously everything bearing on it, and intended in the autumn to go abroad to study land systems on the spot, in order that he might not on this question be confronted with what so often met him on various subjects. Often, just as he was beginning to understand the idea in the mind of anyone he was talking to, and was beginning to explain his own, he would suddenly be told: “But Kauffmann, but Jones, but Dubois, but Michelli? You haven’t read them: they’ve thrashed that question out thoroughly.”\n\nHe saw now distinctly that Kauffmann and Michelli had nothing to tell him. He knew what he wanted. He saw that Russia has splendid land, splendid laborers, and that in certain cases, as at the peasant’s on the way to Sviazhsky’s, the produce raised by the laborers and the land is great—in the majority of cases when capital is applied in the European way the produce is small, and that this simply arises from the fact that the laborers want to work and work well only in their own peculiar way, and that this antagonism is not incidental but invariable, and has its roots in the national spirit. He thought that the Russian people whose task it was to colonize and cultivate vast tracts of unoccupied land, consciously adhered, till all their land was occupied, to the methods suitable to their purpose, and that their methods were by no means so bad as was generally supposed. And he wanted to prove this theoretically in his book and practically on his land.\n\n# Chapter 30\n\nAt the end of September the timber had been carted for building the cattleyard on the land that had been allotted to the association of peasants, and the butter from the cows was sold and the profits divided. In practice the system worked capitally, or, at least, so it seemed to Levin. In order to work out the whole subject theoretically and to complete his book, which, in Levin’s daydreams, was not merely to effect a revolution in political economy, but to annihilate that science entirely and to lay the foundation of a new science of the relation of the people to the soil, all that was left to do was to make a tour abroad, and to study on the spot all that had been done in the same direction, and to collect conclusive evidence that all that had been done there was not what was wanted. Levin was only waiting for the delivery of his wheat to receive the money for it and go abroad. But the rains began, preventing the harvesting of the corn and potatoes left in the fields, and putting a stop to all work, even to the delivery of the wheat.\n\nThe mud was impassable along the roads; two mills were carried away, and the weather got worse and worse.\n\nOn the 30th of September the sun came out in the morning, and hoping for fine weather, Levin began making final preparations for his journey. He gave orders for the wheat to be delivered, sent the bailiff to the merchant to get the money owing him, and went out himself to give some final directions on the estate before setting off.\n\nHaving finished all his business, soaked through with the streams of water which kept running down the leather behind his neck and his gaiters, but in the keenest and most confident temper, Levin returned homewards in the evening. The weather had become worse than ever towards evening; the hail lashed the drenched mare so cruelly that she went along sideways, shaking her head and ears; but Levin was all right under his hood, and he looked cheerfully about him at the muddy streams running under the wheels, at the drops hanging on every bare twig, at the whiteness of the patch of unmelted hailstones on the planks of the bridge, at the thick layer of still juicy, fleshy leaves that lay heaped up about the stripped elm-tree. In spite of the gloominess of nature around him, he felt peculiarly eager. The talks he had been having with the peasants in the further village had shown that they were beginning to get used to their new position. The old servant to whose hut he had gone to get dry evidently approved of Levin’s plan, and of his own accord proposed to enter the partnership by the purchase of cattle.\n\n“I have only to go stubbornly on towards my aim, and I shall attain my end,” thought Levin; “and it’s something to work and take trouble for. This is not a matter of myself individually; the question of the public welfare comes into it. The whole system of culture, the chief element in the condition of the people, must be completely transformed. Instead of poverty, general prosperity and content; instead of hostility, harmony and unity of interests. In short, a bloodless revolution, but a revolution of the greatest magnitude, beginning in the little circle of our district, then the province, then Russia, the whole world. Because a just idea cannot but be fruitful. Yes, it’s an aim worth working for. And its being me, Kostya Levin, who went to a ball in a black tie, and was refused by the Shtcherbatskaya girl, and who was intrinsically such a pitiful, worthless creature—that proves nothing; I feel sure Franklin felt just as worthless, and he too had no faith in himself, thinking of himself as a whole. That means nothing. And he too, most likely, had an Agafea Mihalovna to whom he confided his secrets.”\n\nMusing on such thoughts Levin reached home in the darkness.\n\nThe bailiff, who had been to the merchant, had come back and brought part of the money for the wheat. An agreement had been made with the old servant, and on the road the bailiff had learned that everywhere the corn was still standing in the fields, so that his one hundred and sixty shocks that had not been carried were nothing in comparison with the losses of others.\n\nAfter dinner Levin was sitting, as he usually did, in an easy chair with a book, and as he read he went on thinking of the journey before him in connection with his book. Today all the significance of his book rose before him with special distinctness, and whole periods ranged themselves in his mind in illustration of his theories. “I must write that down,” he thought. “That ought to form a brief introduction, which I thought unnecessary before.” He got up to go to his writing-table, and Laska, lying at his feet, got up too, stretching and looking at him as though to inquire where to go. But he had not time to write it down, for the head peasants had come round, and Levin went out into the hall to them.\n\nAfter his levee, that is to say, giving directions about the labors of the next day, and seeing all the peasants who had business with him, Levin went back to his study and sat down to work.\n\nLaska lay under the table; Agafea Mihalovna settled herself in her place with her stocking.\n\nAfter writing for a little while, Levin suddenly thought with exceptional vividness of Kitty, her refusal, and their last meeting. He got up and began walking about the room.\n\n“What’s the use of being dreary?” said Agafea Mihalovna. “Come, why do you stay on at home? You ought to go to some warm springs, especially now you’re ready for the journey.”\n\n“Well, I am going away the day after tomorrow, Agafea Mihalovna; I must finish my work.”\n\n“There, there, your work, you say! As if you hadn’t done enough for the peasants! Why, as ’tis, they’re saying, ‘Your master will be getting some honor from the Tsar for it.’ Indeed and it is a strange thing; why need you worry about the peasants?”\n\n“I’m not worrying about them; I’m doing it for my own good.”\n\nAgafea Mihalovna knew every detail of Levin’s plans for his land. Levin often put his views before her in all their complexity, and not uncommonly he argued with her and did not agree with her comments. But on this occasion she entirely misinterpreted what he had said.\n\n“Of one’s soul’s salvation we all know and must think before all else,” she said with a sigh. “Parfen Denisitch now, for all he was no scholar, he died a death that God grant everyone of us the like,” she said, referring to a servant who had died recently. “Took the sacrament and all.”\n\n“That’s not what I mean,” said he. “I mean that I’m acting for my own advantage. It’s all the better for me if the peasants do their work better.”\n\n“Well, whatever you do, if he’s a lazy good-for-nought, everything’ll be at sixes and sevens. If he has a conscience, he’ll work, and if not, there’s no doing anything.”\n\n“Oh, come, you say yourself Ivan has begun looking after the cattle better.”\n\n“All I say is,” answered Agafea Mihalovna, evidently not speaking at random, but in strict sequence of idea, “that you ought to get married, that’s what I say.”\n\nAgafea Mihalovna’s allusion to the very subject he had only just been thinking about, hurt and stung him. Levin scowled, and without answering her, he sat down again to his work, repeating to himself all that he had been thinking of the real significance of that work. Only at intervals he listened in the stillness to the click of Agafea Mihalovna’s needles, and recollecting what he did not want to remember, he frowned again.\n\nAt nine o’clock they heard the bell and the faint vibration of a carriage over the mud.\n\n“Well, here’s visitors come to us, and you won’t be dull,” said Agafea Mihalovna, getting up and going to the door. But Levin overtook her. His work was not going well now, and he was glad of a visitor, whoever it might be.\n\n# Chapter 31\n\nRunning halfway down the staircase, Levin caught a sound he knew, a familiar cough in the hall. But he heard it indistinctly through the sound of his own footsteps, and hoped he was mistaken. Then he caught sight of a long, bony, familiar figure, and now it seemed there was no possibility of mistake; and yet he still went on hoping that this tall man taking off his fur cloak and coughing was not his brother Nikolay.\n\nLevin loved his brother, but being with him was always a torture. Just now, when Levin, under the influence of the thoughts that had come to him, and Agafea Mihalovna’s hint, was in a troubled and uncertain humor, the meeting with his brother that he had to face seemed particularly difficult. Instead of a lively, healthy visitor, some outsider who would, he hoped, cheer him up in his uncertain humor, he had to see his brother, who knew him through and through, who would call forth all the thoughts nearest his heart, would force him to show himself fully. And that he was not disposed to do.\n\nAngry with himself for so base a feeling, Levin ran into the hall; as soon as he had seen his brother close, this feeling of selfish disappointment vanished instantly and was replaced by pity. Terrible as his brother Nikolay had been before in his emaciation and sickliness, now he looked still more emaciated, still more wasted. He was a skeleton covered with skin.\n\nHe stood in the hall, jerking his long thin neck, and pulling the scarf off it, and smiled a strange and pitiful smile. When he saw that smile, submissive and humble, Levin felt something clutching at his throat.\n\n“You see, I’ve come to you,” said Nikolay in a thick voice, never for one second taking his eyes off his brother’s face. “I’ve been meaning to a long while, but I’ve been unwell all the time. Now I’m ever so much better,” he said, rubbing his beard with his big thin hands.\n\n“Yes, yes!” answered Levin. And he felt still more frightened when, kissing him, he felt with his lips the dryness of his brother’s skin and saw close to him his big eyes, full of a strange light.\n\nA few weeks before, Konstantin Levin had written to his brother that through the sale of the small part of the property, that had remained undivided, there was a sum of about two thousand roubles to come to him as his share.\n\nNikolay said that he had come now to take this money and, what was more important, to stay a while in the old nest, to get in touch with the earth, so as to renew his strength like the heroes of old for the work that lay before him. In spite of his exaggerated stoop, and the emaciation that was so striking from his height, his movements were as rapid and abrupt as ever. Levin led him into his study.\n\nHis brother dressed with particular care—a thing he never used to do—combed his scanty, lank hair, and, smiling, went upstairs.\n\nHe was in the most affectionate and good-humored mood, just as Levin often remembered him in childhood. He even referred to Sergey Ivanovitch without rancor. When he saw Agafea Mihalovna, he made jokes with her and asked after the old servants. The news of the death of Parfen Denisitch made a painful impression on him. A look of fear crossed his face, but he regained his serenity immediately.\n\n“Of course he was quite old,” he said, and changed the subject. “Well, I’ll spend a month or two with you, and then I’m off to Moscow. Do you know, Myakov has promised me a place there, and I’m going into the service. Now I’m going to arrange my life quite differently,” he went on. “You know I got rid of that woman.”\n\n“Marya Nikolaevna? Why, what for?”\n\n“Oh, she was a horrid woman! She caused me all sorts of worries.” But he did not say what the annoyances were. He could not say that he had cast off Marya Nikolaevna because the tea was weak, and, above all, because she would look after him, as though he were an invalid.\n\n“Besides, I want to turn over a new leaf completely now. I’ve done silly things, of course, like everyone else, but money’s the last consideration; I don’t regret it. So long as there’s health, and my health, thank God, is quite restored.”\n\nLevin listened and racked his brains, but could think of nothing to say. Nikolay probably felt the same; he began questioning his brother about his affairs; and Levin was glad to talk about himself, because then he could speak without hypocrisy. He told his brother of his plans and his doings.\n\nHis brother listened, but evidently he was not interested by it.\n\nThese two men were so akin, so near each other, that the slightest gesture, the tone of voice, told both more than could be said in words.\n\nBoth of them now had only one thought—the illness of Nikolay and the nearness of his death—which stifled all else. But neither of them dared to speak of it, and so whatever they said—not uttering the one thought that filled their minds—was all falsehood. Never had Levin been so glad when the evening was over and it was time to go to bed. Never with any outside person, never on any official visit had he been so unnatural and false as he was that evening. And the consciousness of this unnaturalness, and the remorse he felt at it, made him even more unnatural. He wanted to weep over his dying, dearly loved brother, and he had to listen and keep on talking of how he meant to live.\n\nAs the house was damp, and only one bedroom had been kept heated, Levin put his brother to sleep in his own bedroom behind a screen.\n\nHis brother got into bed, and whether he slept or did not sleep, tossed about like a sick man, coughed, and when he could not get his throat clear, mumbled something. Sometimes when his breathing was painful, he said, “Oh, my God!” Sometimes when he was choking he muttered angrily, “Ah, the devil!” Levin could not sleep for a long while, hearing him. His thoughts were of the most various, but the end of all his thoughts was the same—death. Death, the inevitable end of all, for the first time presented itself to him with irresistible force. And death, which was here in this loved brother, groaning half asleep and from habit calling without distinction on God and the devil, was not so remote as it had hitherto seemed to him. It was in himself too, he felt that. If not today, tomorrow, if not tomorrow, in thirty years, wasn’t it all the same! And what was this inevitable death—he did not know, had never thought about it, and what was more, had not the power, had not the courage to think about it.\n\n“I work, I want to do something, but I had forgotten it must all end; I had forgotten—death.”\n\nHe sat on his bed in the darkness, crouched up, hugging his knees, and holding his breath from the strain of thought, he pondered. But the more intensely he thought, the clearer it became to him that it was indubitably so, that in reality, looking upon life, he had forgotten one little fact—that death will come, and all ends; that nothing was even worth beginning, and that there was no helping it anyway. Yes, it was awful, but it was so.\n\n“But I am alive still. Now what’s to be done? what’s to be done?” he said in despair. He lighted a candle, got up cautiously and went to the looking-glass, and began looking at his face and hair. Yes, there were gray hairs about his temples. He opened his mouth. His back teeth were beginning to decay. He bared his muscular arms. Yes, there was strength in them. But Nikolay, who lay there breathing with what was left of lungs, had had a strong, healthy body too. And suddenly he recalled how they used to go to bed together as children, and how they only waited till Fyodor Bogdanitch was out of the room to fling pillows at each other and laugh, laugh irrepressibly, so that even their awe of Fyodor Bogdanitch could not check the effervescing, overbrimming sense of life and happiness. “And now that bent, hollow chest ... and I, not knowing what will become of me, or wherefore....”\n\n“K...ha! K...ha! Damnation! Why do you keep fidgeting, why don’t you go to sleep?” his brother’s voice called to him.\n\n“Oh, I don’t know, I’m not sleepy.”\n\n“I have had a good sleep, I’m not in a sweat now. Just see, feel my shirt; it’s not wet, is it?”\n\nLevin felt, withdrew behind the screen, and put out the candle, but for a long while he could not sleep. The question how to live had hardly begun to grow a little clearer to him, when a new, insoluble question presented itself—death.\n\n“Why, he’s dying—yes, he’ll die in the spring, and how help him? What can I say to him? What do I know about it? I’d even forgotten that it was at all.”\n\n# Chapter 32\n\nLevin had long before made the observation that when one is uncomfortable with people from their being excessively amenable and meek, one is apt very soon after to find things intolerable from their touchiness and irritability. He felt that this was how it would be with his brother. And his brother Nikolay’s gentleness did in fact not last out for long. The very next morning he began to be irritable, and seemed doing his best to find fault with his brother, attacking him on his tenderest points.\n\nLevin felt himself to blame, and could not set things right. He felt that if they had both not kept up appearances, but had spoken, as it is called, from the heart—that is to say, had said only just what they were thinking and feeling—they would simply have looked into each other’s faces, and Konstantin could only have said, “You’re dying, you’re dying!” and Nikolay could only have answered, “I know I’m dying, but I’m afraid, I’m afraid, I’m afraid!” And they could have said nothing more, if they had said only what was in their hearts. But life like that was impossible, and so Konstantin tried to do what he had been trying to do all his life, and never could learn to do, though, as far as he could observe, many people knew so well how to do it, and without it there was no living at all. He tried to say what he was not thinking, but he felt continually that it had a ring of falsehood, that his brother detected him in it, and was exasperated at it.\n\nThe third day Nikolay induced his brother to explain his plan to him again, and began not merely attacking it, but intentionally confounding it with communism.\n\n“You’ve simply borrowed an idea that’s not your own, but you’ve distorted it, and are trying to apply it where it’s not applicable.”\n\n“But I tell you it’s nothing to do with it. They deny the justice of property, of capital, of inheritance, while I do not deny this chief stimulus.” (Levin felt disgusted himself at using such expressions, but ever since he had been engrossed by his work, he had unconsciously come more and more frequently to use words not Russian.) “All I want is to regulate labor.”\n\n“Which means, you’ve borrowed an idea, stripped it of all that gave it its force, and want to make believe that it’s something new,” said Nikolay, angrily tugging at his necktie.\n\n“But my idea has nothing in common....”\n\n“That, anyway,” said Nikolay Levin, with an ironical smile, his eyes flashing malignantly, “has the charm of—what’s one to call it?—geometrical symmetry, of clearness, of definiteness. It may be a Utopia. But if once one allows the possibility of making of all the past a tabula rasa—no property, no family—then labor would organize itself. But you gain nothing....”\n\n“Why do you mix things up? I’ve never been a communist.”\n\n“But I have, and I consider it’s premature, but rational, and it has a future, just like Christianity in its first ages.”\n\n“All that I maintain is that the labor force ought to be investigated from the point of view of natural science; that is to say, it ought to be studied, its qualities ascertained....”\n\n“But that’s utter waste of time. That force finds a certain form of activity of itself, according to the stage of its development. There have been slaves first everywhere, then metayers; and we have the half-crop system, rent, and day laborers. What are you trying to find?”\n\nLevin suddenly lost his temper at these words, because at the bottom of his heart he was afraid that it was true—true that he was trying to hold the balance even between communism and the familiar forms, and that this was hardly possible.\n\n“I am trying to find means of working productively for myself and for the laborers. I want to organize....” he answered hotly.\n\n“You don’t want to organize anything; it’s simply just as you’ve been all your life, that you want to be original to pose as not exploiting the peasants simply, but with some idea in view.”\n\n“Oh, all right, that’s what you think—and let me alone!” answered Levin, feeling the muscles of his left cheek twitching uncontrollably.\n\n“You’ve never had, and never have, convictions; all you want is to please your vanity.”\n\n“Oh, very well; then let me alone!”\n\n“And I will let you alone! and it’s high time I did, and go to the devil with you! and I’m very sorry I ever came!”\n\nIn spite of all Levin’s efforts to soothe his brother afterwards, Nikolay would listen to nothing he said, declaring that it was better to part, and Konstantin saw that it simply was that life was unbearable to him.\n\nNikolay was just getting ready to go, when Konstantin went in to him again and begged him, rather unnaturally, to forgive him if he had hurt his feelings in any way.\n\n“Ah, generosity!” said Nikolay, and he smiled. “If you want to be right, I can give you that satisfaction. You’re in the right; but I’m going all the same.”\n\nIt was only just at parting that Nikolay kissed him, and said, looking with sudden strangeness and seriousness at his brother:\n\n“Anyway, don’t remember evil against me, Kostya!” and his voice quivered. These were the only words that had been spoken sincerely between them. Levin knew that those words meant, “You see, and you know, that I’m in a bad way, and maybe we shall not see each other again.” Levin knew this, and the tears gushed from his eyes. He kissed his brother once more, but he could not speak, and knew not what to say.\n\nThree days after his brother’s departure, Levin too set off for his foreign tour. Happening to meet Shtcherbatsky, Kitty’s cousin, in the railway train, Levin greatly astonished him by his depression.\n\n“What’s the matter with you?” Shtcherbatsky asked him.\n\n“Oh, nothing; there’s not much happiness in life.”\n\n“Not much? You come with me to Paris instead of to Mulhausen. You shall see how to be happy.”\n\n“No, I’ve done with it all. It’s time I was dead.”\n\n“Well, that’s a good one!” said Shtcherbatsky, laughing; “why, I’m only just getting ready to begin.”\n\n“Yes, I thought the same not long ago, but now I know I shall soon be dead.”\n\nLevin said what he had genuinely been thinking of late. He saw nothing but death or the advance towards death in everything. But his cherished scheme only engrossed him the more. Life had to be got through somehow till death did come. Darkness had fallen upon everything for him; but just because of this darkness he felt that the one guiding clue in the darkness was his work, and he clutched it and clung to it with all his strength.\n\n# PART FOUR Chapter 1\n\nThe Karenins, husband and wife, continued living in the same house, met every day, but were complete strangers to one another. Alexey Alexandrovitch made it a rule to see his wife every day, so that the servants might have no grounds for suppositions, but avoided dining at home. Vronsky was never at Alexey Alexandrovitch’s house, but Anna saw him away from home, and her husband was aware of it.\n\nThe position was one of misery for all three; and not one of them would have been equal to enduring this position for a single day, if it had not been for the expectation that it would change, that it was merely a temporary painful ordeal which would pass over. Alexey Alexandrovitch hoped that this passion would pass, as everything does pass, that everyone would forget about it, and his name would remain unsullied. Anna, on whom the position depended, and for whom it was more miserable than for anyone, endured it because she not merely hoped, but firmly believed, that it would all very soon be settled and come right. She had not the least idea what would settle the position, but she firmly believed that something would very soon turn up now. Vronsky, against his own will or wishes, followed her lead, hoped too that something, apart from his own action, would be sure to solve all difficulties.\n\nIn the middle of the winter Vronsky spent a very tiresome week. A foreign prince, who had come on a visit to Petersburg, was put under his charge, and he had to show him the sights worth seeing. Vronsky was of distinguished appearance; he possessed, moreover, the art of behaving with respectful dignity, and was used to having to do with such grand personages—that was how he came to be put in charge of the prince. But he felt his duties very irksome. The prince was anxious to miss nothing of which he would be asked at home, had he seen that in Russia? And on his own account he was anxious to enjoy to the utmost all Russian forms of amusement. Vronsky was obliged to be his guide in satisfying both these inclinations. The mornings they spent driving to look at places of interest; the evenings they passed enjoying the national entertainments. The prince rejoiced in health exceptional even among princes. By gymnastics and careful attention to his health he had brought himself to such a point that in spite of his excess in pleasure he looked as fresh as a big glossy green Dutch cucumber. The prince had traveled a great deal, and considered one of the chief advantages of modern facilities of communication was the accessibility of the pleasures of all nations.\n\nHe had been in Spain, and there had indulged in serenades and had made friends with a Spanish girl who played the mandolin. In Switzerland he had killed chamois. In England he had galloped in a red coat over hedges and killed two hundred pheasants for a bet. In Turkey he had got into a harem; in India he had hunted on an elephant, and now in Russia he wished to taste all the specially Russian forms of pleasure.\n\nVronsky, who was, as it were, chief master of the ceremonies to him, was at great pains to arrange all the Russian amusements suggested by various persons to the prince. They had race horses, and Russian pancakes and bear hunts and three-horse sledges, and gypsies and drinking feasts, with the Russian accompaniment of broken crockery. And the prince with surprising ease fell in with the Russian spirit, smashed trays full of crockery, sat with a gypsy girl on his knee, and seemed to be asking—what more, and does the whole Russian spirit consist in just this?\n\nIn reality, of all the Russian entertainments the prince liked best French actresses and ballet dancers and white-seal champagne. Vronsky was used to princes, but, either because he had himself changed of late, or that he was in too close proximity to the prince, that week seemed fearfully wearisome to him. The whole of that week he experienced a sensation such as a man might have set in charge of a dangerous madman, afraid of the madman, and at the same time, from being with him, fearing for his own reason. Vronsky was continually conscious of the necessity of never for a second relaxing the tone of stern official respectfulness, that he might not himself be insulted. The prince’s manner of treating the very people who, to Vronsky’s surprise, were ready to descend to any depths to provide him with Russian amusements, was contemptuous. His criticisms of Russian women, whom he wished to study, more than once made Vronsky crimson with indignation. The chief reason why the prince was so particularly disagreeable to Vronsky was that he could not help seeing himself in him. And what he saw in this mirror did not gratify his self-esteem. He was a very stupid and very self-satisfied and very healthy and very well-washed man, and nothing else. He was a gentleman—that was true, and Vronsky could not deny it. He was equable and not cringing with his superiors, was free and ingratiating in his behavior with his equals, and was contemptuously indulgent with his inferiors. Vronsky was himself the same, and regarded it as a great merit to be so. But for this prince he was an inferior, and his contemptuous and indulgent attitude to him revolted him.\n\n“Brainless beef! can I be like that?” he thought.\n\nBe that as it might, when, on the seventh day, he parted from the prince, who was starting for Moscow, and received his thanks, he was happy to be rid of his uncomfortable position and the unpleasant reflection of himself. He said good-bye to him at the station on their return from a bear hunt, at which they had had a display of Russian prowess kept up all night.\n\n# Chapter 2\n\nWhen he got home, Vronsky found there a note from Anna. She wrote, “I am ill and unhappy. I cannot come out, but I cannot go on longer without seeing you. Come in this evening. Alexey Alexandrovitch goes to the council at seven and will be there till ten.” Thinking for an instant of the strangeness of her bidding him come straight to her, in spite of her husband’s insisting on her not receiving him, he decided to go.\n\nVronsky had that winter got his promotion, was now a colonel, had left the regimental quarters, and was living alone. After having some lunch, he lay down on the sofa immediately, and in five minutes memories of the hideous scenes he had witnessed during the last few days were confused together and joined on to a mental image of Anna and of the peasant who had played an important part in the bear hunt, and Vronsky fell asleep. He waked up in the dark, trembling with horror, and made haste to light a candle. “What was it? What? What was the dreadful thing I dreamed? Yes, yes; I think a little dirty man with a disheveled beard was stooping down doing something, and all of a sudden he began saying some strange words in French. Yes, there was nothing else in the dream,” he said to himself. “But why was it so awful?” He vividly recalled the peasant again and those incomprehensible French words the peasant had uttered, and a chill of horror ran down his spine.\n\n“What nonsense!” thought Vronsky, and glanced at his watch.\n\nIt was half-past eight already. He rang up his servant, dressed in haste, and went out onto the steps, completely forgetting the dream and only worried at being late. As he drove up to the Karenins’ entrance he looked at his watch and saw it was ten minutes to nine. A high, narrow carriage with a pair of grays was standing at the entrance. He recognized Anna’s carriage. “She is coming to me,” thought Vronsky, “and better she should. I don’t like going into that house. But no matter; I can’t hide myself,” he thought, and with that manner peculiar to him from childhood, as of a man who has nothing to be ashamed of, Vronsky got out of his sledge and went to the door. The door opened, and the hall-porter with a rug on his arm called the carriage. Vronsky, though he did not usually notice details, noticed at this moment the amazed expression with which the porter glanced at him. In the very doorway Vronsky almost ran up against Alexey Alexandrovitch. The gas jet threw its full light on the bloodless, sunken face under the black hat and on the white cravat, brilliant against the beaver of the coat. Karenin’s fixed, dull eyes were fastened upon Vronsky’s face. Vronsky bowed, and Alexey Alexandrovitch, chewing his lips, lifted his hand to his hat and went on. Vronsky saw him without looking round get into the carriage, pick up the rug and the opera-glass at the window and disappear. Vronsky went into the hall. His brows were scowling, and his eyes gleamed with a proud and angry light in them.\n\n“What a position!” he thought. “If he would fight, would stand up for his honor, I could act, could express my feelings; but this weakness or baseness.... He puts me in the position of playing false, which I never meant and never mean to do.”\n\nVronsky’s ideas had changed since the day of his conversation with Anna in the Vrede garden. Unconsciously yielding to the weakness of Anna—who had surrendered herself up to him utterly, and simply looked to him to decide her fate, ready to submit to anything—he had long ceased to think that their tie might end as he had thought then. His ambitious plans had retreated into the background again, and feeling that he had got out of that circle of activity in which everything was definite, he had given himself entirely to his passion, and that passion was binding him more and more closely to her.\n\nHe was still in the hall when he caught the sound of her retreating footsteps. He knew she had been expecting him, had listened for him, and was now going back to the drawing-room.\n\n“No,” she cried, on seeing him, and at the first sound of her voice the tears came into her eyes. “No; if things are to go on like this, the end will come much, much too soon.”\n\n“What is it, dear one?”\n\n“What? I’ve been waiting in agony for an hour, two hours ... No, I won’t ... I can’t quarrel with you. Of course you couldn’t come. No, I won’t.” She laid her two hands on his shoulders, and looked a long while at him with a profound, passionate, and at the same time searching look. She was studying his face to make up for the time she had not seen him. She was, every time she saw him, making the picture of him in her imagination (incomparably superior, impossible in reality) fit with him as he really was.\n\n# Chapter 3\n\n“You met him?” she asked, when they had sat down at the table in the lamplight. “You’re punished, you see, for being late.”\n\n“Yes; but how was it? Wasn’t he to be at the council?”\n\n“He had been and come back, and was going out somewhere again. But that’s no matter. Don’t talk about it. Where have you been? With the prince still?”\n\nShe knew every detail of his existence. He was going to say that he had been up all night and had dropped asleep, but looking at her thrilled and rapturous face, he was ashamed. And he said he had had to go to report on the prince’s departure.\n\n“But it’s over now? He is gone?”\n\n“Thank God it’s over! You wouldn’t believe how insufferable it’s been for me.”\n\n“Why so? Isn’t it the life all of you, all young men, always lead?” she said, knitting her brows; and taking up the crochet work that was lying on the table, she began drawing the hook out of it, without looking at Vronsky.\n\n“I gave that life up long ago,” said he, wondering at the change in her face, and trying to divine its meaning. “And I confess,” he said, with a smile, showing his thick, white teeth, “this week I’ve been, as it were, looking at myself in a glass, seeing that life, and I didn’t like it.”\n\nShe held the work in her hands, but did not crochet, and looked at him with strange, shining, and hostile eyes.\n\n“This morning Liza came to see me—they’re not afraid to call on me, in spite of the Countess Lidia Ivanovna,” she put in—“and she told me about your Athenian evening. How loathsome!”\n\n“I was just going to say....”\n\nShe interrupted him. “It was that Thérèse you used to know?”\n\n“I was just saying....”\n\n“How disgusting you are, you men! How is it you can’t understand that a woman can never forget that,” she said, getting more and more angry, and so letting him see the cause of her irritation, “especially a woman who cannot know your life? What do I know? What have I ever known?” she said, “what you tell me. And how do I know whether you tell me the truth?...”\n\n“Anna, you hurt me. Don’t you trust me? Haven’t I told you that I haven’t a thought I wouldn’t lay bare to you?”\n\n“Yes, yes,” she said, evidently trying to suppress her jealous thoughts. “But if only you knew how wretched I am! I believe you, I believe you.... What were you saying?”\n\nBut he could not at once recall what he had been going to say. These fits of jealousy, which of late had been more and more frequent with her, horrified him, and however much he tried to disguise the fact, made him feel cold to her, although he knew the cause of her jealousy was her love for him. How often he had told himself that her love was happiness; and now she loved him as a woman can love when love has outweighed for her all the good things of life—and he was much further from happiness than when he had followed her from Moscow. Then he had thought himself unhappy, but happiness was before him; now he felt that the best happiness was already left behind. She was utterly unlike what she had been when he first saw her. Both morally and physically she had changed for the worse. She had broadened out all over, and in her face at the time when she was speaking of the actress there was an evil expression of hatred that distorted it. He looked at her as a man looks at a faded flower he has gathered, with difficulty recognizing in it the beauty for which he picked and ruined it. And in spite of this he felt that then, when his love was stronger, he could, if he had greatly wished it, have torn that love out of his heart; but now, when as at that moment it seemed to him he felt no love for her, he knew that what bound him to her could not be broken.\n\n“Well, well, what was it you were going to say about the prince? I have driven away the fiend,” she added. The fiend was the name they had given her jealousy. “What did you begin to tell me about the prince? Why did you find it so tiresome?”\n\n“Oh, it was intolerable!” he said, trying to pick up the thread of his interrupted thought. “He does not improve on closer acquaintance. If you want him defined, here he is: a prime, well-fed beast such as takes medals at the cattle shows, and nothing more,” he said, with a tone of vexation that interested her.\n\n“No; how so?” she replied. “He’s seen a great deal, anyway; he’s cultured?”\n\n“It’s an utterly different culture—their culture. He’s cultivated, one sees, simply to be able to despise culture, as they despise everything but animal pleasures.”\n\n“But don’t you all care for these animal pleasures?” she said, and again he noticed a dark look in her eyes that avoided him.\n\n“How is it you’re defending him?” he said, smiling.\n\n“I’m not defending him, it’s nothing to me; but I imagine, if you had not cared for those pleasures yourself, you might have got out of them. But if it affords you satisfaction to gaze at Thérèse in the attire of Eve....”\n\n“Again, the devil again,” Vronsky said, taking the hand she had laid on the table and kissing it.\n\n“Yes; but I can’t help it. You don’t know what I have suffered waiting for you. I believe I’m not jealous. I’m not jealous: I believe you when you’re here; but when you’re away somewhere leading your life, so incomprehensible to me....”\n\nShe turned away from him, pulled the hook at last out of the crochet work, and rapidly, with the help of her forefinger, began working loop after loop of the wool that was dazzling white in the lamplight, while the slender wrist moved swiftly, nervously in the embroidered cuff.\n\n“How was it, then? Where did you meet Alexey Alexandrovitch?” Her voice sounded in an unnatural and jarring tone.\n\n“We ran up against each other in the doorway.”\n\n“And he bowed to you like this?”\n\nShe drew a long face, and half-closing her eyes, quickly transformed her expression, folded her hands, and Vronsky suddenly saw in her beautiful face the very expression with which Alexey Alexandrovitch had bowed to him. He smiled, while she laughed gaily, with that sweet, deep laugh, which was one of her greatest charms.\n\n“I don’t understand him in the least,” said Vronsky. “If after your avowal to him at your country house he had broken with you, if he had called me out—but this I can’t understand. How can he put up with such a position? He feels it, that’s evident.”\n\n“He?” she said sneeringly. “He’s perfectly satisfied.”\n\n“What are we all miserable for, when everything might be so happy?”\n\n“Only not he. Don’t I know him, the falsity in which he’s utterly steeped?... Could one, with any feeling, live as he is living with me? He understands nothing, and feels nothing. Could a man of any feeling live in the same house with his unfaithful wife? Could he talk to her, call her ‘my dear’?”\n\nAnd again she could not help mimicking him: “‘Anna, ma chère; Anna, dear!’”\n\n“He’s not a man, not a human being—he’s a doll! No one knows him; but I know him. Oh, if I’d been in his place, I’d long ago have killed, have torn to pieces a wife like me. I wouldn’t have said, ‘Anna, ma chère’! He’s not a man, he’s an official machine. He doesn’t understand that I’m your wife, that he’s outside, that he’s superfluous.... Don’t let’s talk of him!...”\n\n“You’re unfair, very unfair, dearest,” said Vronsky, trying to soothe her. “But never mind, don’t let’s talk of him. Tell me what you’ve been doing? What is the matter? What has been wrong with you, and what did the doctor say?”\n\nShe looked at him with mocking amusement. Evidently she had hit on other absurd and grotesque aspects in her husband and was awaiting the moment to give expression to them.\n\nBut he went on:\n\n“I imagine that it’s not illness, but your condition. When will it be?”\n\nThe ironical light died away in her eyes, but a different smile, a consciousness of something, he did not know what, and of quiet melancholy, came over her face.\n\n“Soon, soon. You say that our position is miserable, that we must put an end to it. If you knew how terrible it is to me, what I would give to be able to love you freely and boldly! I should not torture myself and torture you with my jealousy.... And it will come soon, but not as we expect.”\n\nAnd at the thought of how it would come, she seemed so pitiable to herself that tears came into her eyes, and she could not go on. She laid her hand on his sleeve, dazzling and white with its rings in the lamplight.\n\n“It won’t come as we suppose. I didn’t mean to say this to you, but you’ve made me. Soon, soon, all will be over, and we shall all, all be at peace, and suffer no more.”\n\n“I don’t understand,” he said, understanding her.\n\n“You asked when? Soon. And I shan’t live through it. Don’t interrupt me!” and she made haste to speak. “I know it; I know for certain. I shall die; and I’m very glad I shall die, and release myself and you.”\n\nTears dropped from her eyes; he bent down over her hand and began kissing it, trying to hide his emotion, which, he knew, had no sort of grounds, though he could not control it.\n\n“Yes, it’s better so,” she said, tightly gripping his hand. “That’s the only way, the only way left us.”\n\nHe had recovered himself, and lifted his head.\n\n“How absurd! What absurd nonsense you are talking!”\n\n“No, it’s the truth.”\n\n“What, what’s the truth?”\n\n“That I shall die. I have had a dream.”\n\n“A dream?” repeated Vronsky, and instantly he recalled the peasant of his dream.\n\n“Yes, a dream,” she said. “It’s a long while since I dreamed it. I dreamed that I ran into my bedroom, that I had to get something there, to find out something; you know how it is in dreams,” she said, her eyes wide with horror; “and in the bedroom, in the corner, stood something.”\n\n“Oh, what nonsense! How can you believe....”\n\nBut she would not let him interrupt her. What she was saying was too important to her.\n\n“And the something turned round, and I saw it was a peasant with a disheveled beard, little, and dreadful looking. I wanted to run away, but he bent down over a sack, and was fumbling there with his hands....”\n\nShe showed how he had moved his hands. There was terror in her face. And Vronsky, remembering his dream, felt the same terror filling his soul.\n\n“He was fumbling and kept talking quickly, quickly in French, you know: Il faut le battre, le fer, le broyer, le pétrir.... And in my horror I tried to wake up, and woke up ... but woke up in the dream. And I began asking myself what it meant. And Korney said to me: ‘In childbirth you’ll die, ma’am, you’ll die....’ And I woke up.”\n\n“What nonsense, what nonsense!” said Vronsky; but he felt himself that there was no conviction in his voice.\n\n“But don’t let’s talk of it. Ring the bell, I’ll have tea. And stay a little now; it’s not long I shall....”\n\nBut all at once she stopped. The expression of her face instantaneously changed. Horror and excitement were suddenly replaced by a look of soft, solemn, blissful attention. He could not comprehend the meaning of the change. She was listening to the stirring of the new life within her.\n\n# Chapter 4\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch, after meeting Vronsky on his own steps, drove, as he had intended, to the Italian opera. He sat through two acts there, and saw everyone he had wanted to see. On returning home, he carefully scrutinized the hat stand, and noticing that there was not a military overcoat there, he went, as usual, to his own room. But, contrary to his usual habit, he did not go to bed, he walked up and down his study till three o’clock in the morning. The feeling of furious anger with his wife, who would not observe the proprieties and keep to the one stipulation he had laid on her, not to receive her lover in her own home, gave him no peace. She had not complied with his request, and he was bound to punish her and carry out his threat—obtain a divorce and take away his son. He knew all the difficulties connected with this course, but he had said he would do it, and now he must carry out his threat. Countess Lidia Ivanovna had hinted that this was the best way out of his position, and of late the obtaining of divorces had been brought to such perfection that Alexey Alexandrovitch saw a possibility of overcoming the formal difficulties. Misfortunes never come singly, and the affairs of the reorganization of the native tribes, and of the irrigation of the lands of the Zaraisky province, had brought such official worries upon Alexey Alexandrovitch that he had been of late in a continual condition of extreme irritability.\n\nHe did not sleep the whole night, and his fury, growing in a sort of vast, arithmetical progression, reached its highest limits in the morning. He dressed in haste, and as though carrying his cup full of wrath, and fearing to spill any over, fearing to lose with his wrath the energy necessary for the interview with his wife, he went into her room directly he heard she was up.\n\nAnna, who had thought she knew her husband so well, was amazed at his appearance when he went in to her. His brow was lowering, and his eyes stared darkly before him, avoiding her eyes; his mouth was tightly and contemptuously shut. In his walk, in his gestures, in the sound of his voice there was a determination and firmness such as his wife had never seen in him. He went into her room, and without greeting her, walked straight up to her writing-table, and taking her keys, opened a drawer.\n\n“What do you want?” she cried.\n\n“Your lover’s letters,” he said.\n\n“They’re not here,” she said, shutting the drawer; but from that action he saw he had guessed right, and roughly pushing away her hand, he quickly snatched a portfolio in which he knew she used to put her most important papers. She tried to pull the portfolio away, but he pushed her back.\n\n“Sit down! I have to speak to you,” he said, putting the portfolio under his arm, and squeezing it so tightly with his elbow that his shoulder stood up. Amazed and intimidated, she gazed at him in silence.\n\n“I told you that I would not allow you to receive your lover in this house.”\n\n“I had to see him to....”\n\nShe stopped, not finding a reason.\n\n“I do not enter into the details of why a woman wants to see her lover.”\n\n“I meant, I only....” she said, flushing hotly. This coarseness of his angered her, and gave her courage. “Surely you must feel how easy it is for you to insult me?” she said.\n\n“An honest man and an honest woman may be insulted, but to tell a thief he’s a thief is simply la constatation d’un fait.”\n\n“This cruelty is something new I did not know in you.”\n\n“You call it cruelty for a husband to give his wife liberty, giving her the honorable protection of his name, simply on the condition of observing the proprieties: is that cruelty?”\n\n“It’s worse than cruel—it’s base, if you want to know!” Anna cried, in a rush of hatred, and getting up, she was going away.\n\n“No!” he shrieked, in his shrill voice, which pitched a note higher than usual even, and his big hands clutching her by the arm so violently that red marks were left from the bracelet he was squeezing, he forcibly sat her down in her place.\n\n“Base! If you care to use that word, what is base is to forsake husband and child for a lover, while you eat your husband’s bread!”\n\nShe bowed her head. She did not say what she had said the evening before to her lover, that he was her husband, and her husband was superfluous; she did not even think that. She felt all the justice of his words, and only said softly:\n\n“You cannot describe my position as worse than I feel it to be myself; but what are you saying all this for?”\n\n“What am I saying it for? what for?” he went on, as angrily. “That you may know that since you have not carried out my wishes in regard to observing outward decorum, I will take measures to put an end to this state of things.”\n\n“Soon, very soon, it will end, anyway,” she said; and again, at the thought of death near at hand and now desired, tears came into her eyes.\n\n“It will end sooner than you and your lover have planned! If you must have the satisfaction of animal passion....”\n\n“Alexey Alexandrovitch! I won’t say it’s not generous, but it’s not like a gentleman to strike anyone who’s down.”\n\n“Yes, you only think of yourself! But the sufferings of a man who was your husband have no interest for you. You don’t care that his whole life is ruined, that he is thuff ... thuff....”\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch was speaking so quickly that he stammered, and was utterly unable to articulate the word “suffering.” In the end he pronounced it “thuffering.” She wanted to laugh, and was immediately ashamed that anything could amuse her at such a moment. And for the first time, for an instant, she felt for him, put herself in his place, and was sorry for him. But what could she say or do? Her head sank, and she sat silent. He too was silent for some time, and then began speaking in a frigid, less shrill voice, emphasizing random words that had no special significance.\n\n“I came to tell you....” he said.\n\nShe glanced at him. “No, it was my fancy,” she thought, recalling the expression of his face when he stumbled over the word “suffering.” “No; can a man with those dull eyes, with that self-satisfied complacency, feel anything?”\n\n“I cannot change anything,” she whispered.\n\n“I have come to tell you that I am going tomorrow to Moscow, and shall not return again to this house, and you will receive notice of what I decide through the lawyer into whose hands I shall intrust the task of getting a divorce. My son is going to my sister’s,” said Alexey Alexandrovitch, with an effort recalling what he had meant to say about his son.\n\n“You take Seryozha to hurt me,” she said, looking at him from under her brows. “You do not love him.... Leave me Seryozha!”\n\n“Yes, I have lost even my affection for my son, because he is associated with the repulsion I feel for you. But still I shall take him. Good-bye!”\n\nAnd he was going away, but now she detained him.\n\n“Alexey Alexandrovitch, leave me Seryozha!” she whispered once more. “I have nothing else to say. Leave Seryozha till my ... I shall soon be confined; leave him!”\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch flew into a rage, and, snatching his hand from her, he went out of the room without a word.\n\n# Chapter 5\n\nThe waiting-room of the celebrated Petersburg lawyer was full when Alexey Alexandrovitch entered it. Three ladies—an old lady, a young lady, and a merchant’s wife—and three gentlemen—one a German banker with a ring on his finger, the second a merchant with a beard, and the third a wrathful-looking government clerk in official uniform, with a cross on his neck—had obviously been waiting a long while already. Two clerks were writing at tables with scratching pens. The appurtenances of the writing-tables, about which Alexey Alexandrovitch was himself very fastidious, were exceptionally good. He could not help observing this. One of the clerks, without getting up, turned wrathfully to Alexey Alexandrovitch, half closing his eyes. “What are you wanting?”\n\nHe replied that he had to see the lawyer on some business.\n\n“He is engaged,” the clerk responded severely, and he pointed with his pen at the persons waiting, and went on writing.\n\n“Can’t he spare time to see me?” said Alexey Alexandrovitch.\n\n“He has no time free; he is always busy. Kindly wait your turn.”\n\n“Then I must trouble you to give him my card,” Alexey Alexandrovitch said with dignity, seeing the impossibility of preserving his incognito.\n\nThe clerk took the card and, obviously not approving of what he read on it, went to the door.\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch was in principle in favor of the publicity of legal proceedings, though for some higher official considerations he disliked the application of the principle in Russia, and disapproved of it, as far as he could disapprove of anything instituted by authority of the Emperor. His whole life had been spent in administrative work, and consequently, when he did not approve of anything, his disapproval was softened by the recognition of the inevitability of mistakes and the possibility of reform in every department. In the new public law courts he disliked the restrictions laid on the lawyers conducting cases. But till then he had had nothing to do with the law courts, and so had disapproved of their publicity simply in theory; now his disapprobation was strengthened by the unpleasant impression made on him in the lawyer’s waiting room.\n\n“Coming immediately,” said the clerk; and two minutes later there did actually appear in the doorway the large figure of an old solicitor who had been consulting with the lawyer himself.\n\nThe lawyer was a little, squat, bald man, with a dark, reddish beard, light-colored long eyebrows, and an overhanging brow. He was attired as though for a wedding, from his cravat to his double watch-chain and varnished boots. His face was clever and manly, but his dress was dandified and in bad taste.\n\n“Pray walk in,” said the lawyer, addressing Alexey Alexandrovitch; and, gloomily ushering Karenin in before him, he closed the door.\n\n“Won’t you sit down?” He indicated an armchair at a writing-table covered with papers. He sat down himself, and, rubbing his little hands with short fingers covered with white hairs, he bent his head on one side. But as soon as he was settled in this position a moth flew over the table. The lawyer, with a swiftness that could never have been expected of him, opened his hands, caught the moth, and resumed his former attitude.\n\n“Before beginning to speak of my business,” said Alexey Alexandrovitch, following the lawyer’s movements with wondering eyes, “I ought to observe that the business about which I have to speak to you is to be strictly private.”\n\nThe lawyer’s overhanging reddish mustaches were parted in a scarcely perceptible smile.\n\n“I should not be a lawyer if I could not keep the secrets confided to me. But if you would like proof....”\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch glanced at his face, and saw that the shrewd, gray eyes were laughing, and seemed to know all about it already.\n\n“You know my name?” Alexey Alexandrovitch resumed.\n\n“I know you and the good”—again he caught a moth—“work you are doing, like every Russian,” said the lawyer, bowing.\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch sighed, plucking up his courage. But having once made up his mind he went on in his shrill voice, without timidity—or hesitation, accentuating here and there a word.\n\n“I have the misfortune,” Alexey Alexandrovitch began, “to have been deceived in my married life, and I desire to break off all relations with my wife by legal means—that is, to be divorced, but to do this so that my son may not remain with his mother.”\n\nThe lawyer’s gray eyes tried not to laugh, but they were dancing with irrepressible glee, and Alexey Alexandrovitch saw that it was not simply the delight of a man who has just got a profitable job: there was triumph and joy, there was a gleam like the malignant gleam he saw in his wife’s eyes.\n\n“You desire my assistance in securing a divorce?”\n\n“Yes, precisely so; but I ought to warn you that I may be wasting your time and attention. I have come simply to consult you as a preliminary step. I want a divorce, but the form in which it is possible is of great consequence to me. It is very possible that if that form does not correspond with my requirements I may give up a legal divorce.”\n\n“Oh, that’s always the case,” said the lawyer, “and that’s always for you to decide.”\n\nHe let his eyes rest on Alexey Alexandrovitch’s feet, feeling that he might offend his client by the sight of his irrepressible amusement. He looked at a moth that flew before his nose, and moved his hands, but did not catch it from regard for Alexey Alexandrovitch’s position.\n\n“Though in their general features our laws on this subject are known to me,” pursued Alexey Alexandrovitch, “I should be glad to have an idea of the forms in which such things are done in practice.”\n\n“You would be glad,” the lawyer, without lifting his eyes, responded, adopting, with a certain satisfaction, the tone of his client’s remarks, “for me to lay before you all the methods by which you could secure what you desire?”\n\nAnd on receiving an assuring nod from Alexey Alexandrovitch, he went on, stealing a glance now and then at Alexey Alexandrovitch’s face, which was growing red in patches.\n\n“Divorce by our laws,” he said, with a slight shade of disapprobation of our laws, “is possible, as you are aware, in the following cases.... Wait a little!” he called to a clerk who put his head in at the door, but he got up all the same, said a few words to him, and sat down again. “... In the following cases: physical defect in the married parties, desertion without communication for five years,” he said, crooking a short finger covered with hair, “adultery” (this word he pronounced with obvious satisfaction), “subdivided as follows” (he continued to crook his fat fingers, though the three cases and their subdivisions could obviously not be classified together): “physical defect of the husband or of the wife, adultery of the husband or of the wife.” As by now all his fingers were used up, he uncrooked all his fingers and went on: “This is the theoretical view; but I imagine you have done me the honor to apply to me in order to learn its application in practice. And therefore, guided by precedents, I must inform you that in practice cases of divorce may all be reduced to the following—there’s no physical defect, I may assume, nor desertion?...”\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch bowed his head in assent.\n\n“—May be reduced to the following: adultery of one of the married parties, and the detection in the fact of the guilty party by mutual agreement, and failing such agreement, accidental detection. It must be admitted that the latter case is rarely met with in practice,” said the lawyer, and stealing a glance at Alexey Alexandrovitch he paused, as a man selling pistols, after enlarging on the advantages of each weapon, might await his customer’s choice. But Alexey Alexandrovitch said nothing, and therefore the lawyer went on: “The most usual and simple, the sensible course, I consider, is adultery by mutual consent. I should not permit myself to express it so, speaking with a man of no education,” he said, “but I imagine that to you this is comprehensible.”\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch was, however, so perturbed that he did not immediately comprehend all the good sense of adultery by mutual consent, and his eyes expressed this uncertainty; but the lawyer promptly came to his assistance.\n\n“People cannot go on living together—here you have a fact. And if both are agreed about it, the details and formalities become a matter of no importance. And at the same time this is the simplest and most certain method.”\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch fully understood now. But he had religious scruples, which hindered the execution of such a plan.\n\n“That is out of the question in the present case,” he said. “Only one alternative is possible: undesigned detection, supported by letters which I have.”\n\nAt the mention of letters the lawyer pursed up his lips, and gave utterance to a thin little compassionate and contemptuous sound.\n\n“Kindly consider,” he began, “cases of that kind are, as you are aware, under ecclesiastical jurisdiction; the reverend fathers are fond of going into the minutest details in cases of that kind,” he said with a smile, which betrayed his sympathy with the reverend fathers’ taste. “Letters may, of course, be a partial confirmation; but detection in the fact there must be of the most direct kind, that is, by eyewitnesses. In fact, if you do me the honor to intrust your confidence to me, you will do well to leave me the choice of the measures to be employed. If one wants the result, one must admit the means.”\n\n“If it is so....” Alexey Alexandrovitch began, suddenly turning white; but at that moment the lawyer rose and again went to the door to speak to the intruding clerk.\n\n“Tell her we don’t haggle over fees!” he said, and returned to Alexey Alexandrovitch.\n\nOn his way back he caught unobserved another moth. “Nice state my rep curtains will be in by the summer!” he thought, frowning.\n\n“And so you were saying?...” he said.\n\n“I will communicate my decision to you by letter,” said Alexey Alexandrovitch, getting up, and he clutched at the table. After standing a moment in silence, he said: “From your words I may consequently conclude that a divorce may be obtained? I would ask you to let me know what are your terms.”\n\n“It may be obtained if you give me complete liberty of action,” said the lawyer, not answering his question. “When can I reckon on receiving information from you?” he asked, moving towards the door, his eyes and his varnished boots shining.\n\n“In a week’s time. Your answer as to whether you will undertake to conduct the case, and on what terms, you will be so good as to communicate to me.”\n\n“Very good.”\n\nThe lawyer bowed respectfully, let his client out of the door, and, left alone, gave himself up to his sense of amusement. He felt so mirthful that, contrary to his rules, he made a reduction in his terms to the haggling lady, and gave up catching moths, finally deciding that next winter he must have the furniture covered with velvet, like Sigonin’s.\n\n# Chapter 6\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch had gained a brilliant victory at the sitting of the Commission of the 17th of August, but in the sequel this victory cut the ground from under his feet. The new commission for the inquiry into the condition of the native tribes in all its branches had been formed and despatched to its destination with an unusual speed and energy inspired by Alexey Alexandrovitch. Within three months a report was presented. The condition of the native tribes was investigated in its political, administrative, economic, ethnographic, material, and religious aspects. To all these questions there were answers admirably stated, and answers admitting no shade of doubt, since they were not a product of human thought, always liable to error, but were all the product of official activity. The answers were all based on official data furnished by governors and heads of churches, and founded on the reports of district magistrates and ecclesiastical superintendents, founded in their turn on the reports of parochial overseers and parish priests; and so all of these answers were unhesitating and certain. All such questions as, for instance, of the cause of failure of crops, of the adherence of certain tribes to their ancient beliefs, etc.—questions which, but for the convenient intervention of the official machine, are not, and cannot be solved for ages—received full, unhesitating solution. And this solution was in favor of Alexey Alexandrovitch’s contention. But Stremov, who had felt stung to the quick at the last sitting, had, on the reception of the commission’s report, resorted to tactics which Alexey Alexandrovitch had not anticipated. Stremov, carrying with him several members, went over to Alexey Alexandrovitch’s side, and not contenting himself with warmly defending the measure proposed by Karenin, proposed other more extreme measures in the same direction. These measures, still further exaggerated in opposition to what was Alexey Alexandrovitch’s fundamental idea, were passed by the commission, and then the aim of Stremov’s tactics became apparent. Carried to an extreme, the measures seemed at once to be so absurd that the highest authorities, and public opinion, and intellectual ladies, and the newspapers, all at the same time fell foul of them, expressing their indignation both with the measures and their nominal father, Alexey Alexandrovitch. Stremov drew back, affecting to have blindly followed Karenin, and to be astounded and distressed at what had been done. This meant the defeat of Alexey Alexandrovitch. But in spite of failing health, in spite of his domestic griefs, he did not give in. There was a split in the commission. Some members, with Stremov at their head, justified their mistake on the ground that they had put faith in the commission of revision, instituted by Alexey Alexandrovitch, and maintained that the report of the commission was rubbish, and simply so much waste paper. Alexey Alexandrovitch, with a following of those who saw the danger of so revolutionary an attitude to official documents, persisted in upholding the statements obtained by the revising commission. In consequence of this, in the higher spheres, and even in society, all was chaos, and although everyone was interested, no one could tell whether the native tribes really were becoming impoverished and ruined, or whether they were in a flourishing condition. The position of Alexey Alexandrovitch, owing to this, and partly owing to the contempt lavished on him for his wife’s infidelity, became very precarious. And in this position he took an important resolution. To the astonishment of the commission, he announced that he should ask permission to go himself to investigate the question on the spot. And having obtained permission, Alexey Alexandrovitch prepared to set off to these remote provinces.\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch’s departure made a great sensation, the more so as just before he started he officially returned the posting-fares allowed him for twelve horses, to drive to his destination.\n\n“I think it very noble,” Betsy said about this to the Princess Myakaya. “Why take money for posting-horses when everyone knows that there are railways everywhere now?”\n\nBut Princess Myakaya did not agree, and the Princess Tverskaya’s opinion annoyed her indeed.\n\n“It’s all very well for you to talk,” said she, “when you have I don’t know how many millions; but I am very glad when my husband goes on a revising tour in the summer. It’s very good for him and pleasant traveling about, and it’s a settled arrangement for me to keep a carriage and coachman on the money.”\n\nOn his way to the remote provinces Alexey Alexandrovitch stopped for three days at Moscow.\n\nThe day after his arrival he was driving back from calling on the governor-general. At the crossroads by Gazetoy Place, where there are always crowds of carriages and sledges, Alexey Alexandrovitch suddenly heard his name called out in such a loud and cheerful voice that he could not help looking round. At the corner of the pavement, in a short, stylish overcoat and a low-crowned fashionable hat, jauntily askew, with a smile that showed a gleam of white teeth and red lips, stood Stepan Arkadyevitch, radiant, young, and beaming. He called him vigorously and urgently, and insisted on his stopping. He had one arm on the window of a carriage that was stopping at the corner, and out of the window were thrust the heads of a lady in a velvet hat, and two children. Stepan Arkadyevitch was smiling and beckoning to his brother-in-law. The lady smiled a kindly smile too, and she too waved her hand to Alexey Alexandrovitch. It was Dolly with her children.\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch did not want to see anyone in Moscow, and least of all his wife’s brother. He raised his hat and would have driven on, but Stepan Arkadyevitch told his coachman to stop, and ran across the snow to him.\n\n“Well, what a shame not to have let us know! Been here long? I was at Dussots’ yesterday and saw ‘Karenin’ on the visitors’ list, but it never entered my head that it was you,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, sticking his head in at the window of the carriage, “or I should have looked you up. I am glad to see you!” he said, knocking one foot against the other to shake the snow off. “What a shame of you not to let us know!” he repeated.\n\n“I had no time; I am very busy,” Alexey Alexandrovitch responded dryly.\n\n“Come to my wife, she does so want to see you.”\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch unfolded the rug in which his frozen feet were wrapped, and getting out of his carriage made his way over the snow to Darya Alexandrovna.\n\n“Why, Alexey Alexandrovitch, what are you cutting us like this for?” said Dolly, smiling.\n\n“I was very busy. Delighted to see you!” he said in a tone clearly indicating that he was annoyed by it. “How are you?”\n\n“Tell me, how is my darling Anna?”\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch mumbled something and would have gone on. But Stepan Arkadyevitch stopped him.\n\n“I tell you what we’ll do tomorrow. Dolly, ask him to dinner. We’ll ask Koznishev and Pestsov, so as to entertain him with our Moscow celebrities.”\n\n“Yes, please, do come,” said Dolly; “we will expect you at five, or six o’clock, if you like. How is my darling Anna? How long....”\n\n“She is quite well,” Alexey Alexandrovitch mumbled, frowning. “Delighted!” and he moved away towards his carriage.\n\n“You will come?” Dolly called after him.\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch said something which Dolly could not catch in the noise of the moving carriages.\n\n“I shall come round tomorrow!” Stepan Arkadyevitch shouted to him.\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch got into his carriage, and buried himself in it so as neither to see nor be seen.\n\n“Queer fish!” said Stepan Arkadyevitch to his wife, and glancing at his watch, he made a motion of his hand before his face, indicating a caress to his wife and children, and walked jauntily along the pavement.\n\n“Stiva! Stiva!” Dolly called, reddening.\n\nHe turned round.\n\n“I must get coats, you know, for Grisha and Tanya. Give me the money.”\n\n“Never mind; you tell them I’ll pay the bill!” and he vanished, nodding genially to an acquaintance who drove by.\n\n# Chapter 7\n\nThe next day was Sunday. Stepan Arkadyevitch went to the Grand Theater to a rehearsal of the ballet, and gave Masha Tchibisova, a pretty dancing-girl whom he had just taken under his protection, the coral necklace he had promised her the evening before, and behind the scenes in the dim daylight of the theater, managed to kiss her pretty little face, radiant over her present. Besides the gift of the necklace he wanted to arrange with her about meeting after the ballet. After explaining that he could not come at the beginning of the ballet, he promised he would come for the last act and take her to supper. From the theater Stepan Arkadyevitch drove to Ohotny Row, selected himself the fish and asparagus for dinner, and by twelve o’clock was at Dussots’, where he had to see three people, luckily all staying at the same hotel: Levin, who had recently come back from abroad and was staying there; the new head of his department, who had just been promoted to that position, and had come on a tour of revision to Moscow; and his brother-in-law, Karenin, whom he must see, so as to be sure of bringing him to dinner.\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch liked dining, but still better he liked to give a dinner, small, but very choice, both as regards the food and drink and as regards the selection of guests. He particularly liked the program of that day’s dinner. There would be fresh perch, asparagus, and la pièce de resistance—first-rate, but quite plain, roast beef, and wines to suit: so much for the eating and drinking. Kitty and Levin would be of the party, and that this might not be obtrusively evident, there would be a girl cousin too, and young Shtcherbatsky, and la pièce de resistance among the guests—Sergey Koznishev and Alexey Alexandrovitch. Sergey Ivanovitch was a Moscow man, and a philosopher; Alexey Alexandrovitch a Petersburger, and a practical politician. He was asking, too, the well-known eccentric enthusiast, Pestsov, a liberal, a great talker, a musician, an historian, and the most delightfully youthful person of fifty, who would be a sauce or garnish for Koznishev and Karenin. He would provoke them and set them off.\n\nThe second installment for the forest had been received from the merchant and was not yet exhausted; Dolly had been very amiable and good-humored of late, and the idea of the dinner pleased Stepan Arkadyevitch from every point of view. He was in the most light-hearted mood. There were two circumstances a little unpleasant, but these two circumstances were drowned in the sea of good-humored gaiety which flooded the soul of Stepan Arkadyevitch. These two circumstances were: first, that on meeting Alexey Alexandrovitch the day before in the street he had noticed that he was cold and reserved with him, and putting the expression of Alexey Alexandrovitch’s face and the fact that he had not come to see them or let them know of his arrival with the rumors he had heard about Anna and Vronsky, Stepan Arkadyevitch guessed that something was wrong between the husband and wife.\n\nThat was one disagreeable thing. The other slightly disagreeable fact was that the new head of his department, like all new heads, had the reputation already of a terrible person, who got up at six o’clock in the morning, worked like a horse, and insisted on his subordinates working in the same way. Moreover, this new head had the further reputation of being a bear in his manners, and was, according to all reports, a man of a class in all respects the opposite of that to which his predecessor had belonged, and to which Stepan Arkadyevitch had hitherto belonged himself. On the previous day Stepan Arkadyevitch had appeared at the office in a uniform, and the new chief had been very affable and had talked to him as to an acquaintance. Consequently Stepan Arkadyevitch deemed it his duty to call upon him in his non-official dress. The thought that the new chief might not tender him a warm reception was the other unpleasant thing. But Stepan Arkadyevitch instinctively felt that everything would come round all right. “They’re all people, all men, like us poor sinners; why be nasty and quarrelsome?” he thought as he went into the hotel.\n\n“Good-day, Vassily,” he said, walking into the corridor with his hat cocked on one side, and addressing a footman he knew; “why, you’ve let your whiskers grow! Levin, number seven, eh? Take me up, please. And find out whether Count Anitchkin” (this was the new head) “is receiving.”\n\n“Yes, sir,” Vassily responded, smiling. “You’ve not been to see us for a long while.”\n\n“I was here yesterday, but at the other entrance. Is this number seven?”\n\nLevin was standing with a peasant from Tver in the middle of the room, measuring a fresh bearskin, when Stepan Arkadyevitch went in.\n\n“What! you killed him?” cried Stepan Arkadyevitch. “Well done! A she-bear? How are you, Arhip!”\n\nHe shook hands with the peasant and sat down on the edge of a chair, without taking off his coat and hat.\n\n“Come, take off your coat and stay a little,” said Levin, taking his hat.\n\n“No, I haven’t time; I’ve only looked in for a tiny second,” answered Stepan Arkadyevitch. He threw open his coat, but afterwards did take it off, and sat on for a whole hour, talking to Levin about hunting and the most intimate subjects.\n\n“Come, tell me, please, what you did abroad? Where have you been?” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, when the peasant had gone.\n\n“Oh, I stayed in Germany, in Prussia, in France, and in England—not in the capitals, but in the manufacturing towns, and saw a great deal that was new to me. And I’m glad I went.”\n\n“Yes, I knew your idea of the solution of the labor question.”\n\n“Not a bit: in Russia there can be no labor question. In Russia the question is that of the relation of the working people to the land; though the question exists there too—but there it’s a matter of repairing what’s been ruined, while with us....”\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch listened attentively to Levin.\n\n“Yes, yes!” he said, “it’s very possible you’re right. But I’m glad you’re in good spirits, and are hunting bears, and working, and interested. Shtcherbatsky told me another story—he met you—that you were in such a depressed state, talking of nothing but death....”\n\n“Well, what of it? I’ve not given up thinking of death,” said Levin. “It’s true that it’s high time I was dead; and that all this is nonsense. It’s the truth I’m telling you. I do value my idea and my work awfully; but in reality only consider this: all this world of ours is nothing but a speck of mildew, which has grown up on a tiny planet. And for us to suppose we can have something great—ideas, work—it’s all dust and ashes.”\n\n“But all that’s as old as the hills, my boy!”\n\n“It is old; but do you know, when you grasp this fully, then somehow everything becomes of no consequence. When you understand that you will die tomorrow, if not today, and nothing will be left, then everything is so unimportant! And I consider my idea very important, but it turns out really to be as unimportant too, even if it were carried out, as doing for that bear. So one goes on living, amusing oneself with hunting, with work—anything so as not to think of death!”\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch smiled a subtle affectionate smile as he listened to Levin.\n\n“Well, of course! Here you’ve come round to my point. Do you remember you attacked me for seeking enjoyment in life? Don’t be so severe, O moralist!”\n\n“No; all the same, what’s fine in life is....” Levin hesitated—“oh, I don’t know. All I know is that we shall soon be dead.”\n\n“Why so soon?”\n\n“And do you know, there’s less charm in life, when one thinks of death, but there’s more peace.”\n\n“On the contrary, the finish is always the best. But I must be going,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, getting up for the tenth time.\n\n“Oh, no, stay a bit!” said Levin, keeping him. “Now, when shall we see each other again? I’m going tomorrow.”\n\n“I’m a nice person! Why, that’s just what I came for! You simply must come to dinner with us today. Your brother’s coming, and Karenin, my brother-in-law.”\n\n“You don’t mean to say he’s here?” said Levin, and he wanted to inquire about Kitty. He had heard at the beginning of the winter that she was at Petersburg with her sister, the wife of the diplomat, and he did not know whether she had come back or not; but he changed his mind and did not ask. “Whether she’s coming or not, I don’t care,” he said to himself.\n\n“So you’ll come?”\n\n“Of course.”\n\n“At five o’clock, then, and not evening dress.”\n\nAnd Stepan Arkadyevitch got up and went down below to the new head of his department. Instinct had not misled Stepan Arkadyevitch. The terrible new head turned out to be an extremely amenable person, and Stepan Arkadyevitch lunched with him and stayed on, so that it was four o’clock before he got to Alexey Alexandrovitch.\n\n# Chapter 8\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch, on coming back from church service, had spent the whole morning indoors. He had two pieces of business before him that morning; first, to receive and send on a deputation from the native tribes which was on its way to Petersburg, and now at Moscow; secondly, to write the promised letter to the lawyer. The deputation, though it had been summoned at Alexey Alexandrovitch’s instigation, was not without its discomforting and even dangerous aspect, and he was glad he had found it in Moscow. The members of this deputation had not the slightest conception of their duty and the part they were to play. They naïvely believed that it was their business to lay before the commission their needs and the actual condition of things, and to ask assistance of the government, and utterly failed to grasp that some of their statements and requests supported the contention of the enemy’s side, and so spoiled the whole business. Alexey Alexandrovitch was busily engaged with them for a long while, drew up a program for them from which they were not to depart, and on dismissing them wrote a letter to Petersburg for the guidance of the deputation. He had his chief support in this affair in the Countess Lidia Ivanovna. She was a specialist in the matter of deputations, and no one knew better than she how to manage them, and put them in the way they should go. Having completed this task, Alexey Alexandrovitch wrote the letter to the lawyer. Without the slightest hesitation he gave him permission to act as he might judge best. In the letter he enclosed three of Vronsky’s notes to Anna, which were in the portfolio he had taken away.\n\nSince Alexey Alexandrovitch had left home with the intention of not returning to his family again, and since he had been at the lawyer’s and had spoken, though only to one man, of his intention, since especially he had translated the matter from the world of real life to the world of ink and paper, he had grown more and more used to his own intention, and by now distinctly perceived the feasibility of its execution.\n\nHe was sealing the envelope to the lawyer, when he heard the loud tones of Stepan Arkadyevitch’s voice. Stepan Arkadyevitch was disputing with Alexey Alexandrovitch’s servant, and insisting on being announced.\n\n“No matter,” thought Alexey Alexandrovitch, “so much the better. I will inform him at once of my position in regard to his sister, and explain why it is I can’t dine with him.”\n\n“Come in!” he said aloud, collecting his papers, and putting them in the blotting-paper.\n\n“There, you see, you’re talking nonsense, and he’s at home!” responded Stepan Arkadyevitch’s voice, addressing the servant, who had refused to let him in, and taking off his coat as he went, Oblonsky walked into the room. “Well, I’m awfully glad I’ve found you! So I hope....” Stepan Arkadyevitch began cheerfully.\n\n“I cannot come,” Alexey Alexandrovitch said coldly, standing and not asking his visitor to sit down.\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch had thought to pass at once into those frigid relations in which he ought to stand with the brother of a wife against whom he was beginning a suit for divorce. But he had not taken into account the ocean of kindliness brimming over in the heart of Stepan Arkadyevitch.\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch opened wide his clear, shining eyes.\n\n“Why can’t you? What do you mean?” he asked in perplexity, speaking in French. “Oh, but it’s a promise. And we’re all counting on you.”\n\n“I want to tell you that I can’t dine at your house, because the terms of relationship which have existed between us must cease.”\n\n“How? How do you mean? What for?” said Stepan Arkadyevitch with a smile.\n\n“Because I am beginning an action for divorce against your sister, my wife. I ought to have....”\n\nBut, before Alexey Alexandrovitch had time to finish his sentence, Stepan Arkadyevitch was behaving not at all as he had expected. He groaned and sank into an armchair.\n\n“No, Alexey Alexandrovitch! What are you saying?” cried Oblonsky, and his suffering was apparent in his face.\n\n“It is so.”\n\n“Excuse me, I can’t, I can’t believe it!”\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch sat down, feeling that his words had not had the effect he anticipated, and that it would be unavoidable for him to explain his position, and that, whatever explanations he might make, his relations with his brother-in-law would remain unchanged.\n\n“Yes, I am brought to the painful necessity of seeking a divorce,” he said.\n\n“I will say one thing, Alexey Alexandrovitch. I know you for an excellent, upright man; I know Anna—excuse me, I can’t change my opinion of her—for a good, an excellent woman; and so, excuse me, I cannot believe it. There is some misunderstanding,” said he.\n\n“Oh, if it were merely a misunderstanding!...”\n\n“Pardon, I understand,” interposed Stepan Arkadyevitch. “But of course.... One thing: you must not act in haste. You must not, you must not act in haste!”\n\n“I am not acting in haste,” Alexey Alexandrovitch said coldly, “but one cannot ask advice of anyone in such a matter. I have quite made up my mind.”\n\n“This is awful!” said Stepan Arkadyevitch. “I would do one thing, Alexey Alexandrovitch. I beseech you, do it!” he said. “No action has yet been taken, if I understand rightly. Before you take advice, see my wife, talk to her. She loves Anna like a sister, she loves you, and she’s a wonderful woman. For God’s sake, talk to her! Do me that favor, I beseech you!”\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch pondered, and Stepan Arkadyevitch looked at him sympathetically, without interrupting his silence.\n\n“You will go to see her?”\n\n“I don’t know. That was just why I have not been to see you. I imagine our relations must change.”\n\n“Why so? I don’t see that. Allow me to believe that apart from our connection you have for me, at least in part, the same friendly feeling I have always had for you ... and sincere esteem,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, pressing his hand. “Even if your worst suppositions were correct, I don’t—and never would—take on myself to judge either side, and I see no reason why our relations should be affected. But now, do this, come and see my wife.”\n\n“Well, we look at the matter differently,” said Alexey Alexandrovitch coldly. “However, we won’t discuss it.”\n\n“No; why shouldn’t you come today to dine, anyway? My wife’s expecting you. Please, do come. And, above all, talk it over with her. She’s a wonderful woman. For God’s sake, on my knees, I implore you!”\n\n“If you so much wish it, I will come,” said Alexey Alexandrovitch, sighing.\n\nAnd, anxious to change the conversation, he inquired about what interested them both—the new head of Stepan Arkadyevitch’s department, a man not yet old, who had suddenly been promoted to so high a position.\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch had previously felt no liking for Count Anitchkin, and had always differed from him in his opinions. But now, from a feeling readily comprehensible to officials—that hatred felt by one who has suffered a defeat in the service for one who has received a promotion, he could not endure him.\n\n“Well, have you seen him?” said Alexey Alexandrovitch with a malignant smile.\n\n“Of course; he was at our sitting yesterday. He seems to know his work capitally, and to be very energetic.”\n\n“Yes, but what is his energy directed to?” said Alexey Alexandrovitch. “Is he aiming at doing anything, or simply undoing what’s been done? It’s the great misfortune of our government—this paper administration, of which he’s a worthy representative.”\n\n“Really, I don’t know what fault one could find with him. His policy I don’t know, but one thing—he’s a very nice fellow,” answered Stepan Arkadyevitch. “I’ve just been seeing him, and he’s really a capital fellow. We lunched together, and I taught him how to make, you know that drink, wine and oranges. It’s so cooling. And it’s a wonder he didn’t know it. He liked it awfully. No, really he’s a capital fellow.”\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch glanced at his watch.\n\n“Why, good heavens, it’s four already, and I’ve still to go to Dolgovushin’s! So please come round to dinner. You can’t imagine how you will grieve my wife and me.”\n\nThe way in which Alexey Alexandrovitch saw his brother-in-law out was very different from the manner in which he had met him.\n\n“I’ve promised, and I’ll come,” he answered wearily.\n\n“Believe me, I appreciate it, and I hope you won’t regret it,” answered Stepan Arkadyevitch, smiling.\n\nAnd, putting on his coat as he went, he patted the footman on the head, chuckled, and went out.\n\n“At five o’clock, and not evening dress, please,” he shouted once more, turning at the door.\n\n# Chapter 9\n\nIt was past five, and several guests had already arrived, before the host himself got home. He went in together with Sergey Ivanovitch Koznishev and Pestsov, who had reached the street door at the same moment. These were the two leading representatives of the Moscow intellectuals, as Oblonsky had called them. Both were men respected for their character and their intelligence. They respected each other, but were in complete and hopeless disagreement upon almost every subject, not because they belonged to opposite parties, but precisely because they were of the same party (their enemies refused to see any distinction between their views); but, in that party, each had his own special shade of opinion. And since no difference is less easily overcome than the difference of opinion about semi-abstract questions, they never agreed in any opinion, and had long, indeed, been accustomed to jeer without anger, each at the other’s incorrigible aberrations.\n\nThey were just going in at the door, talking of the weather, when Stepan Arkadyevitch overtook them. In the drawing-room there were already sitting Prince Alexander Dmitrievitch Shtcherbatsky, young Shtcherbatsky, Turovtsin, Kitty, and Karenin.\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch saw immediately that things were not going well in the drawing-room without him. Darya Alexandrovna, in her best gray silk gown, obviously worried about the children, who were to have their dinner by themselves in the nursery, and by her husband’s absence, was not equal to the task of making the party mix without him. All were sitting like so many priests’ wives on a visit (so the old prince expressed it), obviously wondering why they were there, and pumping up remarks simply to avoid being silent. Turovtsin—good, simple man—felt unmistakably a fish out of water, and the smile with which his thick lips greeted Stepan Arkadyevitch said, as plainly as words: “Well, old boy, you have popped me down in a learned set! A drinking party now, or the Château des Fleurs, would be more in my line!” The old prince sat in silence, his bright little eyes watching Karenin from one side, and Stepan Arkadyevitch saw that he had already formed a phrase to sum up that politician of whom guests were invited to partake as though he were a sturgeon. Kitty was looking at the door, calling up all her energies to keep her from blushing at the entrance of Konstantin Levin. Young Shtcherbatsky, who had not been introduced to Karenin, was trying to look as though he were not in the least conscious of it. Karenin himself had followed the Petersburg fashion for a dinner with ladies and was wearing evening dress and a white tie. Stepan Arkadyevitch saw by his face that he had come simply to keep his promise, and was performing a disagreeable duty in being present at this gathering. He was indeed the person chiefly responsible for the chill benumbing all the guests before Stepan Arkadyevitch came in.\n\nOn entering the drawing-room Stepan Arkadyevitch apologized, explaining that he had been detained by that prince, who was always the scapegoat for all his absences and unpunctualities, and in one moment he had made all the guests acquainted with each other, and, bringing together Alexey Alexandrovitch and Sergey Koznishev, started them on a discussion of the Russification of Poland, into which they immediately plunged with Pestsov. Slapping Turovtsin on the shoulder, he whispered something comic in his ear, and set him down by his wife and the old prince. Then he told Kitty she was looking very pretty that evening, and presented Shtcherbatsky to Karenin. In a moment he had so kneaded together the social dough that the drawing-room became very lively, and there was a merry buzz of voices. Konstantin Levin was the only person who had not arrived. But this was so much the better, as going into the dining-room, Stepan Arkadyevitch found to his horror that the port and sherry had been procured from Depré, and not from Levy, and, directing that the coachman should be sent off as speedily as possible to Levy’s, he was going back to the drawing-room.\n\nIn the dining-room he was met by Konstantin Levin.\n\n“I’m not late?”\n\n“You can never help being late!” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, taking his arm.\n\n“Have you a lot of people? Who’s here?” asked Levin, unable to help blushing, as he knocked the snow off his cap with his glove.\n\n“All our own set. Kitty’s here. Come along, I’ll introduce you to Karenin.”\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch, for all his liberal views, was well aware that to meet Karenin was sure to be felt a flattering distinction, and so treated his best friends to this honor. But at that instant Konstantin Levin was not in a condition to feel all the gratification of making such an acquaintance. He had not seen Kitty since that memorable evening when he met Vronsky, not counting, that is, the moment when he had had a glimpse of her on the highroad. He had known at the bottom of his heart that he would see her here today. But to keep his thoughts free, he had tried to persuade himself that he did not know it. Now when he heard that she was here, he was suddenly conscious of such delight, and at the same time of such dread, that his breath failed him and he could not utter what he wanted to say.\n\n“What is she like, what is she like? Like what she used to be, or like what she was in the carriage? What if Darya Alexandrovna told the truth? Why shouldn’t it be the truth?” he thought.\n\n“Oh, please, introduce me to Karenin,” he brought out with an effort, and with a desperately determined step he walked into the drawing-room and beheld her.\n\nShe was not the same as she used to be, nor was she as she had been in the carriage; she was quite different.\n\nShe was scared, shy, shame-faced, and still more charming from it. She saw him the very instant he walked into the room. She had been expecting him. She was delighted, and so confused at her own delight that there was a moment, the moment when he went up to her sister and glanced again at her, when she, and he, and Dolly, who saw it all, thought she would break down and would begin to cry. She crimsoned, turned white, crimsoned again, and grew faint, waiting with quivering lips for him to come to her. He went up to her, bowed, and held out his hand without speaking. Except for the slight quiver of her lips and the moisture in her eyes that made them brighter, her smile was almost calm as she said:\n\n“How long it is since we’ve seen each other!” and with desperate determination she pressed his hand with her cold hand.\n\n“You’ve not seen me, but I’ve seen you,” said Levin, with a radiant smile of happiness. “I saw you when you were driving from the railway station to Ergushovo.”\n\n“When?” she asked, wondering.\n\n“You were driving to Ergushovo,” said Levin, feeling as if he would sob with the rapture that was flooding his heart. “And how dared I associate a thought of anything not innocent with this touching creature? And, yes, I do believe it’s true what Darya Alexandrovna told me,” he thought.\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch took him by the arm and led him away to Karenin.\n\n“Let me introduce you.” He mentioned their names.\n\n“Very glad to meet you again,” said Alexey Alexandrovitch coldly, shaking hands with Levin.\n\n“You are acquainted?” Stepan Arkadyevitch asked in surprise.\n\n“We spent three hours together in the train,” said Levin smiling, “but got out, just as in a masquerade, quite mystified—at least I was.”\n\n“Nonsense! Come along, please,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, pointing in the direction of the dining-room.\n\nThe men went into the dining-room and went up to a table, laid with six sorts of spirits and as many kinds of cheese, some with little silver spades and some without, caviar, herrings, preserves of various kinds, and plates with slices of French bread.\n\nThe men stood round the strong-smelling spirits and salt delicacies, and the discussion of the Russification of Poland between Koznishev, Karenin, and Pestsov died down in anticipation of dinner.\n\nSergey Ivanovitch was unequaled in his skill in winding up the most heated and serious argument by some unexpected pinch of Attic salt that changed the disposition of his opponent. He did this now.\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch had been maintaining that the Russification of Poland could only be accomplished as a result of larger measures which ought to be introduced by the Russian government.\n\nPestsov insisted that one country can only absorb another when it is the more densely populated.\n\nKoznishev admitted both points, but with limitations. As they were going out of the drawing-room to conclude the argument, Koznishev said, smiling:\n\n“So, then, for the Russification of our foreign populations there is but one method—to bring up as many children as one can. My brother and I are terribly in fault, I see. You married men, especially you, Stepan Arkadyevitch, are the real patriots: what number have you reached?” he said, smiling genially at their host and holding out a tiny wine-glass to him.\n\nEveryone laughed, and Stepan Arkadyevitch with particular good humor.\n\n“Oh, yes, that’s the best method!” he said, munching cheese and filling the wine-glass with a special sort of spirit. The conversation dropped at the jest.\n\n“This cheese is not bad. Shall I give you some?” said the master of the house. “Why, have you been going in for gymnastics again?” he asked Levin, pinching his muscle with his left hand. Levin smiled, bent his arm, and under Stepan Arkadyevitch’s fingers the muscles swelled up like a sound cheese, hard as a knob of iron, through the fine cloth of the coat.\n\n“What biceps! A perfect Samson!”\n\n“I imagine great strength is needed for hunting bears,” observed Alexey Alexandrovitch, who had the mistiest notions about the chase. He cut off and spread with cheese a wafer of bread fine as a spider-web.\n\nLevin smiled.\n\n“Not at all. Quite the contrary; a child can kill a bear,” he said, with a slight bow moving aside for the ladies, who were approaching the table.\n\n“You have killed a bear, I’ve been told!” said Kitty, trying assiduously to catch with her fork a perverse mushroom that would slip away, and setting the lace quivering over her white arm. “Are there bears on your place?” she added, turning her charming little head to him and smiling.\n\nThere was apparently nothing extraordinary in what she said, but what unutterable meaning there was for him in every sound, in every turn of her lips, her eyes, her hand as she said it! There was entreaty for forgiveness, and trust in him, and tenderness—soft, timid tenderness—and promise and hope and love for him, which he could not but believe in and which choked him with happiness.\n\n“No, we’ve been hunting in the Tver province. It was coming back from there that I met your beau-frère in the train, or your beau-frère’s brother-in-law,” he said with a smile. “It was an amusing meeting.”\n\nAnd he began telling with droll good-humor how, after not sleeping all night, he had, wearing an old fur-lined, full-skirted coat, got into Alexey Alexandrovitch’s compartment.\n\n“The conductor, forgetting the proverb, would have chucked me out on account of my attire; but thereupon I began expressing my feelings in elevated language, and ... you, too,” he said, addressing Karenin and forgetting his name, “at first would have ejected me on the ground of the old coat, but afterwards you took my part, for which I am extremely grateful.”\n\n“The rights of passengers generally to choose their seats are too ill-defined,” said Alexey Alexandrovitch, rubbing the tips of his fingers on his handkerchief.\n\n“I saw you were in uncertainty about me,” said Levin, smiling good-naturedly, “but I made haste to plunge into intellectual conversation to smooth over the defects of my attire.” Sergey Ivanovitch, while he kept up a conversation with their hostess, had one ear for his brother, and he glanced askance at him. “What is the matter with him today? Why such a conquering hero?” he thought. He did not know that Levin was feeling as though he had grown wings. Levin knew she was listening to his words and that she was glad to listen to him. And this was the only thing that interested him. Not in that room only, but in the whole world, there existed for him only himself, with enormously increased importance and dignity in his own eyes, and she. He felt himself on a pinnacle that made him giddy, and far away down below were all those nice excellent Karenins, Oblonskys, and all the world.\n\nQuite without attracting notice, without glancing at them, as though there were no other places left, Stepan Arkadyevitch put Levin and Kitty side by side.\n\n“Oh, you may as well sit there,” he said to Levin.\n\nThe dinner was as choice as the china, in which Stepan Arkadyevitch was a connoisseur. The soupe Marie-Louise was a splendid success; the tiny pies eaten with it melted in the mouth and were irreproachable. The two footmen and Matvey, in white cravats, did their duty with the dishes and wines unobtrusively, quietly, and swiftly. On the material side the dinner was a success; it was no less so on the immaterial. The conversation, at times general and at times between individuals, never paused, and towards the end the company was so lively that the men rose from the table, without stopping speaking, and even Alexey Alexandrovitch thawed.\n\n# Chapter 10\n\nPestsov liked thrashing an argument out to the end, and was not satisfied with Sergey Ivanovitch’s words, especially as he felt the injustice of his view.\n\n“I did not mean,” he said over the soup, addressing Alexey Alexandrovitch, “mere density of population alone, but in conjunction with fundamental ideas, and not by means of principles.”\n\n“It seems to me,” Alexey Alexandrovitch said languidly, and with no haste, “that that’s the same thing. In my opinion, influence over another people is only possible to the people which has the higher development, which....”\n\n“But that’s just the question,” Pestsov broke in in his bass. He was always in a hurry to speak, and seemed always to put his whole soul into what he was saying. “In what are we to make higher development consist? The English, the French, the Germans, which is at the highest stage of development? Which of them will nationalize the other? We see the Rhine provinces have been turned French, but the Germans are not at a lower stage!” he shouted. “There is another law at work there.”\n\n“I fancy that the greater influence is always on the side of true civilization,” said Alexey Alexandrovitch, slightly lifting his eyebrows.\n\n“But what are we to lay down as the outward signs of true civilization?” said Pestsov.\n\n“I imagine such signs are generally very well known,” said Alexey Alexandrovitch.\n\n“But are they fully known?” Sergey Ivanovitch put in with a subtle smile. “It is the accepted view now that real culture must be purely classical; but we see most intense disputes on each side of the question, and there is no denying that the opposite camp has strong points in its favor.”\n\n“You are for classics, Sergey Ivanovitch. Will you take red wine?” said Stepan Arkadyevitch.\n\n“I am not expressing my own opinion of either form of culture,” Sergey Ivanovitch said, holding out his glass with a smile of condescension, as to a child. “I only say that both sides have strong arguments to support them,” he went on, addressing Alexey Alexandrovitch. “My sympathies are classical from education, but in this discussion I am personally unable to arrive at a conclusion. I see no distinct grounds for classical studies being given a preeminence over scientific studies.”\n\n“The natural sciences have just as great an educational value,” put in Pestsov. “Take astronomy, take botany, or zoology with its system of general principles.”\n\n“I cannot quite agree with that,” responded Alexey Alexandrovitch. “It seems to me that one must admit that the very process of studying the forms of language has a peculiarly favorable influence on intellectual development. Moreover, it cannot be denied that the influence of the classical authors is in the highest degree moral, while, unfortunately, with the study of the natural sciences are associated the false and noxious doctrines which are the curse of our day.”\n\nSergey Ivanovitch would have said something, but Pestsov interrupted him in his rich bass. He began warmly contesting the justice of this view. Sergey Ivanovitch waited serenely to speak, obviously with a convincing reply ready.\n\n“But,” said Sergey Ivanovitch, smiling subtly, and addressing Karenin, “One must allow that to weigh all the advantages and disadvantages of classical and scientific studies is a difficult task, and the question which form of education was to be preferred would not have been so quickly and conclusively decided if there had not been in favor of classical education, as you expressed it just now, its moral—disons le mot—anti-nihilist influence.”\n\n“Undoubtedly.”\n\n“If it had not been for the distinctive property of anti-nihilistic influence on the side of classical studies, we should have considered the subject more, have weighed the arguments on both sides,” said Sergey Ivanovitch with a subtle smile, “we should have given elbow-room to both tendencies. But now we know that these little pills of classical learning possess the medicinal property of anti-nihilism, and we boldly prescribe them to our patients.... But what if they had no such medicinal property?” he wound up humorously.\n\nAt Sergey Ivanovitch’s little pills, everyone laughed; Turovtsin in especial roared loudly and jovially, glad at last to have found something to laugh at, all he ever looked for in listening to conversation.\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch had not made a mistake in inviting Pestsov. With Pestsov intellectual conversation never flagged for an instant. Directly Sergey Ivanovitch had concluded the conversation with his jest, Pestsov promptly started a new one.\n\n“I can’t agree even,” said he, “that the government had that aim. The government obviously is guided by abstract considerations, and remains indifferent to the influence its measures may exercise. The education of women, for instance, would naturally be regarded as likely to be harmful, but the government opens schools and universities for women.”\n\nAnd the conversation at once passed to the new subject of the education of women.\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch expressed the idea that the education of women is apt to be confounded with the emancipation of women, and that it is only so that it can be considered dangerous.\n\n“I consider, on the contrary, that the two questions are inseparably connected together,” said Pestsov; “it is a vicious circle. Woman is deprived of rights from lack of education, and the lack of education results from the absence of rights. We must not forget that the subjection of women is so complete, and dates from such ages back that we are often unwilling to recognize the gulf that separates them from us,” said he.\n\n“You said rights,” said Sergey Ivanovitch, waiting till Pestsov had finished, “meaning the right of sitting on juries, of voting, of presiding at official meetings, the right of entering the civil service, of sitting in parliament....”\n\n“Undoubtedly.”\n\n“But if women, as a rare exception, can occupy such positions, it seems to me you are wrong in using the expression ‘rights.’ It would be more correct to say duties. Every man will agree that in doing the duty of a juryman, a witness, a telegraph clerk, we feel we are performing duties. And therefore it would be correct to say that women are seeking duties, and quite legitimately. And one can but sympathize with this desire to assist in the general labor of man.”\n\n“Quite so,” Alexey Alexandrovitch assented. “The question, I imagine, is simply whether they are fitted for such duties.”\n\n“They will most likely be perfectly fitted,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, “when education has become general among them. We see this....”\n\n“How about the proverb?” said the prince, who had a long while been intent on the conversation, his little comical eyes twinkling. “I can say it before my daughter: her hair is long, because her wit is....”\n\n“Just what they thought of the negroes before their emancipation!” said Pestsov angrily.\n\n“What seems strange to me is that women should seek fresh duties,” said Sergey Ivanovitch, “while we see, unhappily, that men usually try to avoid them.”\n\n“Duties are bound up with rights—power, money, honor; those are what women are seeking,” said Pestsov.\n\n“Just as though I should seek the right to be a wet-nurse and feel injured because women are paid for the work, while no one will take me,” said the old prince.\n\nTurovtsin exploded in a loud roar of laughter and Sergey Ivanovitch regretted that he had not made this comparison. Even Alexey Alexandrovitch smiled.\n\n“Yes, but a man can’t nurse a baby,” said Pestsov, “while a woman....”\n\n“No, there was an Englishman who did suckle his baby on board ship,” said the old prince, feeling this freedom in conversation permissible before his own daughters.\n\n“There are as many such Englishmen as there would be women officials,” said Sergey Ivanovitch.\n\n“Yes, but what is a girl to do who has no family?” put in Stepan Arkadyevitch, thinking of Masha Tchibisova, whom he had had in his mind all along, in sympathizing with Pestsov and supporting him.\n\n“If the story of such a girl were thoroughly sifted, you would find she had abandoned a family—her own or a sister’s, where she might have found a woman’s duties,” Darya Alexandrovna broke in unexpectedly in a tone of exasperation, probably suspecting what sort of girl Stepan Arkadyevitch was thinking of.\n\n“But we take our stand on principle as the ideal,” replied Pestsov in his mellow bass. “Woman desires to have rights, to be independent, educated. She is oppressed, humiliated by the consciousness of her disabilities.”\n\n“And I’m oppressed and humiliated that they won’t engage me at the Foundling,” the old prince said again, to the huge delight of Turovtsin, who in his mirth dropped his asparagus with the thick end in the sauce.\n\n# Chapter 11\n\nEveryone took part in the conversation except Kitty and Levin. At first, when they were talking of the influence that one people has on another, there rose to Levin’s mind what he had to say on the subject. But these ideas, once of such importance in his eyes, seemed to come into his brain as in a dream, and had now not the slightest interest for him. It even struck him as strange that they should be so eager to talk of what was of no use to anyone. Kitty, too, should, one would have supposed, have been interested in what they were saying of the rights and education of women. How often she had mused on the subject, thinking of her friend abroad, Varenka, of her painful state of dependence, how often she had wondered about herself what would become of her if she did not marry, and how often she had argued with her sister about it! But it did not interest her at all. She and Levin had a conversation of their own, yet not a conversation, but some sort of mysterious communication, which brought them every moment nearer, and stirred in both a sense of glad terror before the unknown into which they were entering.\n\nAt first Levin, in answer to Kitty’s question how he could have seen her last year in the carriage, told her how he had been coming home from the mowing along the highroad and had met her.\n\n“It was very, very early in the morning. You were probably only just awake. Your mother was asleep in the corner. It was an exquisite morning. I was walking along wondering who it could be in a four-in-hand? It was a splendid set of four horses with bells, and in a second you flashed by, and I saw you at the window—you were sitting like this, holding the strings of your cap in both hands, and thinking awfully deeply about something,” he said, smiling. “How I should like to know what you were thinking about then! Something important?”\n\n“Wasn’t I dreadfully untidy?” she wondered, but seeing the smile of ecstasy these reminiscences called up, she felt that the impression she had made had been very good. She blushed and laughed with delight; “Really I don’t remember.”\n\n“How nicely Turovtsin laughs!” said Levin, admiring his moist eyes and shaking chest.\n\n“Have you known him long?” asked Kitty.\n\n“Oh, everyone knows him!”\n\n“And I see you think he’s a horrid man?”\n\n“Not horrid, but nothing in him.”\n\n“Oh, you’re wrong! And you must give up thinking so directly!” said Kitty. “I used to have a very poor opinion of him too, but he, he’s an awfully nice and wonderfully good-hearted man. He has a heart of gold.”\n\n“How could you find out what sort of heart he has?”\n\n“We are great friends. I know him very well. Last winter, soon after ... you came to see us,” she said, with a guilty and at the same time confiding smile, “all Dolly’s children had scarlet fever, and he happened to come and see her. And only fancy,” she said in a whisper, “he felt so sorry for her that he stayed and began to help her look after the children. Yes, and for three weeks he stopped with them, and looked after the children like a nurse.”\n\n“I am telling Konstantin Dmitrievitch about Turovtsin in the scarlet fever,” she said, bending over to her sister.\n\n“Yes, it was wonderful, noble!” said Dolly, glancing towards Turovtsin, who had become aware they were talking of him, and smiling gently to him. Levin glanced once more at Turovtsin, and wondered how it was he had not realized all this man’s goodness before.\n\n“I’m sorry, I’m sorry, and I’ll never think ill of people again!” he said gaily, genuinely expressing what he felt at the moment.\n\n# Chapter 12\n\nConnected with the conversation that had sprung up on the rights of women there were certain questions as to the inequality of rights in marriage improper to discuss before the ladies. Pestsov had several times during dinner touched upon these questions, but Sergey Ivanovitch and Stepan Arkadyevitch carefully drew him off them.\n\nWhen they rose from the table and the ladies had gone out, Pestsov did not follow them, but addressing Alexey Alexandrovitch, began to expound the chief ground of inequality. The inequality in marriage, in his opinion, lay in the fact that the infidelity of the wife and the infidelity of the husband are punished unequally, both by the law and by public opinion. Stepan Arkadyevitch went hurriedly up to Alexey Alexandrovitch and offered him a cigar.\n\n“No, I don’t smoke,” Alexey Alexandrovitch answered calmly, and as though purposely wishing to show that he was not afraid of the subject, he turned to Pestsov with a chilly smile.\n\n“I imagine that such a view has a foundation in the very nature of things,” he said, and would have gone on to the drawing-room. But at this point Turovtsin broke suddenly and unexpectedly into the conversation, addressing Alexey Alexandrovitch.\n\n“You heard, perhaps, about Pryatchnikov?” said Turovtsin, warmed up by the champagne he had drunk, and long waiting for an opportunity to break the silence that had weighed on him. “Vasya Pryatchnikov,” he said, with a good-natured smile on his damp, red lips, addressing himself principally to the most important guest, Alexey Alexandrovitch, “they told me today he fought a duel with Kvitsky at Tver, and has killed him.”\n\nJust as it always seems that one bruises oneself on a sore place, so Stepan Arkadyevitch felt now that the conversation would by ill luck fall every moment on Alexey Alexandrovitch’s sore spot. He would again have got his brother-in-law away, but Alexey Alexandrovitch himself inquired, with curiosity:\n\n“What did Pryatchnikov fight about?”\n\n“His wife. Acted like a man, he did! Called him out and shot him!”\n\n“Ah!” said Alexey Alexandrovitch indifferently, and lifting his eyebrows, he went into the drawing-room.\n\n“How glad I am you have come,” Dolly said with a frightened smile, meeting him in the outer drawing-room. “I must talk to you. Let’s sit here.”\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch, with the same expression of indifference, given him by his lifted eyebrows, sat down beside Darya Alexandrovna, and smiled affectedly.\n\n“It’s fortunate,” said he, “especially as I was meaning to ask you to excuse me, and to be taking leave. I have to start tomorrow.”\n\nDarya Alexandrovna was firmly convinced of Anna’s innocence, and she felt herself growing pale and her lips quivering with anger at this frigid, unfeeling man, who was so calmly intending to ruin her innocent friend.\n\n“Alexey Alexandrovitch,” she said, with desperate resolution looking him in the face, “I asked you about Anna, you made me no answer. How is she?”\n\n“She is, I believe, quite well, Darya Alexandrovna,” replied Alexey Alexandrovitch, not looking at her.\n\n“Alexey Alexandrovitch, forgive me, I have no right ... but I love Anna as a sister, and esteem her; I beg, I beseech you to tell me what is wrong between you? what fault do you find with her?”\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch frowned, and almost closing his eyes, dropped his head.\n\n“I presume that your husband has told you the grounds on which I consider it necessary to change my attitude to Anna Arkadyevna?” he said, not looking her in the face, but eyeing with displeasure Shtcherbatsky, who was walking across the drawing-room.\n\n“I don’t believe it, I don’t believe it, I can’t believe it!” Dolly said, clasping her bony hands before her with a vigorous gesture. She rose quickly, and laid her hand on Alexey Alexandrovitch’s sleeve. “We shall be disturbed here. Come this way, please.”\n\nDolly’s agitation had an effect on Alexey Alexandrovitch. He got up and submissively followed her to the schoolroom. They sat down to a table covered with an oilcloth cut in slits by penknives.\n\n“I don’t, I don’t believe it!” Dolly said, trying to catch his glance that avoided her.\n\n“One cannot disbelieve facts, Darya Alexandrovna,” said he, with an emphasis on the word “facts.”\n\n“But what has she done?” said Darya Alexandrovna. “What precisely has she done?”\n\n“She has forsaken her duty, and deceived her husband. That’s what she has done,” said he.\n\n“No, no, it can’t be! No, for God’s sake, you are mistaken,” said Dolly, putting her hands to her temples and closing her eyes.\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch smiled coldly, with his lips alone, meaning to signify to her and to himself the firmness of his conviction; but this warm defense, though it could not shake him, reopened his wound. He began to speak with greater heat.\n\n“It is extremely difficult to be mistaken when a wife herself informs her husband of the fact—informs him that eight years of her life, and a son, all that’s a mistake, and that she wants to begin life again,” he said angrily, with a snort.\n\n“Anna and sin—I cannot connect them, I cannot believe it!”\n\n“Darya Alexandrovna,” he said, now looking straight into Dolly’s kindly, troubled face, and feeling that his tongue was being loosened in spite of himself, “I would give a great deal for doubt to be still possible. When I doubted, I was miserable, but it was better than now. When I doubted, I had hope; but now there is no hope, and still I doubt of everything. I am in such doubt of everything that I even hate my son, and sometimes do not believe he is my son. I am very unhappy.”\n\nHe had no need to say that. Darya Alexandrovna had seen that as soon as he glanced into her face; and she felt sorry for him, and her faith in the innocence of her friend began to totter.\n\n“Oh, this is awful, awful! But can it be true that you are resolved on a divorce?”\n\n“I am resolved on extreme measures. There is nothing else for me to do.”\n\n“Nothing else to do, nothing else to do....” she replied, with tears in her eyes. “Oh no, don’t say nothing else to do!” she said.\n\n“What is horrible in a trouble of this kind is that one cannot, as in any other—in loss, in death—bear one’s trouble in peace, but that one must act,” said he, as though guessing her thought. “One must get out of the humiliating position in which one is placed; one can’t live à trois.”\n\n“I understand, I quite understand that,” said Dolly, and her head sank. She was silent for a little, thinking of herself, of her own grief in her family, and all at once, with an impulsive movement, she raised her head and clasped her hands with an imploring gesture. “But wait a little! You are a Christian. Think of her! What will become of her, if you cast her off?”\n\n“I have thought, Darya Alexandrovna, I have thought a great deal,” said Alexey Alexandrovitch. His face turned red in patches, and his dim eyes looked straight before him. Darya Alexandrovna at that moment pitied him with all her heart. “That was what I did indeed when she herself made known to me my humiliation; I left everything as of old. I gave her a chance to reform, I tried to save her. And with what result? She would not regard the slightest request—that she should observe decorum,” he said, getting heated. “One may save anyone who does not want to be ruined; but if the whole nature is so corrupt, so depraved, that ruin itself seems to be her salvation, what’s to be done?”\n\n“Anything, only not divorce!” answered Darya Alexandrovna\n“But what is anything?”\n\n“No, it is awful! She will be no one’s wife, she will be lost!”\n\n“What can I do?” said Alexey Alexandrovitch, raising his shoulders and his eyebrows. The recollection of his wife’s last act had so incensed him that he had become frigid, as at the beginning of the conversation. “I am very grateful for your sympathy, but I must be going,” he said, getting up.\n\n“No, wait a minute. You must not ruin her. Wait a little; I will tell you about myself. I was married, and my husband deceived me; in anger and jealousy, I would have thrown up everything, I would myself.... But I came to myself again; and who did it? Anna saved me. And here I am living on. The children are growing up, my husband has come back to his family, and feels his fault, is growing purer, better, and I live on.... I have forgiven it, and you ought to forgive!”\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch heard her, but her words had no effect on him now. All the hatred of that day when he had resolved on a divorce had sprung up again in his soul. He shook himself, and said in a shrill, loud voice:\n\n“Forgive I cannot, and do not wish to, and I regard it as wrong. I have done everything for this woman, and she has trodden it all in the mud to which she is akin. I am not a spiteful man, I have never hated anyone, but I hate her with my whole soul, and I cannot even forgive her, because I hate her too much for all the wrong she has done me!” he said, with tones of hatred in his voice.\n\n“Love those that hate you....” Darya Alexandrovna whispered timorously.\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch smiled contemptuously. That he knew long ago, but it could not be applied to his case.\n\n“Love those that hate you, but to love those one hates is impossible. Forgive me for having troubled you. Everyone has enough to bear in his own grief!” And regaining his self-possession, Alexey Alexandrovitch quietly took leave and went away.\n\n# Chapter 13\n\nWhen they rose from table, Levin would have liked to follow Kitty into the drawing-room; but he was afraid she might dislike this, as too obviously paying her attention. He remained in the little ring of men, taking part in the general conversation, and without looking at Kitty, he was aware of her movements, her looks, and the place where she was in the drawing-room.\n\nHe did at once, and without the smallest effort, keep the promise he had made her—always to think well of all men, and to like everyone always. The conversation fell on the village commune, in which Pestsov saw a sort of special principle, called by him the “choral” principle. Levin did not agree with Pestsov, nor with his brother, who had a special attitude of his own, both admitting and not admitting the significance of the Russian commune. But he talked to them, simply trying to reconcile and soften their differences. He was not in the least interested in what he said himself, and even less so in what they said; all he wanted was that they and everyone should be happy and contented. He knew now the one thing of importance; and that one thing was at first there, in the drawing-room, and then began moving across and came to a standstill at the door. Without turning round he felt the eyes fixed on him, and the smile, and he could not help turning round. She was standing in the doorway with Shtcherbatsky, looking at him.\n\n“I thought you were going towards the piano,” said he, going up to her. “That’s something I miss in the country—music.”\n\n“No; we only came to fetch you and thank you,” she said, rewarding him with a smile that was like a gift, “for coming. What do they want to argue for? No one ever convinces anyone, you know.”\n\n“Yes; that’s true,” said Levin; “it generally happens that one argues warmly simply because one can’t make out what one’s opponent wants to prove.”\n\nLevin had often noticed in discussions between the most intelligent people that after enormous efforts, and an enormous expenditure of logical subtleties and words, the disputants finally arrived at being aware that what they had so long been struggling to prove to one another had long ago, from the beginning of the argument, been known to both, but that they liked different things, and would not define what they liked for fear of its being attacked. He had often had the experience of suddenly in a discussion grasping what it was his opponent liked and at once liking it too, and immediately he found himself agreeing, and then all arguments fell away as useless. Sometimes, too, he had experienced the opposite, expressing at last what he liked himself, which he was devising arguments to defend, and, chancing to express it well and genuinely, he had found his opponent at once agreeing and ceasing to dispute his position. He tried to say this.\n\nShe knitted her brow, trying to understand. But directly he began to illustrate his meaning, she understood at once.\n\n“I know: one must find out what he is arguing for, what is precious to him, then one can....”\n\nShe had completely guessed and expressed his badly expressed idea. Levin smiled joyfully; he was struck by this transition from the confused, verbose discussion with Pestsov and his brother to this laconic, clear, almost wordless communication of the most complex ideas.\n\nShtcherbatsky moved away from them, and Kitty, going up to a card-table, sat down, and, taking up the chalk, began drawing diverging circles over the new green cloth.\n\nThey began again on the subject that had been started at dinner—the liberty and occupations of women. Levin was of the opinion of Darya Alexandrovna that a girl who did not marry should find a woman’s duties in a family. He supported this view by the fact that no family can get on without women to help; that in every family, poor or rich, there are and must be nurses, either relations or hired.\n\n“No,” said Kitty, blushing, but looking at him all the more boldly with her truthful eyes; “a girl may be so circumstanced that she cannot live in the family without humiliation, while she herself....”\n\nAt the hint he understood her.\n\n“Oh, yes,” he said. “Yes, yes, yes—you’re right; you’re right!”\n\nAnd he saw all that Pestsov had been maintaining at dinner of the liberty of woman, simply from getting a glimpse of the terror of an old maid’s existence and its humiliation in Kitty’s heart; and loving her, he felt that terror and humiliation, and at once gave up his arguments.\n\nA silence followed. She was still drawing with the chalk on the table. Her eyes were shining with a soft light. Under the influence of her mood he felt in all his being a continually growing tension of happiness.\n\n“Ah! I’ve scribbled all over the table!” she said, and, laying down the chalk, she made a movement as though to get up.\n\n“What! shall I be left alone—without her?” he thought with horror, and he took the chalk. “Wait a minute,” he said, sitting down to the table. “I’ve long wanted to ask you one thing.”\n\nHe looked straight into her caressing, though frightened eyes.\n\n“Please, ask it.”\n\n“Here,” he said; and he wrote the initial letters, w, y, t, m, i, c, n, b, d, t, m, n, o, t. These letters meant, “When you told me it could never be, did that mean never, or then?” There seemed no likelihood that she could make out this complicated sentence; but he looked at her as though his life depended on her understanding the words. She glanced at him seriously, then leaned her puckered brow on her hands and began to read. Once or twice she stole a look at him, as though asking him, “Is it what I think?”\n\n“I understand,” she said, flushing a little.\n\n“What is this word?” he said, pointing to the n that stood for never.\n\n“It means never,” she said; “but that’s not true!”\n\nHe quickly rubbed out what he had written, gave her the chalk, and stood up. She wrote, t, i, c, n, a, d.\n\nDolly was completely comforted in the depression caused by her conversation with Alexey Alexandrovitch when she caught sight of the two figures: Kitty with the chalk in her hand, with a shy and happy smile looking upwards at Levin, and his handsome figure bending over the table with glowing eyes fastened one minute on the table and the next on her. He was suddenly radiant: he had understood. It meant, “Then I could not answer differently.”\n\nHe glanced at her questioningly, timidly.\n“Only then?”\n\n“Yes,” her smile answered.\n\n“And n... and now?” he asked.\n\n“Well, read this. I’ll tell you what I should like—should like so much!” she wrote the initial letters, i, y, c, f, a, f, w, h. This meant, “If you could forget and forgive what happened.”\n\nHe snatched the chalk with nervous, trembling fingers, and breaking it, wrote the initial letters of the following phrase, “I have nothing to forget and to forgive; I have never ceased to love you.”\n\nShe glanced at him with a smile that did not waver.\n\n“I understand,” she said in a whisper.\n\nHe sat down and wrote a long phrase. She understood it all, and without asking him, “Is it this?” took the chalk and at once answered.\n\nFor a long while he could not understand what she had written, and often looked into her eyes. He was stupefied with happiness. He could not supply the word she had meant; but in her charming eyes, beaming with happiness, he saw all he needed to know. And he wrote three letters. But he had hardly finished writing when she read them over her arm, and herself finished and wrote the answer, “Yes.”\n\n“You’re playing secrétaire?” said the old prince. “But we must really be getting along if you want to be in time at the theater.”\n\nLevin got up and escorted Kitty to the door.\n\nIn their conversation everything had been said; it had been said that she loved him, and that she would tell her father and mother that he would come tomorrow morning.\n\n# Chapter 14\n\nWhen Kitty had gone and Levin was left alone, he felt such uneasiness without her, and such an impatient longing to get as quickly, as quickly as possible, to tomorrow morning, when he would see her again and be plighted to her forever, that he felt afraid, as though of death, of those fourteen hours that he had to get through without her. It was essential for him to be with someone to talk to, so as not to be left alone, to kill time. Stepan Arkadyevitch would have been the companion most congenial to him, but he was going out, he said, to a soirée, in reality to the ballet. Levin only had time to tell him he was happy, and that he loved him, and would never, never forget what he had done for him. The eyes and the smile of Stepan Arkadyevitch showed Levin that he comprehended that feeling fittingly.\n\n“Oh, so it’s not time to die yet?” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, pressing Levin’s hand with emotion.\n\n“N-n-no!” said Levin.\n\nDarya Alexandrovna too, as she said good-bye to him, gave him a sort of congratulation, saying, “How glad I am you have met Kitty again! One must value old friends.” Levin did not like these words of Darya Alexandrovna’s. She could not understand how lofty and beyond her it all was, and she ought not to have dared to allude to it. Levin said good-bye to them, but, not to be left alone, he attached himself to his brother.\n\n“Where are you going?”\n\n“I’m going to a meeting.”\n\n“Well, I’ll come with you. May I?”\n\n“What for? Yes, come along,” said Sergey Ivanovitch, smiling. “What is the matter with you today?”\n\n“With me? Happiness is the matter with me!” said Levin, letting down the window of the carriage they were driving in. “You don’t mind?—it’s so stifling. It’s happiness is the matter with me! Why is it you have never married?”\n\nSergey Ivanovitch smiled.\n\n“I am very glad, she seems a nice gi....” Sergey Ivanovitch was beginning.\n\n“Don’t say it! don’t say it!” shouted Levin, clutching at the collar of his fur coat with both hands, and muffling him up in it. “She’s a nice girl” were such simple, humble words, so out of harmony with his feeling.\n\nSergey Ivanovitch laughed outright a merry laugh, which was rare with him. “Well, anyway, I may say that I’m very glad of it.”\n\n“That you may do tomorrow, tomorrow and nothing more! Nothing, nothing, silence,” said Levin, and muffling him once more in his fur coat, he added: “I do like you so! Well, is it possible for me to be present at the meeting?”\n\n“Of course it is.”\n\n“What is your discussion about today?” asked Levin, never ceasing smiling.\n\nThey arrived at the meeting. Levin heard the secretary hesitatingly read the minutes which he obviously did not himself understand; but Levin saw from this secretary’s face what a good, nice, kind-hearted person he was. This was evident from his confusion and embarrassment in reading the minutes. Then the discussion began. They were disputing about the misappropriation of certain sums and the laying of certain pipes, and Sergey Ivanovitch was very cutting to two members, and said something at great length with an air of triumph; and another member, scribbling something on a bit of paper, began timidly at first, but afterwards answered him very viciously and delightfully. And then Sviazhsky (he was there too) said something too, very handsomely and nobly. Levin listened to them, and saw clearly that these missing sums and these pipes were not anything real, and that they were not at all angry, but were all the nicest, kindest people, and everything was as happy and charming as possible among them. They did no harm to anyone, and were all enjoying it. What struck Levin was that he could see through them all today, and from little, almost imperceptible signs knew the soul of each, and saw distinctly that they were all good at heart. And Levin himself in particular they were all extremely fond of that day. That was evident from the way they spoke to him, from the friendly, affectionate way even those he did not know looked at him.\n\n“Well, did you like it?” Sergey Ivanovitch asked him.\n\n“Very much. I never supposed it was so interesting! Capital! Splendid!”\n\nSviazhsky went up to Levin and invited him to come round to tea with him. Levin was utterly at a loss to comprehend or recall what it was he had disliked in Sviazhsky, what he had failed to find in him. He was a clever and wonderfully good-hearted man.\n\n“Most delighted,” he said, and asked after his wife and sister-in-law. And from a queer association of ideas, because in his imagination the idea of Sviazhsky’s sister-in-law was connected with marriage, it occurred to him that there was no one to whom he could more suitably speak of his happiness, and he was very glad to go and see them.\n\nSviazhsky questioned him about his improvements on his estate, presupposing, as he always did, that there was no possibility of doing anything not done already in Europe, and now this did not in the least annoy Levin. On the contrary, he felt that Sviazhsky was right, that the whole business was of little value, and he saw the wonderful softness and consideration with which Sviazhsky avoided fully expressing his correct view. The ladies of the Sviazhsky household were particularly delightful. It seemed to Levin that they knew all about it already and sympathized with him, saying nothing merely from delicacy. He stayed with them one hour, two, three, talking of all sorts of subjects but the one thing that filled his heart, and did not observe that he was boring them dreadfully, and that it was long past their bedtime.\n\nSviazhsky went with him into the hall, yawning and wondering at the strange humor his friend was in. It was past one o’clock. Levin went back to his hotel, and was dismayed at the thought that all alone now with his impatience he had ten hours still left to get through. The servant, whose turn it was to be up all night, lighted his candles, and would have gone away, but Levin stopped him. This servant, Yegor, whom Levin had noticed before, struck him as a very intelligent, excellent, and, above all, good-hearted man.\n\n“Well, Yegor, it’s hard work not sleeping, isn’t it?”\n\n“One’s got to put up with it! It’s part of our work, you see. In a gentleman’s house it’s easier; but then here one makes more.”\n\nIt appeared that Yegor had a family, three boys and a daughter, a sempstress, whom he wanted to marry to a cashier in a saddler’s shop.\n\nLevin, on hearing this, informed Yegor that, in his opinion, in marriage the great thing was love, and that with love one would always be happy, for happiness rests only on oneself.\n\nYegor listened attentively, and obviously quite took in Levin’s idea, but by way of assent to it he enunciated, greatly to Levin’s surprise, the observation that when he had lived with good masters he had always been satisfied with his masters, and now was perfectly satisfied with his employer, though he was a Frenchman.\n\n“Wonderfully good-hearted fellow!” thought Levin.\n\n“Well, but you yourself, Yegor, when you got married, did you love your wife?”\n\n“Ay! and why not?” responded Yegor.\n\nAnd Levin saw that Yegor too was in an excited state and intending to express all his most heartfelt emotions.\n\n“My life, too, has been a wonderful one. From a child up....” he was beginning with flashing eyes, apparently catching Levin’s enthusiasm, just as people catch yawning.\n\nBut at that moment a ring was heard. Yegor departed, and Levin was left alone. He had eaten scarcely anything at dinner, had refused tea and supper at Sviazhsky’s, but he was incapable of thinking of supper. He had not slept the previous night, but was incapable of thinking of sleep either. His room was cold, but he was oppressed by heat. He opened both the movable panes in his window and sat down to the table opposite the open panes. Over the snow-covered roofs could be seen a decorated cross with chains, and above it the rising triangle of Charles’s Wain with the yellowish light of Capella. He gazed at the cross, then at the stars, drank in the fresh freezing air that flowed evenly into the room, and followed as though in a dream the images and memories that rose in his imagination. At four o’clock he heard steps in the passage and peeped out at the door. It was the gambler Myaskin, whom he knew, coming from the club. He walked gloomily, frowning and coughing. “Poor, unlucky fellow!” thought Levin, and tears came into his eyes from love and pity for this man. He would have talked with him, and tried to comfort him, but remembering that he had nothing but his shirt on, he changed his mind and sat down again at the open pane to bathe in the cold air and gaze at the exquisite lines of the cross, silent, but full of meaning for him, and the mounting lurid yellow star. At seven o’clock there was a noise of people polishing the floors, and bells ringing in some servants’ department, and Levin felt that he was beginning to get frozen. He closed the pane, washed, dressed, and went out into the street.\n\n# Chapter 15\n\nThe streets were still empty. Levin went to the house of the Shtcherbatskys. The visitors’ doors were closed and everything was asleep. He walked back, went into his room again, and asked for coffee. The day servant, not Yegor this time, brought it to him. Levin would have entered into conversation with him, but a bell rang for the servant, and he went out. Levin tried to drink coffee and put some roll in his mouth, but his mouth was quite at a loss what to do with the roll. Levin, rejecting the roll, put on his coat and went out again for a walk. It was nine o’clock when he reached the Shtcherbatskys’ steps the second time. In the house they were only just up, and the cook came out to go marketing. He had to get through at least two hours more.\n\nAll that night and morning Levin lived perfectly unconsciously, and felt perfectly lifted out of the conditions of material life. He had eaten nothing for a whole day, he had not slept for two nights, had spent several hours undressed in the frozen air, and felt not simply fresher and stronger than ever, but felt utterly independent of his body; he moved without muscular effort, and felt as if he could do anything. He was convinced he could fly upwards or lift the corner of the house, if need be. He spent the remainder of the time in the street, incessantly looking at his watch and gazing about him.\n\nAnd what he saw then, he never saw again after. The children especially going to school, the bluish doves flying down from the roofs to the pavement, and the little loaves covered with flour, thrust out by an unseen hand, touched him. Those loaves, those doves, and those two boys were not earthly creatures. It all happened at the same time: a boy ran towards a dove and glanced smiling at Levin; the dove, with a whir of her wings, darted away, flashing in the sun, amid grains of snow that quivered in the air, while from a little window there came a smell of fresh-baked bread, and the loaves were put out. All of this together was so extraordinarily nice that Levin laughed and cried with delight. Going a long way round by Gazetny Place and Kislovka, he went back again to the hotel, and putting his watch before him, he sat down to wait for twelve o’clock. In the next room they were talking about some sort of machines, and swindling, and coughing their morning coughs. They did not realize that the hand was near twelve. The hand reached it. Levin went out onto the steps. The sledge-drivers clearly knew all about it. They crowded round Levin with happy faces, quarreling among themselves, and offering their services. Trying not to offend the other sledge drivers, and promising to drive with them too, Levin took one and told him to drive to the Shtcherbatskys’. The sledge-driver was splendid in a white shirt-collar sticking out over his overcoat and into his strong, full-blooded red neck. The sledge was high and comfortable, and altogether such a one as Levin never drove in after, and the horse was a good one, and tried to gallop but didn’t seem to move. The driver knew the Shtcherbatskys’ house, and drew up at the entrance with a curve of his arm and a “Wo!” especially indicative of respect for his fare. The Shtcherbatskys’ hall-porter certainly knew all about it. This was evident from the smile in his eyes and the way he said:\n\n“Well, it’s a long while since you’ve been to see us, Konstantin Dmitrievitch!”\n\nNot only he knew all about it, but he was unmistakably delighted and making efforts to conceal his joy. Looking into his kindly old eyes, Levin realized even something new in his happiness.\n\n“Are they up?”\n\n“Pray walk in! Leave it here,” said he, smiling, as Levin would have come back to take his hat. That meant something.\n\n“To whom shall I announce your honor?” asked the footman.\n\nThe footman, though a young man, and one of the new school of footmen, a dandy, was a very kind-hearted, good fellow, and he too knew all about it.\n\n“The princess ... the prince ... the young princess....” said Levin.\n\nThe first person he saw was Mademoiselle Linon. She walked across the room, and her ringlets and her face were beaming. He had only just spoken to her, when suddenly he heard the rustle of a skirt at the door, and Mademoiselle Linon vanished from Levin’s eyes, and a joyful terror came over him at the nearness of his happiness. Mademoiselle Linon was in great haste, and leaving him, went out at the other door. Directly she had gone out, swift, swift light steps sounded on the parquet, and his bliss, his life, himself—what was best in himself, what he had so long sought and longed for—was quickly, so quickly approaching him. She did not walk, but seemed, by some unseen force, to float to him. He saw nothing but her clear, truthful eyes, frightened by the same bliss of love that flooded his heart. Those eyes were shining nearer and nearer, blinding him with their light of love. She stopped still close to him, touching him. Her hands rose and dropped onto his shoulders.\n\nShe had done all she could—she had run up to him and given herself up entirely, shy and happy. He put his arms round her and pressed his lips to her mouth that sought his kiss.\n\nShe too had not slept all night, and had been expecting him all the morning.\n\nHer mother and father had consented without demur, and were happy in her happiness. She had been waiting for him. She wanted to be the first to tell him her happiness and his. She had got ready to see him alone, and had been delighted at the idea, and had been shy and ashamed, and did not know herself what she was doing. She had heard his steps and voice, and had waited at the door for Mademoiselle Linon to go. Mademoiselle Linon had gone away. Without thinking, without asking herself how and what, she had gone up to him, and did as she was doing.\n\n“Let us go to mamma!” she said, taking him by the hand. For a long while he could say nothing, not so much because he was afraid of desecrating the loftiness of his emotion by a word, as that every time he tried to say something, instead of words he felt that tears of happiness were welling up. He took her hand and kissed it.\n\n“Can it be true?” he said at last in a choked voice. “I can’t believe you love me, dear!”\n\nShe smiled at that “dear,” and at the timidity with which he glanced at her.\n\n“Yes!” she said significantly, deliberately. “I am so happy!”\n\nNot letting go his hands, she went into the drawing-room. The princess, seeing them, breathed quickly, and immediately began to cry and then immediately began to laugh, and with a vigorous step Levin had not expected, ran up to him, and hugging his head, kissed him, wetting his cheeks with her tears.\n\n“So it is all settled! I am glad. Love her. I am glad.... Kitty!”\n\n“You’ve not been long settling things,” said the old prince, trying to seem unmoved; but Levin noticed that his eyes were wet when he turned to him.\n\n“I’ve long, always wished for this!” said the prince, taking Levin by the arm and drawing him towards himself. “Even when this little feather-head fancied....”\n\n“Papa!” shrieked Kitty, and shut his mouth with her hands.\n\n“Well, I won’t!” he said. “I’m very, very ... plea... Oh, what a fool I am....”\n\nHe embraced Kitty, kissed her face, her hand, her face again, and made the sign of the cross over her.\n\nAnd there came over Levin a new feeling of love for this man, till then so little known to him, when he saw how slowly and tenderly Kitty kissed his muscular hand.\n\n# Chapter 16\n\nThe princess sat in her armchair, silent and smiling; the prince sat down beside her. Kitty stood by her father’s chair, still holding his hand. All were silent.\n\nThe princess was the first to put everything into words, and to translate all thoughts and feelings into practical questions. And all equally felt this strange and painful for the first minute.\n\n“When is it to be? We must have the benediction and announcement. And when’s the wedding to be? What do you think, Alexander?”\n\n“Here he is,” said the old prince, pointing to Levin—“he’s the principal person in the matter.”\n\n“When?” said Levin blushing. “Tomorrow. If you ask me, I should say, the benediction today and the wedding tomorrow.”\n\n“Come, mon cher, that’s nonsense!”\n\n“Well, in a week.”\n\n“He’s quite mad.”\n“No, why so?”\n\n“Well, upon my word!” said the mother, smiling, delighted at this haste. “How about the trousseau?”\n\n“Will there really be a trousseau and all that?” Levin thought with horror. “But can the trousseau and the benediction and all that—can it spoil my happiness? Nothing can spoil it!” He glanced at Kitty, and noticed that she was not in the least, not in the very least, disturbed by the idea of the trousseau. “Then it must be all right,” he thought.\n\n“Oh, I know nothing about it; I only said what I should like,” he said apologetically.\n\n“We’ll talk it over, then. The benediction and announcement can take place now. That’s very well.”\n\nThe princess went up to her husband, kissed him, and would have gone away, but he kept her, embraced her, and, tenderly as a young lover, kissed her several times, smiling. The old people were obviously muddled for a moment, and did not quite know whether it was they who were in love again or their daughter. When the prince and the princess had gone, Levin went up to his betrothed and took her hand. He was self-possessed now and could speak, and he had a great deal he wanted to tell her. But he said not at all what he had to say.\n\n“How I knew it would be so! I never hoped for it; and yet in my heart I was always sure,” he said. “I believe that it was ordained.”\n\n“And I!” she said. “Even when....” She stopped and went on again, looking at him resolutely with her truthful eyes, “Even when I thrust from me my happiness. I always loved you alone, but I was carried away. I ought to tell you.... Can you forgive that?”\n\n“Perhaps it was for the best. You will have to forgive me so much. I ought to tell you....”\n\nThis was one of the things he had meant to speak about. He had resolved from the first to tell her two things—that he was not chaste as she was, and that he was not a believer. It was agonizing, but he considered he ought to tell her both these facts.\n\n“No, not now, later!” he said.\n\n“Very well, later, but you must certainly tell me. I’m not afraid of anything. I want to know everything. Now it is settled.”\n\nHe added: “Settled that you’ll take me whatever I may be—you won’t give me up? Yes?”\n\n“Yes, yes.”\n\nTheir conversation was interrupted by Mademoiselle Linon, who with an affected but tender smile came to congratulate her favorite pupil. Before she had gone, the servants came in with their congratulations. Then relations arrived, and there began that state of blissful absurdity from which Levin did not emerge till the day after his wedding. Levin was in a continual state of awkwardness and discomfort, but the intensity of his happiness went on all the while increasing. He felt continually that a great deal was being expected of him—what, he did not know; and he did everything he was told, and it all gave him happiness. He had thought his engagement would have nothing about it like others, that the ordinary conditions of engaged couples would spoil his special happiness; but it ended in his doing exactly as other people did, and his happiness being only increased thereby and becoming more and more special, more and more unlike anything that had ever happened.\n\n“Now we shall have sweetmeats to eat,” said Mademoiselle Linon—and Levin drove off to buy sweetmeats.\n\n“Well, I’m very glad,” said Sviazhsky. “I advise you to get the bouquets from Fomin’s.”\n\n“Oh, are they wanted?” And he drove to Fomin’s.\n\nHis brother offered to lend him money, as he would have so many expenses, presents to give....\n\n“Oh, are presents wanted?” And he galloped to Foulde’s.\n\nAnd at the confectioner’s, and at Fomin’s, and at Foulde’s he saw that he was expected; that they were pleased to see him, and prided themselves on his happiness, just as everyone whom he had to do with during those days. What was extraordinary was that everyone not only liked him, but even people previously unsympathetic, cold, and callous, were enthusiastic over him, gave way to him in everything, treated his feeling with tenderness and delicacy, and shared his conviction that he was the happiest man in the world because his betrothed was beyond perfection. Kitty too felt the same thing. When Countess Nordston ventured to hint that she had hoped for something better, Kitty was so angry and proved so conclusively that nothing in the world could be better than Levin, that Countess Nordston had to admit it, and in Kitty’s presence never met Levin without a smile of ecstatic admiration.\n\nThe confession he had promised was the one painful incident of this time. He consulted the old prince, and with his sanction gave Kitty his diary, in which there was written the confession that tortured him. He had written this diary at the time with a view to his future wife. Two things caused him anguish: his lack of purity and his lack of faith. His confession of unbelief passed unnoticed. She was religious, had never doubted the truths of religion, but his external unbelief did not affect her in the least. Through love she knew all his soul, and in his soul she saw what she wanted, and that such a state of soul should be called unbelieving was to her a matter of no account. The other confession set her weeping bitterly.\n\nLevin, not without an inner struggle, handed her his diary. He knew that between him and her there could not be, and should not be, secrets, and so he had decided that so it must be. But he had not realized what an effect it would have on her, he had not put himself in her place. It was only when the same evening he came to their house before the theater, went into her room and saw her tear-stained, pitiful, sweet face, miserable with suffering he had caused and nothing could undo, he felt the abyss that separated his shameful past from her dovelike purity, and was appalled at what he had done.\n\n“Take them, take these dreadful books!” she said, pushing away the notebooks lying before her on the table. “Why did you give them me? No, it was better anyway,” she added, touched by his despairing face. “But it’s awful, awful!”\n\nHis head sank, and he was silent. He could say nothing.\n\n“You can’t forgive me,” he whispered.\n\n“Yes, I forgive you; but it’s terrible!”\n\nBut his happiness was so immense that this confession did not shatter it, it only added another shade to it. She forgave him; but from that time more than ever he considered himself unworthy of her, morally bowed down lower than ever before her, and prized more highly than ever his undeserved happiness.\n\n# Chapter 17\n\nUnconsciously going over in his memory the conversations that had taken place during and after dinner, Alexey Alexandrovitch returned to his solitary room. Darya Alexandrovna’s words about forgiveness had aroused in him nothing but annoyance. The applicability or non-applicability of the Christian precept to his own case was too difficult a question to be discussed lightly, and this question had long ago been answered by Alexey Alexandrovitch in the negative. Of all that had been said, what stuck most in his memory was the phrase of stupid, good-natured Turovtsin—“Acted like a man, he did! Called him out and shot him!” Everyone had apparently shared this feeling, though from politeness they had not expressed it.\n\n“But the matter is settled, it’s useless thinking about it,” Alexey Alexandrovitch told himself. And thinking of nothing but the journey before him, and the revision work he had to do, he went into his room and asked the porter who escorted him where his man was. The porter said that the man had only just gone out. Alexey Alexandrovitch ordered tea to be sent him, sat down to the table, and taking the guidebook, began considering the route of his journey.\n\n“Two telegrams,” said his manservant, coming into the room. “I beg your pardon, your excellency; I’d only just that minute gone out.”\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch took the telegrams and opened them. The first telegram was the announcement of Stremov’s appointment to the very post Karenin had coveted. Alexey Alexandrovitch flung the telegram down, and flushing a little, got up and began to pace up and down the room. “Quos vult perdere dementat,” he said, meaning by quos the persons responsible for this appointment. He was not so much annoyed that he had not received the post, that he had been conspicuously passed over; but it was incomprehensible, amazing to him that they did not see that the wordy phrase-monger Stremov was the last man fit for it. How could they fail to see how they were ruining themselves, lowering their prestige by this appointment?\n\n“Something else in the same line,” he said to himself bitterly, opening the second telegram. The telegram was from his wife. Her name, written in blue pencil, “Anna,” was the first thing that caught his eye. “I am dying; I beg, I implore you to come. I shall die easier with your forgiveness,” he read. He smiled contemptuously, and flung down the telegram. That this was a trick and a fraud, of that, he thought for the first minute, there could be no doubt.\n\n“There is no deceit she would stick at. She was near her confinement. Perhaps it is the confinement. But what can be their aim? To legitimize the child, to compromise me, and prevent a divorce,” he thought. “But something was said in it: I am dying....” He read the telegram again, and suddenly the plain meaning of what was said in it struck him.\n\n“And if it is true?” he said to himself. “If it is true that in the moment of agony and nearness to death she is genuinely penitent, and I, taking it for a trick, refuse to go? That would not only be cruel, and everyone would blame me, but it would be stupid on my part.”\n\n“Piotr, call a coach; I am going to Petersburg,” he said to his servant.\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch decided that he would go to Petersburg and see his wife. If her illness was a trick, he would say nothing and go away again. If she was really in danger, and wished to see him before her death, he would forgive her if he found her alive, and pay her the last duties if he came too late.\n\nAll the way he thought no more of what he ought to do.\n\nWith a sense of weariness and uncleanness from the night spent in the train, in the early fog of Petersburg Alexey Alexandrovitch drove through the deserted Nevsky and stared straight before him, not thinking of what was awaiting him. He could not think about it, because in picturing what would happen, he could not drive away the reflection that her death would at once remove all the difficulty of his position. Bakers, closed shops, night-cabmen, porters sweeping the pavements flashed past his eyes, and he watched it all, trying to smother the thought of what was awaiting him, and what he dared not hope for, and yet was hoping for. He drove up to the steps. A sledge and a carriage with the coachman asleep stood at the entrance. As he went into the entry, Alexey Alexandrovitch, as it were, got out his resolution from the remotest corner of his brain, and mastered it thoroughly. Its meaning ran: “If it’s a trick, then calm contempt and departure. If truth, do what is proper.”\n\nThe porter opened the door before Alexey Alexandrovitch rang. The porter, Kapitonitch, looked queer in an old coat, without a tie, and in slippers.\n\n“How is your mistress?”\n\n“A successful confinement yesterday.”\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch stopped short and turned white. He felt distinctly now how intensely he had longed for her death.\n\n“And how is she?”\n\nKorney in his morning apron ran downstairs.\n\n“Very ill,” he answered. “There was a consultation yesterday, and the doctor’s here now.”\n\n“Take my things,” said Alexey Alexandrovitch, and feeling some relief at the news that there was still hope of her death, he went into the hall.\n\nOn the hatstand there was a military overcoat. Alexey Alexandrovitch noticed it and asked:\n\n“Who is here?”\n\n“The doctor, the midwife, and Count Vronsky.”\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch went into the inner rooms.\n\nIn the drawing-room there was no one; at the sound of his steps there came out of her boudoir the midwife in a cap with lilac ribbons.\n\nShe went up to Alexey Alexandrovitch, and with the familiarity given by the approach of death took him by the arm and drew him towards the bedroom.\n\n“Thank God you’ve come! She keeps on about you and nothing but you,” she said.\n\n“Make haste with the ice!” the doctor’s peremptory voice said from the bedroom.\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch went into her boudoir.\n\nAt the table, sitting sideways in a low chair, was Vronsky, his face hidden in his hands, weeping. He jumped up at the doctor’s voice, took his hands from his face, and saw Alexey Alexandrovitch. Seeing the husband, he was so overwhelmed that he sat down again, drawing his head down to his shoulders, as if he wanted to disappear; but he made an effort over himself, got up and said:\n\n“She is dying. The doctors say there is no hope. I am entirely in your power, only let me be here ... though I am at your disposal. I....”\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch, seeing Vronsky’s tears, felt a rush of that nervous emotion always produced in him by the sight of other people’s suffering, and turning away his face, he moved hurriedly to the door, without hearing the rest of his words. From the bedroom came the sound of Anna’s voice saying something. Her voice was lively, eager, with exceedingly distinct intonations. Alexey Alexandrovitch went into the bedroom, and went up to the bed. She was lying turned with her face towards him. Her cheeks were flushed crimson, her eyes glittered, her little white hands thrust out from the sleeves of her dressing gown were playing with the quilt, twisting it about. It seemed as though she were not only well and blooming, but in the happiest frame of mind. She was talking rapidly, musically, and with exceptionally correct articulation and expressive intonation.\n\n“For Alexey—I am speaking of Alexey Alexandrovitch (what a strange and awful thing that both are Alexey, isn’t it?)—Alexey would not refuse me. I should forget, he would forgive.... But why doesn’t he come? He’s so good he doesn’t know himself how good he is. Ah, my God, what agony! Give me some water, quick! Oh, that will be bad for her, my little girl! Oh, very well then, give her to a nurse. Yes, I agree, it’s better in fact. He’ll be coming; it will hurt him to see her. Give her to the nurse.”\n\n“Anna Arkadyevna, he has come. Here he is!” said the midwife, trying to attract her attention to Alexey Alexandrovitch.\n\n“Oh, what nonsense!” Anna went on, not seeing her husband. “No, give her to me; give me my little one! He has not come yet. You say he won’t forgive me, because you don’t know him. No one knows him. I’m the only one, and it was hard for me even. His eyes I ought to know—Seryozha has just the same eyes—and I can’t bear to see them because of it. Has Seryozha had his dinner? I know everyone will forget him. He would not forget. Seryozha must be moved into the corner room, and Mariette must be asked to sleep with him.”\n\nAll of a sudden she shrank back, was silent; and in terror, as though expecting a blow, as though to defend herself, she raised her hands to her face. She had seen her husband.\n\n“No, no!” she began. “I am not afraid of him; I am afraid of death. Alexey, come here. I am in a hurry, because I’ve no time, I’ve not long left to live; the fever will begin directly and I shall understand nothing more. Now I understand, I understand it all, I see it all!”\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch’s wrinkled face wore an expression of agony; he took her by the hand and tried to say something, but he could not utter it; his lower lip quivered, but he still went on struggling with his emotion, and only now and then glanced at her. And each time he glanced at her, he saw her eyes gazing at him with such passionate and triumphant tenderness as he had never seen in them.\n\n“Wait a minute, you don’t know ... stay a little, stay!...” She stopped, as though collecting her ideas. “Yes,” she began; “yes, yes, yes. This is what I wanted to say. Don’t be surprised at me. I’m still the same.... But there is another woman in me, I’m afraid of her: she loved that man, and I tried to hate you, and could not forget about her that used to be. I’m not that woman. Now I’m my real self, all myself. I’m dying now, I know I shall die, ask him. Even now I feel—see here, the weights on my feet, on my hands, on my fingers. My fingers—see how huge they are! But this will soon all be over.... Only one thing I want: forgive me, forgive me quite. I’m terrible, but my nurse used to tell me; the holy martyr—what was her name? She was worse. And I’ll go to Rome; there’s a wilderness, and there I shall be no trouble to anyone, only I’ll take Seryozha and the little one.... No, you can’t forgive me! I know, it can’t be forgiven! No, no, go away, you’re too good!” She held his hand in one burning hand, while she pushed him away with the other.\n\nThe nervous agitation of Alexey Alexandrovitch kept increasing, and had by now reached such a point that he ceased to struggle with it. He suddenly felt that what he had regarded as nervous agitation was on the contrary a blissful spiritual condition that gave him all at once a new happiness he had never known. He did not think that the Christian law that he had been all his life trying to follow, enjoined on him to forgive and love his enemies; but a glad feeling of love and forgiveness for his enemies filled his heart. He knelt down, and laying his head in the curve of her arm, which burned him as with fire through the sleeve, he sobbed like a little child. She put her arm around his head, moved towards him, and with defiant pride lifted up her eyes.\n\n“That is he. I knew him! Now, forgive me, everyone, forgive me!... They’ve come again; why don’t they go away?... Oh, take these cloaks off me!”\n\nThe doctor unloosed her hands, carefully laying her on the pillow, and covered her up to the shoulders. She lay back submissively, and looked before her with beaming eyes.\n\n“Remember one thing, that I needed nothing but forgiveness, and I want nothing more.... Why doesn’t he come?” she said, turning to the door towards Vronsky. “Do come, do come! Give him your hand.”\n\nVronsky came to the side of the bed, and seeing Anna, again hid his face in his hands.\n\n“Uncover your face—look at him! He’s a saint,” she said. “Oh! uncover your face, do uncover it!” she said angrily. “Alexey Alexandrovitch, do uncover his face! I want to see him.”\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch took Vronsky’s hands and drew them away from his face, which was awful with the expression of agony and shame upon it.\n\n“Give him your hand. Forgive him.”\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch gave him his hand, not attempting to restrain the tears that streamed from his eyes.\n\n“Thank God, thank God!” she said, “now everything is ready. Only to stretch my legs a little. There, that’s capital. How badly these flowers are done—not a bit like a violet,” she said, pointing to the hangings. “My God, my God! when will it end? Give me some morphine. Doctor, give me some morphine! Oh, my God, my God!”\n\nAnd she tossed about on the bed.\n\nThe doctors said that it was puerperal fever, and that it was ninety-nine chances in a hundred it would end in death. The whole day long there was fever, delirium, and unconsciousness. At midnight the patient lay without consciousness, and almost without pulse.\n\nThe end was expected every minute.\n\nVronsky had gone home, but in the morning he came to inquire, and Alexey Alexandrovitch meeting him in the hall, said: “Better stay, she might ask for you,” and himself led him to his wife’s boudoir. Towards morning, there was a return again of excitement, rapid thought and talk, and again it ended in unconsciousness. On the third day it was the same thing, and the doctors said there was hope. That day Alexey Alexandrovitch went into the boudoir where Vronsky was sitting, and closing the door sat down opposite him.\n\n“Alexey Alexandrovitch,” said Vronsky, feeling that a statement of the position was coming, “I can’t speak, I can’t understand. Spare me! However hard it is for you, believe me, it is more terrible for me.”\n\nHe would have risen; but Alexey Alexandrovitch took him by the hand and said:\n\n“I beg you to hear me out; it is necessary. I must explain my feelings, the feelings that have guided me and will guide me, so that you may not be in error regarding me. You know I had resolved on a divorce, and had even begun to take proceedings. I won’t conceal from you that in beginning this I was in uncertainty, I was in misery; I will confess that I was pursued by a desire to revenge myself on you and on her. When I got the telegram, I came here with the same feelings; I will say more, I longed for her death. But....” He paused, pondering whether to disclose or not to disclose his feeling to him. “But I saw her and forgave her. And the happiness of forgiveness has revealed to me my duty. I forgive completely. I would offer the other cheek, I would give my cloak if my coat be taken. I pray to God only not to take from me the bliss of forgiveness!”\n\nTears stood in his eyes, and the luminous, serene look in them impressed Vronsky.\n\n“This is my position: you can trample me in the mud, make me the laughing-stock of the world, I will not abandon her, and I will never utter a word of reproach to you,” Alexey Alexandrovitch went on. “My duty is clearly marked for me; I ought to be with her, and I will be. If she wishes to see you, I will let you know, but now I suppose it would be better for you to go away.”\n\nHe got up, and sobs cut short his words. Vronsky too was getting up, and in a stooping, not yet erect posture, looked up at him from under his brows. He did not understand Alexey Alexandrovitch’s feeling, but he felt that it was something higher and even unattainable for him with his view of life.\n\n# Chapter 18\n\nAfter the conversation with Alexey Alexandrovitch, Vronsky went out onto the steps of the Karenins’ house and stood still, with difficulty remembering where he was, and where he ought to walk or drive. He felt disgraced, humiliated, guilty, and deprived of all possibility of washing away his humiliation. He felt thrust out of the beaten track along which he had so proudly and lightly walked till then. All the habits and rules of his life that had seemed so firm, had turned out suddenly false and inapplicable. The betrayed husband, who had figured till that time as a pitiful creature, an incidental and somewhat ludicrous obstacle to his happiness, had suddenly been summoned by her herself, elevated to an awe-inspiring pinnacle, and on the pinnacle that husband had shown himself, not malignant, not false, not ludicrous, but kind and straightforward and large. Vronsky could not but feel this, and the parts were suddenly reversed. Vronsky felt his elevation and his own abasement, his truth and his own falsehood. He felt that the husband was magnanimous even in his sorrow, while he had been base and petty in his deceit. But this sense of his own humiliation before the man he had unjustly despised made up only a small part of his misery. He felt unutterably wretched now, for his passion for Anna, which had seemed to him of late to be growing cooler, now that he knew he had lost her forever, was stronger than ever it had been. He had seen all of her in her illness, had come to know her very soul, and it seemed to him that he had never loved her till then. And now when he had learned to know her, to love her as she should be loved, he had been humiliated before her, and had lost her forever, leaving with her nothing of himself but a shameful memory. Most terrible of all had been his ludicrous, shameful position when Alexey Alexandrovitch had pulled his hands away from his humiliated face. He stood on the steps of the Karenins’ house like one distraught, and did not know what to do.\n\n“A sledge, sir?” asked the porter.\n\n“Yes, a sledge.”\n\nOn getting home, after three sleepless nights, Vronsky, without undressing, lay down flat on the sofa, clasping his hands and laying his head on them. His head was heavy. Images, memories, and ideas of the strangest description followed one another with extraordinary rapidity and vividness. First it was the medicine he had poured out for the patient and spilt over the spoon, then the midwife’s white hands, then the queer posture of Alexey Alexandrovitch on the floor beside the bed.\n\n“To sleep! To forget!” he said to himself with the serene confidence of a healthy man that if he is tired and sleepy, he will go to sleep at once. And the same instant his head did begin to feel drowsy and he began to drop off into forgetfulness. The waves of the sea of unconsciousness had begun to meet over his head, when all at once—it was as though a violent shock of electricity had passed over him. He started so that he leaped up on the springs of the sofa, and leaning on his arms got in a panic onto his knees. His eyes were wide open as though he had never been asleep. The heaviness in his head and the weariness in his limbs that he had felt a minute before had suddenly gone.\n\n“You may trample me in the mud,” he heard Alexey Alexandrovitch’s words and saw him standing before him, and saw Anna’s face with its burning flush and glittering eyes, gazing with love and tenderness not at him but at Alexey Alexandrovitch; he saw his own, as he fancied, foolish and ludicrous figure when Alexey Alexandrovitch took his hands away from his face. He stretched out his legs again and flung himself on the sofa in the same position and shut his eyes.\n\n“To sleep! To forget!” he repeated to himself. But with his eyes shut he saw more distinctly than ever Anna’s face as it had been on the memorable evening before the races.\n\n“That is not and will not be, and she wants to wipe it out of her memory. But I cannot live without it. How can we be reconciled? how can we be reconciled?” he said aloud, and unconsciously began to repeat these words. This repetition checked the rising up of fresh images and memories, which he felt were thronging in his brain. But repeating words did not check his imagination for long. Again in extraordinarily rapid succession his best moments rose before his mind, and then his recent humiliation. “Take away his hands,” Anna’s voice says. He takes away his hands and feels the shamestruck and idiotic expression of his face.\n\nHe still lay down, trying to sleep, though he felt there was not the smallest hope of it, and kept repeating stray words from some chain of thought, trying by this to check the rising flood of fresh images. He listened, and heard in a strange, mad whisper words repeated: “I did not appreciate it, did not make enough of it. I did not appreciate it, did not make enough of it.”\n\n“What’s this? Am I going out of my mind?” he said to himself. “Perhaps. What makes men go out of their minds; what makes men shoot themselves?” he answered himself, and opening his eyes, he saw with wonder an embroidered cushion beside him, worked by Varya, his brother’s wife. He touched the tassel of the cushion, and tried to think of Varya, of when he had seen her last. But to think of anything extraneous was an agonizing effort. “No, I must sleep!” He moved the cushion up, and pressed his head into it, but he had to make an effort to keep his eyes shut. He jumped up and sat down. “That’s all over for me,” he said to himself. “I must think what to do. What is left?” His mind rapidly ran through his life apart from his love of Anna.\n\n“Ambition? Serpuhovskoy? Society? The court?” He could not come to a pause anywhere. All of it had had meaning before, but now there was no reality in it. He got up from the sofa, took off his coat, undid his belt, and uncovering his hairy chest to breathe more freely, walked up and down the room. “This is how people go mad,” he repeated, “and how they shoot themselves ... to escape humiliation,” he added slowly.\n\nHe went to the door and closed it, then with fixed eyes and clenched teeth he went up to the table, took a revolver, looked round him, turned it to a loaded barrel, and sank into thought. For two minutes, his head bent forward with an expression of an intense effort of thought, he stood with the revolver in his hand, motionless, thinking.\n\n“Of course,” he said to himself, as though a logical, continuous, and clear chain of reasoning had brought him to an indubitable conclusion. In reality this “of course,” that seemed convincing to him, was simply the result of exactly the same circle of memories and images through which he had passed ten times already during the last hour—memories of happiness lost forever. There was the same conception of the senselessness of everything to come in life, the same consciousness of humiliation. Even the sequence of these images and emotions was the same.\n\n“Of course,” he repeated, when for the third time his thought passed again round the same spellbound circle of memories and images, and pulling the revolver to the left side of his chest, and clutching it vigorously with his whole hand, as it were, squeezing it in his fist, he pulled the trigger. He did not hear the sound of the shot, but a violent blow on his chest sent him reeling. He tried to clutch at the edge of the table, dropped the revolver, staggered, and sat down on the ground, looking about him in astonishment. He did not recognize his room, looking up from the ground, at the bent legs of the table, at the wastepaper basket, and the tiger-skin rug. The hurried, creaking steps of his servant coming through the drawing-room brought him to his senses. He made an effort at thought, and was aware that he was on the floor; and seeing blood on the tiger-skin rug and on his arm, he knew he had shot himself.\n\n“Idiotic! Missed!” he said, fumbling after the revolver. The revolver was close beside him—he sought further off. Still feeling for it, he stretched out to the other side, and not being strong enough to keep his balance, fell over, streaming with blood.\n\nThe elegant, whiskered manservant, who used to be continually complaining to his acquaintances of the delicacy of his nerves, was so panic-stricken on seeing his master lying on the floor, that he left him losing blood while he ran for assistance. An hour later Varya, his brother’s wife, had arrived, and with the assistance of three doctors, whom she had sent for in all directions, and who all appeared at the same moment, she got the wounded man to bed, and remained to nurse him.\n\n# Chapter 19\n\nThe mistake made by Alexey Alexandrovitch in that, when preparing for seeing his wife, he had overlooked the possibility that her repentance might be sincere, and he might forgive her, and she might not die—this mistake was two months after his return from Moscow brought home to him in all its significance. But the mistake made by him had arisen not simply from his having overlooked that contingency, but also from the fact that until that day of his interview with his dying wife, he had not known his own heart. At his sick wife’s bedside he had for the first time in his life given way to that feeling of sympathetic suffering always roused in him by the sufferings of others, and hitherto looked on by him with shame as a harmful weakness. And pity for her, and remorse for having desired her death, and most of all, the joy of forgiveness, made him at once conscious, not simply of the relief of his own sufferings, but of a spiritual peace he had never experienced before. He suddenly felt that the very thing that was the source of his sufferings had become the source of his spiritual joy; that what had seemed insoluble while he was judging, blaming, and hating, had become clear and simple when he forgave and loved.\n\nHe forgave his wife and pitied her for her sufferings and her remorse. He forgave Vronsky, and pitied him, especially after reports reached him of his despairing action. He felt more for his son than before. And he blamed himself now for having taken too little interest in him. But for the little newborn baby he felt a quite peculiar sentiment, not of pity, only, but of tenderness. At first, from a feeling of compassion alone, he had been interested in the delicate little creature, who was not his child, and who was cast on one side during her mother’s illness, and would certainly have died if he had not troubled about her, and he did not himself observe how fond he became of her. He would go into the nursery several times a day, and sit there for a long while, so that the nurses, who were at first afraid of him, got quite used to his presence. Sometimes for half an hour at a stretch he would sit silently gazing at the saffron-red, downy, wrinkled face of the sleeping baby, watching the movements of the frowning brows, and the fat little hands, with clenched fingers, that rubbed the little eyes and nose. At such moments particularly, Alexey Alexandrovitch had a sense of perfect peace and inward harmony, and saw nothing extraordinary in his position, nothing that ought to be changed.\n\nBut as time went on, he saw more and more distinctly that however natural the position now seemed to him, he would not long be allowed to remain in it. He felt that besides the blessed spiritual force controlling his soul, there was another, a brutal force, as powerful, or more powerful, which controlled his life, and that this force would not allow him that humble peace he longed for. He felt that everyone was looking at him with inquiring wonder, that he was not understood, and that something was expected of him. Above all, he felt the instability and unnaturalness of his relations with his wife.\n\nWhen the softening effect of the near approach of death had passed away, Alexey Alexandrovitch began to notice that Anna was afraid of him, ill at ease with him, and could not look him straight in the face. She seemed to be wanting, and not daring, to tell him something; and as though foreseeing their present relations could not continue, she seemed to be expecting something from him.\n\nTowards the end of February it happened that Anna’s baby daughter, who had been named Anna too, fell ill. Alexey Alexandrovitch was in the nursery in the morning, and leaving orders for the doctor to be sent for, he went to his office. On finishing his work, he returned home at four. Going into the hall he saw a handsome groom, in a braided livery and a bear fur cape, holding a white fur cloak.\n\n“Who is here?” asked Alexey Alexandrovitch.\n\n“Princess Elizaveta Federovna Tverskaya,” the groom answered, and it seemed to Alexey Alexandrovitch that he grinned.\n\nDuring all this difficult time Alexey Alexandrovitch had noticed that his worldly acquaintances, especially women, took a peculiar interest in him and his wife. All these acquaintances he observed with difficulty concealing their mirth at something; the same mirth that he had perceived in the lawyer’s eyes, and just now in the eyes of this groom. Everyone seemed, somehow, hugely delighted, as though they had just been at a wedding. When they met him, with ill-disguised enjoyment they inquired after his wife’s health. The presence of Princess Tverskaya was unpleasant to Alexey Alexandrovitch from the memories associated with her, and also because he disliked her, and he went straight to the nursery. In the day nursery Seryozha, leaning on the table with his legs on a chair, was drawing and chatting away merrily. The English governess, who had during Anna’s illness replaced the French one, was sitting near the boy knitting a shawl. She hurriedly got up, curtseyed, and pulled Seryozha.\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch stroked his son’s hair, answered the governess’s inquiries about his wife, and asked what the doctor had said of the baby.\n\n“The doctor said it was nothing serious, and he ordered a bath, sir.”\n\n“But she is still in pain,” said Alexey Alexandrovitch, listening to the baby’s screaming in the next room.\n\n“I think it’s the wet-nurse, sir,” the Englishwoman said firmly.\n\n“What makes you think so?” he asked, stopping short.\n\n“It’s just as it was at Countess Paul’s, sir. They gave the baby medicine, and it turned out that the baby was simply hungry: the nurse had no milk, sir.”\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch pondered, and after standing still a few seconds he went in at the other door. The baby was lying with its head thrown back, stiffening itself in the nurse’s arms, and would not take the plump breast offered it; and it never ceased screaming in spite of the double hushing of the wet-nurse and the other nurse, who was bending over her.\n\n“Still no better?” said Alexey Alexandrovitch.\n\n“She’s very restless,” answered the nurse in a whisper.\n\n“Miss Edwarde says that perhaps the wet-nurse has no milk,” he said.\n\n“I think so too, Alexey Alexandrovitch.”\n\n“Then why didn’t you say so?”\n\n“Who’s one to say it to? Anna Arkadyevna still ill....” said the nurse discontentedly.\n\nThe nurse was an old servant of the family. And in her simple words there seemed to Alexey Alexandrovitch an allusion to his position.\n\nThe baby screamed louder than ever, struggling and sobbing. The nurse, with a gesture of despair, went to it, took it from the wet-nurse’s arms, and began walking up and down, rocking it.\n\n“You must ask the doctor to examine the wet-nurse,” said Alexey Alexandrovitch. The smartly dressed and healthy-looking nurse, frightened at the idea of losing her place, muttered something to herself, and covering her bosom, smiled contemptuously at the idea of doubts being cast on her abundance of milk. In that smile, too, Alexey Alexandrovitch saw a sneer at his position.\n\n“Luckless child!” said the nurse, hushing the baby, and still walking up and down with it.\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch sat down, and with a despondent and suffering face watched the nurse walking to and fro.\n\nWhen the child at last was still, and had been put in a deep bed, and the nurse, after smoothing the little pillow, had left her, Alexey Alexandrovitch got up, and walking awkwardly on tiptoe, approached the baby. For a minute he was still, and with the same despondent face gazed at the baby; but all at once a smile, that moved his hair and the skin of his forehead, came out on his face, and he went as softly out of the room.\n\nIn the dining-room he rang the bell, and told the servant who came in to send again for the doctor. He felt vexed with his wife for not being anxious about this exquisite baby, and in this vexed humor he had no wish to go to her; he had no wish, either, to see Princess Betsy. But his wife might wonder why he did not go to her as usual; and so, overcoming his disinclination, he went towards the bedroom. As he walked over the soft rug towards the door, he could not help overhearing a conversation he did not want to hear.\n\n“If he hadn’t been going away, I could have understood your answer and his too. But your husband ought to be above that,” Betsy was saying.\n\n“It’s not for my husband; for myself I don’t wish it. Don’t say that!” answered Anna’s excited voice.\n\n“Yes, but you must care to say good-bye to a man who has shot himself on your account....”\n\n“That’s just why I don’t want to.”\n\nWith a dismayed and guilty expression, Alexey Alexandrovitch stopped and would have gone back unobserved. But reflecting that this would be undignified, he turned back again, and clearing his throat, he went up to the bedroom. The voices were silent, and he went in.\n\nAnna, in a gray dressing gown, with a crop of short clustering black curls on her round head, was sitting on a settee. The eagerness died out of her face, as it always did, at the sight of her husband; she dropped her head and looked round uneasily at Betsy. Betsy, dressed in the height of the latest fashion, in a hat that towered somewhere over her head like a shade on a lamp, in a blue dress with violet crossway stripes slanting one way on the bodice and the other way on the skirt, was sitting beside Anna, her tall flat figure held erect. Bowing her head, she greeted Alexey Alexandrovitch with an ironical smile.\n\n“Ah!” she said, as though surprised. “I’m very glad you’re at home. You never put in an appearance anywhere, and I haven’t seen you ever since Anna has been ill. I have heard all about it—your anxiety. Yes, you’re a wonderful husband!” she said, with a meaning and affable air, as though she were bestowing an order of magnanimity on him for his conduct to his wife.\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch bowed frigidly, and kissing his wife’s hand, asked how she was.\n\n“Better, I think,” she said, avoiding his eyes.\n\n“But you’ve rather a feverish-looking color,” he said, laying stress on the word “feverish.”\n\n“We’ve been talking too much,” said Betsy. “I feel it’s selfishness on my part, and I am going away.”\n\nShe got up, but Anna, suddenly flushing, quickly caught at her hand.\n\n“No, wait a minute, please. I must tell you ... no, you.” she turned to Alexey Alexandrovitch, and her neck and brow were suffused with crimson. “I won’t and can’t keep anything secret from you,” she said.\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch cracked his fingers and bowed his head.\n\n“Betsy’s been telling me that Count Vronsky wants to come here to say good-bye before his departure for Tashkend.” She did not look at her husband, and was evidently in haste to have everything out, however hard it might be for her. “I told her I could not receive him.”\n\n“You said, my dear, that it would depend on Alexey Alexandrovitch,” Betsy corrected her.\n\n“Oh, no, I can’t receive him; and what object would there....” She stopped suddenly, and glanced inquiringly at her husband (he did not look at her). “In short, I don’t wish it....”\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch advanced and would have taken her hand.\n\nHer first impulse was to jerk back her hand from the damp hand with big swollen veins that sought hers, but with an obvious effort to control herself she pressed his hand.\n\n“I am very grateful to you for your confidence, but....” he said, feeling with confusion and annoyance that what he could decide easily and clearly by himself, he could not discuss before Princess Tverskaya, who to him stood for the incarnation of that brute force which would inevitably control him in the life he led in the eyes of the world, and hinder him from giving way to his feeling of love and forgiveness. He stopped short, looking at Princess Tverskaya.\n\n“Well, good-bye, my darling,” said Betsy, getting up. She kissed Anna, and went out. Alexey Alexandrovitch escorted her out.\n\n“Alexey Alexandrovitch! I know you are a truly magnanimous man,” said Betsy, stopping in the little drawing-room, and with special warmth shaking hands with him once more. “I am an outsider, but I so love her and respect you that I venture to advise. Receive him. Alexey Vronsky is the soul of honor, and he is going away to Tashkend.”\n\n“Thank you, princess, for your sympathy and advice. But the question of whether my wife can or cannot see anyone she must decide herself.”\n\nHe said this from habit, lifting his brows with dignity, and reflected immediately that whatever his words might be, there could be no dignity in his position. And he saw this by the suppressed, malicious, and ironical smile with which Betsy glanced at him after this phrase.\n\n# Chapter 20\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch took leave of Betsy in the drawing-room, and went to his wife. She was lying down, but hearing his steps she sat up hastily in her former attitude, and looked in a scared way at him. He saw she had been crying.\n\n“I am very grateful for your confidence in me.” He repeated gently in Russian the phrase he had said in Betsy’s presence in French, and sat down beside her. When he spoke to her in Russian, using the Russian “thou” of intimacy and affection, it was insufferably irritating to Anna. “And I am very grateful for your decision. I, too, imagine that since he is going away, there is no sort of necessity for Count Vronsky to come here. However, if....”\n\n“But I’ve said so already, so why repeat it?” Anna suddenly interrupted him with an irritation she could not succeed in repressing. “No sort of necessity,” she thought, “for a man to come and say good-bye to the woman he loves, for whom he was ready to ruin himself, and has ruined himself, and who cannot live without him. No sort of necessity!” she compressed her lips, and dropped her burning eyes to his hands with their swollen veins. They were rubbing each other.\n\n“Let us never speak of it,” she added more calmly.\n\n“I have left this question to you to decide, and I am very glad to see....” Alexey Alexandrovitch was beginning.\n\n“That my wish coincides with your own,” she finished quickly, exasperated at his talking so slowly while she knew beforehand all he would say.\n\n“Yes,” he assented; “and Princess Tverskaya’s interference in the most difficult private affairs is utterly uncalled for. She especially....”\n\n“I don’t believe a word of what’s said about her,” said Anna quickly. “I know she really cares for me.”\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch sighed and said nothing. She played nervously with the tassel of her dressing-gown, glancing at him with that torturing sensation of physical repulsion for which she blamed herself, though she could not control it. Her only desire now was to be rid of his oppressive presence.\n\n“I have just sent for the doctor,” said Alexey Alexandrovitch.\n\n“I am very well; what do I want the doctor for?”\n\n“No, the little one cries, and they say the nurse hasn’t enough milk.”\n\n“Why didn’t you let me nurse her, when I begged to? Anyway” (Alexey Alexandrovitch knew what was meant by that “anyway”), “she’s a baby, and they’re killing her.” She rang the bell and ordered the baby to be brought her. “I begged to nurse her, I wasn’t allowed to, and now I’m blamed for it.”\n\n“I don’t blame....”\n\n“Yes, you do blame me! My God! why didn’t I die!” And she broke into sobs. “Forgive me, I’m nervous, I’m unjust,” she said, controlling herself, “but do go away....”\n\n“No, it can’t go on like this,” Alexey Alexandrovitch said to himself decidedly as he left his wife’s room.\n\nNever had the impossibility of his position in the world’s eyes, and his wife’s hatred of him, and altogether the might of that mysterious brutal force that guided his life against his spiritual inclinations, and exacted conformity with its decrees and change in his attitude to his wife, been presented to him with such distinctness as that day. He saw clearly that all the world and his wife expected of him something, but what exactly, he could not make out. He felt that this was rousing in his soul a feeling of anger destructive of his peace of mind and of all the good of his achievement. He believed that for Anna herself it would be better to break off all relations with Vronsky; but if they all thought this out of the question, he was even ready to allow these relations to be renewed, so long as the children were not disgraced, and he was not deprived of them nor forced to change his position. Bad as this might be, it was anyway better than a rupture, which would put her in a hopeless and shameful position, and deprive him of everything he cared for. But he felt helpless; he knew beforehand that everyone was against him, and that he would not be allowed to do what seemed to him now so natural and right, but would be forced to do what was wrong, though it seemed the proper thing to them.\n\n# Chapter 21\n\nBefore Betsy had time to walk out of the drawing-room, she was met in the doorway by Stepan Arkadyevitch, who had just come from Yeliseev’s, where a consignment of fresh oysters had been received.\n\n“Ah! princess! what a delightful meeting!” he began. “I’ve been to see you.”\n\n“A meeting for one minute, for I’m going,” said Betsy, smiling and putting on her glove.\n\n“Don’t put on your glove yet, princess; let me kiss your hand. There’s nothing I’m so thankful to the revival of the old fashions for as the kissing the hand.” He kissed Betsy’s hand. “When shall we see each other?”\n\n“You don’t deserve it,” answered Betsy, smiling.\n\n“Oh, yes, I deserve a great deal, for I’ve become a most serious person. I don’t only manage my own affairs, but other people’s too,” he said, with a significant expression.\n\n“Oh, I’m so glad!” answered Betsy, at once understanding that he was speaking of Anna. And going back into the drawing-room, they stood in a corner. “He’s killing her,” said Betsy in a whisper full of meaning. “It’s impossible, impossible....”\n\n“I’m so glad you think so,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, shaking his head with a serious and sympathetically distressed expression, “that’s what I’ve come to Petersburg for.”\n\n“The whole town’s talking of it,” she said. “It’s an impossible position. She pines and pines away. He doesn’t understand that she’s one of those women who can’t trifle with their feelings. One of two things: either let him take her away, act with energy, or give her a divorce. This is stifling her.”\n\n“Yes, yes ... just so....” Oblonsky said, sighing. “That’s what I’ve come for. At least not solely for that ... I’ve been made a Kammerherr; of course, one has to say thank you. But the chief thing was having to settle this.”\n\n“Well, God help you!” said Betsy.\n\nAfter accompanying Betsy to the outside hall, once more kissing her hand above the glove, at the point where the pulse beats, and murmuring to her such unseemly nonsense that she did not know whether to laugh or be angry, Stepan Arkadyevitch went to his sister. He found her in tears.\n\nAlthough he happened to be bubbling over with good spirits, Stepan Arkadyevitch immediately and quite naturally fell into the sympathetic, poetically emotional tone which harmonized with her mood. He asked her how she was, and how she had spent the morning.\n\n“Very, very miserably. Today and this morning and all past days and days to come,” she said.\n\n“I think you’re giving way to pessimism. You must rouse yourself, you must look life in the face. I know it’s hard, but....”\n\n“I have heard it said that women love men even for their vices,” Anna began suddenly, “but I hate him for his virtues. I can’t live with him. Do you understand? the sight of him has a physical effect on me, it makes me beside myself. I can’t, I can’t live with him. What am I to do? I have been unhappy, and used to think one couldn’t be more unhappy, but the awful state of things I am going through now, I could never have conceived. Would you believe it, that knowing he’s a good man, a splendid man, that I’m not worth his little finger, still I hate him. I hate him for his generosity. And there’s nothing left for me but....”\n\nShe would have said death, but Stepan Arkadyevitch would not let her finish.\n\n“You are ill and overwrought,” he said; “believe me, you’re exaggerating dreadfully. There’s nothing so terrible in it.”\n\nAnd Stepan Arkadyevitch smiled. No one else in Stepan Arkadyevitch’s place, having to do with such despair, would have ventured to smile (the smile would have seemed brutal); but in his smile there was so much of sweetness and almost feminine tenderness that his smile did not wound, but softened and soothed. His gentle, soothing words and smiles were as soothing and softening as almond oil. And Anna soon felt this.\n\n“No, Stiva,” she said, “I’m lost, lost! worse than lost! I can’t say yet that all is over; on the contrary, I feel that it’s not over. I’m an overstrained string that must snap. But it’s not ended yet ... and it will have a fearful end.”\n\n“No matter, we must let the string be loosened, little by little. There’s no position from which there is no way of escape.”\n\n“I have thought, and thought. Only one....”\n\nAgain he knew from her terrified eyes that this one way of escape in her thought was death, and he would not let her say it.\n\n“Not at all,” he said. “Listen to me. You can’t see your own position as I can. Let me tell you candidly my opinion.” Again he smiled discreetly his almond-oil smile. “I’ll begin from the beginning. You married a man twenty years older than yourself. You married him without love and not knowing what love was. It was a mistake, let’s admit.”\n\n“A fearful mistake!” said Anna.\n\n“But I repeat, it’s an accomplished fact. Then you had, let us say, the misfortune to love a man not your husband. That was a misfortune; but that, too, is an accomplished fact. And your husband knew it and forgave it.” He stopped at each sentence, waiting for her to object, but she made no answer. “That’s so. Now the question is: can you go on living with your husband? Do you wish it? Does he wish it?”\n\n“I know nothing, nothing.”\n\n“But you said yourself that you can’t endure him.”\n\n“No, I didn’t say so. I deny it. I can’t tell, I don’t know anything about it.”\n\n“Yes, but let....”\n\n“You can’t understand. I feel I’m lying head downwards in a sort of pit, but I ought not to save myself. And I can’t....”\n\n“Never mind, we’ll slip something under and pull you out. I understand you: I understand that you can’t take it on yourself to express your wishes, your feelings.”\n\n“There’s nothing, nothing I wish ... except for it to be all over.”\n\n“But he sees this and knows it. And do you suppose it weighs on him any less than on you? You’re wretched, he’s wretched, and what good can come of it? while divorce would solve the difficulty completely.” With some effort Stepan Arkadyevitch brought out his central idea, and looked significantly at her.\n\nShe said nothing, and shook her cropped head in dissent. But from the look in her face, that suddenly brightened into its old beauty, he saw that if she did not desire this, it was simply because it seemed to her unattainable happiness.\n\n“I’m awfully sorry for you! And how happy I should be if I could arrange things!” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, smiling more boldly. “Don’t speak, don’t say a word! God grant only that I may speak as I feel. I’m going to him.”\n\nAnna looked at him with dreamy, shining eyes, and said nothing.\n\n# Chapter 22\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch, with the same somewhat solemn expression with which he used to take his presidential chair at his board, walked into Alexey Alexandrovitch’s room. Alexey Alexandrovitch was walking about his room with his hands behind his back, thinking of just what Stepan Arkadyevitch had been discussing with his wife.\n\n“I’m not interrupting you?” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, on the sight of his brother-in-law becoming suddenly aware of a sense of embarrassment unusual with him. To conceal this embarrassment he took out a cigarette case he had just bought that opened in a new way, and sniffing the leather, took a cigarette out of it.\n\n“No. Do you want anything?” Alexey Alexandrovitch asked without eagerness.\n\n“Yes, I wished ... I wanted ... yes, I wanted to talk to you,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, with surprise aware of an unaccustomed timidity.\n\nThis feeling was so unexpected and so strange that he did not believe it was the voice of conscience telling him that what he was meaning to do was wrong.\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch made an effort and struggled with the timidity that had come over him.\n\n“I hope you believe in my love for my sister and my sincere affection and respect for you,” he said, reddening.\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch stood still and said nothing, but his face struck Stepan Arkadyevitch by its expression of an unresisting sacrifice.\n\n“I intended ... I wanted to have a little talk with you about my sister and your mutual position,” he said, still struggling with an unaccustomed constraint.\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch smiled mournfully, looked at his brother-in-law, and without answering went up to the table, took from it an unfinished letter, and handed it to his brother-in-law.\n\n“I think unceasingly of the same thing. And here is what I had begun writing, thinking I could say it better by letter, and that my presence irritates her,” he said, as he gave him the letter.\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch took the letter, looked with incredulous surprise at the lusterless eyes fixed so immovably on him, and began to read.\n\n“I see that my presence is irksome to you. Painful as it is to me to believe it, I see that it is so, and cannot be otherwise. I don’t blame you, and God is my witness that on seeing you at the time of your illness I resolved with my whole heart to forget all that had passed between us and to begin a new life. I do not regret, and shall never regret, what I have done; but I have desired one thing—your good, the good of your soul—and now I see I have not attained that. Tell me yourself what will give you true happiness and peace to your soul. I put myself entirely in your hands, and trust to your feeling of what’s right.”\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch handed back the letter, and with the same surprise continued looking at his brother-in-law, not knowing what to say. This silence was so awkward for both of them that Stepan Arkadyevitch’s lips began twitching nervously, while he still gazed without speaking at Karenin’s face.\n\n“That’s what I wanted to say to her,” said Alexey Alexandrovitch, turning away.\n\n“Yes, yes....” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, not able to answer for the tears that were choking him.\n\n“Yes, yes, I understand you,” he brought out at last.\n\n“I want to know what she would like,” said Alexey Alexandrovitch.\n\n“I am afraid she does not understand her own position. She is not a judge,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, recovering himself. “She is crushed, simply crushed by your generosity. If she were to read this letter, she would be incapable of saying anything, she would only hang her head lower than ever.”\n\n“Yes, but what’s to be done in that case? how explain, how find out her wishes?”\n\n“If you will allow me to give my opinion, I think that it lies with you to point out directly the steps you consider necessary to end the position.”\n\n“So you consider it must be ended?” Alexey Alexandrovitch interrupted him. “But how?” he added, with a gesture of his hands before his eyes not usual with him. “I see no possible way out of it.”\n\n“There is some way of getting out of every position,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, standing up and becoming more cheerful. “There was a time when you thought of breaking off.... If you are convinced now that you cannot make each other happy....”\n\n“Happiness may be variously understood. But suppose that I agree to everything, that I want nothing: what way is there of getting out of our position?”\n\n“If you care to know my opinion,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch with the same smile of softening, almond-oil tenderness with which he had been talking to Anna. His kindly smile was so winning that Alexey Alexandrovitch, feeling his own weakness and unconsciously swayed by it, was ready to believe what Stepan Arkadyevitch was saying.\n\n“She will never speak out about it. But one thing is possible, one thing she might desire,” he went on, “that is the cessation of your relations and all memories associated with them. To my thinking, in your position what’s essential is the formation of a new attitude to one another. And that can only rest on a basis of freedom on both sides.”\n\n“Divorce,” Alexey Alexandrovitch interrupted, in a tone of aversion.\n\n“Yes, I imagine that divorce—yes, divorce,” Stepan Arkadyevitch repeated, reddening. “That is from every point of view the most rational course for married people who find themselves in the position you are in. What can be done if married people find that life is impossible for them together? That may always happen.”\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch sighed heavily and closed his eyes.\n\n“There’s only one point to be considered: is either of the parties desirous of forming new ties? If not, it is very simple,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, feeling more and more free from constraint.\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch, scowling with emotion, muttered something to himself, and made no answer. All that seemed so simple to Stepan Arkadyevitch, Alexey Alexandrovitch had thought over thousands of times. And, so far from being simple, it all seemed to him utterly impossible. Divorce, the details of which he knew by this time, seemed to him now out of the question, because the sense of his own dignity and respect for religion forbade his taking upon himself a fictitious charge of adultery, and still more suffering his wife, pardoned and beloved by him, to be caught in the fact and put to public shame. Divorce appeared to him impossible also on other still more weighty grounds.\n\nWhat would become of his son in case of a divorce? To leave him with his mother was out of the question. The divorced mother would have her own illegitimate family, in which his position as a stepson and his education would not be good. Keep him with him? He knew that would be an act of vengeance on his part, and that he did not want. But apart from this, what more than all made divorce seem impossible to Alexey Alexandrovitch was, that by consenting to a divorce he would be completely ruining Anna. The saying of Darya Alexandrovna at Moscow, that in deciding on a divorce he was thinking of himself, and not considering that by this he would be ruining her irrevocably, had sunk into his heart. And connecting this saying with his forgiveness of her, with his devotion to the children, he understood it now in his own way. To consent to a divorce, to give her her freedom, meant in his thoughts to take from himself the last tie that bound him to life—the children whom he loved; and to take from her the last prop that stayed her on the path of right, to thrust her down to her ruin. If she were divorced, he knew she would join her life to Vronsky’s, and their tie would be an illegitimate and criminal one, since a wife, by the interpretation of the ecclesiastical law, could not marry while her husband was living. “She will join him, and in a year or two he will throw her over, or she will form a new tie,” thought Alexey Alexandrovitch. “And I, by agreeing to an unlawful divorce, shall be to blame for her ruin.” He had thought it all over hundreds of times, and was convinced that a divorce was not at all simple, as Stepan Arkadyevitch had said, but was utterly impossible. He did not believe a single word Stepan Arkadyevitch said to him; to every word he had a thousand objections to make, but he listened to him, feeling that his words were the expression of that mighty brutal force which controlled his life and to which he would have to submit.\n\n“The only question is on what terms you agree to give her a divorce. She does not want anything, does not dare ask you for anything, she leaves it all to your generosity.”\n\n“My God, my God! what for?” thought Alexey Alexandrovitch, remembering the details of divorce proceedings in which the husband took the blame on himself, and with just the same gesture with which Vronsky had done the same, he hid his face for shame in his hands.\n\n“You are distressed, I understand that. But if you think it over....”\n\n“Whosoever shall smite thee on thy right cheek, turn to him the other also; and if any man take away thy coat, let him have thy cloak also,” thought Alexey Alexandrovitch.\n\n“Yes, yes!” he cried in a shrill voice. “I will take the disgrace on myself, I will give up even my son, but ... but wouldn’t it be better to let it alone? Still you may do as you like....”\n\nAnd turning away so that his brother-in-law could not see him, he sat down on a chair at the window. There was bitterness, there was shame in his heart, but with bitterness and shame he felt joy and emotion at the height of his own meekness.\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch was touched. He was silent for a space.\n\n“Alexey Alexandrovitch, believe me, she appreciates your generosity,” he said. “But it seems it was the will of God,” he added, and as he said it felt how foolish a remark it was, and with difficulty repressed a smile at his own foolishness.\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch would have made some reply, but tears stopped him.\n\n“This is an unhappy fatality, and one must accept it as such. I accept the calamity as an accomplished fact, and am doing my best to help both her and you,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch.\n\nWhen he went out of his brother-in-law’s room he was touched, but that did not prevent him from being glad he had successfully brought the matter to a conclusion, for he felt certain Alexey Alexandrovitch would not go back on his words. To this satisfaction was added the fact that an idea had just struck him for a riddle turning on his successful achievement, that when the affair was over he would ask his wife and most intimate friends. He put this riddle into two or three different ways. “But I’ll work it out better than that,” he said to himself with a smile.\n\n# Chapter 23\n\nVronsky’s wound had been a dangerous one, though it did not touch the heart, and for several days he had lain between life and death. The first time he was able to speak, Varya, his brother’s wife, was alone in the room.\n\n“Varya,” he said, looking sternly at her, “I shot myself by accident. And please never speak of it, and tell everyone so. Or else it’s too ridiculous.”\n\nWithout answering his words, Varya bent over him, and with a delighted smile gazed into his face. His eyes were clear, not feverish; but their expression was stern.\n\n“Thank God!” she said. “You’re not in pain?”\n\n“A little here.” He pointed to his breast.\n\n“Then let me change your bandages.”\n\nIn silence, stiffening his broad jaws, he looked at her while she bandaged him up. When she had finished he said:\n\n“I’m not delirious. Please manage that there may be no talk of my having shot myself on purpose.”\n\n“No one does say so. Only I hope you won’t shoot yourself by accident any more,” she said, with a questioning smile.\n\n“Of course I won’t, but it would have been better....”\n\nAnd he smiled gloomily.\n\nIn spite of these words and this smile, which so frightened Varya, when the inflammation was over and he began to recover, he felt that he was completely free from one part of his misery. By his action he had, as it were, washed away the shame and humiliation he had felt before. He could now think calmly of Alexey Alexandrovitch. He recognized all his magnanimity, but he did not now feel himself humiliated by it. Besides, he got back again into the beaten track of his life. He saw the possibility of looking men in the face again without shame, and he could live in accordance with his own habits. One thing he could not pluck out of his heart, though he never ceased struggling with it, was the regret, amounting to despair, that he had lost her forever. That now, having expiated his sin against the husband, he was bound to renounce her, and never in future to stand between her with her repentance and her husband, he had firmly decided in his heart; but he could not tear out of his heart his regret at the loss of her love, he could not erase from his memory those moments of happiness that he had so little prized at the time, and that haunted him in all their charm.\n\nSerpuhovskoy had planned his appointment at Tashkend, and Vronsky agreed to the proposition without the slightest hesitation. But the nearer the time of departure came, the bitterer was the sacrifice he was making to what he thought his duty.\n\nHis wound had healed, and he was driving about making preparations for his departure for Tashkend.\n\n“To see her once and then to bury myself, to die,” he thought, and as he was paying farewell visits, he uttered this thought to Betsy. Charged with this commission, Betsy had gone to Anna, and brought him back a negative reply.\n\n“So much the better,” thought Vronsky, when he received the news. “It was a weakness, which would have shattered what strength I have left.”\n\nNext day Betsy herself came to him in the morning, and announced that she had heard through Oblonsky as a positive fact that Alexey Alexandrovitch had agreed to a divorce, and that therefore Vronsky could see Anna.\n\nWithout even troubling himself to see Betsy out of his flat, forgetting all his resolutions, without asking when he could see her, where her husband was, Vronsky drove straight to the Karenins’. He ran up the stairs seeing no one and nothing, and with a rapid step, almost breaking into a run, he went into her room. And without considering, without noticing whether there was anyone in the room or not, he flung his arms round her, and began to cover her face, her hands, her neck with kisses.\n\nAnna had been preparing herself for this meeting, had thought what she would say to him, but she did not succeed in saying anything of it; his passion mastered her. She tried to calm him, to calm herself, but it was too late. His feeling infected her. Her lips trembled so that for a long while she could say nothing.\n\n“Yes, you have conquered me, and I am yours,” she said at last, pressing his hands to her bosom.\n\n“So it had to be,” he said. “So long as we live, it must be so. I know it now.”\n\n“That’s true,” she said, getting whiter and whiter, and embracing his head. “Still there is something terrible in it after all that has happened.”\n\n“It will all pass, it will all pass; we shall be so happy. Our love, if it could be stronger, will be strengthened by there being something terrible in it,” he said, lifting his head and parting his strong teeth in a smile.\n\nAnd she could not but respond with a smile—not to his words, but to the love in his eyes. She took his hand and stroked her chilled cheeks and cropped head with it.\n\n“I don’t know you with this short hair. You’ve grown so pretty. A boy. But how pale you are!”\n\n“Yes, I’m very weak,” she said, smiling. And her lips began trembling again.\n\n“We’ll go to Italy; you will get strong,” he said.\n\n“Can it be possible we could be like husband and wife, alone, your family with you?” she said, looking close into his eyes.\n\n“It only seems strange to me that it can ever have been otherwise.”\n\n“Stiva says that he has agreed to everything, but I can’t accept his generosity,” she said, looking dreamily past Vronsky’s face. “I don’t want a divorce; it’s all the same to me now. Only I don’t know what he will decide about Seryozha.”\n\nHe could not conceive how at this moment of their meeting she could remember and think of her son, of divorce. What did it all matter?\n\n“Don’t speak of that, don’t think of it,” he said, turning her hand in his, and trying to draw her attention to him; but still she did not look at him.\n\n“Oh, why didn’t I die! it would have been better,” she said, and silent tears flowed down both her cheeks; but she tried to smile, so as not to wound him.\n\nTo decline the flattering and dangerous appointment at Tashkend would have been, Vronsky had till then considered, disgraceful and impossible. But now, without an instant’s consideration, he declined it, and observing dissatisfaction in the most exalted quarters at this step, he immediately retired from the army.\n\nA month later Alexey Alexandrovitch was left alone with his son in his house at Petersburg, while Anna and Vronsky had gone abroad, not having obtained a divorce, but having absolutely declined all idea of one.\n\n# PART FIVE Chapter 1\n\nPrincess Shtcherbatskaya considered that it was out of the question for the wedding to take place before Lent, just five weeks off, since not half the trousseau could possibly be ready by that time. But she could not but agree with Levin that to fix it for after Lent would be putting it off too late, as an old aunt of Prince Shtcherbatsky’s was seriously ill and might die, and then the mourning would delay the wedding still longer. And therefore, deciding to divide the trousseau into two parts—a larger and smaller trousseau—the princess consented to have the wedding before Lent. She determined that she would get the smaller part of the trousseau all ready now, and the larger part should be made later, and she was much vexed with Levin because he was incapable of giving her a serious answer to the question whether he agreed to this arrangement or not. The arrangement was the more suitable as, immediately after the wedding, the young people were to go to the country, where the more important part of the trousseau would not be wanted.\n\nLevin still continued in the same delirious condition in which it seemed to him that he and his happiness constituted the chief and sole aim of all existence, and that he need not now think or care about anything, that everything was being done and would be done for him by others. He had not even plans and aims for the future, he left its arrangement to others, knowing that everything would be delightful. His brother Sergey Ivanovitch, Stepan Arkadyevitch, and the princess guided him in doing what he had to do. All he did was to agree entirely with everything suggested to him. His brother raised money for him, the princess advised him to leave Moscow after the wedding. Stepan Arkadyevitch advised him to go abroad. He agreed to everything. “Do what you choose, if it amuses you. I’m happy, and my happiness can be no greater and no less for anything you do,” he thought. When he told Kitty of Stepan Arkadyevitch’s advice that they should go abroad, he was much surprised that she did not agree to this, and had some definite requirements of her own in regard to their future. She knew Levin had work he loved in the country. She did not, as he saw, understand this work, she did not even care to understand it. But that did not prevent her from regarding it as a matter of great importance. And then she knew their home would be in the country, and she wanted to go, not abroad where she was not going to live, but to the place where their home would be. This definitely expressed purpose astonished Levin. But since he did not care either way, he immediately asked Stepan Arkadyevitch, as though it were his duty, to go down to the country and to arrange everything there to the best of his ability with the taste of which he had so much.\n\n“But I say,” Stepan Arkadyevitch said to him one day after he had come back from the country, where he had got everything ready for the young people’s arrival, “have you a certificate of having been at confession?”\n\n“No. But what of it?”\n\n“You can’t be married without it.”\n\n“Aïe, aïe, aïe!” cried Levin. “Why, I believe it’s nine years since I’ve taken the sacrament! I never thought of it.”\n\n“You’re a pretty fellow!” said Stepan Arkadyevitch laughing, “and you call me a Nihilist! But this won’t do, you know. You must take the sacrament.”\n\n“When? There are four days left now.”\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch arranged this also, and Levin had to go to confession. To Levin, as to any unbeliever who respects the beliefs of others, it was exceedingly disagreeable to be present at and take part in church ceremonies. At this moment, in his present softened state of feeling, sensitive to everything, this inevitable act of hypocrisy was not merely painful to Levin, it seemed to him utterly impossible. Now, in the heyday of his highest glory, his fullest flower, he would have to be a liar or a scoffer. He felt incapable of being either. But though he repeatedly plied Stepan Arkadyevitch with questions as to the possibility of obtaining a certificate without actually communicating, Stepan Arkadyevitch maintained that it was out of the question.\n\n“Besides, what is it to you—two days? And he’s an awfully nice clever old fellow. He’ll pull the tooth out for you so gently, you won’t notice it.”\n\nStanding at the first litany, Levin attempted to revive in himself his youthful recollections of the intense religious emotion he had passed through between the ages of sixteen and seventeen.\n\nBut he was at once convinced that it was utterly impossible to him. He attempted to look at it all as an empty custom, having no sort of meaning, like the custom of paying calls. But he felt that he could not do that either. Levin found himself, like the majority of his contemporaries, in the vaguest position in regard to religion. Believe he could not, and at the same time he had no firm conviction that it was all wrong. And consequently, not being able to believe in the significance of what he was doing nor to regard it with indifference as an empty formality, during the whole period of preparing for the sacrament he was conscious of a feeling of discomfort and shame at doing what he did not himself understand, and what, as an inner voice told him, was therefore false and wrong.\n\nDuring the service he would first listen to the prayers, trying to attach some meaning to them not discordant with his own views; then feeling that he could not understand and must condemn them, he tried not to listen to them, but to attend to the thoughts, observations, and memories which floated through his brain with extreme vividness during this idle time of standing in church.\n\nHe had stood through the litany, the evening service and the midnight service, and the next day he got up earlier than usual, and without having tea went at eight o’clock in the morning to the church for the morning service and the confession.\n\nThere was no one in the church but a beggar soldier, two old women, and the church officials. A young deacon, whose long back showed in two distinct halves through his thin undercassock, met him, and at once going to a little table at the wall read the exhortation. During the reading, especially at the frequent and rapid repetition of the same words, “Lord, have mercy on us!” which resounded with an echo, Levin felt that thought was shut and sealed up, and that it must not be touched or stirred now or confusion would be the result; and so standing behind the deacon he went on thinking of his own affairs, neither listening nor examining what was said. “It’s wonderful what expression there is in her hand,” he thought, remembering how they had been sitting the day before at a corner table. They had nothing to talk about, as was almost always the case at this time, and laying her hand on the table she kept opening and shutting it, and laughed herself as she watched her action. He remembered how he had kissed it and then had examined the lines on the pink palm. “Have mercy on us again!” thought Levin, crossing himself, bowing, and looking at the supple spring of the deacon’s back bowing before him. “She took my hand then and examined the lines. ‘You’ve got a splendid hand,’ she said.” And he looked at his own hand and the short hand of the deacon. “Yes, now it will soon be over,” he thought. “No, it seems to be beginning again,” he thought, listening to the prayers. “No, it’s just ending: there he is bowing down to the ground. That’s always at the end.”\n\nThe deacon’s hand in a plush cuff accepted a three-rouble note unobtrusively, and the deacon said he would put it down in the register, and his new boots creaking jauntily over the flagstones of the empty church, he went to the altar. A moment later he peeped out thence and beckoned to Levin. Thought, till then locked up, began to stir in Levin’s head, but he made haste to drive it away. “It will come right somehow,” he thought, and went towards the altar-rails. He went up the steps, and turning to the right saw the priest. The priest, a little old man with a scanty grizzled beard and weary, good-natured eyes, was standing at the altar-rails, turning over the pages of a missal. With a slight bow to Levin he began immediately reading prayers in the official voice. When he had finished them he bowed down to the ground and turned, facing Levin.\n\n“Christ is present here unseen, receiving your confession,” he said, pointing to the crucifix. “Do you believe in all the doctrines of the Holy Apostolic Church?” the priest went on, turning his eyes away from Levin’s face and folding his hands under his stole.\n\n“I have doubted, I doubt everything,” said Levin in a voice that jarred on himself, and he ceased speaking.\n\nThe priest waited a few seconds to see if he would not say more, and closing his eyes he said quickly, with a broad, Vladimirsky accent:\n\n“Doubt is natural to the weakness of mankind, but we must pray that God in His mercy will strengthen us. What are your special sins?” he added, without the slightest interval, as though anxious not to waste time.\n\n“My chief sin is doubt. I have doubts of everything, and for the most part I am in doubt.”\n\n“Doubt is natural to the weakness of mankind,” the priest repeated the same words. “What do you doubt about principally?”\n\n“I doubt of everything. I sometimes even have doubts of the existence of God,” Levin could not help saying, and he was horrified at the impropriety of what he was saying. But Levin’s words did not, it seemed, make much impression on the priest.\n\n“What sort of doubt can there be of the existence of God?” he said hurriedly, with a just perceptible smile.\n\nLevin did not speak.\n\n“What doubt can you have of the Creator when you behold His creation?” the priest went on in the rapid customary jargon. “Who has decked the heavenly firmament with its lights? Who has clothed the earth in its beauty? How explain it without the Creator?” he said, looking inquiringly at Levin.\n\nLevin felt that it would be improper to enter upon a metaphysical discussion with the priest, and so he said in reply merely what was a direct answer to the question.\n\n“I don’t know,” he said.\n\n“You don’t know! Then how can you doubt that God created all?” the priest said, with good-humored perplexity.\n\n“I don’t understand it at all,” said Levin, blushing, and feeling that his words were stupid, and that they could not be anything but stupid in such a position.\n\n“Pray to God and beseech Him. Even the holy fathers had doubts, and prayed to God to strengthen their faith. The devil has great power, and we must resist him. Pray to God, beseech Him. Pray to God,” he repeated hurriedly.\n\nThe priest paused for some time, as though meditating.\n\n“You’re about, I hear, to marry the daughter of my parishioner and son in the spirit, Prince Shtcherbatsky?” he resumed, with a smile. “An excellent young lady.”\n\n“Yes,” answered Levin, blushing for the priest. “What does he want to ask me about this at confession for?” he thought.\n\nAnd, as though answering his thought, the priest said to him:\n\n“You are about to enter into holy matrimony, and God may bless you with offspring. Well, what sort of bringing-up can you give your babes if you do not overcome the temptation of the devil, enticing you to infidelity?” he said, with gentle reproachfulness. “If you love your child as a good father, you will not desire only wealth, luxury, honor for your infant; you will be anxious for his salvation, his spiritual enlightenment with the light of truth. Eh? What answer will you make him when the innocent babe asks you: ‘Papa! who made all that enchants me in this world—the earth, the waters, the sun, the flowers, the grass?’ Can you say to him: ‘I don’t know’? You cannot but know, since the Lord God in His infinite mercy has revealed it to us. Or your child will ask you: ‘What awaits me in the life beyond the tomb?’ What will you say to him when you know nothing? How will you answer him? Will you leave him to the allurements of the world and the devil? That’s not right,” he said, and he stopped, putting his head on one side and looking at Levin with his kindly, gentle eyes.\n\nLevin made no answer this time, not because he did not want to enter upon a discussion with the priest, but because, so far, no one had ever asked him such questions, and when his babes did ask him those questions, it would be time enough to think about answering them.\n\n“You are entering upon a time of life,” pursued the priest, “when you must choose your path and keep to it. Pray to God that He may in His mercy aid you and have mercy on you!” he concluded. “Our Lord and God, Jesus Christ, in the abundance and riches of His loving-kindness, forgives this child....” and, finishing the prayer of absolution, the priest blessed him and dismissed him.\n\nOn getting home that day, Levin had a delightful sense of relief at the awkward position being over and having been got through without his having to tell a lie. Apart from this, there remained a vague memory that what the kind, nice old fellow had said had not been at all so stupid as he had fancied at first, and that there was something in it that must be cleared up.\n\n“Of course, not now,” thought Levin, “but some day later on.” Levin felt more than ever now that there was something not clear and not clean in his soul, and that, in regard to religion, he was in the same position which he perceived so clearly and disliked in others, and for which he blamed his friend Sviazhsky.\n\nLevin spent that evening with his betrothed at Dolly’s, and was in very high spirits. To explain to Stepan Arkadyevitch the state of excitement in which he found himself, he said that he was happy like a dog being trained to jump through a hoop, who, having at last caught the idea, and done what was required of him, whines and wags its tail, and jumps up to the table and the windows in its delight.\n\n# Chapter 2\n\nOn the day of the wedding, according to the Russian custom (the princess and Darya Alexandrovna insisted on strictly keeping all the customs), Levin did not see his betrothed, and dined at his hotel with three bachelor friends, casually brought together at his rooms. These were Sergey Ivanovitch, Katavasov, a university friend, now professor of natural science, whom Levin had met in the street and insisted on taking home with him, and Tchirikov, his best man, a Moscow conciliation-board judge, Levin’s companion in his bear-hunts. The dinner was a very merry one: Sergey Ivanovitch was in his happiest mood, and was much amused by Katavasov’s originality. Katavasov, feeling his originality was appreciated and understood, made the most of it. Tchirikov always gave a lively and good-humored support to conversation of any sort.\n\n“See, now,” said Katavasov, drawling his words from a habit acquired in the lecture-room, “what a capable fellow was our friend Konstantin Dmitrievitch. I’m not speaking of present company, for he’s absent. At the time he left the university he was fond of science, took an interest in humanity; now one-half of his abilities is devoted to deceiving himself, and the other to justifying the deceit.”\n\n“A more determined enemy of matrimony than you I never saw,” said Sergey Ivanovitch.\n\n“Oh, no, I’m not an enemy of matrimony. I’m in favor of division of labor. People who can do nothing else ought to rear people while the rest work for their happiness and enlightenment. That’s how I look at it. To muddle up two trades is the error of the amateur; I’m not one of their number.”\n\n“How happy I shall be when I hear that you’re in love!” said Levin. “Please invite me to the wedding.”\n\n“I’m in love now.”\n\n“Yes, with a cuttlefish! You know,” Levin turned to his brother, “Mihail Semyonovitch is writing a work on the digestive organs of the....”\n\n“Now, make a muddle of it! It doesn’t matter what about. And the fact is, I certainly do love cuttlefish.”\n\n“But that’s no hindrance to your loving your wife.”\n\n“The cuttlefish is no hindrance. The wife is the hindrance.”\n\n“Why so?”\n“Oh, you’ll see! You care about farming, hunting,—well, you’d better look out!”\n\n“Arhip was here today; he said there were a lot of elks in Prudno, and two bears,” said Tchirikov.\n\n“Well, you must go and get them without me.”\n\n“Ah, that’s the truth,” said Sergey Ivanovitch. “And you may say good-bye to bear-hunting for the future—your wife won’t allow it!”\n\nLevin smiled. The picture of his wife not letting him go was so pleasant that he was ready to renounce the delights of looking upon bears forever.\n\n“Still, it’s a pity they should get those two bears without you. Do you remember last time at Hapilovo? That was a delightful hunt!” said Tchirikov.\n\nLevin had not the heart to disillusion him of the notion that there could be something delightful apart from her, and so said nothing.\n\n“There’s some sense in this custom of saying good-bye to bachelor life,” said Sergey Ivanovitch. “However happy you may be, you must regret your freedom.”\n\n“And confess there is a feeling that you want to jump out of the window, like Gogol’s bridegroom?”\n\n“Of course there is, but it isn’t confessed,” said Katavasov, and he broke into loud laughter.\n\n“Oh, well, the window’s open. Let’s start off this instant to Tver! There’s a big she-bear; one can go right up to the lair. Seriously, let’s go by the five o’clock! And here let them do what they like,” said Tchirikov, smiling.\n\n“Well, now, on my honor,” said Levin, smiling, “I can’t find in my heart that feeling of regret for my freedom.”\n\n“Yes, there’s such a chaos in your heart just now that you can’t find anything there,” said Katavasov. “Wait a bit, when you set it to rights a little, you’ll find it!”\n\n“No; if so, I should have felt a little, apart from my feeling” (he could not say love before them) “and happiness, a certain regret at losing my freedom.... On the contrary, I am glad at the very loss of my freedom.”\n\n“Awful! It’s a hopeless case!” said Katavasov. “Well, let’s drink to his recovery, or wish that a hundredth part of his dreams may be realized—and that would be happiness such as never has been seen on earth!”\n\nSoon after dinner the guests went away to be in time to be dressed for the wedding.\n\nWhen he was left alone, and recalled the conversation of these bachelor friends, Levin asked himself: had he in his heart that regret for his freedom of which they had spoken? He smiled at the question. “Freedom! What is freedom for? Happiness is only in loving and wishing her wishes, thinking her thoughts, that is to say, not freedom at all—that’s happiness!”\n\n“But do I know her ideas, her wishes, her feelings?” some voice suddenly whispered to him. The smile died away from his face, and he grew thoughtful. And suddenly a strange feeling came upon him. There came over him a dread and doubt—doubt of everything.\n\n“What if she does not love me? What if she’s marrying me simply to be married? What if she doesn’t see herself what she’s doing?” he asked himself. “She may come to her senses, and only when she is being married realize that she does not and cannot love me.” And strange, most evil thoughts of her began to come to him. He was jealous of Vronsky, as he had been a year ago, as though the evening he had seen her with Vronsky had been yesterday. He suspected she had not told him everything.\n\nHe jumped up quickly. “No, this can’t go on!” he said to himself in despair. “I’ll go to her; I’ll ask her; I’ll say for the last time: we are free, and hadn’t we better stay so? Anything’s better than endless misery, disgrace, unfaithfulness!” With despair in his heart and bitter anger against all men, against himself, against her, he went out of the hotel and drove to her house.\n\nHe found her in one of the back rooms. She was sitting on a chest and making some arrangements with her maid, sorting over heaps of dresses of different colors, spread on the backs of chairs and on the floor.\n\n“Ah!” she cried, seeing him, and beaming with delight. “Kostya! Konstantin Dmitrievitch!” (These latter days she used these names almost alternately.) “I didn’t expect you! I’m going through my wardrobe to see what’s for whom....”\n\n“Oh! that’s very nice!” he said gloomily, looking at the maid.\n\n“You can go, Dunyasha, I’ll call you presently,” said Kitty. “Kostya, what’s the matter?” she asked, definitely adopting this familiar name as soon as the maid had gone out. She noticed his strange face, agitated and gloomy, and a panic came over her.\n\n“Kitty! I’m in torture. I can’t suffer alone,” he said with despair in his voice, standing before her and looking imploringly into her eyes. He saw already from her loving, truthful face, that nothing could come of what he had meant to say, but yet he wanted her to reassure him herself. “I’ve come to say that there’s still time. This can all be stopped and set right.”\n\n“What? I don’t understand. What is the matter?”\n\n“What I have said a thousand times over, and can’t help thinking ... that I’m not worthy of you. You couldn’t consent to marry me. Think a little. You’ve made a mistake. Think it over thoroughly. You can’t love me.... If ... better say so,” he said, not looking at her. “I shall be wretched. Let people say what they like; anything’s better than misery.... Far better now while there’s still time....”\n\n“I don’t understand,” she answered, panic-stricken; “you mean you want to give it up ... don’t want it?”\n\n“Yes, if you don’t love me.”\n\n“You’re out of your mind!” she cried, turning crimson with vexation. But his face was so piteous, that she restrained her vexation, and flinging some clothes off an armchair, she sat down beside him. “What are you thinking? tell me all.”\n\n“I am thinking you can’t love me. What can you love me for?”\n\n“My God! what can I do?...” she said, and burst into tears.\n\n“Oh! what have I done?” he cried, and kneeling before her, he fell to kissing her hands.\n\nWhen the princess came into the room five minutes later, she found them completely reconciled. Kitty had not simply assured him that she loved him, but had gone so far—in answer to his question, what she loved him for—as to explain what for. She told him that she loved him because she understood him completely, because she knew what he would like, and because everything he liked was good. And this seemed to him perfectly clear. When the princess came to them, they were sitting side by side on the chest, sorting the dresses and disputing over Kitty’s wanting to give Dunyasha the brown dress she had been wearing when Levin proposed to her, while he insisted that that dress must never be given away, but Dunyasha must have the blue one.\n\n“How is it you don’t see? She’s a brunette, and it won’t suit her.... I’ve worked it all out.”\n\nHearing why he had come, the princess was half humorously, half seriously angry with him, and sent him home to dress and not to hinder Kitty’s hair-dressing, as Charles the hair-dresser was just coming.\n\n“As it is, she’s been eating nothing lately and is losing her looks, and then you must come and upset her with your nonsense,” she said to him. “Get along with you, my dear!”\n\nLevin, guilty and shamefaced, but pacified, went back to his hotel. His brother, Darya Alexandrovna, and Stepan Arkadyevitch, all in full dress, were waiting for him to bless him with the holy picture. There was no time to lose. Darya Alexandrovna had to drive home again to fetch her curled and pomaded son, who was to carry the holy pictures after the bride. Then a carriage had to be sent for the best man, and another that would take Sergey Ivanovitch away would have to be sent back.... Altogether there were a great many most complicated matters to be considered and arranged. One thing was unmistakable, that there must be no delay, as it was already half-past six.\n\nNothing special happened at the ceremony of benediction with the holy picture. Stepan Arkadyevitch stood in a comically solemn pose beside his wife, took the holy picture, and telling Levin to bow down to the ground, he blessed him with his kindly, ironical smile, and kissed him three times; Darya Alexandrovna did the same, and immediately was in a hurry to get off, and again plunged into the intricate question of the destinations of the various carriages.\n\n“Come, I’ll tell you how we’ll manage: you drive in our carriage to fetch him, and Sergey Ivanovitch, if he’ll be so good, will drive there and then send his carriage.”\n\n“Of course; I shall be delighted.”\n\n“We’ll come on directly with him. Are your things sent off?” said Stepan Arkadyevitch.\n\n“Yes,” answered Levin, and he told Kouzma to put out his clothes for him to dress.\n\n# Chapter 3\n\nA crowd of people, principally women, was thronging round the church lighted up for the wedding. Those who had not succeeded in getting into the main entrance were crowding about the windows, pushing, wrangling, and peeping through the gratings.\n\nMore than twenty carriages had already been drawn up in ranks along the street by the police. A police officer, regardless of the frost, stood at the entrance, gorgeous in his uniform. More carriages were continually driving up, and ladies wearing flowers and carrying their trains, and men taking off their helmets or black hats kept walking into the church. Inside the church both lusters were already lighted, and all the candles before the holy pictures. The gilt on the red ground of the holy picture-stand, and the gilt relief on the pictures, and the silver of the lusters and candlesticks, and the stones of the floor, and the rugs, and the banners above in the choir, and the steps of the altar, and the old blackened books, and the cassocks and surplices—all were flooded with light. On the right side of the warm church, in the crowd of frock coats and white ties, uniforms and broadcloth, velvet, satin, hair and flowers, bare shoulders and arms and long gloves, there was discreet but lively conversation that echoed strangely in the high cupola. Every time there was heard the creak of the opened door the conversation in the crowd died away, and everybody looked round expecting to see the bride and bridegroom come in. But the door had opened more than ten times, and each time it was either a belated guest or guests, who joined the circle of the invited on the right, or a spectator, who had eluded or softened the police officer, and went to join the crowd of outsiders on the left. Both the guests and the outside public had by now passed through all the phases of anticipation.\n\nAt first they imagined that the bride and bridegroom would arrive immediately, and attached no importance at all to their being late. Then they began to look more and more often towards the door, and to talk of whether anything could have happened. Then the long delay began to be positively discomforting, and relations and guests tried to look as if they were not thinking of the bridegroom but were engrossed in conversation.\n\nThe head deacon, as though to remind them of the value of his time, coughed impatiently, making the window-panes quiver in their frames. In the choir the bored choristers could be heard trying their voices and blowing their noses. The priest was continually sending first the beadle and then the deacon to find out whether the bridegroom had not come, more and more often he went himself, in a lilac vestment and an embroidered sash, to the side door, expecting to see the bridegroom. At last one of the ladies, glancing at her watch, said, “It really is strange, though!” and all the guests became uneasy and began loudly expressing their wonder and dissatisfaction. One of the bridegroom’s best men went to find out what had happened. Kitty meanwhile had long ago been quite ready, and in her white dress and long veil and wreath of orange blossoms she was standing in the drawing-room of the Shtcherbatskys’ house with her sister, Madame Lvova, who was her bridal-mother. She was looking out of the window, and had been for over half an hour anxiously expecting to hear from the best man that her bridegroom was at the church.\n\nLevin meanwhile, in his trousers, but without his coat and waistcoat, was walking to and fro in his room at the hotel, continually putting his head out of the door and looking up and down the corridor. But in the corridor there was no sign of the person he was looking for and he came back in despair, and frantically waving his hands addressed Stepan Arkadyevitch, who was smoking serenely.\n\n“Was ever a man in such a fearful fool’s position?” he said.\n\n“Yes, it is stupid,” Stepan Arkadyevitch assented, smiling soothingly. “But don’t worry, it’ll be brought directly.”\n\n“No, what is to be done!” said Levin, with smothered fury. “And these fools of open waistcoats! Out of the question!” he said, looking at the crumpled front of his shirt. “And what if the things have been taken on to the railway station!” he roared in desperation.\n\n“Then you must put on mine.”\n\n“I ought to have done so long ago, if at all.”\n\n“It’s not nice to look ridiculous.... Wait a bit! it will come round.”\n\nThe point was that when Levin asked for his evening suit, Kouzma, his old servant, had brought him the coat, waistcoat, and everything that was wanted.\n\n“But the shirt!” cried Levin.\n\n“You’ve got a shirt on,” Kouzma answered, with a placid smile.\n\nKouzma had not thought of leaving out a clean shirt, and on receiving instructions to pack up everything and send it round to the Shtcherbatskys’ house, from which the young people were to set out the same evening, he had done so, packing everything but the dress suit. The shirt worn since the morning was crumpled and out of the question with the fashionable open waistcoat. It was a long way to send to the Shtcherbatskys’. They sent out to buy a shirt. The servant came back; everything was shut up—it was Sunday. They sent to Stepan Arkadyevitch’s and brought a shirt—it was impossibly wide and short. They sent finally to the Shtcherbatskys’ to unpack the things. The bridegroom was expected at the church while he was pacing up and down his room like a wild beast in a cage, peeping out into the corridor, and with horror and despair recalling what absurd things he had said to Kitty and what she might be thinking now.\n\nAt last the guilty Kouzma flew panting into the room with the shirt.\n\n“Only just in time. They were just lifting it into the van,” said Kouzma.\n\nThree minutes later Levin ran full speed into the corridor, not looking at his watch for fear of aggravating his sufferings.\n\n“You won’t help matters like this,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch with a smile, hurrying with more deliberation after him. “It will come round, it will come round ... I tell you.”\n\n# Chapter 4\n\n“They’ve come!” “Here he is!” “Which one?” “Rather young, eh?” “Why, my dear soul, she looks more dead than alive!” were the comments in the crowd, when Levin, meeting his bride in the entrance, walked with her into the church.\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch told his wife the cause of the delay, and the guests were whispering it with smiles to one another. Levin saw nothing and no one; he did not take his eyes off his bride.\n\nEveryone said she had lost her looks dreadfully of late, and was not nearly so pretty on her wedding day as usual; but Levin did not think so. He looked at her hair done up high, with the long white veil and white flowers and the high, stand-up, scalloped collar, that in such a maidenly fashion hid her long neck at the sides and only showed it in front, her strikingly slender figure, and it seemed to him that she looked better than ever—not because these flowers, this veil, this gown from Paris added anything to her beauty; but because, in spite of the elaborate sumptuousness of her attire, the expression of her sweet face, of her eyes, of her lips was still her own characteristic expression of guileless truthfulness.\n\n“I was beginning to think you meant to run away,” she said, and smiled to him.\n\n“It’s so stupid, what happened to me, I’m ashamed to speak of it!” he said, reddening, and he was obliged to turn to Sergey Ivanovitch, who came up to him.\n\n“This is a pretty story of yours about the shirt!” said Sergey Ivanovitch, shaking his head and smiling.\n\n“Yes, yes!” answered Levin, without an idea of what they were talking about.\n\n“Now, Kostya, you have to decide,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch with an air of mock dismay, “a weighty question. You are at this moment just in the humor to appreciate all its gravity. They ask me, are they to light the candles that have been lighted before or candles that have never been lighted? It’s a matter of ten roubles,” he added, relaxing his lips into a smile. “I have decided, but I was afraid you might not agree.”\n\nLevin saw it was a joke, but he could not smile.\n\n“Well, how’s it to be then?—unlighted or lighted candles? that’s the question.”\n\n“Yes, yes, unlighted.”\n\n“Oh, I’m very glad. The question’s decided!” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, smiling. “How silly men are, though, in this position,” he said to Tchirikov, when Levin, after looking absently at him, had moved back to his bride.\n\n“Kitty, mind you’re the first to step on the carpet,” said Countess Nordston, coming up. “You’re a nice person!” she said to Levin.\n\n“Aren’t you frightened, eh?” said Marya Dmitrievna, an old aunt.\n\n“Are you cold? You’re pale. Stop a minute, stoop down,” said Kitty’s sister, Madame Lvova, and with her plump, handsome arms she smilingly set straight the flowers on her head.\n\nDolly came up, tried to say something, but could not speak, cried, and then laughed unnaturally.\n\nKitty looked at all of them with the same absent eyes as Levin.\n\nMeanwhile the officiating clergy had got into their vestments, and the priest and deacon came out to the lectern, which stood in the forepart of the church. The priest turned to Levin saying something. Levin did not hear what the priest said.\n\n“Take the bride’s hand and lead her up,” the best man said to Levin.\n\nIt was a long while before Levin could make out what was expected of him. For a long time they tried to set him right and made him begin again—because he kept taking Kitty by the wrong arm or with the wrong arm—till he understood at last that what he had to do was, without changing his position, to take her right hand in his right hand. When at last he had taken the bride’s hand in the correct way, the priest walked a few paces in front of them and stopped at the lectern. The crowd of friends and relations moved after them, with a buzz of talk and a rustle of skirts. Someone stooped down and pulled out the bride’s train. The church became so still that the drops of wax could be heard falling from the candles.\n\nThe little old priest in his ecclesiastical cap, with his long silvery-gray locks of hair parted behind his ears, was fumbling with something at the lectern, putting out his little old hands from under the heavy silver vestment with the gold cross on the back of it.\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch approached him cautiously, whispered something, and making a sign to Levin, walked back again.\n\nThe priest lighted two candles, wreathed with flowers, and holding them sideways so that the wax dropped slowly from them he turned, facing the bridal pair. The priest was the same old man that had confessed Levin. He looked with weary and melancholy eyes at the bride and bridegroom, sighed, and putting his right hand out from his vestment, blessed the bridegroom with it, and also with a shade of solicitous tenderness laid the crossed fingers on the bowed head of Kitty. Then he gave them the candles, and taking the censer, moved slowly away from them.\n\n“Can it be true?” thought Levin, and he looked round at his bride. Looking down at her he saw her face in profile, and from the scarcely perceptible quiver of her lips and eyelashes he knew she was aware of his eyes upon her. She did not look round, but the high scalloped collar, that reached her little pink ear, trembled faintly. He saw that a sigh was held back in her throat, and the little hand in the long glove shook as it held the candle.\n\nAll the fuss of the shirt, of being late, all the talk of friends and relations, their annoyance, his ludicrous position—all suddenly passed away and he was filled with joy and dread.\n\nThe handsome, stately head-deacon wearing a silver robe and his curly locks standing out at each side of his head, stepped smartly forward, and lifting his stole on two fingers, stood opposite the priest.\n\n“Blessed be the name of the Lord,” the solemn syllables rang out slowly one after another, setting the air quivering with waves of sound.\n\n“Blessed is the name of our God, from the beginning, is now, and ever shall be,” the little old priest answered in a submissive, piping voice, still fingering something at the lectern. And the full chorus of the unseen choir rose up, filling the whole church, from the windows to the vaulted roof, with broad waves of melody. It grew stronger, rested for an instant, and slowly died away.\n\nThey prayed, as they always do, for peace from on high and for salvation, for the Holy Synod, and for the Tsar; they prayed, too, for the servants of God, Konstantin and Ekaterina, now plighting their troth.\n\n“Vouchsafe to them love made perfect, peace and help, O Lord, we beseech Thee,” the whole church seemed to breathe with the voice of the head deacon.\n\nLevin heard the words, and they impressed him. “How did they guess that it is help, just help that one wants?” he thought, recalling all his fears and doubts of late. “What do I know? what can I do in this fearful business,” he thought, “without help? Yes, it is help I want now.”\n\nWhen the deacon had finished the prayer for the Imperial family, the priest turned to the bridal pair with a book: “Eternal God, that joinest together in love them that were separate,” he read in a gentle, piping voice: “who hast ordained the union of holy wedlock that cannot be set asunder, Thou who didst bless Isaac and Rebecca and their descendants, according to Thy Holy Covenant; bless Thy servants, Konstantin and Ekaterina, leading them in the path of all good works. For gracious and merciful art Thou, our Lord, and glory be to Thee, the Father, the Son, and the Holy Ghost, now and ever shall be.”\n\n“Amen!” the unseen choir sent rolling again upon the air.\n\n“‘Joinest together in love them that were separate.’ What deep meaning in those words, and how they correspond with what one feels at this moment,” thought Levin. “Is she feeling the same as I?”\n\nAnd looking round, he met her eyes, and from their expression he concluded that she was understanding it just as he was. But this was a mistake; she almost completely missed the meaning of the words of the service; she had not heard them, in fact. She could not listen to them and take them in, so strong was the one feeling that filled her breast and grew stronger and stronger. That feeling was joy at the completion of the process that for the last month and a half had been going on in her soul, and had during those six weeks been a joy and a torture to her. On the day when in the drawing-room of the house in Arbaty Street she had gone up to him in her brown dress, and given herself to him without a word—on that day, at that hour, there took place in her heart a complete severance from all her old life, and a quite different, new, utterly strange life had begun for her, while the old life was actually going on as before. Those six weeks had for her been a time of the utmost bliss and the utmost misery. All her life, all her desires and hopes were concentrated on this one man, still uncomprehended by her, to whom she was bound by a feeling of alternate attraction and repulsion, even less comprehended than the man himself, and all the while she was going on living in the outward conditions of her old life. Living the old life, she was horrified at herself, at her utter insurmountable callousness to all her own past, to things, to habits, to the people she had loved, who loved her—to her mother, who was wounded by her indifference, to her kind, tender father, till then dearer than all the world. At one moment she was horrified at this indifference, at another she rejoiced at what had brought her to this indifference. She could not frame a thought, not a wish apart from life with this man; but this new life was not yet, and she could not even picture it clearly to herself. There was only anticipation, the dread and joy of the new and the unknown. And now behold—anticipation and uncertainty and remorse at the abandonment of the old life—all was ending, and the new was beginning. This new life could not but have terrors for her inexperience; but, terrible or not, the change had been wrought six weeks before in her soul, and this was merely the final sanction of what had long been completed in her heart.\n\nTurning again to the lectern, the priest with some difficulty took Kitty’s little ring, and asking Levin for his hand, put it on the first joint of his finger. “The servant of God, Konstantin, plights his troth to the servant of God, Ekaterina.” And putting his big ring on Kitty’s touchingly weak, pink little finger, the priest said the same thing.\n\nAnd the bridal pair tried several times to understand what they had to do, and each time made some mistake and were corrected by the priest in a whisper. At last, having duly performed the ceremony, having signed the rings with the cross, the priest handed Kitty the big ring, and Levin the little one. Again they were puzzled, and passed the rings from hand to hand, still without doing what was expected.\n\nDolly, Tchirikov, and Stepan Arkadyevitch stepped forward to set them right. There was an interval of hesitation, whispering, and smiles; but the expression of solemn emotion on the faces of the betrothed pair did not change: on the contrary, in their perplexity over their hands they looked more grave and deeply moved than before, and the smile with which Stepan Arkadyevitch whispered to them that now they would each put on their own ring died away on his lips. He had a feeling that any smile would jar on them.\n\n“Thou who didst from the beginning create male and female,” the priest read after the exchange of rings, “from Thee woman was given to man to be a helpmeet to him, and for the procreation of children. O Lord, our God, who hast poured down the blessings of Thy Truth according to Thy Holy Covenant upon Thy chosen servants, our fathers, from generation to generation, bless Thy servants Konstantin and Ekaterina, and make their troth fast in faith, and union of hearts, and truth, and love....”\n\nLevin felt more and more that all his ideas of marriage, all his dreams of how he would order his life, were mere childishness, and that it was something he had not understood hitherto, and now understood less than ever, though it was being performed upon him. The lump in his throat rose higher and higher, tears that would not be checked came into his eyes.\n\n# Chapter 5\n\nIn the church there was all Moscow, all the friends and relations; and during the ceremony of plighting troth, in the brilliantly lighted church, there was an incessant flow of discreetly subdued talk in the circle of gaily dressed women and girls, and men in white ties, frockcoats, and uniforms. The talk was principally kept up by the men, while the women were absorbed in watching every detail of the ceremony, which always means so much to them.\n\nIn the little group nearest to the bride were her two sisters: Dolly, and the other one, the self-possessed beauty, Madame Lvova, who had just arrived from abroad.\n\n“Why is it Marie’s in lilac, as bad as black, at a wedding?” said Madame Korsunskaya.\n\n“With her complexion, it’s the one salvation,” responded Madame Trubetskaya. “I wonder why they had the wedding in the evening? It’s like shop-people....”\n\n“So much prettier. I was married in the evening too....” answered Madame Korsunskaya, and she sighed, remembering how charming she had been that day, and how absurdly in love her husband was, and how different it all was now.\n\n“They say if anyone’s best man more than ten times, he’ll never be married. I wanted to be for the tenth time, but the post was taken,” said Count Siniavin to the pretty Princess Tcharskaya, who had designs on him.\n\nPrincess Tcharskaya only answered with a smile. She looked at Kitty, thinking how and when she would stand with Count Siniavin in Kitty’s place, and how she would remind him then of his joke today.\n\nShtcherbatsky told the old maid of honor, Madame Nikolaeva, that he meant to put the crown on Kitty’s chignon for luck.\n\n“She ought not to have worn a chignon,” answered Madame Nikolaeva, who had long ago made up her mind that if the elderly widower she was angling for married her, the wedding should be of the simplest. “I don’t like such grandeur.”\n\nSergey Ivanovitch was talking to Darya Dmitrievna, jestingly assuring her that the custom of going away after the wedding was becoming common because newly married people always felt a little ashamed of themselves.\n\n“Your brother may feel proud of himself. She’s a marvel of sweetness. I believe you’re envious.”\n\n“Oh, I’ve got over that, Darya Dmitrievna,” he answered, and a melancholy and serious expression suddenly came over his face.\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch was telling his sister-in-law his joke about divorce.\n\n“The wreath wants setting straight,” she answered, not hearing him.\n\n“What a pity she’s lost her looks so,” Countess Nordston said to Madame Lvova. “Still he’s not worth her little finger, is he?”\n\n“Oh, I like him so—not because he’s my future beau-frère,” answered Madame Lvova. “And how well he’s behaving! It’s so difficult, too, to look well in such a position, not to be ridiculous. And he’s not ridiculous, and not affected; one can see he’s moved.”\n\n“You expected it, I suppose?”\n\n“Almost. She always cared for him.”\n\n“Well, we shall see which of them will step on the rug first. I warned Kitty.”\n\n“It will make no difference,” said Madame Lvova; “we’re all obedient wives; it’s in our family.”\n\n“Oh, I stepped on the rug before Vassily on purpose. And you, Dolly?”\n\nDolly stood beside them; she heard them, but she did not answer. She was deeply moved. The tears stood in her eyes, and she could not have spoken without crying. She was rejoicing over Kitty and Levin; going back in thought to her own wedding, she glanced at the radiant figure of Stepan Arkadyevitch, forgot all the present, and remembered only her own innocent love. She recalled not herself only, but all her women-friends and acquaintances. She thought of them on the one day of their triumph, when they had stood like Kitty under the wedding crown, with love and hope and dread in their hearts, renouncing the past, and stepping forward into the mysterious future. Among the brides that came back to her memory, she thought too of her darling Anna, of whose proposed divorce she had just been hearing. And she had stood just as innocent in orange flowers and bridal veil. And now? “It’s terribly strange,” she said to herself. It was not merely the sisters, the women-friends and female relations of the bride who were following every detail of the ceremony. Women who were quite strangers, mere spectators, were watching it excitedly, holding their breath, in fear of losing a single movement or expression of the bride and bridegroom, and angrily not answering, often not hearing, the remarks of the callous men, who kept making joking or irrelevant observations.\n\n“Why has she been crying? Is she being married against her will?”\n\n“Against her will to a fine fellow like that? A prince, isn’t he?”\n\n“Is that her sister in the white satin? Just listen how the deacon booms out, ‘And fearing her husband.’”\n\n“Are the choristers from Tchudovo?”\n\n“No, from the Synod.”\n\n“I asked the footman. He says he’s going to take her home to his country place at once. Awfully rich, they say. That’s why she’s being married to him.”\n\n“No, they’re a well-matched pair.”\n\n“I say, Marya Vassilievna, you were making out those fly-away crinolines were not being worn. Just look at her in the puce dress—an ambassador’s wife they say she is—how her skirt bounces out from side to side!”\n\n“What a pretty dear the bride is—like a lamb decked with flowers! Well, say what you will, we women feel for our sister.”\n\nSuch were the comments in the crowd of gazing women who had succeeded in slipping in at the church doors.\n\n# Chapter 6\n\nWhen the ceremony of plighting troth was over, the beadle spread before the lectern in the middle of the church a piece of pink silken stuff, the choir sang a complicated and elaborate psalm, in which the bass and tenor sang responses to one another, and the priest turning round pointed the bridal pair to the pink silk rug. Though both had often heard a great deal about the saying that the one who steps first on the rug will be the head of the house, neither Levin nor Kitty were capable of recollecting it, as they took the few steps towards it. They did not hear the loud remarks and disputes that followed, some maintaining he had stepped on first, and others that both had stepped on together.\n\nAfter the customary questions, whether they desired to enter upon matrimony, and whether they were pledged to anyone else, and their answers, which sounded strange to themselves, a new ceremony began. Kitty listened to the words of the prayer, trying to make out their meaning, but she could not. The feeling of triumph and radiant happiness flooded her soul more and more as the ceremony went on, and deprived her of all power of attention.\n\nThey prayed: “Endow them with continence and fruitfulness, and vouchsafe that their hearts may rejoice looking upon their sons and daughters.” They alluded to God’s creation of a wife from Adam’s rib “and for this cause a man shall leave father and mother, and cleave unto his wife, and they two shall be one flesh,” and that “this is a great mystery”; they prayed that God would make them fruitful and bless them, like Isaac and Rebecca, Joseph, Moses and Zipporah, and that they might look upon their children’s children. “That’s all splendid,” thought Kitty, catching the words, “all that’s just as it should be,” and a smile of happiness, unconsciously reflected in everyone who looked at her, beamed on her radiant face.\n\n“Put it on quite,” voices were heard urging when the priest had put on the wedding crowns and Shtcherbatsky, his hand shaking in its three-button glove, held the crown high above her head.\n\n“Put it on!” she whispered, smiling.\n\nLevin looked round at her, and was struck by the joyful radiance on her face, and unconsciously her feeling infected him. He too, like her felt glad and happy.\n\nThey enjoyed hearing the epistle read, and the roll of the head deacon’s voice at the last verse, awaited with such impatience by the outside public. They enjoyed drinking out of the shallow cup of warm red wine and water, and they were still more pleased when the priest, flinging back his stole and taking both their hands in his, led them round the lectern to the accompaniment of bass voices chanting “Glory to God.”\n\nShtcherbatsky and Tchirikov, supporting the crowns and stumbling over the bride’s train, smiling too and seeming delighted at something, were at one moment left behind, at the next treading on the bridal pair as the priest came to a halt. The spark of joy kindled in Kitty seemed to have infected everyone in the church. It seemed to Levin that the priest and the deacon too wanted to smile just as he did.\n\nTaking the crowns off their heads the priest read the last prayer and congratulated the young people. Levin looked at Kitty, and he had never before seen her look as she did. She was charming with the new radiance of happiness in her face. Levin longed to say something to her, but he did not know whether it was all over. The priest got him out of his difficulty. He smiled his kindly smile and said gently, “Kiss your wife, and you kiss your husband,” and took the candles out of their hands.\n\nLevin kissed her smiling lips with timid care, gave her his arm, and with a new strange sense of closeness, walked out of the church. He did not believe, he could not believe, that it was true. It was only when their wondering and timid eyes met that he believed in it, because he felt that they were one.\n\nAfter supper, the same night, the young people left for the country.\n\n# Chapter 7\n\nVronsky and Anna had been traveling for three months together in Europe. They had visited Venice, Rome, and Naples, and had just arrived at a small Italian town where they meant to stay some time. A handsome head waiter, with thick pomaded hair parted from the neck upwards, an evening coat, a broad white cambric shirt front, and a bunch of trinkets hanging above his rounded stomach, stood with his hands in the full curve of his pockets, looking contemptuously from under his eyelids while he gave some frigid reply to a gentleman who had stopped him. Catching the sound of footsteps coming from the other side of the entry towards the staircase, the head waiter turned round, and seeing the Russian count, who had taken their best rooms, he took his hands out of his pockets deferentially, and with a bow informed him that a courier had been, and that the business about the palazzo had been arranged. The steward was prepared to sign the agreement.\n\n“Ah! I’m glad to hear it,” said Vronsky. “Is madame at home or not?”\n\n“Madame has been out for a walk but has returned now,” answered the waiter.\n\nVronsky took off his soft, wide-brimmed hat and passed his handkerchief over his heated brow and hair, which had grown half over his ears, and was brushed back covering the bald patch on his head. And glancing casually at the gentleman, who still stood there gazing intently at him, he would have gone on.\n\n“This gentleman is a Russian, and was inquiring after you,” said the head waiter.\n\nWith mingled feelings of annoyance at never being able to get away from acquaintances anywhere, and longing to find some sort of diversion from the monotony of his life, Vronsky looked once more at the gentleman, who had retreated and stood still again, and at the same moment a light came into the eyes of both.\n\n“Golenishtchev!”\n\n“Vronsky!”\n\nIt really was Golenishtchev, a comrade of Vronsky’s in the Corps of Pages. In the corps Golenishtchev had belonged to the liberal party; he left the corps without entering the army, and had never taken office under the government. Vronsky and he had gone completely different ways on leaving the corps, and had only met once since.\n\nAt that meeting Vronsky perceived that Golenishtchev had taken up a sort of lofty, intellectually liberal line, and was consequently disposed to look down upon Vronsky’s interests and calling in life. Hence Vronsky had met him with the chilling and haughty manner he so well knew how to assume, the meaning of which was: “You may like or dislike my way of life, that’s a matter of the most perfect indifference to me; you will have to treat me with respect if you want to know me.” Golenishtchev had been contemptuously indifferent to the tone taken by Vronsky. This second meeting might have been expected, one would have supposed, to estrange them still more. But now they beamed and exclaimed with delight on recognizing one another. Vronsky would never have expected to be so pleased to see Golenishtchev, but probably he was not himself aware how bored he was. He forgot the disagreeable impression of their last meeting, and with a face of frank delight held out his hand to his old comrade. The same expression of delight replaced the look of uneasiness on Golenishtchev’s face.\n\n“How glad I am to meet you!” said Vronsky, showing his strong white teeth in a friendly smile.\n\n“I heard the name Vronsky, but I didn’t know which one. I’m very, very glad!”\n\n“Let’s go in. Come, tell me what you’re doing.”\n\n“I’ve been living here for two years. I’m working.”\n\n“Ah!” said Vronsky, with sympathy; “let’s go in.” And with the habit common with Russians, instead of saying in Russian what he wanted to keep from the servants, he began to speak in French.\n\n“Do you know Madame Karenina? We are traveling together. I am going to see her now,” he said in French, carefully scrutinizing Golenishtchev’s face.\n\n“Ah! I did not know” (though he did know), Golenishtchev answered carelessly. “Have you been here long?” he added.\n\n“Four days,” Vronsky answered, once more scrutinizing his friend’s face intently.\n\n“Yes, he’s a decent fellow, and will look at the thing properly,” Vronsky said to himself, catching the significance of Golenishtchev’s face and the change of subject. “I can introduce him to Anna, he looks at it properly.”\n\nDuring those three months that Vronsky had spent abroad with Anna, he had always on meeting new people asked himself how the new person would look at his relations with Anna, and for the most part, in men, he had met with the “proper” way of looking at it. But if he had been asked, and those who looked at it “properly” had been asked, exactly how they did look at it, both he and they would have been greatly puzzled to answer.\n\nIn reality, those who in Vronsky’s opinion had the “proper” view had no sort of view at all, but behaved in general as well-bred persons do behave in regard to all the complex and insoluble problems with which life is encompassed on all sides; they behaved with propriety, avoiding allusions and unpleasant questions. They assumed an air of fully comprehending the import and force of the situation, of accepting and even approving of it, but of considering it superfluous and uncalled for to put all this into words.\n\nVronsky at once divined that Golenishtchev was of this class, and therefore was doubly pleased to see him. And in fact, Golenishtchev’s manner to Madame Karenina, when he was taken to call on her, was all that Vronsky could have desired. Obviously without the slightest effort he steered clear of all subjects which might lead to embarrassment.\n\nHe had never met Anna before, and was struck by her beauty, and still more by the frankness with which she accepted her position. She blushed when Vronsky brought in Golenishtchev, and he was extremely charmed by this childish blush overspreading her candid and handsome face. But what he liked particularly was the way in which at once, as though on purpose that there might be no misunderstanding with an outsider, she called Vronsky simply Alexey, and said they were moving into a house they had just taken, what was here called a palazzo. Golenishtchev liked this direct and simple attitude to her own position. Looking at Anna’s manner of simple-hearted, spirited gaiety, and knowing Alexey Alexandrovitch and Vronsky, Golenishtchev fancied that he understood her perfectly. He fancied that he understood what she was utterly unable to understand: how it was that, having made her husband wretched, having abandoned him and her son and lost her good name, she yet felt full of spirits, gaiety, and happiness.\n\n“It’s in the guide-book,” said Golenishtchev, referring to the palazzo Vronsky had taken. “There’s a first-rate Tintoretto there. One of his latest period.”\n\n“I tell you what: it’s a lovely day, let’s go and have another look at it,” said Vronsky, addressing Anna.\n\n“I shall be very glad to; I’ll go and put on my hat. Would you say it’s hot?” she said, stopping short in the doorway and looking inquiringly at Vronsky. And again a vivid flush overspread her face.\n\nVronsky saw from her eyes that she did not know on what terms he cared to be with Golenishtchev, and so was afraid of not behaving as he would wish.\n\nHe looked a long, tender look at her.\n\n“No, not very,” he said.\n\nAnd it seemed to her that she understood everything, most of all, that he was pleased with her; and smiling to him, she walked with her rapid step out at the door.\n\nThe friends glanced at one another, and a look of hesitation came into both faces, as though Golenishtchev, unmistakably admiring her, would have liked to say something about her, and could not find the right thing to say, while Vronsky desired and dreaded his doing so.\n\n“Well then,” Vronsky began to start a conversation of some sort; “so you’re settled here? You’re still at the same work, then?” he went on, recalling that he had been told Golenishtchev was writing something.\n\n“Yes, I’m writing the second part of the Two Elements,” said Golenishtchev, coloring with pleasure at the question—“that is, to be exact, I am not writing it yet; I am preparing, collecting materials. It will be of far wider scope, and will touch on almost all questions. We in Russia refuse to see that we are the heirs of Byzantium,” and he launched into a long and heated explanation of his views.\n\nVronsky at the first moment felt embarrassed at not even knowing of the first part of the Two Elements, of which the author spoke as something well known. But as Golenishtchev began to lay down his opinions and Vronsky was able to follow them even without knowing the Two Elements, he listened to him with some interest, for Golenishtchev spoke well. But Vronsky was startled and annoyed by the nervous irascibility with which Golenishtchev talked of the subject that engrossed him. As he went on talking, his eyes glittered more and more angrily; he was more and more hurried in his replies to imaginary opponents, and his face grew more and more excited and worried. Remembering Golenishtchev, a thin, lively, good-natured and well-bred boy, always at the head of the class, Vronsky could not make out the reason of his irritability, and he did not like it. What he particularly disliked was that Golenishtchev, a man belonging to a good set, should put himself on a level with some scribbling fellows, with whom he was irritated and angry. Was it worth it? Vronsky disliked it, yet he felt that Golenishtchev was unhappy, and was sorry for him. Unhappiness, almost mental derangement, was visible on his mobile, rather handsome face, while without even noticing Anna’s coming in, he went on hurriedly and hotly expressing his views.\n\nWhen Anna came in in her hat and cape, and her lovely hand rapidly swinging her parasol, and stood beside him, it was with a feeling of relief that Vronsky broke away from the plaintive eyes of Golenishtchev which fastened persistently upon him, and with a fresh rush of love looked at his charming companion, full of life and happiness. Golenishtchev recovered himself with an effort, and at first was dejected and gloomy, but Anna, disposed to feel friendly with everyone as she was at that time, soon revived his spirits by her direct and lively manner. After trying various subjects of conversation, she got him upon painting, of which he talked very well, and she listened to him attentively. They walked to the house they had taken, and looked over it.\n\n“I am very glad of one thing,” said Anna to Golenishtchev when they were on their way back, “Alexey will have a capital atelier. You must certainly take that room,” she said to Vronsky in Russian, using the affectionately familiar form as though she saw that Golenishtchev would become intimate with them in their isolation, and that there was no need of reserve before him.\n\n“Do you paint?” said Golenishtchev, turning round quickly to Vronsky.\n\n“Yes, I used to study long ago, and now I have begun to do a little,” said Vronsky, reddening.\n\n“He has great talent,” said Anna with a delighted smile. “I’m no judge, of course. But good judges have said the same.”\n\n# Chapter 8\n\nAnna, in that first period of her emancipation and rapid return to health, felt herself unpardonably happy and full of the joy of life. The thought of her husband’s unhappiness did not poison her happiness. On one side that memory was too awful to be thought of. On the other side her husband’s unhappiness had given her too much happiness to be regretted. The memory of all that had happened after her illness: her reconciliation with her husband, its breakdown, the news of Vronsky’s wound, his visit, the preparations for divorce, the departure from her husband’s house, the parting from her son—all that seemed to her like a delirious dream, from which she had waked up alone with Vronsky abroad. The thought of the harm caused to her husband aroused in her a feeling like repulsion, and akin to what a drowning man might feel who has shaken off another man clinging to him. That man did drown. It was an evil action, of course, but it was the sole means of escape, and better not to brood over these fearful facts.\n\nOne consolatory reflection upon her conduct had occurred to her at the first moment of the final rupture, and when now she recalled all the past, she remembered that one reflection. “I have inevitably made that man wretched,” she thought; “but I don’t want to profit by his misery. I too am suffering, and shall suffer; I am losing what I prized above everything—I am losing my good name and my son. I have done wrong, and so I don’t want happiness, I don’t want a divorce, and shall suffer from my shame and the separation from my child.” But, however sincerely Anna had meant to suffer, she was not suffering. Shame there was not. With the tact of which both had such a large share, they had succeeded in avoiding Russian ladies abroad, and so had never placed themselves in a false position, and everywhere they had met people who pretended that they perfectly understood their position, far better indeed than they did themselves. Separation from the son she loved—even that did not cause her anguish in these early days. The baby girl—his child—was so sweet, and had so won Anna’s heart, since she was all that was left her, that Anna rarely thought of her son.\n\nThe desire for life, waxing stronger with recovered health, was so intense, and the conditions of life were so new and pleasant, that Anna felt unpardonably happy. The more she got to know Vronsky, the more she loved him. She loved him for himself, and for his love for her. Her complete ownership of him was a continual joy to her. His presence was always sweet to her. All the traits of his character, which she learned to know better and better, were unutterably dear to her. His appearance, changed by his civilian dress, was as fascinating to her as though she were some young girl in love. In everything he said, thought, and did, she saw something particularly noble and elevated. Her adoration of him alarmed her indeed; she sought and could not find in him anything not fine. She dared not show him her sense of her own insignificance beside him. It seemed to her that, knowing this, he might sooner cease to love her; and she dreaded nothing now so much as losing his love, though she had no grounds for fearing it. But she could not help being grateful to him for his attitude to her, and showing that she appreciated it. He, who had in her opinion such a marked aptitude for a political career, in which he would have been certain to play a leading part—he had sacrificed his ambition for her sake, and never betrayed the slightest regret. He was more lovingly respectful to her than ever, and the constant care that she should not feel the awkwardness of her position never deserted him for a single instant. He, so manly a man, never opposed her, had indeed, with her, no will of his own, and was anxious, it seemed, for nothing but to anticipate her wishes. And she could not but appreciate this, even though the very intensity of his solicitude for her, the atmosphere of care with which he surrounded her, sometimes weighed upon her.\n\nVronsky, meanwhile, in spite of the complete realization of what he had so long desired, was not perfectly happy. He soon felt that the realization of his desires gave him no more than a grain of sand out of the mountain of happiness he had expected. It showed him the mistake men make in picturing to themselves happiness as the realization of their desires. For a time after joining his life to hers, and putting on civilian dress, he had felt all the delight of freedom in general of which he had known nothing before, and of freedom in his love,—and he was content, but not for long. He was soon aware that there was springing up in his heart a desire for desires—ennui. Without conscious intention he began to clutch at every passing caprice, taking it for a desire and an object. Sixteen hours of the day must be occupied in some way, since they were living abroad in complete freedom, outside the conditions of social life which filled up time in Petersburg. As for the amusements of bachelor existence, which had provided Vronsky with entertainment on previous tours abroad, they could not be thought of, since the sole attempt of the sort had led to a sudden attack of depression in Anna, quite out of proportion with the cause—a late supper with bachelor friends. Relations with the society of the place—foreign and Russian—were equally out of the question owing to the irregularity of their position. The inspection of objects of interest, apart from the fact that everything had been seen already, had not for Vronsky, a Russian and a sensible man, the immense significance Englishmen are able to attach to that pursuit.\n\nAnd just as the hungry stomach eagerly accepts every object it can get, hoping to find nourishment in it, Vronsky quite unconsciously clutched first at politics, then at new books, and then at pictures.\n\nAs he had from a child a taste for painting, and as, not knowing what to spend his money on, he had begun collecting engravings, he came to a stop at painting, began to take interest in it, and concentrated upon it the unoccupied mass of desires which demanded satisfaction.\n\nHe had a ready appreciation of art, and probably, with a taste for imitating art, he supposed himself to have the real thing essential for an artist, and after hesitating for some time which style of painting to select—religious, historical, realistic, or genre painting—he set to work to paint. He appreciated all kinds, and could have felt inspired by anyone of them; but he had no conception of the possibility of knowing nothing at all of any school of painting, and of being inspired directly by what is within the soul, without caring whether what is painted will belong to any recognized school. Since he knew nothing of this, and drew his inspiration, not directly from life, but indirectly from life embodied in art, his inspiration came very quickly and easily, and as quickly and easily came his success in painting something very similar to the sort of painting he was trying to imitate.\n\nMore than any other style he liked the French—graceful and effective—and in that style he began to paint Anna’s portrait in Italian costume, and the portrait seemed to him, and to everyone who saw it, extremely successful.\n\n# Chapter 9\n\nThe old neglected palazzo, with its lofty carved ceilings and frescoes on the walls, with its floors of mosaic, with its heavy yellow stuff curtains on the windows, with its vases on pedestals, and its open fireplaces, its carved doors and gloomy reception rooms, hung with pictures—this palazzo did much, by its very appearance after they had moved into it, to confirm in Vronsky the agreeable illusion that he was not so much a Russian country gentleman, a retired army officer, as an enlightened amateur and patron of the arts, himself a modest artist who had renounced the world, his connections, and his ambition for the sake of the woman he loved.\n\nThe pose chosen by Vronsky with their removal into the palazzo was completely successful, and having, through Golenishtchev, made acquaintance with a few interesting people, for a time he was satisfied. He painted studies from nature under the guidance of an Italian professor of painting, and studied mediæval Italian life. Mediæval Italian life so fascinated Vronsky that he even wore a hat and flung a cloak over his shoulder in the mediæval style, which, indeed, was extremely becoming to him.\n\n“Here we live, and know nothing of what’s going on,” Vronsky said to Golenishtchev as he came to see him one morning. “Have you seen Mihailov’s picture?” he said, handing him a Russian gazette he had received that morning, and pointing to an article on a Russian artist, living in the very same town, and just finishing a picture which had long been talked about, and had been bought beforehand. The article reproached the government and the academy for letting so remarkable an artist be left without encouragement and support.\n\n“I’ve seen it,” answered Golenishtchev. “Of course, he’s not without talent, but it’s all in a wrong direction. It’s all the Ivanov-Strauss-Renan attitude to Christ and to religious painting.”\n\n“What is the subject of the picture?” asked Anna.\n\n“Christ before Pilate. Christ is represented as a Jew with all the realism of the new school.”\n\nAnd the question of the subject of the picture having brought him to one of his favorite theories, Golenishtchev launched forth into a disquisition on it.\n\n“I can’t understand how they can fall into such a gross mistake. Christ always has His definite embodiment in the art of the great masters. And therefore, if they want to depict, not God, but a revolutionist or a sage, let them take from history a Socrates, a Franklin, a Charlotte Corday, but not Christ. They take the very figure which cannot be taken for their art, and then....”\n\n“And is it true that this Mihailov is in such poverty?” asked Vronsky, thinking that, as a Russian Mæcenas, it was his duty to assist the artist regardless of whether the picture were good or bad.\n\n“I should say not. He’s a remarkable portrait-painter. Have you ever seen his portrait of Madame Vassiltchikova? But I believe he doesn’t care about painting any more portraits, and so very likely he is in want. I maintain that....”\n\n“Couldn’t we ask him to paint a portrait of Anna Arkadyevna?” said Vronsky.\n\n“Why mine?” said Anna. “After yours I don’t want another portrait. Better have one of Annie” (so she called her baby girl). “Here she is,” she added, looking out of the window at the handsome Italian nurse, who was carrying the child out into the garden, and immediately glancing unnoticed at Vronsky. The handsome nurse, from whom Vronsky was painting a head for his picture, was the one hidden grief in Anna’s life. He painted with her as his model, admired her beauty and mediævalism, and Anna dared not confess to herself that she was afraid of becoming jealous of this nurse, and was for that reason particularly gracious and condescending both to her and her little son. Vronsky, too, glanced out of the window and into Anna’s eyes, and, turning at once to Golenishtchev, he said:\n\n“Do you know this Mihailov?”\n\n“I have met him. But he’s a queer fish, and quite without breeding. You know, one of those uncouth new people one’s so often coming across nowadays, one of those free-thinkers you know, who are reared d’emblée in theories of atheism, scepticism, and materialism. In former days,” said Golenishtchev, not observing, or not willing to observe, that both Anna and Vronsky wanted to speak, “in former days the free-thinker was a man who had been brought up in ideas of religion, law, and morality, and only through conflict and struggle came to free-thought; but now there has sprung up a new type of born free-thinkers who grow up without even having heard of principles of morality or of religion, of the existence of authorities, who grow up directly in ideas of negation in everything, that is to say, savages. Well, he’s of that class. He’s the son, it appears, of some Moscow butler, and has never had any sort of bringing-up. When he got into the academy and made his reputation he tried, as he’s no fool, to educate himself. And he turned to what seemed to him the very source of culture—the magazines. In old times, you see, a man who wanted to educate himself—a Frenchman, for instance—would have set to work to study all the classics and theologians and tragedians and historians and philosophers, and, you know, all the intellectual work that came in his way. But in our day he goes straight for the literature of negation, very quickly assimilates all the extracts of the science of negation, and he’s ready. And that’s not all—twenty years ago he would have found in that literature traces of conflict with authorities, with the creeds of the ages; he would have perceived from this conflict that there was something else; but now he comes at once upon a literature in which the old creeds do not even furnish matter for discussion, but it is stated baldly that there is nothing else—evolution, natural selection, struggle for existence—and that’s all. In my article I’ve....”\n\n“I tell you what,” said Anna, who had for a long while been exchanging wary glances with Vronsky, and knew that he was not in the least interested in the education of this artist, but was simply absorbed by the idea of assisting him, and ordering a portrait of him; “I tell you what,” she said, resolutely interrupting Golenishtchev, who was still talking away, “let’s go and see him!”\n\nGolenishtchev recovered his self-possession and readily agreed. But as the artist lived in a remote suburb, it was decided to take the carriage.\n\nAn hour later Anna, with Golenishtchev by her side and Vronsky on the front seat of the carriage, facing them, drove up to a new ugly house in the remote suburb. On learning from the porter’s wife, who came out to them, that Mihailov saw visitors at his studio, but that at that moment he was in his lodging only a couple of steps off, they sent her to him with their cards, asking permission to see his picture.\n\n# Chapter 10\n\nThe artist Mihailov was, as always, at work when the cards of Count Vronsky and Golenishtchev were brought to him. In the morning he had been working in his studio at his big picture. On getting home he flew into a rage with his wife for not having managed to put off the landlady, who had been asking for money.\n\n“I’ve said it to you twenty times, don’t enter into details. You’re fool enough at all times, and when you start explaining things in Italian you’re a fool three times as foolish,” he said after a long dispute.\n\n“Don’t let it run so long; it’s not my fault. If I had the money....”\n\n“Leave me in peace, for God’s sake!” Mihailov shrieked, with tears in his voice, and, stopping his ears, he went off into his working room, the other side of a partition wall, and closed the door after him. “Idiotic woman!” he said to himself, sat down to the table, and, opening a portfolio, he set to work at once with peculiar fervor at a sketch he had begun.\n\nNever did he work with such fervor and success as when things went ill with him, and especially when he quarreled with his wife. “Oh! damn them all!” he thought as he went on working. He was making a sketch for the figure of a man in a violent rage. A sketch had been made before, but he was dissatisfied with it. “No, that one was better ... where is it?” He went back to his wife, and scowling, and not looking at her, asked his eldest little girl, where was that piece of paper he had given them? The paper with the discarded sketch on it was found, but it was dirty, and spotted with candle-grease. Still, he took the sketch, laid it on his table, and, moving a little away, screwing up his eyes, he fell to gazing at it. All at once he smiled and gesticulated gleefully.\n\n“That’s it! that’s it!” he said, and, at once picking up the pencil, he began rapidly drawing. The spot of tallow had given the man a new pose.\n\nHe had sketched this new pose, when all at once he recalled the face of a shopkeeper of whom he had bought cigars, a vigorous face with a prominent chin, and he sketched this very face, this chin on to the figure of the man. He laughed aloud with delight. The figure from a lifeless imagined thing had become living, and such that it could never be changed. That figure lived, and was clearly and unmistakably defined. The sketch might be corrected in accordance with the requirements of the figure, the legs, indeed, could and must be put differently, and the position of the left hand must be quite altered; the hair too might be thrown back. But in making these corrections he was not altering the figure but simply getting rid of what concealed the figure. He was, as it were, stripping off the wrappings which hindered it from being distinctly seen. Each new feature only brought out the whole figure in all its force and vigor, as it had suddenly come to him from the spot of tallow. He was carefully finishing the figure when the cards were brought him.\n\n“Coming, coming!”\n\nHe went in to his wife.\n\n“Come, Sasha, don’t be cross!” he said, smiling timidly and affectionately at her. “You were to blame. I was to blame. I’ll make it all right.” And having made peace with his wife he put on an olive-green overcoat with a velvet collar and a hat, and went towards his studio. The successful figure he had already forgotten. Now he was delighted and excited at the visit of these people of consequence, Russians, who had come in their carriage.\n\nOf his picture, the one that stood now on his easel, he had at the bottom of his heart one conviction—that no one had ever painted a picture like it. He did not believe that his picture was better than all the pictures of Raphael, but he knew that what he tried to convey in that picture, no one ever had conveyed. This he knew positively, and had known a long while, ever since he had begun to paint it. But other people’s criticisms, whatever they might be, had yet immense consequence in his eyes, and they agitated him to the depths of his soul. Any remark, the most insignificant, that showed that the critic saw even the tiniest part of what he saw in the picture, agitated him to the depths of his soul. He always attributed to his critics a more profound comprehension than he had himself, and always expected from them something he did not himself see in the picture. And often in their criticisms he fancied that he had found this.\n\nHe walked rapidly to the door of his studio, and in spite of his excitement he was struck by the soft light on Anna’s figure as she stood in the shade of the entrance listening to Golenishtchev, who was eagerly telling her something, while she evidently wanted to look round at the artist. He was himself unconscious how, as he approached them, he seized on this impression and absorbed it, as he had the chin of the shopkeeper who had sold him the cigars, and put it away somewhere to be brought out when he wanted it. The visitors, not agreeably impressed beforehand by Golenishtchev’s account of the artist, were still less so by his personal appearance. Thick-set and of middle height, with nimble movements, with his brown hat, olive-green coat and narrow trousers—though wide trousers had been a long while in fashion,—most of all, with the ordinariness of his broad face, and the combined expression of timidity and anxiety to keep up his dignity, Mihailov made an unpleasant impression.\n\n“Please step in,” he said, trying to look indifferent, and going into the passage he took a key out of his pocket and opened the door.\n\n# Chapter 11\n\nOn entering the studio, Mihailov once more scanned his visitors and noted down in his imagination Vronsky’s expression too, and especially his jaws. Although his artistic sense was unceasingly at work collecting materials, although he felt a continually increasing excitement as the moment of criticizing his work drew nearer, he rapidly and subtly formed, from imperceptible signs, a mental image of these three persons.\n\nThat fellow (Golenishtchev) was a Russian living here. Mihailov did not remember his surname nor where he had met him, nor what he had said to him. He only remembered his face as he remembered all the faces he had ever seen; but he remembered, too, that it was one of the faces laid by in his memory in the immense class of the falsely consequential and poor in expression. The abundant hair and very open forehead gave an appearance of consequence to the face, which had only one expression—a petty, childish, peevish expression, concentrated just above the bridge of the narrow nose. Vronsky and Madame Karenina must be, Mihailov supposed, distinguished and wealthy Russians, knowing nothing about art, like all those wealthy Russians, but posing as amateurs and connoisseurs. “Most likely they’ve already looked at all the antiques, and now they’re making the round of the studios of the new people, the German humbug, and the cracked Pre-Raphaelite English fellow, and have only come to me to make the point of view complete,” he thought. He was well acquainted with the way dilettanti have (the cleverer they were the worse he found them) of looking at the works of contemporary artists with the sole object of being in a position to say that art is a thing of the past, and that the more one sees of the new men the more one sees how inimitable the works of the great old masters have remained. He expected all this; he saw it all in their faces, he saw it in the careless indifference with which they talked among themselves, stared at the lay figures and busts, and walked about in leisurely fashion, waiting for him to uncover his picture. But in spite of this, while he was turning over his studies, pulling up the blinds and taking off the sheet, he was in intense excitement, especially as, in spite of his conviction that all distinguished and wealthy Russians were certain to be beasts and fools, he liked Vronsky, and still more Anna.\n\n“Here, if you please,” he said, moving on one side with his nimble gait and pointing to his picture, “it’s the exhortation to Pilate. Matthew, chapter xxvii,” he said, feeling his lips were beginning to tremble with emotion. He moved away and stood behind them.\n\nFor the few seconds during which the visitors were gazing at the picture in silence Mihailov too gazed at it with the indifferent eye of an outsider. For those few seconds he was sure in anticipation that a higher, juster criticism would be uttered by them, by those very visitors whom he had been so despising a moment before. He forgot all he had thought about his picture before during the three years he had been painting it; he forgot all its qualities which had been absolutely certain to him—he saw the picture with their indifferent, new, outside eyes, and saw nothing good in it. He saw in the foreground Pilate’s irritated face and the serene face of Christ, and in the background the figures of Pilate’s retinue and the face of John watching what was happening. Every face that, with such agony, such blunders and corrections had grown up within him with its special character, every face that had given him such torments and such raptures, and all these faces so many times transposed for the sake of the harmony of the whole, all the shades of color and tones that he had attained with such labor—all of this together seemed to him now, looking at it with their eyes, the merest vulgarity, something that had been done a thousand times over. The face dearest to him, the face of Christ, the center of the picture, which had given him such ecstasy as it unfolded itself to him, was utterly lost to him when he glanced at the picture with their eyes. He saw a well-painted (no, not even that—he distinctly saw now a mass of defects) repetition of those endless Christs of Titian, Raphael, Rubens, and the same soldiers and Pilate. It was all common, poor, and stale, and positively badly painted—weak and unequal. They would be justified in repeating hypocritically civil speeches in the presence of the painter, and pitying him and laughing at him when they were alone again.\n\nThe silence (though it lasted no more than a minute) became too intolerable to him. To break it, and to show he was not agitated, he made an effort and addressed Golenishtchev.\n\n“I think I’ve had the pleasure of meeting you,” he said, looking uneasily first at Anna, then at Vronsky, in fear of losing any shade of their expression.\n\n“To be sure! We met at Rossi’s, do you remember, at that soirée when that Italian lady recited—the new Rachel?” Golenishtchev answered easily, removing his eyes without the slightest regret from the picture and turning to the artist.\n\nNoticing, however, that Mihailov was expecting a criticism of the picture, he said:\n\n“Your picture has got on a great deal since I saw it last time; and what strikes me particularly now, as it did then, is the figure of Pilate. One so knows the man: a good-natured, capital fellow, but an official through and through, who does not know what it is he’s doing. But I fancy....”\n\nAll Mihailov’s mobile face beamed at once; his eyes sparkled. He tried to say something, but he could not speak for excitement, and pretended to be coughing. Low as was his opinion of Golenishtchev’s capacity for understanding art, trifling as was the true remark upon the fidelity of the expression of Pilate as an official, and offensive as might have seemed the utterance of so unimportant an observation while nothing was said of more serious points, Mihailov was in an ecstasy of delight at this observation. He had himself thought about Pilate’s figure just what Golenishtchev said. The fact that this reflection was but one of millions of reflections, which as Mihailov knew for certain would be true, did not diminish for him the significance of Golenishtchev’s remark. His heart warmed to Golenishtchev for this remark, and from a state of depression he suddenly passed to ecstasy. At once the whole of his picture lived before him in all the indescribable complexity of everything living. Mihailov again tried to say that that was how he understood Pilate, but his lips quivered intractably, and he could not pronounce the words. Vronsky and Anna too said something in that subdued voice in which, partly to avoid hurting the artist’s feelings and partly to avoid saying out loud something silly—so easily said when talking of art—people usually speak at exhibitions of pictures. Mihailov fancied that the picture had made an impression on them too. He went up to them.\n\n“How marvelous Christ’s expression is!” said Anna. Of all she saw she liked that expression most of all, and she felt that it was the center of the picture, and so praise of it would be pleasant to the artist. “One can see that He is pitying Pilate.”\n\nThis again was one of the million true reflections that could be found in his picture and in the figure of Christ. She said that He was pitying Pilate. In Christ’s expression there ought to be indeed an expression of pity, since there is an expression of love, of heavenly peace, of readiness for death, and a sense of the vanity of words. Of course there is the expression of an official in Pilate and of pity in Christ, seeing that one is the incarnation of the fleshly and the other of the spiritual life. All this and much more flashed into Mihailov’s thoughts.\n\n“Yes, and how that figure is done—what atmosphere! One can walk round it,” said Golenishtchev, unmistakably betraying by this remark that he did not approve of the meaning and idea of the figure.\n\n“Yes, there’s a wonderful mastery!” said Vronsky. “How those figures in the background stand out! There you have technique,” he said, addressing Golenishtchev, alluding to a conversation between them about Vronsky’s despair of attaining this technique.\n\n“Yes, yes, marvelous!” Golenishtchev and Anna assented. In spite of the excited condition in which he was, the sentence about technique had sent a pang to Mihailov’s heart, and looking angrily at Vronsky he suddenly scowled. He had often heard this word technique, and was utterly unable to understand what was understood by it. He knew that by this term was understood a mechanical facility for painting or drawing, entirely apart from its subject. He had noticed often that even in actual praise technique was opposed to essential quality, as though one could paint well something that was bad. He knew that a great deal of attention and care was necessary in taking off the coverings, to avoid injuring the creation itself, and to take off all the coverings; but there was no art of painting—no technique of any sort—about it. If to a little child or to his cook were revealed what he saw, it or she would have been able to peel the wrappings off what was seen. And the most experienced and adroit painter could not by mere mechanical facility paint anything if the lines of the subject were not revealed to him first. Besides, he saw that if it came to talking about technique, it was impossible to praise him for it. In all he had painted and repainted he saw faults that hurt his eyes, coming from want of care in taking off the wrappings—faults he could not correct now without spoiling the whole. And in almost all the figures and faces he saw, too, remnants of the wrappings not perfectly removed that spoiled the picture.\n\n“One thing might be said, if you will allow me to make the remark....” observed Golenishtchev.\n\n“Oh, I shall be delighted, I beg you,” said Mihailov with a forced smile.\n\n“That is, that you make Him the man-god, and not the God-man. But I know that was what you meant to do.”\n\n“I cannot paint a Christ that is not in my heart,” said Mihailov gloomily.\n\n“Yes; but in that case, if you will allow me to say what I think.... Your picture is so fine that my observation cannot detract from it, and, besides, it is only my personal opinion. With you it is different. Your very motive is different. But let us take Ivanov. I imagine that if Christ is brought down to the level of an historical character, it would have been better for Ivanov to select some other historical subject, fresh, untouched.”\n\n“But if this is the greatest subject presented to art?”\n\n“If one looked one would find others. But the point is that art cannot suffer doubt and discussion. And before the picture of Ivanov the question arises for the believer and the unbeliever alike, ‘Is it God, or is it not God?’ and the unity of the impression is destroyed.”\n\n“Why so? I think that for educated people,” said Mihailov, “the question cannot exist.”\n\nGolenishtchev did not agree with this, and confounded Mihailov by his support of his first idea of the unity of the impression being essential to art.\n\nMihailov was greatly perturbed, but he could say nothing in defense of his own idea.\n\n# Chapter 12\n\nAnna and Vronsky had long been exchanging glances, regretting their friend’s flow of cleverness. At last Vronsky, without waiting for the artist, walked away to another small picture.\n\n“Oh, how exquisite! What a lovely thing! A gem! How exquisite!” they cried with one voice.\n\n“What is it they’re so pleased with?” thought Mihailov. He had positively forgotten that picture he had painted three years ago. He had forgotten all the agonies and the ecstasies he had lived through with that picture when for several months it had been the one thought haunting him day and night. He had forgotten, as he always forgot, the pictures he had finished. He did not even like to look at it, and had only brought it out because he was expecting an Englishman who wanted to buy it.\n\n“Oh, that’s only an old study,” he said.\n\n“How fine!” said Golenishtchev, he too, with unmistakable sincerity, falling under the spell of the picture.\n\nTwo boys were angling in the shade of a willow-tree. The elder had just dropped in the hook, and was carefully pulling the float from behind a bush, entirely absorbed in what he was doing. The other, a little younger, was lying in the grass leaning on his elbows, with his tangled, flaxen head in his hands, staring at the water with his dreamy blue eyes. What was he thinking of?\n\nThe enthusiasm over this picture stirred some of the old feeling for it in Mihailov, but he feared and disliked this waste of feeling for things past, and so, even though this praise was grateful to him, he tried to draw his visitors away to a third picture.\n\nBut Vronsky asked whether the picture was for sale. To Mihailov at that moment, excited by visitors, it was extremely distasteful to speak of money matters.\n\n“It is put up there to be sold,” he answered, scowling gloomily.\n\nWhen the visitors had gone, Mihailov sat down opposite the picture of Pilate and Christ, and in his mind went over what had been said, and what, though not said, had been implied by those visitors. And, strange to say, what had had such weight with him, while they were there and while he mentally put himself at their point of view, suddenly lost all importance for him. He began to look at his picture with all his own full artist vision, and was soon in that mood of conviction of the perfectibility, and so of the significance, of his picture—a conviction essential to the most intense fervor, excluding all other interests—in which alone he could work.\n\nChrist’s foreshortened leg was not right, though. He took his palette and began to work. As he corrected the leg he looked continually at the figure of John in the background, which his visitors had not even noticed, but which he knew was beyond perfection. When he had finished the leg he wanted to touch that figure, but he felt too much excited for it. He was equally unable to work when he was cold and when he was too much affected and saw everything too much. There was only one stage in the transition from coldness to inspiration, at which work was possible. Today he was too much agitated. He would have covered the picture, but he stopped, holding the cloth in his hand, and, smiling blissfully, gazed a long while at the figure of John. At last, as it were regretfully tearing himself away, he dropped the cloth, and, exhausted but happy, went home.\n\nVronsky, Anna, and Golenishtchev, on their way home, were particularly lively and cheerful. They talked of Mihailov and his pictures. The word talent, by which they meant an inborn, almost physical, aptitude apart from brain and heart, and in which they tried to find an expression for all the artist had gained from life, recurred particularly often in their talk, as though it were necessary for them to sum up what they had no conception of, though they wanted to talk of it. They said that there was no denying his talent, but that his talent could not develop for want of education—the common defect of our Russian artists. But the picture of the boys had imprinted itself on their memories, and they were continually coming back to it. “What an exquisite thing! How he has succeeded in it, and how simply! He doesn’t even comprehend how good it is. Yes, I mustn’t let it slip; I must buy it,” said Vronsky.\n\n# Chapter 13\n\nMihailov sold Vronsky his picture, and agreed to paint a portrait of Anna. On the day fixed he came and began the work.\n\nFrom the fifth sitting the portrait impressed everyone, especially Vronsky, not only by its resemblance, but by its characteristic beauty. It was strange how Mihailov could have discovered just her characteristic beauty. “One needs to know and love her as I have loved her to discover the very sweetest expression of her soul,” Vronsky thought, though it was only from this portrait that he had himself learned this sweetest expression of her soul. But the expression was so true that he, and others too, fancied they had long known it.\n\n“I have been struggling on for ever so long without doing anything,” he said of his own portrait of her, “and he just looked and painted it. That’s where technique comes in.”\n\n“That will come,” was the consoling reassurance given him by Golenishtchev, in whose view Vronsky had both talent, and what was most important, culture, giving him a wider outlook on art. Golenishtchev’s faith in Vronsky’s talent was propped up by his own need of Vronsky’s sympathy and approval for his own articles and ideas, and he felt that the praise and support must be mutual.\n\nIn another man’s house, and especially in Vronsky’s palazzo, Mihailov was quite a different man from what he was in his studio. He behaved with hostile courtesy, as though he were afraid of coming closer to people he did not respect. He called Vronsky “your excellency,” and notwithstanding Anna’s and Vronsky’s invitations, he would never stay to dinner, nor come except for the sittings. Anna was even more friendly to him than to other people, and was very grateful for her portrait. Vronsky was more than cordial with him, and was obviously interested to know the artist’s opinion of his picture. Golenishtchev never let slip an opportunity of instilling sound ideas about art into Mihailov. But Mihailov remained equally chilly to all of them. Anna was aware from his eyes that he liked looking at her, but he avoided conversation with her. Vronsky’s talk about his painting he met with stubborn silence, and he was as stubbornly silent when he was shown Vronsky’s picture. He was unmistakably bored by Golenishtchev’s conversation, and he did not attempt to oppose him.\n\nAltogether Mihailov, with his reserved and disagreeable, as it were, hostile attitude, was quite disliked by them as they got to know him better; and they were glad when the sittings were over, and they were left with a magnificent portrait in their possession, and he gave up coming. Golenishtchev was the first to give expression to an idea that had occurred to all of them, which was that Mihailov was simply jealous of Vronsky.\n\n“Not envious, let us say, since he has talent; but it annoys him that a wealthy man of the highest society, and a count, too (you know they all detest a title), can, without any particular trouble, do as well, if not better, than he who has devoted all his life to it. And more than all, it’s a question of culture, which he is without.”\n\nVronsky defended Mihailov, but at the bottom of his heart he believed it, because in his view a man of a different, lower world would be sure to be envious.\n\nAnna’s portrait—the same subject painted from nature both by him and by Mihailov—ought to have shown Vronsky the difference between him and Mihailov; but he did not see it. Only after Mihailov’s portrait was painted he left off painting his portrait of Anna, deciding that it was now not needed. His picture of mediæval life he went on with. And he himself, and Golenishtchev, and still more Anna, thought it very good, because it was far more like the celebrated pictures they knew than Mihailov’s picture.\n\nMihailov meanwhile, although Anna’s portrait greatly fascinated him, was even more glad than they were when the sittings were over, and he had no longer to listen to Golenishtchev’s disquisitions upon art, and could forget about Vronsky’s painting. He knew that Vronsky could not be prevented from amusing himself with painting; he knew that he and all dilettanti had a perfect right to paint what they liked, but it was distasteful to him. A man could not be prevented from making himself a big wax doll, and kissing it. But if the man were to come with the doll and sit before a man in love, and begin caressing his doll as the lover caressed the woman he loved, it would be distasteful to the lover. Just such a distasteful sensation was what Mihailov felt at the sight of Vronsky’s painting: he felt it both ludicrous and irritating, both pitiable and offensive.\n\nVronsky’s interest in painting and the Middle Ages did not last long. He had enough taste for painting to be unable to finish his picture. The picture came to a standstill. He was vaguely aware that its defects, inconspicuous at first, would be glaring if he were to go on with it. The same experience befell him as Golenishtchev, who felt that he had nothing to say, and continually deceived himself with the theory that his idea was not yet mature, that he was working it out and collecting materials. This exasperated and tortured Golenishtchev, but Vronsky was incapable of deceiving and torturing himself, and even more incapable of exasperation. With his characteristic decision, without explanation or apology, he simply ceased working at painting.\n\nBut without this occupation, the life of Vronsky and of Anna, who wondered at his loss of interest in it, struck them as intolerably tedious in an Italian town. The palazzo suddenly seemed so obtrusively old and dirty, the spots on the curtains, the cracks in the floors, the broken plaster on the cornices became so disagreeably obvious, and the everlasting sameness of Golenishtchev, and the Italian professor and the German traveler became so wearisome, that they had to make some change. They resolved to go to Russia, to the country. In Petersburg Vronsky intended to arrange a partition of the land with his brother, while Anna meant to see her son. The summer they intended to spend on Vronsky’s great family estate.\n\n# Chapter 14\n\nLevin had been married three months. He was happy, but not at all in the way he had expected to be. At every step he found his former dreams disappointed, and new, unexpected surprises of happiness. He was happy; but on entering upon family life he saw at every step that it was utterly different from what he had imagined. At every step he experienced what a man would experience who, after admiring the smooth, happy course of a little boat on a lake, should get himself into that little boat. He saw that it was not all sitting still, floating smoothly; that one had to think too, not for an instant to forget where one was floating; and that there was water under one, and that one must row; and that his unaccustomed hands would be sore; and that it was only to look at it that was easy; but that doing it, though very delightful, was very difficult.\n\nAs a bachelor, when he had watched other people’s married life, seen the petty cares, the squabbles, the jealousy, he had only smiled contemptuously in his heart. In his future married life there could be, he was convinced, nothing of that sort; even the external forms, indeed, he fancied, must be utterly unlike the life of others in everything. And all of a sudden, instead of his life with his wife being made on an individual pattern, it was, on the contrary, entirely made up of the pettiest details, which he had so despised before, but which now, by no will of his own, had gained an extraordinary importance that it was useless to contend against. And Levin saw that the organization of all these details was by no means so easy as he had fancied before. Although Levin believed himself to have the most exact conceptions of domestic life, unconsciously, like all men, he pictured domestic life as the happiest enjoyment of love, with nothing to hinder and no petty cares to distract. He ought, as he conceived the position, to do his work, and to find repose from it in the happiness of love. She ought to be beloved, and nothing more. But, like all men, he forgot that she too would want work. And he was surprised that she, his poetic, exquisite Kitty, could, not merely in the first weeks, but even in the first days of their married life, think, remember, and busy herself about tablecloths, and furniture, about mattresses for visitors, about a tray, about the cook, and the dinner, and so on. While they were still engaged, he had been struck by the definiteness with which she had declined the tour abroad and decided to go into the country, as though she knew of something she wanted, and could still think of something outside her love. This had jarred upon him then, and now her trivial cares and anxieties jarred upon him several times. But he saw that this was essential for her. And, loving her as he did, though he did not understand the reason of them, and jeered at these domestic pursuits, he could not help admiring them. He jeered at the way in which she arranged the furniture they had brought from Moscow; rearranged their room; hung up curtains; prepared rooms for visitors; a room for Dolly; saw after an abode for her new maid; ordered dinner of the old cook; came into collision with Agafea Mihalovna, taking from her the charge of the stores. He saw how the old cook smiled, admiring her, and listening to her inexperienced, impossible orders, how mournfully and tenderly Agafea Mihalovna shook her head over the young mistress’s new arrangements. He saw that Kitty was extraordinarily sweet when, laughing and crying, she came to tell him that her maid, Masha, was used to looking upon her as her young lady, and so no one obeyed her. It seemed to him sweet, but strange, and he thought it would have been better without this.\n\nHe did not know how great a sense of change she was experiencing; she, who at home had sometimes wanted some favorite dish, or sweets, without the possibility of getting either, now could order what she liked, buy pounds of sweets, spend as much money as she liked, and order any puddings she pleased.\n\nShe was dreaming with delight now of Dolly’s coming to them with her children, especially because she would order for the children their favorite puddings and Dolly would appreciate all her new housekeeping. She did not know herself why and wherefore, but the arranging of her house had an irresistible attraction for her. Instinctively feeling the approach of spring, and knowing that there would be days of rough weather too, she built her nest as best she could, and was in haste at the same time to build it and to learn how to do it.\n\nThis care for domestic details in Kitty, so opposed to Levin’s ideal of exalted happiness, was at first one of the disappointments; and this sweet care of her household, the aim of which he did not understand, but could not help loving, was one of the new happy surprises.\n\nAnother disappointment and happy surprise came in their quarrels. Levin could never have conceived that between him and his wife any relations could arise other than tender, respectful and loving, and all at once in the very early days they quarreled, so that she said he did not care for her, that he cared for no one but himself, burst into tears, and wrung her arms.\n\nThis first quarrel arose from Levin’s having gone out to a new farmhouse and having been away half an hour too long, because he had tried to get home by a short cut and had lost his way. He drove home thinking of nothing but her, of her love, of his own happiness, and the nearer he drew to home, the warmer was his tenderness for her. He ran into the room with the same feeling, with an even stronger feeling than he had had when he reached the Shtcherbatskys’ house to make his offer. And suddenly he was met by a lowering expression he had never seen in her. He would have kissed her; she pushed him away.\n\n“What is it?”\n\n“You’ve been enjoying yourself,” she began, trying to be calm and spiteful. But as soon as she opened her mouth, a stream of reproach, of senseless jealousy, of all that had been torturing her during that half hour which she had spent sitting motionless at the window, burst from her. It was only then, for the first time, that he clearly understood what he had not understood when he led her out of the church after the wedding. He felt now that he was not simply close to her, but that he did not know where he ended and she began. He felt this from the agonizing sensation of division that he experienced at that instant. He was offended for the first instant, but the very same second he felt that he could not be offended by her, that she was himself. He felt for the first moment as a man feels when, having suddenly received a violent blow from behind, he turns round, angry and eager to avenge himself, to look for his antagonist, and finds that it is he himself who has accidentally struck himself, that there is no one to be angry with, and that he must put up with and try to soothe the pain.\n\nNever afterwards did he feel it with such intensity, but this first time he could not for a long while get over it. His natural feeling urged him to defend himself, to prove to her she was wrong; but to prove her wrong would mean irritating her still more and making the rupture greater that was the cause of all his suffering. One habitual feeling impelled him to get rid of the blame and to pass it on to her. Another feeling, even stronger, impelled him as quickly as possible to smooth over the rupture without letting it grow greater. To remain under such undeserved reproach was wretched, but to make her suffer by justifying himself was worse still. Like a man half-awake in an agony of pain, he wanted to tear out, to fling away the aching place, and coming to his senses, he felt that the aching place was himself. He could do nothing but try to help the aching place to bear it, and this he tried to do.\n\nThey made peace. She, recognizing that she was wrong, though she did not say so, became tenderer to him, and they experienced new, redoubled happiness in their love. But that did not prevent such quarrels from happening again, and exceedingly often too, on the most unexpected and trivial grounds. These quarrels frequently arose from the fact that they did not yet know what was of importance to each other and that all this early period they were both often in a bad temper. When one was in a good temper, and the other in a bad temper, the peace was not broken; but when both happened to be in an ill-humor, quarrels sprang up from such incomprehensibly trifling causes, that they could never remember afterwards what they had quarreled about. It is true that when they were both in a good temper their enjoyment of life was redoubled. But still this first period of their married life was a difficult time for them.\n\nDuring all this early time they had a peculiarly vivid sense of tension, as it were, a tugging in opposite directions of the chain by which they were bound. Altogether their honeymoon—that is to say, the month after their wedding—from which from tradition Levin expected so much, was not merely not a time of sweetness, but remained in the memories of both as the bitterest and most humiliating period in their lives. They both alike tried in later life to blot out from their memories all the monstrous, shameful incidents of that morbid period, when both were rarely in a normal frame of mind, both were rarely quite themselves.\n\nIt was only in the third month of their married life, after their return from Moscow, where they had been staying for a month, that their life began to go more smoothly.\n\n# Chapter 15\n\nThey had just come back from Moscow, and were glad to be alone. He was sitting at the writing-table in his study, writing. She, wearing the dark lilac dress she had worn during the first days of their married life, and put on again today, a dress particularly remembered and loved by him, was sitting on the sofa, the same old-fashioned leather sofa which had always stood in the study in Levin’s father’s and grandfather’s days. She was sewing at broderie anglaise. He thought and wrote, never losing the happy consciousness of her presence. His work, both on the land and on the book, in which the principles of the new land system were to be laid down, had not been abandoned; but just as formerly these pursuits and ideas had seemed to him petty and trivial in comparison with the darkness that overspread all life, now they seemed as unimportant and petty in comparison with the life that lay before him suffused with the brilliant light of happiness. He went on with his work, but he felt now that the center of gravity of his attention had passed to something else, and that consequently he looked at his work quite differently and more clearly. Formerly this work had been for him an escape from life. Formerly he had felt that without this work his life would be too gloomy. Now these pursuits were necessary for him that life might not be too uniformly bright. Taking up his manuscript, reading through what he had written, he found with pleasure that the work was worth his working at. Many of his old ideas seemed to him superfluous and extreme, but many blanks became distinct to him when he reviewed the whole thing in his memory. He was writing now a new chapter on the causes of the present disastrous condition of agriculture in Russia. He maintained that the poverty of Russia arises not merely from the anomalous distribution of landed property and misdirected reforms, but that what had contributed of late years to this result was the civilization from without abnormally grafted upon Russia, especially facilities of communication, as railways, leading to centralization in towns, the development of luxury, and the consequent development of manufactures, credit and its accompaniment of speculation—all to the detriment of agriculture. It seemed to him that in a normal development of wealth in a state all these phenomena would arise only when a considerable amount of labor had been put into agriculture, when it had come under regular, or at least definite, conditions; that the wealth of a country ought to increase proportionally, and especially in such a way that other sources of wealth should not outstrip agriculture; that in harmony with a certain stage of agriculture there should be means of communication corresponding to it, and that in our unsettled condition of the land, railways, called into being by political and not by economic needs, were premature, and instead of promoting agriculture, as was expected of them, they were competing with agriculture and promoting the development of manufactures and credit, and so arresting its progress; and that just as the one-sided and premature development of one organ in an animal would hinder its general development, so in the general development of wealth in Russia, credit, facilities of communication, manufacturing activity, indubitably necessary in Europe, where they had arisen in their proper time, had with us only done harm, by throwing into the background the chief question calling for settlement—the question of the organization of agriculture.\n\nWhile he was writing his ideas she was thinking how unnaturally cordial her husband had been to young Prince Tcharsky, who had, with great want of tact, flirted with her the day before they left Moscow. “He’s jealous,” she thought. “Goodness! how sweet and silly he is! He’s jealous of me! If he knew that I think no more of them than of Piotr the cook,” she thought, looking at his head and red neck with a feeling of possession strange to herself. “Though it’s a pity to take him from his work (but he has plenty of time!), I must look at his face; will he feel I’m looking at him? I wish he’d turn round ... I’ll will him to!” and she opened her eyes wide, as though to intensify the influence of her gaze.\n\n“Yes, they draw away all the sap and give a false appearance of prosperity,” he muttered, stopping to write, and, feeling that she was looking at him and smiling, he looked round.\n\n“Well?” he queried, smiling, and getting up.\n\n“He looked round,” she thought.\n\n“It’s nothing; I wanted you to look round,” she said, watching him, and trying to guess whether he was vexed at being interrupted or not.\n\n“How happy we are alone together!—I am, that is,” he said, going up to her with a radiant smile of happiness.\n\n“I’m just as happy. I’ll never go anywhere, especially not to Moscow.”\n\n“And what were you thinking about?”\n\n“I? I was thinking.... No, no, go along, go on writing; don’t break off,” she said, pursing up her lips, “and I must cut out these little holes now, do you see?”\n\nShe took up her scissors and began cutting them out.\n\n“No; tell me, what was it?” he said, sitting down beside her and watching the tiny scissors moving round.\n\n“Oh! what was I thinking about? I was thinking about Moscow, about the back of your head.”\n\n“Why should I, of all people, have such happiness! It’s unnatural, too good,” he said, kissing her hand.\n\n“I feel quite the opposite; the better things are, the more natural it seems to me.”\n\n“And you’ve got a little curl loose,” he said, carefully turning her head round.\n\n“A little curl, oh yes. No, no, we are busy at our work!”\n\nWork did not progress further, and they darted apart from one another like culprits when Kouzma came in to announce that tea was ready.\n\n“Have they come from the town?” Levin asked Kouzma.\n\n“They’ve just come; they’re unpacking the things.”\n\n“Come quickly,” she said to him as she went out of the study, “or else I shall read your letters without you.”\n\nLeft alone, after putting his manuscripts together in the new portfolio bought by her, he washed his hands at the new washstand with the elegant fittings, that had all made their appearance with her. Levin smiled at his own thoughts, and shook his head disapprovingly at those thoughts; a feeling akin to remorse fretted him. There was something shameful, effeminate, Capuan, as he called it to himself, in his present mode of life. “It’s not right to go on like this,” he thought. “It’ll soon be three months, and I’m doing next to nothing. Today, almost for the first time, I set to work seriously, and what happened? I did nothing but begin and throw it aside. Even my ordinary pursuits I have almost given up. On the land I scarcely walk or drive about at all to look after things. Either I am loath to leave her, or I see she’s dull alone. And I used to think that, before marriage, life was nothing much, somehow didn’t count, but that after marriage, life began in earnest. And here almost three months have passed, and I have spent my time so idly and unprofitably. No, this won’t do; I must begin. Of course, it’s not her fault. She’s not to blame in any way. I ought myself to be firmer, to maintain my masculine independence of action; or else I shall get into such ways, and she’ll get used to them too.... Of course she’s not to blame,” he told himself.\n\nBut it is hard for anyone who is dissatisfied not to blame someone else, and especially the person nearest of all to him, for the ground of his dissatisfaction. And it vaguely came into Levin’s mind that she herself was not to blame (she could not be to blame for anything), but what was to blame was her education, too superficial and frivolous. (“That fool Tcharsky: she wanted, I know, to stop him, but didn’t know how to.”) “Yes, apart from her interest in the house (that she has), apart from dress and broderie anglaise, she has no serious interests. No interest in her work, in the estate, in the peasants, nor in music, though she’s rather good at it, nor in reading. She does nothing, and is perfectly satisfied.” Levin, in his heart, censured this, and did not as yet understand that she was preparing for that period of activity which was to come for her when she would at once be the wife of her husband and mistress of the house, and would bear, and nurse, and bring up children. He knew not that she was instinctively aware of this, and preparing herself for this time of terrible toil, did not reproach herself for the moments of carelessness and happiness in her love that she enjoyed now while gaily building her nest for the future.\n\n# Chapter 16\n\nWhen Levin went upstairs, his wife was sitting near the new silver samovar behind the new tea service, and, having settled old Agafea Mihalovna at a little table with a full cup of tea, was reading a letter from Dolly, with whom they were in continual and frequent correspondence.\n\n“You see, your good lady’s settled me here, told me to sit a bit with her,” said Agafea Mihalovna, smiling affectionately at Kitty.\n\nIn these words of Agafea Mihalovna, Levin read the final act of the drama which had been enacted of late between her and Kitty. He saw that, in spite of Agafea Mihalovna’s feelings being hurt by a new mistress taking the reins of government out of her hands, Kitty had yet conquered her and made her love her.\n\n“Here, I opened your letter too,” said Kitty, handing him an illiterate letter. “It’s from that woman, I think, your brother’s....” she said. “I did not read it through. This is from my people and from Dolly. Fancy! Dolly took Tanya and Grisha to a children’s ball at the Sarmatskys’: Tanya was a French marquise.”\n\nBut Levin did not hear her. Flushing, he took the letter from Marya Nikolaevna, his brother’s former mistress, and began to read it. This was the second letter he had received from Marya Nikolaevna. In the first letter, Marya Nikolaevna wrote that his brother had sent her away for no fault of hers, and, with touching simplicity, added that though she was in want again, she asked for nothing, and wished for nothing, but was only tormented by the thought that Nikolay Dmitrievitch would come to grief without her, owing to the weak state of his health, and begged his brother to look after him. Now she wrote quite differently. She had found Nikolay Dmitrievitch, had again made it up with him in Moscow, and had moved with him to a provincial town, where he had received a post in the government service. But that he had quarreled with the head official, and was on his way back to Moscow, only he had been taken so ill on the road that it was doubtful if he would ever leave his bed again, she wrote. “It’s always of you he has talked, and, besides, he has no more money left.”\n\n“Read this; Dolly writes about you,” Kitty was beginning, with a smile; but she stopped suddenly, noticing the changed expression on her husband’s face.\n\n“What is it? What’s the matter?”\n\n“She writes to me that Nikolay, my brother, is at death’s door. I shall go to him.”\n\nKitty’s face changed at once. Thoughts of Tanya as a marquise, of Dolly, all had vanished.\n\n“When are you going?” she said.\n\n“Tomorrow.”\n\n“And I will go with you, can I?” she said.\n\n“Kitty! What are you thinking of?” he said reproachfully.\n\n“How do you mean?” offended that he should seem to take her suggestion unwillingly and with vexation. “Why shouldn’t I go? I shan’t be in your way. I....”\n\n“I’m going because my brother is dying,” said Levin. “Why should you....”\n\n“Why? For the same reason as you.”\n\n“And, at a moment of such gravity for me, she only thinks of her being dull by herself,” thought Levin. And this lack of candor in a matter of such gravity infuriated him.\n\n“It’s out of the question,” he said sternly.\n\nAgafea Mihalovna, seeing that it was coming to a quarrel, gently put down her cup and withdrew. Kitty did not even notice her. The tone in which her husband had said the last words wounded her, especially because he evidently did not believe what she had said.\n\n“I tell you, that if you go, I shall come with you; I shall certainly come,” she said hastily and wrathfully. “Why out of the question? Why do you say it’s out of the question?”\n\n“Because it’ll be going God knows where, by all sorts of roads and to all sorts of hotels. You would be a hindrance to me,” said Levin, trying to be cool.\n\n“Not at all. I don’t want anything. Where you can go, I can....”\n\n“Well, for one thing then, because this woman’s there whom you can’t meet.”\n\n“I don’t know and don’t care to know who’s there and what. I know that my husband’s brother is dying and my husband is going to him, and I go with my husband too....”\n\n“Kitty! Don’t get angry. But just think a little: this is a matter of such importance that I can’t bear to think that you should bring in a feeling of weakness, of dislike to being left alone. Come, you’ll be dull alone, so go and stay at Moscow a little.”\n\n“There, you always ascribe base, vile motives to me,” she said with tears of wounded pride and fury. “I didn’t mean, it wasn’t weakness, it wasn’t ... I feel that it’s my duty to be with my husband when he’s in trouble, but you try on purpose to hurt me, you try on purpose not to understand....”\n\n“No; this is awful! To be such a slave!” cried Levin, getting up, and unable to restrain his anger any longer. But at the same second he felt that he was beating himself.\n\n“Then why did you marry? You could have been free. Why did you, if you regret it?” she said, getting up and running away into the drawing-room.\n\nWhen he went to her, she was sobbing.\n\nHe began to speak, trying to find words not to dissuade but simply to soothe her. But she did not heed him, and would not agree to anything. He bent down to her and took her hand, which resisted him. He kissed her hand, kissed her hair, kissed her hand again—still she was silent. But when he took her face in both his hands and said “Kitty!” she suddenly recovered herself, and began to cry, and they were reconciled.\n\nIt was decided that they should go together the next day. Levin told his wife that he believed she wanted to go simply in order to be of use, agreed that Marya Nikolaevna’s being with his brother did not make her going improper, but he set off at the bottom of his heart dissatisfied both with her and with himself. He was dissatisfied with her for being unable to make up her mind to let him go when it was necessary (and how strange it was for him to think that he, so lately hardly daring to believe in such happiness as that she could love him—now was unhappy because she loved him too much!), and he was dissatisfied with himself for not showing more strength of will. Even greater was the feeling of disagreement at the bottom of his heart as to her not needing to consider the woman who was with his brother, and he thought with horror of all the contingencies they might meet with. The mere idea of his wife, his Kitty, being in the same room with a common wench, set him shuddering with horror and loathing.\n\n# Chapter 17\n\nThe hotel of the provincial town where Nikolay Levin was lying ill was one of those provincial hotels which are constructed on the newest model of modern improvements, with the best intentions of cleanliness, comfort, and even elegance, but owing to the public that patronizes them, are with astounding rapidity transformed into filthy taverns with a pretension of modern improvement that only makes them worse than the old-fashioned, honestly filthy hotels. This hotel had already reached that stage, and the soldier in a filthy uniform smoking in the entry, supposed to stand for a hall-porter, and the cast-iron, slippery, dark, and disagreeable staircase, and the free and easy waiter in a filthy frock coat, and the common dining-room with a dusty bouquet of wax flowers adorning the table, and filth, dust, and disorder everywhere, and at the same time the sort of modern up-to-date self-complacent railway uneasiness of this hotel, aroused a most painful feeling in Levin after their fresh young life, especially because the impression of falsity made by the hotel was so out of keeping with what awaited them.\n\nAs is invariably the case, after they had been asked at what price they wanted rooms, it appeared that there was not one decent room for them; one decent room had been taken by the inspector of railroads, another by a lawyer from Moscow, a third by Princess Astafieva from the country. There remained only one filthy room, next to which they promised that another should be empty by the evening. Feeling angry with his wife because what he had expected had come to pass, which was that at the moment of arrival, when his heart throbbed with emotion and anxiety to know how his brother was getting on, he should have to be seeing after her, instead of rushing straight to his brother, Levin conducted her to the room assigned them.\n\n“Go, do go!” she said, looking at him with timid and guilty eyes.\n\nHe went out of the door without a word, and at once stumbled over Marya Nikolaevna, who had heard of his arrival and had not dared to go in to see him. She was just the same as when he saw her in Moscow; the same woolen gown, and bare arms and neck, and the same good-naturedly stupid, pockmarked face, only a little plumper.\n\n“Well, how is he? how is he?”\n\n“Very bad. He can’t get up. He has kept expecting you. He.... Are you ... with your wife?”\n\nLevin did not for the first moment understand what it was confused her, but she immediately enlightened him.\n\n“I’ll go away. I’ll go down to the kitchen,” she brought out. “Nikolay Dmitrievitch will be delighted. He heard about it, and knows your lady, and remembers her abroad.”\n\nLevin realized that she meant his wife, and did not know what answer to make.\n\n“Come along, come along to him!” he said.\n\nBut as soon as he moved, the door of his room opened and Kitty peeped out. Levin crimsoned both from shame and anger with his wife, who had put herself and him in such a difficult position; but Marya Nikolaevna crimsoned still more. She positively shrank together and flushed to the point of tears, and clutching the ends of her apron in both hands, twisted them in her red fingers without knowing what to say and what to do.\n\nFor the first instant Levin saw an expression of eager curiosity in the eyes with which Kitty looked at this awful woman, so incomprehensible to her; but it lasted only a single instant.\n\n“Well! how is he?” she turned to her husband and then to her.\n\n“But one can’t go on talking in the passage like this!” Levin said, looking angrily at a gentleman who walked jauntily at that instant across the corridor, as though about his affairs.\n\n“Well then, come in,” said Kitty, turning to Marya Nikolaevna, who had recovered herself, but noticing her husband’s face of dismay, “or go on; go, and then come for me,” she said, and went back into the room.\n\nLevin went to his brother’s room. He had not in the least expected what he saw and felt in his brother’s room. He had expected to find him in the same state of self-deception which he had heard was so frequent with the consumptive, and which had struck him so much during his brother’s visit in the autumn. He had expected to find the physical signs of the approach of death more marked—greater weakness, greater emaciation, but still almost the same condition of things. He had expected himself to feel the same distress at the loss of the brother he loved and the same horror in face of death as he had felt then, only in a greater degree. And he had prepared himself for this; but he found something utterly different.\n\nIn a little dirty room with the painted panels of its walls filthy with spittle, and conversation audible through the thin partition from the next room, in a stifling atmosphere saturated with impurities, on a bedstead moved away from the wall, there lay covered with a quilt, a body. One arm of this body was above the quilt, and the wrist, huge as a rake-handle, was attached, inconceivably it seemed, to the thin, long bone of the arm smooth from the beginning to the middle. The head lay sideways on the pillow. Levin could see the scanty locks wet with sweat on the temples and tense, transparent-looking forehead.\n\n“It cannot be that that fearful body was my brother Nikolay?” thought Levin. But he went closer, saw the face, and doubt became impossible. In spite of the terrible change in the face, Levin had only to glance at those eager eyes raised at his approach, only to catch the faint movement of the mouth under the sticky mustache, to realize the terrible truth that this death-like body was his living brother.\n\nThe glittering eyes looked sternly and reproachfully at his brother as he drew near. And immediately this glance established a living relationship between living men. Levin immediately felt the reproach in the eyes fixed on him, and felt remorse at his own happiness.\n\nWhen Konstantin took him by the hand, Nikolay smiled. The smile was faint, scarcely perceptible, and in spite of the smile the stern expression of the eyes was unchanged.\n\n“You did not expect to find me like this,” he articulated with effort.\n\n“Yes ... no,” said Levin, hesitating over his words. “How was it you didn’t let me know before, that is, at the time of my wedding? I made inquiries in all directions.”\n\nHe had to talk so as not to be silent, and he did not know what to say, especially as his brother made no reply, and simply stared without dropping his eyes, and evidently penetrated to the inner meaning of each word. Levin told his brother that his wife had come with him. Nikolay expressed pleasure, but said he was afraid of frightening her by his condition. A silence followed. Suddenly Nikolay stirred, and began to say something. Levin expected something of peculiar gravity and importance from the expression of his face, but Nikolay began speaking of his health. He found fault with the doctor, regretting he had not a celebrated Moscow doctor. Levin saw that he still hoped.\n\nSeizing the first moment of silence, Levin got up, anxious to escape, if only for an instant, from his agonizing emotion, and said that he would go and fetch his wife.\n\n“Very well, and I’ll tell her to tidy up here. It’s dirty and stinking here, I expect. Marya! clear up the room,” the sick man said with effort. “Oh, and when you’ve cleared up, go away yourself,” he added, looking inquiringly at his brother.\n\nLevin made no answer. Going out into the corridor, he stopped short. He had said he would fetch his wife, but now, taking stock of the emotion he was feeling, he decided that he would try on the contrary to persuade her not to go in to the sick man. “Why should she suffer as I am suffering?” he thought.\n\n“Well, how is he?” Kitty asked with a frightened face.\n\n“Oh, it’s awful, it’s awful! What did you come for?” said Levin.\n\nKitty was silent for a few seconds, looking timidly and ruefully at her husband; then she went up and took him by the elbow with both hands.\n\n“Kostya! take me to him; it will be easier for us to bear it together. You only take me, take me to him, please, and go away,” she said. “You must understand that for me to see you, and not to see him, is far more painful. There I might be a help to you and to him. Please, let me!” she besought her husband, as though the happiness of her life depended on it.\n\nLevin was obliged to agree, and regaining his composure, and completely forgetting about Marya Nikolaevna by now, he went again in to his brother with Kitty.\n\nStepping lightly, and continually glancing at her husband, showing him a valorous and sympathetic face, Kitty went into the sick-room, and, turning without haste, noiselessly closed the door. With inaudible steps she went quickly to the sick man’s bedside, and going up so that he had not to turn his head, she immediately clasped in her fresh young hand the skeleton of his huge hand, pressed it, and began speaking with that soft eagerness, sympathetic and not jarring, which is peculiar to women.\n\n“We have met, though we were not acquainted, at Soden,” she said. “You never thought I was to be your sister?”\n\n“You would not have recognized me?” he said, with a radiant smile at her entrance.\n\n“Yes, I should. What a good thing you let us know! Not a day has passed that Kostya has not mentioned you, and been anxious.”\n\nBut the sick man’s interest did not last long.\n\nBefore she had finished speaking, there had come back into his face the stern, reproachful expression of the dying man’s envy of the living.\n\n“I am afraid you are not quite comfortable here,” she said, turning away from his fixed stare, and looking about the room. “We must ask about another room,” she said to her husband, “so that we might be nearer.”\n\n# Chapter 18\n\nLevin could not look calmly at his brother; he could not himself be natural and calm in his presence. When he went in to the sick man, his eyes and his attention were unconsciously dimmed, and he did not see and did not distinguish the details of his brother’s position. He smelt the awful odor, saw the dirt, disorder, and miserable condition, and heard the groans, and felt that nothing could be done to help. It never entered his head to analyze the details of the sick man’s situation, to consider how that body was lying under the quilt, how those emaciated legs and thighs and spine were lying huddled up, and whether they could not be made more comfortable, whether anything could not be done to make things, if not better, at least less bad. It made his blood run cold when he began to think of all these details. He was absolutely convinced that nothing could be done to prolong his brother’s life or to relieve his suffering. But a sense of his regarding all aid as out of the question was felt by the sick man, and exasperated him. And this made it still more painful for Levin. To be in the sick-room was agony to him, not to be there still worse. And he was continually, on various pretexts, going out of the room, and coming in again, because he was unable to remain alone.\n\nBut Kitty thought, and felt, and acted quite differently. On seeing the sick man, she pitied him. And pity in her womanly heart did not arouse at all that feeling of horror and loathing that it aroused in her husband, but a desire to act, to find out all the details of his state, and to remedy them. And since she had not the slightest doubt that it was her duty to help him, she had no doubt either that it was possible, and immediately set to work. The very details, the mere thought of which reduced her husband to terror, immediately engaged her attention. She sent for the doctor, sent to the chemist’s, set the maid who had come with her and Marya Nikolaevna to sweep and dust and scrub; she herself washed up something, washed out something else, laid something under the quilt. Something was by her directions brought into the sick-room, something else was carried out. She herself went several times to her room, regardless of the men she met in the corridor, got out and brought in sheets, pillow cases, towels, and shirts.\n\nThe waiter, who was busy with a party of engineers dining in the dining hall, came several times with an irate countenance in answer to her summons, and could not avoid carrying out her orders, as she gave them with such gracious insistence that there was no evading her. Levin did not approve of all this; he did not believe it would be of any good to the patient. Above all, he feared the patient would be angry at it. But the sick man, though he seemed and was indifferent about it, was not angry, but only abashed, and on the whole as it were interested in what she was doing with him. Coming back from the doctor to whom Kitty had sent him, Levin, on opening the door, came upon the sick man at the instant when, by Kitty’s directions, they were changing his linen. The long white ridge of his spine, with the huge, prominent shoulder blades and jutting ribs and vertebrae, was bare, and Marya Nikolaevna and the waiter were struggling with the sleeve of the night shirt, and could not get the long, limp arm into it. Kitty, hurriedly closing the door after Levin, was not looking that way; but the sick man groaned, and she moved rapidly towards him.\n\n“Make haste,” she said.\n\n“Oh, don’t you come,” said the sick man angrily. “I’ll do it my myself....”\n\n“What say?” queried Marya Nikolaevna. But Kitty heard and saw he was ashamed and uncomfortable at being naked before her.\n\n“I’m not looking, I’m not looking!” she said, putting the arm in. “Marya Nikolaevna, you come this side, you do it,” she added.\n\n“Please go for me, there’s a little bottle in my small bag,” she said, turning to her husband, “you know, in the side pocket; bring it, please, and meanwhile they’ll finish clearing up here.”\n\nReturning with the bottle, Levin found the sick man settled comfortably and everything about him completely changed. The heavy smell was replaced by the smell of aromatic vinegar, which Kitty with pouting lips and puffed-out, rosy cheeks was squirting through a little pipe. There was no dust visible anywhere, a rug was laid by the bedside. On the table stood medicine bottles and decanters tidily arranged, and the linen needed was folded up there, and Kitty’s broderie anglaise. On the other table by the patient’s bed there were candles and drink and powders. The sick man himself, washed and combed, lay in clean sheets on high raised pillows, in a clean night-shirt with a white collar about his astoundingly thin neck, and with a new expression of hope looked fixedly at Kitty.\n\nThe doctor brought by Levin, and found by him at the club, was not the one who had been attending Nikolay Levin, as the patient was dissatisfied with him. The new doctor took up a stethoscope and sounded the patient, shook his head, prescribed medicine, and with extreme minuteness explained first how to take the medicine and then what diet was to be kept to. He advised eggs, raw or hardly cooked, and seltzer water, with warm milk at a certain temperature. When the doctor had gone away the sick man said something to his brother, of which Levin could distinguish only the last words: “Your Katya.” By the expression with which he gazed at her, Levin saw that he was praising her. He called indeed to Katya, as he called her.\n\n“I’m much better already,” he said. “Why, with you I should have got well long ago. How nice it is!” he took her hand and drew it towards his lips, but as though afraid she would dislike it he changed his mind, let it go, and only stroked it. Kitty took his hand in both hers and pressed it.\n\n“Now turn me over on the left side and go to bed,” he said.\n\nNo one could make out what he said but Kitty; she alone understood. She understood because she was all the while mentally keeping watch on what he needed.\n\n“On the other side,” she said to her husband, “he always sleeps on that side. Turn him over, it’s so disagreeable calling the servants. I’m not strong enough. Can you?” she said to Marya Nikolaevna.\n\n“I’m afraid not,” answered Marya Nikolaevna.\n\nTerrible as it was to Levin to put his arms round that terrible body, to take hold of that under the quilt, of which he preferred to know nothing, under his wife’s influence he made his resolute face that she knew so well, and putting his arms into the bed took hold of the body, but in spite of his own strength he was struck by the strange heaviness of those powerless limbs. While he was turning him over, conscious of the huge emaciated arm about his neck, Kitty swiftly and noiselessly turned the pillow, beat it up and settled in it the sick man’s head, smoothing back his hair, which was sticking again to his moist brow.\n\nThe sick man kept his brother’s hand in his own. Levin felt that he meant to do something with his hand and was pulling it somewhere. Levin yielded with a sinking heart: yes, he drew it to his mouth and kissed it. Levin, shaking with sobs and unable to articulate a word, went out of the room.\n\n# Chapter 19\n\n“Thou hast hid these things from the wise and prudent, and hast revealed them unto babes.” So Levin thought about his wife as he talked to her that evening.\n\nLevin thought of the text, not because he considered himself “wise and prudent.” He did not so consider himself, but he could not help knowing that he had more intellect than his wife and Agafea Mihalovna, and he could not help knowing that when he thought of death, he thought with all the force of his intellect. He knew too that the brains of many great men, whose thoughts he had read, had brooded over death and yet knew not a hundredth part of what his wife and Agafea Mihalovna knew about it. Different as those two women were, Agafea Mihalovna and Katya, as his brother Nikolay had called her, and as Levin particularly liked to call her now, they were quite alike in this. Both knew, without a shade of doubt, what sort of thing life was and what was death, and though neither of them could have answered, and would even not have understood the questions that presented themselves to Levin, both had no doubt of the significance of this event, and were precisely alike in their way of looking at it, which they shared with millions of people. The proof that they knew for a certainty the nature of death lay in the fact that they knew without a second of hesitation how to deal with the dying, and were not frightened of them. Levin and other men like him, though they could have said a great deal about death, obviously did not know this since they were afraid of death, and were absolutely at a loss what to do when people were dying. If Levin had been alone now with his brother Nikolay, he would have looked at him with terror, and with still greater terror waited, and would not have known what else to do.\n\nMore than that, he did not know what to say, how to look, how to move. To talk of outside things seemed to him shocking, impossible, to talk of death and depressing subjects—also impossible. To be silent, also impossible. “If I look at him he will think I am studying him, I am afraid; if I don’t look at him, he’ll think I’m thinking of other things. If I walk on tiptoe, he will be vexed; to tread firmly, I’m ashamed.” Kitty evidently did not think of herself, and had no time to think about herself: she was thinking about him because she knew something, and all went well. She told him about herself even and about her wedding, and smiled and sympathized with him and petted him, and talked of cases of recovery and all went well; so then she must know. The proof that her behavior and Agafea Mihalovna’s was not instinctive, animal, irrational, was that apart from the physical treatment, the relief of suffering, both Agafea Mihalovna and Kitty required for the dying man something else more important than the physical treatment, and something which had nothing in common with physical conditions. Agafea Mihalovna, speaking of the man just dead, had said: “Well, thank God, he took the sacrament and received absolution; God grant each one of us such a death.” Katya in just the same way, besides all her care about linen, bedsores, drink, found time the very first day to persuade the sick man of the necessity of taking the sacrament and receiving absolution.\n\nOn getting back from the sick-room to their own two rooms for the night, Levin sat with hanging head not knowing what to do. Not to speak of supper, of preparing for bed, of considering what they were going to do, he could not even talk to his wife; he was ashamed to. Kitty, on the contrary, was more active than usual. She was even livelier than usual. She ordered supper to be brought, herself unpacked their things, and herself helped to make the beds, and did not even forget to sprinkle them with Persian powder. She showed that alertness, that swiftness of reflection which comes out in men before a battle, in conflict, in the dangerous and decisive moments of life—those moments when a man shows once and for all his value, and that all his past has not been wasted but has been a preparation for these moments.\n\nEverything went rapidly in her hands, and before it was twelve o’clock all their things were arranged cleanly and tidily in her rooms, in such a way that the hotel rooms seemed like home: the beds were made, brushes, combs, looking-glasses were put out, table napkins were spread.\n\nLevin felt that it was unpardonable to eat, to sleep, to talk even now, and it seemed to him that every movement he made was unseemly. She arranged the brushes, but she did it all so that there was nothing shocking in it.\n\nThey could neither of them eat, however, and for a long while they could not sleep, and did not even go to bed.\n\n“I am very glad I persuaded him to receive extreme unction tomorrow,” she said, sitting in her dressing jacket before her folding looking-glass, combing her soft, fragrant hair with a fine comb. “I have never seen it, but I know, mamma has told me, there are prayers said for recovery.”\n\n“Do you suppose he can possibly recover?” said Levin, watching a slender tress at the back of her round little head that was continually hidden when she passed the comb through the front.\n\n“I asked the doctor; he said he couldn’t live more than three days. But can they be sure? I’m very glad, anyway, that I persuaded him,” she said, looking askance at her husband through her hair. “Anything is possible,” she added with that peculiar, rather sly expression that was always in her face when she spoke of religion.\n\nSince their conversation about religion when they were engaged neither of them had ever started a discussion of the subject, but she performed all the ceremonies of going to church, saying her prayers, and so on, always with the unvarying conviction that this ought to be so. In spite of his assertion to the contrary, she was firmly persuaded that he was as much a Christian as she, and indeed a far better one; and all that he said about it was simply one of his absurd masculine freaks, just as he would say about her broderie anglaise that good people patch holes, but that she cut them on purpose, and so on.\n\n“Yes, you see this woman, Marya Nikolaevna, did not know how to manage all this,” said Levin. “And ... I must own I’m very, very glad you came. You are such purity that....” He took her hand and did not kiss it (to kiss her hand in such closeness to death seemed to him improper); he merely squeezed it with a penitent air, looking at her brightening eyes.\n\n“It would have been miserable for you to be alone,” she said, and lifting her hands which hid her cheeks flushing with pleasure, twisted her coil of hair on the nape of her neck and pinned it there. “No,” she went on, “she did not know how.... Luckily, I learned a lot at Soden.”\n\n“Surely there are not people there so ill?”\n“Worse.”\n\n“What’s so awful to me is that I can’t see him as he was when he was young. You would not believe how charming he was as a youth, but I did not understand him then.”\n\n“I can quite, quite believe it. How I feel that we might have been friends!” she said; and, distressed at what she had said, she looked round at her husband, and tears came into her eyes.\n\n“Yes, might have been,” he said mournfully. “He’s just one of those people of whom they say they’re not for this world.”\n\n“But we have many days before us; we must go to bed,” said Kitty, glancing at her tiny watch.\n\n# Chapter 20\n\nThe next day the sick man received the sacrament and extreme unction. During the ceremony Nikolay Levin prayed fervently. His great eyes, fastened on the holy image that was set out on a card-table covered with a colored napkin, expressed such passionate prayer and hope that it was awful to Levin to see it. Levin knew that this passionate prayer and hope would only make him feel more bitterly parting from the life he so loved. Levin knew his brother and the workings of his intellect: he knew that his unbelief came not from life being easier for him without faith, but had grown up because step by step the contemporary scientific interpretation of natural phenomena crushed out the possibility of faith; and so he knew that his present return was not a legitimate one, brought about by way of the same working of his intellect, but simply a temporary, interested return to faith in a desperate hope of recovery. Levin knew too that Kitty had strengthened his hope by accounts of the marvelous recoveries she had heard of. Levin knew all this; and it was agonizingly painful to him to behold the supplicating, hopeful eyes and the emaciated wrist, lifted with difficulty, making the sign of the cross on the tense brow, and the prominent shoulders and hollow, gasping chest, which one could not feel consistent with the life the sick man was praying for. During the sacrament Levin did what he, an unbeliever, had done a thousand times. He said, addressing God, “If Thou dost exist, make this man to recover” (of course this same thing has been repeated many times), “and Thou wilt save him and me.”\n\nAfter extreme unction the sick man became suddenly much better. He did not cough once in the course of an hour, smiled, kissed Kitty’s hand, thanking her with tears, and said he was comfortable, free from pain, and that he felt strong and had an appetite. He even raised himself when his soup was brought, and asked for a cutlet as well. Hopelessly ill as he was, obvious as it was at the first glance that he could not recover, Levin and Kitty were for that hour both in the same state of excitement, happy, though fearful of being mistaken.\n\n“Is he better?”\n“Yes, much.”\n\n“It’s wonderful.”\n\n“There’s nothing wonderful in it.”\n\n“Anyway, he’s better,” they said in a whisper, smiling to one another.\n\nThis self-deception was not of long duration. The sick man fell into a quiet sleep, but he was waked up half an hour later by his cough. And all at once every hope vanished in those about him and in himself. The reality of his suffering crushed all hopes in Levin and Kitty and in the sick man himself, leaving no doubt, no memory even of past hopes.\n\nWithout referring to what he had believed in half an hour before, as though ashamed even to recall it, he asked for iodine to inhale in a bottle covered with perforated paper. Levin gave him the bottle, and the same look of passionate hope with which he had taken the sacrament was now fastened on his brother, demanding from him the confirmation of the doctor’s words that inhaling iodine worked wonders.\n\n“Is Katya not here?” he gasped, looking round while Levin reluctantly assented to the doctor’s words. “No; so I can say it.... It was for her sake I went through that farce. She’s so sweet; but you and I can’t deceive ourselves. This is what I believe in,” he said, and, squeezing the bottle in his bony hand, he began breathing over it.\n\nAt eight o’clock in the evening Levin and his wife were drinking tea in their room when Marya Nikolaevna ran in to them breathlessly. She was pale, and her lips were quivering. “He is dying!” she whispered. “I’m afraid will die this minute.”\n\nBoth of them ran to him. He was sitting raised up with one elbow on the bed, his long back bent, and his head hanging low.\n\n“How do you feel?” Levin asked in a whisper, after a silence.\n\n“I feel I’m setting off,” Nikolay said with difficulty, but with extreme distinctness, screwing the words out of himself. He did not raise his head, but simply turned his eyes upwards, without their reaching his brother’s face. “Katya, go away!” he added.\n\nLevin jumped up, and with a peremptory whisper made her go out.\n\n“I’m setting off,” he said again.\n\n“Why do you think so?” said Levin, so as to say something.\n\n“Because I’m setting off,” he repeated, as though he had a liking for the phrase. “It’s the end.”\n\nMarya Nikolaevna went up to him.\n\n“You had better lie down; you’d be easier,” she said.\n\n“I shall lie down soon enough,” he pronounced slowly, “when I’m dead,” he said sarcastically, wrathfully. “Well, you can lay me down if you like.”\n\nLevin laid his brother on his back, sat down beside him, and gazed at his face, holding his breath. The dying man lay with closed eyes, but the muscles twitched from time to time on his forehead, as with one thinking deeply and intensely. Levin involuntarily thought with him of what it was that was happening to him now, but in spite of all his mental efforts to go along with him he saw by the expression of that calm, stern face that for the dying man all was growing clearer and clearer that was still as dark as ever for Levin.\n\n“Yes, yes, so,” the dying man articulated slowly at intervals. “Wait a little.” He was silent. “Right!” he pronounced all at once reassuringly, as though all were solved for him. “O Lord!” he murmured, and sighed deeply.\n\nMarya Nikolaevna felt his feet. “They’re getting cold,” she whispered.\n\nFor a long while, a very long while it seemed to Levin, the sick man lay motionless. But he was still alive, and from time to time he sighed. Levin by now was exhausted from mental strain. He felt that, with no mental effort, could he understand what it was that was right. He could not even think of the problem of death itself, but with no will of his own thoughts kept coming to him of what he had to do next; closing the dead man’s eyes, dressing him, ordering the coffin. And, strange to say, he felt utterly cold, and was not conscious of sorrow nor of loss, less still of pity for his brother. If he had any feeling for his brother at that moment, it was envy for the knowledge the dying man had now that he could not have.\n\nA long time more he sat over him so, continually expecting the end. But the end did not come. The door opened and Kitty appeared. Levin got up to stop her. But at the moment he was getting up, he caught the sound of the dying man stirring.\n\n“Don’t go away,” said Nikolay and held out his hand. Levin gave him his, and angrily waved to his wife to go away.\n\nWith the dying man’s hand in his hand, he sat for half an hour, an hour, another hour. He did not think of death at all now. He wondered what Kitty was doing; who lived in the next room; whether the doctor lived in a house of his own. He longed for food and for sleep. He cautiously drew away his hand and felt the feet. The feet were cold, but the sick man was still breathing. Levin tried again to move away on tiptoe, but the sick man stirred again and said: “Don’t go.”\n\nThe dawn came; the sick man’s condition was unchanged. Levin stealthily withdrew his hand, and without looking at the dying man, went off to his own room and went to sleep. When he woke up, instead of news of his brother’s death which he expected, he learned that the sick man had returned to his earlier condition. He had begun sitting up again, coughing, had begun eating again, talking again, and again had ceased to talk of death, again had begun to express hope of his recovery, and had become more irritable and more gloomy than ever. No one, neither his brother nor Kitty, could soothe him. He was angry with everyone, and said nasty things to everyone, reproached everyone for his sufferings, and insisted that they should get him a celebrated doctor from Moscow. To all inquiries made him as to how he felt, he made the same answer with an expression of vindictive reproachfulness, “I’m suffering horribly, intolerably!”\n\nThe sick man was suffering more and more, especially from bedsores, which it was impossible now to remedy, and grew more and more angry with everyone about him, blaming them for everything, and especially for not having brought him a doctor from Moscow. Kitty tried in every possible way to relieve him, to soothe him; but it was all in vain, and Levin saw that she herself was exhausted both physically and morally, though she would not admit it. The sense of death, which had been evoked in all by his taking leave of life on the night when he had sent for his brother, was broken up. Everyone knew that he must inevitably die soon, that he was half dead already. Everyone wished for nothing but that he should die as soon as possible, and everyone, concealing this, gave him medicines, tried to find remedies and doctors, and deceived him and themselves and each other. All this was falsehood, disgusting, irreverent deceit. And owing to the bent of his character, and because he loved the dying man more than anyone else did, Levin was most painfully conscious of this deceit.\n\nLevin, who had long been possessed by the idea of reconciling his brothers, at least in face of death, had written to his brother, Sergey Ivanovitch, and having received an answer from him, he read this letter to the sick man. Sergey Ivanovitch wrote that he could not come himself, and in touching terms he begged his brother’s forgiveness.\n\nThe sick man said nothing.\n\n“What am I to write to him?” said Levin. “I hope you are not angry with him?”\n\n“No, not the least!” Nikolay answered, vexed at the question. “Tell him to send me a doctor.”\n\nThree more days of agony followed; the sick man was still in the same condition. The sense of longing for his death was felt by everyone now at the mere sight of him, by the waiters and the hotel-keeper and all the people staying in the hotel, and the doctor and Marya Nikolaevna and Levin and Kitty. The sick man alone did not express this feeling, but on the contrary was furious at their not getting him doctors, and went on taking medicine and talking of life. Only at rare moments, when the opium gave him an instant’s relief from the never-ceasing pain, he would sometimes, half asleep, utter what was ever more intense in his heart than in all the others: “Oh, if it were only the end!” or: “When will it be over?”\n\nHis sufferings, steadily growing more intense, did their work and prepared him for death. There was no position in which he was not in pain, there was not a minute in which he was unconscious of it, not a limb, not a part of his body that did not ache and cause him agony. Even the memories, the impressions, the thoughts of this body awakened in him now the same aversion as the body itself. The sight of other people, their remarks, his own reminiscences, everything was for him a source of agony. Those about him felt this, and instinctively did not allow themselves to move freely, to talk, to express their wishes before him. All his life was merged in the one feeling of suffering and desire to be rid of it.\n\nThere was evidently coming over him that revulsion that would make him look upon death as the goal of his desires, as happiness. Hitherto each individual desire, aroused by suffering or privation, such as hunger, fatigue, thirst, had been satisfied by some bodily function giving pleasure. But now no physical craving or suffering received relief, and the effort to relieve them only caused fresh suffering. And so all desires were merged in one—the desire to be rid of all his sufferings and their source, the body. But he had no words to express this desire of deliverance, and so he did not speak of it, and from habit asked for the satisfaction of desires which could not now be satisfied. “Turn me over on the other side,” he would say, and immediately after he would ask to be turned back again as before. “Give me some broth. Take away the broth. Talk of something: why are you silent?” And directly they began to talk he would close his eyes, and would show weariness, indifference, and loathing.\n\nOn the tenth day from their arrival at the town, Kitty was unwell. She suffered from headache and sickness, and she could not get up all the morning.\n\nThe doctor opined that the indisposition arose from fatigue and excitement, and prescribed rest.\n\nAfter dinner, however, Kitty got up and went as usual with her work to the sick man. He looked at her sternly when she came in, and smiled contemptuously when she said she had been unwell. That day he was continually blowing his nose, and groaning piteously.\n\n“How do you feel?” she asked him.\n\n“Worse,” he articulated with difficulty. “In pain!”\n\n“In pain, where?”\n\n“Everywhere.”\n\n“It will be over today, you will see,” said Marya Nikolaevna. Though it was said in a whisper, the sick man, whose hearing Levin had noticed was very keen, must have heard. Levin said hush to her, and looked round at the sick man. Nikolay had heard; but these words produced no effect on him. His eyes had still the same intense, reproachful look.\n\n“Why do you think so?” Levin asked her, when she had followed him into the corridor.\n\n“He has begun picking at himself,” said Marya Nikolaevna.\n\n“How do you mean?”\n\n“Like this,” she said, tugging at the folds of her woolen skirt. Levin noticed, indeed, that all that day the patient pulled at himself, as it were, trying to snatch something away.\n\nMarya Nikolaevna’s prediction came true. Towards night the sick man was not able to lift his hands, and could only gaze before him with the same intensely concentrated expression in his eyes. Even when his brother or Kitty bent over him, so that he could see them, he looked just the same. Kitty sent for the priest to read the prayer for the dying.\n\nWhile the priest was reading it, the dying man did not show any sign of life; his eyes were closed. Levin, Kitty, and Marya Nikolaevna stood at the bedside. The priest had not quite finished reading the prayer when the dying man stretched, sighed, and opened his eyes. The priest, on finishing the prayer, put the cross to the cold forehead, then slowly returned it to the stand, and after standing for two minutes more in silence, he touched the huge, bloodless hand that was turning cold.\n\n“He is gone,” said the priest, and would have moved away; but suddenly there was a faint stir in the mustaches of the dead man that seemed glued together, and quite distinctly in the hush they heard from the bottom of the chest the sharply defined sounds:\n\n“Not quite ... soon.”\n\nAnd a minute later the face brightened, a smile came out under the mustaches, and the women who had gathered round began carefully laying out the corpse.\n\nThe sight of his brother, and the nearness of death, revived in Levin that sense of horror in face of the insoluble enigma, together with the nearness and inevitability of death, that had come upon him that autumn evening when his brother had come to him. This feeling was now even stronger than before; even less than before did he feel capable of apprehending the meaning of death, and its inevitability rose up before him more terrible than ever. But now, thanks to his wife’s presence, that feeling did not reduce him to despair. In spite of death, he felt the need of life and love. He felt that love saved him from despair, and that this love, under the menace of despair, had become still stronger and purer. The one mystery of death, still unsolved, had scarcely passed before his eyes, when another mystery had arisen, as insoluble, urging him to love and to life.\n\nThe doctor confirmed his suppositions in regard to Kitty. Her indisposition was a symptom that she was with child.\n\n# Chapter 21\n\nFrom the moment when Alexey Alexandrovitch understood from his interviews with Betsy and with Stepan Arkadyevitch that all that was expected of him was to leave his wife in peace, without burdening her with his presence, and that his wife herself desired this, he felt so distraught that he could come to no decision of himself; he did not know himself what he wanted now, and putting himself in the hands of those who were so pleased to interest themselves in his affairs, he met everything with unqualified assent. It was only when Anna had left his house, and the English governess sent to ask him whether she should dine with him or separately, that for the first time he clearly comprehended his position, and was appalled by it. Most difficult of all in this position was the fact that he could not in any way connect and reconcile his past with what was now. It was not the past when he had lived happily with his wife that troubled him. The transition from that past to a knowledge of his wife’s unfaithfulness he had lived through miserably already; that state was painful, but he could understand it. If his wife had then, on declaring to him her unfaithfulness, left him, he would have been wounded, unhappy, but he would not have been in the hopeless position—incomprehensible to himself—in which he felt himself now. He could not now reconcile his immediate past, his tenderness, his love for his sick wife, and for the other man’s child with what was now the case, that is with the fact that, as it were, in return for all this he now found himself alone, put to shame, a laughing-stock, needed by no one, and despised by everyone.\n\nFor the first two days after his wife’s departure Alexey Alexandrovitch received applicants for assistance and his chief secretary, drove to the committee, and went down to dinner in the dining-room as usual. Without giving himself a reason for what he was doing, he strained every nerve of his being for those two days, simply to preserve an appearance of composure, and even of indifference. Answering inquiries about the disposition of Anna Arkadyevna’s rooms and belongings, he had exercised immense self-control to appear like a man in whose eyes what had occurred was not unforeseen nor out of the ordinary course of events, and he attained his aim: no one could have detected in him signs of despair. But on the second day after her departure, when Korney gave him a bill from a fashionable draper’s shop, which Anna had forgotten to pay, and announced that the clerk from the shop was waiting, Alexey Alexandrovitch told him to show the clerk up.\n\n“Excuse me, your excellency, for venturing to trouble you. But if you direct us to apply to her excellency, would you graciously oblige us with her address?”\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch pondered, as it seemed to the clerk, and all at once, turning round, he sat down at the table. Letting his head sink into his hands, he sat for a long while in that position, several times attempted to speak and stopped short. Korney, perceiving his master’s emotion, asked the clerk to call another time. Left alone, Alexey Alexandrovitch recognized that he had not the strength to keep up the line of firmness and composure any longer. He gave orders for the carriage that was awaiting him to be taken back, and for no one to be admitted, and he did not go down to dinner.\n\nHe felt that he could not endure the weight of universal contempt and exasperation, which he had distinctly seen in the face of the clerk and of Korney, and of everyone, without exception, whom he had met during those two days. He felt that he could not turn aside from himself the hatred of men, because that hatred did not come from his being bad (in that case he could have tried to be better), but from his being shamefully and repulsively unhappy. He knew that for this, for the very fact that his heart was torn with grief, they would be merciless to him. He felt that men would crush him as dogs strangle a torn dog yelping with pain. He knew that his sole means of security against people was to hide his wounds from them, and instinctively he tried to do this for two days, but now he felt incapable of keeping up the unequal struggle.\n\nHis despair was even intensified by the consciousness that he was utterly alone in his sorrow. In all Petersburg there was not a human being to whom he could express what he was feeling, who would feel for him, not as a high official, not as a member of society, but simply as a suffering man; indeed he had not such a one in the whole world.\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch grew up an orphan. There were two brothers. They did not remember their father, and their mother died when Alexey Alexandrovitch was ten years old. The property was a small one. Their uncle, Karenin, a government official of high standing, at one time a favorite of the late Tsar, had brought them up.\n\nOn completing his high school and university courses with medals, Alexey Alexandrovitch had, with his uncle’s aid, immediately started in a prominent position in the service, and from that time forward he had devoted himself exclusively to political ambition. In the high school and the university, and afterwards in the service, Alexey Alexandrovitch had never formed a close friendship with anyone. His brother had been the person nearest to his heart, but he had a post in the Ministry of Foreign Affairs, and was always abroad, where he had died shortly after Alexey Alexandrovitch’s marriage.\n\nWhile he was governor of a province, Anna’s aunt, a wealthy provincial lady, had thrown him—middle-aged as he was, though young for a governor—with her niece, and had succeeded in putting him in such a position that he had either to declare himself or to leave the town. Alexey Alexandrovitch was not long in hesitation. There were at the time as many reasons for the step as against it, and there was no overbalancing consideration to outweigh his invariable rule of abstaining when in doubt. But Anna’s aunt had through a common acquaintance insinuated that he had already compromised the girl, and that he was in honor bound to make her an offer. He made the offer, and concentrated on his betrothed and his wife all the feeling of which he was capable.\n\nThe attachment he felt to Anna precluded in his heart every need of intimate relations with others. And now among all his acquaintances he had not one friend. He had plenty of so-called connections, but no friendships. Alexey Alexandrovitch had plenty of people whom he could invite to dinner, to whose sympathy he could appeal in any public affair he was concerned about, whose interest he could reckon upon for anyone he wished to help, with whom he could candidly discuss other people’s business and affairs of state. But his relations with these people were confined to one clearly defined channel, and had a certain routine from which it was impossible to depart. There was one man, a comrade of his at the university, with whom he had made friends later, and with whom he could have spoken of a personal sorrow; but this friend had a post in the Department of Education in a remote part of Russia. Of the people in Petersburg the most intimate and most possible were his chief secretary and his doctor.\n\nMihail Vassilievitch Sludin, the chief secretary, was a straightforward, intelligent, good-hearted, and conscientious man, and Alexey Alexandrovitch was aware of his personal goodwill. But their five years of official work together seemed to have put a barrier between them that cut off warmer relations.\n\nAfter signing the papers brought him, Alexey Alexandrovitch had sat for a long while in silence, glancing at Mihail Vassilievitch, and several times he attempted to speak, but could not. He had already prepared the phrase: “You have heard of my trouble?” But he ended by saying, as usual: “So you’ll get this ready for me?” and with that dismissed him.\n\nThe other person was the doctor, who had also a kindly feeling for him; but there had long existed a taciturn understanding between them that both were weighed down by work, and always in a hurry.\n\nOf his women friends, foremost amongst them Countess Lidia Ivanovna, Alexey Alexandrovitch never thought. All women, simply as women, were terrible and distasteful to him.\n\n# Chapter 22\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch had forgotten the Countess Lidia Ivanovna, but she had not forgotten him. At the bitterest moment of his lonely despair she came to him, and without waiting to be announced, walked straight into his study. She found him as he was sitting with his head in both hands.\n\n“J’ai forcé la consigne,” she said, walking in with rapid steps and breathing hard with excitement and rapid exercise. “I have heard all! Alexey Alexandrovitch! Dear friend!” she went on, warmly squeezing his hand in both of hers and gazing with her fine pensive eyes into his.\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch, frowning, got up, and disengaging his hand, moved her a chair.\n\n“Won’t you sit down, countess? I’m seeing no one because I’m unwell, countess,” he said, and his lips twitched.\n\n“Dear friend!” repeated Countess Lidia Ivanovna, never taking her eyes off his, and suddenly her eyebrows rose at the inner corners, describing a triangle on her forehead, her ugly yellow face became still uglier, but Alexey Alexandrovitch felt that she was sorry for him and was preparing to cry. And he too was softened; he snatched her plump hand and proceeded to kiss it.\n\n“Dear friend!” she said in a voice breaking with emotion. “You ought not to give way to grief. Your sorrow is a great one, but you ought to find consolation.”\n\n“I am crushed, I am annihilated, I am no longer a man!” said Alexey Alexandrovitch, letting go her hand, but still gazing into her brimming eyes. “My position is so awful because I can find nowhere, I cannot find within me strength to support me.”\n\n“You will find support; seek it—not in me, though I beseech you to believe in my friendship,” she said, with a sigh. “Our support is love, that love that He has vouchsafed us. His burden is light,” she said, with the look of ecstasy Alexey Alexandrovitch knew so well. “He will be your support and your succor.”\n\nAlthough there was in these words a flavor of that sentimental emotion at her own lofty feelings, and that new mystical fervor which had lately gained ground in Petersburg, and which seemed to Alexey Alexandrovitch disproportionate, still it was pleasant to him to hear this now.\n\n“I am weak. I am crushed. I foresaw nothing, and now I understand nothing.”\n\n“Dear friend,” repeated Lidia Ivanovna.\n\n“It’s not the loss of what I have not now, it’s not that!” pursued Alexey Alexandrovitch. “I do not grieve for that. But I cannot help feeling humiliated before other people for the position I am placed in. It is wrong, but I can’t help it, I can’t help it.”\n\n“Not you it was performed that noble act of forgiveness, at which I was moved to ecstasy, and everyone else too, but He, working within your heart,” said Countess Lidia Ivanovna, raising her eyes rapturously, “and so you cannot be ashamed of your act.”\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch knitted his brows, and crooking his hands, he cracked his fingers.\n\n“One must know all the facts,” he said in his thin voice. “A man’s strength has its limits, countess, and I have reached my limits. The whole day I have had to be making arrangements, arrangements about household matters arising” (he emphasized the word arising) “from my new, solitary position. The servants, the governess, the accounts.... These pinpricks have stabbed me to the heart, and I have not the strength to bear it. At dinner ... yesterday, I was almost getting up from the dinner-table. I could not bear the way my son looked at me. He did not ask me the meaning of it all, but he wanted to ask, and I could not bear the look in his eyes. He was afraid to look at me, but that is not all....” Alexey Alexandrovitch would have referred to the bill that had been brought him, but his voice shook, and he stopped. That bill on blue paper, for a hat and ribbons, he could not recall without a rush of self-pity.\n\n“I understand, dear friend,” said Lidia Ivanovna. “I understand it all. Succor and comfort you will find not in me, though I have come only to aid you if I can. If I could take from off you all these petty, humiliating cares ... I understand that a woman’s word, a woman’s superintendence is needed. You will intrust it to me?”\n\nSilently and gratefully Alexey Alexandrovitch pressed her hand.\n\n“Together we will take care of Seryozha. Practical affairs are not my strong point. But I will set to work. I will be your housekeeper. Don’t thank me. I do it not from myself....”\n\n“I cannot help thanking you.”\n\n“But, dear friend, do not give way to the feeling of which you spoke—being ashamed of what is the Christian’s highest glory: he who humbles himself shall be exalted. And you cannot thank me. You must thank Him, and pray to Him for succor. In Him alone we find peace, consolation, salvation, and love,” she said, and turning her eyes heavenwards, she began praying, as Alexey Alexandrovitch gathered from her silence.\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch listened to her now, and those expressions which had seemed to him, if not distasteful, at least exaggerated, now seemed to him natural and consolatory. Alexey Alexandrovitch had disliked this new enthusiastic fervor. He was a believer, who was interested in religion primarily in its political aspect, and the new doctrine which ventured upon several new interpretations, just because it paved the way to discussion and analysis, was in principle disagreeable to him. He had hitherto taken up a cold and even antagonistic attitude to this new doctrine, and with Countess Lidia Ivanovna, who had been carried away by it, he had never argued, but by silence had assiduously parried her attempts to provoke him into argument. Now for the first time he heard her words with pleasure, and did not inwardly oppose them.\n\n“I am very, very grateful to you, both for your deeds and for your words,” he said, when she had finished praying.\n\nCountess Lidia Ivanovna once more pressed both her friend’s hands.\n\n“Now I will enter upon my duties,” she said with a smile after a pause, as she wiped away the traces of tears. “I am going to Seryozha. Only in the last extremity shall I apply to you.” And she got up and went out.\n\nCountess Lidia Ivanovna went into Seryozha’s part of the house, and dropping tears on the scared child’s cheeks, she told him that his father was a saint and his mother was dead.\n\nCountess Lidia Ivanovna kept her promise. She did actually take upon herself the care of the organization and management of Alexey Alexandrovitch’s household. But she had not overstated the case when saying that practical affairs were not her strong point. All her arrangements had to be modified because they could not be carried out, and they were modified by Korney, Alexey Alexandrovitch’s valet, who, though no one was aware of the fact, now managed Karenin’s household, and quietly and discreetly reported to his master while he was dressing all it was necessary for him to know. But Lidia Ivanovna’s help was none the less real; she gave Alexey Alexandrovitch moral support in the consciousness of her love and respect for him, and still more, as it was soothing to her to believe, in that she almost turned him to Christianity—that is, from an indifferent and apathetic believer she turned him into an ardent and steadfast adherent of the new interpretation of Christian doctrine, which had been gaining ground of late in Petersburg. It was easy for Alexey Alexandrovitch to believe in this teaching. Alexey Alexandrovitch, like Lidia Ivanovna indeed, and others who shared their views, was completely devoid of vividness of imagination, that spiritual faculty in virtue of which the conceptions evoked by the imagination become so vivid that they must needs be in harmony with other conceptions, and with actual fact. He saw nothing impossible and inconceivable in the idea that death, though existing for unbelievers, did not exist for him, and that, as he was possessed of the most perfect faith, of the measure of which he was himself the judge, therefore there was no sin in his soul, and he was experiencing complete salvation here on earth.\n\nIt is true that the erroneousness and shallowness of this conception of his faith was dimly perceptible to Alexey Alexandrovitch, and he knew that when, without the slightest idea that his forgiveness was the action of a higher power, he had surrendered directly to the feeling of forgiveness, he had felt more happiness than now when he was thinking every instant that Christ was in his heart, and that in signing official papers he was doing His will. But for Alexey Alexandrovitch it was a necessity to think in that way; it was such a necessity for him in his humiliation to have some elevated standpoint, however imaginary, from which, looked down upon by all, he could look down on others, that he clung, as to his one salvation, to his delusion of salvation.\n\n# Chapter 23\n\nThe Countess Lidia Ivanovna had, as a very young and sentimental girl, been married to a wealthy man of high rank, an extremely good-natured, jovial, and extremely dissipated rake. Two months after marriage her husband abandoned her, and her impassioned protestations of affection he met with a sarcasm and even hostility that people knowing the count’s good heart, and seeing no defects in the sentimental Lidia, were at a loss to explain. Though they were divorced and lived apart, yet whenever the husband met the wife, he invariably behaved to her with the same malignant irony, the cause of which was incomprehensible.\n\nCountess Lidia Ivanovna had long given up being in love with her husband, but from that time she had never given up being in love with someone. She was in love with several people at once, both men and women; she had been in love with almost everyone who had been particularly distinguished in any way. She was in love with all the new princes and princesses who married into the imperial family; she had been in love with a high dignitary of the Church, a vicar, and a parish priest; she had been in love with a journalist, three Slavophiles, with Komissarov, with a minister, a doctor, an English missionary and Karenin. All these passions constantly waning or growing more ardent, did not prevent her from keeping up the most extended and complicated relations with the court and fashionable society. But from the time that after Karenin’s trouble she took him under her special protection, from the time that she set to work in Karenin’s household looking after his welfare, she felt that all her other attachments were not the real thing, and that she was now genuinely in love, and with no one but Karenin. The feeling she now experienced for him seemed to her stronger than any of her former feelings. Analyzing her feeling, and comparing it with former passions, she distinctly perceived that she would not have been in love with Komissarov if he had not saved the life of the Tsar, that she would not have been in love with Ristitch-Kudzhitsky if there had been no Slavonic question, but that she loved Karenin for himself, for his lofty, uncomprehended soul, for the sweet—to her—high notes of his voice, for his drawling intonation, his weary eyes, his character, and his soft white hands with their swollen veins. She was not simply overjoyed at meeting him, but she sought in his face signs of the impression she was making on him. She tried to please him, not by her words only, but in her whole person. For his sake it was that she now lavished more care on her dress than before. She caught herself in reveries on what might have been, if she had not been married and he had been free. She blushed with emotion when he came into the room, she could not repress a smile of rapture when he said anything amiable to her.\n\nFor several days now Countess Lidia Ivanovna had been in a state of intense excitement. She had learned that Anna and Vronsky were in Petersburg. Alexey Alexandrovitch must be saved from seeing her, he must be saved even from the torturing knowledge that that awful woman was in the same town with him, and that he might meet her any minute.\n\nLidia Ivanovna made inquiries through her friends as to what those infamous people, as she called Anna and Vronsky, intended doing, and she endeavored so to guide every movement of her friend during those days that he could not come across them. The young adjutant, an acquaintance of Vronsky, through whom she obtained her information, and who hoped through Countess Lidia Ivanovna to obtain a concession, told her that they had finished their business and were going away next day. Lidia Ivanovna had already begun to calm down, when the next morning a note was brought her, the handwriting of which she recognized with horror. It was the handwriting of Anna Karenina. The envelope was of paper as thick as bark; on the oblong yellow paper there was a huge monogram, and the letter smelt of agreeable scent.\n\n“Who brought it?”\n\n“A commissionaire from the hotel.”\n\nIt was some time before Countess Lidia Ivanovna could sit down to read the letter. Her excitement brought on an attack of asthma, to which she was subject. When she had recovered her composure, she read the following letter in French:\n\n“Madame la Comtesse,\n\n“The Christian feelings with which your heart is filled give me the, I feel, unpardonable boldness to write to you. I am miserable at being separated from my son. I entreat permission to see him once before my departure. Forgive me for recalling myself to your memory. I apply to you and not to Alexey Alexandrovitch, simply because I do not wish to cause that generous man to suffer in remembering me. Knowing your friendship for him, I know you will understand me. Could you send Seryozha to me, or should I come to the house at some fixed hour, or will you let me know when and where I could see him away from home? I do not anticipate a refusal, knowing the magnanimity of him with whom it rests. You cannot conceive the craving I have to see him, and so cannot conceive the gratitude your help will arouse in me.\n\n“Anna.”\n\nEverything in this letter exasperated Countess Lidia Ivanovna: its contents and the allusion to magnanimity, and especially its free and easy—as she considered—tone.\n\n“Say that there is no answer,” said Countess Lidia Ivanovna, and immediately opening her blotting-book, she wrote to Alexey Alexandrovitch that she hoped to see him at one o’clock at the levee.\n\n“I must talk with you of a grave and painful subject. There we will arrange where to meet. Best of all at my house, where I will order tea as you like it. Urgent. He lays the cross, but He gives the strength to bear it,” she added, so as to give him some slight preparation. Countess Lidia Ivanovna usually wrote some two or three letters a day to Alexey Alexandrovitch. She enjoyed that form of communication, which gave opportunity for a refinement and air of mystery not afforded by their personal interviews.\n\n# Chapter 24\n\nThe levee was drawing to a close. People met as they were going away, and gossiped of the latest news, of the newly bestowed honors and the changes in the positions of the higher functionaries.\n\n“If only Countess Marya Borissovna were Minister of War, and Princess Vatkovskaya were Commander-in-Chief,” said a gray-headed, little old man in a gold-embroidered uniform, addressing a tall, handsome maid of honor who had questioned him about the new appointments.\n\n“And me among the adjutants,” said the maid of honor, smiling.\n\n“You have an appointment already. You’re over the ecclesiastical department. And your assistant’s Karenin.”\n\n“Good-day, prince!” said the little old man to a man who came up to him.\n\n“What were you saying of Karenin?” said the prince.\n\n“He and Putyatov have received the Alexander Nevsky.”\n\n“I thought he had it already.”\n\n“No. Just look at him,” said the little old man, pointing with his embroidered hat to Karenin in a court uniform with the new red ribbon across his shoulders, standing in the doorway of the hall with an influential member of the Imperial Council. “Pleased and happy as a brass farthing,” he added, stopping to shake hands with a handsome gentleman of the bedchamber of colossal proportions.\n\n“No; he’s looking older,” said the gentleman of the bedchamber.\n\n“From overwork. He’s always drawing up projects nowadays. He won’t let a poor devil go nowadays till he’s explained it all to him under heads.”\n\n“Looking older, did you say? Il fait des passions. I believe Countess Lidia Ivanovna’s jealous now of his wife.”\n\n“Oh, come now, please don’t say any harm of Countess Lidia Ivanovna.”\n\n“Why, is there any harm in her being in love with Karenin?”\n\n“But is it true Madame Karenina’s here?”\n\n“Well, not here in the palace, but in Petersburg. I met her yesterday with Alexey Vronsky, bras dessous, bras dessous, in the Morsky.”\n\n“C’est un homme qui n’a pas,...” the gentleman of the bedchamber was beginning, but he stopped to make room, bowing, for a member of the Imperial family to pass.\n\nThus people talked incessantly of Alexey Alexandrovitch, finding fault with him and laughing at him, while he, blocking up the way of the member of the Imperial Council he had captured, was explaining to him point by point his new financial project, never interrupting his discourse for an instant for fear he should escape.\n\nAlmost at the same time that his wife left Alexey Alexandrovitch there had come to him that bitterest moment in the life of an official—the moment when his upward career comes to a full stop. This full stop had arrived and everyone perceived it, but Alexey Alexandrovitch himself was not yet aware that his career was over. Whether it was due to his feud with Stremov, or his misfortune with his wife, or simply that Alexey Alexandrovitch had reached his destined limits, it had become evident to everyone in the course of that year that his career was at an end. He still filled a position of consequence, he sat on many commissions and committees, but he was a man whose day was over, and from whom nothing was expected. Whatever he said, whatever he proposed, was heard as though it were something long familiar, and the very thing that was not needed. But Alexey Alexandrovitch was not aware of this, and, on the contrary, being cut off from direct participation in governmental activity, he saw more clearly than ever the errors and defects in the action of others, and thought it his duty to point out means for their correction. Shortly after his separation from his wife, he began writing his first note on the new judicial procedure, the first of the endless series of notes he was destined to write in the future.\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch did not merely fail to observe his hopeless position in the official world, he was not merely free from anxiety on this head, he was positively more satisfied than ever with his own activity.\n\n“He that is unmarried careth for the things that belong to the Lord, how he may please the Lord: but he that is married careth for the things that are of the world, how he may please his wife,” says the Apostle Paul, and Alexey Alexandrovitch, who was now guided in every action by Scripture, often recalled this text. It seemed to him that ever since he had been left without a wife, he had in these very projects of reform been serving the Lord more zealously than before.\n\nThe unmistakable impatience of the member of the Council trying to get away from him did not trouble Alexey Alexandrovitch; he gave up his exposition only when the member of the Council, seizing his chance when one of the Imperial family was passing, slipped away from him.\n\nLeft alone, Alexey Alexandrovitch looked down, collecting his thoughts, then looked casually about him and walked towards the door, where he hoped to meet Countess Lidia Ivanovna.\n\n“And how strong they all are, how sound physically,” thought Alexey Alexandrovitch, looking at the powerfully built gentleman of the bedchamber with his well-combed, perfumed whiskers, and at the red neck of the prince, pinched by his tight uniform. He had to pass them on his way. “Truly is it said that all the world is evil,” he thought, with another sidelong glance at the calves of the gentleman of the bedchamber.\n\nMoving forward deliberately, Alexey Alexandrovitch bowed with his customary air of weariness and dignity to the gentleman who had been talking about him, and looking towards the door, his eyes sought Countess Lidia Ivanovna.\n\n“Ah! Alexey Alexandrovitch!” said the little old man, with a malicious light in his eyes, at the moment when Karenin was on a level with them, and was nodding with a frigid gesture, “I haven’t congratulated you yet,” said the old man, pointing to his newly received ribbon.\n\n“Thank you,” answered Alexey Alexandrovitch. “What an exquisite day today,” he added, laying emphasis in his peculiar way on the word exquisite.\n\nThat they laughed at him he was well aware, but he did not expect anything but hostility from them; he was used to that by now.\n\nCatching sight of the yellow shoulders of Lidia Ivanovna jutting out above her corset, and her fine pensive eyes bidding him to her, Alexey Alexandrovitch smiled, revealing untarnished white teeth, and went towards her.\n\nLidia Ivanovna’s dress had cost her great pains, as indeed all her dresses had done of late. Her aim in dress was now quite the reverse of that she had pursued thirty years before. Then her desire had been to adorn herself with something, and the more adorned the better. Now, on the contrary, she was perforce decked out in a way so inconsistent with her age and her figure, that her one anxiety was to contrive that the contrast between these adornments and her own exterior should not be too appalling. And as far as Alexey Alexandrovitch was concerned she succeeded, and was in his eyes attractive. For him she was the one island not only of goodwill to him, but of love in the midst of the sea of hostility and jeering that surrounded him.\n\nPassing through rows of ironical eyes, he was drawn as naturally to her loving glance as a plant to the sun.\n\n“I congratulate you,” she said to him, her eyes on his ribbon.\n\nSuppressing a smile of pleasure, he shrugged his shoulders, closing his eyes, as though to say that that could not be a source of joy to him. Countess Lidia Ivanovna was very well aware that it was one of his chief sources of satisfaction, though he never admitted it.\n\n“How is our angel?” said Countess Lidia Ivanovna, meaning Seryozha.\n\n“I can’t say I was quite pleased with him,” said Alexey Alexandrovitch, raising his eyebrows and opening his eyes. “And Sitnikov is not satisfied with him.” (Sitnikov was the tutor to whom Seryozha’s secular education had been intrusted.) “As I have mentioned to you, there’s a sort of coldness in him towards the most important questions which ought to touch the heart of every man and every child....” Alexey Alexandrovitch began expounding his views on the sole question that interested him besides the service—the education of his son.\n\nWhen Alexey Alexandrovitch with Lidia Ivanovna’s help had been brought back anew to life and activity, he felt it his duty to undertake the education of the son left on his hands. Having never before taken any interest in educational questions, Alexey Alexandrovitch devoted some time to the theoretical study of the subject. After reading several books on anthropology, education, and didactics, Alexey Alexandrovitch drew up a plan of education, and engaging the best tutor in Petersburg to superintend it, he set to work, and the subject continually absorbed him.\n\n“Yes, but the heart. I see in him his father’s heart, and with such a heart a child cannot go far wrong,” said Lidia Ivanovna with enthusiasm.\n\n“Yes, perhaps.... As for me, I do my duty. It’s all I can do.”\n\n“You’re coming to me,” said Countess Lidia Ivanovna, after a pause; “we have to speak of a subject painful for you. I would give anything to have spared you certain memories, but others are not of the same mind. I have received a letter from her. She is here in Petersburg.”\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch shuddered at the allusion to his wife, but immediately his face assumed the deathlike rigidity which expressed utter helplessness in the matter.\n\n“I was expecting it,” he said.\n\nCountess Lidia Ivanovna looked at him ecstatically, and tears of rapture at the greatness of his soul came into her eyes.\n\n# Chapter 25\n\nWhen Alexey Alexandrovitch came into the Countess Lidia Ivanovna’s snug little boudoir, decorated with old china and hung with portraits, the lady herself had not yet made her appearance.\n\nShe was changing her dress.\n\nA cloth was laid on a round table, and on it stood a china tea service and a silver spirit-lamp and tea kettle. Alexey Alexandrovitch looked idly about at the endless familiar portraits which adorned the room, and sitting down to the table, he opened a New Testament lying upon it. The rustle of the countess’s silk skirt drew his attention off.\n\n“Well now, we can sit quietly,” said Countess Lidia Ivanovna, slipping hurriedly with an agitated smile between the table and the sofa, “and talk over our tea.”\n\nAfter some words of preparation, Countess Lidia Ivanovna, breathing hard and flushing crimson, gave into Alexey Alexandrovitch’s hands the letter she had received.\n\nAfter reading the letter, he sat a long while in silence.\n\n“I don’t think I have the right to refuse her,” he said, timidly lifting his eyes.\n\n“Dear friend, you never see evil in anyone!”\n\n“On the contrary, I see that all is evil. But whether it is just....”\n\nHis face showed irresolution, and a seeking for counsel, support, and guidance in a matter he did not understand.\n\n“No,” Countess Lidia Ivanovna interrupted him; “there are limits to everything. I can understand immorality,” she said, not quite truthfully, since she never could understand that which leads women to immorality; “but I don’t understand cruelty: to whom? to you! How can she stay in the town where you are? No, the longer one lives the more one learns. And I’m learning to understand your loftiness and her baseness.”\n\n“Who is to throw a stone?” said Alexey Alexandrovitch, unmistakably pleased with the part he had to play. “I have forgiven all, and so I cannot deprive her of what is exacted by love in her—by her love for her son....”\n\n“But is that love, my friend? Is it sincere? Admitting that you have forgiven—that you forgive—have we the right to work on the feelings of that angel? He looks on her as dead. He prays for her, and beseeches God to have mercy on her sins. And it is better so. But now what will he think?”\n\n“I had not thought of that,” said Alexey Alexandrovitch, evidently agreeing.\n\nCountess Lidia Ivanovna hid her face in her hands and was silent. She was praying.\n\n“If you ask my advice,” she said, having finished her prayer and uncovered her face, “I do not advise you to do this. Do you suppose I don’t see how you are suffering, how this has torn open your wounds? But supposing that, as always, you don’t think of yourself, what can it lead to?—to fresh suffering for you, to torture for the child. If there were a trace of humanity left in her, she ought not to wish for it herself. No, I have no hesitation in saying I advise not, and if you will intrust it to me, I will write to her.”\n\nAnd Alexey Alexandrovitch consented, and Countess Lidia Ivanovna sent the following letter in French:\n\n“Dear Madame,\n\n“To be reminded of you might have results for your son in leading to questions on his part which could not be answered without implanting in the child’s soul a spirit of censure towards what should be for him sacred, and therefore I beg you to interpret your husband’s refusal in the spirit of Christian love. I pray to Almighty God to have mercy on you.\n\n“Countess Lidia.”\n\nThis letter attained the secret object which Countess Lidia Ivanovna had concealed from herself. It wounded Anna to the quick.\n\nFor his part, Alexey Alexandrovitch, on returning home from Lidia Ivanovna’s, could not all that day concentrate himself on his usual pursuits, and find that spiritual peace of one saved and believing which he had felt of late.\n\nThe thought of his wife, who had so greatly sinned against him, and towards whom he had been so saintly, as Countess Lidia Ivanovna had so justly told him, ought not to have troubled him; but he was not easy; he could not understand the book he was reading; he could not drive away harassing recollections of his relations with her, of the mistake which, as it now seemed, he had made in regard to her. The memory of how he had received her confession of infidelity on their way home from the races (especially that he had insisted only on the observance of external decorum, and had not sent a challenge) tortured him like a remorse. He was tortured too by the thought of the letter he had written her; and most of all, his forgiveness, which nobody wanted, and his care of the other man’s child made his heart burn with shame and remorse.\n\nAnd just the same feeling of shame and regret he felt now, as he reviewed all his past with her, recalling the awkward words in which, after long wavering, he had made her an offer.\n\n“But how have I been to blame?” he said to himself. And this question always excited another question in him—whether they felt differently, did their loving and marrying differently, these Vronskys and Oblonskys ... these gentlemen of the bedchamber, with their fine calves. And there passed before his mind a whole series of these mettlesome, vigorous, self-confident men, who always and everywhere drew his inquisitive attention in spite of himself. He tried to dispel these thoughts, he tried to persuade himself that he was not living for this transient life, but for the life of eternity, and that there was peace and love in his heart.\n\nBut the fact that he had in this transient, trivial life made, as it seemed to him, a few trivial mistakes tortured him as though the eternal salvation in which he believed had no existence. But this temptation did not last long, and soon there was reestablished once more in Alexey Alexandrovitch’s soul the peace and the elevation by virtue of which he could forget what he did not want to remember.\n\n# Chapter 26\n\n“Well, Kapitonitch?” said Seryozha, coming back rosy and good-humored from his walk the day before his birthday, and giving his overcoat to the tall old hall-porter, who smiled down at the little person from the height of his long figure. “Well, has the bandaged clerk been here today? Did papa see him?”\n\n“He saw him. The minute the chief secretary came out, I announced him,” said the hall-porter with a good-humored wink. “Here, I’ll take it off.”\n\n“Seryozha!” said the tutor, stopping in the doorway leading to the inner rooms. “Take it off yourself.” But Seryozha, though he heard his tutor’s feeble voice, did not pay attention to it. He stood keeping hold of the hall-porter’s belt, and gazing into his face.\n\n“Well, and did papa do what he wanted for him?”\n\nThe hall-porter nodded his head affirmatively. The clerk with his face tied up, who had already been seven times to ask some favor of Alexey Alexandrovitch, interested both Seryozha and the hall-porter. Seryozha had come upon him in the hall, and had heard him plaintively beg the hall-porter to announce him, saying that he and his children had death staring them in the face.\n\nSince then Seryozha, having met him a second time in the hall, took great interest in him.\n\n“Well, was he very glad?” he asked.\n\n“Glad? I should think so! Almost dancing as he walked away.”\n\n“And has anything been left?” asked Seryozha, after a pause.\n\n“Come, sir,” said the hall-porter; then with a shake of his head he whispered, “Something from the countess.”\n\nSeryozha understood at once that what the hall-porter was speaking of was a present from Countess Lidia Ivanovna for his birthday.\n\n“What do you say? Where?”\n\n“Korney took it to your papa. A fine plaything it must be too!”\n\n“How big? Like this?”\n\n“Rather small, but a fine thing.”\n\n“A book.”\n\n“No, a thing. Run along, run along, Vassily Lukitch is calling you,” said the porter, hearing the tutor’s steps approaching, and carefully taking away from his belt the little hand in the glove half pulled off, he signed with his head towards the tutor.\n\n“Vassily Lukitch, in a tiny minute!” answered Seryozha with that gay and loving smile which always won over the conscientious Vassily Lukitch.\n\nSeryozha was too happy, everything was too delightful for him to be able to help sharing with his friend the porter the family good fortune of which he had heard during his walk in the public gardens from Lidia Ivanovna’s niece. This piece of good news seemed to him particularly important from its coming at the same time with the gladness of the bandaged clerk and his own gladness at toys having come for him. It seemed to Seryozha that this was a day on which everyone ought to be glad and happy.\n\n“You know papa’s received the Alexander Nevsky today?”\n\n“To be sure I do! People have been already to congratulate him.”\n\n“And is he glad?”\n\n“Glad at the Tsar’s gracious favor! I should think so! It’s a proof he’s deserved it,” said the porter severely and seriously.\n\nSeryozha fell to dreaming, gazing up at the face of the porter, which he had thoroughly studied in every detail, especially the chin that hung down between the gray whiskers, never seen by anyone but Seryozha, who saw him only from below.\n\n“Well, and has your daughter been to see you lately?”\n\nThe porter’s daughter was a ballet dancer.\n\n“When is she to come on week-days? They’ve their lessons to learn too. And you’ve your lesson, sir; run along.”\n\nOn coming into the room, Seryozha, instead of sitting down to his lessons, told his tutor of his supposition that what had been brought him must be a machine. “What do you think?” he inquired.\n\nBut Vassily Lukitch was thinking of nothing but the necessity of learning the grammar lesson for the teacher, who was coming at two.\n\n“No, do just tell me, Vassily Lukitch,” he asked suddenly, when he was seated at their work table with the book in his hands, “what is greater than the Alexander Nevsky? You know papa’s received the Alexander Nevsky?”\n\nVassily Lukitch replied that the Vladimir was greater than the Alexander Nevsky.\n\n“And higher still?”\n\n“Well, highest of all is the Andrey Pervozvanny.”\n\n“And higher than the Andrey?”\n\n“I don’t know.”\n\n“What, you don’t know?” and Seryozha, leaning on his elbows, sank into deep meditation.\n\nHis meditations were of the most complex and diverse character. He imagined his father’s having suddenly been presented with both the Vladimir and the Andrey today, and in consequence being much better tempered at his lesson, and dreamed how, when he was grown up, he would himself receive all the orders, and what they might invent higher than the Andrey. Directly any higher order were invented, he would win it. They would make a higher one still, and he would immediately win that too.\n\nThe time passed in such meditations, and when the teacher came, the lesson about the adverbs of place and time and manner of action was not ready, and the teacher was not only displeased, but hurt. This touched Seryozha. He felt he was not to blame for not having learned the lesson; however much he tried, he was utterly unable to do that. As long as the teacher was explaining to him, he believed him and seemed to comprehend, but as soon as he was left alone, he was positively unable to recollect and to understand that the short and familiar word “suddenly” is an adverb of manner of action. Still he was sorry that he had disappointed the teacher.\n\nHe chose a moment when the teacher was looking in silence at the book.\n\n“Mihail Ivanitch, when is your birthday?” he asked all, of a sudden.\n\n“You’d much better be thinking about your work. Birthdays are of no importance to a rational being. It’s a day like any other on which one has to do one’s work.”\n\nSeryozha looked intently at the teacher, at his scanty beard, at his spectacles, which had slipped down below the ridge on his nose, and fell into so deep a reverie that he heard nothing of what the teacher was explaining to him. He knew that the teacher did not think what he said; he felt it from the tone in which it was said. “But why have they all agreed to speak just in the same manner always the dreariest and most useless stuff? Why does he keep me off; why doesn’t he love me?” he asked himself mournfully, and could not think of an answer.\n\n# Chapter 27\n\nAfter the lesson with the grammar teacher came his father’s lesson. While waiting for his father, Seryozha sat at the table playing with a penknife, and fell to dreaming. Among Seryozha’s favorite occupations was searching for his mother during his walks. He did not believe in death generally, and in her death in particular, in spite of what Lidia Ivanovna had told him and his father had confirmed, and it was just because of that, and after he had been told she was dead, that he had begun looking for her when out for a walk. Every woman of full, graceful figure with dark hair was his mother. At the sight of such a woman such a feeling of tenderness was stirred within him that his breath failed him, and tears came into his eyes. And he was on the tiptoe of expectation that she would come up to him, would lift her veil. All her face would be visible, she would smile, she would hug him, he would sniff her fragrance, feel the softness of her arms, and cry with happiness, just as he had one evening lain on her lap while she tickled him, and he laughed and bit her white, ring-covered fingers. Later, when he accidentally learned from his old nurse that his mother was not dead, and his father and Lidia Ivanovna had explained to him that she was dead to him because she was wicked (which he could not possibly believe, because he loved her), he went on seeking her and expecting her in the same way. That day in the public gardens there had been a lady in a lilac veil, whom he had watched with a throbbing heart, believing it to be she as she came towards them along the path. The lady had not come up to them, but had disappeared somewhere. That day, more intensely than ever, Seryozha felt a rush of love for her, and now, waiting for his father, he forgot everything, and cut all round the edge of the table with his penknife, staring straight before him with sparkling eyes and dreaming of her.\n\n“Here is your papa!” said Vassily Lukitch, rousing him.\n\nSeryozha jumped up and went up to his father, and kissing his hand, looked at him intently, trying to discover signs of his joy at receiving the Alexander Nevsky.\n\n“Did you have a nice walk?” said Alexey Alexandrovitch, sitting down in his easy chair, pulling the volume of the Old Testament to him and opening it. Although Alexey Alexandrovitch had more than once told Seryozha that every Christian ought to know Scripture history thoroughly, he often referred to the Bible himself during the lesson, and Seryozha observed this.\n\n“Yes, it was very nice indeed, papa,” said Seryozha, sitting sideways on his chair and rocking it, which was forbidden. “I saw Nadinka” (Nadinka was a niece of Lidia Ivanovna’s who was being brought up in her house). “She told me you’d been given a new star. Are you glad, papa?”\n\n“First of all, don’t rock your chair, please,” said Alexey Alexandrovitch. “And secondly, it’s not the reward that’s precious, but the work itself. And I could have wished you understood that. If you now are going to work, to study in order to win a reward, then the work will seem hard to you; but when you work” (Alexey Alexandrovitch, as he spoke, thought of how he had been sustained by a sense of duty through the wearisome labor of the morning, consisting of signing one hundred and eighty papers), “loving your work, you will find your reward in it.”\n\nSeryozha’s eyes, that had been shining with gaiety and tenderness, grew dull and dropped before his father’s gaze. This was the same long-familiar tone his father always took with him, and Seryozha had learned by now to fall in with it. His father always talked to him—so Seryozha felt—as though he were addressing some boy of his own imagination, one of those boys that exist in books, utterly unlike himself. And Seryozha always tried with his father to act being the story-book boy.\n\n“You understand that, I hope?” said his father.\n\n“Yes, papa,” answered Seryozha, acting the part of the imaginary boy.\n\nThe lesson consisted of learning by heart several verses out of the Gospel and the repetition of the beginning of the Old Testament. The verses from the Gospel Seryozha knew fairly well, but at the moment when he was saying them he became so absorbed in watching the sharply protruding, bony knobbiness of his father’s forehead, that he lost the thread, and he transposed the end of one verse and the beginning of another. So it was evident to Alexey Alexandrovitch that he did not understand what he was saying, and that irritated him.\n\nHe frowned, and began explaining what Seryozha had heard many times before and never could remember, because he understood it too well, just as that “suddenly” is an adverb of manner of action. Seryozha looked with scared eyes at his father, and could think of nothing but whether his father would make him repeat what he had said, as he sometimes did. And this thought so alarmed Seryozha that he now understood nothing. But his father did not make him repeat it, and passed on to the lesson out of the Old Testament. Seryozha recounted the events themselves well enough, but when he had to answer questions as to what certain events prefigured, he knew nothing, though he had already been punished over this lesson. The passage at which he was utterly unable to say anything, and began fidgeting and cutting the table and swinging his chair, was where he had to repeat the patriarchs before the Flood. He did not know one of them, except Enoch, who had been taken up alive to heaven. Last time he had remembered their names, but now he had forgotten them utterly, chiefly because Enoch was the personage he liked best in the whole of the Old Testament, and Enoch’s translation to heaven was connected in his mind with a whole long train of thought, in which he became absorbed now while he gazed with fascinated eyes at his father’s watch-chain and a half-unbuttoned button on his waistcoat.\n\nIn death, of which they talked to him so often, Seryozha disbelieved entirely. He did not believe that those he loved could die, above all that he himself would die. That was to him something utterly inconceivable and impossible. But he had been told that all men die; he had asked people, indeed, whom he trusted, and they too, had confirmed it; his old nurse, too, said the same, though reluctantly. But Enoch had not died, and so it followed that everyone did not die. “And why cannot anyone else so serve God and be taken alive to heaven?” thought Seryozha. Bad people, that is those Seryozha did not like, they might die, but the good might all be like Enoch.\n\n“Well, what are the names of the patriarchs?”\n\n“Enoch, Enos—”\n\n“But you have said that already. This is bad, Seryozha, very bad. If you don’t try to learn what is more necessary than anything for a Christian,” said his father, getting up, “whatever can interest you? I am displeased with you, and Piotr Ignatitch” (this was the most important of his teachers) “is displeased with you.... I shall have to punish you.”\n\nHis father and his teacher were both displeased with Seryozha, and he certainly did learn his lessons very badly. But still it could not be said he was a stupid boy. On the contrary, he was far cleverer than the boys his teacher held up as examples to Seryozha. In his father’s opinion, he did not want to learn what he was taught. In reality he could not learn that. He could not, because the claims of his own soul were more binding on him than those claims his father and his teacher made upon him. Those claims were in opposition, and he was in direct conflict with his education. He was nine years old; he was a child; but he knew his own soul, it was precious to him, he guarded it as the eyelid guards the eye, and without the key of love he let no one into his soul. His teachers complained that he would not learn, while his soul was brimming over with thirst for knowledge. And he learned from Kapitonitch, from his nurse, from Nadinka, from Vassily Lukitch, but not from his teachers. The spring his father and his teachers reckoned upon to turn their mill-wheels had long dried up at the source, but its waters did their work in another channel.\n\nHis father punished Seryozha by not letting him go to see Nadinka, Lidia Ivanovna’s niece; but this punishment turned out happily for Seryozha. Vassily Lukitch was in a good humor, and showed him how to make windmills. The whole evening passed over this work and in dreaming how to make a windmill on which he could turn himself—clutching at the sails or tying himself on and whirling round. Of his mother Seryozha did not think all the evening, but when he had gone to bed, he suddenly remembered her, and prayed in his own words that his mother tomorrow for his birthday might leave off hiding herself and come to him.\n\n“Vassily Lukitch, do you know what I prayed for tonight extra besides the regular things?”\n\n“That you might learn your lessons better?”\n\n“No.”\n“Toys?”\n\n“No. You’ll never guess. A splendid thing; but it’s a secret! When it comes to pass I’ll tell you. Can’t you guess!”\n\n“No, I can’t guess. You tell me,” said Vassily Lukitch with a smile, which was rare with him. “Come, lie down, I’m putting out the candle.”\n\n“Without the candle I can see better what I see and what I prayed for. There! I was almost telling the secret!” said Seryozha, laughing gaily.\n\nWhen the candle was taken away, Seryozha heard and felt his mother. She stood over him, and with loving eyes caressed him. But then came windmills, a knife, everything began to be mixed up, and he fell asleep.\n\n# Chapter 28\n\nOn arriving in Petersburg, Vronsky and Anna stayed at one of the best hotels; Vronsky apart in a lower story, Anna above with her child, its nurse, and her maid, in a large suite of four rooms.\n\nOn the day of his arrival Vronsky went to his brother’s. There he found his mother, who had come from Moscow on business. His mother and sister-in-law greeted him as usual: they asked him about his stay abroad, and talked of their common acquaintances, but did not let drop a single word in allusion to his connection with Anna. His brother came the next morning to see Vronsky, and of his own accord asked him about her, and Alexey Vronsky told him directly that he looked upon his connection with Madame Karenina as marriage; that he hoped to arrange a divorce, and then to marry her, and until then he considered her as much a wife as any other wife, and he begged him to tell their mother and his wife so.\n\n“If the world disapproves, I don’t care,” said Vronsky; “but if my relations want to be on terms of relationship with me, they will have to be on the same terms with my wife.”\n\nThe elder brother, who had always a respect for his younger brother’s judgment, could not well tell whether he was right or not till the world had decided the question; for his part he had nothing against it, and with Alexey he went up to see Anna.\n\nBefore his brother, as before everyone, Vronsky addressed Anna with a certain formality, treating her as he might a very intimate friend, but it was understood that his brother knew their real relations, and they talked about Anna’s going to Vronsky’s estate.\n\nIn spite of all his social experience Vronsky was, in consequence of the new position in which he was placed, laboring under a strange misapprehension. One would have thought he must have understood that society was closed for him and Anna; but now some vague ideas had sprung up in his brain that this was only the case in old-fashioned days, and that now with the rapidity of modern progress (he had unconsciously become by now a partisan of every sort of progress) the views of society had changed, and that the question whether they would be received in society was not a foregone conclusion. “Of course,” he thought, “she would not be received at court, but intimate friends can and must look at it in the proper light.” One may sit for several hours at a stretch with one’s legs crossed in the same position, if one knows that there’s nothing to prevent one’s changing one’s position; but if a man knows that he must remain sitting so with crossed legs, then cramps come on, the legs begin to twitch and to strain towards the spot to which one would like to draw them. This was what Vronsky was experiencing in regard to the world. Though at the bottom of his heart he knew that the world was shut on them, he put it to the test whether the world had not changed by now and would not receive them. But he very quickly perceived that though the world was open for him personally, it was closed for Anna. Just as in the game of cat and mouse, the hands raised for him were dropped to bar the way for Anna.\n\nOne of the first ladies of Petersburg society whom Vronsky saw was his cousin Betsy.\n\n“At last!” she greeted him joyfully. “And Anna? How glad I am! Where are you stopping? I can fancy after your delightful travels you must find our poor Petersburg horrid. I can fancy your honeymoon in Rome. How about the divorce? Is that all over?”\n\nVronsky noticed that Betsy’s enthusiasm waned when she learned that no divorce had as yet taken place.\n\n“People will throw stones at me, I know,” she said, “but I shall come and see Anna; yes, I shall certainly come. You won’t be here long, I suppose?”\n\nAnd she did certainly come to see Anna the same day, but her tone was not at all the same as in former days. She unmistakably prided herself on her courage, and wished Anna to appreciate the fidelity of her friendship. She only stayed ten minutes, talking of society gossip, and on leaving she said:\n\n“You’ve never told me when the divorce is to be? Supposing I’m ready to fling my cap over the mill, other starchy people will give you the cold shoulder until you’re married. And that’s so simple nowadays. Ça se fait. So you’re going on Friday? Sorry we shan’t see each other again.”\n\nFrom Betsy’s tone Vronsky might have grasped what he had to expect from the world; but he made another effort in his own family. His mother he did not reckon upon. He knew that his mother, who had been so enthusiastic over Anna at their first acquaintance, would have no mercy on her now for having ruined her son’s career. But he had more hope of Varya, his brother’s wife. He fancied she would not throw stones, and would go simply and directly to see Anna, and would receive her in her own house.\n\nThe day after his arrival Vronsky went to her, and finding her alone, expressed his wishes directly.\n\n“You know, Alexey,” she said after hearing him, “how fond I am of you, and how ready I am to do anything for you; but I have not spoken, because I knew I could be of no use to you and to Anna Arkadyevna,” she said, articulating the name “Anna Arkadyevna” with particular care. “Don’t suppose, please, that I judge her. Never; perhaps in her place I should have done the same. I don’t and can’t enter into that,” she said, glancing timidly at his gloomy face. “But one must call things by their names. You want me to go and see her, to ask her here, and to rehabilitate her in society; but do understand that I cannot do so. I have daughters growing up, and I must live in the world for my husband’s sake. Well, I’m ready to come and see Anna Arkadyevna: she will understand that I can’t ask her here, or I should have to do so in such a way that she would not meet people who look at things differently; that would offend her. I can’t raise her....”\n\n“Oh, I don’t regard her as fallen more than hundreds of women you do receive!” Vronsky interrupted her still more gloomily, and he got up in silence, understanding that his sister-in-law’s decision was not to be shaken.\n\n“Alexey! don’t be angry with me. Please understand that I’m not to blame,” began Varya, looking at him with a timid smile.\n\n“I’m not angry with you,” he said still as gloomily; “but I’m sorry in two ways. I’m sorry, too, that this means breaking up our friendship—if not breaking up, at least weakening it. You will understand that for me, too, it cannot be otherwise.”\n\nAnd with that he left her.\n\nVronsky knew that further efforts were useless, and that he had to spend these few days in Petersburg as though in a strange town, avoiding every sort of relation with his own old circle in order not to be exposed to the annoyances and humiliations which were so intolerable to him. One of the most unpleasant features of his position in Petersburg was that Alexey Alexandrovitch and his name seemed to meet him everywhere. He could not begin to talk of anything without the conversation turning on Alexey Alexandrovitch; he could not go anywhere without risk of meeting him. So at least it seemed to Vronsky, just as it seems to a man with a sore finger that he is continually, as though on purpose, grazing his sore finger on everything.\n\nTheir stay in Petersburg was the more painful to Vronsky that he perceived all the time a sort of new mood that he could not understand in Anna. At one time she would seem in love with him, and then she would become cold, irritable, and impenetrable. She was worrying over something, and keeping something back from him, and did not seem to notice the humiliations which poisoned his existence, and for her, with her delicate intuition, must have been still more unbearable.\n\n# Chapter 29\n\nOne of Anna’s objects in coming back to Russia had been to see her son. From the day she left Italy the thought of it had never ceased to agitate her. And as she got nearer to Petersburg, the delight and importance of this meeting grew ever greater in her imagination. She did not even put to herself the question how to arrange it. It seemed to her natural and simple to see her son when she should be in the same town with him. But on her arrival in Petersburg she was suddenly made distinctly aware of her present position in society, and she grasped the fact that to arrange this meeting was no easy matter.\n\nShe had now been two days in Petersburg. The thought of her son never left her for a single instant, but she had not yet seen him. To go straight to the house, where she might meet Alexey Alexandrovitch, that she felt she had no right to do. She might be refused admittance and insulted. To write and so enter into relations with her husband—that it made her miserable to think of doing; she could only be at peace when she did not think of her husband. To get a glimpse of her son out walking, finding out where and when he went out, was not enough for her; she had so looked forward to this meeting, she had so much she must say to him, she so longed to embrace him, to kiss him. Seryozha’s old nurse might be a help to her and show her what to do. But the nurse was not now living in Alexey Alexandrovitch’s house. In this uncertainty, and in efforts to find the nurse, two days had slipped by.\n\nHearing of the close intimacy between Alexey Alexandrovitch and Countess Lidia Ivanovna, Anna decided on the third day to write to her a letter, which cost her great pains, and in which she intentionally said that permission to see her son must depend on her husband’s generosity. She knew that if the letter were shown to her husband, he would keep up his character of magnanimity, and would not refuse her request.\n\nThe commissionaire who took the letter had brought her back the most cruel and unexpected answer, that there was no answer. She had never felt so humiliated as at the moment when, sending for the commissionaire, she heard from him the exact account of how he had waited, and how afterwards he had been told there was no answer. Anna felt humiliated, insulted, but she saw that from her point of view Countess Lidia Ivanovna was right. Her suffering was the more poignant that she had to bear it in solitude. She could not and would not share it with Vronsky. She knew that to him, although he was the primary cause of her distress, the question of her seeing her son would seem a matter of very little consequence. She knew that he would never be capable of understanding all the depth of her suffering, that for his cool tone at any allusion to it she would begin to hate him. And she dreaded that more than anything in the world, and so she hid from him everything that related to her son. Spending the whole day at home she considered ways of seeing her son, and had reached a decision to write to her husband. She was just composing this letter when she was handed the letter from Lidia Ivanovna. The countess’s silence had subdued and depressed her, but the letter, all that she read between the lines in it, so exasperated her, this malice was so revolting beside her passionate, legitimate tenderness for her son, that she turned against other people and left off blaming herself.\n\n“This coldness—this pretense of feeling!” she said to herself. “They must needs insult me and torture the child, and I am to submit to it! Not on any consideration! She is worse than I am. I don’t lie, anyway.” And she decided on the spot that next day, Seryozha’s birthday, she would go straight to her husband’s house, bribe or deceive the servants, but at any cost see her son and overturn the hideous deception with which they were encompassing the unhappy child.\n\nShe went to a toy shop, bought toys and thought over a plan of action. She would go early in the morning at eight o’clock, when Alexey Alexandrovitch would be certain not to be up. She would have money in her hand to give the hall-porter and the footman, so that they should let her in, and not raising her veil, she would say that she had come from Seryozha’s godfather to congratulate him, and that she had been charged to leave the toys at his bedside. She had prepared everything but the words she should say to her son. Often as she had dreamed of it, she could never think of anything.\n\nThe next day, at eight o’clock in the morning, Anna got out of a hired sledge and rang at the front entrance of her former home.\n\n“Run and see what’s wanted. Some lady,” said Kapitonitch, who, not yet dressed, in his overcoat and galoshes, had peeped out of the window and seen a lady in a veil standing close up to the door. His assistant, a lad Anna did not know, had no sooner opened the door to her than she came in, and pulling a three-rouble note out of her muff put it hurriedly into his hand.\n\n“Seryozha—Sergey Alexeitch,” she said, and was going on. Scrutinizing the note, the porter’s assistant stopped her at the second glass door.\n\n“Whom do you want?” he asked.\n\nShe did not hear his words and made no answer.\n\nNoticing the embarrassment of the unknown lady, Kapitonitch went out to her, opened the second door for her, and asked her what she was pleased to want.\n\n“From Prince Skorodumov for Sergey Alexeitch,” she said.\n\n“His honor’s not up yet,” said the porter, looking at her attentively.\n\nAnna had not anticipated that the absolutely unchanged hall of the house where she had lived for nine years would so greatly affect her. Memories sweet and painful rose one after another in her heart, and for a moment she forgot what she was here for.\n\n“Would you kindly wait?” said Kapitonitch, taking off her fur cloak.\n\nAs he took off the cloak, Kapitonitch glanced at her face, recognized her, and made her a low bow in silence.\n\n“Please walk in, your excellency,” he said to her.\n\nShe tried to say something, but her voice refused to utter any sound; with a guilty and imploring glance at the old man she went with light, swift steps up the stairs. Bent double, and his galoshes catching in the steps, Kapitonitch ran after her, trying to overtake her.\n\n“The tutor’s there; maybe he’s not dressed. I’ll let him know.”\n\nAnna still mounted the familiar staircase, not understanding what the old man was saying.\n\n“This way, to the left, if you please. Excuse its not being tidy. His honor’s in the old parlor now,” the hall-porter said, panting. “Excuse me, wait a little, your excellency; I’ll just see,” he said, and overtaking her, he opened the high door and disappeared behind it. Anna stood still waiting. “He’s only just awake,” said the hall-porter, coming out. And at the very instant the porter said this, Anna caught the sound of a childish yawn. From the sound of this yawn alone she knew her son and seemed to see him living before her eyes.\n\n“Let me in; go away!” she said, and went in through the high doorway. On the right of the door stood a bed, and sitting up in the bed was the boy. His little body bent forward with his nightshirt unbuttoned, he was stretching and still yawning. The instant his lips came together they curved into a blissfully sleepy smile, and with that smile he slowly and deliciously rolled back again.\n\n“Seryozha!” she whispered, going noiselessly up to him.\n\nWhen she was parted from him, and all this latter time when she had been feeling a fresh rush of love for him, she had pictured him as he was at four years old, when she had loved him most of all. Now he was not even the same as when she had left him; he was still further from the four-year-old baby, more grown and thinner. How thin his face was, how short his hair was! What long hands! How he had changed since she left him! But it was he with his head, his lips, his soft neck and broad little shoulders.\n\n“Seryozha!” she repeated just in the child’s ear.\n\nHe raised himself again on his elbow, turned his tangled head from side to side as though looking for something, and opened his eyes. Slowly and inquiringly he looked for several seconds at his mother standing motionless before him, then all at once he smiled a blissful smile, and shutting his eyes, rolled not backwards but towards her into her arms.\n\n“Seryozha! my darling boy!” she said, breathing hard and putting her arms round his plump little body. “Mother!” he said, wriggling about in her arms so as to touch her hands with different parts of him.\n\nSmiling sleepily still with closed eyes, he flung fat little arms round her shoulders, rolled towards her, with the delicious sleepy warmth and fragrance that is only found in children, and began rubbing his face against her neck and shoulders.\n\n“I know,” he said, opening his eyes; “it’s my birthday today. I knew you’d come. I’ll get up directly.”\n\nAnd saying that he dropped asleep.\n\nAnna looked at him hungrily; she saw how he had grown and changed in her absence. She knew, and did not know, the bare legs so long now, that were thrust out below the quilt, those short-cropped curls on his neck in which she had so often kissed him. She touched all this and could say nothing; tears choked her.\n\n“What are you crying for, mother?” he said, waking completely up. “Mother, what are you crying for?” he cried in a tearful voice.\n\n“I won’t cry ... I’m crying for joy. It’s so long since I’ve seen you. I won’t, I won’t,” she said, gulping down her tears and turning away. “Come, it’s time for you to dress now,” she added, after a pause, and, never letting go his hands, she sat down by his bedside on the chair, where his clothes were put ready for him.\n\n“How do you dress without me? How....” she tried to begin talking simply and cheerfully, but she could not, and again she turned away.\n\n“I don’t have a cold bath, papa didn’t order it. And you’ve not seen Vassily Lukitch? He’ll come in soon. Why, you’re sitting on my clothes!”\n\nAnd Seryozha went off into a peal of laughter. She looked at him and smiled.\n\n“Mother, darling, sweet one!” he shouted, flinging himself on her again and hugging her. It was as though only now, on seeing her smile, he fully grasped what had happened.\n\n“I don’t want that on,” he said, taking off her hat. And as it were, seeing her afresh without her hat, he fell to kissing her again.\n\n“But what did you think about me? You didn’t think I was dead?”\n\n“I never believed it.”\n\n“You didn’t believe it, my sweet?”\n\n“I knew, I knew!” he repeated his favorite phrase, and snatching the hand that was stroking his hair, he pressed the open palm to his mouth and kissed it.\n\n# Chapter 30\n\nMeanwhile Vassily Lukitch had not at first understood who this lady was, and had learned from their conversation that it was no other person than the mother who had left her husband, and whom he had not seen, as he had entered the house after her departure. He was in doubt whether to go in or not, or whether to communicate with Alexey Alexandrovitch. Reflecting finally that his duty was to get Seryozha up at the hour fixed, and that it was therefore not his business to consider who was there, the mother or anyone else, but simply to do his duty, he finished dressing, went to the door and opened it.\n\nBut the embraces of the mother and child, the sound of their voices, and what they were saying, made him change his mind.\n\nHe shook his head, and with a sigh he closed the door. “I’ll wait another ten minutes,” he said to himself, clearing his throat and wiping away tears.\n\nAmong the servants of the household there was intense excitement all this time. All had heard that their mistress had come, and that Kapitonitch had let her in, and that she was even now in the nursery, and that their master always went in person to the nursery at nine o’clock, and everyone fully comprehended that it was impossible for the husband and wife to meet, and that they must prevent it. Korney, the valet, going down to the hall-porter’s room, asked who had let her in, and how it was he had done so, and ascertaining that Kapitonitch had admitted her and shown her up, he gave the old man a talking-to. The hall-porter was doggedly silent, but when Korney told him he ought to be sent away, Kapitonitch darted up to him, and waving his hands in Korney’s face, began:\n\n“Oh yes, to be sure you’d not have let her in! After ten years’ service, and never a word but of kindness, and there you’d up and say, ‘Be off, go along, get away with you!’ Oh yes, you’re a shrewd one at politics, I dare say! You don’t need to be taught how to swindle the master, and to filch fur coats!”\n\n“Soldier!” said Korney contemptuously, and he turned to the nurse who was coming in. “Here, what do you think, Marya Efimovna: he let her in without a word to anyone,” Korney said addressing her. “Alexey Alexandrovitch will be down immediately—and go into the nursery!”\n\n“A pretty business, a pretty business!” said the nurse. “You, Korney Vassilievitch, you’d best keep him some way or other, the master, while I’ll run and get her away somehow. A pretty business!”\n\nWhen the nurse went into the nursery, Seryozha was telling his mother how he and Nadinka had had a fall in sledging downhill, and had turned over three times. She was listening to the sound of his voice, watching his face and the play of expression on it, touching his hand, but she did not follow what he was saying. She must go, she must leave him,—this was the only thing she was thinking and feeling. She heard the steps of Vassily Lukitch coming up to the door and coughing; she heard, too, the steps of the nurse as she came near; but she sat like one turned to stone, incapable of beginning to speak or to get up.\n\n“Mistress, darling!” began the nurse, going up to Anna and kissing her hands and shoulders. “God has brought joy indeed to our boy on his birthday. You aren’t changed one bit.”\n\n“Oh, nurse dear, I didn’t know you were in the house,” said Anna, rousing herself for a moment.\n\n“I’m not living here, I’m living with my daughter. I came for the birthday, Anna Arkadyevna, darling!”\n\nThe nurse suddenly burst into tears, and began kissing her hand again.\n\nSeryozha, with radiant eyes and smiles, holding his mother by one hand and his nurse by the other, pattered on the rug with his fat little bare feet. The tenderness shown by his beloved nurse to his mother threw him into an ecstasy.\n\n“Mother! She often comes to see me, and when she comes....” he was beginning, but he stopped, noticing that the nurse was saying something in a whisper to his mother, and that in his mother’s face there was a look of dread and something like shame, which was so strangely unbecoming to her.\n\nShe went up to him.\n\n“My sweet!” she said.\n\nShe could not say good-bye, but the expression on her face said it, and he understood. “Darling, darling Kootik!” she used the name by which she had called him when he was little, “you won’t forget me? You....” but she could not say more.\n\nHow often afterwards she thought of words she might have said. But now she did not know how to say it, and could say nothing. But Seryozha knew all she wanted to say to him. He understood that she was unhappy and loved him. He understood even what the nurse had whispered. He had caught the words “always at nine o’clock,” and he knew that this was said of his father, and that his father and mother could not meet. That he understood, but one thing he could not understand—why there should be a look of dread and shame in her face?... She was not in fault, but she was afraid of him and ashamed of something. He would have liked to put a question that would have set at rest this doubt, but he did not dare; he saw that she was miserable, and he felt for her. Silently he pressed close to her and whispered, “Don’t go yet. He won’t come just yet.”\n\nThe mother held him away from her to see what he was thinking, what to say to him, and in his frightened face she read not only that he was speaking of his father, but, as it were, asking her what he ought to think about his father.\n\n“Seryozha, my darling,” she said, “love him; he’s better and kinder than I am, and I have done him wrong. When you grow up you will judge.”\n\n“There’s no one better than you!...” he cried in despair through his tears, and, clutching her by the shoulders, he began squeezing her with all his force to him, his arms trembling with the strain.\n\n“My sweet, my little one!” said Anna, and she cried as weakly and childishly as he.\n\nAt that moment the door opened. Vassily Lukitch came in.\n\nAt the other door there was the sound of steps, and the nurse in a scared whisper said, “He’s coming,” and gave Anna her hat.\n\nSeryozha sank onto the bed and sobbed, hiding his face in his hands. Anna removed his hands, once more kissed his wet face, and with rapid steps went to the door. Alexey Alexandrovitch walked in, meeting her. Seeing her, he stopped short and bowed his head.\n\nAlthough she had just said he was better and kinder than she, in the rapid glance she flung at him, taking in his whole figure in all its details, feelings of repulsion and hatred for him and jealousy over her son took possession of her. With a swift gesture she put down her veil, and, quickening her pace, almost ran out of the room.\n\nShe had not time to undo, and so carried back with her, the parcel of toys she had chosen the day before in a toy shop with such love and sorrow.\n\n# Chapter 31\n\nAs intensely as Anna had longed to see her son, and long as she had been thinking of it and preparing herself for it, she had not in the least expected that seeing him would affect her so deeply. On getting back to her lonely rooms in the hotel she could not for a long while understand why she was there. “Yes, it’s all over, and I am again alone,” she said to herself, and without taking off her hat she sat down in a low chair by the hearth. Fixing her eyes on a bronze clock standing on a table between the windows, she tried to think.\n\nThe French maid brought from abroad came in to suggest she should dress. She gazed at her wonderingly and said, “Presently.” A footman offered her coffee. “Later on,” she said.\n\nThe Italian nurse, after having taken the baby out in her best, came in with her, and brought her to Anna. The plump, well-fed little baby, on seeing her mother, as she always did, held out her fat little hands, and with a smile on her toothless mouth, began, like a fish with a float, bobbing her fingers up and down the starched folds of her embroidered skirt, making them rustle. It was impossible not to smile, not to kiss the baby, impossible not to hold out a finger for her to clutch, crowing and prancing all over; impossible not to offer her a lip which she sucked into her little mouth by way of a kiss. And all this Anna did, and took her in her arms and made her dance, and kissed her fresh little cheek and bare little elbows; but at the sight of this child it was plainer than ever to her that the feeling she had for her could not be called love in comparison with what she felt for Seryozha. Everything in this baby was charming, but for some reason all this did not go deep to her heart. On her first child, though the child of an unloved father, had been concentrated all the love that had never found satisfaction. Her baby girl had been born in the most painful circumstances and had not had a hundredth part of the care and thought which had been concentrated on her first child. Besides, in the little girl everything was still in the future, while Seryozha was by now almost a personality, and a personality dearly loved. In him there was a conflict of thought and feeling; he understood her, he loved her, he judged her, she thought, recalling his words and his eyes. And she was forever—not physically only but spiritually—divided from him, and it was impossible to set this right.\n\nShe gave the baby back to the nurse, let her go, and opened the locket in which there was Seryozha’s portrait when he was almost of the same age as the girl. She got up, and, taking off her hat, took up from a little table an album in which there were photographs of her son at different ages. She wanted to compare them, and began taking them out of the album. She took them all out except one, the latest and best photograph. In it he was in a white smock, sitting astride a chair, with frowning eyes and smiling lips. It was his best, most characteristic expression. With her little supple hands, her white, delicate fingers, that moved with a peculiar intensity today, she pulled at a corner of the photograph, but the photograph had caught somewhere, and she could not get it out. There was no paper-knife on the table, and so, pulling out the photograph that was next to her son’s (it was a photograph of Vronsky taken at Rome in a round hat and with long hair), she used it to push out her son’s photograph. “Oh, here is he!” she said, glancing at the portrait of Vronsky, and she suddenly recalled that he was the cause of her present misery. She had not once thought of him all the morning. But now, coming all at once upon that manly, noble face, so familiar and so dear to her, she felt a sudden rush of love for him.\n\n“But where is he? How is it he leaves me alone in my misery?” she thought all at once with a feeling of reproach, forgetting she had herself kept from him everything concerning her son. She sent to ask him to come to her immediately; with a throbbing heart she awaited him, rehearsing to herself the words in which she would tell him all, and the expressions of love with which he would console her. The messenger returned with the answer that he had a visitor with him, but that he would come immediately, and that he asked whether she would let him bring with him Prince Yashvin, who had just arrived in Petersburg. “He’s not coming alone, and since dinner yesterday he has not seen me,” she thought; “he’s not coming so that I could tell him everything, but coming with Yashvin.” And all at once a strange idea came to her: what if he had ceased to love her?\n\nAnd going over the events of the last few days, it seemed to her that she saw in everything a confirmation of this terrible idea. The fact that he had not dined at home yesterday, and the fact that he had insisted on their taking separate sets of rooms in Petersburg, and that even now he was not coming to her alone, as though he were trying to avoid meeting her face to face.\n\n“But he ought to tell me so. I must know that it is so. If I knew it, then I know what I should do,” she said to herself, utterly unable to picture to herself the position she would be in if she were convinced of his not caring for her. She thought he had ceased to love her, she felt close upon despair, and consequently she felt exceptionally alert. She rang for her maid and went to her dressing-room. As she dressed, she took more care over her appearance than she had done all those days, as though he might, if he had grown cold to her, fall in love with her again because she had dressed and arranged her hair in the way most becoming to her.\n\nShe heard the bell ring before she was ready. When she went into the drawing-room it was not he, but Yashvin, who met her eyes. Vronsky was looking through the photographs of her son, which she had forgotten on the table, and he made no haste to look round at her.\n\n“We have met already,” she said, putting her little hand into the huge hand of Yashvin, whose bashfulness was so queerly out of keeping with his immense frame and coarse face. “We met last year at the races. Give them to me,” she said, with a rapid movement snatching from Vronsky the photographs of her son, and glancing significantly at him with flashing eyes. “Were the races good this year? Instead of them I saw the races in the Corso in Rome. But you don’t care for life abroad,” she said with a cordial smile. “I know you and all your tastes, though I have seen so little of you.”\n\n“I’m awfully sorry for that, for my tastes are mostly bad,” said Yashvin, gnawing at his left mustache.\n\nHaving talked a little while, and noticing that Vronsky glanced at the clock, Yashvin asked her whether she would be staying much longer in Petersburg, and unbending his huge figure reached after his cap.\n\n“Not long, I think,” she said hesitatingly, glancing at Vronsky.\n\n“So then we shan’t meet again?”\n\n“Come and dine with me,” said Anna resolutely, angry it seemed with herself for her embarrassment, but flushing as she always did when she defined her position before a fresh person. “The dinner here is not good, but at least you will see him. There is no one of his old friends in the regiment Alexey cares for as he does for you.”\n\n“Delighted,” said Yashvin with a smile, from which Vronsky could see that he liked Anna very much.\n\nYashvin said good-bye and went away; Vronsky stayed behind.\n\n“Are you going too?” she said to him.\n\n“I’m late already,” he answered. “Run along! I’ll catch you up in a moment,” he called to Yashvin.\n\nShe took him by the hand, and without taking her eyes off him, gazed at him while she ransacked her mind for the words to say that would keep him.\n\n“Wait a minute, there’s something I want to say to you,” and taking his broad hand she pressed it on her neck. “Oh, was it right my asking him to dinner?”\n\n“You did quite right,” he said with a serene smile that showed his even teeth, and he kissed her hand.\n\n“Alexey, you have not changed to me?” she said, pressing his hand in both of hers. “Alexey, I am miserable here. When are we going away?”\n\n“Soon, soon. You wouldn’t believe how disagreeable our way of living here is to me too,” he said, and he drew away his hand.\n\n“Well, go, go!” she said in a tone of offense, and she walked quickly away from him.\n\n# Chapter 32\n\nWhen Vronsky returned home, Anna was not yet home. Soon after he had left, some lady, so they told him, had come to see her, and she had gone out with her. That she had gone out without leaving word where she was going, that she had not yet come back, and that all the morning she had been going about somewhere without a word to him—all this, together with the strange look of excitement in her face in the morning, and the recollection of the hostile tone with which she had before Yashvin almost snatched her son’s photographs out of his hands, made him serious. He decided he absolutely must speak openly with her. And he waited for her in her drawing-room. But Anna did not return alone, but brought with her her old unmarried aunt, Princess Oblonskaya. This was the lady who had come in the morning, and with whom Anna had gone out shopping. Anna appeared not to notice Vronsky’s worried and inquiring expression, and began a lively account of her morning’s shopping. He saw that there was something working within her; in her flashing eyes, when they rested for a moment on him, there was an intense concentration, and in her words and movements there was that nervous rapidity and grace which, during the early period of their intimacy, had so fascinated him, but which now so disturbed and alarmed him.\n\nThe dinner was laid for four. All were gathered together and about to go into the little dining-room when Tushkevitch made his appearance with a message from Princess Betsy. Princess Betsy begged her to excuse her not having come to say good-bye; she had been indisposed, but begged Anna to come to her between half-past six and nine o’clock. Vronsky glanced at Anna at the precise limit of time, so suggestive of steps having been taken that she should meet no one; but Anna appeared not to notice it.\n\n“Very sorry that I can’t come just between half-past six and nine,” she said with a faint smile.\n\n“The princess will be very sorry.”\n\n“And so am I.”\n\n“You’re going, no doubt, to hear Patti?” said Tushkevitch.\n\n“Patti? You suggest the idea to me. I would go if it were possible to get a box.”\n\n“I can get one,” Tushkevitch offered his services.\n\n“I should be very, very grateful to you,” said Anna. “But won’t you dine with us?”\n\nVronsky gave a hardly perceptible shrug. He was at a complete loss to understand what Anna was about. What had she brought the old Princess Oblonskaya home for, what had she made Tushkevitch stay to dinner for, and, most amazing of all, why was she sending him for a box? Could she possibly think in her position of going to Patti’s benefit, where all the circle of her acquaintances would be? He looked at her with serious eyes, but she responded with that defiant, half-mirthful, half-desperate look, the meaning of which he could not comprehend. At dinner Anna was in aggressively high spirits—she almost flirted both with Tushkevitch and with Yashvin. When they got up from dinner and Tushkevitch had gone to get a box at the opera, Yashvin went to smoke, and Vronsky went down with him to his own rooms. After sitting there for some time he ran upstairs. Anna was already dressed in a low-necked gown of light silk and velvet that she had had made in Paris, and with costly white lace on her head, framing her face, and particularly becoming, showing up her dazzling beauty.\n\n“Are you really going to the theater?” he said, trying not to look at her.\n\n“Why do you ask with such alarm?” she said, wounded again at his not looking at her. “Why shouldn’t I go?”\n\nShe appeared not to understand the motive of his words.\n\n“Oh, of course, there’s no reason whatever,” he said, frowning.\n\n“That’s just what I say,” she said, willfully refusing to see the irony of his tone, and quietly turning back her long, perfumed glove.\n\n“Anna, for God’s sake! what is the matter with you?” he said, appealing to her exactly as once her husband had done.\n\n“I don’t understand what you are asking.”\n\n“You know that it’s out of the question to go.”\n\n“Why so? I’m not going alone. Princess Varvara has gone to dress, she is going with me.”\n\nHe shrugged his shoulders with an air of perplexity and despair.\n\n“But do you mean to say you don’t know?...” he began.\n\n“But I don’t care to know!” she almost shrieked. “I don’t care to. Do I regret what I have done? No, no, no! If it were all to do again from the beginning, it would be the same. For us, for you and for me, there is only one thing that matters, whether we love each other. Other people we need not consider. Why are we living here apart and not seeing each other? Why can’t I go? I love you, and I don’t care for anything,” she said in Russian, glancing at him with a peculiar gleam in her eyes that he could not understand. “If you have not changed to me, why don’t you look at me?”\n\nHe looked at her. He saw all the beauty of her face and full dress, always so becoming to her. But now her beauty and elegance were just what irritated him.\n\n“My feeling cannot change, you know, but I beg you, I entreat you,” he said again in French, with a note of tender supplication in his voice, but with coldness in his eyes.\n\nShe did not hear his words, but she saw the coldness of his eyes, and answered with irritation:\n\n“And I beg you to explain why I should not go.”\n\n“Because it might cause you....” he hesitated.\n\n“I don’t understand. Yashvin n’est pas compromettant, and Princess Varvara is no worse than others. Oh, here she is!”\n\n# Chapter 33\n\nVronsky for the first time experienced a feeling of anger against Anna, almost a hatred for her willfully refusing to understand her own position. This feeling was aggravated by his being unable to tell her plainly the cause of his anger. If he had told her directly what he was thinking, he would have said:\n\n“In that dress, with a princess only too well known to everyone, to show yourself at the theater is equivalent not merely to acknowledging your position as a fallen woman, but is flinging down a challenge to society, that is to say, cutting yourself off from it forever.”\n\nHe could not say that to her. “But how can she fail to see it, and what is going on in her?” he said to himself. He felt at the same time that his respect for her was diminished while his sense of her beauty was intensified.\n\nHe went back scowling to his rooms, and sitting down beside Yashvin, who, with his long legs stretched out on a chair, was drinking brandy and seltzer water, he ordered a glass of the same for himself.\n\n“You were talking of Lankovsky’s Powerful. That’s a fine horse, and I would advise you to buy him,” said Yashvin, glancing at his comrade’s gloomy face. “His hind-quarters aren’t quite first-rate, but the legs and head—one couldn’t wish for anything better.”\n\n“I think I will take him,” answered Vronsky.\n\nTheir conversation about horses interested him, but he did not for an instant forget Anna, and could not help listening to the sound of steps in the corridor and looking at the clock on the chimney piece.\n\n“Anna Arkadyevna gave orders to announce that she has gone to the theater.”\n\nYashvin, tipping another glass of brandy into the bubbling water, drank it and got up, buttoning his coat.\n\n“Well, let’s go,” he said, faintly smiling under his mustache, and showing by this smile that he knew the cause of Vronsky’s gloominess, and did not attach any significance to it.\n\n“I’m not going,” Vronsky answered gloomily.\n\n“Well, I must, I promised to. Good-bye, then. If you do, come to the stalls; you can take Kruzin’s stall,” added Yashvin as he went out.\n\n“No, I’m busy.”\n\n“A wife is a care, but it’s worse when she’s not a wife,” thought Yashvin, as he walked out of the hotel.\n\nVronsky, left alone, got up from his chair and began pacing up and down the room.\n\n“And what’s today? The fourth night.... Yegor and his wife are there, and my mother, most likely. Of course all Petersburg’s there. Now she’s gone in, taken off her cloak and come into the light. Tushkevitch, Yashvin, Princess Varvara,” he pictured them to himself.... “What about me? Either that I’m frightened or have given up to Tushkevitch the right to protect her? From every point of view—stupid, stupid!... And why is she putting me in such a position?” he said with a gesture of despair.\n\nWith that gesture he knocked against the table, on which there was standing the seltzer water and the decanter of brandy, and almost upset it. He tried to catch it, let it slip, and angrily kicked the table over and rang.\n\n“If you care to be in my service,” he said to the valet who came in, “you had better remember your duties. This shouldn’t be here. You ought to have cleared away.”\n\nThe valet, conscious of his own innocence, would have defended himself, but glancing at his master, he saw from his face that the only thing to do was to be silent, and hurriedly threading his way in and out, dropped down on the carpet and began gathering up the whole and broken glasses and bottles.\n\n“That’s not your duty; send the waiter to clear away, and get my dress coat out.”\n\nVronsky went into the theater at half-past eight. The performance was in full swing. The little old box-keeper, recognizing Vronsky as he helped him off with his fur coat, called him “Your Excellency,” and suggested he should not take a number but should simply call Fyodor. In the brightly lighted corridor there was no one but the box-opener and two attendants with fur cloaks on their arms listening at the doors. Through the closed doors came the sounds of the discreet staccato accompaniment of the orchestra, and a single female voice rendering distinctly a musical phrase. The door opened to let the box-opener slip through, and the phrase drawing to the end reached Vronsky’s hearing clearly. But the doors were closed again at once, and Vronsky did not hear the end of the phrase and the cadence of the accompaniment, though he knew from the thunder of applause that it was over. When he entered the hall, brilliantly lighted with chandeliers and gas jets, the noise was still going on. On the stage the singer, bowing and smiling, with bare shoulders flashing with diamonds, was, with the help of the tenor who had given her his arm, gathering up the bouquets that were flying awkwardly over the footlights. Then she went up to a gentleman with glossy pomaded hair parted down the center, who was stretching across the footlights holding out something to her, and all the public in the stalls as well as in the boxes was in excitement, craning forward, shouting and clapping. The conductor in his high chair assisted in passing the offering, and straightened his white tie. Vronsky walked into the middle of the stalls, and, standing still, began looking about him. That day less than ever was his attention turned upon the familiar, habitual surroundings, the stage, the noise, all the familiar, uninteresting, particolored herd of spectators in the packed theater.\n\nThere were, as always, the same ladies of some sort with officers of some sort in the back of the boxes; the same gaily dressed women—God knows who—and uniforms and black coats; the same dirty crowd in the upper gallery; and among the crowd, in the boxes and in the front rows, were some forty of the real people. And to those oases Vronsky at once directed his attention, and with them he entered at once into relation.\n\nThe act was over when he went in, and so he did not go straight to his brother’s box, but going up to the first row of stalls stopped at the footlights with Serpuhovskoy, who, standing with one knee raised and his heel on the footlights, caught sight of him in the distance and beckoned to him, smiling.\n\nVronsky had not yet seen Anna. He purposely avoided looking in her direction. But he knew by the direction of people’s eyes where she was. He looked round discreetly, but he was not seeking her; expecting the worst, his eyes sought for Alexey Alexandrovitch. To his relief Alexey Alexandrovitch was not in the theater that evening.\n\n“How little of the military man there is left in you!” Serpuhovskoy was saying to him. “A diplomat, an artist, something of that sort, one would say.”\n\n“Yes, it was like going back home when I put on a black coat,” answered Vronsky, smiling and slowly taking out his opera-glass.\n\n“Well, I’ll own I envy you there. When I come back from abroad and put on this,” he touched his epaulets, “I regret my freedom.”\n\nSerpuhovskoy had long given up all hope of Vronsky’s career, but he liked him as before, and was now particularly cordial to him.\n\n“What a pity you were not in time for the first act!”\n\nVronsky, listening with one ear, moved his opera-glass from the stalls and scanned the boxes. Near a lady in a turban and a bald old man, who seemed to wave angrily in the moving opera-glass, Vronsky suddenly caught sight of Anna’s head, proud, strikingly beautiful, and smiling in the frame of lace. She was in the fifth box, twenty paces from him. She was sitting in front, and slightly turning, was saying something to Yashvin. The setting of her head on her handsome, broad shoulders, and the restrained excitement and brilliance of her eyes and her whole face reminded him of her just as he had seen her at the ball in Moscow. But he felt utterly different towards her beauty now. In his feeling for her now there was no element of mystery, and so her beauty, though it attracted him even more intensely than before, gave him now a sense of injury. She was not looking in his direction, but Vronsky felt that she had seen him already.\n\nWhen Vronsky turned the opera-glass again in that direction, he noticed that Princess Varvara was particularly red, and kept laughing unnaturally and looking round at the next box. Anna, folding her fan and tapping it on the red velvet, was gazing away and did not see, and obviously did not wish to see, what was taking place in the next box. Yashvin’s face wore the expression which was common when he was losing at cards. Scowling, he sucked the left end of his mustache further and further into his mouth, and cast sidelong glances at the next box.\n\nIn that box on the left were the Kartasovs. Vronsky knew them, and knew that Anna was acquainted with them. Madame Kartasova, a thin little woman, was standing up in her box, and, her back turned upon Anna, she was putting on a mantle that her husband was holding for her. Her face was pale and angry, and she was talking excitedly. Kartasov, a fat, bald man, was continually looking round at Anna, while he attempted to soothe his wife. When the wife had gone out, the husband lingered a long while, and tried to catch Anna’s eye, obviously anxious to bow to her. But Anna, with unmistakable intention, avoided noticing him, and talked to Yashvin, whose cropped head was bent down to her. Kartasov went out without making his salutation, and the box was left empty.\n\nVronsky could not understand exactly what had passed between the Kartasovs and Anna, but he saw that something humiliating for Anna had happened. He knew this both from what he had seen, and most of all from the face of Anna, who, he could see, was taxing every nerve to carry through the part she had taken up. And in maintaining this attitude of external composure she was completely successful. Anyone who did not know her and her circle, who had not heard all the utterances of the women expressive of commiseration, indignation, and amazement, that she should show herself in society, and show herself so conspicuously with her lace and her beauty, would have admired the serenity and loveliness of this woman without a suspicion that she was undergoing the sensations of a man in the stocks.\n\nKnowing that something had happened, but not knowing precisely what, Vronsky felt a thrill of agonizing anxiety, and hoping to find out something, he went towards his brother’s box. Purposely choosing the way round furthest from Anna’s box, he jostled as he came out against the colonel of his old regiment talking to two acquaintances. Vronsky heard the name of Madame Karenina, and noticed how the colonel hastened to address Vronsky loudly by name, with a meaning glance at his companions.\n\n“Ah, Vronsky! When are you coming to the regiment? We can’t let you off without a supper. You’re one of the old set,” said the colonel of his regiment.\n\n“I can’t stop, awfully sorry, another time,” said Vronsky, and he ran upstairs towards his brother’s box.\n\nThe old countess, Vronsky’s mother, with her steel-gray curls, was in his brother’s box. Varya with the young Princess Sorokina met him in the corridor.\n\nLeaving the Princess Sorokina with her mother, Varya held out her hand to her brother-in-law, and began immediately to speak of what interested him. She was more excited than he had ever seen her.\n\n“I think it’s mean and hateful, and Madame Kartasova had no right to do it. Madame Karenina....” she began.\n\n“But what is it? I don’t know.”\n\n“What? you’ve not heard?”\n\n“You know I should be the last person to hear of it.”\n\n“There isn’t a more spiteful creature than that Madame Kartasova!”\n\n“But what did she do?”\n\n“My husband told me.... She has insulted Madame Karenina. Her husband began talking to her across the box, and Madame Kartasova made a scene. She said something aloud, he says, something insulting, and went away.”\n\n“Count, your maman is asking for you,” said the young Princess Sorokina, peeping out of the door of the box.\n\n“I’ve been expecting you all the while,” said his mother, smiling sarcastically. “You were nowhere to be seen.”\n\nHer son saw that she could not suppress a smile of delight.\n\n“Good evening, maman. I have come to you,” he said coldly.\n\n“Why aren’t you going to faire la cour à Madame Karenina?” she went on, when Princess Sorokina had moved away. “Elle fait sensation. On oublie la Patti pour elle.”\n\n“Maman, I have asked you not to say anything to me of that,” he answered, scowling.\n\n“I’m only saying what everyone’s saying.”\n\nVronsky made no reply, and saying a few words to Princess Sorokina, he went away. At the door he met his brother.\n\n“Ah, Alexey!” said his brother. “How disgusting! Idiot of a woman, nothing else.... I wanted to go straight to her. Let’s go together.”\n\nVronsky did not hear him. With rapid steps he went downstairs; he felt that he must do something, but he did not know what. Anger with her for having put herself and him in such a false position, together with pity for her suffering, filled his heart. He went down, and made straight for Anna’s box. At her box stood Stremov, talking to her.\n\n“There are no more tenors. Le moule en est brisé!”\n\nVronsky bowed to her and stopped to greet Stremov.\n\n“You came in late, I think, and have missed the best song,” Anna said to Vronsky, glancing ironically, he thought, at him.\n\n“I am a poor judge of music,” he said, looking sternly at her.\n\n“Like Prince Yashvin,” she said smiling, “who considers that Patti sings too loud.”\n\n“Thank you,” she said, her little hand in its long glove taking the playbill Vronsky picked up, and suddenly at that instant her lovely face quivered. She got up and went into the interior of the box.\n\nNoticing in the next act that her box was empty, Vronsky, rousing indignant “hushes” in the silent audience, went out in the middle of a solo and drove home.\n\nAnna was already at home. When Vronsky went up to her, she was in the same dress as she had worn at the theater. She was sitting in the first armchair against the wall, looking straight before her. She looked at him, and at once resumed her former position.\n\n“Anna,” he said.\n\n“You, you are to blame for everything!” she cried, with tears of despair and hatred in her voice, getting up.\n\n“I begged, I implored you not to go, I knew it would be unpleasant....”\n\n“Unpleasant!” she cried—“hideous! As long as I live I shall never forget it. She said it was a disgrace to sit beside me.”\n\n“A silly woman’s chatter,” he said: “but why risk it, why provoke?...”\n\n“I hate your calm. You ought not to have brought me to this. If you had loved me....”\n\n“Anna! How does the question of my love come in?”\n\n“Oh, if you loved me, as I love, if you were tortured as I am!...” she said, looking at him with an expression of terror.\n\nHe was sorry for her, and angry notwithstanding. He assured her of his love because he saw that this was the only means of soothing her, and he did not reproach her in words, but in his heart he reproached her.\n\nAnd the asseverations of his love, which seemed to him so vulgar that he was ashamed to utter them, she drank in eagerly, and gradually became calmer. The next day, completely reconciled, they left for the country.\n\n# PART SIX Chapter 1\n\nDarya Alexandrovna spent the summer with her children at Pokrovskoe, at her sister Kitty Levin’s. The house on her own estate was quite in ruins, and Levin and his wife had persuaded her to spend the summer with them. Stepan Arkadyevitch greatly approved of the arrangement. He said he was very sorry his official duties prevented him from spending the summer in the country with his family, which would have been the greatest happiness for him; and remaining in Moscow, he came down to the country from time to time for a day or two. Besides the Oblonskys, with all their children and their governess, the old princess too came to stay that summer with the Levins, as she considered it her duty to watch over her inexperienced daughter in her interesting condition. Moreover, Varenka, Kitty’s friend abroad, kept her promise to come to Kitty when she was married, and stayed with her friend. All of these were friends or relations of Levin’s wife. And though he liked them all, he rather regretted his own Levin world and ways, which was smothered by this influx of the “Shtcherbatsky element,” as he called it to himself. Of his own relations there stayed with him only Sergey Ivanovitch, but he too was a man of the Koznishev and not the Levin stamp, so that the Levin spirit was utterly obliterated.\n\nIn the Levins’ house, so long deserted, there were now so many people that almost all the rooms were occupied, and almost every day it happened that the old princess, sitting down to table, counted them all over, and put the thirteenth grandson or granddaughter at a separate table. And Kitty, with her careful housekeeping, had no little trouble to get all the chickens, turkeys, and geese, of which so many were needed to satisfy the summer appetites of the visitors and children.\n\nThe whole family were sitting at dinner. Dolly’s children, with their governess and Varenka, were making plans for going to look for mushrooms. Sergey Ivanovitch, who was looked up to by all the party for his intellect and learning, with a respect that almost amounted to awe, surprised everyone by joining in the conversation about mushrooms.\n\n“Take me with you. I am very fond of picking mushrooms,” he said, looking at Varenka; “I think it’s a very nice occupation.”\n\n“Oh, we shall be delighted,” answered Varenka, coloring a little. Kitty exchanged meaningful glances with Dolly. The proposal of the learned and intellectual Sergey Ivanovitch to go looking for mushrooms with Varenka confirmed certain theories of Kitty’s with which her mind had been very busy of late. She made haste to address some remark to her mother, so that her look should not be noticed. After dinner Sergey Ivanovitch sat with his cup of coffee at the drawing-room window, and while he took part in a conversation he had begun with his brother, he watched the door through which the children would start on the mushroom-picking expedition. Levin was sitting in the window near his brother.\n\nKitty stood beside her husband, evidently awaiting the end of a conversation that had no interest for her, in order to tell him something.\n\n“You have changed in many respects since your marriage, and for the better,” said Sergey Ivanovitch, smiling to Kitty, and obviously little interested in the conversation, “but you have remained true to your passion for defending the most paradoxical theories.”\n\n“Katya, it’s not good for you to stand,” her husband said to her, putting a chair for her and looking significantly at her.\n\n“Oh, and there’s no time either,” added Sergey Ivanovitch, seeing the children running out.\n\nAt the head of them all Tanya galloped sideways, in her tightly-drawn stockings, and waving a basket and Sergey Ivanovitch’s hat, she ran straight up to him.\n\nBoldly running up to Sergey Ivanovitch with shining eyes, so like her father’s fine eyes, she handed him his hat and made as though she would put it on for him, softening her freedom by a shy and friendly smile.\n\n“Varenka’s waiting,” she said, carefully putting his hat on, seeing from Sergey Ivanovitch’s smile that she might do so.\n\nVarenka was standing at the door, dressed in a yellow print gown, with a white kerchief on her head.\n\n“I’m coming, I’m coming, Varvara Andreevna,” said Sergey Ivanovitch, finishing his cup of coffee, and putting into their separate pockets his handkerchief and cigar-case.\n\n“And how sweet my Varenka is! eh?” said Kitty to her husband, as soon as Sergey Ivanovitch rose. She spoke so that Sergey Ivanovitch could hear, and it was clear that she meant him to do so. “And how good-looking she is—such a refined beauty! Varenka!” Kitty shouted. “Shall you be in the mill copse? We’ll come out to you.”\n\n“You certainly forget your condition, Kitty,” said the old princess, hurriedly coming out at the door. “You mustn’t shout like that.”\n\nVarenka, hearing Kitty’s voice and her mother’s reprimand, went with light, rapid steps up to Kitty. The rapidity of her movement, her flushed and eager face, everything betrayed that something out of the common was going on in her. Kitty knew what this was, and had been watching her intently. She called Varenka at that moment merely in order mentally to give her a blessing for the important event which, as Kitty fancied, was bound to come to pass that day after dinner in the wood.\n\n“Varenka, I should be very happy if a certain something were to happen,” she whispered as she kissed her.\n\n“And are you coming with us?” Varenka said to Levin in confusion, pretending not to have heard what had been said.\n\n“I am coming, but only as far as the threshing-floor, and there I shall stop.”\n\n“Why, what do you want there?” said Kitty.\n\n“I must go to have a look at the new wagons, and to check the invoice,” said Levin; “and where will you be?”\n\n“On the terrace.”\n\n# Chapter 2\n\nOn the terrace were assembled all the ladies of the party. They always liked sitting there after dinner, and that day they had work to do there too. Besides the sewing and knitting of baby clothes, with which all of them were busy, that afternoon jam was being made on the terrace by a method new to Agafea Mihalovna, without the addition of water. Kitty had introduced this new method, which had been in use in her home. Agafea Mihalovna, to whom the task of jam-making had always been intrusted, considering that what had been done in the Levin household could not be amiss, had nevertheless put water with the strawberries, maintaining that the jam could not be made without it. She had been caught in the act, and was now making jam before everyone, and it was to be proved to her conclusively that jam could be very well made without water.\n\nAgafea Mihalovna, her face heated and angry, her hair untidy, and her thin arms bare to the elbows, was turning the preserving-pan over the charcoal stove, looking darkly at the raspberries and devoutly hoping they would stick and not cook properly. The princess, conscious that Agafea Mihalovna’s wrath must be chiefly directed against her, as the person responsible for the raspberry jam-making, tried to appear to be absorbed in other things and not interested in the jam, talked of other matters, but cast stealthy glances in the direction of the stove.\n\n“I always buy my maids’ dresses myself, of some cheap material,” the princess said, continuing the previous conversation. “Isn’t it time to skim it, my dear?” she added, addressing Agafea Mihalovna. “There’s not the slightest need for you to do it, and it’s hot for you,” she said, stopping Kitty.\n\n“I’ll do it,” said Dolly, and getting up, she carefully passed the spoon over the frothing sugar, and from time to time shook off the clinging jam from the spoon by knocking it on a plate that was covered with yellow-red scum and blood-colored syrup. “How they’ll enjoy this at tea-time!” she thought of her children, remembering how she herself as a child had wondered how it was the grown-up people did not eat what was best of all—the scum of the jam.\n\n“Stiva says it’s much better to give money.” Dolly took up meanwhile the weighty subject under discussion, what presents should be made to servants. “But....”\n\n“Money’s out of the question!” the princess and Kitty exclaimed with one voice. “They appreciate a present....”\n\n“Well, last year, for instance, I bought our Matrona Semyenovna, not a poplin, but something of that sort,” said the princess.\n\n“I remember she was wearing it on your nameday.”\n\n“A charming pattern—so simple and refined,—I should have liked it myself, if she hadn’t had it. Something like Varenka’s. So pretty and inexpensive.”\n\n“Well, now I think it’s done,” said Dolly, dropping the syrup from the spoon.\n\n“When it sets as it drops, it’s ready. Cook it a little longer, Agafea Mihalovna.”\n\n“The flies!” said Agafea Mihalovna angrily. “It’ll be just the same,” she added.\n\n“Ah! how sweet it is! don’t frighten it!” Kitty said suddenly, looking at a sparrow that had settled on the step and was pecking at the center of a raspberry.\n\n“Yes, but you keep a little further from the stove,” said her mother.\n\n“À propos de Varenka,” said Kitty, speaking in French, as they had been doing all the while, so that Agafea Mihalovna should not understand them, “you know, mamma, I somehow expect things to be settled today. You know what I mean. How splendid it would be!”\n\n“But what a famous matchmaker she is!” said Dolly. “How carefully and cleverly she throws them together!...”\n\n“No; tell me, mamma, what do you think?”\n\n“Why, what is one to think? He” (he meant Sergey Ivanovitch) “might at any time have been a match for anyone in Russia; now, of course, he’s not quite a young man, still I know ever so many girls would be glad to marry him even now.... She’s a very nice girl, but he might....”\n\n“Oh, no, mamma, do understand why, for him and for her too, nothing better could be imagined. In the first place, she’s charming!” said Kitty, crooking one of her fingers.\n\n“He thinks her very attractive, that’s certain,” assented Dolly.\n\n“Then he occupies such a position in society that he has no need to look for either fortune or position in his wife. All he needs is a good, sweet wife—a restful one.”\n\n“Well, with her he would certainly be restful,” Dolly assented.\n\n“Thirdly, that she should love him. And so it is ... that is, it would be so splendid!... I look forward to seeing them coming out of the forest—and everything settled. I shall see at once by their eyes. I should be so delighted! What do you think, Dolly?”\n\n“But don’t excite yourself. It’s not at all the thing for you to be excited,” said her mother.\n\n“Oh, I’m not excited, mamma. I fancy he will make her an offer today.”\n\n“Ah, that’s so strange, how and when a man makes an offer!... There is a sort of barrier, and all at once it’s broken down,” said Dolly, smiling pensively and recalling her past with Stepan Arkadyevitch.\n\n“Mamma, how did papa make you an offer?” Kitty asked suddenly.\n\n“There was nothing out of the way, it was very simple,” answered the princess, but her face beamed all over at the recollection.\n\n“Oh, but how was it? You loved him, anyway, before you were allowed to speak?”\n\nKitty felt a peculiar pleasure in being able now to talk to her mother on equal terms about those questions of such paramount interest in a woman’s life.\n\n“Of course I did; he had come to stay with us in the country.”\n\n“But how was it settled between you, mamma?”\n\n“You imagine, I dare say, that you invented something quite new? It’s always just the same: it was settled by the eyes, by smiles....”\n\n“How nicely you said that, mamma! It’s just by the eyes, by smiles that it’s done,” Dolly assented.\n\n“But what words did he say?”\n\n“What did Kostya say to you?”\n\n“He wrote it in chalk. It was wonderful.... How long ago it seems!” she said.\n\nAnd the three women all fell to musing on the same thing. Kitty was the first to break the silence. She remembered all that last winter before her marriage, and her passion for Vronsky.\n\n“There’s one thing ... that old love affair of Varenka’s,” she said, a natural chain of ideas bringing her to this point. “I should have liked to say something to Sergey Ivanovitch, to prepare him. They’re all—all men, I mean,” she added, “awfully jealous over our past.”\n\n“Not all,” said Dolly. “You judge by your own husband. It makes him miserable even now to remember Vronsky. Eh? that’s true, isn’t it?”\n\n“Yes,” Kitty answered, a pensive smile in her eyes.\n\n“But I really don’t know,” the mother put in in defense of her motherly care of her daughter, “what there was in your past that could worry him? That Vronsky paid you attentions—that happens to every girl.”\n\n“Oh, yes, but we didn’t mean that,” Kitty said, flushing a little.\n\n“No, let me speak,” her mother went on, “why, you yourself would not let me have a talk to Vronsky. Don’t you remember?”\n\n“Oh, mamma!” said Kitty, with an expression of suffering.\n\n“There’s no keeping you young people in check nowadays.... Your friendship could not have gone beyond what was suitable. I should myself have called upon him to explain himself. But, my darling, it’s not right for you to be agitated. Please remember that, and calm yourself.”\n\n“I’m perfectly calm, maman.”\n\n“How happy it was for Kitty that Anna came then,” said Dolly, “and how unhappy for her. It turned out quite the opposite,” she said, struck by her own ideas. “Then Anna was so happy, and Kitty thought herself unhappy. Now it is just the opposite. I often think of her.”\n\n“A nice person to think about! Horrid, repulsive woman—no heart,” said her mother, who could not forget that Kitty had married not Vronsky, but Levin.\n\n“What do you want to talk of it for?” Kitty said with annoyance. “I never think about it, and I don’t want to think of it.... And I don’t want to think of it,” she said, catching the sound of her husband’s well-known step on the steps of the terrace.\n\n“What’s that you don’t want to think about?” inquired Levin, coming onto the terrace.\n\nBut no one answered him, and he did not repeat the question.\n\n“I’m sorry I’ve broken in on your feminine parliament,” he said, looking round on everyone discontentedly, and perceiving that they had been talking of something which they would not talk about before him.\n\nFor a second he felt that he was sharing the feeling of Agafea Mihalovna, vexation at their making jam without water, and altogether at the outside Shtcherbatsky element. He smiled, however, and went up to Kitty.\n\n“Well, how are you?” he asked her, looking at her with the expression with which everyone looked at her now.\n\n“Oh, very well,” said Kitty, smiling, “and how have things gone with you?”\n\n“The wagons held three times as much as the old carts did. Well, are we going for the children? I’ve ordered the horses to be put in.”\n\n“What! you want to take Kitty in the wagonette?” her mother said reproachfully.\n\n“Yes, at a walking pace, princess.”\n\nLevin never called the princess “maman” as men often do call their mothers-in-law, and the princess disliked his not doing so. But though he liked and respected the princess, Levin could not call her so without a sense of profaning his feeling for his dead mother.\n\n“Come with us, maman,” said Kitty.\n\n“I don’t like to see such imprudence.”\n\n“Well, I’ll walk then, I’m so well.” Kitty got up and went to her husband and took his hand.\n\n“You may be well, but everything in moderation,” said the princess.\n\n“Well, Agafea Mihalovna, is the jam done?” said Levin, smiling to Agafea Mihalovna, and trying to cheer her up. “Is it all right in the new way?”\n\n“I suppose it’s all right. For our notions it’s boiled too long.”\n\n“It’ll be all the better, Agafea Mihalovna, it won’t mildew, even though our ice has begun to thaw already, so that we’ve no cool cellar to store it,” said Kitty, at once divining her husband’s motive, and addressing the old housekeeper with the same feeling; “but your pickle’s so good, that mamma says she never tasted any like it,” she added, smiling, and putting her kerchief straight.\n\nAgafea Mihalovna looked angrily at Kitty.\n\n“You needn’t try to console me, mistress. I need only to look at you with him, and I feel happy,” she said, and something in the rough familiarity of that with him touched Kitty.\n\n“Come along with us to look for mushrooms, you will show us the best places.” Agafea Mihalovna smiled and shook her head, as though to say: “I should like to be angry with you too, but I can’t.”\n\n“Do it, please, by my receipt,” said the princess; “put some paper over the jam, and moisten it with a little rum, and without even ice, it will never go mildewy.”\n\n# Chapter 3\n\nKitty was particularly glad of a chance of being alone with her husband, for she had noticed the shade of mortification that had passed over his face—always so quick to reflect every feeling—at the moment when he had come onto the terrace and asked what they were talking of, and had got no answer.\n\nWhen they had set off on foot ahead of the others, and had come out of sight of the house onto the beaten dusty road, marked with rusty wheels and sprinkled with grains of corn, she clung faster to his arm and pressed it closer to her. He had quite forgotten the momentary unpleasant impression, and alone with her he felt, now that the thought of her approaching motherhood was never for a moment absent from his mind, a new and delicious bliss, quite pure from all alloy of sense, in the being near to the woman he loved. There was no need of speech, yet he longed to hear the sound of her voice, which like her eyes had changed since she had been with child. In her voice, as in her eyes, there was that softness and gravity which is found in people continually concentrated on some cherished pursuit.\n\n“So you’re not tired? Lean more on me,” said he.\n\n“No, I’m so glad of a chance of being alone with you, and I must own, though I’m happy with them, I do regret our winter evenings alone.”\n\n“That was good, but this is even better. Both are better,” he said, squeezing her hand.\n\n“Do you know what we were talking about when you came in?”\n\n“About jam?”\n\n“Oh, yes, about jam too; but afterwards, about how men make offers.”\n\n“Ah!” said Levin, listening more to the sound of her voice than to the words she was saying, and all the while paying attention to the road, which passed now through the forest, and avoiding places where she might make a false step.\n\n“And about Sergey Ivanovitch and Varenka. You’ve noticed?... I’m very anxious for it,” she went on. “What do you think about it?” And she peeped into his face.\n\n“I don’t know what to think,” Levin answered, smiling. “Sergey seems very strange to me in that way. I told you, you know....”\n\n“Yes, that he was in love with that girl who died....”\n\n“That was when I was a child; I know about it from hearsay and tradition. I remember him then. He was wonderfully sweet. But I’ve watched him since with women; he is friendly, some of them he likes, but one feels that to him they’re simply people, not women.”\n\n“Yes, but now with Varenka ... I fancy there’s something....”\n\n“Perhaps there is.... But one has to know him.... He’s a peculiar, wonderful person. He lives a spiritual life only. He’s too pure, too exalted a nature.”\n\n“Why? Would this lower him, then?”\n\n“No, but he’s so used to a spiritual life that he can’t reconcile himself with actual fact, and Varenka is after all fact.”\n\nLevin had grown used by now to uttering his thought boldly, without taking the trouble of clothing it in exact language. He knew that his wife, in such moments of loving tenderness as now, would understand what he meant to say from a hint, and she did understand him.\n\n“Yes, but there’s not so much of that actual fact about her as about me. I can see that he would never have cared for me. She is altogether spiritual.”\n\n“Oh, no, he is so fond of you, and I am always so glad when my people like you....”\n\n“Yes, he’s very nice to me; but....”\n\n“It’s not as it was with poor Nikolay ... you really cared for each other,” Levin finished. “Why not speak of him?” he added. “I sometimes blame myself for not; it ends in one’s forgetting. Ah, how terrible and dear he was!... Yes, what were we talking about?” Levin said, after a pause.\n\n“You think he can’t fall in love,” said Kitty, translating into her own language.\n\n“It’s not so much that he can’t fall in love,” Levin said, smiling, “but he has not the weakness necessary.... I’ve always envied him, and even now, when I’m so happy, I still envy him.”\n\n“You envy him for not being able to fall in love?”\n\n“I envy him for being better than I,” said Levin. “He does not live for himself. His whole life is subordinated to his duty. And that’s why he can be calm and contented.”\n\n“And you?” Kitty asked, with an ironical and loving smile.\n\nShe could never have explained the chain of thought that made her smile; but the last link in it was that her husband, in exalting his brother and abasing himself, was not quite sincere. Kitty knew that this insincerity came from his love for his brother, from his sense of shame at being too happy, and above all from his unflagging craving to be better—she loved it in him, and so she smiled.\n\n“And you? What are you dissatisfied with?” she asked, with the same smile.\n\nHer disbelief in his self-dissatisfaction delighted him, and unconsciously he tried to draw her into giving utterance to the grounds of her disbelief.\n\n“I am happy, but dissatisfied with myself....” he said.\n\n“Why, how can you be dissatisfied with yourself if you are happy?”\n\n“Well, how shall I say?... In my heart I really care for nothing whatever but that you should not stumble—see? Oh, but really you mustn’t skip about like that!” he cried, breaking off to scold her for too agile a movement in stepping over a branch that lay in the path. “But when I think about myself, and compare myself with others, especially with my brother, I feel I’m a poor creature.”\n\n“But in what way?” Kitty pursued with the same smile. “Don’t you too work for others? What about your co-operative settlement, and your work on the estate, and your book?...”\n\n“Oh, but I feel, and particularly just now—it’s your fault,” he said, pressing her hand—“that all that doesn’t count. I do it in a way halfheartedly. If I could care for all that as I care for you!... Instead of that, I do it in these days like a task that is set me.”\n\n“Well, what would you say about papa?” asked Kitty. “Is he a poor creature then, as he does nothing for the public good?”\n\n“He?—no! But then one must have the simplicity, the straightforwardness, the goodness of your father: and I haven’t got that. I do nothing, and I fret about it. It’s all your doing. Before there was you—and this too,” he added with a glance towards her waist that she understood—“I put all my energies into work; now I can’t, and I’m ashamed; I do it just as though it were a task set me, I’m pretending....”\n\n“Well, but would you like to change this minute with Sergey Ivanovitch?” said Kitty. “Would you like to do this work for the general good, and to love the task set you, as he does, and nothing else?”\n\n“Of course not,” said Levin. “But I’m so happy that I don’t understand anything. So you think he’ll make her an offer today?” he added after a brief silence.\n\n“I think so, and I don’t think so. Only, I’m awfully anxious for it. Here, wait a minute.” She stooped down and picked a wild camomile at the edge of the path. “Come, count: he does propose, he doesn’t,” she said, giving him the flower.\n\n“He does, he doesn’t,” said Levin, tearing off the white petals.\n\n“No, no!” Kitty, snatching at his hand, stopped him. She had been watching his fingers with interest. “You picked off two.”\n\n“Oh, but see, this little one shan’t count to make up,” said Levin, tearing off a little half-grown petal. “Here’s the wagonette overtaking us.”\n\n“Aren’t you tired, Kitty?” called the princess.\n\n“Not in the least.”\n\n“If you are you can get in, as the horses are quiet and walking.”\n\nBut it was not worth while to get in, they were quite near the place, and all walked on together.\n\n# Chapter 4\n\nVarenka, with her white kerchief on her black hair, surrounded by the children, gaily and good-humoredly looking after them, and at the same time visibly excited at the possibility of receiving a declaration from the man she cared for, was very attractive. Sergey Ivanovitch walked beside her, and never left off admiring her. Looking at her, he recalled all the delightful things he had heard from her lips, all the good he knew about her, and became more and more conscious that the feeling he had for her was something special that he had felt long, long ago, and only once, in his early youth. The feeling of happiness in being near her continually grew, and at last reached such a point that, as he put a huge, slender-stalked agaric fungus in her basket, he looked straight into her face, and noticing the flush of glad and alarmed excitement that overspread her face, he was confused himself, and smiled to her in silence a smile that said too much.\n\n“If so,” he said to himself, “I ought to think it over and make up my mind, and not give way like a boy to the impulse of a moment.”\n\n“I’m going to pick by myself apart from all the rest, or else my efforts will make no show,” he said, and he left the edge of the forest where they were walking on low silky grass between old birch trees standing far apart, and went more into the heart of the wood, where between the white birch trunks there were gray trunks of aspen and dark bushes of hazel. Walking some forty paces away, Sergey Ivanovitch, knowing he was out of sight, stood still behind a bushy spindle-tree in full flower with its rosy red catkins. It was perfectly still all round him. Only overhead in the birches under which he stood, the flies, like a swarm of bees, buzzed unceasingly, and from time to time the children’s voices were floated across to him. All at once he heard, not far from the edge of the wood, the sound of Varenka’s contralto voice, calling Grisha, and a smile of delight passed over Sergey Ivanovitch’s face. Conscious of this smile, he shook his head disapprovingly at his own condition, and taking out a cigar, he began lighting it. For a long while he could not get a match to light against the trunk of a birch tree. The soft scales of the white bark rubbed off the phosphorus, and the light went out. At last one of the matches burned, and the fragrant cigar smoke, hovering uncertainly in flat, wide coils, stretched away forwards and upwards over a bush under the overhanging branches of a birch tree. Watching the streak of smoke, Sergey Ivanovitch walked gently on, deliberating on his position.\n\n“Why not?” he thought. “If it were only a passing fancy or a passion, if it were only this attraction—this mutual attraction (I can call it a mutual attraction), but if I felt that it was in contradiction with the whole bent of my life—if I felt that in giving way to this attraction I should be false to my vocation and my duty ... but it’s not so. The only thing I can say against it is that, when I lost Marie, I said to myself that I would remain faithful to her memory. That’s the only thing I can say against my feeling.... That’s a great thing,” Sergey Ivanovitch said to himself, feeling at the same time that this consideration had not the slightest importance for him personally, but would only perhaps detract from his romantic character in the eyes of others. “But apart from that, however much I searched, I should never find anything to say against my feeling. If I were choosing by considerations of suitability alone, I could not have found anything better.”\n\nHowever many women and girls he thought of whom he knew, he could not think of a girl who united to such a degree all, positively all, the qualities he would wish to see in his wife. She had all the charm and freshness of youth, but she was not a child; and if she loved him, she loved him consciously as a woman ought to love; that was one thing. Another point: she was not only far from being worldly, but had an unmistakable distaste for worldly society, and at the same time she knew the world, and had all the ways of a woman of the best society, which were absolutely essential to Sergey Ivanovitch’s conception of the woman who was to share his life. Thirdly: she was religious, and not like a child, unconsciously religious and good, as Kitty, for example, was, but her life was founded on religious principles. Even in trifling matters, Sergey Ivanovitch found in her all that he wanted in his wife: she was poor and alone in the world, so she would not bring with her a mass of relations and their influence into her husband’s house, as he saw now in Kitty’s case. She would owe everything to her husband, which was what he had always desired too for his future family life. And this girl, who united all these qualities, loved him. He was a modest man, but he could not help seeing it. And he loved her. There was one consideration against it—his age. But he came of a long-lived family, he had not a single gray hair, no one would have taken him for forty, and he remembered Varenka’s saying that it was only in Russia that men of fifty thought themselves old, and that in France a man of fifty considers himself dans la force de l’âge, while a man of forty is un jeune homme. But what did the mere reckoning of years matter when he felt as young in heart as he had been twenty years ago? Was it not youth to feel as he felt now, when coming from the other side to the edge of the wood he saw in the glowing light of the slanting sunbeams the gracious figure of Varenka in her yellow gown with her basket, walking lightly by the trunk of an old birch tree, and when this impression of the sight of Varenka blended so harmoniously with the beauty of the view, of the yellow oatfield lying bathed in the slanting sunshine, and beyond it the distant ancient forest flecked with yellow and melting into the blue of the distance? His heart throbbed joyously. A softened feeling came over him. He felt that he had made up his mind. Varenka, who had just crouched down to pick a mushroom, rose with a supple movement and looked round. Flinging away the cigar, Sergey Ivanovitch advanced with resolute steps towards her.\n\n# Chapter 5\n\n“Varvara Andreevna, when I was very young, I set before myself the ideal of the woman I loved and should be happy to call my wife. I have lived through a long life, and now for the first time I have met what I sought—in you. I love you, and offer you my hand.”\n\nSergey Ivanovitch was saying this to himself while he was ten paces from Varvara. Kneeling down, with her hands over the mushrooms to guard them from Grisha, she was calling little Masha.\n\n“Come here, little ones! There are so many!” she was saying in her sweet, deep voice.\n\nSeeing Sergey Ivanovitch approaching, she did not get up and did not change her position, but everything told him that she felt his presence and was glad of it.\n\n“Well, did you find some?” she asked from under the white kerchief, turning her handsome, gently smiling face to him.\n\n“Not one,” said Sergey Ivanovitch. “Did you?”\n\nShe did not answer, busy with the children who thronged about her.\n\n“That one too, near the twig,” she pointed out to little Masha a little fungus, split in half across its rosy cap by the dry grass from under which it thrust itself. Varenka got up while Masha picked the fungus, breaking it into two white halves. “This brings back my childhood,” she added, moving apart from the children beside Sergey Ivanovitch.\n\nThey walked on for some steps in silence. Varenka saw that he wanted to speak; she guessed of what, and felt faint with joy and panic. They had walked so far away that no one could hear them now, but still he did not begin to speak. It would have been better for Varenka to be silent. After a silence it would have been easier for them to say what they wanted to say than after talking about mushrooms. But against her own will, as it were accidentally, Varenka said:\n\n“So you found nothing? In the middle of the wood there are always fewer, though.” Sergey Ivanovitch sighed and made no answer. He was annoyed that she had spoken about the mushrooms. He wanted to bring her back to the first words she had uttered about her childhood; but after a pause of some length, as though against his own will, he made an observation in response to her last words.\n\n“I have heard that the white edible funguses are found principally at the edge of the wood, though I can’t tell them apart.”\n\nSome minutes more passed, they moved still further away from the children, and were quite alone. Varenka’s heart throbbed so that she heard it beating, and felt that she was turning red and pale and red again.\n\nTo be the wife of a man like Koznishev, after her position with Madame Stahl, was to her imagination the height of happiness. Besides, she was almost certain that she was in love with him. And this moment it would have to be decided. She felt frightened. She dreaded both his speaking and his not speaking.\n\nNow or never it must be said—that Sergey Ivanovitch felt too. Everything in the expression, the flushed cheeks and the downcast eyes of Varenka betrayed a painful suspense. Sergey Ivanovitch saw it and felt sorry for her. He felt even that to say nothing now would be a slight to her. Rapidly in his own mind he ran over all the arguments in support of his decision. He even said over to himself the words in which he meant to put his offer, but instead of those words, some utterly unexpected reflection that occurred to him made him ask:\n\n“What is the difference between the ‘birch’ mushroom and the ‘white’ mushroom?”\n\nVarenka’s lips quivered with emotion as she answered:\n\n“In the top part there is scarcely any difference, it’s in the stalk.”\n\nAnd as soon as these words were uttered, both he and she felt that it was over, that what was to have been said would not be said; and their emotion, which had up to then been continually growing more intense, began to subside.\n\n“The birch mushroom’s stalk suggests a dark man’s chin after two days without shaving,” said Sergey Ivanovitch, speaking quite calmly now.\n\n“Yes, that’s true,” answered Varenka smiling, and unconsciously the direction of their walk changed. They began to turn towards the children. Varenka felt both sore and ashamed; at the same time she had a sense of relief.\n\nWhen he had got home again and went over the whole subject, Sergey Ivanovitch thought his previous decision had been a mistaken one. He could not be false to the memory of Marie.\n\n“Gently, children, gently!” Levin shouted quite angrily to the children, standing before his wife to protect her when the crowd of children flew with shrieks of delight to meet them.\n\nBehind the children Sergey Ivanovitch and Varenka walked out of the wood. Kitty had no need to ask Varenka; she saw from the calm and somewhat crestfallen faces of both that her plans had not come off.\n\n“Well?” her husband questioned her as they were going home again.\n\n“It doesn’t bite,” said Kitty, her smile and manner of speaking recalling her father, a likeness Levin often noticed with pleasure.\n\n“How doesn’t bite?”\n\n“I’ll show you,” she said, taking her husband’s hand, lifting it to her mouth, and just faintly brushing it with closed lips. “Like a kiss on a priest’s hand.”\n\n“Which didn’t it bite with?” he said, laughing.\n\n“Both. But it should have been like this....”\n\n“There are some peasants coming....”\n\n“Oh, they didn’t see.”\n\n# Chapter 6\n\nDuring the time of the children’s tea the grown-up people sat in the balcony and talked as though nothing had happened, though they all, especially Sergey Ivanovitch and Varenka, were very well aware that there had happened an event which, though negative, was of very great importance. They both had the same feeling, rather like that of a schoolboy after an examination, which has left him in the same class or shut him out of the school forever. Everyone present, feeling too that something had happened, talked eagerly about extraneous subjects. Levin and Kitty were particularly happy and conscious of their love that evening. And their happiness in their love seemed to imply a disagreeable slur on those who would have liked to feel the same and could not—and they felt a prick of conscience.\n\n“Mark my words, Alexander will not come,” said the old princess.\n\nThat evening they were expecting Stepan Arkadyevitch to come down by train, and the old prince had written that possibly he might come too.\n\n“And I know why,” the princess went on; “he says that young people ought to be left alone for a while at first.”\n\n“But papa has left us alone. We’ve never seen him,” said Kitty. “Besides, we’re not young people!—we’re old, married people by now.”\n\n“Only if he doesn’t come, I shall say good-bye to you children,” said the princess, sighing mournfully.\n\n“What nonsense, mamma!” both the daughters fell upon her at once.\n\n“How do you suppose he is feeling? Why, now....”\n\nAnd suddenly there was an unexpected quiver in the princess’s voice. Her daughters were silent, and looked at one another. “Maman always finds something to be miserable about,” they said in that glance. They did not know that happy as the princess was in her daughter’s house, and useful as she felt herself to be there, she had been extremely miserable, both on her own account and her husband’s, ever since they had married their last and favorite daughter, and the old home had been left empty.\n\n“What is it, Agafea Mihalovna?” Kitty asked suddenly of Agafea Mihalovna, who was standing with a mysterious air, and a face full of meaning.\n\n“About supper.”\n\n“Well, that’s right,” said Dolly; “you go and arrange about it, and I’ll go and hear Grisha repeat his lesson, or else he will have nothing done all day.”\n\n“That’s my lesson! No, Dolly, I’m going,” said Levin, jumping up.\n\nGrisha, who was by now at a high school, had to go over the lessons of the term in the summer holidays. Darya Alexandrovna, who had been studying Latin with her son in Moscow before, had made it a rule on coming to the Levins’ to go over with him, at least once a day, the most difficult lessons of Latin and arithmetic. Levin had offered to take her place, but the mother, having once overheard Levin’s lesson, and noticing that it was not given exactly as the teacher in Moscow had given it, said resolutely, though with much embarrassment and anxiety not to mortify Levin, that they must keep strictly to the book as the teacher had done, and that she had better undertake it again herself. Levin was amazed both at Stepan Arkadyevitch, who, by neglecting his duty, threw upon the mother the supervision of studies of which she had no comprehension, and at the teachers for teaching the children so badly. But he promised his sister-in-law to give the lessons exactly as she wished. And he went on teaching Grisha, not in his own way, but by the book, and so took little interest in it, and often forgot the hour of the lesson. So it had been today.\n\n“No, I’m going, Dolly, you sit still,” he said. “We’ll do it all properly, like the book. Only when Stiva comes, and we go out shooting, then we shall have to miss it.”\n\nAnd Levin went to Grisha.\n\nVarenka was saying the same thing to Kitty. Even in the happy, well-ordered household of the Levins Varenka had succeeded in making herself useful.\n\n“I’ll see to the supper, you sit still,” she said, and got up to go to Agafea Mihalovna.\n\n“Yes, yes, most likely they’ve not been able to get chickens. If so, ours....”\n\n“Agafea Mihalovna and I will see about it,” and Varenka vanished with her.\n\n“What a nice girl!” said the princess.\n\n“Not nice, maman; she’s an exquisite girl; there’s no one else like her.”\n\n“So you are expecting Stepan Arkadyevitch today?” said Sergey Ivanovitch, evidently not disposed to pursue the conversation about Varenka. “It would be difficult to find two sons-in-law more unlike than yours,” he said with a subtle smile. “One all movement, only living in society, like a fish in water; the other our Kostya, lively, alert, quick in everything, but as soon as he is in society, he either sinks into apathy, or struggles helplessly like a fish on land.”\n\n“Yes, he’s very heedless,” said the princess, addressing Sergey Ivanovitch. “I’ve been meaning, indeed, to ask you to tell him that it’s out of the question for her” (she indicated Kitty) “to stay here; that she positively must come to Moscow. He talks of getting a doctor down....”\n\n“Maman, he’ll do everything; he has agreed to everything,” Kitty said, angry with her mother for appealing to Sergey Ivanovitch to judge in such a matter.\n\nIn the middle of their conversation they heard the snorting of horses and the sound of wheels on the gravel. Dolly had not time to get up to go and meet her husband, when from the window of the room below, where Grisha was having his lesson, Levin leaped out and helped Grisha out after him.\n\n“It’s Stiva!” Levin shouted from under the balcony. “We’ve finished, Dolly, don’t be afraid!” he added, and started running like a boy to meet the carriage.\n\n“Is ea id, ejus, ejus, ejus!” shouted Grisha, skipping along the avenue.\n\n“And someone else too! Papa, of course!” cried Levin, stopping at the entrance of the avenue. “Kitty, don’t come down the steep staircase, go round.”\n\nBut Levin had been mistaken in taking the person sitting in the carriage for the old prince. As he got nearer to the carriage he saw beside Stepan Arkadyevitch not the prince but a handsome, stout young man in a Scotch cap, with long ends of ribbon behind. This was Vassenka Veslovsky, a distant cousin of the Shtcherbatskys, a brilliant young gentleman in Petersburg and Moscow society. “A capital fellow, and a keen sportsman,” as Stepan Arkadyevitch said, introducing him.\n\nNot a whit abashed by the disappointment caused by his having come in place of the old prince, Veslovsky greeted Levin gaily, claiming acquaintance with him in the past, and snatching up Grisha into the carriage, lifted him over the pointer that Stepan Arkadyevitch had brought with him.\n\nLevin did not get into the carriage, but walked behind. He was rather vexed at the non-arrival of the old prince, whom he liked more and more the more he saw of him, and also at the arrival of this Vassenka Veslovsky, a quite uncongenial and superfluous person. He seemed to him still more uncongenial and superfluous when, on approaching the steps where the whole party, children and grown-up, were gathered together in much excitement, Levin saw Vassenka Veslovsky, with a particularly warm and gallant air, kissing Kitty’s hand.\n\n“Your wife and I are cousins and very old friends,” said Vassenka Veslovsky, once more shaking Levin’s hand with great warmth.\n\n“Well, are there plenty of birds?” Stepan Arkadyevitch said to Levin, hardly leaving time for everyone to utter their greetings. “We’ve come with the most savage intentions. Why, maman, they’ve not been in Moscow since! Look, Tanya, here’s something for you! Get it, please, it’s in the carriage, behind!” he talked in all directions. “How pretty you’ve grown, Dolly,” he said to his wife, once more kissing her hand, holding it in one of his, and patting it with the other.\n\nLevin, who a minute before had been in the happiest frame of mind, now looked darkly at everyone, and everything displeased him.\n\n“Who was it he kissed yesterday with those lips?” he thought, looking at Stepan Arkadyevitch’s tender demonstrations to his wife. He looked at Dolly, and he did not like her either.\n\n“She doesn’t believe in his love. So what is she so pleased about? Revolting!” thought Levin.\n\nHe looked at the princess, who had been so dear to him a minute before, and he did not like the manner in which she welcomed this Vassenka, with his ribbons, just as though she were in her own house.\n\nEven Sergey Ivanovitch, who had come out too onto the steps, seemed to him unpleasant with the show of cordiality with which he met Stepan Arkadyevitch, though Levin knew that his brother neither liked nor respected Oblonsky.\n\nAnd Varenka, even she seemed hateful, with her air sainte nitouche making the acquaintance of this gentleman, while all the while she was thinking of nothing but getting married.\n\nAnd more hateful than anyone was Kitty for falling in with the tone of gaiety with which this gentleman regarded his visit in the country, as though it were a holiday for himself and everyone else. And, above all, unpleasant was that particular smile with which she responded to his smile.\n\nNoisily talking, they all went into the house; but as soon as they were all seated, Levin turned and went out.\n\nKitty saw something was wrong with her husband. She tried to seize a moment to speak to him alone, but he made haste to get away from her, saying he was wanted at the counting-house. It was long since his own work on the estate had seemed to him so important as at that moment. “It’s all holiday for them,” he thought; “but these are no holiday matters, they won’t wait, and there’s no living without them.”\n\n# Chapter 7\n\nLevin came back to the house only when they sent to summon him to supper. On the stairs were standing Kitty and Agafea Mihalovna, consulting about wines for supper.\n\n“But why are you making all this fuss? Have what we usually do.”\n\n“No, Stiva doesn’t drink ... Kostya, stop, what’s the matter?” Kitty began, hurrying after him, but he strode ruthlessly away to the dining-room without waiting for her, and at once joined in the lively general conversation which was being maintained there by Vassenka Veslovsky and Stepan Arkadyevitch.\n\n“Well, what do you say, are we going shooting tomorrow?” said Stepan Arkadyevitch.\n\n“Please, do let’s go,” said Veslovsky, moving to another chair, where he sat down sideways, with one fat leg crossed under him.\n\n“I shall be delighted, we will go. And have you had any shooting yet this year?” said Levin to Veslovsky, looking intently at his leg, but speaking with that forced amiability that Kitty knew so well in him, and that was so out of keeping with him. “I can’t answer for our finding grouse, but there are plenty of snipe. Only we ought to start early. You’re not tired? Aren’t you tired, Stiva?”\n\n“Me tired? I’ve never been tired yet. Suppose we stay up all night. Let’s go for a walk!”\n\n“Yes, really, let’s not go to bed at all! Capital!” Veslovsky chimed in.\n\n“Oh, we all know you can do without sleep, and keep other people up too,” Dolly said to her husband, with that faint note of irony in her voice which she almost always had now with her husband. “But to my thinking, it’s time for bed now.... I’m going, I don’t want supper.”\n\n“No, do stay a little, Dolly,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, going round to her side behind the table where they were having supper. “I’ve so much still to tell you.”\n\n“Nothing really, I suppose.”\n\n“Do you know Veslovsky has been at Anna’s, and he’s going to them again? You know they’re hardly fifty miles from you, and I too must certainly go over there. Veslovsky, come here!”\n\nVassenka crossed over to the ladies, and sat down beside Kitty.\n\n“Ah, do tell me, please; you have stayed with her? How was she?” Darya Alexandrovna appealed to him.\n\nLevin was left at the other end of the table, and though never pausing in his conversation with the princess and Varenka, he saw that there was an eager and mysterious conversation going on between Stepan Arkadyevitch, Dolly, Kitty, and Veslovsky. And that was not all. He saw on his wife’s face an expression of real feeling as she gazed with fixed eyes on the handsome face of Vassenka, who was telling them something with great animation.\n\n“It’s exceedingly nice at their place,” Veslovsky was telling them about Vronsky and Anna. “I can’t, of course, take it upon myself to judge, but in their house you feel the real feeling of home.”\n\n“What do they intend doing?”\n\n“I believe they think of going to Moscow.”\n\n“How jolly it would be for us all to go over to them together! When are you going there?” Stepan Arkadyevitch asked Vassenka.\n\n“I’m spending July there.”\n\n“Will you go?” Stepan Arkadyevitch said to his wife.\n\n“I’ve been wanting to a long while; I shall certainly go,” said Dolly. “I am sorry for her, and I know her. She’s a splendid woman. I will go alone, when you go back, and then I shall be in no one’s way. And it will be better indeed without you.”\n\n“To be sure,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch. “And you, Kitty?”\n\n“I? Why should I go?” Kitty said, flushing all over, and she glanced round at her husband.\n\n“Do you know Anna Arkadyevna, then?” Veslovsky asked her. “She’s a very fascinating woman.”\n\n“Yes,” she answered Veslovsky, crimsoning still more. She got up and walked across to her husband.\n\n“Are you going shooting, then, tomorrow?” she said.\n\nHis jealousy had in these few moments, especially at the flush that had overspread her cheeks while she was talking to Veslovsky, gone far indeed. Now as he heard her words, he construed them in his own fashion. Strange as it was to him afterwards to recall it, it seemed to him at the moment clear that in asking whether he was going shooting, all she cared to know was whether he would give that pleasure to Vassenka Veslovsky, with whom, as he fancied, she was in love.\n\n“Yes, I’m going,” he answered her in an unnatural voice, disagreeable to himself.\n\n“No, better spend the day here tomorrow, or Dolly won’t see anything of her husband, and set off the day after,” said Kitty.\n\nThe motive of Kitty’s words was interpreted by Levin thus: “Don’t separate me from him. I don’t care about your going, but do let me enjoy the society of this delightful young man.”\n\n“Oh, if you wish, we’ll stay here tomorrow,” Levin answered, with peculiar amiability.\n\nVassenka meanwhile, utterly unsuspecting the misery his presence had occasioned, got up from the table after Kitty, and watching her with smiling and admiring eyes, he followed her.\n\nLevin saw that look. He turned white, and for a minute he could hardly breathe. “How dare he look at my wife like that!” was the feeling that boiled within him.\n\n“Tomorrow, then? Do, please, let us go,” said Vassenka, sitting down on a chair, and again crossing his leg as his habit was.\n\nLevin’s jealousy went further still. Already he saw himself a deceived husband, looked upon by his wife and her lover as simply necessary to provide them with the conveniences and pleasures of life.... But in spite of that he made polite and hospitable inquiries of Vassenka about his shooting, his gun, and his boots, and agreed to go shooting next day.\n\nHappily for Levin, the old princess cut short his agonies by getting up herself and advising Kitty to go to bed. But even at this point Levin could not escape another agony. As he said good-night to his hostess, Vassenka would again have kissed her hand, but Kitty, reddening, drew back her hand and said with a naïve bluntness, for which the old princess scolded her afterwards:\n\n“We don’t like that fashion.”\n\nIn Levin’s eyes she was to blame for having allowed such relations to arise, and still more to blame for showing so awkwardly that she did not like them.\n\n“Why, how can one want to go to bed!” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, who, after drinking several glasses of wine at supper, was now in his most charming and sentimental humor. “Look, Kitty,” he said, pointing to the moon, which had just risen behind the lime trees—“how exquisite! Veslovsky, this is the time for a serenade. You know, he has a splendid voice; we practiced songs together along the road. He has brought some lovely songs with him, two new ones. Varvara Andreevna and he must sing some duets.”\n\nWhen the party had broken up, Stepan Arkadyevitch walked a long while about the avenue with Veslovsky; their voices could be heard singing one of the new songs.\n\nLevin hearing these voices sat scowling in an easy-chair in his wife’s bedroom, and maintained an obstinate silence when she asked him what was wrong. But when at last with a timid glance she hazarded the question: “Was there perhaps something you disliked about Veslovsky?”—it all burst out, and he told her all. He was humiliated himself at what he was saying, and that exasperated him all the more.\n\nHe stood facing her with his eyes glittering menacingly under his scowling brows, and he squeezed his strong arms across his chest, as though he were straining every nerve to hold himself in. The expression of his face would have been grim, and even cruel, if it had not at the same time had a look of suffering which touched her. His jaws were twitching, and his voice kept breaking.\n\n“You must understand that I’m not jealous, that’s a nasty word. I can’t be jealous, and believe that.... I can’t say what I feel, but this is awful.... I’m not jealous, but I’m wounded, humiliated that anybody dare think, that anybody dare look at you with eyes like that.”\n\n“Eyes like what?” said Kitty, trying as conscientiously as possible to recall every word and gesture of that evening and every shade implied in them.\n\nAt the very bottom of her heart she did think there had been something precisely at the moment when he had crossed over after her to the other end of the table; but she dared not own it even to herself, and would have been even more unable to bring herself to say so to him, and so increase his suffering.\n\n“And what can there possibly be attractive about me as I am now?...”\n\n“Ah!” he cried, clutching at his head, “you shouldn’t say that!... If you had been attractive then....”\n\n“Oh, no, Kostya, oh, wait a minute, oh, do listen!” she said, looking at him with an expression of pained commiseration. “Why, what can you be thinking about! When for me there’s no one in the world, no one, no one!... Would you like me never to see anyone?”\n\nFor the first minute she had been offended at his jealousy; she was angry that the slightest amusement, even the most innocent, should be forbidden her; but now she would readily have sacrificed, not merely such trifles, but everything, for his peace of mind, to save him from the agony he was suffering.\n\n“You must understand the horror and comedy of my position,” he went on in a desperate whisper; “that he’s in my house, that he’s done nothing improper positively except his free and easy airs and the way he sits on his legs. He thinks it’s the best possible form, and so I’m obliged to be civil to him.”\n\n“But, Kostya, you’re exaggerating,” said Kitty, at the bottom of her heart rejoicing at the depth of his love for her, shown now in his jealousy.\n\n“The most awful part of it all is that you’re just as you always are, and especially now when to me you’re something sacred, and we’re so happy, so particularly happy—and all of a sudden a little wretch.... He’s not a little wretch; why should I abuse him? I have nothing to do with him. But why should my, and your, happiness....”\n\n“Do you know, I understand now what it’s all come from,” Kitty was beginning.\n\n“Well, what? what?”\n\n“I saw how you looked while we were talking at supper.”\n\n“Well, well!” Levin said in dismay.\n\nShe told him what they had been talking about. And as she told him, she was breathless with emotion. Levin was silent for a space, then he scanned her pale and distressed face, and suddenly he clutched at his head.\n\n“Katya, I’ve been worrying you! Darling, forgive me! It’s madness! Katya, I’m a criminal. And how could you be so distressed at such idiocy?”\n\n“Oh, I was sorry for you.”\n\n“For me? for me? How mad I am!... But why make you miserable? It’s awful to think that any outsider can shatter our happiness.”\n\n“It’s humiliating too, of course.”\n\n“Oh, then I’ll keep him here all the summer, and will overwhelm him with civility,” said Levin, kissing her hands. “You shall see. Tomorrow.... Oh, yes, we are going tomorrow.”\n\n# Chapter 8\n\nNext day, before the ladies were up, the wagonette and a trap for the shooting party were at the door, and Laska, aware since early morning that they were going shooting, after much whining and darting to and fro, had sat herself down in the wagonette beside the coachman, and, disapproving of the delay, was excitedly watching the door from which the sportsmen still did not come out. The first to come out was Vassenka Veslovsky, in new high boots that reached half-way up his thick thighs, in a green blouse, with a new Russian leather cartridge-belt, and in his Scotch cap with ribbons, with a brand-new English gun without a sling. Laska flew up to him, welcomed him, and jumping up, asked him in her own way whether the others were coming soon, but getting no answer from him, she returned to her post of observation and sank into repose again, her head on one side, and one ear pricked up to listen. At last the door opened with a creak, and Stepan Arkadyevitch’s spot-and-tan pointer Krak flew out, running round and round and turning over in the air. Stepan Arkadyevitch himself followed with a gun in his hand and a cigar in his mouth.\n\n“Good dog, good dog, Krak!” he cried encouragingly to the dog, who put his paws up on his chest, catching at his game bag. Stepan Arkadyevitch was dressed in rough leggings and spats, in torn trousers and a short coat. On his head there was a wreck of a hat of indefinite form, but his gun of a new patent was a perfect gem, and his game bag and cartridge belt, though worn, were of the very best quality.\n\nVassenka Veslovsky had had no notion before that it was truly chic for a sportsman to be in tatters, but to have his shooting outfit of the best quality. He saw it now as he looked at Stepan Arkadyevitch, radiant in his rags, graceful, well-fed, and joyous, a typical Russian nobleman. And he made up his mind that next time he went shooting he would certainly adopt the same get-up.\n\n“Well, and what about our host?” he asked.\n\n“A young wife,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, smiling.\n\n“Yes, and such a charming one!”\n\n“He came down dressed. No doubt he’s run up to her again.”\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch guessed right. Levin had run up again to his wife to ask her once more if she forgave him for his idiocy yesterday, and, moreover, to beg her for Christ’s sake to be more careful. The great thing was for her to keep away from the children—they might any minute push against her. Then he had once more to hear her declare that she was not angry with him for going away for two days, and to beg her to be sure to send him a note next morning by a servant on horseback, to write him, if it were but two words only, to let him know that all was well with her.\n\nKitty was distressed, as she always was, at parting for a couple of days from her husband, but when she saw his eager figure, looking big and strong in his shooting-boots and his white blouse, and a sort of sportsman elation and excitement incomprehensible to her, she forgot her own chagrin for the sake of his pleasure, and said good-bye to him cheerfully.\n\n“Pardon, gentlemen!” he said, running out onto the steps. “Have you put the lunch in? Why is the chestnut on the right? Well, it doesn’t matter. Laska, down; go and lie down!”\n\n“Put it with the herd of oxen,” he said to the herdsman, who was waiting for him at the steps with some question. “Excuse me, here comes another villain.”\n\nLevin jumped out of the wagonette, in which he had already taken his seat, to meet the carpenter, who came towards the steps with a rule in his hand.\n\n“You didn’t come to the counting house yesterday, and now you’re detaining me. Well, what is it?”\n\n“Would your honor let me make another turning? It’s only three steps to add. And we make it just fit at the same time. It will be much more convenient.”\n\n“You should have listened to me,” Levin answered with annoyance. “I said: Put the lines and then fit in the steps. Now there’s no setting it right. Do as I told you, and make a new staircase.”\n\nThe point was that in the lodge that was being built the carpenter had spoiled the staircase, fitting it together without calculating the space it was to fill, so that the steps were all sloping when it was put in place. Now the carpenter wanted, keeping the same staircase, to add three steps.\n\n“It will be much better.”\n\n“But where’s your staircase coming out with its three steps?”\n\n“Why, upon my word, sir,” the carpenter said with a contemptuous smile. “It comes out right at the very spot. It starts, so to speak,” he said, with a persuasive gesture; “it comes down, and comes down, and comes out.”\n\n“But three steps will add to the length too ... where is it to come out?”\n\n“Why, to be sure, it’ll start from the bottom and go up and go up, and come out so,” the carpenter said obstinately and convincingly.\n\n“It’ll reach the ceiling and the wall.”\n\n“Upon my word! Why, it’ll go up, and up, and come out like this.”\n\nLevin took out a ramrod and began sketching him the staircase in the dust.\n\n“There, do you see?”\n\n“As your honor likes,” said the carpenter, with a sudden gleam in his eyes, obviously understanding the thing at last. “It seems it’ll be best to make a new one.”\n\n“Well, then, do it as you’re told,” Levin shouted, seating himself in the wagonette. “Down! Hold the dogs, Philip!”\n\nLevin felt now at leaving behind all his family and household cares such an eager sense of joy in life and expectation that he was not disposed to talk. Besides that, he had that feeling of concentrated excitement that every sportsman experiences as he approaches the scene of action. If he had anything on his mind at that moment, it was only the doubt whether they would start anything in the Kolpensky marsh, whether Laska would show to advantage in comparison with Krak, and whether he would shoot well that day himself. Not to disgrace himself before a new spectator—not to be outdone by Oblonsky—that too was a thought that crossed his brain.\n\nOblonsky was feeling the same, and he too was not talkative. Vassenka Veslovsky kept up alone a ceaseless flow of cheerful chatter. As he listened to him now, Levin felt ashamed to think how unfair he had been to him the day before. Vassenka was really a nice fellow, simple, good-hearted, and very good-humored. If Levin had met him before he was married, he would have made friends with him. Levin rather disliked his holiday attitude to life and a sort of free and easy assumption of elegance. It was as though he assumed a high degree of importance in himself that could not be disputed, because he had long nails and a stylish cap, and everything else to correspond; but this could be forgiven for the sake of his good nature and good breeding. Levin liked him for his good education, for speaking French and English with such an excellent accent, and for being a man of his world.\n\nVassenka was extremely delighted with the left horse, a horse of the Don Steppes. He kept praising him enthusiastically. “How fine it must be galloping over the steppes on a steppe horse! Eh? isn’t it?” he said. He had imagined riding on a steppe horse as something wild and romantic, and it turned out nothing of the sort. But his simplicity, particularly in conjunction with his good looks, his amiable smile, and the grace of his movements, was very attractive. Either because his nature was sympathetic to Levin, or because Levin was trying to atone for his sins of the previous evening by seeing nothing but what was good in him, anyway he liked his society.\n\nAfter they had driven over two miles from home, Veslovsky all at once felt for a cigar and his pocketbook, and did not know whether he had lost them or left them on the table. In the pocketbook there were thirty-seven pounds, and so the matter could not be left in uncertainty.\n\n“Do you know what, Levin, I’ll gallop home on that left trace-horse. That will be splendid. Eh?” he said, preparing to get out.\n\n“No, why should you?” answered Levin, calculating that Vassenka could hardly weigh less than seventeen stone. “I’ll send the coachman.”\n\nThe coachman rode back on the trace-horse, and Levin himself drove the remaining pair.\n\n# Chapter 9\n\n“Well, now what’s our plan of campaign? Tell us all about it,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch.\n\n“Our plan is this. Now we’re driving to Gvozdyov. In Gvozdyov there’s a grouse marsh on this side, and beyond Gvozdyov come some magnificent snipe marshes where there are grouse too. It’s hot now, and we’ll get there—it’s fifteen miles or so—towards evening and have some evening shooting; we’ll spend the night there and go on tomorrow to the bigger moors.”\n\n“And is there nothing on the way?”\n\n“Yes; but we’ll reserve ourselves; besides it’s hot. There are two nice little places, but I doubt there being anything to shoot.”\n\nLevin would himself have liked to go into these little places, but they were near home; he could shoot them over any time, and they were only little places—there would hardly be room for three to shoot. And so, with some insincerity, he said that he doubted there being anything to shoot. When they reached a little marsh Levin would have driven by, but Stepan Arkadyevitch, with the experienced eye of a sportsman, at once detected reeds visible from the road.\n\n“Shan’t we try that?” he said, pointing to the little marsh.\n\n“Levin, do, please! how delightful!” Vassenka Veslovsky began begging, and Levin could but consent.\n\nBefore they had time to stop, the dogs had flown one before the other into the marsh.\n\n“Krak! Laska!...”\n\nThe dogs came back.\n\n“There won’t be room for three. I’ll stay here,” said Levin, hoping they would find nothing but peewits, who had been startled by the dogs, and turning over in their flight, were plaintively wailing over the marsh.\n\n“No! Come along, Levin, let’s go together!” Veslovsky called.\n\n“Really, there’s not room. Laska, back, Laska! You won’t want another dog, will you?”\n\nLevin remained with the wagonette, and looked enviously at the sportsmen. They walked right across the marsh. Except little birds and peewits, of which Vassenka killed one, there was nothing in the marsh.\n\n“Come, you see now that it was not that I grudged the marsh,” said Levin, “only it’s wasting time.”\n\n“Oh, no, it was jolly all the same. Did you see us?” said Vassenka Veslovsky, clambering awkwardly into the wagonette with his gun and his peewit in his hands. “How splendidly I shot this bird! Didn’t I? Well, shall we soon be getting to the real place?”\n\nThe horses started off suddenly, Levin knocked his head against the stock of someone’s gun, and there was the report of a shot. The gun did actually go off first, but that was how it seemed to Levin. It appeared that Vassenka Veslovsky had pulled only one trigger, and had left the other hammer still cocked. The charge flew into the ground without doing harm to anyone. Stepan Arkadyevitch shook his head and laughed reprovingly at Veslovsky. But Levin had not the heart to reprove him. In the first place, any reproach would have seemed to be called forth by the danger he had incurred and the bump that had come up on Levin’s forehead. And besides, Veslovsky was at first so naïvely distressed, and then laughed so good-humoredly and infectiously at their general dismay, that one could not but laugh with him.\n\nWhen they reached the second marsh, which was fairly large, and would inevitably take some time to shoot over, Levin tried to persuade them to pass it by. But Veslovsky again overpersuaded him. Again, as the marsh was narrow, Levin, like a good host, remained with the carriage.\n\nKrak made straight for some clumps of sedge. Vassenka Veslovsky was the first to run after the dog. Before Stepan Arkadyevitch had time to come up, a grouse flew out. Veslovsky missed it and it flew into an unmown meadow. This grouse was left for Veslovsky to follow up. Krak found it again and pointed, and Veslovsky shot it and went back to the carriage. “Now you go and I’ll stay with the horses,” he said.\n\nLevin had begun to feel the pangs of a sportsman’s envy. He handed the reins to Veslovsky and walked into the marsh.\n\nLaska, who had been plaintively whining and fretting against the injustice of her treatment, flew straight ahead to a hopeful place that Levin knew well, and that Krak had not yet come upon.\n\n“Why don’t you stop her?” shouted Stepan Arkadyevitch.\n\n“She won’t scare them,” answered Levin, sympathizing with his bitch’s pleasure and hurrying after her.\n\nAs she came nearer and nearer to the familiar breeding places there was more and more earnestness in Laska’s exploration. A little marsh bird did not divert her attention for more than an instant. She made one circuit round the clump of reeds, was beginning a second, and suddenly quivered with excitement and became motionless.\n\n“Come, come, Stiva!” shouted Levin, feeling his heart beginning to beat more violently; and all of a sudden, as though some sort of shutter had been drawn back from his straining ears, all sounds, confused but loud, began to beat on his hearing, losing all sense of distance. He heard the steps of Stepan Arkadyevitch, mistaking them for the tramp of the horses in the distance; he heard the brittle sound of the twigs on which he had trodden, taking this sound for the flying of a grouse. He heard too, not far behind him, a splashing in the water, which he could not explain to himself.\n\nPicking his steps, he moved up to the dog.\n\n“Fetch it!”\n\nNot a grouse but a snipe flew up from beside the dog. Levin had lifted his gun, but at the very instant when he was taking aim, the sound of splashing grew louder, came closer, and was joined with the sound of Veslovsky’s voice, shouting something with strange loudness. Levin saw he had his gun pointed behind the snipe, but still he fired.\n\nWhen he had made sure he had missed, Levin looked round and saw the horses and the wagonette not on the road but in the marsh.\n\nVeslovsky, eager to see the shooting, had driven into the marsh, and got the horses stuck in the mud.\n\n“Damn the fellow!” Levin said to himself, as he went back to the carriage that had sunk in the mire. “What did you drive in for?” he said to him dryly, and calling the coachman, he began pulling the horses out.\n\nLevin was vexed both at being hindered from shooting and at his horses getting stuck in the mud, and still more at the fact that neither Stepan Arkadyevitch nor Veslovsky helped him and the coachman to unharness the horses and get them out, since neither of them had the slightest notion of harnessing. Without vouchsafing a syllable in reply to Vassenka’s protestations that it had been quite dry there, Levin worked in silence with the coachman at extricating the horses. But then, as he got warm at the work and saw how assiduously Veslovsky was tugging at the wagonette by one of the mud-guards, so that he broke it indeed, Levin blamed himself for having under the influence of yesterday’s feelings been too cold to Veslovsky, and tried to be particularly genial so as to smooth over his chilliness. When everything had been put right, and the carriage had been brought back to the road, Levin had the lunch served.\n\n“Bon appétit—bonne conscience! Ce poulet va tomber jusqu’au fond de mes bottes,” Vassenka, who had recovered his spirits, quoted the French saying as he finished his second chicken. “Well, now our troubles are over, now everything’s going to go well. Only, to atone for my sins, I’m bound to sit on the box. That’s so? eh? No, no! I’ll be your Automedon. You shall see how I’ll get you along,” he answered, not letting go the rein, when Levin begged him to let the coachman drive. “No, I must atone for my sins, and I’m very comfortable on the box.” And he drove.\n\nLevin was a little afraid he would exhaust the horses, especially the chestnut, whom he did not know how to hold in; but unconsciously he fell under the influence of his gaiety and listened to the songs he sang all the way on the box, or the descriptions and representations he gave of driving in the English fashion, four-in-hand; and it was in the very best of spirits that after lunch they drove to the Gvozdyov marsh.\n\n# Chapter 10\n\nVassenka drove the horses so smartly that they reached the marsh too early, while it was still hot.\n\nAs they drew near this more important marsh, the chief aim of their expedition, Levin could not help considering how he could get rid of Vassenka and be free in his movements. Stepan Arkadyevitch evidently had the same desire, and on his face Levin saw the look of anxiety always present in a true sportsman when beginning shooting, together with a certain good-humored slyness peculiar to him.\n\n“How shall we go? It’s a splendid marsh, I see, and there are hawks,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, pointing to two great birds hovering over the reeds. “Where there are hawks, there is sure to be game.”\n\n“Now, gentlemen,” said Levin, pulling up his boots and examining the lock of his gun with rather a gloomy expression, “do you see those reeds?” He pointed to an oasis of blackish green in the huge half-mown wet meadow that stretched along the right bank of the river. “The marsh begins here, straight in front of us, do you see—where it is greener? From here it runs to the right where the horses are; there are breeding places there, and grouse, and all round those reeds as far as that alder, and right up to the mill. Over there, do you see, where the pools are? That’s the best place. There I once shot seventeen snipe. We’ll separate with the dogs and go in different directions, and then meet over there at the mill.”\n\n“Well, which shall go to left and which to right?” asked Stepan Arkadyevitch. “It’s wider to the right; you two go that way and I’ll take the left,” he said with apparent carelessness.\n\n“Capital! we’ll make the bigger bag! Yes, come along, come along!” Vassenka exclaimed.\n\nLevin could do nothing but agree, and they divided.\n\nAs soon as they entered the marsh, the two dogs began hunting about together and made towards the green, slime-covered pool. Levin knew Laska’s method, wary and indefinite; he knew the place too and expected a whole covey of snipe.\n\n“Veslovsky, beside me, walk beside me!” he said in a faint voice to his companion splashing in the water behind him. Levin could not help feeling an interest in the direction his gun was pointed, after that casual shot near the Kolpensky marsh.\n\n“Oh, I won’t get in your way, don’t trouble about me.”\n\nBut Levin could not help troubling, and recalled Kitty’s words at parting: “Mind you don’t shoot one another.” The dogs came nearer and nearer, passed each other, each pursuing its own scent. The expectation of snipe was so intense that to Levin the squelching sound of his own heel, as he drew it up out of the mire, seemed to be the call of a snipe, and he clutched and pressed the lock of his gun.\n\n“Bang! bang!” sounded almost in his ear. Vassenka had fired at a flock of ducks which was hovering over the marsh and flying at that moment towards the sportsmen, far out of range. Before Levin had time to look round, there was the whir of one snipe, another, a third, and some eight more rose one after another.\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch hit one at the very moment when it was beginning its zigzag movements, and the snipe fell in a heap into the mud. Oblonsky aimed deliberately at another, still flying low in the reeds, and together with the report of the shot, that snipe too fell, and it could be seen fluttering out where the sedge had been cut, its unhurt wing showing white beneath.\n\nLevin was not so lucky: he aimed at his first bird too low, and missed; he aimed at it again, just as it was rising, but at that instant another snipe flew up at his very feet, distracting him so that he missed again.\n\nWhile they were loading their guns, another snipe rose, and Veslovsky, who had had time to load again, sent two charges of small-shot into the water. Stepan Arkadyevitch picked up his snipe, and with sparkling eyes looked at Levin.\n\n“Well, now let us separate,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, and limping on his left foot, holding his gun in readiness and whistling to his dog, he walked off in one direction. Levin and Veslovsky walked in the other.\n\nIt always happened with Levin that when his first shots were a failure he got hot and out of temper, and shot badly the whole day. So it was that day. The snipe showed themselves in numbers. They kept flying up from just under the dogs, from under the sportsmen’s legs, and Levin might have retrieved his ill luck. But the more he shot, the more he felt disgraced in the eyes of Veslovsky, who kept popping away merrily and indiscriminately, killing nothing, and not in the slightest abashed by his ill success. Levin, in feverish haste, could not restrain himself, got more and more out of temper, and ended by shooting almost without a hope of hitting. Laska, indeed, seemed to understand this. She began looking more languidly, and gazed back at the sportsmen, as it were, with perplexity or reproach in her eyes. Shots followed shots in rapid succession. The smoke of the powder hung about the sportsmen, while in the great roomy net of the game bag there were only three light little snipe. And of these one had been killed by Veslovsky alone, and one by both of them together. Meanwhile from the other side of the marsh came the sound of Stepan Arkadyevitch’s shots, not frequent, but, as Levin fancied, well-directed, for almost after each they heard “Krak, Krak, apporte!”\n\nThis excited Levin still more. The snipe were floating continually in the air over the reeds. Their whirring wings close to the earth, and their harsh cries high in the air, could be heard on all sides; the snipe that had risen first and flown up into the air, settled again before the sportsmen. Instead of two hawks there were now dozens of them hovering with shrill cries over the marsh.\n\nAfter walking through the larger half of the marsh, Levin and Veslovsky reached the place where the peasants’ mowing-grass was divided into long strips reaching to the reeds, marked off in one place by the trampled grass, in another by a path mown through it. Half of these strips had already been mown.\n\nThough there was not so much hope of finding birds in the uncut part as the cut part, Levin had promised Stepan Arkadyevitch to meet him, and so he walked on with his companion through the cut and uncut patches.\n\n“Hi, sportsmen!” shouted one of a group of peasants, sitting on an unharnessed cart; “come and have some lunch with us! Have a drop of wine!”\n\nLevin looked round.\n\n“Come along, it’s all right!” shouted a good-humored-looking bearded peasant with a red face, showing his white teeth in a grin, and holding up a greenish bottle that flashed in the sunlight.\n\n“Qu’est-ce qu’ils disent?” asked Veslovsky.\n\n“They invite you to have some vodka. Most likely they’ve been dividing the meadow into lots. I should have some,” said Levin, not without some guile, hoping Veslovsky would be tempted by the vodka, and would go away to them.\n\n“Why do they offer it?”\n\n“Oh, they’re merry-making. Really, you should join them. You would be interested.”\n\n“Allons, c’est curieux.”\n\n“You go, you go, you’ll find the way to the mill!” cried Levin, and looking round he perceived with satisfaction that Veslovsky, bent and stumbling with weariness, holding his gun out at arm’s length, was making his way out of the marsh towards the peasants.\n\n“You come too!” the peasants shouted to Levin. “Never fear! You taste our cake!”\n\nLevin felt a strong inclination to drink a little vodka and to eat some bread. He was exhausted, and felt it a great effort to drag his staggering legs out of the mire, and for a minute he hesitated. But Laska was setting. And immediately all his weariness vanished, and he walked lightly through the swamp towards the dog. A snipe flew up at his feet; he fired and killed it. Laska still pointed.—“Fetch it!” Another bird flew up close to the dog. Levin fired. But it was an unlucky day for him; he missed it, and when he went to look for the one he had shot, he could not find that either. He wandered all about the reeds, but Laska did not believe he had shot it, and when he sent her to find it, she pretended to hunt for it, but did not really. And in the absence of Vassenka, on whom Levin threw the blame of his failure, things went no better. There were plenty of snipe still, but Levin made one miss after another.\n\nThe slanting rays of the sun were still hot; his clothes, soaked through with perspiration, stuck to his body; his left boot full of water weighed heavily on his leg and squeaked at every step; the sweat ran in drops down his powder-grimed face, his mouth was full of the bitter taste, his nose of the smell of powder and stagnant water, his ears were ringing with the incessant whir of the snipe; he could not touch the stock of his gun, it was so hot; his heart beat with short, rapid throbs; his hands shook with excitement, and his weary legs stumbled and staggered over the hillocks and in the swamp, but still he walked on and still he shot. At last, after a disgraceful miss, he flung his gun and his hat on the ground.\n\n“No, I must control myself,” he said to himself. Picking up his gun and his hat, he called Laska, and went out of the swamp. When he got on to dry ground he sat down, pulled off his boot and emptied it, then walked to the marsh, drank some stagnant-tasting water, moistened his burning hot gun, and washed his face and hands. Feeling refreshed, he went back to the spot where a snipe had settled, firmly resolved to keep cool.\n\nHe tried to be calm, but it was the same again. His finger pressed the cock before he had taken a good aim at the bird. It got worse and worse.\n\nHe had only five birds in his game-bag when he walked out of the marsh towards the alders where he was to rejoin Stepan Arkadyevitch.\n\nBefore he caught sight of Stepan Arkadyevitch he saw his dog. Krak darted out from behind the twisted root of an alder, black all over with the stinking mire of the marsh, and with the air of a conqueror sniffed at Laska. Behind Krak there came into view in the shade of the alder tree the shapely figure of Stepan Arkadyevitch. He came to meet him, red and perspiring, with unbuttoned neckband, still limping in the same way.\n\n“Well? You have been popping away!” he said, smiling good-humoredly.\n\n“How have you got on?” queried Levin. But there was no need to ask, for he had already seen the full game bag.\n\n“Oh, pretty fair.”\n\nHe had fourteen birds.\n\n“A splendid marsh! I’ve no doubt Veslovsky got in your way. It’s awkward too, shooting with one dog,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, to soften his triumph.\n\n# Chapter 11\n\nWhen Levin and Stepan Arkadyevitch reached the peasant’s hut where Levin always used to stay, Veslovsky was already there. He was sitting in the middle of the hut, clinging with both hands to the bench from which he was being pulled by a soldier, the brother of the peasant’s wife, who was helping him off with his miry boots. Veslovsky was laughing his infectious, good-humored laugh.\n\n“I’ve only just come. Ils ont été charmants. Just fancy, they gave me drink, fed me! Such bread, it was exquisite! Délicieux! And the vodka, I never tasted any better. And they would not take a penny for anything. And they kept saying: ‘Excuse our homely ways.’”\n\n“What should they take anything for? They were entertaining you, to be sure. Do you suppose they keep vodka for sale?” said the soldier, succeeding at last in pulling the soaked boot off the blackened stocking.\n\nIn spite of the dirtiness of the hut, which was all muddied by their boots and the filthy dogs licking themselves clean, and the smell of marsh mud and powder that filled the room, and the absence of knives and forks, the party drank their tea and ate their supper with a relish only known to sportsmen. Washed and clean, they went into a hay-barn swept ready for them, where the coachman had been making up beds for the gentlemen.\n\nThough it was dusk, not one of them wanted to go to sleep.\n\nAfter wavering among reminiscences and anecdotes of guns, of dogs, and of former shooting parties, the conversation rested on a topic that interested all of them. After Vassenka had several times over expressed his appreciation of this delightful sleeping place among the fragrant hay, this delightful broken cart (he supposed it to be broken because the shafts had been taken out), of the good nature of the peasants that had treated him to vodka, of the dogs who lay at the feet of their respective masters, Oblonsky began telling them of a delightful shooting party at Malthus’s, where he had stayed the previous summer.\n\nMalthus was a well-known capitalist, who had made his money by speculation in railway shares. Stepan Arkadyevitch described what grouse moors this Malthus had bought in the Tver province, and how they were preserved, and of the carriages and dogcarts in which the shooting party had been driven, and the luncheon pavilion that had been rigged up at the marsh.\n\n“I don’t understand you,” said Levin, sitting up in the hay; “how is it such people don’t disgust you? I can understand a lunch with Lafitte is all very pleasant, but don’t you dislike just that very sumptuousness? All these people, just like our spirit monopolists in old days, get their money in a way that gains them the contempt of everyone. They don’t care for their contempt, and then they use their dishonest gains to buy off the contempt they have deserved.”\n\n“Perfectly true!” chimed in Vassenka Veslovsky. “Perfectly! Oblonsky, of course, goes out of bonhomie, but other people say: ‘Well, Oblonsky stays with them.’...”\n\n“Not a bit of it.” Levin could hear that Oblonsky was smiling as he spoke. “I simply don’t consider him more dishonest than any other wealthy merchant or nobleman. They’ve all made their money alike—by their work and their intelligence.”\n\n“Oh, by what work? Do you call it work to get hold of concessions and speculate with them?”\n\n“Of course it’s work. Work in this sense, that if it were not for him and others like him, there would have been no railways.”\n\n“But that’s not work, like the work of a peasant or a learned profession.”\n\n“Granted, but it’s work in the sense that his activity produces a result—the railways. But of course you think the railways useless.”\n\n“No, that’s another question; I am prepared to admit that they’re useful. But all profit that is out of proportion to the labor expended is dishonest.”\n\n“But who is to define what is proportionate?”\n\n“Making profit by dishonest means, by trickery,” said Levin, conscious that he could not draw a distinct line between honesty and dishonesty. “Such as banking, for instance,” he went on. “It’s an evil—the amassing of huge fortunes without labor, just the same thing as with the spirit monopolies, it’s only the form that’s changed. Le roi est mort, vive le roi. No sooner were the spirit monopolies abolished than the railways came up, and banking companies; that, too, is profit without work.”\n\n“Yes, that may all be very true and clever.... Lie down, Krak!” Stepan Arkadyevitch called to his dog, who was scratching and turning over all the hay. He was obviously convinced of the correctness of his position, and so talked serenely and without haste. “But you have not drawn the line between honest and dishonest work. That I receive a bigger salary than my chief clerk, though he knows more about the work than I do—that’s dishonest, I suppose?”\n\n“I can’t say.”\n\n“Well, but I can tell you: your receiving some five thousand, let’s say, for your work on the land, while our host, the peasant here, however hard he works, can never get more than fifty roubles, is just as dishonest as my earning more than my chief clerk, and Malthus getting more than a station-master. No, quite the contrary; I see that society takes up a sort of antagonistic attitude to these people, which is utterly baseless, and I fancy there’s envy at the bottom of it....”\n\n“No, that’s unfair,” said Veslovsky; “how could envy come in? There is something not nice about that sort of business.”\n\n“You say,” Levin went on, “that it’s unjust for me to receive five thousand, while the peasant has fifty; that’s true. It is unfair, and I feel it, but....”\n\n“It really is. Why is it we spend our time riding, drinking, shooting, doing nothing, while they are forever at work?” said Vassenka Veslovsky, obviously for the first time in his life reflecting on the question, and consequently considering it with perfect sincerity.\n\n“Yes, you feel it, but you don’t give him your property,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, intentionally, as it seemed, provoking Levin.\n\nThere had arisen of late something like a secret antagonism between the two brothers-in-law; as though, since they had married sisters, a kind of rivalry had sprung up between them as to which was ordering his life best, and now this hostility showed itself in the conversation, as it began to take a personal note.\n\n“I don’t give it away, because no one demands that from me, and if I wanted to, I could not give it away,” answered Levin, “and have no one to give it to.”\n\n“Give it to this peasant, he would not refuse it.”\n\n“Yes, but how am I to give it up? Am I to go to him and make a deed of conveyance?”\n\n“I don’t know; but if you are convinced that you have no right....”\n\n“I’m not at all convinced. On the contrary, I feel I have no right to give it up, that I have duties both to the land and to my family.”\n\n“No, excuse me, but if you consider this inequality is unjust, why is it you don’t act accordingly?...”\n\n“Well, I do act negatively on that idea, so far as not trying to increase the difference of position existing between him and me.”\n\n“No, excuse me, that’s a paradox.”\n\n“Yes, there’s something of a sophistry about that,” Veslovsky agreed. “Ah! our host; so you’re not asleep yet?” he said to the peasant who came into the barn, opening the creaking door. “How is it you’re not asleep?”\n\n“No, how’s one to sleep! I thought our gentlemen would be asleep, but I heard them chattering. I want to get a hook from here. She won’t bite?” he added, stepping cautiously with his bare feet.\n\n“And where are you going to sleep?”\n\n“We are going out for the night with the beasts.”\n\n“Ah, what a night!” said Veslovsky, looking out at the edge of the hut and the unharnessed wagonette that could be seen in the faint light of the evening glow in the great frame of the open doors. “But listen, there are women’s voices singing, and, on my word, not badly too. Who’s that singing, my friend?”\n\n“That’s the maids from hard by here.”\n\n“Let’s go, let’s have a walk! We shan’t go to sleep, you know. Oblonsky, come along!”\n\n“If one could only do both, lie here and go,” answered Oblonsky, stretching. “It’s capital lying here.”\n\n“Well, I shall go by myself,” said Veslovsky, getting up eagerly, and putting on his shoes and stockings. “Good-bye, gentlemen. If it’s fun, I’ll fetch you. You’ve treated me to some good sport, and I won’t forget you.”\n\n“He really is a capital fellow, isn’t he?” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, when Veslovsky had gone out and the peasant had closed the door after him.\n\n“Yes, capital,” answered Levin, still thinking of the subject of their conversation just before. It seemed to him that he had clearly expressed his thoughts and feelings to the best of his capacity, and yet both of them, straightforward men and not fools, had said with one voice that he was comforting himself with sophistries. This disconcerted him.\n\n“It’s just this, my dear boy. One must do one of two things: either admit that the existing order of society is just, and then stick up for one’s rights in it; or acknowledge that you are enjoying unjust privileges, as I do, and then enjoy them and be satisfied.”\n\n“No, if it were unjust, you could not enjoy these advantages and be satisfied—at least I could not. The great thing for me is to feel that I’m not to blame.”\n\n“What do you say, why not go after all?” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, evidently weary of the strain of thought. “We shan’t go to sleep, you know. Come, let’s go!”\n\nLevin did not answer. What they had said in the conversation, that he acted justly only in a negative sense, absorbed his thoughts. “Can it be that it’s only possible to be just negatively?” he was asking himself.\n\n“How strong the smell of the fresh hay is, though,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, getting up. “There’s not a chance of sleeping. Vassenka has been getting up some fun there. Do you hear the laughing and his voice? Hadn’t we better go? Come along!”\n\n“No, I’m not coming,” answered Levin.\n\n“Surely that’s not a matter of principle too,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, smiling, as he felt about in the dark for his cap.\n\n“It’s not a matter of principle, but why should I go?”\n\n“But do you know you are preparing trouble for yourself,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, finding his cap and getting up.\n\n“How so?”\n\n“Do you suppose I don’t see the line you’ve taken up with your wife? I heard how it’s a question of the greatest consequence, whether or not you’re to be away for a couple of days’ shooting. That’s all very well as an idyllic episode, but for your whole life that won’t answer. A man must be independent; he has his masculine interests. A man has to be manly,” said Oblonsky, opening the door.\n\n“In what way? To go running after servant girls?” said Levin.\n\n“Why not, if it amuses him? Ça ne tire pas à conséquence. It won’t do my wife any harm, and it’ll amuse me. The great thing is to respect the sanctity of the home. There should be nothing in the home. But don’t tie your own hands.”\n\n“Perhaps so,” said Levin dryly, and he turned on his side. “Tomorrow, early, I want to go shooting, and I won’t wake anyone, and shall set off at daybreak.”\n\n“Messieurs, venez vite!” they heard the voice of Veslovsky coming back. “Charmante! I’ve made such a discovery. Charmante! a perfect Gretchen, and I’ve already made friends with her. Really, exceedingly pretty,” he declared in a tone of approval, as though she had been made pretty entirely on his account, and he was expressing his satisfaction with the entertainment that had been provided for him.\n\nLevin pretended to be asleep, while Oblonsky, putting on his slippers, and lighting a cigar, walked out of the barn, and soon their voices were lost.\n\nFor a long while Levin could not get to sleep. He heard the horses munching hay, then he heard the peasant and his elder boy getting ready for the night, and going off for the night watch with the beasts, then he heard the soldier arranging his bed on the other side of the barn, with his nephew, the younger son of their peasant host. He heard the boy in his shrill little voice telling his uncle what he thought about the dogs, who seemed to him huge and terrible creatures, and asking what the dogs were going to hunt next day, and the soldier in a husky, sleepy voice, telling him the sportsmen were going in the morning to the marsh, and would shoot with their guns; and then, to check the boy’s questions, he said, “Go to sleep, Vaska; go to sleep, or you’ll catch it,” and soon after he began snoring himself, and everything was still. He could only hear the snort of the horses, and the guttural cry of a snipe.\n\n“Is it really only negative?” he repeated to himself. “Well, what of it? It’s not my fault.” And he began thinking about the next day.\n\n“Tomorrow I’ll go out early, and I’ll make a point of keeping cool. There are lots of snipe; and there are grouse too. When I come back there’ll be the note from Kitty. Yes, Stiva may be right, I’m not manly with her, I’m tied to her apron-strings.... Well, it can’t be helped! Negative again....”\n\nHalf asleep, he heard the laughter and mirthful talk of Veslovsky and Stepan Arkadyevitch. For an instant he opened his eyes: the moon was up, and in the open doorway, brightly lighted up by the moonlight, they were standing talking. Stepan Arkadyevitch was saying something of the freshness of one girl, comparing her to a freshly peeled nut, and Veslovsky with his infectious laugh was repeating some words, probably said to him by a peasant: “Ah, you do your best to get round her!” Levin, half asleep, said:\n\n“Gentlemen, tomorrow before daylight!” and fell asleep.\n\n# Chapter 12\n\nWaking up at earliest dawn, Levin tried to wake his companions. Vassenka, lying on his stomach, with one leg in a stocking thrust out, was sleeping so soundly that he could elicit no response. Oblonsky, half asleep, declined to get up so early. Even Laska, who was asleep, curled up in the hay, got up unwillingly, and lazily stretched out and straightened her hind legs one after the other. Getting on his boots and stockings, taking his gun, and carefully opening the creaking door of the barn, Levin went out into the road. The coachmen were sleeping in their carriages, the horses were dozing. Only one was lazily eating oats, dipping its nose into the manger. It was still gray out-of-doors.\n\n“Why are you up so early, my dear?” the old woman, their hostess, said, coming out of the hut and addressing him affectionately as an old friend.\n\n“Going shooting, granny. Do I go this way to the marsh?”\n\n“Straight out at the back; by our threshing floor, my dear, and hemp patches; there’s a little footpath.” Stepping carefully with her sunburnt, bare feet, the old woman conducted Levin, and moved back the fence for him by the threshing floor.\n\n“Straight on and you’ll come to the marsh. Our lads drove the cattle there yesterday evening.”\n\nLaska ran eagerly forward along the little path. Levin followed her with a light, rapid step, continually looking at the sky. He hoped the sun would not be up before he reached the marsh. But the sun did not delay. The moon, which had been bright when he went out, by now shone only like a crescent of quicksilver. The pink flush of dawn, which one could not help seeing before, now had to be sought to be discerned at all. What were before undefined, vague blurs in the distant countryside could now be distinctly seen. They were sheaves of rye. The dew, not visible till the sun was up, wetted Levin’s legs and his blouse above his belt in the high growing, fragrant hemp patch, from which the pollen had already fallen out. In the transparent stillness of morning the smallest sounds were audible. A bee flew by Levin’s ear with the whizzing sound of a bullet. He looked carefully, and saw a second and a third. They were all flying from the beehives behind the hedge, and they disappeared over the hemp patch in the direction of the marsh. The path led straight to the marsh. The marsh could be recognized by the mist which rose from it, thicker in one place and thinner in another, so that the reeds and willow bushes swayed like islands in this mist. At the edge of the marsh and the road, peasant boys and men, who had been herding for the night, were lying, and in the dawn all were asleep under their coats. Not far from them were three hobbled horses. One of them clanked a chain. Laska walked beside her master, pressing a little forward and looking round. Passing the sleeping peasants and reaching the first reeds, Levin examined his pistols and let his dog off. One of the horses, a sleek, dark-brown three-year-old, seeing the dog, started away, switched its tail and snorted. The other horses too were frightened, and splashing through the water with their hobbled legs, and drawing their hoofs out of the thick mud with a squelching sound, they bounded out of the marsh. Laska stopped, looking ironically at the horses and inquiringly at Levin. Levin patted Laska, and whistled as a sign that she might begin.\n\nLaska ran joyfully and anxiously through the slush that swayed under her.\n\nRunning into the marsh among the familiar scents of roots, marsh plants, and slime, and the extraneous smell of horse dung, Laska detected at once a smell that pervaded the whole marsh, the scent of that strong-smelling bird that always excited her more than any other. Here and there among the moss and marsh plants this scent was very strong, but it was impossible to determine in which direction it grew stronger or fainter. To find the direction, she had to go farther away from the wind. Not feeling the motion of her legs, Laska bounded with a stiff gallop, so that at each bound she could stop short, to the right, away from the wind that blew from the east before sunrise, and turned facing the wind. Sniffing in the air with dilated nostrils, she felt at once that not their tracks only but they themselves were here before her, and not one, but many. Laska slackened her speed. They were here, but where precisely she could not yet determine. To find the very spot, she began to make a circle, when suddenly her master’s voice drew her off. “Laska! here?” he asked, pointing her to a different direction. She stopped, asking him if she had better not go on doing as she had begun. But he repeated his command in an angry voice, pointing to a spot covered with water, where there could not be anything. She obeyed him, pretending she was looking, so as to please him, went round it, and went back to her former position, and was at once aware of the scent again. Now when he was not hindering her, she knew what to do, and without looking at what was under her feet, and to her vexation stumbling over a high stump into the water, but righting herself with her strong, supple legs, she began making the circle which was to make all clear to her. The scent of them reached her, stronger and stronger, and more and more defined, and all at once it became perfectly clear to her that one of them was here, behind this tuft of reeds, five paces in front of her; she stopped, and her whole body was still and rigid. On her short legs she could see nothing in front of her, but by the scent she knew it was sitting not more than five paces off. She stood still, feeling more and more conscious of it, and enjoying it in anticipation. Her tail was stretched straight and tense, and only wagging at the extreme end. Her mouth was slightly open, her ears raised. One ear had been turned wrong side out as she ran up, and she breathed heavily but warily, and still more warily looked round, but more with her eyes than her head, to her master. He was coming along with the face she knew so well, though the eyes were always terrible to her. He stumbled over the stump as he came, and moved, as she thought, extraordinarily slowly. She thought he came slowly, but he was running.\n\nNoticing Laska’s special attitude as she crouched on the ground, as it were, scratching big prints with her hind paws, and with her mouth slightly open, Levin knew she was pointing at grouse, and with an inward prayer for luck, especially with the first bird, he ran up to her. Coming quite close up to her, he could from his height look beyond her, and he saw with his eyes what she was seeing with her nose. In a space between two little thickets, at a couple of yards’ distance, he could see a grouse. Turning its head, it was listening. Then lightly preening and folding its wings, it disappeared round a corner with a clumsy wag of its tail.\n\n“Fetch it, fetch it!” shouted Levin, giving Laska a shove from behind.\n\n“But I can’t go,” thought Laska. “Where am I to go? From here I feel them, but if I move forward I shall know nothing of where they are or who they are.” But then he shoved her with his knee, and in an excited whisper said, “Fetch it, Laska.”\n\n“Well, if that’s what he wishes, I’ll do it, but I can’t answer for myself now,” she thought, and darted forward as fast as her legs would carry her between the thick bushes. She scented nothing now; she could only see and hear, without understanding anything.\n\nTen paces from her former place a grouse rose with a guttural cry and the peculiar round sound of its wings. And immediately after the shot it splashed heavily with its white breast on the wet mire. Another bird did not linger, but rose behind Levin without the dog. When Levin turned towards it, it was already some way off. But his shot caught it. Flying twenty paces further, the second grouse rose upwards, and whirling round like a ball, dropped heavily on a dry place.\n\n“Come, this is going to be some good!” thought Levin, packing the warm and fat grouse into his game bag. “Eh, Laska, will it be good?”\n\nWhen Levin, after loading his gun, moved on, the sun had fully risen, though unseen behind the storm-clouds. The moon had lost all of its luster, and was like a white cloud in the sky. Not a single star could be seen. The sedge, silvery with dew before, now shone like gold. The stagnant pools were all like amber. The blue of the grass had changed to yellow-green. The marsh birds twittered and swarmed about the brook and upon the bushes that glittered with dew and cast long shadows. A hawk woke up and settled on a haycock, turning its head from side to side and looking discontentedly at the marsh. Crows were flying about the field, and a bare-legged boy was driving the horses to an old man, who had got up from under his long coat and was combing his hair. The smoke from the gun was white as milk over the green of the grass.\n\nOne of the boys ran up to Levin.\n\n“Uncle, there were ducks here yesterday!” he shouted to him, and he walked a little way off behind him.\n\nAnd Levin was doubly pleased, in sight of the boy, who expressed his approval, at killing three snipe, one after another, straight off.\n\n# Chapter 13\n\nThe sportsman’s saying, that if the first beast or the first bird is not missed, the day will be lucky, turned out correct.\n\nAt ten o’clock Levin, weary, hungry, and happy after a tramp of twenty miles, returned to his night’s lodging with nineteen head of fine game and one duck, which he tied to his belt, as it would not go into the game bag. His companions had long been awake, and had had time to get hungry and have breakfast.\n\n“Wait a bit, wait a bit, I know there are nineteen,” said Levin, counting a second time over the grouse and snipe, that looked so much less important now, bent and dry and bloodstained, with heads crooked aside, than they did when they were flying.\n\nThe number was verified, and Stepan Arkadyevitch’s envy pleased Levin. He was pleased too on returning to find the man sent by Kitty with a note was already there.\n\n“I am perfectly well and happy. If you were uneasy about me, you can feel easier than ever. I’ve a new bodyguard, Marya Vlasyevna,”—this was the midwife, a new and important personage in Levin’s domestic life. “She has come to have a look at me. She found me perfectly well, and we have kept her till you are back. All are happy and well, and please, don’t be in a hurry to come back, but, if the sport is good, stay another day.”\n\nThese two pleasures, his lucky shooting and the letter from his wife, were so great that two slightly disagreeable incidents passed lightly over Levin. One was that the chestnut trace horse, who had been unmistakably overworked on the previous day, was off his feed and out of sorts. The coachman said he was “Overdriven yesterday, Konstantin Dmitrievitch. Yes, indeed! driven ten miles with no sense!”\n\nThe other unpleasant incident, which for the first minute destroyed his good humor, though later he laughed at it a great deal, was to find that of all the provisions Kitty had provided in such abundance that one would have thought there was enough for a week, nothing was left. On his way back, tired and hungry from shooting, Levin had so distinct a vision of meat-pies that as he approached the hut he seemed to smell and taste them, as Laska had smelt the game, and he immediately told Philip to give him some. It appeared that there were no pies left, nor even any chicken.\n\n“Well, this fellow’s appetite!” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, laughing and pointing at Vassenka Veslovsky. “I never suffer from loss of appetite, but he’s really marvelous!...”\n\n“Well, it can’t be helped,” said Levin, looking gloomily at Veslovsky. “Well, Philip, give me some beef, then.”\n\n“The beef’s been eaten, and the bones given to the dogs,” answered Philip.\n\nLevin was so hurt that he said, in a tone of vexation, “You might have left me something!” and he felt ready to cry.\n\n“Then put away the game,” he said in a shaking voice to Philip, trying not to look at Vassenka, “and cover them with some nettles. And you might at least ask for some milk for me.”\n\nBut when he had drunk some milk, he felt ashamed immediately at having shown his annoyance to a stranger, and he began to laugh at his hungry mortification.\n\nIn the evening they went shooting again, and Veslovsky had several successful shots, and in the night they drove home.\n\nTheir homeward journey was as lively as their drive out had been. Veslovsky sang songs and related with enjoyment his adventures with the peasants, who had regaled him with vodka, and said to him, “Excuse our homely ways,” and his night’s adventures with kiss-in-the-ring and the servant-girl and the peasant, who had asked him was he married, and on learning that he was not, said to him, “Well, mind you don’t run after other men’s wives—you’d better get one of your own.” These words had particularly amused Veslovsky.\n\n“Altogether, I’ve enjoyed our outing awfully. And you, Levin?”\n\n“I have, very much,” Levin said quite sincerely. It was particularly delightful to him to have got rid of the hostility he had been feeling towards Vassenka Veslovsky at home, and to feel instead the most friendly disposition to him.\n\n# Chapter 14\n\nNext day at ten o’clock Levin, who had already gone his rounds, knocked at the room where Vassenka had been put for the night.\n\n“Entrez!” Veslovsky called to him. “Excuse me, I’ve only just finished my ablutions,” he said, smiling, standing before him in his underclothes only.\n\n“Don’t mind me, please.” Levin sat down in the window. “Have you slept well?”\n\n“Like the dead. What sort of day is it for shooting?”\n\n“What will you take, tea or coffee?”\n\n“Neither. I’ll wait till lunch. I’m really ashamed. I suppose the ladies are down? A walk now would be capital. You show me your horses.”\n\nAfter walking about the garden, visiting the stable, and even doing some gymnastic exercises together on the parallel bars, Levin returned to the house with his guest, and went with him into the drawing-room.\n\n“We had splendid shooting, and so many delightful experiences!” said Veslovsky, going up to Kitty, who was sitting at the samovar. “What a pity ladies are cut off from these delights!”\n\n“Well, I suppose he must say something to the lady of the house,” Levin said to himself. Again he fancied something in the smile, in the all-conquering air with which their guest addressed Kitty....\n\nThe princess, sitting on the other side of the table with Marya Vlasyevna and Stepan Arkadyevitch, called Levin to her side, and began to talk to him about moving to Moscow for Kitty’s confinement, and getting ready rooms for them. Just as Levin had disliked all the trivial preparations for his wedding, as derogatory to the grandeur of the event, now he felt still more offensive the preparations for the approaching birth, the date of which they reckoned, it seemed, on their fingers. He tried to turn a deaf ear to these discussions of the best patterns of long clothes for the coming baby; tried to turn away and avoid seeing the mysterious, endless strips of knitting, the triangles of linen, and so on, to which Dolly attached special importance. The birth of a son (he was certain it would be a son) which was promised him, but which he still could not believe in—so marvelous it seemed—presented itself to his mind, on one hand, as a happiness so immense, and therefore so incredible; on the other, as an event so mysterious, that this assumption of a definite knowledge of what would be, and consequent preparation for it, as for something ordinary that did happen to people, jarred on him as confusing and humiliating.\n\nBut the princess did not understand his feelings, and put down his reluctance to think and talk about it to carelessness and indifference, and so she gave him no peace. She had commissioned Stepan Arkadyevitch to look at a flat, and now she called Levin up.\n\n“I know nothing about it, princess. Do as you think fit,” he said.\n\n“You must decide when you will move.”\n\n“I really don’t know. I know millions of children are born away from Moscow, and doctors ... why....”\n\n“But if so....”\n“Oh, no, as Kitty wishes.”\n\n“We can’t talk to Kitty about it! Do you want me to frighten her? Why, this spring Natalia Golitzina died from having an ignorant doctor.”\n\n“I will do just what you say,” he said gloomily.\n\nThe princess began talking to him, but he did not hear her. Though the conversation with the princess had indeed jarred upon him, he was gloomy, not on account of that conversation, but from what he saw at the samovar.\n\n“No, it’s impossible,” he thought, glancing now and then at Vassenka bending over Kitty, telling her something with his charming smile, and at her, flushed and disturbed.\n\nThere was something not nice in Vassenka’s attitude, in his eyes, in his smile. Levin even saw something not nice in Kitty’s attitude and look. And again the light died away in his eyes. Again, as before, all of a sudden, without the slightest transition, he felt cast down from a pinnacle of happiness, peace, and dignity, into an abyss of despair, rage, and humiliation. Again everything and everyone had become hateful to him.\n\n“You do just as you think best, princess,” he said again, looking round.\n\n“Heavy is the cap of Monomach,” Stepan Arkadyevitch said playfully, hinting, evidently, not simply at the princess’s conversation, but at the cause of Levin’s agitation, which he had noticed.\n\n“How late you are today, Dolly!”\n\nEveryone got up to greet Darya Alexandrovna. Vassenka only rose for an instant, and with the lack of courtesy to ladies characteristic of the modern young man, he scarcely bowed, and resumed his conversation again, laughing at something.\n\n“I’ve been worried about Masha. She did not sleep well, and is dreadfully tiresome today,” said Dolly.\n\nThe conversation Vassenka had started with Kitty was running on the same lines as on the previous evening, discussing Anna, and whether love is to be put higher than worldly considerations. Kitty disliked the conversation, and she was disturbed both by the subject and the tone in which it was conducted, and also by the knowledge of the effect it would have on her husband. But she was too simple and innocent to know how to cut short this conversation, or even to conceal the superficial pleasure afforded her by the young man’s very obvious admiration. She wanted to stop it, but she did not know what to do. Whatever she did she knew would be observed by her husband, and the worst interpretation put on it. And, in fact, when she asked Dolly what was wrong with Masha, and Vassenka, waiting till this uninteresting conversation was over, began to gaze indifferently at Dolly, the question struck Levin as an unnatural and disgusting piece of hypocrisy.\n\n“What do you say, shall we go and look for mushrooms today?” said Dolly.\n\n“By all means, please, and I shall come too,” said Kitty, and she blushed. She wanted from politeness to ask Vassenka whether he would come, and she did not ask him. “Where are you going, Kostya?” she asked her husband with a guilty face, as he passed by her with a resolute step. This guilty air confirmed all his suspicions.\n\n“The mechanician came when I was away; I haven’t seen him yet,” he said, not looking at her.\n\nHe went downstairs, but before he had time to leave his study he heard his wife’s familiar footsteps running with reckless speed to him.\n\n“What do you want?” he said to her shortly. “We are busy.”\n\n“I beg your pardon,” she said to the German mechanician; “I want a few words with my husband.”\n\nThe German would have left the room, but Levin said to him:\n\n“Don’t disturb yourself.”\n\n“The train is at three?” queried the German. “I mustn’t be late.”\n\nLevin did not answer him, but walked out himself with his wife.\n\n“Well, what have you to say to me?” he said to her in French.\n\nHe did not look her in the face, and did not care to see that she in her condition was trembling all over, and had a piteous, crushed look.\n\n“I ... I want to say that we can’t go on like this; that this is misery....” she said.\n\n“The servants are here at the sideboard,” he said angrily; “don’t make a scene.”\n\n“Well, let’s go in here!”\n\nThey were standing in the passage. Kitty would have gone into the next room, but there the English governess was giving Tanya a lesson.\n\n“Well, come into the garden.”\n\nIn the garden they came upon a peasant weeding the path. And no longer considering that the peasant could see her tear-stained and his agitated face, that they looked like people fleeing from some disaster, they went on with rapid steps, feeling that they must speak out and clear up misunderstandings, must be alone together, and so get rid of the misery they were both feeling.\n\n“We can’t go on like this! It’s misery! I am wretched; you are wretched. What for?” she said, when they had at last reached a solitary garden seat at a turn in the lime tree avenue.\n\n“But tell me one thing: was there in his tone anything unseemly, not nice, humiliatingly horrible?” he said, standing before her again in the same position with his clenched fists on his chest, as he had stood before her that night.\n\n“Yes,” she said in a shaking voice; “but, Kostya, surely you see I’m not to blame? All the morning I’ve been trying to take a tone ... but such people.... Why did he come? How happy we were!” she said, breathless with the sobs that shook her.\n\nAlthough nothing had been pursuing them, and there was nothing to run away from, and they could not possibly have found anything very delightful on that garden seat, the gardener saw with astonishment that they passed him on their way home with comforted and radiant faces.\n\n# Chapter 15\n\nAfter escorting his wife upstairs, Levin went to Dolly’s part of the house. Darya Alexandrovna, for her part, was in great distress too that day. She was walking about the room, talking angrily to a little girl, who stood in the corner roaring.\n\n“And you shall stand all day in the corner, and have your dinner all alone, and not see one of your dolls, and I won’t make you a new frock,” she said, not knowing how to punish her.\n\n“Oh, she is a disgusting child!” she turned to Levin. “Where does she get such wicked propensities?”\n\n“Why, what has she done?” Levin said without much interest, for he had wanted to ask her advice, and so was annoyed that he had come at an unlucky moment.\n\n“Grisha and she went into the raspberries, and there ... I can’t tell you really what she did. It’s a thousand pities Miss Elliot’s not with us. This one sees to nothing—she’s a machine.... Figurez-vous que la petite?...”\n\nAnd Darya Alexandrovna described Masha’s crime.\n\n“That proves nothing; it’s not a question of evil propensities at all, it’s simply mischief,” Levin assured her.\n\n“But you are upset about something? What have you come for?” asked Dolly. “What’s going on there?”\n\nAnd in the tone of her question Levin heard that it would be easy for him to say what he had meant to say.\n\n“I’ve not been in there, I’ve been alone in the garden with Kitty. We’ve had a quarrel for the second time since ... Stiva came.”\n\nDolly looked at him with her shrewd, comprehending eyes.\n\n“Come, tell me, honor bright, has there been ... not in Kitty, but in that gentleman’s behavior, a tone which might be unpleasant—not unpleasant, but horrible, offensive to a husband?”\n\n“You mean, how shall I say.... Stay, stay in the corner!” she said to Masha, who, detecting a faint smile in her mother’s face, had been turning round. “The opinion of the world would be that he is behaving as young men do behave. Il fait la cour à une jeune et jolie femme, and a husband who’s a man of the world should only be flattered by it.”\n\n“Yes, yes,” said Levin gloomily; “but you noticed it?”\n\n“Not only I, but Stiva noticed it. Just after breakfast he said to me in so many words, Je crois que Veslovsky fait un petit brin de cour à Kitty.”\n\n“Well, that’s all right then; now I’m satisfied. I’ll send him away,” said Levin.\n\n“What do you mean! Are you crazy?” Dolly cried in horror; “nonsense, Kostya, only think!” she said, laughing. “You can go now to Fanny,” she said to Masha. “No, if you wish it, I’ll speak to Stiva. He’ll take him away. He can say you’re expecting visitors. Altogether he doesn’t fit into the house.”\n\n“No, no, I’ll do it myself.”\n\n“But you’ll quarrel with him?”\n\n“Not a bit. I shall so enjoy it,” Levin said, his eyes flashing with real enjoyment. “Come, forgive her, Dolly, she won’t do it again,” he said of the little sinner, who had not gone to Fanny, but was standing irresolutely before her mother, waiting and looking up from under her brows to catch her mother’s eye.\n\nThe mother glanced at her. The child broke into sobs, hid her face on her mother’s lap, and Dolly laid her thin, tender hand on her head.\n\n“And what is there in common between us and him?” thought Levin, and he went off to look for Veslovsky.\n\nAs he passed through the passage he gave orders for the carriage to be got ready to drive to the station.\n\n“The spring was broken yesterday,” said the footman.\n\n“Well, the covered trap, then, and make haste. Where’s the visitor?”\n\n“The gentleman’s gone to his room.”\n\nLevin came upon Veslovsky at the moment when the latter, having unpacked his things from his trunk, and laid out some new songs, was putting on his gaiters to go out riding.\n\nWhether there was something exceptional in Levin’s face, or that Vassenka was himself conscious that ce petit brin de cour he was making was out of place in this family, but he was somewhat (as much as a young man in society can be) disconcerted at Levin’s entrance.\n\n“You ride in gaiters?”\n\n“Yes, it’s much cleaner,” said Vassenka, putting his fat leg on a chair, fastening the bottom hook, and smiling with simple-hearted good humor.\n\nHe was undoubtedly a good-natured fellow, and Levin felt sorry for him and ashamed of himself, as his host, when he saw the shy look on Vassenka’s face.\n\nOn the table lay a piece of stick which they had broken together that morning, trying their strength. Levin took the fragment in his hands and began smashing it up, breaking bits off the stick, not knowing how to begin.\n\n“I wanted....” He paused, but suddenly, remembering Kitty and everything that had happened, he said, looking him resolutely in the face: “I have ordered the horses to be put-to for you.”\n\n“How so?” Vassenka began in surprise. “To drive where?”\n\n“For you to drive to the station,” Levin said gloomily.\n\n“Are you going away, or has something happened?”\n\n“It happens that I expect visitors,” said Levin, his strong fingers more and more rapidly breaking off the ends of the split stick. “And I’m not expecting visitors, and nothing has happened, but I beg you to go away. You can explain my rudeness as you like.”\n\nVassenka drew himself up.\n\n“I beg you to explain....” he said with dignity, understanding at last.\n\n“I can’t explain,” Levin said softly and deliberately, trying to control the trembling of his jaw; “and you’d better not ask.”\n\nAnd as the split ends were all broken off, Levin clutched the thick ends in his finger, broke the stick in two, and carefully caught the end as it fell.\n\nProbably the sight of those nervous fingers, of the muscles he had proved that morning at gymnastics, of the glittering eyes, the soft voice, and quivering jaws, convinced Vassenka better than any words. He bowed, shrugging his shoulders, and smiling contemptuously.\n\n“Can I not see Oblonsky?”\n\nThe shrug and the smile did not irritate Levin.\n\n“What else was there for him to do?” he thought.\n\n“I’ll send him to you at once.”\n\n“What madness is this?” Stepan Arkadyevitch said when, after hearing from his friend that he was being turned out of the house, he found Levin in the garden, where he was walking about waiting for his guest’s departure. “Mais c’est ridicule! What fly has stung you? Mais c’est du dernier ridicule! What did you think, if a young man....”\n\nBut the place where Levin had been stung was evidently still sore, for he turned pale again, when Stepan Arkadyevitch would have enlarged on the reason, and he himself cut him short.\n\n“Please don’t go into it! I can’t help it. I feel ashamed of how I’m treating you and him. But it won’t be, I imagine, a great grief to him to go, and his presence was distasteful to me and to my wife.”\n\n“But it’s insulting to him! Et puis c’est ridicule.”\n\n“And to me it’s both insulting and distressing! And I’m not at fault in any way, and there’s no need for me to suffer.”\n\n“Well, this I didn’t expect of you! On peut être jaloux, mais à ce point, c’est du dernier ridicule!”\n\nLevin turned quickly, and walked away from him into the depths of the avenue, and he went on walking up and down alone. Soon he heard the rumble of the trap, and saw from behind the trees how Vassenka, sitting in the hay (unluckily there was no seat in the trap) in his Scotch cap, was driven along the avenue, jolting up and down over the ruts.\n\n“What’s this?” Levin thought, when a footman ran out of the house and stopped the trap. It was the mechanician, whom Levin had totally forgotten. The mechanician, bowing low, said something to Veslovsky, then clambered into the trap, and they drove off together.\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch and the princess were much upset by Levin’s action. And he himself felt not only in the highest degree ridicule, but also utterly guilty and disgraced. But remembering what sufferings he and his wife had been through, when he asked himself how he should act another time, he answered that he should do just the same again.\n\nIn spite of all this, towards the end of that day, everyone except the princess, who could not pardon Levin’s action, became extraordinarily lively and good-humored, like children after a punishment or grown-up people after a dreary, ceremonious reception, so that by the evening Vassenka’s dismissal was spoken of, in the absence of the princess, as though it were some remote event. And Dolly, who had inherited her father’s gift of humorous storytelling, made Varenka helpless with laughter as she related for the third and fourth time, always with fresh humorous additions, how she had only just put on her new shoes for the benefit of the visitor, and on going into the drawing-room, heard suddenly the rumble of the trap. And who should be in the trap but Vassenka himself, with his Scotch cap, and his songs and his gaiters, and all, sitting in the hay.\n\n“If only you’d ordered out the carriage! But no! and then I hear: ‘Stop!’ Oh, I thought they’ve relented. I look out, and behold a fat German being sat down by him and driving away.... And my new shoes all for nothing!...”\n\n# Chapter 16\n\nDarya Alexandrovna carried out her intention and went to see Anna. She was sorry to annoy her sister and to do anything Levin disliked. She quite understood how right the Levins were in not wishing to have anything to do with Vronsky. But she felt she must go and see Anna, and show her that her feelings could not be changed, in spite of the change in her position. That she might be independent of the Levins in this expedition, Darya Alexandrovna sent to the village to hire horses for the drive; but Levin learning of it went to her to protest.\n\n“What makes you suppose that I dislike your going? But, even if I did dislike it, I should still more dislike your not taking my horses,” he said. “You never told me that you were going for certain. Hiring horses in the village is disagreeable to me, and, what’s of more importance, they’ll undertake the job and never get you there. I have horses. And if you don’t want to wound me, you’ll take mine.”\n\nDarya Alexandrovna had to consent, and on the day fixed Levin had ready for his sister-in-law a set of four horses and relays, getting them together from the farm and saddle-horses—not at all a smart-looking set, but capable of taking Darya Alexandrovna the whole distance in a single day. At that moment, when horses were wanted for the princess, who was going, and for the midwife, it was a difficult matter for Levin to make up the number, but the duties of hospitality would not let him allow Darya Alexandrovna to hire horses when staying in his house. Moreover, he was well aware that the twenty roubles that would be asked for the journey were a serious matter for her; Darya Alexandrovna’s pecuniary affairs, which were in a very unsatisfactory state, were taken to heart by the Levins as if they were their own.\n\nDarya Alexandrovna, by Levin’s advice, started before daybreak. The road was good, the carriage comfortable, the horses trotted along merrily, and on the box, besides the coachman, sat the counting-house clerk, whom Levin was sending instead of a groom for greater security. Darya Alexandrovna dozed and waked up only on reaching the inn where the horses were to be changed.\n\nAfter drinking tea at the same well-to-do peasant’s with whom Levin had stayed on the way to Sviazhsky’s, and chatting with the women about their children, and with the old man about Count Vronsky, whom the latter praised very highly, Darya Alexandrovna, at ten o’clock, went on again. At home, looking after her children, she had no time to think. So now, after this journey of four hours, all the thoughts she had suppressed before rushed swarming into her brain, and she thought over all her life as she never had before, and from the most different points of view. Her thoughts seemed strange even to herself. At first she thought about the children, about whom she was uneasy, although the princess and Kitty (she reckoned more upon her) had promised to look after them. “If only Masha does not begin her naughty tricks, if Grisha isn’t kicked by a horse, and Lily’s stomach isn’t upset again!” she thought. But these questions of the present were succeeded by questions of the immediate future. She began thinking how she had to get a new flat in Moscow for the coming winter, to renew the drawing-room furniture, and to make her elder girl a cloak. Then questions of the more remote future occurred to her: how she was to place her children in the world. “The girls are all right,” she thought; “but the boys?”\n\n“It’s very well that I’m teaching Grisha, but of course that’s only because I am free myself now, I’m not with child. Stiva, of course, there’s no counting on. And with the help of good-natured friends I can bring them up; but if there’s another baby coming?...” And the thought struck her how untruly it was said that the curse laid on woman was that in sorrow she should bring forth children.\n\n“The birth itself, that’s nothing; but the months of carrying the child—that’s what’s so intolerable,” she thought, picturing to herself her last pregnancy, and the death of the last baby. And she recalled the conversation she had just had with the young woman at the inn. On being asked whether she had any children, the handsome young woman had answered cheerfully:\n\n“I had a girl baby, but God set me free; I buried her last Lent.”\n\n“Well, did you grieve very much for her?” asked Darya Alexandrovna.\n\n“Why grieve? The old man has grandchildren enough as it is. It was only a trouble. No working, nor nothing. Only a tie.”\n\nThis answer had struck Darya Alexandrovna as revolting in spite of the good-natured and pleasing face of the young woman; but now she could not help recalling these words. In those cynical words there was indeed a grain of truth.\n\n“Yes, altogether,” thought Darya Alexandrovna, looking back over her whole existence during those fifteen years of her married life, “pregnancy, sickness, mental incapacity, indifference to everything, and most of all—hideousness. Kitty, young and pretty as she is, even Kitty has lost her looks; and I when I’m with child become hideous, I know it. The birth, the agony, the hideous agonies, that last moment ... then the nursing, the sleepless nights, the fearful pains....”\n\nDarya Alexandrovna shuddered at the mere recollection of the pain from sore breasts which she had suffered with almost every child. “Then the children’s illnesses, that everlasting apprehension; then bringing them up; evil propensities” (she thought of little Masha’s crime among the raspberries), “education, Latin—it’s all so incomprehensible and difficult. And on the top of it all, the death of these children.” And there rose again before her imagination the cruel memory, that always tore her mother’s heart, of the death of her last little baby, who had died of croup; his funeral, the callous indifference of all at the little pink coffin, and her own torn heart, and her lonely anguish at the sight of the pale little brow with its projecting temples, and the open, wondering little mouth seen in the coffin at the moment when it was being covered with the little pink lid with a cross braided on it.\n\n“And all this, what’s it for? What is to come of it all? That I’m wasting my life, never having a moment’s peace, either with child, or nursing a child, forever irritable, peevish, wretched myself and worrying others, repulsive to my husband, while the children are growing up unhappy, badly educated, and penniless. Even now, if it weren’t for spending the summer at the Levins’, I don’t know how we should be managing to live. Of course Kostya and Kitty have so much tact that we don’t feel it; but it can’t go on. They’ll have children, they won’t be able to keep us; it’s a drag on them as it is. How is papa, who has hardly anything left for himself, to help us? So that I can’t even bring the children up by myself, and may find it hard with the help of other people, at the cost of humiliation. Why, even if we suppose the greatest good luck, that the children don’t die, and I bring them up somehow. At the very best they’ll simply be decent people. That’s all I can hope for. And to gain simply that—what agonies, what toil!... One’s whole life ruined!” Again she recalled what the young peasant woman had said, and again she was revolted at the thought; but she could not help admitting that there was a grain of brutal truth in the words.\n\n“Is it far now, Mihail?” Darya Alexandrovna asked the counting-house clerk, to turn her mind from thoughts that were frightening her.\n\n“From this village, they say, it’s five miles.” The carriage drove along the village street and onto a bridge. On the bridge was a crowd of peasant women with coils of ties for the sheaves on their shoulders, gaily and noisily chattering. They stood still on the bridge, staring inquisitively at the carriage. All the faces turned to Darya Alexandrovna looked to her healthy and happy, making her envious of their enjoyment of life. “They’re all living, they’re all enjoying life,” Darya Alexandrovna still mused when she had passed the peasant women and was driving uphill again at a trot, seated comfortably on the soft springs of the old carriage, “while I, let out, as it were from prison, from the world of worries that fret me to death, am only looking about me now for an instant. They all live; those peasant women and my sister Natalia and Varenka and Anna, whom I am going to see—all, but not I.\n\n“And they attack Anna. What for? am I any better? I have, anyway, a husband I love—not as I should like to love him, still I do love him, while Anna never loved hers. How is she to blame? She wants to live. God has put that in our hearts. Very likely I should have done the same. Even to this day I don’t feel sure I did right in listening to her at that terrible time when she came to me in Moscow. I ought then to have cast off my husband and have begun my life fresh. I might have loved and have been loved in reality. And is it any better as it is? I don’t respect him. He’s necessary to me,” she thought about her husband, “and I put up with him. Is that any better? At that time I could still have been admired, I had beauty left me still,” Darya Alexandrovna pursued her thoughts, and she would have liked to look at herself in the looking-glass. She had a traveling looking-glass in her handbag, and she wanted to take it out; but looking at the backs of the coachman and the swaying counting-house clerk, she felt that she would be ashamed if either of them were to look round, and she did not take out the glass.\n\nBut without looking in the glass, she thought that even now it was not too late; and she thought of Sergey Ivanovitch, who was always particularly attentive to her, of Stiva’s good-hearted friend, Turovtsin, who had helped her nurse her children through the scarlatina, and was in love with her. And there was someone else, a quite young man, who—her husband had told her it as a joke—thought her more beautiful than either of her sisters. And the most passionate and impossible romances rose before Darya Alexandrovna’s imagination. “Anna did quite right, and certainly I shall never reproach her for it. She is happy, she makes another person happy, and she’s not broken down as I am, but most likely just as she always was, bright, clever, open to every impression,” thought Darya Alexandrovna,—and a sly smile curved her lips, for, as she pondered on Anna’s love affair, Darya Alexandrovna constructed on parallel lines an almost identical love affair for herself, with an imaginary composite figure, the ideal man who was in love with her. She, like Anna, confessed the whole affair to her husband. And the amazement and perplexity of Stepan Arkadyevitch at this avowal made her smile.\n\nIn such daydreams she reached the turning of the highroad that led to Vozdvizhenskoe.\n\n# Chapter 17\n\nThe coachman pulled up his four horses and looked round to the right, to a field of rye, where some peasants were sitting on a cart. The counting-house clerk was just going to jump down, but on second thoughts he shouted peremptorily to the peasants instead, and beckoned to them to come up. The wind, that seemed to blow as they drove, dropped when the carriage stood still; gadflies settled on the steaming horses that angrily shook them off. The metallic clank of a whetstone against a scythe, that came to them from the cart, ceased. One of the peasants got up and came towards the carriage.\n\n“Well, you are slow!” the counting-house clerk shouted angrily to the peasant who was stepping slowly with his bare feet over the ruts of the rough dry road. “Come along, do!”\n\nA curly-headed old man with a bit of bast tied round his hair, and his bent back dark with perspiration, came towards the carriage, quickening his steps, and took hold of the mud-guard with his sunburnt hand.\n\n“Vozdvizhenskoe, the manor house? the count’s?” he repeated; “go on to the end of this track. Then turn to the left. Straight along the avenue and you’ll come right upon it. But whom do you want? The count himself?”\n\n“Well, are they at home, my good man?” Darya Alexandrovna said vaguely, not knowing how to ask about Anna, even of this peasant.\n\n“At home for sure,” said the peasant, shifting from one bare foot to the other, and leaving a distinct print of five toes and a heel in the dust. “Sure to be at home,” he repeated, evidently eager to talk. “Only yesterday visitors arrived. There’s a sight of visitors come. What do you want?” He turned round and called to a lad, who was shouting something to him from the cart. “Oh! They all rode by here not long since, to look at a reaping machine. They’ll be home by now. And who will you be belonging to?...”\n\n“We’ve come a long way,” said the coachman, climbing onto the box. “So it’s not far?”\n\n“I tell you, it’s just here. As soon as you get out....” he said, keeping hold all the while of the carriage.\n\nA healthy-looking, broad-shouldered young fellow came up too.\n\n“What, is it laborers they want for the harvest?” he asked.\n\n“I don’t know, my boy.”\n\n“So you keep to the left, and you’ll come right on it,” said the peasant, unmistakably loth to let the travelers go, and eager to converse.\n\nThe coachman started the horses, but they were only just turning off when the peasant shouted: “Stop! Hi, friend! Stop!” called the two voices. The coachman stopped.\n\n“They’re coming! They’re yonder!” shouted the peasant. “See what a turn-out!” he said, pointing to four persons on horseback, and two in a char-à-banc, coming along the road.\n\nThey were Vronsky with a jockey, Veslovsky and Anna on horseback, and Princess Varvara and Sviazhsky in the char-à-banc. They had gone out to look at the working of a new reaping machine.\n\nWhen the carriage stopped, the party on horseback were coming at a walking pace. Anna was in front beside Veslovsky. Anna, quietly walking her horse, a sturdy English cob with cropped mane and short tail, her beautiful head with her black hair straying loose under her high hat, her full shoulders, her slender waist in her black riding habit, and all the ease and grace of her deportment, impressed Dolly.\n\nFor the first minute it seemed to her unsuitable for Anna to be on horseback. The conception of riding on horseback for a lady was, in Darya Alexandrovna’s mind, associated with ideas of youthful flirtation and frivolity, which, in her opinion, was unbecoming in Anna’s position. But when she had scrutinized her, seeing her closer, she was at once reconciled to her riding. In spite of her elegance, everything was so simple, quiet, and dignified in the attitude, the dress and the movements of Anna, that nothing could have been more natural.\n\nBeside Anna, on a hot-looking gray cavalry horse, was Vassenka Veslovsky in his Scotch cap with floating ribbons, his stout legs stretched out in front, obviously pleased with his own appearance. Darya Alexandrovna could not suppress a good-humored smile as she recognized him. Behind rode Vronsky on a dark bay mare, obviously heated from galloping. He was holding her in, pulling at the reins.\n\nAfter him rode a little man in the dress of a jockey. Sviazhsky and Princess Varvara in a new char-à-banc with a big, raven-black trotting horse, overtook the party on horseback.\n\nAnna’s face suddenly beamed with a joyful smile at the instant when, in the little figure huddled in a corner of the old carriage, she recognized Dolly. She uttered a cry, started in the saddle, and set her horse into a gallop. On reaching the carriage she jumped off without assistance, and holding up her riding habit, she ran up to greet Dolly.\n\n“I thought it was you and dared not think it. How delightful! You can’t fancy how glad I am!” she said, at one moment pressing her face against Dolly and kissing her, and at the next holding her off and examining her with a smile.\n\n“Here’s a delightful surprise, Alexey!” she said, looking round at Vronsky, who had dismounted, and was walking towards them.\n\nVronsky, taking off his tall gray hat, went up to Dolly.\n\n“You wouldn’t believe how glad we are to see you,” he said, giving peculiar significance to the words, and showing his strong white teeth in a smile.\n\nVassenka Veslovsky, without getting off his horse, took off his cap and greeted the visitor by gleefully waving the ribbons over his head.\n\n“That’s Princess Varvara,” Anna said in reply to a glance of inquiry from Dolly as the char-à-banc drove up.\n\n“Ah!” said Darya Alexandrovna, and unconsciously her face betrayed her dissatisfaction.\n\nPrincess Varvara was her husband’s aunt, and she had long known her, and did not respect her. She knew that Princess Varvara had passed her whole life toadying on her rich relations, but that she should now be sponging on Vronsky, a man who was nothing to her, mortified Dolly on account of her kinship with her husband. Anna noticed Dolly’s expression, and was disconcerted by it. She blushed, dropped her riding habit, and stumbled over it.\n\nDarya Alexandrovna went up to the char-à-banc and coldly greeted Princess Varvara. Sviazhsky too she knew. He inquired how his queer friend with the young wife was, and running his eyes over the ill-matched horses and the carriage with its patched mud-guards, proposed to the ladies that they should get into the char-à-banc.\n\n“And I’ll get into this vehicle,” he said. “The horse is quiet, and the princess drives capitally.”\n\n“No, stay as you were,” said Anna, coming up, “and we’ll go in the carriage,” and taking Dolly’s arm, she drew her away.\n\nDarya Alexandrovna’s eyes were fairly dazzled by the elegant carriage of a pattern she had never seen before, the splendid horses, and the elegant and gorgeous people surrounding her. But what struck her most of all was the change that had taken place in Anna, whom she knew so well and loved. Any other woman, a less close observer, not knowing Anna before, or not having thought as Darya Alexandrovna had been thinking on the road, would not have noticed anything special in Anna. But now Dolly was struck by that temporary beauty, which is only found in women during the moments of love, and which she saw now in Anna’s face. Everything in her face, the clearly marked dimples in her cheeks and chin, the line of her lips, the smile which, as it were, fluttered about her face, the brilliance of her eyes, the grace and rapidity of her movements, the fulness of the notes of her voice, even the manner in which, with a sort of angry friendliness, she answered Veslovsky when he asked permission to get on her cob, so as to teach it to gallop with the right leg foremost—it was all peculiarly fascinating, and it seemed as if she were herself aware of it, and rejoicing in it.\n\nWhen both the women were seated in the carriage, a sudden embarrassment came over both of them. Anna was disconcerted by the intent look of inquiry Dolly fixed upon her. Dolly was embarrassed because after Sviazhsky’s phrase about “this vehicle,” she could not help feeling ashamed of the dirty old carriage in which Anna was sitting with her. The coachman Philip and the counting-house clerk were experiencing the same sensation. The counting-house clerk, to conceal his confusion, busied himself settling the ladies, but Philip the coachman became sullen, and was bracing himself not to be overawed in future by this external superiority. He smiled ironically, looking at the raven horse, and was already deciding in his own mind that this smart trotter in the char-à-banc was only good for promenage, and wouldn’t do thirty miles straight off in the heat.\n\nThe peasants had all got up from the cart and were inquisitively and mirthfully staring at the meeting of the friends, making their comments on it.\n\n“They’re pleased, too; haven’t seen each other for a long while,” said the curly-headed old man with the bast round his hair.\n\n“I say, Uncle Gerasim, if we could take that raven horse now, to cart the corn, that ’ud be quick work!”\n\n“Look-ee! Is that a woman in breeches?” said one of them, pointing to Vassenka Veslovsky sitting in a side saddle.\n\n“Nay, a man! See how smartly he’s going it!”\n\n“Eh, lads! seems we’re not going to sleep, then?”\n\n“What chance of sleep today!” said the old man, with a sidelong look at the sun. “Midday’s past, look-ee! Get your hooks, and come along!”\n\n# Chapter 18\n\nAnna looked at Dolly’s thin, care-worn face, with its wrinkles filled with dust from the road, and she was on the point of saying what she was thinking, that is, that Dolly had got thinner. But, conscious that she herself had grown handsomer, and that Dolly’s eyes were telling her so, she sighed and began to speak about herself.\n\n“You are looking at me,” she said, “and wondering how I can be happy in my position? Well! it’s shameful to confess, but I ... I’m inexcusably happy. Something magical has happened to me, like a dream, when you’re frightened, panic-stricken, and all of a sudden you wake up and all the horrors are no more. I have waked up. I have lived through the misery, the dread, and now for a long while past, especially since we’ve been here, I’ve been so happy!...” she said, with a timid smile of inquiry looking at Dolly.\n\n“How glad I am!” said Dolly smiling, involuntarily speaking more coldly than she wanted to. “I’m very glad for you. Why haven’t you written to me?”\n\n“Why?... Because I hadn’t the courage.... You forget my position....”\n\n“To me? Hadn’t the courage? If you knew how I ... I look at....”\n\nDarya Alexandrovna wanted to express her thoughts of the morning, but for some reason it seemed to her now out of place to do so.\n\n“But of that we’ll talk later. What’s this, what are all these buildings?” she asked, wanting to change the conversation and pointing to the red and green roofs that came into view behind the green hedges of acacia and lilac. “Quite a little town.”\n\nBut Anna did not answer.\n\n“No, no! How do you look at my position, what do you think of it?” she asked.\n\n“I consider....” Darya Alexandrovna was beginning, but at that instant Vassenka Veslovsky, having brought the cob to gallop with the right leg foremost, galloped past them, bumping heavily up and down in his short jacket on the chamois leather of the side saddle. “He’s doing it, Anna Arkadyevna!” he shouted.\n\nAnna did not even glance at him; but again it seemed to Darya Alexandrovna out of place to enter upon such a long conversation in the carriage, and so she cut short her thought.\n\n“I don’t think anything,” she said, “but I always loved you, and if one loves anyone, one loves the whole person, just as they are and not as one would like them to be....”\n\nAnna, taking her eyes off her friend’s face and dropping her eyelids (this was a new habit Dolly had not seen in her before), pondered, trying to penetrate the full significance of the words. And obviously interpreting them as she would have wished, she glanced at Dolly.\n\n“If you had any sins,” she said, “they would all be forgiven you for your coming to see me and these words.”\n\nAnd Dolly saw that tears stood in her eyes. She pressed Anna’s hand in silence.\n\n“Well, what are these buildings? How many there are of them!” After a moment’s silence she repeated her question.\n\n“These are the servants’ houses, barns, and stables,” answered Anna. “And there the park begins. It had all gone to ruin, but Alexey had everything renewed. He is very fond of this place, and, what I never expected, he has become intensely interested in looking after it. But his is such a rich nature! Whatever he takes up, he does splendidly. So far from being bored by it, he works with passionate interest. He—with his temperament as I know it—he has become careful and businesslike, a first-rate manager, he positively reckons every penny in his management of the land. But only in that. When it’s a question of tens of thousands, he doesn’t think of money.” She spoke with that gleefully sly smile with which women often talk of the secret characteristics only known to them—of those they love. “Do you see that big building? that’s the new hospital. I believe it will cost over a hundred thousand; that’s his hobby just now. And do you know how it all came about? The peasants asked him for some meadowland, I think it was, at a cheaper rate, and he refused, and I accused him of being miserly. Of course it was not really because of that, but everything together, he began this hospital to prove, do you see, that he was not miserly about money. C’est une petitesse, if you like, but I love him all the more for it. And now you’ll see the house in a moment. It was his grandfather’s house, and he has had nothing changed outside.”\n\n“How beautiful!” said Dolly, looking with involuntary admiration at the handsome house with columns, standing out among the different-colored greens of the old trees in the garden.\n\n“Isn’t it fine? And from the house, from the top, the view is wonderful.”\n\nThey drove into a courtyard strewn with gravel and bright with flowers, in which two laborers were at work putting an edging of stones round the light mould of a flower bed, and drew up in a covered entry.\n\n“Ah, they’re here already!” said Anna, looking at the saddle horses, which were just being led away from the steps. “It is a nice horse, isn’t it? It’s my cob; my favorite. Lead him here and bring me some sugar. Where is the count?” she inquired of two smart footmen who darted out. “Ah, there he is!” she said, seeing Vronsky coming to meet her with Veslovsky.\n\n“Where are you going to put the princess?” said Vronsky in French, addressing Anna, and without waiting for a reply, he once more greeted Darya Alexandrovna, and this time he kissed her hand. “I think the big balcony room.”\n\n“Oh, no, that’s too far off! Better in the corner room, we shall see each other more. Come, let’s go up,” said Anna, as she gave her favorite horse the sugar the footman had brought her.\n\n“Et vous oubliez votre devoir,” she said to Veslovsky, who came out too on the steps.\n\n“Pardon, j’en ai tout plein les poches,” he answered, smiling, putting his fingers in his waistcoat pocket.\n\n“Mais vous venez trop tard,” she said, rubbing her handkerchief on her hand, which the horse had made wet in taking the sugar.\n\nAnna turned to Dolly. “You can stay some time? For one day only? That’s impossible!”\n\n“I promised to be back, and the children....” said Dolly, feeling embarrassed both because she had to get her bag out of the carriage, and because she knew her face must be covered with dust.\n\n“No, Dolly, darling!... Well, we’ll see. Come along, come along!” and Anna led Dolly to her room.\n\nThat room was not the smart guest chamber Vronsky had suggested, but the one of which Anna had said that Dolly would excuse it. And this room, for which excuse was needed, was more full of luxury than any in which Dolly had ever stayed, a luxury that reminded her of the best hotels abroad.\n\n“Well, darling, how happy I am!” Anna said, sitting down in her riding habit for a moment beside Dolly. “Tell me about all of you. Stiva I had only a glimpse of, and he cannot tell one about the children. How is my favorite, Tanya? Quite a big girl, I expect?”\n\n“Yes, she’s very tall,” Darya Alexandrovna answered shortly, surprised herself that she should respond so coolly about her children. “We are having a delightful stay at the Levins’,” she added.\n\n“Oh, if I had known,” said Anna, “that you do not despise me!... You might have all come to us. Stiva’s an old friend and a great friend of Alexey’s, you know,” she added, and suddenly she blushed.\n\n“Yes, but we are all....” Dolly answered in confusion.\n\n“But in my delight I’m talking nonsense. The one thing, darling, is that I am so glad to have you!” said Anna, kissing her again. “You haven’t told me yet how and what you think about me, and I keep wanting to know. But I’m glad you will see me as I am. The chief thing I shouldn’t like would be for people to imagine I want to prove anything. I don’t want to prove anything; I merely want to live, to do no one harm but myself. I have the right to do that, haven’t I? But it is a big subject, and we’ll talk over everything properly later. Now I’ll go and dress and send a maid to you.”\n\n# Chapter 19\n\nLeft alone, Darya Alexandrovna, with a good housewife’s eye, scanned her room. All she had seen in entering the house and walking through it, and all she saw now in her room, gave her an impression of wealth and sumptuousness and of that modern European luxury of which she had only read in English novels, but had never seen in Russia and in the country. Everything was new from the new French hangings on the walls to the carpet which covered the whole floor. The bed had a spring mattress, and a special sort of bolster and silk pillowcases on the little pillows. The marble washstand, the dressing table, the little sofa, the tables, the bronze clock on the chimney piece, the window curtains, and the portières were all new and expensive.\n\nThe smart maid, who came in to offer her services, with her hair done up high, and a gown more fashionable than Dolly’s, was as new and expensive as the whole room. Darya Alexandrovna liked her neatness, her deferential and obliging manners, but she felt ill at ease with her. She felt ashamed of her seeing the patched dressing jacket that had unluckily been packed by mistake for her. She was ashamed of the very patches and darned places of which she had been so proud at home. At home it had been so clear that for six dressing jackets there would be needed twenty-four yards of nainsook at sixteen pence the yard, which was a matter of thirty shillings besides the cutting-out and making, and these thirty shillings had been saved. But before the maid she felt, if not exactly ashamed, at least uncomfortable.\n\nDarya Alexandrovna had a great sense of relief when Annushka, whom she had known for years, walked in. The smart maid was sent for to go to her mistress, and Annushka remained with Darya Alexandrovna.\n\nAnnushka was obviously much pleased at that lady’s arrival, and began to chatter away without a pause. Dolly observed that she was longing to express her opinion in regard to her mistress’s position, especially as to the love and devotion of the count to Anna Arkadyevna, but Dolly carefully interrupted her whenever she began to speak about this.\n\n“I grew up with Anna Arkadyevna; my lady’s dearer to me than anything. Well, it’s not for us to judge. And, to be sure, there seems so much love....”\n\n“Kindly pour out the water for me to wash now, please,” Darya Alexandrovna cut her short.\n\n“Certainly. We’ve two women kept specially for washing small things, but most of the linen’s done by machinery. The count goes into everything himself. Ah, what a husband!...”\n\nDolly was glad when Anna came in, and by her entrance put a stop to Annushka’s gossip.\n\nAnna had put on a very simple batiste gown. Dolly scrutinized that simple gown attentively. She knew what it meant, and the price at which such simplicity was obtained.\n\n“An old friend,” said Anna of Annushka.\n\nAnna was not embarrassed now. She was perfectly composed and at ease. Dolly saw that she had now completely recovered from the impression her arrival had made on her, and had assumed that superficial, careless tone which, as it were, closed the door on that compartment in which her deeper feelings and ideas were kept.\n\n“Well, Anna, and how is your little girl?” asked Dolly.\n\n“Annie?” (This was what she called her little daughter Anna.) “Very well. She has got on wonderfully. Would you like to see her? Come, I’ll show her to you. We had a terrible bother,” she began telling her, “over nurses. We had an Italian wet-nurse. A good creature, but so stupid! We wanted to get rid of her, but the baby is so used to her that we’ve gone on keeping her still.”\n\n“But how have you managed?...” Dolly was beginning a question as to what name the little girl would have; but noticing a sudden frown on Anna’s face, she changed the drift of her question.\n\n“How did you manage? have you weaned her yet?”\n\nBut Anna had understood.\n\n“You didn’t mean to ask that? You meant to ask about her surname. Yes? That worries Alexey. She has no name—that is, she’s a Karenina,” said Anna, dropping her eyelids till nothing could be seen but the eyelashes meeting. “But we’ll talk about all that later,” her face suddenly brightening. “Come, I’ll show you her. Elle est très gentille. She crawls now.”\n\nIn the nursery the luxury which had impressed Dolly in the whole house struck her still more. There were little go-carts ordered from England, and appliances for learning to walk, and a sofa after the fashion of a billiard table, purposely constructed for crawling, and swings and baths, all of special pattern, and modern. They were all English, solid, and of good make, and obviously very expensive. The room was large, and very light and lofty.\n\nWhen they went in, the baby, with nothing on but her little smock, was sitting in a little elbow chair at the table, having her dinner of broth, which she was spilling all over her little chest. The baby was being fed, and the Russian nursery maid was evidently sharing her meal. Neither the wet-nurse nor the head-nurse were there; they were in the next room, from which came the sound of their conversation in the queer French which was their only means of communication.\n\nHearing Anna’s voice, a smart, tall, English nurse with a disagreeable face and a dissolute expression walked in at the door, hurriedly shaking her fair curls, and immediately began to defend herself though Anna had not found fault with her. At every word Anna said, the English nurse said hurriedly several times, “Yes, my lady.”\n\nThe rosy baby with her black eyebrows and hair, her sturdy red little body with tight goose-flesh skin, delighted Darya Alexandrovna in spite of the cross expression with which she stared at the stranger. She positively envied the baby’s healthy appearance. She was delighted, too, at the baby’s crawling. Not one of her own children had crawled like that. When the baby was put on the carpet and its little dress tucked up behind, it was wonderfully charming. Looking round like some little wild animal at the grown-up big people with her bright black eyes, she smiled, unmistakably pleased at their admiring her, and holding her legs sideways, she pressed vigorously on her arms, and rapidly drew her whole back up after, and then made another step forward with her little arms.\n\nBut the whole atmosphere of the nursery, and especially the English nurse, Darya Alexandrovna did not like at all. It was only on the supposition that no good nurse would have entered so irregular a household as Anna’s that Darya Alexandrovna could explain to herself how Anna with her insight into people could take such an unprepossessing, disreputable-looking woman as nurse to her child.\n\nBesides, from a few words that were dropped, Darya Alexandrovna saw at once that Anna, the two nurses, and the child had no common existence, and that the mother’s visit was something exceptional. Anna wanted to get the baby her plaything, and could not find it.\n\nMost amazing of all was the fact that on being asked how many teeth the baby had, Anna answered wrong, and knew nothing about the two last teeth.\n\n“I sometimes feel sorry I’m so superfluous here,” said Anna, going out of the nursery and holding up her skirt so as to escape the plaything standing in the doorway. “It was very different with my first child.”\n\n“I expected it to be the other way,” said Darya Alexandrovna shyly.\n\n“Oh, no! By the way, do you know I saw Seryozha?” said Anna, screwing up her eyes, as though looking at something far away. “But we’ll talk about that later. You wouldn’t believe it, I’m like a hungry beggar woman when a full dinner is set before her, and she does not know what to begin on first. The dinner is you, and the talks I have before me with you, which I could never have with anyone else; and I don’t know which subject to begin upon first. Mais je ne vous ferai grâce de rien. I must have everything out with you.”\n\n“Oh, I ought to give you a sketch of the company you will meet with us,” she went on. “I’ll begin with the ladies. Princess Varvara—you know her, and I know your opinion and Stiva’s about her. Stiva says the whole aim of her existence is to prove her superiority over Auntie Katerina Pavlovna: that’s all true; but she’s a good-natured woman, and I am so grateful to her. In Petersburg there was a moment when a chaperon was absolutely essential for me. Then she turned up. But really she is good-natured. She did a great deal to alleviate my position. I see you don’t understand all the difficulty of my position ... there in Petersburg,” she added. “Here I’m perfectly at ease and happy. Well, of that later on, though. Then Sviazhsky—he’s the marshal of the district, and he’s a very good sort of a man, but he wants to get something out of Alexey. You understand, with his property, now that we are settled in the country, Alexey can exercise great influence. Then there’s Tushkevitch—you have seen him, you know—Betsy’s admirer. Now he’s been thrown over and he’s come to see us. As Alexey says, he’s one of those people who are very pleasant if one accepts them for what they try to appear to be, et puis il est comme il faut, as Princess Varvara says. Then Veslovsky ... you know him. A very nice boy,” she said, and a sly smile curved her lips. “What’s this wild story about him and the Levins? Veslovsky told Alexey about it, and we don’t believe it. Il est très gentil et naïf,” she said again with the same smile. “Men need occupation, and Alexey needs a circle, so I value all these people. We have to have the house lively and gay, so that Alexey may not long for any novelty. Then you’ll see the steward—a German, a very good fellow, and he understands his work. Alexey has a very high opinion of him. Then the doctor, a young man, not quite a Nihilist perhaps, but you know, eats with his knife ... but a very good doctor. Then the architect.... Une petite cour!”\n\n# Chapter 20\n\n“Here’s Dolly for you, princess, you were so anxious to see her,” said Anna, coming out with Darya Alexandrovna onto the stone terrace where Princess Varvara was sitting in the shade at an embroidery frame, working at a cover for Count Alexey Kirillovitch’s easy chair. “She says she doesn’t want anything before dinner, but please order some lunch for her, and I’ll go and look for Alexey and bring them all in.”\n\nPrincess Varvara gave Dolly a cordial and rather patronizing reception, and began at once explaining to her that she was living with Anna because she had always cared more for her than her sister Katerina Pavlovna, the aunt that had brought Anna up, and that now, when everyone had abandoned Anna, she thought it her duty to help her in this most difficult period of transition.\n\n“Her husband will give her a divorce, and then I shall go back to my solitude; but now I can be of use, and I am doing my duty, however difficult it may be for me—not like some other people. And how sweet it is of you, how right of you to have come! They live like the best of married couples; it’s for God to judge them, not for us. And didn’t Biryuzovsky and Madame Avenieva ... and Sam Nikandrov, and Vassiliev and Madame Mamonova, and Liza Neptunova.... Did no one say anything about them? And it has ended by their being received by everyone. And then, c’est un intérieur si joli, si comme il faut. Tout-à-fait à l’anglaise. On se réunit le matin au breakfast, et puis on se sépare. Everyone does as he pleases till dinner time. Dinner at seven o’clock. Stiva did very rightly to send you. He needs their support. You know that through his mother and brother he can do anything. And then they do so much good. He didn’t tell you about his hospital? Ce sera admirable—everything from Paris.”\n\nTheir conversation was interrupted by Anna, who had found the men of the party in the billiard room, and returned with them to the terrace. There was still a long time before the dinner-hour, it was exquisite weather, and so several different methods of spending the next two hours were proposed. There were very many methods of passing the time at Vozdvizhenskoe, and these were all unlike those in use at Pokrovskoe.\n\n“Une partie de lawn-tennis,” Veslovsky proposed, with his handsome smile. “We’ll be partners again, Anna Arkadyevna.”\n\n“No, it’s too hot; better stroll about the garden and have a row in the boat, show Darya Alexandrovna the river banks.” Vronsky proposed.\n\n“I agree to anything,” said Sviazhsky.\n\n“I imagine that what Dolly would like best would be a stroll—wouldn’t you? And then the boat, perhaps,” said Anna.\n\nSo it was decided. Veslovsky and Tushkevitch went off to the bathing place, promising to get the boat ready and to wait there for them.\n\nThey walked along the path in two couples, Anna with Sviazhsky, and Dolly with Vronsky. Dolly was a little embarrassed and anxious in the new surroundings in which she found herself. Abstractly, theoretically, she did not merely justify, she positively approved of Anna’s conduct. As is indeed not unfrequent with women of unimpeachable virtue, weary of the monotony of respectable existence, at a distance she not only excused illicit love, she positively envied it. Besides, she loved Anna with all her heart. But seeing Anna in actual life among these strangers, with this fashionable tone that was so new to Darya Alexandrovna, she felt ill at ease. What she disliked particularly was seeing Princess Varvara ready to overlook everything for the sake of the comforts she enjoyed.\n\nAs a general principle, abstractly, Dolly approved of Anna’s action; but to see the man for whose sake her action had been taken was disagreeable to her. Moreover, she had never liked Vronsky. She thought him very proud, and saw nothing in him of which he could be proud except his wealth. But against her own will, here in his own house, he overawed her more than ever, and she could not be at ease with him. She felt with him the same feeling she had had with the maid about her dressing jacket. Just as with the maid she had felt not exactly ashamed, but embarrassed at her darns, so she felt with him not exactly ashamed, but embarrassed at herself.\n\nDolly was ill at ease, and tried to find a subject of conversation. Even though she supposed that, through his pride, praise of his house and garden would be sure to be disagreeable to him, she did all the same tell him how much she liked his house.\n\n“Yes, it’s a very fine building, and in the good old-fashioned style,” he said.\n\n“I like so much the court in front of the steps. Was that always so?”\n\n“Oh, no!” he said, and his face beamed with pleasure. “If you could only have seen that court last spring!”\n\nAnd he began, at first rather diffidently, but more and more carried away by the subject as he went on, to draw her attention to the various details of the decoration of his house and garden. It was evident that, having devoted a great deal of trouble to improve and beautify his home, Vronsky felt a need to show off the improvements to a new person, and was genuinely delighted at Darya Alexandrovna’s praise.\n\n“If you would care to look at the hospital, and are not tired, indeed, it’s not far. Shall we go?” he said, glancing into her face to convince himself that she was not bored. “Are you coming, Anna?” he turned to her.\n\n“We will come, won’t we?” she said, addressing Sviazhsky. “Mais il ne faut pas laisser le pauvre Veslovsky et Tushkevitch se morfondre là dans le bateau. We must send and tell them.”\n\n“Yes, this is a monument he is setting up here,” said Anna, turning to Dolly with that sly smile of comprehension with which she had previously talked about the hospital.\n\n“Oh, it’s a work of real importance!” said Sviazhsky. But to show he was not trying to ingratiate himself with Vronsky, he promptly added some slightly critical remarks.\n\n“I wonder, though, count,” he said, “that while you do so much for the health of the peasants, you take so little interest in the schools.”\n\n“C’est devenu tellement commun les écoles,” said Vronsky. “You understand it’s not on that account, but it just happens so, my interest has been diverted elsewhere. This way then to the hospital,” he said to Darya Alexandrovna, pointing to a turning out of the avenue.\n\nThe ladies put up their parasols and turned into the side path. After going down several turnings, and going through a little gate, Darya Alexandrovna saw standing on rising ground before her a large pretentious-looking red building, almost finished. The iron roof, which was not yet painted, shone with dazzling brightness in the sunshine. Beside the finished building another had been begun, surrounded by scaffolding. Workmen in aprons, standing on scaffolds, were laying bricks, pouring mortar out of vats, and smoothing it with trowels.\n\n“How quickly work gets done with you!” said Sviazhsky. “When I was here last time the roof was not on.”\n\n“By the autumn it will all be ready. Inside almost everything is done,” said Anna.\n\n“And what’s this new building?”\n\n“That’s the house for the doctor and the dispensary,” answered Vronsky, seeing the architect in a short jacket coming towards him; and excusing himself to the ladies, he went to meet him.\n\nGoing round a hole where the workmen were slaking lime, he stood still with the architect and began talking rather warmly.\n\n“The front is still too low,” he said to Anna, who had asked what was the matter.\n\n“I said the foundation ought to be raised,” said Anna.\n\n“Yes, of course it would have been much better, Anna Arkadyevna,” said the architect, “but now it’s too late.”\n\n“Yes, I take a great interest in it,” Anna answered Sviazhsky, who was expressing his surprise at her knowledge of architecture. “This new building ought to have been in harmony with the hospital. It was an afterthought, and was begun without a plan.”\n\nVronsky, having finished his talk with the architect, joined the ladies, and led them inside the hospital.\n\nAlthough they were still at work on the cornices outside and were painting on the ground floor, upstairs almost all the rooms were finished. Going up the broad cast-iron staircase to the landing, they walked into the first large room. The walls were stuccoed to look like marble, the huge plate-glass windows were already in, only the parquet floor was not yet finished, and the carpenters, who were planing a block of it, left their work, taking off the bands that fastened their hair, to greet the gentry.\n\n“This is the reception room,” said Vronsky. “Here there will be a desk, tables, and benches, and nothing more.”\n\n“This way; let us go in here. Don’t go near the window,” said Anna, trying the paint to see if it were dry. “Alexey, the paint’s dry already,” she added.\n\nFrom the reception room they went into the corridor. Here Vronsky showed them the mechanism for ventilation on a novel system. Then he showed them marble baths, and beds with extraordinary springs. Then he showed them the wards one after another, the storeroom, the linen room, then the heating stove of a new pattern, then the trolleys, which would make no noise as they carried everything needed along the corridors, and many other things. Sviazhsky, as a connoisseur in the latest mechanical improvements, appreciated everything fully. Dolly simply wondered at all she had not seen before, and, anxious to understand it all, made minute inquiries about everything, which gave Vronsky great satisfaction.\n\n“Yes, I imagine that this will be the solitary example of a properly fitted hospital in Russia,” said Sviazhsky.\n\n“And won’t you have a lying-in ward?” asked Dolly. “That’s so much needed in the country. I have often....”\n\nIn spite of his usual courtesy, Vronsky interrupted her.\n\n“This is not a lying-in home, but a hospital for the sick, and is intended for all diseases, except infectious complaints,” he said. “Ah! look at this,” and he rolled up to Darya Alexandrovna an invalid chair that had just been ordered for the convalescents. “Look.” He sat down in the chair and began moving it. “The patient can’t walk—still too weak, perhaps, or something wrong with his legs, but he must have air, and he moves, rolls himself along....”\n\nDarya Alexandrovna was interested by everything. She liked everything very much, but most of all she liked Vronsky himself with his natural, simple-hearted eagerness. “Yes, he’s a very nice, good man,” she thought several times, not hearing what he said, but looking at him and penetrating into his expression, while she mentally put herself in Anna’s place. She liked him so much just now with his eager interest that she saw how Anna could be in love with him.\n\n# Chapter 21\n\n“No, I think the princess is tired, and horses don’t interest her,” Vronsky said to Anna, who wanted to go on to the stables, where Sviazhsky wished to see the new stallion. “You go on, while I escort the princess home, and we’ll have a little talk,” he said, “if you would like that?” he added, turning to her.\n\n“I know nothing about horses, and I shall be delighted,” answered Darya Alexandrovna, rather astonished.\n\nShe saw by Vronsky’s face that he wanted something from her. She was not mistaken. As soon as they had passed through the little gate back into the garden, he looked in the direction Anna had taken, and having made sure that she could neither hear nor see them, he began:\n\n“You guess that I have something I want to say to you,” he said, looking at her with laughing eyes. “I am not wrong in believing you to be a friend of Anna’s.” He took off his hat, and taking out his handkerchief, wiped his head, which was growing bald.\n\nDarya Alexandrovna made no answer, and merely stared at him with dismay. When she was left alone with him, she suddenly felt afraid; his laughing eyes and stern expression scared her.\n\nThe most diverse suppositions as to what he was about to speak of to her flashed into her brain. “He is going to beg me to come to stay with them with the children, and I shall have to refuse; or to create a set that will receive Anna in Moscow.... Or isn’t it Vassenka Veslovsky and his relations with Anna? Or perhaps about Kitty, that he feels he was to blame?” All her conjectures were unpleasant, but she did not guess what he really wanted to talk about to her.\n\n“You have so much influence with Anna, she is so fond of you,” he said; “do help me.”\n\nDarya Alexandrovna looked with timid inquiry into his energetic face, which under the lime-trees was continually being lighted up in patches by the sunshine, and then passing into complete shadow again. She waited for him to say more, but he walked in silence beside her, scratching with his cane in the gravel.\n\n“You have come to see us, you, the only woman of Anna’s former friends—I don’t count Princess Varvara—but I know that you have done this not because you regard our position as normal, but because, understanding all the difficulty of the position, you still love her and want to be a help to her. Have I understood you rightly?” he asked, looking round at her.\n\n“Oh, yes,” answered Darya Alexandrovna, putting down her sunshade, “but....”\n\n“No,” he broke in, and unconsciously, oblivious of the awkward position into which he was putting his companion, he stopped abruptly, so that she had to stop short too. “No one feels more deeply and intensely than I do all the difficulty of Anna’s position; and that you may well understand, if you do me the honor of supposing I have any heart. I am to blame for that position, and that is why I feel it.”\n\n“I understand,” said Darya Alexandrovna, involuntarily admiring the sincerity and firmness with which he said this. “But just because you feel yourself responsible, you exaggerate it, I am afraid,” she said. “Her position in the world is difficult, I can well understand.”\n\n“In the world it is hell!” he brought out quickly, frowning darkly. “You can’t imagine moral sufferings greater than what she went through in Petersburg in that fortnight ... and I beg you to believe it.”\n\n“Yes, but here, so long as neither Anna ... nor you miss society....”\n\n“Society!” he said contemptuously, “how could I miss society?”\n\n“So far—and it may be so always—you are happy and at peace. I see in Anna that she is happy, perfectly happy, she has had time to tell me so much already,” said Darya Alexandrovna, smiling; and involuntarily, as she said this, at the same moment a doubt entered her mind whether Anna really were happy.\n\nBut Vronsky, it appeared, had no doubts on that score.\n\n“Yes, yes,” he said, “I know that she has revived after all her sufferings; she is happy. She is happy in the present. But I?... I am afraid of what is before us ... I beg your pardon, you would like to walk on?”\n\n“No, I don’t mind.”\n\n“Well, then, let us sit here.”\n\nDarya Alexandrovna sat down on a garden seat in a corner of the avenue. He stood up facing her.\n\n“I see that she is happy,” he repeated, and the doubt whether she were happy sank more deeply into Darya Alexandrovna’s mind. “But can it last? Whether we have acted rightly or wrongly is another question, but the die is cast,” he said, passing from Russian to French, “and we are bound together for life. We are united by all the ties of love that we hold most sacred. We have a child, we may have other children. But the law and all the conditions of our position are such that thousands of complications arise which she does not see and does not want to see. And that one can well understand. But I can’t help seeing them. My daughter is by law not my daughter, but Karenin’s. I cannot bear this falsity!” he said, with a vigorous gesture of refusal, and he looked with gloomy inquiry towards Darya Alexandrovna.\n\nShe made no answer, but simply gazed at him. He went on:\n\n“One day a son may be born, my son, and he will be legally a Karenin; he will not be the heir of my name nor of my property, and however happy we may be in our home life and however many children we may have, there will be no real tie between us. They will be Karenins. You can understand the bitterness and horror of this position! I have tried to speak of this to Anna. It irritates her. She does not understand, and to her I cannot speak plainly of all this. Now look at another side. I am happy, happy in her love, but I must have occupation. I have found occupation, and am proud of what I am doing and consider it nobler than the pursuits of my former companions at court and in the army. And most certainly I would not change the work I am doing for theirs. I am working here, settled in my own place, and I am happy and contented, and we need nothing more to make us happy. I love my work here. Ce n’est pas un pis-aller, on the contrary....”\n\nDarya Alexandrovna noticed that at this point in his explanation he grew confused, and she did not quite understand this digression, but she felt that having once begun to speak of matters near his heart, of which he could not speak to Anna, he was now making a clean breast of everything, and that the question of his pursuits in the country fell into the same category of matters near his heart, as the question of his relations with Anna.\n\n“Well, I will go on,” he said, collecting himself. “The great thing is that as I work I want to have a conviction that what I am doing will not die with me, that I shall have heirs to come after me,—and this I have not. Conceive the position of a man who knows that his children, the children of the woman he loves, will not be his, but will belong to someone who hates them and cares nothing about them! It is awful!”\n\nHe paused, evidently much moved.\n\n“Yes, indeed, I see that. But what can Anna do?” queried Darya Alexandrovna.\n\n“Yes, that brings me to the object of my conversation,” he said, calming himself with an effort. “Anna can, it depends on her.... Even to petition the Tsar for legitimization, a divorce is essential. And that depends on Anna. Her husband agreed to a divorce—at that time your husband had arranged it completely. And now, I know, he would not refuse it. It is only a matter of writing to him. He said plainly at that time that if she expressed the desire, he would not refuse. Of course,” he said gloomily, “it is one of those Pharisaical cruelties of which only such heartless men are capable. He knows what agony any recollection of him must give her, and knowing her, he must have a letter from her. I can understand that it is agony to her. But the matter is of such importance, that one must passer par-dessus toutes ces finesses de sentiment. Il y va du bonheur et de l’existence d’Anne et de ses enfants. I won’t speak of myself, though it’s hard for me, very hard,” he said, with an expression as though he were threatening someone for its being hard for him. “And so it is, princess, that I am shamelessly clutching at you as an anchor of salvation. Help me to persuade her to write to him and ask for a divorce.”\n\n“Yes, of course,” Darya Alexandrovna said dreamily, as she vividly recalled her last interview with Alexey Alexandrovitch. “Yes, of course,” she repeated with decision, thinking of Anna.\n\n“Use your influence with her, make her write. I don’t like—I’m almost unable to speak about this to her.”\n\n“Very well, I will talk to her. But how is it she does not think of it herself?” said Darya Alexandrovna, and for some reason she suddenly at that point recalled Anna’s strange new habit of half-closing her eyes. And she remembered that Anna drooped her eyelids just when the deeper questions of life were touched upon. “Just as though she half-shut her eyes to her own life, so as not to see everything,” thought Dolly. “Yes, indeed, for my own sake and for hers I will talk to her,” Dolly said in reply to his look of gratitude.\n\nThey got up and walked to the house.\n\n# Chapter 22\n\nWhen Anna found Dolly at home before her, she looked intently in her eyes, as though questioning her about the talk she had had with Vronsky, but she made no inquiry in words.\n\n“I believe it’s dinner time,” she said. “We’ve not seen each other at all yet. I am reckoning on the evening. Now I want to go and dress. I expect you do too; we all got splashed at the buildings.”\n\nDolly went to her room and she felt amused. To change her dress was impossible, for she had already put on her best dress. But in order to signify in some way her preparation for dinner, she asked the maid to brush her dress, changed her cuffs and tie, and put some lace on her head.\n\n“This is all I can do,” she said with a smile to Anna, who came in to her in a third dress, again of extreme simplicity.\n\n“Yes, we are too formal here,” she said, as it were apologizing for her magnificence. “Alexey is delighted at your visit, as he rarely is at anything. He has completely lost his heart to you,” she added. “You’re not tired?”\n\nThere was no time for talking about anything before dinner. Going into the drawing-room they found Princess Varvara already there, and the gentlemen of the party in black frock-coats. The architect wore a swallow-tail coat. Vronsky presented the doctor and the steward to his guest. The architect he had already introduced to her at the hospital.\n\nA stout butler, resplendent with a smoothly shaven round chin and a starched white cravat, announced that dinner was ready, and the ladies got up. Vronsky asked Sviazhsky to take in Anna Arkadyevna, and himself offered his arm to Dolly. Veslovsky was before Tushkevitch in offering his arm to Princess Varvara, so that Tushkevitch with the steward and the doctor walked in alone.\n\nThe dinner, the dining-room, the service, the waiting at table, the wine, and the food, were not simply in keeping with the general tone of modern luxury throughout all the house, but seemed even more sumptuous and modern. Darya Alexandrovna watched this luxury which was novel to her, and as a good housekeeper used to managing a household—although she never dreamed of adapting anything she saw to her own household, as it was all in a style of luxury far above her own manner of living—she could not help scrutinizing every detail, and wondering how and by whom it was all done. Vassenka Veslovsky, her husband, and even Sviazhsky, and many other people she knew, would never have considered this question, and would have readily believed what every well-bred host tries to make his guests feel, that is, that all that is well-ordered in his house has cost him, the host, no trouble whatever, but comes of itself. Darya Alexandrovna was well aware that even porridge for the children’s breakfast does not come of itself, and that therefore, where so complicated and magnificent a style of luxury was maintained, someone must give earnest attention to its organization. And from the glance with which Alexey Kirillovitch scanned the table, from the way he nodded to the butler, and offered Darya Alexandrovna her choice between cold soup and hot soup, she saw that it was all organized and maintained by the care of the master of the house himself. It was evident that it all rested no more upon Anna than upon Veslovsky. She, Sviazhsky, the princess, and Veslovsky, were equally guests, with light hearts enjoying what had been arranged for them.\n\nAnna was the hostess only in conducting the conversation. The conversation was a difficult one for the lady of the house at a small table with persons present, like the steward and the architect, belonging to a completely different world, struggling not to be overawed by an elegance to which they were unaccustomed, and unable to sustain a large share in the general conversation. But this difficult conversation Anna directed with her usual tact and naturalness, and indeed she did so with actual enjoyment, as Darya Alexandrovna observed. The conversation began about the row Tushkevitch and Veslovsky had taken alone together in the boat, and Tushkevitch began describing the last boat races in Petersburg at the Yacht Club. But Anna, seizing the first pause, at once turned to the architect to draw him out of his silence.\n\n“Nikolay Ivanitch was struck,” she said, meaning Sviazhsky, “at the progress the new building had made since he was here last; but I am there every day, and every day I wonder at the rate at which it grows.”\n\n“It’s first-rate working with his excellency,” said the architect with a smile (he was respectful and composed, though with a sense of his own dignity). “It’s a very different matter to have to do with the district authorities. Where one would have to write out sheaves of papers, here I call upon the count, and in three words we settle the business.”\n\n“The American way of doing business,” said Sviazhsky, with a smile.\n\n“Yes, there they build in a rational fashion....”\n\nThe conversation passed to the misuse of political power in the United States, but Anna quickly brought it round to another topic, so as to draw the steward into talk.\n\n“Have you ever seen a reaping machine?” she said, addressing Darya Alexandrovna. “We had just ridden over to look at one when we met. It’s the first time I ever saw one.”\n\n“How do they work?” asked Dolly.\n\n“Exactly like little scissors. A plank and a lot of little scissors. Like this.”\n\nAnna took a knife and fork in her beautiful white hands covered with rings, and began showing how the machine worked. It was clear that she saw nothing would be understood from her explanation; but aware that her talk was pleasant and her hands beautiful she went on explaining.\n\n“More like little penknives,” Veslovsky said playfully, never taking his eyes off her.\n\nAnna gave a just perceptible smile, but made no answer. “Isn’t it true, Karl Fedoritch, that it’s just like little scissors?” she said to the steward.\n\n“Oh, ja,” answered the German. “Es ist ein ganz einfaches Ding,” and he began to explain the construction of the machine.\n\n“It’s a pity it doesn’t bind too. I saw one at the Vienna exhibition, which binds with a wire,” said Sviazhsky. “They would be more profitable in use.”\n\n“Es kommt drauf an.... Der Preis vom Draht muss ausgerechnet werden.” And the German, roused from his taciturnity, turned to Vronsky. “Das lässt sich ausrechnen, Erlaucht.” The German was just feeling in the pocket where were his pencil and the notebook he always wrote in, but recollecting that he was at a dinner, and observing Vronsky’s chilly glance, he checked himself. “Zu compliziert, macht zu viel Klopot,” he concluded.\n\n“Wünscht man Dochots, so hat man auch Klopots,” said Vassenka Veslovsky, mimicking the German. “J’adore l’allemand,” he addressed Anna again with the same smile.\n\n“Cessez,” she said with playful severity.\n\n“We expected to find you in the fields, Vassily Semyonitch,” she said to the doctor, a sickly-looking man; “have you been there?”\n\n“I went there, but I had taken flight,” the doctor answered with gloomy jocoseness.\n\n“Then you’ve taken a good constitutional?”\n\n“Splendid!”\n“Well, and how was the old woman? I hope it’s not typhus?”\n\n“Typhus it is not, but it’s taking a bad turn.”\n\n“What a pity!” said Anna, and having thus paid the dues of civility to her domestic circle, she turned to her own friends.\n\n“It would be a hard task, though, to construct a machine from your description, Anna Arkadyevna,” Sviazhsky said jestingly.\n\n“Oh, no, why so?” said Anna with a smile that betrayed that she knew there was something charming in her disquisitions upon the machine that had been noticed by Sviazhsky. This new trait of girlish coquettishness made an unpleasant impression on Dolly.\n\n“But Anna Arkadyevna’s knowledge of architecture is marvelous,” said Tushkevitch.\n\n“To be sure, I heard Anna Arkadyevna talking yesterday about plinths and damp-courses,” said Veslovsky. “Have I got it right?”\n\n“There’s nothing marvelous about it, when one sees and hears so much of it,” said Anna. “But, I dare say, you don’t even know what houses are made of?”\n\nDarya Alexandrovna saw that Anna disliked the tone of raillery that existed between her and Veslovsky, but fell in with it against her will.\n\nVronsky acted in this matter quite differently from Levin. He obviously attached no significance to Veslovsky’s chattering; on the contrary, he encouraged his jests.\n\n“Come now, tell us, Veslovsky, how are the stones held together?”\n\n“By cement, of course.”\n\n“Bravo! And what is cement?”\n\n“Oh, some sort of paste ... no, putty,” said Veslovsky, raising a general laugh.\n\nThe company at dinner, with the exception of the doctor, the architect, and the steward, who remained plunged in gloomy silence, kept up a conversation that never paused, glancing off one subject, fastening on another, and at times stinging one or the other to the quick. Once Darya Alexandrovna felt wounded to the quick, and got so hot that she positively flushed and wondered afterwards whether she had said anything extreme or unpleasant. Sviazhsky began talking of Levin, describing his strange view that machinery is simply pernicious in its effects on Russian agriculture.\n\n“I have not the pleasure of knowing this M. Levin,” Vronsky said, smiling, “but most likely he has never seen the machines he condemns; or if he has seen and tried any, it must have been after a queer fashion, some Russian imitation, not a machine from abroad. What sort of views can anyone have on such a subject?”\n\n“Turkish views, in general,” Veslovsky said, turning to Anna with a smile.\n\n“I can’t defend his opinions,” Darya Alexandrovna said, firing up; “but I can say that he’s a highly cultivated man, and if he were here he would know very well how to answer you, though I am not capable of doing so.”\n\n“I like him extremely, and we are great friends,” Sviazhsky said, smiling good-naturedly. “Mais pardon, il est un petit peu toqué; he maintains, for instance, that district councils and arbitration boards are all of no use, and he is unwilling to take part in anything.”\n\n“It’s our Russian apathy,” said Vronsky, pouring water from an iced decanter into a delicate glass on a high stem; “we’ve no sense of the duties our privileges impose upon us, and so we refuse to recognize these duties.”\n\n“I know no man more strict in the performance of his duties,” said Darya Alexandrovna, irritated by Vronsky’s tone of superiority.\n\n“For my part,” pursued Vronsky, who was evidently for some reason or other keenly affected by this conversation, “such as I am, I am, on the contrary, extremely grateful for the honor they have done me, thanks to Nikolay Ivanitch” (he indicated Sviazhsky), “in electing me a justice of the peace. I consider that for me the duty of being present at the session, of judging some peasants’ quarrel about a horse, is as important as anything I can do. And I shall regard it as an honor if they elect me for the district council. It’s only in that way I can pay for the advantages I enjoy as a landowner. Unluckily they don’t understand the weight that the big landowners ought to have in the state.”\n\nIt was strange to Darya Alexandrovna to hear how serenely confident he was of being right at his own table. She thought how Levin, who believed the opposite, was just as positive in his opinions at his own table. But she loved Levin, and so she was on his side.\n\n“So we can reckon upon you, count, for the coming elections?” said Sviazhsky. “But you must come a little beforehand, so as to be on the spot by the eighth. If you would do me the honor to stop with me.”\n\n“I rather agree with your beau-frère,” said Anna, “though not quite on the same ground as he,” she added with a smile. “I’m afraid that we have too many of these public duties in these latter days. Just as in old days there were so many government functionaries that one had to call in a functionary for every single thing, so now everyone’s doing some sort of public duty. Alexey has been here now six months, and he’s a member, I do believe, of five or six different public bodies. Du train que cela va, the whole time will be wasted on it. And I’m afraid that with such a multiplicity of these bodies, they’ll end in being a mere form. How many are you a member of, Nikolay Ivanitch?” she turned to Sviazhsky—“over twenty, I fancy.”\n\nAnna spoke lightly, but irritation could be discerned in her tone. Darya Alexandrovna, watching Anna and Vronsky attentively, detected it instantly. She noticed, too, that as she spoke Vronsky’s face had immediately taken a serious and obstinate expression. Noticing this, and that Princess Varvara at once made haste to change the conversation by talking of Petersburg acquaintances, and remembering what Vronsky had without apparent connection said in the garden of his work in the country, Dolly surmised that this question of public activity was connected with some deep private disagreement between Anna and Vronsky.\n\nThe dinner, the wine, the decoration of the table were all very good; but it was all like what Darya Alexandrovna had seen at formal dinners and balls which of late years had become quite unfamiliar to her; it all had the same impersonal and constrained character, and so on an ordinary day and in a little circle of friends it made a disagreeable impression on her.\n\nAfter dinner they sat on the terrace, then they proceeded to play lawn tennis. The players, divided into two parties, stood on opposite sides of a tightly drawn net with gilt poles on the carefully leveled and rolled croquet-ground. Darya Alexandrovna made an attempt to play, but it was a long time before she could understand the game, and by the time she did understand it, she was so tired that she sat down with Princess Varvara and simply looked on at the players. Her partner, Tushkevitch, gave up playing too, but the others kept the game up for a long time. Sviazhsky and Vronsky both played very well and seriously. They kept a sharp lookout on the balls served to them, and without haste or getting in each other’s way, they ran adroitly up to them, waited for the rebound, and neatly and accurately returned them over the net. Veslovsky played worse than the others. He was too eager, but he kept the players lively with his high spirits. His laughter and outcries never paused. Like the other men of the party, with the ladies’ permission, he took off his coat, and his solid, comely figure in his white shirt-sleeves, with his red perspiring face and his impulsive movements, made a picture that imprinted itself vividly on the memory.\n\nWhen Darya Alexandrovna lay in bed that night, as soon as she closed her eyes, she saw Vassenka Veslovsky flying about the croquet ground.\n\nDuring the game Darya Alexandrovna was not enjoying herself. She did not like the light tone of raillery that was kept up all the time between Vassenka Veslovsky and Anna, and the unnaturalness altogether of grown-up people, all alone without children, playing at a child’s game. But to avoid breaking up the party and to get through the time somehow, after a rest she joined the game again, and pretended to be enjoying it. All that day it seemed to her as though she were acting in a theater with actors cleverer than she, and that her bad acting was spoiling the whole performance. She had come with the intention of staying two days, if all went well. But in the evening, during the game, she made up her mind that she would go home next day. The maternal cares and worries, which she had so hated on the way, now, after a day spent without them, struck her in quite another light, and tempted her back to them.\n\nWhen, after evening tea and a row by night in the boat, Darya Alexandrovna went alone to her room, took off her dress, and began arranging her thin hair for the night, she had a great sense of relief.\n\nIt was positively disagreeable to her to think that Anna was coming to see her immediately. She longed to be alone with her own thoughts.\n\n# Chapter 23\n\nDolly was wanting to go to bed when Anna came in to see her, attired for the night. In the course of the day Anna had several times begun to speak of matters near her heart, and every time after a few words she had stopped: “Afterwards, by ourselves, we’ll talk about everything. I’ve got so much I want to tell you,” she said.\n\nNow they were by themselves, and Anna did not know what to talk about. She sat in the window looking at Dolly, and going over in her own mind all the stores of intimate talk which had seemed so inexhaustible beforehand, and she found nothing. At that moment it seemed to her that everything had been said already.\n\n“Well, what of Kitty?” she said with a heavy sigh, looking penitently at Dolly. “Tell me the truth, Dolly: isn’t she angry with me?”\n\n“Angry? Oh, no!” said Darya Alexandrovna, smiling.\n\n“But she hates me, despises me?”\n\n“Oh, no! But you know that sort of thing isn’t forgiven.”\n\n“Yes, yes,” said Anna, turning away and looking out of the open window. “But I was not to blame. And who is to blame? What’s the meaning of being to blame? Could it have been otherwise? What do you think? Could it possibly have happened that you didn’t become the wife of Stiva?”\n\n“Really, I don’t know. But this is what I want you to tell me....”\n\n“Yes, yes, but we’ve not finished about Kitty. Is she happy? He’s a very nice man, they say.”\n\n“He’s much more than very nice. I don’t know a better man.”\n\n“Ah, how glad I am! I’m so glad! Much more than very nice,” she repeated.\n\nDolly smiled.\n\n“But tell me about yourself. We’ve a great deal to talk about. And I’ve had a talk with....” Dolly did not know what to call him. She felt it awkward to call him either the count or Alexey Kirillovitch.\n\n“With Alexey,” said Anna, “I know what you talked about. But I wanted to ask you directly what you think of me, of my life?”\n\n“How am I to say like that straight off? I really don’t know.”\n\n“No, tell me all the same.... You see my life. But you mustn’t forget that you’re seeing us in the summer, when you have come to us and we are not alone.... But we came here early in the spring, lived quite alone, and shall be alone again, and I desire nothing better. But imagine me living alone without him, alone, and that will be ... I see by everything that it will often be repeated, that he will be half the time away from home,” she said, getting up and sitting down close by Dolly.\n\n“Of course,” she interrupted Dolly, who would have answered, “of course I won’t try to keep him by force. I don’t keep him indeed. The races are just coming, his horses are running, he will go. I’m very glad. But think of me, fancy my position.... But what’s the use of talking about it?” She smiled. “Well, what did he talk about with you?”\n\n“He spoke of what I want to speak about of myself, and it’s easy for me to be his advocate; of whether there is not a possibility ... whether you could not....” (Darya Alexandrovna hesitated) “correct, improve your position.... You know how I look at it.... But all the same, if possible, you should get married....”\n\n“Divorce, you mean?” said Anna. “Do you know, the only woman who came to see me in Petersburg was Betsy Tverskaya? You know her, of course? Au fond, c’est la femme la plus depravée qui existe. She had an intrigue with Tushkevitch, deceiving her husband in the basest way. And she told me that she did not care to know me so long as my position was irregular. Don’t imagine I would compare ... I know you, darling. But I could not help remembering.... Well, so what did he say to you?” she repeated.\n\n“He said that he was unhappy on your account and his own. Perhaps you will say that it’s egoism, but what a legitimate and noble egoism. He wants first of all to legitimize his daughter, and to be your husband, to have a legal right to you.”\n\n“What wife, what slave can be so utterly a slave as I, in my position?” she put in gloomily.\n\n“The chief thing he desires ... he desires that you should not suffer.”\n\n“That’s impossible. Well?”\n\n“Well, and the most legitimate desire—he wishes that your children should have a name.”\n\n“What children?” Anna said, not looking at Dolly, and half closing her eyes.\n\n“Annie and those to come....”\n\n“He need not trouble on that score; I shall have no more children.”\n\n“How can you tell that you won’t?”\n\n“I shall not, because I don’t wish it.” And, in spite of all her emotion, Anna smiled, as she caught the naïve expression of curiosity, wonder, and horror on Dolly’s face.\n\n“The doctor told me after my illness....”\n\n“Impossible!” said Dolly, opening her eyes wide.\n\nFor her this was one of those discoveries the consequences and deductions from which are so immense that all that one feels for the first instant is that it is impossible to take it all in, and that one will have to reflect a great, great deal upon it.\n\nThis discovery, suddenly throwing light on all those families of one or two children, which had hitherto been so incomprehensible to her, aroused so many ideas, reflections, and contradictory emotions, that she had nothing to say, and simply gazed with wide-open eyes of wonder at Anna. This was the very thing she had been dreaming of, but now learning that it was possible, she was horrified. She felt that it was too simple a solution of too complicated a problem.\n\n“N’est-ce pas immoral?” was all she said, after a brief pause.\n\n“Why so? Think, I have a choice between two alternatives: either to be with child, that is an invalid, or to be the friend and companion of my husband—practically my husband,” Anna said in a tone intentionally superficial and frivolous.\n\n“Yes, yes,” said Darya Alexandrovna, hearing the very arguments she had used to herself, and not finding the same force in them as before.\n\n“For you, for other people,” said Anna, as though divining her thoughts, “there may be reason to hesitate; but for me.... You must consider, I am not his wife; he loves me as long as he loves me. And how am I to keep his love? Not like this!”\n\nShe moved her white hands in a curve before her waist with extraordinary rapidity, as happens during moments of excitement; ideas and memories rushed into Darya Alexandrovna’s head. “I,” she thought, “did not keep my attraction for Stiva; he left me for others, and the first woman for whom he betrayed me did not keep him by being always pretty and lively. He deserted her and took another. And can Anna attract and keep Count Vronsky in that way? If that is what he looks for, he will find dresses and manners still more attractive and charming. And however white and beautiful her bare arms are, however beautiful her full figure and her eager face under her black curls, he will find something better still, just as my disgusting, pitiful, and charming husband does.”\n\nDolly made no answer, she merely sighed. Anna noticed this sigh, indicating dissent, and she went on. In her armory she had other arguments so strong that no answer could be made to them.\n\n“Do you say that it’s not right? But you must consider,” she went on; “you forget my position. How can I desire children? I’m not speaking of the suffering, I’m not afraid of that. Think only, what are my children to be? Ill-fated children, who will have to bear a stranger’s name. For the very fact of their birth they will be forced to be ashamed of their mother, their father, their birth.”\n\n“But that is just why a divorce is necessary.” But Anna did not hear her. She longed to give utterance to all the arguments with which she had so many times convinced herself.\n\n“What is reason given me for, if I am not to use it to avoid bringing unhappy beings into the world!” She looked at Dolly, but without waiting for a reply she went on:\n\n“I should always feel I had wronged these unhappy children,” she said. “If they are not, at any rate they are not unhappy; while if they are unhappy, I alone should be to blame for it.”\n\nThese were the very arguments Darya Alexandrovna had used in her own reflections; but she heard them without understanding them. “How can one wrong creatures that don’t exist?” she thought. And all at once the idea struck her: could it possibly, under any circumstances, have been better for her favorite Grisha if he had never existed? And this seemed to her so wild, so strange, that she shook her head to drive away this tangle of whirling, mad ideas.\n\n“No, I don’t know; it’s not right,” was all she said, with an expression of disgust on her face.\n\n“Yes, but you mustn’t forget that you and I.... And besides that,” added Anna, in spite of the wealth of her arguments and the poverty of Dolly’s objections, seeming still to admit that it was not right, “don’t forget the chief point, that I am not now in the same position as you. For you the question is: do you desire not to have any more children; while for me it is: do I desire to have them? And that’s a great difference. You must see that I can’t desire it in my position.”\n\nDarya Alexandrovna made no reply. She suddenly felt that she had got far away from Anna; that there lay between them a barrier of questions on which they could never agree, and about which it was better not to speak.\n\n# Chapter 24\n\n“Then there is all the more reason for you to legalize your position, if possible,” said Dolly.\n\n“Yes, if possible,” said Anna, speaking all at once in an utterly different tone, subdued and mournful.\n\n“Surely you don’t mean a divorce is impossible? I was told your husband had consented to it.”\n\n“Dolly, I don’t want to talk about that.”\n\n“Oh, we won’t then,” Darya Alexandrovna hastened to say, noticing the expression of suffering on Anna’s face. “All I see is that you take too gloomy a view of things.”\n\n“I? Not at all! I’m always bright and happy. You see, je fais des passions. Veslovsky....”\n\n“Yes, to tell the truth, I don’t like Veslovsky’s tone,” said Darya Alexandrovna, anxious to change the subject.\n\n“Oh, that’s nonsense! It amuses Alexey, and that’s all; but he’s a boy, and quite under my control. You know, I turn him as I please. It’s just as it might be with your Grisha.... Dolly!”—she suddenly changed the subject—“you say I take too gloomy a view of things. You can’t understand. It’s too awful! I try not to take any view of it at all.”\n\n“But I think you ought to. You ought to do all you can.”\n\n“But what can I do? Nothing. You tell me to marry Alexey, and say I don’t think about it. I don’t think about it!” she repeated, and a flush rose into her face. She got up, straightening her chest, and sighed heavily. With her light step she began pacing up and down the room, stopping now and then. “I don’t think of it? Not a day, not an hour passes that I don’t think of it, and blame myself for thinking of it ... because thinking of that may drive me mad. Drive me mad!” she repeated. “When I think of it, I can’t sleep without morphine. But never mind. Let us talk quietly. They tell me, divorce. In the first place, he won’t give me a divorce. He’s under the influence of Countess Lidia Ivanovna now.”\n\nDarya Alexandrovna, sitting erect on a chair, turned her head, following Anna with a face of sympathetic suffering.\n\n“You ought to make the attempt,” she said softly.\n\n“Suppose I make the attempt. What does it mean?” she said, evidently giving utterance to a thought, a thousand times thought over and learned by heart. “It means that I, hating him, but still recognizing that I have wronged him—and I consider him magnanimous—that I humiliate myself to write to him.... Well, suppose I make the effort; I do it. Either I receive a humiliating refusal or consent.... Well, I have received his consent, say....” Anna was at that moment at the furthest end of the room, and she stopped there, doing something to the curtain at the window. “I receive his consent, but my ... my son? They won’t give him up to me. He will grow up despising me, with his father, whom I’ve abandoned. Do you see, I love ... equally, I think, but both more than myself—two creatures, Seryozha and Alexey.”\n\nShe came out into the middle of the room and stood facing Dolly, with her arms pressed tightly across her chest. In her white dressing gown her figure seemed more than usually grand and broad. She bent her head, and with shining, wet eyes looked from under her brows at Dolly, a thin little pitiful figure in her patched dressing jacket and nightcap, shaking all over with emotion.\n\n“It is only those two creatures that I love, and one excludes the other. I can’t have them together, and that’s the only thing I want. And since I can’t have that, I don’t care about the rest. I don’t care about anything, anything. And it will end one way or another, and so I can’t, I don’t like to talk of it. So don’t blame me, don’t judge me for anything. You can’t with your pure heart understand all that I’m suffering.” She went up, sat down beside Dolly, and with a guilty look, peeped into her face and took her hand.\n\n“What are you thinking? What are you thinking about me? Don’t despise me. I don’t deserve contempt. I’m simply unhappy. If anyone is unhappy, I am,” she articulated, and turning away, she burst into tears.\n\nLeft alone, Darya Alexandrovna said her prayers and went to bed. She had felt for Anna with all her heart while she was speaking to her, but now she could not force herself to think of her. The memories of home and of her children rose up in her imagination with a peculiar charm quite new to her, with a sort of new brilliance. That world of her own seemed to her now so sweet and precious that she would not on any account spend an extra day outside it, and she made up her mind that she would certainly go back next day.\n\nAnna meantime went back to her boudoir, took a wine-glass and dropped into it several drops of a medicine, of which the principal ingredient was morphine. After drinking it off and sitting still a little while, she went into her bedroom in a soothed and more cheerful frame of mind.\n\nWhen she went into the bedroom, Vronsky looked intently at her. He was looking for traces of the conversation which he knew that, staying so long in Dolly’s room, she must have had with her. But in her expression of restrained excitement, and of a sort of reserve, he could find nothing but the beauty that always bewitched him afresh though he was used to it, the consciousness of it, and the desire that it should affect him. He did not want to ask her what they had been talking of, but he hoped that she would tell him something of her own accord. But she only said:\n\n“I am so glad you like Dolly. You do, don’t you?”\n\n“Oh, I’ve known her a long while, you know. She’s very good-hearted, I suppose, mais excessivement terre-à-terre. Still, I’m very glad to see her.”\n\nHe took Anna’s hand and looked inquiringly into her eyes.\n\nMisinterpreting the look, she smiled to him. Next morning, in spite of the protests of her hosts, Darya Alexandrovna prepared for her homeward journey. Levin’s coachman, in his by no means new coat and shabby hat, with his ill-matched horses and his coach with the patched mud-guards, drove with gloomy determination into the covered gravel approach.\n\nDarya Alexandrovna disliked taking leave of Princess Varvara and the gentlemen of the party. After a day spent together, both she and her hosts were distinctly aware that they did not get on together, and that it was better for them not to meet. Only Anna was sad. She knew that now, from Dolly’s departure, no one again would stir up within her soul the feelings that had been roused by their conversation. It hurt her to stir up these feelings, but yet she knew that that was the best part of her soul, and that that part of her soul would quickly be smothered in the life she was leading.\n\nAs she drove out into the open country, Darya Alexandrovna had a delightful sense of relief, and she felt tempted to ask the two men how they had liked being at Vronsky’s, when suddenly the coachman, Philip, expressed himself unasked:\n\n“Rolling in wealth they may be, but three pots of oats was all they gave us. Everything cleared up till there wasn’t a grain left by cockcrow. What are three pots? A mere mouthful! And oats now down to forty-five kopecks. At our place, no fear, all comers may have as much as they can eat.”\n\n“The master’s a screw,” put in the counting-house clerk.\n\n“Well, did you like their horses?” asked Dolly.\n\n“The horses!—there’s no two opinions about them. And the food was good. But it seemed to me sort of dreary there, Darya Alexandrovna. I don’t know what you thought,” he said, turning his handsome, good-natured face to her.\n\n“I thought so too. Well, shall we get home by evening?”\n\n“Eh, we must!”\n\nOn reaching home and finding everyone entirely satisfactory and particularly charming, Darya Alexandrovna began with great liveliness telling them how she had arrived, how warmly they had received her, of the luxury and good taste in which the Vronskys lived, and of their recreations, and she would not allow a word to be said against them.\n\n“One has to know Anna and Vronsky—I have got to know him better now—to see how nice they are, and how touching,” she said, speaking now with perfect sincerity, and forgetting the vague feeling of dissatisfaction and awkwardness she had experienced there.\n\n# Chapter 25\n\nVronsky and Anna spent the whole summer and part of the winter in the country, living in just the same condition, and still taking no steps to obtain a divorce. It was an understood thing between them that they should not go away anywhere; but both felt, the longer they lived alone, especially in the autumn, without guests in the house, that they could not stand this existence, and that they would have to alter it.\n\nTheir life was apparently such that nothing better could be desired. They had the fullest abundance of everything; they had a child, and both had occupation. Anna devoted just as much care to her appearance when they had no visitors, and she did a great deal of reading, both of novels and of what serious literature was in fashion. She ordered all the books that were praised in the foreign papers and reviews she received, and read them with that concentrated attention which is only given to what is read in seclusion. Moreover, every subject that was of interest to Vronsky, she studied in books and special journals, so that he often went straight to her with questions relating to agriculture or architecture, sometimes even with questions relating to horse-breeding or sport. He was amazed at her knowledge, her memory, and at first was disposed to doubt it, to ask for confirmation of her facts; and she would find what he asked for in some book, and show it to him.\n\nThe building of the hospital, too, interested her. She did not merely assist, but planned and suggested a great deal herself. But her chief thought was still of herself—how far she was dear to Vronsky, how far she could make up to him for all he had given up. Vronsky appreciated this desire not only to please, but to serve him, which had become the sole aim of her existence, but at the same time he wearied of the loving snares in which she tried to hold him fast. As time went on, and he saw himself more and more often held fast in these snares, he had an ever growing desire, not so much to escape from them, as to try whether they hindered his freedom. Had it not been for this growing desire to be free, not to have scenes every time he wanted to go to the town to a meeting or a race, Vronsky would have been perfectly satisfied with his life. The rôle he had taken up, the rôle of a wealthy landowner, one of that class which ought to be the very heart of the Russian aristocracy, was entirely to his taste; and now, after spending six months in that character, he derived even greater satisfaction from it. And his management of his estate, which occupied and absorbed him more and more, was most successful. In spite of the immense sums cost him by the hospital, by machinery, by cows ordered from Switzerland, and many other things, he was convinced that he was not wasting, but increasing his substance. In all matters affecting income, the sales of timber, wheat, and wool, the letting of lands, Vronsky was hard as a rock, and knew well how to keep up prices. In all operations on a large scale on this and his other estates, he kept to the simplest methods involving no risk, and in trifling details he was careful and exacting to an extreme degree. In spite of all the cunning and ingenuity of the German steward, who would try to tempt him into purchases by making his original estimate always far larger than really required, and then representing to Vronsky that he might get the thing cheaper, and so make a profit, Vronsky did not give in. He listened to his steward, cross-examined him, and only agreed to his suggestions when the implement to be ordered or constructed was the very newest, not yet known in Russia, and likely to excite wonder. Apart from such exceptions, he resolved upon an increased outlay only where there was a surplus, and in making such an outlay he went into the minutest details, and insisted on getting the very best for his money; so that by the method on which he managed his affairs, it was clear that he was not wasting, but increasing his substance.\n\nIn October there were the provincial elections in the Kashinsky province, where were the estates of Vronsky, Sviazhsky, Koznishev, Oblonsky, and a small part of Levin’s land.\n\nThese elections were attracting public attention from several circumstances connected with them, and also from the people taking part in them. There had been a great deal of talk about them, and great preparations were being made for them. Persons who never attended the elections were coming from Moscow, from Petersburg, and from abroad to attend these. Vronsky had long before promised Sviazhsky to go to them. Before the elections Sviazhsky, who often visited Vozdvizhenskoe, drove over to fetch Vronsky. On the day before there had been almost a quarrel between Vronsky and Anna over this proposed expedition. It was the very dullest autumn weather, which is so dreary in the country, and so, preparing himself for a struggle, Vronsky, with a hard and cold expression, informed Anna of his departure as he had never spoken to her before. But, to his surprise, Anna accepted the information with great composure, and merely asked when he would be back. He looked intently at her, at a loss to explain this composure. She smiled at his look. He knew that way she had of withdrawing into herself, and knew that it only happened when she had determined upon something without letting him know her plans. He was afraid of this; but he was so anxious to avoid a scene that he kept up appearances, and half sincerely believed in what he longed to believe in—her reasonableness.\n\n“I hope you won’t be dull?”\n\n“I hope not,” said Anna. “I got a box of books yesterday from Gautier’s. No, I shan’t be dull.”\n\n“She’s trying to take that tone, and so much the better,” he thought, “or else it would be the same thing over and over again.”\n\nAnd he set off for the elections without appealing to her for a candid explanation. It was the first time since the beginning of their intimacy that he had parted from her without a full explanation. From one point of view this troubled him, but on the other side he felt that it was better so. “At first there will be, as this time, something undefined kept back, and then she will get used to it. In any case I can give up anything for her, but not my masculine independence,” he thought.\n\n# Chapter 26\n\nIn September Levin moved to Moscow for Kitty’s confinement. He had spent a whole month in Moscow with nothing to do, when Sergey Ivanovitch, who had property in the Kashinsky province, and took great interest in the question of the approaching elections, made ready to set off to the elections. He invited his brother, who had a vote in the Seleznevsky district, to come with him. Levin had, moreover, to transact in Kashin some extremely important business relating to the wardship of land and to the receiving of certain redemption money for his sister, who was abroad.\n\nLevin still hesitated, but Kitty, who saw that he was bored in Moscow, and urged him to go, on her own authority ordered him the proper nobleman’s uniform, costing seven pounds. And that seven pounds paid for the uniform was the chief cause that finally decided Levin to go. He went to Kashin....\n\nLevin had been six days in Kashin, visiting the assembly each day, and busily engaged about his sister’s business, which still dragged on. The district marshals of nobility were all occupied with the elections, and it was impossible to get the simplest thing done that depended upon the court of wardship. The other matter, the payment of the sums due, was met too by difficulties. After long negotiations over the legal details, the money was at last ready to be paid; but the notary, a most obliging person, could not hand over the order, because it must have the signature of the president, and the president, though he had not given over his duties to a deputy, was at the elections. All these worrying negotiations, this endless going from place to place, and talking with pleasant and excellent people, who quite saw the unpleasantness of the petitioner’s position, but were powerless to assist him—all these efforts that yielded no result, led to a feeling of misery in Levin akin to the mortifying helplessness one experiences in dreams when one tries to use physical force. He felt this frequently as he talked to his most good-natured solicitor. This solicitor did, it seemed, everything possible, and strained every nerve to get him out of his difficulties. “I tell you what you might try,” he said more than once; “go to so-and-so and so-and-so,” and the solicitor drew up a regular plan for getting round the fatal point that hindered everything. But he would add immediately, “It’ll mean some delay, anyway, but you might try it.” And Levin did try, and did go. Everyone was kind and civil, but the point evaded seemed to crop up again in the end, and again to bar the way. What was particularly trying, was that Levin could not make out with whom he was struggling, to whose interest it was that his business should not be done. That no one seemed to know; the solicitor certainly did not know. If Levin could have understood why, just as he saw why one can only approach the booking office of a railway station in single file, it would not have been so vexatious and tiresome to him. But with the hindrances that confronted him in his business, no one could explain why they existed.\n\nBut Levin had changed a good deal since his marriage; he was patient, and if he could not see why it was all arranged like this, he told himself that he could not judge without knowing all about it, and that most likely it must be so, and he tried not to fret.\n\nIn attending the elections, too, and taking part in them, he tried now not to judge, not to fall foul of them, but to comprehend as fully as he could the question which was so earnestly and ardently absorbing honest and excellent men whom he respected. Since his marriage there had been revealed to Levin so many new and serious aspects of life that had previously, through his frivolous attitude to them, seemed of no importance, that in the question of the elections too he assumed and tried to find some serious significance.\n\nSergey Ivanovitch explained to him the meaning and object of the proposed revolution at the elections. The marshal of the province in whose hands the law had placed the control of so many important public functions—the guardianship of wards (the very department which was giving Levin so much trouble just now), the disposal of large sums subscribed by the nobility of the province, the high schools, female, male, and military, and popular instruction on the new model, and finally, the district council—the marshal of the province, Snetkov, was a nobleman of the old school,—dissipating an immense fortune, a good-hearted man, honest after his own fashion, but utterly without any comprehension of the needs of modern days. He always took, in every question, the side of the nobility; he was positively antagonistic to the spread of popular education, and he succeeded in giving a purely party character to the district council which ought by rights to be of such an immense importance. What was needed was to put in his place a fresh, capable, perfectly modern man, of contemporary ideas, and to frame their policy so as from the rights conferred upon the nobles, not as the nobility, but as an element of the district council, to extract all the powers of self-government that could possibly be derived from them. In the wealthy Kashinsky province, which always took the lead of other provinces in everything, there was now such a preponderance of forces that this policy, once carried through properly there, might serve as a model for other provinces for all Russia. And hence the whole question was of the greatest importance. It was proposed to elect as marshal in place of Snetkov either Sviazhsky, or, better still, Nevyedovsky, a former university professor, a man of remarkable intelligence and a great friend of Sergey Ivanovitch.\n\nThe meeting was opened by the governor, who made a speech to the nobles, urging them to elect the public functionaries, not from regard for persons, but for the service and welfare of their fatherland, and hoping that the honorable nobility of the Kashinsky province would, as at all former elections, hold their duty as sacred, and vindicate the exalted confidence of the monarch.\n\nWhen he had finished with his speech, the governor walked out of the hall, and the noblemen noisily and eagerly—some even enthusiastically—followed him and thronged round him while he put on his fur coat and conversed amicably with the marshal of the province. Levin, anxious to see into everything and not to miss anything, stood there too in the crowd, and heard the governor say: “Please tell Marya Ivanovna my wife is very sorry she couldn’t come to the Home.” And thereupon the nobles in high good-humor sorted out their fur coats and all drove off to the cathedral.\n\nIn the cathedral Levin, lifting his hand like the rest and repeating the words of the archdeacon, swore with most terrible oaths to do all the governor had hoped they would do. Church services always affected Levin, and as he uttered the words “I kiss the cross,” and glanced round at the crowd of young and old men repeating the same, he felt touched.\n\nOn the second and third days there was business relating to the finances of the nobility and the female high school, of no importance whatever, as Sergey Ivanovitch explained, and Levin, busy seeing after his own affairs, did not attend the meetings. On the fourth day the auditing of the marshal’s accounts took place at the high table of the marshal of the province. And then there occurred the first skirmish between the new party and the old. The committee who had been deputed to verify the accounts reported to the meeting that all was in order. The marshal of the province got up, thanked the nobility for their confidence, and shed tears. The nobles gave him a loud welcome, and shook hands with him. But at that instant a nobleman of Sergey Ivanovitch’s party said that he had heard that the committee had not verified the accounts, considering such a verification an insult to the marshal of the province. One of the members of the committee incautiously admitted this. Then a small gentleman, very young-looking but very malignant, began to say that it would probably be agreeable to the marshal of the province to give an account of his expenditures of the public moneys, and that the misplaced delicacy of the members of the committee was depriving him of this moral satisfaction. Then the members of the committee tried to withdraw their admission, and Sergey Ivanovitch began to prove that they must logically admit either that they had verified the accounts or that they had not, and he developed this dilemma in detail. Sergey Ivanovitch was answered by the spokesman of the opposite party. Then Sviazhsky spoke, and then the malignant gentleman again. The discussion lasted a long time and ended in nothing. Levin was surprised that they should dispute upon this subject so long, especially as, when he asked Sergey Ivanovitch whether he supposed that money had been misappropriated, Sergey Ivanovitch answered:\n\n“Oh, no! He’s an honest man. But those old-fashioned methods of paternal family arrangements in the management of provincial affairs must be broken down.”\n\nOn the fifth day came the elections of the district marshals. It was rather a stormy day in several districts. In the Seleznevsky district Sviazhsky was elected unanimously without a ballot, and he gave a dinner that evening.\n\n# Chapter 27\n\nThe sixth day was fixed for the election of the marshal of the province.\n\nThe rooms, large and small, were full of noblemen in all sorts of uniforms. Many had come only for that day. Men who had not seen each other for years, some from the Crimea, some from Petersburg, some from abroad, met in the rooms of the Hall of Nobility. There was much discussion around the governor’s table under the portrait of the Tsar.\n\nThe nobles, both in the larger and the smaller rooms, grouped themselves in camps, and from their hostile and suspicious glances, from the silence that fell upon them when outsiders approached a group, and from the way that some, whispering together, retreated to the farther corridor, it was evident that each side had secrets from the other. In appearance the noblemen were sharply divided into two classes: the old and the new. The old were for the most part either in old uniforms of the nobility, buttoned up closely, with spurs and hats, or in their own special naval, cavalry, infantry, or official uniforms. The uniforms of the older men were embroidered in the old-fashioned way with epaulets on their shoulders; they were unmistakably tight and short in the waist, as though their wearers had grown out of them. The younger men wore the uniform of the nobility with long waists and broad shoulders, unbuttoned over white waistcoats, or uniforms with black collars and with the embroidered badges of justices of the peace. To the younger men belonged the court uniforms that here and there brightened up the crowd.\n\nBut the division into young and old did not correspond with the division of parties. Some of the young men, as Levin observed, belonged to the old party; and some of the very oldest noblemen, on the contrary, were whispering with Sviazhsky, and were evidently ardent partisans of the new party.\n\nLevin stood in the smaller room, where they were smoking and taking light refreshments, close to his own friends, and listening to what they were saying, he conscientiously exerted all his intelligence trying to understand what was said. Sergey Ivanovitch was the center round which the others grouped themselves. He was listening at that moment to Sviazhsky and Hliustov, the marshal of another district, who belonged to their party. Hliustov would not agree to go with his district to ask Snetkov to stand, while Sviazhsky was persuading him to do so, and Sergey Ivanovitch was approving of the plan. Levin could not make out why the opposition was to ask the marshal to stand whom they wanted to supersede.\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch, who had just been drinking and taking some lunch, came up to them in his uniform of a gentleman of the bedchamber, wiping his lips with a perfumed handkerchief of bordered batiste.\n\n“We are placing our forces,” he said, pulling out his whiskers, “Sergey Ivanovitch!”\n\nAnd listening to the conversation, he supported Sviazhsky’s contention.\n\n“One district’s enough, and Sviazhsky’s obviously of the opposition,” he said, words evidently intelligible to all except Levin.\n\n“Why, Kostya, you here too! I suppose you’re converted, eh?” he added, turning to Levin and drawing his arm through his. Levin would have been glad indeed to be converted, but could not make out what the point was, and retreating a few steps from the speakers, he explained to Stepan Arkadyevitch his inability to understand why the marshal of the province should be asked to stand.\n\n“O sancta simplicitas!” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, and briefly and clearly he explained it to Levin. If, as at previous elections, all the districts asked the marshal of the province to stand, then he would be elected without a ballot. That must not be. Now eight districts had agreed to call upon him: if two refused to do so, Snetkov might decline to stand at all; and then the old party might choose another of their party, which would throw them completely out in their reckoning. But if only one district, Sviazhsky’s, did not call upon him to stand, Snetkov would let himself be balloted for. They were even, some of them, going to vote for him, and purposely to let him get a good many votes, so that the enemy might be thrown off the scent, and when a candidate of the other side was put up, they too might give him some votes. Levin understood to some extent, but not fully, and would have put a few more questions, when suddenly everyone began talking and making a noise and they moved towards the big room.\n\n“What is it? eh? whom?” “No guarantee? whose? what?” “They won’t pass him?” “No guarantee?” “They won’t let Flerov in?” “Eh, because of the charge against him?” “Why, at this rate, they won’t admit anyone. It’s a swindle!” “The law!” Levin heard exclamations on all sides, and he moved into the big room together with the others, all hurrying somewhere and afraid of missing something. Squeezed by the crowding noblemen, he drew near the high table where the marshal of the province, Sviazhsky, and the other leaders were hotly disputing about something.\n\n# Chapter 28\n\nLevin was standing rather far off. A nobleman breathing heavily and hoarsely at his side, and another whose thick boots were creaking, prevented him from hearing distinctly. He could only hear the soft voice of the marshal faintly, then the shrill voice of the malignant gentleman, and then the voice of Sviazhsky. They were disputing, as far as he could make out, as to the interpretation to be put on the act and the exact meaning of the words: “liable to be called up for trial.”\n\nThe crowd parted to make way for Sergey Ivanovitch approaching the table. Sergey Ivanovitch, waiting till the malignant gentleman had finished speaking, said that he thought the best solution would be to refer to the act itself, and asked the secretary to find the act. The act said that in case of difference of opinion, there must be a ballot.\n\nSergey Ivanovitch read the act and began to explain its meaning, but at that point a tall, stout, round-shouldered landowner, with dyed whiskers, in a tight uniform that cut the back of his neck, interrupted him. He went up to the table, and striking it with his finger ring, he shouted loudly: “A ballot! Put it to the vote! No need for more talking!” Then several voices began to talk all at once, and the tall nobleman with the ring, getting more and more exasperated, shouted more and more loudly. But it was impossible to make out what he said.\n\nHe was shouting for the very course Sergey Ivanovitch had proposed; but it was evident that he hated him and all his party, and this feeling of hatred spread through the whole party and roused in opposition to it the same vindictiveness, though in a more seemly form, on the other side. Shouts were raised, and for a moment all was confusion, so that the marshal of the province had to call for order.\n\n“A ballot! A ballot! Every nobleman sees it! We shed our blood for our country!... The confidence of the monarch.... No checking the accounts of the marshal; he’s not a cashier.... But that’s not the point.... Votes, please! Beastly!...” shouted furious and violent voices on all sides. Looks and faces were even more violent and furious than their words. They expressed the most implacable hatred. Levin did not in the least understand what was the matter, and he marveled at the passion with which it was disputed whether or not the decision about Flerov should be put to the vote. He forgot, as Sergey Ivanovitch explained to him afterwards, this syllogism: that it was necessary for the public good to get rid of the marshal of the province; that to get rid of the marshal it was necessary to have a majority of votes; that to get a majority of votes it was necessary to secure Flerov’s right to vote; that to secure the recognition of Flerov’s right to vote they must decide on the interpretation to be put on the act.\n\n“And one vote may decide the whole question, and one must be serious and consecutive, if one wants to be of use in public life,” concluded Sergey Ivanovitch. But Levin forgot all that, and it was painful to him to see all these excellent persons, for whom he had a respect, in such an unpleasant and vicious state of excitement. To escape from this painful feeling he went away into the other room where there was nobody except the waiters at the refreshment bar. Seeing the waiters busy over washing up the crockery and setting in order their plates and wine-glasses, seeing their calm and cheerful faces, Levin felt an unexpected sense of relief as though he had come out of a stuffy room into the fresh air. He began walking up and down, looking with pleasure at the waiters. He particularly liked the way one gray-whiskered waiter, who showed his scorn for the other younger ones and was jeered at by them, was teaching them how to fold up napkins properly. Levin was just about to enter into conversation with the old waiter, when the secretary of the court of wardship, a little old man whose specialty it was to know all the noblemen of the province by name and patronymic, drew him away.\n\n“Please come, Konstantin Dmitrievitch,” he said, “your brother’s looking for you. They are voting on the legal point.”\n\nLevin walked into the room, received a white ball, and followed his brother, Sergey Ivanovitch, to the table where Sviazhsky was standing with a significant and ironical face, holding his beard in his fist and sniffing at it. Sergey Ivanovitch put his hand into the box, put the ball somewhere, and making room for Levin, stopped. Levin advanced, but utterly forgetting what he was to do, and much embarrassed, he turned to Sergey Ivanovitch with the question, “Where am I to put it?” He asked this softly, at a moment when there was talking going on near, so that he had hoped his question would not be overheard. But the persons speaking paused, and his improper question was overheard. Sergey Ivanovitch frowned.\n\n“That is a matter for each man’s own decision,” he said severely.\n\nSeveral people smiled. Levin crimsoned, hurriedly thrust his hand under the cloth, and put the ball to the right as it was in his right hand. Having put it in, he recollected that he ought to have thrust his left hand too, and so he thrust it in though too late, and, still more overcome with confusion, he beat a hasty retreat into the background.\n\n“A hundred and twenty-six for admission! Ninety-eight against!” sang out the voice of the secretary, who could not pronounce the letter r. Then there was a laugh; a button and two nuts were found in the box. The nobleman was allowed the right to vote, and the new party had conquered.\n\nBut the old party did not consider themselves conquered. Levin heard that they were asking Snetkov to stand, and he saw that a crowd of noblemen was surrounding the marshal, who was saying something. Levin went nearer. In reply Snetkov spoke of the trust the noblemen of the province had placed in him, the affection they had shown him, which he did not deserve, as his only merit had been his attachment to the nobility, to whom he had devoted twelve years of service. Several times he repeated the words: “I have served to the best of my powers with truth and good faith, I value your goodness and thank you,” and suddenly he stopped short from the tears that choked him, and went out of the room. Whether these tears came from a sense of the injustice being done him, from his love for the nobility, or from the strain of the position he was placed in, feeling himself surrounded by enemies, his emotion infected the assembly, the majority were touched, and Levin felt a tenderness for Snetkov.\n\nIn the doorway the marshal of the province jostled against Levin.\n\n“Beg pardon, excuse me, please,” he said as to a stranger, but recognizing Levin, he smiled timidly. It seemed to Levin that he would have liked to say something, but could not speak for emotion. His face and his whole figure in his uniform with the crosses, and white trousers striped with braid, as he moved hurriedly along, reminded Levin of some hunted beast who sees that he is in evil case. This expression in the marshal’s face was particularly touching to Levin, because, only the day before, he had been at his house about his trustee business and had seen him in all his grandeur, a kind-hearted, fatherly man. The big house with the old family furniture; the rather dirty, far from stylish, but respectful footmen, unmistakably old house serfs who had stuck to their master; the stout, good-natured wife in a cap with lace and a Turkish shawl, petting her pretty grandchild, her daughter’s daughter; the young son, a sixth form high school boy, coming home from school, and greeting his father, kissing his big hand; the genuine, cordial words and gestures of the old man—all this had the day before roused an instinctive feeling of respect and sympathy in Levin. This old man was a touching and pathetic figure to Levin now, and he longed to say something pleasant to him.\n\n“So you’re sure to be our marshal again,” he said.\n\n“It’s not likely,” said the marshal, looking round with a scared expression. “I’m worn out, I’m old. If there are men younger and more deserving than I, let them serve.”\n\nAnd the marshal disappeared through a side door.\n\nThe most solemn moment was at hand. They were to proceed immediately to the election. The leaders of both parties were reckoning white and black on their fingers.\n\nThe discussion upon Flerov had given the new party not only Flerov’s vote, but had also gained time for them, so that they could send to fetch three noblemen who had been rendered unable to take part in the elections by the wiles of the other party. Two noble gentlemen, who had a weakness for strong drink, had been made drunk by the partisans of Snetkov, and a third had been robbed of his uniform.\n\nOn learning this, the new party had made haste, during the dispute about Flerov, to send some of their men in a sledge to clothe the stripped gentleman, and to bring along one of the intoxicated to the meeting.\n\n“I’ve brought one, drenched him with water,” said the landowner, who had gone on this errand, to Sviazhsky. “He’s all right? he’ll do.”\n\n“Not too drunk, he won’t fall down?” said Sviazhsky, shaking his head.\n\n“No, he’s first-rate. If only they don’t give him any more here.... I’ve told the waiter not to give him anything on any account.”\n\n# Chapter 29\n\nThe narrow room, in which they were smoking and taking refreshments, was full of noblemen. The excitement grew more intense, and every face betrayed some uneasiness. The excitement was specially keen for the leaders of each party, who knew every detail, and had reckoned up every vote. They were the generals organizing the approaching battle. The rest, like the rank and file before an engagement, though they were getting ready for the fight, sought for other distractions in the interval. Some were lunching, standing at the bar, or sitting at the table; others were walking up and down the long room, smoking cigarettes, and talking with friends whom they had not seen for a long while.\n\nLevin did not care to eat, and he was not smoking; he did not want to join his own friends, that is Sergey Ivanovitch, Stepan Arkadyevitch, Sviazhsky and the rest, because Vronsky in his equerry’s uniform was standing with them in eager conversation. Levin had seen him already at the meeting on the previous day, and he had studiously avoided him, not caring to greet him. He went to the window and sat down, scanning the groups, and listening to what was being said around him. He felt depressed, especially because everyone else was, as he saw, eager, anxious, and interested, and he alone, with an old, toothless little man with mumbling lips wearing a naval uniform, sitting beside him, had no interest in it and nothing to do.\n\n“He’s such a blackguard! I have told him so, but it makes no difference. Only think of it! He couldn’t collect it in three years!” he heard vigorously uttered by a round-shouldered, short, country gentleman, who had pomaded hair hanging on his embroidered collar, and new boots obviously put on for the occasion, with heels that tapped energetically as he spoke. Casting a displeased glance at Levin, this gentleman sharply turned his back.\n\n“Yes, it’s a dirty business, there’s no denying,” a small gentleman assented in a high voice.\n\nNext, a whole crowd of country gentlemen, surrounding a stout general, hurriedly came near Levin. These persons were unmistakably seeking a place where they could talk without being overheard.\n\n“How dare he say I had his breeches stolen! Pawned them for drink, I expect. Damn the fellow, prince indeed! He’d better not say it, the beast!”\n\n“But excuse me! They take their stand on the act,” was being said in another group; “the wife must be registered as noble.”\n\n“Oh, damn your acts! I speak from my heart. We’re all gentlemen, aren’t we? Above suspicion.”\n\n“Shall we go on, your excellency, fine champagne?”\n\nAnother group was following a nobleman, who was shouting something in a loud voice; it was one of the three intoxicated gentlemen.\n\n“I always advised Marya Semyonovna to let for a fair rent, for she can never save a profit,” he heard a pleasant voice say. The speaker was a country gentleman with gray whiskers, wearing the regimental uniform of an old general staff-officer. It was the very landowner Levin had met at Sviazhsky’s. He knew him at once. The landowner too stared at Levin, and they exchanged greetings.\n\n“Very glad to see you! To be sure! I remember you very well. Last year at our district marshal, Nikolay Ivanovitch’s.”\n\n“Well, and how is your land doing?” asked Levin.\n\n“Oh, still just the same, always at a loss,” the landowner answered with a resigned smile, but with an expression of serenity and conviction that so it must be. “And how do you come to be in our province?” he asked. “Come to take part in our coup d’état?” he said, confidently pronouncing the French words with a bad accent. “All Russia’s here—gentlemen of the bedchamber, and everything short of the ministry.” He pointed to the imposing figure of Stepan Arkadyevitch in white trousers and his court uniform, walking by with a general.\n\n“I ought to own that I don’t very well understand the drift of the provincial elections,” said Levin.\n\nThe landowner looked at him.\n\n“Why, what is there to understand? There’s no meaning in it at all. It’s a decaying institution that goes on running only by the force of inertia. Just look, the very uniforms tell you that it’s an assembly of justices of the peace, permanent members of the court, and so on, but not of noblemen.”\n\n“Then why do you come?” asked Levin.\n\n“From habit, nothing else. Then, too, one must keep up connections. It’s a moral obligation of a sort. And then, to tell the truth, there’s one’s own interests. My son-in-law wants to stand as a permanent member; they’re not rich people, and he must be brought forward. These gentlemen, now, what do they come for?” he said, pointing to the malignant gentleman, who was talking at the high table.\n\n“That’s the new generation of nobility.”\n\n“New it may be, but nobility it isn’t. They’re proprietors of a sort, but we’re the landowners. As noblemen, they’re cutting their own throats.”\n\n“But you say it’s an institution that’s served its time.”\n\n“That it may be, but still it ought to be treated a little more respectfully. Snetkov, now.... We may be of use, or we may not, but we’re the growth of a thousand years. If we’re laying out a garden, planning one before the house, you know, and there you’ve a tree that’s stood for centuries in the very spot.... Old and gnarled it may be, and yet you don’t cut down the old fellow to make room for the flowerbeds, but lay out your beds so as to take advantage of the tree. You won’t grow him again in a year,” he said cautiously, and he immediately changed the conversation. “Well, and how is your land doing?”\n\n“Oh, not very well. I make five per cent.”\n\n“Yes, but you don’t reckon your own work. Aren’t you worth something too? I’ll tell you my own case. Before I took to seeing after the land, I had a salary of three hundred pounds from the service. Now I do more work than I did in the service, and like you I get five per cent. on the land, and thank God for that. But one’s work is thrown in for nothing.”\n\n“Then why do you do it, if it’s a clear loss?”\n\n“Oh, well, one does it! What would you have? It’s habit, and one knows it’s how it should be. And what’s more,” the landowner went on, leaning his elbows on the window and chatting on, “my son, I must tell you, has no taste for it. There’s no doubt he’ll be a scientific man. So there’ll be no one to keep it up. And yet one does it. Here this year I’ve planted an orchard.”\n\n“Yes, yes,” said Levin, “that’s perfectly true. I always feel there’s no real balance of gain in my work on the land, and yet one does it.... It’s a sort of duty one feels to the land.”\n\n“But I tell you what,” the landowner pursued; “a neighbor of mine, a merchant, was at my place. We walked about the fields and the garden. ‘No,’ said he, ‘Stepan Vassilievitch, everything’s well looked after, but your garden’s neglected.’ But, as a fact, it’s well kept up. ‘To my thinking, I’d cut down that lime-tree. Here you’ve thousands of limes, and each would make two good bundles of bark. And nowadays that bark’s worth something. I’d cut down the lot.’”\n\n“And with what he made he’d increase his stock, or buy some land for a trifle, and let it out in lots to the peasants,” Levin added, smiling. He had evidently more than once come across those commercial calculations. “And he’d make his fortune. But you and I must thank God if we keep what we’ve got and leave it to our children.”\n\n“You’re married, I’ve heard?” said the landowner.\n\n“Yes,” Levin answered, with proud satisfaction. “Yes, it’s rather strange,” he went on. “So we live without making anything, as though we were ancient vestals set to keep in a fire.”\n\nThe landowner chuckled under his white mustaches.\n\n“There are some among us, too, like our friend Nikolay Ivanovitch, or Count Vronsky, that’s settled here lately, who try to carry on their husbandry as though it were a factory; but so far it leads to nothing but making away with capital on it.”\n\n“But why is it we don’t do like the merchants? Why don’t we cut down our parks for timber?” said Levin, returning to a thought that had struck him.\n\n“Why, as you said, to keep the fire in. Besides that’s not work for a nobleman. And our work as noblemen isn’t done here at the elections, but yonder, each in our corner. There’s a class instinct, too, of what one ought and oughtn’t to do. There’s the peasants, too, I wonder at them sometimes; any good peasant tries to take all the land he can. However bad the land is, he’ll work it. Without a return too. At a simple loss.”\n\n“Just as we do,” said Levin. “Very, very glad to have met you,” he added, seeing Sviazhsky approaching him.\n\n“And here we’ve met for the first time since we met at your place,” said the landowner to Sviazhsky, “and we’ve had a good talk too.”\n\n“Well, have you been attacking the new order of things?” said Sviazhsky with a smile.\n\n“That we’re bound to do.”\n\n“You’ve relieved your feelings?”\n\n# Chapter 30\n\nSviazhsky took Levin’s arm, and went with him to his own friends.\n\nThis time there was no avoiding Vronsky. He was standing with Stepan Arkadyevitch and Sergey Ivanovitch, and looking straight at Levin as he drew near.\n\n“Delighted! I believe I’ve had the pleasure of meeting you ... at Princess Shtcherbatskaya’s,” he said, giving Levin his hand.\n\n“Yes, I quite remember our meeting,” said Levin, and blushing crimson, he turned away immediately, and began talking to his brother.\n\nWith a slight smile Vronsky went on talking to Sviazhsky, obviously without the slightest inclination to enter into conversation with Levin. But Levin, as he talked to his brother, was continually looking round at Vronsky, trying to think of something to say to him to gloss over his rudeness.\n\n“What are we waiting for now?” asked Levin, looking at Sviazhsky and Vronsky.\n\n“For Snetkov. He has to refuse or to consent to stand,” answered Sviazhsky.\n\n“Well, and what has he done, consented or not?”\n\n“That’s the point, that he’s done neither,” said Vronsky.\n\n“And if he refuses, who will stand then?” asked Levin, looking at Vronsky.\n\n“Whoever chooses to,” said Sviazhsky.\n\n“Shall you?” asked Levin.\n\n“Certainly not I,” said Sviazhsky, looking confused, and turning an alarmed glance at the malignant gentleman, who was standing beside Sergey Ivanovitch.\n\n“Who then? Nevyedovsky?” said Levin, feeling he was putting his foot into it.\n\nBut this was worse still. Nevyedovsky and Sviazhsky were the two candidates.\n\n“I certainly shall not, under any circumstances,” answered the malignant gentleman.\n\nThis was Nevyedovsky himself. Sviazhsky introduced him to Levin.\n\n“Well, you find it exciting too?” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, winking at Vronsky. “It’s something like a race. One might bet on it.”\n\n“Yes, it is keenly exciting,” said Vronsky. “And once taking the thing up, one’s eager to see it through. It’s a fight!” he said, scowling and setting his powerful jaws.\n\n“What a capable fellow Sviazhsky is! Sees it all so clearly.”\n\n“Oh, yes!” Vronsky assented indifferently.\n\nA silence followed, during which Vronsky—since he had to look at something—looked at Levin, at his feet, at his uniform, then at his face, and noticing his gloomy eyes fixed upon him, he said, in order to say something:\n\n“How is it that you, living constantly in the country, are not a justice of the peace? You are not in the uniform of one.”\n\n“It’s because I consider that the justice of the peace is a silly institution,” Levin answered gloomily. He had been all the time looking for an opportunity to enter into conversation with Vronsky, so as to smooth over his rudeness at their first meeting.\n\n“I don’t think so, quite the contrary,” Vronsky said, with quiet surprise.\n\n“It’s a plaything,” Levin cut him short. “We don’t want justices of the peace. I’ve never had a single thing to do with them during eight years. And what I have had was decided wrongly by them. The justice of the peace is over thirty miles from me. For some matter of two roubles I should have to send a lawyer, who costs me fifteen.”\n\nAnd he related how a peasant had stolen some flour from the miller, and when the miller told him of it, had lodged a complaint for slander. All this was utterly uncalled for and stupid, and Levin felt it himself as he said it.\n\n“Oh, this is such an original fellow!” said Stepan Arkadyevitch with his most soothing, almond-oil smile. “But come along; I think they’re voting....”\n\nAnd they separated.\n\n“I can’t understand,” said Sergey Ivanovitch, who had observed his brother’s clumsiness, “I can’t understand how anyone can be so absolutely devoid of political tact. That’s where we Russians are so deficient. The marshal of the province is our opponent, and with him you’re ami cochon, and you beg him to stand. Count Vronsky, now ... I’m not making a friend of him; he’s asked me to dinner, and I’m not going; but he’s one of our side—why make an enemy of him? Then you ask Nevyedovsky if he’s going to stand. That’s not a thing to do.”\n\n“Oh, I don’t understand it at all! And it’s all such nonsense,” Levin answered gloomily.\n\n“You say it’s all such nonsense, but as soon as you have anything to do with it, you make a muddle.”\n\nLevin did not answer, and they walked together into the big room.\n\nThe marshal of the province, though he was vaguely conscious in the air of some trap being prepared for him, and though he had not been called upon by all to stand, had still made up his mind to stand. All was silence in the room. The secretary announced in a loud voice that the captain of the guards, Mihail Stepanovitch Snetkov, would now be balloted for as marshal of the province.\n\nThe district marshals walked carrying plates, on which were balls, from their tables to the high table, and the election began.\n\n“Put it in the right side,” whispered Stepan Arkadyevitch, as with his brother Levin followed the marshal of his district to the table. But Levin had forgotten by now the calculations that had been explained to him, and was afraid Stepan Arkadyevitch might be mistaken in saying “the right side.” Surely Snetkov was the enemy. As he went up, he held the ball in his right hand, but thinking he was wrong, just at the box he changed to the left hand, and undoubtedly put the ball to the left. An adept in the business, standing at the box and seeing by the mere action of the elbow where each put his ball, scowled with annoyance. It was no good for him to use his insight.\n\nEverything was still, and the counting of the balls was heard. Then a single voice rose and proclaimed the numbers for and against. The marshal had been voted for by a considerable majority. All was noise and eager movement towards the doors. Snetkov came in, and the nobles thronged round him, congratulating him.\n\n“Well, now is it over?” Levin asked Sergey Ivanovitch.\n\n“It’s only just beginning,” Sviazhsky said, replying for Sergey Ivanovitch with a smile. “Some other candidate may receive more votes than the marshal.”\n\nLevin had quite forgotten about that. Now he could only remember that there was some sort of trickery in it, but he was too bored to think what it was exactly. He felt depressed, and longed to get out of the crowd.\n\nAs no one was paying any attention to him, and no one apparently needed him, he quietly slipped away into the little room where the refreshments were, and again had a great sense of comfort when he saw the waiters. The little old waiter pressed him to have something, and Levin agreed. After eating a cutlet with beans and talking to the waiters of their former masters, Levin, not wishing to go back to the hall, where it was all so distasteful to him, proceeded to walk through the galleries. The galleries were full of fashionably dressed ladies, leaning over the balustrade and trying not to lose a single word of what was being said below. With the ladies were sitting and standing smart lawyers, high school teachers in spectacles, and officers. Everywhere they were talking of the election, and of how worried the marshal was, and how splendid the discussions had been. In one group Levin heard his brother’s praises. One lady was telling a lawyer:\n\n“How glad I am I heard Koznishev! It’s worth losing one’s dinner. He’s exquisite! So clear and distinct all of it! There’s not one of you in the law courts that speaks like that. The only one is Meidel, and he’s not so eloquent by a long way.”\n\nFinding a free place, Levin leaned over the balustrade and began looking and listening.\n\nAll the noblemen were sitting railed off behind barriers according to their districts. In the middle of the room stood a man in a uniform, who shouted in a loud, high voice:\n\n“As a candidate for the marshalship of the nobility of the province we call upon staff-captain Yevgeney Ivanovitch Apuhtin!” A dead silence followed, and then a weak old voice was heard: “Declined!”\n\n“We call upon the privy councilor Pyotr Petrovitch Bol,” the voice began again.\n\n“Declined!” a high boyish voice replied.\n\nAgain it began, and again “Declined.” And so it went on for about an hour. Levin, with his elbows on the balustrade, looked and listened. At first he wondered and wanted to know what it meant; then feeling sure that he could not make it out he began to be bored. Then recalling all the excitement and vindictiveness he had seen on all the faces, he felt sad; he made up his mind to go, and went downstairs. As he passed through the entry to the galleries he met a dejected high school boy walking up and down with tired-looking eyes. On the stairs he met a couple—a lady running quickly on her high heels and the jaunty deputy prosecutor.\n\n“I told you you weren’t late,” the deputy prosecutor was saying at the moment when Levin moved aside to let the lady pass.\n\nLevin was on the stairs to the way out, and was just feeling in his waistcoat pocket for the number of his overcoat, when the secretary overtook him.\n\n“This way, please, Konstantin Dmitrievitch; they are voting.”\n\nThe candidate who was being voted on was Nevyedovsky, who had so stoutly denied all idea of standing. Levin went up to the door of the room; it was locked. The secretary knocked, the door opened, and Levin was met by two red-faced gentlemen, who darted out.\n\n“I can’t stand any more of it,” said one red-faced gentleman.\n\nAfter them the face of the marshal of the province was poked out. His face was dreadful-looking from exhaustion and dismay.\n\n“I told you not to let anyone out!” he cried to the doorkeeper.\n\n“I let someone in, your excellency!”\n\n“Mercy on us!” and with a heavy sigh the marshal of the province walked with downcast head to the high table in the middle of the room, his legs staggering in his white trousers.\n\nNevyedovsky had scored a higher majority, as they had planned, and he was the new marshal of the province. Many people were amused, many were pleased and happy, many were in ecstasies, many were disgusted and unhappy. The former marshal of the province was in a state of despair, which he could not conceal. When Nevyedovsky went out of the room, the crowd thronged round him and followed him enthusiastically, just as they had followed the governor who had opened the meetings, and just as they had followed Snetkov when he was elected.\n\n# Chapter 31\n\nThe newly elected marshal and many of the successful party dined that day with Vronsky.\n\nVronsky had come to the elections partly because he was bored in the country and wanted to show Anna his right to independence, and also to repay Sviazhsky by his support at the election for all the trouble he had taken for Vronsky at the district council election, but chiefly in order strictly to perform all those duties of a nobleman and landowner which he had taken upon himself. But he had not in the least expected that the election would so interest him, so keenly excite him, and that he would be so good at this kind of thing. He was quite a new man in the circle of the nobility of the province, but his success was unmistakable, and he was not wrong in supposing that he had already obtained a certain influence. This influence was due to his wealth and reputation, the capital house in the town lent him by his old friend Shirkov, who had a post in the department of finances and was director of a flourishing bank in Kashin; the excellent cook Vronsky had brought from the country, and his friendship with the governor, who was a schoolfellow of Vronsky’s—a schoolfellow he had patronized and protected indeed. But what contributed more than all to his success was his direct, equable manner with everyone, which very quickly made the majority of the noblemen reverse the current opinion of his supposed haughtiness. He was himself conscious that, except that whimsical gentleman married to Kitty Shtcherbatskaya, who had à propos de bottes poured out a stream of irrelevant absurdities with such spiteful fury, every nobleman with whom he had made acquaintance had become his adherent. He saw clearly, and other people recognized it, too, that he had done a great deal to secure the success of Nevyedovsky. And now at his own table, celebrating Nevyedovsky’s election, he was experiencing an agreeable sense of triumph over the success of his candidate. The election itself had so fascinated him that, if he could succeed in getting married during the next three years, he began to think of standing himself—much as after winning a race ridden by a jockey, he had longed to ride a race himself.\n\nToday he was celebrating the success of his jockey. Vronsky sat at the head of the table, on his right hand sat the young governor, a general of high rank. To all the rest he was the chief man in the province, who had solemnly opened the elections with his speech, and aroused a feeling of respect and even of awe in many people, as Vronsky saw; to Vronsky he was little Katka Maslov—that had been his nickname in the Pages’ Corps—whom he felt to be shy and tried to mettre à son aise. On the left hand sat Nevyedovsky with his youthful, stubborn, and malignant face. With him Vronsky was simple and deferential.\n\nSviazhsky took his failure very light-heartedly. It was indeed no failure in his eyes, as he said himself, turning, glass in hand, to Nevyedovsky; they could not have found a better representative of the new movement, which the nobility ought to follow. And so every honest person, as he said, was on the side of today’s success and was rejoicing over it.\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch was glad, too, that he was having a good time, and that everyone was pleased. The episode of the elections served as a good occasion for a capital dinner. Sviazhsky comically imitated the tearful discourse of the marshal, and observed, addressing Nevyedovsky, that his excellency would have to select another more complicated method of auditing the accounts than tears. Another nobleman jocosely described how footmen in stockings had been ordered for the marshal’s ball, and how now they would have to be sent back unless the new marshal would give a ball with footmen in stockings.\n\nContinually during dinner they said of Nevyedovsky: “our marshal,” and “your excellency.”\n\nThis was said with the same pleasure with which a bride is called “Madame” and her husband’s name. Nevyedovsky affected to be not merely indifferent but scornful of this appellation, but it was obvious that he was highly delighted, and had to keep a curb on himself not to betray the triumph which was unsuitable to their new liberal tone.\n\nAfter dinner several telegrams were sent to people interested in the result of the election. And Stepan Arkadyevitch, who was in high good humor, sent Darya Alexandrovna a telegram: “Nevyedovsky elected by twenty votes. Congratulations. Tell people.” He dictated it aloud, saying: “We must let them share our rejoicing.” Darya Alexandrovna, getting the message, simply sighed over the rouble wasted on it, and understood that it was an after-dinner affair. She knew Stiva had a weakness after dining for faire jouer le télégraphe.\n\nEverything, together with the excellent dinner and the wine, not from Russian merchants, but imported direct from abroad, was extremely dignified, simple, and enjoyable. The party—some twenty—had been selected by Sviazhsky from among the more active new liberals, all of the same way of thinking, who were at the same time clever and well bred. They drank, also half in jest, to the health of the new marshal of the province, of the governor, of the bank director, and of “our amiable host.”\n\nVronsky was satisfied. He had never expected to find so pleasant a tone in the provinces.\n\nTowards the end of dinner it was still more lively. The governor asked Vronsky to come to a concert for the benefit of the Servians which his wife, who was anxious to make his acquaintance, had been getting up.\n\n“There’ll be a ball, and you’ll see the belle of the province. Worth seeing, really.”\n\n“Not in my line,” Vronsky answered. He liked that English phrase. But he smiled, and promised to come.\n\nBefore they rose from the table, when all of them were smoking, Vronsky’s valet went up to him with a letter on a tray.\n\n“From Vozdvizhenskoe by special messenger,” he said with a significant expression.\n\n“Astonishing! how like he is to the deputy prosecutor Sventitsky,” said one of the guests in French of the valet, while Vronsky, frowning, read the letter.\n\nThe letter was from Anna. Before he read the letter, he knew its contents. Expecting the elections to be over in five days, he had promised to be back on Friday. Today was Saturday, and he knew that the letter contained reproaches for not being back at the time fixed. The letter he had sent the previous evening had probably not reached her yet.\n\nThe letter was what he had expected, but the form of it was unexpected, and particularly disagreeable to him. “Annie is very ill, the doctor says it may be inflammation. I am losing my head all alone. Princess Varvara is no help, but a hindrance. I expected you the day before yesterday, and yesterday, and now I am sending to find out where you are and what you are doing. I wanted to come myself, but thought better of it, knowing you would dislike it. Send some answer, that I may know what to do.”\n\nThe child ill, yet she had thought of coming herself. Their daughter ill, and this hostile tone.\n\nThe innocent festivities over the election, and this gloomy, burdensome love to which he had to return struck Vronsky by their contrast. But he had to go, and by the first train that night he set off home.\n\n# Chapter 32\n\nBefore Vronsky’s departure for the elections, Anna had reflected that the scenes constantly repeated between them each time he left home, might only make him cold to her instead of attaching him to her, and resolved to do all she could to control herself so as to bear the parting with composure. But the cold, severe glance with which he had looked at her when he came to tell her he was going had wounded her, and before he had started her peace of mind was destroyed.\n\nIn solitude afterwards, thinking over that glance which had expressed his right to freedom, she came, as she always did, to the same point—the sense of her own humiliation. “He has the right to go away when and where he chooses. Not simply to go away, but to leave me. He has every right, and I have none. But knowing that, he ought not to do it. What has he done, though?... He looked at me with a cold, severe expression. Of course that is something indefinable, impalpable, but it has never been so before, and that glance means a great deal,” she thought. “That glance shows the beginning of indifference.”\n\nAnd though she felt sure that a coldness was beginning, there was nothing she could do, she could not in any way alter her relations to him. Just as before, only by love and by charm could she keep him. And so, just as before, only by occupation in the day, by morphine at night, could she stifle the fearful thought of what would be if he ceased to love her. It is true there was still one means; not to keep him—for that she wanted nothing more than his love—but to be nearer to him, to be in such a position that he would not leave her. That means was divorce and marriage. And she began to long for that, and made up her mind to agree to it the first time he or Stiva approached her on the subject.\n\nAbsorbed in such thoughts, she passed five days without him, the five days that he was to be at the elections.\n\nWalks, conversation with Princess Varvara, visits to the hospital, and, most of all, reading—reading of one book after another—filled up her time. But on the sixth day, when the coachman came back without him, she felt that now she was utterly incapable of stifling the thought of him and of what he was doing there, just at that time her little girl was taken ill. Anna began to look after her, but even that did not distract her mind, especially as the illness was not serious. However hard she tried, she could not love this little child, and to feign love was beyond her powers. Towards the evening of that day, still alone, Anna was in such a panic about him that she decided to start for the town, but on second thoughts wrote him the contradictory letter that Vronsky received, and without reading it through, sent it off by a special messenger. The next morning she received his letter and regretted her own. She dreaded a repetition of the severe look he had flung at her at parting, especially when he knew that the baby was not dangerously ill. But still she was glad she had written to him. At this moment Anna was positively admitting to herself that she was a burden to him, that he would relinquish his freedom regretfully to return to her, and in spite of that she was glad he was coming. Let him weary of her, but he would be here with her, so that she would see him, would know of every action he took.\n\nShe was sitting in the drawing-room near a lamp, with a new volume of Taine, and as she read, listening to the sound of the wind outside, and every minute expecting the carriage to arrive. Several times she had fancied she heard the sound of wheels, but she had been mistaken. At last she heard not the sound of wheels, but the coachman’s shout and the dull rumble in the covered entry. Even Princess Varvara, playing patience, confirmed this, and Anna, flushing hotly, got up; but instead of going down, as she had done twice before, she stood still. She suddenly felt ashamed of her duplicity, but even more she dreaded how he might meet her. All feeling of wounded pride had passed now; she was only afraid of the expression of his displeasure. She remembered that her child had been perfectly well again for the last two days. She felt positively vexed with her for getting better from the very moment her letter was sent off. Then she thought of him, that he was here, all of him, with his hands, his eyes. She heard his voice. And forgetting everything, she ran joyfully to meet him.\n\n“Well, how is Annie?” he said timidly from below, looking up to Anna as she ran down to him.\n\nHe was sitting on a chair, and a footman was pulling off his warm over-boot.\n\n“Oh, she is better.”\n\n“And you?” he said, shaking himself.\n\nShe took his hand in both of hers, and drew it to her waist, never taking her eyes off him.\n\n“Well, I’m glad,” he said, coldly scanning her, her hair, her dress, which he knew she had put on for him. All was charming, but how many times it had charmed him! And the stern, stony expression that she so dreaded settled upon his face.\n\n“Well, I’m glad. And are you well?” he said, wiping his damp beard with his handkerchief and kissing her hand.\n\n“Never mind,” she thought, “only let him be here, and so long as he’s here he cannot, he dare not, cease to love me.”\n\nThe evening was spent happily and gaily in the presence of Princess Varvara, who complained to him that Anna had been taking morphine in his absence.\n\n“What am I to do? I couldn’t sleep.... My thoughts prevented me. When he’s here I never take it—hardly ever.”\n\nHe told her about the election, and Anna knew how by adroit questions to bring him to what gave him most pleasure—his own success. She told him of everything that interested him at home; and all that she told him was of the most cheerful description.\n\nBut late in the evening, when they were alone, Anna, seeing that she had regained complete possession of him, wanted to erase the painful impression of the glance he had given her for her letter. She said:\n\n“Tell me frankly, you were vexed at getting my letter, and you didn’t believe me?”\n\nAs soon as she had said it, she felt that however warm his feelings were to her, he had not forgiven her for that.\n\n“Yes,” he said, “the letter was so strange. First, Annie ill, and then you thought of coming yourself.”\n\n“It was all the truth.”\n\n“Oh, I don’t doubt it.”\n\n“Yes, you do doubt it. You are vexed, I see.”\n\n“Not for one moment. I’m only vexed, that’s true, that you seem somehow unwilling to admit that there are duties....”\n\n“The duty of going to a concert....”\n\n“But we won’t talk about it,” he said.\n\n“Why not talk about it?” she said.\n\n“I only meant to say that matters of real importance may turn up. Now, for instance, I shall have to go to Moscow to arrange about the house.... Oh, Anna, why are you so irritable? Don’t you know that I can’t live without you?”\n\n“If so,” said Anna, her voice suddenly changing, “it means that you are sick of this life.... Yes, you will come for a day and go away, as men do....”\n\n“Anna, that’s cruel. I am ready to give up my whole life.”\n\nBut she did not hear him.\n\n“If you go to Moscow, I will go too. I will not stay here. Either we must separate or else live together.”\n\n“Why, you know, that’s my one desire. But for that....”\n\n“We must get a divorce. I will write to him. I see I cannot go on like this.... But I will come with you to Moscow.”\n\n“You talk as if you were threatening me. But I desire nothing so much as never to be parted from you,” said Vronsky, smiling.\n\nBut as he said these words there gleamed in his eyes not merely a cold look, but the vindictive look of a man persecuted and made cruel.\n\nShe saw the look and correctly divined its meaning.\n\n“If so, it’s a calamity!” that glance told her. It was a moment’s impression, but she never forgot it.\n\nAnna wrote to her husband asking him about a divorce, and towards the end of November, taking leave of Princess Varvara, who wanted to go to Petersburg, she went with Vronsky to Moscow. Expecting every day an answer from Alexey Alexandrovitch, and after that the divorce, they now established themselves together like married people.\n\n# PART SEVEN Chapter 1\n\nThe Levins had been three months in Moscow. The date had long passed on which, according to the most trustworthy calculations of people learned in such matters, Kitty should have been confined. But she was still about, and there was nothing to show that her time was any nearer than two months ago. The doctor, the monthly nurse, and Dolly and her mother, and most of all Levin, who could not think of the approaching event without terror, began to be impatient and uneasy. Kitty was the only person who felt perfectly calm and happy.\n\nShe was distinctly conscious now of the birth of a new feeling of love for the future child, for her to some extent actually existing already, and she brooded blissfully over this feeling. He was not by now altogether a part of herself, but sometimes lived his own life independently of her. Often this separate being gave her pain, but at the same time she wanted to laugh with a strange new joy.\n\nAll the people she loved were with her, and all were so good to her, so attentively caring for her, so entirely pleasant was everything presented to her, that if she had not known and felt that it must all soon be over, she could not have wished for a better and pleasanter life. The only thing that spoiled the charm of this manner of life was that her husband was not here as she loved him to be, and as he was in the country.\n\nShe liked his serene, friendly, and hospitable manner in the country. In the town he seemed continually uneasy and on his guard, as though he were afraid someone would be rude to him, and still more to her. At home in the country, knowing himself distinctly to be in his right place, he was never in haste to be off elsewhere. He was never unoccupied. Here in town he was in a continual hurry, as though afraid of missing something, and yet he had nothing to do. And she felt sorry for him. To others, she knew, he did not appear an object of pity. On the contrary, when Kitty looked at him in society, as one sometimes looks at those one loves, trying to see him as if he were a stranger, so as to catch the impression he must make on others, she saw with a panic even of jealous fear that he was far indeed from being a pitiable figure, that he was very attractive with his fine breeding, his rather old-fashioned, reserved courtesy with women, his powerful figure, and striking, as she thought, and expressive face. But she saw him not from without, but from within; she saw that here he was not himself; that was the only way she could define his condition to herself. Sometimes she inwardly reproached him for his inability to live in the town; sometimes she recognized that it was really hard for him to order his life here so that he could be satisfied with it.\n\nWhat had he to do, indeed? He did not care for cards; he did not go to a club. Spending the time with jovial gentlemen of Oblonsky’s type—she knew now what that meant ... it meant drinking and going somewhere after drinking. She could not think without horror of where men went on such occasions. Was he to go into society? But she knew he could only find satisfaction in that if he took pleasure in the society of young women, and that she could not wish for. Should he stay at home with her, her mother and her sisters? But much as she liked and enjoyed their conversations forever on the same subjects—“Aline-Nadine,” as the old prince called the sisters’ talks—she knew it must bore him. What was there left for him to do? To go on writing at his book he had indeed attempted, and at first he used to go to the library and make extracts and look up references for his book. But, as he told her, the more he did nothing, the less time he had to do anything. And besides, he complained that he had talked too much about his book here, and that consequently all his ideas about it were muddled and had lost their interest for him.\n\nOne advantage in this town life was that quarrels hardly ever happened between them here in town. Whether it was that their conditions were different, or that they had both become more careful and sensible in that respect, they had no quarrels in Moscow from jealousy, which they had so dreaded when they moved from the country.\n\nOne event, an event of great importance to both from that point of view, did indeed happen—that was Kitty’s meeting with Vronsky.\n\nThe old Princess Marya Borissovna, Kitty’s godmother, who had always been very fond of her, had insisted on seeing her. Kitty, though she did not go into society at all on account of her condition, went with her father to see the venerable old lady, and there met Vronsky.\n\nThe only thing Kitty could reproach herself for at this meeting was that at the instant when she recognized in his civilian dress the features once so familiar to her, her breath failed her, the blood rushed to her heart, and a vivid blush—she felt it—overspread her face. But this lasted only a few seconds. Before her father, who purposely began talking in a loud voice to Vronsky, had finished, she was perfectly ready to look at Vronsky, to speak to him, if necessary, exactly as she spoke to Princess Marya Borissovna, and more than that, to do so in such a way that everything to the faintest intonation and smile would have been approved by her husband, whose unseen presence she seemed to feel about her at that instant.\n\nShe said a few words to him, even smiled serenely at his joke about the elections, which he called “our parliament.” (She had to smile to show she saw the joke.) But she turned away immediately to Princess Marya Borissovna, and did not once glance at him till he got up to go; then she looked at him, but evidently only because it would be uncivil not to look at a man when he is saying good-bye.\n\nShe was grateful to her father for saying nothing to her about their meeting Vronsky, but she saw by his special warmth to her after the visit during their usual walk that he was pleased with her. She was pleased with herself. She had not expected she would have had the power, while keeping somewhere in the bottom of her heart all the memories of her old feeling for Vronsky, not only to seem but to be perfectly indifferent and composed with him.\n\nLevin flushed a great deal more than she when she told him she had met Vronsky at Princess Marya Borissovna’s. It was very hard for her to tell him this, but still harder to go on speaking of the details of the meeting, as he did not question her, but simply gazed at her with a frown.\n\n“I am very sorry you weren’t there,” she said. “Not that you weren’t in the room ... I couldn’t have been so natural in your presence ... I am blushing now much more, much, much more,” she said, blushing till the tears came into her eyes. “But that you couldn’t see through a crack.”\n\nThe truthful eyes told Levin that she was satisfied with herself, and in spite of her blushing he was quickly reassured and began questioning her, which was all she wanted. When he had heard everything, even to the detail that for the first second she could not help flushing, but that afterwards she was just as direct and as much at her ease as with any chance acquaintance, Levin was quite happy again and said he was glad of it, and would not now behave as stupidly as he had done at the election, but would try the first time he met Vronsky to be as friendly as possible.\n\n“It’s so wretched to feel that there’s a man almost an enemy whom it’s painful to meet,” said Levin. “I’m very, very glad.”\n\n# Chapter 2\n\n“Go, please, go then and call on the Bols,” Kitty said to her husband, when he came in to see her at eleven o’clock before going out. “I know you are dining at the club; papa put down your name. But what are you going to do in the morning?”\n\n“I am only going to Katavasov,” answered Levin.\n\n“Why so early?”\n\n“He promised to introduce me to Metrov. I wanted to talk to him about my work. He’s a distinguished scientific man from Petersburg,” said Levin.\n\n“Yes; wasn’t it his article you were praising so? Well, and after that?” said Kitty.\n\n“I shall go to the court, perhaps, about my sister’s business.”\n\n“And the concert?” she queried.\n\n“I shan’t go there all alone.”\n\n“No? do go; there are going to be some new things.... That interested you so. I should certainly go.”\n\n“Well, anyway, I shall come home before dinner,” he said, looking at his watch.\n\n“Put on your frock coat, so that you can go straight to call on Countess Bola.”\n\n“But is it absolutely necessary?”\n\n“Oh, absolutely! He has been to see us. Come, what is it? You go in, sit down, talk for five minutes of the weather, get up and go away.”\n\n“Oh, you wouldn’t believe it! I’ve got so out of the way of all this that it makes me feel positively ashamed. It’s such a horrible thing to do! A complete outsider walks in, sits down, stays on with nothing to do, wastes their time and worries himself, and walks away!”\n\nKitty laughed.\n\n“Why, I suppose you used to pay calls before you were married, didn’t you?”\n\n“Yes, I did, but I always felt ashamed, and now I’m so out of the way of it that, by Jove! I’d sooner go two days running without my dinner than pay this call! One’s so ashamed! I feel all the while that they’re annoyed, that they’re saying, ‘What has he come for?’”\n\n“No, they won’t. I’ll answer for that,” said Kitty, looking into his face with a laugh. She took his hand. “Well, good-bye.... Do go, please.”\n\nHe was just going out after kissing his wife’s hand, when she stopped him.\n\n“Kostya, do you know I’ve only fifty roubles left?”\n\n“Oh, all right, I’ll go to the bank and get some. How much?” he said, with the expression of dissatisfaction she knew so well.\n\n“No, wait a minute.” She held his hand. “Let’s talk about it, it worries me. I seem to spend nothing unnecessary, but money seems to fly away simply. We don’t manage well, somehow.”\n\n“Oh, it’s all right,” he said with a little cough, looking at her from under his brows.\n\nThat cough she knew well. It was a sign of intense dissatisfaction, not with her, but with himself. He certainly was displeased not at so much money being spent, but at being reminded of what he, knowing something was unsatisfactory, wanted to forget.\n\n“I have told Sokolov to sell the wheat, and to borrow an advance on the mill. We shall have money enough in any case.”\n\n“Yes, but I’m afraid that altogether....”\n\n“Oh, it’s all right, all right,” he repeated. “Well, good-bye, darling.”\n\n“No, I’m really sorry sometimes that I listened to mamma. How nice it would have been in the country! As it is, I’m worrying you all, and we’re wasting our money.”\n\n“Not at all, not at all. Not once since I’ve been married have I said that things could have been better than they are....”\n\n“Truly?” she said, looking into his eyes.\n\nHe had said it without thinking, simply to console her. But when he glanced at her and saw those sweet truthful eyes fastened questioningly on him, he repeated it with his whole heart. “I was positively forgetting her,” he thought. And he remembered what was before them, so soon to come.\n\n“Will it be soon? How do you feel?” he whispered, taking her two hands.\n\n“I have so often thought so, that now I don’t think about it or know anything about it.”\n\n“And you’re not frightened?”\n\nShe smiled contemptuously.\n\n“Not the least little bit,” she said.\n\n“Well, if anything happens, I shall be at Katavasov’s.”\n\n“No, nothing will happen, and don’t think about it. I’m going for a walk on the boulevard with papa. We’re going to see Dolly. I shall expect you before dinner. Oh, yes! Do you know that Dolly’s position is becoming utterly impossible? She’s in debt all round; she hasn’t a penny. We were talking yesterday with mamma and Arseny” (this was her sister’s husband Lvov), “and we determined to send you with him to talk to Stiva. It’s really unbearable. One can’t speak to papa about it.... But if you and he....”\n\n“Why, what can we do?” said Levin.\n\n“You’ll be at Arseny’s, anyway; talk to him, he will tell what we decided.”\n\n“Oh, I agree to everything Arseny thinks beforehand. I’ll go and see him. By the way, if I do go to the concert, I’ll go with Natalia. Well, good-bye.”\n\nOn the steps Levin was stopped by his old servant Kouzma, who had been with him before his marriage, and now looked after their household in town.\n\n“Beauty” (that was the left shaft-horse brought up from the country) “has been badly shod and is quite lame,” he said. “What does your honor wish to be done?”\n\nDuring the first part of their stay in Moscow, Levin had used his own horses brought up from the country. He had tried to arrange this part of their expenses in the best and cheapest way possible; but it appeared that their own horses came dearer than hired horses, and they still hired too.\n\n“Send for the veterinary, there may be a bruise.”\n\n“And for Katerina Alexandrovna?” asked Kouzma.\n\nLevin was not by now struck as he had been at first by the fact that to get from one end of Moscow to the other he had to have two powerful horses put into a heavy carriage, to take the carriage three miles through the snowy slush and to keep it standing there four hours, paying five roubles every time.\n\nNow it seemed quite natural.\n\n“Hire a pair for our carriage from the jobmaster,” said he.\n\n“Yes, sir.”\n\nAnd so, simply and easily, thanks to the facilities of town life, Levin settled a question which, in the country, would have called for so much personal trouble and exertion, and going out onto the steps, he called a sledge, sat down, and drove to Nikitsky. On the way he thought no more of money, but mused on the introduction that awaited him to the Petersburg savant, a writer on sociology, and what he would say to him about his book.\n\nOnly during the first days of his stay in Moscow Levin had been struck by the expenditure, strange to one living in the country, unproductive but inevitable, that was expected of him on every side. But by now he had grown used to it. That had happened to him in this matter which is said to happen to drunkards—the first glass sticks in the throat, the second flies down like a hawk, but after the third they’re like tiny little birds. When Levin had changed his first hundred-rouble note to pay for liveries for his footmen and hall-porter he could not help reflecting that these liveries were of no use to anyone—but they were indubitably necessary, to judge by the amazement of the princess and Kitty when he suggested that they might do without liveries,—that these liveries would cost the wages of two laborers for the summer, that is, would pay for about three hundred working days from Easter to Ash Wednesday, and each a day of hard work from early morning to late evening—and that hundred-rouble note did stick in his throat. But the next note, changed to pay for providing a dinner for their relations, that cost twenty-eight roubles, though it did excite in Levin the reflection that twenty-eight roubles meant nine measures of oats, which men would with groans and sweat have reaped and bound and thrashed and winnowed and sifted and sown,—this next one he parted with more easily. And now the notes he changed no longer aroused such reflections, and they flew off like little birds. Whether the labor devoted to obtaining the money corresponded to the pleasure given by what was bought with it, was a consideration he had long ago dismissed. His business calculation that there was a certain price below which he could not sell certain grain was forgotten too. The rye, for the price of which he had so long held out, had been sold for fifty kopecks a measure cheaper than it had been fetching a month ago. Even the consideration that with such an expenditure he could not go on living for a year without debt, that even had no force. Only one thing was essential: to have money in the bank, without inquiring where it came from, so as to know that one had the wherewithal to buy meat for tomorrow. And this condition had hitherto been fulfilled; he had always had the money in the bank. But now the money in the bank had gone, and he could not quite tell where to get the next installment. And this it was which, at the moment when Kitty had mentioned money, had disturbed him; but he had no time to think about it. He drove off, thinking of Katavasov and the meeting with Metrov that was before him.\n\n# Chapter 3\n\nLevin had on this visit to town seen a great deal of his old friend at the university, Professor Katavasov, whom he had not seen since his marriage. He liked in Katavasov the clearness and simplicity of his conception of life. Levin thought that the clearness of Katavasov’s conception of life was due to the poverty of his nature; Katavasov thought that the disconnectedness of Levin’s ideas was due to his lack of intellectual discipline; but Levin enjoyed Katavasov’s clearness, and Katavasov enjoyed the abundance of Levin’s untrained ideas, and they liked to meet and to discuss.\n\nLevin had read Katavasov some parts of his book, and he had liked them. On the previous day Katavasov had met Levin at a public lecture and told him that the celebrated Metrov, whose article Levin had so much liked, was in Moscow, that he had been much interested by what Katavasov had told him about Levin’s work, and that he was coming to see him tomorrow at eleven, and would be very glad to make Levin’s acquaintance.\n\n“You’re positively a reformed character, I’m glad to see,” said Katavasov, meeting Levin in the little drawing-room. “I heard the bell and thought: Impossible that it can be he at the exact time!... Well, what do you say to the Montenegrins now? They’re a race of warriors.”\n\n“Why, what’s happened?” asked Levin.\n\nKatavasov in a few words told him the last piece of news from the war, and going into his study, introduced Levin to a short, thick-set man of pleasant appearance. This was Metrov. The conversation touched for a brief space on politics and on how recent events were looked at in the higher spheres in Petersburg. Metrov repeated a saying that had reached him through a most trustworthy source, reported as having been uttered on this subject by the Tsar and one of the ministers. Katavasov had heard also on excellent authority that the Tsar had said something quite different. Levin tried to imagine circumstances in which both sayings might have been uttered, and the conversation on that topic dropped.\n\n“Yes, here he’s written almost a book on the natural conditions of the laborer in relation to the land,” said Katavasov; “I’m not a specialist, but I, as a natural science man, was pleased at his not taking mankind as something outside biological laws; but, on the contrary, seeing his dependence on his surroundings, and in that dependence seeking the laws of his development.”\n\n“That’s very interesting,” said Metrov.\n\n“What I began precisely was to write a book on agriculture; but studying the chief instrument of agriculture, the laborer,” said Levin, reddening, “I could not help coming to quite unexpected results.”\n\nAnd Levin began carefully, as it were, feeling his ground, to expound his views. He knew Metrov had written an article against the generally accepted theory of political economy, but to what extent he could reckon on his sympathy with his own new views he did not know and could not guess from the clever and serene face of the learned man.\n\n“But in what do you see the special characteristics of the Russian laborer?” said Metrov; “in his biological characteristics, so to speak, or in the condition in which he is placed?”\n\nLevin saw that there was an idea underlying this question with which he did not agree. But he went on explaining his own idea that the Russian laborer has a quite special view of the land, different from that of other people; and to support this proposition he made haste to add that in his opinion this attitude of the Russian peasant was due to the consciousness of his vocation to people vast unoccupied expanses in the East.\n\n“One may easily be led into error in basing any conclusion on the general vocation of a people,” said Metrov, interrupting Levin. “The condition of the laborer will always depend on his relation to the land and to capital.”\n\nAnd without letting Levin finish explaining his idea, Metrov began expounding to him the special point of his own theory.\n\nIn what the point of his theory lay, Levin did not understand, because he did not take the trouble to understand. He saw that Metrov, like other people, in spite of his own article, in which he had attacked the current theory of political economy, looked at the position of the Russian peasant simply from the point of view of capital, wages, and rent. He would indeed have been obliged to admit that in the eastern—much the larger—part of Russia rent was as yet nil, that for nine-tenths of the eighty millions of the Russian peasants wages took the form simply of food provided for themselves, and that capital does not so far exist except in the form of the most primitive tools. Yet it was only from that point of view that he considered every laborer, though in many points he differed from the economists and had his own theory of the wage-fund, which he expounded to Levin.\n\nLevin listened reluctantly, and at first made objections. He would have liked to interrupt Metrov, to explain his own thought, which in his opinion would have rendered further exposition of Metrov’s theories superfluous. But later on, feeling convinced that they looked at the matter so differently, that they could never understand one another, he did not even oppose his statements, but simply listened. Although what Metrov was saying was by now utterly devoid of interest for him, he yet experienced a certain satisfaction in listening to him. It flattered his vanity that such a learned man should explain his ideas to him so eagerly, with such intensity and confidence in Levin’s understanding of the subject, sometimes with a mere hint referring him to a whole aspect of the subject. He put this down to his own credit, unaware that Metrov, who had already discussed his theory over and over again with all his intimate friends, talked of it with special eagerness to every new person, and in general was eager to talk to anyone of any subject that interested him, even if still obscure to himself.\n\n“We are late though,” said Katavasov, looking at his watch directly Metrov had finished his discourse.\n\n“Yes, there’s a meeting of the Society of Amateurs today in commemoration of the jubilee of Svintitch,” said Katavasov in answer to Levin’s inquiry. “Pyotr Ivanovitch and I were going. I’ve promised to deliver an address on his labors in zoology. Come along with us, it’s very interesting.”\n\n“Yes, and indeed it’s time to start,” said Metrov. “Come with us, and from there, if you care to, come to my place. I should very much like to hear your work.”\n\n“Oh, no! It’s no good yet, it’s unfinished. But I shall be very glad to go to the meeting.”\n\n“I say, friends, have you heard? He has handed in the separate report,” Katavasov called from the other room, where he was putting on his frock coat.\n\nAnd a conversation sprang up upon the university question, which was a very important event that winter in Moscow. Three old professors in the council had not accepted the opinion of the younger professors. The young ones had registered a separate resolution. This, in the judgment of some people, was monstrous, in the judgment of others it was the simplest and most just thing to do, and the professors were split up into two parties.\n\nOne party, to which Katavasov belonged, saw in the opposite party a scoundrelly betrayal and treachery, while the opposite party saw in them childishness and lack of respect for the authorities. Levin, though he did not belong to the university, had several times already during his stay in Moscow heard and talked about this matter, and had his own opinion on the subject. He took part in the conversation that was continued in the street, as they all three walked to the buildings of the old university.\n\nThe meeting had already begun. Round the cloth-covered table, at which Katavasov and Metrov seated themselves, there were some half-dozen persons, and one of these was bending close over a manuscript, reading something aloud. Levin sat down in one of the empty chairs that were standing round the table, and in a whisper asked a student sitting near what was being read. The student, eyeing Levin with displeasure, said:\n\n“Biography.”\n\nThough Levin was not interested in the biography, he could not help listening, and learned some new and interesting facts about the life of the distinguished man of science.\n\nWhen the reader had finished, the chairman thanked him and read some verses of the poet Ment sent him on the jubilee, and said a few words by way of thanks to the poet. Then Katavasov in his loud, ringing voice read his address on the scientific labors of the man whose jubilee was being kept.\n\nWhen Katavasov had finished, Levin looked at his watch, saw it was past one, and thought that there would not be time before the concert to read Metrov his book, and indeed, he did not now care to do so. During the reading he had thought over their conversation. He saw distinctly now that though Metrov’s ideas might perhaps have value, his own ideas had a value too, and their ideas could only be made clear and lead to something if each worked separately in his chosen path, and that nothing would be gained by putting their ideas together. And having made up his mind to refuse Metrov’s invitation, Levin went up to him at the end of the meeting. Metrov introduced Levin to the chairman, with whom he was talking of the political news. Metrov told the chairman what he had already told Levin, and Levin made the same remarks on his news that he had already made that morning, but for the sake of variety he expressed also a new opinion which had only just struck him. After that the conversation turned again on the university question. As Levin had already heard it all, he made haste to tell Metrov that he was sorry he could not take advantage of his invitation, took leave, and drove to Lvov’s.\n\n# Chapter 4\n\nLvov, the husband of Natalia, Kitty’s sister, had spent all his life in foreign capitals, where he had been educated, and had been in the diplomatic service.\n\nDuring the previous year he had left the diplomatic service, not owing to any “unpleasantness” (he never had any “unpleasantness” with anyone), and was transferred to the department of the court of the palace in Moscow, in order to give his two boys the best education possible.\n\nIn spite of the striking contrast in their habits and views and the fact that Lvov was older than Levin, they had seen a great deal of one another that winter, and had taken a great liking to each other.\n\nLvov was at home, and Levin went in to him unannounced.\n\nLvov, in a house coat with a belt and in chamois leather shoes, was sitting in an armchair, and with a pince-nez with blue glasses he was reading a book that stood on a reading desk, while in his beautiful hand he held a half-burned cigarette daintily away from him.\n\nHis handsome, delicate, and still youthful-looking face, to which his curly, glistening silvery hair gave a still more aristocratic air, lighted up with a smile when he saw Levin.\n\n“Capital! I was meaning to send to you. How’s Kitty? Sit here, it’s more comfortable.” He got up and pushed up a rocking chair. “Have you read the last circular in the Journal de St. Pétersbourg? I think it’s excellent,” he said, with a slight French accent.\n\nLevin told him what he had heard from Katavasov was being said in Petersburg, and after talking a little about politics, he told him of his interview with Metrov, and the learned society’s meeting. To Lvov it was very interesting.\n\n“That’s what I envy you, that you are able to mix in these interesting scientific circles,” he said. And as he talked, he passed as usual into French, which was easier to him. “It’s true I haven’t the time for it. My official work and the children leave me no time; and then I’m not ashamed to own that my education has been too defective.”\n\n“That I don’t believe,” said Levin with a smile, feeling, as he always did, touched at Lvov’s low opinion of himself, which was not in the least put on from a desire to seem or to be modest, but was absolutely sincere.\n\n“Oh, yes, indeed! I feel now how badly educated I am. To educate my children I positively have to look up a great deal, and in fact simply to study myself. For it’s not enough to have teachers, there must be someone to look after them, just as on your land you want laborers and an overseer. See what I’m reading”—he pointed to Buslaev’s Grammar on the desk—“it’s expected of Misha, and it’s so difficult.... Come, explain to me.... Here he says....”\n\nLevin tried to explain to him that it couldn’t be understood, but that it had to be taught; but Lvov would not agree with him.\n\n“Oh, you’re laughing at it!”\n\n“On the contrary, you can’t imagine how, when I look at you, I’m always learning the task that lies before me, that is the education of one’s children.”\n\n“Well, there’s nothing for you to learn,” said Lvov.\n\n“All I know,” said Levin, “is that I have never seen better brought-up children than yours, and I wouldn’t wish for children better than yours.”\n\nLvov visibly tried to restrain the expression of his delight, but he was positively radiant with smiles.\n\n“If only they’re better than I! That’s all I desire. You don’t know yet all the work,” he said, “with boys who’ve been left like mine to run wild abroad.”\n\n“You’ll catch all that up. They’re such clever children. The great thing is the education of character. That’s what I learn when I look at your children.”\n\n“You talk of the education of character. You can’t imagine how difficult that is! You have hardly succeeded in combating one tendency when others crop up, and the struggle begins again. If one had not a support in religion—you remember we talked about that—no father could bring children up relying on his own strength alone without that help.”\n\nThis subject, which always interested Levin, was cut short by the entrance of the beauty Natalia Alexandrovna, dressed to go out.\n\n“I didn’t know you were here,” she said, unmistakably feeling no regret, but a positive pleasure, in interrupting this conversation on a topic she had heard so much of that she was by now weary of it. “Well, how is Kitty? I am dining with you today. I tell you what, Arseny,” she turned to her husband, “you take the carriage.”\n\nAnd the husband and wife began to discuss their arrangements for the day. As the husband had to drive to meet someone on official business, while the wife had to go to the concert and some public meeting of a committee on the Eastern Question, there was a great deal to consider and settle. Levin had to take part in their plans as one of themselves. It was settled that Levin should go with Natalia to the concert and the meeting, and that from there they should send the carriage to the office for Arseny, and he should call for her and take her to Kitty’s; or that, if he had not finished his work, he should send the carriage back and Levin would go with her.\n\n“He’s spoiling me,” Lvov said to his wife; “he assures me that our children are splendid, when I know how much that’s bad there is in them.”\n\n“Arseny goes to extremes, I always say,” said his wife. “If you look for perfection, you will never be satisfied. And it’s true, as papa says,—that when we were brought up there was one extreme—we were kept in the basement, while our parents lived in the best rooms; now it’s just the other way—the parents are in the wash house, while the children are in the best rooms. Parents now are not expected to live at all, but to exist altogether for their children.”\n\n“Well, what if they like it better?” Lvov said, with his beautiful smile, touching her hand. “Anyone who didn’t know you would think you were a stepmother, not a true mother.”\n\n“No, extremes are not good in anything,” Natalia said serenely, putting his paper-knife straight in its proper place on the table.\n\n“Well, come here, you perfect children,” Lvov said to the two handsome boys who came in, and after bowing to Levin, went up to their father, obviously wishing to ask him about something.\n\nLevin would have liked to talk to them, to hear what they would say to their father, but Natalia began talking to him, and then Lvov’s colleague in the service, Mahotin, walked in, wearing his court uniform, to go with him to meet someone, and a conversation was kept up without a break upon Herzegovina, Princess Korzinskaya, the town council, and the sudden death of Madame Apraksina.\n\nLevin even forgot the commission intrusted to him. He recollected it as he was going into the hall.\n\n“Oh, Kitty told me to talk to you about Oblonsky,” he said, as Lvov was standing on the stairs, seeing his wife and Levin off.\n\n“Yes, yes, maman wants us, les beaux-frères, to attack him,” he said, blushing. “But why should I?”\n\n“Well, then, I will attack him,” said Madame Lvova, with a smile, standing in her white sheepskin cape, waiting till they had finished speaking. “Come, let us go.”\n\n# Chapter 5\n\nAt the concert in the afternoon two very interesting things were performed. One was a fantasia, King Lear; the other was a quartette dedicated to the memory of Bach. Both were new and in the new style, and Levin was eager to form an opinion of them. After escorting his sister-in-law to her stall, he stood against a column and tried to listen as attentively and conscientiously as possible. He tried not to let his attention be distracted, and not to spoil his impression by looking at the conductor in a white tie, waving his arms, which always disturbed his enjoyment of music so much, or the ladies in bonnets, with strings carefully tied over their ears, and all these people either thinking of nothing at all or thinking of all sorts of things except the music. He tried to avoid meeting musical connoisseurs or talkative acquaintances, and stood looking at the floor straight before him, listening.\n\nBut the more he listened to the fantasia of King Lear the further he felt from forming any definite opinion of it. There was, as it were, a continual beginning, a preparation of the musical expression of some feeling, but it fell to pieces again directly, breaking into new musical motives, or simply nothing but the whims of the composer, exceedingly complex but disconnected sounds. And these fragmentary musical expressions, though sometimes beautiful, were disagreeable, because they were utterly unexpected and not led up to by anything. Gaiety and grief and despair and tenderness and triumph followed one another without any connection, like the emotions of a madman. And those emotions, like a madman’s, sprang up quite unexpectedly.\n\nDuring the whole of the performance Levin felt like a deaf man watching people dancing, and was in a state of complete bewilderment when the fantasia was over, and felt a great weariness from the fruitless strain on his attention. Loud applause resounded on all sides. Everyone got up, moved about, and began talking. Anxious to throw some light on his own perplexity from the impressions of others, Levin began to walk about, looking for connoisseurs, and was glad to see a well-known musical amateur in conversation with Pestsov, whom he knew.\n\n“Marvelous!” Pestsov was saying in his mellow bass. “How are you, Konstantin Dmitrievitch? Particularly sculpturesque and plastic, so to say, and richly colored is that passage where you feel Cordelia’s approach, where woman, das ewig Weibliche, enters into conflict with fate. Isn’t it?”\n\n“You mean ... what has Cordelia to do with it?” Levin asked timidly, forgetting that the fantasia was supposed to represent King Lear.\n\n“Cordelia comes in ... see here!” said Pestsov, tapping his finger on the satiny surface of the program he held in his hand and passing it to Levin.\n\nOnly then Levin recollected the title of the fantasia, and made haste to read in the Russian translation the lines from Shakespeare that were printed on the back of the program.\n\n“You can’t follow it without that,” said Pestsov, addressing Levin, as the person he had been speaking to had gone away, and he had no one to talk to.\n\nIn the entr’acte Levin and Pestsov fell into an argument upon the merits and defects of music of the Wagner school. Levin maintained that the mistake of Wagner and all his followers lay in their trying to take music into the sphere of another art, just as poetry goes wrong when it tries to paint a face as the art of painting ought to do, and as an instance of this mistake he cited the sculptor who carved in marble certain poetic phantasms flitting round the figure of the poet on the pedestal. “These phantoms were so far from being phantoms that they were positively clinging on the ladder,” said Levin. The comparison pleased him, but he could not remember whether he had not used the same phrase before, and to Pestsov, too, and as he said it he felt confused.\n\nPestsov maintained that art is one, and that it can attain its highest manifestations only by conjunction with all kinds of art.\n\nThe second piece that was performed Levin could not hear. Pestsov, who was standing beside him, was talking to him almost all the time, condemning the music for its excessive affected assumption of simplicity, and comparing it with the simplicity of the Pre-Raphaelites in painting. As he went out Levin met many more acquaintances, with whom he talked of politics, of music, and of common acquaintances. Among others he met Count Bol, whom he had utterly forgotten to call upon.\n\n“Well, go at once then,” Madame Lvova said, when he told her; “perhaps they’ll not be at home, and then you can come to the meeting to fetch me. You’ll find me still there.”\n\n# Chapter 6\n\n“Perhaps they’re not at home?” said Levin, as he went into the hall of Countess Bola’s house.\n\n“At home; please walk in,” said the porter, resolutely removing his overcoat.\n\n“How annoying!” thought Levin with a sigh, taking off one glove and stroking his hat. “What did I come for? What have I to say to them?”\n\nAs he passed through the first drawing-room Levin met in the doorway Countess Bola, giving some order to a servant with a care-worn and severe face. On seeing Levin she smiled, and asked him to come into the little drawing-room, where he heard voices. In this room there were sitting in armchairs the two daughters of the countess, and a Moscow colonel, whom Levin knew. Levin went up, greeted them, and sat down beside the sofa with his hat on his knees.\n\n“How is your wife? Have you been at the concert? We couldn’t go. Mamma had to be at the funeral service.”\n\n“Yes, I heard.... What a sudden death!” said Levin.\n\nThe countess came in, sat down on the sofa, and she too asked after his wife and inquired about the concert.\n\nLevin answered, and repeated an inquiry about Madame Apraksina’s sudden death.\n\n“But she was always in weak health.”\n\n“Were you at the opera yesterday?”\n\n“Yes, I was.”\n\n“Lucca was very good.”\n\n“Yes, very good,” he said, and as it was utterly of no consequence to him what they thought of him, he began repeating what they had heard a hundred times about the characteristics of the singer’s talent. Countess Bola pretended to be listening. Then, when he had said enough and paused, the colonel, who had been silent till then, began to talk. The colonel too talked of the opera, and about culture. At last, after speaking of the proposed folle journée at Turin’s, the colonel laughed, got up noisily, and went away. Levin too rose, but he saw by the face of the countess that it was not yet time for him to go. He must stay two minutes longer. He sat down.\n\nBut as he was thinking all the while how stupid it was, he could not find a subject for conversation, and sat silent.\n\n“You are not going to the public meeting? They say it will be very interesting,” began the countess.\n\n“No, I promised my belle-sœur to fetch her from it,” said Levin.\n\nA silence followed. The mother once more exchanged glances with a daughter.\n\n“Well, now I think the time has come,” thought Levin, and he got up. The ladies shook hands with him, and begged him to say mille choses to his wife for them.\n\nThe porter asked him, as he gave him his coat, “Where is your honor staying?” and immediately wrote down his address in a big handsomely bound book.\n\n“Of course I don’t care, but still I feel ashamed and awfully stupid,” thought Levin, consoling himself with the reflection that everyone does it. He drove to the public meeting, where he was to find his sister-in-law, so as to drive home with her.\n\nAt the public meeting of the committee there were a great many people, and almost all the highest society. Levin was in time for the report which, as everyone said, was very interesting. When the reading of the report was over, people moved about, and Levin met Sviazhsky, who invited him very pressingly to come that evening to a meeting of the Society of Agriculture, where a celebrated lecture was to be delivered, and Stepan Arkadyevitch, who had only just come from the races, and many other acquaintances; and Levin heard and uttered various criticisms on the meeting, on the new fantasia, and on a public trial. But, probably from the mental fatigue he was beginning to feel, he made a blunder in speaking of the trial, and this blunder he recalled several times with vexation. Speaking of the sentence upon a foreigner who had been condemned in Russia, and of how unfair it would be to punish him by exile abroad, Levin repeated what he had heard the day before in conversation from an acquaintance.\n\n“I think sending him abroad is much the same as punishing a carp by putting it into the water,” said Levin. Then he recollected that this idea, which he had heard from an acquaintance and uttered as his own, came from a fable of Krilov’s, and that the acquaintance had picked it up from a newspaper article.\n\nAfter driving home with his sister-in-law, and finding Kitty in good spirits and quite well, Levin drove to the club.\n\n# Chapter 7\n\nLevin reached the club just at the right time. Members and visitors were driving up as he arrived. Levin had not been at the club for a very long while—not since he lived in Moscow, when he was leaving the university and going into society. He remembered the club, the external details of its arrangement, but he had completely forgotten the impression it had made on him in old days. But as soon as, driving into the wide semicircular court and getting out of the sledge, he mounted the steps, and the hall-porter, adorned with a crossway scarf, noiselessly opened the door to him with a bow; as soon as he saw in the porter’s room the cloaks and galoshes of members who thought it less trouble to take them off downstairs; as soon as he heard the mysterious ringing bell that preceded him as he ascended the easy, carpeted staircase, and saw the statue on the landing, and the third porter at the top doors, a familiar figure grown older, in the club livery, opening the door without haste or delay, and scanning the visitors as they passed in—Levin felt the old impression of the club come back in a rush, an impression of repose, comfort, and propriety.\n\n“Your hat, please,” the porter said to Levin, who forgot the club rule to leave his hat in the porter’s room. “Long time since you’ve been. The prince put your name down yesterday. Prince Stepan Arkadyevitch is not here yet.”\n\nThe porter did not only know Levin, but also all his ties and relationships, and so immediately mentioned his intimate friends.\n\nPassing through the outer hall, divided up by screens, and the room partitioned on the right, where a man sits at the fruit buffet, Levin overtook an old man walking slowly in, and entered the dining-room full of noise and people.\n\nHe walked along the tables, almost all full, and looked at the visitors. He saw people of all sorts, old and young; some he knew a little, some intimate friends. There was not a single cross or worried-looking face. All seemed to have left their cares and anxieties in the porter’s room with their hats, and were all deliberately getting ready to enjoy the material blessings of life. Sviazhsky was here and Shtcherbatsky, Nevyedovsky and the old prince, and Vronsky and Sergey Ivanovitch.\n\n“Ah! why are you late?” the prince said smiling, and giving him his hand over his own shoulder. “How’s Kitty?” he added, smoothing out the napkin he had tucked in at his waistcoat buttons.\n\n“All right; they are dining at home, all the three of them.”\n\n“Ah, ‘Aline-Nadine,’ to be sure! There’s no room with us. Go to that table, and make haste and take a seat,” said the prince, and turning away he carefully took a plate of eel soup.\n\n“Levin, this way!” a good-natured voice shouted a little farther on. It was Turovtsin. He was sitting with a young officer, and beside them were two chairs turned upside down. Levin gladly went up to them. He had always liked the good-hearted rake, Turovtsin—he was associated in his mind with memories of his courtship—and at that moment, after the strain of intellectual conversation, the sight of Turovtsin’s good-natured face was particularly welcome.\n\n“For you and Oblonsky. He’ll be here directly.”\n\nThe young man, holding himself very erect, with eyes forever twinkling with enjoyment, was an officer from Petersburg, Gagin. Turovtsin introduced them.\n\n“Oblonsky’s always late.”\n\n“Ah, here he is!”\n\n“Have you only just come?” said Oblonsky, coming quickly towards them. “Good day. Had some vodka? Well, come along then.”\n\nLevin got up and went with him to the big table spread with spirits and appetizers of the most various kinds. One would have thought that out of two dozen delicacies one might find something to one’s taste, but Stepan Arkadyevitch asked for something special, and one of the liveried waiters standing by immediately brought what was required. They drank a wine-glassful and returned to their table.\n\nAt once, while they were still at the soup, Gagin was served with champagne, and told the waiter to fill four glasses. Levin did not refuse the wine, and asked for a second bottle. He was very hungry, and ate and drank with great enjoyment, and with still greater enjoyment took part in the lively and simple conversation of his companions. Gagin, dropping his voice, told the last good story from Petersburg, and the story, though improper and stupid, was so ludicrous that Levin broke into roars of laughter so loud that those near looked round.\n\n“That’s in the same style as, ‘that’s a thing I can’t endure!’ You know the story?” said Stepan Arkadyevitch. “Ah, that’s exquisite! Another bottle,” he said to the waiter, and he began to relate his good story.\n\n“Pyotr Illyitch Vinovsky invites you to drink with him,” a little old waiter interrupted Stepan Arkadyevitch, bringing two delicate glasses of sparkling champagne, and addressing Stepan Arkadyevitch and Levin. Stepan Arkadyevitch took the glass, and looking towards a bald man with red mustaches at the other end of the table, he nodded to him, smiling.\n\n“Who’s that?” asked Levin.\n\n“You met him once at my place, don’t you remember? A good-natured fellow.”\n\nLevin did the same as Stepan Arkadyevitch and took the glass.\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch’s anecdote too was very amusing. Levin told his story, and that too was successful. Then they talked of horses, of the races, of what they had been doing that day, and of how smartly Vronsky’s Atlas had won the first prize. Levin did not notice how the time passed at dinner.\n\n“Ah! and here they are!” Stepan Arkadyevitch said towards the end of dinner, leaning over the back of his chair and holding out his hand to Vronsky, who came up with a tall officer of the Guards. Vronsky’s face too beamed with the look of good-humored enjoyment that was general in the club. He propped his elbow playfully on Stepan Arkadyevitch’s shoulder, whispering something to him, and he held out his hand to Levin with the same good-humored smile.\n\n“Very glad to meet you,” he said. “I looked out for you at the election, but I was told you had gone away.”\n\n“Yes, I left the same day. We’ve just been talking of your horse. I congratulate you,” said Levin. “It was very rapidly run.”\n\n“Yes; you’ve race horses too, haven’t you?”\n\n“No, my father had; but I remember and know something about it.”\n\n“Where have you dined?” asked Stepan Arkadyevitch.\n\n“We were at the second table, behind the columns.”\n\n“We’ve been celebrating his success,” said the tall colonel. “It’s his second Imperial prize. I wish I might have the luck at cards he has with horses. Well, why waste the precious time? I’m going to the ‘infernal regions,’” added the colonel, and he walked away.\n\n“That’s Yashvin,” Vronsky said in answer to Turovtsin, and he sat down in the vacated seat beside them. He drank the glass offered him, and ordered a bottle of wine. Under the influence of the club atmosphere or the wine he had drunk, Levin chatted away to Vronsky of the best breeds of cattle, and was very glad not to feel the slightest hostility to this man. He even told him, among other things, that he had heard from his wife that she had met him at Princess Marya Borissovna’s.\n\n“Ah, Princess Marya Borissovna, she’s exquisite!” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, and he told an anecdote about her which set them all laughing. Vronsky particularly laughed with such simplehearted amusement that Levin felt quite reconciled to him.\n\n“Well, have we finished?” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, getting up with a smile. “Let us go.”\n\n# Chapter 8\n\nGetting up from the table, Levin walked with Gagin through the lofty room to the billiard room, feeling his arms swing as he walked with a peculiar lightness and ease. As he crossed the big room, he came upon his father-in-law.\n\n“Well, how do you like our Temple of Indolence?” said the prince, taking his arm. “Come along, come along!”\n\n“Yes, I wanted to walk about and look at everything. It’s interesting.”\n\n“Yes, it’s interesting for you. But its interest for me is quite different. You look at those little old men now,” he said, pointing to a club member with bent back and projecting lip, shuffling towards them in his soft boots, “and imagine that they were shlupiks like that from their birth up.”\n\n“How shlupiks?”\n\n“I see you don’t know that name. That’s our club designation. You know the game of rolling eggs: when one’s rolled a long while it becomes a shlupik. So it is with us; one goes on coming and coming to the club, and ends by becoming a shlupik. Ah, you laugh! but we look out, for fear of dropping into it ourselves. You know Prince Tchetchensky?” inquired the prince; and Levin saw by his face that he was just going to relate something funny.\n\n“No, I don’t know him.”\n\n“You don’t say so! Well, Prince Tchetchensky is a well-known figure. No matter, though. He’s always playing billiards here. Only three years ago he was not a shlupik and kept up his spirits and even used to call other people shlupiks. But one day he turns up, and our porter ... you know Vassily? Why, that fat one; he’s famous for his bon mots. And so Prince Tchetchensky asks him, ‘Come, Vassily, who’s here? Any shlupiks here yet?’ And he says, ‘You’re the third.’ Yes, my dear boy, that he did!”\n\nTalking and greeting the friends they met, Levin and the prince walked through all the rooms: the great room where tables had already been set, and the usual partners were playing for small stakes; the divan room, where they were playing chess, and Sergey Ivanovitch was sitting talking to somebody; the billiard room, where, about a sofa in a recess, there was a lively party drinking champagne—Gagin was one of them. They peeped into the “infernal regions,” where a good many men were crowding round one table, at which Yashvin was sitting. Trying not to make a noise, they walked into the dark reading room, where under the shaded lamps there sat a young man with a wrathful countenance, turning over one journal after another, and a bald general buried in a book. They went, too, into what the prince called the intellectual room, where three gentlemen were engaged in a heated discussion of the latest political news.\n\n“Prince, please come, we’re ready,” said one of his card party, who had come to look for him, and the prince went off. Levin sat down and listened, but recalling all the conversation of the morning he felt all of a sudden fearfully bored. He got up hurriedly, and went to look for Oblonsky and Turovtsin, with whom it had been so pleasant.\n\nTurovtsin was one of the circle drinking in the billiard room, and Stepan Arkadyevitch was talking with Vronsky near the door at the farther corner of the room.\n\n“It’s not that she’s dull; but this undefined, this unsettled position,” Levin caught, and he was hurrying away, but Stepan Arkadyevitch called to him.\n\n“Levin,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, and Levin noticed that his eyes were not full of tears exactly, but moist, which always happened when he had been drinking, or when he was touched. Just now it was due to both causes. “Levin, don’t go,” he said, and he warmly squeezed his arm above the elbow, obviously not at all wishing to let him go.\n\n“This is a true friend of mine—almost my greatest friend,” he said to Vronsky. “You have become even closer and dearer to me. And I want you, and I know you ought, to be friends, and great friends, because you’re both splendid fellows.”\n\n“Well, there’s nothing for us now but to kiss and be friends,” Vronsky said, with good-natured playfulness, holding out his hand.\n\nLevin quickly took the offered hand, and pressed it warmly.\n\n“I’m very, very glad,” said Levin.\n\n“Waiter, a bottle of champagne,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch.\n\n“And I’m very glad,” said Vronsky.\n\nBut in spite of Stepan Arkadyevitch’s desire, and their own desire, they had nothing to talk about, and both felt it.\n\n“Do you know, he has never met Anna?” Stepan Arkadyevitch said to Vronsky. “And I want above everything to take him to see her. Let us go, Levin!”\n\n“Really?” said Vronsky. “She will be very glad to see you. I should be going home at once,” he added, “but I’m worried about Yashvin, and I want to stay on till he finishes.”\n\n“Why, is he losing?”\n\n“He keeps losing, and I’m the only friend that can restrain him.”\n\n“Well, what do you say to pyramids? Levin, will you play? Capital!” said Stepan Arkadyevitch. “Get the table ready,” he said to the marker.\n\n“It has been ready a long while,” answered the marker, who had already set the balls in a triangle, and was knocking the red one about for his own diversion.\n\n“Well, let us begin.”\n\nAfter the game Vronsky and Levin sat down at Gagin’s table, and at Stepan Arkadyevitch’s suggestion Levin took a hand in the game.\n\nVronsky sat down at the table, surrounded by friends, who were incessantly coming up to him. Every now and then he went to the “infernal” to keep an eye on Yashvin. Levin was enjoying a delightful sense of repose after the mental fatigue of the morning. He was glad that all hostility was at an end with Vronsky, and the sense of peace, decorum, and comfort never left him.\n\nWhen the game was over, Stepan Arkadyevitch took Levin’s arm.\n\n“Well, let us go to Anna’s, then. At once? Eh? She is at home. I promised her long ago to bring you. Where were you meaning to spend the evening?”\n\n“Oh, nowhere specially. I promised Sviazhsky to go to the Society of Agriculture. By all means, let us go,” said Levin.\n\n“Very good; come along. Find out if my carriage is here,” Stepan Arkadyevitch said to the waiter.\n\nLevin went up to the table, paid the forty roubles he had lost; paid his bill, the amount of which was in some mysterious way ascertained by the little old waiter who stood at the counter, and swinging his arms he walked through all the rooms to the way out.\n\n# Chapter 9\n\n“Oblonsky’s carriage!” the porter shouted in an angry bass. The carriage drove up and both got in. It was only for the first few moments, while the carriage was driving out of the clubhouse gates, that Levin was still under the influence of the club atmosphere of repose, comfort, and unimpeachable good form. But as soon as the carriage drove out into the street, and he felt it jolting over the uneven road, heard the angry shout of a sledge driver coming towards them, saw in the uncertain light the red blind of a tavern and the shops, this impression was dissipated, and he began to think over his actions, and to wonder whether he was doing right in going to see Anna. What would Kitty say? But Stepan Arkadyevitch gave him no time for reflection, and, as though divining his doubts, he scattered them.\n\n“How glad I am,” he said, “that you should know her! You know Dolly has long wished for it. And Lvov’s been to see her, and often goes. Though she is my sister,” Stepan Arkadyevitch pursued, “I don’t hesitate to say that she’s a remarkable woman. But you will see. Her position is very painful, especially now.”\n\n“Why especially now?”\n\n“We are carrying on negotiations with her husband about a divorce. And he’s agreed; but there are difficulties in regard to the son, and the business, which ought to have been arranged long ago, has been dragging on for three months past. As soon as the divorce is over, she will marry Vronsky. How stupid these old ceremonies are, that no one believes in, and which only prevent people being comfortable!” Stepan Arkadyevitch put in. “Well, then their position will be as regular as mine, as yours.”\n\n“What is the difficulty?” said Levin.\n\n“Oh, it’s a long and tedious story! The whole business is in such an anomalous position with us. But the point is she has been for three months in Moscow, where everyone knows her, waiting for the divorce; she goes out nowhere, sees no woman except Dolly, because, do you understand, she doesn’t care to have people come as a favor. That fool Princess Varvara, even she has left her, considering this a breach of propriety. Well, you see, in such a position any other woman would not have found resources in herself. But you’ll see how she has arranged her life—how calm, how dignified she is. To the left, in the crescent opposite the church!” shouted Stepan Arkadyevitch, leaning out of the window. “Phew! how hot it is!” he said, in spite of twelve degrees of frost, flinging his open overcoat still wider open.\n\n“But she has a daughter: no doubt she’s busy looking after her?” said Levin.\n\n“I believe you picture every woman simply as a female, une couveuse,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch. “If she’s occupied, it must be with her children. No, she brings her up capitally, I believe, but one doesn’t hear about her. She’s busy, in the first place, with what she writes. I see you’re smiling ironically, but you’re wrong. She’s writing a children’s book, and doesn’t talk about it to anyone, but she read it to me and I gave the manuscript to Vorkuev ... you know the publisher ... and he’s an author himself too, I fancy. He understands those things, and he says it’s a remarkable piece of work. But are you fancying she’s an authoress?—not a bit of it. She’s a woman with a heart, before everything, but you’ll see. Now she has a little English girl with her, and a whole family she’s looking after.”\n\n“Oh, something in a philanthropic way?”\n\n“Why, you will look at everything in the worst light. It’s not from philanthropy, it’s from the heart. They—that is, Vronsky—had a trainer, an Englishman, first-rate in his own line, but a drunkard. He’s completely given up to drink—delirium tremens—and the family were cast on the world. She saw them, helped them, got more and more interested in them, and now the whole family is on her hands. But not by way of patronage, you know, helping with money; she’s herself preparing the boys in Russian for the high school, and she’s taken the little girl to live with her. But you’ll see her for yourself.”\n\nThe carriage drove into the courtyard, and Stepan Arkadyevitch rang loudly at the entrance where sledges were standing.\n\nAnd without asking the servant who opened the door whether the lady were at home, Stepan Arkadyevitch walked into the hall. Levin followed him, more and more doubtful whether he was doing right or wrong.\n\nLooking at himself in the glass, Levin noticed that he was red in the face, but he felt certain he was not drunk, and he followed Stepan Arkadyevitch up the carpeted stairs. At the top Stepan Arkadyevitch inquired of the footman, who bowed to him as to an intimate friend, who was with Anna Arkadyevna, and received the answer that it was M. Vorkuev.\n\n“Where are they?”\n\n“In the study.”\n\nPassing through the dining-room, a room not very large, with dark, paneled walls, Stepan Arkadyevitch and Levin walked over the soft carpet to the half-dark study, lighted up by a single lamp with a big dark shade. Another lamp with a reflector was hanging on the wall, lighting up a big full-length portrait of a woman, which Levin could not help looking at. It was the portrait of Anna, painted in Italy by Mihailov. While Stepan Arkadyevitch went behind the treillage, and the man’s voice which had been speaking paused, Levin gazed at the portrait, which stood out from the frame in the brilliant light thrown on it, and he could not tear himself away from it. He positively forgot where he was, and not even hearing what was said, he could not take his eyes off the marvelous portrait. It was not a picture, but a living, charming woman, with black curling hair, with bare arms and shoulders, with a pensive smile on the lips, covered with soft down; triumphantly and softly she looked at him with eyes that baffled him. She was not living only because she was more beautiful than a living woman can be.\n\n“I am delighted!” He heard suddenly near him a voice, unmistakably addressing him, the voice of the very woman he had been admiring in the portrait. Anna had come from behind the treillage to meet him, and Levin saw in the dim light of the study the very woman of the portrait, in a dark blue shot gown, not in the same position nor with the same expression, but with the same perfection of beauty which the artist had caught in the portrait. She was less dazzling in reality, but, on the other hand, there was something fresh and seductive in the living woman which was not in the portrait.\n\n# Chapter 10\n\nShe had risen to meet him, not concealing her pleasure at seeing him; and in the quiet ease with which she held out her little vigorous hand, introduced him to Vorkuev and indicated a red-haired, pretty little girl who was sitting at work, calling her her pupil, Levin recognized and liked the manners of a woman of the great world, always self-possessed and natural.\n\n“I am delighted, delighted,” she repeated, and on her lips these simple words took for Levin’s ears a special significance. “I have known you and liked you for a long while, both from your friendship with Stiva and for your wife’s sake.... I knew her for a very short time, but she left on me the impression of an exquisite flower, simply a flower. And to think she will soon be a mother!”\n\nShe spoke easily and without haste, looking now and then from Levin to her brother, and Levin felt that the impression he was making was good, and he felt immediately at home, simple and happy with her, as though he had known her from childhood.\n\n“Ivan Petrovitch and I settled in Alexey’s study,” she said in answer to Stepan Arkadyevitch’s question whether he might smoke, “just so as to be able to smoke”—and glancing at Levin, instead of asking whether he would smoke, she pulled closer a tortoise-shell cigar-case and took a cigarette.\n\n“How are you feeling today?” her brother asked her.\n\n“Oh, nothing. Nerves, as usual.”\n\n“Yes, isn’t it extraordinarily fine?” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, noticing that Levin was scrutinizing the picture.\n\n“I have never seen a better portrait.”\n\n“And extraordinarily like, isn’t it?” said Vorkuev.\n\nLevin looked from the portrait to the original. A peculiar brilliance lighted up Anna’s face when she felt his eyes on her. Levin flushed, and to cover his confusion would have asked whether she had seen Darya Alexandrovna lately; but at that moment Anna spoke. “We were just talking, Ivan Petrovitch and I, of Vashtchenkov’s last pictures. Have you seen them?”\n\n“Yes, I have seen them,” answered Levin.\n\n“But, I beg your pardon, I interrupted you ... you were saying?...”\n\nLevin asked if she had seen Dolly lately.\n\n“She was here yesterday. She was very indignant with the high school people on Grisha’s account. The Latin teacher, it seems, had been unfair to him.”\n\n“Yes, I have seen his pictures. I didn’t care for them very much,” Levin went back to the subject she had started.\n\nLevin talked now not at all with that purely businesslike attitude to the subject with which he had been talking all the morning. Every word in his conversation with her had a special significance. And talking to her was pleasant; still pleasanter it was to listen to her.\n\nAnna talked not merely naturally and cleverly, but cleverly and carelessly, attaching no value to her own ideas and giving great weight to the ideas of the person she was talking to.\n\nThe conversation turned on the new movement in art, on the new illustrations of the Bible by a French artist. Vorkuev attacked the artist for a realism carried to the point of coarseness.\n\nLevin said that the French had carried conventionality further than anyone, and that consequently they see a great merit in the return to realism. In the fact of not lying they see poetry.\n\nNever had anything clever said by Levin given him so much pleasure as this remark. Anna’s face lighted up at once, as at once she appreciated the thought. She laughed.\n\n“I laugh,” she said, “as one laughs when one sees a very true portrait. What you said so perfectly hits off French art now, painting and literature too, indeed—Zola, Daudet. But perhaps it is always so, that men form their conceptions from fictitious, conventional types, and then—all the combinaisons made—they are tired of the fictitious figures and begin to invent more natural, true figures.”\n\n“That’s perfectly true,” said Vorknev.\n\n“So you’ve been at the club?” she said to her brother.\n\n“Yes, yes, this is a woman!” Levin thought, forgetting himself and staring persistently at her lovely, mobile face, which at that moment was all at once completely transformed. Levin did not hear what she was talking of as she leaned over to her brother, but he was struck by the change of her expression. Her face—so handsome a moment before in its repose—suddenly wore a look of strange curiosity, anger, and pride. But this lasted only an instant. She dropped her eyelids, as though recollecting something.\n\n“Oh, well, but that’s of no interest to anyone,” she said, and she turned to the English girl.\n\n“Please order the tea in the drawing-room,” she said in English.\n\nThe girl got up and went out.\n\n“Well, how did she get through her examination?” asked Stepan Arkadyevitch.\n\n“Splendidly! She’s a very gifted child and a sweet character.”\n\n“It will end in your loving her more than your own.”\n\n“There a man speaks. In love there’s no more nor less. I love my daughter with one love, and her with another.”\n\n“I was just telling Anna Arkadyevna,” said Vorkuev, “that if she were to put a hundredth part of the energy she devotes to this English girl to the public question of the education of Russian children, she would be doing a great and useful work.”\n\n“Yes, but I can’t help it; I couldn’t do it. Count Alexey Kirillovitch urged me very much” (as she uttered the words Count Alexey Kirillovitch she glanced with appealing timidity at Levin, and he unconsciously responded with a respectful and reassuring look); “he urged me to take up the school in the village. I visited it several times. The children were very nice, but I could not feel drawn to the work. You speak of energy. Energy rests upon love; and come as it will, there’s no forcing it. I took to this child—I could not myself say why.”\n\nAnd she glanced again at Levin. And her smile and her glance—all told him that it was to him only she was addressing her words, valuing his good opinion, and at the same time sure beforehand that they understood each other.\n\n“I quite understand that,” Levin answered. “It’s impossible to give one’s heart to a school or such institutions in general, and I believe that’s just why philanthropic institutions always give such poor results.”\n\nShe was silent for a while, then she smiled.\n\n“Yes, yes,” she agreed; “I never could. Je n’ai pas le cœur assez large to love a whole asylum of horrid little girls. Cela ne m’a jamais réussi. There are so many women who have made themselves une position sociale in that way. And now more than ever,” she said with a mournful, confiding expression, ostensibly addressing her brother, but unmistakably intending her words only for Levin, “now when I have such need of some occupation, I cannot.” And suddenly frowning (Levin saw that she was frowning at herself for talking about herself) she changed the subject. “I know about you,” she said to Levin; “that you’re not a public-spirited citizen, and I have defended you to the best of my ability.”\n\n“How have you defended me?”\n\n“Oh, according to the attacks made on you. But won’t you have some tea?” She rose and took up a book bound in morocco.\n\n“Give it to me, Anna Arkadyevna,” said Vorkuev, indicating the book. “It’s well worth taking up.”\n\n“Oh, no, it’s all so sketchy.”\n\n“I told him about it,” Stepan Arkadyevitch said to his sister, nodding at Levin.\n\n“You shouldn’t have. My writing is something after the fashion of those little baskets and carving which Liza Mertsalova used to sell me from the prisons. She had the direction of the prison department in that society,” she turned to Levin; “and they were miracles of patience, the work of those poor wretches.”\n\nAnd Levin saw a new trait in this woman, who attracted him so extraordinarily. Besides wit, grace, and beauty, she had truth. She had no wish to hide from him all the bitterness of her position. As she said that she sighed, and her face suddenly taking a hard expression, looked as it were turned to stone. With that expression on her face she was more beautiful than ever; but the expression was new; it was utterly unlike that expression, radiant with happiness and creating happiness, which had been caught by the painter in her portrait. Levin looked more than once at the portrait and at her figure, as taking her brother’s arm she walked with him to the high doors and he felt for her a tenderness and pity at which he wondered himself.\n\nShe asked Levin and Vorkuev to go into the drawing-room, while she stayed behind to say a few words to her brother. “About her divorce, about Vronsky, and what he’s doing at the club, about me?” wondered Levin. And he was so keenly interested by the question of what she was saying to Stepan Arkadyevitch, that he scarcely heard what Vorkuev was telling him of the qualities of the story for children Anna Arkadyevna had written.\n\nAt tea the same pleasant sort of talk, full of interesting matter, continued. There was not a single instant when a subject for conversation was to seek; on the contrary, it was felt that one had hardly time to say what one had to say, and eagerly held back to hear what the others were saying. And all that was said, not only by her, but by Vorkuev and Stepan Arkadyevitch—all, so it seemed to Levin, gained peculiar significance from her appreciation and her criticism. While he followed this interesting conversation, Levin was all the time admiring her—her beauty, her intelligence, her culture, and at the same time her directness and genuine depth of feeling. He listened and talked, and all the while he was thinking of her inner life, trying to divine her feelings. And though he had judged her so severely hitherto, now by some strange chain of reasoning he was justifying her and was also sorry for her, and afraid that Vronsky did not fully understand her. At eleven o’clock, when Stepan Arkadyevitch got up to go (Vorkuev had left earlier), it seemed to Levin that he had only just come. Regretfully Levin too rose.\n\n“Good-bye,” she said, holding his hand and glancing into his face with a winning look. “I am very glad que la glace est rompue.”\n\nShe dropped his hand, and half closed her eyes.\n\n“Tell your wife that I love her as before, and that if she cannot pardon me my position, then my wish for her is that she may never pardon it. To pardon it, one must go through what I have gone through, and may God spare her that.”\n\n“Certainly, yes, I will tell her....” Levin said, blushing.\n\n# Chapter 11\n\n“What a marvelous, sweet and unhappy woman!” he was thinking, as he stepped out into the frosty air with Stepan Arkadyevitch.\n\n“Well, didn’t I tell you?” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, seeing that Levin had been completely won over.\n\n“Yes,” said Levin dreamily, “an extraordinary woman! It’s not her cleverness, but she has such wonderful depth of feeling. I’m awfully sorry for her!”\n\n“Now, please God, everything will soon be settled. Well, well, don’t be hard on people in future,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, opening the carriage door. “Good-bye; we don’t go the same way.”\n\nStill thinking of Anna, of everything, even the simplest phrase in their conversation with her, and recalling the minutest changes in her expression, entering more and more into her position, and feeling sympathy for her, Levin reached home.\n\nAt home Kouzma told Levin that Katerina Alexandrovna was quite well, and that her sisters had not long been gone, and he handed him two letters. Levin read them at once in the hall, that he might not overlook them later. One was from Sokolov, his bailiff. Sokolov wrote that the corn could not be sold, that it was fetching only five and a half roubles, and that more than that could not be got for it. The other letter was from his sister. She scolded him for her business being still unsettled.\n\n“Well, we must sell it at five and a half if we can’t get more,” Levin decided the first question, which had always before seemed such a weighty one, with extraordinary facility on the spot. “It’s extraordinary how all one’s time is taken up here,” he thought, considering the second letter. He felt himself to blame for not having got done what his sister had asked him to do for her. “Today, again, I’ve not been to the court, but today I’ve certainly not had time.” And resolving that he would not fail to do it next day, he went up to his wife. As he went in, Levin rapidly ran through mentally the day he had spent. All the events of the day were conversations, conversations he had heard and taken part in. All the conversations were upon subjects which, if he had been alone at home, he would never have taken up, but here they were very interesting. And all these conversations were right enough, only in two places there was something not quite right. One was what he had said about the carp, the other was something not “quite the thing” in the tender sympathy he was feeling for Anna.\n\nLevin found his wife low-spirited and dull. The dinner of the three sisters had gone off very well, but then they had waited and waited for him, all of them had felt dull, the sisters had departed, and she had been left alone.\n\n“Well, and what have you been doing?” she asked him, looking straight into his eyes, which shone with rather a suspicious brightness. But that she might not prevent his telling her everything, she concealed her close scrutiny of him, and with an approving smile listened to his account of how he had spent the evening.\n\n“Well, I’m very glad I met Vronsky. I felt quite at ease and natural with him. You understand, I shall try not to see him, but I’m glad that this awkwardness is all over,” he said, and remembering that by way of trying not to see him, he had immediately gone to call on Anna, he blushed. “We talk about the peasants drinking; I don’t know which drinks most, the peasantry or our own class; the peasants do on holidays, but....”\n\nBut Kitty took not the slightest interest in discussing the drinking habits of the peasants. She saw that he blushed, and she wanted to know why.\n\n“Well, and then where did you go?”\n\n“Stiva urged me awfully to go and see Anna Arkadyevna.”\n\nAnd as he said this, Levin blushed even more, and his doubts as to whether he had done right in going to see Anna were settled once for all. He knew now that he ought not to have done so.\n\nKitty’s eyes opened in a curious way and gleamed at Anna’s name, but controlling herself with an effort, she concealed her emotion and deceived him.\n\n“Oh!” was all she said.\n\n“I’m sure you won’t be angry at my going. Stiva begged me to, and Dolly wished it,” Levin went on.\n\n“Oh, no!” she said, but he saw in her eyes a constraint that boded him no good.\n\n“She is a very sweet, very, very unhappy, good woman,” he said, telling her about Anna, her occupations, and what she had told him to say to her.\n\n“Yes, of course, she is very much to be pitied,” said Kitty, when he had finished. “Whom was your letter from?”\n\nHe told her, and believing in her calm tone, he went to change his coat.\n\nComing back, he found Kitty in the same easy chair. When he went up to her, she glanced at him and broke into sobs.\n\n“What? what is it?” he asked, knowing beforehand what.\n\n“You’re in love with that hateful woman; she has bewitched you! I saw it in your eyes. Yes, yes! What can it all lead to? You were drinking at the club, drinking and gambling, and then you went ... to her of all people! No, we must go away.... I shall go away tomorrow.”\n\nIt was a long while before Levin could soothe his wife. At last he succeeded in calming her, only by confessing that a feeling of pity, in conjunction with the wine he had drunk, had been too much for him, that he had succumbed to Anna’s artful influence, and that he would avoid her. One thing he did with more sincerity confess to was that living so long in Moscow, a life of nothing but conversation, eating and drinking, he was degenerating. They talked till three o’clock in the morning. Only at three o’clock were they sufficiently reconciled to be able to go to sleep.\n\n# Chapter 12\n\nAfter taking leave of her guests, Anna did not sit down, but began walking up and down the room. She had unconsciously the whole evening done her utmost to arouse in Levin a feeling of love—as of late she had fallen into doing with all young men—and she knew she had attained her aim, as far as was possible in one evening, with a married and conscientious man. She liked him indeed extremely, and, in spite of the striking difference, from the masculine point of view, between Vronsky and Levin, as a woman she saw something they had in common, which had made Kitty able to love both. Yet as soon as he was out of the room, she ceased to think of him.\n\nOne thought, and one only, pursued her in different forms, and refused to be shaken off. “If I have so much effect on others, on this man, who loves his home and his wife, why is it he is so cold to me?... not cold exactly, he loves me, I know that! But something new is drawing us apart now. Why wasn’t he here all the evening? He told Stiva to say he could not leave Yashvin, and must watch over his play. Is Yashvin a child? But supposing it’s true. He never tells a lie. But there’s something else in it if it’s true. He is glad of an opportunity of showing me that he has other duties; I know that, I submit to that. But why prove that to me? He wants to show me that his love for me is not to interfere with his freedom. But I need no proofs, I need love. He ought to understand all the bitterness of this life for me here in Moscow. Is this life? I am not living, but waiting for an event, which is continually put off and put off. No answer again! And Stiva says he cannot go to Alexey Alexandrovitch. And I can’t write again. I can do nothing, can begin nothing, can alter nothing; I hold myself in, I wait, inventing amusements for myself—the English family, writing, reading—but it’s all nothing but a sham, it’s all the same as morphine. He ought to feel for me,” she said, feeling tears of self-pity coming into her eyes.\n\nShe heard Vronsky’s abrupt ring and hurriedly dried her tears—not only dried her tears, but sat down by a lamp and opened a book, affecting composure. She wanted to show him that she was displeased that he had not come home as he had promised—displeased only, and not on any account to let him see her distress, and least of all, her self-pity. She might pity herself, but he must not pity her. She did not want strife, she blamed him for wanting to quarrel, but unconsciously put herself into an attitude of antagonism.\n\n“Well, you’ve not been dull?” he said, eagerly and good-humoredly, going up to her. “What a terrible passion it is—gambling!”\n\n“No, I’ve not been dull; I’ve learned long ago not to be dull. Stiva has been here and Levin.”\n\n“Yes, they meant to come and see you. Well, how did you like Levin?” he said, sitting down beside her.\n\n“Very much. They have not long been gone. What was Yashvin doing?”\n\n“He was winning—seventeen thousand. I got him away. He had really started home, but he went back again, and now he’s losing.”\n\n“Then what did you stay for?” she asked, suddenly lifting her eyes to him. The expression of her face was cold and ungracious. “You told Stiva you were staying on to get Yashvin away. And you have left him there.”\n\nThe same expression of cold readiness for the conflict appeared on his face too.\n\n“In the first place, I did not ask him to give you any message; and secondly, I never tell lies. But what’s the chief point, I wanted to stay, and I stayed,” he said, frowning. “Anna, what is it for, why will you?” he said after a moment’s silence, bending over towards her, and he opened his hand, hoping she would lay hers in it.\n\nShe was glad of this appeal for tenderness. But some strange force of evil would not let her give herself up to her feelings, as though the rules of warfare would not permit her to surrender.\n\n“Of course you wanted to stay, and you stayed. You do everything you want to. But what do you tell me that for? With what object?” she said, getting more and more excited. “Does anyone contest your rights? But you want to be right, and you’re welcome to be right.”\n\nHis hand closed, he turned away, and his face wore a still more obstinate expression.\n\n“For you it’s a matter of obstinacy,” she said, watching him intently and suddenly finding the right word for that expression that irritated her, “simply obstinacy. For you it’s a question of whether you keep the upper hand of me, while for me....” Again she felt sorry for herself, and she almost burst into tears. “If you knew what it is for me! When I feel as I do now that you are hostile, yes, hostile to me, if you knew what this means for me! If you knew how I feel on the brink of calamity at this instant, how afraid I am of myself!” And she turned away, hiding her sobs.\n\n“But what are you talking about?” he said, horrified at her expression of despair, and again bending over her, he took her hand and kissed it. “What is it for? Do I seek amusements outside our home? Don’t I avoid the society of women?”\n\n“Well, yes! If that were all!” she said.\n\n“Come, tell me what I ought to do to give you peace of mind? I am ready to do anything to make you happy,” he said, touched by her expression of despair; “what wouldn’t I do to save you from distress of any sort, as now, Anna!” he said.\n\n“It’s nothing, nothing!” she said. “I don’t know myself whether it’s the solitary life, my nerves.... Come, don’t let us talk of it. What about the race? You haven’t told me!” she inquired, trying to conceal her triumph at the victory, which had anyway been on her side.\n\nHe asked for supper, and began telling her about the races; but in his tone, in his eyes, which became more and more cold, she saw that he did not forgive her for her victory, that the feeling of obstinacy with which she had been struggling had asserted itself again in him. He was colder to her than before, as though he were regretting his surrender. And she, remembering the words that had given her the victory, “how I feel on the brink of calamity, how afraid I am of myself,” saw that this weapon was a dangerous one, and that it could not be used a second time. And she felt that beside the love that bound them together there had grown up between them some evil spirit of strife, which she could not exorcise from his, and still less from her own heart.\n\n# Chapter 13\n\nThere are no conditions to which a man cannot become used, especially if he sees that all around him are living in the same way. Levin could not have believed three months before that he could have gone quietly to sleep in the condition in which he was that day, that leading an aimless, irrational life, living too beyond his means, after drinking to excess (he could not call what happened at the club anything else), forming inappropriately friendly relations with a man with whom his wife had once been in love, and a still more inappropriate call upon a woman who could only be called a lost woman, after being fascinated by that woman and causing his wife distress—he could still go quietly to sleep. But under the influence of fatigue, a sleepless night, and the wine he had drunk, his sleep was sound and untroubled.\n\nAt five o’clock the creak of a door opening waked him. He jumped up and looked round. Kitty was not in bed beside him. But there was a light moving behind the screen, and he heard her steps.\n\n“What is it?... what is it?” he said, half-asleep. “Kitty! What is it?”\n\n“Nothing,” she said, coming from behind the screen with a candle in her hand. “I felt unwell,” she said, smiling a particularly sweet and meaning smile.\n\n“What? has it begun?” he said in terror. “We ought to send....” and hurriedly he reached after his clothes.\n\n“No, no,” she said, smiling and holding his hand. “It’s sure to be nothing. I was rather unwell, only a little. It’s all over now.”\n\nAnd getting into bed, she blew out the candle, lay down and was still. Though he thought her stillness suspicious, as though she were holding her breath, and still more suspicious the expression of peculiar tenderness and excitement with which, as she came from behind the screen, she said “nothing,” he was so sleepy that he fell asleep at once. Only later he remembered the stillness of her breathing, and understood all that must have been passing in her sweet, precious heart while she lay beside him, not stirring, in anticipation of the greatest event in a woman’s life. At seven o’clock he was waked by the touch of her hand on his shoulder, and a gentle whisper. She seemed struggling between regret at waking him, and the desire to talk to him.\n\n“Kostya, don’t be frightened. It’s all right. But I fancy.... We ought to send for Lizaveta Petrovna.”\n\nThe candle was lighted again. She was sitting up in bed, holding some knitting, which she had been busy upon during the last few days.\n\n“Please, don’t be frightened, it’s all right. I’m not a bit afraid,” she said, seeing his scared face, and she pressed his hand to her bosom and then to her lips.\n\nHe hurriedly jumped up, hardly awake, and kept his eyes fixed on her, as he put on his dressing gown; then he stopped, still looking at her. He had to go, but he could not tear himself from her eyes. He thought he loved her face, knew her expression, her eyes, but never had he seen it like this. How hateful and horrible he seemed to himself, thinking of the distress he had caused her yesterday. Her flushed face, fringed with soft curling hair under her night cap, was radiant with joy and courage.\n\nThough there was so little that was complex or artificial in Kitty’s character in general, Levin was struck by what was revealed now, when suddenly all disguises were thrown off and the very kernel of her soul shone in her eyes. And in this simplicity and nakedness of her soul, she, the very woman he loved in her, was more manifest than ever. She looked at him, smiling; but all at once her brows twitched, she threw up her head, and going quickly up to him, clutched his hand and pressed close up to him, breathing her hot breath upon him. She was in pain and was, as it were, complaining to him of her suffering. And for the first minute, from habit, it seemed to him that he was to blame. But in her eyes there was a tenderness that told him that she was far from reproaching him, that she loved him for her sufferings. “If not I, who is to blame for it?” he thought unconsciously, seeking someone responsible for this suffering for him to punish; but there was no one responsible. She was suffering, complaining, and triumphing in her sufferings, and rejoicing in them, and loving them. He saw that something sublime was being accomplished in her soul, but what? He could not make it out. It was beyond his understanding.\n\n“I have sent to mamma. You go quickly to fetch Lizaveta Petrovna ... Kostya!... Nothing, it’s over.”\n\nShe moved away from him and rang the bell.\n\n“Well, go now; Pasha’s coming. I am all right.”\n\nAnd Levin saw with astonishment that she had taken up the knitting she had brought in in the night and begun working at it again.\n\nAs Levin was going out of one door, he heard the maid-servant come in at the other. He stood at the door and heard Kitty giving exact directions to the maid, and beginning to help her move the bedstead.\n\nHe dressed, and while they were putting in his horses, as a hired sledge was not to be seen yet, he ran again up to the bedroom, not on tiptoe, it seemed to him, but on wings. Two maid-servants were carefully moving something in the bedroom.\n\nKitty was walking about knitting rapidly and giving directions.\n\n“I’m going for the doctor. They have sent for Lizaveta Petrovna, but I’ll go on there too. Isn’t there anything wanted? Yes, shall I go to Dolly’s?”\n\nShe looked at him, obviously not hearing what he was saying.\n\n“Yes, yes. Do go,” she said quickly, frowning and waving her hand to him.\n\nHe had just gone into the drawing-room, when suddenly a plaintive moan sounded from the bedroom, smothered instantly. He stood still, and for a long while he could not understand.\n\n“Yes, that is she,” he said to himself, and clutching at his head he ran downstairs.\n\n“Lord have mercy on us! pardon us! aid us!” he repeated the words that for some reason came suddenly to his lips. And he, an unbeliever, repeated these words not with his lips only. At that instant he knew that all his doubts, even the impossibility of believing with his reason, of which he was aware in himself, did not in the least hinder his turning to God. All of that now floated out of his soul like dust. To whom was he to turn if not to Him in whose hands he felt himself, his soul, and his love?\n\nThe horse was not yet ready, but feeling a peculiar concentration of his physical forces and his intellect on what he had to do, he started off on foot without waiting for the horse, and told Kouzma to overtake him.\n\nAt the corner he met a night cabman driving hurriedly. In the little sledge, wrapped in a velvet cloak, sat Lizaveta Petrovna with a kerchief round her head. “Thank God! thank God!” he said, overjoyed to recognize her little fair face which wore a peculiarly serious, even stern expression. Telling the driver not to stop, he ran along beside her.\n\n“For two hours, then? Not more?” she inquired. “You should let Pyotr Dmitrievitch know, but don’t hurry him. And get some opium at the chemist’s.”\n\n“So you think that it may go on well? Lord have mercy on us and help us!” Levin said, seeing his own horse driving out of the gate. Jumping into the sledge beside Kouzma, he told him to drive to the doctor’s.\n\n# Chapter 14\n\nThe doctor was not yet up, and the footman said that “he had been up late, and had given orders not to be waked, but would get up soon.” The footman was cleaning the lamp-chimneys, and seemed very busy about them. This concentration of the footman upon his lamps, and his indifference to what was passing in Levin, at first astounded him, but immediately on considering the question he realized that no one knew or was bound to know his feelings, and that it was all the more necessary to act calmly, sensibly, and resolutely to get through this wall of indifference and attain his aim.\n\n“Don’t be in a hurry or let anything slip,” Levin said to himself, feeling a greater and greater flow of physical energy and attention to all that lay before him to do.\n\nHaving ascertained that the doctor was not getting up, Levin considered various plans, and decided on the following one: that Kouzma should go for another doctor, while he himself should go to the chemist’s for opium, and if when he came back the doctor had not yet begun to get up, he would either by tipping the footman, or by force, wake the doctor at all hazards.\n\nAt the chemist’s the lank shopman sealed up a packet of powders for a coachman who stood waiting, and refused him opium with the same callousness with which the doctor’s footman had cleaned his lamp chimneys. Trying not to get flurried or out of temper, Levin mentioned the names of the doctor and midwife, and explaining what the opium was needed for, tried to persuade him. The assistant inquired in German whether he should give it, and receiving an affirmative reply from behind the partition, he took out a bottle and a funnel, deliberately poured the opium from a bigger bottle into a little one, stuck on a label, sealed it up, in spite of Levin’s request that he would not do so, and was about to wrap it up too. This was more than Levin could stand; he took the bottle firmly out of his hands, and ran to the big glass doors. The doctor was not even now getting up, and the footman, busy now in putting down the rugs, refused to wake him. Levin deliberately took out a ten rouble note, and, careful to speak slowly, though losing no time over the business, he handed him the note, and explained that Pyotr Dmitrievitch (what a great and important personage he seemed to Levin now, this Pyotr Dmitrievitch, who had been of so little consequence in his eyes before!) had promised to come at any time; that he would certainly not be angry! and that he must therefore wake him at once.\n\nThe footman agreed, and went upstairs, taking Levin into the waiting room.\n\nLevin could hear through the door the doctor coughing, moving about, washing, and saying something. Three minutes passed; it seemed to Levin that more than an hour had gone by. He could not wait any longer.\n\n“Pyotr Dmitrievitch, Pyotr Dmitrievitch!” he said in an imploring voice at the open door. “For God’s sake, forgive me! See me as you are. It’s been going on more than two hours already.”\n\n“In a minute; in a minute!” answered a voice, and to his amazement Levin heard that the doctor was smiling as he spoke.\n\n“For one instant.”\n\n“In a minute.”\n\nTwo minutes more passed while the doctor was putting on his boots, and two minutes more while the doctor put on his coat and combed his hair.\n\n“Pyotr Dmitrievitch!” Levin was beginning again in a plaintive voice, just as the doctor came in dressed and ready. “These people have no conscience,” thought Levin. “Combing his hair, while we’re dying!”\n\n“Good morning!” the doctor said to him, shaking hands, and, as it were, teasing him with his composure. “There’s no hurry. Well now?”\n\nTrying to be as accurate as possible, Levin began to tell him every unnecessary detail of his wife’s condition, interrupting his account repeatedly with entreaties that the doctor would come with him at once.\n\n“Oh, you needn’t be in any hurry. You don’t understand, you know. I’m certain I’m not wanted, still I’ve promised, and if you like, I’ll come. But there’s no hurry. Please sit down; won’t you have some coffee?”\n\nLevin stared at him with eyes that asked whether he was laughing at him; but the doctor had no notion of making fun of him.\n\n“I know, I know,” the doctor said, smiling; “I’m a married man myself; and at these moments we husbands are very much to be pitied. I’ve a patient whose husband always takes refuge in the stables on such occasions.”\n\n“But what do you think, Pyotr Dmitrievitch? Do you suppose it may go all right?”\n\n“Everything points to a favorable issue.”\n\n“So you’ll come immediately?” said Levin, looking wrathfully at the servant who was bringing in the coffee.\n\n“In an hour’s time.”\n\n“Oh, for mercy’s sake!”\n\n“Well, let me drink my coffee, anyway.”\n\nThe doctor started upon his coffee. Both were silent.\n\n“The Turks are really getting beaten, though. Did you read yesterday’s telegrams?” said the doctor, munching some roll.\n\n“No, I can’t stand it!” said Levin, jumping up. “So you’ll be with us in a quarter of an hour.”\n\n“In half an hour.”\n\n“On your honor?”\n\nWhen Levin got home, he drove up at the same time as the princess, and they went up to the bedroom door together. The princess had tears in her eyes, and her hands were shaking. Seeing Levin, she embraced him, and burst into tears.\n\n“Well, my dear Lizaveta Petrovna?” she queried, clasping the hand of the midwife, who came out to meet them with a beaming and anxious face.\n\n“She’s going on well,” she said; “persuade her to lie down. She will be easier so.”\n\nFrom the moment when he had waked up and understood what was going on, Levin had prepared his mind to bear resolutely what was before him, and without considering or anticipating anything, to avoid upsetting his wife, and on the contrary to soothe her and keep up her courage. Without allowing himself even to think of what was to come, of how it would end, judging from his inquiries as to the usual duration of these ordeals, Levin had in his imagination braced himself to bear up and to keep a tight rein on his feelings for five hours, and it had seemed to him he could do this. But when he came back from the doctor’s and saw her sufferings again, he fell to repeating more and more frequently: “Lord, have mercy on us, and succor us!” He sighed, and flung his head up, and began to feel afraid he could not bear it, that he would burst into tears or run away. Such agony it was to him. And only one hour had passed.\n\nBut after that hour there passed another hour, two hours, three, the full five hours he had fixed as the furthest limit of his sufferings, and the position was still unchanged; and he was still bearing it because there was nothing to be done but bear it; every instant feeling that he had reached the utmost limits of his endurance, and that his heart would break with sympathy and pain.\n\nBut still the minutes passed by and the hours, and still hours more, and his misery and horror grew and were more and more intense.\n\nAll the ordinary conditions of life, without which one can form no conception of anything, had ceased to exist for Levin. He lost all sense of time. Minutes—those minutes when she sent for him and he held her moist hand, that would squeeze his hand with extraordinary violence and then push it away—seemed to him hours, and hours seemed to him minutes. He was surprised when Lizaveta Petrovna asked him to light a candle behind a screen, and he found that it was five o’clock in the afternoon. If he had been told it was only ten o’clock in the morning, he would not have been more surprised. Where he was all this time, he knew as little as the time of anything. He saw her swollen face, sometimes bewildered and in agony, sometimes smiling and trying to reassure him. He saw the old princess too, flushed and overwrought, with her gray curls in disorder, forcing herself to gulp down her tears, biting her lips; he saw Dolly too and the doctor, smoking fat cigarettes, and Lizaveta Petrovna with a firm, resolute, reassuring face, and the old prince walking up and down the hall with a frowning face. But why they came in and went out, where they were, he did not know. The princess was with the doctor in the bedroom, then in the study, where a table set for dinner suddenly appeared; then she was not there, but Dolly was. Then Levin remembered he had been sent somewhere. Once he had been sent to move a table and sofa. He had done this eagerly, thinking it had to be done for her sake, and only later on he found it was his own bed he had been getting ready. Then he had been sent to the study to ask the doctor something. The doctor had answered and then had said something about the irregularities in the municipal council. Then he had been sent to the bedroom to help the old princess to move the holy picture in its silver and gold setting, and with the princess’s old waiting maid he had clambered on a shelf to reach it and had broken the little lamp, and the old servant had tried to reassure him about the lamp and about his wife, and he carried the holy picture and set it at Kitty’s head, carefully tucking it in behind the pillow. But where, when, and why all this had happened, he could not tell. He did not understand why the old princess took his hand, and looking compassionately at him, begged him not to worry himself, and Dolly persuaded him to eat something and led him out of the room, and even the doctor looked seriously and with commiseration at him and offered him a drop of something.\n\nAll he knew and felt was that what was happening was what had happened nearly a year before in the hotel of the country town at the deathbed of his brother Nikolay. But that had been grief—this was joy. Yet that grief and this joy were alike outside all the ordinary conditions of life; they were loop-holes, as it were, in that ordinary life through which there came glimpses of something sublime. And in the contemplation of this sublime something the soul was exalted to inconceivable heights of which it had before had no conception, while reason lagged behind, unable to keep up with it.\n\n“Lord, have mercy on us, and succor us!” he repeated to himself incessantly, feeling, in spite of his long and, as it seemed, complete alienation from religion, that he turned to God just as trustfully and simply as he had in his childhood and first youth.\n\nAll this time he had two distinct spiritual conditions. One was away from her, with the doctor, who kept smoking one fat cigarette after another and extinguishing them on the edge of a full ashtray, with Dolly, and with the old prince, where there was talk about dinner, about politics, about Marya Petrovna’s illness, and where Levin suddenly forgot for a minute what was happening, and felt as though he had waked up from sleep; the other was in her presence, at her pillow, where his heart seemed breaking and still did not break from sympathetic suffering, and he prayed to God without ceasing. And every time he was brought back from a moment of oblivion by a scream reaching him from the bedroom, he fell into the same strange terror that had come upon him the first minute. Every time he heard a shriek, he jumped up, ran to justify himself, remembered on the way that he was not to blame, and he longed to defend her, to help her. But as he looked at her, he saw again that help was impossible, and he was filled with terror and prayed: “Lord, have mercy on us, and help us!” And as time went on, both these conditions became more intense; the calmer he became away from her, completely forgetting her, the more agonizing became both her sufferings and his feeling of helplessness before them. He jumped up, would have liked to run away, but ran to her.\n\nSometimes, when again and again she called upon him, he blamed her; but seeing her patient, smiling face, and hearing the words, “I am worrying you,” he threw the blame on God; but thinking of God, at once he fell to beseeching God to forgive him and have mercy.\n\n# Chapter 15\n\nHe did not know whether it was late or early. The candles had all burned out. Dolly had just been in the study and had suggested to the doctor that he should lie down. Levin sat listening to the doctor’s stories of a quack mesmerizer and looking at the ashes of his cigarette. There had been a period of repose, and he had sunk into oblivion. He had completely forgotten what was going on now. He heard the doctor’s chat and understood it. Suddenly there came an unearthly shriek. The shriek was so awful that Levin did not even jump up, but holding his breath, gazed in terrified inquiry at the doctor. The doctor put his head on one side, listened, and smiled approvingly. Everything was so extraordinary that nothing could strike Levin as strange. “I suppose it must be so,” he thought, and still sat where he was. Whose scream was this? He jumped up, ran on tiptoe to the bedroom, edged round Lizaveta Petrovna and the princess, and took up his position at Kitty’s pillow. The scream had subsided, but there was some change now. What it was he did not see and did not comprehend, and he had no wish to see or comprehend. But he saw it by the face of Lizaveta Petrovna. Lizaveta Petrovna’s face was stern and pale, and still as resolute, though her jaws were twitching, and her eyes were fixed intently on Kitty. Kitty’s swollen and agonized face, a tress of hair clinging to her moist brow, was turned to him and sought his eyes. Her lifted hands asked for his hands. Clutching his chill hands in her moist ones, she began squeezing them to her face.\n\n“Don’t go, don’t go! I’m not afraid, I’m not afraid!” she said rapidly. “Mamma, take my earrings. They bother me. You’re not afraid? Quick, quick, Lizaveta Petrovna....”\n\nShe spoke quickly, very quickly, and tried to smile. But suddenly her face was drawn, she pushed him away.\n\n“Oh, this is awful! I’m dying, I’m dying! Go away!” she shrieked, and again he heard that unearthly scream.\n\nLevin clutched at his head and ran out of the room.\n\n“It’s nothing, it’s nothing, it’s all right,” Dolly called after him.\n\nBut they might say what they liked, he knew now that all was over. He stood in the next room, his head leaning against the door post, and heard shrieks, howls such as he had never heard before, and he knew that what had been Kitty was uttering these shrieks. He had long ago ceased to wish for the child. By now he loathed this child. He did not even wish for her life now, all he longed for was the end of this awful anguish.\n\n“Doctor! What is it? What is it? By God!” he said, snatching at the doctor’s hand as he came up.\n\n“It’s the end,” said the doctor. And the doctor’s face was so grave as he said it that Levin took the end as meaning her death.\n\nBeside himself, he ran into the bedroom. The first thing he saw was the face of Lizaveta Petrovna. It was even more frowning and stern. Kitty’s face he did not know. In the place where it had been was something that was fearful in its strained distortion and in the sounds that came from it. He fell down with his head on the wooden framework of the bed, feeling that his heart was bursting. The awful scream never paused, it became still more awful, and as though it had reached the utmost limit of terror, suddenly it ceased. Levin could not believe his ears, but there could be no doubt; the scream had ceased and he heard a subdued stir and bustle, and hurried breathing, and her voice, gasping, alive, tender, and blissful, uttered softly, “It’s over!”\n\nHe lifted his head. With her hands hanging exhausted on the quilt, looking extraordinarily lovely and serene, she looked at him in silence and tried to smile, and could not.\n\nAnd suddenly, from the mysterious and awful far-away world in which he had been living for the last twenty-two hours, Levin felt himself all in an instant borne back to the old every-day world, glorified though now, by such a radiance of happiness that he could not bear it. The strained chords snapped, sobs and tears of joy which he had never foreseen rose up with such violence that his whole body shook, that for long they prevented him from speaking.\n\nFalling on his knees before the bed, he held his wife’s hand before his lips and kissed it, and the hand, with a weak movement of the fingers, responded to his kiss. And meanwhile, there at the foot of the bed, in the deft hands of Lizaveta Petrovna, like a flickering light in a lamp, lay the life of a human creature, which had never existed before, and which would now with the same right, with the same importance to itself, live and create in its own image.\n\n“Alive! alive! And a boy too! Set your mind at rest!” Levin heard Lizaveta Petrovna saying, as she slapped the baby’s back with a shaking hand.\n\n“Mamma, is it true?” said Kitty’s voice.\n\nThe princess’s sobs were all the answers she could make. And in the midst of the silence there came in unmistakable reply to the mother’s question, a voice quite unlike the subdued voices speaking in the room. It was the bold, clamorous, self-assertive squall of the new human being, who had so incomprehensibly appeared.\n\nIf Levin had been told before that Kitty was dead, and that he had died with her, and that their children were angels, and that God was standing before him, he would have been surprised at nothing. But now, coming back to the world of reality, he had to make great mental efforts to take in that she was alive and well, and that the creature squalling so desperately was his son. Kitty was alive, her agony was over. And he was unutterably happy. That he understood; he was completely happy in it. But the baby? Whence, why, who was he?... He could not get used to the idea. It seemed to him something extraneous, superfluous, to which he could not accustom himself.\n\n# Chapter 16\n\nAt ten o’clock the old prince, Sergey Ivanovitch, and Stepan Arkadyevitch were sitting at Levin’s. Having inquired after Kitty, they had dropped into conversation upon other subjects. Levin heard them, and unconsciously, as they talked, going over the past, over what had been up to that morning, he thought of himself as he had been yesterday till that point. It was as though a hundred years had passed since then. He felt himself exalted to unattainable heights, from which he studiously lowered himself so as not to wound the people he was talking to. He talked, and was all the time thinking of his wife, of her condition now, of his son, in whose existence he tried to school himself into believing. The whole world of woman, which had taken for him since his marriage a new value he had never suspected before, was now so exalted that he could not take it in in his imagination. He heard them talk of yesterday’s dinner at the club, and thought: “What is happening with her now? Is she asleep? How is she? What is she thinking of? Is he crying, my son Dmitri?” And in the middle of the conversation, in the middle of a sentence, he jumped up and went out of the room.\n\n“Send me word if I can see her,” said the prince.\n\n“Very well, in a minute,” answered Levin, and without stopping, he went to her room.\n\nShe was not asleep, she was talking gently with her mother, making plans about the christening.\n\nCarefully set to rights, with hair well-brushed, in a smart little cap with some blue in it, her arms out on the quilt, she was lying on her back. Meeting his eyes, her eyes drew him to her. Her face, bright before, brightened still more as he drew near her. There was the same change in it from earthly to unearthly that is seen in the face of the dead. But then it means farewell, here it meant welcome. Again a rush of emotion, such as he had felt at the moment of the child’s birth, flooded his heart. She took his hand and asked him if he had slept. He could not answer, and turned away, struggling with his weakness.\n\n“I have had a nap, Kostya!” she said to him; “and I am so comfortable now.”\n\nShe looked at him, but suddenly her expression changed.\n\n“Give him to me,” she said, hearing the baby’s cry. “Give him to me, Lizaveta Petrovna, and he shall look at him.”\n\n“To be sure, his papa shall look at him,” said Lizaveta Petrovna, getting up and bringing something red, and queer, and wriggling. “Wait a minute, we’ll make him tidy first,” and Lizaveta Petrovna laid the red wobbling thing on the bed, began untrussing and trussing up the baby, lifting it up and turning it over with one finger and powdering it with something.\n\nLevin, looking at the tiny, pitiful creature, made strenuous efforts to discover in his heart some traces of fatherly feeling for it. He felt nothing towards it but disgust. But when it was undressed and he caught a glimpse of wee, wee, little hands, little feet, saffron-colored, with little toes, too, and positively with a little big toe different from the rest, and when he saw Lizaveta Petrovna closing the wide-open little hands, as though they were soft springs, and putting them into linen garments, such pity for the little creature came upon him, and such terror that she would hurt it, that he held her hand back.\n\nLizaveta Petrovna laughed.\n\n“Don’t be frightened, don’t be frightened!”\n\nWhen the baby had been put to rights and transformed into a firm doll, Lizaveta Petrovna dandled it as though proud of her handiwork, and stood a little away so that Levin might see his son in all his glory.\n\nKitty looked sideways in the same direction, never taking her eyes off the baby. “Give him to me! give him to me!” she said, and even made as though she would sit up.\n\n“What are you thinking of, Katerina Alexandrovna, you mustn’t move like that! Wait a minute. I’ll give him to you. Here we’re showing papa what a fine fellow we are!”\n\nAnd Lizaveta Petrovna, with one hand supporting the wobbling head, lifted up on the other arm the strange, limp, red creature, whose head was lost in its swaddling clothes. But it had a nose, too, and slanting eyes and smacking lips.\n\n“A splendid baby!” said Lizaveta Petrovna.\n\nLevin sighed with mortification. This splendid baby excited in him no feeling but disgust and compassion. It was not at all the feeling he had looked forward to.\n\nHe turned away while Lizaveta Petrovna put the baby to the unaccustomed breast.\n\nSuddenly laughter made him look round. The baby had taken the breast.\n\n“Come, that’s enough, that’s enough!” said Lizaveta Petrovna, but Kitty would not let the baby go. He fell asleep in her arms.\n\n“Look, now,” said Kitty, turning the baby so that he could see it. The aged-looking little face suddenly puckered up still more and the baby sneezed.\n\nSmiling, hardly able to restrain his tears, Levin kissed his wife and went out of the dark room. What he felt towards this little creature was utterly unlike what he had expected. There was nothing cheerful and joyous in the feeling; on the contrary, it was a new torture of apprehension. It was the consciousness of a new sphere of liability to pain. And this sense was so painful at first, the apprehension lest this helpless creature should suffer was so intense, that it prevented him from noticing the strange thrill of senseless joy and even pride that he had felt when the baby sneezed.\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch’s affairs were in a very bad way.\n\nThe money for two-thirds of the forest had all been spent already, and he had borrowed from the merchant in advance at ten per cent discount, almost all the remaining third. The merchant would not give more, especially as Darya Alexandrovna, for the first time that winter insisting on her right to her own property, had refused to sign the receipt for the payment of the last third of the forest. All his salary went on household expenses and in payment of petty debts that could not be put off. There was positively no money.\n\nThis was unpleasant and awkward, and in Stepan Arkadyevitch’s opinion things could not go on like this. The explanation of the position was, in his view, to be found in the fact that his salary was too small. The post he filled had been unmistakably very good five years ago, but it was so no longer.\n\nPetrov, the bank director, had twelve thousand; Sventitsky, a company director, had seventeen thousand; Mitin, who had founded a bank, received fifty thousand.\n\n“Clearly I’ve been napping, and they’ve overlooked me,” Stepan Arkadyevitch thought about himself. And he began keeping his eyes and ears open, and towards the end of the winter he had discovered a very good berth and had formed a plan of attack upon it, at first from Moscow through aunts, uncles, and friends, and then, when the matter was well advanced, in the spring, he went himself to Petersburg. It was one of those snug, lucrative berths of which there are so many more nowadays than there used to be, with incomes ranging from one thousand to fifty thousand roubles. It was the post of secretary of the committee of the amalgamated agency of the southern railways, and of certain banking companies. This position, like all such appointments, called for such immense energy and such varied qualifications, that it was difficult for them to be found united in any one man. And since a man combining all the qualifications was not to be found, it was at least better that the post be filled by an honest than by a dishonest man. And Stepan Arkadyevitch was not merely an honest man—unemphatically—in the common acceptation of the words, he was an honest man—emphatically—in that special sense which the word has in Moscow, when they talk of an “honest” politician, an “honest” writer, an “honest” newspaper, an “honest” institution, an “honest” tendency, meaning not simply that the man or the institution is not dishonest, but that they are capable on occasion of taking a line of their own in opposition to the authorities.\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch moved in those circles in Moscow in which that expression had come into use, was regarded there as an honest man, and so had more right to this appointment than others.\n\nThe appointment yielded an income of from seven to ten thousand a year, and Oblonsky could fill it without giving up his government position. It was in the hands of two ministers, one lady, and two Jews, and all these people, though the way had been paved already with them, Stepan Arkadyevitch had to see in Petersburg. Besides this business, Stepan Arkadyevitch had promised his sister Anna to obtain from Karenin a definite answer on the question of divorce. And begging fifty roubles from Dolly, he set off for Petersburg.\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch sat in Karenin’s study listening to his report on the causes of the unsatisfactory position of Russian finance, and only waiting for the moment when he would finish to speak about his own business or about Anna.\n\n“Yes, that’s very true,” he said, when Alexey Alexandrovitch took off the pince-nez, without which he could not read now, and looked inquiringly at his former brother-in-law, “that’s very true in particular cases, but still the principle of our day is freedom.”\n\n“Yes, but I lay down another principle, embracing the principle of freedom,” said Alexey Alexandrovitch, with emphasis on the word “embracing,” and he put on his pince-nez again, so as to read the passage in which this statement was made. And turning over the beautifully written, wide-margined manuscript, Alexey Alexandrovitch read aloud over again the conclusive passage.\n\n“I don’t advocate protection for the sake of private interests, but for the public weal, and for the lower and upper classes equally,” he said, looking over his pince-nez at Oblonsky. “But they cannot grasp that, they are taken up now with personal interests, and carried away by phrases.”\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch knew that when Karenin began to talk of what they were doing and thinking, the persons who would not accept his report and were the cause of everything wrong in Russia, that it was coming near the end. And so now he eagerly abandoned the principle of free-trade, and fully agreed. Alexey Alexandrovitch paused, thoughtfully turning over the pages of his manuscript.\n\n“Oh, by the way,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, “I wanted to ask you, some time when you see Pomorsky, to drop him a hint that I should be very glad to get that new appointment of secretary of the committee of the amalgamated agency of the southern railways and banking companies.” Stepan Arkadyevitch was familiar by now with the title of the post he coveted, and he brought it out rapidly without mistake.\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch questioned him as to the duties of this new committee, and pondered. He was considering whether the new committee would not be acting in some way contrary to the views he had been advocating. But as the influence of the new committee was of a very complex nature, and his views were of very wide application, he could not decide this straight off, and taking off his pince-nez, he said:\n\n“Of course, I can mention it to him; but what is your reason precisely for wishing to obtain the appointment?”\n\n“It’s a good salary, rising to nine thousand, and my means....”\n\n“Nine thousand!” repeated Alexey Alexandrovitch, and he frowned. The high figure of the salary made him reflect that on that side Stepan Arkadyevitch’s proposed position ran counter to the main tendency of his own projects of reform, which always leaned towards economy.\n\n“I consider, and I have embodied my views in a note on the subject, that in our day these immense salaries are evidence of the unsound economic assiette of our finances.”\n\n“But what’s to be done?” said Stepan Arkadyevitch. “Suppose a bank director gets ten thousand—well, he’s worth it; or an engineer gets twenty thousand—after all, it’s a growing thing, you know!”\n\n“I assume that a salary is the price paid for a commodity, and it ought to conform with the law of supply and demand. If the salary is fixed without any regard for that law, as, for instance, when I see two engineers leaving college together, both equally well trained and efficient, and one getting forty thousand while the other is satisfied with two; or when I see lawyers and hussars, having no special qualifications, appointed directors of banking companies with immense salaries, I conclude that the salary is not fixed in accordance with the law of supply and demand, but simply through personal interest. And this is an abuse of great gravity in itself, and one that reacts injuriously on the government service. I consider....”\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch made haste to interrupt his brother-in-law.\n\n“Yes; but you must agree that it’s a new institution of undoubted utility that’s being started. After all, you know, it’s a growing thing! What they lay particular stress on is the thing being carried on honestly,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch with emphasis.\n\nBut the Moscow significance of the word “honest” was lost on Alexey Alexandrovitch.\n\n“Honesty is only a negative qualification,” he said.\n\n“Well, you’ll do me a great service, anyway,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, “by putting in a word to Pomorsky—just in the way of conversation....”\n\n“But I fancy it’s more in Volgarinov’s hands,” said Alexey Alexandrovitch.\n\n“Volgarinov has fully assented, as far as he’s concerned,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, turning red. Stepan Arkadyevitch reddened at the mention of that name, because he had been that morning at the Jew Volgarinov’s, and the visit had left an unpleasant recollection.\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch believed most positively that the committee in which he was trying to get an appointment was a new, genuine, and honest public body, but that morning when Volgarinov had—intentionally, beyond a doubt—kept him two hours waiting with other petitioners in his waiting room, he had suddenly felt uneasy.\n\nWhether he was uncomfortable that he, a descendant of Rurik, Prince Oblonsky, had been kept for two hours waiting to see a Jew, or that for the first time in his life he was not following the example of his ancestors in serving the government, but was turning off into a new career, anyway he was very uncomfortable. During those two hours in Volgarinov’s waiting room Stepan Arkadyevitch, stepping jauntily about the room, pulling his whiskers, entering into conversation with the other petitioners, and inventing an epigram on his position, assiduously concealed from others, and even from himself, the feeling he was experiencing.\n\nBut all the time he was uncomfortable and angry, he could not have said why—whether because he could not get his epigram just right, or from some other reason. When at last Volgarinov had received him with exaggerated politeness and unmistakable triumph at his humiliation, and had all but refused the favor asked of him, Stepan Arkadyevitch had made haste to forget it all as soon as possible. And now, at the mere recollection, he blushed.\n\n# Chapter 18\n\n“Now there is something I want to talk about, and you know what it is. About Anna,” Stepan Arkadyevitch said, pausing for a brief space, and shaking off the unpleasant impression.\n\nAs soon as Oblonsky uttered Anna’s name, the face of Alexey Alexandrovitch was completely transformed; all the life was gone out of it, and it looked weary and dead.\n\n“What is it exactly that you want from me?” he said, moving in his chair and snapping his pince-nez.\n\n“A definite settlement, Alexey Alexandrovitch, some settlement of the position. I’m appealing to you” (“not as an injured husband,” Stepan Arkadyevitch was going to say, but afraid of wrecking his negotiation by this, he changed the words) “not as a statesman” (which did not sound à propos), “but simply as a man, and a good-hearted man and a Christian. You must have pity on her,” he said.\n\n“That is, in what way precisely?” Karenin said softly.\n\n“Yes, pity on her. If you had seen her as I have!—I have been spending all the winter with her—you would have pity on her. Her position is awful, simply awful!”\n\n“I had imagined,” answered Alexey Alexandrovitch in a higher, almost shrill voice, “that Anna Arkadyevna had everything she had desired for herself.”\n\n“Oh, Alexey Alexandrovitch, for heaven’s sake, don’t let us indulge in recriminations! What is past is past, and you know what she wants and is waiting for—divorce.”\n\n“But I believe Anna Arkadyevna refuses a divorce, if I make it a condition to leave me my son. I replied in that sense, and supposed that the matter was ended. I consider it at an end,” shrieked Alexey Alexandrovitch.\n\n“But, for heaven’s sake, don’t get hot!” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, touching his brother-in-law’s knee. “The matter is not ended. If you will allow me to recapitulate, it was like this: when you parted, you were as magnanimous as could possibly be; you were ready to give her everything—freedom, divorce even. She appreciated that. No, don’t think that. She did appreciate it—to such a degree that at the first moment, feeling how she had wronged you, she did not consider and could not consider everything. She gave up everything. But experience, time, have shown that her position is unbearable, impossible.”\n\n“The life of Anna Arkadyevna can have no interest for me,” Alexey Alexandrovitch put in, lifting his eyebrows.\n\n“Allow me to disbelieve that,” Stepan Arkadyevitch replied gently. “Her position is intolerable for her, and of no benefit to anyone whatever. She has deserved it, you will say. She knows that and asks you for nothing; she says plainly that she dare not ask you. But I, all of us, her relatives, all who love her, beg you, entreat you. Why should she suffer? Who is any the better for it?”\n\n“Excuse me, you seem to put me in the position of the guilty party,” observed Alexey Alexandrovitch.\n\n“Oh, no, oh, no, not at all! please understand me,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, touching his hand again, as though feeling sure this physical contact would soften his brother-in-law. “All I say is this: her position is intolerable, and it might be alleviated by you, and you will lose nothing by it. I will arrange it all for you, so that you’ll not notice it. You did promise it, you know.”\n\n“The promise was given before. And I had supposed that the question of my son had settled the matter. Besides, I had hoped that Anna Arkadyevna had enough generosity....” Alexey Alexandrovitch articulated with difficulty, his lips twitching and his face white.\n\n“She leaves it all to your generosity. She begs, she implores one thing of you—to extricate her from the impossible position in which she is placed. She does not ask for her son now. Alexey Alexandrovitch, you are a good man. Put yourself in her position for a minute. The question of divorce for her in her position is a question of life and death. If you had not promised it once, she would have reconciled herself to her position, she would have gone on living in the country. But you promised it, and she wrote to you, and moved to Moscow. And here she’s been for six months in Moscow, where every chance meeting cuts her to the heart, every day expecting an answer. Why, it’s like keeping a condemned criminal for six months with the rope round his neck, promising him perhaps death, perhaps mercy. Have pity on her, and I will undertake to arrange everything. Vos scrupules....”\n\n“I am not talking about that, about that....” Alexey Alexandrovitch interrupted with disgust. “But, perhaps, I promised what I had no right to promise.”\n\n“So you go back from your promise?”\n\n“I have never refused to do all that is possible, but I want time to consider how much of what I promised is possible.”\n\n“No, Alexey Alexandrovitch!” cried Oblonsky, jumping up, “I won’t believe that! She’s unhappy as only an unhappy woman can be, and you cannot refuse in such....”\n\n“As much of what I promised as is possible. Vous professez d’être libre penseur. But I as a believer cannot, in a matter of such gravity, act in opposition to the Christian law.”\n\n“But in Christian societies and among us, as far as I’m aware, divorce is allowed,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch. “Divorce is sanctioned even by our church. And we see....”\n\n“It is allowed, but not in the sense....”\n\n“Alexey Alexandrovitch, you are not like yourself,” said Oblonsky, after a brief pause. “Wasn’t it you (and didn’t we all appreciate it in you?) who forgave everything, and moved simply by Christian feeling was ready to make any sacrifice? You said yourself: if a man take thy coat, give him thy cloak also, and now....”\n\n“I beg,” said Alexey Alexandrovitch shrilly, getting suddenly onto his feet, his face white and his jaws twitching, “I beg you to drop this ... to drop ... this subject!”\n\n“Oh, no! Oh, forgive me, forgive me if I have wounded you,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, holding out his hand with a smile of embarrassment; “but like a messenger I have simply performed the commission given me.”\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch gave him his hand, pondered a little, and said:\n\n“I must think it over and seek for guidance. The day after tomorrow I will give you a final answer,” he said, after considering a moment.\n\n# Chapter 19\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch was about to go away when Korney came in to announce:\n\n“Sergey Alexyevitch!”\n\n“Who’s Sergey Alexyevitch?” Stepan Arkadyevitch was beginning, but he remembered immediately.\n\n“Ah, Seryozha!” he said aloud. “Sergey Alexyevitch! I thought it was the director of a department. Anna asked me to see him too,” he thought.\n\nAnd he recalled the timid, piteous expression with which Anna had said to him at parting: “Anyway, you will see him. Find out exactly where he is, who is looking after him. And Stiva ... if it were possible! Could it be possible?” Stepan Arkadyevitch knew what was meant by that “if it were possible,”—if it were possible to arrange the divorce so as to let her have her son.... Stepan Arkadyevitch saw now that it was no good to dream of that, but still he was glad to see his nephew.\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch reminded his brother-in-law that they never spoke to the boy of his mother, and he begged him not to mention a single word about her.\n\n“He was very ill after that interview with his mother, which we had not foreseen,” said Alexey Alexandrovitch. “Indeed, we feared for his life. But with rational treatment, and sea-bathing in the summer, he regained his strength, and now, by the doctor’s advice, I have let him go to school. And certainly the companionship of school has had a good effect on him, and he is perfectly well, and making good progress.”\n\n“What a fine fellow he’s grown! He’s not Seryozha now, but quite full-fledged Sergey Alexyevitch!” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, smiling, as he looked at the handsome, broad-shouldered lad in blue coat and long trousers, who walked in alertly and confidently. The boy looked healthy and good-humored. He bowed to his uncle as to a stranger, but recognizing him, he blushed and turned hurriedly away from him, as though offended and irritated at something. The boy went up to his father and handed him a note of the marks he had gained in school.\n\n“Well, that’s very fair,” said his father, “you can go.”\n\n“He’s thinner and taller, and has grown out of being a child into a boy; I like that,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch. “Do you remember me?”\n\nThe boy looked back quickly at his uncle.\n\n“Yes, mon oncle,” he answered, glancing at his father, and again he looked downcast.\n\nHis uncle called him to him, and took his hand.\n\n“Well, and how are you getting on?” he said, wanting to talk to him, and not knowing what to say.\n\nThe boy, blushing and making no answer, cautiously drew his hand away. As soon as Stepan Arkadyevitch let go his hand, he glanced doubtfully at his father, and like a bird set free, he darted out of the room.\n\nA year had passed since the last time Seryozha had seen his mother. Since then he had heard nothing more of her. And in the course of that year he had gone to school, and made friends among his schoolfellows. The dreams and memories of his mother, which had made him ill after seeing her, did not occupy his thoughts now. When they came back to him, he studiously drove them away, regarding them as shameful and girlish, below the dignity of a boy and a schoolboy. He knew that his father and mother were separated by some quarrel, he knew that he had to remain with his father, and he tried to get used to that idea.\n\nHe disliked seeing his uncle, so like his mother, for it called up those memories of which he was ashamed. He disliked it all the more as from some words he had caught as he waited at the study door, and still more from the faces of his father and uncle, he guessed that they must have been talking of his mother. And to avoid condemning the father with whom he lived and on whom he was dependent, and, above all, to avoid giving way to sentimentality, which he considered so degrading, Seryozha tried not to look at his uncle who had come to disturb his peace of mind, and not to think of what he recalled to him.\n\nBut when Stepan Arkadyevitch, going out after him, saw him on the stairs, and calling to him, asked him how he spent his playtime at school, Seryozha talked more freely to him away from his father’s presence.\n\n“We have a railway now,” he said in answer to his uncle’s question. “It’s like this, do you see: two sit on a bench—they’re the passengers; and one stands up straight on the bench. And all are harnessed to it by their arms or by their belts, and they run through all the rooms—the doors are left open beforehand. Well, and it’s pretty hard work being the conductor!”\n\n“That’s the one that stands?” Stepan Arkadyevitch inquired, smiling.\n\n“Yes, you want pluck for it, and cleverness too, especially when they stop all of a sudden, or someone falls down.”\n\n“Yes, that must be a serious matter,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, watching with mournful interest the eager eyes, like his mother’s; not childish now—no longer fully innocent. And though he had promised Alexey Alexandrovitch not to speak of Anna, he could not restrain himself.\n\n“Do you remember your mother?” he asked suddenly.\n\n“No, I don’t,” Seryozha said quickly. He blushed crimson, and his face clouded over. And his uncle could get nothing more out of him. His tutor found his pupil on the staircase half an hour later, and for a long while he could not make out whether he was ill-tempered or crying.\n\n“What is it? I expect you hurt yourself when you fell down?” said the tutor. “I told you it was a dangerous game. And we shall have to speak to the director.”\n\n“If I had hurt myself, nobody should have found it out, that’s certain.”\n\n“Well, what is it, then?”\n\n“Leave me alone! If I remember, or if I don’t remember?... what business is it of his? Why should I remember? Leave me in peace!” he said, addressing not his tutor, but the whole world.\n\n# Chapter 20\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch, as usual, did not waste his time in Petersburg. In Petersburg, besides business, his sister’s divorce, and his coveted appointment, he wanted, as he always did, to freshen himself up, as he said, after the mustiness of Moscow.\n\nIn spite of its cafés chantants and its omnibuses, Moscow was yet a stagnant bog. Stepan Arkadyevitch always felt it. After living for some time in Moscow, especially in close relations with his family, he was conscious of a depression of spirits. After being a long time in Moscow without a change, he reached a point when he positively began to be worrying himself over his wife’s ill-humor and reproaches, over his children’s health and education, and the petty details of his official work; even the fact of being in debt worried him. But he had only to go and stay a little while in Petersburg, in the circle there in which he moved, where people lived—really lived—instead of vegetating as in Moscow, and all such ideas vanished and melted away at once, like wax before the fire. His wife?... Only that day he had been talking to Prince Tchetchensky. Prince Tchetchensky had a wife and family, grown-up pages in the corps, ... and he had another illegitimate family of children also. Though the first family was very nice too, Prince Tchetchensky felt happier in his second family; and he used to take his eldest son with him to his second family, and told Stepan Arkadyevitch that he thought it good for his son, enlarging his ideas. What would have been said to that in Moscow?\n\nHis children? In Petersburg children did not prevent their parents from enjoying life. The children were brought up in schools, and there was no trace of the wild idea that prevailed in Moscow, in Lvov’s household, for instance, that all the luxuries of life were for the children, while the parents have nothing but work and anxiety. Here people understood that a man is in duty bound to live for himself, as every man of culture should live.\n\nHis official duties? Official work here was not the stiff, hopeless drudgery that it was in Moscow. Here there was some interest in official life. A chance meeting, a service rendered, a happy phrase, a knack of facetious mimicry, and a man’s career might be made in a trice. So it had been with Bryantsev, whom Stepan Arkadyevitch had met the previous day, and who was one of the highest functionaries in government now. There was some interest in official work like that.\n\nThe Petersburg attitude on pecuniary matters had an especially soothing effect on Stepan Arkadyevitch. Bartnyansky, who must spend at least fifty thousand to judge by the style he lived in, had made an interesting comment the day before on that subject.\n\nAs they were talking before dinner, Stepan Arkadyevitch said to Bartnyansky:\n\n“You’re friendly, I fancy, with Mordvinsky; you might do me a favor: say a word to him, please, for me. There’s an appointment I should like to get—secretary of the agency....”\n\n“Oh, I shan’t remember all that, if you tell it to me.... But what possesses you to have to do with railways and Jews?... Take it as you will, it’s a low business.”\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch did not say to Bartnyansky that it was a “growing thing”—Bartnyansky would not have understood that.\n\n“I want the money, I’ve nothing to live on.”\n\n“You’re living, aren’t you?”\n\n“Yes, but in debt.”\n\n“Are you, though? Heavily?” said Bartnyansky sympathetically.\n\n“Very heavily: twenty thousand.”\n\nBartnyansky broke into good-humored laughter.\n\n“Oh, lucky fellow!” said he. “My debts mount up to a million and a half, and I’ve nothing, and still I can live, as you see!”\n\nAnd Stepan Arkadyevitch saw the correctness of this view not in words only but in actual fact. Zhivahov owed three hundred thousand, and hadn’t a farthing to bless himself with, and he lived, and in style too! Count Krivtsov was considered a hopeless case by everyone, and yet he kept two mistresses. Petrovsky had run through five millions, and still lived in just the same style, and was even a manager in the financial department with a salary of twenty thousand. But besides this, Petersburg had physically an agreeable effect on Stepan Arkadyevitch. It made him younger. In Moscow he sometimes found a gray hair in his head, dropped asleep after dinner, stretched, walked slowly upstairs, breathing heavily, was bored by the society of young women, and did not dance at balls. In Petersburg he always felt ten years younger.\n\nHis experience in Petersburg was exactly what had been described to him on the previous day by Prince Pyotr Oblonsky, a man of sixty, who had just come back from abroad:\n\n“We don’t know the way to live here,” said Pyotr Oblonsky. “I spent the summer in Baden, and you wouldn’t believe it, I felt quite a young man. At a glimpse of a pretty woman, my thoughts.... One dines and drinks a glass of wine, and feels strong and ready for anything. I came home to Russia—had to see my wife, and, what’s more, go to my country place; and there, you’d hardly believe it, in a fortnight I’d got into a dressing gown and given up dressing for dinner. Needn’t say I had no thoughts left for pretty women. I became quite an old gentleman. There was nothing left for me but to think of my eternal salvation. I went off to Paris—I was as right as could be at once.”\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch felt exactly the difference that Pyotr Oblonsky described. In Moscow he degenerated so much that if he had had to be there for long together, he might in good earnest have come to considering his salvation; in Petersburg he felt himself a man of the world again.\n\nBetween Princess Betsy Tverskaya and Stepan Arkadyevitch there had long existed rather curious relations. Stepan Arkadyevitch always flirted with her in jest, and used to say to her, also in jest, the most unseemly things, knowing that nothing delighted her so much. The day after his conversation with Karenin, Stepan Arkadyevitch went to see her, and felt so youthful that in this jesting flirtation and nonsense he recklessly went so far that he did not know how to extricate himself, as unluckily he was so far from being attracted by her that he thought her positively disagreeable. What made it hard to change the conversation was the fact that he was very attractive to her. So that he was considerably relieved at the arrival of Princess Myakaya, which cut short their tête-à-tête.\n\n“Ah, so you’re here!” said she when she saw him. “Well, and what news of your poor sister? You needn’t look at me like that,” she added. “Ever since they’ve all turned against her, all those who’re a thousand times worse than she, I’ve thought she did a very fine thing. I can’t forgive Vronsky for not letting me know when she was in Petersburg. I’d have gone to see her and gone about with her everywhere. Please give her my love. Come, tell me about her.”\n\n“Yes, her position is very difficult; she....” began Stepan Arkadyevitch, in the simplicity of his heart accepting as sterling coin Princess Myakaya’s words “tell me about her.” Princess Myakaya interrupted him immediately, as she always did, and began talking herself.\n\n“She’s done what they all do, except me—only they hide it. But she wouldn’t be deceitful, and she did a fine thing. And she did better still in throwing up that crazy brother-in-law of yours. You must excuse me. Everybody used to say he was so clever, so very clever; I was the only one that said he was a fool. Now that he’s so thick with Lidia Ivanovna and Landau, they all say he’s crazy, and I should prefer not to agree with everybody, but this time I can’t help it.”\n\n“Oh, do please explain,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch; “what does it mean? Yesterday I was seeing him on my sister’s behalf, and I asked him to give me a final answer. He gave me no answer, and said he would think it over. But this morning, instead of an answer, I received an invitation from Countess Lidia Ivanovna for this evening.”\n\n“Ah, so that’s it, that’s it!” said Princess Myakaya gleefully, “they’re going to ask Landau what he’s to say.”\n\n“Ask Landau? What for? Who or what’s Landau?”\n\n“What! you don’t know Jules Landau, le fameux Jules Landau, le clairvoyant? He’s crazy too, but on him your sister’s fate depends. See what comes of living in the provinces—you know nothing about anything. Landau, do you see, was a commis in a shop in Paris, and he went to a doctor’s; and in the doctor’s waiting room he fell asleep, and in his sleep he began giving advice to all the patients. And wonderful advice it was! Then the wife of Yury Meledinsky—you know, the invalid?—heard of this Landau, and had him to see her husband. And he cured her husband, though I can’t say that I see he did him much good, for he’s just as feeble a creature as ever he was, but they believed in him, and took him along with them and brought him to Russia. Here there’s been a general rush to him, and he’s begun doctoring everyone. He cured Countess Bezzubova, and she took such a fancy to him that she adopted him.”\n\n“Adopted him?”\n\n“Yes, as her son. He’s not Landau any more now, but Count Bezzubov. That’s neither here nor there, though; but Lidia—I’m very fond of her, but she has a screw loose somewhere—has lost her heart to this Landau now, and nothing is settled now in her house or Alexey Alexandrovitch’s without him, and so your sister’s fate is now in the hands of Landau, alias Count Bezzubov.”\n\n# Chapter 21\n\nAfter a capital dinner and a great deal of cognac drunk at Bartnyansky’s, Stepan Arkadyevitch, only a little later than the appointed time, went in to Countess Lidia Ivanovna’s.\n\n“Who else is with the countess?—a Frenchman?” Stepan Arkadyevitch asked the hall-porter, as he glanced at the familiar overcoat of Alexey Alexandrovitch and a queer, rather artless-looking overcoat with clasps.\n\n“Alexey Alexandrovitch Karenin and Count Bezzubov,” the porter answered severely.\n\n“Princess Myakaya guessed right,” thought Stepan Arkadyevitch, as he went upstairs. “Curious! It would be quite as well, though, to get on friendly terms with her. She has immense influence. If she would say a word to Pomorsky, the thing would be a certainty.”\n\nIt was still quite light out-of-doors, but in Countess Lidia Ivanovna’s little drawing-room the blinds were drawn and the lamps lighted. At a round table under a lamp sat the countess and Alexey Alexandrovitch, talking softly. A short, thinnish man, very pale and handsome, with feminine hips and knock-kneed legs, with fine brilliant eyes and long hair lying on the collar of his coat, was standing at the end of the room gazing at the portraits on the wall. After greeting the lady of the house and Alexey Alexandrovitch, Stepan Arkadyevitch could not resist glancing once more at the unknown man.\n\n“Monsieur Landau!” the countess addressed him with a softness and caution that impressed Oblonsky. And she introduced them.\n\nLandau looked round hurriedly, came up, and smiling, laid his moist, lifeless hand in Stepan Arkadyevitch’s outstretched hand and immediately walked away and fell to gazing at the portraits again. The countess and Alexey Alexandrovitch looked at each other significantly.\n\n“I am very glad to see you, particularly today,” said Countess Lidia Ivanovna, pointing Stepan Arkadyevitch to a seat beside Karenin.\n\n“I introduced you to him as Landau,” she said in a soft voice, glancing at the Frenchman and again immediately after at Alexey Alexandrovitch, “but he is really Count Bezzubov, as you’re probably aware. Only he does not like the title.”\n\n“Yes, I heard so,” answered Stepan Arkadyevitch; “they say he completely cured Countess Bezzubova.”\n\n“She was here today, poor thing!” the countess said, turning to Alexey Alexandrovitch. “This separation is awful for her. It’s such a blow to her!”\n\n“And he positively is going?” queried Alexey Alexandrovitch.\n\n“Yes, he’s going to Paris. He heard a voice yesterday,” said Countess Lidia Ivanovna, looking at Stepan Arkadyevitch.\n\n“Ah, a voice!” repeated Oblonsky, feeling that he must be as circumspect as he possibly could in this society, where something peculiar was going on, or was to go on, to which he had not the key.\n\nA moment’s silence followed, after which Countess Lidia Ivanovna, as though approaching the main topic of conversation, said with a fine smile to Oblonsky:\n\n“I’ve known you for a long while, and am very glad to make a closer acquaintance with you. Les amis de nos amis sont nos amis. But to be a true friend, one must enter into the spiritual state of one’s friend, and I fear that you are not doing so in the case of Alexey Alexandrovitch. You understand what I mean?” she said, lifting her fine pensive eyes.\n\n“In part, countess, I understand the position of Alexey Alexandrovitch....” said Oblonsky. Having no clear idea what they were talking about, he wanted to confine himself to generalities.\n\n“The change is not in his external position,” Countess Lidia Ivanovna said sternly, following with eyes of love the figure of Alexey Alexandrovitch as he got up and crossed over to Landau; “his heart is changed, a new heart has been vouchsafed him, and I fear you don’t fully apprehend the change that has taken place in him.”\n\n“Oh, well, in general outlines I can conceive the change. We have always been friendly, and now....” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, responding with a sympathetic glance to the expression of the countess, and mentally balancing the question with which of the two ministers she was most intimate, so as to know about which to ask her to speak for him.\n\n“The change that has taken place in him cannot lessen his love for his neighbors; on the contrary, that change can only intensify love in his heart. But I am afraid you do not understand me. Won’t you have some tea?” she said, with her eyes indicating the footman, who was handing round tea on a tray.\n\n“Not quite, countess. Of course, his misfortune....”\n\n“Yes, a misfortune which has proved the highest happiness, when his heart was made new, was filled full of it,” she said, gazing with eyes full of love at Stepan Arkadyevitch.\n\n“I do believe I might ask her to speak to both of them,” thought Stepan Arkadyevitch.\n\n“Oh, of course, countess,” he said; “but I imagine such changes are a matter so private that no one, even the most intimate friend, would care to speak of them.”\n\n“On the contrary! We ought to speak freely and help one another.”\n\n“Yes, undoubtedly so, but there is such a difference of convictions, and besides....” said Oblonsky with a soft smile.\n\n“There can be no difference where it is a question of holy truth.”\n\n“Oh, no, of course; but....” and Stepan Arkadyevitch paused in confusion. He understood at last that they were talking of religion.\n\n“I fancy he will fall asleep immediately,” said Alexey Alexandrovitch in a whisper full of meaning, going up to Lidia Ivanovna.\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch looked round. Landau was sitting at the window, leaning on his elbow and the back of his chair, his head drooping. Noticing that all eyes were turned on him he raised his head and smiled a smile of childlike artlessness.\n\n“Don’t take any notice,” said Lidia Ivanovna, and she lightly moved a chair up for Alexey Alexandrovitch. “I have observed....” she was beginning, when a footman came into the room with a letter. Lidia Ivanovna rapidly ran her eyes over the note, and excusing herself, wrote an answer with extraordinary rapidity, handed it to the man, and came back to the table. “I have observed,” she went on, “that Moscow people, especially the men, are more indifferent to religion than anyone.”\n\n“Oh, no, countess, I thought Moscow people had the reputation of being the firmest in the faith,” answered Stepan Arkadyevitch.\n\n“But as far as I can make out, you are unfortunately one of the indifferent ones,” said Alexey Alexandrovitch, turning to him with a weary smile.\n\n“How anyone can be indifferent!” said Lidia Ivanovna.\n\n“I am not so much indifferent on that subject as I am waiting in suspense,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, with his most deprecating smile. “I hardly think that the time for such questions has come yet for me.”\n\nAlexey Alexandrovitch and Lidia Ivanovna looked at each other.\n\n“We can never tell whether the time has come for us or not,” said Alexey Alexandrovitch severely. “We ought not to think whether we are ready or not ready. God’s grace is not guided by human considerations: sometimes it comes not to those that strive for it, and comes to those that are unprepared, like Saul.”\n\n“No, I believe it won’t be just yet,” said Lidia Ivanovna, who had been meanwhile watching the movements of the Frenchman. Landau got up and came to them.\n\n“Do you allow me to listen?” he asked.\n\n“Oh, yes; I did not want to disturb you,” said Lidia Ivanovna, gazing tenderly at him; “sit here with us.”\n\n“One has only not to close one’s eyes to shut out the light,” Alexey Alexandrovitch went on.\n\n“Ah, if you knew the happiness we know, feeling His presence ever in our hearts!” said Countess Lidia Ivanovna with a rapturous smile.\n\n“But a man may feel himself unworthy sometimes to rise to that height,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, conscious of hypocrisy in admitting this religious height, but at the same time unable to bring himself to acknowledge his free-thinking views before a person who, by a single word to Pomorsky, might procure him the coveted appointment.\n\n“That is, you mean that sin keeps him back?” said Lidia Ivanovna. “But that is a false idea. There is no sin for believers, their sin has been atoned for. Pardon,” she added, looking at the footman, who came in again with another letter. She read it and gave a verbal answer: “Tomorrow at the Grand Duchess’s, say.” “For the believer sin is not,” she went on.\n\n“Yes, but faith without works is dead,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, recalling the phrase from the catechism, and only by his smile clinging to his independence.\n\n“There you have it—from the epistle of St. James,” said Alexey Alexandrovitch, addressing Lidia Ivanovna, with a certain reproachfulness in his tone. It was unmistakably a subject they had discussed more than once before. “What harm has been done by the false interpretation of that passage! Nothing holds men back from belief like that misinterpretation. ‘I have not works, so I cannot believe,’ though all the while that is not said. But the very opposite is said.”\n\n“Striving for God, saving the soul by fasting,” said Countess Lidia Ivanovna, with disgusted contempt, “those are the crude ideas of our monks.... Yet that is nowhere said. It is far simpler and easier,” she added, looking at Oblonsky with the same encouraging smile with which at court she encouraged youthful maids of honor, disconcerted by the new surroundings of the court.\n\n“We are saved by Christ who suffered for us. We are saved by faith,” Alexey Alexandrovitch chimed in, with a glance of approval at her words.\n\n“Vous comprenez l’anglais?” asked Lidia Ivanovna, and receiving a reply in the affirmative, she got up and began looking through a shelf of books.\n\n“I want to read him ‘Safe and Happy,’ or ‘Under the Wing,’” she said, looking inquiringly at Karenin. And finding the book, and sitting down again in her place, she opened it. “It’s very short. In it is described the way by which faith can be reached, and the happiness, above all earthly bliss, with which it fills the soul. The believer cannot be unhappy because he is not alone. But you will see.” She was just settling herself to read when the footman came in again. “Madame Borozdina? Tell her, tomorrow at two o’clock. Yes,” she said, putting her finger in the place in the book, and gazing before her with her fine pensive eyes, “that is how true faith acts. You know Marie Sanina? You know about her trouble? She lost her only child. She was in despair. And what happened? She found this comforter, and she thanks God now for the death of her child. Such is the happiness faith brings!”\n\n“Oh, yes, that is most....” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, glad they were going to read, and let him have a chance to collect his faculties. “No, I see I’d better not ask her about anything today,” he thought. “If only I can get out of this without putting my foot in it!”\n\n“It will be dull for you,” said Countess Lidia Ivanovna, addressing Landau; “you don’t know English, but it’s short.”\n\n“Oh, I shall understand,” said Landau, with the same smile, and he closed his eyes. Alexey Alexandrovitch and Lidia Ivanovna exchanged meaningful glances, and the reading began.\n\n# Chapter 22\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch felt completely nonplussed by the strange talk which he was hearing for the first time. The complexity of Petersburg, as a rule, had a stimulating effect on him, rousing him out of his Moscow stagnation. But he liked these complications, and understood them only in the circles he knew and was at home in. In these unfamiliar surroundings he was puzzled and disconcerted, and could not get his bearings. As he listened to Countess Lidia Ivanovna, aware of the beautiful, artless—or perhaps artful, he could not decide which—eyes of Landau fixed upon him, Stepan Arkadyevitch began to be conscious of a peculiar heaviness in his head.\n\nThe most incongruous ideas were in confusion in his head. “Marie Sanina is glad her child’s dead.... How good a smoke would be now!... To be saved, one need only believe, and the monks don’t know how the thing’s to be done, but Countess Lidia Ivanovna does know.... And why is my head so heavy? Is it the cognac, or all this being so queer? Anyway, I fancy I’ve done nothing unsuitable so far. But anyway, it won’t do to ask her now. They say they make one say one’s prayers. I only hope they won’t make me! That’ll be too imbecile. And what stuff it is she’s reading! but she has a good accent. Landau—Bezzubov—what’s he Bezzubov for?” All at once Stepan Arkadyevitch became aware that his lower jaw was uncontrollably forming a yawn. He pulled his whiskers to cover the yawn, and shook himself together. But soon after he became aware that he was dropping asleep and on the very point of snoring. He recovered himself at the very moment when the voice of Countess Lidia Ivanovna was saying “he’s asleep.” Stepan Arkadyevitch started with dismay, feeling guilty and caught. But he was reassured at once by seeing that the words “he’s asleep” referred not to him, but to Landau. The Frenchman was asleep as well as Stepan Arkadyevitch. But Stepan Arkadyevitch’s being asleep would have offended them, as he thought (though even this, he thought, might not be so, as everything seemed so queer), while Landau’s being asleep delighted them extremely, especially Countess Lidia Ivanovna.\n\n“Mon ami,” said Lidia Ivanovna, carefully holding the folds of her silk gown so as not to rustle, and in her excitement calling Karenin not Alexey Alexandrovitch, but “mon ami,” “donnez-lui la main. Vous voyez? Sh!” she hissed at the footman as he came in again. “Not at home.”\n\nThe Frenchman was asleep, or pretending to be asleep, with his head on the back of his chair, and his moist hand, as it lay on his knee, made faint movements, as though trying to catch something. Alexey Alexandrovitch got up, tried to move carefully, but stumbled against the table, went up and laid his hand in the Frenchman’s hand. Stepan Arkadyevitch got up too, and opening his eyes wide, trying to wake himself up if he were asleep, he looked first at one and then at the other. It was all real. Stepan Arkadyevitch felt that his head was getting worse and worse.\n\n“Que la personne qui est arrivée la dernière, celle qui demande, qu’elle sorte! Qu’elle sorte!” articulated the Frenchman, without opening his eyes.\n\n“Vous m’excuserez, mais vous voyez.... Revenez vers dix heures, encore mieux demain.”\n\n“Qu’elle sorte!” repeated the Frenchman impatiently.\n\n“C’est moi, n’est-ce pas?” And receiving an answer in the affirmative, Stepan Arkadyevitch, forgetting the favor he had meant to ask of Lidia Ivanovna, and forgetting his sister’s affairs, caring for nothing, but filled with the sole desire to get away as soon as possible, went out on tiptoe and ran out into the street as though from a plague-stricken house. For a long while he chatted and joked with his cab-driver, trying to recover his spirits.\n\nAt the French theater where he arrived for the last act, and afterwards at the Tatar restaurant after his champagne, Stepan Arkadyevitch felt a little refreshed in the atmosphere he was used to. But still he felt quite unlike himself all that evening.\n\nOn getting home to Pyotr Oblonsky’s, where he was staying, Stepan Arkadyevitch found a note from Betsy. She wrote to him that she was very anxious to finish their interrupted conversation, and begged him to come next day. He had scarcely read this note, and frowned at its contents, when he heard below the ponderous tramp of the servants, carrying something heavy.\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch went out to look. It was the rejuvenated Pyotr Oblonsky. He was so drunk that he could not walk upstairs; but he told them to set him on his legs when he saw Stepan Arkadyevitch, and clinging to him, walked with him into his room and there began telling him how he had spent the evening, and fell asleep doing so.\n\nStepan Arkadyevitch was in very low spirits, which happened rarely with him, and for a long while he could not go to sleep. Everything he could recall to his mind, everything was disgusting; but most disgusting of all, as if it were something shameful, was the memory of the evening he had spent at Countess Lidia Ivanovna’s.\n\nNext day he received from Alexey Alexandrovitch a final answer, refusing to grant Anna’s divorce, and he understood that this decision was based on what the Frenchman had said in his real or pretended trance.\n\n# Chapter 23\n\nIn order to carry through any undertaking in family life, there must necessarily be either complete division between the husband and wife, or loving agreement. When the relations of a couple are vacillating and neither one thing nor the other, no sort of enterprise can be undertaken.\n\nMany families remain for years in the same place, though both husband and wife are sick of it, simply because there is neither complete division nor agreement between them.\n\nBoth Vronsky and Anna felt life in Moscow insupportable in the heat and dust, when the spring sunshine was followed by the glare of summer, and all the trees in the boulevards had long since been in full leaf, and the leaves were covered with dust. But they did not go back to Vozdvizhenskoe, as they had arranged to do long before; they went on staying in Moscow, though they both loathed it, because of late there had been no agreement between them.\n\nThe irritability that kept them apart had no external cause, and all efforts to come to an understanding intensified it, instead of removing it. It was an inner irritation, grounded in her mind on the conviction that his love had grown less; in his, on regret that he had put himself for her sake in a difficult position, which she, instead of lightening, made still more difficult. Neither of them gave full utterance to their sense of grievance, but they considered each other in the wrong, and tried on every pretext to prove this to one another.\n\nIn her eyes the whole of him, with all his habits, ideas, desires, with all his spiritual and physical temperament, was one thing—love for women, and that love, she felt, ought to be entirely concentrated on her alone. That love was less; consequently, as she reasoned, he must have transferred part of his love to other women or to another woman—and she was jealous. She was jealous not of any particular woman but of the decrease of his love. Not having got an object for her jealousy, she was on the lookout for it. At the slightest hint she transferred her jealousy from one object to another. At one time she was jealous of those low women with whom he might so easily renew his old bachelor ties; then she was jealous of the society women he might meet; then she was jealous of the imaginary girl whom he might want to marry, for whose sake he would break with her. And this last form of jealousy tortured her most of all, especially as he had unwarily told her, in a moment of frankness, that his mother knew him so little that she had had the audacity to try and persuade him to marry the young Princess Sorokina.\n\nAnd being jealous of him, Anna was indignant against him and found grounds for indignation in everything. For everything that was difficult in her position she blamed him. The agonizing condition of suspense she had passed in Moscow, the tardiness and indecision of Alexey Alexandrovitch, her solitude—she put it all down to him. If he had loved her he would have seen all the bitterness of her position, and would have rescued her from it. For her being in Moscow and not in the country, he was to blame too. He could not live buried in the country as she would have liked to do. He must have society, and he had put her in this awful position, the bitterness of which he would not see. And again, it was his fault that she was forever separated from her son.\n\nEven the rare moments of tenderness that came from time to time did not soothe her; in his tenderness now she saw a shade of complacency, of self-confidence, which had not been of old, and which exasperated her.\n\nIt was dusk. Anna was alone, and waiting for him to come back from a bachelor dinner. She walked up and down in his study (the room where the noise from the street was least heard), and thought over every detail of their yesterday’s quarrel. Going back from the well-remembered, offensive words of the quarrel to what had been the ground of it, she arrived at last at its origin. For a long while she could hardly believe that their dissension had arisen from a conversation so inoffensive, of so little moment to either. But so it actually had been. It all arose from his laughing at the girls’ high schools, declaring they were useless, while she defended them. He had spoken slightingly of women’s education in general, and had said that Hannah, Anna’s English protégée, had not the slightest need to know anything of physics.\n\nThis irritated Anna. She saw in this a contemptuous reference to her occupations. And she bethought her of a phrase to pay him back for the pain he had given her. “I don’t expect you to understand me, my feelings, as anyone who loved me might, but simple delicacy I did expect,” she said.\n\nAnd he had actually flushed with vexation, and had said something unpleasant. She could not recall her answer, but at that point, with an unmistakable desire to wound her too, he had said:\n\n“I feel no interest in your infatuation over this girl, that’s true, because I see it’s unnatural.”\n\nThe cruelty with which he shattered the world she had built up for herself so laboriously to enable her to endure her hard life, the injustice with which he had accused her of affectation, of artificiality, aroused her.\n\n“I am very sorry that nothing but what’s coarse and material is comprehensible and natural to you,” she said and walked out of the room.\n\nWhen he had come in to her yesterday evening, they had not referred to the quarrel, but both felt that the quarrel had been smoothed over, but was not at an end.\n\nToday he had not been at home all day, and she felt so lonely and wretched in being on bad terms with him that she wanted to forget it all, to forgive him, and be reconciled with him; she wanted to throw the blame on herself and to justify him.\n\n“I am myself to blame. I’m irritable, I’m insanely jealous. I will make it up with him, and we’ll go away to the country; there I shall be more at peace.”\n\n“Unnatural!” She suddenly recalled the word that had stung her most of all, not so much the word itself as the intent to wound her with which it was said. “I know what he meant; he meant—unnatural, not loving my own daughter, to love another person’s child. What does he know of love for children, of my love for Seryozha, whom I’ve sacrificed for him? But that wish to wound me! No, he loves another woman, it must be so.”\n\nAnd perceiving that, while trying to regain her peace of mind, she had gone round the same circle that she had been round so often before, and had come back to her former state of exasperation, she was horrified at herself. “Can it be impossible? Can it be beyond me to control myself?” she said to herself, and began again from the beginning. “He’s truthful, he’s honest, he loves me. I love him, and in a few days the divorce will come. What more do I want? I want peace of mind and trust, and I will take the blame on myself. Yes, now when he comes in, I will tell him I was wrong, though I was not wrong, and we will go away tomorrow.”\n\nAnd to escape thinking any more, and being overcome by irritability, she rang, and ordered the boxes to be brought up for packing their things for the country.\n\nAt ten o’clock Vronsky came in.\n\n# Chapter 24\n\n“Well, was it nice?” she asked, coming out to meet him with a penitent and meek expression.\n\n“Just as usual,” he answered, seeing at a glance that she was in one of her good moods. He was used by now to these transitions, and he was particularly glad to see it today, as he was in a specially good humor himself.\n\n“What do I see? Come, that’s good!” he said, pointing to the boxes in the passage.\n\n“Yes, we must go. I went out for a drive, and it was so fine I longed to be in the country. There’s nothing to keep you, is there?”\n\n“It’s the one thing I desire. I’ll be back directly, and we’ll talk it over; I only want to change my coat. Order some tea.”\n\nAnd he went into his room.\n\nThere was something mortifying in the way he had said “Come, that’s good,” as one says to a child when it leaves off being naughty, and still more mortifying was the contrast between her penitent and his self-confident tone; and for one instant she felt the lust of strife rising up in her again, but making an effort she conquered it, and met Vronsky as good-humoredly as before.\n\nWhen he came in she told him, partly repeating phrases she had prepared beforehand, how she had spent the day, and her plans for going away.\n\n“You know it came to me almost like an inspiration,” she said. “Why wait here for the divorce? Won’t it be just the same in the country? I can’t wait any longer! I don’t want to go on hoping, I don’t want to hear anything about the divorce. I have made up my mind it shall not have any more influence on my life. Do you agree?”\n\n“Oh, yes!” he said, glancing uneasily at her excited face.\n\n“What did you do? Who was there?” she said, after a pause.\n\nVronsky mentioned the names of the guests. “The dinner was first rate, and the boat race, and it was all pleasant enough, but in Moscow they can never do anything without something ridicule. A lady of a sort appeared on the scene, teacher of swimming to the Queen of Sweden, and gave us an exhibition of her skill.”\n\n“How? did she swim?” asked Anna, frowning.\n\n“In an absurd red costume de natation; she was old and hideous too. So when shall we go?”\n\n“What an absurd fancy! Why, did she swim in some special way, then?” said Anna, not answering.\n\n“There was absolutely nothing in it. That’s just what I say, it was awfully stupid. Well, then, when do you think of going?”\n\nAnna shook her head as though trying to drive away some unpleasant idea.\n\n“When? Why, the sooner the better! By tomorrow we shan’t be ready. The day after tomorrow.”\n\n“Yes ... oh, no, wait a minute! The day after tomorrow’s Sunday, I have to be at maman’s,” said Vronsky, embarrassed, because as soon as he uttered his mother’s name he was aware of her intent, suspicious eyes. His embarrassment confirmed her suspicion. She flushed hotly and drew away from him. It was now not the Queen of Sweden’s swimming-mistress who filled Anna’s imagination, but the young Princess Sorokina. She was staying in a village near Moscow with Countess Vronskaya.\n\n“Can’t you go tomorrow?” she said.\n\n“Well, no! The deeds and the money for the business I’m going there for I can’t get by tomorrow,” he answered.\n\n“If so, we won’t go at all.”\n\n“But why so?”\n\n“I shall not go later. Monday or never!”\n\n“What for?” said Vronsky, as though in amazement. “Why, there’s no meaning in it!”\n\n“There’s no meaning in it to you, because you care nothing for me. You don’t care to understand my life. The one thing that I cared for here was Hannah. You say it’s affectation. Why, you said yesterday that I don’t love my daughter, that I love this English girl, that it’s unnatural. I should like to know what life there is for me that could be natural!”\n\nFor an instant she had a clear vision of what she was doing, and was horrified at how she had fallen away from her resolution. But even though she knew it was her own ruin, she could not restrain herself, could not keep herself from proving to him that he was wrong, could not give way to him.\n\n“I never said that; I said I did not sympathize with this sudden passion.”\n\n“How is it, though you boast of your straightforwardness, you don’t tell the truth?”\n\n“I never boast, and I never tell lies,” he said slowly, restraining his rising anger. “It’s a great pity if you can’t respect....”\n\n“Respect was invented to cover the empty place where love should be. And if you don’t love me any more, it would be better and more honest to say so.”\n\n“No, this is becoming unbearable!” cried Vronsky, getting up from his chair; and stopping short, facing her, he said, speaking deliberately: “What do you try my patience for?” looking as though he might have said much more, but was restraining himself. “It has limits.”\n\n“What do you mean by that?” she cried, looking with terror at the undisguised hatred in his whole face, and especially in his cruel, menacing eyes.\n\n“I mean to say....” he was beginning, but he checked himself. “I must ask what it is you want of me?”\n\n“What can I want? All I can want is that you should not desert me, as you think of doing,” she said, understanding all he had not uttered. “But that I don’t want; that’s secondary. I want love, and there is none. So then all is over.”\n\nShe turned towards the door.\n\n“Stop! sto-op!” said Vronsky, with no change in the gloomy lines of his brows, though he held her by the hand. “What is it all about? I said that we must put off going for three days, and on that you told me I was lying, that I was not an honorable man.”\n\n“Yes, and I repeat that the man who reproaches me with having sacrificed everything for me,” she said, recalling the words of a still earlier quarrel, “that he’s worse than a dishonorable man—he’s a heartless man.”\n\n“Oh, there are limits to endurance!” he cried, and hastily let go her hand.\n\n“He hates me, that’s clear,” she thought, and in silence, without looking round, she walked with faltering steps out of the room. “He loves another woman, that’s even clearer,” she said to herself as she went into her own room. “I want love, and there is none. So, then, all is over.” She repeated the words she had said, “and it must be ended.”\n\n“But how?” she asked herself, and she sat down in a low chair before the looking-glass.\n\nThoughts of where she would go now, whether to the aunt who had brought her up, to Dolly, or simply alone abroad, and of what he was doing now alone in his study; whether this was the final quarrel, or whether reconciliation were still possible; and of what all her old friends at Petersburg would say of her now; and of how Alexey Alexandrovitch would look at it, and many other ideas of what would happen now after this rupture, came into her head; but she did not give herself up to them with all her heart. At the bottom of her heart was some obscure idea that alone interested her, but she could not get clear sight of it. Thinking once more of Alexey Alexandrovitch, she recalled the time of her illness after her confinement, and the feeling which never left her at that time. “Why didn’t I die?” and the words and the feeling of that time came back to her. And all at once she knew what was in her soul. Yes, it was that idea which alone solved all. “Yes, to die!... And the shame and disgrace of Alexey Alexandrovitch and of Seryozha, and my awful shame, it will all be saved by death. To die! and he will feel remorse; will be sorry; will love me; he will suffer on my account.” With the trace of a smile of commiseration for herself she sat down in the armchair, taking off and putting on the rings on her left hand, vividly picturing from different sides his feelings after her death.\n\nApproaching footsteps—his steps—distracted her attention. As though absorbed in the arrangement of her rings, she did not even turn to him.\n\nHe went up to her, and taking her by the hand, said softly:\n\n“Anna, we’ll go the day after tomorrow, if you like. I agree to everything.”\n\nShe did not speak.\n\n“What is it?” he urged.\n\n“You know,” she said, and at the same instant, unable to restrain herself any longer, she burst into sobs.\n\n“Cast me off!” she articulated between her sobs. “I’ll go away tomorrow ... I’ll do more. What am I? An immoral woman! A stone round your neck. I don’t want to make you wretched, I don’t want to! I’ll set you free. You don’t love me; you love someone else!”\n\nVronsky besought her to be calm, and declared that there was no trace of foundation for her jealousy; that he had never ceased, and never would cease, to love her; that he loved her more than ever.\n\n“Anna, why distress yourself and me so?” he said to her, kissing her hands. There was tenderness now in his face, and she fancied she caught the sound of tears in his voice, and she felt them wet on her hand. And instantly Anna’s despairing jealousy changed to a despairing passion of tenderness. She put her arms round him, and covered with kisses his head, his neck, his hands.\n\n# Chapter 25\n\nFeeling that the reconciliation was complete, Anna set eagerly to work in the morning preparing for their departure. Though it was not settled whether they should go on Monday or Tuesday, as they had each given way to the other, Anna packed busily, feeling absolutely indifferent whether they went a day earlier or later. She was standing in her room over an open box, taking things out of it, when he came in to see her earlier than usual, dressed to go out.\n\n“I’m going off at once to see maman; she can send me the money by Yegorov. And I shall be ready to go tomorrow,” he said.\n\nThough she was in such a good mood, the thought of his visit to his mother’s gave her a pang.\n\n“No, I shan’t be ready by then myself,” she said; and at once reflected, “so then it was possible to arrange to do as I wished.” “No, do as you meant to do. Go into the dining-room, I’m coming directly. It’s only to turn out those things that aren’t wanted,” she said, putting something more on the heap of frippery that lay in Annushka’s arms.\n\nVronsky was eating his beefsteak when she came into the dining-room.\n\n“You wouldn’t believe how distasteful these rooms have become to me,” she said, sitting down beside him to her coffee. “There’s nothing more awful than these chambres garnies. There’s no individuality in them, no soul. These clocks, and curtains, and, worst of all, the wallpapers—they’re a nightmare. I think of Vozdvizhenskoe as the promised land. You’re not sending the horses off yet?”\n\n“No, they will come after us. Where are you going to?”\n\n“I wanted to go to Wilson’s to take some dresses to her. So it’s really to be tomorrow?” she said in a cheerful voice; but suddenly her face changed.\n\nVronsky’s valet came in to ask him to sign a receipt for a telegram from Petersburg. There was nothing out of the way in Vronsky’s getting a telegram, but he said, as though anxious to conceal something from her, that the receipt was in his study, and he turned hurriedly to her.\n\n“By tomorrow, without fail, I will finish it all.”\n\n“From whom is the telegram?” she asked, not hearing him.\n\n“From Stiva,” he answered reluctantly.\n\n“Why didn’t you show it to me? What secret can there be between Stiva and me?”\n\nVronsky called the valet back, and told him to bring the telegram.\n\n“I didn’t want to show it to you, because Stiva has such a passion for telegraphing: why telegraph when nothing is settled?”\n\n“About the divorce?”\n\n“Yes; but he says he has not been able to come at anything yet. He has promised a decisive answer in a day or two. But here it is; read it.”\n\nWith trembling hands Anna took the telegram, and read what Vronsky had told her. At the end was added: “Little hope; but I will do everything possible and impossible.”\n\n“I said yesterday that it’s absolutely nothing to me when I get, or whether I never get, a divorce,” she said, flushing crimson. “There was not the slightest necessity to hide it from me.” “So he may hide and does hide his correspondence with women from me,” she thought.\n\n“Yashvin meant to come this morning with Voytov,” said Vronsky; “I believe he’s won from Pyevtsov all and more than he can pay, about sixty thousand.”\n\n“No,” she said, irritated by his so obviously showing by this change of subject that he was irritated, “why did you suppose that this news would affect me so, that you must even try to hide it? I said I don’t want to consider it, and I should have liked you to care as little about it as I do.”\n\n“I care about it because I like definiteness,” he said.\n\n“Definiteness is not in the form but the love,” she said, more and more irritated, not by his words, but by the tone of cool composure in which he spoke. “What do you want it for?”\n\n“My God! love again,” he thought, frowning.\n\n“Oh, you know what for; for your sake and your children’s in the future.”\n\n“There won’t be children in the future.”\n\n“That’s a great pity,” he said.\n\n“You want it for the children’s sake, but you don’t think of me?” she said, quite forgetting or not having heard that he had said, “For your sake and the children’s.”\n\nThe question of the possibility of having children had long been a subject of dispute and irritation to her. His desire to have children she interpreted as a proof he did not prize her beauty.\n\n“Oh, I said: for your sake. Above all for your sake,” he repeated, frowning as though in pain, “because I am certain that the greater part of your irritability comes from the indefiniteness of the position.”\n\n“Yes, now he has laid aside all pretense, and all his cold hatred for me is apparent,” she thought, not hearing his words, but watching with terror the cold, cruel judge who looked mocking her out of his eyes.\n\n“The cause is not that,” she said, “and, indeed, I don’t see how the cause of my irritability, as you call it, can be that I am completely in your power. What indefiniteness is there in the position? on the contrary....”\n\n“I am very sorry that you don’t care to understand,” he interrupted, obstinately anxious to give utterance to his thought. “The indefiniteness consists in your imagining that I am free.”\n\n“On that score you can set your mind quite at rest,” she said, and turning away from him, she began drinking her coffee.\n\nShe lifted her cup, with her little finger held apart, and put it to her lips. After drinking a few sips she glanced at him, and by his expression, she saw clearly that he was repelled by her hand, and her gesture, and the sound made by her lips.\n\n“I don’t care in the least what your mother thinks, and what match she wants to make for you,” she said, putting the cup down with a shaking hand.\n\n“But we are not talking about that.”\n\n“Yes, that’s just what we are talking about. And let me tell you that a heartless woman, whether she’s old or not old, your mother or anyone else, is of no consequence to me, and I would not consent to know her.”\n\n“Anna, I beg you not to speak disrespectfully of my mother.”\n\n“A woman whose heart does not tell her where her son’s happiness and honor lie has no heart.”\n\n“I repeat my request that you will not speak disrespectfully of my mother, whom I respect,” he said, raising his voice and looking sternly at her.\n\nShe did not answer. Looking intently at him, at his face, his hands, she recalled all the details of their reconciliation the previous day, and his passionate caresses. “There, just such caresses he has lavished, and will lavish, and longs to lavish on other women!” she thought.\n\n“You don’t love your mother. That’s all talk, and talk, and talk!” she said, looking at him with hatred in her eyes.\n\n“Even if so, you must....”\n\n“Must decide, and I have decided,” she said, and she would have gone away, but at that moment Yashvin walked into the room. Anna greeted him and remained.\n\nWhy, when there was a tempest in her soul, and she felt she was standing at a turning point in her life, which might have fearful consequences—why, at that minute, she had to keep up appearances before an outsider, who sooner or later must know it all—she did not know. But at once quelling the storm within her, she sat down and began talking to their guest.\n\n“Well, how are you getting on? Has your debt been paid you?” she asked Yashvin.\n\n“Oh, pretty fair; I fancy I shan’t get it all, but I shall get a good half. And when are you off?” said Yashvin, looking at Vronsky, and unmistakably guessing at a quarrel.\n\n“The day after tomorrow, I think,” said Vronsky.\n\n“You’ve been meaning to go so long, though.”\n\n“But now it’s quite decided,” said Anna, looking Vronsky straight in the face with a look which told him not to dream of the possibility of reconciliation.\n\n“Don’t you feel sorry for that unlucky Pyevtsov?” she went on, talking to Yashvin.\n\n“I’ve never asked myself the question, Anna Arkadyevna, whether I’m sorry for him or not. You see, all my fortune’s here”—he touched his breast pocket—“and just now I’m a wealthy man. But today I’m going to the club, and I may come out a beggar. You see, whoever sits down to play with me—he wants to leave me without a shirt to my back, and so do I him. And so we fight it out, and that’s the pleasure of it.”\n\n“Well, but suppose you were married,” said Anna, “how would it be for your wife?”\n\nYashvin laughed.\n\n“That’s why I’m not married, and never mean to be.”\n\n“And Helsingfors?” said Vronsky, entering into the conversation and glancing at Anna’s smiling face. Meeting his eyes, Anna’s face instantly took a coldly severe expression as though she were saying to him: “It’s not forgotten. It’s all the same.”\n\n“Were you really in love?” she said to Yashvin.\n\n“Oh heavens! ever so many times! But you see, some men can play but only so that they can always lay down their cards when the hour of a rendezvous comes, while I can take up love, but only so as not to be late for my cards in the evening. That’s how I manage things.”\n\n“No, I didn’t mean that, but the real thing.” She would have said Helsingfors, but would not repeat the word used by Vronsky.\n\nVoytov, who was buying the horse, came in. Anna got up and went out of the room.\n\nBefore leaving the house, Vronsky went into her room. She would have pretended to be looking for something on the table, but ashamed of making a pretense, she looked straight in his face with cold eyes.\n\n“What do you want?” she asked in French.\n\n“To get the guarantee for Gambetta, I’ve sold him,” he said, in a tone which said more clearly than words, “I’ve no time for discussing things, and it would lead to nothing.”\n\n“I’m not to blame in any way,” he thought. “If she will punish herself, tant pis pour elle. But as he was going he fancied that she said something, and his heart suddenly ached with pity for her.\n\n“Eh, Anna?” he queried.\n\n“I said nothing,” she answered just as coldly and calmly.\n\n“Oh, nothing, tant pis then,” he thought, feeling cold again, and he turned and went out. As he was going out he caught a glimpse in the looking-glass of her face, white, with quivering lips. He even wanted to stop and to say some comforting word to her, but his legs carried him out of the room before he could think what to say. The whole of that day he spent away from home, and when he came in late in the evening the maid told him that Anna Arkadyevna had a headache and begged him not to go in to her.\n\n# Chapter 26\n\nNever before had a day been passed in quarrel. Today was the first time. And this was not a quarrel. It was the open acknowledgment of complete coldness. Was it possible to glance at her as he had glanced when he came into the room for the guarantee?—to look at her, see her heart was breaking with despair, and go out without a word with that face of callous composure? He was not merely cold to her, he hated her because he loved another woman—that was clear.\n\nAnd remembering all the cruel words he had said, Anna supplied, too, the words that he had unmistakably wished to say and could have said to her, and she grew more and more exasperated.\n\n“I won’t prevent you,” he might say. “You can go where you like. You were unwilling to be divorced from your husband, no doubt so that you might go back to him. Go back to him. If you want money, I’ll give it to you. How many roubles do you want?”\n\nAll the most cruel words that a brutal man could say, he said to her in her imagination, and she could not forgive him for them, as though he had actually said them.\n\n“But didn’t he only yesterday swear he loved me, he, a truthful and sincere man? Haven’t I despaired for nothing many times already?” she said to herself afterwards.\n\nAll that day, except for the visit to Wilson’s, which occupied two hours, Anna spent in doubts whether everything were over or whether there were still hope of reconciliation, whether she should go away at once or see him once more. She was expecting him the whole day, and in the evening, as she went to her own room, leaving a message for him that her head ached, she said to herself, “If he comes in spite of what the maid says, it means that he loves me still. If not, it means that all is over, and then I will decide what I’m to do!...”\n\nIn the evening she heard the rumbling of his carriage stop at the entrance, his ring, his steps and his conversation with the servant; he believed what was told him, did not care to find out more, and went to his own room. So then everything was over.\n\nAnd death rose clearly and vividly before her mind as the sole means of bringing back love for her in his heart, of punishing him and of gaining the victory in that strife which the evil spirit in possession of her heart was waging with him.\n\nNow nothing mattered: going or not going to Vozdvizhenskoe, getting or not getting a divorce from her husband—all that did not matter. The one thing that mattered was punishing him. When she poured herself out her usual dose of opium, and thought that she had only to drink off the whole bottle to die, it seemed to her so simple and easy, that she began musing with enjoyment on how he would suffer, and repent and love her memory when it would be too late. She lay in bed with open eyes, by the light of a single burned-down candle, gazing at the carved cornice of the ceiling and at the shadow of the screen that covered part of it, while she vividly pictured to herself how he would feel when she would be no more, when she would be only a memory to him. “How could I say such cruel things to her?” he would say. “How could I go out of the room without saying anything to her? But now she is no more. She has gone away from us forever. She is....” Suddenly the shadow of the screen wavered, pounced on the whole cornice, the whole ceiling; other shadows from the other side swooped to meet it, for an instant the shadows flitted back, but then with fresh swiftness they darted forward, wavered, commingled, and all was darkness. “Death!” she thought. And such horror came upon her that for a long while she could not realize where she was, and for a long while her trembling hands could not find the matches and light another candle, instead of the one that had burned down and gone out. “No, anything—only to live! Why, I love him! Why, he loves me! This has been before and will pass,” she said, feeling that tears of joy at the return to life were trickling down her cheeks. And to escape from her panic she went hurriedly to his room.\n\nHe was asleep there, and sleeping soundly. She went up to him, and holding the light above his face, she gazed a long while at him. Now when he was asleep, she loved him so that at the sight of him she could not keep back tears of tenderness. But she knew that if he waked up he would look at her with cold eyes, convinced that he was right, and that before telling him of her love, she would have to prove to him that he had been wrong in his treatment of her. Without waking him, she went back, and after a second dose of opium she fell towards morning into a heavy, incomplete sleep, during which she never quite lost consciousness.\n\nIn the morning she was waked by a horrible nightmare, which had recurred several times in her dreams, even before her connection with Vronsky. A little old man with unkempt beard was doing something bent down over some iron, muttering meaningless French words, and she, as she always did in this nightmare (it was what made the horror of it), felt that this peasant was taking no notice of her, but was doing something horrible with the iron—over her. And she waked up in a cold sweat.\n\nWhen she got up, the previous day came back to her as though veiled in mist.\n\n“There was a quarrel. Just what has happened several times. I said I had a headache, and he did not come in to see me. Tomorrow we’re going away; I must see him and get ready for the journey,” she said to herself. And learning that he was in his study, she went down to him. As she passed through the drawing-room she heard a carriage stop at the entrance, and looking out of the window she saw the carriage, from which a young girl in a lilac hat was leaning out giving some direction to the footman ringing the bell. After a parley in the hall, someone came upstairs, and Vronsky’s steps could be heard passing the drawing-room. He went rapidly downstairs. Anna went again to the window. She saw him come out onto the steps without his hat and go up to the carriage. The young girl in the lilac hat handed him a parcel. Vronsky, smiling, said something to her. The carriage drove away, he ran rapidly upstairs again.\n\nThe mists that had shrouded everything in her soul parted suddenly. The feelings of yesterday pierced the sick heart with a fresh pang. She could not understand now how she could have lowered herself by spending a whole day with him in his house. She went into his room to announce her determination.\n\n“That was Madame Sorokina and her daughter. They came and brought me the money and the deeds from maman. I couldn’t get them yesterday. How is your head, better?” he said quietly, not wishing to see and to understand the gloomy and solemn expression of her face.\n\nShe looked silently, intently at him, standing in the middle of the room. He glanced at her, frowned for a moment, and went on reading a letter. She turned, and went deliberately out of the room. He still might have turned her back, but she had reached the door, he was still silent, and the only sound audible was the rustling of the note paper as he turned it.\n\n“Oh, by the way,” he said at the very moment she was in the doorway, “we’re going tomorrow for certain, aren’t we?”\n\n“You, but not I,” she said, turning round to him.\n\n“Anna, we can’t go on like this....”\n\n“You, but not I,” she repeated.\n\n“This is getting unbearable!”\n\n“You ... you will be sorry for this,” she said, and went out.\n\nFrightened by the desperate expression with which these words were uttered, he jumped up and would have run after her, but on second thoughts he sat down and scowled, setting his teeth. This vulgar—as he thought it—threat of something vague exasperated him. “I’ve tried everything,” he thought; “the only thing left is not to pay attention,” and he began to get ready to drive into town, and again to his mother’s to get her signature to the deeds.\n\nShe heard the sound of his steps about the study and the dining-room. At the drawing-room he stood still. But he did not turn in to see her, he merely gave an order that the horse should be given to Voytov if he came while he was away. Then she heard the carriage brought round, the door opened, and he came out again. But he went back into the porch again, and someone was running upstairs. It was the valet running up for his gloves that had been forgotten. She went to the window and saw him take the gloves without looking, and touching the coachman on the back he said something to him. Then without looking up at the window he settled himself in his usual attitude in the carriage, with his legs crossed, and drawing on his gloves he vanished round the corner.\n\n# Chapter 27\n\n“He has gone! It is over!” Anna said to herself, standing at the window; and in answer to this statement the impression of the darkness when the candle had flickered out, and of her fearful dream mingling into one, filled her heart with cold terror.\n\n“No, that cannot be!” she cried, and crossing the room she rang the bell. She was so afraid now of being alone, that without waiting for the servant to come in, she went out to meet him.\n\n“Inquire where the count has gone,” she said. The servant answered that the count had gone to the stable.\n\n“His honor left word that if you cared to drive out, the carriage would be back immediately.”\n\n“Very good. Wait a minute. I’ll write a note at once. Send Mihail with the note to the stables. Make haste.”\n\nShe sat down and wrote:\n\n“I was wrong. Come back home; I must explain. For God’s sake come! I’m afraid.”\n\nShe sealed it up and gave it to the servant.\n\nShe was afraid of being left alone now; she followed the servant out of the room, and went to the nursery.\n\n“Why, this isn’t it, this isn’t he! Where are his blue eyes, his sweet, shy smile?” was her first thought when she saw her chubby, rosy little girl with her black, curly hair instead of Seryozha, whom in the tangle of her ideas she had expected to see in the nursery. The little girl sitting at the table was obstinately and violently battering on it with a cork, and staring aimlessly at her mother with her pitch-black eyes. Answering the English nurse that she was quite well, and that she was going to the country tomorrow, Anna sat down by the little girl and began spinning the cork to show her. But the child’s loud, ringing laugh, and the motion of her eyebrows, recalled Vronsky so vividly that she got up hurriedly, restraining her sobs, and went away. “Can it be all over? No, it cannot be!” she thought. “He will come back. But how can he explain that smile, that excitement after he had been talking to her? But even if he doesn’t explain, I will believe. If I don’t believe, there’s only one thing left for me, and I can’t.”\n\nShe looked at her watch. Twenty minutes had passed. “By now he has received the note and is coming back. Not long, ten minutes more.... But what if he doesn’t come? No, that cannot be. He mustn’t see me with tear-stained eyes. I’ll go and wash. Yes, yes; did I do my hair or not?” she asked herself. And she could not remember. She felt her head with her hand. “Yes, my hair has been done, but when I did it I can’t in the least remember.” She could not believe the evidence of her hand, and went up to the pier-glass to see whether she really had done her hair. She certainly had, but she could not think when she had done it. “Who’s that?” she thought, looking in the looking-glass at the swollen face with strangely glittering eyes, that looked in a scared way at her. “Why, it’s I!” she suddenly understood, and looking round, she seemed all at once to feel his kisses on her, and twitched her shoulders, shuddering. Then she lifted her hand to her lips and kissed it.\n\n“What is it? Why, I’m going out of my mind!” and she went into her bedroom, where Annushka was tidying the room.\n\n“Annushka,” she said, coming to a standstill before her, and she stared at the maid, not knowing what to say to her.\n\n“You meant to go and see Darya Alexandrovna,” said the girl, as though she understood.\n\n“Darya Alexandrovna? Yes, I’ll go.”\n\n“Fifteen minutes there, fifteen minutes back. He’s coming, he’ll be here soon.” She took out her watch and looked at it. “But how could he go away, leaving me in such a state? How can he live, without making it up with me?” She went to the window and began looking into the street. Judging by the time, he might be back now. But her calculations might be wrong, and she began once more to recall when he had started and to count the minutes.\n\nAt the moment when she had moved away to the big clock to compare it with her watch, someone drove up. Glancing out of the window, she saw his carriage. But no one came upstairs, and voices could be heard below. It was the messenger who had come back in the carriage. She went down to him.\n\n“We didn’t catch the count. The count had driven off on the lower city road.”\n\n“What do you say? What!...” she said to the rosy, good-humored Mihail, as he handed her back her note.\n\n“Why, then, he has never received it!” she thought.\n\n“Go with this note to Countess Vronskaya’s place, you know? and bring an answer back immediately,” she said to the messenger.\n\n“And I, what am I going to do?” she thought. “Yes, I’m going to Dolly’s, that’s true or else I shall go out of my mind. Yes, and I can telegraph, too.” And she wrote a telegram. “I absolutely must talk to you; come at once.” After sending off the telegram, she went to dress. When she was dressed and in her hat, she glanced again into the eyes of the plump, comfortable-looking Annushka. There was unmistakable sympathy in those good-natured little gray eyes.\n\n“Annushka, dear, what am I to do?” said Anna, sobbing and sinking helplessly into a chair.\n\n“Why fret yourself so, Anna Arkadyevna? Why, there’s nothing out of the way. You drive out a little, and it’ll cheer you up,” said the maid.\n\n“Yes, I’m going,” said Anna, rousing herself and getting up. “And if there’s a telegram while I’m away, send it on to Darya Alexandrovna’s ... but no, I shall be back myself.”\n\n“Yes, I mustn’t think, I must do something, drive somewhere, and most of all, get out of this house,” she said, feeling with terror the strange turmoil going on in her own heart, and she made haste to go out and get into the carriage.\n\n“Where to?” asked Pyotr before getting onto the box.\n\n“To Znamenka, the Oblonskys’.”\n\n# Chapter 28\n\nIt was bright and sunny. A fine rain had been falling all the morning, and now it had not long cleared up. The iron roofs, the flags of the roads, the flints of the pavements, the wheels and leather, the brass and the tinplate of the carriages—all glistened brightly in the May sunshine. It was three o’clock, and the very liveliest time in the streets.\n\nAs she sat in a corner of the comfortable carriage, that hardly swayed on its supple springs, while the grays trotted swiftly, in the midst of the unceasing rattle of wheels and the changing impressions in the pure air, Anna ran over the events of the last days, and she saw her position quite differently from how it had seemed at home. Now the thought of death seemed no longer so terrible and so clear to her, and death itself no longer seemed so inevitable. Now she blamed herself for the humiliation to which she had lowered herself. “I entreat him to forgive me. I have given in to him. I have owned myself in fault. What for? Can’t I live without him?” And leaving unanswered the question how she was going to live without him, she fell to reading the signs on the shops. “Office and warehouse. Dental surgeon. Yes, I’ll tell Dolly all about it. She doesn’t like Vronsky. I shall be sick and ashamed, but I’ll tell her. She loves me, and I’ll follow her advice. I won’t give in to him; I won’t let him train me as he pleases. Filippov, bun shop. They say they send their dough to Petersburg. The Moscow water is so good for it. Ah, the springs at Mitishtchen, and the pancakes!”\n\nAnd she remembered how, long, long ago, when she was a girl of seventeen, she had gone with her aunt to Troitsa. “Riding, too. Was that really me, with red hands? How much that seemed to me then splendid and out of reach has become worthless, while what I had then has gone out of my reach forever! Could I ever have believed then that I could come to such humiliation? How conceited and self-satisfied he will be when he gets my note! But I will show him.... How horrid that paint smells! Why is it they’re always painting and building? Modes et robes, she read. A man bowed to her. It was Annushka’s husband. “Our parasites”; she remembered how Vronsky had said that. “Our? Why our? What’s so awful is that one can’t tear up the past by its roots. One can’t tear it out, but one can hide one’s memory of it. And I’ll hide it.” And then she thought of her past with Alexey Alexandrovitch, of how she had blotted the memory of it out of her life. “Dolly will think I’m leaving my second husband, and so I certainly must be in the wrong. As if I cared to be right! I can’t help it!” she said, and she wanted to cry. But at once she fell to wondering what those two girls could be smiling about. “Love, most likely. They don’t know how dreary it is, how low.... The boulevard and the children. Three boys running, playing at horses. Seryozha! And I’m losing everything and not getting him back. Yes, I’m losing everything, if he doesn’t return. Perhaps he was late for the train and has come back by now. Longing for humiliation again!” she said to herself. “No, I’ll go to Dolly, and say straight out to her, I’m unhappy, I deserve this, I’m to blame, but still I’m unhappy, help me. These horses, this carriage—how loathsome I am to myself in this carriage—all his; but I won’t see them again.”\n\nThinking over the words in which she would tell Dolly, and mentally working her heart up to great bitterness, Anna went upstairs.\n\n“Is there anyone with her?” she asked in the hall.\n\n“Katerina Alexandrovna Levin,” answered the footman.\n\n“Kitty! Kitty, whom Vronsky was in love with!” thought Anna, “the girl he thinks of with love. He’s sorry he didn’t marry her. But me he thinks of with hatred, and is sorry he had anything to do with me.”\n\nThe sisters were having a consultation about nursing when Anna called. Dolly went down alone to see the visitor who had interrupted their conversation.\n\n“Well, so you’ve not gone away yet? I meant to have come to you,” she said; “I had a letter from Stiva today.”\n\n“We had a telegram too,” answered Anna, looking round for Kitty.\n\n“He writes that he can’t make out quite what Alexey Alexandrovitch wants, but he won’t go away without a decisive answer.”\n\n“I thought you had someone with you. Can I see the letter?”\n\n“Yes; Kitty,” said Dolly, embarrassed. “She stayed in the nursery. She has been very ill.”\n\n“So I heard. May I see the letter?”\n\n“I’ll get it directly. But he doesn’t refuse; on the contrary, Stiva has hopes,” said Dolly, stopping in the doorway.\n\n“I haven’t, and indeed I don’t wish it,” said Anna.\n\n“What’s this? Does Kitty consider it degrading to meet me?” thought Anna when she was alone. “Perhaps she’s right, too. But it’s not for her, the girl who was in love with Vronsky, it’s not for her to show me that, even if it is true. I know that in my position I can’t be received by any decent woman. I knew that from the first moment I sacrificed everything to him. And this is my reward! Oh, how I hate him! And what did I come here for? I’m worse here, more miserable.” She heard from the next room the sisters’ voices in consultation. “And what am I going to say to Dolly now? Amuse Kitty by the sight of my wretchedness, submit to her patronizing? No; and besides, Dolly wouldn’t understand. And it would be no good my telling her. It would only be interesting to see Kitty, to show her how I despise everyone and everything, how nothing matters to me now.”\n\nDolly came in with the letter. Anna read it and handed it back in silence.\n\n“I knew all that,” she said, “and it doesn’t interest me in the least.”\n\n“Oh, why so? On the contrary, I have hopes,” said Dolly, looking inquisitively at Anna. She had never seen her in such a strangely irritable condition. “When are you going away?” she asked.\n\nAnna, half-closing her eyes, looked straight before her and did not answer.\n\n“Why does Kitty shrink from me?” she said, looking at the door and flushing red.\n\n“Oh, what nonsense! She’s nursing, and things aren’t going right with her, and I’ve been advising her.... She’s delighted. She’ll be here in a minute,” said Dolly awkwardly, not clever at lying. “Yes, here she is.”\n\nHearing that Anna had called, Kitty had wanted not to appear, but Dolly persuaded her. Rallying her forces, Kitty went in, walked up to her, blushing, and shook hands.\n\n“I am so glad to see you,” she said with a trembling voice.\n\nKitty had been thrown into confusion by the inward conflict between her antagonism to this bad woman and her desire to be nice to her. But as soon as she saw Anna’s lovely and attractive face, all feeling of antagonism disappeared.\n\n“I should not have been surprised if you had not cared to meet me. I’m used to everything. You have been ill? Yes, you are changed,” said Anna.\n\nKitty felt that Anna was looking at her with hostile eyes. She ascribed this hostility to the awkward position in which Anna, who had once patronized her, must feel with her now, and she felt sorry for her.\n\nThey talked of Kitty’s illness, of the baby, of Stiva, but it was obvious that nothing interested Anna.\n\n“I came to say good-bye to you,” she said, getting up.\n\n“Oh, when are you going?”\n\nBut again not answering, Anna turned to Kitty.\n\n“Yes, I am very glad to have seen you,” she said with a smile. “I have heard so much of you from everyone, even from your husband. He came to see me, and I liked him exceedingly,” she said, unmistakably with malicious intent. “Where is he?”\n\n“He has gone back to the country,” said Kitty, blushing.\n\n“Remember me to him, be sure you do.”\n\n“I’ll be sure to!” Kitty said naïvely, looking compassionately into her eyes.\n\n“So good-bye, Dolly.” And kissing Dolly and shaking hands with Kitty, Anna went out hurriedly.\n\n“She’s just the same and just as charming! She’s very lovely!” said Kitty, when she was alone with her sister. “But there’s something piteous about her. Awfully piteous!”\n\n“Yes, there’s something unusual about her today,” said Dolly. “When I went with her into the hall, I fancied she was almost crying.”\n\n# Chapter 29\n\nAnna got into the carriage again in an even worse frame of mind than when she set out from home. To her previous tortures was added now that sense of mortification and of being an outcast which she had felt so distinctly on meeting Kitty.\n\n“Where to? Home?” asked Pyotr.\n\n“Yes, home,” she said, not even thinking now where she was going.\n\n“How they looked at me as something dreadful, incomprehensible, and curious! What can he be telling the other with such warmth?” she thought, staring at two men who walked by. “Can one ever tell anyone what one is feeling? I meant to tell Dolly, and it’s a good thing I didn’t tell her. How pleased she would have been at my misery! She would have concealed it, but her chief feeling would have been delight at my being punished for the happiness she envied me for. Kitty, she would have been even more pleased. How I can see through her! She knows I was more than usually sweet to her husband. And she’s jealous and hates me. And she despises me. In her eyes I’m an immoral woman. If I were an immoral woman I could have made her husband fall in love with me ... if I’d cared to. And, indeed, I did care to. There’s someone who’s pleased with himself,” she thought, as she saw a fat, rubicund gentleman coming towards her. He took her for an acquaintance, and lifted his glossy hat above his bald, glossy head, and then perceived his mistake. “He thought he knew me. Well, he knows me as well as anyone in the world knows me. I don’t know myself. I know my appetites, as the French say. They want that dirty ice cream, that they do know for certain,” she thought, looking at two boys stopping an ice cream seller, who took a barrel off his head and began wiping his perspiring face with a towel. “We all want what is sweet and nice. If not sweetmeats, then a dirty ice. And Kitty’s the same—if not Vronsky, then Levin. And she envies me, and hates me. And we all hate each other. I Kitty, Kitty me. Yes, that’s the truth. ‘Tiutkin, coiffeur.’ Je me fais coiffer par Tiutkin.... I’ll tell him that when he comes,” she thought and smiled. But the same instant she remembered that she had no one now to tell anything amusing to. “And there’s nothing amusing, nothing mirthful, really. It’s all hateful. They’re singing for vespers, and how carefully that merchant crosses himself! as if he were afraid of missing something. Why these churches and this singing and this humbug? Simply to conceal that we all hate each other like these cab drivers who are abusing each other so angrily. Yashvin says, ‘He wants to strip me of my shirt, and I him of his.’ Yes, that’s the truth!”\n\nShe was plunged in these thoughts, which so engrossed her that she left off thinking of her own position, when the carriage drew up at the steps of her house. It was only when she saw the porter running out to meet her that she remembered she had sent the note and the telegram.\n\n“Is there an answer?” she inquired.\n\n“I’ll see this minute,” answered the porter, and glancing into his room, he took out and gave her the thin square envelope of a telegram. “I can’t come before ten o’clock.—Vronsky,” she read.\n\n“And hasn’t the messenger come back?”\n\n“No,” answered the porter.\n\n“Then, since it’s so, I know what I must do,” she said, and feeling a vague fury and craving for revenge rising up within her, she ran upstairs. “I’ll go to him myself. Before going away forever, I’ll tell him all. Never have I hated anyone as I hate that man!” she thought. Seeing his hat on the rack, she shuddered with aversion. She did not consider that his telegram was an answer to her telegram and that he had not yet received her note. She pictured him to herself as talking calmly to his mother and Princess Sorokina and rejoicing at her sufferings. “Yes, I must go quickly,” she said, not knowing yet where she was going. She longed to get away as quickly as possible from the feelings she had gone through in that awful house. The servants, the walls, the things in that house—all aroused repulsion and hatred in her and lay like a weight upon her.\n\n“Yes, I must go to the railway station, and if he’s not there, then go there and catch him.” Anna looked at the railway timetable in the newspapers. An evening train went at two minutes past eight. “Yes, I shall be in time.” She gave orders for the other horses to be put in the carriage, and packed in a traveling-bag the things needed for a few days. She knew she would never come back here again.\n\nAmong the plans that came into her head she vaguely determined that after what would happen at the station or at the countess’s house, she would go as far as the first town on the Nizhni road and stop there.\n\nDinner was on the table; she went up, but the smell of the bread and cheese was enough to make her feel that all food was disgusting. She ordered the carriage and went out. The house threw a shadow now right across the street, but it was a bright evening and still warm in the sunshine. Annushka, who came down with her things, and Pyotr, who put the things in the carriage, and the coachman, evidently out of humor, were all hateful to her, and irritated her by their words and actions.\n\n“I don’t want you, Pyotr.”\n\n“But how about the ticket?”\n\n“Well, as you like, it doesn’t matter,” she said crossly.\n\nPyotr jumped on the box, and putting his arms akimbo, told the coachman to drive to the booking-office.\n\n# Chapter 30\n\n“Here it is again! Again I understand it all!” Anna said to herself, as soon as the carriage had started and swaying lightly, rumbled over the tiny cobbles of the paved road, and again one impression followed rapidly upon another.\n\n“Yes; what was the last thing I thought of so clearly?” she tried to recall it. “‘Tiutkin, coiffeur?’—no, not that. Yes, of what Yashvin says, the struggle for existence and hatred is the one thing that holds men together. No, it’s a useless journey you’re making,” she said, mentally addressing a party in a coach and four, evidently going for an excursion into the country. “And the dog you’re taking with you will be no help to you. You can’t get away from yourselves.” Turning her eyes in the direction Pyotr had turned to look, she saw a factory-hand almost dead-drunk, with hanging head, being led away by a policeman. “Come, he’s found a quicker way,” she thought. “Count Vronsky and I did not find that happiness either, though we expected so much from it.” And now for the first time Anna turned that glaring light in which she was seeing everything on to her relations with him, which she had hitherto avoided thinking about. “What was it he sought in me? Not love so much as the satisfaction of vanity.” She remembered his words, the expression of his face, that recalled an abject setter-dog, in the early days of their connection. And everything now confirmed this. “Yes, there was the triumph of success in him. Of course there was love too, but the chief element was the pride of success. He boasted of me. Now that’s over. There’s nothing to be proud of. Not to be proud of, but to be ashamed of. He has taken from me all he could, and now I am no use to him. He is weary of me and is trying not to be dishonorable in his behavior to me. He let that out yesterday—he wants divorce and marriage so as to burn his ships. He loves me, but how? The zest is gone, as the English say. That fellow wants everyone to admire him and is very much pleased with himself,” she thought, looking at a red-faced clerk, riding on a riding-school horse. “Yes, there’s not the same flavor about me for him now. If I go away from him, at the bottom of his heart he will be glad.”\n\nThis was not mere supposition, she saw it distinctly in the piercing light, which revealed to her now the meaning of life and human relations.\n\n“My love keeps growing more passionate and egoistic, while his is waning and waning, and that’s why we’re drifting apart.” She went on musing. “And there’s no help for it. He is everything for me, and I want him more and more to give himself up to me entirely. And he wants more and more to get away from me. We walked to meet each other up to the time of our love, and then we have been irresistibly drifting in different directions. And there’s no altering that. He tells me I’m insanely jealous, and I have told myself that I am insanely jealous; but it’s not true. I’m not jealous, but I’m unsatisfied. But....” she opened her lips, and shifted her place in the carriage in the excitement, aroused by the thought that suddenly struck her. “If I could be anything but a mistress, passionately caring for nothing but his caresses; but I can’t and I don’t care to be anything else. And by that desire I rouse aversion in him, and he rouses fury in me, and it cannot be different. Don’t I know that he wouldn’t deceive me, that he has no schemes about Princess Sorokina, that he’s not in love with Kitty, that he won’t desert me! I know all that, but it makes it no better for me. If without loving me, from duty he’ll be good and kind to me, without what I want, that’s a thousand times worse than unkindness! That’s—hell! And that’s just how it is. For a long while now he hasn’t loved me. And where love ends, hate begins. I don’t know these streets at all. Hills it seems, and still houses, and houses.... And in the houses always people and people.... How many of them, no end, and all hating each other! Come, let me try and think what I want, to make me happy. Well? Suppose I am divorced, and Alexey Alexandrovitch lets me have Seryozha, and I marry Vronsky.” Thinking of Alexey Alexandrovitch, she at once pictured him with extraordinary vividness as though he were alive before her, with his mild, lifeless, dull eyes, the blue veins in his white hands, his intonations and the cracking of his fingers, and remembering the feeling which had existed between them, and which was also called love, she shuddered with loathing. “Well, I’m divorced, and become Vronsky’s wife. Well, will Kitty cease looking at me as she looked at me today? No. And will Seryozha leave off asking and wondering about my two husbands? And is there any new feeling I can awaken between Vronsky and me? Is there possible, if not happiness, some sort of ease from misery? No, no!” she answered now without the slightest hesitation. “Impossible! We are drawn apart by life, and I make his unhappiness, and he mine, and there’s no altering him or me. Every attempt has been made, the screw has come unscrewed. Oh, a beggar woman with a baby. She thinks I’m sorry for her. Aren’t we all flung into the world only to hate each other, and so to torture ourselves and each other? Schoolboys coming—laughing Seryozha?” she thought. “I thought, too, that I loved him, and used to be touched by my own tenderness. But I have lived without him, I gave him up for another love, and did not regret the exchange till that love was satisfied.” And with loathing she thought of what she meant by that love. And the clearness with which she saw life now, her own and all men’s, was a pleasure to her. “It’s so with me and Pyotr, and the coachman, Fyodor, and that merchant, and all the people living along the Volga, where those placards invite one to go, and everywhere and always,” she thought when she had driven under the low-pitched roof of the Nizhigorod station, and the porters ran to meet her.\n\n“A ticket to Obiralovka?” said Pyotr.\n\nShe had utterly forgotten where and why she was going, and only by a great effort she understood the question.\n\n“Yes,” she said, handing him her purse, and taking a little red bag in her hand, she got out of the carriage.\n\nMaking her way through the crowd to the first-class waiting-room, she gradually recollected all the details of her position, and the plans between which she was hesitating. And again at the old sore places, hope and then despair poisoned the wounds of her tortured, fearfully throbbing heart. As she sat on the star-shaped sofa waiting for the train, she gazed with aversion at the people coming and going (they were all hateful to her), and thought how she would arrive at the station, would write him a note, and what she would write to him, and how he was at this moment complaining to his mother of his position, not understanding her sufferings, and how she would go into the room, and what she would say to him. Then she thought that life might still be happy, and how miserably she loved and hated him, and how fearfully her heart was beating.\n\n# Chapter 31\n\nA bell rang, some young men, ugly and impudent, and at the same time careful of the impression they were making, hurried by. Pyotr, too, crossed the room in his livery and top-boots, with his dull, animal face, and came up to her to take her to the train. Some noisy men were quiet as she passed them on the platform, and one whispered something about her to another—something vile, no doubt. She stepped up on the high step, and sat down in a carriage by herself on a dirty seat that had been white. Her bag lay beside her, shaken up and down by the springiness of the seat. With a foolish smile Pyotr raised his hat, with its colored band, at the window, in token of farewell; an impudent conductor slammed the door and the latch. A grotesque-looking lady wearing a bustle (Anna mentally undressed the woman, and was appalled at her hideousness), and a little girl laughing affectedly ran down the platform.\n\n“Katerina Andreevna, she’s got them all, ma tante!” cried the girl.\n\n“Even the child’s hideous and affected,” thought Anna. To avoid seeing anyone, she got up quickly and seated herself at the opposite window of the empty carriage. A misshapen-looking peasant covered with dirt, in a cap from which his tangled hair stuck out all round, passed by that window, stooping down to the carriage wheels. “There’s something familiar about that hideous peasant,” thought Anna. And remembering her dream, she moved away to the opposite door, shaking with terror. The conductor opened the door and let in a man and his wife.\n\n“Do you wish to get out?”\n\nAnna made no answer. The conductor and her two fellow-passengers did not notice under her veil her panic-stricken face. She went back to her corner and sat down. The couple seated themselves on the opposite side, and intently but surreptitiously scrutinized her clothes. Both husband and wife seemed repulsive to Anna. The husband asked, would she allow him to smoke, obviously not with a view to smoking but to getting into conversation with her. Receiving her assent, he said to his wife in French something about caring less to smoke than to talk. They made inane and affected remarks to one another, entirely for her benefit. Anna saw clearly that they were sick of each other, and hated each other. And no one could have helped hating such miserable monstrosities.\n\nA second bell sounded, and was followed by moving of luggage, noise, shouting and laughter. It was so clear to Anna that there was nothing for anyone to be glad of, that this laughter irritated her agonizingly, and she would have liked to stop up her ears not to hear it. At last the third bell rang, there was a whistle and a hiss of steam, and a clank of chains, and the man in her carriage crossed himself. “It would be interesting to ask him what meaning he attaches to that,” thought Anna, looking angrily at him. She looked past the lady out of the window at the people who seemed whirling by as they ran beside the train or stood on the platform. The train, jerking at regular intervals at the junctions of the rails, rolled by the platform, past a stone wall, a signal-box, past other trains; the wheels, moving more smoothly and evenly, resounded with a slight clang on the rails. The window was lighted up by the bright evening sun, and a slight breeze fluttered the curtain. Anna forgot her fellow passengers, and to the light swaying of the train she fell to thinking again, as she breathed the fresh air.\n\n“Yes, what did I stop at? That I couldn’t conceive a position in which life would not be a misery, that we are all created to be miserable, and that we all know it, and all invent means of deceiving each other. And when one sees the truth, what is one to do?”\n\n“That’s what reason is given man for, to escape from what worries him,” said the lady in French, lisping affectedly, and obviously pleased with her phrase.\n\nThe words seemed an answer to Anna’s thoughts.\n\n“To escape from what worries him,” repeated Anna. And glancing at the red-cheeked husband and the thin wife, she saw that the sickly wife considered herself misunderstood, and the husband deceived her and encouraged her in that idea of herself. Anna seemed to see all their history and all the crannies of their souls, as it were turning a light upon them. But there was nothing interesting in them, and she pursued her thought.\n\n“Yes, I’m very much worried, and that’s what reason was given me for, to escape; so then one must escape: why not put out the light when there’s nothing more to look at, when it’s sickening to look at it all? But how? Why did the conductor run along the footboard, why are they shrieking, those young men in that train? why are they talking, why are they laughing? It’s all falsehood, all lying, all humbug, all cruelty!...”\n\nWhen the train came into the station, Anna got out into the crowd of passengers, and moving apart from them as if they were lepers, she stood on the platform, trying to think what she had come here for, and what she meant to do. Everything that had seemed to her possible before was now so difficult to consider, especially in this noisy crowd of hideous people who would not leave her alone. One moment porters ran up to her proffering their services, then young men, clacking their heels on the planks of the platform and talking loudly, stared at her; people meeting her dodged past on the wrong side. Remembering that she had meant to go on further if there were no answer, she stopped a porter and asked if her coachman were not here with a note from Count Vronsky.\n\n“Count Vronsky? They sent up here from the Vronskys just this minute, to meet Princess Sorokina and her daughter. And what is the coachman like?”\n\nJust as she was talking to the porter, the coachman Mihail, red and cheerful in his smart blue coat and chain, evidently proud of having so successfully performed his commission, came up to her and gave her a letter. She broke it open, and her heart ached before she had read it.\n\n“I am very sorry your note did not reach me. I will be home at ten,” Vronsky had written carelessly....\n\n“Yes, that’s what I expected!” she said to herself with an evil smile.\n\n“Very good, you can go home then,” she said softly, addressing Mihail. She spoke softly because the rapidity of her heart’s beating hindered her breathing. “No, I won’t let you make me miserable,” she thought menacingly, addressing not him, not herself, but the power that made her suffer, and she walked along the platform.\n\nTwo maid-servants walking along the platform turned their heads, staring at her and making some remarks about her dress. “Real,” they said of the lace she was wearing. The young men would not leave her in peace. Again they passed by, peering into her face, and with a laugh shouting something in an unnatural voice. The station-master coming up asked her whether she was going by train. A boy selling kvas never took his eyes off her. “My God! where am I to go?” she thought, going farther and farther along the platform. At the end she stopped. Some ladies and children, who had come to meet a gentleman in spectacles, paused in their loud laughter and talking, and stared at her as she reached them. She quickened her pace and walked away from them to the edge of the platform. A luggage train was coming in. The platform began to sway, and she fancied she was in the train again.\n\nAnd all at once she thought of the man crushed by the train the day she had first met Vronsky, and she knew what she had to do. With a rapid, light step she went down the steps that led from the tank to the rails and stopped quite near the approaching train.\n\nShe looked at the lower part of the carriages, at the screws and chains and the tall cast-iron wheel of the first carriage slowly moving up, and trying to measure the middle between the front and back wheels, and the very minute when that middle point would be opposite her.\n\n“There,” she said to herself, looking into the shadow of the carriage, at the sand and coal dust which covered the sleepers—“there, in the very middle, and I will punish him and escape from everyone and from myself.”\n\nShe tried to fling herself below the wheels of the first carriage as it reached her; but the red bag which she tried to drop out of her hand delayed her, and she was too late; she missed the moment. She had to wait for the next carriage. A feeling such as she had known when about to take the first plunge in bathing came upon her, and she crossed herself. That familiar gesture brought back into her soul a whole series of girlish and childish memories, and suddenly the darkness that had covered everything for her was torn apart, and life rose up before her for an instant with all its bright past joys. But she did not take her eyes from the wheels of the second carriage. And exactly at the moment when the space between the wheels came opposite her, she dropped the red bag, and drawing her head back into her shoulders, fell on her hands under the carriage, and lightly, as though she would rise again at once, dropped on to her knees. And at the same instant she was terror-stricken at what she was doing. “Where am I? What am I doing? What for?” She tried to get up, to drop backwards; but something huge and merciless struck her on the head and rolled her on her back. “Lord, forgive me all!” she said, feeling it impossible to struggle. A peasant muttering something was working at the iron above her. And the light by which she had read the book filled with troubles, falsehoods, sorrow, and evil, flared up more brightly than ever before, lighted up for her all that had been in darkness, flickered, began to grow dim, and was quenched forever.\n\n# PART EIGHT Chapter 1\n\nAlmost two months had passed. The hot summer was half over, but Sergey Ivanovitch was only just preparing to leave Moscow.\n\nSergey Ivanovitch’s life had not been uneventful during this time. A year ago he had finished his book, the fruit of six years’ labor, “Sketch of a Survey of the Principles and Forms of Government in Europe and Russia.” Several sections of this book and its introduction had appeared in periodical publications, and other parts had been read by Sergey Ivanovitch to persons of his circle, so that the leading ideas of the work could not be completely novel to the public. But still Sergey Ivanovitch had expected that on its appearance his book would be sure to make a serious impression on society, and if it did not cause a revolution in social science it would, at any rate, make a great stir in the scientific world.\n\nAfter the most conscientious revision the book had last year been published, and had been distributed among the booksellers.\n\nThough he asked no one about it, reluctantly and with feigned indifference answered his friends’ inquiries as to how the book was going, and did not even inquire of the booksellers how the book was selling, Sergey Ivanovitch was all on the alert, with strained attention, watching for the first impression his book would make in the world and in literature.\n\nBut a week passed, a second, a third, and in society no impression whatever could be detected. His friends who were specialists and savants, occasionally—unmistakably from politeness—alluded to it. The rest of his acquaintances, not interested in a book on a learned subject, did not talk of it at all. And society generally—just now especially absorbed in other things—was absolutely indifferent. In the press, too, for a whole month there was not a word about his book.\n\nSergey Ivanovitch had calculated to a nicety the time necessary for writing a review, but a month passed, and a second, and still there was silence.\n\nOnly in the Northern Beetle, in a comic article on the singer Drabanti, who had lost his voice, there was a contemptuous allusion to Koznishev’s book, suggesting that the book had been long ago seen through by everyone, and was a subject of general ridicule.\n\nAt last in the third month a critical article appeared in a serious review. Sergey Ivanovitch knew the author of the article. He had met him once at Golubtsov’s.\n\nThe author of the article was a young man, an invalid, very bold as a writer, but extremely deficient in breeding and shy in personal relations.\n\nIn spite of his absolute contempt for the author, it was with complete respect that Sergey Ivanovitch set about reading the article. The article was awful.\n\nThe critic had undoubtedly put an interpretation upon the book which could not possibly be put on it. But he had selected quotations so adroitly that for people who had not read the book (and obviously scarcely anyone had read it) it seemed absolutely clear that the whole book was nothing but a medley of high-flown phrases, not even—as suggested by marks of interrogation—used appropriately, and that the author of the book was a person absolutely without knowledge of the subject. And all this was so wittily done that Sergey Ivanovitch would not have disowned such wit himself. But that was just what was so awful.\n\nIn spite of the scrupulous conscientiousness with which Sergey Ivanovitch verified the correctness of the critic’s arguments, he did not for a minute stop to ponder over the faults and mistakes which were ridiculed; but unconsciously he began immediately trying to recall every detail of his meeting and conversation with the author of the article.\n\n“Didn’t I offend him in some way?” Sergey Ivanovitch wondered.\n\nAnd remembering that when they met he had corrected the young man about something he had said that betrayed ignorance, Sergey Ivanovitch found the clue to explain the article.\n\nThis article was followed by a deadly silence about the book both in the press and in conversation, and Sergey Ivanovitch saw that his six years’ task, toiled at with such love and labor, had gone, leaving no trace.\n\nSergey Ivanovitch’s position was still more difficult from the fact that, since he had finished his book, he had had no more literary work to do, such as had hitherto occupied the greater part of his time.\n\nSergey Ivanovitch was clever, cultivated, healthy, and energetic, and he did not know what use to make of his energy. Conversations in drawing-rooms, in meetings, assemblies, and committees—everywhere where talk was possible—took up part of his time. But being used for years to town life, he did not waste all his energies in talk, as his less experienced younger brother did, when he was in Moscow. He had a great deal of leisure and intellectual energy still to dispose of.\n\nFortunately for him, at this period so difficult for him from the failure of his book, the various public questions of the dissenting sects, of the American alliance, of the Samara famine, of exhibitions, and of spiritualism, were definitely replaced in public interest by the Slavonic question, which had hitherto rather languidly interested society, and Sergey Ivanovitch, who had been one of the first to raise this subject, threw himself into it heart and soul.\n\nIn the circle to which Sergey Ivanovitch belonged, nothing was talked of or written about just now but the Servian War. Everything that the idle crowd usually does to kill time was done now for the benefit of the Slavonic States. Balls, concerts, dinners, matchboxes, ladies’ dresses, beer, restaurants—everything testified to sympathy with the Slavonic peoples.\n\nFrom much of what was spoken and written on the subject, Sergey Ivanovitch differed on various points. He saw that the Slavonic question had become one of those fashionable distractions which succeed one another in providing society with an object and an occupation. He saw, too, that a great many people were taking up the subject from motives of self-interest and self-advertisement. He recognized that the newspapers published a great deal that was superfluous and exaggerated, with the sole aim of attracting attention and outbidding one another. He saw that in this general movement those who thrust themselves most forward and shouted the loudest were men who had failed and were smarting under a sense of injury—generals without armies, ministers not in the ministry, journalists not on any paper, party leaders without followers. He saw that there was a great deal in it that was frivolous and absurd. But he saw and recognized an unmistakable growing enthusiasm, uniting all classes, with which it was impossible not to sympathize. The massacre of men who were fellow Christians, and of the same Slavonic race, excited sympathy for the sufferers and indignation against the oppressors. And the heroism of the Servians and Montenegrins struggling for a great cause begot in the whole people a longing to help their brothers not in word but in deed.\n\nBut in this there was another aspect that rejoiced Sergey Ivanovitch. That was the manifestation of public opinion. The public had definitely expressed its desire. The soul of the people had, as Sergey Ivanovitch said, found expression. And the more he worked in this cause, the more incontestable it seemed to him that it was a cause destined to assume vast dimensions, to create an epoch.\n\nHe threw himself heart and soul into the service of this great cause, and forgot to think about his book. His whole time now was engrossed by it, so that he could scarcely manage to answer all the letters and appeals addressed to him. He worked the whole spring and part of the summer, and it was only in July that he prepared to go away to his brother’s in the country.\n\nHe was going both to rest for a fortnight, and in the very heart of the people, in the farthest wilds of the country, to enjoy the sight of that uplifting of the spirit of the people, of which, like all residents in the capital and big towns, he was fully persuaded. Katavasov had long been meaning to carry out his promise to stay with Levin, and so he was going with him.\n\n# Chapter 2\n\nSergey Ivanovitch and Katavasov had only just reached the station of the Kursk line, which was particularly busy and full of people that day, when, looking round for the groom who was following with their things, they saw a party of volunteers driving up in four cabs. Ladies met them with bouquets of flowers, and followed by the rushing crowd they went into the station.\n\nOne of the ladies, who had met the volunteers, came out of the hall and addressed Sergey Ivanovitch.\n\n“You too come to see them off?” she asked in French.\n\n“No, I’m going away myself, princess. To my brother’s for a holiday. Do you always see them off?” said Sergey Ivanovitch with a hardly perceptible smile.\n\n“Oh, that would be impossible!” answered the princess. “Is it true that eight hundred have been sent from us already? Malvinsky wouldn’t believe me.”\n\n“More than eight hundred. If you reckon those who have been sent not directly from Moscow, over a thousand,” answered Sergey Ivanovitch.\n\n“There! That’s just what I said!” exclaimed the lady. “And it’s true too, I suppose, that more than a million has been subscribed?”\n\n“Yes, princess.”\n\n“What do you say to today’s telegram? Beaten the Turks again.”\n\n“Yes, so I saw,” answered Sergey Ivanovitch. They were speaking of the last telegram stating that the Turks had been for three days in succession beaten at all points and put to flight, and that tomorrow a decisive engagement was expected.\n\n“Ah, by the way, a splendid young fellow has asked leave to go, and they’ve made some difficulty, I don’t know why. I meant to ask you; I know him; please write a note about his case. He’s being sent by Countess Lidia Ivanovna.”\n\nSergey Ivanovitch asked for all the details the princess knew about the young man, and going into the first-class waiting-room, wrote a note to the person on whom the granting of leave of absence depended, and handed it to the princess.\n\n“You know Count Vronsky, the notorious one ... is going by this train?” said the princess with a smile full of triumph and meaning, when he found her again and gave her the letter.\n\n“I had heard he was going, but I did not know when. By this train?”\n\n“I’ve seen him. He’s here: there’s only his mother seeing him off. It’s the best thing, anyway, that he could do.”\n\n“Oh, yes, of course.”\n\nWhile they were talking the crowd streamed by them into the dining-room. They went forward too, and heard a gentleman with a glass in his hand delivering a loud discourse to the volunteers. “In the service of religion, humanity, and our brothers,” the gentleman said, his voice growing louder and louder; “to this great cause mother Moscow dedicates you with her blessing. Jivio!” he concluded, loudly and tearfully.\n\nEveryone shouted Jivio! and a fresh crowd dashed into the hall, almost carrying the princess off her legs.\n\n“Ah, princess! that was something like!” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, suddenly appearing in the middle of the crowd and beaming upon them with a delighted smile. “Capitally, warmly said, wasn’t it? Bravo! And Sergey Ivanovitch! Why, you ought to have said something—just a few words, you know, to encourage them; you do that so well,” he added with a soft, respectful, and discreet smile, moving Sergey Ivanovitch forward a little by the arm.\n\n“No, I’m just off.”\n\n“Where to?”\n\n“To the country, to my brother’s,” answered Sergey Ivanovitch.\n\n“Then you’ll see my wife. I’ve written to her, but you’ll see her first. Please tell her that they’ve seen me and that it’s ‘all right,’ as the English say. She’ll understand. Oh, and be so good as to tell her I’m appointed secretary of the committee.... But she’ll understand! You know, les petites misères de la vie humaine,” he said, as it were apologizing to the princess. “And Princess Myakaya—not Liza, but Bibish—is sending a thousand guns and twelve nurses. Did I tell you?”\n\n“Yes, I heard so,” answered Koznishev indifferently.\n\n“It’s a pity you’re going away,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch. “Tomorrow we’re giving a dinner to two who’re setting off—Dimer-Bartnyansky from Petersburg and our Veslovsky, Grisha. They’re both going. Veslovsky’s only lately married. There’s a fine fellow for you! Eh, princess?” he turned to the lady.\n\nThe princess looked at Koznishev without replying. But the fact that Sergey Ivanovitch and the princess seemed anxious to get rid of him did not in the least disconcert Stepan Arkadyevitch. Smiling, he stared at the feather in the princess’s hat, and then about him as though he were going to pick something up. Seeing a lady approaching with a collecting box, he beckoned her up and put in a five-rouble note.\n\n“I can never see these collecting boxes unmoved while I’ve money in my pocket,” he said. “And how about today’s telegram? Fine chaps those Montenegrins!”\n\n“You don’t say so!” he cried, when the princess told him that Vronsky was going by this train. For an instant Stepan Arkadyevitch’s face looked sad, but a minute later, when, stroking his mustaches and swinging as he walked, he went into the hall where Vronsky was, he had completely forgotten his own despairing sobs over his sister’s corpse, and he saw in Vronsky only a hero and an old friend.\n\n“With all his faults one can’t refuse to do him justice,” said the princess to Sergey Ivanovitch as soon as Stepan Arkadyevitch had left them. “What a typically Russian, Slav nature! Only, I’m afraid it won’t be pleasant for Vronsky to see him. Say what you will, I’m touched by that man’s fate. Do talk to him a little on the way,” said the princess.\n\n“Yes, perhaps, if it happens so.”\n\n“I never liked him. But this atones for a great deal. He’s not merely going himself, he’s taking a squadron at his own expense.”\n“Yes, so I heard.”\n\nA bell sounded. Everyone crowded to the doors. “Here he is!” said the princess, indicating Vronsky, who with his mother on his arm walked by, wearing a long overcoat and wide-brimmed black hat. Oblonsky was walking beside him, talking eagerly of something.\n\nVronsky was frowning and looking straight before him, as though he did not hear what Stepan Arkadyevitch was saying.\n\nProbably on Oblonsky’s pointing them out, he looked round in the direction where the princess and Sergey Ivanovitch were standing, and without speaking lifted his hat. His face, aged and worn by suffering, looked stony.\n\nGoing onto the platform, Vronsky left his mother and disappeared into a compartment.\n\nOn the platform there rang out “God save the Tsar,” then shouts of “hurrah!” and “jivio!” One of the volunteers, a tall, very young man with a hollow chest, was particularly conspicuous, bowing and waving his felt hat and a nosegay over his head. Then two officers emerged, bowing too, and a stout man with a big beard, wearing a greasy forage cap.\n\n# Chapter 3\n\nSaying good-bye to the princess, Sergey Ivanovitch was joined by Katavasov; together they got into a carriage full to overflowing, and the train started.\n\nAt Tsaritsino station the train was met by a chorus of young men singing “Hail to Thee!” Again the volunteers bowed and poked their heads out, but Sergey Ivanovitch paid no attention to them. He had had so much to do with the volunteers that the type was familiar to him and did not interest him. Katavasov, whose scientific work had prevented his having a chance of observing them hitherto, was very much interested in them and questioned Sergey Ivanovitch.\n\nSergey Ivanovitch advised him to go into the second-class and talk to them himself. At the next station Katavasov acted on this suggestion.\n\nAt the first stop he moved into the second-class and made the acquaintance of the volunteers. They were sitting in a corner of the carriage, talking loudly and obviously aware that the attention of the passengers and Katavasov as he got in was concentrated upon them. More loudly than all talked the tall, hollow-chested young man. He was unmistakably tipsy, and was relating some story that had occurred at his school. Facing him sat a middle-aged officer in the Austrian military jacket of the Guards uniform. He was listening with a smile to the hollow-chested youth, and occasionally pulling him up. The third, in an artillery uniform, was sitting on a box beside them. A fourth was asleep.\n\nEntering into conversation with the youth, Katavasov learned that he was a wealthy Moscow merchant who had run through a large fortune before he was two-and-twenty. Katavasov did not like him, because he was unmanly and effeminate and sickly. He was obviously convinced, especially now after drinking, that he was performing a heroic action, and he bragged of it in the most unpleasant way.\n\nThe second, the retired officer, made an unpleasant impression too upon Katavasov. He was, it seemed, a man who had tried everything. He had been on a railway, had been a land-steward, and had started factories, and he talked, quite without necessity, of all he had done, and used learned expressions quite inappropriately.\n\nThe third, the artilleryman, on the contrary, struck Katavasov very favorably. He was a quiet, modest fellow, unmistakably impressed by the knowledge of the officer and the heroic self-sacrifice of the merchant and saying nothing about himself. When Katavasov asked him what had impelled him to go to Servia, he answered modestly:\n\n“Oh, well, everyone’s going. The Servians want help, too. I’m sorry for them.”\n\n“Yes, you artillerymen especially are scarce there,” said Katavasov.\n\n“Oh, I wasn’t long in the artillery, maybe they’ll put me into the infantry or the cavalry.”\n\n“Into the infantry when they need artillery more than anything?” said Katavasov, fancying from the artilleryman’s apparent age that he must have reached a fairly high grade.\n\n“I wasn’t long in the artillery; I’m a cadet retired,” he said, and he began to explain how he had failed in his examination.\n\nAll of this together made a disagreeable impression on Katavasov, and when the volunteers got out at a station for a drink, Katavasov would have liked to compare his unfavorable impression in conversation with someone. There was an old man in the carriage, wearing a military overcoat, who had been listening all the while to Katavasov’s conversation with the volunteers. When they were left alone, Katavasov addressed him.\n\n“What different positions they come from, all those fellows who are going off there,” Katavasov said vaguely, not wishing to express his own opinion, and at the same time anxious to find out the old man’s views.\n\nThe old man was an officer who had served on two campaigns. He knew what makes a soldier, and judging by the appearance and the talk of those persons, by the swagger with which they had recourse to the bottle on the journey, he considered them poor soldiers. Moreover, he lived in a district town, and he was longing to tell how one soldier had volunteered from his town, a drunkard and a thief whom no one would employ as a laborer. But knowing by experience that in the present condition of the public temper it was dangerous to express an opinion opposed to the general one, and especially to criticize the volunteers unfavorably, he too watched Katavasov without committing himself.\n\n“Well, men are wanted there,” he said, laughing with his eyes. And they fell to talking of the last war news, and each concealed from the other his perplexity as to the engagement expected next day, since the Turks had been beaten, according to the latest news, at all points. And so they parted, neither giving expression to his opinion.\n\nKatavasov went back to his own carriage, and with reluctant hypocrisy reported to Sergey Ivanovitch his observations of the volunteers, from which it would appear that they were capital fellows.\n\nAt a big station at a town the volunteers were again greeted with shouts and singing, again men and women with collecting boxes appeared, and provincial ladies brought bouquets to the volunteers and followed them into the refreshment room; but all this was on a much smaller and feebler scale than in Moscow.\n\n# Chapter 4\n\nWhile the train was stopping at the provincial town, Sergey Ivanovitch did not go to the refreshment room, but walked up and down the platform.\n\nThe first time he passed Vronsky’s compartment he noticed that the curtain was drawn over the window; but as he passed it the second time he saw the old countess at the window. She beckoned to Koznishev.\n\n“I’m going, you see, taking him as far as Kursk,” she said.\n\n“Yes, so I heard,” said Sergey Ivanovitch, standing at her window and peeping in. “What a noble act on his part!” he added, noticing that Vronsky was not in the compartment.\n\n“Yes, after his misfortune, what was there for him to do?”\n\n“What a terrible thing it was!” said Sergey Ivanovitch.\n\n“Ah, what I have been through! But do get in.... Ah, what I have been through!” she repeated, when Sergey Ivanovitch had got in and sat down beside her. “You can’t conceive it! For six weeks he did not speak to anyone, and would not touch food except when I implored him. And not for one minute could we leave him alone. We took away everything he could have used against himself. We lived on the ground floor, but there was no reckoning on anything. You know, of course, that he had shot himself once already on her account,” she said, and the old lady’s eyelashes twitched at the recollection. “Yes, hers was the fitting end for such a woman. Even the death she chose was low and vulgar.”\n\n“It’s not for us to judge, countess,” said Sergey Ivanovitch; “but I can understand that it has been very hard for you.”\n\n“Ah, don’t speak of it! I was staying on my estate, and he was with me. A note was brought him. He wrote an answer and sent it off. We hadn’t an idea that she was close by at the station. In the evening I had only just gone to my room, when my Mary told me a lady had thrown herself under the train. Something seemed to strike me at once. I knew it was she. The first thing I said was, he was not to be told. But they’d told him already. His coachman was there and saw it all. When I ran into his room, he was beside himself—it was fearful to see him. He didn’t say a word, but galloped off there. I don’t know to this day what happened there, but he was brought back at death’s door. I shouldn’t have known him. Prostration complète, the doctor said. And that was followed almost by madness. Oh, why talk of it!” said the countess with a wave of her hand. “It was an awful time! No, say what you will, she was a bad woman. Why, what is the meaning of such desperate passions? It was all to show herself something out of the way. Well, and that she did do. She brought herself to ruin and two good men—her husband and my unhappy son.”\n\n“And what did her husband do?” asked Sergey Ivanovitch.\n\n“He has taken her daughter. Alexey was ready to agree to anything at first. Now it worries him terribly that he should have given his own child away to another man. But he can’t take back his word. Karenin came to the funeral. But we tried to prevent his meeting Alexey. For him, for her husband, it was easier, anyway. She had set him free. But my poor son was utterly given up to her. He had thrown up everything, his career, me, and even then she had no mercy on him, but of set purpose she made his ruin complete. No, say what you will, her very death was the death of a vile woman, of no religious feeling. God forgive me, but I can’t help hating the memory of her, when I look at my son’s misery!”\n\n“But how is he now?”\n\n“It was a blessing from Providence for us—this Servian war. I’m old, and I don’t understand the rights and wrongs of it, but it’s come as a providential blessing to him. Of course for me, as his mother, it’s terrible; and what’s worse, they say, ce n’est pas très bien vu à Pétersbourg. But it can’t be helped! It was the one thing that could rouse him. Yashvin—a friend of his—he had lost all he had at cards and he was going to Servia. He came to see him and persuaded him to go. Now it’s an interest for him. Do please talk to him a little. I want to distract his mind. He’s so low-spirited. And as bad luck would have it, he has toothache too. But he’ll be delighted to see you. Please do talk to him; he’s walking up and down on that side.”\n\nSergey Ivanovitch said he would be very glad to, and crossed over to the other side of the station.\n\n# Chapter 5\n\nIn the slanting evening shadows cast by the baggage piled up on the platform, Vronsky in his long overcoat and slouch hat, with his hands in his pockets, strode up and down, like a wild beast in a cage, turning sharply after twenty paces. Sergey Ivanovitch fancied, as he approached him, that Vronsky saw him but was pretending not to see. This did not affect Sergey Ivanovitch in the slightest. He was above all personal considerations with Vronsky.\n\nAt that moment Sergey Ivanovitch looked upon Vronsky as a man taking an important part in a great cause, and Koznishev thought it his duty to encourage him and express his approval. He went up to him.\n\nVronsky stood still, looked intently at him, recognized him, and going a few steps forward to meet him, shook hands with him very warmly.\n\n“Possibly you didn’t wish to see me,” said Sergey Ivanovitch, “but couldn’t I be of use to you?”\n\n“There’s no one I should less dislike seeing than you,” said Vronsky. “Excuse me; and there’s nothing in life for me to like.”\n\n“I quite understand, and I merely meant to offer you my services,” said Sergey Ivanovitch, scanning Vronsky’s face, full of unmistakable suffering. “Wouldn’t it be of use to you to have a letter to Ristitch—to Milan?”\n\n“Oh, no!” Vronsky said, seeming to understand him with difficulty. “If you don’t mind, let’s walk on. It’s so stuffy among the carriages. A letter? No, thank you; to meet death one needs no letters of introduction. Nor for the Turks....” he said, with a smile that was merely of the lips. His eyes still kept their look of angry suffering.\n\n“Yes; but you might find it easier to get into relations, which are after all essential, with anyone prepared to see you. But that’s as you like. I was very glad to hear of your intention. There have been so many attacks made on the volunteers, and a man like you raises them in public estimation.”\n\n“My use as a man,” said Vronsky, “is that life’s worth nothing to me. And that I’ve enough bodily energy to cut my way into their ranks, and to trample on them or fall—I know that. I’m glad there’s something to give my life for, for it’s not simply useless but loathsome to me. Anyone’s welcome to it.” And his jaw twitched impatiently from the incessant gnawing toothache, that prevented him from even speaking with a natural expression.\n\n“You will become another man, I predict,” said Sergey Ivanovitch, feeling touched. “To deliver one’s brother-men from bondage is an aim worth death and life. God grant you success outwardly—and inwardly peace,” he added, and he held out his hand. Vronsky warmly pressed his outstretched hand.\n\n“Yes, as a weapon I may be of some use. But as a man, I’m a wreck,” he jerked out.\n\nHe could hardly speak for the throbbing ache in his strong teeth, that were like rows of ivory in his mouth. He was silent, and his eyes rested on the wheels of the tender, slowly and smoothly rolling along the rails.\n\nAnd all at once a different pain, not an ache, but an inner trouble, that set his whole being in anguish, made him for an instant forget his toothache. As he glanced at the tender and the rails, under the influence of the conversation with a friend he had not met since his misfortune, he suddenly recalled her—that is, what was left of her when he had run like one distraught into the cloak room of the railway station—on the table, shamelessly sprawling out among strangers, the bloodstained body so lately full of life; the head unhurt dropping back with its weight of hair, and the curling tresses about the temples, and the exquisite face, with red, half-opened mouth, the strange, fixed expression, piteous on the lips and awful in the still open eyes, that seemed to utter that fearful phrase—that he would be sorry for it—that she had said when they were quarreling.\n\nAnd he tried to think of her as she was when he met her the first time, at a railway station too, mysterious, exquisite, loving, seeking and giving happiness, and not cruelly revengeful as he remembered her on that last moment. He tried to recall his best moments with her, but those moments were poisoned forever. He could only think of her as triumphant, successful in her menace of a wholly useless remorse never to be effaced. He lost all consciousness of toothache, and his face worked with sobs.\n\nPassing twice up and down beside the baggage in silence and regaining his self-possession, he addressed Sergey Ivanovitch calmly:\n\n“You have had no telegrams since yesterday’s? Yes, driven back for a third time, but a decisive engagement expected for tomorrow.”\n\nAnd after talking a little more of King Milan’s proclamation, and the immense effect it might have, they parted, going to their carriages on hearing the second bell.\n\n# Chapter 6\n\nSergey Ivanovitch had not telegraphed to his brother to send to meet him, as he did not know when he should be able to leave Moscow. Levin was not at home when Katavasov and Sergey Ivanovitch in a fly hired at the station drove up to the steps of the Pokrovskoe house, as black as Moors from the dust of the road. Kitty, sitting on the balcony with her father and sister, recognized her brother-in-law, and ran down to meet him.\n\n“What a shame not to have let us know,” she said, giving her hand to Sergey Ivanovitch, and putting her forehead up for him to kiss.\n\n“We drove here capitally, and have not put you out,” answered Sergey Ivanovitch. “I’m so dirty. I’m afraid to touch you. I’ve been so busy, I didn’t know when I should be able to tear myself away. And so you’re still as ever enjoying your peaceful, quiet happiness,” he said, smiling, “out of the reach of the current in your peaceful backwater. Here’s our friend Fyodor Vassilievitch who has succeeded in getting here at last.”\n\n“But I’m not a negro, I shall look like a human being when I wash,” said Katavasov in his jesting fashion, and he shook hands and smiled, his teeth flashing white in his black face.\n\n“Kostya will be delighted. He has gone to his settlement. It’s time he should be home.”\n\n“Busy as ever with his farming. It really is a peaceful backwater,” said Katavasov; “while we in town think of nothing but the Servian war. Well, how does our friend look at it? He’s sure not to think like other people.”\n\n“Oh, I don’t know, like everybody else,” Kitty answered, a little embarrassed, looking round at Sergey Ivanovitch. “I’ll send to fetch him. Papa’s staying with us. He’s only just come home from abroad.”\n\nAnd making arrangements to send for Levin and for the guests to wash, one in his room and the other in what had been Dolly’s, and giving orders for their luncheon, Kitty ran out onto the balcony, enjoying the freedom, and rapidity of movement, of which she had been deprived during the months of her pregnancy.\n\n“It’s Sergey Ivanovitch and Katavasov, a professor,” she said.\n\n“Oh, that’s a bore in this heat,” said the prince.\n\n“No, papa, he’s very nice, and Kostya’s very fond of him,” Kitty said, with a deprecating smile, noticing the irony on her father’s face.\n\n“Oh, I didn’t say anything.”\n\n“You go to them, darling,” said Kitty to her sister, “and entertain them. They saw Stiva at the station; he was quite well. And I must run to Mitya. As ill-luck would have it, I haven’t fed him since tea. He’s awake now, and sure to be screaming.” And feeling a rush of milk, she hurried to the nursery.\n\nThis was not a mere guess; her connection with the child was still so close, that she could gauge by the flow of her milk his need of food, and knew for certain he was hungry.\n\nShe knew he was crying before she reached the nursery. And he was indeed crying. She heard him and hastened. But the faster she went, the louder he screamed. It was a fine healthy scream, hungry and impatient.\n\n“Has he been screaming long, nurse, very long?” said Kitty hurriedly, seating herself on a chair, and preparing to give the baby the breast. “But give me him quickly. Oh, nurse, how tiresome you are! There, tie the cap afterwards, do!”\n\nThe baby’s greedy scream was passing into sobs.\n\n“But you can’t manage so, ma’am,” said Agafea Mihalovna, who was almost always to be found in the nursery. “He must be put straight. A-oo! a-oo!” she chanted over him, paying no attention to the mother.\n\nThe nurse brought the baby to his mother. Agafea Mihalovna followed him with a face dissolving with tenderness.\n\n“He knows me, he knows me. In God’s faith, Katerina Alexandrovna, ma’am, he knew me!” Agafea Mihalovna cried above the baby’s screams.\n\nBut Kitty did not hear her words. Her impatience kept growing, like the baby’s.\n\nTheir impatience hindered things for a while. The baby could not get hold of the breast right, and was furious.\n\nAt last, after despairing, breathless screaming, and vain sucking, things went right, and mother and child felt simultaneously soothed, and both subsided into calm.\n\n“But poor darling, he’s all in perspiration!” said Kitty in a whisper, touching the baby.\n\n“What makes you think he knows you?” she added, with a sidelong glance at the baby’s eyes, that peered roguishly, as she fancied, from under his cap, at his rhythmically puffing cheeks, and the little red-palmed hand he was waving.\n\n“Impossible! If he knew anyone, he would have known me,” said Kitty, in response to Agafea Mihalovna’s statement, and she smiled.\n\nShe smiled because, though she said he could not know her, in her heart she was sure that he knew not merely Agafea Mihalovna, but that he knew and understood everything, and knew and understood a great deal too that no one else knew, and that she, his mother, had learned and come to understand only through him. To Agafea Mihalovna, to the nurse, to his grandfather, to his father even, Mitya was a living being, requiring only material care, but for his mother he had long been a moral being, with whom there had been a whole series of spiritual relations already.\n\n“When he wakes up, please God, you shall see for yourself. Then when I do like this, he simply beams on me, the darling! Simply beams like a sunny day!” said Agafea Mihalovna.\n\n“Well, well; then we shall see,” whispered Kitty. “But now go away, he’s going to sleep.”\n\n# Chapter 7\n\nAgafea Mihalovna went out on tiptoe; the nurse let down the blind, chased a fly out from under the muslin canopy of the crib, and a bumblebee struggling on the window-frame, and sat down waving a faded branch of birch over the mother and the baby.\n\n“How hot it is! if God would send a drop of rain,” she said.\n\n“Yes, yes, sh—sh—sh——” was all Kitty answered, rocking a little, and tenderly squeezing the plump little arm, with rolls of fat at the wrist, which Mitya still waved feebly as he opened and shut his eyes. That hand worried Kitty; she longed to kiss the little hand, but was afraid to for fear of waking the baby. At last the little hand ceased waving, and the eyes closed. Only from time to time, as he went on sucking, the baby raised his long, curly eyelashes and peeped at his mother with wet eyes, that looked black in the twilight. The nurse had left off fanning, and was dozing. From above came the peals of the old prince’s voice, and the chuckle of Katavasov.\n\n“They have got into talk without me,” thought Kitty, “but still it’s vexing that Kostya’s out. He’s sure to have gone to the bee-house again. Though it’s a pity he’s there so often, still I’m glad. It distracts his mind. He’s become altogether happier and better now than in the spring. He used to be so gloomy and worried that I felt frightened for him. And how absurd he is!” she whispered, smiling.\n\nShe knew what worried her husband. It was his unbelief. Although, if she had been asked whether she supposed that in the future life, if he did not believe, he would be damned, she would have had to admit that he would be damned, his unbelief did not cause her unhappiness. And she, confessing that for an unbeliever there can be no salvation, and loving her husband’s soul more than anything in the world, thought with a smile of his unbelief, and told herself that he was absurd.\n\n“What does he keep reading philosophy of some sort for all this year?” she wondered. “If it’s all written in those books, he can understand them. If it’s all wrong, why does he read them? He says himself that he would like to believe. Then why is it he doesn’t believe? Surely from his thinking so much? And he thinks so much from being solitary. He’s always alone, alone. He can’t talk about it all to us. I fancy he’ll be glad of these visitors, especially Katavasov. He likes discussions with them,” she thought, and passed instantly to the consideration of where it would be more convenient to put Katavasov, to sleep alone or to share Sergey Ivanovitch’s room. And then an idea suddenly struck her, which made her shudder and even disturb Mitya, who glanced severely at her. “I do believe the laundress hasn’t sent the washing yet, and all the best sheets are in use. If I don’t see to it, Agafea Mihalovna will give Sergey Ivanovitch the wrong sheets,” and at the very idea of this the blood rushed to Kitty’s face.\n\n“Yes, I will arrange it,” she decided, and going back to her former thoughts, she remembered that some spiritual question of importance had been interrupted, and she began to recall what. “Yes, Kostya, an unbeliever,” she thought again with a smile.\n\n“Well, an unbeliever then! Better let him always be one than like Madame Stahl, or what I tried to be in those days abroad. No, he won’t ever sham anything.”\n\nAnd a recent instance of his goodness rose vividly to her mind. A fortnight ago a penitent letter had come from Stepan Arkadyevitch to Dolly. He besought her to save his honor, to sell her estate to pay his debts. Dolly was in despair, she detested her husband, despised him, pitied him, resolved on a separation, resolved to refuse, but ended by agreeing to sell part of her property. After that, with an irrepressible smile of tenderness, Kitty recalled her husband’s shamefaced embarrassment, his repeated awkward efforts to approach the subject, and how at last, having thought of the one means of helping Dolly without wounding her pride, he had suggested to Kitty—what had not occurred to her before—that she should give up her share of the property.\n\n“He an unbeliever indeed! With his heart, his dread of offending anyone, even a child! Everything for others, nothing for himself. Sergey Ivanovitch simply considers it as Kostya’s duty to be his steward. And it’s the same with his sister. Now Dolly and her children are under his guardianship; all these peasants who come to him every day, as though he were bound to be at their service.”\n\n“Yes, only be like your father, only like him,” she said, handing Mitya over to the nurse, and putting her lips to his cheek.\n\n# Chapter 8\n\nEver since, by his beloved brother’s deathbed, Levin had first glanced into the questions of life and death in the light of these new convictions, as he called them, which had during the period from his twentieth to his thirty-fourth year imperceptibly replaced his childish and youthful beliefs—he had been stricken with horror, not so much of death, as of life, without any knowledge of whence, and why, and how, and what it was. The physical organization, its decay, the indestructibility of matter, the law of the conservation of energy, evolution, were the words which usurped the place of his old belief. These words and the ideas associated with them were very well for intellectual purposes. But for life they yielded nothing, and Levin felt suddenly like a man who has changed his warm fur cloak for a muslin garment, and going for the first time into the frost is immediately convinced, not by reason, but by his whole nature that he is as good as naked, and that he must infallibly perish miserably.\n\nFrom that moment, though he did not distinctly face it, and still went on living as before, Levin had never lost this sense of terror at his lack of knowledge.\n\nHe vaguely felt, too, that what he called his new convictions were not merely lack of knowledge, but that they were part of a whole order of ideas, in which no knowledge of what he needed was possible.\n\nAt first, marriage, with the new joys and duties bound up with it, had completely crowded out these thoughts. But of late, while he was staying in Moscow after his wife’s confinement, with nothing to do, the question that clamored for solution had more and more often, more and more insistently, haunted Levin’s mind.\n\nThe question was summed up for him thus: “If I do not accept the answers Christianity gives to the problems of my life, what answers do I accept?” And in the whole arsenal of his convictions, so far from finding any satisfactory answers, he was utterly unable to find anything at all like an answer.\n\nHe was in the position of a man seeking food in toy shops and tool shops.\n\nInstinctively, unconsciously, with every book, with every conversation, with every man he met, he was on the lookout for light on these questions and their solution.\n\nWhat puzzled and distracted him above everything was that the majority of men of his age and circle had, like him, exchanged their old beliefs for the same new convictions, and yet saw nothing to lament in this, and were perfectly satisfied and serene. So that, apart from the principal question, Levin was tortured by other questions too. Were these people sincere? he asked himself, or were they playing a part? or was it that they understood the answers science gave to these problems in some different, clearer sense than he did? And he assiduously studied both these men’s opinions and the books which treated of these scientific explanations.\n\nOne fact he had found out since these questions had engrossed his mind, was that he had been quite wrong in supposing from the recollections of the circle of his young days at college, that religion had outlived its day, and that it was now practically non-existent. All the people nearest to him who were good in their lives were believers. The old prince, and Lvov, whom he liked so much, and Sergey Ivanovitch, and all the women believed, and his wife believed as simply as he had believed in his earliest childhood, and ninety-nine hundredths of the Russian people, all the working people for whose life he felt the deepest respect, believed.\n\nAnother fact of which he became convinced, after reading many scientific books, was that the men who shared his views had no other construction to put on them, and that they gave no explanation of the questions which he felt he could not live without answering, but simply ignored their existence and attempted to explain other questions of no possible interest to him, such as the evolution of organisms, the materialistic theory of consciousness, and so forth.\n\nMoreover, during his wife’s confinement, something had happened that seemed extraordinary to him. He, an unbeliever, had fallen into praying, and at the moment he prayed, he believed. But that moment had passed, and he could not make his state of mind at that moment fit into the rest of his life.\n\nHe could not admit that at that moment he knew the truth, and that now he was wrong; for as soon as he began thinking calmly about it, it all fell to pieces. He could not admit that he was mistaken then, for his spiritual condition then was precious to him, and to admit that it was a proof of weakness would have been to desecrate those moments. He was miserably divided against himself, and strained all his spiritual forces to the utmost to escape from this condition.\n\n# Chapter 9\n\nThese doubts fretted and harassed him, growing weaker or stronger from time to time, but never leaving him. He read and thought, and the more he read and the more he thought, the further he felt from the aim he was pursuing.\n\nOf late in Moscow and in the country, since he had become convinced that he would find no solution in the materialists, he had read and re-read thoroughly Plato, Spinoza, Kant, Schelling, Hegel, and Schopenhauer, the philosophers who gave a non-materialistic explanation of life.\n\nTheir ideas seemed to him fruitful when he was reading or was himself seeking arguments to refute other theories, especially those of the materialists; but as soon as he began to read or sought for himself a solution of problems, the same thing always happened. As long as he followed the fixed definition of obscure words such as spirit, will, freedom, essence, purposely letting himself go into the snare of words the philosophers set for him, he seemed to comprehend something. But he had only to forget the artificial train of reasoning, and to turn from life itself to what had satisfied him while thinking in accordance with the fixed definitions, and all this artificial edifice fell to pieces at once like a house of cards, and it became clear that the edifice had been built up out of those transposed words, apart from anything in life more important than reason.\n\nAt one time, reading Schopenhauer, he put in place of his will the word love, and for a couple of days this new philosophy charmed him, till he removed a little away from it. But then, when he turned from life itself to glance at it again, it fell away too, and proved to be the same muslin garment with no warmth in it.\n\nHis brother Sergey Ivanovitch advised him to read the theological works of Homiakov. Levin read the second volume of Homiakov’s works, and in spite of the elegant, epigrammatic, argumentative style which at first repelled him, he was impressed by the doctrine of the church he found in them. He was struck at first by the idea that the apprehension of divine truths had not been vouchsafed to man, but to a corporation of men bound together by love—to the church. What delighted him was the thought how much easier it was to believe in a still existing living church, embracing all the beliefs of men, and having God at its head, and therefore holy and infallible, and from it to accept the faith in God, in the creation, the fall, the redemption, than to begin with God, a mysterious, far-away God, the creation, etc. But afterwards, on reading a Catholic writer’s history of the church, and then a Greek orthodox writer’s history of the church, and seeing that the two churches, in their very conception infallible, each deny the authority of the other, Homiakov’s doctrine of the church lost all its charm for him, and this edifice crumbled into dust like the philosophers’ edifices.\n\nAll that spring he was not himself, and went through fearful moments of horror.\n\n“Without knowing what I am and why I am here, life’s impossible; and that I can’t know, and so I can’t live,” Levin said to himself.\n\n“In infinite time, in infinite matter, in infinite space, is formed a bubble-organism, and that bubble lasts a while and bursts, and that bubble is Me.”\n\nIt was an agonizing error, but it was the sole logical result of ages of human thought in that direction.\n\nThis was the ultimate belief on which all the systems elaborated by human thought in almost all their ramifications rested. It was the prevalent conviction, and of all other explanations Levin had unconsciously, not knowing when or how, chosen it, as anyway the clearest, and made it his own.\n\nBut it was not merely a falsehood, it was the cruel jeer of some wicked power, some evil, hateful power, to whom one could not submit.\n\nHe must escape from this power. And the means of escape every man had in his own hands. He had but to cut short this dependence on evil. And there was one means—death.\n\nAnd Levin, a happy father and husband, in perfect health, was several times so near suicide that he hid the cord that he might not be tempted to hang himself, and was afraid to go out with his gun for fear of shooting himself.\n\nBut Levin did not shoot himself, and did not hang himself; he went on living.\n\n# Chapter 10\n\nWhen Levin thought what he was and what he was living for, he could find no answer to the questions and was reduced to despair, but he left off questioning himself about it. It seemed as though he knew both what he was and for what he was living, for he acted and lived resolutely and without hesitation. Indeed, in these latter days he was far more decided and unhesitating in life than he had ever been.\n\nWhen he went back to the country at the beginning of June, he went back also to his usual pursuits. The management of the estate, his relations with the peasants and the neighbors, the care of his household, the management of his sister’s and brother’s property, of which he had the direction, his relations with his wife and kindred, the care of his child, and the new bee-keeping hobby he had taken up that spring, filled all his time.\n\nThese things occupied him now, not because he justified them to himself by any sort of general principles, as he had done in former days; on the contrary, disappointed by the failure of his former efforts for the general welfare, and too much occupied with his own thought and the mass of business with which he was burdened from all sides, he had completely given up thinking of the general good, and he busied himself with all this work simply because it seemed to him that he must do what he was doing—that he could not do otherwise. In former days—almost from childhood, and increasingly up to full manhood—when he had tried to do anything that would be good for all, for humanity, for Russia, for the whole village, he had noticed that the idea of it had been pleasant, but the work itself had always been incoherent, that then he had never had a full conviction of its absolute necessity, and that the work that had begun by seeming so great, had grown less and less, till it vanished into nothing. But now, since his marriage, when he had begun to confine himself more and more to living for himself, though he experienced no delight at all at the thought of the work he was doing, he felt a complete conviction of its necessity, saw that it succeeded far better than in old days, and that it kept on growing more and more.\n\nNow, involuntarily it seemed, he cut more and more deeply into the soil like a plough, so that he could not be drawn out without turning aside the furrow.\n\nTo live the same family life as his father and forefathers—that is, in the same condition of culture—and to bring up his children in the same, was incontestably necessary. It was as necessary as dining when one was hungry. And to do this, just as it was necessary to cook dinner, it was necessary to keep the mechanism of agriculture at Pokrovskoe going so as to yield an income. Just as incontestably as it was necessary to repay a debt was it necessary to keep the property in such a condition that his son, when he received it as a heritage, would say “thank you” to his father as Levin had said “thank you” to his grandfather for all he built and planted. And to do this it was necessary to look after the land himself, not to let it, and to breed cattle, manure the fields, and plant timber.\n\nIt was impossible not to look after the affairs of Sergey Ivanovitch, of his sister, of the peasants who came to him for advice and were accustomed to do so—as impossible as to fling down a child one is carrying in one’s arms. It was necessary to look after the comfort of his sister-in-law and her children, and of his wife and baby, and it was impossible not to spend with them at least a short time each day.\n\nAnd all this, together with shooting and his new bee-keeping, filled up the whole of Levin’s life, which had no meaning at all for him, when he began to think.\n\nBut besides knowing thoroughly what he had to do, Levin knew in just the same way how he had to do it all, and what was more important than the rest.\n\nHe knew he must hire laborers as cheaply as possible; but to hire men under bond, paying them in advance at less than the current rate of wages, was what he must not do, even though it was very profitable. Selling straw to the peasants in times of scarcity of provender was what he might do, even though he felt sorry for them; but the tavern and the pothouse must be put down, though they were a source of income. Felling timber must be punished as severely as possible, but he could not exact forfeits for cattle being driven onto his fields; and though it annoyed the keeper and made the peasants not afraid to graze their cattle on his land, he could not keep their cattle as a punishment.\n\nTo Pyotr, who was paying a money-lender ten per cent. a month, he must lend a sum of money to set him free. But he could not let off peasants who did not pay their rent, nor let them fall into arrears. It was impossible to overlook the bailiff’s not having mown the meadows and letting the hay spoil; and it was equally impossible to mow those acres where a young copse had been planted. It was impossible to excuse a laborer who had gone home in the busy season because his father was dying, however sorry he might feel for him, and he must subtract from his pay those costly months of idleness. But it was impossible not to allow monthly rations to the old servants who were of no use for anything.\n\nLevin knew that when he got home he must first of all go to his wife, who was unwell, and that the peasants who had been waiting for three hours to see him could wait a little longer. He knew too that, regardless of all the pleasure he felt in taking a swarm, he must forego that pleasure, and leave the old man to see to the bees alone, while he talked to the peasants who had come after him to the bee-house.\n\nWhether he were acting rightly or wrongly he did not know, and far from trying to prove that he was, nowadays he avoided all thought or talk about it.\n\nReasoning had brought him to doubt, and prevented him from seeing what he ought to do and what he ought not. When he did not think, but simply lived, he was continually aware of the presence of an infallible judge in his soul, determining which of two possible courses of action was the better and which was the worse, and as soon as he did not act rightly, he was at once aware of it.\n\nSo he lived, not knowing and not seeing any chance of knowing what he was and what he was living for, and harassed at this lack of knowledge to such a point that he was afraid of suicide, and yet firmly laying down his own individual definite path in life.\n\n# Chapter 11\n\nThe day on which Sergey Ivanovitch came to Pokrovskoe was one of Levin’s most painful days. It was the very busiest working time, when all the peasantry show an extraordinary intensity of self-sacrifice in labor, such as is never shown in any other conditions of life, and would be highly esteemed if the men who showed these qualities themselves thought highly of them, and if it were not repeated every year, and if the results of this intense labor were not so simple.\n\nTo reap and bind the rye and oats and to carry it, to mow the meadows, turn over the fallows, thrash the seed and sow the winter corn—all this seems so simple and ordinary; but to succeed in getting through it all everyone in the village, from the old man to the young child, must toil incessantly for three or four weeks, three times as hard as usual, living on rye-beer, onions, and black bread, thrashing and carrying the sheaves at night, and not giving more than two or three hours in the twenty-four to sleep. And every year this is done all over Russia.\n\nHaving lived the greater part of his life in the country and in the closest relations with the peasants, Levin always felt in this busy time that he was infected by this general quickening of energy in the people.\n\nIn the early morning he rode over to the first sowing of the rye, and to the oats, which were being carried to the stacks, and returning home at the time his wife and sister-in-law were getting up, he drank coffee with them and walked to the farm, where a new thrashing machine was to be set working to get ready the seed-corn.\n\nHe was standing in the cool granary, still fragrant with the leaves of the hazel branches interlaced on the freshly peeled aspen beams of the new thatch roof. He gazed through the open door in which the dry bitter dust of the thrashing whirled and played, at the grass of the thrashing floor in the sunlight and the fresh straw that had been brought in from the barn, then at the speckly-headed, white-breasted swallows that flew chirping in under the roof and, fluttering their wings, settled in the crevices of the doorway, then at the peasants bustling in the dark, dusty barn, and he thought strange thoughts.\n\n“Why is it all being done?” he thought. “Why am I standing here, making them work? What are they all so busy for, trying to show their zeal before me? What is that old Matrona, my old friend, toiling for? (I doctored her, when the beam fell on her in the fire)” he thought, looking at a thin old woman who was raking up the grain, moving painfully with her bare, sun-blackened feet over the uneven, rough floor. “Then she recovered, but today or tomorrow or in ten years she won’t; they’ll bury her, and nothing will be left either of her or of that smart girl in the red jacket, who with that skillful, soft action shakes the ears out of their husks. They’ll bury her and this piebald horse, and very soon too,” he thought, gazing at the heavily moving, panting horse that kept walking up the wheel that turned under him. “And they will bury her and Fyodor the thrasher with his curly beard full of chaff and his shirt torn on his white shoulders—they will bury him. He’s untying the sheaves, and giving orders, and shouting to the women, and quickly setting straight the strap on the moving wheel. And what’s more, it’s not them alone—me they’ll bury too, and nothing will be left. What for?”\n\nHe thought this, and at the same time looked at his watch to reckon how much they thrashed in an hour. He wanted to know this so as to judge by it the task to set for the day.\n\n“It’ll soon be one, and they’re only beginning the third sheaf,” thought Levin. He went up to the man that was feeding the machine, and shouting over the roar of the machine he told him to put it in more slowly. “You put in too much at a time, Fyodor. Do you see—it gets choked, that’s why it isn’t getting on. Do it evenly.”\n\nFyodor, black with the dust that clung to his moist face, shouted something in response, but still went on doing it as Levin did not want him to.\n\nLevin, going up to the machine, moved Fyodor aside, and began feeding the corn in himself. Working on till the peasants’ dinner hour, which was not long in coming, he went out of the barn with Fyodor and fell into talk with him, stopping beside a neat yellow sheaf of rye laid on the thrashing floor for seed.\n\nFyodor came from a village at some distance from the one in which Levin had once allotted land to his cooperative association. Now it had been let to a former house porter.\n\nLevin talked to Fyodor about this land and asked whether Platon, a well-to-do peasant of good character belonging to the same village, would not take the land for the coming year.\n\n“It’s a high rent; it wouldn’t pay Platon, Konstantin Dmitrievitch,” answered the peasant, picking the ears off his sweat-drenched shirt.\n\n“But how does Kirillov make it pay?”\n\n“Mituh!” (so the peasant called the house porter, in a tone of contempt), “you may be sure he’ll make it pay, Konstantin Dmitrievitch! He’ll get his share, however he has to squeeze to get it! He’s no mercy on a Christian. But Uncle Fokanitch” (so he called the old peasant Platon), “do you suppose he’d flay the skin off a man? Where there’s debt, he’ll let anyone off. And he’ll not wring the last penny out. He’s a man too.”\n\n“But why will he let anyone off?”\n\n“Oh, well, of course, folks are different. One man lives for his own wants and nothing else, like Mituh, he only thinks of filling his belly, but Fokanitch is a righteous man. He lives for his soul. He does not forget God.”\n\n“How thinks of God? How does he live for his soul?” Levin almost shouted.\n\n“Why, to be sure, in truth, in God’s way. Folks are different. Take you now, you wouldn’t wrong a man....”\n\n“Yes, yes, good-bye!” said Levin, breathless with excitement, and turning round he took his stick and walked quickly away towards home. At the peasant’s words that Fokanitch lived for his soul, in truth, in God’s way, undefined but significant ideas seemed to burst out as though they had been locked up, and all striving towards one goal, they thronged whirling through his head, blinding him with their light.\n\n# Chapter 12\n\nLevin strode along the highroad, absorbed not so much in his thoughts (he could not yet disentangle them) as in his spiritual condition, unlike anything he had experienced before.\n\nThe words uttered by the peasant had acted on his soul like an electric shock, suddenly transforming and combining into a single whole the whole swarm of disjointed, impotent, separate thoughts that incessantly occupied his mind. These thoughts had unconsciously been in his mind even when he was talking about the land.\n\nHe was aware of something new in his soul, and joyfully tested this new thing, not yet knowing what it was.\n\n“Not living for his own wants, but for God? For what God? And could one say anything more senseless than what he said? He said that one must not live for one’s own wants, that is, that one must not live for what we understand, what we are attracted by, what we desire, but must live for something incomprehensible, for God, whom no one can understand nor even define. What of it? Didn’t I understand those senseless words of Fyodor’s? And understanding them, did I doubt of their truth? Did I think them stupid, obscure, inexact? No, I understood him, and exactly as he understands the words. I understood them more fully and clearly than I understand anything in life, and never in my life have I doubted nor can I doubt about it. And not only I, but everyone, the whole world understands nothing fully but this, and about this only they have no doubt and are always agreed.\n\n“And I looked out for miracles, complained that I did not see a miracle which would convince me. A material miracle would have persuaded me. And here is a miracle, the sole miracle possible, continually existing, surrounding me on all sides, and I never noticed it!\n\n“Fyodor says that Kirillov lives for his belly. That’s comprehensible and rational. All of us as rational beings can’t do anything else but live for our belly. And all of a sudden the same Fyodor says that one mustn’t live for one’s belly, but must live for truth, for God, and at a hint I understand him! And I and millions of men, men who lived ages ago and men living now—peasants, the poor in spirit and the learned, who have thought and written about it, in their obscure words saying the same thing—we are all agreed about this one thing: what we must live for and what is good. I and all men have only one firm, incontestable, clear knowledge, and that knowledge cannot be explained by the reason—it is outside it, and has no causes and can have no effects.\n\n“If goodness has causes, it is not goodness; if it has effects, a reward, it is not goodness either. So goodness is outside the chain of cause and effect.\n\n“And yet I know it, and we all know it.\n\n“What could be a greater miracle than that?\n\n“Can I have found the solution of it all? can my sufferings be over?” thought Levin, striding along the dusty road, not noticing the heat nor his weariness, and experiencing a sense of relief from prolonged suffering. This feeling was so delicious that it seemed to him incredible. He was breathless with emotion and incapable of going farther; he turned off the road into the forest and lay down in the shade of an aspen on the uncut grass. He took his hat off his hot head and lay propped on his elbow in the lush, feathery, woodland grass.\n\n“Yes, I must make it clear to myself and understand,” he thought, looking intently at the untrampled grass before him, and following the movements of a green beetle, advancing along a blade of couch-grass and lifting up in its progress a leaf of goat-weed. “What have I discovered?” he asked himself, bending aside the leaf of goat-weed out of the beetle’s way and twisting another blade of grass above for the beetle to cross over onto it. “What is it makes me glad? What have I discovered?\n\n“I have discovered nothing. I have only found out what I knew. I understand the force that in the past gave me life, and now too gives me life. I have been set free from falsity, I have found the Master.\n\n“Of old I used to say that in my body, that in the body of this grass and of this beetle (there, she didn’t care for the grass, she’s opened her wings and flown away), there was going on a transformation of matter in accordance with physical, chemical, and physiological laws. And in all of us, as well as in the aspens and the clouds and the misty patches, there was a process of evolution. Evolution from what? into what?—Eternal evolution and struggle.... As though there could be any sort of tendency and struggle in the eternal! And I was astonished that in spite of the utmost effort of thought along that road I could not discover the meaning of life, the meaning of my impulses and yearnings. Now I say that I know the meaning of my life: ‘To live for God, for my soul.’ And this meaning, in spite of its clearness, is mysterious and marvelous. Such, indeed, is the meaning of everything existing. Yes, pride,” he said to himself, turning over on his stomach and beginning to tie a noose of blades of grass, trying not to break them.\n\n“And not merely pride of intellect, but dulness of intellect. And most of all, the deceitfulness; yes, the deceitfulness of intellect. The cheating knavishness of intellect, that’s it,” he said to himself.\n\nAnd he briefly went through, mentally, the whole course of his ideas during the last two years, the beginning of which was the clear confronting of death at the sight of his dear brother hopelessly ill.\n\nThen, for the first time, grasping that for every man, and himself too, there was nothing in store but suffering, death, and forgetfulness, he had made up his mind that life was impossible like that, and that he must either interpret life so that it would not present itself to him as the evil jest of some devil, or shoot himself.\n\nBut he had not done either, but had gone on living, thinking, and feeling, and had even at that very time married, and had had many joys and had been happy, when he was not thinking of the meaning of his life.\n\nWhat did this mean? It meant that he had been living rightly, but thinking wrongly.\n\nHe had lived (without being aware of it) on those spiritual truths that he had sucked in with his mother’s milk, but he had thought, not merely without recognition of these truths, but studiously ignoring them.\n\nNow it was clear to him that he could only live by virtue of the beliefs in which he had been brought up.\n\n“What should I have been, and how should I have spent my life, if I had not had these beliefs, if I had not known that I must live for God and not for my own desires? I should have robbed and lied and killed. Nothing of what makes the chief happiness of my life would have existed for me.” And with the utmost stretch of imagination he could not conceive the brutal creature he would have been himself, if he had not known what he was living for.\n\n“I looked for an answer to my question. And thought could not give an answer to my question—it is incommensurable with my question. The answer has been given me by life itself, in my knowledge of what is right and what is wrong. And that knowledge I did not arrive at in any way, it was given to me as to all men, given, because I could not have got it from anywhere.\n\n“Where could I have got it? By reason could I have arrived at knowing that I must love my neighbor and not oppress him? I was told that in my childhood, and I believed it gladly, for they told me what was already in my soul. But who discovered it? Not reason. Reason discovered the struggle for existence, and the law that requires us to oppress all who hinder the satisfaction of our desires. That is the deduction of reason. But loving one’s neighbor reason could never discover, because it’s irrational.”\n\n# Chapter 13\n\nAnd Levin remembered a scene he had lately witnessed between Dolly and her children. The children, left to themselves, had begun cooking raspberries over the candles and squirting milk into each other’s mouths with a syringe. Their mother, catching them at these pranks, began reminding them in Levin’s presence of the trouble their mischief gave to the grown-up people, and that this trouble was all for their sake, and that if they smashed the cups they would have nothing to drink their tea out of, and that if they wasted the milk, they would have nothing to eat, and die of hunger.\n\nAnd Levin had been struck by the passive, weary incredulity with which the children heard what their mother said to them. They were simply annoyed that their amusing play had been interrupted, and did not believe a word of what their mother was saying. They could not believe it indeed, for they could not take in the immensity of all they habitually enjoyed, and so could not conceive that what they were destroying was the very thing they lived by.\n\n“That all comes of itself,” they thought, “and there’s nothing interesting or important about it because it has always been so, and always will be so. And it’s all always the same. We’ve no need to think about that, it’s all ready. But we want to invent something of our own, and new. So we thought of putting raspberries in a cup, and cooking them over a candle, and squirting milk straight into each other’s mouths. That’s fun, and something new, and not a bit worse than drinking out of cups.”\n\n“Isn’t it just the same that we do, that I did, searching by the aid of reason for the significance of the forces of nature and the meaning of the life of man?” he thought.\n\n“And don’t all the theories of philosophy do the same, trying by the path of thought, which is strange and not natural to man, to bring him to a knowledge of what he has known long ago, and knows so certainly that he could not live at all without it? Isn’t it distinctly to be seen in the development of each philosopher’s theory, that he knows what is the chief significance of life beforehand, just as positively as the peasant Fyodor, and not a bit more clearly than he, and is simply trying by a dubious intellectual path to come back to what everyone knows?\n\n“Now then, leave the children to themselves to get things alone and make their crockery, get the milk from the cows, and so on. Would they be naughty then? Why, they’d die of hunger! Well, then, leave us with our passions and thoughts, without any idea of the one God, of the Creator, or without any idea of what is right, without any idea of moral evil.\n\n“Just try and build up anything without those ideas!\n\n“We only try to destroy them, because we’re spiritually provided for. Exactly like the children!\n\n“Whence have I that joyful knowledge, shared with the peasant, that alone gives peace to my soul? Whence did I get it?\n\n“Brought up with an idea of God, a Christian, my whole life filled with the spiritual blessings Christianity has given me, full of them, and living on those blessings, like the children I did not understand them, and destroy, that is try to destroy, what I live by. And as soon as an important moment of life comes, like the children when they are cold and hungry, I turn to Him, and even less than the children when their mother scolds them for their childish mischief, do I feel that my childish efforts at wanton madness are reckoned against me.\n\n“Yes, what I know, I know not by reason, but it has been given to me, revealed to me, and I know it with my heart, by faith in the chief thing taught by the church.\n\n“The church! the church!” Levin repeated to himself. He turned over on the other side, and leaning on his elbow, fell to gazing into the distance at a herd of cattle crossing over to the river.\n\n“But can I believe in all the church teaches?” he thought, trying himself, and thinking of everything that could destroy his present peace of mind. Intentionally he recalled all those doctrines of the church which had always seemed most strange and had always been a stumbling block to him.\n\n“The Creation? But how did I explain existence? By existence? By nothing? The devil and sin. But how do I explain evil?... The atonement?...\n\n“But I know nothing, nothing, and I can know nothing but what has been told to me and all men.”\n\nAnd it seemed to him that there was not a single article of faith of the church which could destroy the chief thing—faith in God, in goodness, as the one goal of man’s destiny.\n\nUnder every article of faith of the church could be put the faith in the service of truth instead of one’s desires. And each doctrine did not simply leave that faith unshaken, each doctrine seemed essential to complete that great miracle, continually manifest upon earth, that made it possible for each man and millions of different sorts of men, wise men and imbeciles, old men and children—all men, peasants, Lvov, Kitty, beggars and kings to understand perfectly the same one thing, and to build up thereby that life of the soul which alone is worth living, and which alone is precious to us.\n\nLying on his back, he gazed up now into the high, cloudless sky. “Do I not know that that is infinite space, and that it is not a round arch? But, however I screw up my eyes and strain my sight, I cannot see it not round and not bounded, and in spite of my knowing about infinite space, I am incontestably right when I see a solid blue dome, and more right than when I strain my eyes to see beyond it.”\n\nLevin ceased thinking, and only, as it were, listened to mysterious voices that seemed talking joyfully and earnestly within him.\n\n“Can this be faith?” he thought, afraid to believe in his happiness. “My God, I thank Thee!” he said, gulping down his sobs, and with both hands brushing away the tears that filled his eyes.\n\n# Chapter 14\n\nLevin looked before him and saw a herd of cattle, then he caught sight of his trap with Raven in the shafts, and the coachman, who, driving up to the herd, said something to the herdsman. Then he heard the rattle of the wheels and the snort of the sleek horse close by him. But he was so buried in his thoughts that he did not even wonder why the coachman had come for him.\n\nHe only thought of that when the coachman had driven quite up to him and shouted to him. “The mistress sent me. Your brother has come, and some gentleman with him.”\n\nLevin got into the trap and took the reins. As though just roused out of sleep, for a long while Levin could not collect his faculties. He stared at the sleek horse flecked with lather between his haunches and on his neck, where the harness rubbed, stared at Ivan the coachman sitting beside him, and remembered that he was expecting his brother, thought that his wife was most likely uneasy at his long absence, and tried to guess who was the visitor who had come with his brother. And his brother and his wife and the unknown guest seemed to him now quite different from before. He fancied that now his relations with all men would be different.\n\n“With my brother there will be none of that aloofness there always used to be between us, there will be no disputes; with Kitty there shall never be quarrels; with the visitor, whoever he may be, I will be friendly and nice; with the servants, with Ivan, it will all be different.”\n\nPulling the stiff rein and holding in the good horse that snorted with impatience and seemed begging to be let go, Levin looked round at Ivan sitting beside him, not knowing what to do with his unoccupied hand, continually pressing down his shirt as it puffed out, and he tried to find something to start a conversation about with him. He would have said that Ivan had pulled the saddle-girth up too high, but that was like blame, and he longed for friendly, warm talk. Nothing else occurred to him.\n\n“Your honor must keep to the right and mind that stump,” said the coachman, pulling the rein Levin held.\n\n“Please don’t touch and don’t teach me!” said Levin, angered by this interference. Now, as always, interference made him angry, and he felt sorrowfully at once how mistaken had been his supposition that his spiritual condition could immediately change him in contact with reality.\n\nHe was not a quarter of a mile from home when he saw Grisha and Tanya running to meet him.\n\n“Uncle Kostya! mamma’s coming, and grandfather, and Sergey Ivanovitch, and someone else,” they said, clambering up into the trap.\n\n“Who is he?”\n\n“An awfully terrible person! And he does like this with his arms,” said Tanya, getting up in the trap and mimicking Katavasov.\n\n“Old or young?” asked Levin, laughing, reminded of someone, he did not know whom, by Tanya’s performance.\n\n“Oh, I hope it’s not a tiresome person!” thought Levin.\n\nAs soon as he turned, at a bend in the road, and saw the party coming, Levin recognized Katavasov in a straw hat, walking along swinging his arms just as Tanya had shown him. Katavasov was very fond of discussing metaphysics, having derived his notions from natural science writers who had never studied metaphysics, and in Moscow Levin had had many arguments with him of late.\n\nAnd one of these arguments, in which Katavasov had obviously considered that he came off victorious, was the first thing Levin thought of as he recognized him.\n\n“No, whatever I do, I won’t argue and give utterance to my ideas lightly,” he thought.\n\nGetting out of the trap and greeting his brother and Katavasov, Levin asked about his wife.\n\n“She has taken Mitya to Kolok” (a copse near the house). “She meant to have him out there because it’s so hot indoors,” said Dolly. Levin had always advised his wife not to take the baby to the wood, thinking it unsafe, and he was not pleased to hear this.\n\n“She rushes about from place to place with him,” said the prince, smiling. “I advised her to try putting him in the ice cellar.”\n\n“She meant to come to the bee-house. She thought you would be there. We are going there,” said Dolly.\n\n“Well, and what are you doing?” said Sergey Ivanovitch, falling back from the rest and walking beside him.\n\n“Oh, nothing special. Busy as usual with the land,” answered Levin. “Well, and what about you? Come for long? We have been expecting you for such a long time.”\n\n“Only for a fortnight. I’ve a great deal to do in Moscow.”\n\nAt these words the brothers’ eyes met, and Levin, in spite of the desire he always had, stronger than ever just now, to be on affectionate and still more open terms with his brother, felt an awkwardness in looking at him. He dropped his eyes and did not know what to say.\n\nCasting over the subjects of conversation that would be pleasant to Sergey Ivanovitch, and would keep him off the subject of the Servian war and the Slavonic question, at which he had hinted by the allusion to what he had to do in Moscow, Levin began to talk of Sergey Ivanovitch’s book.\n\n“Well, have there been reviews of your book?” he asked.\n\nSergey Ivanovitch smiled at the intentional character of the question.\n\n“No one is interested in that now, and I less than anyone,” he said. “Just look, Darya Alexandrovna, we shall have a shower,” he added, pointing with a sunshade at the white rain clouds that showed above the aspen tree-tops.\n\nAnd these words were enough to re-establish again between the brothers that tone—hardly hostile, but chilly—which Levin had been so longing to avoid.\n\nLevin went up to Katavasov.\n\n“It was jolly of you to make up your mind to come,” he said to him.\n\n“I’ve been meaning to a long while. Now we shall have some discussion, we’ll see to that. Have you been reading Spencer?”\n\n“No, I’ve not finished reading him,” said Levin. “But I don’t need him now.”\n\n“How’s that? that’s interesting. Why so?”\n\n“I mean that I’m fully convinced that the solution of the problems that interest me I shall never find in him and his like. Now....”\n\nBut Katavasov’s serene and good-humored expression suddenly struck him, and he felt such tenderness for his own happy mood, which he was unmistakably disturbing by this conversation, that he remembered his resolution and stopped short.\n\n“But we’ll talk later on,” he added. “If we’re going to the bee-house, it’s this way, along this little path,” he said, addressing them all.\n\nGoing along the narrow path to a little uncut meadow covered on one side with thick clumps of brilliant heart’s-ease among which stood up here and there tall, dark green tufts of hellebore, Levin settled his guests in the dense, cool shade of the young aspens on a bench and some stumps purposely put there for visitors to the bee-house who might be afraid of the bees, and he went off himself to the hut to get bread, cucumbers, and fresh honey, to regale them with.\n\nTrying to make his movements as deliberate as possible, and listening to the bees that buzzed more and more frequently past him, he walked along the little path to the hut. In the very entry one bee hummed angrily, caught in his beard, but he carefully extricated it. Going into the shady outer room, he took down from the wall his veil, that hung on a peg, and putting it on, and thrusting his hands into his pockets, he went into the fenced-in bee-garden, where there stood in the midst of a closely mown space in regular rows, fastened with bast on posts, all the hives he knew so well, the old stocks, each with its own history, and along the fences the younger swarms hived that year. In front of the openings of the hives, it made his eyes giddy to watch the bees and drones whirling round and round about the same spot, while among them the working bees flew in and out with spoils or in search of them, always in the same direction into the wood to the flowering lime trees and back to the hives.\n\nHis ears were filled with the incessant hum in various notes, now the busy hum of the working bee flying quickly off, then the blaring of the lazy drone, and the excited buzz of the bees on guard protecting their property from the enemy and preparing to sting. On the farther side of the fence the old bee-keeper was shaving a hoop for a tub, and he did not see Levin. Levin stood still in the midst of the beehives and did not call him.\n\nHe was glad of a chance to be alone to recover from the influence of ordinary actual life, which had already depressed his happy mood. He thought that he had already had time to lose his temper with Ivan, to show coolness to his brother, and to talk flippantly with Katavasov.\n\n“Can it have been only a momentary mood, and will it pass and leave no trace?” he thought. But the same instant, going back to his mood, he felt with delight that something new and important had happened to him. Real life had only for a time overcast the spiritual peace he had found, but it was still untouched within him.\n\nJust as the bees, whirling round him, now menacing him and distracting his attention, prevented him from enjoying complete physical peace, forced him to restrain his movements to avoid them, so had the petty cares that had swarmed about him from the moment he got into the trap restricted his spiritual freedom; but that lasted only so long as he was among them. Just as his bodily strength was still unaffected, in spite of the bees, so too was the spiritual strength that he had just become aware of.\n\n# Chapter 15\n\n“Do you know, Kostya, with whom Sergey Ivanovitch traveled on his way here?” said Dolly, doling out cucumbers and honey to the children; “with Vronsky! He’s going to Servia.”\n\n“And not alone; he’s taking a squadron out with him at his own expense,” said Katavasov.\n\n“That’s the right thing for him,” said Levin. “Are volunteers still going out then?” he added, glancing at Sergey Ivanovitch.\n\nSergey Ivanovitch did not answer. He was carefully with a blunt knife getting a live bee covered with sticky honey out of a cup full of white honeycomb.\n\n“I should think so! You should have seen what was going on at the station yesterday!” said Katavasov, biting with a juicy sound into a cucumber.\n\n“Well, what is one to make of it? For mercy’s sake, do explain to me, Sergey Ivanovitch, where are all those volunteers going, whom are they fighting with?” asked the old prince, unmistakably taking up a conversation that had sprung up in Levin’s absence.\n\n“With the Turks,” Sergey Ivanovitch answered, smiling serenely, as he extricated the bee, dark with honey and helplessly kicking, and put it with the knife on a stout aspen leaf.\n\n“But who has declared war on the Turks?—Ivan Ivanovitch Ragozov and Countess Lidia Ivanovna, assisted by Madame Stahl?”\n\n“No one has declared war, but people sympathize with their neighbors’ sufferings and are eager to help them,” said Sergey Ivanovitch.\n\n“But the prince is not speaking of help,” said Levin, coming to the assistance of his father-in-law, “but of war. The prince says that private persons cannot take part in war without the permission of the government.”\n\n“Kostya, mind, that’s a bee! Really, they’ll sting us!” said Dolly, waving away a wasp.\n\n“But that’s not a bee, it’s a wasp,” said Levin.\n\n“Well now, well, what’s your own theory?” Katavasov said to Levin with a smile, distinctly challenging him to a discussion. “Why have not private persons the right to do so?”\n\n“Oh, my theory’s this: war is on one side such a beastly, cruel, and awful thing, that no one man, not to speak of a Christian, can individually take upon himself the responsibility of beginning wars; that can only be done by a government, which is called upon to do this, and is driven inevitably into war. On the other hand, both political science and common sense teach us that in matters of state, and especially in the matter of war, private citizens must forego their personal individual will.”\n\nSergey Ivanovitch and Katavasov had their replies ready, and both began speaking at the same time.\n\n“But the point is, my dear fellow, that there may be cases when the government does not carry out the will of the citizens and then the public asserts its will,” said Katavasov.\n\nBut evidently Sergey Ivanovitch did not approve of this answer. His brows contracted at Katavasov’s words and he said something else.\n\n“You don’t put the matter in its true light. There is no question here of a declaration of war, but simply the expression of a human Christian feeling. Our brothers, one with us in religion and in race, are being massacred. Even supposing they were not our brothers nor fellow-Christians, but simply children, women, old people, feeling is aroused and Russians go eagerly to help in stopping these atrocities. Fancy, if you were going along the street and saw drunken men beating a woman or a child—I imagine you would not stop to inquire whether war had been declared on the men, but would throw yourself on them, and protect the victim.”\n\n“But I should not kill them,” said Levin.\n\n“Yes, you would kill them.”\n\n“I don’t know. If I saw that, I might give way to my impulse of the moment, but I can’t say beforehand. And such a momentary impulse there is not, and there cannot be, in the case of the oppression of the Slavonic peoples.”\n\n“Possibly for you there is not; but for others there is,” said Sergey Ivanovitch, frowning with displeasure. “There are traditions still extant among the people of Slavs of the true faith suffering under the yoke of the ‘unclean sons of Hagar.’ The people have heard of the sufferings of their brethren and have spoken.”\n\n“Perhaps so,” said Levin evasively; “but I don’t see it. I’m one of the people myself, and I don’t feel it.”\n\n“Here am I too,” said the old prince. “I’ve been staying abroad and reading the papers, and I must own, up to the time of the Bulgarian atrocities, I couldn’t make out why it was all the Russians were all of a sudden so fond of their Slavonic brethren, while I didn’t feel the slightest affection for them. I was very much upset, thought I was a monster, or that it was the influence of Carlsbad on me. But since I have been here, my mind’s been set at rest. I see that there are people besides me who’re only interested in Russia, and not in their Slavonic brethren. Here’s Konstantin too.”\n\n“Personal opinions mean nothing in such a case,” said Sergey Ivanovitch; “it’s not a matter of personal opinions when all Russia—the whole people—has expressed its will.”\n\n“But excuse me, I don’t see that. The people don’t know anything about it, if you come to that,” said the old prince.\n\n“Oh, papa!... how can you say that? And last Sunday in church?” said Dolly, listening to the conversation. “Please give me a cloth,” she said to the old man, who was looking at the children with a smile. “Why, it’s not possible that all....”\n\n“But what was it in church on Sunday? The priest had been told to read that. He read it. They didn’t understand a word of it. Then they were told that there was to be a collection for a pious object in church; well, they pulled out their halfpence and gave them, but what for they couldn’t say.”\n\n“The people cannot help knowing; the sense of their own destinies is always in the people, and at such moments as the present that sense finds utterance,” said Sergey Ivanovitch with conviction, glancing at the old bee-keeper.\n\nThe handsome old man, with black grizzled beard and thick silvery hair, stood motionless, holding a cup of honey, looking down from the height of his tall figure with friendly serenity at the gentlefolk, obviously understanding nothing of their conversation and not caring to understand it.\n\n“That’s so, no doubt,” he said, with a significant shake of his head at Sergey Ivanovitch’s words.\n\n“Here, then, ask him. He knows nothing about it and thinks nothing,” said Levin. “Have you heard about the war, Mihalitch?” he said, turning to him. “What they read in the church? What do you think about it? Ought we to fight for the Christians?”\n\n“What should we think? Alexander Nikolaevitch our Emperor has thought for us; he thinks for us indeed in all things. It’s clearer for him to see. Shall I bring a bit more bread? Give the little lad some more?” he said addressing Darya Alexandrovna and pointing to Grisha, who had finished his crust.\n\n“I don’t need to ask,” said Sergey Ivanovitch, “we have seen and are seeing hundreds and hundreds of people who give up everything to serve a just cause, come from every part of Russia, and directly and clearly express their thought and aim. They bring their halfpence or go themselves and say directly what for. What does it mean?”\n\n“It means, to my thinking,” said Levin, who was beginning to get warm, “that among eighty millions of people there can always be found not hundreds, as now, but tens of thousands of people who have lost caste, ne’er-do-wells, who are always ready to go anywhere—to Pogatchev’s bands, to Khiva, to Servia....”\n\n“I tell you that it’s not a case of hundreds or of ne’er-do-wells, but the best representatives of the people!” said Sergey Ivanovitch, with as much irritation as if he were defending the last penny of his fortune. “And what of the subscriptions? In this case it is a whole people directly expressing their will.”\n\n“That word ‘people’ is so vague,” said Levin. “Parish clerks, teachers, and one in a thousand of the peasants, maybe, know what it’s all about. The rest of the eighty millions, like Mihalitch, far from expressing their will, haven’t the faintest idea what there is for them to express their will about. What right have we to say that this is the people’s will?”\n\n# Chapter 16\n\nSergey Ivanovitch, being practiced in argument, did not reply, but at once turned the conversation to another aspect of the subject.\n\n“Oh, if you want to learn the spirit of the people by arithmetical computation, of course it’s very difficult to arrive at it. And voting has not been introduced among us and cannot be introduced, for it does not express the will of the people; but there are other ways of reaching that. It is felt in the air, it is felt by the heart. I won’t speak of those deep currents which are astir in the still ocean of the people, and which are evident to every unprejudiced man; let us look at society in the narrow sense. All the most diverse sections of the educated public, hostile before, are merged in one. Every division is at an end, all the public organs say the same thing over and over again, all feel the mighty torrent that has overtaken them and is carrying them in one direction.”\n\n“Yes, all the newspapers do say the same thing,” said the prince. “That’s true. But so it is the same thing that all the frogs croak before a storm. One can hear nothing for them.”\n\n“Frogs or no frogs, I’m not the editor of a paper and I don’t want to defend them; but I am speaking of the unanimity in the intellectual world,” said Sergey Ivanovitch, addressing his brother. Levin would have answered, but the old prince interrupted him.\n\n“Well, about that unanimity, that’s another thing, one may say,” said the prince. “There’s my son-in-law, Stepan Arkadyevitch, you know him. He’s got a place now on the committee of a commission and something or other, I don’t remember. Only there’s nothing to do in it—why, Dolly, it’s no secret!—and a salary of eight thousand. You try asking him whether his post is of use, he’ll prove to you that it’s most necessary. And he’s a truthful man too, but there’s no refusing to believe in the utility of eight thousand roubles.”\n\n“Yes, he asked me to give a message to Darya Alexandrovna about the post,” said Sergey Ivanovitch reluctantly, feeling the prince’s remark to be ill-timed.\n\n“So it is with the unanimity of the press. That’s been explained to me: as soon as there’s war their incomes are doubled. How can they help believing in the destinies of the people and the Slavonic races ... and all that?”\n\n“I don’t care for many of the papers, but that’s unjust,” said Sergey Ivanovitch.\n\n“I would only make one condition,” pursued the old prince. “Alphonse Karr said a capital thing before the war with Prussia: ‘You consider war to be inevitable? Very good. Let everyone who advocates war be enrolled in a special regiment of advance-guards, for the front of every storm, of every attack, to lead them all!’”\n\n“A nice lot the editors would make!” said Katavasov, with a loud roar, as he pictured the editors he knew in this picked legion.\n\n“But they’d run,” said Dolly, “they’d only be in the way.”\n\n“Oh, if they ran away, then we’d have grape-shot or Cossacks with whips behind them,” said the prince.\n\n“But that’s a joke, and a poor one too, if you’ll excuse my saying so, prince,” said Sergey Ivanovitch.\n\n“I don’t see that it was a joke, that....” Levin was beginning, but Sergey Ivanovitch interrupted him.\n\n“Every member of society is called upon to do his own special work,” said he. “And men of thought are doing their work when they express public opinion. And the single-hearted and full expression of public opinion is the service of the press and a phenomenon to rejoice us at the same time. Twenty years ago we should have been silent, but now we have heard the voice of the Russian people, which is ready to rise as one man and ready to sacrifice itself for its oppressed brethren; that is a great step and a proof of strength.”\n\n“But it’s not only making a sacrifice, but killing Turks,” said Levin timidly. “The people make sacrifices and are ready to make sacrifices for their soul, but not for murder,” he added, instinctively connecting the conversation with the ideas that had been absorbing his mind.\n\n“For their soul? That’s a most puzzling expression for a natural science man, do you understand? What sort of thing is the soul?” said Katavasov, smiling.\n\n“Oh, you know!”\n\n“No, by God, I haven’t the faintest idea!” said Katavasov with a loud roar of laughter.\n\n“‘I bring not peace, but a sword,’ says Christ,” Sergey Ivanovitch rejoined for his part, quoting as simply as though it were the easiest thing to understand the very passage that had always puzzled Levin most.\n\n“That’s so, no doubt,” the old man repeated again. He was standing near them and responded to a chance glance turned in his direction.\n\n“Ah, my dear fellow, you’re defeated, utterly defeated!” cried Katavasov good-humoredly.\n\nLevin reddened with vexation, not at being defeated, but at having failed to control himself and being drawn into argument.\n\n“No, I can’t argue with them,” he thought; “they wear impenetrable armor, while I’m naked.”\n\nHe saw that it was impossible to convince his brother and Katavasov, and he saw even less possibility of himself agreeing with them. What they advocated was the very pride of intellect that had almost been his ruin. He could not admit that some dozens of men, among them his brother, had the right, on the ground of what they were told by some hundreds of glib volunteers swarming to the capital, to say that they and the newspapers were expressing the will and feeling of the people, and a feeling which was expressed in vengeance and murder. He could not admit this, because he neither saw the expression of such feelings in the people among whom he was living, nor found them in himself (and he could not but consider himself one of the persons making up the Russian people), and most of all because he, like the people, did not know and could not know what is for the general good, though he knew beyond a doubt that this general good could be attained only by the strict observance of that law of right and wrong which has been revealed to every man, and therefore he could not wish for war or advocate war for any general objects whatever. He said as Mihalitch did and the people, who had expressed their feeling in the traditional invitations of the Varyagi: “Be princes and rule over us. Gladly we promise complete submission. All the labor, all humiliations, all sacrifices we take upon ourselves; but we will not judge and decide.” And now, according to Sergey Ivanovitch’s account, the people had foregone this privilege they had bought at such a costly price.\n\nHe wanted to say too that if public opinion were an infallible guide, then why were not revolutions and the commune as lawful as the movement in favor of the Slavonic peoples? But these were merely thoughts that could settle nothing. One thing could be seen beyond doubt—that was that at the actual moment the discussion was irritating Sergey Ivanovitch, and so it was wrong to continue it. And Levin ceased speaking and then called the attention of his guests to the fact that the storm clouds were gathering, and that they had better be going home before it rained.\n\n# Chapter 17\n\nThe old prince and Sergey Ivanovitch got into the trap and drove off; the rest of the party hastened homewards on foot.\n\nBut the storm-clouds, turning white and then black, moved down so quickly that they had to quicken their pace to get home before the rain. The foremost clouds, lowering and black as soot-laden smoke, rushed with extraordinary swiftness over the sky. They were still two hundred paces from home and a gust of wind had already blown up, and every second the downpour might be looked for.\n\nThe children ran ahead with frightened and gleeful shrieks. Darya Alexandrovna, struggling painfully with her skirts that clung round her legs, was not walking, but running, her eyes fixed on the children. The men of the party, holding their hats on, strode with long steps beside her. They were just at the steps when a big drop fell splashing on the edge of the iron guttering. The children and their elders after them ran into the shelter of the house, talking merrily.\n\n“Katerina Alexandrovna?” Levin asked of Agafea Mihalovna, who met them with kerchiefs and rugs in the hall.\n\n“We thought she was with you,” she said.\n\n“And Mitya?”\n\n“In the copse, he must be, and the nurse with him.”\n\nLevin snatched up the rugs and ran towards the copse.\n\nIn that brief interval of time the storm clouds had moved on, covering the sun so completely that it was dark as an eclipse. Stubbornly, as though insisting on its rights, the wind stopped Levin, and tearing the leaves and flowers off the lime trees and stripping the white birch branches into strange unseemly nakedness, it twisted everything on one side—acacias, flowers, burdocks, long grass, and tall tree-tops. The peasant girls working in the garden ran shrieking into shelter in the servants’ quarters. The streaming rain had already flung its white veil over all the distant forest and half the fields close by, and was rapidly swooping down upon the copse. The wet of the rain spurting up in tiny drops could be smelt in the air.\n\nHolding his head bent down before him, and struggling with the wind that strove to tear the wraps away from him, Levin was moving up to the copse and had just caught sight of something white behind the oak tree, when there was a sudden flash, the whole earth seemed on fire, and the vault of heaven seemed crashing overhead. Opening his blinded eyes, Levin gazed through the thick veil of rain that separated him now from the copse, and to his horror the first thing he saw was the green crest of the familiar oak-tree in the middle of the copse uncannily changing its position. “Can it have been struck?” Levin hardly had time to think when, moving more and more rapidly, the oak tree vanished behind the other trees, and he heard the crash of the great tree falling upon the others.\n\nThe flash of lightning, the crash of thunder, and the instantaneous chill that ran through him were all merged for Levin in one sense of terror.\n\n“My God! my God! not on them!” he said.\n\nAnd though he thought at once how senseless was his prayer that they should not have been killed by the oak which had fallen now, he repeated it, knowing that he could do nothing better than utter this senseless prayer.\n\nRunning up to the place where they usually went, he did not find them there.\n\nThey were at the other end of the copse under an old lime-tree; they were calling him. Two figures in dark dresses (they had been light summer dresses when they started out) were standing bending over something. It was Kitty with the nurse. The rain was already ceasing, and it was beginning to get light when Levin reached them. The nurse was not wet on the lower part of her dress, but Kitty was drenched through, and her soaked clothes clung to her. Though the rain was over, they still stood in the same position in which they had been standing when the storm broke. Both stood bending over a perambulator with a green umbrella.\n\n“Alive? Unhurt? Thank God!” he said, splashing with his soaked boots through the standing water and running up to them.\n\nKitty’s rosy wet face was turned towards him, and she smiled timidly under her shapeless sopped hat.\n\n“Aren’t you ashamed of yourself? I can’t think how you can be so reckless!” he said angrily to his wife.\n\n“It wasn’t my fault, really. We were just meaning to go, when he made such a to-do that we had to change him. We were just....” Kitty began defending herself.\n\nMitya was unharmed, dry, and still fast asleep.\n\n“Well, thank God! I don’t know what I’m saying!”\n\nThey gathered up the baby’s wet belongings; the nurse picked up the baby and carried it. Levin walked beside his wife, and, penitent for having been angry, he squeezed her hand when the nurse was not looking.\n\n# Chapter 18\n\nDuring the whole of that day, in the extremely different conversations in which he took part, only as it were with the top layer of his mind, in spite of the disappointment of not finding the change he expected in himself, Levin had been all the while joyfully conscious of the fulness of his heart.\n\nAfter the rain it was too wet to go for a walk; besides, the storm clouds still hung about the horizon, and gathered here and there, black and thundery, on the rim of the sky. The whole party spent the rest of the day in the house.\n\nNo more discussions sprang up; on the contrary, after dinner everyone was in the most amiable frame of mind.\n\nAt first Katavasov amused the ladies by his original jokes, which always pleased people on their first acquaintance with him. Then Sergey Ivanovitch induced him to tell them about the very interesting observations he had made on the habits and characteristics of common houseflies, and their life. Sergey Ivanovitch, too, was in good spirits, and at tea his brother drew him on to explain his views of the future of the Eastern question, and he spoke so simply and so well, that everyone listened eagerly.\n\nKitty was the only one who did not hear it all—she was summoned to give Mitya his bath.\n\nA few minutes after Kitty had left the room she sent for Levin to come to the nursery.\n\nLeaving his tea, and regretfully interrupting the interesting conversation, and at the same time uneasily wondering why he had been sent for, as this only happened on important occasions, Levin went to the nursery.\n\nAlthough he had been much interested by Sergey Ivanovitch’s views of the new epoch in history that would be created by the emancipation of forty millions of men of Slavonic race acting with Russia, a conception quite new to him, and although he was disturbed by uneasy wonder at being sent for by Kitty, as soon as he came out of the drawing-room and was alone, his mind reverted at once to the thoughts of the morning. And all the theories of the significance of the Slav element in the history of the world seemed to him so trivial compared with what was passing in his own soul, that he instantly forgot it all and dropped back into the same frame of mind that he had been in that morning.\n\nHe did not, as he had done at other times, recall the whole train of thought—that he did not need. He fell back at once into the feeling which had guided him, which was connected with those thoughts, and he found that feeling in his soul even stronger and more definite than before. He did not, as he had had to do with previous attempts to find comforting arguments, need to revive a whole chain of thought to find the feeling. Now, on the contrary, the feeling of joy and peace was keener than ever, and thought could not keep pace with feeling.\n\nHe walked across the terrace and looked at two stars that had come out in the darkening sky, and suddenly he remembered. “Yes, looking at the sky, I thought that the dome that I see is not a deception, and then I thought something, I shirked facing something,” he mused. “But whatever it was, there can be no disproving it! I have but to think, and all will come clear!”\n\nJust as he was going into the nursery he remembered what it was he had shirked facing. It was that if the chief proof of the Divinity was His revelation of what is right, how is it this revelation is confined to the Christian church alone? What relation to this revelation have the beliefs of the Buddhists, Mohammedans, who preached and did good too?\n\nIt seemed to him that he had an answer to this question; but he had not time to formulate it to himself before he went into the nursery.\n\nKitty was standing with her sleeves tucked up over the baby in the bath. Hearing her husband’s footstep, she turned towards him, summoning him to her with her smile. With one hand she was supporting the fat baby that lay floating and sprawling on its back, while with the other she squeezed the sponge over him.\n\n“Come, look, look!” she said, when her husband came up to her. “Agafea Mihalovna’s right. He knows us!”\n\nMitya had on that day given unmistakable, incontestable signs of recognizing all his friends.\n\nAs soon as Levin approached the bath, the experiment was tried, and it was completely successful. The cook, sent for with this object, bent over the baby. He frowned and shook his head disapprovingly. Kitty bent down to him, he gave her a beaming smile, propped his little hands on the sponge and chirruped, making such a queer little contented sound with his lips, that Kitty and the nurse were not alone in their admiration. Levin, too, was surprised and delighted.\n\nThe baby was taken out of the bath, drenched with water, wrapped in towels, dried, and after a piercing scream, handed to his mother.\n\n“Well, I am glad you are beginning to love him,” said Kitty to her husband, when she had settled herself comfortably in her usual place, with the baby at her breast. “I am so glad! It had begun to distress me. You said you had no feeling for him.”\n\n“No; did I say that? I only said I was disappointed.”\n\n“What! disappointed in him?”\n\n“Not disappointed in him, but in my own feeling; I had expected more. I had expected a rush of new delightful emotion to come as a surprise. And then instead of that—disgust, pity....”\n\nShe listened attentively, looking at him over the baby, while she put back on her slender fingers the rings she had taken off while giving Mitya his bath.\n\n“And most of all, at there being far more apprehension and pity than pleasure. Today, after that fright during the storm, I understand how I love him.”\n\nKitty’s smile was radiant.\n\n“Were you very much frightened?” she said. “So was I too, but I feel it more now that it’s over. I’m going to look at the oak. How nice Katavasov is! And what a happy day we’ve had altogether. And you’re so nice with Sergey Ivanovitch, when you care to be.... Well, go back to them. It’s always so hot and steamy here after the bath.”\n\n# Chapter 19\n\nGoing out of the nursery and being again alone, Levin went back at once to the thought, in which there was something not clear.\n\nInstead of going into the drawing-room, where he heard voices, he stopped on the terrace, and leaning his elbows on the parapet, he gazed up at the sky.\n\nIt was quite dark now, and in the south, where he was looking, there were no clouds. The storm had drifted on to the opposite side of the sky, and there were flashes of lightning and distant thunder from that quarter. Levin listened to the monotonous drip from the lime trees in the garden, and looked at the triangle of stars he knew so well, and the Milky Way with its branches that ran through its midst. At each flash of lightning the Milky Way, and even the bright stars, vanished, but as soon as the lightning died away, they reappeared in their places as though some hand had flung them back with careful aim.\n\n“Well, what is it perplexes me?” Levin said to himself, feeling beforehand that the solution of his difficulties was ready in his soul, though he did not know it yet. “Yes, the one unmistakable, incontestable manifestation of the Divinity is the law of right and wrong, which has come into the world by revelation, and which I feel in myself, and in the recognition of which—I don’t make myself, but whether I will or not—I am made one with other men in one body of believers, which is called the church. Well, but the Jews, the Mohammedans, the Confucians, the Buddhists—what of them?” he put to himself the question he had feared to face. “Can these hundreds of millions of men be deprived of that highest blessing without which life has no meaning?” He pondered a moment, but immediately corrected himself. “But what am I questioning?” he said to himself. “I am questioning the relation to Divinity of all the different religions of all mankind. I am questioning the universal manifestation of God to all the world with all those misty blurs. What am I about? To me individually, to my heart has been revealed a knowledge beyond all doubt, and unattainable by reason, and here I am obstinately trying to express that knowledge in reason and words.\n\n“Don’t I know that the stars don’t move?” he asked himself, gazing at the bright planet which had shifted its position up to the topmost twig of the birch-tree. “But looking at the movements of the stars, I can’t picture to myself the rotation of the earth, and I’m right in saying that the stars move.\n\n“And could the astronomers have understood and calculated anything, if they had taken into account all the complicated and varied motions of the earth? All the marvelous conclusions they have reached about the distances, weights, movements, and deflections of the heavenly bodies are only founded on the apparent motions of the heavenly bodies about a stationary earth, on that very motion I see before me now, which has been so for millions of men during long ages, and was and will be always alike, and can always be trusted. And just as the conclusions of the astronomers would have been vain and uncertain if not founded on observations of the seen heavens, in relation to a single meridian and a single horizon, so would my conclusions be vain and uncertain if not founded on that conception of right, which has been and will be always alike for all men, which has been revealed to me as a Christian, and which can always be trusted in my soul. The question of other religions and their relations to Divinity I have no right to decide, and no possibility of deciding.”\n\n“Oh, you haven’t gone in then?” he heard Kitty’s voice all at once, as she came by the same way to the drawing-room.\n\n“What is it? you’re not worried about anything?” she said, looking intently at his face in the starlight.\n\nBut she could not have seen his face if a flash of lightning had not hidden the stars and revealed it. In that flash she saw his face distinctly, and seeing him calm and happy, she smiled at him.\n\n“She understands,” he thought; “she knows what I’m thinking about. Shall I tell her or not? Yes, I’ll tell her.” But at the moment he was about to speak, she began speaking.\n\n“Kostya! do something for me,” she said; “go into the corner room and see if they’ve made it all right for Sergey Ivanovitch. I can’t very well. See if they’ve put the new wash stand in it.”\n\n“Very well, I’ll go directly,” said Levin, standing up and kissing her.\n\n“No, I’d better not speak of it,” he thought, when she had gone in before him. “It is a secret for me alone, of vital importance for me, and not to be put into words.\n\n“This new feeling has not changed me, has not made me happy and enlightened all of a sudden, as I had dreamed, just like the feeling for my child. There was no surprise in this either. Faith—or not faith—I don’t know what it is—but this feeling has come just as imperceptibly through suffering, and has taken firm root in my soul.\n\n“I shall go on in the same way, losing my temper with Ivan the coachman, falling into angry discussions, expressing my opinions tactlessly; there will be still the same wall between the holy of holies of my soul and other people, even my wife; I shall still go on scolding her for my own terror, and being remorseful for it; I shall still be as unable to understand with my reason why I pray, and I shall still go on praying; but my life now, my whole life apart from anything that can happen to me, every minute of it is no more meaningless, as it was before, but it has the positive meaning of goodness, which I have the power to put into it.”";
if(typeof BODIES_TRANS!=="undefined") BODIES_TRANS["gb-1399"]="행복한 가정은 모두 엇비슷하지만, 불행한 가정은 저마다의 이유로 불행하다.\n\n오블론스키 집안은 온통 뒤죽박죽이었다. 아내는 남편이 한때 자기 집에서 가정교사로 있던 프랑스 여자와 부정한 관계를 맺어 왔다는 사실을 알아냈고, 더 이상 한집에서 같이 살 수 없다고 남편에게 선언해 버렸다. 이런 상태가 사흘째 이어지고 있었고, 부부 당사자들뿐 아니라 가족과 하인들까지 모두 그것을 괴롭게 의식하고 있었다. 집 안의 모든 사람이 자기들이 한데 모여 사는 데에 아무 의미가 없으며, 어느 여인숙에서 우연히 모인 뜨내기 손님들조차 자기들, 그러니까 오블론스키 집안의 가족이며 하인들보다는 서로 더 통하는 데가 있으리라고 느꼈다. 아내는 자기 방에서 나오지 않았고, 남편은 사흘째 집에 들어오지 않았다. 아이들은 온 집 안을 제멋대로 뛰어다녔다. 영국인 가정교사는 가정부와 다투고 친구에게 새 일자리를 알아봐 달라는 편지를 썼다. 남자 요리사는 어제 마침 저녁 식사 때 나가 버렸고, 부엌데기와 마부는 그만두겠다고 통고해 왔다.\n\n다툼이 있은 지 사흘째 되던 날, 스테판 아르카지치 오블론스키 공작—사교계에서 부르는 이름으로는 스티바—은 평소와 같은 시각, 그러니까 아침 여덟 시에 잠에서 깼는데, 아내의 침실이 아니라 서재의 가죽 소파 위에서였다. 그는 마치 다시 긴 잠에 빠져들기라도 할 것처럼, 잘 관리된 통통한 몸을 탄력 있는 소파 위에서 뒤척이며 반대편의 베개를 힘껏 끌어안고 거기에 얼굴을 파묻었다. 그러다 별안간 벌떡 일어나 소파에 앉아 눈을 떴다.\n\n\"그래, 그래, 어떻게 됐더라?\" 그는 꿈을 더듬으며 생각했다. \"그러니까 어떻게 됐지? 옳지! 알라빈이 다름슈타트에서 만찬을 열고 있었어. 아니, 다름슈타트가 아니라 뭔가 미국적인 데였지. 그래, 하지만 그 다름슈타트가 미국에 있었던 거야. 그래, 알라빈은 유리 식탁에서 만찬을 열고 있었고, 그 식탁들이 노래를 불렀어. Il mio tesoro라고—아니, Il mio tesoro가 아니라 그보다 더 좋은 거였지, 그리고 식탁 위에는 작은 유리병 같은 것들이 있었는데, 그것들이 또 여자들이기도 했지.\" 그는 떠올렸다.\n\n* Il mio tesoro: 이탈리아어로 '나의 보물'. 모차르트 오페라 「돈 조반니」의 아리아.\n스테판 아르카지치의 눈이 즐겁게 반짝였고, 그는 미소를 지으며 곰곰이 생각에 잠겼다. \"그래, 좋았어, 아주 좋았어. 그 밖에도 흐뭇한 게 잔뜩 있었는데, 다만 그건 말로 옮길 수도 없고, 깨어 있는 상태로는 생각으로 표현할 수조차 없단 말이야.\" 그러다 서지 커튼 한쪽 옆으로 한 줄기 빛이 새어 드는 것을 보고는, 기분 좋게 소파 가장자리 너머로 발을 내려뜨려 슬리퍼를 발로 더듬어 찾았다. 그것은 지난 생일에 아내가 금빛 모로코가죽에 손수 수를 놓아 만들어 준 선물이었다. 그러고는 지난 구 년 동안 매일 그래 왔듯이, 일어나지도 않은 채 침실에서 늘 실내복이 걸려 있던 자리를 향해 손을 뻗었다. 그러다 문득 자기가 아내의 방이 아니라 서재에서 자고 있다는 것을, 그리고 그 까닭을 떠올렸다. 미소가 얼굴에서 사라졌고, 그는 미간을 찌푸렸다.\n\n\"아, 아, 아! 어휴!……\" 그는 일어났던 일을 모조리 떠올리며 중얼거렸다. 그러자 다시 아내와 다툰 일의 온갖 세세한 정황이며, 자기 처지의 절망스러움이며, 무엇보다도 자기 자신의 잘못이 그의 상상 속에 생생하게 되살아났다.\n\n\"그래, 그녀는 나를 용서하지 않을 거고, 또 용서할 수도 없어. 그리고 무엇보다 끔찍한 건 모두 내 탓이라는 거지—전부 내 탓인데, 그렇다고 내가 비난받을 만한 짓을 한 것도 아니야. 바로 거기에 이 모든 상황의 핵심이 있는 거지.\" 그는 곰곰이 생각했다. \"오, 오, 오!\" 그는 이 다툼이 자기에게 안겨 준 견디기 힘든 괴로운 감각을 떠올리며 절망 속에서 거듭 되뇌었다.\n\n무엇보다 가장 불쾌했던 것은 첫 순간이었다. 극장에서 행복하고 기분 좋은 얼굴로, 아내에게 줄 커다란 배 하나를 손에 들고 돌아온 그는, 응접실에서 아내를 찾지 못했고, 놀랍게도 서재에서도 찾지 못했으며, 마침내 침실에서 모든 것을 폭로하는 불운한 편지를 손에 든 아내를 보았던 것이다.\n\n그의 돌리, 평소 그가 보기에는 집안일의 자질구레한 일에 늘 부산을 떨고 안달복달하며 생각의 폭도 좁은 그 여자가, 편지를 손에 든 채 꼼짝도 않고 앉아, 공포와 절망과 분노의 표정으로 그를 바라보고 있었다.\n\n\"이게 뭐예요? 이게?\" 그녀가 편지를 가리키며 물었다.\n\n그리고 이 기억을 떠올리면서, 흔히 그렇듯이 스테판 아르카지치는 그 사실 자체보다도 아내의 말에 자기가 대응했던 방식 때문에 더 괴로웠다.\n\n그 순간 그에게 일어난 일은, 사람들이 아주 수치스러운 일을 하다가 뜻밖에 들켰을 때 흔히 일어나는 바로 그것이었다. 그는 자기 잘못이 드러남으로써 아내 앞에 놓이게 된 처지에 자기 얼굴을 알맞게 맞추지 못했다. 상처받은 표정을 짓거나, 부인하거나, 변명하거나, 용서를 빌거나, 하다못해 무심한 척하거나—그가 실제로 한 행동보다는 무엇이든 더 나았을 것이다—그렇게 하는 대신, 그의 얼굴은 전혀 의지와 상관없이(척수 반사 작용이지, 하고 생리학을 좋아하는 스테판 아르카지치는 생각했다) 전혀 의지와 상관없이 늘 짓던 그 기분 좋은, 그래서 더없이 멍청한 미소를 지어 버린 것이다.\n\n그 멍청한 미소만큼은 자기 자신을 용서할 수가 없었다. 그 미소를 본 순간, 돌리는 육체적 고통이라도 느낀 듯 몸서리를 쳤고, 그녀 특유의 격정으로 모진 말을 쏟아 내고는 방에서 뛰쳐나가 버렸다. 그 후로 그녀는 남편 보기를 거부했다.\n\n\"이 모든 게 그 멍청한 미소 탓이야.\" 스테판 아르카지치는 생각했다.\n\n\"하지만 어쩌면 좋지? 어쩌면 좋단 말이야?\" 그는 절망에 빠져 혼잣말을 했지만, 아무런 답도 찾지 못했다.\n\n# Chapter 2\n\n스테판 아르카지치는 자기 자신을 대하는 데 있어서는 정직한 사람이었다. 그는 자신을 속이거나 자기 행동을 뉘우치고 있다고 스스로를 설득할 수 없는 사람이었다. 잘생기고 다정다감한 서른네 살의 남자인 자기가, 살아 있는 아이 다섯과 죽은 아이 둘의 어머니이자 자기보다 겨우 한 살 어린 아내를 더는 사랑하지 않는다는 사실을, 지금 와서 뉘우칠 수는 없었다. 그가 뉘우치는 것이라고는 오직 그 사실을 아내에게 더 잘 감추지 못했다는 것뿐이었다. 그러나 그는 자기 처지의 곤란함을 절절히 느꼈고, 아내와 아이들과 자기 자신이 가여웠다. 만약 그 사실이 발각되었을 때 아내가 그토록 충격받으리라는 것을 미리 알았더라면, 어쩌면 자기 죄를 더 잘 숨길 수도 있었을 것이다. 그는 이 문제를 한 번도 또렷이 따져 본 적이 없었지만, 아내가 자기의 부정을 진작에 의심하면서도 그 사실에 눈을 감고 있으리라고 막연히 짐작하고 있었다. 게다가 그는, 이제 젊지도 예쁘지도 않고 어디 하나 두드러지거나 흥미로울 것도 없이 그저 좋은 어머니일 뿐인, 지칠 대로 지친 여자인 아내가, 공정함을 생각해서라도 너그럽게 보아 넘겨야 마땅하다고까지 여기고 있었다. 그런데 결과는 전혀 딴판으로 드러났다.\n\n\"아, 끔찍해! 아아, 끔찍하다고!\" 스테판 아르카지치는 혼자 거듭 되뇌었지만, 무엇을 해야 할지 아무 생각도 떠오르지 않았다. \"게다가 지금까지는 모든 게 얼마나 잘 굴러갔는데! 우리는 얼마나 잘 지냈는데 말이야! 그녀는 아이들 덕에 만족하고 행복해했고, 나는 무슨 일에도 그녀를 간섭하지 않았으며, 아이들이며 집안일이며 모두 제 마음대로 꾸려 가게 내버려 두었어. 하긴 하필 그 여자가 우리 집 가정교사였다는 건 좋지 않아. 그건 나빠! 제 집 가정교사와 시시덕거린다는 건 어딘가 천박하고 상스러운 데가 있지. 하지만 그게 또 어떤 가정교사였느냐고!\" (그는 롤랑 양의 짓궂은 검은 눈동자와 그 미소를 생생하게 떠올렸다.) \"하지만 어쨌든 그녀가 우리 집에 있는 동안에는 나도 자제했단 말이야. 게다가 무엇보다 고약한 건 그녀가 벌써…… 마치 불운이 일부러 그렇게 만든 것만 같다니까! 아, 아! 하지만 어쩌면, 어쩌면 좋단 말이야?\"\n\n해답이라고는 없었다. 다만 삶이 가장 복잡하고 풀 수 없는 문제까지 포함해 모든 물음에 내려 주는 그 보편적인 해답이 있을 뿐이었다. 그 답이란 이런 것이다. 하루하루의 필요에 따라 살아야 한다는 것—다시 말해 자기 자신을 잊어버려야 한다는 것이다. 잠 속에서 자기를 잊는 것은 이제, 적어도 밤이 되기 전까지는 불가능했다. 유리병 여자들이 부르던 그 음악으로 돌아갈 수도 없었다. 그러니 그는 일상이라는 꿈속에서 자기 자신을 잊어야만 했다.\n\n\"그러고 나면 두고 보지.\" 스테판 아르카지치는 혼자 중얼거리고는, 일어나 푸른 비단으로 안을 댄 회색 실내복을 걸치고 술 장식을 매듭지어 묶었다. 그러고는 넓고 헐벗은 가슴 가득히 깊이 숨을 들이마시며, 그 풍채 좋은 몸을 가볍게 떠받치는 두 발을 바깥으로 벌린 채, 늘 그렇듯 자신감 넘치는 걸음으로 창가로 걸어갔다. 그는 블라인드를 끌어 올리고 종을 요란하게 울렸다. 곧바로 오랜 벗인 시종 마트베이가 옷가지와 부츠와 전보 한 통을 들고 나타나 그 부름에 응했다. 마트베이 뒤로는 면도에 필요한 도구를 죄다 갖춘 이발사가 따라 들어왔다.\n\n\"관청에서 온 서류가 있나?\" 스테판 아르카지치가 전보를 받아 들고 거울 앞에 앉으며 물었다.\n\n\"탁자 위에 있습니다.\" 마트베이는 캐묻는 듯한 동정 어린 눈길로 주인을 흘끔 쳐다보며 대답했다. 그러고는 잠시 뜸을 들였다가 능청스러운 미소를 지으며 덧붙였다. \"마차 대여점에서 사람이 다녀갔습니다.\"\n\n스테판 아르카지치는 아무 대꾸도 하지 않고 거울 속으로 마트베이를 흘끗 보기만 했다. 거울 속에서 마주친 그 눈길로 보아, 두 사람이 서로를 이해하고 있음이 분명했다. 스테판 아르카지치의 눈은 이렇게 묻고 있었다. \"왜 그런 말을 나한테 하는 거야? 자네도 모르나?\"\n\n마트베이는 두 손을 윗옷 주머니에 찔러 넣고 한쪽 다리를 앞으로 내민 채, 옅은 미소를 띠고 사람 좋게 잠자코 주인을 바라보았다.\n\n\"일요일에 다시 오라고, 그때까지는 공연히 나리도 자기네도 번거롭게 하지 말라고 일러 두었습니다.\" 그는 말했다. 분명 미리 준비해 둔 문장이었다.\n\n스테판 아르카지치는 마트베이가 농담을 던져 제 쪽으로 관심을 끌고 싶어 한다는 것을 알아차렸다. 그는 전보를 찢어 열고는, 전보에서 늘 그렇듯 틀리게 적힌 단어들을 짐작해 가며 끝까지 읽어 내려갔다. 그러자 그의 얼굴이 환해졌다.\n\n\"마트베이, 내 누이 안나 아르카지예브나가 내일 이리로 온다네.\" 그는 길고 곱슬곱슬한 구레나룻 사이로 분홍빛 길을 내고 있던 이발사의 매끈하고 통통한 손을 잠시 멈춰 세우며 말했다.\n\n\"하느님 감사합니다!\" 마트베이가 말했다. 이 대답으로 그는, 주인과 마찬가지로 이 방문의 의미를—다시 말해 주인이 그토록 아끼는 누이 안나 아르카지예브나가 부부 사이의 화해를 이끌어 낼지도 모른다는 것을—알고 있음을 드러냈다.\n\n\"혼자 오십니까, 아니면 부군과 함께 오십니까?\" 마트베이가 물었다.\n\n이발사가 윗입술을 손질하고 있던 터라 스테판 아르카지치는 대답할 수 없어 손가락 하나를 들어 올렸다. 마트베이가 거울을 향해 고개를 끄덕였다.\n\n\"혼자라네. 위층에 방을 준비할까요?\"\n\n\"다리아 알렉산드로브나께 여쭤서, 그분이 정하시는 대로 하게.\"\n\n\"다리아 알렉산드로브나께요?\" 마트베이가 미심쩍다는 듯 되물었다.\n\n\"그래, 그분께 여쭤. 자, 전보를 받아 가서 그분께 드리고, 그분이 시키는 대로 하게.\"\n\n'한번 떠보시려는 게군.' 마트베이는 알아차렸지만, 그저 이렇게만 말했다. \"알겠습니다, 나리.\"\n\n스테판 아르카지치가 이미 몸을 씻고 머리를 빗어 옷을 입을 채비를 끝냈을 무렵, 마트베이가 삐걱거리는 부츠를 신고 일부러 천천히 걸어 전보를 손에 든 채 방으로 돌아왔다. 이발사는 이미 가고 없었다.\n\n\"다리아 알렉산드로브나께서, 자기는 떠날 거라고 나리께 전하라 하셨습니다. 그분—그러니까 나리—께서는 마음대로 하시라고요.\" 그는 눈으로만 웃으며 말하고는, 두 손을 주머니에 찔러 넣고 고개를 한쪽으로 갸웃한 채 주인을 지켜보았다. 스테판 아르카지치는 잠시 잠자코 있었다. 이윽고 그의 잘생긴 얼굴에 사람 좋으면서도 다소 가련한 미소가 떠올랐다.\n\n\"어이, 마트베이?\" 그가 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 말했다.\n\n\"괜찮습니다, 나리. 마님께서도 마음을 돌리실 겁니다.\" 마트베이가 말했다.\n\n\"마음을 돌린다고?\"\n\n\"네, 나리.\"\n\n\"그렇게 생각하나? 거기 누구야?\" 문가에서 여자의 옷자락 스치는 소리를 듣고 스테판 아르카지치가 물었다.\n\n\"저예요.\" 단호하고 듣기 좋은 여자의 목소리가 들리더니, 유모 마트료나 필리모노브나의 곰보 자국 난 엄한 얼굴이 문틈으로 쑥 들이밀어졌다.\n\n\"무슨 일인가, 마트료나?\" 스테판 아르카지치가 문가로 다가가며 물었다.\n\n스테판 아르카지치는 아내에 대해서는 전적으로 잘못한 것이고 그 자신도 그것을 의식하고 있었지만, 집 안의 거의 모든 사람이(다리아 알렉산드로브나의 든든한 한편인 유모마저도) 그의 편이었다.\n\n\"그래, 무슨 일인가?\" 그는 풀 죽은 목소리로 물었다.\n\n\"마님께 가 보세요, 나리. 다시 한번 잘못을 비세요. 어쩌면 하느님께서 도와주실지도 몰라요. 마님이 어찌나 괴로워하시는지 차마 보기 딱할 지경이에요. 게다가 집 안이 온통 뒤죽박죽이고요. 나리, 아이들을 가엾게 여기셔야지요. 마님께 용서를 비세요, 나리. 달리 도리가 없잖아요! 저지른 일은 감당하셔야지요…….\"\n\"하지만 그 사람이 날 보려고 하지 않아.\"\n\n\"나리는 나리 할 일을 하시면 돼요. 하느님은 자비로우시니까요. 하느님께 기도하세요, 나리, 하느님께 기도하세요.\"\n\n\"자, 그만하면 됐으니 가 보게.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 별안간 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다. \"자, 이제 옷을 입혀 주게.\" 그는 마트베이를 향해 돌아서며 단호하게 실내복을 벗어 던졌다.\n\n마트베이는 이미 말의 멍에처럼 셔츠를 받쳐 들고 있다가, 보이지도 않는 먼지를 후 불어 내고는, 잘 손질된 주인의 몸 위로 흐뭇한 기색이 역력하게 그것을 미끄러뜨려 입혔다.\n\n# Chapter 3\n\n옷을 다 차려입고 나서 스테판 아르카지치는 향수를 약간 뿌리고, 셔츠 소맷부리를 끌어내리고, 담배와 지갑과 성냥, 그리고 두 겹 줄과 인장이 달린 시계를 주머니에 나누어 넣고는, 손수건을 탁탁 털었다. 그리고 자신이 깨끗하고 향기롭고 건강하며, 불행한 처지에도 불구하고 몸은 더없이 개운하다고 느끼면서, 두 다리를 가볍게 흔드는 걸음걸이로 식당으로 들어갔다. 거기에는 이미 커피가 준비되어 있었고, 커피 옆에는 관청에서 온 편지며 서류가 놓여 있었다.\n\n그는 편지를 읽었다. 한 통은 몹시 불쾌한 것으로, 아내의 영지에 있는 숲을 사려는 상인에게서 온 것이었다. 이 숲을 파는 일은 반드시 해야만 했다. 하지만 지금으로서는, 아내와 화해하기 전에는 그 문제를 입에 올릴 수가 없었다. 무엇보다 가장 불쾌한 것은, 자기의 금전적 이해관계가 이런 식으로 아내와의 화해 문제에 끼어든다는 점이었다. 그리고 자기가 그 이해관계에 끌려, 숲을 팔기 위해 아내와의 화해를 구할지도 모른다는 생각—그 생각이 그를 아프게 했다.\n\n편지를 다 읽고 나서 스테판 아르카지치는 관청 서류를 가까이 끌어당겨 두 건의 사안을 빠르게 훑어보고, 큼직한 연필로 몇 자 적어 넣은 다음, 서류를 밀쳐 놓고 커피로 향했다. 커피를 홀짝이면서 그는 아직 축축한 조간신문을 펼쳐 읽기 시작했다.\n\n스테판 아르카지치가 받아 보고 읽는 신문은 자유주의 성향의 것이었는데, 극단적인 것이 아니라 다수가 견지하는 견해를 표방하는 것이었다. 그리고 학문이며 예술이며 정치가 그에게 특별한 관심거리가 아니었음에도, 그는 이 모든 주제에 대해 다수와 자기 신문이 견지하는 견해를 굳게 지녔고, 다수가 그것을 바꿀 때에야 비로소 자기 견해도 바꾸었다—더 엄밀히 말하면, 그는 그것을 바꾸는 것이 아니라, 그의 내부에서 견해가 어느새 저절로 바뀌어 갔다.\n\n스테판 아르카지치는 자기의 정치적 견해나 관점을 스스로 고른 것이 아니었다. 이 정치적 견해와 관점은 저절로 그에게 찾아온 것이었으니, 그가 모자와 외투의 모양을 스스로 고르지 않고 그저 남들이 쓰고 입는 것을 따르는 것과 마찬가지였다. 그리고 특정한 사교계에서 살아가는 그에게는—철든 나이가 되면 으레 생겨나는, 어느 정도의 지적 활동을 향한 욕구 때문에—견해를 갖는다는 것이 모자를 갖는 것만큼이나 필수적이었다. 만일 그가 자기 집단의 많은 사람들도 견지하는 보수주의보다 자유주의를 선호하는 데에 어떤 이유가 있었다면, 그것은 그가 자유주의를 더 합리적이라고 여겨서가 아니라, 그것이 자기 생활 방식에 더 잘 들어맞기 때문이었다. 자유주의 진영은 러시아에서는 모든 것이 잘못되어 있다고 말했는데, 과연 스테판 아르카지치는 빚이 많았고 돈에 쪼들리는 처지가 분명했다. 자유주의 진영은 결혼이란 완전히 시대에 뒤떨어진 제도이며 개조가 필요하다고 말했는데, 과연 가정생활은 스테판 아르카지치에게 별다른 만족을 주지 못했고, 그의 천성에 그토록 거슬리는 거짓말과 위선을 강요했다. 자유주의 진영은 종교란 야만적인 계층의 사람들을 억누르기 위한 굴레에 지나지 않는다고 말했다—아니, 그렇게 이해되도록 내버려 두었다—그리고 스테판 아르카지치는 짧은 예배조차 서 있느라 다리가 아파 견디지 못했고, 이 세상에서의 삶이 이토록 즐거울 수 있는데 저세상에 관한 그 온갖 무시무시하고 거창한 말들이 다 무엇을 위한 것인지 도무지 알 수 없었다. 게다가 농담을 좋아하는 스테판 아르카지치는, 자기 혈통을 자랑하는 사람을 두고 만약 그가 출신을 자랑한다면 류리크에서 멈출 게 아니라 자기 가문의 최초의 시조인 원숭이를 부정하지 말아야 한다고 말하며, 우직한 사람을 당황하게 만드는 일을 즐겼다. 그리하여 자유주의는 스테판 아르카지치에게 하나의 습관이 되었고, 그는 식후에 피우는 시가를 좋아하듯 자기 신문을 좋아했는데, 그것이 머릿속에 옅은 안개를 퍼뜨려 주기 때문이었다. 그는 사설을 읽었는데, 거기에는 오늘날 급진주의가 모든 보수적 요소를 집어삼키려 위협하고 있으며 정부가 혁명의 히드라를 짓밟을 조치를 취해야 한다고 외쳐 대는 것은 전혀 분별없는 짓이고, 오히려 \"우리 견해로는 위험은 그 환상적인 혁명의 히드라에 있는 것이 아니라 진보를 가로막는 전통주의의 완고함에 있다\"는 등등의 주장이 펼쳐져 있었다. 그는 또 다른 기사, 곧 재정에 관한 기사도 읽었는데, 거기에는 벤담과 밀이 언급되어 있었고 내각을 겨냥한 몇몇 빈정거림이 슬쩍 끼어 있었다. 그는 특유의 명민함으로 빈정거림 하나하나의 속뜻을 알아챘고, 그것이 어디서 나왔으며 누구를 어떤 근거로 겨냥한 것인지를 간파했다. 그리고 그것은 늘 그렇듯이 그에게 어떤 만족감을 주었다. 그러나 오늘은 그 만족감이 마트료나 필리모노브나의 충고와 만족스럽지 못한 집안 형편 때문에 씁쓸해져 있었다. 그는 또한 베이스트 백작이 비스바덴으로 떠났다는 소문이 돈다는 것, 더 이상 흰머리가 생기지 않게 해 준다는 것, 경마차를 판다는 것, 어떤 젊은이가 일자리를 구한다는 것 따위도 읽었다. 그러나 이런 정보들은 평소처럼 그에게 조용하고 빈정대는 만족감을 주지 못했다. 신문과 두 잔째의 커피와 버터 바른 빵을 다 비우고 나서 그는 일어나 조끼에 묻은 빵 부스러기를 털어 냈다. 그리고 넓은 가슴을 쫙 펴며 즐겁게 미소를 지었다. 마음속에 특별히 유쾌한 일이 있어서가 아니라—그 즐거운 미소는 좋은 소화에서 비롯된 것이었다.\n\n그러나 이 즐거운 미소가 곧바로 모든 것을 그에게 다시 떠올리게 했고, 그는 생각에 잠겼다.\n\n문밖에서 아이들의 목소리 둘이 들려왔다(스테판 아르카지치는 막내아들 그리샤와 맏딸 타냐의 목소리임을 알아챘다). 아이들은 무언가를 나르다가 떨어뜨린 것이었다.\n\n\"지붕 위에 승객을 앉히지 말랬잖아.\" 여자아이가 영어로 말했다. \"자, 어서 주워!\"\n\n\"온통 뒤죽박죽이군.\" 스테판 아르카지치는 생각했다. \"애들이 저렇게 제멋대로 뛰어다니고 말이야.\" 그러고는 문가로 가서 아이들을 불렀다. 아이들은 기차를 본뜬 상자를 내던지고 아버지에게로 들어왔다.\n\n아버지가 가장 귀여워하는 여자아이는 대담하게 달려와 그를 끌어안고는, 늘 그러듯 그의 구레나룻에서 풍기는 향수 냄새를 즐기며 웃음 띤 얼굴로 그의 목에 매달렸다. 마침내 여자아이는 허리를 숙인 자세 탓에 발그레해지고 다정함으로 환히 빛나는 그의 얼굴에 입을 맞추고는, 두 손을 풀고 다시 달아나려 했다. 그러나 아버지가 그녀를 붙잡았다.\n\n\"엄마는 어떠시니?\" 그는 딸아이의 매끄럽고 보드라운 작은 목을 손으로 쓸어 주며 물었다. \"잘 잤니.\" 그는 인사하러 다가온 아들에게 미소를 지으며 말했다. 그는 자기가 아들을 덜 사랑한다는 것을 의식하고 있었고, 늘 공평하려고 애썼다. 그러나 아들은 그것을 느꼈고, 아버지의 차가운 미소에 미소로 답하지 않았다.\n\n\"엄마요? 일어나셨어요.\" 여자아이가 대답했다.\n\n스테판 아르카지치는 한숨을 쉬었다. '그렇다는 건 또 밤새 한숨도 못 잤다는 거지.' 그는 생각했다.\n\n\"그래, 기분은 좋으시던?\"\n\n여자아이는 아버지와 어머니 사이에 다툼이 있었다는 것을, 그래서 어머니의 기분이 좋을 리 없다는 것을, 또 아버지도 이를 알고 있을 것이며, 이렇게 가볍게 묻는 것은 시치미를 떼는 것임을 알고 있었다. 그래서 아이는 아버지가 부끄러워졌다. 그는 곧바로 그것을 알아챘고, 그 역시 얼굴이 붉어졌다.\n\n\"모르겠어요.\" 아이가 말했다. \"공부하라고는 안 하셨고, 미스 훌이랑 외할머니 댁에 산책 가라고만 하셨어요.\"\n\n\"그래, 가렴, 타냐, 우리 귀염둥이. 아, 잠깐만 기다려라.\" 그는 여전히 아이를 붙든 채 보드라운 작은 손을 쓰다듬으며 말했다.\n\n그는 어제 벽난로 선반 위에 올려 둔 사탕 상자를 집어 들고, 아이가 좋아하는 것으로 골라 초콜릿 하나와 봉봉 하나를 주었다.\n\n\"그리샤 거예요?\" 여자아이가 초콜릿을 가리키며 물었다.\n\n\"그래, 그래.\" 그러고는 여전히 작은 어깨를 쓰다듬으며 아이의 머리카락이 난 언저리와 목에 입을 맞추고 보내 주었다.\n\n\"마차가 준비됐습니다.\" 마트베이가 말했다. \"그런데 청원하러 온 손님이 있습니다.\"\n\n\"온 지 오래됐나?\" 스테판 아르카지치가 물었다.\n\n\"반 시간쯤 됐습니다.\"\n\n\"바로 알리라고 몇 번이나 일렀나?\"\n\n\"적어도 커피는 편히 드시게 해 드려야지요.\" 마트베이가, 화를 낼 수 없게 만드는 그 다정스레 무뚝뚝한 어조로 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 어서 그 사람을 들여보내게.\" 오블론스키는 짜증스럽게 미간을 찌푸리며 말했다.\n\n청원하러 온 사람은 칼리닌이라는 참모 대위의 미망인이었는데, 들어줄 수도 없고 사리에도 맞지 않는 부탁을 가지고 왔다. 그러나 스테판 아르카지치는 늘 그러듯이 그녀를 앉히고, 말을 가로막지 않은 채 끝까지 주의 깊게 들어 주었으며, 어떻게 누구에게 청원해야 하는지 자세히 일러 주었다. 게다가 그 크고 활달하면서도 보기 좋고 또렷한 글씨로, 그녀에게 도움이 될 만한 인사에게 보내는 자신만만하고 유려한 짧은 편지까지 써 주었다. 참모 대위의 미망인을 보내고 나서 스테판 아르카지치는 모자를 집어 들고는, 뭔가 잊은 것은 없는지 떠올리려고 멈춰 섰다. 잊고 싶었던 것—바로 아내 외에는 아무것도 잊은 게 없는 듯했다.\n\n\"아, 그렇지!\" 그는 고개를 떨구었고, 그의 잘생긴 얼굴에 괴로운 표정이 떠올랐다. '갈까, 가지 말까!' 그는 혼잣말을 했다. 그리고 내면의 목소리는 그에게 가서는 안 된다고, 거기서 나올 것이라곤 거짓밖에 없다고, 둘 사이의 관계를 바로잡고 고치는 것은 불가능하다고 일러 주었다. 왜냐하면 그녀를 다시 매력적이고 사랑을 불러일으킬 수 있게 만들거나, 그를 사랑에 무감한 노인으로 만드는 것이 불가능하기 때문이었다. 이제는 기만과 거짓 외에 아무것도 나올 수 없었다. 그리고 기만과 거짓은 그의 천성에 어긋났다.\n\n'그래도 언젠가는 해야 해. 이대로 계속 갈 수는 없잖아.' 그는 스스로 용기를 내려 애쓰며 말했다. 그는 가슴을 쫙 펴고 담배를 꺼내어 두 모금 빨고는 자개 재떨이에 던져 넣고, 빠른 걸음으로 응접실을 가로질러 아내의 침실로 통하는 다른 쪽 문을 열었다.\n\n# Chapter 4\n\n다리아 알렉산드로브나는 실내복 차림으로, 한때는 풍성하고 아름다웠으나 이제는 숱이 적어진 머리를 목덜미에 핀으로 꽂아 올린 채, 야위어 홀쭉해진 얼굴에 그 야윈 탓에 도드라져 보이는 크고 놀란 눈을 하고서, 방 안 곳곳에 온갖 물건이 어지러이 흩어진 가운데, 열린 서랍장 앞에 서서 무언가를 꺼내고 있었다. 남편의 발소리를 듣자 그녀는 동작을 멈추고 문 쪽을 바라보면서, 애써 자기 표정에 엄격하고 경멸하는 빛을 담으려고 했다. 그녀는 남편이 두렵고, 다가오는 이 대면이 두렵다는 것을 느꼈다. 그녀는 막 지난 사흘 동안 벌써 열 번이나 시도했던 일을, 그러니까 아이들의 물건과 자기 물건을 챙겨 친정 어머니에게 가지고 가려고 추리는 일을 다시 해 보려던 참이었는데, 또다시 차마 그 일을 해낼 수가 없었다. 하지만 지금도, 전에 매번 그랬듯이, 그녀는 \"이대로 살 수는 없으며, 무슨 조치든 취해서\" 그를 벌하고, 그에게 수치를 주고, 그가 자기에게 안겨 준 고통의 적어도 작은 일부만이라도 갚아 주어야 한다고 스스로에게 거듭 다짐하고 있었다. 그녀는 여전히 그를 떠나야 한다고 자신에게 말하고 있었지만, 그것이 불가능하다는 것도 의식하고 있었다. 불가능했던 것은, 그를 자기 남편으로 여기고 사랑하는 습관에서 벗어날 수가 없었기 때문이었다. 게다가 그녀는, 비록 여기 자기 집에서조차 다섯 아이를 제대로 돌보기가 버거운 마당에, 아이들을 모두 데리고 가려는 그곳에서는 형편이 한층 더 나빠지리라는 것을 깨달았다. 사실 이 사흘 동안만 해도, 막내는 상한 수프를 먹고 탈이 났고, 다른 아이들은 어제 저녁을 거의 굶다시피 했다. 그녀는 떠나는 것이 불가능하다는 것을 의식하고 있었지만, 그러면서도 자신을 속이며 여전히 물건을 추리고 떠날 것처럼 가장하고 있었다.\n\n남편을 보자 그녀는 무언가를 찾는 척 서랍장 안으로 손을 떨구었고, 그가 자기에게 바싹 다가왔을 때에야 비로소 그를 돌아보았다. 하지만 엄격하고 단호한 표정을 지으려 애쓰던 그녀의 얼굴은 당혹감과 고통을 드러내고 있었다.\n\n\"돌리!\" 그가 가라앉고 머뭇거리는 목소리로 말했다. 그는 고개를 어깨 쪽으로 숙이고 가엾고 비굴해 보이려고 애썼지만, 그럼에도 싱그러움과 건강함으로 환히 빛나고 있었다. 그녀는 재빠른 눈길로 건강과 싱그러움으로 빛나는 그의 모습을 훑어보았다. '그래, 저이는 행복하고 만족스럽지!' 그녀는 생각했다. '그런데 나는…… 게다가 저 역겨운 호인 기질, 누구나 저이를 좋아하고 칭찬하게 만드는 저 호인 기질—나는 저이의 저 호인 기질이 싫어.' 그녀는 생각했다. 그녀의 입이 굳어졌고, 창백하고 신경질적인 얼굴의 오른쪽 뺨 근육이 경련하듯 당겨졌다.\n\n\"무슨 일이에요?\" 그녀가 빠르고 낮고 부자연스러운 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"돌리!\" 그가 떨리는 목소리로 다시 불렀다. \"오늘 안나가 와.\"\n\n\"그게 나하고 무슨 상관이죠? 난 그 사람 못 만나요!\" 그녀가 소리쳤다.\n\n\"하지만 정말 만나 줘야 해, 돌리…….\"\n\n\"가요, 가요, 가 버려요!\" 그녀가 그를 쳐다보지도 않고 비명을 질렀는데, 마치 그 비명이 육체적 고통에서 터져 나오기라도 한 것 같았다.\n\n스테판 아르카지치는 아내를 떠올릴 때에는 침착할 수 있었고, 마트베이의 표현대로 그녀가 마음을 돌리리라고 기대할 수 있었으며, 조용히 신문을 읽고 커피를 마실 수도 있었다. 하지만 고통에 짓눌려 괴로워하는 그녀의 얼굴을 보고, 운명에 순응하며 절망으로 가득 찬 그녀의 목소리를 들었을 때, 숨이 턱 막히고 목구멍에 무언가가 치밀어 올랐으며, 눈에는 눈물이 어리기 시작했다.\n\n\"맙소사! 내가 무슨 짓을 한 거지? 돌리! 제발!…… 당신도 알잖아…….\" 그는 말을 잇지 못했다. 목구멍에서 흐느낌이 솟구쳤다.\n\n그녀는 서랍장을 쾅 닫고는 그를 흘낏 보았다.\n\n\"돌리, 내가 무슨 말을 할 수 있겠어?…… 한 가지만, 용서해 줘…… 생각해 봐, 내 인생 구 년이 한순간의…… 속죄가 될 수는 없겠어…….\"\n\n그녀는 눈을 떨군 채, 그가 무슨 말을 할지 기다리며 귀를 기울였는데, 마치 어떻게든 자기를 달리 믿게 해 달라고 그에게 애원하는 듯했다.\n\n\"—한순간의 격정 말이야?\" 그가 말하고는 계속하려고 했지만, 그 말에 마치 육체적 고통이 찌르기라도 한 듯 그녀의 입술이 다시 굳어졌고, 오른쪽 뺨의 근육이 다시 실룩거렸다.\n\n\"가요, 방에서 나가요!\" 그녀가 한층 더 날카롭게 비명을 질렀다. \"그리고 당신의 격정이니 당신의 그 추잡함이니 하는 얘기는 내게 하지 말아요.\"\n\n그녀는 나가려고 했지만 비틀거렸고, 몸을 가누려고 의자 등받이를 붙잡았다. 그의 얼굴이 풀어졌고, 입술이 부풀었으며, 눈에는 눈물이 그렁그렁했다.\n\n\"돌리!\" 그가 이제는 흐느끼며 말했다. \"제발, 아이들을 생각해 줘. 아이들에게는 죄가 없잖아! 잘못은 나한테 있어, 나를 벌해, 내 잘못을 속죄하게 해 줘. 내가 할 수 있는 일이라면 무엇이든, 무엇이든 할 각오가 돼 있어! 잘못은 나한테 있어, 내가 얼마나 잘못했는지는 어떤 말로도 다 표현할 수 없어! 하지만 돌리, 나를 용서해 줘!\"\n\n그녀는 자리에 앉았다. 그는 그녀의 거칠고 무거운 숨소리에 귀를 기울였고, 그녀가 이루 말할 수 없이 가여웠다. 그녀는 몇 번이나 말을 꺼내려 했지만 할 수 없었다. 그는 기다렸다.\n\n\"당신은 아이들을 떠올릴 때 같이 놀아 주려고 떠올리죠, 스티바. 하지만 나는 아이들을 떠올릴 때 이게 그 애들의 파멸을 뜻한다는 걸 알아요.\" 그녀가 말했다—분명 지난 며칠 동안 스스로에게 여러 번 되뇌던 말 가운데 하나였다.\n\n그녀가 그를 \"스티바\"라고 불렀고, 그는 고마운 마음으로 그녀를 흘낏 보고는 그녀의 손을 잡으려고 다가갔지만, 그녀는 혐오감에 그에게서 몸을 움츠렸다.\n\n\"나는 아이들을 생각해요, 그래서 그 애들을 구할 수 있다면 세상 무슨 일이든 하겠어요, 하지만 어떻게 그 애들을 구해야 할지 나 자신도 모르겠어요. 아버지에게서 떼어 놓아야 할지, 아니면 부도덕한 아버지 곁에 남겨 두어야 할지—그래요, 부도덕한 아버지…… 말해 봐요, 그…… 일이 있고 난 뒤에, 우리가 함께 살 수 있겠어요? 그게 가능해요? 말해 봐요, 네, 그게 가능하냐고요?\" 그녀가 목소리를 높이며 거듭 물었다. \"내 남편이, 내 아이들의 아버지가, 제 자식들의 가정교사와 정을 통한 마당에?\"\n\n\"하지만 내가 어쩔 수 있었겠어? 내가 어쩔 수 있었겠냐고?\" 그가 자기가 무슨 말을 하는지도 모른 채, 고개를 점점 더 깊이 떨구며 가엾은 목소리로 거듭 말했다.\n\n\"당신은 내게 역겨워요, 혐오스러워요!\" 그녀가 점점 더 격해지며 비명을 질렀다. \"당신 눈물 따위 아무 의미도 없어요! 당신은 나를 사랑한 적이 한 번도 없어요. 당신에게는 마음도, 명예로운 감정도 없어요! 당신은 내게 가증스럽고, 역겹고, 남이에요—그래요, 완전한 남이라고요!\" 고통과 분노에 차서 그녀는 자기 자신에게도 그토록 끔찍한 말을—남이라는 말을 내뱉었다.\n\n그는 그녀를 바라보았고, 그녀의 얼굴에 드러난 격분에 놀라며 당황했다. 그는 자기가 그녀를 가엾게 여기는 것이 어째서 그녀를 격분시키는지 이해하지 못했다. 그녀는 그에게서 자기에 대한 동정은 보았으나 사랑은 보지 못했다. '아니, 저 사람은 나를 미워해. 나를 용서하지 않을 거야.' 그는 생각했다.\n\n\"끔찍해! 끔찍하다고!\" 그가 말했다.\n\n바로 그 순간 옆방에서 아이가 울기 시작했다. 아마 넘어진 모양이었다. 다리아 알렉산드로브나는 귀를 기울였고, 그녀의 얼굴이 갑자기 부드러워졌다.\n\n그녀는 자기가 어디 있는지, 무엇을 하고 있는지조차 모르겠다는 듯, 몇 초 동안 정신을 추스르는 것 같더니, 황급히 일어나 문 쪽으로 움직였다.\n\n'그래, 저 사람은 내 아이를 사랑하지.' 그는 아이의 울음소리에 그녀의 얼굴이 변하는 것을 알아채고는 생각했다. '내 아이를 말이야. 그런데 어떻게 나를 미워할 수 있겠어?'\n\n\"돌리, 한마디만 더,\" 그가 그녀를 뒤따르며 말했다.\n\n\"내게 가까이 오면 하인들을, 아이들을 부르겠어요! 당신이 비열한 놈이라는 걸 다들 알아도 좋아요! 나는 지금 당장 떠날 거고, 당신은 여기서 당신 정부랑 살면 되잖아요!\"\n\n그리고 그녀는 문을 쾅 닫으며 나가 버렸다.\n\n스테판 아르카지치는 한숨을 쉬고 얼굴을 훔치고는, 가만가만한 걸음으로 방을 나섰다. '마트베이는 그녀가 마음을 돌릴 거라고 하지만, 어떻게? 도무지 그럴 가망이 조금도 보이질 않아. 아, 어휴, 어쩌면 이렇게 끔찍할까! 게다가 어쩌면 그렇게 천박하게 소리를 질러 댔는지.' 그는 그녀의 비명과 그 말들—\"비열한 놈\"이며 \"정부\"며 하는 말을 떠올리며 혼잣말을 했다. '게다가 하녀들이 듣고 있었을지도 몰라! 끔찍하게 천박해! 끔찍해!' 스테판 아르카지치는 몇 초 동안 혼자 서서 얼굴을 훔치고는, 가슴을 펴고 방을 나섰다.\n\n금요일이었고, 식당에서는 독일인 시계공이 시계의 태엽을 감고 있었다. 스테판 아르카지치는 이 시간을 정확히 지키는 대머리 시계공에 대해 했던 농담을—\"저 독일인 자신이 시계 태엽을 감기 위해 평생 태엽이 감긴 사람\"이라던 농담을 떠올리고 미소 지었다. 스테판 아르카지치는 농담을 좋아했다. '그리고 어쩌면 그녀가 마음을 돌릴지도 몰라! 거 참 괜찮은 표현이야, \"마음을 돌린다\"는 거.' 그는 생각했다. '저 말은 꼭 한번 써먹어야지.'\n\n\"마트베이!\" 그가 소리쳤다. \"안나 아르카지예브나를 위해 다리야와 함께 응접실을 다 준비해 두게.\" 마트베이가 들어오자 그는 그에게 말했다.\n\n\"알겠습니다, 나리.\"\n\n스테판 아르카지치는 모피 외투를 걸치고 현관 계단으로 나섰다.\n\n\"댁에서 식사하지 않으십니까?\" 마트베이가 그를 배웅하며 말했다.\n\n\"그건 형편 봐서. 그런데 자, 이건 살림에 보태게.\" 그가 지갑에서 십 루블을 꺼내며 말했다. \"이만하면 넉넉할 거야.\"\n\n\"넉넉하든 모자라든, 그걸로 어떻게든 꾸려 봐야지요.\" 마트베이가 마차 문을 쾅 닫고 다시 계단 위로 물러서며 말했다.\n\n그동안 다리아 알렉산드로브나는 아이를 달래고 나서, 마차 소리로 그가 떠난 것을 알고는 다시 자기 침실로 돌아갔다. 그곳은 그녀가 나오기만 하면 곧장 밀려드는 집안일의 근심에서 벗어날 수 있는 유일한 피난처였다. 지금만 해도, 잠깐 육아실에 다녀온 사이에 영국인 가정교사와 마트료나 필리모노브나는 미룰 수 없는, 그리고 오직 그녀만이 답할 수 있는 몇 가지 질문을 그녀에게 던지는 데 용케 성공했다. \"아이들이 산책 나갈 때 뭘 입혀야 하나요? 우유를 먹여야 할까요? 새 요리사를 부르러 보내야 하지 않을까요?\"\n\n\"아, 날 좀 내버려 둬요, 좀 내버려 두라고요!\" 그녀가 말하고는 침실로 돌아가, 남편과 이야기할 때 앉아 있던 바로 그 자리에 앉아, 앙상한 손가락에서 흘러내리는 반지를 낀 야윈 두 손을 꼭 맞잡고서, 모든 대화를 기억 속에서 하나하나 되짚어 보기 시작했다. '저이는 가 버렸어! 하지만 그 여자랑은 끝낸 걸까?' 그녀는 생각했다. '아직도 그 여자를 만나고 있는 건 아닐까? 왜 물어보지 않았을까! 아니, 아니야, 화해란 불가능해. 설령 한집에 남아 있더라도 우리는 남이야—영원히 남이라고!' 그녀는 자기에게 그토록 끔찍한 그 말을 특별한 무게를 담아 다시 되뇌었다. '내가 저이를 얼마나 사랑했는데! 맙소사, 얼마나 사랑했는데!…… 얼마나 사랑했는데! 그런데 지금은 사랑하지 않는 걸까? 전보다 더 사랑하고 있는 건 아닐까? 무엇보다 끔찍한 건…….' 그녀는 생각을 시작했지만 끝맺지 못했는데, 마트료나 필리모노브나가 문틈으로 고개를 들이밀었기 때문이었다.\n\n\"제 오라비를 부르러 보내야겠어요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그래도 그이가 끼니는 차려 줄 테니까요, 안 그러면 어제처럼 또 아이들이 여섯 시까지 아무것도 못 먹게 될 거예요.\"\n\n\"좋아요, 곧 가서 살펴볼게요. 그런데 새 우유는 가지러 보냈어요?\"\n\n그리고 다리아 알렉산드로브나는 하루의 일과 속으로 뛰어들어, 한동안 그 속에 자기 슬픔을 가라앉혔다.\n\n# Chapter 5\n\n스테판 아르카지치는 빼어난 재능 덕분에 학교에서 별 어려움 없이 공부했지만, 게으르고 장난이 심해서 반에서 늘 꼴찌에 가까운 축에 끼었다. 그러나 평소의 방탕한 생활 방식과 관직에서의 낮은 직급, 비교적 젊은 나이에도 불구하고, 그는 모스크바의 어느 관청 국장이라는 명예롭고 수입이 짭짤한 자리를 차지하고 있었다. 이 자리는 누이 안나의 남편인 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치 카레닌을 통해 얻은 것이었는데, 카레닌은 모스크바의 그 관청이 속한 부처에서 가장 중요한 직책 중 하나를 맡고 있었다. 그러나 설령 카레닌이 처남에게 이 자리를 마련해 주지 않았더라도, 형제며 자매며 사촌이며 삼촌이며 이모며 고모 같은 수백 명의 다른 인물들을 통해 스티바 오블론스키는 이 자리나 그와 비슷한 다른 자리를, 그에게 절대적으로 필요한 육천 루블의 봉급과 함께 얻었을 것이다. 그도 그럴 것이, 아내의 상당한 재산에도 불구하고 그의 형편은 궁색한 처지였기 때문이다.\n\n모스크바와 페테르부르크 절반이 스테판 아르카지치의 친구이자 친척이었다. 그는 이 세상의 권력자였고 또 권력자인 사람들 한가운데서 태어났다. 정부 인사들 가운데 나이 든 사람의 삼분의 일은 그의 아버지의 친구였고 그가 어린 시절부터 그를 알고 지냈으며, 또 다른 삼분의 일은 그의 절친한 단짝이었고, 나머지는 친한 지인이었다. 따라서 자리며 임대료며 주식이며 하는 형태로 이 세상의 온갖 복을 나눠 주는 사람들이 모두 그의 친구였고, 자기편의 한 사람을 모른 척할 수가 없었다. 그러니 오블론스키는 수입 좋은 자리를 얻기 위해 특별히 애쓸 필요가 전혀 없었다. 그는 다만 거절하지 않고, 질투하지 않고, 시비를 걸거나 화를 내지만 않으면 되었는데, 타고난 선한 성품 덕분에 그는 그런 짓을 한 적이 없었다. 자기가 바라는 봉급의 자리를 얻지 못할 것이라는 말을 들었다면 그는 어처구니없게 여겼을 것이다. 더구나 그는 분에 넘치는 것을 바라는 것도 아니었다. 그저 자기 또래에 자기와 같은 지위에 있는 사람들이 으레 얻는 것을 원했을 뿐이고, 그런 종류의 직무를 수행하는 데 다른 누구보다 못한 자격을 갖춘 것도 아니었다.\n\n스테판 아르카지치는 그를 아는 모든 사람에게서 단지 그 좋은 성정 때문만이 아니라 밝은 기질과 의심할 여지 없는 정직함 때문에도 사랑을 받았다. 잘생기고 환한 그의 모습, 반짝이는 눈, 검은 머리칼과 눈썹, 희고 붉은 얼굴빛에는 그를 만나는 사람들에게 친절함과 유쾌함의 물리적 효과를 일으키는 무언가가 있었다. \"어이! 스티바! 오블론스키! 여기 왔구먼!\" 그를 만났을 때 사람들은 거의 언제나 반가운 미소를 지으며 이렇게 말했다. 더러 그와 이야기를 나눈 뒤에 특별히 즐거운 일은 아무것도 없었던 것처럼 여겨지는 때가 있어도, 그다음 날이나 그다음다음 날이나 사람들은 그를 다시 만나면 똑같이 반가워했다.\n\n모스크바의 어느 관청 국장 자리를 삼 년 동안 지내면서, 스테판 아르카지치는 동료 관리들과 부하들과 상관들, 그리고 그와 업무로 엮인 모든 사람들로부터 호감뿐 아니라 존경까지 얻어 냈다. 관직에서 이런 보편적인 존경을 그에게 가져다준 스테판 아르카지치의 주된 자질은, 첫째로 자기 자신의 결점에 대한 자각에 바탕을 둔, 남에 대한 지극한 너그러움이었다. 둘째로 완벽한 자유주의였는데, 그것은 그가 신문에서 읽은 자유주의가 아니라 그의 핏속에 흐르는 자유주의였다. 그 덕분에 그는 상대가 어떤 재산을 가졌든 어떤 직업에 종사하든 모든 사람을 완벽하게 똑같이, 정확히 동등하게 대했다. 그리고 셋째로—이것이 가장 중요한 점인데—자기가 종사하는 업무에 대한 완벽한 무관심이었다. 그 덕분에 그는 결코 일에 휩쓸리지 않았고, 결코 실수하는 법이 없었다.\n\n관청에 도착한 스테판 아르카지치는 서류 가방을 든 공손한 수위의 안내를 받으며 자기의 작은 집무실로 들어가 제복을 입고 회의실로 들어갔다. 서기들과 필경사들이 모두 일어나 유쾌하고 공손한 태도로 그를 맞이했다. 스테판 아르카지치는 여느 때처럼 잰걸음으로 자기 자리로 가서 동료들과 악수를 나누고 자리에 앉았다. 그는 농담을 한두 마디 던지고, 격식에 어긋나지 않을 만큼만 이야기를 나눈 뒤 일을 시작했다. 자유로움과 소탈함과 관청 특유의 딱딱함 사이에서, 업무를 기분 좋게 처리하는 데 필요한 정확한 선을 짚어 내는 데 스테판 아르카지치보다 능한 사람은 없었다. 스테판 아르카지치의 관청에 있는 모든 사람에게 공통된 유쾌하고 공손한 태도로 서기 한 명이 서류를 들고 다가와, 스테판 아르카지치가 도입한 그 친근하고 편안한 어조로 말을 꺼냈다.\n\n\"펜자의 정부 부처에서 정보를 얻는 데 성공했습니다. 자, 보시겠습니까?……\"\n\n\"드디어 손에 넣었군?\" 스테판 아르카지치가 서류에 손가락을 짚으며 말했다. \"자, 여러분…….\"\n\n그리고 회의가 시작되었다.\n\n'저들이 알기나 할까,' 그는 보고를 들으며 자못 진지한 태도로 고개를 숙인 채 생각했다. '자기들 국장이 삼십 분 전에 얼마나 죄지은 어린애 같았는지를.' 그리고 보고가 낭독되는 동안 그의 눈은 웃고 있었다. 회의는 두 시까지 쉼 없이 이어질 것이고, 두 시가 되면 휴식 시간과 점심 식사가 있을 터였다.\n\n아직 두 시가 되지 않았는데, 회의실의 커다란 유리문이 갑자기 열리며 누군가 들어왔다.\n\n황제와 독수리 초상화 아래 안쪽에 앉아 있던 관리들은 어떤 기분 전환거리든 반가운 터라 일제히 문 쪽을 돌아보았다. 그러나 문가에 서 있던 수위가 곧장 침입자를 쫓아내고 그 뒤로 유리문을 닫아 버렸다.\n\n안건을 다 검토하고 나자, 스테판 아르카지치는 일어나 기지개를 켜고는, 시대의 자유주의에 대한 경의의 표시로 회의실에서 담배 한 개비를 꺼내 들고 자기 집무실로 들어갔다. 관청에서 오래 근무한 노련한 베테랑 니키틴과 시종보 그리네비치, 이 두 동료가 그와 함께 들어갔다.\n* 시종보(Kammerjunker): 러시아 궁정의 하급 시종 관직.\n\n\"점심 먹고 마저 끝낼 시간은 있겠어.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 말했다.\n\n\"틀림없이 있고말고요!\" 니키틴이 말했다.\n\n\"이 포민이라는 친구, 제법 약삭빠른 작자인가 봅니다.\" 그리네비치가 그들이 검토 중인 사건에 연루된 한 인물을 두고 말했다.\n\n스테판 아르카지치는 그리네비치의 말에 미간을 찌푸려, 섣불리 판단을 내리는 것은 온당치 못하다는 뜻을 그에게 내비치고는 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았다.\n\n\"방금 들어온 게 누군가?\" 그가 수위에게 물었다.\n\n\"각하, 누군가가 제가 등을 돌린 사이에 허락도 없이 슬그머니 들어왔습니다. 각하를 찾던데요. 제가 그랬습죠, 위원님들이 나오시면 그때…….\"\n\n\"그자가 어디 있나?\"\n\n\"복도로 나갔는지도 모릅니다만, 마침 저기 오는군요. 저 사람입니다.\" 수위가 가리킨 곳에는 곱슬곱슬한 턱수염을 기른, 단단한 체격에 어깨가 떡 벌어진 사내가 있었다. 그는 양가죽 모자도 벗지 않은 채 돌계단의 닳은 디딤돌을 가볍고 빠르게 뛰어 올라오고 있었다. 계단을 내려가던 위원 한 명—서류 가방을 든 마른 관리—이 그에게 길을 비켜 주며 낯선 사내의 다리를 못마땅한 눈길로 흘끗 보더니 오블론스키를 향해 묻는 듯한 시선을 던졌다.\n\n스테판 아르카지치는 계단 꼭대기에 서 있었다. 제복의 수놓인 깃 위로 환하게 빛나던 그의 선량한 얼굴은, 올라오는 사내를 알아본 순간 그 어느 때보다 환하게 빛났다.\n\n\"아니, 정말 자네로군, 레빈, 드디어 왔네그려!\" 그는 다가오는 레빈을 찬찬히 훑어보며 다정하면서도 놀리는 듯한 미소를 띠고 말했다. \"이런 소굴까지 황송하게도 날 찾아 주다니, 어쩐 일인가?\" 스테판 아르카지치는 이렇게 말하고는, 악수만으로는 성에 차지 않는 듯 친구에게 입을 맞추었다. \"온 지 오래됐나?\"\n\n\"방금 막 왔네. 자네가 무척 보고 싶었어.\" 레빈은 수줍어하면서도 한편으론 화난 듯, 불안한 듯 주위를 둘러보며 말했다.\n\n\"자, 내 방으로 가세.\" 친구의 예민하고 신경질적인 수줍음을 잘 아는 스테판 아르카지치는, 마치 위험 속을 헤쳐 그를 인도하기라도 하듯 그의 팔을 잡고 끌고 갔다.\n\n스테판 아르카지치는 거의 모든 지인들과 흉허물 없는 사이였고, 거의 모든 이를 세례명으로 불렀다. 예순의 노인이든 스무 살의 청년이든, 배우든 장관이든 상인이든 시종무관장이든 가리지 않았다. 그래서 그의 절친한 단짝들 가운데는 사회적 사다리의 양극단에 자리한 이들이 있었는데, 그들은 오블론스키를 매개로 자기들에게 무언가 공통점이 있다는 사실을 알면 무척 놀랄 터였다. 그는 함께 샴페인 한 잔을 기울인 모든 이의 흉허물 없는 친구였고, 또 누구하고나 샴페인 한 잔을 기울였다. 그러다 부하들 앞에서 그가 농담 삼아 흔히 '못된 단짝들'이라 부르던 친구들 가운데 누구라도 마주치면, 그는 타고난 재치로 그들에게 끼친 불쾌한 인상을 누그러뜨리는 법을 잘 알았다. 레빈은 못된 단짝은 아니었지만, 오블론스키는 특유의 민첩한 재치로, 부하들 앞에서 자기와의 친분을 드러내는 것을 레빈이 달가워하지 않을지도 모른다고 짐작하고는 서둘러 그를 자기 방으로 데리고 들어갔다.\n\n레빈은 오블론스키와 거의 동갑이었다. 그들의 친분은 단지 샴페인에만 기댄 것이 아니었다. 레빈은 그의 어린 시절의 친구이자 동무였다. 두 사람은 성격과 취향의 차이에도 불구하고, 어린 시절을 함께 보낸 친구들이 서로 아끼듯 서로를 아꼈다. 그러나 그런데도 두 사람은—서로 다른 길을 택한 사람들에게 흔히 그렇듯—이야기를 나눌 때면 상대방의 길을 두둔하면서도 마음속으로는 그것을 경멸했다. 두 사람 각자에게는 자기가 사는 삶만이 유일하게 진짜 삶이고, 친구가 사는 삶은 한낱 허깨비처럼 여겨졌다. 오블론스키는 레빈을 보자 살짝 놀리는 듯한 미소를 참을 수 없었다. 시골에서 무언가를 하던 그가 모스크바로 올라오는 것을 그동안 얼마나 자주 보았던가. 그런데 그가 정확히 무엇을 하는지는 스테판 아르카지치도 도무지 알 수 없었고, 사실 그 일에 아무런 관심도 없었다. 레빈은 언제나 흥분한 채 서두르며, 다소 거북해하고 또 자기가 거북해하는 것에 짜증을 내며, 대개는 사물에 대한 전혀 새롭고 뜻밖의 견해를 품고 모스크바에 나타났다. 스테판 아르카지치는 이런 점을 보고 웃었지만 또 좋아했다. 마찬가지로 레빈도 마음속으로는 친구의 도시적인 생활 방식을, 그리고 그가 비웃으며 하찮게 여기던 친구의 관직 업무를 경멸했다. 다만 차이가 있다면, 오블론스키는 남들이 다 하는 것을 자기도 하는 터라 흡족하고 유쾌하게 웃었던 반면, 레빈은 흡족함 없이, 때로는 화를 내며 웃었다는 점이다.\n\n\"우린 오래전부터 자네를 기다리고 있었네.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 자기 방으로 들어서며, 이제 모든 위험이 끝났다는 것을 보여 주기라도 하듯 레빈의 손을 놓으며 말했다. \"정말, 정말 반갑네.\" 그가 말을 이었다. \"그래, 어떻게 지냈나? 응? 언제 왔어?\"\n\n레빈은 오블론스키의 두 동료의 낯선 얼굴을, 특히 우아한 그리네비치의 손을 바라보며 입을 다물고 있었다. 그 손은 길고 흰 손가락에, 길고 노르스름한 개암 모양 손톱에, 셔츠 소맷부리에 박힌 어찌나 큼지막하게 번쩍이는 단추가 달려 있었던지, 보아하니 그것이 그의 주의를 온통 빨아들여 자유로이 생각할 여지조차 주지 않는 듯했다. 오블론스키는 이를 곧바로 알아채고 미소 지었다.\n\n\"아, 참, 소개하지.\" 그가 말했다. \"내 동료들이네. 필립 이바니치 니키틴, 미하일 스타니슬라비치 그리네비치.\" 그러고는 레빈 쪽으로 돌아서서 말했다. \"이쪽은 군 자치회 의원이자 신식 자치회 위원이고, 한 손으로 십삼 스톤을 들어 올리는 운동가요, 가축 사육가에 사냥꾼이며, 내 친구인 콘스탄틴 드미트리예비치 레빈이라네. 세르게이 이바노비치 코즈니셰프의 동생일세.\"\n\n\"반갑습니다.\" 베테랑이 말했다.\n\n\"형님이신 세르게이 이바노비치를 알게 되어 영광입니다.\" 그리네비치가 긴 손톱이 달린 가느다란 손을 내밀며 말했다.\n\n레빈은 미간을 찌푸리며 냉랭하게 악수하고는 곧장 오블론스키 쪽으로 돌아섰다. 온 러시아에 널리 알려진 작가인 이복형을 그는 무척 존경했지만, 사람들이 자기를 콘스탄틴 레빈이 아니라 그 유명한 코즈니셰프의 동생으로 대하는 것은 견딜 수가 없었다.\n\n\"아니, 난 이제 자치회 의원이 아닐세. 그자들과 모조리 사이가 틀어졌고, 더 이상 회의에도 안 나가.\" 그가 오블론스키 쪽으로 돌아서며 말했다.\n\n\"행동이 빠르군!\" 오블론스키가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"한데 어쩌다가? 왜?\"\n\n\"긴 얘기야. 언제 들려주지.\" 레빈은 이렇게 말했지만 곧장 이야기를 시작했다. \"뭐, 짧게 말하자면, 자치회로는 실제로 아무것도 이루어지는 게 없고 앞으로도 결코 이루어질 수 없다는 걸 확신하게 됐어.\" 그는 마치 누가 방금 자기를 모욕하기라도 한 양 입을 열었다. \"한편으로 그건 장난감이야. 자기들이 무슨 의회라도 되는 양 놀음을 하는 거지. 난 장난감을 가지고 즐기기엔 그리 젊지도 그리 늙지도 않았어. 또 다른 한편으로는\" (그는 말을 더듬었다) \"그건 군의 패거리가 돈을 버는 수단이지. 예전엔 후견인이니 재판소니 하는 게 있었는데, 이제는 자치회가 있는 거야—뇌물의 형태가 아니라 일하지 않고 받는 봉급의 형태로 말이야.\" 그는 마치 그 자리에 있는 누군가가 자기 의견에 반대라도 한 양 열을 올리며 말했다.\n\n\"아하! 또 새로운 국면에 접어들었군그래—보수주의자로 말이야.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 말했다. \"하지만 그 얘긴 나중에 하지.\"\n\n\"그래, 나중에. 한데 난 자네가 보고 싶었어.\" 레빈은 그리네비치의 손을 증오 어린 눈길로 바라보며 말했다.\n\n스테판 아르카지치는 거의 알아챌 수 없는 미소를 지었다.\n\n\"다시는 유럽식 옷을 안 입겠다고 늘 말하던 게 어떻게 된 건가?\" 그는 분명 프랑스 재단사가 지었을 친구의 새 양복을 훑어보며 말했다. \"아! 알겠네, 새로운 국면이로군.\"\n\n레빈은 별안간 얼굴을 붉혔는데, 그것은 어른들이 자기도 모르게 살짝 붉히는 식이 아니라, 자기가 수줍음 때문에 우스꽝스러워 보인다고 느끼고는 그것이 부끄러워 더욱더, 거의 눈물이 날 지경으로 얼굴을 붉히는 소년들의 식이었다. 그리고 이 분별 있고 사내다운 얼굴이 그토록 어린애 같은 곤경에 빠진 모습을 보는 것이 어찌나 묘했던지, 오블론스키는 그를 바라보던 눈길을 거두었다.\n\n\"아, 우리 어디서 만날까? 자네하고 꼭 얘기를 나누고 싶은데 말이야.\" 레빈이 말했다.\n\n오블론스키는 곰곰이 생각하는 듯했다.\n\n\"이러면 어떻겠나. 구린 식당에 가서 점심을 먹지. 거기서 얘기하면 되겠어. 난 세 시까지 한가하니까.\"\n\n\"아니야.\" 레빈은 잠시 생각하더니 대답했다. \"난 다른 데 또 가 봐야 해.\"\n\n\"그럼 좋아, 같이 저녁이나 먹세.\"\n\n\"같이 저녁을? 하지만 난 특별히 할 얘기가 있는 건 아니고, 그저 몇 마디 하고 싶은 말이랑 묻고 싶은 게 하나 있을 뿐이야. 얘긴 그다음에 나눠도 되고.\"\n\n\"그럼 그 몇 마디를 지금 당장 하게나. 잡담은 저녁 먹고 나서 하면 되지.\"\n\n\"그게 말이야,\" 레빈이 말했다. \"한데 별로 중요한 건 아니야.\"\n\n수줍음을 떨쳐 내려 안간힘을 쓰느라 그의 얼굴은 별안간 화가 난 듯한 표정을 띠었다.\n\n\"셰르바츠키 댁은 어떻게들 지내나? 다들 예전 그대로인가?\" 그가 말했다.\n\n레빈이 자기 처제 키티에게 반해 있다는 것을 오래전부터 알고 있던 스테판 아르카지치는 거의 알아챌 수 없는 미소를 짓고는, 그의 눈이 즐겁게 반짝였다.\n\n\"몇 마디라고 했지만, 난 몇 마디로는 대답할 수가 없어. 왜냐하면…… 잠깐만 실례하지…….\"\n\n서기 한 명이 들어왔다. 공손하면서도 흉허물 없는 태도로, 그리고 모든 서기에게 공통된, 업무 지식에서만큼은 상관보다 우월하다는 겸손한 자의식을 풍기며 그는 서류 몇 장을 들고 오블론스키에게 다가가, 질문을 하는 척하며 어떤 이의 사항을 설명하기 시작했다. 스테판 아르카지치는 그의 말을 끝까지 듣지도 않고 서기의 소맷자락에 다정하게 손을 얹었다.\n\n\"아니, 내가 말한 대로 하게.\" 그는 미소로 말을 부드럽게 누그러뜨리며 이렇게 말했다. 그러고는 그 문제에 대한 자기 견해를 간략히 설명한 뒤 서류에서 손을 떼며 말했다. \"그러니 그렇게 처리해 주게, 자하르 니키티치.\"\n\n서기는 당황한 채 물러갔다. 서기와 의논하는 동안 레빈은 거북함에서 완전히 벗어나 있었다. 그는 의자 등받이에 두 팔꿈치를 괴고 서서, 얼굴에는 빈정거리는 듯한 주의 깊은 표정을 띠고 있었다.\n\n\"난 이해가 안 가, 도무지 이해가 안 가.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"뭐가 이해가 안 간다는 건가?\" 오블론스키가 여느 때처럼 환하게 웃으며 담배 한 개비를 집어 들었다. 그는 레빈에게서 어떤 기이한 발언이 터져 나오리라 예상하고 있었다.\n\n\"난 자네가 하는 일이 이해가 안 가.\" 레빈이 어깨를 으쓱하며 말했다. \"어떻게 이런 걸 진지하게 할 수가 있나?\"\n\n\"왜 못 하겠나?\"\n\n\"아니, 거기엔 아무 알맹이가 없으니까.\"\n\n\"자넨 그렇게 생각하겠지만, 우린 일에 파묻혀 산다네.\"\n\n\"종이 위에서나 그렇지. 하지만 자네야 그쪽에 타고난 재능이 있으니까.\" 레빈이 덧붙였다.\n\n\"그러니까 자네 말은, 나한테 뭔가 부족한 게 있다는 거로군?\"\n\n\"그럴지도 모르지.\" 레빈이 말했다. \"하지만 어쨌든 나는 자네의 위풍당당함에 감탄하고, 그토록 대단한 인물을 친구로 둔 게 자랑스럽네. 그런데 자넨 아직 내 질문에 답을 안 했어.\" 그는 필사적으로 안간힘을 쓰며 오블론스키의 얼굴을 똑바로 바라보고 말을 이었다.\n\n\"오, 그건 다 좋은데 말이야. 좀만 기다려 보게, 자네도 언젠가 이렇게 될 테니까. 자네야 카라진스키 군에 육천 에이커가 넘는 땅이 있고, 그런 근육에, 열두 살 소녀 같은 싱싱함이 있으니 좋지. 그래도 언젠가는 자네도 우리 같은 사람이 될 거야. 그래, 자네 질문에 답하자면, 달라진 건 없어. 다만 자네가 그렇게 오래 떠나 있었던 게 안타깝군.\"\n\n\"오, 어째서?\" 레빈이 겁에 질려 캐물었다.\n\n\"오, 아무것도 아니야.\" 오블론스키가 대답했다. \"그 얘긴 나중에 함께 나누지. 그런데 자넨 무슨 일로 시내에 올라온 건가?\"\n\n\"오, 그것도 나중에 얘기하지.\" 레빈은 다시 귀밑까지 빨개지며 말했다.\n\n\"알았네. 알겠어.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 말했다. \"있잖아, 우리 집에 들르라고 청하고 싶지만, 아내가 좀 편치 않아서 말이야. 그건 그렇고 이러면 어떻겠나. 자네가 그 집 사람들을 보고 싶다면, 지금쯤이면 분명 동물원에 네 시부터 다섯 시까지 있을 거야. 키티가 스케이트를 타거든. 자넨 그리로 가 있게. 내가 자네를 데리러 가서, 함께 어디 가서 저녁이나 먹지.\"\n\n\"좋아. 그럼 그때 봄세.\"\n\n\"이봐, 명심하게. 자넨 잊어버리거나, 아니면 시골 집으로 냅다 달아날 게 뻔하니까 말이야. 내가 자넬 잘 알지!\" 스테판 아르카지치가 웃으며 소리쳤다.\n\n\"아니야, 정말이야!\"\n그러고서 레빈은 방을 나섰는데, 문간에 이르러서야 오블론스키의 동료들에게 작별 인사하는 것을 잊었다는 사실을 떠올렸다.\n\n\"저 신사 분은 굉장히 정력적인 사람인가 봅니다.\" 레빈이 떠나자 그리네비치가 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 이 사람아.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다. \"운 좋은 친구지! 카라진스키 군에 육천 에이커가 넘는 땅을 가졌고, 앞날이 창창한 데다, 그 젊음과 활력이라니! 우리 같은 사람들하곤 다르지.\"\n\n\"당신도 불만스러운 게 어디 한둘이겠습니까, 스테판 아르카지치?\"\n\n\"아, 그래, 난 형편이 말이 아니야, 아주 나빠.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 무겁게 한숨을 쉬며 말했다.\n\n# Chapter 6\n\n오블론스키가 레빈에게 무슨 일로 시내에 왔느냐고 묻자, 레빈은 얼굴을 붉혔고, 그러면서 얼굴을 붉히는 자기 자신에게 화가 났다. 왜냐하면 \"당신 처제에게 청혼하러 왔지요\"라고 대답할 수가 없었기 때문이다. 사실 바로 그것을 하러 온 것이었는데도 말이다.\n\n레빈 집안과 시체르바츠키 집안은 둘 다 모스크바의 오랜 귀족 가문이었고, 늘 친밀하고 다정한 사이를 유지해 왔다. 이 친분은 레빈의 대학 시절에 한층 더 두터워졌다. 그는 키티와 돌리의 오빠인 젊은 시체르바츠키 공작과 함께 대학 입시를 준비했고, 그와 같은 시기에 입학했다. 그 무렵 레빈은 시체르바츠키 집안에 자주 드나들었고, 그 집안에 푹 빠져 있었다. 이상하게 들릴지 모르지만, 콘스탄틴 레빈이 사랑에 빠진 것은 바로 그 가정, 그 가족이었고, 특히 그 집안의 여성들이었다. 레빈은 자기 어머니를 기억하지 못했고, 하나뿐인 누이는 그보다 나이가 많았다. 그래서 그가 부모를 여의면서 잃어버린, 유서 깊고 고결하며 교양 있고 명예로운 가정의 그 내밀한 삶을 처음으로 본 곳이 바로 시체르바츠키 집안이었다. 그 집안의 모든 사람, 특히 여성들은 그의 눈에 마치 신비로운 시적 베일에 감싸인 것처럼 비쳤다. 그는 그들에게서 아무런 결점도 보지 못했을 뿐 아니라, 그들을 감싼 그 시적 베일 아래에 가장 고귀한 감정과 온갖 완전함이 깃들어 있으리라 여겼다. 세 아가씨가 어째서 어느 날은 프랑스어를 쓰고 다음 날은 영어를 써야 하는지, 어째서 정해진 시각이면 번갈아 가며 피아노를 쳐서, 학생들이 공부하던 위층 오빠의 방에까지 그 소리가 들려오는지, 어째서 프랑스 문학과 음악과 그림과 춤을 가르치는 그 선생들이 찾아오는지, 어째서 정해진 시각이면 세 아가씨가 마드무아젤 리농과 함께 새틴 망토를 두르고—돌리는 긴 것을, 나탈리아는 절반쯤 오는 것을, 키티는 너무 짧아서 팽팽하게 당겨 신은 붉은 스타킹을 신은 맵시 있는 다리가 누구의 눈에나 다 보이는 것을 두르고—마차를 타고 트베르스코이 가로수길로 나가는지, 어째서 모자에 금빛 장식을 단 하인의 호위를 받으며 트베르스코이 가로수길을 거닐어야 하는지—이 모든 것과, 그들의 신비로운 세계에서 행해지는 그 밖의 수많은 일을 그는 이해하지 못했다. 하지만 그는 거기서 행해지는 모든 것이 더없이 훌륭하다고 확신했고, 바로 그 모든 행위의 신비로움에 사랑을 느꼈다.\n\n대학 시절 그는 맏딸 돌리에게 거의 사랑에 빠질 뻔했지만, 그녀는 곧 오블론스키와 결혼해 버렸다. 그러자 그는 둘째 딸을 사랑하기 시작했다. 그는 마치 자매 가운데 누군가 한 사람을 사랑해야만 한다고 느끼는 듯했고, 다만 그게 누구인지를 좀처럼 분간하지 못했다. 그러나 나탈리아 역시 사교계에 모습을 드러내자마자 외교관 르보프와 결혼해 버렸다. 레빈이 대학을 떠날 무렵 키티는 아직 어린아이였다. 젊은 시체르바츠키는 해군에 입대했다가 발트해에서 익사했고, 오블론스키와의 우정에도 불구하고 시체르바츠키 집안과 레빈의 관계는 점점 소원해졌다. 그러나 올해 초겨울, 레빈이 시골에서 일 년을 보낸 뒤 모스크바로 와서 시체르바츠키 집안 사람들을 보았을 때, 그는 자기가 정말로 사랑할 운명인 사람이 세 자매 중 누구인지를 깨달았다.\n\n좋은 가문 출신에, 가난하다기보다는 차라리 부유한 편이고, 서른두 살이나 된 그가 젊은 시체르바츠카야 공작 영애에게 청혼하는 것보다 더 간단한 일은 없으리라고 누구나 생각했을 것이다. 십중팔구 그는 단번에 괜찮은 신랑감으로 여겨졌을 것이다. 그러나 레빈은 사랑에 빠져 있었고, 그래서 그에게 키티는 모든 면에서 너무도 완벽하여 이 세상 모든 것을 까마득히 초월한 존재로 보였다. 반면 자신은 너무도 비천하고 너무도 세속적인 존재여서, 다른 사람들이며 그녀 자신이 그를 그녀에게 어울리는 사람으로 여길 수 있으리라는 것은 상상조차 할 수 없을 정도였다.\n\n황홀한 상태로 두 달을 모스크바에서 보내며, 그녀를 만나려고 드나든 사교계에서 거의 날마다 키티를 보았던 그는, 불현듯 이건 안 될 일이라고 마음먹고 시골로 돌아가 버렸다.\n\n이건 안 될 일이라는 레빈의 확신은 이런 생각에 바탕을 두고 있었다. 그녀 가족의 눈에 그는 사랑스러운 키티에게 불리하고 보잘것없는 신랑감이며, 키티 자신도 그를 사랑할 수 없으리라는 것이었다. 그녀 가족의 눈에 그는 사교계에서 번듯하고 뚜렷한 경력도 지위도 없는 사람이었던 반면, 그와 동년배인 사람들은 그가 서른두 살이 된 지금 이미 누구는 대령이 되고, 누구는 교수가 되고, 누구는 은행과 철도의 이사가 되거나, 오블론스키처럼 위원회 의장이 되어 있었다. 그러나 그는(다른 사람들 눈에 자기가 어떻게 보일지 그는 너무도 잘 알았다) 가축을 기르고, 사냥감을 쏘고, 헛간을 짓는 데 매달려 사는 시골 지주였다. 다시 말해 아무 재능도 없고, 변변히 성공하지도 못했으며, 세상의 통념으로 보자면 달리 아무짝에도 쓸모없는 인간들이 하는 바로 그 일을 하고 있는 사내였다.\n\n신비롭고 매혹적인 키티가, 스스로 생각하기에 자기처럼 못생긴 사람을, 무엇보다 그토록 평범하고 도무지 눈에 띄는 데라곤 없는 사람을 사랑할 리가 없었다. 게다가 과거 키티를 대했던 그의 태도—그녀 오빠와의 우정에서 비롯된, 어른이 아이를 대하는 듯한 태도—가 그에게는 사랑을 가로막는 또 하나의 장애물로 여겨졌다. 못생겼지만 마음씨 좋은 남자는, 그가 스스로를 그렇게 여겼듯, 친구로서는 호감을 살 수 있을지 몰라도, 자기가 키티를 사랑하는 그런 사랑으로 사랑받으려면 잘생긴 남자, 그리고 더 나아가 출중한 남자여야 한다고 그는 생각했다.\n\n여자들이 흔히 못생기고 평범한 남자를 좋아하기도 한다는 말을 그는 들은 적이 있었지만, 그는 그 말을 믿지 않았다. 자기 자신을 기준으로 판단했기 때문이었고, 그 자신은 아름답고 신비롭고 비범한 여자가 아니면 누구도 사랑할 수 없었기 때문이다.\n\n그러나 시골에서 홀로 두 달을 보낸 뒤, 그는 확신하게 되었다. 이것은 젊은 시절에 겪었던 그런 한때의 열정 따위가 아니라는 것을, 이 감정이 그에게 한순간의 안식도 주지 않는다는 것을, 그녀가 자기 아내가 될 것인지 아닐 것인지 그 문제를 결정하지 않고서는 도저히 살아갈 수 없다는 것을, 그리고 자기의 절망이라는 것이 오로지 자기 망상에서 비롯되었을 뿐, 거절당하리라는 어떤 증거도 자기에게는 없다는 것을 말이다. 그리하여 그는 이제 청혼하겠다는, 받아들여지면 결혼하겠다는 굳은 결심을 품고 모스크바로 온 것이었다. 아니면…… 만약 거절당한다면 자기가 어떻게 될지는 그도 상상할 수 없었다.\n\n# Chapter 7\n\n아침 기차로 모스크바에 도착한 레빈은 이복형 코즈니셰프의 집에 묵었다. 옷을 갈아입은 뒤 그는 곧장 자기가 찾아온 용건을 형과 의논하고 조언을 구할 생각으로 아래층 형의 서재로 내려갔다. 그러나 형은 혼자가 아니었다. 형 곁에는 어떤 매우 중요한 철학적 문제를 둘러싸고 두 사람 사이에 생겨난 견해 차이를 매듭짓고자 일부러 하리코프에서 찾아온, 이름난 철학 교수가 와 있었다. 교수는 유물론자들을 상대로 뜨거운 논전을 벌이는 중이었다. 세르게이 코즈니셰프는 흥미를 가지고 이 논전을 지켜보아 왔으며, 교수의 마지막 논문을 읽고 나서 자기 반론을 적은 편지를 그에게 써 보냈던 터였다. 그는 교수가 유물론자들에게 지나치게 양보하고 있다고 비판했다. 그러자 교수는 그 문제를 끝까지 따져 보려고 곧바로 찾아온 것이다. 논의의 핵심은 당시 유행하던 바로 그 문제였다. 인간에게 심리적 현상과 생리적 현상 사이에 경계선을 그을 수 있는가? 그렇다면 그것은 어디인가?\n\n세르게이 이바노비치는 늘 누구에게나 짓는 차갑고 다정한 미소로 동생을 맞이하고는, 그를 교수에게 소개한 뒤 다시 대화를 이어 갔다.\n\n좁은 이마에 안경을 쓴 자그마한 남자가 잠시 논의에서 벗어나 레빈에게 인사를 건네고는, 더 이상 그에게는 아무 관심도 두지 않고 이야기를 계속했다. 레빈은 교수가 돌아갈 때까지 기다리려고 자리에 앉았는데, 곧 논의되는 주제에 흥미가 일기 시작했다.\n\n레빈은 그들이 논쟁하고 있는 잡지의 논문들을 우연히 접해 읽은 적이 있었는데, 대학에서 자연과학을 공부한 그에게 친숙한, 과학의 제일원리가 전개되는 글로서 흥미를 느꼈던 것이다. 그러나 그는 동물로서의 인간의 기원이며 반사작용이며 생물학이며 사회학에 관한 이런 과학적 추론들을, 요즘 들어 점점 더 자주 그의 머릿속을 맴돌던 삶과 죽음의 의미에 관한 문제들과 결부시켜 본 적은 한 번도 없었다.\n\n형과 교수의 논쟁에 귀를 기울이면서 그는 두 사람이 이런 과학적 문제들을 저 정신적인 문제들과 연결시키고 있으며, 이따금 후자에 거의 닿을 듯하다는 것을 알아차렸다. 그러나 그에게 가장 중요한 지점으로 여겨지는 데 가까이 다가설 때마다 그들은 곧바로 황급히 물러나 다시 미묘한 구별과 단서와 인용과 암시와 권위에의 호소가 넘실대는 바다로 뛰어들었고, 그래서 그는 그들이 무엇에 대해 이야기하는지 가까스로 이해할 따름이었다.\n\n\"나는 그것을 인정할 수 없네.\" 세르게이 이바노비치가 늘 그렇듯이 명료하고 정확한 표현과 우아한 어법으로 말했다. \"나는 외부 세계에 대한 나의 모든 관념이 지각에서 비롯되었다는 카이스의 주장에는 어떤 경우에도 동의할 수 없네. 가장 근본적인 관념, 곧 존재라는 관념은 감각을 통해 내게 받아들여진 것이 아니야. 실제로 그런 관념을 전달할 특별한 감각기관 따위는 없으니까.\"\n\n\"그렇지요, 하지만 그들은—부르트, 크나우스트, 프리파소프 같은 이들은—이렇게 답할 겁니다. 당신의 존재에 대한 의식은 당신의 모든 감각의 결합에서 비롯된 것이며, 그 존재에 대한 의식이야말로 당신의 감각의 결과라고 말입니다. 실제로 부르트는, 감각이 없다고 가정하면 결국 존재의 관념도 없다는 결론이 나온다고 분명히 말합니다.\"\n\n\"나는 그 반대를 주장하네.\" 세르게이 이바노비치가 입을 열었다.\n\n그러나 여기서 레빈에게는 그들이 문제의 본질에 막 다가서는 듯하다가 다시 물러나 버리는 것처럼 보였고, 그는 교수에게 한 가지 질문을 던지기로 마음먹었다.\n\n\"그 말에 따르자면, 만약 나의 감각이 사라지고 나의 육체가 죽는다면, 나는 어떤 종류의 존재도 가질 수 없다는 겁니까?\" 그가 물었다.\n\n교수는 짜증이 나서, 마치 그 방해 때문에 정신적 고통이라도 느끼는 듯한 표정으로, 철학자라기보다는 뱃사공에 가까워 보이는 그 낯선 질문자를 돌아보고는, 마치 \"저 사람에게 무슨 말을 해야 하나?\" 하고 묻기라도 하듯이 세르게이 이바노비치에게로 눈길을 돌렸다. 그러나 교수보다 훨씬 덜 열을 올리고 덜 한쪽으로 치우쳐 이야기해 온 데다, 교수에게 답할 만큼 넉넉한 식견을 갖추고 있으면서도 동시에 그 질문이 나온 단순하고 자연스러운 관점까지 헤아릴 줄 알았던 세르게이 이바노비치는 빙긋 웃으며 말했다.\n\n\"그 질문에는 우리가 아직 답할 권리가 없다네.\"\n\n\"필요한 자료가 우리에게 없으니까요.\" 교수가 맞장구를 치고는 다시 자기 논지로 돌아갔다. \"아니지요.\" 그가 말했다. \"나는 이 점을 지적하고 싶습니다. 만약 프리파소프가 직접 단언하듯이 지각이 감각에 기초하는 것이라면, 우리는 이 두 개념을 분명하게 구별하지 않을 수 없습니다.\"\n\n레빈은 더 이상 귀 기울이지 않았고, 그저 교수가 돌아가기만을 기다렸다.\n\n# Chapter 8\n\n교수가 가고 나자 세르게이 이바노비치는 동생을 돌아보았다.\n\n\"네가 와서 정말 기쁘구나. 한동안 머물 거냐? 농사일은 어떻게 돼 가지?\"\n\n레빈은 형이 농사일에는 별 관심이 없으면서 단지 자기를 배려해 그렇게 물었을 뿐임을 알았고, 그래서 밀을 판 일과 돈 문제에 대해서만 이야기했다.\n\n레빈은 결혼하기로 결심했다는 것을 형에게 털어놓고 조언을 구하려 했었다. 사실 그렇게 하기로 단단히 마음먹고 있었던 것이다. 그러나 형을 보고, 형이 교수와 나누는 대화를 듣고, 그러고 나서 형이 농사 문제에 대해 자기에게 물을 때의 무의식적으로 깔보는 듯한 어조를 듣고 나니(어머니의 재산은 아직 나뉘지 않은 채였고, 레빈이 두 사람 몫을 모두 관리하고 있었다), 레빈은 왠지 결혼할 생각을 형에게 꺼낼 수 없을 것 같다는 기분이 들었다. 형이 그 일을 자기가 바라는 식으로 봐 주지 않으리라는 것이 느껴졌다.\n\n\"그래, 너희 군 자치회는 어떻게 돌아가고 있냐?\" 세르게이 이바노비치가 물었다. 그는 이런 지방 자치 기구에 큰 관심을 두고 있었고 그것에 큰 의미를 부여하고 있었다.\n\n\"정말이지 잘 모르겠어요.\"\n\n\"뭐라고! 아니, 네가 자치회 의원이지 않냐?\"\n\n\"아뇨, 이제 의원이 아니에요. 사임했거든요.\" 레빈이 대답했다. \"이젠 회의에도 나가지 않아요.\"\n\n\"이런, 안타깝구나!\" 세르게이 이바노비치가 미간을 찌푸리며 한마디 했다.\n\n레빈은 자기변호를 하려고 자기 군의 회의에서 어떤 일이 벌어지는지 설명하기 시작했다.\n\n\"늘 그런 식이지!\" 세르게이 이바노비치가 그의 말을 가로막았다. \"우리 러시아 사람들은 언제나 그래. 어쩌면 그게 정말 우리의 장점일지도 모르지, 자기 결점을 볼 줄 아는 능력 말이야. 하지만 우린 그걸 도가 지나치게 하는 거야. 늘 혀끝에 매달려 있는 빈정거림으로 스스로를 위로하지. 내가 하고 싶은 말은 이거야. 우리 지방 자치 같은 권리를 다른 유럽 민족 누구에게라도 줘 봐라—독일인이든 영국인이든 그걸 발판 삼아 자유로 나아갔을 텐데, 우리는 그저 그걸 조롱거리로 만들어 버린단 말이지.\"\n\n\"하지만 어쩌겠어요?\" 레빈이 뉘우치듯 말했다. \"그게 제 마지막 노력이었어요. 정말 온 마음을 다해 해 봤다고요. 안 되더군요. 전 그런 일에 소질이 없어요.\"\n\n\"소질이 없는 게 아니야.\" 세르게이 이바노비치가 말했다. \"네가 그걸 마땅히 봐야 할 대로 보지 않는 거지.\"\n\n\"그럴지도 모르죠.\" 레빈이 풀 죽어 대답했다.\n\n\"아! 그런데 너 형 니콜라이가 또 나타났다는 거 아냐?\"\n\n이 형 니콜라이는 콘스탄틴 레빈의 손위 형이자 세르게이 이바노비치의 이복형으로, 완전히 신세를 망친 사람이었다. 재산의 대부분을 탕진해 버리고, 더없이 괴상하고 천한 무리와 어울려 지냈으며, 형제들과도 사이가 틀어진 상태였다.\n\n\"뭐라고요?\" 레빈이 경악하며 외쳤다. \"어떻게 아셨어요?\"\n\n\"프로코피가 거리에서 봤다더라.\"\n\n\"여기 모스크바에서요? 어디 있는데요? 아세요?\" 레빈은 당장이라도 떠날 듯이 의자에서 벌떡 일어났다.\n\n\"네게 말한 걸 후회한다.\" 세르게이 이바노비치가 동생의 흥분에 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 말했다. \"그가 어디 사는지 알아보라고 사람을 보냈고, 트루빈에게 진 차용증서를 내가 갚아 주고 그에게 보냈지. 이게 그가 내게 보낸 답장이다.\"\n\n그러고는 세르게이 이바노비치가 문진 밑에서 쪽지 한 장을 꺼내 동생에게 건넸다.\n\n레빈은 그 기묘하고도 낯익은 필체를 읽었다. \"삼가 부탁하건대 나를 좀 가만 내버려 두기를 바라네. 그것이 너그러운 형제들에게 내가 청하는 유일한 부탁일세.—니콜라이 레빈.\"\n\n레빈은 그것을 읽고는 고개도 들지 않은 채, 쪽지를 손에 쥐고 세르게이 이바노비치를 마주한 채 서 있었다.\n\n그의 마음속에서는 불행한 형을 잠시나마 잊고 싶다는 바람과, 그렇게 하는 것은 비열한 짓이라는 자각이 서로 다투고 있었다.\n\n\"저 녀석은 분명 나를 모욕하고 싶어 하는 거야.\" 세르게이 이바노비치가 말을 이었다. \"하지만 나를 모욕할 수는 없지. 나는 진심으로 그를 돕고 싶었지만, 그게 불가능하다는 걸 알아.\"\n\n\"그래요, 그래요.\" 레빈이 되뇌었다. \"형이 그를 대하는 태도를 이해하고 또 높이 사요. 하지만 저는 가서 그를 만나 보겠어요.\"\n\n\"가고 싶으면 가렴. 하지만 나라면 권하지 않겠다.\" 세르게이 이바노비치가 말했다. \"나로서는 네가 그렇게 하는 게 두렵진 않아. 그가 너를 나와 틀어지게 만들지는 못할 테니까. 다만 너 자신을 위해서 하는 말인데, 가지 않는 편이 나을 거야. 너는 그에게 아무런 도움도 줄 수 없어. 하지만 네 마음대로 하렴.\"\n\n\"제가 아무 도움도 못 줄 가능성이 크겠죠. 하지만 저는 느껴요—특히 이런 순간에는요—아니 그건 또 다른 문제고—마음이 편할 수가 없을 것 같아요.\"\n\n\"글쎄, 그건 나로선 이해가 안 되는구나.\" 세르게이 이바노비치가 말했다. \"내가 이해하는 한 가지는,\" 그가 덧붙였다. \"그게 겸손에 대한 교훈이라는 거야. 형 니콜라이가 지금 같은 사람이 된 뒤로, 나는 이른바 파렴치하다는 것에 대해 아주 다르게, 훨씬 너그럽게 보게 됐어…… 그 녀석이 무슨 짓을 했는지 너도 알잖니…….\"\n\n\"아, 끔찍해요, 끔찍해!\" 레빈이 거듭 말했다.\n\n세르게이 이바노비치의 하인에게서 형의 주소를 받아 낸 레빈은 당장 그를 만나러 떠나려 했지만, 다시 생각한 끝에 방문을 저녁으로 미루기로 했다. 마음을 가라앉히기 위해 가장 먼저 해야 할 일은 자기가 모스크바에 온 목적을 이루는 것이었다. 형의 집을 나선 레빈은 오블론스키의 관청으로 갔고, 거기서 셰르바츠키 집안 소식을 들은 뒤, 키티를 만날 수 있을 거라고 들은 장소로 마차를 몰았다.\n\n# Chapter 9\n\n네 시에, 가슴이 두근거리는 것을 느끼면서, 레빈은 동물원 앞에서 삯썰매에서 내려 얼어붙은 언덕과 스케이트장으로 이어진 길로 접어들었다. 입구에서 셰르바츠키 집안의 마차를 본 터라, 그곳에 가면 틀림없이 그녀를 만날 수 있으리라는 것을 알고 있었다.\n\n화창하고 서리가 내린 날이었다. 마차와 썰매, 마부와 경관 들이 줄지어 진입로에 서 있었다. 햇빛에 모자를 반짝이며 잘 차려입은 사람들이 무리를 지어 입구 주변과, 러시아풍 조각으로 장식된 작은 집들 사이로 잘 쓸어 놓은 오솔길을 따라 북적였다. 잔가지마다 눈을 잔뜩 인 정원의 오래된 곱슬자작나무들은 막 거룩한 제의(祭衣)로 새로 단장한 것처럼 보였다.\n\n그는 스케이트장으로 이어진 길을 따라 걸으며 자기 자신에게 거듭 말했다. \"흥분하면 안 돼, 침착해야 해. 도대체 뭐가 문제야? 뭘 원하는 거야? 가만히 있어, 멍청아.\" 그는 자기 심장에게 명령했다. 그러나 마음을 가라앉히려 애쓰면 애쓸수록 숨이 더 가빠지는 것이었다. 아는 사람 하나가 그를 만나 이름을 불렀지만, 레빈은 그가 누구인지조차 알아보지 못했다. 그는 언덕 쪽으로 걸어갔다. 그쪽에서는 썰매가 미끄러져 내려가거나 끌어올려질 때 나는 쇠사슬 소리며, 미끄러지는 썰매의 덜컹거리는 소리며, 즐거운 목소리들이 들려왔다. 그가 몇 걸음 더 걷자 스케이트장이 눈앞에 펼쳐졌고, 그 순간 그는 그 모든 스케이트를 타는 사람들 가운데서 단번에 그녀를 알아보았다.\n\n그녀가 거기 있다는 것을 그는 자기 가슴을 사로잡은 환희와 공포로 알았다. 그녀는 스케이트장 맞은편 끝에서 어떤 부인과 이야기를 나누며 서 있었다. 그녀의 옷차림에도 자세에도 눈에 띄는 데는 딱히 없는 듯했다. 그러나 레빈에게는 쐐기풀 속의 장미를 찾아내듯 그 무리 속에서 그녀를 찾아내는 것이 쉬운 일이었다. 모든 것이 그녀로 인해 환해졌다. 그녀는 주위 모든 것에 빛을 뿌리는 미소였다. '정말 내가 저 얼음판을 가로질러 저쪽으로 가서, 그녀에게 다가갈 수 있을까?' 그는 생각했다. 그녀가 서 있는 자리가 그에게는 다가갈 수 없는 거룩한 성소처럼 여겨졌고, 공포에 그토록 압도된 나머지 하마터면 발길을 돌릴 뻔한 순간도 있었다. 그는 자신을 다잡으려고 애를 써야 했고, 온갖 부류의 사람들이 그녀 주변을 오가고 있으며 자기도 스케이트를 타러 거기 갈 수 있는 것이라고 스스로에게 일깨워야 했다. 그는 마치 태양을 보지 않으려 하듯 오랫동안 그녀를 보지 않으려 애쓰며 내려갔지만, 태양을 그러하듯 보지 않고도 그녀를 보고 있었다.\n\n한 주 가운데 그 요일, 하루 가운데 그 시각에는 서로 다 아는 사이인 한 무리의 사람들이 얼음판에서 만나곤 했다. 거기에는 솜씨를 뽐내는 일류 스케이트 선수들도 있었고, 겁먹은 어설픈 동작으로 의자에 매달리는 초보자들도 있었으며, 소년들과, 건강을 위한 목적으로 스케이트를 타는 노인들도 있었다. 레빈에게는 그들이, 그녀 곁 이곳에 있다는 이유만으로, 선택받은 행복한 존재들의 무리처럼 보였다. 모든 스케이트 타는 사람들은 더없이 침착하게 그녀를 향해 미끄러져 가고, 그녀 곁을 스쳐 가고, 심지어 그녀에게 말을 걸기까지 하면서, 그녀와는 전혀 상관없이 좋은 얼음과 화창한 날씨를 즐기며 행복해하는 듯 보였다.\n\n키티의 사촌인 니콜라이 셰르바츠키는 짧은 재킷에 꽉 끼는 바지 차림으로 스케이트를 신은 채 정원 벤치에 앉아 있었다. 레빈을 보고 그가 소리쳤다.\n\n\"아, 러시아 제일의 스케이트 선수로군! 온 지 오래됐나? 얼음이 끝내주는걸—얼른 스케이트나 신게.\"\n\n\"스케이트를 안 가져왔어.\" 레빈은 그녀가 있는 자리에서 보이는 이런 대담함과 여유로움에 놀라며, 그녀를 보고 있지는 않으면서도 한순간도 그녀에게서 눈을 떼지 못한 채 대답했다. 그는 마치 태양이 자기에게 가까이 다가오는 듯한 느낌이 들었다. 그녀는 한쪽 구석에 있었고, 굽 높은 부츠를 신은 날씬한 발을 눈에 띄게 머뭇거리며 바깥쪽으로 돌리면서 그를 향해 미끄러져 왔다. 러시아풍 옷차림을 한 소년 하나가 두 팔을 미친 듯이 휘저으며 땅에 닿을 듯 몸을 굽힌 채 그녀를 앞질러 갔다. 그녀는 다소 불안하게 미끄러졌다. 끈에 매달린 작은 토시에서 두 손을 빼내 만일에 대비하듯 준비한 채, 자기가 알아본 레빈 쪽을 바라보며 그에게, 그리고 자기 자신의 두려움에 미소를 지었다. 모퉁이를 돌고 나자 그녀는 한쪽 발로 박차를 가하며 곧장 셰르바츠키에게로 미끄러져 갔다. 그의 팔을 붙잡으며 그녀는 미소 띤 얼굴로 레빈에게 고갯짓을 했다. 그녀는 그가 상상했던 것보다 더 눈부셨다.\n\n그녀를 떠올릴 때면, 그는 그녀의 생생한 모습을, 특히 맵시 있는 소녀다운 어깨 위에 그토록 자유로이 얹혀 있고, 어린아이 같은 밝음과 명랑함으로 가득한 그 작고 고운 머리의 매력을 마음속에 불러낼 수 있었다. 그녀 표정의 어린아이다움이 그 가냘프고 아름다운 자태와 어우러져 그녀만의 독특한 매력을 이루고 있었고, 그는 그것을 충분히 알고 있었다. 그러나 늘 무언가 예기치 못한 것으로 그를 사로잡은 것은, 부드럽고 고요하며 진실한 그녀의 눈빛이었고, 무엇보다도 그녀의 미소였다. 그 미소는 언제나 레빈을 마법의 세계로 데려갔고, 그곳에서 그는 아주 어린 시절의 어느 날들에 자기 모습이 그러했던 것처럼 마음이 누그러지고 다정해지는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n\"온 지 오래됐어요?\" 그녀가 손을 내밀며 말했다. \"고마워요.\" 그녀의 토시에서 떨어진 손수건을 그가 주워 들자 그녀가 덧붙였다.\n\n\"나요? 온 지 얼마 안 됐어요…… 어제…… 아니, 오늘이요…… 도착했어요.\" 레빈은 격앙된 나머지 그녀의 물음을 단번에 알아듣지 못한 채 대답했다. \"댁에 찾아뵈러 가려던 참이었어요.\" 그가 말했다. 그러고는 무슨 마음으로 그녀를 만나려 했는지를 떠올리고는 곧바로 당혹감에 사로잡혀 얼굴을 붉혔다.\n\n\"스케이트를 탈 줄 아는지, 그것도 이렇게 잘 타는지 몰랐어요.\"\n\n그녀는 그의 당혹감의 까닭을 알아내고 싶기라도 한 듯 진지하게 그를 바라보았다.\n\n\"당신이 칭찬해 주니 값진 거네요. 여기서는 당신이 최고의 스케이트 선수라는 게 전설처럼 전해 오거든요.\" 그녀는 검은 장갑을 낀 작은 손으로 토시에 내려앉은 서리 알갱이를 털어 내며 말했다.\n\n\"네, 한때는 열정적으로 스케이트를 탔어요. 완벽의 경지에 이르고 싶었죠.\"\n\n\"당신은 뭐든 열정적으로 하는 분 같아요.\" 그녀가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"당신이 스케이트 타는 걸 정말 보고 싶어요. 스케이트를 신어요, 그리고 같이 타요.\"\n\n'같이 타다니! 그게 가능할까?' 레빈은 그녀를 바라보며 생각했다.\n\n\"당장 신을게요.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n그러고는 스케이트를 가지러 갔다.\n\n\"한참 만에 뵙는군요, 나리.\" 관리인이 그의 발을 받쳐 들고 스케이트 뒤축을 죄며 말했다. \"나리 말고는 일류 스케이트 선수인 신사분이 한 분도 안 계시지요. 이만하면 됐습니까?\" 그가 끈을 단단히 조이며 물었다.\n\n\"오, 그래, 됐네. 제발 좀 서둘러 주게.\" 레빈은 얼굴에 번지려 하는 환희의 미소를 가까스로 억누르며 대답했다. '그래.' 그는 생각했다. '이게 바로 인생이야, 이게 바로 행복이야! 같이, 라고 그녀가 말했어. 같이 타요, 라고! 지금 그녀에게 말할까? 하지만 바로 그래서 말하기가 두려운 거야—지금 행복하니까, 어쨌든 희망 속에서 행복하니까…… 그러고 나면?…… 하지만 해야 해! 해야 해! 해야 한단 말이야! 나약함은 떨쳐 버려!'\n\n레빈은 일어나 외투를 벗고는, 오두막 주변의 울퉁불퉁한 얼음 위를 종종걸음으로 지나 매끄러운 얼음판으로 나와, 그야말로 단순히 의지를 발휘하는 것만으로 힘들이지 않고 미끄러지며 속도를 올렸다 늦추었다 하고 방향을 바꾸었다. 그는 머뭇거리며 다가갔지만, 다시 한번 그녀의 미소가 그를 안심시켰다.\n\n그녀가 그에게 손을 내밀었고, 두 사람은 나란히 미끄러져 가며 점점 더 빨라졌다. 빨라지면 빨라질수록 그녀는 그의 손을 더 꽉 움켜쥐었다.\n\n\"당신과 함께라면 금방 배울 것 같아요. 어쩐지 당신을 믿게 되네요.\" 그녀가 그에게 말했다.\n\n\"그리고 당신이 내게 기대고 있으면 나도 나 자신이 믿어져요.\" 그는 그렇게 말했지만, 자기가 한 말에 곧바로 겁에 질려 얼굴을 붉혔다. 그리고 정말로, 그가 이 말을 입 밖에 내자마자, 별안간 해가 구름 뒤로 숨듯 그녀의 얼굴에서 다정함이 죄다 사라졌고, 레빈은 그녀의 표정에서 생각이 작동하고 있음을 나타내는 그 낯익은 변화를 알아챘다. 매끈한 이마에 주름이 잡혔다.\n\n\"무슨 걱정거리라도 있어요?—이런 걸 물을 자격이 내겐 없지만요.\" 그가 황급히 덧붙였다.\n\n\"오, 어째서요?…… 아니에요, 걱정거리 따위 없어요.\" 그녀는 차갑게 대답했다. 그러고는 곧바로 덧붙였다. \"리농 양은 아직 못 봤죠?\"\n\n\"아직요.\"\n\n\"가서 인사드려요, 당신을 무척 좋아하시니까요.\"\n\n'뭐가 잘못된 거지? 내가 그녀의 마음을 상하게 했어. 하느님, 도와주소서!' 레빈은 그렇게 생각하며, 벤치에 앉아 있던, 잿빛 고수머리를 한 늙은 프랑스 여자에게로 날아가듯 다가갔다. 그녀는 미소를 지으며 의치를 드러낸 채 옛 친구를 대하듯 그를 맞이했다.\n\n\"그래요, 보다시피 우리는 자라고 있죠.\" 그녀는 키티 쪽을 흘끗 바라보며 말했다. \"그리고 늙어 가고요. 꼬마 곰이 이젠 다 컸네요!\" 프랑스 여자는 웃으면서 말을 이었고, 그가 세 아가씨를 영국 동화에 나오는 세 마리 곰에 빗대었던 농담을 그에게 일깨워 주었다. \"그렇게 부르곤 했던 거 기억나요?\"\n\n그는 정말이지 아무것도 기억나지 않았지만, 그녀는 이 농담을 두고 십 년째 웃어 오고 있었고, 그 농담을 무척 좋아했다.\n\n\"자, 가서 타요, 가서 타요. 우리 키티가 스케이트를 곧잘 타게 됐죠, 안 그래요?\"\n\n레빈이 쏜살같이 키티에게로 다가갔을 때 그녀의 얼굴은 더 이상 굳어 있지 않았다. 그녀의 눈은 여전한 진실함과 다정함으로 그를 바라보았지만, 레빈은 그 다정함 속에 일부러 꾸민 차분함의 기색이 어려 있는 듯한 느낌을 받았다. 그래서 그는 의기소침해졌다. 그녀의 옛 가정교사와 그녀의 별난 점들에 대해 잠깐 이야기를 나눈 뒤, 그녀는 그에게 그의 생활에 대해 물었다.\n\n\"겨울에 시골에 있으면 분명 따분하겠죠, 안 그래요?\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"아니요, 따분하지 않아요. 아주 바쁘게 지내거든요.\" 그는 그녀가 그 차분한 어조로 자기를 묶어 두고 있으며, 겨울이 시작될 무렵에 그랬던 것처럼 자기에게는 그것을 뚫고 나갈 힘이 없으리라는 것을 느끼며 말했다.\n\n\"도시에 오래 머물 건가요?\" 키티가 그에게 물었다.\n\n\"모르겠어요.\" 그는 자기가 무슨 말을 하는지도 생각하지 못한 채 대답했다. 그녀의 조용히 다정한 어조에 묶여 있다가는 결국 아무것도 결정하지 못한 채 다시 돌아가 버리게 되리라는 생각이 그의 머릿속에 떠올랐고, 그는 그것에 맞서 싸워 보기로 마음먹었다.\n\n\"어째서 모른다는 거예요?\"\n\n\"모르겠어요. 그건 당신에게 달렸거든요.\" 그가 말했고, 자기 말에 곧바로 소스라치게 놀랐다.\n\n그의 말을 들었기 때문인지, 아니면 그 말을 듣고 싶지 않았기 때문인지, 그녀는 살짝 비틀거리는 듯하더니 두어 번 발을 내딛고는 황급히 그에게서 미끄러져 멀어졌다. 그녀는 리농 양에게로 미끄러져 가 무언가 말을 하고는, 부인들이 스케이트를 벗는 정자 쪽으로 갔다.\n\n'맙소사! 내가 무슨 짓을 한 거지! 자비로우신 하느님! 저를 도우시고, 이끌어 주소서.' 레빈은 마음속으로 기도하며, 동시에 격렬하게 몸을 움직일 필요를 느끼면서 안쪽과 바깥쪽으로 원을 그리며 스케이트를 탔다.\n\n그 순간 그날 최고의 스케이트 선수인 젊은이 하나가 입에 담배를 문 채 스케이트를 신고 다방에서 나왔다. 달려와서는 스케이트를 신은 채 우당탕 요란하게 위아래로 튀어 오르며 계단을 내달려 내려갔다. 그는 날듯이 내려와서는 두 손의 자세를 바꾸지도 않은 채 얼음판 위를 미끄러져 가 버렸다.\n\n\"아, 저거 새로운 묘기로군!\" 레빈은 그렇게 말하고는 곧바로 그 새로운 묘기를 해 보려고 꼭대기로 뛰어올라 갔다.\n\n\"목 부러뜨리지 말게! 연습이 필요한 거야!\" 니콜라이 셰르바츠키가 그의 등 뒤에서 소리쳤다.\n\n레빈은 계단으로 가서 위에서부터 할 수 있는 한 힘껏 달려 내려오며, 두 손으로 이 익숙지 않은 동작 속에서 균형을 잡았다. 마지막 계단에서 그는 비틀거렸지만, 한 손으로 얼음을 살짝 짚었을 뿐 안간힘을 써서 몸을 가누고는 웃으면서 미끄러져 갔다.\n\n'어쩜 저렇게 멋질까, 어쩜 저렇게 좋은 사람일까!' 그때 키티는 리농 양과 함께 정자에서 나오며, 마치 그가 사랑하는 오빠라도 되는 양 조용한 애정이 담긴 미소를 지으며 그를 바라보았다. '그런데 이게 내 잘못일까, 내가 뭔가 잘못한 게 있을까? 사람들은 교태를 부린다고들 하지. 내가 사랑하는 게 그 사람이 아니라는 건 알아. 하지만 그래도 난 그 사람과 있으면 행복하고, 그 사람은 참 유쾌하지. 다만, 왜 그런 말을 했을까?……' 그녀는 골똘히 생각에 잠겼다.\n\n키티가 떠나가는 것을, 그리고 그녀의 어머니가 계단에서 그녀를 맞이하는 것을 본 레빈은, 격렬하게 몸을 움직인 탓에 얼굴이 상기된 채로 멈춰 서서 잠깐 생각에 잠겼다. 그는 스케이트를 벗고, 정원 입구에서 모녀를 따라잡았다.\n\n\"만나서 반가워요.\" 셰르바츠카야 공작 부인이 말했다. \"목요일에는 늘 그렇듯 집에 있답니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 오늘인가요?\"\n\n\"오시면 기쁘겠어요.\" 공작 부인이 딱딱하게 말했다.\n\n이 딱딱함이 키티의 마음을 아프게 했고, 그녀는 어머니의 냉랭함을 누그러뜨리고 싶은 마음을 억누를 수가 없었다. 그녀는 고개를 돌리고 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 저녁때 뵐게요.\"\n\n그 순간 스테판 아르카지치가, 모자를 한쪽으로 비뚜름하게 쓰고 얼굴과 눈을 환하게 빛내며 정복하고 돌아온 영웅처럼 성큼성큼 정원으로 들어섰다. 그러나 장모에게 다가가자 그는 돌리의 건강을 묻는 그녀의 물음에 구슬프고 풀 죽은 어조로 대답했다. 장모와 나직하고 침울하게 잠시 이야기를 나눈 뒤, 그는 다시 가슴을 활짝 펴고 레빈의 팔에 자기 팔을 끼었다.\n\n\"자, 그럼 가 볼까?\" 그가 물었다. \"그동안 줄곧 자네 생각을 했네. 자네가 와 줘서 정말, 정말 기쁘네.\" 그는 의미심장한 표정으로 그의 얼굴을 들여다보며 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 가세.\" 레빈은 황홀경에 빠져 대답했다. 그의 귀에는 \"그럼 저녁때 뵐게요\"라고 말하던 그 목소리가 끊임없이 들려왔고, 그 말과 함께 짓던 미소가 눈앞에 어른거렸다.\n\n\"앙글레테르로 갈까, 에르미타시로 갈까?\"\n\n\"난 어느 쪽이든 상관없네.\"\n\n\"좋아, 그럼 앙글레테르로.\" 스테판 아르카지치는 그 식당을 골랐는데, 에르미타시보다 거기에 진 빚이 더 많았기에 그곳을 피하는 것은 비겁한 짓이라고 여겼기 때문이다. \"썰매는 있나? 그것참 잘됐군, 난 마차를 집으로 돌려보냈거든.\"\n\n가는 내내 두 친구는 거의 말이 없었다. 레빈은 키티의 표정에 일었던 그 변화가 무엇을 뜻하는지 궁금해하며, 희망이 있다고 자신을 다독였다가 다시 절망에 빠졌다가 하면서, 자기 희망이 미친 짓임을 분명히 알면서도, 그러면서도 줄곧 자기가 그녀의 미소와 \"그럼 저녁때 뵐게요\"라는 그 말 이전의 자기와는 전혀 다른, 완전히 딴사람이 된 것 같다고 느꼈다.\n\n스테판 아르카지치는 가는 동안 만찬의 메뉴를 짜는 데 골몰해 있었다.\n\n\"자네 가자미 좋아하지, 안 그래?\" 도착할 무렵 그가 레빈에게 말했다.\n\n\"어?\" 레빈이 대답했다. \"가자미? 그래, 난 가자미를 정말이지 무척 좋아하네.\"\n\n# Chapter 10\n\n레빈이 오블론스키와 함께 식당에 들어섰을 때, 그는 스테판 아르카지치의 얼굴과 온몸에서 어딘가 독특한 표정, 말하자면 억눌린 광채 같은 것을 알아채지 않을 수 없었다. 오블론스키는 외투를 벗고 모자를 한쪽으로 비스듬히 쓴 채 식당 안으로 들어가며, 연미복 차림에 냅킨을 들고 그의 주위로 몰려든 타타르인 종업원들에게 이런저런 지시를 내렸다. 마주치는 사람들에게 좌우로 인사를 건네며, 여기서도 여느 곳에서나 그렇듯 즐겁게 아는 이들과 인사를 나누고는, 그는 생선과 보드카로 입가심을 할 요량으로 곁상 쪽으로 다가갔다. 그러고는 카운터 뒤편에 리본과 레이스와 곱슬머리로 잔뜩 치장하고 화장을 한 프랑스 여자에게 무언가 어찌나 우스운 말을 건넸던지, 그 프랑스 여자조차 진심으로 웃음을 터뜨릴 정도였다. 레빈으로서는 보드카를 입에 대지 않았는데, 그것은 단지 그 프랑스 여자가 가짜 머리카락과 poudre de riz와 vinaigre de toilette로만 이루어진 것 같아서 진저리가 났기 때문이었다. 그는 마치 더러운 곳에서 벗어나듯 서둘러 그녀에게서 멀어졌다. 그의 영혼은 온통 키티에 대한 기억으로 가득 차 있었고, 그의 눈에는 승리와 행복의 미소가 빛나고 있었다.\n* poudre de riz: 프랑스어로 쌀가루로 만든 분(粉). vinaigre de toilette: 화장용 향수 식초.\n\n\"이쪽으로 오시지요, 나리. 이 자리시면 나리께서 방해받으실 일이 없습니다.\" 엉덩이가 어마어마하게 크고 뒤로 옷자락이 활짝 벌어진, 머리가 허연 유난히 끈질긴 타타르인 노인이 말했다. \"어서 드시지요, 나리.\" 그는 레빈에게도 말했는데, 스테판 아르카지치에 대한 존경의 표시로 그 손님에게도 신경을 쓰는 것이었다.\n\n청동 샹들리에 아래 둥근 식탁에는 이미 식탁보가 깔려 있었지만, 그는 곧바로 새 식탁보를 휙 펼쳐 덮고는 벨벳 의자들을 끌어다 놓았다. 그러고는 냅킨과 차림표를 손에 든 채 스테판 아르카지치 앞에 서서 분부를 기다렸다.\n\n\"원하신다면, 나리, 곧 별실 하나가 빌 겁니다. 골리친 공작이 어떤 부인과 함께 계신데요. 싱싱한 굴도 들어왔습니다.\"\n\n\"오! 굴이라.\"\n\n스테판 아르카지치는 생각에 잠겼다.\n\n\"우리 계획을 좀 바꿔 보는 게 어떨까, 레빈?\" 그는 차림표 위에 손가락을 댄 채 말했다. 그의 얼굴에는 자못 진지한 망설임이 떠올라 있었다. \"굴이 괜찮은가? 잘 살펴보게.\"\n\n\"플렌스부르크산입니다, 나리. 오스텐드산은 없습니다.\"\n\n\"플렌스부르크산이라도 좋아, 그런데 싱싱한가?\"\n\n\"어제 막 들어온 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그럼, 굴로 시작해서 계획을 통째로 바꾸는 게 어떨까? 응?\"\n\n\"나는 아무래도 좋아. 무엇보다 양배추 수프하고 죽이 제일 좋겠지만, 물론 여기에 그런 건 없겠지.\"\n\n\"러시아식 죽 말씀이십니까, 나리?\" 타타르인이 마치 아이에게 말하는 유모처럼 레빈에게 몸을 굽히며 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 농담은 그만두고, 자네가 고르는 거라면 뭐든 분명 맛있을 거야. 스케이트를 탔더니 배가 고프군. 그렇다고,\" 그는 오블론스키의 얼굴에 떠오른 불만스러운 기색을 알아채고 덧붙였다. \"자네가 고른 걸 내가 못 알아본다고는 생각하지 말게. 나도 좋은 음식은 좋아하니까.\"\n\n\"그래야지! 어쨌든 그것도 인생의 즐거움 가운데 하나니까.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 말했다. \"그럼, 이봐, 굴을 두 다스, 아니 세 다스가 낫겠군, 그리고 채소를 넣은 맑은 수프……\"\n\n\"프랭타니에르 말씀이시군요.\" 타타르인이 거들었다. 하지만 스테판 아르카지치는 그가 요리의 프랑스 이름을 입에 올리는 만족을 누리도록 내버려 둘 생각이 없는 듯했다.\n\n\"채소를 넣은 거 말일세. 그다음에 진한 소스를 곁들인 넙치, 그러고는…… 로스트비프. 잘 만들어야 해. 그래, 거세한 수탉 요리도 좋겠군, 그리고 단것을 내오게.\"\n\n타타르인은 스테판 아르카지치가 프랑스 차림표의 이름대로 요리를 부르지 않는 버릇이 있다는 것을 떠올리고는, 그를 따라 되풀이하지 않았다. 다만 차림표대로 메뉴 전부를 혼자 되뇌고 싶은 마음만은 참을 수가 없었다. \"프랭타니에르 수프, 넙치, 보마르셰 소스, 에스트라공을 넣은 영계 요리, 과일 마세두안……\" 그러고는 마치 용수철로 움직이듯 즉시 끈으로 묶은 차림표 하나를 내려놓고 다른 하나, 곧 포도주 목록을 집어 들어 스테판 아르카지치에게 내밀었다.\n\n\"무엇을 마실까?\"\n\n\"자네 좋을 대로, 다만 너무 많이는 말고. 샴페인이 좋겠어.\" 레빈이 말했다.\n\n\"뭐! 시작부터? 하긴 자네 말이 맞아. 화이트 실 좋아하나?\"\n\n\"카셰 블랑 말씀이시군요.\" 타타르인이 거들었다.\n\n\"좋아, 그럼 그 상표로 굴과 함께 내오고, 다음은 그때 가서 보지.\"\n\n\"네, 나리. 식탁용 포도주는 무엇으로 할까요?\"\n\n\"뉘를 주게. 아니, 고전적인 샤블리가 낫겠군.\"\n\n\"네, 나리. 치즈는 어떤 것으로 하시겠습니까, 나리?\"\n\n\"아, 그래, 파르메산으로. 아니면 자네 다른 게 좋겠나?\"\n\n\"아니, 나는 아무래도 좋아.\" 레빈이 미소를 참지 못하며 말했다.\n\n타타르인은 옷자락을 휘날리며 달려갔다가, 오 분 뒤에는 자개껍데기에 담긴 까 놓은 굴 한 접시를 들고, 손가락 사이에는 병 하나를 끼운 채 쏜살같이 들어왔다.\n\n스테판 아르카지치는 빳빳한 냅킨을 구겨 조끼 사이에 끼우고는, 두 팔을 편하게 자리 잡은 다음 굴을 먹기 시작했다.\n\n\"나쁘지 않군.\" 그는 은제 포크로 진주빛 껍데기에서 굴을 떼어 내 하나씩 삼키며 말했다. \"나쁘지 않아.\" 그는 촉촉하고 반짝이는 눈을 레빈에게서 타타르인 쪽으로 돌리며 거듭 말했다.\n\n레빈은 흰 빵과 치즈가 더 좋았겠지만, 그래도 굴을 먹었다. 다만 그는 오블론스키를 바라보며 감탄하고 있었다. 병마개를 따서 거품이 이는 포도주를 가느다란 잔에 따르던 타타르인조차 스테판 아르카지치를 흘끗 바라보고는, 흡족함이 역력한 미소를 띤 채 자기 흰 넥타이를 매만졌다.\n\n\"자네는 굴을 별로 좋아하지 않는 모양이군?\" 스테판 아르카지치가 포도주잔을 비우며 말했다. \"아니면 뭔가 걱정거리가 있거나. 응?\"\n\n그는 레빈이 기분 좋게 지내기를 바랐다. 하지만 레빈은 기분이 나쁜 것이 아니라, 마음이 편치 않은 것이었다. 마음속에 품은 것을 안고 있는 그로서는, 남자들이 부인들과 식사를 하고 있는 별실들 한가운데, 이 온갖 부산함과 법석 속에서 식당에 있는 것이 쓰라리고 거북했다. 청동 장식이며 거울이며 가스등이며 종업원들에 둘러싸인 이 모든 것이 그에게는 거슬렸다. 그는 영혼 가득히 차오른 것을 더럽힐까 봐 두려웠던 것이다.\n\n\"나? 그래, 걱정이 있긴 해. 하지만 그뿐 아니라 이 모든 게 다 거북해.\" 그가 말했다. \"나 같은 시골 사람한테 이 모든 게 얼마나 기이하게 느껴지는지 자네는 짐작도 못 할 거야. 자네 사무실에서 본 그 신사 손톱만큼이나 기이하단 말이지……\"\n\n\"그래, 자네가 그 가엾은 그리네비치의 손톱에 얼마나 관심을 가졌는지 보았네.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 웃으며 말했다.\n\n\"나로선 도무지 견딜 수가 없어.\" 레빈이 대답했다. \"한번 내 처지가 되어 시골 사람의 눈으로 봐 보게. 우리 시골에선 일하기 가장 편한 상태로 손을 만들려고 애를 쓰지. 그래서 손톱을 깎고, 가끔은 소매를 걷어 올려. 그런데 여기 사람들은 일부러 손톱을 마음껏 길게 기르고, 단추 대신 작은 접시 같은 걸 매달아서, 손으로는 아무 일도 못 하게 만들어 놓았단 말이야.\"\n\n스테판 아르카지치가 유쾌하게 미소 지었다.\n\n\"오, 그래, 그건 바로 그가 거친 일을 할 필요가 없다는 표시일 뿐이야. 그의 일이란 머리로 하는 거니까……\"\n\n\"그럴지도 모르지. 하지만 그래도 나한테는 기이하게 느껴져. 지금 이 순간에도 기이하게 느껴지는 게, 우리 시골 사람들은 어서 일할 채비를 하려고 식사를 되도록 빨리 끝내려 애쓰는데, 여기서 우리는 식사를 되도록 오래 끌려고 애쓰면서 그러느라 굴을 먹고 있단 말이지……\"\n\n\"아니, 물론 그렇지.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 반박했다. \"하지만 그게 바로 문명의 목적이야. 모든 것을 즐거움의 원천으로 만드는 것 말이야.\"\n\n\"흠, 그게 목적이라면, 난 차라리 야만인이 되겠어.\"\n\n\"그러니까 자네는 야만인이야. 자네 레빈 집안사람들은 죄다 야만인이라니까.\"\n\n레빈은 한숨을 쉬었다. 그는 형 니콜라이를 떠올리고는 부끄럽고 쓰라린 마음에 얼굴을 찡그렸다. 하지만 오블론스키가 곧 그의 주의를 끄는 화제를 꺼냈다.\n\n\"아 참, 오늘 밤 우리 집안 식구들, 그러니까 셰르바츠키 댁에 갈 텐가?\" 그는 빈 거친 껍데기들을 밀어 놓고 치즈를 끌어당기며, 의미심장하게 눈을 반짝이며 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 꼭 갈 생각이야.\" 레빈이 대답했다. \"다만 공작 부인이 초대하면서 그리 살갑지는 않았던 것 같긴 하지만.\"\n\n\"무슨 소리! 그건 그분 방식이야…… 이봐, 수프를 가져오게!…… 그게 그분 방식이라니까, grande dame 말이야.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 말했다. \"나도 갈 거야, 다만 그 전에 보니나 백작 부인 댁 예행연습에 가 봐야 해. 자, 자네가 야만인이라는 게 정말 맞지 않은가? 자네가 그렇게 느닷없이 모스크바에서 사라진 걸 어떻게 설명할 텐가? 셰르바츠키 댁에서는 마치 내가 다 알고 있어야 한다는 듯이 줄곧 자네에 대해 물었지. 내가 아는 거라곤, 자네가 늘 남들은 하지 않는 짓만 한다는 것뿐이야.\"\n* grande dame: 프랑스어로 '귀부인'.\n\n\"그래.\" 레빈이 천천히, 감정을 실어 말했다. \"자네 말이 맞아. 난 야만인이지. 다만 내 야만성은 떠나 버린 데 있는 게 아니라, 지금 이렇게 다시 온 데 있어. 이제 나는 왔거든……\"\n\n\"오, 자네는 얼마나 운 좋은 사람인가!\" 스테판 아르카지치가 레빈의 눈을 들여다보며 끼어들었다.\n\n\"왜?\"\n\n\"'나는 확실한 표징으로 늠름한 준마를 알아보고, 그 눈으로 사랑에 빠진 젊은이를 알아보네.'\" 스테판 아르카지치가 낭송하듯 읊었다. \"자네 앞에는 모든 것이 펼쳐져 있어.\"\n\n\"왜, 자네한테는 벌써 다 끝났나?\"\n\n\"아니, 딱히 끝난 건 아니지만, 미래는 자네 것이고 현재는 내 것이지. 그리고 그 현재라는 게, 흠, 마땅히 그래야 할 그런 것은 못 되네.\"\n\n\"어째서?\"\n\n\"오, 일이 잘 안 풀리고 있어. 하지만 내 얘긴 하고 싶지 않고, 게다가 전부 다 설명할 수도 없어.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 말했다. \"그래, 그럼 자네는 무슨 일로 모스크바에 온 건가?…… 이봐! 이건 치워 주게!\" 그가 타타르인에게 외쳤다.\n\n\"짐작하면서?\" 레빈이 깊은 빛의 우물 같은 눈을 스테판 아르카지치에게 고정한 채 대답했다.\n\n\"짐작은 하지만, 내가 먼저 그 얘기를 꺼낼 수는 없지. 내가 옳게 짚었는지 그른지는 그걸 보면 알 수 있을 거야.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 묘한 미소를 띠고 레빈을 바라보며 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 그럼 자네는 나한테 무슨 말을 해 주겠나?\" 레빈이 떨리는 목소리로 말했다. 그는 자기 얼굴의 모든 근육이 떨리고 있음을 느꼈다. \"자네는 이 문제를 어떻게 보나?\"\n\n스테판 아르카지치는 레빈에게서 눈을 떼지 않은 채, 샤블리잔을 천천히 비웠다.\n\n\"나? 나야말로 그것만큼 바라는 게 없네, 그것 말고는 아무것도! 그건 일어날 수 있는 일 가운데 가장 좋은 일이 될 거야.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 자네가 잘못 짚은 건 아니지? 우리가 무슨 얘기를 하고 있는지 알고 있나?\" 레빈이 그를 눈으로 꿰뚫으며 말했다. \"자네는 그게 가능하다고 생각하나?\"\n\n\"가능하다고 생각해. 왜 가능하지 않겠나?\"\n\n\"아니! 자네 정말로 가능하다고 생각하는 거야? 아니, 자네가 생각하는 걸 다 말해 주게! 오, 하지만 만약…… 만약 거절이 나를 기다리고 있다면!…… 정말이지 그럴 것만 같아……\"\n\n\"왜 그렇게 생각하나?\" 스테판 아르카지치가 그의 흥분에 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"가끔 그렇게 느껴져. 그렇게 되면 나한테도, 그녀한테도 끔찍한 일이 될 거야.\"\n\n\"흠, 어쨌든 처녀에게는 그게 끔찍한 일이랄 게 없어. 어떤 처녀든 청혼을 자랑스러워하니까.\"\n\n\"그래, 어떤 처녀든 그렇겠지, 하지만 그녀는 아니야.\"\n\n스테판 아르카지치가 미소 지었다. 그는 레빈의 그 마음을 너무도 잘 알고 있었다. 레빈에게는 세상의 모든 처녀가 두 부류로 나뉘었다. 한 부류는 그녀를 제외한 세상의 모든 처녀로, 온갖 인간적 약점을 지닌 지극히 평범한 처녀들이었다. 다른 한 부류는 오직 그녀 하나뿐으로, 어떤 약점도 없고 온 인류보다 높은 존재였다.\n\n\"가만, 소스 좀 들게.\" 그는 소스를 밀어내는 레빈의 손을 붙잡으며 말했다.\n\n레빈은 순순히 소스를 덜었지만, 스테판 아르카지치가 계속 식사를 하도록 내버려 두지 않았다.\n\n\"아니, 잠깐만, 잠깐만.\" 그가 말했다. \"이건 나한테 사느냐 죽느냐의 문제라는 걸 자네는 알아야 해. 난 이걸 누구한테도 말한 적이 없어. 그리고 자네 말고는 이걸 털어놓을 사람이 아무도 없어. 우리는 서로 완전히 다르다는 거, 취향도 견해도 모든 게 다르다는 거 나도 알아. 하지만 자네가 나를 좋아하고 나를 이해해 준다는 걸 알기에, 그래서 난 자네를 끔찍이 좋아하는 거야. 그러니 제발, 나한테 아주 솔직하게 말해 주게.\"\n\n\"내가 생각하는 걸 말해 주겠네.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"하지만 한마디 더 보태지. 내 아내는 놀라운 여자야……\" 스테판 아르카지치는 아내와의 처지를 떠올리며 한숨을 쉬더니, 잠시 잠자코 있다가 다시 말을 이었다. \"그 사람한테는 앞일을 내다보는 재능이 있어. 사람 속을 훤히 꿰뚫어 보지. 하지만 그게 다가 아니야. 그 사람은 무슨 일이 일어날지를 알아맞히는데, 특히 혼사에 관해선 더 그래. 이를테면 샤홉스카야 공작 영애가 브렌텔른과 결혼할 거라고 미리 말했지. 아무도 믿지 않았는데, 그대로 됐어. 그런데 그 사람이 자네 편이야.\"\n\n\"무슨 뜻이야?\"\n\n\"그저 자네를 좋아한다는 것뿐이 아니라, 키티가 틀림없이 자네 아내가 될 거라고 말한단 말이야.\"\n\n이 말에 레빈의 얼굴은 별안간 미소로 환해졌다. 그것은 감격의 눈물에 가까운 미소였다.\n\n\"그녀가 그렇게 말했다고!\" 레빈이 외쳤다. \"내가 늘 말했잖아, 자네 아내는 더없이 훌륭한 사람이라고. 자, 이제 됐어, 이 얘긴 이쯤 하면 충분해.\" 그는 자리에서 일어서며 말했다.\n\n\"알았어, 그러니 좀 앉게.\"\n\n하지만 레빈은 도무지 앉아 있을 수가 없었다. 그는 새장만 한 작은 방을 단단한 걸음걸이로 두 차례 오갔고, 눈물이 떨어지지 않도록 눈을 깜빡거리고는, 그제야 식탁으로 돌아와 앉았다.\n\n\"자네는 이해해야 해.\" 그가 말했다. \"이건 사랑이 아니야. 난 사랑에 빠져 본 적이 있지만, 이건 그게 아니야. 이건 내 감정이 아니라, 내 밖에 있는 어떤 힘 같은 것이 나를 사로잡은 거야. 알겠지만, 나는 떠나 버렸어. 그건 결코 이루어질 수 없는 일이라고, 그러니까 이 땅에서는 찾아오지 않는 행복이라고 마음을 굳혔으니까. 하지만 나 자신과 싸워 본 결과, 그것 없이는 살 수 없다는 걸 알게 됐어. 그러니 결판을 내야 해.\"\n\n\"왜 떠났던 건가?\"\n\n\"아, 잠깐만! 아, 머릿속으로 밀려드는 온갖 생각들이라니! 스스로에게 던져야 하는 온갖 물음들이라니! 들어 보게. 자네가 한 그 말로 나한테 어떤 일을 해 줬는지 자네는 상상도 못 할 거야. 난 너무 행복해서 정말이지 밉살스러운 인간이 돼 버렸어. 모든 걸 잊어버렸지. 오늘 형 니콜라이가…… 알다시피 그가 여기 와 있는데…… 그 형마저 잊고 있었어. 형도 행복할 것만 같단 말이야. 이건 일종의 광기야. 하지만 한 가지가 끔찍해…… 이봐, 자네는 결혼했으니까 그 감정을 알겠지…… 끔찍한 건, 우리처럼 나이 든 사람, 과거를 지닌 사람이…… 사랑이 아니라 죄들로 얼룩진 과거를 지닌 사람이…… 순결하고 결백한 존재 곁으로 단숨에 그토록 가까이 끌려간다는 거야. 그건 역겨운 일이고, 그래서 스스로가 그럴 자격이 없다고 느끼지 않을 수 없는 거지.\"\n\n\"오, 흠, 자네 양심에 죄가 그리 많은 건 아니잖나.\"\n\n\"아아! 그래도 마찬가지야.\" 레빈이 말했다. \"혐오감을 안고 내 삶을 돌이켜 보면, 나는 몸서리치고 저주하고 쓰라리게 후회하게 돼…… 그래.\"\n\n\"그럼 어쩌겠나? 세상이 그렇게 생겨먹은걸.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 말했다.\n\n\"한 가지 위안이 있다면, 내가 늘 좋아했던 그 기도와 같은 거야. '저의 무가치함이 아니라, 당신의 자비로우심에 따라 저를 용서하소서.' 그녀가 나를 용서할 수 있는 길도 오직 그것뿐이지.\"\n\n레빈은 잔을 비웠고, 둘은 잠시 말이 없었다.\n\n\"한 가지 더 말해 둘 게 있어. 자네, 브론스키를 아나?\" 스테판 아르카지치가 레빈에게 물었다.\n\n\"아니, 몰라. 왜 묻는데?\"\n\n\"한 병 더 주게.\" 스테판 아르카지치는, 필요하지도 않을 때 잔을 채우며 주위를 부산스레 맴도는 타타르인에게 일렀다.\n\n\"자네가 브론스키를 알아 둬야 하는 까닭은, 그가 자네의 경쟁자 가운데 한 사람이기 때문이지.\"\n\n\"브론스키가 누군데?\" 레빈이 말했다. 그러자 그의 얼굴은, 방금 전 오블론스키가 흐뭇하게 바라보던 어린아이 같은 환희의 표정에서 별안간 성난, 불쾌한 표정으로 바뀌었다.\n\n\"브론스키는 키릴 이바노비치 브론스키 백작의 아들들 가운데 하나이고, 페테르부르크의 금빛 청년들 중에서도 가장 빼어난 표본 가운데 하나야. 나는 공무로 트베리에 가 있을 때 그를 알게 됐는데, 그는 신병 모집 때문에 거기 왔지. 어마어마하게 부자에다, 잘생겼고, 인맥도 넓고, 시종무관인 데다, 게다가 더없이 좋은 사람, 마음씨 고운 친구야. 한데 그저 마음씨 고운 친구 정도가 아니라는 걸, 여기서 알게 됐어—교양도 갖춘 사람이고, 머리도 아주 좋아. 한자리 할 인물이지.\"\n\n레빈은 얼굴을 찌푸리고 입을 다물었다.\n\n\"그래, 그가 자네 떠난 직후에 여기 나타났는데, 내가 보기엔 키티한테 홀딱 반해 있어. 게다가 자네도 알다시피 그 애 어머니가……\"\n\n\"미안하지만, 난 아무것도 몰라.\" 레빈이 침울하게 미간을 찡그리며 말했다. 그리고 곧바로 형 니콜라이가, 그리고 그 형을 잊고 있을 수 있었던 자신이 얼마나 가증스러운지가 떠올랐다.\n\n\"가만, 좀 가만있어 봐.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 미소를 지으며 그의 손을 어루만지듯 짚으며 말했다. \"내가 아는 걸 자네한테 말해 준 거고, 다시 한번 말하지만 이 미묘하고 섬세한 문제에서, 짐작이 닿는 한, 내 생각엔 가능성이 자네 쪽에 있어.\"\n\n레빈은 의자에 등을 기댔다. 그의 얼굴은 창백했다.\n\n\"하지만 이 일은 되도록 빨리 매듭짓는 게 좋겠어.\" 오블론스키는 자기 잔을 채우며 말을 이었다.\n\n\"아니, 됐어, 더는 못 마시겠어.\" 레빈이 잔을 밀어내며 말했다. \"취하겠어……. 자, 자네 얘기나 해 봐, 요즘 어떻게 지내?\" 그는 분명 화제를 바꾸고 싶어 하며 말을 이었다.\n\n\"한마디만 더. 어쨌든 그 문제는 빨리 결판을 짓는 게 좋겠어. 오늘 밤엔 말을 꺼내지 않는 게 좋고.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 말했다. \"내일 아침에 찾아가서, 격식을 갖춰 청혼을 하게. 그러면 하느님의 가호가 있기를…….\"\n\n\"아 참, 자네 아직도 나한테 사냥하러 올 생각 있어? 내년 봄에 오게, 꼭.\" 레빈이 말했다.\n\n이제 그의 온 영혼은 스테판 아르카지치와 이런 이야기를 시작한 데 대한 후회로 가득 찼다. 그가 품은 것과 같은 감정은, 어느 페테르부르크 장교와의 경쟁이라느니, 스테판 아르카지치의 추측이며 조언이라느니 하는 이야기로 더럽혀지는 것이었다.\n\n스테판 아르카지치는 미소를 지었다. 그는 레빈의 영혼 속에서 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지 알고 있었다.\n\n\"언젠가 가겠네.\" 그가 말했다. \"한데 말이야, 여자란 모든 게 그걸 축으로 돌아가는 그런 거야. 나는 형편이 안 좋아, 아주 안 좋지. 그게 전부 여자 때문이고. 자, 이제 솔직하게 말해 봐.\" 그는 시가를 집어 들고 한 손은 잔에 얹은 채 말을 이었다. \"나한테 조언 좀 해 줘.\"\n\n\"뭔데, 무슨 일인데?\"\n\n\"말해 줄게. 자네가 결혼을 했다고 치자. 아내를 사랑해. 그런데 다른 여자한테 마음을 빼앗긴 거야…….\"\n\n\"미안하지만, 난 도무지 이해가 안 돼, 어떻게…… 그건 마치, 지금 저녁을 먹고 나서 곧장 빵집에 가 둥근 빵을 훔칠 수 있다는 걸 이해 못 하는 것과 같아.\"\n\n스테판 아르카지치의 눈이 평소보다 더 반짝였다.\n\n\"왜 안 돼? 빵이란 게 때로 어찌나 좋은 냄새를 풍기는지, 도무지 못 참을 때가 있다고.\"\n\n\"내가 나의 지상의 욕망을 다스렸을 때, 그건 천상의 기쁨이라.\n\n그러나 만약 다스리지 못했다 해도, 나 또한 제법 멋진 즐거움을 누렸으리!\"\n\n이렇게 말하면서 스테판 아르카지치는 은근한 미소를 지었다. 레빈도 웃지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n* 독일어로 된 시. 하인리히 하이네의 풍자시에서 인용된 구절로 알려져 있다.\n\"그래, 하지만 농담은 접어 두고.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 다시 말을 이었다. \"자넨 이걸 알아야 해. 그 여자는 다정하고, 온화하고, 사랑이 넘치는 존재이고, 가난하고 외로운 데다, 모든 걸 희생했단 말이야. 자, 일이 이미 벌어진 마당에, 알겠나, 어떻게 그 여자를 내칠 수가 있겠어? 설령 자기 가정을 깨뜨리지 않으려고 헤어진다 쳐도, 그래도 그 여자를 가엾이 여기고, 두 발로 서게 해 주고, 그 처지를 좀 누그러뜨려 주지 않을 수가 있겠나?\"\n\n\"글쎄, 그 점에선 날 좀 봐줘야겠어. 자네도 알다시피 나한테 여자란 두 부류로 나뉘거든……. 아니, 적어도…… 더 정확히 말하면, 여자가 있고, 그리고…… 또…… 나는 매혹적인 타락한 존재라는 걸 본 적도 없고, 앞으로도 못 볼 거야. 한데 저 계산대에 있는, 곱슬머리에 분칠한 프랑스 여자 같은 것들은 내가 보기엔 해충이야. 타락한 여자들은 다 마찬가지지.\"\n\n\"하지만 막달라 마리아는?\"\n\n\"아, 그만둬! 그리스도께서 그 말씀이 어떻게 악용될지 아셨다면, 결코 그런 말씀을 하지 않으셨을 거야. 복음서를 통틀어, 사람들이 기억하는 건 그 말씀뿐이지. 어쨌든 난 내가 생각하는 걸 말하는 게 아니라, 내가 느끼는 걸 말하는 거야. 난 타락한 여자들이 혐오스러워. 자넨 거미를 무서워하고, 난 그런 해충을 무서워하는 거지. 자넨 십중팔구 거미를 연구해 본 적이 없어서 그 본성을 모를 거야. 나도 그렇다고.\"\n\n\"자네야 그렇게 말하면 그만이지. 그건 디킨스에 나오는 그 신사하고 아주 비슷해. 어려운 질문은 죄다 자기 오른쪽 어깨 너머로 휙 던져 버리던 그 사람 말이야. 하지만 사실을 부정하는 건 답이 못 돼. 그럼 어떻게 해야 하지—자네가 말해 봐, 어떻게 해야 하느냐고? 아내는 나이를 먹어 가는데, 자넨 생기가 넘쳐. 미처 둘러볼 새도 없이, 아무리 아내를 존중한다 해도 더는 사랑으로 그 사람을 사랑할 수 없다는 걸 느끼게 돼. 그러다 별안간 사랑이 나타나면, 자넨 끝장나는 거야, 끝장나는 거라고.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 지친 절망감으로 말했다.\n\n레빈은 살짝 미소를 지었다.\n\n\"그래, 끝장나는 거지.\" 오블론스키가 다시 말했다. \"하지만 어떻게 해야 하느냐고?\"\n\n\"빵을 훔치지 마.\"\n\n스테판 아르카지치는 소리 내어 웃음을 터뜨렸다.\n\n\"오, 도덕군자 나셨네! 하지만 자넨 이걸 알아야 해, 두 여자가 있어. 하나는 오직 자기 권리만 주장하는데, 그 권리란 게 바로 자네의 사랑이고, 자넨 그걸 줄 수가 없어. 다른 하나는 자넬 위해 모든 걸 희생하면서도 아무것도 바라지 않아. 그럼 어떻게 해야 하지? 어떻게 처신해야 하느냐고? 거기엔 무시무시한 비극이 있단 말이야.\"\n\n\"그 문제에 관해서 내 신앙 고백이 궁금하다면, 말해 주지. 난 거기에 무슨 비극이 있었다고 믿지 않아. 그리고 그 까닭은 이래. 내가 보기에 사랑이란…… 자네도 기억하겠지만, 플라톤이 『향연』에서 정의한 그 두 종류의 사랑 말이야, 그건 사람을 가려내는 시금석 노릇을 해. 어떤 사람은 한 종류만 이해하고, 또 어떤 사람은 다른 한 종류만 이해하지. 그리고 비(非)플라토닉한 사랑만 아는 사람들은 비극을 운운할 필요가 없어. 그런 사랑엔 어떤 종류의 비극도 있을 수 없으니까. '즐겁게 해 주셔서 정말 고맙습니다, 그럼 이만 실례합니다'—그게 비극의 전부야. 그리고 플라토닉한 사랑에도 비극이란 있을 수 없어. 왜냐하면 그 사랑 안에서는 모든 게 맑고 순수하니까, 왜냐하면…….\"\n\n바로 그 순간 레빈은 자신의 죄와, 자기가 겪어 온 내면의 갈등을 떠올렸다. 그리고 뜻밖에도 이렇게 덧붙였다.\n\n\"하지만 어쩌면 자네 말이 옳을지도 몰라. 충분히 그럴 수 있지…… 모르겠어, 나도 모르겠어.\"\n\n\"이건 말이지, 알겠나.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 말했다. \"자넨 한 덩어리로 똘똘 뭉친 사람이야. 그게 자네의 강점이자 약점이지. 자넨 한 덩어리로 된 성격을 지녀서, 인생 전체도 한 덩어리로 되어 있길 바라는데—세상은 그렇게 돌아가질 않아. 자넨 공직 업무를 멸시하지. 현실이라는 게 늘 한결같이 목적에 딱 들어맞기를 바라니까—그런데 세상은 그렇지가 않거든. 자넨 사람의 일도 늘 분명한 목적을 가져야 하고, 사랑과 가정생활도 늘 하나로 나뉘지 않은 채여야 한다고 바라지만—세상은 그렇지가 않아. 인생의 온갖 다채로움, 온갖 매력, 온갖 아름다움은 빛과 그림자로 이루어지는 거야.\"\n\n레빈은 한숨을 쉬고 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았다. 그는 자기 자신의 일을 생각하고 있었고, 오블론스키의 말은 귀에 들어오지 않았다.\n\n그리고 문득 두 사람은, 자기들이 친구 사이이고, 함께 저녁을 먹고 술을 마셨으니 응당 더 가까워졌어야 하는데도, 저마다 오직 제 일만 생각하고 있을 뿐 서로 아무 상관이 없다는 것을 느꼈다. 오블론스키는 만찬 뒤에 친밀감 대신 이런 지독한 거리감이 밀려드는 것을 여러 번 겪어 본 터라, 그럴 때 어떻게 해야 하는지 알고 있었다.\n\n\"계산서!\" 그는 이렇게 외치고는 옆방으로 갔다. 거기서 곧 안면이 있는 시종무관과 마주쳐, 어느 여배우와 그 후원자에 관한 이야기에 빠져들었다. 그리고 그 시종무관과 이야기를 나누자마자 오블론스키는, 늘 정신적으로나 영혼으로나 너무 큰 긴장을 강요하던 레빈과의 대화 뒤에 찾아온 느슨함과 안도감을 맛보았다.\n\n타타르인이 자기 몫의 팁 말고도 26루블 몇 코페이카짜리 계산서를 들고 나타났을 때, 평소 같으면 시골 사람이 다 그렇듯 자기 몫인 14루블에 기겁했을 레빈은, 그것을 알아차리지도 못한 채 값을 치르고는, 옷을 갈아입고 셰르바츠키 댁으로 가서 자신의 운명을 결정짓기 위해 집으로 향했다.\n\n# Chapter 12\n\n젊은 공작 영애 키티 셰르바츠카야는 열여덟 살이었다. 그녀가 사교계에 발을 들인 것은 이번 겨울이 처음이었다. 사교계에서 거둔 성공은 두 언니 그 누구보다도 컸으며, 심지어 어머니가 기대했던 것보다도 더 컸다. 모스크바 무도회에서 춤추던 젊은 남자들이 거의 모두 키티에게 반해 있었다는 것은 말할 것도 없고, 이 첫 겨울에 벌써 진지한 구혼자가 둘이나 나타났다. 레빈, 그리고 그가 떠나자마자 등장한 브론스키 백작이었다.\n\n겨울 초입에 레빈이 나타나, 자주 찾아오며 키티를 향한 사랑을 분명히 드러내자, 키티의 부모는 처음으로 딸의 장래에 관해 진지하게 이야기를 나누게 되었고, 그러다 둘 사이에 다툼이 일었다. 공작은 레빈의 편이었다. 그는 키티에게 그보다 더 나은 상대를 바랄 게 없다고 말했다. 한편 공작 부인은 여자 특유의 방식으로 문제를 빙빙 돌려 가며, 키티가 너무 어리다느니, 레빈이 진지한 뜻을 품었다는 것을 입증할 만한 일을 하나도 한 게 없다느니, 키티가 그에게 별다른 호감을 느끼지 못한다느니 하는 이런저런 곁가지 이유를 늘어놓았다. 그러나 정작 핵심은 입에 올리지 않았으니, 그것은 그녀가 딸에게 더 좋은 혼처를 바라고 있으며, 레빈은 자기 마음에 들지 않고 또 그를 도무지 이해할 수 없다는 점이었다. 레빈이 돌연 떠나 버리자 공작 부인은 기뻐하며 의기양양하게 남편에게 말했다. \"거 봐요, 내 말이 맞았잖아요.\" 브론스키가 등장하자 그녀는 더더욱 기뻐하며, 키티가 그저 좋은 정도가 아니라 눈부신 혼인을 하게 되리라는 자신의 견해를 굳혔다.\n\n어머니의 눈에 브론스키와 레빈은 비교할 거리가 못 되었다. 그녀는 레빈에게서 그의 괴상하고 타협을 모르는 견해며, 사교계에서의 수줍음을 못마땅해했는데, 그 수줍음은 그녀가 보기에 그의 자존심과, 가축이며 농민들에 파묻혀 사는—그녀가 보기에는—기이한 삶의 방식에서 비롯된 것이었다. 그녀는 그가 딸을 사랑하면서도 마치 무언가를 기다리기라도 하듯, 청혼을 하면 자기들에게 너무 큰 영광을 베푸는 게 아닌가 두려워하기라도 하듯 살피며 여섯 주 동안이나 집을 드나든 것이 그리 마음에 들지 않았고, 미혼의 처녀가 있는 집을 끊임없이 찾아오는 남자라면 자기 뜻을 분명히 밝혀야 마땅하다는 것을 그가 깨닫지 못하는 것도 못마땅했다. 그런데 그는 그렇게 하지도 않은 채 별안간 종적을 감춰 버린 것이다. \"키티가 반할 만큼 매력적이지 않았던 게 차라리 다행이지.\" 어머니는 그렇게 생각했다.\n\n브론스키는 어머니의 온갖 바람을 만족시켜 주었다. 대단한 부자에다, 영리하고, 귀족 가문 출신이며, 군에서나 궁정에서나 눈부신 출셋길로 나아가고 있었고, 게다가 매력적인 남자였다. 더 바랄 게 없었다.\n\n브론스키는 무도회에서 키티에게 드러내 놓고 추파를 던지며 함께 춤을 추었고 끊임없이 집을 찾아왔으니, 그의 의도가 진지하다는 것은 의심할 여지가 없었다. 그러나 그럼에도 어머니는 그 겨울 내내 끔찍한 불안과 동요 속에서 지냈다.\n\n셰르바츠카야 공작 부인 자신이 결혼한 것은 삼십 년 전 일로, 그녀의 이모가 혼사를 주선했다. 모든 것이 미리 소상히 알려진 남편이 찾아와, 미래의 신부를 보았고, 또 신부의 눈에 비쳤다. 중매를 맡은 이모가 두 사람의 상호 인상을 확인하여 전해 주었다. 그 인상은 호의적이었다. 그 후 미리 정해 둔 날에 기다리던 청혼이 부모에게 전해졌고, 받아들여졌다. 모든 것이 아주 간단하고 수월하게 흘러갔다. 적어도 공작 부인에게는 그렇게 여겨졌다. 그러나 자기 딸들 차례가 되자, 겉보기에는 그토록 흔해 빠진 일인 딸을 시집보내는 일이 얼마나 간단하지도 수월하지도 않은 일인지 절감하게 되었다. 큰딸 둘, 다리아와 나탈리아를 시집보내며 견뎌 낸 그 숱한 공포며, 골똘히 곱씹던 그 온갖 생각이며, 허비한 돈이며, 남편과 벌인 다툼이라니! 이제 막내딸이 사교계에 나오자 그녀는 똑같은 두려움과 똑같은 의혹을, 게다가 언니들 때보다 한층 더 격렬한 부부 싸움을 다시 겪고 있었다. 노공작은 사실 모든 아버지가 그렇듯 딸들의 명예와 평판 문제에 지나치리만큼 까다로웠다. 그는 딸들을, 특히 그가 가장 아끼던 키티를 두고 분별없이 질투했다. 그는 사사건건 딸의 체면을 손상시킨다며 공작 부인과 한바탕씩 했다. 다른 딸들 때부터 이미 이런 일에 익숙해진 공작 부인이었지만, 이제는 공작이 신경을 곤두세우는 데에 더 그럴 만한 까닭이 있다고 느꼈다. 그녀는 최근 몇 년 사이 사교계의 풍속이 많이 달라졌으며, 어머니의 의무가 한층 더 어려워졌다는 것을 알았다. 키티 또래의 처녀들이 무슨 모임 같은 것을 만들고, 무슨 강연 같은 데를 다니며, 남자들과 거리낌 없이 어울리고, 혼자서 거리를 마차로 돌아다니는가 하면, 그중 다수가 무릎을 굽혀 인사도 하지 않았다. 그리고 무엇보다 중요한 것은, 처녀들이 하나같이 남편을 고르는 일은 부모가 아니라 자기들 소관이라고 굳게 믿고 있다는 점이었다. \"요즘은 결혼이 옛날처럼 이루어지지 않아요.\" 이 젊은 처녀들 모두가, 심지어 그들의 윗사람들까지도 그렇게 생각하고 또 말했다. 그러나 지금은 결혼이 어떻게 이루어지는지, 공작 부인은 누구에게서도 알아낼 수가 없었다. 부모가 자식의 장래를 정해 주는 프랑스식은 받아들여지지 않았고, 오히려 비난받았다. 처녀들의 완전한 독립이라는 영국식 또한 받아들여지지 않았고, 러시아 사교계에서는 가능하지도 않았다. 중간에 사람을 두어 혼사를 맺는 러시아식은 어찌 된 일인지 점잖지 못한 것으로 여겨졌으니, 모든 사람이, 또 공작 부인 자신도 그것을 비웃었다. 그러나 처녀들을 어떻게 시집보내야 하는지, 부모가 그들을 어떻게 결혼시켜야 하는지는 아무도 알지 못했다. 공작 부인이 어쩌다 이 문제를 의논하게 된 사람마다 똑같은 말을 했다. \"이런, 세상에, 우리 시대에는 그 케케묵은 일일랑 다 던져 버릴 때도 됐지요. 결혼은 젊은 사람들이 하는 거지 부모가 하는 게 아니니까요. 그러니 젊은 사람들이 알아서 정하도록 내버려 둬야지요.\" 딸이 없는 사람이야 그렇게 말하기가 매우 쉬웠지만, 공작 부인은 서로 알아 가는 과정에서 딸이 사랑에 빠질 수도 있으며, 그것도 딸과 결혼할 마음이 없거나 도무지 남편감으로는 어울리지 않는 사람에게 빠질 수도 있다는 것을 잘 알았다. 그리고 요즘 같은 시대에는 젊은이들이 자기 삶을 스스로 꾸려야 한다는 말이 아무리 공작 부인의 머리에 주입되어도, 그녀는 그것을 믿을 수가 없었다. 마치 어느 시대를 막론하고 다섯 살배기 아이에게 가장 알맞은 장난감이 장전된 권총이라는 말을 믿을 수 없었던 것과 똑같이. 그래서 공작 부인은 언니들 때보다 키티를 두고 더 불안해했다.\n\n이제 그녀는 브론스키가 딸에게 그저 추파를 던지는 데서 그치지나 않을까 두려웠다. 그녀는 딸이 그를 사랑하고 있음을 알았지만, 그가 명예를 아는 사람이니 그런 짓은 하지 않으리라는 생각으로 스스로를 위로하려 했다. 그러나 동시에 그녀는 오늘날의 자유로운 풍속 속에서 처녀의 마음을 흔들어 놓기가 얼마나 쉬운지, 또 남자들이 흔히 그런 죄를 얼마나 가볍게 여기는지도 알았다. 일주일 전에 키티는 마주르카를 추는 동안 브론스키와 나눈 대화를 어머니에게 들려준 적이 있었다. 그 대화는 공작 부인을 어느 정도 안심시켜 주었지만, 그렇다고 완전히 마음을 놓을 수는 없었다. 브론스키는 키티에게, 자기와 형은 어머니에게 순종하는 데 워낙 익숙해서 어떤 중요한 일도 어머니와 상의하지 않고는 결심한 적이 없다고 말했던 것이다. \"그래서 지금 저는 어머니가 페테르부르크에서 오시기를, 유독 다행스러운 일로 여기며 애타게 기다리고 있답니다.\" 그는 그녀에게 그렇게 말했다.\n\n키티는 그 말에 아무 의미도 두지 않고 그대로 옮겼다. 그러나 어머니는 그것을 다른 시각으로 보았다. 그녀는 노부인이 하루하루 오기를 기다리던 참이며, 부인이 아들의 선택을 기뻐하리라는 것을 알았기에, 그가 어머니의 심기를 거스를까 두려워 청혼을 하지 못한다는 게 이상하게 느껴졌다. 하지만 그녀는 그 결혼 자체를, 그리고 그보다 더 자신의 두려움에서 벗어나기를 너무도 간절히 바란 나머지, 정말 그러하리라고 믿어 버렸다. 남편을 떠나려는 지경에 이른 큰딸 돌리의 불행을 지켜보는 일이 공작 부인에게 아무리 쓰라렸어도, 막내딸의 운명이 어떻게 결정되느냐에 대한 불안이 그녀의 온 마음을 사로잡고 있었다. 오늘 레빈이 다시 나타나면서 새로운 불안의 씨앗이 돋아났다. 그녀는 한때, 그녀 생각으로는, 레빈에게 마음이 있었던 딸이 지나친 명예심 때문에 브론스키를 거절하지나 않을까, 또 레빈의 등장이 거의 매듭지어진 일을 전반적으로 복잡하게 만들고 지연시키지나 않을까 두려웠다.\n\n\"그래, 그 사람 온 지 오래됐니?\" 집으로 돌아오는 길에 공작 부인이 레빈에 관해 물었다.\n\n\"오늘 왔어요, 어머니.\"\n\n\"한 가지 말해 두고 싶은 게…….\" 공작 부인이 입을 떼자, 진지하고 빈틈없는 어머니의 얼굴에서 키티는 그것이 무슨 말일지 짐작했다.\n\n\"어머니,\" 그녀는 얼굴이 화끈 달아오르며 어머니에게 휙 돌아서서 말했다. \"제발, 제발 그 이야기는 아무 말도 마세요. 알아요, 다 알고 있어요.\"\n\n그녀는 어머니가 바라는 것을 똑같이 바랐지만, 어머니가 그것을 바라는 동기가 그녀의 마음에 상처를 주었다.\n\n\"그저 괜한 희망을 품게 하는 건…….\"\n\n\"어머니, 제발요, 부탁이에요, 그 얘기는 하지 마세요. 그런 얘기를 입에 올리는 게 너무 끔찍해요.\"\n\n\"안 할게.\" 어머니는 딸의 눈에 맺힌 눈물을 보고 말했다. \"하지만 한 가지만, 얘야. 너 나한테 아무것도 숨기지 않겠다고 약속했지. 안 그러겠지?\"\n\n\"절대로요, 어머니, 아무것도요.\" 키티는 살짝 얼굴을 붉히며 어머니의 얼굴을 똑바로 바라보고 대답했다. \"하지만 어머니께 말씀드릴 게 아무것도 없어요. 그리고 저는…… 저는…… 설령 말하고 싶어도, 뭐라고 해야 할지, 또 어떻게 해야 할지 모르겠어요…… 정말 모르겠어요…….\"\n\n'아니, 저 눈으로 거짓말을 할 수는 없지.' 어머니는 딸의 동요와 행복을 보며 미소 지으면서 생각했다. 공작 부인은 지금 이 가엾은 아이의 마음속에서 벌어지고 있는 일이 그 아이에게는 그토록 엄청나고 그토록 중대하게 여겨진다는 것에 미소를 지었다.\n\n# Chapter 13\n\n만찬이 끝나고 저녁이 시작될 때까지, 키티는 전투를 앞둔 젊은 병사가 느낄 법한 감각과 비슷한 것을 느꼈다. 심장이 격렬하게 두근거렸고, 어떤 것에도 생각이 머물러 있지 못했다.\n\n그녀는 두 사람을 처음으로 한자리에서 만나게 될 오늘 저녁이 자기 인생의 전환점이 되리라고 느꼈다. 그리고 그녀는 끊임없이 두 사람을 마음속에 그려 보았다. 어느 순간엔 각자 따로따로, 그러다 또 둘을 함께. 지난날을 떠올릴 때면, 그녀는 즐겁게, 다정하게, 레빈과의 관계에 얽힌 기억들에 잠겼다. 어린 시절의 추억이며, 레빈과 죽은 오빠 사이의 우정은 그와의 관계에 특별한 시적 정취를 더해 주었다. 그녀가 확신하고 있는 그의 사랑은 그녀에게 흐뭇하고 기쁜 것이었다. 레빈을 생각하는 일은 그녀에게 즐거웠다. 그런데 브론스키에 대한 기억에는 늘 어떤 어색함이 끼어들었다. 그가 더없이 교양 있고 침착한 사람이었는데도, 마치 어딘가 거짓된 음이 섞여 있는 것 같았다. 브론스키에게 있는 것이 아니라—그는 무척 소박하고 친절했다—그녀 자신 안에 있는 것이었다. 반면 레빈과 함께 있을 때 그녀는 더없이 소박하고 또렷한 자신을 느꼈다. 그러나 또 한편으로, 브론스키와의 미래를 생각하면 그녀 앞에는 눈부신 행복의 전경이 펼쳐졌다. 레빈과의 미래는 안개에 싸인 듯했다.\n\n옷을 갈아입으러 위층으로 올라가 거울을 들여다본 그녀는, 오늘이 자기 모습이 좋은 날 가운데 하나이며, 자신의 모든 힘을 온전히 갖추고 있다는 것을 기쁘게 알아챘다. 앞에 놓인 일을 위해 이런 상태가 그토록 필요했던 것이다. 그녀는 겉으로 드러나는 차분함과 거침없이 우아한 몸놀림을 의식하고 있었다.\n\n일곱 시 반에 응접실로 막 내려간 그녀에게, 하인이 \"콘스탄틴 드미트리예비치 레빈\"이라고 알렸다. 공작 부인은 아직 자기 방에 있었고, 공작은 아직 들어오지 않았다. '드디어 올 것이 왔구나.' 키티는 생각했고, 온몸의 피가 심장으로 쏠리는 것 같았다. 거울을 흘끗 들여다본 그녀는 자기 얼굴이 핼쑥한 데에 소스라쳤다. 그 순간 그녀는, 그가 자기를 혼자 만나서 청혼하려고 일부러 일찍 왔다는 것을 의심할 여지 없이 알아차렸다. 그리고 바로 그때서야 비로소 이 모든 일이 새롭고 다른 면모로 그녀 앞에 떠올랐다. 그제야 그녀는 이 문제가 자기에게만, 그러니까 누구와 함께 행복할지, 누구를 사랑하는지의 문제로만 그치지 않으며, 자기가 좋아하는 한 남자에게 이 순간 상처를 주어야 한다는 것을 깨달았다. 그것도 잔인하게 상처를 주어야 한다는 것을. 무엇 때문에? 그 사람이, 그 다정한 사람이 그녀를 사랑하기 때문에, 그녀에게 마음을 빼앗겼기 때문에. 하지만 어쩔 도리가 없었고, 그래야만 했으며, 그렇게 될 수밖에 없었다.\n\n'맙소사! 내가 정말 직접 그 사람한테 말해야 하는 거야?' 그녀는 생각했다. '당신을 사랑하지 않는다고 그 사람한테 말할 수 있을까? 그건 거짓말이지. 대체 뭐라고 말해야 하지? 다른 사람을 사랑한다고? 아니, 그건 안 돼. 가야겠어, 가야겠어.'\n\n그녀가 문 앞에 이르렀을 때, 그의 발소리가 들렸다. '안 돼! 이건 정직하지 못해. 내가 뭘 두려워하는 거지? 난 잘못한 게 없어. 될 일은 되겠지! 사실대로 말하자. 그리고 저 사람 앞에서는 거북해할 까닭이 없어. 저기 오는구나.' 그녀는 빛나는 눈으로 자기를 응시하는, 다부지고 수줍은 그의 모습을 보며 혼잣말을 했다. 그녀는 부디 자기를 봐 달라고 애원하듯 그의 얼굴을 똑바로 들여다보며 손을 내밀었다.\n\n\"아직 시간이 안 됐죠. 제가 너무 일찍 온 것 같네요.\" 그가 텅 빈 응접실을 둘러보며 말했다. 자기 기대가 들어맞았다는 것을, 그러니까 말을 꺼내는 데에 아무런 방해도 없다는 것을 알아챘을 때, 그의 얼굴이 어두워졌다.\n\n\"아, 아니에요.\" 키티가 말하며 탁자 앞에 앉았다.\n\n\"하지만 저는 바로 이걸, 당신을 혼자 만나기를 바랐어요.\" 그는 용기를 잃지 않으려고 앉지도 않고, 그녀를 보지도 않은 채 말을 꺼냈다.\n\n\"엄마가 곧 내려오실 거예요. 너무 피곤해하셔서…… 어제……\"\n\n그녀는 자기 입에서 무슨 말이 나오는지도 모른 채 계속 지껄였고, 애원하듯 어루만지는 듯한 눈길을 그에게서 떼지 못했다.\n\n그가 그녀를 흘끗 보았다. 그녀는 얼굴을 붉히며 말을 멈췄다.\n\n\"제가 여기 오래 머물지 어떨지 모르겠다고 말씀드렸죠…… 그게 당신에게 달려 있다고……\"\n\n그녀는 다가오는 말에 자기가 무슨 대답을 해야 할지도 모른 채, 점점 더 고개를 떨구었다.\n\n\"그게 당신에게 달려 있다고요.\" 그가 되풀이했다. \"제가 하려던 말은…… 제가 하려던 말은…… 저는 이걸 위해 왔어요…… 제 아내가 되어 달라고요!\" 그는 자기가 무슨 말을 하는지도 모른 채 내뱉었다. 하지만 가장 무서운 말은 다 했다고 느끼며 입을 다물고 그녀를 바라보았다.\n\n그녀는 그를 보지 않은 채 가쁘게 숨을 쉬고 있었다. 그녀는 황홀감을 느끼고 있었다. 그녀의 영혼은 행복으로 흘러넘쳤다. 사랑의 고백이 자기에게 이토록 강렬한 작용을 일으키리라고는 결코 예상하지 못했다. 하지만 그것은 한순간뿐이었다. 그녀는 브론스키를 떠올렸다. 그녀는 맑고 진실한 눈을 들어, 절망에 찬 그의 얼굴을 보고는 황급히 대답했다.\n\n\"그건 안 돼요…… 용서하세요.\"\n\n조금 전만 해도 그녀는 그에게 얼마나 가까웠던가, 그의 삶에서 얼마나 소중했던가! 그런데 지금 그녀는 그에게서 얼마나 멀어지고 동떨어진 존재가 되어 버렸는가!\n\n\"그렇게 될 수밖에 없었던 거죠.\" 그가 그녀를 보지 않은 채 말했다.\n\n그는 고개를 숙이고는 물러나려 했다.\n\n# Chapter 14\n\n바로 그 순간 공작 부인이 들어왔다. 둘만 있는 모습과 흐트러진 두 사람의 얼굴을 본 그녀의 얼굴에는 공포의 빛이 어렸다. 레빈은 그녀에게 인사를 하고는 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 키티도 입을 열지 않았고 눈을 들지도 않았다. '다행이야, 거절했구나.' 어머니는 그렇게 생각했고, 그녀의 얼굴은 목요일마다 손님들을 맞을 때 짓던 그 익숙한 미소로 환하게 밝아졌다. 그녀는 자리에 앉아 레빈에게 시골 생활에 관해 이것저것 묻기 시작했다. 그는 눈에 띄지 않게 물러날 작정으로, 다른 손님들이 올 때까지 기다리며 다시 앉았다.\n\n오 분쯤 지나자 지난겨울에 결혼한 키티의 친구, 노르드스톤 백작 부인이 들어왔다.\n\n그녀는 야위고 누런 안색에 병약하고 신경질적인 여자였으며, 반짝이는 검은 눈을 가지고 있었다. 그녀는 키티를 좋아했고, 결혼한 여자들이 처녀들에게 보이는 애정이 늘 그렇듯이, 그녀의 애정 역시 자신이 그리는 결혼 행복의 이상에 맞추어 키티의 짝을 지어 주고 싶은 욕망으로 드러났다. 그녀는 키티가 브론스키와 결혼하기를 바랐다. 레빈은 초겨울에 쉬체르바츠키 집안에서 자주 마주쳤는데, 그녀는 늘 그를 싫어했다. 그와 마주칠 때면 그녀가 변함없이 즐겨 하던 일은 그를 놀려 대는 것이었다.\n\n\"나는 그 사람이 자기 위대함의 높이에서 나를 내려다볼 때나, 내가 바보라서 학식 있는 대화를 도중에 끊어 버릴 때나, 나한테 잘난 척 인심 쓰듯 굴 때가 정말 좋아요. 그게 그렇게 좋다니까요. 그 사람이 잘난 척하는 꼴을 보는 게 말예요! 그가 나를 못 견뎌 하니 얼마나 기쁜지 몰라요.\" 그녀는 그에 관해 그렇게 말하곤 했다.\n\n그녀가 옳았다. 레빈은 실제로 그녀를 견딜 수 없었고, 그녀가 자랑스러워하며 훌륭한 특징이라고 여기는 것들—그녀의 신경질, 거칠고 세속적인 모든 것에 대한 그녀의 섬세한 경멸과 무관심—때문에 그녀를 경멸했다.\n\n노르드스톤 백작 부인과 레빈은 사교계에서 드물지 않게 보이는 그런 관계, 곧 겉으로는 우호적인 사이를 유지하는 두 사람이 서로를 너무나도 경멸한 나머지 상대를 진지하게 대할 수조차 없고 서로에게 화를 낼 수조차 없는 그런 관계에 빠져 있었다.\n\n노르드스톤 백작 부인은 곧장 레빈에게 달려들었다.\n\n\"아, 콘스탄틴 드미트리치! 이렇게 우리 타락한 바빌론으로 돌아오셨군요.\" 그녀는 작고 누런 손을 그에게 내밀며, 그가 초겨울에 모스크바는 바빌론이라고 우연히 했던 말을 떠올리게 하며 말했다. \"어디, 바빌론이 개혁되었나요, 아니면 당신이 타락한 건가요?\" 그녀는 키티를 향해 짐짓 웃는 얼굴로 흘끗 보며 덧붙였다.\n\n\"제 말을 그렇게 잘 기억해 주시다니 저로서는 무척 영광입니다, 백작 부인.\" 어느새 평정을 되찾은 레빈이 대답했고, 곧 습관처럼 노르드스톤 백작 부인을 향한 농담 섞인 적대의 어조로 돌아갔다. \"분명 제 말이 부인께 깊은 인상을 남긴 모양이군요.\"\n\n\"오, 그럼요! 저는 늘 그걸 빠짐없이 기록해 둔답니다. 그런데 키티, 또 스케이트 타러 갔었니?……\"\n\n그리고 그녀는 키티와 이야기를 나누기 시작했다. 레빈이 지금 자리를 뜨는 것이 거북한 일이긴 했지만, 그래도 저녁 내내 남아서 이따금 자기를 흘끗 보다가 눈길을 피하는 키티를 보고 있느니 차라리 그 거북한 짓을 저질러 버리는 편이 더 쉬웠을 것이다. 그가 막 일어서려는 참에, 그가 잠자코 있는 것을 알아챈 공작 부인이 그에게 말을 건넸다.\n\n\"모스크바에 오래 계실 건가요? 그래도 군 의회 일로 바쁘셔서 오래 떠나 계실 수는 없겠죠?\"\n\n\"아닙니다, 공작 부인, 저는 더 이상 의회 위원이 아닙니다.\" 그가 말했다. \"며칠 일정으로 올라왔습니다.\"\n\n'저 사람한테 무슨 일이 있나 봐.' 노르드스톤 백작 부인은 그의 굳고 진지한 얼굴을 흘끗 보며 생각했다. '예전의 그 따지기 좋아하던 기분이 아니네. 하지만 내가 끌어내 줘야지. 키티 앞에서 저 사람을 바보로 만드는 게 얼마나 좋은지 몰라. 꼭 그렇게 하고 말겠어.'\n\n\"콘스탄틴 드미트리치.\" 그녀가 그에게 말했다. \"제발 좀 설명해 주세요, 이게 무슨 뜻인지요. 당신은 그런 일을 다 아시잖아요. 우리 칼루가 시골 마을에서는 농민들도 아낙네들도 가진 걸 죄다 술로 탕진해 버려서, 이제 우리한테 소작료를 한 푼도 못 낸답니다. 그게 무슨 뜻일까요? 당신은 늘 농민들을 그렇게 칭찬하시잖아요.\"\n\n바로 그때 또 다른 부인이 방으로 들어왔고, 레빈은 일어섰다.\n\n\"죄송합니다만, 백작 부인, 저는 정말 그 일에 대해 아무것도 모르고, 아무 말씀도 드릴 수가 없습니다.\" 그가 말하며, 그 부인의 뒤를 따라 들어온 장교를 돌아보았다.\n\n'저 사람이 브론스키구나.' 레빈은 생각했고, 그것을 확인하려고 키티를 흘끗 보았다. 그녀는 이미 브론스키를 바라본 뒤였고, 다시 레빈을 돌아보았다. 그리고 무의식중에 더 환해진 그녀의 눈빛만으로, 레빈은 그녀가 저 사람을 사랑한다는 것을 알았다. 그녀가 말로 그렇게 일러 준 것처럼 확실하게 알았다. 하지만 그는 어떤 사람일까? 이제 좋든 나쁘든 레빈은 남아 있을 수밖에 없었다. 그녀가 사랑하는 그 사람이 어떤 인물인지 알아내야만 했다.\n\n성공한 연적을—그것이 어떤 분야에서든—만나면 곧장 그 사람의 좋은 점은 모조리 외면하고 나쁜 점만 보려 드는 부류의 사람들이 있다. 반면에 무엇보다도 그 운 좋은 연적에게서 자신을 앞지르게 한 바로 그 자질들을 찾아내려 하고, 가슴이 아릿하게 욱신거리면서도 오로지 좋은 점만을 찾는 부류의 사람들도 있다. 레빈은 두 번째 부류에 속했다. 그런데 그는 브론스키에게서 좋고 매력적인 점을 찾는 데 아무런 어려움도 겪지 않았다. 그것은 첫눈에 분명히 드러났다. 브론스키는 다부진 체격에 검은 머리를 한, 그리 크지 않은 키의 남자였으며, 선량하고 잘생긴, 더없이 침착하고 단호한 얼굴을 하고 있었다. 짧게 깎은 검은 머리와 갓 면도한 턱부터 헐렁하게 맞는 새 군복에 이르기까지, 그의 얼굴과 풍채의 모든 것은 단순하면서도 동시에 우아했다. 브론스키는 들어온 부인에게 길을 비켜 주고 나서 공작 부인에게로, 이어서 키티에게로 다가갔다.\n\n그녀에게 다가가면서 그의 아름다운 눈은 유난히 다정한 빛으로 빛났고, 희미하면서도 행복하고 겸손하게 의기양양한 미소를 띤 채(레빈에게는 그렇게 보였다) 그녀에게 조심스럽고 정중하게 몸을 숙이며, 작고 넓은 손을 그녀에게 내밀었다.\n\n그는 모두에게 인사하고 몇 마디 말을 건넨 뒤, 자기에게서 한 번도 눈을 떼지 않던 레빈을 단 한 번도 쳐다보지 않은 채 자리에 앉았다.\n\n\"두 분을 소개하지요.\" 공작 부인이 레빈을 가리키며 말했다. \"콘스탄틴 드미트리치 레빈, 알렉세이 키릴로비치 브론스키 백작.\"\n\n브론스키는 일어나 레빈을 다정하게 바라보며 그와 악수했다.\n\n\"이번 겨울에 당신과 함께 식사하기로 되어 있었던 것 같은데요.\" 그가 소박하고 솔직한 미소를 지으며 말했다. \"그런데 당신이 뜻밖에 시골로 떠나 버리셨더군요.\"\n\n\"콘스탄틴 드미트리치는 도시와 우리 같은 도시 사람들을 경멸하고 미워한답니다.\" 노르드스톤 백작 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"제 말을 그렇게 잘 기억하시는 걸 보니 제 말이 부인께 깊은 인상을 남긴 게 틀림없군요.\" 레빈은 그렇게 말하고는, 문득 자기가 조금 전에도 똑같은 말을 했다는 것을 깨닫고 얼굴을 붉혔다.\n\n브론스키는 레빈과 노르드스톤 백작 부인을 보며 미소 지었다.\n\n\"항상 시골에 계시나요?\" 그가 물었다. \"겨울에는 따분할 것 같은데요.\"\n\n\"할 일이 있으면 따분하지 않습니다. 게다가 혼자 있어도 따분하지 않고요.\" 레빈이 퉁명스럽게 대꾸했다.\n\n\"저는 시골을 좋아합니다.\" 브론스키는 레빈의 어조를 알아채고도 짐짓 못 알아챈 척하며 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 백작, 늘 시골에서만 사는 것에는 동의하지 않으시겠죠.\" 노르드스톤 백작 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"모르겠습니다. 오래 살아 본 적이 없어서요. 한번은 이상한 기분을 느낀 적이 있습니다.\" 그가 말을 이었다. \"어머니와 함께 니스에서 겨울을 보낼 때만큼 시골이, 짚신과 농민들이 있는 러시아 시골이 그토록 그리웠던 적이 없었습니다. 아시다시피 니스 자체는 꽤 따분하지요. 사실 나폴리도 소렌토도 잠깐 동안만 즐거울 뿐입니다. 그리고 바로 거기에서야말로 러시아가, 특히 시골이 가장 생생하게 제게 되살아나더군요. 마치……\"\n\n그는 키티와 레빈 양쪽을 향해 이야기하며, 평온하고 다정한 눈길을 이 사람에서 저 사람으로 옮겼고, 머릿속에 떠오르는 대로 거리낌 없이 말하는 것이 분명했다.\n\n노르드스톤 백작 부인이 뭔가 말하고 싶어 하는 것을 알아채자, 그는 꺼낸 이야기를 끝맺지도 않고 뚝 멈춘 채 그녀의 말에 주의 깊게 귀를 기울였다.\n\n대화는 한순간도 늘어지지 않았으므로, 화제가 떨어질 경우를 대비해 늘 묵직한 대포 두 문—고전 교육과 현대 교육의 상대적 이점, 그리고 국민개병제—을 예비로 마련해 두던 공작 부인은 그중 어느 것도 꺼낼 필요가 없었고, 노르드스톤 백작 부인도 레빈을 놀려 댈 기회를 잡지 못했다.\n\n레빈은 다 함께 나누는 대화에 끼고 싶었지만 그러지 못했다. 매 순간 '자, 가자.' 하고 스스로에게 말하면서도, 무언가를 기다리기라도 하는 듯 여전히 자리를 뜨지 못했다.\n\n대화는 탁자 돌리기와 영혼에 관한 이야기로 흘러갔고, 심령술을 믿는 노르드스톤 백작 부인은 자기가 본 신기한 일들을 늘어놓기 시작했다.\n\n\"아, 백작 부인, 제발 저도 꼭 데려가 주세요, 부탁이니 그것들을 보러 데려가 주세요! 저는 어디서나 늘 신기한 것을 찾아 헤매는데도 여태 비범한 것이라곤 본 적이 없거든요.\" 브론스키가 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"좋아요, 다음 주 토요일에요.\" 노르드스톤 백작 부인이 대답했다. \"그런데 콘스탄틴 드미트리치, 당신은 그걸 믿으세요?\" 그녀가 레빈에게 물었다.\n\n\"왜 저한테 물으십니까? 제가 무슨 말을 할지 다 아시면서요.\"\n\n\"그래도 당신 의견을 듣고 싶어요.\"\n\n\"제 의견은 말이죠,\" 레빈이 대답했다. \"그저 이 탁자 돌리기라는 것이 소위 교양 있는 사회라는 게 농민들보다 조금도 나을 게 없다는 것을 증명할 뿐이라는 겁니다. 농민들은 흉안이며 마법이며 징조 같은 것을 믿는데, 우리는……\"\n\n\"오, 그럼 당신은 그걸 안 믿는군요?\"\n\n\"믿을 수가 없습니다, 백작 부인.\"\n\n\"하지만 제가 직접 봤다면요?\"\n\n\"농민 아낙네들도 도깨비를 봤다고들 하지요.\"\n\n\"그럼 제가 거짓말을 한다는 건가요?\"\n\n그러고는 그녀가 즐거움 없는 웃음을 웃었다.\n\n\"오, 아니에요, 마샤, 콘스탄틴 드미트리치는 그걸 믿을 수 없다고 말한 거예요.\" 키티가 레빈을 대신해 얼굴을 붉히며 말했고, 레빈은 이것을 보고 더욱 화가 나서 뭔가 대답하려 했지만, 브론스키가 그 밝고 솔직한 미소로 불쾌해질 위기에 놓인 대화를 구하러 끼어들었다.\n\n\"당신은 그것이 가능하다는 것 자체를 전혀 인정하지 않으십니까?\" 그가 물었다. \"하지만 왜죠? 우리는 아무것도 모르는 전기의 존재도 인정하지 않습니까. 어째서 우리가 아직 모르는 어떤 새로운 힘이 있어선 안 되는 거죠, 그것이……\"\n\n\"전기가 발견되었을 때,\" 레빈이 황급히 말을 잘랐다. \"발견된 것은 단지 현상뿐이었고, 그것이 무엇에서 비롯되며 그 작용이 무엇인지는 알려지지 않았으며, 그 응용 방법이 고안되기까지는 여러 세대가 흘렀습니다. 그런데 심령술사들은 탁자가 자기들에게 글을 써 주고 영혼이 자기들 앞에 나타나는 것에서 시작해 놓고는, 나중에야 그것이 미지의 힘이라고 말하기 시작한 겁니다.\"\n\n브론스키는 늘 그러듯이 그의 말에 분명 흥미를 느끼며 주의 깊게 레빈의 말에 귀를 기울였다.\n\n\"네, 하지만 심령술사들은 현재로서는 이 힘이 무엇인지 모르지만, 어쨌든 힘은 존재하며, 이것이 그 힘이 작용하는 조건이라고 말합니다. 그 힘이 무엇으로 이루어졌는지는 과학자들이 밝혀내게 하면 되지요. 아니, 저는 왜 새로운 힘이 있어선 안 되는지 모르겠습니다, 만약 그것이……\"\n\n\"왜냐하면, 전기의 경우에는,\" 레빈이 다시 말을 잘랐다. \"송진을 양모에 문지를 때마다 매번 확인된 현상이 나타나지만, 이 경우에는 그것이 매번 일어나지 않으니, 따라서 그것은 자연 현상이 아니라는 결론이 나오는 겁니다.\"\n\n대화가 응접실에서 나누기에는 너무 진지한 어조로 흐른다고 느꼈는지, 브론스키는 더 반박하지 않고, 화제를 바꿔 보려는 듯 밝게 미소 지으며 부인들 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸다.\n\n\"백작 부인, 우리 당장 한번 해 봐요.\" 그가 말했지만, 레빈은 자기 생각을 끝까지 말하려 했다.\n\n\"제 생각엔 말입니다,\" 그가 말을 이었다. \"심령술사들이 자기들의 신기한 일을 일종의 새로운 자연력으로 설명하려는 이 시도는 더없이 부질없는 짓입니다. 그들은 대담하게 영적인 힘을 운운하다가, 그것을 물질적인 실험에 종속시키려 드는 거죠.\"\n\n모두가 그의 말이 끝나기를 기다리고 있었고, 그는 그것을 느꼈다.\n\n\"제 생각엔 당신이야말로 일류 영매가 될 것 같아요.\" 노르드스톤 백작 부인이 말했다. \"당신에게는 뭔가 열정적인 데가 있거든요.\"\n\n레빈은 입을 열어 뭔가 말하려다가, 얼굴을 붉히고는 아무 말도 하지 않았다.\n\n\"제발, 당장 탁자 돌리기를 해 봐요.\" 브론스키가 말했다. \"공작 부인, 허락해 주시겠습니까?\"\n\n그러고는 브론스키가 자그마한 탁자를 찾으며 일어섰다.\n\n키티는 탁자를 가지러 일어났고, 지나가다가 그녀의 눈이 레빈의 눈과 마주쳤다. 그녀는 온 마음으로 그를 가엾게 여겼는데, 자기 자신이 그 원인인 괴로움 때문에 그를 가엾어하는 만큼 더욱 그러했다. '용서할 수 있다면, 저를 용서해 주세요.' 그녀의 눈이 말했다. '저는 정말 행복해요.'\n\n'나는 저들 모두가, 그리고 당신이, 또 나 자신이 밉습니다.' 그의 눈이 대답했고, 그는 모자를 집어 들었다. 하지만 그는 빠져나갈 운명이 아니었다. 사람들이 막 탁자 둘레로 자리를 잡고, 레빈이 막 물러나려는 참에, 노공작이 들어와 부인들에게 인사한 뒤 레빈에게 말을 건넸다.\n\n\"아!\" 그가 반갑게 입을 열었다. \"여기 온 지 오래됐나, 이 사람아? 자네가 도시에 와 있는 줄도 몰랐네. 보게 되어 아주 반갑구먼.\" 노공작은 레빈을 끌어안았고, 그와 이야기하느라, 일어서서 자기에게 말을 돌릴 때까지 차분히 기다리고 있던 브론스키를 알아채지 못했다.\n\n키티는 일어난 일이 있고 난 뒤 아버지의 다정함이 레빈에게 얼마나 불쾌할지를 느꼈다. 그녀는 또 아버지가 마침내 브론스키의 인사에 얼마나 냉랭하게 응했는지, 그리고 브론스키가 어떻게 그리고 왜 누군가가 자기에게 적대적일 수 있는지 이해하려다 이해하지 못하는 듯, 상냥하면서도 어리둥절한 표정으로 자기 아버지를 바라보는지도 보았고, 얼굴을 붉혔다.\n\n\"공작님, 콘스탄틴 드미트리치를 우리한테 양보해 주세요.\" 노르드스톤 백작 부인이 말했다. \"실험을 한번 해 보려고요.\"\n\n\"무슨 실험? 탁자 돌리기 말인가? 글쎄, 신사 숙녀 여러분, 미안하지만 내 생각엔 반지 놀이를 하는 게 더 재미있을 것 같네.\" 노공작은 브론스키를 보며, 그것이 그의 제안이었으리라 짐작하고 말했다. \"어쨌든 그쪽이 그래도 뭔가 말이 되니까.\"\n\n브론스키는 단호한 눈으로 의아한 듯 공작을 바라보더니, 희미한 미소를 띠며 곧장 노르드스톤 백작 부인과 다음 주에 열릴 성대한 무도회에 관해 이야기하기 시작했다.\n\n\"당신도 거기 오시겠죠?\" 그가 키티에게 말했다. 노공작이 그에게서 돌아서자마자, 레빈은 눈에 띄지 않게 밖으로 나갔고, 그날 저녁 그가 가지고 나온 마지막 인상은 무도회에 관한 브론스키의 물음에 답하는 키티의 미소 짓는 행복한 얼굴이었다.\n\n# Chapter 15\n\n저녁이 끝날 무렵 키티는 어머니에게 레빈과 나눈 대화를 이야기했고, 레빈에게 느끼는 온갖 연민에도 불구하고 자기가 청혼을 받았다는 생각에 기뻤다. 그녀는 자기가 옳게 처신했다는 데에 의심이 없었다. 그러나 잠자리에 든 뒤에도 한참 동안 잠들지 못했다. 한 가지 인상이 끈질기게 그녀를 따라다녔다. 그것은 레빈의 얼굴이었다. 미간을 잔뜩 찌푸린 채, 그 아래로 어둡고 침울하게 내다보는 다정한 눈, 그가 그녀의 아버지에게 귀를 기울이며 그녀를, 또 브론스키를 흘끔거리던 그 모습이었다. 그러자 그가 너무도 가엾어 눈물이 핑 돌았다. 그러나 곧바로 그녀는 그를 버리고 택한 사내를 떠올렸다. 그 사내의 남자다운, 단호한 얼굴, 그 고귀한 침착함, 그리고 모든 사람을 향한 매사에 드러나는 선량함이 생생하게 되살아났다. 자기가 사랑하는 그 사내가 자기를 사랑한다는 것을 떠올리자, 다시 한 번 그녀의 영혼은 온통 기쁨으로 차올랐고, 그녀는 베개에 기대 행복하게 미소 지으며 누워 있었다. \"미안해요, 미안해요. 하지만 나더러 어쩌라고요? 내 잘못이 아니잖아요.\" 그녀는 혼잣말을 했다. 그러나 마음속의 어떤 목소리는 그녀에게 다른 무언가를 일러 주었다. 레빈의 사랑을 얻은 것에 대한 가책인지, 아니면 그를 거절한 것에 대한 가책인지, 그녀는 알 수 없었다. 그러나 그녀의 행복은 의혹에 물들어 버렸다. \"주여, 우리를 불쌍히 여기소서. 주여, 우리를 불쌍히 여기소서. 주여, 우리를 불쌍히 여기소서!\" 그녀는 잠이 들 때까지 마음속으로 거듭 되뇌었다.\n\n한편 아래층 공작의 작은 서재에서는, 그 애지중지하는 딸 때문에 부부 사이에 그토록 자주 되풀이되던 장면 가운데 하나가 벌어지고 있었다.\n\n\"뭐라고? 내가 말해 주지!\" 공작이 두 팔을 휘저으며 소리를 질렀고, 곧장 다람쥐 털을 덧댄 실내복을 다시 몸에 둘렀다. \"당신한테는 자존심도, 품위도 없다는 거야. 이런 천박하고 멍청한 중매질로 딸을 망신시키고, 신세를 망쳐 놓고 있다는 거지!\"\n\n\"하지만, 정말이지, 제발, 여보, 내가 뭘 어쨌다는 거예요?\" 공작 부인이 거의 울먹이며 말했다.\n\n딸과 대화를 나눈 뒤 흐뭇하고 행복해진 그녀는 평소처럼 잘 자라는 인사를 하러 공작에게 갔고, 레빈의 청혼이며 키티의 거절을 그에게 알릴 생각은 없었으나, 그래도 남편에게 브론스키와는 일이 사실상 정해진 것 같다고, 그의 어머니가 도착하는 대로 그가 청혼할 거라는 듯이 넌지시 비쳤던 것이다. 그러자 그 말에 공작은 별안간 격분하여 점잖지 못한 말을 쏟아 내기 시작했다.\n\n\"당신이 뭘 어쨌냐고? 말해 주지. 우선 당신은 신랑감으로 괜찮은 신사를 낚으려 들고 있어. 그러면 모스크바가 온통 그 얘기로 떠들썩해질 거고, 그것도 그럴 만하지. 야회를 연다면 다들 초대해, 가능성 있는 신랑 후보들만 골라 뽑지 말고. 젊은 한량들을 죄다 불러. 피아노 연주자를 부르고, 춤을 추게 하란 말이야. 요즘 당신이 하는 식으로 좋은 혼처를 찾아 헤매지 말고. 보고 있으면 메스꺼워, 메스껍다고. 당신은 그 가엾은 계집애의 머리를 어지럽혀 놓을 때까지 그 짓을 계속해 왔어. 레빈이 천 배는 나은 사내야. 이 페테르부르크의 멋쟁이 도련님으로 말할 것 같으면, 그런 작자들은 기계로 찍어 내듯 죄다 한 틀에서 나와서, 전부 변변찮은 쓰레기야. 하지만 설령 그가 왕족 혈통의 공작이라 한들, 내 딸이 아무 사내한테나 매달릴 필요는 없어.\"\n\n\"하지만 내가 뭘 어쨌다는 거예요?\"\n\n\"아니, 당신은…….\" 공작은 노여움에 울부짖다시피 했다.\n\n\"당신 말대로 따른다면,\" 공작 부인이 말을 가로막았다. \"우린 영영 딸을 시집보내지 못할 거예요. 정 그렇다면 차라리 시골로 내려가는 게 낫겠네요.\"\n\n\"그래, 그러는 편이 낫겠지.\"\n\n\"하지만 잠깐만요. 내가 그 사람들을 낚으려 한다고요? 난 그 사람들을 낚으려는 마음이 조금도 없어요. 어떤 젊은이가, 그것도 아주 괜찮은 젊은이가 그 애한테 반했고, 그 애도 내 생각에는…….\"\n\n\"오, 그래, 당신 생각에는! 그러다 정말로 그 애가 반해 버렸는데, 그자는 나만큼이나 결혼은 생각도 안 하고 있으면 어쩔 텐가!…… 오, 내가 이런 꼴을 보려고 살아왔단 말인가! 아! 강신술! 아! 멋져라! 아! 무도회!\" 그러고는 공작은 아내를 흉내 낸답시고, 한 마디 한 마디마다 점잔 빼는 절을 해 보였다. \"이런 식으로 우리는 키티에게 불행을 마련해 주고 있는 거야. 게다가 그 애는 진짜로 그런 생각을 머릿속에 박아 넣고 있다고…….\"\n\n\"하지만 왜 그렇게 생각하시는 거예요?\"\n\n\"생각하는 게 아니라 아는 거야. 여자들한테는 없지만 우리한테는 그런 걸 보는 눈이 있거든. 진지한 속셈을 가진 사내가 누군지 난 보여, 그게 레빈이지. 그리고 이 머리 텅 빈 작자처럼 그저 제 재미나 보려는 공작새 같은 놈도 난 보인단 말이야.\"\n\n\"오, 참, 당신은 한번 머릿속에 무슨 생각이 박히면!……\"\n\n\"좋아, 두고 보면 내 말이 떠오를 거야. 하지만 그땐 너무 늦지, 돌리 때처럼 말이야.\"\n\n\"그래요, 그래, 그 얘긴 그만둬요.\" 공작 부인은 불운한 돌리를 떠올리며 그의 말을 막았다.\n\n\"좋아, 그럼 잘 자게!\"\n\n그러고서 부부는 서로에게 성호를 그어 주고, 입을 맞추며 헤어졌다. 두 사람 다 저마다 제 생각을 그대로 고집하고 있다는 것을 느끼면서.\n\n공작 부인은 처음에는 그날 저녁으로 키티의 장래가 정해졌으며, 브론스키의 속셈에는 의심의 여지가 없다고 확신하고 있었으나, 남편의 말에 마음이 어지러워졌다. 그리고 자기 방으로 돌아와, 알 수 없는 미래 앞에서 두려움에 사로잡혀, 그녀 또한 키티처럼 마음속으로 여러 번 되뇌었다. \"주여, 불쌍히 여기소서. 주여, 불쌍히 여기소서. 주여, 불쌍히 여기소서.\"\n\n# Chapter 16\n\n브론스키는 진정한 가정생활이라는 것을 가져 본 적이 없었다. 그의 어머니는 젊은 시절 사교계에서 화려하게 빛나던 여인으로, 결혼 생활 중에도, 그리고 그 후로는 한층 더, 온 상류 사교계에 소문이 자자한 연애 사건을 숱하게 겪은 사람이었다. 아버지는 거의 기억나지 않았고, 그는 근위 무관 학교에서 교육을 받았다.\n\n아주 젊은 나이에 빼어난 장교가 되어 학교를 떠난 그는, 곧장 페테르부르크의 부유한 군인 무리 속으로 들어갔다. 페테르부르크 사교계에 어느 정도 드나들기는 했지만, 그의 연애 사건은 여태껏 늘 그 바깥에서 일어났다.\n\n페테르부르크에서의 사치스럽고 거친 생활을 거친 뒤, 그는 모스크바에서 처음으로 자기와 같은 신분이며 자기를 좋아해 주는, 다정하고 순결한 처녀와 친밀하게 지내는 매력을 온전히 느꼈다. 키티와의 관계에 무언가 해로운 점이 있을 수 있다는 생각은 그의 머릿속에 떠오른 적조차 없었다. 무도회에서 그는 주로 그녀와 춤을 추었다. 그는 그녀의 집을 끊임없이 드나들었다. 그는 사교계에서 사람들이 흔히 나누는 식으로, 그러니까 온갖 시답잖은 이야기를 그녀에게 건넸지만, 그녀를 상대로 할 때만큼은 그 시답잖은 말에 특별한 의미를 담지 않을 수 없었다. 비록 그가 그녀에게 한 말은 누구 앞에서나 할 수 있는 것뿐이었지만, 그는 그녀가 점점 더 자기에게 기대게 되어 가는 것을 느꼈고, 그렇게 느끼면 느낄수록 그것이 더 좋았으며, 그녀를 향한 마음도 더 애틋해졌다. 그는 키티에 대한 자기 행동 방식에 분명한 성격이 있다는 것을, 그것이 결혼할 마음도 없으면서 젊은 처녀에게 구애하는 짓이며, 그런 구애야말로 자기 같은 화려한 젊은이들 사이에 흔한 못된 행실 가운데 하나라는 것을 알지 못했다. 그에게는 이 즐거움을 자기가 처음으로 발견한 것처럼 여겨졌고, 그는 그 발견을 즐기고 있었다.\n\n만일 그날 저녁 그녀의 부모가 나눈 이야기를 그가 들을 수 있었다면, 만일 그가 그 가족의 입장에 서서 자기가 키티와 결혼하지 않으면 그녀가 불행해지리라는 말을 들을 수 있었다면, 그는 몹시 놀랐을 것이고, 그 말을 믿지 않았을 것이다. 자기에게, 그리고 무엇보다 그녀에게 그토록 크고 섬세한 기쁨을 주는 일이 잘못된 것일 수 있다는 사실을 그는 믿을 수 없었다. 자기가 결혼을 해야 한다는 것은 더더욱 믿을 수 없었다.\n\n결혼이 그에게 하나의 가능성으로 떠오른 적은 한 번도 없었다. 그는 가정생활을 싫어했을 뿐 아니라, 가족이라는 것, 특히 남편이라는 존재를, 그가 몸담은 독신 세계에 두루 퍼진 견해에 따라, 무언가 낯설고 거슬리며, 무엇보다도 우스꽝스러운 것으로 여겼다.\n\n그러나 브론스키는 부모가 무슨 이야기를 나누는지 조금도 짐작하지 못했지만, 셰르바츠키가를 나서면서 자기와 키티 사이에 존재하는 은밀한 정신적 유대가 그날 저녁 너무도 강해진 나머지, 이제 무언가 조치를 취해야만 한다는 것을 느꼈다. 그러나 어떤 조치를 취할 수 있고 또 취해야 하는지는 도무지 상상이 가지 않았다.\n\n\"정말 황홀한 건 말이야.\" 그는 셰르바츠키가에서 돌아오며 생각했다. 그는 늘 그렇듯이 청량하고 순결한 기분을 가슴에 안고 돌아오고 있었는데, 그것은 한편으로는 저녁 내내 담배를 피우지 않은 덕분이기도 했고, 거기에 더해 자기를 향한 그녀의 사랑에서 우러난 새로운 애틋함도 섞여 있었다. \"정말 황홀한 건, 나도 그녀도 단 한마디 말도 하지 않았는데, 눈빛과 말투라는 이 보이지 않는 언어로 우리가 서로를 그토록 잘 이해한다는 거야. 오늘 저녁 그녀는 그 어느 때보다 또렷하게 나를 사랑한다고 말했어. 그것도 얼마나 은밀하게, 소박하게, 그리고 무엇보다 얼마나 믿음을 담아서! 나는 내가 더 나아지고, 더 순결해진 것 같은 기분이야. 나에게도 마음이라는 게 있고, 내 안에 좋은 구석이 잔뜩 있다는 걸 느껴. 그 사랑스럽고 다정한 눈동자라니! 그녀가 '정말 그래요……'라고 했을 때 말이야.\n\n\"자, 그래서 어쩌라고? 아, 아무것도 아니지. 나한테도 좋고, 그녀한테도 좋은 일이야.\" 그러고 나서 그는 저녁을 어디서 마무리할지 궁리하기 시작했다.\n\n그는 자기가 갈 만한 곳들을 하나하나 떠올려 보았다. \"클럽? 베지크 한판에, 이그나토프와 샴페인이나? 아니, 안 가. Château des Fleurs? 거기 가면 오블론스키랑, 노래에, 캉캉이 기다리고 있겠지. 아니, 그것도 지긋지긋해. 셰르바츠키가가 좋은 게 바로 그 점이야, 내가 점점 나아지고 있다는 거지. 집에 가야겠다.\" 그는 곧장 뒤소 호텔의 자기 방으로 가서 야식을 주문하고는 옷을 벗었고, 베개에 머리가 닿기가 무섭게 깊은 잠에 빠져들었다.\n* Château des Fleurs: 프랑스어로 '꽃의 성관(城館)'. 당시 유흥장을 가리킨다.\n\n# Chapter 17\n\n이튿날 아침 열한 시에 브론스키는 어머니를 마중하러 페테르부르크 철도역으로 마차를 몰고 갔는데, 큰 계단에서 가장 먼저 마주친 사람은 같은 기차로 누이를 기다리고 있던 오블론스키였다.\n\n\"아! 각하!\" 오블론스키가 소리쳤다. \"누구를 마중 나오셨소?\"\n\n\"어머니요.\" 오블론스키를 만나는 사람이라면 누구나 그러듯이 브론스키도 미소를 지으며 대답했다. 그는 오블론스키와 악수를 나누고 함께 계단을 올라갔다. \"오늘 페테르부르크에서 오신다더군요.\"\n\n\"어젯밤 두 시까지 자넬 찾았네. 셰르바츠키 댁에서 나와 어디로 갔던 건가?\"\n\n\"집으로요.\" 브론스키가 대답했다. \"솔직히 어제는 셰르바츠키 댁에서 나온 뒤에 어찌나 기분이 흐뭇하던지 어디 다른 데 갈 마음이 안 나더군요.\"\n\n\"준마는 확실한 표징으로 알아보고,\n\n사랑에 빠진 젊은이는 그 눈으로 알아보네,\"\n\n스테판 아르카지치는 전에 레빈에게 했던 것과 똑같이 시를 읊었다.\n\n브론스키는 부정하지 않는다는 듯한 표정으로 미소를 지었지만, 곧 화제를 돌렸다.\n\n\"그러는 당신은 누굴 마중 나오셨소?\" 그가 물었다.\n\n\"나? 나야 어여쁜 여인을 마중 나왔지.\" 오블론스키가 말했다.\n\"설마!\"\n\n\"Honi soit qui mal y pense! 내 누이 안나라네.\"\n\n\"아! 카레니나 부인 말이군요.\" 브론스키가 말했다.\n\n\"틀림없이 그녀를 알겠지?\"\n\n\"아는 것 같기도 하고요. 아니면 모르는 것 같기도 하고…… 정말 잘 모르겠네요.\" 브론스키는 카레니나라는 이름이 불러일으키는, 뭔가 뻣뻣하고 따분한 것에 대한 어렴풋한 기억을 떠올리며 무심하게 대답했다.\n\n\"하지만 내 유명한 매부 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 자네도 틀림없이 알겠지. 세상 사람들이 다 아는 인물이니까.\"\n\n\"평판으로도 알고 얼굴로도 압니다. 영리하고, 학식 있고, 좀 신앙심도 깊은 분이라는 것도 알고요…… 하지만 그건, 아시다시피 나하고는 not in my line이죠.\" 브론스키는 영어로 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 그는 아주 비범한 사람이지. 다소 보수적이긴 해도, 훌륭한 사람이야.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 말했다. \"훌륭한 사람이고말고.\"\n\n\"아, 그래요, 그분께는 그게 더 잘된 일이겠죠.\" 브론스키가 미소를 지으며 말했다. \"오, 자네 왔구먼.\" 그가 문가에 서 있던, 어머니를 모시는 키 큰 늙은 하인에게 말했다. \"이리 오게.\"\n\n오블론스키가 누구에게나 풍기는 그 매력 말고도, 브론스키는 요즘 들어 그가 자기 상상 속에서 키티와 연결되어 있다는 사실 때문에 특별히 그에게 끌리고 있었다.\n\n\"그래, 어떻게 생각하나? 일요일에 그 diva를 위해 만찬을 열까?\" 그가 오블론스키의 팔을 잡으며 미소와 함께 말했다.\n\n\"물론이지. 내가 회비를 걷고 있다네. 아, 그런데 내 친구 레빈하고는 인사를 나눴나?\" 스테판 아르카지치가 물었다.\n\n\"네, 그런데 일찍 가 버리던걸요.\"\n\n\"썩 괜찮은 친구지.\" 오블론스키가 말을 이었다. \"그렇지 않은가?\"\n\n\"왜 그런지는 모르겠지만요.\" 브론스키가 대답했다. \"모스크바 사람들은 죄다—물론 지금 여기 계신 분은 빼고요.\" 그가 농담조로 끼워 넣었다. \"어딘가 융통성이 없어요. 다들 잔뜩 방어적이고, 욱하기 일쑤고, 마치 모두들 남에게 뭔가를 느끼게 하고 싶어 하는 것 같단 말입니다……\"\n\n\"그래, 그건 사실이지, 정말 그렇다네.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 사람 좋게 웃으며 말했다.\n\n\"기차가 곧 들어옵니까?\" 브론스키가 철도 직원에게 물었다.\n\n\"신호가 들어왔습니다.\" 그 사내가 대답했다.\n\n기차가 다가오고 있다는 것이 역 안의 채비를 갖추는 분주함과, 짐꾼들이 분주히 오가는 모습과, 경관들과 역무원들의 움직임과, 기차를 맞으러 나온 사람들로 점점 더 뚜렷해졌다. 서리 낀 수증기 사이로 짧은 양가죽 외투에 부드러운 펠트 장화를 신은 인부들이 굽은 선로를 가로질러 가는 것이 보였다. 멀리 떨어진 선로에서 보일러의 쉭쉭거리는 소리와, 뭔가 묵직한 것이 우르릉거리는 소리가 들려왔다.\n\n\"아니야.\" 키티에 대한 레빈의 속내를 브론스키에게 들려주고 싶은 마음이 굴뚝같았던 스테판 아르카지치가 말했다. \"아니, 자넨 레빈에 대해 제대로 된 인상을 갖지 못한 거야. 그는 무척 신경이 예민한 사람이라 가끔 기분이 언짢기도 하지, 그건 사실이네만, 또 종종 아주 다정한 사람이라네. 워낙 진실하고 정직한 천성에다, 마음씨가 황금 같은 사람이지. 하지만 어제는 특별한 사정이 있었어.\" 스테판 아르카지치는 의미심장한 미소를 지으며 말을 이었는데, 전날 친구에게 느꼈던 진심 어린 동정은 까맣게 잊고서, 이제는 똑같은 동정을 오로지 브론스키에게만 느끼고 있었다. \"그래, 그가 유난히 행복하거나 유난히 불행할 수밖에 없었던 사정이 있었던 거지.\"\n\n브론스키는 우뚝 멈춰 서서 단도직입적으로 물었다. \"어째서요? 어제 그가 당신의 belle-sœur에게 청혼했다는 말입니까?\"\n\n\"어쩌면 그랬을지도.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 말했다. \"어제 그런 느낌이 들었거든. 그래, 일찍 가 버린 데다 기분까지 언짢았다면, 그건 분명…… 그렇게 오랫동안 사랑해 왔으니, 나도 그가 무척 안됐어.\"\n\n\"그런 거였군요! 하지만 제 생각엔, 그녀는 더 나은 혼처를 기대할 수도 있겠는데요.\" 브론스키가 몸을 곧추세우며 다시 거닐기 시작했다. \"물론 저야 그를 모릅니다만.\" 그가 덧붙였다. \"그래요, 그건 참 곤혹스러운 처지죠! 그래서 사내들 대부분은 클라라 같은 여자들을 상대하는 걸 더 좋아하는 거고요. 그런 여자들에게 실패하면 그저 돈이 모자랐다는 것만 드러날 뿐이지만, 이 경우엔 자존심이 걸린 문제니까요. 아, 기차가 들어옵니다.\"\n\n기관차는 벌써 저 멀리서 기적을 울렸다. 몇 순간 뒤 플랫폼이 흔들리기 시작했고, 추위에 낮게 깔린 증기를 뿜어내며 기관차가 들어왔다. 가운데 바퀴의 연결봉이 율동적으로 오르내렸고, 서리를 뒤집어쓴 기관사가 몸을 숙인 채였다. 탄수차 뒤로, 플랫폼을 점점 더 느릿느릿 흔들면서 안에서 개 한 마리가 낑낑대는 수하물차가 따라왔다. 마침내 객차들이 멈춰 서기 전에 좌우로 흔들거리며 들어왔다.\n\n날렵한 차장이 호루라기를 불며 뛰어내렸고, 그 뒤를 이어 성마른 승객들이 하나둘씩 내리기 시작했다. 몸을 꼿꼿이 세운 채 매서운 눈으로 주위를 둘러보는 근위대 장교, 작은 가방을 든 채 유쾌하게 미소 짓는 민첩하고 자그마한 상인, 어깨에 자루를 둘러멘 농부.\n\n오블론스키 곁에 서 있던 브론스키는 어머니에 대해서는 까맣게 잊은 채 객차들과 승객들을 바라보고 있었다. 방금 키티에 대해 들은 이야기가 그를 들뜨게 하고 기쁘게 했다. 자기도 모르게 그는 가슴을 부풀렸고, 두 눈이 번득였다. 그는 자신이 승리자가 된 듯한 기분이었다.\n\n\"브론스카야 백작 부인은 저 칸에 계십니다.\" 날렵한 차장이 브론스키에게 다가오며 말했다.\n\n차장의 말이 그를 일깨워, 어머니와 곧 있을 그녀와의 만남을 떠올리게 했다. 그는 마음속으로 어머니를 존경하지 않았고, 스스로 인정하지는 않았지만 어머니를 사랑하지도 않았다. 다만 그가 몸담은 무리의 통념과 자기가 받은 교육에 비추어 볼 때, 그는 어머니에 대해 더없이 깍듯하고 순종적이지 않은 어떤 태도도 상상할 수 없었으나, 겉으로 순종적이고 깍듯하게 굴면 굴수록 마음속으로는 그만큼 덜 어머니를 존경하고 사랑했다.\n* Honi soit qui mal y pense: 프랑스어로 '나쁜 마음을 품은 자에게 화 있으라'. 영국 가터 훈장의 표어. * diva: 이탈리아어로 '여가수, 프리마돈나'. * belle-sœur: 프랑스어로 '처제, 시누이' 등 손아래 여자 인척.\n\n# Chapter 18\n\n브론스키는 차장을 따라 객차로 갔는데, 칸막이 객실 문 앞에서 마침 내리려는 한 부인에게 길을 비켜 주려고 우뚝 멈춰 섰다.\n\n사교계 인사 특유의 통찰력으로, 브론스키는 이 부인의 모습을 한 번 흘끗 보는 것만으로 그녀를 상류 사회에 속한 사람으로 분류했다. 그는 양해를 구하고 객차로 들어가려 했지만, 그녀를 한 번 더 봐야겠다는 생각이 들었다. 그녀가 매우 아름다워서도 아니었고, 그녀의 온몸에서 드러나는 우아함과 정숙한 기품 때문도 아니었으며, 그녀가 그의 곁을 스쳐 지나갈 때 그 매혹적인 얼굴 표정에 무언가 유난히 다정하고 부드러운 것이 깃들어 있었기 때문이었다. 그가 돌아보는 순간, 그녀도 고개를 돌렸다. 짙은 속눈썹 때문에 어두워 보이는 그녀의 반짝이는 잿빛 눈은, 마치 그를 알아보기라도 한 듯 다정한 관심을 담고 그의 얼굴에 머물렀다가, 누군가를 찾기라도 하는 듯 곧 지나가는 군중 쪽으로 옮겨 갔다. 그 짧은 시선 속에서 브론스키는 그녀의 얼굴에 어른거리는, 그리고 빛나는 두 눈과 붉은 입술을 살짝 휘어 놓는 희미한 미소 사이를 오가는 억눌린 생기를 알아챌 수 있었다. 그녀의 천성이 무언가로 너무도 흘러넘쳐서, 그녀의 의지와는 상관없이 어떤 때는 눈빛의 광채로, 또 어떤 때는 미소로 그것이 저절로 드러나는 것만 같았다. 그녀는 일부러 눈 속의 빛을 가렸지만, 그 빛은 그녀의 의지를 거슬러 희미하게 감지되는 미소 속에서 반짝였다.\n\n브론스키는 객차로 들어섰다. 검은 눈에 곱슬머리를 한, 바싹 마른 노부인인 그의 어머니는 눈을 가늘게 뜨고 아들을 찬찬히 살펴보더니 얇은 입술로 살짝 미소 지었다. 그녀는 자리에서 일어나 하녀에게 가방을 건네고, 작고 주름진 손을 아들에게 내밀어 입을 맞추게 하고는, 그의 머리를 손에서 들어 올려 뺨에 입을 맞추었다.\n\n\"내 전보 받았니? 잘 지냈고? 다행이로구나.\"\n\n\"여행은 편안하셨어요?\" 아들이 그녀 곁에 앉으며 말했는데, 그러면서 자기도 모르게 문밖에서 들려오는 여자 목소리에 귀를 기울이고 있었다. 그는 그것이 문 앞에서 마주친 그 부인의 목소리라는 것을 알았다.\n\n\"그래도 저는 당신 의견에 동의하지 않아요.\" 그 부인의 목소리가 말했다.\n\n\"그건 페테르부르크식 견해입니다, 부인.\"\n\n\"페테르부르크식이 아니라, 그냥 여자다운 거예요.\" 그녀가 응수했다.\n\n\"자, 자, 그럼 손에 입맞추게 허락해 주십시오.\"\n\n\"안녕히 가세요, 이반 페트로비치. 그리고 제 오빠가 와 있는지 좀 봐 주시고, 그이를 제게 보내 주실 수 있겠어요?\" 부인은 문간에서 그렇게 말하고는 다시 객실 안으로 들어섰다.\n\n\"그래, 오라버니는 찾으셨어요?\" 브론스카야 백작 부인이 그 부인에게 말을 건넸다.\n\n이제 브론스키는 이 사람이 카레니나 부인이라는 것을 알아차렸다.\n\n\"오라버님은 여기 와 계십니다.\" 그가 일어서며 말했다. \"실례했습니다, 알아뵙지 못했습니다. 사실 우리가 안면을 튼 것이 워낙 잠깐이라,\" 브론스키가 고개를 숙이며 말했다. \"분명 저를 기억하지 못하시겠지요.\"\n\n\"오, 아니에요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"저는 당신을 알아봤을 거예요. 당신 어머님과 제가, 제 생각에는 오는 내내 당신 이야기밖에 안 한 것 같으니까요.\" 그녀는 말하면서, 기어이 비어져 나오려는 그 생기를 미소 속에 드러내 보였다. \"그런데도 오빠는 아직 보이지 않네요.\"\n\n\"좀 불러 보렴, 알렉세이.\" 노백작 부인이 말했다. 브론스키는 플랫폼으로 나가 외쳤다.\n\n\"오블론스키! 여기야!\"\n\n그러나 카레니나 부인은 오빠를 기다리지 않고, 그를 발견하자 가볍고도 단호한 걸음으로 객차에서 내렸다. 그리고 오빠가 그녀에게 다가오기가 무섭게, 그녀는 그 결단성과 우아함으로 브론스키를 놀라게 한 동작으로 왼팔을 오빠의 목에 두르고, 그를 재빨리 자기 쪽으로 끌어당겨 따뜻하게 입을 맞추었다. 브론스키는 그녀에게서 눈을 떼지 못한 채 바라보았고, 까닭은 알 수 없었지만 미소를 지었다. 그러나 어머니가 자기를 기다리고 있다는 것을 떠올리고는, 다시 객차 안으로 들어갔다.\n\"참 사랑스러운 분이지, 그렇지 않니?\" 백작 부인이 카레니나 부인을 두고 말했다. \"남편이 저 사람을 내게 맡겼는데, 함께하게 되어 나도 무척 기뻤단다. 오는 내내 이야기를 나눴지. 그리고 너로 말하자면, 내가 듣자 하니…… vous filez le parfait amour. Tant mieux, mon cher, tant mieux.\"\n\n* vous filez le parfait amour. Tant mieux, mon cher, tant mieux: 프랑스어로 '너는 완벽한 사랑을 키워 가고 있다는구나. 잘됐다, 얘야, 잘됐어'라는 뜻.\n\n\"무슨 말씀을 하시는지 모르겠습니다, maman.\" 그가 차갑게 대답했다. \"자, maman, 그만 가시지요.\"\n\n카레니나 부인은 백작 부인에게 작별 인사를 하려고 다시 객차로 들어섰다.\n\n\"자, 백작 부인, 부인께서는 아드님을 만나셨고, 저는 오빠를 만났네요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그리고 제 수다도 이제 다 떨어졌어요. 더는 말씀드릴 게 없을 거예요.\"\n\n\"오, 아니에요.\" 백작 부인이 그녀의 손을 잡으며 말했다. \"나는 당신과 함께라면 온 세상을 돌아다녀도 결코 지루하지 않을 거예요. 당신은 함께 있으면 말하는 것도 침묵하는 것도 다 즐거운, 그런 사랑스러운 여인들 중 하나예요. 자, 부디 아드님 일로 너무 마음 졸이지 말아요. 영영 떨어지지 않고 살 수는 없는 법이니까요.\"\n\n카레니나 부인은 몸을 아주 꼿꼿이 세우고 가만히 서 있었는데, 그녀의 두 눈은 웃고 있었다.\n\n\"안나 아르카지예브나는,\" 백작 부인이 아들에게 설명하듯 말했다. \"내 생각에 여덟 살 난 어린 아들이 있는데, 전에는 한 번도 그 아이와 떨어져 본 적이 없어서, 아이를 두고 온 일로 줄곧 마음을 졸이고 있단다.\"\n\n\"네, 백작 부인과 저는 내내 이야기를 나눴어요. 저는 제 아들 이야기를, 부인은 부인의 아드님 이야기를 했죠.\" 카레니나 부인이 말했고, 다시 그녀의 얼굴에 미소가 환히 떠올랐는데, 그를 향한 다정한 미소였다.\n\n\"틀림없이 무척 지루하셨겠군요.\" 그가 그녀가 던진 교태의 공을 냉큼 받아치며 말했다. 그러나 그녀는 그런 어조로 대화를 이어 갈 마음이 없는 듯, 노백작 부인 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸다.\n\n\"정말 감사했어요. 시간이 어찌나 빨리 지났는지요. 안녕히 가세요, 백작 부인.\"\n\n\"잘 가요, 내 사랑.\" 백작 부인이 대답했다. \"그 예쁜 얼굴에 입맞추게 해 줘요. 내 나이가 되면 거리낌 없이 말하게 되는데, 솔직히 말해서 나는 당신에게 마음을 빼앗겨 버렸어요.\"\n\n상투적인 말이었지만, 카레니나 부인은 그 말을 분명히 믿었고 또 그 말에 기뻐했다. 그녀는 얼굴을 붉히고 살짝 몸을 숙여 백작 부인의 입술에 뺨을 댔다가, 다시 몸을 일으켰다. 그리고 입술과 두 눈 사이를 오가는 그 똑같은 미소를 띤 채, 브론스키에게 손을 내밀었다. 그가 그녀가 내민 작은 손을 잡자, 그녀가 거리낌 없이 힘차게 그의 손을 흔드는 그 야무진 악수에 그는 무언가 특별한 일을 만난 듯 기뻤다. 그녀는 제법 풍만하게 발달한 몸을 그토록 이상하리만치 가볍게 실어 나르는 그 빠른 걸음으로 나가 버렸다.\n\n\"정말 매력적이로구나.\" 백작 부인이 말했다.\n\n그것은 그녀의 아들이 막 떠올리고 있던 바로 그 생각이었다. 그의 눈은 그녀의 우아한 자태가 시야에서 사라질 때까지 그녀를 좇았고, 그러고 나서도 미소가 그의 얼굴에 남아 있었다. 그는 창밖으로, 그녀가 오빠에게 다가가 팔짱을 끼고 무언가를 열심히 이야기하기 시작하는 것을 보았는데, 분명 자기, 그러니까 브론스키와는 아무 상관없는 이야기였고, 그래서 그는 짜증이 났다.\n\n\"그래, maman, 정말 편안하셨어요?\" 그가 어머니를 향해 고쳐 물었다.\n\n\"모든 게 즐거웠단다. 알렉산드르는 아주 착했고, 마리는 무척 예뻐졌더구나. 그 애는 참 흥미로운 아이야.\"\n\n그리고 그녀는 다시, 자기가 가장 마음을 쓰는 일—손자의 세례식, 그리고 그것 때문에 페테르부르크에 머물렀던 일이며, 황제께서 큰아들에게 베푸신 특별한 은총에 대해 그에게 이야기하기 시작했다.\n\n\"라브렌티가 왔네요.\" 브론스키가 창밖을 내다보며 말했다. \"이제 원하시면 가실 수 있습니다.\"\n\n백작 부인과 함께 여행해 온 늙은 집사가 모든 준비가 끝났다고 알리려고 객차로 왔고, 백작 부인은 가려고 일어섰다.\n\n\"가시지요. 이제는 그렇게 붐비지도 않습니다.\" 브론스키가 말했다.\n\n하녀는 손가방과 애완견을 들었고, 집사와 짐꾼은 나머지 짐을 들었다. 브론스키는 어머니에게 팔을 내밀었다. 그런데 그들이 막 객차에서 내리려는 순간, 몇몇 사내들이 공포에 질린 얼굴로 별안간 곁을 달려 지나갔다. 역장 또한 그 유난스러운 색깔의 모자를 쓴 채 달려갔다. 무언가 심상찮은 일이 벌어진 것이 분명했다. 기차에서 내렸던 군중이 다시 되돌아 달려가고 있었다.\n\n\"뭐라고요?…… 뭐예요?…… 어디서요?…… 몸을 던졌대!…… 깔렸대!……\" 하는 소리가 군중 사이에서 들려왔다. 누이와 팔짱을 낀 스테판 아르카지치도 발길을 돌렸다. 그들 역시 겁먹은 얼굴로, 군중을 피하려고 객차 문 앞에 멈춰 섰다.\n\n부인들은 객차로 들어갔고, 그동안 브론스키와 스테판 아르카지치는 그 참사의 자세한 내막을 알아보려고 군중을 따라갔다.\n\n차장 한 사람이, 술에 취했거나 아니면 매서운 추위에 너무 단단히 몸을 싸맨 탓에, 기차가 뒤로 움직이는 소리를 듣지 못하고 깔려 버린 것이었다.\n\n브론스키와 오블론스키가 돌아오기도 전에 부인들은 집사에게서 그 사실을 들었다.\n\n오블론스키와 브론스키는 둘 다 그 짓이겨진 시신을 보았다. 오블론스키는 분명 충격을 받은 모습이었다. 그는 미간을 찌푸렸고, 금방이라도 울 것만 같았다.\n\n\"아, 어찌나 끔찍한지! 아, 안나, 네가 그걸 봤다면! 아, 어찌나 끔찍한지!\" 그가 말했다.\n\n브론스키는 말이 없었다. 그의 잘생긴 얼굴은 진지했지만, 더없이 침착했다.\n\n\"오, 백작 부인, 부인께서 그걸 보셨다면,\" 스테판 아르카지치가 말했다. \"그리고 그 사람의 아내가 거기 있었어요…… 그 여자를 보는 것은 정말 끔찍했습니다!…… 그 여자는 시신 위로 몸을 던졌어요. 그 사람이 대가족의 유일한 부양자였다더군요. 정말 끔찍해요!\"\n\n\"그 여자를 위해 뭐라도 해 줄 수는 없을까요?\" 카레니나 부인이 동요한 목소리로 속삭이듯 말했다.\n\n브론스키는 그녀를 흘끗 보고는 곧장 객차에서 내렸다.\n\n\"금방 돌아오겠습니다, maman.\" 그가 문간에서 돌아서며 말했다.\n\n몇 분 뒤 그가 돌아왔을 때, 스테판 아르카지치는 이미 백작 부인과 새로 나온 가수에 대해 이야기를 나누고 있었고, 백작 부인은 아들을 기다리며 초조하게 문 쪽을 바라보고 있었다.\n\n\"자, 이제 출발하시지요.\" 브론스키가 들어오며 말했다. 그들은 함께 나갔다. 브론스키가 어머니와 함께 앞장섰다. 그 뒤로 카레니나 부인이 오빠와 함께 걸었다. 그들이 막 역을 나서려는데 역장이 브론스키를 뒤쫓아 왔다.\n\n\"제 부하 직원에게 이백 루블을 주셨더군요. 누구를 위해 쓰실 작정이신지 말씀해 주시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"미망인을 위해서요.\" 브론스키가 어깨를 으쓱하며 말했다. \"굳이 물을 것까지야 없을 텐데요.\"\n\n\"자네가 그걸 줬다고?\" 뒤에 있던 오블론스키가 외쳤고, 누이의 손을 꼭 쥐며 덧붙였다. \"정말 훌륭하군, 정말 훌륭해! 저 친구 멋진 사람 아니냐? 안녕히 가십시오, 백작 부인.\"\n\n그리고 그와 누이는 하녀를 찾으며 그 자리에 멈춰 섰다.\n\n그들이 밖으로 나왔을 때 브론스키의 마차는 이미 떠나고 없었다. 안으로 들어오는 사람들은 여전히 방금 일어난 일을 이야기하고 있었다.\n\n\"정말 끔찍한 죽음이로군!\" 한 신사가 곁을 지나가며 말했다. \"두 동강이 났다더군요.\"\n\n\"천만에, 내 생각에는 그게 가장 편한 죽음이오. 한순간이니까.\" 다른 사람이 한마디 했다.\n\n\"어째서 제대로 된 안전 조치를 안 하는 거야?\" 또 다른 사람이 말했다.\n\n카레니나 부인은 마차에 자리를 잡고 앉았는데, 스테판 아르카지치는 그녀의 입술이 떨리고 있고, 그녀가 간신히 눈물을 참고 있다는 것을 알아채고 놀랐다.\n\n\"왜 그러니, 안나?\" 마차가 몇백 미터쯤 달렸을 때 그가 물었다.\n\n\"불길한 징조예요.\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"무슨 그런 터무니없는 소리를!\" 스테판 아르카지치가 말했다. \"네가 와 줬다는 것, 그게 가장 중요한 일이야. 내가 너한테 얼마나 큰 희망을 걸고 있는지 너는 짐작도 못 할 거다.\"\n\n\"브론스키를 안 지는 오래되셨어요?\" 그녀가 물었다.\n\n\"그래. 너도 알다시피, 우리는 그가 키티와 결혼하기를 바라고 있단다.\"\n\n\"그래요?\" 안나가 나직이 말했다. \"자, 이제 오빠 이야기를 해요.\" 그녀는 마치 자기를 짓누르는 무언가 거추장스러운 것을 몸으로 떨쳐 내려는 듯 고개를 흔들며 덧붙였다. \"오빠 일을 이야기해 봐요. 편지를 받고 이렇게 왔잖아요.\"\n\n\"그래, 내 모든 희망은 너한테 달려 있어.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 말했다.\n\n\"자, 그럼 다 이야기해 봐요.\"\n\n그리고 스테판 아르카지치는 자기 이야기를 풀어놓기 시작했다.\n\n집에 다다르자 오블론스키는 누이가 내리는 것을 도와주고, 한숨을 쉬고, 그녀의 손을 꼭 쥐고는, 자기 관청으로 떠났다.\n\n# Chapter 19\n\n안나가 방으로 들어갔을 때, 돌리는 작은 응접실에 머리가 하얗고 통통한, 벌써 아버지를 닮은 사내아이와 함께 앉아 프랑스어 읽기를 가르치고 있었다. 아이는 책을 읽으면서 거의 떨어질 듯 말 듯 한 윗옷 단추를 계속 비틀며 떼어 내려고 했다. 어머니가 몇 번이나 단추에서 그 손을 떼어 냈지만, 통통한 작은 손은 다시 단추로 돌아갔다. 어머니는 단추를 뜯어 자기 호주머니에 넣었다.\n\n\"손 좀 가만히 둬라, 그리샤.\" 그녀는 말하고는, 오래전부터 만들어 오던 침대보를 다시 집어 들었다. 그녀는 늘 마음이 가라앉을 때 그 일에 손을 댔는데, 지금은 손가락을 떨며 코를 세면서 신경질적으로 떴다. 비록 그녀는 그 전날 남편에게, 그의 누이가 오든 말든 자기와는 아무 상관이 없다고 전갈을 보냈지만, 정작 그녀를 맞을 채비는 모두 갖추어 놓았고, 마음이 술렁이며 시누이를 기다리고 있었다.\n\n돌리는 슬픔에 짓눌려, 그 슬픔에 완전히 삼켜져 있었다. 그래도 그녀는 시누이인 안나가 페테르부르크에서 가장 중요한 인물 가운데 하나의 아내이며, 페테르부르크의 grande dame라는 사실을 잊지 않았다. 그리고 바로 그 사정 덕분에, 그녀는 남편에게 했던 위협을 실행에 옮기지 않았다. 다시 말해, 시누이가 온다는 것을 기억해 두었던 것이다. \"게다가 어쨌든 안나에게는 아무 잘못도 없잖아.\" 돌리는 생각했다. \"나는 그녀에 대해 더없이 좋은 것밖에는 아는 게 없고, 그녀가 나를 대하는 태도에서도 친절과 애정밖에는 본 적이 없어.\" 사실 페테르부르크에서 카레닌 집안에 머물던 때의 인상을 떠올려 보면, 그 집안 자체가 마음에 들지는 않았다. 그들의 가정생활 전체의 틀에는 어딘가 꾸민 듯한 데가 있었다. \"하지만 내가 왜 그녀를 맞지 못한단 말이야? 그저 그녀가 나를 위로하겠다고 마음먹지만 않는다면!\" 돌리는 생각했다. \"온갖 위로와 충고와 기독교적 용서, 그런 건 내가 이미 천 번도 더 곱씹어 봤지만, 다 아무 소용이 없어.\"\n\n* grande dame: 프랑스어로 '귀부인'.\n이 며칠 동안 돌리는 아이들과 단둘이서 지냈다. 자기 슬픔에 대해 이야기하고 싶지는 않았지만, 그 슬픔을 가슴에 품은 채로는 바깥의 일들에 대해서도 이야기할 수가 없었다. 그녀는 어떤 식으로든 안나에게 모든 것을 털어놓게 되리라는 것을 알았고, 그래서 마음을 터놓고 이야기할 수 있으리라는 생각에 기뻐하다가도, 다른 한편으로는 하필 그의 누이인 그녀에게 자신의 굴욕을 이야기하고, 미리 준비된 듯한 충고와 위로의 말을 들어야 한다는 것에 화가 나기도 하며 마음이 오락가락했다. 그녀는 안나를 기다리며 줄곧 시계를 들여다보았지만, 흔히 그렇듯이, 손님이 도착한 바로 그 순간을 놓쳐 버려 초인종 소리를 듣지 못했다.\n\n문가에서 치맛자락 스치는 소리와 가벼운 발소리가 들리자 그녀는 돌아보았고, 근심에 찌든 그녀의 얼굴에는 저도 모르게 반가움이 아니라 놀라움이 떠올랐다. 그녀는 일어나 시누이를 껴안았다.\n\n\"어머, 벌써 왔어요!\" 그녀는 입을 맞추며 말했다.\n\n\"돌리, 만나서 정말 기뻐요!\"\n\n\"나도 기뻐요.\" 돌리는 희미하게 미소 지으며, 안나의 얼굴 표정에서 그녀가 알고 있는지를 알아내려 애쓰며 말했다. \"아마 알고 있을 거야.\" 그녀는 안나의 얼굴에 어린 연민을 알아채고 생각했다. \"자, 어서 와요, 방으로 안내할게요.\" 그녀는 속내를 털어놓는 순간을 되도록 오래 미루려 애쓰며 말을 이었다.\n\n\"얘가 그리샤인가요? 어머나, 정말 많이 컸네요!\" 안나가 말했다. 그러고는 돌리에게서 눈을 떼지 않은 채 아이에게 입을 맞추고는, 가만히 멈춰 서서 얼굴을 살짝 붉혔다. \"아니에요, 부탁이니, 우리 그냥 여기 있어요.\"\n\n그녀는 머릿수건과 모자를 벗었는데, 모자가 곱슬곱슬한 검은 머리카락 한 가닥에 걸리자 고개를 흔들어 머리카락을 풀어 내렸다.\n\n\"건강과 행복으로 빛나고 있네요!\" 돌리가 거의 부러움 어린 어조로 말했다.\n\n\"내가요?…… 그래요.\" 안나가 말했다. \"어머나, 타냐! 우리 세료자랑 동갑이네.\" 그녀는 뛰어 들어오는 여자아이를 보고 그렇게 덧붙였다. 그녀는 아이를 안아 들고 입을 맞췄다. \"정말 사랑스러운 아이네, 사랑스러워! 아이들을 모두 보여 줘요.\"\n\n그녀는 이름뿐만 아니라 나이며 개월 수며 성격이며 앓았던 병까지, 아이들 하나하나를 기억하며 일일이 입에 올렸고, 돌리는 그것을 고맙게 여기지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n\"좋아요, 아이들에게 가 봐요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"바샤가 자고 있는 게 안타깝네요.\"\n\n아이들을 둘러본 뒤, 두 사람은 이제 단둘이 응접실에 앉아 커피를 마셨다. 안나는 쟁반을 집어 들었다가, 이내 자기 앞에서 밀쳐 냈다.\n\n\"돌리.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그이가 나한테 다 말했어요.\"\n\n돌리는 안나를 차갑게 바라보았다. 이제 그녀는 판에 박힌 위로의 말이 나오기를 기다리고 있었지만, 안나는 그런 말은 한마디도 하지 않았다.\n\n\"돌리, 그대.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"나는 그이를 두둔하려는 것도 아니고, 그대를 위로하려는 것도 아니에요. 그런 건 불가능하니까요. 하지만, 그대, 나는 그저 마음이 아파요, 진심으로 그대가 안쓰러워요!\"\n\n반짝이는 그녀의 두 눈, 그 짙은 속눈썹 아래로 갑자기 눈물이 글썽였다. 그녀는 시누이에게 더 가까이 다가앉아, 야무진 작은 손으로 돌리의 손을 잡았다. 돌리는 손을 빼지는 않았지만, 그녀의 얼굴은 여전히 차가운 표정을 잃지 않았다. 그녀는 말했다.\n\n\"나를 위로하는 건 불가능해요. 이런 일이 벌어진 뒤로는 모든 게 끝장났어요, 모든 게 다 끝났다고요!\"\n\n그렇게 말하자마자, 그녀의 얼굴은 갑자기 부드러워졌다. 안나는 돌리의 야위고 가느다란 손을 들어 올려 입을 맞추고는 말했다.\n\n\"하지만, 돌리, 어떻게 해야 하죠, 어떻게 해야 좋을까요? 이 끔찍한 상황에서 어떻게 처신하는 것이 가장 나은지—그대가 생각해야 할 건 바로 그거예요.\"\n\n\"다 끝났어요, 그뿐이에요.\" 돌리가 말했다. \"그리고 무엇보다 끔찍한 건, 그러니까, 내가 그이를 내칠 수 없다는 거예요. 아이들이 있으니까, 나는 묶여 있는 거죠. 그렇다고 그이와 함께 살 수도 없어요! 그이를 보는 것 자체가 나에겐 고문이라고요.\"\n\n\"돌리, 그대, 그이가 나한테 이야기하긴 했지만, 나는 그대 입으로 듣고 싶어요. 그 일에 대해 말해 줘요.\"\n\n돌리는 캐묻는 눈으로 그녀를 바라보았다.\n\n안나의 얼굴에는 꾸밈없는 연민과 사랑이 드러나 있었다.\n\n\"좋아요.\" 그녀가 불쑥 말했다. \"하지만 처음부터 다 이야기할게요. 내가 어떻게 결혼했는지 알잖아요. 엄마가 우리한테 시킨 그런 교육을 받아서, 나는 순진하다 못해 어리석을 정도였어요. 아무것도 몰랐죠. 남자들은 아내에게 자기 과거를 이야기한다고들 하지만, 스티바는\"—그녀는 말을 고쳤다—\"스테판 아르카지치는 나에게 아무 말도 하지 않았어요. 믿기 어렵겠지만, 나는 지금까지도 그이가 알았던 여자가 나 하나뿐이라고 생각했어요. 그렇게 팔 년을 살았어요. 알아 둬요, 나는 부정을 의심하기는커녕, 그런 일은 있을 수 없다고 여겼고, 그런데—한번 상상해 봐요—그런 생각을 품고 있다가, 갑자기 그 모든 끔찍함과 역겨움을 알게 된 거예요…… 나를 좀 이해하려고 해 봐요. 자기 행복을 철석같이 믿고 있다가, 한순간에……\" 돌리는 흐느낌을 억누르며 말을 이었다. \"편지 한 장을 받았어요…… 그이가 자기 정부에게, 우리 집 가정교사에게 쓴 편지를요. 안 돼요, 너무 끔찍해요!\" 그녀는 황급히 손수건을 꺼내 얼굴을 묻었다. \"감정에 휩쓸리는 건 이해할 수 있어요.\" 그녀는 잠시 침묵한 뒤 말을 이었다. \"하지만 일부러, 음흉하게 나를 속이다니…… 그것도 누구하고?…… 그 여자랑 함께 지내면서 계속 내 남편 노릇을 하다니…… 너무 끔찍해요! 그댄 이해 못 할 거예요……\"\n\n\"오, 맞아요, 나는 이해해요! 이해한다고요! 돌리, 가엾은 그대, 정말로 이해해요.\" 안나가 그녀의 손을 꼭 쥐며 말했다.\n\n\"그리고 그이가 내 처지가 얼마나 끔찍한지 알기나 한다고 생각해요?\" 돌리가 다시 말을 이었다. \"조금도 몰라요! 행복하고 만족스러워하고 있다고요.\"\n\n\"오, 아니에요!\" 안나가 얼른 끼어들었다. \"그이는 가엾은 사람이에요, 자책으로 짓눌려 있어요……\"\n\n\"그이가 자책 같은 걸 할 줄 알기나 해요?\" 돌리가 시누이의 얼굴을 뚫어지게 바라보며 말을 가로막았다.\n\n\"네. 나는 그이를 알아요. 그이를 보면 안쓰럽지 않을 수가 없었어요. 우리 둘 다 그이를 알잖아요. 그이는 마음씨는 착하지만 자존심이 강한데, 지금 너무나도 비참해진 거예요. 무엇보다 내 마음을 아프게 한 건……\" (그리고 여기서 안나는 무엇이 돌리의 마음을 가장 울릴지 헤아렸다) \"그이는 두 가지 때문에 괴로워하고 있어요. 하나는 아이들을 볼 면목이 없다는 것, 또 하나는 그대를 사랑하면서도—그래요, 그래요, 그대를 이 세상 그 무엇보다 사랑하면서도,\" 그녀는 대답하려는 돌리의 말을 황급히 막았다. \"그대에게 상처를 주고, 가슴을 후벼 팠다는 거예요. '아니야, 아니야, 그 사람은 나를 용서하지 못할 거야.' 그이는 자꾸만 그렇게 말해요.\"\n\n돌리는 그녀의 말을 들으면서 시누이 너머 먼 곳을 꿈꾸듯 바라보았다.\n\n\"그래요, 그이의 처지가 끔찍하다는 건 알아요. 죄지은 사람은 죄 없는 사람보다 더 괴로운 법이니까요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"이 모든 불행이 자기 잘못에서 비롯됐다는 걸 느낀다면 말이에요. 하지만 내가 어떻게 그이를 용서하죠, 그 여자가 있고 난 뒤에 내가 어떻게 다시 그이의 아내가 되겠어요? 지금 그이와 함께 산다는 건 나에겐 고문일 거예요, 바로 내가 그이를 향한 지난날의 사랑을 사랑하고 있기 때문에……\"\n\n그리고 흐느낌이 그녀의 말을 끊었다. 하지만 마치 일부러 그러기로 작정이라도 한 듯이, 마음이 누그러질 때마다 그녀는 자기를 격분하게 만드는 일에 대해 다시 이야기하기 시작했다.\n\n\"그 여잔 젊잖아요, 알다시피, 예쁘고요.\" 그녀가 말을 이었다. \"알아요, 안나, 내 젊음도 내 아름다움도 다 사라졌어요, 누가 가져갔게요? 그이와 그이의 아이들이 가져갔어요. 나는 그이를 위해 일했고, 내가 가진 모든 걸 그이에게 바쳤는데, 이제 와서 당연하게도 싱싱하고 천박한 계집이 그이에겐 더 매력적인 거예요. 틀림없이 그 둘은 함께 내 이야기를 했거나, 아니 더 나쁘게는, 입을 다물고 있었겠죠. 무슨 말인지 알겠어요?\"\n\n다시 그녀의 두 눈이 증오로 이글거렸다.\n\n\"그러고도 그이는 나한테 말하겠죠…… 뭐라고요! 내가 그이를 믿을 수 있겠어요? 절대로! 안 돼요, 모든 게 끝났어요, 한때 내 위안이었고, 내 노고와 내 고통에 대한 보상이었던 모든 것이…… 믿어지나요, 방금 전에 나는 그리샤를 가르치고 있었어요. 한때는 그게 나에게 기쁨이었는데, 지금은 고문이에요. 내가 무엇을 위해 애쓰고 수고해야 하죠? 무엇 때문에 아이들이 여기 있는 거죠? 정말 끔찍한 건, 한순간에 내 마음이 뒤집혀서, 사랑과 애정 대신 그이에 대한 증오밖에 남지 않았다는 거예요. 그래요, 증오요. 그이를 죽여 버릴 수도 있을 것 같아요.\"\n\n\"가엾은 돌리, 나는 이해해요, 하지만 자신을 괴롭히지 말아요. 그대는 너무 상심하고 너무 흥분한 나머지, 많은 것을 잘못 보고 있어요.\"\n\n돌리는 차츰 진정되었고, 두 사람은 잠시 동안 말이 없었다.\n\n\"어떻게 하면 좋죠? 나 대신 생각해 줘요, 안나, 나를 도와줘요. 나는 모든 걸 다 생각해 봤지만, 아무것도 안 보여요.\"\n\n안나도 아무것도 떠오르지 않았지만, 그녀의 마음은 시누이의 말 하나하나에, 표정의 변화 하나하나에 곧바로 응답했다.\n\n\"한 가지만 말할게요.\" 안나가 입을 열었다. \"나는 그이의 누이라서 그이의 성격을 알아요. 무엇이든, 무엇이든 다 잊어버리는 그 버릇\" (그녀는 자기 이마 앞에서 손을 내저었다), \"완전히 휩쓸려 버리는 그 버릇, 하지만 또 완전히 뉘우치기도 하는 그 버릇 말이에요. 그이는 도무지 믿을 수 없는 거예요, 자기가 어떻게 그런 짓을 저질렀는지 지금으로선 도무지 이해할 수 없는 거예요.\"\n\n\"아니에요. 그인 다 알아요, 알고 있었다고요!\" 돌리가 끼어들었다. \"하지만 나는…… 그대는 나를 잊고 있어요…… 그런다고 내 마음이 편해지나요?\"\n\n\"잠깐만요. 그이가 나한테 이야기했을 때, 솔직히 말하면 나는 그대 처지가 얼마나 끔찍한지 미처 다 깨닫지 못했어요. 그이밖에 안 보였고, 한 가정이 깨졌다는 것밖에 안 보였어요. 그이가 안쓰러웠죠. 하지만 그대와 이야기하고 나니, 같은 여자로서, 전혀 다르게 보여요. 그대의 고통이 보이고, 그대가 얼마나 안쓰러운지 이루 말할 수가 없어요! 하지만, 돌리, 그대, 나는 그대의 괴로움을 충분히 알아요, 다만 한 가지 모르는 게 있어요. 나는 모르겠어요…… 모르겠어요…… 그대 마음에 아직 그이를 향한 사랑이 얼마나 남아 있는지를요. 그건 그대만이 알겠죠—그이를 용서할 수 있을 만큼의 사랑이 남아 있는지를요. 만약 남아 있다면, 그이를 용서해요!\"\n\n\"아니에요.\" 돌리가 말을 꺼내려 했지만, 안나는 다시 한번 그녀의 손에 입을 맞추며 그 말을 잘랐다.\n\n\"나는 그대보다 세상을 더 많이 알아요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"스티바 같은 남자들이 그걸 어떻게 보는지 나는 알아요. 그대는 그이가 그 여자와 함께 그대 이야기를 했다고 말하지만, 그런 일은 결코 없었어요. 그런 남자들은 부정을 저지르긴 해도, 자기 가정과 아내는 신성하게 여겨요. 어찌 된 일인지 그런 여자들은 그들에게는 여전히 경멸의 대상일 뿐이고, 그들이 가족에게 품은 감정에는 손끝 하나 닿지 못해요. 그들은 자기들과 가족 사이에 절대 넘을 수 없는 일종의 선을 그어 놓는 거예요. 나는 이해할 수 없지만, 어쨌든 그래요.\"\n\n\"그래요, 하지만 그이는 그 여자에게 입을 맞췄잖아요……\"\n\n\"돌리, 쉿, 그대. 나는 스티바가 그대를 사랑하던 때를 봤어요. 그이가 나를 찾아와 그대 이야기를 하며 울던 때를, 그대를 향한 그이의 감정이 얼마나 시적이고 고결했는지를 나는 기억해요. 그리고 그대와 오래 살면 살수록 그이의 눈에 그대가 점점 더 고결하게 비쳤다는 걸 나는 알아요. 알다시피 우리는 그이가 말끝마다 '돌리는 정말 굉장한 여자야'라고 끼워 넣는 것을 두고 가끔 놀려 대곤 했잖아요. 그대는 늘 그이에게 여신 같은 존재였고, 지금도 여전히 그래요. 이건 마음에서 우러난 부정이 아니었어요……\"\n\n\"하지만 만약 또 그런다면요?\"\n\n\"내가 아는 한, 그럴 수는 없어요……\"\n\n\"그래요, 하지만 그댄 그런 일을 용서할 수 있겠어요?\"\n\n\"모르겠어요, 나는 판단할 수가 없어요…… 아니, 할 수 있어요.\" 안나는 잠시 생각하더니 말했다. 그러고는 그 상황을 머릿속으로 헤아려 자기 내면의 저울에 달아 본 뒤 덧붙였다. \"네, 할 수 있어요, 할 수 있어요, 할 수 있어요. 네, 나라면 용서할 수 있을 거예요. 예전과 똑같을 수는 없겠죠, 그래요. 하지만 용서할 수 있을 거예요, 마치 아예 없었던 일처럼, 처음부터 전혀 없었던 일처럼 용서할 거예요……\"\n\n\"오, 물론이죠.\" 돌리가, 한 번 이상 생각해 본 것을 말하기라도 하듯, 얼른 끼어들었다. \"안 그러면 용서가 아니죠. 용서한다면, 완전히, 완전히 해야 해요. 자, 가요. 방으로 안내할게요.\" 그녀는 일어서며 말하고는, 가는 길에 안나를 껴안았다. \"그대, 와 줘서 정말 기뻐요. 덕분에 한결 나아졌어요, 훨씬 더 나아졌어요.\"\n\n# Chapter 20\n\n그날 하루 종일 안나는 집에서, 그러니까 오블론스키 집안에서 보냈고, 몇몇 지인은 이미 그녀가 도착했다는 소식을 듣고 그날로 찾아오기도 했지만 아무도 만나지 않았다. 안나는 오전 내내 돌리와 아이들과 함께 지냈다. 그녀는 그저 오빠에게, 집에서 꼭 저녁을 먹어야 한다고 짧은 쪽지를 보냈을 뿐이다. \"오세요, 하느님은 자비로우시니까요.\" 그녀는 그렇게 적었다.\n\n오블론스키는 정말로 집에서 저녁을 먹었다. 대화는 모두 함께 나누는 것이었고, 그의 아내는 그에게 말을 건넬 때 전에는 그러지 않았던 \"스티바\"라는 호칭으로 그를 불렀다. 부부 사이에는 여전히 똑같은 서먹함이 남아 있었지만, 이제 별거 이야기는 나오지 않았고, 스테판 아르카지치는 해명과 화해의 가능성을 보았다.\n\n저녁 식사가 끝나자마자 키티가 들어왔다. 그녀는 안나 아르카지예브나를 알기는 했으나 그저 아주 어렴풋이 알 뿐이었고, 모두가 그토록 높이 칭송하는 이 세련된 페테르부르크 부인을 만난다는 생각에 적잖이 가슴을 두근거리며 언니 집을 찾아왔다. 그러나 그녀는 안나 아르카지예브나에게 호감을 주었다—키티는 그것을 단번에 알아차렸다. 안나는 분명 그녀의 사랑스러움과 젊음을 감탄하며 바라보고 있었다. 키티는 자기가 어찌 된 영문인지 깨닫기도 전에, 그저 안나의 매력에 사로잡힌 정도가 아니라 그녀에게 반해 버린 자신을 발견했다. 어린 처녀들이 나이 든 기혼 여인에게 곧잘 반하듯이. 안나는 세련된 부인 같지도, 여덟 살배기 사내아이의 어머니 같지도 않았다. 그녀의 움직임에 깃든 탄력, 그녀의 얼굴에 어려 미소와 눈빛으로 번지는 그 싱그러움과 가실 줄 모르는 생기를 보면, 키티의 마음을 두드리며 끌어당기던 그 진지하고 때로 우수에 잠긴 눈빛만 아니었다면 차라리 스무 살 아가씨로 보였을 것이다. 키티는 안나가 더없이 소박하고 아무것도 감추지 않는다는 것을, 그러나 자기로서는 닿을 수 없는, 복잡하고 시적인 또 다른 더 높은 관심의 세계를 지니고 있다는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n저녁 식사가 끝나고 돌리가 자기 방으로 물러가자, 안나는 잽싸게 일어나 막 시가에 불을 붙이고 있던 오빠에게 다가갔다.\n\n\"스티바,\" 그녀는 명랑하게 윙크하며, 그에게 성호를 그어 주고는 문 쪽을 흘긋 보며 말했다. \"가, 하느님이 도와주실 거야.\"\n\n그는 그녀의 뜻을 알아채고 시가를 던져 버린 뒤 문을 지나 나갔다.\n\n스테판 아르카지치가 사라지자, 그녀는 아이들에게 둘러싸인 채 앉아 있던 소파로 돌아갔다. 아이들이 자기 엄마가 이 이모를 좋아한다는 것을 알아서인지, 아니면 그들 스스로 그녀에게서 특별한 매력을 느껴서인지, 위의 두 아이와 그 뒤를 따르는 막내까지, 아이들이 흔히 그러듯, 저녁 식사 전부터 이 새 이모에게 들러붙어 그녀 곁을 떠나려 하지 않았다. 그리고 이모에게 되도록 바싹 붙어 앉아 그녀를 만지고, 그 작은 손을 잡고, 거기에 입을 맞추고, 반지를 가지고 놀거나 심지어 치맛단 주름을 만지작거리는 것이 그들 사이에서는 일종의 놀이가 되어 있었다.\n\n\"자, 자, 우리 아까처럼 앉자.\" 안나 아르카지예브나가 제자리에 앉으며 말했다.\n\n그러자 그리샤가 다시 그녀의 팔 밑으로 조그만 얼굴을 들이밀고는, 자랑스럽고 행복해서 환하게 빛나는 얼굴로 그녀의 옷자락에 머리를 기댔다.\n\n\"다음 무도회는 언제니?\" 그녀가 키티에게 물었다.\n\n\"다음 주예요, 아주 멋진 무도회죠. 언제나 즐거운 그런 무도회 중 하나예요.\"\n\n\"아니, 언제나 즐거운 무도회 같은 게 있어?\" 안나가 다정한 비꼼을 섞어 말했다.\n\n\"이상하지만, 있어요. 보브리시체프 댁에서는 언제나 즐겁고, 니키틴 댁에서도 그런데, 메시코프 댁에서는 언제나 따분하거든요. 못 느끼셨어요?\"\n\n\"아니, 얘야, 나한테는 이제 즐거운 무도회 같은 건 없단다.\" 안나가 말했고, 키티는 그녀의 눈 속에서 자기에게는 열리지 않은 그 신비로운 세계를 알아챘다. \"나한테는 덜 따분하고 덜 지겨운 무도회가 있을 뿐이지.\"\n\n\"어떻게 무도회에서 따분하실 수가 있어요?\"\n\n\"왜 내가 무도회에서 따분하면 안 되는데?\" 안나가 물었다.\n\n키티는 안나가 어떤 대답이 나올지 알고 있다는 것을 알아차렸다.\n\n\"언제나 누구보다 아름다우시니까요.\"\n\n안나에게는 얼굴을 붉히는 재주가 있었다. 그녀는 살짝 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다.\n\n\"첫째로 그건 결코 그렇지가 않고, 둘째로 설령 그렇다 한들 그게 나한테 무슨 상관이겠니?\"\n\n\"이번 무도회에 오실 거예요?\" 키티가 물었다.\n\n\"안 갈 수가 없을 것 같구나. 자, 이거 받으렴.\" 그녀가, 가느다란 손가락 끝에서 헐겁게 끼워진 반지를 빼고 있던 타냐에게 말했다.\n\n\"오시면 정말 기쁠 거예요. 무도회에서 이모님을 너무 뵙고 싶어요.\"\n\n\"어쨌든 내가 가게 되면, 그게 너에게 기쁨이 된다는 생각으로 마음을 달래야겠구나…… 그리샤, 머리 잡아당기지 마라. 그러지 않아도 충분히 헝클어졌으니까.\" 그녀가 그리샤가 가지고 놀던, 흘러내린 머리카락 한 가닥을 매만지며 말했다.\n\n\"전 이모님이 연보랏빛 옷을 입고 무도회에 계신 모습을 그려 봐요.\"\n\n\"왜 하필 연보랏빛이니?\" 안나가 미소 지으며 물었다. \"자, 얘들아, 어서 가, 어서 가렴. 들리니? 훌 선생님이 차 마시러 오라고 부르신다.\" 그녀는 아이들을 떼어 내며 식당으로 보냈다.\n\n\"이모님이 왜 저더러 무도회에 오라고 자꾸 채근하시는지 알아요. 이모님은 이 무도회에 큰 기대를 걸고 계셔서, 거기 다들 와서 함께하기를 바라시는 거예요.\"\n\n\"어떻게 알았니? 그래, 맞아.\"\n\n\"아, 너는 참 행복한 시절을 보내고 있구나.\" 안나가 말을 이었다. \"나도 기억하고 있단다. 스위스 산을 덮은 안개 같은 그 푸른 아지랑이를 알아. 어린 시절이 막 끝나 가는 그 더없이 행복한 시절의 모든 것을 감싸는 그 안개 말이야. 행복하고 즐거운 그 드넓은 동그라미에서 점점 좁아져 가는 길 하나가 뻗어 나오고, 그토록 환하고 화려한 무도회장으로 들어서는 일은 황홀하면서도 두렵지…… 그 시절을 안 겪은 사람이 어디 있겠니?\"\n\n키티는 말없이 미소 지었다. '하지만 이모는 어떻게 그 시절을 겪었을까? 이모의 사랑 이야기를 죄다 알 수만 있다면!' 키티는 그녀의 남편인 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 낭만이라곤 없는 모습을 떠올리며 생각했다.\n\n\"나도 좀 알고 있단다. 스티바가 얘기해 주었어, 축하한다. 난 그 사람이 무척 마음에 들더라.\" 안나가 말을 이었다. \"기차역에서 브론스키를 만났어.\"\n\n\"아, 그분이 거기 계셨어요?\" 키티가 얼굴을 붉히며 물었다. \"스티바 오빠가 이모님께 무슨 말을 했어요?\"\n\n\"스티바가 그 이야기를 죄다 떠벌렸지. 그리고 나도 정말 기쁠 거야…… 어제 나는 브론스키의 어머니와 함께 여행했단다.\" 그녀가 말을 이었다. \"그분 어머니는 쉴 새 없이 아들 이야기를 하시더구나, 그 사람이 어머니가 가장 아끼는 아들이거든. 어머니들이란 으레 편애하기 마련인 줄은 알지만……\"\n\n\"그분 어머니가 뭐라고 하셨어요?\"\n\n\"아, 아주 많은 얘기를! 그 사람이 어머니가 가장 아끼는 아들이라는 건 나도 알겠더라. 그래도 그가 얼마나 의협심이 강한지 알 수 있었어…… 그러니까 예를 들면, 그가 자기 재산을 전부 형에게 넘겨주려 했다는 거며, 아주 어릴 적에 물에 빠진 여자를 구해 낸 특별한 일을 했다는 거며. 한마디로 영웅이지.\" 안나가 미소 지으며, 그가 역에서 내놓았던 이백 루블을 떠올리며 말했다.\n\n그러나 그녀는 키티에게 그 이백 루블 이야기는 하지 않았다. 어쩐 일인지 그 일을 생각하는 것이 불쾌했다. 그녀는 거기에 자기와 관련된 무언가가, 그리고 있어서는 안 될 무언가가 있다고 느꼈다.\n\n\"그분 어머니가 한번 찾아와 달라고 무척 채근하셨단다.\" 안나가 말을 이었다. \"그래서 나도 내일 기꺼이 찾아뵐 생각이야. 스티바가 돌리 방에 오래도록 있구나, 다행이지.\" 안나가 화제를 바꾸며 덧붙이고는 일어섰는데, 키티가 보기에 무언가에 언짢아하는 듯했다.\n\n\"아냐, 내가 먼저야! 아냐, 나라니까!\" 차를 다 마신 아이들이 안나 이모에게 달려오며 소리쳤다.\n\n\"다 같이.\" 안나는 그렇게 말하며 웃음을 터뜨리고 아이들에게 마주 달려가, 기뻐서 꺅꺅 소리 지르며 우글거리는 아이들 무리를 모조리 끌어안고 빙글빙글 돌렸다.\n\n# Chapter 21\n\n돌리는 어른들의 차 모임에 나오려고 방에서 나왔다. 스테판 아르카지치는 나오지 않았다. 아마도 다른 문으로 아내의 방을 빠져나간 모양이었다.\n\n\"위층은 추울까 봐 걱정이에요.\" 돌리가 안나를 향해 말했다. \"당신을 아래층으로 옮기고 싶어요. 그러면 우리도 더 가까이 있게 되고요.\"\n\n\"아, 제발 저 때문에 신경 쓰지 마세요.\" 화해가 이루어졌는지 아닌지 가늠해 보려고 돌리의 얼굴을 뚫어지게 들여다보며 안나가 대답했다.\n\n\"여기가 당신한테는 더 밝을 거예요.\" 올케가 대답했다.\n\n\"정말이지 저는 어디서든 잘 자요, 늘 마멋처럼요.\"\n\n\"무슨 이야기지?\" 스테판 아르카지치가 자기 방에서 나오며 아내를 향해 물었다.\n\n그의 말투에서 키티도 안나도 화해가 이루어졌음을 알아챘다.\n\n\"안나를 아래층으로 옮기고 싶은데, 블라인드를 달아야 해요. 그걸 어떻게 다는지 아무도 모르니, 내가 직접 봐야겠어요.\" 돌리가 그를 향해 대답했다.\n\n'정말로 완전히 화해했는지는 하느님만 아시겠지.' 차갑고 차분한 그녀의 말투를 들으며 안나는 생각했다.\n\n\"아, 말도 안 돼, 돌리, 당신은 늘 일을 어렵게 만든단 말이야.\" 남편이 대답했다. \"자, 당신만 좋다면 내가 다 할게…….\"\n\n'그래, 두 사람은 분명 화해한 거야.' 안나는 생각했다.\n\n\"당신이 무슨 일이든 어떻게 하는지 내가 잘 알죠.\" 돌리가 대답했다. \"할 수도 없는 일을 마트베이한테 시켜 놓고는 당신은 가 버리고, 결국 그 사람이 죄다 엉망으로 만들게 내버려 두잖아요.\" 그렇게 말하면서 늘 그렇듯 빈정대는 미소가 돌리의 입꼬리를 휘게 했다.\n\n'완전히, 완전히 화해한 거야, 완전히.' 안나는 생각했다. '아, 다행이야!' 그리고 자기가 그 원인이 된 것을 기뻐하며, 그녀는 돌리에게 다가가 입을 맞추었다.\n\n\"천만에. 당신은 왜 늘 나하고 마트베이를 깔보는 거야?\" 스테판 아르카지치가 거의 알아챌 수 없을 만큼 희미하게 미소 지으며 아내를 향해 말했다.\n\n저녁 내내 돌리는 늘 그렇듯 남편에게 다소 빈정대는 말투를 썼고, 스테판 아르카지치는 행복하고 명랑했지만, 용서받았다고 해서 자기 잘못을 잊어버린 것처럼 보일 정도는 아니었다.\n\n아홉 시 반에 오블론스키 집안의 차 탁자에서 오가던 유난히 즐겁고 화기애애한 가족의 대화는, 겉보기엔 단순한 한 가지 일로 깨지고 말았다. 그러나 이 단순한 일은 무슨 까닭에서인지 모두에게 이상하게 느껴졌다. 페테르부르크의 공통 지인들에 관한 이야기를 하다가 안나가 재빨리 일어섰다.\n\n\"그 여자는 내 앨범에 있어요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그리고 이참에 우리 세료자도 보여 드릴게요.\" 그녀는 자랑스러운 어머니의 미소를 띠며 덧붙였다.\n\n평소 열 시쯤이면 그녀는 아들에게 잘 자라는 인사를 했고, 무도회에 가기 전에도 종종 손수 아들을 재우곤 했는데, 지금은 아들과 이토록 멀리 떨어져 있다는 사실에 마음이 울적했다. 그래서 무슨 이야기를 하든 머릿속에서는 자꾸만 곱슬머리의 세료자에게로 되돌아갔다. 그녀는 아들의 사진을 보고, 아들에 관해 이야기하고 싶어 견딜 수가 없었다. 첫 번째 구실을 잡아채자마자 그녀는 일어나, 그 가볍고 단호한 걸음으로 앨범을 가지러 갔다. 그녀의 방으로 올라가는 계단은 따뜻하고 큼직한 본계단의 층계참으로 이어져 있었다.\n\n그녀가 막 응접실을 나서려던 참에 현관에서 초인종 소리가 들렸다.\n\n\"누굴까요?\" 돌리가 말했다.\n\n\"저를 데리러 오기엔 이르고, 다른 누군가가 오기엔 늦은 시간이네요.\" 키티가 말했다.\n\n\"분명 내게 서류를 가져온 사람일 거야.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 끼어들었다. 안나가 계단 꼭대기를 지날 때, 하인 하나가 손님이 왔음을 알리려고 뛰어 올라오고 있었고, 그 손님은 등불 아래에 서 있었다. 아래를 흘끗 내려다본 안나는 단번에 브론스키를 알아보았고, 기쁨과 동시에 무언가에 대한 두려움이라는 야릇한 감정이 그녀의 가슴속에서 일렁였다. 그는 외투도 벗지 않고 가만히 서서 주머니에서 무언가를 꺼내고 있었다. 그녀가 막 계단을 마주한 순간, 그가 눈을 들어 그녀를 보았고, 그의 얼굴 표정에는 당혹과 낭패의 그늘이 스쳐 지나갔다. 그녀는 살짝 고개를 숙여 인사하고는 지나쳐 갔는데, 등 뒤에서 스테판 아르카지치가 올라오라고 큰 소리로 부르는 것과, 그것을 거절하는 브론스키의 조용하고 부드럽고 차분한 목소리가 들려왔다.\n\n안나가 앨범을 가지고 돌아왔을 때 그는 이미 가 버린 뒤였고, 스테판 아르카지치는 그가 막 도착한 어느 명사를 위해 내일 열기로 한 만찬에 관해 물어보러 들렀던 것이라고 이야기하고 있었다. \"그런데 무슨 수를 써도 올라오려 하질 않더라고. 참 별난 친구야!\" 스테판 아르카지치가 덧붙였다.\n\n키티는 얼굴을 붉혔다. 그가 왜 왔는지, 그리고 왜 올라오려 하지 않았는지 아는 사람은 자기뿐이라고 그녀는 생각했다. '그이는 우리 집에 갔다가,' 그녀는 생각했다, '나를 못 찾았고, 내가 여기 있을 거라고 짐작한 거야. 하지만 시간이 늦었고 안나도 여기 있으니까 올라오지 않은 거지.'\n\n그들은 모두 아무 말 없이 서로를 바라보다가, 안나의 앨범을 보기 시작했다.\n\n어떤 남자가 아홉 시 반에 친구를 찾아와 예정된 만찬에 관한 세세한 사정을 물어보고는 안으로 들어오지 않았다는 데에 유별나거나 이상할 것은 전혀 없었지만, 그 일은 그들 모두에게 이상하게 여겨졌다. 무엇보다도 안나에게 그것은 이상하고 온당치 못한 일로 느껴졌다.\n\n# Chapter 22\n\n무도회는 키티와 어머니가 환한 불빛이 넘쳐흐르고 꽃과 분을 바른 빨간 제복 차림의 하인들이 늘어선 큰 계단을 올라갈 무렵에야 막 시작되고 있었다. 방마다 벌집에서 나는 것 같은 끊임없고 고른 웅성거림과 움직임의 살랑거리는 소리가 흘러나왔고, 나무들 사이의 층계참에서 거울 앞에 서서 머리와 옷매무새에 마지막 손질을 하는 동안, 두 사람은 무도회장에서 오케스트라의 바이올린들이 첫 왈츠를 시작하며 또렷이 신경 써서 켜는 음을 들었다. 다른 거울 앞에서 희끗한 곱슬머리를 매만지며 향수 냄새를 풍기던 평복 차림의 작은 노인이 계단에서 두 사람과 부딪치고는 옆으로 비켜서서, 누군지도 모르는 키티를 보고 분명 감탄하고 있었다. 늙은 셰르바츠키 공작이 \"젊은 멋쟁이\"라고 부르는 사교계 청년들 중 하나인 수염 없는 젊은이가, 한껏 깊이 파인 조끼 차림으로 가면서 흰 넥타이를 바로잡으며 두 사람에게 인사하더니, 지나쳤다가 도로 돌아와 키티에게 카드리유를 청했다. 첫 번째 카드리유는 이미 브론스키에게 약속해 두었기에, 그녀는 이 젊은이에게 두 번째 카드리유를 약속해야 했다. 한 장교가 장갑 단추를 채우며 문간에 비켜서서, 콧수염을 쓰다듬으며 발그레한 키티를 바라보고 있었다.\n\n비록 그녀의 옷과 머리 모양, 그리고 무도회를 위한 모든 준비가 키티에게 적잖은 수고와 고심을 안겨 주었지만, 이 순간 그녀는 분홍빛 속치마 위에 정교한 튈 드레스를 걸치고, 그 모든 로제트며 레이스며 옷차림의 자잘한 세부가 그녀나 그녀의 가족에게 단 한순간의 신경도 쓰게 한 적이 없다는 듯이, 마치 그 튈과 레이스를 입은 채로, 머리를 높이 틀어 올리고 그 위에 장미 한 송이와 잎새 두 장을 얹은 채로 태어나기라도 한 듯이, 그토록 수월하고 단순하게 무도회장으로 걸어 들어갔다.\n\n무도회장에 들어서기 직전, 어머니인 공작 부인이 띠의 리본을 겉으로 바로잡아 주려 하자, 키티는 살짝 몸을 뒤로 뺐다. 그녀는 모든 것이 저절로 제대로 되어 있고 우아하며, 무엇 하나 바로잡을 필요가 없으리라고 느꼈다.\n\n그날은 키티에게 가장 좋은 날 가운데 하루였다. 드레스는 어디 하나 거북한 데가 없었고, 레이스로 된 베르타는 어디 하나 늘어지지 않았으며, 로제트는 짓눌리거나 뜯겨 나가지 않았고, 높고 안쪽이 옴폭한 굽이 달린 분홍 슬리퍼는 발을 죄지 않고 도리어 발을 즐겁게 했다. 빛깔 고운 가발의 도톰한 타래는 마치 제 머리카락인 듯 머리 위에 잘 얹혀 있었다. 손의 선을 가리지 않으면서 손을 덮은 긴 장갑의 단추 세 개는 하나도 뜯기지 않고 다 채워졌다. 로켓을 단 검은 벨벳 끈은 유난히 부드럽게 그녀의 목을 감싸고 있었다. 그 벨벳은 황홀했다. 집에서 거울에 비친 자기 목을 들여다보며, 키티는 그 벨벳이 말을 거는 것만 같았다. 다른 모든 것에는 의심이 갈지 몰라도, 벨벳만큼은 황홀했다. 무도회장에서도 키티는 거울에 비친 그것을 흘끗 보고 다시 미소를 지었다. 드러난 어깨와 팔은 키티에게 차가운 대리석 같은 느낌을 주었는데, 그것은 그녀가 유난히 좋아하는 감각이었다. 그녀의 눈이 반짝였고, 자신의 매력을 의식한 탓에 발그레한 입술은 미소를 참을 수가 없었다. 그녀가 무도회장에 들어서서 춤 신청을 기다리는, 온통 튈과 리본과 레이스와 꽃으로 뒤덮인 여인들의 무리—키티는 결코 그 무리에 속한 적이 없었다—에 채 닿기도 전에, 그녀에게 왈츠 신청이 들어왔고, 그것도 가장 훌륭한 파트너, 무도회장의 서열에서 으뜸가는 별이며 이름난 무도 지휘자이자 기혼이면서 잘생기고 균형 잡힌 몸매를 가진 예고루시카 코르순스키가 신청한 것이었다. 그는 바로 조금 전, 보니나 백작 부인과 왈츠의 전반부를 추다가 막 그녀 곁을 떠난 참이었고, 자기 왕국—그러니까 춤을 추기 시작한 몇 쌍의 남녀—을 둘러보다가 들어오는 키티를 발견하고는, 무도회 지휘자들에게만 있는 그 독특하고 가뿐한 종종걸음으로 그녀에게 날아왔다. 그녀가 춤을 추고 싶은지 묻지도 않은 채, 그는 그녀의 가는 허리를 감으려고 팔을 내밀었다. 그녀가 부채를 맡길 사람을 찾아 두리번거리자, 그녀에게 미소 짓던 안주인이 그것을 받아 주었다.\n\n\"제때 와 주셔서 참 좋군요.\" 그가 그녀의 허리를 감싸며 말했다. \"늦는 건 아주 나쁜 버릇이지요.\" 그녀는 왼손을 굽혀 그의 어깨에 얹었고, 분홍 슬리퍼를 신은 자그마한 발이 음악에 맞추어 미끄러운 마룻바닥 위를 재빠르고 가뿐하며 리듬감 있게 움직이기 시작했다.\n\n\"당신과 왈츠를 추면 마치 쉬는 것 같군요.\" 그들이 왈츠의 첫 느린 스텝으로 접어들 때 그가 말했다. \"절묘해요. 이 가벼움, 이 정확함이라니.\" 그는 잘 아는 거의 모든 파트너에게 하던 말을 그녀에게 그대로 했다.\n\n그녀는 그의 칭찬에 미소 지으면서도, 그의 어깨 너머로 계속 방 안을 둘러보았다. 그녀는 무도회장의 모든 얼굴이 하나의 동화 같은 환영으로 녹아드는, 첫 무도회에 나온 소녀 같지 않았다. 또한 무도회의 진부한 순례를 거듭한 끝에 무도회장의 모든 얼굴이 낯익고 지겨워진 소녀도 아니었다. 그녀는 이 둘 사이의 중간 단계에 있었다. 들떠 있으면서도 동시에 관찰할 수 있을 만큼의 침착함을 갖추고 있었던 것이다. 무도회장의 왼쪽 구석에서 그녀는 사교계의 정수가 한데 모여 있는 것을 보았다. 그곳에는—믿을 수 없으리만치 드러낸 차림으로—코르순스키의 아내인 미인 리디가 있었고, 안주인이 있었으며, 가장 훌륭한 사람들이 모인 곳이라면 늘 나타나는 크리빈의 대머리가 빛나고 있었다. 젊은 남자들은 감히 다가서지 못한 채 그쪽을 바라보고 있었다. 그곳에서 그녀는 스티바도 찾아냈고, 검은 벨벳 드레스를 입은 안나의 절묘한 자태와 두상도 보았다. 그리고 그가 거기 있었다. 키티는 레빈을 거절한 그날 저녁 이후로 그를 보지 못했다. 멀리 보는 눈으로 그녀는 단번에 그를 알아보았고, 심지어 그가 자기를 바라보고 있다는 것까지 알아챘다.\n\n\"한 바퀴 더 돌까요, 응? 지치진 않으셨고요?\" 코르순스키가 약간 숨을 헐떡이며 말했다.\n\n\"아뇨, 괜찮아요!\"\n\n\"어디로 모실까요?\"\n\n\"카레니나 부인이 저기 계신 것 같은데…… 그분께 데려다주세요.\"\n\n\"분부대로 어디든지요.\"\n\n그리고 코르순스키는 절도 있는 스텝으로 왼쪽 구석의 무리를 향해 곧장 왈츠를 추어 나가면서, \"Pardon, mesdames, pardon, pardon, mesdames\"라고 끊임없이 되뇌었고, 레이스와 튈과 리본의 바다를 헤치고 나아가면서도 깃털 하나 흐트러뜨리지 않은 채 파트너를 홱 돌렸는데, 그 바람에 가벼운 비치는 스타킹에 감싸인 그녀의 가느다란 발목이 드러났고 그녀의 트레인이 부채꼴로 펼쳐지며 크리빈의 무릎을 덮었다. 코르순스키는 절을 하고, 풀어진 셔츠 앞섶을 바로잡고는, 그녀를 안나 아르카지예브나에게로 데려가려고 팔을 내밀었다. 얼굴이 발그레해진 키티는 크리빈의 무릎에서 트레인을 거두어들이고, 약간 어지러워하며 안나를 찾아 주위를 둘러보았다. 안나는 키티가 그토록 간절히 바랐던 라일락 빛깔이 아니라, 풍만한 목과 어깨를 드러낸 검은색의 깊이 파인 벨벳 드레스 차림이었는데, 그 목과 어깨는 마치 오래된 상아로 깎아 놓은 듯했고, 둥근 팔에는 자그맣고 가느다란 손목이 달려 있었다. 드레스 전체에 베네치아산 기퓌르 레이스가 둘려 있었다. 머리에는, 가발을 덧대지 않은 그녀 자신의 검은 머리카락 사이에, 팬지꽃으로 엮은 작은 화관이 얹혀 있었고, 흰 레이스 사이 띠의 검은 리본에도 같은 꽃다발이 달려 있었다. 그녀의 머리 모양은 눈에 띄지 않았다. 눈에 띄는 것이라곤 목덜미와 관자놀이 언저리에서 늘 제멋대로 풀려 나오곤 하는 곱슬한 작은 잔머리뿐이었다. 잘 빠진 단단한 목에는 진주 한 줄이 둘려 있었다.\n\n키티는 날마다 안나를 보아 왔고, 그녀를 흠모했으며, 한결같이 라일락 빛깔을 입은 모습으로 그녀를 그려 왔다. 그러나 이제 검은 옷을 입은 그녀를 보고서, 키티는 자기가 그녀의 매력을 온전히 보지 못했음을 느꼈다. 이제 그녀는 안나를 전혀 새롭고 놀라운 사람으로 보게 되었다. 이제 그녀는 안나가 라일락 빛깔을 입을 수 없는 사람이며, 그녀의 매력이란 바로 그녀가 늘 자기 옷차림을 압도하며 도드라진다는 데에, 그녀가 입은 옷이 결코 그녀 위에서 눈에 띌 수 없다는 데에 있음을 깨달았다. 호화로운 레이스가 달린 그녀의 검은 드레스도 그녀 위에서 눈에 띄지 않았다. 그것은 단지 액자일 뿐이었고, 보이는 것이라곤 오로지 그녀—소박하고 자연스럽고 우아하며 동시에 명랑하고 생기 넘치는 그녀뿐이었다.\n\n그녀는 언제나처럼 몸을 아주 꼿꼿이 세운 채 서 있었고, 키티가 그 무리에 다가갔을 때 그녀는 머리를 약간 그쪽으로 돌린 채 주인에게 이야기하고 있었다.\n\n\"아뇨, 저는 돌을 던지지 않아요.\" 무언가에 대답하며 그녀가 말하고 있었다. \"비록 이해할 수는 없지만요.\" 그녀는 어깨를 으쓱하며 말을 이었고, 곧 부드럽게 감싸 주는 듯한 미소를 띠며 키티 쪽으로 돌아섰다. 날렵하고 여성스러운 눈길로 그녀는 키티의 옷차림을 훑어보고는, 거의 알아챌 수 없을 만큼 미세하지만 키티는 알아챈, 그녀의 옷과 용모를 인정한다는 뜻의 고갯짓을 했다. \"춤을 추며 방으로 들어오시던걸요.\" 그녀가 덧붙였다.\n\n\"이분은 제 가장 충실한 후원자 가운데 한 분이지요.\" 아직 만난 적이 없던 안나 아르카지예브나에게 절을 하며 코르순스키가 말했다. \"이 영애님은 무도회를 즐겁고 성공적으로 만드는 데 일조하시지요. 안나 아르카지예브나, 왈츠 한 곡 어떠십니까?\" 그가 그녀에게 몸을 굽히며 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 두 분은 서로 아는 사이였소?\" 주인이 물었다.\n\n\"저희가 모르는 사람이 어디 있겠습니까? 제 아내와 저는 흰 늑대 같은 존재지요. 누구나 다 저희를 알아본답니다.\" 코르순스키가 대답했다. \"왈츠 한 곡, 안나 아르카지예브나?\"\n\n\"안 춰도 될 때면 저는 춤을 추지 않는답니다.\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 오늘 밤은 안 출 수가 없지요.\" 코르순스키가 대답했다.\n\n바로 그 순간 브론스키가 다가왔다.\n\n\"그럼, 오늘 밤은 안 출 수가 없다니, 시작하지요.\" 그녀는 브론스키의 인사를 알아채지 못한 채 말하고는, 황급히 코르순스키의 어깨에 손을 얹었다.\n\n'무엇 때문에 그에게 화가 난 걸까?' 안나가 일부러 브론스키의 인사에 응하지 않았다는 것을 알아채고 키티는 생각했다. 브론스키는 키티에게 다가와 첫 번째 카드리유를 일깨우며, 이렇게 오랫동안 그녀를 보지 못해 유감이었다고 말했다. 키티는 왈츠를 추는 안나를 감탄 어린 눈길로 바라보면서 그의 말을 들었다. 그녀는 그가 왈츠를 청하리라 기대했지만 그는 청하지 않았고, 그녀는 의아한 눈으로 그를 흘끗 보았다. 그는 살짝 얼굴을 붉히더니 서둘러 왈츠를 청했지만, 그가 막 그녀의 허리에 팔을 두르고 첫 스텝을 뗀 순간 음악이 별안간 멈춰 버렸다. 키티는 자기 얼굴과 그토록 가까이 있던 그의 얼굴을 들여다보았는데, 그 후로 오랫동안—몇 해가 지나도록—그가 아무런 응답도 하지 않았던, 사랑으로 가득 찬 그 눈길은 수치의 고통으로 그녀의 가슴을 후벼 팠다.\n\n\"Pardon! pardon! 왈츠! 왈츠!\" 코르순스키가 방 저편에서 소리치며, 마주친 첫 번째 아가씨를 붙잡고 자기가 직접 춤을 추기 시작했다.\n\n# Chapter 23\n\n브론스키와 키티는 홀을 몇 바퀴 돌며 왈츠를 추었다. 첫 왈츠가 끝난 뒤 키티는 어머니에게로 갔는데, 노르츠톤 백작부인과 몇 마디 나눌 틈도 없이 브론스키가 첫 카드리유를 청하러 다시 다가왔다. 카드리유를 추는 동안에는 별다른 의미 있는 말이 오가지 않았다. 두 사람 사이에는 코르순스키 부부에 관한 끊어진 이야기가 오갔는데, 그는 그들을 마흔 살에도 사랑스러운 아이들 같다며 아주 재미나게 묘사했고, 또 앞으로 들어설 시립 극장에 관한 이야기도 했다. 그리고 단 한 번 대화가 그녀의 가슴 한구석을 찔렀는데, 그가 레빈에 관해 그가 여기 와 있는지 물으면서 자기는 그를 무척 좋아한다고 덧붙였을 때였다. 하지만 키티는 카드리유에 큰 기대를 걸지 않았다. 그녀는 가슴을 두근거리며 마주르카를 고대하고 있었다. 마주르카에서 모든 것이 결정되리라고 그녀는 상상했다. 그가 카드리유를 추는 동안 마주르카를 청하지 않았다는 사실은 그녀를 불안하게 하지 않았다. 그녀는 예전 무도회에서 그랬듯이 그와 마주르카를 추리라고 확신했고, 마주르카는 이미 약속이 되어 있다고 말하며 다섯 명의 젊은이를 거절했다. 마지막 카드리유에 이르기까지 무도회 전체가 키티에게는 즐거운 색채와 소리와 움직임으로 이루어진 황홀한 환영이었다. 그녀는 너무 지쳐서 잠시 쉬게 해 달라고 청할 때만 자리에 앉았다. 그런데 차마 거절할 수 없었던 따분한 젊은이 가운데 한 사람과 마지막 카드리유를 추다가, 그녀는 우연히 브론스키와 안나와 마주 보는 자리에 서게 되었다. 저녁이 시작된 이후로 안나 곁에 다시 가 본 적이 없었던 그녀는, 이제 또다시 안나가 어딘가 완전히 새롭고 놀라운 모습으로 보였다. 그녀는 안나에게서 자기 자신이 너무도 잘 아는 그 성공의 흥분의 징후들을 보았다. 자기가 불러일으킨 기쁨에 찬 찬탄에 안나가 취해 있는 것을 보았다. 그녀는 그 감정을 알았고 그 징후들을 알았으며, 그것들을 안나에게서 보았다. 그녀의 두 눈에서 떨리며 번득이는 빛을, 입가에 자기도 모르게 어리는 행복과 흥분의 미소를, 그리고 그녀의 동작이 지닌 의도된 우아함과 정확함과 가벼움을 보았다.\n\n\"누구지?\" 그녀는 자신에게 물었다. \"모두에게 그러는 걸까, 아니면 한 사람에게?\" 그러고는 함께 춤추던 시달린 젊은이가 대화의 실마리를 놓쳐 다시 잡지 못하는 것을 도와주지도 않은 채, 그녀는 겉으로만 활기차게, 모두를 grand rond로 이끌었다가 다시 chaîne로 몰아넣는 코르순스키의 단호한 외침에 따랐고, 그러는 동안에도 점점 더 커지는 가슴의 통증을 느끼며 지켜보았다. \"아니야, 그녀를 취하게 한 건 군중의 찬탄이 아니라 단 한 사람의 흠모야. 그런데 그 한 사람이라면? 정말 그 사람일까?\" 그가 안나에게 말을 건넬 때마다 안나의 눈에 기쁨의 빛이 번득였고, 행복의 미소가 그녀의 붉은 입술을 휘게 했다. 그녀는 스스로를 억누르려고, 이 기쁨의 징후들을 드러내지 않으려고 애쓰는 듯했지만, 그것들은 저절로 그녀의 얼굴에 떠올랐다. \"하지만 그는 어떨까?\" 키티는 그를 바라보았고 공포에 사로잡혔다. 안나의 얼굴이라는 거울에 그토록 또렷하게 비치던 것을 그녀는 그에게서도 보았다. 그의 늘 침착하고 단호하던 태도와, 무심하리만큼 평온하던 얼굴 표정은 어디로 사라진 것일까? 이제 그는 그녀에게로 돌아설 때마다 마치 그녀의 발치에라도 엎드릴 것처럼 고개를 숙였고, 그의 눈에는 겸허한 복종과 두려움밖에 없었다. \"당신을 거스르고 싶지 않습니다.\" 그의 눈은 매번 이렇게 말하는 듯했다. \"하지만 나는 나 자신을 구하고 싶고, 그런데 어떻게 해야 할지 모르겠습니다.\" 그의 얼굴에는 키티가 이전에 한 번도 본 적 없는 표정이 떠올라 있었다.\n\n두 사람은 서로 아는 사람들에 관해 더할 나위 없이 하찮은 이야기를 나누고 있었지만, 키티에게는 그들이 주고받는 모든 말이 그들의 운명과 자기 운명을 결정짓고 있는 것처럼 여겨졌다. 그리고 이상하게도 실제로 그들은 이반 이바노비치가 프랑스어를 쓰는 게 얼마나 우스꽝스러운지, 옐레츠키 집안의 딸이 어떻게 더 나은 혼처를 잡을 수도 있었을지를 이야기하고 있었는데도, 그 말들은 내내 두 사람에게 어떤 의미를 지니고 있었고, 그들도 키티와 똑같이 느끼고 있었다. 키티의 마음속에서는 무도회 전체가, 온 세상이, 모든 것이 안개 속으로 사라진 듯했다. 오직 그녀가 받아 온 엄격한 교육의 단련만이 그녀를 지탱해 주며, 사람들이 그녀에게 기대하는 것을, 다시 말해 춤을 추고, 질문에 답하고, 이야기하고, 심지어 미소까지 짓도록 강요했다. 그러나 마주르카가 시작되기 전, 의자들을 다시 늘어놓기 시작하고 몇 쌍이 작은 방들에서 큰 홀로 나올 무렵, 키티에게 절망과 공포의 순간이 찾아왔다. 그녀는 다섯 명의 파트너를 거절했는데, 이제 마주르카를 출 상대가 없었다. 마주르카를 청해 올 것이라는 희망조차 없었으니, 그녀가 사교계에서 너무도 인기가 좋았던 탓에 그녀가 지금까지 상대 없이 남아 있으리라는 생각은 누구의 머릿속에도 떠오르지 않을 터였다. 어머니에게 몸이 안 좋다고 말하고 집으로 돌아가야 했지만, 그녀에게는 그럴 기운조차 없었다. 그녀는 무너지는 기분이었다. 그녀는 작은 응접실의 가장 안쪽으로 가서 낮은 의자에 주저앉았다. 가볍고 비치는 치맛자락이 그녀의 가느다란 허리를 감싸며 구름처럼 부풀어 올랐다. 맨살이 드러난 가늘고 부드러운 소녀다운 한쪽 팔은 맥없이 늘어진 채 분홍빛 튜닉 주름 속에 묻혀 있었고, 다른 손에는 부채를 들고 짧고 빠른 부채질로 화끈거리는 얼굴을 식히고 있었다. 그러나 풀잎에 매달려 무지갯빛 날개를 활짝 펼치고 새로운 비상에 나서려는 나비처럼 보이는 그 순간에도, 그녀의 가슴은 끔찍한 절망으로 아파 왔다.\n\n\"하지만 어쩌면 내가 잘못 본 걸지도 몰라, 어쩌면 그게 아니었을지도?\" 그러고는 그녀는 자기가 본 모든 것을 다시 떠올렸다.\n\n\"키티, 무슨 일이니?\" 노르츠톤 백작부인이 카펫 위로 소리 없이 다가서며 말했다. \"도무지 모르겠구나.\"\n\n키티의 아랫입술이 떨리기 시작했고, 그녀는 얼른 일어섰다.\n\n\"키티, 너 마주르카 안 추니?\"\n\n\"네, 안 춰요.\" 키티가 눈물로 떨리는 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"그가 나보다 먼저 그녀에게 마주르카를 청하더구나.\" 노르츠톤 백작부인이 키티가 '그'와 '그녀'가 누구인지 알아들으리라는 것을 알고서 말했다. \"그러니까 그녀가 이러더라. '아니, 시체르바츠카야 공작 영애와 추시는 거 아니었어요?'\"\n\n\"아, 전 상관없어요!\" 키티가 대답했다.\n\n그녀 자신 말고는 아무도 그녀의 처지를 이해하지 못했다. 그녀가 어쩌면 사랑하는지도 모를 남자를 방금 거절했다는 것을, 그것도 다른 한 사람을 믿었기에 거절했다는 것을 아는 사람은 아무도 없었다.\n\n노르츠톤 백작부인은 함께 마주르카를 추기로 한 코르순스키를 찾아내어 키티에게 청하라고 말했다.\n\n키티는 첫 번째 쌍에서 춤을 추었는데, 다행스럽게도 그녀는 말을 할 필요가 없었으니, 코르순스키가 내내 이리저리 뛰어다니며 춤의 대형을 지휘하고 있었기 때문이다. 브론스키와 안나는 거의 그녀의 맞은편에 앉아 있었다. 그녀는 멀리 보는 눈으로 그들을 바라보았고, 대형 속에서 마주칠 때면 가까이서도 그들을 보았는데, 그들을 보면 볼수록 자기 불행이 완전한 것임을 더욱 확신하게 되었다. 그녀는 그들이 사람들로 붐비는 그 홀 안에서 자기들 둘만 있는 것처럼 느낀다는 것을 알아챘다. 그리고 늘 그토록 단단하고 자주적이던 브론스키의 얼굴에서, 그녀의 마음을 쳤던 그 표정을, 잘못을 저지른 영리한 개의 표정 같은 당혹과 겸허한 복종의 빛을 보았다.\n\n안나가 미소 지으면 그 미소가 그에게로 반사되었다. 그녀가 생각에 잠기면 그도 진지해졌다. 어떤 초자연적인 힘이 키티의 눈길을 안나의 얼굴로 끌어당겼다. 그 수수한 검은 드레스 차림의 그녀는 매혹적이었고, 팔찌를 두른 그 둥근 두 팔도 매혹적이었으며, 진주 한 줄을 두른 그 탄탄한 목도 매혹적이었고, 풀어 헝클어진 머리의 흘러내린 곱슬머리도 매혹적이었으며, 작은 발과 손의 우아하고 가벼운 움직임도 매혹적이었고, 열기에 찬 그 사랑스러운 얼굴도 매혹적이었지만, 그 매혹에는 어딘가 무섭고 잔인한 무언가가 있었다.\n\n키티는 그 어느 때보다 그녀에게 감탄했고, 그럴수록 그녀의 고통은 점점 더 날카로워졌다. 키티는 짓눌리는 기분이었고, 그것이 얼굴에 그대로 드러났다. 브론스키는 마주르카에서 그녀와 마주쳤을 때, 그녀가 너무도 달라져 있어 한눈에 알아보지 못했다.\n\n\"멋진 무도회죠!\" 그는 무슨 말이라도 하려고 그녀에게 건넸다.\n\n\"네.\" 그녀가 대답했다.\n\n마주르카 중간에, 코르순스키가 새로 고안한 복잡한 대형을 되풀이하던 중, 안나가 원의 한가운데로 나아가 두 신사를 고르고, 한 부인과 키티를 불렀다. 키티는 다가가며 당혹스러운 눈으로 그녀를 바라보았다. 안나는 눈꺼풀을 내리뜬 채 그녀를 바라보며 미소 지었고, 그녀의 손을 꼭 쥐었다. 하지만 키티가 자기 미소에 절망과 경악의 눈빛으로만 응한다는 것을 알아채고는, 그녀에게서 몸을 돌려 다른 부인과 즐겁게 이야기하기 시작했다.\n\n\"그래, 저 여자에게는 어딘가 섬뜩하고 악마적이며 매혹적인 무언가가 있어.\" 키티는 속으로 중얼거렸다.\n\n안나는 야식 자리에 남을 생각이 없었지만, 집주인이 남아 달라고 조르기 시작했다.\n\n\"말도 안 됩니다, 안나 아르카지예브나.\" 코르순스키가 그녀의 맨팔을 자기 연미복 소맷자락 아래로 끌어당기며 말했다. \"코티용을 위해 기막힌 생각이 떠올랐거든요! Un bijou!\"\n\n그러고는 그녀를 함께 끌고 가려 애쓰며 조금씩 앞으로 나아갔다. 집주인은 흐뭇한 미소를 지었다.\n\n\"아니에요, 전 남지 않겠어요.\" 안나가 미소 지으며 대답했지만, 그 미소에도 불구하고 코르순스키도 집주인도 그녀의 단호한 어조에서 그녀가 남지 않으리라는 것을 알아챘다.\n\n\"아니요, 이런 식이라면, 전 페테르부르크에서 겨우내 춘 것보다 모스크바 당신 무도회에서 더 많이 춤을 췄는걸요.\" 안나가 곁에 서 있던 브론스키를 돌아보며 말했다. \"여행을 떠나기 전에 좀 쉬어 둬야 해요.\"\n\n\"그럼 내일 정말로 떠나시는 겁니까?\" 브론스키가 물었다.\n\n\"네, 그럴 것 같아요.\" 안나는 그의 질문이 대담하다는 듯 대답했다. 하지만 그녀가 그 말을 하는 순간, 억누를 수 없이 떨리며 빛나는 그녀의 두 눈과 미소가 그를 불태웠다.\n\n안나 아르카지예브나는 야식 자리에 남지 않고 집으로 돌아갔다.\n\n# Chapter 24\n\n“그래, 내 안에는 뭔가 밉살스럽고 역겨운 데가 있어.” 레빈은 셰르바츠키가에서 나와 형의 하숙집 쪽으로 걸어가며 생각했다. “게다가 난 다른 사람들과 잘 어울리지도 못해. 자존심 때문이라고들 하지. 아니, 난 자존심 같은 거 없어. 자존심이 조금이라도 있었다면 나 자신을 이런 처지에 몰아넣지도 않았겠지.” 그러면서 그는 행복하고 호인이며 영리하고 침착한 브론스키를, 오늘 저녁 자기가 처했던 그 끔찍한 처지에는 결코 빠질 리 없는 그 사람을 떠올렸다. “그래, 그녀는 그를 택할 수밖에 없었어. 그렇게 되어야 했던 거고, 난 누구도 무엇도 탓할 수 없어. 내 잘못이지. 그녀가 자기 인생을 내 인생에 묶고 싶어 할 거라고 상상할 권리가 내게 어디 있었나? 난 누구고 또 뭔데? 아무것도 아닌 인간, 누구에게도 필요치 않고 누구에게도 쓸모없는 인간이지.” 그러고는 형 니콜라이를 떠올리며 그 생각에 즐겁게 잠겼다. “세상의 모든 것이 비루하고 혐오스럽다는 형의 말이 옳지 않은가? 그리고 우리가 니콜라이 형을 두고 내리는 판단이 과연 공정할까? 물론 프로코피의 눈으로, 찢어진 외투를 걸치고 술에 취한 그를 본다면, 그는 멸시받아 마땅한 인간이겠지. 하지만 난 그를 다르게 알아. 그의 영혼을 알고, 우리도 그와 같은 인간이라는 걸 알지. 그런데 난 그를 찾아갈 생각은 하지 않고 만찬에 나갔다가 여기로 왔단 말이야.” 레빈은 가로등 기둥으로 다가가 수첩에 적힌 형의 주소를 읽고 썰매를 불렀다. 형의 집까지 가는 긴 길 내내 레빈은 형 니콜라이의 삶에 관해 잘 알고 있던 사실들을 생생히 떠올렸다. 형이 대학에 다닐 때, 그리고 졸업 후 일 년 동안 동료들의 조롱에도 불구하고 수도사처럼 살았던 것을, 모든 종교 의식과 예배와 단식을 엄격히 지키며 온갖 쾌락, 특히 여자를 멀리했던 것을 떠올렸다. 그러다 별안간 어떻게 폭발해 버렸는지도—가장 끔찍한 인간들과 어울리며 더없이 무분별한 방탕에 빠져들었던 일을 떠올렸다. 또 시골에서 데려와 키우려던 소년을 둘러싼 추문도 기억났다. 형은 분노의 발작에 사로잡혀 그 아이를 어찌나 심하게 때렸던지 불법 상해죄로 고소까지 당했다. 그다음에는 사기 도박꾼과 얽힌 추문을 떠올렸다. 형은 그자에게 돈을 잃고 차용증을 써 주었으면서도, 그자가 자기를 속였다고 주장하며 도리어 고소를 했던 것이다. (이때 갚아 준 돈이 바로 세르게이 이바노비치가 낸 것이었다.) 또 형이 거리에서 행패를 부린 죄로 유치장에서 하룻밤을 보낸 일도 떠올렸다. 어머니 재산 중 자기 몫을 주지 않았다며 형 세르게이 이바노비치를 걸어 일으키려 했던 그 수치스러운 소송도, 그리고 관리 자격으로 서부 어느 지방에 갔다가 거기서 촌장을 폭행해 곤경에 처했던 마지막 추문도 떠올렸다……. 그 모든 일이 끔찍하게 역겨웠다. 하지만 레빈에게는 그것이, 니콜라이를 모르고 그의 모든 사연을 모르고 그의 마음을 모르는 이들에게 비칠 그 역겨운 빛깔로는 결코 보이지 않았다.\n\n레빈은 니콜라이가 독실하던 시절, 단식과 수도사와 교회 예배의 시기, 그러니까 종교에서 자기 격정적 기질을 떠받칠 버팀목이자 그것을 억누를 재갈을 찾던 그 시절에, 누구도 그를 격려하기는커녕 모두가 그를 조롱했고, 자신도 다른 이들과 함께 그랬다는 사실을 떠올렸다. 사람들은 형을 놀려 대며 노아니 수도사니 불렀고, 형이 폭발해 버렸을 때 아무도 그를 돕지 않은 채 모두가 공포와 혐오로 그에게서 등을 돌렸던 것이다.\n\n레빈은 형 니콜라이가, 그의 삶이 아무리 추악하더라도, 그 영혼에서, 그 영혼의 가장 깊은 곳에서는, 자기를 멸시하는 사람들보다 조금도 더 그릇되지 않다고 느꼈다. 형이 고삐 풀린 기질과 어딘가 한정된 지성을 지니고 태어난 것은 그의 죄가 아니었다. 그러나 그는 늘 선해지기를 원했다. ‘형한테 숨김없이 다 털어놓을 거야. 그리고 형도 숨김없이 이야기하게 만들 거야. 내가 형을 사랑하고 그래서 형을 이해한다는 걸 보여 줄 거야.’ 레빈은 열한 시가 다 되어 주소에 적힌 호텔에 이르렀을 때 이렇게 마음먹었다.\n\n“맨 위층, 12호하고 13호입니다.” 문지기가 레빈의 물음에 대답했다.\n\n“계신가요?”\n\n“계실 게 틀림없습니다.”\n\n12호의 문은 반쯤 열려 있었고, 그 새어 나오는 한 줄기 빛 속으로 값싸고 질 나쁜 담배의 짙은 연기와, 레빈이 모르는 어떤 목소리가 흘러나왔다. 하지만 그는 형이 그 안에 있음을 단번에 알아챘다. 형의 기침 소리를 들었던 것이다.\n\n문 안으로 들어서는데 그 낯선 목소리가 이렇게 말하고 있었다.\n\n“그게 다 얼마나 분별과 지식을 갖고 일을 하느냐에 달린 거지요.”\n\n콘스탄틴 레빈은 문 안을 들여다보았고, 말하는 이가 머리숱이 엄청나게 많고 러시아식 윗도리를 걸친 젊은 남자라는 것을, 그리고 깃도 소맷부리도 없는 모직 가운을 입은 곰보 여자가 소파에 앉아 있는 것을 보았다. 형은 보이지 않았다. 콘스탄틴은 형이 이런 묘한 무리 속에서 인생을 보내고 있다는 생각에 가슴이 날카롭게 저려 왔다. 아무도 그의 인기척을 듣지 못했고, 콘스탄틴은 덧신을 벗으며 윗도리 차림의 남자가 하는 말에 귀를 기울였다. 그는 무슨 사업 이야기를 하고 있었다.\n\n“흥, 그놈들, 특권 계급 놈들 따위 악마한테 가죽이나 벗기라지.” 형의 목소리가 기침과 함께 대꾸했다. “마샤! 저녁거리 좀 차려 오고, 술 남은 게 있으면 그것도. 없으면 나가서 사 와.”\n\n여자가 일어나 칸막이 뒤에서 나왔다가 콘스탄틴을 보았다.\n\n“손님이 오셨어요, 니콜라이 드미트리예비치.” 그녀가 말했다.\n\n“누굴 찾으시오?” 니콜라이 레빈의 목소리가 성난 어조로 물었다.\n\n“나야.” 콘스탄틴 레빈이 빛 속으로 나서며 대답했다.\n\n“‘나’라니 누구야?” 니콜라이의 목소리가 더욱더 성난 어조로 다시 물었다. 황급히 일어나다 무언가에 부딪치는 소리가 들렸고, 레빈은 문간에서 자기를 마주 보고 선, 크고 겁먹은 눈과, 너무도 낯익으면서도 그 기괴함과 병색이 사람을 깜짝 놀라게 하는, 거대하고 야위고 구부정한 형의 모습을 보았다.\n\n형은 콘스탄틴 레빈이 마지막으로 보았던 삼 년 전보다도 한층 더 야위어 있었다. 짧은 외투를 걸치고 있었는데, 손과 굵은 뼈마디가 어느 때보다 더 거대해 보였다. 머리숱은 더 줄었고, 똑같은 곧은 콧수염이 입술을 가렸으며, 똑같은 눈이 방문객을 묘하고도 천진하게 바라보았다.\n\n“아, 코스챠!” 형이 동생을 알아보고 별안간 소리쳤고, 그의 눈이 기쁨으로 빛났다. 하지만 바로 그 순간 그는 젊은 남자 쪽을 돌아보더니, 콘스탄틴이 너무도 잘 아는, 마치 옷깃이 목을 죄기라도 하는 듯 머리와 목을 신경질적으로 홱 젖히는 동작을 했다. 그러자 사뭇 다른 표정이, 사납고 고통스럽고 잔인한 표정이 그의 수척한 얼굴에 자리 잡았다.\n\n“너랑 세르게이 이바노비치 둘 다한테 편지로 썼잖아, 너희를 모르고 알고 싶지도 않다고. 뭘 원하는 거야?”\n\n형은 콘스탄틴이 그려 보던 모습과는 전혀 딴판이었다. 그의 성격에서 가장 고약하고 가장 진저리 나는 부분, 그와의 모든 관계를 그토록 어렵게 만드는 그 부분을, 콘스탄틴 레빈은 형을 떠올릴 때면 까맣게 잊고 있었던 것이다. 그런데 이제 형의 얼굴을, 특히 그 신경질적인 머리 경련을 보자, 그 모든 것이 다시 떠올랐다.\n\n“형을 만나는 데 무슨 다른 뜻이 있었던 게 아니야.” 그는 머뭇거리며 대답했다. “그냥 형을 보러 온 거야.”\n\n동생의 머뭇거리는 태도가 분명히 니콜라이의 마음을 누그러뜨렸다. 그의 입술이 씰룩거렸다.\n\n“오, 그래서 그런 거였어?” 그가 말했다. “그럼 들어와, 앉아. 저녁 좀 먹겠어? 마샤, 세 사람 분 저녁 차려 와. 아니, 잠깐 기다려. 너 이 사람이 누군지 아니?” 그는 동생을 향해 윗도리 차림의 남자를 가리키며 말했다. “이 사람은 크리츠키 씨야, 키예프에서부터 알고 지낸 내 친구지, 아주 비범한 사람이야. 물론 경찰한테 박해를 받고 있지, 악당이 아니니까 말이야.”\n\n그러고는 늘 그러듯 방 안에 있는 사람들을 둘러보았다. 문간에 서 있던 여자가 나가려고 움직이는 것을 보자 그가 소리쳤다. “기다리라고 했잖아.” 그러고는 콘스탄틴이 너무도 잘 아는, 자기 생각을 표현하지 못하는 그 횡설수설하는 말투로, 다시 한번 모두를 둘러보면서 동생에게 크리츠키의 이야기를 들려주기 시작했다. 그가 가난한 학생들을 위한 공제회와 일요 학교를 세우려다 대학에서 쫓겨난 일, 그 후 농민 학교 교사가 되었다가 거기서도 내쫓긴 일, 그러고 나서 무슨 일로 유죄 선고까지 받은 일에 대해서였다.\n\n“키예프 대학 출신이십니까?” 그 뒤에 이어진 어색한 침묵을 깨려고 콘스탄틴 레빈이 크리츠키에게 물었다.\n\n“그렇소, 키예프 출신이오.” 크리츠키가 얼굴을 어둡게 하며 성난 어조로 대답했다.\n\n“그리고 이 여자는,” 니콜라이 레빈이 그녀를 가리키며 동생의 말을 가로막았다. “내 인생의 반려, 마리야 니콜라예브나야. 내가 나쁜 집에서 데려왔지.” 그는 이 말을 하면서 목을 홱 젖혔다. “하지만 난 이 여자를 사랑하고 존중해. 그러니 나를 알고 지내고 싶은 사람은 누구든,” 그는 목소리를 높이고 미간을 찌푸리며 덧붙였다. “이 여자를 사랑하고 존중해 줘야 해. 이 여자는 내 아내나 똑같아, 똑같다고. 자, 이제 네가 누굴 상대하는지 알았겠지. 그리고 이게 네 격을 떨어뜨리는 일이라고 생각된다면, 자, 여기 바닥이 있고 저기 문이 있으니.\"\n\n그러고는 또다시 그의 눈이 캐묻듯 그들 모두를 훑었다.\n\n“내 격이 왜 떨어진다는 건지 난 모르겠어.”\n\n“그럼, 마샤, 저녁 가져오라고 해. 세 사람 분, 보드카하고 포도주도……. 아니, 잠깐 기다려……. 아니, 됐어……. 가 봐.”\n\n# Chapter 25\n\n니콜라이 레빈은 이마를 괴롭게 찌푸리며 몸을 씰룩거렸다. \"그러니까 알겠지.\" 그가 말을 이었다.\n\n무슨 말을 하고 무엇을 해야 할지 떠올리기가 그에게는 분명 힘겨워 보였다.\n\n\"여기, 보이지?……\" 그는 방 한구석에 끈으로 묶인 채 놓여 있는 무슨 쇠막대 같은 것들을 가리켰다. \"저게 보이나? 저게 우리가 새로 시작하려는 일의 출발점이야. 생산 협동조합이라고…….\"\n\n콘스탄친은 그의 말을 거의 듣지 못했다. 그는 형의 병약하고 폐병 든 얼굴을 들여다보고 있었고, 점점 더 형이 가여워졌으며, 형이 협동조합에 대해 늘어놓는 이야기에 도무지 귀를 기울일 수가 없었다. 그는 이 협동조합이라는 것이 형에게는 자기혐오에서 벗어나기 위한 한낱 닻에 지나지 않는다는 것을 알아차렸다. 니콜라이 레빈은 계속 말을 이었다.\n\n\"알다시피 자본은 노동자를 억압해. 우리네 노동자들, 그러니까 농민들이 노동의 짐을 전부 짊어지고 있는데, 그들은 아무리 일해도 짐을 나르는 짐승 같은 처지에서 벗어날 수 없도록 놓여 있단 말이지. 노동에서 나오는 모든 이윤, 그걸로 그들이 자기 처지를 개선하고 여가를 얻고 그다음엔 교육까지 받을 수 있을 텐데, 그 모든 잉여 가치를 자본가들이 빼앗아 가지. 그리고 사회라는 게 그렇게 짜여 있어서, 그들이 열심히 일하면 일할수록 상인과 지주의 이윤만 커지고, 그들은 끝까지 짐 나르는 짐승으로 남는 거야. 그런 상태는 반드시 바뀌어야 해.\" 그는 이렇게 말을 맺고는 미심쩍은 눈으로 동생을 바라보았다.\n\n\"그래, 물론이지.\" 콘스탄친은 형의 튀어나온 광대뼈에 돋아난 붉은 반점을 바라보며 말했다.\n\n\"그래서 우리는 자물쇠공 협동조합을 세우려는 거야. 거기서는 모든 생산과 이윤, 그리고 주요한 생산 도구가 공동의 것이 되지.\"\n\n\"그 협동조합은 어디에 두는데?\" 콘스탄친 레빈이 물었다.\n\n\"카잔 현 보즈드렘 마을에.\"\n\n\"하지만 왜 하필 마을에? 내 생각엔 마을엔 지금도 일거리가 넘치는데. 왜 마을에 자물쇠공 협동조합이지?\"\n\n\"왜냐고? 농민들이 예나 지금이나 똑같이 노예이기 때문이고, 바로 그래서 형이랑 세르게이 이바노비치는 사람들이 그들을 그 노예 상태에서 끌어내려고 애쓰는 걸 못마땅해하는 거지.\" 니콜라이 레빈이 그 반박에 발끈하며 말했다.\n\n콘스탄친 레빈은 한숨을 내쉬며 그 음울하고 더러운 방을 둘러보았다. 이 한숨이 니콜라이를 더욱 발끈하게 만든 듯했다.\n\n\"네놈과 세르게이 이바노비치의 그 귀족적인 관점을 내가 알지. 그 사람이 자기 지성의 온갖 힘을 다해 현존하는 악을 정당화하는 데에 쓰고 있다는 걸 안다고.\"\n\n\"아니, 그런데 왜 세르게이 이바노비치 얘기를 꺼내는 거야?\" 레빈이 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"세르게이 이바노비치? 왜 꺼내냐고 말해 주지!\" 니콜라이 레빈은 세르게이 이바노비치라는 이름에 별안간 소리를 질렀다. \"왜 그러는지 말해 주마…… 하지만 떠들어 봐야 무슨 소용이야? 다만 한 가지뿐이지…… 넌 뭐 하러 나를 찾아왔어? 넌 이걸 깔보고 있고, 그래도 좋아—그러니 가 버려, 제발 가 버리라고!\" 그는 의자에서 벌떡 일어나며 소리쳤다. \"가 버려, 가 버리란 말이야!\"\n\n\"난 이걸 전혀 깔보지 않아.\" 콘스탄친 레빈이 머뭇거리며 말했다. \"반박조차 하지 않잖아.\"\n\n바로 그 순간 마리야 니콜라예브나가 돌아왔다. 니콜라이 레빈은 성난 눈으로 그녀를 돌아보았다. 그녀는 얼른 그에게 다가가 무어라고 속삭였다.\n\n\"내가 몸이 안 좋아서 신경이 곤두서 있었어.\" 니콜라이 레빈이 차분해지면서도 괴롭게 숨을 몰아쉬며 말했다. \"게다가 너는 나한테 세르게이 이바노비치니 그의 논문이니 하는 얘기를 꺼내고. 그건 죄다 쓰레기야, 죄다 거짓말이고, 죄다 자기기만이지. 정의에 대해 아무것도 모르는 인간이 정의에 관해 뭘 쓸 수 있겠어? 너 그 사람 논문 읽어 봤나?\" 그는 크리츠키에게 물으며 다시 탁자에 앉더니, 자리를 비우려고 흩어진 담배를 절반쯤 옆으로 밀어냈다.\n\n\"안 읽었습니다.\" 크리츠키는 대화에 끼어들고 싶지 않은 기색이 역력하게 침울하게 대답했다.\n\n\"왜 안 읽었어?\" 니콜라이 레빈이 이번엔 발끈하며 크리츠키에게로 돌아앉았다.\n\n\"거기에 시간을 허비할 가치가 없다고 봤으니까요.\"\n\n\"오, 하지만 실례지만, 그게 시간 낭비라는 걸 어떻게 알았지? 그 논문은 많은 사람에게 너무 깊어서—그러니까 그들 머리를 훌쩍 넘어선단 말이야. 하지만 나는 다르지. 나는 그의 생각을 꿰뚫어 보고, 그 약점이 어디 있는지도 안다고.\"\n\n모두가 입을 다물었다. 크리츠키가 느릿느릿 일어나 모자를 집었다.\n\n\"저녁 안 먹고 가나? 좋아, 잘 가게! 내일 자물쇠공 데리고 다시 들르라고.\"\n\n크리츠키가 채 나가기도 전에 니콜라이 레빈은 미소를 지으며 눈을 찡긋했다.\n\n\"저자도 별 볼 일 없어.\" 그가 말했다. \"물론 나도 알지…….\"\n\n하지만 바로 그 순간 문가에서 크리츠키가 그를 불렀다……\n\n\"또 뭘 바라는 거야?\" 그는 이렇게 말하며 복도로 그를 따라 나갔다. 마리야 니콜라예브나와 단둘이 남게 되자 레빈은 그녀에게로 돌아섰다.\n\n\"내 형과 오래 함께 지냈소?\" 그가 그녀에게 물었다.\n\n\"네, 일 년 넘게요. 니콜라이 드미트리예비치의 건강이 무척 나빠졌어요. 니콜라이 드미트리예비치는 술을 너무 많이 마셔요.\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"그게…… 어떻게 마신단 말이오?\"\n\n\"보드카를 마시는데, 몸에 해롭지요.\"\n\n\"그것도 많이?\" 레빈이 속삭였다.\n\n\"네.\" 그녀는 니콜라이 레빈이 다시 모습을 드러낸 문간 쪽을 머뭇머뭇 바라보며 말했다.\n\n\"무슨 얘기를 하고 있었어?\" 그가 미간을 찌푸리며 겁먹은 눈을 이쪽저쪽으로 옮기며 말했다. \"뭐였어?\"\n\n\"아, 아무것도 아니야.\" 콘스탄친이 당황하여 대답했다.\n\n\"오, 말하기 싫으면 말고. 다만 저 여자한테 얘기해 봐야 소용없어. 저 여잔 계집이고, 넌 신사니까.\" 그는 목을 홱 젖히며 말했다. \"넌 다 이해하고, 다 평가하고, 내 결점들을 가엾다는 눈으로 내려다보지.\" 그는 목소리를 높이며 다시 말을 꺼냈다.\n\n\"니콜라이 드미트리예비치, 니콜라이 드미트리예비치.\" 마리야 니콜라예브나가 다시 그에게 다가가며 속삭였다.\n\n\"오, 알았어, 알았다고!…… 그런데 저녁은 어디 있지? 아, 여기 왔군.\" 그는 쟁반을 든 종업원을 보고 말했다. \"자, 여기 놓게.\" 그는 성마르게 덧붙이고는, 냉큼 보드카를 집어 한 잔 가득 따라 게걸스럽게 들이켰다. \"한잔할 텐가?\" 그는 동생에게로 돌아섰고, 이내 기분이 한결 누그러졌다.\n\n\"자, 세르게이 이바노비치 얘긴 그만하지. 어쨌든 널 보니 반갑구나. 이러니저러니 해도 우린 남이 아니잖아. 자, 한잔하자. 너는 뭘 하고 지내는지 얘기해 봐.\" 그는 빵 한 조각을 게걸스레 씹으면서 또 한 잔을 가득 따르며 말을 이었다. \"어떻게 살고 있어?\"\n\n\"전처럼 시골에서 혼자 살아. 땅을 돌보느라 바쁘지.\" 콘스탄친은 형이 먹고 마시는 그 게걸스러움을 끔찍한 심정으로 지켜보며, 자기가 그걸 알아챘다는 티를 감추려 애쓰며 대답했다.\n\n\"넌 왜 결혼을 안 해?\"\n\n\"그렇게 안 됐어.\" 콘스탄친이 살짝 얼굴을 붉히며 대답했다.\n\n\"왜 안 했어? 나야 이제…… 다 끝났지! 내 인생은 엉망진창으로 만들어 버렸어. 하지만 이건 전에도 말했고 지금도 말하는데, 내가 필요로 할 때 내 몫을 받았더라면 내 인생 전체가 달랐을 거라고.\"\n\n콘스탄친은 서둘러 화제를 돌렸다.\n\n\"형의 그 어린 바냐가 나랑 같이 있는 거 알아? 포크롭스코예 회계 사무소의 사무원으로 말이야.\"\n\n니콜라이는 목을 홱 젖히더니 생각에 잠겼다.\n\n\"그래, 포크롭스코예가 어떤지 말해 봐. 그 집은 여전히 서 있나, 자작나무들이랑 우리 공부방도? 그리고 정원사 필립은, 그자는 살아 있어? 정자랑 그 의자가 어찌나 생각나는지! 자, 명심해, 집 안의 어떤 것도 바꾸지 말고, 어서 결혼이나 해서 모든 걸 예전처럼 다시 만들어. 그러면 네 아내가 괜찮은 사람이라면 내가 보러 가지.\"\n\n\"하지만 지금 나한테로 와.\" 레빈이 말했다. \"우리가 얼마나 멋지게 꾸려 나갈지!\"\n\n\"세르게이 이바노비치를 마주칠 일이 없다는 걸 확신할 수만 있으면 가서 너를 보겠지.\"\n\n\"거기서 그 사람을 마주칠 일 없어. 난 그 사람과 완전히 따로 살아.\"\n\n\"그래, 하지만 네가 뭐라고 하든, 너는 나와 그 사람 중에서 골라야 할 거야.\" 그는 머뭇거리며 동생의 얼굴을 들여다보며 말했다.\n\n이 머뭇거림이 콘스탄친의 마음을 건드렸다.\n\n\"이 문제에 대한 내 신념을 듣고 싶다면 말해 주지. 형과 세르게이 이바노비치의 다툼에서 난 어느 편도 들지 않아. 둘 다 틀렸어. 형은 겉으로 더 틀렸고, 그 사람은 속으로 더 틀렸지.\"\n\n\"아, 아! 너도 그게 보이는구나, 보이는구나!\" 니콜라이가 기쁘게 외쳤다.\n\n\"하지만 나 개인적으로는 형과의 정겨운 관계를 더 소중히 여겨. 왜냐하면…….\"\n\n\"왜, 왜지?\"\n\n콘스탄친은, 니콜라이가 불행하고 애정을 필요로 하기에 그것을 더 소중히 여긴다고는 차마 말할 수 없었다. 하지만 니콜라이는 동생이 하려던 말이 바로 그것임을 알아챘고, 인상을 찌푸리며 다시 보드카를 집어 들었다.\n\n\"그만하세요, 니콜라이 드미트리예비치!\" 마리야 니콜라예브나가 통통한 맨팔을 술병 쪽으로 뻗으며 말했다.\n\n\"내버려 둬! 고집 부리지 마! 한 대 때려 줄 테니!\" 그가 소리쳤다.\n\n마리야 니콜라예브나는 다정하고 너그러운 미소를 지었고, 그 미소는 곧장 니콜라이의 얼굴에도 비쳤다. 그녀는 술병을 거두었다.\n\n\"그래, 너는 저 여자가 아무것도 이해 못 한다고 생각하지?\" 니콜라이가 말했다. \"저 여자는 우리 누구보다도 모든 걸 더 잘 이해해. 저 여자한테 뭔가 착하고 사랑스러운 데가 있는 게 사실 아니냐?\"\n\n\"전에 모스크바에 와 본 적이 없소?\" 콘스탄친은 무어라도 말하려고 그녀에게 물었다.\n\n\"단, 저 여자한테 예의 차린답시고 딱딱하게 굴진 마. 저 여잔 그러면 겁먹어. 저 여자한테 그렇게 말을 건넨 건 매음굴에서 빠져나오려 했다고 저 여자를 재판한 치안 판사들뿐이었으니까. 맙소사, 세상에 이런 몰지각함이라니!\" 그가 별안간 외쳤다. \"이 새로운 기관들, 이 치안 판사들이며 지방 의회며, 죄다 얼마나 흉측한 노릇인지!\"\n\n그러고는 그 새로운 기관들과의 충돌담을 늘어놓기 시작했다.\n\n콘스탄친 레빈은 그의 말을 들었는데, 자기도 그와 공유하며 곧잘 입에 올리던 모든 공공 기관의 의의에 대한 그 불신이, 이제 형의 입에서 나오니 거북하게 느껴졌다.\n\n\"다른 세상에 가면 우린 그걸 죄다 이해하게 될 거야.\" 그가 가볍게 말했다.\n\n\"다른 세상! 아, 난 그 다른 세상이 싫어! 싫다고.\" 그는 겁먹은 눈을 동생의 눈에 머무르게 하며 말했다. \"여기서야 자기 것이든 남의 것이든 그 온갖 비루함과 난장판에서 벗어나는 게 좋은 일이겠거니 싶지만, 그래도 난 죽음이 두려워, 죽는 게 끔찍이도 두려워.\" 그는 몸서리쳤다. \"하지만 뭐라도 좀 마셔. 샴페인 한잔하겠어? 아니면 어디 나갈까? 집시들한테 가자고! 내가 집시들이랑 러시아 노래에 어찌나 빠졌는지 너도 알면 좋을 텐데.\"\n\n그의 말은 어눌해지기 시작했고, 그는 이 화제에서 저 화제로 불쑥불쑥 건너뛰었다. 콘스탄친은 마샤의 도움을 받아 그가 어디로도 나가지 않도록 설득했고, 그를 가망 없이 취한 채 침대에 눕혔다.\n\n마샤는 필요한 경우 콘스탄친에게 편지를 쓰고, 니콜라이 레빈이 동생에게 가서 함께 지내도록 설득하겠다고 약속했다.\n\n# Chapter 26\n\n아침에 콘스탄틴 레빈은 모스크바를 떠나, 저녁 무렵 집에 도착했다. 기차 안에서 그는 옆자리 사람들과 정치며 새로 놓인 철도에 관해 이야기를 나누었는데, 모스크바에서와 똑같이 생각이 뒤엉킨 듯한 혼란, 자기 자신에 대한 불만, 무언지 모를 부끄러움에 사로잡혔다. 그러나 자기 역에 내려 외눈박이 마부 이그나트가 외투 깃을 세운 모습을 보았을 때, 역의 등불이 반사된 어슴푸레한 빛 속에서 고리와 술로 장식된 마구를 채우고 꼬리를 묶은 자기 말들과 자기 썰매를 보았을 때, 그리고 마부 이그나트가 짐을 실으면서 마을 소식을—청부업자가 도착했고 파바가 송아지를 낳았다고—전해 주었을 때, 그는 조금씩 그 혼란이 걷히고 부끄러움과 자기 불만이 사라져 가는 것을 느꼈다. 그저 이그나트와 말들을 보기만 했는데도 그런 느낌이 들었다. 그러나 자기를 위해 가져온 양가죽 외투를 걸치고, 썰매에 몸을 감싸고 앉아, 마을에서 앞으로 해야 할 일을 곰곰이 생각하면서, 한때는 자기 안장말이었으나 이제는 한창때가 지났어도 여전히 기운 넘치는 돈산(産) 곁말을 바라보며 달리기 시작했을 때, 그는 자기에게 일어난 일을 전혀 다른 빛 속에서 보기 시작했다. 그는 자기 자신을 느꼈고, 다른 누군가가 되고 싶지 않았다. 이제 그가 바라는 것은 그저 전보다 나은 사람이 되는 것뿐이었다. 우선 그는 그날부터 결혼이 분명 가져다주었을 어떤 특별한 행복을 바라기를 그만두기로 결심했고, 따라서 자기가 실제로 가진 것을 그렇게 업신여기지 않으리라 마음먹었다. 둘째로, 청혼하기로 마음을 굳히던 동안 그를 그토록 괴롭혔던 그 기억, 즉 저열한 욕정에 다시는 자기를 내맡기지 않기로 했다. 그러다 형 니콜라이를 떠올리고는, 결코 형을 잊지 않겠다고, 형을 끝까지 챙기고 시야에서 놓치지 않겠다고, 그래서 형의 형편이 나빠질 때 도울 준비를 해 두겠다고 스스로에게 다짐했다. 그리고 그런 일은 머잖아 닥치리라고 그는 느꼈다. 또 그때는 그토록 가볍게 흘려들었던 형의 공산주의 이야기가 이제 와서 그를 생각에 잠기게 했다. 그는 경제적 조건의 혁명이란 터무니없는 소리라고 여겼다. 그러나 그는 농민들의 가난에 견주어 자기가 누리는 풍요로움이 부당하다는 것을 늘 느꼈고, 이제 그는 떳떳한 마음을 갖기 위해, 비록 전에도 열심히 일했고 결코 사치스럽게 살지 않았지만, 앞으로는 더욱 열심히 일하고 사치는 한층 더 줄이기로 결심했다. 그리고 이 모든 것이 그에게는 자기 자신을 이겨 내는 너무도 손쉬운 일처럼 여겨져서, 그는 가는 길 내내 더없이 즐거운 공상에 잠겨 있었다. 새롭고 더 나은 삶에 대한 굳은 희망을 품고, 그는 밤 아홉 시가 되기 전에 집에 도착했다.\n\n집 앞 작은 마당의 눈이 그의 집에서 가정부 일을 맡고 있는 늙은 유모 아가피야 미하일로브나의 침실 창에서 새어 나오는 빛에 환히 비쳤다. 그녀는 아직 잠들지 않았다. 그녀가 깨운 쿠즈마가 졸린 걸음으로 비척비척 현관 계단으로 나왔다. 세터 암캐 라스카도 뛰쳐나와 하마터면 쿠즈마를 넘어뜨릴 뻔하면서 낑낑대며 레빈의 무릎 둘레를 맴돌았고, 펄쩍펄쩍 뛰어오르며 앞발을 그의 가슴에 얹고 싶어 했으나 차마 그러지는 못했다.\n\n\"빨리 돌아오셨네요, 나리.\" 아가피야 미하일로브나가 말했다.\n\n\"지겨워졌어, 아가피야 미하일로브나. 친구들과 있으면 좋긴 한데, 집에 있는 게 더 낫지.\" 그가 대답하고는 서재로 들어갔다.\n\n촛불이 들어오자 서재가 천천히 밝아졌다. 낯익은 것들이 모습을 드러냈다. 사슴뿔, 책장, 거울, 손볼 데가 생긴 지 오래된 통풍구 달린 난로, 아버지가 쓰던 소파, 커다란 탁자, 그 탁자 위에 펼쳐진 책 한 권, 깨진 재떨이, 그의 필체로 채워진 원고 노트. 이 모든 것을 보는 순간, 길에서 줄곧 꿈꾸어 온 새 삶을 꾸려 갈 수 있을지 한순간 의심이 그를 덮쳤다. 그의 삶의 이 모든 흔적이 그를 붙잡고 이렇게 말하는 것 같았다. \"안 돼, 너는 우리에게서 벗어나지 못해, 너는 달라지지 못하고, 늘 그래 왔던 그대로일 거야. 의심하고, 자기 자신에 대한 끝없는 불만에 시달리고, 고치려는 헛된 노력을 하고, 또 무너지고, 그러면서 네가 결코 얻지 못할, 너에게는 불가능한 행복을 영원히 기다리면서 말이야.\"\n\n물건들은 그에게 이렇게 말했지만, 그의 마음속 또 다른 목소리는 과거에 휘둘려서는 안 된다고, 사람은 자기 자신을 가지고 무엇이든 할 수 있다고 말하고 있었다. 그리고 그 목소리에 귀를 기울이며, 그는 무거운 아령 두 개가 놓인 구석으로 가서 체조 선수처럼 그것들을 휘두르며 자신만만한 기분을 되찾으려 했다. 문가에서 계단이 삐걱거리는 소리가 났다. 그는 황급히 아령을 내려놓았다.\n\n집사가 들어와 모든 일이 다행히도 잘되고 있다고 말했지만, 새 건조기에 넣은 메밀이 조금 그슬렸다는 소식을 전했다. 이 소식에 레빈은 짜증이 났다. 새 건조기는 레빈이 직접 만들고 부분적으로는 고안하기까지 한 것이었다. 집사는 줄곧 그 건조기를 반대해 왔고, 이제 메밀이 그슬렸노라고 알리는 그의 목소리에는 억눌린 의기양양함이 묻어 있었다. 레빈은 만약 메밀이 그슬렸다면 그것은 오로지 자기가 수백 번이나 지시했던 예방 조치를 취하지 않았기 때문임을 굳게 확신했다. 그는 화가 나서 집사를 나무랐다. 그러나 중요하고도 기쁜 일이 하나 있었다. 품평회에서 비싼 값에 사 온 그의 가장 좋은 암소 파바가 송아지를 낳은 것이었다.\n\n\"쿠즈마, 내 양가죽 외투를 가져와. 그리고 자네는 등불을 들라고 일러 주게. 가서 그 녀석을 봐야겠어.\" 그가 집사에게 말했다.\n\n값이 더 나가는 소들을 위한 외양간은 집 바로 뒤에 있었다. 마당을 가로질러, 라일락 나무 옆의 눈더미를 지나, 그는 외양간으로 들어갔다. 얼어붙은 문을 열자 따뜻하고 김이 서린 거름 냄새가 풍겼고, 등불의 낯선 빛에 놀란 소들이 새로 깐 짚 위에서 몸을 뒤척였다. 그는 홀란드카의 넓고 매끄러운, 검은색과 얼룩이 섞인 등을 얼핏 보았다. 황소 베르쿠트는 입술에 코뚜레를 꿴 채 누워 있다가 일어서려는 듯하더니 마음을 고쳐먹고는, 그들이 곁을 지나갈 때 콧김을 두어 번 내뿜을 뿐이었다. 더없이 아름다운, 하마처럼 거대한 파바가 등을 돌리고 서서 송아지를 온통 핥아 대는 바람에, 그들은 송아지를 볼 수가 없었다.\n\n레빈은 우리 안으로 들어가 파바를 살펴본 뒤, 붉고 얼룩진 송아지를 길고 비틀거리는 다리로 일으켜 세웠다. 파바가 불안해하며 음매 울기 시작했지만, 레빈이 송아지를 가까이 붙여 주자 진정되어, 무겁게 한숨을 내쉬며 거친 혀로 송아지를 핥기 시작했다. 송아지는 더듬더듬 어미의 젖통 밑으로 코를 들이밀고는 꼬리를 빳빳이 곧추세웠다.\n\n\"이리로, 표도르, 불을 가져와, 이쪽으로.\" 레빈이 송아지를 살펴보며 말했다. \"어미를 닮았군! 빛깔은 아비를 닮았지만, 그건 아무것도 아니지. 아주 좋아. 엉덩이가 길고 넓어. 바실리 표도로비치, 이 녀석 훌륭하지 않은가?\" 그는 송아지에 대한 기쁨에 사로잡혀 메밀 일은 완전히 용서한 채 집사에게 말했다.\n\n\"훌륭하지 않을 리가 있겠습니까요? 아, 청부업자 세묜이 나리 떠나신 다음 날 왔습니다. 그자와 셈을 치르셔야 합니다, 콘스탄틴 드미트리치.\" 집사가 말했다. \"건조기 일은 제가 분명 말씀드렸지요.\"\n\n이 한마디는 레빈을 큰 규모에다 복잡하기 그지없는 영지 일의 온갖 세세한 부분으로 도로 끌어가기에 충분했다. 그는 외양간에서 곧장 사무실로 가서, 집사와 청부업자 세묜과 잠시 이야기를 나눈 뒤, 집으로 돌아와 곧장 위층 응접실로 올라갔다.\n\n# Chapter 27\n\n집은 크고 고풍스러웠는데, 레빈은 혼자 살면서도 집 전체에 난방을 하고 모든 방을 쓰고 있었다. 그는 이것이 어리석은 일임을 알았고, 분명히 옳지 않으며 지금 자기의 새로운 계획에도 어긋난다는 것을 알았지만, 이 집은 레빈에게 하나의 온전한 세계였다. 그것은 그의 아버지와 어머니가 살다가 세상을 떠난 세계였다. 두 분은 레빈에게는 완벽의 이상으로 여겨지는 바로 그런 삶을 살았고, 그는 아내와 가족과 더불어 그런 삶을 시작하기를 꿈꿔 왔다.\n\n레빈은 어머니를 거의 기억하지 못했다. 어머니에 대한 그의 관념은 그에게 신성한 기억이었고, 그의 미래의 아내는 그의 상상 속에서 자기 어머니가 그러했던 여인의 그 절묘하고 거룩한 이상을 되풀이하는 존재일 수밖에 없었다.\n\n그는 결혼과 동떨어진 여인에 대한 사랑이란 것을 도무지 떠올릴 수 없었기에, 머릿속으로 먼저 가족을 그렸고, 그에게 가족을 안겨 줄 여인은 그저 그다음에 떠올렸다. 따라서 결혼에 대한 그의 생각은 그가 아는 대다수의 사람들과는 사뭇 달랐는데, 그들에게 결혼이란 사교 생활의 숱한 일들 가운데 하나일 뿐이었다. 레빈에게 결혼은 삶의 으뜸가는 일이었고, 삶의 행복 전부가 거기에 달려 있었다. 그런데 이제 그는 그것을 단념해야만 했다.\n\n그가 늘 차를 마시던 작은 응접실로 들어가 책 한 권을 들고 안락의자에 자리를 잡고 앉자, 아가피야 미하일로브나가 차를 가져왔고, 평소처럼 \"그럼 잠깐 앉아 있겠습니다, 나리.\" 하고는 창가의 의자에 앉았다. 그때 그는, 아무리 이상하게 여겨질지라도, 자기가 공상에서 결코 헤어 나오지 못했으며 그 공상 없이는 살 수 없다는 것을 느꼈다. 그녀든 다른 여자든, 어쨌든 그 삶은 펼쳐질 것이었다. 그는 책을 읽으며 읽고 있는 내용을 생각했고, 지치지도 않고 수다를 떠는 아가피야 미하일로브나의 말에 귀 기울이려 이따금 멈추기도 했지만, 그러면서도 앞날의 가정생활과 일에 관한 온갖 그림이 두서없이 그의 상상 앞에 떠올랐다. 그는 자기 영혼 깊은 곳에서 무언가가 제자리를 찾고, 가라앉고, 잠잠해졌음을 느꼈다.\n\n그는 아가피야 미하일로브나가, 프로호르가 하느님에 대한 도리를 잊고서 레빈이 말을 사라고 준 돈으로 쉴 새 없이 술을 퍼마시고 아내를 반쯤 죽도록 때렸다는 이야기를 늘어놓는 것을 들었다. 그는 귀 기울이면서 책을 읽었고, 그 독서가 불러일으킨 일련의 생각을 모두 떠올렸다. 그것은 틴들의 『열에 관한 논고』였다. 그는 자기가 틴들을 두고 비판했던 점들을, 즉 그가 자기 실험의 영리함에 흡족해하는 안일한 만족과 철학적 통찰의 결여를 두고 했던 비판을 떠올렸다. 그러다 별안간 즐거운 생각 하나가 그의 마음속으로 떠올랐다. \"이 년만 지나면 나는 네덜란드 암소 두 마리를 갖게 될 거야. 어쩌면 파바도 그때까지 살아 있을지 모르고, 베르쿠트의 어린 딸 열두 마리에다 다른 세 마리까지—얼마나 멋진 일인가!\"\n\n그는 다시 책을 집어 들었다. \"아주 좋아, 전기와 열은 같은 것이지. 하지만 어떤 문제를 풀기 위한 방정식에서 한 양을 다른 양으로 대체하는 게 가능할까? 아니지. 그래, 그렇다면 그게 어쨌다는 거야? 자연의 모든 힘들 사이의 연관은 본능적으로 느껴지는 거지…… 파바의 딸이 붉은 점박이 암소라면 정말 좋겠고, 그러면 무리 전체가 그놈을 닮을 테고, 다른 세 마리도 그럴 테지! 굉장해! 아내와 손님들과 함께 나가서 가축 떼를 맞이하는 거야…… 아내가 말하겠지. '코스챠와 나는 저 송아지를 자식처럼 보살폈답니다.' '그게 어쩜 그렇게 흥미로우세요?' 하고 손님이 묻겠지. '그이가 흥미로워하는 건 전부 저도 흥미로워요.' 한데 그녀가 누가 될까?\" 그러자 그는 모스크바에서 있었던 일을 떠올렸다…… \"그래, 어쩔 도리가 없지…… 내 잘못은 아니야. 하지만 이제부터는 모든 게 새롭게 흘러갈 거야. 삶이 그걸 허락하지 않으리라느니, 과거가 그걸 허락하지 않으리라느니 하는 건 어리석은 소리야. 더 잘 살려고, 훨씬 더 잘 살려고 애써야 해.\"…… 그는 고개를 들고 공상에 잠겨 들었다. 늙은 라스카는 그가 돌아온 기쁨을 아직 다 삭이지 못한 채 짖어 대려고 마당으로 뛰쳐나갔다가, 꼬리를 흔들며 돌아와 신선한 바깥 공기 냄새를 풍기며 그에게 슬그머니 다가와, 그의 손 밑에 머리를 들이밀고 쓰다듬어 달라는 듯 애처롭게 낑낑거렸다.\n\n\"저것 좀 보세요, 누가 생각이나 했겠어요?\" 아가피야 미하일로브나가 말했다. \"이제 저 개까지…… 글쎄, 주인 나리가 집에 돌아왔다는 걸, 그리고 나리가 기운이 없으시다는 걸 다 안다니까요.\"\n\n\"기운이 없다니, 왜?\"\n\n\"제가 그걸 못 볼 것 같으세요, 나리? 이제 양반님네 속을 알 때도 됐지요. 어릴 적부터 이 댁 분들 곁에서 자랐는걸요. 괜찮습니다, 나리, 몸 성하고 양심에 거리낄 게 없으면 그만이지요.\"\n\n레빈은 그녀가 자기 마음을 얼마나 잘 아는지에 놀라며 그녀를 뚫어지게 바라보았다.\n\n\"한 잔 더 가져다드릴까요?\" 그녀가 이렇게 말하고는 찻잔을 들고 나갔다.\n\n라스카는 자꾸만 그의 손 밑으로 머리를 들이밀었다. 그가 쓰다듬어 주자 개는 곧장 그의 발치에 몸을 웅크리고 뒷발 위에 머리를 얹었다. 그리고 이제 모든 것이 흡족하고 좋다는 표시로, 개는 입을 살짝 벌려 입맛을 다시고는, 끈적한 입술을 늙은 이빨 둘레에 더 편안하게 자리 잡고서 더없이 행복한 휴식에 빠져들었다. 레빈은 개의 모든 움직임을 유심히 지켜보았다.\n\n\"나도 저렇게 할 거야.\" 그는 혼잣말로 중얼거렸다. \"나도 저렇게 할 거야! 잘못된 건 아무것도 없어…… 다 괜찮아.\"\n\n# Chapter 28\n\n무도회가 끝난 뒤, 이튿날 이른 아침에 안나 아르카지예브나는 남편에게 그날로 모스크바를 떠나겠다는 전보를 보냈다.\n\n\"아니에요, 가야 해요, 가야 해요.\" 그녀는 올케에게 계획이 바뀐 까닭을 설명하면서, 떠올려야 할 일이 너무 많아 일일이 다 헤아릴 수 없다는 투로 말했다. \"아니에요, 역시 오늘이 낫겠어요!\"\n\n스테판 아르카지치는 집에서 저녁을 먹지 않았지만, 일곱 시에 와서 누이를 배웅하겠다고 약속했다.\n\n키티 역시 오지 않고, 머리가 아프다는 쪽지를 보내왔다. 돌리와 안나는 아이들과 영국인 가정교사하고만 저녁을 먹었다. 아이들이 변덕스러워서였는지, 아니면 예민한 감각으로 안나가 그날따라 자기들이 그토록 따랐던 때와는 사뭇 다르며, 이제는 자기들에게 관심이 없다는 것을 느꼈기 때문인지, 아이들은 별안간 고모와의 놀이와 고모에 대한 애정을 뚝 끊어 버리고는 고모가 떠난다는 데에도 도무지 무관심했다. 안나는 아침 내내 출발 준비에 골몰했다. 모스크바의 지인들에게 쪽지를 쓰고, 셈을 정리하고, 짐을 꾸렸다. 통틀어 돌리가 보기에 그녀는 마음이 평온한 상태가 아니라, 돌리 자신도 잘 아는 그 불안한 기분에 빠져 있는 것 같았다. 그것은 까닭 없이 찾아오는 법이 없으며, 대개는 자기 자신에 대한 불만을 감추고 있는 기분이었다. 저녁 식사가 끝난 뒤, 안나는 옷을 갈아입으러 자기 방으로 올라갔고, 돌리도 뒤따라갔다.\n\n\"오늘 어쩜 이렇게 이상해요!\" 돌리가 그녀에게 말했다.\n\n\"제가요? 그렇게 보여요? 이상한 게 아니라, 제가 좀 못된 거예요. 가끔 이래요. 자꾸 울고 싶어지는 거 있죠. 참 어리석은 일이지만, 곧 지나갈 거예요.\" 안나가 빠르게 말하며, 잠잘 때 쓰는 모자와 고운 삼베 손수건 몇 장을 챙겨 넣던 작은 가방 위로 상기된 얼굴을 숙였다. 그녀의 눈은 유난히 반짝였고, 줄곧 눈물이 그렁그렁 차올랐다. \"꼭 마찬가지로 페테르부르크를 떠나기도 싫었는데, 이제는 또 여기서 떠나기가 싫네요.\"\n\n\"올케는 여기 와서 좋은 일을 했어요.\" 돌리가 그녀를 뚫어지게 바라보며 말했다.\n\n안나는 눈물에 젖은 눈으로 그녀를 바라보았다.\n\n\"그런 말 마요, 돌리. 저는 아무것도 하지 않았고, 할 수도 없었어요. 사람들이 어째서 다들 한통속이 되어 저를 추켜세우는지 종종 의아하다니까요. 제가 무얼 했고, 무얼 할 수 있었겠어요? 형님 마음속에 용서할 만큼의 사랑이 있었던 거지요……\"\n\n\"올케가 아니었다면, 하느님만이 무슨 일이 벌어졌을지 아시겠지요! 올케는 참 행복한 사람이에요, 안나!\" 돌리가 말했다. \"올케 마음속은 모든 게 맑고 좋잖아요.\"\n\n\"영국 사람들 말마따나, 마음마다 저마다의 skeleton이 있는 법이에요.\"\n\n* skeleton: 영어로 '해골'. '벽장 속의 해골(skeleton in the closet)'이라는 표현에서 온 말로, 남에게 숨기고 싶은 비밀을 뜻한다.\n\n\"올케한테는 그런 skeleton 같은 건 없잖아요? 올케는 모든 게 그토록 맑은데.\"\n\n\"있어요!\" 안나가 불쑥 말했다. 그러더니 눈물 끝에 뜻밖에도 짓궂고 비꼬는 듯한 미소가 그녀의 입가에 번졌다.\n\n\"어머, 올케의 그 skeleton은 어쨌든 우습기는 해도 우울하진 않은가 봐요.\" 돌리가 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\"아니에요, 우울한걸요. 제가 왜 내일이 아니라 오늘 떠나는지 알아요? 마음을 짓누르는 고백이 하나 있는데, 그걸 형님한테 털어놓고 싶어요.\" 안나가 단호하게 안락의자에 몸을 떨구며, 돌리의 얼굴을 똑바로 들여다보며 말했다.\n\n그러자 놀랍게도 돌리는 안나가 귀밑까지, 목덜미의 곱슬곱슬한 검은 머리칼이 닿는 데까지 새빨개지는 것을 보았다.\n\n\"그래요.\" 안나가 말을 이었다. \"키티가 왜 저녁 먹으러 오지 않았는지 알아요? 저를 질투하는 거예요. 제가 망쳐 버렸어요…… 그 애한테 무도회가 즐거움이 아니라 고통이 되게 만든 게 저예요. 하지만 정말이지, 정말이지 제 잘못은 아니에요. 아니면 아주 조금만 제 잘못이거나요.\" 그녀는 \"아주 조금\"이라는 말을 곱게 늘여 말했다.\n\n\"어머, 그 말투가 어쩜 그렇게 스티바를 닮았어요!\" 돌리가 웃으며 말했다.\n\n안나는 기분이 상했다.\n\n\"아니, 아니에요! 저는 스티바가 아니에요.\" 그녀가 미간을 찌푸리며 말했다. \"제가 형님한테 이런 이야기를 하는 건, 바로 단 한순간도 제 자신을 의심하는 걸 견딜 수 없어서예요.\" 안나가 말했다.\n\n하지만 바로 그 말을 입에 올리는 순간, 그녀는 그것이 사실이 아님을 느꼈다. 그녀는 단지 자기 자신을 의심하는 데 그치지 않았다. 브론스키를 떠올리면 가슴이 설렜고, 단지 그와 마주치는 것을 피하려고 본래 마음먹었던 것보다 일찍 떠나는 것이었다.\n\n\"그래요, 스티바가 그러던데, 올케가 그 사람하고 마주르카를 췄다고, 그리고 그 사람이……\"\n\n\"그게 어쩜 그렇게 어이없게 흘러갔는지 형님은 상상도 못 할 거예요. 저는 그저 중매를 서려 했을 뿐인데, 별안간 전혀 딴판으로 되어 버렸지 뭐예요. 어쩌면 제 뜻과는 어긋나게……\"\n\n그녀는 새빨개져서 말을 멈췄다.\n\n\"오, 남자들은 그런 걸 단박에 느끼나 봐요?\" 돌리가 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 그 사람 쪽에서 뭔가 진지한 게 있다면 저는 절망스러울 거예요.\" 안나가 그녀의 말을 가로막았다. \"그리고 저는 다 잊히고, 키티도 저를 미워하길 그만두리라고 확신해요.\"\n\n\"그래도 안나, 솔직히 말하면 저는 키티가 이 결혼을 하길 그렇게 바라는 건 아니에요. 그리고 만약 그 사람, 브론스키가 단 하루 만에 올케한테 반할 수 있는 사람이라면, 차라리 아무 일도 없는 편이 나아요.\"\n\n\"오, 세상에, 그건 너무 어처구니없는 일이죠!\" 안나가 말했고, 자기를 사로잡고 있던 그 생각이 말로 옮겨지는 것을 듣자, 다시금 기쁨의 짙은 홍조가 그녀의 얼굴에 떠올랐다. \"그래서 이렇게 제가 떠나는 거예요. 그토록 좋아했던 키티를 적으로 만들어 놓고서요! 아, 그 애는 어쩜 그렇게 사랑스러운지! 하지만 형님이 잘 풀어 주실 거죠, 돌리? 네?\"\n\n돌리는 미소를 가까스로 억눌렀다. 그녀는 안나를 사랑했지만, 안나에게도 약점이 있다는 것을 보는 게 즐거웠다.\n\n\"적이라고요? 그럴 리가 없어요.\"\n\n\"저는 정말이지 형님네 모두가 제가 형님들을 아끼는 만큼 저를 아껴 주길 바랐어요. 그리고 이제는 그 어느 때보다 형님을 아껴요.\" 안나가 눈물을 글썽이며 말했다. \"아, 오늘 제가 어쩜 이렇게 바보 같은지!\"\n\n그녀는 손수건으로 얼굴을 닦고 옷을 갈아입기 시작했다.\n\n막 출발하려는 바로 그 순간에, 스테판 아르카지치가 늦게, 발그레한 얼굴로 기분 좋게 술과 시가 냄새를 풍기며 도착했다.\n\n안나의 감상적인 기분이 돌리에게도 옮아, 마지막으로 올케를 끌어안으며 그녀는 속삭였다. \"기억해요, 안나, 올케가 나를 위해 해 준 일을—나는 결코 잊지 않을 거예요. 그리고 기억해요, 내가 올케를 사랑한다는 걸, 가장 소중한 벗으로 언제까지나 사랑하리라는 걸!\"\n\n\"왜 그러는지 모르겠네요.\" 안나가 그녀에게 입을 맞추고 눈물을 감추며 말했다.\n\n\"형님은 나를 이해했고, 또 이해하고 있어요. 잘 있어요, 내 사랑스러운 형님!\"\n\n# Chapter 29\n\n\"자, 다 끝났어. 정말 다행이야!\" 안나 아르카지예브나가 오라버니에게 마지막 작별 인사를 했을 때 떠오른 첫 번째 생각이었다. 오라버니는 세 번째 종이 울릴 때까지 객차 입구를 가로막은 채 서 있었다. 그녀는 안누슈카 옆 소파에 앉아 침대칸의 어스름한 빛 속에서 주위를 둘러보았다. \"정말 다행이야! 내일이면 세료자와 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치를 만날 테고, 내 삶은 예전처럼 다시 흘러갈 거야. 모든 게 다 좋고, 늘 그렇듯이 평온하게.\"\n\n그날 하루 종일 그래 왔듯이 여전히 들뜬 마음으로, 안나는 여행 채비를 정성껏 하는 데서 즐거움을 느꼈다. 작고 야무진 손으로 그녀는 작은 빨간 가방을 열었다 닫았다 하며 방석을 꺼내 무릎 위에 올려놓고, 두 발을 꼼꼼히 감싼 뒤 편안하게 자리를 잡았다. 병약한 부인 하나는 벌써 자려고 누워 있었다. 다른 두 부인이 안나에게 말을 걸기 시작했고, 뚱뚱한 노부인은 발을 끌어당기며 객차의 난방에 대해 한마디 늘어놓았다. 안나는 몇 마디 대꾸했지만, 그 대화에서 별다른 재미를 기대할 수 없었으므로 안누슈카에게 등을 가져오라고 부탁해 좌석 팔걸이에 걸고는, 가방에서 종이칼과 영국 소설책을 꺼냈다. 처음에는 독서가 도무지 진척되지 않았다. 부산스러운 소동이 그녀를 방해했다. 그러다 기차가 출발하자 그 소음에 귀를 기울이지 않을 수 없었다. 그다음에는 왼쪽 창문을 때리며 유리창에 들러붙는 눈이며, 한쪽이 온통 눈에 뒤덮인 채 지나가는 둘둘 싸맨 차장의 모습이며, 바깥에 몰아치는 무서운 눈보라에 대한 이야기들이 그녀의 주의를 흐트러뜨렸다. 더 가서도 똑같은 것이 거듭거듭 되풀이되었다. 똑같은 흔들림과 덜컹거림, 똑같이 창문에 부딪치는 눈, 무더운 열기에서 추위로, 다시 열기로 빠르게 옮겨 가는 똑같은 변화, 어스름 속에서 똑같은 모습들이 스쳐 지나가는 똑같은 광경, 그리고 똑같은 목소리들. 그러다 안나는 책을 읽기 시작했고, 읽은 것을 이해하기 시작했다. 안누슈카는 벌써 꾸벅꾸벅 졸고 있었는데, 장갑 낀 넓적한 손으로 무릎 위의 빨간 가방을 움켜쥐고 있었고, 그 장갑 한 짝은 찢어져 있었다. 안나 아르카지예브나는 읽으며 이해했지만, 읽는다는 것, 그러니까 다른 사람들의 삶이 비친 모습을 좇는다는 것이 그녀에게는 못마땅했다. 그녀에게는 자기 자신이 살고 싶다는 욕망이 너무도 컸다. 소설의 여주인공이 병자를 간호한다는 대목을 읽으면, 그녀 자신이 병자의 방을 소리 없는 발걸음으로 거닐고 싶어졌다. 어느 국회의원이 연설을 한다는 대목을 읽으면, 그녀 자신이 그 연설을 하고 싶어졌다. 메리 부인이 사냥개들을 뒤따라 말을 달려 시누이의 부아를 돋우고 그 대담함으로 모두를 놀라게 했다는 대목을 읽으면, 그녀 역시 똑같은 일을 하고 싶어졌다. 하지만 무언가를 할 가능성은 없었다. 그래서 매끄러운 종이칼을 작은 손으로 만지작거리며, 그녀는 억지로 책을 읽어 나갔다.\n\n소설의 주인공은 어느덧 영국식 행복, 그러니까 준남작 작위와 영지를 거의 손에 넣어 가고 있었고, 안나는 그와 함께 영지로 가고 싶다는 욕망을 느끼고 있었는데, 그때 문득 그가 부끄러움을 느껴야 마땅하다는 생각이, 그리고 그녀 자신이 바로 그 같은 일을 부끄러워하고 있다는 생각이 들었다. 하지만 그가 부끄러워해야 할 일이 무엇이란 말인가? \"내가 부끄러워해야 할 일이 뭐가 있다고?\" 그녀는 상처받은 듯 놀라며 스스로에게 물었다. 그녀는 책을 내려놓고 의자 등받이에 기댄 채, 두 손으로 종이칼을 꽉 움켜쥐었다. 아무것도 없었다. 그녀는 모스크바에서의 추억을 하나하나 되짚어 보았다. 모두 좋고 즐거운 것들이었다. 무도회를 떠올렸고, 브론스키와 노예처럼 흠모하던 그의 얼굴을 떠올렸으며, 그와 함께한 자기 행동을 모조리 떠올렸다. 부끄러울 만한 것은 하나도 없었다. 그런데도 기억 속의 바로 그 대목에 이르면 부끄러운 감정이 더욱 강해졌으니, 마치 어떤 내면의 목소리가, 그녀가 브론스키를 떠올리는 바로 그 순간에 \"따뜻해, 아주 따뜻해, 뜨거워\" 하고 말하기라도 하는 것 같았다. \"그래, 대체 뭐야?\" 그녀는 단호하게 스스로에게 말하며 소파 위에서 자리를 고쳐 앉았다. \"이게 무슨 뜻이지? 내가 이걸 똑바로 마주 보기가 두려운 걸까? 아니, 도대체 뭐란 말이야? 나와 이 젊은 장교 사이에, 누구와도 흔히 있을 법한 그런 사이 말고 다른 어떤 관계가 있다거나, 있을 수 있다는 건 아니겠지?\" 그녀는 경멸하듯 웃고는 다시 책을 집어 들었다. 하지만 이제는 읽은 것을 도무지 좇을 수가 없었다. 그녀는 종이칼을 창유리에 갖다 댔다가, 그 매끄럽고 서늘한 면을 뺨에 대 보고는, 아무런 까닭도 없이 별안간 그녀를 사로잡은 기쁨에 거의 소리 내어 웃을 뻔했다. 그녀는 마치 자기 신경이 어떤 나사못 위에서 점점 더 팽팽하게 죄어지는 현(絃)이 된 것 같은 기분이었다. 두 눈이 점점 더 크게 뜨이고, 손가락과 발가락이 신경질적으로 떨리며, 가슴속 무언가가 숨을 짓누르는 것을 느꼈고, 그러는 동안 어슴푸레한 빛 속에서 온갖 형체와 소리가 낯설게 또렷한 모습으로 그녀를 덮쳐 오는 것 같았다. 끊임없이 의심의 순간들이 그녀를 덮쳤으니, 기차가 앞으로 가는 건지 뒤로 가는 건지, 아니면 아예 멈춰 서 있는 건지, 옆에 있는 것이 안누슈카인지 낯선 사람인지 확신할 수 없었다. \"팔걸이 위에 있는 저건 뭐지, 모피 외투인가 아니면 무슨 짐승인가? 그리고 나 자신은 뭐지? 나인가, 아니면 다른 어떤 여자인가?\" 그녀는 이 헛것에 자신을 내맡기기가 두려웠다. 하지만 무언가가 그녀를 그쪽으로 끌어당겼고, 그녀는 마음먹기에 따라 그것에 굴복할 수도 저항할 수도 있었다. 그녀는 정신을 차리려고 일어나 무릎덮개와 따뜻한 옷의 케이프를 벗었다. 잠시 그녀는 평정을 되찾았고, 단추가 몇 개 떨어져 나간 긴 외투를 입고 들어온 깡마른 농부가 화부(火夫)라는 것을, 그가 온도계를 들여다보고 있다는 것을, 그의 뒤를 따라 문으로 바람과 눈이 몰아쳐 들어왔다는 것을 깨달았다. 하지만 그러고 나자 모든 것이 다시 흐릿해졌다…… 허리가 긴 그 농부는 벽에서 무언가를 갉아먹고 있는 것 같았고, 노부인은 객차 끝까지 두 다리를 쭉 뻗기 시작하며 객차 안을 검은 구름으로 가득 채웠다. 그러더니 누군가가 갈가리 찢기기라도 하는 듯한 무서운 비명과 쾅쾅거리는 소리가 났고, 이어 눈앞에서 붉은 불꽃이 눈을 멀게 할 듯 번쩍이더니 벽 하나가 솟아올라 모든 것을 가려 버리는 것 같았다. 안나는 마치 자기가 아래로 가라앉는 것 같은 기분이었다. 하지만 그것은 무섭지 않고 오히려 황홀했다. 둘둘 싸맨 채 눈을 뒤집어쓴 남자의 목소리가 그녀의 귓가에 무언가를 외쳤다. 그녀는 일어나 정신을 가다듬었다. 기차가 어느 역에 닿았다는 것을, 그리고 이 사람이 차장이라는 것을 깨달았다. 그녀는 안누슈카에게 벗어 두었던 케이프와 숄을 건네 달라고 부탁해 그것들을 걸치고 문 쪽으로 갔다.\n\n\"내리시려고요?\" 안누슈카가 물었다.\n\n\"응, 바람을 좀 쐬고 싶어. 여긴 너무 더워.\" 그러고 그녀는 문을 열었다. 휘몰아치는 눈과 바람이 그녀를 맞으러 달려들어 문을 두고 그녀와 다투었다. 하지만 그녀는 그 다툼이 즐거웠다.\n\n그녀는 문을 열고 밖으로 나갔다. 바람은 마치 그녀를 노리고 매복해 있던 것 같았다. 신이 난 듯 휘파람 소리를 내며 그녀를 낚아채 데려가려 했지만, 그녀는 차가운 문기둥에 매달려 치맛자락을 붙든 채 승강장으로, 객차들이 가려 주는 그늘 밑으로 내려섰다. 계단 위에서는 바람이 거셌으나, 승강장 위, 객차들이 바람을 막아 주는 쪽은 잠잠했다. 그녀는 얼어붙은 눈 섞인 공기를 즐거이 깊이 들이마시고는, 객차 옆에 서서 승강장과 불 밝힌 역사를 둘러보았다.\n\n# Chapter 30\n\n사납게 몰아치는 폭풍이 객차의 바퀴 사이로, 비계 주위로, 역 모퉁이를 돌며 휘파람 소리를 내며 휘몰아쳤다. 객차며 기둥이며 사람들이며, 눈에 보이는 모든 것이 한쪽 면에 눈을 뒤집어쓴 채 점점 더 두껍게 덮여 가고 있었다. 한순간 폭풍이 잠잠해지는가 싶다가도, 이내 다시 도저히 맞서 버틸 수 없을 것만 같은 기세로 들이닥쳤다. 그러는 동안에도 사람들은 이리저리 뛰어다니며 즐겁게 이야기를 나누었고, 큰 문을 끊임없이 여닫는 통에 플랫폼 위에서 그들의 발소리가 자작자작 울렸다. 한 남자의 구부정한 그림자가 그녀의 발치를 스쳐 지나갔고, 쇠를 두드리는 망치 소리가 들려왔다. \"그 전보 이리 내!\" 반대편의 폭풍 어린 어둠 속에서 성난 목소리가 들려왔다. \"이쪽이오! 28호!\" 다시 여러 목소리가 제각각 외쳤고, 눈을 뒤집어쓴 사람들이 몸을 싸맨 채 뛰어 지나갔다. 불붙인 담배를 문 신사 둘이 그녀 곁을 지나갔다. 그녀는 신선한 공기를 한 번 더 깊이 들이마시고는, 객차로 돌아가려고 토시에서 손을 빼 문기둥을 잡으려던 참이었다. 그때 군용 외투를 입은 또 한 남자가 그녀 바로 옆으로 다가와 그녀와 가로등의 깜빡이는 불빛 사이로 들어섰다. 그녀가 돌아보자, 바로 그 순간 브론스키의 얼굴을 알아보았다. 그는 모자챙에 손을 갖다 대며 그녀에게 인사하고는, 무언가 필요한 것이 있는지, 자기가 무엇이든 도와드릴 수 있는지 물었다. 그녀는 대답도 없이 꽤 오랫동안 그를 바라보았고, 그가 그늘 속에 서 있는데도 그의 얼굴 표정과 두 눈을 보았다, 아니 본 듯한 느낌이 들었다. 그것은 어제 그녀를 그토록 흔들어 놓았던 바로 그 경건한 황홀의 표정이었다. 지난 며칠 동안 그녀는 여러 번, 그리고 불과 몇 분 전에도 다시, 브론스키란 자기에게 어디서나 마주치는, 늘 한결같은 그 수백 명의 젊은이들 가운데 하나일 뿐이며, 결코 그에게 한 가닥 생각조차 허락하지 않으리라고 스스로에게 다짐했었다. 그러나 지금 그를 만난 첫 순간, 그녀는 기쁨에 찬 자랑스러움에 사로잡혔다. 그가 무엇 때문에 왔는지 물어볼 필요도 없었다. 그가 그녀에게 직접 말이라도 한 듯, 그가 그녀가 있는 곳에 있기 위해 여기 왔다는 것을 그녀는 분명히 알고 있었다.\n\n\"당신이 떠나는 줄은 몰랐어요. 무엇 때문에 오시는 거죠?\" 그녀가 문기둥을 잡았던 손을 떨구며 말했다. 그리고 억누를 수 없는 기쁨과 열망이 그녀의 얼굴에서 빛났다.\n\n\"제가 무엇 때문에 오느냐고요?\" 그가 그녀의 눈을 똑바로 들여다보며 되뇌었다. \"제가 당신이 있는 곳에 있으려고 왔다는 걸 당신은 알고 계시지요.\" 그가 말했다. \"어쩔 수가 없습니다.\"\n\n바로 그 순간, 바람이 마치 모든 장애물을 넘어서기라도 한 듯 객차 지붕에서 눈을 날려 보냈고, 어딘가에서 뜯어낸 양철판을 덜그럭거렸다. 그러는 사이 기관차의 쉰 기적 소리가 앞쪽에서 구슬프고 음울하게 울부짖었다. 폭풍의 그 온갖 무시무시함이 이제 그녀에게는 더욱 찬란하게 느껴졌다. 그는 그녀의 영혼이 듣기를 갈망하던 것을, 비록 이성으로는 두려워하던 것을 말해 버린 것이다. 그녀는 아무 대답도 하지 않았고, 그는 그녀의 얼굴에서 갈등을 보았다.\n\n\"제 말이 마음에 들지 않으셨다면, 용서해 주십시오.\" 그가 겸손하게 말했다.\n\n그는 정중하게, 공손하게 말했지만, 그러면서도 어찌나 단호하고 끈질기게 말하던지, 그녀는 한참 동안 아무 대답도 할 수 없었다.\n\n\"당신이 하는 말은 옳지 않아요. 그러니 부탁드려요, 당신이 좋은 분이라면, 제가 잊듯이 당신이 한 말을 잊어 주세요.\" 그녀가 마침내 말했다.\n\n\"당신의 말 한마디도, 몸짓 하나도 저는 결코 잊지 못할 겁니다, 잊을 수도 없습니다……\"\n\n\"그만, 그만하세요!\" 그녀는 그가 게걸스레 들여다보고 있는 자기 얼굴에 애써 엄한 표정을 지으려 안간힘을 쓰며 외쳤다. 그러고는 차가운 문기둥을 움켜쥐고 계단을 기어올라 잰걸음으로 객차의 통로 안으로 들어섰다. 그러나 그 좁은 통로에서 그녀는 걸음을 멈추고, 방금 일어난 일을 머릿속에서 곱씹어 보았다. 자기가 한 말도, 그가 한 말도 떠올릴 수는 없었지만, 그녀는 그 순간의 대화가 두 사람을 무섭도록 가깝게 만들어 놓았음을 본능적으로 깨달았다. 그리고 그 일에 그녀는 공포에 사로잡히면서도 더없이 행복했다. 몇 초 동안 가만히 서 있다가, 그녀는 객차 안으로 들어가 자기 자리에 앉았다. 전에 그녀를 괴롭히던 그 극도로 긴장된 상태가 다시 돌아왔을 뿐 아니라 더욱 심해졌고, 마침내 지나친 긴장으로 자기 안에서 무언가 뚝 끊어지지나 않을까 매 순간 두려울 만큼 극에 달했다. 그녀는 밤새 한숨도 자지 못했다. 그러나 그 신경의 긴장에도, 또 그녀의 상상을 가득 채운 환영들에도 불쾌하거나 음울한 것이라곤 전혀 없었다. 오히려 더없이 행복하고 뜨겁게 타오르며 가슴 벅찬 무언가가 있었다. 아침이 가까워질 무렵 안나는 자리에 앉은 채 깜빡 잠이 들었고, 깨어 보니 날은 밝아 있었고 기차는 페테르부르크에 가까이 와 있었다. 곧바로 집 생각, 남편과 아들 생각, 그리고 그날과 이튿날의 자질구레한 일들이 그녀에게 밀려들었다.\n\n페테르부르크에서 기차가 멈추고 그녀가 내리자마자 그녀의 주의를 끈 첫 번째 사람은 남편이었다. \"어머나! 저이 귀가 왜 저렇게 보이지?\" 그녀는 차갑고 위엄 있는 그의 모습을, 특히 그 순간 둥근 모자챙을 떠받치고 있는 것처럼 그녀의 눈에 띈 두 귀를 바라보며 생각했다. 그녀를 알아본 그는 마중하러 다가왔는데, 그의 입술은 늘 짓던 그 빈정대는 미소로 일그러졌고, 크고 지친 두 눈은 그녀를 똑바로 바라보고 있었다. 그의 고집스럽고 지친 시선과 마주쳤을 때, 마치 그가 다른 모습이기를 기대하기라도 했던 듯, 불쾌한 감각이 그녀의 가슴을 옥죄었다. 무엇보다 그녀를 충격에 빠뜨린 것은 그를 만나면서 느낀 자기 자신에 대한 불만이었다. 그것은 남편과의 관계에서 그녀가 늘 느껴 온, 위선에 대한 자각과도 같은, 은밀하고 익숙한 감정이었다. 그러나 지금까지 그녀는 그 감정을 눈여겨보지 않았는데, 이제는 그것을 또렷하고도 고통스럽게 의식하게 되었다.\n\n\"그래, 보시다시피, 당신의 다정한 남편이, 결혼 첫해처럼 헌신적인 남편이, 당신을 만나고 싶어 안달이 나서 왔소.\" 그가 신중하고 높은 목소리로, 그리고 그가 그녀에게 거의 언제나 쓰는 그 어조로, 그러니까 자기가 하는 말을 진지하게 받아들이는 사람이 있다면 누구든 비웃는 듯한 그 어조로 말했다.\n\n\"세료자는 잘 있나요?\" 그녀가 물었다.\n\n\"이게 내 열정에 대한 보답이란 말이오?\" 그가 말했다. \"그 애는 아주 잘 있소……\"\n\n# Chapter 31\n\n브론스키는 그날 밤 내내 잠들려고 시도조차 하지 않았다. 그는 안락의자에 앉아 정면을 똑바로 응시하거나 타고 내리는 사람들을 훑어보았다. 전에도 그가 거리낌 없는 침착한 태도로 자기를 모르는 사람들에게 강한 인상을 남기곤 했다면, 지금은 그 어느 때보다 더 거만하고 자신만만해 보였다. 그는 마치 사람들을 사물 보듯 바라보았다. 맞은편에 앉은, 법원 서기인 신경질적인 젊은이는 그 시선 때문에 그를 미워했다. 젊은이는 그에게 불을 빌려 달라고 청하고는 말을 걸어왔고, 심지어 자기가 사물이 아니라 사람이라는 걸 느끼게 하려고 일부러 그를 밀치기까지 했다. 그러나 브론스키는 램프를 보던 그대로 그를 바라보았고, 젊은이는 자신을 사람으로 인정하지 않는 이 거부의 압박 아래 침착함을 잃어 가는 걸 느끼며 얼굴을 찌푸렸다.\n\n브론스키에게는 아무것도, 아무도 보이지 않았다. 그는 자신을 왕처럼 느꼈는데, 그것은 자기가 안나에게 어떤 인상을 남겼다고 믿어서가 아니라—그는 아직 그것을 믿지 않았다—그녀가 자기에게 남긴 인상이 그에게 행복과 자긍심을 안겨 주었기 때문이었다.\n\n이 모든 일에서 무엇이 나올지 그는 알지 못했고, 생각조차 하지 않았다. 그는 지금까지 흩어지고 허비되어 온 자기의 모든 힘이 한곳에 모여, 무서운 기세로 하나의 더없이 행복한 목표를 향해 쏠리는 것을 느꼈다. 그리고 그는 그것이 행복했다. 그가 아는 것이라곤 자기가 그녀에게 진실을 말했다는 것, 그녀가 있는 곳으로 자기가 왔다는 것, 자기 삶의 모든 행복이, 자기에게 삶의 유일한 의미가 이제 그녀를 보고 듣는 데에 있다는 것뿐이었다. 그리고 볼로고보에서 셀처 물을 마시려고 객차에서 내렸다가 안나를 보았을 때, 그의 첫마디는 자기도 모르게 자기가 생각하던 것을 그대로 그녀에게 전하고 말았다. 그리고 그는 그것을 그녀에게 말했다는 것이, 이제 그녀가 그것을 알고 그것을 생각하고 있다는 것이 기뻤다. 그는 밤새 한숨도 자지 못했다. 객차로 돌아온 그는 끊임없이, 그녀를 보았던 모든 모습, 그녀가 내뱉었던 모든 말을 되짚었고, 그의 상상 속에는, 감격으로 그의 심장을 아련하게 하면서, 있을 법한 미래의 그림들이 떠다녔다.\n\n페테르부르크에서 기차에서 내렸을 때, 그는 잠 못 이룬 밤을 보냈는데도 마치 찬물로 목욕이라도 한 듯 날카롭고 상쾌한 기분이었다. 그는 그녀가 내리기를 기다리며 자기 객실 곁에 멈춰 섰다. \"한 번 더.\" 그는 자기도 모르게 미소를 지으며 혼잣말을 했다. \"한 번 더 나는 그녀가 걷는 모습을, 그녀의 얼굴을 보게 될 거야. 그녀는 무언가 말하겠지, 고개를 돌리고, 흘끗 보고, 어쩌면 미소를 지을지도 몰라.\" 그러나 그녀를 알아보기 전에, 그는 그녀의 남편을 보았다. 역장이 공손하게 그를 군중 사이로 안내하고 있었다. \"아, 그렇지! 남편.\" 이제 처음으로 브론스키는 그녀에게 딸린 사람이, 남편이 있다는 사실을 분명하게 깨달았다. 그녀에게 남편이 있다는 것은 알고 있었지만, 그 존재를 거의 믿지 않았는데, 지금에야 그를, 그의 머리와 어깨를, 검은 바지를 입은 그의 다리를 온전히 믿게 되었다. 특히 이 남편이 소유주 같은 마음으로 침착하게 그녀의 팔을 잡는 것을 보았을 때 그러했다.\n\n페테르부르크다운 얼굴에 엄격하고 자신만만한 풍채를 한 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가, 둥근 모자를 쓰고, 다소 튀어나온 등을 한 모습을 보자, 그는 그의 존재를 믿게 되었고, 불쾌한 감각을 느꼈다. 갈증에 시달리던 사람이 샘에 다다랐는데, 거기서 이미 물을 마셔 흙탕으로 만들어 놓은 개나 양이나 돼지를 발견했을 때 느낄 법한 그런 감각이었다. 엉덩이를 흔들고 발을 평평하게 디디며 걷는 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 걸음걸이가 특히 브론스키의 심사를 거슬렀다. 그는 그녀를 사랑할 의심할 바 없는 권리를 자기 자신 외에는 그 누구에게서도 인정할 수 없었다. 그러나 그녀는 여전히 그대로였고, 그녀를 보는 것은 그를 똑같이 동요시켰다. 그를 육체적으로 되살려 내고, 그를 흔들어 놓고, 그의 영혼을 황홀감으로 가득 채웠다. 그는 이등칸에서 달려온 독일인 시종에게 짐을 챙겨 먼저 가라고 이르고는, 자기는 그녀에게 다가갔다. 그는 부부의 첫 만남을 보았고, 연인의 통찰력으로 그녀가 남편에게 말할 때의 가벼운 서먹함의 기색을 알아챘다. \"아니야, 그녀는 그를 사랑하지 않고, 사랑할 수도 없어.\" 그는 혼자 단정 지었다.\n\n그가 안나 아르카지예브나에게 다가가던 바로 그 순간, 그는 그녀가 자기가 가까이 있는 것을 의식하고 있다는 것을, 그래서 그녀가 둘레를 둘러보다가 자기를 보고는 다시 남편에게로 고개를 돌렸다는 것을 기쁘게 알아챘다.\n\n\"밤새 편히 주무셨습니까?\" 그는 그녀와 그녀의 남편에게 동시에 인사를 하며 물었고, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 그 인사를 자기 몫으로 받아들이든 말든, 그것을 알은체하든 말든 마음대로 하도록 맡겨 두었다.\n\n\"고마워요, 아주 편히 잤어요.\" 그녀가 대답했다.\n\n그녀의 얼굴은 피곤해 보였고, 미소와 눈빛으로 엿보이던 그 생기 넘치는 활기가 거기엔 없었다. 그러나 그녀가 그를 흘끗 보던 한순간, 그녀의 눈 속에서 무언가가 번뜩였고, 비록 그 번뜩임이 곧 사라지긴 했지만, 그는 그 순간이 행복했다. 그녀는 남편이 브론스키를 아는지 알아보려고 그를 흘끗 보았다. 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 이 사람이 누구인지 어렴풋이 떠올리면서 불쾌한 듯 브론스키를 바라보았다. 브론스키의 침착함과 자신감은 여기서, 마치 낫이 돌에 부딪치듯, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 차가운 자신감과 부딪쳤다.\n\n\"브론스키 백작이에요.\" 안나가 말했다.\n\n\"아! 우리는 서로 아는 사이인 듯합니다.\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 무심하게 손을 내밀며 말했다.\n\n\"올 때는 어머니와 함께 떠나셨다가 돌아올 때는 아들과 함께 오셨군요.\" 그는 마치 한 음절 한 음절이 자기가 베푸는 별개의 호의이기라도 한 듯, 한 음절씩 또박또박 발음하며 말했다.\n\n\"휴가에서 돌아오시는 길인가 보군요?\" 그는 이렇게 말하고는, 대답도 기다리지 않고 농담조로 아내에게 돌아섰다. \"그래, 모스크바에서 헤어질 때 눈물깨나 흘렸겠지?\"\n\n이렇게 아내에게 말을 건넴으로써 그는 자기를 좀 내버려 두기를 바란다는 것을 브론스키에게 알렸고, 그를 향해 살짝 몸을 돌리며 모자에 손을 댔다. 그러나 브론스키는 안나 아르카지예브나에게로 돌아섰다.\n\n\"댁으로 방문할 영광을 누릴 수 있었으면 합니다.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 피곤한 눈으로 브론스키를 흘끗 보았다.\n\n\"기쁘게 맞이하지요.\" 그가 쌀쌀맞게 말했다. \"월요일마다 우리는 집에 있습니다. 정말 다행이오.\" 그는 브론스키 따위는 아예 무시하고 아내에게 말했다. \"당신을 마중하러 나올 삼십 분이 마침 있어서 말이오, 그래야 내 헌신을 증명할 수 있을 테니.\" 그는 여전히 같은 농담조로 말을 이었다.\n\n\"당신은 헌신을 너무 강조하시니 내가 그걸 그리 대단하게 여길 수가 없네요.\" 그녀도 같은 농담조로 대꾸하면서, 자기도 모르게 뒤에서 들려오는 브론스키의 발소리에 귀를 기울이고 있었다. \"하지만 그게 나랑 무슨 상관이야?\" 그녀는 속으로 중얼거리고는, 자기가 없는 동안 세료자가 어떻게 지냈는지 남편에게 묻기 시작했다.\n\n\"오, 아주 잘 지냈소! 마리에트 말로는 그 애가 무척 착하게 굴었다는군. 그리고…… 당신을 실망시켜야겠지만…… 그 애는 당신 남편만큼 당신을 그리워하지 않았다오. 하지만 다시 한번 merci, 여보, 하루를 내줘서 말이오. 우리의 사랑스러운 Samovar가 무척 기뻐할 거요.\" (그는 사교계에 잘 알려진 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인을, 그녀가 늘 흥분으로 부글부글 끓어오르는 까닭에, 사모바르라고 부르곤 했다.) \"그 부인이 계속 당신 안부를 물었다오. 그리고 말이오, 내가 감히 조언을 좀 하자면, 당신 오늘 그 부인을 찾아가 보는 게 좋겠소. 그 부인이 모든 일을 얼마나 가슴에 담아 두는지 당신도 알지 않소. 지금도, 자기 일만으로도 벅찰 텐데, 오블론스키 부부를 화해시키는 일로 애를 태우고 있다오.\"\n\n* merci: 프랑스어로 '고맙소'. * Samovar: 러시아식 찻물 끓이는 주전자. 사모바르.\n리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인은 그녀 남편의 친구였고, 안나가 남편을 통해 가장 가깝게 관계 맺고 있던 페테르부르크 사교계의 한 동아리의 중심인물이었다.\n\n\"하지만 내가 그분께 편지를 쓴 거 아시잖아요?\"\n\n\"그래도 자세한 이야기를 듣고 싶어 할 거요. 너무 피곤하지 않거든 찾아가 보구려, 여보. 자, 콘드라티가 당신을 마차로 데려다줄 거요, 나는 위원회에 가야 하니. 또다시 혼자 저녁을 먹지 않아도 되겠군.\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 더 이상 빈정대는 어조가 아니게 말을 이었다. \"내가 얼마나 그리웠는지 당신은 믿지 못할 거요…….\" 그러고는 그녀의 손을 오래도록 꼭 쥐고 의미심장한 미소를 지으며 그녀를 마차에 태웠다.\n\n# Chapter 32\n\n집에서 안나를 가장 먼저 맞이한 사람은 아들이었다. 가정교사가 부르는 소리에도 아랑곳없이 아이는 계단을 뛰어 내려와 그녀에게로 달려왔고, 미친 듯한 기쁨에 차 \"엄마! 엄마!\" 하고 소리쳤다. 그녀에게 달려온 아이는 그녀의 목에 매달렸다.\n\n\"엄마라고 했잖아요!\" 아이가 가정교사를 향해 외쳤다. \"내가 알았다고요!\"\n\n남편이 그랬듯 아들 역시 안나에게 실망과 비슷한 감정을 불러일으켰다. 그녀는 실제보다 더 나은 아이를 상상하고 있었던 것이다. 그녀는 있는 그대로의 아이를 즐기기 위해 현실로 자신을 끌어내려야 했다. 하지만 있는 그대로라 해도 아이는 사랑스러웠다. 금빛 곱슬머리와 푸른 눈, 그리고 팽팽하게 끌어 올린 양말 속의 통통하고 우아한 작은 다리. 아이의 순진하고 신뢰에 찬, 사랑이 담긴 눈길을 마주하고 그 천진한 질문을 들으니, 안나는 아이가 곁에 있다는 느낌과 그 어루만짐, 그리고 마음의 위안 속에서 거의 육체적인 기쁨을 느꼈다. 안나는 돌리네 아이들이 보낸 선물을 꺼냈고, 모스크바의 타냐가 어떤 여자아이인지, 또 타냐가 글을 읽을 줄 알고 다른 아이들까지 가르친다는 이야기를 아들에게 들려주었다.\n\n\"그럼, 나는 그 누나만큼 착하지 않아요?\" 세료자가 물었다.\n\n\"내겐 네가 이 세상 누구보다도 더 착하지.\"\n\n\"나도 알아요.\" 세료자가 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n안나가 미처 커피를 다 마시기도 전에 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인이 왔다는 전갈이 왔다. 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인은 키가 크고 살집이 있는 여자로, 병색이 도는 누런 얼굴에 깊은 생각에 잠긴 듯한 멋진 검은 눈을 하고 있었다. 안나는 그녀를 좋아했지만, 오늘은 그녀의 온갖 결점을 마치 처음 보는 것 같은 기분이었다.\n\n\"그래, 여보, 그러니까 화해를 했나요?\" 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인이 방에 들어서자마자 물었다.\n* 화해의 표시인 올리브 가지를 가져왔느냐는 비유적 표현.\n\n\"네, 다 끝났어요. 그런데 우리가 생각했던 것보다 훨씬 덜 심각한 일이었어요.\" 안나가 대답했다. \"제 belle-sœur가 대체로 너무 성급해서요.\"\n\n* belle-sœur: 프랑스어로 '올케' 또는 '시누이'. 여기서는 올케 돌리를 가리킨다.\n하지만 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인은 자기와 상관없는 모든 일에는 관심을 보이면서도, 정작 자기가 관심을 두는 것은 절대 들으려 하지 않는 버릇이 있었다. 그녀는 안나의 말을 가로막았다.\n\n\"그래요, 세상엔 슬픔과 악이 넘쳐 나죠. 나는 오늘 정말 속이 상해요.\"\n\n\"어머, 왜요?\" 안나가 미소를 누르려 애쓰며 물었다.\n\n\"진리를 위해 보람도 없이 싸우는 데 지치기 시작했어요. 가끔은 그것 때문에 마음이 완전히 무너질 지경이에요. '작은 자매회'가\" (이것은 종교적·애국적인 자선 단체였다) \"아주 잘 굴러가고 있었는데, 그 양반들하고는 도무지 아무 일도 할 수가 없어요.\" 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인이 운명에 빈정대듯 순응하는 어조로 덧붙였다. \"그들은 어떤 생각에 달려들어서 그것을 왜곡하고, 그러고는 그걸 아주 옹졸하고 천하게 처리해 버려요. 당신 남편을 포함한 두세 사람만이 이 일의 중요성을 다 이해하고, 나머지는 그냥 그걸 끌어내릴 뿐이에요. 어제 프라브딘이 내게 편지를 보냈는데…….\"\n\n프라브딘은 국외에서 잘 알려진 범슬라브주의자였고, 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인은 그의 편지 요지를 설명했다.\n\n그런 다음 백작 부인은 교회 통합 사업에 반대하는 더 많은 불화와 암투에 대해 이야기하고는, 그날 어느 단체 모임에도 가야 하고 또 슬라브 위원회에도 가야 한다며 황급히 떠났다.\n\n\"물론 전에도 다 마찬가지였지. 그런데 왜 나는 전에는 이걸 알아채지 못했을까?\" 안나는 스스로에게 물었다. \"아니면 오늘 그 사람이 무척 신경이 곤두서 있었던 걸까? 정말 우스운 일이야. 저 사람의 목적은 선을 행하는 거고, 기독교도이면서도 늘 화를 내고 있어. 게다가 늘 적이 있는데, 그것도 언제나 기독교와 선행의 이름으로 만들어진 적이지.\"\n\n리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인이 다녀간 뒤에는 또 다른 친구, 어느 수석 비서관의 아내가 와서 시내의 온갖 소식을 전해 주었다. 세 시에 그녀도 만찬에 오겠다고 약속하며 떠났다. 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 관청에 있었다. 혼자 남은 안나는 만찬 때까지의 시간을 아들의 식사를 거드는 데(아이는 부모와 따로 식사를 했다), 그리고 자기 물건들을 정리하고, 책상 위에 쌓여 있던 쪽지며 편지들을 읽고 답하는 데 보냈다.\n\n여행 중에 느꼈던 까닭 모를 부끄러움도, 그 흥분도 이제 완전히 사라지고 없었다. 익숙한 삶의 조건 속에서 그녀는 다시금 단호하고 떳떳한 자신을 느꼈다.\n\n그녀는 어제 자신의 마음 상태를 의아하게 떠올렸다. \"그게 뭐였지? 아무것도 아니었어. 브론스키가 어리석은 말을 했고, 그건 쉽게 그만두게 할 수 있는 거였고, 나는 마땅히 해야 할 대로 대답했지. 그 일을 남편한테 이야기한다는 건 불필요하고 말도 안 되는 짓이야. 그 일을 이야기하는 건 아무 의미도 없는 일에 의미를 부여하는 셈이 될 테니까.\" 그녀는 페테르부르크에서 남편의 부하 직원 가운데 하나인 한 젊은이가 거의 고백이나 다름없는 말을 했던 일을 남편에게 이야기한 적이 있다는 것, 그리고 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 사교계에 사는 여자라면 누구나 그런 일을 겪게 마련이지만 자신은 그녀의 분별력을 전적으로 믿으며 결코 질투 때문에 그녀와 자신을 깎아내릴 수는 없노라고 대답했던 일을 떠올렸다. \"그러니 그 일을 이야기할 까닭이 없지 않은가? 게다가 정말이지, 하느님 감사하게도, 이야기할 거리가 아무것도 없어.\" 그녀는 스스로에게 말했다.\n\n# Chapter 33\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 네 시에 장관 회의에서 돌아왔지만, 흔히 그렇듯 아내에게 들를 틈이 없었다. 그는 청원서를 들고 자기를 기다리는 사람들을 만나고, 수석 비서가 가져온 서류 몇 장에 서명하기 위해 곧장 서재로 들어갔다. 만찬 시각(카레닌가에는 늘 몇몇 손님이 함께 식사하곤 했다)이 되자 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 사촌 노부인과 부서의 수석 비서 부부, 그리고 관직에 들이라고 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에게 추천받은 한 젊은이가 찾아왔다. 안나는 이 손님들을 맞이하러 응접실로 나갔다. 정확히 다섯 시, 표트르 대제 모양의 청동 시계가 다섯 번째 종을 치기 전에 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 흰 넥타이를 매고 별 두 개가 달린 연미복 차림으로 들어왔다. 만찬이 끝나는 대로 곧장 나가야 했기 때문이다. 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 삶은 매 순간이 나뉘어 빈틈없이 채워져 있었다. 그리고 날마다 자기 앞에 놓인 일을 다 해내기 위한 시간을 만들어 내려고, 그는 더없이 엄격한 시간 엄수를 고수했다. \"서두르지 않되 쉬지도 않는다\"가 그의 좌우명이었다. 그는 식당으로 들어와 모두에게 인사를 건넨 뒤, 아내에게 미소를 지으며 서둘러 자리에 앉았다.\n\n\"그래, 내 외로운 신세도 끝났군. 혼자 식사하는 게 얼마나 불편한지 당신은 믿지 못할 거요.\" (그는 '불편하다'는 말에 힘을 주었다.)\n\n만찬 자리에서 그는 아내와 모스크바 일에 관해 잠깐 이야기를 나누고, 비꼬는 듯한 미소를 지으며 스테판 아르카지치의 안부를 물었다. 그러나 대화는 대체로 일반적인 것이었고, 페테르부르크의 관청과 사교계 소식에 관한 것이었다. 만찬이 끝난 뒤 그는 손님들과 삼십 분쯤 함께 시간을 보낸 다음, 다시 미소를 지으며 아내의 손을 꼭 쥐고는 자리에서 물러나 마차를 타고 회의장으로 떠났다. 안나는 그날 저녁 그녀가 돌아왔다는 소식을 듣고 초대해 준 벳시 트베르스카야 공작부인에게도, 또 그날 저녁 좌석을 잡아 둔 극장에도 가지 않았다. 그녀가 외출하지 않은 것은 무엇보다 입으려고 했던 옷이 준비되지 않았기 때문이었다. 손님들이 떠난 뒤 옷차림을 살펴보게 된 안나는 몹시 화가 났다. 그녀는 대체로 큰돈을 들이지 않고도 옷을 잘 차려입는 솜씨를 지니고 있었고, 모스크바를 떠나기 전에 양재사에게 드레스 세 벌을 고쳐 달라고 맡겨 둔 터였다. 드레스는 알아볼 수 없을 만큼 손을 봐야 했고, 사흘 전에는 완성되어 있어야 마땅했다. 그런데 알고 보니 두 벌은 아예 손도 대지 않았고, 나머지 한 벌도 안나가 원하던 대로 고쳐지지 않았다. 양재사가 와서 변명하며 자기가 한 대로가 더 낫다고 우겨 댔고, 안나는 어찌나 화가 났던지 나중에 그 일을 떠올리면 부끄러울 정도였다. 마음의 평온을 온전히 되찾기 위해 그녀는 아이 방으로 가서 저녁 내내 아들과 함께 시간을 보냈고, 직접 아이를 재우며 성호를 그어 주고 이불을 덮어 주었다. 그녀는 어디에도 나가지 않고 저녁을 이렇게 잘 보낸 것이 기뻤다. 마음이 어찌나 가볍고 평온하던지, 기차 여행 중에 그토록 중요하게 여겨졌던 일들이 사실은 사교계 생활의 흔하고 사소한 사건 가운데 하나일 뿐이며, 다른 누구 앞에서도 또 자기 자신 앞에서도 부끄러워할 이유가 없다는 것을 또렷이 알 수 있었다. 안나는 영국 소설책을 들고 난롯가에 앉아 남편을 기다렸다. 정확히 아홉 시 반에 그녀는 그가 초인종 누르는 소리를 들었고, 그가 방으로 들어왔다.\n\n\"이제야 오셨네요!\" 그녀는 그에게 손을 내밀며 말했다.\n\n그는 아내의 손에 입을 맞추고 그녀 곁에 앉았다.\n\n\"그래, 결국 당신 방문이 성공적이었던 모양이군.\" 그가 그녀에게 말했다.\n\n\"네, 그래요.\" 그녀는 이렇게 대답하고는 처음부터 모든 일을 그에게 이야기하기 시작했다. 브론스카야 백작부인과 함께한 여행이며, 도착한 일이며, 역에서 있었던 사고까지. 그러고 나서 그녀는 처음에는 오라버니에 대해, 그다음에는 돌리에 대해 느꼈던 연민을 묘사했다.\n\n\"그 사람이 당신 오라버니라고는 하지만, 그런 남자를 비난에서 면해 줄 수는 없을 것 같소.\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 엄하게 말했다.\n\n안나는 미소를 지었다. 그녀는 그가 그 말을 한 것이 단지 가족이라는 사정도 자기 진심을 표명하는 것을 막을 수 없음을 보여 주기 위해서임을 알고 있었다. 그녀는 남편의 그런 특성을 알고 있었고, 또 그것을 좋아했다.\n\n\"모든 일이 그렇게 무난하게 끝나고, 당신이 다시 돌아와서 기쁘오.\" 그가 말을 이었다. \"그래, 내가 회의에서 통과시킨 새 법안에 대해 사람들이 뭐라고 하던가요?\"\n\n안나는 이 법안에 대해 아무것도 들은 바가 없었고, 그에게 그토록 중요한 일을 이렇게 쉽게 잊어버릴 수 있었다는 것에 양심의 가책을 느꼈다.\n\n\"여기서는 반대로 그 일이 큰 반향을 일으켰소.\" 그가 흡족한 미소를 지으며 말했다.\n\n안나는 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 그 일에 관해 자기에게 흐뭇한 무언가를 말하고 싶어 한다는 것을 알아챘고, 질문을 던져 그가 그 이야기를 하도록 이끌었다. 그는 여전히 흡족한 미소를 지으며 자기가 통과시킨 법안 덕분에 받은 갈채에 대해 그녀에게 이야기했다.\n\n\"나는 정말, 정말 기뻤소. 마침내 이 문제에 대한 이성적이고 확고한 견해가 우리 사이에 널리 퍼지고 있다는 걸 보여 주는 거니까.\"\n\n크림을 넣은 차 두 잔과 빵을 다 들고 나서, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 자리에서 일어나 서재 쪽으로 가려 했다.\n\n\"그런데 당신은 오늘 저녁 아무 데도 안 갔소? 따분했겠구려?\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"아, 아니에요!\" 그녀는 그를 따라 일어나 방을 가로질러 서재까지 그를 바래다주며 대답했다. \"지금은 무얼 읽고 계세요?\" 그녀가 물었다.\n\n\"지금은 뒤크 드 릴의 『Poésie des Enfers』를 읽고 있소.\" 그가 대답했다. \"아주 주목할 만한 책이지.\"\n\n안나는 사람들이 사랑하는 이의 약점을 보며 짓는 그런 미소를 지으며 그의 팔 밑으로 손을 넣어 서재 문까지 그를 바래다주었다. 그녀는 저녁마다 책을 읽는, 이제는 필수가 되어 버린 그의 습관을 알고 있었다. 또한 그녀는 그가 거의 모든 시간을 삼켜 버리는 공무에도 불구하고 지성계에 나타나는 주목할 만한 것이라면 무엇이든 따라잡는 것을 자신의 의무로 여긴다는 것도 알고 있었다. 그녀는 또한 그가 정치와 철학, 신학을 다루는 책에는 진심으로 관심이 있지만 예술은 그의 천성과는 전혀 동떨어진 것임을 알고 있었다. 그런데도, 아니 오히려 그렇기 때문에 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 예술계에서 일어나는 어떤 것도 그냥 지나치는 법이 없었고, 모든 것을 읽는 것을 자신의 의무로 삼았다. 그녀는 정치와 철학, 신학에 관해서는 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 종종 의심을 품고 탐구를 했지만, 예술과 시, 그리고 무엇보다 그가 도무지 이해하지 못하는 음악에 관해서는 더없이 분명하고 단호한 견해를 지니고 있다는 것을 알고 있었다. 그는 셰익스피어와 라파엘로, 베토벤에 대해, 시와 음악의 새로운 유파가 지니는 의의에 대해 이야기하기를 좋아했는데, 그 모든 것은 그에 의해 매우 두드러진 일관성을 가지고 분류되어 있었다.\n\n\"그럼, 잘 다녀오세요.\" 그녀는 서재 문 앞에서 말했는데, 그곳에는 이미 갓을 씌운 촛불과 물병이 그의 안락의자 옆에 놓여 있었다. \"나는 모스크바로 편지를 쓸게요.\"\n\n그는 아내의 손을 꼭 쥐고는 다시 입을 맞추었다.\n\n'그래도 저이는 좋은 사람이야. 진실하고, 마음씨가 곱고, 자기 분야에서는 비범하지.' 안나는 자기 방으로 돌아가며, 마치 그를 비난하면서 그를 사랑할 수 없다고 말한 누군가에게 그를 변호하기라도 하듯 속으로 되뇌었다. '그런데 왜 저이는 귀가 저렇게 이상하게 튀어나와 있을까? 아니면 머리를 깎은 건가?'\n\n정확히 열두 시, 안나가 아직 책상에 앉아 돌리에게 쓰는 편지를 마무리하고 있을 때, 그녀는 슬리퍼를 끌며 일정하게 다가오는 발소리를 들었고, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 막 씻고 머리를 빗은 채 책 한 권을 옆구리에 끼고 그녀에게로 들어왔다.\n\n\"이제 잘 시간이오, 잘 시간이야.\" 그가 의미심장한 미소를 지으며 말하고는 침실로 들어갔다.\n\n'그런데 저 사람한테 무슨 권리가 있어서 그이를 그런 눈으로 본 거지?' 안나는 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치를 향했던 브론스키의 시선을 떠올리며 생각했다.\n\n옷을 벗고 그녀는 침실로 들어갔다. 그러나 그녀의 얼굴에는 모스크바에 머무는 동안 두 눈과 미소에서 그토록 환히 빛나던 그 생기가 조금도 남아 있지 않았다. 오히려 지금은 그녀 안의 불이 꺼져 버린 듯, 어딘가 저 멀리에 숨어 버린 듯했다.\n\n# Chapter 34\n\n브론스키는 페테르부르크에서 모스크바로 떠나면서 모르스카야 거리에 있는 자신의 넓은 셋방을 친구이자 가장 아끼는 동료인 페트리츠키에게 맡겨 두었다.\n\n페트리츠키는 젊은 중위로, 특별히 좋은 연줄이 있는 것도 아니었고, 부유하기는커녕 늘 가망 없이 빚에 쪼들리는 신세였다. 저녁이면 으레 술에 취해 있었고, 온갖 우스꽝스럽고 망신스러운 추문 끝에 영창에 갇히기 일쑤였지만, 그래도 동료들에게나 상관들에게나 사랑받는 인물이었다. 정거장에서 열두 시에 자기 셋방에 도착한 브론스키는 바깥문 앞에 낯익은 삯마차 한 대가 세워져 있는 것을 보았다. 아직 자기 집 문밖에서 초인종을 누르는 동안, 그는 남자의 웃음소리와 혀짤배기 같은 여자 목소리, 그리고 페트리츠키의 목소리를 들었다. \"그게 그 악당들 중 하나거든, 들이지 마!\" 브론스키는 하인에게 자기가 왔다는 것을 알리지 말라고 이르고는, 첫 번째 방으로 조용히 들어섰다. 페트리츠키의 친구인 실톤 남작부인이, 발그레한 작은 얼굴에 아마빛 머리칼을 하고, 연보랏빛 새틴 가운을 눈부시게 차려입은 채, 카나리아처럼 파리 사투리의 수다로 방 안을 가득 채우며, 둥근 탁자에 앉아 커피를 끓이고 있었다. 외투를 걸친 페트리츠키와, 정복 차림으로 아마도 막 근무를 마치고 온 듯한 기병 대위 카메롭스키가 그녀의 양옆에 앉아 있었다.\n\n\"브라보! 브론스키!\" 페트리츠키가 의자를 드르륵 끌며 벌떡 일어나 외쳤다. \"집주인이 몸소 납셨군! 남작부인, 새 커피포트로 끓인 커피 좀 따라 주십시오. 아니, 자네가 올 줄은 몰랐는데! 자네 서재의 장식품이 마음에 드시길 바라네.\" 그는 남작부인을 가리키며 말했다. \"물론 서로 아는 사이지?\"\n\n\"그럼, 알다마다.\" 브론스키가 환한 미소를 지으며 남작부인의 작은 손을 잡고 말했다. \"무슨 소리야! 우리는 오랜 친구인데.\"\n\n\"여행에서 막 돌아오셨군요.\" 남작부인이 말했다. \"그럼 저는 날아가야겠어요. 아, 제가 방해가 된다면 당장 떠나겠어요.\"\n\n\"부인은 어디에 계시든 그곳이 집이지요, 남작부인.\" 브론스키가 말했다. \"잘 지냈나, 카메롭스키?\" 그가 카메롭스키와 차갑게 악수를 나누며 덧붙였다.\n\n\"보세요, 당신은 절대로 저렇게 멋들어진 말을 할 줄 모르잖아요.\" 남작부인이 페트리츠키를 돌아보며 말했다.\n\n\"무슨 소리, 그게 무슨 소용이람? 식사 후라면 나도 저만큼 좋은 말을 한다고.\"\n\n\"식사 후엔 그런 말에 무슨 값어치가 있겠어요? 자, 그럼 제가 커피를 끓여 드릴 테니, 가서 씻고 채비나 하세요.\" 남작부인이 다시 자리에 앉으며 새 커피포트의 나사를 조심스럽게 돌리며 말했다. \"피에르, 커피 좀 줘요.\" 그녀는 페트리츠키에게 말을 건넸는데, 그의 성을 줄여 피에르라고 불렀으며, 그와의 관계를 조금도 숨기지 않았다. \"제가 넣을게요.\"\n\n\"망쳐 놓을 거면서!\"\n\n\"아니, 안 망쳐요! 그런데, 부인은요?\" 남작부인이 갑자기 브론스키와 동료의 대화를 가로막으며 말했다. \"여기서 우리가 당신을 장가보내 놓았거든요. 부인을 데려오셨나요?\"\n\n\"아닙니다, 남작부인. 저는 보헤미안으로 태어났으니, 보헤미안으로 죽을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그게 훨씬 낫지요, 훨씬 나아요. 그 약속으로 악수해요.\"\n\n그러고 나서 남작부인은 브론스키를 붙들어 두고, 농담을 잔뜩 섞어 가며 자신의 새로운 인생 계획을 늘어놓기 시작했고, 그에게 조언을 구했다.\n\n\"그 사람이 한사코 이혼을 안 해 주려는 거예요! 자, 제가 어쩌면 좋겠어요?\" (그 사람이란 그녀의 남편이었다.) \"이제 그 사람을 상대로 소송을 걸려고 해요. 어떻게 하라고 충고해 주시겠어요? 카메롭스키, 커피 좀 봐줘요, 넘치고 있잖아요. 보세요, 저는 사업에 푹 빠져 있다니까요! 소송이 필요한 건, 제 재산을 꼭 찾아야 하기 때문이에요. 그 어처구니없는 짓 좀 이해해 보세요, 제가 자기를 배신했다는 핑계로,\" 그녀가 경멸하듯 말했다. \"그 사람은 제 재산을 가로채려 한다니까요.\"\n\n브론스키는 어여쁜 여자의 이 가벼운 재잘거림을 즐겁게 들으며, 그녀의 말에 맞장구를 치고, 반쯤 농담 섞인 충고를 건넸으며, 이내 그런 부류의 여자들과 이야기할 때면 으레 짓던 그 말투에 완전히 빠져들었다. 그가 속한 페테르부르크 사교계에서는 모든 사람이 서로 정반대되는 두 부류로 나뉘었다. 하나는 천박하고 멍청하며, 무엇보다 우스꽝스러운 하층 부류로, 남편이란 모름지기 합법적으로 결혼한 한 명의 아내와 살아야 한다든가, 처녀는 순결해야 하고 여자는 정숙해야 하며 남자는 사내답고 자제력 있고 강건해야 한다든가, 자식을 길러 내고 제 밥벌이를 하고 빚을 갚아야 한다든가 하는 갖가지 비슷한 어리석은 소리를 믿는 사람들이었다. 이것이 구식이고 우스꽝스러운 사람들의 부류였다. 그러나 또 다른 부류, 진짜 사람들의 부류가 있었다. 그들은 모두 이 부류에 속했고, 거기서 중요한 것은 우아하고 너그럽고 대담하고 쾌활하며, 부끄러운 기색 하나 없이 온갖 정열에 몸을 내맡기고, 그 밖의 모든 것은 비웃어 버리는 일이었다.\n\n처음 한순간만큼은, 모스크바에서 가지고 온 전혀 다른 세계의 인상이 남아 있던 브론스키도 흠칫 놀랐다. 그러나 곧, 낡은 슬리퍼에 발을 꿰어 넣듯이, 그는 늘 살아오던 그 가볍고 유쾌한 세계로 도로 미끄러져 들어갔다.\n\n커피는 끝내 제대로 끓여지지 못하고 모두에게 튀어 끓어 넘쳐 버렸지만, 정확히 그것에게 요구되던 역할을 해냈다. 다시 말해 한바탕 떠들썩하게 웃고 떠들 거리를 잔뜩 제공하고, 값비싼 양탄자와 남작부인의 가운을 망쳐 놓았던 것이다.\n\n\"자, 그럼 안녕히, 안 그러면 당신은 영영 씻지도 못할 테고, 신사가 저지를 수 있는 가장 큰 죄가 제 양심에 얹히게 될 테니까요. 그러니까 그 사람 목에 칼을 들이대라고 충고하시는 거죠?\"\n\n\"물론이지요, 그리고 당신 손이 그 사람 입술에서 너무 멀리 있지 않도록 처신하세요. 그 사람이 당신 손에 입을 맞출 테고, 그러면 모든 게 흡족하게 끝날 겁니다.\" 브론스키가 대답했다.\n\n\"그럼 프랑세 극장에서!\" 그러고는 치맛자락 스치는 소리와 함께 그녀는 사라졌다.\n\n카메롭스키도 일어났고, 브론스키는 그가 나가기를 기다리지 않고 악수를 한 뒤 화장실로 가 버렸다.\n\n브론스키가 씻는 동안, 페트리츠키는 브론스키가 페테르부르크를 떠난 뒤로 바뀐 자신의 처지를 대략적인 윤곽으로 들려주었다. 돈은 한 푼도 없었다. 아버지는 한 푼도 주지 않을 것이며 빚도 갚아 주지 않겠다고 했다. 재단사는 그를 영창에 처넣으려 하고, 다른 녀석도 마찬가지로 그를 가둬 버리겠다고 으름장을 놓고 있었다. 연대장은 이런 추문이 그치지 않으면 그가 군을 떠나야 할 거라고 통고했다. 남작부인으로 말하면, 특히 그녀가 끊임없이 돈을 빌려주겠다고 들이대기 시작한 뒤로는 정나미가 떨어질 만큼 지긋지긋했다. 그러나 그는 한 여자를 찾아냈는데—브론스키에게 보여 주겠다고 했다—기막힌 미인이요, 더없이 빼어나며, 엄격한 동방풍에다, \"이를테면 노예 레베카 같은 분위기지, 자네도 알 거 아닌가.\" 그는 또 베르코쇼프와도 다툼이 있어, 그에게 결투 입회인을 보낼 작정이었지만, 물론 아무 일도 없이 끝날 것이었다. 한마디로 모든 것이 더할 나위 없이 재미있고 유쾌했다. 그러고는 페트리츠키는 자기 처지에 관한 시시콜콜한 사정으로 동료를 더는 끌어들이지 않고, 곧장 온갖 흥미로운 소식을 들려주기 시작했다. 지난 삼 년을 보낸 그 낯익은 방의 낯익은 분위기 속에서 페트리츠키의 익숙한 이야기들을 들으면서, 브론스키는 자기가 늘 몸담아 온 그 태평스러운 페테르부르크 생활로 되돌아온다는 즐거운 감각을 느꼈다.\n\n\"설마!\" 그는 건강하고 불그레한 목덜미를 적시던 세면기의 페달을 내려놓으며 소리쳤다. \"설마!\" 그는 라우라가 페르팅호프를 차 버리고 밀레예프와 눈이 맞았다는 소식에 외쳤다. \"그래, 그 친구는 여전히 멍청하면서도 좋다고 헤벌쭉거리나? 그건 그렇고, 부줄루코프는 어떻게 지내?\"\n\n\"오, 부줄루코프에 관해선 정말 끝내주는 이야기가 있지!\" 페트리츠키가 외쳤다. \"그 친구가 무도회라면 사족을 못 쓰는 거 알잖아, 궁정 무도회는 한 번도 빠지는 법이 없거든. 그가 새 투구를 쓰고 큰 무도회에 갔단 말이야. 그 새 투구 본 적 있나? 아주 멋지고, 더 가벼워졌어. 자, 그래서 그가 서 있는데…… 아니, 이봐, 잘 들어 봐.\"\n\n\"듣고 있어.\" 브론스키가 거친 수건으로 몸을 문지르며 대답했다.\n\n\"대공비께서 어느 대사인지 하는 사람과 함께 다가오시는데, 하필이면 운 나쁘게도 그 새 투구 이야기를 그 친구한테 꺼내신 거야. 대공비께서는 기어이 그 새 투구를 대사에게 보여 주고 싶으셨던 거지. 마침 우리 친구가 거기 서 있는 게 보였단 말이야.\" (페트리츠키는 그가 투구를 들고 서 있던 모습을 흉내 냈다.) \"대공비께서 그 친구한테 투구를 달라고 청하셨는데, 이 친구가 안 드리는 거야. 그게 말이 돼? 자, 다들 그 친구한테 눈짓을 하고, 고갯짓을 하고, 눈살을 찌푸리는 거지—어서 드려, 어서! 그래도 안 드려. 벙어리처럼 입을 꾹 다물고 있어. 한번 상상해 봐!…… 그래서, 그…… 이름이 뭐더라, 아무튼 그 사람이…… 그 친구한테서 투구를 빼앗으려고 하는데…… 안 내놓는 거야!…… 그가 억지로 잡아채서 대공비께 바쳤지. '여기, 전하,' 하고 그가 말했어, '새 투구이옵니다.' 대공비께서 투구를 거꾸로 뒤집으셨는데, 그러자—한번 상상해 봐!—투구에서 배 한 알과 사탕이, 그것도 사탕이 무려 두 파운드나 와르르 쏟아진 거야!…… 이 귀여운 녀석이 거기다 잔뜩 쟁여 두었던 거지!\"\n\n브론스키는 와르르 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 그리고 한참 뒤에 다른 이야기를 하다가도, 그 투구가 떠오를 때면 튼튼하고 촘촘한 잇줄을 드러내며 건강한 웃음을 터뜨리곤 했다.\n\n소식을 모두 듣고 난 뒤, 브론스키는 시종의 도움을 받아 정복으로 갈아입고 신고를 하러 나섰다. 그 일을 마치면 형의 집과 벳시의 집에 들르고, 카레니나 부인을 만날 수 있을 만한 그 사교계에 발을 들여놓기 위해 몇 군데 방문할 작정이었다. 페테르부르크에서 늘 그랬듯이, 그는 밤늦도록 돌아오지 않을 생각으로 집을 나섰다.\n\n# PART TWO Chapter 1\n\n겨울이 끝나갈 무렵, 셰르바츠키가에서는 키티의 건강 상태와 쇠약해진 기력을 회복시키기 위해 어떤 조치를 취해야 할지 진단하는 진료 상담이 열렸다. 키티는 병을 앓고 있었는데, 봄이 다가오면서 상태가 더 나빠졌다. 가족 주치의는 그녀에게 처음에는 간유를, 다음에는 철분제를, 그다음에는 질산은을 주었다. 그러나 첫 번째 것도, 두 번째 것도, 세 번째 것도 한결같이 아무 효험이 없었고, 봄이 오자 그가 외국으로 가라고 조언했으므로, 어느 저명한 의사가 불려 왔다. 그 저명한 의사는 매우 잘생긴, 아직 젊은 축에 드는 남자로, 환자를 진찰하게 해 달라고 청했다. 그는 처녀의 수줍음이란 야만의 잔재에 불과하며, 아직 젊은 축에 드는 남자가 발가벗은 처녀를 다루는 것보다 더 자연스러운 일은 없다고, 보아하니 묘하게 흡족해하며 주장했다. 그는 매일 그런 일을 하기 때문에 그것을 자연스럽다고 여겼고, 그렇게 하면서도 자기 생각으로는 아무런 잘못된 점을 느끼지도 생각하지도 않았으며, 따라서 처녀의 수줍음을 단지 야만의 잔재로뿐만 아니라 자기 자신에 대한 모욕으로까지 여겼다.\n\n따를 수밖에 다른 도리가 없었다. 모든 의사가 같은 학교에서 공부하고, 같은 책을 읽고, 같은 학문을 익혔으며, 더구나 이 저명한 의사가 형편없는 의사라고 말하는 사람들도 있었지만, 공작 부인의 집안과 그 모임에서는 어쩐 이유에선지 이 저명한 의사만이 어떤 특별한 지식을 갖추고 있으며, 오직 그만이 키티를 구할 수 있다고 받아들여졌던 것이다. 수치심에 멍해진 채 어쩔 줄 모르는 환자를 꼼꼼히 진찰하고 청진한 뒤, 그 저명한 의사는 손을 정성껏 씻고 나서 응접실에 서서 공작과 이야기를 나누었다. 공작은 의사의 말을 들으며 미간을 찌푸리고 헛기침을 했다. 세상 물정을 어느 정도 겪었고, 바보도 환자도 아닌 사람으로서 그는 의술을 믿지 않았으며, 마음속으로는 이 모든 익살극에 화가 치밀어 올랐다. 게다가 어쩌면 그만이 키티가 앓는 병의 원인을 온전히 헤아리고 있었기 때문에 더욱 그랬다. \"건방진 멍청이 같으니!\" 그는 자기 딸의 증세를 두고 떠벌리는 저명한 의사의 수다를 들으며 생각했다. 한편 의사는 이 늙은 양반에 대한 경멸의 빛을 간신히 억누르며, 그의 지능 수준에 맞추어 자세를 낮추느라 애를 먹었다. 그는 이 노인에게 이야기해 봤자 소용없으며, 이 집안의 중심인물은 어머니라는 것을 알아차렸다. 그는 그녀 앞에 자기의 진주를 뿌리기로 마음먹었다. 바로 그 순간 공작 부인이 가족 주치의와 함께 응접실로 들어왔다. 공작은 이 모든 연극을 얼마나 우스꽝스럽게 여기는지 내색하지 않으려 애쓰며 자리를 떴다. 공작 부인은 정신이 산란하여 어찌할 바를 몰랐다. 그녀는 자기가 키티에게 죄를 지었다고 느꼈다.\n\n\"자, 선생님, 우리 운명을 결정해 주세요.\" 공작 부인이 말했다. \"다 말씀해 주세요.\"\n\n\"희망이 있나요?\" 그녀는 이렇게 묻고 싶었지만, 입술이 떨려 그 질문을 입 밖에 낼 수가 없었다. \"어떤가요, 선생님?\"\n\n\"곧 말씀드리겠습니다, 부인. 제 동료와 상의한 뒤에 제 소견을 부인께 말씀드리는 영광을 누리겠습니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 저희가 자리를 비켜 드리는 게 좋겠지요?\"\n\n\"좋으실 대로 하십시오.\"\n\n공작 부인은 한숨을 쉬며 나갔다.\n의사들만 남게 되자, 가족 주치의가 머뭇거리며 자기 소견을 설명하기 시작했다. 결핵성 질환의 초기가 시작된 것 같지만…… 하는 식이었다. 저명한 의사는 그의 말을 듣다가, 문장 한가운데서 자기의 커다란 금시계를 들여다보았다.\n\n\"그렇군요.\" 그가 말했다. \"하지만…….\"\n\n가족 주치의는 자기 소견을 늘어놓다 말고 공손히 입을 다물었다.\n\n\"결핵성 진행의 초기는, 아시다시피 우리가 규정할 수 없습니다. 공동이 생기기 전에는 확실한 것이 아무것도 없으니까요. 하지만 의심해 볼 수는 있지요. 그리고 징후들이 있습니다. 영양 부족, 신경의 흥분, 그 밖의 것들 말입니다. 문제는 이렇게 됩니다. 결핵성 진행의 징후가 있는 상황에서, 영양을 유지하기 위해 무엇을 해야 하는가, 하는 것이지요.\"\n\n\"하지만 아시다시피, 이런 경우에는 늘 그 배후에 정신적, 심적 원인이 있게 마련이지요.\" 가족 주치의가 묘한 미소를 띠며 한마디 끼워 넣었다.\n\n\"그렇지요, 그건 다 아는 사실입니다.\" 저명한 의사가 다시 시계를 흘끗 보며 대꾸했다. \"실례지만, 야우스키 다리는 이제 다 됐나요, 아니면 빙 둘러 가야 합니까?\" 그가 물었다. \"아! 됐군요. 오, 그렇다면 이십 분이면 갈 수 있겠습니다. 그래서, 우리가 이야기하던 문제는 이렇게 정리할 수 있겠지요. 영양을 유지하고 신경에 활기를 주는 것 말입니다. 이 둘은 서로 긴밀히 연관되어 있으니, 양쪽을 동시에 공략해야 합니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 외국 여행은 어떻습니까?\" 가족 주치의가 물었다.\n\n\"저는 외국 여행을 좋아하지 않습니다. 그리고 유념하십시오. 만약 결핵성 진행의 초기 단계라면, 그건 우리가 확신할 수 없는 것이지만, 외국 여행은 아무 소용이 없을 겁니다. 필요한 것은 영양을 개선하는 수단이지, 떨어뜨리는 것이 아니니까요.\" 그러고 나서 저명한 의사는 소덴 광천수를 이용한 치료 계획을 설명했는데, 그것은 분명 무엇보다도 해를 끼칠 일이 없다는 근거에서 처방된 요법이었다.\n\n가족 주치의는 주의 깊게, 그리고 공손하게 귀를 기울였다.\n\n\"하지만 외국 여행을 지지하는 입장에서, 저는 생활 습관의 변화와, 옛 기억을 불러일으키는 환경에서 벗어나는 것을 강조하고 싶습니다. 게다가 어머니께서 그것을 바라고 계시고요.\" 그가 덧붙였다.\n\n\"아! 음, 그렇다면 물론 보내십시오. 다만, 그 독일 돌팔이들이 해롭단 말입니다…… 잘 설득해야 할 텐데…… 뭐, 그럼 보내시지요.\"\n\n그는 다시 한번 시계를 흘끗 보았다.\n\n\"오! 벌써 시간이 다 됐군요.\" 그러고는 문 쪽으로 갔다. 저명한 의사는 공작 부인에게 환자를 한 번 더 봐야겠다고 알렸다(자기가 마땅히 해야 할 도리라는 느낌이 그렇게 하도록 시킨 것이었다).\n\n\"네? 또 진찰을 한다고요!\" 어머니가 질겁하며 소리쳤다.\n\n\"오, 아닙니다, 그저 몇 가지 세부 사항일 뿐입니다, 부인.\"\n\n\"이쪽으로 오세요.\"\n\n그리고 어머니는 의사를 데리고 키티가 있는 응접실로 들어갔다. 야위고 얼굴이 달아오른 키티는, 겪어야 했던 수치의 고통이 남겨 놓은 묘한 광채를 두 눈에 띤 채 방 한가운데 서 있었다. 의사가 들어오자 그녀는 얼굴이 새빨개졌고, 두 눈에 눈물이 그렁그렁 고였다. 자기의 병이며 치료가 모두 너무나 어리석은, 심지어 우스꽝스러운 일로만 여겨졌다! 자기를 치료한다는 것이, 깨진 꽃병 조각들을 도로 맞추는 것만큼이나 터무니없게 느껴졌다. 그녀의 마음은 깨져 버렸다. 그런데 어째서 알약이며 가루약으로 그녀를 고치려 드는 것일까? 하지만 어머니를 슬프게 할 수는 없었다. 더구나 어머니가 자기 탓이라고 여기고 있었으니 더욱 그랬다.\n\n\"앉으시라고 청해도 될까요, 아가씨?\" 저명한 의사가 그녀에게 말했다.\n\n그는 미소를 지으며 그녀를 마주 보고 앉아, 맥을 짚고는, 다시 따분한 질문들을 던지기 시작했다. 그녀는 대답하다가, 별안간 화가 치밀어 벌떡 일어섰다.\n\n\"죄송하지만 선생님, 이건 정말이지 아무 의미가 없어요. 똑같은 걸 물으시는 게 벌써 세 번째예요.\"\n\n저명한 의사는 기분 나빠하지 않았다.\n\n\"신경의 과민이로군요.\" 키티가 방에서 나가자 그가 공작 부인에게 말했다. \"어쨌든, 저는 끝냈습니다…….\"\n\n그러고 나서 의사는 공작 부인을 유난히 총명한 부인으로 대하며 젊은 아가씨의 상태를 학문적으로 설명하기 시작했고, 분명 해롭지 않은 광천수를 마실 것을 강조하는 것으로 끝맺었다. 외국으로 보내야 하느냐는 질문에, 의사는 무슨 중대한 문제라도 풀듯이 깊은 명상에 잠겼다. 마침내 그의 결정이 내려졌다. 외국으로 보내되, 외국 돌팔이들은 믿지 말고, 무슨 일이 생기면 자기에게 문의하라는 것이었다.\n\n의사가 떠난 뒤에는 마치 무슨 좋은 일이라도 일어난 것 같았다. 어머니는 딸에게 돌아가면서 훨씬 명랑해졌고, 키티도 더 명랑한 척했다. 이제 그녀는 종종, 거의 늘 무언가를 꾸며 내야 했다.\n\n\"정말이지 저는 아주 멀쩡해요, 엄마. 하지만 외국에 가고 싶으시면, 가요!\" 그녀는 이렇게 말하고는, 예정된 여행에 관심이 있는 척하려 애쓰며 여행 준비에 대해 이야기하기 시작했다.\n\n# Chapter 2\n\n의사가 다녀간 직후 돌리가 와 있었다. 그녀는 그날 진찰이 있으리란 걸 알고 있었기에, 비록 해산한 지 얼마 되지 않아 이제 막 자리에서 일어났음에도(겨울 끝 무렵에 또 아기를, 딸아이를 낳았다), 그리고 자기 나름의 근심과 걱정이 차고 넘쳤음에도, 갓난아기와 병든 아이를 두고서 그날 결정될 키티의 운명을 들으러 온 것이었다.\n\n\"그래, 어떻게 됐어?\" 그녀는 모자도 벗지 않은 채 응접실로 들어서며 말했다. \"다들 기분이 좋아 보이네. 그럼 좋은 소식이야?\"\n\n다들 의사가 한 말을 그녀에게 전하려 했지만, 의사가 충분히 또렷하게, 그것도 아주 길게 이야기했음에도 그가 한 말을 옮긴다는 것은 전혀 불가능한 일임이 드러났다. 관심을 끄는 단 하나의 사실은, 외국으로 떠나기로 정해졌다는 것뿐이었다.\n\n돌리는 한숨을 내쉬지 않을 수 없었다. 더없이 사랑하는 친구이자 동생이 멀리 떠나려 하고 있었다. 게다가 그녀 자신의 삶도 즐겁다고는 할 수 없었다. 화해한 뒤 스테판 아르카지치와의 관계는 굴욕적인 것이 되어 있었다. 안나가 단단히 이어 붙여 준 결합은 끝내 견고하지 못한 것으로 드러났고, 가정의 화목은 같은 지점에서 다시금 무너져 가고 있었다. 뚜렷하게 정해진 것은 없었지만, 스테판 아르카지치는 거의 집에 붙어 있지 않았고, 돈 또한 좀처럼 들어오지 않았으며, 돌리는 부정에 대한 의심으로 끊임없이 시달리면서도 이미 한 번 겪어 낸 질투의 고통이 두려워 그 의심을 떨쳐 버리려 애쓰고 있었다. 한 번 살아 낸 질투의 첫 습격은 두 번 다시 되돌아올 수 없는 것이어서, 이제는 부정을 알아챈다 해도 처음처럼 그녀를 뒤흔들 수는 없었다. 지금 그런 사실을 알게 된들 그것은 가정의 일상을 깨뜨리는 일에 지나지 않을 터였기에, 그녀는 자기가 속아 넘어가는 것을 그대로 내버려 두었고, 그를, 그리고 그 나약함 때문에 자기 자신을 더욱더 경멸했다. 이것 말고도 대식구를 건사하는 일이 그녀에게는 끊임없는 걱정거리였다. 처음엔 갓난아기에게 젖을 물리는 일이 순조롭지 않았고, 그다음엔 유모가 나가 버렸으며, 이제는 아이들 가운데 하나가 병이 들었다.\n\n\"그래, 너희들은 다 어떻게 지내니?\" 어머니가 물었다.\n\n\"아, 엄마, 우리도 우리대로 걱정이 한가득이에요. 릴리가 아픈데, 성홍열일까 봐 겁이 나요. 지금은 키티 일을 들으러 온 거지만, 만약—제발 그런 일은 없어야 할 텐데—성홍열이면 저는 아예 집에 틀어박혀 있어야 할 거예요.\"\n\n의사가 떠난 뒤 늙은 공작도 서재에서 나왔는데, 돌리에게 뺨을 내밀어 입맞춤을 받고 몇 마디 건넨 뒤 아내 쪽으로 돌아섰다.\n\n\"어떻게 정했소? 떠나는 거요? 그럼 나는 어쩌라는 거지?\"\n\n\"당신은 여기 남아 있는 게 좋을 거예요, 알렉산드르.\" 아내가 말했다.\n\n\"당신 좋을 대로 하구려.\"\n\n\"엄마, 아버지도 우리랑 같이 가면 안 돼요?\" 키티가 말했다. \"아버지한테도, 우리한테도 그게 더 좋을 텐데요.\"\n\n늙은 공작이 일어나 키티의 머리를 쓰다듬었다. 그녀는 고개를 들어 억지 미소를 지으며 그를 바라보았다. 그녀에게는 늘 아버지가 식구들 중 누구보다도 자기를 잘 이해하는 것처럼 느껴졌다. 비록 그가 그녀에 대해 말을 많이 하지는 않았지만 말이다. 막내인 그녀는 아버지가 가장 아끼는 딸이었고, 그 사랑이 아버지에게 통찰을 준다고 그녀는 믿었다. 지금 그녀의 시선이 자기를 뚫어지게 바라보는 아버지의 다정한 파란 눈과 마주쳤을 때, 그녀에게는 아버지가 자기 속을 훤히 들여다보며 마음속에서 일어나는 좋지 못한 것들을 죄다 알아챈 것만 같았다. 얼굴을 붉히며 그녀는 입맞춤을 기대하고 아버지 쪽으로 몸을 내밀었지만, 그는 그저 그녀의 머리를 토닥이며 말했다.\n\n\"이 멍청한 시뇽 같으니! 진짜 딸한테는 손이 닿지도 않는구나. 그저 죽은 여자들 빳빳한 털이나 쓰다듬는 셈이지. 자, 돌린카,\" 그는 큰딸 쪽으로 돌아섰다, \"네 그 젊은 한량은 요즘 뭘 하고 지내냐, 응?\"\n\n\"아무것도요, 아버지.\" 남편을 두고 하는 말임을 알아챈 돌리가 대답했다. \"늘 밖으로만 돌아서, 저는 얼굴 보기도 힘들어요.\" 그녀는 비꼬는 미소를 띠며 덧붙이지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n\"아니, 그 사람 아직도 시골에 안 갔어—그 숲을 파는 일을 보러 말이다?\"\n\n\"아뇨, 아직 떠날 채비만 하고 있어요.\"\n\n\"오, 그렇구나!\" 공작이 말했다. \"그럼 나도 떠날 채비를 해야 한다는 거로군? 분부대로 하지요.\" 그는 아내에게 이렇게 말하며 자리에 앉았다. \"그리고 내가 하나 말해 주마, 카탸,\" 그는 작은딸을 향해 말을 이었다. \"너는 어느 화창한 날 잠에서 깨어 혼잣말을 해야 한다. 어머나, 나는 아주 멀쩡하고 즐거우니, 다시 아버지랑 서리 내린 이른 아침에 산책을 나가야지. 어때, 응?\"\n\n아버지가 한 말은 지극히 단순한 것 같았지만, 그 말에 키티는 들킨 죄인처럼 당황하며 어쩔 줄을 몰랐다. '그래, 아버지는 다 알아, 다 이해하셔. 그리고 이 말로, 내가 부끄럽긴 해도 그 부끄러움을 이겨 내야 한다고 말씀하시는 거야.' 그녀는 도무지 무어라 대답할 기운을 낼 수 없었다. 입을 떼려다가 별안간 울음을 터뜨리며 방에서 뛰쳐나가고 말았다.\n\n\"당신의 그 농담이 무슨 꼴을 만드는지 좀 봐요!\" 공작 부인이 남편에게 와락 달려들었다. \"당신은 늘……\" 그녀는 줄줄이 비난을 늘어놓기 시작했다.\n\n공작은 한동안 말없이 공작 부인의 잔소리를 들었지만, 그의 얼굴은 점점 더 험하게 일그러졌다.\n\n\"저 아이는 너무 가여워요, 불쌍한 것, 너무 가엾다고요. 당신은 그 일의 까닭을 조금만 입에 올려도 저 아이가 얼마나 마음 아파하는지 느끼지도 못하죠. 아! 사람을 그렇게 잘못 볼 수가 있다니!\" 공작 부인이 말했고, 그 말투가 달라진 것으로 보아 그녀가 브론스키를 두고 하는 말임을 돌리도 공작도 알아챘다. \"어째서 그런 비열하고 파렴치한 인간들을 처벌하는 법이 없는지 모르겠어요.\"\n\n\"아, 더는 못 듣겠소!\" 공작이 침울하게 말하며 낮은 의자에서 일어났는데, 어서 자리를 뜨고 싶어 하는 듯하면서도 문가에 멈춰 섰다. \"법은 있소, 부인. 그리고 당신이 나한테 따져 물으니, 누구 탓에 이 모든 일이 벌어졌는지 말해 주겠소. 당신이오, 바로 당신, 당신 말고는 아무도 없소. 그런 젊은 난봉꾼들을 다스리는 법은 예전에도 늘 있었고, 지금도 있단 말이오! 그래, 만약 있어서는 안 될 일이 없었다면, 이 늙은 나이에도 나는 그 멋쟁이 애송이를 결투장으로 불러냈을 거요. 그래, 그런데 당신은 저 아이한테 약이나 먹이고 이런 돌팔이들을 불러들이고 있단 말이지.\"\n\n공작은 아직 할 말이 많은 듯했지만, 그 말투를 듣자마자 공작 부인은 곧장 누그러졌고, 심각한 상황이면 늘 그렇듯 뉘우치는 기색이 되었다.\n\n\"알렉산드르, 알렉산드르.\" 그녀는 그에게 다가가 울먹이기 시작하며 속삭였다.\n\n그녀가 울기 시작하자 공작도 마음이 가라앉았다. 그는 그녀에게 다가갔다.\n\n\"자, 됐소, 됐어! 당신도 비참하다는 거 알아. 어쩔 수 없는 일이지. 큰 탈이 난 것도 아니고. 하느님은 자비로우시니…… 고맙소……\" 그는 자기 손에 와 닿는 공작 부인의 눈물 어린 입맞춤에 답하며, 자기가 무슨 말을 하는지도 모른 채 중얼거렸다. 그러고는 방을 나갔다.\n\n이에 앞서, 키티가 울며 방을 나서자마자 돌리는 어머니다운, 한 가정을 꾸리는 사람 특유의 본능으로 여기에 여자가 해야 할 일이 놓여 있음을 곧바로 알아챘고, 그 일을 할 채비를 했다. 그녀는 모자를 벗고, 말하자면 소매를 걷어붙이며 행동에 나설 태세를 갖추었다. 어머니가 아버지를 몰아붙이는 동안에는 자식으로서 갖춰야 할 공경심이 허락하는 한도 안에서 어머니를 말리려 애썼다. 공작이 격분하는 동안에는 잠자코 있었다. 어머니가 부끄러웠고, 그토록 빨리 다시 다정해진 아버지에게는 애틋함을 느꼈다. 그러나 아버지가 자리를 뜨자, 그녀는 무엇보다 꼭 필요한 일, 곧 키티에게 가서 위로해 주는 일을 할 채비를 했다.\n\n\"오래전부터 엄마한테 할 말이 있었어요, 엄마. 레빈이 지난번에 여기 왔을 때 키티한테 청혼할 마음이었던 거 알고 계셨어요? 그이가 스티바한테 그렇게 말했대요.\"\n\n\"그래, 그래서? 무슨 소린지 모르겠구나……\"\n\n\"그래서 키티가 어쩌면 그이를 거절한 게 아닐까요?…… 엄마한테 그런 얘기 안 했어요?\"\n\n\"안 했어, 이 일도 저 일도 나한테는 아무 말이 없었어. 그 애는 자존심이 너무 세니까. 하지만 다 그 다른 사람 때문이라는 건 나도 알아.\"\n\n\"그래요, 그런데 만약 그 애가 레빈을 거절했고, 그 다른 사람만 아니었다면 거절하지 않았을 거라면요? 저는 그렇다는 걸 알아요. 그런데 그 사람은 그 애를 그렇게 끔찍하게 속였잖아요.\"\n\n자기가 딸에게 얼마나 잘못을 저질렀는지 떠올리는 것은 공작 부인으로서는 너무도 끔찍한 일이었기에, 그녀는 발끈 화를 냈다.\n\n\"오, 난 정말 모르겠다! 요즘 애들은 죄다 제멋대로 굴고, 어미는 무슨 일에도 한마디 거들 수가 없으니, 그러다……\"\n\n\"엄마, 제가 그 애한테 올라가 볼게요.\"\n\n\"그래, 그러렴. 내가 가지 말라고 했니?\" 어머니가 말했다.\n\n# Chapter 3\n\n키티의 작은 방, 예쁘고 분홍빛이 도는 그 작은 방으로 들어섰을 때—vieux saxe 자기 장식품들로 가득하고, 두 달 전의 키티 자신이 그러했듯이 싱그럽고 분홍빛에 하얗고 발랄한 그 방으로—돌리는 작년에 둘이서 얼마나 큰 애정과 즐거움으로 이 방을 함께 꾸몄던가를 떠올렸다. 문가의 낮은 의자에 앉아 양탄자 한 귀퉁이에 시선을 못 박은 채 꼼짝도 않고 있는 키티를 보았을 때, 돌리의 가슴은 서늘하게 식어 버렸다. 키티는 언니를 흘끗 보았지만, 차갑고 다소 심술궂은 그 얼굴 표정은 변하지 않았다.\n* vieux saxe: 프랑스어로 '옛 작센', 곧 독일 마이센에서 만든 고급 자기를 가리킨다.\n\n\"난 이제 곧 가야 하는데, 그러고 나면 집에만 있어야 해서 너도 날 보러 못 올 거야.\" 돌리가 그녀 곁에 앉으며 말했다. \"너랑 이야기를 좀 하고 싶구나.\"\n\n\"무슨 이야기요?\" 키티가 놀란 듯 고개를 들며 재빨리 물었다.\n\n\"네 일 말고 또 뭐가 있겠니?\"\n\n\"전 아무 일 없어요.\"\n\n\"무슨 소리니, 키티. 내가 모르고 있을 거라고 생각하니? 난 다 알아. 그리고 정말이지, 그건 별것도 아니란다…… 우리 모두 다 겪은 일이야.\"\n\n키티는 입을 열지 않았고, 그 얼굴에는 굳은 표정이 어려 있었다.\n\n\"그 사람은 네가 슬퍼할 만한 가치도 없는 사람이야.\" 다리야 알렉산드로브나가 곧장 본론으로 들어가며 말을 이었다.\n\n\"아니요, 그 사람이 절 멸시했으니까요.\" 키티가 갈라지는 목소리로 말했다. \"그 얘기는 하지 마세요! 제발, 그 얘기는 하지 마세요!\"\n\n\"하지만 대체 누가 너한테 그런 말을 할 수 있었겠니? 아무도 그런 말 안 했어. 난 확신해, 그 사람은 널 사랑했고, 만약…… 그런 일만 없었다면 지금도 널 사랑하고 있을 거야……\"\n\n\"아, 저한테는 그 무엇보다 끔찍한 게 바로 이런 동정이에요!\" 키티가 갑자기 격분하며 날카롭게 외쳤다. 그녀는 의자 위에서 홱 돌아앉았고, 새빨갛게 얼굴을 붉힌 채 손가락을 재빠르게 움직여 허리띠 죔쇠를 한 손으로 쥐었다가 또 다른 손으로 쥐었다 했다. 돌리는 동생이 몹시 흥분하면 두 손을 꽉 쥐는 이런 버릇이 있다는 것을 알고 있었다. 또한 흥분한 순간이면 키티가 자제력을 잃고 지나치게 많은 말을 쏟아 내곤 한다는 것도 알고 있었기에, 돌리는 그녀를 달래 주고 싶었지만, 이미 너무 늦어 버렸다.\n\n\"대체, 언니는 저한테 무얼 느끼게 하고 싶은 거예요, 네?\" 키티가 빠르게 말했다. \"제가 저 따위는 눈곱만큼도 신경 쓰지 않는 남자를 사랑했고, 그 사람을 사랑한 나머지 죽어 가고 있다는 거? 게다가 이런 말을 다름 아닌 제 친언니가 하다니, 그래 놓고 자기가…… 자기가…… 자기가 절 동정한다고 생각하고 있다니!…… 전 그런 위로니 위선이니 하는 건 바라지 않아요!\"\n\n\"키티, 너 너무하는구나.\"\n\n\"왜 절 괴롭히는 거예요?\"\n\n\"하지만 난…… 정반대인데…… 네가 불행해하는 게 보여서……\"\n\n그러나 격분한 키티에게는 언니의 말이 들리지 않았다.\n\n\"전 슬퍼할 것도, 위로받을 것도 없어요. 전 절 사랑하지도 않는 남자한테 마음을 두는 걸 스스로에게 허락할 만큼 그렇게 자존심 없는 사람이 아니에요.\"\n\n\"그래, 나도 그렇다고는 안 해…… 다만 한 가지만. 솔직하게 말해 주렴.\" 다리야 알렉산드로브나가 그녀의 손을 잡으며 말했다. \"말해 봐, 레빈이 너한테 말했니?……\"\n\n레빈의 이름이 입에 오르자 키티에게 남아 있던 마지막 자제력마저 사라져 버린 듯했다. 그녀는 의자에서 벌떡 일어나 죔쇠를 바닥에 내동댕이치더니, 두 손을 빠르게 휘저으며 말했다.\n\n\"왜 레빈까지 끌어들이는 거예요? 언니가 절 왜 이렇게 괴롭히려 드는지 도무지 모르겠어요. 제가 말했고, 다시 말하지만, 전 자존심이라는 게 있어요. 그래서 절대로, 절대로 언니처럼은 하지 않을 거예요—언니를 속인 남자, 다른 여자한테 마음을 둔 남자한테 되돌아가는 짓 따위는요. 전 그걸 이해할 수가 없어요! 언니는 할 수 있을지 몰라도, 전 못 해요!\"\n\n그리고 이 말을 내뱉으며 그녀는 언니를 흘끗 보았는데, 돌리가 슬픈 듯 고개를 떨군 채 말없이 앉아 있는 것을 보고는, 원래 하려던 대로 방에서 뛰쳐나가는 대신 문가에 앉아 손수건에 얼굴을 파묻었다.\n\n침묵이 이 분 동안 이어졌다. 돌리는 자기 자신에 대해 생각하고 있었다. 늘 의식하고 있던 그 굴욕이, 동생이 그것을 일깨우자 유난히 쓰라리게 되살아났다. 그녀는 동생에게서 이런 잔인함을 기대하지 않았기에, 동생에게 화가 났다. 그런데 갑자기 치맛자락이 스치는 소리가 들렸고, 그와 함께 가슴이 찢어지는 듯한, 억눌린 흐느낌 소리가 들리더니, 두 팔이 자기 목을 감싸 안는 것이 느껴졌다. 키티가 그녀 앞에 무릎을 꿇고 있었다.\n\n\"돌린카, 난, 난 너무, 너무 비참해요!\" 그녀가 뉘우치듯 속삭였다. 그리고 눈물로 뒤덮인 그 사랑스러운 얼굴이 다리야 알렉산드로브나의 치맛자락 속으로 숨어들었다.\n\n마치 눈물이 없어서는 안 될 윤활유여서, 그것 없이는 두 자매 사이에 서로를 믿는 마음의 톱니바퀴가 매끄럽게 돌아가지 못하기라도 하듯, 자매는 눈물을 흘리고 난 뒤 마음속 가장 깊은 곳에 있는 것에 대해서는 말하지 않았지만, 겉도는 이야기를 하면서도 서로를 이해했다. 키티는 남편의 부정과 굴욕적인 처지에 대해 홧김에 내뱉은 말이 가엾은 언니의 가슴을 도려냈다는 것을, 그러나 언니가 자기를 용서했다는 것을 알았다. 돌리는 돌리대로 알고 싶었던 모든 것을 알아냈다. 그녀는 자신의 짐작이 옳다고 확신했다. 키티의 불행이, 그 위로할 길 없는 불행이, 다름 아니라 레빈이 그녀에게 청혼했으나 그녀가 그를 거절했고, 브론스키가 그녀를 속였다는 사실에서 비롯되었으며, 그녀가 이제는 레빈을 사랑하고 브론스키를 혐오할 마음의 준비가 충분히 되어 있다는 것을 확신했다. 키티는 그것에 대해서는 한마디도 하지 않았다. 그녀는 오로지 자신의 마음 상태에 대해서만 이야기했다.\n\n\"전 비참해할 만한 게 아무것도 없어요.\" 그녀가 한결 차분해지며 말했다. \"그렇지만 언니, 이해할 수 있어요? 모든 게 제게는 밉고, 역겹고, 천하게 느껴진다는 걸, 그리고 무엇보다 저 자신이 가장 그렇다는 걸요? 제가 모든 것에 대해 얼마나 역겨운 생각들을 하는지 언니는 상상도 못 할 거예요.\"\n\n\"아니, 대체 어떤 역겨운 생각을 한다는 거니?\" 돌리가 미소를 지으며 물었다.\n\n\"더없이 역겹고 천한 생각들이에요. 차마 말도 못 하겠어요. 그건 불행도, 의기소침도 아니고, 그보다 훨씬 더 나쁜 거예요. 마치 제 안에 있던 좋은 것은 전부 어딘가로 숨어 버리고, 가장 역겨운 것만 남은 것 같아요. 자, 이걸 어떻게 말해야 할까요?\" 그녀는 언니의 눈에 어린 어리둥절한 빛을 보며 말을 이었다. \"아까 아버지가 저한테 뭔가 말씀하기 시작하셨는데…… 제가 보기엔 아버지는 제가 바라는 게 오로지 시집가는 것뿐이라고 생각하시는 것 같아요. 어머니는 절 무도회에 데려가시는데, 제 눈엔 어머니가 절 한시바삐 시집보내서 떼어 버리려고 데려가시는 것처럼만 보여요. 그게 사실이 아닌 걸 알면서도, 그런 생각을 떨쳐 낼 수가 없어요. 사람들이 신랑감이라 부르는 그런 남자들—전 그 사람들을 보는 것조차 견딜 수가 없어요. 제 눈엔 그 사람들이 절 품평하고 값을 매기는 것처럼만 보이거든요. 예전엔 무도회 드레스를 입고 어딜 가는 게 저한테는 그저 단순한 기쁨이었어요, 제 모습에 스스로 흐뭇해했죠. 그런데 지금은 부끄럽고 어색하기만 해요. 게다가! 그 의사…… 그리고……\" 키티는 머뭇거렸다. 그녀는 자기 안에 이런 변화가 일어난 뒤로 스테판 아르카지치가 견딜 수 없을 만큼 혐오스러워졌으며, 그를 보기만 하면 더없이 추잡하고 끔찍한 상상들이 머릿속에 떠올라 그를 마주 볼 수가 없다는 말까지 하고 싶었다.\n\n\"아, 뭐, 모든 게 제겐 가장 천하고 가장 역겨운 빛깔로 비쳐요.\" 그녀가 말을 이었다. \"그게 제 병이에요. 어쩌면 지나갈지도 모르죠.\"\n\n\"하지만 그런 건 생각하지 말아야 해.\"\n\n\"어쩔 수가 없는걸요. 전 언니 집에서 아이들이랑 있을 때 말고는 도무지 행복하질 않아요.\"\n\n\"네가 나랑 같이 못 있다니 정말 아쉽구나!\"\n\n\"아, 아니에요, 갈 거예요. 전 성홍열을 앓은 적이 있으니까, 엄마를 설득해서 허락받을게요.\"\n\n키티는 자기 뜻을 굽히지 않고 끝내 언니 집에 가서 머물렀고, 성홍열을 앓는 내내 아이들을 간호했다. 그것은 정말로 성홍열로 밝혀졌던 것이다. 두 자매는 여섯 아이 모두를 무사히 그 병에서 회복시켰지만, 키티의 건강은 조금도 나아지지 않았고, 사순절에 셰르바츠키 일가는 외국으로 떠났다.\n\n# Chapter 4\n\n페테르부르크 최상류 사교계는 본질적으로 하나다. 그 안에서는 모두가 서로를 알고, 심지어 서로 왕래까지 한다. 그러나 이 거대한 집단에는 그 나름의 세분된 갈래가 있었다. 안나 아르카지예브나 카레니나는 이 최상류 사교계의 서로 다른 세 무리에 친구와 가까운 인연을 두고 있었다. 한 무리는 남편의 관직 동료들로 이루어진 집단으로, 남편의 동료들과 부하들이 더없이 다양하고 변덕스러운 방식으로 한데 모여 있었으며, 각기 다른 사회 계층에 속해 있었다. 안나는 이제 처음에 이 사람들에게 품었던, 거의 경외에 가까운 존경의 감정을 떠올리기가 어려웠다. 이제 그녀는 시골 소도시 사람들이 서로를 알듯이 그들 모두를 속속들이 알았다. 그들의 습관과 약점을, 그리고 저마다 어디가 가려운지를 다 알았다. 그녀는 그들 서로 간의 관계며 최고위층과의 관계를 알았고, 누가 누구 편인지, 저마다 어떻게 자기 자리를 지켜 내는지, 어디서 의견이 맞고 어디서 갈리는지를 알았다. 그러나 정치적이고 남성적인 관심사의 무리는 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인의 영향에도 불구하고 한 번도 그녀의 흥미를 끈 적이 없었기에, 그녀는 그것을 멀리했다.\n\n안나가 가깝게 지낸 또 다른 작은 무리는, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 출세를 이룬 발판이 된 바로 그 집단이었다. 이 무리의 중심에는 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인이 있었다. 그것은 나이 들고 못생기고 자비롭고 신앙심 깊은 여인들과, 영리하고 학식 있고 야심 찬 남자들로 이루어진 집단이었다. 이 무리에 속한 영리한 사람 가운데 하나가 그것을 \"페테르부르크 사교계의 양심\"이라고 불렀다. 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 이 무리를 더없이 높이 평가했고, 누구와도 잘 어울리는 특별한 재주를 지닌 안나는 페테르부르크 생활 초기에 이 무리에서도 친구를 사귀었다. 그러나 이제, 모스크바에서 돌아온 뒤로 그녀는 이 집단을 견딜 수 없게 느꼈다. 그녀 자신도, 그들 모두도 진실하지 못하다는 생각이 들었고, 그 세계에서 너무도 따분하고 거북했던 탓에 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인을 찾아가는 일을 되도록 줄였다.\n\n안나가 인연을 맺은 세 번째 무리는 무엇보다도 유행을 좇는 사교계, 즉 무도회와 만찬과 호화로운 의상의 세계였으니, 데미몽드 수준으로 전락하지 않으려고 한 손으로 궁정에 매달려 있는 세계였다. 그 유행하는 세계의 사람들은 자기들이 데미몽드를 경멸한다고 믿었지만, 사실 그들의 취향은 단지 비슷한 정도가 아니라 실은 똑같았다. 이 무리와의 연결은 사촌의 아내인 벳시 트베르스카야 공작 부인을 통해 유지되었는데, 그녀는 연 십이만 루블의 수입이 있었고, 안나가 처음 사교계에 나온 이래로 그녀를 무척 마음에 들어 해 많은 관심을 기울였으며, 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인의 패거리를 비웃으면서 안나를 자기 무리로 끌어들였다.\n* 데미몽드(demi-monde): 프랑스어로 '반쪽 사교계'. 부유한 남성의 정부 노릇을 하는 여인들의 세계를 가리킨다.\n\n\"내가 늙고 못생겨지면 나도 그렇게 될 거야.\" 벳시는 입버릇처럼 말했다. \"하지만 너처럼 예쁘고 젊은 여자한테는 그 자선의 집에 들어가긴 아직 일러.\"\n\n안나는 처음에는 트베르스카야 공작 부인의 세계를 되도록 멀리했는데, 그 세계가 자기 형편에 넘치는 지출을 요구했기 때문이고, 게다가 마음속으로는 첫 번째 무리를 더 좋아했기 때문이다. 그러나 모스크바를 다녀온 뒤로 그녀는 정반대로 했다. 그녀는 진지한 친구들을 피하고 유행하는 사교계로 나갔다. 거기서 그녀는 브론스키를 만났고, 그 만남에서 가슴 설레는 기쁨을 느꼈다. 그녀는 특히 벳시의 집에서 브론스키를 자주 만났는데, 벳시는 본래 브론스키 집안 사람으로 그의 사촌이었기 때문이다. 브론스키는 안나를 만날 가망이 조금이라도 있는 곳이라면 어디든 나타났고, 기회가 닿을 때면 자기 사랑을 그녀에게 이야기했다. 그녀는 그에게 아무런 부추김도 주지 않았지만, 그를 만날 때마다 그녀의 가슴속에는 처음 그를 보았던 그날 열차 안에서 솟아올랐던 바로 그 활기 돋는 생명의 감정이 다시금 차올랐다. 그녀는 자기 기쁨이 눈 속에서 반짝이고 입술을 미소로 휘어 놓는 것을 스스로 의식했지만, 그 기쁨의 표정을 억누를 수가 없었다.\n\n처음에 안나는 그가 감히 자기를 쫓아다니는 것 때문에 그에게 불쾌함을 느낀다고 진심으로 믿었다. 모스크바에서 돌아온 지 얼마 안 되어, 그를 만나리라 기대했던 어느 soirée에 도착했는데 그가 그 자리에 없자, 밀려드는 실망감에서 그녀는 자기가 스스로를 속여 왔다는 것을, 그리고 이 추적이 단지 싫지 않을 뿐 아니라 자기 삶의 모든 흥미를 이룬다는 것을 또렷이 깨달았다.\n\n* soirée: 프랑스어로 '야회(夜會)', 저녁 모임.\n이름난 가수가 두 번째 무대를 노래하고 있었고, 유행하는 사교계 전체가 극장에 모여 있었다. 브론스키는 앞줄 일등석에서 사촌을 보고는 막간까지 기다리지 않고 그녀의 칸막이 좌석으로 갔다.\n\n\"왜 만찬에 안 왔어요?\" 그녀가 그에게 말했다. \"연인들의 예감이란 참 놀라워.\" 그녀는 그 말고는 아무도 못 듣게 미소를 지으며 덧붙였다. \"그 여자는 거기 없었거든. 그래도 오페라 끝나고 와요.\"\n\n브론스키는 묻는 듯한 눈길로 그녀를 바라보았다. 그녀가 고개를 끄덕였다. 그는 미소로 고마움을 표하고는 그녀 곁에 앉았다.\n\n\"근데 내가 당신 비웃던 거 어찌나 잘 기억나는지!\" 벳시 공작 부인이 말을 이었다. 그녀는 이 연정을 끝까지 따라가 성공으로 이끄는 데서 남다른 즐거움을 느끼고 있었다. \"그게 다 어떻게 된 거지? 잡혔잖아, 내 사랑스러운 친구.\"\n\n\"바로 그게 내 유일한 소원이죠, 잡히는 것 말입니다.\" 브론스키가 그 평온하고 쾌활한 미소를 지으며 대답했다. \"내가 무얼 한탄한다면, 솔직히 말해 충분히 잡히지 못한 것뿐이에요. 슬슬 희망을 잃기 시작했거든요.\"\n\n\"아니, 도대체 무슨 희망을 품을 수 있다는 거예요?\" 벳시가 친구를 대신해 발끈하며 말했다. \"Entendons nous.......\" 그러나 그녀의 눈 속에는, 그가 무슨 희망을 품을 수 있을지 자기도 그와 똑같이 완벽하게 그리고 정확하게 알고 있음을 드러내는 빛이 어른거렸다.\n\n* Entendons nous: 프랑스어로 '서로 분명히 해 둡시다', '말이 통하게 합시다'.\n\"전혀 없어요.\" 브론스키가 웃으며 가지런한 잇줄을 드러내고 말했다. \"실례.\" 그는 그녀의 손에서 오페라글라스를 받아 들고는, 그녀의 드러난 어깨 너머로 맞은편의 칸막이 좌석 줄을 살피기 시작하며 덧붙였다. \"내가 우스워지고 있는 것 같아서요.\"\n\n그는 벳시나 다른 유행하는 사람들의 눈에 자기가 우스워질 위험이 전혀 없음을 잘 알고 있었다. 그들의 눈에 처녀나, 혹은 결혼이 자유로운 어떤 여인의 구애에 실패한 연인의 처지는 우스워 보일 수 있다는 것을 그는 잘 알았다. 그러나 유부녀를 쫓으며, 모든 것을 아랑곳하지 않고 그녀를 간통으로 끌어들이는 데 목숨을 거는 남자의 처지에는 무언가 멋지고 웅장한 데가 있어 결코 우스워질 수 없었다. 그리하여 그는 콧수염 아래 자랑스럽고 유쾌한 미소를 띤 채 오페라글라스를 내리고 사촌을 바라보았다.\n\n\"근데 왜 만찬에 안 왔어요?\" 그녀가 그를 흐뭇하게 바라보며 말했다.\n\n\"그 얘기를 해 드려야겠군요. 나는 바쁘게 일하고 있었는데, 무얼 했는지 짐작이 갑니까? 백 번을 맞혀 보세요, 천 번이라도......도저히 못 맞힐 겁니다. 자기 아내를 모욕한 남자와 그 남편을 화해시키고 있었어요. 정말이에요!\"\n\n\"그래서, 성공했어요?\"\n\n\"거의.\"\n\n\"그 얘긴 꼭 들려줘야 해요.\" 그녀가 일어서며 말했다. \"다음 막간에 나한테 와요.\"\n\n\"안 돼요, 프랑스 극장에 가야 하거든요.\"\n\n\"닐손을 두고요?\" 벳시가 질겁하며 물었다. 정작 그녀 자신은 닐손의 목소리를 어떤 합창단 여자의 목소리와도 구별하지 못했을 텐데도.\n\n\"어쩔 수 없어요. 거기 약속이 있어서요, 죄다 내 평화 사명과 관련된 일이죠.\"\n\n\"'화평하게 하는 자는 복이 있나니, 천국이 저희 것임이요.'\" 벳시가 누군가에게서 비슷한 말을 들었던 것을 어렴풋이 떠올리며 말했다. \"좋아요, 그럼 앉아서 도대체 무슨 일인지 얘기나 해 봐요.\"\n\n그러고는 다시 자리에 앉았다.\n\n# Chapter 5\n\n\"좀 경솔한 얘기지만, 어찌나 재미있는지 들려주고 싶어서 못 견디겠군요.\" 브론스키는 웃음 띤 눈으로 그녀를 바라보며 말했다. \"이름은 일절 대지 않겠습니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 내가 알아맞힐 텐데요, 그럼 더 좋고요.\"\n\n\"자, 들어 보세요. 흥에 겨운 두 젊은이가 마차를 몰고 가고 있었지요.\"\n\n\"물론 당신 연대의 장교들이겠지요?\"\n\n\"장교라고는 안 했습니다. 그저 점심을 먹고 난 두 젊은이였지요.\"\n\n\"바꿔 말하면 술을 마셨다는 거군요.\"\n\n\"아마도 그렇겠지요. 그들은 친구와 저녁 식사를 하러 가는 길이었는데, 더없이 들뜬 기분으로 마차를 몰고 있었습니다. 그러다 삯썰매를 탄 어여쁜 여인을 보게 됐지요. 그 여인이 그들을 앞지르더니, 그들 쪽을 돌아보고는, 적어도 그들이 느끼기로는, 고개를 끄덕이며 웃었답니다. 그들은 물론 그 여인을 뒤쫓았지요. 전속력으로 내달렸습니다. 그런데 놀랍게도 그 미인은 다름 아닌 자기들이 가려던 바로 그 집 현관 앞에서 썰매에서 내리는 게 아니겠습니까. 미인은 맨 위층으로 쏜살같이 올라갔지요. 그들은 짧은 베일 아래로 드러난 붉은 입술과 더없이 곱고 자그마한 발을 언뜻 보았답니다.\"\n\n\"어찌나 실감 나게 묘사하는지, 당신이 그 두 사람 중 하나가 아닌가 싶군요.\"\n\n\"방금 그렇게 말씀하시고서야! 자, 두 젊은이는 친구네 집으로 들어갔지요. 친구는 송별 만찬을 열고 있었습니다. 거기서 그들은 분명 술을 좀 과하게 마셨지요, 송별 만찬에서는 늘 그러기 마련이니까요. 그러다 식사 도중에 그 집 맨 위층에 누가 사는지를 물었답니다. 아무도 모르더군요. 다만 그 집 주인의 시종이, 맨 위층에 '아가씨들'이 사느냐는 그들의 물음에, 그 근방에 그런 여자들이 아주 많다고 대답했지요. 식사를 마치고 두 젊은이는 주인의 서재로 들어가, 미지의 미인에게 편지를 썼답니다. 열렬한 사연을, 그러니까 사실상 사랑의 고백을 써 내려가고는, 편지에서 어쩌면 영 알아듣기 어려울지도 모를 대목을 직접 설명하려고 그 편지를 손수 위층으로 들고 올라갔지요.\"\n\n\"왜 이런 끔찍한 얘기를 들려주시는 거예요? 그래서요?\"\n\n\"그들이 초인종을 울립니다. 하녀가 문을 열자, 그들은 하녀에게 편지를 건네고는, 둘 다 어찌나 사랑에 빠졌는지 이 문 앞에서 당장 죽어 버릴 지경이라고 우겨 댔지요. 어리둥절해진 하녀가 그들의 말을 안에 전합니다. 그러자 별안간 소시지처럼 생긴 구레나룻을 기르고 바닷가재처럼 시뻘건 신사가 나타나, 이 집에는 자기 아내 말고는 아무도 살지 않는다고 선언하더니 두 사람을 다 쫓아냈답니다.\"\n\n\"그 사람한테 말씀처럼 소시지 같은 구레나룻이 있었다는 걸 어떻게 아세요?\"\n\n\"아, 들어 보세요. 내가 방금 그들 사이를 화해시키고 오는 길이거든요.\"\n\n\"그래서, 그다음엔 어떻게 됐나요?\"\n\n\"바로 그게 이 이야기에서 가장 흥미로운 대목이지요. 알고 보니 그들은 금실 좋은 부부, 관청 서기와 그 부인이었답니다. 관청 서기가 항의를 제기했고, 내가 중재자로 나섰는데, 그것도 얼마나 대단한 중재자였는지요!…… 정말이지 탈레랑도 나한테는 댈 게 아닐 겁니다.\"\n\n\"아니, 도대체 뭐가 그리 어려웠는데요?\"\n\n\"아, 들어 보세요…… 우리는 격식을 갖춰 사과를 했지요. 우리는 절망에 빠져 있노라고, 이 불행한 오해를 용서해 달라고 간청했습니다. 소시지 구레나룻을 단 그 관청 서기가 누그러지기 시작하더군요. 그런데 자기도 속내를 털어놓고 싶어 하더니, 막상 털어놓기 시작하자마자 점점 흥분해서는 험한 말을 쏟아 내는 겁니다. 그래서 나는 또다시 외교적 수완을 죄다 끌어내야 했지요. 나는 그들의 행동이 잘못되었음을 인정하면서도, 그들의 경솔함과 젊음을 헤아려 달라고 호소했습니다. 게다가 그 젊은이들은 마침 함께 점심을 먹고 온 참이었다고요. '아시잖습니까. 그들은 깊이 뉘우치고 있으며, 자기들의 무례를 너그러이 보아 달라고 빌고 있습니다.' 관청 서기가 다시 한번 마음을 풀더군요. '백작님, 좋습니다, 나도 눈감아 줄 용의가 있습니다. 하지만 보세요, 내 아내가, 내 아내는 점잖은 여자입니다만, 그 아내가 어린 풋내기들, 그 망나니들의 추근거림과 모욕과 무례를 당했단 말입니다……' 그런데 아시겠지만, 그 풋내기들은 줄곧 그 자리에 있었고, 나는 그들 사이를 화해시켜야 했지요. 나는 또다시 외교술을 죄다 동원했고, 또다시 일이 거의 마무리될 즈음이면 우리의 관청 서기 양반은 흥분해서 시뻘게지고, 그 소시지가 분노로 곤두서고, 그러면 나는 다시금 외교적 술책을 펼쳐야 했답니다.\"\n\n\"아, 이 사람이 당신한테 이 얘기를 꼭 들려줘야 해요!\" 베치는 자기 칸막이 좌석으로 들어온 부인에게 웃으며 말했다. \"어찌나 날 웃겨 댔는지 몰라요.\"\n\n\"자, 그럼 bonne chance!\" 그녀는 부채를 든 손의 손가락 하나를 브론스키에게 내밀며 덧붙였다. 그러고는 어깨를 한번 으쓱하며, 위로 말려 올라간 드레스 상의를 아래로 잡아당겼다. 각광 앞으로, 가스등 불빛 속으로, 뭇 시선 앞으로 나아가면서 어울리게끔 어깨를 드러내기 위해서였다.\n* bonne chance: 프랑스어로 '행운을 빌어요'.\n\n브론스키는 프랑스 극장으로 마차를 몰았다. 거기서 정말이지 자기 연대의 대령을 만나야 했는데, 그는 그 극장의 공연이라면 단 한 회도 놓치는 법이 없었다. 브론스키는 그를 만나, 지난 사흘 동안 자기를 사로잡고 즐겁게 해 준 그 중재의 결과를 보고하려 했다. 이 사건에는 그가 아끼는 페트리츠키가 연루되어 있었고, 또 다른 장본인은 최근 연대에 들어온, 더없이 훌륭하고 일류 동료인 젊은 케드로프 공작이었다. 그리고 무엇보다 중요한 것은, 거기에 연대의 이해관계까지 얽혀 있다는 점이었다.\n\n두 젊은이 모두 브론스키의 중대 소속이었다. 연대의 대령에게 관청 서기 벤덴이 찾아와, 자기 아내를 모욕한 장교들을 고발했던 것이다. 벤덴의 말로는, 결혼한 지 반년밖에 안 된 그의 젊은 아내가 어머니와 함께 교회에 있었는데, 임신한 몸이라 별안간 몸이 불편해져서 더 이상 서 있을 수가 없게 되자, 마침 눈에 띈 산뜻해 보이는 첫 번째 썰매를 잡아타고 집으로 돌아갔다고 한다. 바로 그 자리에서 장교들이 그녀를 뒤쫓기 시작했고, 그녀는 겁에 질려 한층 더 속이 안 좋아진 채로 계단을 뛰어 올라가 집으로 들어갔다. 벤덴 본인은 관청에서 돌아오다가 자기 집 초인종 소리와 사람 목소리를 듣고 나가 보았는데, 술 취한 장교들이 편지를 들고 있는 것을 보고는 그들을 쫓아냈다. 그는 엄중한 처벌을 요구했다.\n\n\"그래, 다 좋은데 말이지,\" 대령은 자기를 보러 오라고 청한 브론스키에게 말했다. \"페트리츠키가 점점 손쓸 수 없게 되어 가는군. 무슨 추문 하나 없이 지나가는 주가 없으니. 이 관청 서기는 이 일을 그냥 넘기지 않고 계속 물고 늘어질 거야.\"\n\n브론스키는 이 일이 얼마나 보람 없는 것인지, 또 거기에 결투 따위는 있을 수 없는 일이며, 모든 수를 써서 관청 서기를 누그러뜨리고 일을 덮어야 한다는 것을 알았다. 대령이 브론스키를 부른 것은 다름 아니라, 그를 명예롭고 영리한 사람으로, 무엇보다 연대의 명예를 소중히 여기는 사람으로 알고 있었기 때문이다. 두 사람은 이 일을 의논한 끝에, 페트리츠키와 케드로프가 브론스키와 함께 벤덴의 집으로 가서 사과해야 한다는 결론을 내렸다. 대령과 브론스키는 둘 다, 브론스키의 이름과 지위가 모욕당한 남편의 감정을 누그러뜨리는 데 분명 큰 보탬이 되리라는 것을 충분히 알고 있었다.\n\n그리고 실제로 이 두 가지 힘은 효과가 없지 않았다. 다만 브론스키가 묘사한 대로, 그 결과는 여전히 불확실한 채로 남아 있었다.\n\n프랑스 극장에 도착한 브론스키는 대령과 함께 휴게실로 물러나, 자기의 성공인지 실패인지 모를 결과를 보고했다. 대령은 모든 것을 곰곰이 따져 본 끝에, 이 일을 더 이상 끌고 가지 않기로 마음먹었다. 그러면서도 자기 자신의 흥을 위해 브론스키에게 그 면담에 대해 꼬치꼬치 캐묻기 시작했다. 그리고 관청 서기가 한동안 가라앉아 있다가도 그 일의 세세한 정황을 떠올리고는 별안간 다시 발끈했다는 것을, 또 브론스키가 화해의 마지막 반 마디 말이 끝나자마자 페트리츠키를 앞으로 떠밀며 능숙하게 후퇴하는 책략을 부렸다는 것을 듣고는, 한참 동안이나 웃음을 참지 못했다.\n\n\"창피한 얘기지만, 정말 배꼽 빠지겠군. 케드로프가 설마 그 양반과 결투를 할 수야 없지! 그렇게나 펄펄 끓더란 말인가?\" 그는 웃으며 한마디 덧붙였다. \"그건 그렇고, 오늘 클레르는 어떻던가? 정말 굉장하지,\" 그는 새로 온 프랑스 여배우 이야기로 말을 이었다. \"아무리 자주 봐도, 날마다 다르단 말이야. 그건 프랑스 사람들이나 할 수 있는 일이지.\"\n\n# Chapter 6\n\n베치 공작부인은 마지막 막이 끝나기를 기다리지 않고 극장에서 마차를 타고 집으로 돌아왔다. 그녀는 화장실로 들어가 길고 창백한 얼굴에 분을 뿌려 문지르고 옷매무새를 바로잡은 다음 큰 응접실에 차를 내오라고 일러둘 시간밖에 없었는데, 그러는 사이 마차들이 줄지어 볼샤야 모르스카야 거리에 있는 그녀의 거대한 저택으로 들어섰다. 손님들은 널찍한 현관에서 내렸고, 아침이면 유리문 안쪽에서 신문을 읽으며 지나가는 사람들을 흐뭇하게 해 주던 뚱뚱한 문지기가 소리 없이 그 거대한 문을 열어 방문객들이 자기 옆을 지나 집 안으로 들어가게 했다.\n\n거의 같은 순간에, 새로 머리를 매만지고 얼굴을 산뜻하게 가다듬은 안주인은 응접실의 한쪽 문으로 들어왔고, 손님들은 다른 쪽 문으로 들어왔다. 응접실은 어두운 벽과 보드라운 양탄자가 깔린 큰 방이었고, 촛불과 흰 식탁보, 은빛 사모바르와 투명한 도자기 찻그릇이 어우러진 빛으로 환하게 반짝이는 식탁이 놓여 있었다.\n\n안주인은 식탁에 앉아 장갑을 벗었다. 방 안을 거의 눈에 띄지 않게 오가는 하인들의 도움으로 의자들이 놓였고, 일행은 자리를 잡으며 두 무리로 갈라졌다. 하나는 안주인 곁의 사모바르를 둘러싼 무리였고, 다른 하나는 응접실 반대편 끝에서 검은 벨벳을 입고 또렷한 검은 눈썹을 가진 어느 대사의 아름다운 아내를 둘러싼 무리였다. 두 무리 모두에서 대화는 으레 그렇듯 처음 몇 분간은 만남과 인사, 차 권하기로 끊기며 흔들렸고, 마치 무언가 기댈 거리를 더듬어 찾는 듯했다.\n\n\"그 여자는 배우로서 유난히 뛰어나요. 카울바흐를 연구한 게 보여요.\" 대사 부인을 둘러싼 무리에서 외교관 한 사람이 말했다. \"그 여자가 쓰러지는 장면 보셨어요?……\"\n\n\"아, 제발, 닐손 이야기는 그만둬요! 누구도 그 여자에 대해 새로운 말을 할 수는 없어요.\" 눈썹도 시뇽도 없이 낡은 비단 드레스를 입은, 뚱뚱하고 얼굴이 붉으며 머리가 아마빛인 부인이 말했다. 이 사람은 소탈함과 거친 태도로 유명해 enfant terrible이라는 별명으로 불리는 먀흐카야 공작부인이었다. 먀흐카야 공작부인은 두 무리 사이 한가운데에 앉아 양쪽 말을 다 들으며, 먼저 이쪽 대화에 끼었다가 다시 저쪽 대화에 끼곤 했다. \"오늘 벌써 세 사람이나 카울바흐에 대해 나한테 그 말을 똑같이 했어요. 마치 무슨 약속이라도 한 것처럼요. 그런데 왜들 그 말을 그렇게 좋아하는지 통 모르겠어요.\"\n\n* enfant terrible: 프랑스어로 '무서운 아이', 못 할 말을 거리낌 없이 하는 사람을 뜻한다.\n\n이 한마디로 대화가 끊겨, 다시 새 화제를 생각해 내야 했다.\n\"뭔가 재미있되 심술궂지 않은 이야기를 해 줘요.\" 영국인들이 small talk이라 부르는 그 우아한 대화술의 명수인 대사 부인이 말했다. 그녀는 이제 무슨 이야기로 시작해야 할지 막막해하는 외교관에게 말을 건넸다.\n\n\"그게 어려운 일이라고들 하지요. 심술궂지 않으면 재미있는 게 없다고요.\" 그가 미소를 지으며 말문을 열었다. \"하지만 한번 해 보지요. 화제를 주세요. 모든 건 화제에 달려 있으니까요. 화제만 주어지면 그걸 두고 뭔가를 엮어 내기는 쉽지요. 저는 지난 세기의 그 유명한 입담꾼들이 지금이라면 재치 있게 말하기가 어려웠으리라고 종종 생각해요. 재치 있는 건 죄다 너무 진부해져서요…….\"\n\n\"그것도 이미 오래전에 나온 말이에요.\" 대사 부인이 웃으며 그의 말을 가로막았다.\n\n대화는 다정하게 시작되었지만, 바로 그 다정함이 지나친 탓에 다시 멈추고 말았다. 그들은 결코 실패하는 법이 없는 확실한 화제, 즉 험담에 의지할 수밖에 없었다.\n\n\"투시케비치한테 뭔가 루이 캥즈풍이 있다고 생각지 않으세요?\" 그가 식탁 옆에 서 있는 잘생긴 금발의 젊은이를 흘긋 보며 말했다.\n\n\"오, 그래요! 이 응접실하고 같은 양식이지요. 그래서 그렇게 자주 여기 오는 거고요.\"\n\n이 대화는 그 방에서 입에 올릴 수 없는 것에 대한 암시에 기대고 있었기에 이어질 수 있었다. 다시 말해 투시케비치와 안주인의 관계에 대한 암시였다.\n\n한편 사모바르와 안주인을 둘러싼 무리에서도 대화는 똑같은 식으로 피할 수 없는 세 가지 화제 사이에서 흔들리고 있었다. 가장 최근의 시국 소식, 극장, 그리고 추문이었다. 이쪽 역시 결국 마지막 화제, 즉 악의에 찬 험담에 가서 멈추었다.\n\n\"몰티시체바 부인 이야기 들으셨어요? 딸이 아니라 어머니 말이에요. 그 여자가 diable rose 색으로 의상을 맞췄대요.\"\n\n\"말도 안 돼요! 어머, 너무 멋진데요!\"\n\n\"그 여자만 한 분별력으로—바보는 아니잖아요—자기가 얼마나 우스꽝스러운지 모르다니 신기하네요.\"\n\n저마다 불운한 몰티시체바 부인을 헐뜯거나 조롱할 말 한마디씩을 보탰고, 대화는 활활 타오르는 장작더미처럼 즐겁게 탁탁거렸다.\n\n판화를 열성적으로 수집하는 인심 좋고 뚱뚱한 사내인 베치 공작부인의 남편은 아내에게 손님이 왔다는 말을 듣고 클럽으로 가기 전에 응접실로 들어왔다. 그는 두꺼운 양탄자 위를 소리 없이 밟으며 먀흐카야 공작부인에게 다가갔다.\n\n\"닐손은 어떠셨어요?\" 그가 물었다.\n\n\"아니, 어떻게 그렇게 사람한테 몰래 다가오시나요! 깜짝 놀랐잖아요!\" 그녀가 대꾸했다. \"제발 오페라 이야기는 하지 마세요. 음악에 대해선 아무것도 모르시잖아요. 차라리 당신이 잘 아는 분야에서 만나는 게 낫겠어요. 당신의 마욜리카랑 판화 이야기를 합시다. 자, 요즘 고미술상에서 무슨 보물을 사들이셨어요?\"\n\n\"보여 드릴까요? 하지만 당신은 그런 걸 이해하지 못하실 텐데요.\"\n\n\"오, 보여 주세요! 저 그런 거 배우고 있어요, 그—이름이 뭐더라?…… 그 은행가들 말이에요…… 그 사람들한테 멋진 판화들이 있어요. 우리한테 보여 줬어요.\"\n\n\"아니, 슈츠부르크 댁에 가셨었어요?\" 사모바르 쪽에서 안주인이 물었다.\n\n\"네, ma chère. 그 사람들이 나하고 남편을 만찬에 초대했는데, 그 만찬에 나온 소스가 백 파운드나 들었다고 하더군요.\" 먀흐카야 공작부인이 모두가 듣고 있다는 걸 의식하며 큰 소리로 말했다. \"그런데 그 소스가 아주 형편없는, 무슨 푸르죽죽한 죽 같은 거였어요. 우리도 그 사람들을 초대해야 했는데, 나는 일 실링 육 펜스짜리 소스를 만들어 냈더니 다들 아주 좋아하더군요. 나는 백 파운드짜리 소스 같은 건 엄두도 못 내요.\"\n\n\"정말 둘도 없는 분이라니까요!\" 안주인이 말했다.\n\n\"기막혀요!\" 누군가가 말했다.\n\n먀흐카야 공작부인의 말이 불러일으키는 반응은 언제나 남다른 것이었는데, 그 반응의 비밀은, 지금처럼 늘 적절하게 말하는 것은 아니어도 그녀가 나름의 의미가 담긴 단순한 것들을 말한다는 데 있었다. 그녀가 사는 사교계에서는 그런 솔직한 말이 더없이 재치 있는 경구 같은 효과를 냈다. 먀흐카야 공작부인은 왜 그런 효과가 나는지 도무지 알 수 없었지만, 그런 효과가 난다는 것만은 알았고 그것을 이용했다.\n\n먀흐카야 공작부인이 말하는 동안 모두가 귀를 기울였던 탓에 대사 부인을 둘러싼 대화가 끊겼으므로, 베치 공작부인은 일행을 하나로 모으려고 대사 부인 쪽으로 돌아앉았다.\n\n\"정말 차 안 드실 거예요? 이쪽 우리 옆으로 오셔야지요.\"\n\n\"아니에요, 우리는 여기가 아주 좋아요.\" 대사 부인이 미소를 지으며 대답하고는 시작했던 대화를 이어 갔다.\n\n그것은 아주 흥미진진한 대화였다. 그들은 카레닌 부부, 남편과 아내를 도마에 올리고 있었다.\n\n\"안나는 모스크바에 다녀온 뒤로 아주 달라졌어요. 뭔가 이상한 데가 있어요.\" 그녀의 친구가 말했다.\n\n\"가장 큰 변화는 그녀가 알렉세이 브론스키라는 그림자를 데리고 돌아왔다는 거지요.\" 대사 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"그래서 어떻다는 거예요? 그림자 없는 남자, 그림자를 잃어버린 남자에 관한 그림 동화가 있잖아요. 그게 무슨 일에 대한 벌이라죠. 나는 그게 어째서 벌인지 도무지 이해할 수 없었어요. 하지만 여자라면 그림자가 없는 건 싫겠지요.\"\n\n\"네, 하지만 그림자를 가진 여자는 대개 끝이 좋지 않지요.\" 안나의 친구가 말했다.\n\n\"그 혓바닥에 불이라도 떨어지라지!\" 먀흐카야 공작부인이 불쑥 말했다. \"카레니나 부인은 훌륭한 여자예요. 나는 그 남편은 싫지만 그녀는 아주 좋아해요.\"\n\n\"왜 그 남편을 싫어하세요? 그렇게 뛰어난 사람인데.\" 대사 부인이 말했다. \"우리 남편 말로는 유럽에 그만한 정치가가 드물대요.\"\n\n\"우리 남편도 똑같은 말을 하지만, 나는 안 믿어요.\" 먀흐카야 공작부인이 말했다. \"우리 남편들이 우리한테 그런 말을 안 한다면, 우리는 사실을 있는 그대로 볼 수 있을 거예요. 내 생각에 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 그냥 바보예요. 작은 소리로 말하는 거지만요…… 그렇게 보면 모든 게 분명해지지 않아요? 전에 그 사람을 똑똑하다고 여기라는 말을 들었을 때, 나는 그 사람의 능력을 찾아 헤매면서 그걸 보지 못하는 나 자신을 바보라고 여겼어요. 그런데 비록 작은 소리이긴 해도 일단 그 사람을 바보라고 말하고 나니 모든 게 설명되더라고요, 안 그래요?\"\n\n\"오늘 어쩜 그렇게 심술궂으세요!\"\n\n\"전혀 아니에요. 달리 빠져나갈 길이 없었던 거예요. 둘 중 하나는 바보여야 했으니까요. 그리고, 뭐, 자기 자신을 두고 그런 말은 할 수 없잖아요.\"\n\n\"'누구도 자기 운에는 만족하지 않지만, 누구나 자기 재치에는 만족한다.'\" 외교관이 그 프랑스 격언을 되풀이했다.\n\n\"바로 그거예요, 바로 그거.\" 먀흐카야 공작부인이 그를 돌아보며 말했다. \"하지만 중요한 건, 나는 안나를 당신들 손에 내맡기지 않겠다는 거예요. 그녀는 아주 친절하고 아주 매력적인 사람이에요. 다들 그녀를 사랑하고 그림자처럼 졸졸 따라다니는데, 그게 그녀 탓이겠어요?\"\n\n\"오, 나는 그걸 두고 그녀를 탓할 생각은 없었어요.\" 안나의 친구가 변명하듯 말했다.\n\n\"아무도 우리를 그림자처럼 따라다니지 않는다고 해서, 우리한테 그녀를 탓할 권리가 있는 건 아니지요.\"\n\n그리고 안나의 친구를 적절히 눌러 놓은 뒤, 먀흐카야 공작부인은 자리에서 일어나 대사 부인과 함께 식탁 곁의 무리에 합류했는데, 거기서는 프로이센 왕에 관한 이야기가 오가고 있었다.\n\n\"저쪽에서 무슨 고약한 험담들을 하고 있었어요?\" 베치가 물었다.\n\n\"카레닌 부부 이야기요. 공작부인이 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치를 한 폭의 그림처럼 묘사해 주셨어요.\" 대사 부인이 미소를 지으며 식탁에 앉으며 말했다.\n\n\"우리가 못 들은 게 아쉽네요!\" 베치 공작부인이 문 쪽을 흘긋 보며 말했다. \"아, 드디어 오셨군요!\" 그녀가 들어오는 브론스키를 향해 미소를 지으며 돌아앉으며 말했다.\n\n브론스키는 여기서 만나는 사람들과 단순히 아는 사이인 정도가 아니라, 그들 모두를 날마다 보았다. 그래서 그는 방금 헤어진 사람들로 가득한 방에 들어설 때처럼 차분한 태도로 들어왔다.\n\n\"제가 어디서 왔느냐고요?\" 그가 대사 부인의 물음에 답하며 말했다. \"뭐, 어쩔 수 없이 고백해야겠군요. opera bouffe에서 왔습니다. 정말이지 백 번은 본 것 같은데, 볼 때마다 새롭게 즐겁단 말이에요. 일품이지요! 부끄러운 일인 줄은 알지만, 저는 오페라에서는 잠이 들어 버리면서도 opera bouffe는 마지막 순간까지 자리에 앉아 즐긴답니다. 오늘 저녁에는…….\"\n\n그는 어느 프랑스 여배우의 이름을 대며 그녀에 관한 이야기를 하려 했지만, 대사 부인이 짐짓 질겁하는 시늉을 하며 그의 말을 잘랐다.\n\n\"제발 그 끔찍한 이야기는 하지 마세요.\"\n\n\"알겠습니다, 안 하지요. 더구나 그 끔찍한 이야기들은 다들 아실 테니까요.\"\n\n\"그리고 그게 오페라처럼 점잖은 일로 받아들여진다면 우리도 다 보러 갈 거고요.\" 먀흐카야 공작부인이 끼어들며 말했다.\n\n# Chapter 7\n\n문가에서 발소리가 들리자 베시 공작부인은 그것이 카레니나 부인임을 알아채고 브론스키를 흘끗 바라보았다. 그는 문 쪽을 보고 있었고, 그의 얼굴에는 야릇한 새로운 표정이 떠올라 있었다. 기쁘게, 골똘하게, 그러면서도 동시에 머뭇거리며, 그는 다가오는 자태를 응시했고, 천천히 자리에서 일어섰다. 안나가 응접실로 걸어 들어왔다. 늘 그렇듯 몸을 더없이 꼿꼿이 세우고, 똑바로 앞을 바라보며, 다른 모든 사교계 여인들과 그녀를 구별 짓는 그 빠르고 단호하고 가벼운 걸음걸이로, 그녀는 안주인까지의 짧은 거리를 가로질러 그녀와 손을 맞잡고 미소 지었으며, 그 똑같은 미소로 브론스키 쪽을 돌아보았다. 브론스키는 깊이 머리를 숙이고 그녀에게 의자를 밀어 권했다.\n\n그녀는 가볍게 고개를 까딱하는 것으로만 그에게 답하고는, 얼굴을 살짝 붉히며 미간을 찌푸렸다. 그러나 곧장 지인들과 빠르게 인사를 나누고 자기에게 내미는 손들을 맞잡으면서 베시 공작부인에게 말을 건넸다.\n\n\"리디아 백작부인 댁에 있었어요. 더 일찍 오려고 했는데 그만 눌러앉아 버렸지 뭐예요. 거기에 존 경이 와 있었거든요. 아주 흥미로운 분이에요.\"\n\n\"오, 그 선교사 말이군요?\"\n\n\"네, 그분이 인도에서의 삶에 대해 이야기해 주셨는데, 정말 흥미로운 것들이었어요.\"\n\n그녀가 들어오는 바람에 끊겼던 대화가 마치 꺼져 가던 등불의 불빛처럼 다시 너울너울 살아났다.\n\n\"존 경! 그래요, 존 경. 저도 그분을 본 적이 있어요. 말씀을 잘하시지요. 블라시예바 집안 아가씨가 그분에게 홀딱 반해 있어요.\"\n\n\"그런데 작은 블라시예바 아가씨가 토포프와 결혼한다는 게 사실인가요?\"\n\n\"네, 다들 거의 정해진 일이라고 하더군요.\"\n\n\"부모들이 놀라워요! 사랑해서 하는 결혼이라던데요.\"\n\n\"사랑이라고요? 어쩜 그렇게 케케묵은 생각을 갖고 계세요! 요즘 세상에 사랑을 입에 올릴 수 있나요?\" 대사 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"어쩌겠습니까? 아직도 명맥을 잇고 있는 어리석은 옛 풍습이지요.\" 브론스키가 말했다.\n\n\"그 풍습을 따르는 사람들에게나 더 안된 노릇이지요. 제가 아는 행복한 결혼이라곤 분별에 따른 결혼뿐이에요.\"\n\n\"그래요, 하지만 그렇게 분별 있는 결혼의 행복이라는 게, 인정하기를 거부했던 바로 그 열정이 불쑥 나타나는 바람에 얼마나 자주 먼지처럼 날아가 버립니까.\" 브론스키가 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 분별에 따른 결혼이라고 할 때, 우리는 양쪽 다 이미 젊은 날의 객기를 다 부려 본 경우를 두고 하는 말이에요. 그건 성홍열 같은 거지요. 한 번 앓고 넘겨야 하는 거예요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 천연두처럼 사랑에도 예방 접종하는 법을 알아내야겠군요.\"\n\n\"나는 젊은 시절에 어느 부제와 사랑에 빠진 적이 있어요.\" 먀흐카야 공작부인이 말했다. \"그게 내게 무슨 도움이 됐는지는 모르겠지만요.\"\n\n\"아니에요, 제 생각엔, 농담은 접어 두고, 사랑을 알려면 실수를 저지르고 그것을 바로잡아야 하는 것 같아요.\" 베시 공작부인이 말했다.\n\n\"결혼한 다음에도요?\" 대사 부인이 장난스럽게 말했다.\n\n\"'고치는 데 너무 늦은 때란 없다.'\" 외교관이 그 영국 속담을 되뇌었다.\n\n\"바로 그거예요.\" 베시가 맞장구쳤다. \"실수를 저지르고 그것을 바로잡아야 하는 거지요. 당신은 어떻게 생각하세요?\" 그녀는 입가에 어렴풋이 드러나는 단호한 미소를 띤 채 말없이 대화에 귀 기울이고 있던 안나에게 돌아보며 물었다.\n\n\"제 생각엔,\" 안나는 벗어 둔 장갑을 만지작거리며 말했다. \"제 생각엔…… 사람이 많으면 많은 만큼 생각도 가지가지이듯, 분명 마음도 가지가지일 테고, 사랑의 종류도 가지가지일 거예요.\"\n\n브론스키는 안나를 응시하며, 가슴이 졸아드는 심정으로 그녀가 무슨 말을 할지 기다리고 있었다. 그녀가 그 말을 입에 올렸을 때, 그는 위기를 모면한 것처럼 한숨을 내쉬었다.\n\n안나가 별안간 그에게로 돌아섰다.\n\n\"아, 모스크바에서 편지가 왔어요. 키티 셰르바츠카야가 몹시 아프다고 쓰여 있더군요.\"\n\n\"정말입니까?\" 브론스키가 미간을 찌푸리며 말했다.\n\n안나가 그를 엄하게 바라보았다.\n\n\"그게 당신에겐 관심거리가 아닌가요?\"\n\n\"그 반대입니다, 아주 관심이 있어요. 정확히 뭐라고 알려 왔는지, 여쭤도 될까요?\" 그가 물었다.\n\n안나가 일어나 베시에게로 갔다.\n\n\"차 한 잔 주세요.\" 그녀가 베시의 탁자 앞에 서서 말했다.\n\n베시가 차를 따르는 동안 브론스키는 안나에게 다가갔다.\n\n\"그쪽에서 뭐라고 써 보냈습니까?\" 그가 거듭 물었다.\n\n\"남자들은 늘 명예에 대해 입에 올리면서도 정작 명예롭지 못한 게 무엇인지에 대해선 도무지 모르는 것 같아요.\" 안나는 그의 물음에는 답하지 않고 말했다. \"이 말을 오래전부터 당신에게 하고 싶었어요.\" 그녀는 그렇게 덧붙이고는 몇 걸음 옮겨 가, 앨범들이 놓인 구석의 탁자에 앉았다.\n\n\"당신 말씀의 의미를 잘 모르겠습니다.\" 그가 찻잔을 건네며 말했다.\n\n그녀가 자기 옆의 소파 쪽으로 눈길을 던지자, 그가 즉시 그곳에 앉았다.\n\n\"네, 줄곧 당신에게 말하고 싶었어요.\" 그녀는 그를 보지 않은 채 말했다. \"당신은 잘못 처신했어요, 아주 잘못요.\"\n\n\"내가 잘못 처신했다는 걸 모를 것 같습니까? 하지만 내가 그렇게 한 게 누구 탓이었지요?\"\n\n\"무엇 때문에 그런 말을 제게 하는 거죠?\" 그녀가 그를 엄하게 흘끗 보며 말했다.\n\n\"무엇 때문인지 당신도 알지 않습니까.\" 그가 그녀의 눈길을 마주하며 눈을 떨어뜨리지도 않은 채, 대담하고 기쁘게 대답했다.\n\n당황한 것은 그가 아니라 그녀였다.\n\n\"그건 당신에게 마음이 없다는 걸 보여 줄 뿐이에요.\" 그녀가 말했다. 그러나 그녀의 눈은 그에게 마음이 있다는 것을, 그리고 바로 그래서 자기가 그를 두려워한다는 것을 말하고 있었다.\n\n\"방금 당신이 말한 그것은 실수였지, 사랑이 아니었습니다.\"\n\n\"그 단어를, 그 끔찍한 단어를 입에 올리는 것을 내가 금했다는 걸 잊지 마세요.\" 안나가 몸서리를 치며 말했다. 그러나 그녀는 곧, 바로 그 '금했다'는 말로써 자기가 그에 대해 어떤 권리를 인정하고 있음을 드러냈고, 바로 그 사실로 인해 그가 사랑을 입에 올리도록 부추기고 있음을 느꼈다. \"오래전부터 이 말을 당신에게 하려고 했어요.\" 그녀는 단호하게 그의 눈을 들여다보며, 두 뺨에 타오르는 홍조로 온몸이 달아오른 채 말을 이었다. \"당신을 만나게 되리라는 걸 알고서 오늘 저녁 일부러 온 거예요. 이 일을 끝내야 한다고 당신에게 말하려고 왔어요. 나는 누구 앞에서도 얼굴을 붉혀 본 적이 없는데, 당신은 내가 무언가에 대해 죄책감을 느끼게 만들어요.\"\n\n그는 그녀를 바라보았고, 그녀의 얼굴에 깃든 새로운 영적인 아름다움에 충격을 받았다.\n\n\"내게 무엇을 바라십니까?\" 그가 단순하고 진지하게 말했다.\n\n\"모스크바로 가서 키티에게 용서를 구하길 바라요.\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"당신은 그걸 바라지 않잖습니까?\" 그가 말했다.\n\n그녀가 하고 싶은 말이 아니라 억지로 자신에게 시킨 말을 하고 있음을 그는 알아챘다.\n\n\"당신 말처럼 정말 나를 사랑한다면,\" 그녀가 속삭였다. \"내가 평안할 수 있도록 그렇게 해 줘요.\"\n\n그의 얼굴이 환하게 빛났다.\n\n\"당신이 내겐 삶의 전부라는 걸 모르십니까? 하지만 나는 평안을 모르고, 당신에게 그것을 줄 수도 없습니다. 내 자신 전부를—그리고 사랑을…… 그래요. 나는 당신과 나를 따로 떼어 놓고는 생각할 수가 없어요. 내게 당신과 나는 하나입니다. 그리고 나는 우리 앞에 나에게든 당신에게든 평안의 가능성이 보이지 않아요. 절망의 가능성, 비참함의 가능성이 보이거나…… 아니면 더없는 행복의 가능성, 그 얼마나 큰 행복입니까!…… 그것이 정녕 불가능할 수가 있겠습니까?\" 그가 입술로 중얼거렸지만, 그녀에게는 들렸다.\n\n그녀는 마땅히 해야 할 말을 하려고 온 정신의 힘을 쥐어짰다. 그러나 그러기는커녕 사랑이 가득 담긴 눈길을 그에게 머무르게 한 채 아무런 대답도 하지 않았다.\n\n'왔어!' 그는 황홀경 속에서 생각했다. '내가 절망하기 시작하고, 끝이라곤 없을 것 같던 바로 그때—그것이 왔어! 그녀가 나를 사랑해! 그녀가 그것을 인정하고 있어!'\n\n\"그러니 나를 위해 이렇게 해 줘요. 다시는 그런 말을 제게 하지 말고, 우리 친구로 지내요.\" 그녀가 말로는 그렇게 했지만, 그녀의 눈은 전혀 다른 말을 하고 있었다.\n\n\"우리는 결코 친구가 될 수 없어요, 당신 자신도 그걸 알지 않습니까. 우리가 세상에서 가장 행복한 사람이 될지 가장 비참한 사람이 될지—그건 당신 손에 달려 있습니다.\"\n\n그녀가 무슨 말인가 하려 했지만, 그가 가로막았다.\n\n\"나는 단 한 가지만을 청합니다. 희망할 권리를, 지금 이대로 괴로워할 권리를 청해요. 하지만 그것조차 안 된다면, 내게 사라지라고 명령하세요, 그러면 사라지겠습니다. 내 존재가 당신에게 거슬린다면, 당신은 나를 다시는 보지 않게 될 겁니다.\"\n\n\"당신을 내쫓고 싶진 않아요.\"\n\n\"그저 아무것도 바꾸지 말아 주세요, 모든 걸 지금 그대로 두세요.\" 그가 떨리는 목소리로 말했다. \"당신 남편이 오시네요.\"\n\n바로 그 순간, 실제로 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 차분하면서도 어색한 걸음걸이로 방 안으로 걸어 들어왔다.\n\n그는 아내와 브론스키를 흘끗 보고는 안주인에게로 가서, 차 한 잔을 마시려고 자리에 앉아, 늘 또렷이 들리는 신중한 목소리로, 누군가를 비웃는 평소의 농조로 이야기를 시작했다.\n\n\"댁의 랑부예가 한창 회동 중이로군요.\" 그가 좌중을 둘러보며 말했다. \"미의 여신들과 시의 여신들이 다 모였어요.\"\n\n그러나 베시 공작부인은 그가 영국 단어를 써 가며 '냉소적'이라고 부른 그런 어조를 견디지 못했고, 능란한 안주인답게 곧바로 그를 국민 개병제라는 주제에 관한 진지한 대화로 끌어들였다. 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 즉시 그 주제에 흥미를 느꼈고, 그것을 공격했던 베시 공작부인에 맞서 새로운 제국 칙령을 진지하게 옹호하기 시작했다.\n\n브론스키와 안나는 여전히 작은 탁자에 앉아 있었다.\n\n\"이건 점점 꼴사나워지는데요.\" 한 부인이 카레니나 부인과 브론스키와 그녀의 남편을 의미심장하게 흘끗 보며 속삭였다.\n\n\"제가 뭐라고 했어요?\" 안나의 친구가 말했다.\n\n그러나 그 부인들뿐 아니라 방 안의 거의 모든 사람들이, 심지어 먀흐카야 공작부인과 베시 자신까지도, 그것이 무언가 거슬리는 사실이라도 되는 양 전체 무리에서 떨어져 나간 그 두 사람 쪽을 여러 차례 바라보았다. 그 방향을 단 한 번도 보지 않은 사람은 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치뿐이었고, 그는 자기가 빠져든 흥미로운 토론에서 한눈을 팔지 않았다.\n\n베시 공작부인은 모두에게 불쾌한 인상이 새겨지고 있음을 알아채고는, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 말을 들을 사람을 다른 누군가로 슬쩍 바꿔 자기 자리에 들여보낸 뒤, 안나에게로 갔다.\n\n\"나는 당신 남편의 말솜씨가 그토록 명료하고 정확한 데 늘 놀라곤 해요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그분이 말씀하실 때면 더없이 초월적인 관념들조차 내 손에 잡힐 것만 같다니까요.\"\n\n\"오, 그래요!\" 안나는 행복한 미소로 환하게 빛나며 말했지만, 베시가 한 말을 한마디도 알아듣지 못했다. 그녀는 큰 탁자 쪽으로 건너가 전체 대화에 끼어들었다.\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 삼십 분쯤 머문 뒤, 아내에게 다가가 함께 집으로 돌아가자고 권했다. 그러나 그녀는 그를 보지도 않은 채 저녁 식사까지 남아 있겠다고 대답했다. 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 인사를 하고 물러갔다.\n\n카레니나 부인의 마부인 뚱뚱한 늙은 타타르인은, 추위에 얼어붙어 입구에서 앞발을 쳐드는 그녀의 잿빛 말 한 쌍 중 한 마리를 간신히 붙들고 있었다. 하인 하나가 마차 문을 열고 서 있었다. 문지기는 저택의 큰 문을 활짝 열어 잡고 서 있었다. 안나 아르카지예브나는 자기의 잰 작은 손으로, 모피 외투 갈고리에 걸린 소맷자락의 레이스를 풀어내며, 고개를 숙인 채 자기를 배웅하며 브론스키가 중얼거리는 말에 귀를 기울이고 있었다.\n\n\"당신은 물론 아무 말도 하지 않았고, 나도 아무것도 청하지 않습니다.\" 그가 말하고 있었다. \"하지만 내가 바라는 게 우정이 아니라는 걸 당신은 알지 않습니까. 내 삶에 행복이라곤 단 하나뿐이라는 걸, 당신이 그토록 싫어하는 그 단어…… 그래요, 사랑이라는 걸요!……\"\n\n\"사랑.\" 그녀는 속으로 삼키는 목소리로 천천히 되뇌었다가, 레이스를 갈고리에서 막 풀어낸 바로 그 순간 불쑥 덧붙였다. \"내가 그 단어를 싫어하는 건 그것이 내겐 너무 많은 의미를 지니기 때문이에요, 당신이 이해할 수 있는 것보다 훨씬 더요.\" 그러고는 그의 얼굴을 흘끗 들여다보았다. \"Au revoir!\"\n\n그녀는 그에게 손을 내밀고는, 잰 탄력 있는 걸음으로 문지기 곁을 지나 마차 안으로 사라졌다.\n\n그녀의 눈길, 그녀의 손길이 그를 활활 타오르게 했다. 그는 그녀가 닿았던 자기 손바닥에 입을 맞추고, 그날 저녁 자기가 지난 두 달 동안보다 자기 목표의 달성에 더 가까이 다가갔다는 느낌에 행복해하며 집으로 돌아갔다.\n* Au revoir: 프랑스어로 '안녕히 가세요, 또 만나요'.\n\n# Chapter 8\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 자기 아내가 브론스키와 함께 따로 떨어진 탁자에 앉아 무언가에 대해 열심히 이야기를 나누고 있다는 사실에서 별다른 이상함이나 부적절함을 보지 못했다. 하지만 그 자리에 있던 다른 사람들에게는 그것이 무언가 두드러지고 부적절하게 보인다는 것을 알아차렸고, 그 때문에 그에게도 그것이 부적절하게 여겨졌다. 그는 이 일에 대해 아내에게 말해야겠다고 마음먹었다.\n\n집에 도착한 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 평소처럼 서재로 가서 나지막한 안락의자에 앉았고, 페이퍼나이프를 끼워 두었던 자리에서 교황권에 관한 책을 펼쳐 평소처럼 한 시까지 읽었다. 하지만 이따금 그는 무언가를 떨쳐 내려는 듯 넓은 이마를 문지르고 고개를 흔들었다. 평소와 같은 시각에 그는 일어나 잠자리에 들 채비를 했다. 안나 아르카지예브나는 아직 돌아오지 않았다. 그는 책을 옆구리에 끼고 위층으로 올라갔다. 하지만 이날 저녁에는 평소처럼 공무의 세세한 사항에 대한 생각과 사색에 잠기는 대신, 그의 생각이 온통 아내와 그녀에게 얽힌 무언가 언짢은 일에 사로잡혀 있었다. 그는 평소의 습관과 달리 침대에 들지 않고 두 손을 등 뒤로 맞잡은 채 방들을 이리저리 거닐기 시작했다. 그는 잠자리에 들 수가 없었다. 무엇보다 먼저 방금 일어난 상황을 철저히 따져 보는 일이 절대적으로 필요하다고 느꼈기 때문이다.\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 이 일에 대해 아내와 이야기를 나눠야겠다고 마음먹었을 때만 해도, 그것은 아주 쉽고 간단한 일처럼 여겨졌다. 하지만 이제 막 떠오른 그 문제를 곰곰이 생각하기 시작하자, 그것이 그에게 몹시 복잡하고 어렵게 느껴졌다.\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 질투하는 사람이 아니었다. 그의 생각으로 질투란 아내에 대한 모욕이며, 사람은 자기 아내를 믿어야 마땅했다. 왜 믿어야 마땅한가—다시 말해 자기의 젊은 아내가 언제까지나 자기를 사랑하리라고 왜 완전히 확신해야 하는가—를 그는 스스로에게 묻지 않았다. 하지만 그는 믿음의 결핍이라는 것을 경험한 적이 없었으니, 아내를 믿고 있었고 또 마땅히 믿어야 한다고 스스로에게 말해 왔기 때문이다. 이제 질투가 수치스러운 감정이며 사람은 마땅히 믿음을 품어야 한다는 그의 확신이 무너지지는 않았지만, 그는 자기가 무언가 비논리적이고 불합리한 것과 정면으로 마주하고 있음을 느꼈고, 어찌해야 할지 알지 못했다. 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 삶과, 자기 아내가 자기 아닌 다른 누군가를 사랑할 수 있다는 가능성과 정면으로 마주하고 있었으며, 이것이 그에게는 몹시 불합리하고 이해할 수 없는 일로 여겨졌으니, 그것이 바로 삶 그 자체였기 때문이다. 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 평생을 삶의 반영을 다루는 공무의 영역에서 살고 일해 왔다. 그리고 삶 그 자체에 부딪칠 때마다 그는 그것으로부터 움츠러들었다. 이제 그는 다리를 건너 벼랑을 차분히 지나가다가, 별안간 다리가 끊어져 있고 그 아래에 심연이 입을 벌리고 있음을 발견한 사람의 심정과 비슷한 감정을 맛보았다. 그 심연은 삶 그 자체였고, 다리는 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 살아온 그 인위적인 삶이었다. 난생처음 자기 아내가 다른 누군가를 사랑할 수도 있다는 의문이 그에게 떠올랐고, 그는 그것에 소스라쳤다.\n\n그는 옷도 벗지 않은 채, 등불 하나가 타고 있는 식당의 울리는 마룻바닥 위를, 소파 위에 걸린 자기의 큼직한 새 초상화에 빛이 반사되는 어두운 응접실의 양탄자 위를, 그리고 두 자루의 양초가 타며 그녀 부모와 여자 친구들의 초상화며 그가 너무도 잘 아는 그녀 책상의 예쁜 자질구레한 장식품들을 비추고 있는 그녀의 내실을 가로질러 일정한 걸음으로 거닐었다. 그는 그녀의 내실을 가로질러 침실 문까지 갔다가 다시 돌아섰다. 걸음을 돌릴 때마다, 특히 불 밝힌 식당의 마룻바닥에서, 그는 멈춰 서서 스스로에게 말했다. \"그래, 이건 내가 결단을 내려 끝장을 봐야 해. 이에 대한 내 견해와 결정을 밝혀야 해.\" 그러고는 다시 돌아섰다. \"하지만 무엇을 밝힌단 말인가—무슨 결정을?\" 응접실에서 그는 스스로에게 물었지만, 아무런 답도 찾지 못했다. \"하지만 결국,\" 그는 내실로 돌아서기 전에 스스로에게 물었다. \"무슨 일이 일어났단 말인가? 아무 일도 없었어. 그녀가 그와 오랫동안 이야기를 나눴지. 하지만 그게 어쨌단 말인가? 사교계의 여자라면 마음에 드는 누구와도 이야기할 수 있는 법이지. 게다가 질투란 나 자신과 그녀를 둘 다 깎아내리는 짓이야.\" 그녀의 내실로 들어서면서 그는 스스로에게 말했다. 그러나 전에는 그토록 무게를 지녔던 이 격언이 이제는 아무런 무게도 의미도 지니지 못했다. 그리고 침실 문에서 그는 또다시 돌아섰지만, 어두운 응접실로 들어서자 어떤 내면의 목소리가 그렇지 않다고, 다른 이들이 그것을 알아차렸다는 것은 무언가가 있다는 뜻이라고 말했다. 그래서 식당에서 그는 또다시 스스로에게 말했다. \"그래, 나는 결단을 내려 이를 끝장내야 하고, 이에 대한 내 견해를 밝혀야 해……\" 그리고 또다시 응접실의 모퉁이에서 그는 스스로에게 물었다. \"어떻게 결단을 내린단 말인가?\" 그리고 또다시 스스로에게 물었다. \"무슨 일이 일어났던가?\" 그러고는 \"아무 일도 없었어.\" 하고 답했고, 질투란 아내를 모욕하는 감정임을 떠올렸다. 하지만 응접실에서 그는 또다시 무언가 일이 일어났다고 확신했다. 그의 생각은 그의 몸과 마찬가지로 새로운 것에 이르지 못한 채 완전한 한 바퀴를 돌고 있었다. 그는 이를 알아차리고 이마를 문지른 뒤 그녀의 내실에 앉았다.\n\n거기서 그녀의 책상을, 그 위에 놓인 공작석 압지함과 쓰다 만 편지를 바라보자, 그의 생각이 별안간 바뀌었다. 그는 그녀를, 그녀가 무엇을 생각하고 느끼는지를 떠올리기 시작했다. 난생처음 그는 그녀의 개인적인 삶과 그녀의 생각과 욕망을 또렷이 그려 보았고, 그녀가 그녀만의 별개의 삶을 가질 수 있고 또 가져야 한다는 생각이 그에게는 너무도 섬뜩하게 여겨져서 서둘러 그것을 떨쳐 버렸다. 그것은 그가 들여다보기 두려워한 심연이었다. 다른 사람의 처지에 자기 자신을 생각과 감정으로 놓아 보는 일은 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에게는 자연스럽지 않은 정신적 훈련이었다. 그는 이 정신적 훈련을 공상을 해롭고 위험하게 남용하는 짓으로 여겼다.\n\n'그리고 이 모든 일에서 가장 고약한 건,' 그는 생각했다. '바로 지금, 나의 위대한 과업이 완성에 가까워지는 바로 이 순간에'(그는 그 무렵 추진하고 있던 사업을 생각하고 있었다) '내게 온갖 정신적 평온과 모든 기력이 필요한 바로 이때에, 하필 이 어리석은 걱정거리가 나를 덮친다는 거야. 하지만 어쩌겠나? 나는 그것들과 맞설 의지력도 없이 불안과 걱정에 굴복하는 그런 부류의 사내가 아니야.'\n\n\"나는 이 일을 따져 보고, 결단에 이르러, 머릿속에서 떨쳐 내야 해.\" 그는 소리 내어 말했다.\n\n'그녀의 감정에 관한 문제, 그녀의 마음속에서 무슨 일이 지나갔고 또 지나가고 있을지에 관한 문제, 그건 내 소관이 아니야. 그건 그녀의 양심의 일이고, 종교의 영역에 속하는 거지.' 그는 스스로에게 말했고, 이 새로운 상황을 규율하는 원리들 가운데 어느 항목에 마땅히 귀속시킬 수 있을지 찾아냈다는 느낌에서 위안을 얻었다.\n\n'그러니,' 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 스스로에게 말했다. '그녀의 감정 등에 관한 문제들은 그녀의 양심에 속한 문제들이고, 나는 그것과 아무런 상관이 없어. 나의 의무는 명백히 규정되어 있어. 한 가정의 가장으로서 나는 의무상 그녀를 인도해야 할 사람이고, 따라서 어느 정도는 책임을 진 사람이지. 나는 내가 감지하는 위험을 지적하고, 그녀에게 경고하고, 심지어 내 권위를 행사할 의무가 있어. 나는 그녀에게 분명히 말해야 해.' 그리고 그가 오늘 밤 아내에게 할 말이 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 머릿속에서 또렷한 형체를 갖추었다. 무엇을 말할지 곰곰이 따져 보면서, 그는 그토록 보잘것없는 성과를 위해 자기의 시간과 정신력을 가정사에 써야 한다는 것이 다소 유감스러웠지만, 그럼에도 불구하고 그가 할 연설의 형식과 내용은 마치 장관 보고서처럼 그의 머릿속에서 또렷하고 분명하게 형체를 갖추어 갔다.\n\n'나는 다음의 항목들을 말하고 충분히 밝혀야 해. 첫째, 여론과 예법에 부여해야 할 가치에 대한 설명. 둘째, 결혼의 종교적 의의에 대한 설명. 셋째, 필요하다면, 우리 아들에게 일어날 수도 있는 불행에 대한 언급. 넷째, 그녀 자신에게 닥칠 법한 불행에 대한 언급.' 그러고는 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 손가락을 맞물려 펴 보았고, 손가락 마디에서 우두둑 소리가 났다. 손가락을 우두둑 꺾는 이 버릇은, 나쁜 습관이긴 했지만, 늘 그를 진정시키고 그의 생각에 정확성을 부여해 주었으니, 지금 이 고비에서 그에게 너무도 필요한 것이었다.\n\n현관 앞으로 마차가 다가오는 소리가 들렸다. 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 방 한가운데에서 멈춰 섰다.\n\n여자의 발소리가 계단을 올라오는 것이 들렸다. 연설할 준비를 갖춘 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 맞물린 손가락을 꽉 누른 채 서서, 또 우두둑 소리가 나지 않을까 기다렸다. 마디 하나가 우두둑 소리를 냈다.\n\n계단 위로 들려오는 가벼운 발소리만으로도 그는 이미 그녀가 가까이 왔음을 알아차렸고, 자기의 연설에 만족하면서도 자기 앞에 닥친 해명을 두려워하는 마음이 들었다……\n\n# Chapter 9\n\n안나는 고개를 숙인 채, 후드의 술을 만지작거리며 들어왔다. 그녀의 얼굴은 환하게 빛나며 달아올라 있었다. 하지만 그 빛은 밝음에서 오는 빛이 아니었다. 그것은 어두운 밤 한복판에서 타오르는 화재의 무서운 불빛을 떠올리게 했다. 남편을 보자 안나는 고개를 들고, 마치 방금 잠에서 깬 사람처럼 미소를 지었다.\n\n\"아직 안 주무셨어요? 별일이네요!\" 그녀는 후드를 떨어뜨리며 이렇게 말하고는, 걸음을 멈추지 않고 화장실로 들어갔다. \"늦었어요, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치.\" 문을 지나서며 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"안나, 당신과 이야기를 좀 해야겠소.\"\n\n\"저와요?\" 그녀가 의아한 듯 말했다. 그녀는 화장실 문 뒤에서 나와 그를 바라보았다. \"왜요, 무슨 일인데요? 무슨 이야기요?\" 그녀가 앉으며 물었다. \"그래요, 정 그렇게 필요하다면 이야기하죠. 하지만 자는 편이 더 나을 텐데요.\"\n\n안나는 입에서 나오는 대로 말하면서, 자기가 하는 말을 들으며 스스로의 거짓말하는 능력에 놀랐다. 그 말이 얼마나 단순하고 자연스러운지, 그리고 그녀가 그저 졸린 것처럼 보일 법한지! 그녀는 자기가 꿰뚫을 수 없는 거짓의 갑옷으로 무장한 듯한 느낌이 들었다. 그녀는 어떤 보이지 않는 힘이 자기를 도우러 와서 떠받쳐 주고 있는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n\"안나, 당신에게 경고해 둬야겠소.\" 그가 입을 열었다.\n\n\"경고요?\" 그녀가 말했다. \"무엇에 대해서요?\"\n\n그녀는 너무도 천연덕스럽게, 너무도 환하게 그를 바라보았기에, 남편처럼 그녀를 잘 알지 못하는 사람이라면 그 말의 어조에서도 의미에서도 부자연스러운 점을 전혀 알아채지 못했을 것이다. 하지만 그녀를 잘 아는 그에게는, 그가 평소보다 오 분만 늦게 잠자리에 들어도 그녀가 그것을 알아채고 그 까닭을 묻는다는 것을 아는 그에게는, 그녀가 느끼는 온갖 기쁨이며 즐거움이며 고통을 곧바로 그에게 털어놓는다는 것을 아는 그에게는, 지금 그녀가 그의 심정을 알아채려 하지 않고, 자기 자신에 대해 한마디도 말하려 하지 않는 것을 보는 것이 큰 의미가 있었다. 그는 지금까지 늘 그의 앞에 활짝 열려 있던 그녀 영혼의 가장 깊은 구석들이 그에게 닫혀 버렸음을 알았다. 그뿐만 아니라, 그는 그녀의 말투에서 그녀가 그것을 조금도 동요하지 않고, 오히려 대놓고 그에게 이렇게 말하는 것처럼 느꼈다. \"그래요, 닫혔어요, 그리고 닫혀 있어야만 하고, 앞으로도 그럴 거예요.\" 이제 그는 집에 돌아왔다가 자기 집이 잠겨 있는 것을 발견한 사람이 느낄 법한 그런 감정을 경험했다. \"하지만 어쩌면 아직 열쇠를 찾을 수 있을지도 몰라.\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 생각했다.\n\n\"내가 경고하고 싶은 건,\" 그가 낮은 목소리로 말했다. \"당신이 경솔하고 조심성이 없어서 사교계에 당신에 관한 입방아거리를 만들 수 있다는 거요. 오늘 저녁 당신이 브론스키 백작과 나눈 너무도 활기찬 대화는,\" 그는 단호하게 일부러 힘주어 그 이름을 또박또박 발음했다. \"사람들의 주의를 끌었소.\"\n\n그는 말하면서 그녀의 웃는 눈을 바라보았는데, 그 눈은 이제 그 꿰뚫을 수 없는 표정으로 그를 두렵게 했다. 그리고 그렇게 말하는 동안, 그는 자기 말이 얼마나 쓸데없고 부질없는지를 절감했다.\n\n\"당신은 언제나 그런 식이군요.\" 그녀는 마치 그를 완전히 오해한 것처럼, 그리고 그가 한 말 가운데 마지막 구절만 알아들은 것처럼 대답했다. \"어떤 때는 제가 따분하게 구는 게 마음에 안 든다고 하고, 또 어떤 때는 제가 활기찬 게 마음에 안 든다고 하시네요. 저는 따분하게 굴지 않았어요. 그게 기분 나쁘세요?\"\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 몸을 떨면서, 손가락 마디에서 우두둑 소리를 내려고 손을 구부렸다.\n\n\"오, 제발, 그러지 마세요, 저는 그게 정말 싫어요.\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"안나, 이게 당신이오?\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 가만히 자신을 억누르며, 손가락의 움직임을 멈추고 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 도대체 무슨 일이에요?\" 그녀가 너무도 진심 어린, 그리고 우스꽝스러운 의아함을 띠고 말했다. \"저한테 뭘 바라시는 거예요?\"\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 말을 멈추고 이마와 눈을 문질렀다. 그는 자기가 의도했던 일—다시 말해 세상의 눈에 비칠 실수에 대해 아내에게 경고하는 일—을 하는 대신, 자기도 모르게 그녀의 양심에 속한 문제를 두고 흥분해 버렸고, 두 사람 사이에 있다고 여겨지는 그 장벽과 씨름하고 있음을 깨달았다.\n\n\"내가 당신에게 하려던 말은 이거요.\" 그가 차갑고 침착하게 말을 이었다. \"그러니 부디 들어 주기 바라오. 당신도 알다시피 나는 질투를 굴욕적이고 천박한 감정으로 여기며, 결코 거기에 휘둘리도록 나 자신을 내버려 두지 않을 거요. 하지만 함부로 무시했다가는 대가를 치르게 되는 어떤 예법의 규칙이라는 게 있는 법이오. 오늘 저녁 그것을 알아챈 건 내가 아니라, 좌중에 끼친 인상으로 미루어 보건대 모두가 알아챘소, 당신의 행동거지가 결코 바람직하다고 할 수 없었다는 걸 말이오.\"\n\n\"저는 정말 이해가 안 가요.\" 안나는 어깨를 으쓱하며 말했다. '이이는 신경도 안 쓰는 거야.' 그녀는 생각했다. '하지만 다른 사람들이 알아챘고, 그게 이이를 속상하게 한 거지.' \"당신 몸이 안 좋으신 거예요, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치.\" 그녀는 이렇게 덧붙이고는 일어나서 문 쪽으로 가려 했다. 하지만 그가 그녀를 막기라도 하려는 듯 앞으로 나섰다.\n\n그의 얼굴은 안나가 한 번도 본 적 없을 만큼 흉하고 험상궂었다. 그녀는 걸음을 멈추고, 고개를 뒤로 젖혀 한쪽으로 기울인 채, 잰 손놀림으로 머리핀을 빼기 시작했다.\n\n\"좋아요, 그다음에 무슨 말이 나올지 듣고 있어요.\" 그녀가 차분하고 비꼬는 투로 말했다. \"정말이지 흥미롭게 듣고 있다고요, 도대체 무슨 일인지 알고 싶으니까요.\"\n\n그녀는 말하면서, 자기가 말하고 있는 그 확신에 차고 차분하고 자연스러운 어조에, 그리고 자기가 골라 쓰는 말들에 스스로 놀랐다.\n\n\"당신 감정의 온갖 세세한 부분까지 파고들 권리는 내게 없소, 게다가 나는 그것이 쓸데없을뿐더러 해롭기까지 하다고 여기오.\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 입을 열었다. \"자기 영혼을 들쑤시다 보면, 흔히 거기 그냥 눈에 띄지 않게 묻혀 있어도 좋았을 무언가를 들쑤셔 내곤 하지. 당신의 감정은 당신 양심에 속한 문제요. 하지만 나는 당신에게, 나 자신에게, 그리고 하느님께 당신의 의무를 일러 줘야 할 책무가 있소. 우리의 삶은 사람이 아니라 하느님께서 맺어 주신 것이오. 그 결합은 오직 죄악으로만 끊어질 수 있으며, 그런 종류의 죄악은 그 자체로 응징을 불러오는 법이오.\"\n\n\"한마디도 이해가 안 가요. 게다가, 아이고! 공교롭게도 어찌나 졸린지요.\" 그녀는 머리에 남은 핀을 더듬으며 재빠르게 손을 머리카락 사이로 훑으며 말했다.\n\n\"안나, 제발 그런 식으로 말하지 마시오!\" 그가 부드럽게 말했다. \"어쩌면 내가 잘못 생각한 것일지도 모르오, 하지만 내 말을 믿어 주시오, 내가 하는 말은 당신을 위해서인 만큼이나 나 자신을 위해서이기도 하오. 나는 당신의 남편이고, 당신을 사랑하오.\"\n\n한순간 그녀의 얼굴이 굳어졌고, 눈에 어렸던 조롱의 빛이 사그라들었다. 하지만 사랑이라는 말은 그녀를 다시 반발하게 했다. 그녀는 생각했다. '사랑? 이이가 사랑을 할 수 있다고? 사랑이라는 게 있다는 걸 들은 적이 없었다면, 이이는 그런 말을 절대 쓰지 않았을 거야. 이이는 사랑이 뭔지조차 몰라.'\n\n\"알렉세이 알렉산드로비치, 정말 저는 이해가 안 가요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"당신이 무엇을 보았다는 건지 분명히 말해 주세요…….\"\n\n\"미안하지만, 내가 할 말을 다 하게 해 주시오. 나는 당신을 사랑하오. 하지만 내 이야기를 하는 게 아니오. 이 문제에서 가장 중요한 사람은 우리 아들과 당신이오. 다시 말하지만, 내 말이 당신에게는 전혀 불필요하고 엉뚱하게 들릴 수도 있소. 어쩌면 그 말들이 내 잘못된 인상에서 비롯된 것일 수도 있소. 그렇다면 부디 나를 용서해 주시오. 하지만 만약 당신 스스로 그 말들에 아주 작은 근거라도 있음을 느낀다면, 부디 조금만 생각해 보고, 그리고 마음이 시키거든 나에게 털어놓아 주시오…….\"\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 자기도 모르게 미리 준비해 둔 것과는 전혀 다른 말을 하고 있었다.\n\n\"저는 할 말이 없어요. 게다가,\" 그녀는 미소를 가까스로 억누르며 서둘러 말했다. \"정말이지 이제 잘 시간이에요.\"\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 한숨을 쉬고는, 더 이상 말하지 않고 침실로 들어갔다.\n\n그녀가 침실로 들어왔을 때, 그는 이미 잠자리에 누워 있었다. 그의 입술은 굳게 다물려 있었고, 그의 눈은 그녀를 외면하고 있었다. 안나는 자기 침대로 들어가, 그가 다시 자기에게 말을 걸어오리라고 매 순간 기다리며 누워 있었다. 그녀는 그가 말하는 것을 두려워하면서도 또한 바랐다. 하지만 그는 잠자코 있었다. 그녀는 오랫동안 꼼짝도 않고 기다렸고, 그러다 그를 잊어버렸다. 그녀는 그 다른 사람을 생각했다. 그녀는 그를 그려 보았고, 그를 떠올리는 것만으로 자기 가슴이 감정과 죄스러운 환희로 벅차오르는 것을 느꼈다. 그때 별안간 고르고 평온한 코 고는 소리가 들려왔다. 처음 한순간 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 자기 코 고는 소리에 흠칫 놀란 듯 멈추었다. 하지만 두어 번 숨을 쉬는 사이를 두고 나서 코 고는 소리는 새로운 평온한 가락으로 다시 울리기 시작했다.\n\n\"늦었어, 늦었어.\" 그녀는 미소를 띠고 속삭였다. 그녀는 오랫동안 꼼짝도 않고 눈을 뜬 채 누워 있었는데, 어둠 속에서도 그 눈의 광채를 자기 스스로 볼 수 있을 것만 같았다.\n\n# Chapter 10\n\n그때부터 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치와 그의 아내에게 새로운 삶이 시작되었다. 특별한 일은 아무것도 일어나지 않았다. 안나는 늘 그래 왔듯이 사교계에 드나들었고, 특히 베시 공작 부인의 집에 자주 갔으며, 어디서나 브론스키와 마주쳤다. 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치도 이를 보았지만 어찌할 도리가 없었다. 그녀를 솔직한 대화로 끌어들이려는 그의 온갖 노력에 그녀는, 무언가 재미있어하는 듯한 어리둥절함으로 이루어진, 그가 도무지 뚫고 들어갈 수 없는 장벽으로 맞섰다. 겉으로는 모든 것이 그대로였지만, 두 사람의 내면적 관계는 완전히 달라져 있었다. 정치의 세계에서 막강한 권력을 지닌 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치도 이 일에서만큼은 자신이 무력하다고 느꼈다. 고개를 숙인 황소처럼, 그는 자기 머리 위로 들어 올려졌다고 느껴지는 일격을 순순히 기다렸다. 그 일을 생각하기 시작할 때마다 그는 한 번 더 노력해 봐야 한다고, 다정함과 부드러움과 설득으로 아직 그녀를 구원할, 그녀를 제정신으로 되돌릴 희망이 남아 있다고 느꼈고, 날마다 그녀와 이야기할 채비를 했다. 그러나 막상 그녀와 이야기를 시작할 때마다, 그녀를 사로잡은 악과 기만의 영(靈)이 자기까지도 사로잡고 있음을 느꼈고, 그래서 그는 본디 말하려던 것과는 전혀 다른 어조로 그녀에게 말하고 말았다. 본의 아니게 그는, 자기가 지금 하고 있는 그런 말을 입에 담는 사람을 비웃을 때 쓰던 그 습관적인 어조로 그녀에게 말했다. 그리고 그런 어조로는 그녀에게 정작 해야 할 말을 도무지 할 수가 없었다.\n\n# Chapter 11\n\n브론스키에게는 거의 일 년 동안 옛 욕망들을 모조리 밀어내며 삶을 통째로 사로잡았던 단 하나의 갈망이었던 것, 안나에게는 불가능하고 끔찍하며, 바로 그렇기에 더욱 황홀한 행복의 꿈이었던 것, 그 갈망이 이루어졌다. 그는 창백한 얼굴로 아래턱을 떨면서 그녀 앞에 서서, 어떻게 해야 할지도 왜 그래야 하는지도 모른 채, 진정하라고 그녀에게 애원했다.\n\n\"안나! 안나!\" 그가 목멘 소리로 말했다. \"안나, 제발 부탁이오!……\"\n\n그러나 그가 큰 소리로 말할수록 그녀는, 한때 자랑스럽고 명랑했으나 이제는 수치심에 짓눌린 고개를 점점 더 떨구었고, 몸을 숙이더니 앉아 있던 소파에서 미끄러져 그의 발치 마룻바닥으로 내려앉았다. 그가 붙들지 않았다면 그녀는 양탄자 위에 쓰러졌을 것이다.\n\n\"하느님! 저를 용서해 주세요!\" 그녀는 흐느끼며 그의 두 손을 자기 가슴에 꼭 눌러 댔다.\n\n그녀는 자신이 너무도 죄 많고 너무도 죄스럽게 느껴져, 자신을 낮추고 용서를 비는 것 외에는 아무것도 남아 있지 않았다. 그리고 이제 그녀의 삶에는 그 말고 아무도 없었기에, 그녀는 그에게 용서를 비는 기도를 올렸다. 그를 바라보면서 그녀는 자신의 굴욕을 육체로 느꼈고, 더 이상 아무 말도 할 수 없었다. 그는 살인자가 자기 손으로 목숨을 앗은 시신을 마주했을 때 느낄 법한 감정을 느꼈다. 그가 목숨을 앗은 그 시신은 바로 그들의 사랑, 그들 사랑의 첫 단계였다. 이 끔찍한 수치의 대가로 사들인 것의 기억 속에는 무언가 무섭고 역겨운 데가 있었다. 영혼이 발가벗겨진 데 대한 수치심이 그녀를 짓눌렀고, 그에게도 옮아 왔다. 그러나 제 희생물의 시신 앞에서 느끼는 온갖 살인자의 공포에도 불구하고, 그는 그 시신을 토막 내고, 시신을 감추고, 그 살인으로 얻은 것을 써먹어야만 한다.\n\n그리하여 마치 격정에 사로잡힌 듯 분노에 차서, 살인자가 시신에 달려들어 그것을 끌고 토막 내듯이, 그는 그녀의 얼굴과 어깨를 입맞춤으로 뒤덮었다. 그녀는 그의 손을 잡은 채 꼼짝도 하지 않았다. \"그래, 이 입맞춤들—이게 바로 이 수치로 사들인 거야. 그래, 그리고 이 한 손, 언제까지나 내 것이 될 손—내 공범의 손.\" 그녀는 그 손을 들어 입을 맞추었다. 그는 무릎을 꿇고 그녀의 얼굴을 보려 했지만, 그녀는 얼굴을 감추며 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 마침내 그녀는 자신을 다잡으려는 듯 일어나 그를 밀어냈다. 그녀의 얼굴은 여전히 아름다웠지만, 그래서 더욱 가엾어 보일 뿐이었다.\n\n\"모든 게 끝났어요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"나에겐 당신밖에 없어요. 그걸 기억해 줘요.\"\n\n\"내 삶 전부인 것을 어떻게 잊을 수 있겠소. 이 행복의 한순간을 위해서라면……\"\n\n\"행복이라고요!\" 그녀는 공포와 혐오에 차서 말했고, 그녀의 그 공포는 자기도 모르게 그에게 옮아 갔다. \"제발, 한마디도, 더는 한마디도 하지 말아요.\"\n\n그녀는 재빨리 일어나 그에게서 멀어졌다.\n\n\"더는 한마디도.\" 그녀는 거듭 말했고, 그로서는 이해할 수 없는 싸늘한 절망의 빛을 띤 채 그와 헤어졌다. 그녀는 그 순간 새로운 삶으로 발을 들여놓는 데 대한 수치심과 환희와 공포의 감정을 말로 옮길 수 없음을 느꼈고, 또 그것을 입에 올리고 싶지도, 어울리지 않는 말로 이 감정을 천박하게 만들고 싶지도 않았다. 그러나 나중에도, 그다음 날에도, 사흘째 되던 날에도, 그녀는 여전히 자기 감정의 복잡함을 표현할 말을 찾지 못했다. 아니, 자기 영혼 속에 든 모든 것을 또렷이 헤아려 볼 생각조차 떠올릴 수가 없었다.\n\n그녀는 혼잣말을 했다. \"아니, 지금은 그걸 생각할 수가 없어, 나중에, 좀 더 차분해지면.\" 그러나 생각할 만한 차분함은 끝내 찾아오지 않았다. 자기가 한 일과 자기에게 닥칠 일과 자기가 마땅히 해야 할 일에 대한 생각이 떠오를 때마다 공포가 그녀를 덮쳤고, 그녀는 그 생각들을 떨쳐 내 버렸다.\n\n\"나중에, 나중에.\" 그녀는 말했다. \"좀 더 차분해지면.\"\n\n그러나 생각을 통제할 수 없는 꿈속에서는, 그녀의 처지가 온갖 추악한 알몸을 드러낸 채 그녀에게 나타났다. 한 가지 꿈이 거의 매일 밤 그녀를 따라다녔다. 그녀는 두 사람 다 동시에 자기 남편이며, 둘 다 자기에게 애무를 퍼붓는 꿈을 꾸었다. 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 울면서 그녀의 손에 입을 맞추며 \"지금 우리는 정말 행복하군!\" 하고 말했다. 그리고 알렉세이 브론스키도 거기 있었고, 그 역시 그녀의 남편이었다. 그리고 그녀는 한때 이런 일이 불가능하게 여겨졌다는 것이 놀라워, 웃으면서 두 사람에게 이게 훨씬 더 간단한 일이며 이제 둘 다 행복하고 만족스럽다고 설명하고 있었다. 그러나 이 꿈은 악몽처럼 그녀를 짓눌렀고, 그녀는 공포에 질려 꿈에서 깨어났다.\n\n# Chapter 12\n\n모스크바에서 돌아온 직후의 며칠 동안, 레빈은 청혼을 거절당한 치욕을 떠올릴 때마다 몸서리를 치고 얼굴이 붉어졌는데, 그럴 때면 스스로에게 이렇게 말하곤 했다. \"물리 시험에서 떨어져 진급을 못 했을 때, 내가 완전히 끝장났다고 생각하며 바로 이렇게 몸서리치고 낯을 붉혔지. 그리고 내게 맡겨진 누이의 그 일을 그르쳤을 때도 완전히 망했다고 생각했어. 그런데 세월이 지난 지금 그 일을 떠올리면, 어떻게 그게 나를 그토록 괴롭힐 수 있었는지 의아하단 말이야. 이번 일도 마찬가지일 거야. 시간이 흐르면 이 일도 아무렇지 않게 되겠지.\"\n\n그러나 석 달이 지났는데도 그는 여전히 그 일을 떨쳐 내지 못했다. 그 일을 떠올리는 것은 첫 며칠 동안과 다름없이 괴로웠다. 그토록 오랫동안 가정생활을 꿈꿔 왔고, 스스로 그럴 만큼 무르익었다고 느꼈으면서도, 여전히 결혼하지 못한 데다 결혼과는 그 어느 때보다 멀어져 있었기에 그는 마음이 편할 수 없었다. 자기 나이쯤 되면 혼자 지내는 것은 좋지 않다는 것을, 주위 사람들이 다 그러하듯 그 자신도 뼈저리게 느끼고 있었다. 모스크바로 떠나기 전, 그는 즐겨 이야기를 나누던 순박한 목동 니콜라이에게 이렇게 말한 적이 있었다. \"그래, 니콜라이! 나는 장가갈 생각이야.\" 그러자 니콜라이는 추호도 의심할 여지가 없는 일이라는 듯 곧바로 대꾸했다. \"암요, 진작 그러셨어야죠, 콘스탄친 드미트리치.\" 하지만 이제 결혼은 그 어느 때보다 멀어져 버렸다. 그 자리는 이미 채워졌고, 자기가 아는 어떤 처녀를 그 자리에 넣어 그려 보려 해도 도무지 불가능하다는 것을 그는 느꼈다. 게다가 거절당한 일과 그 일에서 자기가 했던 역할을 떠올리면 수치심으로 괴로웠다. 자기에게는 아무 잘못도 없다고 아무리 스스로를 타일러도, 그 기억은 다른 비슷한 굴욕적인 회상들이 그러하듯 그를 움찔하게 하고 낯을 붉히게 했다. 다른 모든 사람과 마찬가지로 그의 과거에도 스스로 나쁜 짓이라고 인정하는, 양심이 그를 괴롭혀야 마땅한 행동들이 있었다. 그러나 그 못된 행동들에 대한 기억은, 사소하지만 굴욕적인 그 회상들만큼은 그를 괴롭히지 못했다. 그런 상처들은 결코 아물지 않았다. 그리고 이제 그 기억들 틈에 거절당한 일과, 그날 저녁 다른 사람들 눈에 비쳤을 자신의 가련한 처지가 나란히 자리 잡았다. 그러나 시간과 일이 제 몫을 해냈다. 쓰라린 기억들은 그의 눈에는 하찮으나 실은 중요한 시골 생활의 자질구레한 사건들에 점점 덮여 갔다. 한 주 한 주가 지날수록 그는 키티 생각을 덜 하게 되었다. 그는 그녀가 결혼했다거나 곧 결혼한다는 소식이 오기를 안달하며 기다렸는데, 그런 소식이 이를 뽑는 것처럼 자기를 완전히 낫게 해 주리라 바랐던 것이다.\n\n그러는 동안 봄이 찾아왔다. 봄 특유의 지체나 변덕도 없이, 아름답고 다정하게—식물과 짐승과 사람이 다 함께 기뻐하는, 그 드문 봄 중 하나였다. 이 사랑스러운 봄은 레빈을 한층 더 들뜨게 했고, 자신의 과거를 모두 단념하고 외로운 삶을 굳건하고 독립적으로 세워 나가겠다는 결심을 더욱 단단하게 해 주었다. 시골로 돌아오며 세웠던 계획 중 많은 것이 실행되지 못했지만, 그래도 그의 가장 중요한 결심—순결을 지키겠다는 결심만은 지켜졌다. 그는 타락한 뒤 늘 자신을 괴롭히던 그 수치심에서 자유로웠고, 누구의 얼굴이든 똑바로 마주 볼 수 있었다. 2월에 그는 마리야 니콜라예브나에게서 형 니콜라이의 건강이 점점 나빠지는데도 충고를 들으려 하지 않는다는 편지를 받았고, 이 편지 때문에 모스크바의 형에게로 가서 의사를 만나고 외국의 온천지로 떠나도록 설득하는 데 성공했다. 형을 자극하지 않으면서 설득하고, 여행 경비까지 빌려주는 데 어찌나 잘 성공했던지, 그는 그 일에서만큼은 스스로에게 만족했다. 봄에 특별히 신경 써야 할 농사일과 독서에 더해, 레빈은 그해 겨울 농업에 관한 저술을 시작했는데, 그 구상의 핵심은 토양과 기후처럼 농업이라는 문제의 바꿀 수 없는 자료 가운데 하나로 땅에서 일하는 노동자의 성격을 고려에 넣는다는 것, 따라서 과학적 경작의 모든 원리를 단지 토양과 기후의 자료에서만이 아니라 토양과 기후, 그리고 노동자의 어떤 바꿀 수 없는 성격이라는 자료에서 끌어낸다는 것이었다. 그리하여 고독함에도 불구하고, 아니 어쩌면 고독한 덕분에 그의 삶은 더없이 충만했다. 다만 그는 아가피야 미하일로브나 말고 다른 누군가에게 자기가 떠올린 단편적인 생각들을 들려주고 싶은, 채워지지 않는 욕구로 이따금 괴로워할 뿐이었다. 사실 그녀와는 물리학이며 농업 이론, 특히 철학을 두고 적잖이 토론에 빠져들곤 했는데, 철학은 아가피야 미하일로브나가 가장 좋아하는 주제였다.\n\n봄은 더디게 피어났다. 지난 몇 주 동안은 줄곧 맑고 서리 내리는 날씨가 이어졌다. 낮에는 햇볕에 눈이 녹았지만, 밤이면 영하 7도까지 내려갔다. 눈 위에 얼음이 얼어붙어 단단해진 덕분에 사람들은 길이 아닌 아무 데로나 수레를 몰고 다녔다. 부활절은 눈 속에서 찾아왔다. 그러다 별안간, 부활절 다음 월요일에 따뜻한 바람이 일고 먹구름이 몰려들더니, 사흘 밤낮 동안 따뜻한 비가 들이치며 줄기차게 쏟아졌다. 목요일에는 바람이 잦아들었고, 짙은 잿빛 안개가 마치 자연 속에서 벌어지는 변화의 신비를 감추기라도 하듯 대지 위에 자욱이 드리웠다. 안개 너머에서는 물이 흐르고, 얼음이 쩍쩍 갈라지며 떠다니고, 흙탕물이 거품을 일으키며 빠르게 내달았다. 그리고 그다음 월요일 저녁, 안개가 걷히고 먹구름이 동그랗게 말린 작은 구름 조각들로 흩어지면서 하늘이 맑게 개고, 진짜 봄이 찾아왔다. 아침에 해가 눈부시게 떠올라 물 위를 덮고 있던 얇은 얼음을 순식간에 녹여 버렸고, 따뜻한 공기는 온통 되살아난 대지에서 피어오르는 아지랑이로 일렁였다. 묵은 풀은 한결 푸르게 보였고, 어린 풀은 가느다란 싹을 밀어 올렸다. 백당나무와 까치밥나무의 봉오리며 끈적한 자작나무 싹은 물이 올라 부풀었고, 버드나무에 점점이 박힌 황금빛 꽃 둘레로는 먹이를 찾아 나선 벌이 윙윙거렸다. 보드라운 초록빛 들판과 얼음에 덮인 그루터기 밭 위에서는 보이지 않는 종달새들이 지저귀었고, 웅덩이 물이 넘쳐흐른 저지대와 늪지 위에서는 댕기물떼새들이 구슬피 울었다. 두루미와 기러기들은 봄의 울음소리를 내며 하늘 높이 가로질러 날았다. 새 털이 아직 다 자라지 않아 여기저기 털이 빠진 가축들이 목초지에서 음매 울었고, 다리가 휜 새끼 양들은 음매거리는 어미 곁을 깡충깡충 뛰어다녔다. 날쌘 아이들은 맨발 자국이 가득 찍힌, 말라 가는 오솔길을 뛰어다녔다. 연못가에서는 빨래하는 아낙들의 즐거운 수다 소리가, 마당에서는 농부들이 쟁기와 써레를 손보는 도끼질 소리가 울려 퍼졌다. 진짜 봄이 찾아왔다.\n\n# Chapter 13\n\n레빈은 큰 장화를 신었고, 모피 외투 대신에 처음으로 천 재킷을 걸친 채, 햇빛에 반짝여 눈이 부신 시냇물 줄기를 건너뛰고, 어느 순간엔 얼음을, 다음 순간엔 끈적한 진창을 밟으며 자기 농장을 둘러보러 나갔다.\n\n봄은 계획과 구상의 시절이다. 농장 마당으로 나서면서, 부풀어 오른 봉오리 속에 갇힌 어린 싹과 가지가 어떤 모양을 띠게 될지 알지 못하는 봄날의 나무처럼, 레빈도 자기에게 그토록 소중한 농사일에서 이제 어떤 일에 착수하게 될지 좀처럼 알 수 없었다. 하지만 그는 자기가 더없이 훌륭한 계획과 구상으로 가득 차 있음을 느꼈다. 우선 그는 가축에게 갔다. 소들은 우리 밖 작은 방목장으로 풀려나 있었는데, 매끈한 옆구리는 벌써 새로 돋은 윤기 나는 봄 털로 반들거렸다. 소들은 햇볕을 쬐며 초원으로 나가게 해 달라고 음매 울었다. 레빈은 그 상태의 가장 사소한 데까지 속속들이 알고 있는 소들을 흐뭇하게 바라보다가, 소들은 초원으로 몰고 나가고 송아지들은 방목장에 들이라고 일렀다. 목동은 신이 나서 초원에 나갈 채비를 하러 달려갔다. 소 치는 처녀들은 치마를 걷어 올리고, 아직 햇볕에 그을지 않아 하얀 맨다리로 진창을 철벅거리며 달렸고, 손에 든 잔가지를 휘두르며 봄의 환희 속에 깡충거리는 송아지들을 뒤쫓았다.\n\n그해 태어난 어린 것들에 감탄하고 나서—올해 것들은 유난히 잘 자라서, 일찍 태어난 송아지는 농부의 암소만 했고, 파바의 딸은 생후 석 달 만에 한 살배기만큼이나 컸다—레빈은 여물통을 내오게 하고 방목장에서 송아지들에게 먹이를 주라고 일렀다. 그런데 알고 보니 겨울 동안 방목장을 쓰지 않은 탓에, 가을에 만들어 둔 울짱이 부서져 있었다. 그는 목수를 부르러 사람을 보냈는데, 목수는 그의 지시대로라면 탈곡기를 손보고 있어야 했다. 하지만 알고 보니 목수는 사순절 전에 고쳐 놓았어야 할 써레를 수리하고 있었다. 이것이 레빈에게는 몹시 짜증스러웠다. 그토록 여러 해 동안 온 힘을 다해 맞서 싸워 온 농사일의 그 끝없는 게으름을 또다시 마주하는 것이 짜증스러웠다. 그가 확인한 바로는, 겨울에는 쓸 데가 없던 울짱이 짐말 마구간으로 옮겨졌고, 거기서 부서졌는데, 그것은 송아지 먹이를 줄 용도로만 가볍게 만든 것이었기 때문이다. 게다가 겨울 동안 점검하고 수리하라고 지시했고 바로 그 목적으로 목수 셋을 고용했던 써레와 모든 농기구가 손질되지 않았으며, 들판에서 써레질을 하고 있어야 할 때에 써레를 수리하고 있다는 것도 분명히 드러났다. 레빈은 관리인을 부르러 사람을 보냈으나, 곧장 직접 그를 찾으러 나섰다. 그날 다른 모두가 그렇듯 온통 환한 얼굴로, 아스트라한 모피로 가장자리를 두른 양가죽 외투를 입은 관리인이, 손에서 짚오라기를 비비 꼬며 헛간에서 나왔다.\n\n\"왜 목수가 탈곡기를 손보고 있지 않은 거지?\"\n\n\"아, 어제 말씀드리려 했는데요, 써레를 수리해야 합니다. 이제 들판에서 일할 때가 됐으니까요.\"\n\n\"그럼 겨울 동안에는 뭘 한 거야?\"\n\n\"그런데 목수가 왜 필요하셨던 겁니까?\"\n\n\"송아지 방목장 울짱은 어디 있나?\"\n\n\"준비해 두라고 일러 놨습니다. 그 농부들을 어쩌겠습니까!\" 관리인이 손을 내저으며 말했다.\n\n\"그 농부들이 아니라 이 관리인이 문제지!\" 레빈이 화가 치밀어 말했다. \"아니, 그럼 내가 자네를 뭐 하러 두고 있는 건가?\" 그가 소리쳤다. 하지만 이래 봤자 사태에 도움이 되지 않으리라는 생각이 들어, 그는 말을 하다 말고 뚝 멈추고는 그저 한숨만 내쉬었다. \"자, 어떤가? 파종을 시작할 수 있겠나?\" 그가 잠시 뒤에 물었다.\n\n\"투르킨 너머는 내일이나 모레쯤 시작할 수 있겠습니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 클로버는?\"\n\n\"바실리와 미시카를 보냈습니다. 지금 파종하고 있어요. 다만 끝까지 해낼 수 있을지는 모르겠습니다. 워낙 질척거려서요.\"\n\n\"몇 에이커나 되지?\"\n\n\"왜 전부 파종하지 않는 겁니까?\" 레빈이 외쳤다.\n\n마흔다섯 에이커 전부가 아니라 겨우 열다섯 에이커에만 클로버를 파종하고 있다는 것이 그에게는 더욱더 짜증스러웠다. 책에서나 자기 경험으로나 그가 알기로, 클로버는 가능한 한 일찍, 거의 눈 속에서 파종하지 않으면 결코 잘 자라지 않았다. 그런데도 레빈은 도무지 이 일을 제대로 해낼 수가 없었다.\n\n\"보낼 사람이 없습니다. 이런 농부들을 어쩌겠습니까? 셋은 나타나지도 않았어요. 게다가 세묜이라는 작자도 있고요……\"\n\n\"그럼 지붕 이는 데서 사람을 좀 빼 왔어야지.\"\n\n\"이미 그렇게 했습니다, 지금.\"\n\n\"그럼 농부들은 어디 있나?\"\n\n\"다섯은 콤포트를 만들고 있고\"(이는 퇴비를 뜻했다), \"넷은 곰팡이가 슬까 봐 귀리를 옮기고 있습니다, 콘스탄친 드미트리치.\"\n\n곰팡이가 슨다는 말이 곧 자기의 영국산 종자 귀리가 이미 못 쓰게 되었다는 뜻임을 레빈은 너무도 잘 알았다. 또다시 그들은 그가 지시한 대로 하지 않은 것이다.\n\n\"아니, 내가 사순절 동안에 환기관을 설치하라고 했잖나,\" 그가 소리쳤다.\n\n\"너무 걱정 마십시오. 제때에 다 해 놓겠습니다.\"\n\n레빈은 화가 나서 손을 내젓고는 귀리를 살펴보러 곳간으로 들어갔다가, 다시 마구간으로 향했다. 귀리는 아직 상하지 않았다. 하지만 농부들은 그냥 아래쪽 곳간으로 미끄러뜨려 내려보내면 될 것을 삽으로 귀리를 퍼 나르고 있었다. 그것을 미끄러뜨려 내려보내도록 조처하고, 거기서 일꾼 둘을 빼내 클로버 파종에 보낸 뒤, 레빈은 관리인에 대한 짜증을 가라앉혔다. 사실 너무도 화창한 날이라 화를 내고 있을 수가 없었다.\n\n\"이그나트!\" 그는 소매를 걷어 올리고 마차 바퀴를 닦고 있던 마부를 불렀다. \"안장을 얹어 다오……\"\n\n\"음, 콜피크로 하지.\"\n\n\"예, 나리.\"\n\n말에 안장을 얹는 동안, 레빈은 눈에 띄는 곳에서 어슬렁대던 관리인을 화해하려는 마음에 다시 불러서는, 앞으로 있을 봄철 작업과 농장에 대한 자기 계획을 이야기하기 시작했다.\n\n이른 풀베기 전에 모든 일을 끝내려면 짐수레로 거름을 더 일찍 실어 나르기 시작해야 했다. 그리고 멀리 떨어진 밭을 묵힌 채로 익게 하려면 그곳 갈이질을 쉬지 않고 진행해야 했다. 풀베기는 절반 소작으로 하지 말고 전부 품삯 일꾼을 써서 해야 했다. 관리인은 주의 깊게 들었고, 분명 고용주의 구상에 찬동하려고 애를 썼다. 하지만 그는 여전히 레빈이 너무도 잘 아는, 늘 그를 짜증나게 하던 그 표정을, 가망 없음과 낙담의 표정을 짓고 있었다. 그 표정은 이렇게 말하고 있었다. \"다 좋은 말씀입니다만, 하느님 뜻대로 되는 거죠.\"\n\n이 어조만큼 레빈을 무참하게 만드는 것은 없었다. 하지만 그것은 그가 거느려 본 모든 관리인에게 공통된 어조였다. 그들 모두가 그의 계획에 대해 그런 태도를 취해 왔고, 그래서 이제 그는 그것에 화가 나지는 않았지만 무참한 기분이 들었으며, 끊임없이 자기에게 맞서 늘어선, 보아하니 근원적인 듯한 그 힘, \"하느님 뜻대로\"라는 말 외에는 달리 표현할 길이 없는 그 힘에 맞서 싸워야겠다는 마음이 한층 더 끓어올랐다.\n\n\"우리가 해낼 수 있다면 말입니다, 콘스탄친 드미트리치,\" 관리인이 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 대체 왜 못 해낸단 말인가?\"\n\n\"일꾼이 열다섯은 더 있어야 합니다, 꼭이요. 그런데 나타나질 않아요. 오늘 여기 몇 명 왔었는데 여름 한 철에 칠십 루블을 달라더군요.\"\n\n레빈은 잠자코 있었다. 또다시 그는 그 맞서는 힘과 정면으로 마주하게 되었다. 아무리 애를 써도 적당한 돈으로는 마흔 명 이상은—어쩌면 서른일곱이나 서른여덟—일꾼을 고용할 수 없다는 것을 그는 알고 있었다. 마흔 명쯤 받아들였고, 더는 없었다. 하지만 그래도 그는 그것에 맞서 발버둥치지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n\"수리로, 체피로프카로 사람을 보내게. 그래도 오지 않으면 우리가 찾아 나서야지.\"\n\n\"아, 보내고말고요, 물론,\" 바실리 표도로비치가 풀이 죽어 말했다. \"하지만 말들도 문제입니다, 그놈들도 별 쓸모가 없어요.\"\n\n\"몇 마리 더 들이지. 물론 나도 잘 알아,\" 레빈이 웃으며 덧붙였다. \"자네는 늘 되도록 적게, 되도록 질 낮은 걸로 때우려 들지. 하지만 올해는 자네 마음대로 하게 두지 않겠네. 모든 걸 내가 직접 챙길 거야.\"\n\n\"아니, 지금도 별로 쉬지도 못하시는 것 같은데요. 주인 나리 눈앞에서 일하면 저희야 기운이 나지요……\"\n\n\"그래, 자작나무 골짜기 너머에서 클로버를 파종하고 있다고 했지? 가서 한번 봐야겠어,\" 그는 마부가 끌고 온 작은 적갈색 조랑말 콜피크에 올라타며 말했다.\n\n\"시냇물을 건너실 수가 없습니다, 콘스탄친 드미트리치,\" 마부가 외쳤다.\n\n\"괜찮아, 숲을 지나서 가지.\"\n\n그리하여 레빈은 농장 마당의 진창을 지나 문으로, 그리고 탁 트인 들판으로 말을 몰고 나갔다. 그 좋은 작은 말은 오랜 휴식 끝에 씩씩하게 발을 내디뎠고, 물웅덩이를 만날 때마다 콧김을 내뿜으며 마치 길을 일러 달라는 듯했다. 가축 우리와 농장 마당에서 행복을 느꼈다면, 탁 트인 들판에서는 더한층 행복했다. 그 좋은 작은 조랑말의 느릿느릿한 걸음에 맞춰 리듬 있게 몸을 흔들며, 눈과 공기의 따스하면서도 신선한 향기를 들이마시며, 군데군데 아직 남아 녹아 가는 발자국으로 뒤덮인 채 부스러져 사라져 가는 눈 위로 자기 숲을 지나 말을 몰면서, 그는 껍질에 이끼가 되살아나고 가지마다 봉오리가 부풀어 오른 나무 한 그루 한 그루마다 기뻐했다. 숲에서 나오자, 그의 앞에 펼쳐진 광활한 평원에서 풀밭들이 끊김 없는 초록빛 양탄자처럼 뻗어 있었는데, 헐벗은 자리나 늪 하나 없이, 다만 움푹 팬 곳마다 녹아 가는 눈 무더기가 군데군데 점점이 박혀 있을 뿐이었다. 그는 농부들의 말과 망아지들이 자기의 어린 풀을 짓밟는 광경을 보고도(그는 마침 마주친 농부에게 그것들을 몰아내라고 일렀다), 또 길에서 마주쳐 \"이봐, 이파트, 곧 파종을 시작할 수 있겠나?\" 하고 물었던 농부 이파트의 비꼬는 듯한 멍청한 대답을 듣고도 기분이 상하지 않았다. \"갈이질부터 끝내야지요, 콘스탄친 드미트리치,\" 하고 이파트는 대답했다. 말을 몰아 멀리 갈수록 그는 더 행복해졌고, 땅에 대한 계획이 줄줄이 떠올랐는데 하나하나가 앞의 것보다 더 나았다. 남쪽 경계를 따라 모든 밭에 울타리를 둘러 그 아래에 눈이 쌓이지 않게 하고, 밭을 경작지 여섯과 목초지 및 건초지 셋으로 나누고, 영지의 먼 끝자락에 가축 마당을 짓고, 못을 파고, 땅에 거름을 주는 방편으로 가축용 이동식 우리를 만드는 것이었다. 그러고 나서 밀 팔백 에이커, 감자 삼백 에이커, 클로버 사백 에이커를 두되, 단 한 에이커도 지력을 다 소진하지 않게 하는 것이었다.\n\n그런 꿈에 잠겨, 어린 작물을 짓밟지 않으려고 말을 울타리 가까이로 조심스레 몰며, 그는 클로버를 파종하러 보낸 일꾼들에게 다가갔다. 씨앗을 실은 짐수레가 밭 가장자리가 아니라 한가운데에 서 있었고, 겨울 밀이 바퀴에 뜯겨 나가고 말발굽에 짓밟혀 있었다. 두 일꾼은 울타리에 앉아, 아마도 함께 파이프 담배를 피우고 있는 듯했다. 씨앗과 섞인 짐수레 속의 흙은 가루로 으깨진 것이 아니라 딱딱하게 굳었거나 흙덩이로 뭉쳐 들러붙어 있었다. 주인을 보자 일꾼 바실리는 짐수레 쪽으로 향했고, 미시카는 파종 일을 시작했다. 이것은 마땅한 모습이 아니었으나, 레빈은 일꾼들에게는 좀처럼 화를 내지 않았다. 바실리가 다가오자, 레빈은 말을 울타리 쪽으로 끌고 가라고 일렀다.\n\n\"괜찮습니다, 나리, 다시 돋아날 겁니다,\" 바실리가 대꾸했다.\n\n\"제발 말대꾸하지 말고,\" 레빈이 말했다. \"시키는 대로 하게.\"\n\n\"예, 나리,\" 바실리는 대답하며 말 머리를 잡았다. \"이게 무슨 파종입니까, 콘스탄친 드미트리치,\" 그가 머뭇거리며 말했다. \"최고지요. 다만 돌아다니는 게 일입니다요! 신발에 흙을 한 짐씩 끌고 다녀야 하니까요.\"\n\n\"왜 자네들은 체로 거르지도 않은 흙을 쓰고 있나?\" 레빈이 말했다.\n\n\"뭐, 손으로 부숴 가며 합니다,\" 바실리는 씨앗을 한 줌 집어 들고 손바닥에 흙을 비벼 굴리며 대답했다.\n\n체로 거르지 않은 흙으로 짐수레를 채운 것은 바실리의 잘못이 아니었지만, 그래도 짜증스러웠다.\n\n레빈은 이미 여러 번, 화를 가라앉히고 어두워 보이던 모든 것을 다시 제대로 돌려놓는, 자기가 아는 한 가지 방법을 시도해 본 적이 있었는데, 이번에도 그 방법을 시도했다. 그는 미시카가 두 발마다 들러붙은 커다란 흙덩이를 휘저으며 성큼성큼 걸어가는 모습을 지켜보다가, 말에서 내려 바실리에게서 체를 받아 들고는 직접 파종을 시작했다.\n\n\"어디까지 했지?\"\n\n바실리가 발로 표시한 자리를 가리켰고, 레빈은 할 수 있는 한 최선을 다해 앞으로 나아가며 씨앗을 땅에 뿌렸다. 걷는 것이 마치 늪 위를 걷는 것만큼이나 힘들어서, 한 고랑을 끝낼 무렵 레빈은 온몸이 후끈 달아올랐고, 그는 멈춰 서서 바실리에게 체를 넘겨주었다.\n\n\"자, 나리, 여름이 오거든 이 고랑들 때문에 저를 나무라지나 마십시오,\" 바실리가 말했다.\n\n\"응?\" 레빈은 벌써 자기 방법의 효과를 느끼며 명랑하게 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 여름철이 되면 보실 겁니다. 다른 모습이 될 테니까요. 보십시오, 제가 지난봄에 파종한 데를요. 제가 얼마나 공을 들였는지! 저는 최선을 다합니다, 콘스탄친 드미트리치, 아시겠지만, 제 친아버지 일이라도 되는 양 말입니다. 저는 엉성한 일을 좋아하지 않고, 남이 그러는 것도 두고 보지 못합니다. 주인 나리께 좋은 게 저희에게도 좋은 거지요. 저 멀리를 보면요, 지금,\" 바실리가 손가락으로 가리키며 말했다. \"마음이 절로 흐뭇해집니다.\"\n\n\"멋진 봄이군, 바실리.\"\n\n\"아니, 이런 봄은 노인네들도 그 비슷한 걸 기억하지 못합니다. 제가 고향 집에 올라갔었는데요, 거기 한 노인이 밀도 파종했더군요, 한 에이커쯤요. 그 양반 말이, 호밀하고 구별이 안 될 정도라더군요.\"\n\n\"밀을 파종한 지 오래됐나?\"\n\n\"아니, 나리, 재작년에 나리께서 가르쳐 주셨잖습니까. 저한테 두 되를 주셨지요. 여덟 부셸쯤 팔고 사분의 일 에이커를 파종했습니다.\"\n\n\"그래, 흙덩이 잘 부수도록 하고,\" 레빈이 자기 말 쪽으로 가며 말했다. \"미시카도 잘 지켜보고. 작황이 좋으면 에이커마다 반 루블씩 주겠네.\"\n\n\"황송합니다. 지금도 저희는 아주 흡족합니다, 나리.\"\n\n레빈은 말에 올라 작년에 클로버를 심었던 밭과, 봄 곡식을 위해 갈이질을 해 둔 밭이 있는 쪽으로 말을 몰았다.\n\n그루터기 사이로 돋아 오르는 클로버 작황은 훌륭했다. 그것은 모든 것을 견뎌 내고, 작년 밀의 부러진 줄기들 사이로 선명한 초록빛을 띠며 솟아 있었다. 말은 발목까지 푹푹 빠졌고, 반쯤 녹은 땅에서 발굽 하나하나를 쪽쪽 소리를 내며 뽑아 올렸다. 갈아 둔 밭 위로는 말을 타고 가는 것이 도무지 불가능했다. 말은 얼음이 있는 곳에서나 겨우 발을 디딜 수 있었고, 녹아 가는 고랑에서는 걸음마다 깊이 빠져들었다. 갈아 둔 밭은 더없이 좋은 상태였다. 이틀이면 써레질과 파종을 하기에 알맞을 것이었다. 모든 것이 더할 나위 없었고, 모든 것이 흐뭇했다. 레빈은 물이 빠졌기를 바라며 시냇물을 건너 되돌아갔다. 그리고 실제로 건너는 데 성공했고, 오리 두 마리를 놀라게 했다. \"도요새도 분명 있겠지,\" 하고 그는 생각했고, 마침 집으로 돌아가는 길목에 이르렀을 때 산지기를 만났는데, 그가 도요새에 관한 그의 짐작을 확인해 주었다.\n\n레빈은 저녁 식사를 할 시간과 저녁 사냥을 위해 총을 손볼 시간을 벌려고, 빠른 걸음으로 집으로 향했다.\n\n# Chapter 14\n\n더없이 행복한 기분으로 집을 향해 말을 달려가던 레빈은 저택의 정문 옆에서 종이 울리는 소리를 들었다.\n\n\"그래, 기차역에서 온 누군가야.\" 그는 생각했다. \"모스크바발 기차로 여기 도착할 시간이군…… 누구일까? 혹시 형 니콜라이가 아닐까? 형은 말했지. '온천에 갈지도 모르고, 어쩌면 네게 내려갈지도 모른다'고 말이야.\" 그는 처음 한순간 형 니콜라이가 나타나 자신의 행복한 봄날의 기분을 흐트러뜨릴까 봐 낙담하고 언짢았다. 그러나 곧 그런 감정이 부끄러워졌고, 즉시 마음의 두 팔을 활짝 벌리듯 했으며, 부드러워진 기쁨과 기대의 감정으로 이제는 그것이 형이기를 온 마음으로 바랐다. 그는 말에 박차를 가했고, 아카시아나무 뒤편에서 빠져나오자 기차역에서 빌려 온 삼두마차 썰매와 모피 외투를 입은 신사가 보였다. 형은 아니었다. \"오, 조금이라도 이야기를 나눌 수 있는 괜찮은 사람이기만 하다면!\" 그는 생각했다.\n\n\"아!\" 레빈이 두 손을 번쩍 치켜들며 기쁘게 소리쳤다. \"이렇게 반가운 손님이라니! 아, 자넬 만나니 정말 기쁘군!\" 그는 스테판 아르카지치를 알아보고는 외쳤다.\n\n\"그녀가 결혼했는지, 아니면 언제 결혼할 예정인지 확실히 알아낼 수 있겠어.\" 그는 생각했다. 그리고 그 감미로운 봄날에 그는 그녀를 떠올려도 전혀 마음이 아프지 않다는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n\"이거, 날 만날 줄 몰랐지, 응?\" 콧등이며 뺨이며 눈썹에 진흙이 튄 채로, 그러나 건강과 좋은 기분으로 환하게 빛나며 썰매에서 내린 스테판 아르카지치가 말했다. \"첫째로는 자넬 보러 왔고,\" 그는 레빈을 끌어안고 입을 맞추며 말했다. \"둘째로는 사냥을 좀 하려고, 셋째로는 예르구쇼보의 숲을 팔려고 왔지.\"\n\n\"반가워! 이거 정말 멋진 봄이로군! 대체 썰매를 타고 어떻게 온 거야?\"\n\n\"수레로 왔으면 더 고생했을 겁니다요, 콘스탄틴 드미트리치.\" 그를 알아본 마부가 대답했다.\n\n\"그래, 정말이지 자넬 만나서 너무너무 기뻐.\" 레빈은 어린아이 같은 진심 어린 기쁨의 미소를 지으며 말했다.\n\n레빈은 친구를 손님방으로 안내했고, 스테판 아르카지치의 짐도 그리로 옮겨졌다—가방 하나, 케이스에 든 총 한 자루, 시가를 넣는 작은 가방 하나였다. 그를 거기 남겨 두어 씻고 옷을 갈아입게 하고, 레빈은 경작과 클로버에 대해 의논하러 사무실로 갔다. 늘 집안의 체면을 무척 염려하는 아가피야 미하일로브나가 저녁 식사에 관해 물어보려고 현관에서 그를 맞았다.\n\n\"당신 좋을 대로 하세요, 다만 되도록 빨리 차려 주시고요.\" 그는 이렇게 말하고 관리인에게 갔다.\n\n그가 돌아오자, 말끔히 씻고 머리를 빗은 스테판 아르카지치가 환한 미소를 지으며 자기 방에서 나왔고, 두 사람은 함께 위층으로 올라갔다.\n\n\"이야, 자네한테 빠져나올 수 있어서 정말 기뻐! 이제야 자네가 늘 여기 파묻혀 골몰하는 그 신비로운 일이 뭔지 알 것 같군. 아니, 정말이지 자네가 부러워. 이 집이며, 이 모든 게 얼마나 멋진지! 이렇게 밝고, 이렇게 경쾌하다니!\" 스테판 아르카지치는 늘 그날처럼 봄날의 화창한 날씨인 것은 아니라는 사실을 잊은 채 말했다. \"그리고 자네 가정부도 정말 매력적이야! 앞치마를 두른 예쁜 하녀라면 어쩌면 더 흐뭇할지도 모르지만, 자네의 엄격한 수도원식 살림살이에는 이쪽이 아주 잘 어울려.\"\n\n스테판 아르카지치는 그에게 흥미로운 소식을 많이 들려주었는데, 그중에서도 레빈에게 특히 흥미로웠던 것은 형 세르게이 이바노비치가 여름에 그를 찾아올 작정이라는 소식이었다.\n\n스테판 아르카지치는 키티와 셰르바츠키 집안에 관해서는 한마디도 하지 않았다. 그저 아내가 안부를 전한다고만 했을 뿐이다. 레빈은 그의 세심한 배려에 고마웠고 그가 찾아와 준 것이 무척 기뻤다. 홀로 지낼 때면 늘 그렇듯이, 그의 안에는 주위 사람들에게 전할 수 없는 숱한 생각과 감정이 쌓여 있었다. 그래서 이제 그는 스테판 아르카지치에게 봄에 대한 자신의 시적인 기쁨이며, 토지를 두고 겪은 실패와 계획이며, 읽어 온 책들에 대한 생각과 비판이며, 자신이 쓰려는 책에 대한 구상을 쏟아 냈는데, 그 책의 바탕은 사실—본인은 자각하지 못했지만—농업에 관한 모든 옛 서적에 대한 비판이었다. 늘 매력적이고 아주 사소한 언급만으로도 모든 것을 이해하는 스테판 아르카지치는 이번 방문에서 유독 매력적이었고, 레빈은 그에게서 어떤 각별한 다정함이랄까, 그리고 자신을 우쭐하게 만드는 새로운 존경의 어조를 느꼈다.\n\n저녁 식사를 특별히 잘 차리려던 아가피야 미하일로브나와 요리사의 노력은, 굶주린 두 친구가 전채 요리에 달려들어 버터 바른 빵이며 소금에 절인 거위 고기며 소금에 절인 버섯을 잔뜩 먹어 치우고, 마침내 레빈이 요리사가 손님에게 특별히 강한 인상을 주려고 준비했던 작은 파이도 곁들이지 말고 수프부터 내오라고 시키는 것으로 끝나 버렸다. 그러나 스테판 아르카지치는 전혀 다른 식사에 익숙했음에도 모든 것이 훌륭하다고 여겼다. 약초 보드카며, 빵이며, 버터며, 무엇보다 소금에 절인 거위 고기와 버섯이며, 쐐기풀 수프며, 흰 소스를 끼얹은 닭고기며, 크림산 백포도주까지—모든 것이 더없이 훌륭하고 맛있었다.\n\n\"근사해, 근사하군!\" 그는 구운 고기를 먹은 뒤 굵은 시가에 불을 붙이며 말했다. \"자네한테 오니, 마치 증기선의 소음과 흔들림을 뒤로하고 평화로운 기슭에 닿은 기분이야. 그러니까 자네 주장은, 노동자 자신이 연구 대상이며 농업에서 방법을 선택하는 기준이 된다는 거지. 물론 나야 무지한 외부인이지만, 이론과 그 적용이 노동자에게도 영향을 미치리라고 생각하는데.\"\n\n\"그래, 하지만 잠깐만. 난 정치경제학을 이야기하는 게 아니라 농업의 과학을 이야기하는 거야. 그건 자연과학 같아야 하고, 주어진 현상과 노동자를 그의 경제적, 민족지학적인……\"\n\n바로 그 순간 아가피야 미하일로브나가 잼을 들고 들어왔다.\n\n\"오, 아가피야 미하일로브나,\" 스테판 아르카지치가 통통한 손가락 끝에 입을 맞추며 말했다. \"그 거위 고기는 또 어떻고, 그 약초 보드카는 또 어떻소!…… 어때, 슬슬 나설 시간 아닌가, 코스챠?\" 그가 덧붙였다.\n\n레빈은 창밖으로 숲의 헐벗은 나무 꼭대기 너머로 지고 있는 해를 바라보았다.\n\n\"그래, 갈 시간이야.\" 그가 말했다. \"쿠지마, 마차를 준비해 둬.\" 그러고는 아래층으로 달려 내려갔다.\n\n스테판 아르카지치는 아래층으로 내려가, 옻칠한 자신의 총 케이스에서 천 덮개를 손수 조심스럽게 벗기고는, 그것을 열어 값비싸고 신식인 자기 총을 준비하기 시작했다. 이미 두둑한 팁 냄새를 맡은 쿠지마는 스테판 아르카지치의 곁을 한시도 떠나지 않고 그에게 양말과 장화를 신겨 주었는데, 스테판 아르카지치는 기꺼이 그 일을 그에게 맡겼다.\n\n\"코스챠, 만약 상인 랴비닌이 오거든…… 오늘 오라고 일러 뒀거든, 안으로 들여서 날 기다리게 하라고 일러 두게……\"\n\n\"아니, 숲을 랴비닌한테 판다는 말인가?\"\n\n\"그래. 자네 그를 아나?\"\n\n\"물론 알지. 그와 거래해야 했던 적이 있어. '확실하고도 결정적으로' 말이야.\"\n\n스테판 아르카지치가 웃었다. \"확실하고도 결정적으로\"는 그 상인이 즐겨 쓰는 말이었다.\n\n\"그래, 그 사람 말투가 정말 우습기 짝이 없지. 이 녀석은 제 주인이 어디 가는지 다 안다니까!\" 그는 끙끙대며 레빈의 손이며 장화며 총을 핥아 대며 그의 주위를 맴도는 라스카를 쓰다듬으며 덧붙였다.\n\n그들이 나갔을 때 마차는 이미 현관 계단 앞에 와 있었다.\n\n\"마차를 돌려 오라고 일러 뒀어. 아니면 걸어가는 게 낫겠나?\"\n\n\"아니, 타고 가는 게 낫겠어.\" 스테판 아르카지치는 마차에 오르며 말했다. 그는 자리에 앉아 호랑이 가죽 무릎덮개를 몸에 두르고 시가에 불을 붙였다. \"자넨 어째서 담배를 안 피우나? 시가란 게 말이야, 딱히 즐거움이라고 할 수는 없어도, 즐거움의 정점이자 겉으로 드러난 표시 같은 거지. 자, 이게 바로 인생이야! 얼마나 근사한가! 나도 이렇게 살고 싶다니까!\"\n\n\"아니, 누가 자넬 말리기라도 하나?\" 레빈이 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"아니야, 자넨 운 좋은 사람이야! 자넨 좋아하는 걸 다 가졌잖아. 말을 좋아하면—말이 있고, 개를 좋아하면—개가 있고, 사냥을 좋아하면—사냥이 있고, 농사를 좋아하면—농사가 있고.\"\n\n\"아마도 내가 가진 것에 기뻐하고, 갖지 못한 것을 안달하지 않기 때문이겠지.\" 레빈은 키티를 떠올리며 말했다.\n\n스테판 아르카지치는 그 말뜻을 알아채고 그를 바라보았으나, 아무 말도 하지 않았다.\n\n레빈은 오블론스키가 한결같은 재치로 자신이 셰르바츠키 집안에 관한 이야기를 두려워한다는 것을 눈치채고 그들에 대해 아무 말도 하지 않는 것이 고마웠다. 그러나 이제 레빈은 무엇이 그토록 친구를 괴롭히고 있는지 알아내고 싶었지만, 차마 입을 뗄 용기가 나지 않았다.\n\n\"자, 자네 사정은 어떤지 이야기해 보게.\" 레빈은 자기 자신만 생각하는 것이 좋지 않다는 생각이 들어 말했다.\n\n스테판 아르카지치의 눈이 즐겁게 반짝였다.\n\n\"자넨 인정하지 않지, 나도 알아. 빵을 배급받았으면서도 새 롤빵을 좋아할 수 있다는 걸 말이야—자네 생각으론 그게 죄겠지. 하지만 난 사랑이 없는 삶은 삶으로 치지 않아.\" 그는 레빈의 질문을 자기 식대로 받아들이며 말했다. \"어쩌겠나? 난 그렇게 생겨 먹은걸. 게다가 정말이지 누구에게 끼치는 해는 그토록 적고, 자기 자신은 그토록 큰 즐거움을 얻는데……\"\n\n\"뭐라고! 그럼 뭔가 새로운 일이 있다는 건가?\" 레빈이 물었다.\n\n\"그렇다네, 이 친구야, 있고말고! 자, 봐, 자네도 오시안의 여인들 같은 부류를 알 거야…… 꿈속에서나 보는 그런 여자들 말이야…… 그런데 그런 여자들을 이따금 현실에서도 만날 수 있거든…… 그리고 그런 여자들은 무서워. 여자란, 자네도 알겠지만, 아무리 연구해도 언제나 완전히 새로운 그런 대상이란 말이야.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 차라리 연구하지 않는 편이 낫겠군.\"\n\n\"아니야. 어느 수학자가 말했지, 즐거움은 진리를 찾는 데 있지 진리를 발견하는 데 있는 게 아니라고.\"\n\n레빈은 잠자코 듣고 있었지만, 아무리 애를 써도 친구의 감정 속으로 조금도 들어갈 수 없었고, 그런 여자들을 연구하는 데서 오는 그 정서와 매력을 이해할 수 없었다.\n\n# Chapter 15\n\n총사냥을 하기로 정한 자리는 시냇물 바로 위편, 작은 사시나무 덤불 속이었다. 덤불에 다다르자 레빈은 마차에서 내려 오블론스키를 데리고, 이미 눈이 말끔히 녹은 이끼 낀 늪지대 빈터 한구석으로 안내했다. 그러고는 자기는 반대편의 한 쌍으로 갈라진 자작나무 곁으로 돌아가, 죽은 아래쪽 가지가 두 갈래로 벌어진 곳에 총을 기대 세우고, 두툼한 외투를 벗어 다시 허리띠를 졸라매고는, 팔이 자유롭게 움직이는지 휘둘러 보았다.\n\n그들을 따라온 잿빛 늙은 라스카가 그의 맞은편에 조심스레 앉아 귀를 쫑긋 세웠다. 해가 울창한 숲 뒤로 지고 있었고, 노을빛 속에서 사시나무 덤불 사이사이에 흩어져 선 자작나무들이 늘어진 잔가지와 거의 터질 듯 부풀어 오른 눈을 단 채 또렷이 드러났다.\n\n아직 눈이 남아 있는 덤불 가장 깊숙한 곳에서는, 좁다랗게 굽이쳐 흘러내리는 가느다란 물줄기의 희미한 소리가 들려왔다. 작은 새들이 지저귀며, 이따금 나무에서 나무로 푸드덕 옮겨 다녔다.\n\n완전한 정적이 잠시 멈출 때마다, 흙이 녹고 풀이 돋아나며 들썩이는 지난해 묵은 잎들의 바스락거리는 소리가 들려왔다.\n\n'세상에! 풀이 자라는 소리가 들리고 그게 보이다니!' 레빈은 어린 풀잎 곁에서 젖은 잿빛 사시나무 잎이 움직이는 것을 보며 속으로 중얼거렸다. 그는 선 채로 귀를 기울이며, 때로는 젖은 이끼 낀 땅을 내려다보고, 때로는 온 신경을 곤두세워 듣고 있는 라스카를 보고, 때로는 그의 아래 비탈로 펼쳐진 헐벗은 나무 꼭대기들의 바다를 보고, 때로는 흰 구름 줄기로 뒤덮인 채 점점 어두워지는 하늘을 바라보았다.\n\n매 한 마리가 느릿느릿 날개를 휘저으며 저 멀리 숲 위를 높이 날아갔고, 또 한 마리가 똑같은 몸짓으로 같은 방향으로 날아가다 사라졌다. 새들이 덤불 속에서 점점 더 크게, 더 분주하게 지저귀었다. 멀지 않은 곳에서 부엉이가 울자, 라스카가 흠칫하더니 조심스레 몇 발짝 앞으로 나아가 고개를 한쪽으로 기울이고 골똘히 귀를 기울이기 시작했다. 시냇물 너머에서는 뻐꾸기 소리가 들려왔다. 뻐꾸기는 평소처럼 두 번 울고는, 쉰 듯한 급한 소리를 한 번 내뱉더니 뚝 그쳤다.\n\n\"세상에, 벌써 뻐꾸기가!\" 스테판 아르카지치가 덤불 뒤에서 나오며 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 들려.\" 레빈은 마지못해 자기 목소리로 정적을 깨뜨리며 대답했는데, 그 소리가 스스로도 듣기 거북했다. \"이제 날아오겠어!\"\n\n스테판 아르카지치의 모습이 다시 덤불 뒤로 사라졌고, 레빈에게는 성냥불의 환한 섬광에 이어 담뱃불의 붉은 빛과 푸른 연기만 보였다.\n\n\"찰칵! 찰칵!\" 스테판 아르카지치가 총의 공이치기를 당기는 소리가 났다.\n\n\"저 우는 소리는 뭐지?\" 오블론스키가 마치 망아지가 장난스레 높은 목소리로 히힝거리는 듯한, 길게 늘어지는 울음소리에 레빈의 주의를 돌리며 물었다.\n\n\"아, 자네 저것도 모르나? 산토끼야. 그건 그렇고 말은 그만! 들어 봐, 날아오고 있어!\" 레빈이 공이치기를 당기며 거의 비명을 지르듯 외쳤다.\n\n저 멀리서 날카로운 휘파람 소리가 들렸고, 사냥꾼에게 너무도 익숙한 바로 그 시간 간격에 맞춰 이 초쯤 뒤에 또 한 번, 그리고 세 번째 소리가 났으며, 세 번째 휘파람 뒤에는 쉰 듯한 목쉰 울음소리가 들려왔다.\n\n레빈은 오른쪽 왼쪽을 두루 둘러보았고, 바로 거기, 부드러운 사시나무 새싹들이 어수선하게 뒤엉킨 무더기 위쪽 어스레한 푸른 하늘을 배경으로, 자기 정면에서 날아오는 새를 보았다. 새는 곧장 그를 향해 날아오고 있었다. 무언가 질긴 천이 고르게 찢기는 듯한 목쉰 울음소리가 그의 귓전 가까이에서 울렸고, 새의 긴 부리와 목이 보였다. 그리고 레빈이 막 겨냥하는 바로 그 순간, 오블론스키가 서 있는 덤불 뒤에서 붉은 번개가 번쩍였다. 새는 화살처럼 떨어졌다가 다시 위로 솟구쳤다. 또 한 번 붉은 섬광과 명중하는 소리가 났고, 새는 공중에 떠 있으려는 듯 날개를 푸드덕거리다 멈추더니, 한순간 가만히 있다가 질척한 땅 위로 묵직하게 철퍼덕 떨어졌다.\n\n\"내가 놓친 건가?\" 연기 때문에 앞이 보이지 않는 스테판 아르카지치가 소리쳤다.\n\n\"여기 있어!\" 레빈이 라스카를 가리키며 말했다. 라스카는 한쪽 귀를 쫑긋 세우고 텁수룩한 꼬리 끝을 흔들며, 마치 그 즐거움을 늘이려는 듯 천천히 돌아왔고, 흡사 미소를 짓는 양 죽은 새를 주인에게 물어다 주었다. \"음, 자네가 명중시켜서 기쁘군.\" 레빈은 그렇게 말했지만, 동시에 자기는 도요새를 쏘아 맞히지 못했다는 부러움도 느꼈다.\n\n\"오른쪽 총신에서 나간 게 형편없는 사격이었어.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 총을 장전하며 대꾸했다. \"쉿... 날아온다!\"\n\n날카로운 휘파람 소리가 잇따라 빠르게 다시 들려왔다. 도요새 두 마리가 장난치며 서로 쫓고, 울지는 않고 휘파람만 불면서, 곧장 사냥꾼들의 머리 위로 날아들었다. 네 발의 총성이 울렸고, 도요새들은 제비처럼 공중에서 잽싸게 공중제비를 돌더니 시야에서 사라졌다.\n\n총사냥은 더없이 훌륭했다. 스테판 아르카지치가 두 마리를 더 쏘았고, 레빈도 두 마리를 잡았는데, 그중 한 마리는 찾지 못했다. 날이 어두워지기 시작했다. 밝고 은빛으로 빛나는 금성이 자작나무들 뒤편 서쪽 하늘 낮은 곳에서 부드러운 빛을 내뿜었고, 동쪽 높은 곳에서는 아르크투루스의 붉은 빛이 깜빡였다. 레빈은 머리 위로 큰곰자리의 별들을 알아보았다가 다시 놓쳤다. 도요새는 더 이상 날지 않았다. 하지만 레빈은 자작나무 가지 아래로 보이는 금성이 그 위로 떠오르고 큰곰자리의 별들이 완전히 또렷해질 때까지 조금 더 머물기로 마음먹었다. 금성은 가지 위로 떠올랐고, 손잡이가 달린 큰곰자리의 국자가 이제 짙푸른 하늘을 배경으로 또렷이 다 보였는데도, 그는 여전히 기다렸다.\n\n\"이제 집에 갈 때가 되지 않았나?\" 스테판 아르카지치가 말했다.\n\n이제 덤불 속은 아주 고요했고, 새 한 마리 움직이지 않았다.\n\n\"조금만 더 있세.\" 레빈이 대답했다.\n\n\"자네 좋을 대로.\"\n\n이제 그들은 서로 열다섯 걸음쯤 떨어져 서 있었다.\n\n\"스티바!\" 레빈이 불쑥 말했다. \"자네 처제가 벌써 결혼했는지, 아니면 언제 할 건지 어째서 내게 말해 주지 않는 거지?\"\n\n레빈은 어떤 대답이 나와도 자기 마음을 흔들지 못하리라 여길 만큼 단호하고 평온한 기분이었다. 하지만 스테판 아르카지치가 한 대답은 꿈에도 생각지 못한 것이었다.\n\n\"그 애는 결혼할 생각을 해 본 적도 없고, 지금도 그럴 생각이 없어. 다만 몸이 몹시 아파서 의사들이 외국으로 보냈지. 다들 그 애가 살지 못할까 봐 정말로 걱정하고 있어.\"\n\n\"뭐라고!\" 레빈이 외쳤다. \"몹시 아프다고? 어디가 아픈 건데? 어쩌다 그 애가...?\"\n\n그들이 이런 말을 주고받는 동안, 라스카는 귀를 쫑긋 세운 채 하늘을 올려다보고, 또 그들을 나무라듯 바라보고 있었다.\n\n'하필 이런 때에 이야기들을 하다니.' 라스카는 생각했다. '날고 있잖아... 저기, 그래, 맞아. 저것들이 놓치겠어.' 라스카는 생각했다.\n\n하지만 바로 그 순간 둘은 갑자기 귓전을 때리는 듯한 날카로운 휘파람 소리를 들었고, 둘 다 별안간 총을 움켜쥐었으며, 두 줄기 섬광이 번쩍이고 두 발의 총성이 똑같은 순간에 울렸다. 높이 날던 도요새가 즉시 날개를 접고 가녀린 새싹들을 휘어뜨리며 덤불 속으로 떨어졌다.\n\n\"멋지군! 동시에 맞혔어!\" 레빈이 외치며, 도요새를 찾으러 라스카와 함께 덤불 속으로 달려갔다.\n\n'아, 그래, 뭐가 그렇게 불쾌했더라?' 그는 의아해했다. '그래, 키티가 아프다지... 음, 어쩔 수 없지. 정말 안됐어.' 그는 생각했다.\n\n\"찾았구나! 영리한 녀석이지?\" 그는 라스카의 입에서 따뜻한 새를 받아 들어, 거의 가득 찬 사냥 자루에 챙겨 넣으며 말했다. \"잡았어, 스티바!\" 그가 소리쳤다.\n\n# Chapter 16\n\n집으로 돌아오는 길에 레빈은 키티의 병에 대해, 그리고 쉬체르바츠키 집안의 사정에 대해 시시콜콜 캐물었다. 인정하기는 부끄러웠지만, 그는 자기가 들은 이야기에 마음이 흡족했다. 아직 희망이 남아 있다는 사실이 기뻤고, 자신을 그토록 괴롭게 만든 그 여자가 지금 괴로워하고 있다는 사실은 더더욱 기뻤다. 하지만 스테판 아르카지치가 키티의 병의 원인을 이야기하면서 브론스키의 이름을 입에 올리자, 레빈은 그의 말을 잘라 버렸다.\n\n\"나는 그 집안일을 알 권리가 조금도 없고, 솔직히 말하면 관심도 없어.\"\n\n스테판 아르카지치는 레빈의 얼굴에서 자기가 너무도 잘 아는 그 순간적인 변화를 알아채고는, 거의 알아보기 힘들 만큼 희미하게 미소 지었다. 일 분 전만 해도 환하던 그 얼굴이 어느새 침울하게 흐려져 있었다.\n\n\"랴비닌과 숲에 관한 일은 다 매듭지었나?\" 레빈이 물었다.\n\n\"그래, 다 끝났어. 값을 아주 잘 받았지. 삼만 팔천. 당장 팔천을 받고, 나머지는 육 년에 걸쳐 받기로 했어. 그 일로 어찌나 오래 골치를 앓았는지 몰라. 그보다 더 쳐줄 사람은 아무도 없었거든.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 자네는 숲을 거저 내준 거나 다름없군.\" 레빈이 침울하게 말했다.\n\n\"거저라니, 그게 무슨 소린가?\" 스테판 아르카지치는 지금 레빈의 눈에는 무엇 하나 옳게 보일 리 없다는 걸 알면서도 사람 좋은 미소를 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"그 숲은 적어도 일 데샤티나에 백오십 루블은 나가거든.\" 레빈이 대답했다.\n\n\"오, 이 농사꾼들 좀 보게!\" 스테판 아르카지치가 장난스레 말했다. \"우리 같은 가련한 도시 사람들을 깔보는 그 말투라니!…… 하지만 막상 일을 처리하는 데는 우리가 누구보다도 더 잘한다네. 정말이지 내가 다 계산해 봤어.\" 그가 말했다. \"그 숲은 아주 좋은 값을 받는 거야. 어찌나 좋은지 사실 나는 저 친구가 도로 무를까 봐 겁이 날 지경이라고. 그게 '재목감'이 아니라는 걸 자네도 알잖나.\" 스테판 아르카지치는 이 구별을 들어 레빈에게 그 의심이 부당하다는 것을 완전히 납득시킬 수 있으리라 기대하며 말했다. \"일 데샤티나에서 나오는 장작이라야 스물다섯 사젠을 넘지 않을 텐데, 저 친구는 나한테 일 데샤티나에 칠십 루블씩 쳐주는 거란 말이야.\"\n\n레빈은 경멸하는 듯 빙긋 웃었다. '알지.' 그는 생각했다. '저런 버릇은 이 친구뿐 아니라 모든 도시 사람들한테 있는 거야. 십 년에 두 번쯤 시골에 와 보고는 두세 마디 말을 주워듣고, 그게 맞든 안 맞든 함부로 써먹으면서 자기들이 그 일을 죄다 안다고 굳게 믿는 거지. 〈재목감이라느니, 일 데샤티나에 몇 사젠이 나온다느니.〉 저 친구는 자기가 무슨 말을 하는지도 모르면서 그런 말을 늘어놓고 있어.'\n\n\"나라면 자네가 관청에서 쓰는 서류 따위에 대해 가르치려 들지 않을 거야.\" 그가 말했다. \"필요한 일이 생기면 자네한테 와서 물어보겠지. 그런데 자네는 숲에 관한 지식이라면 전부 다 안다고 그렇게 자신만만하니. 그게 어려운 거라네. 자네, 나무를 세어 보기는 했나?\"\n\n\"나무를 어떻게 세나?\" 스테판 아르카지치는 여전히 친구를 언짢은 기분에서 끌어내려 애쓰며 웃었다. \"바다의 모래알을 세고 별의 수를 헤아리라는 거나 마찬가지지. 더 높은 권능을 가진 분이라면 모를까.\"\n\n\"오, 그렇지, 랴비닌의 더 높은 권능이라면 셀 수 있고말고. 나무를 세지 않고 숲을 사는 상인은 단 하나도 없어. 지금 자네처럼 거저 주는 거라면 또 모를까. 나는 자네 숲을 잘 알아. 해마다 거기 가서 사냥을 하거든. 자네 숲은 현금으로 일 데샤티나에 백오십 루블은 나가는데, 저 친구는 분할로 육십 루블을 쳐주는 거야. 그러니까 자네는 사실상 저 친구한테 삼만 루블을 선물하는 셈이지.\"\n\n\"그만, 상상이 자네를 끌고 가게 내버려 두지 말게나.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 가련한 목소리로 말했다. \"그럼 어째서 아무도 더 쳐주려 하지 않았겠나?\"\n\n\"그야 저 친구가 상인들과 짜고 있으니까 그렇지. 그들을 매수해 버린 거야. 나는 그자들 모두와 거래를 해 봤거든. 잘 알아. 그들은 상인이 아니라, 알겠나, 투기꾼들이야. 십 퍼센트나 십오 퍼센트쯤 이익이 남는 흥정 따위는 거들떠보지도 않아. 일 루블짜리를 이십 코페이카에 사들이려고 잔뜩 버티고 있는 자들이지.\"\n\n\"그래, 그만하면 됐네! 자네 기분이 상해 있군.\"\n\n\"전혀 그렇지 않아.\" 레빈이 침울하게 말했다. 그러는 사이 그들은 집 앞에 다다랐다.\n\n현관 계단 앞에는 쇠와 가죽으로 단단히 감싼 마차 한 대가 서 있었고, 거기에는 넓은 멍에 끈으로 단단히 매인 윤기 흐르는 말 한 마리가 묶여 있었다. 마차 안에는 랴비닌의 마부 노릇을 하는, 통통하고 허리띠를 꽉 졸라맨 사무원이 앉아 있었다. 랴비닌은 이미 집 안에 들어와 있었고, 현관에서 두 친구를 맞이했다. 랴비닌은 키가 크고 다소 마른, 중년의 사내였다. 콧수염을 기르고 턱은 말끔히 면도해 앞으로 튀어나와 있었으며, 진흙처럼 탁해 보이는 두 눈이 불거져 있었다. 그는 자락이 긴 푸른색 외투를 입고 있었는데, 뒤쪽 허리 아래에 단추가 달려 있었고, 발목께는 주름이 잡히고 종아리께는 곧게 펴진 높은 장화를 신고 그 위에 커다란 덧신을 끼고 있었다. 그는 손수건으로 얼굴을 문지른 다음, 가뜩이나 몸에 아주 잘 맞는 외투를 한층 단단히 여미고는, 마치 무언가를 붙잡으려는 듯 스테판 아르카지치에게 손을 내밀며 미소로 그들을 맞았다.\n\n\"아, 여기 와 계셨군요.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 그에게 손을 내밀며 말했다. \"잘됐습니다.\"\n\n\"길이 형편없이 나빴지만, 그래도 각하의 분부를 감히 어길 수가 없었습죠. 정말이지 내내 걸어오다시피 했습니다만, 그래도 약속한 시간에 닿았습니다. 콘스탄틴 드미트리치, 안녕하십니까.\" 그는 레빈에게 돌아서며 그의 손까지 붙잡으려 했다. 그러나 레빈은 얼굴을 찌푸린 채 그의 손을 못 본 척하고는 멧도요를 꺼냈다. \"나리들께서는 사냥으로 재미를 보고 오셨군요? 실례입니다만, 그게 대체 무슨 새입니까요?\" 랴비닌은 멧도요를 경멸하듯 바라보며 덧붙였다. \"아주 별미겠습죠.\" 그러고는 이 사냥감이 과연 수고할 만한 값어치가 있는지 깊이 의심스럽다는 듯, 못마땅하게 고개를 가로저었다.\n\n\"내 서재로 들어가시겠소?\" 레빈이 침울하게 인상을 쓰며 프랑스어로 스테판 아르카지치에게 말했다. \"내 서재로 가게. 거기서 이야기하면 되니까.\"\n\n\"그러시지요, 어디든 좋으실 대로.\" 랴비닌은 마치 남들은 어떻게 처신해야 할지 난처해할지 몰라도, 자기만큼은 어떤 일에도 결코 난처해하는 법이 없다는 걸 일깨우려는 듯, 경멸 섞인 위엄을 풍기며 말했다.\n\n서재에 들어서자 랴비닌은 늘 하던 버릇대로 성상을 찾기라도 하는 양 사방을 둘러보았으나, 막상 그것을 찾고 나서도 성호를 긋지는 않았다. 그는 책장과 책꽂이들을 훑어보더니, 멧도요를 바라볼 때와 똑같은 미심쩍은 기색으로, 이 사냥감이 수고할 값어치가 있다고는 도무지 인정하지 못하겠다는 듯, 경멸하듯 빙긋 웃고는 못마땅하게 고개를 가로저었다.\n\n\"자, 돈은 가져오셨소?\" 오블론스키가 물었다. \"앉으시지요.\"\n\n\"오, 돈일랑 염려 마십시오. 저는 그 일을 의논하러 뵈러 온 겁니다.\"\n\n\"의논할 게 뭐가 있단 말이오? 어쨌든 앉으시오.\"\n\n\"앉아도 괜찮으시다면 앉지요.\" 랴비닌은 자리에 앉으며 자기로서는 더없이 불편한 자세로 의자 등받이에 팔꿈치를 괴었다. \"값을 조금 깎아 주셔야겠습니다, 공작님. 이대로는 너무 심합니다. 돈은 마지막 한 푼까지 빈틈없이 다 준비해 두었습니다. 현금을 치르는 데는 아무 차질도 없을 겁니다.\"\n\n그러는 사이 총을 찬장에 넣고 있던 레빈은 마침 문을 나서려던 참이었는데, 그 상인의 말을 듣고는 걸음을 멈추었다.\n\n\"아니, 자네는 지금도 그 숲을 거저 얻는 거나 다름없잖아.\" 그가 말했다. \"저 사람이 나한테 너무 늦게 왔어. 안 그랬으면 내가 값을 정해 줬을 텐데.\"\n\n랴비닌은 자리에서 일어나, 말없이 미소를 띤 채 레빈을 위아래로 훑어보았다.\n\n\"콘스탄틴 드미트리치께서는 돈에 어찌나 짜신지요.\" 그는 미소를 지으며 스테판 아르카지치에게 몸을 돌려 말했다. \"도무지 거래라는 걸 할 수가 없는 분입죠. 제가 이분한테서 밀을 흥정한 적이 있는데, 값도 꽤 후하게 쳐 드렸건만요.\"\n\n\"내 물건을 왜 자네한테 거저 주겠나? 땅에서 주운 것도 아니고, 훔친 것도 아닌데.\"\n\n\"아이고, 천만의 말씀입니다요! 요즘 세상에 훔칠 기회 따위가 어디 있겠습니까. 재판도 공개로 하고 만사가 격식대로 돌아가는 요즘 세상에 훔친다는 건 어림도 없는 얘깁죠. 저희야 그저 신사답게 의논이나 하자는 겁니다. 각하께서 숲값을 너무 많이 부르셔서요. 도무지 수지가 맞질 않습니다. 조금만 양보해 주십사 부탁드릴 수밖에요.\"\n\n\"한데 두 사람 사이에 일이 매듭지어진 거요, 아니오? 매듭지어졌다면 흥정해 봐야 소용없는 일이고, 아직 아니라면\" 하고 레빈이 말했다. \"그 숲은 내가 사겠소.\"\n\n랴비닌의 얼굴에서 미소가 단번에 사라졌다. 그 자리에는 매처럼 탐욕스럽고 잔인한 표정이 남았다. 그는 뼈만 앙상한 손가락을 재빨리 놀려 외투의 단추를 풀었고, 셔츠와 청동빛 조끼 단추와 시곗줄을 드러내며 두툼하고 낡은 지갑을 잽싸게 꺼냈다.\n\n\"자, 여기 있습니다. 그 숲은 제 겁니다.\" 그는 재빨리 성호를 긋고 손을 내밀며 말했다. \"돈을 받으십시오. 이건 제 숲입니다. 이게 바로 랴비닌이 거래하는 방식입죠. 반 푼 가지고 입씨름하는 법이 없습니다.\" 그는 인상을 쓰며 지갑을 흔들어 댔다.\n\n\"나 같으면 그렇게 서두르지 않겠소.\" 레빈이 말했다.\n\n\"이런, 정말이지.\" 오블론스키가 놀라서 말했다. \"내가 이미 약속을 했지 않은가, 그래.\"\n\n레빈은 문을 쾅 닫고 방을 나가 버렸다. 랴비닌은 그 문 쪽을 바라보며 미소 띤 얼굴로 고개를 가로저었다.\n\n\"다 젊은 혈기지요. 그야말로 애송이 티 말고는 아무것도 아닙니다. 제가 이걸 사는 건 말입죠, 정말이지 명예를 걸고 말씀드리는데, 그저, 믿어 주십시오, 오블론스키 님의 숲을 다른 누구도 아닌 이 랴비닌이 샀다는 그 영광 하나 때문입니다. 이문으로 말하자면야, 뭐, 하느님이 주시는 대로 챙겨야지요. 하느님의 이름으로 말입니다. 죄송합니다만 권리 증서에 서명을 좀 해 주신다면야……\"\n\n한 시간이 채 안 되어 그 상인은 큼직한 외투 자락을 매무새 좋게 쓸어내리고 윗도리 단추를 채운 다음, 계약서를 호주머니에 넣고 단단히 감싼 자기 마차에 올라앉아 집으로 떠났다.\n\n\"어휴, 저런 양반들이라니!\" 그가 사무원에게 말했다. \"저, 저것들 참 별난 작자들이야!\"\n\n\"그렇습죠.\" 사무원이 그에게 고삐를 건네주고 가죽 앞가리개의 단추를 채우며 대답했다. \"그건 그렇고, 미하일 이그나티치, 이번 거래는 축하드려도 되겠습니까요?\"\n\n\"음, 글쎄다……\"\n\n# Chapter 17\n\n스테판 아르카지치는 상인이 석 달치를 미리 치러 준 지폐로 주머니가 불룩해진 채 위층으로 올라갔다. 숲에 관한 거래는 끝났고, 돈은 주머니에 들어 있었으며, 사냥도 더할 나위 없이 좋았다. 그래서 스테판 아르카지치는 더없이 행복한 기분이었고, 그런 만큼 레빈에게 덮친 언짢은 기분을 풀어 주고 싶은 마음이 각별했다. 그는 하루를 시작할 때처럼 즐겁게 만찬으로 마무리하고 싶었다.\n\n레빈은 분명 기분이 좋지 않았고, 매력적인 손님에게 다정하고 살갑게 대하고 싶은 온갖 마음에도 불구하고 자기 기분을 다스리지 못했다. 키티가 결혼하지 않았다는 소식이 안겨 준 도취감이 차츰 그를 사로잡기 시작했던 것이다.\n\n키티는 결혼하지 않았지만, 병이 들었고, 그것도 그녀를 무시한 남자에 대한 사랑으로 병이 든 것이었다. 이 무시는 말하자면 그에게로 되튀어 왔다. 브론스키가 그녀를 무시했고, 그녀는 그를, 레빈을 무시했다. 따라서 브론스키는 레빈을 경멸할 권리가 있었고, 그러므로 그의 적이었다. 그러나 레빈이 이 모든 것을 곰곰이 따져 본 것은 아니었다. 그는 거기에 자기를 모욕하는 무언가가 있다는 것을 어렴풋이 느꼈고, 자기를 어지럽힌 것에 지금 화가 난 것이 아니라, 눈앞에 나타나는 모든 것에 시비를 걸었다. 어리석은 숲 매매며, 오블론스키에게 자행되어 자기 집에서 마무리된 그 사기가 그를 분통 터지게 했다.\n\n\"자, 끝났나?\" 그는 위층에서 스테판 아르카지치를 맞으며 말했다. \"야식이라도 들겠나?\"\n\n\"음, 마다하진 않겠네. 시골에 오니 어찌나 식욕이 도는지! 놀라워! 그런데 자넨 왜 랴비닌에게 뭐라도 권하지 않았나?\"\n\n\"아, 그자는 빌어먹을!\"\n\n\"그래도 그렇지, 자네 그 사람을 그렇게 대하다니!\" 오블론스키가 말했다. \"악수조차 하지 않더군. 악수 좀 하면 어떤가?\"\n\n\"나는 웨이터하고는 악수하지 않으니까. 그런데 웨이터가 그자보다 백배는 낫지.\"\n\n\"자넨 정말이지 영락없는 반동주의자로군! 그럼 계급의 융합은 어쩌고?\" 오블론스키가 말했다.\n\n\"융합하고 싶은 자는 누구든 마음대로 하라지. 하지만 나는 역겨워.\"\n\n\"보아하니 자넨 천생 반동주의자야.\"\n\n\"정말이지, 내가 뭔지 한 번도 생각해 본 적이 없네. 나는 콘스탄틴 레빈일 뿐, 그 이상도 이하도 아니야.\"\n\n\"게다가 잔뜩 심사가 뒤틀린 콘스탄틴 레빈이지.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 나는 심사가 뒤틀렸네. 그 이유를 아나? 미안하지만, 자네의 그 어리석은 매매 때문이야…….\"\n\n스테판 아르카지치는 아무 잘못도 없이 놀림받고 공격당한다고 느끼는 사람처럼, 그러나 너그러운 표정으로 눈살을 찌푸렸다.\n\n\"이런, 그만하면 됐네!\" 그가 말했다. \"누가 무엇을 팔든 거래가 끝난 직후에 '그건 훨씬 더 값이 나갔는데'라는 말을 듣지 않는 경우가 어디 있나? 하지만 막상 팔려고 하면, 아무도 한 푼 주려 들지 않는단 말일세……. 아니, 보아하니 자넨 그 불운한 랴비닌에게 원한이 있나 보군.\"\n\n\"어쩌면 그럴지도 모르지. 그 이유를 아나? 자넨 또 내가 반동주의자라느니, 뭔가 끔찍한 말을 늘어놓겠지. 하지만 어쨌든 사방에서 내가 속한 귀족 계급이 빈곤해져 가는 꼴을 보는 게 나를 화나게 하고 짜증 나게 해. 게다가 나는 계급의 융합에도 불구하고 이 계급에 속한 게 기쁘단 말이야. 그리고 그들의 빈곤은 사치 탓이 아니야. 그거라면 아무것도 아니지. 격식 있게 사는 거야 귀족에게 합당한 일이고, 그렇게 할 줄 아는 건 오직 귀족뿐이니까. 요즘 우리 주변의 농민들이 땅을 사고 있는데, 나는 그건 개의치 않아. 신사는 아무 일도 하지 않고, 농민은 일을 해서 게으른 자를 밀어내지. 그건 마땅히 그래야 하는 거야. 나는 농민을 위해 정말 기쁘게 생각하네. 하지만 일종의 — 뭐라 불러야 할지 모르겠지만 — 순진함에서 비롯되는 빈곤의 과정을 보는 건 정말 견디기 힘들어. 여기 어떤 폴란드인 투기꾼이 니스에 사는 젊은 아가씨에게서 굉장한 영지를 그 값의 절반에 사들였지. 또 저기서는 어떤 상인이 십 루블짜리 땅 삼 에이커를 일 루블 빌려주는 담보로 차지할 거고. 그리고 자넨, 아무 까닭도 없이 그 악당에게 삼만 루블을 선물한 셈이야.\"\n\n\"그럼, 내가 어떻게 했어야 하나? 나무를 일일이 세기라도 했어야 한단 말인가?\"\n\n\"물론, 세어 봤어야지. 자넨 세지 않았지만, 랴비닌은 셌어. 랴비닌의 자식들은 살아갈 밑천과 교육받을 수단을 갖게 될 테고, 자네 자식들은 어쩌면 못 가질지도 모른단 말일세!\"\n\n\"이거 미안하지만, 그렇게 일일이 세는 데에는 뭔가 치사한 구석이 있어. 우리에겐 우리의 일이 있고 그들에겐 그들의 일이 있으니, 그들도 이윤을 남겨야지. 어쨌든 일은 다 끝났으니, 이걸로 끝이야. 아, 그리고 여기 수란이 나오는군. 내가 제일 좋아하는 요리지. 아가피야 미하일로브나가 그 기막힌 약초 브랜디도 내올 거고…….\"\n\n스테판 아르카지치는 식탁에 앉아 아가피야 미하일로브나에게 농을 걸며, 이렇게 훌륭한 점심과 야식을 맛본 게 참 오랜만이라고 추어올렸다.\n\n\"하긴, 어쨌든 칭찬을 하시니 고맙긴 합니다만,\" 아가피야 미하일로브나가 말했다. \"콘스탄틴 드미트리치 도련님은, 무얼 드려도 — 빵 한 조각이라도 — 그냥 드시고 일어나 버리신답니다.\"\n\n레빈은 자제하려 애썼지만, 침울하고 말이 없었다. 그는 스테판 아르카지치에게 한 가지 묻고 싶은 것이 있었으나, 좀처럼 그 대목에 다다르지 못했고, 그것을 물을 말도 순간도 찾지 못했다. 스테판 아르카지치는 아래층 자기 방으로 내려가 옷을 벗고 다시 몸을 씻은 다음, 주름 장식이 달린 잠옷을 걸치고 잠자리에 들었는데, 그래도 레빈은 여전히 그의 방에서 어정거리며 이런저런 사소한 이야기를 늘어놓을 뿐, 정작 알고 싶은 것은 감히 묻지 못했다.\n\n\"이 비누는 정말 기막히게 잘 만들었군.\" 그는 손에 만지작거리던 비누 한 조각을 들여다보며 말했다. 아가피야 미하일로브나가 손님을 위해 준비해 두었으나 오블론스키가 쓰지 않은 것이었다. \"보게나. 이건 그야말로 예술품이야.\"\n\n\"그래, 요즘은 뭐든 그렇게 완벽의 경지에 이르렀지.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 촉촉하고 더없이 흐뭇한 하품과 함께 말했다. \"이를테면 극장이며, 오락거리며……아—아—아!\" 그는 하품을 했다. \"어디든 전등불이고……아—아—아!\"\n\n\"그래, 전등불이지.\" 레빈이 말했다. \"그래. 아, 그런데 브론스키는 지금 어디 있나?\" 그는 비누를 내려놓으며 불쑥 물었다.\n\n\"브론스키?\" 스테판 아르카지치가 하품을 멈추며 말했다. \"페테르부르크에 있네. 자네가 떠나고 얼마 안 있어 떠났고, 그 뒤로 한 번도 모스크바에 온 적이 없지. 그런데 말이야, 코스챠, 솔직히 말하겠네.\" 그는 식탁에 팔꿈치를 괸 채, 촉촉하고 선량하며 졸음이 어린 두 눈이 별처럼 빛나는 잘생긴 불그스름한 얼굴을 손으로 받치며 말을 이었다. \"그건 자네 잘못이야. 자넨 연적을 보고 겁을 먹어 버렸어. 하지만 그때도 말했듯이, 나는 어느 쪽이 더 가망이 있는지 알 수 없었네. 왜 끝까지 싸워 보지 않았나? 그때도 자네한테 말하지 않았나…….\" 그는 입을 벌리지 않은 채 속으로 하품을 했다.\n\n'이 사람은 내가 청혼했던 걸 아는 걸까, 모르는 걸까?' 레빈은 그를 응시하며 의아해했다. '그래, 저 얼굴에는 뭔가 능청스럽고 외교적인 데가 있어.' 그러고는 자기가 얼굴을 붉히고 있음을 느끼며, 아무 말 없이 스테판 아르카지치의 얼굴을 똑바로 바라보았다.\n\n\"그때 그녀 쪽에 무언가 있었다 해도, 그건 한낱 겉으로 끌리는 마음에 지나지 않았어.\" 오블론스키가 말을 이었다. \"그가 그렇게 완벽한 귀족이라는 점, 알다시피, 그리고 그의 장래 사교계 지위가 영향을 미친 건 그녀가 아니라 그녀 어머니에게였지.\"\n\n레빈은 인상을 찌푸렸다. 거절당한 굴욕이, 마치 방금 막 입은 생생한 상처라도 되는 듯, 그의 가슴을 찔렀다. 그러나 그는 자기 집에 있었고, 자기 집의 벽은 하나의 버팀목이었다.\n\n\"잠깐, 잠깐.\" 그는 오블론스키의 말을 끊으며 입을 열었다. \"자넨 그가 귀족이라고 말하는군. 그런데 묻겠는데, 도대체 그게 무엇으로 이루어져 있나? 그 브론스키든 누구든, 그 앞에서 내가 멸시당할 만한 그 귀족성이라는 게 말이야? 자넨 브론스키를 귀족이라 여기지만, 나는 그렇게 보지 않아. 아비라는 자는 술수를 부려 맨바닥에서 기어 올라왔고, 어미라는 자는 — 하느님만이 아실 일이지, 누구와 얽히지 않았겠나……. 아니, 미안하지만, 나는 나 자신을, 그리고 나 같은 사람들을 귀족이라 여기네. 과거로 거슬러 올라가 서너 대에 걸쳐 명예로운 가문, 최고의 교양을 지닌 가문을 가리킬 수 있고(재능과 지성은, 물론 그건 또 다른 문제지만), 또 우리 아버지나 할아버지처럼 누구에게도 아첨한 적 없고, 무엇 하나 누구에게 기댄 적 없는 사람들 말이야. 나는 그런 사람을 많이 알고 있네. 자넨 내가 내 숲의 나무를 세는 걸 치사하다고 여기면서, 정작 자네는 랴비닌에게 삼만 루블을 선물하지. 하지만 자넨 영지에서 소작료며 뭔지 모를 수입을 거둬들이는데, 나는 그게 없어. 그래서 나는 조상에게서 물려받았거나 힘든 노동으로 얻은 것을 소중히 여기는 거야……. 우리야말로 귀족이지, 이 세상 권력자들의 비위를 맞춰야만 존재할 수 있고 동전 두 닢 반에도 매수당하는 그런 자들이 아니란 말이야.\"\n\n\"그래, 한데 자넨 누굴 공격하는 건가? 나는 자네 말에 동의하네.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 진심으로, 그리고 다정하게 말했다. 다만 그는 동전 두 닢 반에 매수당할 수 있는 부류 안에 레빈이 자기까지 셈하고 있다는 것을 알고 있었다. 레빈의 열기가 그에게 진정한 즐거움을 주었던 것이다. \"자넨 누굴 공격하는 거야? 자네가 브론스키에 대해 한 말 중에 사실이 아닌 게 적지 않지만, 그 얘긴 하지 않겠네. 단도직입적으로 말하지. 내가 자네라면, 나하고 같이 모스크바로 돌아가서…….\"\n\n\"아니. 자네가 그걸 아는지 모르는지 모르겠지만, 나는 상관없어. 그리고 말해 두겠는데 — 나는 청혼을 했고 거절당했어. 카체리나 알렉산드로브나는 이제 나에게 고통스럽고 굴욕적인 추억일 뿐이야.\"\n\n\"대체 왜 그런가? 무슨 그런 말도 안 되는 소릴!\"\n\n\"하지만 그 얘긴 그만하세. 내가 못되게 굴었다면, 부디 용서해 주게.\" 레빈이 말했다. 이제 마음을 털어놓고 나니, 그는 아침에 그랬던 모습으로 돌아갔다. \"나한테 화난 건 아니지, 스티바? 제발 화내지 말게.\" 그는 이렇게 말하며 미소 짓고는 그의 손을 잡았다.\n\n\"물론 아니지. 조금도, 그럴 까닭도 없네. 우리가 터놓고 이야기해서 기쁘군. 그런데 말이야, 아침에 하는 매복 사냥이 유난히 좋아 — 가지 않겠나? 어차피 밤새 잠도 못 잘 텐데, 사냥하고 나서 곧장 역으로 가면 되니까.\"\n\n\"그거 좋군.\"\n\n# Chapter 18\n\n브론스키의 내면생활은 온통 사랑에 빠져 있었지만, 외적 생활은 변함없이 그리고 어쩔 수 없이 사교계와 연대(聯隊)의 인연이며 관심사라는, 익숙한 옛 노선을 따라 흘러갔다. 연대의 일은 브론스키의 삶에서 중요한 자리를 차지했는데, 그가 연대를 좋아하기 때문이기도 했고, 또 연대가 그를 좋아하기 때문이기도 했다. 연대 사람들은 그저 그를 좋아하는 데 그치지 않고 그를 존경하기도 했으며, 그를 자랑스러워했다. 막대한 재산과 빛나는 교양과 재능을 가졌고, 온갖 종류의 성공과 명예와 출세의 길이 눈앞에 열려 있는 이 사내가, 그 모든 것을 마다하고, 삶의 온갖 관심사 가운데 연대와 전우들의 일을 가장 가슴 가까이에 두고 있다는 사실이 자랑스러웠던 것이다. 브론스키는 전우들이 자기를 어떻게 보는지 잘 알았고, 그 생활을 좋아하는 마음에 더해, 그 평판을 지켜야 한다는 의무감마저 느꼈다.\n\n그가 전우들 누구에게도 자신의 사랑을 입에 올리지 않았다는 것, 술자리가 아무리 흥청거려도(하긴 그는 자제력을 완전히 잃을 만큼 취한 적이 한 번도 없었다) 비밀을 누설하지 않았다는 것은 말할 필요도 없다. 그리고 그는 무심코 자기 관계를 들먹이려는 전우가 있으면 누구든 입을 막아 버렸다. 하지만 그럼에도 그의 사랑은 도시 전체에 알려져 있었다. 누구나 어느 정도 확신을 가지고 그와 카레니나 부인의 관계를 짐작했다. 젊은 축의 대다수는, 정작 그의 사랑에서 가장 성가신 요소였던 바로 그것, 즉 카레닌의 드높은 지위와, 그로 인해 두 사람의 관계가 사교계에 공공연히 드러나게 된 사정을 두고 그를 부러워했다.\n\n안나를 부러워하면서도 그녀가 정숙하다는 말을 듣는 데 오래전부터 진력이 나 있던 젊은 여자들의 다수는, 자기들의 예언이 실현된 것을 기뻐했으며, 여론이 결정적으로 돌아서기만 하면 멸시의 무게를 온통 그녀에게 쏟아부으려고 기다리고 있을 뿐이었다. 그들은 때가 무르익으면 그녀에게 던질 진흙을 이미 한 움큼씩 손에 그러쥐고 준비해 두고 있었다. 중년층의 대다수와 일부 고관들은 사교계에 닥쳐올 추문의 조짐을 못마땅하게 여겼다.\n* liaison: 프랑스어로 '정사(情事)·밀통'.\n\n브론스키의 어머니는 아들의 관계에 대해 처음 들었을 때는 도리어 흡족해했다. 그녀의 생각으로는 상류 사교계에서의 liaison만큼 빛나는 청년에게 마무리의 광택을 더해 주는 것이 없었기 때문이다. 또한 그토록 자기 마음에 들었고 자기 아들 이야기를 그토록 많이 했던 카레니나 부인이, 결국 다른 예쁘고 교양 있는 여자들과 다를 바 없었다는 사실도—적어도 브론스카야 백작 부인의 관념으로는 그러했다—흐뭇했다. 하지만 그녀는 최근에, 아들이 출세에 더없이 중요한 자리를 제의받고도, 오로지 카레니나 부인을 늘 만날 수 있는 연대에 남기 위해 그것을 거절했다는 말을 들었다. 그 일로 고관들이 아들을 못마땅해한다는 것을 알게 되자, 그녀는 생각을 바꾸었다. 게다가 이 관계에 대해 알아낼 수 있는 모든 것으로 미루어, 그것이 자기가 기꺼이 반겼을 그 빛나고 우아하고 세속적인 liaison이 아니라, 듣자 하니 일종의 베르테르식의 절망적인 정열이어서, 아들을 경솔한 짓으로 몰아갈 수도 있다는 점도 그녀를 언짢게 했다. 그녀는 아들이 모스크바를 급작스레 떠난 뒤로 한 번도 그를 보지 못했고, 그래서 맏아들을 보내 자기를 보러 오라고 일렀다.\n\n이 맏아들 또한 동생을 못마땅하게 여겼다. 그는 동생의 사랑이 어떤 종류인지, 큰 것인지 작은 것인지, 정열적인 것인지 무덤덤한 것인지, 오래갈 것인지 스쳐 지날 것인지 따위는 구분하지 않았다(그 자신도 한 집안의 가장이면서 발레리나 하나를 데리고 살았으니, 이런 문제에는 너그러웠다). 다만 이 연애가 비위를 맞추어야 할 사람들에게 못마땅하게 비치고 있다는 것을 알았기에, 동생의 처신에 찬성하지 않았다.\n\n직무와 사교계 말고도 브론스키에게는 또 하나의 큰 관심사가 있었다—말이었다. 그는 말을 미치도록 좋아했다.\n\n그해에는 장교들을 위한 경마와 장애물 경주가 마련되어 있었다. 브론스키는 출전 신청을 하고, 순종 영국산 암말을 한 마리 사들였으며, 연애 사건이 있는 와중에도 겉으로는 자제하면서도 속으로는 격렬한 흥분 속에서 경마를 손꼽아 기다리고 있었다.……\n\n이 두 가지 열정은 서로 방해가 되지 않았다. 오히려 그는 자기를 휘젓는 격렬한 감정에서 기운을 되찾고 휴식을 얻기 위해서라도, 사랑과는 전혀 별개의 몰두할 일과 기분 전환거리가 필요했다.\n\n# Chapter 19\n\n크라스노예 셀로에서 경마가 열리는 날, 브론스키는 평소보다 일찍 연대의 공동 식당으로 와서 비프스테이크를 먹었다. 그는 자신에게 그리 엄격할 필요가 없었으니, 요구되는 가벼운 체중까지 이미 아주 빠르게 떨어뜨려 둔 터였다. 그래도 살이 붙는 것은 피해야 했으므로 전분과 단것이 든 요리는 멀리했다. 그는 흰 조끼 위에 입은 군복 상의의 단추를 풀어 헤친 채로 두 팔꿈치를 식탁에 괴고 앉아, 주문해 둔 스테이크를 기다리는 동안 접시 위에 펼쳐 놓은 프랑스 소설을 들여다보았다. 책을 들여다본 것은 그저 들락거리는 장교들과 말을 섞지 않으려는 것이었을 뿐, 그는 생각에 잠겨 있었다.\n\n그는 경마가 끝난 뒤 오늘 만나 주겠다던 안나의 약속을 생각하고 있었다. 그러나 사흘째 그녀를 보지 못했고, 그녀의 남편이 막 외국에서 돌아온 터라 오늘 그녀가 자기를 만날 수 있을지 어떨지 알 수 없었으며, 그것을 알아낼 방도도 알지 못했다. 그녀와의 마지막 만남은 사촌 누이 벳시의 여름 별장에서였다. 그는 카레닌 부부의 여름 별장에는 되도록 드물게 드나들었다. 지금 그는 그곳에 가고 싶었고, 어떻게 하면 좋을지 그 방법을 곰곰이 따져 보았다.\n\n\"물론 벳시가 그녀에게 경마에 오는지 물어보라고 나를 보냈다고 하면 되지. 물론, 가야지.\" 그는 책에서 고개를 들며 결심했다. 그리고 그녀를 만나는 행복을 생생히 그려 보자, 그의 얼굴이 환해졌다.\n\n\"우리 집으로 사람을 보내서, 마차와 말 세 필을 되도록 빨리 준비해 두라고 일러.\" 그는 뜨거운 은쟁반에 스테이크를 받쳐 건네주는 하인에게 말하고는, 쟁반을 끌어당겨 먹기 시작했다.\n\n옆방 당구장에서 공이 부딪치는 소리와 이야기하고 웃는 소리가 들려왔다. 입구 문가에 두 장교가 나타났다. 하나는 시동 군단에서 최근 연대로 들어온, 가냘프고 섬세한 얼굴을 한 젊은 사내였고, 다른 하나는 손목에 팔찌를 차고 살에 파묻혀 작아 보이는 눈을 한, 통통하고 나이 든 장교였다.\n\n브론스키는 그들을 흘낏 보고는 눈살을 찌푸렸고, 마치 그들을 보지 못한 양 책으로 시선을 떨군 채 먹으면서 동시에 읽기를 계속했다.\n\n\"왜 그래? 자네 일을 위해 기력을 보충하는 중인가?\" 통통한 장교가 그의 곁에 앉으며 말했다.\n\n\"보다시피.\" 브론스키는 미간을 찌푸리고 입을 닦으며, 그 장교 쪽은 보지도 않은 채 대답했다.\n\n\"그럼 살찌는 게 무섭지 않다는 건가?\" 통통한 장교가 젊은 장교를 위해 의자를 돌려 놓으며 말했다.\n\n\"뭐라고?\" 브론스키는 화가 나서, 역겹다는 듯 얼굴을 찡그리며 가지런한 이를 드러낸 채 말했다.\n\n\"살찌는 게 무섭지 않냐고?\"\n\n\"웨이터, 셰리주!\" 브론스키는 대꾸도 하지 않고 그렇게 말하고는, 책을 반대쪽으로 옮겨 놓고 계속 읽어 나갔다.\n\n통통한 장교는 술 목록을 집어 들고 젊은 장교 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸다.\n\n\"우리가 뭘 마실지 자네가 골라.\" 그는 카드를 건네며 그를 바라보고 말했다.\n\n\"라인 와인으로 부탁드립니다.\" 젊은 장교는 브론스키에게 겁먹은 듯한 시선을 슬쩍 던지며, 거의 보이지도 않는 콧수염을 잡아당기려 애쓰며 말했다. 브론스키가 돌아보지 않는 것을 보고는 젊은 장교가 일어섰다.\n\n\"당구장으로 갑시다.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n통통한 장교는 순순히 일어났고, 두 사람은 문 쪽으로 걸어갔다.\n\n바로 그때 키가 크고 다부진 야시빈 대위가 방으로 들어왔다. 그는 두 장교에게 거만한 경멸의 기색으로 고갯짓을 하고는 브론스키에게 다가갔다.\n\n\"아! 여기 있었군!\" 그가 큼직한 손을 브론스키의 견장 위로 묵직하게 내리치며 소리쳤다. 브론스키는 성난 듯 돌아보았지만, 그의 얼굴은 곧바로 그 특유의 다정하고 사내다운 평온의 표정으로 환해졌다.\n\n\"바로 그거야, 알렉세이.\" 대위가 큰 바리톤 목소리로 말했다. \"지금은 딱 한 입만 먹고, 자그마한 잔으로 딱 한 잔만 마셔야 하네.\"\n\n\"아, 난 배가 안 고파.\"\n\n\"저기 떼려야 뗄 수 없는 단짝들이 가시는군.\" 야시빈은 그 순간 방을 나서던 두 장교를 비꼬듯 흘낏 보며 한마디 흘렸다. 그러고는 꽉 끼는 승마 바지로 감싼 긴 다리를 구부려, 자기에게는 너무 낮은 의자에 앉았는데, 그 바람에 무릎이 날카로운 각을 이루며 잔뜩 오므라들었다.\n\n\"어제 적색 극장엔 왜 안 왔나? 누메로바가 영 나쁘지 않던데. 자넨 어디 있었어?\"\n\n\"트베르스코이 댁에 늦게까지 있었어.\" 브론스키가 말했다.\n\n\"아!\" 야시빈이 대꾸했다.\n\n도박꾼이자 난봉꾼이며, 도덕적 원칙이 없을 뿐 아니라 비도덕적 원칙을 지닌 사내인 야시빈은 연대에서 브론스키의 가장 가까운 친구였다. 브론스키가 그를 좋아한 것은 그의 남다른 체력 때문이기도 했는데, 그 체력은 주로 술을 물처럼 마셔 대고 잠을 자지 않고도 조금도 영향을 받지 않는 데서 드러났다. 또한 동료들과 상관들과의 관계에서 두려움과 존경을 동시에 자아내며 발휘되는 그의 강한 성격 때문이기도 했고, 카드놀이에서 수만 루블을 걸고 아무리 술을 많이 마셨어도 늘 솜씨 좋고 결단력 있게 게임을 해서 영국 클럽 최고의 선수로 꼽히는 그 모습 때문이기도 했다. 브론스키가 야시빈을 각별히 존경하고 좋아한 것은, 야시빈이 자기를 그 이름이나 돈 때문이 아니라 자기 자신 때문에 좋아한다는 것을 느꼈기 때문이다. 그리고 브론스키가 자신의 사랑에 대해 이야기하고 싶은 마음이 드는 유일한 사내가 바로 그였다. 야시빈은 온갖 종류의 감정을 겉으로는 경멸하는 듯 보이면서도, 지금 자기 삶 전체를 가득 채운 이 강렬한 열정을 이해할 수 있는 유일한 사내라고 브론스키는 여겼다. 게다가 그는 야시빈이 험담이나 추문을 즐기지 않으며, 자신의 감정을 올바르게 해석하리라는 것을, 다시 말해 이 열정이 농담도 아니고 한때의 소일거리도 아니며 무언가 더 진지하고 중요한 것임을 알고 믿어 주리라는 것을 확신했다.\n\n브론스키는 그에게 자신의 열정에 대해 한 번도 이야기한 적이 없었지만, 그가 그것을 모두 알고 있으며 올바르게 해석하고 있다는 것을 알았고, 그의 눈빛에서 그것을 읽어 내는 것이 기뻤다.\n\n\"아! 그래.\" 그는 브론스키가 트베르스코이 댁에 있었다는 말에 그렇게 대꾸했다. 그러고는 그 검은 눈을 빛내며, 왼쪽 콧수염을 잡아당겨 입속으로 비틀어 넣기 시작했는데, 그것은 그의 못된 버릇이었다.\n\n\"그래, 어제 자넨 뭘 했나? 뭐 좀 땄어?\" 브론스키가 물었다.\n\n\"팔천 땄지. 하지만 그중 삼천은 안 쳐. 그 친구가 안 갚을 거거든.\"\n\n\"오, 그럼 자네 나한테는 좀 잃어도 되겠군.\" 브론스키가 웃으며 말했다. (야시빈은 경마에서 브론스키에게 큰돈을 걸었던 것이다.)\n\n\"내가 잃을 일은 없어. 마호틴 하나만이 위험하지.\"\n\n그리고 대화는 다가오는 경마에 대한 예측으로 넘어갔는데, 그것이야말로 지금 브론스키가 생각할 수 있는 유일한 것이었다.\n\n\"가지, 난 다 먹었네.\" 브론스키가 말하고 일어나 문 쪽으로 갔다. 야시빈도 긴 다리와 긴 등을 쭉 펴며 일어섰다.\n\n\"식사하기엔 나한텐 너무 이르지만, 한잔은 해야겠어. 곧 따라가지. 어이, 술!\" 그는 훈련 때면 늘 그토록 우렁차게 울리던 그 풍부한 목소리로 외쳤고, 이번에는 그 소리에 창문이 흔들렸다.\n\n\"아니, 됐어.\" 그는 곧바로 다시 외쳤다. \"자네 집에 가는 거니까, 나도 같이 가지.\"\n\n그러고는 브론스키와 함께 걸어 나갔다.\n\n# Chapter 20\n\n브론스키는 칸막이로 둘로 나뉜, 널찍하고 깨끗한 핀란드식 오두막에 묵고 있었다. 페트리츠키도 진영에서 그와 함께 지냈다. 브론스키와 야시빈이 오두막으로 들어왔을 때 페트리츠키는 잠들어 있었다.\n\n\"일어나, 더 자지 말고.\" 야시빈이 칸막이 뒤로 돌아가, 헝클어진 머리에 베개에 코를 박고 누워 있는 페트리츠키의 어깨를 툭 찌르며 말했다.\n\n페트리츠키는 별안간 무릎을 꿇고 벌떡 일어나 주위를 둘러보았다.\n\n\"자네 형이 왔다 갔어.\" 그가 브론스키에게 말했다. \"젠장, 그가 날 깨우더니, 다시 들르겠다고 했어.\" 그러더니 담요를 끌어당겨 다시 베개에 몸을 던졌다. \"아, 좀 닥쳐, 야시빈!\" 그는 담요를 끌어당겨 벗기는 야시빈에게 잔뜩 화를 내며 말했다. \"닥쳐!\" 그는 몸을 뒤척이며 눈을 떴다. \"그보다 뭘 마시면 좋을지나 말해 줘. 입안이 이렇게 텁텁하니, 원……\"\n\n\"브랜디가 제일이지.\" 야시빈이 우렁차게 말했다. \"테레시첸코! 주인 나리께 브랜디하고 오이를 가져와.\" 그는 자기 목소리의 울림을 즐기는 게 분명한 어조로 소리쳤다.\n\n\"브랜디라고, 그게 좋겠어? 응?\" 페트리츠키가 눈을 깜박이며 비비더니 물었다. \"자네도 한잔할 거지? 좋아, 그럼 같이 마시자고! 브론스키, 한잔하겠나?\" 페트리츠키는 일어서서 호랑이 가죽 담요를 몸에 두르며 말했다. 그는 칸막이벽의 문 쪽으로 가서 두 손을 들고 프랑스어로 흥얼거렸다. \"툴레에 한 왕이 있었네.\" \"브론스키, 한잔하겠나?\"\n\n\"됐어, 그만둬.\" 브론스키가 시종이 건네준 외투를 입으며 말했다.\n\n\"어디 가는데?\" 야시빈이 물었다. \"아, 자네 삼두마차가 왔군.\" 마차가 다가오는 것을 보고 그가 덧붙였다.\n\n\"마구간에, 그리고 말 때문에 브랸스키도 만나 봐야 해.\" 브론스키가 말했다.\n\n브론스키는 사실 페테르고프에서 8마일쯤 떨어진 브랸스키의 집에 들러, 말 값으로 빚진 돈을 가져다주기로 약속해 둔 터였다. 그 일도 처리할 시간이 있기를 바랐다. 하지만 동료들은 그가 거기만 가려는 게 아니라는 것을 단번에 알아챘다.\n\n페트리츠키는 여전히 흥얼거리며 한쪽 눈을 찡긋하고 입술을 삐죽 내밀었다. 마치 \"그래, 그래, 자네 브랸스키가 어떤 양반인지 우리도 알지\" 하고 말하려는 듯이.\n\n\"늦지 않게 조심해!\" 야시빈은 그 말만 했다. 그러고는 화제를 바꾸려고 \"내 적갈색 말은 어때? 잘 지내고 있나?\" 하고 물으며, 창밖으로 자기가 브론스키에게 판, 세 마리 중 가운뎃말을 내다보았다.\n\n\"잠깐!\" 브론스키가 막 나가려는데 페트리츠키가 외쳤다. \"자네 형이 자네한테 편지하고 쪽지를 남기고 갔어. 잠깐만, 그게 어디 있더라?\"\n\n브론스키는 걸음을 멈췄다.\n\n\"그래, 어디 있는데?\"\n\n\"어디 있느냐, 그게 바로 문제란 말이지!\" 페트리츠키가 집게손가락을 코에서 위로 치켜올리며 엄숙하게 말했다.\n\n\"이봐, 어서 말해. 이거 우습잖아!\" 브론스키가 웃으며 말했다.\n\n\"불을 안 붙였는데. 여기 어디쯤 있을 텐데.\"\n\n\"이봐, 장난 그만! 편지가 어디 있어?\"\n\n\"아니, 정말 깜빡했어. 아니면 꿈이었나? 잠깐, 잠깐만! 그런데 화낼 게 뭐 있어. 자네도 어제 나처럼 네 병을 들이켰으면 어디 누웠는지조차 잊어버렸을걸. 잠깐만, 떠올려 볼게!\"\n\n페트리츠키는 칸막이 뒤로 돌아가 자기 침대에 누웠다.\n\n\"잠깐! 내가 이렇게 누워 있었고, 형은 이렇게 서 있었지. 그래, 그래, 그래…… 여기 있다!\" 페트리츠키는 매트리스 밑에 숨겨 두었던 편지를 끄집어냈다.\n\n브론스키는 편지와 형의 쪽지를 받아 들었다. 그것은 그가 예상하던 편지였다. 어머니가 자기를 보러 오지 않았다고 그를 나무라는 편지였고, 쪽지는 형이 잠깐 할 이야기가 있다고 적은 것이었다. 브론스키는 그것이 모두 같은 일에 관한 것임을 알고 있었다. '그게 그들과 무슨 상관이람!' 브론스키는 생각하고는, 길에서 찬찬히 읽으려고 편지들을 구겨 외투 단추 사이에 끼워 넣었다. 오두막 현관에서 그는 두 장교와 마주쳤다. 하나는 자기 연대 소속이었고, 하나는 다른 연대 소속이었다.\n\n브론스키의 숙소는 늘 모든 장교들의 만남의 장소였다.\n\n\"어디 가나?\"\n\n\"페테르고프에 가야 해.\"\n\n\"차르스코예에서 암말이 왔나?\"\n\n\"응, 하지만 아직 못 봤어.\"\n\n\"마호틴의 글라디아토르가 절름거린다던데.\"\n\n\"말도 안 돼! 그런데 자네는 대체 이런 진창에서 어떻게 경마를 할 셈인가?\" 다른 장교가 말했다.\n\n\"내 구원자들이 오셨군!\" 페트리츠키가 그들이 들어오는 것을 보고 외쳤다. 그의 앞에는 브랜디와 소금에 절인 오이를 쟁반에 받쳐 든 당번병이 서 있었다. \"야시빈이 나더러 해장술을 마시라는군.\"\n\n\"이봐, 어제 자네 때문에 우리가 얼마나 혼났는지 아나.\" 들어온 장교 중 하나가 말했다. \"밤새도록 한숨도 못 자게 했잖아.\"\n\n\"아, 우리 멋들어지게 끝장을 봤지!\" 페트리츠키가 말했다. \"볼코프가 지붕 위로 기어올라가더니 자기가 얼마나 슬픈지 늘어놓기 시작했지. 그래서 내가 '음악을 틀자고, 장송 행진곡으로!' 했더니, 글쎄 그 장송 행진곡을 들으면서 지붕 위에서 그대로 잠들어 버렸지 뭐야.\"\n\n\"마셔, 자네는 무조건 브랜디를 마셔야 해. 그러고 나서 셀처 탄산수에 레몬을 듬뿍 넣어 마시고.\" 야시빈이 마치 아이에게 약을 먹이는 어머니처럼 페트리츠키 위로 몸을 굽힌 채 말했다. \"그다음에 샴페인을 조금, 딱 작은 병으로 하나만.\"\n\n\"이봐, 그 말엔 일리가 있군. 잠깐 있어 봐, 브론스키. 다 같이 한잔하자고.\"\n\n\"아니, 다들 잘 있게. 난 오늘은 안 마실 거야.\"\n\n\"왜, 살이라도 찌고 있나? 좋아, 그럼 우리끼리 마셔야지. 셀처 탄산수에 레몬 가져와.\"\n\n\"브론스키!\" 그가 이미 밖에 나와 있을 때 누군가가 소리쳤다.\n\n\"왜?\"\n\n\"머리나 좀 자르지 그래. 무게에 짓눌리겠어, 특히 정수리 쪽이.\"\n\n브론스키는 사실 때 이르게 머리가 조금씩 벗어지기 시작하던 참이었다. 그는 고른 치아를 드러내며 유쾌하게 웃고는, 머리숱이 적어진 자리에 모자를 눌러쓰고 밖으로 나가 마차에 올랐다.\n\n\"마구간으로!\" 그는 말하고는, 편지들을 다 읽으려고 막 꺼내 들었다가, 다시 생각을 고쳐먹고는, 암말을 보기 전에 주의가 흐트러지지 않도록 읽기를 미루었다. '나중에!'\n\n# Chapter 21\n\n경마장 가까이에 임시 마구간으로 쓸 목조 헛간이 세워졌고, 그의 암말은 전날 그리로 옮겨졌어야 했다. 그는 아직 거기서 말을 보지 못했다.\n\n지난 며칠 동안 그는 직접 말을 끌고 나가 운동을 시키지 않고 조련사에게 맡겨 두었기에, 지금은 자기 암말이 어제 어떤 상태로 도착했는지, 또 오늘은 어떤지 도무지 알지 못했다. 그가 마차에서 채 내리기도 전에, 이른바 '마구간지기 소년'이라 불리는 마부가 멀찍이서 마차를 알아보고 조련사를 불렀다. 긴 장화에 짧은 재킷을 입고, 턱 밑에 수염 한 줌만 남긴 채 말끔히 면도한, 메마른 인상의 영국인이 그를 맞으러 나왔다. 기수 특유의 투박한 걸음걸이로, 팔꿈치를 바깥으로 벌리고 좌우로 몸을 흔들면서.\n\n\"그래, 프루프루는 좀 어떤가?\" 브론스키가 영어로 물었다.\n\n\"괜찮습니다, 나리.\" 영국인의 목소리가 어딘가 목구멍 깊숙한 데서 흘러나왔다. \"안 들어가시는 게 낫겠습니다.\" 그가 모자에 손을 대며 덧붙였다. \"재갈을 물려 놓았는데, 말이 안절부절못합니다. 안 들어가시는 게 낫겠습니다. 들어가시면 말이 흥분합니다.\"\n\n\"아니, 들어갈 거야. 한번 보고 싶군.\"\n\n\"그럼 따라오시지요.\" 영국인이 미간을 찌푸리고 입을 닫은 채 말하더니, 팔꿈치를 흔들며 그 어긋난 걸음걸이로 앞장서 갔다.\n\n그들은 헛간 앞의 작은 마당으로 들어갔다. 나들이옷을 차려입어 말쑥하고 멀끔한 마구간지기 소년이 빗자루를 손에 들고 그들을 맞더니 뒤를 따라왔다. 헛간 안에는 다섯 마리의 말이 칸막이마다 따로 들어 있었고, 브론스키는 자기 최대의 경쟁마인 글라디아토르가, 아주 키가 큰 밤색 말이 그리로 옮겨져 그 사이에 서 있으리라는 것을 알고 있었다. 브론스키는 자기 암말보다도 한 번도 본 적 없는 글라디아토르를 더 보고 싶었다. 하지만 경마장의 예법으로는 그 말을 보는 것이 불가능할 뿐만 아니라, 그에 관해 묻는 것조차 무례한 일이라는 것을 그는 알고 있었다. 그가 마침 통로를 지나가는데, 소년이 왼편 두 번째 마구간 칸의 문을 열었고, 브론스키는 흰 다리를 가진 커다란 밤색 말을 언뜻 보았다. 그는 그것이 글라디아토르임을 알았지만, 남이 펼쳐 놓은 편지에서 눈을 돌리는 사람의 심정으로 몸을 돌려 프루프루의 칸으로 들어갔다.\n\n\"여기 있는 이 말은 마크……마크……, 도무지 그 이름을 부를 수가 없네요.\" 영국인이 어깨 너머로, 굵은 손가락과 더러운 손톱을 글라디아토르의 칸 쪽으로 가리키며 말했다.\n\n\"마호틴 말인가? 그래, 그가 내 가장 만만찮은 경쟁자지.\" 브론스키가 말했다.\n\n\"나리께서 저 말을 타신다면,\" 영국인이 말했다, \"저는 나리께 걸겠습니다.\"\n\n\"프루프루가 더 예민하고, 저쪽이 더 힘이 세지.\" 브론스키가 자기 승마 실력에 대한 칭찬에 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"장애물 경마에서는 모든 게 기수의 솜씨와 배짱에 달려 있지요.\" 영국인이 말했다.\n\n배짱에 대해서라면—그러니까 기력과 용기 말이다—브론스키는 단지 그것이 충분하다고 느끼는 정도가 아니었다. 그보다 훨씬 더 중요한 것은, 세상 누구도 자기보다 이 '배짱'을 더 많이 가질 수는 없으리라고 그가 굳게 확신하고 있다는 점이었다.\n\n\"내가 살을 좀 더 빼야 한다고 보지는 않나?\"\n\n\"오, 아닙니다.\" 영국인이 대답했다. \"제발 큰 소리로 말씀하지 마십시오. 말이 안절부절못합니다.\" 그가 그들이 바로 앞에 서 있는, 안에서 짚을 밟으며 부산하게 발을 구르는 소리가 들려오는 마구간 칸을 향해 고갯짓하며 덧붙였다.\n\n그가 문을 열자 브론스키는 작은 창 하나로 어슴푸레하게 밝은 마구간 칸으로 들어갔다. 칸 안에는 짙은 적갈색 암말이 재갈을 문 채 발굽으로 신선한 짚을 헤집으며 서 있었다. 칸 안의 어스름 속에서 둘러보던 브론스키는 무심결에 한 번 더, 자기가 아끼는 암말의 모든 특징을 한눈에 죽 훑었다. 프루프루는 중간 크기의 짐승으로, 사육가의 눈으로 보면 흠이 전혀 없다고는 할 수 없었다. 온몸의 뼈대가 가늘었고, 가슴은 앞쪽으로 유난히 도드라졌지만 폭이 좁았다. 뒷부분은 다소 처져 있었고, 앞다리에도, 그리고 뒷다리에는 한층 더, 눈에 띄는 굽음이 있었다. 뒷다리와 앞다리의 근육은 그리 굵지 않았다. 하지만 어깨 너비만큼은 이 암말이 유달리 넓었는데, 조련으로 살이 빠진 지금에 와서 그 특징이 특히 두드러져 보였다. 무릎 아래 다리뼈는 앞에서 보면 손가락 굵기밖에 안 되는 듯했지만, 옆에서 보면 유독 굵었다. 어깨 너비를 빼고 보면, 말은 옆구리가 죄어들고 깊이만 짓눌려 늘어난 듯한 모양새였다. 그러나 모든 결점을 잊게 만드는 자질만큼은 그 어떤 말보다도 높은 정도로 지니고 있었다. 그 자질은 곧 혈통이었다. 영어 표현대로 하자면, 그 자체로 말을 해 주는 혈통이었다. 비단처럼 부드럽고 섬세하며 잘 움직이는 살갗에 덮인 힘줄의 그물 아래로 근육이 또렷하게 솟아 있었고, 그것은 뼈처럼 단단했다. 또렷하게 빚어진 그 머리에는 도드라지고 빛나며 생기 넘치는 눈이 박혀 있었고, 벌어진 콧구멍에서 넓어졌는데, 그 안쪽 연골에는 붉은 핏기가 비쳤다. 온몸에서, 특히 머리에서 어떤 기력의 표정이, 그러면서 동시에 부드러움의 표정이 풍겨 났다. 그 말은 입의 구조가 허락하지 않을 뿐, 말을 못 한다는 것만 빼면 거의 사람 같은 그런 짐승의 하나였다.\n\n적어도 브론스키에게는, 자기가 그 순간 말을 바라보며 느끼는 모든 것을 그 말이 다 알아차린 듯이 보였다.\n\n브론스키가 곧장 다가가자, 말은 깊이 숨을 들이쉬더니 도드라진 눈을 뒤로 굴려 흰자위가 충혈된 것처럼 보이도록 한 채, 반대편에서 다가오는 모습에 흠칫 놀라 재갈을 흔들며 이쪽저쪽으로 가볍게 다리를 옮겨 디뎠다.\n\n\"보십시오, 저렇게 안절부절못하지 않습니까.\" 영국인이 말했다.\n\n\"자, 착하지! 자!\" 브론스키가 말에게 다가가며 달래듯 말했다.\n\n하지만 그가 가까이 갈수록 말은 더욱 흥분했다. 그가 말의 머리맡에 섰을 때에야 비로소 말은 갑자기 잠잠해졌고, 다만 부드럽고 섬세한 털가죽 아래로 근육이 떨렸다. 브론스키는 그 단단한 목을 토닥이고, 날카로운 기갑 위로 반대편으로 흘러내린 갈기 한 가닥을 바로잡아 주었으며, 박쥐 날개처럼 투명하게 벌어진 콧구멍 가까이 얼굴을 가져갔다. 말은 큰 소리로 숨을 들이쉬더니 팽팽해진 콧구멍으로 거칠게 콧김을 뿜었고, 흠칫하더니 날카로운 귀를 쫑긋 세우고, 마치 그의 소맷자락을 물어 잡으려는 듯 억세고 검은 입술을 브론스키 쪽으로 내밀었다. 하지만 재갈이 물려 있다는 것을 떠올린 듯 그것을 흔들더니, 다시 안절부절못하며 맵시 좋은 다리를 차례로 굴러 대기 시작했다.\n\n\"가만, 착하지, 가만!\" 그는 다시 한번 말의 뒷부분을 토닥이며 말했다. 그리고 자기 암말이 더할 나위 없이 좋은 상태라는 흐뭇한 기분을 안고 마구간 칸을 나왔다.\n\n말의 흥분이 브론스키에게도 옮아붙었다. 그는 제 심장이 두근거리는 것을, 그리고 자기도 그 말처럼 움직이고 싶고 물어뜯고 싶어진다는 것을 느꼈다. 그것은 끔찍하면서도 황홀한 기분이었다.\n\n\"그럼, 자네만 믿겠네.\" 그가 영국인에게 말했다. \"여섯 시 반에 경마장에서 보세.\"\n\n\"알겠습니다.\" 영국인이 말했다. \"오, 어디로 가십니까, 나리?\" 그가 갑자기 그동안 거의 써 본 적이 없는 '나리'라는 호칭을 쓰며 물었다.\n\n브론스키는 놀라 고개를 들고, 그 질문의 무례함에 어안이 벙벙한 채로, 자기가 곧잘 그러듯 영국인의 눈이 아니라 이마를 빤히 바라보았다. 하지만 이렇게 물으면서 영국인이 자기를 고용주가 아니라 한 사람의 기수로 보고 있었음을 깨닫고는 이렇게 답했다.\n\n\"브랸스키에게 들러야 하네. 한 시간 안에 집에 돌아올 걸세.\"\n\n'오늘 이 질문을 대체 몇 번이나 받는 건지!' 그는 속으로 중얼거렸고, 좀처럼 없던 일이지만 얼굴을 붉혔다. 영국인은 그를 진지하게 바라보더니, 마치 자기도 브론스키가 어디로 가는지 안다는 듯 이렇게 덧붙였다.\n\n\"경기 전에 무엇보다 중요한 건 침착함을 잃지 않는 겁니다.\" 그가 말했다. \"성을 내거나 무슨 일로든 마음을 흩뜨리지 마십시오.\"\n\n\"알겠네.\" 브론스키가 미소 지으며 대답했다. 그러고는 마차에 뛰어올라 페테르고프로 가라고 마부에게 일렀다.\n\n마차가 몇 걸음 채 나아가기도 전에, 온종일 비를 머금고 위협하던 먹구름이 터지면서 굵은 빗줄기가 쏟아져 내렸다.\n\n'아이고, 야단났군!' 브론스키는 마차 지붕을 올리며 생각했다. '전에도 진창이었는데, 이제 완전히 늪이 되겠어.' 닫힌 마차 안에 홀로 앉아, 그는 어머니의 편지와 형의 쪽지를 꺼내어 처음부터 끝까지 읽었다.\n\n그래, 또 그 소리, 똑같은 소리의 되풀이였다. 모두가, 어머니도, 형도, 모두가 자기 마음의 일에 간섭하는 것을 마땅한 일로 여겼다. 이런 간섭은 그의 마음속에 성난 증오의 감정을 불러일으켰다. 전에는 좀처럼 느껴 본 적 없는 감정이었다. '대체 저들과 무슨 상관이람? 어째서 다들 나를 두고 한마디씩 해야 한다고 여기는 거지? 또 어째서 이렇게 나를 못살게 구는 걸까? 그저 이게 자기들이 이해할 수 없는 무엇이라는 걸 알기 때문이야. 만약 이게 흔해 빠진, 천박한 세속의 정사였다면 다들 나를 가만 내버려 두었겠지. 저들은 이게 뭔가 다르다는 걸, 한낱 심심풀이 놀음이 아니라는 걸, 이 여자가 나에게 목숨보다 소중하다는 걸 느끼고 있어. 그래서 이해할 수 없는 거고, 그래서 신경에 거슬리는 거지. 우리 운명이 어떻든, 또 앞으로 어떻게 되든, 그건 우리가 스스로 만든 것이고, 우리는 거기에 대해 불평하지 않아.' 그는 우리라는 말 속에 자기와 안나를 한데 묶으며 생각했다. '아니, 저들은 기어이 우리에게 어떻게 살아야 하는지를 가르치려 들지. 행복이 무엇인지 손톱만큼도 모르면서. 우리의 사랑이 없다면 우리에게는 행복도 불행도 없다는 걸, 삶 자체가 없다는 걸 모르는 거야.' 그는 생각했다.\n\n그가 저들 모두의 간섭에 화가 난 것은, 바로 저들이, 이 모든 사람이 옳다는 것을 마음속으로 느끼고 있었기 때문이었다. 자기를 안나에게 묶어 놓은 사랑이, 세속의 정사가 그러하듯 지나가 버리고 마는, 그래서 두 사람의 삶에 즐겁거나 불쾌한 기억 말고는 아무런 흔적도 남기지 않는 한순간의 충동이 아님을 그는 느끼고 있었다. 그는 자기와 그녀의 처지가 안고 있는 온갖 고통을, 온 세상의 눈에 그토록 두드러진 두 사람이 사랑을 감추고, 거짓말하고 속이는 일에서 겪는 온갖 어려움을 느끼고 있었다. 그리고 둘을 하나로 묶는 정열이 너무도 격렬하여 둘 다 자기들의 사랑 외에는 모든 것을 잊고 마는 마당에, 거짓말하고 속이고 꾸며 대며 끊임없이 남들을 신경 써야 하는 그 어려움을.\n\n거짓말과 속임수가 불가피하게 끊임없이 되풀이되던 온갖 경우가 그의 머릿속에 생생하게 떠올랐다. 그것은 그의 천성에 너무도 어긋나는 것이었다. 특히 생생하게 떠오른 것은, 거짓말하고 속여야 하는 이 필요 앞에서 그녀가 부끄러워하는 모습을 한두 번이 아니게 알아챘던 일이었다. 그리고 안나를 향한 은밀한 사랑이 시작된 뒤로 이따금 그에게 찾아들곤 했던 그 기묘한 감정을 그는 다시 맛보았다. 그것은 무언가에 대한 혐오의 감정이었는데, 그것이 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에 대한 것인지, 자기 자신에 대한 것인지, 아니면 온 세상에 대한 것인지 그로서는 말할 수 없었다. 하지만 그는 늘 이 기묘한 감정을 쫓아 버렸다. 지금도 그는 그것을 떨쳐 내고 다시 생각의 실마리를 이어 갔다.\n\n'그래, 그녀는 전에도 불행했지만, 자긍심을 지녔고 마음이 평온했어. 그런데 지금은 겉으로 드러내지는 않아도 마음의 평온도, 자기 존엄에 대한 안도감도 누리지 못해. 그래, 우리는 이걸 끝내야 해.' 그는 마음을 정했다.\n\n그리고 처음으로 이 거짓된 처지에 끝장을 내야 한다는, 그것도 빠르면 빠를수록 좋다는 생각이 또렷하게 떠올랐다. '모든 걸 내던지고, 그녀와 내가 우리의 사랑과 함께 어딘가 단둘이 숨어 버리는 거야.' 그는 혼잣말을 했다.\n\n# Chapter 22\n\n비는 오래가지 않았고, 브론스키가 도착할 무렵에는, 그의 끌채말이 전속력으로 달리며 고삐를 늘어뜨린 채 진창 속을 질주하는 곁말들을 끌고 오는 동안, 해가 다시 빼꼼 얼굴을 내밀어, 별장들의 지붕이며 큰길 양편 정원에 늘어선 늙은 보리수들이 젖은 채 반짝반짝 빛났고, 잔가지에서는 기분 좋게 물방울이 떨어지고 지붕에서는 물줄기가 콸콸 흘러내렸다. 그는 소나기가 경마장을 망쳐 버린 것 따위는 더 이상 생각하지 않고, 다만 이 비 덕분에 그녀가 분명 집에 혼자 있으리라는 사실에 기뻐하고 있었다. 그는 최근 외국의 온천지에서 돌아온 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 아직 페테르부르크를 떠나지 않았다는 것을 알고 있었기 때문이다.\n\n그녀를 혼자 만날 수 있기를 바라며, 브론스키는 늘 그러듯 사람들의 눈에 띄지 않기 위해 다리를 건너기 전에 마차에서 내려 집까지 걸어갔다. 그는 길 쪽 현관 계단으로 올라가지 않고 안뜰로 들어갔다.\n\n\"주인 나리께서는 돌아오셨나?\" 그가 정원사에게 물었다.\n\n\"아닙니다, 나리. 마님께서는 집에 계십니다. 하지만 부디 정문으로 가 주십시오. 거기에 하인들이 있습니다.\" 정원사가 대답했다. \"그들이 문을 열어 드릴 겁니다.\"\n\n\"아니, 정원 쪽으로 들어가겠네.\"\n\n그리고 그녀가 혼자 있다는 사실에 만족하며, 오늘 오겠다고 약속한 적도 없고 그녀가 경마 전에 자기가 오리라고는 분명 기대하지도 않았으니 그녀를 놀래 주고 싶은 마음에, 그는 칼을 손에 쥐고 꽃이 가장자리에 늘어선 모랫길을 조심스레 밟으며 정원이 내다보이는 테라스를 향해 걸어갔다. 브론스키는 이제 자기들 처지의 고난과 어려움에 대해 오는 길에 생각했던 것을 모조리 잊어버렸다. 그는 다만 곧 그녀를 만나리라는 것, 상상 속에서가 아니라 살아 있는 그녀를, 현실 속의 모습 그대로 온전히 보게 되리라는 것만을 생각했다. 그가 삐걱거리는 소리를 내지 않으려고 발바닥을 온전히 디디며 테라스의 닳은 계단을 올라 막 안으로 들어가려는 순간, 그는 늘 잊고 있다가 문득 떠올리곤 하던 것, 그녀와의 관계에서 가장 고통스러운 측면을 자아내는 것을 별안간 떠올렸다. 그것은 그녀의 아들, 그 무언가를 캐묻는 듯한—그가 보기에는 적대적인—눈빛이었다.\n\n이 소년은 그 누구보다도 자주 그들의 자유에 제동을 거는 존재였다. 아이가 곁에 있을 때면, 브론스키와 안나는 모든 사람 앞에서 되풀이할 수 없는 어떤 것에 대해 말하기를 피하는 정도가 아니라, 아이가 이해하지 못하는 어떤 것을 넌지시 암시하는 것조차 스스로에게 허락하지 않았다. 그들은 이에 대해 아무런 약속도 한 적이 없었으나, 그것은 저절로 그렇게 정해졌다. 그들은 아이를 속이는 것이 곧 자기 자신에게 상처를 입히는 일처럼 느껴졌을 것이다. 아이 앞에서 그들은 지인처럼 이야기했다. 그러나 이런 조심에도 불구하고, 브론스키는 종종 아이의 진지하고 어리둥절한 시선이 자기에게 고정되어 있는 것을, 그리고 그를 대하는 아이의 태도에서 어떤 이상한 수줍음과 망설임, 어느 때는 다정함, 또 어느 때는 차가움과 거리감을 보았다. 마치 이 남자와 어머니 사이에 어떤 중대한 끈이 존재하는데 그 의미를 이해할 수 없다고 아이가 느끼기라도 하는 것 같았다.\n\n사실 아이는 이 관계를 이해할 수 없다고 느꼈고, 이 남자에게 어떤 감정을 품어야 하는지 괴롭도록 애써 보았으나 스스로에게 분명히 밝힐 수 없었다. 모든 감정의 발현에 대한 아이 특유의 예민한 본능으로, 아이는 자기 아버지와 가정교사와 유모가—그들 모두가 브론스키를 그저 싫어하는 정도가 아니라, 비록 그에 대해 결코 아무 말도 하지 않았지만, 공포와 혐오의 눈으로 바라보는 것을, 반면 어머니는 그를 더없이 친한 벗으로 여기는 것을 또렷이 보았다.\n\n\"이게 무슨 뜻일까? 저 사람은 누구지? 나는 저 사람을 어떻게 사랑해야 하는 거지? 내가 모른다면 그건 내 잘못이야. 내가 멍청하거나 못된 아이인 거야.\" 아이는 그렇게 생각했다. 그리고 바로 이것이 아이의 미심쩍고 캐묻는 듯한, 때로는 적대적인 표정과, 브론스키가 그토록 거북하게 여기던 수줍음과 망설임을 자아낸 것이었다. 이 아이의 존재는 언제나 어김없이 브론스키 안에, 최근 들어 그가 겪어 온 그 이상한, 설명할 수 없는 혐오의 감정을 불러일으켰다. 이 아이의 존재는 브론스키와 안나 두 사람 안에, 나침반을 보고서 자기가 빠르게 나아가고 있는 방향이 옳은 방향과는 한참 어긋나 있음을 알면서도 그 움직임을 멈추는 것은 자기 힘으로 어쩔 수 없고, 매 순간이 자기를 점점 더 멀리 데려가고 있으며, 옳은 방향에서 벗어났음을 스스로 인정하는 것은 곧 자신의 확실한 파멸을 인정하는 것과 같다는 사실을 깨달은 뱃사람의 심정과 비슷한 감정을 불러일으켰다.\n\n이 아이는, 삶에 대한 그 순진한 시선으로, 그들이 알고 있으면서도 알고 싶어 하지 않는 그 지점에서 자기들이 얼마나 멀어졌는지를 가리켜 보이는 나침반이었다.\n\n이번에는 세료자가 집에 없었고, 그녀는 완전히 혼자였다. 그녀는 산책을 나갔다가 비를 만난 아들이 돌아오기를 기다리며 테라스에 앉아 있었다. 그녀는 하인 하나와 하녀 하나를 아이를 찾으러 내보낸 터였다. 정교하게 수를 놓은 흰 가운을 입고, 그녀는 테라스 한구석 꽃들 뒤에 앉아 있어 그의 기척을 듣지 못했다. 곱슬곱슬한 검은 머리를 숙인 채, 그녀는 난간 위에 놓인 차가운 물뿌리개에 이마를 댄 채로, 그가 너무도 잘 아는 반지들을 낀 두 아름다운 손으로 그 물뿌리개를 감싸 쥐고 있었다. 그녀의 온몸과 머리와 목과 손이 자아내는 아름다움은 매번 브론스키에게 무언가 새롭고 뜻밖의 것으로 다가왔다. 그는 황홀경에 빠져 그녀를 바라보며 가만히 서 있었다. 그러나 그가 그녀에게 한 걸음 더 다가서려는 바로 그 순간, 그녀는 그의 존재를 알아채고 물뿌리개를 밀어내며 발그레한 얼굴을 그에게로 돌렸다.\n\n\"무슨 일이오? 어디가 아프오?\" 그가 다가가며 프랑스어로 말했다. 그는 그녀에게 달려가고 싶었으나, 보는 눈이 있을지도 모른다는 생각에 발코니 문 쪽을 돌아보고는, 늘 그렇듯 두려워하고 경계해야 한다는 것을 느끼며 살짝 얼굴을 붉혔다.\n\n\"아뇨, 아주 멀쩡해요.\" 그녀가 일어서서 그가 내민 손을 꼭 쥐며 말했다. \"당신이 올 줄은…… 몰랐어요.\"\n\n\"이런! 손이 어쩜 이렇게 차오!\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"당신 때문에 놀랐잖아요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"혼자서 세료자를 기다리고 있었어요. 산책을 나갔거든요. 이쪽으로 들어올 거예요.\"\n\n그러나 침착해지려는 노력에도 불구하고, 그녀의 입술은 떨리고 있었다.\n\n\"이렇게 찾아와서 미안하오. 하지만 당신을 보지 못하고는 하루를 보낼 수가 없었소.\" 그는 늘 그러듯 프랑스어로 계속 말했다. 두 사람 사이에서는 도저히 견딜 수 없을 만큼 쌀쌀맞게 들리는 딱딱한 러시아어 복수형과, 위험할 만큼 다정한 단수형을 쓰지 않기 위해서였다.\n\n\"미안하다뇨? 난 정말 기쁜걸요!\"\n\n\"하지만 당신은 아프거나 무슨 걱정이 있는 거요.\" 그가 그녀의 손을 놓지 않고 몸을 숙이며 말을 이었다. \"무슨 생각을 하고 있었소?\"\n\n\"늘 같은 거죠.\" 그녀가 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n그녀는 진실을 말한 것이었다. 어느 순간이든 무슨 생각을 하고 있느냐는 질문을 받았다면, 그녀는 진실되게 대답할 수 있었을 것이다. 같은 것, 자신의 행복과 자신의 불행에 대해서라고. 그가 불쑥 나타났을 때 그녀가 생각하고 있던 것은 바로 이것이었다. 어째서 다른 사람들에게는, 베치(그녀는 베치가 투시케비치와 은밀히 관계를 맺고 있다는 것을 알고 있었다)에게는 이 모든 것이 그토록 쉬운데, 자기에게는 이렇게나 고통스러운 것일까. 오늘은 어떤 다른 정황들 때문에 이 생각이 특히나 가슴을 후벼 팠다. 그녀는 그에게 경마에 대해 물었다. 그는 그녀의 물음에 답했고, 그녀가 동요하고 있는 것을 보고는, 그녀를 진정시키려고 더없이 담담한 어조로 경마를 위한 자신의 준비 과정을 자세히 이야기하기 시작했다.\n\n'말할까, 말까?' 그녀는 그의 고요하고 다정한 눈을 들여다보며 생각했다. '저이는 너무나 행복해하고, 너무나 경마에 빠져 있어서, 마땅히 이해해야 할 만큼 이해하지 못할 거야. 이 사실이 우리에게 얼마나 중대한 일인지 깨닫지 못할 거야.'\n\n\"그런데 당신은 내가 들어왔을 때 무슨 생각을 하고 있었는지 아직 말해 주지 않았소.\" 그가 자기 이야기를 끊으며 말했다. \"제발 말해 주오!\"\n\n그녀는 대답하지 않고 고개를 약간 숙인 채, 긴 속눈썹 아래 빛나는 눈으로 눈썹 밑에서 미심쩍게 그를 바라보았다. 그녀가 따 든 잎사귀를 만지작거리는 손이 떨렸다. 그는 그것을 보았고, 그의 얼굴에는 그녀의 마음을 사로잡는 데 그토록 큰 몫을 한 그 온전한 복종, 그 노예 같은 헌신이 떠올랐다.\n\n\"무슨 일이 있었군. 당신이 나와 나누지 않는 어떤 괴로움을 안고 있는데, 내가 마음 편할 수 있을 것 같소? 제발, 부디 말해 주오.\" 그가 애원하듯 거듭 말했다.\n\n'그래, 저이가 이 일의 중대함을 모조리 깨닫지 못한다면 난 저이를 용서할 수 없을 거야. 차라리 말하지 말까. 어째서 저이를 시험에 들게 하는 거지?' 그녀는 여전히 같은 눈빛으로 그를 응시하며, 잎사귀를 쥔 손이 점점 더 떨리는 것을 느끼면서 생각했다.\n\n\"제발 부탁이오!\" 그가 그녀의 손을 잡으며 거듭 말했다.\n\n\"말해 드릴까요?\"\n\n\"그래, 그래요, 어서……\"\n\n\"나 아이를 가졌어요.\" 그녀가 부드럽고 또박또박 말했다. 그녀의 손에 든 잎사귀가 더 격하게 떨렸으나, 그녀는 그가 이 말을 어떻게 받아들이는지 지켜보며 그에게서 눈을 떼지 않았다. 그는 새파랗게 질려 무어라 말하려다가 멈추었고, 그녀의 손을 놓더니 고개를 가슴께로 떨구었다. '그래, 저이는 이 일의 중대함을 모조리 깨달은 거야.' 그녀는 생각하며 고맙다는 듯 그의 손을 꼭 쥐었다.\n\n그러나 그가 여자인 그녀가 깨달은 것처럼 이 사실의 중대함을 깨달았으리라고 생각한 것은 그녀의 착각이었다. 그 말을 듣자, 그는 누군가를 향한 그 이상한 혐오의 감정이 열 배의 강도로 자기에게 밀려드는 것을 느꼈다. 그러나 동시에, 그가 그토록 갈망해 온 전환점이 이제 찾아왔다는 것을, 그녀의 남편에게 일들을 계속 숨기는 것은 불가능하며, 어떤 식으로든 머지않아 이 부자연스러운 처지에 종지부를 찍어야 한다는 것이 불가피하다는 것을 느꼈다. 게다가 그것 말고도, 그녀의 격정은 똑같은 방식으로 그에게도 육체적인 영향을 미쳤다. 그는 순종적인 다정함이 담긴 눈길로 그녀를 바라보고는, 그녀의 손에 입을 맞추고 일어나 말없이 테라스를 이리저리 거닐었다.\n\n\"그래.\" 그가 단호하게 그녀에게 다가서며 말했다. \"당신도 나도 우리 관계를 한때의 유희로 여긴 적이 없소. 이제 우리의 운명은 정해졌소. 우리가 살아가고 있는 이 기만에\"—그는 말하면서 주위를 둘러보았다—\"반드시 종지부를 찍어야만 하오.\"\n\n\"종지부를 찍는다고요? 어떻게 종지부를 찍죠, 알렉세이?\" 그녀가 부드럽게 말했다.\n\n그녀는 이제 한결 차분해져, 그녀의 얼굴이 다정한 미소로 환해졌다.\n\n\"당신 남편을 떠나서 우리의 삶을 하나로 합칩시다.\"\n\n\"지금 이대로도 하나인걸요.\" 그녀가 거의 들리지 않을 만큼 작게 대답했다.\n\n\"그래, 하지만 완전히 말이오. 완전히.\"\n\n\"하지만 어떻게요, 알렉세이, 어떻게 하면 되는지 말해 줘요.\" 그녀가 자기 처지의 가망 없음을 향한 우울한 조소를 담아 말했다. \"이런 처지에서 빠져나갈 길이 있긴 한가요? 난 내 남편의 아내가 아닌가요?\"\n\n\"어떤 처지에서든 빠져나갈 길은 있소. 우리는 우리의 길을 택해야 하오.\" 그가 말했다. \"당신이 지금 살아가고 있는 처지보다는 무엇이든 더 나을 거요. 물론 나도 당신이 온갖 것을 두고 스스로를 괴롭히고 있다는 걸 알고 있소. 세상이며, 당신 아들이며, 당신 남편이며.\"\n\n\"오, 남편을 두고는 아니에요.\" 그녀가 조용한 미소를 띠며 말했다. \"난 그 사람을 몰라요. 그 사람 생각은 하지 않아요. 그 사람은 존재하지 않아요.\"\n\n\"당신은 진심으로 말하는 게 아니오. 난 당신을 알아. 당신은 그 사람도 걱정하고 있소.\"\n\n\"오, 그이는 알지도 못하는걸요.\" 그녀가 말했고, 별안간 그녀의 얼굴에 뜨거운 홍조가 번졌다. 그녀의 뺨과 이마와 목이 새빨개졌고, 수치의 눈물이 그녀의 눈에 차올랐다. \"하지만 그 사람 이야기는 하지 말아요.\"\n\n# Chapter 23\n\n브론스키는 이미 여러 번, 지금처럼 단호하게는 아니었으나, 그녀에게 두 사람의 처지를 직시하도록 만들려고 애써 왔다. 그러나 그때마다 그는 지금 그녀가 자신의 호소를 받아넘기는 것과 똑같은 피상성과 경박함에 부딪혀 왔다. 마치 거기에는 그녀가 마주할 수 없거나 마주하려 하지 않는 무언가가 있는 것 같았고, 그녀가 이 문제를 입에 올리기 시작하면 진짜 안나는 어쩐 일인지 자기 안으로 물러나 버리고, 그가 사랑하지 않으며 두려워하고 또 그에게 맞서는, 어딘가 낯설고 알 수 없는 다른 여자가 나오는 것만 같았다. 그러나 오늘 그는 끝장을 보기로 작정하고 있었다.\n\n\"그가 알든 모르든,\" 브론스키는 평소의 차분하고 단호한 어조로 말했다. \"그건 우리와는 상관없는 일이오. 우리는…… 당신은 이런 식으로 지낼 수 없소, 특히 지금은.\"\n\n\"그럼 당신 생각에는 어떻게 해야 하죠?\" 그녀는 여전히 경박한 빈정거림을 담아 물었다. 그가 자기 처지를 너무 가볍게 받아들일까 봐 그토록 두려워했던 그녀가, 이제는 그가 거기서 어떤 조치를 취해야 한다는 결론을 끌어낸다고 그에게 짜증을 부리고 있었다.\n\n\"그에게 전부 털어놓고, 그를 떠나시오.\"\n\n\"좋아요, 내가 그렇게 한다고 쳐요,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그러면 그 결과가 어떻게 될지 아세요? 내가 미리 전부 말해 줄 수 있어요.\" 그러자 조금 전까지만 해도 그토록 부드럽던 그녀의 눈에 사악한 빛이 번뜩였다. \"'아, 당신은 다른 남자를 사랑해서 그와 죄스러운 밀회를 맺었단 말이지?'\" (남편을 흉내 내며 그녀는 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 하던 것처럼 '죄스러운'이라는 말에 힘을 주었다.) \"'나는 종교적으로나, 시민적으로나, 가정적으로나 그 결과를 당신에게 경고했소. 당신은 내 말을 듣지 않았소. 이제 나는 당신이 내 이름을 더럽히도록 둘 수 없소—'\" '그리고 내 아들을'이라고 그녀는 말하려 했지만, 아들에 대해서는 농담할 수가 없었다—\"'내 이름을 더럽히게 둘 수 없고, 또—' 뭐 이런 식으로 더 이어지겠죠.\" 그녀가 덧붙였다. \"한마디로, 그는 그 관청식 말투로, 더할 나위 없이 또렷하고 정확하게, 나를 보내 줄 수 없으며 추문을 막기 위해 자기 권한 안에서 할 수 있는 모든 조치를 취하겠다고 말할 거예요. 그러고는 자기가 한 말 그대로 침착하고 빈틈없이 행동하겠죠. 그게 벌어질 일이에요. 그는 사람이 아니라 기계예요, 화가 나면 심술궂은 기계라고요.\" 그녀는 이렇게 말하면서 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 몸매며 말투의 온갖 특이한 점까지 죄다 떠올렸고, 자기가 그에게 저지르고 있는 크나큰 잘못은 조금도 누그러뜨리지 않은 채, 그에게서 찾아낼 수 있는 결점이란 결점은 모조리 그의 탓으로 헤아렸다.\n\n\"하지만 안나,\" 브론스키가 그녀를 달래려고 부드럽고 설득하는 목소리로 말했다. \"어쨌든 우리는 무슨 일이 있어도 그에게 말해야 하고, 그런 다음 그가 취하는 태도에 따라 움직여야 하오.\"\n\n\"뭐라고요, 달아나자고요?\"\n\n\"달아나면 왜 안 되오? 나는 우리가 이런 식으로 계속할 수 있다고 보지 않소. 나 자신을 위해서가 아니오—나는 당신이 괴로워하는 걸 보고 있단 말이오.\"\n\n\"그래요, 달아나서 당신의 정부가 되라는 거군요.\" 그녀가 화를 내며 말했다.\n\n\"안나.\" 그가 책망하는 듯한 다정함을 담아 말했다.\n\n\"그래요,\" 그녀가 계속했다. \"당신의 정부가 되고, 그리고 완전히 망쳐 버리는 거죠…….\"\n\n또다시 그녀는 '내 아들을'이라고 말하려 했지만, 그 말을 입 밖에 낼 수가 없었다.\n\n브론스키는 그녀처럼 강하고 진실한 천성을 지닌 여자가 어떻게 이런 기만의 상태를 견디면서 거기서 벗어나기를 갈망하지 않을 수 있는지 이해할 수가 없었다. 그러나 그는 그 주된 원인이 그녀가 차마 입에 올리지 못하는 말—아들이라는 단어에 있다는 것을 짐작하지 못했다. 자기 아들을, 그리고 아버지를 버린 어머니에 대한 아들의 장차의 태도를 떠올릴 때면, 그녀는 자기가 저지른 일에 너무도 큰 공포를 느껴 그것을 마주할 수 없었다. 다만 여자답게, 모든 것이 늘 그래 왔던 그대로 남아 있으리라는, 그리고 아들이 어떻게 될 것인가 하는 무서운 물음을 잊는 것이 가능하리라는 거짓된 다짐으로 스스로를 위로하려 애쓸 뿐이었다.\n\n\"부탁이에요, 간청할게요,\" 그녀가 그의 손을 잡고 사뭇 다른, 진실하고 다정한 어조로 갑자기 말했다. \"절대로 내게 그 얘기는 하지 말아 줘요!\"\n\n\"하지만 안나…….\"\n\n\"절대로요. 그건 내게 맡겨 둬요. 나는 내 처지의 온갖 비열함과 온갖 끔찍함을 다 알아요. 하지만 그건 당신이 생각하는 것처럼 그렇게 쉽게 정리되는 게 아니에요. 그러니 내게 맡기고, 내가 말하는 대로 해 줘요. 절대로 그 얘기는 내게 하지 말아요. 약속해 주겠어요?…… 아니, 아니, 약속해요!……\"\n\n\"무엇이든 약속하겠소. 하지만 나는 마음이 편할 수가 없소, 특히 당신이 방금 내게 한 말을 듣고는. 당신이 편치 못한데 내가 어떻게 편할 수 있겠소…….\"\n\n\"내가요?\" 그녀가 되물었다. \"그래요, 가끔은 나도 괴로워요. 하지만 당신이 이 얘기를 절대 꺼내지 않는다면 그건 지나갈 거예요. 당신이 그 얘기를 할 때—오직 그럴 때만 내가 괴로워지는 거니까요.\"\n\n\"이해할 수가 없군.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"알아요,\" 그녀가 그의 말을 가로막았다. \"당신의 진실한 천성으로는 거짓말하는 게 얼마나 힘든지를요. 그래서 나는 당신이 가여워요. 나는 당신이 나 때문에 당신의 인생 전부를 망쳐 버렸다고 자주 생각해요.\"\n\n\"나도 방금 똑같은 생각을 하고 있었소,\" 그가 말했다. \"어떻게 당신이 나를 위해 모든 것을 희생할 수 있단 말이오? 당신이 불행하다는 게 나는 나 자신을 용서할 수가 없소!\"\n\n\"내가 불행하다고요?\" 그녀가 그에게 더 가까이 다가오며, 사랑의 황홀한 미소를 띠고 그를 바라보며 말했다. \"나는 굶주리다가 음식을 받은 사람 같아요. 그는 추위에 떨고, 누더기를 걸치고, 부끄러울지 몰라도, 불행하진 않아요. 내가 불행하다고요? 아니에요, 이게 바로 나의 불행이라면…….\"\n\n자기 쪽으로 다가오는 아들의 목소리가 들려오자, 그녀는 테라스를 재빨리 둘러보고는 충동적으로 일어섰다. 그녀의 눈은 그가 너무도 잘 아는 그 불꽃으로 타올랐다. 그녀는 재빠른 동작으로 반지를 잔뜩 낀 그 사랑스러운 두 손을 들어 올려 그의 머리를 감싸 쥐고, 그의 얼굴을 오래도록 들여다보다가, 미소를 띤 채 살짝 벌어진 입술로 얼굴을 들어 올려, 잽싸게 그의 입과 두 눈에 입을 맞추고는 그를 밀어냈다. 그대로 가려 했지만 그가 그녀를 붙들었다.\n\n\"언제?\" 그가 황홀하게 그녀를 바라보며 속삭이듯 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"오늘 밤, 한 시에요.\" 그녀가 속삭였다. 그러고는 무겁게 한숨을 내쉬며, 가볍고 빠른 걸음으로 아들을 맞으러 걸어갔다.\n\n세료자는 큰 정원에서 비를 만나, 유모와 함께 정자에 몸을 피하고 있었다.\n\n\"그럼, au revoir.\" 그녀가 브론스키에게 말했다. \"곧 경마 갈 채비를 해야 해요. 벳시가 데리러 오기로 했거든요.\"\n브론스키는 회중시계를 보고는 황급히 자리를 떴다.\n\n* au revoir: 프랑스어로 '안녕히 가세요, 또 봐요'.\n\n# Chapter 24\n\n브론스키가 카레닌 집의 발코니에서 시계를 보았을 때, 그는 너무나 흥분한 데다 생각에 잠겨 있어서 문자판의 숫자는 눈에 들어왔으나 몇 시인지 도무지 헤아릴 수가 없었다. 그는 큰길로 나와 진흙탕을 조심조심 골라 디디며 마차 쪽으로 걸어갔다. 안나에 대한 자기 감정에 온통 빠져 있던 터라, 지금이 몇 시인지, 브랸스키네 집에 갈 시간이 있는지조차 생각하지 못했다. 흔히 그러하듯, 그에게 남은 것은 기억의 외적 능력뿐이어서, 그것이 다음에 내디뎌야 할 발걸음을 하나씩 하나씩 일러줄 따름이었다. 그는 벌써 길게 늘어진 그늘 아래, 우거진 보리수 그늘 속 마부석에서 졸고 있는 마부에게 다가갔다. 그는 더운 말들 위를 맴도는 깔따구 떼의 변화무쌍한 구름을 흐뭇하게 바라보다가, 마부를 깨워 마차에 뛰어올라서는 브랸스키네 집으로 가자고 일렀다. 거의 오 베르스타쯤 달리고 나서야 그는 충분히 정신을 차려 시계를 보았고, 시각이 다섯 시 반이며 자기가 늦었다는 것을 깨달았다.\n\n그날은 경마가 여러 차례 예정되어 있었다. 기마 친위대 경주, 그다음에는 장교들의 일 마일 반 경주, 그다음에는 삼 마일 경주, 그리고 그가 출전하는 경주였다. 자기 경주에는 아직 늦지 않을 수 있었지만, 브랸스키네 집에 들렀다가는 간신히 시간을 맞출 뿐이고, 도착할 무렵이면 궁정 사람들은 모두 제자리에 앉아 있을 터였다. 그건 유감스러운 일이었다. 그러나 그는 브랸스키에게 가겠다고 약속했던 터라, 그대로 마차를 몰기로 하고 마부에게 말을 아끼지 말라고 일렀다.\n\n그는 브랸스키네 집에 도착해 거기서 오 분을 보내고 다시 달려 돌아왔다. 이 빠른 질주가 그를 진정시켰다. 안나와의 관계에서 괴로웠던 모든 것, 두 사람의 대화가 남긴 막막한 감정이 죄다 그의 마음에서 빠져나가 버렸다. 그는 이제 즐거움과 흥분 속에서 경주에 대해, 어쨌든 늦지 않게 도착하리라는 데 대해 생각했고, 이따금 그날 밤 자기를 기다리는 더없이 행복한 밀회의 상상이 타오르는 불빛처럼 머릿속을 스쳐 지나갔다.\n\n다가오는 경주의 흥분이 점점 그를 사로잡았다. 그가 별장지에서, 또는 페테르부르크에서 올라오는 마차들을 앞질러 가며 경마장의 분위기 속으로 자꾸만 깊이 들어갈수록 그러했다.\n\n그의 거처에는 아무도 남아 있지 않았다. 다들 경마장에 가 있었고, 하인만 대문에서 그를 기다리고 있었다. 그가 옷을 갈아입는 동안, 하인은 두 번째 경주가 벌써 시작되었으며, 여러 신사분이 그를 찾아왔었고, 마구간에서 소년이 두 번이나 달려왔다고 일러주었다. 서두르지 않고 옷을 갈아입은 (그는 결코 서두르는 법이 없었고, 침착함을 잃는 법도 없었다) 브론스키는 마차를 몰아 마구간으로 향했다. 마구간에서는 마차들의 망망한 바다며, 도보로 온 사람들이며, 경마장을 둘러싼 병사들이며, 사람들로 우글거리는 관람석이 보였다. 두 번째 경주가 진행 중인 모양으로, 그가 마구간으로 들어서는 바로 그 순간 종소리가 울리는 게 들렸다. 마구간 쪽으로 가다가, 그는 푸른 사료용 마포를 두르고 푸른 테를 두른 거대한 귀처럼 보이는 것을 단 채 경마장으로 끌려가는, 흰 다리의 밤색 말 마호틴의 글라디아토르와 마주쳤다.\n\n\"코드는 어디 있나?\" 그가 마구간지기 소년에게 물었다.\n\n\"마구간에서 안장을 얹고 있습니다.\"\n\n열린 마방 안에는 안장을 다 채운 프루프루가 서 있었다. 막 끌어내려는 참이었다.\n\n\"내가 너무 늦진 않았나?\"\n\n\"괜찮습니다! 괜찮아요!\" 영국인이 말했다. \"마음 졸이지 마십시오!\"\n\n브론스키는 온몸을 부르르 떨고 있는 자기 애마의 절묘한 선을 한 번 더 한눈에 담았고, 애써 그 모습에서 시선을 떼어내고는 마구간을 나섰다. 그는 주목을 피하기에 더없이 적당한 순간에 관람석 쪽으로 갔다. 일 마일 반 경주가 막 끝나가던 참이라, 모든 눈은 결승점 가까이에서 마지막 안간힘으로 말을 몰아대는, 앞선 기마 친위병과 그 뒤의 가벼운 경기병에게 쏠려 있었다. 트랙 안쪽과 바깥쪽에서 사람들이 모조리 결승점으로 몰려들었고, 한 무리의 기마 친위대 병사들과 장교들이 자기네 장교이자 동료의 승리를 기대하며 환희에 차서 큰 소리로 외쳐대고 있었다. 브론스키는 경주의 종료를 알리는 종이 울린 바로 그 순간, 키가 크고 진흙을 뒤집어쓴 채 일등으로 들어온 기마 친위병이 안장 위로 몸을 굽히며, 땀에 젖어 거뭇해 보이는 헐떡이는 잿빛 말의 고삐를 늦추는 그 순간에 거의 맞춰, 사람들 사이로 눈에 띄지 않게 끼어들었다.\n\n말은 다리를 뻣뻣이 뻗으며 안간힘으로 그 빠른 질주를 멈추었고, 기마 친위대 장교는 깊은 잠에서 깨어나는 사람처럼 주위를 둘러보더니 간신히 미소를 지었다. 친구들과 구경꾼들이 그를 빙 둘러쌌다.\n\n브론스키는 관람석 앞에서 점잖게, 자유로이 거닐며 이야기를 나누는 그 상류 사회의 선택받은 무리를 일부러 피했다. 그는 그곳에 카레니나 부인이, 또 벳시가, 그리고 형수가 있다는 것을 알았으나, 무언가에 주의를 빼앗길까 두려워 일부러 그들에게 가까이 가지 않았다. 그러나 그는 끊임없이 아는 사람들과 마주쳐 발이 묶였고, 그들은 그에게 앞서의 경주들에 관해 이야기하며 왜 그렇게 늦었느냐고 자꾸 물었다.\n\n기수들이 상을 받으러 관람석으로 가야 할 때가 되어 모든 시선이 그쪽으로 쏠렸을 무렵, 브론스키의 형 알렉산드르가 다가왔다. 묵직한 술 장식을 단 견장을 단 대령이었다. 그는 키가 크지는 않았으나 알렉세이만큼 어깨가 떡 벌어졌고, 동생보다 더 잘생기고 혈색이 좋았다. 코가 붉었고, 트인 데다 술에 취한 듯한 얼굴이었다.\n\n\"내 쪽지 받았나?\" 그가 말했다. \"도무지 너를 찾을 수가 있어야지.\"\n\n알렉산드르 브론스키는 방탕한 생활, 특히 그가 악명을 떨치던 술버릇에도 불구하고 어엿한 궁정 사람의 한 명이었다.\n\n지금 그는 동생에게 몹시 불쾌할 게 분명한 일을 이야기하면서, 여러 사람의 눈이 자기에게 쏠려 있을지 모른다는 것을 알았기에, 마치 동생과 사소한 일로 농담이라도 주고받는 듯 줄곧 웃는 얼굴을 유지했다.\n\n\"받았어요. 그런데 형이 대체 뭘 그렇게 안달하시는지 정말 모르겠네요.\" 알렉세이가 말했다.\n\n\"내가 안달하는 건 말이다, 방금 누가 나한테 네가 여기 없었다고, 월요일에 페테르고프에서 너를 봤다고 말했기 때문이야.\"\n\n\"오직 직접 관련된 당사자들만 상관할 일들이 있는 법이고, 형이 그렇게 걱정하는 그 일도……\"\n\n\"그래, 하지만 그렇다면 차라리 군복무를 그만두는 게 낫겠지……\"\n\n\"제발 참견하지 마시기를 부탁드립니다. 제가 할 말은 그뿐이에요.\"\n\n알렉세이 브론스키의 찌푸린 얼굴이 하얗게 질렸고, 좀처럼 없는 일이지만 튀어나온 아래턱이 떨렸다. 그는 마음이 몹시 따뜻한 사람이라 좀체 화를 내지 않았으나, 일단 화가 나서 턱이 떨릴 때면, 알렉산드르 브론스키도 알다시피, 위험한 사람이었다. 알렉산드르 브론스키는 명랑하게 웃었다.\n\n\"난 그저 어머니 편지를 전해주려던 것뿐이야. 답장이나 하고, 경주를 코앞에 두고서 아무 걱정도 하지 마라. Bonne chance.\" 그는 웃으며 이렇게 덧붙이고는 동생에게서 멀어졌다. 그러나 그가 떠나자마자 또 다른 친밀한 인사가 브론스키의 발걸음을 멈춰 세웠다.\n\n* Bonne chance: 프랑스어로 '행운을 빈다'.\n\"친구도 못 알아보는군! 안녕한가, mon cher?\" 스테판 아르카지치가 말했다. 그는 모스크바에서 그랬듯이 페테르부르크의 온갖 화려함 한가운데서도 유난히 눈부셨고, 얼굴은 발그레했으며, 구레나룻은 매끈하게 윤이 났다. \"어제 올라왔는데, 자네의 승리를 보게 되어 기쁘이. 우리 언제 만날까?\"\n\n* mon cher: 프랑스어로 '여보게', '친애하는 친구'.\n\"내일 장교 식당으로 오게.\" 브론스키는 이렇게 말하고는 미안하다며 그의 외투 소매를 한번 꼭 쥐고서, 대장애물 경주를 위해 말들이 끌려 나오고 있는 경마장 한복판으로 걸어갔다.\n\n지난 경주에서 달린 말들은 김을 내뿜으며 지쳐 마구간지기 소년들에게 이끌려 돌아가고 있었고, 다가오는 경주에 나설 싱싱한 말들이 하나둘 모습을 드러냈다. 대부분 마포를 두른 영국산 경주마였고, 홀쭉하게 당겨진 배 때문에 기이하고 거대한 새처럼 보였다. 오른편으로 프루프루가 끌려 들어왔다. 야위었으나 아름다웠고, 마치 용수철에 떠밀리듯 탄력 있고 다소 긴 발목을 들어 올리고 있었다. 그 멀지 않은 곳에서 사람들이 늘어진 귀의 글라디아토르에게서 마포를 벗기고 있었다. 굵직하고 절묘하며 더할 나위 없이 반듯한 수말의 선, 훌륭한 뒷다리와 발굽에 거의 닿을 만큼 지나치게 짧은 발목이 본의 아니게 브론스키의 시선을 끌었다. 그는 자기 애마에게 다가가려 했으나, 또다시 아는 사람에게 붙들렸다.\n\n\"오, 저기 카레닌이 있군!\" 그와 잡담을 나누던 지인이 말했다. \"아내를 찾는 모양인데, 부인은 관람석 한가운데 있어. 자네는 못 봤나?\"\n\n\"못 봤네.\" 브론스키는 이렇게 대답하고는, 친구가 카레니나 부인을 가리키는 관람석 쪽으로 눈길 한 번 돌리지 않은 채 자기 애마에게 다가갔다.\n\n브론스키가 무언가 지시를 내려야 할 안장을 미처 살펴볼 새도 없이, 출전자들에게 번호와 출발선의 자리를 받으러 관람석으로 오라는 호출이 떨어졌다. 진지하고 엄숙한 표정의, 더러는 창백한 얼굴을 한 열일곱 명의 장교가 관람석에 모여 번호를 뽑았다. 브론스키는 칠 번을 뽑았다. \"탑승!\" 하는 외침이 들렸다.\n\n다른 출전자들과 더불어 자기가 모든 시선이 쏠리는 중심이라는 것을 느끼며, 브론스키는 평소 신경이 곤두설 때면 으레 그러하듯 동작이 한결 신중하고 침착해지는 그 상태로 자기 애마에게 걸어갔다. 코드는 경주를 기리는 뜻에서 가장 좋은 옷을 차려입었다. 단추를 끝까지 채운 검은 외투, 빳빳하게 풀 먹여 양 볼을 떠받치는 깃, 둥근 검은 모자, 그리고 목이 긴 장화였다. 그는 여느 때처럼 차분하고 위엄 있게, 프루프루 앞에 똑바로 서서 두 손으로 양쪽 고삐를 잡고 있었다. 프루프루는 여전히 열병에 걸린 듯 떨고 있었다. 불꽃이 가득 담긴 그 눈이 곁눈질로 브론스키를 흘낏 보았다. 브론스키는 손가락을 뱃대끈 밑으로 밀어 넣었다. 암말이 그를 비스듬히 흘겨보더니 입술을 추켜올리고 귀를 쫑긋거렸다. 영국인은 누군가 자기가 채운 안장을 검사하더라도 좋다는 뜻으로 미소를 지으려는 양 입술을 오므렸다.\n\n\"올라타십시오. 그러면 그렇게 흥분되지 않으실 겁니다.\"\n\n브론스키는 마지막으로 경쟁자들을 둘러보았다. 경주 중에는 그들을 보지 못하리라는 것을 알았다. 둘은 벌써 출발 지점을 향해 말을 몰고 나아가고 있었다. 브론스키의 친구이자 더 만만찮은 경쟁자 중 하나인 갈친은 도무지 자기를 태워주지 않으려는 밤색 말 주위를 빙빙 돌고 있었다. 꽉 끼는 승마 바지를 입은 자그마한 경기병 하나가, 영국 기수를 흉내 내어 고양이처럼 안장 위에 잔뜩 웅크린 채 질주해 나갔다. 쿠조블레프 공작은 그라보프스키 종마장의 순종 암말 위에 하얗게 질린 얼굴로 앉아 있었고, 영국인 마부가 굴레를 잡고 끌어주고 있었다. 브론스키와 그의 동료들은 모두 쿠조블레프와 그 특유의 \"약한 신경\"과 끔찍한 허영심을 알고 있었다. 그들은 그가 모든 것을 두려워하며, 사나운 말을 타는 것을 두려워한다는 것을 알았다. 그러나 지금 그는, 바로 그것이 무시무시하기 때문에, 사람들이 목이 부러지고, 장애물마다 의사가 서 있고, 십자가 표시가 된 구급마차와 자비의 수녀가 있기 때문에, 경주에 참가하기로 마음먹은 것이었다. 두 사람의 눈이 마주쳤고, 브론스키는 다정하게 격려하는 뜻으로 그에게 고개를 끄덕여 보였다. 다만 한 사람, 그의 가장 큰 경쟁자인 글라디아토르를 탄 마호틴만은 보지 못했다.\n\n\"서두르지 마십시오.\" 코드가 브론스키에게 말했다. \"그리고 한 가지만 기억하세요. 울타리 앞에서 말을 잡아당기지 말고, 몰아붙이지도 마십시오. 제멋대로 가도록 놓아두세요.\"\n\n\"알았네, 알았어.\" 브론스키는 고삐를 잡으며 말했다.\n\n\"가능하면 선두에서 달리십시오. 하지만 뒤처지더라도 마지막 순간까지 낙심하지 마세요.\"\n\n암말이 채 움직일 새도 없이, 브론스키는 날렵하고 힘찬 동작으로 톱니 달린 강철 등자에 발을 디뎠고, 삐걱거리는 가죽 안장 위에 가뿐하면서도 단단하게 올라앉았다. 오른발을 등자에 끼우고는 늘 하던 대로 손가락 사이로 이중 고삐를 매만졌고, 코드가 손을 놓았다.\n\n마치 어느 발을 먼저 내디뎌야 할지 모르는 듯 프루프루는 긴 목으로 고삐를 잡아당기며 출발했고, 마치 용수철 위에 있는 것처럼 제 기수를 이쪽저쪽으로 흔들어댔다. 코드는 걸음을 빨리하여 그 뒤를 따랐다. 흥분한 암말은 먼저 한쪽으로, 다음엔 다른 쪽으로 기수를 떨쳐내려 애쓰며 고삐를 잡아당겼고, 브론스키는 목소리와 손으로 달래보았으나 헛일이었다.\n\n그들은 출발 지점으로 가는 길에 둑을 쌓아 막은 개울에 막 다다르고 있었다. 기수 몇은 앞에, 몇은 뒤에 있었는데, 그때 별안간 브론스키는 자기 뒤에서 진흙탕을 박차며 질주하는 말발굽 소리를 들었고, 흰 다리에 늘어진 귀의 글라디아토르를 탄 마호틴에게 추월당했다. 마호틴은 긴 이를 드러내며 웃었으나, 브론스키는 화난 눈으로 그를 바라보았다. 그는 그를 좋아하지 않았고, 지금은 가장 만만찮은 경쟁자로 여기고 있었다. 그는 마호틴이 곁을 스쳐 질주하며 제 암말을 흥분시킨 데 화가 났다. 프루프루는 왼발을 앞세워 질주로 들어서더니, 두 번 껑충 뛰고는 팽팽해진 고삐에 안달하며 덜컹거리는 속보로 옮겨 가, 기수를 위아래로 들썩거리게 했다. 코드도 얼굴을 찌푸리며 거의 속보에 가까운 걸음으로 브론스키를 뒤따랐다.\n\n# Chapter 25\n\n이 경주에는 모두 열일곱 명의 장교가 참가했다. 경주로는 관람석 앞에 펼쳐진, 타원형으로 둘레가 삼 마일에 이르는 커다란 코스였다. 이 코스에는 아홉 개의 장애물이 마련되어 있었다. 시냇물, 관람석 바로 앞에 놓인 높이 오 피트의 크고 견고한 장벽, 마른 도랑, 물이 가득 찬 도랑, 가파른 비탈, 아일랜드식 방벽(가장 까다로운 장애물 가운데 하나로, 잡목으로 둘러싼 둔덕 뒤에 말의 눈에는 보이지 않는 도랑이 있어서, 말이 두 장애물을 한꺼번에 뛰어넘지 못하면 죽을 수도 있었다), 그다음 물이 가득 찬 도랑 두 개와 마른 도랑 하나가 더 있었고, 경주의 종착점은 바로 관람석 정면이었다. 그러나 경주는 그 둘레에서가 아니라 거기서 이백 야드 떨어진 곳에서 시작되었고, 코스의 그 부분에 첫 번째 장애물, 즉 너비 칠 피트의 둑을 쌓아 만든 시냇물이 있었는데, 경주자들은 그것을 뛰어넘든 헤치고 건너든 마음대로 택할 수 있었다.\n\n세 번이나 출발하려고 줄을 맞춰 섰지만, 그때마다 어떤 말이 대열에서 비어져 나와 다시 시작해야 했다. 출발 신호를 맡은 심판인 세스트린 대령은 슬슬 화를 내기 시작했고, 마침내 네 번째에 \"출발!\"이라고 외치자 경주자들이 내달렸다.\n\n그들이 출발선에 늘어선 순간, 모든 눈과 모든 오페라글라스가 화려한 색깔의 기수 무리에게로 향했다.\n\n\"출발했어! 시작했어!\" 숨죽인 기대의 정적이 흐른 뒤, 사방에서 이런 소리가 들렸다.\n\n그리고 관중 가운데 작은 무리들과 외따로 선 사람들이 더 잘 보려고 이곳저곳으로 뛰어다니기 시작했다. 첫 순간에 빽빽이 모여 있던 기수 무리가 흩어졌고, 그들이 두셋씩 무리를 짓거나 한 사람씩 앞뒤로 늘어선 채 시냇물로 다가가는 것이 보였다. 구경꾼들에게는 그들이 모두 동시에 출발한 것처럼 보였지만, 경주자들에게는 그들에게 큰 의미가 있는 몇 초의 차이가 있었다.\n\n흥분한 데다 지나치게 예민해진 프루프루는 첫 순간을 놓쳤고, 몇 마리의 말이 그녀보다 앞서 출발했지만, 시냇물에 이르기 전에 브론스키는 고삐를 잡아당기는 암말을 온 힘을 다해 제어하면서 어렵지 않게 세 마리를 따라잡았고, 그의 앞에는 마호친의 밤색 글라디아토르—그 뒷다리가 브론스키 바로 앞에서 가볍고 율동적으로 오르내렸다—와, 모두의 맨 앞에 거의 죽은 사람처럼 늘어진 쿠조블레프를 태운 우아한 암말 디아나만 남았다.\n\n처음 한순간 브론스키는 자기 자신도 암말도 다스리지 못했다. 첫 번째 장애물인 시냇물에 이르기까지 그는 암말의 움직임을 이끌 수가 없었다.\n\n글라디아토르와 디아나가 거의 같은 순간에 함께 그곳에 다다랐다. 동시에 그들은 시냇물 위로 솟구쳐 건너편으로 날아갔다. 프루프루도 날아오르듯 그 뒤를 좇아 뛰어들었다. 그러나 브론스키가 공중에 떠 있음을 느낀 바로 그 순간, 그는 자기 암말의 발굽 거의 바로 아래에서 디아나와 함께 시냇물 건너편에서 허우적거리는 쿠조블레프를 문득 보았다(쿠조블레프는 도약하는 순간 고삐를 놓쳤고, 암말이 그를 머리 너머로 내동댕이쳤다). 그 세세한 정황은 나중에 알게 된 것이고, 그 순간 그가 본 것이라곤 바로 자기 아래, 프루프루가 내려앉아야 할 그곳에 디아나의 다리나 머리가 걸리적거릴지도 모른다는 것뿐이었다. 그러나 프루프루는 떨어지는 고양이처럼 도약하는 바로 그 동작 속에서 다리와 등을 끌어당겼고, 다른 암말을 넘어 그녀 너머에 내려앉았다.\n\n'오, 내 사랑!' 브론스키는 생각했다.\n\n시냇물을 건넌 뒤 브론스키는 암말을 완전히 장악했고, 마호친 뒤를 따라 그 큰 장벽을 넘은 다음 그 뒤로 이어지는 오백 야드가량의 탁 트인 땅에서 그를 따라잡을 작정으로 암말을 제어하기 시작했다.\n\n그 큰 장벽은 황제의 관람석 바로 앞에 서 있었다. 그 견고한 장벽은 '악마'라고 불렸는데, 그들이—그러니까 그와, 그보다 말 한 마리 길이만큼 앞선 마호친이—그 '악마'로 다가갈 때, 황제와 온 궁정 사람들과 군중이 모두 그들을 바라보고 있었다. 브론스키는 사방에서 자기에게 쏠린 그 눈길들을 의식했지만, 자기 암말의 귀와 목, 자기를 향해 마주 달려오는 땅, 그리고 그의 앞에서 빠르게 박자를 맞추며 늘 같은 거리를 유지하는 글라디아토르의 등과 흰 다리 말고는 아무것도 보이지 않았다. 글라디아토르가 무엇엔가 부딪치는 소리 하나 없이 솟구쳤다. 짧은 꼬리를 한 번 흔들더니 브론스키의 시야에서 사라졌다.\n\n\"브라보!\" 한 목소리가 외쳤다.\n\n같은 순간, 브론스키의 눈앞 바로 그 앞에서 장벽의 말뚝들이 번쩍였다. 그의 암말은 동작에 조금의 변화도 없이 그 위로 날아올랐다. 말뚝들이 사라졌고, 그는 그저 뒤에서 무언가 부딪치는 소리만 들었다. 글라디아토르가 앞서 가는 것에 흥분한 암말이 장벽 앞에서 너무 일찍 솟구치는 바람에 뒷발굽으로 그것을 스쳤던 것이다. 그러나 그녀의 속도는 조금도 변하지 않았고, 브론스키는 얼굴에 진흙이 튀는 것을 느끼며 자기가 다시 글라디아토르와 같은 거리에 있음을 깨달았다. 그는 다시 자기 앞에서 똑같은 등과 짧은 꼬리를, 그리고 다시 멀어지지 않는 똑같은 흰 다리가 빠르게 움직이는 것을 보았다.\n\n이제 마호친을 따라잡을 때라고 브론스키가 생각한 바로 그 순간, 프루프루 자신이 그의 생각을 알아채고는 그가 부추기지도 않았는데 상당히 거리를 좁혀, 가장 유리한 쪽, 즉 안쪽 줄에 바짝 붙어 마호친과 나란히 가기 시작했다. 마호친은 그쪽으로 그녀를 지나가게 두지 않으려 했다. 어쩌면 바깥쪽으로 지나갈 수 있겠다는 생각이 브론스키의 머릿속에 채 떠오르기도 전에, 프루프루가 보폭을 바꾸어 다른 쪽으로 그를 추월하기 시작했다. 이제 땀으로 거뭇해지기 시작한 프루프루의 어깨가 글라디아토르의 등과 나란해졌다. 그들은 몇 마리 길이만큼 나란히 달렸다. 그러나 다가오는 장애물 앞에서 브론스키는 바깥쪽으로 도는 것을 피하려고 마음 졸이며 고삐를 다루기 시작했고, 바로 그 비탈에서 재빨리 마호친을 앞질렀다. 그는 휙 지나가면서 진흙투성이가 된 그의 얼굴을 언뜻 보았다. 심지어 그가 미소 짓는 것처럼 느껴지기도 했다. 브론스키는 마호친을 앞질렀지만, 곧바로 그가 자기 바로 뒤에 바짝 붙어 있음을 의식했고, 글라디아토르의 고른 발굽 소리와 빠르고도 여전히 제법 생생한 숨소리가 끊임없이 들렸다.\n\n다음 두 장애물, 수로와 장벽은 어렵지 않게 넘었지만, 브론스키는 글라디아토르의 콧김 소리와 발굽 소리가 더 가까이 들리기 시작했다. 그가 암말을 재촉하자, 기쁘게도 그녀가 어렵지 않게 속도를 높이는 것이 느껴졌고, 글라디아토르의 발굽 소리는 다시 같은 거리에서 들렸다.\n\n브론스키는 자기가 바라던 대로, 또 코드가 충고한 대로 경주의 선두에 섰고, 이제 자신이 승리하리라고 확신했다. 그의 흥분, 기쁨, 그리고 프루프루에 대한 애틋함이 점점 더 깊어졌다. 그는 다시 한번 뒤를 돌아보고 싶었지만 감히 그러지 못했고, 글라디아토르가 여전히 힘을 비축하고 있는 것이 느껴지는 만큼 자기 암말도 그 힘을 그대로 남겨 두기 위해, 침착하게 굴며 암말을 재촉하지 않으려 애썼다. 이제 가장 까다로운 장애물 하나만 남아 있었다. 그것을 다른 이들보다 앞서 넘을 수만 있다면 그가 일등으로 들어올 터였다. 그는 아일랜드식 방벽을 향해 날아가고 있었고, 프루프루와 그는 함께 멀리 그 방벽을 보았으며, 사람도 암말도 한순간 망설였다. 그는 암말의 귀에서 망설임을 보고 채찍을 들었지만, 동시에 자신의 두려움이 근거 없는 것임을 느꼈다. 암말은 무엇이 필요한지 알고 있었다. 그녀는 그가 그려 보았던 그대로 속도를 높이며 매끄럽게 솟구쳤고, 땅을 박차고 오르면서 도약의 힘에 몸을 내맡겨 그 힘으로 도랑 저 멀리까지 건너갔다. 그러고는 똑같은 율동으로, 힘들이지 않고, 똑같은 다리를 앞으로 내디디며 프루프루는 다시 제 보폭을 되찾았다.\n\n\"브라보, 브론스키!\" 그는 한 무리의 사람들에게서 외침이 들렸다. 장애물 곁에 서 있는 그들이 자기 연대 친구들임을 그는 알았다. 그는 야슈빈을 보지는 못했지만 그의 목소리를 알아채지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n'오, 내 사랑!' 그는 뒤에서 무슨 일이 일어나는지 귀를 기울이며 프루프루에게 속으로 말했다. '저놈도 넘었군!' 그는 뒤에서 글라디아토르의 발굽 소리를 알아채며 생각했다. 이제 물이 가득 찬 너비 오 피트의 마지막 도랑만 남았다. 브론스키는 그것을 쳐다보지도 않았고, 그저 멀찍이 앞서 들어가고 싶은 마음에 암말의 머리를 들어 올렸다 보폭에 맞추어 놓아 주며 고삐를 톱질하듯 다루기 시작했다. 그는 암말이 마지막 남은 힘까지 쥐어짜고 있음을 느꼈다. 목과 어깨만 젖은 것이 아니라, 갈기와 머리와 뾰족한 귀에 땀이 방울방울 맺혀 있었고, 그녀의 숨은 짧고 거칠게 헐떡였다. 그러나 그는 남은 오백 야드를 달리기에 그녀에게 충분하고도 남는 힘이 있음을 알았다. 다만 자기 몸이 땅에 더 가까워진 느낌과 자기 움직임의 그 야릇한 매끄러움에서, 브론스키는 암말이 얼마나 크게 속도를 높였는지 알 수 있었다. 그녀는 그 도랑을 알아채지도 못한 듯 날아넘었다. 새처럼 그 위를 날아넘었다. 그러나 같은 순간, 브론스키는 끔찍하게도 자기가 암말의 보폭에 맞추지 못했다는 것을, 안장에서 자세를 다시 잡으면서 어떻게 그랬는지도 모르게 무시무시하고 용서할 수 없는 실수를 저질렀다는 것을 느꼈다. 별안간 그의 자세가 흐트러졌고, 그는 무언가 끔찍한 일이 일어났음을 알았다. 무슨 일이 일어났는지 아직 분간하지 못하는데, 밤색 말의 흰 다리가 그의 바로 곁에서 번쩍이더니 마호친이 빠른 질주로 지나갔다. 브론스키는 한쪽 발로 땅을 딛고 있었고, 그의 암말은 그쪽으로 무너지고 있었다. 그는 가까스로 다리를 빼낼 틈이 있었는데, 그녀는 한쪽으로 쓰러지며 고통스럽게 헐떡였고, 가냘프고 흠뻑 젖은 목으로 일어나려 헛되이 애쓰면서 총 맞은 새처럼 그의 발치에서 땅바닥에 푸드덕거렸다. 브론스키가 한 그 서툰 동작이 그녀의 등을 부러뜨린 것이다. 그러나 그것을 그가 안 것은 한참 뒤의 일이었다. 그 순간 그가 안 것이라곤, 마호친이 빠르게 휙 지나가 버렸고, 자기는 진흙투성이의 미동도 없는 땅 위에 홀로 비틀거리며 서 있으며, 프루프루가 그 앞에 헐떡이며 누워 고개를 뒤로 젖힌 채 그 아름다운 눈으로 자기를 바라보고 있다는 것뿐이었다. 무슨 일이 일어났는지 여전히 깨닫지 못한 채 브론스키는 암말의 고삐를 잡아당겼다. 그녀는 다시 물고기처럼 온몸을 버둥거렸고, 어깨로 안장을 들썩이며 앞다리로 일어섰지만 뒷부분을 들지 못한 채 온몸을 떨다가 다시 옆으로 쓰러졌다. 격정으로 얼굴이 흉하게 일그러지고 아래턱이 떨리고 두 뺨이 하얗게 질린 채, 브론스키는 발뒤꿈치로 그녀의 배를 걷어차고는 다시 고삐를 잡아당기기 시작했다. 그녀는 꼼짝도 않고, 코를 땅에 처박은 채 말하는 듯한 눈으로 그저 자기 주인을 바라보았다.\n\n\"아—아—아!\" 브론스키는 머리를 움켜쥐며 신음했다. \"아! 내가 무슨 짓을 한 거야!\" 그가 부르짖었다. \"경주를 망쳤어! 게다가 내 잘못이야! 부끄럽고, 용서받을 수 없어! 그리고 가엾은 내 사랑, 못 쓰게 된 암말! 아! 내가 무슨 짓을 한 거야!\"\n\n한 무리의 사람들, 의사와 그 조수, 그의 연대 장교들이 그에게 달려왔다. 비참하게도 그는 자기가 멀쩡하고 다친 데 없이 온전하다는 것을 느꼈다. 암말은 등이 부러졌고, 그녀를 쏘아 죽이기로 결정되었다. 브론스키는 질문에 답할 수도, 누구에게 말을 건넬 수도 없었다. 그는 몸을 돌려, 떨어진 모자도 줍지 않고 자기가 어디로 가는지도 모른 채 경주로에서 걸어 나왔다. 그는 더없이 비참했다. 난생처음으로 그는 가장 쓰라린 종류의 불행을, 돌이킬 수 없으며 제 잘못으로 빚어진 불행을 알게 되었다.\n\n야슈빈이 모자를 들고 그를 뒤따라와 집으로 데려갔고, 삼십 분쯤 뒤에 브론스키는 마음의 평정을 되찾았다. 그러나 그 경주의 기억은 그의 가슴속에 오래도록 남아, 그의 생애에서 가장 잔혹하고 쓰라린 기억이 되었다.\n\n# Chapter 26\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치와 그의 아내의 표면적인 관계는 변함없이 유지되고 있었다. 다만 한 가지 차이가 있다면, 그가 그 어느 때보다 분주하게 일에 몰두하고 있다는 사실뿐이었다. 예년과 마찬가지로 봄이 시작될 무렵 그는 건강을 위해 외국의 온천 휴양지로 떠났다. 해마다 더 무거워지는 겨울철 격무로 망가진 건강을 추스르기 위해서였다. 그리고 늘 그래 왔듯이 그는 칠월에 돌아와 곧장 평소처럼, 오히려 더 왕성한 기운으로 일에 매달렸다. 또한 늘 그래 왔듯이 그의 아내는 여름을 나기 위해 교외의 별장으로 거처를 옮겼고, 그는 페테르부르크에 남았다. 트베르스카야 공작부인 댁의 모임 이후 나누었던 그 대화 이래로, 그는 자신의 의심과 질투에 관해 안나에게 두 번 다시 입을 열지 않았으며, 평소처럼 빈정대듯 흉내 내는 그 말투가 지금 아내를 대하는 그의 태도에 가장 알맞은 어조였다. 그는 아내에게 조금 더 냉랭해졌다. 다만 그녀가 거부해 버린 그 첫 한밤중의 대화 때문에 그녀에게 가벼운 불만을 품은 것처럼 보일 뿐이었다. 그녀를 대하는 태도에는 어렴풋한 짜증의 기색이 있었지만, 그 이상은 아니었다. \"당신은 내게 솔직하려 하지 않았어.\" 그는 마음속으로 그녀에게 말하는 듯했다. \"그러니 당신만 더 손해지. 이제 당신이 아무리 빌어도 나는 당신에게 솔직하게 굴지 않을 거야. 그래 봐야 당신만 손해야!\" 그는 마음속으로 그렇게 말했다. 마치 불을 끄려고 헛되이 애쓰다가 그 헛된 노력에 화가 치밀어 \"아, 그래, 좋아! 그럼 다 타 버려라!\" 하고 내뱉는 사람처럼. 공무에서는 그토록 섬세하고 빈틈없던 이 사람이, 아내를 대하는 이런 태도가 얼마나 무의미한지는 도무지 깨닫지 못했다. 그가 그것을 깨닫지 못한 까닭은, 자신의 실제 처지를 깨닫는 것이 너무도 끔찍했기 때문이다. 그래서 그는 자기 가족, 그러니까 아내와 아들을 향한 감정이 숨어 있는 그 은밀한 곳을 마음속 깊이 닫고 잠그고 봉인해 버렸다. 그토록 자상한 아버지였던 그는 그해 겨울이 끝날 무렵부터 아들에게 유난히 차가워졌고, 아내에게 쓰던 것과 똑같은 빈정대는 말투를 아들에게도 쓰기 시작했다. \"어이, 젊은이!\" 그가 아들을 맞으며 건네는 인사는 이런 식이었다.\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 어느 해에도 그해만큼 공무가 많았던 적이 없다고 단언했고, 또 그렇게 믿었다. 하지만 그는 자기가 그해 들어 스스로 일거리를 찾고 있다는 사실은 알아차리지 못했다. 그것이 아내와 아들을 향한 감정, 그리고 그들에 관한 생각이 숨어 있는 그 은밀한 곳을 닫아 두기 위한 한 가지 방편이라는 것을—그것들은 그곳에 오래 묻혀 있을수록 더욱 끔찍해졌다—그는 알지 못했다. 만약 누군가가 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에게 아내의 행실을 어떻게 생각하느냐고 물을 권리가 있었다면, 온화하고 평화로운 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 아무 대답도 하지 않았을 것이고, 그 문제로 자기에게 캐묻는 자에게는 누구든 몹시 화를 냈을 것이다. 바로 그 때문에 누군가 아내의 안부를 물을 때마다 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 얼굴에는 어김없이 거만하고 준엄한 표정이 떠올랐다. 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 아내의 행실에 관해 아예 생각하고 싶어 하지 않았고, 실제로 그것에 관해 전혀 생각하지 않는 데 성공하고 있었다.\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 여름마다 머무는 별장은 페테르고프에 있었고, 리디아 이바노브나 백작부인은 으레 그곳에서 여름을 보내며 안나의 가까이에서 끊임없이 그녀를 만나곤 했다. 그런데 그해에는 리디아 이바노브나 백작부인이 페테르고프에 자리 잡기를 마다했고, 안나 아르카지예브나에게 한 번도 들르지 않았으며, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치와 이야기를 나누는 자리에서 안나가 베치며 브론스키와 가깝게 지내는 것이 적절치 않다는 점을 넌지시 비쳤다. 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 자기 아내가 의심받을 여지가 없는 사람이라고 단호히 못 박으며 그녀의 말을 매몰차게 잘랐고, 그때부터 리디아 이바노브나 백작부인을 피하기 시작했다. 그는 사교계의 많은 이들이 자기 아내에게 미심쩍은 시선을 던지고 있다는 것을 보고 싶어 하지 않았고, 또 보지도 않았다. 그는 아내가 어째서 그토록 한사코 차르스코예에—베치가 머물고 있고, 브론스키 연대의 주둔지에서 멀지 않은 그곳에—머무르겠다고 고집을 부렸는지 이해하고 싶어 하지 않았고, 또 이해하지도 않았다. 그는 자기 자신이 그것에 관해 생각하도록 두지 않았고, 그래서 생각하지 않았다. 하지만 그럼에도, 비록 스스로에게 결코 인정하지 않았고 아무 증거도, 심지어 의심스러운 정황조차 없었음에도, 마음 깊은 곳에서 그는 자기가 속은 남편이라는 것을 조금의 의심도 없이 알고 있었고, 그 때문에 깊디깊은 비참함에 잠겨 있었다.\n\n아내와 함께 행복하게 산 그 팔 년 동안, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 다른 남자들의 부정한 아내와 속아 넘어간 남편들을 바라보며 얼마나 자주 자문했던가. \"사람들이 어떻게 저 지경까지 떨어질 수 있지? 어째서 저토록 흉측한 처지에 종지부를 찍지 못하는 거지?\" 하지만 막상 그 불행이 자기 자신에게 닥친 지금, 그는 그 처지에 종지부를 찍을 생각을 하기는커녕 그것을 아예 인정하려 들지 않았다. 그것이 너무도 끔찍하고 너무도 부자연스럽다는 바로 그 이유로 인정하려 들지 않았다.\n\n외국에서 돌아온 이래로 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 교외의 별장에 두 번 다녀왔다. 한 번은 거기서 만찬을 했고, 또 한 번은 친구들과 어울려 저녁 시간을 보냈지만, 예년의 습관과 달리 단 한 번도 그곳에서 밤을 보내지는 않았다.\n\n경마가 열리는 날은 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에게 무척 분주한 하루였다. 하지만 아침에 마음속으로 하루 일정을 그려 보면서, 그는 만찬이 끝나는 대로 교외의 집으로 가서 아내를 보고, 거기서 곧장 경마장으로 가기로 마음먹었다. 경마는 궁정 사람들이 모두 참관하는 행사였고, 그도 반드시 참석해야만 했다. 그가 아내를 보러 가려는 것은, 체면을 지키기 위해 일주일에 한 번은 그녀를 보기로 작정해 두었기 때문이다. 게다가 그날은 십오일이었으므로, 늘 정해 둔 약속대로 아내에게 살림에 쓸 돈을 얼마간 건네주어야 했다.\n\n자기 생각을 다스리는 평소의 절제력으로, 그는 아내에 관해 이 모든 것을 생각하면서도 그녀에 관한 생각이 더 멀리 뻗어 나가도록 내버려 두지는 않았다.\n\n그날 아침은 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에게 무척 바쁜 시간이었다. 전날 저녁 리디아 이바노브나 백작부인이 페테르부르크에 머물고 있는, 중국을 여행한 어느 저명한 여행가의 소책자를 보내왔는데, 거기에 쪽지 한 장을 동봉하여 그 여행가를 직접 만나 보라고 청했다. 여러 면에서 대단히 흥미로운 인물이며 유용한 점도 있을 것이라는 이유였다. 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 전날 저녁에 그 소책자를 끝까지 읽을 틈이 없어서 아침에 마저 읽었다. 그러고 나자 청원서를 든 사람들이 찾아오기 시작했고, 이어서 보고와 면담, 임명과 해임, 포상과 연금과 보조금의 배정, 각종 통지 등, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 일상의 다람쥐 쳇바퀴라고 부르는, 늘 그토록 많은 시간을 잡아먹는 일들이 줄지어 이어졌다. 그다음에는 자신의 사적인 용무가 있었으니, 의사와 영지를 관리하는 집사의 방문이었다. 집사는 그리 많은 시간을 빼앗지 않았다. 그는 그저 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에게 필요한 돈을 건네주며 살림 형편에 관한 짤막한 보고를 곁들였을 뿐인데, 그 형편이라는 것이 그다지 만족스럽지는 못했다. 그해에는 지출이 늘어나는 바람에 평소보다 더 많은 돈이 나갔고, 그래서 적자가 생겼던 것이다. 그러나 의사는—알렉세이 알렉산드로비치와 가깝게 지내던 페테르부르크의 저명한 의사였는데—꽤 많은 시간을 잡아먹었다. 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 그날 그가 오리라고는 예상하지 못했기에 그의 방문에 놀랐고, 의사가 자신의 건강에 관해 아주 세심하게 캐묻고 호흡 소리를 들으며 간을 두드려 보았을 때는 더욱 놀랐다. 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 자신의 벗 리디아 이바노브나가 그해 그의 건강이 평소만 못한 것을 눈치채고 의사에게 가서 그를 진찰해 달라고 부탁했다는 것을 알지 못했다. \"저를 봐서라도 이렇게 해 주세요.\" 리디아 이바노브나 백작부인이 그에게 말했었다.\n\n\"러시아를 봐서라도 그렇게 하겠습니다, 백작부인.\" 의사가 대답했다.\n\n\"더없이 귀한 분이세요!\" 리디아 이바노브나 백작부인이 말했다.\n\n의사는 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 상태에 대해 몹시 못마땅해했다. 그는 간이 상당히 비대해졌고 소화 기능이 약해졌으며, 광천수 요법은 아무런 효과도 없었음을 알아냈다. 그는 되도록 운동을 더 많이 하고, 되도록 정신적 긴장을 덜 하며, 무엇보다도 근심을 하지 말 것을 처방했다—다시 말해, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치로서는 숨을 쉬지 않는 것만큼이나 불가능한 일들이었다. 그러고 나서 그는 물러갔고, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에게는 자신에게 무언가 이상이 있다는, 그리고 그것을 고칠 가망이 없다는 불쾌한 느낌만이 남았다.\n\n의사는 돌아가는 길에 계단에서 우연히 아는 사람을 만났는데, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치 부처의 비서관인 슬루딘이었다. 두 사람은 대학 시절의 동무였고, 좀처럼 만나는 일은 없었지만 서로를 높이 평가하며 더없이 가까운 벗으로 지냈다. 그래서 의사가 환자에 관한 자신의 소견을 슬루딘만큼 거리낌 없이 털어놓을 만한 상대는 따로 없었다.\n\n\"마침 자네가 그분을 진찰해 주었다니 얼마나 다행인지!\" 슬루딘이 말했다. \"그분이 영 편찮으셔서, 내 짐작에는…… 자, 자네가 보기엔 어떤가?\"\n\n\"내 말해 주지.\" 의사가 슬루딘의 머리 너머로 마부에게 마차를 대라고 손짓하며 말했다. \"바로 이런 거라네.\" 의사는 하얀 손으로 새끼 염소가죽 장갑의 손가락 끝을 잡고 당기면서 말을 이었다. \"줄을 팽팽히 당기지 않은 채로 그것을 끊으려 하면 여간 어려운 일이 아니지. 하지만 줄을 한껏 팽팽하게 당겨 놓으면, 그 팽팽한 줄 위에 손가락 하나의 무게만 얹어도 그냥 툭 끊어져 버린다네. 그런데 그분은 그 빈틈없는 근면함과 양심적으로 일에 헌신하는 태도 탓에 한껏 팽팽하게 당겨져 있어. 게다가 바깥에서 짓누르는 어떤 부담이, 그것도 가볍지 않은 부담이 있단 말이지.\" 의사는 의미심장하게 눈썹을 치켜올리며 말을 맺었다. \"자네도 경마장에 올 텐가?\" 그는 마차 좌석에 몸을 파묻으며 덧붙였다.\n\n\"그래, 그래, 그럼 가고말고. 정말이지 시간을 너무 많이 잡아먹는단 말이야.\" 의사는 미처 알아듣지 못한 슬루딘의 어떤 대꾸에 막연히 대답했다.\n\n그토록 많은 시간을 잡아먹은 의사가 떠난 직후에 그 저명한 여행가가 찾아왔는데, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 방금 막 다 읽은 소책자와 그 주제에 관해 전부터 알고 있던 지식을 동원하여, 그 주제에 대한 깊은 식견과 그것을 바라보는 폭넓고 계몽된 시각으로 여행가에게 깊은 인상을 남겼다.\n\n그 여행가와 같은 시각에 페테르부르크를 방문 중인 어느 지방 귀족 단장의 내방이 알려졌는데, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 그와도 몇 마디 이야기를 나누어야 했다. 그가 돌아간 뒤에는 비서와 함께 그날의 통상 업무를 마무리해야 했고, 그러고도 중대하고 심각한 사안으로 어느 대단한 인물을 찾아가 만나야 했다. 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 만찬 시각인 다섯 시에 가까스로 맞춰 돌아왔고, 비서와 함께 식사를 한 뒤 그에게 자기 교외 별장과 경마장에 함께 가자고 청했다.\n\n스스로에게 인정하지는 않았지만, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 요즈음 아내와 만나는 자리에는 늘 제삼자가 함께 있도록 애쓰고 있었다.\n\n# Chapter 27\n\n안나는 위층에서 거울 앞에 서서 안누슈카의 도움을 받아 가운에 마지막 리본을 꽂고 있다가, 현관 앞 자갈 위로 마차 바퀴가 사각거리는 소리를 들었다.\n\n\"베시가 오기엔 너무 이른데.\" 그녀는 그렇게 생각하며 창밖을 내다보다가 마차와 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 검은 모자, 그리고 그 모자 양옆으로 삐죽 솟은 익히 아는 귀를 발견했다. \"이런 운도 없지! 하룻밤 묵으려는 건 아니겠지?\" 그녀는 의아해했고, 그런 우연한 일에서 빚어질 수 있는 온갖 결과를 떠올리자 너무도 끔찍하고 무섭게 느껴진 나머지, 잠시도 거기 마음을 두지 않은 채 밝고 환한 얼굴로 그를 맞으러 내려갔다. 그리고 요즘 들어 자기 안에 깃든 것을 알게 된 그 거짓과 기만의 영(靈)을 의식하면서, 그녀는 그 영에 자신을 내맡기고는 자기가 무슨 말을 하는지도 잘 모른 채 떠들기 시작했다.\n\n\"아, 와 주시다니 정말 고마워요!\" 그녀는 남편에게 손을 내밀며 말했고, 집안 식구나 다름없는 슬류딘에게도 미소로 인사했다. \"하룻밤 묵으실 거죠?\" 거짓의 영이 그녀에게 일러 준 첫마디였다. \"그럼 같이 나가요. 다만 베시한테 약속을 해 둔 게 아쉽네요. 절 데리러 오기로 했거든요.\"\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 베시의 이름에 미간을 찌푸렸다.\n\n\"오, 떼려야 뗄 수 없는 사이를 갈라놓을 생각은 없소.\" 그는 평소의 농담조로 말했다. \"난 미하일 바실리예비치와 함께 가겠소. 나도 의사들한테 운동을 하라는 명을 받았거든. 걸으면서, 다시 온천에라도 와 있는 양 상상해 보지.\"\n\n\"서두르실 것 없어요.\" 안나가 말했다. \"차라도 드시겠어요?\"\n\n그녀가 벨을 울렸다.\n\"차를 내오고, 세료자한테 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 오셨다고 전해 줘요. 자, 말씀해 보세요, 그동안 어떻게 지내셨어요? 미하일 바실리예비치, 전에는 절 보러 와 주신 적이 없었죠. 보세요, 테라스 밖이 얼마나 좋은지.\" 그녀는 한 사람에게서 다른 사람에게로 번갈아 몸을 돌리며 말했다.\n\n그녀는 아주 단순하고 자연스럽게 말했지만, 너무 많이, 너무 빠르게 말했다. 미하일 바실리예비치가 자기에게 던지는 캐묻는 듯한 눈길에서, 그가 말하자면 자기를 감시하고 있다는 것을 알아차렸기에 그녀는 더욱 그것을 의식했다.\n\n미하일 바실리예비치는 곧장 테라스로 나갔다.\n\n그녀는 남편 곁에 앉았다.\n\n\"안색이 그리 좋지 않으시네요.\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"그렇소.\" 그가 말했다. \"오늘 의사가 와서 한 시간이나 내 시간을 허비하게 했지. 우리 친구들 중 누가 그를 보낸 게 틀림없다는 생각이 들더군. 내 건강이 어지간히 소중한 모양이오.\"\n\n\"아니, 의사가 뭐라고 하던가요?\"\n\n그녀는 그의 건강과 그동안 한 일들에 대해 물으며, 좀 쉬다가 자기와 함께 나가자고 그를 설득하려 했다.\n\n이 모든 말을 그녀는 밝게, 빠르게, 그리고 두 눈에 묘한 광채를 띠며 했다. 그러나 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 이제 그녀의 그런 어조에 아무런 특별한 의미를 두지 않았다. 그는 오로지 그녀의 말만을 들었고, 그 말이 지닌 직접적인 뜻만을 받아들였다. 그래서 그는 농담조이긴 했지만 단순하게 대답했다. 이 모든 대화에는 별달리 두드러진 점이 없었으나, 그 후로 안나는 두고두고 이 짧은 장면을 떠올릴 때마다 견딜 수 없는 수치심에 가슴이 아렸다.\n\n세료자가 가정교사를 앞세우고 들어왔다. 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 눈여겨볼 마음만 있었다면, 세료자가 먼저 아버지를, 이어 어머니를 흘끔거리는 그 겁먹고 당황한 눈빛을 알아챘을 것이다. 그러나 그는 아무것도 보려 하지 않았고, 그래서 보지 못했다.\n\n\"아, 우리 젊은이! 키가 컸구나. 정말이지 어엿한 사내가 다 되었어. 어떻게 지내느냐, 젊은이?\"\n\n그러고는 겁에 질린 아이에게 손을 내밀었다. 세료자는 전부터 아버지를 어려워했는데, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 자기를 젊은이라고 부르기 시작한 뒤로, 또 브론스키가 친구인지 적인지 하는 풀리지 않는 의문이 떠오른 뒤로는 아버지를 피했다. 아이는 마치 피난처라도 찾는 양 어머니 쪽으로 돌아보았다. 아이가 마음 편할 수 있는 사람은 오직 어머니뿐이었다. 그러는 동안 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 가정교사와 이야기하며 아들의 어깨를 붙잡고 있었고, 세료자는 너무도 비참하고 거북해서, 안나는 아이가 막 울음을 터뜨리려 한다는 것을 알아차렸다.\n\n아들이 들어온 순간 살짝 얼굴을 붉혔던 안나는, 세료자가 거북해하는 것을 알아채고는 황급히 일어나 아들의 어깨에서 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 손을 떼어 내고, 아이에게 입을 맞춘 뒤 테라스로 데리고 나갔다가 얼른 돌아왔다.\n\n\"그래도 이제 나설 시간이네요.\" 그녀는 시계를 흘끗 보며 말했다. \"베시는 어째서 안 오는 걸까요?……\"\n\n\"그래.\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 말하고는, 일어나서 두 손을 맞잡고 손가락을 우두둑 꺾었다. \"당신한테 줄 돈도 가져왔소. 나이팅게일도 옛이야기만으로는 살 수 없는 법이니 말이오.\" 그가 말했다. \"필요하지 않소?\"\n\n\"아니에요, 필요…… 아니, 필요해요.\" 그녀는 그를 보지 않은 채, 머리끝까지 새빨개지며 말했다. \"그런데 경마가 끝나면 다시 이리로 오시는 거죠?\"\n\n\"오, 그럼!\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 대답했다. \"그리고 저기 페테르고프의 명물, 트베르스카야 공작부인이 오는군.\" 그는 창밖으로 자그마한 좌석이 유난히 높이 달린 우아한 영국식 마차를 내다보며 덧붙였다. \"정말 우아하군! 멋져! 자, 그럼 우리도 나섭시다.\"\n\n트베르스카야 공작부인은 마차에서 내리지 않았으나, 긴 장화에 케이프, 검은 모자를 쓴 마부가 현관 앞으로 잽싸게 뛰어나왔다.\n\n\"전 가요. 안녕히 계세요!\" 안나는 그렇게 말하고 아들에게 입을 맞춘 뒤, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에게 다가가 손을 내밀었다. \"와 주셔서 정말 고마웠어요.\"\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 그녀의 손에 입을 맞췄다.\n\n\"그럼 au revoir예요! 차 마시러 다시 오시는 거죠. 정말 기뻐요!\" 그녀는 이렇게 말하고는 명랑하고 환한 얼굴로 나갔다. 그러나 그가 더 이상 보이지 않게 되자, 그녀는 그의 입술이 닿았던 손등의 그 자리를 의식했고, 혐오감에 몸서리를 쳤다.\n* au revoir: 프랑스어로 '안녕히 가세요, 또 만나요'.\n\n# Chapter 28\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 경마장에 도착했을 때, 안나는 이미 베치 옆자리, 최상류 사교계 사람들이 모두 모여 있는 그 관람석에 앉아 있었다. 그녀는 멀리서 남편의 모습을 알아보았다. 남편과 연인, 이 두 남자가 그녀 존재의 두 중심이었고, 그녀는 외적인 감각의 도움 없이도 그들이 가까이 있음을 알아챘다. 그녀는 멀리서부터 남편이 다가오는 것을 느꼈고, 그가 움직이고 있는 출렁이는 인파 속에서 자기도 모르게 그의 모습을 좇았다. 그녀는 그가 관람석을 향해 나아가는 모습을, 어떤 때는 아첨 섞인 인사에 거드름을 피우며 답례하고, 어떤 때는 자기와 같은 지위의 사람들과 친근하면서도 무심하게 인사를 주고받고, 또 어떤 때는 이 세상의 어느 거물의 눈에 들려고 애쓰며 귀 끝을 죄는 커다란 둥근 모자를 벗는 모습을 지켜보았다. 이 모든 행동거지를 그녀는 알고 있었고, 그 모두가 그녀에게는 가증스러웠다. \"오직 야심뿐, 오직 출세하려는 욕망뿐이지, 저이 영혼 속에 있는 거라곤 그게 전부야.\" 그녀는 생각했다. \"저 고상한 이상이니, 교양에 대한 사랑이니, 종교니 하는 것들도 다 출세를 위한 도구일 뿐이고.\"\n\n여자들이 있는 관람석 쪽을 향한 그의 시선으로 보아(그는 그녀 쪽을 똑바로 바라보고 있었지만, 모슬린과 리본과 깃털과 양산과 꽃의 바다 속에서 아내를 분간해 내지는 못했다) 그녀는 그가 자기를 찾고 있다는 것을 알았지만, 일부러 그를 알아보지 않은 척했다.\n\n\"알렉세이 알렉산드로비치!\" 베치 공작부인이 그를 불렀다. \"부인이 안 보이시는 모양인데, 여기 계세요.\"\n\n그는 차가운 미소를 지었다.\n\n\"여긴 화려한 게 너무 많아서 눈이 부실 지경입니다.\" 그는 이렇게 말하고는 관람석으로 들어갔다. 그는 방금 헤어졌다가 다시 만난 아내에게 으레 짓는 그런 미소로 아내에게 미소를 짓고, 공작부인과 다른 지인들에게 인사하며 저마다에게 합당한 몫을 나누어 주었다. 다시 말해 부인들에게는 농담을 건네고, 남자들에게는 다정한 인사를 던졌던 것이다. 관람석 아래쪽 가까이에는 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 높이 평가하는 한 시종무관장이 서 있었는데, 그는 지성과 교양으로 이름난 사람이었다. 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 그와 대화를 나누기 시작했다.\n\n경주와 경주 사이에 휴식 시간이 있었으므로 대화를 방해하는 것은 아무것도 없었다. 시종무관장은 경마에 대한 못마땅함을 드러냈다. 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 경마를 옹호하며 응수했다. 안나는 그의 높고 절제된 어조를 한마디도 놓치지 않고 들었는데, 그 한마디 한마디가 거짓으로 느껴졌고 고통스럽게 그녀의 귀를 찔러 댔다.\n\n삼 마일 장애물 경주가 시작되자, 그녀는 몸을 앞으로 기울인 채 브론스키가 말에게 다가가 올라타는 모습을 뚫어지게 바라보았는데, 동시에 그녀는 남편의 그 혐오스럽고 끊임없이 이어지는 목소리를 들었다. 그녀는 브론스키 때문에 공포로 괴로워했지만, 더 큰 괴로움은, 그녀에게는 결코 그치지 않을 듯 들리던, 익숙한 억양이 밴 남편의 새된 목소리의 흐름이었다.\n\n\"나는 사악한 여자야, 타락한 여자지.\" 그녀는 생각했다. \"하지만 나는 거짓말을 싫어해, 거짓을 견딜 수가 없어. 그런데 저이(남편)에게는 거짓이 곧 생명의 호흡이야. 저이는 모든 걸 다 알고, 다 보고 있어. 그러면서 저렇게 태연히 떠들 수 있다면 도대체 무슨 마음을 쓰고 있는 거지? 차라리 저이가 나를 죽인다면, 브론스키를 죽인다면, 나는 저이를 존경할 수도 있을 거야. 아냐, 저이가 원하는 건 오직 거짓과 체면뿐이야.\" 안나는 자기가 남편에게 정확히 무엇을 바라는지, 그가 어떻게 행동하기를 원하는지는 헤아리지 못한 채 그렇게 혼잣말을 했다. 그녀는 또한 그날 그토록 그녀의 화를 돋우던 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 유별난 수다가 실은 그의 내면의 고통과 불안의 표현일 뿐이라는 것도 이해하지 못했다. 다친 아이가 통증을 잊으려고 온몸의 근육을 움직여 가며 깡충거리듯이, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치도 아내에 대한 생각을 잊기 위한 정신적 운동이 필요했던 것이다. 아내가 곁에 있고 브론스키가 곁에 있고 그의 이름이 끊임없이 되풀이되는 가운데, 그 생각들은 억지로라도 그의 주의를 비집고 들어오려 했던 것이다. 그리고 그에게는 말을 잘하고 영리하게 하는 것이, 마치 아이가 깡충거리는 것이 자연스러운 만큼이나 자연스러운 일이었다. 그는 이렇게 말하고 있었다.\n\n\"장교들, 그러니까 기병들의 경주에 따르는 위험은 경마의 본질적인 요소입니다. 영국이 군사(軍史)에서 가장 빛나는 기병의 위업을 자랑할 수 있는 것은, 단지 영국이 짐승과 사람 양쪽에서 이 힘을 역사적으로 발전시켜 온 덕분이지요. 스포츠란, 제 생각으로는, 대단한 가치를 지니고 있는데, 늘 그렇듯이 우리는 가장 피상적인 면밖에는 보지 못하는 겁니다.\"\n\n\"피상적이지 않은데요.\" 트베르스카야 공작부인이 말했다. \"장교 하나가 갈비뼈 두 대가 부러졌다잖아요.\"\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 그 특유의 미소, 이를 드러내기는 하나 그 이상은 아무것도 드러내지 않는 미소를 지었다.\n\n\"공작부인, 그게 피상적이지 않다는 건 인정하지요.\" 그가 말했다. \"내적인 일이니까요. 하지만 그게 핵심은 아닙니다.\" 그러고는 진지하게 이야기를 나누던 장군 쪽으로 다시 몸을 돌렸다. \"우리는 경주에 참가하는 자들이 그 길을 스스로 택한 군인들이라는 점을 잊어선 안 됩니다. 그리고 모든 직업에는 저마다 달갑지 않은 면이 있다는 것도 인정해야지요. 그것은 장교의 임무의 한 부분을 이루는 겁니다. 권투니 에스파냐 투우니 하는 저속한 스포츠는 야만의 표지입니다. 하지만 전문적인 기량의 시험은 발전의 표지지요.\"\n\n\"아뇨, 저는 다음번엔 안 올래요. 너무 마음이 흔들려서요.\" 베치 공작부인이 말했다. \"그렇지 않아, 안나?\"\n\n\"마음이 흔들리긴 해도 눈을 뗄 수가 없네요.\" 다른 부인이 말했다. \"내가 로마 여인이었다면 원형 경기는 단 한 번도 놓치지 않았을 거예요.\"\n\n안나는 아무 말도 하지 않고, 오페라글라스를 든 채 줄곧 한곳만 바라보았다.\n\n그 순간 키가 큰 장군 하나가 관람석을 가로질러 지나갔다. 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 하던 말을 멈추고, 위엄을 잃지 않으면서도 서둘러 일어나 그 장군에게 깊이 허리를 숙였다.\n\n\"경마에 안 나가십니까?\" 장교가 그를 놀리듯 물었다.\n\n\"제 경주는 그보다 더 힘든 것이지요.\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 공손하게 답했다.\n\n그 대답에는 아무런 뜻도 없었지만, 장군은 마치 재치 있는 사람에게서 재치 있는 말을 들은 듯한 표정을 지으며 la pointe de la sauce를 한껏 음미했다.\n\n* la pointe de la sauce: 프랑스어로 '소스의 묘미', 즉 농담의 핵심·맛을 뜻한다.\n\"여기엔 두 부류가 있습니다.\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 말을 이었다. \"직접 참가하는 자들과 구경하는 자들이지요. 그리고 그런 구경거리를 좋아한다는 것은 구경꾼의 발전 정도가 낮다는 틀림없는 증거인데, 인정합니다만……\"\n\n\"공작부인, 내기요!\" 아래쪽에서 스테판 아르카지치가 베치를 향해 소리쳤다. \"어느 쪽이 좋으세요?\"\n\n\"안나하고 나는 쿠조블레프 편이에요.\" 베치가 대답했다.\n\n\"난 브론스키 편입니다. 장갑 한 켤레 거실까요?\"\n\n\"좋아요!\"\n\n\"하지만 멋진 광경이긴 하지 않습니까?\"\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 자기에 대한 이야기가 오가는 동안 입을 다물었다가, 곧바로 다시 말을 시작했다.\n\n\"남자다운 스포츠가……라는 건 인정합니다만.\" 그는 말을 이어 가고 있었다.\n\n그러나 바로 그 순간 경주마들이 출발했고, 모든 대화가 그쳤다. 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치도 입을 다물었고, 모두가 일어나 물길 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸다. 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 경주에 관심이 없었으므로 경주마들을 보지 않고, 지친 눈으로 무심하게 관중을 훑어보기 시작했다. 그의 눈길이 안나에게 멈추었다.\n\n그녀의 얼굴은 창백하게 굳어 있었다. 그녀가 오직 한 사람 외에는 아무것도, 아무도 보고 있지 않다는 것은 명백했다. 그녀의 손은 발작적으로 부채를 움켜쥐고 있었고, 그녀는 숨을 멈추고 있었다. 그는 그녀를 바라보다가 황급히 눈을 돌려 다른 얼굴들을 살폈다.\n\n\"하지만 이 부인뿐 아니라 다른 사람들도 무척 동요하고 있어. 지극히 자연스러운 일이지.\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 혼잣말을 했다. 그는 그녀를 보지 않으려 했지만, 무의식중에 그의 눈은 그녀에게로 끌려갔다. 그는 그 얼굴에 그토록 또렷이 적혀 있는 것을 읽지 않으려 애쓰며 다시 그 얼굴을 살폈고, 자기 의지와는 반대로, 끔찍한 심정으로 자기가 알고 싶지 않던 것을 그 얼굴에서 읽어 냈다.\n\n첫 번째 낙마, 즉 물길에서의 쿠조블레프의 낙마는 모두를 동요시켰지만, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 안나의 창백하고 의기양양한 얼굴에서 그녀가 지켜보던 그 남자가 떨어지지 않았음을 또렷이 보았다. 마호틴과 브론스키가 가장 험한 장애물을 넘은 뒤, 다음 장교가 그 장애물에서 머리부터 곤두박질쳐 치명상을 입어 온 관중 사이로 공포의 전율이 번졌을 때에도, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 안나가 그것을 알아채지조차 못한다는 것을 보았고, 사람들이 자기 주위에서 무슨 이야기를 하고 있는지 알아듣는 데에 애를 먹었다. 그러나 그는 점점 더 자주, 점점 더 집요하게 그녀를 지켜보았다. 경주에 온통 빠져 있던 안나는 옆에서 자기에게 고정된 남편의 차가운 눈길을 알아챘다.\n\n그녀는 한순간 돌아보더니 의아한 눈으로 그를 바라보다가, 가볍게 미간을 찌푸리며 다시 고개를 돌렸다.\n\n\"아, 어찌 되든 상관없어!\" 그녀는 그에게 그렇게 말하는 듯했고, 그 뒤로는 단 한 번도 그를 돌아보지 않았다.\n\n이 경주는 불운한 경주여서, 출전한 열일곱 명의 장교 가운데 절반이 넘는 수가 낙마하여 다쳤다. 경주가 끝나 갈 무렵에는 모두가 동요에 휩싸였고, 그 동요는 황제께서 언짢아하셨다는 사실로 더욱 깊어졌다.\n\n# Chapter 29\n\n모두가 큰 소리로 못마땅함을 드러내고 있었고, 누군가 내뱉은 한마디를 모두가 따라 외고 있었으며—\"이러다 사자와 검투사까지 나오겠군\"—모두가 끔찍하다는 듯이 느끼고 있었다. 그래서 브론스키가 땅에 떨어지고 안나가 큰 소리로 신음을 토했을 때도, 거기에 그리 별스러운 데는 없었다. 그러나 그 후 안나의 얼굴에 일어난 변화는 정말이지 예법을 벗어난 것이었다. 그녀는 완전히 제정신을 잃었다. 새장에 갇힌 새처럼 안절부절못하기 시작해, 어느 순간에는 자리에서 일어나 떠나려 하다가도, 다음 순간에는 베시 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸다.\n\n\"가요, 우리 가요!\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n그러나 베시는 그녀의 말을 듣지 못했다. 베시는 몸을 숙이고, 자기에게 다가온 어느 장군과 이야기를 나누고 있었다.\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 안나에게 다가가 정중하게 자기 팔을 내밀었다.\n\n\"원한다면 갑시다.\" 그가 프랑스어로 말했지만, 안나는 장군의 말에 귀를 기울이고 있어서 남편을 알아차리지 못했다.\n\n\"저 사람도 다리가 부러졌다더군요.\" 장군이 말하고 있었다. \"이건 정말 너무하지 않습니까.\"\n\n안나는 남편에게 대답도 하지 않은 채 오페라글라스를 들어 올려 브론스키가 떨어진 자리 쪽을 바라보았다. 그러나 너무 멀고, 그 주위에 사람들이 잔뜩 몰려 있어서, 그녀는 아무것도 분간할 수 없었다. 그녀는 오페라글라스를 내려놓고 떠나려 했지만, 바로 그 순간 한 장교가 말을 달려와 황제에게 무언가를 알렸다. 안나는 몸을 앞으로 빼고 귀를 기울였다.\n\n\"스티바! 스티바!\" 그녀가 오빠를 향해 소리쳤다.\n\n그러나 오빠는 그녀의 말을 듣지 못했다. 그녀는 다시 자리를 뜨려고 했다.\n\n\"가고 싶다면, 한 번 더 내 팔을 빌려주겠소.\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 그녀의 손을 향해 손을 뻗으며 말했다.\n\n그녀는 혐오감을 느끼며 그에게서 몸을 뺐고, 그의 얼굴은 쳐다보지도 않은 채 대답했다.\n\n\"아니요, 아니요, 내버려 두세요, 여기 있을 거예요.\"\n\n그녀는 이제 브론스키가 사고를 당한 자리에서 한 장교가 트랙을 가로질러 관람석 쪽으로 달려오는 것을 보았다. 베시가 그에게 손수건을 흔들었다. 장교는 기수는 죽지 않았지만 말이 등뼈가 부러졌다는 소식을 가져왔다.\n\n이 말을 듣고 안나는 황급히 자리에 앉아 부채로 얼굴을 가렸다. 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 그녀가 울고 있는 것을, 그리고 그녀가 눈물도, 가슴을 들썩이게 하는 흐느낌조차 억누르지 못하고 있다는 것을 보았다. 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 그녀를 가려 주려는 듯이 서서, 그녀에게 마음을 추스를 시간을 주었다.\n\n\"세 번째로 내 팔을 빌려주겠소.\" 그가 잠시 후 그녀를 돌아보며 말했다. 안나는 그를 멍하니 바라보며 무슨 말을 해야 할지 몰랐다. 베시 공작부인이 그녀를 구하러 나섰다.\n\n\"아니에요, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치. 안나는 제가 데려왔으니 제가 집까지 데려다주기로 했어요.\" 베시가 끼어들었다.\n\n\"실례합니다만, 공작부인.\" 그가 정중하게 미소를 지으면서도 그녀의 얼굴을 아주 단호하게 들여다보며 말했다. \"보아하니 안나의 몸이 좋지 않은 듯하군요. 나와 함께 집으로 갔으면 합니다.\"\n\n안나는 겁먹은 듯이 주위를 둘러보고는, 순순히 일어나 남편의 팔에 손을 얹었다.\n\n\"그에게 사람을 보내 알아보고 당신에게 알려 줄게요.\" 베시가 그녀에게 속삭였다.\n\n관람석을 나서면서, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 늘 그렇듯 마주치는 사람들과 이야기를 나누었고, 안나도 늘 그렇듯 이야기하고 대답을 해야 했다. 그러나 그녀는 완전히 넋이 나가 있었고, 꿈을 꾸는 듯 남편의 팔에 매달려 움직였다.\n\n'그가 죽었을까, 아닐까? 정말일까? 그가 올까, 안 올까? 오늘 그를 볼 수 있을까?' 그녀는 생각하고 있었다.\n\n그녀는 말없이 남편의 마차에 올랐고, 말없이 마차들의 무리에서 빠져나왔다. 자기가 본 모든 것에도 불구하고, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 여전히 아내의 진짜 상태를 헤아려 보려 하지 않았다. 그는 겉으로 드러난 증상만 보았을 뿐이다. 그는 그녀가 부적절하게 처신하고 있음을 보았고, 그것을 그녀에게 말해 주는 것이 자기 의무라고 여겼다. 그러나 그 이상은 말하지 않고, 오직 그것만 말하기란 그에게 무척 어려운 일이었다. 그는 그녀가 부적절하게 처신했다고 말하려고 입을 열었으나, 전혀 다른 말을 하지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n\"그래도 우리 모두 이런 잔혹한 구경거리를 얼마나 좋아하는지.\" 그가 말했다. \"내가 보기엔……\"\n\n\"네? 무슨 말인지 모르겠어요.\" 안나가 경멸하듯 말했다.\n\n그는 모욕감을 느꼈고, 곧바로 하려던 말을 하기 시작했다.\n\n\"당신에게 할 말이 있소.\" 그가 입을 뗐다.\n\n'이제 결판을 내려는 거구나.' 그녀는 생각했고, 두려워졌다.\n\n\"오늘 당신의 행동이 부적절했다는 말을 하지 않을 수 없소.\" 그가 프랑스어로 그녀에게 말했다.\n\n\"내 행동이 어떻게 부적절했다는 거죠?\" 그녀는 큰 소리로 말하며 재빨리 고개를 돌려 그의 얼굴을 똑바로 바라보았다. 무언가를 감추는 듯한 그 밝은 표정이 아니라, 자기가 느끼는 당혹감을 가까스로 감춘 결연한 눈빛으로.\n\n\"조심하시오.\" 그가 마부 맞은편의 열린 창문을 가리키며 말했다.\n\n그는 일어나서 창문을 끌어 올렸다.\n\n\"내 행동에서 뭐가 부적절했다는 거죠?\" 그녀가 되물었다.\n\n\"기수 중 한 사람이 사고를 당했을 때 당신이 감추지 못한 그 절망 말이오.\"\n\n그는 그녀가 대답하기를 기다렸지만, 그녀는 입을 다문 채 앞만 똑바로 응시하고 있었다.\n\n\"나는 이미 당신에게, 아무리 악의에 찬 입들이라도 당신에 대해 흠잡을 거리를 찾지 못하도록 사교계에서 처신하라고 부탁했소. 한때는 당신의 내면적 태도에 대해 말한 적이 있었지만, 지금은 그것을 말하는 게 아니오. 지금은 오직 당신의 외면적 태도에 대해서만 말하는 거요. 당신은 부적절하게 처신했고, 나는 그런 일이 다시는 없기를 바라오.\"\n\n그녀는 그가 하는 말의 절반도 듣지 못했다. 그녀는 그 앞에서 공포에 사로잡혀 있었고, 브론스키가 죽지 않았다는 게 사실인지를 생각하고 있었다. 기수는 다치지 않았는데 말이 등뼈가 부러졌다고 했을 때, 그들이 말한 것이 그였을까? 그가 말을 마쳤을 때 그녀는 짐짓 비꼬는 척하며 미소 지었을 뿐 아무 대답도 하지 않았다. 그의 말을 듣지 못했기 때문이다. 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 대담하게 말을 시작했지만, 자기가 무엇에 대해 말하고 있는지를 또렷이 깨닫게 되자, 그녀가 느끼고 있던 당혹감이 그에게도 옮아 왔다. 그는 그 미소를 보았고, 기묘한 오해에 사로잡혔다.\n\n'아내가 내 의심을 비웃고 있어. 그래, 곧 전에 했던 말을 또 하겠지. 내 의심에는 아무 근거가 없으며, 터무니없는 일이라고.'\n\n모든 것이 폭로될 위기가 그의 머리 위에 드리워진 그 순간, 그가 무엇보다 바란 것은 그녀가 전처럼 비웃듯이 그의 의심이 터무니없고 전혀 근거가 없다고 대답해 주는 것이었다. 그가 알고 있는 것이 너무도 끔찍해서, 이제 그는 무엇이든 믿을 준비가 되어 있었다. 그러나 겁에 질리고 침울한 그녀의 표정은 이제 거짓말조차 약속해 주지 않았다.\n\n\"어쩌면 내가 잘못 본 것일 수도 있겠지.\" 그가 말했다. \"그렇다면 용서를 빌겠소.\"\n\n\"아니요, 당신은 잘못 보지 않았어요.\" 그녀는 그의 차가운 얼굴을 절망스럽게 바라보며 또박또박 말했다. \"당신은 잘못 보지 않았어요. 잘못 본 건 나였고, 나는 절망하지 않을 수 없었어요. 당신 말이 들리긴 하지만, 나는 그 사람을 생각하고 있어요. 나는 그를 사랑해요, 나는 그의 정부예요. 나는 당신을 견딜 수 없어요. 나는 당신이 두렵고, 당신이 미워요…… 나한테 마음대로 하세요.\"\n\n그러고는 마차 구석에 몸을 던지듯 기대며, 두 손에 얼굴을 묻고 흐느껴 울기 시작했다. 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 꼼짝도 하지 않은 채, 계속 앞만 똑바로 바라보았다. 그러나 그의 온 얼굴이 갑자기 죽은 사람처럼 엄숙하게 굳어 버렸고, 집으로 돌아오는 내내 그의 표정은 변하지 않았다. 집에 다다랐을 때, 그는 여전히 그 표정 그대로 그녀에게 고개를 돌렸다.\n\n\"좋소! 하지만 내가 내 명예를 지킬 방도를 마련해서 그것을 당신에게 알릴 때까지는\"—그의 목소리가 떨렸다—\"외면적인 예법만큼은 엄격히 지켜 주기를 바라오.\"\n\n그는 먼저 내려서 그녀가 내리는 것을 도왔다. 하인들 앞에서 그는 그녀의 손을 꼭 쥐고는, 마차에 올라 페테르부르크로 돌아갔다. 그 직후 베시 공작부인이 보낸 하인이 와서 안나에게 쪽지를 전했다.\n\n\"그가 어떤지 알아보려고 알렉세이에게 사람을 보냈더니, 아주 멀쩡하고 다친 데도 없지만 절망에 빠져 있다는 답장이 왔어요.\"\n\n'그러니까 그가 여기로 오겠구나.' 그녀는 생각했다. '그에게 다 털어놓길 정말 잘했어!'\n\n그녀는 시계를 흘끗 보았다. 아직 세 시간이나 기다려야 했고, 마지막으로 만났던 기억이 그녀의 피를 들끓게 했다.\n\n'아, 어쩜 이렇게 환할까! 끔찍하긴 해도, 나는 정말 그의 얼굴을 보는 게 좋아, 그리고 이 환상적인 빛이 정말 좋아…… 내 남편! 아! 그래…… 그래도, 다행이야! 그 사람과는 모든 게 끝났으니.'\n\n# Chapter 30\n\n쉬체르바츠키 가족이 머물게 된 독일의 작은 온천 휴양지에서도, 사람들이 모여드는 다른 모든 곳에서와 마찬가지로, 말하자면 사교계가 결정(結晶)을 이루어 가는 그 익숙한 과정이 진행되어, 그 사교계의 구성원 각자에게 분명하고 변할 수 없는 자리가 정해지고 있었다. 서리 속의 물방울이 분명하고 변할 수 없이 눈송이라는 특별한 결정의 형태를 띠게 되듯이, 온천에 새로 도착한 사람도 저마다 곧장 자기만의 특별한 자리에 놓이게 되었다.\n\nFürst 쉬체르바츠키는 sammt Gemahlin und Tochter, 그들이 잡은 숙소로 보나, 그들의 이름으로 보나, 그들이 사귄 친구들로 보나, 즉시 그들에게 정해진 분명한 자리에 결정처럼 굳어졌다.\n\n* Fürst: 독일어로 '공작'. sammt Gemahlin und Tochter: 독일어로 '부인과 딸을 동반한'.\n그해 그 휴양지에는 진짜 독일인 Fürstin이 와 있었고, 그 덕분에 결정화 과정은 그 어느 때보다 활발하게 진행되었다. 쉬체르바츠카야 공작 부인은 무엇보다도 자기 딸을 이 독일 공작 부인에게 소개하고 싶어 했고, 도착한 다음 날 격식에 맞게 그 의식을 치렀다. 키티는 파리에서 주문해 온, 아주 수수한, 다시 말해 아주 우아한 드레스를 입고 낮고 우아하게 무릎을 굽혀 인사했다. 독일 공작 부인은 \"이 예쁘고 작은 얼굴에 곧 장밋빛이 돌아오길 바랍니다\"라고 말했고, 그것으로 쉬체르바츠키 가족에게는 결코 벗어날 수 없는 일정한 존재의 선들이 즉시 그어졌다. 쉬체르바츠키 가족은 어느 영국인 레이디 아무개의 가족과도, 또 독일인 백작 부인과 지난 전쟁에서 부상당한 그녀의 아들과도, 어느 학식 있는 스웨덴 사람과도, 그리고 카뉘 씨와 그의 누이와도 알게 되었다. 그러나 그럼에도 쉬체르바츠키 가족은 어쩔 수 없이 모스크바 부인 마리야 예브게니예브나 르티셰바와 그 딸의 무리 속으로 가장 많이 끌려들어 가게 되었다. 키티가 그 딸을 싫어했던 까닭은, 그 딸도 자기처럼 연애 사건으로 병이 났기 때문이었다. 그리고 키티가 어릴 적부터 알고 지냈고 늘 군복에 견장을 단 모습으로만 보아 온 어느 모스크바 대령의 무리에도 끌려들었는데, 그는 지금 작은 눈에 목을 드러내고 꽃무늬 넥타이를 두른 모습이 유난히 우스꽝스럽고 따분했으며, 도무지 떼어 낼 수가 없었기 때문이었다. 이 모든 것이 그렇게 단단히 자리를 잡자, 키티는 몹시 따분해지기 시작했는데, 특히 공작이 카를스바트로 떠나 버려 어머니와 단둘이 남게 된 뒤로 더욱 그러했다. 그녀는 자기가 아는 사람들에게는 아무 흥미도 느끼지 못했고, 그들에게서 새로운 것이라곤 아무것도 나오지 않으리라고 여겼다. 이 휴양지에서 그녀의 주된 정신적 관심사는 자기가 모르는 사람들을 관찰하며 그들에 관해 이런저런 짐작을 세우는 것이었다. 키티에게는 사람들의 모든 것을 언제나 가능한 한 가장 호의적인 빛 속에서 상상하는 특징이 있었는데, 특히 모르는 사람들에 대해서는 더욱 그러했다. 그래서 지금 그녀는 저 사람들이 누구인지, 서로 어떤 관계인지, 또 어떤 사람들인지 추측하면서, 그들에게 더없이 놀랍고 고귀한 성품을 부여했고, 자신의 관찰 속에서 그 생각을 확인하는 증거를 찾아냈다.\n\n이 사람들 가운데 그녀의 마음을 가장 끈 것은, 다들 슈탈 부인이라고 부르는 병약한 러시아 부인과 함께 휴양지에 온 어느 러시아 처녀였다. 슈탈 부인은 최상류 사교계에 속했지만, 너무 아파서 걷지도 못했고, 유난히 날이 좋은 날에만 환자용 마차를 타고 온천에 나타났다. 그러나 슈탈 부인이 그곳의 러시아 사람들 가운데 누구와도 사귀지 않은 것은, 쉬체르바츠카야 공작 부인이 해석하기로는, 병약함 때문이라기보다는 오만함 때문이었다. 그 러시아 처녀는 슈탈 부인을 돌보았고, 그뿐 아니라 키티가 보기에는 온천에 많이 와 있던, 중병에 걸린 환자들 모두와 친하게 지내며 더없이 자연스러운 태도로 그들을 보살폈다. 키티가 알아낸 바로는, 이 러시아 처녀는 슈탈 부인의 친척도 아니었고 보수를 받는 시중꾼도 아니었다. 슈탈 부인은 그녀를 바렌카라고 불렀고, 다른 사람들은 그녀를 \"마드무아젤 바렌카\"라고 불렀다. 이 처녀가 슈탈 부인이며 다른 낯선 사람들과 맺은 관계에 키티가 느낀 흥미와는 별개로, 키티는 흔히 그러하듯 마드무아젤 바렌카에게 설명할 수 없는 끌림을 느꼈고, 두 사람의 눈이 마주칠 때면 그녀 역시 자기를 좋아한다는 것을 알아챘다.\n\n마드무아젤 바렌카를 두고 첫 청춘이 지났다고는 할 수 없겠지만, 그녀는 말하자면 청춘이 없는 존재였다. 열아홉 살로 볼 수도 있었고 서른 살로 볼 수도 있었다. 이목구비를 따로따로 뜯어본다면, 병색이 도는 얼굴빛에도 불구하고 못생겼다기보다는 어여뻤다. 또한 지나치게 마른 몸과, 중간쯤 되는 키에 비해 너무 큰 머리만 아니었다면 몸매도 좋았을 것이다. 그러나 그녀가 남자들의 마음을 끌 것 같지는 않았다. 그녀는 한창때가 이미 지나 향기를 잃었으나 꽃잎만은 아직 시들지 않은, 아름다운 한 송이 꽃 같았다. 게다가 그녀는, 키티에게는 지나치게 많았던 바로 그것—억눌린 생명력의 불꽃과 자기 매력에 대한 의식—이 없다는 점에서도 남자들의 마음을 끌지 못할 것이었다.\n\n그녀는 언제나 의심의 여지가 없는 어떤 일에 몰두해 있는 듯 보였고, 그래서 그 밖의 무엇에도 흥미를 가질 수 없는 것처럼 보였다. 키티에게 마드무아젤 바렌카의 커다란 매력은 바로 자기 처지와 이루는 이러한 대조에 있었다. 키티는 그녀에게서, 그녀의 삶의 방식에서, 자기가 지금 그토록 괴롭게 찾고 있는 것의 본보기를 발견하리라고 느꼈다. 그것은 삶에 대한 흥미, 삶의 품위였다—키티가 그토록 역겨워했고, 지금은 살 사람을 찾아 물건을 부끄럽게 떠벌리며 파는 짓처럼 여겨지는, 처녀들과 남자들 사이의 세속적인 관계와는 무관한 것이었다. 키티가 이 낯선 친구를 더 주의 깊게 지켜볼수록, 이 처녀가 자기가 상상하던 그대로 완벽한 존재라는 확신이 더욱 굳어졌고, 그녀와 사귀고 싶은 마음도 더욱 간절해졌다.\n\n두 처녀는 하루에도 몇 번씩 마주치곤 했는데, 마주칠 때마다 키티의 눈은 이렇게 말했다. \"당신은 누구죠? 당신은 어떤 사람인가요? 당신은 정말로 내가 상상하는 그 빼어난 사람인가요? 하지만 제발,\" 그녀의 눈이 덧붙였다. \"내가 당신에게 억지로 사귀자고 들이댄다고는 생각하지 말아 줘요. 나는 그저 당신을 흠모하고 좋아할 뿐이에요.\" \"나도 당신이 좋아요, 당신은 아주, 아주 사랑스러워요. 시간만 있다면 당신을 더 좋아하게 될 텐데요.\" 낯선 처녀의 눈이 대답했다. 과연 키티는 그녀가 늘 바쁘다는 것을 알 수 있었다. 그녀는 어느 러시아 가족의 아이들을 온천에서 집으로 데려가거나, 아픈 부인을 위해 숄을 가져다 감싸 주거나, 신경질적인 환자의 흥미를 끌어 보려 애쓰거나, 누군가를 위해 차에 곁들일 케이크를 골라 사 오곤 했다.\n\n쉬체르바츠키 가족이 도착하고 얼마 지나지 않아, 아침의 온천 인파 속에 모두의 시선을, 그것도 곱지 않은 시선을 끄는 두 사람이 나타났다. 한 사람은 구부정한 몸에 커다란 손을 가진, 자기 몸에 비해 너무 짧은 낡은 외투를 걸친 키 큰 남자로, 검고 순박하면서도 어딘가 무시무시한 눈을 하고 있었다. 다른 한 사람은 얽은 자국이 있는, 인상이 선해 보이는 여자로, 옷차림은 몹시 형편없고 멋이라곤 없었다. 이들이 러시아 사람임을 알아본 키티는, 벌써 상상 속에서 그들에 관한 즐겁고 가슴 뭉클한 이야기를 지어내기 시작했다. 그러나 공작 부인이 투숙객 명단에서 이들이 니콜라이 레빈과 마리야 니콜라예브나라는 것을 알아내고는, 이 레빈이라는 자가 얼마나 못된 사람인지 키티에게 설명해 주자, 이 두 사람에 대한 그녀의 온갖 공상은 사라져 버렸다. 어머니가 들려준 이야기 때문이라기보다는, 그가 콘스탄틴의 형이라는 사실 때문에, 이 한 쌍은 갑자기 키티에게 몹시 불쾌하게 느껴졌다. 끊임없이 고개를 씰룩거리는 이 레빈은, 이제 그녀의 안에 억누를 수 없는 혐오감을 불러일으켰다.\n\n집요하게 자기를 좇는 그의 크고 무시무시한 눈이 증오와 경멸의 감정을 드러내는 것만 같아서, 그녀는 그와 마주치는 것을 피하려고 애썼다.\n\n# Chapter 31\n\n비 오는 날이었다. 아침 내내 비가 내렸고, 환자들은 양산을 받쳐 든 채 아케이드로 몰려들었다.\n\n키티는 어머니와 모스크바에서 온 대령과 함께 그곳을 거닐고 있었다. 대령은 프랑크푸르트에서 기성품으로 사 입은 유럽풍 외투 차림으로 말쑥하고 멋스러웠다. 그들은 아케이드 한쪽으로 걸으며, 맞은편을 걷고 있는 레빈을 피하려 애썼다. 바렌카는 어두운 빛깔의 드레스를 입고 챙이 아래로 늘어진 검은 모자를 쓴 채, 눈먼 프랑스 여인과 함께 아케이드의 끝에서 끝까지 오가고 있었고, 키티와 마주칠 때마다 둘은 다정한 눈길을 주고받았다.\n\n\"엄마, 저분에게 말을 걸어 보면 안 될까요?\" 키티는 알지도 못하는 자기 친구를 바라보며 말했다. 그녀가 샘 쪽으로 가는 것을 알아챘고, 거기서 함께 만날 수도 있겠다 싶었던 것이다.\n\n\"오, 정 그러고 싶으면 내가 먼저 저 여자에 대해 알아보고 직접 인사를 트마.\" 어머니가 대답했다. \"저 여자한테서 뭐 특별한 게 보이기라도 하니? 말동무겠지, 분명. 네가 원한다면 슈탈 부인하고 인사를 트마. 그 댁 belle-sœur는 내가 알던 사이였단다.\" 공작 부인은 거만하게 고개를 들며 덧붙였다.\n\n키티는 슈탈 부인이 자신과 인사하기를 피하는 듯 보였다는 사실에 어머니가 기분이 상해 있음을 알았다. 키티는 더 고집부리지 않았다.\n* belle-sœur: 프랑스어로 '시누이' 또는 '올케', '동서'.\n\n\"정말이지 더할 나위 없이 상냥한 분이에요!\" 마침 바렌카가 프랑스 여인에게 잔을 건네는 모습을 바라보며 키티가 말했다. \"보세요, 저 모든 게 얼마나 자연스럽고 사랑스러운지.\"\n\n\"네 engouements를 보고 있으면 어찌나 우습던지.\" 공작 부인이 말했다. \"아니, 우린 돌아가는 게 낫겠다.\" 그녀는 레빈이 일행과 독일인 의사를 데리고 자기들 쪽으로 다가오는 것을 알아채고 덧붙였다. 레빈은 그 의사에게 몹시 시끄럽고 성난 목소리로 뭔가를 떠들어 대고 있었다.\n\n* engouements: 프랑스어로 '심취', '열광'.\n그들이 돌아서려는데, 별안간 시끄러운 이야기 소리가 아니라 고함 소리가 들려왔다. 레빈이 우뚝 멈춰 서서 의사에게 소리를 지르고 있었고, 의사 또한 흥분해 있었다. 그들 주위로 사람들이 모여들었다. 공작 부인과 키티는 황급히 자리를 피했고, 대령은 무슨 일인지 알아보려고 사람들 틈에 끼어들었다.\n\n몇 분 뒤 대령이 그들을 따라잡았다.\n\n\"무슨 일이었어요?\" 공작 부인이 물었다.\n\n\"수치스럽고 망신스러운 일입니다!\" 대령이 대답했다. \"외국에서 가장 두려운 일이라면 러시아 사람을 만나는 것이지요. 그 키 큰 양반이 의사한테 욕설을 퍼붓고 있었습니다. 자기 마음에 꼭 들게 치료해 주지 않는다고 온갖 모욕을 쏟아붓더니, 급기야 지팡이를 휘두르기 시작하더군요. 정말이지 추태가 따로 없습니다!\"\n\n\"아, 정말 불쾌하기도 해라!\" 공작 부인이 말했다. \"그래서, 어떻게 끝났나요?\"\n\n\"다행히 그때 그…… 버섯 모양 모자를 쓴 그분이…… 끼어들었지요. 러시아 부인인 것 같던데요.\" 대령이 말했다.\n\n\"바렌카 양 말씀이세요?\" 키티가 물었다.\n\n\"그래요, 그래. 누구보다 먼저 나서서 도와주더군요. 그 남자의 팔을 잡고는 데리고 가 버렸어요.\"\n\n\"보세요, 엄마.\" 키티가 말했다. \"제가 저분에게 푹 빠진 걸 이상하게 여기시잖아요.\"\n\n이튿날, 키티는 알지도 못하는 자기 친구를 바라보다가, 바렌카 양이 이미 다른 protégés들을 대하듯 레빈과 그 일행과도 격의 없이 지내고 있음을 알아챘다. 그녀는 그들에게 다가가 이야기를 나누었고, 외국어라곤 한마디도 못 하는 그 여인을 위해 통역 노릇을 해 주었다.\n* protégés: 프랑스어로 '돌봐 주는 사람들', '피보호자들'.\n\n키티는 바렌카와 가까워지게 해 달라고 어머니에게 더욱더 간절히 졸랐다. 공작 부인으로서는 잘난 체하기 좋아하는 슈탈 부인에게 먼저 인사를 청하는 듯한 모양새가 마뜩잖았지만, 그래도 바렌카에 대해 알아보았고, 그 교제에 별 이로움은 없을지언정 해로울 것도 없다는 점을 입증하는 자세한 내막을 확인한 뒤에는, 자기가 직접 바렌카에게 다가가 인사를 텄다.\n\n딸이 샘으로 간 틈을 타, 바렌카가 빵집 앞에 멈춰 서 있을 때, 공작 부인은 그녀에게 다가갔다.\n\n\"통성명을 했으면 하는데요.\" 그녀는 위엄 있는 미소를 지으며 말했다. \"내 딸이 아가씨한테 홀딱 반했답니다.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"혹시 나를 모를 수도 있겠군요. 나는……\"\n\n\"그 마음이라면 저야말로 그 이상이랍니다, 공작 부인.\" 바렌카가 다급히 대답했다.\n\n\"어제 그 가엾은 우리 동포에게 얼마나 갸륵한 일을 해 주었던지요!\" 공작 부인이 말했다.\n\n바렌카는 살짝 얼굴을 붉혔다. \"기억이 안 나는데요. 제가 뭘 했다고는 생각하지 않아요.\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 그 레빈이라는 분을 곤란한 일에서 구해 주었잖아요.\"\n\n\"네, sa compagne가 저를 부르더군요. 그래서 그분을 달래 보았어요. 그분은 몹시 편찮으셨고, 의사에게 불만이 있으셨거든요. 저는 그런 환자들을 돌보는 데 익숙해서요.\"\n* sa compagne: 프랑스어로 '그의 동행 여인', '그의 동반자'.\n\n\"그렇군요. 아가씨가 멘토네에서 이모와—그러니까—슈탈 부인과 함께 산다고 들었어요. 그 댁 belle-sœur는 내가 알던 사이였답니다.\"\n\n\"아니에요, 그분은 제 이모가 아니에요. 저는 그분을 엄마라고 부르지만, 친척은 아니랍니다. 그분 손에 자랐을 뿐이에요.\" 바렌카가 다시 살짝 얼굴을 붉히며 대답했다.\n\n너무도 꾸밈없이 한 말이었고, 진실하고 솔직한 그 얼굴 표정이 어찌나 사랑스럽던지, 공작 부인은 키티가 왜 그토록 바렌카에게 마음을 빼앗겼는지 알 수 있었다.\n\n\"그래서, 그 레빈이라는 분은 어떻게 한답니까?\" 공작 부인이 물었다.\n\n\"떠나신답니다.\" 바렌카가 대답했다.\n\n바로 그때 키티가 샘에서 올라왔다. 어머니가 알지도 못하는 자기 친구와 인사를 텄다는 사실에 기쁨으로 환히 빛나는 얼굴이었다.\n\n\"자, 보렴, 키티, 네가 그토록 간절히 사귀고 싶어 하던 그 아가씨가……\"\n\n\"바렌카예요.\" 바렌카가 미소 지으며 끼어들었다. \"다들 저를 그렇게 불러요.\"\n\n키티는 기쁨으로 얼굴을 붉혔고, 아무 말 없이 천천히 새 친구의 손을 꼭 잡았다. 그 손은 키티의 손길에 응하지 않고 그저 그녀의 손안에 가만히 놓여 있었다. 손은 키티의 손길에 응하지 않았지만, 바렌카 양의 얼굴은 다소 애잔하면서도 부드럽고 기쁜 미소로 환하게 빛났고, 그 미소에 크지만 가지런한 이가 드러났다.\n\n\"저도 오래전부터 이러기를 바랐어요.\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 아가씨는 무척 바쁘잖아요.\"\n\n\"오, 아니에요, 저는 전혀 바쁘지 않아요.\" 바렌카가 대답했지만, 바로 그 순간 그녀는 새 친구들 곁을 떠나야 했다. 어느 환자의 자식인 러시아 소녀 둘이 그녀에게 달려왔기 때문이다.\n\n\"바렌카, 엄마가 불러요!\" 아이들이 외쳤다.\n\n그리고 바렌카는 아이들을 따라갔다.\n\n# Chapter 32\n\n공작 부인이 바렌카의 과거와 슈탈 부인과의 관계에 대해 알아낸 자세한 사정은 다음과 같았다.\n\n슈탈 부인에 대해 어떤 이들은 그녀가 남편을 들볶아 죽음으로 몰아갔다고 했고, 또 어떤 이들은 부도덕한 행실로 그녀를 비참하게 만든 쪽이 남편이었다고 했는데, 그녀는 본래부터 몸이 약하고 격정적인 기질을 지닌 여자였다. 남편과 헤어진 뒤 하나뿐인 아이를 낳았을 때 그 아이는 거의 곧바로 죽고 말았고, 그녀의 예민한 감수성을 잘 아는 슈탈 부인의 가족은 그 소식이 그녀를 죽게 할까 두려워 다른 아이를, 그러니까 같은 날 밤 페테르부르크의 같은 집에서 태어난 황실 수석 요리사의 딸을 바꿔치기했다. 그 아이가 바로 바렌카였다. 슈탈 부인은 나중에 바렌카가 자기 친자식이 아니라는 것을 알았지만, 계속 그녀를 키웠다. 더욱이 얼마 지나지 않아 바렌카에게는 살아 있는 혈육이 하나도 남지 않았기 때문이다. 슈탈 부인은 이제 십 년 넘게 줄곧 외국에서, 남쪽 지방에서 소파에 누운 채 한 번도 일어나지 못하며 지내 왔다. 어떤 이들은 슈탈 부인이 자선가이자 신앙심 깊은 여인이라는 사교계의 지위를 스스로 만들어 냈다고 했고, 또 어떤 이들은 그녀가 스스로 내세우는 대로 오직 이웃의 행복만을 위해 사는, 진심으로 더없이 윤리적인 인간이라고 했다. 그녀의 신앙이 무엇인지—가톨릭인지, 신교인지, 정교인지—아무도 알지 못했다. 그러나 한 가지 사실만은 의심의 여지가 없었으니, 그녀가 모든 교회와 종파의 최고 고위 성직자들과 두루 친밀한 관계를 맺고 있다는 점이었다.\n\n바렌카는 외국에 머무는 내내 그녀와 함께 살았고, 슈탈 부인을 아는 사람이라면 누구나 모두가 부르는 대로 마드무아젤 바렌카를 알고 좋아했다.\n\n이 모든 사실을 알고 난 공작 부인은 딸이 바렌카와 가까이 지내는 데에 흠잡을 것이 전혀 없다고 여겼다. 게다가 바렌카의 가정교육과 학식은 더없이 훌륭했으며—프랑스어와 영어를 빼어나게 잘했다—무엇보다 중요한 것은, 슈탈 부인이 건강이 나빠 공작 부인과 인사를 나누지 못해 유감이라는 전갈을 바렌카가 전해 왔다는 점이었다.\n\n바렌카와 알고 지내게 되면서 키티는 점점 더 친구에게 매료되었고, 날마다 그녀에게서 새로운 미덕을 발견했다.\n\n공작 부인은 바렌카가 목소리가 좋다는 말을 듣고, 저녁에 와서 노래를 불러 달라고 청했다.\n\n\"키티가 피아노를 치고, 우리 집에 피아노도 있어요. 좋은 건 아니지만요, 그래도 우리한테 큰 기쁨을 줄 거예요.\" 공작 부인은 그 꾸민 듯한 미소를 지으며 말했는데, 키티는 바렌카가 노래할 마음이 없다는 것을 알아챘기 때문에 그때만큼은 그 미소가 유난히 거슬렸다. 그래도 바렌카는 저녁에 악보 한 묶음을 들고 왔다. 공작 부인은 마리야 예브게니예브나와 그 딸, 그리고 대령을 초대해 놓았다.\n\n바렌카는 모르는 사람들이 있다는 것에 조금도 거리낌이 없어 보였고, 곧장 피아노 쪽으로 갔다. 그녀는 직접 반주를 하지는 못했지만, 악보를 보고 노래하는 것은 무척 잘했다. 피아노를 잘 치는 키티가 반주를 맡았다.\n\n\"당신은 놀라운 재능을 지녔군요.\" 바렌카가 첫 곡을 더없이 훌륭하게 부르고 나자 공작 부인이 그녀에게 말했다.\n\n마리야 예브게니예브나와 그 딸도 고마움과 감탄을 표했다.\n\n\"보세요.\" 창밖을 내다보며 대령이 말했다. \"당신의 노래를 들으려고 청중이 이렇게나 모였어요.\" 실제로 창문 아래에는 제법 많은 사람들이 모여 있었다.\n\n\"여러분께 기쁨을 드린다니 저도 무척 기뻐요.\" 바렌카가 담담하게 대답했다.\n\n키티는 자랑스러운 마음으로 친구를 바라보았다. 그녀는 바렌카의 재능에, 그 목소리에, 그 얼굴에 매료되었지만, 무엇보다 그 태도에, 바렌카가 자기 노래를 대수롭지 않게 여기고 사람들의 칭찬에도 조금도 동요하지 않는 그 모습에 사로잡혔다. 그녀는 그저 이렇게 묻고 있는 것 같았다. \"또 불러야 하나요, 아니면 이만하면 됐나요?\"\n\n'만약 나였다면,' 키티는 생각했다. '나는 얼마나 우쭐했을까! 창문 아래 모인 저 사람들을 보고 얼마나 기뻐했을까! 그런데 저 애는 조금도 동요하지 않아. 거절하지 않고 엄마를 기쁘게 해 드리려는 게 유일한 이유야. 저 애 안에는 뭐가 있는 걸까? 무엇이 저 애에게 모든 것을 내려다보고, 무엇에도 흔들리지 않고 차분할 수 있는 힘을 주는 걸까? 그걸 알고 싶어, 저 애한테서 배우고 싶어!' 키티는 친구의 평온한 얼굴을 들여다보며 생각했다. 공작 부인이 한 곡 더 불러 달라고 청하자, 바렌카는 또 다른 곡을 역시나 매끄럽고 또렷하게, 훌륭하게 불렀다. 피아노 앞에 꼿꼿이 선 채, 가무잡잡하고 가느다란 손으로 피아노를 두드리며 박자를 맞추면서.\n\n악보집의 다음 곡은 이탈리아 노래였다. 키티는 첫 소절을 치고는 바렌카를 돌아보았다.\n\n\"그건 건너뛰어요.\" 바렌카가 살짝 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다. 키티는 당황과 의아함이 담긴 눈길로 바렌카의 얼굴을 가만히 바라보았다.\n\n\"좋아요, 그럼 다음 곡으로.\" 키티는 황급히 말하며 책장을 넘겼고, 곧 그 노래에 무언가 사연이 얽혀 있다는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n\"아니에요.\" 바렌카가 미소를 지으며 악보 위에 손을 얹고 대답했다. \"아니, 그걸 불러요.\" 그리고 그녀는 다른 곡들과 똑같이 조용하게, 똑같이 차분하게, 똑같이 훌륭하게 그 노래를 불렀다.\n\n노래를 마치자 모두 다시 그녀에게 고마움을 표하고 차를 마시러 갔다. 키티와 바렌카는 집에 딸린 작은 정원으로 나갔다.\n\n\"내 생각이 맞나요, 그 노래에 무슨 추억이 얽혀 있죠?\" 키티가 말했다. \"말하지 않아도 돼요.\" 그녀는 서둘러 덧붙였다. \"그냥 내 생각이 맞는지만 말해 줘요.\"\n\n\"아니, 못 할 게 뭐 있어요? 그냥 말해 줄게요.\" 바렌카는 이렇게 말하고는 대답을 기다리지도 않고 이어 갔다. \"맞아요, 그 노래는 추억을 떠올리게 해요. 한때는 괴로운 추억이었죠. 옛날에 누군가를 좋아했는데, 그 사람에게 그 노래를 불러 주곤 했어요.\"\n\n키티는 크게 뜬 눈으로 말없이, 안쓰러운 마음으로 바렌카를 바라보았다.\n\n\"나는 그를 좋아했고, 그도 나를 좋아했어요. 그런데 그의 어머니가 원하지 않았고, 그는 다른 여자와 결혼했어요. 지금 그 사람은 우리 집에서 멀지 않은 곳에 살고 있어서, 가끔 만나기도 해요. 나한테도 연애 이야기가 있을 줄은 몰랐죠.\" 그녀가 말했다. 그 아름다운 얼굴에는, 한때 그녀의 온몸을 타오르게 했으리라고 키티가 느낀 그 불꽃이 희미하게 어른거렸다.\n\n\"몰랐냐고요? 아니, 내가 남자였다면 당신을 알고 난 다음에는 결코 다른 사람을 좋아할 수 없었을 거예요. 다만 어떻게 그 사람이 어머니를 기쁘게 하려고 당신을 잊고 당신을 불행하게 만들 수 있었는지는 도무지 이해가 안 가요. 그 사람은 심장이 없었던 거예요.\"\n\n\"오, 아니에요, 그는 아주 좋은 사람이에요. 그리고 나는 불행하지 않아요. 오히려 정반대로, 아주 행복해요. 자, 그럼 이제 더는 노래하지 않겠네요.\" 그녀는 집 쪽으로 돌아서며 덧붙였다.\n\n\"당신은 어쩜 그렇게 좋은 사람일까요! 정말 좋은 사람이에요!\" 키티가 외치며 그녀를 멈춰 세우고 입을 맞추었다. \"내가 당신을 조금이라도 닮을 수 있다면!\"\n\n\"왜 누구를 닮으려고 해요? 당신은 지금 모습 그대로 사랑스러운데.\" 바렌카가 그 부드럽고 지친 미소를 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 난 조금도 사랑스럽지 않아요. 자, 말해 줘요…… 잠깐만요, 우리 앉아요.\" 키티는 그녀를 다시 자기 곁에 앉히며 말했다. \"말해 줘요, 어떤 남자가 당신의 사랑을 무시했다고, 그가 그 사랑에 마음 쓰지 않았다고 생각하면 굴욕스럽지 않아요?……\"\n\n\"하지만 그는 내 사랑을 무시하지 않았어요. 내 생각엔 그도 나를 좋아했지만, 효성 깊은 아들이었던 거죠……\"\n\n\"맞아요, 하지만 만약 그의 어머니 때문이 아니라, 그 자신이 한 일이었다면요?……\" 키티는 자기가 비밀을 털어놓고 있다는 것을, 그리고 수치심으로 화끈 달아오른 자기 얼굴이 이미 자신을 드러내 버렸다는 것을 느끼며 말했다.\n\n\"그랬다면 그가 잘못한 거고, 나는 그를 안타까워하지 않았을 거예요.\" 바렌카가 대답했다. 그녀는 이제 그들이 자기 이야기가 아니라 키티의 이야기를 하고 있다는 것을 분명히 알아챈 듯했다.\n\n\"하지만 그 굴욕은요.\" 키티가 말했다. \"결코 잊을 수 없는, 결코 잊을 수 없는 그 굴욕은요.\" 그녀는 지난번 무도회에서 음악이 멈춘 사이에 자기가 지었던 그 눈길을 떠올리며 말했다.\n\n\"굴욕이라니, 어디에 굴욕이 있어요? 당신은 아무 잘못도 안 했잖아요?\"\n\n\"잘못보다 더 나쁜 거예요. 부끄러운 일이죠.\"\n\n바렌카는 고개를 가로젓고 키티의 손 위에 자기 손을 얹었다.\n\n\"아니, 부끄러울 게 뭐가 있어요?\" 그녀가 말했다. \"당신을 좋아하지도 않는 남자한테, 당신이 그를 사랑한다고 말한 건 아니잖아요, 그렇죠?\"\n\n\"물론 그러지 않았죠. 한마디도 하지 않았어요. 하지만 그는 알고 있었어요. 아니, 아니에요, 눈빛이라는 게 있고, 몸짓이라는 게 있잖아요. 백 년을 산다 해도 그걸 잊을 수 없을 거예요.\"\n\n\"왜 그래요? 난 이해가 안 가요. 핵심은 지금 당신이 그를 사랑하느냐 아니냐 하는 거잖아요.\" 무엇이든 있는 그대로 이름 붙여 부르는 바렌카가 말했다.\n\n\"난 그가 미워요. 나 자신을 용서할 수가 없어요.\"\n\n\"왜요, 뭣 때문에요?\"\n\n\"그 수치심, 그 굴욕 때문이죠!\"\n\n\"오! 다들 당신만큼 예민하다면 어쩌겠어요!\" 바렌카가 말했다. \"똑같은 일을 겪어 보지 않은 처녀는 없어요. 그리고 그건 다 그렇게 대단치 않은 일이에요.\"\n\n\"그럼 뭐가 대단한 거예요?\" 키티가 호기심 어린 놀라움으로 그녀의 얼굴을 들여다보며 말했다.\n\n\"오, 대단한 건 정말 많아요.\" 바렌카가 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"그게 뭔데요?\"\n\n\"오, 그보다 훨씬 더 대단한 게 많죠.\" 바렌카는 뭐라고 말해야 할지 몰라 대답했다. 그런데 바로 그 순간 창문에서 공작 부인의 목소리가 들려왔다. \"키티, 춥구나! 숄을 두르든지, 아니면 안으로 들어오렴.\"\n\n\"정말 들어갈 시간이네요!\" 바렌카가 일어서며 말했다. \"베르트 부인 댁에도 가 봐야 해요. 와 달라고 했거든요.\"\n\n키티는 그녀의 손을 잡고, 간절한 호기심과 애원이 담긴 눈으로 그녀에게 물었다. '대체 뭐예요, 당신에게 그런 평온함을 주는 그 대단한 게 뭐냐고요? 알고 있잖아요, 말해 줘요!' 하지만 바렌카는 키티의 눈이 무엇을 묻고 있는지조차 알지 못했다. 그녀는 그저 그날 저녁 베르트 부인도 만나러 가야 한다는 것과, 열두 시에 있을 maman의 차 시간에 늦지 않게 서둘러 집으로 돌아가야 한다는 것만 생각하고 있었다. 그녀는 안으로 들어가 악보를 챙기고, 모두에게 작별 인사를 하고는 막 나가려 했다.\n* maman: 프랑스어로 '엄마'.\n\n\"댁까지 바래다드리지요.\" 대령이 말했다.\n\n\"그럼요, 이렇게 밤에 어떻게 혼자 가겠어요?\" 공작 부인도 거들었다. \"어쨌든 파라샤를 딸려 보낼게요.\"\n\n키티는 바렌카가 자기에게 동행이 필요하다는 말에 웃음을 간신히 참고 있는 것을 보았다.\n\n\"아니에요, 저는 늘 혼자 다니지만 아무 일도 없었는걸요.\" 그녀가 모자를 집어 들며 말했다. 그리고 키티에게 다시 한번 입을 맞추고는, 무엇이 대단한 것인지 말해 주지 않은 채, 악보를 옆구리에 낀 채 씩씩하게 발을 내디뎌 여름밤의 어스름 속으로 사라졌다. 무엇이 대단한 것인지, 그리고 무엇이 그토록 부러운 평온과 기품을 그녀에게 주는지, 그 비밀을 안고서.\n\n# Chapter 33\n\n키티는 슈탈 부인과도 친분을 맺게 되었는데, 이 친분은 바렌카와의 우정과 더불어 그녀에게 커다란 영향을 미쳤을 뿐 아니라 마음의 고통 속에 있던 그녀를 위로해 주기도 했다. 그녀는 이 친분을 통해 자기 앞에 활짝 열린 완전히 새로운 세계 속에서 그 위안을 발견했다. 그것은 자신의 과거와는 아무런 공통점도 없는 세계, 고결하고 숭고한 세계였으며, 그 높은 곳에서 그녀는 자기 과거를 차분히 내려다볼 수 있었다. 키티가 그때까지 몸을 맡겨 온 본능적인 삶 말고도 영적인 삶이 존재한다는 것이 그녀에게 드러났다. 이 삶은 종교 안에서 모습을 드러냈는데, 그것은 키티가 어린 시절부터 알아 온 종교, 그러니까 친구들과 마주칠 수 있는 미망인의 집에서 열리는 기도식과 철야 예배, 사제와 함께 슬라브어 성경 구절을 외우는 일로 표현되던 그 종교와는 아무런 공통점도 없는 종교였다. 이것은 고귀하고 신비로운 종교였으며, 일련의 숭고한 사상과 감정에 연결되어 있어서, 그저 그렇게 하라는 말을 들어서 믿는 것 이상으로, 사랑할 수 있는 종교였다.\n\n키티가 이 모든 것을 알게 된 것은 말을 통해서가 아니었다. 슈탈 부인은 마치 젊은 시절의 추억을 바라보듯 흐뭇하게 바라보게 되는 사랑스러운 아이를 대하듯 키티에게 이야기했고, 단 한 번 지나가는 말로, 인간의 모든 슬픔 속에서 위안을 주는 것은 오직 사랑과 믿음뿐이며, 우리를 향한 그리스도의 연민 앞에서는 어떤 슬픔도 하찮지 않다고 말했을 뿐, 곧바로 다른 이야기로 넘어갔다. 그러나 슈탈 부인의 모든 몸짓에서, 모든 말에서, 키티가 천상의 것이라 부르던 모든 눈길에서, 그리고 무엇보다 바렌카에게서 들은 그녀의 일생 이야기 전체에서, 키티는 그때까지 전혀 알지 못했던 그 무언가, '중요한 것'을 알아보았다.\n\n그러나 슈탈 부인의 성품이 아무리 고결하고, 그녀의 일생이 아무리 가슴을 울리며, 그녀의 말이 아무리 숭고하고 감동적이었다 해도, 키티는 그녀에게서 자신을 어리둥절하게 만드는 몇 가지 면모를 발견하지 않을 수 없었다. 그녀의 가족에 대해 물었을 때 슈탈 부인이 경멸하듯 미소 지었다는 것을 키티는 알아챘는데, 그것은 기독교적인 온유함과는 어울리지 않았다. 또 슈탈 부인 곁에서 가톨릭 사제를 발견했을 때, 그녀가 일부러 갓 그늘 속에 얼굴을 감추고 묘한 방식으로 미소 지었다는 것도 알아챘다. 이 두 가지 관찰은 사소한 것이었지만 그녀를 어리둥절하게 만들었고, 그녀는 슈탈 부인에 대해 의심을 품게 되었다. 그러나 다른 한편으로 바렌카는, 이 세상에 홀로 친구도 친척도 없이, 과거에 우울한 환멸을 겪었으면서도 아무것도 바라지 않고 아무것도 후회하지 않는, 바로 키티가 감히 꿈꾸지도 못할 그 완벽함 자체였다. 바렌카를 통해 그녀는 자기 자신을 잊고 남을 사랑하기만 하면 차분해지고 행복해지며 고결해진다는 것을 깨달았다. 그것이 바로 키티가 되고자 갈망하던 것이었다. 무엇이 가장 중요한 것인지를 이제 분명히 보게 된 키티는 그것에 열광하는 것만으로는 만족하지 못했다. 그녀는 곧바로 자기 앞에 열리고 있는 새로운 삶에 온 영혼을 다해 자신을 내맡겼다. 바렌카가 들려준 슈탈 부인과 그녀가 언급한 다른 사람들의 행적에 관한 이야기를 통해 키티는 이미 자기 미래의 삶에 대한 계획을 세워 놓고 있었다. 그녀는 바렌카가 자주 이야기해 준 슈탈 부인의 조카 알린처럼, 자기가 어디에 살든 어려움에 처한 사람들을 찾아내어 할 수 있는 한 그들을 돕고, 그들에게 복음서를 주고, 병자와 죄인과 죽어 가는 이들에게 복음서를 읽어 줄 작정이었다. 알린이 그랬듯 죄인들에게 복음서를 읽어 준다는 생각은 특히 키티를 매혹시켰다. 그러나 이 모든 것은 은밀한 꿈이었고, 키티는 그것을 어머니에게도 바렌카에게도 이야기하지 않았다.\n\n그러나 자신의 계획을 큰 규모로 실행에 옮길 때를 기다리는 동안에도, 키티는 그때 이미 병들고 불행한 사람들이 그토록 많은 온천에서 바렌카를 본받아 자신의 새로운 원칙을 실천할 기회를 어렵지 않게 찾아냈다.\n\n처음에 공작 부인은 키티가 그녀의 말마따나 슈탈 부인에 대한, 그리고 더욱이 바렌카에 대한 앙구망에 푹 빠져 있다는 것밖에는 알아채지 못했다. 그녀는 키티가 바렌카의 행동을 본받을 뿐 아니라 무의식적으로 그녀의 걸음걸이며 말투며 눈을 깜박이는 방식까지 흉내 내고 있는 것을 보았다. 그러나 나중에 공작 부인은 이러한 숭배 말고도 딸에게서 어떤 진지한 영적 변화가 일어나고 있음을 알아챘다.\n* 앙구망(engouement): 프랑스어로 '맹목적인 심취', '열중'.\n\n공작 부인은 저녁이면 키티가 슈탈 부인에게서 받은 프랑스어 성경을 읽는 것을 보았다. 전에는 한 번도 한 적 없는 일이었다. 또 딸이 사교계의 지인들을 피하고 바렌카의 보살핌을 받는 병자들, 특히 병든 화가 페트로프의 가난한 가족과 어울리는 것을 보았다. 키티가 그 가족에게 자비의 수녀 역할을 하는 것을 자랑스러워한다는 것은 의심할 여지가 없었다. 이 모든 것은 그런대로 괜찮았고, 공작 부인도 그것에 대해 딱히 할 말은 없었다. 더욱이 페트로프의 아내는 더없이 착한 여자였고, 키티의 헌신을 본 독일 공작 부인이 그녀를 위로의 천사라 부르며 칭찬하기까지 했으니 말이다. 지나침만 없었다면 이 모든 것은 아주 좋았을 것이다. 그러나 공작 부인은 딸이 극단으로 치닫고 있는 것을 보았고, 그래서 정말로 딸에게 그렇게 말했다.\n\n\"무슨 일이든 결코 도를 넘어서는 안 된단다.\" 그녀가 딸에게 말했다.\n\n딸은 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았지만, 마음속으로는 기독교에 관한 일에 지나침을 운운할 수는 없다고 생각했다. 한쪽 뺨을 맞으면 다른 쪽 뺨을 내밀고, 겉옷을 빼앗기면 속옷마저 내주라고 명하는 교리를 실천하는 데 무슨 지나침이 있을 수 있겠는가? 그러나 공작 부인은 이러한 지나침이 못마땅했고, 딸이 자기에게 속마음을 다 보여 주려 하지 않는다는 것이 더더욱 못마땅했다. 키티는 실제로 자신의 새로운 견해와 감정을 어머니에게 숨겼다. 그녀가 그것을 숨긴 것은 어머니를 존경하지 않거나 사랑하지 않아서가 아니라, 단지 그녀가 자기 어머니였기 때문이다. 그녀는 어머니보다는 차라리 다른 누구에게라도 먼저 그것을 털어놓았을 것이다.\n* 무슨 일이든…안 된단다(Il ne faut jamais rien outrer): 프랑스어.\n\n\"안나 파블로브나는 어째서 그렇게 오랫동안 우리를 보러 오지 않는 거니?\" 어느 날 공작 부인이 페트로바 부인을 두고 말했다. \"내가 청해 봤는데도 뭔가에 기분이 상한 것 같더구나.\"\n\n\"아니에요, 저는 못 느꼈는데요, maman.\" 키티가 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다.\n\n\"그 사람들을 보러 간 지 오래됐니?\"\n\n\"내일 산으로 소풍을 갈 작정이에요.\" 키티가 대답했다.\n\n\"그래, 다녀오렴.\" 공작 부인은 딸의 당황한 얼굴을 바라보며 그 당혹감의 원인을 알아내려 애쓰면서 대답했다.\n\n그날 바렌카가 저녁 식사를 하러 와서, 안나 파블로브나가 마음을 바꿔 내일 소풍을 그만두기로 했다고 전했다. 그리고 공작 부인은 키티가 또다시 얼굴을 붉히는 것을 알아챘다.\n\n\"키티, 페트로프네 사람들과 무슨 오해라도 있었던 거니?\" 둘만 남게 되었을 때 공작 부인이 말했다. \"어째서 그 사람이 아이들을 보내지도 않고 우리를 보러 오지도 않게 된 거지?\"\n\n키티는 그들 사이에 아무 일도 없었으며, 안나 파블로브나가 왜 자기에게 언짢아하는 것 같은지 알 수 없다고 대답했다. 키티는 더없이 정직하게 대답했다. 그녀는 안나 파블로브나가 자기에게 태도를 바꾼 이유를 알지 못했지만, 짐작은 했다. 그녀는 어머니에게 말할 수 없는, 자기 자신에게조차 말로 표현하지 못하는 무언가를 짐작했다. 그것은 알고는 있으나 자기 자신에게조차 결코 입에 담을 수 없는 그런 것들 중 하나였으니, 만약 잘못 짚은 것이라면 너무도 끔찍하고 부끄러운 일일 터였다.\n\n그녀는 그 가족과 맺은 모든 관계를 거듭거듭 기억 속에서 되짚어 보았다. 만날 때마다 안나 파블로브나의 둥글고 사람 좋은 얼굴에 떠오르던 순수한 기쁨을 떠올렸다. 환자에 관해 둘이서 나누던 은밀한 의논, 그에게 금지된 일에서 그를 떼어 놓고 바깥바람을 쐬게 하려던 둘의 모의도 떠올렸다. 그녀를 \"나의 키티\"라고 부르며 그녀 없이는 잠자리에 들려고 하지 않던 막내아이의 애정도 떠올렸다. 그 모든 것이 얼마나 좋았던가! 그러고 나서 그녀는 갈색 외투를 걸친 페트로프의 무섭도록 마르고 야윈 모습을, 긴 목과 숱 적은 곱슬머리, 처음에는 키티에게 그토록 무섭게 느껴지던 무언가를 묻는 듯한 푸른 눈, 그녀 앞에서 기운차고 활기차 보이려고 애쓰던 안쓰러운 모습을 떠올렸다. 모든 폐병 환자에게 그렇듯 그에게도 느끼던 혐오감을 이겨 내려고 처음에 기울였던 노력과, 그에게 무슨 말을 할까 궁리하느라 들였던 수고를 떠올렸다. 그가 그녀를 바라보던 수줍고 부드러워진 눈길과, 그때 느꼈던 연민과 어색함이라는 묘한 감정, 그리고 나중에는 자기 자신이 착한 사람이라는 느낌까지도 떠올렸다. 그 모든 것이 얼마나 좋았던가! 그러나 그것은 다 처음의 일이었다. 그런데 며칠 전, 모든 것이 갑자기 망가져 버렸다. 안나 파블로브나는 가식적인 다정함으로 키티를 맞이했고, 그녀와 자기 남편을 끊임없이 감시했다.\n\n그녀가 가까이 다가갈 때 그가 보이던 그 가슴 뭉클한 기쁨이 안나 파블로브나가 냉랭해진 원인일 수 있을까?\n\n\"그래.\" 그녀는 곰곰이 생각했다. \"그저께 안나 파블로브나가 화난 목소리로 '저것 봐요, 그이는 계속 당신을 기다릴 거예요. 그렇게 끔찍이도 쇠약해졌으면서도 당신 없이는 커피도 안 마시려 들었다니까요'라고 했을 때, 그녀에게는 뭔가 부자연스러운, 그녀의 선량한 천성과는 전혀 어울리지 않는 데가 있었어.\"\n\n\"그래, 어쩌면 내가 그에게 무릎 덮개를 건넸을 때 그녀가 그걸 못마땅해했는지도 몰라. 그건 정말 아무것도 아닌 일이었는데, 그가 그걸 너무 어색하게 받고 너무 오래도록 내게 고마워하는 바람에 나까지 어색해졌지. 게다가 그가 그토록 잘 그려 준 내 초상화도 있고. 그리고 무엇보다 그 당황하고 다정한 눈길이! 그래, 그래, 바로 그거야!\" 키티는 공포에 질려 속으로 거듭 되뇌었다. \"아니야, 그럴 리 없어, 그래서는 안 돼! 그는 너무도 가엾은 사람인데!\" 그녀는 곧바로 자기 자신에게 말했다.\n\n이 의심이 그녀의 새로운 삶이 지닌 매력을 망가뜨렸다.\n\n# Chapter 34\n\n치료를 위한 온천 음용 요법이 끝나기 전에, 카를스바트를 떠나 바덴과 키싱겐의 러시아 친구들을 찾아갔던—본인 말로는 러시아 공기를 좀 쐬러 갔던—셰르바츠키 공작이 아내와 딸에게로 돌아왔다.\n\n외국 생활에 대한 공작과 공작 부인의 견해는 완전히 정반대였다. 공작 부인은 무엇이든 다 즐거워했고, 러시아 사교계에서 확고한 지위를 누리고 있었음에도 외국에서는 유럽풍의 멋쟁이 귀부인처럼 굴려고 애썼다. 그러나 그녀는 그런 부류가 아니었으니—이유는 단순했다, 그녀는 전형적인 러시아 귀부인이었으므로—그래서 그녀의 태도는 어딘가 가식적이었고, 그것이 그녀에게 그리 어울리지도 않았다. 공작은 그와 반대로, 외국적인 것이라면 무엇이든 질색했고, 유럽 생활에 넌더리를 냈으며, 자신의 러시아식 습관을 고수했고, 외국에서는 일부러 실제보다 덜 유럽적으로 보이려 들었다.\n\n공작은 더 야윈 모습으로, 두 뺨의 살가죽이 헐렁한 주머니처럼 늘어진 채 돌아왔지만, 더없이 쾌활한 기분이었다. 키티가 완전히 회복한 것을 보자 그의 좋은 기분은 한층 더해졌다. 키티가 슈탈 부인과 바렌카와 가깝게 지낸다는 소식, 그리고 공작 부인이 키티에게서 어떤 변화를 알아챘다며 전한 이야기는 공작의 마음을 어지럽혔고, 자기 딸을 자기에게서 떼어 놓는 모든 것에 대한 그의 습관적인 질투심과, 자기 딸이 자신의 영향이 미치지 못하는 곳, 그가 다다를 수 없는 영역으로 빠져나가 버렸을지도 모른다는 두려움을 불러일으켰다. 그러나 이런 불쾌한 일들은 그의 안에 늘 자리 잡고 있던, 그리고 카를스바트 온천 요법 이후로 그 어느 때보다 넘쳐흐르던 친절과 명랑함의 바다에 죄다 잠겨 버렸다.\n\n도착한 다음 날, 공작은 긴 외투를 걸치고, 풀 먹인 깃으로 러시아식 주름과 늘어진 뺨을 떠받친 채, 더없이 좋은 기분으로 딸과 함께 온천으로 향했다.\n\n화창한 아침이었다. 작은 정원이 딸린 밝고 명랑한 집들, 발그레한 얼굴과 발그레한 팔로 맥주를 들이켜며 즐겁게 일하는 독일 여종업원들의 모습은 마음을 흐뭇하게 했다. 그러나 온천에 가까워질수록 병자들과 더 자주 마주쳤고, 그들의 모습은 풍요로운 독일 생활의 일상적인 풍경 속에서 그 어느 때보다 더 가련해 보였다. 키티는 더 이상 이런 대조에 충격을 받지 않았다. 그녀에게는 밝은 햇살도, 눈부신 초록빛 나뭇잎도, 흘러나오는 음악 소리도, 그녀가 더 야위어 가는지 아니면 회복되어 가는지 눈여겨보던 그 낯익은 얼굴들의 자연스러운 배경일 뿐이었다. 그러나 공작에게는 6월 아침의 밝음과 흥겨움도, 당시 유행하던 경쾌한 왈츠를 연주하는 오케스트라 소리도, 무엇보다 건강한 수행원들의 모습도, 유럽 곳곳에서 모여든 이 느릿느릿 움직이며 죽어 가는 형체들과 나란히 놓이니 어딘가 꼴사납고 흉측한 것으로 비쳤다. 가장 아끼는 딸을 팔에 걸치고 느끼는 자랑스러움과, 말하자면 다시 젊어진 듯한 기분에도 불구하고, 그는 어색함을 느꼈고, 자신의 힘찬 걸음걸이와 튼튼하고 우람한 팔다리가 거의 부끄러울 지경이었다. 마치 군중 속에서 혼자 옷을 제대로 갖춰 입지 못한 사람이 된 듯한 기분이었다.\n\n\"네 새 친구들을 좀 소개해 다오.\" 그는 팔꿈치로 딸의 손을 꼭 누르며 말했다. \"너를 이렇게 다시 건강하게 해 준 그 끔찍한 조덴까지도 마음에 드는구나. 다만 여긴 우울해, 무척이나 우울하구나. 저 사람은 누구냐?\"\n\n키티는 마주치는 사람들의 이름을 일일이 댔는데, 그중에는 아는 사람도 있었고 모르는 사람도 있었다. 정원 입구에서 그들은 눈먼 여인 베르트 부인과 그녀를 인도하는 사람과 마주쳤고, 공작은 그 늙은 프랑스 여인이 키티의 목소리를 듣자 얼굴이 환해지는 것을 보고 기뻐했다. 그녀는 곧장 프랑스식의 과장된 정중함으로 그에게 말을 걸기 시작했고, 이렇게 사랑스러운 딸을 두었다며 그를 칭찬하고, 키티를 면전에서 하늘 끝까지 추켜세우며, 그녀를 보배니 진주니 위안을 주는 천사니 하고 불렀다.\n\"그렇다면 이 아이는 두 번째 천사로군요.\" 공작이 미소 지으며 말했다. \"마드무아젤 바렌카를 일 번 천사라고 부른다지요.\"\n\n\"오! 마드무아젤 바렌카, 그 아이야말로 진짜 천사지요, allez.\" 베르트 부인이 맞장구를 쳤다.\n\n* allez: 프랑스어로 '정말이지', '그렇고말고요' 정도의 감탄적 표현.\n\n아케이드에서 그들은 바렌카와 마주쳤다. 그녀는 우아한 빨간 가방을 들고 빠른 걸음으로 그들 쪽으로 다가오고 있었다.\n\n\"아빠가 오셨어요.\" 키티가 그녀에게 말했다.\n\n바렌카는—그녀가 무엇이든 그렇게 하듯 소박하고 자연스럽게—절과 무릎 굽혀 인사하는 동작의 중간쯤 되는 몸짓을 했고, 곧바로 수줍음 없이, 자연스럽게, 누구에게나 말하듯 공작에게 말을 건넸다.\n\n\"물론 알고말고요, 아주 잘 알지요.\" 공작이 미소를 띠며 그녀에게 말했는데, 키티는 그 미소에서 아버지가 자기 친구를 마음에 들어 한다는 것을 기쁘게 알아차렸다. \"그렇게 서둘러 어디로 가는 길인가요?\"\n\n\"엄마가 여기 계세요.\" 그녀가 키티를 돌아보며 말했다. \"밤새 한숨도 못 주무셔서, 의사가 바깥바람을 쐬라고 권했거든. 손일거리를 갖다 드리는 길이야.\"\n\n\"그래, 저 아이가 일 번 천사인 거냐?\" 바렌카가 가 버리자 공작이 물었다.\n\n키티는 아버지가 바렌카를 놀려 줄 작정이었으나, 그녀가 마음에 들어서 그러지 못한다는 것을 알아차렸다.\n\n\"자, 그러니 네 친구들을 다 만나 보게 되겠구나.\" 그가 말을 이었다. \"슈탈 부인이 황송하게도 나를 알아봐 준다면 그 부인까지도 말이다.\"\n\n\"아니, 그 부인을 아세요, 아빠?\" 키티가 슈탈 부인이라는 이름이 나오자 공작의 눈에 번뜩이는 아이러니의 기색을 알아채고는 조심스럽게 물었다.\n\n\"그 여자 남편을 알았고, 그 여자도 조금은 알았지, 경건주의자들 무리에 끼기 전에 말이다.\"\n\n\"경건주의자가 뭐예요, 아빠?\" 슈탈 부인에게서 자기가 그토록 높이 떠받들던 것에 이름이 붙어 있다는 것을 알고 당황한 키티가 물었다.\n\n\"나도 잘은 몰라. 다만 그 여자가 무슨 일이든, 어떤 불행에 대해서든 하느님께 감사를 드린다는 것, 게다가 남편이 죽은 것에도 하느님께 감사를 드린다는 것만은 안다. 그런데 그게 좀 우습단 말이지, 두 사람은 사이가 좋지 않았으니까.\"\n\n\"저 사람은 누구냐? 어쩜 저리 가련한 얼굴인지!\" 그가 갈색 외투에 흰 바지를 입고 벤치에 앉아 있는 중키의 병자를 알아보고 물었다. 그 바지는 길고 살 없는 다리께에서 이상한 주름을 지으며 흘러내려 있었다. 그 남자는 밀짚모자를 들어 올리며, 듬성듬성한 곱슬머리와 모자에 눌려 보기 안쓰럽게 붉어진 높은 이마를 드러냈다.\n\n\"화가 페트로프예요.\" 키티가 얼굴을 붉히며 대답했다. \"그리고 저쪽이 그의 아내고요.\" 그녀가 안나 파블로브나를 가리키며 덧붙였는데, 그 부인은 마치 일부러 그러기라도 하듯, 그들이 다가가는 바로 그 순간에 오솔길을 따라 달아난 아이를 쫓아가 버렸다.\n\n\"가엾은 사람! 게다가 얼마나 선한 얼굴인지!\" 공작이 말했다. \"어째서 가서 인사를 안 하느냐? 너에게 말을 걸고 싶어 했잖니.\"\n\n\"그럼, 가 봐요.\" 키티가 단호하게 돌아서며 말했다. \"오늘은 좀 어떠세요?\" 그녀가 페트로프에게 물었다.\n\n페트로프는 지팡이에 의지해 일어서서는 수줍게 공작을 바라보았다.\n\n\"제 딸입니다.\" 공작이 말했다. \"통성명이나 합시다.\"\n\n화가는 고개 숙여 인사하고 미소 지었는데, 묘하게 눈부신 흰 이가 드러났다.\n\n\"어제 오시기를 기다렸는데요, 공작 영애.\" 그가 키티에게 말했다. 이 말을 하면서 그는 비틀거렸고, 그러고는 마치 일부러 그런 것처럼 보이려고 같은 동작을 다시 해 보였다.\n\n\"오려고 했는데, 안나 파블로브나가 안 오신다고 전해 왔다고 바렌카가 그러더라고요.\"\n\n\"안 오다니!\" 페트로프가 얼굴을 붉히며 곧 기침을 하기 시작했고, 그의 눈은 아내를 찾았다. \"아니타! 아니타!\" 그가 큰 소리로 불렀고, 가늘고 흰 목에 부풀어 오른 핏줄이 밧줄처럼 돋아났다.\n\n안나 파블로브나가 다가왔다.\n\n\"공작 영애께 우리가 안 간다고 전했단 말이오!\" 그가 목소리를 잃은 채 화가 나서 그녀에게 속삭였다.\n\n\"안녕하세요, 공작 영애.\" 안나 파블로브나가 전과는 전혀 딴판인 꾸민 미소를 지으며 말했다. \"뵙게 되어 무척 기쁩니다.\" 그녀가 공작에게 말했다. \"오래전부터 기다렸답니다, 공작님.\"\n\n\"공작 영애께 우리가 안 간다고 도대체 왜 전했느냔 말이오?\" 화가가 다시 한번, 한층 더 화가 나서 쉰 목소리로 속삭였는데, 목소리가 말을 듣지 않아 자기가 바라는 만큼 말에 표정을 실을 수 없다는 것에 분명 약이 올라 있었다.\n\n\"어머, 세상에! 난 우리가 안 가는 줄 알았죠.\" 아내가 짜증스럽게 대꾸했다.\n\n\"아니, 언제 우리가…….\" 그는 기침을 하고는 손을 내저었다. 공작은 모자를 벗고 딸과 함께 자리를 떴다.\n\n\"아아! 아아!\" 그가 깊이 한숨을 쉬었다. \"오, 가엾은 사람들 같으니!\"\n\n\"네, 아빠.\" 키티가 대답했다. \"그리고 저분들은 아이가 셋이나 있는데, 하인도 없고, 가진 것도 거의 없다는 걸 아셔야 해요. 그분은 아카데미에서 얼마쯤 받는대요.\" 그녀는 안나 파블로브나의 태도가 묘하게 바뀐 것이 자기 마음에 불러일으킨 괴로움을 떨쳐 내려 애쓰며 활기차게 말을 이었다.\n\n\"오, 저기 슈탈 부인이 계세요.\" 키티가 환자용 마차를 가리키며 말했다. 그 안에는 베개에 기댄 채, 회색과 푸른색의 무언가가 양산 아래 누워 있었다. 그것이 슈탈 부인이었다. 그녀 뒤에는 마차를 미는, 침울하고 건강해 보이는 독일인 일꾼이 서 있었다. 바로 곁에는 키티가 이름은 알고 있던 아마빛 머리의 스웨덴 백작이 서 있었다. 몇몇 병자들이 그 낮은 마차 가까이서 서성이며, 마치 무슨 구경거리라도 되는 양 그 부인을 빤히 바라보고 있었다.\n\n공작이 그녀에게 다가갔고, 키티는 그의 눈에 떠오른 그 사람을 당황케 하는 아이러니의 기색을 알아챘다. 그는 슈탈 부인에게 다가가, 요즈음에는 그토록 구사하는 이가 드문 그 훌륭한 프랑스어로 더없는 정중함과 상냥함으로 그녀에게 말을 건넸다.\n\n\"저를 기억하실지 모르겠습니다만, 제 딸에게 베풀어 주신 친절에 감사를 드리고자 제 소개를 다시 해야겠군요.\" 그가 모자를 벗고는 다시 쓰지 않은 채 말했다.\n\n\"알렉산드르 셰르바츠키 공작이시군요.\" 슈탈 부인이 그를 향해 천상의 눈을 들어 올리며 말했는데, 키티는 그 눈에서 짜증의 기색을 알아보았다. \"반갑습니다! 따님이 무척 마음에 들었답니다.\"\n\n\"여전히 몸이 편찮으십니까?\"\n\n\"네, 이젠 익숙해졌답니다.\" 슈탈 부인은 이렇게 말하고는 공작을 스웨덴 백작에게 소개했다.\n\n\"조금도 변하지 않으셨군요.\" 공작이 그녀에게 말했다. \"당신을 뵙는 영광을 누린 지 십 년인가 십일 년인가 되었습니다만.\"\n\n\"네, 하느님께서는 십자가를 내리시고 또 그것을 짊어질 힘도 내려 주시지요. 이따금 이 삶의 목적이 무엇인가 하는 의문이 들기도 한답니다……. 반대쪽이요!\" 그녀는 발치의 무릎덮개를 마음에 차지 않게 고쳐 놓은 바렌카에게 신경질적으로 말했다.\n\n\"아마도 선을 행하는 것이겠지요.\" 공작이 눈을 반짝이며 말했다.\n\n\"그건 우리가 판단할 일이 아니지요.\" 슈탈 부인이 공작의 얼굴에 떠오른 미묘한 표정을 알아채고 말했다. \"그럼 그 책을 보내 주시겠지요, 친애하는 백작님? 무척 감사드립니다.\" 그녀가 젊은 스웨덴 사람에게 말했다.\n\n\"아!\" 공작이 가까이 서 있는 모스크바의 대령을 알아보고 소리쳤고, 슈탈 부인에게 인사를 한 뒤 딸과, 그들에게 합류한 모스크바의 대령과 함께 자리를 떴다.\n\n\"저런 것이 우리네 귀족이라지요, 공작님!\" 모스크바의 대령이 빈정대는 속내를 담아 말했다. 그는 슈탈 부인이 자기와 통성명을 해 주지 않은 것에 앙심을 품고 있었다.\n\n\"여전하군요.\" 공작이 대꾸했다.\n\n\"병이 나기 전에, 그러니까 자리보전을 하기 전에 저 부인을 아셨습니까, 공작님?\"\n\n\"네. 저 여자가 자리보전을 하는 걸 내 눈으로 봤지요.\" 공작이 말했다.\n\n\"저 부인이 두 발로 선 지가 십 년이 되었다더군요.\"\n\n\"저 여자가 일어서지 않는 건 다리가 너무 짧아서요. 몸매가 아주 형편없거든.\"\n\n\"아빠, 그럴 리가 없어요!\" 키티가 소리쳤다.\n\n\"심술궂은 입들이 그렇게들 말한단다, 얘야. 그리고 네 바렌카까지 덩달아 욕을 먹지.\" 그가 덧붙였다. \"오, 이놈의 병약한 부인네들이란!\"\n\n\"오, 아니에요, 아빠!\" 키티가 발끈하며 반박했다. \"바렌카는 그분을 우러러 받든다고요. 게다가 그분은 그렇게나 많은 선을 베푸시는걸요! 아무한테나 물어보세요! 다들 그분을, 알린 슈탈을 안다고요.\"\n\n\"그럴지도 모르지.\" 공작이 팔꿈치로 그녀의 손을 꼭 누르며 말했다. \"하지만 선이라는 건 아무한테나 물어도 아무도 모르게끔 행할 때가 더 나은 법이란다.\"\n\n키티는 대답하지 않았는데, 할 말이 없어서가 아니라 자기 속마음을 아버지에게조차 드러내고 싶지 않았기 때문이었다. 그러나 이상하게도, 아버지의 견해에 휘둘리지 않겠다고, 자기 마음 가장 깊은 성소에 아버지를 들이지 않겠다고 그토록 단단히 마음먹었건만, 그녀는 한 달 내내 가슴속에 품어 왔던 슈탈 부인의 천상의 이미지가, 아무렇게나 벗어 던진 옷가지로 이루어진 환상 속의 형체가 그저 옷자락이 거기 놓여 있을 뿐임을 알아채는 순간 사라져 버리듯, 영영 돌아오지 않을 길로 사라져 버렸음을 느꼈다. 남은 것이라곤 다리가 짧은 한 여자, 몸매가 형편없어서 자리에 누웠고, 무릎덮개를 자기 마음에 들게 놓아 주지 않았다고 참을성 많은 바렌카를 들볶는 여자뿐이었다. 그리고 키티는 아무리 상상력을 짜내어도 예전의 슈탈 부인을 되살려 낼 수가 없었다.\n\n# Chapter 35\n\n공작은 자신의 유쾌한 기분을 식구들과 친구들에게, 심지어 셰르바츠키 일가가 머무는 방의 주인인 독일인 집주인에게까지 옮겨 주었다.\n\n키티와 함께 온천에서 돌아온 공작은 대령과 마리야 예브게니예브나, 그리고 바렌카를 모두 불러 함께 커피를 들자고 청해 놓고는, 정원의 밤나무 아래로 탁자와 의자를 내가고 거기에 점심을 차리라고 지시했다. 집주인과 하인들 역시 그의 유쾌한 기분에 영향을 받아 한결 활기를 띠었다. 그들은 그의 씀씀이가 후하다는 것을 알고 있었다. 그리고 반 시간 뒤, 맨 위층에 사는 함부르크 출신의 병약한 의사는 밤나무 아래 모여든 건강한 러시아인들의 흥겨운 무리를 창밖으로 부럽게 내다보았다. 나뭇잎이 드리우는, 떨리는 그늘의 동그라미 속에서, 하얀 천을 덮고 커피포트며 버터 바른 빵이며 치즈며 차가운 들짐승 고기를 차려 놓은 탁자에 라일락빛 리본을 단 높다란 모자를 쓴 공작 부인이 앉아 잔과 버터 바른 빵을 나누어 주고 있었다. 맞은편 끝에는 공작이 앉아 맛있게 먹으며 큰 소리로 유쾌하게 떠들었다. 공작은 자기 곁에 사들인 물건들을 펼쳐 놓았는데, 조각이 새겨진 상자들이며 자질구레한 장식품들, 온갖 종류의 편지 칼들이었다. 그는 온천지에 갈 때마다 그것들을 한 무더기씩 사들여서는 모두에게, 하녀인 리셴에게도 집주인에게도 나누어 주었으며, 우스꽝스러울 만큼 서툰 독일어로 집주인에게 농담을 건네며, 키티를 낫게 한 것은 물이 아니라 그의 훌륭한 요리, 특히 자두 수프라고 단언했다. 공작 부인은 남편의 러시아식 행동을 보며 웃었지만, 온천에 머무는 내내 보였던 것보다 훨씬 활기차고 기분이 좋았다. 대령은 늘 그렇듯 공작의 농담에 미소를 지었으나, 자기가 면밀히 연구하고 있다고 자부하는 유럽에 관해서만큼은 공작 부인 편을 들었다. 순박한 마리야 예브게니예브나는 공작이 내뱉는 우스꽝스러운 말마다 그저 폭소를 터뜨렸고, 그의 농담은 바렌카를 가냘프지만 사람을 전염시키는 웃음으로 어쩔 줄 모르게 만들었는데, 그것은 키티가 한 번도 본 적 없는 모습이었다.\n\n키티는 이 모든 것이 기뻤지만 마음이 가벼워지지는 않았다. 그녀는 아버지가 자신의 친구들과 그토록 자신을 끌어당겼던 삶을 유쾌하게 바라봄으로써 무심코 자기에게 던져 놓은 문제를 풀 수가 없었다. 이 의혹에 더해, 그날 아침 그토록 두드러지고 불쾌하게 드러났던 페트로프 일가와의 관계 변화가 겹쳤다. 모두가 유쾌했지만 키티는 유쾌해질 수 없었고, 이것이 그녀의 괴로움을 더욱 키웠다. 그녀는 어린 시절, 벌을 받느라 방에 갇혀 밖에서 들려오는 언니들의 흥겨운 웃음소리를 들을 때 느꼈던 것과 같은 감정을 느꼈다.\n\n\"그런데 이 많은 물건들은 다 뭐 하러 샀어요?\" 공작 부인이 미소 지으며 남편에게 커피를 한 잔 건네며 말했다.\n\n“산책을 나가서 가게를 들여다보면 사라고 하지요. ‘엘라우흐트, 두르흘라우흐트?’ 그들이 ‘두르흘라우흐트’라고 하면 나는 버틸 수가 없어요. 10탈러를 잃고 말지요.”\n\n\"그건 그냥 심심해서 그런 거예요.\" 공작 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"물론 그렇지. 어찌나 심심한지, 여보, 도무지 제 몸을 어쩔 줄 모를 지경이니.\"\n\n\"어떻게 심심하실 수가 있어요, 공작님? 요즘 독일엔 흥미로운 게 정말 많은데요.\" 마리야 예브게니예브나가 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 난 흥미로운 건 죄다 알고 있소. 자두 수프도 알고, 완두콩 소시지도 알지. 다 안다고.\"\n\n\"아니에요, 뭐라고 하셔도, 공작님, 이 나라의 제도들에는 흥미로운 점이 있습니다.\" 대령이 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 그게 뭐가 흥미롭다는 거요? 다들 한 푼짜리 동전처럼 흡족해하고 있잖소. 모두를 정복해 놓았으니 말이야, 한데 내가 왜 그걸 기뻐해야 하느냔 말이오? 난 아무도 정복하지 않았소. 게다가 내 손으로 직접 장화를 벗어야 하고, 그래, 그걸 또 직접 치워야 하지. 아침엔 일어나자마자 곧장 옷을 입고 식당으로 나가서 맛없는 차를 마셔야 하고! 집에서는 얼마나 다른가! 서두를 것 없이 일어나서, 좀 짜증도 내고, 투덜대다가, 다시 기분이 풀리지. 무엇이든 곰곰이 생각할 시간도 있고, 서두를 것도 없고 말이야.\"\n\n\"하지만 시간은 돈입니다, 그걸 잊으셨군요.\" 대령이 말했다.\n\n\"시간이라, 흥, 그건 경우에 따라 다르지! 어떤 시간은 한 달치를 육 펜스에 내줘도 좋을 때가 있고, 또 어떤 시간은 반 시간조차 어떤 돈을 줘도 못 내줄 때가 있는 법이오. 안 그러냐, 카친카? 왜 그래? 왜 그렇게 풀이 죽어 있는 거냐?\"\n\n\"풀이 죽은 거 아니에요.\"\n\n\"어딜 가려고? 좀 더 있다 가지.\" 그가 바렌카에게 말했다.\n\n\"이제 집에 가 봐야 해요.\" 바렌카가 일어서며 말하고는 또다시 까르르 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 웃음을 가라앉힌 그녀는 작별 인사를 하고 모자를 가지러 집 안으로 들어갔다.\n\n키티가 그녀를 따라갔다. 바렌카마저 달리 보였다. 더 나빠진 것은 아니었지만, 전에 그녀가 그려 보았던 것과는 달랐다.\n\n\"어머나! 이렇게 실컷 웃어 본 게 정말 오랜만이에요!\" 바렌카가 양산과 가방을 챙기며 말했다. \"정말 좋은 분이시네요, 당신 아버님은!\"\n\n키티는 말이 없었다.\n\n\"우리 언제 또 만나죠?\" 바렌카가 물었다.\n\n\"엄마가 페트로프 댁에 가 보려고 하셨어요. 거기 안 오세요?\" 키티가 바렌카의 마음을 떠보려고 말했다.\n\n\"가요.\" 바렌카가 대답했다. \"그 댁이 떠날 채비를 하고 있어서, 짐 싸는 걸 돕겠다고 약속했거든요.\"\n\n\"그럼 저도 갈게요.\"\n\n\"아니, 왜요?\"\n\n\"왜 안 돼요? 왜요? 왜요?\" 키티가 두 눈을 동그랗게 뜨고, 바렌카를 놓치지 않으려는 듯 그녀의 양산을 움켜쥐며 말했다. \"아니, 잠깐만요. 왜 안 되죠?\"\n\n\"아, 아무것도 아니에요. 당신 아버님도 오셨고, 게다가 당신이 거들면 그분들이 거북해하실 거예요.\"\n\n\"아니, 왜 제가 페트로프 댁에 자주 가는 걸 원치 않는지 말해 줘요. 당신은 제가 그러는 걸 원치 않잖아요. 왜요?\"\n\n\"그런 말 한 적 없어요.\" 바렌카가 조용히 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 제발 말해 줘요!\"\n\n\"전부 다 말하라고요?\" 바렌카가 물었다.\n\n\"전부 다요, 전부!\" 키티가 동의했다.\n\n\"음, 사실 대수로운 건 전혀 없어요. 그저 미하일 알렉세예비치가\" (화가의 이름이었다) \"원래는 더 일찍 떠나려고 했는데, 이제는 떠나고 싶어 하지 않는다는 것뿐이에요.\" 바렌카가 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"그래서요, 그래서요!\" 키티가 바렌카를 어둡게 쳐다보며 안달이 나서 재촉했다.\n\n\"음, 그런데 무슨 이유에서인지 안나 파블로브나가 그분에게, 당신이 여기 있어서 떠나고 싶어 하지 않는 거라고 말했어요. 물론 터무니없는 소리였죠. 하지만 그 일로 다툼이 있었어요, 당신을 두고 말이에요. 그런 환자들이 얼마나 신경이 예민한지 당신도 알잖아요.\"\n\n키티는 더욱 얼굴을 찌푸린 채 잠자코 있었고, 바렌카는 혼자서 계속 말을 이어 가며, 그녀를 누그러뜨리거나 달래려 애썼다. 폭풍이 다가오는 것이 느껴졌으나, 그것이 눈물의 폭풍일지 말의 폭풍일지는 알 수 없었다.\n\n\"그러니 가지 않는 편이 좋겠어요…… 이해하시죠? 기분 상하지 않으시겠죠?……\"\n\n\"그래요, 다 제가 잘못한 거예요! 다 제 탓이라고요!\" 키티가 바렌카의 손에서 양산을 낚아채며, 친구의 얼굴 너머를 바라보며 빠르게 외쳤다.\n\n바렌카는 그녀의 어린아이 같은 분노를 보며 미소가 나오려 했지만, 그녀에게 상처를 줄까 봐 두려웠다.\n\n\"어째서 당신 잘못이라는 거예요? 이해가 안 돼요.\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"제 탓이에요, 모든 게 가짜였으니까요. 모든 게 일부러 한 거였지, 진심에서 우러난 게 아니었으니까요. 제가 무슨 자격으로 남의 일에 끼어들었을까요? 그러다 결국 제가 다툼의 원인이 되고, 아무도 부탁하지 않은 일을 저질러 버렸어요. 죄다 가짜였기 때문이에요! 가짜! 가짜!……\"\n\n\"가짜라니, 무슨 목적으로요?\" 바렌카가 부드럽게 말했다.\n\n\"아, 너무 어리석어요! 너무 혐오스러워요! 제가 나설 필요는 조금도 없었는데…… 죄다 가짜뿐이었어요!\" 그녀가 양산을 폈다 접었다 하며 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 무슨 목적으로요?\"\n\n\"남들 눈에, 제 눈에, 하느님 눈에 더 나아 보이려고요. 모두를 속이려고요. 아니에요! 이제 그런 짓까지 하진 않겠어요. 차라리 나쁜 사람이 되겠어요. 어쨌든 거짓말쟁이, 사기꾼은 안 될 거예요.\"\n\n\"그런데 누가 사기꾼이라는 거예요?\" 바렌카가 나무라듯 말했다. \"마치……\"\n\n하지만 키티는 격분에 휩싸여 그녀가 말을 맺도록 내버려 두지 않았다.\n\n\"당신 얘기가 아니에요, 당신 얘기는 전혀 아니에요. 당신은 완벽한 사람이죠. 그래요, 그래, 당신이 더없이 완벽하다는 걸 알아요. 하지만 제가 나쁜 사람이라면 어쩌란 말이에요? 제가 나쁘지 않았다면 이런 일은 절대 없었을 거예요. 그러니 제가 생긴 그대로 살게 내버려 둬요. 저는 가짜로 살지 않을래요. 안나 파블로브나가 저랑 무슨 상관이에요? 그 사람들은 그들 길을 가고, 저는 제 길을 가게 해요. 저는 달라질 수가 없어요…… 그런데 그게 아니에요, 그게 아니라고요.\"\n\n\"그게 아니라니 뭐가요?\" 바렌카가 어리둥절해하며 물었다.\n\n\"전부 다요. 저는 진심에서 우러나는 게 아니면 행동할 수가 없는데, 당신은 원칙에 따라 행동하잖아요. 저는 그냥 당신이 좋아서 그랬는데, 당신은 아마 그저 저를 구원하고, 저를 더 낫게 만들고 싶었을 뿐이겠죠.\"\n\n\"그건 부당해요.\" 바렌카가 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 저는 다른 사람들 얘기를 하는 게 아니에요, 제 자신 얘기를 하는 거예요.\"\n\n\"키티야,\" 어머니의 목소리가 들렸다. \"이리 와서 아빠한테 네 목걸이 좀 보여 드리렴.\"\n\n키티는 도도한 태도로, 친구와 화해도 하지 않은 채 탁자에서 작은 상자에 든 목걸이를 집어 들고 어머니에게로 갔다.\n\n\"무슨 일이냐? 왜 그렇게 얼굴이 빨갛지?\" 어머니와 아버지가 한목소리로 그녀에게 물었다.\n\n\"아무것도 아니에요.\" 그녀가 대답했다. \"금방 돌아올게요.\" 그러고는 다시 뛰어갔다.\n\n'아직 거기 있어.' 그녀는 생각했다. '뭐라고 말해야 하지? 아, 어쩌면 좋아! 내가 무슨 짓을 한 거지, 무슨 말을 한 거야? 왜 그녀에게 무례하게 굴었을까? 어떻게 하지? 그녀에게 뭐라고 말해야 하지?' 키티는 그렇게 생각하며 문간에 멈춰 섰다.\n\n모자를 쓰고 손에 양산을 든 바렌카는 탁자에 앉아 키티가 부러뜨린 양산 살을 살펴보고 있었다. 그녀가 고개를 들었다.\n\n\"바렌카, 용서해 줘요, 제발 용서해 줘요.\" 키티가 그녀에게 다가가며 속삭였다. \"제가 무슨 말을 했는지 기억도 안 나요. 저는……\"\n\n\"저도 정말 당신을 아프게 할 생각은 없었어요.\" 바렌카가 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n화해가 이루어졌다. 그러나 아버지가 오면서 키티가 살아가던 세계 전체가 그녀에게는 완전히 달라져 버렸다. 그녀는 자기가 배운 것을 모두 버리지는 않았지만, 자기가 되고 싶은 사람이 될 수 있으리라 여겼던 것이 스스로를 속인 것이었음을 깨닫게 되었다. 그녀의 눈이, 말하자면, 떠진 셈이었다. 그녀는 자기가 오르고자 했던 그 정상에서 위선과 자만 없이 스스로를 지탱한다는 것이 얼마나 어려운 일인지를 절실히 느꼈다. 게다가 그녀는 자기가 살아온, 슬픔과 병자와 죽어 가는 사람들로 이루어진 세계의 온갖 적막함을 깨닫게 되었다. 그것을 좋아하려고 기울였던 노력들이 견딜 수 없게 여겨졌고, 그녀는 어서 빨리 신선한 공기 속으로, 러시아로, 편지를 통해 알고 있듯이 언니 돌리가 이미 아이들과 함께 가 있는 예르구쇼보로 돌아가고 싶은 갈망을 느꼈다.\n\n그러나 바렌카에 대한 그녀의 애정은 식지 않았다. 작별 인사를 하면서 키티는 바렌카에게 러시아에 있는 자기들에게로 와 달라고 청했다.\n\n\"당신이 결혼하면 갈게요.\" 바렌카가 말했다.\n\n\"저는 절대 결혼 안 할 거예요.\"\n\n\"그럼, 저도 절대 안 가요.\"\n\n\"그럼, 저는 그것만을 위해서라도 결혼할래요. 자, 이제 약속 잊지 말아요.\" 키티가 말했다.\n\n의사의 예측은 들어맞았다. 키티는 병이 나아 러시아의 집으로 돌아왔다. 그녀는 예전처럼 명랑하고 철없지는 않았지만, 평온했다. 모스크바에서의 괴로움은 그녀에게 하나의 추억이 되어 있었다.\n\n# PART THREE Chapter 1\n\n세르게이 이바노비치 코즈니셰프는 정신노동에서 벗어나 쉬고 싶었고, 평소처럼 외국으로 떠나는 대신 5월 말경에 동생이 사는 시골로 와서 머물게 되었다. 그의 판단으로는 시골 생활이야말로 가장 훌륭한 삶의 방식이었다. 이제 그는 동생의 집에서 그런 삶을 누리려고 온 것이었다. 콘스탄틴 레빈은 형이 와서 무척 기뻤는데, 특히 그해 여름에는 형 니콜라이가 오지 않으리라고 예상했기 때문이다. 그러나 세르게이 이바노비치에 대한 애정과 존경에도 불구하고, 콘스탄틴 레빈은 시골에서 형과 함께 지내는 것이 불편했다. 시골을 대하는 형의 태도를 보고 있으면 마음이 편치 않았고, 솔직히 짜증이 났다. 콘스탄틴 레빈에게 시골은 삶의 바탕, 그러니까 즐거움과 노력과 노동의 바탕이었다. 세르게이 이바노비치에게 시골은 한편으로는 일에서 벗어난 휴식이었고, 다른 한편으로는 도시의 타락한 영향을 막아 주는 귀중한 해독제였으며, 그는 그것을 만족감과 유익함을 의식하며 받아들였다. 콘스탄틴 레빈에게 시골이 좋은 첫째 이유는 그것이 그 유익함을 의심할 여지 없는 노동의 터전을 마련해 주기 때문이었다. 세르게이 이바노비치에게 시골이 특히 좋은 이유는 그곳에서는 아무것도 하지 않는 것이 가능하고 또 어울리기 때문이었다. 게다가 농민을 대하는 세르게이 이바노비치의 태도는 콘스탄틴의 마음을 적잖이 거슬리게 했다. 세르게이 이바노비치는 자기가 농민을 잘 알고 또 좋아한다고 말하곤 했고, 자주 농민들과 이야기를 나누었는데, 그것을 꾸밈이나 거들먹거림 없이 해내는 재주가 있었다. 그리고 그런 대화를 나눌 때마다 그는 농민에게 유리한, 또한 자기가 그들을 잘 안다는 사실을 입증해 주는 일반적인 결론을 끌어내곤 했다. 콘스탄틴 레빈은 농민을 대하는 그런 태도가 마음에 들지 않았다. 콘스탄틴에게 농민이란 그저 함께하는 노동의 가장 중요한 동반자였고, 농민에 대해 거의 혈연에 가까운 온갖 존경과 사랑을 품고 있었음에도—아마도 그 자신이 말하듯, 유모인 농민 여인의 젖과 함께 빨아들인 것이리라—그래도 자기와 함께 일하는 동료로서, 때로는 그들의 활력과 온순함과 정의로움에 감탄하면서도, 함께하는 노동이 다른 자질을 요구할 때면 그들의 부주의함과 두서없음과 술버릇과 거짓말에 매우 자주 화가 치밀었다. 만약 누군가 농민을 좋아하느냐 싫어하느냐고 그에게 물었다면, 콘스탄틴 레빈은 무어라 대답해야 할지 도무지 갈피를 잡지 못했을 것이다. 그는 농민을 좋아하기도 하고 싫어하기도 했으니, 그것은 마치 사람을 일반적으로 좋아하기도 하고 싫어하기도 하는 것과 같았다. 물론 마음씨 고운 사람인 그는 사람을 싫어하기보다는 좋아하는 편이었고, 농민에 대해서도 마찬가지였다. 그러나 \"민중\"을 무언가 따로 떨어진 것으로 여겨 좋아하거나 싫어하는 것은 그로서는 할 수 없는 일이었으니, 그것은 단지 그가 \"민중\"과 더불어 살았고 그의 모든 관심사가 그들의 것과 얽혀 있었기 때문만이 아니라, 그 자신이 \"민중\"의 일부라고 여겨 자기와 \"민중\"을 구별 짓는 어떤 특별한 자질이나 결점도 보지 못했고, 자기를 그들과 견줄 수도 없었기 때문이다. 더구나 그는 농부로서, 또 중재인으로서, 더 나아가 조언자로서(농민들은 그를 신뢰했고, 삼십 베르스타 사방에서 그에게 조언을 구하러 오곤 했다) 그토록 오랫동안 농민과 더없이 긴밀한 관계를 맺으며 살아왔음에도, \"민중\"에 대한 명확한 견해가 없었으며, 자기가 \"민중\"을 아느냐는 물음에도 그들을 좋아하느냐는 물음 못지않게 답하기 곤란했을 것이다. 그에게 농민을 안다고 말하는 것은 사람을 안다고 말하는 것과 매한가지였을 것이다. 그는 온갖 부류의 사람들을, 그 가운데 농민들을 끊임없이 관찰하며 알아 갔는데, 그들을 선량하고 흥미로운 사람들로 여겼으며, 그들에게서 끊임없이 새로운 점을 발견하고 그들에 대한 이전의 견해를 바꾸어 가며 새로운 견해를 세웠다. 세르게이 이바노비치는 그와 정반대였다. 그가 싫어하는 삶에 견주어 시골 생활을 좋아하고 칭송했듯이, 그는 자기가 싫어하는 계층의 사람들과 대비하여 농민을 좋아했고, 또 그렇게 농민을 사람 일반과 구별되고 대립되는 무언가로서 알고 있었다. 그의 체계적인 머릿속에는 농민 생활의 어떤 측면들이 명확히 정식화되어 있었는데, 그것은 일부는 그 생활 자체에서, 그러나 주로는 다른 생활 양식과의 대비에서 끌어낸 것이었다. 그는 농민에 대한 자기 견해도, 그들을 향한 공감적인 태도도 결코 바꾸지 않았다.\n\n농민에 대한 견해를 두고 형제 사이에 벌어지는 논쟁에서 세르게이 이바노비치는 늘 동생을 이겼는데, 그것은 바로 세르게이 이바노비치가 농민에 대해—그 성격이며 자질이며 취향에 대해 명확한 생각을 가지고 있었기 때문이다. 콘스탄틴 레빈은 그 문제에 대해 명확하고 변치 않는 생각이 없었으므로, 논쟁에서 콘스탄틴은 쉽사리 자기모순을 범한 자로 몰리고 말았다.\n\n세르게이 이바노비치가 보기에 동생은 마음씨가 올바른(그가 프랑스어로 표현하듯) 훌륭한 녀석이었지만, 그 머리는 제법 빠르긴 해도 순간의 인상에 너무 휘둘려, 그 결과 모순으로 가득 차 있었다. 형으로서의 온갖 너그러움을 품고 그는 때때로 동생에게 사물의 참된 의미를 설명해 주곤 했지만, 동생과 논쟁하는 데서 얻는 만족은 별로 없었으니, 그를 너무 쉽게 이겨 버렸기 때문이다.\n\n콘스탄틴 레빈은 형을 어마어마한 지성과 교양을 갖춘 사람으로, 그 말의 가장 높은 의미에서 관대한 사람으로, 그리고 공익을 위해 일하는 특별한 재능을 지닌 사람으로 여겼다. 그러나 마음 깊은 곳에서는, 나이가 들수록, 또 형을 더 속속들이 알아 갈수록, 자기에게는 전혀 없다고 느끼는 그 공익을 위해 일하는 재능이라는 것이 어쩌면 하나의 자질이라기보다는 무언가의 결핍이 아닐까 하는 생각이 점점 더 자주 들었다—선하고 정직하고 고결한 욕망이나 취향의 결핍이 아니라, 생명력의 결핍, 이른바 가슴이라 불리는 것의 결핍, 사람으로 하여금 삶의 헤아릴 수 없는 길들 가운데 하나를 골라 오직 그 하나만을 마음에 두게 만드는 그 충동의 결핍 말이다. 형을 알면 알수록 그는 세르게이 이바노비치를, 그리고 공공의 복리를 위해 일하는 다른 많은 사람들을 더욱 눈여겨보게 되었는데, 그들은 가슴의 충동에 이끌려 공익을 마음에 두는 것이 아니라, 공공의 일에 관심을 갖는 것이 옳다고 지적인 고려에서 추론하여 그 결과 그것에 관심을 갖는 것이었다. 레빈은 형이 공공의 복리에 관한 문제나 영혼불멸의 문제를 체스 문제나 새로운 기계의 정교한 설계만큼도 더 마음에 두지 않는다는 것을 관찰함으로써 이런 일반화에 더욱 확신을 갖게 되었다.\n\n이 밖에도 콘스탄틴 레빈은 형과 함께 있는 것이 마음 편치 않았는데, 여름에 시골에서 레빈은 끊임없이 땅에서 하는 일로 바빴고 긴 여름날도 해야 할 일을 다 끝내기에는 모자랐던 반면, 세르게이 이바노비치는 휴가를 보내고 있었기 때문이다. 그러나 지금 휴가를 보내고 있다고는 해도, 즉 글을 쓰지 않고 있다고는 해도, 그는 지적인 활동에 너무도 익숙해져 있어서 머릿속에 떠오른 생각들을 간결하고 유려한 형태로 다듬는 것을 좋아했고, 그것을 들어 줄 누군가가 있는 것을 좋아했다. 그의 가장 흔하고 자연스러운 청자는 동생이었다. 그래서 두 사람의 관계가 다정하고 거리낌이 없었음에도, 콘스탄틴은 형을 혼자 내버려 두는 것에 어색함을 느꼈다. 세르게이 이바노비치는 햇볕 아래 풀밭에 몸을 쭉 뻗고 누워, 그렇게 누워 볕을 쬐며 느긋하게 이야기 나누기를 좋아했다.\n\n\"넌 믿지 못할 거다.\" 그는 동생에게 말하곤 했다. \"이 시골의 게으름이 나한테 얼마나 큰 즐거움인지 말이야. 머릿속에 생각 하나 없이, 텅 빈 북처럼 비어 있다니까!\"\n\n그러나 콘스탄틴 레빈은 앉아서 형의 이야기를 듣고 있는 것이 따분했는데, 특히 자기가 자리를 비운 사이에 미처 갈아 두지 않은 밭에 거름을 실어 날라 아무렇게나 무더기로 쌓아 둔다는 것을, 그리고 쟁기에 보습을 단단히 죄어 두지 않아 빠져 버리게 해 놓고는 새 쟁기란 멍청한 발명품이고 옛날 안드레예브나 쟁기만 한 것이 없다는 둥 그런 소리를 한다는 것을 알 때면 더욱 그러했다.\n\n\"이봐, 이 더위에 그만큼 쏘다녔으면 됐다.\" 세르게이 이바노비치가 그에게 말하곤 했다.\n\n\"아뇨, 잠깐 사무소에 좀 다녀와야 해서요.\" 레빈은 이렇게 대답하고는 밭으로 달려가곤 했다.\n\n# Chapter 2\n\n6월 초에 이런 일이 있었다. 늙은 유모이자 가정부인 아가피야 미하일로브나가 방금 절인 버섯 항아리를 지하실로 나르다가 미끄러져 넘어지면서 손목을 삐었다. 학업을 막 마친, 말 많은 젊은 의대생인 지역 의사가 그녀를 보러 왔다. 그는 손목을 살펴보고는 부러진 데는 없다고 말했고, 저명한 세르게이 이바노비치 코즈니셰프와 이야기할 기회가 생긴 것을 기뻐하며, 자신의 진보적인 견해를 과시하려고 지역의 온갖 추문을 그에게 늘어놓았으며, 지역 의회가 형편없는 지경에 빠졌다고 불평했다. 세르게이 이바노비치는 귀 기울여 듣고 질문을 던졌으며, 새로운 청자에게 자극을 받아 막힘없이 이야기를 펼쳐 몇 마디 예리하고 묵직한 견해를 내놓았는데, 젊은 의사는 그것을 정중하게 경탄했다. 그러더니 그는 동생이 너무도 잘 아는, 빛나고 열띤 대화 뒤에 늘 그에게 찾아오는 그 들뜬 기분 속으로 곧 빠져들었다. 의사가 떠난 뒤 그는 낚싯대를 들고 강으로 가고 싶어 했다. 세르게이 이바노비치는 낚시를 좋아했고, 그토록 어리석은 소일거리에 마음을 쏟을 수 있다는 것을 도리어 자랑스러워하는 듯했다.\n\n농경지와 풀밭에 그가 있어야 했던 콘스탄틴 레빈은 형을 데리러 마차를 몰고 왔다.\n\n올해 작황이 확실해지고, 내년 파종을 생각하기 시작하며, 풀베기가 코앞에 다가온 때, 여름의 전환점이었다. 호밀은 이삭이 다 패었으나 그 이삭은 아직 가벼워 여물지 않았고, 잿빛 도는 초록빛 물결을 이루며 바람에 일렁였다. 푸른 귀리는 군데군데 누런 풀잎 다발이 섞인 채 늦게 뿌린 밭 위로 들쭉날쭉 늘어졌고, 일찍 심은 메밀은 벌써 꽃이 피어 땅을 가렸다. 가축들이 돌처럼 단단히 밟아 놓은 휴경지는 절반쯤 갈렸는데, 쟁기가 닿지 않은 길이 그대로 남아 있었다. 밭으로 실어 온 마른 거름 더미에서는 해 질 녘이면 풀거름과 조팝나무 냄새가 뒤섞여 풍겨 왔고, 낮은 지대의 강가 풀밭은 풀베기를 기다리는 짙은 풀의 바다를 이루었으며, 그 사이로 거뭇해진 수영 줄기 더미들이 흩어져 있었다.\n\n해마다 되풀이되며 해마다 농부들의 온 신경을 곤두서게 하는 수확의 노동이 시작되기 직전, 밭일에 잠깐의 쉼이 찾아오는 때였다. 작황은 훌륭했고, 짧은 이슬 밤과 함께 밝고 무더운 여름날이 이어지고 있었다.\n\n형제는 풀밭에 닿으려면 숲을 가로질러 마차를 몰아야 했다. 세르게이 이바노비치는 내내 잎이 무성하게 뒤엉킨 숲의 아름다움에 감탄하며, 동생에게 그늘진 쪽은 거뭇하고 누런 턱잎이 환하게 점점이 박힌, 막 꽃을 피우려는 늙은 보리수를 가리켰다가, 다시 에메랄드빛으로 눈부신 올해 묘목의 새순을 가리켰다. 콘스탄틴 레빈은 자연의 아름다움에 대해 이야기하고 듣는 것을 좋아하지 않았다. 그에게는 말이 자기가 본 것의 아름다움을 앗아 갔다. 그는 형의 말에 맞장구를 쳤지만, 자기도 모르게 다른 일들을 생각하기 시작했다. 숲에서 빠져나오자 그의 모든 주의는 고지대의 휴경지 풍경에 쏠렸는데, 어떤 곳은 풀이 누렇고, 어떤 곳은 밟혀서 이랑으로 격자무늬를 이루고, 어떤 곳은 거름 더미가 점점이 박혀 있고, 또 어떤 곳은 갈리기까지 했다. 짐수레들이 줄지어 그 위를 가로질러 움직이고 있었다. 레빈은 수레를 세어 보고는 필요한 만큼 다 실어 왔다는 것에 흐뭇해했고, 풀밭을 바라보자 그의 생각은 풀베기로 옮겨 갔다. 그는 건초 만드는 일에서 늘 무언가 그를 가슴 깊이 움직이는 특별한 것을 느꼈다. 풀밭에 이르자 레빈은 말을 멈춰 세웠다.\n\n아침 이슬이 아직 빽빽한 풀밭 아랫자락에 내려앉아 있어서, 발이 젖지 않도록 세르게이 이바노비치는 잉어를 낚던 버드나무까지 마차로 데려다 달라고 동생에게 청했다. 콘스탄틴 레빈은 풀베기할 풀을 짓밟는 것이 안타까웠지만 형을 풀밭 안으로 몰고 들어갔다. 키 큰 풀은 바퀴와 말 다리를 부드럽게 감돌며 젖은 차축과 바큇살에 씨앗을 들러붙게 했다. 형은 덤불 아래 자리를 잡고 앉아 낚시 도구를 챙겼고, 그동안 레빈은 말을 끌고 가 매어 놓은 뒤 바람 한 점 일지 않는 잿빛 도는 초록빛 풀의 드넓은 바다로 걸어 들어갔다. 여문 씨를 단 비단결 같은 풀은 가장 축축한 자리에서는 거의 그의 허리까지 닿았다.\n\n풀밭을 가로지른 콘스탄틴 레빈은 길로 나섰고, 눈이 부어오른 노인이 어깨에 벌통을 메고 가는 것과 마주쳤다.\n\n\"어때요? 도망친 벌 떼를 잡았어요, 포미치?\" 그가 물었다.\n\n\"아니요, 천만에요, 콘스탄틴 드미트리치! 우리 것 간수하기도 벅찹니다요! 벌써 두 번째 벌 떼가 날아가 버렸지 뭡니까…… 다행히 애들이 잡았지요. 마침 나리 밭을 갈고 있었거든요. 말을 풀어 놓고 벌 떼를 쫓아 달려갔지요.\"\n\n\"그래, 포미치, 어떻게 생각해요—풀베기를 시작할까요, 아니면 좀 기다릴까요?\"\n\n\"글쎄요. 우리 식으로는 성 베드로 축일까지 기다리지요. 하지만 나리는 늘 더 일찍 베시잖습니까. 뭐, 하느님 덕분에 건초는 좋습니다요. 가축들 먹일 게 넉넉할 겁니다.\"\n\n\"날씨는 어떨 것 같소?\"\n\n\"그야 하느님 손에 달렸지요. 어쩌면 맑을지도 모르고요.\"\n\n레빈은 형에게 다가갔다.\n\n세르게이 이바노비치는 한 마리도 잡지 못했지만 지루해하지 않았고, 더없이 명랑한 기분에 잠겨 있는 듯했다. 레빈은 형이 의사와 나눈 대화에 자극을 받아 이야기를 하고 싶어 한다는 것을 알아챘다. 반면에 레빈은 한시바삐 집으로 돌아가 이튿날 풀 벨 일꾼들을 불러 모으라는 지시를 내리고, 그를 몹시 사로잡고 있던 풀베기에 대한 의구심을 가라앉히고 싶었다.\n\n\"자, 이제 가시죠.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"뭘 그리 서두르느냐? 좀 더 있자꾸나. 그런데 너 어쩜 그리 흠뻑 젖었니! 한 마리도 못 잡는다 해도 이건 좋은 거란다. 온갖 놀이 중에서 가장 좋은 점이 바로 자연을 상대한다는 거지. 이 강철빛 물이 얼마나 곱냐!\" 세르게이 이바노비치가 말했다. \"이런 강가 둑을 보면 늘 그 수수께끼가 떠오르는구나—너도 아니? '풀이 물에게 말하기를, 우리는 흔들리고 또 흔들린다.'\"\n\n\"그 수수께끼는 모르겠는데요.\" 레빈이 지친 듯이 대답했다.\n\n# Chapter 3\n\n\"있잖아, 내가 너에 대해 생각해 봤어.\" 세르게이 이바노비치가 말했다. \"그 의사가 내게 들려준 바로는, 군(郡)에서 벌어지고 있는 일이란 게 정말 이만저만이 아니더구나. 아주 똑똑한 친구야. 그리고 전에도 말했지만 다시 한 번 말하마. 네가 회의에 나가지 않고, 군의 일에서 아예 발을 빼고 있는 건 옳지 않아. 점잖은 사람들이 거기에 끼어들지 않으면, 당연히 모든 게 엉망이 될 수밖에 없어. 우리는 돈을 내는데, 그게 전부 봉급으로 나가 버리고, 학교도 없고, 군 간호사도, 산파도, 약방도—아무것도 없어.\"\n\n\"음, 나도 해 보긴 했어, 알잖아.\" 레빈이 천천히, 마지못해 말했다. \"안 되더라고! 그러니 어쩔 도리가 없는 거지.\"\n\n\"하지만 왜 안 된다는 거야? 솔직히 난 도무지 이해가 안 돼. 무관심이라거나 무능이라거나—그런 건 인정하지 않겠어. 설마 단순히 게을러서는 아니겠지?\"\n\n\"그런 것들은 다 아니야. 해 봤는데, 내가 아무것도 할 수 없다는 걸 알았을 뿐이야.\" 레빈이 말했다.\n\n그는 형이 하는 말을 거의 알아듣지 못했다. 강 건너 밭을 바라보던 그는 무언가 검은 것을 알아보았지만, 그게 말인지 말을 탄 관리인인지는 분간할 수 없었다.\n\n\"왜 네가 아무것도 할 수 없다는 거지? 시도를 해 봤는데 네 생각에 성공하지 못했다고 그냥 포기하다니. 어떻게 그렇게 자존심이 없을 수 있어?\"\n\n\"자존심이라니!\" 형의 말에 정곡을 찔린 레빈이 말했다. \"난 이해가 안 돼. 만약 대학에서 다른 사람들은 다 적분을 이해하는데 나만 이해하지 못한다고 했다면, 그땐 자존심 문제가 끼어들었겠지. 하지만 이런 경우엔 무엇보다 먼저 자기가 이런 종류의 일에 어떤 자질을 갖추고 있다는 확신이 있어야 하고, 특히 그 모든 일이 대단히 중요하다는 확신이 있어야 하는 거야.\"\n\n\"뭐라고! 그러니까 그게 중요하지 않다는 말이냐?\" 세르게이 이바노비치도 정곡을 찔린 듯 말했다. 자기가 관심을 두는 일을 동생이 중요하지 않게 여기는 것이, 그리고 더욱이 자기가 하는 말에 동생이 분명 거의 주의를 기울이지 않는 것이 그를 찔렀던 것이다.\n\n\"난 그게 중요하다고 생각하지 않아. 도무지 마음에 와닿지가 않는걸. 어쩔 수 없어.\" 레빈이 대답하면서, 자기가 본 것이 관리인이며, 그 관리인이 농부들을 밭에서 내보내고 있는 듯하다는 것을 알아챘다. 그들은 쟁기를 뒤집어 놓고 있었다. '벌써 밭갈이를 끝낸 건가?' 그는 궁금했다.\n\n\"이봐, 그래도 정말이지.\" 형이 잘생기고 영리한 얼굴을 찌푸리며 말했다. \"모든 일에는 한계가 있는 법이야. 독창적이고 진실한 것도, 관습적인 모든 걸 싫어하는 것도 좋아—그건 나도 잘 알아. 하지만 정말이지, 네가 하는 말은 아무 의미가 없거나, 아니면 아주 잘못된 의미를 담고 있어. 네가 사랑한다고 주장하는 그 농부가……\"\n\n'난 그런 주장을 한 적이 없는데.' 콘스탄틴 레빈은 생각했다.\n\n\"……도움 없이 죽어 가는 게 중요하지 않은 일이라고 어떻게 생각할 수 있어? 무지한 농촌 아낙들은 아이들을 굶기고, 사람들은 어둠 속에 정체되어 마을 서기들의 손아귀에서 속수무책으로 휘둘리는데, 너는 그들을 도울 수단을 손에 쥐고 있으면서도, 네 생각엔 그게 중요하지 않다는 이유로 돕지 않는단 말이지.\"\n\n그러고는 세르게이 이바노비치가 그 앞에 양자택일을 들이밀었다. 즉, 너는 네가 할 수 있는 모든 일을 보지 못할 만큼 미숙하거나, 아니면 그 일을 하기 위해 네 편안함이나 허영심이나 그 무엇이든 희생하기를 원치 않거나, 둘 중 하나라는 것이었다.\n\n콘스탄틴 레빈은 굴복하거나, 아니면 공익을 위한 열의가 부족함을 자백하는 것 외에는 자기에게 열려 있는 길이 없다고 느꼈다. 그리고 이것이 그의 자존심을 짓밟았고 그의 감정을 상하게 했다.\n\n\"둘 다야.\" 그가 단호하게 말했다. \"난 그게 가능했다고 보지 않아……\"\n\n\"뭐라고! 돈을 제대로 썼다면 의료 지원을 제공하는 게 불가능했단 말이냐?\"\n\n\"내가 보기엔 불가능해…… 우리 군의 그 삼천 평방마일에 걸쳐, 해빙이며 폭풍우며 밭일까지 겹치는데, 어떻게 온 군에 두루 의료 지원을 할 수 있다는 건지 난 모르겠어. 게다가 난 의학을 믿지 않아.\"\n\n\"오, 그건 공정하지 못한 말이군…… 내가 너에게 수천 가지 사례를 들려줄 수도 있어…… 하지만 학교는, 어쨌든 학교는 말이야.\"\n\n\"학교는 뭣 하러 세워?\"\n\n\"무슨 소리야? 교육의 이로움에 대해 두 가지 의견이 있을 수 있겠어? 그게 너에게 좋은 거라면, 모두에게도 좋은 거지.\"\n\n콘스탄틴 레빈은 자기가 도덕적으로 벽에 몰린 듯한 기분이 들었고, 그래서 발끈해서는 공적인 일에 무관심한 자기 마음의 가장 큰 원인을 무심코 내뱉어 버렸다.\n\n\"어쩌면 그게 다 아주 좋은 일일지도 모르지. 하지만 내가 결코 이용하지도 않을 진료소를 세우고, 내 아이들은 결코 보내지도 않을, 농부들조차 자기 아이들을 보내고 싶어 하지 않는, 게다가 그들이 거기에 아이들을 보내야 한다는 확고한 믿음도 내겐 없는 그런 학교를 세우는 일에 내가 왜 골머리를 앓아야 하지?\" 그가 말했다.\n\n세르게이 이바노비치는 이 뜻밖의 관점에 잠시 놀랐지만, 곧바로 새로운 공격 계획을 세웠다. 그는 잠시 입을 다물고 낚싯바늘을 끌어올렸다가 다시 던지고는, 미소를 지으며 동생에게로 몸을 돌렸다.\n\n\"이봐…… 우선 진료소는 필요해. 우리도 아가피야 미하일로브나를 위해 군 의사를 불러왔잖아.\"\n\n\"아, 그렇긴 한데, 내 생각엔 그 사람 손목은 다시는 펴지지 않을 것 같던데.\"\n\n\"그건 두고 봐야 알 일이지…… 다음으로, 읽고 쓸 줄 아는 농부는 일꾼으로서 너에게 더 쓸모 있고 가치가 있어.\"\n\n\"아니, 누구한테든 물어봐.\" 콘스탄틴 레빈이 단호하게 대답했다. \"읽고 쓸 줄 아는 사람은 일꾼으로서는 훨씬 못해. 그리고 큰길을 보수하는 일은 불가능해. 다리를 놓기가 무섭게 도둑맞으니까.\"\n\n\"그래도 그건 요점이 아니야.\" 세르게이 이바노비치가 미간을 찌푸리며 말했다. 그는 반박당하는 걸 싫어했고, 더욱이 끊임없이 이것에서 저것으로 건너뛰며 새롭고 연관성도 없는 논점들을 끌어들여 어디에 대답해야 할지 알 수 없게 만드는 논쟁을 싫어했다. \"교육이 백성에게 이롭다는 건 인정하니?\"\n\n\"그래, 인정해.\" 레빈이 별생각 없이 말했고, 곧바로 자기가 생각지도 않은 말을 했다는 것을 의식했다. 그것을 인정하면, 자기가 줄곧 의미 없는 헛소리를 지껄여 왔다는 게 증명되리라는 것을 그는 느꼈다. 그게 어떻게 증명될지는 알 수 없었지만, 형이 그것을 자기에게 논리적으로 반드시 증명해 보이리라는 것은 알았고, 그래서 그 증명을 기다렸다.\n\n논쟁은 그가 예상했던 것보다 훨씬 단순한 것으로 드러났다.\n\n\"그게 이롭다는 걸 인정한다면,\" 세르게이 이바노비치가 말했다. \"정직한 사람으로서 너는 그것에 마음을 쓰지 않을 수 없고, 그 운동에 공감하지 않을 수 없고, 따라서 그것을 위해 일하고 싶어 하지 않을 수 없는 거지.\"\n\n\"하지만 난 아직도 이 운동이 정당하다고는 인정하지 않아.\" 콘스탄틴 레빈이 약간 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다.\n\n\"뭐라고! 하지만 방금 네 입으로……\"\n\n\"그러니까, 난 그게 좋다거나 가능하다고 인정한 건 아니라는 말이야.\"\n\n\"그건 시도해 보지 않고는 알 수 없는 거잖아.\"\n\n\"그래, 그렇다고 치자.\" 레빈은 전혀 그렇게 생각하지 않으면서도 말했다. \"설령 그렇다 쳐도, 그래도 난 여전히 모르겠어. 내가 왜 그 일에 골머리를 앓아야 하는지 말이야.\"\n\n\"어째서 그렇다는 거지?\" \"아니, 이왕 이야기가 나왔으니, 철학적 관점에서 그걸 나한테 설명해 봐.\" 레빈이 말했다.\n\n\"여기서 철학이 어디서 끼어드는 건지 난 모르겠는데.\" 세르게이 이바노비치가 말했는데, 레빈이 느끼기에 그것은 마치 동생이 철학을 논할 권리가 있다는 걸 인정하지 않는다는 투였다. 그리고 그것이 레빈을 짜증나게 했다.\n\n\"그럼 내가 말해 보지.\" 그가 열을 내며 말했다. \"내 생각에 우리 모든 행동의 근본 동력은 결국 사리사욕이야. 그런데 난 귀족으로서, 지방 기관에서 내 번영에 도움이 될 만한 건 아무것도 못 봤어. 길은 더 나아지지도 않았고, 더 나아질 수도 없어. 내 말들은 나쁜 길에서도 나를 충분히 잘 실어 나르니까. 의사나 진료소도 내겐 쓸모가 없어. 분쟁 조정관도 내겐 쓸모가 없어. 난 그에게 호소한 적도 없고, 앞으로도 결코 호소하지 않을 거야. 학교도 내겐 아무 도움이 안 될뿐더러, 너한테 말했듯이 도리어 해로워. 내게 군 기관이란 그저 삼 에이커마다 사 펜스 반의 세금을 낼 의무를 지고, 읍내까지 마차를 몰고 가서, 빈대와 함께 자고, 온갖 멍청하고 역겨운 소리를 듣는 것을 의미할 뿐이야. 사리사욕은 내게 아무런 유인도 주지 않아.\"\n\n\"실례지만,\" 세르게이 이바노비치가 미소를 띠며 끼어들었다. \"농노 해방을 위해 일하도록 우리를 부추긴 건 사리사욕이 아니었지만, 우리는 그 일을 위해 일했지.\"\n\n\"아니!\" 콘스탄틴 레빈이 한층 더 열을 내며 가로막았다. \"농노 해방은 다른 문제였어. 거기엔 사리사욕이 분명 끼어 있었지. 우리, 그러니까 우리 가운데 점잖은 사람들 모두를 짓누르던 그 멍에를 벗어 던지기를 갈망했으니까. 하지만 시(市)의원이 되어 청소부가 몇 명이나 필요한지, 내가 살지도 않는 도시에서 굴뚝을 어떻게 지을지 따위를 논의하고—배심원으로 나가 베이컨 한 덩이를 훔친 농부를 재판하고, 변호인과 검사가 늘어놓는 온갖 횡설수설을 여섯 시간씩 내리 듣고, 재판장이 내 늙고 모자란 알료시카를 심문하면서 '피고인, 베이컨을 빼돌린 사실을 인정하나?' '예?'\"\n\n콘스탄틴 레빈은 자기 이야기에 열이 올라, 재판장과 모자란 알료시카를 흉내 내기 시작했다. 그에게는 그 모든 게 요점에 딱 들어맞는 것처럼 여겨졌다.\n\n하지만 세르게이 이바노비치는 어깨를 으쓱했다.\n\n\"그래서, 대체 무슨 말을 하려는 거냐?\"\n\n\"내 말은 그저 이거야. 나와 관련된 권리들…… 내 이익에 관한 것이라면, 난 언제나 힘닿는 데까지 지킬 거라는 거지. 그들이 우리 대학생들을 급습하고 경찰이 우리 편지를 읽었을 때, 난 그 권리들을, 교육과 자유에 대한 내 권리를 끝까지 지킬 각오가 되어 있었어. 내 아이들과 형제들, 그리고 나 자신에게 영향을 미치는 의무 병역도 이해할 수 있어. 나와 관련된 일이라면 기꺼이 숙고할 거야. 하지만 군 의회 돈 사만 루블을 어떻게 쓸지 따위를 숙고하거나, 모자란 알료시카를 재판하는 일은—난 이해할 수도 없고, 할 수도 없어.\"\n\n콘스탄틴 레빈은 마치 말의 수문이 활짝 터져 버린 것처럼 이야기했다. 세르게이 이바노비치가 미소를 지었다.\n\n\"하지만 내일은 네가 재판받을 차례가 될 수도 있어. 그러면 옛날의 형사 재판소에서 재판받는 게 네 취향엔 더 맞았겠니?\"\n\n\"난 재판받을 일 없어. 난 누구도 죽이지 않을 거고, 그럴 필요도 없으니까. 자, 그럼 내가 말해 주지.\" 그는 또다시 전혀 요점에서 벗어난 주제로 날아가며 말을 이었다. \"우리 군의 지방 자치니 그 밖의 모든 것들이니 하는 건 말이야, 이를테면 우리가 삼위일체 축일에 땅에 꽂아 놓는 자작나무 가지 같은 거야. 유럽에서 절로 자라난 작은 숲처럼 보이게 하려고 말이지. 그런데 난 그런 자작나무 가지에 호들갑을 떨며 그것들을 믿을 수가 없어.\"\n\n세르게이 이바노비치는 그저 어깨를 으쓱했을 뿐인데, 마치 자작나무 가지가 어쩌다 그 대목에서 자기들 논쟁에 끼어들게 됐는지 의아하다는 것을 표현하려는 듯했다. 사실 그는 동생이 무슨 말을 하려는지 곧바로 알아챘으면서도 말이다.\n\n\"실례지만, 그런 식으로는 정말 논쟁이 안 된다는 걸 너도 알잖아.\" 그가 한마디 했다.\n\n하지만 콘스탄틴 레빈은 자기도 의식하고 있는 그 결점, 즉 공익을 위한 열의가 부족하다는 것을 변명하고 싶어서 말을 이어 갔다.\n\n\"내 생각엔,\" 그가 말했다. \"어떤 종류의 활동이든 사리사욕에 기초하지 않으면 오래갈 수 없을 것 같아. 그건 보편적인 원리야, 철학적인 원리지.\" 그는 마치 자기도 남들 못지않게 철학을 논할 권리가 있음을 보여 주고 싶다는 듯이, 단호하게 \"철학적인\"이라는 말을 되풀이했다.\n\n세르게이 이바노비치가 미소를 지었다. '이 녀석도 제 타고난 성향을 떠받드는 제 나름의 철학을 가지고 있군.' 그는 생각했다.\n\n\"이봐, 넌 철학은 그냥 가만 놔두는 게 낫겠다.\" 그가 말했다. \"동서고금 모든 철학의 핵심 문제는 바로 개인의 이익과 사회의 이익 사이에 존재하는 필수불가결한 연결을 찾아내는 데 있어. 하지만 그건 요점이 아니고. 요점은 네 비유에서 내가 한 가지 바로잡아야 할 게 있다는 거야. 자작나무는 그냥 꽂아 두는 게 아니라, 어떤 건 씨를 뿌리고 어떤 건 심는 거야. 그리고 그것들은 정성껏 다뤄야 하는 거고. 자기네 제도 속에서 무엇이 중요하고 의미 있는지를 직관적으로 감지하고, 그것을 평가할 줄 아는 민족만이 앞날을 가지는 거야—그런 민족만을 진정으로 역사적인 민족이라 부를 수 있는 거지.\"\n\n그러고는 세르게이 이바노비치가 그 주제를 콘스탄틴 레빈이 따라갈 수 없는 철학적 역사의 영역으로 끌고 가서는, 동생의 견해가 얼마나 그릇된 것인지를 낱낱이 보여 주었다.\n\n\"네가 그걸 싫어하는 것으로 말하자면, 이런 말 해서 미안하다만, 그건 그저 우리 러시아인 특유의 게으름이고 옛 농노주(農奴主)의 습성일 뿐이야. 그리고 난 네 경우에 그게 일시적인 잘못일 뿐이고 곧 지나가리라고 확신해.\"\n\n콘스탄틴은 입을 다물었다. 그는 자기가 사방에서 패배했다고 느꼈지만, 동시에 자기가 하고 싶은 말을 형이 알아듣지 못했다고도 느꼈다. 다만 그것이 자기가 자기 뜻을 명료하게 표현할 능력이 없어서인지, 아니면 형이 자기를 이해하려 하지 않거나 이해할 수 없어서인지는 도무지 마음을 정할 수가 없었다. 하지만 그는 그 궁리를 더 좇지 않았고, 대꾸도 하지 않은 채 전혀 다른, 자기 개인의 문제에 대한 상념에 빠져들었다.\n\n세르게이 이바노비치는 마지막 낚싯줄을 감아올리고 말을 풀었고, 둘은 마차를 몰고 떠났다.\n\n# Chapter 4\n\n레빈이 형과 대화를 나누는 동안 그의 마음을 사로잡고 있던 사사로운 문제란 이런 것이었다. 언젠가 지난해에 그는 풀 베는 광경을 보러 나갔다가, 관리인 때문에 잔뜩 화가 나서 자기가 화를 가라앉히는 데 가장 즐겨 쓰는 방법에 의지했다. 그는 한 농부에게서 낫을 받아 풀을 베기 시작했던 것이다.\n\n그는 그 일이 어찌나 마음에 들었던지 그 후로도 몇 번이나 풀베기에 손을 댔다. 그는 자기 집 앞 풀밭 전체를 베어 냈고, 올해는 이른 봄부터 농부들과 함께 며칠 내내 풀을 베리라는 계획을 마음에 품어 왔다. 형이 찾아온 이래로 그는 풀을 벨지 말지 망설이고 있었다. 하루 종일 형을 혼자 내버려 두기가 꺼림칙했고, 형이 그 일을 두고 자기를 비웃지나 않을까 두려웠다. 하지만 풀밭으로 마차를 몰고 들어가 풀 베는 감각을 떠올리자, 그는 풀을 베러 가기로 거의 마음을 굳히게 되었다. 형과 신경을 긁는 토론을 벌인 뒤, 그는 이 의도를 다시 한번 곱씹어 보았다.\n\n'몸을 움직이는 운동을 해야 해. 안 그러면 내 성미가 분명 엉망이 되고 말 거야.' 그는 이렇게 생각하며, 형 앞에서나 농부들 앞에서나 아무리 어색하게 느껴질지언정 풀을 베러 가기로 결심했다.\n\n저녁 무렵 콘스탄틴 레빈은 사무실로 가서 해야 할 일에 대한 지시를 내리고, 자기 풀밭 가운데 가장 넓고 가장 좋은 칼리노프 풀밭의 건초를 베기 위해 내일 풀 벨 일꾼들을 불러 모으라고 마을 곳곳에 사람을 보냈다.\n\n\"그리고 내 낫은 티트에게 보내 날을 세워 두었다가 내일 가져오게 해 주게. 어쩌면 나도 풀을 좀 벨지 모르니까.\" 그는 멋쩍어하지 않으려 애쓰며 말했다.\n\n관리인은 빙긋 웃으며 말했다. \"예, 나리.\"\n\n그날 저녁 차를 마시며 레빈은 형에게 말했다.\n\n\"날씨가 계속 좋을 것 같아요. 내일 풀을 베기 시작할 거예요.\"\n\n\"나는 그런 종류의 들일이 참 좋더구나.\" 세르게이 이바노비치가 말했다.\n\n\"저는 그 일을 무척 좋아해요. 가끔 농부들과 함께 직접 풀을 베곤 하는데, 내일은 하루 종일 풀을 베어 볼 작정이에요.\"\n\n세르게이 이바노비치는 고개를 들고 흥미로운 눈빛으로 동생을 바라보았다.\n\n\"그게 무슨 말이냐? 농부들처럼 하루 종일이란 말이냐?\"\n\n\"네, 아주 즐거운 일이거든요.\" 레빈이 말했다.\n\n\"운동으로는 더없이 좋지. 다만 네가 그걸 견뎌 낼 수 있을 것 같지 않구나.\" 세르게이 이바노비치가 비꼬는 기색이라곤 조금도 없이 말했다.\n\n\"해 봤어요. 처음에는 힘든 일이지만, 점점 몸에 익게 돼요. 어떻게든 버텨 낼 수 있을 거예요…….\"\n\n\"정말이냐! 별 생각을 다 하는구나! 그런데 말해 보렴, 농부들은 그걸 어떻게 보느냐? 자기네 주인이 그런 별난 사람이라고 속으로 비웃지 않더냐?\"\n\n\"아니요, 그렇진 않은 것 같아요. 게다가 그건 너무나 즐거우면서도 동시에 너무나 고된 일이라, 그런 걸 생각할 틈이 없거든요.\"\n\n\"그런데 그들과 함께 식사하는 건 어떻게 할 작정이냐? 너한테 라피트 한 병과 칠면조 구이를 거기까지 보내자면 좀 어색하겠는걸.\"\n\n\"아니요, 그들이 한낮에 쉴 때 그냥 집으로 돌아오면 돼요.\"\n\n이튿날 아침 콘스탄틴 레빈은 평소보다 일찍 일어났지만, 농장 일을 지시하느라 발이 묶였고, 풀 베는 풀밭에 다다랐을 때는 일꾼들이 이미 두 번째 줄을 베고 있었다.\n\n높은 지대에서는 아래쪽으로 그늘이 진, 풀이 베어진 풀밭의 한쪽이 내려다보였다. 베어 낸 풀의 잿빛 두둑들과, 일꾼들이 풀을 베기 시작한 자리에 벗어 던진 검은 외투 더미들이 보였다.\n\n풀밭 쪽으로 말을 몰고 다가가는 동안 차츰 농부들의 모습이 눈에 들어왔다. 어떤 이들은 외투를 입고, 어떤 이들은 셔츠 바람으로 풀을 베며 긴 줄을 이루어 하나씩 뒤따라가면서 저마다 다른 모양새로 낫을 휘두르고 있었다. 그는 마흔두 명을 헤아렸다.\n\n그들은 예전에 둑이 있던, 평탄하지 못한 낮은 풀밭 위에서 천천히 풀을 베어 나가고 있었다. 레빈은 자기 집 사람 몇몇을 알아보았다. 여기에는 아주 긴 흰색 작업복을 입고 몸을 앞으로 숙여 낫을 휘두르는 늙은 예르밀이 있었고, 저기에는 한때 레빈의 마부였던 바스카라는 젊은 친구가 한 줄 한 줄을 큼직하게 후려치며 베고 있었다. 여기에는 또 레빈에게 풀베기 기술을 가르쳐 준 스승 티트가 있었는데, 그는 마르고 자그마한 농부였다. 그는 맨 앞에서 마치 낫을 가지고 노는 것처럼 몸을 굽히지도 않고 넓은 줄을 베어 나갔다.\n\n레빈은 암말에서 내려 길가에 말을 매어 두고 티트를 맞으러 갔다. 티트는 덤불에서 두 번째 낫을 꺼내 그에게 건넸다.\n\n\"준비됐습니다, 나리. 면도날 같아서 저절로 베어집니다.\" 티트가 미소를 지으며 모자를 벗고 낫을 건네주며 말했다.\n\n레빈은 낫을 받아 들고 시험 삼아 휘둘러 보기 시작했다. 자기 줄을 다 벤 일꾼들이 땀에 젖은 채 기분 좋은 얼굴로 하나씩 길로 나와, 가볍게 웃으며 주인에게 인사를 건넸다. 그들은 모두 그를 빤히 쳐다보았지만 누구도 아무 말 하지 않았다. 그러다 짧은 양가죽 윗옷을 입은, 주름지고 수염 없는 얼굴의 키 큰 노인이 길로 나와 그에게 말을 걸었다.\n\n\"이것 보십쇼, 나리. 일단 밧줄을 잡았다 하면 놓을 수가 없는 법입니다요!\" 노인이 말했고, 레빈은 일꾼들 사이에서 새어 나오는 웃음소리를 들었다.\n\n\"놓지 않도록 애써 보겠네.\" 레빈은 티트의 뒤에 자리를 잡고 서서, 시작할 때를 기다리며 말했다.\n\n\"명심하십쇼.\" 노인이 거듭 말했다.\n\n티트가 자리를 비켜 주었고, 레빈은 그 뒤에서 시작했다. 길에 가까운 곳의 풀은 짧았는데, 오랫동안 풀을 베어 본 적이 없는 데다 자기에게 쏟아지는 시선에 마음이 흐트러진 레빈은, 낫을 힘차게 휘둘렀음에도 처음 얼마간은 서툴게 베었다. 등 뒤에서 목소리들이 들려왔다.\n\n\"날을 제대로 안 세웠구먼. 자루가 너무 높아. 저것 봐, 저렇게 몸을 굽혀야 하잖나.\" 한 사람이 말했다.\n\n\"뒤꿈치 쪽을 더 눌러야지.\" 또 다른 사람이 말했다.\n\n\"괜찮아, 잘하게 될 거야.\" 노인이 다시 말을 이었다.\n\n\"시작은 했구먼……. 너무 넓게 휘두르면 금세 지쳐 버려요……. 주인 나리도 분명 제 딴엔 애쓰시는 거지! 그래도 저기 빠뜨리고 못 벤 풀 좀 보게! 우리 같은 놈들이 저런 식으로 했다간 혼쭐이 났을걸!\"\n\n풀이 점점 부드러워졌고, 레빈은 대꾸 없이 귀를 기울이며 티트의 뒤를 따라 최선을 다하려 애썼다. 그들은 백 걸음쯤 나아갔다. 티트는 조금도 지친 기색 없이 멈추지 않고 계속 나아갔지만, 레빈은 벌써 버텨 내지 못할까 봐 두려워지기 시작했다. 그만큼 그는 지쳐 있었다.\n\n낫을 휘두르면서 그는 자기 기력이 바닥나 버렸음을 느꼈고, 티트에게 멈춰 달라고 청할 마음을 먹고 있었다. 그런데 바로 그 순간 티트가 제 스스로 멈춰서더니, 몸을 굽혀 풀을 한 줌 집어 낫을 문지르고 날을 갈기 시작했다. 레빈은 몸을 곧추세우고 깊이 숨을 들이쉬며 주위를 둘러보았다. 그의 뒤에는 한 농부가 따라오고 있었는데, 그 역시 분명 지쳐 있었다. 레빈이 있는 데까지 베어 오기를 기다리지도 않고 그 자리에서 곧장 멈춰 서서 낫을 갈기 시작했기 때문이다. 티트는 자기 낫과 레빈의 낫을 갈았고, 그들은 다시 나아갔다. 그다음에도 똑같았다. 티트는 멈추지도 지친 기색도 없이 낫을 한 번 한 번 휘두르며 나아갔다. 레빈은 뒤처지지 않으려 애쓰며 그를 따라갔지만, 점점 더 힘에 부쳤다. 더는 기력이 남지 않았다고 느끼는 순간이 찾아왔지만, 바로 그 순간 티트가 멈춰 서서 낫을 갈았다.\n\n그렇게 그들은 첫 번째 줄을 베어 냈다. 그리고 이 긴 줄은 레빈에게 유난히 고된 일처럼 여겨졌다. 하지만 끝에 다다라 티트가 낫을 어깨에 걸치고 베어 낸 풀밭에 자기 뒤꿈치가 남긴 자국을 따라 느릿한 걸음으로 되돌아가기 시작했을 때, 그리고 레빈도 자기가 베어 낸 자리를 똑같이 되짚어 걸어가던 그때, 그는 얼굴 위로 줄줄 흘러 코끝에서 방울방울 떨어지는 땀에도, 마치 물에 흠뻑 젖은 듯 등을 적시는 땀에도 아랑곳없이 무척 행복했다. 그를 특히나 기쁘게 한 것은, 이제 자기가 끝까지 버텨 낼 수 있으리라는 것을 알게 되었다는 사실이었다.\n\n다만 그의 줄이 잘 베어지지 않았다는 점이 그의 즐거움을 흐트러뜨렸다. '팔로 휘두르는 건 줄이고 온몸으로 더 휘둘러야겠어.' 그는 줄을 그은 듯 가지런해 보이는 티트의 줄을, 고르지 못하고 들쭉날쭉하게 누워 있는 자기 줄과 견주어 보며 생각했다.\n\n레빈이 알아챈 바로는, 첫 번째 줄을 티트는 유난히 빠르게 베었는데, 아마도 주인을 시험해 보고 싶었던 데다 그 줄이 마침 긴 줄이었기 때문일 것이다. 다음 줄들은 더 수월했지만, 그래도 레빈은 농부들에게 뒤처지지 않으려고 온 신경을 곤두세워야 했다.\n\n그는 아무것도 생각하지 않았고, 농부들에게 뒤처지지 않는 것과 자기 일을 되도록 잘 해내는 것 외에는 아무것도 바라지 않았다. 그의 귀에는 낫을 휘두르는 쉭쉭 소리밖에 들리지 않았고, 눈앞에는 풀을 베어 나가는 티트의 꼿꼿한 모습과, 베어 낸 풀이 그리는 초승달 모양의 곡선과, 그의 낫날 앞에서 천천히 율동적으로 쓰러지는 풀과 꽃송이들과, 저 앞에서 쉴 참이 기다리고 있는 줄의 끝이 보일 뿐이었다.\n\n문득, 일에 몰두하던 와중에, 그것이 무엇인지 어디서 온 것인지도 모른 채, 그는 뜨겁고 축축한 어깨에 기분 좋은 서늘함을 느꼈다. 그는 낫을 가는 짬에 하늘을 흘긋 올려다보았다. 무겁게 내리깔린 먹구름이 몰려와 굵은 빗방울이 떨어지고 있었다. 몇몇 농부들은 외투가 있는 데로 가서 그것을 걸쳤고, 또 어떤 이들은—바로 레빈 자신처럼—그저 어깨를 으쓱하며 그 기분 좋은 서늘함을 즐겼다.\n\n또 한 줄, 그리고 또 한 줄이 이어졌다. 긴 줄과 짧은 줄, 좋은 풀과 시원찮은 풀이 번갈아 나왔다. 레빈은 시간 감각을 완전히 잃어버려, 지금이 늦은 시각인지 이른 시각인지조차 알 수 없었다. 그의 일에 변화가 일기 시작했고, 그것이 그에게 엄청난 만족감을 주었다. 일에 몰두하던 가운데, 자기가 무엇을 하고 있는지 잊어버리는 순간들이 있었는데, 그럴 때는 모든 것이 수월하게 풀렸고, 바로 그 순간들에 그의 줄은 티트의 줄에 못지않게 매끄럽고 가지런하게 베어졌다. 하지만 자기가 무엇을 하고 있는지 떠올리고 더 잘하려고 애쓰기 시작하자마자, 그는 곧바로 자기 일의 온갖 어려움을 의식하게 되었고, 그러면 줄은 엉망으로 베어졌다.\n\n또 한 줄을 다 베고서 그는 다음 줄을 시작하러 다시 풀밭 위쪽으로 돌아가려 했지만, 티트가 멈춰 서더니 노인에게 다가가 나직한 목소리로 무언가 말했다. 두 사람은 해를 올려다보았다. '저들은 무슨 이야기를 하고 있는 거지, 그리고 저 사람은 왜 돌아가지 않는 걸까?' 농부들이 쉬지 않고 네 시간 가까이 풀을 베어 왔고 이제 점심을 먹을 때라는 것은 짐작도 못 한 채 레빈은 생각했다.\n\n\"점심 드실 때입니다, 나리.\" 노인이 말했다.\n\n\"벌써 그럴 때가 됐나? 그래, 그럼 점심을 먹지.\"\n\n레빈은 자기 낫을 티트에게 건네고, 외투 더미에서 빵을 가지러 비에 살짝 적셔진 길게 베어 낸 풀밭을 가로질러 가는 농부들과 함께 자기 집 쪽으로 걸어갔다. 그제야 그는 자기가 날씨를 잘못 짚었으며 비가 자기 건초를 흠뻑 적시고 있다는 사실을 불현듯 깨달았다.\n\n\"건초가 못 쓰게 되겠어.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"천만에요, 나리. 비 올 때 풀을 베면 날 갠 뒤에 긁어모으는 법입죠!\" 노인이 말했다.\n\n레빈은 말을 풀어 커피를 마시러 집으로 말을 몰았다. 세르게이 이바노비치는 이제 막 일어나는 참이었다. 레빈은 커피를 다 마시고서, 세르게이 이바노비치가 옷을 차려입고 식당으로 내려올 새도 없이 다시 풀밭으로 말을 달려 돌아갔다.\n\n# Chapter 5\n\n점심을 먹은 뒤 레빈은 풀 베는 사람들의 줄에서 아까와 같은 자리에 서지 않고, 농담을 건네며 자기 옆자리로 와 달라고 청한 노인과, 가을에야 막 장가를 들어 이번 여름 처음으로 풀을 베는 젊은 농부 사이에 섰다.\n\n노인은 몸을 꼿꼿이 세운 채 발끝을 바깥으로 벌리고 앞으로 나아가며, 길고 고른 걸음을 내디뎠다. 그러고는 마치 걸을 때 팔을 흔드는 것 이상으로는 아무런 힘도 들지 않는다는 듯, 그저 놀이라도 하는 양 정확하고 규칙적인 동작으로 높고 가지런한 풀 줄을 눕혀 나갔다. 마치 그가 아니라 날 선 낫이 제 스스로 물기 가득한 풀 사이를 쓱쓱 베어 나가는 것 같았다.\n\n레빈의 뒤에는 미시카라는 젊은이가 따라왔다. 갓 벤 풀을 한 가닥 머리에 둘러 묶은 그의 귀엽고 앳된 얼굴은 온통 안간힘으로 일그러져 있었다. 그러나 누군가 자기를 쳐다보기만 하면 그는 빙긋 웃었다. 일이 힘들다고 인정하느니 차라리 죽는 편을 택할 게 분명했다.\n\n레빈은 두 사람 사이를 지켰다. 한낮의 가장 뜨거운 시각인데도 풀베기가 그에게 그리 고된 일로 느껴지지는 않았다. 온몸을 흠뻑 적신 땀이 그를 식혀 주었고, 등과 머리와 팔꿈치까지 드러난 두 팔을 태우는 햇살은 그의 노동에 활력과 끈질긴 기운을 불어넣었다. 그리고 이제는 자기가 무엇을 하고 있는지 생각하지 않아도 되는 무의식의 순간이 점점 더 자주 찾아왔다. 낫은 저절로 베어 나갔다. 그것은 행복한 순간이었다. 더욱 즐거운 것은 풀 줄이 끝나는 개울가에 다다랐을 때였다. 노인은 젖은 두꺼운 풀로 낫을 문질러 닦고, 개울의 맑은 물에 칼날을 헹구고는, 양철 국자로 물을 조금 떠서 레빈에게 마시라고 권했다.\n\n\"내가 직접 빚은 술 맛이 어떤가, 응? 좋지, 응?\" 그가 눈을 찡긋하며 말했다.\n\n그리고 실제로 레빈은 푸르스름한 부스러기가 둥둥 떠 있고 양철 국자의 쇠 냄새가 배어든 이 미지근한 물보다 더 좋은 술을 마셔 본 적이 없었다. 그러고 나면 곧바로 낫에 손을 얹은 채 천천히 거니는 더없이 달콤한 시간이 이어졌다. 그동안 그는 줄줄 흐르는 땀을 닦아 내고, 깊이 숨을 들이쉬며, 길게 늘어선 풀 베는 사람들과 숲과 들판에서 벌어지는 온갖 광경을 둘러볼 수 있었다.\n\n레빈이 풀을 오래 벨수록 무의식의 순간은 더 자주 찾아왔다. 그럴 때면 낫을 휘두르는 것은 그의 두 손이 아니라, 제 나름의 생명과 의식으로 가득 찬 낫 자신이 저절로 풀을 베어 나가는 것 같았다. 그리고 마치 마법이라도 부린 듯, 일에 대해 생각하지 않는데도 작업은 절로 고르고 깔끔하게 마무리되었다. 그것이 가장 더없이 행복한 순간이었다.\n\n힘든 것은 오직 그 무의식적으로 된 동작을 끊고 생각을 해야 할 때, 그러니까 작은 흙무더기나 수영 풀숲을 빙 돌아가며 베어야 할 때뿐이었다. 노인은 이런 일을 손쉽게 해냈다. 흙무더기를 만나면 그는 동작을 바꾸어, 어떤 때는 낫의 뒷부분으로, 또 어떤 때는 낫끝으로, 짧은 동작으로 흙무더기 양쪽을 둘러 가며 깎아 냈다. 그러면서도 그는 계속 주위를 둘러보며 눈에 들어오는 것들을 살폈다. 어느 순간에는 산딸기를 따서 자기가 먹거나 레빈에게 권했고, 어느 때는 낫날로 나뭇가지를 휙 쳐 내고, 또 어느 때는 메추라기 둥지를 들여다보았는데 거기서 새가 낫 바로 아래로 푸드덕 날아올랐다. 길을 가로지르는 뱀을 잡아 마치 작살에 꿰듯 낫에 들어 올려 레빈에게 보여 주고는 멀리 던져 버리기도 했다.\n\n레빈에게도, 그의 뒤를 따르는 젊은 농부에게도 그런 자세의 변화는 어려웠다. 두 사람 다 같은 긴장된 동작을 거듭하고 또 거듭하면서 완전히 노동의 광란에 빠져 있었기에, 자세를 바꾸는 동시에 눈앞에서 벌어지는 일을 살피는 것은 도무지 할 수 없었다.\n\n레빈은 시간이 어떻게 흘러가는지 알아차리지 못했다. 만약 누군가 그에게 얼마나 오래 일했느냐고 물었다면, 그는 반 시간쯤이라고 대답했을 것이다. 그런데 어느새 저녁 식사 때가 가까워지고 있었다. 베어 낸 풀 위를 걸어 돌아오는 길에, 노인은 사방에서 다가오는 어린 계집아이들과 사내아이들에게 레빈의 주의를 돌렸다. 그 아이들은 키 큰 풀 사이로 거의 보이지 않을 만큼 작은 몸으로, 길을 따라 풀 베는 사람들 쪽으로 오고 있었는데, 작은 손에 빵 자루를 질질 끌고 천으로 둘둘 감싼 시큼한 호밀 맥주 단지를 들고 있었다.\n\n\"저것 좀 보게, 꼬마 개미들이 기어 오는구먼!\" 그가 아이들을 가리키며 말하고는, 손으로 눈 위를 가린 채 해를 올려다보았다. 그들은 두 줄을 더 베었다. 그러자 노인이 멈춰 섰다.\n\n\"자, 나리, 저녁 먹을 때요!\" 그가 활기차게 말했다. 개울에 다다르자 풀 베는 사람들은 베어 낸 풀 줄을 가로질러 자기들 외투를 쌓아 둔 곳으로 옮겨 갔는데, 거기서는 저녁밥을 가져온 아이들이 앉아 그들을 기다리고 있었다. 농부들은 무리를 지어 모였다. 멀리 있는 사람들은 짐수레 아래에, 가까이 있는 사람들은 버드나무 덤불 아래에 자리를 잡았다.\n\n레빈은 그들 곁에 앉았다. 그는 자리를 뜨고 싶지 않았다.\n\n나리 앞이라는 거북함은 진작에 사라지고 없었다. 농부들은 저녁 먹을 채비를 했다. 어떤 이들은 몸을 씻었고, 젊은 녀석들은 개울에서 멱을 감았으며, 다른 이들은 쉬기 좋게 자리를 만들고 빵 자루를 풀어 호밀 맥주 단지의 덮개를 벗겼다. 노인은 빵을 잘게 부숴 컵에 담고 숟가락 자루로 휘저은 다음, 국자로 물을 부어 빵을 좀 더 부수고 소금으로 간을 한 뒤, 동쪽을 향해 기도를 드렸다.\n\n\"자, 나리, 내가 만든 빵죽을 맛보시오.\" 그가 컵 앞에 무릎을 꿇으며 말했다.\n\n빵죽이 어찌나 맛있던지 레빈은 집으로 돌아갈 생각을 접었다. 그는 노인과 함께 저녁을 먹으며 노인의 집안일에 대해 더없이 깊은 관심을 가지고 이야기를 나누었고, 자기 자신의 일이며 노인이 흥미로워할 만한 온갖 사정도 들려주었다. 그는 자기 형보다도 이 노인에게 훨씬 더 가까움을 느꼈고, 이 사람에게 품은 애정에 절로 미소 짓지 않을 수 없었다. 노인이 다시 일어나 기도를 드린 뒤 풀을 베개 삼아 머리에 받치고 덤불 아래 드러눕자, 레빈도 똑같이 했다. 그러고는 햇볕 속에서 그토록 끈질기게 들러붙는 파리떼와 달아오른 얼굴과 몸을 간질이는 각다귀들에도 아랑곳없이 곧바로 잠이 들었고, 해가 덤불 반대편으로 넘어가 그에게 닿았을 때에야 비로소 깨어났다. 노인은 진작에 깨어나 일어나 앉은 채 젊은 녀석들의 낫을 갈고 있었다.\n\n레빈은 주위를 둘러보았으나 그 장소를 좀처럼 알아보지 못했다. 모든 것이 너무도 달라져 있었던 것이다. 드넓게 펼쳐진 풀밭은 모조리 베어져, 저무는 저녁 햇살의 비스듬한 빛 속에서 벌써 향긋한 내음을 풍기는 풀 줄들이 늘어선 채 유난히 싱그러운 광채로 반짝였다. 강가의 덤불들은 베여 나갔고, 전에는 보이지 않던 강 자체가 이제 굽이굽이 강철처럼 번득였으며, 움직이며 비탈을 올라오는 농부들과, 아직 베지 않은 풀밭 부분의 날 선 풀 벽과, 헐벗은 풀밭 위를 맴도는 매들까지—모든 것이 완전히 새로웠다. 몸을 일으킨 레빈은 얼마나 베었는지, 그리고 오늘 안에 얼마나 더 할 수 있는지를 헤아리기 시작했다.\n\n마흔두 사람이 해낸 일치고는 유난히 많았다. 그들은 큰 풀밭을 통째로 베어 냈는데, 농노 노동 시절에는 낫 서른 자루로 이틀이 걸리던 일이었다. 이제 줄이 짧은 모퉁이들만 남아 있었다. 그러나 레빈은 오늘 안에 되도록 많은 풀을 베고 싶다는 갈망을 느꼈고, 하늘에서 너무도 빨리 가라앉는 해가 야속하기만 했다. 그는 조금도 피곤하지 않았다. 오직 자기 일을 점점 더 빨리, 되도록 많이 해내고 싶을 뿐이었다.\n\n\"마시킨 고지대도 벨 수 있을까요? 어떻게 생각하세요?\" 그가 노인에게 물었다.\n\n\"하느님 뜻대로지요. 해가 아직 안 졌으니. 녀석들한테 보드카나 좀 줄까요?\"\n\n오후 휴식 때 그들이 다시 자리에 앉고, 담배 피우는 자들이 파이프에 불을 붙이자, 노인은 사람들에게 \"마시킨 고지대를 벨 거라네. 그러면 보드카가 좀 나올 게야.\" 하고 일러 주었다.\n\n\"안 벨 게 뭐 있나? 가세, 티트! 후딱 해치우자고! 밥은 밤에 먹으면 되지. 가세!\" 여기저기서 목소리가 터져 나왔고, 풀 베는 사람들은 빵을 다 먹어 치우고 다시 일하러 갔다.\n\n\"자, 다들, 힘내세!\" 티트가 말하고는 거의 종종걸음으로 앞장서 달려 나갔다.\n\n\"가자고, 가자고!\" 노인이 그의 뒤를 서둘러 좇으며 손쉽게 따라잡고는 말했다. \"내가 자네를 베어 넘길 테니, 조심하라고!\"\n\n그리고 젊은이도 늙은이도 마치 서로 경주라도 하듯 풀을 베어 나갔다. 그러나 아무리 빨리 일해도 그들은 풀을 망치지 않았고, 풀 줄은 한결같이 깔끔하고 정확하게 눕혀졌다. 모퉁이에 베지 않고 남은 작은 부분은 오 분 만에 베어졌다. 맨 뒤의 사람들이 막 자기 줄을 끝낼 때쯤, 맨 앞의 사람들은 벌써 외투를 어깨에 둘러메고 길을 가로질러 마시킨 고지대 쪽으로 향했다.\n\n그들이 국자를 절렁이며 마시킨 고지대의 우거진 골짜기로 들어섰을 때, 해는 이미 나무들 사이로 가라앉고 있었다. 골짜기 한가운데에서는 풀이 허리까지 차올랐는데, 부드럽고 연하며 깃털처럼 보드라운 그 풀밭에는 나무들 사이로 여기저기 들제비꽃이 점점이 박혀 있었다.\n\n길게 줄을 잡을지 비스듬히 잡을지 잠깐 의논한 뒤, 역시 이름난 풀꾼인 프로호르 예르밀린이라는 덩치 크고 머리 검은 농부가 앞장섰다. 그는 꼭대기까지 올라가 다시 돌아서서 풀을 베기 시작했고, 모두가 그의 뒤로 줄을 지어 골짜기 아래로 내려갔다가 숲 가장자리까지 비탈을 올라갔다. 해가 숲 너머로 가라앉았다. 어느새 이슬이 내리고 있었다. 풀 베는 사람들은 비탈에서만 햇빛 속에 있었고, 안개가 피어오르는 아래쪽과 맞은편 비탈에서는 싱그러운 이슬 그늘 속으로 베어 들어갔다. 일은 빠르게 진척되었다. 풀은 물기 어린 소리를 내며 베여 곧바로 높고 향긋한 줄로 눕혀졌다. 짧은 줄에 더 바싹 모여든 사방의 풀꾼들은 절렁이는 국자 소리와 쟁쟁거리는 낫 소리, 낫을 가는 숫돌의 쉭쉭 소리, 그리고 흥겨운 외침 속에서 서로를 부추겨 댔다.\n\n레빈은 여전히 젊은 농부와 노인 사이를 지켰다. 짧은 양가죽 윗도리를 걸친 노인은 한결같이 흥겹고 익살스러우며 동작이 거침없었다. 나무들 사이에서 그들은 물기 많은 풀 속에 통통하게 부풀어 오른 이른바 \"자작나무버섯\"을 낫으로 줄곧 베어 냈다. 그러나 노인은 버섯을 만날 때마다 매번 허리를 굽혀 그것을 주워 품에 넣었다. \"우리 마누라한테 줄 또 하나의 선물이지.\" 그는 그러면서 말했다.\n\n젖어서 부드러운 풀을 베기는 쉬웠지만 골짜기의 가파른 비탈을 오르내리는 것은 고된 일이었다. 그러나 이것이 노인을 괴롭히지는 못했다. 그는 여전히 낫을 휘두르며 크고 엮어 만든 신발을 신은 발을 단단하고 잰걸음으로 옮기면서 가파른 곳을 천천히 올라갔다. 작업복 아래로 비져 나온 바지며 온몸이 안간힘으로 떨렸지만, 그는 가는 길에 풀 한 포기, 버섯 하나도 놓치지 않았고, 농부들과 레빈에게 계속 농담을 건넸다. 레빈은 그의 뒤를 따라가며, 맨몸으로도 기어오르기 힘들 가파른 벼랑을 낫까지 들고 오르는 노인이 곧 굴러떨어질 것만 같다고 몇 번이나 생각했다. 그러나 노인은 끝까지 기어올라 자기 할 일을 해냈다. 마치 어떤 외부의 힘이 그를 움직이고 있는 것만 같았다.\n\n# Chapter 6\n\n마시킨 풀밭은 깎였고, 마지막 줄까지 끝나자 농부들은 외투를 걸쳐 입고 흥겹게 터벅터벅 집으로 향하고 있었다. 레빈은 말에 올라, 농부들과 아쉬운 작별을 하고 집을 향해 말을 몰았다. 언덕배기에서 그는 뒤를 돌아보았다. 골짜기에서 피어오른 안개 속에서 그들은 보이지 않았다. 다만 거칠고 유쾌한 목소리며, 웃음소리며, 낫이 부딪치는 소리만 들려올 따름이었다.\n\n세르게이 이바노비치는 진작 식사를 마치고, 자기 방에서 얼음을 띄운 레몬수를 마시며 우편으로 방금 받은 평론지와 신문을 훑어보고 있었다. 그때 레빈이 즐겁게 떠들며 방으로 뛰어들었는데, 젖어서 엉겨 붙은 머리카락이 이마에 들러붙어 있었고, 등과 가슴은 땀에 절어 거뭇거뭇했다.\n\n\"풀밭을 통째로 다 깎았어! 아, 좋아, 정말 끝내준다고! 형은 어떻게 지냈어?\" 레빈은 전날의 그 거북한 대화 따위는 까맣게 잊고 말했다.\n\n\"맙소사! 그 꼴이 뭐냐!\" 세르게이 이바노비치가 처음 순간에는 다소 못마땅한 얼굴로 둘러보며 말했다. \"그리고 문, 문 좀 닫아!\" 그가 소리쳤다. \"적어도 열댓 마리는 들여보냈을 게다.\"\n\n세르게이 이바노비치는 파리를 견디지 못했고, 자기 방에서는 밤이 아니면 결코 창문을 열지 않았으며, 문도 늘 조심스럽게 닫아 두었다.\n\n\"한 마리도 안 들어왔어, 정말로. 만약 들어왔으면 내가 다 잡을게. 형은 이게 얼마나 즐거운 일인지 믿지 못할 거야! 형은 하루를 어떻게 보냈어?\"\n\n\"아주 잘 보냈지. 그런데 정말 하루 종일 풀을 벴단 말이냐? 늑대처럼 배가 고프겠구나. 쿠즈마가 네 식사를 다 차려 놨다.\"\n\n\"아니, 배고픈 줄도 모르겠어. 거기서 뭘 좀 먹었거든. 그래도 일단 가서 씻어야겠어.\"\n\n\"그래, 가거라, 가, 나도 곧 갈 테니.\" 세르게이 이바노비치가 동생을 바라보며 고개를 절레절레 흔들고 말했다. \"어서 가, 서둘러.\" 그는 미소를 지으며 덧붙이고는, 책들을 챙겨 들고 자기도 일어날 채비를 했다. 그 역시 별안간 기분이 좋아지면서 동생 곁을 떠나기가 싫어졌던 것이다. \"그런데 비가 올 때는 어떻게 했어?\"\n\n\"비? 아니, 거의 한 방울도 안 왔는걸. 곧 올게. 그래, 형도 좋은 하루를 보냈다고? 그거 잘됐네.\" 그러고 레빈은 옷을 갈아입으러 가 버렸다.\n\n오 분 뒤 형제는 식당에서 만났다. 레빈은 자기가 배고프지 않은 것 같았고, 그저 쿠즈마의 마음을 상하게 하지 않으려고 식탁 앞에 앉았다. 그런데 막상 먹기 시작하자 음식이 기막히게 맛있게 느껴졌다. 세르게이 이바노비치는 미소를 띤 채 그를 지켜보았다.\n\n\"아, 참, 너한테 온 편지가 있다.\" 그가 말했다. \"쿠즈마, 그것 좀 가져오게. 그리고 문 닫는 거 잊지 말고.\"\n\n편지는 오블론스키에게서 온 것이었다. 레빈은 그것을 소리 내어 읽었다. 오블론스키는 페테르부르크에서 그에게 이렇게 써 보냈다. \"돌리에게서 편지를 받았네. 그 사람은 지금 에르구쇼보에 있는데, 거기 일이 죄다 엉망인 모양이야. 제발 한번 가서 그녀를 좀 봐 주게나. 조언을 해 줘. 자넨 그쪽 일을 다 알지 않나. 자넬 보면 그 사람도 정말 기뻐할 걸세. 가엾게도 혼자 외톨이로 있다네. 장모님이며 다른 식구들은 아직 다 외국에 있고.\"\n\n\"이거 잘됐어! 꼭 그녀에게 다녀와야겠어.\" 레빈이 말했다. \"아니면 같이 가자고. 정말 훌륭한 여자야, 안 그래?\"\n\n\"그럼 여기서 멀지는 않은 거냐?\"\n\n\"이십오 베르스타쯤. 어쩌면 삼십 베르스타일지도. 그래도 길이 아주 좋아. 끝내주지, 마차로 가면 돼.\"\n\n\"나도 기꺼이 가마.\" 세르게이 이바노비치가 여전히 미소를 지으며 말했다. 동생의 모습을 보자 단번에 기분이 좋아졌던 것이다.\n\n\"이야, 식욕이 대단하구나!\" 그가 접시 위로 숙인, 검붉게 햇볕에 그을린 동생의 얼굴과 목덜미를 바라보며 말했다.\n\n\"끝내주지! 이게 온갖 어리석은 짓에 얼마나 효험 좋은 치료법인지 형은 상상도 못 할 거야. 나는 의학에 새 낱말 하나를 보태고 싶어. Arbeitskur라고 말이지.\"\n\n* Arbeitskur: 독일어로 '노동 요법'이라는 뜻.\n\n\"그래, 그렇지만 너한테는 그게 필요 없을 것 같은데.\"\n\n\"내가 아니라, 온갖 신경증 환자들을 위해서 말이야.\"\n\"그래, 한번 시도해 볼 만하지. 나도 네가 풀 베는 모습을 보러 가려고 했는데, 어찌나 견딜 수 없게 더운지 숲까지밖에 못 갔다. 거기 잠깐 앉았다가, 숲을 따라 마을까지 갔지. 거기서 네 옛 유모를 만나 농부들이 너를 어떻게 보는지 슬쩍 떠봤다. 내가 짐작하기로는, 그들은 그걸 못마땅해해. 유모가 그러더구나. '그건 나리가 하실 일이 아니지요.' 통틀어 보면, 사람들의 관념 속에는 그들이 말하는 이른바 '나리다운' 처신의 선이라는 게 아주 명확하고 분명하게 자리 잡고 있는 것 같아. 그래서 그들은 양반들이 자기네 관념 속에 또렷이 그어진 경계 밖으로 나가는 걸 인정하지 않는 거지.\"\n\n\"그럴지도 모르지. 하지만 어쨌든 이건 내가 평생 한 번도 맛본 적 없는 즐거움이야. 그리고 거기에 해로울 거라곤 없잖아, 안 그래? 그렇지?\" 레빈이 대답했다. \"그들이 싫어한다고 해도 난 어쩔 수 없어. 그래도 난 이게 옳다고 믿어. 응?\"\n\n\"통틀어 말해서,\" 세르게이 이바노비치가 말을 이었다. \"넌 오늘 하루에 만족하느냐?\"\n\n\"아주 만족해. 풀밭을 통째로 다 베었거든. 게다가 거기서 정말 훌륭한 노인하고 친해졌어! 그 노인이 얼마나 유쾌한 사람인지 형은 상상도 못 할 거야!\"\n\n\"그래, 그러니 너도 네 하루에 만족하는 거고, 나도 그렇다. 우선 나는 체스 문제 두 개를 풀었는데, 그중 하나는 아주 멋진 거였어. 폰으로 여는 수였지. 나중에 보여 주마. 그러고 나서는, 어제 우리가 나눈 대화를 곰곰이 생각해 봤다.\"\n\n\"응? 어제 우리 대화?\" 식사를 마친 레빈이 더없이 흐뭇하게 눈꺼풀을 내리깔고 깊은 숨을 들이쉬며 말했지만, 어제 무슨 대화를 했는지 도무지 떠올릴 수가 없었다.\n\n\"네 말도 어느 정도는 옳다고 생각해. 우리 의견 차이를 정리하자면 이런 거지. 너는 사리사욕을 원동력으로 보는 데 반해, 나는 어느 정도 발전한 단계에 이른 사람이라면 누구에게나 공동의 복리에 대한 관심이 반드시 존재한다고 보는 거야. 어쩌면 물질적 이익에 바탕을 둔 행동이 더 바람직하다는 네 말도 옳을지 모르지. 너는 통틀어 말해, 프랑스 사람들 표현대로 너무 primesautière한 천성이라, 격렬하고 정력적인 행동이 아니면 아예 안 하려고 들거든.\"\n\n* primesautière: 프랑스어로 '충동적인', '즉흥적인'이라는 뜻.\n레빈은 형의 말을 들으면서도 한마디도 이해하지 못했고, 이해하고 싶지도 않았다. 그는 그저 형이 무슨 질문이라도 던져서 자기가 듣고 있지 않았다는 것이 드러날까 봐 두려울 따름이었다.\n\n\"그러니까 내 생각은 그렇다는 거야, 얘야.\" 세르게이 이바노비치가 동생의 어깨를 짚으며 말했다.\n\n\"응, 물론이지. 그런데 있잖아? 난 내 생각을 고집하지 않을래.\" 레빈이 죄지은 듯한, 아이 같은 미소를 지으며 대답했다. '내가 도대체 무슨 논쟁을 하고 있었더라?' 그는 생각했다. '물론 나도 옳고, 형도 옳지. 그러니 다 잘된 거야. 다만 나는 사무소에 들러 일을 좀 살펴봐야 해.' 그는 기지개를 켜고 미소를 지으며 일어났다. 세르게이 이바노비치도 미소를 지었다.\n\n\"나가려거든 같이 가자.\" 그가 말했다. 그는 싱싱함과 활기를 그야말로 내뿜는 듯한 동생 곁을 떠나기가 싫었다. \"자, 사무소에 가야 한다면 거기로 가자고.\"\n\n\"아, 이런!\" 레빈이 어찌나 크게 소리를 질렀는지 세르게이 이바노비치가 깜짝 놀랄 정도였다.\n\n\"왜, 무슨 일이냐?\"\n\n\"아가피아 미하일로브나 손은 좀 어때?\" 레빈이 자기 머리를 탁 치며 말했다. \"그 사람을 까맣게 잊고 있었다니.\"\n\n\"많이 나았다.\"\n\n\"어쨌든 잠깐 내려가 봐야겠어. 형이 모자를 쓰기도 전에 돌아올게.\"\n\n그러고 그는 봄철 딸랑이처럼 구두 뒤축을 딸각거리며 아래층으로 달려 내려갔다.\n\n# Chapter 7\n\n스테판 아르카지치는 정부 관직에 몸담은 사람이라면 누구나 잘 알지만 바깥 사람들에게는 도무지 이해되지 않는, 더없이 자연스럽고 긴요한 공무를 수행하기 위해 페테르부르크로 떠나 있었다. 그 공무란, 그것 없이는 관직에 머물러 있기조차 어려운 것으로서, 다름 아니라 자신의 존재를 부처(部處)에 상기시키는 일이었다. 그리고 이 의식을 제대로 치르느라 집 안에 있던 현금을 죄다 챙겨 가지고 떠난 그는, 경마장과 여름 별장들을 오가며 즐겁고 기분 좋게 나날을 보내고 있었다. 한편 돌리와 아이들은 비용을 최대한 줄이기 위해 시골로 옮겨 와 있었다. 그녀가 간 곳은 자기 지참금이었던 영지 예르구쇼보로, 봄에 그곳의 숲을 팔았던 바로 그 영지였다. 그곳은 레빈의 포크로프스코예에서 사십 마일가량 떨어져 있었다. 예르구쇼보의 크고 오래된 본채는 오래전에 헐려 없어졌고, 노공작은 별채를 손보아 증축해 둔 터였다. 이십 년 전, 돌리가 어린아이였을 무렵에는 이 별채가 넓고 안락했으나, 여느 별채가 다 그렇듯 진입로 가로수길에 옆구리를 대고 남쪽을 향해 서 있었다. 그러나 이제 이 별채는 낡고 허물어져 가고 있었다. 봄에 스테판 아르카지치가 숲을 팔려고 내려갔을 때, 돌리는 그에게 집을 둘러보고 필요한 수리를 지시해 달라고 부탁했다. 스테판 아르카지치는, 사실 부정한 남편들이 다 그렇듯이, 아내의 편안함에 무척 마음을 쓰는 사람이라, 손수 집을 둘러보고 필요하다고 여겨지는 일을 죄다 지시해 두었다. 그가 필요하다고 여긴 일이란, 가구를 죄다 사라사 천으로 씌우고, 커튼을 달고, 정원의 잡초를 뽑고, 연못에 작은 다리를 놓고, 꽃을 심는 것이었다. 그러나 그는 정작 긴요한 다른 일들은 많이 잊어버렸고, 나중에 다리야 알렉산드로브나는 그것이 없어 몹시 애를 먹었다.\n\n스테판 아르카지치는 자상한 아버지이자 남편이 되려고 애를 썼지만, 자기에게 아내와 자식들이 있다는 사실을 도무지 마음에 담아 두지 못했다. 그는 독신자의 취향을 지니고 있었고, 그 취향에 맞추어 자기 삶을 꾸렸다. 모스크바로 돌아온 그는 모든 게 준비되었으며 집이 작은 낙원이 될 거라고, 그러니 꼭 가는 게 좋겠다고 아내에게 자랑스레 알렸다. 아내가 시골에 가 있는 것은 스테판 아르카지치에게 모든 면에서 매우 흡족한 일이었다. 아이들에게도 이롭고, 비용도 줄고, 자신은 한결 자유로워졌으니 말이다. 다리야 알렉산드로브나는 여름을 시골에서 보내는 것이 아이들에게, 특히 성홍열을 앓은 뒤로 기운을 채 회복하지 못한 어린 딸아이에게 꼭 필요하다고 여겼고, 또한 자기를 비참하게 만들던 자잘한 수모들, 그러니까 장작 장수며 생선 장수며 구두장이에게 진 소소한 외상값에서 벗어나는 방편이라고 여겼다. 이뿐 아니라 그녀가 시골로 가는 것을 기뻐한 까닭은, 동생 키티를 그곳에 와서 함께 지내게 할 꿈을 꾸고 있었기 때문이다. 키티는 한여름께 외국에서 돌아올 예정이었고, 해수욕을 하라는 처방을 받은 터였다. 키티는 두 사람 모두에게 어린 시절의 추억이 가득한 예르구쇼보에서 돌리와 함께 여름을 보내는 것만큼 마음을 끄는 일은 없다고 편지에 썼다.\n\n시골 생활의 첫 며칠은 돌리에게 무척 힘들었다. 그녀는 어린 시절 시골에서 지냈던 터라, 시골이란 도시의 온갖 불쾌한 일에서 벗어나는 피난처이며, 비록 호사스럽지는 않더라도—돌리는 그쯤은 기꺼이 받아들일 수 있었다—값싸고 편안한 곳이고, 무엇이든 넉넉하고, 무엇이든 값이 싸며, 무엇이든 구할 수 있고, 아이들은 행복하다는 인상을 간직하고 있었다. 그러나 이제 한 집안의 가장으로서 시골에 와 보니, 모든 것이 자기가 그려 왔던 것과는 전혀 딴판임을 알게 되었다.\n\n도착한 이튿날 비가 억수같이 쏟아졌고, 밤사이 복도와 아이들 방으로 물이 새어 들어, 침대들을 응접실로 옮겨야 했다. 부엌데기는 도무지 구할 수가 없었다. 젖소 아홉 마리도 소치는 여자의 말로는, 더러는 곧 새끼를 낳을 참이고, 더러는 막 낳은 참이며, 더러는 늙었고, 또 더러는 젖통이 굳어 있어서, 아이들이 먹을 버터도 우유도 넉넉지 않았다. 달걀도 없었다. 닭도 구할 수 없어, 굽거나 삶을 거라곤 늙고 푸르죽죽하고 질긴 수탉들뿐이었다. 바닥을 닦을 여자도 구할 수 없었으니—다들 감자밭을 매고 있었던 것이다. 말 한 마리가 사나워서 멍에를 단 채 날뛰는 통에 마차를 타고 나가는 것도 어림없는 일이었다. 해수욕을 할 만한 곳도 없었으니, 강기슭은 온통 가축들이 밟아 놓은 데다 길 쪽으로 훤히 트여 있었다. 산책조차 할 수 없었던 것이, 가축들이 울타리 틈으로 정원에 들어왔고, 게다가 끔찍한 황소 한 마리가 음매음매 울어 대는 통에 누군가를 들이받을 것만 같았다. 옷을 넣어 둘 변변한 장도 없었다. 있는 장이라곤 아예 닫히지 않거나, 누가 곁을 지나가기만 하면 벌컥 열려 버리는 것들뿐이었다. 냄비도 솥도 없었고, 빨래방에는 구리 가마솥도 없었으며, 하녀들 방에는 다리미질할 판자 하나조차 없었다.\n\n평온과 휴식 대신 그녀가 보기에 이 모든 끔찍한 재난들을 맞닥뜨리고, 다리야 알렉산드로브나는 처음에는 절망에 빠졌다. 있는 힘을 다해 애를 썼지만 형편이 가망 없음을 느꼈고, 순간순간 눈에 차오르는 눈물을 억눌러야 했다. 잘생기고 공손한 현관지기다운 외모 때문에 스테판 아르카지치가 마음에 들어 집사로 앉힌 퇴역 병참 하사관 출신의 그 집사는, 다리야 알렉산드로브나의 시름에 조금도 동정을 보이지 않았다. 그는 \"어쩔 도리가 없습죠, 농사꾼들이 워낙 한심한 작자들이라서요\" 하고 공손히 말할 뿐, 그녀를 돕는 일은 아무것도 하지 않았다.\n\n형편은 가망 없어 보였다. 그러나 오블론스키 집안에도, 사실 어느 집안에나 그렇듯이, 눈에 잘 띄지 않지만 더없이 귀하고 쓸모 있는 사람이 하나 있었으니, 바로 마리야 필리모노브나였다. 그녀는 안주인을 달래며 모든 게 잘 풀릴 거라고 장담했고(이 표현은 그녀의 것이었는데, 마트베이가 그녀에게서 빌려 쓴 것이었다), 부산을 떨거나 서두르는 일 없이 손수 일에 착수했다. 그녀는 곧장 집사의 아내와 친해졌고, 바로 첫날부터 아카시아 나무 아래서 집사 내외와 차를 마시며 형편의 온갖 사정을 함께 짚어 보았다. 머지않아 마리야 필리모노브나는 아카시아 나무 아래에 이를테면 자기만의 모임을 꾸렸고, 바로 이 모임, 그러니까 집사의 아내며 마을 촌장이며 회계 사무소 서기로 이루어진 이 모임에서 살림살이의 온갖 어려움이 차츰차츰 풀려 나갔으며, 일주일 만에 정말로 모든 게 잘 풀렸다. 지붕이 고쳐졌고, 부엌데기—마을 촌장의 단짝이었다—를 구했으며, 암탉들을 사들였고, 젖소들이 우유를 내기 시작했으며, 정원 울타리는 말뚝으로 틈을 메웠고, 목수가 빨래 짜는 기계를 만들었으며, 장에는 걸쇠를 달아 제멋대로 벌컥 열리는 일이 없어졌고, 군용 천을 씌운 다리미판이 의자 팔걸이에서 서랍장에 걸쳐 놓이자, 하녀들 방에서 다리미 냄새가 풍기게 되었다.\n\n\"자, 보세요, 그렇게 절망하시더니.\" 마리야 필리모노브나가 다리미판을 가리키며 말했다. 그들은 짚으로 엮은 발로 목욕할 헛간까지 마련했다. 릴리가 해수욕을 하기 시작했고, 다리야 알렉산드로브나는 비록 부분적으로나마, 평온하지는 않을지언정 적어도 편안한 시골 생활에 대한 자기 기대가 이루어지는 것을 보게 되었다. 여섯 아이를 데리고 다리야 알렉산드로브나가 평온할 수는 없었다. 하나가 앓아눕고, 다른 하나가 자칫 그렇게 될 수 있고, 또 하나는 무언가 꼭 필요한 것이 없고, 또 다른 하나는 못된 성미의 기미를 보이는 식이었다. 평온한 짧은 시기는 정말이지 드물었다. 그러나 이런 근심과 걱정이야말로 다리야 알렉산드로브나에게는 누릴 수 있는 유일한 행복이었다. 그것들이 없었다면 그녀는 자기를 사랑하지 않는 남편을 곱씹으며 홀로 남겨졌을 것이다. 게다가 어머니로서 병에 대한 두려움이며, 병 그 자체며, 아이들에게서 못된 기질의 조짐을 보는 슬픔을 견디기가 힘들기는 했어도, 아이들 자신이 지금도 그 고통을 작은 기쁨으로 갚아 주고 있었다. 그 기쁨들은 모래 속의 금처럼 너무도 작아서 눈에 띄지 않은 채 지나가 버렸고, 안 좋은 순간에는 고통밖에, 모래밖에 보이지 않았다. 그러나 좋은 순간도 있어서, 그때는 기쁨밖에, 금밖에 보이지 않았다.\n\n이제 시골의 고적함 속에서 그녀는 점점 더 자주 그 기쁨들을 알아차리게 되었다. 아이들을 바라보며 그녀는 자기가 잘못 생각하는 거라고, 어머니라서 제 자식들에게 치우쳐 있는 거라고 스스로를 타이르려 갖은 애를 쓰곤 했다. 그러면서도 자기 아이들이 여섯 다 저마다 다른 모습으로 사랑스럽고, 좀처럼 만나 보기 힘든 그런 아이들이며, 그래서 자기는 그들로 하여 행복하고 그들이 자랑스럽다고 스스로에게 말하지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n# Chapter 8\n\n5월 말, 모든 일이 그럭저럭 만족스럽게 정리되었을 무렵, 그녀는 시골 살림이 엉망진창이라는 자신의 하소연에 대한 남편의 답장을 받았다. 그는 미리 모든 것을 헤아리지 못한 점에 대해 용서를 빌었고, 틈이 나는 대로 곧 내려가겠다고 약속했다. 그러나 그런 틈은 좀처럼 나지 않았고, 6월 초까지 다리야 알렉산드로브나는 시골에서 홀로 지냈다.\n\n성 베드로 주간의 일요일에 다리야 알렉산드로브나는 아이들 모두에게 성찬식을 받게 하려고 마차를 타고 미사에 갔다. 다리야 알렉산드로브나는 언니와 어머니와 친구들과 나누는 은밀하고 철학적인 이야기 속에서 종교에 관한 자유로운 견해로 그들을 자주 놀라게 하곤 했다. 그녀는 영혼의 윤회라는, 그녀만의 기묘한 종교를 지니고 있었고, 그것을 굳게 믿었으며, 교회의 교리에는 별로 마음을 쓰지 않았다. 그러나 자기 가족 안에서는 교회가 요구하는 모든 것을 엄격하게 지켰는데, 단지 본보기를 보이기 위해서가 아니라 온 마음을 다해서였다. 아이들이 거의 일 년 가까이 성찬식을 받지 못했다는 사실이 그녀를 몹시 걱정스럽게 만들었고, 마리야 필리모노브나의 전적인 찬동과 공감을 얻어 그녀는 그것을 이제 여름에 치르기로 결정했다.\n\n며칠 전부터 다리야 알렉산드로브나는 아이들에게 무엇을 입힐지 분주하게 궁리했다. 옷을 새로 짓거나 고치거나 빨았고, 솔기와 주름 장식을 늘렸으며, 단추를 달고 리본을 마련했다. 영국인 가정교사가 맡은 타냐의 옷 하나가 다리야 알렉산드로브나의 속을 단단히 썩였다. 영국인 가정교사는 그 옷을 고치면서 솔기를 엉뚱한 데에 만들고 소매를 너무 많이 잡아 올려서 옷을 아예 망쳐 버렸다. 옷이 타냐의 어깨에 어찌나 끼는지 보고 있기가 안쓰러울 지경이었다. 그러나 마리야 필리모노브나가 무를 대고 작은 어깨 망토를 덧붙이자는 좋은 생각을 떠올렸다. 옷은 제대로 손질되었지만, 영국인 가정교사와 하마터면 다툼이 날 뻔했다. 그렇지만 아침이 되자 모든 것이 다행히 정리되었고, 열 시 무렵—그들이 미사를 위해 사제에게 기다려 달라고 부탁한 시각—새 옷을 입고 환한 얼굴을 한 아이들이 마차 앞 계단에 서서 어머니를 기다리고 있었다.\n\n마차에는 마리야 필리모노브나의 권유 덕분에 사나운 보론 대신 집사의 말 부르니를 매어 놓았고, 자기 옷차림에 대한 걱정으로 늦어진 다리야 알렉산드로브나가 흰 모슬린 가운을 입고 나와 올라탔다.\n\n다리야 알렉산드로브나는 머리를 매만지고 정성을 들여 들뜬 마음으로 차려입었다. 옛날에는 예뻐 보이고 칭찬을 받으려고 자기 자신을 위해 차려입었다. 그러다 나이가 들면서 차려입는 일이 점점 더 싫어졌다. 자신이 고운 용모를 잃어 가고 있음을 알았기 때문이다. 그러나 이제 그녀는 다시 차려입는 일에 기쁨과 흥미를 느끼기 시작했다. 이제는 자기 자신을 위해서가 아니라, 자기 미모를 위해서가 아니라, 단지 그 사랑스러운 아이들의 어머니로서 전체적인 효과를 망치지 않기 위해서 차려입었다. 그리고 마지막으로 거울에 자신을 비춰 보고 그녀는 만족했다. 보기 좋았다. 옛날 무도회에서 보이고 싶었던 식의 아름다움은 아니었지만, 이제 그녀가 마음에 둔 목적에는 보기 좋았다.\n\n교회에는 농부들과 하인들, 그리고 그 아낙네들 말고는 아무도 없었다. 그러나 다리야 알렉산드로브나는 자기 아이들과 자기가 불러일으킨 반향을 보았다고, 아니 보았다고 여겼다. 아이들은 맵시 있는 작은 옷차림이 보기에 아름다웠을 뿐 아니라 행동거지마저 매력적이었다. 알료샤가 자세를 제대로 못 잡은 것은 사실이었다. 그 애는 자기 짧은 윗도리를 뒤에서 보려고 자꾸만 몸을 돌렸다. 하지만 그래도 더없이 귀여웠다. 타냐는 어른처럼 행동하며 어린 동생들을 돌보았다. 그리고 가장 어린 릴리는 모든 것에 순진하게 놀라워하는 모습이 사람을 홀릴 듯했고, 성찬을 받고 나서 영어로 \"Please, some more.\"라고 말했을 때는 웃음을 참기가 어려웠다.\n\n집으로 돌아오는 길에 아이들은 무언가 엄숙한 일이 일어났다고 느껴 아주 점잖게 굴었다.\n\n집에서도 모든 일이 즐겁게 흘러갔다. 그런데 점심 식사 때 그리샤가 휘파람을 불기 시작했고, 더 나쁘게도 영국인 가정교사에게 말을 듣지 않아서 타르트를 먹지 못하게 되었다. 만일 다리야 알렉산드로브나가 그 자리에 있었더라면 그런 날에 일을 그렇게까지 몰고 가지는 않았을 것이다. 그러나 그녀는 영국인 가정교사의 권위를 받쳐 주어야 했기에, 그리샤에게 타르트를 주지 말라는 그녀의 결정을 지지했다. 이 일이 모두의 좋은 기분을 다소 망쳐 놓았다. 그리샤는 울면서 니콜린카도 휘파람을 불었는데 벌을 받지 않았다고, 자기는 타르트 때문에 우는 게 아니라고—그깟 건 아무래도 좋다고—다만 부당한 대우를 받아서 운다고 항변했다. 이건 정말이지 너무 비극적이어서, 다리야 알렉산드로브나는 영국인 가정교사를 설득해 그리샤를 용서하게 하기로 마음먹고 그녀에게 말하러 갔다. 그런데 가는 길에 응접실을 지나다가, 그녀는 어떤 광경을 보았고, 그 광경이 가슴을 기쁨으로 가득 채워 눈물이 고이게 했으며, 그녀 자신이 그 죄인을 용서해 버렸다.\n\n죄인은 응접실 구석 창가에 앉아 있었다. 그 곁에는 접시를 든 타냐가 서 있었다. 자기 인형들에게 밥을 먹이고 싶다는 핑계로 그 애는 자기 몫의 타르트를 아이 방으로 가져가게 해 달라고 가정교사의 허락을 받고는, 그 대신 그것을 오빠에게 가져온 것이었다. 그는 여전히 자기가 받은 벌의 부당함을 울면서, 타르트를 먹으며 흐느낌 사이로 자꾸만 말했다. \"너도 먹어, 같이 먹자……함께 먹자.\"\n\n타냐는 처음에는 그리샤에 대한 연민에 사로잡혔다가, 그다음에는 자기의 고결한 행동에 대한 감격에 젖었고, 그 애의 눈에도 눈물이 고였다. 하지만 거절하지 않고 자기 몫을 먹었다.\n\n어머니를 발견하자 아이들은 당황했지만, 그녀의 얼굴을 들여다보고는 자기들이 잘못하고 있는 게 아님을 알았다. 아이들은 웃음을 터뜨렸고, 타르트로 입이 가득 찬 채 미소 짓는 입술을 손으로 닦으며, 환한 얼굴에 눈물과 잼을 온통 칠해 버렸다.\n\n\"어쩜! 너희 새 흰 옷! 타냐! 그리샤!\" 어머니는 옷을 구하려 애쓰면서 말했지만, 눈에는 눈물이 고인 채, 더없이 행복하고 황홀한 미소를 짓고 있었다.\n\n새 옷을 벗겼고, 여자아이들에게는 블라우스를, 사내아이들에게는 낡은 윗도리를 입히라는 지시가 내려졌으며, 짐마차를 매라는 명령이 떨어졌다. 부르니를 다시 채에 매니 집사로서는 못마땅했지만, 버섯을 따고 멱을 감으러 나가기 위해서였다. 아이 방에서 기뻐 날뛰는 함성이 터져 나왔고, 멱 감는 곳을 향해 출발할 때까지 그칠 줄을 몰랐다.\n\n아이들은 한 바구니 가득 버섯을 땄다. 릴리마저 자작나무 버섯 하나를 찾아냈다. 전에는 늘 미스 훌이 버섯을 발견해 그 애에게 알려 주곤 했는데, 이번에는 그 애가 큼직한 것을 혼자서 찾아냈고, \"릴리가 버섯을 찾았다!\" 하는 환성이 다 함께 터져 나왔다.\n\n이윽고 그들은 강가에 이르러 자작나무 아래에 말을 매어 두고 멱 감는 곳으로 갔다. 마부 테렌티는 파리를 자꾸만 쫓아 대는 말들을 나무에 묶고는, 풀을 밟아 누이고 자작나무 그늘에 누워 싸구려 담배를 피웠다. 그동안 멱 감는 곳에서 그칠 줄 모르는 아이들의 환성이 그에게로 떠밀려 왔다.\n\n아이들을 모두 돌보고 그 거친 장난을 말리기란 고된 일이었고, 머릿속에 하나하나 새겨 두어 양말이며 작은 바지며 신발을 각기 다른 다리에 헷갈리지 않게 하고, 온갖 끈과 단추를 풀었다 다시 채우기도 어려운 일이었지만, 본디 멱 감기를 좋아했고 그것이 아이들에게 아주 좋다고 믿어 온 다리야 알렉산드로브나는 아이들 모두와 함께 멱을 감는 일만큼 즐거운 것이 없었다. 그 통통한 작은 다리들을 일일이 어루만지며 양말을 신기고, 그 작은 알몸들을 두 팔로 안아 물에 담그고, 기쁨과 놀라움이 섞인 비명을 듣고, 첨벙대는 천사 같은 아이들 모두의 크게 뜬 겁먹은 듯 행복한 눈과 숨 가쁜 얼굴을 보는 것이 그녀에게는 더없는 기쁨이었다.\n\n아이들 절반쯤이 옷을 입었을 때, 약초를 캐러 나온 명절 옷차림의 농가 아낙 몇이 멱 감는 헛간으로 다가와 수줍게 멈춰 섰다. 마리야 필리모노브나가 그중 하나를 불러 물에 빠졌던 시트와 셔츠를 건네며 말려 달라고 했고, 다리야 알렉산드로브나는 아낙들과 이야기를 나누기 시작했다. 처음에는 손으로 입을 가리고 웃기만 하며 그녀의 물음을 알아듣지 못했지만, 이내 대담해져서 말을 하기 시작했고, 아이들에 대해 보여 주는 진정 어린 감탄으로 단번에 다리야 알렉산드로브나의 마음을 사로잡았다.\n\n\"어머나, 정말 예쁘기도 해라! 설탕처럼 새하얗네.\" 한 아낙이 고개를 저으며 타니치카에게 감탄해서 말했다. \"그런데 말랐어……\"\n\n\"네, 그동안 아팠거든요.\"\n\n\"그래서 이 아기도 멱을 감기고 있구먼.\" 다른 아낙이 갓난아기를 보고 말했다.\n\n\"아니에요, 이 애는 이제 겨우 석 달밖에 안 됐어요.\" 다리야 알렉산드로브나가 자랑스럽게 대답했다.\n\n\"세상에!\"\n\n\"댁들도 아이가 있어요?\"\n\n\"넷을 낳았는데 둘이 살아 있어요. 사내애 하나, 계집애 하나죠. 지난 사육제 때 그 애 젖을 뗐어요.\"\n\n\"몇 살인데요?\"\n\n\"두 살이죠, 뭐.\"\n\n\"왜 그렇게 오래 젖을 먹였어요?\"\n\n\"우리네 풍습이 그래요. 세 번의 사순절 동안은……\"\n\n그리고 그 대화는 다리야 알렉산드로브나에게 더없이 흥미로워졌다. 출산 때는 어땠는가? 사내애는 무슨 병이 있었는가? 남편은 어디에 있는가? 그런 일이 자주 있는가?\n\n다리야 알렉산드로브나는 농가 아낙들 곁을 떠나기가 싫었다. 그들과의 대화가 그토록 흥미로웠고, 그들의 관심사가 자기 것과 완전히 똑같았기 때문이다. 무엇보다 그녀를 기쁘게 한 것은, 아낙들 모두가 다른 무엇보다 그녀가 그토록 많은, 그것도 그토록 잘난 아이들을 두었다는 사실을 감탄한다는 것을 똑똑히 보았다는 점이었다. 농가 아낙들은 다리야 알렉산드로브나를 웃게까지 만들었고, 영국인 가정교사를 언짢게 했는데, 가정교사로서는 알아듣지도 못하는 웃음의 원인이 자기였기 때문이다. 젊은 아낙 하나가 다른 사람들이 다 옷을 입은 뒤에야 옷을 입는 영국 여자를 빤히 쳐다보더니, 그 여자가 세 번째 속치마를 입자 한마디 하지 않을 수 없었다. \"어머나, 저이는 입고 또 입고 하니 도무지 끝이 없겠네!\" 하고 그녀가 말하자, 모두가 한바탕 웃음을 터뜨렸다.\n\n# Chapter 9\n\n다리야 알렉산드로브나가 아이들을 죄다 거느리고, 막 멱을 감고 나와 아직 머리가 젖은 채인 아이들과 함께, 자기 머리에는 수건을 동여매고 집 가까이에 다다랐을 때, 마부가 말했다. \"저기 어떤 나리가 오는데요, 아무래도 포크롭스코예의 주인 같습니다.\"\n\n다리야 알렉산드로브나가 앞쪽을 내다보다가, 회색 모자에 회색 외투를 걸치고 그들을 맞으러 걸어오는 레빈의 낯익은 모습을 알아보고는 기뻐했다. 그녀는 언제든 그를 보면 반가웠지만, 마침 이 순간에는 자신의 위풍당당한 모습을 그가 봐 주는 것이 유난히 기뻤다. 그녀의 위엄을 레빈만큼 잘 알아볼 수 있는 사람도 없었다.\n\n그녀를 본 레빈은, 자기가 가족과 함께하는 삶에 대해 그리던 백일몽의 한 장면과 정면으로 마주한 듯한 느낌이 들었다.\n\n\"꼭 병아리를 거느린 암탉 같으십니다, 다리야 알렉산드로브나.\"\n\n\"아, 정말 반가워요!\" 그녀가 그에게 손을 내밀며 말했다.\n\n\"저를 보고 반가워하시면서도, 제게 알려 주지는 않으셨군요. 제 형이 저와 함께 지내고 있습니다. 부인이 여기 와 계시다는 걸 스티바가 보낸 쪽지로 알았어요.\"\n\n\"스티바한테서요?\" 다리야 알렉산드로브나가 놀라서 물었다.\n\n\"네, 부인이 여기 계시고, 제가 부인께 뭔가 도움을 드려도 좋겠다고 생각한다고 적혀 있더군요.\" 레빈은 이렇게 말하다가 갑자기 당황스러워졌고, 말을 뚝 그치고는 보리수나무의 새순을 뜯어 잘근잘근 씹으면서 마차 옆을 말없이 걸어갔다. 그가 당황한 까닭은, 마땅히 남편에게서 받아야 할 도움을 외부 사람에게서 받게 된다면 다리야 알렉산드로브나가 언짢아하리라는 것을 느꼈기 때문이었다. 다리야 알렉산드로브나는 분명히, 자기 집안일을 남에게 떠넘기는 스테판 아르카지치의 이런 버릇을 좋아하지 않았다. 그리고 그녀는 레빈도 그 점을 알고 있다는 것을 곧바로 알아챘다. 바로 이 섬세한 통찰력, 이 세심함 때문에 다리야 알렉산드로브나는 레빈을 좋아했다.\n\n\"물론 압니다.\" 레빈이 말했다. \"그건 그저 부인이 저를 보고 싶어 하신다는 뜻일 뿐이고, 저는 정말이지 기쁩니다. 다만, 도시 살림에 익숙하신 분이니 이런 시골 한복판에서는 불편하실 거라고 짐작이 가고, 필요한 게 있으시면 저는 무엇이든 부인 뜻대로 하겠습니다.\"\n\n\"아, 아니에요!\" 돌리가 말했다. \"처음엔 좀 불편했지만, 지금은 모든 걸 아주 훌륭하게 자리 잡아 놨답니다. 우리 늙은 유모 덕분이지요.\" 그녀가 마리야 필리모노브나를 가리키며 말하자, 그녀는 자기 이야기를 하고 있다는 걸 알아채고는 레빈에게 환하고 다정하게 미소 지었다. 그녀는 레빈을 알고 있었고, 그가 자기 아가씨에게 좋은 짝이 되리라는 것도 알고 있었기에 그 일이 성사되기를 무척 바랐다.\n\n\"안 타시겠어요, 나리? 이쪽으로 자리를 만들어 드릴게요!\" 그녀가 그에게 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 걸어가겠소. 얘들아, 누가 나랑 말이랑 달리기 시합할 사람?\" 아이들은 레빈을 잘 알지 못했고 그를 언제 봤는지도 기억하지 못했지만, 아이들이 위선적인 어른들에게 흔히 느끼는, 그래서 그 일로 종종 비참하게 벌을 받곤 하는 그 묘한 수줍음이나 적대감 같은 것을 그에게는 조금도 느끼지 않았다. 무슨 일에서든 위선은 가장 영리하고 통찰력 있는 사람조차 속일 수 있지만, 아무리 둔한 아이라도 그것을 알아채고, 제아무리 교묘하게 꾸며 댄들 그것에 진저리를 친다. 레빈에게 어떤 결점이 있든, 그에게는 위선의 흔적이라곤 없었기에 아이들은 자기 어머니의 얼굴에서 본 것과 똑같은 친근함을 그에게 보였다. 그의 청에, 큰 두 아이가 곧장 그에게 뛰어내려, 유모나 미스 훌이나 어머니와 함께할 때처럼 거리낌 없이 그와 함께 달렸다. 릴리도 그에게 가겠다고 조르기 시작했고, 어머니가 그 애를 그에게 건네주었다. 그는 아이를 어깨에 앉히고 함께 달렸다.\n\n\"걱정 마세요, 걱정 마세요, 다리야 알렉산드로브나!\" 그가 어머니에게 사람 좋게 미소 지으며 말했다. \"이 애를 다치게 하거나 떨어뜨릴 일은 절대 없으니까요.\"\n\n그러고는 그의 힘차고 날렵하면서도 지나치다 싶을 만큼 조심스럽고 쓸데없이 신중한 동작을 바라보며 어머니는 마음이 놓였고, 그를 지켜보면서 즐겁게, 흐뭇하게 미소 지었다.\n\n이곳 시골에서, 아이들과 함께, 그리고 마음이 통하는 다리야 알렉산드로브나와 함께 있으니, 레빈은 그에게 드물지 않은, 그녀가 그에게서 특히 좋아하는 어린아이 같은 명랑한 기분에 젖어 있었다. 그는 아이들과 달리면서 체조 묘기를 가르쳐 주었고, 우스꽝스러운 영어 발음으로 미스 훌을 웃겼으며, 다리야 알렉산드로브나에게는 시골에서 하는 자기 일에 대해 이야기했다.\n\n저녁 식사 후, 다리야 알렉산드로브나는 발코니에서 그와 단둘이 앉아 키티 이야기를 꺼냈다.\n\n\"있잖아요, 키티가 이리로 와서 저랑 여름을 보내기로 했어요.\"\n\n\"정말입니까?\" 그가 얼굴을 붉히며 말하고는, 곧장 화제를 돌리려고 이렇게 말했다. \"그럼 젖소 두 마리를 보내 드릴까요? 굳이 값을 치르시겠다면 한 달에 5루블을 내시면 됩니다. 하지만 그건 정말 너무하신 거예요.\"\n\n\"아니에요, 고마워요. 지금은 아주 잘 꾸려 나가고 있는걸요.\"\n\n\"아, 그럼, 부인 댁 젖소들을 한번 살펴보겠습니다. 그리고 괜찮으시다면 그 녀석들 먹이에 대해 일러 드리지요. 모든 게 먹이에 달려 있거든요.\"\n\n그러고서 레빈은 화제를 돌리려고, 젖소란 그저 먹이를 우유로 바꿔 주는 기계일 뿐이라는 원리에 근거한 젖소 사육 이론 따위를 다리야 알렉산드로브나에게 설명했다.\n\n그는 이런 이야기를 하면서도, 키티에 대해 더 듣고 싶어 안달이 나면서도 동시에 그것을 듣게 될까 봐 두려웠다. 그는 그토록 애써서 얻어 낸 내면의 평화가 깨지는 것이 무서웠다.\n\n\"네, 하지만 그래도 이 모든 걸 다 돌봐야 하는데, 그걸 돌볼 사람이 어디 있어야지요?\" 다리야 알렉산드로브나가 무심하게 대꾸했다.\n\n그녀는 이제 마리야 필리모노브나 덕분에 집안일을 아주 만족스럽게 정돈해 놓은 터라, 거기에 어떤 변화도 주고 싶지 않았다. 게다가 그녀는 레빈의 농사 지식을 믿지 않았다. 젖소가 우유를 생산하는 기계라는 따위의 일반 원리를 그녀는 미심쩍게 여겼다. 그런 원리란 농장 운영에 방해만 될 뿐이라고 그녀에게는 여겨졌다. 그녀에게는 모든 것이 훨씬 더 단순한 일로 보였다. 마리야 필리모노브나가 설명했듯이, 필요한 것이라곤 브린들과 화이트브레스트에게 먹이와 물을 더 주는 것, 그리고 요리사가 부엌의 음식 찌꺼기를 죄다 세탁부의 젖소에게 갖다 주지 못하게 하는 것뿐이었다. 그건 분명한 일이었다. 하지만 곡물 사료니 풀이니 하는 먹이에 관한 일반론은 미덥지 못하고 모호했다. 그리고 무엇보다 중요한 건, 그녀가 키티 이야기를 하고 싶어 한다는 것이었다.\n\n# Chapter 10\n\n\"키티가 편지에 쓰기를, 자기가 무엇보다 간절히 바라는 건 고요함과 혼자 있는 거래요.\" 침묵이 흐른 뒤에 돌리가 말했다.\n\n\"그래서 그 애는 좀 어때요, 나아졌나요?\" 레빈이 마음 졸이며 물었다.\n\n\"고맙게도 이제는 아주 건강해졌어요. 나는 그 애 폐가 나쁘다는 말을 한 번도 믿은 적이 없었어요.\"\n\n\"오, 정말 기쁩니다!\" 레빈이 말했고, 그가 이렇게 말하며 말없이 자기 얼굴을 들여다보는 모습에서 돌리는 무언가 가슴을 찌르는 듯한, 어쩔 줄 모르는 기색을 본 것 같았다.\n\n\"한 가지 물어봐도 될까요, 콘스탄틴 드미트리치.\" 다리아 알렉산드로브나가 다정하면서도 조금은 놀리는 듯한 미소를 지으며 말했다. \"어째서 당신은 키티에게 화가 나 있는 거죠?\"\n\n\"제가요? 저는 그 애한테 화가 난 게 아닙니다.\" 레빈이 말했다.\n\n\"아니에요, 화가 나 있어요. 모스크바에 있었을 때 왜 우리 집에도, 그 애들 집에도 오지 않았던 거죠?\"\n\n\"다리아 알렉산드로브나.\" 그가 머리끝까지 새빨개지며 말했다. \"당신처럼 마음씨 고운 분이 이걸 느끼지 못한다는 게 정말 놀랍군요. 다른 건 몰라도, 당신이 알면서도 어떻게 저를 가엾게 여기지 않을 수가 있는지……\"\n\n\"내가 뭘 안다는 거죠?\"\n\n\"제가 청혼을 했고 거절당했다는 걸 당신은 알고 있잖습니까.\" 레빈이 말했고, 일 분 전까지 키티에게 느꼈던 온갖 애틋한 감정은 자기가 받은 모욕에 대한 분노로 바뀌어 버렸다.\n\n\"내가 안다고 어떻게 단정하는 거죠?\"\n\n\"누구나 다 아는 일이니까요……\"\n\n\"바로 거기서 당신이 잘못 알고 있는 거예요. 나는 그 일을 몰랐어요. 그런 일이 있었으리라고 짐작만 했을 뿐이지.\"\n\n\"그럼, 이제 아셨군요.\"\n\n\"내가 알았던 거라곤, 그 애를 끔찍이 비참하게 만든 무슨 일이 있었다는 것, 그리고 그 일에 대해서는 절대 입에 올리지 말아 달라고 나한테 부탁했다는 것뿐이에요. 나한테도 말하지 않으려 했으니, 다른 누구에게도 분명 말하지 않았을 거예요. 그런데 당신들 사이에 대체 무슨 일이 있었던 거죠? 말해 봐요.\"\n\n\"이미 말씀드렸습니다.\"\n\n\"그게 언제였죠?\"\n\n\"마지막으로 그 댁에 갔을 때였습니다.\"\n\n\"이거 아세요?\" 다리아 알렉산드로브나가 말했다. \"나는 그 애가 정말, 정말 가여워요. 당신은 그저 자존심 때문에 괴로워하는 것뿐이에요……\"\n\n\"아마 그럴지도 모르죠.\" 레빈이 말했다. \"하지만……\"\n\n그녀가 그의 말을 잘랐다.\n\n\"하지만 그 애는, 가엾은 것이…… 나는 그 애가 정말, 정말 가여워요. 이제야 모든 게 다 보이네요.\"\n\n\"자, 다리아 알렉산드로브나, 이만 실례하겠습니다.\" 그가 일어서며 말했다. \"안녕히 계세요, 다리아 알렉산드로브나, 다음에 또 뵙죠.\"\n\n\"아니, 잠깐만요.\" 그녀가 그의 소매를 붙잡으며 말했다. \"잠깐만, 앉아요.\"\n\n\"제발, 제발 부탁이니 이 얘기는 그만하시죠.\" 그가 자리에 앉으며 말했다. 그러면서 동시에 묻혀 버렸다고 믿었던 어떤 희망이 가슴속에서 솟아올라 꿈틀거리는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n\"내가 당신을 좋아하지 않았더라면,\" 그녀가 말했고 눈에는 눈물이 차올랐다. \"내가 지금처럼 당신을 잘 알지 못했더라면……\"\n\n죽은 듯했던 그 감정이 점점 더 되살아나 솟구치더니 레빈의 가슴을 온통 사로잡았다.\n\n\"그래요, 이제 모든 게 다 이해돼요.\" 다리아 알렉산드로브나가 말했다. \"당신은 그걸 이해할 수 없어요. 자유로운 몸으로 스스로 선택하는 당신네 남자들에게는, 자기가 누구를 사랑하는지 늘 분명하니까요. 하지만 여자는 마음을 졸이며 기다리는 처지예요. 여자다운, 처녀다운 온갖 수줍음을 안고서, 당신네 남자들을 멀찍이서 바라보고, 모든 걸 그대로 믿어 버리는 처녀로서—처녀는 무어라 말해야 할지 알 수 없는 그런 감정을 품을 수도 있고, 또 흔히 품기도 하지요.\"\n\n\"그렇죠, 마음이 말을 하지 않는다면……\"\n\n\"아니에요, 마음은 말을 해요. 하지만 한번 생각해 봐요. 당신네 남자들은 어떤 처녀를 두고 마음을 정하고, 그 집에 드나들고, 친해지고, 따져 보고, 자기가 사랑하는 상대를 찾았는지 두고 보다가, 그러고 나서 그 애를 사랑한다고 확신이 서면 청혼을 하잖아요……\"\n\n\"뭐, 꼭 그렇지만은 않습니다.\"\n\n\"어쨌든 당신들은 사랑이 무르익었을 때, 아니면 저울질하던 두 사람 사이에서 마음이 완전히 한쪽으로 기울었을 때 청혼을 해요. 하지만 처녀에게는 아무도 물어봐 주지 않아요. 처녀더러 스스로 선택하기를 바라면서도, 정작 처녀는 선택할 수가 없고, 그저 '예'나 '아니오'로 답할 수밖에 없지요.\"\n\n'그래, 나와 브론스키 사이에서 고르라는 거지.' 레빈은 생각했고, 그의 안에서 되살아났던 죽은 것이 다시 죽어 버려, 그저 가슴을 짓누르며 쓰라리게 할 뿐이었다.\n\n\"다리아 알렉산드로브나,\" 그가 말했다. \"그건 새 드레스나 무슨 물건을 고르는 방식이지, 사랑은 아닙니다. 선택은 이미 끝났고, 그러는 편이 차라리 나아요…… 다시 되풀이될 수도 없는 일이고요.\"\n\n\"아아, 자존심, 그놈의 자존심!\" 다리아 알렉산드로브나는 오직 여자만이 아는 저 다른 감정에 비하면 이런 감정의 천박함이 한심하다는 듯이 말했다. \"당신이 키티에게 청혼했을 때, 그 애는 마침 대답을 할 수 없는 처지에 있었어요. 망설이고 있었던 거죠. 당신과 브론스키 사이에서요. 그 사람은 날마다 보고 있었고, 당신은 오랫동안 보지 못했으니까요. 그 애가 좀 더 나이가 들었더라면…… 가령 나라면, 그 애 입장에서 아무런 망설임도 느끼지 않았을 거예요. 나는 그 사람을 늘 싫어했고, 결국 이렇게 드러난 거잖아요.\"\n\n레빈은 키티의 대답을 떠올렸다. 그녀는 이렇게 말했었다. \"아니에요, 그건 있을 수 없는 일이에요……\"\n\n\"다리아 알렉산드로브나,\" 그가 무뚝뚝하게 말했다. \"저를 믿어 주시는 마음은 고맙습니다만, 제가 보기엔 당신이 잘못 알고 계신 겁니다. 하지만 제가 옳든 그르든, 당신이 그토록 한심해하는 그 자존심 때문에 카체리나 알렉산드로브나에 대한 어떤 생각도 저로서는 도무지 가당치 않은 일이 되어 버렸습니다—아시겠습니까, 완전히 가당치 않은 일이라고요.\"\n\n\"한 가지만 더 말할게요. 내가 지금 말하고 있는 건 내 친동생, 내 자식들만큼이나 사랑하는 그 애에 관한 얘기라는 걸 알아주세요. 그 애가 당신을 마음에 두고 있었다고 말하려는 게 아니에요. 내가 하고 싶은 말은 그저, 그때 그 애가 당신을 거절했다는 것이 아무것도 증명하지 못한다는 거예요.\"\n\n\"모르겠습니다!\" 레빈이 벌떡 일어나며 말했다. \"당신이 저를 얼마나 아프게 하고 있는지 아신다면. 이건 마치 당신의 아이가 죽었는데, 사람들이 당신한테 이렇게 말하는 것과 똑같아요. 그 아이는 이렇게도 저렇게도 자랐을 거고, 살아 있었을 수도 있고, 그러면 당신이 그 애로 인해 얼마나 행복했겠느냐고요. 하지만 그 애는 죽었어요, 죽었다고요, 죽어 버렸단 말입니다!……\"\n\n\"당신은 어쩜 그리 엉뚱한지!\" 다리아 알렉산드로브나가 레빈의 흥분한 모습을 애틋하면서도 서글픈 눈길로 바라보며 말했다. \"그래요, 점점 더 모든 게 또렷이 보이네요.\" 그녀가 생각에 잠겨 말을 이었다. \"그러니까 키티가 와 있을 때는 우리 집에 오지 않겠다는 거죠?\"\n\n\"네, 안 오겠습니다. 물론 카체리나 알렉산드로브나와 마주치는 걸 피하겠다는 건 아닙니다만, 할 수 있는 한, 제 존재로 그 애를 불편하게 만드는 일은 없도록 애쓰겠습니다.\"\n\n\"당신은 정말, 정말 엉뚱한 사람이에요.\" 다리아 알렉산드로브나가 그의 얼굴을 애틋하게 들여다보며 거듭 말했다. \"그럼 좋아요, 우리가 이 얘기를 꺼내지 않은 셈 칠게요. 무슨 일이니, 타냐?\" 그녀가 방으로 들어온 어린 딸에게 프랑스어로 말했다.\n\n\"엄마, 내 삽 어디 있어요?\"\n\n\"엄마는 프랑스어로 말하고 있잖니, 너도 그래야지.\"\n\n어린 딸은 프랑스어로 말해 보려 했지만 삽이 프랑스어로 뭔지 떠올리지 못했다. 어머니가 일러 주었고, 이어서 어디서 삽을 찾으면 되는지 프랑스어로 알려 주었다. 그리고 이것이 레빈에게 거북한 인상을 남겼다.\n\n이제 다리아 알렉산드로브나의 집이며 아이들 안의 모든 것이, 조금 전과는 달리 도무지 사랑스럽게 느껴지지 않았다. '대체 왜 아이들과 프랑스어로 말하는 걸까?' 그는 생각했다. '얼마나 부자연스럽고 거짓된 일인가! 아이들도 그걸 느끼고 있어. 프랑스어를 익히면서 진실함을 잃어 가는 거지.' 그는 속으로 이렇게 생각했지만, 다리아 알렉산드로브나가 이미 그 모든 걸 스무 번도 넘게 곱씹어 보았다는 것, 그리고 진실함을 다소 잃는 대가를 치르더라도 자기 아이들에게 그런 식으로 프랑스어를 가르치는 게 필요하다고 믿고 있다는 것은 알지 못했다.\n\n\"그런데 왜 가시려고요? 좀 더 계세요.\"\n\n레빈은 차를 마시려고 남았다. 하지만 그의 좋던 기분은 사라져 버렸고, 마음이 영 편치 않았다.\n\n차를 마신 뒤 그는 말을 매어 두라고 일러 두려고 현관으로 나갔다가, 돌아와 보니 다리아 알렉산드로브나가 몹시 심란한 얼굴로, 근심에 찬 표정에 눈에는 눈물이 고인 채 있었다. 레빈이 밖에 나가 있는 동안, 그날 하루 그녀가 느끼던 행복과 아이들에 대한 자부심을 송두리째 산산조각 내 버린 사건이 일어났던 것이다. 그리샤와 타냐가 공을 두고 싸웠다. 다리아 알렉산드로브나는 아이들 방에서 비명 소리를 듣고 달려갔다가 끔찍한 광경을 보았다. 타냐는 그리샤의 머리채를 잡아당기고 있었고, 그리샤는 분노로 일그러진 흉측한 얼굴로 닥치는 대로 주먹질을 해 대고 있었다. 이 광경을 본 순간 다리아 알렉산드로브나의 가슴속에서 무언가가 툭 끊어졌다. 마치 그녀의 삶 위로 어둠이 덮쳐 내린 것 같았다. 자기가 그토록 자랑스러워하던 이 아이들이 그저 더없이 평범한 정도가 아니라, 거칠고 잔인한 성정을 지닌, 영락없이 못되고 버르장머리 없는 아이들—심술궂은 아이들이라는 생각이 들었던 것이다.\n\n그녀는 다른 어떤 것도 입에 올리거나 생각할 수가 없었고, 자기의 비참한 심정을 레빈에게 털어놓을 수도 없었다.\n\n레빈은 그녀가 괴로워하는 것을 보고 위로하려 애쓰며, 그건 나쁜 게 아니라고, 아이들은 다 싸운다고 말했다. 하지만 그렇게 말하면서도 속으로는 이렇게 생각하고 있었다. '아니야, 나는 가식적으로 굴며 내 아이들과 프랑스어로 얘기하지는 않을 거야. 하지만 내 아이들은 저렇지 않을 거야. 그저 아이들을 망쳐 놓지 않고, 그 천성을 비뚤어지게 하지만 않으면, 아이들은 사랑스러워질 테니까. 그래, 내 아이들은 저렇지 않을 거야.'\n\n그는 작별 인사를 하고 마차를 몰아 떠났고, 그녀는 그를 붙잡으려 하지 않았다.\n\n# Chapter 11\n\n7월 중순, 포크로프스코예에서 십오 베르스타쯤 떨어진 레빈의 누이 영지의 촌장이 그곳 형편과 건초에 관해 보고하러 레빈을 찾아왔다. 누이 영지의 주된 수입원은 강가의 풀밭이었다. 예전에는 농부들이 삼 데샤티나당 이십 루블에 건초를 사들였다. 레빈이 영지 관리를 맡게 되었을 때, 그는 초지를 살펴보고 그것이 그 이상의 값어치가 있다고 판단하여 삼 데샤티나당 이십오 루블로 값을 매겼다. 농부들은 그 값을 치르려 하지 않았고, 레빈이 짐작한 대로 다른 매수인들이 접근하지 못하게 막았다. 그러자 레빈은 손수 마차를 몰고 가, 일부는 품팔이 일꾼을 써서, 일부는 수확량의 일정 몫을 품삯으로 치르는 방식으로 풀을 베도록 손을 써 두었다. 그의 농부들은 이 새로운 방식을 두고 갖은 방해를 다 부렸지만, 그래도 그 일은 이루어졌고, 첫해에 풀밭은 거의 두 배에 가까운 이익을 냈다. 지난해—그러니까 세 번째 해—에도 농부들은 그 방식에 똑같이 반대했고, 건초는 같은 방식으로 베어졌다. 올해 농부들은 건초 수확량의 삼분의 일을 받는 조건으로 풀베기를 도맡았고, 촌장이 이제 와서 건초를 다 베었으며, 비가 올까 봐 회계 사무소의 서기를 불러다가 그가 보는 앞에서 수확물을 나누었고, 주인 몫으로 열한 더미를 긁어모아 쌓아 두었다고 알리러 온 것이었다. 주된 풀밭에서 건초를 얼마나 베었느냐는 물음에 대한 모호한 대답에서, 허락도 구하지 않고 분배를 해 버린 촌장의 서두르는 태도에서, 그 농부의 말투 전체에서, 레빈은 건초 분배에 무언가 미심쩍은 구석이 있음을 알아채고는 손수 마차를 몰고 가 그 일을 들여다보기로 마음먹었다.\n\n마침 만찬 시간에 맞춰 마을에 다다른 레빈은, 옛 친구이자 형의 유모 남편인 노인의 오두막에 말을 맡겨 두고, 건초에 관한 진실을 캐낼 작정으로 그 노인을 만나러 양봉장으로 갔다. 말 많고 잘생긴 노인 파르메니치는 레빈을 무척 따뜻하게 맞이하며 자기가 하는 일을 죄다 보여 주었고, 자기 벌들과 그해의 분봉에 관해 온갖 이야기를 들려주었다. 그러나 풀베기에 관한 레빈의 물음에는 모호하고 마지못한 대답만 했다. 이것이 레빈의 의심을 한층 더 굳혀 주었다. 그는 건초밭으로 가 더미들을 살펴보았다. 건초 더미가 한 더미당 쉰 짐을 담고 있을 리가 없었다. 농부들의 거짓을 밝혀내기 위해 레빈은 건초를 실어 나른 짐수레들을 곧장 끌고 오게 해서, 더미 하나를 들어 올려 곳간으로 실어 나르라고 일렀다. 그 더미에는 서른두 짐밖에 들어 있지 않은 것으로 드러났다. 건초가 잘 눌리고 더미 속에서 가라앉는 법이라는 촌장의 주장에도, 모든 일을 하느님을 두려워하는 마음으로 했다는 맹세에도 아랑곳없이, 레빈은 건초가 자기 지시 없이 분배되었으며 따라서 그 건초를 한 더미당 쉰 짐으로는 인정할 수 없다는 자기 주장을 굽히지 않았다. 한참 옥신각신한 끝에, 농부들이 그 열한 더미를 한 더미당 쉰 짐으로 쳐서 가져가는 것으로 일은 매듭지어졌다. 옥신각신과 건초 더미 분배는 오후 내내 이어졌다. 마지막 건초까지 다 나누어지자, 레빈은 나머지 감독을 회계 사무소 서기에게 맡기고는 버드나무 말뚝으로 표시해 둔 건초 더미 위에 앉아, 농부들로 북적이는 풀밭을 흐뭇하게 바라보았다.\n\n그의 앞쪽, 늪 너머 강이 굽이지는 곳에는 알록달록한 농촌 아낙네들의 행렬이 움직이고 있었고, 흩어져 있던 건초는 연초록 그루터기 위로 잿빛으로 구불구불 이어지는 줄로 빠르게 모아지고 있었다. 아낙네들 뒤로는 쇠스랑을 든 남자들이 따랐고, 그 잿빛 줄에서 넓고 높고 부드러운 건초 더미가 자라났다. 왼편에서는 이미 치워진 풀밭 위로 짐수레들이 덜컹거리며 지나갔고, 건초 더미들은 거대한 쇠스랑 가득 퍼올려져 하나둘 사라졌으며, 그 자리에는 향긋한 건초를 잔뜩 실어 말들의 엉덩이 위로 늘어뜨린 묵직한 짐수레들이 솟아오르고 있었다.\n\n\"건초 말리기에 딱 좋은 날씨군! 이게 무슨 건초람!\" 레빈 곁에 쪼그려 앉으며 한 노인이 말했다. \"이건 건초가 아니라 차라니까! 사람들이 거두어들이는 꼴이 꼭 오리한테 곡식 흩뿌려 주는 것 같구먼!\" 그는 자라나는 건초 더미들을 가리키며 덧붙였다. \"만찬 때부터 벌써 절반 넘게 실어 날랐다오.\"\n\n\"저게 마지막 짐인가?\" 그는 빈 수레 앞쪽에 서서 새끼줄 고삐를 흔들며 지나가는 젊은 농부에게 소리쳤다.\n\n\"마지막 짐이에요, 아버지!\" 젊은이가 말을 멈춰 세우며 마주 소리치고는, 빙긋 웃으며 수레에 앉아 역시 웃고 있는 발그레한 뺨의 싱그러운 처녀를 돌아보고는 다시 몰고 갔다.\n\n\"누군가? 자네 아들인가?\" 레빈이 물었다.\n\n\"제 막내랍니다.\" 노인이 다정한 미소를 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"참 훌륭한 청년이군!\"\n\n\"그 녀석, 괜찮은 놈이지요.\"\n\n\"벌써 장가들었나?\"\n\n\"예, 지난 성 필립 축일로 두 해째랍니다.\"\n\n\"아이는 있고?\"\n\n\"아이라뇨! 아, 그 녀석은 일 년이 넘도록 제가 갓난애처럼 숫총각인 데다 부끄럼까지 타던걸요.\" 노인이 대답했다. \"원, 이 건초 좀 보시오! 차처럼 향긋하다니까!\" 그는 화제를 바꾸고 싶어 거듭 말했다.\n\n레빈은 이반 파르메노프와 그의 아내를 좀 더 유심히 바라보았다. 두 사람은 레빈에게서 멀지 않은 곳에서 건초 더미를 수레에 싣고 있었다. 이반 파르메노프는 수레 위에 서서, 예쁘고 젊은 아내가 처음에는 한 아름씩, 그다음에는 쇠스랑으로 능숙하게 건네 올리는 커다란 건초 다발을 받아 제자리에 놓고 밟아 다졌다. 젊은 아내는 가뿐하고 흥겹고 야무지게 일했다. 단단히 뭉친 건초는 그녀의 쇠스랑에서 한 번도 흘러내리지 않았다. 그녀는 먼저 건초를 그러모은 다음 그 안에 쇠스랑을 꽂고, 빠르고 나긋한 동작으로 온몸의 무게를 거기에 실었다가, 곧바로 붉은 허리띠 아래 등을 굽혀 몸을 일으키고는, 흰 작업복 아래 풍만한 가슴을 활처럼 내밀며 날렵하게 몸을 틀어 두 팔로 쇠스랑을 휘둘러 건초 다발을 수레 위로 높이 던져 올렸다. 이반은 아내가 쓸데없이 힘쓰는 순간을 단 일 분이라도 덜어 주려고 분명 애를 쓰면서, 다발을 붙잡아 수레에 놓으려고 두 팔을 벌린 채 서둘렀다. 남은 건초를 긁어모으던 젊은 아내는 목덜미에 떨어진 건초 부스러기를 털어 내고는, 햇볕에 그을린 얼굴과는 달리 햇볕을 타지 않은 흰 이마 위로 흘러내린 붉은 머릿수건을 바로잡고서, 짐을 묶으려고 수레 밑으로 기어들어 갔다. 이반은 가로대에 새끼줄을 어떻게 매면 되는지 일러 주었고, 그녀가 뭐라고 한마디 하자 큰 소리로 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 두 사람의 얼굴 표정에는 힘차고 젊고 갓 깨어난 사랑이 어려 있었다.\n* 베르스타: 러시아의 거리 단위로 약 1.06킬로미터. * 데샤티나: 러시아의 토지 면적 단위로 약 1.09헥타르.\n\n# Chapter 12\n\n짐이 다 묶였다. 이반이 뛰어내려 얌전하고 윤기 흐르는 말을 고삐로 잡았다. 젊은 아내는 갈퀴를 짐 위로 휙 던져 올리고는, 두 팔을 휘저으며 당찬 걸음으로 건초 일꾼들의 춤판을 만들려고 둥글게 모여드는 여인들 쪽으로 갔다. 이반은 길로 마차를 몰고 나가 짐을 실은 다른 수레들과 줄을 맞추었다. 여인들은 갈퀴를 어깨에 메고 화사한 꽃으로 곱게 꾸민 채, 낭랑하고 흥겨운 목소리로 재잘대며 건초 수레 뒤를 따라 걸었다. 거칠고 다듬어지지 않은 한 여자의 목소리가 노래를 터뜨려 한 소절을 혼자 부르고 나자, 거칠고 고운, 온갖 종류의 건강한 목소리 쉰여 개가 그 같은 소절을 받아 한목소리로 되풀이해 불렀다.\n\n여인들은 모두 노래를 부르며 레빈에게 가까이 다가오기 시작했고, 그는 마치 흥겨움의 천둥소리를 몰고 폭풍이 자기에게 덮쳐 오는 것 같은 기분이 들었다. 폭풍이 덮쳐 와 그와 그가 누워 있던 건초 더미를, 그리고 다른 건초 더미들을, 짐을 실은 수레들을, 풀밭 전체와 멀리 떨어진 들판까지를 온통 감쌌고, 모든 것이 고함과 휘파람과 박수가 어우러진 이 거칠고 흥겨운 노래의 가락에 맞추어 흔들리며 노래하는 듯했다. 레빈은 이 건강함과 즐거움이 부러웠다. 그는 이 삶의 기쁨을 표현하는 데에 끼고 싶었다. 그러나 그는 아무것도 할 수 없었고, 그저 누워서 바라보고 귀를 기울일 수밖에 없었다. 노래하는 농부들이 시야와 귓전에서 사라지고 나자, 자기만 따로 떨어져 있다는 것, 자기 몸이 아무 일도 하지 않고 있다는 것, 자기가 이 세계로부터 동떨어져 있다는 것에 대한 지친 낙담의 감정이 레빈에게 밀려들었다.\n\n건초 문제를 놓고 그와 가장 극성스럽게 옥신각신했던 바로 그 농부들 가운데 몇몇, 그가 모욕적으로 대했던 몇몇, 그를 속이려 들었던 몇몇, 바로 그 농부들이 그를 흔쾌히 맞아 주었고, 그들에게는 그에 대한 어떤 원한도, 어떤 후회도, 그를 속이려 했다는 기억조차도 분명 없었으며 또 있을 수가 없었다. 그 모든 것이 흥겨운 공동 노동의 바다 속에 잠겨 버린 것이다. 신이 하루를 주었고, 신이 힘을 주었다. 그리고 그 하루와 그 힘은 노동에 바쳐졌으며, 그 노동 자체가 그 보상이었다. 누구를 위한 노동인가? 그것의 결실은 무엇이 될 것인가? 이런 것은 부질없는 생각이었고, 요점에서 벗어난 것이었다.\n\n레빈은 종종 이런 삶을 동경했고, 종종 이런 삶을 사는 이들에게 부러움을 느꼈다. 그러나 오늘 처음으로, 특히 이반 파르메노프가 자기 젊은 아내를 대하던 태도에서 본 것의 영향으로, 자기가 살고 있는 따분하고 인위적이며 게으르고 개인주의적인 삶을 이 노동으로 가득 차고 순수하며 함께 어울려 즐거운 삶과 맞바꾸는 것이 자기 능력 안에 있다는 생각이 또렷이 그의 머릿속에 떠올랐다.\n\n그의 곁에 앉아 있던 노인은 이미 오래전에 집으로 돌아갔다. 사람들은 모두 흩어졌다. 가까이 사는 이들은 집으로 갔고, 멀리서 온 이들은 저녁을 먹고 풀밭에서 밤을 지내려고 한 무리로 모여 있었다. 레빈은 농부들 눈에 띄지 않은 채 여전히 건초 더미 위에 누워, 여전히 바라보고 귀 기울이며 사색에 잠겨 있었다. 풀밭에서 밤을 지내는 농부들은 짧은 여름밤 내내 거의 잠을 이루지 못했다. 처음에는 저녁을 먹으며 다 함께 흥겹게 이야기하고 웃는 소리가 들렸고, 그다음에는 다시 노래와 웃음이 이어졌다.\n\n고된 노동으로 가득했던 그 긴 하루는 그들에게 마음의 가벼움 말고는 아무런 흔적도 남기지 않았다. 이른 새벽이 오기 전 모든 것이 고요해졌다. 늪에서 끊임없이 울어 대는 개구리들의 밤 소리와, 아침을 앞두고 풀밭 위로 피어오르는 안개 속에서 말들이 콧김을 내뿜는 소리 말고는 아무것도 들리지 않았다. 레빈은 정신을 차리고 건초 더미에서 일어나 별들을 바라보다가, 밤이 다 지났음을 알았다.\n\n\"자, 나는 어떻게 해야 하지? 어떻게 손을 대야 하나?\" 그는 그 짧은 밤 동안 거쳐 온 모든 생각과 감정을 스스로에게 표현해 보려 애쓰며 혼잣말을 했다. 그가 거쳐 온 모든 생각과 감정은 세 갈래의 서로 다른 사고의 흐름으로 나뉘었다. 하나는 옛 삶에 대한, 자기의 더할 나위 없이 쓸모없는 교육에 대한 단념이었다. 이 단념은 그에게 만족감을 주었고, 쉽고도 단순했다. 또 다른 갈래의 생각과 마음속 이미지들은 이제 그가 살고 싶어 하는 삶과 관련된 것이었다. 이 삶의 단순함과 순수함과 건전함을 그는 또렷이 느꼈고, 자기가 그토록 비참하게 결핍을 의식하고 있던 그 만족과 평안과 존엄을 그 안에서 찾을 수 있으리라고 확신했다. 그러나 세 번째 갈래의 생각은 옛 삶에서 새 삶으로의 이 이행을 어떻게 이루어 낼 것인가 하는 문제로 돌아갔다. 그리고 거기에서는 아무것도 그에게 또렷한 형태를 갖추지 못했다. \"아내를 얻을까? 일을, 그리고 일을 해야 할 필연을 가질까? 포크롭스코예를 떠날까? 땅을 살까? 농민 공동체의 일원이 될까? 농촌 처녀와 결혼할까? 어떻게 손을 대야 하지?\" 그는 다시 스스로에게 물었지만, 답을 찾을 수 없었다. \"그래도 밤새 한숨도 못 잤으니, 또렷하게 생각해 낼 수가 없군.\" 그는 혼잣말을 했다. \"나중에 풀어 봐야지. 한 가지는 분명해, 이 밤이 내 운명을 결정했다는 것. 가정생활에 대한 내 옛 꿈은 모두 터무니없었어, 진짜가 아니었지.\" 그는 스스로에게 말했다. \"전부 훨씬 더 단순하고 더 나은 거야……\"\n\n\"어찌 이리 아름다운가!\" 그는 하늘 한복판 바로 머리 위에 떠 있는, 어쩐지 진주조개 껍데기 같은 희고 보송보송한 작은 구름들을 바라보며 생각했다. \"이 황홀한 밤에 모든 것이 어찌 이토록 황홀한가! 그런데 저 구름 껍데기가 만들어질 틈이 언제 있었던 거지? 방금만 해도 하늘을 보았을 때는 아무것도 없었는데—그저 흰 줄기 두 가닥뿐이었지. 그래, 내 삶에 대한 생각도 이렇게 알아채지 못하는 사이에 바뀐 거야!\"\n\n그는 풀밭을 나와 마을 쪽으로 한길을 따라 걸었다. 가벼운 바람이 일었고, 하늘은 잿빛으로 음울해 보였다. 흔히 동틀 녘, 빛이 어둠을 완전히 이기기 직전에 찾아오는 그 음울한 순간이 온 것이다.\n\n추위에 몸을 움츠리며 레빈은 땅을 내려다본 채 빠르게 걸었다. \"저게 뭐지? 누가 오는군.\" 그는 방울 소리를 알아채고 고개를 들며 생각했다. 그에게서 마흔 걸음쯤 떨어진 곳에서, 말 네 마리를 나란히 맨 마차 한 대가 그가 걷고 있던 풀이 난 길을 따라 그를 향해 달려오고 있었다. 끌채에 맨 말들은 바큇자국 때문에 끌채 쪽으로 기우뚱했지만, 마부석에 앉은 솜씨 좋은 마부가 끌채를 바큇자국 위로 들어 올려, 바퀴가 길의 평평한 부분으로 굴러가게 했다.\n\n레빈이 알아챈 것은 이것뿐이었고, 그것이 누구일까 궁금해하지도 않은 채 그는 멍하니 마차를 바라보았다.\n\n마차 안에는 한쪽 구석에서 졸고 있는 노부인이 있었고, 창가에는 분명 막 잠에서 깬 듯한 젊은 처녀가 흰 모자의 리본을 두 손으로 잡고 앉아 있었다. 빛과 생각으로 가득하고, 레빈에게서는 멀리 떨어진 어떤 섬세하고 복잡한 내면의 삶으로 가득한 얼굴로, 그녀는 그를 지나쳐 동트는 노을의 빛을 바라보고 있었다.\n\n이 환영이 막 사라지려는 바로 그 순간, 그 진실한 눈이 그를 흘끗 보았다. 그녀는 그를 알아보았고, 그녀의 얼굴은 놀라움 어린 기쁨으로 환해졌다.\n\n그가 잘못 보았을 리 없었다. 세상에 그런 눈은 또 없었다. 세상에 그를 위해 삶의 모든 빛과 의미를 하나로 모을 수 있는 존재는 오직 하나뿐이었다. 그녀였다. 키티였다. 그는 그녀가 기차역에서 예르구쇼보로 가는 길임을 알아차렸다. 그리고 그 잠 못 이룬 밤 내내 레빈을 뒤흔들던 모든 것, 그가 세웠던 모든 결심이 순식간에 사라져 버렸다. 그는 농촌 처녀와 결혼하겠다던 자기 꿈을 끔찍한 심정으로 떠올렸다. 오직 그곳, 길 반대편으로 건너가 빠르게 사라져 가는 저 마차 안에서만, 오직 그곳에서만 그는 최근 자기를 그토록 고통스럽게 짓눌러 온 자기 삶의 수수께끼에 대한 해답을 찾을 수 있었다.\n\n그녀는 다시 내다보지 않았다. 마차 용수철 소리는 더 이상 들리지 않았고, 방울 소리도 거의 들리지 않았다. 개 짖는 소리로 마차가 마을에 다다랐음을 알 수 있었고, 남은 것이라고는 사방의 텅 빈 들판과, 앞쪽의 마을과, 그 모든 것에서 외따로 떨어져 인적 없는 한길을 홀로 헤매는 그 자신뿐이었다.\n\n그는 하늘을 흘끗 올려다보며, 거기에서 자기가 감탄하며 그 밤의 생각과 감정의 상징으로 삼았던 그 구름 껍데기를 찾으려 했다. 하늘에는 껍데기 비슷한 것이라곤 조금도 없었다. 저 멀리 높은 곳에서는 신비로운 변화가 이루어진 뒤였다. 껍데기의 흔적은 없었고, 하늘의 족히 절반에 걸쳐 점점 더 자잘해지는 작은 구름들이 고르게 덮여 펼쳐져 있었다. 하늘은 푸르고 환하게 변해 있었다. 그리고 그 똑같은 부드러움으로, 그러나 그 똑같은 아득함으로, 하늘은 묻고 있는 그의 시선을 맞이했다.\n\n\"아니야.\" 그는 혼잣말을 했다. \"그 단순함과 노동의 삶이 아무리 좋다 한들, 나는 그리로 돌아갈 수 없어. 나는 그녀를 사랑하니까.\"\n\n# Chapter 13\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치와 가장 가까운 사람들만이 알고 있었다. 겉으로는 더없이 냉정하고 이성적인 사내였지만, 그에게는 그의 전반적인 성격과는 정반대되는 약점이 하나 있다는 사실을. 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 아이나 여자가 우는 것을 듣거나 보면 마음이 흔들리지 않을 수 없었다. 눈물을 보면 그는 신경이 곤두서는 동요 상태에 빠졌고, 사고하는 능력을 완전히 잃어버렸다. 그가 속한 부처의 수석 비서관과 개인 비서는 이 사실을 알고 있어서, 청원을 가지고 찾아오는 여자들에게 일을 망치고 싶지 않거든 절대로 눈물을 흘려서는 안 된다고 미리 일러두곤 했다. \"그분은 화를 내실 거고, 당신 말을 들어주지 않으실 겁니다.\" 그들은 이렇게 말하곤 했다. 그리고 사실, 그런 경우에 눈물을 보고 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치 안에서 일어나는 감정적 동요는 성급한 분노로 표출되곤 했다. \"내가 해 줄 수 있는 일이 없소. 그만 방에서 나가 주시오!\" 그런 경우에 그는 흔히 이렇게 소리치곤 했다.\n\n경마장에서 돌아오는 길에 안나가 자기와 브론스키의 관계를 그에게 털어놓고는, 곧바로 두 손에 얼굴을 묻은 채 울음을 터뜨렸을 때, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 그녀를 향해 끓어오르는 온갖 분노에도 불구하고 동시에 눈물이 언제나 그의 안에 불러일으키는 그 감정적 동요가 밀려드는 것을 느꼈다. 그것을 의식하고, 또 그 순간에 자기 감정을 어떤 식으로든 드러내는 것이 이 상황에 어울리지 않으리라는 것을 의식한 그는, 자기 안에서 생명의 모든 표현을 억누르려 애썼고, 그래서 몸을 움직이지도 그녀를 쳐다보지도 않았다. 안나에게 그토록 깊은 인상을 남겼던, 죽은 사람처럼 굳어 버린 그 기이한 표정이 그의 얼굴에 떠오른 것은 바로 이 때문이었다.\n\n집에 다다르자 그는 그녀가 마차에서 내리는 것을 도왔고, 스스로를 다잡으려 애쓰며 평소와 같은 정중함으로 그녀에게 작별을 고했다. 그리고 자신을 아무것에도 얽매지 않는 그 문구를 입에 올렸다. 내일 자기 결정을 알려 주겠노라고 그는 말했다.\n\n최악의 의심을 확인시켜 준 아내의 말은 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 가슴에 잔인한 통증을 안겨 주었다. 그 통증은 그녀의 눈물이 불러일으킨, 그녀에 대한 기묘한 육체적 연민 때문에 한층 심해졌다. 그러나 마차 안에 혼자 남게 되자 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 놀랍고도 기쁘게도, 이 연민에서도, 또 질투의 의혹과 고뇌에서도 완전히 벗어나는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n그는 오랫동안 치통에 시달리다 마침내 이를 뽑아낸 사람과 같은 느낌을 경험했다. 무시무시한 고통과, 머리통 자체보다도 더 큰 무언가가 턱에서 뜯겨 나오는 듯한 느낌을 겪고 난 뒤, 환자는 자기에게 찾아온 행운을 좀처럼 믿지 못하다가, 그토록 오랫동안 자기 삶을 좀먹고 주의를 옭아매던 것이 더 이상 존재하지 않으며, 이제 다시 살아갈 수도 생각할 수도 있고, 자기 이 말고 다른 것들에도 관심을 가질 수 있다는 것을 문득 깨닫는다. 바로 이 느낌을 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 경험하고 있었다. 고통은 기이하고 끔찍했지만, 이제 끝났다. 그는 다시 살아갈 수 있고, 아내가 아닌 다른 무언가를 생각할 수 있다는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n\"명예도 없고, 마음도 없고, 신앙도 없는, 타락한 여자야. 나는 늘 그것을 알고 있었고 늘 보고 있었어. 그 여자를 봐주려고 나 자신을 속이려 애쓰긴 했지만.\" 그는 혼잣말을 했다. 그리고 정말로 그에게는 자기가 늘 그것을 보아 왔던 것처럼 여겨졌다. 그는 지난 삶의 사건들을 떠올렸는데, 전에는 그 안에서 아무런 잘못된 점도 보지 못했던 것들이었다. 그런데 이제 그 사건들은 그녀가 늘 타락한 여자였음을 분명히 증명해 주었다. \"내 삶을 그 여자의 삶에 엮은 것은 실수였어. 하지만 내 실수에 잘못된 것은 없으니, 나는 불행해질 수 없어. 비난받아야 할 사람은 내가 아니라,\" 그는 스스로에게 말했다, \"그 여자야. 하지만 나는 그 여자와 아무 상관이 없어. 그 여자는 나에게 더 이상 존재하지 않아…….\"\n\n그녀와 관련된 모든 것, 그리고 그녀에 대한 만큼이나 감정이 변해 버린 그녀의 아들과 관련된 모든 것이 그의 흥미를 끌지 못하게 되었다. 이제 그의 관심을 끄는 유일한 것은, 어떤 방식으로 하면 자기에게 가장 타당하고 가장 마음 편하게, 그리하여 가장 정당하게, 그녀가 자기 추락 속에서 그에게 튀긴 진창에서 빠져나와 능동적이고 명예롭고 유익한 삶의 길을 따라 다시 나아갈 수 있을까 하는 문제였다.\n\n\"경멸스러운 여자가 죄를 저질렀다고 해서 내가 불행해질 수는 없어. 나는 그 여자가 나를 몰아넣은 이 어려운 처지에서 벗어날 최선의 방법을 찾기만 하면 돼. 그리고 나는 그것을 찾아낼 거야.\" 그는 점점 더 미간을 찌푸리며 스스로에게 말했다. \"내가 처음도 아니고 마지막도 아니지.\" 그리고 최근 모든 이의 기억 속에 되살아난, 메넬라오스의 '아름다운 헬레네'로 거슬러 올라가는 역사적 사례들은 말할 것도 없고, 상류 사회에서 부정한 아내를 둔 남편들의 현대적 사례들이 줄줄이 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 상상 앞에 떠올랐다. \"다랴로프, 폴타프스키, 카리바노프 공작, 파스쿠딘 백작, 드람…… 그래, 드람마저도, 그토록 정직하고 유능한 친구가…… 세묘노프, 차긴, 시고닌.\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 이렇게 떠올렸다. \"어떤 도무지 이치에 맞지 않는 조롱이 이런 남자들의 몫이 된다는 것은 인정하지만, 그래도 나는 거기서 불행 말고 다른 것을 본 적이 없고, 늘 그것에 동정을 느껴 왔어.\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 스스로에게 말했지만, 실은 그것은 사실이 아니었다. 그는 그런 종류의 불행에 동정을 느낀 적이 한 번도 없었고, 오히려 부정한 아내가 남편을 배신한 사례를 자주 들으면 들을수록 자기 자신을 더 높이 평가해 왔다. \"그것은 누구에게나 닥칠 수 있는 불행이야. 그리고 그 불행이 나에게 닥친 거지. 할 일은 단 하나, 이 처지에서 최선을 끌어내는 것뿐이야.\"\n* '아름다운 헬레네': 그리스 신화에서 스파르타 왕 메넬라오스의 아내로, 트로이의 왕자 파리스와 달아나 트로이 전쟁의 원인이 되었다.\n\n그리고 그는 자기와 같은 처지에 놓였던 남자들이 취한 대처 방식들을 차례로 검토하기 시작했다.\n\n\"다랴로프는 결투를 했지…….\"\n\n결투는 특히 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 젊은 시절에 그의 생각을 사로잡았는데, 다름 아니라 그가 육체적으로 겁쟁이였고, 또 그 사실을 스스로 잘 알고 있었기 때문이다. 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 자기를 겨눈 권총을 떠올리기만 해도 공포에 사로잡혔고, 평생 어떤 무기도 다뤄 본 적이 없었다. 이 공포는 젊은 시절 그를 결투에 대해 깊이 생각하게 만들었고, 자기 목숨을 위험에 내맡겨야 하는 처지에 놓인 자기 모습을 상상하게 만들었다. 세상에서 성공과 확고한 지위를 얻은 그는 오래전에 이 감정을 잊어버렸다. 그러나 습관처럼 굳어진 감정의 성향이 다시 고개를 들었고, 자기 자신의 비겁함에 대한 두려움이 지금조차도 어찌나 강했던지, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 결투의 문제를 온갖 측면에서 한참 동안 곱씹으며 결투라는 생각을 끌어안았다. 비록 어떤 상황에서도 자기는 결코 결투 같은 것을 하지 않으리라는 것을 미리 충분히 알고 있었으면서도 말이다.\n\n\"우리 사회가 아직도 너무나 야만적이어서(영국에서는 그렇지 않지) 아주 많은 이들이\"—그리고 그 가운데에는 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 특히 그 견해를 중히 여기는 사람들도 있었다—\"결투를 호의적으로 본다는 데에는 의심의 여지가 없어. 하지만 그것으로 무슨 결과를 얻는단 말인가? 가령 내가 그를 불러낸다 치자.\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 혼자 생각을 이어 가다가, 도전장을 던진 뒤에 보내게 될 밤과 자기를 겨눈 권총을 생생하게 떠올리고는 몸서리를 쳤고, 자기는 결코 그러지 못하리라는 것을 알았다. \"가령 내가 그를 불러낸다 치자. 가령 내가 사격을 배운다 치자.\" 그는 계속 생각에 잠겼다. \"내가 방아쇠를 당긴다.\" 그는 눈을 감으며 스스로에게 말했다. \"그리고 결국 내가 그를 죽이게 된다.\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 스스로에게 말하고는, 그런 어리석은 생각을 떨쳐 버리려는 듯 고개를 저었다. \"죄지은 아내와 아들에 대한 내 관계를 규정하기 위해 한 사람을 죽이는 것에 무슨 의미가 있단 말인가? 그렇게 해도 나는 여전히 그 여자를 어떻게 해야 할지 똑같이 결정해야 할 거야. 하지만 더 있음직한 일, 틀림없이 일어날 일은—내가 죽거나 다치는 거지. 죄 없는 내가 희생자가 되는 거야—죽거나 다치는 거지. 그건 더더욱 무의미해. 그뿐 아니라, 결투를 신청하는 것은 내 쪽에서 보면 도무지 정직하지 못한 행동일 거야. 내 친구들이 결코 나에게 결투를 허락하지 않으리라는 것을—러시아에 필요한 정치가의 목숨을 위험에 내맡기는 것을 결코 허락하지 않으리라는 것을 내가 완벽히 알고 있지 않은가? 결국 이 일이 실제 위험으로까지 가지 않으리라는 것을 미리 완벽히 알면서, 그런 도전으로 어떤 거짓 명성이나 얻으려 드는 꼴이 될 거야. 그건 정직하지 못한 일이고, 거짓된 일이며, 나 자신과 남들을 속이는 일이지. 결투는 도무지 이치에 맞지 않고, 아무도 나에게 그것을 기대하지 않아. 내 목표는 그저 내 명성을 지키는 것뿐인데, 그것은 내 공적인 임무를 중단 없이 수행하는 데에 꼭 필요한 거야.\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 눈에 늘 대단히 중요했던 공직의 임무가, 이 순간 그의 마음에는 각별히 중요하게 여겨졌다. 결투를 검토하고 물리친 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 이혼으로 눈길을 돌렸는데, 그것은 그가 기억하는 남편들 가운데 몇몇이 택한 또 다른 해결책이었다. 자기가 아는 이혼 사례를 모조리 머릿속으로 검토해 보았지만(그가 아주 잘 아는 바로 그 최상류 사회에는 그런 사례가 수두룩했다), 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 이혼의 목적이 자기가 염두에 둔 것과 같은 사례를 단 하나도 찾을 수 없었다. 그 모든 사례에서 남편은 사실상 부정한 아내를 양보하거나 팔아넘긴 셈이었고, 잘못을 저질러 새 결혼을 맺을 권리가 없는 바로 그 당사자가, 자칭 남편이라는 자와 가짜의, 결혼을 흉내 낸 관계를 맺고 있었다. 자기 경우에는, 법적인 이혼, 다시 말해 죄지은 아내만을 내치는 이혼은 이루어질 수 없다는 것을 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 알았다. 그들이 영위하는 삶의 복잡한 조건들 때문에 법이 요구하는 아내의 죄에 대한 노골적인 증거를 대는 것은 어림도 없다는 것을 그는 알았다. 그런 삶에 깃든 어떤 세련됨이, 설령 그에게 증거가 있다 한들 그런 증거를 들이대는 것을 용납하지 않으리라는 것을, 그리고 그런 증거를 들이대는 것은 그녀보다 오히려 그를 세간의 평가에서 더 해치리라는 것을 그는 알았다.\n\n이혼을 시도해 봤자 세간의 추문 말고는 아무것도 얻을 게 없을 터였고, 그것은 사교계에서 그의 높은 지위를 헐뜯고 공격하려는 그의 적들에게 더없이 좋은 먹잇감이 될 것이었다. 가능한 한 가장 적은 동요로 처지를 규정한다는 그의 주된 목표도 이혼으로는 이루어지지 않을 터였다. 게다가 이혼할 경우, 아니 이혼을 시도하기만 해도, 아내가 남편과 모든 관계를 끊고 정부와 운명을 함께하리라는 것은 뻔한 일이었다. 그리고 그가 생각하기에 지금 아내에게 느끼는 완전한 경멸과 무관심에도 불구하고, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 마음 깊은 곳에는 그녀에 대한 감정이 하나 남아 있었다—그녀가 자유롭게 브론스키와 운명을 함께하여 그녀의 죄가 그녀에게 득이 되는 꼴을 보고 싶지 않다는 것이었다. 이 생각만으로도 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 어찌나 격분했던지, 그 생각이 머릿속에 떠오르기가 무섭게 그는 속에서 끓어오르는 고통에 신음하며 자리에서 일어나 마차 안에서 자리를 옮겼고, 그 후로도 한참 동안 미간을 찌푸린 채 마비된 앙상한 두 다리를 보드라운 무릎 덮개로 감싸고 앉아 있었다.\n\n\"정식 이혼이 아니더라도, 카리바노프나 파스쿠딘, 그리고 그 선량한 친구 드람처럼 할 수도 있겠지—그러니까 아내와 별거하는 거야.\" 그는 다시 평정을 되찾자 계속 생각해 나갔다. 그러나 이 방안도 이혼과 마찬가지로 세간의 추문이라는 똑같은 결점을 안고 있었고, 더욱이 별거도 정식 이혼과 다름없이 아내를 브론스키의 품으로 던져 넣는 것이었다. \"안 돼, 그건 말도 안 돼, 말도 안 되는 일이야!\" 그는 또다시 무릎 덮개를 몸에 둘둘 감으며 거듭 말했다. \"나는 불행해질 수 없지만, 그 여자도 그자도 행복해서는 안 돼.\"\n\n불확실한 시기 내내 그를 괴롭히던 질투의 감정은, 아내의 말로 인해 그 이가 고통스럽게 뽑혀 나간 순간 사라져 버렸다. 그러나 그 감정은 또 다른 감정으로 대체되었으니, 그녀가 의기양양하지 못하게 하는 것은 물론이고, 그녀가 자기 죄에 합당한 벌을 받아야 한다는 욕구였다. 그는 이 감정을 인정하지는 않았지만, 마음 깊은 곳에서는 그녀가 자기 마음의 평화를—자기 명예를 파괴한 대가로 고통받기를 갈망했다. 그리고 결투, 이혼, 별거에서 떼어 낼 수 없는 조건들을 다시 한번 하나하나 짚어 보고, 다시 한번 그것들을 물리친 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 해결책이 단 하나뿐이라는 것을 확신하게 되었다—그녀를 자기 곁에 붙들어 두고, 일어난 일을 세상으로부터 감추며, 자기 능력이 미치는 모든 수단을 동원해 그 밀회를 끊어 버리고, 게다가—비록 이것은 스스로에게 인정하지 않았지만—그녀를 벌하는 것이었다. \"그 여자가 가족을 몰아넣은 이 끔찍한 처지를 곰곰이 생각해 보건대, 다른 어떤 해결책도 겉으로 드러나는 현 상태를 유지하는 것보다 양쪽 모두에게 더 나쁘리라는 것이 내 결론이며, 따라서 나는 그 여자가 내 뜻에 복종한다는, 다시 말해 정부와의 모든 교제를 끊는다는 엄격한 조건 아래 그 상태를 유지하는 데 동의한다고 그 여자에게 알려야겠어.\" 이 결정이 마침내 채택되자, 그것을 뒷받침하는 또 하나의 묵직한 고려 사항이 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에게 떠올랐다. \"이런 방침을 통해서만 나는 종교의 가르침에 따라 행동하는 것이 될 거야.\" 그는 스스로에게 말했다. \"이 방침을 택함으로써 나는 죄지은 아내를 내치는 것이 아니라, 그 여자에게 바로잡을 기회를 주는 것이고, 게다가 나에게 아무리 힘든 과업일지라도, 나는 내 힘의 일부를 그 여자의 갱생과 구원에 바칠 거야.\"\n\n비록 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 자기가 아내에게 어떤 도덕적 영향력도 행사할 수 없다는 것을, 그런 갱생의 시도가 거짓 말고는 아무것도 낳지 못하리라는 것을 완벽히 알고 있었지만, 또 이 힘겨운 순간들을 지나오는 동안 종교에서 인도를 구할 생각은 단 한 번도 하지 않았지만, 그래도 지금, 자기 결론이 그가 보기에 종교의 요구와 부합하게 되자, 자기 결정에 대한 이 종교적 승인은 그에게 완전한 만족감을 주었고, 어느 정도는 마음의 평화를 되찾아 주었다. 그는 삶의 이토록 중대한 위기에서조차, 그가 전반적인 냉담과 무관심 속에서도 늘 그 깃발을 높이 치켜들어 온 그 종교의 원칙에 따라 자기가 행동하지 않았다고 그 누구도 말할 수 없으리라는 생각에 흐뭇했다. 이후의 전개를 곰곰이 생각해 보며, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 실로 어째서 아내와 자기의 관계가 사실상 예전과 똑같이 유지되어서는 안 되는지 그 까닭을 알 수 없었다. 물론 그녀가 다시는 그의 존경을 되찾을 수 없겠지만, 그녀가 나쁘고 부정한 아내라는 이유로 그의 삶이 어지럽혀지고 그가 고통받아야 할 까닭은 어떤 것도 없었고, 또 있을 수도 없었다. \"그래, 시간이 흐를 거야. 모든 것을 정돈하는 시간이 흐르면 옛 관계가 다시 세워질 거야.\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 스스로에게 말했다. \"다시 세워진다는 건, 그러니까 내 삶의 연속성에 균열이 생긴 것을 내가 느끼지 못할 정도로 말이지. 그 여자는 불행할 수밖에 없겠지만, 나는 잘못이 없으니, 나는 불행해질 수 없어.\"\n\n# Chapter 14\n\n페테르부르크에 가까워질수록 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 자신의 결정을 한 치도 흩트리지 않았을 뿐 아니라, 아내에게 쓸 편지를 머릿속으로 짓고 있기까지 했다. 수위실로 들어선 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 관청에서 가져온 편지와 서류들을 한번 훑어보고는, 그것들을 서재로 가져오라고 일렀다.\n\n\"말은 풀어 두게, 그리고 누구도 만나지 않겠네.\" 그는 흡족한 기분을 드러내는 어떤 즐거움을 담아 수위에게 대답하면서, \"누구도 만나지 않겠네\"라는 말에 힘을 주었다.\n\n서재에서 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 두어 번 왔다 갔다 하다가, 먼저 와 있던 시종이 벌써 여섯 자루의 양초를 켜 둔 거대한 책상 앞에 멈춰 섰다. 그는 손가락 마디를 뚝뚝 꺾고 나서 자리에 앉아 필기도구들을 정리했다. 두 팔꿈치를 책상에 괴고 고개를 한쪽으로 기울인 채 잠시 생각하더니, 한순간도 멈추지 않고 쓰기 시작했다. 그는 아내를 부르는 어떤 호칭도 쓰지 않고, 러시아어의 해당 형식만큼 차갑게 들리지 않는 복수형 vous를 사용하여 프랑스어로 써 내려갔다.\n* vous: 프랑스어로 '당신'에 해당하는 존칭·복수형 인칭대명사.\n\n\"우리가 마지막으로 나눈 대화에서, 나는 그 대화의 주제에 관한 내 결정을 당신에게 알리겠다는 뜻을 밝혔소. 모든 것을 신중하게 고려한 끝에, 그 약속을 이행하고자 지금 이 편지를 쓰는 것이오. 내 결정은 다음과 같소. 당신의 행실이 어떠했든 간에, 나는 우리를 더 높은 권능으로 맺어 준 인연을 끊을 권리가 내게 있다고 보지 않소. 가정이라는 것은 변덕이나 일시적 기분, 심지어 부부 중 한 사람의 죄로 인해 깨질 수 있는 것이 아니며, 우리의 생활은 지난날과 다름없이 계속되어야만 하오. 이것은 나를 위해서도, 당신을 위해서도, 그리고 우리 아들을 위해서도 반드시 필요한 일이오. 나는 당신이 이 편지를 쓰게 만든 일을 뉘우쳤고 또 지금도 뉘우치고 있으리라고, 그리고 우리가 멀어진 원인을 뿌리 뽑고 과거를 잊는 데 나와 함께 협력하리라고 굳게 믿소. 만일 그렇지 않을 경우, 당신과 당신 아들에게 무엇이 닥칠지 짐작할 수 있을 것이오. 이 모든 것에 대해서는 직접 만나 더 자세히 논의하기를 바라오. 계절이 끝나 가고 있으니, 되도록 빨리, 늦어도 화요일까지는 페테르부르크로 돌아와 주기를 청하오. 당신이 이곳에 도착하는 데 필요한 모든 준비는 갖추어 둘 것이오. 이 요청을 따르는 일에 내가 특별한 의미를 두고 있음을 명심해 주기 바라오.\n\nA. 카레닌\n\n추신—당신의 비용에 필요할 돈을 동봉하오.\"\n\n그는 편지를 처음부터 끝까지 읽고는 흡족함을 느꼈고, 특히 돈을 동봉할 것을 잊지 않고 떠올렸다는 점이 마음에 들었다. 그 안에는 모진 말 한마디도, 비난 한 줄도 없었고, 그렇다고 지나친 관용도 없었다. 무엇보다도 그것은 돌아올 수 있는 황금 다리였다. 편지를 접어 묵직한 상아 칼로 매끈하게 누른 뒤 돈과 함께 봉투에 넣고, 그는 잘 정돈된 책상의 비품들을 사용할 때면 늘 느끼는 만족감에 젖어 종을 울렸다.\n\n\"이것을 전령에게 주어 내일 안나 아르카지예브나에게 별장으로 전달하도록 하게.\" 그는 일어서며 말했다.\n\n\"알겠습니다, 각하. 차는 서재로 가져올까요?\"\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 차를 서재로 가져오라고 분부하고는, 묵직한 종이칼을 만지작거리며 안락의자 쪽으로 옮겨 갔다. 그 곁에는 등불과, 그가 읽기 시작한 이집트 상형문자에 관한 프랑스 책이 미리 준비되어 있었다. 안락의자 위쪽에는 금테 액자에 든 안나의 타원형 초상화가 걸려 있었는데, 어느 이름난 화가가 그린 훌륭한 그림이었다. 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 그것을 한번 바라보았다. 헤아릴 수 없는 두 눈이 비웃듯이, 그리고 뻔뻔하게 그를 응시하고 있었다. 화가가 솜씨 좋게 살려 낸 머리 둘레의 검은 레이스며, 검은 머리카락이며, 반지를 낀 손가락 하나를 치켜든 아름다운 흰 손—그 모든 것이 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 눈에는 참을 수 없을 만큼 뻔뻔하고 도전적으로 비쳤다. 잠시 초상화를 바라보던 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 입술이 떨릴 만큼 몸서리를 치며 \"브르르\" 하는 소리를 내고는 고개를 돌렸다. 그는 서둘러 안락의자에 앉아 책을 펼쳤다. 읽어 보려 했지만, 전에 이집트 상형문자에 대해 느꼈던 그 생생한 흥미를 되살릴 수가 없었다. 그는 책을 들여다보면서도 다른 무언가를 생각하고 있었다. 아내를 생각한 것이 아니라, 당시 그의 공직 생활에서 가장 중요한 관심사를 이루던, 거기서 불거진 어떤 복잡한 사안을 떠올렸다. 그는 이 얽히고설킨 문제를 전에 없이 깊이 파고들었다고 느꼈고, 또—자화자찬 없이 말할 수 있건대—이 모든 일을 명료하게 정리하고, 자신의 관직 경력을 굳건히 하고, 정적들을 당혹스럽게 만들며, 그리하여 정부에 더없이 큰 이익을 가져다줄 만한 하나의 주도적인 착상을 떠올렸다고 느꼈다. 하인이 차를 차려 놓고 방을 나가기가 무섭게, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 일어나 책상으로 갔다. 흡족한 자기만족의 미소를 좀처럼 알아채기 어려울 만큼 희미하게 띤 채, 그는 서류철을 책상 한가운데로 옮겨 놓고, 필통에서 연필 한 자루를 꺼내 들고는 지금의 복잡한 사안과 관련된 까다로운 보고서를 읽는 데 몰두했다. 그 복잡한 사안의 성격은 이러했다. 정치가로서 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 지닌 특유의 자질, 출세하는 관리라면 누구나 갖추는 그 독특한 개인적 자격, 즉 식을 줄 모르는 야심과 과묵함과 정직함, 그리고 자신감과 더불어 그의 경력을 쌓아 올린 그 자격은, 형식적 관료주의에 대한 경멸, 서신 왕래를 줄이는 태도, 가능한 한 살아 있는 사실과 직접 접촉하려는 자세, 그리고 절약 정신이었다. 마침 그 유명한 6월 2일 위원회가 자라이스크 현의 토지 관개에 관한 조사에 착수했는데, 이 문제는 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 부서 소관이었고, 헛된 지출과 서류상의 개혁을 보여 주는 노골적인 본보기였다. 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 그것이 사실임을 알고 있었다. 자라이스크 현의 이 토지 관개 사업은 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 전임자의 전임자가 시작한 것이었다. 그리고 실제로 막대한 돈이 이 사업에 쓰였고 지금도 쓰이고 있었지만, 전혀 아무런 성과도 없었으며, 이 사업 전체가 결국 아무런 결실도 맺지 못하리라는 것은 명백했다. 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 부임하자마자 이 점을 간파했고, 관개위원회에 손을 대고 싶어 했다. 그러나 처음에는, 아직 자기 자리가 확고하다고 느끼지 못하던 무렵이라, 그 일이 너무 많은 이해관계에 영향을 미치게 되어 경솔한 처사가 되리라는 것을 알았다. 그 후로는 다른 문제들에 골몰하느라 관개위원회를 그냥 잊어버리고 말았다. 그것은 그런 류의 모든 위원회가 그렇듯, 단지 타성의 힘만으로 저절로 굴러갔다. (많은 사람이 관개위원회 덕에 생계를 꾸렸는데, 특히 대단히 양심적이고 음악을 좋아하는 한 가족이 그러했다. 그 집 딸들은 모두 현악기를 다루었고, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 그 가족을 알고 있었으며 맏딸 가운데 하나의 대부 노릇을 맡기도 했다.) 적대적인 부서가 이 문제를 들고나온 것은, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 보기에 떳떳치 못한 처사였다. 어느 부서에나 이와 비슷하거나 더한 일들이 있건만, 관료 사회의 잘 알려진 예법상의 이유로 아무도 그런 것을 캐묻지 않았기 때문이다. 그러나 이제 자신에게 도전장이 던져진 이상, 그는 그것을 대담하게 집어 들어, 자라이스크 현 토지 관개위원회의 운영을 조사하고 검증할 특별위원회의 설치를 요구했다. 다만 그 대신 적에게도 한 치의 인정을 베풀지 않았다. 그는 토착민족 조직 위원회 문제를 조사할 또 다른 특별위원회의 설치를 요구했다. 토착민족 문제는 6월 2일 위원회에서 우연히 거론되었던 것으로, 토착민족들의 개탄스러운 처지를 이유로 한시도 지체할 수 없는 사안이라 하여 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 적극적으로 밀어붙였던 것이다. 위원회에서 이 문제는 여러 부서 사이의 다툼거리가 되었다. 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에게 적대적인 부서는, 토착민족들의 처지가 더없이 번창하고 있으며, 제안된 개편이 도리어 그들의 번영을 망쳐 놓을 수 있고, 만일 무언가 잘못된 점이 있다면 그것은 주로 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 부서가 법으로 정해진 조치들을 시행하지 못한 데서 비롯된 것이라고 주장했다. 이제 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 다음과 같은 것을 요구할 작정이었다. 첫째, 토착민족들의 처지를 현지에서 조사할 권한을 가진 새 위원회를 구성할 것. 둘째, 만일 토착민족들의 처지가 위원회가 손에 쥔 공문서에 나타난 그대로임이 밝혀진다면, 토착민족들의 개탄스러운 처지를 (1) 정치적, (2) 행정적, (3) 경제적, (4) 민족지학적, (5) 물질적, (6) 종교적 관점에서 조사할 또 다른 새 학술위원회를 임명할 것. 셋째, 토착민족들이 지금 처한 비참한 상황을 막기 위해 지난 십 년 동안 경쟁 부서가 취해 온 조치들에 관한 증거를 그 부서에 요구할 것. 그리고 넷째이자 마지막으로, 위원회 앞에 제출된 증거, 즉 1863년 12월 5일자 제17,015호와 1864년 6월 7일자 제18,038호에 나타난 바와 같이, 어째서 그 부서가 법령 T… 제18조 및 제36조 비고의 취지에 정면으로 어긋나는 행동을 했는지를 그 부서가 해명하도록 할 것. 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 이런 착상들의 요지를 자기 자신을 위해 빠르게 적어 내려가는 동안, 그의 얼굴에 열의의 빛이 번졌다. 종이 한 장을 가득 채우고 나서 그는 일어나 종을 울리고는, 필요한 몇 가지 사실을 찾아봐 달라는 쪽지를 부서의 수석 비서에게 보냈다. 일어나 방 안을 거닐던 그는 초상화를 다시 한번 바라보고는 눈살을 찌푸리며 경멸하듯 미소 지었다. 이집트 상형문자에 관한 책을 조금 더 읽으며 그 흥미를 되살린 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 열한 시에 잠자리에 들었고, 침대에 누운 채 아내와 있었던 일을 떠올려 보니, 이제는 그것이 결코 그렇게까지 음울하게는 여겨지지 않았다.\n\n# Chapter 15\n\n안나는 브론스키가 둘의 처지가 견딜 수 없는 것이라고 말했을 때, 짜증을 내며 고집스럽게 반박했지만, 마음 깊은 곳에서는 자기 처지가 거짓되고 떳떳하지 못하다고 여기고 있었으며, 온 영혼을 다해 그것을 바꾸고 싶어 했다. 경마장에서 돌아오는 길에 그녀는 격앙된 순간에 남편에게 진실을 털어놓았고, 그렇게 하면서 겪은 고통에도 불구하고 그것을 잘했다고 여겼다. 남편이 그녀를 떠난 뒤, 그녀는 자신에게 잘된 일이라고, 이제 모든 것이 분명해졌으며 적어도 더 이상 거짓과 속임은 없을 거라고 말했다. 이제 자기 처지가 영원히 분명해졌다는 것은 그녀에게 의심의 여지가 없어 보였다. 이 새로운 처지가 나쁠지는 몰라도 분명할 것이며, 거기에는 불확실함이나 거짓이 없을 것이었다. 그 말들을 입에 올리면서 자신과 남편에게 안겨 준 고통은, 이제 모든 것이 분명해짐으로써 보상받으리라고 그녀는 생각했다. 그날 저녁 그녀는 브론스키를 만났지만, 처지를 분명히 하려면 그에게 말해야 했음에도, 자기와 남편 사이에 있었던 일을 그에게 말하지 않았다.\n\n이튿날 아침 잠에서 깼을 때 가장 먼저 그녀의 머릿속에 떠오른 것은 남편에게 했던 말이었고, 그 말들이 어찌나 끔찍해 보였던지 그녀는 이제 어떻게 자기가 그런 이상하고 거친 말을 입에 올릴 수 있었는지 도무지 이해할 수 없었으며, 그것이 어떤 결과를 가져올지 상상조차 할 수 없었다. 하지만 말은 이미 뱉어졌고, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 아무 말 없이 떠나 버렸다. '나는 브론스키를 만나고도 그에게 말하지 않았어. 그가 떠나려던 바로 그 순간 그를 돌려세워 말하려고 했지만, 마음을 바꾸었지. 처음 그 순간에 말하지 않은 게 이상했으니까. 왜 말하고 싶었으면서도 말하지 않은 거지?' 그리고 이 물음에 대한 대답으로 부끄러움에 타오르는 홍조가 그녀의 얼굴에 번졌다. 그녀는 무엇이 자기를 가로막았는지 알고 있었다. 자기가 부끄러웠다는 것을 알고 있었다. 전날 밤만 해도 단순해 보였던 그녀의 처지가, 이제 갑자기 단순하기는커녕 완전히 절망적인 것으로 다가왔다. 그녀는 그전까지 한 번도 생각해 본 적 없는 치욕에 겁이 났다. 남편이 어떻게 나올지 생각하자마자, 가장 끔찍한 상상들이 그녀의 머릿속에 떠올랐다. 그녀는 집에서 쫓겨나고, 자기 수치가 온 세상에 공표되는 환영을 보았다. 집에서 쫓겨나면 어디로 가야 하나 자문해 보았지만, 답을 찾을 수 없었다.\n\n브론스키를 떠올리자, 그가 자기를 사랑하지 않으며, 벌써 자기에게 싫증을 내기 시작했고, 자기는 그에게 스스로를 내맡길 수도 없다는 생각이 들었고, 그래서 그녀는 그에게 쓰라린 원망을 품었다. 남편에게 했던, 그리고 상상 속에서 끊임없이 되풀이했던 그 말들을, 자기가 모든 사람에게 한 것 같았고, 모든 사람이 그것을 들은 것만 같았다. 그녀는 차마 집안 식구들의 얼굴을 똑바로 볼 수 없었다. 차마 하녀를 부를 수도 없었고, 아래층으로 내려가 아들과 그의 가정교사를 보는 것은 더더욱 할 수 없었다.\n\n오랫동안 그녀의 방문 앞에서 엿듣고 있던 하녀가 제 발로 방에 들어왔다. 안나는 캐묻듯이 그녀의 얼굴을 흘끗 보고는 겁먹은 표정으로 얼굴을 붉혔다. 하녀는 종이 울린 줄 알았다며 들어온 것을 용서해 달라고 했다. 그녀는 안나의 옷가지와 쪽지 하나를 가져왔다. 쪽지는 벳시에게서 온 것이었다. 벳시는 그날 아침 리자 메르칼로바와 시톨츠 남작 부인이 자기네 숭배자인 칼루시스키, 그리고 노인 스트레모프와 함께 크로케를 하러 그녀에게 오기로 했다는 것을 일깨워 주었다. \"그저 풍속 연구 삼아서라도 오세요. 기다리고 있을게요.\" 그녀는 그렇게 끝맺었다.\n\n안나는 쪽지를 읽고 깊은 한숨을 내쉬었다.\n\n\"아무것도, 아무것도 필요 없어.\" 화장대 위의 작은 병들과 솔들을 정리하고 있던 안누시카에게 그녀가 말했다. \"가도 돼. 곧 옷을 갈아입고 내려갈 테니까. 아무것도 필요 없어.\"\n\n안누시카는 나갔지만, 안나는 옷을 갈아입기 시작하지 않은 채, 머리와 두 손을 맥없이 늘어뜨리고 똑같은 자세로 앉아 있었으며, 이따금 온몸을 부르르 떨면서 무슨 몸짓이라도 하고 무슨 말이라도 할 듯하다가, 다시 생기 없는 상태로 가라앉곤 했다. 그녀는 끊임없이 \"오, 하느님! 하느님!\" 하고 되뇌었다. 그러나 '하느님'도 '나의'도 그녀에게는 아무런 의미가 없었다. 자기 곤경에서 종교에 도움을 구한다는 생각은, 비록 자라면서 받아들인 신앙을 한 번도 의심한 적이 없었음에도, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치 본인에게 도움을 구하는 것만큼이나 그녀에게서 멀었다. 그녀는 종교의 떠받침이라는 것이 오로지 자기에게 삶의 의미 전부를 이루는 것을 포기한다는 조건에서만 가능하다는 것을 알고 있었다. 그녀는 단지 비참한 것만이 아니라, 한 번도 겪어 본 적 없는, 자기가 처한 새로운 영적 상태에 두려움을 느끼기 시작했다. 그녀는 마치 지나치게 피로한 눈에 사물이 이따금 둘로 보이듯, 자기 영혼 속에서 모든 것이 둘로 갈라지기 시작하는 것 같았다. 그녀는 이따금 자기가 무엇을 두려워하고 무엇을 바라는지조차 거의 알 수 없었다. 이미 일어난 일을 두려워하는 건지 바라는 건지, 아니면 앞으로 일어날 일을 두려워하는 건지 바라는 건지, 또 정확히 무엇을 갈망하는 건지, 그녀로서는 말할 수 없었다.\n\n\"아, 내가 무슨 짓을 하고 있는 거야!\" 그녀는 머리의 양쪽이 별안간 욱신거리는 아픔을 느끼며 혼잣말을 했다. 정신을 차리고 보니, 자기가 두 손으로 관자놀이 양쪽의 머리카락을 움켜쥐고 잡아당기고 있었다. 그녀는 벌떡 일어나 방 안을 거닐기 시작했다.\n\n\"커피가 준비됐고, 마드무아젤과 세료자가 기다리고 있습니다.\" 다시 돌아온 안누시카가 똑같은 자세로 있는 안나를 발견하고 말했다.\n\n\"세료자? 세료자가 어쨌다고?\" 그날 아침 처음으로 아들의 존재를 떠올리며 안나가 별안간 간절하게 물었다.\n\n\"좀 말썽을 부린 것 같아요.\" 안누시카가 미소를 지으며 대답했다.\n\n\"어떻게?\"\n\n\"구석 방 탁자 위에 복숭아가 몇 개 놓여 있었거든요. 도련님이 몰래 들어가서 그중 하나를 슬쩍 먹어 버린 것 같아요.\"\n\n아들에 대한 기억이 별안간 안나를 자기가 빠져 있던 무력한 상태에서 끌어올렸다. 그녀는 최근 몇 년 동안 자신이 맡아 온, 아이를 위해 사는 어머니라는, 부분적으로는 진심이지만 크게 과장된 역할을 떠올렸고, 자기가 처한 곤경 속에서, 남편이나 브론스키와의 관계와는 전혀 별개로 자기에게 떠받쳐 줄 것이 있다는 사실을 기쁘게 느꼈다. 그 떠받침은 바로 아들이었다. 어떤 처지에 놓이게 되든, 그녀는 아들을 잃을 수 없었다. 남편이 그녀에게 수치를 안기고 그녀를 쫓아낼지도 모르고, 브론스키가 그녀에게 차가워져 자기만의 삶을 따로 살아갈지도 모르지만(그녀는 다시 쓰라림과 원망 속에서 그를 떠올렸다), 그녀는 아들을 떠날 수 없었다. 그녀에게는 삶의 목표가 있었다. 그러니 그녀는 행동해야 했다. 아들과의 이 관계를 지키고, 아들을 자기에게서 빼앗기지 않도록 행동해야 했다. 정말로 빨리, 되도록 빨리, 아들을 빼앗기기 전에 행동에 나서야 했다. 아들을 데리고 떠나야 했다. 이것이 지금 그녀가 해야 할 단 하나의 일이었다. 그녀에게는 위안이 필요했다. 마음을 가라앉히고, 이 견딜 수 없는 처지에서 빠져나와야 했다. 자기를 아들과 맺어 주는 즉각적인 행동, 아이와 함께 어딘가로 떠난다는 생각이 그녀에게 그 위안을 주었다.\n\n그녀는 재빨리 옷을 갈아입고 아래층으로 내려가, 단호한 걸음으로 응접실로 들어섰다. 거기에는 늘 그렇듯 그녀를 기다리며 커피와 세료자, 그리고 그의 가정교사가 있었다. 온통 흰옷을 입은 세료자는 등과 머리를 숙인 채 거울 아래 탁자 앞에 서서, 그녀가 잘 아는, 그리고 그 점에서 아버지를 닮은 강렬한 집중의 표정으로, 손에 든 꽃들에 뭔가를 하고 있었다.\n\n가정교사는 유난히 엄한 표정을 짓고 있었다. 세료자는 늘 그러듯 새된 소리로 \"아, 엄마!\" 하고 외치고는 멈춰 서서, 엄마를 맞으러 가서 꽃을 내려놓을지, 아니면 화관 만들기를 마저 끝내고 꽃을 들고 갈지 망설였다.\n\n가정교사는 아침 인사를 한 뒤 세료자의 말썽에 대해 길고 자세하게 늘어놓기 시작했지만, 안나는 그 말을 듣고 있지 않았다. 그녀는 이 여자를 데려갈지 말지를 궁리하고 있었다. '아니, 데려가지 않겠어.' 그녀는 결심했다. '내 아이만 데리고 혼자 가겠어.'\n\n\"그래, 그건 아주 잘못한 일이야.\" 안나는 그렇게 말하고는 아들의 어깨를 잡고 그를 바라보았는데, 엄하게가 아니라 머뭇거리는 눈길로 바라보았고, 그 눈길은 아이를 어리둥절하게 하면서도 기쁘게 했다. 그러고 나서 그녀는 아들에게 입을 맞추었다. \"이 아이는 제게 맡기세요.\" 그녀는 어리둥절해하는 가정교사에게 말하고는, 아들을 놓지 않은 채 자기 앞에 커피가 차려진 탁자에 앉았다.\n\n\"엄마! 저······ 저······ 안 그랬어요······.\" 그가 복숭아에 관해 자기에게 무슨 일이 닥칠지 엄마의 표정에서 알아내려 애쓰며 말했다.\n\n\"세료자.\" 가정교사가 방에서 나가자마자 그녀가 말했다. \"그건 잘못한 일이야. 하지만 다시는 안 그럴 거지, 그렇지?······ 너 엄마 사랑하지?\"\n\n그녀는 눈에 눈물이 차오르는 것을 느꼈다. '내가 어떻게 이 아이를 사랑하지 않을 수 있겠어?' 그녀는 겁먹은 동시에 기뻐하는 아이의 눈을 깊이 들여다보며 혼잣말을 했다. '그리고 이 아이가 언제고 아버지 편에 서서 나를 벌할 수 있을까? 이 아이가 나를 가엾게 여기지 않을 수 있을까?' 이미 눈물이 얼굴을 타고 흐르고 있었고, 그것을 감추려고 그녀는 불쑥 일어나 거의 뛰다시피 테라스로 나갔다.\n\n지난 며칠간의 천둥소나기 뒤로 춥고 맑은 날씨가 찾아와 있었다. 갓 씻긴 잎사귀들 사이로 비쳐 드는 밝은 햇빛 속에서도 공기는 차가웠다.\n\n그녀는 추위 때문에, 그리고 탁 트인 바깥에서 새삼 거센 힘으로 그녀를 움켜쥔 내면의 공포 때문에 부르르 떨었다.\n\n\"가, 어서 마리에트에게로 가렴.\" 그녀는 자기를 따라 나온 세료자에게 말하고는 테라스의 짚자리 위를 이리저리 거닐기 시작했다. '저들이 정말로 나를 용서하지 않을까, 이 모든 게 어쩔 수 없었다는 걸 이해하지 못할까?' 그녀는 혼잣말을 했다.\n\n가만히 멈춰 서서, 차가운 햇살 속에 갓 씻겨 환하게 반짝이는 잎사귀를 단 사시나무 꼭대기가 바람에 흔들리는 것을 바라보면서, 그녀는 저들이 자기를 용서하지 않으리라는 것을, 이제 모든 사람과 모든 것이 저 하늘과 저 초록처럼 자기에게 무자비하리라는 것을 알았다. 그리고 다시 그녀는 자기 영혼 속에서 모든 것이 둘로 쪼개지는 것을 느꼈다. '생각하면 안 돼, 생각하면 안 돼.' 그녀는 혼잣말을 했다. '준비를 해야 해. 어디로 가지? 언제? 누구를 데리고? 그래, 저녁 기차로 모스크바로 가는 거야. 안누시카와 세료자, 그리고 꼭 필요한 것들만. 하지만 먼저 두 사람 모두에게 편지를 써야 해.' 그녀는 재빨리 안으로 들어가 자기 내실로 가서 탁자 앞에 앉아 남편에게 편지를 썼다. \"이번 일이 있은 후, 저는 더 이상 당신의 집에 머무를 수 없습니다. 저는 떠나며, 아들을 데리고 갑니다. 저는 법을 모르고, 따라서 아들이 부모 중 누구와 함께 있어야 하는지도 모릅니다. 하지만 저는 그 아이 없이는 살 수 없기에 그를 데리고 갑니다. 너그럽게, 그 아이를 제게 남겨 주세요.\"\n\n여기까지는 빠르고 자연스럽게 썼지만, 그에게서 인정한 적 없는 자질인 그의 너그러움에 호소한다는 점, 그리고 편지를 무언가 마음을 움직이는 말로 마무리해야 한다는 필요가 그녀를 멈칫하게 했다. \"제 잘못과 후회에 대해서는 말할 수 없습니다. 왜냐하면······\"\n\n그녀는 다시 멈추었다. 생각들 사이에 아무 연결고리도 찾을 수 없었다. '아니야.' 그녀는 혼잣말을 했다. '아무것도 필요 없어.' 그러고는 편지를 찢어 버리고, 너그러움에 대한 언급을 빼고 다시 썼으며, 그것을 봉했다.\n\n브론스키에게도 편지를 한 통 써야 했다. \"저는 남편에게 말했어요.\" 그녀는 그렇게 썼고, 그 이상 쓰지 못한 채 오랫동안 앉아 있었다. 그것은 너무나 거칠고, 너무나 여자답지 못했다. '그리고 그에게 무슨 말을 더 써야 하지?' 그녀는 혼잣말을 했다. 다시 부끄러움의 홍조가 그녀의 얼굴에 번졌다. 그녀는 그의 침착함을 떠올렸고, 그에 대한 분노가 치밀어 자기가 써 놓은 그 문장이 적힌 종이를 잘게 찢어 버렸다. '아무것도 필요 없어.' 그녀는 혼잣말을 하고는 압지첩을 덮고 위층으로 올라가, 가정교사와 하인들에게 자기가 그날 모스크바로 떠난다고 일렀으며, 곧바로 짐을 꾸리는 일에 착수했다.\n\n# Chapter 16\n\n별장의 모든 방은 짐을 들고 이리저리 오가는 짐꾼과 정원사, 하인들로 가득했다. 장롱과 궤짝들이 죄다 열려 있었고, 끈을 사 오라고 가게에 두 번이나 사람을 보냈으며, 신문지 조각들이 바닥에 나뒹굴고 있었다. 트렁크 두 개와 가방 몇 개, 끈으로 묶은 무릎덮개 따위가 현관으로 옮겨져 있었다. 마차 한 대와 세를 낸 삯마차 두 대가 현관 계단 앞에서 기다리고 있었다. 짐을 꾸리는 일에 내면의 동요를 잊은 채, 안나는 자기 안방의 탁자 앞에 서서 여행용 가방을 챙기고 있었는데, 그때 안누슈카가 어떤 마차가 다가오는 덜컹거리는 소리에 그녀의 주의를 돌렸다. 안나는 창밖을 내다보았고, 현관 계단 위에서 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 급사가 현관 초인종을 울리는 것을 보았다.\n\n\"어서 가서 무슨 일인지 알아보거라.\" 그녀는 말했다. 그리고 무슨 일에든 마음의 준비가 되어 있다는 차분한 느낌으로, 그녀는 낮은 의자에 앉아 두 손을 무릎 위에 모았다. 한 하인이 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 필체로 주소가 적힌 두툼한 봉투를 들고 들어왔다.\n\n\"급사가 답장을 기다리라는 분부를 받았다고 합니다.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"알았다.\" 그녀는 말했고, 그가 방에서 나가자마자 떨리는 손가락으로 편지를 뜯어 열었다. 봉투 안에서 종이로 싼, 접지 않은 지폐 한 뭉치가 떨어졌다. 그녀는 편지를 꺼내 끝부분부터 읽기 시작했다. \"당신이 이리로 돌아올 수 있도록 준비를 갖추어 두겠소…… 이에 따라 주기를 각별히 중요하게 여기는 바이오……\" 그녀는 읽었다. 죽 읽어 내려가다가 다시 거슬러 올라가, 전부 읽고는, 처음부터 한 번 더 편지를 끝까지 읽었다. 다 읽고 났을 때, 그녀는 온몸이 차갑게 식는 것을, 그리고 자기가 예상치 못했던 무시무시한 재앙이 자기 위로 들이닥쳤음을 느꼈다.\n\n아침에 그녀는 남편에게 말한 것을 후회했고, 그 말들이 입 밖에 나오지 않은 것이 되기를 무엇보다 간절히 바랐다. 그런데 이 편지는 그 말들을 없던 것으로 여기며, 그녀가 원했던 바를 그대로 주고 있었다. 하지만 지금 이 편지는 그녀가 상상할 수 있었던 그 무엇보다도 끔찍하게 느껴졌다.\n\n\"그이가 옳아!\" 그녀는 말했다. \"당연히, 그이는 늘 옳지. 그이는 기독교인이고, 너그러운 사람이니까! 그래, 비열하고 천한 인간 같으니! 그리고 나 말고는 아무도 그걸 이해하지 못하고, 앞으로도 영영 못 할 거야. 나로서는 설명할 수도 없어. 사람들은 그이가 신앙심이 깊고, 고결한 원칙을 지니고, 정직하고, 영리하다고들 하지. 하지만 그들은 내가 본 것을 보지 못해. 그이가 어떻게 팔 년 동안 내 인생을 짓밟았는지, 내 안에 살아 있던 모든 것을 짓이겨 놓았는지 그들은 몰라. 그이는 내가 사랑을 필요로 하는, 살아 있는 여자라는 걸 단 한 번도 생각해 본 적이 없어. 그이가 어떻게 걸음마다 나를 모욕하면서도 그렇게 흐뭇해했는지 그들은 몰라. 나는 안 그랬던가, 내 삶에 의미를 줄 무언가를 찾으려고 온 힘을 다해 애쓰고 또 애쓰지 않았던가? 남편을 사랑할 수 없을 때 내 아들이라도 사랑하려고, 그이를 사랑하려고 발버둥치지 않았던가? 하지만 더 이상 나 자신을 속일 수 없다는 걸, 내가 살아 있다는 걸, 내게 잘못이 없다는 걸, 하느님이 나를 사랑하고 살아가야 하는 존재로 만드셨다는 걸 깨닫는 때가 왔어. 그런데 이제 그이는 어떻게 하지? 그이가 나를 죽였다면, 그 사람을 죽였다면, 나는 무엇이든 견딜 수 있었을 거고, 무엇이든 용서할 수 있었을 거야. 하지만 아니, 그이는…… 어째서 나는 그이가 어떻게 나올지 짐작하지 못했을까? 그이는 그 비열한 성격에 딱 어울리는 짓을 하고 있는 거야. 그이는 자기는 옳은 쪽에 머물면서, 파멸한 나를 더더욱 끌어내려 더 끔찍한 파멸로 몰아넣겠지…….\"\n\n그녀는 편지에 적힌 말들을 떠올렸다. \"당신과 당신 아들을 무엇이 기다리고 있을지 짐작이 갈 거요…….\" \"저건 내 아이를 빼앗겠다는 위협이고, 그들의 어리석은 법대로라면 십중팔구 그이가 그렇게 할 수 있겠지. 하지만 그이가 왜 그런 말을 하는지 나는 아주 잘 알아. 그이는 내가 내 아이를 사랑한다는 것조차 믿지 않거나, 아니면 그걸 경멸하는 거야(늘 그걸 비웃곤 했던 것처럼). 그이는 내 안의 그 감정을 경멸하지. 하지만 내가 내 아이를 버리지 않으리라는 것을, 내 아이를 버릴 수 없다는 것을, 내 아이가 없으면 내게는, 내가 사랑하는 그 사람과 함께 있더라도, 삶이 있을 수 없다는 것을 그이는 알아. 그리고 만약 내가 내 아이를 버리고 그이에게서 도망친다면, 가장 파렴치하고 가장 천한 여자처럼 행동하는 셈이 되리라는 것을 그이는 알지. 그이는 그것을 알고, 또 내가 그런 짓을 할 수 없는 사람이라는 것도 알아.\"\n\n그녀는 편지에 적힌 또 다른 문장을 떠올렸다. \"우리의 생활은 예전과 다름없이 계속되어야 하오…….\" \"그 생활은 예전에도 충분히 비참했어. 요즘은 끔찍했지. 그러니 이제는 어떻게 될까? 그이는 그걸 다 알아. 그이는 내가 숨 쉬는 것을, 내가 사랑하는 것을 뉘우칠 수 없다는 걸 알아. 그것이 거짓과 기만 말고는 아무것도 낳을 수 없다는 걸 알면서도, 그이는 계속 나를 괴롭히고 싶어 해. 나는 그이를 알아. 나는 그이가 물속을 헤엄치는 물고기처럼 거짓 속에서 편안하고 행복해한다는 걸 알아. 안 돼, 나는 그이에게 그런 행복을 주지 않을 거야. 무슨 일이 있어도, 그이가 나를 옭아매려는 그 거짓의 거미줄을 뚫고 나가겠어. 거짓과 기만보다는 무엇이든 나아.\"\n\n\"하지만 어떻게? 맙소사! 맙소사! 나만큼 비참한 여자가 또 있었을까?…….\"\n\n\"아니, 나는 뚫고 나갈 거야, 뚫고 나가겠어!\" 그녀는 눈물을 억누르며 벌떡 일어나 소리쳤다. 그리고 그에게 또 다른 편지를 쓰려고 책상으로 갔다. 하지만 마음 깊은 곳에서, 그녀는 자기가 무언가를 뚫고 나갈 만큼 강하지 못하다는 것을, 그것이 아무리 거짓되고 떳떳하지 못할지라도 자기의 옛 처지에서 벗어날 만큼 강하지 못하다는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n그녀는 책상 앞에 앉았지만, 쓰는 대신 두 손을 탁자 위에 포개고는 거기에 머리를 묻고, 우는 아이처럼 흐느끼며 가슴을 들썩이면서 울음을 터뜨렸다. 그녀는 자기 처지가 분명하고 확실하게 정리되리라던 꿈이 영원히 무너져 버린 것을 두고 울고 있었다. 그녀는 모든 것이 예전 방식대로, 아니 사실은 예전보다 훨씬 더 나쁘게 흘러가리라는 것을 미리부터 알고 있었다. 그녀가 누리고 있던, 아침에는 그토록 하찮게 여겨졌던 그 사교계에서의 지위가 자기에게 소중하다는 것을, 남편과 아이를 버리고 정부에게로 달아난 여자라는 수치스러운 처지와 그 지위를 맞바꿀 힘이 자기에게 없으리라는 것을, 아무리 발버둥쳐도 자기 자신보다 더 강해질 수는 없으리라는 것을 그녀는 느꼈다. 그녀는 결코 사랑 안에서 자유를 알지 못할 것이며, 매 순간 발각될지 모른다는 위협이 머리 위에 드리운 채, 영원히 죄지은 아내로 남을 터였다. 그녀와 떨어져 따로 사는, 결코 그 삶을 함께할 수 없는 한 남자와의 수치스러운 관계를 위해 남편을 속이면서. 그녀는 일이 이렇게 되리라는 것을 알았고, 동시에 그것이 너무도 끔찍해서 그 끝이 어떻게 될지 짐작조차 할 수 없었다. 그래서 그녀는 벌받는 아이가 울듯이 거리낌 없이 울었다.\n\n하인의 발소리에 그녀는 정신을 차릴 수밖에 없었고, 그에게서 얼굴을 가리며 무언가를 쓰는 척했다.\n\n\"급사가 답장이 있는지 여쭙니다.\" 하인이 알렸다.\n\n\"답장? 그래.\" 안나가 말했다. \"기다리라고 해라. 내가 종을 울리마.\"\n\n'무슨 말을 써야 하나?' 그녀는 생각했다. '나 혼자서 무엇을 결정할 수 있단 말인가? 내가 무엇을 알지? 내가 무엇을 원하지? 내게 소중한 게 뭐가 있지?' 다시 그녀는 자기 영혼이 둘로 쪼개지기 시작하는 것을 느꼈다. 이 느낌에 그녀는 또다시 겁이 났고, 자기 자신에게서 생각을 돌릴 수 있는 무언가를 할 첫 번째 구실에 매달렸다. '알렉세이를 만나야 해(그녀는 마음속으로 브론스키를 그렇게 불렀다). 내가 어떻게 해야 할지 말해 줄 사람은 그이밖에 없어. 베치네 집으로 가자, 어쩌면 거기서 그이를 만날 수 있을지도 몰라.' 그녀는 혼잣말을 했다. 어제 트베르스카야 공작부인 댁에 가지 않겠다고 그에게 말했을 때, 그렇다면 자기도 가지 않겠다고 그가 했던 말은 까맣게 잊은 채였다. 그녀는 탁자로 가서 남편에게 이렇게 썼다. \"당신의 편지를 받았습니다.—A.\" 그러고는 종을 울려 하인에게 그것을 건넸다.\n\n\"우리는 가지 않는다.\" 그녀는 안누슈카가 들어오자 말했다.\n\n\"아예 안 가시는 거예요?\"\n\n\"그래. 내일까지는 짐을 풀지 말고, 마차는 기다리게 해 둬라. 나는 공작부인 댁에 갈 거야.\"\n\n\"어느 드레스를 준비해 드릴까요?\"\n\n# Chapter 17\n\n트베르스카야 공작 부인이 안나를 초대한 크로켓 모임은 부인 둘과 그들을 흠모하는 남자들로 이루어질 예정이었다. 이 두 부인은 어떤 모방을 또 모방하여 les sept merveilles du monde라고 별명 붙은, 페테르부르크의 선택받은 새 사교계를 대표하는 인물들이었다. 이 부인들은 최상류 사교계에 속하면서도 안나가 드나드는 사교계와는 철저히 적대적인 모임의 일원이었다. 게다가 페테르부르크에서 가장 영향력 있는 인물 가운데 하나이자 리자 메르칼로바의 나이 든 숭배자인 스트레모프는 정치계에서 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 적이었다. 이 모든 사정을 고려해 안나는 가지 않을 작정이었고, 트베르스카야 공작 부인의 쪽지에 담긴 암시도 그녀의 거절을 두고 한 말이었다. 그러나 지금 안나는 브론스키를 만날 수 있으리라는 기대로 가고 싶어 안달이 났다.\n* les sept merveilles du monde: 프랑스어로 '세계의 일곱 가지 불가사의'.\n\n안나는 다른 손님들보다 먼저 트베르스카야 공작 부인의 집에 도착했다.\n\n그녀가 들어서는 바로 그 순간, 시종처럼 빗어 넘긴 구레나룻을 한 브론스키의 하인도 안으로 들어왔다. 그는 문 앞에 멈춰 서서 모자를 벗고 그녀에게 길을 내주었다. 안나는 그를 알아보았고, 그제야 어제 브론스키가 오지 않겠다고 말한 것을 떠올렸다. 아마 그가 그것을 알리는 쪽지를 보낸 모양이었다.\n* Kammerjunker: 독일어로 궁정 시종을 뜻하는 관직명.\n\n현관에서 겉옷을 벗는 동안, 그녀는 그 하인이 시종처럼 'r' 발음을 또렷이 굴리며 \"백작님이 공작 부인께 보내는 것입니다\"라고 말하며 쪽지를 건네는 소리를 들었다.\n\n그녀는 그에게 주인이 어디 있는지 묻고 싶어 견딜 수 없었다. 발길을 돌려 그에게 자기를 만나러 와 달라는 편지를 보내거나, 아니면 자기가 직접 그를 만나러 가고 싶어 견딜 수 없었다. 그러나 첫 번째도, 두 번째도, 세 번째도 불가능했다. 벌써 그녀의 도착을 알리는 종소리가 그녀보다 앞서 울리는 것이 들렸고, 트베르스카야 공작 부인의 하인은 그녀가 안쪽 방으로 들어가기를 기다리며 열린 문 앞에 서 있었다.\n\n\"공작 부인께서는 정원에 계십니다. 곧 알려 드리겠습니다. 정원으로 드시겠습니까?\" 다른 방에서 또 다른 하인이 아뢰었다.\n\n불확실하고 결단을 내리지 못하는 처지는 집에 있을 때와 똑같았다—아니, 사실 더 나빴다. 아무런 행동도 취할 수 없고, 브론스키를 만날 수도 없으며, 지금 자기 기분에 그토록 맞지 않는 사람들 틈에 끼어 이곳에 남아 있어야 했으니까. 그러나 그녀는 자기에게 잘 어울리는 줄 아는 옷을 입고 있었다. 그녀는 혼자가 아니었고, 사방에는 그녀가 익숙한, 한가로움이 빚어내는 그 호화로운 분위기가 펼쳐져 있었으므로, 집에 있을 때보다는 덜 비참했다. 무엇을 해야 할지 굳이 생각할 필요도 없었다. 모든 것이 저절로 풀려 갈 터였다. 우아함으로 그녀를 사로잡는 하얀 드레스 차림으로 마주 걸어오는 벳시를 만나자, 안나는 늘 그러듯 그녀에게 미소를 지었다. 트베르스카야 공작 부인은 투슈케비치, 그리고 친척인 젊은 아가씨와 함께 걷고 있었는데, 그 아가씨는 지방에 있는 부모를 더없이 기쁘게 하며 그 유행을 선도하는 공작 부인과 함께 여름을 보내는 중이었다.\n\n안나에게는 아마 어딘가 예사롭지 않은 데가 있었던 모양이다. 벳시가 단번에 그것을 알아챘으니까.\n\n\"잠을 설쳤어요.\" 안나는 자기들을 맞으러 온, 그리고 그녀 생각에는 브론스키의 쪽지를 가져온 하인을 뚫어지게 바라보며 대답했다.\n\n\"와 줘서 정말 기뻐요!\" 벳시가 말했다. \"난 지쳤어요. 다른 사람들이 오기 전에 차나 좀 마시고 싶던 참이었죠. 당신은\"—그녀가 투슈케비치 쪽으로 돌아섰다—\"마샤랑 같이 가서, 저쪽에 풀을 깎아 둔 크로켓 경기장을 한번 봐 줘요. 우린 차를 마시면서 잠깐 얘기나 나눌게요. 둘이서 오붓하게 수다나 떨자고요, 응?\" 그녀는 양산을 쥔 손으로 안나의 손을 꼭 누르며 미소 띤 얼굴로 영어로 말했다.\n\n\"좋아요, 그렇잖아도 당신과 오래 있을 수가 없거든요. 브레데 노부인 댁에 들러야 해서요. 백 년 전부터 가겠다고 약속만 해 놓고 있었지 뭐예요.\" 안나가 말했다. 거짓말이란 그녀의 천성에 그토록 낯선 것이었으나, 사교계에서는 그저 쉽고 자연스러운 일이 되었을 뿐 아니라 오히려 흡족함을 안겨 주는 일이 되어 있었다. 바로 일 초 전까지만 해도 생각조차 하지 않았던 이 말을 왜 했는지, 그녀 자신도 설명할 수 없었으리라. 그녀가 이 말을 한 것은 그저, 브론스키가 여기 오지 않을 테니 차라리 자기 자유를 확보해 두었다가 어떻게든 그를 만나 보는 편이 낫겠다는 생각에서였다. 하지만 다른 많은 사람을 찾아가야 하듯 찾아가야 하는 브레데 노부인을 왜 입에 올렸는지는 설명할 수 없었으리라. 그러나 나중에 드러난 바로는, 브론스키를 만나기 위해 아무리 교묘한 책략을 꾸몄다 한들 이보다 더 나은 수는 생각해 낼 수 없었을 것이다.\n\n\"아니요. 난 무슨 일이 있어도 당신을 보내 주지 않을 거예요.\" 벳시가 안나의 얼굴을 뚫어지게 들여다보며 대답했다. \"정말이지, 내가 당신을 좋아하지 않았다면 모욕감을 느꼈을 거예요. 마치 내 곁에 있으면 당신 체면이 깎이기라도 할까 봐 두려워하는 사람처럼 구네요. 작은 식당에 차를 내 줘요.\" 그녀는 하인에게 말을 건넬 때면 늘 그러듯 눈을 반쯤 감으며 말했다.\n\n그에게서 쪽지를 받아 든 그녀는 그것을 읽었다.\n\n\"알렉세이가 우릴 골탕 먹이네요.\" 그녀는 프랑스어로 말했다. \"올 수 없다고 쓰여 있어요.\" 그녀는 브론스키가 안나에게 크로켓 한 판 이상의 어떤 의미를 지닐 수 있으리라고는 머릿속에 떠올릴 수조차 없다는 듯, 더없이 단순하고 자연스러운 어조로 덧붙였다. 안나는 벳시가 모든 것을 알고 있다는 것을 알았지만, 자기 앞에서 브론스키를 두고 그녀가 말하는 것을 들으니, 순간 그녀가 아무것도 모른다고 거의 믿을 뻔했다.\n\n\"어머!\" 안나는 그 일에 별로 관심이 없는 듯 무심하게 말하고는 계속 미소를 지으며 말을 이었다. \"당신이나 당신 친구들이 어떻게 누군가의 체면을 깎을 수가 있겠어요?\"\n\n이런 말장난, 비밀을 감추는 이 놀이는 안나에게 큰 매력으로 다가왔고, 사실 모든 여자에게 그러하듯이 말이다. 그리고 그녀를 끌어당긴 것은 감출 필요성도, 그 감춤이 노리는 목적도 아니라, 감추는 그 과정 자체였다.\n\n\"교황보다 더 가톨릭적일 수는 없잖아요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"스트레모프와 리자 메르칼로바라니, 그 사람들이야말로 사교계의 알짜배기 중의 알짜배기인걸요. 게다가 어딜 가나 환영받는 사람들이고, 나는\"—그녀는 '나'라는 말에 특별히 힘을 주었다—\"단 한 번도 깐깐하거나 옹졸하게 군 적이 없어요. 그저 시간이 없을 뿐이죠.\"\n\n\"아니요, 어쩌면 스트레모프를 만나기 싫은 거 아니에요? 그 사람이랑 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 위원회에서 서로 창을 겨누든 말든—그건 우리가 상관할 일이 아니죠. 하지만 사교계에서는 그 사람이 내가 아는 가장 상냥한 남자랍니다. 게다가 크로켓에 푹 빠져 있고요. 곧 알게 될 거예요. 그리고 그 나이에 리자에게 상사병이 든 우스꽝스러운 처지인데도, 그 우스운 처지를 얼마나 멋들어지게 넘기는지 좀 봐야 해요. 아주 괜찮은 사람이에요. 사포 시톨츠는 모르죠? 오, 그건 새로운 유형이에요, 아주 새롭죠.\"\n\n벳시는 이 모든 말을 했고, 동시에 그녀의 쾌활하고 영악한 눈빛에서 안나는 그녀가 자기 곤경을 어느 정도 눈치챘으며, 자기를 위해 무언가를 꾸미고 있다는 것을 느꼈다. 두 사람은 작은 내실에 있었다.\n\n\"그래도 알렉세이에게는 편지를 써야겠어요.\" 그러고는 벳시가 탁자 앞에 앉아 몇 줄 끄적이고는 쪽지를 봉투에 넣었다.\n\n\"저녁 식사에 오라고 쓰는 거예요. 식사에 부인 한 명이 더 오는데 모셔 들일 남자가 없거든요. 내가 뭐라고 썼는지 봐요, 이러면 그 사람 마음이 움직일까요? 미안하지만 잠깐 자리를 비워야겠어요. 이걸 봉해서 보내 주겠어요?\" 그녀가 문 쪽에서 말했다. \"지시할 게 좀 있어서요.\"\n\n안나는 잠깐의 망설임도 없이 벳시의 편지를 들고 탁자 앞에 앉았고, 그것을 읽지도 않은 채 그 아래에 이렇게 썼다. \"당신을 꼭 만나야 해요. 브레데 정원으로 와요. 여섯 시에 거기 있을게요.\" 그녀는 그것을 봉했고, 벳시가 돌아오자 그녀가 보는 앞에서 쪽지를 부치라고 건넸다.\n\n서늘한 작은 응접실에 놓인 작은 찻상으로 차가 들어왔고, 손님들이 오기 전에 트베르스카야 공작 부인이 약속했던 오붓한 수다가 정말로 두 여인 사이에서 벌어졌다. 그들은 오기로 한 사람들을 도마에 올렸고, 화제는 리자 메르칼로바에게로 옮겨 갔다.\n\n\"그 사람 참 사랑스러워요, 난 늘 그 사람이 좋았어요.\" 안나가 말했다.\n\n\"좋아할 만하죠. 그 사람도 당신한테 푹 빠져 있는걸요. 어제 경마가 끝나고 나한테 다가오더니 당신을 찾지 못했다고 절망하더라고요. 당신이 진짜 소설 속 여주인공 같다면서, 자기가 남자였다면 당신을 위해 온갖 미친 짓도 마다하지 않았을 거라지 뭐예요. 스트레모프는 지금도 그 사람이 그러고 있다고 하던데요.\"\n\n\"그런데 부탁이니 말 좀 해 줘요, 난 도무지 알 수가 없거든요.\" 안나가 한동안 입을 다물고 있다가, 한가한 질문을 하는 게 아니라 자기가 묻는 것이 마땅한 정도 이상으로 그녀에게 중요한 일임을 드러내는 어조로 말했다. \"부탁이니 말 좀 해 줘요, 그 사람이랑 칼루시스키 공작, 미시카라고 불리는 그 사람 사이는 대체 어떤 관계예요? 난 그 사람들을 너무 가끔밖에 못 봐서요. 그게 무슨 뜻이에요?\"\n\n벳시는 눈으로 미소를 지으며 안나를 뚫어지게 바라보았다.\n\n\"그게 새로운 방식이에요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"다들 그 방식을 따르고 있죠. 체면이고 뭐고 다 내던져 버린 거예요. 하지만 내던지는 데도 이런저런 방식이 있는 법이죠.\"\n\n\"네, 그런데 그 사람이랑 칼루시스키의 관계가 정확히 어떤 거냐고요.\"\n\n벳시가 뜻밖에도 유쾌하고 억누를 수 없는 웃음을 터뜨렸는데, 그녀에게는 좀처럼 없는 일이었다.\n\n\"이제 미야흐카야 공작 부인의 전문 영역을 넘보고 있군요. 그건 enfant terrible나 던질 법한 질문이에요.\" 벳시는 분명 자제하려 애썼지만 그러지 못하고, 자주 웃지 않는 사람들이나 짓는 그 전염성 강한 폭소를 터뜨리고 말았다. \"차라리 그 사람들한테 직접 물어보는 게 낫겠어요.\" 그녀가 웃음의 눈물 사이로 간신히 말했다.\n* enfant terrible: 프랑스어로 '무서운 아이', 거리낌 없이 곤란한 말을 내뱉는 사람을 이르는 말.\n\n\"아니요, 당신은 웃지만요.\" 안나도 자기도 모르게 따라 웃으며 말했다. \"난 도무지 이해할 수가 없어요. 거기서 남편이 맡는 역할을 알 수가 없단 말이죠.\"\n\n\"남편이요? 리자 메르칼로바의 남편은 그 사람 숄이나 들고 다니고, 늘 도울 채비가 되어 있죠. 하지만 실제로 그 이상의 무언가에 대해서는, 아무도 캐물으려 들지 않아요. 알다시피 점잖은 사교계에서는 몸치장의 어떤 세세한 부분에 대해 말하지도, 생각하지도 않잖아요. 이것도 바로 그런 거예요.\"\n\n\"롤란다크 부인의 잔치에는 갈 거예요?\" 안나가 화제를 바꾸려고 물었다.\n\n\"안 갈 것 같아요.\" 벳시가 대답하고는, 친구를 보지도 않은 채 향긋한 차를 작은 투명 잔에 따르기 시작했다. 안나 앞에 잔을 놓아 준 그녀는 담배 한 개비를 꺼내 은제 물부리에 끼우고 불을 붙였다.\n\n\"이런 거예요, 봐요. 난 운이 좋은 처지에 있죠.\" 그녀가 잔을 들며 이제 자못 진지하게 입을 열었다. \"난 당신을 이해하고, 리자도 이해해요. 리자는 어린아이들처럼 무엇이 좋고 무엇이 나쁜지 모르는 그런 순진한 천성을 지닌 사람 중 하나예요. 어쨌거나 아주 어렸을 때는 그걸 이해하지 못했죠. 그리고 지금은 그렇게 이해하지 못하는 게 자기한테 어울린다는 걸 알아챈 거예요. 어쩌면 지금은 일부러 모르는 척하는 건지도 모르죠.\" 벳시가 야릇한 미소를 띠며 말했다. \"하지만 어쨌든 그게 그 사람한테 어울려요. 알겠어요, 똑같은 일이라도 비극적으로 바라보면 비참한 게 되어 버리지만, 단순하게, 심지어 익살스럽게 바라볼 수도 있는 거예요. 어쩌면 당신은 매사를 너무 비극적으로 바라보는 경향이 있는지도 몰라요.\"\n\n\"다른 사람들을 나 자신처럼 속속들이 알 수 있다면 얼마나 좋을까요!\" 안나가 진지하게, 그리고 꿈꾸듯 말했다. \"난 다른 사람들보다 못한 걸까요, 아니면 나은 걸까요? 난 내가 못하다고 생각해요.\"\n\n\"Enfant terrible, enfant terrible 같으니!\" 벳시가 거듭 말했다. \"어머, 다들 왔네요.\"\n\n# Chapter 18\n\n발소리와 남자의 목소리가 들리고, 이어 여자의 목소리와 웃음소리가 들리더니, 곧바로 기다리던 손님들이 들어왔다. 사포 시톨츠와, 넘쳐 나는 건강미로 환하게 빛나는 젊은 남자, 이른바 바스카였다. 비프스테이크와 송로버섯과 부르고뉴 포도주가 적당한 시간에 그에게 넉넉히 공급되어 한 번도 끊긴 적이 없으리라는 것이 한눈에 보였다. 바스카는 두 부인에게 인사를 하고 그들을 흘끗 보았으나, 그것도 단 일 초뿐이었다. 그는 사포를 따라 응접실로 들어와, 마치 그녀에게 사슬로 묶이기라도 한 듯 그녀의 뒤를 졸졸 따라다니며, 그녀를 잡아먹기라도 할 것처럼 반짝이는 눈을 그녀에게서 떼지 않았다. 사포 시톨츠는 검은 눈을 가진 금발 미인이었다. 그녀는 굽 높은 구두를 신고 멋스러운 종종걸음으로 걸으며, 남자처럼 힘차게 부인들과 악수를 나누었다.\n\n안나는 이 새로운 유행의 별을 한 번도 만난 적이 없었기에, 그녀의 아름다움과, 극단적으로 과장된 옷차림과, 대담한 몸가짐에 깜짝 놀랐다. 그녀의 머리 위에는 진짜와 가짜가 뒤섞인 부드러운 금빛 머리칼이 어찌나 높이 쌓아 올려져 있는지, 머리통이 우아하게 둥글린, 앞이 잔뜩 드러난 가슴팍과 맞먹는 크기였다. 그녀의 움직임은 충동적이고 갑작스러워, 한 걸음 내디딜 때마다 옷 아래로 무릎과 허벅지 윗부분의 윤곽이 또렷이 드러났고, 앞은 그토록 벌거벗고 뒤와 아래로는 그토록 감추어진, 그토록 작고 가냘픈 이 여자의 진짜 몸이, 뒤쪽으로 출렁이며 산처럼 쌓아 올린 옷감 속 어디쯤에서 실제로 끝나는 것인지, 절로 궁금증이 떠올랐다.\n\n베치는 서둘러 그녀를 안나에게 소개했다.\n\n\"글쎄, 우리 하마터면 병사 둘을 칠 뻔했지 뭐예요.\" 그녀는 눈을 굴리고 미소를 지으며 단번에 한쪽으로 휙 젖힌 드레스 자락을 흔들면서 곧장 이야기를 늘어놓기 시작했다. \"바스카랑 같이 여기까지 왔어요…… 아, 참, 두 분은 서로 모르시지.\" 그러고는 그의 성을 대며 젊은 남자를 소개하더니, 살짝 얼굴을 붉히며 자신의 실수에—그러니까 낯선 사람 앞에서 그를 바스카라고 불러 버린 것에—까르르 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 바스카는 안나에게 다시 한번 인사를 했으나, 그녀에게는 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 그는 사포를 향해 말했다. \"내기에 졌어요. 우리가 먼저 도착했잖아요. 갚으셔야죠.\" 그가 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n사포는 더욱더 즐겁게 웃었다.\n\n\"지금은 안 돼요.\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"아, 알았어요, 나중에 받죠.\"\n\n\"좋아요, 좋아. 아, 참.\" 그녀가 갑자기 베치 공작 부인 쪽으로 돌아섰다. \"나 정말 괜찮은 사람이에요…… 깜빡 잊고 있었지 뭐예요…… 손님 한 분을 모시고 왔거든요. 저기 오시네요.\" 사포가 초대해 놓고는 잊고 있었던 뜻밖의 젊은 손님은, 그러나 그의 젊음에도 불구하고 두 부인이 그의 등장에 자리에서 일어날 만큼 대단한 인물이었다.\n\n그는 사포의 새로운 숭배자였다. 그도 이제 바스카처럼 그녀의 발자취를 졸졸 따라다녔다.\n\n곧이어 칼루주스키 공작이 도착했고, 리자 메르칼로바가 스트레모프와 함께 왔다. 리자 메르칼로바는 동양적이고 나른한 인상의 얼굴에, 다들 입버릇처럼 말하듯 더없이 빼어나고 수수께끼 같은 눈을 가진, 호리호리한 갈색 머리 여자였다. 그녀의 짙은 색 드레스의 색조는(안나는 즉시 그것을 알아채고 높이 평가했다) 그녀의 아름다운 분위기와 완벽하게 어우러져 있었다. 사포가 멋스럽고 갑작스러운 만큼이나, 리자는 부드럽고 나른했다.\n\n그러나 안나의 취향에는 리자가 훨씬 더 매력적이었다. 베치는 안나에게 리자가 순진한 어린아이 같은 태도를 꾸며 보인다고 말한 적이 있었으나, 안나가 직접 그녀를 보니 그것이 사실이 아니라는 느낌이 들었다. 그녀는 정말로 순진하면서도 타락한, 그러나 사랑스럽고 수동적인 여자였다. 그녀의 태도가 사포와 똑같다는 것은 사실이었다. 사포처럼 그녀에게도 하나는 젊고 하나는 늙은, 두 남자가 따라붙어 눈으로 그녀를 집어삼키고 있다는 것도. 그러나 그녀에게는 주위를 둘러싼 것들보다 한층 높은 무언가가 있었다. 그녀에게는 유리 모조품들 사이에 섞인 진짜 다이아몬드의 광채가 있었다. 그 광채는 빼어나고 정말로 수수께끼 같은 그녀의 두 눈에서 뿜어져 나왔다. 짙은 그늘에 둘러싸인 그 눈의, 지치고도 동시에 정열적인 시선은 더없이 진실한 느낌으로 사람을 사로잡았다. 그 눈을 들여다보는 사람은 누구나 자기가 그녀를 온전히 안다고 여겼고, 또 그녀를 아는 이상 그녀를 사랑하지 않을 수 없다고 여겼다. 안나를 보는 순간, 그녀의 온 얼굴이 단번에 반가운 미소로 환해졌다.\n\n\"아, 만나서 정말 반가워요!\" 그녀가 다가오며 말했다. \"어제 경마장에서 당신에게 가려고만 했는데, 그새 가 버리셨더군요. 어제는 특히 더 만나 뵙고 싶었어요. 정말 끔찍하지 않았나요?\" 그녀는 마치 자기 영혼을 온통 드러내 보이는 듯한 눈으로 안나를 바라보며 말했다.\n\n\"그래요, 그렇게 손에 땀을 쥐게 할 줄은 미처 몰랐어요.\" 안나가 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다.\n\n바로 그 순간 사람들이 정원으로 나가려고 자리에서 일어났다.\n\n\"난 안 갈래요.\" 리자가 미소 지으며 안나 곁으로 바짝 자리를 옮기면서 말했다. \"당신도 안 가실 거죠, 그렇죠? 누가 크로케 따위를 하고 싶겠어요?\"\n\n\"아, 난 그거 좋아하는데요.\" 안나가 말했다.\n\n\"세상에, 어쩌면 당신은 그 무엇에도 지루해하는 법이 없죠? 당신을 보고 있으면 즐거워요. 당신은 생기가 넘치는데, 난 지루하기만 하니.\"\n\n\"어떻게 지루할 수가 있어요? 페테르부르크에서 제일 활기찬 모임에서 지내시면서.\" 안나가 말했다.\n\n\"어쩌면 우리 모임에 끼지 못한 사람들이 더 지루할지도 모르죠. 하지만 우리는—적어도 나는—행복하지 않아요, 그저 지독하게, 지독하게 지루할 뿐이에요.\"\n\n사포는 담배를 피우며 두 젊은 남자와 함께 정원으로 나갔다. 베치와 스트레모프는 다탁에 남았다.\n\n\"아니, 지루하다니!\" 베치가 말했다. \"사포 말로는 어젯밤 당신 집에서 다들 엄청나게 즐거워했다던데.\"\n\n\"아, 얼마나 따분했는지 몰라요!\" 리자 메르칼로바가 말했다. \"경마가 끝나고 다 같이 우리 집으로 몰려갔죠. 그런데 늘 똑같은 사람들, 늘 똑같아요. 늘 똑같은 짓이죠. 저녁 내내 소파에 늘어져 빈둥거렸어요. 그런 데서 무슨 즐길 거리가 있겠어요? 안 돼요, 정말로 당신은 어떻게 지루해하지 않는지 좀 가르쳐 줘요.\" 그녀가 다시 안나를 향해 말했다. \"당신을 한 번 보기만 해도 알 수 있어요, 여기 행복하거나 불행할 수는 있어도 지루해하지는 않는 여자가 있구나, 하고요. 그 비결 좀 말해 줘요?\"\n\n\"난 아무것도 안 해요.\" 이 캐묻는 질문에 얼굴을 붉히며 안나가 대답했다.\n\n\"그게 바로 최고의 방법이죠.\" 스트레모프가 끼어들었다. 스트레모프는 머리가 희끗희끗하지만 아직 정정해 보이는 쉰 살의 남자로, 무척 못생겼으나 개성 있고 영리한 얼굴을 하고 있었다. 리자 메르칼로바는 그의 아내의 조카딸이었고, 그는 한가한 시간을 죄다 그녀와 함께 보냈다. 정부 내에서 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 정적이었던 그는, 안나 카레니나를 만나자, 영리하고 세상 물정에 밝은 사람답게, 정적의 아내인 그녀에게 유난히 다정하게 굴려고 애썼다.\n\n\"'아무것도 안 한다'는 것.\" 그가 묘한 미소를 지으며 끼어들었다. \"그것이야말로 최고의 방법이죠. 내가 진작에 말했잖아요.\" 그가 리자 메르칼로바를 향해 돌아서며 말했다. \"지루해지고 싶지 않으면, 지루해질 거라고 생각하지 말아야 한다고. 그건 마치 잠을 못 이룰까 봐 걱정한다면 잠들지 못할까 두려워하지 말아야 하는 것과 같죠. 안나 아르카지예브나가 방금 한 말이 바로 그거예요.\"\n\n\"내가 그런 말을 했다면 정말 기쁘겠네요, 영리할 뿐만 아니라 진실이기도 하니까요.\" 안나가 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"아니에요, 그러면 왜 사람이 잠을 이루지 못하고, 또 지루해할 수밖에 없는지 좀 말해 줘요?\"\n\n\"잘 자려면 일을 해야 하고, 즐기려면 그것도 일을 해야 하는 거죠.\"\n\n\"내 일이 아무에게도 쓸모가 없는데 무엇 때문에 일을 해야 하죠? 그렇다고 알면서 그런 척 꾸며 댈 수도 없고, 그러고 싶지도 않아요.\"\n\n\"당신은 구제 불능이군요.\" 스트레모프가 그녀를 보지도 않은 채 말하고는, 다시 안나에게 말을 걸었다. 안나를 만나는 일이 드물었던 터라 그녀에게는 의례적인 말밖에 할 수 없었지만, 그는 언제 페테르부르크로 돌아오느냐, 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인이 그녀를 얼마나 좋아하느냐 따위의 그런 의례적인 말들을, 온 영혼을 다해 그녀를 기쁘게 해 주고 그녀에 대한 존경을, 아니 그 이상의 무언가를 보여 주고 싶어 하는 듯한 표정으로 늘어놓았다.\n\n투시케비치가 들어와, 사람들이 크로케를 시작하려고 나머지 선수들을 기다리고 있다고 알렸다.\n\n\"안 돼요, 가지 마세요, 제발요.\" 안나가 가려 한다는 것을 듣고 리자 메르칼로바가 애원했다. 스트레모프도 그녀의 간청에 가세했다.\n\n\"이런 자리에서 늙은 브레데 부인에게로 가다니, 너무 격한 전환이지요.\" 그가 말했다. \"게다가 그분에게는 한낱 험담거리만 안겨 줄 뿐이지만, 여기서는 더없이 고상하고 정반대되는 온갖 감정만을 불러일으키시니까요.\" 그가 그녀에게 말했다.\n\n안나는 잠시 망설이며 생각에 잠겼다. 이 영리한 남자의 아첨하는 말들, 리자 메르칼로바가 보여 주는 순진하고 어린아이 같은 애정, 그리고 그녀가 익숙해져 있던 그 모든 사교계의 분위기—그 모든 것이 너무도 편안했고, 그녀를 기다리고 있는 것은 너무도 괴로운 일이어서, 그녀는 잠시 동안 여기 남을지 말지, 그 고통스러운 해명의 순간을 조금 더 미룰지 말지 망설였다. 그러나 만약 자기가 어떤 결단도 내리지 못한 채 집에 홀로 있을 때 무엇이 기다리고 있을지를 떠올리고, 두 손으로 머리칼을 움켜쥐던 그 몸짓—기억 속에서조차 끔찍한 그 몸짓을 떠올리고는, 작별 인사를 하고 자리를 떴다.\n\n# Chapter 19\n\n브론스키는 겉으로는 사교계에서 경박한 생활을 하는 듯 보였지만, 사실 질서가 흐트러지는 것을 몹시 싫어하는 사람이었다. 젊은 시절 근위 시동대(侍童隊)에 있을 때, 곤경에 빠져 돈을 빌리려다 거절당하는 굴욕을 겪은 적이 있었는데, 그 후로는 단 한 번도 같은 처지에 자신을 두지 않았다.\n\n자기 일들을 어느 정도 정리된 상태로 유지하기 위해, 그는 일 년에 다섯 번쯤(형편에 따라 더 잦기도 하고 더 드물기도 했다) 혼자 틀어박혀서 모든 일을 분명한 형태로 정리하곤 했다. 그는 이것을 자신의 결산일, 또는 faire la lessive라고 부르곤 했다.\n\n* faire la lessive: 프랑스어로 '빨래하기', 즉 깨끗이 정리한다는 뜻.\n경마가 있던 다음 날 잠에서 깬 브론스키는 흰 리넨 코트를 걸치고, 면도도 하지 않고 목욕도 하지 않은 채 탁자 위에 돈과 청구서와 편지들을 펼쳐 놓고 일에 착수했다. 이런 때 그가 신경이 날카롭다는 것을 잘 아는 페트리츠키는, 잠에서 깨어나 동료가 책상 앞에 앉아 있는 것을 보자 조용히 옷을 챙겨 입고는 그를 방해하지 않고 밖으로 나갔다.\n\n자신을 둘러싼 온갖 조건의 복잡함을 그 미세한 세부까지 속속들이 아는 사람은, 그 조건들의 복잡함과 그것을 정리하는 일의 어려움이 무언가 예외적이고 개인적인 것, 자기에게만 고유한 것이라고 상상하지 않을 수 없으며, 다른 사람들도 자기와 똑같이 복잡하게 얽힌 개인적인 일들에 둘러싸여 있으리라고는 결코 생각하지 못한다. 브론스키에게도 정말 그렇게 여겨졌다. 그래서 내심 적잖은 자부심을 느끼며, 또 근거 없이는 아니게, 그는 다른 어떤 사람이라도 이런 어려운 처지에 놓였더라면 진작에 곤경에 빠졌을 것이고, 무언가 떳떳하지 못한 길로 내몰렸을 것이라고 생각했다. 하지만 브론스키는 곤경에 빠지지 않으려면 지금이야말로 특히 자기 처지를 정리하고 분명히 해 둘 필요가 있다고 느꼈다.\n\n브론스키가 가장 손쉬운 것으로 여겨 제일 먼저 손을 댄 것은 금전 문제였다. 자기가 빚진 모든 것을 작은 글씨로 노트에 적어 내려간 뒤 그 액수를 합산해 보니, 빚이 만 칠천 루블에 몇백 루블이 더 되었는데, 그는 명확함을 위해 몇백 루블은 빼 버렸다. 자기 돈과 은행 통장을 따져 보니, 남은 것이 천팔백 루블이었고, 새해가 되기 전에는 들어올 돈이 아무것도 없었다. 빚 목록을 다시 한번 따져 본 다음, 브론스키는 그것을 세 가지로 나누어 옮겨 적었다. 첫째 항목에는 당장 갚아야 할 빚, 혹은 어쨌든 지불을 요구받았을 때 한순간도 지체 없이 갚을 수 있도록 돈을 마련해 두어야 하는 빚을 넣었다. 그런 빚은 약 사천 루블이었다. 말 값으로 천오백 루블, 그리고 젊은 동료 베노프스키의 보증으로 이천오백 루블이었는데, 베노프스키는 브론스키가 보는 앞에서 어떤 사기 도박꾼에게 그 돈을 잃었던 것이다. 브론스키는 그때 그 돈을 갚으려 했지만(당시에는 그만한 돈이 있었다), 베노프스키와 야쉬빈이 노름을 하지 않은 브론스키가 아니라 자기들이 갚겠다고 우겼다. 거기까지는 좋았지만, 브론스키는 이 더러운 일에서—비록 자기 몫이라곤 입으로 베노프스키의 보증을 서겠다고 한 것뿐이었지만—그 이천오백 루블을 마련해 두어, 그 사기꾼에게 던져 주고는 더 이상 그자와 말을 섞지 않을 수 있어야 한다는 것을 알고 있었다. 그래서 이 첫째이자 가장 중요한 항목을 위해 그는 사천 루블이 필요했다. 둘째 항목은—팔천 루블로—덜 중요한 빚이었다. 이것들은 주로 경주마와 관련된 외상이었는데, 귀리와 건초를 대는 상인, 영국인 마구상 따위에게 진 빚이었다. 마음을 온전히 놓으려면 이 빚들에 대해서도 이천 루블쯤은 갚아야 했다. 마지막 항목의 빚은—상점, 호텔, 양복점 따위에 진 것으로—고려하지 않아도 될 만한 것이었다. 그러니까 그는 당장 쓸 비용으로 적어도 육천 루블이 필요했는데, 가진 것은 천팔백 루블뿐이었다. 모두가 브론스키의 수입으로 잡고 있던 십만 루블의 연수입을 가진 사람에게라면, 이런 빚쯤이야 별로 거북할 게 없으리라고 여길 법했다. 하지만 사실 그는 십만 루블을 가지기는커녕 어림도 없었다. 그것 하나만으로도 해마다 이십만 루블의 수입을 내던 아버지의 막대한 재산은 형제 사이에 나뉘지 않은 채로 남아 있었다. 형이 빚더미에 올라앉은 채로, 한 푼의 재산도 없는 데카브리스트의 딸인 바랴 치르코바 공작 영애와 결혼했을 때, 알렉세이는 아버지 영지에서 나오는 수입을 거의 전부 형에게 넘겨주고, 자기 몫으로는 거기서 연 이만오천 루블만 남겨 두었다. 그때 알렉세이는 형에게, 그 정도면 자기가 결혼할 때까지는 충분할 것이고, 결혼은 아마 영영 하지 않을 것이라고 말했다. 그리고 가장 돈이 많이 드는 연대 중 하나를 지휘하던 데다 막 결혼한 참이던 형은 그 선물을 거절할 수 없었다. 자기만의 별도 재산을 가지고 있던 어머니는, 알렉세이가 따로 남겨 둔 이만오천 루블 외에 해마다 이만 루블을 더 보태 주었고, 알렉세이는 그것을 모조리 써 버렸다. 근래에 들어 어머니는 그의 연애 사건과 모스크바를 떠난 일로 그에게 화가 나서 돈을 보내는 것을 그만두었다. 그래서 한 해에 사만오천 루블 규모로 살아 버릇하던 브론스키는 그해에는 이만 루블밖에 받지 못한 탓에 지금 곤경에 처하게 된 것이었다. 이 곤경에서 벗어나기 위해 어머니에게 돈을 청할 수는 없었다. 전날 받은 어머니의 마지막 편지는, 사교계와 군대에서 출세하도록 그를 도울 마음은 얼마든지 있지만, 점잖은 사회 전체에 추문이 되는 그런 생활을 하도록 도울 마음은 없다는 암시로 그를 유독 격분하게 만들었다. 자기를 돈으로 사들이려는 어머니의 시도가 그의 가슴을 후벼 파, 그 어느 때보다 어머니에게 더 차갑게 느끼게 만들었다. 하지만 일단 입 밖에 낸 그 너그러운 말을 이제 와서 거둬들일 수는 없었다. 카레니나 부인과의 밀회가 어떤 결말로 흘러갈지 막연히 예감하면서, 그 너그러운 말이 경솔하게 내뱉어진 것이며, 자기가 결혼하지 않더라도 십만 루블의 수입이 전부 필요할지도 모른다고 지금은 느끼고 있었지만 말이다. 그렇지만 거둬들이는 것은 불가능했다. 형수를 떠올리기만 하면, 그 사랑스럽고 유쾌한 바랴가 기회가 닿을 때마다 자기가 그의 너그러움을 기억하고 있으며 고맙게 여기고 있다는 것을 그에게 일깨우려 애쓰던 모습을 떠올리기만 하면, 자기가 준 선물을 도로 거둬들이는 것이 얼마나 불가능한 일인지 절감할 수 있었다. 그것은 여자를 때리거나, 도둑질을 하거나, 거짓말을 하는 것만큼이나 불가능한 일이었다. 할 수 있고 또 해야 할 일은 단 하나뿐이었고, 브론스키는 한순간의 망설임도 없이 그것을 결심했다. 고리대금업자에게서 만 루블을 빌리는 일이었는데, 이는 아무런 어려움도 없는 일이었다. 그리고 씀씀이를 전반적으로 줄이고, 경주마를 파는 것이었다. 이렇게 결심하고 나서, 그는 한 번 이상 사람을 보내 자기에게 말을 팔라고 청했던 롤란다크에게 곧장 쪽지를 써 보냈다. 그런 다음 영국인과 고리대금업자를 부르러 보내고, 갚으려고 마음먹은 외상에 따라 가진 돈을 나누었다. 이 일을 끝낸 뒤, 그는 어머니에게 차갑고 매정한 답장을 썼다. 그러고 나서 수첩에서 안나의 쪽지 세 장을 꺼내 다시 읽고는 불태워 버렸고, 전날 나눈 대화를 떠올리며 깊은 생각에 잠겼다.\n\n# Chapter 20\n\n브론스키의 삶이 유난히 행복했던 까닭은, 그에게 자신이 무엇을 해야 하고 무엇을 하지 말아야 하는지를 한 치의 어긋남도 없이 확실하게 규정해 주는 원칙의 규범이 있었기 때문이다. 이 원칙의 규범은 극히 좁은 범위의 우발적 상황만을 다루었지만, 그래도 그 원칙들은 결코 의심스럽지 않았으며, 브론스키는 그 범위 밖으로 나가는 일이 없었기에 자신이 마땅히 해야 할 일을 하는 데 한순간도 망설인 적이 없었다. 이 원칙들은 불변의 규칙으로 다음과 같은 것들을 정해 놓았다. 노름 사기꾼에게는 돈을 갚아야 하지만 재단사에게는 갚지 않아도 된다는 것, 남자에게는 결코 거짓말을 해서는 안 되지만 여자에게는 해도 된다는 것, 누구도 속여서는 안 되지만 남편은 속여도 된다는 것, 모욕은 결코 용서해서는 안 되지만 모욕을 줄 수는 있다는 것, 그 밖에도 이와 비슷한 것들이었다. 이 원칙들은 어쩌면 합리적이지도 않고 좋은 것도 아니었으나, 한 치의 어긋남도 없이 확실했고, 그것을 지키는 한 브론스키는 자기 마음이 평온하며 떳떳하게 고개를 들 수 있다고 느꼈다. 다만 아주 최근에 들어서야 안나와의 관계에 관한 한, 브론스키는 자신의 원칙의 규범이 일어날 수 있는 모든 우발적 상황을 온전히 아우르지는 못한다는 것을 느끼기 시작했고, 앞으로 닥칠 어려움과 곤혹스러움 가운데 자신을 이끌어 줄 어떤 실마리도 찾을 수 없는 것들을 예감하기 시작했다.\n\n지금 안나와, 그리고 그녀의 남편과 맺고 있는 관계는 그의 마음속에서 분명하고 단순했다. 그것은 그가 따르는 원칙의 규범 안에 또렷하고 정확하게 규정되어 있었다.\n\n그녀는 자신의 사랑을 그에게 바친 명예로운 여인이었고, 그도 그녀를 사랑했으며, 따라서 그가 보기에 그녀는 합법적인 아내와 똑같은, 아니 그 이상의 존중을 받을 권리가 있는 여인이었다. 그는 말 한마디로, 암시 하나로 그녀를 모욕하거나, 여인이 기대할 수 있는 가장 온전한 존중에 조금이라도 미치지 못하는 것보다는 차라리 자기 손이 잘리는 편을 택했을 것이다.\n\n사교계에 대한 그의 태도 또한 분명했다. 누구나 그것을 알 수도, 짐작할 수도 있었지만, 누구도 감히 그것을 입에 올려서는 안 되었다. 만약 누군가가 그렇게 한다면, 그는 입을 놀리려는 자들을 모조리 침묵시키고 자신이 사랑하는 여인의 존재하지도 않는 명예를 존중하게 만들 각오가 되어 있었다.\n\n남편에 대한 그의 태도는 그중에서도 가장 분명했다. 안나가 브론스키를 사랑한 순간부터 그는 그녀에 대한 자신의 권리를 결코 침해할 수 없는 유일한 것으로 여겨 왔다. 그녀의 남편은 그저 군더더기 같고 성가신 사람일 뿐이었다. 물론 그가 가엾은 처지에 놓인 것은 사실이었지만, 그것을 어찌하겠는가? 남편에게 권리가 있는 단 한 가지는 손에 무기를 들고 결투를 요구하는 것뿐이었고, 브론스키는 언제라도 거기에 응할 준비가 되어 있었다.\n\n그러나 최근 들어 그와 그녀 사이에 새로운 내면의 관계가 생겨났고, 그것은 그 불확실함으로 브론스키를 두렵게 했다. 바로 전날 그녀는 그에게 자신이 아이를 가졌다고 말했던 것이다. 그리고 그는 이 사실과 그녀가 자신에게 기대하는 것이, 그가 지금껏 인생을 살아오면서 길잡이로 삼아 온 그 원칙의 규범 안에 온전히 규정되어 있지 않은 무언가를 요구한다고 느꼈다. 그리고 그는 실제로 허를 찔렸던 것이고, 그녀가 자신의 처지에 대해 처음 이야기를 꺼낸 순간, 그의 마음은 남편을 떠나라고 그녀에게 청하라고 충동했다. 그는 그렇게 말했지만, 이제 곰곰이 생각해 보니 그것은 어떻게든 피하는 편이 낫겠다는 것이 분명히 보였다. 그러면서도 동시에, 스스로에게 그렇게 말하면서도, 그것이 잘못된 일은 아닐까 두려웠다.\n\n\"만약 그녀에게 남편을 떠나라고 한다면, 그건 그녀의 삶을 내 삶과 하나로 합친다는 뜻이겠지. 나는 그럴 준비가 되어 있나? 돈도 없는 지금 어떻게 그녀를 데려올 수 있겠어? 설령 어떻게든 마련한다 해도…… 하지만 내가 복무 중인데 어떻게 그녀를 데려온단 말인가? 만약 그렇게 말한다면, 그걸 실행할 준비가 되어 있어야 한다는 거지. 그러니까 돈이 있어야 하고 군대에서 물러나야 한다는 거야.\"\n\n그러고는 생각에 잠겼다. 군에서 물러날 것인가 말 것인가 하는 문제는 그를 또 다른, 그리고 어쩌면 그의 인생에서 가장 중요한, 그러나 감추어진 관심사로 이끌었다. 그것은 그 자신 말고는 아무도 알지 못하는 것이었다.\n\n야망은 그의 젊은 시절과 어린 시절부터 품어 온 오랜 꿈이었고, 자기 자신에게조차 고백하지 않은 꿈이었지만, 그 꿈이 어찌나 강렬했던지 이제는 이 열망이 그의 사랑과 싸움을 벌이고 있을 정도였다. 사교계와 군무에서 그가 내디딘 첫걸음은 성공적이었으나, 이 년 전 그는 큰 실수를 저질렀다. 자신의 독립성을 보이고 출세하고 싶은 마음에, 그는 제안받은 자리를 거절했고, 그 거절이 자신의 가치를 높여 주리라 기대했다. 그러나 너무 대담했던 것으로 드러났고, 그는 밀려나고 말았다. 그리고 좋든 싫든 독립적인 인간이라는 입지를 스스로 떠맡은 그는, 누구에게도 원한을 품지 않고, 자신이 어떤 식으로든 해를 입었다고 여기지도 않으며, 그저 즐겁게 지내고 있으니 그냥 내버려 두기만을 바란다는 듯이 처신하면서, 대단한 기지와 분별로 그 입지를 능숙하게 감당해 냈다. 사실 그는 그보다 훨씬 전부터, 그러니까 모스크바로 떠났던 작년부터 이미 즐거움을 느끼지 못하게 되었다. 무엇이든 할 수 있었으나 아무것도 하려 하지 않는 사람이라는 이 독립적인 태도가 벌써 시들해지기 시작했다는 것을, 그리고 많은 이들이 그가 솔직하고 사람 좋은 친구라는 것 말고는 사실 아무것도 할 능력이 없다고 여기기 시작했다는 것을 느꼈다. 마담 카레니나와의 관계는 그토록 많은 화제를 불러일으키고 사람들의 이목을 끌면서 그에게 새로운 명성을 안겨 주었고, 그것이 한동안 그를 갉아먹던 야망의 벌레를 달래 주었으나, 일주일 전 그 벌레는 다시 새로운 힘으로 깨어났다. 그의 어린 시절 친구이자 같은 무리, 같은 동아리에 속했고, 근위 사관학교의 동기이며, 함께 학교를 마치고 학업에서도, 체조에서도, 짓궂은 장난에서도, 영광의 꿈에서도 그의 경쟁자였던 세르푸호프스코이가, 며칠 전 중앙아시아에서 돌아왔던 것이다. 그곳에서 그는 두 계급이나 진급했고, 그토록 젊은 장군에게는 좀처럼 수여되지 않는 훈장까지 받았다.\n\n페테르부르크에 도착하자마자 사람들은 그를 새로이 떠오른 일등성이라고 입에 올리기 시작했다. 브론스키의 학교 동기이자 같은 나이였던 그는 이미 장군이 되어 있었고, 정치적 사건의 흐름에 영향을 미칠 수도 있는 지휘권을 기다리고 있었다. 반면 브론스키는 독립적이고 빛나는 인물이며 매혹적인 여인의 사랑을 받고 있긴 했어도, 그저 마음껏 독립적으로 굴도록 선선히 내버려 두어지는 일개 기병 대위에 지나지 않았다. \"물론 나는 세르푸호프스코이를 부러워하지 않고, 부러워할 수도 없어. 하지만 그의 출세는 내게 보여 주지. 그저 기회를 잘 살피기만 하면 나 같은 사람의 경력도 아주 빠르게 쌓일 수 있다는 걸. 삼 년 전만 해도 그는 지금의 나와 똑같은 처지였어. 내가 군을 떠난다면, 나는 배수의 진을 치는 셈이지. 군에 남는다면, 나는 잃을 게 없어. 그녀 스스로도 자기 처지를 바꾸고 싶지 않다고 말했잖아. 그리고 그녀의 사랑이 있으니 나는 세르푸호프스코이를 부러워할 수 없어.\" 그러고는 천천히 콧수염을 비비 꼬면서 그는 탁자에서 일어나 방 안을 거닐었다. 그의 두 눈은 유난히 밝게 빛났고, 그는 자신의 처지를 철저히 마주한 뒤에 늘 찾아오는, 그 확신에 차고 차분하며 행복한 마음 상태에 잠겨 있었다. 모든 것이 곧고 분명했다. 지난날 셈을 따져 보고 난 뒤처럼. 그는 면도를 하고, 냉수욕을 하고, 옷을 차려입은 뒤 밖으로 나갔다.\n\n# Chapter 21\n\n\"자넬 데리러 왔네. 오늘 자네 lessive가 꽤 길었더군.\" 페트리츠키가 말했다. \"그래, 다 끝났나?\"\n\n* lessive: 프랑스어로 '세탁'. 여기서는 비유적으로 '말끔히 정리하는 일'을 뜻한다.\n\"끝났네.\" 브론스키가 눈으로만 미소를 지으며 대답했다. 그러고는 마치 모든 일을 완벽하게 정돈해 놓은 뒤라 너무 과감하거나 성급한 동작 하나로도 그 질서가 흐트러지기라도 할 듯, 조심스럽게 콧수염 끝을 비비 꼬았다.\n\n\"자넨 그러고 나면 늘 목욕탕에서 막 나온 사람 같단 말이야.\" 페트리츠키가 말했다. \"그리츠키 집에서 오는 길이야\"—그들이 연대장을 부르는 별명이었다—\"다들 자넬 기다리고 있어.\"\n\n브론스키는 대꾸도 없이, 다른 무언가를 생각하며 동료를 바라보았다.\n\n\"그래, 저게 그 사람 집에서 들리는 음악인가?\" 그가 폴카와 왈츠의 익숙한 가락이 둥둥 떠 와 닿는 것에 귀를 기울이며 말했다. \"무슨 잔치라도 있어?\"\n\n\"세르푸호프스코이가 왔어.\"\n\n\"아하!\" 브론스키가 말했다. \"이런, 난 몰랐는걸.\"\n\n그의 눈 속 미소가 그 어느 때보다 환하게 빛났다.\n\n자기가 사랑 속에서 행복하다고, 그 사랑을 위해 자기 야심을 희생했다고 한번 마음을 정한 이상—어쨌든 이런 입장을 취한 이상, 브론스키는 세르푸호프스코이를 시기하거나, 그가 연대에 와서 자기에게 제일 먼저 들르지 않았다고 해서 서운해할 수가 없었다. 세르푸호프스코이는 좋은 친구였고, 그는 그가 와 준 것이 기뻤다.\n\n\"아, 정말 반가운걸!\"\n\n연대장 데민은 시골에 큰 저택 한 채를 빌려 놓고 있었다. 일행은 모두 아래층의 넓은 발코니에 있었다. 안마당에 들어선 브론스키의 눈에 맨 먼저 들어온 것은 보드카 통 곁에 늘어선, 흰 무명 외투 차림의 합창단 한 무리와, 장교들에게 둘러싸인 연대장의 건장하고 쾌활한 모습이었다. 연대장은 발코니의 첫 계단까지 나와서, 오펜바흐의 카드리유를 연주하는 악대 너머로 큰 소리를 질러 대며 팔을 휘젓고는, 한쪽에 서 있는 병사 몇몇에게 무어라 지시를 내리고 있었다. 한 무리의 병사들과 병참장교, 그리고 하급 장교 몇이 브론스키와 함께 발코니로 다가왔다. 연대장은 식탁으로 돌아갔다가 손에 큰 술잔을 들고 다시 계단으로 나와 건배를 청했다. \"우리의 옛 전우, 용맹한 장군 세르푸호프스코이 공작의 건강을 위하여. 만세!\"\n\n연대장의 뒤를 이어 세르푸호프스코이가 손에 잔을 들고 미소를 지으며 계단으로 나왔다.\n\n\"자네는 갈수록 젊어지는군, 본다렌코.\" 그가 바로 앞에 서 있는, 두 번째 복무 기간을 보내는 중인데도 여전히 젊어 보이는, 발그레한 뺨에 똘똘한 인상의 병참장교에게 말했다.\n\n브론스키가 세르푸호프스코이를 본 지 삼 년 만이었다. 그는 더 건장해졌고 구레나룻을 길렀지만, 여전히 똑같이 우아한 사람이었다. 그 얼굴과 풍채는 아름답다기보다 부드럽고 기품 있어서 한층 더 눈길을 끌었다. 브론스키가 그에게서 발견한 단 하나의 변화는, 성공을 거두고 또 모두가 자기 성공을 인정해 주리라 확신하는 사람들의 얼굴에 자리 잡는, 그 차분하면서도 끊임없이 흘러나오는 흐뭇한 빛이었다. 브론스키는 그 빛나는 분위기를 잘 알고 있었고, 세르푸호프스코이에게서 곧바로 그것을 알아보았다.\n\n세르푸호프스코이는 계단을 내려오다가 브론스키를 보았다. 기쁜 미소가 그의 얼굴을 환히 밝혔다. 그는 고개를 위로 젖히고 손에 든 잔을 흔들어 브론스키에게 인사를 건넸고, 그 몸짓으로, 입술을 쑥 내밀고 입맞춤을 기다리며 서 있는 병참장교보다 먼저 그에게 갈 수 없음을 알려 주었다.\n\n\"저기 왔구먼!\" 연대장이 소리쳤다. \"야슈빈이 그러던데 자네가 잔뜩 우울한 기분에 빠져 있다더군.\"\n\n세르푸호프스코이는 늠름한 병참장교의 촉촉하고 싱싱한 입술에 입을 맞추고, 손수건으로 입을 닦으며 브론스키에게 다가갔다.\n\n\"정말 반갑네!\" 그가 브론스키의 손을 꼭 쥐고 한쪽으로 데려가며 말했다.\n\n\"자네가 이 친구를 좀 돌봐 주게.\" 연대장이 브론스키를 가리키며 야슈빈에게 소리쳤다. 그러고는 아래쪽 병사들에게로 내려갔다.\n\n\"어제는 왜 경마에 안 왔나? 거기서 자넬 볼 줄 알았는데.\" 브론스키가 세르푸호프스코이를 찬찬히 살피며 말했다.\n\n\"가긴 갔는데 늦었어. 미안하게 됐네.\" 그가 덧붙이고는 부관 쪽으로 돌아섰다. \"이걸 내 몫으로 나눠 주게. 각자 돌아가는 만큼씩 말이야.\" 그러고는 살짝 얼굴을 붉히며 수첩에서 삼백 루블짜리 지폐를 황급히 꺼냈다.\n\n\"브론스키! 뭐 좀 먹거나 마시겠나?\" 야슈빈이 물었다. \"어이, 백작님께 먹을 것 좀 갖다 드리게! 아, 여기 있군. 자, 한잔하게!\"\n\n연대장 집의 잔치는 오래도록 이어졌다. 술이 어마어마하게 돌았다. 그들은 세르푸호프스코이를 공중에 던졌다가 다시 받아 내기를 몇 번이고 되풀이했다. 그다음에는 연대장에게도 똑같이 했다. 그러고 나서는 악대의 반주에 맞춰 연대장이 직접 페트리츠키와 춤을 추었다. 이윽고 기운이 빠지기 시작한 연대장은 안마당의 벤치에 앉아, 야슈빈에게 러시아가 특히 기병 돌격에서 프로이센보다 우월하다는 것을 설파하기 시작했고, 한순간 흥청거림이 잦아들었다. 세르푸호프스코이는 손을 씻으러 집 안의 욕실로 들어갔다가 거기서 브론스키를 만났다. 브론스키는 머리에 물을 끼얹고 있었다. 그는 윗옷을 벗고 햇볕에 그을린 털 많은 목을 수도꼭지 아래에 댄 채, 손으로 목과 머리를 문지르고 있었다. 다 끝내고 나서 브론스키는 세르푸호프스코이 곁에 앉았다. 두 사람은 욕실의 긴 의자에 나란히 앉았고, 둘 모두에게 무척 흥미로운 대화가 시작되었다.\n\n\"난 늘 아내를 통해 자네 소식을 듣고 있었네.\" 세르푸호프스코이가 말했다. \"자네가 그 사람을 꽤 자주 만나 주었다니 기쁘군.\"\n\n\"그 사람은 바랴와 친하게 지내지. 그리고 그 두 사람이야말로 페테르부르크에서 내가 만나고 싶은 유일한 여인들이야.\" 브론스키가 미소 지으며 대답했다. 그가 미소 지은 것은 대화가 어떤 화제로 흘러갈지 미리 짐작했고, 또 그게 반가웠기 때문이다.\n\n\"유일한 사람들이라고?\" 세르푸호프스코이가 미소 지으며 되물었다.\n\n\"그래. 그리고 나도 자네 소식을 들었지. 하지만 자네 부인을 통해서만은 아니었어.\" 브론스키가 짐짓 엄한 표정으로 그 암시를 눌러 막으며 말했다. \"자네 성공을 듣고 무척 기뻤네. 하지만 조금도 놀라지는 않았어. 난 그 이상까지도 기대했으니까.\"\n\n세르푸호프스코이가 미소를 지었다. 자기에 대한 그런 평가가 그에게는 분명 흐뭇한 것이었고, 그는 그것을 굳이 감출 필요를 느끼지 못했다.\n\n\"음, 난 반대로 더 적게 기대했지—솔직히 털어놓자면 말이야. 하지만 기뻐, 아주 기뻐. 난 야심가야. 그게 내 약점이지, 그건 인정하네.\"\n\n\"성공하지 못했더라면 자넨 그걸 인정하지 않았을지도 모르지.\" 브론스키가 말했다.\n\n\"그렇진 않을걸세.\" 세르푸호프스코이가 다시 미소 지으며 말했다. \"그게 없으면 살 가치가 없다고까진 안 하겠네. 그래도 따분하긴 할 거야. 물론 내가 틀렸을 수도 있지만, 난 내가 택한 길에 어느 정도 소질이 있다고 생각해. 그리고 내 손에 들어온 권력이라면, 그게 어떤 종류든, 만약 쥐어지게 된다면, 내가 아는 숱한 사람들의 손에 들어가는 것보다는 나을 거라고 보네.\" 세르푸호프스코이가 성공을 의식한 환한 빛을 띠며 말했다. \"그러니 거기에 가까워질수록 난 그만큼 더 흐뭇해지는 거지.\"\n\n\"자네한테는 그게 사실일지 몰라도 모두에게 그런 건 아니야. 나도 한때는 그렇게 생각했지. 하지만 지금 이렇게 살아 보니, 인생이란 게 오직 그것만을 위해 살 가치가 있는 건 아니더군.\"\n\n\"이제 나왔구먼! 드디어 나왔어!\" 세르푸호프스코이가 웃으며 말했다. \"자네 얘기를, 자네가 거절했다는 얘기를 들은 이후로 나는…… 물론 자네가 한 일은 옳다고 봐. 하지만 무슨 일이든 하는 방식이 있는 법이지. 자네 행동 자체는 훌륭했다고 생각하지만, 자네는 그걸 마땅히 했어야 할 방식대로 한 건 아니었어.\"\n\n\"이미 한 일은 돌이킬 수 없어. 그리고 자네도 알다시피 난 내가 한 일을 결코 번복하지 않아. 게다가 난 지금 아주 만족스럽게 지내고 있고.\"\n\n\"아주 만족스럽다—당분간은 그렇겠지. 하지만 자넨 그걸로 만족하지 못해. 자네 형한테라면 이런 말 안 하겠네. 그 사람은 착한 어린애야, 여기 우리 주인장처럼 말이지. 저기 또 시작이군!\" 그가 \"만세!\" 하고 터지는 함성에 귀를 기울이며 덧붙였다. \"그리고 그 사람은 행복하지. 하지만 자네는 그런 걸로 만족하지 못해.\"\n\n\"난 그게 날 만족시킨다고 말한 적 없네.\"\n\n\"그래, 하지만 그게 전부가 아니지. 자네 같은 사람들이 필요하단 말이야.\"\n\n\"누구한테?\"\n\n\"누구한테냐고? 사회한테, 러시아한테. 러시아엔 사람이 필요해. 러시아엔 당파가 필요하다고. 안 그러면 모든 게 망가지고, 또 망가져 갈 거야.\"\n\n\"무슨 말인가? 러시아 공산주의자들에 맞서는 베르테네프의 당파 말인가?\"\n\n\"아니.\" 세르푸호프스코이가 그런 터무니없는 짐작을 받은 데 짜증을 내며 미간을 찌푸렸다. \"Tout ça est une blague. 그런 건 늘 있어 왔고 앞으로도 늘 있을 거야. 공산주의자 따위는 없어. 하지만 음모를 꾸미는 자들은 유해하고 위험한 당파를 하나 만들어 낼 필요가 있는 거지. 그건 낡은 수법이야. 아니, 정작 필요한 건 자네나 나처럼 독립적인 사람들의 강력한 당파야.\"\n\n* Tout ça est une blague: 프랑스어로 '그건 전부 헛소리야'.\n\n\"하지만 어째서?\" 브론스키가 권력을 쥐고 있는 몇몇 인물을 거론했다. \"그자들은 왜 독립적인 사람들이 아닌가?\"\n\"단지 그들이 태생부터 독립적인 재산을 가지지 못했거나 못 가졌기 때문이지. 그들은 가문도 없고, 우리처럼 태양과 중심에 가까이 있어 본 적도 없어. 그들은 돈으로든 은혜로든 매수당할 수 있어. 그래서 그들은 어떤 정책을 꾸며 내서 자기들의 버팀목으로 삼아야 하는 거지. 그러고는 자기들도 믿지 않는, 해만 끼치는 어떤 발상이나 정책을 내세우는 거야. 그 모든 정책이란 게 실은 관저 하나와 얼마간의 수입을 얻기 위한 수단일 뿐이야. Cela n’est pas plus fin que ça, 그들의 패를 슬쩍 들여다보면 말이지. 어쩌면 난 그들보다 못할지도, 더 어리석을지도 모르지. 하긴 내가 왜 그들보다 못해야 하는지는 모르겠지만. 그래도 자네와 나는 그들에 비해 한 가지 중요한 이점이 분명 있어. 우리는 매수하기가 더 어렵다는 거지. 그리고 그런 사람들이야말로 그 어느 때보다 필요하단 말이야.\"\n\n* Cela n’est pas plus fin que ça: 프랑스어로 '그건 그 이상으로 교묘한 게 아니야'.\n브론스키는 주의 깊게 들었지만, 그 말의 뜻보다는 세르푸호프스코이의 태도에 더 마음이 끌렸다. 세르푸호프스코이는 벌써 기존 권력과의 싸움을 구상하고 있었고, 그 더 높은 세계 안에 이미 자기 나름의 호오를 가지고 있었던 반면, 통치 세계에 대한 브론스키 자신의 관심은 자기 연대의 이해관계를 넘어서지 못했다. 또한 브론스키는, 세르푸호프스코이가 자기가 몸담은 세계에서는 좀처럼 만나기 힘든, 사물을 끝까지 사유하고 또 받아들이는 그 의심할 바 없는 능력과 그 지성과 언변의 재능을 통해 얼마나 강력한 인물이 될 수 있을지를 느꼈다. 그리고 그런 감정이 부끄러웠지만, 그는 시기심을 느꼈다.\n\n\"그래도 난 거기에 무엇보다 중요한 한 가지가 없네.\" 그가 대답했다. \"권력에 대한 욕망이 없어. 한때는 있었지만, 이제는 사라졌지.\"\n\n\"미안하지만, 그건 사실이 아니야.\" 세르푸호프스코이가 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 그게 사실이야, 사실이라고…… 지금은!\" 솔직하려고 브론스키가 덧붙였다.\n\n\"그래, 지금은 사실이지, 그건 또 다른 얘기야. 하지만 그 지금이 영원히 가진 않을 거야.\"\n\n\"어쩌면.\" 브론스키가 대답했다.\n\n\"자네는 어쩌면이라고 하지만.\" 세르푸호프스코이가 그의 생각을 알아챈 듯 말을 이었다. \"난 틀림없다고 하겠네. 바로 그래서 내가 자넬 보고 싶었던 거야. 자네 행동은 마땅히 그래야 할 그대로였어. 그건 나도 알아. 하지만 자넨 그걸 계속 끌고 가서는 안 돼. 난 그저 자네가 내게 carte blanche를 달라는 것뿐이야. 난 자네에게 내 비호를 베풀겠다는 게 아니야…… 하긴, 내가 자넬 비호하지 못할 이유가 또 뭐가 있나?—자네야말로 날 충분히 비호해 주지 않았던가! 우리 우정은 그런 따위는 다 넘어서는 거라고 믿고 싶군. 그래.\" 그가 여자처럼 다정한 미소를 그에게 지으며 말했다. \"내게 carte blanche를 주고, 연대에서 물러나게. 그러면 내가 자넬 슬그머니 위로 끌어올려 주겠네.\"\n* carte blanche: 프랑스어로 '전권', '백지 위임'.\n\n\"하지만 자네도 알아야 해. 난 아무것도 바라는 게 없어.\" 브론스키가 말했다. \"그저 모든 게 지금 이대로이기만을 바랄 뿐이지.\"\n\n세르푸호프스코이가 일어나 그를 마주 보고 섰다.\n\n\"자넨 모든 게 지금 이대로이기를 바란다고 하는군. 그게 무슨 뜻인지 알아. 하지만 들어 보게. 우린 동갑이고, 자넨 아마 나보다 더 많은 여자들을 알았겠지.\" 세르푸호프스코이의 미소와 몸짓은, 두려워할 것 없다고, 자기가 그 아픈 곳을 건드릴 때 다정하고 조심스럽게 굴겠다고 브론스키에게 일러 주었다. \"하지만 난 결혼한 몸이야. 그리고 내 말을 믿게나. 누군가가 말했듯, 자기 아내를 사랑한다면, 그 아내 한 사람을 속속들이 알게 됨으로써, 수천 명의 여자를 아는 것보다 모든 여자를 더 잘 알게 되는 법이지.\"\n\n\"곧 가겠소!\" 브론스키가 방을 들여다보며 두 사람을 연대장에게 오라고 부르는 장교에게 소리쳤다.\n\n브론스키는 이제 세르푸호프스코이가 무슨 말을 해 줄지 끝까지 듣고 알고 싶어 안달이 났다.\n\n\"그리고 이게 자네한테 들려줄 내 의견이야. 여자란 남자의 출세에서 가장 큰 걸림돌이지. 여자를 사랑하면서 무언가를 해낸다는 건 어려운 일이야. 사랑을 거추장스러운 방해물로 만들지 않으면서 편하게 누리는 길은 단 하나뿐이지—그게 바로 결혼이야. 어떻게, 어떻게 내 말뜻을 자네한테 설명하면 좋을까?\" 비유를 좋아하는 세르푸호프스코이가 말했다. \"잠깐만, 잠깐만! 그래, 꼭 이런 거야. fardeau를 등에 묶어 놓아야만 그걸 짊어진 채 두 손으로 무언가를 할 수 있는 것과 같지. 그게 바로 결혼이야. 결혼했을 때 내가 느낀 게 바로 그거였어. 갑자기 내 두 손이 자유로워졌거든. 하지만 결혼도 하지 않고 그 fardeau를 끌고 다니면, 두 손이 늘 꽉 차 있어서 아무것도 할 수가 없게 돼. 마잔코프를 보게, 크루포프를 보게. 그들은 여자 때문에 출세를 망쳐 버렸지.\"\n* fardeau: 프랑스어로 '짐', '부담'.\n\n\"무슨 여자들 말인가!\" 브론스키가, 그가 거론한 두 사람과 얽혀 있던 프랑스 여자와 여배우를 떠올리며 말했다.\n\n\"여자가 사교계에서 발을 더 단단히 디디고 있을수록 더 나쁜 거야. 그건 마치 그 fardeau를 두 팔로 짊어지는 것만이 아니라—다른 누군가에게서 빼앗아 오는 것과 다름없는 일이거든.\"\n\n\"자넨 한 번도 사랑해 본 적이 없군.\" 브론스키가 똑바로 앞을 바라보며 안나를 생각하면서 나직이 말했다.\n\n\"어쩌면 그럴지도. 하지만 내가 자네한테 한 말은 기억해 두게. 그리고 또 한 가지, 여자들은 죄다 남자들보다 더 물질적이야. 우린 사랑을 가지고 뭔가 어마어마한 걸 만들어 내지만, 그들은 늘 terre-à-terre거든.\"\n\n* terre-à-terre: 프랑스어로 '현실적인', '땅에 발붙인'.\n\"곧 가겠소, 곧!\" 그가 들어온 하인에게 외쳤다. 그러나 하인은 그가 생각한 것처럼 두 사람을 다시 부르러 온 게 아니었다. 하인은 브론스키에게 쪽지 하나를 가져왔다.\n\n\"트베르스카야 공작부인 댁에서 어떤 사람이 가져왔습니다.\"\n\n브론스키는 편지를 열어 보고는 얼굴이 새빨개졌다.\n\n\"머리가 아프기 시작했군. 난 집에 가야겠네.\" 그가 세르푸호프스코이에게 말했다.\n\n\"오, 그럼 잘 가게. carte blanche를 내게 주는 거지!\"\n\n\"그건 나중에 얘기하세. 페테르부르크에서 자넬 찾아가겠네.\"\n\n# Chapter 22\n\n벌써 여섯 시였고, 그래서 빨리 그곳에 가려면서도 동시에 누구나 다 아는 자기 말들을 타고 가지 않으려고, 브론스키는 야시빈이 빌린 마차에 올라타 마부에게 되도록 빨리 몰라고 일렀다. 그것은 넷이 앉을 수 있는 좌석이 딸린, 널찍하고 구식인 마차였다. 그는 한쪽 구석에 앉아 두 다리를 앞좌석에 뻗고 깊은 생각에 잠겨들었다.\n\n자기 일들이 정리된 어떤 질서에 대한 어렴풋한 느낌, 자기를 꼭 필요한 사람으로 여겨 준 세르푸호프스코이의 호의와 찬사에 대한 어렴풋한 기억, 그리고 무엇보다도 곧 있을 밀회에 대한 기대—이 모든 것이 한데 어우러져 인생에 대한 전반적이고 즐거운 감각으로 녹아들었다. 이 느낌이 어찌나 강렬했던지 그는 미소를 짓지 않을 수 없었다. 그는 두 다리를 내리고 한쪽 다리를 반대편 무릎 위에 포갠 다음, 손으로 그것을 감싸고는 어제 낙마로 까진 자리, 그 단단한 종아리 근육을 어루만졌다. 그리고 등을 뒤로 기대며 몇 번 깊게 숨을 들이쉬었다.\n\n\"행복해, 정말 행복해!\" 그는 혼잣말로 중얼거렸다. 전에도 자기 몸이 주는 이런 육체적 기쁨을 자주 느껴 본 적은 있었지만, 그 순간만큼 자기 자신을, 자기 몸을 사랑스럽게 느낀 적은 한 번도 없었다. 그는 튼튼한 다리에서 느껴지는 가벼운 통증을 즐겼고, 숨을 쉴 때마다 가슴에서 느껴지는 움직임의 근육 감각을 즐겼다. 안나에게 그토록 절망감을 안겨 주었던 밝고 차가운 팔월의 날이 그에게는 짜릿하게 자극적으로 느껴졌고, 차가운 물기로 여전히 얼얼한 그의 얼굴과 목을 산뜻하게 해 주었다. 콧수염에 바른 브릴리언틴 향기가 상쾌한 공기 속에서 유난히 기분 좋게 다가왔다. 마차 창문 너머로 보이는 모든 것이, 그 차갑고 맑은 공기 속의, 노을의 희미한 빛 속의 모든 것이, 그 자신처럼 산뜻하고 즐겁고 강건했다. 지는 햇살을 받아 반짝이는 집들의 지붕, 울타리며 건물 모서리의 날카로운 윤곽선, 행인들의 모습, 이따금 마주치는 마차들, 미동도 없는 나무와 풀의 초록빛, 감자 이랑이 고르게 그어진 밭들, 그리고 집과 나무와 덤불에서, 심지어 감자 이랑에서까지 비스듬히 드리워진 그림자들—모든 것이 막 완성되어 갓 니스를 칠한 아름다운 풍경화처럼 환하게 빛났다.\n\n\"어서, 어서!\" 그는 창밖으로 고개를 내밀며 마부에게 말했고, 주머니에서 삼 루블짜리 지폐를 꺼내 마부가 뒤를 돌아보는 순간 그것을 건넸다. 마부의 손이 등불 옆에서 무언가를 더듬거리더니, 채찍이 휙 소리를 내며 갈라졌고, 마차는 매끄러운 큰길을 따라 빠르게 굴러갔다.\n\n\"이 행복 말고는 아무것도, 아무것도 바라지 않아.\" 그는 창문 사이 공간에 달린 호출용 뼈 단추를 응시하며 생각했고, 마지막으로 보았던 그대로의 안나를 떠올렸다. \"그리고 이렇게 다가갈수록, 나는 그녀를 점점 더 사랑하게 돼. 여기가 브레데 별장의 정원이군. 그녀는 어디쯤 있을까? 어디에? 어떻게? 왜 하필 이곳을 만날 장소로 정했고, 또 왜 베치의 편지에다 써 보낸 걸까?\" 그는 이제야 처음으로 그것이 의아하게 여겨졌다. 하지만 이제 의아해할 겨를이 없었다. 그는 가로수길에 이르기 전에 마부에게 멈추라고 일렀고, 문을 열어 아직 움직이는 마차에서 뛰어내려 집으로 이어진 가로수길로 들어섰다. 가로수길에는 아무도 없었다. 그러나 오른쪽을 둘러보다가 그녀의 모습을 발견했다. 그녀의 얼굴은 베일에 가려 있었지만, 그는 그녀만의 독특한 걸음걸이며, 어깨의 기울기며, 머리를 둔 자세를 기쁨에 찬 눈으로 빨아들이듯 바라보았고, 순간 일종의 전기 충격 같은 것이 온몸을 훑고 지나갔다. 그는 새삼스러운 힘으로, 탄력 있게 움직이는 두 다리에서부터 숨을 쉴 때 부풀어 오르는 폐의 움직임에 이르기까지 자기 자신을 의식했고, 무언가가 그의 입술을 실룩이게 했다.\n\n그녀가 그에게 다가와 그의 손을 꼭 쥐었다.\n\n\"제가 사람을 보내 불러서 화나신 거예요? 도저히 당신을 만나지 않을 수 없었어요.\" 그녀가 말했고, 베일 아래로 보이는 그녀의 진지하고 굳게 다문 입매가 그의 기분을 단번에 바꿔 놓았다.\n\n\"내가 화를 내다니! 그런데 어떻게 왔어요, 어디서 온 거예요?\"\n\n\"그건 상관없어요.\" 그녀가 그의 손에 자기 손을 얹으며 말했다. \"함께 가요, 당신에게 할 말이 있어요.\"\n\n그는 무언가 일이 벌어졌으며, 이 밀회가 즐거운 것이 되지 않으리라는 걸 알아차렸다. 그녀 앞에서 그에게는 자기 의지란 것이 없었다. 그녀가 괴로워하는 까닭을 알지 못한 채로도, 그는 이미 그 똑같은 괴로움이 무의식중에 자기에게로 옮겨 오는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n\"무슨 일이에요? 무슨?\" 그는 팔꿈치로 그녀의 손을 꼭 누르며 물었고, 그녀의 얼굴에서 그 생각을 읽어 내려 애썼다.\n\n그녀는 용기를 끌어모으며 말없이 몇 걸음 걸었다. 그러다 별안간 걸음을 멈췄다.\n\n\"어제는 말하지 않았는데.\" 그녀가 가쁘고 괴롭게 숨을 몰아쉬며 입을 열었다. \"알렉세이 알렉산드로비치와 함께 집으로 돌아오면서 그에게 다 말해 버렸어요…… 그의 아내로 살 수 없다고 말했고, 그러니까…… 그에게 다 말해 버렸어요.\"\n\n그는 마치 그렇게 함으로써 그녀의 처지가 안긴 고통을 덜어 주기라도 하려는 듯, 무의식중에 온몸을 그녀에게로 기울인 채 그녀의 말을 들었다. 그러나 그녀가 이 말을 하자마자 그는 갑자기 몸을 곧추세웠고, 그의 얼굴에 자랑스럽고 굳은 표정이 떠올랐다.\n\n\"그래요, 그래, 그게 더 낫지, 천 배는 더 나아요! 얼마나 괴로웠을지 잘 알아요.\" 그가 말했다. 하지만 그녀는 그의 말을 듣고 있지 않았다. 그녀는 그의 얼굴 표정에서 그의 속생각을 읽어 내고 있었다. 그 표정이 브론스키에게 가장 먼저 떠오른 생각—이제 결투를 피할 수 없다는 생각에서 비롯되었다는 것을 그녀는 짐작할 수 없었다. 결투라는 생각은 그녀의 머릿속을 한 번도 스친 적이 없었고, 그래서 그녀는 스쳐 지나가는 이 굳은 표정을 다르게 해석했다.\n\n남편의 편지를 받았을 때, 그녀는 마음 깊은 곳에서 이미 모든 것이 예전 그대로 흘러가리라는 것을, 자기에게는 지금의 처지를 떨치고 아들을 버리고 연인과 결합할 만한 의지력이 없으리라는 것을 알았다. 트베르스카야 공작부인 댁에서 보낸 아침나절이 그녀에게 이 생각을 더욱 굳혀 주었다. 하지만 이 밀회는 그녀에게 여전히 더없이 중대한 것이었다. 그녀는 이 밀회가 자기 처지를 바꿔 놓고 자기를 구원해 주기를 바랐다. 만약 그가 이 소식을 듣고서 단호하게, 열정적으로, 한순간의 망설임도 없이 그녀에게 \"모든 것을 던져 버리고 나와 함께 갑시다!\" 하고 말해 준다면, 그녀는 아들을 버리고 그와 함께 떠나갈 것이었다. 하지만 이 소식은 그녀가 그에게서 기대했던 바를 끌어내지 못했다. 그는 그저 무슨 모욕이라도 당해 분개하는 사람처럼 보일 뿐이었다.\n\n\"저로서는 조금도 괴롭지 않았어요. 저절로 일어난 일인걸요.\" 그녀가 짜증스럽게 말했다. \"그리고 보세요……\" 그녀가 장갑 속에서 남편의 편지를 꺼냈다.\n\n\"알아요, 알아.\" 그는 편지를 받아 들었으나 읽지는 않은 채 그녀를 달래려 애쓰며 그녀의 말을 가로막았다. \"내가 그토록 바라던 단 한 가지, 내가 그토록 기도하던 단 한 가지는 이 처지를 끝장내고 내 삶을 당신의 행복에 바치는 것이었어요.\"\n\n\"왜 그런 말씀을 하세요?\" 그녀가 말했다. \"제가 그걸 의심할 거라고 생각하세요? 제가 만약 의심했다면……\"\n\n\"저기 오는 게 누구지?\" 브론스키가 자기들 쪽으로 걸어오는 두 부인을 가리키며 불쑥 말했다. \"어쩌면 우리를 아는 사람일지도!\" 그러고는 서둘러 방향을 틀어 그녀를 옆길로 이끌었다.\n\n\"오, 전 상관없어요!\" 그녀가 말했다. 그녀의 입술이 떨리고 있었다. 그리고 그는 베일 아래로 그녀의 눈이 이상한 분노를 띠고 자기를 바라보는 것 같다는 생각이 들었다. \"그게 중요한 게 아니라고 말씀드렸잖아요—그건 의심할 수 없어요. 하지만 그가 제게 뭐라고 썼는지 보세요. 읽어 보세요.\" 그녀가 다시 걸음을 멈췄다.\n\n남편과 결별한 사실을 처음 들었던 그 순간과 똑같이, 브론스키는 편지를 읽으면서 배신당한 남편에 대한 자기 관계가 불러일으킨 자연스러운 감정에 무의식중에 휩쓸렸다. 이제 손에 그 편지를 든 채로, 그는 아마도 오늘이나 내일쯤 집에서 받게 될 결투 신청장과, 결투 그 자체를 떠올리지 않을 수 없었다. 그 결투에서, 그는 지금 자기 얼굴에 떠오르고 있는 것과 똑같이 차갑고 오만한 표정으로, 먼저 허공에 한 발을 쏘아 보낸 뒤 모욕당한 남편의 총탄을 기다릴 것이다. 그리고 바로 그 순간, 세르푸호프스코이가 방금 자기에게 했던 말, 그리고 자기가 아침에 스스로 생각했던 것—자기를 얽어매지 않는 편이 낫다는 생각—이 그의 머릿속을 스쳤고, 그는 이 생각만은 그녀에게 말할 수 없다는 것을 알았다.\n\n편지를 다 읽고 나서 그는 그녀에게로 눈을 들었는데, 그 눈에는 아무런 결단도 담겨 있지 않았다. 그녀는 그가 전에 혼자서 이 일을 생각해 보았다는 것을 단번에 알아챘다. 그가 무슨 말을 하든, 자기 생각을 전부 말하지는 않으리라는 것을 그녀는 알았다. 그리고 자기의 마지막 희망이 무너져 버렸다는 것을 알았다. 이것은 그녀가 기대하고 있던 바가 아니었다.\n\n\"그가 어떤 사람인지 아시겠죠.\" 그녀가 떨리는 목소리로 말했다. \"그는……\"\n\n\"미안하지만, 나는 이게 기뻐요.\" 브론스키가 말을 가로막았다. \"제발, 끝까지 말하게 해 줘요!\" 그가 덧붙였고, 그의 눈은 자기 말을 설명할 시간을 달라고 그녀에게 애원하고 있었다. \"내가 기쁜 건, 그가 생각하는 대로 일이 그대로 머물 수는, 도저히 그대로 머물 수는 없기 때문이에요.\"\n\n\"왜 그대로 머물 수 없죠?\" 안나가 눈물을 억누르며 말했는데, 그가 하는 말에 아무런 의미도 두지 않는 것이 분명했다. 그녀는 자기 운명이 이미 정해졌다고 느꼈다.\n\n브론스키가 말하려던 것은, 그가 보기에 피할 수 없는 결투가 끝난 뒤에는 일이 예전처럼 흘러갈 수 없다는 뜻이었으나, 그는 다른 말을 했다.\n\n\"이대로 갈 수는 없어요. 이제 당신이 그를 떠나기를 바라요. 바라건대\"—그는 당황했고 얼굴을 붉혔다—\"내가 우리 삶을 정리하고 계획하도록 맡겨 줘요. 내일……\" 그가 말을 꺼냈다.\n\n그녀는 그가 말을 잇도록 두지 않았다.\n\n\"하지만 제 아이는요!\" 그녀가 비명을 질렀다. \"그가 뭐라고 썼는지 보셨잖아요! 저는 아이를 두고 떠나야 할 텐데, 저는 그럴 수 없고, 그러지도 않을 거예요.\"\n\n\"하지만, 제발, 어느 쪽이 더 낫겠어요?—아이를 두고 떠나는 것과, 이 모욕적인 처지를 계속 이어가는 것 중에?\"\n\n\"누구에게 모욕적이라는 거죠?\"\n\n\"모두에게, 그리고 무엇보다도 당신에게요.\"\n\n\"모욕적이라고 하시는데…… 그런 말은 하지 마세요. 그런 말은 제겐 아무 의미도 없어요.\" 그녀가 떨리는 목소리로 말했다. 그녀는 이제 그가 진실이 아닌 말을 하기를 바라지 않았다. 그녀에게 남은 것이라곤 그의 사랑뿐이었고, 그녀는 그를 사랑하고 싶었다. \"제가 당신을 사랑하게 된 그날부터 제게는 모든 것이 달라졌다는 걸 모르시겠어요? 제겐 단 한 가지, 오직 단 한 가지밖에 없어요—당신의 사랑이요. 그것이 제 것이라면, 저는 너무도 고양되고 너무도 강해진 기분이라서 그 무엇도 제게 굴욕이 될 수 없어요. 저는 제 처지가 자랑스러워요, 왜냐하면…… 저는 자랑스러운…… 자랑스러운……\" 그녀는 자기가 무엇을 자랑스러워하는지 말할 수 없었다. 수치와 절망의 눈물이 그녀의 말문을 막았다. 그녀는 우뚝 멈춰 서서 흐느꼈다.\n\n그 역시 무언가가 목구멍에서 부풀어 오르고 콧날이 시큰해지는 것을 느꼈고, 난생처음으로 울음이 터질 것만 같은 기분이 들었다. 무엇이 자기를 그토록 건드렸는지 그는 정확히 말할 수 없었을 것이다. 그는 그녀가 가여웠고, 자기가 그녀를 도울 수 없다는 것을 느꼈으며, 그러면서도 그녀의 비참함이 자기 탓이라는 것을, 자기가 뭔가 잘못을 저질렀다는 것을 알았다.\n\n\"이혼은 가능하지 않을까요?\" 그가 힘없이 말했다. 그녀는 대답 없이 고개를 저었다. \"아들을 데리고 와서, 그래도 그를 떠날 수는 없을까요?\"\n\n\"네, 하지만 그건 다 그에게 달렸어요. 이제 저는 그에게 가 봐야 해요.\" 그녀가 짧게 말했다. 모든 것이 다시 예전 그대로 흘러가리라는 그녀의 예감은 그녀를 속이지 않았다.\n\n\"화요일에 페테르부르크로 가겠어요, 그러면 모든 것을 정리할 수 있을 거예요.\"\n\n\"네.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"하지만 이제 그 일은 더 이상 이야기하지 말아요.\"\n\n돌려보내면서 브레데 정원의 작은 문으로 다시 오라고 일러두었던 안나의 마차가 다가왔다. 안나는 브론스키에게 작별을 고하고 집으로 향했다.\n\n# Chapter 23\n\n월요일에는 6월 2일 위원회의 정례 회의가 있었다. 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 회의가 열리는 회의실로 걸어 들어가 늘 그렇듯 위원들과 의장에게 인사한 뒤, 자기 자리에 앉아 미리 준비되어 놓여 있는 서류 위에 손을 얹었다. 이 서류들 사이에는 필요한 증거 자료와 그가 하려는 연설의 대략적인 윤곽이 들어 있었다. 하지만 그에게 이 문서들이 실제로 필요하지는 않았다. 그는 요점 하나하나를 모두 기억하고 있었으며, 자기가 말할 내용을 머릿속에서 미리 더듬어 볼 필요가 있다고 생각하지 않았다. 그는 알고 있었다. 때가 오면, 그리고 자기 앞에 마주 선 적이, 애써 무관심한 표정을 지으려 하는 그 모습을 보게 되면, 자기 연설은 지금 준비할 수 있는 것보다 한결 더 술술 흘러나오리라는 것을. 그는 자기 연설의 무게가 워낙 막중해서 한 마디 한 마디가 비중을 지니리라고 느꼈다. 그러는 동안에도 늘 하는 보고를 듣고 있는 그의 태도는 더없이 순진하고 악의 없어 보였다. 부풀어 오른 핏줄과 긴 손가락이 달린 그의 흰 손이 앞에 놓인 흰 종이의 가장자리를 더없이 부드럽게 쓰다듬는 모습이며, 한쪽으로 고개를 떨군 그 나른한 기색을 바라보고서, 몇 분 뒤 그의 입술에서 무서운 폭풍을 불러일으킬, 위원들을 소리치게 하고 서로 공격하게 만들며 의장으로 하여금 정숙을 호소하지 않을 수 없게 할 말의 격류가 쏟아져 나오리라고 의심할 사람은 아무도 없었다. 보고가 끝나자 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 차분하고 섬세한 목소리로, 원주민 부족 재편성 위원회와 관련하여 회의에 제기할 사항이 몇 가지 있다고 밝혔다. 모든 시선이 그에게 쏠렸다. 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 헛기침을 한 뒤, 자기 상대를 바라보지 않고, 연설을 할 때면 늘 그러듯이 맞은편에 앉은 첫 번째 사람을, 위원회에서 어떤 종류의 의견도 가져 본 적 없는 악의 없는 자그마한 노인을 골라 자기 견해를 펼치기 시작했다. 그가 기본적이고 근본적인 법률에 관한 대목에 이르자, 그의 상대가 벌떡 일어나 항의하기 시작했다. 역시 위원회의 일원이었고 마찬가지로 정곡을 찔려 발끈한 스트레모프가 자신을 변호하기 시작했고, 결국 폭풍 같은 회의가 이어졌다. 그러나 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 승리했고, 그의 동의안은 가결되었으며, 새로운 위원회 세 개가 임명되었다. 다음 날 페테르부르크의 어느 모임에서는 이 회의 말고는 다른 화제가 입에 오르지 않았다. 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 성공은 그가 예상했던 것보다도 더 컸다.\n\n이튿날 아침, 화요일에 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 잠에서 깨어 전날의 승리를 흐뭇하게 떠올렸고, 그의 환심을 사려고 안달이 난 부서의 수석 비서관이 위원회에서 일어난 일에 관해 자기 귀에 들어온 소문을 알려 주었을 때, 무관심한 척하려 애썼지만 미소를 짓지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n수석 비서관과 업무에 몰두해 있던 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 그날이 화요일, 곧 자기가 안나 아르카지예브나의 귀가일로 정해 둔 날이라는 것을 까맣게 잊고 있었고, 하인이 들어와 그녀의 도착을 알리자 깜짝 놀라며 짜증의 충격을 받았다.\n\n안나는 아침 일찍 페테르부르크에 도착했다. 그녀의 전보에 따라 마차가 마중 나가 있었으니, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치도 그녀의 도착을 알 수 있었을 터였다. 그러나 그녀가 도착했을 때 그는 그녀를 맞이하러 나오지 않았다. 그녀는 그가 아직 외출하지 않았으며 비서관과 일을 보고 있다는 말을 들었다. 그녀는 자기가 왔다는 전갈을 남편에게 보낸 뒤 자기 방으로 가서, 그가 자기에게 오리라 기대하며 짐을 정리하는 데 몰두했다. 그러나 한 시간이 지나도 그는 오지 않았다. 그녀는 몇 가지 지시를 한다는 핑계로 식당으로 가서, 그가 그리로 나오기를 기대하며 일부러 큰 소리로 말했다. 하지만 그가 수석 비서관과 헤어지면서 서재 문으로 가는 소리는 들렸어도, 그는 나오지 않았다. 그녀는 그가 평소 사무실로 서둘러 나간다는 것을 알고 있었기에, 그 전에 그를 만나 두 사람 사이의 태도가 분명히 정해지기를 바랐다.\n\n그녀는 응접실을 가로질러 단호하게 그에게로 갔다. 그녀가 서재로 들어갔을 때 그는 관복을 입고 있었으며, 누가 보아도 외출 준비가 끝난 채로 작은 탁자에 두 팔꿈치를 괴고 앉아 풀 죽은 얼굴로 앞을 바라보고 있었다. 그녀는 그가 자기를 보기 전에 그를 보았고, 그가 자기 생각을 하고 있다는 것을 알아차렸다.\n\n그녀를 보고서 그는 일어서려다가 마음을 바꾸었고, 이내 그의 얼굴이 화끈 달아올랐다. 안나가 한 번도 본 적 없는 일이었다. 그러더니 그는 재빨리 일어나 그녀를 맞으러 갔는데, 그녀의 눈이 아니라 그 위쪽의 이마와 머리카락을 바라보았다. 그는 그녀에게 다가가 손을 잡고 자리에 앉으라고 청했다.\n\n\"당신이 와서 정말 기쁘오.\" 그가 그녀 곁에 앉으며 말했다. 무언가 할 말이 있는 게 분명한데도 그는 말을 더듬었다. 몇 번이나 입을 떼려다가 멈추었다. 그를 만날 채비를 하면서 그를 경멸하고 비난하리라고 스스로를 다잡았건만, 그녀는 그에게 무슨 말을 해야 할지 알 수 없었고, 그가 가엾게 느껴졌다. 그래서 침묵이 한동안 이어졌다. \"세료자는 잘 있소?\" 그가 말하더니, 대답을 기다리지도 않고 덧붙였다. \"오늘은 집에서 식사를 못 하오, 지금 바로 나가 봐야 하니까.\"\n\n\"저는 모스크바로 갈까 생각했었어요.\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 당신이 온 건 아주, 아주 잘한 일이오.\" 그가 말하고는 다시 입을 다물었다.\n\n그가 대화를 시작할 힘이 없다는 것을 알아차린 그녀가 직접 입을 열었다.\n\n\"알렉세이 알렉산드로비치.\" 그녀는 그를 바라보며, 자기 머리카락을 집요하게 응시하는 그의 시선 아래서도 눈을 내리깔지 않은 채 말했다. \"저는 죄 지은 여자예요, 저는 나쁜 여자예요. 하지만 저는 그때 당신에게 말씀드린 그대로, 예전 그대로의 저예요. 그리고 저는 아무것도 바꿀 수 없다는 말을 하러 왔어요.\"\n\n\"나는 그것에 관해 당신에게 아무것도 묻지 않았소.\" 그가 불쑥, 단호하게, 증오를 담아 그녀의 얼굴을 똑바로 바라보며 말했다. \"그건 내가 짐작했던 대로였소.\" 분노의 영향 아래 그는 분명 자기 능력을 온전히 되찾은 것 같았다. \"하지만 그때 내가 당신에게 말했고 또 편지로도 썼듯이,\" 그가 가늘고 날카로운 목소리로 말했다. \"지금 다시 한번 말하겠소, 나는 이것을 알아야 할 의무가 없소. 나는 그것을 모르는 걸로 하겠소. 모든 아내가 당신처럼 친절해서 그렇게 서둘러 남편에게 그런 유쾌한 소식을 전하지는 않으니까.\" 그는 '유쾌한'이라는 말에 특별히 힘을 주었다. \"세상이 그것을 전혀 모르는 한, 내 이름이 더럽혀지지 않는 한, 나는 그것을 모르는 걸로 하겠소. 그래서 나는 그저 당신에게 알려 두겠소, 우리 관계는 늘 그래 왔던 그대로여야 하며, 당신이 나를 망신시키는 경우에 한해서만 내 명예를 지키기 위한 조처를 취하지 않을 수 없으리라고.\"\n\n\"하지만 우리 관계는 늘 그랬던 그대로일 수 없어요.\" 안나가 겁먹은 목소리로, 당혹스러운 얼굴로 그를 바라보며 입을 열었다.\n\n다시금 그 침착한 몸짓을 보고, 그 날카롭고 어린아이 같고 비아냥거리는 목소리를 듣자, 그에 대한 혐오감이 그를 향한 연민을 꺼 버렸고, 그녀는 오직 두려움만을 느꼈으나, 무슨 수를 써서라도 자기 처지를 분명히 밝히고 싶었다.\n\n\"저는 당신의 아내일 수 없어요, 제가……\" 그녀가 입을 열었다.\n\n그가 차갑고 악의에 찬 웃음을 터뜨렸다.\n\n\"당신이 선택한 삶의 방식이, 짐작건대, 당신의 생각에도 반영된 모양이군. 나는 너무나 큰 존경 혹은 경멸을, 아니면 그 둘 다를…… 나는 당신의 과거를 존경하고 당신의 현재를 경멸하오…… 그러니까 당신이 내 말에 갖다 붙인 그런 해석과는 거리가 멀었단 말이오.\"\n\n안나는 한숨을 쉬며 고개를 숙였다.\n\n\"하긴 정말이지 나로서는 이해할 수가 없소, 당신이 보이는 그 독립성으로,\" 그가 흥분해서 말을 이었다. \"—남편에게 자기 부정을 알리면서 거기서 비난받을 것을 전혀 보지 못하는 듯한 당신이, 어떻게 남편에 대한 아내의 의무를 다하는 데서는 비난받을 무언가를 볼 수 있는지.\"\n\n\"알렉세이 알렉산드로비치! 도대체 저한테 뭘 바라시는 거예요?\"\n\n\"내가 바라는 건 당신이 그 남자를 이 집에서 만나지 않는 것, 그리고 세상도 하인들도 당신을 비난할 수 없도록 처신하는 것이오…… 그를 만나지 않는 것 말이오. 그리 대단한 건 아니라고 생각하오. 그 대가로 당신은 아내의 의무를 다하지 않고도 정숙한 아내의 온갖 특권을 누릴 거요. 당신에게 할 말은 그게 전부요. 이제 나갈 시간이군. 집에서 식사는 하지 않겠소.\" 그는 일어나 문 쪽으로 걸어갔다.\n\n안나도 일어났다. 그는 말없이 고개를 숙여, 그녀가 자기보다 먼저 지나가도록 길을 비켜 주었다.\n\n# Chapter 24\n\n레빈이 건초 더미 위에서 보낸 그 밤은 그에게 아무 결과 없이 지나가지 않았다. 그동안 자기 영지를 경영해 온 방식이 그에게 역겹게 느껴졌고, 그것은 그에게 모든 매력을 잃어버렸다. 훌륭한 수확에도 불구하고, 그해처럼 그와 농민들 사이에 그토록 많은 방해와 그토록 많은 다툼이 있었던 적은—적어도 그가 보기에는—없었다. 그리고 이 실패들과 이 적대감의 근원이 이제 그에게 완전히 이해되었다. 그가 노동 그 자체에서 맛본 기쁨, 그로 인해 농민들과 한층 가까워진 것, 그가 그들에 대해, 그들의 삶에 대해 느낀 부러움, 그 삶을 받아들이고 싶다는 욕망—그것은 그날 밤 그에게 한낱 꿈이 아니라 하나의 결심이었고, 그 실행을 그는 세세하게 머릿속으로 짜 두기까지 했다—이 모든 것이 그가 경영해 온 농사에 대한 그의 시각을 완전히 뒤바꾸어 놓아서, 그는 더 이상 거기에 예전 같은 흥미를 가질 수 없었고, 또 그 모든 것의 토대를 이루는, 자기와 일꾼들 사이의 그 불쾌한 관계를 보지 않을 수 없었다. 파바 같은 개량종 암소들의 무리, 갈아엎어 비옥하게 만든 온 토지, 울타리로 둘러친 평평한 아홉 개의 밭, 거름을 듬뿍 준 이백사십 에이커, 줄뿌림으로 뿌린 씨앗, 그 밖의 모든 것—이 모든 것은, 만약 그 일이 자기들 자신을 위해, 혹은 자기들과 동료들을 위해—그들과 마음이 통하는 사람들을 위해 행해진 것이었다면 더없이 훌륭한 일이었을 것이다. 그러나 이제 그는 분명히 깨달았다(농업에 관한 책—그 책에서 농사의 주요 요소는 노동자가 되어야 했다—을 집필한 일이 이 점에서 그에게 크게 도움이 되었다). 그가 해 오던 종류의 농사란 그와 노동자들 사이의 잔혹하고 완강한 투쟁에 다름 아니며, 그 투쟁에서 한쪽—그의 쪽—에는 모든 것을 자기가 더 낫다고 여기는 방식으로 바꾸려는 끊임없고 격렬한 노력이 있었고, 다른 쪽에는 사물의 자연스러운 질서가 있었다. 그리고 이 투쟁에서 그는, 자기 쪽에서는 막대한 힘을 쏟아붓는데도, 다른 쪽에서는 아무런 노력도, 심지어 아무런 의도조차도 없는 채로, 결국 얻어지는 것이라곤 일이 어느 쪽의 마음에도 들지 않는다는 것, 그리고 훌륭한 농기구와 훌륭한 가축과 토지가 누구에게도 이로움 없이 망가진다는 것뿐임을 보았다. 무엇보다 나쁜 것은, 이 일에 쏟아부은 에너지가 단순히 낭비되는 데 그치지 않는다는 점이었다. 이 체계의 의미가 그에게 분명해진 지금, 그는 자기 에너지의 목적이 더없이 보잘것없는 것임을 느끼지 않을 수 없었다. 사실 이 투쟁은 무엇을 위한 것이었던가? 그는 자기 몫의 한 푼 한 푼을 위해 싸우고 있었다(또 그럴 수밖에 없었으니, 노력을 조금만 늦추어도 일꾼들의 품삯을 치를 돈이 없었을 테니까). 반면에 그들은 그저 자기들 일을 쉽고 기분 좋게, 다시 말해 늘 해 오던 대로 할 수 있도록 싸우고 있을 뿐이었다. 그의 이익을 위해서는 일꾼 하나하나가 가능한 한 열심히 일하고, 그러면서도 정신을 바짝 차려 키질 기계며, 말이 끄는 갈퀴며, 탈곡기를 망가뜨리지 않으려 애쓰고, 자기가 하는 일에 주의를 기울여야 했다. 일꾼이 원하는 것은 가능한 한 즐겁게, 쉬어 가며, 무엇보다도 아무 생각 없이 되는대로 무심하게 일하는 것이었다. 그해 여름 레빈은 이것을 발걸음마다 보았다. 그는 사람들을 보내 건초로 쓸 클로버를 베게 했는데, 클로버가 풀과 잡초로 뒤덮여 씨앗으로는 쓸모없는, 가장 못한 자리를 골라 지정해 주었다. 그런데도 그들은 거듭거듭 가장 좋은 클로버 밭을 베어 버리고는, 집사가 그렇게 시켰다는 핑계로 자기들을 변명하면서, 훌륭한 건초가 될 거라는 장담으로 그를 달래려 했다. 그러나 그는 그것이 그 밭이 베기에 훨씬 더 수월했기 때문임을 알고 있었다. 그가 건초를 던져 올리려고 건초 기계를 내보내자—그것은 첫 줄에서 망가지고 말았으니, 머리 위로 커다란 날개가 펄럭이는 동안 앞자리에 앉아 있는 것이 농민에게는 따분한 일이었기 때문이다. 그러자 그는 이런 말을 들었다. \"걱정 마십쇼, 나리. 아낙네들이 금방 던져 올릴 겁니다요.\" 쟁기는 사실상 쓸모가 없었으니, 일꾼이 쟁기를 돌릴 때 보습을 들어 올릴 생각을 도무지 하지 않고 억지로 돌리는 바람에 말을 혹사하고 땅을 헤집어 놓았기 때문이다. 그런데도 레빈은 그런 것에 신경 쓰지 말라는 부탁을 받았다. 말들은 밀밭으로 제멋대로 들어가도록 방치되었으니, 일꾼 가운데 단 한 명도 야간 경비를 맡으려 하지 않았기 때문이다. 그리고 그러지 말라는 명령에도 불구하고 일꾼들은 한사코 돌아가며 야간 근무를 서겠다고 고집했고, 이반은 온종일 일한 뒤에 잠들어 버리고는, 자기 잘못을 몹시 뉘우치며 이렇게 말했다. \"저를 어떻게 하시든 나리 좋으실 대로 하십쇼.\"\n\n그들은 가장 좋은 송아지 세 마리를 죽이고 말았으니, 클로버를 벤 뒤에 다시 자란 풀밭에 송아지들을 풀어 놓으면서 물 먹이는 일을 소홀히 했기 때문이다. 그런데도 송아지들이 클로버 때문에 배가 부풀어 죽었다는 것을 어떤 말로도 사람들에게 믿게 할 수 없었고, 그들은 그를 위로한답시고, 그의 이웃 가운데 한 사람은 사흘 만에 가축 백열두 마리를 잃었다고 일러 주었다. 이 모든 일은 누군가가 레빈이나 그의 농장에 악의를 품어서 일어난 것이 아니었다. 오히려 그는 그들이 자기를 좋아하고 자기를 소탈한 나리라고(그것이 그들의 최고 찬사였다) 여긴다는 것을 알고 있었다. 그저 그 일은, 그들이 원하는 것이라곤 신명 나게 되는대로 일하는 것뿐이고, 그의 이익이란 그들에게 멀고 이해할 수 없는 것일 뿐 아니라 그들의 더없이 정당한 요구와 치명적으로 어긋나는 것이었기에 일어났을 뿐이다. 오래전부터 레빈은 토지에 관한 자기 자신의 처지에 불만을 느껴 왔다. 그는 자기 배가 어디서 물이 새는지 알고 있었지만, 그 새는 곳을 찾으려 하지 않았다. 어쩌면 일부러 자기 자신을 속였는지도 모른다. (만약 그것에 대한 믿음을 잃는다면 그에게 남는 것은 아무것도 없었을 테니까.) 그러나 이제 그는 더 이상 자기 자신을 속일 수 없었다. 그가 경영해 온 방식의 농사는 그에게 단순히 매력 없는 것을 넘어 역겨운 것이 되어 버렸고, 그는 더 이상 거기에 흥미를 가질 수 없었다.\n\n이제 여기에 더하여, 불과 이십오 마일 떨어진 곳에 키티 셰르바츠카야가 있다는 사실이 끼어들었다. 그는 그녀를 보고 싶었지만 볼 수가 없었다. 다리야 알렉산드로브나 오블론스카야는 그가 그쪽에 와 있을 때 그를 초대했었다—그녀의 여동생에게 다시 청혼하라는 목적으로 오라고 했고, 그녀가 넌지시 알려 준 바로는 이제 동생이 그를 받아들이리라는 것이었다. 레빈 자신도 키티 셰르바츠카야를 보고서 자기가 그녀를 사랑하기를 한 번도 멈춘 적이 없음을 느꼈다. 그러나 그녀가 거기 있다는 것을 알면서 오블론스키 집에 갈 수는 없었다. 그가 그녀에게 청혼했고 그녀가 그를 거절했다는 사실이, 그녀와 그 사이에 넘을 수 없는 장벽을 세워 놓았던 것이다. \"그녀가 자기가 결혼하고 싶었던 남자의 아내가 될 수 없게 되었다는 이유만으로 내 아내가 되어 달라고 청할 수는 없어.\" 그는 혼잣말을 했다. 이 생각이 그를 그녀에게 차갑고 적대적으로 만들었다. \"나는 비난의 감정 없이는 그녀에게 말을 건넬 수 없을 거야. 원망 없이는 그녀를 바라볼 수 없을 테고. 그러면 그녀는 나를 더욱더 미워하겠지, 당연히 그럴 수밖에. 게다가, 다리야 알렉산드로브나가 내게 한 말을 듣고 난 지금, 내가 어떻게 그들을 보러 갈 수 있겠어? 그녀가 내게 한 말을 내가 알고 있다는 걸 드러내지 않을 수 있겠어? 그러고선 내가 너그럽게 그녀를 용서하고 가엾게 여기러 간다고! 그녀 앞에서 용서하는 연기를 하고, 황송하게도 내 사랑을 베풀어 준다고!…… 대체 무엇 때문에 다리야 알렉산드로브나는 그런 말을 내게 했을까? 우연히라면 내가 그녀를 만났을지도 모르고, 그랬다면 모든 것이 저절로 일어났을 텐데. 하지만 이렇게 된 이상, 그건 말도 안 돼, 말도 안 되는 일이야!\"\n\n다리야 알렉산드로브나가 그에게 편지를 보내, 키티가 쓸 부인용 안장을 청했다. \"당신에게 부인용 안장이 있다고 들었어요.\" 그녀는 그에게 이렇게 썼다. \"당신이 직접 그것을 가져다주기를 바라요.\"\n\n이것은 그가 견딜 수 있는 것 이상이었다. 분별 있고 섬세한 여자라면 어떻게 자기 여동생을 이토록 굴욕적인 처지에 몰아넣을 수 있단 말인가! 그는 짧은 편지 열 통을 썼다가 모조리 찢어 버리고, 아무 답장 없이 안장만 보냈다. 가겠다고 쓰는 것은 불가능했으니, 갈 수가 없었기 때문이다. 무슨 일이 생겨서 못 간다든가, 멀리 가 있겠다고 쓰는 것은 더더욱 나빴다. 그는 답장 없이 안장만 보냈고, 무언가 부끄러운 짓을 했다는 느낌이 들었다. 그는 이제 역겨워진 영지의 모든 일을 집사에게 넘기고, 이튿날 멀리 떨어진 군으로 친구 스비야시스키를 만나러 떠났다. 그의 이웃에는 멧닭이 가득한 훌륭한 늪지가 있었고, 그는 최근에 오래전부터 해 둔 약속대로 자기 집에 와서 묵으라는 편지를 레빈에게 보내왔던 것이다. 수로프스키 군에 있는 그 멧닭 늪지는 오래전부터 레빈을 유혹해 왔지만, 그는 영지 일 때문에 이 방문을 계속 미뤄 왔다. 이제 그는 셰르바츠키 집안과의 이웃 관계로부터, 그리고 더더욱 자기 농장 일로부터 벗어나게 된 것이 기뻤다. 특히 사냥 나들이라면 더욱 그러했으니, 그것은 곤경에 처할 때마다 늘 최고의 위안이 되어 주었다.\n\n# Chapter 25\n\n수롭스키 군에는 철도도 역마차도 없어서, 레빈은 자기 말들을 매단 크고 구식인 마차를 타고 그곳으로 향했다.\n\n그는 가는 길의 절반쯤 되는 곳에서 부유한 농부의 집에 들러 말들에게 먹이를 주었다. 대머리에 정정한 노인이, 뺨에는 희끗한 기운이 도는 넓고 붉은 수염을 기른 채 대문을 열고는, 세 마리 말이 지나가도록 문기둥에 몸을 바싹 붙였다. 노인은 마부에게 헛간 아래 자리를 일러 주었는데, 크고 깨끗하고 정돈된 마당에는 그을린 구식 쟁기들이 놓여 있었고, 노인은 레빈에게 거실로 들어오라고 청했다. 단정하게 차려입은 젊은 여인이 맨발에 나막신을 신고 새로 들인 바깥방 바닥을 닦고 있었다. 그녀는 레빈을 뒤따라 뛰어든 개를 보고 겁을 먹어 비명을 질렀지만, 개가 해치지 않을 거라는 말을 듣자 곧 자기가 놀란 것이 우스워 웃기 시작했다. 그녀는 맨팔로 레빈에게 거실로 들어가는 문을 가리키고는, 다시 몸을 숙여 잘생긴 얼굴을 가린 채 바닥을 계속 닦았다.\n\n\"사모바르를 올릴까요?\" 그녀가 물었다.\n\n\"네, 부탁합니다.\"\n\n거실은 큰 방이었는데, 네덜란드식 난로가 있었고 칸막이 하나가 방을 둘로 나누고 있었다. 성화 아래에는 무늬가 그려진 탁자와 긴 의자, 의자 두 개가 놓여 있었다. 입구 가까이에는 그릇이 가득한 찬장이 있었다. 덧창이 닫혀 있어 파리가 거의 없었고, 어찌나 깨끗한지 레빈은 길을 달려오며 웅덩이에서 멱을 감은 라스카가 바닥을 더럽힐까 봐 걱정되어, 문가 구석 자리로 가 있으라고 명했다. 거실을 둘러본 뒤 레빈은 뒷마당으로 나갔다. 나막신을 신은 그 잘생긴 젊은 여인이 멜대에 빈 물통을 흔들흔들 매달고 그보다 앞서 물을 길으러 우물로 달려갔다.\n\n\"서둘러라, 얘야!\" 노인이 그녀의 뒤에 대고 기분 좋게 소리치고는 레빈에게 다가왔다. \"그런데 나리, 니콜라이 이바노비치 스비야시스키 댁으로 가시는 길입니까? 그분도 우리 집에 들르신답니다.\" 그는 계단 난간에 팔꿈치를 괴고 이야기를 늘어놓기 시작했다. 노인이 스비야시스키와 알고 지낸 사연을 한창 이야기하던 중에 대문이 다시 삐걱거리더니, 일꾼들이 나무 쟁기와 써레를 들고 들에서 마당으로 들어왔다. 쟁기와 써레에 매인 말들은 윤기가 흐르고 살이 올라 있었다. 일꾼들은 한눈에 봐도 그 집 식구들이었다. 둘은 무명 셔츠를 입고 모자를 쓴 젊은이였고, 다른 둘은 손으로 짠 셔츠를 입은 고용 일꾼으로, 하나는 노인이고 다른 하나는 젊은 사내였다. 노인은 계단에서 물러나 말들에게 다가가 마구를 벗기기 시작했다.\n\n\"무얼 갈았소?\" 레빈이 물었다.\n\n\"감자밭을 갈았습니다. 우리도 땅을 좀 빌려 부치고 있지요. 페도트, 거세마는 풀어놓지 말고 여물통으로 끌고 가거라, 다른 놈을 마구에 맬 테니.\"\n\n\"아버지, 제가 주문한 보습 말이에요, 그자가 가져왔나요?\" 한눈에 노인의 아들인 게 분명한 덩치 좋고 건강해 보이는 사내가 물었다.\n\n\"저기…… 바깥방에 있다.\" 노인은 벗겨 낸 마구를 한데 뭉쳐 땅바닥에 내던지며 대답했다. \"저 사람들 밥 먹는 동안 네가 끼워 놓으려무나.\"\n\n잘생긴 젊은 여인이 가득 찬 물통을 어깨에 늘어뜨린 채 바깥방으로 들어왔다. 어디선가 여인들이 더 나타났는데, 젊고 잘생긴 여자, 중년 여자, 늙고 못생긴 여자, 아이를 데린 여자, 아이 없는 여자가 뒤섞여 있었다.\n\n사모바르가 노래하기 시작했다. 말들을 처리한 일꾼들과 식구들이 밥을 먹으러 들어왔다. 레빈은 마차에서 먹을거리를 꺼내 와서는 노인에게 함께 차를 들자고 청했다.\n\n\"글쎄요, 오늘은 벌써 좀 마셨습니다만.\" 노인은 한눈에도 기뻐하며 그 청을 받아들였다. \"그래도 어울리는 셈치고 한 잔만 하지요.\"\n\n차를 마시는 동안 레빈은 노인의 농사에 관한 이야기를 죄다 들었다. 십 년 전에 노인은 땅을 소유한 부인에게서 삼백 에이커를 빌렸고, 일 년 전에는 그 땅을 사들인 데다 이웃 지주에게서 또 삼백 에이커를 빌렸다. 그 땅의 일부, 그러니까 가장 나쁜 부분은 세를 놓고, 백 에이커의 경작지는 가족과 고용 일꾼 둘과 함께 손수 일구었다. 노인은 형편이 나쁘다고 푸념했다. 하지만 레빈이 보기에 그것은 그저 예의상 하는 말일 뿐, 그의 농장은 번창하고 있었다. 만약 농사가 잘 안 됐다면 에이커당 삼십오 루블에 땅을 사들이지도, 세 아들과 조카를 장가들이지도, 불이 난 뒤 두 번이나, 그것도 매번 더 큰 규모로 집을 다시 짓지도 않았을 것이다. 노인은 푸념을 늘어놓았지만, 그가 자기의 번영을 자랑스러워하며, 그것도 정당하게 자랑스러워하고 있다는 것은 분명했다. 그는 아들들과 조카, 며느리들, 자기 말들과 소들을 자랑스러워했고, 무엇보다 이 모든 농사를 두루 꾸려 가고 있다는 사실을 자랑스러워했다. 노인과 이야기를 나누면서 레빈은 그가 새로운 방식에도 거부감이 없다는 것을 알았다. 그는 감자를 아주 많이 심었는데, 레빈이 지나오며 보았듯이 그의 감자는 이미 꽃이 지고 시들기 시작한 반면, 레빈의 감자는 이제 막 꽃이 피고 있었다. 그는 이웃 지주에게서 빌린 신식 쟁기로 감자에 흙을 북돋워 주었다. 그는 밀을 뿌렸다. 노인이 호밀을 솎으면서 솎아 낸 호밀을 말 먹이로 쓴다는 사소한 사실이 특히 레빈의 마음을 쳤다. 레빈은 이 훌륭한 사료가 버려지는 것을 여러 차례 보았고, 그것을 거두려고 애써 봤지만, 번번이 불가능한 것으로 드러났었다. 그런데 이 농부는 그것을 해내고 있었고, 그것을 가축 먹이로 칭찬하는 데 입에 침이 마를 줄 몰랐다.\n\n\"계집애들이 무얼 하겠습니까? 그것을 다발로 묶어 길가로 날라 오면, 수레가 실어 오지요.\"\n\n\"그런데 우리 지주들은 일꾼들을 데리고 일이 잘 안 풀리는군요.\" 레빈이 그에게 차 한 잔을 건네며 말했다.\n\n\"고맙습니다.\" 노인은 잔을 받았지만, 남겨 둔 설탕 덩어리를 가리키며 더는 사양했다. \"그 작자들은 그저 폐물이지요.\" 그가 말했다. \"이를테면 스비야시스키 댁을 보십시오. 그 땅이 어떤 땅인지 우리야 알지요. 일등급인데도 자랑할 만한 수확이 별로 없습니다. 충분히 돌보질 않아서지요, 그게 다입니다!\"\n\n\"하지만 노인장도 고용 일꾼들을 데리고 땅을 일구지 않습니까?\"\n\n\"우리야 다 같이 농사꾼 아닙니까. 무슨 일이든 직접 나섭니다. 쓸모없는 사람은 가면 그만이고, 우리끼리 꾸려 갈 수 있지요.\"\n\n\"아버지, 피노겐이 타르를 좀 달래요.\" 나막신을 신은 젊은 여인이 들어오며 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 그래, 그렇다니까요, 나리!\" 노인은 일어나 천천히 성호를 그은 뒤, 레빈에게 고맙다는 인사를 하고 나갔다.\n\n레빈이 마부를 부르러 부엌으로 들어가 보니 온 식구가 식사를 하고 있었다. 여인들은 선 채로 그들의 시중을 들고 있었다. 젊고 다부진 아들이 푸딩으로 입을 가득 채운 채 무언가 우스운 이야기를 하고 있었고, 모두 웃고 있었는데, 양배추 수프를 그릇에 따르고 있던 나막신을 신은 여인이 가장 즐겁게 웃고 있었다.\n\n이 농부의 집이 레빈에게 안겨 준 풍족함의 인상에는 나막신을 신은 그 젊은 여인의 잘생긴 얼굴이 적잖이 작용했을 테지만, 그 인상이 어찌나 강렬했던지 레빈은 도무지 그것을 떨쳐 낼 수가 없었다. 그리고 노인의 집에서 스비야시스키의 집으로 가는 내내, 마치 이 인상 속에 자기의 특별한 주의를 요구하는 무언가가 있기라도 한 것처럼, 그는 이 농가를 자꾸만 떠올렸다.\n\n# Chapter 26\n\n스비아시스키는 자기 군(郡)의 귀족 단장이었다. 레빈보다 다섯 살 위였고, 결혼한 지 오래되었다. 그의 집에는 레빈이 무척 좋아하던 젊은 처녀, 곧 그의 처제가 함께 살고 있었다. 그리고 레빈은 스비아시스키와 그 아내가 그 처녀를 자기와 맺어 주고 싶어 한다는 것을 알고 있었다. 이른바 결혼 적령기의 청년들이 늘 그렇듯, 레빈도 그것을 확실히 알고 있었지만, 누구에게도 차마 그 이야기를 입에 올리지는 못했다. 또한 그는 결혼을 하고 싶었고, 모든 정황으로 보아 이 매력적인 처녀가 훌륭한 아내가 되리라는 것도 알았지만, 설령 자기가 키티 셰르바츠카야를 사랑하지 않았더라도 하늘로 날아오르는 것이 불가능한 만큼이나 그 처녀와 결혼하는 일은 불가능하리라는 것 또한 알고 있었다. 그리고 이런 인식이 스비아시스키 집을 방문하면서 기대했던 즐거움에 독을 풀어 놓았다.\n\n사냥 초대가 담긴 스비아시스키의 편지를 받자마자 레빈은 곧바로 이 점을 떠올렸다. 그러나 그럼에도 그는 스비아시스키가 자기에 대해 그런 생각을 품고 있다는 것이 자신의 근거 없는 억측일 뿐이라고 마음을 고쳐먹고는, 그래도 가기로 했다. 게다가 마음 깊은 곳에는 그 처녀에 대해 자기 자신을 시험해 보고, 스스로를 점검해 보고 싶은 욕망이 있었다. 스비아시스키 집안의 가정생활은 더없이 유쾌했고, 레빈이 아는 한 지방 자치 활동에 참여하는 사람들 중 가장 훌륭한 본보기인 스비아시스키 자신도 그에게는 무척 흥미로운 인물이었다.\n\n스비아시스키는 레빈에게 늘 놀라움의 원천이 되는 부류의 사람 중 하나였다. 그들의 신념은 매우 논리적이지만 결코 독창적이지는 않으면서 제 갈 길을 따로 가고, 그들의 삶은 방향이 더없이 분명하고 확고하면서도 그 신념과는 완전히 동떨어져, 거의 언제나 정반대로 흘러간다. 스비아시스키는 지극히 진보적인 사람이었다. 그는 귀족을 경멸했고, 귀족 대다수가 속으로는 농노제를 지지하면서 비겁한 탓에 자기 생각을 감추고 있을 뿐이라고 믿었다. 그는 러시아를, 차라리 터키에 가까운 망해 버린 나라로 여겼고, 러시아 정부를 어찌나 형편없게 여겼던지 그 정부가 하는 일을 진지하게 비판하는 것조차 스스로에게 결코 허락하지 않았다. 그러면서도 그는 바로 그 정부의 관리였고, 모범적인 귀족 단장이었으며, 마차를 타고 다닐 때면 언제나 관직을 나타내는 모표를 달고 붉은 띠가 둘린 모자를 썼다. 그는 인간의 삶이 외국에서나 그럭저럭 견딜 만하다고 여겼고, 기회만 있으면 외국에 나가 머물렀다. 그러면서도 동시에 러시아에서 복잡하고 개량된 농법을 시행했고, 러시아에서 이루어지는 모든 일을 극진한 관심으로 좇으며 죄다 알고 있었다. 그는 러시아 농민이 유인원과 인간 사이의 중간 발달 단계에 머물러 있다고 여겼지만, 동시에 지방 의회에서는 그 누구보다 기꺼이 농민과 악수를 나누고 그들의 의견에 귀를 기울였다. 그는 신도 악마도 믿지 않았지만, 성직자들의 처우 개선과 그들의 수입 유지 문제에는 큰 관심을 쏟았고, 자기 마을의 교회를 유지하기 위해 각별히 애를 썼다.\n\n여성 문제에 관해서는 여성의 완전한 자유, 특히 노동의 권리를 주장하는 극단적 옹호자들 편에 섰다. 그러면서도 그는 아내와, 자식 없이도 다정한 가정생활로 모두의 부러움을 살 만한 관계를 유지하며 살았고, 아내가 아무 일도 하지 않으면서, 그저 자기 시간을 최대한 행복하고 유쾌하게 보내려는 남편의 노력을 함께 나누는 것 말고는 아무것도 할 수 없도록 아내의 삶을 꾸려 놓았다.\n\n사람들을 가장 호의적으로 해석하는 것이 레빈의 천성이 아니었더라면, 스비아시스키의 성격이 그에게 아무런 의문이나 곤혹스러움도 안기지 않았을 것이다. \"바보거나 악당이거나\" 하고 스스로 단정해 버렸을 테고, 그러면 모든 것이 분명해 보였을 것이다. 그러나 그는 \"바보\"라고 말할 수 없었으니, 스비아시스키는 의심할 여지 없이 영리한 데다, 더욱이 자기 교양을 유난히 겸손하게 다루는 고도로 교양 있는 사람이었기 때문이다. 그가 전혀 모르는 분야란 없었다. 그러나 그는 어쩔 수 없이 그래야 할 때를 빼고는 자기 지식을 드러내지 않았다. \"악당\"이라고 말하는 것은 더더욱 불가능했으니, 스비아시스키는 의심할 여지 없이 정직하고 마음씨 착하며 분별 있는 사람으로, 자기 일을 쾌활하고 열정적이며 끈기 있게 해 나갔기 때문이다. 그는 주위의 모든 사람에게서 높은 존경을 받았고, 분명 한 번도 의식적으로 비열한 짓을 저지른 적이 없었으며, 실로 그런 짓을 할 줄 모르는 사람이었다.\n\n레빈은 그를 이해하려 애썼으나 끝내 이해할 수 없었고, 그와 그의 삶을 마치 살아 있는 수수께끼처럼 바라보았다.\n\n레빈과 그는 매우 친한 사이였으므로, 레빈은 곧잘 스비아시스키의 속을 떠보아 그의 인생관의 가장 밑바닥에 다가가려고 시도하곤 했다. 그러나 그것은 언제나 헛수고였다. 모두에게 친절히 열려 있는 스비아시스키 정신의 바깥 방들을 넘어 그 안으로 파고들려고 할 때마다, 레빈은 스비아시스키가 살짝 당황하는 것을 알아챘다. 마치 레빈이 자기를 이해해 버릴까 봐 두려워하기라도 하듯, 그의 눈에는 희미한 경계의 빛이 어렸고, 그러면 그는 다정하고 쾌활하게 그를 밀어냈다.\n\n마침 이즈음, 농사에 환멸을 느낀 터라 레빈은 스비아시스키 집에 머무는 것이 유난히 반가웠다. 자기들이며 다른 모든 사람에게 그토록 만족하는 이 행복하고 다정한 부부와 그 잘 정돈된 가정의 모습이 언제나 레빈의 마음을 밝게 해 주었다는 사실은 차치하더라도, 그는 자기 삶에 그토록 불만스러운 지금, 스비아시스키에게 인생에서 그토록 명료함과 확고함과 굳센 용기를 안겨 주는 그 비밀에 다가가고 싶은 갈망을 느꼈다. 더욱이 레빈은 스비아시스키의 집에서 인근의 지주들을 만나게 되리라는 것을 알고 있었고, 마침 지금은 작물이며 일꾼들의 품삯 따위에 관한 그 시골식 대화를 듣고 거기에 끼어드는 것이 그에게는 각별히 흥미로웠다. 그런 대화가 관습적으로 무언가 아주 천한 것으로 여겨진다는 것을 그도 알고 있었지만, 지금 그에게는 바로 그것이야말로 유일하게 중요한 주제로 여겨졌다. '농노제 시절에는 어쩌면 그것이 중요하지 않았는지도 모르고, 영국에서도 중요하지 않을지 모르지. 두 경우 모두 농업의 조건이 확고하게 자리 잡혀 있으니까. 하지만 모든 것이 뒤집어지고 이제 막 형태를 갖추어 가는 우리에게는, 이 조건들이 어떤 형태를 띠게 될 것인가 하는 문제야말로 러시아에서 유일하게 중요한 문제야.' 레빈은 생각했다.\n\n사냥은 레빈이 기대했던 것보다 형편없었다. 늪은 메말랐고, 멧닭은 한 마리도 없었다. 그는 하루 종일 돌아다니고도 새 세 마리밖에 잡지 못했지만, 그 대신 사냥에서 늘 그러했듯이 왕성한 식욕과 더없이 좋은 기분, 그리고 격렬한 육체 활동에 언제나 따라오던 그 예리하고 지적인 상태를 안고 돌아왔다. 그리고 사냥을 하는 동안, 아무 생각도 하지 않는 것 같다가도 별안간 그 노인과 그 가족이 자꾸만 머릿속에 떠올랐고, 그들에 대한 인상은 단지 그의 주의뿐만 아니라 그들과 얽힌 어떤 문제의 해답까지 요구하는 듯했다.\n\n저녁 차 시간에는 후견 문제로 찾아온 두 지주가 자리를 함께했고, 레빈이 고대하던 흥미로운 대화가 시작되었다.\n\n레빈은 차 탁자 옆 안주인 곁에 앉아, 그녀와 맞은편에 앉은 그녀의 동생과 대화를 이어 가야 했다. 스비아시스카야 부인은 둥근 얼굴에 금발의, 키가 다소 작은 여자로, 온통 미소와 보조개로 가득했다. 레빈은 그녀를 통해 그녀의 남편이 자기 마음에 던진 묵직한 수수께끼의 해답을 얻으려 했다. 그러나 그는 생각을 마음껏 펼칠 수가 없었으니, 당혹감으로 괴로워하고 있었기 때문이다. 이 당혹스러운 괴로움은, 그의 짐작으로는 그를 위해 일부러 차려입은 듯한 드레스를 입은 처제가 맞은편에 앉아 있다는 사실 때문이었다. 그 드레스는 그녀의 하얀 가슴께가 사다리꼴 모양으로 유난히 깊게 파여 있었다. 가슴이 매우 희었음에도, 아니 오히려 매우 흰 탓에, 이 사각형으로 파인 부분은 레빈에게서 능력을 온전히 발휘할 여유를 앗아 가 버렸다. 그는 아마도 잘못된 짐작이겠지만, 이 깊게 파인 상의가 자기 때문에 만들어진 것이라고 상상했고, 자기에게는 그것을 바라볼 권리가 없다고 느껴 보지 않으려 애썼지만, 그 깊게 파인 상의가 만들어진 그 사실 자체가 자기 탓인 것처럼 느꼈다. 레빈에게는 자기가 누군가를 속인 것 같았고, 무언가를 해명해야 할 것 같았지만, 그것을 해명하기란 불가능했으며, 그런 까닭에 그는 끊임없이 얼굴을 붉히며 거북하고 어색해했다. 그의 어색함은 그 어여쁜 처제에게도 옮아갔다. 그러나 안주인은 이를 알아채지 못한 듯, 일부러 자꾸만 동생을 대화 속으로 끌어들였다.\n\n\"당신은,\" 그녀가 앞서 꺼낸 화제를 이어 가며 말했다, \"제 남편이 러시아적인 것에 관심을 가질 수 없다고 하시는군요. 정반대예요. 그이는 외국에서는 언제나 기분이 들떠 있지만, 여기서 같지는 않아요. 여기서야말로 자기 자리에 있다고 느끼는걸요. 할 일도 아주 많고, 무엇에든 흥미를 느끼는 재주가 있답니다. 아, 우리 학교는 아직 못 보셨죠?\"\n\n\"봤습니다…… 담쟁이덩굴로 뒤덮인 그 작은 집, 맞죠?\"\n\n\"네, 그건 나스티야가 한 일이에요.\" 그녀가 동생을 가리키며 말했다.\n\n\"직접 그 학교에서 가르치십니까?\" 레빈이 그 파인 목께보다 위쪽을 보려 애쓰면서 물었지만, 그쪽으로 시선을 두면 어디를 보든 그것이 보일 것만 같았다.\n\n\"네, 예전에는 제가 직접 가르쳤고 지금도 가르치지만, 이제는 아주 훌륭한 여선생님이 한 분 계세요. 그리고 체조 수업도 시작했답니다.\"\n\n\"아니요, 고맙습니다, 차는 이제 됐습니다.\" 레빈이 말했다. 그러고는 무례한 짓을 하고 있다는 것을 의식하면서도 더는 대화를 이어 갈 수 없어, 얼굴을 붉히며 자리에서 일어났다. \"저기서 아주 흥미로운 대화가 들리는군요.\" 그는 이렇게 덧붙이고는, 스비아시스키가 인근의 두 신사와 함께 앉아 있는 탁자 반대편 끝으로 걸어갔다. 스비아시스키는 비스듬히 앉아 한쪽 팔꿈치를 탁자에 괸 채 한 손에는 찻잔을 들고, 다른 손으로는 자기 수염을 그러모아 코에 갖다 대었다가 다시 놓곤 했는데, 마치 냄새를 맡는 듯한 시늉이었다. 그의 반짝이는 검은 눈은 회색 구레나룻을 기른, 흥분한 시골 신사를 똑바로 바라보고 있었고, 보아하니 그 신사의 말에서 재미를 느끼는 듯했다. 그 신사는 농민들에 대해 불평하고 있었다. 스비아시스키가 이 신사의 불평에 대해, 그의 주장 전체를 단번에 무너뜨릴 답을 알고 있으면서도, 자기 처지로는 그 답을 입 밖에 낼 수 없어, 적잖은 즐거움을 느끼며 그 지주의 우스꽝스러운 연설에 귀를 기울이고 있다는 것이 레빈에게는 분명히 보였다.\n\n회색 구레나룻을 기른 그 신사는 분명 농노제의 골수 신봉자이자, 평생을 시골에서 살아온 열렬한 농사꾼이었다. 레빈은 그의 옷차림에서, 분명 평상복은 아닌 그 구식의 다 해진 코트에서, 영리하고 움푹한 눈에서, 거침없이 술술 흘러나오는 토속적인 러시아어에서, 오랜 세월 몸에 밴 위압적인 어조에서, 그리고 새끼손가락에 오래된 약혼반지를 낀, 크고 붉으며 햇볕에 그을린 두 손의 단호한 몸짓에서 그 증거를 보았다.\n\n# Chapter 27\n\n\"이미 굴러가기 시작한 일을 내던질 만한 배짱만 있다면야…… 그토록 많은 수고를 헛되이 쏟아붓고 나서…… 나는 이 모든 일에 등을 돌리고, 죄다 팔아 치우고, 니콜라이 이바노비치처럼 훌쩍 떠나 버릴 텐데…… La Belle Hélène이나 들으러 말이오.\" 영리한 노인의 얼굴에 흐뭇한 미소가 번지며 지주가 말했다.\n\n* La Belle Hélène: 프랑스어로 '아름다운 엘렌'. 오펜바흐의 오페레타.\n\n\"하지만 그렇게 던져 버리지 않으시잖습니까.\" 니콜라이 이바노비치 스비야시스키가 말했다. \"그러니 분명 얻는 게 있는 거지요.\"\n\"얻는 거라곤 사거나 빌리지 않은 내 집에서 산다는 것뿐이오. 게다가 농민들이 철이 들 거라고 줄곧 바라게 되거든. 한데 그러기는커녕, 도무지 믿어지지 않을 만큼—그 술주정에다 그 타락이라니! 자기들 땅뙈기를 노상 쪼개고 바꿔 대지요. 말이나 소 한 마리 그림자도 안 보여요. 농민은 굶어 죽을 지경이면서도, 막상 일꾼으로 데려다 쓰려고 하면, 어떻게든 당신한테 해코지를 하려 들고, 그러고는 치안판사에게 당신을 고발한단 말이오.\"\n\n\"하지만 그러면 당신도 치안판사에게 호소하면 되지요.\" 스비야시스키가 말했다.\n\n\"내가 호소를 한다고요? 무슨 일이 있어도 그건 안 할 거요! 어찌나 말이 많고 어찌나 소란스러운지, 후회할 일만 생긴다니까. 예를 들어 작업장에서는, 선급금을 챙겨서 달아나 버렸지요. 판사가 어떻게 했게요? 아니, 그자들을 무죄로 풀어 줬단 말이오. 그자들을 단속할 수 있는 건 자기네 촌락 재판소와 마을 장로뿐이오. 그 양반이 옛날 식으로 호되게 매질을 해 주거든! 그게 아니면 죄다 집어치우고 도망치는 수밖에 없지요.\"\n\n지주가 스비야시스키를 놀려 대고 있는 게 분명했지만, 스비야시스키는 분개하기는커녕 도리어 재미있어하는 눈치였다.\n\n\"하지만 보세요, 우리는 그런 극단적인 방법 없이도 우리 땅을 잘 꾸려 가고 있잖습니까.\" 그가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"레빈하고 나, 그리고 여기 이분 말입니다.\"\n\n그는 다른 지주를 가리켰다.\n\n\"그렇소, 미하일 페트로비치 댁에서는 그렇게 되고 있지요. 한데 어떻게 되고 있는지 한번 물어보시오. 그걸 합리적인 방식이라고 하시오?\" 지주가 '합리적'이라는 말이 자못 자랑스럽다는 듯이 말했다.\n\n\"내 방식이야 아주 간단하오.\" 미하일 페트로비치가 말했다. \"고맙게도 말이오. 내 살림이라는 게 다 가을 세금 낼 돈을 미리 마련해 두는 데 달려 있는데, 그러면 농민들이 나한테 찾아와서 '아버님, 나리, 우리 좀 도와주십쇼!' 하지요. 뭐, 농민들이야 다 이웃 아니겠소. 안쓰러운 마음이 들지요. 그래서 삼분의 일쯤 선급으로 내어 주면서 이렇게 말하지요. '얘들아, 잊지 마라, 내가 너희를 도와줬으니, 내가 필요로 할 때는 너희가 나를 도와줘야 한다—귀리 파종이든, 풀베기든, 추수든 말이다.' 그래서, 뭐, 그렇게 합의를 보는 거요, 납세자 한 사람당 얼마씩으로—하긴 그중에는 정직하지 못한 작자들도 있긴 하오, 사실이지.\"\n\n이런 가부장적 방식에 오래전부터 익숙해 있던 레빈은 스비야시스키와 눈짓을 주고받고는 미하일 페트로비치의 말을 가로막으며, 다시 잿빛 구레나룻의 신사 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸다.\n\n\"그러면 어떻게 생각하십니까?\" 그가 물었다. \"요즘 같은 시대에는 어떤 방식을 채택해야 할까요?\"\n\n\"왜, 미하일 페트로비치처럼 꾸려 가든가, 아니면 수확의 절반을 받기로 하거나 소작료를 받기로 하고 농민들에게 땅을 빌려주든가 하면 되지요. 그거야 할 수 있소—다만 그게 바로 나라 전체의 번영을 망쳐 놓는 길이라오. 농노 노동에 좋은 경영을 곁들였을 때 아홉 배 수확을 내던 땅이, 수확 절반 나누기 방식으로는 세 배밖에 못 내지요. 러시아는 그 해방 때문에 망한 거요!\"\n\n스비야시스키는 미소 띤 눈으로 레빈을 바라보며 그에게 슬며시 비꼬는 듯한 몸짓까지 해 보였다. 하지만 레빈은 그 지주의 말이 터무니없다고 여기지 않았고, 오히려 스비야시스키의 말보다 더 잘 이해할 수 있었다. 러시아가 어떤 식으로 해방 때문에 망했는지 보여 주려고 잿빛 구레나룻의 신사가 한 말 가운데 많은 부분이, 사실 레빈에게는 매우 옳고, 자신에게는 새로우며, 도무지 반박할 수 없는 것으로 느껴졌다. 그 지주는 틀림없이 자기 자신의 개인적인 생각을 말하고 있었는데—이는 매우 드물게 일어나는 일이었다—그것도 한가한 머리에 운동거리를 찾아 주려는 욕구에서 나온 생각이 아니라, 자기 삶의 조건들 속에서 자라난 생각, 마을의 고독 속에서 곰곰이 곱씹어 온 생각, 모든 측면에서 따져 본 생각이었다.\n\n\"요점은 말이오, 아시겠지만, 온갖 종류의 진보라는 게 오직 권위를 사용해야만 이루어진다는 거요.\" 그가 자기에게 교양이 없지 않음을 보여 주려는 듯이 말했다. \"표트르 대제의 개혁이나, 예카테리나, 알렉산드르의 개혁을 보시오. 유럽의 역사를 보시오. 그리고 무엇보다도 농업의 진보를 보란 말이오—예를 들면 감자, 그것도 우리한테는 강제로 들여온 거요. 나무 쟁기도 늘 쓰던 게 아니었소. 그것도 아마 제국 이전 시대에 들여왔겠지만, 십중팔구 강제로 들여왔을 거요. 자, 우리 시대만 봐도, 농노제 시절에 우리 지주들은 농사에 갖가지 개량법을 썼소. 건조기에 탈곡기에, 거름 나르기며 온갖 근대적 농기구를—그 모든 걸 우리가 우리 권위로 도입했고, 농민들은 처음에는 반대하다가 결국 우리를 흉내 내게 됐지요. 그런데 농노제가 폐지되면서 우리는 우리 권위를 빼앗겨 버렸소. 그래서 높은 수준까지 끌어올려졌던 우리 농사가 가장 야만적이고 원시적인 상태로 가라앉을 수밖에 없게 된 거요. 내 눈에는 그렇게 보인단 말이오.\"\n\n\"하지만 어째서 그렇습니까? 그게 합리적인 거라면, 고용 노동을 가지고도 같은 방식을 유지하실 수 있을 텐데요.\" 스비야시스키가 말했다.\n\n\"우리한테는 그자들을 다스릴 힘이 없소. 도대체 누구를 데리고 그 방식을 운영하라는 거요, 한번 물어봅시다?\"\n\n'바로 저거야—노동력—농업의 핵심 요소.' 레빈은 생각했다.\n\n\"일꾼들을 데리고요.\"\n\n\"일꾼들은 일을 제대로 하지도 않고, 좋은 농기구로 일하려고 하지도 않소. 우리 일꾼이 할 줄 아는 거라곤 돼지처럼 술 퍼마시는 것뿐이고, 취하면 당신이 맡긴 걸 죄다 망쳐 놓지요. 말한테 물을 너무 먹여 병들게 하고, 멀쩡한 마구를 잘라 버리고, 바퀴 테를 술과 바꿔 먹고, 탈곡기에 쇳조각을 던져 넣어 망가뜨려 버린단 말이오. 자기 방식에 맞지 않는 건 뭐든 꼴도 보기 싫어하지요. 그래서 농사 수준 전체가 이렇게 떨어진 거요. 땅은 묵어 잡초가 무성하거나, 농민들에게 쪼개져 나가고, 수백만 부셸이 나던 곳에서 십만 부셸을 얻게 됐으니, 나라의 부가 줄어든 거요. 똑같이 했더라도, 조심해서 했다면 그게……\"\n\n그러더니 그는 이런 폐단을 피할 수 있었을 자기 나름의 해방 방안을 펼쳐 보이기 시작했다.\n\n이건 레빈의 흥미를 끌지 못했지만, 그가 말을 마치자 레빈은 처음의 입장으로 돌아가, 스비야시스키를 향해, 그에게서 진지한 의견을 끌어내 보려 애쓰며 말했다.\n\n\"교양의 수준이 떨어지고 있다는 것, 그리고 농민들과의 우리의 현재 관계로는 이익을 낼 수 있는 합리적 방식으로 농사를 지을 가능성이 없다는 것—그건 전적으로 옳습니다.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"나는 그렇게 생각하지 않아요.\" 스비야시스키가 자못 진지하게 대답했다. \"내가 보기에는, 우리가 땅을 일굴 줄 모른다는 것, 그리고 농노 시절 우리 농업 방식이 결코 너무 높았던 게 아니라 오히려 너무 낮았다는 것뿐이오. 우리한테는 기계도 없고, 좋은 가축도 없고, 효율적인 감독도 없소. 회계를 어떻게 하는지조차 모르지요. 아무 지주에게나 물어보시오. 어떤 작물이 수지가 맞고 어떤 게 안 맞는지 말해 줄 수 있는 사람이 없을 거요.\"\n\n\"이탈리아식 부기로구려.\" 잿빛 구레나룻의 신사가 비꼬듯 말했다. \"장부야 마음대로 적을 수 있겠지만, 그자들이 죄다 망쳐 놓으면 무슨 이익이 있겠소.\"\n\n\"왜 망쳐 놓는다는 거요? 형편없는 탈곡기나 댁의 러시아식 압착기야 그자들이 부숴 놓겠지만, 내 증기 압착기는 못 부순단 말이오. 변변찮은 러시아 조랑말이야 망쳐 놓겠지만, 좋은 짐말은 두면—못 망쳐요. 다 그런 식이오. 우리는 우리 농사를 더 높은 수준으로 끌어올려야 하오.\"\n\n\"오, 그럴 수단만 있다면야, 니콜라이 이바노비치! 댁이야 다 좋겠지요. 하지만 나처럼 대학에 다니는 아들을 두고, 김나지움에서 교육시켜야 할 아이들이 있는 사람은—내가 무슨 수로 그런 짐말을 사겠소?\"\n\n\"뭐, 그러라고 토지은행이 있는 거 아닙니까.\"\n\n\"나한테 남은 걸 경매에 부쳐 팔라고? 아니, 사양하겠소.\"\n\n\"농업 수준을 더 높이 끌어올리는 게 필요하거나 가능하다는 데에는 저도 동의하지 않습니다.\" 레빈이 말했다. \"저는 거기에 몰두하고 있고, 수단도 있지만, 아무것도 해낼 수가 없어요. 은행으로 말하자면, 그게 누구한테 무슨 도움이 되는지 모르겠습니다. 적어도 저로서는, 농사에 무슨 돈을 쏟아부었든 간에, 그게 다 손해였어요. 가축도—손해, 기계도—손해였지요.\"\n\n\"바로 그렇소.\" 잿빛 구레나룻의 신사가 만족스러운 듯 그야말로 껄껄 웃으며 맞장구를 쳤다.\n\n\"그리고 저만 그런 게 아닙니다.\" 레빈이 말을 이었다. \"저는 합리적 방식으로 자기 땅을 일구는 이웃 지주들과 두루 어울리는데, 그분들도 드문 예외를 빼고는 다들 손해를 보면서 그렇게 하고 있어요. 자, 댁의 땅은 어떤지 말씀해 보세요—수지가 맞습니까?\" 레빈이 말하자, 곧바로 스비야시스키의 눈에서, 스비야시스키 마음의 바깥쪽 방 너머를 들여다보려 할 때마다 그가 알아차리곤 했던 그 스쳐 지나가는 경계의 빛이 보였다.\n\n게다가 레빈의 이 질문은 그다지 진심에서 나온 것도 아니었다. 스비야시스카야 부인이 방금 차를 마시면서 그에게 말해 주기를, 그들이 그해 여름 모스크바에서 회계에 정통한 독일인 전문가를 불러왔는데, 그 사람이 오백 루블의 사례를 받고 그들 영지의 경영을 조사한 결과, 삼천 루블 남짓한 손해를 보고 있더라는 것이었다. 그녀는 정확한 액수는 기억하지 못했지만, 그 독일인이 그것을 한 푼의 단위까지 계산해 냈던 모양이었다.\n\n잿빛 구레나룻의 지주는 스비야시스키 농사의 수익 이야기가 나오자 미소를 지었다. 자기 이웃이자 귀족회장이 얼마나 이득을 보고 있을지를 빤히 안다는 듯이.\n\n\"수지가 안 맞을 수도 있지요.\" 스비야시스키가 대답했다. \"하지만 그건 단지 내가 경영을 잘 못한다는 것이거나, 아니면 내 소작료를 늘리려고 자본을 묻어 두었다는 것을 증명할 뿐이오.\"\n\n\"오, 소작료라니!\" 레빈이 질겁하며 외쳤다. \"소작료라는 건 유럽에서나 있을 수 있어요, 거기서는 거기에 투입된 노동으로 땅이 개량됐으니까요. 하지만 우리 땅은 죄다 거기에 투입된 노동으로 황폐해지고 있단 말입니다—다시 말해, 땅의 힘을 짜내고 있는 거지요. 그러니 소작료라는 건 문제가 안 됩니다.\"\n\n\"어째서 소작료가 없다는 거요? 그건 법칙인데.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 우리는 그 법칙 바깥에 있는 거지요. 소작료로는 우리한테 아무것도 설명되지 않고, 그저 우리를 헷갈리게 할 뿐입니다. 아니, 어떻게 소작료 이론이라는 게 성립할 수 있는지 말씀해 보세요……\"\n\n\"준켓 좀 드시겠소? 마샤, 우리한테 준켓이나 산딸기 좀 건네주구려.\" 그가 아내 쪽을 돌아보며 말했다. \"올해는 산딸기가 유난히 늦게까지 가는구려.\"\n\n* 준켓: 우유에 응유 효소를 넣어 만든 디저트.\n그러더니 스비야시스키는 더없이 기분 좋은 표정으로 자리에서 일어나 가 버렸다. 레빈에게는 대화가 이제 막 시작된 것처럼 여겨지던 바로 그 지점에서, 그는 대화가 끝났다고 여기는 모양이었다.\n\n논쟁 상대를 잃은 레빈은 잿빛 구레나룻의 지주와 대화를 계속하면서, 이 모든 어려움이 우리가 우리 일꾼의 특성과 습성을 제대로 알아내지 못하는 데서 비롯한다는 것을 그에게 증명해 보이려 애썼다. 하지만 그 지주는, 홀로 고립된 채 독자적으로 사고하는 모든 사람이 그러하듯, 다른 사람의 생각을 받아들이는 데 더뎠고, 특히 자기 생각에 치우쳐 있었다. 그는 러시아 농민이란 돼지 같은 존재이며 돼지 같은 짓을 좋아한다는 것, 그리고 그자들을 그 돼지 같은 행태에서 벗어나게 하려면 권위가 있어야 하는데 그게 없다는 것, 회초리가 있어야 하는데 우리가 너무 자유주의적이 되어 버린 나머지, 천 년 동안 우리에게 도움이 되어 온 회초리를 별안간 변호사와 모범 감옥으로 갈아 치워 버렸고, 거기서는 그 쓸모없고 악취 나는 농민이 좋은 수프를 얻어먹고 일정량의 입방피트짜리 공기까지 배급받는다는 주장을 고집했다.\n\n\"무엇 때문에 생각하십니까,\" 레빈이 다시 본래 문제로 돌아가려 애쓰며 말했다. \"노동이 생산적인 것으로 바뀔 만한 어떤 관계를 일꾼과 맺는 게 불가능하다고 말이죠?\"\n\n\"러시아 농민을 데리고는 결코 그렇게 될 수 없소. 우리한테는 그자들을 다스릴 힘이 없으니까.\" 지주가 대답했다.\n\n\"어떻게 새로운 조건들을 찾아낼 수 있다는 거요?\" 스비야시스키가 말했다. 준켓을 좀 먹고 담배에 불을 붙인 그가 다시 토론으로 돌아온 것이었다. \"노동력과 맺을 수 있는 가능한 관계는 죄다 규정되고 연구됐어요.\" 그가 말했다. \"야만의 유물인, 만인이 만인을 보증하는 그 원시적 공동체는 저절로 사라질 거요. 농노제는 폐지됐으니—이제 남은 건 자유 노동뿐인데, 그 형태들은 이미 정해지고 다 만들어져 있으니, 그걸 채택하기만 하면 되는 거요. 상시 고용 일꾼, 날품팔이, 다지기 인부—그런 형태들에서 벗어날 수는 없소.\"\n\n\"하지만 유럽은 그런 형태들에 만족하지 못하고 있잖습니까.\"\n\n\"만족하지 못하고, 새로운 걸 찾고 있지요. 그리고 십중팔구 찾아낼 거요.\"\n\n\"바로 그게 제 말이었습니다.\" 레빈이 대답했다. \"어째서 우리는 우리 자신을 위해 그걸 찾으면 안 된다는 겁니까?\"\n\n\"왜냐하면 그건 철도 부설 방법을 새삼스레 다시 발명하는 거나 마찬가지일 테니까요. 그건 이미 준비돼 있고, 발명돼 있단 말이오.\"\n\n\"하지만 그게 우리한테 안 맞는다면, 그게 어리석은 거라면요?\" 레빈이 말했다.\n\n그리고 그는 또다시 스비야시스키의 눈에 어린 경계의 빛을 알아차렸다.\n\n\"오, 그렇겠지요. 우리는 모자 하나로 온 세상을 뒤덮어 버릴 테고! 유럽이 찾아 헤매던 비밀을 우리가 찾아냈다는 거로군! 그런 말이야 나도 다 들어 봤소. 하지만, 미안하지만, 노동의 조직 문제에 관해 유럽에서 무슨 일이 이루어졌는지 전부 알고 계시오?\"\n\n\"아뇨, 아주 조금밖에 모릅니다.\"\n\"그 문제는 지금 유럽 최고의 지성들을 사로잡고 있소. 슐체델리치 운동이라든지…… 그리고 노동 문제에 관한 이 방대한 문헌들, 더없이 자유주의적인 라살레 운동이라든지…… 뮐하우젠 실험은요? 아마 아시겠지만, 그건 이제 엄연한 사실이 됐소.\"\n\n\"어렴풋이는 알지만, 아주 막연합니다.\"\n\n\"아니, 말씀만 그렇게 하시는 거지요. 분명 나만큼이나 다 알고 계실 텐데. 물론 내가 사회학 교수는 아니지만, 그게 내 흥미를 끌었거든. 정말이지, 댁도 흥미가 있다면, 한번 연구해 보셔야 하오.\"\n\n\"그래서 그들은 어떤 결론에 이르렀습니까?\"\n\n\"미안하오만……\"\n\n두 이웃이 자리에서 일어섰고, 스비야시스키는 자기 마음의 바깥쪽 방 너머를 들여다보려는 레빈의 그 거북한 버릇을 또 한 번 막으며, 손님들을 배웅하러 나갔다.\n\n# Chapter 28\n\n레빈은 그날 저녁 부인들과 함께 있는 것이 견딜 수 없이 지루했다. 그런데 자기가 영지 경영 방식에 대해 느끼는 불만이 예외적인 경우가 아니라 러시아의 일반적인 상황이라는 생각, 그리고 자기가 스비아시스키 집으로 오는 길 중간쯤에서 만난 농부의 경우처럼 노동자들과 그들이 일하게 될 토지 사이에 어떤 관계를 조직하는 일이 한낱 꿈이 아니라 반드시 풀어야 할 문제라는 생각에, 그는 일찍이 느껴 본 적 없을 만큼 마음이 들끓었다. 그리고 그에게는 그 문제가 풀릴 수 있을 것 같았고, 자기가 그것을 풀어 보아야 한다는 생각이 들었다.\n\n부인들에게 작별 인사를 하고, 다음 날 하루를 온전히 머물면서 함께 말을 타고 국유림에 있는 흥미로운 폐허를 보러 가기로 약속한 뒤, 레빈은 잠자리에 들기 전에 스비아시스키가 권해 준 노동 문제에 관한 책을 가지러 집주인의 서재로 갔다. 스비아시스키의 서재는 사방이 책장으로 둘러싸인 거대한 방이었고, 그 안에는 탁자가 두 개 있었다. 하나는 방 한가운데 놓인 묵직한 책상이었고, 다른 하나는 둥근 탁자로, 갖가지 언어로 된 최근의 평론지와 잡지가 별빛처럼 램프를 중심으로 펼쳐져 있었다. 책상 위에는 금색 글자가 새겨지고 온갖 종류의 서류로 가득 찬 서랍장이 놓여 있었다.\n\n스비아시스키는 책들을 꺼내 들고 흔들의자에 앉았다.\n\n\"거기서 뭘 보고 계십니까?\" 그가 둥근 탁자 옆에 서서 평론지를 훑어보고 있던 레빈에게 물었다.\n\n\"아, 그래요, 거기 아주 흥미로운 글이 하나 있습니다.\" 레빈이 손에 든 평론지를 두고 스비아시스키가 말했다. \"그러니까,\" 그는 열렬한 관심을 보이며 말을 이었다. \"결국 폴란드 분할에 대해 주로 책임이 있는 사람이 프리드리히가 아니었다는 거예요. 그게 증명됐다는군요…….\"\n\n그러고는 특유의 명료함으로 그 새롭고도 매우 중요하고 흥미로운 사실들을 요약해 주었다. 그 순간 레빈은 토지 문제에 관한 자신의 생각에 빠져 있었지만, 스비아시스키의 말을 들으면서 의아했다. '이 사람 속에는 도대체 뭐가 들어 있는 걸까? 그리고 왜, 왜 이 사람은 폴란드 분할에 관심을 갖는 거지?' 스비아시스키가 말을 마치자 레빈은 묻지 않을 수 없었다. \"그래서, 그게 어쨌다는 겁니까?\" 그러나 뒤따를 것은 아무것도 없었다. 그저 그것이 이러저러하다고 증명되었다는 사실이 흥미로울 뿐이었다. 하지만 스비아시스키는 그것이 왜 자기에게 흥미로운지를 설명하지 않았고, 설명할 필요도 느끼지 못했다.\n\n\"그렇군요, 그런데 저는 당신의 그 성마른 이웃 분이 무척 흥미로웠습니다.\" 레빈이 한숨을 쉬며 말했다. \"영리한 사람이고, 옳은 말도 많이 하던데요.\"\n\n\"아, 그만하세요! 그 사람도 마음속으로는 다른 사람들이랑 똑같이 뿌리 깊은 농노제 옹호자라니까요!\" 스비아시스키가 말했다.\n\n\"당신이 귀족 단장으로 있는 그 사람들 말이지요.\"\n\n\"그렇죠, 다만 저는 그 사람들을 다른 방향으로 이끌고 있을 뿐입니다.\" 스비아시스키가 웃으며 말했다.\n\n\"제가 무엇에 큰 관심이 있는지 말씀드리지요.\" 레빈이 말했다. \"우리 방식이, 그러니까 합리적 농업이라는 게 효과가 없다는 그 사람 말이 옳아요. 효과가 있는 건 그 온순해 보이는 신사처럼 고리대금 방식이거나, 아니면 아주 단순한 방식뿐이라는 거죠……. 그게 누구 탓일까요?\"\n\n\"물론 우리 자신 탓이죠. 게다가 효과가 없다는 것도 사실이 아닙니다. 바실치코프네서는 효과가 있어요.\"\n\n\"공장이…….\"\n\n\"하지만 저는 당신이 도대체 무엇에 놀라는지 정말 모르겠습니다. 민중은 이성적·도덕적 발달 단계가 어찌나 낮은지, 자기들에게 낯선 모든 것에 반대할 수밖에 없다는 건 뻔한 일이거든요. 유럽에서는 합리적 방식이 효과가 있는데, 그건 민중이 교육받았기 때문이지요. 그러니 우리도 민중을 교육해야 한다는 결론이 나오는 겁니다. 그게 전부예요.\"\n\n\"하지만 어떻게 민중을 교육한단 말입니까?\"\n\n\"민중을 교육하려면 세 가지가 필요합니다. 학교, 그리고 학교, 또 학교지요.\"\n\n\"하지만 당신 스스로 민중이 물질적 발달 단계가 그렇게 낮다고 하지 않았습니까. 그런 데 학교가 무슨 도움이 됩니까?\"\n\n\"있잖아요, 당신은 어떤 병자에게 해 준 충고 이야기를 떠올리게 하는군요. 설사약을 한번 써 보시오. 써 봤다, 더 나빠졌다. 거머리를 써 보시오. 써 봤다, 더 나빠졌다. 그럼, 이제 남은 건 하느님께 기도하는 것뿐이오. 해 봤다, 더 나빠졌다. 우리 처지가 꼭 그래요. 제가 정치경제학이라고 하면, 당신은 더 나빠진다고 하지요. 제가 사회주의라고 하면, 더 나빠진다. 교육이라고 하면, 더 나빠진다고요.\"\n\n\"하지만 학교가 어떻게 사정을 나아지게 한단 말입니까?\"\n\n\"농부에게 새로운 욕구를 주지요.\"\n\n\"음, 그게 바로 제가 도무지 이해할 수 없는 점입니다.\" 레빈이 열을 올리며 대꾸했다. \"학교가 어떤 방식으로 민중의 물질적 처지를 개선하도록 돕는다는 겁니까? 학교가, 교육이 그들에게 새로운 욕구를 준다고 하셨지요. 그들은 그 욕구를 채울 능력이 없을 테니, 오히려 더 나빠지는 거예요. 덧셈과 뺄셈, 교리문답에 관한 지식이 어떤 방식으로 그들의 물질적 처지를 개선한다는 건지 저는 도무지 알 수가 없습니다. 그저께 저녁에 어린 아기를 안은 농촌 아낙을 만나서 어디 가느냐고 물었습니다. 점쟁이 노파한테 간다고 하더군요. 아이가 발작하듯 비명을 질러서 치료를 받게 하러 데려가는 길이라고요. 제가 물었지요. '아니, 그 점쟁이 노파가 비명 지르는 발작을 어떻게 고친답니까?' 그랬더니, '닭장 홰에 아이를 올려놓고 무슨 주문을 외운대요…….'\"\n\n\"음, 당신이 직접 그 말을 하고 있군요! 그 여자가 비명 발작을 고치겠다고 아이를 닭장 홰로 데려가는 일을 막으려면 필요한 게 바로…….\" 스비아시스키가 사람 좋게 웃으며 말했다.\n\n\"아, 아닙니다!\" 레빈이 짜증스럽게 말했다. \"그 치료법은 그저 학교로 민중을 치료하는 일에 빗댄 비유였을 뿐입니다. 민중은 가난하고 무지하지요. 그건 저 아낙이 아이가 비명을 지르니까 병들었다고 확실히 아는 것만큼이나 우리도 확실히 압니다. 하지만 이 가난과 무지의 병폐를 어떤 방식으로 학교로 고친다는 건지는, 닭장 홰가 비명에 어떤 영향을 미치는지만큼이나 이해가 안 갑니다. 고쳐야 할 것은 그를 가난하게 만드는 바로 그것이라고요.\"\n\n\"음, 적어도 그 점에서는 당신이 그렇게도 싫어하는 스펜서와 의견이 같군요. 그도 교육이란 더 큰 번영과 안락의 결과일 수 있다고, 자기 말로는 더 자주 씻게 된 결과일 수는 있어도 읽고 쓸 수 있게 된 결과는 아니라고 했거든요…….\"\n\n\"음, 그렇다면 제가 스펜서와 의견이 같다니 무척 기쁘군요. 아니, 그 반대로 무척 유감입니다. 다만 저는 그걸 오래전부터 알고 있었어요. 학교는 아무 도움이 안 됩니다. 도움이 될 것은 민중이 더 부유해지고 더 많은 여가를 갖게 되는 경제 조직입니다. 그렇게 되면 그때 학교도 생기겠지요.\"\n\n\"그래도 지금은 유럽 어디에서나 학교가 의무화되어 있지 않습니까.\"\n\n\"그러면 당신 자신은 그 점에서 스펜서와 얼마나 의견이 같습니까?\" 레빈이 물었다.\n\n그러나 스비아시스키의 눈에는 경계의 빛이 번뜩였고, 그는 미소를 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 그 비명 이야기는 정말이지 일품이에요! 정말 직접 들으신 겁니까?\"\n\n레빈은 이 사람의 삶과 그의 생각 사이의 연관성을 끝내 알아낼 수 없으리라는 것을 깨달았다. 분명히 그는 자기의 추론이 어떤 결론에 이르는지에는 조금도 관심이 없었다. 그가 원하는 것은 오직 추론하는 과정뿐이었다. 그리고 그는 추론하는 과정이 자기를 막다른 골목으로 몰아넣을 때면 그것을 싫어했다. 그것만이 그가 싫어하는 유일한 것이었고, 그는 화제를 기분 좋고 유쾌한 무언가로 바꿈으로써 그것을 피했다.\n\n그날 받은 모든 인상은, 말하자면 그날의 온갖 개념과 생각의 근본 토대 노릇을 한 그 늙은 농부에게서 받은 인상부터 시작해서, 레빈을 격렬한 흥분으로 몰아넣었다. 그저 사교적 목적을 위해 한 무더기의 생각들을 갖춰 두고 있을 뿐 분명 레빈에게는 숨겨진 다른 어떤 신념을 가지고 있는 이 친절하고 선량한 스비아시스키, 그 무리는 이름이 군단이라 할 만큼 많은 군중을 향해서는 자기도 공감하지 않는 생각들로 여론을 이끄는 이 사람. 삶에 시달린 끝에 다다른 결론에서는 완벽하게 옳았으나 한 계급 전체에, 그것도 러시아 최고의 계급에 대한 격분에서는 그릇된 저 성마른 시골 지주. 자기가 해 온 일에 대한 자신의 불만, 그리고 이 모든 것에 대한 해결책을 찾으리라는 막연한 희망. 이 모든 것이 내면의 동요와 가까운 데 있는 어떤 해결책에 대한 예감 속에 한데 뒤섞였다.\n\n배정받은 방에 홀로 남아, 팔이나 다리를 움직일 때마다 뜻밖에 푹 꺼지는 스프링 매트리스 위에 누운 레빈은 오랫동안 잠들지 못했다. 스비아시스키가 영리한 말을 많이 했음에도 그와 나눈 대화 가운데 어느 하나 레빈의 흥미를 끈 것은 없었다. 그러나 성마른 지주의 결론은 깊이 생각해 볼 만했다. 레빈은 그가 한 말을 한마디 한마디 떠올리며, 상상 속에서 자신의 대답을 고쳐 보지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n'그래, 그에게 이렇게 말했어야 했어. 당신은 우리 농사가 효과가 없는 건 농부가 개량을 싫어하기 때문이고, 그러니 권위로 그에게 개량을 강요해야 한다고 말하지. 만약 이 개량 없이는 어떤 농사 방식도 도무지 효과가 없다면, 당신 말이 전적으로 옳을 거야. 하지만 효과가 있는 유일한 방식은, 여기 오는 길 중간의 그 늙은 농부 땅에서처럼 노동자가 자기 습관에 맞게 일하는 경우란 말이지. 그 방식에 대한 당신과 우리의 전반적인 불만은, 우리에게 잘못이 있거나 노동자들에게 잘못이 있다는 걸 보여 주는 거야. 우리는 우리 노동력의 특성에 대해서는 자문해 보지도 않은 채, 우리 길을—유럽식 길을—오랫동안 걸어왔어. 노동력을 추상적인 힘이 아니라 자기 본능을 지닌 러시아 농부로 바라보도록 해 보세. 그러면 우리는 그에 맞게 우리 경작 방식을 짜게 될 테지. 그에게 이렇게 말했어야 했어. 당신이 저 늙은 농부와 똑같은 방식을 갖추고 있다고, 당신이 노동자들로 하여금 일의 성공에 관심을 갖게 할 방도를 찾아냈고, 그들이 받아들일 만한 개량의 적절한 중용을 찾아냈다고 상상해 보라고. 그러면 당신은 토양을 고갈시키지 않고도 전에 거두던 수확량의 두세 배를 얻게 될 거야. 그걸 절반씩 나눠서 절반을 노동의 몫으로 주면, 당신에게 남는 잉여는 더 커질 테고, 노동의 몫 또한 더 커질 거야. 그리고 이렇게 하려면 농사의 수준을 낮추고 노동자들이 그 성공에 관심을 갖게 해야 해. 어떻게 이걸 하느냐고? 그건 세부적인 문제지. 하지만 의심할 바 없이 해낼 수 있어.'\n\n이 생각은 레빈을 큰 흥분으로 몰아넣었다. 그는 자기 생각을 실행에 옮기는 방안을 세세히 궁리하느라 밤의 절반을 자지 못했다. 그는 다음 날 떠날 생각이 없었지만, 이제 아침 일찍 집으로 돌아가기로 마음을 정했다. 게다가 가슴이 깊이 파인 보디스를 입은 그 처형은 그에게 무언가 더없이 천박한 짓을 저지른 듯한 수치심과 회한에 가까운 감정을 불러일으켰다. 무엇보다도 그는 지체 없이 돌아가야만 했다. 겨울밀 파종이 새로운 토대 위에서 이루어질 수 있도록, 파종 전에 서둘러 자신의 새 계획을 농부들에게 제시해야 했던 것이다. 그는 자신의 전체 방식을 혁신하기로 결심했다.\n\n# Chapter 29\n\n레빈의 계획을 실행에 옮기는 데에는 여러 어려움이 따랐다. 하지만 그는 안간힘을 다해 분투했고, 비록 바라던 바는 아니었으나, 그 시도가 수고를 들일 만한 가치가 있었다고 자기기만 없이 믿을 수 있을 만큼의 결과는 거두었다. 가장 큰 어려움 가운데 하나는, 토지 경작 과정이 한창 돌아가고 있어서 모든 것을 멈추고 처음부터 다시 시작하기란 불가능하다는 것, 그러니까 기계가 돌아가는 도중에 그것을 고쳐야 한다는 데 있었다.\n\n집에 도착한 날 저녁, 그가 관리인에게 자신의 계획을 알렸을 때, 관리인은 지금까지 해 온 모든 일이 어리석고 쓸모없었다고 지적하는 한에서는 눈에 띄게 흡족해하며 그의 말에 동의했다. 관리인은 자기도 오래전부터 그렇게 말해 왔으나 아무도 귀를 기울이지 않았다고 했다. 그러나 레빈이 내놓은 제안, 그러니까 각각의 농사일에 일꾼들과 더불어 동업자로서 한몫을 맡자는 제안에 대해서는, 관리인은 그저 깊은 낙담을 드러낼 뿐 분명한 의견을 내놓지 않았고, 곧장 다음 날 남은 호밀 단을 실어 날라야 할 긴급한 필요라든가, 두 번째 갈이를 위해 사람들을 내보내야 한다는 이야기를 꺼내기 시작했다. 그래서 레빈은 지금이 그것을 논할 때가 아니라고 느꼈다.\n\n농부들에게 그 이야기를 꺼내며 새로운 조건으로 땅을 내주겠다고 제안하기 시작했을 때, 그는 똑같은 커다란 어려움에 부딪혔다. 그들은 그날그날의 당면한 일에 너무 골몰해 있어서, 제안된 방안의 이로움과 해로움을 곰곰이 따져 볼 겨를이 없었던 것이다.\n\n순박한 마음을 가진 목동 이반은 레빈의 제안을, 그러니까 가족과 함께 가축 마당의 수익에서 한몫을 받자는 제안을 온전히 알아듣는 듯했고, 그 방안에 전적으로 공감했다. 그러나 레빈이 앞으로의 이로움을 넌지시 비치자, 이반의 얼굴에는 그가 하려는 말을 다 들을 수 없다는 데 대한 불안과 아쉬움이 떠올랐고, 그는 서둘러 도무지 미룰 수 없는 일거리를 스스로 찾아냈다. 우리에서 건초를 끄집어내려고 쇠스랑을 집어 들거나, 물을 길으러 달려가거나, 거름을 치우러 가는 식이었다.\n\n또 다른 어려움은, 지주의 목적이라는 게 자기들에게서 짜낼 수 있는 만큼 짜내려는 욕심 말고 다른 무엇일 리 없다는 농부들의 꺾을 수 없는 불신에 있었다. 그들은 (그가 무슨 말을 하든) 그의 진짜 속셈은 늘 그가 말하지 않은 데에 있으리라고 굳게 믿고 있었다. 그리고 그들 자신도 의견을 말할 때면 많은 말을 늘어놓으면서도 정작 자기들의 진짜 목적은 결코 말하지 않았다. 게다가 (레빈은 그 성마른 지주의 말이 옳았다고 느꼈다) 농부들은 어떤 합의에서든 첫째가는, 그리고 바꿀 수 없는 조건으로, 어떤 종류의 새로운 경작 방식도 강요받지 않을 것, 새로운 농기구도 쓰지 않을 것을 내세웠다. 그들은 신식 쟁기가 더 잘 갈고 중경기가 일을 더 빨리 해낸다는 데에는 동의하면서도, 그 둘 중 어느 것도 도저히 쓸 수 없게 만드는 수천 가지 이유를 찾아냈다. 그리고 그는 경작 수준을 낮춰야 하리라는 확신을 받아들이기는 했으나, 이로움이 그토록 분명한 개량된 방식을 포기하는 것이 못내 아쉬웠다. 그러나 이 모든 어려움에도 불구하고 그는 자기 뜻을 관철했고, 가을 무렵에는 그 제도가 돌아가고 있었다. 적어도 그에게는 그렇게 보였다.\n\n처음에 레빈은 땅의 농사 전체를 있는 그대로 새로운 동업 조건 아래 농부들과 일꾼들과 관리인에게 넘겨줄 생각이었다. 하지만 이내 그것이 불가능하다는 것을 깨닫고, 그것을 나누기로 마음먹었다. 가축 마당과 정원, 건초밭, 그리고 여러 부분으로 나뉜 경작지는 각각 별개의 몫으로 만들어야 했다. 순박한 마음을 가진 목동 이반은, 레빈이 보기에 그 누구보다도 이 일을 잘 이해하고 있었는데, 주로 자기 가족으로 이루어진 일꾼 패거리를 모아 가축 마당의 동업자가 되었다. 영지의 외진 한쪽, 팔 년 동안 묵혀 둔 황무지 한 자락은 영리한 목수 표도르 라줄료노프의 도움으로 여섯 농가가 새로운 동업 조건 아래 맡았고, 농부 슈라예프는 같은 조건으로 채소밭 전체의 관리를 떠맡았다. 나머지 땅은 여전히 옛 방식대로 경작되었지만, 이 세 동업체는 전체를 새롭게 조직하기 위한 첫걸음이었고, 그것들이 레빈의 시간을 완전히 차지했다.\n\n가축 마당에서는 사실 전보다 나아진 것이 하나도 없었다. 이반은 따뜻한 축사와 신선한 크림으로 만든 버터를 한사코 반대했고, 소는 차게 키워야 먹이가 덜 든다느니, 버터는 시큼한 크림으로 만들어야 더 이문이 남는다느니 우겨 댔으며, 옛 방식 그대로 품삯을 달라고 요구했고, 자기가 받는 돈이 품삯이 아니라 앞으로 받을 수익 몫에서 미리 받는 가불이라는 사실에는 털끝만큼의 관심도 보이지 않았다.\n\n표도르 라줄료노프의 동업체는, 사실 합의한 대로 씨를 뿌리기 전에 땅을 두 번 갈지 않았고, 시간이 너무 빠듯했다는 핑계로 자기들을 정당화했다. 같은 동업체의 농부들은, 사실 새로운 조건으로 땅을 부치기로 합의해 놓고도 늘 그 땅을 동업으로 맡은 것이 아니라 수확의 절반을 내는 소작으로 빌린 것처럼 말했고, 농부들과 라줄료노프 본인은 한두 번이 아니게 레빈에게 이렇게 말했다. \"땅값을 받으시면, 나리께서도 수고를 더실 테고, 저희도 한결 홀가분할 텐데요.\" 게다가 그 농부들은 합의한 대로 땅에 가축 마당과 헛간을 짓는 일을 갖가지 핑계로 자꾸 미루었고, 겨울이 될 때까지 질질 끌었다.\n\n슈라예프는, 사실 자기가 맡은 채소밭을 작은 구획으로 쪼개어 농부들에게 다시 빌려주고 싶어 했다. 그는 분명 땅이 자기에게 주어진 조건을 완전히 오해하고 있었고, 보아하니 일부러 오해하고 있는 것 같았다.\n\n또한 농부들에게 그 방안의 모든 이로움을 이야기하고 설명하는 동안, 레빈은 종종 농부들이 자기 목소리의 소리 말고는 아무것도 듣고 있지 않으며, 그가 무슨 말을 하든 결코 속아 넘어가지 않겠다고 굳게 작정하고 있음을 느꼈다. 농부들 가운데 가장 영리한 라줄료노프와 이야기할 때 그는 특히 이것을 느꼈고, 레빈을 향한 비웃는 듯한 즐거움과, 만약 누군가 속아 넘어간다면 그건 자기, 그러니까 라줄료노프는 아닐 것이라는 굳은 확신을 그토록 분명히 드러내는 그의 눈빛을 알아챘다. 그러나 이 모든 것에도 불구하고 레빈은 그 제도가 돌아가고 있다고, 그리고 장부를 엄격하게 기록하고 자기 방식을 끝까지 고집하면, 앞으로 그들에게 이 방식의 이로움을 입증하게 될 것이고, 그러면 그 제도가 저절로 굴러가리라고 생각했다.\n\n이런 일들이, 여전히 그의 손에 남아 있는 토지 경영이며 책을 쓰는 실내 작업과 더불어, 여름 내내 레빈을 어찌나 사로잡았던지 그는 사냥을 거의 나가지 않았다. 팔월 말에 그는 부인용 안장을 돌려주러 온 하인에게서 오블론스키 집안이 모스크바로 떠났다는 소식을 들었다. 그는 다리아 알렉산드로브나의 편지에 답하지 않음으로써, 떠올릴 때마다 부끄러움으로 얼굴이 화끈거리는 그 무례함으로써, 스스로 배수진을 불태워 버렸으며, 다시는 그들을 보러 가지 못하리라고 느꼈다. 스비아시스키 집안에게도 그는 꼭 그만큼 무례하게 굴어, 작별 인사도 없이 떠나 버렸다. 하지만 그들 또한 다시는 보러 가지 않을 것이었다. 이제 그런 것은 아무래도 상관없었다. 자기 땅의 농사를 새로 조직하는 일이, 마치 그의 인생에 다른 무엇도 결코 없을 것처럼 그를 온전히 사로잡았다. 그는 스비아시스키가 빌려준 책들을 읽었고, 갖고 있지 않은 것은 베껴 쓰면서, 그 주제에 관한 경제학 책과 사회주의 책을 모두 읽었지만, 예상했던 대로 자기가 떠맡은 방안과 관계되는 것은 아무것도 찾아내지 못했다. 정치경제학 책들에서는, 이를테면 그가 자기를 사로잡은 물음들에 대한 답을 매 순간 찾아내리라는 희망으로 처음에 큰 열정을 가지고 연구했던 밀의 책에서는, 유럽의 토지 경작 상황에서 연역된 법칙들을 발견했다. 하지만 그는 러시아에는 적용되지 않는 그 법칙들이 어째서 보편적이어야 하는지 알 수 없었다. 그는 사회주의 책들에서도 꼭 같은 것을 보았다. 그것들은 그가 학생 시절에 매혹되었던 아름답지만 실행 불가능한 환상이거나, 아니면 유럽이 처한 경제적 처지를 개선하고 바로잡으려는 시도들이었는데, 러시아의 토지 보유 제도는 그것과 아무런 공통점도 없었다. 정치경제학은 그에게 유럽의 부가 발전해 왔고 또 발전하고 있는 법칙들이 보편적이고 불변한다고 말했다. 사회주의는 그에게 이런 노선을 따른 발전이 파멸로 이어진다고 말했다. 그리고 둘 중 어느 것도, 그러니까 그 자신 레빈이, 그리고 모든 러시아 농부들과 지주들이, 수백만의 일손과 수백만 에이커의 땅을 공공의 이익을 위해 되도록 생산적으로 만들려면 어떻게 해야 하는가 하는 물음에는, 답은커녕 실마리조차 주지 못했다.\n\n일단 이 주제를 붙들고 나자, 그는 그것과 관계되는 모든 것을 성실하게 읽었고, 가을에는 외국으로 나가 토지 제도를 현장에서 연구할 작정이었다. 여러 주제에서 그토록 자주 부딪히곤 했던 일을 이 문제에서만큼은 다시 마주치지 않기 위해서였다. 그가 이야기 나누는 상대의 머릿속에 든 생각을 막 이해하기 시작하고 자기 생각을 막 설명하려고 할 때면, 종종 그는 느닷없이 이런 말을 듣곤 했다. \"하지만 카우프만은요? 존스는요? 뒤부아는요? 미첼리는요? 그것들을 안 읽으셨군요. 그 사람들이 그 문제를 철저하게 파헤쳐 놓았는데요.\"\n\n이제 그는 카우프만과 미첼리가 자기에게 들려줄 것이 아무것도 없다는 것을 분명히 알았다. 그는 자기가 무엇을 원하는지 알았다. 그는 러시아에 훌륭한 땅과 훌륭한 일꾼들이 있다는 것을, 그리고 어떤 경우에는, 가령 스비아시스키 집으로 가는 길에 들렀던 농부의 경우처럼, 일꾼들과 땅이 길러 내는 산물이 풍성하다는 것을 알았다. 하지만 자본이 유럽식으로 투입되는 대다수의 경우에는 산물이 빈약하다는 것, 그리고 이것은 단지 일꾼들이 오직 자기들 특유의 방식으로만 일하고 싶어 하고 또 잘하려 한다는 사실에서 비롯된다는 것, 그리고 이 적대는 우연한 것이 아니라 변함없는 것이며 민족정신에 그 뿌리를 두고 있다는 것을 알았다. 그는 광대한 미점령지를 개척하고 경작하는 것을 과업으로 삼아 온 러시아 민족이, 자기들의 모든 땅이 점령될 때까지는 그 목적에 알맞은 방식을 의식적으로 고수해 왔으며, 그들의 방식이 흔히 생각되는 것만큼 결코 나쁘지 않다고 생각했다. 그리고 그는 이것을 책 속에서 이론적으로, 또 자기 땅에서 실천적으로 입증하고 싶었다.\n\n# Chapter 30\n\n9월 말에는 농민 조합에 분배된 토지 위에 가축우리를 짓기 위한 목재가 실려 왔고, 젖소에서 나온 버터가 팔려 그 이익이 분배되었다. 실제로 이 제도는 훌륭하게 돌아갔다. 적어도 레빈에게는 그렇게 보였다. 이 주제 전체를 이론적으로 정리하고 자기 책을 완성하기 위해—레빈의 공상 속에서 그 책은 단지 정치경제학에 혁명을 일으키는 데 그치지 않고 그 학문을 송두리째 절멸시키고 민중과 토지의 관계에 관한 새로운 학문의 토대를 놓을 것이었다—이제 남은 일이라곤 외국을 한 바퀴 둘러보며 같은 방향으로 행해진 모든 것을 현장에서 연구하고, 그곳에서 이루어진 모든 것이 정작 필요한 것이 아니었음을 입증할 결정적인 증거를 수집하는 일뿐이었다. 레빈은 밀이 인도되어 그 대금을 받고 외국으로 떠나기만을 기다리고 있었다. 그런데 비가 내리기 시작해 밭에 남아 있던 곡식과 감자의 수확을 가로막았고, 밀의 인도까지 포함한 모든 작업을 중단시켰다.\n\n길마다 진창이 되어 다닐 수가 없었고, 물방앗간 두 곳이 떠내려갔으며, 날씨는 점점 더 나빠졌다.\n\n9월 30일 아침에 해가 떴고, 날이 갤 것을 기대하며 레빈은 여행을 위한 마지막 준비를 시작했다. 그는 밀을 인도하라고 지시했고, 자기에게 갚을 돈을 받아 오라고 관리인을 상인에게 보냈으며, 출발하기 전에 영지에 몇 가지 마지막 지시를 내리려고 직접 나갔다.\n\n모든 일을 마치고, 목덜미 뒤쪽의 가죽과 각반을 타고 끊임없이 흘러내리는 물줄기에 흠뻑 젖었으면서도 더없이 열정적이고 자신감에 찬 기분으로, 레빈은 저녁 무렵 집으로 돌아왔다. 저녁이 되자 날씨는 그 어느 때보다 더 나빠졌다. 우박이 흠뻑 젖은 암말을 어찌나 모질게 후려치는지 말은 고개와 귀를 흔들며 옆걸음으로 나아갔다. 그러나 레빈은 마차의 덮개 아래에서 멀쩡했고, 바퀴 밑으로 흘러가는 진흙탕 물줄기며, 헐벗은 잔가지마다 매달린 물방울이며, 다리의 널빤지 위에 녹지 않고 하얗게 남은 우박 자국이며, 잎이 떨어진 느릅나무 둘레에 수북이 쌓인 채 아직도 물기가 도는 도톰한 잎사귀를 즐겁게 둘러보았다. 그를 둘러싼 자연의 음울함에도 불구하고 그는 유난히 들떠 있는 기분이었다. 더 먼 마을에서 농민들과 나눈 이야기를 통해 그들이 새로운 처지에 익숙해지기 시작했음을 알 수 있었다. 그가 몸을 말리려고 들어갔던 오두막의 늙은 하인은 레빈의 계획을 분명히 찬성했고, 자기가 먼저 나서서 가축을 사들여 조합에 가입하겠다고 했다.\n\n\"그저 내 목표를 향해 끈기 있게 나아가기만 하면 돼. 그러면 끝내 목적을 이루는 거지.\" 레빈은 생각했다. \"그리고 이건 애써 노력하고 수고할 만한 가치가 있는 일이야. 이건 나 개인만의 문제가 아니야. 공공의 복리라는 문제가 여기 걸려 있어. 민중의 처지를 좌우하는 가장 중요한 요소인 농경 제도 전체가 완전히 탈바꿈해야 해. 빈곤 대신 전반적인 번영과 만족을, 적대 대신 화합과 이해의 일치를. 한마디로, 피 흘리지 않는 혁명이지만 더없이 거대한 규모의 혁명이지. 우리 군의 작은 모임에서 시작해서 그다음엔 현, 그다음엔 러시아, 나아가 온 세계로. 정의로운 사상은 결실을 맺지 않을 수 없으니까. 그래, 이건 애써 일할 만한 목표야. 그리고 그 일을 하는 사람이 검은 넥타이를 매고 무도회에 갔다가 셰르바츠키 집안 딸에게 퇴짜를 맞은, 본질적으로 그토록 가련하고 보잘것없는 인간인 나, 코스챠 레빈이라는 것—그건 아무것도 증명하지 못해. 프랭클린도 분명 똑같이 자신을 보잘것없다고 느꼈을 거야. 그 사람도 자기 자신을 전체로 놓고 생각하면 스스로를 믿지 못했겠지. 그래도 아무 상관 없어. 그리고 그 사람한테도 십중팔구 비밀을 털어놓을 아가피야 미하일로브나 같은 사람이 있었을 거야.\"\n\n이런 생각에 잠긴 채 레빈은 어둠 속에서 집에 다다랐다.\n\n상인에게 다녀온 관리인이 돌아와 밀 대금의 일부를 가져왔다. 늙은 하인과는 합의가 이루어졌고, 관리인은 오는 길에 어디나 곡식이 아직 밭에 서 있다는 것을 알게 되었다. 그래서 미처 거둬들이지 못한 레빈의 곡식 단 백예순 더미는 다른 사람들의 손실에 비하면 아무것도 아니었다.\n\n저녁 식사를 마친 뒤 레빈은 여느 때처럼 책을 들고 안락의자에 앉았고, 책을 읽으면서 자기 책과 관련지어 눈앞에 닥친 여행에 관해 계속 생각했다. 오늘은 자기 책의 의의 전체가 유난히 뚜렷하게 떠올랐고, 자기 이론을 뒷받침하는 문장 전체가 머릿속에 줄지어 정렬되었다. '이걸 적어 두어야겠어.' 그는 생각했다. '이걸로 짤막한 서문을 만들면 되겠어. 전에는 필요 없다고 여겼던 건데.' 그는 책상으로 가려고 일어섰고, 발치에 누워 있던 라스카도 일어나 기지개를 켜며 어디로 가는지 묻기라도 하듯 그를 쳐다보았다. 그러나 그는 그것을 적어 둘 틈이 없었다. 마을 우두머리 농민들이 찾아와, 레빈이 그들을 맞으러 현관으로 나간 것이다.\n\n그렇게 알현을 마친 뒤, 그러니까 이튿날의 작업에 관해 지시를 내리고 자기와 볼일이 있는 농민들을 모두 만나고 나서, 레빈은 서재로 돌아와 일을 하려고 자리에 앉았다.\n\n라스카는 탁자 밑에 누웠고, 아가피야 미하일로브나는 양말 뜨개질감을 들고 제자리에 자리를 잡았다.\n\n잠깐 글을 쓰던 레빈은 별안간 유난히 생생하게 키티를, 그녀의 거절을, 그리고 그들의 마지막 만남을 떠올렸다. 그는 일어나 방 안을 거닐기 시작했다.\n\n\"뭣 하러 그렇게 울적해하세요?\" 아가피야 미하일로브나가 말했다. \"자, 왜 집에만 붙어 계셔요? 어디 따뜻한 온천에라도 가셔야지요, 더구나 이렇게 여행 채비도 다 해 두셨는데.\"\n\n\"그게, 내일모레는 떠날 거예요, 아가피야 미하일로브나. 일을 끝내야 해서요.\"\n\n\"원, 원, 일이라고 하시네요! 마치 농민들을 위해 할 만큼 안 하셨다는 것처럼요! 그래, 지금도 사람들이 '나리께서 그 덕에 황제 폐하한테서 무슨 훈장이라도 받으시겠다'고들 하는걸요. 참말 이상한 일이지요. 뭣 하러 농민들 일로 애를 태우셔요?\"\n\n\"애를 태우는 게 아니에요. 내 자신을 위해서 하는 일이에요.\"\n\n아가피야 미하일로브나는 자기 땅에 대한 레빈의 계획을 속속들이 알고 있었다. 레빈은 자주 그 복잡한 견해를 그녀 앞에 펼쳐 놓았고, 그녀와 논쟁을 벌이며 그녀의 의견에 동의하지 않는 일도 드물지 않았다. 그러나 이번에는 그녀가 그의 말을 완전히 잘못 알아들었다.\n\n\"제 영혼의 구원이야 우리 모두가 알고, 또 무엇보다 먼저 생각해야 할 일이지요.\" 그녀가 한숨을 쉬며 말했다. \"파르펜 데니시치만 해도, 배운 사람은 아니었지만, 하느님이 우리 모두에게 그런 죽음을 내려 주십사 할 만큼 좋은 임종을 맞았는걸요.\" 그녀는 최근에 죽은 한 하인을 두고 말했다. \"성찬도 받고 다 했지요.\"\n\n\"내 말은 그런 게 아니에요.\" 그가 말했다. \"내 말은 내 이익을 위해 하는 일이라는 거예요. 농민들이 일을 더 잘하면 내게도 그만큼 더 좋은 거니까요.\"\n\n\"그래요, 나리께서 뭘 하시든, 그자가 게으른 쓸모없는 놈이면 모든 게 엉망진창이 되고 마는 법이에요. 양심이 있는 자면 일을 하고, 없는 자면 아무것도 안 되지요.\"\n\n\"이런, 당신도 이반이 가축을 더 잘 돌보기 시작했다고 직접 말했잖아요.\"\n\n\"제가 드리는 말씀은 그저,\" 아가피야 미하일로브나가 대답했다. 어림짐작으로 하는 말이 아니라 분명히 생각의 줄기를 따라 하는 말이었다. \"나리께서 장가를 드셔야 한다는 거예요, 그게 제가 드리는 말씀이지요.\"\n\n마침 방금 전까지 생각하고 있던 바로 그 화제를 아가피야 미하일로브나가 넌지시 건드리자, 그는 마음이 상하고 따끔했다. 레빈은 얼굴을 찌푸렸고, 그녀에게 대답도 하지 않은 채 다시 일감 앞에 앉아, 그 일의 진정한 의의에 관해 생각하던 모든 것을 스스로에게 되뇌었다. 다만 이따금 그는 고요 속에서 아가피야 미하일로브나의 뜨개바늘이 부딪치는 소리에 귀를 기울였고, 떠올리고 싶지 않던 것을 떠올리고는 다시 미간을 찌푸렸다.\n\n아홉 시에 그들은 초인종 소리와, 진창 위를 달려오는 마차의 희미한 진동을 들었다.\n\n\"자, 손님이 오셨네요, 이제 따분하실 일은 없겠어요.\" 아가피야 미하일로브나가 일어나 문 쪽으로 가며 말했다. 그러나 레빈이 그녀를 앞질렀다. 이제 그의 일은 잘 풀리지 않고 있었고, 그는 그게 누구든 손님이 온 것이 반가웠다.\n\n# Chapter 31\n\n계단을 절반쯤 내려가던 레빈은 귀에 익은 소리, 현관에서 들려오는 낯익은 기침 소리를 들었다. 하지만 자기 발소리에 묻혀 또렷이 들리지 않았기에, 그는 자기가 잘못 들었기를 바랐다. 그러다 길쭉하고 앙상한 낯익은 형체가 눈에 들어왔고, 이제는 더 이상 잘못 본 게 아닌 듯했다. 그래도 그는 모피 외투를 벗으며 기침을 해 대는 이 키 큰 사내가 자기 형 니콜라이가 아니기를 여전히 바랐다.\n\n레빈은 형을 사랑했지만, 형과 함께 있는 것은 언제나 고통이었다. 더구나 지금, 자기에게 떠오른 생각들과 아가피야 미하일로브나의 암시에 영향을 받아 마음이 어지럽고 갈피를 잡지 못하는 상태였던 레빈에게, 형과 마주해야 한다는 것은 유독 견디기 어려운 일로 다가왔다. 어수선한 자기 기분을 북돋아 줄 만한, 활기차고 건강한 손님, 그러니까 누군가 바깥사람을 만나기를 바랐건만, 그는 자기를 속속들이 꿰뚫어 보는 형, 마음 깊은 곳에 자리한 온갖 생각들을 불러내고 자신을 남김없이 드러내도록 다그칠 형을 마주해야 했다. 그러나 그는 그러고 싶지 않았다.\n\n이토록 비열한 감정에 스스로 화가 난 레빈은 현관으로 뛰어 내려갔다. 그리고 형을 가까이에서 본 순간, 이기적인 실망의 감정은 순식간에 사라지고 그 자리를 연민이 채웠다. 형 니콜라이는 전부터 수척하고 병약했지만, 지금은 더욱 야위고 더욱 쇠잔해 보였다. 가죽만 남은 해골이나 다름없었다.\n\n그는 현관에 서서, 가늘고 긴 목을 비틀어 그것을 감은 목도리를 풀며, 기이하고 가련한 미소를 지었다. 순종적이고 비굴한 그 미소를 보았을 때, 레빈은 무언가가 목구멍을 옥죄는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n\"보다시피, 너를 찾아왔다.\" 니콜라이가 동생의 얼굴에서 단 한순간도 눈을 떼지 않은 채 탁한 목소리로 말했다. \"오래전부터 오려고 했는데, 줄곧 몸이 안 좋았어. 이젠 훨씬 나아졌단다.\" 그는 크고 가느다란 손으로 턱수염을 쓸며 말했다.\n\n\"그래요, 그래요!\" 레빈이 대답했다. 그리고 그에게 입을 맞추며 형의 피부가 메마른 것을 입술로 느끼고, 기이한 빛으로 가득한 그 큰 눈을 코앞에서 보았을 때, 그는 더욱 두려워졌다.\n\n몇 주 전에 콘스탄틴 레빈은 형에게, 나누지 않고 남겨 두었던 작은 영지를 처분하여 형의 몫으로 2천 루블가량의 돈이 돌아갈 것이라고 편지를 써 보냈었다.\n\n니콜라이는 지금 이 돈을 받으러 왔으며, 더 중요하게는 옛 둥지에서 얼마간 머물면서 대지와 다시 닿아, 옛 영웅들처럼 앞에 놓인 일을 위해 기력을 새로이 하려고 왔노라고 말했다. 지나치게 굽은 등과 큰 키 때문에 더욱 두드러져 보이는 여윈 몸에도 불구하고, 그의 동작은 여전히 빠르고 급작스러웠다. 레빈은 그를 서재로 안내했다.\n\n형은 유난히 정성 들여 옷매무새를 가다듬었는데—전에는 한 번도 하지 않던 일이었다—숱이 적고 축 늘어진 머리를 빗고는, 미소를 지으며 위층으로 올라갔다.\n\n그는 더없이 다정하고 기분 좋은 상태였으니, 레빈이 어린 시절의 형을 떠올릴 때면 흔히 기억하던 바로 그 모습이었다. 그는 세르게이 이바노비치 이야기까지 원망 없이 꺼냈다. 아가피야 미하일로브나를 보았을 때는 그녀에게 농을 건네며 옛 하인들의 안부를 물었다. 파르펜 데니시치가 죽었다는 소식은 그에게 고통스러운 인상을 남겼다. 두려움의 빛이 그의 얼굴을 스쳤지만, 그는 곧 평정을 되찾았다.\n\n\"하긴 꽤 늙었으니까.\" 그는 이렇게 말하고는 화제를 돌렸다. \"자, 너랑 한두 달 지내다가 모스크바로 떠날 생각이야. 그거 아니? 먀코프가 거기 일자리를 약속해 줬거든. 관청에 들어갈 거야. 이제 내 인생을 완전히 다르게 꾸려 갈 거란다.\" 그가 말을 이었다. \"그 여자랑은 헤어졌어, 알지?\"\n\n\"마리야 니콜라예브나요? 아니, 왜요?\"\n\n\"아, 끔찍한 여자였어! 온갖 골칫거리를 다 안겨 줬지.\" 하지만 그는 그 골칫거리가 무엇이었는지는 말하지 않았다. 차가 묽다는 이유로, 그리고 무엇보다 그녀가 자기를 병자라도 되는 양 돌보려 한다는 이유로 마리야 니콜라예브나를 내쳤다고는 말할 수 없었다.\n\n\"게다가 이제 나는 완전히 새 출발을 하고 싶어. 물론 남들처럼 어리석은 짓도 했지만, 돈은 가장 나중 문제야. 그건 후회 안 해. 건강만 있으면 되지, 내 건강은, 다행히도, 완전히 회복됐고 말이야.\"\n\n레빈은 귀를 기울이며 머리를 쥐어짰지만, 무슨 말을 해야 할지 떠오르지 않았다. 니콜라이도 아마 같은 심정이었을 것이다. 그는 동생에게 일이 어떻게 돼 가는지 묻기 시작했고, 레빈은 자기 이야기를 할 수 있어 기뻤다. 그래야 위선 없이 말할 수 있었기 때문이다. 그는 형에게 자기 계획과 하는 일들을 들려주었다.\n\n형은 듣고는 있었지만, 분명 거기에 흥미를 느끼지는 않았다.\n\n이 두 사람은 너무도 닮았고, 너무도 가까운 사이여서, 아주 사소한 몸짓이나 목소리의 어조만으로도 말로 다 할 수 있는 것보다 더 많은 것을 서로에게 전했다.\n\n이제 두 사람 모두 단 하나의 생각밖에 없었으니—니콜라이의 병과 다가오는 그의 죽음—그것이 다른 모든 것을 짓눌렀다. 하지만 둘 중 누구도 감히 그것을 입에 올리지 못했고, 그래서 그들이 무슨 말을 하든—마음을 가득 채운 그 단 하나의 생각을 발설하지 않았으므로—전부 거짓이었다. 저녁이 끝나고 잠자리에 들 시간이 되었을 때만큼 레빈이 기뻤던 적이 없었다. 어떤 낯선 사람과 함께든, 어떤 공식적인 방문에서든, 그날 저녁만큼 부자연스럽고 거짓되었던 적은 없었다. 그리고 이 부자연스러움에 대한 의식과 거기서 느끼는 자책이 그를 한층 더 부자연스럽게 만들었다. 그는 죽어 가는, 더없이 사랑하는 형을 두고 울고 싶었건만, 자기가 어떻게 살아갈 작정인지 듣고 또 줄곧 이야기해야만 했다.\n\n집 안에 습기가 차고 침실 하나만 난방이 되어 있었기에, 레빈은 형을 자기 침실 칸막이 뒤에서 재웠다.\n\n형은 침대에 누웠는데, 자는지 안 자는지, 병자처럼 몸을 뒤척이고 기침을 해 댔으며, 목을 가다듬지 못할 때면 무어라 웅얼거렸다. 이따금 숨쉬기가 괴로울 때면 \"오, 하느님!\" 하고 말했다. 이따금 숨이 막힐 때면 성난 목소리로 \"아, 빌어먹을!\" 하고 중얼거렸다. 레빈은 그 소리를 들으며 오랫동안 잠들지 못했다. 그의 생각은 갖가지로 뻗어 나갔지만, 모든 생각의 끝은 한결같았으니—죽음이었다. 만물의 피할 수 없는 종착점인 죽음이, 처음으로 거역할 수 없는 힘으로 그에게 모습을 드러냈다. 그리고 이 사랑하는 형 안에 깃든, 비몽사몽간에 신음하며 습관처럼 하느님과 악마를 가리지 않고 불러 대는 그 죽음은, 지금껏 그에게 여겨졌던 것만큼 멀리 있지 않았다. 그것은 자기 안에도 있었으니, 그는 그것을 느꼈다. 오늘이 아니면 내일, 내일이 아니면 삼십 년 후, 결국 다 마찬가지 아니겠는가! 그리고 이 피할 수 없는 죽음이 무엇인지—그는 알지 못했고, 한 번도 생각해 본 적이 없었으며, 더구나 그것을 생각할 힘도, 용기도 없었다.\n\n\"나는 일을 하고, 무언가를 하고 싶어 하면서도, 이 모든 게 끝날 수밖에 없다는 걸 잊고 있었어. 잊고 있었던 거지—죽음을.\"\n\n그는 어둠 속에서 침대에 앉아, 몸을 웅크려 무릎을 끌어안고, 생각에 골몰한 나머지 숨을 멈춘 채 곰곰이 생각에 잠겼다. 하지만 골똘히 생각하면 할수록, 그것이 의심할 여지 없는 사실임이, 즉 실은 자기가 삶을 바라보면서 한 가지 작은 사실을 잊고 있었다는 것이 더욱 분명해졌다—죽음이 찾아오고, 모든 것이 끝난다는 것, 그 무엇도 시작할 가치조차 없으며, 어차피 어쩔 도리가 없다는 것을. 그래, 그것은 무서운 일이었지만, 사실이 그러했다.\n\n\"하지만 나는 아직 살아 있어. 그럼 이제 어쩌지? 어쩌면 좋단 말이야?\" 그는 절망에 빠져 중얼거렸다. 그는 촛불을 켜고 조심스레 일어나 거울 앞으로 가서, 자기 얼굴과 머리카락을 들여다보기 시작했다. 그렇다, 관자놀이 언저리에 흰머리가 보였다. 그는 입을 벌렸다. 어금니가 썩기 시작하고 있었다. 그는 근육질의 팔을 드러냈다. 그렇다, 거기에는 힘이 있었다. 하지만 저기 누워 얼마 남지 않은 폐로 숨을 쉬고 있는 니콜라이도 한때는 튼튼하고 건강한 몸을 가졌었다. 그리고 문득 그는 어린 시절 둘이서 함께 잠자리에 들곤 하던 일을, 표도르 보그다니치가 방에서 나가기만을 기다렸다가 서로에게 베개를 던지며 웃어 대던 일을, 표도르 보그다니치에 대한 경외심조차 막을 수 없을 만큼 부글부글 끓어 넘치는 생명과 행복의 감각에 사로잡혀 걷잡을 수 없이 웃어 대던 일을 떠올렸다. \"그런데 이제 저 굽고 움푹 꺼진 가슴이라니…… 게다가 나는, 내가 어떻게 될지, 무엇을 위해 사는지도 모른 채……\"\n\n\"콜록! 콜록! 빌어먹을! 왜 그렇게 부스럭거리는 거야, 왜 안 자는 거냐?\" 형의 목소리가 그를 불렀다.\n\n\"아, 모르겠어요, 잠이 안 와서요.\"\n\n\"난 한숨 잘 잤어. 이젠 땀도 안 나. 어디, 내 셔츠 좀 만져 봐. 젖지 않았지?\"\n\n레빈은 만져 보고는, 칸막이 뒤로 물러나 촛불을 껐지만, 오랫동안 잠들지 못했다. 어떻게 살아야 하는가 하는 물음이 이제 막 조금 또렷해지기 시작했을 때, 풀 수 없는 새로운 물음이 그 앞에 닥쳐왔다—죽음이었다.\n\n\"그래, 형은 죽어 가고 있어—그래, 봄이 되면 죽을 거야. 그런데 어떻게 도울 수 있단 말이야? 형에게 무슨 말을 할 수 있겠어? 내가 그것에 대해 뭘 알지? 나는 그런 게 있다는 사실조차 잊고 있었는데.\"\n\n# Chapter 32\n\n레빈은 오래전부터 한 가지를 깨닫고 있었다. 사람들이 지나치게 고분고분하고 온순해서 불편할 때면, 머지않아 바로 그 사람들의 신경질과 짜증 때문에 견딜 수 없어지기 십상이라는 것이었다. 형의 경우도 그렇게 되리라고 그는 느꼈다. 그리고 실제로 형 니콜라이의 온순함은 오래가지 못했다. 바로 다음 날 아침부터 형은 짜증을 부리기 시작했고, 동생의 가장 아픈 데를 들쑤시며 어떻게든 트집을 잡으려 안달하는 것 같았다.\n\n레빈은 자기에게 잘못이 있다고 느꼈지만, 사태를 바로잡을 수가 없었다. 그는 만약 두 사람이 겉치레를 하지 않고, 흔히 말하듯 마음에서 우러나오는 대로—그러니까 생각하고 느끼는 것만을 그대로—이야기했더라면, 그저 서로의 얼굴을 들여다보았을 것이고, 콘스탄친은 그저 \"형은 죽어 가고 있어, 죽어 가고 있다고!\"라고 말할 수밖에 없었을 것이며, 니콜라이는 그저 \"내가 죽어 가고 있는 걸 알아, 하지만 무서워, 무서워, 무섭단 말이야!\"라고 대답할 수밖에 없었으리라고 느꼈다. 그리고 마음에 있는 것만을 말했다면 그 이상은 아무 말도 할 수 없었을 것이다. 그러나 그런 식의 삶은 불가능했고, 그래서 콘스탄친은 평생토록 해 보려 애쓰면서도 끝내 배우지 못한 일을, 비록 자기가 관찰한 바로는 많은 사람들이 아주 잘 해내고 또 그것 없이는 도무지 살아갈 수 없는 그 일을 또 해 보려 했다. 그는 생각하지 않는 것을 말하려 애썼지만, 거기에 거짓의 울림이 있다는 것을, 형이 그것을 간파하고 그 때문에 부아를 낸다는 것을 끊임없이 느꼈다.\n\n사흘째 되던 날, 니콜라이는 동생에게 자기 계획을 다시 설명해 보라고 하더니, 그것을 공격하는 데 그치지 않고 일부러 공산주의와 뒤섞어 버리기 시작했다.\n\n\"너는 그저 네 것도 아닌 생각을 빌려 와 놓고는 그걸 왜곡해서, 들어맞지도 않는 데에 갖다 붙이려는 거야.\"\n\n\"하지만 그것과는 아무 상관도 없다니까. 그들은 소유와 자본과 상속의 정당성을 부정하지만, 나는 그 주요한 자극을 부정하지 않아.\" (레빈은 자기가 그런 표현을 쓴다는 것에 스스로 역겨움을 느꼈으나, 일에 몰두하게 된 이래로 그는 자기도 모르게 러시아어가 아닌 말들을 점점 더 자주 쓰게 되었다.) \"내가 바라는 건 노동을 조정하는 것뿐이야.\"\n\n\"그 말인즉슨, 너는 생각을 하나 빌려다가 거기서 힘을 주던 모든 것을 벗겨 내 버리고는, 그게 뭔가 새로운 것인 양 믿게 하려는 거지.\" 니콜라이가 화를 내며 넥타이를 잡아당겼다.\n\n\"하지만 내 생각은 그것과 아무런 공통점도……\"\n\n\"그건 어쨌든,\" 니콜라이 레빈은 빈정대는 미소를 지으며 두 눈을 악의에 차게 번득였다. \"뭐라고 불러야 할까—기하학적 대칭, 명료함, 명확함의 매력은 갖추고 있지. 그건 유토피아일지도 몰라. 하지만 일단 모든 과거를 tabula rasa로 만들 가능성을 허용한다면—소유도 없고 가족도 없다면—그때는 노동이 스스로 조직될 거야. 그런데 너는 아무것도 얻지 못해…….\"\n* tabula rasa: 라틴어로 '아무것도 쓰여 있지 않은 백지'.\n\n\"왜 자꾸 뒤섞는 거야? 난 한 번도 공산주의자였던 적이 없어.\"\n\n\"하지만 나는 공산주의자였고, 그게 시기상조이긴 해도 합리적이며, 초기 시대의 기독교처럼 미래가 있다고 생각해.\"\n\n\"내가 주장하는 건 단지 노동력을 자연과학의 관점에서 조사해야 한다는 것뿐이야. 그러니까 그것을 연구하고 그 성질을 규명해야 한다는…….\"\n\n\"하지만 그건 완전한 시간 낭비야. 그 힘은 발전 단계에 따라 스스로 어떤 활동 형태를 찾아내거든. 어디서나 처음에는 노예가 있었고, 그다음에는 소작인이 있었지. 그리고 우리에게는 절반 소작제, 소작료, 날품팔이가 있어. 너는 대체 뭘 찾으려는 거야?\"\n\n레빈은 이 말에 별안간 화가 치밀었다. 마음 깊은 곳에서 그것이 사실일까 봐—자기가 공산주의와 익숙한 형태들 사이에서 어떻게든 균형을 잡으려 애쓰고 있으며, 그것이 거의 불가능하다는 것이 사실일까 봐 두려웠기 때문이다.\n\n\"나는 나 자신을 위해서나 노동자들을 위해서나 생산적으로 일할 수 있는 방법을 찾으려는 거야. 나는 조직하고 싶어…….\" 그가 발끈하며 대답했다.\n\n\"너는 아무것도 조직하고 싶은 게 아니야. 그저 평생토록 그래 온 그대로, 그냥 농민들을 착취하는 게 아니라 뭔가 이상을 품고 그런다는 식으로 독창적인 척하며 폼을 잡고 싶은 거지.\"\n\n\"오, 좋아, 형은 그렇게 생각한단 말이지—그러면 나 좀 내버려 둬!\" 레빈은 왼쪽 뺨의 근육이 걷잡을 수 없이 실룩거리는 것을 느끼며 대답했다.\n\n\"너는 신념이라는 걸 가져 본 적도 없고 지금도 없어. 네가 바라는 건 그저 네 허영심을 만족시키는 것뿐이야.\"\n\n\"오, 좋아. 그러면 나 좀 내버려 두라고!\"\n\n\"내버려 두고말고! 진작 그랬어야 했어. 너 같은 놈은 악마한테나 가 버려! 내가 여기 온 게 정말 후회스럽다!\"\n\n그 뒤로 레빈이 아무리 형을 달래려 애써도, 니콜라이는 그가 하는 말을 도무지 들으려 하지 않고 헤어지는 편이 낫겠다고 잘라 말했다. 콘스탄친은 그것이 그저 형에게 삶이 견딜 수 없는 것일 뿐임을 알아차렸다.\n\n니콜라이가 막 떠날 채비를 하고 있을 때, 콘스탄친은 다시 그에게 가서, 다소 부자연스럽게, 혹시 자기가 어떤 식으로든 형의 마음을 상하게 했다면 용서해 달라고 청했다.\n\n\"아, 관대하시기도 하지!\" 니콜라이가 말하며 미소를 지었다. \"네가 옳은 사람이 되고 싶다면, 그 만족감은 줄 수 있어. 네가 옳아. 하지만 나는 그래도 가겠다.\"\n\n니콜라이가 동생에게 입을 맞춘 것은 헤어지는 바로 그 순간이었다. 그는 별안간 낯설고 진지한 눈빛으로 동생을 바라보며 말했다.\n\n\"어쨌든, 나를 나쁘게 생각하지는 말아 다오, 코스챠!\" 그의 목소리가 떨렸다. 이것이 두 사람 사이에 오간 유일하게 진심 어린 말이었다. 레빈은 그 말이 \"너도 보다시피, 또 알다시피, 나는 처지가 좋지 않고, 어쩌면 우리는 다시 만나지 못할지도 몰라.\"라는 뜻임을 알았다. 레빈은 그것을 알았고, 두 눈에서 눈물이 솟구쳤다. 그는 형에게 다시 한번 입을 맞추었지만, 말을 할 수 없었고, 무슨 말을 해야 할지 알지 못했다.\n\n형이 떠나고 사흘 뒤, 레빈도 외국 여행길에 올랐다. 기차 안에서 우연히 키티의 사촌인 시체르바츠키를 만난 레빈은 자신의 침울함으로 그를 몹시 놀라게 했다.\n\n\"무슨 일 있어요?\" 시체르바츠키가 그에게 물었다.\n\n\"아, 아무것도 아니에요. 인생에 별 행복이 없어서요.\"\n\n\"별로 없다고요? 뮐루즈 대신 나랑 같이 파리로 갑시다. 어떻게 하면 행복해지는지 보여 줄 테니.\"\n\n\"아니요, 난 그런 건 다 끝냈어요. 이제 죽을 때가 됐죠.\"\n\n\"이런, 그것 참 걸작이군요!\" 시체르바츠키가 웃으며 말했다. \"아니, 난 이제 막 시작할 채비를 하고 있는데요.\"\n\n\"네, 나도 얼마 전엔 그렇게 생각했어요. 하지만 지금은 곧 죽으리란 걸 알아요.\"\n\n레빈은 요즘 들어 진정으로 생각해 온 것을 말한 것이었다. 그는 모든 것에서 죽음, 아니면 죽음을 향해 다가가는 것밖에는 아무것도 보지 못했다. 그러나 그가 소중히 품은 계획은 오히려 그를 더욱 사로잡았다. 죽음이 올 때까지는 어떻게든 삶을 살아 내야 했다. 그에게는 모든 것 위로 어둠이 내려앉았다. 하지만 바로 그 어둠 때문에, 그는 그 어둠 속에서 자기를 인도해 주는 단 하나의 실마리가 자신의 일임을 느꼈고, 그것을 움켜쥐고 온 힘을 다해 매달렸다.\n\n# PART FOUR Chapter 1\n\n카레닌 부부는 한집에서 계속 살았고, 날마다 마주쳤지만, 서로에게는 완전히 남남이었다. 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 하인들에게 억측의 빌미를 주지 않으려고 날마다 아내를 보는 것을 원칙으로 삼았지만, 집에서 식사하는 일은 피했다. 브론스키는 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 집에 결코 발을 들이지 않았으나, 안나는 집 밖에서 그를 만났고, 남편도 그 사실을 알고 있었다.\n\n세 사람 모두에게 그 처지는 비참한 것이었다. 그리고 그 누구도, 이런 상태가 변하리라는 기대가 없었다면, 그저 지나가 버릴 일시적이고 괴로운 시련에 지나지 않는다는 기대가 없었다면, 단 하루도 이런 처지를 견뎌 내지 못했을 것이다. 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 이 열정도 다른 모든 것이 지나가듯이 지나가고, 모두가 그 일을 잊어버릴 것이며, 자기 이름은 더럽혀지지 않은 채 남으리라고 기대했다. 이 처지가 좌우되고, 또 누구보다도 이 처지로 인해 더 비참했던 안나는, 머지않아 모든 것이 해결되어 바로잡히리라고 그저 기대만 하는 것이 아니라 굳게 믿었기에 그것을 견뎌 냈다. 그녀는 무엇이 이 처지를 해결해 줄지에 대해서는 조금도 짐작하지 못했지만, 이제 곧 뭔가가 일어나리라고 굳게 믿었다. 브론스키는 제 의지나 바람과는 상관없이 그녀가 이끄는 대로 따랐고, 자기 행동과는 별개로 뭔가가 모든 어려움을 틀림없이 풀어 주리라고 그 역시 기대했다.\n\n겨울이 한창일 때 브론스키는 무척 진저리 나는 한 주를 보냈다. 페테르부르크에 방문차 온 어느 외국 공작이 그의 담당으로 맡겨졌고, 그는 볼 만한 명소들을 그에게 안내해야 했다. 브론스키는 풍채가 당당했고, 게다가 정중한 위엄을 갖추어 처신하는 기술을 지녔으며, 그런 지체 높은 인사들을 상대하는 데 익숙했다. 바로 그래서 그가 공작의 담당을 맡게 된 것이었다. 하지만 그는 그 임무를 무척 성가시게 여겼다. 공작은 집에 돌아가 러시아에서 그것을 보았느냐는 질문을 받았을 때 빠뜨린 것이 하나도 없도록 신경을 곤두세웠고, 또 제 나름대로는 러시아의 온갖 오락거리를 한껏 즐기려고 안달이었다. 브론스키는 이 두 가지 욕구를 만족시켜 주는 안내자 노릇을 해야 했다. 그들은 오전이면 마차를 타고 명소를 구경하러 다녔고, 저녁이면 그 나라 특유의 유흥을 즐기며 보냈다. 공작은 공작들 사이에서도 유독 보기 드문 건강을 누렸다. 체조와 세심한 건강 관리로 그는 쾌락에 탐닉했음에도 불구하고 윤기 흐르는 커다란 초록빛 네덜란드 오이처럼 싱싱해 보일 만큼 제 몸을 만들어 놓았다. 공작은 여행을 많이 다녔고, 현대 교통수단의 가장 큰 이점 가운데 하나로 온갖 나라의 쾌락에 손쉽게 다가갈 수 있다는 점을 꼽았다.\n\n그는 에스파냐에 가서 세레나데에 흠뻑 빠지고 만돌린을 켜는 에스파냐 처녀와 친분을 맺었다. 스위스에서는 영양을 잡았다. 영국에서는 붉은 외투를 걸치고 울타리를 뛰어넘으며 말을 달렸고, 내기로 꿩 이백 마리를 잡았다. 터키에서는 하렘에 들어갔고, 인도에서는 코끼리를 타고 사냥을 했으며, 이제 러시아에서는 러시아 특유의 온갖 쾌락을 맛보고자 했다.\n\n그에게는 이를테면 의전 책임자나 다름없던 브론스키는, 여러 사람이 공작에게 권하는 러시아의 온갖 유흥을 마련하느라 무던히도 애를 썼다. 그들에게는 경주마가 있었고, 러시아식 팬케이크와 곰 사냥과 삼두마차와 집시들과 그릇을 깨부수는 러시아식 여흥이 곁들여진 술잔치가 있었다. 그리고 공작은 놀랄 만큼 수월하게 러시아의 정취에 빠져들어, 그릇이 가득 담긴 쟁반을 내동댕이치고, 집시 처녀를 무릎에 앉히고는, 마치 이렇게 묻는 듯했다. 더 없는가, 그리고 러시아의 정취란 게 고작 이게 다인가?\n\n실은 러시아의 온갖 유흥 가운데 공작이 가장 좋아한 것은 프랑스 여배우들과 발레 무용수들, 그리고 화이트실 샴페인이었다. 브론스키는 공작들을 상대하는 데 익숙했지만, 근래 자기 자신이 변한 탓인지, 아니면 그 공작과 너무 가까이 붙어 지낸 탓인지, 그 한 주가 그에게는 끔찍하리만치 지겹게 느껴졌다. 그 한 주 내내 그는 위험한 미치광이를 떠맡은 사람이 느낄 법한 감각을 맛보았다. 그 미치광이를 두려워하면서, 동시에 그와 함께 지내는 탓에 제 정신마저 잃을까 봐 두려워하는 그런 감각이었다. 브론스키는 자기 자신이 모욕당하지 않으려면 단 한순간도 엄격한 공적 정중함의 어조를 늦춰서는 안 된다는 것을 끊임없이 의식하고 있었다. 공작은, 브론스키가 놀랍게도 그에게 러시아식 유흥을 마련해 주려고 어떤 비굴함도 마다하지 않던 바로 그 사람들을 경멸하듯 대했다. 공작이 연구하고 싶어 했던 러시아 여인들에 대해 늘어놓는 평가는 한두 번이 아니라 브론스키를 분노로 얼굴이 새빨개지게 만들었다. 공작이 브론스키에게 그토록 유독 못마땅하게 느껴진 가장 큰 이유는, 그 자신을 그에게서 보지 않을 수 없었기 때문이었다. 그리고 그 거울에 비친 모습은 그의 자존심을 흡족하게 해 주지 않았다. 그는 몹시 멍청하고 몹시 자기만족에 차 있으며 몹시 건강하고 몹시 깔끔하게 씻은 사내일 뿐, 그 이상도 그 이하도 아니었다. 그가 신사라는 것은 사실이었고, 브론스키도 그것을 부인할 수 없었다. 그는 윗사람들에게 한결같으면서도 굽실거리지 않았고, 동등한 자들에게는 격의 없고 상냥하게 처신했으며, 아랫사람들에게는 경멸이 섞인 관대함을 보였다. 브론스키 자신도 똑같았고, 그렇게 처신하는 것을 큰 미덕으로 여겼다. 하지만 이 공작에게는 그가 아랫사람이었고, 자기를 향한 공작의 경멸 섞인 관대한 태도는 그의 비위를 거슬렀다.\n\n\"멍청한 쇠고기 덩어리 같으니! 내가 저럴 수가 있단 말인가?\" 그는 생각했다.\n\n어찌 됐든, 이레째 되던 날, 모스크바로 떠나려던 공작과 작별하고 그의 감사 인사를 받았을 때, 그는 불편한 처지와 자기 자신의 불쾌한 반영에서 벗어나게 되어 기뻤다. 그는 밤새 러시아인의 기개를 마음껏 과시한 곰 사냥에서 돌아오는 길에 역에서 그와 작별 인사를 나누었다.\n\n# Chapter 2\n\n집에 돌아온 브론스키는 안나에게서 온 쪽지 한 통을 발견했다. 그녀는 이렇게 썼다. \"나는 아프고 불행해요. 나갈 수가 없지만, 더 이상 당신을 보지 못하고는 견딜 수가 없어요. 오늘 저녁에 와 줘요. 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 일곱 시에 회의에 가서 열 시까지 거기 있을 거예요.\" 남편이 그를 집에 들이지 말라고 못 박았는데도 곧장 자기에게 오라고 하는 그녀의 말이 이상하다고 한순간 생각했지만, 그는 가기로 마음먹었다.\n\n그해 겨울 브론스키는 진급하여 이제 대령이 되었고, 연대 숙소를 떠나 혼자 살고 있었다. 점심을 좀 먹고 나서 그는 곧장 소파에 드러누웠고, 오 분 만에 지난 며칠 동안 목격한 끔찍한 장면들의 기억이 뒤엉키며 안나의 모습과, 그 곰 사냥에서 중요한 역할을 했던 농부의 모습과 한데 이어졌다. 그리고 브론스키는 잠이 들었다. 그는 공포에 떨며 어둠 속에서 깨어나, 황급히 촛불을 켰다. \"뭐였지? 뭐였더라? 내가 꾼 그 끔찍한 게 뭐였지? 그래, 그래, 헝클어진 수염을 단 더럽고 자그마한 사내가 몸을 굽히고 뭔가를 하고 있었던 것 같아. 그러다 별안간 프랑스어로 무슨 이상한 말을 하기 시작했어. 그래, 꿈에는 그것 말고는 아무것도 없었어.\" 그는 혼잣말을 했다. \"그런데 왜 그렇게 무서웠던 거지?\" 그는 다시 그 농부와 농부가 내뱉은 그 알 수 없는 프랑스어를 생생하게 떠올렸고, 등줄기를 타고 공포의 한기가 흘러내렸다.\n\n\"무슨 헛소리람!\" 브론스키는 그렇게 생각하며 시계를 흘끗 보았다.\n\n벌써 여덟 시 반이었다. 그는 하인을 부르고 황급히 옷을 차려입은 뒤 현관 계단으로 나섰다. 꿈은 까맣게 잊은 채 늦었다는 것만이 마음에 걸렸다. 카레닌 집 입구로 마차를 몰고 다가가며 시계를 보니 아홉 시 십 분 전이었다. 잿빛 말 두 필을 맨, 높고 폭이 좁은 마차 한 대가 입구에 서 있었다. 그는 안나의 마차임을 알아보았다. \"그녀가 나에게 오는 거로군.\" 브론스키는 생각했다. \"차라리 그편이 낫지. 나는 저 집에 들어가는 게 싫으니까. 하지만 상관없어. 숨을 수는 없는 노릇이니.\" 그는 그렇게 생각하며, 부끄러울 것이 하나도 없는 사람다운, 어릴 적부터 몸에 밴 그 특유의 태도로 썰매에서 내려 문으로 향했다. 문이 열리고, 팔에 담요를 걸친 현관지기가 마차를 불렀다. 브론스키는 평소 세세한 것을 눈여겨보는 편이 아니었지만, 이 순간만큼은 현관지기가 자기를 흘끗 보는 그 놀란 표정을 알아챘다. 바로 그 문간에서 브론스키는 하마터면 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치와 부딪칠 뻔했다. 가스등이 검은 모자 아래 핏기 없이 움푹 꺼진 얼굴과, 외투의 비버 모피를 배경으로 환하게 빛나는 흰 넥타이를 환히 비추었다. 카레닌의 고정된 흐릿한 두 눈이 브론스키의 얼굴에 못 박혔다. 브론스키가 인사를 하자, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 입술을 우물거리며 손을 들어 모자에 가져다 댄 뒤 지나갔다. 브론스키는 그가 돌아보지도 않은 채 마차에 올라타, 창가에 놓아둔 담요와 오페라글라스를 집어 들고 사라지는 것을 보았다. 브론스키는 현관으로 들어섰다. 그의 미간은 찌푸려져 있었고, 두 눈에는 오만하고 노여운 빛이 번득였다.\n\n\"이게 무슨 처지람!\" 그는 생각했다. \"저자가 결투라도 하려 든다면, 자기 명예를 지키려 든다면, 나도 행동에 나설 수 있고 내 감정을 드러낼 수 있을 텐데. 하지만 이 나약함이라니, 아니면 이 비열함이라니…… 저자는 나를, 내가 한 번도 그러려 한 적 없고 앞으로도 그럴 마음이 없는, 거짓을 꾸미는 처지로 몰아넣고 있어.\"\n\n브레데 정원에서 안나와 이야기를 나눈 그날 이후 브론스키의 생각은 달라져 있었다. 자기를 송두리째 그에게 내맡기고, 그저 그가 자기 운명을 결정해 주기만 바라며 무엇이든 따를 각오가 되어 있는 안나—그 안나의 나약함에 자기도 모르게 굴복하면서, 그는 그때 생각했던 식으로 둘의 관계가 끝날 수도 있다는 생각을 진작에 접었다. 야심에 찬 계획들은 다시 뒤편으로 물러났고, 모든 것이 명확했던 그 활동의 영역에서 자기가 벗어났음을 느끼며 그는 자신의 열정에 온전히 몸을 맡겼다. 그리고 그 열정은 그를 점점 더 단단히 그녀에게 묶어 갔다.\n\n그가 아직 현관에 있을 때, 그는 멀어져 가는 그녀의 발소리를 들었다. 그녀가 자기를 기다리며 귀 기울이고 있다가, 이제 응접실로 돌아가는 길임을 그는 알았다.\n\n\"안 돼요.\" 그를 보자 그녀가 외쳤고, 그 목소리가 처음 새어 나오는 순간 두 눈에 눈물이 차올랐다. \"안 돼요. 이런 식으로 계속 간다면, 끝은 너무, 너무 빨리 오고 말 거예요.\"\n\n\"왜 그래요, 여보?\"\n\n\"왜냐고요? 나는 한 시간, 두 시간이나 괴로움 속에서 기다렸어요…… 아니, 그러지 않을래요…… 당신과 다투지 않을래요. 당연히 당신은 올 수 없었겠죠. 아니, 그러지 않을래요.\" 그녀는 두 손을 그의 어깨에 얹고, 깊고 격정적이면서도 동시에 무언가를 살피는 눈빛으로 한참 동안 그를 바라보았다. 그녀는 그를 보지 못한 동안의 시간을 메우려는 듯 그의 얼굴을 찬찬히 살폈다. 그녀는 그를 볼 때마다, 자기 상상 속에 그려 놓은 그의 모습(현실에서는 있을 수 없으리만치 비할 데 없이 빼어난 모습)을 실제 그의 모습과 맞춰 보곤 했다.\n\n# Chapter 3\n\n\"그 사람을 만났어요?\" 램프 불빛 아래 식탁에 앉았을 때 그녀가 물었다. \"늦게 온 벌이에요, 보세요.\"\n\n\"그래, 그런데 어떻게 된 거지? 그 사람 회의에 가 있어야 하는 거 아니었어?\"\n\n\"갔다가 돌아왔다가, 또 어딘가로 나가는 길이었어요. 하지만 그건 아무래도 좋아요. 그 얘긴 하지 마요. 어디 있었어요? 아직도 그 공작이랑?\"\n\n그녀는 그의 생활을 시시콜콜 다 알고 있었다. 그는 밤을 새우다가 깜빡 잠들어 버렸다고 말하려 했지만, 들뜨고 황홀해하는 그녀의 얼굴을 보자 부끄러웠다. 그래서 공작의 출발을 보고하러 가야 했다고 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 이젠 끝난 거죠? 갔나요?\"\n\n\"하느님 감사하게도 끝났지! 그게 나한테 얼마나 견디기 힘들었는지 당신은 믿지 못할 거야.\"\n\n\"왜요? 그건 당신들 모두가, 모든 젊은 남자가 늘 보내는 삶 아닌가요?\" 그녀가 미간을 찌푸리며 말했다. 그러고는 탁자 위에 놓여 있던 뜨개질감을 집어 들어, 브론스키를 쳐다보지도 않고 거기서 코바늘을 빼내기 시작했다.\n\n\"난 그런 삶을 진작에 그만뒀어.\" 그가 말했다. 그녀의 얼굴빛이 달라진 것에 의아해하며 그 의미를 헤아리려 애쓰면서. \"그리고 솔직히 말하면,\" 그는 미소를 지으며 두툼하고 하얀 이를 드러내며 말했다. \"이번 주에는 마치 거울 속에 비친 나 자신을 들여다보듯 그런 삶을 봤는데, 마음에 들지 않더군.\"\n\n그녀는 뜨개질감을 손에 든 채 뜨개질은 하지 않고, 낯설고 번득이는, 적의에 찬 눈으로 그를 바라보았다.\n\n\"오늘 아침에 리자가 나를 찾아왔어요. 그 사람들은 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인이 뭐라 하든 아랑곳없이 나를 방문하기를 두려워하지 않거든요.\" 그녀가 끼워 넣듯 말했다. \"그리고 당신네 아테네식 밤 모임에 대해 얘기해 줬어요. 얼마나 역겨운지!\"\n\n\"마침 그 얘길 하려던 참이었는데…….\"\n\n그녀가 그의 말을 가로막았다. \"당신이 알고 지내던 그 테레즈였다면서요?\"\n\n\"마침 말하려던 거였어…….\"\n\n\"당신네 남자들은 어쩜 그렇게 끔찍한지! 여자는 그런 걸 절대 잊을 수 없다는 걸 어째서 이해하지 못하는 거예요.\" 그녀는 점점 더 화를 내며 말했고, 그렇게 자기가 짜증을 내는 원인을 그에게 드러내 보였다. \"더구나 당신의 삶을 알 수도 없는 여자는요? 내가 뭘 알겠어요? 내가 여태 뭘 알았겠어요?\" 그녀가 말했다. \"당신이 나한테 들려주는 것뿐이죠. 게다가 당신이 진실을 말하는지 아닌지 내가 어떻게 알겠어요?……\"\n\n\"안나, 당신 나를 아프게 하는군. 나를 믿지 못하겠어? 당신한테 털어놓지 못할 생각은 하나도 없다고 내가 말하지 않았던가?\"\n\n\"그래요, 그래요.\" 그녀가 질투 어린 생각을 억누르려 애쓰는 게 역력한 채로 말했다. \"하지만 내가 얼마나 비참한지 당신이 알기만 한다면! 당신을 믿어요, 믿는다고요…… 무슨 말을 하고 있었죠?\"\n\n그러나 그는 자기가 무슨 말을 하려 했었는지 곧바로 떠올릴 수 없었다. 요즘 들어 점점 더 잦아진 이런 질투의 발작은 그를 섬뜩하게 했고, 아무리 그 사실을 숨기려 애써도 그녀에 대해 차가워지게 만들었다. 그녀의 질투의 원인이 그를 향한 사랑이라는 것을 알면서도 그랬다. 그녀의 사랑이 행복이라고 그는 얼마나 자주 스스로에게 말해 왔던가. 그리고 이제 그녀는, 사랑이 삶의 온갖 좋은 것들을 그녀에게서 압도해 버렸을 때 한 여자가 사랑할 수 있는 그런 방식으로 그를 사랑하고 있었다. 그런데 그는 모스크바에서 그녀를 뒤따라왔을 때보다 행복에서 훨씬 더 멀어져 있었다. 그때 그는 자기가 불행하다고 여겼지만, 행복은 앞에 놓여 있었다. 이제 그는 가장 좋은 행복은 이미 뒤에 남겨졌다고 느꼈다. 그녀는 그가 처음 보았을 때와는 영 딴판이었다. 정신적으로나 육체적으로나 그녀는 나쁜 쪽으로 변해 있었다. 온몸이 펑퍼짐해졌고, 그 여배우 이야기를 하던 순간 그녀의 얼굴에는 그것을 일그러뜨리는 사악한 증오의 표정이 어려 있었다. 그는, 꺾어 든 시든 꽃을 바라보는 사람이 그것을 꺾어 망쳐 버리게 만든 그 아름다움을 좀처럼 알아보지 못하듯, 그렇게 그녀를 바라보았다. 그러면서도 그는, 사랑이 더 강했던 그때라면 정말로 마음만 먹었다면 그 사랑을 가슴에서 뜯어낼 수도 있었으련만, 지금은 바로 이 순간 그녀에 대한 사랑을 전혀 느끼지 못하는 것만 같으면서도, 자기를 그녀에게 묶어 놓는 것이 끊어질 수 없다는 것을 알고 있다고 느꼈다.\n\n\"자, 자, 그 공작에 대해 무슨 얘기를 하려고 했죠? 마귀는 내가 쫓아냈어요.\" 그녀가 덧붙였다. 마귀란 그녀의 질투에 두 사람이 붙인 이름이었다. \"공작에 대해 나한테 무슨 얘기를 시작했었죠? 왜 그렇게 따분했던 거예요?\"\n\n\"아, 견딜 수가 없었어!\" 그가 끊긴 생각의 실마리를 다시 붙잡으려 애쓰며 말했다. \"그 사람은 가까이서 사귀어 봐도 나아지질 않아. 한마디로 규정하자면, 이런 거지. 가축 품평회에서 상이나 타 가는 잘 먹은 일등급 짐승, 그 이상도 그 이하도 아니야.\" 그가 짜증 섞인 어조로 말했는데, 그녀는 그 어조에 흥미가 일었다.\n\n\"아니에요, 어째서요?\" 그녀가 대꾸했다. \"그래도 그 사람은 견문이 넓잖아요. 교양 있고요?\"\n\n\"그건 전혀 다른 교양이야. 그들만의 교양이지. 교양을 경멸할 수 있기 위해 교양을 쌓은 거야, 동물적 쾌락 말고는 모든 걸 경멸하듯이 말이야. 보면 알 수 있어.\"\n\n\"하지만 당신들 모두 그 동물적 쾌락을 좋아하지 않나요?\" 그녀가 말했고, 그는 다시 그를 피해 가는 그녀의 눈에 어두운 빛이 도는 것을 알아챘다.\n\n\"어째서 그 사람을 변호하는 거지?\" 그가 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"변호하는 게 아니에요, 나랑은 아무 상관도 없는 사람인걸요. 다만, 당신이 그런 쾌락을 좋아하지 않았더라면 거기서 빠져나올 수 있었으리라는 생각이 드는 거죠. 하지만 이브 차림의 테레즈를 바라보는 게 당신에게 만족을 준다면야…….\"\n\n\"또 마귀가, 또 마귀가.\" 브론스키가 그녀가 탁자 위에 올려 둔 손을 잡아 입을 맞추며 말했다.\n\n\"그래요, 하지만 어쩔 수가 없어요. 당신을 기다리며 내가 얼마나 괴로웠는지 당신은 몰라요. 난 질투하는 게 아니라고 믿어요. 질투하는 게 아니에요. 당신이 여기 있을 때는 당신을 믿어요. 하지만 당신이 어딘가 멀리서, 내가 도무지 헤아릴 수 없는 그 삶을 살고 있을 때면…….\"\n\n그녀는 그에게서 몸을 돌리고, 마침내 뜨개질감에서 코바늘을 빼내더니, 집게손가락을 놀려 가며 램프 불빛 아래 눈부시게 새하얀 털실로 한 코, 한 코 빠르게 떠 나가기 시작했다. 그러는 동안 가느다란 손목이 수놓인 소맷부리 안에서 잽싸게, 신경질적으로 움직였다.\n\n\"그래서 어땠어요? 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치를 어디서 만났어요?\" 그녀의 목소리가 부자연스럽고 거슬리는 어조로 울렸다.\n\n\"문간에서 딱 마주쳤지.\"\n\n\"그래서 이렇게 당신한테 인사하던가요?\"\n\n그녀는 얼굴을 길게 빼고 눈을 반쯤 감더니, 재빨리 표정을 바꾸고 두 손을 모았다. 그러자 브론스키는 문득 그녀의 아름다운 얼굴에서 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 자기에게 인사하던 바로 그 표정을 보았다. 그는 미소 지었고, 그녀는 그 달콤하고 깊은 웃음으로 명랑하게 웃었다. 그것은 그녀의 가장 큰 매력 가운데 하나였다.\n\n\"난 그 사람을 도무지 이해할 수가 없어.\" 브론스키가 말했다. \"당신네 시골 별장에서 당신이 그에게 고백한 후에 그가 당신과 갈라섰다면, 나한테 결투를 신청했다면 모르겠는데, 이건 정말 이해가 안 돼. 어떻게 그런 처지를 견딜 수가 있지? 그도 느끼고 있어, 그건 분명한데.\"\n\n\"그가요?\" 그녀가 비웃듯 말했다. \"그는 더할 나위 없이 만족하고 있어요.\"\n\n\"모든 게 이토록 행복할 수 있는데, 우리는 다들 무엇 때문에 비참한 걸까?\"\n\n\"오직 그 사람만은 아니죠. 내가 그를 모를까요, 그 사람이 푹 절어 있는 그 거짓을?…… 어떤 감정이라도 있는 사람이라면 지금 그 사람처럼 나와 함께 살 수 있을까요? 그는 아무것도 이해하지 못하고, 아무것도 느끼지 못해요. 감정이 조금이라도 있는 남자라면 부정한 아내와 한집에서 살 수 있겠어요? 아내한테 말을 걸고, '여보'라고 부를 수 있겠어요?\"\n\n그러고는 다시 그녀는 그를 흉내 내지 않을 수 없었다. \"'안나, ma chère, 안나, 여보!'\"\n\n\"그는 남자가 아니에요, 인간이 아니에요. 인형이에요! 아무도 그를 모르지만, 난 알아요. 오, 내가 그의 처지였다면, 나 같은 아내는 진작에 죽여 버리고, 갈가리 찢어 버렸을 거예요. '안나, ma chère' 따위는 말하지 않았을 거라고요! 그는 남자가 아니에요, 관청의 기계예요. 그는 내가 당신의 아내라는 것을, 자기가 바깥 사람이라는 것을, 자기가 군더더기라는 것을 이해하지 못해요…… 그 사람 얘긴 그만해요!……\"\n\n* ma chère: 프랑스어로 '내 사랑하는 사람'.\n\"당신은 불공평해, 아주 불공평해, 여보.\" 브론스키가 그녀를 달래려 애쓰며 말했다. \"하지만 괜찮아, 그 사람 얘긴 그만하자. 당신이 뭘 하고 지냈는지 얘기해 봐. 무슨 일이야? 어디가 안 좋았던 거지, 의사는 뭐라고 했어?\"\n\n그녀는 조롱 섞인 즐거움이 어린 눈으로 그를 바라보았다. 그녀는 분명 남편에게서 또 다른 우스꽝스럽고 기괴한 면들을 찾아낸 듯했고, 그것을 입 밖에 낼 순간을 기다리고 있었다.\n\n그러나 그는 말을 이어 갔다.\n\n\"내 생각엔 그건 병이 아니라 당신의 상태인 것 같은데. 언제쯤이지?\"\n\n그녀의 눈에서 비꼬는 빛이 사라졌지만, 다른 미소가, 무언가에 대한—그가 무엇인지 알 수 없는 무언가에 대한—깨달음과 조용한 우수가 그녀의 얼굴에 떠올랐다.\n\n\"곧, 곧이에요. 당신은 우리 처지가 비참하다고, 끝장을 내야 한다고 말하죠. 자유롭고 떳떳하게 당신을 사랑할 수 있다면, 그게 나한테 얼마나 간절한지, 그러지 못하는 게 나한테 얼마나 끔찍한지 당신이 알기만 한다면! 그러면 내 질투로 나 자신을 괴롭히고 당신을 괴롭히지도 않을 텐데…… 그리고 그건 곧 올 거예요, 하지만 우리가 기대하는 식은 아니에요.\"\n\n그리고 그것이 어떻게 올지를 생각하니 자신이 너무도 가엾게 느껴져 눈에 눈물이 차올랐고, 그녀는 말을 이어 가지 못했다. 그녀는 램프 불빛 속에서 반지들과 함께 눈부시게 새하얀 손을 그의 소매에 얹었다.\n\n\"그건 우리가 짐작하는 식으로 오지 않을 거예요. 당신한테 이 말을 하려던 건 아니었는데, 당신이 하게 만들었어요. 곧, 곧, 모든 게 끝나고, 우리 모두, 우리 모두 평안해질 거예요, 더는 괴로워하지 않을 거예요.\"\n\n\"무슨 말인지 모르겠어.\" 그는 그녀의 말을 이해하면서도 그렇게 말했다.\n\n\"언제냐고 물었죠? 곧이에요. 그리고 난 그걸 견디고 살아남지 못할 거예요. 내 말 가로막지 마요!\" 그녀가 서둘러 말했다. \"난 알아요. 확실히 알아요. 난 죽을 거예요. 그리고 내가 죽어서 나 자신과 당신을 풀어 주게 되어서 무척 기뻐요.\"\n\n그녀의 눈에서 눈물이 떨어졌다. 그는 그녀의 손 위로 몸을 숙여 거기에 입을 맞추기 시작했다. 아무런 근거도 없다는 것을 알면서도 제어할 수 없는 자신의 감정을 감추려 애쓰면서.\n\n\"그래요, 그편이 나아요.\" 그녀가 그의 손을 꽉 움켜쥐며 말했다. \"그게 유일한 길이에요, 우리에게 남은 유일한 길이에요.\"\n\n그는 마음을 추스르고 고개를 들었다.\n\n\"얼마나 터무니없는 소리야! 당신 지금 얼마나 어이없는 헛소리를 하고 있는 거지!\"\n\n\"아니에요, 그건 진실이에요.\"\n\n\"뭐가, 뭐가 진실이라는 거야?\"\n\n\"내가 죽으리라는 거요. 꿈을 꿨어요.\"\n\n\"꿈?\" 브론스키가 되뇌었고, 그 순간 자기 꿈에 나온 농부가 떠올랐다.\n\n\"그래요, 꿈이에요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"꽤 오래전에 꾼 꿈이에요. 꿈속에서 난 침실로 뛰어 들어갔어요. 거기서 뭔가를 가져와야 했고, 뭔가를 알아내야 했죠. 꿈에서란 게 어떤지 당신도 알잖아요.\" 그녀가 공포로 눈을 크게 뜨고 말했다. \"그런데 침실 구석에 뭔가가 서 있었어요.\"\n\n\"오, 무슨 헛소리야! 어떻게 그런 걸 믿을 수가…….\"\n\n그러나 그녀는 그가 말을 가로막게 두지 않았다. 자기가 하는 말이 그녀에게는 너무도 중요했다.\n\n\"그런데 그 뭔가가 돌아섰고, 보니까 그건 농부였어요. 수염이 텁수룩하고, 키가 작고, 끔찍하게 생긴. 난 달아나려고 했는데, 그가 자루 위로 몸을 숙이고 거기서 두 손으로 뭔가를 더듬거리고 있었어요…….\"\n\n그녀는 그가 어떻게 손을 움직였는지 흉내 내 보였다. 그녀의 얼굴에는 공포가 어려 있었다. 그리고 브론스키는 자기 꿈을 떠올리며 똑같은 공포가 자기 영혼을 채우는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n\"그는 더듬거리면서 프랑스어로 계속 빠르게, 빠르게 중얼거렸어요. Il faut le battre, le fer, le broyer, le pétrir……라고요. 그래서 난 무서워서 깨어나려고 했고, 깨어났어요…… 그런데 꿈속에서 깨어난 거였어요. 그러고는 그게 무슨 뜻인지 자문하기 시작했죠. 그러자 코르네이가 나한테 말하는 거예요. '해산하다가 죽으실 거예요, 마님, 죽으실 거예요……'라고. 그러고서 난 잠에서 깼어요.\"\n\n\"무슨 헛소리야, 무슨 헛소리.\" 브론스키가 말했지만, 자기 목소리에 아무런 확신도 실려 있지 않다는 것을 스스로도 느꼈다.\n\n* Il faut le battre, le fer, le broyer, le pétrir: 프랑스어로 '쇠를 두드려야 한다, 그것을 빻고, 그것을 반죽해야 한다'.\n\n\"하지만 그 얘긴 그만해요. 종을 울려 줘요, 차를 마실래요. 그리고 이제 잠깐만 더 있어 줘요. 내가 오래…….\"\n그러나 그녀는 별안간 말을 멈췄다. 그녀의 얼굴 표정이 순식간에 달라졌다. 공포와 흥분이 갑자기 부드럽고 엄숙하며 더없이 행복한 주의(注意)의 표정으로 바뀌었다. 그는 그 변화의 의미를 이해할 수 없었다. 그녀는 자기 안에서 꿈틀거리는 새 생명의 움직임에 귀 기울이고 있었던 것이다.\n\n# Chapter 4\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 자기 집 현관 계단에서 브론스키와 마주친 뒤, 애초 작정한 대로 이탈리아 오페라 극장으로 마차를 몰았다. 그곳에서 두 막을 끝까지 보았고, 보고 싶었던 사람들을 모두 만났다. 집으로 돌아와서는 모자걸이를 주의 깊게 살폈는데, 거기에 군용 외투가 걸려 있지 않은 것을 확인하고는 여느 때처럼 자기 방으로 갔다. 그러나 평소의 습관과 달리 잠자리에 들지 않고 새벽 세 시까지 서재를 이리저리 거닐었다. 아내에 대한 격렬한 분노의 감정이 그를 가만두지 않았다. 아내는 예법을 지키지 않았고, 그가 단 하나 내건 조건, 곧 자기 집에서 정부를 맞아들이지 말라는 그 조건마저 지키지 않았던 것이다. 아내는 그의 요구를 따르지 않았고, 그는 아내를 벌하고 위협한 대로 실행해야만 했다. 이혼하고 아들을 데려가야 했다. 그는 이 일에 따르는 온갖 난관을 잘 알고 있었지만, 자기가 그렇게 하겠다고 말했으니 이제 그 위협을 실행에 옮겨야 했다. 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인은 그것이 그의 처지에서 벗어나는 가장 좋은 길이라고 넌지시 일러 준 바 있었고, 더구나 근래에는 이혼 수속이 워낙 완벽하게 다듬어진 터라 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 형식상의 난관을 극복할 가능성이 있다고 보았다. 불행은 결코 홀로 오는 법이 없으니, 토착 부족들의 재편 문제며 자라이스크 현의 토지 관개 문제까지 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에게 어찌나 많은 공무상의 근심을 안겨 주었던지, 그는 근래 들어 줄곧 극도로 신경이 곤두선 상태였다.\n\n그는 밤새 한숨도 자지 못했고, 그의 분노는 일종의 거대한 산술급수처럼 불어나 아침이 되자 절정에 이르렀다. 그는 서둘러 옷을 입었고, 마치 분노로 가득 찬 잔을 받쳐 들고 한 방울도 흘리지 않으려는 듯, 그 분노와 함께 아내와의 담판에 필요한 기력마저 잃지 않으려는 듯, 아내가 일어났다는 소리를 듣자마자 곧장 그녀의 방으로 들어갔다.\n\n남편을 잘 안다고 여겨 왔던 안나는 그가 방으로 들어서는 순간 그 모습에 깜짝 놀랐다. 그의 이마는 잔뜩 찌푸려져 있었고, 두 눈은 그녀의 눈길을 피한 채 음울하게 앞을 노려보고 있었으며, 입은 경멸하듯 굳게 다물려 있었다. 그의 걸음걸이에도, 몸짓에도, 목소리에도, 아내가 여태껏 그에게서 한 번도 본 적 없는 단호함과 결연함이 깃들어 있었다. 그는 그녀의 방으로 들어와 인사도 없이 곧장 그녀의 책상으로 다가가더니, 열쇠를 집어 서랍을 열었다.\n\n\"뭘 찾으시는 거예요?\" 그녀가 소리쳤다.\n\n\"당신 정부의 편지들이오.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"거긴 없어요.\" 그녀는 서랍을 닫으며 말했다. 하지만 그 행동을 보고 그는 자기가 제대로 짚었음을 알아챘고, 그녀의 손을 거칠게 밀쳐 내면서 그녀가 가장 중요한 서류들을 넣어 두곤 한다는 것을 아는 손가방을 잽싸게 낚아챘다. 그녀가 그 손가방을 잡아당기려 했지만, 그는 그녀를 떠밀었다.\n\n\"앉아요! 당신한테 할 말이 있소.\" 그는 손가방을 겨드랑이에 끼고 팔꿈치로 어찌나 세게 눌렀던지 어깨가 위로 솟아올랐다. 그녀는 놀라고 겁에 질린 채 말없이 그를 바라보았다.\n\n\"이 집에서 당신 정부를 맞아들이는 일은 용납하지 않겠다고 내가 말했을 텐데.\"\n\n\"그이를 만나야 했어요, 그건…….\"\n\n그녀는 이유를 찾지 못해 말을 멈췄다.\n\n\"여자가 무엇 때문에 정부를 만나고 싶어 하는지, 그 자세한 사정까지 내가 따지지는 않겠소.\"\n\n\"제 말은, 그저…….\" 그녀가 얼굴을 화끈 붉히며 말했다. 그의 이런 천박함이 그녀를 화나게 했고, 동시에 용기를 주었다. \"당신은 나를 모욕하는 게 얼마나 쉬운 일인지 정녕 모르세요?\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"정직한 남자, 정직한 여자라면 모욕이라 할 수 있겠지. 하지만 도둑한테 도둑이라고 말하는 건 그저 la constatation d'un fait일 뿐이오.\"\n\n* la constatation d'un fait: 프랑스어로 '사실의 확인'.\n\n\"이런 잔인함은 내가 몰랐던 당신의 새로운 면이네요.\"\n\"남편이 아내에게 자유를 주고, 제 이름이라는 떳떳한 보호막까지 내주면서, 단지 예법을 지키라는 조건 하나를 다는 것을 두고 당신은 잔인하다 하는 거요? 그게 잔인한 거요?\"\n\n\"잔인한 것보다 더 나빠요. 알고 싶다면 말해 주죠, 그건 비열한 거예요!\" 안나가 증오를 터뜨리며 외쳤고, 일어나 나가려 했다.\n\n\"안 돼!\" 그가 여느 때보다 한 음정이나 높아진 날카로운 목소리로 새된 비명을 질렀고, 큼직한 두 손으로 어찌나 거칠게 그녀의 팔을 움켜쥐었던지 그가 짓누른 팔찌 자국이 벌겋게 남았다. 그는 그녀를 억지로 제자리에 도로 앉혔다.\n\n\"비열하다고! 굳이 그 말을 쓰겠다면, 정작 비열한 건 남편의 빵을 먹으면서 정부 때문에 남편과 자식을 저버리는 짓이지!\"\n\n그녀는 고개를 숙였다. 그녀는 전날 저녁 정부에게 했던 말, 곧 그이가 자기 남편이고 당신은 군더더기일 뿐이라는 말을 입에 올리지 않았다. 그런 생각조차 하지 않았다. 그녀는 그의 말이 모두 옳다고 느꼈고, 그저 나직이 말했다.\n\n\"내 처지를 두고 나 자신이 느끼는 것보다 더 나쁘게 그릴 수는 없을 거예요. 그런데 이런 말씀을 왜 다 하시는 거죠?\"\n\n\"왜 하느냐고? 무엇 때문에?\" 그가 여전히 노기 띤 목소리로 말을 이었다. \"겉으로나마 체면을 지키라는 내 뜻을 당신이 따르지 않았으니, 이런 상황을 끝내기 위한 조치를 내가 취하겠다는 걸 알리려는 거요.\"\n\n\"어차피 곧, 아주 곧 끝날 거예요.\" 그녀는 말했다. 그리고 다시금, 가까이 다가온, 이제는 도리어 바라게 된 죽음을 떠올리자 눈에 눈물이 차올랐다.\n\n\"당신과 당신 정부가 꾸민 것보다 더 빨리 끝나게 될 거요! 짐승 같은 욕정의 쾌락을 기어이 채워야겠다면…….\"\n\n\"알렉세이 알렉산드로비치! 너그럽지 못하다고는 안 하겠어요. 하지만 쓰러진 사람을 때리는 건 신사다운 짓이 아니에요.\"\n\n\"그래, 당신은 자기 생각밖에 안 하지! 한때 당신 남편이었던 사람의 고통 따위엔 관심도 없고. 그자의 인생 전부가 망가졌다는 것도, 그자가 고통스러… 고통스러…….\" 그래 봤자 당신은 아무렇지도 않겠지.\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 어찌나 빨리 말을 쏟아 냈던지 말을 더듬었고, '고통스럽다'라는 단어를 도무지 제대로 발음하지 못했다. 결국 그는 그것을 '고통스러읍다'라고 내뱉었다. 그녀는 웃음이 터질 것 같았고, 이런 순간에 무언가가 자기를 웃길 수 있다는 사실에 곧바로 부끄러워졌다. 그리고 처음으로, 한순간, 그녀는 그의 심정을 헤아렸고, 그의 처지에 자신을 놓아 보았으며, 그가 가엾어졌다. 하지만 그녀가 무슨 말을, 무슨 행동을 할 수 있었겠는가? 그녀는 고개를 떨군 채 말없이 앉아 있었다. 그도 한동안 말이 없다가, 이내 차갑고 덜 날카로운 목소리로, 별다른 의미도 없는 말들을 되는대로 강조해 가며 말하기 시작했다.\n\n\"당신한테 말하러 왔소…….\" 그가 말했다.\n\n그녀는 그를 흘끗 보았다. '아니야, 내 착각이었어.' 그녀는 그가 '고통스럽다'라는 단어에서 더듬거릴 때의 얼굴 표정을 떠올리며 생각했다. '아니, 저렇게 흐리멍덩한 눈에, 저런 자기만족에 젖은 사람이 무얼 느낄 수 있겠어?'\n\n\"나는 아무것도 바꿀 수 없어요.\" 그녀가 속삭이듯 말했다.\n\n\"당신한테 말해 두러 왔소. 나는 내일 모스크바로 떠날 것이고, 다시는 이 집으로 돌아오지 않을 거요. 그리고 이혼을 처리하는 일을 맡길 변호사를 통해 내가 결정한 바를 통보받게 될 거요. 내 아들은 내 누이에게 보낼 거요.\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 아들에 관해 하려 했던 말을 가까스로 떠올리며 말했다.\n\n\"당신은 나를 괴롭히려고 세료자를 데려가는 거예요.\" 그녀가 이맛살 아래로 그를 올려다보며 말했다. \"당신은 그 애를 사랑하지 않잖아요…… 세료자만은 내게 남겨 줘요!\"\n\n\"그래, 나는 아들에 대한 정마저 잃었소. 그 애가 당신에게 느끼는 혐오와 얽혀 있으니까. 하지만 그래도 나는 그 애를 데려가겠소. 잘 있으시오!\"\n\n그러고는 그가 나가려 했지만, 이번에는 그녀가 그를 붙잡았다.\n\n\"알렉세이 알렉산드로비치, 세료자만은 내게 남겨 줘요!\" 그녀가 다시 한번 속삭였다. \"더는 할 말이 없어요. 내가…… 곧 몸을 풀 때까지만 세료자를 남겨 줘요. 그 애를 남겨 줘요!\"\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 발끈 화를 내며 그녀에게서 손을 홱 빼더니, 한마디 말도 없이 방을 나가 버렸다.\n\n# Chapter 5\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 페테르부르크의 저명한 변호사 사무실 대기실에 들어섰을 때, 그곳은 사람들로 가득 차 있었다. 여자 셋—노부인 한 명, 젊은 여자 한 명, 상인의 아내 한 명—과 신사 셋—손가락에 반지를 낀 독일인 은행가 한 명, 턱수염을 기른 상인 한 명, 그리고 목에 십자가를 단, 관복을 입은 성난 얼굴의 관리 한 명—이 누가 봐도 이미 오랫동안 기다려 온 모양이었다. 서기 둘이 책상에서 사각거리는 펜으로 무언가를 쓰고 있었다. 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치 자신이 책상 비품에 대해서는 무척 까다로운 편이었는데, 이곳의 비품은 유난히 훌륭했다. 그는 그것을 알아보지 않을 수 없었다. 서기 하나가 일어서지도 않은 채 눈을 반쯤 감으며 성난 얼굴로 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치 쪽으로 돌아앉았다. \"무슨 일로 오셨소?\"\n\n그는 어떤 용무로 변호사를 만나야 한다고 대답했다.\n\n\"바쁘십니다.\" 서기가 무뚝뚝하게 대꾸하고는 펜으로 기다리는 사람들을 가리키더니, 다시 글을 써 내려갔다.\n\n\"나를 만날 시간을 좀 내줄 수 없겠소?\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 말했다.\n\n\"빈 시간이 없으십니다. 늘 바쁘시니까요. 차례를 기다려 주십시오.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 수고스럽겠지만 이 명함을 그분께 전해 주시오.\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 자기 신분을 감추는 것이 불가능함을 깨닫고 위엄 있게 말했다.\n\n서기는 명함을 받아 들고, 거기 적힌 것이 마뜩잖은 기색이 분명한 채로 문 쪽으로 갔다.\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 원칙적으로 재판 공개에 찬성하는 편이었지만, 더 높은 관료적 견지에서 그 원칙을 러시아에 적용하는 것은 탐탁지 않게 여겼고, 황제의 권위로 제정된 것을 그가 못마땅해할 수 있는 한도 내에서 그것에 반대했다. 그의 평생은 행정 업무로 점철되어 왔고, 따라서 그가 무언가를 못마땅하게 여길 때면 그 불만은 어느 부서에나 실수란 불가피하며 개혁의 여지가 있다는 인식으로 누그러지곤 했다. 그는 새로운 공개 법정에서 사건을 맡은 변호사들에게 가해지는 제약을 못마땅하게 여겼다. 하지만 그때까지 그는 법정과 아무 관계도 없었고, 그래서 단지 이론적으로만 재판 공개에 반대해 온 셈이었다. 그런데 이제 그 불만은 변호사 대기실에서 받은 불쾌한 인상으로 한층 강해졌다.\n\n\"곧 들어오시랍니다.\" 서기가 말했다. 그리고 이 분쯤 지나자 정말로 문간에 변호사와 직접 상담하고 나온 어느 늙은 사무 변호사의 커다란 형체가 나타났다.\n\n변호사는 작고 땅딸막한 대머리 사내로, 거무스름한 불그스레한 턱수염에 연한 빛깔의 긴 눈썹, 툭 튀어나온 이마를 가지고 있었다. 그는 크라바트에서부터 이중으로 늘어뜨린 회중시곗줄과 반들반들 윤이 나는 부츠에 이르기까지, 마치 결혼식에라도 가는 차림이었다. 얼굴은 영리하고 남자다웠지만, 옷차림은 멋쟁이연하며 천박한 데가 있었다.\n\n\"자, 들어오시지요.\" 변호사가 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에게 말하고는, 카레닌을 앞세워 침울하게 안으로 들이고 문을 닫았다.\n\n\"앉으시지 않겠습니까?\" 그는 서류로 뒤덮인 책상 옆 안락의자를 가리켰다. 그 자신도 자리에 앉아, 흰 털이 덮인 짤막한 손가락의 작은 두 손을 비비며 고개를 한쪽으로 갸웃했다. 그러나 그가 이런 자세로 막 자리를 잡자마자 나방 한 마리가 책상 위로 날아들었다. 변호사는 도무지 그에게서 기대할 수 없을 만큼 날쌘 동작으로 두 손을 펴 나방을 잡고는 원래의 자세로 돌아갔다.\n\n\"제 용무를 말씀드리기에 앞서,\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 놀란 눈으로 변호사의 동작을 좇으며 말했다. \"선생께 의논드릴 용무가 철저히 비밀에 부쳐져야 한다는 점을 말씀드려야겠습니다.\"\n\n변호사의 툭 튀어나온 불그스레한 콧수염이 거의 알아챌 수 없는 미소로 양옆으로 벌어졌다.\n\n\"제게 맡겨진 비밀을 지키지 못한다면 변호사라 할 수 없겠지요. 하지만 증거를 원하신다면……\"\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 그의 얼굴을 흘끗 보았고, 그 약삭빠른 잿빛 눈이 웃고 있으며, 이미 모든 것을 알고 있는 듯하다는 것을 알아챘다.\n\n\"내 이름을 아시오?\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 말을 이었다.\n\n\"모든 러시아인이 그러하듯, 저도 선생을, 그리고 선생께서 하시는 훌륭한\"—그는 또 나방 한 마리를 잡았다—\"일을 알고 있습니다.\" 변호사가 머리를 숙이며 말했다.\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 한숨을 쉬며 용기를 끌어모았다. 그러나 일단 마음을 정하자 그는 주눅 들거나 머뭇거리지 않고, 군데군데 한 단어씩 힘주어 강조해 가며 날카로운 목소리로 말을 이었다.\n\n\"불행하게도,\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 입을 열었다. \"저는 결혼 생활에서 기만을 당했고, 법적인 수단으로 아내와의 모든 관계를 끊고자 합니다. 다시 말해 이혼을 하되, 제 아들이 어미 곁에 남지 않도록 하는 식으로 말입니다.\"\n\n변호사의 잿빛 눈은 애써 웃지 않으려 했으나, 억누를 수 없는 환희로 춤추고 있었고, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 그것이 그저 돈벌이가 될 일감을 막 얻은 사람의 기쁨만은 아님을 알아챘다. 거기에는 승리감과 환희가 있었고, 그가 아내의 눈에서 보았던 악의에 찬 번득임과 같은 번득임이 있었다.\n\n\"이혼을 성사시키는 데 제 도움을 원하신다는 말씀입니까?\"\n\n\"그렇소, 바로 그렇소. 다만 미리 말해 두어야겠는데, 어쩌면 내가 선생의 시간과 수고를 헛되이 할지도 모르겠소. 나는 그저 예비적인 단계로 의논하러 온 것이오. 나는 이혼을 원하지만, 그것이 어떤 형식으로 가능한지가 내게는 매우 중요하오. 만일 그 형식이 내 요구에 부합하지 않는다면, 법적 이혼을 단념할 수도 있소.\"\n\n\"오, 늘 그런 법이지요.\" 변호사가 말했다. \"그리고 그것은 늘 선생께서 결정하실 일입니다.\"\n\n그는 억누를 수 없는 즐거운 기색을 내보였다가 의뢰인의 기분을 상하게 할까 봐, 시선을 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 발에 내려놓았다. 그는 코앞으로 날아든 나방을 바라보며 손을 움직였으나, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 처지를 헤아려 잡지는 않았다.\n\n\"이 문제에 관한 우리 법률은 대략적인 윤곽이라면 제가 알고 있습니다만,\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 말을 이었다. \"그런 일들이 실제로 어떤 형식을 거쳐 이루어지는지 짐작이라도 했으면 합니다.\"\n\n\"그러니까,\" 변호사는 눈을 들지 않은 채, 흡족한 기색으로 의뢰인의 어투를 따라 가며 대꾸했다. \"선생께서 원하시는 바를 이룰 수 있는 모든 방법을 제가 앞에 펼쳐 보여 드리기를 바라신다는 말씀이군요?\"\n\n그리고 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 그렇다는 듯 고개를 끄덕이자, 그는 군데군데 붉게 물들어 가는 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 얼굴을 이따금 슬쩍슬쩍 훔쳐보며 말을 이었다.\n\n\"우리 법률에 따르면 이혼은,\" 그는 우리 법률에 대한 미미한 못마땅함의 기색을 띠며 말했다. \"아시다시피 다음과 같은 경우에 가능합니다…… 잠깐만!\" 그는 문틈으로 머리를 들이민 서기에게 외치고는, 그래도 자리에서 일어나 그에게 몇 마디 건넨 뒤 다시 앉았다. \"……다음과 같은 경우입니다. 부부 가운데 한쪽의 신체적 결함, 연락 없이 오 년간 지속된 유기,\" 그는 털 덮인 짤막한 손가락 하나를 꼬부리며 말했다. \"간통\" (이 단어를 그는 분명한 만족감을 담아 발음했다) \"이는 다음과 같이 세분됩니다\" (그는 세 경우와 그 세부 항목이 도무지 한데 묶일 수 없는 것임에도 살진 손가락을 계속 꼬부려 나갔다). \"남편 또는 아내의 신체적 결함, 남편 또는 아내의 간통.\" 이제 손가락을 다 써 버린 그는 꼬부린 손가락을 모두 펴며 말을 이었다. \"이상이 이론적인 견해입니다. 하지만 선생께서 영광스럽게도 저를 찾아오신 것은 그 실제 적용을 알고자 하심이리라 짐작됩니다. 그러니 선례에 비추어 말씀드리자면, 실제로 이혼 사건은 모두 다음의 경우로 귀결될 수 있습니다. 신체적 결함도, 유기도 없는 것으로 보아도 되겠지요……?\"\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 그렇다는 뜻으로 고개를 숙였다.\n\n\"……그렇다면 다음의 경우로 귀결됩니다. 부부 가운데 한쪽의 간통, 그리고 쌍방 합의에 의한 유죄 당사자의 현장 적발, 그런 합의가 이루어지지 않을 경우에는 우발적인 적발입니다. 다만 후자의 경우는 실제로는 드물다는 점을 인정해야겠지요.\" 변호사는 이렇게 말하고는 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치를 슬쩍 훔쳐보며, 마치 권총을 파는 사람이 각 무기의 장점을 늘어놓은 뒤 손님의 선택을 기다리듯 말을 멈추었다. 그러나 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 아무 말도 하지 않았으므로, 변호사는 말을 이었다. \"제 생각에 가장 흔하고 단순하며 분별 있는 방법은 쌍방 합의에 의한 간통입니다. 교양 없는 사람을 상대로라면 이렇게 표현하지는 않겠습니다만,\" 그가 말했다. \"선생께라면 이 정도는 이해하시리라 짐작됩니다.\"\n\n그러나 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 너무도 혼란스러운 나머지, 쌍방 합의에 의한 간통이라는 것의 온갖 합리성을 단번에 이해하지 못했고, 그의 눈은 그 어리둥절함을 드러냈다. 하지만 변호사가 곧바로 그를 도왔다.\n\n\"사람들이 더 이상 함께 살 수 없다—여기에 한 가지 사실이 있는 셈입니다. 그리고 양쪽이 그 점에 합의한다면, 세부 사항이나 절차 따위는 중요치 않게 됩니다. 동시에 이것이야말로 가장 단순하고 가장 확실한 방법이지요.\"\n\n이제 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 충분히 이해했다. 그러나 그에게는 그런 계획을 실행하는 데 걸림돌이 되는 종교적 양심의 가책이 있었다.\n\n\"이번 경우에는 그것이 불가능하오.\" 그가 말했다. \"단 하나의 대안만이 가능하오. 내가 가지고 있는 편지들로 뒷받침되는, 의도치 않은 적발 말이오.\"\n\n편지 이야기가 나오자 변호사는 입술을 오므리며, 가늘고 작은, 측은해하면서도 경멸하는 듯한 소리를 냈다.\n\n\"부디 헤아려 주십시오.\" 그가 입을 열었다. \"그런 종류의 사건은 아시다시피 교회의 관할에 속합니다. 그리고 신부님들은 그런 종류의 사건이라면 사소하기 그지없는 세부 사항까지 파고들기를 즐기시지요.\" 그는 신부들의 그런 취향에 공감하는 기색을 내비치며 미소 지었다. \"물론 편지가 부분적인 증거는 될 수 있습니다. 하지만 현장 적발이 가장 직접적인 방식으로, 그러니까 목격자에 의해 이루어져야만 합니다. 사실, 영광스럽게도 제게 일을 맡겨 주신다면, 어떤 방법을 쓸지는 제 선택에 맡겨 두시는 편이 좋습니다. 결과를 원하신다면, 수단도 받아들이셔야 하니까요.\"\n\n\"만일 그렇다면……\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 별안간 새파랗게 질리며 입을 열었다. 그러나 바로 그 순간 변호사가 일어서더니, 또다시 끼어든 서기에게 말을 하러 문 쪽으로 갔다.\n\n\"수임료를 흥정하지 않는다고 그 부인께 전하게!\" 그가 말하고는 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에게 돌아왔다.\n\n돌아오는 길에 그는 들키지 않게 나방 한 마리를 또 잡았다. '여름쯤이면 내 렙 커튼이 꼴이 말이 아니겠군!' 그는 인상을 찌푸리며 생각했다.\n\n* 렙: 골이 진 직물.\n\n\"그래, 무슨 말씀을 하시던 참이었지요……?\" 그가 말했다.\n\"내 결정은 편지로 알려 드리겠소.\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 일어서며 말하고는 책상을 움켜잡았다. 잠시 말없이 서 있다가 그가 말했다. \"그러니까 선생의 말씀으로 미루어, 이혼은 가능하다고 결론지어도 되겠소? 선생의 조건이 어떻게 되는지 알려 주셨으면 하오.\"\n\n\"제게 행동의 완전한 자유를 주신다면 가능합니다.\" 변호사는 그의 질문에는 대답하지 않은 채 말했다. \"선생의 기별은 언제쯤 받을 수 있다고 보면 되겠습니까?\" 그가 눈과 반들거리는 부츠를 빛내며 문 쪽으로 다가가며 물었다.\n\n\"일주일 안에. 이 사건을 맡으실지, 또 어떤 조건으로 하실지에 대한 선생의 답은 부디 내게 알려 주시오.\"\n\n\"잘 알겠습니다.\"\n\n변호사는 정중히 머리를 숙이고 의뢰인을 문밖으로 내보낸 뒤, 혼자 남게 되자 즐거운 기분에 흠뻑 빠져들었다. 어찌나 유쾌했던지, 그는 자기 원칙을 어기면서까지 흥정을 하던 그 부인에게 수임료를 깎아 주었고, 나방 잡기도 그만두었으며, 마침내 다음 겨울에는 시고닌의 집처럼 가구에 벨벳을 씌워야겠다고 마음먹었다.\n\n# Chapter 6\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 8월 17일 위원회 회의에서 눈부신 승리를 거두었지만, 그 결과 이 승리는 도리어 그의 발밑을 허물어 버렸다. 모든 부문에 걸쳐 토착 부족들의 상태를 조사하기 위한 새 위원회가 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 불어넣은 보기 드문 신속함과 열의로 구성되어 목적지로 파견되었다. 석 달 만에 보고서가 제출되었다. 토착 부족들의 상태가 정치적, 행정적, 경제적, 인종지학적, 물질적, 종교적 측면에서 조사되었다. 이 모든 질문에는 훌륭하게 진술된 답변들이 갖추어져 있었고, 그 답변들은 한 점의 의심도 허용하지 않았는데, 그것이 늘 오류를 범하기 마련인 인간의 사고에서 나온 산물이 아니라 모두가 관청 활동의 산물이었기 때문이다. 답변들은 모두 지사들과 교회 수장들이 제공한 공식 자료에 근거했고, 그것은 다시 군 치안판사와 교회 감독관의 보고에 근거했으며, 그 보고는 또다시 교구 감독관과 교구 사제의 보고에 근거한 것이었다. 그리하여 이 모든 답변은 망설임 없이 단정적이었다. 예컨대 흉작의 원인이라든가, 어떤 부족들이 자기네 오랜 신앙을 고집하는 이유 같은—관청이라는 기계가 편리하게 개입하지 않는 한 수백 년이 지나도 풀리지 않고 풀릴 수도 없는—온갖 질문들이 충실하고 망설임 없는 해답을 얻었다. 그리고 이 해답은 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 주장에 유리한 것이었다. 그러나 지난 회의에서 뼛속까지 찔린 듯한 모욕을 느꼈던 스트레모프는 위원회 보고서를 받자 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 미처 예상하지 못한 책략을 썼다. 스트레모프는 몇몇 위원을 거느리고 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치 편으로 넘어왔고, 카레닌이 제안한 조치를 열렬히 옹호하는 데 만족하지 않고 같은 방향으로 한층 극단적인 조치들을 제안했다. 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 근본 취지에 어긋나게 더욱 과장된 이 조치들은 위원회에서 통과되었고, 그제야 스트레모프 책략의 목적이 드러났다. 극단으로 치달은 그 조치들은 대번에 너무도 터무니없어 보여서, 최고 당국이며 여론이며 지성을 갖춘 부인들이며 신문들이 일제히 들고일어나 그 조치들과 그것들의 명목상 아버지인 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에게 분노를 쏟아 냈다. 스트레모프는 마치 자기는 카레닌을 맹목적으로 따랐을 뿐이고, 벌어진 일에 깜짝 놀라 괴로워하기라도 하는 듯이 꾸미며 뒤로 물러났다. 이것은 곧 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 패배를 뜻했다. 그러나 그는 쇠약해진 건강에도, 가정의 비탄에도 굴하지 않았다. 위원회에는 분열이 생겼다. 스트레모프를 우두머리로 하는 일부 위원들은, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 설립한 조사 위원회를 믿었던 것이라며 자기들의 실수를 변명했고, 그 위원회의 보고서는 쓰레기에 불과하며 그저 한 무더기의 휴지 조각일 뿐이라고 주장했다. 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 공식 문서에 대한 그처럼 혁명적인 태도가 안고 있는 위험을 알아챈 추종자들을 거느리고, 조사 위원회가 얻어 낸 진술들을 계속 옹호했다. 그 결과 상층부에서도, 심지어 사교계에서도 모든 것이 혼돈에 빠졌고, 누구나 관심을 가졌지만 토착 부족들이 정말로 빈곤해지고 몰락해 가는지 아니면 번영하는 상태에 있는지 아무도 알 수 없었다. 이로 인해, 또 한편으로는 아내의 부정 때문에 그에게 퍼부어진 멸시로 인해, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 처지는 매우 위태로워졌다. 그리고 이런 처지에서 그는 중대한 결심을 했다. 위원회가 놀란 가운데, 그는 직접 현지로 가서 이 문제를 조사하겠다고 허가를 청하겠노라 선언했다. 그리고 허가를 얻어 낸 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 이 머나먼 지방으로 떠날 채비를 했다.\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 출발은 커다란 화제를 불러일으켰는데, 출발 직전 그가 목적지까지 가는 데 쓰라고 책정된 말 열두 필분의 역마 비용을 공식적으로 반납했기 때문에 더욱 그러했다.\n\n\"참으로 고결하다고 생각해요.\" 베치가 이 일을 두고 먀흐카야 공작 부인에게 말했다. \"이제 어디에나 철도가 있다는 걸 누구나 아는 마당에 무엇하러 역마 비용을 받겠어요?\"\n\n그러나 먀흐카야 공작 부인은 동의하지 않았고, 트베르스카야 공작 부인의 의견은 오히려 그녀의 비위를 거슬렀다.\n\n\"댁이야 그렇게 말하기 좋겠지요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"몇백만 루블이나 가졌는지 모를 정도니까. 하지만 나는 우리 남편이 여름에 시찰을 나가면 정말 기뻐요. 그이한테도 좋고 여기저기 돌아다니는 게 즐거운 일이거든요. 게다가 그 돈으로 마차와 마부를 두기로 우리끼리 정해 둔 거고요.\"\n\n머나먼 지방으로 가는 길에 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 모스크바에 사흘간 머물렀다.\n\n도착한 이튿날 그는 총독을 방문하고 마차로 돌아오는 중이었다. 언제나 마차와 썰매가 붐비는 가제트니 광장 부근의 사거리에서, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 불현듯 누군가가 자기 이름을 어찌나 크고 명랑한 목소리로 부르는지 돌아보지 않을 수 없었다. 보도 모퉁이에, 짧고 멋스러운 외투에 챙 낮은 유행하는 모자를 한쪽으로 비스듬히 멋들어지게 쓰고서, 하얀 이와 붉은 입술을 반짝 드러내며 미소 짓는 스테판 아르카지치가 환하게 빛을 내는 젊고 발랄한 모습으로 서 있었다. 그는 기운차고 다급하게 그를 부르며 멈추라고 졸랐다. 그는 한 팔을 모퉁이에 멈춰 선 마차의 창에 걸쳐 두고 있었고, 그 창 밖으로 벨벳 모자를 쓴 부인과 두 아이의 머리가 내밀어져 있었다. 스테판 아르카지치는 미소 지으며 매부에게 손짓을 했다. 부인도 다정한 미소를 지으며 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치를 향해 손을 흔들었다. 아이들을 데리고 나온 돌리였다.\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 모스크바에서 아무도 만나고 싶지 않았고, 그중에서도 아내의 오빠는 더더욱 만나고 싶지 않았다. 그는 모자를 들어 보이고 그대로 지나치려 했지만, 스테판 아르카지치는 마부에게 멈추라 이르고 눈 위를 가로질러 그에게 달려왔다.\n\n\"이런, 알리지도 않다니 너무하잖나! 온 지 오래됐나? 어제 뒤소 호텔에 들렀다가 방문객 명단에서 '카레닌'을 봤는데, 그게 자네일 줄은 꿈에도 몰랐지.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 마차 창 안으로 머리를 들이밀며 말했다. \"알았더라면 찾아갔을 텐데. 만나서 반갑네!\" 그가 눈을 털어 내려고 한쪽 발을 다른 발에 부딪치며 말했다. \"알리지도 않다니, 정말 너무하지 않나!\" 그는 거듭 말했다.\n\n\"시간이 없었네. 무척 바빠서.\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 무뚝뚝하게 대꾸했다.\n\n\"내 아내한테 가 보게. 자네를 그렇게 보고 싶어 하니.\"\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 얼어붙은 발을 감싸고 있던 무릎덮개를 펼쳐 내고, 마차에서 내려 눈을 밟으며 다리야 알렉산드로브나에게로 갔다.\n\n\"아니, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치, 어쩌자고 이렇게 우리를 모른 척하시는 거예요?\" 돌리가 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"무척 바빴습니다. 만나서 반갑습니다!\" 그는 그것이 성가시다는 기색이 역력한 어조로 말했다. \"어떻게 지내십니까?\"\n\n\"말씀해 주세요, 우리 사랑스러운 안나는 어떻게 지내나요?\"\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 무언가 우물거리고는 그대로 가려 했다. 그러나 스테판 아르카지치가 그를 붙잡았다.\n\n\"내일 이렇게 하세. 돌리, 식사하자고 청해 봐. 코즈니셰프와 페스초프를 불러서 우리 모스크바의 명사들로 대접해 드리자고.\"\n\n\"네, 부디 오세요.\" 돌리가 말했다. \"다섯 시에, 아니면 원하시면 여섯 시에 기다릴게요. 우리 사랑스러운 안나는 어떻게 지내나요? 얼마나 오래…….\"\n\n\"아주 잘 지냅니다.\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 미간을 찌푸리며 우물거렸다. \"반갑습니다!\" 그러고는 자기 마차 쪽으로 걸음을 옮겼다.\n\n\"오실 거죠?\" 돌리가 그의 뒤에 대고 외쳤다.\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 무어라 말했지만, 오가는 마차들의 소음에 묻혀 돌리는 알아듣지 못했다.\n\n\"내일 들르겠네!\" 스테판 아르카지치가 그에게 소리쳤다.\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 자기 마차에 올라타, 누구도 보지 않고 누구에게도 보이지 않으려는 듯 그 안에 몸을 파묻었다.\n\n\"별난 친구로군!\" 스테판 아르카지치가 아내에게 말하고는, 시계를 흘긋 본 뒤 아내와 아이들에게 애정을 표하는 양 얼굴 앞에서 손짓을 해 보이고는 보도를 따라 경쾌하게 걸어갔다.\n\n\"스티바! 스티바!\" 돌리가 얼굴을 붉히며 불렀다.\n\n그가 돌아보았다.\n\n\"그리샤랑 타냐 외투를 사야 해요, 알잖아요. 돈을 줘요.\"\n\n\"걱정 말아. 청구서는 내가 치를 테니 가게에 그렇게 말해 둬!\" 그러고는 마차로 지나가는 아는 사람에게 상냥하게 고개를 끄덕여 보이며 사라져 버렸다.\n\n# Chapter 7\n\n다음 날은 일요일이었다. 스테판 아르카지치는 볼쇼이 극장의 발레 리허설을 보러 갔고, 얼마 전부터 자기가 후견을 맡게 된 어여쁜 무용수 마샤 치비소바에게 전날 저녁 약속했던 산호 목걸이를 건넸으며, 무대 뒤편 극장의 어슴푸레한 한낮의 빛 속에서 선물을 받고 환하게 빛나는 그녀의 어여쁜 작은 얼굴에 입을 맞추는 데 성공했다. 목걸이 선물 말고도 그는 발레가 끝난 뒤 만날 약속을 그녀와 잡고 싶었다. 발레 시작 무렵에는 올 수 없다고 설명한 뒤, 그는 마지막 막에 와서 그녀를 데리고 저녁 식사를 하러 가겠다고 약속했다. 극장에서 나온 스테판 아르카지치는 오호트니 거리로 마차를 몰아 가서 직접 만찬에 쓸 생선과 아스파라거스를 골랐고, 열두 시에는 뒤소 호텔에 도착했다. 그곳에서 그는 세 사람을 만나야 했는데, 다행히도 모두 같은 호텔에 묵고 있었다. 최근 외국에서 돌아와 그곳에 묵고 있는 레빈, 막 그 자리로 승진하여 모스크바로 시찰을 온 그의 부서의 새 책임자, 그리고 처남인 카레닌이었다. 카레닌은 만찬에 꼭 데려오기 위해 반드시 만나 두어야 했다.\n\n스테판 아르카지치는 만찬을 즐기는 것을 좋아했지만, 그보다 더 좋아한 것은 만찬을 베푸는 일이었다. 규모는 작지만 음식과 술은 물론 손님 선정에 이르기까지 아주 까다롭게 고른 만찬이었다. 그는 그날 만찬의 차림표가 특히 마음에 들었다. 싱싱한 농어, 아스파라거스, 그리고 la pièce de resistance—더할 나위 없이 훌륭하면서도 아주 소박한 로스트비프, 거기에 어울리는 포도주들이 나올 것이다. 먹고 마실 것은 그쯤으로 됐다. 키티와 레빈도 자리에 함께할 텐데, 그게 너무 빤히 드러나지 않도록 하기 위해 처녀 사촌 한 명과 젊은 셰르바츠키도 부르고, 손님들 가운데 la pièce de resistance에 해당하는 인물로 세르게이 코즈니셰프와 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치를 부를 것이다. 세르게이 이바노비치는 모스크바 사람이자 철학자였고, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 페테르부르크 사람이자 실무에 밝은 정치가였다. 그는 또한 잘 알려진 괴짜 열정가 페스초프도 부를 생각이었다. 자유주의자이자 굉장한 수다쟁이에 음악가요 역사가이며, 쉰 살이면서도 더없이 유쾌하고 젊은 사람인 그는, 코즈니셰프와 카레닌에게 곁들이는 양념이나 고명 노릇을 할 것이다. 그가 두 사람을 자극하여 서로 맞붙게 할 것이다.\n\n숲을 판 두 번째 대금이 상인에게서 들어왔는데 아직 다 쓰지 않았고, 돌리는 요즘 들어 무척 상냥하고 기분이 좋았으며, 만찬에 대한 생각은 어느 모로 보나 스테판 아르카지치를 흐뭇하게 했다. 그는 더할 나위 없이 들뜬 기분이었다. 조금 불쾌한 정황이 두 가지 있긴 했지만, 그 두 가지 정황은 스테판 아르카지치의 영혼에 넘쳐흐르는 유쾌한 명랑함의 바다에 잠겨 버렸다. 그 두 가지 정황이란 이러했다. 첫째는, 전날 거리에서 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치를 만났을 때 그가 자기를 냉랭하고 서먹하게 대하는 것을 알아챘다는 점이었다. 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 표정과, 그가 그들을 찾아오지도 도착을 알려 오지도 않았다는 사실을, 안나와 브론스키에 관해 들은 소문과 견주어 보고서, 스테판 아르카지치는 그 부부 사이에 뭔가 어긋난 일이 있다고 짐작했다.\n\n그것이 한 가지 거북한 일이었다. 또 한 가지 조금 거북한 사실은, 그의 부서의 새 책임자가 모든 새 책임자들이 그렇듯 벌써부터 무시무시한 인물이라는 평판을 얻고 있다는 점이었다. 그는 아침 여섯 시에 일어나 말처럼 일하고, 부하 직원들에게도 똑같이 일하라고 다그친다는 것이었다. 게다가 이 새 책임자는 태도가 곰처럼 무뚝뚝하다는 평판까지 있었고, 모든 소문에 따르면 그는 전임자가 속해 있던, 그리고 스테판 아르카지치 자신이 지금껏 속해 있던 부류와는 모든 면에서 정반대인 부류의 사람이었다. 전날 스테판 아르카지치는 제복 차림으로 관청에 나타났는데, 새 책임자는 무척 상냥하게 굴며 마치 아는 사이처럼 그와 이야기를 나누었다. 그래서 스테판 아르카지치는 평복 차림으로 그를 찾아가는 것이 도리라고 여겼다. 새 책임자가 자기를 따뜻하게 맞아 주지 않을지도 모른다는 생각, 그것이 또 하나의 불쾌한 일이었다. 하지만 스테판 아르카지치는 모든 것이 잘 풀리리라는 것을 본능적으로 느꼈다. '다들 사람이지, 다들 우리처럼 가엾은 죄인들이야. 무엇 때문에 심술궂게 다투겠어?' 그는 호텔로 들어가며 이렇게 생각했다.\n\n\"안녕한가, 바실리.\" 모자를 한쪽으로 비스듬히 쓴 채 복도로 들어서며, 그는 안면이 있는 하인에게 말을 걸었다. \"아니, 구레나룻을 길렀군! 레빈, 7호실이지, 그렇지? 좀 데려다주게. 그리고 아니치킨 백작이\"—이 사람이 새 책임자였다—\"손님을 받고 있는지 알아봐 주게.\"\n\n\"예, 나리.\" 바실리가 미소 지으며 대답했다. \"오랫동안 저희를 찾아 주시지 않았습니다.\"\n\n\"어제 왔었네, 다른 입구로 들어갔지. 여기가 7호실인가?\"\n\n스테판 아르카지치가 들어섰을 때, 레빈은 트베리에서 온 농부와 함께 방 한가운데 서서 갓 벗긴 곰 가죽의 치수를 재고 있었다.\n\n\"뭐야! 자네가 곰을 잡은 건가?\" 스테판 아르카지치가 소리쳤다. \"잘했네! 암곰이야? 잘 있었나, 아르히프!\"\n\n그는 농부와 악수를 나누고는, 외투와 모자도 벗지 않은 채 의자 끝에 걸터앉았다.\n\n\"이봐, 외투를 벗고 좀 앉아 있게나.\" 레빈이 그의 모자를 받으며 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 시간이 없어. 그저 잠깐 들렀을 뿐이야.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 대답했다. 그는 외투 앞섶을 풀어 헤쳤다가 이내 아예 벗어 버리고는, 한 시간 내내 앉아 레빈과 사냥이며 더없이 은밀한 화제들에 대해 이야기를 나누었다.\n\n\"자, 어디 말해 보게, 외국에서 뭘 했나? 어디어디 가 봤어?\" 농부가 나가자 스테판 아르카지치가 물었다.\n\n\"아, 독일, 프로이센, 프랑스, 영국에 머물렀지. 수도가 아니라 공업 도시들에 말이야. 그래서 내게는 새로운 것들을 아주 많이 봤어. 가길 잘했다 싶네.\"\n\n\"그래, 노동 문제 해결에 대한 자네 생각은 알고 있지.\"\n\n\"천만에. 러시아에는 노동 문제란 게 있을 수 없어. 러시아의 문제는 일하는 사람들과 토지의 관계 문제야. 그곳에도 그런 문제가 있긴 하지만, 거기서는 망가진 것을 고치는 일이고, 우리에게는…….\"\n\n스테판 아르카지치는 레빈의 말에 귀를 기울였다.\n\n\"그래, 그래!\" 그가 말했다. \"자네 말이 옳을 수도 있겠지. 하지만 자네가 활기차고, 곰 사냥도 하고, 일도 하고, 뭔가에 관심을 갖고 있다니 기쁘군. 셰르바츠키한테서 다른 얘기를 들었는데—그 친구가 자넬 만났다더군—자네가 영 우울한 상태로, 줄곧 죽음 얘기만 하더라고…….\"\n\n\"그래서 어떻단 말인가? 난 죽음에 대한 생각을 버린 게 아니야.\" 레빈이 말했다. \"내가 죽을 때가 됐다는 것도, 이 모든 게 다 부질없다는 것도 사실이지. 진심으로 하는 말이야. 난 내 생각과 내 일을 정말로 끔찍이 소중히 여겨. 하지만 실은 한번 생각해 보게. 우리의 이 온 세상이라는 것도 결국 자그마한 행성 위에 자라난 한 점 곰팡이에 지나지 않아. 그런데 우리가 뭔가 위대한 것을—사상이니 일이니 하는 것을—가질 수 있다고 여기는 건, 죄다 먼지요 재나 다름없어.\"\n\n\"하지만 그건 다 케케묵은 얘기 아닌가, 이 사람아!\"\n\n\"케케묵은 얘기지. 하지만 알겠나, 이걸 온전히 깨닫고 나면 어쩐지 모든 게 다 아무 의미도 없어져 버려. 오늘은 아니라도 내일이면 죽고, 아무것도 남지 않으리란 걸 깨닫고 나면, 모든 게 정말 하찮아지지! 난 내 생각이 무척 중요하다고 여기지만, 실은 그것이 설령 이루어진다 해도 저 곰을 잡는 일만큼이나 하찮은 것으로 드러나거든. 그러니 사람은 살아가는 거야, 사냥으로, 일로 즐기면서—죽음을 생각하지 않으려고 뭐든 하면서 말이야!\"\n\n스테판 아르카지치는 레빈의 말을 들으며 은근하고 다정한 미소를 지었다.\n\n\"자, 그것 보게! 이제 자네가 내 생각 쪽으로 돌아온 거야. 자네가 인생에서 즐거움을 좇는다고 날 몰아세웠던 거 기억나나? 그렇게 모질게 굴지 말게, 오, 도덕가 선생!\"\n\n\"아니, 그래도 인생에서 멋진 건…….\" 레빈이 머뭇거렸다. \"아, 나도 모르겠어. 내가 아는 건 우리가 머지않아 죽으리라는 것뿐이야.\"\n\n\"어째서 그렇게 빨리?\"\n\n\"그리고 알겠나, 죽음을 생각하면 인생에 매력은 덜해지지만, 그 대신 평온은 더해지는 거야.\"\n\n\"천만에, 끝마무리야말로 늘 가장 좋은 거지. 하지만 난 이만 가 봐야겠네.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 열 번째로 일어서며 말했다.\n\n\"아, 안 돼, 좀 더 있게나!\" 레빈이 그를 붙잡으며 말했다. \"그래, 우리 언제 다시 보지? 난 내일 떠나네.\"\n\n\"내가 참 좋은 사람이지! 아니, 바로 그 일 때문에 온 건데! 자네 오늘 우리 집 만찬에 꼭 와야 하네. 자네 형도 오고, 내 처남 카레닌도 와.\"\n\n\"설마 그가 여기 와 있다는 건 아니겠지?\" 레빈이 말하며 키티에 대해 물어보고 싶어졌다. 그는 겨울 초입에 그녀가 외교관의 아내인 언니와 함께 페테르부르크에 가 있다는 말을 들었고, 그녀가 돌아왔는지 어떤지는 알지 못했다. 하지만 그는 마음을 고쳐먹고 묻지 않았다. '그녀가 오든 말든, 내 알 바 아니지.' 그는 속으로 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"그래서 오는 거지?\"\n\n\"물론이야.\"\n\n\"그럼 다섯 시일세, 정장은 입지 말고.\"\n\n그러고 나서 스테판 아르카지치는 일어나 아래층으로 그의 부서의 새 책임자를 만나러 내려갔다. 스테판 아르카지치의 본능은 그를 잘못 인도하지 않았다. 그 무시무시한 새 책임자는 더없이 싹싹한 사람으로 드러났고, 스테판 아르카지치는 그와 함께 점심을 먹고 더 머무는 바람에, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에게 도착한 것은 네 시가 되어서였다.\n\n# Chapter 8\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 예배에서 돌아온 후 오전 내내 집 안에 머물렀다. 그날 아침 그에게는 두 가지 처리할 일이 있었다. 첫째는 페테르부르크로 향하는 길에 지금 모스크바에 와 있는 원주민 부족의 사절단을 맞아들였다가 보내는 일이었고, 둘째는 변호사에게 약속한 편지를 쓰는 일이었다. 이 사절단은 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 발의로 소집되긴 했으나 불편한, 심지어 위험한 측면이 없지 않았으므로, 그는 그들을 모스크바에서 만나게 된 것이 다행스러웠다. 이 사절단의 구성원들은 자기들의 임무며 자기들이 맡아야 할 역할에 대해 조금도 이해하지 못하고 있었다. 그들은 위원회 앞에 자기들의 곤궁과 실제 상황을 늘어놓고 정부의 지원을 요청하는 것이 자기들의 소임이라고 순진하게 믿었으며, 그들의 진술과 요청 가운데 어떤 것들은 적대 측의 주장을 뒷받침하여 결국 일 전체를 그르치게 된다는 것을 전혀 깨닫지 못했다. 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 오랫동안 그들과 분주하게 씨름하며, 그들이 벗어나서는 안 될 지침을 마련해 주었고, 그들을 돌려보내면서 사절단을 지도하기 위해 페테르부르크로 편지 한 통을 써 보냈다. 이 일에서 그가 가장 크게 의지한 사람은 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인이었다. 그녀는 사절단 문제의 전문가였으며, 그들을 어떻게 다루고 어떻게 가야 할 길로 이끌어야 하는지를 그녀보다 더 잘 아는 사람은 없었다. 이 과업을 마친 후 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 변호사에게 편지를 썼다. 그는 조금도 망설이지 않고 변호사가 최선이라 판단하는 대로 행동해도 좋다는 허락을 내렸다. 그는 편지 안에, 자기가 가져온 서류첩에 들어 있던 브론스키가 안나에게 보낸 쪽지 세 통을 동봉했다.\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 다시는 가족에게로 돌아가지 않을 작정으로 집을 떠났고, 변호사를 찾아가 비록 한 사람에게뿐이긴 했으나 자기 의도를 말했으며, 특히 그 문제를 실생활의 세계에서 잉크와 종이의 세계로 옮겨 놓았으므로, 그는 자기 의도에 점점 더 익숙해졌고 이제는 그것을 실행하는 일이 충분히 가능하다는 것을 또렷이 느끼게 되었다.\n\n그가 변호사에게 보낼 봉투를 봉하고 있을 때, 스테판 아르카지치의 우렁찬 목소리가 들려왔다. 스테판 아르카지치는 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 하인과 다투며 자기가 왔음을 전하라고 우기고 있었다.\n\n'상관없지.' 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 생각했다. '오히려 잘됐어. 처남에게 그의 누이에 대한 내 입장을 당장 알리고, 왜 내가 그와 함께 식사할 수 없는지 설명해 줘야지.'\n\n\"들어오게!\" 그는 서류를 챙겨 압지 사이에 끼워 넣으며 소리 내어 말했다.\n\n\"보게, 자네가 헛소리를 한 거잖아, 댁에 계시지 않은가!\" 안으로 들이기를 거부한 하인을 향해 스테판 아르카지치의 목소리가 응수했고, 오블론스키는 들어오면서 외투를 벗으며 방 안으로 걸어 들어왔다. \"이야, 자네를 만나게 되어 정말 기쁘군! 그래서 말인데, 부디……\" 스테판 아르카지치가 명랑하게 입을 열었다.\n\n\"갈 수가 없네.\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 선 채로 손님에게 앉으라는 말도 하지 않고 차갑게 말했다.\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는, 이혼 소송을 막 시작하려는 아내의 오빠와 자기가 마땅히 유지해야 할 그 냉랭한 관계로 곧장 들어서려 했다. 그러나 그는 스테판 아르카지치의 가슴속에 넘쳐흐르는 너그러움의 바다를 미처 계산에 넣지 못했다.\n\n스테판 아르카지치는 맑고 빛나는 두 눈을 크게 떴다.\n\n\"왜 못 온다는 건가? 무슨 말이야?\" 그는 어리둥절하여, 프랑스어로 물었다. \"아니, 약속이잖아. 우리 모두 자네만 기다리고 있는데.\"\n\n\"자네 집에서 식사할 수 없다는 걸 말해 두려 하네. 우리 사이에 존재해 온 친족 관계가 끊어져야 하기 때문이지.\"\n\n\"어떻게? 무슨 뜻이야? 무엇 때문에?\" 스테판 아르카지치가 미소를 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"내가 자네 누이, 그러니까 내 아내를 상대로 이혼 소송을 시작하려 하기 때문이네. 진작에……\"\n\n그러나 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 말을 채 끝내기도 전에, 스테판 아르카지치는 그가 예상했던 것과는 전혀 다르게 행동했다. 그는 신음을 내뱉으며 안락의자에 털썩 주저앉았다.\n\n\"아니, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치! 무슨 말을 하는 건가?\" 오블론스키가 외쳤고, 그의 괴로움이 얼굴에 역력히 드러났다.\n\n\"사실이네.\"\n\n\"미안하지만, 난 믿을 수가 없어, 믿을 수가 없네!\"\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는, 자기 말이 예상했던 효과를 내지 못했다는 것을, 그리고 자기 입장을 설명하는 일이 불가피하리라는 것을, 또한 어떤 설명을 한다 해도 처남과의 관계는 변함없이 그대로 남으리라는 것을 느끼며 자리에 앉았다.\n\n\"그래, 나는 이혼을 청해야 하는 괴로운 처지에 몰리고 말았네.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"한 가지만 말하겠네, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치. 나는 자네가 더없이 훌륭하고 정직한 사람임을 알고 있네. 또 안나를 — 미안하지만 그녀에 대한 내 생각은 바꿀 수 없어 — 착하고 더없이 훌륭한 여자로 알고 있어. 그러니, 미안하지만 나는 믿을 수가 없네. 뭔가 오해가 있는 거야.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"오, 그것이 한낱 오해이기만 하다면!……\"\n\n\"잠깐, 알겠네.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 끼어들었다. \"하지만 물론이지…… 한 가지, 자네 서둘러서는 안 되네. 그러면 안 돼, 절대 서둘러서는 안 돼!\"\n\n\"나는 서두르는 게 아니네.\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 차갑게 말했다. \"하지만 이런 일에서는 누구에게도 조언을 구할 수가 없는 법이지. 나는 이미 결심을 굳혔네.\"\n\n\"이건 끔찍한 일이야!\" 스테판 아르카지치가 말했다. \"한 가지만 하겠네, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치. 부디 그렇게 해 주게!\" 그가 말했다. \"내가 제대로 이해했다면, 아직 아무런 조처도 취해지지 않았어. 조언을 구하기 전에, 내 아내를 만나 그녀와 이야기를 나눠 보게. 그녀는 안나를 친자매처럼 사랑하고, 자네도 사랑하며, 또 놀라운 여자라네. 제발, 그녀와 이야기해 주게! 부디 그 부탁을 들어주게, 간청하네!\"\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 곰곰이 생각에 잠겼고, 스테판 아르카지치는 그의 침묵을 방해하지 않으며 동정 어린 눈으로 그를 바라보았다.\n\n\"그녀를 만나러 가 주겠나?\"\n\n\"모르겠네. 바로 그 때문에 자네를 찾아가지 않았던 거야. 우리 관계도 달라질 수밖에 없으리라 생각하네.\"\n\n\"왜 그렇지? 나는 그렇게 보지 않네. 우리의 인척 관계와는 별개로, 자네가 적어도 얼마간은 내가 늘 자네에게 품어 온 것과 똑같은 우정을…… 그리고 진심 어린 존경을 나에게 품고 있다고 믿게 해 주게.\" 스테판 아르카지치는 그의 손을 잡으며 말했다. \"설령 자네의 최악의 추측이 옳다 하더라도, 나는 어느 한쪽을 판단하는 일을 떠맡지 않을 거고, 또 결코 그러지 않을 거야. 그러니 우리 관계가 영향을 받아야 할 이유를 나는 모르겠네. 자, 그러니 이렇게 해 주게, 와서 내 아내를 만나 주게.\"\n\n\"글쎄, 우리는 이 문제를 다르게 보고 있군.\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 차갑게 말했다. \"하지만, 그 이야기는 그만하세.\"\n\n\"아니, 어쨌든 오늘 식사하러 오지 못할 까닭이 뭔가? 내 아내가 자네를 기다리고 있네. 부디, 와 주게. 그리고 무엇보다, 그녀와 그 일을 의논해 보게. 그녀는 놀라운 여자야. 제발, 무릎이라도 꿇고 자네에게 간청하네!\"\n\n\"자네가 그토록 원한다면, 가겠네.\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 한숨을 쉬며 말했다.\n\n그러고는 화제를 돌리고 싶어, 그는 두 사람 모두에게 흥미로운 일에 대해 물었다. 그것은 스테판 아르카지치가 속한 부서의 새 수장에 관한 것으로, 아직 늙지 않은 한 사내가 별안간 그토록 높은 자리로 승진한 일이었다.\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 전부터 아니치킨 백작에게 호감을 느끼지 못했고, 늘 그와 의견을 달리해 왔다. 그러나 이제는, 관료들이라면 쉽게 이해할 만한 감정에서 — 즉 관직에서 패배를 겪은 자가 승진한 자에게 느끼는 그 증오심에서 — 그를 견딜 수가 없었다.\n\n\"그래, 그를 만나 봤나?\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 악의 어린 미소를 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"물론이지. 어제 우리 회의에 나왔더군. 일을 아주 훌륭하게 파악하고 있는 것 같고, 무척 정력적이야.\"\n\n\"그래, 한데 그 정력이 어디로 향하고 있나?\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 말했다. \"그는 무언가 이루어 내려는 건가, 아니면 그저 이미 해 놓은 것을 허물려는 건가? 그 종이뭉치 행정이야말로 우리 정부의 크나큰 불행인데, 그가 바로 그것의 가장 합당한 대표자란 말이지.\"\n\n\"정말이지, 그에게서 무슨 흠을 잡을 수 있을지 난 모르겠네. 그의 정책이야 나도 모르지만, 한 가지는 분명하지 — 그는 아주 좋은 친구야.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 대답했다. \"방금 그를 만나고 오는 길인데, 정말 훌륭한 친구더군. 함께 점심을 들었고, 내가 그에게 그 음료, 자네도 알지, 포도주에 오렌지를 넣어 만드는 거 말이야, 그걸 만드는 법을 가르쳐 줬지. 어찌나 시원한지. 그런데 그가 그걸 모르고 있었다니 놀랍더군. 무척 마음에 들어 했어. 아니, 정말이지 그는 훌륭한 친구야.\"\n\n스테판 아르카지치가 시계를 흘끗 보았다.\n\n\"이런, 맙소사, 벌써 네 시잖아, 게다가 돌고부신네에도 가야 하는데! 그러니 부디 식사하러 들러 주게. 자네가 나와 내 아내를 얼마나 슬프게 할지 자네는 상상도 못 할 거야.\"\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 처남을 배웅하는 모습은, 그를 맞이했던 태도와는 사뭇 달랐다.\n\n\"약속했으니, 가겠네.\" 그가 지친 듯이 대답했다.\n\n\"정말 고맙네, 자네가 후회하지 않으리라 믿어.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 미소를 지으며 대답했다.\n\n그러고는 걸어가면서 외투를 걸치고, 하인의 머리를 토닥이며 빙긋 웃고는 밖으로 나갔다.\n\n\"다섯 시야, 그리고 정장은 입지 말고 와 주게.\" 그는 문간에서 돌아서며 다시 한번 소리쳤다.\n\n# Chapter 9\n\n다섯 시가 지나 손님 몇몇이 이미 도착했을 무렵에야 주인이 집에 돌아왔다. 그는 세르게이 이바노비치 코즈니셰프와 페스초프와 함께 들어왔는데, 그 두 사람은 같은 순간에 현관 앞에 다다른 참이었다. 오블론스키가 부르는 표현대로 하자면, 이들은 모스크바 지식인을 대표하는 두 거물이었다. 둘 다 인품과 지성으로 존경받는 사람들이었다. 그들은 서로를 존중했지만, 거의 모든 주제에서 완전하고 절망적으로 의견이 엇갈렸는데, 그것은 그들이 서로 반대 진영에 속해서가 아니라 바로 같은 진영에 속해 있었기 때문이었다(그들의 적들은 두 사람의 견해에서 어떤 차이도 인정하려 들지 않았다). 다만 그 같은 진영 안에서도 저마다 자기만의 독특한 색채를 띤 의견을 지니고 있었다. 그리고 반쯤 추상적인 문제에 관한 의견 차이만큼 좀처럼 극복하기 어려운 차이는 없는 법이라, 두 사람은 어떤 의견에서도 결코 일치하지 못했고, 실은 오래전부터 서로 상대방의 고질적인 빗나감을 화내지 않고 비웃는 데 익숙해져 있었다.\n\n날씨 이야기를 하며 막 문으로 들어서려던 참에 스테판 아르카지치가 그들을 따라잡았다. 응접실에는 이미 알렉산드르 드미트리예비치 셰르바츠키 공작, 젊은 셰르바츠키, 투롭친, 키티, 그리고 카레닌이 앉아 있었다.\n\n스테판 아르카지치는 자기가 없는 사이 응접실의 분위기가 신통치 않게 흘러가고 있음을 단번에 알아챘다. 가장 좋은 회색 비단 드레스를 입은 다리아 알렉산드로브나는 보육실에서 따로 저녁을 먹기로 된 아이들 걱정에, 그리고 남편의 부재에 마음을 쓰느라, 남편 없이 손님들을 어우러지게 만드는 일을 감당하지 못하고 있었다. 모두가 마치 방문 온 사제의 아내들처럼(노공작의 표현이었다) 앉아서, 자기들이 왜 여기 와 있는지 못내 의아해하며, 그저 침묵을 면하려고 억지로 말을 짜내고 있었다. 선량하고 소박한 사람인 투롭친은 영락없이 물 밖에 나온 물고기 신세였고, 그의 두툼한 입술이 스테판 아르카지치를 맞이하며 짓는 미소는 마치 말처럼 또렷이 이렇게 말하고 있었다. \"이봐, 친구, 자네가 나를 학자들 틈에 처박아 놓았군! 지금 같아선 술자리나 Château des Fleurs가 내 취향에 더 맞을 텐데 말이야!\" 노공작은 잠자코 앉아 작고 반짝이는 눈으로 한쪽에서 카레닌을 지켜보고 있었고, 스테판 아르카지치는 노인이 이미, 마치 철갑상어라도 되는 양 손님들이 맛보도록 초대된 그 정치가를 한마디로 요약할 문구를 머릿속에 만들어 두었음을 알아챘다. 키티는 문 쪽을 바라보며, 콘스탄틴 레빈이 들어설 때 얼굴이 붉어지지 않도록 온 힘을 그러모으고 있었다. 카레닌에게 소개받지 못한 젊은 셰르바츠키는 그 사실을 조금도 의식하지 않는 척하려 애쓰고 있었다. 카레닌 본인은 부인들과 함께하는 만찬에 어울리는 페테르부르크식 격식을 따라 야회복에 흰 넥타이 차림이었다. 스테판 아르카지치는 그의 얼굴을 보고, 그가 그저 약속을 지키려고 왔을 뿐이며 이 모임에 참석하는 것을 달갑잖은 의무로 여기고 있음을 알아챘다. 실은 스테판 아르카지치가 들어오기 전까지 모든 손님을 얼어붙게 만든 그 한기의 주된 책임자가 바로 그였다.\n\n응접실에 들어선 스테판 아르카지치는, 자기가 자리를 비우거나 시간에 늦을 때마다 늘 희생양이 되곤 하는 바로 그 공작에게 붙들려 있었다고 변명하며 사과하더니, 순식간에 모든 손님을 서로 인사시켰고, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치와 세르게이 코즈니셰프를 한자리에 모아 폴란드의 러시아화를 주제로 토론을 붙여 놓았는데, 두 사람은 페스초프까지 끌어들여 곧장 거기에 몰두했다. 그는 투롭친의 어깨를 툭 치고 귀에다 뭔가 우스운 말을 속삭이고는 아내와 노공작 곁에 앉혀 놓았다. 그러고는 키티에게 오늘 저녁 무척 예뻐 보인다고 말하고, 셰르바츠키를 카레닌에게 소개했다. 그는 순식간에 사교의 반죽을 잘도 주물러 놓아, 응접실은 무척 활기를 띠게 되었고 흥겨운 목소리들이 웅성거렸다. 도착하지 않은 사람은 콘스탄틴 레빈뿐이었다. 그러나 차라리 그편이 나았는데, 식당으로 들어간 스테판 아르카지치가 포트와인과 셰리주를 레비가 아니라 데프레에서 들여왔다는 것을 알고 질겁한 까닭이었다. 그는 마부를 되도록 빨리 레비의 가게로 보내라고 지시한 뒤 응접실로 돌아가려던 참이었다.\n\n식당에서 그는 콘스탄틴 레빈과 마주쳤다.\n\n\"나 늦지 않았나?\"\n\n\"자네가 늦지 않을 수가 있나!\" 스테판 아르카지치가 그의 팔을 붙들며 말했다.\n\n\"사람들 많이 왔나? 누가 와 있지?\" 레빈이 장갑으로 모자에 묻은 눈을 털어내며, 어쩔 수 없이 얼굴을 붉히면서 물었다.\n\n\"다 우리 식구들이지. 키티도 와 있어. 자, 가세, 카레닌에게 소개해 줄 테니.\"\n\n스테판 아르카지치는 자유주의적인 견해를 지녔으면서도, 카레닌과 만나는 것이 분명 영광스러운 대접으로 여겨지리라는 것을 잘 알고 있었기에, 가장 가까운 친구들에게 이 영예를 베풀곤 했다. 그러나 그 순간 콘스탄틴 레빈은 그런 사람과 알게 되는 기쁨을 온전히 느낄 만한 상태가 아니었다. 그는 브론스키를 만났던 그 잊을 수 없는 저녁 이후로 키티를 본 적이 없었다. 큰길에서 그녀를 언뜻 본 그 순간을 빼면 말이다. 그는 마음 깊은 곳에서 오늘 여기서 그녀를 만나게 되리라는 것을 알고 있었다. 그러나 마음을 자유롭게 두려고, 자기는 그것을 모른다고 스스로를 설득하려 애썼다. 이제 그녀가 여기 있다는 말을 듣자, 그는 별안간 너무도 큰 기쁨과 동시에 너무도 큰 두려움을 느껴 숨이 멎고 하려던 말을 입 밖에 낼 수 없었다.\n\n'그녀는 어떤 모습일까, 어떤 모습일까? 예전 그대로일까, 아니면 마차 안에 있던 그 모습일까? 다리아 알렉산드로브나가 한 말이 정말이라면? 정말이지 못할 게 뭐람?' 그는 생각했다.\n\n\"아, 부탁이네, 카레닌에게 소개해 주게.\" 그는 힘겹게 말을 꺼내고는, 필사적으로 마음을 다잡은 걸음걸이로 응접실로 들어가 그녀를 보았다.\n\n그녀는 예전 그대로의 모습도 아니었고, 마차 안에서 보았던 그 모습도 아니었다. 전혀 다른 모습이었다.\n\n그녀는 겁먹고, 수줍어하고, 부끄러워했으며, 그래서 더욱 매력적이었다. 그녀는 그가 방 안에 들어선 바로 그 순간 그를 보았다. 그녀는 그를 기다리고 있었던 것이다. 그녀는 기뻤고, 자기 자신의 기쁨에 너무도 당황한 나머지, 한순간—그가 언니에게 다가가 다시 그녀를 흘긋 바라본 그 순간—그녀도, 그도, 그리고 그 모든 것을 지켜본 돌리도, 그녀가 무너져 울음을 터뜨릴 것이라 생각했다. 그녀는 새빨개졌다가, 새하얘졌다가, 다시 새빨개졌고, 떨리는 입술로 그가 다가오기를 기다리며 아득해졌다. 그가 다가가 고개를 숙이고 말없이 손을 내밀었다. 살짝 떨리는 입술과 눈을 더 빛나게 하는 물기를 빼면, 그녀의 미소는 거의 차분했고, 그녀는 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"우리가 못 본 지 정말 오래됐네요!\" 그러고는 필사적으로 마음을 다잡으며 차가운 손으로 그의 손을 꼭 잡았다.\n\n\"당신은 못 보셨겠지만, 저는 봤어요.\" 레빈이 행복으로 환하게 빛나는 미소를 지으며 말했다. \"당신이 기차역에서 예르구쇼보로 가는 길에 마차를 타고 가시는 걸 봤습니다.\"\n\n\"언제요?\" 그녀가 의아해하며 물었다.\n\n\"예르구쇼보로 가시는 길이었어요.\" 레빈은 가슴을 가득 채우는 환희에 흐느낌이 터질 것만 같은 기분으로 말했다. '그런데 내가 어찌 감히 이 가슴 뭉클한 사람에게 순결하지 못한 생각을 결부시킬 수 있었단 말인가? 그래, 그래, 나는 다리아 알렉산드로브나가 내게 한 말이 정말이라고 믿어.' 그는 생각했다.\n\n스테판 아르카지치가 그의 팔을 붙들고 카레닌에게로 데려갔다.\n\n\"소개하지.\" 그는 두 사람의 이름을 말했다.\n\n\"다시 만나게 되어 무척 반갑습니다.\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 레빈과 악수하며 차갑게 말했다.\n\n\"두 분이 아는 사이인가?\" 스테판 아르카지치가 놀라 물었다.\n\n\"기차 안에서 세 시간을 함께 보냈지요.\" 레빈이 미소 지으며 말했다. \"그런데 가장무도회에서처럼 영문도 모른 채 내렸어요. 적어도 저는 그랬습니다.\"\n\n\"무슨 소리, 자, 어서들 오게.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 식당 쪽을 가리키며 말했다.\n\n남자들은 식당으로 들어가 식탁으로 다가갔다. 식탁에는 여섯 가지 독한 술과 그만큼 여러 종류의 치즈가, 어떤 것은 작은 은제 칼을 곁들이고 어떤 것은 그냥, 그리고 캐비아, 청어, 갖가지 절임, 프랑스 빵 조각을 담은 접시들이 차려져 있었다.\n\n남자들은 독한 냄새를 풍기는 술과 짭짤한 진미들 주위에 둘러섰고, 코즈니셰프와 카레닌과 페스초프 사이에 오가던 폴란드의 러시아화에 관한 토론은 만찬을 기다리며 사그라들었다.\n\n세르게이 이바노비치는 가장 뜨겁고 진지한 논쟁이라도 상대방의 기분을 바꾸어 놓는 뜻밖의 아티카식 재치 한 자밤으로 마무리하는 솜씨가 따를 자가 없었다. 그는 이번에도 그렇게 했다.\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 폴란드의 러시아화가 오로지 러시아 정부가 도입해야 할 더 큰 규모의 조치들의 결과로만 이루어질 수 있다고 주장해 왔다.\n\n페스초프는 한 나라가 다른 나라를 흡수할 수 있는 것은 그 나라가 인구밀도가 더 높을 때뿐이라고 우겼다.\n\n코즈니셰프는 두 견해를 모두 인정하면서도 단서를 달았다. 논쟁을 마무리하려고 응접실에서 나오던 길에 코즈니셰프가 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"그러니까 우리 이민족 주민들의 러시아화를 위한 방법은 단 하나뿐이군요—될 수 있는 한 아이를 많이 낳는 것 말입니다. 보아하니 내 동생과 나는 죄가 막심하군요. 결혼한 분들, 특히 스테판 아르카지치 당신 같은 분들이야말로 진정한 애국자지요. 그래, 자녀를 몇이나 두셨소?\" 그는 주인을 향해 다정하게 웃으며 작은 술잔을 내밀었다.\n\n모두가 웃었고, 스테판 아르카지치는 유달리 흐뭇해했다.\n\n\"아, 그렇고말고요, 그게 최고의 방법이지요!\" 그는 치즈를 우물거리며 특별한 종류의 술을 잔에 채우면서 말했다. 그 농담으로 대화가 끊겼다.\n\n\"이 치즈 괜찮은데. 좀 줄까?\" 주인이 말했다. \"어럽쇼, 또 체조를 하기 시작했나?\" 그는 왼손으로 레빈의 근육을 꼬집으며 물었다. 레빈이 미소 지으며 팔을 굽히자, 스테판 아르카지치의 손가락 아래에서 근육이 단단한 치즈처럼, 쇠뭉치처럼 딴딴하게 솟아올라 코트의 고운 천을 밀어 올렸다.\n\n\"이 이두근 좀 보게! 영락없는 삼손이군!\"\n\n\"곰 사냥에는 굉장한 힘이 필요할 것 같군요.\" 사냥에 관해서는 더없이 어렴풋한 관념밖에 없는 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 말했다. 그는 거미줄처럼 얇은 빵 조각을 잘라 치즈를 발랐다.\n\n레빈이 미소 지었다.\n\n\"천만에요. 오히려 그 반대지요. 곰은 아이라도 죽일 수 있습니다.\" 그는 식탁으로 다가오는 부인들에게 살짝 고개 숙이며 비켜서면서 말했다.\n\n\"곰을 잡으셨다면서요!\" 키티가 자꾸만 미끄러져 달아나는 짓궂은 버섯을 포크로 잡으려 부지런히 애쓰며, 그 바람에 하얀 팔 위로 레이스를 떨리게 하면서 말했다. \"댁의 영지에 곰이 있나요?\" 그녀가 사랑스러운 작은 머리를 그에게로 돌리며 미소 띤 채 덧붙였다.\n\n그녀가 한 말에는 겉보기에 별다른 것이 없었지만, 그녀가 그 말을 할 때의 모든 소리, 입술의 모든 움직임, 두 눈, 그 손에 그에게는 말로 다 할 수 없는 의미가 담겨 있었다! 거기에는 용서를 구하는 간청과, 그에 대한 믿음과, 다정함—부드럽고 수줍은 다정함—과, 약속과, 희망과, 그를 향한 사랑이 담겨 있어, 그는 그것을 믿지 않을 수 없었고 행복에 숨이 막혔다.\n\n\"아니요, 우리는 트베리 지방에서 사냥을 했어요. 거기서 돌아오는 길에 기차에서 당신의 beau-frère, 아니 당신의 beau-frère의 처남을 만났지요.\" 그가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"재미있는 만남이었어요.\"\n\n그러고는 한숨도 못 잔 채 모피를 안에 댄 낡고 자락이 넓은 외투를 입고 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 객실에 들어가게 된 경위를, 익살스럽고 유쾌하게 들려주기 시작했다.\n\n* beau-frère: 프랑스어로 '매부, 자형, 처남, 시아주버니' 등 인척 남자를 두루 이르는 말.\n\"차장은 속담은 잊은 채 내 옷차림을 보고 나를 내쫓으려 했지요. 그래서 내가 고상한 말투로 내 심정을 늘어놓기 시작했더니… 당신도 말입니다.\" 그는 카레닌을 향해 말하면서 그의 이름을 잊어버린 채 말했다. \"처음엔 그 낡은 외투를 이유로 나를 내치려 하셨지만, 나중엔 내 편을 들어 주셨지요. 정말 고맙게 생각합니다.\"\n\n\"대개 승객들이 자리를 고를 권리란 너무 모호하게 정해져 있지요.\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 손수건으로 손가락 끝을 닦으며 말했다.\n\n\"당신이 나를 두고 갸웃거리시는 걸 봤습니다.\" 레빈이 사람 좋게 미소 지으며 말했다. \"그래서 내 옷차림의 결함을 무마하려고 서둘러 지적인 대화에 뛰어들었지요.\" 세르게이 이바노비치는 안주인과 대화를 이어 가면서도 한쪽 귀로는 동생의 말을 듣고 있다가 곁눈질로 그를 흘긋 바라보았다. '쟤가 오늘 왜 저러지? 어쩌자고 저렇게 개선장군 같담?' 그는 생각했다. 그는 레빈이 마치 날개라도 돋친 듯한 기분이라는 것을 알지 못했다. 레빈은 그녀가 자기 말에 귀 기울이고 있으며 자기 말을 즐겨 듣고 있다는 것을 알고 있었다. 그리고 그것만이 그의 유일한 관심사였다. 이 방 안에서만이 아니라 온 세상에서 그에게 존재하는 것은, 자기 눈에 한없이 커진 중요성과 위엄을 띤 자기 자신과, 그녀뿐이었다. 그는 어지러울 만큼 높은 봉우리 위에 서 있는 듯한 기분이었고, 저 아래 까마득한 곳에 그 모든 친절하고 훌륭한 카레닌, 오블론스키 일가, 그리고 온 세상이 있었다.\n\n누구의 주의도 끌지 않고, 그들을 바라보지도 않은 채, 마치 다른 자리는 남아 있지 않다는 듯이, 스테판 아르카지치는 레빈과 키티를 나란히 앉혔다.\n\n\"아, 자네는 거기 앉으면 되겠군.\" 그가 레빈에게 말했다.\n\n만찬은 스테판 아르카지치가 정통한 분야인 도자기만큼이나 정선된 것이었다. soupe Marie-Louise는 훌륭하게 성공했고, 그것과 함께 먹는 자그마한 파이는 입에서 살살 녹았으며 나무랄 데가 없었다. 흰 넥타이를 맨 두 하인과 마트베이는 요리와 술 시중을 눈에 띄지 않게, 조용하고 날렵하게 들었다. 물질적인 면에서 만찬은 성공이었고, 비물질적인 면에서도 그에 못지않게 성공이었다. 때로는 다 함께, 때로는 개인끼리 오가는 대화는 한순간도 끊이지 않았고, 끝 무렵에는 좌중이 어찌나 활기를 띠던지 남자들은 이야기를 멈추지도 않은 채 식탁에서 일어났으며, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치마저 마음이 누그러졌다.\n\n# Chapter 10\n\n페스초프는 논쟁을 끝까지 물고 늘어지기를 좋아했고, 세르게이 이바노비치의 말에 만족하지 못했다. 자기 견해가 옳지 못하다는 것을 느꼈기에 더욱 그러했다.\n\n\"제가 말씀드린 건,\" 그는 수프를 들면서 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치를 향해 말했다. \"단순한 인구 밀도만이 아니라, 그것이 근본적인 사상과 결합한 경우를 말하는 겁니다. 원리에 의해서가 아니라요.\"\n\n\"제가 보기엔,\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 나른하게, 서두르는 기색 없이 말했다. \"그게 결국 같은 얘기 같습니다만. 제 생각에 다른 민족에게 영향을 미치는 일은 더 높이 발전한 민족만이 할 수 있는 것이고, 그 민족이…….\"\n\n\"하지만 바로 그게 문제죠.\" 페스초프가 굵은 저음으로 끼어들었다. 그는 늘 말하기를 서둘렀고, 언제나 자기가 하는 말에 온 영혼을 쏟아붓는 것 같았다. \"도대체 무엇을 더 높은 발전이라고 봐야 합니까? 영국인, 프랑스인, 독일인, 이 중 어느 쪽이 발전의 가장 높은 단계에 있나요? 어느 민족이 다른 민족을 동화시키겠습니까? 라인란트 지방이 프랑스화된 것을 우리는 알고 있지만, 그렇다고 독일인이 더 낮은 단계에 있는 건 아니지 않습니까!\" 그가 소리쳤다. \"거기엔 다른 법칙이 작용하고 있는 겁니다.\"\n\n\"제 생각엔 더 큰 영향력은 언제나 참된 문명 쪽에 있는 것 같습니다.\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 눈썹을 살짝 치켜올리며 말했다.\n\n\"그렇다면 참된 문명의 외적인 표지로 무엇을 내세워야 합니까?\" 페스초프가 말했다.\n\n\"그런 표지는 대체로 아주 잘 알려져 있다고 봅니다만.\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 그게 온전히 알려져 있을까요?\" 세르게이 이바노비치가 은근한 미소를 지으며 끼어들었다. \"지금은 진정한 교양이 순전히 고전적인 것이어야 한다는 견해가 받아들여지고 있지만, 우리는 이 문제를 두고 양쪽 모두에서 격렬한 논쟁이 벌어지는 것을 보고 있습니다. 그리고 반대편 진영에도 유리한 강점이 있다는 것을 부인할 수는 없죠.\"\n\n\"세르게이 이바노비치, 당신은 고전 쪽이시군요. 적포도주 한잔하시겠습니까?\" 스테판 아르카지치가 말했다.\n\n\"저는 어느 쪽 교양에 대해서도 제 개인적인 견해를 표명하는 게 아닙니다.\" 세르게이 이바노비치가 아이에게 하듯 너그러운 미소를 지으며 잔을 내밀었다. \"다만 양쪽 다 그것을 뒷받침할 강력한 논거를 가지고 있다는 것을 말하는 것뿐이죠.\" 그가 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치를 향해 말을 이었다. \"제 호감은 받은 교육 덕분에 고전 쪽으로 기울어 있습니다만, 이 논의에서 저는 개인적으로 결론에 이르지 못하겠습니다. 고전 연구가 과학 연구보다 우위를 점해야 할 뚜렷한 근거를 찾을 수가 없거든요.\"\n\n\"자연과학도 그에 못지않은 교육적 가치를 가지고 있습니다.\" 페스초프가 끼어들었다. \"천문학을 보세요, 식물학을 보세요, 아니면 일반 원리의 체계를 갖춘 동물학을 보세요.\"\n\n\"저는 거기에 전적으로 동의하기는 어렵습니다.\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 대꾸했다. \"언어의 형태를 연구하는 과정 자체가 지적 발달에 유난히 이로운 영향을 미친다는 점은 인정해야 한다고 봅니다. 게다가 고전 작가들의 영향이 더없이 도덕적이라는 점도 부인할 수 없죠. 반면 안타깝게도 자연과학 연구에는 우리 시대의 저주라 할 그릇되고 해로운 학설들이 결부되어 있습니다.\"\n\n세르게이 이바노비치가 무언가 말하려 했지만, 페스초프가 그 풍부한 저음으로 그의 말을 가로막았다. 그는 이 견해의 정당함에 대해 열을 올리며 반박하기 시작했다. 세르게이 이바노비치는 분명 설득력 있는 답변을 준비해 둔 채, 침착하게 말할 차례를 기다렸다.\n\n\"하지만,\" 세르게이 이바노비치가 은근한 미소를 지으며 카레닌을 향해 말했다. \"고전 연구와 과학 연구의 모든 장단점을 저울질하는 것이 어려운 일이라는 점은 인정해야겠지요. 그리고 어느 형태의 교육을 선호해야 하는가 하는 문제도, 방금 당신이 표현하신 대로 고전 교육 편에 그 도덕적인—disons le mot—반(反)니힐리즘적 영향이 없었더라면 이렇게 빠르고 단호하게 결정되지는 않았을 겁니다.\"\n* disons le mot: 프랑스어로 '터놓고 말하자면'.\n\n\"틀림없이 그렇죠.\"\n\n\"만약 고전 연구 편에 반니힐리즘적 영향이라는 그 독특한 성질이 없었더라면, 우리는 이 문제를 더 깊이 고려하고 양쪽 논거를 저울질했을 겁니다.\" 세르게이 이바노비치가 은근한 미소를 지으며 말했다. \"양쪽 경향 모두에 활동할 여지를 주었겠지요. 하지만 지금 우리는 이 고전 학문이라는 작은 알약이 반니힐리즘이라는 약효를 지니고 있다는 것을 알기에, 대담하게 그것을 환자들에게 처방하는 겁니다……. 그런데 만약 그 알약에 그런 약효가 없다면 어떻게 되겠습니까?\" 그가 익살스럽게 말을 맺었다.\n\n세르게이 이바노비치의 작은 알약 이야기에 모두 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 특히 투롭친은 크고 유쾌하게 껄껄 웃어 댔는데, 마침내 웃을 거리를 찾아 기뻐하는 것이었다. 그가 대화에 귀 기울이며 늘 바라던 것은 오직 그것뿐이었다.\n\n스테판 아르카지치는 페스초프를 초대한 것이 잘못이 아니었다. 페스초프가 있으면 지적인 대화가 한순간도 끊기는 법이 없었다. 세르게이 이바노비치가 농담으로 대화를 마무리하자마자 페스초프는 곧바로 새로운 이야기를 꺼냈다.\n\n\"저는 정부가 그런 목적을 가졌다는 데에도 동의할 수 없습니다.\" 그가 말했다. \"정부는 분명 추상적인 고려에 따라 움직이고, 자기들의 조치가 어떤 영향을 미치는지에는 무관심합니다. 예를 들어 여성 교육은 당연히 해로운 것으로 여겨질 만한데도, 정부는 여성을 위한 학교와 대학을 열고 있지 않습니까.\"\n\n그러자 대화는 곧바로 여성 교육이라는 새로운 주제로 넘어갔다.\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 여성 교육이 여성 해방과 혼동되기 쉬우며, 오직 그 점에서만 위험하다고 여겨질 수 있다는 견해를 밝혔다.\n\n\"저는 오히려 그 두 문제가 떼려야 뗄 수 없게 얽혀 있다고 봅니다.\" 페스초프가 말했다. \"그건 악순환이죠. 여성은 교육이 부족해서 권리를 빼앗기고, 권리가 없기 때문에 교육이 부족한 겁니다. 여성에 대한 예속이 너무나 철저하고, 또 너무 먼 옛날부터 이어져 온 것이라, 우리는 종종 그들과 우리를 갈라놓는 심연을 인정하려 들지 않는다는 점을 잊어서는 안 됩니다.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"방금 권리라고 하셨는데,\" 세르게이 이바노비치가 페스초프의 말이 끝나기를 기다렸다가 말했다. \"그건 배심원으로 앉을 권리, 투표할 권리, 공식 회의를 주재할 권리, 관청에 들어갈 권리, 의회에 의석을 둘 권리를 말씀하시는 거겠죠…….\"\n\n\"틀림없이 그렇습니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 여성이 드문 예외로 그런 자리를 차지할 수 있다 하더라도, '권리'라는 표현을 쓰시는 건 잘못인 것 같습니다. 의무라고 하는 편이 더 정확하겠지요. 배심원이나 증인이나 전신국 직원의 직무를 수행할 때, 우리가 의무를 다하고 있다고 느낀다는 데에는 누구나 동의할 겁니다. 그러니 여성들이 의무를 찾고 있다고 말하는 편이 정확하겠고, 그건 지극히 정당한 일이죠. 그리고 인간 전체의 노동을 돕고자 하는 그 바람에는 공감할 수밖에 없습니다.\"\n\n\"바로 그렇습니다.\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 동의했다. \"문제는 그저 그들이 그런 직무에 적합한가 하는 것이겠죠.\"\n\n\"교육이 그들 사이에 두루 퍼지게 되면,\" 스테판 아르카지치가 말했다. \"아마 완벽하게 적합해질 겁니다. 우리는 그것을 보고 있죠…….\"\n\n\"그 속담은 어쩌고?\" 한참 동안 대화에 골똘히 귀 기울이고 있던 공작이 그 작고 익살스러운 눈을 반짝이며 말했다. \"내 딸 앞에서도 할 수 있는 말이지. 머리카락이 길면, 지혜는…….\"\n\n\"흑인들도 해방되기 전엔 꼭 그렇게 여겨졌죠!\" 페스초프가 화를 내며 말했다.\n\n\"제겐 여성들이 새로운 의무를 찾으려 한다는 게 이상하게 느껴집니다.\" 세르게이 이바노비치가 말했다. \"안타깝게도 남자들은 대개 그것을 피하려 드는 걸 보고 있으니 말입니다.\"\n\n\"의무에는 권리가 따라붙죠—권력, 돈, 명예. 여성들이 찾는 건 바로 그것들입니다.\" 페스초프가 말했다.\n\n\"꼭 내가 유모가 될 권리를 찾아 나서서, 여자들은 그 일로 돈을 받는데 나는 아무도 안 써 준다고 억울해하는 격이군.\" 노공작이 말했다.\n\n투롭친이 큰 소리로 웃음을 터뜨렸고, 세르게이 이바노비치는 자기가 그 비유를 하지 못한 것을 아쉬워했다. 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치조차 미소를 지었다.\n\n\"맞습니다, 하지만 남자는 아기에게 젖을 먹일 수가 없죠.\" 페스초프가 말했다. \"반면 여자는…….\"\n\n\"아니, 배 위에서 제 아기에게 젖을 물린 영국인이 있었다오.\" 노공작이 자기 딸들 앞에서 이런 자유로운 대화쯤은 허용된다고 여기며 말했다.\n\n\"그런 영국인은 여성 관리만큼이나 많겠지요.\" 세르게이 이바노비치가 말했다.\n\n\"맞아요, 하지만 가족이 없는 처녀는 어떻게 해야 합니까?\" 스테판 아르카지치가 끼어들었다. 페스초프에게 공감하며 그를 거드는 내내 그는 마샤 치비소바를 마음에 두고 있었던 것이다.\n\n\"그런 처녀의 사연을 낱낱이 들춰 보면, 그 여자가 자기 가족이든 언니의 가족이든 어떤 가정을 버렸다는 걸 알게 될 거예요. 거기서 여자로서의 의무를 찾을 수 있었을 텐데 말이죠.\" 다리야 알렉산드로브나가 뜻밖에도 분에 찬 어조로 불쑥 끼어들었다. 아마 스테판 아르카지치가 어떤 처녀를 떠올리고 있는지 눈치챈 모양이었다.\n\n\"하지만 우리는 이상으로서 원칙에 입각해 이야기하는 겁니다.\" 페스초프가 부드러운 저음으로 대답했다. \"여성은 권리를 갖기를, 독립하기를, 교육받기를 원합니다. 그녀는 자신의 무력함을 의식하며 억압당하고 모욕당하고 있죠.\"\n\n\"나도 양육원에서 날 안 써 준다고 억압당하고 모욕당하고 있다오.\" 노공작이 다시 말했고, 투롭친은 그 말에 크게 기뻐하며 웃다가 아스파라거스를 굵은 쪽이 소스에 빠지도록 떨어뜨렸다.\n\n# Chapter 11\n\n키티와 레빈을 빼고는 모두가 대화에 끼어들었다. 처음에 한 민족이 다른 민족에게 미치는 영향에 관한 이야기가 나왔을 때, 레빈의 머릿속에는 그 주제에 대해 자기가 할 말이 떠올랐다. 하지만 한때 그의 눈에 그토록 중요하게 여겨지던 이 생각들이 이제는 마치 꿈속에서처럼 머릿속에 떠오를 뿐, 더 이상 그에게 조금의 흥미도 주지 못했다. 누구에게도 쓸모없는 이야기를 저토록 열심히 떠드는 것이 그에게는 오히려 이상하게 여겨질 정도였다. 키티 역시 여성의 권리와 교육에 관해 그들이 나누는 이야기에 관심을 가질 법도 했다. 그녀는 외국에 있는 친구 바렌카와 그녀의 고통스러운 종속 상태를 떠올리며 그 주제에 대해 얼마나 자주 골똘히 생각했던가. 결혼하지 않으면 자기는 어떻게 될까 하고 스스로에 대해 얼마나 자주 궁금해했으며, 그 일을 두고 언니와 얼마나 자주 다투었던가! 그런데도 지금은 그것이 전혀 그녀의 흥미를 끌지 못했다. 그녀와 레빈은 그들만의 대화를 나누고 있었는데, 그것은 대화라기보다 어떤 신비로운 교감 같은 것이어서, 매 순간 두 사람을 더 가까이 끌어당겼고, 그들이 막 들어서고 있는 미지의 세계 앞에서 두 사람 모두의 마음속에 기쁜 두려움을 불러일으켰다.\n\n처음에 레빈은 작년에 어떻게 마차 안의 자기를 볼 수 있었느냐는 키티의 물음에, 풀베기를 마치고 큰길을 따라 집으로 돌아오다가 그녀와 마주쳤다고 이야기했다.\n\n\"아주, 아주 이른 아침이었어요. 당신은 아마 막 잠에서 깬 참이었을 거예요. 어머니는 한쪽 구석에서 주무시고 계셨고요. 정말 더없이 아름다운 아침이었죠. 나는 길을 걸으면서 네 마리 말이 끄는 저 마차에 탄 사람이 누굴까 생각하고 있었어요. 방울을 단 멋진 네 필의 말이었는데, 한순간에 당신이 휙 지나갔고, 나는 창가에 있는 당신을 보았죠—당신은 이렇게 앉아 두 손으로 모자 끈을 잡은 채 무언가를 무척이나 깊이 생각하고 있었어요.\" 그는 미소 지으며 말했다. \"그때 당신이 무슨 생각을 하고 있었는지 정말 알고 싶군요! 뭔가 중요한 것이었나요?\"\n\n'내가 그때 형편없이 단정치 못한 모습이지 않았을까?' 그녀는 궁금했지만, 그 추억이 불러일으킨 그의 황홀한 미소를 보고는 자기가 남긴 인상이 매우 좋았음을 느꼈다. 그녀는 얼굴을 붉히며 기쁨에 차 웃었다. \"정말이지 기억이 안 나요.\"\n\n\"투로프친이 웃는 모습이 참 보기 좋네요!\" 레빈이 그의 촉촉한 눈과 들썩이는 가슴을 흐뭇하게 바라보며 말했다.\n\n\"그분을 안 지 오래되셨어요?\" 키티가 물었다.\n\n\"오, 모두가 그를 알죠!\"\n\n\"그런데 보아하니 그분을 형편없는 사람이라고 생각하시는 것 같네요?\"\n\n\"형편없다기보다는, 그저 알맹이가 없는 사람이죠.\"\n\n\"오, 그건 틀렸어요! 그런 생각은 당장 버리셔야 해요!\" 키티가 말했다. \"저도 한때 그분을 아주 보잘것없게 여겼지만, 그분은, 그분은 정말로 좋은 사람이고 더없이 마음씨가 고운 분이에요. 황금 같은 마음을 지녔답니다.\"\n\n\"그가 어떤 마음을 지녔는지 어떻게 알아냈죠?\"\n\n\"우리는 아주 친한 사이거든요. 저는 그분을 아주 잘 알아요. 지난겨울, 그러니까... 당신이 우리 집에 다녀가신 직후에,\" 그녀는 죄지은 듯하면서도 동시에 속을 털어놓는 미소를 지으며 말했다. \"돌리의 아이들이 모두 성홍열에 걸렸는데, 마침 그분이 언니를 보러 왔어요. 그런데 글쎄,\" 그녀가 속삭이듯 말했다. \"그분은 언니를 너무도 안쓰러워한 나머지 그대로 머물면서 아이들 돌보는 일을 거들기 시작했어요. 네, 그렇게 삼 주 동안이나 함께 지내면서 보모처럼 아이들을 돌봐 주었답니다.\"\n\n\"투로프친이 성홍열 때 한 일을 콘스탄틴 드미트리치께 이야기하는 중이야.\" 그녀가 언니 쪽으로 몸을 기울이며 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 정말 훌륭했어, 고귀한 일이었지!\" 돌리가 투로프친 쪽을 흘긋 바라보며 말했다. 그는 사람들이 자기 이야기를 하고 있음을 알아채고는 그녀에게 부드럽게 미소를 지었다. 레빈은 다시 한 번 투로프친을 바라보며, 어째서 자기는 진작 이 사람의 선함을 알아보지 못했을까 의아해했다.\n\n\"미안합니다, 미안해요, 다시는 사람을 나쁘게 생각하지 않겠어요!\" 그는 그 순간 느낀 바를 진심으로 드러내며 명랑하게 말했다.\n\n# Chapter 12\n\n여자의 권리를 둘러싸고 일어난 대화와 연결되어, 결혼 생활에서의 권리 불평등에 관한 몇 가지 문제가 있었는데, 부인들 앞에서 논하기에는 적절치 않은 것들이었다. 페스초프는 만찬 동안 여러 차례 이 문제들을 건드렸지만, 세르게이 이바노비치와 스테판 아르카지치는 조심스럽게 그를 거기에서 떼어 놓았다.\n\n식탁에서 일어나 부인들이 나가 버리자, 페스초프는 그들을 따라가지 않고 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치를 향해 불평등의 주된 근거를 설명하기 시작했다. 그의 견해로는, 결혼 생활에서의 불평등이란 아내의 부정과 남편의 부정이 법으로나 여론으로나 불평등하게 처벌받는다는 사실에 있었다. 스테판 아르카지치는 급히 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에게 다가가 시가를 권했다.\n\n\"아니, 난 담배를 피우지 않네.\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 침착하게 대답했고, 일부러 그 주제를 두려워하지 않음을 보이려는 듯, 차가운 미소를 띠며 페스초프를 향해 돌아섰다.\n\n\"내 생각엔 그런 견해는 사물의 본성 그 자체에 근거를 두고 있는 것 같소.\" 그는 그렇게 말하고는 응접실로 가려 했다. 그런데 이때 투롭친이 별안간 뜻밖에도 대화에 끼어들어 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에게 말을 걸었다.\n\n\"혹시 프라치니코프에 관한 얘기 들으셨소?\" 투롭친이 말했는데, 그는 마신 샴페인으로 들떠 있었고, 자기를 짓누르던 침묵을 깨뜨릴 기회를 오래 기다려 온 터였다. \"바샤 프라치니코프 말이오.\" 그는 축축한 붉은 입술에 사람 좋은 미소를 띠며, 주로 가장 중요한 손님인 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치를 향해 말했다. \"오늘 들은 얘긴데, 트베리에서 크비츠키랑 결투를 벌여서 그를 죽였다지 뭐요.\"\n\n마치 아픈 자리를 자꾸 부딪치게 되는 것처럼 느껴지듯, 스테판 아르카지치는 지금 이 대화가 운 나쁘게도 매 순간 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 아픈 곳으로 떨어진다고 느꼈다. 그는 또다시 처남을 떼어 놓으려 했지만, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치 자신이 호기심을 보이며 물었다.\n\n\"프라치니코프는 뭣 때문에 결투를 한 거요?\"\n\n\"아내 때문이오. 사내답게 굴었지 뭐요! 결투를 신청해서 쏘아 버렸으니!\"\n\n\"아!\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 무심하게 말하고는, 눈썹을 치켜올린 채 응접실로 들어갔다.\n\n\"와 주셔서 정말 기뻐요.\" 돌리가 바깥 응접실에서 그를 맞으며 겁먹은 미소로 말했다. \"당신과 이야기를 좀 해야겠어요. 여기 앉아요.\"\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 치켜올린 눈썹이 만들어 낸 그 무심한 표정 그대로, 다리아 알렉산드로브나 곁에 앉아 어색하게 미소 지었다.\n\n\"잘됐군요.\" 그가 말했다. \"마침 양해를 구하고 작별 인사를 드리려던 참이었으니까요. 내일 떠나야 합니다.\"\n\n다리아 알렉산드로브나는 안나가 결백하다고 굳게 믿고 있었으며, 자기의 죄 없는 친구를 이토록 태연히 파멸시키려 드는 이 냉정하고 무정한 남자에 대한 분노로 자기 얼굴이 창백해지고 입술이 떨리는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n\"알렉세이 알렉산드로비치.\" 그녀가 필사적인 결의로 그의 얼굴을 똑바로 바라보며 말했다. \"제가 안나에 대해 여쭈었는데, 당신은 아무 대답도 해 주지 않으셨어요. 그 애는 어떻게 지내나요?\"\n\n\"잘 지내는 것 같습니다, 다리아 알렉산드로브나.\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 그녀를 보지도 않은 채 대답했다.\n\n\"알렉세이 알렉산드로비치, 용서하세요, 제겐 그럴 권리가 없지만…… 그래도 저는 안나를 자매처럼 사랑하고 또 존경해요. 부탁드려요, 간곡히 부탁드리니, 두 분 사이에 무슨 일이 있었는지 말씀해 주세요. 그 애의 어떤 잘못을 탓하시는 건가요?\"\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 미간을 찌푸리고는 눈을 거의 감다시피 한 채 고개를 떨구었다.\n\n\"내가 안나 아르카지예브나에 대한 태도를 바꿀 필요가 있다고 여기게 된 근거에 대해서는 부인의 남편 되시는 분이 이미 말씀하셨으리라 생각합니다만?\" 그는 그녀의 얼굴을 보지 않은 채, 응접실을 가로질러 걸어가는 셰르바츠키를 못마땅한 눈길로 바라보며 말했다.\n\n\"믿을 수 없어요, 믿을 수 없어요, 그럴 리 없어요!\" 돌리는 앙상한 두 손을 앞으로 모아 힘껏 맞잡으며 말했다. 그녀는 재빨리 일어나 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 소매에 손을 얹었다. \"여기서는 방해를 받겠어요. 이쪽으로 오세요, 제발.\"\n\n돌리의 동요는 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에게 영향을 미쳤다. 그는 일어나 순순히 그녀를 따라 공부방으로 갔다. 두 사람은 주머니칼로 군데군데 베인 자국이 난 방수포가 덮인 탁자에 마주 앉았다.\n\n\"믿어지지 않아요, 믿어지지 않아요!\" 돌리가 그녀를 피하는 그의 눈길을 붙잡으려 애쓰며 말했다.\n\n\"사실을 믿지 않을 수는 없는 노릇이지요, 다리아 알렉산드로브나.\" 그가 '사실'이라는 말에 힘을 주며 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 그 애가 무얼 했단 말인가요?\" 다리아 알렉산드로브나가 말했다. \"도대체 무얼 했는데요?\"\n\n\"제 의무를 저버리고 남편을 속였습니다. 그게 그 사람이 한 짓입니다.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 아니에요, 그럴 리 없어요! 아니에요, 제발, 당신이 잘못 아신 거예요.\" 돌리가 두 손을 관자놀이에 갖다 대고 눈을 감으며 말했다.\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 자기 확신의 굳건함을 그녀에게도 자기 자신에게도 알리려는 듯, 입술만으로 차갑게 미소 지었다. 그러나 이 뜨거운 변호는 그를 흔들어 놓지는 못했어도 그의 상처를 다시 헤집었다. 그는 한층 격앙된 어조로 말하기 시작했다.\n\n\"아내가 직접 남편에게 그 사실을 알릴 때—제 인생의 팔 년 세월도, 아들도, 그 모든 게 다 잘못이었으며, 인생을 새로 시작하고 싶다고 알릴 때, 잘못 안다는 건 지극히 어려운 일이지요.\" 그는 화가 나서 콧방귀를 뀌며 말했다.\n\n\"안나와 죄라니—저는 그 둘을 연결시킬 수가 없어요, 믿을 수가 없어요!\"\n\n\"다리아 알렉산드로브나.\" 그가 말했다. 이제 그는 돌리의 다정하고 근심 어린 얼굴을 똑바로 바라보았고, 자기 의지와는 상관없이 혀가 풀려 가는 것을 느꼈다. \"의심이라도 아직 가능하다면 저는 무엇이든 내놓겠습니다. 의심하던 때 저는 비참했지만, 그래도 지금보다는 나았어요. 의심하던 때는 희망이 있었지요. 그러나 지금은 희망이 없는데도 여전히 모든 것을 의심합니다. 모든 것을 어찌나 의심하는지, 저는 제 아들마저 미워하고, 때로는 그 애가 제 아들이라는 것조차 믿지 못합니다. 저는 정말 불행한 사람입니다.\"\n\n그는 그런 말을 할 필요가 없었다. 다리아 알렉산드로브나는 그가 자기 얼굴을 들여다본 순간 그것을 보아 버렸다. 그래서 그녀는 그가 가엾어졌고, 친구의 결백에 대한 믿음이 흔들리기 시작했다.\n\n\"오, 이건 끔찍해요, 끔찍해요! 그런데 정말로 이혼을 결심하셨다는 게 사실인가요?\"\n\n\"극단적인 조치를 결심했습니다. 제게는 달리 할 수 있는 일이 없어요.\"\n\n\"달리 할 일이 없다, 달리 할 일이 없다고요……\" 그녀가 눈물을 글썽이며 대답했다. \"오, 아니에요, 달리 할 일이 없다는 말씀은 하지 마세요!\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"이런 종류의 괴로움에서 끔찍한 것은, 다른 어떤 경우처럼—상실이나 죽음에서처럼—자기 괴로움을 가만히 견딜 수 없고, 반드시 행동해야 한다는 점입니다.\" 그가 그녀의 생각을 헤아리기라도 한 듯 말했다. \"자기가 놓인 굴욕적인 처지에서 벗어나야만 하는 거지요. 셋이서 함께 살 수는 없으니까요.\"\n\n* à trois: 프랑스어로 '셋이서'.\n\"이해해요, 충분히 이해해요.\" 돌리는 말하며 고개를 떨구었다. 그녀는 잠시 잠자코, 자기 자신의 일을, 가정에서 겪은 자기 자신의 슬픔을 생각하다가, 별안간 충동적인 몸짓으로 고개를 들어 애원하는 동작으로 두 손을 맞잡았다. \"하지만 조금만 기다려 보세요! 당신은 기독교인이잖아요. 그 애를 생각해 보세요! 당신이 그 애를 내쳐 버리면, 그 애는 어떻게 되겠어요?\"\n\n\"저도 생각해 봤습니다, 다리아 알렉산드로브나, 아주 많이 생각해 봤어요.\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 말했다. 그의 얼굴은 군데군데 붉게 물들었고, 흐릿한 두 눈은 앞쪽을 똑바로 바라보았다. 그 순간 다리아 알렉산드로브나는 진심으로 그가 가엾었다. \"그 사람이 직접 제 굴욕을 제게 알렸을 때 저는 정말이지 그렇게 했습니다. 모든 것을 예전 그대로 두었지요. 저는 그 사람에게 고칠 기회를 주었고, 구해 보려고 애썼습니다. 그런데 그 결과가 어땠습니까? 그 사람은 가장 사소한 부탁조차—체면만은 지켜 달라는 부탁조차 들어주지 않았어요.\" 그가 점점 격해지며 말했다. \"파멸하기를 원하지 않는 사람은 누구든 구할 수 있습니다. 그러나 천성 전체가 너무도 부패하고 타락해서 파멸 그 자체가 도리어 구원으로 여겨질 지경이라면, 무엇을 어찌하겠습니까?\"\n\n\"무엇이든 좋아요, 다만 이혼만은 안 돼요!\" 다리아 알렉산드로브나가 대답했다.\n\n\"하지만 무엇이든이라니, 그게 뭡니까?\"\n\n\"안 돼요, 그건 끔찍해요! 그 애는 누구의 아내도 아닌 신세가 되어 버릴 거예요, 그 애는 파멸하고 말 거예요!\"\n\"제가 무얼 할 수 있겠습니까?\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 어깨와 눈썹을 치켜올리며 말했다. 아내의 마지막 행동을 떠올리자 그는 어찌나 화가 났던지 대화를 시작할 때처럼 다시 냉랭해졌다. \"동정해 주셔서 정말 감사합니다만, 이제 가 봐야겠습니다.\" 그가 일어나며 말했다.\n\n\"아니에요, 잠깐만요. 그 애를 파멸시켜서는 안 돼요. 조금만 기다려 보세요. 제 이야기를 들려드릴게요. 저는 결혼했고, 제 남편은 저를 속였어요. 분노와 질투에 사로잡혀 저는 모든 것을 내던지려 했고, 저 스스로…… 그렇지만 다시 정신을 차렸어요. 그런데 누가 그렇게 해 주었을까요? 안나가 저를 구해 주었어요. 그래서 저는 이렇게 살아가고 있는 거예요. 아이들은 자라고, 남편은 가정으로 돌아왔고, 자기 잘못을 느끼며 점점 더 맑아지고 나아지고 있어요. 그리고 저는 이렇게 살아가고 있고요…… 저는 용서했어요, 그러니 당신도 용서하셔야 해요!\"\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 그녀의 말을 들었지만, 이제 그 말은 그에게 아무런 영향도 미치지 못했다. 이혼을 결심하던 그날의 그 모든 증오가 그의 영혼 속에서 다시 솟구쳐 올랐다. 그는 몸을 부르르 떨더니 날카롭고 큰 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"용서할 수 없고, 용서하고 싶지도 않으며, 그것을 옳지 않은 일이라 여깁니다. 저는 그 여자를 위해 모든 것을 다했는데, 그 여자는 그 모든 것을 자기와 어울리는 진창 속에 짓밟아 버렸어요. 저는 악의에 찬 사람이 아니고, 누구도 미워해 본 적이 없습니다만, 그 여자만은 영혼을 다해 미워하며, 그 여자를 용서조차 할 수 없습니다. 그 여자가 제게 저지른 모든 잘못 때문에 그 여자를 너무나도 미워하니까요!\" 그가 증오에 찬 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"너희를 미워하는 자들을 사랑하라……\" 다리아 알렉산드로브나가 겁먹은 듯 속삭였다.\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 경멸하듯 미소 지었다. 그것은 그가 오래전부터 알던 말이었지만, 자기 경우에는 적용될 수 없었다.\n\n\"너희를 미워하는 자들을 사랑하라고는 하지만, 자기가 미워하는 자를 사랑하기란 불가능합니다. 폐를 끼쳐 죄송합니다. 누구나 자기 자신의 슬픔만으로도 견뎌 내야 할 짐이 충분하니까요!\" 그러고는 평정을 되찾은 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 조용히 작별 인사를 하고 떠나갔다.\n\n# Chapter 13\n\n식탁에서 일어났을 때, 레빈은 키티를 따라 응접실로 들어가고 싶었지만, 너무 노골적으로 그녀에게 마음을 쓰는 것처럼 보여 그녀가 싫어할까 봐 두려웠다. 그는 사내들의 작은 무리 속에 남아 전체 대화에 끼어 있었고, 키티를 쳐다보지 않으면서도 그녀의 움직임이며 시선이며 응접실 안에서 그녀가 있는 자리를 알아차리고 있었다.\n\n그는 곧바로, 조금의 노력도 들이지 않고, 그녀에게 한 약속을 지켰다. 늘 모든 사람을 좋게 생각하고 언제나 누구든 좋아하겠다는 약속이었다. 대화는 농촌 공동체로 흘러갔는데, 페스초프는 거기서 일종의 특별한 원리, 그가 \"합창적\" 원리라고 부르는 것을 보았다. 레빈은 페스초프의 생각에도, 또 러시아 공동체의 의의를 인정하면서도 인정하지 않는 자기만의 독특한 태도를 지닌 형의 생각에도 동의하지 않았다. 하지만 그는 그들과 이야기를 나누며, 그저 그들의 견해 차이를 화해시키고 누그러뜨리려 애썼다. 그는 자기가 하는 말에도 조금도 흥미가 없었고, 그들이 하는 말에는 더더욱 흥미가 없었다. 그가 바라는 것은 오직 그들이며 모든 사람이 행복하고 만족스러워하는 것뿐이었다. 그는 이제 중요한 단 한 가지를 알고 있었다. 그리고 그 한 가지는 처음에는 저쪽 응접실에 있었으나, 이윽고 움직이기 시작해 문가에 와서 멈춰 섰다. 돌아보지 않고도 그는 자기에게 못 박힌 시선과 미소를 느꼈고, 돌아보지 않을 수 없었다. 그녀는 셰르바츠키와 함께 문간에 서서 그를 바라보고 있었다.\n\n\"피아노 쪽으로 가시는 줄 알았어요.\" 그는 그녀에게 다가가며 말했다. \"음악, 그게 시골에서 제가 아쉬워하는 거랍니다.\"\n\n\"아니에요. 그냥 당신을 모시러, 또 와 주셔서 감사하다는 인사를 드리러 온 거예요.\" 그녀는 선물 같은 미소로 그에게 보답하며 말했다. \"다들 뭣 때문에 그렇게 논쟁을 하는 걸까요? 아무도 누구를 설득하지 못하는데 말이에요.\"\n\n\"그래요, 맞는 말입니다.\" 레빈이 말했다. \"보통은 그냥 상대가 뭘 증명하려는지 도무지 알아낼 수 없기 때문에 열을 올려 논쟁을 하게 되지요.\"\n\n레빈은 가장 영리한 사람들 사이의 토론에서도 흔히 이런 일이 벌어진다는 것을 자주 보아 왔다. 엄청난 노력을 기울이고, 논리적 정교함과 말을 어마어마하게 쏟아부은 끝에, 논쟁하던 이들은 마침내 자기들이 그토록 오랫동안 서로에게 증명하려 애써 온 것이 사실은 논쟁이 시작될 때부터 양쪽 모두 이미 오래전에 알고 있던 것이며, 다만 서로 좋아하는 것이 다를 뿐인데, 그 좋아하는 것이 공격당할까 두려워 그것을 분명히 밝히지 않으려 한다는 사실을 깨닫게 된다는 것이다. 그는 토론 도중에 문득 상대가 좋아하는 것이 무엇인지 알아채고 자기도 곧장 그것을 좋아하게 되어, 그러자 즉시 동의하게 되고 모든 논쟁이 부질없는 것이 되어 무너져 내리는 경험을 자주 했다. 또 때로는 그 반대의 경험도 했다. 자기가 좋아하는 것을, 그것을 옹호하려고 온갖 논거를 궁리하던 그것을 마침내 입 밖에 냈는데, 우연히 그것을 잘 그리고 진솔하게 표현하게 되면, 상대가 곧바로 동의하며 자기 입장을 두고 더는 다투지 않게 되는 것이었다. 그는 이 이야기를 하려고 했다.\n\n그녀는 이해하려 애쓰며 미간을 찌푸렸다. 하지만 그가 자기 뜻을 예를 들어 설명하기 시작하자, 그녀는 곧바로 알아들었다.\n\n\"알겠어요. 그가 무엇 때문에 논쟁하는지, 그에게 소중한 게 무엇인지 먼저 알아내야 한다는 거죠. 그러면 그제야……\"\n\n그녀는 그가 서툴게 표현한 생각을 완전히 알아맞히고 또렷이 말로 옮겼다. 레빈은 기쁨에 차 미소 지었다. 페스초프며 형과 나눈 그 혼란스럽고 장황한 토론에서, 가장 복잡한 생각이 이렇듯 간결하고 명료하게, 거의 말 없이도 전해지는 소통으로 옮겨 온 것에 그는 놀랐다.\n\n셰르바츠키가 그들에게서 멀어졌고, 키티는 카드 탁자로 다가가 자리에 앉더니 분필을 집어 들고 새 초록색 천 위에 갈라지며 퍼져 나가는 원들을 그리기 시작했다.\n\n그들은 만찬 자리에서 시작되었던 화제로 다시 돌아갔다. 여성의 자유와 일거리에 관한 이야기였다. 레빈은 결혼하지 않은 처녀라면 한 가정에서 여자로서의 의무를 찾아야 한다는 다리야 알렉산드로브나의 의견에 동의했다. 그는 여자들의 도움 없이는 어떤 가정도 꾸려 나갈 수 없다는 사실, 가난하든 부유하든 모든 가정에는 친척이든 고용인이든 유모가 있고 또 있어야만 한다는 사실로 이 견해를 뒷받침했다.\n\n\"아니에요.\" 키티가 얼굴을 붉히면서도 그 진실한 눈으로 한층 더 대담하게 그를 바라보며 말했다. \"어떤 처녀는 그 가정 안에서 굴욕을 겪지 않고는 살 수 없는 처지에 놓일 수도 있어요. 그런데 그녀 자신은……\"\n\n그 말의 암시만으로 그는 그녀를 알아들었다.\n\n\"오, 그래요.\" 그가 말했다. \"그래요, 그래요, 그래요. 당신 말이 맞아요. 맞아요!\"\n\n그리고 그는, 만찬 자리에서 페스초프가 여성의 자유에 대해 주장하던 모든 것을, 그저 노처녀의 삶이 지닌 공포와 그 굴욕을 키티의 마음속에서 언뜻 엿보는 것만으로 다 이해하게 되었다. 그리고 그녀를 사랑하는 까닭에, 그는 그 공포와 굴욕을 느꼈고 곧바로 자기 논거를 거두어들였다.\n\n침묵이 뒤따랐다. 그녀는 여전히 분필로 탁자에 무언가를 그리고 있었다. 그녀의 눈은 부드러운 빛으로 반짝였다. 그녀의 기분에 이끌려, 그는 자기 온몸에서 행복의 긴장이 끊임없이 부풀어 오르는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n\"어머! 탁자를 온통 휘갈겨 놓았네요!\" 그녀가 말하고는 분필을 내려놓고 일어서려는 듯한 동작을 했다.\n\n\"뭐라고! 그럼 나 혼자 남겨지는 건가, 그녀 없이?\" 그는 공포에 사로잡혀 생각하며 분필을 집어 들었다. \"잠깐만요.\" 그는 탁자 앞에 앉으며 말했다. \"오래전부터 당신에게 한 가지 물어보고 싶은 게 있었어요.\"\n\n그는 다정하지만 겁먹은 그녀의 두 눈을 똑바로 들여다보았다.\n\n\"물어보세요, 어서요.\"\n\n\"여기요.\" 그가 말하고는 머리글자들을 적어 나갔다. ㄱ, ㄷ, ㅂ, ㅅ, ㅇ, ㅅ, ㅇ, ㄱ, ㅇ, ㄱ, ㅇ, ㄱ, ㅇ, ㄴ, ㄱㅇ. 이 글자들은 이런 뜻이었다. \"그게 도무지 불가능한 일이라고 당신이 내게 말했을 때, 그건 영영 그렇다는 거였나요, 아니면 그때 그랬다는 건가요?\" 그녀가 이렇게 복잡한 문장을 알아낼 가능성은 전혀 없어 보였다. 하지만 그는 마치 그녀가 그 글자들을 이해하는지에 자기 목숨이 걸리기라도 한 듯 그녀를 바라보았다. 그녀는 그를 진지하게 흘끗 쳐다보더니, 찌푸린 이마를 두 손에 기대고 읽기 시작했다. 한두 번 그녀는 마치 \"이게 내가 생각하는 그건가요?\" 하고 묻듯이 그를 몰래 쳐다보았다.\n\n\"알겠어요.\" 그녀가 살짝 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다.\n\n\"이 글자는 뭐죠?\" 그는 \"영영\"을 뜻하는 ㅇ을 가리키며 말했다.\n\n\"그건 영영이라는 뜻이에요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"하지만 그건 사실이 아니에요!\"\n\n그는 자기가 쓴 것을 재빨리 지우고 그녀에게 분필을 건넨 뒤 일어섰다. 그녀는 적었다. ㄱ, ㄸ, ㄴ, ㄷ, ㄷㄹ, ㄷ.\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치와 나눈 대화로 가라앉아 있던 우울한 기분에서, 돌리는 그 두 사람의 모습을 보고는 완전히 위안을 얻었다. 분필을 손에 든 키티는 수줍고도 행복한 미소를 지으며 레빈을 올려다보고 있었고, 그의 잘생긴 모습은 탁자 위로 몸을 숙인 채 이글거리는 눈으로 한 순간은 탁자를, 다음 순간은 그녀를 응시하고 있었다. 그는 별안간 환하게 빛났다. 알아낸 것이다. 그것은 \"그때는 달리 대답할 수 없었어요\"라는 뜻이었다.\n\n그는 머뭇거리며 미심쩍은 눈으로 그녀를 흘끗 보았다.\n\n\"그때만 그랬다는 건가요?\"\n\n\"네.\" 그녀의 미소가 대답했다.\n\n\"그럼 지…… 그럼 지금은요?\" 그가 물었다.\n\"자, 이걸 읽어 보세요. 제가 바라는 걸 말씀드릴게요. 정말 간절히 바라는 걸요!\" 그녀는 머리글자들을 적었다. ㅈ, ㄴ, ㅇ, ㅇ, ㅇ, ㅇ, ㅈㅇ, ㅁ. 이것은 \"지난 일을 잊고 용서해 주실 수 있다면\"이라는 뜻이었다.\n\n그는 떨리는 손가락으로 신경질적으로 분필을 낚아채 그것을 부러뜨리면서, 다음 문장의 머리글자들을 적었다. \"저는 잊거나 용서할 것이 아무것도 없어요. 저는 단 한 번도 당신을 사랑하기를 멈춘 적이 없습니다.\"\n\n그녀는 흔들림 없는 미소로 그를 흘끗 보았다.\n\n\"알겠어요.\" 그녀가 속삭이듯 말했다.\n\n그는 자리에 앉아 긴 문장을 적었다. 그녀는 그것을 모두 이해했고, 그에게 \"이게 맞나요?\" 하고 묻지도 않은 채 분필을 집어 곧바로 답을 적었다.\n\n오랫동안 그는 그녀가 적은 것을 이해하지 못했고, 자꾸만 그녀의 눈을 들여다보았다. 그는 행복에 멍해져 있었다. 그녀가 뜻한 단어를 채워 넣을 수가 없었다. 하지만 행복으로 빛나는 그녀의 사랑스러운 눈동자 속에서, 그는 자기가 알아야 할 모든 것을 보았다. 그래서 그는 세 글자를 적었다. 그런데 미처 다 적기도 전에 그녀는 그의 팔 너머로 그것을 읽고, 손수 마저 채워 답을 적었다. \"네.\"\n\n\"비밀 편지 놀이라도 하는 게냐?\" 노공작이 말했다. \"한데 극장에 늦지 않으려면 정말 슬슬 가 봐야겠구나.\"\n\n레빈은 일어나 키티를 문까지 바래다주었다.\n\n그들의 대화에서는 모든 것이 다 이야기된 셈이었다. 그녀가 그를 사랑한다는 것도, 그리고 그가 내일 아침 찾아올 것이라고 그녀가 아버지와 어머니에게 말하리라는 것도, 모두 이야기된 것이다.\n\n# Chapter 14\n\n키티가 떠나고 레빈만 홀로 남게 되자, 그는 그녀가 없는 데서 견디기 힘든 불안과, 그녀를 다시 보고 영원히 그녀와 약혼하게 될 내일 아침으로 어떻게든 한시바삐, 한시바삐 다다르고 싶은 초조한 갈망을 느꼈고, 그녀 없이 보내야 할 그 열네 시간이 마치 죽음처럼 두렵게 느껴졌다. 그에게는 혼자 남지 않도록, 시간을 죽이도록, 누군가와 함께 이야기를 나누는 것이 꼭 필요했다. 스테판 아르카지치야말로 가장 마음에 맞는 길동무였을 테지만, 그는 야회에 간다고 했고, 실은 발레를 보러 가는 길이었다. 레빈은 다만 그에게 자기가 행복하다고, 그를 사랑한다고, 그가 자기를 위해 해 준 일을 결코, 결코 잊지 않겠다고 말할 짬밖에 없었다. 스테판 아르카지치의 눈과 미소는, 그가 그 감정을 알맞게 이해하고 있음을 레빈에게 보여 주었다.\n\n\"오, 그럼 아직 죽을 때는 아닌 거로군?\" 스테판 아르카지치가 감격에 겨워 레빈의 손을 꼭 쥐며 말했다.\n\n\"아-아-니!\" 레빈이 말했다.\n\n다리아 알렉산드로브나 역시 그에게 작별 인사를 하면서, \"키티를 다시 만나게 되셔서 정말 기뻐요! 오랜 친구는 소중히 여겨야 하는 법이죠.\" 하고 일종의 축하 같은 말을 건넸다. 레빈은 다리아 알렉산드로브나의 이런 말이 마음에 들지 않았다. 그녀로서는 이 모든 것이 얼마나 숭고하고 자기 따위가 헤아릴 수 없는 것인지 이해할 수 없을 테고, 또 감히 그것에 대해 입을 떼서도 안 되는 것이었다. 레빈은 그들에게 작별을 고했지만, 혼자 남지 않으려고 형에게 들러붙었다.\n\n\"어디 가세요?\"\n\n\"회의에 가는 길이다.\"\n\n\"그럼 저도 같이 가요. 그래도 돼요?\"\n\n\"뭐 하러? 그래, 따라오너라.\" 세르게이 이바노비치가 웃으며 말했다. \"너 오늘 무슨 일 있느냐?\"\n\n\"저요? 행복이 무슨 일이죠!\" 레빈이 그들이 타고 가던 마차의 창문을 내리며 말했다. \"괜찮으시죠?—너무 답답해서요. 행복이 무슨 일이라고요! 형은 왜 한 번도 결혼을 안 하셨어요?\"\n\n세르게이 이바노비치가 미소 지었다.\n\n\"나야 아주 기쁘다, 그 아가씨도 참한 처녀…\" 세르게이 이바노비치가 말을 시작했다.\n\n\"그 말씀은 마세요! 마세요!\" 레빈이 두 손으로 형의 모피 외투 깃을 움켜쥐고 그 속에 얼굴을 파묻게 하며 소리쳤다. \"참한 처녀\"라는 말은 너무도 단순하고 너무도 보잘것없는 말이어서, 그의 감정과는 도무지 어울리지 않았다.\n\n세르게이 이바노비치가, 그에게는 드문 일인, 유쾌하게 큰 소리로 껄껄 웃었다. \"어쨌든, 내가 이 일을 무척 기뻐한다는 것만은 말해도 되겠지.\"\n\n\"그건 내일, 내일 하시고 더는 안 돼요! 아무것도, 아무것도, 입 다무세요.\" 레빈은 이렇게 말하고 다시 한번 형을 모피 외투 속에 파묻게 하며 덧붙였다. \"전 형을 정말 좋아해요! 그런데, 제가 회의에 참석해도 될까요?\"\n\n\"물론 되고말고.\"\n\n\"오늘은 무슨 토론을 하시는데요?\" 레빈이 미소를 거두지 못한 채 물었다.\n\n그들은 회의장에 도착했다. 레빈은 서기가 분명 자기도 이해하지 못하는 의사록을 머뭇거리며 읽어 내려가는 소리를 들었다. 하지만 레빈은 그 서기의 얼굴에서 그가 얼마나 선량하고 호감 가고 마음 따뜻한 사람인지를 알아보았다. 의사록을 읽으며 어쩔 줄 몰라 하고 쩔쩔매는 모습에서 그것이 뚜렷이 드러났다. 그러고 나서 토론이 시작되었다. 그들은 어떤 금액의 유용과 어떤 배관 설치 문제를 두고 다투었고, 세르게이 이바노비치는 두 위원에게 매우 신랄하게 굴며 의기양양한 태도로 무언가를 장황하게 늘어놓았다. 그러자 다른 위원 하나가 종잇조각에 무언가를 끄적이더니, 처음에는 머뭇거리며 시작했으나 이내 더없이 표독스럽고도 통쾌하게 그에게 응수했다. 그다음에는 스비야시스키(그도 그 자리에 있었다)가 매우 훌륭하고 점잖게 한마디 거들었다. 레빈은 그들의 말에 귀를 기울이면서, 이 사라진 금액이며 이 배관이며 하는 것들이 조금도 실질적인 게 아니라는 것을, 그리고 그들이 전혀 화가 나 있지 않으며 모두가 더없이 호감 가고 마음씨 고운 사람들이라는 것을, 그래서 그들 사이의 모든 것이 더없이 행복하고 매력적이라는 것을 분명히 알아챘다. 그들은 누구에게도 해를 끼치지 않았고, 다들 그것을 즐기고 있었다. 레빈을 놀라게 한 것은, 오늘 자기가 그들 모두를 꿰뚫어 볼 수 있다는 것, 작고 거의 알아챌 수 없는 징후들로부터 그들 하나하나의 영혼을 알아내고, 그들 모두가 마음속으로 선량한 사람들임을 똑똑히 본다는 것이었다. 게다가 그날 그들은 특히 레빈 자신을 몹시도 좋아했다. 그것은 그들이 그에게 말을 거는 태도에서, 심지어 그가 모르는 사람들조차 다정하고 살가운 눈길로 그를 바라보는 데서 분명히 드러났다.\n\n\"어떠냐, 마음에 들었느냐?\" 세르게이 이바노비치가 그에게 물었다.\n\n\"아주요. 이렇게 흥미로운 줄은 몰랐어요! 훌륭해요! 멋져요!\"\n\n스비야시스키가 레빈에게 다가와 자기 집에 차를 마시러 들르라고 청했다. 레빈은 자기가 도대체 스비야시스키의 무엇을 마음에 들어 하지 않았던 건지, 그에게서 무엇을 찾지 못했던 건지 도무지 헤아릴 수도 떠올릴 수도 없었다. 그는 영리하고 놀랍도록 마음씨 고운 사람이었다.\n\n\"더없이 기쁩니다.\" 레빈은 이렇게 말하고는 그의 아내와 처제의 안부를 물었다. 그러다 묘한 연상 작용으로—그의 상상 속에서 스비야시스키의 처제라는 생각이 결혼과 결부되어 있었기 때문에—자기 행복에 대해 이야기하기에 그들보다 더 알맞은 상대는 없으리라는 생각이 떠올랐고, 그는 그들을 찾아가게 된 것이 무척 기뻤다.\n\n스비야시스키는 그의 영지 개량에 관해 이것저것 물었는데, 늘 그렇듯 유럽에서 이미 행해지지 않은 일이라면 아무것도 할 수 없다는 것을 전제로 하고 있었다. 그런데 이제 그것이 레빈을 조금도 짜증나게 하지 않았다. 오히려 그는 스비야시스키가 옳으며, 그 모든 일이 별 가치가 없다고 느꼈고, 스비야시스키가 자신의 올바른 견해를 끝까지 드러내기를 피하는 그 놀라운 부드러움과 배려를 알아보았다. 스비야시스키 집안의 부인들은 유난히도 사랑스러웠다. 레빈에게는 그들이 이미 모든 것을 알고 그를 가엾이 여기면서도, 다만 섬세한 마음에서 아무 말도 하지 않는 것처럼 여겨졌다. 그는 한 시간, 두 시간, 세 시간을 그들과 함께 머물며 자기 가슴을 가득 채운 단 한 가지만 빼고 온갖 주제에 대해 이야기를 나누었고, 자기가 그들을 끔찍이도 지루하게 만들고 있다는 것도, 그들이 잠자리에 들 시각이 한참 지났다는 것도 알아채지 못했다.\n\n스비야시스키는 하품을 하고 친구가 빠져 있는 이상한 기분을 의아해하며 현관까지 그를 배웅했다. 한 시가 지나 있었다. 레빈은 호텔로 돌아왔고, 이제 자기 초조함과 함께 홀로 남아 여전히 보내야 할 시간이 열 시간이나 남았다는 생각에 아연실색했다. 밤새 자지 않고 당번을 서던 하인이 그의 양초에 불을 밝히고는 물러가려 했지만, 레빈이 그를 붙잡았다. 전에도 눈여겨본 적이 있던 이 하인 예고르는, 레빈에게 무척 영리하고 훌륭하며, 무엇보다도 마음씨 고운 사람으로 다가왔다.\n\n\"그런데 예고르, 잠을 못 자는 게 힘든 일이지, 안 그런가?\"\n\n\"참아 내야지요! 보시다시피 저희 일이 다 그런걸요. 점잖은 댁에서야 일이 더 수월하지만, 그래도 여기서는 벌이가 더 낫지요.\"\n\n알고 보니 예고르에게는 가족이 있었는데, 아들 셋과 삯바느질하는 딸 하나였고, 그는 그 딸을 마구점 회계원에게 시집보내고 싶어 했다.\n\n이 말을 듣고서 레빈은 예고르에게, 자기 생각에는 결혼에서 가장 중요한 것이 사랑이며, 사랑만 있으면 언제나 행복할 수 있는데, 그것은 행복이란 오로지 자기 자신에게 달린 것이기 때문이라고 일러 주었다.\n\n예고르는 주의 깊게 귀를 기울였고, 레빈의 생각을 분명히 잘 알아들었지만, 그것에 동의하는 뜻으로, 레빈을 몹시도 놀라게 한 한마디를 했으니, 좋은 주인들을 모실 때면 늘 그 주인들에게 만족했고, 지금도 비록 프랑스 사람이긴 하지만 자기 고용주에게 더없이 만족하고 있다는 것이었다.\n\n'놀랍도록 마음씨 고운 친구로군!' 레빈은 생각했다.\n\n\"그런데 예고르 자네는, 결혼할 때 자네 아내를 사랑했는가?\"\n\n\"아이고! 안 할 까닭이 있겠습니까?\" 예고르가 대답했다.\n\n그리고 레빈은 예고르 역시 흥분한 상태로 자기 마음속 깊은 감정을 죄다 털어놓으려 한다는 것을 알아챘다.\n\n\"제 인생도 참 별난 것이었습죠. 어릴 적부터…\" 그는 사람들이 하품을 옮듯 분명 레빈의 열정에 전염된 듯, 눈을 반짝이며 말을 꺼냈다.\n\n하지만 바로 그 순간 초인종 소리가 들렸다. 예고르는 가 버렸고, 레빈은 홀로 남았다. 그는 저녁 식사 때 거의 아무것도 먹지 않았고, 스비야시스키 집에서도 차와 야식을 마다했지만, 야식 생각조차 할 수 없었다. 그는 지난밤에 잠을 자지 못했으면서도, 잠 생각조차 할 수 없었다. 방은 추웠으나, 그는 열기에 짓눌렸다. 그는 창문의 여닫이 환기창을 두 짝 다 열어젖히고, 열린 환기창 맞은편 탁자에 가 앉았다. 눈 덮인 지붕들 너머로 사슬이 달린 장식 십자가가 보였고, 그 위로는 카펠라의 누르스름한 빛과 함께 큰곰자리의 삼각형이 솟아오르고 있었다. 그는 십자가를, 그다음에는 별들을 바라보았고, 방 안으로 고르게 흘러드는 차고 신선한 공기를 들이마시며, 꿈속에서처럼 자기 상상 속에 떠오르는 형상들과 추억들을 좇았다. 네 시에 그는 복도에서 발소리를 듣고 문틈으로 내다보았다. 그가 아는 노름꾼 먀스킨이 클럽에서 돌아오는 길이었다. 그는 침울하게, 미간을 찌푸리고 기침을 하며 걷고 있었다. '가엾고 불운한 친구로군!' 레빈은 생각했고, 이 사람에 대한 사랑과 연민에서 눈에 눈물이 고였다. 그는 그와 이야기를 나누며 위로해 주고 싶었지만, 자기가 셔츠밖에 걸치지 않았다는 것을 떠올리고는 마음을 고쳐먹고, 다시 열린 환기창 곁에 앉아 찬 공기에 몸을 담그고서, 말이 없으나 자기에게는 의미로 가득 찬 십자가의 빼어난 선과, 떠오르는 음산한 노란 별을 바라보았다. 일곱 시에 마룻바닥을 닦는 사람들의 소리와 어느 하인 부서에서 종이 울리는 소리가 들려왔고, 레빈은 자기 몸이 얼어붙기 시작한다는 것을 느꼈다. 그는 환기창을 닫고, 세수를 하고, 옷을 입고, 거리로 나갔다.\n\n# Chapter 15\n\n거리는 여전히 텅 비어 있었다. 레빈은 셰르바츠키가로 갔다. 손님용 현관문은 닫혀 있었고 모든 것이 잠들어 있었다. 그는 발길을 돌려 다시 자기 방으로 들어와 커피를 청했다. 낮 담당 하인이, 이번에는 예고르가 아니었는데, 커피를 가져왔다. 레빈은 그와 이야기를 나누려 했지만 하인을 부르는 종이 울렸고, 그는 나가 버렸다. 레빈은 커피를 마시려 했고 빵을 좀 입에 넣어 보았지만, 그의 입은 그 빵을 어떻게 해야 할지 도무지 갈피를 잡지 못했다. 레빈은 빵을 내려놓고 외투를 걸친 뒤 다시 산책을 나섰다. 두 번째로 셰르바츠키가의 계단에 이르렀을 때는 아홉 시였다. 집 안에서는 사람들이 막 일어나는 참이었고, 요리사가 장을 보러 나왔다. 적어도 두 시간은 더 견뎌야 했다.\n\n그날 밤과 아침 내내 레빈은 완전히 무의식 상태로 지냈고, 물질적인 삶의 조건에서 완전히 들어 올려진 듯한 기분이었다. 그는 온종일 아무것도 먹지 않았고, 이틀 밤을 자지 못했으며, 얼어붙은 공기 속에서 옷을 벗은 채로 몇 시간을 보냈는데도, 그저 어느 때보다 상쾌하고 강해진 정도가 아니라 자기 육체에서 완전히 자유로워진 느낌이었다. 그는 근육의 힘을 들이지 않고도 움직였고, 무엇이든 할 수 있을 것만 같았다. 필요하다면 위로 날아오를 수도, 집 한 귀퉁이를 들어 올릴 수도 있으리라고 확신했다. 그는 남은 시간을 거리에서 보내며, 끊임없이 시계를 들여다보고 주위를 둘러보았다.\n\n그리고 그때 본 것을, 그는 그 후로 다시는 보지 못했다. 특히 학교에 가는 아이들이며, 지붕에서 보도로 날아 내려오는 푸르스름한 비둘기들이며, 보이지 않는 손에 밀려 나온, 밀가루를 뒤집어쓴 작은 빵들이 그의 마음을 울렸다. 그 빵들, 그 비둘기들, 그리고 그 두 소년은 지상의 존재가 아니었다. 모든 것이 한꺼번에 일어났다. 한 소년이 비둘기를 향해 달려가며 레빈을 보고 미소 지었고, 비둘기는 날개를 푸드덕거리며 날아올라, 공중에서 떨리는 눈 알갱이들 사이로 햇빛에 반짝이며 쏜살같이 사라졌으며, 그러는 사이 작은 창문에서는 갓 구운 빵 냄새가 풍겨 왔고, 빵들이 진열되었다. 이 모든 것이 한데 어우러져 어찌나 비할 데 없이 멋졌던지 레빈은 기뻐서 웃다가 울었다. 가제트니 광장과 키슬로프카를 돌아 멀리 에둘러서 그는 다시 호텔로 돌아왔고, 시계를 앞에 놓고 열두 시가 되기를 기다리며 앉아 있었다. 옆방에서는 사람들이 무슨 기계니, 사기니 하는 이야기를 하며 아침 기침을 콜록거리고 있었다. 그들은 시곗바늘이 열두 시에 가까워졌다는 것을 알지 못했다. 시곗바늘이 열두 시에 닿았다. 레빈은 계단으로 나갔다. 썰매꾼들은 분명히 그 일에 대해 다 알고 있었다. 그들은 행복한 얼굴로 레빈을 둘러싸고는, 자기들끼리 다투며 서로 자기를 태워 달라고 했다. 다른 썰매꾼들의 기분을 상하게 하지 않으려고, 그들 썰매도 타겠노라 약속하면서 레빈은 한 사람을 골라 셰르바츠키가로 가자고 일렀다. 썰매꾼은 외투 위로, 그리고 억세고 혈색 좋은 붉은 목으로 삐죽 솟은 흰 셔츠 깃 때문에 멋져 보였다. 썰매는 높고 편안했으며, 도무지 그 후로는 레빈이 다시 타 본 적 없는 그런 썰매였고, 말도 좋은 놈이어서 질주하려 애썼지만 도무지 나아가지 못하는 듯했다. 마부는 셰르바츠키가의 집을 알고 있었고, 팔을 곡선으로 휘두르며 \"워!\" 하고, 특히나 자기 손님에 대한 존경을 드러내는 소리를 내면서 현관 앞에 멈춰 섰다. 셰르바츠키가의 수위도 분명히 그 일에 대해 다 알고 있었다. 그의 눈에 어린 미소와 그가 건넨 말투에서 그것이 분명히 드러났다.\n\n\"아니, 오랜만에 오셨군요, 콘스탄친 드미트리예비치!\"\n\n그는 모든 것을 알고 있었을 뿐만 아니라, 틀림없이 기뻐하며 그 기쁨을 감추려 애쓰고 있었다. 레빈은 그 다정한 노인의 눈을 들여다보며 자기 행복에서 무언가 새로운 것까지 깨달았다.\n\n\"다들 일어나셨나?\"\n\n\"들어가시지요! 그건 여기 두세요.\" 레빈이 모자를 가지러 돌아오려 하자, 그가 미소 지으며 말했다. 그 말에는 어떤 뜻이 담겨 있었다.\n\n\"어느 분께 나리의 방문을 전할까요?\" 하인이 물었다.\n\n그 하인은 젊은이로, 새로운 부류의 멋쟁이 하인이었지만, 마음씨가 무척 곱고 착한 사람이었으며, 그 역시 모든 것을 알고 있었다.\n\n\"공작 부인……공작님……아가씨께……\" 레빈이 말했다.\n\n그가 처음 본 사람은 마드무아젤 리농이었다. 그녀는 방을 가로질러 걸어왔는데, 그녀의 곱슬머리와 얼굴이 환하게 빛나고 있었다. 그녀와 막 말을 나누기 시작했을 때, 갑자기 문가에서 치맛자락 스치는 소리가 들렸고, 마드무아젤 리농은 레빈의 눈앞에서 사라졌으며, 행복이 가까이 다가온 데서 오는 기쁨에 찬 두려움이 그를 사로잡았다. 마드무아젤 리농은 몹시 서두르며 그를 남겨 두고 다른 문으로 나갔다. 그녀가 나가자마자 빠르고도 빠른 가벼운 발소리가 마룻바닥에서 울렸고, 그의 더없는 기쁨이, 그의 삶이, 그 자신이—그 자신 안에서 가장 좋은 것이, 그가 그토록 오래 찾고 갈망해 온 것이—빠르게, 그토록 빠르게 그에게 다가오고 있었다. 그녀는 걷는 것이 아니라 어떤 보이지 않는 힘에 이끌려 그에게로 떠오는 듯했다. 그는 그녀의 맑고 진실한 눈 말고는 아무것도 보지 못했는데, 그 눈은 그의 가슴을 가득 채운 것과 똑같은 사랑의 환희로 겁에 질려 있었다. 그 눈은 점점 더 가까이 빛나며 사랑의 빛으로 그를 눈멀게 했다. 그녀는 그에게 바싹 다가와, 닿을 듯이 멈춰 섰다. 그녀의 두 손이 들렸다가 그의 어깨 위로 내려앉았다.\n\n그녀는 할 수 있는 모든 것을 했다—그에게로 달려와 수줍어하면서도 행복하게 자기 자신을 온전히 내맡겼다. 그는 두 팔로 그녀를 감싸 안고, 자기 입맞춤을 구하는 그녀의 입술에 자기 입술을 포갰다.\n\n그녀 역시 밤새도록 잠을 이루지 못했고, 아침 내내 그를 기다리고 있었다.\n\n그녀의 어머니와 아버지는 두말없이 승낙했고, 그녀의 행복 속에서 행복해했다. 그녀는 그를 기다려 왔다. 그녀는 자기의 행복이자 그의 행복을 가장 먼저 그에게 전하고 싶었다. 그녀는 그를 혼자 만날 채비를 했고, 그 생각에 들떴으며, 수줍고 부끄러웠고, 자기가 무얼 하고 있는지 스스로도 알지 못했다. 그녀는 그의 발소리와 목소리를 듣고는, 문가에서 마드무아젤 리농이 나가기를 기다렸다. 마드무아젤 리농이 나가 버렸다. 어떻게 무엇을 할지 자신에게 묻지도, 생각하지도 않은 채, 그녀는 그에게 다가가 지금 하고 있는 그대로 했다.\n\n\"엄마한테 가요!\" 그녀가 그의 손을 잡으며 말했다. 그는 한참 동안 아무 말도 하지 못했는데, 한마디 말로 자기 감정의 숭고함을 더럽힐까 두려웠다기보다는, 무언가를 말하려 할 때마다 말 대신 행복의 눈물이 차오르는 것을 느꼈기 때문이었다. 그는 그녀의 손을 잡아 입을 맞췄다.\n\n\"이게 정말일까?\" 그가 마침내 목멘 소리로 말했다. \"당신이 날 사랑한다는 게 믿기지 않아, 여보!\"\n\n그녀는 그 \"여보\"라는 말에, 그리고 그가 그녀를 바라보는 그 수줍음에 미소 지었다.\n\n\"네!\" 그녀가 의미심장하게, 또박또박 말했다. \"난 정말 행복해요!\"\n\n그의 두 손을 놓지 않은 채, 그녀는 응접실로 들어갔다. 공작 부인은 그들을 보더니 가쁘게 숨을 몰아쉬며 이내 울기 시작했다가 곧바로 웃기 시작했고, 레빈이 예상치 못한 힘찬 걸음으로 그에게 달려와 그의 머리를 끌어안고 입을 맞추며 눈물로 그의 두 뺨을 적셨다.\n\n\"그래, 다 정해졌구나! 기쁘구나. 이 아이를 사랑해 주게. 정말 기뻐……키티!\"\n\n\"일을 정하는 데 오래 걸리지 않았군.\" 노공작이 짐짓 아무렇지 않은 척하며 말했다. 하지만 레빈은 그가 자기 쪽으로 돌아섰을 때 그의 눈이 젖어 있는 것을 알아챘다.\n\n\"난 오래전부터, 늘 이걸 바라 왔다네!\" 공작이 레빈의 팔을 잡아 자기 쪽으로 끌어당기며 말했다. \"이 가벼운 머리의 아이가 그…… 생각을 품고 있을 때조차도……\"\n\n\"아빠!\" 키티가 비명을 지르며 두 손으로 그의 입을 막았다.\n\n\"그래, 안 하마!\" 그가 말했다. \"난 아주, 아주…… 기…… 아, 내가 참 바보로구나……\"\n\n그는 키티를 끌어안고, 그녀의 얼굴에, 손에, 다시 얼굴에 입을 맞추고는, 그녀에게 성호를 그어 주었다.\n\n그리고 키티가 그의 억센 손에 얼마나 천천히, 다정하게 입맞추는지를 보았을 때, 그때까지 거의 알지 못했던 이 사람에 대한 새로운 사랑의 감정이 레빈에게 밀려왔다.\n\n# Chapter 16\n\n공작 부인은 안락의자에 앉아 말없이 미소 짓고 있었고, 공작은 그 곁에 앉았다. 키티는 아버지의 의자 옆에 서서 여전히 그의 손을 잡고 있었다. 모두가 입을 다물고 있었다.\n\n모든 것을 말로 옮기고, 모든 생각과 감정을 실질적인 질문으로 바꾼 것은 공작 부인이 처음이었다. 그리고 첫 순간만큼은 모두가 똑같이 이 일을 이상하고 괴롭게 느꼈다.\n\n\"언제로 할까요? 약혼식과 발표를 해야겠죠. 그리고 결혼식은 언제로 하죠? 어떻게 생각하세요, 알렉산드르?\"\n\n\"이 사람한테 물어봐요.\" 노공작이 레빈을 가리키며 말했다. \"이 일의 당사자이니.\"\n\n\"언제냐고요?\" 레빈이 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다. \"내일요. 제 생각을 묻는다면, 약혼식은 오늘, 결혼식은 내일이라고 하겠습니다.\"\n\n\"이런, mon cher, 그건 말도 안 돼요!\"\n\n\"그럼, 일주일 안에요.\"\n\n\"이 사람 아주 정신이 나갔네.\"\n\n\"아니, 왜요?\"\n\"세상에, 정말!\" 어머니가 이 성급함에 흐뭇해하며 미소 지었다. \"혼수는 어쩌고요?\"\n\n* mon cher: 프랑스어로 '여보게'.\n\"정말 혼수다 뭐다 그런 게 다 필요한가?\" 레빈은 섬뜩한 기분으로 생각했다. \"하지만 혼수며 약혼식이며 그런 것들이—그게 내 행복을 망칠 수 있을까? 아무것도 내 행복을 망칠 수는 없어!\" 그는 키티를 흘끗 보았고, 그녀가 혼수 생각에 조금도, 정말 조금도 동요하지 않는다는 것을 알아챘다. \"그렇다면 다 괜찮은 거지.\" 그는 생각했다.\n\n\"아, 저는 그런 건 아무것도 모릅니다. 그저 제가 바라는 바를 말씀드렸을 뿐이에요.\" 그가 미안한 듯이 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 그건 우리끼리 의논해 보자꾸나. 약혼식과 발표는 지금 해도 되겠어. 그게 아주 좋겠다.\"\n\n공작 부인이 남편에게 다가가 입을 맞추고는 자리를 뜨려 했지만, 그가 그녀를 붙잡아 끌어안고는 젊은 연인처럼 다정하게 미소 지으며 여러 번 입을 맞추었다. 두 노인은 한순간 분명히 어리둥절해져서, 다시 사랑에 빠진 것이 자기들인지 딸인지 잘 분간하지 못하는 것 같았다. 공작과 공작 부인이 나가자, 레빈은 약혼녀에게 다가가 그녀의 손을 잡았다. 이제 그는 마음을 가라앉히고 말을 할 수 있었고, 그녀에게 하고 싶은 말도 무척 많았다. 하지만 그는 정작 해야 할 말은 전혀 하지 않았다.\n\n\"이렇게 될 줄 나는 어떻게 알았을까요! 한 번도 바란 적은 없었어요. 그런데도 마음속으로는 늘 확신하고 있었어요.\" 그가 말했다. \"이건 미리 정해진 일이었다고 믿어요.\"\n\n\"저도요!\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그때조차도…….\" 그녀는 말을 멈추었다가, 진실한 눈으로 그를 단호하게 바라보며 다시 이어 갔다. \"제가 제 행복을 밀쳐 냈던 그때조차도요. 저는 언제나 당신만을 사랑했지만, 한순간 마음이 휩쓸렸어요. 당신에게 말해야겠어요…… 그걸 용서해 주실 수 있어요?\"\n\n\"어쩌면 그게 오히려 잘된 일인지도 몰라요. 당신은 나를 훨씬 더 많이 용서해 줘야 할 테니까요. 나도 당신에게 말해야 할 게…….\"\n\n이것은 그가 말하려고 마음먹었던 일들 가운데 하나였다. 그는 처음부터 그녀에게 두 가지를 털어놓기로 결심하고 있었다—자기가 그녀처럼 순결하지 못하다는 것, 그리고 자기가 신앙이 없는 사람이라는 것. 그것은 괴로운 일이었지만, 그는 이 두 가지 사실을 모두 그녀에게 말해야 한다고 여겼다.\n\n\"아니, 지금은 아니에요, 나중에!\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"좋아요, 나중에요. 하지만 꼭 말해 줘야 해요. 저는 아무것도 두렵지 않아요. 모든 걸 알고 싶어요. 이제 다 정해졌어요.\"\n\n그가 덧붙였다. \"당신은 내가 어떤 사람이든 나를 받아 주겠다고 정한 거죠—나를 버리지 않을 거죠? 그렇죠?\"\n\n\"네, 그럼요.\"\n\n마드무아젤 리농이 들어와 그들의 대화가 끊겼다. 그녀는 꾸민 듯하지만 다정한 미소를 띠고 가장 아끼는 제자를 축하하러 온 것이었다. 그녀가 가기도 전에 하인들이 들어와 축하의 말을 건넸다. 이어서 친척들이 도착했고, 레빈이 결혼식 다음 날에야 비로소 헤어날 수 있었던 그 행복에 겨운 야단법석이 시작되었다. 레빈은 끊임없이 어색하고 거북한 상태에 놓여 있었지만, 그 와중에도 행복의 강도는 줄곧 더해만 갔다. 그는 사람들이 자기에게 무언가 많은 것을 기대하고 있다는 것을 끊임없이 느꼈다—그게 무엇인지는 몰랐지만. 그래서 그는 시키는 대로 무엇이든 다 했고, 그 모든 것이 그에게 행복을 가져다주었다. 그는 자기 약혼만큼은 다른 이들의 것과는 전혀 다를 것이라고, 약혼한 남녀에게 으레 따르는 평범한 절차들이 자기만의 특별한 행복을 망쳐 버리리라고 생각했었다. 하지만 결국 그는 다른 사람들이 하는 것과 똑같이 하게 되었고, 그의 행복은 그로 인해 오히려 더 커지기만 했으며, 점점 더 특별해지고, 점점 더 지금껏 일어난 그 어떤 일과도 닮지 않은 것이 되어 갔다.\n\n\"이제 우리 사탕과자를 먹어야겠어요.\" 마드무아젤 리농이 말하자—레빈은 사탕과자를 사러 마차를 몰고 나갔다.\n\n\"음, 정말 기쁘네요.\" 스비야시스키가 말했다. \"꽃다발은 포민 가게에서 사라고 권하고 싶습니다.\"\n\n\"아, 그것도 필요한가요?\" 그러고는 그는 포민 가게로 마차를 몰았다.\n\n그의 형은 그가 비용이 많이 들 테고, 선물도 줘야 할 테니 돈을 빌려주겠다고 했다…….\n\n\"아, 선물도 필요한가요?\" 그러고는 그는 풀데 가게로 달려갔다.\n\n그리고 사탕과자 가게에서도, 포민 가게에서도, 풀데 가게에서도 그는 사람들이 자기를 기다리고 있었음을, 자기를 보고 반가워하며 자기의 행복을 자랑스러워한다는 것을 알았다. 그 며칠 동안 그가 상대해야 했던 모든 사람이 그러했듯이. 무엇보다 놀라운 것은, 모두가 그를 좋아할 뿐 아니라, 전에는 무뚝뚝하고 차갑고 매정하던 사람들조차 그에게 열광하며 무슨 일에서든 그에게 양보하고, 그의 감정을 다정하고 섬세하게 대해 주었으며, 그의 약혼녀가 완벽함 그 이상이기에 그가 세상에서 가장 행복한 사람이라는 그의 확신을 함께 나눈다는 것이었다. 키티 역시 똑같이 느꼈다. 노르드스톤 백작 부인이 좀 더 나은 상대를 바랐었노라고 넌지시 비치자, 키티는 어찌나 화를 냈던지, 세상에 레빈보다 더 나은 사람은 있을 수 없다는 것을 너무도 확고하게 증명해 보였고, 결국 노르드스톤 백작 부인도 그것을 인정할 수밖에 없었으며, 키티가 있는 자리에서는 황홀하게 감탄하는 미소 없이는 레빈을 만나는 일이 없었다.\n\n그가 약속했던 고백은 이 시기의 유일하게 괴로운 사건이었다. 그는 노공작과 의논한 끝에, 그의 허락을 받아 키티에게 자기 일기를 건넸다. 거기에는 그를 괴롭히던 고백이 적혀 있었다. 그는 미래의 아내를 염두에 두고 그 무렵 이 일기를 써 두었던 것이다. 두 가지가 그를 고통스럽게 했다. 자기에게 순결함이 없다는 것과 신앙이 없다는 것이었다. 그가 불신앙을 고백한 것은 별다른 주목을 받지 못하고 넘어갔다. 그녀는 신앙심이 깊었고, 종교의 진리들을 한 번도 의심한 적이 없었지만, 그의 겉으로 드러난 불신앙은 그녀에게 조금도 영향을 미치지 못했다. 사랑을 통해 그녀는 그의 영혼을 속속들이 알았고, 그 영혼 속에서 자기가 바라는 것을 보았으며, 그러한 영혼의 상태를 불신앙이라 부른다는 것 따위는 그녀에게 아무런 의미도 없었다. 그러나 다른 한 가지 고백은 그녀를 서럽게 울게 만들었다.\n\n레빈은 내면의 갈등 끝에 그녀에게 일기를 건넸다. 그는 자기와 그녀 사이에 비밀이 있을 수 없고, 또 있어서도 안 된다는 것을 알았기에, 반드시 그래야 한다고 마음먹었던 것이다. 하지만 그것이 그녀에게 어떤 영향을 미칠지는 미처 깨닫지 못했고, 그녀의 입장이 되어 생각해 보지 못했다. 바로 그날 저녁 극장에 가기 전에 그가 그녀의 집에 들러 방으로 들어갔을 때, 자기가 안겨 주었으나 무엇으로도 돌이킬 수 없는 그 괴로움에 비참해진, 눈물범벅이 된 가련하고 사랑스러운 그녀의 얼굴을 보고 나서야, 그는 자기의 수치스러운 과거와 그녀의 비둘기 같은 순결함을 갈라놓는 심연을 느꼈고, 자기가 저지른 일에 소스라치게 놀랐다.\n\n\"가져가요, 이 끔찍한 책을 가져가 버려요!\" 그녀가 탁자 위 자기 앞에 놓인 공책들을 밀어내며 말했다. \"왜 이걸 저에게 주셨어요? 아니, 그래도 이게 나았어요.\" 그녀는 절망에 찬 그의 얼굴에 마음이 움직여 이렇게 덧붙였다. \"하지만 끔찍해요, 끔찍해요!\"\n\n그는 고개를 떨구었고, 입을 다물었다. 아무 말도 할 수 없었다.\n\n\"당신은 나를 용서할 수 없겠죠.\" 그가 속삭였다.\n\n\"네, 용서해요. 하지만 너무 끔찍해요!\"\n\n그러나 그의 행복은 너무도 거대했기에 이 고백도 그것을 산산이 부수지는 못했고, 다만 거기에 또 다른 그늘 하나를 더했을 뿐이었다. 그녀는 그를 용서했다. 하지만 그때부터 그는 그 어느 때보다도 자기가 그녀에게 어울리지 않는 사람이라고 여겼고, 그녀 앞에서 전보다 더 깊이 도덕적으로 고개를 숙였으며, 분에 넘치는 자기의 행복을 그 어느 때보다도 소중히 여기게 되었다.\n\n# Chapter 17\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 만찬 도중과 그 후에 오갔던 대화를 무심결에 기억 속에서 되짚으며 자신의 호젓한 방으로 돌아왔다. 다리야 알렉산드로브나가 용서에 관해 한 말은 그에게 짜증 외에는 아무것도 불러일으키지 못했다. 그리스도교의 가르침이 자기 경우에 적용될 수 있는가 없는가 하는 것은 가볍게 논할 만한 문제가 아니었고, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 오래전에 이미 그 문제를 부정적으로 결론지어 둔 터였다. 오갔던 모든 말 가운데 그의 기억에 가장 또렷이 박힌 것은 멍청하지만 사람 좋은 투롭친이 한 말이었다. \"사내답게 처신했지, 암! 결투를 신청해 쏴 죽였다니까!\" 다들 그 감정에 공감하는 듯했으나, 예의를 차리느라 입 밖에 내지 않았을 뿐이었다.\n\n\"하지만 이미 끝난 일이야, 생각해 봐야 소용없어.\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 스스로에게 일렀다. 그리고 자기 앞에 놓인 여정과 처리해야 할 검토 작업만을 떠올리며 방으로 들어가, 자신을 안내한 문지기에게 하인이 어디 있느냐고 물었다. 문지기는 하인이 방금 막 나갔다고 대답했다. 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 차를 가져오라 이르고 탁자 앞에 앉아 안내서를 집어 들고는 여정의 경로를 살피기 시작했다.\n\n\"전보가 두 통 왔습니다.\" 하인이 방으로 들어오며 말했다. \"용서하십시오, 각하. 방금 막 잠깐 나갔던 참이라.\"\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 전보를 받아 펼쳤다. 첫 번째 전보는 카레닌이 그토록 탐내던 바로 그 자리에 스트레모프가 임명되었다는 통지였다. 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 전보를 내던지고 얼굴을 약간 붉히며 일어나 방 안을 이리저리 거닐기 시작했다. \"Quos vult perdere dementat.\" 그는 quos라는 말로 이번 임명을 책임진 자들을 가리키며 중얼거렸다. 그가 그 자리를 얻지 못한 것, 보란 듯이 제쳐진 것이 그토록 분했던 것은 아니었다. 다만 말만 번지르르하게 늘어놓는 그 허풍쟁이 스트레모프야말로 그 자리에 가장 어울리지 않는 인물임을 저들이 보지 못한다는 사실이 그로서는 이해할 수도 없고 놀라울 따름이었다. 이번 임명으로 저들이 어떻게 스스로를 망치고 자신들의 prestige를 떨어뜨리고 있는지 어떻게 보지 못한단 말인가?\n\n\"또 그 비슷한 거겠지.\" 그는 두 번째 전보를 펼치며 씁쓸하게 혼잣말을 했다. 전보는 아내가 보낸 것이었다. 푸른 연필로 쓴 그녀의 이름, \"안나\"가 가장 먼저 그의 눈에 들어왔다. \"저는 죽어 가고 있습니다. 부디, 간곡히 청하오니 와 주세요. 당신의 용서를 받는다면 더 편히 죽을 수 있겠습니다.\" 그는 이렇게 읽었다. 그는 경멸하듯 미소 짓고 전보를 내던졌다. 이것이 속임수이자 사기라는 것, 그 점에는 처음 한순간 의심의 여지가 없다고 그는 생각했다.\n\n\"저 여자가 못 할 거짓말은 없지. 해산이 가까웠으니. 어쩌면 해산일지도 몰라. 하지만 저들의 목적이 뭘까? 아이를 적자로 만들고, 나를 곤경에 빠뜨려 이혼을 막으려는 거겠지.\" 그는 생각했다. \"그런데 거기 무슨 말이 있었지. 저는 죽어 가고 있습니다…….\" 그는 전보를 다시 읽었고, 거기 적힌 말의 단순한 의미가 문득 그의 가슴을 쳤다.\n\n\"만약 사실이라면?\" 그는 스스로에게 물었다. \"만약 고통과 죽음이 임박한 순간에 그녀가 진심으로 뉘우치고 있는데, 내가 그것을 속임수로 여기고 가기를 거부한다면? 그건 잔인할 뿐만 아니라 모두가 나를 비난할 테고, 게다가 나로서는 어리석은 짓이기도 하겠지.\"\n\n\"표트르, 마차를 불러 다오. 페테르부르크로 가겠다.\" 그는 하인에게 말했다.\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 페테르부르크로 가서 아내를 보기로 결심했다. 그녀의 병이 속임수라면 아무 말 없이 그대로 떠나오리라. 그녀가 정말로 위독하여 죽기 전에 자기를 보고 싶어 하는 것이라면, 살아 있는 그녀를 보게 될 경우 용서해 주고, 너무 늦게 도착하면 마지막 도리를 다하리라.\n\n가는 내내 그는 어떻게 처신해야 할지에 대해서는 더 이상 생각하지 않았다.\n\n기차에서 밤을 보낸 탓에 피로와 불결한 느낌에 젖은 채, 페테르부르크의 이른 안개 속에서 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 인적 없는 네프스키 거리를 지나며 자기를 기다리고 있는 것은 생각지 않은 채 똑바로 앞만 바라보았다. 그는 그것을 생각할 수가 없었다. 앞으로 벌어질 일을 그려 보노라면, 그녀가 죽음으로써 자기 처지의 온갖 곤란이 단번에 사라지리라는 생각을 떨쳐 낼 수가 없었기 때문이다. 빵집들, 닫힌 가게들, 밤 마부들, 보도를 쓰는 문지기들이 그의 눈앞을 스쳐 지나갔고, 그는 자기를 기다리는 것, 차마 바랄 수 없으면서도 바라고 있는 그것에 대한 생각을 억누르려 애쓰며 그 모든 것을 바라보았다. 그는 마차를 몰아 현관 계단 앞에 댔다. 입구에는 마부가 잠든 채로 썰매 한 대와 마차 한 대가 서 있었다. 현관으로 들어서며 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 마치 머릿속 가장 깊숙한 구석에서 자신의 결심을 끄집어내듯 하여 그것을 단단히 다잡았다. 그 결심의 내용은 이러했다. \"속임수라면 차분한 경멸과 떠남. 사실이라면 마땅한 도리를 다할 것.\"\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 벨을 울리기도 전에 문지기가 문을 열었다. 문지기 카피토니치는 낡은 외투에 넥타이도 매지 않고 슬리퍼를 신은 우스꽝스러운 차림이었다.\n\n\"마님은 어떠신가?\"\n\n\"어제 무사히 해산하셨습니다.\"\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 우뚝 멈춰 섰고 얼굴이 하얗게 질렸다. 자기가 얼마나 간절히 그녀의 죽음을 바라 왔는지를 이제 또렷이 느꼈다.\n\n\"그래서 지금은 어떠신가?\"\n\n코르네이가 아침 앞치마를 두른 채 아래층으로 달려 내려왔다.\n\n\"매우 위중하십니다.\" 그가 대답했다. \"어제 진찰이 있었고, 지금은 의사 선생님이 와 계십니다.\"\n\n\"내 짐을 받아라.\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 말했고, 아직 그녀가 죽을 희망이 남아 있다는 소식에 얼마간 안도를 느끼며 현관으로 들어섰다.\n\n모자걸이에는 군용 외투가 걸려 있었다. 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 그것을 알아채고 물었다.\n\n\"누가 와 있느냐?\"\n\n\"의사 선생님과 산파, 그리고 브론스키 백작님입니다.\"\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 안쪽 방들로 들어갔다.\n\n응접실에는 아무도 없었다. 그의 발소리에 그녀의 내실에서 연보랏빛 리본이 달린 모자를 쓴 산파가 나왔다.\n\n산파는 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에게 다가가, 죽음이 임박했을 때 생기는 그 친밀함으로 그의 팔을 잡고 침실 쪽으로 이끌었다.\n\n\"오셔서 정말 다행이에요! 마님이 줄곧 당신 말씀만, 오직 당신 말씀만 하고 계세요.\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"얼음을 어서!\" 침실에서 의사의 단호한 목소리가 들려왔다.\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 그녀의 내실로 들어갔다.\n\n탁자 곁, 낮은 의자에 비스듬히 앉아 두 손에 얼굴을 묻고 흐느끼고 있는 사람은 브론스키였다. 그는 의사의 목소리에 벌떡 일어나 얼굴에서 손을 떼었다가 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치를 보았다. 남편을 본 그는 너무도 당황한 나머지 다시 주저앉으며, 사라지고 싶다는 듯 머리를 어깨 사이로 움츠렸다. 그러나 가까스로 마음을 다잡아 일어서며 말했다.\n\n\"부인은 죽어 가고 있습니다. 의사들 말로는 가망이 없다고 합니다. 저는 전적으로 당신 처분에 맡기겠습니다. 다만 제가 여기 있게만 해 주십시오…… 저는 당신 뜻대로 하겠습니다. 저는…….\"\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 브론스키의 눈물을 보자, 타인의 고통을 볼 때면 늘 솟구치던 그 신경질적인 격정이 밀려오는 것을 느꼈고, 얼굴을 돌린 채 그의 나머지 말은 듣지도 않고 서둘러 문 쪽으로 옮겨 갔다. 침실에서 안나가 뭐라고 말하는 목소리가 들려왔다. 그녀의 목소리는 생기에 차 있었고, 열에 들떠 있었으며, 더없이 또렷한 억양을 띠고 있었다. 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 침실로 들어가 침대로 다가갔다. 그녀는 얼굴을 그쪽으로 향한 채 누워 있었다. 두 뺨은 새빨갛게 달아올라 있었고 두 눈은 반짝였으며, 실내복 소매 밖으로 내민 작고 흰 두 손은 누비이불을 만지작거리며 비비 꼬고 있었다. 그녀는 건강하고 생기에 넘칠 뿐만 아니라 더없이 행복한 심정에 잠긴 듯 보였다. 그녀는 빠르고 음악적으로, 유난히 정확한 발음과 풍부한 억양으로 말하고 있었다.\n\n\"알렉세이라면, 저는 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치를 두고 하는 말이에요(둘 다 알렉세이라니, 참 이상하고 끔찍한 일이죠, 그렇지 않아요?), 알렉세이라면 제 청을 거절하지 않을 거예요. 저는 잊을 거고, 그이는 용서할 거예요…… 그런데 왜 안 오는 걸까요? 그이는 너무 착해서 자기가 얼마나 착한지조차 몰라요. 아, 하느님, 이 고통이라니! 물 좀 줘요, 어서! 아, 그러면 우리 아기에게, 우리 딸에게 안 좋을 텐데! 아, 그럼 좋아요, 아기를 유모에게 맡겨요. 그래요, 동의해요, 사실 그게 나아요. 그이가 올 거예요. 아기를 보면 마음 아파할 거예요. 아기를 유모에게 맡겨요.\"\n\n\"안나 아르카지예브나, 그분이 오셨어요. 여기 계세요!\" 산파가 안나의 주의를 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에게 돌리려 애쓰며 말했다.\n\n\"아, 무슨 말도 안 되는 소리예요!\" 안나는 남편을 보지 못한 채 계속 말했다. \"아니, 그 애를 내게 줘요. 내 아기를 내게 줘요! 그이는 아직 안 왔어요. 당신은 그이가 나를 용서하지 않을 거라고 하지만, 그건 당신이 그이를 모르기 때문이에요. 아무도 그이를 몰라요. 오직 나만 알아요, 그리고 나조차도 그건 힘든 일이었어요. 그이의 눈을 나는 잘 알아야 해요. 세료자도 꼭 그 눈을 가졌죠. 그래서 나는 그 눈을 차마 볼 수가 없어요. 세료자는 저녁을 먹었나요? 다들 그 애를 잊어버릴 거라는 거 알아요. 그이라면 잊지 않을 텐데. 세료자를 구석 방으로 옮기고, 마리에트한테 그 애와 같이 자 달라고 해야 해요.\"\n\n별안간 그녀는 움찔 뒤로 물러서며 입을 다물었다. 그리고 마치 매를 맞을 것을 예상하는 듯, 자신을 지키려는 듯 공포에 질려 두 손을 들어 얼굴을 가렸다. 그녀가 남편을 본 것이다.\n\n\"아니야, 아니야!\" 그녀가 입을 열었다. \"나는 그이가 무섭지 않아요. 나는 죽음이 무서운 거예요. 알렉세이, 이리 와요. 나는 서둘러야 해요, 시간이 없으니까, 살날이 얼마 안 남았으니까. 곧 열이 오르기 시작하면 나는 아무것도 알아듣지 못할 테니까. 지금은 알아요, 다 알아요, 다 보여요!\"\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 주름진 얼굴은 고통스러운 표정을 띠었다. 그는 그녀의 손을 잡고 뭐라고 말하려 했으나 입 밖에 낼 수가 없었다. 아랫입술이 떨렸지만 그는 여전히 자신의 감정과 싸우며 이따금씩 그녀를 흘끗거릴 뿐이었다. 그리고 그녀를 흘끗거릴 때마다, 그는 그녀의 눈이 일찍이 본 적 없는 그토록 열정적이고 의기양양한 다정함으로 자기를 응시하고 있는 것을 보았다.\n\n\"잠깐만요, 당신은 몰라요…… 조금만 있어 줘요, 있어 줘요!……\" 그녀는 마치 생각을 가다듬는 듯 말을 멈추었다. \"그래요.\" 그녀가 입을 열었다. \"그래요, 그래요, 그래요. 이게 바로 내가 하고 싶었던 말이에요. 나를 보고 놀라지 말아요. 나는 여전히 똑같아요…… 하지만 내 안에는 또 다른 여자가 있어요, 나는 그 여자가 무서워요. 그 여자는 그 사람을 사랑했고, 나는 당신을 미워하려 애썼지만 예전의 그 여자를 잊을 수가 없었어요. 나는 그 여자가 아니에요. 지금의 나는 진짜 나예요, 온전한 나예요. 나는 지금 죽어 가요, 죽으리라는 걸 알아요, 그이에게 물어봐요. 지금도 느껴져요, 보세요, 여기 내 발에, 내 손에, 내 손가락에 추가 매달려 있어요. 내 손가락, 보세요, 얼마나 큰지! 하지만 이 모든 게 곧 끝날 거예요…… 오직 한 가지만 바라요, 나를 용서해 줘요, 나를 완전히 용서해 줘요. 나는 끔찍한 여자예요, 하지만 내 유모가 늘 들려주었던 거룩한 순교자, 그 여자 이름이 뭐였더라? 그 여자는 더 나빴어요. 나는 로마로 갈 거예요. 거기 황야가 있어요, 거기서라면 나는 누구에게도 폐가 되지 않을 거예요, 다만 세료자와 어린것만 데려갈래요…… 아니, 당신은 나를 용서할 수 없어요! 알아요, 용서받을 수 없는 일이라는 거! 아니, 아니, 가요, 당신은 너무 착해요!\" 그녀는 불덩이 같은 한 손으로는 그의 손을 잡은 채, 다른 손으로는 그를 밀어냈다.\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 신경질적인 동요는 점점 커져 갔고, 이제는 그가 더 이상 그것과 싸우기를 멈출 지경에 이르렀다. 그는 자신이 신경질적인 동요라고 여겼던 것이 오히려 일찍이 알지 못했던 새로운 행복을 단번에 안겨 주는 더없이 복된 정신 상태임을 문득 느꼈다. 그는 평생 따르려 애써 온 그리스도교의 율법이 자신에게 원수를 용서하고 사랑하라 명한다는 것을 생각한 것이 아니었다. 다만 원수를 향한 사랑과 용서의 기쁜 감정이 그의 가슴을 가득 채웠다. 그는 무릎을 꿇었고, 소매 너머로 불처럼 그를 데우는 그녀의 팔 안쪽 굽이에 머리를 묻고 어린아이처럼 흐느꼈다. 그녀는 한 팔로 그의 머리를 감싸 안고 그에게로 몸을 기울이며, 도전적인 긍지로 눈을 치떴다.\n\n\"바로 저이예요. 나는 저이를 알아봤어요! 자, 모두 나를 용서해 줘요, 나를 용서해 줘요!…… 또 왔네, 왜 가지 않는 거지?…… 아, 이 망토들을 내게서 벗겨 줘요!\"\n\n의사는 그녀의 두 손을 풀어 조심스럽게 베개 위에 누이고는 어깨까지 이불을 덮어 주었다. 그녀는 순순히 누워 빛나는 눈으로 앞을 바라보았다.\n\n\"한 가지만 기억해 줘요, 나는 용서 외에는 아무것도 필요하지 않았고, 더 바라는 것도 없어요…… 왜 저이는 오지 않죠?\" 그녀는 브론스키 쪽으로, 문 쪽으로 돌아서며 말했다. \"이리 와요, 어서 와요! 이이에게 손을 줘요.\"\n\n브론스키는 침대 곁으로 다가왔으나, 안나를 보자 다시 두 손에 얼굴을 묻었다.\n\n\"얼굴에서 손을 떼고 이이를 봐요! 이이는 성자예요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"아! 얼굴을 드러내요, 어서 드러내요!\" 그녀가 화를 내며 말했다. \"알렉세이 알렉산드로비치, 저이의 얼굴을 드러내 줘요! 저이를 보고 싶어요.\"\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 브론스키의 두 손을 잡아 얼굴에서 떼어 냈다. 그 얼굴은 고통과 수치의 표정으로 처참했다.\n\n\"이이에게 손을 줘요. 이이를 용서해요.\"\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 두 눈에서 흘러내리는 눈물을 막으려 하지도 않은 채 그에게 손을 내밀었다.\n\n\"하느님 감사합니다, 하느님 감사합니다!\" 그녀가 말했다. \"이제 모든 게 준비됐어요. 다리만 좀 펴면 돼요. 자, 됐어, 아주 좋아. 이 꽃들은 정말 형편없이 만들었네, 제비꽃이랑은 조금도 안 닮았어.\" 그녀는 휘장을 가리키며 말했다. \"하느님, 하느님! 이게 언제 끝나려나? 모르핀을 줘요. 의사 선생님, 모르핀을 주세요! 아, 하느님, 하느님!\"\n\n그리고 그녀는 침대 위에서 몸부림쳤다.\n\n의사들은 산욕열이라며, 백 중 아흔아홉은 죽음으로 끝날 것이라고 했다. 온종일 열과 헛소리와 의식 불명이 이어졌다. 자정 무렵 환자는 의식도 없이, 맥박도 거의 없이 누워 있었다.\n\n이제나저제나 임종이 닥칠 것으로 예상되었다.\n\n브론스키는 집으로 돌아갔으나, 아침에 안부를 물으러 다시 왔고, 현관에서 그를 만난 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 \"그냥 계시는 게 낫겠습니다, 그녀가 당신을 찾을지도 모르니\" 하고는 직접 그를 아내의 내실로 데려갔다. 동틀 무렵, 다시 흥분과 빠른 생각과 말이 돌아왔고, 또다시 의식 불명으로 끝났다. 사흘째 되는 날도 똑같았으나, 의사들은 희망이 있다고 말했다. 그날 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 브론스키가 앉아 있는 내실로 들어가 문을 닫고 그의 맞은편에 앉았다.\n\n\"알렉세이 알렉산드로비치.\" 브론스키는 이제 처지에 대한 어떤 선언이 나오리라는 것을 느끼며 말했다. \"저는 말을 할 수도 없고, 이해할 수도 없습니다. 저를 가만 내버려 두십시오! 당신에게 아무리 힘든 일이라 한들, 믿어 주십시오, 저에게는 그것이 더 끔찍합니다.\"\n\n그는 일어서려 했으나, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 그의 손을 잡고 말했다.\n\n\"제 말을 끝까지 들어 주시기를 부탁드립니다. 그래야만 합니다. 저는 제 감정을, 저를 이끌어 온 그리고 앞으로도 이끌어 갈 그 감정을 설명해야겠습니다. 그래야 당신이 저에 대해 오해하지 않을 테니까요. 아시다시피 저는 이혼을 결심했고, 절차까지 밟기 시작했습니다. 숨기지 않겠습니다, 이 일을 시작할 때 저는 마음을 정하지 못하고 괴로워했습니다. 고백하건대 당신과 그녀에게 복수하고 싶은 마음에 사로잡혀 있었습니다. 전보를 받았을 때도 같은 심정으로 이리로 왔습니다. 더 말씀드리자면, 저는 그녀의 죽음을 바랐습니다. 그런데…….\" 그는 자신의 감정을 그에게 털어놓을지 말지 망설이며 말을 멈추었다. \"그런데 저는 그녀를 보았고, 그녀를 용서했습니다. 그리고 용서의 행복이 저에게 제 도리를 일깨워 주었습니다. 저는 완전히 용서합니다. 한쪽 뺨을 맞으면 다른 쪽 뺨도 내밀겠고, 겉옷을 빼앗기면 속옷까지 내주겠습니다. 저는 다만 하느님께 용서의 더없는 행복을 제게서 거두지 마시기를 기도할 따름입니다!\"\n\n그의 두 눈에는 눈물이 고여 있었고, 그 안에 어린 맑고 평온한 빛이 브론스키에게 깊은 인상을 남겼다.\n\n\"이것이 제 입장입니다. 당신은 저를 진창에 짓밟을 수도 있고, 세상의 웃음거리로 만들 수도 있겠지요. 그래도 저는 그녀를 버리지 않을 것이며, 당신에게 단 한 마디 비난의 말도 하지 않겠습니다.\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 말을 이었다. \"제 도리는 제게 분명히 정해져 있습니다. 저는 그녀 곁에 있어야 하고, 그렇게 할 것입니다. 그녀가 당신을 보고 싶어 하면 알려 드리겠습니다만, 지금은 당신이 떠나 주시는 편이 나을 것 같습니다.\"\n\n그는 일어섰고, 흐느낌이 그의 말을 끊었다. 브론스키도 일어서고 있었는데, 아직 똑바로 펴지 못한 구부정한 자세로 눈썹 아래에서 그를 올려다보았다. 그는 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 감정을 이해하지는 못했지만, 그것이 자신의 인생관으로는 다다를 수조차 없는 무언가 더 높은 것임을 느꼈다.\n\n# Chapter 18\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치와 이야기를 나눈 뒤, 브론스키는 카레닌 가의 현관 계단으로 나와 우뚝 멈춰 섰다. 자기가 어디에 있는지, 어디로 걸어가야 할지 마차를 타야 할지조차 가까스로 떠올리며. 그는 명예가 더럽혀지고, 모욕당하고, 죄지은 사람이 된 데다, 그 치욕을 씻어 낼 그 어떤 가능성마저 빼앗긴 듯한 기분이었다. 그는 그때까지 그토록 자랑스럽고 가뿐하게 걸어왔던 익숙한 길에서 밀려나 버린 것만 같았다. 그토록 견고하게만 보였던 그의 삶의 모든 습관과 규범이 별안간 거짓되고 쓸모없는 것으로 드러났다. 그때까지 가련한 존재이자 자기 행복에 우연히 끼어든 다소 우스꽝스러운 장애물로만 여겨졌던 배신당한 남편이, 다른 누구도 아닌 그녀 자신에 의해 별안간 불려 나와 경외감을 불러일으키는 높은 자리로 끌어올려졌고, 그 자리에서 그 남편은 악의에 차 있지도, 거짓되지도, 우스꽝스럽지도 않은, 친절하고 솔직하고 도량 넓은 사람으로 자신을 드러냈다. 브론스키는 이것을 느끼지 않을 수 없었으며, 두 사람의 역할이 별안간 뒤바뀌었다. 브론스키는 상대의 높음과 자기 자신의 비천함을, 상대의 진실과 자기 자신의 거짓을 느꼈다. 그는 그 남편이 슬픔 속에서도 관대한 데 반해, 자기는 기만 속에서 비열하고 옹졸했음을 느꼈다. 그러나 부당하게 멸시했던 그 사람 앞에서 느끼는 이 굴욕감은 그의 비참함의 작은 일부에 지나지 않았다. 그는 지금 말로 다 할 수 없을 만큼 비참했으니, 요즈음 들어 식어 가는 것만 같았던 안나를 향한 그의 정열이, 그녀를 영영 잃었다는 것을 알게 된 지금에 와서 그 어느 때보다 더 강렬해졌기 때문이다. 그는 그녀가 앓는 동안 그녀의 모든 것을 보았고, 그녀의 영혼까지 알게 되었으며, 그때까지 한 번도 그녀를 사랑한 적이 없었던 것만 같았다. 그런데 이제 그녀를 알게 되고, 마땅히 사랑해야 할 방식으로 그녀를 사랑하게 되었을 때, 그는 그녀 앞에서 모욕을 당했고, 그녀에게 부끄러운 기억 말고는 자기 자신의 그 무엇도 남기지 못한 채 그녀를 영영 잃어버린 것이다. 무엇보다 끔찍했던 것은 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 그의 굴욕에 찬 얼굴에서 그의 두 손을 떼어 냈을 때의 그 우스꽝스럽고 수치스러운 처지였다. 그는 넋이 나간 사람처럼 카레닌 가의 현관 계단에 서서, 무엇을 해야 할지 알지 못했다.\n\n\"썰매를 부를까요, 나리?\" 문지기가 물었다.\n\n\"그래, 썰매를.\"\n\n사흘 밤을 뜬눈으로 지새운 끝에 집에 돌아온 브론스키는 옷도 벗지 않은 채 소파 위에 반듯이 드러누워, 두 손을 깍지 껴 머리를 그 위에 얹었다. 머리가 무거웠다. 더없이 기이한 형상들과 기억들과 상념들이 놀라운 속도와 선명함으로 잇따라 떠올랐다. 처음에는 환자에게 따라 주다가 숟가락 위로 흘린 약이, 그다음에는 산파의 하얀 두 손이, 그다음에는 침대 옆 바닥에 엎드린 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 기묘한 자세가 떠올랐다.\n\n\"잠을 자자! 잊어버리자!\" 그는 건강한 사람 특유의, 피곤하고 졸리면 곧바로 잠들 수 있다는 차분한 확신을 품고 스스로에게 말했다. 그러자 그 순간 그의 머리가 정말로 나른해지기 시작했고, 그는 망각 속으로 빠져들기 시작했다. 무의식의 바다에서 일어난 물결이 그의 머리 위로 막 한데 모이려는 찰나, 별안간 마치 격렬한 전기 충격이 그의 몸을 훑고 지나가기라도 한 것 같았다. 그는 어찌나 놀랐던지 소파의 스프링 위에서 펄쩍 뛰어올라, 두 팔을 짚고 허둥지둥 무릎을 꿇은 자세가 되었다. 그의 두 눈은 마치 한 번도 잠든 적이 없었다는 듯 활짝 열려 있었다. 조금 전까지 느꼈던 머릿속의 무거움과 팔다리의 노곤함이 별안간 사라져 버렸다.\n\n\"나를 진창에 짓밟아도 좋소.\" 그는 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 말을 듣고, 그가 자기 앞에 서 있는 모습을 보았으며, 타오르는 듯한 홍조와 반짝이는 눈으로, 그가 아니라 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치를 사랑과 다정함으로 바라보는 안나의 얼굴을 보았다. 그는 또 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 그의 얼굴에서 두 손을 떼어 냈을 때, 그가 상상하기에 어리석고 우스꽝스러웠던 자기 자신의 모습을 보았다. 그는 다시 다리를 쭉 뻗고는 아까와 똑같은 자세로 소파에 몸을 던지고 눈을 감았다.\n\n\"잠을 자자! 잊어버리자!\" 그는 스스로에게 거듭 되뇌었다. 그러나 눈을 감으니, 경마가 있던 날 전날 저녁의, 그 잊을 수 없는 저녁의 안나의 얼굴이 그 어느 때보다 또렷하게 보였다.\n\n\"그건 지나간 일이고 다시는 없을 일이야, 그리고 그녀는 그걸 기억에서 지워 버리고 싶어 해. 하지만 나는 그것 없이는 살 수가 없어. 우리가 어떻게 화해할 수 있겠어? 어떻게 화해할 수 있단 말이야?\" 그는 소리 내어 말했고, 자기도 모르게 이 말을 되풀이하기 시작했다. 이렇게 되풀이하니, 머릿속으로 몰려드는 것이 느껴지던 새로운 형상들과 기억들이 솟구쳐 오르는 것이 멎었다. 그러나 말을 되풀이하는 것으로 그의 상상이 오래 멎어지지는 않았다. 다시 놀랍도록 빠르게 잇따라, 그의 가장 행복했던 순간들이 그의 마음 앞에 떠올랐고, 그다음에는 최근의 굴욕이 떠올랐다. \"그의 두 손을 떼어 내요.\" 안나의 목소리가 말한다. 그는 두 손을 떼어 내고, 수치심에 사로잡힌 자기 얼굴의 멍청한 표정을 느낀다.\n\n그는 잠들 가망이 조금도 없다는 것을 느끼면서도 여전히 누운 채 잠을 청하려 애썼고, 어떤 상념의 사슬에서 떨어져 나온 토막 난 말들을 거듭 되뇌며 그것으로 솟구쳐 오르는 새로운 형상들의 물결을 막아 보려 했다. 그는 귀를 기울였고, 기이하고 광기 어린 속삭임으로 거듭되는 말을 들었다. \"난 그걸 소중히 여기지 않았어, 충분히 누리지 못했어. 난 그걸 소중히 여기지 않았어, 충분히 누리지 못했어.\"\n\n\"이게 뭐지? 내가 미쳐 가는 건가?\" 그는 스스로에게 말했다. \"어쩌면 그런지도. 무엇이 사람을 미치게 하지, 무엇이 사람을 권총으로 자살하게 하지?\" 그는 스스로에게 답했고, 눈을 떴다가 자기 곁에 형수 바랴가 수놓아 만든 쿠션이 놓여 있는 것을 보고 놀랐다. 그는 쿠션의 술을 만지며 바랴를 떠올리려, 그녀를 마지막으로 본 게 언제였는지 떠올리려 애썼다. 그러나 다른 무언가를 생각한다는 것은 고통스러운 노력이었다. \"안 돼, 잠을 자야 해!\" 그는 쿠션을 끌어다 거기에 머리를 파묻었지만, 눈을 감고 있으려면 애를 써야만 했다. 그는 벌떡 일어나 앉았다. \"나에게는 모든 게 끝났어.\" 그는 스스로에게 말했다. \"어떻게 해야 할지 생각해야 해. 무엇이 남았지?\" 그의 마음은 안나에 대한 사랑을 빼고 자기 삶을 빠르게 훑어 내려갔다.\n\n\"공명심? 세르푸호프스코이? 사교계? 궁정?\" 그는 어디에서도 멈춰 설 수 없었다. 그 모든 것이 전에는 의미가 있었지만, 이제는 그 어떤 것에도 실체가 없었다. 그는 소파에서 일어나 외투를 벗고 허리띠를 풀고는, 더 자유롭게 숨을 쉬려고 털 난 가슴을 드러낸 채 방 안을 이리저리 거닐었다. \"사람들은 이렇게 미쳐 가는 거지.\" 그는 거듭 되뇌었다. \"그리고 이렇게 권총으로 자살하는 거야…… 치욕에서 벗어나려고.\" 그는 천천히 덧붙였다.\n\n그는 문가로 가서 문을 닫고는, 눈을 한곳에 고정한 채 이를 악물고 탁자로 다가가 권총을 집어 들고 주위를 둘러보더니, 총알이 든 약실로 돌려놓고 생각에 잠겼다. 두 시간 가까이—아니, 이 분 동안 그는 강렬하게 생각에 몰두하는 표정으로 머리를 앞으로 숙이고, 손에 권총을 든 채 꼼짝 않고 서서 생각에 잠겨 있었다.\n\n\"물론이지.\" 그는 마치 논리적이고 연속적이며 명료한 추론의 사슬이 자기를 의심할 여지 없는 결론으로 이끌기라도 한 것처럼 스스로에게 말했다. 실은 그에게 그토록 설득력 있게 여겨진 이 \"물론이지\"는, 지난 한 시간 동안 그가 이미 열 번이나 거쳐 온 바로 그 똑같은 기억과 형상의 순환—영영 잃어버린 행복에 대한 기억—이 낳은 결과에 지나지 않았다. 거기에는 앞으로 살아갈 삶의 모든 것이 무의미하다는 똑같은 생각이, 굴욕에 대한 똑같은 의식이 있었다. 심지어 이 형상들과 감정들의 순서마저 똑같았다.\n\n\"물론이지.\" 그의 생각이 세 번째로 다시 그 똑같은 주술에 걸린 기억과 형상의 순환을 한 바퀴 돌았을 때, 그는 거듭 되뇌었다. 그러고는 권총을 가슴 왼편으로 가져가, 마치 주먹 안에 그것을 꽉 쥐어 짜내려는 것처럼 온 손으로 힘껏 움켜쥐고 방아쇠를 당겼다. 그는 총소리를 듣지 못했지만, 가슴을 강타한 격렬한 충격에 비틀거렸다. 그는 탁자 가장자리를 붙잡으려다 권총을 떨어뜨리고는 휘청거리다가 바닥에 주저앉아, 어리둥절한 표정으로 주위를 둘러보았다. 그는 바닥에서 올려다보며 탁자의 굽은 다리며, 휴지통이며, 호랑이 가죽 깔개를 보면서도 자기 방을 알아보지 못했다. 응접실을 가로질러 다가오는 하인의 다급하고 삐걱거리는 발소리가 그를 제정신으로 돌아오게 했다. 그는 애써 생각해 보았고, 자기가 바닥에 쓰러져 있다는 것을 깨달았다. 그리고 호랑이 가죽 깔개와 자기 팔 위에 묻은 피를 보고는, 자기가 권총으로 자살을 기도했다는 것을 알았다.\n\n\"멍청하긴! 빗나갔어!\" 그는 권총을 더듬어 찾으며 말했다. 권총은 바로 그의 곁에 있었지만, 그는 더 먼 곳을 뒤졌다. 여전히 그것을 더듬으며 그는 반대편으로 손을 뻗었고, 균형을 잡을 만큼 힘이 남아 있지 않아 피를 줄줄 흘리며 옆으로 쓰러졌다.\n\n평소 아는 사람들에게 자기 신경이 예민하다고 끊임없이 하소연하던 멋쟁이 구레나룻 하인은, 주인이 바닥에 쓰러져 있는 것을 보고 어찌나 공포에 사로잡혔던지, 피를 흘리는 그를 내버려 둔 채 도움을 청하러 뛰어갔다. 한 시간 뒤 형수 바랴가 도착했고, 그녀가 사방으로 사람을 보내 부른, 모두 동시에 나타난 세 명의 의사의 도움을 받아 부상당한 그를 침대에 눕히고, 곁에 남아 그를 간호했다.\n\n# Chapter 19\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 아내를 만날 준비를 하면서 그녀의 뉘우침이 진실할 수도 있고, 자신이 그녀를 용서할 수도 있으며, 그녀가 죽지 않을 수도 있다는 가능성을 간과하는 실수를 저질렀는데, 그 실수가 지닌 의미는 모스크바에서 돌아온 지 두 달이 지나서야 비로소 그에게 온전히 와닿았다. 그러나 그가 저지른 실수는 단지 그 가능성을 간과한 데서 비롯된 것만이 아니라, 죽어 가는 아내를 만나던 그날까지 자기 자신의 마음을 알지 못했다는 사실에서 비롯된 것이기도 했다. 병든 아내의 머리맡에서 그는 생애 처음으로, 늘 다른 사람의 고통이 그의 안에서 불러일으켰으나 지금껏 해로운 약점이라 여기며 부끄러워하던 그 동정의 괴로움에 자신을 내맡겼다. 그리고 아내에 대한 연민과, 그녀가 죽기를 바랐던 데 대한 자책과, 무엇보다도 용서의 기쁨이, 자기 고통의 완화뿐 아니라 이제껏 한 번도 경험해 보지 못한 영적 평화를 그에게 단번에 깨닫게 했다. 그는 자기 고통의 원천이었던 바로 그것이 자기 영적 기쁨의 원천이 되었다는 것을, 자신이 심판하고 비난하고 미워하는 동안에는 풀 수 없을 것처럼 보였던 것이 용서하고 사랑하자 명료하고 단순해졌다는 것을 문득 느꼈다.\n\n그는 아내를 용서하고 그녀의 고통과 뉘우침을 가엾게 여겼다. 그는 브론스키를 용서했고, 특히 그의 절망적인 행동에 대한 소식이 그에게 전해진 뒤로는 그를 가엾게 여겼다. 그는 아들에게도 전보다 더 깊은 정을 느꼈다. 그리고 이제는 아들에게 너무 관심을 두지 않았던 자신을 책망했다. 그러나 갓 태어난 작은 아기에 대해서는 연민뿐 아니라 애틋함까지 깃든, 사뭇 독특한 감정을 느꼈다. 처음에는 오직 측은한 마음에서, 자기 아이도 아니고 어미의 병중에 한쪽으로 내팽개쳐졌으며 그가 마음을 쓰지 않았다면 틀림없이 죽고 말았을 그 가냘픈 작은 생명에게 관심을 가졌는데, 자신이 그 아이를 얼마나 좋아하게 되었는지는 스스로도 알아차리지 못했다. 그는 하루에도 몇 번씩 육아실에 들어가 오래도록 거기 앉아 있곤 했고, 그래서 처음에는 그를 두려워하던 유모들도 그가 있는 데에 아주 익숙해졌다. 때로는 내리 반 시간씩 잠든 아기의 사프란빛이 도는 붉고 솜털 보송한 주름진 얼굴을 말없이 들여다보며, 찌푸려진 눈썹의 움직임이며 작은 눈과 코를 비비는, 손가락을 꼭 오므린 통통한 작은 손을 지켜보곤 했다. 특히 그런 순간에 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 완전한 평화와 내면의 조화를 느꼈고, 자기 처지에서 비범한 것이라곤, 바꿔야 할 것이라곤 아무것도 보지 못했다.\n\n그러나 시간이 흐를수록, 지금의 처지가 자기에게 아무리 자연스럽게 보일지라도 자신이 그 안에 오래 머무는 것은 허락되지 않으리라는 것을 점점 더 또렷이 깨달았다. 그는 자기 영혼을 다스리는 축복받은 영적인 힘 외에, 또 다른 힘이, 그만큼 강하거나 그보다 더 강한 잔혹한 힘이 자기 삶을 지배하고 있으며, 그 힘은 그가 갈망하는 그 겸허한 평화를 그에게 허락하지 않으리라는 것을 느꼈다. 그는 모두가 캐묻는 듯한 의아함으로 자신을 바라보고 있다는 것을, 사람들이 자기를 이해하지 못하며 자기에게 무언가를 기대하고 있다는 것을 느꼈다. 무엇보다도 그는 아내와의 관계가 불안정하고 부자연스럽다는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n죽음이 임박하면서 마음을 누그러뜨렸던 영향이 사라지자, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 안나가 자기를 두려워하고, 자기 앞에서 거북해하며, 자기 얼굴을 똑바로 쳐다보지 못한다는 것을 알아차리기 시작했다. 그녀는 그에게 무언가 말하고 싶어 하면서도 차마 그러지 못하는 것 같았다. 그리고 지금의 관계가 계속될 수 없으리라는 것을 예감하기라도 하듯, 그에게서 무언가를 기다리고 있는 것 같았다.\n\n2월 말 무렵, 역시 안나라는 이름이 붙은 안나의 어린 딸이 병에 걸리는 일이 있었다. 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 아침에 육아실에 들렀고, 의사를 부르라고 일러둔 뒤 관청으로 갔다. 일을 마치고 그는 네 시에 집으로 돌아왔다. 현관으로 들어서던 그는 장식 줄이 달린 제복에 곰털 망토를 두른 잘생긴 마부가 흰 모피 외투를 들고 서 있는 것을 보았다.\n\n\"누가 와 있나?\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 물었다.\n\n\"옐리자베타 표도로브나 트베르스카야 공작 부인이십니다.\" 마부가 대답했는데, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에게는 그가 히죽 웃은 것처럼 보였다.\n\n이 힘겨운 시기 내내 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 사교계의 지인들, 특히 여자들이 자신과 아내에게 묘한 관심을 보인다는 것을 알아차렸다. 그는 이 모든 지인들이 무언가를 두고 즐거워하는 기색을 애써 감추는 것을 지켜보았는데, 그것은 변호사의 눈에서 보았던, 그리고 방금 이 마부의 눈에서 본 것과 똑같은 즐거움이었다. 모두가 어쩐지 결혼식에라도 다녀온 것처럼 더없이 들떠 있는 듯했다. 그를 만나면 사람들은 잘 감추지 못한 흥겨움으로 아내의 안부를 물었다. 트베르스카야 공작 부인이 와 있다는 것은 그녀와 얽힌 기억 때문에, 또 그가 그녀를 싫어하기 때문에 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에게는 불쾌한 일이었고, 그래서 그는 곧장 육아실로 갔다. 낮 육아실에서는 세료자가 다리를 의자에 올려놓고 탁자에 기댄 채 그림을 그리며 즐겁게 재잘대고 있었다. 안나가 앓는 동안 프랑스인 가정교사를 대신하게 된 영국인 가정교사가 아이 곁에 앉아 숄을 뜨고 있었다. 그녀는 황급히 일어나 무릎을 굽혀 인사하고는 세료자를 끌어당겼다.\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 아들의 머리를 쓰다듬고, 아내의 안부를 묻는 가정교사의 물음에 답한 뒤, 의사가 아기에 대해 뭐라고 했는지 물었다.\n\n\"의사 선생님께서는 심각한 게 아니라 하시면서 목욕을 시키라고 하셨습니다, 나리.\"\n\n\"하지만 아직 아파하고 있는데.\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 옆방에서 들려오는 아기의 울음소리에 귀를 기울이며 말했다.\n\n\"제 생각엔 유모 탓인 것 같습니다, 나리.\" 영국 여자가 단호하게 말했다.\n\n\"어째서 그렇게 생각하지?\" 그가 걸음을 멈추며 물었다.\n\n\"파울 백작 부인 댁에서도 꼭 이랬거든요, 나리. 아기에게 약을 먹였는데, 알고 보니 아기가 그저 배가 고팠던 거였어요. 유모에게 젖이 없었던 거죠, 나리.\"\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 생각에 잠겼고, 몇 초 동안 가만히 서 있다가 다른 쪽 문으로 들어갔다. 아기는 머리를 뒤로 젖히고 유모의 팔 안에서 몸을 뻣뻣하게 곧추세운 채, 내미는 통통한 젖을 물려 하지 않았다. 그리고 유모와 그 위로 몸을 굽힌 또 다른 보모가 함께 어르며 달래는데도 울음을 그치지 않았다.\n\n\"아직도 나아지지 않았나?\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 말했다.\n\n\"몹시 보채는군요.\" 보모가 속삭이듯 대답했다.\n\n\"에드워드 양 말로는 유모에게 젖이 없는지도 모른다던데.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"저도 그렇게 생각합니다, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치.\"\n\n\"그럼 왜 진작 말하지 않았나?\"\n\n\"누구한테 말씀드리겠어요? 안나 아르카지예브나께서는 아직도 편찮으신데……\" 보모가 못마땅한 듯이 말했다.\n\n보모는 이 집안의 오랜 하인이었다. 그리고 그녀의 소박한 말 속에서 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 자기 처지를 빗댄 암시를 느꼈다.\n\n아기는 발버둥 치며 흐느끼더니 그 어느 때보다 더 크게 울어 댔다. 보모는 절망스러운 몸짓으로 아기에게 다가가 유모의 팔에서 받아 안고는 어르며 이리저리 걷기 시작했다.\n\n\"의사한테 유모를 진찰해 보라고 해야겠군.\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 말했다. 말쑥하게 차려입은 건강해 보이는 유모는 일자리를 잃을지 모른다는 생각에 겁을 먹고 무어라 혼잣말로 중얼거리더니, 가슴을 가리고는 자기 젖이 넘치도록 풍부하다는 것을 의심받는다는 데에 경멸스럽다는 듯 웃었다. 그 웃음에서도 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 자기 처지에 대한 비웃음을 보았다.\n\n\"가엾은 것!\" 유모는 아기를 어르며, 여전히 아기를 안고 이리저리 걸으며 말했다.\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 자리에 앉아, 낙담하고 고통스러운 얼굴로 보모가 왔다 갔다 하는 모습을 지켜보았다.\n\n마침내 아기가 잠잠해지고 푹신한 침대에 뉘어졌으며, 보모가 작은 베개를 매만진 뒤 그 곁을 떠나자, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 일어나 어색하게 발끝으로 걸어 아기에게 다가갔다. 한동안 그는 가만히 서서 여전히 낙담한 얼굴로 아기를 들여다보았다. 그러나 별안간, 머리카락과 이마의 살갗을 움직이는 미소가 그의 얼굴에 떠올랐고, 그는 발소리를 죽이며 방을 나섰다.\n\n식당에서 그는 종을 울려, 들어온 하인에게 다시 의사를 부르러 보내라고 일렀다. 그는 이 사랑스러운 아기를 두고 마음 졸이지 않는 아내가 못마땅했고, 이렇게 못마땅한 기분에 그녀에게 가고 싶지 않았다. 베치 공작 부인을 마주하고 싶지도 않았다. 그러나 아내가 자기가 평소처럼 들르지 않는 것을 의아하게 여길지도 몰랐다. 그래서 그는 내키지 않는 마음을 누르고 침실 쪽으로 갔다. 부드러운 양탄자를 밟으며 문 쪽으로 다가가던 그는, 듣고 싶지 않은 대화를 본의 아니게 엿듣고 말았다.\n\n\"그가 떠나지 않는다면 당신 대답도, 그의 대답도 이해할 수 있어요. 하지만 당신 남편이라면 그쯤은 초연해야지요.\" 베치가 말하고 있었다.\n\n\"내 남편을 위해서가 아니에요. 나 자신을 위해 그걸 원하지 않는 거예요. 그런 말 하지 말아요!\" 안나의 흥분한 목소리가 대답했다.\n\n\"하지만 당신 때문에 스스로 총을 쏜 남자에게 작별 인사쯤은 하고 싶을 텐데요……\"\n\n\"바로 그래서 그러고 싶지 않은 거예요.\"\n\n당황하고 죄스러운 표정으로 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 걸음을 멈추었고, 눈에 띄지 않게 되돌아가려 했다. 그러나 그것이 품위 없는 짓이라는 데에 생각이 미치자, 다시 발길을 돌려 헛기침을 하고는 침실로 들어갔다. 목소리들이 잦아들었고, 그는 안으로 들어섰다.\n\n안나는 회색 실내복 차림으로, 둥근 머리에 짧게 무리 지어 곱슬거리는 검은 머리카락을 한 채 긴 의자에 앉아 있었다. 늘 그렇듯 남편을 보자 그녀의 얼굴에서 생기가 사라졌다. 그녀는 고개를 떨구고 불안하게 베치를 돌아보았다. 최신 유행의 절정을 따라 차려입은 베치는 어쩐지 머리 위로 등갓처럼 우뚝 솟은 모자를 쓰고, 몸통 부분에는 한 방향으로, 치마 부분에는 다른 방향으로 비스듬히 보라색 줄무늬가 가로지른 푸른 드레스를 입은 채, 크고 납작한 몸을 꼿꼿이 세우고 안나 곁에 앉아 있었다. 고개를 숙이며 그녀는 빈정대는 미소로 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치를 맞았다.\n\n\"어머!\" 그녀는 놀란 듯이 말했다. \"집에 계시다니 정말 반갑네요. 어디에도 통 모습을 비치지 않으시니, 안나가 앓기 시작한 뒤로는 한 번도 뵙질 못했어요. 다 들었답니다, 당신이 얼마나 마음 졸였는지를요. 그래요, 당신은 훌륭한 남편이세요!\" 그녀는 마치 아내를 대하는 그의 처신에 너그러움의 훈장이라도 수여하듯, 의미심장하면서도 상냥한 태도로 말했다.\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 차갑게 고개를 숙이고, 아내의 손에 입을 맞춘 뒤 몸은 좀 어떠냐고 물었다.\n\n\"나아진 것 같아요.\" 그녀는 그의 눈길을 피하며 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 안색이 좀 열이 있어 보이는군.\" 그는 '열이 있다'는 말에 힘주어 말했다.\n\n\"우리가 얘기를 너무 많이 했나 봐요.\" 베치가 말했다. \"제 이기심 탓인 것 같으니, 이만 가 볼게요.\"\n\n그녀가 일어섰지만, 안나는 별안간 얼굴을 붉히며 재빨리 그녀의 손을 붙잡았다.\n\n\"아니, 잠깐만 있어 줘요, 제발. 당신께 말씀드려야 할 게 있어요…… 아니, 당신께요.\" 그녀는 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치를 향해 돌아섰고, 목과 이마가 새빨갛게 물들었다. \"당신께는 아무것도 숨기지 않을 거고, 숨길 수도 없어요.\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 손가락을 우두둑 꺾고 고개를 숙였다.\n\n\"베치 말로는 브론스키 백작이 타슈켄트로 떠나기 전에 작별 인사를 하러 여기 오고 싶어 한다는군요.\" 그녀는 남편을 쳐다보지 않았고, 자기에게 아무리 힘든 일일지라도 모든 것을 다 털어놓으려고 서두르는 게 분명했다. \"나는 그를 맞을 수 없다고 했어요.\"\n\n\"그건 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치께 달려 있다고 하셨잖아요.\" 베치가 그녀의 말을 바로잡았다.\n\n\"아니에요, 나는 그를 맞을 수 없어요. 게다가 그게 무슨 소용이……\" 그녀는 갑자기 말을 멈추고 캐묻는 듯이 남편을 흘끗 보았다(그는 그녀를 쳐다보지 않았다). \"한마디로, 나는 그걸 원하지 않아요……\"\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 다가가 그녀의 손을 잡으려 했다.\n\n그녀의 첫 충동은 굵게 부풀어 오른 핏줄이 도드라진 축축한 손이 자기 손을 찾아오자 그것을 홱 빼는 것이었지만, 그녀는 애써 자제하며 그의 손을 꼭 쥐었다.\n\n\"당신이 이렇게 믿고 말해 주니 정말 고맙소, 하지만……\" 그는 혼자서라면 쉽고 명료하게 결정할 수 있는 일을 트베르스카야 공작 부인 앞에서는 의논할 수 없다는 것을 당혹스럽고도 짜증스럽게 느끼며 말했다. 그에게 그녀는, 세상의 눈앞에서 영위하는 삶 속에서 자신을 필연적으로 지배하며, 사랑과 용서의 감정에 자신을 내맡기지 못하게 가로막는 그 잔혹한 힘의 화신이었던 것이다. 그는 트베르스카야 공작 부인을 바라보며 말을 멈추었다.\n\n\"그럼, 잘 있어요, 내 사랑.\" 베치가 일어서며 말했다. 그녀는 안나에게 입을 맞추고 밖으로 나갔다. 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 그녀를 배웅했다.\n\n\"알렉세이 알렉산드로비치! 저는 당신이 진정으로 너그러운 분이라는 걸 알아요.\" 베치는 작은 응접실에서 걸음을 멈추고, 유난히 따뜻하게 그와 다시 한번 악수하며 말했다. \"저는 남이지만, 그녀를 무척 사랑하고 당신을 존경하기에 감히 충고드리는 거예요. 그를 맞아 주세요. 알렉세이 브론스키는 더없이 명예로운 사람이고, 타슈켄트로 떠난답니다.\"\n\n\"공작 부인, 호의와 충고에 감사드립니다. 하지만 제 아내가 누구를 만날 수 있느냐 없느냐 하는 문제는 그녀 자신이 결정해야 합니다.\"\n\n그는 위엄 있게 눈썹을 치켜올리며 습관처럼 이렇게 말했지만, 곧바로 자기 말이 어떻든 간에 자기 처지에는 위엄이라곤 있을 수 없다는 데에 생각이 미쳤다. 그리고 그는 이 말이 끝난 뒤 베치가 자신을 흘끗 보며 지은, 억눌린 짓궂고 빈정대는 미소에서 그것을 알아차렸다.\n\n# Chapter 20\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 응접실에서 베시와 작별 인사를 나누고 아내에게로 갔다. 그녀는 누워 있었지만 그의 발걸음 소리를 듣고는 아까의 자세로 황급히 몸을 일으켜 앉으며 겁먹은 듯 그를 바라보았다. 그는 그녀가 울고 있었다는 것을 알아챘다.\n\n\"나에 대한 당신의 신뢰에 진심으로 감사하오.\" 그는 베시 앞에서 프랑스어로 했던 말을 러시아어로 부드럽게 되풀이하고는 그녀 곁에 앉았다. 그가 러시아어로, 그것도 친밀함과 애정을 담은 러시아어의 '너'를 쓰며 말을 건넬 때면, 안나는 견딜 수 없이 짜증이 났다. \"그리고 당신의 결정에도 진심으로 감사하오. 나 역시 그가 떠나는 마당에 브론스키 백작이 이리로 올 필요 따위는 전혀 없으리라 생각하오. 하지만, 만약……\"\n\n\"하지만 그건 이미 말했잖아요. 그런데 왜 또 되풀이하시는 거예요?\" 안나는 도무지 억누를 수 없는 짜증을 드러내며 불쑥 그의 말을 가로막았다. '필요 따위는 전혀 없다니.' 그녀는 생각했다. '사랑하는 여자, 그를 위해 자기 자신을 망칠 각오까지 했고 실제로 망쳐 버린 여자, 그가 없이는 살 수도 없는 여자에게 작별을 고하러 오는 데에 아무런 필요도 없다니. 필요 따위는 전혀 없다니!' 그녀는 입술을 꼭 다물고, 핏줄이 불거진 그의 손으로 타오르는 듯한 눈길을 떨어뜨렸다. 그 두 손은 서로를 비비고 있었다.\n\n\"우리 이 일에 대해서는 두 번 다시 말하지 말아요.\" 그녀는 좀 더 차분하게 덧붙였다.\n\n\"이 문제는 당신이 결정하도록 맡겼고, 또 이렇게 보게 되어 무척 기쁘오……\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 입을 떼기 시작했다.\n\n\"제 바람이 당신 바람과 일치한다는 것을 말이죠.\" 그녀는 그가 무슨 말을 할지 미리 다 알면서도 그토록 느릿느릿 말하는 것에 부아가 치밀어 얼른 말을 맺어 버렸다.\n\n\"그렇소.\" 그가 수긍했다. \"그리고 트베르스카야 공작 부인이 이토록 까다로운 사사로운 일에 끼어드는 것은 전혀 당치 않은 일이오. 특히 그 여자는……\"\n\n\"그 사람에 대해 사람들이 하는 말은 한마디도 믿지 않아요.\" 안나가 얼른 말했다. \"그 사람이 진심으로 저를 아낀다는 걸 저는 알아요.\"\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 한숨을 쉬고 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 그녀는 신경질적으로 실내복의 술을 만지작거리며, 스스로도 어쩌지 못해 자책하면서도 어쩔 수 없는 그 육체적 혐오의 괴로운 감각에 사로잡힌 채 그를 흘끔거렸다. 지금 그녀의 유일한 바람은 그의 짓누르는 듯한 존재로부터 벗어나는 것뿐이었다.\n\n\"방금 의사를 부르러 사람을 보냈소.\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 말했다.\n\n\"저는 멀쩡해요. 의사가 왜 필요하죠?\"\n\n\"아니, 아기가 우는데, 유모의 젖이 모자란다고들 하더군.\"\n\n\"제가 그렇게 사정했을 때 왜 직접 젖을 먹이지 못하게 하셨어요? 어쨌든\" (알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 그 '어쨌든'이 무엇을 뜻하는지 알고 있었다.) \"저 애는 갓난아이인데, 사람들이 저 애를 죽이고 있는 거예요.\" 그녀는 종을 울려 아기를 데려오라고 일렀다. \"제가 젖을 먹이게 해 달라고 빌었지만 허락받지 못했고, 그런데 이제 와서 그것 때문에 비난을 받는군요.\"\n\n\"나는 비난하는 게 아니라……\"\n\n\"아니에요, 당신은 저를 비난하고 있어요! 오, 하느님! 저는 왜 죽지 않았을까요!\" 그러고서 그녀는 흐느껴 울기 시작했다. \"용서하세요, 제가 신경이 곤두서서, 부당하게 굴고 있어요.\" 그녀가 마음을 다잡으며 말했다. \"그러니 제발 나가 주세요……\"\n\n\"안 되겠어, 이대로 계속될 수는 없어.\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 아내의 방을 나서며 단호하게 혼잣말을 했다.\n\n세상의 눈에 비친 자기 처지의 가망 없음과, 자기를 향한 아내의 증오와, 무엇보다 자기의 정신적 성향을 거스르며 자기 삶을 끌고 가면서 그 명령에 순응할 것과 아내를 대하는 태도를 바꿀 것을 강요하는 그 알 수 없는 잔혹한 힘의 위력이, 그날만큼 또렷하게 그에게 다가온 적은 없었다. 그는 온 세상과 자기 아내가 자기에게서 무언가를 기대하고 있음을 분명히 알았지만, 그것이 정확히 무엇인지는 도무지 헤아릴 수가 없었다. 그는 이것이 자기 영혼 속에, 마음의 평화와 자기가 이룬 모든 좋은 것을 파괴하는 분노의 감정을 불러일으키고 있음을 느꼈다. 그는 안나 자신을 위해서도 브론스키와의 관계를 모조리 끊는 편이 더 나으리라고 믿었다. 하지만 사람들 모두가 그것을 도저히 있을 수 없는 일이라 여긴다면, 아이들이 수치를 당하지 않고, 자기가 아이들을 빼앗기거나 자기 처지를 바꾸도록 강요당하지만 않는다면, 그는 그 관계가 다시 이어지도록 내버려 둘 각오마저 되어 있었다. 그것이 아무리 나쁘다 한들, 어쨌든 파국보다는 나았다. 파국은 그녀를 가망도 없고 수치스러운 처지에 빠뜨리고, 그에게서 그가 소중히 여기는 모든 것을 앗아 갈 터였다. 그러나 그는 무력함을 느꼈다. 그는 모두가 자기에게 맞서고 있다는 것을, 그리고 지금 자기에게 그토록 자연스럽고 옳게 여겨지는 일을 하도록 허락받지 못하고, 도리어 잘못된 일을, 그러나 그들에게는 마땅해 보이는 그 일을 하도록 강요당하리라는 것을 미리부터 알고 있었다.\n\n# Chapter 21\n\n베치가 미처 응접실에서 나서기도 전에, 그녀는 문간에서 스테판 아르카지치와 마주쳤다. 그는 막 옐리세예프 상점에서 오는 길이었는데, 거기에 신선한 굴 한 상자가 들어와 있었던 것이다.\n\n\"아! 공작 부인! 이렇게 반가운 만남이라니!\" 그가 운을 뗐다. \"마침 부인을 뵈러 가던 참이었어요.\"\n\n\"잠깐 동안만 뵐 수 있겠네요, 막 나가려던 참이라서요.\" 베치가 미소를 지으며 장갑을 끼면서 말했다.\n\n\"아직 장갑은 끼지 마세요, 공작 부인. 손에 입 맞추게 해 주세요. 옛 풍습이 되살아난 것 중에 손에 입 맞추는 것만큼 고마운 게 없답니다.\" 그는 베치의 손에 입을 맞췄다. \"언제 또 뵐 수 있을까요?\"\n\n\"그럴 자격이 없으신데요.\" 베치가 미소 지으며 대꾸했다.\n\n\"오, 아니죠, 저는 자격이 충분합니다. 아주 진지한 사람이 되었거든요. 제 일만 처리하는 게 아니라 남의 일까지 챙기고 있답니다.\" 그가 의미심장한 표정으로 말했다.\n\n\"오, 정말 기뻐요!\" 베치는 그가 안나 이야기를 하고 있다는 것을 곧바로 알아채고 대답했다. 그러고는 두 사람은 응접실로 다시 들어가 한쪽 구석에 섰다. \"그이가 그녀를 죽이고 있어요.\" 베치가 의미심장하게 가득 찬 목소리로 속삭였다. \"이건 안 될 일이에요, 안 될 일이라고요…….\"\n\n\"그렇게 생각해 주시니 정말 기쁩니다.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 진지하고 동정 어린 괴로운 표정으로 고개를 저으며 말했다. \"바로 그 일 때문에 페테르부르크에 온 거니까요.\"\n\n\"온 도시가 그 이야기뿐이에요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"있을 수 없는 처지예요. 그녀는 나날이 시들어 가고 있어요. 그이는 그녀가 자기 감정을 가지고 장난칠 수 없는 그런 여자라는 걸 이해하지 못해요. 둘 중 하나죠. 그이가 그녀를 데리고 떠나든가, 단호하게 행동하든가, 아니면 이혼을 해 주든가. 이러다간 그녀가 숨이 막혀 죽고 말 거예요.\"\n\n\"그래요, 그래요…… 바로 그겁니다…….\" 오블론스키가 한숨을 쉬며 말했다. \"그것 때문에 온 거예요. 적어도 그것만을 위해서 온 건 아니지만요…… 시종보로 임명되었거든요. 물론 감사 인사를 하러 가야지요. 하지만 가장 중요한 건 이 일을 매듭짓는 거였습니다.\"\n\n\"그럼, 부디 잘되시길 빌어요!\" 베치가 말했다.\n\n스테판 아르카지치는 베치를 현관까지 배웅하면서 다시 한번 장갑 위쪽, 맥박이 뛰는 자리에 입을 맞추고, 그녀가 웃어야 할지 화를 내야 할지 모를 만큼 점잖지 못한 농지거리를 속삭인 다음, 누이에게로 갔다. 그는 그녀가 눈물에 젖어 있는 것을 보았다.\n\n비록 기분이 한껏 들떠 있었지만, 스테판 아르카지치는 곧장 아주 자연스럽게 그녀의 기분에 어울리는, 동정 어리고 시적으로 감상적인 어조로 빠져들었다. 그는 그녀에게 어떻게 지내는지, 아침나절을 어떻게 보냈는지 물었다.\n\n\"아주, 아주 비참했어요. 오늘도, 오늘 아침도, 지나간 모든 날도, 앞으로 다가올 모든 날도요.\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"내가 보기엔 네가 비관에 빠져 있는 것 같구나. 기운을 차려야지, 인생을 똑바로 바라봐야 해. 힘든 건 알지만…….\"\n\n\"여자들은 남자의 결점까지도 사랑한다는 말을 들은 적이 있어요.\" 안나가 불쑥 입을 열었다. \"하지만 저는 그이의 미덕 때문에 그이가 미워요. 그이와는 같이 살 수 없어요. 아시겠어요? 그이를 보기만 해도 몸에 반응이 일어나요, 제정신이 아니게 돼요. 그이와는 살 수 없어요, 살 수 없다고요. 어떻게 해야 하죠? 저는 불행했어요, 그리고 이보다 더 불행할 수는 없으리라고 생각하곤 했어요. 그런데 지금 제가 겪고 있는 이 끔찍한 처지는 도저히 상상조차 할 수 없는 거였어요. 믿어지세요? 그이가 좋은 사람이라는 걸, 훌륭한 사람이라는 걸, 제가 그이의 새끼손가락만큼의 가치도 없다는 걸 알면서도, 그래도 그이가 미워요. 그이의 너그러움 때문에 그이가 미운 거예요. 그러니 제게 남은 건 오직…….\"\n\n그녀는 죽음이라고 말하려 했지만, 스테판 아르카지치는 그 말을 끝맺도록 내버려 두지 않았다.\n\n\"넌 병들어 있고 신경이 곤두서 있어.\" 그가 말했다. \"내 말 믿으렴, 넌 지금 끔찍하게 과장하고 있어. 그렇게까지 무서운 일은 아니야.\"\n\n그러면서 스테판 아르카지치는 미소를 지었다. 그런 절망을 마주한 처지에서 스테판 아르카지치 자리에 있었다면 누구도 감히 미소 지을 엄두를 내지 못했을 것이다(그 미소는 잔인하게 보였을 테니까). 하지만 그의 미소에는 더없이 다정하고 거의 여성적이라 할 만한 부드러움이 가득 담겨 있어서, 그 미소는 상처를 주기는커녕 마음을 누그러뜨리고 가라앉혀 주었다. 그의 부드럽고 위로하는 말과 미소는 아몬드 기름처럼 사람을 진정시키고 누그러뜨려 주었다. 안나도 곧 그것을 느꼈다.\n\n\"아니에요, 스티바.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"저는 끝장났어요, 끝장났다고요! 끝장난 것보다 더 나빠요! 아직 모든 게 끝났다고 말할 수는 없어요. 오히려 끝나지 않았다는 게 느껴져요. 저는 끊어질 수밖에 없는 팽팽하게 당겨진 줄이에요. 하지만 아직 끝나지 않았어요…… 그리고 무서운 결말을 맞게 될 거예요.\"\n\n\"상관없어, 줄을 조금씩, 조금씩 느슨하게 풀어 주면 되는 거야. 빠져나갈 길이 없는 처지란 없는 법이니까.\"\n\n\"생각하고, 또 생각했어요. 오직 하나뿐이에요…….\"\n\n그녀의 겁에 질린 눈빛에서, 그는 그녀가 생각하는 그 하나뿐인 탈출구가 죽음이라는 것을 다시금 알아챘고, 그녀가 그 말을 하도록 내버려 두지 않았다.\n\n\"전혀 아니야.\" 그가 말했다. \"내 말 들어 봐. 넌 네 처지를 나만큼 볼 수가 없어. 내 솔직한 생각을 말해 주마.\" 그는 다시 신중하게 아몬드 기름 같은 미소를 지었다. \"처음부터 이야기를 시작하지. 넌 너보다 스무 살이나 많은 남자와 결혼했어. 사랑 없이, 사랑이 뭔지도 모른 채 결혼한 거야. 그게 잘못이었다고, 그건 인정하자.\"\n\n\"끔찍한 잘못이었죠!\" 안나가 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 거듭 말하건대, 그건 이미 끝난 일이야. 그러고 나서, 말하자면, 넌 남편이 아닌 남자를 사랑하는 불행을 겪었어. 그건 불행이었지. 하지만 그것 역시 이미 끝난 일이야. 그리고 네 남편은 그걸 알면서도 용서했어.\" 그는 그녀가 반박하기를 기다리며 한 문장마다 말을 멈추었지만, 그녀는 아무 대답도 하지 않았다. \"바로 그거야. 이제 문제는 이거지. 넌 네 남편과 계속 살아갈 수 있겠니? 네가 그걸 바라니? 그이가 그걸 바라고?\"\n\n\"저는 아무것도 몰라요, 아무것도요.\"\n\n\"하지만 그이를 견딜 수 없다고 네 입으로 말했잖니.\"\n\n\"아니에요, 그렇게 말하지 않았어요. 그 말은 부정하겠어요. 모르겠어요, 저는 그것에 대해 아무것도 모르겠어요.\"\n\n\"그래, 하지만 어쨌든…….\"\n\n\"오빠는 이해 못 해요. 저는 마치 어떤 구덩이 속에 머리를 거꾸로 처박고 있는 기분이에요, 그런데도 저 자신을 구해서는 안 될 것 같아요. 그리고 그럴 수도 없고요…….\"\n\n\"걱정 마라, 우리가 밑에 뭔가를 받쳐 너를 끌어 올려 줄 테니까. 난 너를 이해해. 네가 네 바람을, 네 감정을 스스로 입 밖에 낼 수 없다는 걸 이해한다고.\"\n\n\"바라는 건 아무것도 없어요, 아무것도요…… 그저 모든 게 끝나기만을 바랄 뿐이에요.\"\n\n\"하지만 그이도 이걸 보고 있고 알고 있어. 그리고 이게 너한테보다 그이한테 덜 무겁게 지워진다고 생각하니? 너도 비참하고, 그이도 비참한데, 거기서 무슨 좋은 게 나올 수 있겠어? 반면에 이혼은 그 어려움을 완전히 해결해 줄 거야.\" 스테판 아르카지치는 다소 애를 쓰며 자신의 핵심 생각을 꺼내 놓고, 의미심장하게 그녀를 바라보았다.\n\n그녀는 아무 말도 하지 않고, 짧게 깎은 머리를 저어 반대의 뜻을 나타냈다. 하지만 그녀의 얼굴 표정에서, 갑자기 예전의 아름다움으로 환하게 빛난 그 표정에서, 그는 그녀가 이것을 바라지 않는 것이 단지 그것이 이룰 수 없는 행복처럼 여겨지기 때문임을 알아차렸다.\n\n\"네가 너무나 안쓰럽구나! 그리고 내가 일을 잘 풀어낼 수만 있다면 얼마나 기쁠까!\" 스테판 아르카지치가 한층 대담하게 미소 지으며 말했다. \"말하지 마, 한 마디도 하지 마! 그저 내가 느끼는 대로 말할 수 있게 해 주십사 하느님께 빌 뿐이야. 그이에게 가 보마.\"\n\n안나는 꿈꾸는 듯 반짝이는 눈으로 그를 바라볼 뿐, 아무 말도 하지 않았다.\n\n# Chapter 22\n\n스테판 아르카지치는 이사회에서 의장석에 앉을 때 짓던 것과 똑같이 다소 엄숙한 표정을 한 채 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 방으로 들어섰다. 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 뒷짐을 진 채 방 안을 거닐며, 바로 스테판 아르카지치가 자기 아내와 의논하던 것과 똑같은 일을 생각하고 있었다.\n\n\"방해가 되지 않습니까?\" 매부를 본 순간 평소 자기답지 않은 어떤 거북함을 문득 의식하게 된 스테판 아르카지치가 말했다. 이 거북함을 감추려고 그는 얼마 전에 새로 산, 색다른 방식으로 열리는 담뱃갑을 꺼내어 가죽 냄새를 맡고는 그 안에서 담배 한 개비를 꺼냈다.\n\n\"아니요. 무슨 볼일이라도 있습니까?\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 별다른 열의 없이 물었다.\n\n\"네, 저는…… 저는 하고 싶은…… 그래요, 당신과 이야기를 좀 하고 싶었습니다.\" 평소답지 않은 소심함을 깨닫고는 스스로 놀라며 스테판 아르카지치가 말했다.\n\n이 느낌은 너무도 뜻밖이고 너무도 이상해서, 그는 자기가 하려는 일이 옳지 않다고 말해 주는 양심의 소리라고는 믿지 못했다.\n\n스테판 아르카지치는 애써 마음을 다잡으며 자신을 덮친 소심함과 싸웠다.\n\n\"제가 누이를 사랑하는 마음과, 당신을 향한 진심 어린 애정과 존경을 당신이 믿어 주시리라 생각합니다.\" 그는 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다.\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 가만히 선 채 아무 말도 하지 않았으나, 그 얼굴에 어린 저항 없는 희생의 표정이 스테판 아르카지치의 가슴을 쳤다.\n\n\"저는…… 누이와 두 분 사이의 처지에 대해 당신과 잠깐 이야기를 나누고 싶었습니다.\" 그는 여전히 익숙지 않은 어색함과 싸우며 말했다.\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 서글프게 미소를 짓고 매부를 바라보았으나, 대답은 하지 않고 탁자로 다가가더니 거기서 미처 끝맺지 못한 편지 한 통을 집어 매부에게 건넸다.\n\n\"저도 끊임없이 같은 일만 생각하고 있습니다. 이게 제가 쓰기 시작한 것인데, 직접 마주하는 것이 그녀를 자극하니 편지로 하는 편이 더 낫겠다 싶어서요.\" 그는 편지를 건네며 말했다.\n\n스테판 아르카지치는 편지를 받아 들고, 자기에게 그토록 꼼짝없이 고정된 광채 없는 두 눈을 믿기지 않는다는 듯 놀라워하며 바라보다가 읽기 시작했다.\n\n\"내가 곁에 있는 것이 당신에게 괴로운 일이라는 걸 알겠소. 나로서는 그것을 믿기가 고통스럽지만, 사실이 그렇고 달리 어쩔 수 없다는 것을 압니다. 나는 당신을 탓하지 않으며, 당신이 앓던 때에 당신을 보고는 우리 사이에 있었던 모든 일을 잊고 새로운 삶을 시작하기로 온 마음을 다해 결심했음을 하느님이 증인이 되어 주실 것이오. 나는 내가 한 일을 후회하지 않으며 앞으로도 결코 후회하지 않을 것이오. 다만 나는 한 가지만을, 곧 당신의 행복, 당신 영혼의 행복을 바랐는데, 이제 보니 나는 그것을 이루지 못했소. 무엇이 당신에게 진정한 행복과 영혼의 평화를 줄 수 있을지 당신 입으로 직접 말해 주시오. 나는 나 자신을 온전히 당신 손에 맡기며, 무엇이 옳은가에 대한 당신의 감정을 믿겠소.\"\n\n스테판 아르카지치는 편지를 돌려주고는, 무슨 말을 해야 할지 모른 채 여전히 똑같은 놀라움으로 매부를 계속 바라보았다. 이 침묵이 두 사람 모두에게 너무도 어색하여, 스테판 아르카지치는 여전히 아무 말 없이 카레닌의 얼굴을 응시하는 동안 입술이 신경질적으로 씰룩거리기 시작했다.\n\n\"이게 제가 그녀에게 하고 싶었던 말입니다.\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 몸을 돌리며 말했다.\n\n\"네, 네…….\" 목이 메는 눈물 때문에 대답을 못 하고 스테판 아르카지치가 말했다.\n\n\"네, 네, 당신 마음 이해합니다.\" 그가 마침내 입을 뗐다.\n\n\"저는 그녀가 무엇을 원하는지 알고 싶습니다.\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 말했다.\n\n\"제 생각에는 그녀가 자기 처지를 제대로 이해하지 못하는 것 같습니다. 그녀는 판단을 내릴 사람이 못 됩니다.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 마음을 추스르며 말했다. \"그녀는 당신의 너그러움에 짓눌려 있습니다, 그저 짓눌려 있어요. 만일 이 편지를 읽는다면, 그녀는 아무 말도 못 하고 그저 그 어느 때보다 고개를 더 깊이 떨굴 뿐일 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그래요, 하지만 그렇다면 어떻게 해야 합니까? 어떻게 설명하고, 어떻게 그녀의 바람을 알아냅니까?\"\n\n\"제 의견을 말씀드려도 된다면, 이 처지를 끝내기 위해 필요하다고 생각되는 절차를 곧장 짚어 주시는 것이 당신께 달린 일이라고 봅니다.\"\n\n\"그러니까 당신은 이것이 반드시 끝나야 한다고 보는 겁니까?\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 그의 말을 가로막았다. \"하지만 어떻게요?\" 그는 평소 자기답지 않게 두 손을 눈앞으로 가져가는 동작을 하며 덧붙였다. \"저는 빠져나갈 길이 도무지 보이지 않습니다.\"\n\n\"어떤 처지든 빠져나갈 길은 있는 법입니다.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 일어서며 한결 활기를 띠고 말했다. \"당신이 헤어질 생각을 했던 때가 있지 않았습니까…… 이제 서로를 행복하게 해 줄 수 없다고 확신하신다면……\"\n\n\"행복은 여러 가지로 이해될 수 있지요. 하지만 가령 제가 모든 것에 동의하고 아무것도 바라지 않는다고 칩시다. 그렇다 한들 우리 처지에서 빠져나갈 길이 어디 있습니까?\"\n\n\"제 의견이 궁금하시다면,\" 스테판 아르카지치는 안나에게 이야기할 때와 똑같이 부드럽게 녹아드는, 편도유 같은 다정한 미소를 지으며 말했다. 그의 다정한 미소가 어찌나 마음을 끄는지, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 자기 자신의 나약함을 느끼며 자기도 모르게 그것에 휩쓸려, 스테판 아르카지치가 하는 말을 믿을 마음의 준비가 되어 있었다.\n\n\"그녀는 결코 그것을 입 밖에 내지 않을 겁니다. 하지만 한 가지는 가능하고, 한 가지는 그녀가 바랄 만한 일이지요.\" 그가 말을 이었다. \"그건 바로 두 분의 관계와 그에 얽힌 모든 기억을 끝내는 것입니다. 제 생각에 당신들의 처지에서 본질적인 것은 서로에 대한 새로운 태도를 세우는 일입니다. 그리고 그것은 오직 양쪽 모두의 자유라는 토대 위에서만 가능합니다.\"\n\n\"이혼이로군요.\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 혐오감 어린 어조로 가로막았다.\n\n\"네, 제 생각에는 이혼이…… 그래요, 이혼이지요.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 얼굴을 붉히며 거듭 말했다. \"그것이 당신들 같은 처지에 놓인 부부에게는 어느 모로 보나 가장 합리적인 길입니다. 부부가 함께 사는 것이 불가능하다는 걸 알게 되었을 때 달리 무엇을 할 수 있겠습니까? 그런 일은 언제든 일어날 수 있는 거니까요.\"\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 무겁게 한숨을 내쉬며 눈을 감았다.\n\n\"고려해야 할 점은 단 하나뿐입니다. 두 사람 중 어느 한쪽이 새로운 인연을 맺기를 바라는가 하는 것이지요. 만약 그렇지 않다면 아주 간단합니다.\" 스테판 아르카지치는 점점 더 거북함에서 벗어나는 것을 느끼며 말했다.\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 격한 감정으로 얼굴을 찌푸린 채 혼자 무어라 중얼거렸을 뿐 아무 대답도 하지 않았다. 스테판 아르카지치에게는 그토록 간단해 보이는 그 모든 것을, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 수천 번이나 곱씹어 왔다. 그리고 그에게는 간단하기는커녕 그 모든 것이 도무지 불가능해 보였다. 이제는 그 세세한 절차까지 다 알게 된 이혼이, 그에게는 이제 말도 안 되는 일로 여겨졌다. 자신의 존엄에 대한 의식과 종교에 대한 존경이, 거짓으로 간통의 죄를 자기에게 뒤집어쓰는 것을, 더더욱 자기가 용서하고 사랑하는 아내가 현장에서 발각되어 세상의 망신을 당하게 두는 것을 금했기 때문이다. 이혼은 그 밖에도 한층 더 무거운 다른 이유들 때문에 그에게는 불가능해 보였다.\n\n이혼할 경우 아들은 어떻게 되겠는가? 아들을 어미에게 맡긴다는 것은 말도 안 되는 일이었다. 이혼한 어미는 자기만의 사생아 가정을 꾸릴 테고, 그 안에서 의붓아들이라는 아이의 처지며 양육은 좋을 리가 없었다. 그렇다면 자기가 데리고 있을까? 그것은 자기 쪽의 복수가 되리라는 것을 그는 알았고, 그건 그가 바라는 바가 아니었다. 하지만 이것은 차치하더라도, 무엇보다도 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에게 이혼을 불가능하게 보이게 한 것은, 이혼에 동의함으로써 자기가 안나를 완전히 파멸시키게 되리라는 점이었다. 모스크바에서 다리아 알렉산드로브나가 한 말, 즉 그가 이혼을 결정하는 것은 자기 자신만 생각하는 것이며 이로써 그녀를 돌이킬 수 없이 파멸시키게 된다는 것을 헤아리지 않는 것이라던 그 말이 그의 가슴에 깊이 박혀 있었다. 그리고 이 말을 그녀를 향한 자신의 용서와, 아이들에 대한 자신의 헌신과 연결시켜, 그는 이제 그것을 자기 나름의 방식으로 이해했다. 이혼에 동의하여 그녀에게 자유를 주는 것은, 그의 생각으로는 자기를 삶에 붙잡아 두는 마지막 끈—그가 사랑하는 아이들—을 자기 자신에게서 빼앗는 것이요, 그녀를 올바른 길 위에 머물게 하는 마지막 버팀목을 그녀에게서 빼앗아 그녀를 파멸로 떠미는 것이었다. 만일 그녀가 이혼한다면, 그녀가 자기 삶을 브론스키의 삶과 합치리라는 것을 그는 알았고, 그들의 결합은 부정하고 죄스러운 것이 될 터였다. 교회법의 해석에 따르면 아내는 남편이 살아 있는 동안에는 재혼할 수 없기 때문이었다. \"그녀는 그와 합치겠지, 그리고 한두 해 뒤면 그는 그녀를 내팽개치거나, 아니면 그녀가 새로운 인연을 맺겠지.\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 생각했다. \"그러면 나는 부정한 이혼에 동의함으로써 그녀의 파멸에 책임을 지게 되는 거야.\" 그는 이 모든 것을 수백 번이나 곱씹었고, 스테판 아르카지치가 말한 것처럼 이혼이 결코 간단한 것이 아니라 도무지 불가능한 일임을 확신하고 있었다. 그는 스테판 아르카지치가 자기에게 하는 말을 한마디도 믿지 않았다. 그 한마디 한마디마다 그는 수천 가지 반박할 거리가 있었지만, 그래도 그의 말에 귀를 기울였다. 그 말들이 자기 삶을 지배하며 자기가 끝내 굴복해야만 할 그 거대하고 야만적인 힘의 표현임을 느꼈기 때문이다.\n\n\"문제는 단지 어떤 조건으로 그녀에게 이혼을 허락하실 것인가 하는 것뿐입니다. 그녀는 아무것도 바라지 않고, 감히 당신께 아무것도 청하지 못하며, 모든 것을 당신의 너그러움에 맡기고 있습니다.\"\n\n\"맙소사, 맙소사! 무엇 때문에?\" 남편이 죄를 스스로 뒤집어쓰는 이혼 소송의 세세한 절차를 떠올리며 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 생각했다. 그러고는 브론스키가 똑같은 짓을 할 때 했던 것과 똑같은 동작으로, 부끄러움에 두 손에 얼굴을 파묻었다.\n\n\"괴로우신 거 압니다. 하지만 곰곰이 생각해 보시면……\"\n\n\"누구든지 네 오른뺨을 치거든 왼뺨마저 돌려 대고, 또 누가 네 속옷을 가지려 하거든 겉옷까지도 내주어라.\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 생각했다.\n\n\"네, 네!\" 그가 날카로운 목소리로 외쳤다. \"치욕은 제가 떠맡겠습니다, 아들까지도 포기하겠습니다, 하지만…… 하지만 그냥 그대로 두는 편이 낫지 않겠습니까? 어쨌든 당신 좋을 대로 하십시오……\"\n\n그러고는 매부가 자기를 보지 못하도록 몸을 돌려 창가의 의자에 앉았다. 그의 마음속에는 쓰라림이 있었고 부끄러움이 있었지만, 그 쓰라림과 부끄러움과 더불어 그는 자기 자신의 온순함의 절정에서 기쁨과 감동을 느꼈다.\n\n스테판 아르카지치는 마음이 뭉클했다. 그는 잠시 말이 없었다.\n\n\"알렉세이 알렉산드로비치, 제 말을 믿으십시오, 그녀는 당신의 너그러움을 고맙게 여기고 있습니다.\" 그가 말했다. \"하지만 이건 아무래도 하느님의 뜻이었던 모양입니다.\" 그가 덧붙였는데, 말을 하면서 그것이 얼마나 어리석은 말인지를 느끼고는 자기 자신의 어리석음에 터져 나오는 웃음을 간신히 억눌렀다.\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 무슨 대답이라도 하려 했지만, 눈물이 그를 가로막았다.\n\n\"이것은 불행한 숙명이며, 그런 것으로 받아들여야만 합니다. 저는 이 재앙을 이미 이루어진 사실로 받아들이고 있고, 그녀와 당신 두 분 모두를 돕기 위해 최선을 다하고 있습니다.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 말했다.\n\n매부의 방을 나설 때 그는 마음이 뭉클했으나, 그렇다고 일을 성공적으로 매듭지었다는 데 대한 기쁨이 가시지는 않았다. 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 자기 말을 번복하지 않으리라고 확신했기 때문이다. 이 만족감에 더하여, 마침 이 일을 성공적으로 해낸 것을 소재로 한 수수께끼 하나가 그의 머리에 떠올랐다는 사실까지 보태졌다. 일이 다 끝나면 그것을 아내와 가장 가까운 친구들에게 내볼 작정이었다. 그는 이 수수께끼를 두세 가지 다른 방식으로 만들어 보았다. \"아니, 이것보다는 더 잘 다듬어야겠어.\" 그는 미소를 지으며 혼잣말했다.\n\n# Chapter 23\n\n브론스키의 상처는 위험한 것이었다. 심장은 다치지 않았지만, 며칠 동안 그는 삶과 죽음 사이를 오갔다. 그가 처음으로 말을 할 수 있게 되었을 때, 방 안에는 그의 형수인 바랴 혼자 있었다.\n\n\"바랴.\" 그가 그녀를 엄하게 바라보며 말했다. \"나는 실수로 총을 쏜 거예요. 그러니 부디 그 일에 대해서는 절대 말하지 말고, 모두에게도 그렇게 전해 줘요. 그러지 않으면 너무 우스꽝스러우니까.\"\n\n그의 말에는 아무 대꾸도 하지 않고, 바랴는 그에게 몸을 숙인 채 기쁨에 찬 미소를 지으며 그의 얼굴을 들여다보았다. 그의 눈은 맑았고, 열에 들뜨지도 않았다. 다만 그 표정만은 엄했다.\n\n\"하느님, 감사합니다!\" 그녀가 말했다. \"아프지는 않아요?\"\n\n\"여기가 조금.\" 그는 자기 가슴을 가리켰다.\n\n\"그럼 붕대를 갈아 줄게요.\"\n\n그는 말없이 넓은 턱을 굳힌 채, 그녀가 붕대를 감는 동안 그녀를 바라보았다. 그녀가 다 마치자 그가 말했다.\n\n\"나는 헛소리를 하는 게 아니에요. 부디 내가 일부러 총을 쐈다는 말이 나돌지 않도록 해 줘요.\"\n\n\"아무도 그런 말 안 해요. 다만 다시는 실수로 총을 쏘는 일이 없기를 바랄 뿐이에요.\" 그녀가 묻는 듯한 미소를 띠며 말했다.\n\n\"물론 그러지 않을 거예요. 하지만 차라리 그편이 나았을 텐데…….\"\n\n그러고는 음울하게 미소 지었다.\n\n바랴를 그토록 두렵게 했던 이 말과 이 미소에도 불구하고, 염증이 가라앉고 회복되기 시작하자 그는 자기 고통의 한 부분에서 완전히 벗어났음을 느꼈다. 그 행동으로 그는 이전에 느꼈던 수치와 굴욕을 말하자면 씻어 낸 셈이었다. 이제 그는 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치를 차분히 생각할 수 있었다. 그는 그 사람의 너그러움을 모두 인정했지만, 이제는 그 때문에 굴욕감을 느끼지는 않았다. 게다가 그는 다시 자기 삶의 익숙한 궤도로 돌아왔다. 그는 부끄러움 없이 다시 사람들의 얼굴을 마주 볼 수 있게 되었고, 자기 습관대로 살아갈 수 있었다. 다만 그가 끊임없이 맞서 싸웠으면서도 가슴에서 도려내지 못한 한 가지는, 그녀를 영원히 잃었다는 절망에 가까운 회한이었다. 이제 남편에 대한 자기 죄를 속죄한 이상 그녀를 단념해야 하며, 앞으로는 결코 그녀와 그녀의 참회와 그녀의 남편 사이에 끼어들지 않겠다고 그는 마음속으로 굳게 다짐했다. 하지만 그녀의 사랑을 잃은 회한만큼은 가슴에서 뜯어낼 수 없었고, 그 당시에는 그토록 하찮게 여겼으나 이제는 온갖 매혹으로 그를 사로잡는 그 행복의 순간들을 기억에서 지울 수가 없었다.\n\n세르푸호프스코이가 그를 타슈켄트에 임명되도록 손을 써 두었고, 브론스키는 조금의 망설임도 없이 그 제안을 받아들였다. 그러나 출발할 날이 가까워질수록, 의무라고 여기는 것에 바치는 그 희생은 더욱 쓰라리게 다가왔다.\n\n상처가 아물자, 그는 타슈켄트로 떠날 채비를 하느라 여기저기 마차를 몰고 다녔다.\n\n'그녀를 한 번만 보고, 그런 다음 나 자신을 묻어 버리는 거야, 죽어 버리는 거지.' 그는 생각했다. 그리고 작별 인사차 방문을 다니던 중 이 생각을 베치에게 털어놓았다. 그 부탁을 맡은 베치는 안나를 찾아갔다가, 그에게 거절의 답을 가지고 돌아왔다.\n\n'차라리 잘됐어.' 그 소식을 받았을 때 브론스키는 생각했다. '그건 약한 마음이었고, 내게 남아 있는 힘마저 산산조각 냈을 테니까.'\n\n이튿날 아침 베치가 직접 그를 찾아와, 오블론스키를 통해 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 이혼에 동의했다는 사실을 확실히 들었으며, 따라서 브론스키가 안나를 만날 수 있게 되었다고 알려 주었다.\n\n베치를 집 밖까지 배웅하는 수고조차 잊은 채, 자기가 했던 모든 결심을 잊은 채, 언제 그녀를 만날 수 있는지, 그녀의 남편은 어디 있는지도 묻지 않은 채, 브론스키는 곧장 카레닌 집으로 마차를 몰았다. 그는 아무것도 누구도 보지 않으며 계단을 뛰어올랐고, 거의 달음박질에 가까운 빠른 걸음으로 그녀의 방으로 들어섰다. 그리고 방 안에 누가 있는지 없는지 헤아리지도 알아차리지도 않은 채, 그는 그녀를 두 팔로 와락 끌어안고는 그녀의 얼굴, 두 손, 목에 입맞춤을 퍼붓기 시작했다.\n\n안나는 이 만남에 대비해 그에게 무슨 말을 할지 생각해 두었지만, 그중 어느 것도 입에 올리지 못했다. 그의 열정이 그녀를 사로잡아 버린 것이다. 그녀는 그를 진정시키고 자기 자신도 진정시키려 애썼지만, 이미 너무 늦었다. 그의 감정이 그녀에게 옮아왔다. 그녀의 입술이 어찌나 떨렸던지 한참 동안 그녀는 아무 말도 할 수 없었다.\n\n\"그래요, 당신은 나를 정복했어요, 나는 당신의 것이에요.\" 마침내 그녀가 그의 두 손을 자기 가슴에 꼭 누르며 말했다.\n\n\"이렇게 될 수밖에 없었어요.\" 그가 말했다. \"우리가 살아 있는 한, 이렇게 되어야 했던 거예요. 이제 나는 그걸 알겠어요.\"\n\n\"맞아요.\" 그녀가 점점 더 창백해지며 그의 머리를 끌어안고 말했다. \"하지만 그래도 이 모든 일이 있고 난 뒤에 보면, 거기엔 뭔가 끔찍한 게 있어요.\"\n\n\"다 지나갈 거예요, 다 지나갈 거예요. 우리는 무척 행복해질 거예요. 우리 사랑은, 더 강해질 수 있다면, 그 안에 뭔가 끔찍한 것이 있음으로 해서 더 강해질 거예요.\" 그가 고개를 들고, 튼튼한 이를 드러내며 미소 지은 채 말했다.\n\n그러자 그녀도 미소로 답하지 않을 수 없었다. 그의 말이 아니라 그의 눈에 어린 사랑에 대한 답이었다. 그녀는 그의 손을 잡아, 그것으로 차갑게 식은 자기 두 뺨과 짧게 깎은 머리를 쓰다듬었다.\n\n\"이렇게 머리가 짧아진 당신이 낯설어요. 정말 예뻐졌어요. 꼭 소년 같아요. 그런데 어쩜 이렇게 창백할까요!\"\n\n\"그래요, 나는 아주 약해졌어요.\" 그녀가 미소 지으며 말했다. 그리고 그녀의 입술이 다시 떨리기 시작했다.\n\n\"우리 이탈리아로 가요. 거기서 당신은 건강해질 거예요.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"우리가 정말 부부처럼, 단둘이서, 당신의 가족과 함께 있을 수 있을까요?\" 그녀가 그의 눈을 가까이 들여다보며 말했다.\n\n\"내겐 오히려 그렇지 않은 적이 있었다는 게 이상하게만 느껴져요.\"\n\n\"스티바 말로는 그이가 모든 것에 동의했다고 하지만, 나는 그이의 너그러움을 받아들일 수가 없어요.\" 그녀가 브론스키의 얼굴 너머를 꿈꾸듯 바라보며 말했다. \"나는 이혼을 원하지 않아요. 이제 내게는 다 마찬가지예요. 다만 그이가 세료자를 두고 무슨 결정을 내릴지 모르겠어요.\"\n\n그는 두 사람이 만나는 이 순간에 어떻게 그녀가 아들을, 이혼을 떠올리고 생각할 수 있는지 이해할 수 없었다. 그게 다 무슨 상관이란 말인가?\n\n\"그런 이야긴 하지 말아요, 그런 생각 하지 말아요.\" 그가 그녀의 손을 자기 손안에서 돌려 쥐며, 그녀의 주의를 자기에게 끌려 애쓰며 말했다. 하지만 그녀는 여전히 그를 바라보지 않았다.\n\n\"아, 왜 나는 죽지 않았을까! 차라리 그편이 나았을 텐데.\" 그녀가 말했다. 그러자 소리 없는 눈물이 그녀의 두 뺨을 타고 흘러내렸다. 하지만 그녀는 그에게 상처를 주지 않으려고 미소 지으려 애썼다.\n\n타슈켄트의 그 명예롭고도 위험한 임명을 거절하는 것은, 그때까지만 해도 브론스키가 생각하기에 수치스럽고 불가능한 일이었다. 그러나 이제 그는 한순간도 망설이지 않고 그것을 거절했고, 이 처사에 대해 최고위층에서 불만을 품는 것을 보고는 곧바로 군에서 물러났다.\n\n한 달 뒤 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 페테르부르크의 자기 집에 아들과 단둘이 남았고, 한편 안나와 브론스키는 이혼을 얻어 내지도 않은 채, 오히려 이혼이라는 생각 자체를 완전히 거부한 채 외국으로 떠났다.\n\n# PART FIVE Chapter 1\n\n시쳬르바츠카야 공작 부인은 결혼식을 사순절 전에, 그러니까 겨우 다섯 주밖에 남지 않은 그때 치르는 것은 도무지 말이 안 된다고 여겼다. 그때까지 혼수의 절반도 채 준비할 수 없을 게 분명했기 때문이다. 그렇지만 사순절 후로 미루면 너무 늦어진다는 레빈의 말에는 동의하지 않을 수 없었다. 시쳬르바츠키 공작의 늙은 고모가 위중해서 죽을지도 모르는데, 그렇게 되면 상중이라 결혼식이 더더욱 늦어질 터였기 때문이다. 그래서 부인은 혼수를 큰 혼수와 작은 혼수, 두 부분으로 나누기로 결정하고 사순절 전에 결혼식을 치르는 데 동의했다. 그녀는 작은 부분의 혼수는 지금 당장 모두 준비하고, 큰 부분은 나중에 마련하기로 마음먹었다. 그러면서 레빈에게 몹시 화가 났는데, 이 방안에 동의하느냐 마느냐는 질문에 그가 진지한 대답 하나 내놓지 못했기 때문이었다. 이 방안은 더없이 적절했다. 결혼식 직후 두 젊은이는 시골로 내려갈 예정이었고, 그곳에서는 더 중요한 부분의 혼수가 필요 없을 테니 말이다.\n\n레빈은 여전히 자기와 자기 행복이야말로 모든 존재의 주된, 유일한 목적이며, 이제는 아무것도 생각하거나 신경 쓸 필요가 없고, 모든 일이 다른 사람들에 의해 진행되고 있고 또 진행될 거라고 여겨지는 그 도취 상태에 계속 빠져 있었다. 그는 미래에 대한 계획이나 목적조차 없었고, 모든 것이 멋지게 풀릴 것을 알기에 그 채비를 남들에게 맡겼다. 그의 형 세르게이 이바노비치와 스테판 아르카지치, 그리고 공작 부인이 그가 해야 할 일을 하도록 그를 이끌었다. 그가 한 일이라곤 자기에게 제안된 모든 것에 전적으로 동의하는 것뿐이었다. 형은 그를 위해 돈을 마련했고, 공작 부인은 결혼식 후에 모스크바를 떠나라고 충고했다. 스테판 아르카지치는 외국으로 나가라고 충고했다. 그는 무엇에든 동의했다. '재미있다면 마음대로 하라지. 난 행복하고, 내 행복은 너희가 무얼 하든 더 커지지도 작아지지도 않을 테니까.' 그는 그렇게 생각했다. 스테판 아르카지치가 외국으로 나가는 게 좋겠다고 한 충고를 키티에게 전했을 때, 그녀가 거기에 동의하지 않고 자기들의 미래에 관해 나름의 분명한 요구를 가지고 있다는 사실에 그는 무척 놀랐다. 그녀는 레빈이 시골에서 사랑하는 일을 한다는 것을 알았다. 그가 보기에 그녀는 그 일을 이해하지 못했고, 이해하려고 애쓰지도 않았다. 그래도 그녀가 그 일을 대단히 중요한 것으로 여기는 데에는 아무 지장이 없었다. 그리고 그녀는 자기들의 보금자리가 시골에 있으리란 것을 알았기에, 살지도 않을 외국이 아니라 자기들의 보금자리가 들어설 곳으로 가고 싶어 했다. 이렇게 분명하게 표현된 의향이 레빈을 놀라게 했다. 하지만 그는 어느 쪽이든 상관없었기에, 마치 그것이 자기 의무라도 되는 양 스테판 아르카지치에게 시골로 내려가 그가 그토록 풍부하게 지닌 그 안목으로 거기 모든 것을 최선껏 정돈해 달라고 곧장 부탁했다.\n\n\"그런데 말이야,\" 어느 날 스테판 아르카지치가 두 젊은이의 도착을 위해 모든 것을 준비해 두고 시골에서 돌아온 뒤 그에게 말했다. \"고해성사를 받았다는 증명서가 있나?\"\n\n\"아니. 그게 뭐 어때서?\"\n\n\"그게 없으면 결혼식을 올릴 수 없어.\"\n\n\"아이, 아이, 아이!\" 레빈이 소리쳤다. \"아니, 성찬을 받은 지 한 구 년쯤 된 것 같은데! 그건 생각도 못 했군.\"\n\n\"참 잘난 친구로군!\" 스테판 아르카지치가 웃으며 말했다. \"그러면서 나더러 허무주의자라니! 하지만 이건 안 될 일이야, 알겠나. 자넨 성찬을 받아야 해.\"\n\n\"언제? 이제 나흘밖에 안 남았는데.\"\n\n스테판 아르카지치는 이 일도 처리해 주었고, 레빈은 고해성사를 하러 가야 했다. 다른 사람들의 믿음을 존중하는 여느 비신자와 마찬가지로, 레빈에게도 교회 의식에 참석하고 거기에 동참하는 일은 지극히 거북했다. 지금 이 순간, 모든 것에 예민해진 부드러운 감정 상태에 있는 레빈에게 이 불가피한 위선의 행위는 그저 괴롭기만 한 것이 아니라 도무지 불가능한 일로 여겨졌다. 지금, 더없이 영광스러운 전성기, 활짝 핀 절정에서 그는 거짓말쟁이나 비웃는 자가 되어야 했다. 그는 둘 중 어느 쪽도 될 수 없을 것 같았다. 하지만 실제로 성찬을 받지 않고도 증명서를 얻을 수 있는지를 스테판 아르카지치에게 거듭 캐물었으나, 스테판 아르카지치는 그건 말도 안 되는 일이라고 못 박았다.\n\n\"게다가 그게 자네한테 뭐 대수인가—이틀이잖나? 그리고 그 양반은 정말 친절하고 영리한 노인이야. 자네가 알아차리지도 못할 만큼 살살 이를 뽑아 줄 거야.\"\n\n첫 기도가 진행되는 동안 서 있으면서 레빈은 자기가 열여섯에서 열일곱 살 사이에 거쳐 갔던 강렬한 종교적 감정에 대한 젊은 날의 기억을 자기 안에 되살려 보려 애썼다.\n\n하지만 그는 그것이 자기에게 도무지 불가능하다는 것을 곧바로 깨달았다. 그는 이 모든 것을, 방문 예절 같은 풍습처럼 아무런 의미도 없는 텅 빈 관습으로 바라보려 했다. 하지만 그는 그것도 할 수 없다고 느꼈다. 레빈은 자기 동시대인의 대다수가 그렇듯 종교에 관해 더없이 모호한 처지에 놓여 있는 자신을 발견했다. 그는 믿을 수 없었고, 동시에 그 모든 것이 잘못되었다는 확고한 신념도 없었다. 그래서 자기가 하는 일의 의미를 믿을 수도, 그것을 텅 빈 형식으로 여겨 무심하게 대할 수도 없었기에, 성찬을 준비하는 내내 그는 스스로도 이해하지 못하는, 그리고 내면의 목소리가 일러 주듯 바로 그렇기에 거짓되고 그릇된 일을 하고 있다는 불편함과 부끄러움을 느꼈다.\n\n예배가 진행되는 동안 그는 처음에는 기도에 귀를 기울이며 자기 견해와 어긋나지 않는 어떤 의미를 거기에 갖다 붙이려 애썼다. 그러다 그것을 이해할 수 없고 비난할 수밖에 없다고 느끼자, 기도에 귀 기울이지 않으려 했고, 교회에 서 있는 이 한가한 시간 동안 극도로 생생하게 머릿속을 떠다니는 생각과 관찰과 기억에 마음을 두려 했다.\n\n그는 기도와 저녁 예배, 자정 예배를 내내 서서 견뎌 냈고, 이튿날에는 평소보다 일찍 일어나 차도 마시지 않은 채 아침 여덟 시에 아침 예배와 고해성사를 위해 교회로 갔다.\n\n교회 안에는 구걸하는 병사 하나, 노파 둘, 그리고 교회 직원들 말고는 아무도 없었다. 얇은 속법의 사이로 긴 등이 또렷이 둘로 나뉘어 보이는 젊은 부제가 그를 맞이하더니, 곧장 벽 쪽의 작은 탁자로 가서 권면의 글을 읽었다. 그 낭독이 진행되는 동안, 특히 메아리치며 울려 퍼지는 \"주여, 우리를 불쌍히 여기소서!\"라는 똑같은 말이 빈번하고 빠르게 되풀이될 때, 레빈은 생각이 닫히고 봉인되어 버린 듯한, 그래서 지금은 그것을 건드리거나 휘저어서는 안 되며 그러면 혼란이 빚어질 것 같은 느낌을 받았다. 그래서 부제 뒤에 서서 그는 듣지도, 무슨 말이 오가는지 살피지도 않은 채 자기 자신의 일을 계속 생각했다. '그녀 손에 담긴 그 표정은 정말 놀랍단 말이야.' 어제 모퉁이 탁자에 둘이 앉아 있던 일을 떠올리며 그는 생각했다. 이맘때면 거의 늘 그랬듯이 그들은 별달리 나눌 이야기가 없었고, 그녀는 탁자 위에 손을 올려놓은 채 그것을 폈다 쥐었다 하면서 자기 동작을 지켜보며 혼자 웃었다. 그는 자기가 그 손에 입을 맞추고 나서 분홍빛 손바닥의 손금을 들여다보았던 일을 떠올렸다. '다시 우리를 불쌍히 여기소서!' 레빈은 성호를 긋고 절을 하며, 자기 앞에서 절을 하는 부제의 등이 유연하게 펴졌다 굽혀지는 모습을 바라보면서 생각했다. '그때 그녀는 내 손을 잡고 손금을 들여다봤지. 「당신 손은 정말 멋져요」 하고 그녀는 말했어.' 그러고는 자기 손과 부제의 짧은 손을 바라보았다. '그래, 이제 곧 끝나겠지.' 그는 생각했다. '아니, 또 시작되는 모양이군.' 기도에 귀 기울이며 그는 생각했다. '아니, 막 끝나는군. 저기 그가 땅에 닿도록 절을 하잖아. 저건 늘 끝에 하는 거지.'\n\n플러시 천 소매 속의 부제 손이 눈에 띄지 않게 삼 루블짜리 지폐를 받았고, 부제는 그것을 장부에 적어 두겠다고 말하더니, 텅 빈 교회의 포석 위로 새 부츠를 경쾌하게 삐걱이며 제단으로 갔다. 잠시 후 그는 거기서 고개를 내밀어 레빈에게 손짓했다. 그때까지 잠겨 있던 생각이 레빈의 머릿속에서 꿈틀거리기 시작했지만, 그는 서둘러 그것을 쫓아냈다. '어떻게든 잘되겠지.' 그는 그렇게 생각하며 제단 난간 쪽으로 갔다. 그가 계단을 올라 오른쪽으로 돌자 사제가 보였다. 희끗희끗한 성긴 수염에 지치고 선량한 눈을 가진 자그마한 노인인 사제는 제단 난간 앞에 서서 미사 경본의 책장을 넘기고 있었다. 사제는 레빈에게 가볍게 고개를 숙이고는 곧바로 격식 차린 목소리로 기도문을 읽기 시작했다. 기도를 마치자 그는 땅에 닿도록 절을 하고 몸을 돌려 레빈을 마주 보았다.\n\n\"그리스도께서 보이지 않게 여기 임하시어 그대의 고해를 받으십니다.\" 십자가상을 가리키며 그가 말했다. \"그대는 거룩한 사도 교회의 모든 교리를 믿습니까?\" 사제는 레빈의 얼굴에서 눈을 돌리고 영대 아래로 두 손을 모으며 말을 이었다.\n\n\"저는 의심해 왔고, 모든 것을 의심합니다.\" 레빈은 스스로도 거슬리는 목소리로 말하고는 입을 다물었다.\n\n사제는 그가 더 말하지 않을지 보려고 몇 초 기다렸다가, 눈을 감고 넓적한 블라디미르 사투리로 빠르게 말했다.\n\n\"의심은 인간의 나약함에 따른 자연스러운 일입니다만, 우리는 하느님께서 자비로 우리를 굳세게 해 주시길 기도해야 합니다. 그대의 특별한 죄는 무엇입니까?\" 그는 시간을 허비하지 않으려는 듯 조금의 틈도 두지 않고 덧붙였다.\n\n\"저의 가장 큰 죄는 의심입니다. 저는 모든 것을 의심하고, 대개 의심 속에 있습니다.\"\n\n\"의심은 인간의 나약함에 따른 자연스러운 일입니다.\" 사제는 똑같은 말을 되풀이했다. \"그대는 주로 무엇을 의심합니까?\"\n\n\"저는 모든 것을 의심합니다. 때로는 하느님의 존재마저 의심합니다.\" 레빈은 그렇게 말하지 않을 수 없었고, 자기가 하는 말의 부적절함에 소스라쳤다. 하지만 레빈의 말은 사제에게 별 인상을 남기지 못한 듯했다.\n\n\"하느님의 존재에 무슨 의심이 있을 수 있겠습니까?\" 사제는 어렴풋이 알아챌 만한 미소를 띠고 서둘러 말했다.\n\n레빈은 입을 열지 않았다.\n\n\"그대가 창조주의 창조물을 바라보면서 어찌 그분을 의심할 수 있습니까?\" 사제는 익숙한 빠른 말투로 말을 이었다. \"누가 하늘의 궁창을 빛으로 꾸몄습니까? 누가 대지에 아름다움의 옷을 입혔습니까? 창조주 없이 그것을 어떻게 설명하겠습니까?\" 그는 캐묻는 듯한 눈으로 레빈을 바라보며 말했다.\n\n레빈은 사제와 형이상학적 논쟁에 들어가는 것은 부적절하리라고 느꼈고, 그래서 그저 질문에 대한 직접적인 답만을 대답으로 내놓았다.\n\n\"모르겠습니다.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"모른다고요! 그런데 어떻게 하느님께서 만물을 창조하셨다는 것을 의심할 수 있습니까?\" 사제는 선량한 당혹감을 띠고 말했다.\n\n\"저는 그것을 전혀 이해하지 못하겠습니다.\" 레빈은 얼굴을 붉히며 말했고, 자기 말이 어리석으며 이런 상황에서는 어리석을 수밖에 없다고 느꼈다.\n\n\"하느님께 기도하고 그분께 간구하십시오. 거룩한 교부들조차 의심을 품었고, 자기 믿음을 굳세게 해 달라고 하느님께 기도했습니다. 악마는 큰 힘을 가졌으니, 우리는 그에게 맞서야 합니다. 하느님께 기도하십시오, 그분께 간구하십시오. 하느님께 기도하십시오.\" 그가 서둘러 되풀이했다.\n\n사제는 무언가 명상이라도 하듯 한동안 말을 멈추었다.\n\n\"내가 듣기로, 그대는 내 교구민이자 영적인 아들인 시쳬르바츠키 공작의 따님과 결혼하려 한다지요?\" 그가 미소를 지으며 다시 말을 이었다. \"참으로 훌륭한 아가씨입니다.\"\n\n\"네.\" 레빈은 사제 대신 얼굴을 붉히며 대답했다. '도대체 고해성사에서 이 일을 왜 나한테 물어보는 거지?' 그는 생각했다.\n\n그러자 마치 그의 생각에 답이라도 하듯 사제가 그에게 말했다.\n\n\"그대는 거룩한 혼인에 들어서려 하고, 하느님께서 그대에게 자식을 점지하실지도 모릅니다. 그런데 그대가 불신앙으로 그대를 꾀어내는 악마의 유혹을 이겨 내지 못한다면, 그대의 아기들을 어찌 길러 내겠습니까?\" 그가 부드러운 책망을 담아 말했다. \"그대가 좋은 아버지로서 자기 아이를 사랑한다면, 그 어린것에게 부와 사치와 명예만을 바라지는 않을 것입니다. 그대는 그 아이의 구원을, 진리의 빛으로 영적으로 깨우치는 일을 염려할 것입니다. 그렇지요? 죄 없는 아기가 그대에게 '아빠! 이 세상에서 나를 황홀하게 하는 이 모든 것—땅과 물과 해와 꽃과 풀을 누가 만들었어요?' 하고 물으면 그대는 무어라 답하겠습니까? 그 아이에게 '난 모른단다'라고 말할 수 있겠습니까? 주 하느님께서 무한한 자비로 우리에게 그것을 계시하셨으니, 그대는 모를 수가 없습니다. 혹은 그대의 아이가 '무덤 너머의 삶에서는 무엇이 나를 기다리고 있나요?' 하고 물을 것입니다. 그대가 아무것도 모를 때 그 아이에게 무어라 말하겠습니까? 어떻게 답하겠습니까? 그 아이를 세상과 악마의 유혹에 내맡길 것입니까? 그건 옳지 않습니다.\" 그는 그렇게 말하고는 멈추어, 고개를 한쪽으로 기울이고 자애롭고 부드러운 눈으로 레빈을 바라보았다.\n\n이번에 레빈은 아무 대답도 하지 않았는데, 사제와 논쟁에 들어가고 싶지 않아서가 아니라, 지금껏 아무도 그에게 그런 질문을 한 적이 없었고, 또 자기 아기들이 정말로 그런 질문을 하게 되면 그때 가서 답을 생각해도 충분하리라 여겼기 때문이었다.\n\n\"그대는,\" 사제가 말을 이었다. \"자기 길을 택하여 그것을 지켜 나가야 할 인생의 시기에 들어서고 있습니다. 하느님께서 자비로 그대를 도우시고 그대를 불쌍히 여기시길 기도하십시오!\" 그가 끝맺었다. \"우리 주 하느님이신 예수 그리스도께서 풍성하고 넉넉한 사랑과 자비로 이 자식을 용서하시니……\" 그러고는 사죄의 기도를 마치며 사제는 그를 축복하고 돌려보냈다.\n\n그날 집에 돌아오자 레빈은 거북한 상황이 끝났고, 거짓말을 하지 않고도 그것을 무사히 넘겼다는 데에 흐뭇한 안도감을 느꼈다. 그 외에도, 그 친절하고 다정한 노인이 한 말이 처음에 짐작했던 만큼 그렇게 어리석지는 않았으며, 거기에는 풀어내야 할 무언가가 있다는 어렴풋한 기억이 남아 있었다.\n\n'물론 지금은 아니지.' 레빈은 생각했다. '하지만 언젠가 나중에는.' 레빈은 이제 그 어느 때보다도 자기 영혼 속에 무언가 맑지 못하고 깨끗하지 못한 데가 있으며, 종교에 관한 한 자기가 남들에게서 그토록 또렷이 알아보고 싫어했던, 그리고 친구 스비아시스키를 두고 나무랐던 바로 그 처지에 자기 자신이 놓여 있음을 느꼈다.\n\n레빈은 그날 저녁을 돌리의 집에서 약혼녀와 함께 보냈고, 기분이 무척 좋았다. 자기가 빠져 있는 들뜬 상태를 스테판 아르카지치에게 설명하면서, 그는 자기가 마치 둥근 고리를 통과하도록 훈련받는 개와 같은 행복을 느낀다고 말했다. 마침내 요령을 깨치고 요구받은 것을 해낸 그 개가, 기뻐서 낑낑대며 꼬리를 흔들고, 좋아라 탁자와 창문으로 뛰어오르는 것처럼 말이다.\n\n# Chapter 2\n\n결혼식 날, 러시아의 관습에 따라(공작 부인과 다리야 알렉산드로브나는 모든 관습을 엄격히 지켜야 한다고 고집했다) 레빈은 신부를 보지 않았고, 자기 방으로 우연히 모인 세 명의 독신 친구들과 함께 호텔에서 저녁을 먹었다. 그들은 세르게이 이바노비치, 그리고 대학 동창이자 지금은 자연과학 교수인 카타바소프였는데, 레빈은 거리에서 그를 만나 기어이 자기 집으로 데려온 터였다. 또 한 사람은 들러리인 치리코프로, 모스크바 화해 조정관이자 레빈과 함께 곰 사냥을 다니던 동료였다. 저녁 식사는 무척 흥겨웠다. 세르게이 이바노비치는 더없이 기분 좋은 상태였고, 카타바소프의 독특함을 무척 재미있어했다. 카타바소프는 자기의 독특함이 인정받고 이해받고 있다고 느끼자 그것을 한껏 발휘했다. 치리코프는 어떤 종류의 대화든 늘 생기 있고 유쾌하게 거들었다.\n\n\"자, 보십시오.\" 강의실에서 익힌 버릇대로 말을 길게 늘이며 카타바소프가 말했다. \"우리 친구 콘스탄틴 드미트리예비치가 얼마나 재능 있는 사람이었는지 말입니다. 지금 이 자리에 있는 사람을 두고 하는 말은 아닙니다, 그는 여기 없으니까요. 대학을 떠날 무렵에 그는 학문을 사랑했고 인류에 관심을 가졌지요. 그런데 지금은 자기 재능의 절반은 스스로를 속이는 데 바치고, 나머지 절반은 그 기만을 정당화하는 데 바치고 있단 말입니다.\"\n\n\"자네만큼 결혼을 단호하게 적대시하는 사람은 본 적이 없네.\" 세르게이 이바노비치가 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 천만에요, 저는 결혼의 적이 아닙니다. 저는 분업에 찬성하는 거지요. 아무것도 할 수 없는 사람들은 사람을 길러 내야 하고, 나머지 사람들은 그들의 행복과 계몽을 위해 일해야 한다는 겁니다. 저는 그렇게 봅니다. 두 가지 일을 뒤죽박죽 섞는 건 아마추어의 잘못이지요. 저는 그런 부류가 아닙니다.\"\n\n\"자네가 사랑에 빠졌다는 소식을 들으면 내가 얼마나 기쁠까!\" 레빈이 말했다. \"그땐 꼭 결혼식에 초대해 주게.\"\n\n\"전 지금 사랑에 빠져 있는데요.\"\n\n\"그래, 오징어와 말이지!\" 레빈이 형을 돌아보며 말했다. \"있잖아, 미하일 세묘노비치가 지금 소화 기관에 관한 논문을 쓰고 있거든, 그러니까……\"\n\n\"자, 또 뒤죽박죽으로 만드시는군! 무엇에 관한 거든 상관없습니다. 사실 저는 분명히 오징어를 사랑하니까요.\"\n\n\"하지만 그게 아내를 사랑하는 데 방해가 되지는 않잖나.\"\n\n\"오징어는 방해가 안 되지요. 방해가 되는 건 아내입니다.\"\n\"어째서 그렇지?\"\n\n\"오, 곧 알게 되실 겁니다! 당신은 농사니 사냥이니 하는 것에 마음을 쓰시지요. 그러니 조심하시는 게 좋을 겁니다!\"\n\n\"오늘 아르히프가 다녀갔는데, 프루드노에 큰 사슴이 잔뜩 있고 곰이 두 마리 있다더군요.\" 치리코프가 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 자네들끼리 가서 잡게나, 나 없이.\"\n\n\"아, 그게 맞는 말이네.\" 세르게이 이바노비치가 말했다. \"그리고 앞으로 곰 사냥과는 작별 인사를 해 두는 게 좋을 거야. 자네 아내가 허락하지 않을 테니!\"\n\n레빈은 미소 지었다. 아내가 자기를 못 가게 한다는 그림이 너무도 흐뭇해서, 그는 곰을 보는 즐거움쯤은 영영 단념해도 좋다는 마음이었다.\n\n\"그래도 자네 없이 그 곰 두 마리를 잡아 버린다니 안된 일이군. 지난번 하필로보에서 있었던 일 기억나나? 그땐 정말 신나는 사냥이었지!\" 치리코프가 말했다.\n\n레빈은 그녀와 떨어진 곳에 무언가 즐거운 것이 있을 수 있다는 그 생각을 깨뜨려 줄 마음이 차마 들지 않아, 아무 말도 하지 않았다.\n\n\"독신 생활과 작별을 고하는 이런 관습에는 일리가 있어.\" 세르게이 이바노비치가 말했다. \"아무리 행복하다 해도 자기 자유는 아쉬울 수밖에 없으니까.\"\n\n\"그리고 고골의 신랑처럼 창밖으로 뛰쳐나가고 싶은 기분이 든다는 것도 솔직히 인정해야겠지?\"\n\n\"물론 그런 기분이 들지요, 다만 인정하지 않을 뿐입니다.\" 카타바소프가 이렇게 말하고는 큰 소리로 웃음을 터뜨렸다.\n\n\"오, 그래요, 창문이 열려 있군요. 지금 당장 트베리로 떠납시다! 거기 큰 암곰이 있는데, 굴까지 곧장 다가갈 수 있어요. 진지하게 말하는데, 다섯 시 기차로 갑시다! 그리고 여기 일은 저들이 알아서 하라지요.\" 치리코프가 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"자, 그러니까, 내 명예를 걸고 말하는데,\" 레빈이 미소 지으며 말했다. \"내 마음속에서는 자유를 아쉬워하는 그런 감정을 도무지 찾을 수가 없네.\"\n\n\"그래, 지금 자네 마음속이 어찌나 뒤죽박죽인지 거기서 아무것도 찾을 수가 없는 거지.\" 카타바소프가 말했다. \"조금만 기다려 보게, 마음을 좀 정리하고 나면 찾게 될 테니!\"\n\n\"아니, 만약 그렇다면, 내 감정과(그는 그들 앞에서 사랑이라고는 말할 수 없었다) 행복과는 별개로, 자유를 잃는 데 대한 어떤 아쉬움을 조금이라도 느꼈어야 마땅하잖나…… 그런데 오히려 나는 자유를 잃는 바로 그것이 기쁘단 말이야.\"\n\n\"끔찍하군! 가망 없는 경우야!\" 카타바소프가 말했다. \"자, 그럼 그의 회복을 위해 건배하든가, 아니면 그의 꿈이 백분의 일이라도 이루어지기를 빌어 줍시다. 그렇게만 된다면 이 땅에서 일찍이 본 적 없는 행복일 테니까요!\"\n\n저녁 식사가 끝나고 얼마 지나지 않아 손님들은 결혼식을 위해 옷을 갈아입을 시간에 맞추려고 자리를 떴다.\n\n혼자 남은 레빈은 그 독신 친구들과 나눈 대화를 떠올리며 스스로에게 물었다. 과연 자기 마음속에 그들이 말한 그 자유에 대한 아쉬움이 있는가? 그는 그 물음에 미소 지었다. '자유라니! 자유가 다 무슨 소용이야? 행복은 오로지 그녀를 사랑하고, 그녀가 바라는 것을 바라고, 그녀가 생각하는 것을 생각하는 데에만 있어. 그러니까 그건 전혀 자유가 아닌 거지. 바로 그게 행복이야!'\n\n'하지만 내가 그녀의 생각을, 그녀의 바람을, 그녀의 감정을 알기나 하는가?' 어떤 목소리가 별안간 그에게 속삭였다. 미소가 그의 얼굴에서 사라졌고, 그는 생각에 잠겼다. 그러다 문득 이상한 감정이 그를 덮쳐 왔다. 두려움과 의심이, 모든 것에 대한 의심이 그를 사로잡았다.\n\n'만약 그녀가 나를 사랑하지 않는다면? 만약 그녀가 그저 결혼을 하기 위해 나와 결혼하는 거라면? 만약 그녀가 자기가 무슨 짓을 하고 있는지조차 모르고 있다면?' 그는 스스로에게 물었다. '그녀는 정신을 차리고서, 막상 결혼을 하는 순간에야 자기가 나를 사랑하지 않으며 사랑할 수도 없다는 걸 깨달을지도 몰라.' 그러자 그녀에 대한 이상하고 더없이 사악한 생각들이 그에게 떠오르기 시작했다. 그는 일 년 전과 마찬가지로, 마치 그녀가 브론스키와 함께 있는 것을 본 그날 저녁이 바로 어제 일이기라도 한 듯이 브론스키에게 질투를 느꼈다. 그는 그녀가 자기에게 모든 것을 다 말하지 않았으리라고 의심했다.\n\n그는 벌떡 일어섰다. '안 돼, 이대로는 안 돼!' 그는 절망에 빠져 스스로에게 말했다. '그녀에게 가야겠어. 물어봐야겠어. 마지막으로 이렇게 말하겠어. 우리는 자유로운 몸이니, 차라리 이대로 있는 편이 낫지 않겠느냐고. 끝없는 비참함과 치욕과 부정함보다는 무엇이든 나으니까!' 마음속에 절망을 품고, 모든 사람에 대한, 자기 자신에 대한, 그녀에 대한 쓰디쓴 분노를 안은 채 그는 호텔을 나와 그녀의 집으로 마차를 몰았다.\n\n그는 안쪽 방 가운데 한 곳에서 그녀를 찾아냈다. 그녀는 궤짝 위에 앉아 하녀와 무언가를 정리하며, 의자 등받이와 바닥에 펼쳐 놓은 갖가지 색깔의 드레스 더미를 가려내고 있었다.\n\n\"아!\" 그를 보자 그녀는 기쁨으로 환히 빛나며 소리쳤다. \"코스챠! 콘스탄틴 드미트리예비치!\"(요즘 며칠 동안 그녀는 이 두 이름을 거의 번갈아 부르고 있었다.) \"당신이 올 줄은 몰랐어요! 어느 게 누구 줄 건지 보려고 옷장을 정리하고 있던 참이에요……\"\n\n\"오! 그것 참 좋은 일이군.\" 그는 하녀를 바라보며 침울하게 말했다.\n\n\"두냐샤, 가도 좋아, 곧 부를게.\" 키티가 말했다. \"코스챠, 무슨 일이에요?\" 하녀가 나가자마자 그녀는 이 다정한 이름을 분명하게 골라 부르며 물었다. 그녀는 동요하고 침울한 그의 이상한 얼굴을 알아챘고, 공포가 그녀를 덮쳤다.\n\n\"키티! 나는 고통스러워. 혼자서는 견딜 수가 없어.\" 그는 절망이 담긴 목소리로 그녀 앞에 선 채 애원하듯 그녀의 눈을 들여다보며 말했다. 그는 이미 그녀의 사랑스럽고 진실한 얼굴에서, 자기가 하려던 말이 아무런 결과도 낳지 못하리라는 것을 알았지만, 그래도 그녀가 직접 자기를 안심시켜 주기를 바랐다. \"아직 시간이 있다는 걸 말하러 왔어. 이 모든 걸 멈추고 바로잡을 수 있어.\"\n\n\"뭐라고요? 무슨 말인지 모르겠어요. 무슨 일인데요?\"\n\n\"내가 천 번도 더 말했고 또 생각하지 않을 수 없는 것…… 내가 당신에게 어울리지 않는다는 거야. 당신이 나와 결혼하기로 한 건 동의할 수 없는 일이었어. 좀 생각해 봐. 당신은 잘못 생각한 거야. 곰곰이 다시 생각해 봐. 당신은 나를 사랑할 수 없어…… 만약…… 차라리 그렇다고 말해 줘.\" 그는 그녀를 보지 않은 채 말했다. \"나는 비참해지겠지. 사람들이 뭐라고 하든 내버려 둬. 비참함보다는 무엇이든 나으니까…… 아직 시간이 있는 지금이 훨씬 나아……\"\n\n\"무슨 말인지 모르겠어요.\" 그녀는 공포에 질려 대답했다. \"그러니까 그만두고 싶다는…… 원하지 않는다는 말인가요?\"\n\n\"그래, 당신이 나를 사랑하지 않는다면.\"\n\n\"당신 제정신이 아니에요!\" 그녀는 분해서 새빨개지며 소리쳤다. 하지만 그의 얼굴이 너무도 가여워서, 그녀는 분한 마음을 억누르고 안락의자에서 옷가지 몇 벌을 치우더니 그의 곁에 앉았다. \"무슨 생각을 하고 있는 거예요? 다 말해 봐요.\"\n\n\"당신이 나를 사랑할 수 없을 거라고 생각하고 있어. 무엇 때문에 나를 사랑할 수 있겠어?\"\n\n\"맙소사! 내가 어떻게 해야 하나요?……\" 그녀는 이렇게 말하고는 울음을 터뜨렸다.\n\n\"오! 내가 무슨 짓을 한 거지?\" 그가 소리치며 그녀 앞에 무릎을 꿇고 그녀의 두 손에 입을 맞추기 시작했다.\n\n오 분 뒤 공작 부인이 방에 들어왔을 때, 두 사람은 완전히 화해해 있었다. 키티는 단순히 그를 사랑한다고 안심시켰을 뿐 아니라, 무엇 때문에 자기를 사랑하느냐는 그의 물음에 무엇 때문인지를 설명하는 데까지 나아갔다. 그녀는 자기가 그를 완전히 이해하기 때문에, 그가 무엇을 좋아할지 알기 때문에, 그리고 그가 좋아하는 모든 것이 다 좋기 때문에 그를 사랑한다고 말했다. 그리고 이 말이 그에게는 더없이 분명하게 여겨졌다. 공작 부인이 그들에게 다가왔을 때, 두 사람은 궤짝 위에 나란히 앉아 드레스를 가려내며 다투고 있었는데, 키티는 레빈이 청혼하던 날 입고 있던 갈색 드레스를 두냐샤에게 주려 했고, 그는 그 드레스만큼은 절대 남에게 줘서는 안 되며 두냐샤에게는 파란 드레스를 주어야 한다고 우기고 있었다.\n\n\"어째서 모르는 거예요? 그 애는 살결이 검어서 그 옷이 어울리지 않을 거예요…… 내가 다 생각해 뒀어요.\"\n\n그가 무엇 때문에 왔는지 듣고서 공작 부인은 반은 우스워하고 반은 진지하게 그에게 화를 내며, 마침 머리 손질사 샤를이 오는 참이니 키티의 머리 손질을 방해하지 말고 집으로 가서 옷을 갈아입으라고 그를 돌려보냈다.\n\n\"안 그래도 저 애가 요즘 통 먹질 않아서 얼굴이 상하고 있는데, 자네까지 와서 그런 헛소리로 애를 뒤집어 놓다니.\" 그녀가 그에게 말했다. \"어서 가게나, 이 사람아!\"\n\n레빈은 죄스럽고 부끄러웠지만 마음이 진정되어 호텔로 돌아갔다. 그의 형과 다리야 알렉산드로브나, 그리고 스테판 아르카지치가 모두 정장을 갖춰 입고서, 성화로 그를 축복해 주려고 그를 기다리고 있었다. 지체할 시간이 없었다. 다리야 알렉산드로브나는 신부 뒤에서 성화를 들고 갈 아들을 데려오기 위해 다시 집으로 마차를 몰고 가야 했는데, 아이는 머리를 곱슬곱슬하게 말고 포마드를 바른 상태였다. 그러고 나서 들러리를 데리러 마차 한 대를 보내야 했고, 세르게이 이바노비치를 태우고 갈 또 다른 마차는 다시 돌려보내야 했다…… 이래저래 따지고 정리해야 할 복잡한 일들이 무척 많았다. 한 가지 분명한 것은, 이미 여섯 시 반이니 지체해서는 안 된다는 것이었다.\n\n성화로 축복하는 의식에서는 별다른 일이 일어나지 않았다. 스테판 아르카지치는 우스꽝스러울 만큼 엄숙한 자세로 아내 곁에 서서 성화를 들고는, 레빈에게 땅에 닿도록 절하라고 이르고서 다정하면서도 빈정거리는 듯한 미소를 띤 채 그를 축복하고 세 번 입을 맞추었다. 다리야 알렉산드로브나도 똑같이 하고는 곧바로 서둘러 떠나려 했고, 다시 여러 마차의 행선지라는 그 복잡한 문제 속으로 빠져들었다.\n\n\"자, 어떻게 할지 말해 줄게. 자네는 우리 마차를 타고 가서 그를 데려오고, 세르게이 이바노비치가 친절을 베풀어 주신다면 거기까지 가셨다가 마차를 돌려보내 주시면 돼.\"\n\n\"물론이지, 기꺼이 그렇게 하겠네.\"\n\n\"우리는 곧장 그와 함께 갈 거야. 자네 짐은 다 보냈나?\" 스테판 아르카지치가 말했다.\n\n\"응.\" 레빈이 대답하고는, 쿠지마에게 자기가 갈아입을 옷을 꺼내 놓으라고 일렀다.\n\n# Chapter 3\n\n결혼식을 위해 환하게 불을 밝힌 교회 주위로 사람들이, 주로 여자들이 떼를 지어 몰려들고 있었다. 정문으로 들어가지 못한 이들은 창가로 몰려가 서로 밀치고 다투며 창살 틈으로 안을 들여다보았다.\n\n이미 스무 대가 넘는 마차들이 경찰의 통제에 따라 거리를 따라 줄지어 늘어서 있었다. 한 경관이 추위에도 아랑곳없이 화려한 제복 차림으로 입구에 서 있었다. 마차들은 끊임없이 들어왔고, 꽃을 단 채 드레스 자락을 받쳐 든 부인들과 투구나 검은 모자를 벗는 신사들이 줄지어 교회 안으로 걸어 들어왔다. 교회 안에는 이미 양쪽 샹들리에에 불이 들어와 있었고, 성화 앞의 촛불도 모조리 켜져 있었다. 성화대의 붉은 바탕에 입혀진 금박이며, 성화에 새겨진 금빛 부조며, 샹들리에와 촛대의 은빛이며, 바닥의 돌이며, 양탄자며, 위쪽 성가대석의 깃발이며, 제단의 계단이며, 검게 변한 낡은 책들이며, 사제복과 흰 제의까지—모든 것이 빛으로 흘러넘쳤다. 따뜻한 교회의 오른편에서는 프록코트와 흰 넥타이, 제복과 고급 나사천, 벨벳, 새틴, 머리 장식과 꽃, 드러난 어깨와 팔과 긴 장갑이 뒤섞인 무리 속에서, 높은 둥근 천장에 묘하게 울리는 조심스럽지만 활기찬 대화 소리가 오갔다. 문이 열리며 삐걱대는 소리가 들릴 때마다 사람들 사이의 대화는 잦아들었고, 모두가 신랑 신부가 들어오기를 기대하며 돌아보았다. 그러나 문은 열 번도 넘게 열렸지만, 그때마다 들어온 것은 오른편 초대객 무리에 합류하는 뒤늦은 손님이거나, 경관을 따돌리거나 구슬려 왼편의 구경꾼 무리에 끼어드는 구경꾼이었다. 초대객들도 바깥의 구경꾼들도 이제는 기다림의 온갖 단계를 다 거친 뒤였다.\n\n처음에 사람들은 신랑 신부가 곧 도착하리라 생각하며 그들이 늦는 데에 전혀 의미를 두지 않았다. 그러다 점점 더 자주 문 쪽을 바라보며 무슨 일이라도 생긴 게 아닌지 이야기하기 시작했다. 그러다 그 긴 지체가 정말이지 불편해지기 시작했고, 친척들과 손님들은 신랑 생각은 하지 않는 척, 대화에 푹 빠진 척하려고 애썼다.\n\n수석 부제는 자기 시간의 소중함을 일깨우기라도 하려는 듯 못마땅하게 기침을 했고, 그 바람에 창문 유리가 틀 안에서 떨렸다. 성가대석에서는 지루해진 성가대원들이 목소리를 가다듬고 코를 푸는 소리가 들렸다. 사제는 신랑이 도착하지 않았는지 알아보라며 끊임없이 처음엔 교회지기를, 그다음엔 부제를 보냈고, 점점 더 자주 직접 라일락빛 제의에 수놓은 띠를 두른 채 옆문으로 가서 신랑이 보이기를 기대했다. 마침내 부인들 가운데 한 사람이 시계를 보며 \"정말이지, 이상하네요!\" 하고 말하자, 손님들 모두가 안절부절못하며 큰 소리로 놀라움과 불만을 토로하기 시작했다. 신랑의 들러리 가운데 한 사람이 무슨 일이 생긴 건지 알아보러 갔다. 한편 키티는 이미 오래전에 채비를 다 마치고, 흰 드레스에 긴 면사포를 쓰고 오렌지꽃 화관을 두른 채, 신부의 어머니 역할을 맡은 언니 리보바 부인과 함께 셰르바츠키 가의 응접실에 서 있었다. 그녀는 창밖을 내다보며, 신랑이 교회에 도착했다는 소식을 들러리한테서 듣기를 반 시간이 넘도록 애타게 기다리고 있었다.\n\n한편 레빈은 바지만 입은 채 코트와 조끼는 걸치지 않고 호텔 방 안을 이리저리 서성이며, 연신 문밖으로 머리를 내밀어 복도의 이쪽저쪽을 살폈다. 그러나 복도에는 그가 찾는 사람의 그림자도 보이지 않았고, 그는 절망에 빠져 돌아와서는 미친 듯이 두 손을 내저으며, 태연하게 담배를 피우고 있는 스테판 아르카지치를 향해 말을 쏟아 냈다.\n\n\"이런 끔찍한 바보 꼴이 된 사람이 또 있었을까?\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 한심한 일이지.\" 스테판 아르카지치는 달래듯 미소 지으며 동의했다. \"하지만 걱정 마, 곧 가져올 테니까.\"\n\n\"아니, 어쩌면 좋아!\" 레빈이 억눌린 분노로 말했다. \"게다가 이 빌어먹을 트인 조끼라니! 도저히 안 돼!\" 그는 구겨진 셔츠 앞섶을 바라보며 말했다. \"그것들이 정거장으로 실려 가 버렸으면 어쩌지!\" 그가 절망에 차서 고함쳤다.\n\n\"그럼 내 걸 입어야지.\"\n\n\"그렇게 할 거였으면 진작에 그랬어야지.\"\n\n\"우스꽝스러운 꼴을 보이는 건 좋지 않아…… 잠깐만! 잘 해결될 거야.\"\n\n사정은 이러했다. 레빈이 예복을 가져오라고 하자, 그의 늙은 하인 쿠지마가 코트와 조끼와 필요한 것들을 다 가져왔던 것이다.\n\n\"그런데 셔츠는!\" 레빈이 외쳤다.\n\n\"셔츠는 입고 계시잖습니까.\" 쿠지마가 차분한 미소를 띠며 대답했다.\n\n쿠지마는 깨끗한 셔츠를 따로 챙겨 두어야겠다는 생각을 미처 하지 못했고, 모든 것을 꾸려서 그날 저녁 신랑 신부가 함께 출발하기로 한 셰르바츠키 가로 보내라는 지시를 받자, 그대로 따라서 예복만 빼고 모든 것을 꾸려 보냈던 것이다. 아침부터 입고 있던 셔츠는 구겨져서 유행에 맞는 트인 조끼와는 도저히 어울리지 않았다. 셰르바츠키 가까지는 사람을 보내기에 거리가 멀었다. 그들은 셔츠를 사 오라고 사람을 내보냈다. 하인이 돌아왔는데, 모든 가게가 문을 닫았다고 했다—일요일이었던 것이다. 스테판 아르카지치 집으로 사람을 보내 셔츠를 가져왔지만—말도 안 되게 통이 넓고 길이가 짧았다. 결국 그들은 셰르바츠키 가로 사람을 보내 짐을 풀게 했다. 신랑은 교회에서 기다림을 받고 있는데, 정작 그는 우리에 갇힌 들짐승처럼 방 안을 서성이며 복도를 기웃거렸고, 자기가 키티에게 무슨 터무니없는 말을 했는지, 그리고 지금 그녀가 무슨 생각을 하고 있을지 떠올리며 공포와 절망에 빠져 있었다.\n\n마침내 죄지은 쿠지마가 셔츠를 들고 헐떡이며 방으로 날아들었다.\n\n\"아슬아슬하게 댔습니다. 막 짐칸에 싣고 있던 참이었어요.\" 쿠지마가 말했다.\n\n삼 분 뒤 레빈은 괴로움을 더할까 두려워 시계도 보지 않은 채 전속력으로 복도로 뛰쳐나갔다.\n\n\"이런다고 일이 해결되진 않아.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 미소를 띠며 좀 더 느긋한 걸음으로 그의 뒤를 서둘러 따라가며 말했다. \"잘될 거야, 잘될 거라니까…… 내 말 믿어.\"\n\n# Chapter 4\n\n\"왔다!\" \"신랑이다!\" \"어느 쪽이야?\" \"꽤 젊네, 그렇지?\" \"어머, 세상에, 신부 얼굴이 산 사람보다 죽은 사람 같지 않니!\" 레빈이 입구에서 신부를 맞아 함께 교회 안으로 들어설 때, 군중 사이에서 이런 말들이 오갔다.\n\n스테판 아르카지치는 아내에게 늦어진 까닭을 일러 주었고, 하객들은 미소를 지으며 그 이야기를 서로 소곤거렸다. 레빈에게는 아무것도, 아무도 보이지 않았다. 그는 신부에게서 눈을 떼지 않았다.\n\n사람들은 그녀가 요즘 들어 부쩍 야위었으며, 결혼식 날인데도 평소만큼 예쁘지 않다고들 했다. 하지만 레빈은 그렇게 생각하지 않았다. 그는 높이 틀어 올린 그녀의 머리며, 긴 흰 베일과 흰 꽃이며, 옆에서는 처녀답게 긴 목을 가리고 앞에서만 드러내는 높직한 가리비 무늬 깃이며, 놀랍도록 가냘픈 자태를 바라보았다. 그에게는 그녀가 그 어느 때보다 아름다워 보였다. 이 꽃이며 이 베일이며 파리에서 온 이 드레스가 그녀의 아름다움에 무언가를 보태서가 아니라, 공들여 차려입은 화려한 의상에도 불구하고 그녀의 사랑스러운 얼굴이며 눈이며 입술의 표정이 여전히 거짓 없는 진실함이라는 그녀만의 표정 그대로였기 때문이다.\n\n\"당신이 도망쳐 버린 줄 알았어요.\" 그녀가 그에게 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"나한테 일어난 일이 너무 어처구니없어서, 말하기도 부끄러워요!\" 그는 얼굴을 붉히며 말했고, 마침 다가온 세르게이 이바노비치에게로 몸을 돌려야 했다.\n\n\"셔츠 이야기는 참 가관이더군!\" 세르게이 이바노비치가 고개를 가로저으며 미소 지었다.\n\n\"네, 네!\" 레빈은 무슨 이야기를 하는지도 모른 채 대답했다.\n\n\"자, 코스챠, 자네가 결정해야 해.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 짐짓 난처한 척하며 말했다. \"중대한 문제야. 지금 이 순간 자네야말로 그 무게를 제대로 알아줄 기분일 테지. 사람들이 내게 묻는단 말이야, 전에 한 번 켰던 양초를 켜야 하느냐, 아니면 한 번도 안 켠 새 양초를 켜야 하느냐고. 십 루블이 걸린 문제야.\" 그는 입가를 풀고 미소 지으며 덧붙였다. \"난 이미 결정했지만, 자네가 동의하지 않을까 봐 걱정이었어.\"\n\n레빈은 그것이 농담임을 알았지만 미소 지을 수 없었다.\n\n\"자, 그래서 어떻게 할까? 안 켠 양초야, 켰던 양초야? 그게 문제야.\"\n\n\"네, 네, 안 켠 걸로.\"\n\n\"오, 정말 다행이군. 문제가 해결됐어!\" 스테판 아르카지치가 미소 지으며 말했다. 레빈이 멍하니 그를 바라보다가 다시 신부 곁으로 물러나자, 그는 치리코프에게 말했다. \"그런데 이런 처지에 놓이면 사내들이 어쩜 이렇게 멍청해지는지.\"\n\n\"키티, 카펫에 네가 제일 먼저 발을 디뎌야 한다는 거 잊지 마.\" 노르드스톤 백작 부인이 다가오며 말했다. \"당신은 참 좋은 사람이에요!\" 그녀가 레빈에게 말했다.\n\n\"안 무섭니, 응?\" 늙은 숙모 마리야 드미트리예브나가 말했다.\n\n\"춥니? 얼굴이 창백하구나. 잠깐만, 고개를 좀 숙여 봐.\" 키티의 언니 리보바 부인이 말하며 통통하고 어여쁜 두 팔로 미소 지으며 동생 머리 위의 꽃을 매만져 주었다.\n\n돌리가 다가와 무슨 말을 하려 했지만 말이 나오지 않았고, 울다가 부자연스럽게 웃었다.\n\n키티는 레빈과 똑같이 멍한 눈으로 그들 모두를 바라보았다.\n\n그러는 사이 집전하는 성직자들이 제의를 갖춰 입었고, 사제와 부제가 교회 앞쪽에 놓인 성서대로 나왔다. 사제가 레빈을 향해 무어라 말했다. 레빈은 사제가 무슨 말을 하는지 듣지 못했다.\n\n\"신부의 손을 잡고 모시고 오십시오.\" 들러리가 레빈에게 말했다.\n\n레빈이 자기에게 무엇이 요구되는지 알아차리기까지는 한참이 걸렸다. 그가 자꾸 키티의 엉뚱한 팔을 잡거나 엉뚱한 팔로 잡았기 때문에, 사람들은 오랫동안 그를 바로잡아 주고 다시 시작하게 했다. 마침내 그는 자기 위치를 바꾸지 않은 채 오른손으로 그녀의 오른손을 잡아야 한다는 것을 깨달았다. 마침내 그가 올바른 방식으로 신부의 손을 잡자, 사제가 그들 앞으로 몇 걸음 걸어가 성서대 앞에 멈춰 섰다. 친구들과 친척들의 무리가 두런거리는 말소리와 치맛자락 스치는 소리를 내며 그들을 뒤따라 움직였다. 누군가 몸을 숙여 신부의 끌리는 옷자락을 펴 주었다. 교회 안이 어찌나 고요해졌던지 양초에서 밀랍 방울이 떨어지는 소리까지 들릴 정도였다.\n\n성직자용 모자를 쓴 자그마한 노사제는, 귀 뒤로 가른 긴 은회색 머리채를 늘어뜨린 채, 등에 금빛 십자가가 새겨진 묵직한 은빛 제의 아래로 자그마한 늙은 두 손을 내밀어 성서대에서 무언가를 더듬거리고 있었다.\n\n스테판 아르카지치가 조심스레 그에게 다가가 무어라 속삭이고는, 레빈에게 신호를 보낸 뒤 다시 제자리로 걸어왔다.\n\n사제는 꽃으로 두른 양초 두 자루에 불을 붙이고는, 밀랍이 천천히 떨어지도록 비스듬히 든 채 신랑 신부를 향해 몸을 돌렸다. 그 사제는 전에 레빈의 고해를 받았던 바로 그 노인이었다. 그는 지치고 우수에 잠긴 눈으로 신부와 신랑을 바라보고는 한숨을 쉰 뒤, 제의 아래로 오른손을 내밀어 신랑을 축복하고, 또한 자애로운 다정함을 살짝 머금은 채 십자로 모은 손가락을 키티의 숙인 머리 위에 얹었다. 그러고는 그들에게 양초를 건네고, 향로를 들어 그들에게서 천천히 멀어졌다.\n\n'정말일까?' 레빈은 생각하며 신부를 돌아보았다. 그녀를 내려다보다가 옆얼굴을 보게 되었는데, 입술과 속눈썹이 거의 알아챌 수 없을 만큼 떨리는 것을 보고 그녀가 자기 시선을 의식하고 있음을 알았다. 그녀는 돌아보지 않았지만, 그녀의 작고 분홍빛인 귀에까지 닿는 높직한 가리비 무늬 깃이 희미하게 떨렸다. 그는 그녀의 목구멍에서 한숨이 참아지는 것을, 긴 장갑 속의 작은 손이 양초를 든 채 떨리는 것을 보았다.\n\n셔츠 소동이며, 늦은 것이며, 친구들과 친척들의 온갖 말이며, 그들의 짜증이며, 자신의 우스꽝스러운 처지며—이 모든 것이 별안간 사라지고, 그는 기쁨과 두려움으로 가득 찼다.\n\n은빛 제의를 걸치고 곱슬머리를 머리 양옆으로 뻗친 잘생기고 위엄 있는 수석 부제가 날렵하게 앞으로 나서, 두 손가락으로 영대를 받쳐 들고 사제 맞은편에 섰다.\n\n\"주님의 이름이 찬양받으소서.\" 엄숙한 음절들이 하나하나 천천히 울려 퍼지며, 소리의 물결로 공기를 떨게 했다.\n\n\"우리 하느님의 이름이 태초로부터 찬양받으시며, 지금도 그러하시고 영원토록 그러하시리이다.\" 자그마한 노사제가 순종하는 듯한 가느다란 목소리로, 여전히 성서대에서 무언가를 더듬거리며 응답했다. 그러자 보이지 않는 성가대의 충만한 합창이 솟아올라, 창문부터 둥근 천장에 이르기까지 온 교회를 넓은 선율의 물결로 가득 채웠다. 그 소리는 점점 강해지다가 한순간 멎었고, 천천히 잦아들었다.\n\n그들은 늘 그러하듯 높은 곳에서 내려오는 평화와 구원을 위하여, 거룩한 종교 회의를 위하여, 차르를 위하여 기도했다. 또한 이제 서로 백년가약을 맺는 하느님의 종 콘스탄틴과 예카테리나를 위하여 기도했다.\n\n\"주여, 비옵나니 그들에게 온전한 사랑과 평화와 도우심을 베푸소서.\" 온 교회가 수석 부제의 목소리에 실려 숨을 쉬는 듯했다.\n\n레빈은 그 말을 듣고 마음 깊이 새겼다. '저들은 어떻게 사람이 원하는 게 바로 도움이라는 걸, 다른 게 아니라 도움이라는 걸 알았을까?' 그는 요즘 들어 느꼈던 온갖 두려움과 의혹을 떠올리며 생각했다. '도움 없이 이 무서운 일에서 내가 뭘 알고, 뭘 할 수 있단 말인가.' 그는 생각했다. '그래, 내가 지금 원하는 건 도움이야.'\n\n부제가 황실을 위한 기도를 마치자, 사제가 책을 들고 신랑 신부를 향해 돌아섰다. \"갈라져 있던 자들을 사랑으로 하나 되게 하시는 영원하신 하느님이시여.\" 그는 부드럽고 가느다란 목소리로 읽었다. \"갈라놓을 수 없는 거룩한 혼인의 결합을 정하셨고, 당신의 거룩한 언약을 따라 이삭과 리브가와 그들의 후손을 축복하신 주여, 당신의 종 콘스탄틴과 예카테리나를 축복하시어 그들을 온갖 선한 일의 길로 이끄소서. 자비롭고 인자하신 주여, 성부와 성자와 성령께 지금도 영원토록 영광이 있을지어다.\"\n\n\"아멘!\" 보이지 않는 성가대가 다시금 공중에 그 소리를 굴려 보냈다.\n\n'갈라져 있던 자들을 사랑으로 하나 되게 하신다.' 그 말 속에 얼마나 깊은 뜻이 있는가, 그리고 이 순간 느끼는 감정과 얼마나 잘 들어맞는가.' 레빈은 생각했다. '그녀도 나와 같은 걸 느끼고 있을까?'\n\n그리고 돌아보다가 그녀의 눈과 마주쳤고, 그 눈빛에서 그녀도 자기와 똑같이 그것을 이해하고 있다고 결론지었다. 하지만 이는 착각이었다. 그녀는 예식의 말뜻을 거의 완전히 놓치고 있었다. 사실 그녀는 그 말을 듣지조차 못했다. 그녀의 가슴을 채우고 점점 더 강해지는 그 한 가지 감정이 너무도 강렬해서, 그녀는 그것을 듣고 받아들일 수 없었다. 그 감정은 지난 한 달 반 동안 그녀의 영혼 속에서 진행되어 온 과정이, 그 여섯 주 내내 그녀에게 기쁨이자 고통이었던 그 과정이 완결된 데 대한 기쁨이었다. 아르바티 거리 저택의 응접실에서 갈색 드레스를 입고 그에게 다가가 말없이 자신을 내맡겼던 그날, 바로 그 시각, 그녀의 마음속에서는 옛 삶 전체와의 완전한 단절이 일어났고, 전혀 다른, 새로운, 더없이 낯선 삶이 그녀에게 시작되었다. 그러면서도 옛 삶은 실제로는 예전처럼 이어지고 있었다. 그 여섯 주는 그녀에게 더없는 행복이자 더없는 비참함의 시간이었다. 그녀의 온 삶이, 온갖 욕망과 희망이 이 한 남자에게 집중되어 있었다. 그녀는 아직 그를 다 이해하지 못했고, 그를 향한 끌림과 밀어냄이 번갈아 일어나는 감정—그 남자 자신보다도 더 이해할 수 없는 감정—으로 그에게 묶여 있었으며, 그러는 내내 옛 삶의 외적 조건 속에서 계속 살아가고 있었다. 옛 삶을 살면서 그녀는 자기 자신에게, 자신의 모든 과거에 대해, 사물에 대해, 습관에 대해, 자기가 사랑했고 자기를 사랑해 준 사람들에 대해—자신의 무관심에 상처받은 어머니에 대해, 그때까지 세상 누구보다 소중했던 다정하고 자상한 아버지에 대해—느끼는 도무지 넘어설 수 없는 냉담함에 소스라치게 놀랐다. 어느 순간에는 이 냉담함에 소스라치게 놀랐고, 또 어느 순간에는 자기를 이 냉담함으로 이끈 것을 기뻐했다. 그녀는 이 남자와의 삶을 떠나서는 어떤 생각도, 어떤 소망도 떠올릴 수 없었다. 하지만 이 새로운 삶은 아직 오지 않았고, 그녀는 그것을 또렷이 그려 볼 수조차 없었다. 오직 기대만이, 새롭고 알 수 없는 것에 대한 두려움과 기쁨만이 있을 뿐이었다. 그리고 이제 보라—기대와 불확실함과 옛 삶을 버리는 데 대한 회한, 그 모든 것이 끝나가고 새것이 시작되고 있었다. 이 새로운 삶이 미숙한 그녀에게 두려움을 안기지 않을 수 없었지만, 두렵든 아니든 그 변화는 여섯 주 전에 이미 그녀의 영혼 속에서 이루어졌으며, 이것은 그저 오래전에 그녀의 마음속에서 완결된 것에 대한 마지막 추인일 뿐이었다.\n\n다시 성서대를 향해 돌아선 사제는 다소 힘겹게 키티의 작은 반지를 집어 들고, 레빈에게 손을 달라고 한 뒤 그의 손가락 첫 마디에 끼웠다. \"하느님의 종 콘스탄틴이 하느님의 종 예카테리나에게 백년가약을 맺나이다.\" 그러고는 키티의 가련하리만치 가냘프고 분홍빛인 작은 손가락에 그의 큰 반지를 끼우며, 사제는 같은 말을 했다.\n\n신랑 신부는 자기들이 무엇을 해야 하는지 여러 번 알아내려 애썼지만, 그때마다 무언가 실수를 했고 사제가 속삭이며 바로잡아 주었다. 마침내 예식을 제대로 마치고 반지를 십자로 축성한 뒤, 사제는 키티에게 큰 반지를, 레빈에게 작은 반지를 건넸다. 그들은 또다시 어리둥절해하며 반지를 손에서 손으로 주고받았지만, 여전히 요구되는 대로 하지 못했다.\n\n돌리와 치리코프와 스테판 아르카지치가 그들을 바로잡아 주려고 앞으로 나섰다. 잠시 망설임과 소곤거림과 미소가 오갔지만, 약혼한 두 사람의 얼굴에 어린 엄숙한 감동의 표정은 변하지 않았다. 오히려 손을 두고 쩔쩔매는 그 와중에 그들은 전보다 더 진지하고 깊이 감동한 듯 보였고, 이제 각자 자기 반지를 끼우면 된다고 그들에게 속삭이던 스테판 아르카지치의 미소는 입가에서 사라져 버렸다. 그는 어떤 미소든 그들에게 거슬릴 것만 같은 느낌이 들었다.\n\n\"태초에 남자와 여자를 지으신 주여.\" 사제는 반지를 주고받은 뒤 읽었다. \"당신으로부터 여자가 남자에게 돕는 배필이 되고 자손을 낳도록 주어졌나이다. 우리 주 하느님이시여, 당신의 거룩한 언약을 따라 당신이 택하신 종들, 우리 조상들에게 대대로 당신의 진리의 복을 부어 주신 주여, 당신의 종 콘스탄틴과 예카테리나를 축복하시어, 그들의 가약을 믿음과 마음의 하나 됨과 진리와 사랑 안에 굳건히 하소서…….\"\n\n레빈은 결혼에 대한 자신의 온갖 생각이, 어떻게 삶을 꾸려 가겠다던 온갖 꿈이 그저 어린아이 같은 것이었음을, 그리고 그것이 여태껏 자기가 이해하지 못했고 지금은 그 어느 때보다 더 이해할 수 없는 무언가임을, 비록 그 일이 자기에게 행해지고 있는데도 그러함을 점점 더 절실히 느꼈다. 목구멍에 치미는 덩어리가 점점 더 높이 솟아올랐고, 억누를 수 없는 눈물이 그의 눈에 고였다.\n\n# Chapter 5\n\n교회 안에는 온 모스크바가, 모든 친구들과 친척들이 모여 있었다. 그리고 약혼 의식이 거행되는 동안, 환하게 불을 밝힌 교회 안에서는 화려하게 차려입은 부인들과 아가씨들, 그리고 흰 넥타이에 프록코트나 제복을 입은 남자들의 무리 사이로 조심스레 낮춘 이야기 소리가 끊임없이 흘러나왔다. 이야기는 주로 남자들이 이어 갔고, 여자들은 그네들에게 늘 그토록 의미가 큰 의식의 자질구레한 부분 하나하나를 지켜보는 데 온통 정신을 쏟고 있었다.\n\n신부에게 가장 가까운 작은 무리에는 그녀의 두 언니가 있었다. 돌리와, 또 한 사람, 침착하고 아름다운 르보바 부인이었는데, 그녀는 막 외국에서 돌아온 참이었다.\n\n\"왜 마리는 결혼식에 검은색이나 다름없는 연보라색을 입었을까?\" 코르순스카야 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"그 사람 안색에는 그게 유일한 구원이거든요.\" 트루베츠카야 부인이 대꾸했다. \"그런데 왜 저녁에 식을 올렸을까요? 꼭 장사꾼들 같잖아요…….\"\n\n\"그게 훨씬 더 예쁜걸요. 나도 저녁에 결혼했답니다…….\" 코르순스카야 부인이 대답하며 한숨을 내쉬었다. 그날 자기가 얼마나 매력적이었는지, 남편이 얼마나 우스꽝스러울 만큼 자기에게 빠져 있었는지, 그리고 지금은 모든 것이 얼마나 달라졌는지를 떠올리면서.\n\n\"누구든 들러리를 열 번 넘게 서면 영영 결혼을 못 한대요. 나는 열 번째로 서고 싶었는데, 그 자리를 빼앗겨 버렸지 뭡니까.\" 시니아빈 백작이 자기에게 마음을 두고 있던 예쁜 차르스카야 공작 영애에게 말했다.\n\n차르스카야 공작 영애는 그저 미소로 대답할 뿐이었다. 그녀는 키티를 바라보면서, 자기가 어떻게, 또 언제 키티의 자리에 시니아빈 백작과 나란히 서게 될지, 그리고 그때 그에게 오늘의 이 농담을 어떻게 일깨워 줄지를 생각했다.\n\n시체르바츠키는 늙은 궁녀 니콜라예바 부인에게, 행운을 빌어 주려고 키티의 시뇽 위에 관을 씌워 줄 작정이라고 말했다.\n\n\"시뇽을 하지 말았어야 했어요.\" 니콜라예바 부인이 대답했다. 그녀는 자기가 노리고 있는 그 나이 든 홀아비가 자기와 결혼하게 된다면 식은 더없이 소박하게 치러야겠다고 오래전부터 마음먹고 있었다. \"난 그런 화려한 게 싫어요.\"\n\n세르게이 이바노비치는 다리야 드미트리예브나에게, 결혼식이 끝나면 곧장 떠나 버리는 풍습이 점점 흔해지는 건 갓 결혼한 사람들이 늘 스스로를 조금 부끄러워하기 때문이라며 농담조로 장담하고 있었다.\n\n\"동생분은 자랑스러워해도 되겠어요. 신부는 더없이 사랑스러운 보물이잖아요. 당신은 부러운 거 아니에요?\"\n\n\"오, 그런 마음은 이미 다 지나갔답니다, 다리야 드미트리예브나.\" 그가 대답했고, 우울하고 진지한 표정이 별안간 그의 얼굴에 떠올랐다.\n\n스테판 아르카지치는 손위 처형에게 이혼에 관한 자기 농담을 늘어놓고 있었다.\n\n\"화관을 똑바로 고쳐야겠어요.\" 그녀는 그의 말을 듣지도 않은 채 대답했다.\n\n\"저렇게 미모가 상해 버리다니 안됐어요.\" 노르드스톤 백작 부인이 르보바 부인에게 말했다. \"그래도 저 남자는 신부의 새끼손가락만 한 가치도 없잖아요, 그렇죠?\"\n\n\"오, 난 저이가 참 좋아요. 장차 내 beau-frère가 될 사람이라서가 아니라요.\" 르보바 부인이 대답했다. \"그리고 저이가 얼마나 처신을 잘하는지 보세요! 저런 자리에서 우스꽝스러워지지 않고 의젓하게 보이는 게 어디 쉬운 일인가요. 그런데 저이는 우스꽝스럽지도, 거드름을 피우지도 않잖아요. 마음이 움직이고 있다는 게 보인다니까요.\"\n* beau-frère: 프랑스어로 '매부, 동서'.\n\n\"이렇게 될 줄 짐작하고 계셨겠죠?\"\n\n\"거의요. 저 애는 늘 저이를 마음에 두고 있었으니까요.\"\n\n\"자, 그럼 둘 중 누가 먼저 깔개를 밟는지 두고 보자고요. 난 키티한테 미리 일러두었어요.\"\n\n\"그래 봤자 별 차이 없을걸요.\" 르보바 부인이 말했다. \"우리는 다 고분고분한 아내들이잖아요. 그게 우리 집안 내력이거든요.\"\n\n\"오, 난 일부러 바실리보다 먼저 깔개를 밟았는데. 당신은요, 돌리?\"\n\n돌리는 그들 곁에 서 있었다. 그녀는 그 말을 들었지만 대답하지 않았다. 그녀는 깊이 마음이 움직인 상태였다. 두 눈에 눈물이 고여, 울지 않고서는 한마디도 할 수 없었을 것이다. 그녀는 키티와 레빈을 보며 기뻐하고 있었다. 생각 속에서 자기 자신의 결혼식으로 되돌아가, 환하게 빛나는 스테판 아르카지치의 모습을 흘끗 바라보다가, 지금의 모든 것을 잊고 오직 자기의 순결했던 사랑만을 떠올렸다. 그녀는 자기 자신만이 아니라, 모든 여자 친구들과 아는 여인들까지 떠올렸다. 그네들의 영광스러운 그 하루를, 그네들이 키티처럼 결혼 화관 아래 서서, 가슴속에 사랑과 희망과 두려움을 품은 채 과거를 등지고 신비로운 미래를 향해 발을 내딛던 그 순간을 떠올렸다. 기억 속에 되살아난 신부들 가운데, 그녀는 사랑하는 안나도 떠올렸다. 안나가 이혼을 제기하려 한다는 이야기를 그녀는 막 전해 들은 참이었다. 안나도 오렌지꽃과 면사포를 두르고 꼭 저렇게 순결하게 서 있었더랬다. 그런데 지금은? \"정말 끔찍하게도 이상한 일이야.\" 그녀는 혼잣말을 했다. 의식의 자질구레한 부분 하나하나를 좇고 있는 것은 비단 신부의 언니들이며 여자 친구들이며 여자 친척들만이 아니었다. 전혀 모르는 여인들, 그저 구경 온 사람들까지도 신랑 신부의 동작이나 표정을 단 하나라도 놓칠세라 숨을 죽인 채 들떠서 지켜보고 있었고, 줄곧 농담이나 엉뚱한 소리를 늘어놓는 무신경한 남자들의 말에는 화를 내며 대꾸도 않거나, 흔히 아예 듣지도 않았다.\n\n\"왜 저 신부는 울고 있는 거야? 마음에도 없는 결혼을 하는 건가?\"\n\n\"저렇게 멋진 사내한테 마음에도 없이 시집을 가? 공작이라잖아, 안 그래?\"\n\n\"흰 새틴을 입은 저 사람이 신부 언니인가? 부제가 어떻게 우렁차게 외치는지 좀 들어 봐, '그리고 남편을 두려워하며'라고 말이야.\"\n\n\"성가대는 추도보에서 온 사람들인가?\"\n\n\"아니, 종무원에서 왔대.\"\n\n\"하인한테 물어봤어. 신랑이 곧장 신부를 자기 시골 영지로 데려갈 거라더군. 엄청난 부자라던데. 그래서 신부가 저이한테 시집가는 거지.\"\n\n\"아니야, 저 둘은 잘 어울리는 한 쌍이야.\"\n\n\"이봐요, 마리야 바실리예브나, 당신은 저렇게 활짝 퍼지는 크리놀린은 이제 안 입는다고 우기지 않았어요. 저기 적갈색 드레스 입은 여자 좀 봐요. 대사 부인이라던데, 치마가 양옆으로 어떻게 출렁거리는지 좀 보라고요!\"\n\n\"신부가 정말 예쁘고 사랑스럽기도 해라. 꼭 꽃으로 단장한 양 같잖아! 뭐, 뭐라고들 하든, 우리 여자들은 같은 여자 처지가 안타까운 법이지.\"\n\n교회 문 안으로 용케 끼어 들어온, 눈이 휘둥그레진 여인네들 무리에서 오가는 이야기들이 이러했다.\n\n# Chapter 6\n\n약혼의 예식이 끝나자, 교회 사찰이 한가운데에 놓인 성서대 앞에 분홍빛 비단 천을 펼쳐 놓았고, 성가대는 복잡하고 정교한 시편을 불렀다. 거기서는 베이스와 테너가 서로 화답을 주고받았으며, 사제는 몸을 돌려 신랑 신부에게 분홍빛 비단 깔개를 가리켰다. 깔개에 먼저 발을 디딘 사람이 집안의 가장이 된다는 말을 두 사람 모두 여러 차례 익히 들어 왔건만, 레빈도 키티도 그 깔개를 향해 몇 걸음을 옮기는 동안 그것을 떠올릴 겨를이 없었다. 두 사람은 그 뒤로 이어진 큰 목소리의 말다툼도 듣지 못했는데, 어떤 이들은 그가 먼저 발을 디뎠다고 우겼고 또 어떤 이들은 둘이 동시에 디뎠다고 우겼다.\n\n혼인할 뜻이 있는지, 다른 누군가와 약혼한 바가 없는지 묻는 관례적인 질문이 오가고, 스스로 들어도 낯설게만 들리는 대답을 마치자, 새로운 예식이 시작되었다. 키티는 기도문의 말에 귀를 기울이며 그 뜻을 헤아려 보려 했으나 도무지 알아들을 수가 없었다. 예식이 진행될수록 승리감과 환한 행복감이 점점 더 그녀의 마음을 가득 채웠고, 그 바람에 무언가에 집중할 힘을 모조리 앗아 가 버렸다.\n\n사람들은 이렇게 기도했다. \"그들에게 절제와 다산을 내려 주시고, 그들의 마음이 아들딸을 바라보며 기뻐할 수 있게 해 주소서.\" 그들은 하느님이 아담의 갈빗대에서 아내를 지으셨다는 이야기를, \"이러므로 남자가 부모를 떠나 그 아내와 합하여 둘이 한 몸을 이룰지로다\", 또 \"이 비밀이 크도다\"를 인용했다. 그들은 하느님이 이삭과 레베카, 요셉, 모세와 시포라처럼 그들을 다산하게 하시고 축복해 주시기를, 그들이 자손의 자손을 바라볼 수 있기를 기도했다. '이게 다 멋진 일이야.' 키티는 그 말들을 붙들며 생각했다. '전부 마땅히 그래야 하는 대로야.' 그러자 그녀를 바라보는 모든 사람에게 저도 모르게 비치는 행복의 미소가 그녀의 환한 얼굴 위로 빛났다.\n\n\"끝까지 씌우세요.\" 사제가 결혼 관을 씌우고, 셰르바츠키가 세 단추짜리 장갑 낀 손을 떨면서 관을 그녀의 머리 위로 높이 들고 있을 때, 재촉하는 목소리들이 들려왔다.\n\n\"씌워요!\" 그녀가 미소를 지으며 속삭였다.\n\n레빈은 그녀를 돌아보았고, 그녀의 얼굴에 어린 기쁨의 광채에 마음을 빼앗겼다. 그녀의 감정이 저도 모르게 그에게도 옮아 왔다. 그도 그녀처럼 즐겁고 행복했다.\n\n두 사람은 서한이 낭독되는 것을 듣는 것이 즐거웠고, 바깥의 구경꾼들이 그토록 애타게 기다리던 마지막 구절에서 부사제의 목소리가 우렁차게 울려 퍼지는 것도 즐거웠다. 따뜻하게 데운 붉은 포도주와 물을 담은 얕은 잔으로 마시는 것이 즐거웠고, 사제가 영대를 뒤로 젖히고 두 사람의 손을 자기 손에 맞잡은 채 \"하느님께 영광\"을 노래하는 베이스 목소리에 맞추어 성서대를 빙 돌게 했을 때는 더욱 기뻤다.\n\n셰르바츠키와 치리코프는 관을 받쳐 들고 신부의 끌리는 옷자락에 걸려 비틀거리면서도 역시 미소를 지으며 무언가에 기뻐하는 듯했는데, 어느 순간엔 뒤처졌다가, 또 어느 순간엔 사제가 걸음을 멈추는 바람에 신랑 신부의 발을 밟기도 했다. 키티에게 일었던 기쁨의 불꽃이 교회 안의 모든 이에게 옮아 붙은 듯했다. 레빈에게는 사제와 부사제조차 자기처럼 미소 짓고 싶어 하는 것처럼 보였다.\n\n사제는 두 사람의 머리에서 관을 벗기고 마지막 기도문을 읽은 뒤 젊은 부부를 축하했다. 레빈은 키티를 바라보았는데, 지금껏 그녀의 그런 모습을 본 적이 없었다. 얼굴에 어린 새로운 행복의 광채로 그녀는 매혹적이었다. 레빈은 그녀에게 무언가 말하고 싶었지만, 모든 것이 다 끝난 것인지 알 수 없었다. 사제가 그를 그 난처함에서 구해 주었다. 그는 다정한 미소를 지으며 부드럽게 말했다. \"아내에게 입을 맞추세요, 그리고 그대는 남편에게 입을 맞추세요.\" 그러고는 두 사람의 손에서 초를 거두어 갔다.\n\n레빈은 머뭇거리는 조심스러움으로 그녀의 미소 짓는 입술에 입을 맞추고, 그녀에게 팔을 내주고는, 낯설고도 새로운 친밀감을 느끼며 교회 밖으로 걸어 나왔다. 그는 그것이 사실이라는 것을 믿지 못했고, 믿을 수가 없었다. 두 사람의 놀라움에 찬 머뭇거리는 눈길이 마주쳤을 때에야 비로소 그는 그것을 믿었는데, 두 사람이 하나가 되었음을 느꼈기 때문이었다.\n\n그날 밤, 만찬이 끝난 뒤 젊은 부부는 시골로 떠났다.\n\n# Chapter 7\n\n브론스키와 안나는 석 달째 함께 유럽을 여행하고 있었다. 그들은 베네치아와 로마, 나폴리를 둘러보았고, 얼마간 머물 작정인 이탈리아의 작은 도시에 막 도착한 참이었다. 잘생긴 수석 웨이터는 목덜미부터 위로 가르마를 탄 포마드 바른 숱 많은 머리에 연미복을 입고, 넓은 흰색 캠브릭 셔츠 가슴팍을 드러낸 채, 불룩한 배 위로 자질구레한 장식들을 한 다발 늘어뜨리고서, 두 손을 깊숙한 주머니 곡선 속에 찔러 넣고 서서, 눈꺼풀 아래로 깔보는 시선을 던지며 자기를 붙잡은 신사에게 냉랭하게 대꾸하고 있었다. 입구 반대편에서 계단 쪽으로 다가오는 발소리를 알아채고 수석 웨이터가 몸을 돌렸는데, 가장 좋은 방을 잡은 러시아 백작을 보자 공손히 주머니에서 손을 빼고는, 허리를 굽히며 사람을 보냈던 일이 있었고 그 palazzo에 관한 일이 잘 처리되었음을 알렸다. 관리인이 계약서에 서명할 준비가 되어 있다는 것이었다.\n\n* palazzo: 이탈리아어로 '대저택, 궁전'.\n\n\"아! 그것참 반가운 소식이군.\" 브론스키가 말했다. \"마담은 집에 계신가, 안 계신가?\"\n\n\"마담께서는 산책을 나가셨다가 지금 돌아오셨습니다.\" 웨이터가 대답했다.\n브론스키는 챙이 넓은 부드러운 모자를 벗고, 손수건으로 달아오른 이마와, 귀 절반까지 자라 머리의 벗겨진 부분을 가리며 뒤로 빗어 넘긴 머리카락을 닦았다. 그러고는 여전히 그 자리에 서서 자기를 뚫어지게 바라보는 신사를 무심히 흘끗 보고 그대로 지나치려 했다.\n\n\"이 신사분이 러시아 분인데, 백작님을 찾으셨습니다.\" 수석 웨이터가 말했다.\n\n어딜 가든 아는 사람들에게서 벗어날 수 없다는 데 대한 짜증과, 단조로운 생활에서 무언가 기분 전환거리를 찾고 싶다는 갈망이 뒤섞인 채, 브론스키는 한 발 물러나 다시 가만히 서 있는 신사를 한 번 더 바라보았는데, 바로 그 순간 두 사람의 눈에 동시에 빛이 떠올랐다.\n\n\"골레니셰프!\"\n\n\"브론스키!\"\n\n정말로 골레니셰프였다. 사관학교 시절 브론스키의 동기였다. 학교에서 골레니셰프는 자유주의 파에 속했고, 졸업 후 군대에 들어가지 않았으며, 정부 기관에서 관직을 맡은 적도 없었다. 브론스키와 그는 학교를 나온 뒤 완전히 다른 길을 걸었고, 그동안 단 한 번 마주쳤을 뿐이었다.\n\n그 만남에서 브론스키는 골레니셰프가 일종의 고고하고 지적으로 자유주의적인 노선을 택했으며, 그래서 브론스키의 관심사며 인생의 직분을 깔보는 경향이 있다는 것을 알아챘다. 그래서 브론스키는 그를, 자기가 능숙하게 지을 줄 아는 차갑고 거만한 태도로 대했는데, 그 태도가 의미하는 바는 이런 것이었다. \"자네가 내 삶의 방식을 좋아하든 싫어하든, 그건 나에게 더없이 아무래도 좋은 일이야. 나를 알고 싶다면 나를 존중해야 할 걸세.\" 골레니셰프는 브론스키가 취한 그 태도에 경멸 어린 무관심으로 응했다. 이 두 번째 만남은, 당연히 두 사람을 더욱 멀어지게 했으리라 짐작할 만했다. 하지만 지금 그들은 서로를 알아보고 기쁨에 차서 환하게 빛나며 소리쳤다. 브론스키는 골레니셰프를 만나 이토록 기쁘리라고는 결코 예상하지 못했지만, 아마 자기가 얼마나 따분해하고 있었는지 스스로도 몰랐던 것이리라. 그는 지난 만남의 불쾌한 인상을 잊고, 꾸밈없이 기뻐하는 얼굴로 옛 동기에게 손을 내밀었다. 골레니셰프의 얼굴에서도 불안한 빛이 사라지고 같은 기쁨의 표정이 자리 잡았다.\n\n\"이렇게 만나다니 정말 반갑네!\" 브론스키가 다정한 미소로 희고 튼튼한 이를 드러내며 말했다.\n\n\"브론스키라는 이름은 들었는데, 어느 브론스키인지를 몰랐지 뭔가. 정말, 정말 반가워!\"\n\n\"안으로 들어가세. 자, 자네가 무슨 일을 하고 지내는지 얘기해 주게.\"\n\n\"여기서 산 지 이 년 됐어. 일을 하고 있지.\"\n\n\"아!\" 브론스키가 공감을 담아 말했다. \"들어가세.\" 그러고는 러시아인들 사이에 흔한 버릇대로, 하인들에게 들키고 싶지 않은 말을 러시아어로 하는 대신 프랑스어로 말하기 시작했다.\n\n\"마담 카레니나를 아나? 우리는 함께 여행하는 중이야. 지금 그녀를 만나러 가는 길이지.\" 그는 프랑스어로 말하며 골레니셰프의 얼굴을 주의 깊게 살폈다.\n\n\"아! 몰랐는걸.\" (사실은 알고 있었지만.) 골레니셰프가 아무렇지 않게 대답했다. \"여기 온 지 오래됐나?\" 그가 덧붙였다.\n\n\"나흘 됐네.\" 브론스키가 대답하며 다시 한번 친구의 얼굴을 유심히 살폈다.\n\n'그래, 점잖은 친구야. 이 일을 제대로 볼 줄 알 거야.' 브론스키는 골레니셰프의 표정에 담긴 뜻과 화제를 바꾸는 태도를 알아채고 속으로 생각했다. '안나에게 소개해도 되겠어. 제대로 받아들이는군.'\n\n브론스키가 안나와 함께 외국에서 보낸 그 석 달 동안, 그는 새로운 사람을 만날 때마다 그 사람이 안나와 자기의 관계를 어떻게 볼지 늘 스스로에게 물었고, 남자들의 경우 대개 '제대로' 보는 방식을 만났다. 하지만 만약 그에게, 그리고 그것을 '제대로' 본다는 사람들에게 정확히 어떻게 보느냐고 물었다면, 그도 그들도 대답하기에 몹시 난처했을 것이다.\n\n사실 브론스키가 보기에 '제대로' 된 시각을 가진 자들은 실은 아무런 시각도 가지고 있지 않았고, 다만 삶을 사방에서 에워싸는 온갖 복잡하고 풀리지 않는 문제들에 대해 교양 있는 사람들이 으레 하는 식으로 처신했을 뿐이었다. 그들은 점잖게 굴며 넌지시 비추는 말이나 불쾌한 질문을 피했다. 그들은 상황의 의미와 무게를 충분히 이해하고, 그것을 받아들이며 심지어 인정하기까지 하지만, 그것을 굳이 말로 옮기는 것은 군더더기이고 쓸데없는 짓이라 여기는 듯한 태도를 취했다.\n\n브론스키는 골레니셰프가 이 부류에 속한다는 것을 곧장 알아차렸고, 그래서 그를 보는 것이 갑절로 기뻤다. 그리고 사실 골레니셰프를 데려가 마담 카레니나를 방문하게 했을 때, 그가 그녀를 대하는 태도는 브론스키가 바랄 수 있는 그대로였다. 그는 분명 조금의 애도 쓰지 않고 곤란을 불러올 만한 모든 화제를 매끄럽게 피해 갔다.\n\n그는 안나를 만난 적이 한 번도 없었기에, 그녀의 아름다움에 놀랐고, 자기 처지를 받아들이는 그 솔직함에는 더욱 놀랐다. 브론스키가 골레니셰프를 데리고 들어오자 그녀는 얼굴을 붉혔는데, 그 꾸밈없고 아름다운 얼굴에 번지는 그 어린아이 같은 홍조에 그는 더없이 매료되었다. 하지만 그가 특히 마음에 들어 한 것은, 마치 외부 사람과의 사이에 오해가 없도록 일부러 그러는 것처럼, 그녀가 곧바로 브론스키를 그저 알렉세이라고 부르며, 자기들이 여기서 palazzo라 불리는, 막 빌린 집으로 옮겨 가는 중이라고 말하던 그 방식이었다. 골레니셰프는 자기 처지에 대한 그녀의 이 솔직하고 단순한 태도가 마음에 들었다. 안나의 천진하고 활기찬 명랑함을 바라보며, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치와 브론스키를 알기에, 골레니셰프는 그녀를 완전히 이해했다고 자부했다. 그는 그녀 자신은 도무지 이해할 수 없는 것을 자기는 이해한다고 자부했다. 즉, 남편을 비참하게 만들고, 그와 아들을 버리고 명예를 잃었으면서도, 어떻게 그녀가 여전히 생기와 명랑함과 행복으로 가득 차 있을 수 있는지를 말이다.\n\n\"그건 안내서에도 나와 있지.\" 골레니셰프가 브론스키가 빌린 palazzo를 두고 말했다. \"거기에 일류 틴토레토 작품이 있어. 그의 후기 작품 중 하나지.\"\n\n\"이러면 어떨까. 날씨도 화창하니, 가서 한 번 더 둘러보세.\" 브론스키가 안나를 향해 말했다.\n\n\"아주 좋아요. 가서 모자를 쓰고 올게요. 날씨가 덥다고 생각하세요?\" 그녀가 문간에서 우뚝 멈춰 서서 묻는 듯한 눈빛으로 브론스키를 바라보며 말했다. 그러자 다시 한번 선명한 홍조가 그녀의 얼굴에 번졌다.\n\n브론스키는 그녀의 눈빛에서, 그녀가 자기와 골레니셰프 사이가 어떤 관계인지 몰라서 자기가 바라는 대로 처신하지 못할까 봐 두려워한다는 것을 읽었다.\n\n그는 그녀를 오래도록 다정하게 바라보았다.\n\n\"아니, 그리 덥지 않아.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n그러자 그녀에게는 모든 것이, 무엇보다도 그가 자기에게 만족하고 있다는 것이 이해되는 듯했다. 그녀는 그에게 미소 지으며 빠른 걸음으로 문밖으로 걸어 나갔다.\n\n두 친구는 서로를 흘끗 보았는데, 두 사람의 얼굴에 망설이는 빛이 떠올랐다. 마치 골레니셰프는, 분명 그녀에게 감탄하고 있어서 그녀에 대해 뭔가 말하고 싶지만 알맞은 말을 찾지 못하는 듯했고, 브론스키는 그가 그러기를 바라면서도 동시에 두려워하는 듯했다.\n\n\"그래, 그런데,\" 브론스키가 무언가 대화를 시작하려 운을 뗐다. \"그러니까 자네 여기에 자리를 잡은 건가? 여전히 같은 일을 하고 있고?\" 그는 골레니셰프가 무언가를 쓰고 있다는 말을 들었던 것을 떠올리며 말을 이었다.\n\n\"그래, 「두 가지 요소」의 제2부를 쓰고 있어.\" 골레니셰프가 그 질문에 기뻐서 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다. \"그러니까, 정확히 말하면 아직 쓰는 건 아니고, 준비하면서 자료를 모으는 중이지. 훨씬 더 폭넓은 내용이 될 거고, 거의 모든 문제를 다루게 될 거야. 우리 러시아인들은 우리가 비잔티움의 후예라는 사실을 인정하려 들지 않거든.\" 그러더니 그는 자기 견해를 길고 열띠게 설명하기 시작했다.\n\n브론스키는 처음 한순간, 저자가 잘 알려진 것인 양 말하는 「두 가지 요소」의 제1부조차 모른다는 데 당황했다. 하지만 골레니셰프가 자기 견해를 늘어놓기 시작하자 브론스키는 「두 가지 요소」를 모르고도 그 이야기를 따라갈 수 있었기에, 어느 정도 흥미를 느끼며 귀를 기울였다. 골레니셰프가 말을 잘했기 때문이다. 그러나 골레니셰프가 자기를 사로잡은 그 주제를 이야기할 때 보이는 신경질적인 짜증에 브론스키는 놀랐고 거슬렸다. 말을 이어 갈수록 그의 눈은 점점 더 성난 듯 번득였고, 가상의 적수들에게 대꾸하는 말투가 점점 더 다급해졌으며, 얼굴은 점점 더 흥분하고 근심스러워졌다. 마르고 활기차며 선량하고 교양 있던, 늘 학급에서 일등을 하던 소년 골레니셰프를 떠올리며, 브론스키는 그의 짜증의 까닭을 도무지 헤아릴 수 없었고, 그것이 마음에 들지 않았다. 특히 그가 거슬려 한 것은, 좋은 가문 출신인 골레니셰프가 자기를 짜증나게 하고 화나게 하는 어떤 글쟁이들과 같은 수준으로 자신을 끌어내린다는 점이었다. 그럴 만한 가치가 있는 일인가? 브론스키는 그것이 못마땅했지만, 그러면서도 골레니셰프가 불행하다는 것을 느꼈고, 그가 가여웠다. 안나가 들어오는 것조차 알아채지 못한 채 다급하고 열띠게 자기 견해를 늘어놓는 그의 표정 풍부한, 제법 잘생긴 얼굴에는 불행이, 거의 정신적 착란에 가까운 것이 드러나 있었다.\n\n안나가 모자를 쓰고 망토를 두른 채 사랑스러운 손으로 양산을 빠르게 흔들며 들어와 그의 곁에 섰을 때, 브론스키는 끈질기게 자기에게 들러붙는 골레니셰프의 애처로운 눈길에서 벗어나는 안도감을 느끼며, 새롭게 차오르는 사랑으로 생기와 행복이 가득한 매혹적인 동반자를 바라보았다. 골레니셰프는 애써 정신을 추슬렀고, 처음에는 풀이 죽어 침울했지만, 그 무렵 누구에게나 다정하게 대하려는 마음이었던 안나가 솔직하고 활기찬 태도로 곧 그의 기분을 되살려 주었다. 이런저런 화제를 시도해 본 끝에 그녀는 그를 회화 이야기로 끌어들였고, 그는 그 이야기를 아주 잘했으며, 그녀는 주의 깊게 귀를 기울였다. 그들은 빌린 집까지 걸어가 그곳을 둘러보았다.\n\n\"한 가지 정말 기쁜 게 있어요.\" 돌아오는 길에 안나가 골레니셰프에게 말했다. \"알렉세이가 훌륭한 atelier를 갖게 됐거든요. 당신은 꼭 저 방을 쓰셔야 해요.\" 그녀는 브론스키에게 러시아어로, 골레니셰프가 자기들의 고립된 생활 속에서 가까운 사이가 되리라는 것을, 그래서 그 앞에서 거리낄 필요가 없다는 것을 알아차린 듯, 다정하고 허물없는 말투를 쓰며 말했다.\n* atelier: 프랑스어로 '화실, 작업실'.\n\n\"그림을 그리시나요?\" 골레니셰프가 브론스키 쪽으로 잽싸게 돌아서며 물었다.\n\n\"네, 오래전에 배운 적이 있고, 이제 조금씩 다시 손을 대기 시작했어요.\" 브론스키가 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다.\n\n\"그이는 대단한 재능이 있어요.\" 안나가 기쁨에 찬 미소를 지으며 말했다. \"물론 제가 판단할 처지는 아니지만요. 안목 있는 분들이 그렇게 말씀하셨답니다.\"\n\n# Chapter 8\n\n해방을 맞은 첫 시기, 빠르게 건강을 회복해 가던 안나는 자신이 용서받을 수 없을 만큼 행복하며 삶의 기쁨으로 가득 차 있다고 느꼈다. 남편의 불행에 대한 생각도 그녀의 행복을 망치지는 못했다. 한편으로 그 기억은 떠올리기에 너무나 끔찍했다. 다른 한편으로 남편의 불행은, 후회하기에는 그녀에게 너무 많은 행복을 가져다주었다. 자신이 앓은 뒤 일어난 모든 일들의 기억—남편과의 화해, 그 화해의 파탄, 브론스키가 부상당했다는 소식, 그의 방문, 이혼 준비, 남편의 집에서 나온 일, 아들과의 이별—그 모든 것이 그녀에게는 마치 헛소리 같은 꿈처럼 여겨졌고, 그녀는 외국에서 브론스키와 단둘이 그 꿈에서 깨어난 것 같았다. 남편에게 끼친 해악에 대한 생각은 그녀에게 일종의 혐오감 같은 것을, 물에 빠진 사람이 자기에게 매달린 다른 사람을 떨쳐 냈을 때 느낄 법한 감정과 비슷한 것을 불러일으켰다. 그 사람은 정말로 물에 빠져 죽었다. 물론 그것은 악한 행동이었지만, 그것만이 유일한 살길이었으니, 그 무서운 사실들을 곱씹지 않는 편이 나았다.\n\n마지막 결별의 첫 순간에 그녀는 자신의 행동에 대해 한 가지 위안이 되는 생각을 떠올렸고, 이제 지나간 모든 일을 되돌아볼 때면 그 한 가지 생각이 떠올랐다. \"나는 어쩔 수 없이 그 사람을 비참하게 만들었어.\" 그녀는 생각했다. \"하지만 나는 그의 불행으로 이득을 보고 싶지는 않아. 나도 괴로워하고 있고, 또 앞으로도 괴로워할 거야. 나는 무엇보다 소중히 여기던 것을 잃고 있어—나의 명예와 아들을 잃고 있는 거야. 내가 잘못을 저질렀으니, 나는 행복을 원치 않고, 이혼도 원치 않으며, 나의 치욕과 아이와의 이별로 괴로워할 거야.\" 그러나 안나가 아무리 진심으로 괴로워하려 했어도, 그녀는 괴롭지 않았다. 치욕도 없었다. 둘 다 풍부하게 지닌 그 재치 덕분에 그들은 외국에서 러시아 부인들을 피하는 데 성공했고, 그래서 한 번도 거짓된 처지에 자신들을 놓이게 한 적이 없었으며, 가는 곳마다 자기들의 처지를 완벽히 이해하는 척하는 사람들을, 사실 당사자인 자기들보다 훨씬 더 잘 이해하는 척하는 사람들을 만났다. 사랑하는 아들과의 이별—그것조차도 이 초기 시절에는 그녀에게 고통을 주지 않았다. 그의 아이인 갓난 딸은 너무나 사랑스러웠고, 자기에게 남은 전부라는 사실 때문에 안나의 마음을 온통 사로잡았기에, 안나는 좀처럼 아들 생각을 하지 않았다.\n\n회복된 건강과 더불어 점점 강해지는 삶에 대한 욕망은 너무나 강렬했고, 삶의 조건들은 너무나 새롭고 즐거웠기에, 안나는 용서받을 수 없을 만큼 행복하다고 느꼈다. 브론스키를 알아 가면 갈수록 그녀는 그를 더욱 사랑했다. 그녀는 그 자체로서의 그를 사랑했고, 그가 자기를 사랑한다는 사실 때문에 그를 사랑했다. 그를 온전히 소유하고 있다는 것은 그녀에게 끊임없는 기쁨이었다. 그가 곁에 있다는 것은 언제나 그녀에게 달콤했다. 점점 더 잘 알게 된 그의 성격의 온갖 면면이 그녀에게는 말로 다 할 수 없이 사랑스러웠다. 평복으로 바뀐 그의 모습은, 마치 그녀가 사랑에 빠진 어린 처녀이기라도 한 듯 그녀를 매혹했다. 그가 말하고 생각하고 행하는 모든 것에서 그녀는 무언가 유난히 고귀하고 드높은 것을 보았다. 그를 향한 자신의 흠모는 사실 그녀를 불안하게 했다. 그녀는 그에게서 훌륭하지 않은 점을 찾아보려 했지만 찾을 수 없었다. 그녀는 감히 그 앞에서 자기가 보잘것없는 존재라는 느낌을 그에게 내보일 수 없었다. 이를 알게 되면 그가 더 빨리 자기를 사랑하지 않게 될지도 모른다고 여겼다. 그리고 이제 그녀는 그의 사랑을 잃는 것만큼 두려운 것이 없었다. 그것을 두려워할 근거는 전혀 없었지만 말이다. 그러나 그녀는 자기를 대하는 그의 태도에 감사하지 않을 수 없었고, 그것을 고맙게 여긴다는 것을 내보이지 않을 수 없었다. 그녀가 보기에 정계에서 두각을 나타낼 그토록 분명한 자질을 갖춰, 틀림없이 주도적인 역할을 했을 그가—그는 그녀를 위해 자신의 야망을 희생했으며, 단 한 번도 조금의 후회조차 내비치지 않았다. 그는 그 어느 때보다 다정하고 정중하게 그녀를 대했고, 그녀가 자기 처지의 어색함을 느끼지 않게 하려는 한결같은 배려를 한순간도 거두지 않았다. 그토록 남자다운 사내였던 그는 결코 그녀에게 반대하는 법이 없었고, 그녀 앞에서는 자기 의지라고 할 것이 전혀 없는 듯했으며, 오직 그녀의 바람을 미리 헤아리는 것 말고는 아무것도 바라지 않는 것 같았다. 그리고 그녀는 이를 고맙게 여기지 않을 수 없었다. 비록 그녀를 향한 바로 그 지극한 정성과, 그녀를 에워싼 보살핌의 분위기가 때로 그녀를 짓누르기도 했지만 말이다.\n\n한편 브론스키는 그토록 오래 갈망해 온 것을 완전히 이루었음에도 온전히 행복하지는 않았다. 그는 자신의 욕망이 실현되었다 해도 그것이 자기가 기대했던 행복의 산더미에서 모래 한 알밖에 주지 못한다는 것을 곧 깨달았다. 그것은 사람들이 행복을 자기 욕망의 실현으로 그려 보는 데서 저지르는 오류를 그에게 일깨워 주었다. 자신의 삶을 그녀의 삶과 하나로 합치고 평복을 입은 후 한동안 그는, 전에는 전혀 몰랐던 자유 전반의 온갖 기쁨과 사랑 속에서의 자유의 기쁨을 만끽했고—그는 만족했지만, 그것은 오래가지 않았다. 그는 곧 자기 마음속에서 욕망을 향한 욕망, 곧 권태가 솟아나고 있음을 알아차렸다. 그는 별다른 의도도 없이 스쳐 가는 온갖 변덕을 욕망이자 목표로 여기며 거기에 매달리기 시작했다. 그들은 페테르부르크에서 시간을 채워 주던 사교 생활의 조건 바깥에서, 외국에서 완전한 자유를 누리며 살고 있었기에, 하루 열여섯 시간을 어떻게든 채워야만 했다. 이전의 외국 여행에서 브론스키에게 즐거움을 주었던 독신 시절의 오락들로 말하자면, 그것은 생각조차 할 수 없었다. 그런 종류의 단 한 번의 시도—독신 친구들과의 늦은 밤 만찬—가 그 원인에 전혀 어울리지 않게 안나에게 느닷없는 우울증을 불러일으켰기 때문이다. 그곳 사교계—외국인이든 러시아인이든—와의 교제 역시 그들 처지의 불안정함 때문에 마찬가지로 불가능했다. 흥미로운 명소들을 둘러보는 일은, 이미 모든 것을 다 보았다는 사실은 차치하더라도, 러시아인이자 분별 있는 사내인 브론스키에게는 영국인들이 그 일에 부여할 수 있는 그런 엄청난 의미가 없었다.\n\n그리고 굶주린 위장이 영양분을 얻으리라는 기대로 손에 닿는 모든 것을 게걸스럽게 받아들이듯, 브론스키도 자기도 모르게 처음에는 정치에, 다음에는 신간 서적에, 그다음에는 그림에 매달렸다.\n\n그는 어려서부터 회화에 취미가 있었고, 돈을 어디에 쓸지 몰라 판화를 수집하기 시작했던 터라, 회화에 멈춰 서서 그것에 흥미를 갖기 시작했으며, 충족을 요구하던 미처 다 쓰지 못한 욕망의 덩어리를 거기에 쏟아부었다.\n\n그는 예술을 곧잘 감상할 줄 알았고, 아마도 예술을 모방하는 데 취미가 있었기에 자기에게 화가에게 필요한 진짜 자질이 있다고 여겼다. 그는 어떤 화풍을 택할지—종교화, 역사화, 사실주의, 아니면 풍속화—얼마간 망설인 끝에 그림을 그리기 시작했다. 그는 온갖 화풍을 감상할 줄 알았고 그 어느 것에서든 영감을 받을 수 있었지만, 어떤 화파에 대해서도 아무것도 모를 수 있다는 것, 그리고 그려진 것이 어느 공인된 화파에 속하는지 따지지 않고 영혼 안에 있는 것으로부터 직접 영감을 받을 수 있다는 것은 전혀 생각하지 못했다. 이를 전혀 몰랐기에, 그리고 삶에서 직접 영감을 끌어내지 않고 예술 속에 구현된 삶에서 간접적으로 영감을 끌어냈기에, 그의 영감은 아주 빠르고 손쉽게 찾아왔고, 그가 모방하려던 그림과 매우 비슷한 무언가를 그려 내는 성공도 그만큼 빠르고 손쉽게 찾아왔다.\n\n다른 어떤 화풍보다도 그는 우아하고 효과적인 프랑스풍을 좋아했고, 그 화풍으로 이탈리아 의상을 입은 안나의 초상화를 그리기 시작했는데, 그 초상화는 그 자신에게도, 그것을 본 모든 사람에게도 더없이 성공적으로 보였다.\n\n# Chapter 9\n\n낡고 방치된 그 저택은 높다란 조각 천장과 벽에 그려진 프레스코화, 모자이크로 된 바닥, 창문에 드리운 묵직한 노란 천 커튼, 받침대 위에 놓인 화병들, 활짝 열린 벽난로, 조각이 새겨진 문과 그림이 걸린 음울한 응접실을 갖추고 있었는데, 이 저택은 그들이 그리로 옮겨 온 뒤로 그 외관만으로도, 자기가 러시아의 시골 지주이자 퇴역 장교라기보다는 오히려 계몽된 예술 애호가이자 후원자이며, 사랑하는 여인을 위해 세상과 인맥과 야망을 모두 단념한 겸손한 예술가라는, 브론스키의 그 흐뭇한 환상을 굳히는 데 크게 한몫했다.\n\n저택으로 옮겨 오면서 브론스키가 택한 자세는 완벽하게 성공적이었고, 골레니셰프를 통해 흥미로운 사람들 몇과 알고 지내게 되면서 그는 한동안 만족스러웠다. 그는 이탈리아인 회화 교수의 지도를 받아 실물을 보고 습작을 그렸고, 중세 이탈리아의 생활을 연구했다. 중세 이탈리아의 생활에 브론스키는 어찌나 매료되었던지, 중세풍으로 모자를 쓰고 어깨에 망토를 걸치고 다니기까지 했는데, 사실 그것은 그에게 무척이나 잘 어울렸다.\n\n\"우리는 여기 살면서 바깥에서 무슨 일이 벌어지는지 아무것도 모르고 있군요.\" 어느 날 아침 골레니셰프를 찾아온 브론스키가 그에게 말했다. \"미하일로프의 그림 보셨습니까?\" 그가 그날 아침 받은 러시아 신문을 건네며 어느 러시아 화가에 관한 기사를 가리키며 말했다. 바로 같은 도시에 살고 있으며, 오래전부터 화제가 되어 미리 팔리기까지 한 그림을 이제 막 완성하고 있는 화가였다. 그 기사는 이토록 비범한 화가를 격려도 후원도 없이 내버려 두고 있다며 정부와 아카데미를 책망하고 있었다.\n\n\"봤습니다.\" 골레니셰프가 대답했다. \"물론 그에게 재능이 없는 건 아니지만, 죄다 잘못된 방향이지요. 그리스도와 종교화를 대하는 태도가 죄다 이바노프-슈트라우스-르낭 식이란 말입니다.\"\n\n\"그림의 주제가 뭔가요?\" 안나가 물었다.\n\n\"빌라도 앞에 선 그리스도입니다. 그리스도를 새 화파 특유의 사실주의로 한 사람의 유대인으로 표현했지요.\"\n\n그림의 주제에 관한 질문이 그를 자기가 좋아하는 이론 가운데 하나로 끌어들이자, 골레니셰프는 그것에 대한 장광설을 늘어놓기 시작했다.\n\n\"어떻게 그들이 그토록 큰 잘못에 빠질 수 있는지 도무지 이해할 수가 없어요. 그리스도는 위대한 거장들의 예술 속에 언제나 그 분명한 형상을 지니고 있단 말입니다. 그러니 만일 그들이 신이 아니라 혁명가나 현자를 그리고 싶다면, 역사에서 소크라테스나 프랭클린이나 샤를로트 코르데를 가져오면 되지, 그리스도를 가져올 일이 아니지요. 그들은 하필이면 자기들 예술로는 취할 수 없는 형상을 취해 놓고는, 그러고 나서…….\"\n\n\"그런데 이 미하일로프가 그렇게 가난하다는 게 사실인가요?\" 브론스키가 물었다. 그는 러시아의 한 메케나스로서 그림이 좋든 나쁘든 상관없이 그 화가를 도와주는 것이 자기 의무라고 생각하고 있었다.\n\n\"그렇지는 않을 겁니다. 그는 비범한 초상화가니까요. 바실치코바 부인의 초상을 보신 적 있습니까? 그런데 제 생각엔 그가 더 이상 초상화를 그리는 데에 관심이 없는 것 같으니, 어쩌면 정말로 궁핍할지도 모르지요. 제가 주장하는 바는…….\"\n\n\"우리가 그에게 안나 아르카지예브나의 초상을 그려 달라고 부탁할 수는 없을까요?\" 브론스키가 말했다.\n\n\"왜 제 초상을 그려요?\" 안나가 말했다. \"당신이 그려 준 것이 있는데 다른 초상은 필요 없어요. 차라리 아니의 초상을 그리게 하는 편이 낫겠어요.\" (그녀는 자기 어린 딸을 그렇게 불렀다.) \"마침 저기 있네요.\" 그녀는 창밖으로, 아이를 정원으로 데리고 나가는 아름다운 이탈리아인 유모를 내다보며 덧붙였고, 곧바로 들키지 않게 슬쩍 브론스키를 흘끔거렸다. 그 아름다운 유모는, 브론스키가 자기 그림에 쓸 두상을 그리는 모델이기도 했는데, 안나의 삶에 숨겨진 단 하나의 괴로움이었다. 그는 그녀를 모델 삼아 그림을 그렸고, 그녀의 아름다움과 중세풍을 찬미했으며, 안나는 자기가 이 유모에게 질투를 느끼게 될까 봐 두려워하고 있다는 것을 스스로에게도 차마 인정하지 못했고, 바로 그 때문에 유모와 그녀의 어린 아들에게 유난히 너그럽고 깍듯하게 대했다. 브론스키 역시 창밖을 내다보았다가 안나의 눈을 들여다보고는, 곧장 골레니셰프 쪽으로 돌아서며 말했다.\n\n\"이 미하일로프를 아십니까?\"\n\n\"만난 적은 있습니다. 그런데 괴짜인 데다 교양이라곤 도통 없는 사람이에요. 아시잖습니까, 요즘 그렇게 자주 마주치게 되는 그 거칠고 새로운 부류, 그러니까 무신론과 회의론과 유물론의 이론 속에서 d'emblée 길러진 자유사상가 부류 말입니다. 옛날에는,\" 골레니셰프는 안나도 브론스키도 무언가 말하고 싶어 한다는 것을 알아채지 못하거나 알아채려 하지 않은 채 말을 이어 갔다. \"옛날에는 자유사상가란 종교와 법과 도덕의 관념 속에서 자라난 사람이 오로지 갈등과 투쟁을 통해서만 자유사상에 이른 사람이었지요. 그런데 이제는 새로운 유형의 타고난 자유사상가들이 생겨났단 말입니다. 도덕이나 종교의 원리에 대해, 권위라는 것의 존재에 대해 들어 본 적조차 없이 자라난 사람들, 만사를 부정하는 관념 속에서 곧바로 자라난 사람들, 한마디로 야만인들이지요. 자, 그가 바로 그런 부류입니다. 듣자 하니 모스크바의 어느 집사의 아들이라는데, 변변한 가정교육이라곤 받아 본 적이 없답니다. 아카데미에 들어가 명성을 얻고 나서, 그는 바보가 아닌지라 스스로 교육해 보려고 애를 썼지요. 그러고는 자기에게 교양의 바로 그 원천으로 보이는 것에 눈을 돌렸는데, 그게 잡지였단 말입니다. 보세요, 옛날에는 스스로 교육하고자 하는 사람이라면, 이를테면 프랑스인이라면, 모든 고전과 신학자와 비극 작가와 역사가와 철학자, 그러니까 자기 앞에 놓인 온갖 지적 업적을 다 공부하기 시작했을 겁니다. 그런데 요즘 사람은 곧장 부정의 문헌으로 직행해서, 부정의 학문에서 발췌한 것들을 아주 빠르게 흡수하고는, 그걸로 준비가 다 됐다고 하지요. 그뿐만이 아닙니다. 이십 년 전이라면 그 문헌 속에서 권위와의, 시대의 신조들과의 갈등의 흔적을 발견했을 테고, 그 갈등을 통해 다른 무언가가 있다는 것을 깨달았을 겁니다. 그런데 지금은 옛 신조들이 논의의 거리조차 되지 못하고, 다른 건 아무것도 없다고, 진화와 자연선택과 생존경쟁뿐이라고 노골적으로 단언하는 문헌과 곧장 맞닥뜨리게 되지요. 그게 전부예요. 제 논문에서 저는…….\"\n\n\"제가 한 말씀 드리죠.\" 안나가 말했다. 그녀는 한참 동안 브론스키와 조심스러운 눈짓을 주고받으며, 그가 이 화가의 교육 문제 따위에는 조금도 관심이 없고 단지 그를 돕고 그에게 초상화를 주문하려는 생각에 골몰해 있다는 것을 알고 있었다. \"제가 한 말씀 드릴게요.\" 그녀는 여전히 떠들어 대고 있는 골레니셰프의 말을 단호하게 가로막으며 말했다. \"그를 만나러 가요!\"\n\n골레니셰프는 평정을 되찾고 선뜻 동의했다. 다만 그 화가가 멀리 떨어진 변두리에 살고 있었으므로, 마차를 타고 가기로 했다.\n\n한 시간 뒤, 안나는 곁에 골레니셰프를, 그리고 맞은편 앞자리에 브론스키를 앉히고서, 멀리 떨어진 변두리의 어느 새로 지은 흉한 집 앞에 마차를 댔다. 그들에게 나온 문지기의 아내에게서, 미하일로프가 화실에서는 방문객을 맞지만 지금은 바로 몇 걸음 떨어진 자기 거처에 있다는 말을 듣고, 그들은 그녀에게 명함을 들려 보내며 그림을 볼 수 있게 허락해 달라고 청했다.\n\n# Chapter 10\n\n화가 미하일로프는 브론스키 백작과 골레니셰프의 명함이 그에게 전해졌을 때, 늘 그렇듯이 작업 중이었다. 아침에 그는 자기 아틀리에에서 큰 그림에 매달려 있었다. 집에 돌아오자, 돈을 달라고 졸라 대던 집주인 여자를 잘 돌려보내지 못했다며 아내에게 화를 냈다.\n\n\"내가 스무 번도 더 말했잖아, 자질구레한 얘기에 끼어들지 말라고. 당신은 어느 때나 충분히 멍청한데, 이탈리아 말로 이러쿵저러쿵 설명하기 시작하면 멍청함이 세 배는 더 멍청해진다고.\" 그는 한참 옥신각신한 끝에 말했다.\n\n\"그렇게 질질 끌게 두지 말았어야죠. 내 잘못이 아니에요. 나한테 돈만 있었어도……\"\n\n\"제발 좀 나를 가만히 내버려 둬!\" 미하일로프는 목소리에 울음을 섞어 비명을 질렀고, 두 귀를 틀어막은 채 칸막이벽 너머 자기 작업실로 가 버리더니 문을 닫았다. \"멍청한 여자 같으니!\" 그는 혼잣말로 중얼거리며 탁자 앞에 앉아, 화첩을 펼쳐 들고는 진작 시작해 둔 밑그림 하나에 곧장 유난한 열기로 매달리기 시작했다.\n\n그는 일이 잘 풀리지 않을 때, 특히 아내와 다투었을 때만큼 그토록 열렬하고 성공적으로 작업한 적이 없었다. \"오! 다들 빌어먹으라지!\" 그는 계속 작업하면서 생각했다. 그는 격렬한 분노에 휩싸인 남자의 모습을 밑그림으로 그리는 중이었다. 전에 한 번 밑그림을 그려 두었지만 그것이 마음에 들지 않았다. \"아니야, 그게 더 나았는데…… 그게 어디 있더라?\" 그는 아내에게로 돌아가서, 얼굴을 찌푸린 채 아내 쪽은 쳐다보지도 않고, 자기가 아이들에게 준 그 종이가 어디 있느냐고 큰딸에게 물었다. 버려진 밑그림이 그려진 종이를 찾아냈는데, 더럽고 양초 기름 얼룩이 점점이 묻어 있었다. 그래도 그는 그 밑그림을 집어 탁자 위에 놓고, 조금 물러서서 눈을 가늘게 뜬 채 그것을 응시하기 시작했다. 별안간 그는 미소를 지으며 신이 나서 손짓을 했다.\n\n\"바로 그거야! 바로 그거라고!\" 그는 이렇게 말하고는 곧장 연필을 집어 들어 빠르게 그리기 시작했다. 그 양초 기름 얼룩이 남자에게 새로운 자세를 부여한 것이었다.\n\n그가 이 새로운 자세를 밑그림으로 그려 냈을 때, 별안간 자기에게 시가를 팔았던 어느 가게 주인의 얼굴이, 툭 튀어나온 턱을 가진 그 활기찬 얼굴이 떠올랐고, 그는 바로 그 얼굴을, 그 턱을 남자의 모습에 옮겨 그렸다. 그는 기쁨에 겨워 소리 내어 웃었다. 그 모습은 생명 없이 상상으로 빚어낸 것에서 살아 있는 것으로, 다시는 바꿀 수 없는 그런 것으로 변모해 있었다. 그 모습은 살아 있었고, 또렷하고 틀림없이 규정되어 있었다. 밑그림은 그 모습의 요구에 따라 손볼 수도 있었다. 다리는 사실 다르게 놓을 수 있고 또 그래야만 했으며, 왼손의 위치도 완전히 바꿔야 했다. 머리카락도 뒤로 넘길 수 있었다. 하지만 이런 수정을 하면서도 그는 모습 자체를 바꾸는 것이 아니라 그저 그 모습을 가리고 있는 것을 걷어 낼 따름이었다. 말하자면 그는 그 모습이 또렷이 보이는 것을 방해하는 껍데기를 벗겨 내고 있었다. 새로운 특징 하나하나가 그저, 양초 기름 얼룩에서 그에게 별안간 다가왔던 그대로, 그 모습 전체를 온전한 힘과 활기 속에서 드러내 줄 뿐이었다. 그가 그 모습을 꼼꼼히 마무리하고 있을 때 명함이 그에게 전해진 것이었다.\n\n\"갑니다, 가요!\"\n\n그는 아내에게로 들어갔다.\n\n\"이봐, 사샤, 화내지 마!\" 그는 수줍은 듯 다정하게 아내에게 미소 지으며 말했다. \"당신 잘못이었어. 내 잘못이었지. 내가 다 잘 풀어 볼게.\" 그렇게 아내와 화해한 뒤 그는 벨벳 깃이 달린 올리브색 외투를 걸치고 모자를 쓴 채 아틀리에로 향했다. 잘 그려진 그 모습은 그가 벌써 잊어버린 뒤였다. 이제 그는 마차를 타고 찾아온, 이 지체 높은 사람들, 러시아인들의 방문에 기뻐 들떠 있었다.\n\n지금 자기 이젤 위에 놓여 있는 그림에 대해, 그는 마음 깊은 곳에 한 가지 확신을 품고 있었다. 그 누구도 이런 그림을 그린 적이 없다는 확신이었다. 그는 자기 그림이 라파엘로의 모든 그림보다 낫다고 믿지는 않았지만, 자기가 그 그림에서 전하고자 한 것을 그 누구도 전한 적이 없다는 것만은 알고 있었다. 이것을 그는 확실히, 그것도 오래전부터, 그 그림을 그리기 시작한 바로 그때부터 알고 있었다. 그렇지만 다른 사람들의 평가는, 그것이 어떤 것이든, 그의 눈에는 여전히 엄청난 무게를 지녔고, 그의 영혼 밑바닥까지 뒤흔들어 놓았다. 어떤 말이든, 아무리 하찮은 말이라도, 그 비평가가 자기가 그림 속에서 본 것 중 아주 작은 일부라도 보았음을 드러내는 말이면 그의 영혼 밑바닥까지 뒤흔들었다. 그는 늘 자기 비평가들에게 자기 자신보다 더 깊은 이해력이 있으리라고 여겼고, 늘 그들에게서 자기가 그림 속에서 미처 보지 못한 무언가를 기대했다. 그리고 종종 그들의 비평 속에서 자기가 바로 그것을 찾아냈다고 믿곤 했다.\n\n그는 아틀리에 문으로 빠르게 걸어갔는데, 들뜬 와중에도 입구의 그늘에 선 안나의 모습에 비치는 부드러운 빛에 마음이 사로잡혔다. 그녀는 무언가를 열심히 이야기하는 골레니셰프의 말에 귀를 기울이면서도, 분명 화가 쪽을 돌아보고 싶어 하는 기색이었다. 그가 그들에게 다가가면서 어떻게 이 인상을 붙잡아, 자기에게 시가를 팔았던 가게 주인의 턱을 그랬던 것처럼 빨아들였는지, 그리고 필요할 때 꺼내 쓰려고 그것을 어딘가에 챙겨 두었는지, 그 자신은 의식하지 못했다. 손님들은 골레니셰프가 그 화가에 대해 늘어놓은 이야기에 미리부터 호감을 갖지 못한 터였지만, 그의 외모에는 더더욱 호감이 가지 않았다. 다부진 체격에 중키, 민첩한 동작, 갈색 모자에 올리브색 외투, 그리고 통 넓은 바지가 유행한 지 한참이나 되었는데도 그가 입고 있는 좁은 바지, 무엇보다도 넓적한 얼굴의 그 평범함, 거기에 수줍음과 위신을 지키려는 안달이 뒤섞인 표정까지, 미하일로프는 유쾌하지 못한 인상을 주었다.\n\n\"들어오시지요.\" 그는 무심한 표정을 지으려 애쓰며 말하고는, 복도로 들어가 주머니에서 열쇠를 꺼내 문을 열었다.\n\n# Chapter 11\n\n화실로 들어서면서 미하일로프는 다시 한번 방문객들을 훑어보았고, 상상 속에 브론스키의 표정 또한, 특히 그 턱을 새겨 두었다. 그의 예술적 감각은 끊임없이 재료를 모으느라 쉴 새 없이 작동했고, 자기 작품이 비평받을 순간이 다가올수록 점점 더 커지는 흥분을 느꼈지만, 그는 눈에 띄지 않는 단서들로부터 이 세 사람에 대한 심상을 재빠르고 섬세하게 빚어냈다.\n\n저 사내(골레니셰프)는 이곳에 사는 러시아인이었다. 미하일로프는 그의 성도, 어디서 그를 만났는지도, 그에게 무슨 말을 했는지도 기억하지 못했다. 그저 자기가 본 모든 얼굴을 기억하듯 그의 얼굴을 기억할 뿐이었다. 하지만 그는 그 얼굴이 자기 기억 속에서 거짓되게 거드름을 부리며 표정이 빈곤한 부류라는 거대한 범주에 분류해 둔 얼굴 가운데 하나라는 것도 기억했다. 풍성한 머리카락과 몹시 훤한 이마가 얼굴에 거드름의 분위기를 주었지만, 그 얼굴에는 좁은 콧날 바로 위에 집중된, 옹졸하고 어린애 같으며 짜증스러운 표정 하나밖에 없었다. 브론스키와 마담 카레니나는, 미하일로프가 짐작하기로, 모든 부유한 러시아인이 그렇듯 예술에 대해서는 아무것도 모르면서 애호가요 감식가인 척하는, 지체 높고 부유한 러시아인임이 분명했다. \"필시 저들은 골동품은 이미 다 둘러보았을 거고, 이제 신진들의 화실을, 그러니까 그 독일 사기꾼이며 머리가 좀 돈 라파엘 전파의 영국인 화가 같은 자들을 한 바퀴 돌고 나서, 시각을 완비하려고 내게도 들른 것이겠지.\" 그는 생각했다. 그는 호사가들이(더 영리할수록 그에게는 더 고약하게 느껴졌다) 동시대 화가들의 작품을 바라보는 방식을 익히 알고 있었다. 오로지 예술은 한물간 것이라고, 신진들을 보면 볼수록 위대한 옛 거장들의 작품이 얼마나 흉내 낼 수 없는 것인지 알게 된다고 말할 수 있는 처지에 서기 위해서만 작품을 보는 것이었다. 그는 이 모든 것을 예상하고 있었다. 그는 그것을 그들의 얼굴에서 보았고, 그들이 자기들끼리 무심하게 이야기를 주고받으며 모형 인형이며 흉상을 멍하니 바라보고, 그가 그림을 가릴 것을 벗겨 주기를 기다리며 느긋하게 거니는 그 태평한 무관심에서 보았다. 그러나 그럼에도, 습작들을 뒤적이고 차양을 걷어 올리며 덮개를 벗기는 동안, 그는 극심한 흥분에 휩싸여 있었다. 지체 높고 부유한 러시아인이라면 죄다 짐승이요 멍청이임이 틀림없다는 확신에도 불구하고, 그는 브론스키가 마음에 들었고, 안나는 더더욱 마음에 들었기 때문이었다.\n\n\"여기, 보십시오.\" 그는 날렵한 걸음으로 한쪽으로 비켜서며 자기 그림을 가리키며 말했다. \"빌라도에게 권고하는 장면입니다. 마태복음 27장이지요.\" 그는 감정에 북받쳐 입술이 떨리기 시작하는 것을 느끼며 말했다. 그러고는 물러서서 그들 뒤에 가서 섰다.\n\n방문객들이 말없이 그림을 응시하던 그 몇 초 동안, 미하일로프 또한 무관한 외부인의 무심한 눈으로 그것을 바라보았다. 그 몇 초 동안 그는 조금 전까지만 해도 그토록 경멸하던 바로 그 방문객들에게서 더 높고 더 정당한 비평이 나오리라고 미리 확신했다. 그는 그 그림을 그려 온 삼 년 동안 그것에 대해 생각했던 모든 것을 잊어버렸다. 그에게 절대적으로 확실했던 그것의 온갖 미덕을 잊어버렸다. 그는 그들의 무심하고 새롭고 외부적인 눈으로 그림을 보았고, 거기서 아무런 좋은 점도 보지 못했다. 그는 전경에서 빌라도의 짜증 난 얼굴과 그리스도의 평온한 얼굴을, 배경에서 빌라도의 수행원들의 형상과 벌어지는 일을 지켜보는 요한의 얼굴을 보았다. 그토록 고뇌하며, 그토록 헤매고 고치며 그 안에서 저마다의 독특한 성격을 띠고 자라난 모든 얼굴, 그에게 그토록 큰 고통과 환희를 안겨 준 모든 얼굴, 전체의 조화를 위해 그토록 여러 번 자리를 옮긴 이 모든 얼굴, 그토록 애써 도달한 온갖 색조와 색감—이 모든 것이 한데 어우러져, 그들의 눈으로 바라보니 이제 그에게는 더없이 천박한 것, 천 번도 넘게 그려진 것처럼 보였다. 그에게 가장 소중한 얼굴, 그림의 중심인 그리스도의 얼굴, 그에게 펼쳐지면서 그토록 큰 황홀을 안겨 준 그 얼굴은, 그들의 눈으로 그림을 흘끗 보자 그에게서 완전히 사라져 버렸다. 그는 티치아노, 라파엘로, 루벤스의 그 끝없는 그리스도들과 똑같은 병사들과 빌라도를 잘 그려 낸(아니, 그것조차 아니었다—이제 그는 결함 덩어리를 또렷이 보았다) 반복을 보았다. 그것은 모두 흔하고 빈약하고 진부했으며, 명백히 형편없이 그려진—약하고 고르지 못한 것이었다. 그들이 화가 앞에서 위선적으로 정중한 말을 늘어놓다가, 다시 자기들끼리 남게 되면 그를 가엾어하고 비웃는다 해도 그것은 마땅한 일이었다.\n\n침묵이(채 일 분도 지속되지 않았건만) 그에게는 견딜 수 없을 만큼 괴로워졌다. 침묵을 깨고, 자기가 동요하지 않았음을 보이려고, 그는 애써 골레니셰프에게 말을 건넸다.\n\n\"제가 전에 뵌 적이 있는 것 같습니다만.\" 그는 그들 표정의 어떤 미묘한 결도 놓칠까 두려워하며 불안하게 처음엔 안나를, 다음엔 브론스키를 바라보며 말했다.\n\n\"물론이지요! 로시 댁에서 만났잖습니까, 기억하시나요, 그 이탈리아 부인이—새로운 라셸이라던 그 여인이—낭송하던 그 soirée 자리에서요?\" 골레니셰프는 조금의 아쉬움도 없이 그림에서 시선을 거두며 화가 쪽으로 돌아서서는 편안하게 대답했다.\n* soirée: 프랑스어로 '저녁 모임, 야회'.\n\n그러나 미하일로프가 그림에 대한 비평을 기다리고 있음을 알아채고서 그는 말했다.\n\n\"당신 그림은 지난번에 봤을 때보다 훨씬 진척되었군요. 그리고 지금도 그때처럼 제게 특히 인상적인 것은 빌라도의 형상입니다. 그 인물을 정말 잘 알겠어요. 사람 좋고 더없이 훌륭한 사내지만, 뼛속까지 관리라 자기가 무슨 일을 하는지 모르는 자 말입니다. 하지만 제 생각엔…….\"\n\n미하일로프의 변화무쌍한 얼굴이 한순간에 환하게 빛났다. 그의 눈이 반짝였다. 그는 무언가 말하려 했지만 흥분 때문에 말이 나오지 않아 기침을 하는 척했다. 골레니셰프의 예술 이해 능력에 대한 그의 평가가 아무리 낮았다 해도, 빌라도를 관리로서 표현한 충실함에 관한 그 옳은 지적이 아무리 사소했다 해도, 더 진지한 점에 대해서는 아무 말도 없는 마당에 그처럼 하찮은 관찰을 입에 올린 것이 아무리 거슬릴 만했다 해도, 미하일로프는 이 관찰에 황홀한 기쁨에 휩싸였다. 그 자신이 빌라도의 형상에 대해 골레니셰프가 말한 바로 그대로 생각하고 있었던 것이다. 이 견해가 미하일로프가 확실히 알기로 진실일 수백만 가지 견해 가운데 하나에 불과하다는 사실도, 그에게 골레니셰프의 지적이 갖는 의미를 깎아내리지는 못했다. 그의 마음은 이 지적 덕분에 골레니셰프에게 따뜻해졌고, 그는 침울한 상태에서 갑자기 황홀로 옮겨 갔다. 단숨에 그의 그림 전체가, 살아 있는 모든 것의 형언할 수 없는 복잡함을 고스란히 띤 채 그의 앞에 살아났다. 미하일로프는 다시 빌라도를 자기가 그렇게 이해했노라고 말하려 했지만, 입술이 도무지 말을 듣지 않고 떨려서 그 말을 발음할 수 없었다. 브론스키와 안나 또한 무언가를 그 낮은 목소리로 말했는데, 사람들은 한편으로는 화가의 감정을 상하게 하지 않으려고, 또 한편으로는 예술을 이야기할 때 그토록 쉽게 튀어나오는 어리석은 말을 큰 소리로 내뱉지 않으려고 그림 전시회에서 흔히 그런 목소리로 말하곤 했다. 미하일로프는 그림이 그들에게도 인상을 남겼다고 짐작했다. 그는 그들에게 다가갔다.\n\n\"그리스도의 표정이 정말 경이로워요!\" 안나가 말했다. 그녀가 본 모든 것 가운데 그녀는 그 표정이 가장 마음에 들었고, 그것이 그림의 중심임을 느꼈으므로, 그 표정에 대한 칭찬이 화가에게 흐뭇하리라고 생각했다. \"그분이 빌라도를 가엾어하고 있다는 걸 알 수 있어요.\"\n\n이 또한 그의 그림에서, 그리고 그리스도의 형상에서 찾아낼 수 있는 수백만 가지 옳은 견해 가운데 하나였다. 그녀는 그분이 빌라도를 가엾어하고 있다고 말했다. 그리스도의 표정에는 마땅히 연민의 표정이 있어야 했다. 거기에는 사랑의 표정, 천상의 평화의 표정, 죽음을 받아들일 각오의 표정, 그리고 말의 헛됨에 대한 감각이 있기 때문이다. 물론 빌라도에게는 관리의 표정이, 그리스도에게는 연민의 표정이 있다. 하나는 육신의 화신이고 다른 하나는 영적 삶의 화신이니 말이다. 이 모든 것과 그 밖의 더 많은 것이 미하일로프의 머릿속에 번득였다.\n\n\"그래요, 그리고 저 형상이 어찌나 잘 그려졌는지—저 분위기라니! 그 둘레를 빙 돌아 걸을 수도 있겠어요.\" 골레니셰프가 말했는데, 이 말로 그가 그 형상의 의미와 사상에는 동의하지 않음을 여지없이 드러냈다.\n\n\"그래요, 놀라운 솜씨예요!\" 브론스키가 말했다. \"저 배경의 형상들이 어쩌면 저렇게 도드라지는지! 저것이야말로 기법이라는 거지요.\" 그는 그 기법에 도달하지 못하는 자신의 절망에 대해 둘이서 나눈 대화를 빗대어 골레니셰프를 향해 말했다.\n\n\"그래요, 그래요, 경이롭습니다!\" 골레니셰프와 안나가 맞장구를 쳤다. 흥분한 상태였음에도, 기법에 관한 그 말은 미하일로프의 가슴을 찌르듯 아프게 했고, 그는 브론스키를 성난 눈으로 바라보며 갑자기 인상을 찌푸렸다. 그는 이 기법이라는 말을 자주 들었지만, 그것이 무엇을 뜻하는지 도무지 이해할 수가 없었다. 이 용어로 사람들이 그림의 주제와는 전혀 무관한, 그리거나 칠하는 기계적 숙련을 뜻한다는 것을 그는 알고 있었다. 그는 심지어 칭찬을 할 때조차 기법이 본질적 가치와 대립되곤 한다는 것을, 마치 형편없는 무언가를 잘 그릴 수도 있다는 듯이 여겨진다는 것을 종종 알아챘다. 그는 덮개를 벗겨 내는 데 많은 주의와 정성이 필요하다는 것을, 창작물 자체를 손상시키지 않으면서 모든 덮개를 다 벗겨 내야 한다는 것을 알고 있었다. 그러나 거기에 그림을 그리는 기술이란—그 어떤 기법이란—없었다. 만약 어린아이나 자기 집 요리사에게 자기가 본 것이 드러난다면, 그 아이나 그 여자도 본 것에서 포장을 벗겨 낼 수 있을 것이었다. 그리고 가장 노련하고 솜씨 좋은 화가라도 주제의 선들이 먼저 자기에게 드러나지 않는다면 단순한 기계적 숙련만으로는 아무것도 그릴 수 없었다. 게다가 그는, 기법을 이야기하는 거라면 그 점에서 자기를 칭찬하기란 불가능하다는 것을 알았다. 자기가 그리고 또 그린 모든 것에서 그는 눈에 거슬리는 결함들을, 포장을 벗겨 낼 때 정성이 부족해서 생긴, 이제 와서는 전체를 망치지 않고서는 고칠 수 없는 결함들을 보았다. 그리고 거의 모든 형상과 얼굴에서 그는 또한 완전히 제거되지 못해 그림을 망치는 포장의 잔재를 보았다.\n\n\"한 가지 말씀드리고 싶은 게 있는데요, 한마디 해도 좋다면…….\" 골레니셰프가 의견을 냈다.\n\n\"오, 기꺼이 듣겠습니다, 어서 말씀하세요.\" 미하일로프가 억지웃음을 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"그러니까, 당신은 그분을 신인(神人)이 아니라 인신(人神)으로 그렸다는 겁니다. 하지만 그게 바로 당신이 의도한 바라는 것은 압니다.\"\n\n\"제 마음속에 없는 그리스도는 그릴 수 없습니다.\" 미하일로프가 침울하게 말했다.\n\n\"그래요. 하지만 그렇다면, 제 생각을 말해도 좋다면…… 당신 그림은 너무나 훌륭해서 제 견해 따위가 그것을 깎아내릴 수는 없고, 게다가 이건 제 개인적인 의견일 뿐입니다. 당신은 다르지요. 당신의 동기 자체가 다릅니다. 하지만 이바노프를 예로 들어 봅시다. 제 생각에는, 만약 그리스도가 역사적 인물의 수준으로 끌어내려지는 거라면, 이바노프로서는 다른 어떤 역사적 주제를, 신선하고 손대지 않은 주제를 택하는 편이 더 나았을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"하지만 이것이 예술에 주어진 가장 위대한 주제라면요?\"\n\n\"찾아본다면 다른 것들도 찾을 수 있겠지요. 하지만 요점은 예술이 의심과 논의를 견뎌 낼 수 없다는 겁니다. 그런데 이바노프의 그림 앞에서는 신자에게나 불신자에게나 똑같이 '저것이 신인가, 아니면 신이 아닌가?' 하는 의문이 일어나고, 그러면 인상의 통일성이 깨져 버립니다.\"\n\n\"어째서 그렇지요? 제 생각에 교양 있는 사람에게는,\" 미하일로프가 말했다, \"그런 의문이 있을 수 없습니다.\"\n\n골레니셰프는 이에 동의하지 않았고, 인상의 통일성이 예술에 필수적이라는 자신의 첫 견해를 거듭 옹호하여 미하일로프를 당혹스럽게 했다.\n\n미하일로프는 몹시 마음이 어지러웠지만, 자기 견해를 옹호하여 아무 말도 할 수 없었다.\n\n# Chapter 12\n\n안나와 브론스키는 벌써부터 서로 눈짓을 주고받으며, 친구가 늘어놓는 그 영리한 말들의 흐름을 안타까워하고 있었다. 마침내 브론스키는 화가를 기다리지도 않고 다른 작은 그림 쪽으로 걸어가 버렸다.\n\n\"오, 얼마나 절묘한지! 정말 사랑스러운 작품이군! 보석 같아! 어쩌면 이렇게 절묘할까!\" 두 사람이 한목소리로 외쳤다.\n\n'대체 뭐가 저렇게 좋다는 거지?' 미하일로프는 생각했다. 그는 삼 년 전에 그린 그 그림을 완전히 잊고 있었다. 몇 달 동안 밤낮으로 그를 사로잡았던 단 하나의 생각이었을 때, 그 그림과 함께 겪었던 온갖 고뇌와 환희를 그는 깡그리 잊고 있었던 것이다. 늘 그래 왔듯이, 그는 완성한 그림들을 잊어버렸다. 그는 그 그림을 들여다보는 것조차 좋아하지 않았으며, 그것을 사고 싶어 하는 어느 영국인이 오기를 기다리고 있었기에 마지못해 꺼내 두었을 따름이었다.\n\n\"아, 그건 그냥 오래된 습작입니다.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"얼마나 훌륭한지!\" 골레니셰프도 의심할 여지 없이 진지한 태도로, 역시 그림의 마력에 사로잡혀 말했다.\n\n버드나무 그늘 아래에서 두 소년이 낚시를 하고 있었다. 형으로 보이는 아이는 막 낚싯바늘을 던져 넣고, 자기 일에 온통 몰두한 채 덤불 뒤에서 조심스럽게 찌를 끌어당기고 있었다. 다른 아이는, 형보다 조금 어려 보이는데, 풀밭에 엎드려 팔꿈치를 괴고, 헝클어진 아마빛 머리를 두 손에 받친 채, 꿈꾸는 듯한 파란 눈으로 물을 바라보고 있었다. 무슨 생각을 하고 있던 것일까?\n\n이 그림에 쏟아지는 열광은 미하일로프의 마음속에 그 그림에 대한 옛 감정을 얼마쯤 흔들어 깨웠지만, 그는 지난 일에 대한 감정을 이렇게 헛되이 소모하는 것을 두려워하고 또 싫어했기에, 비록 그 칭찬이 고맙기는 했어도 손님들을 세 번째 그림 쪽으로 이끌어 가려고 애썼다.\n\n그러나 브론스키는 그 그림이 파는 것이냐고 물었다. 손님들 때문에 들떠 있던 미하일로프에게, 그 순간 돈 이야기를 하는 것은 더없이 거북한 일이었다.\n\n\"팔려고 거기 내놓은 겁니다.\" 그는 음울하게 미간을 찌푸리며 대답했다.\n\n손님들이 떠나자 미하일로프는 빌라도와 그리스도를 그린 그림 맞은편에 앉아, 그들이 했던 말과, 입 밖에 내지는 않았으나 그 손님들이 은연중에 내비친 것들을 머릿속으로 되짚어 보았다. 그런데 이상하게도, 그들이 거기 있는 동안, 그리고 그가 마음속으로 그들의 관점에 자기를 세워 두고 있는 동안 그토록 무게를 지녔던 것들이, 별안간 그에게는 아무 의미도 없는 것이 되어 버렸다. 그는 자기만의 온전한 화가의 눈으로 그림을 바라보기 시작했고, 이내 자기 그림이 완성에 이를 수 있다는, 그리하여 의미를 지닌다는 그 확신의 상태에 빠져들었다. 그것은 다른 모든 관심사를 배제하는 가장 격렬한 열정에 꼭 필요한 확신이었으며, 오직 그 안에서만 그는 일할 수 있었다.\n\n그렇지만 단축법으로 그린 그리스도의 다리가 어딘지 잘못되어 있었다. 그는 팔레트를 집어 들고 작업을 시작했다. 다리를 고치면서 그는 배경에 있는 요한의 모습을 끊임없이 바라보았는데, 손님들은 그것을 눈여겨보지도 않았으나 그는 그것이 완성의 경지를 넘어섰음을 알고 있었다. 다리를 다 손보고 나자 그는 그 모습에 손을 대고 싶었으나, 그러기에는 마음이 너무 들떠 있음을 느꼈다. 그는 차갑게 식어 있을 때나, 너무 깊이 감정에 휩쓸려 모든 것이 지나치게 또렷이 보일 때나 똑같이 일을 할 수가 없었다. 차가움에서 영감으로 옮겨 가는 과정에는 작업이 가능한 단 하나의 단계가 있을 뿐이었다. 오늘은 너무 들떠 있었다. 그는 그림을 덮어 버리려다가 천을 손에 쥔 채 멈춰 서서, 더없이 행복하게 미소 지으며 오랫동안 요한의 모습을 바라보았다. 마침내 아쉬워하듯 몸을 떼어 내며 천을 떨어뜨리고는, 지쳤으나 행복한 마음으로 집으로 돌아갔다.\n\n브론스키와 안나, 골레니셰프는 집으로 돌아가는 길에 유난히 활기차고 명랑했다. 그들은 미하일로프와 그의 그림들에 대해 이야기했다. talent라는 말이 그들의 대화에 유독 자주 되풀이되었는데, 그들은 그것으로 머리나 가슴과는 별개인, 타고난, 거의 육체적이라 할 소질을 뜻했고, 그 말 속에서 그 화가가 삶으로부터 얻은 모든 것을 표현해 보려 했다. 마치 자기들이 그것에 대해 이야기하고 싶어 하면서도 정작 아무런 개념도 갖고 있지 못한 그 무언가를 어떻게든 정리해야 한다는 듯이 말이다. 그들은 그의 재능을 부인할 수 없으나, 그 재능이 교양의 부족 탓에 꽃피우지 못한다고, 그것이 우리 러시아 화가들에게 흔한 결함이라고 말했다. 그러나 두 소년을 그린 그림은 그들의 기억 속에 또렷이 새겨져, 그들은 끊임없이 그 그림으로 되돌아왔다. \"정말 절묘한 작품이야! 그 사람이 어쩌면 그렇게, 또 그토록 단순하게 그걸 해냈을까! 그 사람은 그게 얼마나 훌륭한지 알지도 못한다니까. 그래, 이 기회를 놓쳐선 안 돼. 꼭 사야겠어.\" 브론스키가 말했다.\n* talent: 영어로 '재능'.\n\n# Chapter 13\n\n미하일로프는 브론스키에게 그림을 팔았고, 안나의 초상화를 그리는 데에도 동의했다. 정해진 날에 그는 와서 작업을 시작했다.\n\n다섯 번째 포즈를 취한 자리부터 그 초상화는 모든 사람에게, 특히 브론스키에게 깊은 인상을 주었는데, 닮은 것뿐 아니라 그녀 특유의 아름다움 때문이었다. 미하일로프가 어떻게 바로 그녀 특유의 아름다움을 찾아낼 수 있었는지 신기할 따름이었다. \"그녀 영혼의 가장 사랑스러운 표정을 찾아내려면, 내가 그녀를 사랑해 온 것처럼 그녀를 알고 사랑해야만 해.\" 브론스키는 그렇게 생각했지만, 정작 그 자신이 그녀 영혼의 가장 사랑스러운 표정을 알게 된 것도 다름 아닌 이 초상화를 통해서였다. 그러나 그 표정이 어찌나 진실했던지, 그도 다른 사람들도 그것을 오래전부터 알고 있었다고 여길 정도였다.\n\n\"나는 끝없이 오랫동안 끙끙대면서도 아무것도 해내지 못했는데,\" 그는 자기가 그린 그녀의 초상화를 두고 말했다. \"저 사람은 그저 한번 보고는 그려 버렸어. 바로 거기서 기법이 빛을 발하는 거지.\"\n\n\"그것도 차차 갖춰질 겁니다.\" 골레니셰프가 위로하며 안심시키듯 그에게 말했다. 그가 보기에 브론스키에게는 재능이 있었고, 무엇보다 중요한 것으로, 예술에 대한 더 넓은 안목을 갖게 해 주는 교양이 있었다. 브론스키의 재능에 대한 골레니셰프의 믿음은, 자기 자신의 글과 사상에 대한 브론스키의 공감과 인정을 바라는 그 자신의 필요에 떠받쳐지고 있었고, 그는 칭찬과 지지가 서로 주고받는 것이어야 한다고 느꼈다.\n\n남의 집에서, 특히 브론스키의 palazzo에서 미하일로프는 자기 화실에 있을 때와는 완전히 다른 사람이었다. 그는 적의 어린 예의를 갖추고 행동했는데, 마치 자기가 존경하지 않는 사람들에게 가까이 다가가기를 두려워하는 듯했다. 그는 브론스키를 \"각하\"라고 불렀고, 안나와 브론스키가 아무리 청해도 결코 만찬에 머무는 법이 없었으며, 포즈를 취하는 일이 아니고는 오지 않았다. 안나는 다른 사람들에게보다 그에게 더 다정하게 대했고, 자기 초상화에 대해 무척 고마워했다. 브론스키는 그에게 더없이 살가웠고, 화가가 자기 그림을 어떻게 보는지 알고 싶어 하는 기색이 역력했다. 골레니셰프는 미하일로프에게 예술에 관한 건전한 견해를 불어넣을 기회를 결코 놓치지 않았다. 그러나 미하일로프는 그들 모두에게 한결같이 쌀쌀했다. 안나는 그가 자기를 바라보기를 좋아한다는 것을 그의 눈에서 알아챘지만, 그는 그녀와 대화하는 것을 피했다. 브론스키가 자기 그림에 대해 늘어놓는 말에 그는 고집스러운 침묵으로 응했고, 브론스키의 그림을 보여 주었을 때도 똑같이 고집스럽게 침묵했다. 그는 골레니셰프의 이야기에 분명히 따분해했지만, 그에게 반박하려고 하지는 않았다.\n\n요컨대 미하일로프는, 마음을 닫은 채 못마땅해하는, 말하자면 적의 어린 태도 탓에, 그들이 그를 더 잘 알게 될수록 영 미움을 샀다. 그래서 그들은 포즈를 취하는 일이 다 끝나고 멋진 초상화를 손에 넣게 되어 그가 더는 찾아오지 않게 되자 흐뭇해했다. 그들 모두에게 떠올랐던 한 가지 생각을 처음으로 입 밖에 낸 사람은 골레니셰프였는데, 그것은 미하일로프가 그저 브론스키를 질투하고 있을 뿐이라는 것이었다.\n\n\"부러워한다고는 하지 맙시다, 그에게는 재능이 있으니까요. 다만 거슬리는 거지요. 최상류 사교계의 부유한 사람이, 게다가 백작이기까지 한 사람이(아시다시피 저들은 작위라면 다들 질색하잖습니까) 별다른 수고도 들이지 않고, 평생을 그 일에 바쳐 온 자기만큼은, 아니 어쩌면 자기보다 더 잘해 낼 수 있다는 게 말입니다. 그리고 무엇보다도, 그건 교양의 문제인데, 저 사람에게는 그게 없거든요.\"\n\n브론스키는 미하일로프를 두둔했지만, 마음 깊은 곳에서는 그 말을 믿었다. 그가 보기에 다른 세계, 더 낮은 세계에 속한 사람이라면 틀림없이 부러워할 게 분명했으니까.\n\n안나의 초상화—그와 미하일로프가 둘 다 실물을 보고 그린 똑같은 대상—는 브론스키에게 자기와 미하일로프 사이의 차이를 보여 주었어야 마땅했으나, 그는 그것을 보지 못했다. 다만 미하일로프의 초상화가 완성되고 나서, 그는 이제 더는 필요 없다고 판단하고 안나의 초상화를 그리는 일을 그만두었다. 중세 생활을 담은 그의 그림은 계속 그려 나갔다. 그리고 그 자신도, 골레니셰프도, 그리고 안나는 더더욱, 그것을 아주 훌륭하다고 여겼는데, 그것이 미하일로프의 그림보다 자기들이 아는 그 유명한 그림들과 훨씬 더 비슷했기 때문이다.\n\n한편 미하일로프는, 안나의 초상화가 그를 크게 매료시키긴 했지만, 포즈를 취하는 일이 끝나고 더는 예술에 관한 골레니셰프의 일장연설을 듣지 않아도 되고 브론스키의 그림에 대해 잊어버릴 수 있게 되자, 그들보다 한층 더 기뻐했다. 그는 브론스키가 그림으로 소일하는 것을 막을 수 없다는 것을 알았고, 브론스키도 모든 호사가도 자기가 좋아하는 것을 그릴 완전한 권리가 있다는 것을 알았지만, 그것이 그에게는 거슬렸다. 사람이 자기 손으로 커다란 밀랍 인형을 만들어 거기에 입을 맞추는 것을 막을 수는 없는 노릇이다. 그러나 그 사람이 그 인형을 들고 와서 사랑에 빠진 남자 앞에 앉아, 사랑하는 남자가 사랑하는 여자를 어루만지듯 자기 인형을 어루만지기 시작한다면, 그것은 사랑에 빠진 남자에게 거슬리는 일일 것이다. 미하일로프가 브론스키의 그림을 보면서 느낀 것이 바로 그런 거슬리는 감각이었다. 그는 그것을 우습기도 하고 짜증스럽기도 하며, 가엾기도 하고 모욕적이기도 하다고 느꼈다.\n\n회화와 중세에 대한 브론스키의 관심은 오래가지 못했다. 그는 자기 그림을 끝내지 못할 만큼은 회화에 대한 안목을 가지고 있었다. 그림은 멈춰 서고 말았다. 그는 처음에는 눈에 띄지 않던 그 결함들이, 만약 계속 그려 나간다면 두드러지게 드러나리라는 것을 어렴풋이 알고 있었다. 그에게도 골레니셰프와 똑같은 일이 닥친 셈이었는데, 골레니셰프는 자기에게 할 말이 없다는 것을 느끼면서도, 자기 사상이 아직 무르익지 않았을 뿐이며 그것을 다듬어 가며 자료를 모으는 중이라는 이론으로 끊임없이 자신을 속이고 있었다. 이것이 골레니셰프를 화나게 하고 괴롭혔지만, 브론스키는 자기 자신을 속이거나 괴롭힐 줄 모르는 사람이었고, 화를 내는 것은 더더욱 할 줄 몰랐다. 그는 그 특유의 결단력으로, 아무런 해명도 변명도 없이, 그저 그림 작업을 그만두어 버렸다.\n\n그러나 이 일거리가 없어지자, 브론스키의 삶도, 그가 그림에 흥미를 잃은 것을 의아해하던 안나의 삶도, 이탈리아의 한 도시에서 견디기 힘들 만큼 따분하게 느껴졌다. 그 palazzo는 갑자기 거슬리도록 낡고 지저분해 보였고, 커튼의 얼룩이며 마룻바닥의 갈라진 틈이며 처마 돌림띠의 부서진 회반죽이 어찌나 보기 싫게 도드라지는지, 골레니셰프의 한결같은 똑같음이며 이탈리아인 교수와 독일인 여행자가 어찌나 진력나는지, 그들은 뭔가 변화를 주지 않을 수 없었다. 그들은 러시아로, 시골로 가기로 결심했다. 페테르부르크에서 브론스키는 형과 땅을 나누는 일을 처리할 작정이었고, 안나는 아들을 만날 생각이었다. 여름은 브론스키의 큰 영지에서 보낼 생각이었다.\n\n# Chapter 14\n\n레빈은 결혼한 지 석 달이 되었다. 그는 행복했지만, 결코 그가 기대했던 방식으로는 아니었다. 발걸음을 옮길 때마다 그는 예전의 꿈들이 어그러지는 것을 발견했고, 또 행복의 새롭고 뜻하지 않은 놀라움들을 발견했다. 그는 행복했다. 그러나 가정생활에 들어서면서 발걸음을 옮길 때마다 그것이 자기가 상상했던 것과는 완전히 다르다는 것을 깨달았다. 발걸음을 옮길 때마다 그는, 호수 위를 미끄러지듯 행복하게 나아가는 작은 배를 감탄하며 바라보다가 막상 그 작은 배에 올라탄 사람이 겪을 법한 것을 겪었다. 그는 가만히 앉아 매끄럽게 떠가기만 하면 되는 게 아니라는 것을 알게 되었다. 생각도 해야 하고, 자기가 어디로 떠가고 있는지 한순간도 잊어서는 안 되며, 발밑에는 물이 있고, 노를 저어야 하고, 익숙지 않은 손은 쓰라릴 것이며, 그저 바라보기만 하는 게 쉬웠을 뿐, 막상 그것을 하는 일은 무척 즐겁기는 해도 무척 어렵다는 것을 알게 되었다.\n\n총각 시절, 다른 사람들의 결혼 생활을 지켜보며 그 자질구레한 근심거리며 다툼이며 질투를 보았을 때, 그는 마음속으로 그저 경멸하듯 비웃었을 뿐이었다. 그는 자기의 앞으로의 결혼 생활에는 그런 것이라곤 결코 있을 수 없으리라고 확신했다. 게다가 외적인 형식조차도 모든 면에서 다른 사람들의 삶과는 완전히 다를 것이라고 그는 상상했다. 그런데 별안간, 아내와의 삶이 어떤 독자적인 양식으로 이루어지기는커녕, 오히려 전에 그가 그토록 멸시했던 바로 그 자질구레하기 그지없는 세부적인 일들로 온통 이루어지고 말았다. 그리고 그 세부적인 일들은 이제, 그의 의지와는 아무 상관없이, 맞서 싸워 봐야 소용없는 엄청난 중요성을 띠게 되었다. 그리고 레빈은 이 모든 세부적인 일들을 꾸려 나가는 것이 자기가 전에 상상했던 것처럼 결코 쉽지 않다는 것을 알게 되었다. 레빈은 자기가 가정생활에 대해 더없이 정확한 관념을 지니고 있다고 믿었지만, 모든 남자가 그렇듯, 그도 무의식중에 가정생활을 방해하는 것도 없고 마음을 흩뜨리는 자질구레한 근심거리도 없이 사랑을 가장 행복하게 누리는 것으로 그려 보았던 것이다. 그가 그 처지를 머릿속에 그린 바로는, 그는 자기 일을 하고 사랑의 행복 속에서 그 일로부터의 안식을 찾아야 했다. 아내는 사랑받아야 하고, 그것이면 충분했다. 그러나 모든 남자가 그렇듯, 그는 아내도 일을 원하리라는 것을 잊고 있었다. 그래서 그는 자기의 시적이고 우아한 키티가, 결혼 생활의 첫 몇 주뿐 아니라 첫 며칠부터도, 식탁보며 가구며 손님용 매트리스며 쟁반이며 요리사며 저녁 식사며 따위에 대해 생각하고 기억하고 부산을 떨 수 있다는 데에 놀랐다. 아직 약혼 중이었을 때에도, 그녀가 마치 자기가 무엇을 원하는지 알고 있고 또 사랑 바깥의 무언가를 여전히 생각할 수 있기라도 한 것처럼, 외국 여행을 단호하게 거절하고 시골로 가기로 결정한 그 명확함에 그는 충격을 받았었다. 그때도 이 일이 그의 마음을 거슬렀었는데, 이제는 그녀의 사소한 근심과 걱정들이 여러 차례 그의 마음을 거슬렀다. 그러나 그는 이것이 그녀에게 꼭 필요한 일임을 알게 되었다. 그리고 그는 그녀를 사랑했으므로, 비록 그 까닭을 이해하지는 못하고 이 살림살이에 매달리는 일을 비웃기는 했어도, 그것에 감탄하지 않을 수 없었다. 그는 그녀가 모스크바에서 가져온 가구를 배치하는 방식이며, 방을 새로 꾸미고, 커튼을 달고, 손님용 방을 마련하고, 돌리를 위한 방을 준비하고, 자기의 새 하녀가 머물 곳을 돌보고, 늙은 요리사에게 저녁 식사를 주문하고, 아가피야 미하일로브나에게서 식료품 관리를 넘겨받으려고 그녀와 부딪치는 모습을 비웃었다. 그는 늙은 요리사가 그녀에게 감탄하며 미소를 짓고 그녀의 미숙하고 터무니없는 지시에 귀 기울이는 모습을, 아가피야 미하일로브나가 젊은 안주인의 새로운 방식을 두고 얼마나 구슬프고 다정하게 고개를 젓는지를 보았다. 그는 키티가 웃다 울다 하며 자기의 하녀 마샤가 그녀를 여전히 아가씨로 여기는 데에 익숙해서 아무도 자기 말을 따르지 않는다고 그에게 하소연하러 왔을 때 그녀가 유난히 사랑스럽다고 느꼈다. 그것이 그에게는 사랑스럽게, 그러나 묘하게 여겨졌고, 그는 이런 일이 없었더라면 더 좋았을 거라고 생각했다.\n\n그는 그녀가 얼마나 큰 변화를 겪고 있는지 알지 못했다. 친정에 있을 때는 때때로 좋아하는 음식이나 단것이 먹고 싶어도 그 어느 것도 손에 넣을 수 없었던 그녀가, 이제는 마음에 드는 것을 주문할 수 있고, 단것을 몇 파운드씩 사들이고, 원하는 만큼 돈을 쓰고, 좋아하는 푸딩이라면 무엇이든 주문할 수 있게 된 것이다.\n\n그녀는 이제 돌리가 아이들을 데리고 자기들 집에 올 것을 기쁨에 차서 꿈꾸고 있었는데, 특히 아이들에게 그 아이들이 좋아하는 푸딩을 주문해 줄 수 있고 돌리가 자기의 새로운 살림살이를 모두 알아봐 줄 것이기 때문이었다. 그녀 자신도 왜, 무엇 때문인지 알지 못했지만, 집을 꾸리는 일이 그녀에게는 거역할 수 없는 매력을 지니고 있었다. 본능적으로 봄이 다가오는 것을 느끼고, 또 사나운 날씨가 닥치는 날들도 있으리라는 것을 알았기에, 그녀는 할 수 있는 한 정성껏 자기의 둥지를 지었고, 동시에 그것을 짓는 일과 짓는 법을 익히는 일을 서둘렀다.\n\n레빈의 드높은 행복이라는 이상에 그토록 어긋나는 키티의 이 살림살이에 대한 정성은, 처음에는 실망거리 가운데 하나였다. 그러나 그 목적을 그가 이해하지는 못해도 사랑하지 않을 수 없었던 살림에 대한 이 사랑스러운 정성은, 새롭고 행복한 놀라움 가운데 하나였다.\n\n또 다른 실망거리이자 행복한 놀라움은 그들의 다툼 속에서 찾아왔다. 레빈은 자기와 아내 사이에 다정하고 공손하고 사랑이 넘치는 것 말고 다른 어떤 관계가 생길 수 있으리라고는 결코 상상조차 할 수 없었는데, 결혼 생활 초기에 별안간 두 사람이 다투었고, 그래서 그녀는 그가 자기를 아끼지 않으며 자기 자신밖에 모른다고 말하고는 울음을 터뜨리며 두 팔을 비틀었다.\n\n이 첫 번째 다툼은 레빈이 새로 지은 농가에 나갔다가 지름길로 집에 돌아오려다 길을 잃는 바람에 삼십 분이나 더 늦게 돌아온 데서 비롯되었다. 그는 오직 그녀만을, 그녀의 사랑을, 자기 자신의 행복을 생각하며 집으로 마차를 몰았고, 집에 가까워질수록 그녀에 대한 애정은 더욱 뜨거워졌다. 그는 청혼하러 셰르바츠키 집에 다다랐을 때 느꼈던 것과 똑같은, 아니 그보다 더 강렬한 감정을 품고 방으로 뛰어 들어갔다. 그런데 별안간 그녀에게서 한 번도 본 적 없는 음울한 표정과 마주쳤다. 그가 입을 맞추려 하자 그녀가 그를 밀쳐 냈다.\n\n\"무슨 일이야?\"\n\n\"즐기다 오셨군요.\" 그녀는 차분하면서도 앙심을 담으려 애쓰며 입을 열었다. 그러나 입을 열기가 무섭게, 비난과 터무니없는 질투와, 그녀가 창가에 꼼짝 않고 앉아 보낸 그 삼십 분 동안 그녀를 괴롭혔던 그 모든 것이 봇물처럼 쏟아져 나왔다. 그제야, 비로소 처음으로, 그는 결혼식이 끝난 뒤 교회에서 그녀를 데리고 나올 때 이해하지 못했던 것을 분명히 이해하게 되었다. 그는 이제 자기가 단지 그녀와 가까운 것이 아니라, 자기가 어디서 끝나고 그녀가 어디서 시작되는지 알 수 없다는 것을 느꼈다. 그는 그 순간 겪은, 갈라지는 듯한 고통스러운 감각에서 이것을 느꼈다. 그는 처음 한순간 모욕감을 느꼈지만, 바로 그 같은 순간 자기가 그녀에게 모욕감을 느낄 수 없다는 것을, 그녀가 곧 자기 자신이라는 것을 느꼈다. 그는 처음 한순간, 뒤에서 별안간 거센 일격을 맞고 화가 나서 복수하려고, 자기를 친 자를 찾으려고 돌아섰다가, 우연히 자기 자신을 친 것이 다름 아닌 자기 자신이며 화낼 상대가 아무도 없고 그 아픔을 참고 달래려 애쓸 수밖에 없다는 것을 깨달은 사람이 느낄 법한 것을 느꼈다.\n\n그 뒤로 그는 결코 그것을 그토록 강렬하게 느낀 적이 없었지만, 이 첫 번째만큼은 오랫동안 떨쳐 낼 수 없었다. 그의 본능적인 감정은 자기를 방어하라고, 그녀가 틀렸음을 증명해 보이라고 그를 부추겼다. 그러나 그녀가 틀렸음을 증명하는 것은 그녀를 더욱 자극하여, 그의 온갖 괴로움의 원인인 그 균열을 더 크게 벌리는 일이 되고 말 것이었다. 한 습관적인 감정은 그에게 책임을 벗어 던지고 그것을 그녀에게 떠넘기라고 몰아붙였다. 또 다른, 한층 더 강한 감정은 그에게 그 균열이 더 커지지 않도록 되도록 빨리 그것을 봉합하라고 몰아붙였다. 받을 까닭 없는 비난 아래 머무는 것은 비참한 일이었지만, 자기를 변호하여 그녀를 괴롭게 하는 것은 그보다 더 나빴다. 고통에 몸부림치며 반쯤 잠에서 깬 사람처럼, 그는 그 쑤시는 자리를 뜯어내어 멀리 내던지고 싶었지만, 정신을 차리고 보니 그 쑤시는 자리가 바로 자기 자신임을 느꼈다. 그는 그 쑤시는 자리가 아픔을 견디도록 돕는 것밖에 달리 할 수 있는 일이 없었고, 바로 그것을 하려고 애썼다.\n\n그들은 화해했다. 그녀는 입 밖으로 말하지는 않았어도 자기가 틀렸음을 인정하고 그에게 더 다정해졌으며, 두 사람은 그들의 사랑 속에서 새롭고 갑절로 커진 행복을 맛보았다. 그러나 그렇다고 해서 이런 다툼이 다시 일어나지 않는 것은 아니었고, 그것도 더없이 뜻밖의 사소한 이유로 무척 자주 일어났다. 이런 다툼은 그들이 서로에게 무엇이 중요한지 아직 알지 못한다는 사실에서, 그리고 이 초기에 두 사람 다 곧잘 언짢은 기분에 빠지곤 했다는 사실에서 자주 비롯되었다. 한쪽이 기분이 좋고 다른 한쪽이 언짢을 때는 평화가 깨지지 않았다. 그러나 두 사람이 다 같이 언짢은 기분일 때는, 나중에는 도대체 무엇 때문에 다투었는지 결코 기억해 낼 수조차 없을 만큼 영문 모를 사소한 이유에서 다툼이 솟아났다. 두 사람이 다 기분이 좋을 때면 그들의 삶의 기쁨이 갑절이 되는 것은 사실이었다. 그러나 그래도 결혼 생활의 이 첫 시기는 그들에게 힘겨운 시간이었다.\n\n이 초기 동안 내내 그들은 유난히 생생하게, 말하자면 그들을 묶어 놓은 사슬이 서로 반대 방향으로 잡아당겨지는 듯한 긴장감을 느꼈다. 전통에 따라 레빈이 그토록 많은 것을 기대했던 그들의 신혼여행, 즉 결혼 후의 한 달은, 결코 달콤한 시간이 아니었을 뿐 아니라 두 사람의 기억 속에 그들 생애에서 가장 쓰라리고 가장 굴욕적인 시기로 남았다. 두 사람은 똑같이, 훗날의 삶에서 그 병적인 시기의 온갖 끔찍하고 부끄러운 사건들을, 둘 다 좀처럼 정상적인 마음 상태가 아니었고 둘 다 좀처럼 온전히 제정신이 아니었던 그 시기의 일들을 기억에서 지워 내려고 애썼다.\n\n그들의 삶이 한결 순조롭게 흘러가기 시작한 것은, 한 달간 머물렀던 모스크바에서 돌아온 뒤, 결혼 생활의 석 달째에 이르러서였다.\n\n# Chapter 15\n\n그들은 막 모스크바에서 돌아온 참이었고, 둘만 있게 되어 기뻤다. 그는 서재의 책상 앞에 앉아 글을 쓰고 있었다. 그녀는 결혼 생활 첫 며칠 동안 입었다가 오늘 다시 꺼내 입은, 그가 특히 기억하고 사랑하는 짙은 라일락빛 드레스 차림으로, 레빈의 아버지와 할아버지 시절부터 줄곧 서재에 놓여 있던 그 낡은 가죽 소파에 앉아 있었다. 그녀는 broderie anglaise를 수놓고 있었다. 그는 그녀가 곁에 있다는 행복한 의식을 한순간도 잃지 않은 채 생각하며 글을 썼다. 토지에 관한 일이든, 새로운 토지 제도의 원칙을 정리하려는 저서에 관한 일이든 그는 손을 놓지 않았다. 하지만 예전에 이런 일과 사상이 온 인생을 뒤덮은 어둠에 비하면 자질구레하고 하찮게 여겨졌던 것과 꼭 마찬가지로, 이제 그것들은 행복의 찬란한 빛으로 가득 찬, 자기 앞에 펼쳐진 삶에 비하면 사소하고 하찮게 여겨졌다. 그는 일을 계속해 나갔지만, 이제 자기 관심의 무게중심이 다른 무언가로 옮겨 갔으며, 그래서 자기 일을 전혀 다른 눈으로 한층 또렷하게 바라보고 있다고 느꼈다. 예전에 이 일은 그에게 삶으로부터의 도피였다. 예전에 그는 이 일이 없으면 자기 삶이 너무 음울하리라고 느꼈다. 이제 이런 일은 삶이 한결같이 너무 밝기만 하지 않도록 그에게 필요한 것이었다. 원고를 집어 들어 자기가 써 놓은 것을 끝까지 읽어 보며, 그는 이 일이 매달릴 만한 가치가 있음을 흐뭇하게 깨달았다. 옛 생각들 가운데 많은 것이 그에게는 군더더기로, 극단적으로 보였지만, 머릿속에서 전체를 다시 훑어보니 많은 빈자리가 또렷하게 떠올랐다. 그는 지금 러시아 농업의 현재의 참담한 상태가 어디서 비롯되었는가를 다루는 새 장을 쓰고 있었다. 그는 러시아의 빈곤이 단지 토지 소유의 비정상적인 분배와 잘못된 방향의 개혁에서만 생기는 것이 아니라, 최근 몇 년 동안 이 결과에 한몫한 것은 러시아에 비정상적으로 접목된 외래 문명, 특히 철도 같은 교통수단의 편의이며, 이것이 도시로의 집중과 사치의 발달, 그에 따른 제조업과 신용 및 거기에 뒤따르는 투기의 발달을 불러왔고, 이 모든 것이 농업에 해를 끼쳤다고 주장했다. 그가 보기에 한 국가에서 부가 정상적으로 발전한다면 이 모든 현상은 농업에 상당한 노동이 투입되어 그것이 규칙적인, 적어도 일정한 조건 아래 놓이게 되었을 때에야 비로소 생겨나야 했다. 한 나라의 부는 비례적으로, 특히 부의 다른 원천들이 농업을 앞지르지 않는 방식으로 늘어나야 했다. 농업의 일정한 단계와 조화를 이루어 거기에 상응하는 교통수단이 있어야 했고, 토지가 안정되지 못한 우리네 형편에서는 경제적 필요가 아니라 정치적 필요에 의해 생겨난 철도가 시기상조였으며, 사람들이 기대했던 것처럼 농업을 촉진하기는커녕 오히려 농업과 경쟁하면서 제조업과 신용의 발달을 부추겨 농업의 진보를 가로막고 있었다. 그리고 동물의 한 기관이 한쪽으로 치우쳐 너무 일찍 발달하면 전체의 발달을 저해하듯이, 러시아의 전반적인 부의 발달에서도 신용과 교통의 편의와 제조 활동이—유럽에서는 제때 생겨나 의심할 바 없이 필요한 것이었지만—우리에게는 해결을 요하는 가장 중대한 문제, 곧 농업의 조직화 문제를 뒤로 밀어내 버림으로써 오로지 해만 끼쳤다는 것이었다.\n\n그가 자기 생각을 써 나가는 동안, 그녀는 모스크바를 떠나기 전날 큰 무례를 무릅쓰고 자기에게 추근거렸던 젊은 차르스키 공작에게 남편이 어쩌면 그렇게 부자연스럽게 살갑게 굴었는지를 생각하고 있었다. \"질투하는 거야.\" 그녀는 생각했다. \"어머나! 어쩜 저렇게 사랑스럽고 어리석을까! 나를 두고 질투하다니! 내가 그 사람들을 요리사 표트르만큼도 여기지 않는다는 걸 안다면.\" 그녀는 스스로도 낯선 소유의 감정으로 그의 머리와 붉은 목덜미를 바라보며 생각했다. \"일하는 걸 방해하기는 좀 안됐지만(그래도 시간이야 얼마든지 있잖아!), 저 사람 얼굴을 봐야겠어. 내가 보고 있는 걸 느낄까? 좀 돌아봐 주면 좋겠는데…… 내가 돌아보게 만들어야지!\" 그러고는 자기 시선의 힘을 더 강하게 하려는 듯 두 눈을 크게 떴다.\n\n\"그래, 그것들이 모든 수액을 빨아들이고 거짓된 번영의 외양만 만들어 내는 거야.\" 그는 글쓰기를 멈추고 중얼거렸다. 그러다 그녀가 자기를 바라보며 미소 짓고 있음을 느끼고는 뒤를 돌아보았다.\n\n\"왜?\" 그가 미소를 지으며 묻고는 자리에서 일어섰다.\n\n\"돌아봤어.\" 그녀는 생각했다.\n\n\"아무것도 아니에요. 당신이 돌아봤으면 했어요.\" 그녀는 그를 지켜보며, 방해받은 것을 그가 언짢아하는지 아닌지 가늠해 보려 애쓰면서 말했다.\n\n\"우리 둘만 있으니 얼마나 행복한지! 적어도 나는 그래.\" 그가 행복으로 환히 빛나는 미소를 띠고 그녀에게 다가가며 말했다.\n\n\"나도 꼭 그만큼 행복해요. 어디에도 절대 가지 않을 거예요, 특히 모스크바에는요.\"\n\n\"그래, 무슨 생각을 하고 있었어?\"\n\n\"나요? 생각하고 있었어요…… 아니에요, 아니에요, 어서 가요, 어서 글을 써요. 그만두지 말아요.\" 그녀가 입술을 오므리며 말했다. \"그리고 나는 이제 이 작은 구멍들을 도려내야 해요, 보이죠?\"\n\n그녀는 가위를 집어 들고 그것들을 도려내기 시작했다.\n\n\"안 돼, 말해 봐, 무슨 생각이었어?\" 그가 그녀 곁에 앉아 작은 가위가 빙글빙글 움직이는 것을 지켜보며 말했다.\n\n\"아! 내가 무슨 생각을 하고 있었더라? 모스크바를 생각하고 있었어요, 당신 뒤통수를요.\"\n\n\"왜 하필이면 내가, 이런 행복을 누려야 하는 걸까! 부자연스럽잖아, 너무 좋아서 말이야.\" 그가 그녀의 손에 입을 맞추며 말했다.\n\n\"나는 정반대로 느껴요. 좋은 일일수록 더 자연스럽게 여겨지는걸요.\"\n\n\"그리고 당신 머리카락 한 가닥이 살짝 풀렸어.\" 그가 조심스럽게 그녀의 머리를 돌리며 말했다.\n\n\"머리카락 한 가닥, 아 그래요. 안 돼요, 안 돼, 우리 일하는 중이잖아요!\"\n\n일은 더 진척되지 않았고, 쿠즈마가 차가 준비됐다고 알리러 들어오자 두 사람은 죄지은 사람들처럼 서로에게서 떨어졌다.\n\n\"시내에서 사람들이 왔나?\" 레빈이 쿠즈마에게 물었다.\n\n\"방금 도착했습니다. 짐을 풀고 있습니다.\"\n\n\"빨리 와요.\" 그녀가 서재를 나서며 그에게 말했다. \"안 그러면 당신 없이 나 혼자 편지를 읽어 버릴 거예요.\"\n\n혼자 남은 그는 원고를 그녀가 사 준 새 서류첩에 한데 모아 넣은 뒤, 우아한 부속품이 달린 새 세면대에서 손을 씻었다. 이 모든 것이 그녀와 함께 나타난 물건이었다. 레빈은 자기 생각에 미소 지었고, 그러다 그 생각이 못마땅하다는 듯 고개를 저었다. 후회에 가까운 감정이 그를 괴롭혔다. 지금의 생활 방식에는 무언가 부끄럽고, 사내답지 못하고, 그가 스스로 부르는 표현대로 카푸아적인 데가 있었다. \"이렇게 계속 지내는 건 옳지 않아.\" 그는 생각했다. \"곧 석 달이 되는데, 나는 거의 아무것도 한 게 없어. 오늘에야 거의 처음으로 진지하게 일에 매달렸는데, 그래서 어떻게 됐지? 시작만 했다가 내팽개친 것 말고는 한 게 없잖아. 평소에 하던 일조차 거의 손을 놓아 버렸어. 영지에서는 일을 살피러 걷거나 마차를 몰고 돌아보는 일이 거의 없지. 그녀를 두고 떠나기가 싫거나, 아니면 그녀가 혼자 있으면 따분해하는 게 눈에 보이거든. 그리고 나는 결혼 전에는 삶이 별것 아니고, 어쩐지 셈에 들지 않으며, 결혼 후에야 삶이 본격적으로 시작된다고 생각하곤 했지. 그런데 여기 거의 석 달이 지나도록, 나는 시간을 이토록 빈둥거리며 헛되이 보내 버렸어. 안 돼, 이래선 안 돼. 시작해야 해. 물론 그녀 탓은 아니야. 그녀는 어떤 식으로든 비난받을 게 없어. 내가 좀 더 단단해져서 사내다운 행동의 독립성을 지켜야 하는 거지. 안 그러면 나는 이런 버릇이 들어 버릴 테고, 그녀도 거기에 익숙해질 거야…… 물론 그녀 탓은 아니야.\" 그는 스스로에게 일렀다.\n\n* 카푸아적: 고대 이탈리아의 도시 카푸아는 사치와 향락에 빠져 군대를 무력하게 만든 곳으로 알려져, '안일과 방종에 젖은'이라는 뜻으로 쓰인다.\n하지만 불만에 찬 사람이 자기 불만의 근거를 두고 누군가 다른 사람을, 특히 자기와 가장 가까운 사람을 탓하지 않기란 어려운 법이다. 그래서 레빈의 머릿속에는 그녀 자신은 탓할 게 없지만(그녀는 무엇에 대해서도 탓할 수 없었다), 탓할 것은 너무 피상적이고 경박한 그녀의 교육이라는 생각이 어렴풋이 떠올랐다.(\"그 멍청한 차르스키. 그녀가 그를 말리고 싶어 했지만 어떻게 해야 할지 몰랐던 거야, 나는 알아.\") \"그래, 집안일에 대한 관심(그건 있지)을 빼면, 옷과 broderie anglaise를 빼면, 그녀에게는 진지한 관심사가 없어. 자기 일에도, 영지에도, 농민들에게도 관심이 없고, 음악에도—꽤 잘하는데도—관심이 없고, 독서에도 관심이 없지. 아무것도 하지 않으면서, 더없이 만족해하고 있어.\" 레빈은 마음속으로 이것을 못마땅하게 여겼지만, 그녀가 머지않아 자기에게 닥쳐올 그 활동의 시기를 위해 준비하고 있다는 것을 아직 이해하지 못했다. 그때가 되면 그녀는 남편의 아내이자 집안의 안주인이 되는 동시에, 아이를 낳고 젖을 먹이고 길러 낼 터였다. 그는 그녀가 이것을 본능적으로 알고서, 그 무서운 노고의 시기에 대비하고 있으며, 미래를 위해 즐겁게 보금자리를 짓고 있는 지금 자기가 누리는 사랑 속의 무심하고 행복한 순간들에 대해 스스로를 책망하지 않는다는 것을 알지 못했다.\n\n# Chapter 16\n\n레빈이 위층으로 올라갔을 때, 그의 아내는 새로 들인 은제 사모바르 옆, 새 다기 세트 뒤에 앉아, 늙은 아가피야 미하일로브나를 작은 탁자 앞에 앉히고 차를 가득 따른 잔을 권한 뒤, 끊임없이 자주 편지를 주고받는 돌리에게서 온 편지를 읽고 있었다.\n\n\"보세요, 마님이 저를 여기 앉혀 놓고 잠깐 함께 있어 달라고 하셨답니다.\" 아가피야 미하일로브나가 키티를 향해 다정하게 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n아가피야 미하일로브나의 이 말에서 레빈은 요즘 그녀와 키티 사이에서 벌어졌던 드라마의 마지막 막을 읽어 냈다. 새 안주인이 살림의 고삐를 자기 손에서 빼앗아 갔다는 데에 아가피야 미하일로브나의 마음이 상했음에도, 키티가 끝내 그녀를 정복하여 자기를 사랑하게 만들었다는 것을 그는 알아챘다.\n\n\"여기, 당신 편지도 제가 뜯어 봤어요.\" 키티가 글씨가 서툰 편지 한 통을 그에게 건네며 말했다. \"그 여자한테서 온 것 같아요, 당신 형의…….\" 그녀가 말했다. \"끝까지 읽지는 않았어요. 이건 친정 식구들하고 돌리한테서 온 거예요. 글쎄! 돌리가 타냐와 그리샤를 사르마츠키 집안의 아이들 무도회에 데려갔다지 뭐예요. 타냐는 프랑스 후작 부인 차림이었대요.\"\n\n그러나 레빈은 그녀의 말을 듣고 있지 않았다. 얼굴을 붉히며 그는 형의 옛 정부인 마리야 니콜라예브나에게서 온 편지를 받아 들고 읽기 시작했다. 마리야 니콜라예브나에게서 받은 두 번째 편지였다. 첫 번째 편지에서 마리야 니콜라예브나는 형이 자기 잘못도 없는데 자기를 내쫓았다고 썼고, 가슴 뭉클할 만큼 소박한 어조로, 자기는 다시 궁핍한 처지가 되었으나 아무것도 청하지 않고 아무것도 바라지 않으며, 다만 니콜라이 드미트리예비치가 허약한 건강 탓에 자기 없이는 화를 입을까 하는 생각에 괴로울 뿐이라며, 형을 돌봐 달라고 그의 동생에게 부탁한다고 덧붙였다. 그런데 이번에는 사뭇 다른 내용을 썼다. 그녀는 니콜라이 드미트리예비치를 찾아내어 모스크바에서 다시 그와 화해했고, 그가 관청에 일자리를 얻은 어느 지방 도시로 그와 함께 이사했다. 그러나 그가 상관과 다투고 다시 모스크바로 돌아오는 길이었는데, 도중에 너무 심하게 병이 들어 다시는 자리에서 일어나지 못할 것 같다고 그녀는 썼다. \"그분은 늘 도련님 이야기만 하셨어요. 게다가 이젠 돈도 한 푼 남지 않았답니다.\"\n\n\"이것 좀 읽어 봐요. 돌리가 당신 얘기를 썼어요.\" 키티가 미소를 지으며 말을 꺼냈다. 그러나 그녀는 남편의 얼굴에 떠오른 달라진 표정을 알아채고는 별안간 입을 다물었다.\n\n\"왜 그래요? 무슨 일이에요?\"\n\n\"형 니콜라이가 죽음의 문턱에 있다는 편지요. 형한테 가 봐야겠소.\"\n\n키티의 얼굴이 단번에 바뀌었다. 후작 부인 차림의 타냐도, 돌리도, 모두 사라져 버렸다.\n\n\"언제 떠나실 거예요?\" 그녀가 물었다.\n\n\"내일.\"\n\n\"저도 같이 가겠어요. 그래도 되죠?\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"키티! 무슨 생각을 하는 거요?\" 그가 나무라듯 말했다.\n\n\"무슨 뜻이에요?\" 그가 자기 제안을 마지못해, 못마땅한 기색으로 받아들이는 것 같아 그녀는 마음이 상했다. \"내가 가면 안 될 이유가 뭐예요? 방해되진 않을 거예요. 난…….\"\n\n\"내가 가는 건 형이 죽어 가고 있기 때문이오.\" 레빈이 말했다. \"당신이 왜…….\"\n\n\"왜냐고요? 당신과 똑같은 이유죠.\"\n\n'나에게 이토록 중대한 순간에, 저 사람은 혼자 있으면 따분하리라는 것만 생각하는군.' 레빈은 생각했다. 그리고 이토록 중대한 일에 솔직하지 못한 그 태도가 그를 격분하게 했다.\n\n\"말도 안 되는 소리요.\" 그가 매섭게 말했다.\n\n다툼으로 번지고 있음을 알아챈 아가피야 미하일로브나는 잔을 살그머니 내려놓고 물러갔다. 키티는 그녀가 나가는 것조차 알아채지 못했다. 남편이 마지막 말을 내뱉은 그 어조가, 특히 그가 자기 말을 분명 믿지 않는다는 사실이 그녀에게 상처를 주었다.\n\n\"말해 두는데, 당신이 가면 나도 같이 갈 거예요. 꼭 갈 거라고요.\" 그녀가 다급하고 노여운 어조로 말했다. \"왜 말도 안 된다는 거예요? 어째서 말도 안 된다고 하는 거죠?\"\n\n\"하느님만 아는 곳으로, 온갖 길을 거쳐 온갖 여관을 전전해야 할 텐데. 당신은 나한테 짐이 될 거요.\" 레빈이 애써 침착하게 말했다.\n\n\"전혀요. 난 아무것도 바라지 않아요. 당신이 갈 수 있는 곳이면 나도…….\"\n\n\"그럼 우선 한 가지만 말하지. 거기엔 당신이 만나서는 안 될 그 여자가 있소.\"\n\n\"거기에 누가 있는지, 무엇이 있는지 난 몰라요, 알고 싶지도 않아요. 내가 아는 건 내 남편의 형이 죽어 가고 있고 내 남편이 그분께 간다는 것, 그러니 나도 내 남편과 함께 간다는 것뿐이에요…….\"\n\n\"키티! 화내지 마. 하지만 조금만 생각해 봐. 이건 너무나 중대한 일이라, 당신이 거기에 약한 마음이라든지, 혼자 남겨지기 싫은 마음 따위를 끌어들인다고 생각하면 견딜 수가 없어. 자, 당신은 혼자 있으면 따분할 테니, 모스크바에 가서 잠깐 머물러요.\"\n\n\"거봐요, 당신은 늘 나한테 비열하고 천한 동기를 갖다 붙이는군요.\" 그녀가 상처받은 자존심과 분노의 눈물을 흘리며 말했다. \"난 그런 뜻이 아니었어요. 약한 마음 때문도 아니었고, 그게……. 난 남편이 곤경에 빠졌을 때 곁에 있는 게 아내의 도리라고 느끼는데, 당신은 일부러 날 아프게 하려 들고, 일부러 이해하지 않으려 하는군요…….\"\n\n\"아니, 이건 끔찍하군! 이렇게 노예 노릇을 하다니!\" 더는 분노를 억누르지 못한 레빈이 벌떡 일어나며 외쳤다. 그러나 바로 그 순간 그는 자기가 스스로를 매질하고 있음을 느꼈다.\n\n\"그럼 왜 결혼했어요? 자유로울 수도 있었잖아요. 후회할 거라면 대체 왜 한 거예요?\" 그녀가 자리에서 일어나 응접실로 달아나며 말했다.\n\n그가 그녀에게 다가갔을 때, 그녀는 흐느끼고 있었다.\n\n그는 말을 꺼내며, 설득하기 위해서가 아니라 그저 그녀를 달래기 위한 말을 찾으려 애썼다. 그러나 그녀는 그의 말에 귀 기울이지 않았고, 어떤 것에도 응하려 하지 않았다. 그는 그녀에게 몸을 숙여, 뿌리치려는 그녀의 손을 잡았다. 그는 그녀의 손에 입을 맞추고, 머리카락에 입을 맞추고, 다시 손에 입을 맞추었다. 그래도 그녀는 잠자코 있었다. 그러나 그가 두 손으로 그녀의 얼굴을 감싸 쥐고 \"키티!\"라고 부르자, 그녀는 별안간 마음을 가라앉히고는 울음을 터뜨렸고, 두 사람은 화해했다.\n\n이튿날 함께 떠나기로 결정되었다. 레빈은 그녀가 단지 도움이 되고 싶어서 가려는 것이라고 믿는다고 아내에게 말했고, 마리야 니콜라예브나가 형 곁에 있다고 해서 그녀가 가는 것이 부적절해지는 건 아니라는 데에도 동의했지만, 마음 깊은 곳에서는 그녀에게도 자기 자신에게도 불만을 품은 채 길을 나섰다. 정작 필요한 순간에 그를 혼자 보내겠다는 결심을 하지 못하는 그녀가 그는 불만스러웠고(그리고 그녀가 자기를 사랑할 수 있으리라는 그런 행복을 얼마 전까지만 해도 감히 믿지 못했던 그가, 이제는 그녀가 자기를 너무 사랑하기 때문에 불행하다고 생각하다니, 얼마나 이상한 일인가!), 좀 더 강한 의지를 보이지 못한 자기 자신에게도 불만스러웠다. 형 곁에 있는 그 여자를 그녀가 신경 쓸 필요가 없다는 점에 대해서는, 마음 깊은 곳의 거부감이 한층 더 컸으며, 그는 그들이 맞닥뜨릴지도 모를 온갖 상황을 떠올리며 몸서리쳤다. 자기 아내, 자기 키티가 천한 계집과 같은 방에 있게 된다는 생각만으로도 그는 공포와 혐오에 몸을 떨었다.\n\n# Chapter 17\n\n니콜라이 레빈이 병으로 누워 있던 그 지방 도시의 호텔은, 청결과 안락과 심지어 우아함까지 갖추겠다는 더없이 좋은 의도로 최신 개량 양식에 따라 지어진 지방 호텔 가운데 하나였으나, 그곳을 드나드는 손님들 탓에 놀라울 만큼 빠르게 더러운 선술집으로 변해 버렸고, 그러면서도 최신 개량의 허울을 내세운 탓에 차라리 정직하게 더러운 옛날식 호텔보다 더 나빠진 그런 곳이었다. 이 호텔은 이미 그 단계에 이르러 있었으니, 현관에서 담배를 피우며 문지기 노릇을 한다는 더러운 군복 차림의 병사며, 무쇠로 만든 미끄럽고 어둡고 불쾌한 계단이며, 더러운 프록코트를 걸친 데면데면한 보이며, 먼지 앉은 밀랍 꽃다발이 식탁을 장식하고 있는 공동 식당이며, 어디에나 깔린 오물과 먼지와 어수선함이며, 그러면서도 동시에 풍기는 어딘가 현대적이고 최신식인, 철도 같은 자기만족적 불안감이, 싱그럽고 젊은 그들의 삶을 겪은 뒤의 레빈에게 더없이 괴로운 감정을 불러일으켰는데, 특히 이 호텔이 자아내는 거짓스러운 인상이 그들을 기다리고 있는 것과 너무도 어울리지 않았기 때문이었다.\n\n언제나 그렇듯이, 어느 정도 가격의 방을 원하느냐는 물음을 받고 난 뒤에야, 그들에게 내줄 변변한 방이 하나도 없다는 사실이 드러났다. 변변한 방 하나는 철도 감독관이, 또 하나는 모스크바에서 온 변호사가, 세 번째 방은 시골에서 온 아스타피예바 공작 부인이 차지하고 있었다. 남은 것은 더러운 방 하나뿐이었고, 그 옆방이 저녁쯤이면 비게 될 거라고 약속했다. 자기가 예상했던 일이 그대로 벌어진 것에 아내에게 화가 났는데, 그것은 곧 도착하는 순간, 형의 안부를 알고 싶은 마음에 가슴이 격정과 불안으로 두근거리던 바로 그 순간에, 형에게 곧장 달려가는 대신 아내를 챙겨야만 한다는 것이었고, 레빈은 그녀를 정해진 방으로 데려다주었다.\n\n\"가요, 어서 가요!\" 그녀가 겁먹은, 죄지은 듯한 눈으로 그를 바라보며 말했다.\n\n그는 말없이 문을 나서다가, 곧장 마리야 니콜라예브나와 부딪힐 뻔했다. 그녀는 그가 도착했다는 말을 듣고도 감히 그를 만나러 들어가지 못하고 있던 참이었다. 그녀는 모스크바에서 보았을 때와 똑같았다. 똑같은 모직 원피스에, 드러난 팔과 목, 그리고 사람 좋게 멍청한, 곰보 자국이 있는 똑같은 얼굴이었으나, 다만 조금 더 통통해져 있었다.\n\n\"그래, 형은 어때요? 어떻습니까?\"\n\n\"아주 안 좋아요. 일어나지도 못해요. 줄곧 당신을 기다리셨어요. 그분이…… 사모님과…… 함께 오셨나요?\"\n\n레빈은 처음 한순간 무엇이 그녀를 당황하게 했는지 알아채지 못했으나, 그녀가 곧 그를 일깨워 주었다.\n\n\"전 비켜 있을게요. 부엌으로 내려가 있겠어요.\" 그녀가 말을 꺼냈다. \"니콜라이 드미트리예비치도 기뻐하실 거예요. 그분도 소식을 들으셨고, 사모님을 아세요, 외국에서 뵌 걸 기억하시거든요.\"\n\n레빈은 그녀가 자기 아내를 두고 하는 말임을 깨달았으나, 무어라 대답해야 할지 알 수 없었다.\n\n\"같이 가요, 형에게 같이 갑시다!\" 그가 말했다.\n\n그러나 그가 움직이기가 무섭게, 그의 방문이 열리고 키티가 빼꼼히 내다보았다. 레빈은 자기와 자기를 이토록 난처한 처지에 빠뜨린 아내에 대한 수치심과 분노로 얼굴이 붉어졌으나, 마리야 니콜라예브나는 더욱 새빨개졌다. 그녀는 그야말로 몸을 잔뜩 움츠리며 눈물이 핑 돌 만큼 얼굴을 붉혔고, 두 손으로 앞치마 자락을 움켜쥔 채 무슨 말을 하고 무얼 해야 할지 모르는 채로 붉은 손가락으로 그것을 비비 꼬았다.\n\n처음 한순간 레빈은, 자기로서는 도무지 이해할 수 없는 이 끔찍한 여자를 바라보는 키티의 눈에서 강렬한 호기심의 표정을 보았으나, 그것은 단 한순간뿐이었다.\n\n\"그래요! 형님은 어떠세요?\" 그녀가 남편을 향해, 그러고는 그녀를 향해 물었다.\n\n\"하지만 이렇게 복도에서 계속 이야기를 할 수는 없잖아!\" 레빈은 마침 그때 마치 제 볼일이라도 보러 가는 듯 복도를 경쾌하게 가로질러 가는 한 신사를 화난 눈으로 바라보며 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 이리 들어와요.\" 키티가 정신을 차린 마리야 니콜라예브나를 향해 말했으나, 남편의 낭패한 얼굴을 보고는, \"아니면 그냥 가세요. 가셨다가 저를 데리러 와요.\" 그녀가 말하고는 방으로 돌아갔다.\n\n레빈은 형의 방으로 갔다. 형의 방에서 자기가 보고 느낀 것을 그는 조금도 예상하지 못했었다. 그는 형이, 폐병 환자에게 흔하다고 들었고 또 지난가을 형이 찾아왔을 때 자기에게 그토록 강한 인상을 남겼던, 그 똑같은 자기기만의 상태에 있으리라 예상했었다. 그는 죽음이 다가오는 신체적 징후가 더 뚜렷해지긴 했어도—더 쇠약해지고 더 야위었어도—거의 같은 상태이리라 예상했었다. 그는 자기가 사랑하는 형을 잃는 데서 오는 그때와 똑같은 비통함과, 죽음 앞에서의 그때와 똑같은 공포를, 다만 더 큰 정도로 느끼리라 예상했었다. 그래서 그는 거기에 대비해 마음의 준비를 해 두었었다. 그러나 그가 발견한 것은 전혀 다른 것이었다.\n\n벽의 칠한 판자가 가래침으로 더러워지고, 얇은 칸막이 너머 옆방의 이야기 소리가 들려오는, 온갖 더러운 냄새로 숨 막히게 찌든 작고 지저분한 방 안, 벽에서 떼어 놓은 침대 위에, 누비이불을 덮은 채 한 육체가 누워 있었다. 그 육체의 한 팔은 이불 밖으로 나와 있었고, 갈퀴 자루만큼 거대한 손목이, 도무지 믿기지 않게도, 처음부터 가운데까지 매끈한 가늘고 긴 팔뼈에 붙어 있었다. 머리는 베개 위에 옆으로 뉘어 있었다. 레빈은 관자놀이와 팽팽하게 당겨진 투명해 보이는 이마에 땀으로 젖어 들러붙은 성긴 머리카락을 볼 수 있었다.\n\n'저 무시무시한 육체가 내 형 니콜라이일 리가 없어.' 레빈은 생각했다. 그러나 그는 더 가까이 다가가 얼굴을 보았고, 의심은 더 이상 불가능해졌다. 얼굴이 끔찍하게 변해 버렸음에도, 레빈은 자기가 다가가자 들어 올린 그 형형한 눈을 한 번 바라보기만 해도, 끈적이는 콧수염 아래 입가의 희미한 움직임을 한 번 포착하기만 해도, 이 시체 같은 육체가 살아 있는 자기 형이라는 끔찍한 진실을 깨달을 수 있었다.\n\n번득이는 눈은 그가 다가가는 동안 형제를 엄하고 책망하는 듯한 눈빛으로 바라보았다. 그리고 그 눈길이 곧바로 살아 있는 사람들 사이에 살아 있는 관계를 맺어 주었다. 레빈은 곧바로 자기에게 박힌 눈 속의 책망을 느꼈고, 자기 자신의 행복에 대한 회한을 느꼈다.\n\n콘스탄틴이 그의 손을 잡자, 니콜라이는 미소를 지었다. 미소는 희미했고 거의 알아챌 수 없을 정도였으며, 그 미소에도 불구하고 눈의 엄한 표정은 변함이 없었다.\n\n\"나를 이런 꼴로 보게 될 줄은 몰랐겠지.\" 그가 힘겹게 말을 내뱉었다.\n\n\"그래…… 아니.\" 레빈이 말을 더듬으며 말했다. \"어쩌자고 진작, 그러니까 내 결혼식 때라도 알려 주지 않았어? 사방으로 수소문을 했는데.\"\n\n그는 침묵하지 않으려고 말을 해야 했으나, 무슨 말을 해야 할지 알 수 없었으니, 특히 형이 아무 대꾸도 없이 그저 눈을 떼지 않고 빤히 쳐다보며, 분명 말 한마디 한마디의 속뜻까지 꿰뚫어 보고 있었기 때문이었다. 레빈은 형에게 아내가 함께 왔다고 말했다. 니콜라이는 기쁜 기색을 보였으나, 자기 몰골로 그녀를 놀라게 할까 봐 두렵다고 말했다. 침묵이 뒤따랐다. 갑자기 니콜라이가 몸을 움직이며 무언가 말을 꺼내기 시작했다. 레빈은 그 얼굴 표정으로 보아 무언가 각별히 무겁고 중요한 말을 기대했으나, 니콜라이는 자기 건강에 대해 이야기하기 시작했다. 그는 의사를 탓하며, 유명한 모스크바 의사를 두지 못한 것을 아쉬워했다. 레빈은 형이 아직도 희망을 품고 있음을 보았다.\n\n침묵의 첫 순간을 붙잡아, 레빈은 잠시만이라도 자기를 괴롭히는 감정에서 벗어나고 싶은 마음에 자리에서 일어나, 가서 아내를 데려오겠다고 말했다.\n\n\"좋아, 그럼 그 애한테 여기 좀 치우라고 일러야겠다. 여긴 더럽고 냄새가 날 테니까. 마리야! 방 좀 치워.\" 병자가 힘겹게 말했다. \"아, 그리고 다 치우거든 너는 나가 있어.\" 그가 형을 캐묻듯 바라보며 덧붙였다.\n\n레빈은 아무 대답도 하지 않았다. 복도로 나오다가 그는 우뚝 멈춰 섰다. 그는 아내를 데려오겠다고 말했으나, 이제 자기가 느끼는 감정을 헤아려 보고는, 오히려 그녀가 병자에게 들어가지 않도록 설득해 보리라 마음먹었다. '내가 괴로워하는 것처럼 저 사람까지 괴로워해야 할 이유가 어디 있어?' 그는 생각했다.\n\n\"그래, 형님은 어떠세요?\" 키티가 겁에 질린 얼굴로 물었다.\n\n\"아, 끔찍해, 끔찍하다고! 당신은 뭐하러 따라온 거야?\" 레빈이 말했다.\n\n키티는 몇 초간 잠자코, 겁먹은 듯 안쓰러운 눈으로 남편을 바라보았다. 그러더니 다가와 두 손으로 그의 팔꿈치를 잡았다.\n\n\"코스챠! 저를 형님에게 데려가 줘요. 함께라면 우리가 더 견디기 쉬울 거예요. 저를 데려가 줘요, 형님에게 데려다만 주고 나가 있으면 돼요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"당신은 보면서 그분은 못 보는 게 저로선 훨씬 더 괴롭다는 걸 이해해 줘야 해요. 거기 가면 저는 당신에게도 그분에게도 도움이 될 수 있을 거예요. 제발, 가게 해 줘요!\" 그녀는 마치 자기 인생의 행복이 거기에 달려 있기라도 한 듯 남편에게 간청했다.\n\n레빈은 동의하지 않을 수 없었고, 평정을 되찾고 이제는 마리야 니콜라예브나에 대해서는 까맣게 잊은 채, 키티와 함께 다시 형에게 들어갔다.\n\n키티는 가벼운 걸음으로, 남편에게 용감하고 다정한 얼굴을 보이며 끊임없이 그를 흘끔거리면서 병실로 들어갔고, 서두르지 않고 돌아서서 소리 없이 문을 닫았다. 들리지 않는 발걸음으로 그녀는 재빨리 병자의 침대맡으로 다가가, 그가 고개를 돌리지 않아도 되도록 가까이 가서, 곧바로 자기 싱그럽고 젊은 손으로 뼈만 남은 그의 거대한 손을 감싸 쥐고 가볍게 누르며, 여자 특유의 그 부드러운 열의로, 동정 어리되 거슬리지 않는 말투로 이야기를 시작했다.\n\n\"우리, 서로 모르는 사이였지만 소덴에서 만난 적이 있어요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"제가 제수가 될 줄은 미처 생각 못 하셨죠?\"\n\n\"나를 알아보지 못했을 텐데요?\" 그가 그녀가 들어선 것에 환하게 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"아니요, 알아봤을 거예요. 우리에게 알려 주시길 정말 잘하셨어요! 코스챠가 아주버님 이야기를 하지 않은 날이 하루도 없었고, 늘 걱정했답니다.\"\n\n그러나 병자의 관심은 오래가지 않았다.\n\n그녀가 말을 채 마치기도 전에, 그의 얼굴에는 산 자를 시샘하는 죽어 가는 사람 특유의 그 엄하고 책망하는 듯한 표정이 다시 떠올랐다.\n\n\"여기가 영 불편하실 것 같아요.\" 그녀가 그의 붙박인 시선을 피해 방 안을 둘러보며 말했다. \"다른 방을 알아봐야겠어요.\" 그녀가 남편에게 말했다. \"그래야 우리가 더 가까이 있을 수 있을 테니까요.\"\n\n# Chapter 18\n\n레빈은 형을 차분히 바라볼 수가 없었다. 형 앞에서 자신도 자연스럽고 침착하게 굴 수 없었다. 그가 병자에게 들어설 때면 자기도 모르게 눈도 주의도 흐려졌고, 형의 처지에 관한 세세한 정황은 보이지도, 분간되지도 않았다. 끔찍한 냄새를 맡았고, 더러움과 어수선함과 비참한 상태를 보았으며, 신음을 들었고, 어떻게 해도 도울 길이 없다고 느꼈다. 병자의 상태를 세세히 따져 본다든지, 저 몸이 이불 아래에 어떻게 누워 있는지, 저 비쩍 마른 다리며 넓적다리며 등뼈가 어떻게 웅크린 채 놓여 있는지, 그것들을 좀 더 편하게 해 줄 수는 없는지, 사정을 더 낫게는 못 하더라도 적어도 덜 나쁘게라도 해 줄 무언가가 있지는 않은지 헤아려 본다는 것은 그의 머릿속에 떠오르지조차 않았다. 이 모든 세세한 것들을 생각하기 시작하면 피가 차갑게 식었다. 그는 형의 생명을 늘리거나 그 고통을 덜어 줄 길은 전혀 없다고 절대적으로 확신했다. 그러나 그가 어떤 도움도 가망 없는 것으로 여긴다는 느낌은 병자에게도 전해졌고, 그것이 병자를 격분시켰다. 그래서 레빈은 더욱더 괴로웠다. 병실 안에 있는 것이 그에게는 고문이었고, 거기 있지 않는 것은 더한 고문이었다. 그는 혼자 남아 있을 수가 없어, 끊임없이 갖가지 핑계를 대며 방을 나갔다가 다시 들어오곤 했다.\n\n그러나 키티는 생각하고 느끼고 행동하는 방식이 사뭇 달랐다. 그녀는 병자를 보자 그가 가여웠다. 그리고 여자다운 그녀의 마음속에서 연민은 남편에게 불러일으킨 것과 같은 그런 공포와 혐오의 감정을 조금도 불러일으키지 않았다. 도리어 무언가를 하고 싶은 마음, 그의 상태를 세세한 데까지 다 알아내어 손을 쓰고 싶은 마음을 불러일으켰다. 그리고 그를 돕는 것이 자기 의무임을 조금도 의심하지 않았기에, 그것이 가능하다는 것 또한 의심하지 않았고, 곧바로 일에 착수했다. 그저 생각만으로도 남편을 공포에 빠뜨렸던 바로 그 세세한 일들이 곧장 그녀의 주의를 사로잡았다. 그녀는 의사를 부르러 사람을 보내고, 약국에도 사람을 보냈으며, 자기와 함께 온 하녀와 마리야 니콜라예브나에게 쓸고 닦고 문지르게 했다. 그녀 자신도 무언가를 씻고, 또 무언가를 헹구고, 이불 밑에 무언가를 깔아 주었다. 그녀의 지시에 따라 무언가가 병실로 들여와지고, 또 다른 무언가는 밖으로 내가졌다. 그녀 자신도 복도에서 마주치는 남자들에 아랑곳하지 않고 몇 번이나 자기 방을 드나들며 시트며 베갯잇이며 수건이며 셔츠를 꺼내 들고 왔다.\n\n식당에서 식사하는 기사들의 일행을 시중드느라 바쁜 종업원은 그녀의 부름에 못마땅한 낯빛으로 몇 번이나 왔으나, 그녀가 어찌나 정중하면서도 단호하게 분부를 내리는지 빠져나갈 길이 없어 그녀의 명령을 따르지 않을 수 없었다. 레빈은 이 모든 것이 못마땅했다. 그것이 환자에게 무슨 도움이 되리라고 믿지 않았다. 무엇보다도 환자가 거기에 화를 낼까 봐 두려웠다. 그러나 병자는 겉으로도 그러했고 실제로도 무심한 듯했지만, 화를 내지 않았다. 다만 멋쩍어할 뿐이었고, 대체로 그녀가 자기를 데리고 하는 일에 흥미를 느끼는 것 같기도 했다. 키티가 보냈던 의사에게 다녀온 레빈이 문을 열다가, 마침 키티의 지시로 병자의 속옷을 갈아입히고 있는 바로 그 순간과 맞닥뜨렸다. 거대하게 도드라진 어깻죽지뼈며 불거진 갈비뼈와 등뼈가 달린, 길고 하얀 등줄기가 드러나 있었고, 마리야 니콜라예브나와 종업원은 잠옷 소매와 씨름하며 길고 축 늘어진 팔을 거기에 끼워 넣지 못하고 있었다. 레빈이 들어온 뒤 황급히 문을 닫은 키티는 그쪽을 보고 있지 않았다. 그러나 병자가 신음을 내자 그녀는 잽싸게 그에게 다가갔다.\n\n\"서둘러요.\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"아, 당신은 오지 마.\" 병자가 화난 듯이 말했다. \"내가 직접 할 테니…….\"\n\n\"뭐라고 하셨어요?\" 마리야 니콜라예브나가 물었다. 그러나 키티는 그가 자기 앞에서 벌거벗은 채 있는 것을 부끄러워하고 거북해한다는 것을 듣고 또 보았다.\n\n\"안 봐요, 안 봐.\" 그녀가 팔을 끼워 넣으며 말했다. \"마리야 니콜라예브나, 당신은 이쪽으로 와서 해 줘요.\" 그녀가 덧붙였다.\n\n\"미안하지만 좀 다녀와 줘요, 내 작은 가방 안에 자그마한 병이 하나 있어요.\" 그녀가 남편을 향해 돌아서며 말했다. \"옆 주머니에 있는 거, 알죠. 그걸 가져다줘요, 그동안 여기는 이 사람들이 마저 정리할 테니까.\"\n\n병을 가지고 돌아온 레빈은 병자가 편안히 자리를 잡고, 그를 둘러싼 모든 것이 완전히 바뀌어 있는 것을 보았다. 그 무거운 냄새는 향긋한 식초 냄새로 바뀌어 있었는데, 키티가 입술을 오므리고 발그레한 두 뺨을 부풀린 채 작은 관으로 그것을 뿜어내고 있었다. 어디에도 먼지 한 점 보이지 않았고, 침대 곁에는 깔개가 놓여 있었다. 탁자 위에는 약병과 물병이 가지런히 정돈되어 있었고, 필요한 속옷들이 개켜져 있었으며, 키티의 broderie anglaise도 놓여 있었다. 환자의 침대 곁 다른 탁자에는 양초와 마실 것과 가루약이 놓여 있었다. 씻기고 머리를 빗긴 병자 자신은 깨끗한 시트 위에, 높이 괸 베개에 기대 누워 있었으니, 놀랍도록 야윈 목둘레에 하얀 깃이 달린 깨끗한 잠옷을 입은 채, 새로운 희망의 표정을 띠고 키티를 뚫어지게 바라보았다.\n\n* broderie anglaise: 프랑스어로 '영국식 자수'. 구멍을 뚫어 가장자리를 감친 흰 무명 자수.\n레빈이 클럽에서 찾아 데려온 의사는, 그동안 니콜라이 레빈을 진찰해 온 그 의사가 아니었다. 환자가 그 의사에게 불만이 있었기 때문이다. 새 의사는 청진기를 들고 환자의 가슴 소리를 들어 보더니 고개를 가로젓고 약을 처방했으며, 더없이 세세하게 먼저 약을 먹는 법을, 그다음에는 어떤 식이요법을 지켜야 하는지를 일러 주었다. 날달걀이나 살짝 익힌 달걀과 셀처 탄산수, 그리고 일정한 온도로 데운 따뜻한 우유를 권했다. 의사가 가고 나자 병자가 동생에게 무언가 말했는데, 레빈이 알아들은 것이라고는 마지막 말뿐이었다. \"네 카탸 말이야.\" 형이 그녀를 바라보는 표정으로 보아, 레빈은 형이 그녀를 칭찬하고 있음을 알았다. 사실 형은 카탸를, 자기가 부르는 그 이름으로 불렀다.\n\n\"벌써 훨씬 좋아졌어.\" 그가 말했다. \"그래, 네가 곁에 있었더라면 난 진작 다 나았을 텐데. 정말 좋구나!\" 그는 그녀의 손을 잡아 자기 입술로 끌어당겼으나, 그녀가 싫어할까 봐 두려운 듯 마음을 바꾸어 손을 놓고는 그저 쓰다듬기만 했다. 키티는 두 손으로 그의 손을 잡아 꼭 눌렀다.\n\n\"이제 나를 왼쪽으로 돌려 눕히고 자러들 가.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n키티 말고는 아무도 그가 하는 말을 알아듣지 못했다. 그녀만이 이해했다. 그가 무엇을 필요로 하는지 줄곧 마음속으로 살피고 있었기에 알아들은 것이다.\n\n\"반대편으로요.\" 그녀가 남편에게 말했다. \"형님은 늘 저쪽으로 주무세요. 돌려 눕혀 드려요, 하인들을 부르는 건 너무 번거로우니까. 나는 힘이 모자라요. 당신은 할 수 있죠?\" 그녀가 마리야 니콜라예브나에게 말했다.\n\n\"아무래도 못 할 것 같아요.\" 마리야 니콜라예브나가 대답했다.\n\n저 끔찍한 몸뚱이를 두 팔로 끌어안는 것이, 차라리 아무것도 모르고 싶었던 이불 밑의 그것을 붙드는 것이 레빈에게는 끔찍했지만, 아내의 영향 아래 그는 그녀가 익히 아는 그 단호한 얼굴을 짓고는, 두 팔을 침대 안으로 넣어 몸을 붙들었다. 그러나 자기 딴에는 힘깨나 쓰는데도, 그 맥없는 사지의 기이한 무거움에 그는 놀라고 말았다. 형을 돌려 눕히는 동안, 자기 목을 휘감은 거대하게 야윈 팔을 느끼고 있을 때, 키티는 잽싸고 소리 없이 베개를 뒤집어 두드려 부풀린 다음 병자의 머리를 그 위에 누이고, 축축한 이마에 다시 들러붙은 그의 머리카락을 쓸어 넘겼다.\n\n병자는 동생의 손을 자기 손으로 붙들고 있었다. 레빈은 형이 자기 손으로 무언가를 하려 하며 어딘가로 끌어당기고 있음을 느꼈다. 레빈은 가슴이 내려앉은 채 손을 내맡겼다. 그러자 형은 그것을 자기 입으로 끌어당겨 입을 맞추었다. 흐느낌에 몸을 떨며 한마디도 내뱉지 못한 채, 레빈은 방을 나갔다.\n\n# Chapter 19\n\n\"주께서 이것을 지혜롭고 슬기 있는 자들에게는 숨기시고 어린아이들에게는 나타내셨나이다.\" 그날 저녁 아내와 이야기를 나누며 레빈은 그녀를 두고 그렇게 생각했다.\n\n레빈이 이 구절을 떠올린 것은 자기 자신을 \"지혜롭고 슬기 있는 자\"라고 여겼기 때문이 아니었다. 그는 스스로를 그렇게 생각하지 않았지만, 자기가 아내나 아가피야 미하일로브나보다 지력이 뛰어나다는 것을 모를 수 없었고, 또 죽음에 대해 생각할 때면 자기 지력을 총동원해서 생각한다는 것도 모를 수 없었다. 그는 또한, 자기가 그 사상을 읽어 본 수많은 위대한 인물들의 두뇌가 죽음을 두고 깊이 골몰했음에도, 죽음에 관해 자기 아내와 아가피야 미하일로브나가 아는 것의 백분의 일도 알지 못한다는 것도 알고 있었다. 아가피야 미하일로브나와, 형 니콜라이가 부르던 이름이자 이제 레빈도 특히 즐겨 부르게 된 카탸, 이 두 여인은 서로 그렇게 다르면서도 이 점에서는 완전히 똑같았다. 둘 다 추호의 의심도 없이 삶이란 어떤 것이며 죽음이란 무엇인지를 알고 있었고, 비록 둘 중 누구도 레빈에게 떠오른 물음들에 답할 수 없었을뿐더러 그 물음을 이해조차 못 했겠지만, 둘 다 이 사건의 의미에 대해 아무 의심이 없었으며, 수백만 사람들과 함께 나누는 그 시선으로 죽음을 바라보는 방식이 정확히 똑같았다. 그들이 죽음의 본질을 확실히 알고 있다는 증거는, 그들이 한순간의 망설임도 없이 죽어 가는 사람을 어떻게 대해야 할지 알았고 또 그들을 무서워하지 않았다는 사실에 있었다. 레빈과 그와 같은 다른 사람들은, 죽음에 관해 많은 것을 말할 수는 있었지만, 죽음을 두려워하고 사람들이 죽어 갈 때 무엇을 해야 할지 도무지 알지 못한다는 점에서 분명 이를 알지 못했다. 만약 지금 레빈이 형 니콜라이와 단둘이 있었다면, 그는 공포에 질려 형을 바라보며, 더한 공포 속에서 무언가를 기다렸을 것이고, 그 밖에 무엇을 해야 할지 알지 못했을 것이다.\n\n그뿐만 아니라, 그는 무슨 말을 해야 할지, 어떤 표정을 지어야 할지, 어떻게 움직여야 할지조차 알지 못했다. 엉뚱한 일을 이야기하는 것은 그에게 충격적이고 불가능해 보였고, 죽음이나 우울한 주제를 이야기하는 것도—마찬가지로 불가능했다. 침묵하는 것 역시 불가능했다. '내가 형을 쳐다보면, 형은 내가 자기를 관찰한다고, 내가 무서워한다고 생각하겠지. 쳐다보지 않으면, 내가 딴생각을 한다고 여길 거야. 발끝으로 걸으면 형은 언짢아할 테고, 쿵쿵 걸으면 내가 부끄러워.' 키티는 분명 자기 자신을 생각하지 않았고, 자기를 생각할 겨를도 없었다. 그녀는 무언가를 알고 있었기에 형을 생각했고, 그래서 모든 일이 잘 풀렸다. 그녀는 형에게 자기 이야기며 자기 결혼식 이야기까지 들려주었고, 미소를 지으며 형을 안쓰러워하고 다정하게 어루만졌으며, 회복한 사례들을 이야기했고, 그렇게 모든 것이 잘 풀려 나갔다. 그러니 그녀는 알고 있는 게 틀림없었다. 그녀와 아가피야 미하일로브나의 행동이 본능적이고 동물적이며 비이성적인 것이 아니라는 증거는, 신체적 처치와 고통의 완화 말고도 아가피야 미하일로브나와 키티 둘 다 죽어 가는 사람에게 신체적 처치보다 더 중요한 무언가를, 신체적 조건과는 아무 상관없는 무언가를 필요로 했다는 데 있었다. 아가피야 미하일로브나는 방금 죽은 사람을 두고 이렇게 말한 적이 있었다. \"그래도, 다행이지요, 그분이 성찬을 받고 죄 사함도 받았으니. 하느님께서 우리 모두에게 그런 죽음을 내려 주시기를.\" 카탸도 똑같이, 시트며 욕창이며 마실 것에 온갖 신경을 쓰는 와중에도, 바로 첫날에 짬을 내어 병자에게 성찬을 받고 죄 사함을 받아야 한다고 설득했다.\n\n밤을 보내려고 병실에서 자기들 방 두 칸으로 돌아온 레빈은, 무엇을 해야 할지 모른 채 고개를 떨구고 앉아 있었다. 저녁 식사며 잠자리 준비며 앞으로 무엇을 할지 궁리하는 것은 말할 것도 없고, 아내에게 말을 거는 것조차 할 수 없었다. 부끄러웠던 것이다. 반대로 키티는 평소보다 더 활기찼다. 평소보다 더 생기마저 넘쳤다. 그녀는 저녁 식사를 가져오게 하고, 손수 짐을 풀었으며, 침대를 정돈하는 것도 거들었고, 페르시아 가루를 뿌리는 일조차 잊지 않았다. 그녀는 전투를 앞둔 사내들에게서, 다툼 속에서, 삶의 위태롭고 결정적인 순간들에서 드러나는 그 민첩함, 그 재빠른 판단력을 보여 주었다—사람이 단번에 자기 가치를 드러내고, 자기의 모든 과거가 헛되지 않았으며 바로 이 순간들을 위한 준비였음을 보여 주는 그런 순간들 말이다.\n\n그녀의 손에서는 모든 일이 척척 진행되어, 열두 시가 되기도 전에 모든 물건이 그녀의 방에 깨끗하고 단정하게 정돈되었고, 호텔 방이 마치 집처럼 보일 정도였다. 침대가 정돈되었고, 솔과 빗과 거울이 꺼내져 놓였으며, 식탁용 냅킨이 펼쳐졌다.\n\n레빈은 지금 같은 때에 먹고 자고 말하는 것조차 용서받을 수 없는 일이라고 느꼈고, 자기가 하는 동작 하나하나가 부적절하게 여겨졌다. 그녀는 솔을 정리했지만, 그 모든 것을 충격적인 데가 조금도 없게 해냈다.\n\n그러나 두 사람은 아무것도 먹지 못했고, 오랫동안 잠을 이루지도 못했으며, 잠자리에 들지조차 않았다.\n\n\"내일 형님께 종부성사를 받으시도록 설득해서 정말 기뻐요.\" 그녀가 접이식 거울 앞에 잠옷 윗도리 차림으로 앉아, 부드럽고 향기로운 머리카락을 가는 빗으로 빗으며 말했다. \"저는 한 번도 본 적은 없지만, 알아요, 엄마가 말씀해 주셨거든요, 회복을 비는 기도가 있다는 걸요.\"\n\n\"형이 회복할 수 있다고 생각해?\" 레빈이, 그녀가 앞머리를 빗어 넘길 때마다 끊임없이 가려지곤 하는, 동그란 작은 머리 뒤편의 가느다란 머리채를 바라보며 말했다.\n\n\"의사한테 물어봤어요. 사흘 넘게는 못 사실 거라고 하더군요. 하지만 그분들이 어떻게 확신하겠어요? 어쨌든, 형님을 설득해서 정말 기뻐요.\" 그녀가 머리카락 사이로 남편을 곁눈질하며 말했다. \"무슨 일이든 일어날 수 있는 거니까요.\" 그녀는 종교 이야기를 할 때면 늘 얼굴에 떠오르던 그 독특하고 다소 짓궂은 표정을 지으며 덧붙였다.\n\n약혼 시절에 종교에 관해 나눈 그 대화 이후로 두 사람 중 누구도 이 주제로 논쟁을 시작한 적이 없었지만, 그녀는 교회에 가고 기도를 드리는 등의 온갖 의례를, 마땅히 그래야 한다는 변함없는 확신을 가지고 빠짐없이 행했다. 그가 아니라고 주장하는데도, 그녀는 그가 자기만큼이나 그리스도교도이며 사실은 훨씬 더 나은 그리스도교도라고 굳게 믿고 있었다. 그리고 그가 이에 관해 하는 모든 말은 그저 사내다운 터무니없는 변덕 가운데 하나일 뿐이라고 여겼는데, 그것은 마치 그가 그녀의 broderie anglaise를 두고 선량한 사람들은 구멍을 기우는 법인데 그녀는 일부러 구멍을 낸다는 둥 하는 말과 같은 것이었다.\n* broderie anglaise: 프랑스어로 '영국식 자수'. 천에 구멍을 뚫어 그 둘레를 감치는 방식의 흰 자수.\n\n\"그래, 봐, 그 여자, 마리야 니콜라예브나는 이 모든 걸 어떻게 처리해야 할지 몰랐어.\" 레빈이 말했다. \"그리고…… 솔직히 말해서 당신이 와 줘서 정말, 정말 기뻐. 당신은 너무나 순수해서……\" 그는 그녀의 손을 잡았지만 입을 맞추지는 않았다(죽음이 이토록 가까운 데서 그녀의 손에 입을 맞추는 것은 부적절해 보였다). 그는 그저 뉘우치는 듯한 태도로 손을 꼭 쥐며, 환하게 빛나는 그녀의 눈을 바라보았다.\n\n\"혼자 계셨다면 당신은 비참했을 거예요.\" 그녀는 이렇게 말하고, 기쁨으로 발그레해진 두 뺨을 가리던 손을 들어 올려, 목덜미의 땋은 머리를 비틀어 올리고 거기에 핀을 꽂았다. \"아니에요,\" 그녀가 말을 이었다. \"그 여자는 어찌할 줄을 몰랐어요…… 다행히 저는 소덴에서 많이 배웠거든요.\"\n\n\"설마 거기에 그렇게 위중한 사람들이 있는 건 아니겠지?\"\n\n\"더한 사람들도 있어요.\"\n\"나한테 가장 끔찍한 건, 형이 젊었을 때의 모습으로 형을 볼 수가 없다는 거야. 젊었을 적에 형이 얼마나 매력적이었는지 당신은 믿지 못할 거야. 그런데 그때 나는 형을 이해하지 못했어.\"\n\n\"저는 정말, 정말 믿어요. 우리가 친구가 될 수도 있었을 텐데, 그런 느낌이 들어요!\" 그녀가 말했다. 그러고는 자기가 한 말에 마음이 아파, 남편을 돌아보았고, 두 눈에 눈물이 고였다.\n\n\"그래, 그럴 수도 있었을 텐데.\" 그가 슬프게 말했다. \"형은 사람들이 이 세상 사람이 아니라고 말하는, 바로 그런 부류의 사람들 중 하나야.\"\n\n\"하지만 우리 앞에는 많은 날이 남아 있어요. 이제 자야 해요.\" 키티가 자기의 자그마한 시계를 흘긋 보며 말했다.\n\n# Chapter 20\n\n다음 날 병자는 성찬식과 종부성사를 받았다. 의식이 진행되는 동안 니콜라이 레빈은 열렬히 기도했다. 색깔 있는 냅킨이 덮인 카드 탁자 위에 세워 둔 성상을 응시하는 그의 커다란 두 눈에는 어찌나 간절한 기도와 희망이 어려 있던지, 레빈으로서는 그것을 보고 있기가 두려울 지경이었다. 레빈은 이 간절한 기도와 희망이 그토록 사랑하던 삶과 헤어지는 일을 형에게 더 쓰라리게 느끼도록 만들 뿐이라는 것을 알고 있었다. 레빈은 형을 알았고 형의 지성이 작동하는 방식을 알았다. 그의 불신앙이 신앙 없이 사는 편이 그에게 더 편해서가 아니라, 자연 현상에 대한 동시대의 과학적 해석이 한 걸음 한 걸음 신앙의 가능성을 짓밟아 없애 버렸기 때문에 자라난 것임을 그는 알고 있었다. 그래서 그는 형이 지금 신앙으로 되돌아온 것이 동일한 지성의 작동을 거쳐 이루어진 정당한 회귀가 아니라, 회복을 바라는 절망적인 희망 속에서 일시적으로, 이해타산적으로 신앙으로 돌아온 것임을 알고 있었다. 레빈은 또한 키티가 자신이 들어 본 기적적인 회복 사례들을 이야기해 줌으로써 형의 희망을 더 부추겼다는 것도 알고 있었다. 레빈은 이 모든 것을 알았다. 그래서 애원하듯 희망에 찬 두 눈과, 힘겹게 들어 올려 긴장된 이마에 십자를 긋는 앙상한 손목과, 불거진 어깨와 헐떡이는 움푹 꺼진 가슴—병자가 빌고 있는 그 삶과는 도무지 어울려 보이지 않는—을 바라보는 것이 그에게는 견딜 수 없이 괴로웠다. 성찬식이 진행되는 동안 레빈은, 불신자인 그가 수천 번도 더 해 왔던 그 일을 했다. 그는 하느님을 향해 이렇게 말했다. \"당신이 존재하신다면, 이 사람을 회복시켜 주소서\"(물론 이와 똑같은 말은 이미 여러 번 되풀이된 것이었다) \"그러면 당신은 그도 저도 구원하시는 겁니다.\"\n\n종부성사를 받은 뒤 병자는 별안간 훨씬 좋아졌다. 한 시간 내내 한 번도 기침을 하지 않았고, 미소를 지었으며, 키티의 손에 입을 맞추고는 눈물을 흘리며 고맙다고 했고, 편안하고 아프지 않으며 기운이 나고 입맛도 돈다고 말했다. 수프가 들어왔을 때는 몸을 일으키기까지 했고 커틀릿도 달라고 했다. 회복할 가망이 전혀 없는 상태였고, 한눈에 봐도 그가 낫지 못하리라는 것이 분명했건만, 레빈과 키티는 그 한 시간 동안 둘 다 똑같은 흥분 상태에 빠져, 행복해하면서도 혹시 잘못 생각한 게 아닐까 두려워했다.\n\n\"형 좀 나아졌어요?\"\n\n\"그래요, 많이요.\"\n\n\"놀라운 일이에요.\"\n\"놀라울 것도 없죠.\"\n\n\"어쨌든 나아졌어요.\" 두 사람은 서로에게 미소를 지으며 속삭이듯 말했다.\n\n이 자기기만은 오래가지 않았다. 병자는 조용히 잠이 들었으나, 삼십 분 뒤 기침 때문에 깨어났다. 그러자 단번에 주위 사람들에게서도, 그 자신에게서도 모든 희망이 사라져 버렸다. 그가 겪는 고통의 현실은 레빈에게서도, 키티에게서도, 병자 자신에게서도 모든 희망을 짓밟아 버려, 의심의 여지도, 지난 희망의 기억조차도 남기지 않았다.\n\n그는 삼십 분 전에 자기가 믿었던 것을, 마치 떠올리기조차 부끄럽다는 듯 한마디도 언급하지 않은 채, 구멍이 뚫린 종이로 감싼 병에 든 요오드를 흡입하게 해 달라고 했다. 레빈이 병을 건네주자, 성찬을 받을 때 떠올렸던 그 간절한 희망의 표정이 이번에는 동생을 향해 못 박힌 채, 요오드를 흡입하면 기적이 일어난다던 의사의 말이 사실임을 확인해 달라고 그에게 요구했다.\n\n\"카탸는 여기 없지?\" 그는 헐떡이며 주위를 둘러보았고, 레빈은 마지못해 의사의 말에 동의했다. \"없어. 그러니 말할 수 있겠군…… 그 코미디를 한 건 그 애를 위해서였어. 그 앤 참 다정하지. 하지만 너와 나는 우리 자신을 속일 수가 없어. 내가 믿는 건 바로 이거야.\" 그는 이렇게 말하고는, 앙상한 손으로 병을 움켜쥐고 그 위로 숨을 들이쉬기 시작했다.\n\n저녁 여덟 시에 레빈과 그의 아내가 방에서 차를 마시고 있는데 마리야 니콜라예브나가 숨을 헐떡이며 그들에게 뛰어 들어왔다. 그녀는 창백했고 입술이 떨리고 있었다. \"돌아가시려고 해요!\" 그녀가 속삭였다. \"당장이라도 돌아가실까 봐 무서워요.\"\n\n두 사람은 그에게로 달려갔다. 그는 한쪽 팔꿈치를 침대에 짚고 몸을 일으킨 채, 길쭉한 등을 굽히고 고개를 낮게 떨어뜨리고 앉아 있었다.\n\n\"좀 어때요?\" 레빈이 잠시 침묵한 뒤 속삭이듯 물었다.\n\n\"이제 떠나는 것 같아.\" 니콜라이는 힘겹게, 그러나 더없이 또렷하게, 한 마디 한 마디를 자기 안에서 짜내듯 말했다. 그는 고개를 들지 않고 그저 눈만 위로 치떴는데, 그 눈길은 동생의 얼굴까지 닿지 못했다. \"카탸, 나가 있어라!\" 그가 덧붙였다.\n\n레빈은 벌떡 일어나 단호한 속삭임으로 그녀를 나가게 했다.\n\n\"이제 떠나.\" 그가 다시 말했다.\n\n\"왜 그렇게 생각해요?\" 레빈은 무슨 말이든 해야 했기에 물었다.\n\n\"내가 떠나니까.\" 그는 마치 그 말이 마음에 든다는 듯 되풀이했다. \"이게 끝이야.\"\n\n마리야 니콜라예브나가 그에게 다가갔다.\n\n\"누워 계시는 게 좋겠어요. 그러면 한결 편하실 거예요.\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"이제 곧 누울 텐데 뭐.\" 그는 천천히 말했다. \"내가 죽으면 말이야.\" 그가 빈정거리듯, 화난 듯 말했다. \"뭐, 좋다면 눕혀 주든지.\"\n\n레빈은 형을 반듯이 눕히고 그 곁에 앉아, 숨을 죽인 채 형의 얼굴을 바라보았다. 죽어 가는 사람은 눈을 감은 채 누워 있었으나, 깊고 격렬하게 생각에 잠긴 사람처럼 이따금 이마의 근육이 씰룩거렸다. 레빈은 자기도 모르게 형과 함께 지금 형에게 일어나고 있는 일이 무엇인지를 생각했지만, 형을 따라가려고 온갖 정신적 노력을 기울였음에도, 그 고요하고 준엄한 얼굴의 표정을 보고 알 수 있었다. 레빈에게는 여전히 그 어느 때처럼 캄캄한 것이, 죽어 가는 사람에게는 점점 더 또렷해지고 있다는 것을.\n\n\"그래, 그래, 그렇구나.\" 죽어 가는 사람이 사이를 두고 천천히 또렷이 말했다. \"조금만 기다려.\" 그는 잠잠해졌다. \"옳지!\" 그는 마치 모든 것이 풀리기라도 한 듯, 별안간 안심한 어조로 내뱉었다. \"오, 주여!\" 그는 중얼거리고는 깊이 한숨을 내쉬었다.\n\n마리야 니콜라예브나가 그의 발을 만져 보았다. \"차가워지고 있어요.\" 그녀가 속삭였다.\n\n오랫동안, 레빈에게는 아주 오랜 시간처럼 느껴졌으나, 병자는 꼼짝도 않고 누워 있었다. 하지만 그는 여전히 살아 있었고, 이따금 한숨을 내쉬었다. 이제 레빈은 정신적 긴장으로 기진맥진해 있었다. 그는 정신적 노력을 기울이지 않고서는 무엇이 옳은지 이해할 수 없을 것 같았다. 그는 죽음이라는 문제 자체를 생각할 수조차 없었고, 자기 의지와는 무관하게 다음에 해야 할 일에 대한 생각이 자꾸만 떠올랐다. 죽은 사람의 눈을 감겨 주는 일, 옷을 입히는 일, 관을 주문하는 일. 그리고 이상하게도, 그는 완전히 냉랭한 기분이었고, 슬픔도 상실감도 느끼지 못했으며, 형에 대한 연민은 더더욱 느끼지 못했다. 그 순간 형에 대해 어떤 감정이 있었다면, 그것은 죽어 가는 사람이 지금 가지고 있되 자기는 가질 수 없는 그 앎에 대한 부러움이었다.\n\n그는 그렇게 한참 더, 끊임없이 마지막 순간을 기다리며 형 곁에 앉아 있었다. 그러나 마지막은 오지 않았다. 문이 열리고 키티가 나타났다. 레빈은 그녀를 막으려고 일어났다. 하지만 일어나는 순간, 그는 죽어 가는 사람이 몸을 움직이는 소리를 들었다.\n\n\"가지 마라.\" 니콜라이가 말하며 손을 내밀었다. 레빈은 자기 손을 내주고는, 화난 듯 아내에게 나가라고 손짓했다.\n\n죽어 가는 사람의 손을 자기 손에 쥔 채, 그는 삼십 분, 한 시간, 또 한 시간을 앉아 있었다. 이제 그는 죽음에 대해서는 전혀 생각하지 않았다. 그는 키티가 무얼 하고 있을까, 옆방에는 누가 사나, 의사는 제 집을 가지고 있을까 따위를 궁금해했다. 그는 음식과 잠을 갈망했다. 그는 조심스럽게 손을 빼서 발을 만져 보았다. 발은 차가웠으나 병자는 여전히 숨을 쉬고 있었다. 레빈은 다시 발끝으로 살그머니 물러나려 했지만, 병자는 또 몸을 움직이며 말했다. \"가지 마라.\"\n\n날이 밝아 왔다. 병자의 상태는 변함이 없었다. 레빈은 슬그머니 손을 빼고, 죽어 가는 사람을 쳐다보지도 않은 채 자기 방으로 가서 잠이 들었다. 그가 깨어났을 때, 기대했던 형의 부고 대신, 병자가 이전 상태로 되돌아갔다는 소식을 들었다. 그는 다시 일어나 앉기 시작했고, 기침을 했고, 다시 음식을 먹기 시작했으며, 다시 말을 하기 시작했고, 다시 죽음에 대한 이야기를 그쳤으며, 다시 회복에 대한 희망을 입에 올리기 시작했고, 어느 때보다도 더 짜증을 부리고 더 침울해졌다. 누구도, 동생도 키티도 그를 달랠 수 없었다. 그는 모두에게 화를 냈고, 모두에게 험한 말을 했으며, 자기 고통의 책임을 모두에게 돌렸고, 모스크바에서 유명한 의사를 데려와야 한다고 우겨 댔다. 좀 어떠냐는 온갖 물음에 그는 앙심에 찬 비난의 표정으로 똑같은 대답을 했다. \"끔찍하게 아파, 견딜 수가 없어!\"\n\n병자는 점점 더 심하게, 특히 이제는 어떻게 손쓸 수도 없는 욕창 때문에 괴로워했고, 주위의 모든 사람에게 점점 더 화를 냈으며, 모든 일을, 특히 모스크바에서 의사를 데려오지 않은 것을 그들 탓으로 돌렸다. 키티는 가능한 모든 방법으로 그를 편하게 해 주고 달래려 했으나, 모두 헛수고였고, 레빈은 그녀가 인정하려 들지 않을 뿐, 육체적으로나 정신적으로나 기진맥진해 있다는 것을 알았다. 동생을 부르러 보냈던 그날 밤, 그가 삶과 작별함으로써 모두의 마음에 불러일으켜졌던 죽음의 감각은 흩어져 버렸다. 누구나 그가 머지않아 죽을 수밖에 없다는 것을, 이미 반쯤 죽어 있다는 것을 알았다. 누구나 그가 한시라도 빨리 죽기만을 바랐고, 그러면서도 모두 그 마음을 감춘 채 그에게 약을 주고, 치료법과 의사를 찾으려 애쓰며, 그를 속이고 자기 자신을 속이고 서로를 속였다. 이 모든 것은 거짓이었고, 역겹고 불경한 기만이었다. 그리고 그의 성격상, 또 그가 누구보다도 죽어 가는 사람을 사랑했기에, 레빈은 이 기만을 누구보다도 고통스럽게 의식했다.\n\n오래전부터 형제들을, 적어도 죽음을 앞두고서나마 화해시켜야겠다는 생각에 사로잡혀 있던 레빈은 형 세르게이 이바노비치에게 편지를 썼고, 그에게서 답장을 받자 그 편지를 병자에게 읽어 주었다. 세르게이 이바노비치는 자기가 직접 올 수는 없다고 쓰면서, 가슴을 울리는 말로 아우의 용서를 빌었다.\n\n병자는 아무 말도 하지 않았다.\n\n\"형에게 뭐라고 답장을 써야 할까요?\" 레빈이 말했다. \"형한테 화가 난 건 아니죠?\"\n\n\"아니, 조금도!\" 니콜라이가 그 물음에 짜증을 내며 대답했다. \"나한테 의사나 보내 달라고 해.\"\n\n사흘 더 고통의 날들이 이어졌다. 병자는 여전히 똑같은 상태였다. 이제는 그를 보기만 해도 모두가 그의 죽음을 바라는 마음을 느꼈다. 종업원들도, 호텔 주인도, 호텔에 묵는 모든 사람도, 의사도, 마리야 니콜라예브나도, 레빈도, 키티도 그러했다. 오직 병자만이 그런 감정을 드러내지 않았고, 오히려 그들이 의사를 데려오지 않는다고 격분하며, 계속 약을 먹고 삶에 대해 이야기했다. 다만 드물게, 아편이 끊임없는 고통으로부터 잠시나마 그를 놓아 주는 순간이면, 그는 반쯤 잠든 채, 다른 누구의 마음보다도 자기 마음속에서 더 강렬했던 것을 이따금 입 밖에 냈다. \"아, 차라리 끝이라면!\" 또는 \"언제나 끝이 날까?\"\n\n꾸준히 더 심해지는 그의 고통은 제 할 일을 다 하며 그를 죽음으로 준비시켜 갔다. 아프지 않은 자세가 없었고, 고통을 의식하지 않는 순간이 없었으며, 쑤시지 않고 그를 괴롭히지 않는 팔다리나 신체 부위가 한 군데도 없었다. 이제는 이 육체에 대한 기억이며 인상이며 생각마저도 육체 그 자체와 똑같은 혐오감을 그의 안에 불러일으켰다. 다른 사람들의 모습, 그들의 말, 자기 자신의 회상, 그 모든 것이 그에게는 고통의 원천이었다. 주위 사람들은 이를 느꼈고, 본능적으로 그의 앞에서는 자유로이 움직이거나 말하거나 자기 바람을 드러내지 않으려 했다. 그의 삶 전부가 고통이라는, 그리고 그것에서 벗어나고 싶다는 단 하나의 감정으로 녹아들어 있었다.\n\n그에게는 분명, 죽음을 자기 욕망의 목표로, 행복으로 바라보게 만드는 그 거부감이 차오르고 있었다. 지금까지는 고통이나 결핍—이를테면 배고픔, 피로, 갈증 같은 것—으로 일어난 개개의 욕망이 쾌감을 주는 어떤 신체 기능으로 충족되어 왔다. 그러나 이제는 어떤 육체적 욕구도 고통도 풀리지 않았고, 그것들을 풀어 보려는 노력은 도리어 새로운 고통만을 일으켰다. 그래서 모든 욕망은 하나로—자기의 모든 고통과 그 근원인 육체에서 벗어나고 싶다는 욕망으로 녹아들었다. 그러나 그에게는 이 해방의 욕망을 표현할 말이 없었고, 그래서 그것에 대해서는 말하지 않은 채, 습관대로 이제는 충족될 수 없는 욕망들의 충족을 청했다. \"나를 다른 쪽으로 돌려 줘.\" 그는 이렇게 말하고는, 곧바로 다시 전처럼 돌려 달라고 청했다. \"수프를 좀 다오. 수프를 치워라. 무슨 이야기든 해 봐. 왜들 잠자코 있는 거야?\" 그래서 사람들이 이야기를 시작하면, 그는 곧 눈을 감고 피로와 무관심과 혐오를 드러냈다.\n\n그들이 그 도시에 도착한 지 열흘째 되던 날, 키티가 몸이 좋지 않았다. 그녀는 두통과 메스꺼움에 시달렸고, 아침 내내 일어나지 못했다.\n\n의사는 그 부진이 피로와 흥분에서 비롯된 것이라는 소견을 내고 안정을 처방했다.\n\n그러나 점심 식사 후 키티는 일어나, 여느 때처럼 일감을 들고 병자에게 갔다. 그녀가 들어오자 그는 그녀를 준엄하게 바라보았고, 그녀가 몸이 좋지 않았다고 말하자 경멸하듯 미소를 지었다. 그날 그는 줄곧 코를 풀어 댔고, 가엾게 신음했다.\n\n\"좀 어때요?\" 그녀가 그에게 물었다.\n\n\"더 나빠.\" 그가 힘겹게 또렷이 말했다. \"아파!\"\n\n\"아파요, 어디가요?\"\n\n\"온통.\"\n\n\"오늘 끝날 거예요, 두고 보세요.\" 마리야 니콜라예브나가 말했다. 속삭이듯 말했지만, 레빈이 보기에 청력이 무척 예민해진 병자는 분명 들었을 것이었다. 레빈은 그녀에게 쉿 하고는, 병자를 돌아보았다. 니콜라이는 들었으나, 그 말은 그에게 아무런 영향도 주지 않았다. 그의 눈에는 여전히 그 강렬하고 비난에 찬 눈빛이 어려 있었다.\n\n\"왜 그렇게 생각해요?\" 그녀가 그를 따라 복도로 나왔을 때 레빈이 물었다.\n\n\"자기 몸을 쥐어뜯기 시작했어요.\" 마리야 니콜라예브나가 말했다.\n\n\"그게 무슨 말이에요?\"\n\n\"이렇게요.\" 그녀는 자기 모직 치마의 주름을 잡아당기며 말했다. 과연 레빈은 그날 하루 종일 환자가 무언가를 낚아채려는 듯 자기 몸을 잡아당기고 있었음을 알아차렸다.\n\n마리야 니콜라예브나의 예언은 들어맞았다. 밤이 가까워지자 병자는 손을 들어 올릴 수도 없게 되었고, 그저 한결같이 강렬하게 집중된 눈빛으로 앞만 응시할 수 있을 뿐이었다. 동생이나 키티가 그가 볼 수 있도록 그의 위로 몸을 굽혀도 그는 똑같은 눈빛이었다. 키티는 임종 기도를 읽어 달라고 신부를 부르러 보냈다.\n\n신부가 기도를 읽는 동안, 죽어 가는 사람은 아무런 생기의 기미도 보이지 않았다. 그의 눈은 감겨 있었다. 레빈과 키티와 마리야 니콜라예브나가 침대 곁에 서 있었다. 신부가 기도를 채 끝맺기도 전에 죽어 가는 사람이 기지개를 켜고 한숨을 쉬더니 눈을 떴다. 신부는 기도를 마치자 차가운 이마에 십자가를 갖다 댔다가 천천히 받침대에 도로 올려놓고는, 이 분쯤 더 잠자코 서 있다가, 차갑게 식어 가는 거대하고 핏기 없는 손을 만졌다.\n\n\"운명하셨습니다.\" 신부가 말하고 자리를 뜨려 했다. 그런데 별안간, 서로 들러붙은 듯하던 죽은 사람의 콧수염이 희미하게 움찔하더니, 그 정적 속에서 가슴 밑바닥으로부터 또렷이 새어 나오는 분명한 소리가 들렸다.\n\n\"아직…… 곧.\"\n\n그리고 일 분 뒤 얼굴이 환해지더니 콧수염 아래로 미소가 떠올랐고, 둘러서 있던 여자들이 조심스레 시신을 거두기 시작했다.\n\n형의 모습과 죽음의 가까움은, 그해 가을 저녁 형이 그를 찾아왔을 때 그를 덮쳤던 그 감정—풀리지 않는 수수께끼 앞에서의 공포, 그리고 죽음의 가까움과 불가피함에 대한 감정—을 레빈의 안에 다시 일깨웠다. 이 감정은 이제 전보다 한층 더 강했다. 죽음의 의미를 헤아릴 능력은 전보다도 더 없게 느껴졌고, 죽음의 불가피함은 그 어느 때보다도 더 끔찍하게 그의 앞에 솟아올랐다. 그러나 이제는 아내가 곁에 있는 덕분에, 그 감정이 그를 절망으로 몰아넣지는 않았다. 죽음에도 불구하고 그는 삶과 사랑의 필요를 느꼈다. 그는 사랑이 자신을 절망에서 구해 주고 있음을, 그리고 이 사랑이 절망의 위협 아래에서 한층 더 강해지고 순수해졌음을 느꼈다. 여전히 풀리지 않은 죽음이라는 하나의 신비가 그의 눈앞을 채 지나가기도 전에, 또 다른 신비가, 그만큼이나 풀 수 없는 신비가 솟아올라, 그에게 사랑과 삶을 재촉했다.\n\n의사는 키티에 관한 그의 추측을 확인해 주었다. 그녀의 부진은 아이를 가졌다는 징후였다.\n\n# Chapter 21\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 베시 그리고 스테판 아르카지치와 나눈 대화를 통해, 자기에게 기대되는 것이라곤 아내를 자기 존재로 짓누르지 말고 평온히 내버려 두는 일뿐이며, 아내 자신도 그것을 바라고 있음을 깨달은 순간부터, 그는 너무도 심란해져서 스스로는 아무런 결정도 내릴 수가 없었다. 그는 지금 자기가 무엇을 원하는지조차 알지 못했고, 자기 일에 그토록 기꺼이 관심을 보이는 사람들의 손에 자신을 맡긴 채 무엇이든 무조건 동의로 받아들였다. 안나가 집을 떠나고, 영국인 가정교사가 사람을 보내 그와 함께 식사할지 아니면 따로 식사할지를 물어 왔을 때에야 비로소, 그는 처음으로 자기 처지를 또렷이 깨닫고 그것에 경악했다. 이 처지에서 무엇보다도 가장 힘든 것은, 자신의 과거를 지금의 상황과 도무지 연결 짓고 화해시킬 수가 없다는 사실이었다. 그를 괴롭히는 것은 아내와 행복하게 살았던 그 과거가 아니었다. 그 과거에서 아내의 부정을 알게 되기까지의 과정은 이미 비참하게 겪어 냈다. 그 상태는 고통스러웠지만, 그래도 이해할 수는 있었다. 만약 그때 아내가 자기 부정을 그에게 털어놓은 뒤 그를 떠났더라면, 그는 상처받고 불행했겠지만, 지금 자기가 처한 듯한 이 희망 없는 처지—스스로도 이해할 수 없는 처지에는 빠지지 않았을 것이다. 그는 지금 자신의 가장 가까운 과거, 즉 병든 아내와 다른 남자의 아이에게 쏟았던 자신의 다정함과 사랑을, 지금의 상황과 도무지 화해시킬 수가 없었다. 다시 말해 이 모든 것의 대가라도 되는 양, 지금 자기가 외톨이가 되어 수치를 당하고, 웃음거리가 되었으며, 누구에게도 필요치 않고, 모든 사람에게 멸시받는 신세가 되어 버린 사실과 말이다.\n\n아내가 떠난 뒤 처음 이틀 동안 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 도움을 청하러 온 사람들과 수석 비서를 맞이했고, 마차를 타고 위원회로 갔으며, 평소처럼 식당으로 내려가 식사를 했다. 자기가 무엇을 하고 있는지 스스로에게 이유를 대지도 않은 채, 그는 그 이틀 동안 오로지 침착한, 아니 무심한 겉모습마저 지켜 내려고 자기 존재의 온 신경을 곤두세웠다. 안나 아르카지예브나의 방과 소지품을 어떻게 처리할지 묻는 물음에 답하면서, 그는 일어난 일이 자기 눈에 예상치 못한 것도, 흔한 일의 흐름에서 벗어난 것도 아닌 사람처럼 보이려고 엄청난 자제력을 발휘했고, 그 목적을 이루어 냈다. 누구도 그에게서 절망의 기색을 알아채지는 못했을 것이다. 그러나 그녀가 떠난 지 이틀째 되던 날, 코르네이가 안나가 미처 지불하지 않은 어느 고급 포목점의 청구서를 가져오면서, 가게의 점원이 기다리고 있다고 알리자, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 그에게 점원을 들여보내라고 했다.\n\n\"각하, 폐를 끼쳐 송구합니다. 하지만 마님께 청구하라 분부하신다면, 황송하오나 마님의 주소를 알려 주시겠습니까?\"\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 점원이 보기에 곰곰이 생각에 잠긴 듯하더니, 별안간 몸을 돌려 탁자 앞에 앉았다. 두 손에 머리를 파묻은 채 그는 한참 동안 그 자세로 앉아 있었고, 몇 번이나 말을 꺼내려다 입을 다물었다. 코르네이는 주인의 동요를 알아채고 점원에게 다음에 다시 오라고 청했다. 홀로 남게 되자 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 더 이상 단호함과 침착함의 자세를 유지할 힘이 자기에게 없음을 인정했다. 그는 자기를 기다리던 마차를 돌려보내고, 누구도 들이지 말라고 명한 뒤, 식사하러 내려가지 않았다.\n\n그는 그 이틀 동안 마주친 점원과 코르네이, 그리고 예외 없이 모든 사람의 얼굴에서 또렷이 보았던 그 보편적인 경멸과 분노의 무게를 견딜 수 없다고 느꼈다. 그는 사람들의 증오를 자기에게서 돌려놓을 수 없다고 느꼈는데, 그 증오가 그가 나쁜 사람이라서 비롯된 것이 아니라(그렇다면 더 나은 사람이 되려 애써 볼 수도 있었으리라) 그가 수치스럽고 역겹게 불행한 사람이라서 비롯된 것이기 때문이었다. 그는 바로 이 때문에, 바로 그의 마음이 슬픔으로 찢어졌다는 그 사실 때문에 사람들이 그에게 무자비하게 굴리라는 것을 알았다. 그는 개들이 고통으로 깽깽대는 상처 입은 개를 물어 죽이듯, 사람들이 자기를 짓밟아 버리리라고 느꼈다. 그는 사람들로부터 자신을 지킬 유일한 방법이 자기 상처를 그들에게 숨기는 것임을 알았고, 본능적으로 이틀 동안 그렇게 하려 애썼지만, 이제는 그 불공평한 싸움을 더 이상 견뎌 낼 수 없다고 느꼈다.\n\n자기가 이 슬픔 속에서 철저히 혼자라는 의식 때문에 그의 절망은 한층 더 깊어졌다. 페테르부르크를 다 뒤져도, 고위 관료로서도 아니고, 사교계의 일원으로서도 아니고, 그저 고통받는 한 인간으로서 자기가 느끼는 것을 털어놓을 수 있고 자기를 동정해 줄 사람은 단 한 명도 없었다. 아니, 이 세상 어디에도 그런 사람은 없었다.\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 고아로 자랐다. 형제는 둘이었다. 그들은 아버지를 기억하지 못했고, 어머니는 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 열 살 때 세상을 떠났다. 재산은 변변치 않았다. 한때 선왕의 총애를 받았던 고위 관료인 숙부 카레닌이 그들을 키웠다.\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 고등학교와 대학 과정을 우등으로 마친 뒤, 숙부의 도움으로 곧바로 관직의 요직에서 출발했고, 그때부터 줄곧 오로지 정치적 야망에만 몰두했다. 고등학교와 대학에서, 그리고 그 후 관직에 있으면서도 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 그 누구와도 가까운 우정을 맺은 적이 없었다. 형은 그의 마음에 가장 가까운 사람이었지만, 외무성에 자리를 얻어 늘 외국에 나가 있었고, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 결혼하고 얼마 지나지 않아 그곳에서 죽었다.\n\n그가 어느 지방의 지사로 있을 때, 부유한 지방 부인이던 안나의 숙모가 그를—지사치고는 젊었으나 이미 중년이었던 그를 자기 조카딸과 엮어 놓았고, 그가 청혼을 하든지 아니면 그 도시를 떠나든지 해야 할 처지에 몰아넣는 데 성공했다. 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 오래 망설이지 않았다. 그 일에는 찬성할 이유도 반대할 이유도 그만큼이나 많았고, 의심스러울 때는 삼간다는 그의 변함없는 원칙을 뒤엎을 만한 결정적인 사정도 없었다. 그러나 안나의 숙모는 공통의 지인을 통해, 그가 이미 그 처녀의 명예를 손상시켰으며 명예를 지키려면 마땅히 청혼해야 한다고 넌지시 일러 왔다. 그는 청혼을 했고, 자기가 품을 수 있는 모든 감정을 약혼녀와 아내에게 쏟아부었다.\n\n안나에게 느끼는 애착은 그의 마음속에서 다른 이들과 친밀한 관계를 맺을 필요를 모조리 막아 버렸다. 그리하여 지금 그의 지인들 가운데 친구라곤 한 명도 없었다. 이른바 인맥은 차고 넘쳤지만, 우정은 없었다. 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에게는 만찬에 초대할 수 있는 사람, 자기가 관여하는 어떤 공적인 일에 동정을 호소할 수 있는 사람, 자기가 돕고자 하는 누군가를 위해 관심을 기대할 수 있는 사람, 다른 사람들의 일이며 국가의 일을 허심탄회하게 의논할 수 있는 사람은 얼마든지 있었다. 하지만 이 사람들과의 관계는 명확히 정해진 하나의 통로에 국한되어 있었고, 거기서 벗어나기란 불가능한 일정한 틀을 지니고 있었다. 대학 시절 동창으로 훗날 가까워져, 개인적인 슬픔을 털어놓을 수 있었을 사람이 한 명 있긴 했지만, 그 친구는 러시아의 외딴 지방 교육부에 자리를 얻어 가 있었다. 페테르부르크 사람들 중에서 가장 가깝고 가능성이 있는 사람은 그의 수석 비서와 주치의였다.\n\n수석 비서인 미하일 바실리예비치 슬루딘은 솔직하고 영리하며 마음씨 곱고 양심적인 사람이었고, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치도 그의 개인적인 호의를 알고 있었다. 하지만 함께 일한 오 년의 관직 생활이 두 사람 사이에 더 따뜻한 관계를 가로막는 장벽을 세워 놓은 듯했다.\n\n가져온 서류에 서명을 마친 뒤, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 미하일 바실리예비치를 흘끔거리며 한참 동안 말없이 앉아 있었고, 몇 번이나 말을 꺼내려 했지만 그러지 못했다. 그는 이미 \"내가 겪는 고초를 들으셨소?\" 하는 말을 준비해 두기까지 했다. 하지만 결국 평소처럼 \"그럼 이걸 준비해 주시겠소?\" 하고 말하는 것으로 끝맺고는, 그를 물러가게 했다.\n\n또 한 사람은 주치의였는데, 그 역시 그에게 호의를 품고 있었다. 하지만 둘 다 일에 짓눌려 늘 바쁘다는 무언의 양해가 두 사람 사이에 오래전부터 자리 잡고 있었다.\n\n여자 지인들로 말하자면, 그 가운데 으뜸인 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인을 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 결코 떠올리지 않았다. 모든 여자는, 그저 여자라는 이유만으로, 그에게 끔찍하고 역겨운 존재였다.\n\n# Chapter 22\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인을 잊고 있었지만, 그녀는 그를 잊지 않고 있었다. 외로운 절망의 가장 쓰라린 순간에 그녀가 그를 찾아왔고, 안내를 기다리지도 않은 채 곧장 그의 서재로 걸어 들어왔다. 그녀는 그가 두 손에 머리를 파묻고 앉아 있는 모습을 보았다.\n\n\"J'ai forcé la consigne.\" 그녀는 빠른 걸음으로 들어오며, 흥분과 빠른 운동으로 거칠게 숨을 몰아쉬며 말했다. \"다 들었어요! 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치! 친애하는 벗이여!\" 그녀는 두 손으로 그의 손을 따뜻하게 꼭 쥐고, 그 아름답고 생각에 잠긴 눈으로 그의 눈을 응시하며 말을 이었다.\n* J'ai forcé la consigne: 프랑스어로 '면회 금지를 무릅쓰고 들어왔어요'라는 뜻.\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 미간을 찌푸린 채 일어나, 손을 빼며 그녀에게 의자를 권했다.\n\n\"앉으시지요, 백작 부인? 몸이 좋지 않아 아무도 만나지 않고 있습니다, 백작 부인.\" 그가 말했고, 그의 입술이 씰룩거렸다.\n\n\"친애하는 벗이여!\" 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인은 그에게서 눈을 떼지 않은 채 거듭 말했고, 별안간 그녀의 눈썹 안쪽 끝이 치켜 올라가 이마에 삼각형을 그렸으며, 못생긴 누런 얼굴이 한층 더 못생겨졌지만, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 그녀가 자기를 가엾게 여기며 막 울려 한다는 것을 느꼈다. 그리고 그 역시 마음이 누그러졌다. 그는 그녀의 통통한 손을 와락 붙잡아 입을 맞추기 시작했다.\n\n\"친애하는 벗이여!\" 그녀가 감정에 겨워 갈라지는 목소리로 말했다. \"슬픔에 자신을 내맡겨서는 안 돼요. 당신의 슬픔은 크지만, 위안을 찾으셔야 해요.\"\n\n\"나는 짓밟혔고, 무너졌고, 더 이상 사람이 아닙니다!\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 그녀의 손을 놓으면서도 여전히 그녀의 그렁그렁한 눈을 응시하며 말했다. \"내 처지가 너무도 끔찍한 것은, 어디에서도 나를 지탱해 줄 힘을 찾을 수 없기 때문입니다, 내 안에서도 찾을 수가 없어요.\"\n\n\"지탱해 줄 힘을 찾으시게 될 거예요. 그것을 구하세요—내게서가 아니라, 비록 나의 우정을 믿어 주시기를 간청하지만요.\" 그녀가 한숨을 쉬며 말했다. \"우리를 지탱해 주는 것은 사랑, 그분께서 우리에게 베풀어 주신 그 사랑이에요. 그분의 짐은 가벼워요.\" 그녀는 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 너무도 잘 아는 그 황홀한 표정으로 말했다. \"그분이 당신을 지탱해 주시고 도와주실 거예요.\"\n\n이 말들에는 자기 자신의 고매한 감정에 도취된 그 감상적인 감동의 기색과, 근래 페테르부르크에서 세력을 얻어 가던 그 새로운 신비주의적 열정—알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 보기에는 과도해 보이던—이 배어 있었지만, 그래도 지금 이 말을 듣는 것이 그에게는 기분 좋았다.\n\n\"나는 약합니다. 나는 짓밟혔어요. 아무것도 예견하지 못했고, 이제는 아무것도 이해하지 못합니다.\"\n\n\"친애하는 벗이여.\" 리디아 이바노브나가 거듭 말했다.\n\n\"지금 내가 갖지 못한 것을 잃어서가 아닙니다, 그게 아니에요!\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 말을 이었다. \"나는 그것 때문에 슬퍼하는 게 아닙니다. 하지만 내가 처한 처지 때문에 다른 사람들 앞에서 굴욕감을 느끼지 않을 수가 없어요. 잘못된 일이지만, 어쩔 수가 없습니다, 어쩔 수가 없어요.\"\n\n\"내가 황홀경에 빠질 만큼 감동받았고, 다른 모든 이들도 그랬던 그 고귀한 용서의 행위를 행하신 것은 당신이 아니라, 당신의 마음속에서 일하시는 그분이에요.\" 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인이 황홀하게 눈을 치켜뜨며 말했다. \"그러니 당신은 자신의 행위를 부끄러워할 수 없어요.\"\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 미간을 찌푸렸고, 손을 구부려 손가락을 우두둑거렸다.\n\n\"모든 사실을 알아야만 합니다.\" 그가 가느다란 목소리로 말했다. \"인간의 힘에는 한계가 있지요, 백작 부인, 그리고 나는 그 한계에 다다랐습니다. 나는 온종일 내 새로운 고독한 처지에서 비롯되는\"(그는 비롯되는이라는 말에 힘을 주었다) \"집안일에 관한 여러 가지 조치를 취해야 했어요. 하인들, 가정교사, 회계 장부…… 이런 자질구레한 것들이 내 가슴을 찔러 댔고, 나는 그것을 견딜 힘이 없습니다. 식사 자리에서…… 어제는, 식탁에서 거의 일어설 뻔했어요. 아들 녀석이 나를 바라보는 그 눈길을 견딜 수가 없었습니다. 녀석은 이게 다 무슨 일이냐고 묻지는 않았지만, 묻고 싶어 했고, 나는 그 눈빛을 견딜 수가 없었어요. 녀석은 나를 보기를 두려워했지만, 그게 다가 아닙니다…….\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 자기에게 가져다 놓인 청구서 이야기를 꺼내려 했지만, 목소리가 떨려 멈추고 말았다. 모자와 리본 값으로 청구된 그 푸른 종이의 청구서는, 자기 연민이 복받쳐 오르지 않고서는 떠올릴 수가 없었다.\n\n\"이해해요, 친애하는 벗이여.\" 리디아 이바노브나가 말했다. \"전부 이해해요. 도움과 위안은 내게서가 아니라 다른 데서 찾으시겠지만, 그래도 나는 할 수 있다면 당신을 돕고자 온 것뿐이에요. 내가 이 모든 사소하고 굴욕적인 걱정거리들을 당신에게서 떠맡을 수만 있다면…… 여자의 말 한마디, 여자의 보살핌이 필요하다는 걸 잘 알아요. 그 일을 내게 맡겨 주시겠어요?\"\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 말없이 고마운 마음으로 그녀의 손을 꼭 쥐었다.\n\n\"우리 함께 세료자를 돌봐요. 실무적인 일은 내 장기가 아니에요. 하지만 나서서 해 볼게요. 당신의 가정부가 되어 드리겠어요. 고마워하지 마세요. 내가 나 자신을 위해 하는 일이 아니니까요…….\"\n\n\"고마워하지 않을 수가 없습니다.\"\n\n\"하지만, 친애하는 벗이여, 당신이 말씀하신 그 감정—그리스도교도의 가장 큰 영광을 부끄러워하는 마음에 자신을 내맡기지 마세요. 무릇 자기를 낮추는 자는 높아지리라 하셨어요. 그리고 당신은 내게 고마워할 수 없어요. 그분께 감사드리고, 그분께 도움을 청하셔야 해요. 오직 그분 안에서만 우리는 평화와 위안과 구원과 사랑을 찾을 수 있어요.\" 그녀는 그렇게 말하고 하늘을 향해 눈을 들더니, 기도를 시작했다. 그녀의 침묵으로 미루어 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 그렇게 짐작했다.\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 이제 그녀의 말에 귀를 기울였고, 거북하지는 않더라도 적어도 과장스럽게 여겨졌던 그 표현들이 이제는 자연스럽고 위안이 되는 것으로 느껴졌다. 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 이 새로운 열광적인 열정을 싫어했었다. 그는 신앙인이긴 했지만 주로 종교의 정치적 측면에 관심이 있었고, 토론과 분석의 길을 터놓는다는 바로 그 이유로 몇몇 새로운 해석을 감행하는 그 새로운 교리가 원칙적으로 그에게는 못마땅했다. 그는 지금까지 이 새로운 교리에 대해 차갑고 심지어 적대적인 태도를 취해 왔으며, 그것에 심취해 버린 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인과 한 번도 논쟁을 벌인 적이 없었지만, 그를 논쟁으로 끌어들이려는 그녀의 시도를 침묵으로 줄곧 받아넘겨 왔다. 그런데 지금 처음으로 그는 그녀의 말을 기쁘게 들었고, 마음속으로 그것에 반대하지 않았다.\n\n\"당신께 정말, 정말 고맙습니다, 당신이 해 주신 일과 말씀 둘 다에 대해서요.\" 그녀가 기도를 마쳤을 때 그가 말했다.\n\n리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인은 다시 한번 자기 벗의 두 손을 꼭 쥐었다.\n\n\"이제 내 임무를 시작하겠어요.\" 잠시 사이를 두었다가 눈물 자국을 닦으며 그녀가 미소 띤 얼굴로 말했다. \"세료자에게 가 볼게요. 정말 어쩔 수 없는 마지막 순간에만 당신에게 부탁할게요.\" 그러고는 일어나 나갔다.\n\n리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인은 세료자가 지내는 집 한쪽으로 가서, 겁에 질린 아이의 뺨에 눈물을 떨어뜨리며, 아버지는 성자이고 어머니는 죽었다고 아이에게 말했다.\n\n리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인은 약속을 지켰다. 그녀는 실제로 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치 집안의 조직과 관리를 맡았다. 하지만 실무적인 일이 자기 장기가 아니라고 한 말은 결코 과장이 아니었다. 그녀의 모든 조치는 실행될 수 없어서 수정되어야 했고, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 시종인 코르네이가 그것을 수정했는데, 그는 아무도 모르는 사이에 이제 카레닌의 집안을 관리하고 있었으며, 주인이 옷을 입는 동안 그가 알아야 할 모든 것을 조용하고 신중하게 보고했다. 그렇다고 해서 리디아 이바노브나의 도움이 실질적이지 않은 것은 아니었다. 그녀는 그에 대한 자신의 사랑과 존경을 의식하게 함으로써 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에게 도덕적 지지를 주었고, 더욱이 그녀가 그를 거의 그리스도교로 돌려세웠다고 스스로 믿는 것이 그녀에게 위안이 되었으니—다시 말해 무관심하고 무기력한 신앙인이던 그를, 근래 페테르부르크에서 세력을 넓혀 가던 그리스도교 교리의 새로운 해석을 열렬하고 한결같이 따르는 신봉자로 바꿔 놓은 것이다. 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 이 가르침을 믿기란 쉬운 일이었다. 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는, 사실 리디아 이바노브나나 그들과 견해를 같이하는 다른 이들과 마찬가지로, 상상력의 생생함이 완전히 결여되어 있었다. 그 정신적 능력 덕분에 상상이 불러일으키는 관념들이 너무도 생생해져서 다른 관념들이나 실제 사실과 조화를 이루지 않을 수 없게 되는 것인데 말이다. 그는 죽음이 불신자들에게는 존재하지만 자기에게는 존재하지 않는다는 생각, 그리고 자기는 가장 완전한 신앙—그 정도는 자기 스스로가 판단하는—을 지녔으므로 자기 영혼에는 죄가 없으며 여기 이 땅에서 완전한 구원을 경험하고 있다는 생각에서 아무런 불가능하거나 상상조차 할 수 없는 점을 보지 못했다.\n\n자기 신앙에 대한 이 관념이 그릇되고 천박하다는 것을 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 어렴풋이 느끼고 있었던 것은 사실이며, 자기의 용서가 더 높은 힘의 작용이라는 생각이라곤 조금도 없이 곧장 용서의 감정에 자신을 내맡겼을 때가, 매 순간 그리스도가 자기 마음속에 계시며 공문서에 서명하는 것이 그분의 뜻을 행하는 것이라고 생각하는 지금보다 더 큰 행복을 느꼈다는 것도 그는 알고 있었다. 하지만 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에게는 그렇게 생각하는 것이 하나의 필요였다. 자기의 굴욕 속에서, 비록 상상에 불과할지라도, 모든 이에게 멸시받는 처지에서 다른 이들을 내려다볼 수 있는 어떤 드높은 입장을 갖는 것이 그에게는 너무도 절실한 필요였기에, 그는 자신의 유일한 구원에 매달리듯 그 구원의 망상에 매달렸던 것이다.\n\n# Chapter 23\n\n리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인은 아주 어리고 감상적인 처녀 시절에 부유하고 지위 높은 남자, 더없이 선량하고 쾌활하지만 지독히 방탕한 난봉꾼과 결혼했다. 결혼 두 달 후 남편은 그녀를 버렸고, 그녀가 격정적으로 애정을 호소해도 그저 빈정거림으로, 심지어 적의로까지 응답했는데, 백작의 선량한 마음씨를 알고 감상적인 리디아에게서 아무런 흠도 찾지 못하는 사람들로서는 그 까닭을 도무지 설명할 수가 없었다. 두 사람은 이혼하여 따로 살았지만, 그래도 남편은 아내와 마주칠 때마다 어김없이 똑같은 악의에 찬 비아냥거림으로 그녀를 대했으니, 그 까닭은 도무지 알 수 없는 것이었다.\n\n리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인은 남편을 사랑하기를 진작에 그만두었지만, 그 후로 누군가를 사랑하기를 그만둔 적은 한 번도 없었다. 그녀는 동시에 여러 사람을 사랑했는데, 남자도 여자도 있었다. 그녀는 어떤 식으로든 유난히 두드러진 사람이라면 거의 누구든 사랑했다. 그녀는 황실로 시집오거나 장가오는 새로운 공작과 공작 부인을 모두 사랑했고, 교회의 고위 성직자며 보좌 신부며 본당 사제를 사랑한 적도 있었으며, 어느 언론인과 슬라브주의자 셋과 코미사로프와 어느 장관과 의사와 영국인 선교사와 카레닌을 사랑한 적도 있었다. 이 모든 열정은 끊임없이 식었다가 더 뜨거워지곤 했지만, 그렇다고 해서 그녀가 궁정 및 사교계와 더없이 폭넓고 복잡한 관계를 유지하는 데에 방해가 되지는 않았다. 그러나 카레닌이 곤경에 처한 후 그를 자신의 특별한 보호 아래 두게 된 때부터, 카레닌의 집안일에 발 벗고 나서 그의 안녕을 돌보기 시작한 때부터, 그녀는 자신의 다른 모든 애착이 진짜가 아니었으며 이제야말로 자신이 진정으로 사랑하고 있다는 것을, 그것도 카레닌 말고는 그 누구도 아니라는 것을 느꼈다. 지금 그에게 품은 감정은 이전의 그 어떤 감정보다도 강하게 느껴졌다. 자신의 감정을 분석하여 예전의 열정들과 비교해 보면서, 그녀는 만일 코미사로프가 황제의 목숨을 구하지 않았더라면 그를 사랑하지 않았을 것이며, 슬라브 문제가 없었더라면 리스티치쿠지츠키를 사랑하지 않았으리라는 것을, 그러나 카레닌만큼은 그 사람 자체를, 그 드높고 아무도 헤아리지 못하는 영혼을, 그녀에게는 감미롭게 들리는 그 목소리의 높은 음을, 느릿하게 끄는 억양을, 지친 두 눈을, 그 성품을, 그리고 핏줄이 불거진 부드럽고 흰 두 손을 사랑한다는 것을 또렷이 깨달았다. 그녀는 단지 그를 만나는 것이 더없이 기쁠 뿐만 아니라, 그의 얼굴에서 자신이 그에게 어떤 인상을 주고 있는지 그 흔적을 찾으려 했다. 그녀는 말로만이 아니라 자신의 온 존재로 그의 마음에 들려고 애썼다. 그를 위해 이제 그녀는 전보다 옷차림에 더 많은 공을 들였다. 그녀는 만일 자신이 결혼하지 않았고 그가 자유로운 몸이었다면 어땠을까 하는 공상에 빠져 있는 자기 자신을 문득문득 발견하곤 했다. 그가 방으로 들어오면 그녀는 감격에 겨워 얼굴을 붉혔고, 그가 그녀에게 무언가 다정한 말이라도 건네면 황홀한 미소를 억누를 수가 없었다.\n\n벌써 며칠째 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인은 극도로 흥분된 상태에 있었다. 그녀는 안나와 브론스키가 페테르부르크에 와 있다는 것을 알게 되었다. 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 그녀를 보는 일은 어떻게든 막아야 했다. 그 끔찍한 여자가 자기와 같은 도시에 있고 언제라도 마주칠 수 있다는 그 괴로운 사실을 아는 것조차 막아야 했다.\n\n리디아 이바노브나는 자기가 안나와 브론스키를 일컫는 표현 그대로 그 파렴치한 인간들이 무엇을 하려는지 친구들을 통해 알아보았고, 그날들 동안 자기 친구의 모든 움직임을 그들과 마주치지 않도록 인도하려 애썼다. 그녀가 정보를 얻는 통로였던, 브론스키와 아는 사이이며 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인을 통해 어떤 이권을 얻기를 바라던 젊은 부관이, 그들은 볼일을 다 마쳤으며 이튿날 떠날 거라고 그녀에게 일러 주었다. 리디아 이바노브나는 이미 마음을 가라앉히기 시작하던 참이었는데, 다음 날 아침 한 통의 쪽지가 그녀에게 전해졌고, 그 필체를 알아본 그녀는 소스라쳤다. 그것은 안나 카레니나의 필체였다. 봉투는 나무껍질처럼 두꺼운 종이로 되어 있었고, 길쭉한 노란 종이에는 커다란 모노그램이 찍혀 있었으며, 편지에서는 기분 좋은 향내가 풍겼다.\n\n\"누가 가져왔지?\"\n\n\"호텔의 심부름꾼이 가져왔습니다.\"\n\n리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인이 자리에 앉아 편지를 읽기까지는 한참이 걸렸다. 흥분한 탓에 그녀가 평소 앓던 천식 발작이 도졌던 것이다. 마음을 가라앉힌 후 그녀는 프랑스어로 쓰인 다음과 같은 편지를 읽었다.\n\n\"Madame la Comtesse,\n\n당신의 마음을 가득 채우고 있는 그리스도교적 감정이, 제게는 용서받을 수 없으리라 여겨지는 이 대담함을 무릅쓰고 당신께 글을 쓰게 합니다. 저는 아들과 떨어져 있는 것이 너무도 괴롭습니다. 떠나기 전에 단 한 번만이라도 그 아이를 보게 해 주십사 간청드립니다. 다시 제 존재를 당신의 기억에 떠올리게 하는 것을 용서하십시오. 제가 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 아니라 당신께 청하는 것은, 다만 그 너그러운 분이 저를 떠올리며 괴로워하시는 것을 원치 않기 때문입니다. 그분을 향한 당신의 우정을 알기에, 당신께서 저를 이해해 주시리라 믿습니다. 세료자를 제게 보내 주실 수 있는지요, 아니면 정해진 시각에 제가 댁으로 찾아가야 할지요, 그것도 아니면 집 밖에서 그 아이를 언제 어디서 볼 수 있을지 제게 알려 주시겠는지요? 이 일을 결정하실 분의 너그러움을 알기에, 거절은 예상하지 않습니다. 그 아이를 보고 싶어 하는 제 갈망이 얼마나 큰지 당신은 짐작도 못 하실 것이고, 그러니 당신의 도움이 제 안에 불러일으킬 감사가 어떠할지도 짐작하지 못하실 것입니다.\n\n안나.\"\n\n이 편지의 모든 것이 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인의 화를 돋우었다. 그 내용도, 너그러움 운운하는 대목도, 무엇보다 그녀가 보기에 거리낌 없고 태평스러운 그 어조가 그러했다.\n\n\"답장은 없다고 전해라.\" 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인은 이렇게 말하고는 곧바로 압지첩을 펼쳐, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에게 한 시에 궁정 알현식에서 그를 보았으면 한다고 써 보냈다.\n\n\"중대하고 괴로운 일에 관해 당신과 이야기를 나눠야겠습니다. 어디서 만날지는 거기서 정하기로 하지요. 무엇보다도 우리 집이 좋겠습니다. 당신이 좋아하시는 대로 차를 내오라 이르겠어요. 급한 일입니다. 그분께서 십자가를 지우시지만, 또한 그것을 견딜 힘도 주신답니다.\" 그녀는 그에게 마음의 준비를 조금이나마 시켜 두려고 이렇게 덧붙였다. 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인은 보통 하루에 두세 통의 편지를 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에게 써 보내곤 했다. 그녀는 이런 형태의 소통을 즐겼는데, 직접 만나서 이야기할 때는 누릴 수 없는 세련됨과 신비로운 분위기를 거기서 얻을 수 있었기 때문이다.\n\n# Chapter 24\n\n접견회가 막바지에 이르고 있었다. 사람들은 돌아가는 길에 마주치면 최근의 소식들, 새로 내려진 훈장들, 고위 관료들의 인사 변동에 대해 수다를 떨었다.\n\n\"마리야 보리소브나 백작 부인이 육군 장관이 되고, 바트콥스카야 공작 영애가 총사령관이 되기만 한다면 좋을 텐데요.\" 금실로 수놓은 제복을 입은 머리가 희끗희끗한 작은 노인이, 새 인사 발령에 대해 묻던 키 크고 아름다운 시녀를 향해 말했다.\n\n\"그리고 저는 부관들 가운데 끼고요.\" 시녀가 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"당신은 이미 한 자리 차지하고 있지 않습니까. 종무국을 맡고 계시지요. 그리고 보좌관이 카레닌이고요.\"\n\n\"안녕하십니까, 공작!\" 작은 노인이 자기 쪽으로 다가오는 한 남자에게 말했다.\n\n\"카레닌에 대해 무슨 말씀을 하고 계셨습니까?\" 공작이 물었다.\n\n\"그와 푸탸토프가 알렉산드르 넵스키 훈장을 받았답니다.\"\n\n\"이미 받은 줄 알았는데요.\"\n\n\"아닙니다. 저기 좀 보십시오.\" 작은 노인이 수놓은 모자로 카레닌을 가리키며 말했다. 카레닌은 새로 받은 붉은 리본을 어깨에 비스듬히 두른 궁정 제복 차림으로, 황실 자문회의의 영향력 있는 인사 한 명과 함께 홀 문간에 서 있었다. \"동전 한 닢처럼 흐뭇하고 행복해 보이는군요.\" 그는 이렇게 덧붙이며, 거구의 잘생긴 시종과 악수하기 위해 걸음을 멈췄다.\n\n\"아니요, 늙어 보이는데요.\" 시종이 말했다.\n\n\"일을 너무 많이 해서지요. 요즘 그는 늘 무슨 계획안을 작성하고 있답니다. 요즘은 가엾은 상대를 붙잡으면 모든 걸 항목별로 설명해 줄 때까지 놓아주질 않아요.\"\n\n\"늙어 보인다고 하셨습니까? Il fait des passions. 제 생각엔 요즘 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인이 그의 아내를 질투하는 모양입니다.\"\n\n\"오, 그러지 마십시오, 제발 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인에 대해 나쁜 말은 하지 마세요.\"\n\n\"아니, 그녀가 카레닌을 사랑한다고 해서 나쁠 게 뭐 있습니까?\"\n\n\"그런데 카레닌 부인이 여기 와 있다는 게 사실입니까?\"\n\n\"음, 여기 궁정에 있는 건 아니고, 페테르부르크에 있지요. 어제 모르스카야 거리에서 알렉세이 브론스키와 함께 있는 걸 봤어요, bras dessous, bras dessous로요.\"\n\n\"C'est un homme qui n'a pas……\" 시종이 말을 꺼내다가, 황실 일가의 한 사람이 지나가도록 허리를 굽혀 자리를 비키느라 말을 멈췄다.\n\n* Il fait des passions: 프랑스어로 '그가 열정을 불러일으킨다'는 뜻. bras dessous: 프랑스어로 '팔짱을 끼고'라는 뜻. C'est un homme qui n'a pas: 프랑스어로 '그는 ……이 없는 사람이다'라는 뜻.\n\n이렇게 사람들은 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에 대해 흠을 잡고 비웃으며 끊임없이 이야기를 나누었고, 그동안 그는 붙잡아 둔 황실 자문회의 인사의 앞을 가로막은 채, 자신의 새 재정 계획안을 조목조목 설명하면서, 상대가 달아날까 봐 한순간도 자신의 이야기를 멈추지 않았다.\n아내가 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치를 떠난 것과 거의 동시에, 관료의 인생에서 가장 쓰라린 순간이—상승 일로를 걷던 출셋길이 완전히 멈춰 서는 순간이—그에게 찾아왔다. 그 정지의 순간이 도래했고 모두가 그것을 알아차렸지만, 정작 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치 자신은 자기 출셋길이 끝났다는 것을 아직 깨닫지 못하고 있었다. 그것이 스트레모프와의 불화 탓이든, 아내로 인한 불운 탓이든, 아니면 단순히 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 정해진 한계에 도달한 것이든, 그해가 흐르는 동안 그의 출셋길이 끝났다는 것은 모두에게 분명해졌다. 그는 여전히 비중 있는 자리를 차지하고 있었고, 여러 위원회와 분과에 이름을 올리고 있었지만, 그는 이미 전성기가 지난 사람, 더 이상 아무것도 기대할 것이 없는 사람이었다. 그가 무슨 말을 하든, 무엇을 제안하든, 그것은 오래전부터 익히 알려진 것이자 도무지 필요하지 않은 것으로 여겨졌다. 그러나 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 이것을 깨닫지 못했고, 오히려 정부 활동에 직접 참여하는 데서 차단되자, 다른 이들의 행동에서 드러나는 오류와 결함을 그 어느 때보다 또렷이 보았으며, 그것을 바로잡을 방안을 지적하는 것이 자기 의무라고 여겼다. 아내와 헤어진 직후, 그는 새로운 사법 절차에 관한 첫 보고서를 쓰기 시작했는데, 그것은 앞으로 그가 끝없이 쓰게 될 보고서들의 첫 번째였다.\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 관료 사회에서 자기 처지가 가망 없다는 것을 알아차리지 못했을 뿐 아니라, 이 점에 대해 아무런 불안도 느끼지 않았으며, 오히려 자기 활동에 그 어느 때보다 만족하고 있었다.\n\n\"장가들지 않은 자는 주의 일을 염려하여 어찌하여야 주를 기쁘게 할까 하되, 장가든 자는 세상일을 염려하여 어찌하여야 아내를 기쁘게 할까 하느니라.\" 사도 바울은 이렇게 말했고, 이제 모든 행동을 성경에 따라 인도받는 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 이 구절을 자주 떠올렸다. 아내 없이 홀로 남겨진 이래, 자기가 바로 이 개혁 계획들 속에서 전보다 더 열성적으로 주를 섬겨 왔다고 그는 여겼다.\n\n자문회의 인사가 그에게서 벗어나려고 안달하는 기색이 역력했지만, 그것은 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치를 괴롭히지 못했다. 그가 설명을 그만둔 것은, 자문회의 인사가 황실 일가의 한 사람이 지나가는 틈을 놓치지 않고 그에게서 빠져나가 버렸을 때뿐이었다.\n\n홀로 남겨진 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 시선을 떨군 채 생각을 가다듬다가, 무심히 주위를 둘러보고는 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인을 만나게 되리라고 기대하던 문 쪽으로 걸어갔다.\n\n\"저들은 모두 얼마나 건장한가, 얼마나 육체적으로 멀쩡한가.\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 잘 빗어 넘긴 향수 뿌린 구레나룻을 한 건장한 시종과, 꽉 끼는 제복에 짓눌린 공작의 붉은 목덜미를 바라보며 생각했다. 그는 그들 곁을 지나쳐 가야만 했다. \"온 세상이 악하다는 말은 참으로 옳도다.\" 그는 시종의 종아리를 또 한 번 곁눈질하며 생각했다.\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 신중하게 앞으로 나아가며, 자기에 대해 떠들고 있던 신사에게 평소의 그 지친 듯하면서도 위엄 있는 태도로 인사를 하고는, 문 쪽을 바라보며 눈으로 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인을 찾았다.\n\n\"아! 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치!\" 카레닌이 그들과 나란히 서서 쌀쌀맞은 몸짓으로 고개를 끄덕이는 순간, 작은 노인이 눈에 짓궂은 빛을 띠며 말했다. \"아직 축하 인사를 못 드렸군요.\" 노인은 그가 새로 받은 리본을 가리키며 말했다.\n\n\"고맙습니다.\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 대답했다. \"오늘은 참으로 절묘한 날이군요.\" 그는 절묘한이라는 단어에 자기 특유의 방식으로 힘을 주며 덧붙였다.\n\n저들이 자기를 비웃고 있다는 것을 그는 잘 알고 있었지만, 그는 저들에게서 적의 말고는 아무것도 기대하지 않았다. 그는 이제 그것에 익숙해져 있었다.\n\n코르셋 위로 불룩 솟은 리디아 이바노브나의 누런 어깨와, 자기를 부르는 그녀의 곱고 생각에 잠긴 듯한 눈이 시야에 들어오자, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 빛바래지 않은 흰 이를 드러내며 미소 짓고는 그녀 쪽으로 걸어갔다.\n\n리디아 이바노브나의 드레스는, 사실 요즘 그녀의 모든 드레스가 그러했듯, 그녀에게 무던히도 애를 먹였다. 옷차림에서 그녀가 지향하는 바는 이제 삼십 년 전에 추구하던 것과 정반대였다. 그때 그녀의 바람은 무언가로 몸을 치장하는 것이었고, 더 많이 치장할수록 좋았다. 그런데 지금은 그와 반대로, 자기 나이와 몸매에 너무도 어울리지 않게 어쩔 수 없이 잔뜩 꾸며야만 했기에, 그녀의 유일한 근심거리는 이 치장과 자신의 외모 사이의 대비가 너무 끔찍해 보이지 않도록 궁리하는 것이었다. 그리고 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에 관한 한 그녀는 성공을 거두었고, 그의 눈에 매력적으로 비쳤다. 그를 둘러싼 적의와 조롱의 바다 한가운데서, 그녀는 그에게 호의뿐 아니라 사랑까지 베풀어 주는 유일한 섬이었다.\n\n조롱 어린 눈길의 행렬 사이를 지나, 그는 식물이 태양을 향하듯 자연스럽게 그녀의 사랑스러운 눈길로 이끌려 갔다.\n\n\"축하드려요.\" 그녀가 그의 리본에 눈길을 두며 말했다.\n\n기쁜 미소를 억누르며 그는 어깨를 으쓱하고는, 그것이 자기에게 기쁨의 원천이 될 수 없다는 듯이 눈을 감았다. 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인은, 비록 그가 결코 인정하지 않았지만, 그것이 그의 가장 큰 만족거리 가운데 하나라는 것을 아주 잘 알고 있었다.\n\n\"우리 천사는 어떻게 지내나요?\" 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인이 세료자를 두고 말했다.\n\n\"그 애가 아주 만족스럽다고는 말 못 하겠습니다.\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 눈썹을 치켜올리며 눈을 떴다. \"그리고 시트니코프도 그 애에게 만족하지 못하고 있어요.\" (시트니코프는 세료자의 세속 교육을 맡은 가정교사였다.) \"전에도 말씀드렸듯이, 그 애는 모든 사람과 모든 아이의 마음을 움직여야 할 가장 중요한 문제들에 대해 일종의 냉담함을 보입니다…….\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 관직 외에 자기가 관심을 두는 유일한 문제, 곧 아들의 교육에 대한 자신의 견해를 펼치기 시작했다.\n\n리디아 이바노브나의 도움으로 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 새로이 삶과 활동으로 되돌아왔을 때, 그는 자기 손에 남겨진 아들의 교육을 떠맡는 것이 자기 의무라고 느꼈다. 전에는 교육 문제에 아무런 관심도 가져 본 적이 없었던 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 이 주제를 이론적으로 연구하는 데 얼마간 시간을 바쳤다. 인류학, 교육학, 교수법에 관한 책 몇 권을 읽은 뒤, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 교육 계획을 세웠고, 그것을 지도할 페테르부르크 최고의 가정교사를 고용하여 일에 착수했다. 그리고 이 주제는 끊임없이 그를 사로잡았다.\n\n\"네, 하지만 마음이지요. 저는 그 애에게서 그 아버지의 마음을 봅니다. 그런 마음을 가진 아이라면 크게 잘못될 리 없어요.\" 리디아 이바노브나가 열성적으로 말했다.\n\n\"네, 어쩌면 그럴지도요…… 저로 말하자면, 저는 제 의무를 다할 뿐입니다. 제가 할 수 있는 건 그게 전부예요.\"\n\n\"우리 집에 오시는 거죠?\" 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인이 잠시 사이를 두고 말했다. \"당신에게 괴로운 일에 대해 이야기를 나눠야 해요. 어떤 기억들로부터 당신을 지켜 드릴 수만 있다면 무엇이든 다 내놓겠지만, 다른 이들은 그렇게 생각지 않는군요. 저는 그 여자에게서 편지를 받았어요. 그 여자가 여기 페테르부르크에 와 있어요.\"\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 아내에 대한 암시에 몸을 떨었지만, 곧 그의 얼굴은 이 문제에 대해 완전한 무력감을 드러내는 죽은 사람 같은 굳은 표정을 띠었다.\n\n\"예상하고 있었습니다.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인은 황홀해하며 그를 바라보았고, 그의 영혼의 위대함에 감격한 눈물이 그녀의 눈에 차올랐다.\n\n# Chapter 25\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인의 아늑하고 작은 규방으로 들어섰을 때, 오래된 도자기로 꾸며지고 초상화들이 걸린 그 방에는 정작 부인의 모습이 아직 보이지 않았다.\n\n그녀는 옷을 갈아입는 중이었다.\n\n둥근 탁자 위에는 식탁보가 깔려 있었고, 그 위에 도자기 찻잔 세트와 은제 알코올램프, 그리고 찻주전자가 놓여 있었다. 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 방을 장식한, 익숙하기 그지없는 끝없는 초상화들을 멍하니 둘러보다가, 탁자에 앉아 그 위에 놓인 신약성서를 펼쳤다. 백작 부인의 비단 치마가 사각거리는 소리에 그의 주의가 흩어졌다.\n\n\"자, 이제 우리 조용히 앉아서,\" 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인은 들뜬 미소를 지으며 탁자와 소파 사이로 황급히 미끄러지듯 들어와 앉았다. \"차를 마시며 이야기를 나눌 수 있겠네요.\"\n\n미리 준비해 둔 몇 마디를 건넨 뒤, 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인은 숨을 가쁘게 몰아쉬고 얼굴이 새빨갛게 달아오른 채로, 자기가 받은 편지를 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 손에 넘겨주었다.\n\n편지를 다 읽고 나서, 그는 오랫동안 말없이 앉아 있었다.\n\n\"나에게는 그녀의 청을 거절할 권리가 없는 것 같습니다.\" 그가 머뭇거리며 눈을 들어 말했다.\n\n\"친애하는 벗이여, 당신은 누구에게서도 악을 보지 못하시는군요!\"\n\n\"오히려 그 반대입니다. 나는 모든 것이 악이라는 걸 봅니다. 하지만 그것이 과연 옳은 일인지는…….\"\n\n그의 얼굴에는 망설임과, 자기로서는 이해할 수 없는 문제에 대해 조언과 지지와 인도를 구하는 빛이 떠올라 있었다.\n\n\"아니에요.\" 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인이 그의 말을 가로막았다. \"모든 일에는 한계가 있는 법이에요. 부도덕은 이해할 수 있어요.\" 그녀는 이렇게 말했지만 이는 그다지 진실하지 못했다. 여자를 부도덕으로 이끄는 것이 무엇인지 그녀는 결코 이해할 수 없었기 때문이다. \"하지만 잔인함은 이해할 수가 없어요. 대체 누구한테 그러는 거죠? 당신한테요! 어떻게 그 여자가 당신이 있는 도시에 머물 수 있단 말인가요? 아니에요, 사람은 오래 살수록 더 많이 배우는 법이죠. 그리고 나는 당신의 고결함과 그 여자의 비열함을 이해하는 법을 배우고 있어요.\"\n\n\"누가 돌을 던질 수 있겠습니까?\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 자기가 맡은 역할에 분명히 만족스러워하며 말했다. \"나는 모든 것을 용서했습니다. 그러니 그녀 안에 있는 사랑이—아들에 대한 그녀의 사랑이 요구하는 것을 빼앗을 수는 없지요…….\"\n\n\"하지만 그것이 사랑일까요, 벗이여? 그것이 진실한 것일까요? 당신이 용서했다고—용서한다고 칩시다. 그렇다고 우리에게 그 천사 같은 아이의 감정을 흔들 권리가 있을까요? 그 아이는 어머니를 죽은 사람으로 여기고 있어요. 그 아이는 어머니를 위해 기도하고, 그녀의 죄를 자비롭게 여겨 달라고 하느님께 간구하고 있다고요. 그러니 이대로가 더 나아요. 그런데 이제 와서 그 아이가 무슨 생각을 하겠어요?\"\n\n\"거기까지는 미처 생각하지 못했습니다.\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 분명히 동의하며 말했다.\n\n리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인은 두 손에 얼굴을 묻고 말이 없었다. 그녀는 기도를 드리고 있었다.\n\n\"제 조언을 구하신다면,\" 기도를 마치고 얼굴에서 손을 떼면서 그녀가 말했다. \"저는 이 일을 하지 마시라고 권하겠어요. 당신이 얼마나 괴로워하시는지, 이 일이 당신의 상처를 얼마나 헤집어 놓았는지 제가 모를 것 같으세요? 하지만 당신이 늘 그러시듯 자신은 생각하지 않으신다 쳐도, 이 일이 어떤 결과를 가져오겠어요? 당신에게는 새로운 고통을, 그 아이에게는 시련을 안기겠지요. 그 여자에게 인간적인 면이 조금이라도 남아 있다면, 자기 입으로 이런 걸 바라서는 안 되는 거예요. 아니에요, 저는 망설임 없이 말씀드리겠어요. 하지 마시라고요. 그리고 이 일을 제게 맡겨 주신다면, 제가 그녀에게 편지를 쓰겠어요.\"\n\n그리하여 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 동의했고, 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인은 프랑스어로 다음과 같은 편지를 보냈다.\n\n\"친애하는 부인,\n\n당신에 관한 기억을 떠올리는 것은 당신의 아들에게 어떤 결과를 가져올 수 있습니다. 곧 아이의 마음속에 그가 신성하게 여겨야 할 것에 대한 비난의 정신을 심지 않고서는 답할 수 없는 의문을 그 아이가 품게 만들 수 있다는 것입니다. 그러니 부디 당신 남편의 거절을 그리스도교적 사랑의 정신으로 받아들여 주시기를 청합니다. 전능하신 하느님께서 당신에게 자비를 베풀어 주시기를 기도합니다.\n\n리디아 백작 부인.\"\n\n이 편지는 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인이 스스로에게도 숨겨 온 은밀한 목적을 이루었다. 그것은 안나에게 깊은 상처를 입혔다.\n\n한편 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 리디아 이바노브나의 집에서 돌아온 뒤, 그날 하루 종일 평소 하던 일에 마음을 집중할 수도 없었고, 근래에 느껴 오던, 구원받고 믿음을 가진 자의 그 영적 평온도 찾을 수 없었다.\n\n자기에게 그토록 큰 죄를 지은 아내, 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인이 그토록 옳게 말해 주었듯이 자기가 그토록 성스럽게 대해 준 아내에 대한 생각은, 그를 괴롭히지 말았어야 했다. 그런데도 그는 마음이 편치 않았다. 읽고 있는 책을 이해할 수도 없었고, 그녀와의 관계에 대한, 그리고 이제 와서 생각하니 그녀에 대해 저질러 버린 것 같은 그 실수에 대한 괴로운 기억들을 떨쳐 버릴 수도 없었다. 경마장에서 집으로 돌아오던 길에 그녀가 부정을 고백했을 때 자기가 어떻게 받아들였는지(특히 겉으로 드러나는 체면을 지키라고만 했을 뿐 결투를 신청하지 않았다는 것)에 대한 기억은 회한처럼 그를 괴롭혔다. 그녀에게 써 보냈던 편지에 대한 생각도 그를 괴롭혔다. 그리고 무엇보다, 아무도 원하지 않은 그의 용서와, 다른 남자의 자식을 돌본다는 일이 수치심과 회한으로 그의 가슴을 타오르게 했다.\n\n그리고 지금도 그는 똑같은 수치심과 후회를 느꼈다. 그녀와 함께한 자기의 모든 과거를 되짚어 보면서, 오랜 망설임 끝에 그녀에게 청혼하던 그 어색했던 말들을 떠올렸던 것이다.\n\n\"하지만 내가 무슨 잘못을 했단 말인가?\" 그는 혼잣말로 중얼거렸다. 그리고 이 물음은 그의 안에서 언제나 또 다른 물음을 불러일으켰다. 저들, 저 브론스키며 오블론스키 같은 자들은…… 그 늘씬한 종아리를 가진 시종(侍從)들은, 사랑하고 결혼하는 일을 혹시 다르게 느끼고 다르게 하는 것은 아닐까 하는 물음이었다. 그러자 그 혈기 왕성하고 정력적이며 자신만만한 사내들이 줄지어 그의 머릿속을 스쳐 지나갔다. 그들은 그가 원치 않는데도 언제나 어디서나 그의 호기심 어린 주의를 끄는 자들이었다. 그는 이런 생각들을 떨쳐 내려 애썼고, 자기는 이 덧없는 삶을 위해서가 아니라 영원한 삶을 위해 살고 있으며, 자기 마음속에는 평화와 사랑이 깃들어 있다고 스스로를 설득하려 애썼다.\n\n그러나 이 덧없고 하찮은 삶에서 자기가, 보기에는 몇 가지 하찮은 실수를 저질렀다는 사실이, 마치 자기가 믿는 영원한 구원이라는 것이 존재하지 않기라도 한 듯 그를 괴롭혔다. 하지만 이 유혹은 오래가지 않았고, 이내 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 영혼 속에는 다시금 평화와 고양이 자리 잡았다. 그것의 힘으로 그는 기억하고 싶지 않은 것을 잊을 수 있었다.\n\n# Chapter 26\n\n\"카피토니치, 어땠어?\" 세료자는 생일 전날 산책에서 발그레하고 기분 좋은 얼굴로 돌아와, 키 큰 늙은 현관지기에게 외투를 건네며 물었다. 현관지기는 자기 긴 키의 높이에서 그 조그만 사람을 내려다보며 빙긋이 웃었다. \"그래, 붕대 감은 그 관리가 오늘 왔었어? 아빠가 그 사람 만났어?\"\n\n\"만나셨지. 비서관이 나오자마자 제가 들여보냈답니다.\" 현관지기가 기분 좋게 눈을 찡긋하며 말했다. \"자, 제가 벗겨 드리지요.\"\n\n\"세료자!\" 가정교사가 안쪽 방으로 통하는 문간에서 멈춰 서서 말했다. \"혼자서 벗어.\" 그러나 세료자는 가정교사의 가냘픈 목소리를 듣고도 거기에 주의를 기울이지 않았다. 그는 현관지기의 허리띠를 붙잡은 채 그의 얼굴을 빤히 올려다보며 서 있었다.\n\n\"그래서, 아빠가 그 사람을 위해 원하던 걸 해 줬어?\"\n\n현관지기가 그렇다는 듯 고개를 끄덕였다. 얼굴을 싸맨 그 관리는 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에게 무슨 부탁을 하러 벌써 일곱 번이나 찾아온 사람이었는데, 세료자도 현관지기도 그에게 관심을 두고 있었다. 세료자는 현관에서 그와 마주친 적이 있었고, 그가 현관지기에게 자기와 자기 아이들이 죽음을 코앞에 두고 있다고 말하며 처량하게 들여보내 달라고 애원하는 것을 들었다.\n\n그 뒤로 세료자는 현관에서 그를 두 번째로 만나고는 그에게 큰 흥미를 가지게 되었다.\n\n\"그래서, 그 사람 많이 기뻐했어?\" 세료자가 물었다.\n\n\"기뻐했냐고요? 말씀이라고요! 돌아가면서 거의 춤을 추다시피 했답니다.\"\n\n\"그리고 뭐 남겨 둔 거 있어?\" 잠시 뜸을 들인 뒤 세료자가 물었다.\n\n\"이리 오세요, 도련님.\" 현관지기가 말하고는 고개를 한 번 흔들며 속삭였다. \"백작 부인한테서 온 게 있답니다.\"\n\n세료자는 현관지기가 말하는 것이 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인이 자기 생일을 위해 보낸 선물이라는 걸 단번에 알아차렸다.\n\n\"뭐라고? 어디 있는데?\"\n\n\"코르네이가 도련님 아빠한테 가져갔지요. 분명 근사한 장난감일 거예요!\"\n\n\"얼마나 커? 이만큼?\"\n\n\"좀 작긴 한데, 근사한 물건이에요.\"\n\n\"책이구나.\"\n\n\"아니요, 물건이라니까요. 어서요, 어서 가세요, 바실리 루키치가 부르네요.\" 가정교사의 발소리가 다가오는 것을 듣고 현관지기가 말하며, 반쯤 벗겨진 장갑을 낀 작은 손을 자기 허리띠에서 조심스레 떼어 내고는 고갯짓으로 가정교사 쪽을 가리켰다.\n\n\"바실리 루키치, 잠깐만요, 곧 갈게요!\" 세료자는 늘 그 성실한 바실리 루키치의 마음을 사로잡곤 하던, 그 명랑하고 사랑스러운 미소를 지으며 대답했다.\n\n세료자는 너무나 행복했고, 모든 것이 너무나 즐거웠던 터라, 공원에서 산책하는 동안 리디아 이바노브나의 조카에게서 들은 집안의 경사를 친구인 현관지기와 나누지 않고는 견딜 수가 없었다. 이 좋은 소식은, 붕대 감은 관리의 기쁨, 그리고 자기 앞으로 장난감이 왔다는 자기 자신의 기쁨과 때를 같이하여 찾아왔다는 점에서 그에게 특히 중요해 보였다. 세료자에게는 이날이 모두가 기뻐하고 행복해야 하는 날인 것만 같았다.\n\n\"아빠가 오늘 알렉산드르 넵스키 훈장 받은 거 알아?\"\n\n\"그럼 알다마다요! 벌써 축하하러 온 사람들이 있는걸요.\"\n\n\"그래서 아빠가 기뻐해?\"\n\n\"황제 폐하의 자애로운 은총을 받았으니 기뻐하시지요! 말씀이라고요! 그건 그만한 자격이 있으셨다는 증거랍니다.\" 현관지기가 엄숙하고 진지하게 말했다.\n\n세료자는 자기가 구석구석 속속들이 살펴본 적 있는 현관지기의 얼굴을, 특히 회색 구레나룻 사이로 늘어진 턱을 올려다보며 몽상에 잠겼다. 그 턱은 늘 아래에서만 그를 올려다보는 세료자 말고는 누구도 본 적 없는 것이었다.\n\n\"그래, 따님은 요즘 보러 왔었어?\"\n\n현관지기의 딸은 발레 무용수였다.\n\n\"평일에 어떻게 오겠어요? 그 애들도 배워야 할 게 있거든요. 도련님도 공부할 게 있으시잖아요, 어서 가세요.\"\n\n방에 들어온 세료자는 공부 자리에 앉는 대신, 자기에게 온 것이 분명 기계일 거라는 자기 짐작을 가정교사에게 이야기했다. \"선생님은 어떻게 생각하세요?\" 그가 물었다.\n\n그러나 바실리 루키치는 두 시에 오기로 한 선생님을 위해 문법 수업을 익혀 둬야 한다는 생각밖에는 하지 않고 있었다.\n\n\"아니, 그냥 말씀해 주세요, 바실리 루키치.\" 그가 손에 책을 든 채 공부 책상 앞에 앉아 별안간 물었다. \"알렉산드르 넵스키보다 더 높은 게 뭐예요? 아빠가 알렉산드르 넵스키 받은 거 아시죠?\"\n\n바실리 루키치는 블라디미르 훈장이 알렉산드르 넵스키보다 더 높다고 대답했다.\n\n\"그것보다 더 높은 건요?\"\n\n\"음, 제일 높은 건 안드레이 페르보즈반니 훈장이지요.\"\n\n\"안드레이보다 더 높은 건요?\"\n\n\"모르겠는데요.\"\n\n\"뭐예요, 모르신다고요?\" 세료자는 팔꿈치를 괴고 깊은 명상에 빠져들었다.\n\n그의 명상은 더없이 복잡하고 갖가지로 뻗어 나갔다. 그는 아빠가 오늘 별안간 블라디미르 훈장과 안드레이 훈장을 한꺼번에 받게 되어, 그 덕분에 수업 시간에 기분이 훨씬 좋아질 것을 상상해 보았고, 자기가 어른이 되면 직접 모든 훈장을 받게 될 것이며, 안드레이보다 더 높은 무엇을 만들어 낼지를 꿈꿔 보았다. 더 높은 훈장이 만들어지는 즉시 그는 그것을 따낼 것이다. 사람들이 그보다 더 높은 것을 만들어 내면, 그는 곧장 그것도 따낼 것이다.\n\n이런 명상 속에 시간이 흘렀고, 선생님이 왔을 때 장소와 시간과 동작의 양태를 나타내는 부사에 관한 수업 준비가 되어 있지 않았다. 선생님은 불쾌해했을 뿐 아니라 마음이 상했다. 세료자는 이것이 마음에 걸렸다. 그는 수업을 익히지 못한 것이 자기 잘못은 아니라고 느꼈다. 아무리 애써도 그는 도저히 그것을 할 수가 없었다. 선생님이 그에게 설명해 주는 동안에는 그 말을 믿었고 이해하는 듯도 했지만, 혼자 남겨지기만 하면 그 짧고 익숙한 단어 \"갑자기\"가 동작의 양태를 나타내는 부사라는 것을 도무지 떠올리지도, 이해하지도 못했다. 그래도 선생님을 실망시킨 것이 미안했다.\n\n그는 선생님이 말없이 책을 들여다보는 순간을 골랐다.\n\n\"미하일 이바니치, 선생님 생일은 언제예요?\" 그가 느닷없이 물었다.\n\n\"공부 생각이나 하는 게 훨씬 낫겠다. 생일이란 이성을 가진 존재에게는 아무 의미도 없는 거야. 자기 할 일을 해야 하는, 다른 날과 똑같은 하루일 뿐이지.\"\n\n세료자는 선생님을, 그 듬성듬성한 수염을, 콧대 아래로 흘러내린 안경을 뚫어지게 바라보며 깊은 몽상에 빠져들어, 선생님이 자기에게 설명하는 말을 하나도 듣지 못했다. 그는 선생님이 자기가 한 말을 진심으로 생각하는 게 아니라는 것을 알았다. 그 말투에서 그것을 느꼈던 것이다. \"그런데 왜 다들 늘 이렇게 가장 따분하고 가장 쓸데없는 말만 똑같은 식으로 하기로 한 걸까? 왜 선생님은 나를 멀리하는 걸까, 왜 나를 사랑하지 않는 걸까?\" 그는 서글프게 자문해 보았지만 답을 찾을 수 없었다.\n\n# Chapter 27\n\n문법 선생과의 수업이 끝나자 아버지와의 수업이 이어졌다. 아버지를 기다리는 동안 세료자는 책상 앞에 앉아 주머니칼을 만지작거리며 공상에 잠겨 들었다. 세료자가 좋아하는 일 가운데 하나는 산책할 때 어머니를 찾는 것이었다. 그는 죽음이라는 것을 도무지 믿지 않았고, 특히 어머니의 죽음은 더더욱 믿지 않았는데, 리디아 이바노브나가 그렇게 말해 주었고 아버지도 그것을 확인해 주었음에도 그랬다. 그리고 바로 그 때문에, 어머니가 죽었다는 말을 들은 이후로 그는 산책을 나갈 때면 어머니를 찾기 시작한 것이었다. 풍만하고 우아한 몸매에 검은 머리를 한 여자라면 누구나 그에게는 어머니였다. 그런 여자를 볼 때마다 그의 마음속에는 어찌나 다정한 감정이 일었던지 숨이 막혀 왔고 눈에는 눈물이 고였다. 그리고 그는 그 여자가 자기에게 다가와 베일을 들어 올리리라는 기대로 까치발을 들었다. 그러면 얼굴 전체가 드러나고, 어머니가 미소를 짓고, 그를 끌어안을 것이며, 그는 어머니의 향기를 들이마시고 두 팔의 부드러움을 느끼며 행복에 겨워 울 터였다. 언젠가 저녁에 그가 어머니 무릎에 누워 있고 어머니가 그를 간질여서, 그가 웃으며 반지 낀 어머니의 하얀 손가락을 깨물었던 그날처럼 말이다. 그 후 그는 늙은 유모에게서 우연히 어머니가 죽지 않았다는 사실을 알게 되었고, 아버지와 리디아 이바노브나는 그에게 어머니는 사악한 사람이기에 그에게는 죽은 사람이나 마찬가지라고 설명해 주었지만(그는 어머니를 사랑했기에 도저히 그 말을 믿을 수 없었다), 그래도 그는 여전히 같은 방식으로 어머니를 찾고 기다렸다. 그날 공원에는 라일락빛 베일을 쓴 한 부인이 있었는데, 그 부인이 오솔길을 따라 자기들 쪽으로 다가오자 그는 두근거리는 가슴으로 그녀가 바로 어머니라고 믿으며 지켜보았다. 그 부인은 그들에게 다가오지 않고 어디론가 사라져 버렸다. 그날 세료자는 그 어느 때보다도 강하게 어머니를 향한 사랑이 솟구치는 것을 느꼈고, 지금은 아버지를 기다리며 모든 것을 잊은 채 주머니칼로 책상 가장자리를 빙 둘러 깎아 내면서, 반짝이는 눈으로 정면을 똑바로 응시하며 어머니를 꿈꾸고 있었다.\n\n\"아버님께서 오셨다!\" 바실리 루키치가 그를 일깨우며 말했다.\n\n세료자는 벌떡 일어나 아버지에게 다가가 손에 입을 맞추고는, 알렉산드르 네프스키 훈장을 받은 기쁨의 기색을 찾아내려고 아버지를 뚫어지게 바라보았다.\n\n\"산책은 즐거웠느냐?\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 안락의자에 앉아 구약성서 한 권을 끌어당겨 펼치며 물었다. 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 모든 그리스도교인은 성서의 역사를 철저히 알아야 한다고 세료자에게 여러 번 일러 왔으면서도, 정작 수업 중에 자신이 종종 성서를 들춰 보곤 했고, 세료자는 이를 눈여겨보았다.\n\n\"네, 정말 즐거웠어요, 아버지.\" 세료자가 의자에 비스듬히 앉아 의자를 흔들며 대답했는데, 그것은 금지된 행동이었다. \"나딘카를 봤어요.\" (나딘카는 리디아 이바노브나의 조카로 그녀의 집에서 자라고 있었다.) \"나딘카가 그러는데 아버지가 새 훈장을 받으셨대요. 기쁘세요, 아버지?\"\n\n\"우선 의자를 흔들지 말거라.\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 말했다. \"그리고 둘째로, 소중한 것은 보상이 아니라 일 자체란다. 네가 그것을 이해해 주었으면 한다. 만약 네가 지금 보상을 얻기 위해 일하고 공부한다면 일이 너에게 힘들게 느껴질 게다. 하지만 일을 사랑하며 일한다면\" (알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 이렇게 말하면서, 백여든 장의 서류에 서명하는 오전의 따분한 노동을 의무감으로 버텨 냈던 일을 떠올렸다) \"그 일 속에서 보상을 찾게 될 게다.\"\n\n쾌활함과 다정함으로 빛나던 세료자의 눈이 흐릿해지더니 아버지의 시선 앞에서 떨구어졌다. 이것은 아버지가 늘 그에게 쓰던, 오래전부터 익숙한 바로 그 어조였고, 세료자는 이제 거기에 장단을 맞추는 법을 터득해 두고 있었다. 아버지는 늘 그에게—세료자는 그렇게 느꼈다—마치 자기가 상상해 낸 어떤 소년, 책 속에나 존재하는, 자기와는 전혀 다른 그런 소년들 가운데 하나에게 말을 거는 듯이 이야기했다. 그리고 세료자는 아버지 앞에서는 늘 그 이야기책 속 소년인 척 연기하려고 애썼다.\n\n\"이해하겠지, 그렇지?\" 아버지가 말했다.\n\n\"네, 아버지.\" 세료자는 상상 속 소년의 역할을 연기하며 대답했다.\n\n수업은 복음서에서 몇 구절을 외우고 구약성서의 첫머리를 암송하는 것이었다. 복음서 구절은 세료자도 제법 잘 알고 있었지만, 막 그것을 외우려는 순간 아버지의 이마에 날카롭게 튀어나온 뼈마디의 울퉁불퉁함을 바라보는 데 그만 정신이 팔려 버려서 실마리를 놓치고 말았고, 한 구절의 끝과 다른 구절의 첫머리를 뒤바꿔 버렸다. 그래서 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에게는 아들이 자기가 외우는 말의 뜻을 이해하지 못하고 있는 것이 분명해 보였고, 그것이 그를 짜증 나게 했다.\n\n그는 미간을 찌푸리고는, 세료자가 이미 여러 번 들었으면서도 끝내 외우지 못하는 것을 설명하기 시작했다. 너무도 잘 이해하고 있었기 때문에 외우지 못한 것이었으니, 마치 그 \"갑자기\"가 행동의 양태를 나타내는 부사라는 것을 외우지 못하는 것과 같았다. 세료자는 겁에 질린 눈으로 아버지를 바라보면서, 아버지가 가끔 그러듯 자기가 한 말을 다시 외워 보라고 시키지는 않을까 하는 생각밖에 할 수 없었다. 그리고 그 생각이 세료자를 얼마나 불안하게 했던지 이제 그는 아무것도 이해하지 못했다. 하지만 아버지는 그에게 다시 외워 보라고 시키지 않고 구약성서 부분으로 넘어갔다. 세료자는 사건들 자체는 곧잘 늘어놓았지만, 어떤 사건이 무엇을 예표하는가 하는 질문에 답해야 할 때면 아무것도 알지 못했다. 이미 이 대목 때문에 벌을 받은 적이 있었는데도 그랬다. 그가 도무지 한마디도 하지 못하고 안절부절못하며 책상을 깎고 의자를 흔들기 시작한 대목은 대홍수 이전의 족장들을 외워야 하는 부분이었다. 그는 산 채로 하늘로 들어 올려진 에녹 외에는 한 사람도 알지 못했다. 지난번에는 그 이름들을 기억했지만 이제는 완전히 잊어버렸는데, 무엇보다도 에녹이 구약성서 전체에서 그가 가장 좋아하는 인물이었고, 에녹이 하늘로 옮겨진 일이 그의 머릿속에서 길게 이어지는 일련의 생각과 얽혀 있었기 때문이었다. 그는 아버지의 회중시계 줄과 조끼의 반쯤 풀린 단추 하나를 매혹된 눈으로 바라보면서 그 생각에 빠져들었다.\n\n사람들이 그에게 그토록 자주 이야기하는 죽음이라는 것을 세료자는 전혀 믿지 않았다. 그는 자기가 사랑하는 사람들이 죽을 수 있다는 것을, 무엇보다도 자기 자신이 죽으리라는 것을 믿지 않았다. 그것은 그에게 도무지 상상할 수도 있을 법하지도 않은 일이었다. 하지만 그는 모든 사람은 죽는다는 말을 들었고, 실제로 자기가 믿는 사람들에게 물어보기도 했는데, 그들 역시 그것을 확인해 주었으며, 늙은 유모도 마지못해서이긴 했지만 같은 말을 했다. 그렇지만 에녹은 죽지 않았으니, 따라서 모든 사람이 죽는 것은 아니라는 결론이 나오는 것이다. \"그렇다면 어째서 다른 사람은 그렇게 하느님을 섬기고 산 채로 하늘로 들어 올려질 수 없는 거지?\" 세료자는 생각했다. 나쁜 사람들, 그러니까 세료자가 좋아하지 않는 사람들은 죽을지도 모르지만, 착한 사람들은 모두 에녹처럼 될 수 있을 터였다.\n\n\"자, 족장들의 이름이 무엇이냐?\"\n\n\"에녹, 에노스……\"\n\n\"그건 벌써 말하지 않았느냐. 이건 나쁘구나, 세료자, 아주 나쁜 일이야. 그리스도교인에게 무엇보다 가장 필요한 것을 배우려 들지 않는다면,\" 아버지가 일어서며 말했다. \"도대체 무엇에 흥미를 느낄 수 있겠느냐? 나는 너에게 실망했고, 표트르 이그나티치도\" (이 사람은 그의 교사들 중 가장 중요한 사람이었다) \"너에게 실망했단다…… 너에게 벌을 주어야겠다.\"\n\n아버지와 교사는 둘 다 세료자에게 실망했고, 그가 수업을 정말 형편없이 배운 것도 사실이었다. 하지만 그래도 그가 멍청한 아이라고는 할 수 없었다. 오히려 그는 교사가 그에게 모범으로 내세우는 소년들보다 훨씬 영리했다. 아버지의 생각으로는, 그가 가르치는 것을 배우려 들지 않는 것이었다. 실은 그것을 배울 수가 없었던 것이다. 그가 배울 수 없었던 까닭은, 자기 영혼이 요구하는 것이 아버지와 교사가 그에게 들이대는 요구보다 그를 더 강하게 옭아맸기 때문이다. 그 요구들은 서로 대립했고, 그는 자기 교육과 정면으로 충돌하고 있었다. 그는 아홉 살이었고, 아이였지만, 자기 영혼을 알고 있었고, 그 영혼을 소중히 여겼으며, 눈꺼풀이 눈을 지키듯 그것을 지켰고, 사랑이라는 열쇠가 없이는 누구도 자기 영혼 안으로 들이지 않았다. 교사들은 그가 배우려 들지 않는다고 불평했지만, 정작 그의 영혼은 앎에 대한 갈증으로 흘러넘치고 있었다. 그리고 그는 카피토니치에게서, 유모에게서, 나딘카에게서, 바실리 루키치에게서 배웠지만, 교사들에게서는 배우지 않았다. 아버지와 교사들이 자기네 물레방아 바퀴를 돌리려고 기대했던 샘물은 진작에 그 원천에서 말라 버렸지만, 그 물은 다른 물길에서 제 일을 해내고 있었다.\n\n아버지는 세료자에게 리디아 이바노브나의 조카 나딘카를 보러 가지 못하게 하는 벌을 내렸다. 하지만 이 벌은 세료자에게는 다행스러운 결과가 되었다. 바실리 루키치가 기분이 좋아서, 그에게 풍차 만드는 법을 가르쳐 주었던 것이다. 저녁 내내 이 일에 매달리고, 어떻게 하면 자기가 직접 올라타 돌 수 있는 풍차를—날개를 붙잡거나 몸을 묶고서 빙글빙글 도는 풍차를—만들 수 있을까 궁리하며 시간을 보냈다. 세료자는 저녁 내내 어머니를 생각하지 않았지만, 잠자리에 들고 나자 문득 어머니가 떠올라, 자기 나름의 말로, 내일 자기 생일에 어머니가 더 이상 모습을 숨기지 말고 자기에게 와 주기를 기도했다.\n\n\"바실리 루키치, 제가 오늘 밤에 늘 하던 기도 말고 따로 또 무엇을 기도했는지 아세요?\"\n\n\"수업을 더 잘 배우게 해 달라고 했느냐?\"\n\n\"아니에요.\"\n\n\"장난감?\"\n\"아니에요. 절대 못 맞히실 거예요. 아주 멋진 거예요. 그렇지만 그건 비밀이에요! 그게 이루어지면 말씀드릴게요. 못 맞히시겠죠!\"\n\n\"그래, 못 맞히겠구나. 네가 말해 보렴.\" 바실리 루키치가, 그에게는 드문 일인 미소를 띠며 말했다. \"자, 누우렴, 촛불을 끌 테니.\"\n\n\"촛불이 없으면 제가 보는 것과 제가 기도한 것이 더 잘 보인단 말이에요. 어머나! 하마터면 비밀을 말할 뻔했네!\" 세료자가 즐겁게 웃으며 말했다.\n\n촛불이 치워지자 세료자는 어머니를 듣고 느꼈다. 어머니가 그의 위에 서서 사랑이 담긴 눈으로 그를 어루만졌다. 하지만 이내 풍차와 칼, 모든 것이 뒤섞이기 시작했고, 그는 잠이 들었다.\n\n# Chapter 28\n\n페테르부르크에 도착한 브론스키와 안나는 최고급 호텔 가운데 한 곳에 묵었다. 브론스키는 아래층에 따로 들었고, 안나는 위층에 아이와 유모, 하녀와 함께 방 네 개짜리 큰 스위트룸을 잡았다.\n\n도착한 날 브론스키는 형의 집으로 갔다. 그곳에서 그는 볼일이 있어 모스크바에서 온 어머니를 만났다. 어머니와 형수는 여느 때처럼 그를 맞이했다. 그들은 그에게 외국 체류에 대해 물었고, 서로 아는 사람들에 관한 이야기를 나누었지만, 안나와의 관계를 넌지시라도 비추는 말은 단 한마디도 흘리지 않았다. 형은 이튿날 아침 브론스키를 보러 왔고, 먼저 나서서 그녀에 대해 물었다. 그러자 알렉세이 브론스키는 솔직하게, 자기는 카레니나 부인과의 관계를 결혼으로 여기고 있으며, 이혼을 성사시킨 뒤 그녀와 결혼할 작정이고, 그때까지는 그녀를 여느 아내와 다름없는 아내로 여긴다고, 그러니 어머니와 형수에게도 그렇게 전해 달라고 부탁했다.\n\n\"세상이 못마땅하게 여긴다 해도 나는 상관없습니다.\" 브론스키가 말했다. \"하지만 내 친척들이 나와 친척으로서 관계를 이어 가길 바란다면, 내 아내와도 똑같은 관계를 맺어야 할 겁니다.\"\n\n언제나 동생의 판단을 존중해 온 형은, 세상이 이 문제를 결정 내리기 전에는 동생이 옳은지 그른지 딱히 말할 수가 없었다. 자기로서는 반대할 것이 없었으므로, 그는 알렉세이와 함께 안나를 만나러 위층으로 올라갔다.\n\n형 앞에서도, 다른 누구 앞에서나 마찬가지로, 브론스키는 안나를 어떤 격식을 차려 대했고, 아주 가까운 친구를 대하듯 행동했다. 하지만 형이 두 사람의 진짜 관계를 알고 있다는 것은 이해된 일이었고, 그들은 안나가 브론스키의 영지로 가는 것에 대해 이야기를 나누었다.\n\n온갖 사교계 경험에도 불구하고, 브론스키는 자기가 놓인 새로운 처지 탓에 이상한 착각에 빠져 있었다. 사교계가 자기와 안나에게 닫혀 버렸다는 것을 그가 당연히 이해했으리라고 누구나 생각했을 것이다. 그러나 지금 그의 머릿속에는 어떤 막연한 생각들이 솟아났다. 그런 일은 케케묵은 옛 시절에나 그랬을 뿐, 이제는 근대적 진보가 빠르게 이루어지면서(그는 어느새 무의식적으로 온갖 진보의 신봉자가 되어 있었다) 사교계의 견해도 바뀌었으며, 자기들이 사교계에 받아들여질지 어떨지는 이미 정해진 결론이 아니라는 것이었다. '물론' 그는 생각했다. '그녀가 궁정에 받아들여지지는 않겠지. 하지만 가까운 친구들이라면 이 일을 제대로 된 시각으로 봐 줄 수 있고, 또 봐 줘야 마땅하지.' 자세를 바꾸는 데 아무 거리낌이 없다는 것을 알고 있으면, 사람은 다리를 꼰 똑같은 자세로 몇 시간이고 내리 앉아 있을 수 있다. 그러나 다리를 꼰 채 그대로 앉아 있어야만 한다는 것을 알게 되면, 쥐가 나기 시작하고, 다리가 씰룩거리며 끌어당기고 싶은 쪽으로 잡아당겨지기 시작한다. 브론스키가 세상에 대해 겪고 있던 것이 바로 이것이었다. 마음 깊은 곳에서는 세상이 자기들에게 닫혀 버렸음을 알면서도, 그는 이제 세상이 바뀌어 자기들을 받아들여 주지 않을까 시험해 보았다. 그러나 그는 곧, 세상이 자기 개인에게는 열려 있지만 안나에게는 닫혀 있다는 것을 깨달았다. 고양이와 쥐 놀이에서처럼, 그를 위해 들어 올려졌던 손들이 안나의 길을 막으려고 내려와 버린 것이다.\n\n브론스키가 만난 페테르부르크 사교계의 일급 부인들 가운데 한 사람이 그의 사촌 누이 벳시였다.\n\n\"드디어 왔군요!\" 그녀가 반갑게 그를 맞이했다. \"안나는요? 어찌나 기쁜지! 어디 묵고 계세요? 그 즐거운 여행을 하고 나니 우리 가엾은 페테르부르크가 끔찍하게 느껴질 게 뻔하죠. 로마에서 신혼여행을 보냈으리라 짐작이 가는걸요. 이혼은 어떻게 됐어요? 다 끝난 건가요?\"\n\n브론스키는 아직 이혼이 이루어지지 않았다는 것을 알게 되자 벳시의 열기가 식어 버리는 것을 알아챘다.\n\n\"사람들이 내게 돌을 던질 테죠, 알아요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"하지만 나는 안나를 보러 갈 거예요. 그럼요, 꼭 갈 거예요. 여기 오래 머무르시지는 않겠지요?\"\n\n그리고 그녀는 정말로 그날 안나를 보러 왔지만, 그 말투는 예전과는 전혀 같지 않았다. 그녀는 분명 자기 용기를 자랑스러워했고, 안나가 자기 우정의 진실함을 알아주기를 바랐다. 그녀는 사교계의 잡담을 늘어놓으며 겨우 십 분쯤 머물렀고, 떠나면서 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"이혼이 언제 끝나는지는 끝내 말씀을 안 해 주셨네요. 설령 내가 체면 따위 내던질 각오가 되어 있다 해도, 다른 깐깐한 사람들은 당신들이 결혼할 때까지 쌀쌀맞게 대할 거예요. 요즘은 그게 아주 간단한 일인데 말이죠. Ça se fait. 그래서 금요일에 떠나시나요? 다시 못 보게 되어 아쉽네요.\"\n\n* Ça se fait: 프랑스어로 '그건 흔히 있는 일이다, 그렇게들 한다'.\n벳시의 말투에서 브론스키는 세상으로부터 무엇을 기대해야 할지 충분히 헤아릴 수 있었다. 하지만 그는 자기 가족 안에서 한 번 더 시도해 보았다. 어머니는 기대하지 않았다. 처음 알게 되었을 때만 해도 안나에게 그토록 열광했던 어머니가, 이제는 아들의 앞길을 망쳐 놓았다는 이유로 그녀에게 조금도 자비를 베풀지 않으리라는 것을 그는 알고 있었다. 그러나 형수 바랴에게는 더 큰 희망을 걸었다. 그녀라면 돌을 던지지 않을 것이고, 소박하고 거리낌 없이 안나를 보러 가서 자기 집으로 그녀를 맞아들이리라고 그는 짐작했다.\n\n도착한 다음 날 브론스키는 그녀를 찾아갔고, 마침 혼자 있는 그녀를 보고 자기 바람을 솔직하게 털어놓았다.\n\n\"알렉세이, 너도 알다시피,\" 그의 말을 다 듣고 나서 그녀가 말했다. \"내가 너를 얼마나 아끼는지, 또 너를 위해서라면 무엇이든 할 마음이 얼마나 큰지 말이야. 하지만 내가 입을 다물고 있었던 건, 너와 안나 아르카지예브나에게 아무 도움도 될 수 없다는 걸 알았기 때문이야.\" 그녀는 '안나 아르카지예브나'라는 이름을 유난히 조심스럽게 또박또박 발음했다. \"제발 내가 그녀를 비난한다고는 생각하지 말아 줘. 절대 아니야. 어쩌면 내가 그녀 처지였더라도 똑같이 했을지 몰라. 나는 그런 데 끼어들지 않고, 끼어들 수도 없어.\" 그녀는 그의 침울한 얼굴을 겁먹은 눈으로 흘끗 보며 말했다. \"하지만 모든 일은 그 본래 이름대로 불러야 하는 법이지. 너는 내가 가서 그녀를 만나고, 이 집으로 그녀를 초대하고, 그녀를 사교계에서 다시 일으켜 세워 주길 바라는 거야. 하지만 내가 그렇게 할 수 없다는 걸 좀 알아줘. 나는 다 커 가는 딸들이 있고, 남편을 위해서라도 사교계에서 살아가야 해. 그래, 안나 아르카지예브나를 보러 갈 마음은 있어. 그녀도 이해할 거야. 내가 그녀를 이 집으로 초대할 수는 없다는 걸 말이야. 만일 초대한다 해도, 일을 다르게 보는 사람들과 그녀가 마주치지 않도록 해야 할 텐데, 그건 그녀를 모욕하는 셈이 될 거야. 나는 그녀를 끌어올려 줄 수가 없어……\"\n\n\"오, 나는 형수님이 집에 들이는 수백 명의 여자들보다 그녀가 더 타락했다고는 생각하지 않습니다!\" 브론스키가 한층 더 침울하게 그녀의 말을 끊었다. 그러고는 형수의 결심이 흔들리지 않으리라는 것을 알아채고는 말없이 자리에서 일어났다.\n\n\"알렉세이! 나한테 화내지 마. 제발 이게 내 잘못이 아니라는 걸 알아줘.\" 바랴가 겁먹은 미소를 띠고 그를 바라보며 말을 꺼냈다.\n\n\"형수님께 화난 게 아닙니다.\" 그는 여전히 침울하게 말했다. \"다만 두 가지로 마음이 아플 뿐입니다. 이 일로 우리 우정이 깨지게 되었다는 것도 마음이 아프고요. 깨지지는 않더라도, 적어도 약해지긴 하겠지요. 나로서도 달리 어쩔 수 없다는 걸 형수님은 이해하실 겁니다.\"\n\n그리고 그 말을 끝으로 그는 그녀의 곁을 떠났다.\n\n브론스키는 더 이상의 노력이 헛되다는 것을, 그리고 이 며칠을 마치 낯선 도시에 와 있는 것처럼 페테르부르크에서 보내야 한다는 것을 알았다. 그는 자기에게 그토록 견디기 힘든 성가신 일과 굴욕에 노출되지 않으려고, 자기가 속했던 옛 무리와의 온갖 관계를 피했다. 페테르부르크에서 그가 처한 처지의 가장 불쾌한 점 가운데 하나는, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치와 그의 이름이 가는 곳마다 그를 마주치는 것 같다는 것이었다. 무슨 이야기를 꺼내려 해도 대화는 어김없이 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치 쪽으로 흘러갔고, 어디를 가든 그와 마주칠 위험이 있었다. 적어도 브론스키에게는 그렇게 느껴졌는데, 그것은 마치 손가락에 상처가 난 사람이 무엇에든, 마치 일부러 그러기라도 하듯, 끊임없이 그 상처 난 손가락을 부딪치는 것처럼 여겨지는 것과 같았다.\n\n페테르부르크에서의 체류가 브론스키에게 더욱 괴로웠던 것은, 그가 안나에게서 도무지 이해할 수 없는 어떤 새로운 기분 같은 것을 줄곧 느꼈기 때문이다. 어느 때는 그녀가 그를 사랑하는 듯 보이다가도, 곧 차갑고 신경질적이며 속을 알 수 없게 변해 버렸다. 그녀는 무언가를 두고 애를 태우며 그에게 무언가를 숨기고 있었고, 그의 삶을 좀먹는 굴욕들을 알아채지 못하는 듯했다. 섬세한 직관을 지닌 그녀에게는 그 굴욕들이 분명 더욱더 견디기 힘들었을 텐데도 말이다.\n\n# Chapter 29\n\n안나가 러시아로 돌아온 목적 가운데 하나는 아들을 보는 것이었다. 이탈리아를 떠나던 날부터 그 생각이 한순간도 그녀를 흔들어 놓기를 그치지 않았다. 그리고 페테르부르크에 가까워질수록, 이 만남의 기쁨과 중대함은 그녀의 상상 속에서 점점 더 커져만 갔다. 그녀는 그것을 어떻게 마련해야 할지조차 스스로에게 물어보지 않았다. 아들과 같은 도시에 있게 되면 아들을 보는 것이 그녀에게는 자연스럽고 단순한 일로 여겨졌다. 하지만 페테르부르크에 도착하자 그녀는 별안간 자기가 지금 사교계에서 처한 위치를 또렷이 깨닫게 되었고, 이 만남을 마련하는 일이 결코 쉬운 문제가 아니라는 사실을 알아챘다.\n\n그녀는 이제 페테르부르크에 온 지 이틀이 되었다. 아들 생각이 단 한순간도 그녀를 떠나지 않았건만, 그녀는 아직 아들을 보지 못했다. 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치와 마주칠지도 모를 그 집으로 곧장 가는 것은, 자기에게 그럴 권리가 없다고 그녀는 느꼈다. 입장을 거절당하고 모욕을 당할 수도 있었다. 편지를 써서 남편과 관계를 맺는 일은—그것을 생각하는 것만으로도 그녀는 비참해졌다. 그녀는 남편을 생각하지 않을 때에만 마음의 평안을 얻을 수 있었다. 아들이 산책 나가는 장소와 시간을 알아내어 그 모습을 잠깐 엿보는 것으로는 그녀에게 충분하지 않았다. 그녀는 이 만남을 그토록 고대해 왔고, 아들에게 해 줄 말이 그토록 많았으며, 아들을 끌어안고 입맞추기를 그토록 갈망했다. 세료자의 늙은 유모라면 그녀에게 도움을 주고 어떻게 해야 할지 알려 줄 수 있을지도 몰랐다. 하지만 유모는 지금 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 집에서 살고 있지 않았다. 이런 막막함 속에서, 유모를 찾으려는 노력 속에서 이틀이 흘러가 버렸다.\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치와 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인 사이가 매우 가깝다는 이야기를 들은 안나는 사흘째 되던 날 그녀에게 편지를 쓰기로 마음먹었고, 그 편지는 그녀에게 큰 고통을 안겨 주었으며, 거기에 그녀는 아들을 볼 수 있는 허락이 남편의 너그러움에 달려 있어야 한다고 일부러 적어 넣었다. 만약 그 편지를 남편에게 보여 준다면, 남편은 자기의 관대한 인품을 지키려 할 것이고, 그녀의 청을 거절하지 않으리라는 것을 그녀는 알고 있었다.\n\n편지를 가져갔던 심부름꾼이 그녀에게 가져온 것은 더없이 잔인하고 뜻밖의 답변, 곧 답이 없다는 것이었다. 그녀는 심부름꾼을 불러, 그가 어떻게 기다렸으며 그 뒤에 답이 없다는 말을 어떻게 들었는지 정확한 경위를 들었던 그 순간만큼 굴욕감을 느낀 적이 없었다. 안나는 굴욕과 모욕을 느꼈지만, 자기 입장에서 보더라도 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인이 옳다는 것을 알았다. 그녀의 고통은 그것을 홀로 견뎌야 한다는 데에서 한층 더 사무쳤다. 그녀는 그것을 브론스키와 나눌 수도 없었고 나누고 싶지도 않았다. 비록 그가 그녀의 괴로움의 일차적 원인이었음에도, 아들을 보는 문제가 그에게는 별로 대수롭지 않은 일로 여겨지리라는 것을 그녀는 알았다. 그가 자기 고통의 깊이를 결코 온전히 이해하지 못하리라는 것을, 그리고 그것에 관해 무슨 말이라도 나올 때마다 그가 보이는 냉담한 어조 때문에 자기가 그를 미워하기 시작하리라는 것을 그녀는 알고 있었다. 그리고 그녀는 그것을 세상 무엇보다도 두려워했고, 그래서 아들에 관련된 모든 것을 그에게 숨겼다. 온종일 집에서 지내면서 그녀는 아들을 볼 방법을 궁리했고, 남편에게 편지를 쓰기로 결심하기에 이르렀다. 그녀가 막 이 편지를 작성하고 있을 때 리디아 이바노브나에게서 온 편지가 그녀에게 건네졌다. 백작 부인의 침묵은 그녀를 위축시키고 의기소침하게 만들었지만, 그 편지가, 그녀가 그 행간에서 읽어 낸 모든 것이 그녀를 어찌나 격분시켰는지, 아들을 향한 그녀의 열렬하고 정당한 애정 곁에서 이 악의가 어찌나 역겨웠는지, 그녀는 다른 사람들에게 등을 돌리고 자기 자신을 탓하기를 그만두었다.\n\n\"이 냉담함—이 감정의 가식이라니!\" 그녀는 혼자 중얼거렸다. \"저들은 굳이 나를 모욕하고 아이를 괴롭혀야겠고, 나는 거기에 굴복해야 한단 말이지! 무슨 일이 있어도 안 돼! 저 여자가 나보다 더 못된 인간이야. 적어도 나는 거짓말은 하지 않잖아.\" 그리고 그녀는 그 자리에서 결심했다. 다음 날, 세료자의 생일에, 곧장 남편의 집으로 가서, 하인들을 매수하든 속이든, 어떤 대가를 치르더라도 아들을 만나, 사람들이 그 불행한 아이를 옭아매고 있는 그 끔찍한 기만을 뒤엎어 버리겠노라고.\n\n그녀는 장난감 가게로 가서 장난감을 사고 행동 계획을 곰곰이 짰다. 그녀는 아침 일찍 여덟 시에 갈 작정이었다. 그 시각이면 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 틀림없이 일어나지 않았을 테니까. 그녀는 손에 돈을 쥐고 현관지기와 하인에게 쥐여 주어 자기를 들여보내게 할 것이고, 베일을 올리지 않은 채, 자기는 세료자의 대부가 보내서 축하 인사를 전하러 왔으며, 장난감을 그의 머리맡에 두고 가도록 부탁받았다고 말할 작정이었다. 그녀는 아들에게 해야 할 말만 빼고 모든 것을 준비해 두었다. 그토록 자주 그것을 꿈꿔 왔건만, 그녀는 아무 말도 떠올릴 수가 없었다.\n\n다음 날 아침 여덟 시, 안나는 삯썰매에서 내려 옛집의 정문 초인종을 눌렀다.\n\n\"가서 무슨 일인지 보고 와. 어떤 부인이야.\" 아직 옷도 다 입지 않고 외투에 덧신을 신은 채 창밖을 내다보다 문에 바짝 붙어 선 베일 쓴 부인을 본 카피토니치가 말했다. 그의 조수, 안나가 모르는 한 소년이 그녀에게 문을 열어 주기가 무섭게 그녀는 안으로 들어와, 토시에서 삼 루블짜리 지폐 한 장을 꺼내 그의 손에 급히 쥐여 주었다.\n\n\"세료자—세르게이 알렉세이치.\" 그녀가 말하고 계속 걸어가려 했다. 지폐를 찬찬히 들여다보던 현관지기 조수가 두 번째 유리문 앞에서 그녀를 멈춰 세웠다.\n\n\"누굴 찾으십니까?\" 그가 물었다.\n\n그녀는 그의 말을 듣지 못했고 아무 대답도 하지 않았다.\n\n낯선 부인의 당혹스러운 기색을 알아챈 카피토니치가 그녀에게 다가가 두 번째 문을 열어 주고는 무슨 용무인지 물었다.\n\n\"스코로두모프 공작이 보내서 세르게이 알렉세이치에게 왔어요.\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"도련님께서는 아직 안 일어나셨습니다.\" 현관지기가 그녀를 유심히 바라보며 말했다.\n\n안나는 자기가 구 년을 살았던 이 집의 조금도 변하지 않은 현관이 자기를 이토록 크게 흔들어 놓으리라고는 예상하지 못했다. 달콤하면서도 아픈 기억들이 그녀의 가슴속에서 잇따라 솟아올랐고, 한순간 그녀는 자기가 여기에 무엇 때문에 왔는지조차 잊어버렸다.\n\n\"잠시 기다려 주시겠습니까?\" 카피토니치가 그녀의 모피 외투를 벗겨 주며 말했다.\n\n외투를 벗겨 주면서 카피토니치는 그녀의 얼굴을 흘끗 보고 그녀를 알아보았으며, 말없이 그녀에게 깊이 허리를 굽혀 인사했다.\n\n\"안으로 드시지요, 마님.\" 그가 그녀에게 말했다.\n\n그녀는 뭔가 말하려 했지만, 목소리가 어떤 소리도 내기를 거부했다. 죄지은 듯 애원하는 눈길로 노인을 바라보며 그녀는 가볍고 빠른 걸음으로 계단을 올라갔다. 몸을 한껏 굽힌 채, 덧신이 계단에 걸리는 것을 무릅쓰고 카피토니치가 그녀를 따라잡으려 애쓰며 그녀 뒤를 달려 올라갔다.\n\n\"가정교사가 와 있습니다. 아직 옷을 안 입었을지도 모르고요. 제가 알려 드리겠습니다.\"\n\n안나는 노인이 무슨 말을 하는지 알아듣지도 못한 채 여전히 낯익은 계단을 올라갔다.\n\n\"이쪽으로, 왼쪽으로 가시지요. 어수선해서 송구합니다. 도련님께서는 지금 옛날 응접실에 계십니다.\" 현관지기가 숨을 헐떡이며 말했다. \"송구합니다만, 잠시만 기다려 주십시오, 마님. 제가 얼른 보고 오겠습니다.\" 그가 말하고는 그녀를 앞질러 가서 높다란 문을 열고 그 뒤로 사라졌다. 안나는 가만히 서서 기다렸다. \"이제 막 깨셨습니다.\" 현관지기가 나오며 말했다. 그리고 현관지기가 그렇게 말하는 바로 그 순간, 안나는 어린아이가 하품하는 소리를 들었다. 그 하품 소리 하나만으로도 그녀는 아들을 알아보았고, 마치 눈앞에 살아 있는 아들을 보는 것 같았다.\n\n\"들여보내 줘요. 어서 비켜요!\" 그녀가 말하고는 높다란 문을 통해 안으로 들어갔다. 문 오른편에는 침대가 놓여 있었고, 그 침대에 일어나 앉아 있는 것은 아이였다. 잠옷 단추를 풀어 헤친 채 작은 몸을 앞으로 굽히고서 그 애는 기지개를 켜며 여전히 하품을 하고 있었다. 입술이 다물리는 순간 그 입술은 더없이 행복한 졸음에 겨운 미소로 휘어졌고, 그 미소를 띤 채 그 애는 느릿느릿 흐뭇하게 다시 뒤로 뒹굴어 누웠다.\n\n\"세료자!\" 그녀는 소리 없이 그 애에게 다가가며 속삭였다.\n\n그 애와 떨어져 지내는 동안, 그리고 그 애를 향한 새로운 사랑의 물결을 느껴 온 요 근래 내내, 그녀는 그 애를 자기가 가장 사랑했던 네 살 때의 모습으로 그려 왔다. 이제 그 애는 그녀가 떠날 때의 모습조차 아니었다. 네 살배기 아기로부터 더 멀어졌고, 더 자라고 더 야위었다. 얼굴은 어찌나 야위었는지, 머리카락은 어찌나 짧은지! 손은 어찌나 긴지! 자기가 떠난 뒤로 그 애는 어찌나 변했는지! 하지만 그 애는 그 머리, 그 입술, 그 보드라운 목, 작고 넓적한 어깨를 한 바로 그 애였다.\n\n\"세료자!\" 그녀는 아이의 귓가에 대고 거듭 불렀다.\n\n그 애는 다시 팔꿈치로 몸을 일으키고는 뭔가를 찾기라도 하듯 헝클어진 머리를 이리저리 돌리다가 눈을 떴다. 느릿느릿 의아한 눈빛으로 그 애는 자기 앞에 꼼짝 않고 서 있는 어머니를 몇 초 동안 바라보다가, 별안간 더없이 행복한 미소를 짓더니, 눈을 감으며 뒤로가 아니라 그녀 쪽으로, 그녀의 품 안으로 뒹굴어 들었다.\n\n\"세료자! 내 사랑하는 아가!\" 그녀가 가쁜 숨을 몰아쉬며 그 애의 통통한 작은 몸을 두 팔로 끌어안고 말했다. \"엄마!\" 그 애가 그녀의 품 안에서 꼼지락거리며, 자기 몸 여기저기를 그녀의 두 손에 닿게 하면서 말했다.\n\n여전히 눈을 감은 채 졸음에 겨운 미소를 지으며, 그 애는 통통한 작은 두 팔로 그녀의 어깨를 감싸 안고, 오직 아이에게서만 풍기는 달콤하고 졸음에 겨운 온기와 향내와 더불어 그녀 쪽으로 뒹굴어 와서는, 그녀의 목과 어깨에 얼굴을 비비기 시작했다.\n\n\"나 알아요.\" 그 애가 눈을 뜨며 말했다. \"오늘은 내 생일이에요. 엄마가 올 줄 알았어요. 나 금방 일어날게요.\"\n\n그러고는 그렇게 말하면서 다시 잠이 들었다.\n\n안나는 갈망에 차서 그 애를 바라보았다. 그녀는 자기가 없는 사이에 그 애가 어떻게 자라고 변했는지를 보았다. 이불 밑으로 쑥 나온, 이제는 그토록 긴 맨다리, 자기가 그토록 자주 입맞추던 그 목덜미의 짧게 깎은 곱슬머리를 그녀는 알면서도 알 수 없었다. 그녀는 이 모든 것을 어루만졌지만 아무 말도 할 수 없었다. 눈물이 그녀의 목을 메이게 했다.\n\n\"엄마, 왜 울어요?\" 그 애가 완전히 잠에서 깨며 말했다. \"엄마, 왜 우는 거예요?\" 그 애가 울먹이는 목소리로 외쳤다.\n\n\"안 울게……. 기뻐서 우는 거야. 너를 본 지가 너무 오래돼서. 안 울게, 안 울게.\" 그녀가 눈물을 꿀꺽 삼키며 고개를 돌리고 말했다. \"자, 이제 옷 입을 시간이구나.\" 그녀가 잠시 뒤 덧붙이고는, 그 애의 두 손을 결코 놓지 않은 채, 그 애의 옷이 채비되어 놓인 침대맡 의자에 앉았다.\n\n\"넌 나 없이 어떻게 옷을 입니? 어떻게…….\" 그녀는 단순하고 명랑하게 이야기를 시작하려 했지만 그럴 수가 없었고, 또다시 고개를 돌렸다.\n\n\"나 찬물 목욕은 안 해요, 아빠가 시키지 않았거든요. 그런데 엄마, 바실리 루키치 못 봤어요? 곧 올 거예요. 어, 엄마 내 옷 깔고 앉았어요!\"\n\n그러고는 세료자가 까르르 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 그녀는 그 애를 바라보며 미소 지었다.\n\n\"엄마, 사랑하는 엄마, 다정한 엄마!\" 그 애가 다시 그녀에게 몸을 던지며 끌어안고 소리쳤다. 마치 이제야, 그녀가 미소 짓는 것을 보고서야, 무슨 일이 일어났는지를 온전히 깨달은 것 같았다.\n\n\"이건 쓰기 싫어요.\" 그 애가 그녀의 모자를 벗기며 말했다. 그러고는 모자를 벗은 그녀를 새삼 다시 보기라도 하듯, 다시 그녀에게 입을 맞추기 시작했다.\n\n\"그런데 엄마는 나에 대해 무슨 생각을 했어요? 내가 죽었다고는 생각 안 했죠?\"\n\n\"난 절대로 그렇게 믿지 않았어요.\"\n\n\"안 믿었구나, 내 사랑?\"\n\n\"난 알았어요, 알았다고요!\" 그 애가 자기가 좋아하는 말을 되풀이하며, 자기 머리카락을 쓰다듬던 손을 와락 붙잡아 펼친 손바닥을 자기 입에 갖다 대고 입을 맞추었다.\n\n# Chapter 30\n\n그러는 동안 바실리 루키치는 처음에는 이 부인이 누구인지 몰랐다가, 두 사람의 대화를 듣고서 그녀가 다름 아닌, 남편을 버리고 떠난 어머니라는 것을 알게 되었다. 그녀가 떠난 뒤에 이 집에 들어온 그는 한 번도 그녀를 본 적이 없었다. 그는 안으로 들어가야 할지 말아야 할지, 아니면 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에게 알려야 할지 망설였다. 마침내 자신의 임무는 정해진 시각에 세료자를 깨우는 것이며, 따라서 거기 있는 사람이 어머니인지 다른 누구인지를 따지는 것은 자기 일이 아니고, 그저 자기 직분을 다하면 된다고 생각한 그는 옷차림을 마치고 문으로 가서 문을 열었다.\n\n그러나 어머니와 아이가 끌어안는 모습이며, 그들의 목소리며, 그들이 나누는 말은 그의 마음을 돌려놓았다.\n\n그는 고개를 가로젓고는 한숨을 내쉬며 문을 닫았다. \"십 분만 더 기다리자.\" 그는 헛기침을 하고 눈물을 닦으며 혼잣말을 했다.\n\n그동안 집 안의 하인들 사이에서는 격렬한 동요가 일고 있었다. 모두들 마님이 오셨고, 카피토니치가 그분을 들여보냈으며, 지금 그분이 육아실에 계시다는 것을 알고 있었다. 그리고 주인은 늘 아홉 시에 직접 육아실로 들어간다는 것, 따라서 부부가 마주치는 것은 있을 수 없는 일이며 그것을 반드시 막아야 한다는 것을 모두 똑똑히 알고 있었다. 시종인 코르네이는 문지기 방으로 내려가, 누가 마님을 들여보냈는지, 어쩌다 그런 일을 했는지 캐물었고, 카피토니치가 그녀를 들이고 위층으로 안내했다는 것을 알아내고는 노인을 호되게 나무랐다. 문지기는 고집스레 입을 다물고 있었지만, 코르네이가 너 같은 놈은 쫓겨나야 마땅하다고 말하자 카피토니치가 그에게 달려들어 코르네이의 얼굴에다 손을 휘저으며 떠들기 시작했다.\n\n\"오, 그래, 너 같으면 그분을 안 들여보냈겠지! 십 년을 모셨는데, 그동안 따뜻한 말씀밖에 들은 적 없는 그분께, 너라면 떡하니 나서서 '꺼져, 가, 저리 비켜!' 하고 말했겠지! 오, 그래, 너야말로 처세에 약삭빠른 놈이지! 주인을 등쳐 먹고 모피 외투를 슬쩍하는 법쯤은 누가 가르쳐 줄 것도 없는 놈이고!\"\n\n\"군바리 같으니!\" 코르네이가 경멸하듯 내뱉고는, 마침 들어오던 유모를 돌아보았다. \"마리야 예피모브나, 어떻게 생각하시오, 이자가 아무에게도 한마디 없이 그분을 들여보냈단 말이오.\" 코르네이가 그녀에게 말했다. \"알렉세이 알렉산드로비치께서 곧 내려오셔서 육아실로 들어가실 텐데!\"\n\n\"이거 큰일이네, 큰일이야!\" 유모가 말했다. \"코르네이 바실리예비치, 당신은 어떻게든 주인 나리를 좀 붙들어 두구려. 그동안 나는 달려가서 그분을 어떻게든 빼내야겠소. 이거 정말 큰일이네!\"\n\n유모가 육아실로 들어갔을 때, 세료자는 어머니에게 자기와 나딘카가 썰매를 타고 비탈을 내려오다가 넘어져서 세 번이나 굴렀다는 이야기를 하고 있었다. 그녀는 아들의 목소리에 귀를 기울이고, 그 얼굴과 거기 떠오르는 표정의 움직임을 바라보며, 아들의 손을 만지고 있었지만, 아이가 무슨 말을 하는지는 따라가지 못했다. 가야 한다, 아이를 두고 떠나야 한다—그녀가 생각하고 느끼는 것은 오직 이것뿐이었다. 그녀는 바실리 루키치가 문 앞으로 다가와 기침하는 발소리를 들었고, 가까이 다가오는 유모의 발소리도 들었지만, 돌처럼 굳어 앉은 채 말을 꺼내지도, 일어서지도 못했다.\n\n\"마님, 우리 마님!\" 유모가 안나에게 다가가 그녀의 두 손과 어깨에 입을 맞추며 입을 열었다. \"하느님께서 우리 도련님 생신에 정말로 기쁨을 보내 주셨네요. 마님은 조금도 변하지 않으셨어요.\"\n\n\"아, 유모, 이 집에 와 있는 줄 몰랐어요.\" 안나가 잠시 정신을 차리며 말했다.\n\n\"저는 여기 살지 않아요, 딸과 함께 지내요. 생신이라 온 거랍니다, 안나 아르카지예브나, 우리 마님!\"\n\n유모는 갑자기 울음을 터뜨리며 다시 그녀의 손에 입을 맞추기 시작했다.\n\n세료자는 눈을 반짝이며 환하게 웃었고, 한 손으로는 어머니를, 다른 한 손으로는 유모를 붙잡은 채 통통한 작은 맨발로 양탄자를 동동 굴렀다. 사랑하는 유모가 어머니에게 보이는 다정함에 아이는 황홀경에 빠졌다.\n\n\"엄마! 유모는 자주 나를 보러 와요, 그리고 올 때마다……\" 아이는 말을 꺼내다가, 유모가 어머니에게 무언가 속삭이고 있고, 어머니의 얼굴에 그녀에게는 이상하리만치 어울리지 않는, 두려움과 부끄러움 비슷한 빛이 떠오른 것을 알아채고는 입을 다물었다.\n\n그녀는 아이에게 다가갔다.\n\n\"내 사랑!\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n그녀는 작별 인사를 할 수 없었지만, 그녀의 얼굴 표정이 그것을 말하고 있었고, 아이는 알아챘다. \"사랑하는, 사랑하는 쿠치크!\" 그녀는 아이가 어렸을 때 부르던 이름으로 불렀다. \"엄마를 잊지 않을 거지? 너는……\" 그러나 그녀는 더 말을 잇지 못했다.\n\n나중에 그녀는 자기가 했을 수도 있었던 말들을 얼마나 자주 떠올렸던가. 그러나 지금은 어떻게 말해야 할지 몰랐고, 아무 말도 할 수 없었다. 하지만 세료자는 그녀가 자기에게 하고 싶어 하는 말을 모두 알고 있었다. 그는 어머니가 불행하다는 것, 그리고 자기를 사랑한다는 것을 알았다. 그는 유모가 속삭인 말까지도 알아들었다. \"늘 아홉 시에\"라는 말을 들었고, 그것이 아버지에 관한 이야기이며, 아버지와 어머니가 마주칠 수 없다는 뜻임을 알았다. 그것은 이해했지만, 한 가지만은 이해할 수 없었다—어째서 어머니의 얼굴에 두려움과 부끄러움의 빛이 떠올라야 하는 걸까?…… 어머니는 잘못한 게 없는데, 그를 두려워하고 무언가를 부끄러워하고 있었다. 그는 이 의문을 풀어 줄 질문을 던지고 싶었지만, 차마 그러지 못했다. 어머니가 괴로워하는 것을 본 그는 어머니가 가여웠다. 그는 말없이 어머니에게 바싹 붙어 속삭였다. \"아직 가지 마요. 아버지는 아직 안 오실 거예요.\"\n\n어머니는 아이가 무슨 생각을 하는지, 아이에게 무슨 말을 해야 할지 살피려고 아이를 떼어 놓고 바라보았다. 그리고 겁에 질린 아이의 얼굴에서, 아이가 아버지 이야기를 하고 있다는 것뿐만 아니라, 말하자면 아버지에 대해 어떻게 생각해야 하느냐고 자기에게 묻고 있다는 것을 읽어 냈다.\n\n\"세료자, 내 사랑.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"아버지를 사랑하렴. 아버지는 나보다 더 훌륭하고 더 다정한 분이야, 그리고 내가 아버지에게 잘못을 저질렀어. 네가 자라면 판단하게 될 거야.\"\n\n\"엄마보다 더 나은 사람은 없어요!……\" 아이가 눈물을 흘리며 절망에 차 소리쳤고, 어머니의 어깨를 움켜쥐고 온 힘을 다해 자기 쪽으로 끌어안기 시작했다. 그 안간힘에 아이의 두 팔이 떨렸다.\n\n\"내 사랑, 내 어린 것!\" 안나가 말했다. 그리고 그녀는 아이만큼이나 가냘프고 어린아이처럼 울었다.\n\n그 순간 문이 열렸다. 바실리 루키치가 들어왔다.\n\n다른 쪽 문에서도 발소리가 들렸고, 유모가 겁에 질린 목소리로 속삭였다. \"나리가 오세요.\" 그러고는 안나에게 모자를 건넸다.\n\n세료자는 침대 위에 쓰러져 두 손에 얼굴을 묻고 흐느꼈다. 안나는 아이의 손을 떼어 내고, 눈물에 젖은 그 얼굴에 다시 한번 입을 맞춘 뒤, 빠른 걸음으로 문으로 향했다. 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 그녀와 마주치며 걸어 들어왔다. 그녀를 보자 그는 우뚝 멈춰 서서 고개를 숙였다.\n\n조금 전에 그가 자기보다 더 훌륭하고 더 다정한 사람이라고 말했음에도, 그에게 빠르게 던진 시선으로 그의 모습 전체를 구석구석 훑어보는 사이, 그에 대한 혐오와 증오의 감정, 그리고 아들을 두고 느끼는 질투가 그녀를 사로잡았다. 그녀는 재빠른 손짓으로 베일을 내리고는 걸음을 재촉하여 거의 뛰다시피 방을 나섰다.\n\n그녀는 미처 풀어 보지도 못한 채, 전날 장난감 가게에서 그토록 큰 사랑과 슬픔으로 골랐던 장난감 꾸러미를 그대로 도로 들고 나왔다.\n\n# Chapter 31\n\n아들을 보고 싶다고 그토록 간절히 바라 왔고, 그토록 오랫동안 그 일을 생각하며 마음의 준비를 해 왔건만, 안나는 아들을 보는 일이 자신에게 그토록 깊은 영향을 끼치리라고는 조금도 예상하지 못했다. 호텔의 적막한 방으로 돌아온 그녀는 자기가 왜 거기에 있는지 한참 동안 알 수가 없었다. \"그래, 다 끝났어. 나는 또다시 혼자야.\" 그녀는 혼잣말을 하고는 모자도 벗지 않은 채 벽난로 옆의 낮은 안락의자에 앉았다. 창문 사이의 탁자 위에 놓인 청동 시계에 눈길을 고정한 채, 그녀는 생각을 가다듬으려 애썼다.\n\n외국에서 데려온 프랑스인 하녀가 옷을 갈아입으시라고 권하러 들어왔다. 그녀는 하녀를 이상하다는 듯 바라보며 \"이따가.\"라고 말했다. 하인이 커피를 내왔다. \"나중에.\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n이탈리아인 유모는 아기에게 제일 좋은 옷을 입혀 데리고 나갔다가, 다시 안고 들어와 안나에게 데려왔다. 토실토실하고 잘 먹어 통통한 어린 아기는 늘 그러듯 엄마를 보자 살진 작은 손을 내밀고는, 이가 나지 않은 입에 미소를 띤 채, 마치 찌가 달린 물고기처럼 자수 놓인 치마의 풀 먹인 주름을 따라 손가락을 위아래로 까딱이며 사락사락 소리를 냈다. 미소 짓지 않을 수 없었고, 아기에게 입 맞추지 않을 수 없었으며, 아기가 까르륵거리며 온몸을 들썩이게 손가락을 내밀어 쥐게 하지 않을 수 없었다. 또 입맞춤이랍시고 아기가 작은 입으로 쪽쪽 빠는 입술을 내밀지 않을 수 없었다. 안나는 이 모든 것을 다 해 주었고, 아기를 품에 안아 어르며 춤추게 했고, 싱싱한 작은 뺨과 드러난 작은 팔꿈치에 입을 맞췄다. 그러나 이 아이를 보고 있으려니, 자기가 이 아이에게 느끼는 감정이 세료자에게 느끼는 감정에 견주면 사랑이라 부를 수 없는 것임이 그 어느 때보다 분명해졌다. 이 아기는 모든 것이 사랑스러웠지만, 어쩐 일인지 그 모든 것이 그녀의 마음 깊은 곳까지 닿지는 않았다. 비록 사랑하지 않는 남자의 아이였지만, 그녀의 첫아이에게는 만족을 찾지 못했던 모든 사랑이 집중되어 있었다. 어린 딸은 더없이 고통스러운 정황 속에서 태어났고, 첫아이에게 쏟았던 보살핌과 마음의 백분의 일도 받지 못했다. 게다가 이 어린 딸에게는 모든 것이 아직 미래의 일이었지만, 세료자는 이제 거의 하나의 인격체였고, 더없이 사랑하는 인격체였다. 그 아이 안에는 생각과 감정의 갈등이 있었다. 그 아이는 그녀를 이해했고, 그녀를 사랑했고, 그녀를 판단했다고, 그녀는 아이의 말과 눈빛을 떠올리며 생각했다. 그런데 그녀는 그 아이와 영영—육체적으로뿐 아니라 정신적으로도—갈라져 버렸고, 이것은 바로잡을 길이 없었다.\n\n그녀는 아기를 유모에게 돌려주고 내보낸 다음, 세료자가 지금 그 어린 딸과 거의 같은 나이였을 때의 초상이 들어 있는 로켓을 열었다. 그녀는 일어나 모자를 벗고, 작은 탁자에서 아들의 여러 시절 사진이 담긴 앨범을 집어 들었다. 그것들을 비교해 보고 싶어서 그녀는 앨범에서 사진들을 빼내기 시작했다. 그녀는 단 한 장, 가장 최근이자 가장 잘 나온 사진만 빼고 모두 빼냈다. 그 사진 속에서 아이는 하얀 작업복을 입고 의자에 걸터앉아, 미간을 찌푸린 눈에 입가에는 미소를 띠고 있었다. 그것은 아이의 가장 잘 나온, 가장 그 아이다운 표정이었다. 오늘따라 유난히 강렬하게 움직이는 작고 부드러운 손, 희고 가녀린 손가락으로 그녀는 사진 한쪽 귀퉁이를 잡아당겼지만, 사진이 어딘가에 걸려 빠지지 않았다. 탁자 위에는 종이칼이 없었으므로, 그녀는 아들 사진 옆에 있던 사진을(둥근 모자를 쓰고 긴 머리를 한 채 로마에서 찍은 브론스키의 사진이었다) 빼내어 그것으로 아들 사진을 밀어냈다. \"어머, 여기 이이가 있네!\" 그녀는 브론스키의 초상을 흘끗 보며 말했다. 그러자 문득 그가 지금 자기 불행의 원인이라는 사실이 떠올랐다. 그녀는 아침 내내 그를 단 한 번도 생각하지 않았던 것이다. 그러나 이제, 그토록 낯익고 그토록 소중한 그 남자답고 고귀한 얼굴과 별안간 마주치자, 그녀는 그를 향한 사랑이 갑자기 솟구치는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n\"그런데 그이는 어디 있지? 어떻게 내 불행 속에 나를 혼자 내버려 둘 수 있단 말이야?\" 그녀는 자기가 아들에 관한 모든 것을 그에게 숨겨 왔다는 사실은 잊은 채, 별안간 원망스러운 마음으로 생각했다. 그녀는 즉시 자기에게 와 달라고 사람을 보냈다. 두근거리는 가슴으로 그를 기다리며, 그에게 모든 것을 털어놓을 말과 그가 자기를 위로해 줄 사랑의 표현들을 속으로 되뇌었다. 심부름꾼은 그에게 손님이 와 있지만 곧 가겠다는 대답과 함께, 방금 페테르부르크에 도착한 야시빈 공작을 데려가도 되겠느냐고 묻는다는 전갈을 가지고 돌아왔다. \"혼자 오지 않는구나. 어제 저녁 식사 이후로 나를 보지 못했는데도,\" 그녀는 생각했다. \"내가 모든 걸 털어놓을 수 있게 오는 게 아니라 야시빈을 데리고 오는 거야.\" 그러자 별안간 이상한 생각이 떠올랐다. 만약 그가 나를 더 이상 사랑하지 않게 되었다면?\n\n지난 며칠간의 일들을 되짚어 보니, 모든 것에서 이 끔찍한 생각을 뒷받침하는 증거가 보이는 듯했다. 그가 어제 집에서 저녁을 먹지 않은 사실, 페테르부르크에서 따로 방을 나누어 쓰자고 고집한 사실, 그리고 지금조차 마치 얼굴을 맞대고 만나기를 피하려는 듯 혼자 오지 않는 사실까지도.\n\n\"하지만 그이는 내게 그렇다고 말해 줘야 해. 나는 그것이 사실인지 알아야 해. 그걸 안다면, 그땐 내가 어떻게 해야 할지 알 텐데.\" 그녀는 혼잣말을 했지만, 그가 자기를 마음에 두고 있지 않다는 것을 확신하게 된다면 자기가 어떤 처지에 놓일지는 도무지 상상조차 할 수 없었다. 그녀는 그가 자기를 더 이상 사랑하지 않게 되었다고 생각했고, 절망에 거의 다다른 기분이었으며, 그래서 더욱 유난히 신경이 곤두서 있었다. 그녀는 하녀를 부르러 종을 울리고 화장실로 갔다. 옷을 입으면서 그녀는 요 며칠 동안보다 더 정성스레 매무새를 매만졌다. 마치 그가 자기에게 식어 버렸더라도, 자기가 가장 어울리게 옷을 입고 머리를 매만진 덕분에 다시 자기를 사랑하게 되기라도 할 것처럼.\n\n채 준비를 끝내기도 전에 그녀는 초인종 소리를 들었다. 응접실로 들어갔을 때 그녀의 눈에 들어온 것은 그가 아니라 야시빈이었다. 브론스키는 그녀가 탁자 위에 놓아둔 채 잊고 있던 아들의 사진들을 들여다보고 있었고, 서둘러 그녀 쪽을 돌아보지 않았다.\n\n\"우리는 이미 만난 적이 있죠.\" 그녀는 야시빈의 거대한 손에 자기 작은 손을 얹으며 말했다. 그의 수줍음은 거대한 체구와 거친 얼굴과는 묘하게 어울리지 않았다. \"작년 경마장에서 만났어요. 그것들 이리 주세요.\" 그녀는 잽싼 동작으로 브론스키에게서 아들의 사진들을 낚아채며, 번뜩이는 눈으로 의미심장하게 그를 흘끗 보았다. \"올해 경마는 볼만했나요? 저는 그 대신 로마의 코르소에서 경마를 봤어요. 하지만 당신은 외국 생활을 좋아하지 않으시죠.\" 그녀는 다정한 미소를 지으며 말했다. \"당신을 거의 뵌 적도 없지만, 저는 당신과 당신 취향을 모두 알고 있답니다.\"\n\n\"그건 참으로 유감입니다. 제 취향이라는 게 대개 형편없거든요.\" 야시빈이 왼쪽 콧수염을 깨물며 말했다.\n\n잠시 이야기를 나누다가 브론스키가 시계를 흘끗 보는 것을 알아챈 야시빈은, 그녀에게 페테르부르크에 오래 머물 것이냐고 묻고는, 거대한 몸을 펴며 모자를 집으려 손을 뻗었다.\n\n\"오래 있지는 않을 것 같아요.\" 그녀는 브론스키를 흘끗 보며 망설이듯 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 우리는 다시 만나지 못하겠군요?\"\n\n\"저와 식사하러 오세요.\" 안나는 단호하게 말했다. 자기가 당황한 것에 화가 난 듯했지만, 처음 보는 사람 앞에서 자기 처지를 규정해야 할 때면 늘 그러듯 얼굴을 붉혔다. \"여기 음식은 별로지만, 적어도 그이는 만나 보실 수 있을 거예요. 알렉세이가 연대의 옛 친구들 중에서 당신만큼 아끼는 사람은 없답니다.\"\n\n\"기꺼이 가겠습니다.\" 야시빈이 미소를 지으며 말했고, 그 미소에서 브론스키는 그가 안나를 무척 마음에 들어 한다는 것을 알 수 있었다.\n\n야시빈은 작별 인사를 하고 떠났다. 브론스키는 남았다.\n\n\"당신도 가시나요?\" 그녀가 그에게 말했다.\n\n\"이미 늦었소.\" 그가 대답했다. \"먼저 가게! 곧 따라가겠네.\" 그가 야시빈에게 소리쳤다.\n\n그녀는 그의 손을 잡았고, 그를 붙잡아 둘 말을 찾느라 머릿속을 뒤지는 동안 그에게서 눈을 떼지 않은 채 그를 바라보았다.\n\n\"잠깐만요, 당신에게 하고 싶은 말이 있어요.\" 그러고는 그의 넓은 손을 잡아 자기 목에 가져다 댔다. \"아, 그이를 식사에 초대한 게 잘한 일이었나요?\"\n\n\"아주 잘했소.\" 그가 고른 이를 드러내는 평온한 미소를 지으며 말하고는 그녀의 손에 입을 맞췄다.\n\n\"알렉세이, 당신은 내게 변하지 않았죠?\" 그녀가 두 손으로 그의 손을 꼭 쥐며 말했다. \"알렉세이, 나는 여기서 비참해요. 우리는 언제 떠나죠?\"\n\n\"곧, 곧 떠나겠소. 여기서 우리가 사는 방식이 내게도 얼마나 불쾌한지 당신은 믿지 못할 거요.\" 그가 말하고는 손을 빼냈다.\n\n\"그럼, 가요, 가!\" 그녀가 모욕당한 듯한 어조로 말하고는 그에게서 빠르게 멀어져 갔다.\n\n# Chapter 32\n\n브론스키가 집에 돌아왔을 때, 안나는 아직 돌아오지 않았다. 그가 나간 직후에 어떤 부인이 안나를 찾아왔고, 안나는 그 부인과 함께 외출했다고 하인들이 일러 주었다. 어디로 가는지 한마디도 남기지 않고 나가 버린 것, 아직도 돌아오지 않은 것, 그리고 아침 내내 자기에게 아무 말 없이 어딘가를 쏘다닌 것—이 모든 것이, 아침에 그녀의 얼굴에 떠올랐던 묘하게 흥분한 기색이며, 야시빈 앞에서 아들의 사진을 그의 손에서 거의 낚아채다시피 했던 그 적대적인 어조에 대한 기억과 더불어 그를 심각하게 만들었다. 그는 반드시 그녀와 터놓고 이야기해야겠다고 마음먹었다. 그래서 그는 그녀의 응접실에서 그녀를 기다렸다. 그러나 안나는 혼자 돌아오지 않고, 결혼하지 않은 늙은 이모인 오블론스카야 공작 부인을 데리고 왔다. 이 부인이 바로 아침에 찾아왔던 그 부인이었고, 안나는 이 부인과 함께 물건을 사러 나갔던 것이다. 안나는 브론스키의 근심스럽고 캐묻는 듯한 표정을 알아채지 못한 듯, 아침에 물건을 사러 다닌 일을 활기차게 늘어놓기 시작했다. 그는 그녀 안에서 무언가가 작동하고 있음을 알아차렸다. 잠깐씩 그에게 머무는 그녀의 반짝이는 눈에는 강렬한 집중이 어려 있었고, 그녀의 말과 동작에는 두 사람이 막 가까워지던 무렵 그를 그토록 매혹시켰으나 이제는 그를 그토록 불안하게 하고 놀라게 하는 그 신경질적인 민첩함과 우아함이 깃들어 있었다.\n\n식탁은 네 사람 몫으로 차려졌다. 모두 한데 모여 막 작은 식당으로 들어가려는데, 투시케비치가 베시 공작 부인의 전갈을 들고 나타났다. 베시 공작 부인은 작별 인사를 하러 오지 못한 것을 용서해 달라고, 몸이 편치 않았다고 전하면서, 안나에게 여섯 시 반에서 아홉 시 사이에 자기에게 와 달라고 청했다. 브론스키는 아무도 마주치지 않게끔 조치가 취해졌음을 암시하는 그 정확한 시간 제한을 듣고 안나를 흘끗 보았다. 그러나 안나는 그것을 알아채지 못한 듯했다.\n\n\"하필 여섯 시 반에서 아홉 시 사이라니 갈 수 없어서 정말 유감이에요.\" 그녀가 희미하게 웃으며 말했다.\n\n\"공작 부인께서 무척 서운해하실 겁니다.\"\n\n\"저도 그래요.\"\n\n\"틀림없이 파티를 들으러 가시는 거겠죠?\" 투시케비치가 말했다.\n\n\"파티요? 덕분에 그 생각이 났네요. 특별석을 구할 수만 있다면 가고 싶어요.\"\n\n\"제가 구해 드릴 수 있습니다.\" 투시케비치가 자기 봉사를 자청했다.\n\n\"정말, 정말 감사하겠어요.\" 안나가 말했다. \"그런데 우리와 함께 식사하지 않으시겠어요?\"\n\n브론스키는 거의 알아챌 수 없을 만큼 어깨를 으쓱했다. 그는 안나가 무엇을 하려는 것인지 도무지 알 수 없었다. 무엇 때문에 늙은 오블론스카야 공작 부인을 집으로 데려왔으며, 무엇 때문에 투시케비치를 만찬에 붙들어 두었으며, 무엇보다도 놀라운 것은 어째서 그에게 특별석을 구해 오라고 보내는 것일까? 지금 그녀의 처지에 자기가 아는 사람들이 죄다 모일 파티의 자선 공연에 가려는 생각을 정말로 할 수 있단 말인가? 그는 심각한 눈으로 그녀를 바라보았으나, 그녀는 그가 그 뜻을 헤아릴 수 없는, 도전적이면서도 반쯤은 즐거워하고 반쯤은 절망적인 그 시선으로 응수했다. 식사하는 동안 안나는 공격적일 만큼 들떠 있었다. 그녀는 투시케비치에게도 야시빈에게도 거의 추파를 던지다시피 했다. 식탁에서 일어나 투시케비치가 오페라 극장으로 특별석을 구하러 가고, 야시빈은 담배를 피우러 가자, 브론스키는 그와 함께 자기 방으로 내려갔다. 그곳에 한참 앉아 있던 그는 위층으로 뛰어 올라갔다. 안나는 이미 파리에서 맞춘, 목둘레가 깊이 파인 가벼운 비단과 벨벳 드레스를 차려입고, 머리에는 값비싼 흰 레이스를 얹어 얼굴을 감싸도록 했는데, 그것이 유난히 잘 어울려 그녀의 눈부신 아름다움을 한껏 돋보이게 했다.\n\n\"정말 극장에 갈 거요?\" 그는 그녀를 보지 않으려 애쓰며 말했다.\n\n\"왜 그렇게 놀란 듯이 묻는 거예요?\" 그가 자기를 보지 않는 것에 또다시 상처를 입은 그녀가 말했다. \"내가 왜 가면 안 되나요?\"\n\n그녀는 그의 말에 담긴 의도를 이해하지 못하는 듯했다.\n\n\"아, 물론, 가면 안 될 이유야 전혀 없지.\" 그가 미간을 찌푸리며 말했다.\n\n\"그게 바로 내가 하는 말이잖아요.\" 그녀는 그의 어조에 담긴 빈정거림을 짐짓 못 본 체하며, 길고 향내 나는 장갑을 조용히 뒤집어 끼면서 말했다.\n\n\"안나, 제발! 도대체 당신 왜 이러는 거요?\" 그는 언젠가 그녀의 남편이 했던 것과 똑같이 그녀에게 호소했다.\n\n\"무엇을 묻는 건지 모르겠어요.\"\n\n\"극장에 가는 건 말도 안 된다는 걸 당신도 알지 않소.\"\n\n\"왜요? 혼자 가는 것도 아닌데. 바르바라 공작 부인이 옷을 갈아입으러 가셨어요, 나랑 같이 갈 거예요.\"\n\n그는 당혹스럽고 절망스러운 기색으로 어깨를 으쓱했다.\n\n\"하지만 정말 당신은 모른단 말이오?……\" 그가 입을 열었다.\n\n\"하지만 난 알고 싶지도 않아요!\" 그녀가 거의 비명을 지르듯 말했다. \"알고 싶지 않다고요. 내가 한 일을 후회하느냐고요? 아니요, 아니요, 아니요! 처음부터 다시 모든 걸 되풀이한다 해도 똑같을 거예요. 우리에게, 당신과 나에게 중요한 건 단 하나, 우리가 서로를 사랑하느냐 하는 것뿐이에요. 다른 사람들은 신경 쓸 필요도 없어요. 우리가 왜 이렇게 떨어져서, 서로 만나지도 못하고 살아야 하죠? 내가 왜 가면 안 되나요? 난 당신을 사랑해요, 그러니 다른 건 아무래도 좋아요.\" 그녀는 러시아어로 말하며, 그가 이해할 수 없는 묘한 빛을 띤 눈으로 그를 흘끗 바라보았다. \"당신이 나에 대한 마음이 변하지 않았다면, 왜 나를 보지 않는 거죠?\"\n\n그는 그녀를 바라보았다. 늘 그녀에게 그토록 잘 어울리는 그 얼굴과 차림새의 아름다움을 그는 빠짐없이 보았다. 그러나 지금은 바로 그 아름다움과 우아함이 그를 짜증나게 했다.\n\n\"내 감정은 변할 수 없소, 당신도 알잖소, 하지만 부탁이오, 간청하오.\" 그는 다시 프랑스어로, 목소리에는 부드러운 애원의 음색을 담았으나 눈에는 차가움을 띤 채 말했다.\n\n그녀는 그의 말을 듣지 않았지만, 그의 눈에 어린 차가움은 보았고, 짜증스럽게 대답했다.\n\n\"그럼 나도 부탁하는데, 내가 왜 가면 안 되는지 설명해 줘요.\"\n\n\"그게 당신에게…… 화를 부를 수도 있으니까.\" 그가 머뭇거렸다.\n\n\"무슨 말인지 모르겠어요. 야시빈은 n'est pas compromettant이고, 바르바라 공작 부인도 다른 사람들보다 나쁠 게 없잖아요. 아, 마침 저기 오시네요!\"\n* n'est pas compromettant: 프랑스어로 '체면을 손상시킬 만한 사람이 아니다'라는 뜻.\n\n# Chapter 33\n\n브론스키는 난생처음 안나를 향한 분노의 감정을, 자기 처지를 한사코 이해하려 들지 않는 그녀에 대한 거의 증오에 가까운 감정을 느꼈다. 이 감정은 분노의 까닭을 그녀에게 솔직히 말할 수 없다는 사실 때문에 한층 더 격해졌다. 만일 그가 자기 생각을 곧이곧대로 말했더라면, 그는 이렇게 말했을 것이다.\n\n\"그런 옷차림에, 누구나 너무도 잘 아는 공작 부인과 함께 극장에 모습을 드러내는 것은 타락한 여자라는 당신의 처지를 인정하는 것일 뿐만 아니라, 사교계에 도전장을 던지는 것이고, 다시 말해 사교계와 영영 인연을 끊어 버리는 것이야.\"\n\n그는 그 말을 그녀에게 할 수 없었다. \"하지만 어떻게 그걸 못 본단 말인가, 대체 그녀 안에서 무슨 일이 일어나고 있는 거지?\" 그는 혼잣말을 했다. 그러면서 그는 그녀에 대한 존경심은 줄어드는 반면, 그녀의 아름다움에 대한 느낌은 더욱 강렬해지고 있음을 느꼈다.\n\n그는 잔뜩 인상을 쓴 채 자기 방으로 돌아왔고, 긴 다리를 의자 위에 쭉 뻗고 브랜디에 셀처를 타 마시고 있던 야시빈 옆에 앉아, 자기에게도 똑같은 것을 한 잔 가져오라고 시켰다.\n\n\"란콥스키의 모구치 얘길 했지. 그거 좋은 말이야, 그러니 자네한테 사라고 권하고 싶네.\" 야시빈이 친구의 침울한 얼굴을 흘끗 보며 말했다. \"엉덩이 쪽은 그리 일류급은 아니지만, 다리와 머리는—더 바랄 게 없을 정도라네.\"\n\n\"그놈을 사 둘까 하네.\" 브론스키가 대답했다.\n\n말에 관한 그들의 대화가 그의 흥미를 끌었지만, 그는 한순간도 안나를 잊지 못했고, 복도에서 들려오는 발소리에 귀를 기울이고 벽난로 위의 시계를 바라보지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n\"안나 아르카지예브나께서 극장에 가셨다고 전하라 분부하셨습니다.\"\n\n야시빈은 거품이 이는 물에 브랜디를 한 잔 더 따라 들이켠 뒤 일어서서 외투의 단추를 채웠다.\n\n\"자, 가세.\" 그가 콧수염 아래로 희미하게 웃으며 말했는데, 그 미소로 그는 브론스키의 침울함의 까닭을 알고 있으며 그것에 별다른 의미를 두지 않는다는 것을 내비쳤다.\n\n\"난 안 가겠네.\" 브론스키가 침울하게 대답했다.\n\n\"음, 나는 가야겠네, 약속을 했으니까. 그럼 잘 있게. 만약 자네가 오거든 일층 좌석으로 오게. 크루진의 자리를 써도 되니까.\" 야시빈은 나가면서 덧붙였다.\n\n\"아니, 난 바쁘네.\"\n\n'아내는 골칫거리지만, 아내가 아닐 땐 더 골치 아픈 법이지.' 야시빈은 호텔을 나서면서 생각했다.\n\n홀로 남은 브론스키는 의자에서 일어나 방 안을 이리저리 거닐기 시작했다.\n\n'그래, 오늘이 며칠째지? 나흘째 밤이군……. 예고르와 그 아내가 와 있을 테고, 어머니도 십중팔구 와 계실 테지. 물론 페테르부르크 사람들이 죄다 와 있을 테고. 지금쯤 그녀는 안으로 들어가 외투를 벗고 불빛 속에 모습을 드러냈겠지. 투시케비치, 야시빈, 바르바라 공작 부인.' 그는 그 모습을 머릿속에 그려 보았다……. '그럼 나는? 내가 겁을 먹었거나, 아니면 그녀를 보호할 권리를 투시케비치에게 넘겨 버렸다는 건가? 어느 모로 보나—멍청하다, 멍청해!…… 대체 왜 그녀는 나를 이런 처지로 몰아넣는 거지?' 그는 절망적인 몸짓을 하며 말했다.\n\n그 몸짓을 하다가 그는 셀처와 브랜디 술병이 놓여 있는 탁자를 쳐서 거의 엎을 뻔했다. 그는 그것을 붙잡으려다 놓쳐 버렸고, 화가 나서 탁자를 발로 차 넘어뜨리고는 벨을 울렸다.\n\n\"내 밑에서 일할 마음이 있거든,\" 그는 들어온 시종에게 말했다, \"자네 할 일이나 똑똑히 기억하는 게 좋을 거야. 이게 여기 있어서는 안 되지. 진작 치웠어야 했어.\"\n\n시종은 자기에게 잘못이 없음을 알기에 변명을 하려 했지만, 주인을 흘끗 보고는 그 얼굴에서 이럴 땐 입을 다무는 수밖에 없음을 알아채고, 부랴부랴 이리저리 몸을 놀려 양탄자에 주저앉아 깨진 잔과 멀쩡한 잔이며 병들을 주워 모으기 시작했다.\n\n\"그건 자네 일이 아니네. 웨이터를 불러 치우게 하고, 내 연미복이나 꺼내 와.\"\n\n브론스키는 여덟 시 반에 극장으로 들어섰다. 공연은 한창 무르익고 있었다. 자그마한 노인 안내원은 브론스키가 모피 외투 벗는 것을 거들면서 그를 알아보고는 \"각하\"라고 부르며, 표를 받지 말고 그냥 표도르를 부르시라고 권했다. 환하게 밝혀진 복도에는 안내원과, 모피 외투를 팔에 걸친 채 문에 귀를 대고 있는 두 명의 종업원 말고는 아무도 없었다. 닫힌 문 너머로 절제된 스타카토의 오케스트라 반주 소리가 들려왔고, 여자 목소리 하나가 어떤 악구를 또렷하게 부르고 있었다. 안내원이 빠져나가도록 문이 열렸고, 끝을 향해 가던 악구가 브론스키의 귀에 또렷이 들려왔다. 그러나 문은 곧바로 다시 닫혔고, 브론스키는 악구의 끝과 반주의 종지부를 듣지 못했지만, 우레 같은 박수 소리로 그것이 끝났음을 알았다. 그가 샹들리에와 가스등으로 눈부시게 밝혀진 홀로 들어섰을 때도 소음은 여전히 이어지고 있었다. 무대 위에서는 여가수가 드러난 어깨에 다이아몬드를 반짝이며 고개 숙여 인사하고 미소를 지으면서, 자기에게 팔을 내민 테너의 도움을 받아 각광 너머로 어색하게 날아드는 꽃다발들을 주워 모으고 있었다. 그러더니 그녀는 가운데로 가르마를 탄 반들반들 포마드 칠한 머리의 한 신사에게로 다가갔는데, 그 신사는 각광 너머로 몸을 뻗어 그녀에게 무언가를 내밀고 있었고, 일층 관람석과 박스석의 모든 관객이 흥분하여 몸을 앞으로 내밀고 소리치며 박수를 쳐 댔다. 높은 의자에 앉은 지휘자는 그 헌정품을 건네주는 것을 도우면서 자신의 흰 넥타이를 바로잡았다. 브론스키는 일층 좌석 한가운데로 걸어가 멈춰 서서 주위를 둘러보기 시작했다. 그날은 그 어느 때보다도 그의 주의가 낯익고 습관적인 환경, 그러니까 무대며 소음이며, 꽉 들어찬 극장 안의 그 낯익고 시시하며 가지각색인 관객 무리에게로 쏠리지 않았다.\n\n언제나처럼, 박스석 안쪽에는 어떤 부류의 부인들이 어떤 부류의 장교들과 함께 있었고, 화려하게 차려입은 여자들이—누군지는 신만이 알겠지만—제복들과 검은 연미복들 사이에 있었으며, 위층 관람석에는 변함없이 지저분한 군중이 있었다. 그리고 그 군중 가운데, 박스석과 앞줄에는 마흔 명쯤 되는 진짜 사람들이 있었다. 브론스키는 곧장 그 오아시스들에게로 주의를 돌렸고, 곧바로 그들과 관계를 맺었다.\n\n그가 들어섰을 때는 막이 끝나 있었기에, 그는 곧장 형의 박스석으로 가지 않고 일층 앞줄로 올라가 세르푸호프스코이와 함께 각광 옆에 멈춰 섰는데, 한쪽 무릎을 세우고 발뒤꿈치를 각광에 올려놓고 서 있던 세르푸호프스코이는 멀리서 그를 발견하고 미소 지으며 손짓해 불렀다.\n\n브론스키는 아직 안나를 보지 못했다. 그는 일부러 그녀가 있는 쪽을 보지 않으려 했다. 그러나 사람들의 시선이 향하는 방향으로 그녀가 어디 있는지 알 수 있었다. 그는 조심스레 주위를 둘러보았지만, 그녀를 찾는 것은 아니었다. 최악의 사태를 예상하며, 그의 눈은 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치를 찾고 있었다. 다행히도 그날 저녁 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 극장에 없었다.\n\n\"자네한테는 군인다운 데가 어쩜 이렇게도 안 남았나!\" 세르푸호프스코이가 그에게 말했다. \"외교관이라든가 예술가라든가, 뭐 그런 부류라고나 할까.\"\n\n\"그래, 검은 연미복을 입으니 마치 집으로 돌아온 것 같더군.\" 브론스키가 미소를 지으며 천천히 오페라글라스를 꺼내며 대답했다.\n\n\"음, 솔직히 그 점에선 자네가 부럽네. 외국에서 돌아와 이걸 걸칠 때면,\" 그는 자기 견장을 매만졌다, \"자유가 아쉬워진단 말이야.\"\n\n세르푸호프스코이는 브론스키의 출세에 대한 희망은 오래전에 모두 접었지만, 예전과 다름없이 그를 좋아했고, 지금은 유난히 그에게 다정했다.\n\n\"첫 막에 늦은 게 정말 안타깝군!\"\n\n브론스키는 한쪽 귀로만 들으면서 오페라글라스를 일층 좌석에서 옮겨 박스석들을 훑어보았다. 터번을 두른 한 부인과, 움직이는 오페라글라스 속에서 성난 듯 손을 내젓는 것처럼 보이는 대머리 노인 곁에서, 브론스키는 문득 안나의 머리를 발견했다. 도도하고, 눈부시게 아름다우며, 레이스의 테두리 안에서 미소 짓고 있는 머리였다. 그녀는 그에게서 스무 걸음쯤 떨어진 다섯 번째 박스석에 있었다. 앞쪽에 앉아 몸을 약간 돌린 채 야시빈에게 무언가를 말하고 있었다. 아름답고 넓은 어깨 위에 얹힌 머리의 자태며, 절제된 흥분이며, 그녀의 두 눈과 얼굴 전체의 광채는, 모스크바의 무도회에서 그가 그녀를 보았던 바로 그 모습을 떠올리게 했다. 그러나 지금 그는 그녀의 아름다움에 대해 전혀 다른 감정을 느꼈다. 지금 그녀를 향한 그의 감정에는 신비로운 요소가 전혀 없었고, 그래서 그녀의 아름다움은, 전보다 한층 더 강렬하게 그를 끌어당기면서도, 이제는 그에게 모욕감을 안겨 주었다. 그녀는 그가 있는 쪽을 보고 있지 않았지만, 브론스키는 그녀가 이미 자기를 보았음을 느꼈다.\n\n브론스키가 다시 그쪽으로 오페라글라스를 돌렸을 때, 그는 바르바라 공작 부인이 유난히 얼굴이 붉어진 채 부자연스럽게 자꾸 웃으며 옆 박스석을 돌아보고 있다는 것을 알아챘다. 안나는 부채를 접어 붉은 벨벳을 톡톡 두드리며 먼 곳을 바라보고 있었고, 옆 박스석에서 벌어지고 있는 일을 보지 못했으며, 분명히 보고 싶어 하지도 않았다. 야시빈의 얼굴에는 그가 카드 노름에서 잃을 때면 흔히 짓던 표정이 어려 있었다. 그는 인상을 쓰며 콧수염의 왼쪽 끝을 점점 더 입 안으로 빨아들이며 옆 박스석을 곁눈질했다.\n\n왼쪽 그 박스석에는 카르타소프 부부가 있었다. 브론스키는 그들을 알고 있었고, 안나도 그들과 아는 사이라는 것을 알고 있었다. 카르타소프 부인은 마르고 자그마한 여자였는데, 자기 박스석에서 일어선 채 안나에게 등을 돌리고서, 남편이 들어 주고 있는 망토를 걸치고 있었다. 그녀의 얼굴은 창백하고 성나 있었으며, 흥분해서 무언가 떠들고 있었다. 뚱뚱하고 대머리인 카르타소프는 아내를 달래려 애쓰면서 연신 안나 쪽을 돌아보았다. 아내가 나가 버린 뒤에도 남편은 한참을 머뭇거리며 안나의 시선을 붙잡으려 했는데, 분명 그녀에게 인사를 하고 싶어 안달이 난 것이었다. 그러나 안나는 명백한 의도를 가지고 그를 알아보지 않으려 했고, 짧게 깎은 머리를 그녀 쪽으로 숙이고 있는 야시빈과 이야기를 나누었다. 카르타소프는 인사도 못 하고 나가 버렸고, 박스석은 텅 비어 버렸다.\n\n브론스키는 카르타소프 부부와 안나 사이에 정확히 무슨 일이 오갔는지 알 수 없었지만, 안나에게 무언가 모욕적인 일이 벌어졌음을 알아차렸다. 그는 자기가 본 것을 통해, 그리고 무엇보다도 안나의 얼굴을 통해 그것을 알았는데, 그가 보기에 그녀는 자기가 떠맡은 배역을 끝까지 해내기 위해 온 신경을 곤두세우고 있었다. 그리고 겉으로의 침착함이라는 이 태도를 유지하는 데 그녀는 완벽하게 성공하고 있었다. 그녀와 그녀의 세계를 모르는 사람, 그녀가 사교계에 모습을 드러내다니, 그것도 레이스와 아름다움으로 그렇게 눈에 띄게 모습을 드러내다니 하며 측은해하고 분개하고 놀라워하는 여자들의 온갖 말을 듣지 못한 사람이라면, 이 여자가 형틀에 매인 사람의 심정을 겪고 있으리라고는 추호도 의심하지 못한 채, 그저 이 여인의 평온함과 사랑스러움에 감탄했을 것이다.\n\n무언가 일이 벌어졌다는 것은 알지만 그것이 정확히 무엇인지는 모른 채, 브론스키는 괴로운 불안의 전율을 느꼈고, 무언가 알아낼 수 있을까 하는 마음으로 형의 박스석을 향해 갔다. 일부러 안나의 박스석에서 가장 먼 길로 빙 돌아 나가다가, 그는 두 명의 지인과 이야기하고 있던 옛 연대의 대령과 부딪쳤다. 브론스키는 카레니나 부인의 이름을 들었고, 대령이 자기 동행들에게 의미심장한 눈길을 던지며 서둘러 브론스키의 이름을 큰 소리로 부르는 것을 알아챘다.\n\n\"아, 브론스키! 언제 연대에 한번 오겠나? 저녁 한 끼 대접도 없이 자넬 그냥 보낼 순 없지. 자네는 우리 옛 동료니까 말이야.\" 그의 연대 대령이 말했다.\n\n\"지금은 안 됩니다, 정말 죄송합니다, 다음 기회에.\" 브론스키는 이렇게 말하고는 형의 박스석을 향해 위층으로 뛰어 올라갔다.\n\n브론스키의 어머니인 늙은 백작 부인이, 강철빛 잿빛 곱슬머리를 하고 형의 박스석에 와 있었다. 바랴는 젊은 소로키나 공작 영애와 함께 복도에서 그를 맞았다.\n\n바랴는 소로키나 공작 영애를 그녀의 어머니에게 맡겨 두고, 시동생에게 손을 내밀더니, 곧바로 그의 관심사에 대해 말하기 시작했다. 그녀는 그가 지금껏 본 적 없을 만큼 흥분해 있었다.\n\n\"내 생각엔 비열하고 가증스러운 짓이에요, 카르타소바 부인은 그럴 권리가 없었어요. 카레니나 부인은…….\" 그녀가 말문을 열었다.\n\n\"하지만 대체 무슨 일이오? 난 모르오.\"\n\n\"뭐라고요? 못 들으셨어요?\"\n\n\"내가 그걸 제일 늦게 듣는 사람이라는 거, 누님도 알잖소.\"\n\n\"그 카르타소바 부인보다 더 심술궂은 인간은 없을 거예요!\"\n\n\"하지만 그 여자가 무슨 짓을 했단 말이오?\"\n\n\"우리 그이가 말해 줬는데……. 그 여자가 카레니나 부인을 모욕했어요. 그 여자 남편이 박스석 너머로 카레니나 부인에게 말을 걸기 시작했는데, 카르타소바 부인이 한바탕 소동을 벌인 거죠. 그이 말로는, 그 여자가 무언가 모욕적인 말을 큰 소리로 내뱉고는 가 버렸다는 거예요.\"\n\n\"백작님, 어머님께서 찾으세요.\" 젊은 소로키나 공작 영애가 박스석 문밖으로 얼굴을 내밀며 말했다.\n\n\"내내 너를 기다리고 있었다.\" 그의 어머니가 빈정대듯 미소 지으며 말했다. \"어디에도 보이질 않더구나.\"\n\n아들은 어머니가 기쁨에 찬 미소를 억누르지 못하고 있음을 알아챘다.\n\n\"안녕하세요, 어머니. 어머니께 오는 길이었어요.\" 그가 쌀쌀맞게 말했다.\n\n\"왜 너는 카레니나 부인의 시중을 들러 가지 않는 게냐?\" 소로키나 공작 영애가 자리를 뜨자 어머니가 말을 이었다. \"그 여자가 화제의 중심이더구나. 사람들이 그 여자 때문에 파티를 잊을 정도니 말이다.\"\n\n\"어머니, 그 일에 대해서는 아무 말씀도 마시라고 부탁드렸잖아요.\" 그가 인상을 쓰며 대답했다.\n\n\"난 그저 다들 하는 말을 할 뿐이다.\"\n\n브론스키는 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았고, 소로키나 공작 영애에게 몇 마디 건넨 뒤 자리를 떴다. 문에서 그는 형과 마주쳤다.\n\n\"아, 알렉세이!\" 형이 말했다. \"정말 역겨운 일이야! 멍청한 여편네, 그 이상도 이하도 아니지……. 나는 곧장 그녀에게 가려던 참이었어. 같이 가세.\"\n\n브론스키는 형의 말을 듣지 않았다. 그는 빠른 걸음으로 아래층으로 내려갔다. 무언가 해야만 한다고 느꼈지만, 무엇을 해야 할지는 알 수 없었다. 그녀가 자기 자신과 그를 이토록 그릇된 처지에 몰아넣은 데 대한 분노가, 그녀의 고통에 대한 연민과 더불어 그의 가슴을 가득 채웠다. 그는 아래로 내려가 곧장 안나의 박스석으로 향했다. 그녀의 박스석에는 스트레모프가 서서 그녀와 이야기하고 있었다.\n\n\"이제 테너는 더 이상 없어요. 그 거푸집은 깨져 버렸답니다!\"\n\n브론스키는 그녀에게 고개 숙여 인사하고 멈춰 서서 스트레모프와 인사를 나누었다.\n\n\"당신은 늦게 들어오셔서 가장 좋은 노래를 놓치신 것 같네요.\" 안나가 브론스키에게 말하며, 그가 보기엔 빈정대듯 그를 흘끗 쳐다보았다.\n\n\"저는 음악을 잘 모릅니다.\" 그가 그녀를 매섭게 바라보며 말했다.\n\n\"야시빈 공작처럼요.\" 그녀가 미소 지으며 말했다, \"파티가 너무 큰 소리로 노래한다고 여기는 사람 말이에요.\"\n\n\"고마워요.\" 그녀가 긴 장갑을 낀 작은 손으로, 브론스키가 주워 든 프로그램을 받으며 말했는데, 바로 그 순간 갑자기 그녀의 사랑스러운 얼굴이 떨렸다. 그녀는 일어나 박스석 안쪽으로 들어갔다.\n\n다음 막에서 그녀의 박스석이 텅 빈 것을 알아챈 브론스키는, 조용한 관객들 사이에서 분개한 \"쉿\" 소리를 일으키며 독창이 한창인 와중에 밖으로 나가 집으로 마차를 몰았다.\n\n안나는 이미 집에 와 있었다. 브론스키가 그녀에게 다가갔을 때, 그녀는 극장에서 입었던 그대로의 옷차림이었다. 그녀는 벽에 붙은 첫 번째 안락의자에 앉아 똑바로 앞을 바라보고 있었다. 그녀는 그를 쳐다보더니, 곧바로 아까의 자세로 되돌아갔다.\n\n\"안나.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"당신, 당신이 모든 일의 원흉이에요!\" 그녀가 절망과 증오의 눈물을 목소리에 담아 외치며 일어섰다.\n\n\"내가 가지 말라고 빌고 또 애원했잖소, 불쾌한 일이 생길 줄 알고 있었으니까…….\"\n\n\"불쾌하다고요!\" 그녀가 외쳤다—\"끔찍했어요! 살아 있는 한 결코 잊지 못할 거예요. 그 여자가 내 옆에 앉는 건 수치라고 했단 말이에요.\"\n\n\"어리석은 여자의 수다일 뿐이오.\" 그가 말했다. \"하지만 왜 위험을 무릅쓰고, 왜 도발을 한단 말이오?……\"\n\n\"당신의 그 침착함이 싫어요. 당신은 나를 이런 지경으로 몰아넣지 말았어야 했어요. 당신이 나를 사랑했더라면…….\"\n\n\"안나! 내 사랑이 이 일과 무슨 상관이란 말이오?\"\n\n\"오, 당신이 나처럼 사랑했더라면, 당신이 나처럼 괴로워했더라면!…….\" 그녀가 공포의 표정으로 그를 바라보며 말했다.\n\n그는 그녀가 가여웠지만, 그럼에도 화가 났다. 그는 그녀에게 자기 사랑을 확언했는데, 그것만이 그녀를 달래는 유일한 방법임을 알았기 때문이다. 그리고 말로는 그녀를 책망하지 않았지만, 마음속으로는 그녀를 책망했다.\n\n그리고 그가 입에 담기 부끄러울 만큼 천박하게 느껴지던 그 사랑의 맹세를, 그녀는 게걸스럽게 들이마셨고, 차츰 마음을 가라앉혔다. 이튿날 완전히 화해한 두 사람은 시골로 떠났다.\n\n# PART SIX Chapter 1\n\n다리야 알렉산드로브나는 아이들과 함께 여름을 포크로프스코예에서, 여동생인 키티 레빈의 집에서 보냈다. 자기 영지에 있는 집은 거의 폐허나 다름없었고, 그래서 레빈 부부가 그녀에게 여름을 자기들과 함께 보내자고 권했던 것이다. 스테판 아르카지치는 이런 방식을 크게 반겼다. 그는 공무 때문에 가족과 함께 시골에서 여름을 보내지 못하는 것이 무척 안타깝다고, 그렇게 할 수만 있다면 자기에게는 더없는 행복일 거라고 말했다. 그러고는 모스크바에 머물면서도 가끔씩 하루 이틀 시골로 내려오곤 했다. 오블론스키 집안 사람들 말고도, 아이들과 그들의 가정교사 외에 노공작 부인까지 그해 여름 레빈 부부의 집에 머무르러 왔는데, 경험이 부족한 딸이 흥미로운 상태에 있는 만큼 곁에서 돌보아 주는 것이 자기 의무라고 여겼기 때문이다. 게다가 외국에서 키티와 친구가 된 바렌카도, 키티가 결혼하면 찾아오겠다던 약속을 지켜 친구 곁에 머물렀다. 이들은 모두 레빈의 아내 쪽 친구이거나 친척이었다. 그리고 그는 그들 모두를 좋아하기는 했지만, 그가 속으로 이름 붙인 이른바 \"셰르바츠키 분자\"의 밀려듦에 짓눌려 버린 자기만의 레빈 세계와 레빈식 방식이 다소 아쉬웠다. 그의 친척 중에서는 세르게이 이바노비치만 함께 머물렀는데, 그 역시 레빈 기질이 아니라 코즈니셰프 기질의 인물이었던지라, 레빈의 정신은 완전히 지워지고 말았다.\n\n* 흥미로운 상태: 임신을 에둘러 이르는 당시의 완곡 표현.\n오랫동안 비어 있던 레빈 부부의 집에는 이제 사람이 너무 많아 거의 모든 방이 들어찼고, 노공작 부인은 거의 매일같이 식탁에 앉으면 사람 수를 모두 헤아려 보고는, 열세 번째에 해당하는 손자나 손녀를 따로 떨어진 식탁에 앉히곤 했다. 그리고 살림을 꼼꼼히 챙기던 키티는, 손님들과 아이들의 여름 식욕을 채우려면 그토록 많이 필요한 닭과 칠면조와 거위를 모두 마련하느라 적잖이 애를 먹었다.\n\n온 식구가 식탁에 둘러앉아 식사를 하고 있었다. 돌리의 아이들은 가정교사와 바렌카와 함께 버섯을 따러 갈 계획을 세우고 있었다. 그 지성과 학식 덕분에 거의 경외에 가까운 존경을 한 몸에 받던 세르게이 이바노비치가 버섯에 관한 이야기에 끼어들어 모두를 놀라게 했다.\n\n\"나도 데려가 주시오. 나는 버섯 따는 걸 무척 좋아한답니다.\" 그는 바렌카를 바라보며 말했다. \"참 좋은 소일거리라고 생각해요.\"\n\n\"어머, 저희야 기쁘죠.\" 바렌카가 살짝 얼굴을 붉히며 대답했다. 키티는 돌리와 의미심장한 눈길을 주고받았다. 학식 있고 지적인 세르게이 이바노비치가 바렌카와 함께 버섯을 따러 가겠다고 한 그 제안은, 요즘 들어 키티의 머릿속을 분주하게 채우던 어떤 추측을 확인시켜 주었다. 그녀는 자기 눈길이 눈에 띄지 않도록 서둘러 어머니에게 무언가 말을 건넸다. 식사가 끝난 뒤 세르게이 이바노비치는 응접실 창가에 앉아 커피를 마시면서, 동생과 시작한 대화에 끼어 있으면서도, 아이들이 버섯 따기 원정을 떠날 문 쪽을 지켜보고 있었다. 레빈은 형 가까이 창가에 앉아 있었다.\n\n키티는 남편 곁에 서서, 그에게 무언가 할 말이 있는 듯, 자기에게는 아무 흥미도 없는 대화가 끝나기를 분명히 기다리고 있었다.\n\n\"넌 결혼한 뒤로 여러 면에서 변했어, 그것도 더 나은 쪽으로 말이야.\" 세르게이 이바노비치가 키티에게 미소를 지으며, 대화에는 별 흥미가 없는 기색으로 말했다. \"하지만 더없이 역설적인 이론들을 옹호하려는 그 열정만큼은 여전하구나.\"\n\n\"카탸, 서 있는 건 네 몸에 좋지 않아.\" 남편이 그녀에게 의자를 가져다 놓아 주며, 의미심장한 눈길로 그녀를 바라보았다.\n\n\"아, 그리고 시간도 없겠는데.\" 아이들이 뛰쳐나오는 것을 보고 세르게이 이바노비치가 덧붙였다.\n\n그 모두의 맨 앞에서 타냐가 팽팽하게 당겨 신은 양말 차림으로 비스듬히 달려 나오더니, 바구니와 세르게이 이바노비치의 모자를 흔들며 곧장 그에게로 달려왔다.\n\n아버지의 고운 눈을 꼭 빼닮은 반짝이는 눈으로 세르게이 이바노비치에게 거침없이 달려온 그 아이는, 그에게 모자를 건네고는 직접 씌워 주려는 시늉을 하면서, 그 스스럼없는 행동을 수줍고 다정한 미소로 누그러뜨렸다.\n\n\"바렌카가 기다리고 있어요.\" 그 아이는 세르게이 이바노비치의 미소에서 그래도 된다는 것을 알아채고는, 그의 모자를 조심스럽게 씌워 주며 말했다.\n\n바렌카는 노란 무명 드레스를 입고 머리에 흰 두건을 두른 채 문가에 서 있었다.\n\n\"가요, 가, 바르바라 안드레예브나.\" 세르게이 이바노비치가 커피를 마저 마시고는, 손수건과 담배 케이스를 각각 다른 주머니에 넣으며 말했다.\n\n\"우리 바렌카, 정말 사랑스럽지 않아요? 응?\" 세르게이 이바노비치가 일어서자마자 키티가 남편에게 말했다. 그녀는 세르게이 이바노비치가 들을 수 있게끔 말했고, 그가 듣기를 바라는 것이 분명했다. \"게다가 얼마나 예뻐요. 어찌나 기품 있는 미인인지! 바렌카!\" 키티가 큰 소리로 불렀다. \"물방앗간 숲에 있을 거지요? 우리도 그리로 갈게요.\"\n\n\"넌 네 몸 상태를 자꾸 잊는구나, 키티.\" 노공작 부인이 황급히 문가로 나오며 말했다. \"그렇게 소리치면 안 돼.\"\n\n키티의 목소리와 어머니의 꾸지람을 들은 바렌카는 가볍고 빠른 걸음으로 키티에게 다가왔다. 그 동작의 빠름이며, 상기되어 들뜬 얼굴이며, 모든 것이 그녀 안에서 무언가 예사롭지 않은 일이 일어나고 있음을 드러내고 있었다. 키티는 그것이 무엇인지 알았고, 그녀를 유심히 지켜보고 있던 참이었다. 키티가 마침 그 순간 바렌카를 부른 것은, 오로지 마음속으로, 키티가 짐작하기로는 그날 식사 뒤 숲에서 틀림없이 일어나고야 말 그 중대한 일을 위해 그녀에게 축복을 빌어 주기 위해서였다.\n\n\"바렌카, 어떤 일이 일어난다면 난 정말 기쁠 거예요.\" 그녀가 바렌카에게 입을 맞추며 속삭였다.\n\n\"그런데 당신도 우리랑 같이 가시나요?\" 바렌카가 방금 한 말을 듣지 못한 척하며, 당황한 기색으로 레빈에게 물었다.\n\n\"가요, 다만 탈곡장까지만, 거기서 나는 멈출 거예요.\"\n\n\"아니, 거기서 뭘 하시려고요?\" 키티가 물었다.\n\n\"새 짐마차들을 좀 살펴보고 송장을 확인해야 해요.\" 레빈이 말했다. \"그런데 당신은 어디 있을 거요?\"\n\n\"테라스에요.\"\n\n# Chapter 2\n\n테라스에는 일행 가운데 부인들이 모두 모여 있었다. 그들은 식사 후에 늘 거기에 앉아 있기를 좋아했고, 그날은 거기서 할 일도 있었다. 다들 매달려 있던 갓난아기 옷 바느질과 뜨개질 말고도, 그날 오후에는 아가피야 미하일로브나에게는 새로운 방식으로, 즉 물을 넣지 않고 잼을 만드는 일이 테라스에서 벌어지고 있었다. 키티가 친정에서 쓰던 이 새로운 방식을 들여왔던 것이다. 잼 만드는 일을 늘 맡아 오던 아가피야 미하일로브나는 레빈 집안에서 해 오던 방식이 잘못일 리 없다고 여기면서도, 딸기에 물을 넣고는 물 없이는 잼을 만들 수 없다고 우겼다. 그러다 현장에서 들켜, 지금은 모두가 보는 앞에서 잼을 만들고 있었고, 물 없이도 잼을 아주 잘 만들 수 있다는 것을 그녀에게 확실히 증명해 보일 참이었다.\n\n아가피야 미하일로브나는 얼굴이 벌겋게 달아오르고 화가 나서, 머리는 헝클어지고, 가는 팔은 팔꿈치까지 드러낸 채 숯 화로 위에서 졸이는 냄비를 휘젓고 있었는데, 라즈베리를 어둡게 바라보면서 그것이 눌어붙어 제대로 익지 않기를 간절히 바랐다. 공작 부인은 아가피야 미하일로브나의 분노가 라즈베리 잼을 만들게 한 책임자인 자신을 향한 것임을 알아채고는, 다른 일에 정신이 팔려 잼 따위에는 관심이 없는 척하려고 다른 이야기를 하면서도 슬그머니 화로 쪽으로 눈길을 던졌다.\n\n\"나는 늘 하녀들 옷을 직접 사 줘요, 값싼 천으로 말이지.\" 공작 부인이 앞서 하던 대화를 이어 가며 말했다. \"이제 거품을 걷어 낼 때가 되지 않았나, 자네?\" 그녀가 아가피야 미하일로브나에게 말을 건네며 덧붙였다. \"네가 할 필요는 조금도 없단다, 너는 더위를 타니까.\" 그녀가 키티를 막으며 말했다.\n\n\"제가 할게요.\" 돌리가 말하고는 일어나, 거품이 이는 설탕물 위로 숟가락을 조심스레 휘젓더니, 이따금 노르스름하고 불그스름한 찌꺼기와 핏빛 시럽으로 덮인 접시에 숟가락을 두드려 들러붙은 잼을 떨어냈다. '차 마실 때 이걸 얼마나 좋아할까!' 그녀는 아이들을 떠올리며, 자기도 어릴 적에 어째서 어른들은 잼에서 제일 맛있는 것—잼 거품—을 먹지 않을까 궁금해했던 일을 기억해 냈다.\n\n\"스티바는 돈을 주는 편이 훨씬 낫다고 해요.\" 그러는 사이 돌리는 화제로 오른 무거운 주제, 즉 하인들에게 어떤 선물을 해야 하느냐 하는 문제를 꺼냈다. \"하지만…….\"\n\n\"돈은 말도 안 되지!\" 공작 부인과 키티가 한목소리로 외쳤다. \"그들은 선물을 고마워한단다…….\"\n\n\"글쎄, 작년만 해도 나는 우리 마트료나 세묘노브나에게 포플린은 아니지만 그런 종류의 천을 사 줬지.\" 공작 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"어머니 명명일에 그걸 입고 있던 게 기억나요.\"\n\n\"무늬가 아주 예뻤어—소박하면서도 고상했지. 그 애가 갖지 않았더라면 내가 갖고 싶었을 정도였단다. 바렌카가 입던 것 비슷한 거였어. 그렇게 예쁘면서도 값이 싸지.\"\n\n\"자, 이제 다 된 것 같아요.\" 돌리가 숟가락에서 시럽을 떨어뜨리며 말했다.\n\n\"떨어뜨릴 때 엉기면 다 된 거야. 조금만 더 졸이게, 아가피야 미하일로브나.\"\n\n\"파리들이 꼬여요!\" 아가피야 미하일로브나가 화난 목소리로 말했다. \"어차피 마찬가지일 거예요.\" 그녀가 덧붙였다.\n\n\"아! 어쩜 저렇게 귀여울까! 놀라게 하지 마세요!\" 키티가 별안간 계단에 내려앉아 라즈베리 한가운데를 쪼고 있는 참새를 바라보며 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 하지만 너는 화로에서 좀 더 떨어져 있으렴.\" 어머니가 말했다.\n\n\"À propos de Varenka.\" 키티는 아가피야 미하일로브나가 알아듣지 못하도록 그들이 줄곧 그래 왔듯 프랑스어로 말했다. \"있잖아요, 엄마, 저는 어쩐지 오늘 일이 마무리될 것 같아요. 무슨 말인지 아시죠. 그렇게 되면 정말 멋질 텐데!\"\n* À propos de Varenka: 프랑스어로 '바렌카 이야기가 나왔으니 말인데'.\n\n\"하지만 그 애야말로 대단한 중매쟁이지!\" 돌리가 말했다. \"얼마나 조심스럽고 영리하게 둘을 엮어 주는지!……\"\n\n\"아뇨, 말씀해 보세요, 엄마, 어떻게 생각하세요?\"\n\n\"글쎄, 무슨 생각을 하라는 거니? 그 사람은\"(그 사람이란 세르게이 이바노비치를 뜻했다) \"러시아에서 누구하고든 언제라도 짝이 될 수 있었지. 물론 지금은 그리 젊은 나이는 아니다만, 그래도 지금이라도 그 사람과 기꺼이 결혼하려는 처녀가 얼마나 많은지 나는 알아…… 그 애는 아주 좋은 처녀지만, 그 사람은…….\"\n\n\"오, 아뇨, 엄마, 왜 그 사람한테도 그 애한테도 이보다 더 좋은 일은 상상할 수 없는지 좀 이해해 주세요. 우선, 그 애는 사랑스럽잖아요!\" 키티가 손가락 하나를 꼽으며 말했다.\n\n\"그 사람이 그 애를 아주 매력적으로 여기는 건 확실해.\" 돌리가 맞장구쳤다.\n\n\"게다가 그 사람은 사교계에서 그런 지위에 있으니까 아내한테서 재산이나 지위를 바랄 필요가 없어요. 그 사람한테 필요한 건 그저 착하고 다정한 아내—마음을 편하게 해 주는 아내뿐이에요.\"\n\n\"글쎄, 그 애라면 그 사람을 분명 편하게 해 주겠지.\" 돌리가 동의했다.\n\n\"셋째로, 그 애가 그 사람을 사랑해야 한다는 거예요. 그리고 정말 그래요…… 그러니까, 정말 멋진 일이 될 거예요!…… 두 사람이 숲에서 나오는 걸 보면—그리고 모든 게 결정되어 있겠죠. 저는 두 사람 눈을 보면 단번에 알 거예요. 정말 기쁠 거예요! 어떻게 생각해요, 돌리?\"\n\n\"하지만 너무 들뜨지는 말렴. 네가 흥분하는 건 전혀 좋지 않으니까.\" 어머니가 말했다.\n\n\"오, 저 안 흥분했어요, 엄마. 그 사람이 오늘 그 애한테 청혼할 것 같아요.\"\n\n\"아, 참 이상한 일이지, 남자가 어떻게, 언제 청혼하는지 말이야!…… 무슨 장벽 같은 게 있다가, 별안간 그게 무너져 버리거든.\" 돌리가 스테판 아르카지치와의 지난날을 떠올리며 생각에 잠긴 듯 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"엄마, 아빠는 엄마한테 어떻게 청혼했어요?\" 키티가 불쑥 물었다.\n\n\"별다른 일은 없었단다, 아주 단순했지.\" 공작 부인이 대답했지만, 그 회상에 그녀의 얼굴은 온통 환하게 빛났다.\n\n\"오, 그래도 어땠는데요? 어쨌든 말을 꺼내도 좋다는 허락이 떨어지기 전에 이미 아빠를 사랑했죠?\"\n\n키티는 이제 여자의 인생에서 그토록 더없이 중요한 그런 문제들을 어머니와 동등한 입장에서 이야기할 수 있다는 데서 묘한 즐거움을 느꼈다.\n\n\"물론 그랬지. 그이가 시골 우리 집에 와서 묵었거든.\"\n\n\"그런데 두 분 사이는 어떻게 정해졌어요, 엄마?\"\n\n\"아마 네가 뭔가 아주 새로운 걸 만들어 냈다고 생각하는 모양이지? 늘 똑같단다. 눈으로, 미소로 정해지는 거야…….\"\n\n\"엄마, 정말 멋지게 말씀하시네요! 바로 눈으로, 미소로 이루어지는 거죠.\" 돌리가 맞장구쳤다.\n\n\"그런데 아빠가 뭐라고 말했어요?\"\n\n\"코스챠는 너한테 뭐라고 했니?\"\n\n\"그이는 분필로 썼어요. 정말 멋졌죠…… 얼마나 오래전 일인지!\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n그리고 세 여자 모두 똑같은 일을 떠올리며 생각에 잠겼다. 침묵을 가장 먼저 깬 것은 키티였다. 그녀는 결혼 전 그 마지막 겨울과 브론스키에 대한 자신의 열정을 모두 기억해 냈다.\n\n\"한 가지가 있어요…… 바렌카의 그 옛 연애 말이에요.\" 자연스러운 생각의 흐름이 그녀를 이 지점으로 데려와 그녀가 말했다. \"세르게이 이바노비치한테 뭔가 말해 두고 싶었어요, 마음의 준비를 시켜 주려고요. 다들—그러니까 남자들은 다.\" 그녀가 덧붙였다. \"우리 과거에 대해 끔찍하게 질투하잖아요.\"\n\n\"다 그런 건 아니야.\" 돌리가 말했다. \"너는 네 남편을 기준으로 판단하는구나. 그이는 지금도 브론스키를 떠올리면 괴로워하잖니. 응? 정말 그렇지 않아?\"\n\n\"네.\" 키티가 눈에 생각에 잠긴 미소를 띠며 대답했다.\n\n\"하지만 나는 정말 모르겠구나.\" 어머니가 딸을 향한 모성의 보살핌을 변호하듯 끼어들었다. \"네 과거에 그이를 걱정시킬 만한 게 뭐가 있었다는 거니? 브론스키가 너한테 관심을 보였다지만—그런 일은 어느 처녀에게나 일어나는 거야.\"\n\n\"오, 네, 하지만 우리가 말하는 건 그게 아니에요.\" 키티가 살짝 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 내 말 좀 들어 보렴.\" 어머니가 말을 이었다. \"네가 직접 내가 브론스키와 이야기하는 걸 막았잖니. 기억 안 나니?\"\n\n\"오, 엄마!\" 키티가 괴로운 표정으로 말했다.\n\n\"요즘 너희 젊은 사람들은 도무지 단속할 수가 없어…… 너희 사이가 적절한 선을 넘어설 수는 없었겠지. 나라면 직접 그 사람을 불러다 입장을 분명히 밝히게 했을 거야. 하지만 얘야, 네가 동요하는 건 좋지 않아. 부디 그걸 명심하고 마음을 가라앉히렴.\"\n\n\"저는 아주 차분해요, 엄마.\"\n\n\"그때 안나가 와 준 게 키티한테는 얼마나 다행이었는지.\" 돌리가 말했다. \"그리고 또 얼마나 불행한 일이었는지. 결과가 정반대로 나왔잖아요.\" 그녀가 자기 생각에 문득 놀라며 말했다. \"그땐 안나가 그렇게 행복했고, 키티는 자기가 불행하다고 여겼지. 그런데 지금은 정반대예요. 나는 그 사람을 자주 떠올려요.\"\n\n\"떠올릴 만한 사람도 참 많다! 끔찍하고 역겨운 여자—마음이라곤 없는 여자야.\" 키티가 브론스키가 아니라 레빈과 결혼했다는 사실을 잊지 못하는 어머니가 말했다.\n\n\"왜 그 얘기를 하려고 하세요?\" 키티가 짜증스럽게 말했다. \"저는 그 일은 전혀 생각하지 않고, 생각하고 싶지도 않아요…… 그래요, 생각하고 싶지 않아요.\" 그녀가 테라스 계단에 울리는 남편의 낯익은 발소리를 알아채고는 말했다.\n\n\"무엇을 생각하고 싶지 않다는 거요?\" 레빈이 테라스로 올라서며 물었다.\n\n하지만 아무도 그에게 대답하지 않았고, 그도 질문을 되풀이하지 않았다.\n\n\"당신들의 여성 의회를 방해해서 미안하군요.\" 그는 못마땅한 표정으로 모두를 둘러보며, 그들이 자기 앞에서는 말하지 않을 무언가를 이야기하고 있었음을 알아채고 말했다.\n\n순간 그는 아가피야 미하일로브나의 감정, 즉 물 없이 잼을 만드는 데 대한, 그리고 통틀어 외부에서 들어온 셰르바츠키 집안 요소 전반에 대한 못마땅함을 자기도 함께 느꼈다. 그래도 그는 미소를 지으며 키티에게 다가갔다.\n\n\"자, 어떻소?\" 그가 요즘 모두가 그녀를 바라보는 그 표정으로 그녀를 바라보며 물었다.\n\n\"오, 아주 좋아요.\" 키티가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"당신 일은 어떻게 됐어요?\"\n\n\"새 짐마차들이 옛날 수레보다 세 배는 더 실었소. 자, 아이들 데리러 갈까? 말을 매어 두라고 일러뒀소.\"\n\n\"뭐라고요! 키티를 짐마차에 태우겠다는 거예요?\" 어머니가 나무라듯 말했다.\n\n\"네, 천천히 걷는 정도로요, 공작 부인.\"\n\n레빈은 남자들이 흔히 장모를 부르듯 공작 부인을 \"엄마\"라고 부른 적이 한 번도 없었고, 공작 부인은 그가 그렇게 부르지 않는 것을 못마땅해했다. 하지만 레빈은 공작 부인을 좋아하고 존경했음에도, 돌아가신 자기 어머니에 대한 감정을 더럽힌다는 느낌 없이는 그녀를 그렇게 부를 수 없었다.\n\n\"같이 가요, 엄마.\" 키티가 말했다.\n\n\"그런 무모한 짓은 보고 싶지 않구나.\"\n\n\"그럼 저는 걸어갈게요, 몸도 아주 좋으니까.\" 키티가 일어나 남편에게 다가가 그의 손을 잡았다.\n\n\"몸이 좋더라도 무엇이든 적당히 해야지.\" 공작 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"자, 아가피야 미하일로브나, 잼은 다 됐소?\" 레빈이 아가피야 미하일로브나에게 미소 지으며, 그녀를 기운 나게 해 주려고 말했다. \"새 방식으로 해도 괜찮소?\"\n\n\"괜찮은가 봅니다. 우리 식으로 보자면 너무 오래 졸였지만요.\"\n\n\"그게 오히려 더 나을 거요, 아가피야 미하일로브나, 곰팡이가 안 슬 테니까. 우리 얼음이 벌써 녹기 시작해서 보관해 둘 서늘한 광도 없지 않소.\" 키티가 남편의 의도를 곧바로 알아채고, 같은 마음으로 늙은 가정부에게 말을 건넸다. \"그런데 할멈이 만든 절임은 어찌나 맛있는지, 엄마가 그런 건 한 번도 못 먹어 봤다고 하신다니까요.\" 그녀가 미소 지으며, 머릿수건을 바로잡으며 덧붙였다.\n\n아가피야 미하일로브나가 화난 얼굴로 키티를 바라보았다.\n\n\"저를 위로하실 것 없습니다, 아씨. 저는 그저 그분과 함께 있는 아씨를 보기만 해도 행복합니다.\" 그녀가 말했고, 그 '그분과 함께'라는 투박하고 정겨운 말투에 담긴 무언가가 키티의 마음을 건드렸다.\n\n\"우리랑 같이 버섯 따러 가요, 좋은 자리를 알려 줘요.\" 아가피야 미하일로브나는 미소 지으며 고개를 저었는데, 마치 \"저도 아씨한테 화를 내고 싶지만, 그럴 수가 없네요\" 하고 말하는 듯했다.\n\n\"부디 내 방식대로 하게.\" 공작 부인이 말했다. \"잼 위에 종이를 덮고 럼주를 조금 적셔 두면, 얼음이 없어도 절대 곰팡이가 슬지 않는단다.\"\n\n# Chapter 3\n\n키티는 남편과 단둘이 있을 기회가 생긴 것이 무엇보다도 기뻤다. 남편이 테라스로 나와 무슨 이야기를 하고 있었느냐고 물었다가 아무 대답도 듣지 못했던 그 순간, 그의 얼굴—언제나 온갖 감정을 그렇게도 빠르게 비추어 내는 그 얼굴—을 스쳐 지나간 모욕감의 그늘을 그녀는 알아챘던 것이다.\n\n다른 사람들보다 앞서 걸어 나가 집이 보이지 않는 곳까지 이르렀을 때, 그들은 녹슨 바큇자국이 패고 낟알이 흩뿌려진 단단히 다져진 흙길로 들어섰다. 그녀는 그의 팔에 더 바짝 매달리며 그것을 자기 쪽으로 끌어당겼다. 그는 잠시 스쳐 갔던 불쾌한 인상을 까맣게 잊고 있었다. 그녀가 곧 어머니가 되리라는 생각이 한순간도 머릿속을 떠나지 않는 지금, 사랑하는 여인 곁에 있다는 사실 하나만으로 그는 관능의 불순물이라곤 조금도 섞이지 않은, 새롭고도 감미로운 행복을 느꼈다. 말은 필요 없었지만, 그래도 그는 그녀의 목소리를 듣고 싶었다. 그 목소리는 그녀의 눈빛처럼 임신한 뒤로 달라져 있었다. 그녀의 목소리에는, 눈빛에서와 마찬가지로, 무언가 소중한 일에 끊임없이 마음을 쏟는 사람들에게서 발견되는 그 부드러움과 진중함이 깃들어 있었다.\n\n\"그래, 힘들지 않아? 내게 더 기대.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"아니에요, 당신과 단둘이 있을 기회가 생겨서 정말 기뻐요. 그리고 솔직히 말하면, 모두와 함께 있어도 행복하지만, 우리 둘만 보내던 겨울 저녁이 그립긴 해요.\"\n\n\"그때도 좋았지, 하지만 지금은 더 좋아. 둘 다 좋은 거야.\" 그는 그녀의 손을 꼭 쥐며 말했다.\n\n\"당신이 들어왔을 때 우리가 무슨 얘기를 하고 있었는지 알아요?\"\n\n\"잼 얘기?\"\n\n\"아, 그래요, 잼 얘기도 했죠. 하지만 그다음에는, 남자들이 어떻게 청혼을 하는가에 대해서요.\"\n\n\"아!\" 레빈은 그녀가 하는 말보다 그녀의 목소리에 더 귀를 기울이며 말했다. 그러면서도 내내 길에 신경을 쏟고 있었다. 길은 이제 숲을 지나고 있었고, 그는 그녀가 발을 헛디딜 만한 곳을 피해 갔다.\n\n\"그리고 세르게이 이바노비치하고 바렌카 얘기도요. 당신도 눈치챘죠?…… 난 그게 정말 간절해요.\" 그녀가 말을 이었다. \"당신은 어떻게 생각해요?\" 그러고는 그의 얼굴을 들여다보았다.\n\n\"어떻게 생각해야 할지 모르겠어.\" 레빈이 미소 지으며 대답했다. \"세르게이는 그런 면에선 내겐 아주 이상해 보여. 전에 당신한테 말했잖아, 알지…….\"\n\n\"네, 형님이 죽은 그 아가씨를 사랑했었다는 거요…….\"\n\n\"그건 내가 어렸을 때 일이야. 나도 그저 전해 들은 얘기로 알 뿐이지. 그때 형의 모습은 기억나. 정말 다정한 사람이었어. 하지만 그 후로 여자들을 대하는 형을 지켜봤는데, 형은 친절하고, 그중 몇몇은 좋아하기도 하지만, 형에게 그들은 그저 사람일 뿐 여자가 아니라는 느낌이 들어.\"\n\n\"그래요, 하지만 지금 바렌카하고는…… 뭔가 있는 것 같아요…….\"\n\n\"어쩌면 있을지도 모르지…… 하지만 형을 알아야 해…… 형은 별난, 대단한 사람이야. 형은 오직 정신적인 삶만 살지. 너무 순수하고, 너무 고결한 천성이야.\"\n\n\"왜요? 그러면 이 일이 형님을 깎아내리기라도 한다는 거예요?\"\n\n\"아니, 그게 아니라 형은 정신적인 삶에 너무 익숙해져서 실제 현실하고 화해할 수가 없는 거야. 그런데 바렌카는 어쨌든 현실이거든.\"\n\n레빈은 이제 자기 생각을 정확한 말로 차려입히는 수고를 들이지 않고 대담하게 내뱉는 데 익숙해져 있었다. 그는 지금처럼 사랑과 다정함이 넘치는 순간이면 아내가 그저 한마디 암시만으로도 자기가 하려는 말을 알아들으리라는 것을 알았고, 실제로 그녀는 그를 이해했다.\n\n\"그래요, 하지만 바렌카에겐 나만큼 그 현실적인 면이 많지 않아요. 형님은 결코 날 마음에 들어 하지 않았을 거예요. 바렌카는 온통 정신적인 사람이거든요.\"\n\n\"오, 아니야, 형은 당신을 무척 좋아해. 그리고 우리 식구들이 당신을 좋아할 때면 난 늘 정말 기뻐…….\"\n\n\"네, 형님은 내게 아주 잘해 줘요. 하지만…….\"\n\n\"가엾은 니콜라이 형하고는 다르지…… 당신들은 정말로 서로 아꼈으니까.\" 레빈이 말을 맺었다. \"그 형 얘긴 왜 안 하는 거지?\" 그가 덧붙였다. \"난 가끔 그러지 않는 나를 탓하곤 해. 결국엔 잊어버리게 되니까. 아, 형은 얼마나 끔찍하고 또 사랑스러운 사람이었던지!…… 그래, 우리가 무슨 얘길 하고 있었지?\" 레빈은 잠시 말을 멈췄다가 다시 입을 열었다.\n\n\"형님은 사랑에 빠질 수 없는 사람이라고 생각하는 거죠?\" 키티가 자기 식으로 옮겨 말했다.\n\n\"형이 사랑에 빠질 수 없다기보다는,\" 레빈이 미소 지으며 말했다. \"거기에 필요한 약점이 형에겐 없는 거야…… 난 늘 형을 부러워했어. 이렇게 행복한 지금조차도 여전히 형이 부러워.\"\n\n\"사랑에 빠질 수 없다는 걸 부러워한다고요?\"\n\n\"형이 나보다 나은 사람이라서 부러운 거야.\" 레빈이 말했다. \"형은 자기 자신을 위해 살지 않아. 형의 삶 전체가 의무에 종속되어 있지. 그래서 형은 평온하고 만족스러울 수 있는 거야.\"\n\n\"그럼 당신은요?\" 키티가 비꼬는 듯하면서도 사랑이 담긴 미소를 지으며 물었다.\n\n그녀로서는 자기를 웃게 만든 그 생각의 사슬을 결코 설명할 수 없었을 것이다. 다만 그 마지막 고리는, 남편이 형을 치켜세우고 자기 자신을 낮추는 것이 그리 진심에서 우러난 것만은 아니라는 점이었다. 키티는 이 진심 아닌 태도가 형을 향한 그의 사랑에서, 너무 행복한 것에 대한 부끄러움에서, 그리고 무엇보다도 더 나은 사람이 되고자 하는 그의 식을 줄 모르는 갈망에서 비롯된다는 것을 알았다. 그녀는 그에게서 바로 그 점을 사랑했고, 그래서 미소 지었던 것이다.\n\n\"그럼 당신은요? 무엇이 그렇게 불만이에요?\" 그녀가 똑같은 미소를 지으며 물었다.\n\n자기가 스스로에게 만족하지 못한다는 말을 그녀가 믿지 않는 것이 그는 기뻤다. 그래서 그는 자기도 모르게 그녀가 그 불신의 근거를 입 밖으로 꺼내도록 이끌어 내려 했다.\n\n\"난 행복해, 하지만 나 자신엔 불만이야…….\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 행복한데 어떻게 자기 자신에게 불만일 수가 있어요?\"\n\n\"글쎄, 어떻게 말해야 할까?…… 사실 내 마음속엔 오로지 당신이 발을 헛디디지 않게 하는 것 말고는 다른 어떤 것에도 관심이 없어—알겠어? 아니, 정말 그렇게 깡충깡충 뛰면 안 돼!\" 그는 길에 떨어진 나뭇가지를 너무 날렵하게 넘어가는 그녀를 나무라느라 말을 끊고 소리쳤다. \"하지만 나 자신을 생각하고, 다른 사람들과, 특히 형하고 비교해 보면, 내가 한심한 인간이라는 느낌이 들어.\"\n\n\"하지만 어떤 점에서요?\" 키티가 똑같은 미소를 지으며 캐물었다. \"당신도 다른 사람들을 위해 일하잖아요? 당신의 협동 농장이며, 영지 일이며, 책은 또 어떻고요?……\"\n\n\"오, 하지만 난 느껴, 특히 바로 지금—이건 다 당신 탓이야.\" 그는 그녀의 손을 꼭 쥐며 말했다. \"그게 다 아무 의미도 없다는 걸 말이야. 난 그 일을 어딘가 건성으로 하고 있어. 내가 당신을 아끼는 만큼 그 모든 일을 아낄 수만 있다면!…… 그런데 그러기는커녕, 요즘 난 그저 누가 떠맡긴 숙제처럼 그 일을 하고 있어.\"\n\n\"그럼 아버지에 대해선 뭐라고 하시겠어요?\" 키티가 물었다. \"아버지는 공공의 이익을 위해 아무것도 하지 않으니, 그럼 한심한 인간인가요?\"\n\n\"장인어른?—아니지! 하지만 그러려면 당신 아버지 같은 순박함, 솔직함, 선량함이 있어야 해. 그런데 난 그게 없어. 난 아무것도 하지 않으면서 그걸로 안달복달하지. 이게 다 당신 탓이야. 당신이 생기기 전에는—그리고 이 아이도 말이야.\" 그는 그녀가 알아챈 그녀의 허리께를 흘끗 보며 덧붙였다. \"난 내 모든 정력을 일에 쏟았어. 그런데 지금은 그럴 수가 없고, 그게 부끄러워. 난 그저 누가 떠맡긴 숙제라도 되는 양 일하면서, 흉내만 내고 있는 거야…….\"\n\n\"그럼, 당신 지금 당장 세르게이 이바노비치하고 바꾸고 싶어요?\" 키티가 말했다. \"형님처럼 공공의 이익을 위해 이 일을 하고, 떠맡겨진 그 일을 사랑하고, 그 밖엔 아무것도 없는 그런 삶을 살고 싶어요?\"\n\n\"물론 아니지.\" 레빈이 말했다. \"하지만 난 너무 행복해서 아무것도 이해할 수가 없어. 그래, 당신은 형이 오늘 그녀에게 청혼할 거라고 생각해?\" 그는 잠시 침묵한 뒤 덧붙였다.\n\n\"그럴 것도 같고, 아닐 것도 같아요. 다만 난 그게 정말 간절해요. 자, 잠깐만요.\" 그녀는 몸을 굽혀 길가에 핀 들국화 한 송이를 꺾었다. \"자, 세어 봐요. 청혼한다, 안 한다.\" 그녀가 그에게 꽃을 건네며 말했다.\n\n\"한다, 안 한다.\" 레빈이 흰 꽃잎을 하나씩 뜯어내며 말했다.\n\n\"안 돼요, 안 돼!\" 키티가 그의 손을 낚아채며 멈춰 세웠다. 그녀는 흥미롭게 그의 손가락을 지켜보고 있었던 것이다. \"두 장을 한꺼번에 뜯었잖아요.\"\n\n\"오, 하지만 봐, 이 작은 건 수를 맞추는 데 안 치는 걸로 하자.\" 레빈이 덜 자란 작은 꽃잎 하나를 뜯어내며 말했다. \"여기 마차가 우릴 따라잡고 있어.\"\n\n\"키티, 힘들지 않니?\" 공작 부인이 소리쳐 물었다.\n\n\"하나도 안 힘들어요.\"\n\n\"힘들면 타려무나. 말들도 얌전히 걷고 있으니까.\"\n\n하지만 굳이 탈 필요는 없었다. 목적지가 바로 가까이에 있었고, 모두 함께 걸어갔다.\n\n# Chapter 4\n\n바렌카는 검은 머리에 흰 머릿수건을 두르고 아이들에게 둘러싸여 명랑하고 다정하게 그들을 돌보면서도, 마음에 둔 남자에게서 고백을 받을지도 모른다는 가능성에 눈에 띄게 들떠 있어 무척 매력적이었다. 세르게이 이바노비치는 그녀 곁을 걸으며 줄곧 그녀에게 탄복하기를 멈추지 않았다. 그는 그녀를 바라보면서, 그녀의 입에서 들었던 온갖 즐거운 이야기들이며, 그녀에 관해 알고 있는 온갖 좋은 점들을 떠올렸고, 자기가 그녀에게 품은 감정이 아주 오래전, 그것도 딱 한 번, 젊디젊은 시절에 느껴 본 적 있는 특별한 무엇임을 점점 더 또렷하게 의식하게 되었다. 그녀 곁에 있다는 행복감은 끊임없이 커져만 갔고, 마침내 어떤 지경에까지 이르러, 줄기가 가느다란 커다란 광대버섯 하나를 그녀의 바구니에 넣어 주다가 그는 그녀의 얼굴을 똑바로 바라보았는데, 그녀의 얼굴에 번지는 기쁘면서도 놀란 듯한 흥분의 홍조를 알아채고는 자기 자신마저 당황하여, 말없이 너무 많은 것을 담은 미소를 그녀에게 지어 보였다.\n\n\"이러다가는,\" 그는 속으로 중얼거렸다. \"이건 곰곰이 따져 보고 마음을 정해야 할 일이지, 한순간의 충동에 소년처럼 굴복할 일이 아니야.\"\n\n\"나는 따로 떨어져서 나 혼자 딸 겁니다. 안 그러면 내가 딴 게 표가 안 날 테니까요.\" 그는 이렇게 말하고는, 멀찍이 떨어져 선 늙은 자작나무들 사이로 비단처럼 부드러운 낮은 풀이 깔린 숲 가장자리를 벗어나, 흰 자작나무 줄기들 사이로 잿빛 사시나무 줄기와 거뭇한 개암나무 덤불이 우거진 숲 속 더 깊은 곳으로 들어갔다. 마흔 걸음쯤 떨어져 걸어가, 이제 남들 눈에 띄지 않는다는 것을 안 세르게이 이바노비치는 발갛게 물든 꽃차례를 잔뜩 피운 화살나무 덤불 뒤에 멈춰 섰다. 사방이 완전히 고요했다. 다만 그가 서 있는 자작나무들 머리 위에서, 마치 벌떼처럼 날파리들이 쉴 새 없이 윙윙거렸고, 이따금 아이들의 목소리가 그에게로 실려 올 뿐이었다. 그때 별안간 숲 가장자리에서 그리 멀지 않은 곳에서 그리샤를 부르는 바렌카의 콘트랄토 음성이 들려왔고, 세르게이 이바노비치의 얼굴에 기쁜 미소가 스쳐 지나갔다. 그 미소를 의식한 그는 자기 처지가 못마땅한 듯 고개를 가로젓고는, 시가를 꺼내 불을 붙이기 시작했다. 한참 동안이나 그는 자작나무 줄기에 성냥을 그어도 불을 붙일 수가 없었다. 흰 나무껍질의 부드러운 비늘이 인을 문질러 닦아 내는 바람에 불이 꺼져 버린 것이다. 마침내 성냥 하나가 타올랐고, 향긋한 시가 연기가 납작하고 넓게 너울지며 어렴풋이 떠다니다가, 자작나무 가지가 늘어진 아래 덤불 위로 앞으로 또 위로 길게 뻗어 나갔다. 연기 줄기를 바라보며 세르게이 이바노비치는 자기 처지를 곰곰이 헤아리며 천천히 걸음을 옮겼다.\n\n\"안 될 게 뭐 있어?\" 그는 생각했다. \"이게 한낱 스쳐 가는 변덕이나 격정에 지나지 않는다면, 그저 이 끌림—서로의 끌림(이건 서로의 끌림이라고 불러도 좋겠지)에 불과한 거라면 모르겠어. 하지만 만약 이게 내 삶의 모든 방향과 어긋난다고 느껴진다면—이 끌림에 굴복하는 것이 내 소명과 내 의무에 어긋나는 짓이라고 느껴진다면 말이야……. 그런데 그렇지가 않단 말이지. 이걸 반대해서 내가 할 수 있는 말이라곤, 마리를 잃었을 때 그녀를 추억하는 마음에 끝까지 충실하겠노라고 스스로 다짐했다는 것뿐이야. 내 감정을 반대해서 할 수 있는 말은 그것 하나밖에 없어……. 그건 대단한 거지.\" 세르게이 이바노비치는 속으로 이렇게 말하면서도, 동시에 이런 고려가 자기 개인에게는 조금도 중요하지 않으며, 다만 남들 눈에 자기의 낭만적 면모를 떨어뜨리는 데에나 작용할 뿐임을 느꼈다. \"하지만 그것만 빼면, 아무리 뒤져 봐도 내 감정에 반대할 만한 거리를 결코 찾아내지 못할 거야. 만약 내가 오로지 적합성만 따져서 고른다 해도, 이보다 더 나은 상대는 찾을 수 없을걸.\"\n\n자기가 아는 여자며 처녀들을 아무리 많이 떠올려 봐도, 그는 자기가 아내에게서 보고 싶어 할 자질들을 그 정도로 모조리, 정말 하나도 빠짐없이 갖춘 처녀를 떠올릴 수가 없었다. 그녀는 젊음의 온갖 매력과 싱그러움을 지녔으면서도 어린아이는 아니었다. 그리고 만약 그녀가 그를 사랑한다면, 그것은 여자라면 마땅히 사랑해야 하는 방식으로 의식하며 사랑하는 것이리라. 이것이 한 가지였다. 또 한 가지는, 그녀는 속물적인 것과 거리가 멀 뿐 아니라 사교계에 대해 분명한 거부감을 지녔으면서도, 동시에 세상을 알았고, 최고 사교계 여인다운 온갖 몸가짐을 갖추고 있었는데, 이것은 자기 삶을 함께할 여자에 대한 세르게이 이바노비치의 관념에 절대적으로 필요한 점이었다. 셋째로, 그녀는 신앙심이 깊었는데, 이를테면 키티처럼 어린아이같이 무의식적으로 신앙심이 깊고 착한 것이 아니라, 그녀의 삶은 종교적 원리 위에 세워져 있었다. 사소한 일에서까지도 세르게이 이바노비치는 그녀에게서 아내에게 바라는 모든 것을 찾아냈다. 그녀는 가난했고 이 세상에 홀몸이었으니, 지금 키티의 경우에서 보듯이 남편의 집안에 잔뜩 딸린 친척들과 그들의 영향력을 끌어들이지 않을 터였다. 그녀는 모든 것을 남편 덕으로 누리게 될 텐데, 이것이야말로 그가 자기 미래의 가정생활을 위해 늘 바라 왔던 바였다. 그리고 이 모든 자질을 한데 갖춘 이 처녀가 그를 사랑했다. 그는 겸손한 사람이었지만, 그것만은 알아보지 않을 수 없었다. 그리고 그도 그녀를 사랑했다. 이를 반대할 만한 고려가 하나 있었으니—바로 그의 나이였다. 하지만 그는 장수하는 집안 출신이었고, 흰 머리카락 한 올 없었으며, 누구도 그를 마흔으로는 보지 않았을 것이다. 게다가 쉰 먹은 사내가 스스로를 늙었다고 여기는 것은 오직 러시아에서뿐이고, 프랑스에서는 쉰 살 사내가 자기를 dans la force de l’âge라고 여기는 반면 마흔 살 사내는 un jeune homme라고 여긴다던 바렌카의 말이 떠올랐다. 하지만 마음이 스무 해 전과 다름없이 젊게 느껴지는데 햇수를 헤아리는 것이 무슨 대수란 말인가? 지금 그가 느끼는 것을 느끼는 것이야말로 젊음이 아니겠는가? 숲 가장자리 반대편에서 다가오던 그가, 비스듬히 쏟아지는 햇살의 환한 빛 속에서, 노란 드레스 차림에 바구니를 든 바렌카의 우아한 자태가 늙은 자작나무 줄기 곁을 가벼이 거니는 모습을 보았을 때, 그리고 이 바렌카의 모습이 자아내는 인상이 풍경의 아름다움과—비스듬한 햇살에 흠뻑 잠긴 노란 귀리밭과, 그 너머로 노랗게 얼룩진 채 먼 데의 푸른빛 속으로 녹아드는 아득한 옛 숲의 아름다움과—그토록 조화롭게 어우러졌을 때 느끼는 그 감정 말이다. 그의 심장이 기쁘게 두근거렸다. 부드러운 감정이 그를 사로잡았다. 그는 자기가 마음을 정했음을 느꼈다. 막 버섯을 따려고 쪼그려 앉았던 바렌카가 나긋한 동작으로 몸을 일으키며 주위를 둘러보았다. 시가를 내던지고 세르게이 이바노비치는 단호한 걸음으로 그녀를 향해 다가갔다.\n* dans la force de l’âge: 프랑스어로 '한창때의'. un jeune homme: 프랑스어로 '젊은이'.\n\n# Chapter 5\n\n\"바르바라 안드레예브나, 저는 아주 젊었을 때 제가 사랑하고 아내라 부르며 행복을 느낄 여인의 이상을 마음속에 그려 두었습니다. 긴 세월을 살아오는 동안 이제야 처음으로 제가 찾던 그것을 만났습니다—바로 당신에게서요. 당신을 사랑합니다. 그리고 당신에게 제 손을 내밉니다.\"\n\n세르게이 이바노비치는 바렌카에게서 열 걸음쯤 떨어진 곳에서 속으로 이 말을 되뇌고 있었다. 그녀는 그리샤가 따 가지 못하도록 버섯 위로 두 손을 펼친 채 무릎을 꿇고 앉아, 어린 마샤를 부르고 있었다.\n\n\"이리 와, 얘들아! 여기 정말 많아!\" 그녀는 달콤하고 깊은 목소리로 말했다.\n\n세르게이 이바노비치가 다가오는 것을 보고도 그녀는 일어나지도, 자세를 바꾸지도 않았다. 그러나 그녀가 그의 존재를 느끼고 또 그것을 기뻐하고 있다는 사실을 모든 것이 그에게 말해 주고 있었다.\n\n\"그래, 좀 따셨어요?\" 그녀가 하얀 머릿수건 아래에서, 아름답고 부드럽게 미소 짓는 얼굴을 그에게로 돌리며 물었다.\n\n\"하나도요.\" 세르게이 이바노비치가 말했다. \"당신은요?\"\n\n그녀는 자기 주위로 몰려든 아이들 때문에 분주해서 대답하지 못했다.\n\n\"저것도, 저 잔가지 옆에 있는 거 말이야.\" 그녀는 어린 마샤에게 작은 버섯 하나를 가리켜 보였다. 그것은 마른 풀을 비집고 솟아오르느라 장밋빛 갓이 절반으로 갈라져 있었다. 마샤가 버섯을 따서 두 쪽의 하얀 조각으로 쪼개는 동안 바렌카는 일어섰다. \"이러고 있으니 제 어린 시절이 떠오르네요.\" 그녀는 세르게이 이바노비치 옆으로 다가서며 아이들 곁에서 떨어져 나와 이렇게 덧붙였다.\n\n두 사람은 말없이 몇 걸음을 걸었다. 바렌카는 그가 무언가 말하고 싶어 한다는 것을 알아챘다. 무슨 말인지도 짐작했고, 기쁨과 두려움으로 정신이 아득해졌다. 두 사람은 이제 아무도 그들의 말을 들을 수 없을 만큼 멀리 걸어왔는데도 그는 여전히 입을 떼지 못했다. 바렌카로서는 차라리 잠자코 있는 편이 나았을 것이다. 침묵 뒤라면 버섯 이야기를 한 뒤보다 하고 싶은 말을 꺼내기가 그들에게 더 쉬웠을 테니까. 그런데 바렌카는 자기 뜻과는 달리, 말하자면 얼떨결에 이렇게 말해 버렸다.\n\n\"그래서 아무것도 못 따셨다고요? 그래도 숲 한가운데에는 늘 버섯이 더 적은 법이긴 해요.\" 세르게이 이바노비치는 한숨을 쉬며 아무 대답도 하지 않았다. 그는 그녀가 버섯 이야기를 꺼낸 것이 못마땅했다. 그녀가 처음에 했던 어린 시절 이야기로 그녀를 되돌려 놓고 싶었다. 그러나 한참 침묵이 흐른 뒤, 마치 자기 뜻과는 어긋나게, 그는 그녀의 마지막 말에 대꾸하는 한마디를 던지고 말았다.\n\n\"하얀 식용 버섯은 주로 숲 가장자리에서 난다고 들었습니다. 하긴 저는 그것들을 구별할 줄도 모릅니다만.\"\n\n다시 몇 분이 흘렀고, 두 사람은 아이들에게서 더 멀어져 완전히 둘만 남았다. 바렌카는 자기 심장 뛰는 소리가 들릴 만큼 가슴이 두근거렸고, 얼굴이 붉어졌다 하얘졌다 다시 붉어지는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n슈탈 부인 곁에서 지내던 처지에서 벗어나 코즈니셰프 같은 사람의 아내가 된다는 것은, 그녀의 상상 속에서 더없는 행복이었다. 게다가 그녀는 자기가 그를 사랑하고 있다는 것을 거의 확신하고 있었다. 그리고 바로 지금 이 순간 그것이 결판날 참이었다. 그녀는 겁이 났다. 그가 말하는 것도, 말하지 않는 것도 다 두려웠다.\n\n지금이 아니면 영영 말할 수 없으리라는 것을—세르게이 이바노비치 또한 느끼고 있었다. 바렌카의 표정에서, 발갛게 달아오른 뺨과 내리깐 눈에서, 모든 것이 괴로운 기다림을 드러내고 있었다. 세르게이 이바노비치는 그것을 보고 그녀가 가엾어졌다. 지금 아무 말도 하지 않는 것은 그녀에 대한 모욕이리라는 생각마저 들었다. 그는 자기 결심을 뒷받침하는 모든 근거를 마음속으로 재빨리 훑어보았다. 청혼을 담아 전할 말까지 속으로 되뇌어 보았다. 그런데 그 말 대신, 전혀 뜻밖의 생각이 문득 떠올라 그는 이렇게 묻고 말았다.\n\n\"'자작나무' 버섯과 '흰' 버섯은 어떻게 다른가요?\"\n\n바렌카의 입술이 감정으로 떨렸고, 그녀는 대답했다.\n\n\"윗부분은 거의 차이가 없어요. 차이는 대궁에 있죠.\"\n\n그리고 이 말이 입 밖으로 나온 순간, 그도 그녀도 모든 게 끝났음을, 했어야 할 말은 끝내 나오지 않으리라는 것을 느꼈다. 그리고 그때까지 끊임없이 더 격렬해지기만 하던 그들의 감정은 가라앉기 시작했다.\n\n\"자작나무 버섯 대궁을 보면 이틀쯤 면도를 안 한 가무잡잡한 사내의 턱이 떠오르는군요.\" 세르게이 이바노비치는 이제 아주 차분하게 말했다.\n\n\"맞아요, 정말 그래요.\" 바렌카가 미소 지으며 대답했다. 그러자 두 사람이 걷는 방향이 저도 모르게 바뀌었다. 그들은 아이들 쪽으로 발길을 돌리기 시작했다. 바렌카는 마음이 쓰리고 부끄러웠지만, 동시에 안도감을 느꼈다.\n\n세르게이 이바노비치는 집에 돌아와 이 모든 일을 다시 곱씹어 본 뒤, 앞서 내린 자기 결심이 잘못된 것이었다고 생각했다. 그는 마리의 추억을 저버릴 수 없었다.\n\n\"살살, 얘들아, 살살!\" 환호성을 지르며 달려드는 아이들 무리를 막으려 아내 앞을 가로막고 서서, 레빈은 자못 화난 듯이 아이들에게 소리쳤다.\n\n아이들 뒤로 세르게이 이바노비치와 바렌카가 숲에서 걸어 나왔다. 키티는 바렌카에게 물어볼 필요도 없었다. 두 사람의 차분하고 어딘가 풀이 죽은 얼굴을 보고, 자기 계획이 어긋났음을 알아차렸다.\n\n\"어떻게 됐어?\" 다시 집으로 돌아가는 길에 남편이 그녀에게 물었다.\n\n\"입질을 안 해.\" 키티가 말했다. 그녀의 미소와 말투가 친정아버지를 떠올리게 했는데, 레빈은 그 닮은 모습을 보며 종종 흐뭇해하곤 했다.\n\n\"입질을 안 하다니, 어떻게?\"\n\n\"보여 줄게.\" 그녀는 남편의 손을 잡아 입가로 들어 올리고는, 다문 입술로 살며시 스치듯 댔다. \"신부님 손에 입맞춤하듯이 말이야.\"\n\n\"어느 쪽이 입질을 안 했다는 거야?\" 그가 웃으며 물었다.\n\n\"둘 다. 하지만 이렇게 했어야 했는데……\"\n\n\"저기 농부들이 오고 있어……\"\n\n\"어머, 그 사람들은 못 봤어.\"\n\n# Chapter 6\n\n아이들이 차를 마시는 동안 어른들은 발코니에 앉아 아무 일도 없었다는 듯이 이야기를 나누었지만, 사실 그들 모두, 특히 세르게이 이바노비치와 바렌카는 어떤 사건이, 비록 부정적인 것이긴 해도 대단히 중대한 사건이 일어났음을 잘 알고 있었다. 두 사람은 똑같은 감정을 느꼈는데, 그것은 시험을 치른 뒤 같은 학년에 그대로 남게 되었거나 학교에서 영영 쫓겨나게 된 학생의 심정과 비슷한 것이었다. 그 자리에 있던 사람들도 무언가 일이 있었음을 느끼고는 부러 엉뚱한 화제를 들먹이며 열심히 떠들어 댔다. 레빈과 키티는 그날 저녁 유난히 행복했고, 자기들의 사랑을 또렷이 의식했다. 그런데 그들의 그 행복한 사랑은 마치 똑같은 것을 느끼고 싶어도 느끼지 못하는 사람들에게 어떤 불쾌한 모욕을 가하는 것 같았고—그래서 그들은 양심에 찔리는 듯한 느낌을 받았다.\n\n\"내 말 명심해라, 알렉산드르는 안 올 거야.\" 노공작 부인이 말했다.\n\n그날 저녁 그들은 스테판 아르카지치가 기차로 내려오기를 기다리고 있었고, 노공작도 어쩌면 함께 올지 모른다는 편지를 보내온 터였다.\n\n\"그리고 나는 그 까닭을 알지.\" 공작 부인이 말을 이었다. \"그이 말이, 젊은 사람들은 처음 얼마간은 그냥 둘이서만 지내게 해 줘야 한다는 거야.\"\n\n\"하지만 아빠는 우리를 그냥 두고 계셨잖아요. 한 번도 뵌 적이 없는걸요.\" 키티가 말했다. \"게다가 우리는 젊은 사람들도 아니에요!—이젠 결혼한 늙은이들인걸요.\"\n\n\"다만 그이가 안 오면, 너희 아이들에게 작별을 고해야겠구나.\" 공작 부인이 서글프게 한숨지으며 말했다.\n\n\"무슨 말씀이세요, 엄마!\" 두 딸이 동시에 그녀에게 달려들었다.\n\n\"그이가 지금 어떤 심정일지 너희가 어떻게 알겠니? 왜, 지금쯤…….\"\n\n그리고 별안간 공작 부인의 목소리가 뜻밖에 떨렸다. 두 딸은 입을 다물고 서로를 바라보았다. '엄마는 늘 마음 아파할 거리를 찾아내신다니까.' 그 눈길은 그렇게 말하고 있었다. 그들은 알지 못했다. 공작 부인이 딸의 집에서 아무리 행복하고, 또 거기서 자신이 쓸모 있는 존재라고 아무리 느낀다 해도, 그들이 막내이자 사랑하는 딸을 시집보내고 옛집이 텅 비게 된 뒤로 그녀는 자기 자신을 위해서나 남편을 위해서나 몹시도 비참한 심정이었다는 것을.\n\n\"무슨 일이에요, 아가피아 미하일로브나?\" 키티가 무언가 의미심장한 표정으로, 사연이 가득한 얼굴을 한 채 서 있는 아가피아 미하일로브나에게 불쑥 물었다.\n\n\"저녁 식사 일로요.\"\n\n\"그래, 그게 좋겠네.\" 돌리가 말했다. \"당신은 가서 그 일을 챙겨요, 나는 가서 그리샤가 숙제 외우는 걸 들어 봐야겠어요. 안 그러면 그 애가 온종일 아무것도 안 할 테니까.\"\n\n\"그건 내 일이야! 아니야, 돌리, 내가 갈게.\" 레빈이 벌떡 일어서며 말했다.\n\n이제 김나지움에 다니게 된 그리샤는 여름 방학 동안 학기 중에 배운 것들을 복습해야 했다. 전에 모스크바에서 아들과 라틴어를 공부했던 다리야 알렉산드로브나는 레빈 집안에 와서부터, 하루에 적어도 한 번씩 라틴어와 산수 중 가장 어려운 과목을 아들과 함께 복습하기로 정해 두었다. 레빈이 그녀 대신 해 주겠다고 나섰지만, 어머니는 한번 레빈의 수업을 우연히 엿듣고는 그것이 모스크바의 선생이 가르치던 방식과 정확히 같지 않다는 것을 알아채고서, 레빈의 자존심을 상하게 할까 봐 무척 당황하고 마음 졸이면서도 단호하게 말했다. 선생이 했던 대로 책에 엄격하게 맞추어 가야 하니, 자기가 다시 그 일을 맡는 편이 낫겠다고. 레빈은 자기 의무를 소홀히 하여 그 공부를 도무지 이해도 못하는 어머니에게 떠넘긴 스테판 아르카지치에게도 놀랐고, 아이들을 그토록 형편없이 가르치는 선생들에게도 놀랐다. 하지만 그는 처형에게 그녀가 원하는 대로 정확히 가르치겠다고 약속했다. 그래서 그는 자기 방식이 아니라 책에 맞추어 그리샤를 가르쳐 나갔고, 그 때문에 흥미가 거의 일지 않아 수업 시간을 자주 잊어버리곤 했다. 오늘도 그랬다.\n\n\"아니야, 내가 갈게, 돌리, 당신은 가만히 앉아 있어.\" 그가 말했다. \"다 제대로, 책대로 할 거야. 다만 스티바가 와서 우리가 사냥을 나가게 되면, 그땐 빼먹을 수밖에 없겠지만.\"\n\n그리고 레빈은 그리샤에게로 갔다.\n\n바렌카도 키티에게 똑같은 말을 하고 있었다. 행복하고 잘 정돈된 레빈 집안에서조차 바렌카는 자신을 쓸모 있게 만드는 데 성공한 터였다.\n\n\"저녁 식사는 제가 챙길게요, 그냥 앉아 계세요.\" 그녀가 말하고는 아가피아 미하일로브나에게 가려고 일어섰다.\n\n\"네, 네, 아마 닭을 구하지 못했을 거예요. 그렇다면 우리 집 것을…….\"\n\n\"아가피아 미하일로브나하고 제가 알아볼게요.\" 그러더니 바렌카는 그녀와 함께 사라졌다.\n\n\"참 좋은 아가씨야!\" 공작 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"좋은 정도가 아니에요, 엄마. 더없이 훌륭한 아가씨예요. 저런 사람은 또 없을 거예요.\"\n\n\"그래, 오늘 스테판 아르카지치를 기다리고 있는 거니?\" 세르게이 이바노비치가, 바렌카에 관한 이야기를 더 끌고 가고 싶지 않은 기색으로 말했다. \"너희 두 사위만큼 서로 닮지 않은 사람도 찾기 어려울 거다.\" 그가 묘한 미소를 지으며 말했다. \"한 사람은 온통 활동적이라 물속의 물고기처럼 오로지 사교계에서만 살아가지. 다른 한 사람, 우리 코스챠는 활기차고 기민하고 무슨 일에든 재빠르지만, 막상 사교계에만 들어서면 무기력에 빠져 버리거나 뭍에 오른 물고기처럼 어쩔 줄 몰라 허우적댄단 말이야.\"\n\n\"그래요, 그 애는 정말 무심해요.\" 공작 부인이 세르게이 이바노비치를 향해 말했다. \"정말이지 나는 당신에게 그 애한테 좀 일러 달라고 부탁할 참이었어요. 저 애가\"(그녀는 키티를 가리켰다) \"여기 머무는 건 말도 안 되는 일이라고, 반드시 모스크바로 와야 한다고요. 그 애는 의사를 이리로 불러오겠다는 말이나 하고 있으니…….\"\n\n\"엄마, 그이는 뭐든 다 할 거예요. 다 동의했다고요.\" 키티가 그런 문제에서 세르게이 이바노비치에게 판단을 구하는 어머니에게 화가 나서 말했다.\n\n이야기를 나누던 도중 그들은 말이 콧김을 내뿜는 소리와 자갈길 위를 구르는 바퀴 소리를 들었다. 돌리가 미처 일어나 남편을 맞으러 나가기도 전에, 그리샤가 수업을 받고 있던 아래층 방의 창문으로 레빈이 뛰어나오더니 뒤따라 그리샤도 끄집어내려 주었다.\n\n\"스티바야!\" 레빈이 발코니 아래에서 외쳤다. \"우리는 다 끝냈어, 돌리, 걱정 마!\" 그는 이렇게 덧붙이고는 마차를 맞으러 소년처럼 달려가기 시작했다.\n\n\"Is ea id, ejus, ejus, ejus!\" 그리샤가 가로수길을 따라 깡충깡충 뛰어가며 소리쳤다.\n\n* 라틴어 격변화 연습. '그것이다, 그것의, 그것의, 그것의'라는 뜻.\n\"누가 또 있어! 물론 아빠겠지!\" 레빈이 가로수길 어귀에 멈춰 서서 외쳤다. \"키티, 가파른 계단으로 내려오지 말고, 돌아서 와.\"\n\n하지만 마차에 앉아 있는 사람을 노공작으로 본 것은 레빈의 착각이었다. 마차에 가까이 다가서자 그는 스테판 아르카지치 곁에 노공작이 아니라, 뒤로 긴 리본 자락을 늘어뜨린 스코틀랜드 모자를 쓴 잘생기고 살집 좋은 젊은이가 앉아 있는 것을 보았다. 이 사람은 셰르바츠키 집안의 먼 친척으로, 페테르부르크와 모스크바 사교계에서 잘나가는 멋진 젊은 신사인 바센카 베슬로프스키였다. \"썩 괜찮은 친구이고, 열성적인 사냥꾼이지.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 그를 소개하며 그렇게 말했다.\n\n노공작 대신 자기가 온 탓에 빚어진 실망에는 조금도 주눅 들지 않은 채, 베슬로프스키는 명랑하게 레빈에게 인사하며 예전부터 알던 사이라고 주장하고는, 그리샤를 와락 안아 마차 안으로 끌어올리더니 스테판 아르카지치가 데려온 포인터 너머로 그 애를 들어 옮겨 주었다.\n\n레빈은 마차에 타지 않고 뒤를 따라 걸었다. 그는 보면 볼수록 점점 더 좋아지던 노공작이 오지 않은 것이 못내 언짢았고, 또 이 바센카 베슬로프스키라는, 도무지 마음에 들지 않고 군더더기 같은 인물이 온 것도 언짢았다. 그는, 아이들과 어른들 할 것 없이 온 식구가 잔뜩 들떠서 모여 있는 계단 가까이 다가갔을 때, 바센카 베슬로프스키가 유난히 다정하고 정중한 태도로 키티의 손에 입을 맞추는 것을 보고는 그가 한층 더 마음에 들지 않고 더욱 군더더기처럼 느껴졌다.\n\n\"댁의 부인과 저는 사촌간이고 아주 오래된 친구랍니다.\" 바센카 베슬로프스키가 다시 한번 레빈의 손을 무척 따뜻하게 흔들며 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 새가 많은가?\" 스테판 아르카지치가, 모두가 인사를 채 나눌 틈도 거의 주지 않은 채 레빈에게 말했다. \"우리는 더없이 사나운 마음을 먹고 왔다네. 아니, 어머니, 저들은 그 후로 모스크바에 와 본 적도 없다니까요! 봐라, 타냐, 이건 네 거야! 자, 어서, 마차 안에, 뒤쪽에 있어!\" 그는 사방을 향해 떠들어 댔다. \"참 예뻐졌네, 돌리.\" 그가 아내에게 말하며, 다시 한번 그녀의 손에 입을 맞추고는 그것을 한 손으로 쥔 채 다른 손으로 토닥였다.\n\n조금 전까지만 해도 더없이 행복한 마음이던 레빈은, 이제 모든 사람을 어둡게 바라보았고, 모든 것이 못마땅했다.\n\n'저 입술로 어제는 누구한테 입을 맞췄으려나?' 그는 아내에게 다정하게 구는 스테판 아르카지치를 바라보며 생각했다. 그는 돌리를 바라보았고, 그녀 또한 마음에 들지 않았다.\n\n'저 여자는 남편의 사랑을 믿지도 않잖아. 그런데 뭐가 저렇게 좋다는 거야? 역겨워!' 레빈은 생각했다.\n\n그는 조금 전까지만 해도 그토록 사랑스럽게 여겨지던 공작 부인을 바라보았는데, 마치 자기 집에 있기라도 한 듯 리본을 늘어뜨린 이 바센카를 맞아들이는 그녀의 태도가 마음에 들지 않았다.\n\n심지어 역시 계단으로 나와 있던 세르게이 이바노비치마저도, 스테판 아르카지치를 맞이하며 보이는 다정한 태도가 그에게는 불쾌하게 느껴졌다. 형이 오블론스키를 좋아하지도 존경하지도 않는다는 것을 레빈은 알고 있었는데도 말이다.\n\n그리고 바렌카, 그녀조차 미워 보였다. 늘 결혼할 생각밖에 없으면서도, 천연덕스럽게 새침을 떠는 태도로 이 신사와 처음 인사를 나누고 있었으니까.\n\n* sainte nitouche: 프랑스어로 '내숭 떠는 여자, 새침데기'.\n그러나 누구보다도 미운 사람은 키티였다. 이 신사가 시골 방문을 마치 자기와 모든 사람에게 휴일이라도 되는 양 명랑한 어조로 대하는데, 거기에 키티가 장단을 맞추고 있었기 때문이다. 그리고 무엇보다도, 그의 미소에 응답하는 그녀의 그 특유의 미소가 불쾌했다.\n\n다들 시끌벅적 떠들며 집 안으로 들어갔다. 하지만 모두가 자리에 앉자마자 레빈은 돌아서서 밖으로 나가 버렸다.\n\n키티는 남편에게 무언가 잘못된 게 있음을 알아챘다. 그녀는 그와 단둘이 이야기할 틈을 잡으려 했지만, 그는 사무실에서 자기를 찾는다는 핑계로 서둘러 그녀에게서 멀어졌다. 그에게 자기 영지의 일이 지금 이 순간만큼 중요하게 여겨진 적은 오래도록 없었다. '저들에게야 다 휴일이지.' 그는 생각했다. '하지만 이것들은 휴일과는 상관없는 일이야. 기다려 주지도 않을뿐더러, 이게 없으면 살아갈 수가 없단 말이야.'\n\n# Chapter 7\n\n레빈은 저녁 식사에 부르러 사람을 보내고 나서야 집으로 돌아왔다. 계단 위에는 키티와 아가피아 미하일로브나가 서서 저녁 식사에 낼 포도주에 대해 의논하고 있었다.\n\n\"그런데 왜 이렇게 야단법석을 떨고 있는 거요? 평소대로 하면 되지.\"\n\n\"안 돼요, 스티바는 술을 안 마셔요...... 코스챠, 잠깐만, 무슨 일이에요?\" 키티가 그를 뒤따라 서두르며 말을 꺼냈지만, 그는 그녀를 기다리지도 않고 무자비하게 성큼성큼 식당으로 가 버렸고, 거기서 바센카 베슬로프스키와 스테판 아르카지치가 활기차게 이어 가던 대화에 곧장 끼어들었다.\n\n\"그래, 어떻게 생각해, 내일 사냥을 갈까?\" 스테판 아르카지치가 말했다.\n\n\"제발, 갑시다.\" 베슬로프스키가 다른 의자로 옮겨 앉으며 말했다. 그는 통통한 다리 한쪽을 아래로 접어 깔고 옆으로 비스듬히 앉았다.\n\n\"기꺼이 가지요, 갑시다. 그런데 올해 사냥은 좀 해 봤습니까?\" 레빈이 베슬로프스키에게 말했다. 그의 다리를 뚫어지게 바라보면서, 키티가 그에게서 너무도 잘 알고 있는, 그리고 그에게는 너무도 어울리지 않는 그 억지스러운 상냥함을 담아 말했다. \"들꿩을 찾을 수 있을지는 장담 못 하지만, 도요새는 잔뜩 있어요. 다만 일찍 나서야 합니다. 피곤하지 않으십니까? 스티바, 자넨 피곤하지 않나?\"\n\n\"내가 피곤하다고? 난 아직 한 번도 피곤했던 적이 없어. 밤을 꼬박 새워도 좋아. 산책이나 가자고!\"\n\n\"네, 정말, 아예 잠을 자지 맙시다! 좋군요!\" 베슬로프스키가 맞장구쳤다.\n\n\"오, 당신이 잠 안 자고 버틸 수 있다는 것, 그리고 다른 사람들까지 못 자게 한다는 것은 우리 모두가 잘 알지요.\" 돌리가 남편에게 말했다. 요즘 남편을 대할 때면 거의 늘 묻어나던 그 희미한 비꼼의 어조였다. \"하지만 내 생각엔 이제 잘 시간이에요...... 난 가겠어요, 저녁 식사는 안 할래요.\"\n\n\"아니, 좀 더 있어요, 돌리.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 그들이 저녁 식사를 하고 있던 식탁 뒤로 돌아 그녀 곁으로 가며 말했다. \"아직 당신한테 할 얘기가 잔뜩 있단 말이오.\"\n\n\"별것도 아닐 텐데요.\"\n\n\"베슬로프스키가 안나한테 다녀왔고, 또 그쪽으로 갈 거라는 거 알아요? 당신도 알다시피 그 사람들이 당신네서 오십 베르스타도 안 되는 곳에 있어서, 나도 꼭 한번 다녀와야 해. 베슬로프스키, 이리 와요!\"\n\n바센카는 부인들 쪽으로 건너가 키티 옆에 앉았다.\n\n\"아, 제발 좀 얘기해 주세요. 그분과 함께 지내셨다고요? 그분은 어떻던가요?\" 다리아 알렉산드로브나가 그에게 청했다.\n\n레빈은 식탁의 다른 쪽 끝에 남겨졌고, 공작 부인 및 바렌카와의 대화를 한순간도 멈추지 않으면서도, 스테판 아르카지치와 돌리와 키티와 베슬로프스키 사이에 무언가 열띤, 그리고 은밀한 대화가 오가고 있음을 알아챘다. 그게 다가 아니었다. 그는 아내의 얼굴에서 진정한 감정의 표정을 보았다. 아내는 무언가를 신이 나서 떠들어 대는 바센카의 잘생긴 얼굴을 한 점에 시선을 고정한 채 응시하고 있었다.\n\n\"그분들 댁은 더없이 좋습니다.\" 베슬로프스키가 브론스키와 안나에 대해 이야기하고 있었다. \"물론 제가 함부로 판단할 처지는 아니지만, 그 댁에서는 진짜 가정의 정취가 느껴지더군요.\"\n\n\"그분들은 어쩔 작정인가요?\"\n\n\"제 생각엔 모스크바로 갈 마음인 것 같습니다.\"\n\n\"우리 다 함께 그쪽으로 가면 얼마나 즐거울까! 자넨 언제 거기 가나?\" 스테판 아르카지치가 바센카에게 물었다.\n\n\"전 칠월을 거기서 보낼 겁니다.\"\n\n\"당신은 가겠소?\" 스테판 아르카지치가 아내에게 말했다.\n\n\"오래전부터 가고 싶었어요. 꼭 가겠어요.\" 돌리가 말했다. \"난 그 사람이 가엾고, 또 그 사람을 잘 알아요. 멋진 여인이죠. 당신이 돌아가면 나 혼자 갈 테니, 그러면 아무한테도 폐가 되지 않겠죠. 사실 당신이 없는 편이 더 나을 거예요.\"\n\n\"그야 물론이지.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 말했다. \"그럼 자넨, 키티?\"\n\n\"제가요? 제가 왜 가요?\" 키티가 온통 얼굴을 붉히며 말하더니, 남편을 돌아보았다.\n\n\"그럼 안나 아르카지예브나를 아십니까?\" 베슬로프스키가 그녀에게 물었다. \"정말 매혹적인 여인이지요.\"\n\n\"네.\" 그녀가 더욱더 새빨개진 얼굴로 베슬로프스키에게 대답했다. 그녀는 일어나 남편 쪽으로 걸어갔다.\n\n\"그럼 내일 사냥을 가시나요?\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n이 몇 분 사이에, 특히 그녀가 베슬로프스키와 이야기하는 동안 두 뺨에 번지던 그 홍조를 보면서, 그의 질투는 정말로 멀리까지 치달아 있었다. 이제 그녀의 말을 듣자, 그는 그것을 제 나름대로 해석했다. 나중에 떠올리면 스스로도 이상했지만, 그 순간 그에게는 분명해 보였다. 그녀가 사냥을 가느냐고 묻는 것은, 오직 그가, 그녀가 사랑에 빠졌다고 그가 상상하던 그 바센카 베슬로프스키에게 그 즐거움을 안겨 줄 것인지를 알고 싶어서일 뿐이라고.\n\n\"그래, 갈 거요.\" 그는 부자연스러운, 스스로에게도 불쾌한 목소리로 대답했다.\n\n\"아니에요, 내일은 여기서 하루 보내는 게 더 좋아요. 안 그러면 돌리가 남편 얼굴도 못 볼 거고요. 그러고 나서 모레 떠나세요.\" 키티가 말했다.\n\n키티의 말의 동기를 레빈은 이렇게 해석했다. '나를 그이한테서 떼어 놓지 마요. 당신이 가는 거야 상관없지만, 이 즐거운 청년과 함께 어울리는 건 좀 즐기게 해 줘요.'\n\n\"오, 당신이 원한다면 내일은 여기 머물지.\" 레빈이 유난히 상냥하게 대답했다.\n\n한편 바센카는 자기 존재가 빚어낸 괴로움 따위는 전혀 눈치채지 못한 채, 키티를 뒤따라 식탁에서 일어나, 미소 띤 감탄 어린 눈으로 그녀를 바라보며 그 뒤를 따라갔다.\n\n레빈은 그 눈길을 보았다. 그는 얼굴이 하얗게 질렸고, 한순간 숨조차 제대로 쉴 수 없었다. '저자가 감히 어떻게 내 아내를 저런 눈으로 본단 말인가!' 이것이 그의 안에서 끓어오르던 감정이었다.\n\n\"그럼 내일이죠? 제발, 갑시다.\" 바센카가 의자에 앉으며, 또다시 버릇대로 다리를 꼬며 말했다.\n\n레빈의 질투는 한층 더 멀리 나아갔다. 그는 벌써 자신을, 아내와 그 정부(情夫)에게는 그저 삶의 편의와 즐거움을 마련해 주는 데에나 필요한 존재로 여겨지는, 기만당한 남편으로 그려 보고 있었다...... 그러나 그럼에도 그는 바센카에게 사냥과 그의 총과 그의 장화에 대해 정중하고 정성스럽게 물었고, 다음 날 사냥을 가기로 동의했다.\n\n레빈에게는 다행스럽게도, 노공작 부인이 손수 일어나 키티에게 잘 시간이라고 권함으로써 그의 고통을 끊어 주었다. 하지만 이 대목에서도 레빈은 또 한 번의 고통을 피할 수 없었다. 그가 안주인에게 작별 인사를 할 때, 바센카가 또다시 그녀의 손에 입을 맞추려 했는데, 키티는 얼굴을 붉히며 손을 거두고는, 나중에 노공작 부인이 그 때문에 그녀를 나무라게 될 만큼 천진한 무뚝뚝함으로 말했다.\n\n\"우리는 그런 풍습을 좋아하지 않아요.\"\n\n레빈의 눈에는, 그런 관계가 생겨나도록 허락한 것부터가 그녀의 잘못이었고, 더구나 그것을 싫어한다는 티를 그토록 어색하게 낸 것은 더 큰 잘못이었다.\n\n\"아니, 어떻게 잘 생각이 든단 말이오!\" 스테판 아르카지치가 말했다. 그는 저녁 식사 자리에서 포도주를 몇 잔 마신 뒤로, 지금은 더없이 매력적이고 감상적인 기분에 젖어 있었다. \"이봐, 키티.\" 그가 막 보리수나무 뒤로 떠오른 달을 가리키며 말했다. \"얼마나 황홀한가! 베슬로프스키, 지금이 세레나데를 부를 때야. 알다시피 이 친구는 목소리가 기막히게 좋다네. 우리 오는 길에 함께 노래를 연습했지. 이 친구가 멋진 노래들을 가져왔어, 새 노래로 두 곡이나. 바르바라 안드레예브나와 이 친구가 꼭 이중창을 불러야 해.\"\n\n자리가 파한 뒤에도 스테판 아르카지치는 베슬로프스키와 함께 한참을 가로수 길을 거닐었고, 그 새 노래들 중 한 곡을 부르는 두 사람의 목소리가 들려왔다.\n\n이 목소리를 들으며 레빈은 아내의 침실 안락의자에 앉아 인상을 찌푸린 채, 무슨 일이냐고 그녀가 물어도 고집스레 침묵을 지켰다. 그러나 마침내 그녀가 머뭇거리는 눈길로 \"혹시 베슬로프스키한테 뭔가 마음에 안 드는 게 있었던 거예요?\" 하고 조심스레 물어보자, 모든 것이 터져 나왔고, 그는 그녀에게 다 털어놓았다. 그는 자기가 하는 말이 스스로도 굴욕스러웠고, 그 점이 그를 한층 더 격앙시켰다.\n\n그는 찌푸린 눈썹 아래로 위협하듯 번뜩이는 눈으로 그녀를 마주한 채 서서, 마치 온 신경을 곤두세워 자신을 억누르기라도 하는 듯 억센 두 팔을 가슴에 엇갈려 꽉 조였다. 그의 얼굴 표정은 험상궂었을 것이고 잔인하기까지 했을 테지만, 동시에 그녀의 마음을 울리는 고통의 빛이 어려 있지 않았다면 그랬을 것이다. 그의 턱은 씰룩거렸고, 목소리는 자꾸만 갈라졌다.\n\n\"당신은 알아줘야 해, 나는 질투하는 게 아니야, 그건 추잡한 말이지. 나는 질투할 수가 없어, 그걸 믿어 줘...... 내가 느끼는 걸 말로는 못 하겠지만, 이건 끔찍해...... 나는 질투하는 게 아니라, 누군가가 감히 그런 생각을 하고, 누군가가 감히 그런 눈으로 당신을 본다는 게 상처가 되고 굴욕스러운 거야.\"\n\n\"그런 눈이라니, 어떤 눈요?\" 키티가 그날 저녁의 말 한마디 한마디와 몸짓 하나하나, 거기에 담겼던 온갖 미묘한 기색까지 가능한 한 양심껏 떠올려 보려 애쓰며 말했다.\n\n마음속 가장 깊은 바닥에서는, 그가 그녀를 뒤따라 식탁의 다른 쪽 끝으로 건너왔던 바로 그 순간에 정말 무언가가 있었다고 그녀도 생각했다. 하지만 그녀는 자신에게조차 그것을 인정할 엄두를 내지 못했고, 더구나 그것을 그에게 말해 그의 고통을 키우는 일은 도무지 입에 올릴 수도 없었다.\n\n\"게다가 지금의 내 모습에 도대체 무슨 매력적인 데가 있을 수 있겠어요?.....\"\n\n\"아!\" 그가 두 손으로 머리를 움켜쥐며 외쳤다. \"그런 말 하면 안 돼!...... 만약 그때 당신이 매력적이었다면......\"\n\n\"오, 아니에요, 코스챠, 오, 잠깐만요, 오, 좀 들어 봐요!\" 그녀가 아파하는 연민의 표정으로 그를 바라보며 말했다. \"아니, 도대체 무슨 생각을 하는 거예요! 나한테는 이 세상에 아무도 없는데, 아무도, 아무도 없는데!...... 내가 아무도 만나지 않았으면 좋겠어요?\"\n\n처음 한순간 그녀는 그의 질투에 기분이 상했다. 아무리 사소한, 더없이 순수한 즐거움까지 자기에게 금지된다는 게 화가 났다. 하지만 지금은 그의 마음의 평안을 위해서라면, 그가 겪고 있는 고통에서 그를 구해 내기 위해서라면, 그런 사소한 것들뿐 아니라 모든 것이라도 기꺼이 희생할 수 있었다.\n\n\"내 처지의 끔찍함과 우스꽝스러움을 당신은 알아줘야 해.\" 그가 절망적인 속삭임으로 말을 이었다. \"그자가 내 집에 있다는 것, 그런데 그자가 격식 없이 굴고 다리를 꼬고 앉는 것 말고는 딱히 부적절한 짓이라곤 한 게 없다는 것 말이야. 그자는 그게 더없이 세련된 태도라고 여기니까, 나로서는 그자한테 정중하게 굴 수밖에 없는 거지.\"\n\n\"하지만 코스챠, 당신은 과장하고 있어요.\" 키티가 말했다. 마음속 깊은 곳에서는 이제 그의 질투를 통해 드러난 자기에 대한 그 사랑의 깊이에 기뻐하면서.\n\n\"이 모든 일에서 가장 끔찍한 부분은, 당신은 늘 그대로의 당신인데, 더구나 지금처럼 나한테 당신이 무언가 성스러운 존재이고, 우리가 이토록 행복한데, 이토록 각별히 행복한데—그런데 별안간 한 하찮은 녀석이...... 그자는 하찮은 녀석이 아니야. 내가 왜 그자를 욕하겠어? 난 그자와 아무 상관도 없어. 하지만 왜 나의, 그리고 당신의 행복이......\"\n\n\"있잖아요, 난 이제 이게 다 어디서 비롯된 건지 알겠어요.\" 키티가 말문을 열었다.\n\n\"그래, 뭔데? 뭔데?\"\n\n\"우리가 저녁 식사 때 이야기하는 동안 당신이 어떤 표정이었는지 봤어요.\"\n\n\"그래, 그래서!\" 레빈이 당혹스러워하며 말했다.\n\n그녀는 자기들이 무슨 이야기를 나누고 있었는지 그에게 말해 주었다. 그러면서 그녀는 감정에 북받쳐 숨이 가빴다. 레빈은 한동안 잠자코 있다가, 창백하고 괴로워하는 그녀의 얼굴을 살피더니, 별안간 두 손으로 머리를 움켜쥐었다.\n\n\"카챠, 내가 당신을 괴롭혔구려! 여보, 용서해 줘! 이건 미친 짓이야! 카챠, 난 죄인이야. 그런데 어떻게 그런 바보 같은 일로 당신이 그토록 괴로워했단 말이오?\"\n\n\"오, 난 당신이 가여웠어요.\"\n\n\"내가? 나를? 내가 얼마나 미친놈인지!...... 그런데 왜 당신을 비참하게 만든단 말이오? 어떤 외부 사람이 우리의 행복을 깨뜨릴 수 있다고 생각하는 것만으로도 끔찍해.\"\n\n\"물론 굴욕스럽기도 하고요.\"\n\n\"오, 그렇다면 여름 내내 그자를 여기 붙들어 두고, 정중함으로 그자를 흠뻑 젖게 해 주겠어.\" 레빈이 그녀의 두 손에 입을 맞추며 말했다. \"두고 봐. 내일은...... 오, 그래, 우리 내일 가는 거야.\"\n\n# Chapter 8\n\n이튿날, 부인들이 일어나기도 전에 사냥 갈 사람들을 태울 마차와 짐수레가 문 앞에 대기하고 있었다. 라스카는 이른 아침부터 사냥을 나간다는 걸 눈치채고는, 한참 동안 낑낑대며 이리저리 뛰어다니다가 마차의 마부 옆에 자리를 잡고 앉아, 늑장 부리는 꼴이 못마땅하다는 듯, 사냥꾼들이 아직도 나오지 않는 문을 흥분한 눈빛으로 지켜보고 있었다. 맨 먼저 나온 것은 바센카 베슬로프스키였는데, 그는 굵은 허벅지의 절반까지 올라오는 새 장화에 초록색 블라우스를 걸치고, 새 러시아가죽 탄띠를 두르고, 리본이 달린 스코틀랜드식 모자를 쓰고, 멜빵끈도 없는 아주 새 영국제 총을 들고 있었다. 라스카는 그에게 날듯이 달려가 반기며, 펄쩍펄쩍 뛰어 제 나름의 방식으로 다른 사람들은 곧 나오느냐고 물었지만, 그에게서 아무 대답도 얻지 못하자 다시 제 감시 자리로 돌아가, 고개를 한쪽으로 갸웃이 기울이고 한쪽 귀를 쫑긋 세운 채 가만히 자리를 잡았다. 마침내 문이 삐걱 소리를 내며 열리더니, 스테판 아르카지치의 얼룩무늬에 황갈색이 섞인 포인터 크라크가 빙글빙글 돌며 공중에서 몸을 뒤집으며 튀어나왔다. 그 뒤로 스테판 아르카지치 본인이 손에 총을 들고 입에 시가를 문 채 따라 나왔다.\n\n\"착하지, 착해, 크라크!\" 그는 두 앞발을 자기 가슴에 올리며 사냥 자루를 물려고 드는 개에게 다독이듯 외쳤다. 스테판 아르카지치는 투박한 각반과 덧신을 차고, 해진 바지에 짧은 외투를 걸치고 있었다. 머리에는 형체를 가늠하기 어려운, 거의 망가진 모자가 얹혀 있었지만, 새로 특허받은 그의 총은 더없는 일품이었고, 사냥 자루와 탄띠도 비록 낡긴 했어도 최상급이었다.\n\n바센카 베슬로프스키는 사냥꾼이 누더기 차림을 하되 사냥 장비만큼은 최고급으로 갖추는 것이야말로 진정한 chic라는 것을 그전까지 미처 알지 못했다. 누더기 차림에도 빛나는, 우아하고 잘 먹어 윤기가 흐르고 쾌활한, 전형적인 러시아 귀족 스테판 아르카지치를 바라보면서 그는 이제 그것을 깨달았다. 그래서 다음번에 사냥을 나올 때는 반드시 똑같은 차림을 하리라고 마음먹었다.\n* chic: 프랑스어로 '멋', '세련됨'.\n\n\"그런데 우리 주인장은 어떻게 된 거요?\" 그가 물었다.\n\n\"젊은 아내가 있잖소.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 미소를 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"그렇지, 게다가 그토록 매력적인 아내가!\"\n\n\"옷은 다 갖춰 입고 내려왔었소. 분명 또 아내한테 올라간 거겠지.\"\n\n스테판 아르카지치의 짐작이 맞았다. 레빈은 다시 아내에게 올라가, 어제 자기가 저지른 어리석은 짓을 용서해 주었는지 한 번 더 물었고, 더구나 제발 좀 더 조심해 달라고 간청했던 것이다. 무엇보다 중요한 건 아내가 아이들 곁에서 떨어져 있는 것이었다. 아이들이 언제 그녀에게 부딪칠지 모를 일이었다. 그러고 나서 그는 이틀 동안 집을 비우는 데 대해 화가 난 게 아니라는 그녀의 말을 다시 한번 들어야 했고, 내일 아침 반드시 하인을 말에 태워 쪽지를 보내 달라고, 단 두 마디만이라도 좋으니 자기에게 잘 지낸다는 걸 알려 달라고 부탁했다.\n\n키티는 며칠이나마 남편과 헤어지는 것이 늘 그렇듯 마음이 아팠지만, 사냥 장화를 신고 흰 블라우스를 걸친 그의 모습이 듬직하고 강건해 보였고, 그녀로서는 이해할 수 없는 사냥꾼 특유의 들뜸과 흥분이 어려 있는 것을 보자, 그의 즐거움을 위해 자기 서운함을 잊고 명랑하게 작별 인사를 했다.\n\n\"미안하오, 여러분!\" 그는 현관 계단으로 뛰어나오며 말했다. \"점심은 실었소? 왜 밤색 말이 오른쪽에 있지? 뭐, 상관없군. 라스카, 엎드려, 가서 누워 있어!\"\n\n\"황소 떼한테 갖다 넣어 두게.\" 그는 무슨 일로 물어볼 게 있어 계단에서 그를 기다리고 있던 목동에게 말했다. \"실례하오, 또 다른 골칫거리가 오는군.\"\n\n레빈은 이미 자리를 잡고 앉았던 마차에서 뛰어내려, 손에 자를 들고 계단 쪽으로 다가오는 목수를 맞이했다.\n\n\"어제 사무소에 오지 않더니, 이제 와서 날 붙잡는군. 그래, 무슨 일인가?\"\n\n\"나리, 한 번 더 꺾어 돌게 해 주시면 안 되겠습니까요? 계단 세 칸만 더 붙이면 됩니다요. 그러면 동시에 딱 맞게 됩니다요. 훨씬 더 편할 겁니다요.\"\n\n\"내 말을 들었어야지.\" 레빈이 짜증스럽게 대답했다. \"내가 말했지 않나. 먼저 선을 긋고 나서 계단을 맞추라고. 이제는 바로잡을 수가 없어. 내가 시킨 대로 새 계단을 만들게.\"\n\n문제는 짓고 있던 별채에서 목수가 계단을 망쳐 놓은 데 있었다. 들어갈 공간을 계산하지도 않고 짜 맞추는 바람에, 제자리에 놓고 보니 계단이 죄다 기울어져 있었던 것이다. 이제 목수는 같은 계단을 그대로 두고 거기에 세 칸을 덧붙이고 싶어 했다.\n\n\"훨씬 나아질 겁니다요.\"\n\n\"그래, 그럼 그 세 칸을 덧붙인 자네 계단은 어디로 나오나?\"\n\n\"아니, 나리, 정말이지.\" 목수가 깔보는 듯한 미소를 지으며 말했다. \"딱 제자리로 나옵니다요. 말하자면 여기서 시작해서요.\" 그는 설득하려는 듯 손짓을 하며 말을 이었다. \"내려오고, 또 내려와서, 이렇게 나오는 겁니다요.\"\n\n\"하지만 세 칸이면 길이도 더 늘어날 텐데…… 그게 어디로 나온단 말인가?\"\n\n\"아니, 그러니까요, 밑에서 시작해서 올라가고, 또 올라가서, 이렇게 나옵니다요.\" 목수가 고집스럽게, 그러면서도 확신에 차서 말했다.\n\n\"그러면 천장이랑 벽에 닿을 걸세.\"\n\n\"천만에요! 아니, 올라가고, 또 올라가서, 이렇게 나온다니까요.\"\n\n레빈은 꽂을대를 꺼내 흙바닥에 계단을 그려 보였다.\n\n\"자, 보이나?\"\n\n\"나리 뜻대로 하십쇼.\" 목수가 갑자기 눈을 반짝이며 말했는데, 마침내 그 이치를 깨달은 것이 분명했다. \"아무래도 새로 만드는 게 제일 낫겠습니다요.\"\n\n\"그래, 그럼 시킨 대로 하게.\" 레빈이 마차에 올라앉으며 소리쳤다. \"엎드려! 개들을 붙잡아, 필립!\"\n\n레빈은 이제 가족과 집안의 온갖 근심거리를 죄다 뒤로하고 떠나니, 삶에 대한 열렬한 기쁨과 기대가 솟아올라 도무지 말을 하고 싶지 않았다. 게다가 사냥꾼이라면 누구나 작전 지점에 다가갈 때 느끼는, 그 응축된 흥분의 감정에 사로잡혀 있었다. 그 순간 그의 마음에 무언가 걸리는 것이 있었다면, 그저 콜펜스키 늪에서 사냥감을 몰아낼 수 있을지, 라스카가 크라크와 견주어 솜씨를 발휘할 수 있을지, 그리고 오늘 자기가 총을 잘 쏠 수 있을지 하는 의구심뿐이었다. 새로운 구경꾼 앞에서 망신을 당하지 않는 것, 오블론스키에게 뒤지지 않는 것, 그것도 그의 머릿속을 스치는 생각이었다.\n\n오블론스키도 같은 심정이라 역시 말이 없었다. 바센카 베슬로프스키 혼자만 쾌활한 수다를 끊임없이 늘어놓았다. 이제 그의 말을 듣고 있자니, 레빈은 어제 자기가 그에게 얼마나 부당하게 굴었던가 싶어 부끄러웠다. 바센카는 정말이지 좋은 친구였다. 소박하고, 마음씨가 곱고, 더없이 유쾌했다. 결혼하기 전에 만났더라면 레빈은 그와 친구가 되었을 것이다. 레빈은 그가 삶을 마치 휴일처럼 대하는 태도와, 우아함을 거리낌 없이 자처하는 태도가 다소 못마땅했다. 손톱을 길게 기르고 멋들어진 모자를 쓰고 그 밖의 모든 것을 거기에 맞춰 갖췄다는 이유만으로, 마치 자기 자신에게 누구도 따질 수 없는 높은 가치가 있다고 여기는 듯했다. 하지만 그의 선한 천성과 좋은 교양을 봐서 그 정도는 너그러이 넘길 수 있었다. 레빈은 그가 훌륭한 교육을 받았다는 점, 프랑스어와 영어를 그토록 빼어난 발음으로 구사한다는 점, 그리고 자기 세계에 속한 사람이라는 점이 마음에 들었다.\n\n바센카는 왼쪽 말, 돈 초원에서 온 말을 무척이나 마음에 들어 했다. 그는 연신 열광적으로 그 말을 칭찬했다. \"초원의 말을 타고 초원을 내달리면 얼마나 멋질까! 응? 안 그렇소?\" 그가 말했다. 그는 초원의 말을 타는 것을 뭔가 야성적이고 낭만적인 일로 상상해 왔는데, 막상 알고 보니 전혀 그런 게 아니었다. 하지만 그의 소박함은, 특히 잘생긴 외모와 상냥한 미소와 우아한 몸놀림이 어우러지니, 무척 매력적이었다. 그의 천성이 레빈과 잘 통했기 때문인지, 아니면 레빈이 그에게서 좋은 점만 보려고 애쓰며 전날 저녁의 죄를 갚으려 했기 때문인지, 어쨌든 레빈은 그와 함께 있는 게 즐거웠다.\n\n집에서 이 마일쯤 달려왔을 때, 베슬로프스키가 갑자기 시가와 지갑을 찾았는데, 그것을 잃어버렸는지 탁자에 두고 왔는지 알 수가 없었다. 지갑 안에는 삼십칠 파운드가 들어 있었으니, 그 일을 어정쩡한 채로 둘 수는 없었다.\n\n\"이봐요, 레빈, 내가 저 왼쪽 곁말을 타고 집으로 달려갔다 오리다. 그거 아주 멋지겠는걸. 응?\" 그가 내릴 채비를 하며 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 그럴 필요 있겠소?\" 바센카의 몸무게가 십칠 스톤은 족히 나가겠다고 가늠하며 레빈이 대답했다. \"내가 마부를 보내리다.\"\n\n마부가 곁말을 타고 되돌아갔고, 레빈은 남은 두 마리를 직접 몰았다.\n\n# Chapter 9\n\n\"자, 그럼 우리 작전 계획은 어떻게 되나? 다 들려주게.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 말했다.\n\n\"우리 계획은 이렇네. 지금 우리는 그보즈됴프로 가는 길이야. 그보즈됴프에는 이쪽 편에 멧닭 늪이 하나 있고, 그보즈됴프 너머에는 멋진 도요새 늪이 몇 군데 있는데 거기에도 멧닭이 있지. 지금은 더우니까, 거기 도착하면—한 십오 마일쯤 되는데—저녁 무렵이 될 테고, 그때 저녁 사냥을 좀 하는 거야. 거기서 하룻밤 묵고 내일 더 넓은 들판으로 가는 거지.\"\n\n\"가는 길에는 아무것도 없나?\"\n\n\"있긴 하지. 하지만 힘을 아껴 둬야 해. 게다가 덥기도 하고. 괜찮은 곳이 두 군데 있긴 한데, 거기에 잡을 게 있을지는 모르겠어.\"\n\n레빈도 그 작은 곳들에 들러 보고 싶긴 했지만, 그곳은 집에서 가까웠다. 언제든 사냥할 수 있는 데였고, 게다가 작은 곳이라 세 사람이 사냥할 자리도 마땅찮을 터였다. 그래서 그는 다소 마음에 없는 말로, 거기에 잡을 게 있을지 모르겠다고 했다. 어느 작은 늪에 다다랐을 때 레빈은 그냥 지나치려 했지만, 사냥꾼 특유의 노련한 눈을 가진 스테판 아르카지치가 길에서도 보이는 갈대를 단번에 알아챘다.\n\n\"저기 한번 해 볼까?\" 그가 작은 늪을 가리키며 말했다.\n\n\"레빈, 제발 그러죠! 정말 신나겠는데요!\" 바센카 베슬로프스키가 졸라 대기 시작했고, 레빈은 응할 수밖에 없었다.\n\n미처 멈춰 서기도 전에 개들이 앞서거니 뒤서거니 늪으로 뛰어들었다.\n\n\"크라크! 라스카!……\"\n\n개들이 돌아왔다.\n\n\"세 사람 자리는 없을 거야. 난 여기 있겠네.\" 레빈은 개들에게 놀라 후드득 날아올라서는 공중에서 몸을 뒤집으며 늪 위에서 구슬프게 울어 대는 댕기물떼새밖에는 아무것도 없기를 바라며 말했다.\n\n\"아니에요! 가요, 레빈, 같이 갑시다!\" 베슬로프스키가 불렀다.\n\n\"정말 자리가 없어요. 라스카, 돌아와, 라스카! 개가 또 필요하진 않잖아요?\"\n\n레빈은 마차 곁에 남아 부러운 눈으로 사냥꾼들을 바라보았다. 그들은 늪을 가로질러 갔다. 작은 새들과 댕기물떼새 말고는—그중 한 마리를 바센카가 잡았다—늪에는 아무것도 없었다.\n\n\"자, 이제 알겠죠, 내가 늪을 아까워한 게 아니라는 걸.\" 레빈이 말했다. \"그저 시간 낭비라는 거였어요.\"\n\n\"오, 아니에요, 그래도 즐거웠는데요. 보셨어요?\" 바센카 베슬로프스키가 총과 댕기물떼새를 손에 든 채 어설프게 마차로 기어오르며 말했다. \"내가 이 새를 얼마나 멋지게 쐈는지! 안 그래요? 자, 이제 곧 진짜 사냥터에 도착하나요?\"\n\n말들이 갑자기 출발하는 바람에 레빈은 누군가의 총 개머리판에 머리를 부딪쳤고, 총소리가 울렸다. 사실은 총이 먼저 발사된 것이었지만, 레빈에게는 그렇게 보였다. 알고 보니 바센카 베슬로프스키가 방아쇠 하나만 당기고 다른 쪽 공이치기는 그대로 세워 두었던 것이다. 탄환은 아무에게도 해를 끼치지 않고 땅속으로 날아갔다. 스테판 아르카지치는 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 나무라듯 베슬로프스키를 보고 웃었다. 그러나 레빈은 차마 그를 나무랄 마음이 들지 않았다. 우선, 어떤 책망이든 그것은 그가 처한 위험과 레빈의 이마에 솟아오른 혹 때문에 나온 것처럼 보일 터였다. 게다가 베슬로프스키는 처음에는 어찌나 천진하게 괴로워하다가, 이내 모두의 당혹스러움을 두고 어찌나 사람 좋게, 그리고 옮아오게 웃어 대는지, 함께 웃지 않을 수가 없었다.\n\n제법 넓어서 사냥하는 데 시간이 꽤 걸릴 게 분명한 두 번째 늪에 다다랐을 때, 레빈은 그곳을 지나치자고 그들을 설득해 보았다. 그러나 베슬로프스키가 또다시 그를 설복했다. 이번에도 늪이 좁아서, 레빈은 점잖은 주인답게 마차 곁에 남았다.\n\n크라크가 사초 더미 쪽으로 곧장 달려갔다. 바센카 베슬로프스키가 제일 먼저 개를 뒤쫓아 달렸다. 스테판 아르카지치가 미처 다가오기도 전에 멧닭 한 마리가 날아올랐다. 베슬로프스키가 빗맞히는 바람에 멧닭은 풀을 베지 않은 들판으로 날아갔다. 이 멧닭은 베슬로프스키가 쫓아가도록 남겨졌다. 크라크가 다시 그것을 찾아내어 멈춰 서서 가리키자, 베슬로프스키가 쏘아 잡고는 마차로 돌아왔다. \"자, 이번엔 당신이 가요, 난 말들과 남아 있을 테니.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n레빈은 사냥꾼의 부러움이 빚어내는 통증을 느끼기 시작했다. 그는 베슬로프스키에게 고삐를 넘기고 늪으로 들어갔다.\n\n부당한 대접에 구슬프게 낑낑대며 안달하던 라스카가, 레빈이 잘 아는, 그리고 크라크가 아직 들르지 않은 가망 있는 자리로 곧장 날듯이 달려갔다.\n\n\"왜 저놈을 멈추지 않나?\" 스테판 아르카지치가 외쳤다.\n\n\"저 녀석이 새들을 놀라게 하진 않을 거야.\" 레빈은 제 암캐의 기쁨에 공감하며 그 뒤를 서둘러 쫓아가며 대답했다.\n\n낯익은 둥지 자리에 점점 가까워질수록 라스카의 탐색은 점점 더 진지해졌다. 작은 늪새 한 마리도 잠깐밖에는 그녀의 주의를 흐트러뜨리지 못했다. 그녀는 갈대 더미를 한 바퀴 돌고 두 번째 바퀴를 막 시작하다가, 별안간 흥분으로 몸을 떨더니 꼼짝 않고 멈춰 섰다.\n\n\"자, 어서, 스티바!\" 심장이 더욱 거세게 뛰기 시작하는 것을 느끼며 레빈이 외쳤다. 그러자 별안간, 마치 팽팽히 긴장한 귀에서 무슨 덧문 같은 것이 열어젖혀지기라도 한 듯, 온갖 소리들이, 뒤엉키긴 했어도 큼직하게, 거리 감각을 모조리 잃은 채 그의 청각을 두드리기 시작했다. 그는 스테판 아르카지치의 발소리를 멀리서 들려오는 말발굽 소리로 착각했고, 자기가 밟은 잔가지가 부서지는 소리를 멧닭이 날아오르는 소리로 들었다. 또 자기 등 뒤 멀지 않은 곳에서 물을 첨벙대는 소리가 들렸는데, 스스로도 그것을 설명할 수가 없었다.\n\n발걸음을 가려 디디며 그는 개에게 다가갔다.\n\n\"물어 와!\"\n\n멧닭이 아니라 도요새 한 마리가 개 곁에서 날아올랐다. 레빈은 총을 들어 올렸지만, 막 겨냥하려던 바로 그 순간 첨벙대는 소리가 더 커지고 더 가까워지더니, 무언가를 이상하리만치 큰 소리로 외쳐 대는 베슬로프스키의 목소리와 뒤섞였다. 레빈은 총구가 도요새 뒤쪽을 향한 것을 보았지만, 그래도 방아쇠를 당겼다.\n\n빗맞혔다는 것을 확인하고 나서 레빈이 둘러보니, 말들과 마차가 길이 아니라 늪 가운데 들어와 있었다.\n\n사냥을 보고 싶어 안달이 난 베슬로프스키가 늪 속으로 마차를 몰고 들어가, 말들을 진창에 빠뜨려 놓은 것이었다.\n\n\"빌어먹을 놈 같으니!\" 레빈은 진창에 빠진 마차로 돌아가며 혼잣말을 했다. \"도대체 왜 몰고 들어왔어요?\" 그는 그에게 무뚝뚝하게 말하고는, 마부를 불러 말들을 끌어내기 시작했다.\n\n레빈은 사냥을 방해받은 데다 자기 말들이 진창에 빠진 것 때문에, 그리고 무엇보다 스테판 아르카지치도 베슬로프스키도 자기와 마부가 말의 마구를 풀고 끌어내는 것을 돕지 않은 데에 화가 났다. 두 사람 다 마구를 채우는 법을 도무지 알지 못했기 때문이다. 거기는 아주 말라 있었다는 바센카의 항변에 한마디 대꾸할 가치도 두지 않은 채, 레빈은 말없이 마부와 함께 말들을 끌어내는 일에 매달렸다. 그러나 이내 일에 몸이 데워지고, 베슬로프스키가 흙받이 하나를 붙들고 어찌나 열심히 마차를 끌어당기는지 그러다 정말로 그것을 부러뜨리는 것을 보자, 레빈은 어제의 감정에 휘둘려 베슬로프스키에게 너무 쌀쌀맞게 군 자신을 책망하며, 그 냉랭함을 무마하려고 유난히 살갑게 굴려고 애썼다. 모든 것을 제자리로 돌려놓고 마차를 다시 길로 끌어 올린 다음, 레빈은 점심을 차리게 했다.\n\n\"Bon appétit—bonne conscience! Ce poulet va tomber jusqu’au fond de mes bottes.\" 기운을 되찾은 바센카가 두 번째 닭고기를 다 먹으며 그 프랑스 속담을 읊었다. \"자, 이제 우리 고생은 끝났고, 다 잘될 거예요. 다만 내 죄를 갚는 뜻에서, 난 마부석에 앉아야겠어요. 그렇죠? 응? 아니, 아니! 내가 당신의 아우토메돈이 되어 드리죠. 내가 당신들을 얼마나 잘 모셔 가는지 두고 보세요.\" 그는 레빈이 마부에게 몰게 하라고 청했는데도 고삐를 놓지 않은 채 대답했다. \"아니에요, 내 죄를 갚아야 해요, 게다가 난 마부석이 아주 편하답니다.\" 그러고는 그가 마차를 몰았다.\n\n레빈은 그가 말들을, 특히 다룰 줄 모르는 밤색 말을 지치게 할까 봐 조금 걱정스러웠다. 그러나 자기도 모르게 그 명랑함에 물들어, 그가 마부석에서 가는 내내 부르는 노래며, 영국식으로 네 마리를 한데 몰던 마차 운전을 설명하고 흉내 내는 것에 귀를 기울였다. 그렇게 더없이 좋은 기분으로, 점심 식사를 마친 그들은 그보즈됴프 늪으로 마차를 몰아갔다.\n* Bon appétit—bonne conscience! Ce poulet va tomber jusqu’au fond de mes bottes: 프랑스어로 \"맛있게 먹으니 양심도 편하구나! 이 닭고기가 내 장화 밑바닥까지 떨어지겠는걸\"이라는 뜻.\n\n# Chapter 10\n\n바센카가 어찌나 말을 능숙하게 몰았던지, 그들은 너무 일찍, 아직 더위가 가시지 않았을 때 늪에 도착하고 말았다.\n\n이번 사냥에서 가장 중요한, 이 더 큰 늪에 가까워지자 레빈은 어떻게 하면 바센카를 떼어 놓고 자기 행동을 자유롭게 할 수 있을까 하는 생각을 떨칠 수 없었다. 스테판 아르카지치도 분명히 같은 바람을 품고 있었으니, 레빈은 그의 얼굴에서 진짜 사냥꾼이라면 사냥을 시작할 때 으레 떠올리는 그 긴장한 표정과, 그 특유의 사람 좋은 능청스러움이 함께 어린 것을 보았다.\n\n\"어떻게 가지? 보아하니 멋진 늪인데, 매까지 있구먼.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 갈대밭 위를 맴도는 커다란 새 두 마리를 가리키며 말했다. \"매가 있는 곳에는 틀림없이 사냥감이 있는 법이지.\"\n\n\"자, 두 분.\" 레빈이 장화를 추어올리고 다소 침울한 표정으로 총의 노리쇠를 살피며 말했다. \"저 갈대밭 보이세요?\" 그는 강의 오른쪽 기슭을 따라 펼쳐진, 절반쯤 풀을 벤 드넓고 축축한 풀밭 가운데 거무스름한 초록빛으로 우거진 한 자리를 가리켰다. \"늪은 여기, 바로 우리 앞에서 시작됩니다. 보이시죠, 저기 더 푸른 데요. 거기서부터 말들이 있는 오른쪽으로 늪이 이어집니다. 거긴 새들이 알을 까는 곳이라 멧닭도 있고, 저 갈대밭을 빙 둘러 저 오리나무까지, 곧장 물방앗간까지 죽 이어지죠. 저기 보이세요, 물웅덩이가 있는 데요? 거기가 제일 좋은 자리입니다. 거기서 한번은 도요새를 열일곱 마리나 잡았죠. 우리 개를 데리고 흩어져서 각자 다른 방향으로 가다가, 저기 물방앗간에서 다시 만나도록 하죠.\"\n\n\"그럼, 누가 왼쪽으로 가고 누가 오른쪽으로 가지?\" 스테판 아르카지치가 물었다. \"오른쪽이 더 넓으니 자네 둘이 그쪽으로 가게, 난 왼쪽으로 갈 테니.\" 그는 짐짓 무심한 척하며 말했다.\n\n\"좋습니다! 그래야 더 많이 잡죠! 그래요, 갑시다, 가요!\" 바센카가 외쳤다.\n\n레빈은 동의할 수밖에 없었고, 그들은 갈라섰다.\n\n늪에 들어서자마자 두 마리 개는 함께 사냥감을 찾아 헤매기 시작하더니 초록빛 진창에 덮인 웅덩이 쪽으로 향했다. 레빈은 라스카의 방식을, 그 신중하고 종잡을 수 없는 방식을 잘 알았다. 그 자리도 잘 알았기에 도요새 한 무리가 통째로 있으리라 기대했다.\n\n\"베슬로프스키, 내 옆에서, 내 옆에서 걸어요!\" 그는 뒤에서 물을 첨벙거리며 따라오는 일행에게 나직한 목소리로 말했다. 콜펜스키 늪 근처에서 그 무심한 발포가 있은 뒤로, 레빈은 그의 총구가 어디를 향하는지에 신경이 쓰이는 것을 어쩔 수 없었다.\n\n\"오, 방해는 안 할 테니, 나는 신경 쓰지 마요.\"\n\n그러나 레빈은 신경을 쓰지 않을 수 없었고, 헤어질 때 키티가 한 말을 떠올렸다. \"서로 쏘지 않도록 조심하세요.\" 개들은 점점 더 가까이 다가오며 서로 엇갈렸고, 저마다 자기 냄새를 좇았다. 도요새에 대한 기대가 어찌나 강렬했던지, 레빈에게는 진창에서 발뒤꿈치를 빼낼 때 나는 철벅 소리마저 도요새 우는 소리처럼 들렸고, 그는 총의 노리쇠를 움켜쥐고 힘껏 눌렀다.\n\n\"탕! 탕!\" 소리가 거의 귓전에서 울렸다. 바센카가 늪 위를 맴돌다 마침 그 순간 사냥꾼들 쪽으로, 사정거리에서 한참 벗어나 날아오던 오리 떼를 향해 쏜 것이었다. 레빈이 미처 돌아볼 새도 없이 도요새 한 마리가 푸드덕 날아올랐고, 또 한 마리, 세 마리, 이어 여덟 마리쯤이 잇따라 날아올랐다.\n\n스테판 아르카지치는 도요새 한 마리가 지그재그로 날기 시작하는 바로 그 순간에 명중시켰고, 도요새는 진창 속으로 풀썩 떨어졌다. 오블론스키는 아직 갈대밭에서 낮게 날고 있는 또 한 마리를 침착하게 겨누었고, 총성과 함께 그 도요새 또한 떨어져, 사초가 베인 자리로 다친 데 없는 날개의 흰빛을 드러내며 파닥거리는 것이 보였다.\n\n레빈은 그만큼 운이 좋지 못했다. 첫 새를 너무 낮게 겨눠 빗맞혔다. 새가 막 날아오를 때 다시 겨눴지만, 바로 그 순간 또 다른 도요새가 그의 발밑에서 날아올라 그의 정신을 흩뜨려 놓는 바람에 또다시 빗맞혔다.\n\n그들이 총을 장전하는 동안 또 한 마리의 도요새가 날아올랐고, 미리 장전을 끝냈던 베슬로프스키는 산탄 두 발을 물속에 쏘아 박았다. 스테판 아르카지치는 자기가 잡은 도요새를 주워 들고는 반짝이는 눈으로 레빈을 바라보았다.\n\n\"자, 이제 갈라지세.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 말했고, 왼발을 절며, 총을 쏠 준비를 한 채 들고 개에게 휘파람을 불며 한쪽 방향으로 걸어갔다. 레빈과 베슬로프스키는 다른 쪽으로 걸어갔다.\n\n레빈은 늘 첫 발들을 놓치면 안달이 나고 신경질이 솟구쳐 하루 종일 사냥을 망치곤 했다. 그날도 그랬다. 도요새는 무수히 모습을 드러냈다. 녀석들은 개들 바로 밑에서, 사냥꾼들 다리 밑에서 끊임없이 날아올랐고, 레빈은 운 나쁜 첫 출발을 만회할 수도 있었다. 그러나 쏘면 쏠수록, 그는 베슬로프스키 앞에서 더 망신스러워지는 기분이었다. 베슬로프스키는 흥겹게 아무 데나 마구 총을 쏘아 대며 아무것도 잡지 못했지만, 자신의 부진을 조금도 부끄러워하지 않았다. 레빈은 안달이 난 나머지 마음을 다잡지 못했고, 점점 더 신경질이 솟구쳐 나중에는 맞히리라는 기대조차 거의 없이 쏘는 지경에 이르렀다. 정말이지 라스카도 이것을 알아챈 듯했다. 녀석은 점점 더 맥없이 사냥감을 찾기 시작했고, 어리둥절함인지 책망인지 모를 눈빛으로 사냥꾼들을 돌아보았다. 총성이 잇따라 빠르게 울려 퍼졌다. 화약 연기가 사냥꾼들 주위에 자욱하게 깔렸지만, 사냥 자루의 그 널찍한 그물 속에는 작고 가벼운 도요새 세 마리뿐이었다. 그나마 그중 한 마리는 베슬로프스키 혼자 잡은 것이었고, 한 마리는 둘이 함께 잡은 것이었다. 그러는 사이 늪 저편에서는 스테판 아르카지치의 총성이 들려왔는데, 잦지는 않았지만 레빈이 보기에는 잘 겨눈 것이었으니, 거의 매번 총성 뒤로 \"크라크, 크라크, apporte!\" 하는 소리가 들려왔던 것이다.\n\n* apporte: 프랑스어로 '물어 와'. 사냥개에게 잡은 새를 가져오라고 내리는 명령.\n이것이 레빈을 더욱 안달 나게 했다. 도요새는 갈대밭 위 허공을 끊임없이 떠다녔다. 땅 가까이서 푸드덕거리는 날갯소리며, 높은 공중에서 거칠게 우짖는 소리가 사방에서 들려왔다. 먼저 날아올라 공중으로 솟구쳤던 도요새는 다시 사냥꾼들 앞에 내려앉았다. 매도 이제 두 마리가 아니라 수십 마리가 날카로운 울음소리를 내며 늪 위를 맴돌고 있었다.\n\n늪의 절반 넘는 곳을 지나, 레빈과 베슬로프스키는 농부들의 풀밭이 갈대밭까지 길게 이어진 띠 모양으로 나뉜 곳에 이르렀는데, 한쪽은 밟혀 다져진 풀로, 다른 쪽은 그 사이를 베어 낸 오솔길로 경계가 표시되어 있었다. 이 띠들의 절반은 이미 풀을 벤 상태였다.\n\n풀을 베지 않은 쪽이 벤 쪽만큼 새를 찾을 가망이 있는 것은 아니었지만, 레빈은 스테판 아르카지치와 만나기로 약속했던 터라, 일행과 함께 풀을 벤 자리와 베지 않은 자리를 가로질러 걸었다.\n\n\"어이, 사냥꾼 양반들!\" 마구를 벗긴 짐수레에 앉아 있던 한 무리 농부 가운데 하나가 외쳤다. \"이리 와서 우리랑 점심이나 좀 듭시다! 술 한잔하라고!\"\n\n레빈이 둘러보았다.\n\n\"이리 와요, 괜찮으니까!\" 사람 좋아 보이는, 붉은 얼굴에 수염을 기른 농부가 하얀 이를 드러내며 씩 웃고는, 햇빛에 번쩍이는 푸르스름한 병을 들어 올리며 외쳤다.\n\n\"Qu'est-ce qu'ils disent?\" 베슬로프스키가 물었다.\n\n* Qu'est-ce qu'ils disent?: 프랑스어로 '저들이 뭐라는 거예요?'.\n\"보드카를 마시라고 권하는 거예요. 아마 풀밭을 몫몫이 나누던 참이겠죠. 나라면 좀 마시겠는데.\" 레빈은 베슬로프스키가 보드카에 마음이 동해 그들에게 가 버리기를 바라는 약간의 속셈을 품고 말했다.\n\n\"왜 권하는 거죠?\"\n\n\"오, 흥겹게 놀고들 있는 거예요. 정말이지, 한번 어울려 보시지요. 재미있을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"Allons, c'est curieux.\"\n\n* Allons, c'est curieux: 프랑스어로 '갑시다, 흥미롭군요'.\n\"가세요, 가요, 물방앗간 가는 길은 찾을 수 있을 테니!\" 레빈이 외쳤고, 둘러보다가 베슬로프스키가 피곤에 못 이겨 몸을 구부린 채 비틀거리며 총을 팔 길이만큼 앞으로 내밀고 농부들 쪽으로 늪을 빠져나가는 것을 보고 흡족해했다.\n\n\"당신도 오시구려!\" 농부들이 레빈에게 외쳤다. \"겁내지 말고! 우리 빵맛 좀 봐요!\"\n\n레빈은 보드카를 조금 마시고 빵도 좀 먹고 싶은 마음이 간절히 일었다. 그는 기진맥진했고, 비틀거리는 다리를 진창에서 끌어 빼는 것조차 큰일로 여겨져, 잠시 망설였다. 그러나 라스카가 사냥감을 향해 자세를 잡고 있었다. 그러자 곧바로 온갖 피로가 싹 가시고, 그는 늪을 가벼이 헤치며 개 쪽으로 걸어갔다. 도요새 한 마리가 그의 발밑에서 날아올랐다. 그는 쏘았고 명중시켰다. 라스카는 여전히 자세를 잡고 있었다. \"물어 와!\" 또 한 마리 새가 개 가까이서 날아올랐다. 레빈이 쏘았다. 그러나 그날은 그에게 운 없는 날이었다. 빗맞혔고, 게다가 아까 쏘아 잡은 새를 찾으러 갔으나 그것마저 찾을 수가 없었다. 그는 갈대밭을 온통 헤매고 다녔지만, 라스카는 그가 새를 맞혔다고 믿지 않았고, 그가 찾아오라고 보내자 찾는 척만 할 뿐 정말로 찾지는 않았다. 그리고 레빈이 자신의 실패를 뒤집어씌우던 바센카가 곁에 없는데도 사정은 조금도 나아지지 않았다. 도요새는 여전히 수두룩했지만, 레빈은 잇따라 빗맞히기만 했다.\n\n비스듬히 비치는 햇살은 여전히 뜨거웠다. 땀에 흠뻑 젖은 옷이 몸에 들러붙었고, 물이 가득 찬 왼쪽 장화가 다리를 무겁게 짓누르며 걸음마다 찍찍 소리를 냈다. 땀이 화약에 그을린 얼굴을 따라 방울방울 흘러내렸고, 입안은 쓴맛으로 가득했으며, 코에는 화약과 고인 물의 냄새가 배었고, 귀에는 도요새의 끊임없는 날갯소리가 울렸다. 총대는 너무 뜨거워 만질 수가 없었다. 심장은 짧고 빠르게 두근거렸고, 손은 흥분으로 떨렸으며, 지친 다리는 둔덕과 늪 위에서 휘청거리며 비틀댔지만, 그래도 그는 계속 걸었고 계속 쏘았다. 마침내 망신스럽게 또 한 발을 놓치고 나서, 그는 총과 모자를 땅바닥에 내동댕이쳤다.\n\n\"안 되겠어, 마음을 다잡아야 해.\" 그는 혼잣말을 했다. 총과 모자를 집어 들고 라스카를 부른 뒤 늪에서 빠져나왔다. 마른 땅에 올라선 그는 주저앉아 장화를 벗어 물을 비웠다. 그러고는 늪으로 걸어가 고인 듯한 맛이 나는 물을 좀 마시고, 뜨겁게 달궈진 총을 적시고, 얼굴과 손을 씻었다. 한결 개운해진 그는 도요새가 내려앉았던 자리로 돌아가며 침착함을 지키리라 굳게 마음먹었다.\n\n그는 차분해지려고 애썼지만, 또다시 같은 일이 벌어졌다. 그의 손가락은 새를 제대로 겨누기도 전에 방아쇠를 눌러 버렸다. 사정은 점점 더 나빠졌다.\n\n스테판 아르카지치와 다시 만나기로 한 오리나무 쪽으로 늪을 빠져나올 때, 그의 사냥 자루에는 새가 다섯 마리뿐이었다.\n\n스테판 아르카지치를 발견하기에 앞서 그는 그의 개를 보았다. 크라크가 오리나무의 비틀린 뿌리 뒤에서 튀어나왔는데, 온몸이 늪의 고약한 냄새가 나는 진창으로 새카맸고, 정복자 같은 기세로 라스카의 냄새를 맡았다. 크라크 뒤로 오리나무 그늘 속에서 스테판 아르카지치의 늘씬한 모습이 나타났다. 그는 붉게 상기되어 땀을 흘리며, 옷깃을 풀어 헤친 채, 여전히 같은 모양으로 다리를 절며 그를 맞으러 다가왔다.\n\n\"어때? 자네 펑펑 쏘아 대던데!\" 그가 사람 좋게 웃으며 말했다.\n\n\"형님은 어떻게 됐어요?\" 레빈이 물었다. 그러나 물어볼 필요도 없었으니, 이미 가득 찬 사냥 자루를 보았기 때문이다.\n\n\"오, 그럭저럭 괜찮았네.\"\n\n그는 새를 열네 마리나 잡았다.\n\n\"멋진 늪이야! 베슬로프스키가 자네한테 방해가 됐을 게 틀림없어. 개 한 마리로 사냥하는 것도 거북한 노릇이고.\" 스테판 아르카지치는 자신의 승리를 누그러뜨리려는 듯 말했다.\n\n# Chapter 11\n\n레빈과 스테판 아르카지치가 레빈이 늘 묵곤 하던 농가에 다다랐을 때, 베슬로프스키는 이미 그곳에 와 있었다. 그는 농가 한가운데 의자에 앉아 두 손으로 의자를 꽉 붙들고 있었는데, 농부 아내의 오빠인 병사가 그의 진흙투성이 장화를 벗겨 주느라 그를 잡아당기고 있었기 때문이었다. 베슬로프스키는 사람을 절로 따라 웃게 만드는 그 특유의 쾌활한 웃음을 터뜨리고 있었다.\n\n\"이제 막 도착했어요. Ils ont été charmants. 글쎄, 술도 주고 먹을 것도 주지 뭐예요! 그 빵이라니, 정말 일품이었어요! Délicieux! 보드카도, 그렇게 좋은 건 마셔 본 적이 없어요. 그런데 무엇 하나에도 한 푼도 안 받으려 하더군요. 그저 이렇게만 말하는 거예요. '저희 시골 살림이라 누추하지만 양해해 주십시오.'\"\n\n* Ils ont été charmants: 프랑스어로 '그들은 매력적이었다'. Délicieux: '맛있다'.\n\"받긴 뭘 받겠소? 손님 대접을 한 거지요, 당연히. 보드카를 팔려고 두는 줄 아쇼?\" 마침내 그을음투성이 양말에서 흠뻑 젖은 장화를 벗겨 내는 데 성공한 병사가 말했다.\n\n장화 때문에 온통 진흙이 묻고, 더러운 개들이 제 몸을 핥아 대고, 늪의 진흙 냄새와 화약 냄새가 방 안에 가득하고, 칼이며 포크도 없는 누추한 농가였지만, 일행은 사냥꾼들만이 아는 그 맛으로 차를 마시고 저녁을 먹었다. 몸을 씻고 깨끗해진 그들은 마부가 신사들을 위해 잠자리를 마련해 둔, 깨끗이 쓸어 놓은 건초 헛간으로 들어갔다.\n\n어스름이 내렸는데도 누구 하나 자려는 사람이 없었다.\n\n총이며 개며 지난날의 사냥 모임에 대한 회상과 일화 사이를 오가던 끝에, 대화는 일행 모두의 관심을 끄는 주제로 옮겨 갔다. 바센카가 향기로운 건초 사이의 이 즐거운 잠자리며, 이 즐거운 부서진 짐수레(끌채가 빠져 있어 부서진 것이라고 그는 짐작했다)며, 보드카를 대접해 준 농부들의 인심이며, 저마다 제 주인 발치에 누운 개들에 대해 여러 차례 만족을 표한 뒤, 오블론스키가 지난여름 묵었던 말투스의 집에서 열린 멋진 사냥 모임 이야기를 꺼냈다.\n\n말투스는 철도 주식 투기로 돈을 번, 널리 알려진 자본가였다. 스테판 아르카지치는 이 말투스가 트베리 지방에 사들인 멧닭 사냥터며, 그것을 어떻게 관리하는지며, 사냥 모임이 타고 다닌 마차와 이륜마차며, 늪가에 차려 놓은 점심 식사용 천막에 대해 늘어놓았다.\n\n\"난 자네를 이해할 수가 없네.\" 레빈이 건초 위에 몸을 일으키며 말했다. \"어떻게 그런 사람들이 역겹지 않을 수가 있나? 라피트와 함께하는 점심이 더없이 즐겁다는 건 이해하겠어. 하지만 바로 그 호사스러움이 싫지 않나? 그런 자들은 다, 옛날 우리 주류 독점업자들이 그랬듯이, 누구나 경멸할 만한 방식으로 돈을 벌어들이지. 그러고는 그 경멸 따위는 아랑곳하지 않다가, 부정하게 번 돈으로 자기가 마땅히 받아야 할 그 경멸을 사서 무마하는 거야.\"\n* 라피트: 프랑스 보르도산 고급 적포도주.\n\n\"전적으로 옳은 말이에요!\" 바센카 베슬로프스키가 맞장구쳤다. \"전적으로요! 오블론스키야 물론 bonhomie로 가는 거지만, 다른 사람들은 이렇게 말하거든요. '그래, 오블론스키도 그자들 집에 묵잖아.'……\"\n\n* bonhomie: 프랑스어로 '서글서글한 호의'.\n\"천만에.\" 레빈은 오블론스키가 미소를 지으며 말하고 있다는 것을 알아챌 수 있었다. \"나는 그를 다른 어떤 부유한 상인이나 귀족보다 더 부정직하다고 여기지 않을 뿐이야. 그들은 모두 똑같이 돈을 벌었어. 자기 노동과 지력으로 말이지.\"\n\n\"오, 무슨 노동으로? 이권을 손에 넣고 그걸로 투기하는 걸 노동이라 부르나?\"\n\n\"물론 노동이지. 이런 의미에서 노동이야. 그와 그 같은 사람들이 없었다면 철도도 없었을 테니까.\"\n\n\"하지만 그건 농부의 노동이나 학식 있는 직업의 노동 같은 게 아니잖아.\"\n\n\"그렇다 치지. 하지만 그의 활동이 어떤 결과를—철도를 만들어 낸다는 의미에서는 노동이야. 물론 자네는 철도가 쓸모없다고 여기겠지만.\"\n\n\"아니, 그건 다른 문제야. 철도가 유용하다는 건 인정할 용의가 있어. 하지만 들인 노동에 비해 지나친 모든 이윤은 부정직한 거야.\"\n\n\"그럼 무엇이 적당한 비례인지 누가 정하나?\"\n\n\"부정한 수단으로, 속임수로 이윤을 얻는 것 말이야.\" 레빈은 자기가 정직과 부정직 사이에 뚜렷한 선을 그을 수 없다는 것을 의식하면서 말했다. \"예컨대 은행업 같은 거지.\" 그가 말을 이었다. \"그건 악이야—노동 없이 막대한 부를 쌓아 올리는 것, 주류 독점과 똑같은 거지. 다만 형태만 바뀌었을 뿐이야. Le roi est mort, vive le roi. 주류 독점이 폐지되자마자 철도와 은행 회사들이 생겨났어. 그것도 노동 없는 이윤이지.\"\n\n* Le roi est mort, vive le roi: 프랑스어로 '왕은 죽었다, 왕이여 만세'.\n\"그래, 다 옳고 영리한 말일지도 모르지…… 엎드려, 크라크!\" 스테판 아르카지치가 건초를 온통 긁어 헤집고 뒤집어 놓고 있는 자기 개를 향해 소리쳤다. 그는 자기 입장이 옳다고 확신하는 듯, 차분하게 서두르지 않고 이야기했다. \"하지만 자네는 정직한 노동과 부정직한 노동 사이에 선을 긋지 못했어. 내가 일에 대해 나보다 더 잘 아는 수석 서기보다 더 많은 봉급을 받는다—그건 부정직한 건가, 그렇지?\"\n\n\"뭐라 말 못 하겠네.\"\n\n\"좋아, 그렇다면 내가 말해 주지. 자네가 토지에서 일한 대가로, 말하자면 오천쯤 받는 것과, 여기 우리 집주인인 농부가 아무리 죽도록 일해도 오십 루블 넘게는 결코 못 받는 것—그건 내가 수석 서기보다 더 버는 것이나, 말투스가 역장보다 더 버는 것과 똑같이 부정직한 거야. 아니, 오히려 정반대지. 내가 보기엔 사회가 이런 사람들에게 일종의 적대적인 태도를 취하는데, 그건 전혀 근거가 없는 거고, 그 밑바닥에는 시샘이 깔려 있는 것 같단 말이야……\"\n\n\"아니, 그건 부당해요.\" 베슬로프스키가 말했다. \"어떻게 시샘이 끼어들 수 있겠어요? 그런 종류의 사업에는 뭔가 마뜩잖은 데가 있어요.\"\n\n\"자네 말은,\" 레빈이 말을 이었다. \"내가 오천을 받는데 농부는 오십을 받는 게 부당하다는 거지. 그건 맞아. 부당하고, 나도 그걸 느껴. 하지만……\"\n\n\"정말 그래요. 어째서 우리는 말을 타고, 술을 마시고, 사냥을 하고, 아무것도 안 하면서 시간을 보내는데, 저 사람들은 늘 일만 하는 걸까요?\" 바센카 베슬로프스키가 말했는데, 그는 분명 난생처음 이 문제를 곰곰이 생각해 보는 것이었고, 그래서 더없이 진지하게 그것을 따지고 있었다.\n\n\"그래, 자네는 그걸 느끼면서도 자네 재산을 그에게 주지는 않잖아.\" 스테판 아르카지치가, 보아하니 일부러 레빈을 부추기려는 듯 말했다.\n\n근래 두 동서 사이에는 일종의 은밀한 적대감 같은 것이 생겨나 있었다. 마치 자매와 결혼한 뒤로 누가 자기 삶을 더 잘 꾸리고 있느냐를 두고 일종의 경쟁심이 그들 사이에 싹튼 듯했고, 이제 그 적대감이 개인적인 어조를 띠기 시작하면서 대화 속에 드러났다.\n\n\"내가 재산을 내주지 않는 건, 아무도 내게 그걸 요구하지 않기 때문이고, 또 내가 주고 싶다 한들 줄 수도 없기 때문이야.\" 레빈이 대답했다. \"줄 사람도 없고.\"\n\n\"이 농부에게 주게. 거절하지 않을 테니.\"\n\n\"그래, 하지만 내가 어떻게 내준단 말인가? 그에게 가서 양도 증서라도 써 줄까?\"\n\n\"난 모르겠네. 하지만 자네가 권리가 없다고 확신한다면……\"\n\n\"난 전혀 확신하지 않아. 오히려 나는 그걸 내줄 권리가 없다고 느껴. 토지에 대해서도, 내 가족에 대해서도 의무가 있으니까.\"\n\n\"아니, 미안하지만, 자네가 이 불평등이 부당하다고 여긴다면, 어째서 그에 맞게 행동하지 않는 건가?……\"\n\n\"음, 나는 그 생각에 따라 소극적으로는 행동하고 있어. 그와 나 사이에 존재하는 처지의 격차를 더 벌리지 않으려 한다는 점에서는 말이야.\"\n\n\"아니, 미안하지만, 그건 궤변이야.\"\n\n\"그래요, 거기엔 뭔가 궤변 같은 데가 있어요.\" 베슬로프스키가 동의했다. \"아! 집주인 양반, 그래 아직 안 잤소?\" 그는 삐걱거리는 문을 열고 헛간으로 들어온 농부에게 말했다. \"어째서 안 자는 거요?\"\n\n\"아니요, 어떻게 자겠습니까! 나리들이 주무시는 줄 알았는데, 두런두런 말소리가 들리더군요. 여기서 갈고리를 하나 가져가려고요. 이놈이 물진 않겠지요?\" 그는 맨발로 조심스럽게 발을 디디며 덧붙였다.\n\n\"그런데 자네는 어디서 잘 건가?\"\n\n\"우리는 가축들하고 밤을 새우러 나갑니다.\"\n\n\"아, 정말 멋진 밤이군!\" 베슬로프스키가, 활짝 열린 문의 커다란 틀 안으로 저녁놀의 희미한 빛을 받아 보이는 헛간 가장자리와 멍에 풀린 짐마차를 내다보며 말했다. \"그런데 들어 봐요, 여자들 노랫소리가 들리네요. 게다가 정말이지 솜씨도 나쁘지 않은데. 저거 누가 노래하는 거요, 친구?\"\n\n\"바로 이 근처 처녀들입니다.\"\n\n\"가요, 산책이나 합시다! 어차피 잠도 안 올 텐데. 오블론스키, 같이 갑시다!\"\n\n\"여기 누워 있는 것과 가는 것, 둘 다 할 수만 있다면 좋으련만.\" 오블론스키가 기지개를 켜며 대답했다. \"여기 누워 있는 게 기막히게 좋단 말이야.\"\n\n\"좋아요, 그럼 나 혼자 가지요.\" 베슬로프스키가 벌떡 일어나 양말과 신발을 신으며 말했다. \"안녕히들 계세요, 신사 여러분. 재미있으면 모시러 올게요. 좋은 사냥거리를 베풀어 주셨으니, 그 은혜는 잊지 않겠어요.\"\n\n\"저 친구 정말 기막힌 사람이지, 안 그래?\" 베슬로프스키가 나가고 농부가 그의 뒤로 문을 닫자, 스테판 아르카지치가 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 기막힌 사람이야.\" 레빈이, 여전히 방금 전 대화의 주제를 생각하며 대답했다. 그가 보기에 자기는 능력껏 최선을 다해 자기 생각과 감정을 분명하게 표현한 것 같은데, 정직하고 어리석지도 않은 두 사람이 한목소리로 그가 궤변으로 스스로를 위로하고 있다고 말한 것이었다. 이 일이 그의 마음을 어지럽혔다.\n\n\"바로 이거라네, 이 친구야. 둘 중 하나는 해야 해. 현존하는 사회 질서가 정당하다고 인정하고 그 안에서 자기 권리를 지키든가, 아니면 자네가 나처럼 부당한 특권을 누리고 있음을 인정하고 그것을 즐기며 만족하든가.\"\n\n\"아니야, 만약 그게 부당하다면 자네는 그 이점을 누리면서 만족할 수 없을 거야—적어도 나는 못 해. 나에게 가장 중요한 건 내가 비난받을 처지가 아니라고 느끼는 거야.\"\n\n\"그건 그렇고, 어때, 끝내 안 갈 텐가?\" 스테판 아르카지치가, 보아하니 생각의 긴장에 지친 듯 말했다. \"어차피 잠도 안 올 텐데. 자, 가세!\"\n\n레빈은 대답하지 않았다. 대화 중에 그들이 한 말, 즉 자기가 오직 소극적인 의미에서만 정당하게 행동한다는 그 말이 그의 생각을 사로잡았다. '정당해질 수 있는 게 오직 소극적으로만 가능하다는 게 정말일까?' 그는 자신에게 묻고 있었다.\n\n\"그런데 갓 벤 건초 냄새가 어찌나 강한지.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 일어서며 말했다. \"도무지 잘 가망이 없군. 바센카가 저기서 뭔가 재미난 일을 벌이고 있어. 웃음소리며 저 친구 목소리가 들리지? 우리도 가는 게 낫지 않겠나? 자, 같이 가세!\"\n\n\"아니, 난 안 가.\" 레빈이 대답했다.\n\n\"설마 그것도 원칙의 문제는 아니겠지.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 어둠 속에서 모자를 더듬어 찾으며 미소 띤 채 말했다.\n\n\"원칙의 문제는 아니야. 하지만 내가 왜 가야 하나?\"\n\n\"하지만 말이지, 자네는 스스로 골칫거리를 만들고 있는 거야.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 모자를 찾아 들고 일어서며 말했다.\n\n\"어째서?\"\n\n\"내가 자네가 아내한테 어떤 태도를 취하고 있는지 못 본 줄 아나? 자네가 이틀쯤 사냥하러 떠나도 되느냐 안 되느냐가 무슨 막중한 문제라도 되는 양 하더군. 전원시 같은 한 장면으로야 다 좋지. 하지만 평생을 그렇게 살 수는 없는 거야. 남자란 독립적이어야 해. 남자에겐 남자의 관심사가 있는 거니까. 남자는 사내다워야 하는 거야.\" 오블론스키가 문을 열며 말했다.\n\n\"어떤 식으로? 하녀 처녀들이나 쫓아다니면서?\" 레빈이 말했다.\n\n\"재미있다는데 못 할 거 뭐 있나? Ça ne tire pas à conséquence. 내 아내에게 해가 될 것도 없고, 나야 즐거우니까. 중요한 건 가정의 신성함을 존중하는 거야. 가정 안에서는 아무 일도 없어야 하고. 하지만 자기 손발을 스스로 묶진 말라고.\"\n\n* Ça ne tire pas à conséquence: 프랑스어로 '그건 별일 아니다, 뒤탈 없는 일이다'.\n\n\"그럴지도 모르지.\" 레빈이 무뚝뚝하게 말하고는 모로 돌아누웠다. \"내일은 아침 일찍 사냥하러 나갈 거야. 아무도 깨우지 않고, 동틀 무렵에 떠날 거고.\"\n\"Messieurs, venez vite!\" 베슬로프스키가 돌아오며 외치는 소리가 들렸다. \"Charmante! 굉장한 걸 발견했어요. Charmante! 완벽한 그레트헨이에요. 벌써 친해졌다니까요. 정말이지 더없이 예뻐요.\" 그는, 마치 그 처녀가 오로지 자기를 위해 예쁘게 만들어지기라도 한 양, 자기에게 베풀어진 여흥에 대한 만족을 표하는 듯한 흡족한 어조로 단언했다.\n\n* Messieurs, venez vite!: 프랑스어로 '여러분, 어서 와요!'. Charmante: '매력적이다'. 그레트헨: 괴테의 「파우스트」에 나오는 순박한 처녀.\n\n레빈은 자는 척했고, 오블론스키는 슬리퍼를 신고 시가에 불을 붙이고는 헛간을 나갔으며, 이윽고 그들의 목소리는 멀어져 사라졌다.\n레빈은 한참 동안 잠을 이루지 못했다. 말들이 건초를 우적우적 씹는 소리가 들렸고, 이어 농부와 그의 큰아들이 밤을 새울 채비를 하고 가축들과 함께 야간 망을 보러 나가는 소리가 들렸으며, 그다음에는 병사가 헛간 저편에서, 집주인 농부의 작은아들인 조카와 함께 잠자리를 펴는 소리가 들렸다. 사내아이가 가느다란 목소리로 삼촌에게 개들에 대한 자기 생각을—그에게 개들은 거대하고 무시무시한 짐승으로 보이는 모양이었다—이야기하며 내일 개들이 무엇을 사냥하느냐고 묻는 소리가 들렸고, 병사가 잠에 겨운 쉰 목소리로 나리들이 아침에 늪으로 가서 총으로 사냥을 할 거라고 일러 주는 소리가 들렸다. 그러고는 아이의 질문을 막으려고 \"자거라, 바스카. 자, 안 그러면 혼날 줄 알아.\" 하더니, 곧 코를 골기 시작했고, 모든 것이 고요해졌다. 들리는 것이라곤 말들이 코를 푸는 소리와 도요새의 목쉰 울음소리뿐이었다.\n\n'정말로 그저 소극적인 것뿐일까?' 그는 속으로 되뇌었다. '뭐, 그렇다 한들? 내 잘못은 아니잖아.' 그러고는 다음 날에 대해 생각하기 시작했다.\n\n'내일은 일찍 나가서, 꼭 침착함을 잃지 말아야지. 도요새도 많고, 멧닭도 있어. 돌아오면 키티의 쪽지가 와 있겠지. 그래, 스티바 말이 맞을지도 몰라. 난 그녀에게 사내답지 못해. 치맛자락에 매여 있는 거지…… 뭐, 어쩔 수 없지! 또 소극적이군……'\n\n비몽사몽간에 그는 베슬로프스키와 스테판 아르카지치의 웃음소리며 흥겨운 이야기 소리를 들었다. 잠깐 눈을 떴다. 달이 떠 있었고, 달빛에 환히 비친 활짝 열린 문간에 그들이 서서 이야기하고 있었다. 스테판 아르카지치가 한 처녀의 싱그러움에 대해 뭐라고 하며 그녀를 갓 깐 호두에 비유하고 있었고, 베슬로프스키는 그 특유의 사람을 따라 웃게 만드는 웃음을 터뜨리며, 아마도 어느 농부가 그에게 한 말인 듯한 어떤 구절을 되뇌고 있었다. \"아, 자네 그 아가씨 꼬여 보려고 별짓을 다 하는구먼!\" 레빈은 비몽사몽간에 말했다.\n\n\"여러분, 내일 동트기 전에!\" 그러고는 잠이 들었다.\n\n# Chapter 12\n\n이른 새벽에 잠에서 깬 레빈은 일행을 깨우려고 애썼다. 바센카는 배를 깔고 누운 채 양말 신은 한쪽 다리를 쑥 내밀고는 어찌나 깊이 잠들었는지 아무리 해도 반응을 끌어낼 수 없었다. 오블론스키는 비몽사몽간에 이렇게 일찍 일어나기를 마다했다. 건초 속에 몸을 둥글게 말고 자던 라스카조차 마지못해 일어나, 굼뜨게 뒷다리를 하나씩 차례로 쭉 뻗어 펴는 것이었다. 레빈은 장화와 양말을 신고 총을 챙긴 뒤, 삐걱거리는 헛간 문을 조심스레 열고 길로 나섰다. 마부들은 마차 안에서 자고 있었고 말들은 졸고 있었다. 오직 한 마리만이 구유에 코를 박고 굼뜨게 귀리를 씹고 있었다. 바깥은 아직 잿빛이었다.\n\n\"왜 이리 일찍 일어났수, 양반?\" 그들의 안주인인 노파가 오두막에서 나오며, 오랜 벗을 대하듯 정답게 그에게 말을 건넸다.\n\n\"사냥하러 가는 길이오, 할멈. 늪으로 가려면 이쪽으로 가면 되오?\"\n\n\"뒤로 곧장 나가시구려. 우리 탈곡장하고 삼밭을 지나면 작은 오솔길이 하나 있다우.\" 노파는 햇볕에 그을린 맨발로 조심스레 걸음을 옮기며 레빈을 안내했고, 탈곡장 옆의 울타리를 옆으로 치워 그가 지나가게 해 주었다.\n\n\"곧장 가면 늪이 나온다우. 어제저녁에 우리 집 애들이 거기로 소를 몰고 갔지.\"\n\n라스카가 오솔길을 따라 신이 나서 앞서 달려 나갔다. 레빈은 가볍고 빠른 걸음으로 그 뒤를 따르며 끊임없이 하늘을 올려다보았다. 그는 늪에 닿기 전에 해가 떠오르지 않기를 바랐다. 그러나 해는 머뭇거리지 않았다. 그가 나설 때만 해도 환했던 달은 이제 수은빛 초승달처럼 희미하게 빛날 뿐이었다. 조금 전까지만 해도 절로 눈에 들어오던 새벽의 분홍빛 노을은, 이제 일부러 찾지 않으면 분간조차 할 수 없게 되었다. 멀리 들판에서 윤곽이 잡히지 않던 어슴푸레한 얼룩들이 이제는 또렷이 보였다. 그것은 호밀 단이었다. 해가 떠오르기 전까지는 보이지 않던 이슬이, 이미 꽃가루가 다 떨어진 키 큰 향기로운 삼밭에서 레빈의 다리와 허리띠 위쪽 블라우스를 적셨다. 투명한 아침의 정적 속에서는 아주 작은 소리까지 들렸다. 벌 한 마리가 총알 같은 윙 소리를 내며 레빈의 귓가를 스쳐 날아갔다. 그가 가만히 살펴보니 두 번째, 세 번째 벌이 눈에 들어왔다. 모두 울타리 뒤의 벌통에서 날아와, 삼밭 위로 늪 방향으로 사라졌다. 오솔길은 곧장 늪으로 이어졌다. 늪은 거기서 피어오르는 안개로 알아볼 수 있었는데, 안개는 한쪽은 짙고 다른 쪽은 옅어서, 갈대와 버드나무 덤불이 그 안개 속에서 섬처럼 일렁였다. 늪과 길의 가장자리에는 밤새 가축을 친 농부 아이들과 사내들이 누워 있었는데, 새벽녘에 모두 외투를 덮고 잠들어 있었다. 그들에게서 멀지 않은 곳에 발이 묶인 말 세 마리가 있었다. 그중 한 마리가 쇠사슬을 절그럭거렸다. 라스카는 주인 곁에서 조금 앞으로 나서며 주위를 둘러보며 걸었다. 잠든 농부들을 지나 첫 갈대밭에 이른 레빈은 권총을 살펴본 뒤 개를 풀어놓았다. 윤기 흐르는 짙은 갈색의 세 살배기 말 한 마리가 개를 보고는 놀라 뒷걸음치며 꼬리를 휘젓고 콧김을 뿜었다. 다른 말들도 놀라 묶인 다리로 물을 첨벙거리며, 진창에서 발굽을 철벅철벅 빼내며 늪 밖으로 뛰쳐나갔다. 라스카는 말들을 비웃듯 바라보고 레빈을 향해 묻는 듯한 눈길을 보내며 멈춰 섰다. 레빈은 라스카를 토닥이고는 이제 시작해도 좋다는 신호로 휘파람을 불었다.\n\n라스카는 발밑에서 출렁이는 진창을 가로질러 기쁘게, 그러나 조바심을 내며 달렸다.\n\n뿌리며 늪의 풀이며 진흙의 익숙한 냄새, 그리고 말똥의 낯선 냄새 사이로 늪에 뛰어든 라스카는, 늪 전체에 퍼져 있는 어떤 냄새를, 그러니까 늘 그 어떤 새보다도 자기를 흥분시키는 그 독한 냄새를 가진 새의 냄새를 단번에 알아챘다. 이끼며 늪풀 사이 여기저기에 그 냄새가 무척 강했지만, 어느 방향으로 갈수록 짙어지고 옅어지는지는 가늠할 수 없었다. 방향을 잡으려면 바람에서 더 멀어져야 했다. 다리의 움직임도 느끼지 못한 채, 라스카는 한 번 뛸 때마다 단번에 멈출 수 있도록 뻣뻣한 질주로 껑충껑충 뛰어, 해 뜨기 전 동쪽에서 불어오는 바람을 피해 오른쪽으로 갔다가, 바람을 마주 보고 돌아섰다. 벌어진 콧구멍으로 공기를 들이켜며, 라스카는 단번에 그들의 발자취뿐 아니라 그들 자신이, 그것도 한 마리가 아니라 여러 마리가 자기 앞에 있다는 것을 알아챘다. 라스카는 속도를 늦췄다. 그들이 여기 있긴 한데, 정확히 어디인지는 아직 알 수 없었다. 바로 그 자리를 찾으려고 그녀가 원을 그리기 시작했을 때, 갑자기 주인의 목소리가 그녀를 끌어냈다. \"라스카! 여기?\" 그가 다른 방향을 가리키며 물었다. 그녀는 멈춰 서서, 자기가 시작한 대로 계속하는 게 낫지 않겠느냐고 그에게 물었다. 그러나 그는 화난 목소리로 명령을 되풀이하며, 아무것도 있을 리 없는 물로 덮인 곳을 가리켰다. 그녀는 그를 기쁘게 해 주려고 찾는 척하며 복종하여 그곳을 한 바퀴 돌고는, 본래의 자리로 돌아왔고, 곧 다시 그 냄새를 느꼈다. 이제 그가 방해하지 않으니 그녀는 무엇을 해야 할지 알았고, 발밑에 무엇이 있는지 보지도 않은 채—분하게도 높은 그루터기에 걸려 물에 빠지긴 했지만, 튼튼하고 유연한 다리로 몸을 바로 세우며—모든 것을 분명히 밝혀 줄 원을 그리기 시작했다. 그들의 냄새가 점점 더 강하게, 점점 더 또렷하게 그녀에게 다가왔고, 별안간 그녀에게 한 마리가 여기, 이 갈대 덤불 뒤, 다섯 걸음 앞에 있다는 것이 더없이 분명해졌다. 그녀는 멈춰 섰고, 온몸이 고요하게 굳었다. 짧은 다리로는 앞에 아무것도 보이지 않았지만, 냄새로 그것이 다섯 걸음도 안 되는 곳에 앉아 있음을 알았다. 그녀는 그것을 점점 더 또렷이 의식하며, 미리부터 그 즐거움을 맛보며 가만히 서 있었다. 꼬리는 곧고 팽팽하게 뻗은 채 맨 끝만 흔들렸다. 입은 조금 벌어졌고 귀는 쫑긋 섰다. 한쪽 귀는 달려오느라 안팎이 뒤집혀 있었고, 그녀는 무겁게, 그러나 조심스럽게 숨을 쉬었고, 더더욱 조심스럽게 주위를 둘러보았으나, 고개보다는 눈으로 주인을 향해 돌아보았다. 그는 그녀에게 너무도 익숙한 얼굴로 다가오고 있었는데, 그 눈만은 그녀에게 늘 무서웠다. 그는 다가오면서 그루터기에 걸려 비틀거렸고, 그녀가 보기에는 유난히 느릿느릿 움직였다. 그녀는 그가 느리게 온다고 여겼지만, 사실 그는 달리고 있었다.\n\n뒷발로 큼직한 자국을 긁어 파듯이 몸을 잔뜩 웅크리고 입을 조금 벌린 라스카의 그 특이한 자세를 알아챈 레빈은, 그녀가 들꿩을 가리키고 있음을 알았고, 행운을, 특히 첫 새에 행운이 따르기를 마음속으로 빌며 그녀에게 달려갔다. 그녀 곁에 바짝 다가서자, 키가 큰 그는 그녀 너머를 볼 수 있었고, 그녀가 코로 보고 있던 것을 자기 눈으로 보았다. 작은 덤불 두 개 사이의 빈 공간, 두어 야드 거리에 들꿩 한 마리가 보였다. 그것은 고개를 돌린 채 귀를 기울이고 있었다. 그러더니 가볍게 깃을 다듬고 날개를 접고는, 꼬리를 어색하게 한 번 흔들며 모퉁이 뒤로 사라졌다.\n\n\"물어 와, 물어 와!\" 레빈이 라스카를 뒤에서 떠밀며 외쳤다.\n\n'하지만 갈 수가 없어.' 라스카는 생각했다. '어디로 가라는 거지? 여기서는 냄새가 느껴지는데, 앞으로 나가면 그들이 어디 있는지, 누구인지 아무것도 모르게 될 텐데.' 그러나 그때 그가 무릎으로 그녀를 떠밀며 흥분한 속삭임으로 말했다. \"물어 와, 라스카.\"\n\n'좋아, 그게 그가 바라는 거라면 하겠어, 하지만 이제 나도 나를 책임질 수 없어.' 그녀는 그렇게 생각하며 다리가 닿는 데까지 빠르게 빽빽한 덤불 사이로 쏜살같이 달려 나갔다. 이제 그녀는 아무 냄새도 맡지 못했다. 다만 아무것도 이해하지 못한 채 보고 들을 수 있을 뿐이었다.\n\n전에 있던 자리에서 열 걸음 떨어진 곳에서 들꿩 한 마리가 목쉰 울음소리와 날개의 독특한 둥근 소리를 내며 날아올랐다. 그리고 총성이 울리자마자 그것은 하얀 가슴팍으로 젖은 진창에 무겁게 첨벙 떨어졌다. 또 한 마리는 머뭇거리지 않고, 개도 없이 레빈의 등 뒤에서 날아올랐다. 레빈이 그쪽으로 몸을 돌렸을 때는 이미 제법 멀리 가 있었다. 그러나 그의 총알이 그것을 맞혔다. 스무 걸음을 더 날아간 두 번째 들꿩은 위로 솟구쳤다가, 공처럼 빙글빙글 돌며 마른 땅에 무겁게 떨어졌다.\n\n'이거, 제법 잘되겠는걸!' 레빈은 따뜻하고 통통한 들꿩을 사냥 자루에 챙겨 넣으며 생각했다. '어때, 라스카, 잘되겠지?'\n\n레빈이 총을 장전한 뒤 다시 나아갈 무렵에는, 비록 먹구름에 가려 보이지는 않았지만 해가 완전히 떠올라 있었다. 달은 광채를 다 잃고 하늘에 뜬 흰 구름 같았다. 별은 단 하나도 보이지 않았다. 조금 전까지 이슬에 은빛으로 반짝이던 사초는 이제 금빛으로 빛났다. 고인 웅덩이들은 모두 호박빛이었다. 풀의 푸른빛은 누르스름한 초록으로 바뀌어 있었다. 늪의 새들이 지저귀며, 이슬에 반짝이고 긴 그림자를 드리운 개울가와 덤불 위로 떼 지어 모여들었다. 매 한 마리가 잠에서 깨어 건초 더미 위에 내려앉아, 고개를 이리저리 돌리며 불만스럽게 늪을 내려다보았다. 까마귀들이 들판 위를 날아다녔고, 맨다리의 소년 하나가 말들을 한 노인에게 몰고 가고 있었는데, 노인은 긴 외투 밑에서 일어나 머리를 빗고 있었다. 총에서 나는 연기는 풀의 초록 위로 우윳빛처럼 하였다.\n\n소년들 가운데 하나가 레빈에게로 달려왔다.\n\n\"아저씨, 어제 여기 오리들이 있었어요!\" 그가 외치고는 그의 뒤를 조금 떨어져 따라왔다.\n\n그리고 레빈은, 자신을 흐뭇해하는 그 소년이 보는 앞에서 도요새 세 마리를 잇따라 단번에 잡은 것이 갑절로 기뻤다.\n\n# Chapter 13\n\n첫 짐승이나 첫 새를 놓치지 않으면 그날은 운수가 좋다는 사냥꾼들의 속설은 옳은 것으로 드러났다.\n\n스무 베르스타나 걸은 끝에 지치고 배고프고 행복해진 레빈은 열 시에 좋은 사냥감 열아홉 마리와 오리 한 마리를 들고 잠자리로 돌아왔다. 오리는 사냥 자루에 들어가지 않아 허리띠에 매달았다. 그의 일행은 이미 오래전에 깨어 배가 고파져 아침까지 먹은 뒤였다.\n\n\"잠깐, 잠깐만, 열아홉 마리인 거 다 알아.\" 레빈은 멧닭과 도요새를 다시 한 번 세며 말했다. 그것들은 날아다닐 때와 달리 지금은 구부정하고 바싹 마른 채 피로 얼룩지고 고개가 한쪽으로 꺾여, 훨씬 보잘것없어 보였다.\n\n수가 맞았고, 스테판 아르카지치가 부러워하는 모습에 레빈은 흐뭇해졌다. 돌아와 보니 키티가 쪽지를 들려 보낸 심부름꾼이 이미 와 있는 것을 알고 그는 또 한 번 기뻤다.\n\n\"저는 아주 잘 지내고 행복해요. 제 걱정을 하셨다면, 이제 어느 때보다 마음을 놓으셔도 돼요. 새로 호위병이 한 명 생겼답니다, 마리야 블라시예브나라고요.\" 이는 산파로, 레빈의 가정생활에서 새롭고도 중요한 인물이었다. \"그분이 저를 보러 오셨어요. 제가 아주 건강하다고 하셨고, 당신이 돌아오실 때까지 붙잡아 두었어요. 다들 행복하고 무탈하니, 부디 서둘러 돌아오려 하지 마세요. 사냥이 잘되거든 하루 더 머무르셔도 좋아요.\"\n\n운 좋은 사냥과 아내의 편지라는 이 두 가지 기쁨이 너무나 컸던 터라, 살짝 언짢은 두 가지 사건도 레빈을 가볍게 스쳐 지나갔다. 하나는 전날 분명히 과하게 부려 먹은 밤색 곁말이 여물도 마다하고 시들시들해진 것이었다. 마부는 말했다. \"어제 너무 부려 먹은 겁니다, 콘스탄틴 드미트리치. 정말이라니까요! 십 베르스타를 무작정 내달렸으니!\"\n\n다른 불쾌한 사건은 처음 잠깐은 그의 좋은 기분을 망쳐 놓았으나, 나중에는 한참을 두고 웃었던 일로, 키티가 일주일은 너끈히 버틸 만큼 넉넉히 챙겨 준 그 많은 식량 가운데 남은 것이라곤 하나도 없다는 사실을 알게 된 것이었다. 돌아오는 길에 사냥으로 지치고 배가 고팠던 레빈은 고기 파이를 어찌나 또렷이 떠올렸던지, 라스카가 사냥감 냄새를 맡듯 오두막에 다가가면서 그 냄새와 맛이 느껴지는 것만 같았고, 곧장 필리프에게 좀 가져오라고 일렀다. 그런데 파이는 한 조각도 남지 않았고, 닭고기조차 없는 모양이었다.\n\n\"이런, 저 친구 식욕 좀 보게!\" 스테판 아르카지치가 바센카 베슬롭스키를 가리키며 웃으며 말했다. \"나도 식욕이 떨어지는 법이 없는 사람이지만, 저 친구는 정말 대단해!……\"\n\n\"뭐, 어쩔 수 없지.\" 레빈은 베슬롭스키를 침울하게 바라보며 말했다. \"그럼 필리프, 쇠고기라도 좀 주게.\"\n\n\"쇠고기는 다 먹었고, 뼈다귀는 개들에게 주었습니다.\" 필리프가 대답했다.\n\n레빈은 어찌나 서운했던지 분이 난 어조로 말했다. \"내 몫으로 좀 남겨 두었으면 좋았잖아!\" 그는 울고 싶은 심정이었다.\n\n\"그럼 사냥감을 치워 두게.\" 그는 바센카를 보지 않으려 애쓰며 떨리는 목소리로 필리프에게 말했다. \"쐐기풀로 덮어 두고. 그리고 적어도 우유라도 좀 청해 다 주게.\"\n\n그러나 우유를 좀 마시고 나자, 그는 낯선 사람 앞에서 짜증을 드러낸 것이 곧바로 부끄러워졌고, 배고픈 분풀이를 했던 자신을 두고 웃기 시작했다.\n\n저녁이 되자 그들은 다시 사냥을 나갔고, 베슬롭스키는 몇 차례 멋지게 맞혔으며, 밤에는 마차를 몰아 집으로 돌아왔다.\n\n집으로 돌아오는 길은 떠나올 때만큼이나 활기찼다. 베슬롭스키는 노래를 부르고, 농부들과 겪은 일을 신나게 늘어놓았다. 농부들은 그에게 보드카를 대접하며 \"촌스러운 살림이라 죄송합니다요\"라고 했다는 둥, 밤에는 술래잡기 놀이며 하녀와 농부와 얽힌 일이며, 한 농부가 그에게 결혼했느냐고 물었다가 안 했다는 걸 알고는 \"그럼 남의 마누라 뒤꽁무니나 쫓아다니지 말고, 자네 마누라부터 하나 얻는 게 낫겠구먼\"이라고 했다는 둥 떠들어 댔다. 이 말이 베슬롭스키에게는 유난히 우스웠던 모양이었다.\n\n\"통틀어 이번 나들이가 무척 즐거웠습니다. 당신은요, 레빈?\"\n\n\"나도, 아주 즐거웠습니다.\" 레빈은 진심으로 말했다. 집에서 바센카 베슬롭스키에게 품었던 적의를 떨쳐 내고, 그 대신 더없이 다정한 마음을 느끼게 된 것이 그에게는 특히나 기뻤다.\n\n# Chapter 14\n\n이튿날 열 시, 이미 농장을 한 바퀴 둘러보고 온 레빈은 바센카가 하룻밤 묵게 된 방의 문을 두드렸다.\n\n\"Entrez!\" 베슬롭스키가 그를 불렀다. \"미안합니다, 막 세수를 끝냈거든요.\" 그는 속옷 차림으로 레빈 앞에 선 채 웃으며 말했다.\n* Entrez: 프랑스어로 '들어오세요'.\n\n\"저는 신경 쓰지 마십시오.\" 레빈은 창가에 앉았다. \"잘 주무셨습니까?\"\n\n\"죽은 듯이 잤습니다. 사냥하기에는 어떤 날씨인가요?\"\n\n\"뭘 드시겠습니까, 차, 아니면 커피?\"\n\n\"둘 다 됐습니다. 점심까지 기다리지요. 정말 부끄럽군요. 부인들은 벌써 내려오셨겠죠? 지금 산책하면 딱 좋겠는데요. 말을 좀 보여 주십시오.\"\n\n정원을 거닐고, 마구간에 들르고, 심지어 평행봉에서 함께 체조까지 좀 한 뒤에, 레빈은 손님과 함께 집으로 돌아와 그를 데리고 응접실로 들어갔다.\n\n\"사냥이 정말 근사했어요, 즐거운 일이 어찌나 많던지!\" 베슬롭스키가 사모바르 앞에 앉아 있는 키티에게 다가가며 말했다. \"부인들이 이런 즐거움을 누리지 못한다니 안타까운 일입니다!\"\n\n'뭐, 안주인에게 무슨 말이든 해야겠지.' 레빈은 속으로 생각했다. 그런데 또 그 미소에서, 손님이 키티에게 말을 거는 그 거침없는 태도에서 뭔가가 느껴지는 것만 같았다…….\n\n공작 부인은 식탁 반대편에 마리야 블라시예브나, 스테판 아르카지치와 함께 앉아 있다가 레빈을 자기 곁으로 불러, 키티의 해산을 위해 모스크바로 옮기는 일이며 그들을 위해 방을 준비하는 일에 관해 이야기하기 시작했다. 레빈이 결혼식 준비의 온갖 자질구레한 일들을 그 행사의 장엄함을 깎아내리는 것으로 여겨 싫어했던 것처럼, 이제 그는 다가오는 출산을 위한 준비가 한층 더 거슬렸다. 그들은 출산 날짜를 손가락으로 꼽아 가며 헤아리는 것만 같았다. 그는 곧 태어날 아기를 위한 배냇저고리의 본을 어떤 게 가장 좋은지 따지는 이런 이야기들에 귀를 닫으려고 애썼고, 돌리가 각별히 중요하게 여기는 그 신비로운, 끝도 없는 뜨개질 천이며 삼각형으로 접은 아마포 따위를 보지 않으려고 고개를 돌렸다. 그에게 약속된, 그러나 너무도 경이로워 아직도 믿어지지 않는 아들의 탄생은(그는 틀림없이 아들이리라고 확신했다) 한편으로는 너무도 엄청나서 도무지 믿을 수 없는 행복으로, 다른 한편으로는 너무도 신비로운 사건으로 그의 마음속에 떠올랐기에, 무슨 일이 일어날지를 확실히 안다는 듯이 단정하고 사람들에게 으레 일어나는 평범한 일이라도 되는 양 그에 대비하는 이 태도가 그에게는 혼란스럽고 모욕적으로 느껴져 거슬렸다.\n\n그러나 공작 부인은 그의 심정을 이해하지 못했고, 그가 그 일에 관해 생각하고 말하기를 꺼리는 것을 무심함과 무관심 탓으로 돌리고는 그를 가만히 내버려 두지 않았다. 부인은 스테판 아르카지치에게 셋집을 알아보라고 일러 둔 터라, 이제 레빈을 불렀다.\n\n\"저는 그런 건 아무것도 모릅니다, 어머님. 알아서 좋으실 대로 하시지요.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"언제 옮길지는 자네가 정해야지.\"\n\n\"정말 모르겠습니다. 모스크바를 떠난 곳에서도 수백만의 아이가 태어나고, 의사들도…… 왜…….\"\n\"하지만 그렇다 해도…….\"\n\n\"아, 아닙니다, 키티가 원하는 대로 하지요.\"\n\n\"이 일을 어떻게 키티와 의논하겠나! 그 애를 겁주라는 건가? 글쎄, 이번 봄에 나탈리야 골리치나가 무지한 의사를 만나는 바람에 죽었다네.\"\n\n\"말씀하시는 대로 하겠습니다.\" 그가 침울하게 말했다.\n\n공작 부인이 그에게 말을 이어 갔지만, 그는 듣고 있지 않았다. 공작 부인과의 대화가 그를 거슬리게 한 것은 사실이었으나, 그가 침울한 것은 그 대화 때문이 아니라 사모바르 앞에서 본 광경 때문이었다.\n\n'아냐, 있을 수 없는 일이야.' 그는 키티에게 몸을 숙인 채 매력적인 미소를 띠고 무언가 이야기하는 바센카를, 그리고 얼굴을 붉히며 안절부절못하는 키티를 이따금 흘끔거리며 생각했다.\n\n바센카의 자세에도, 그 눈빛에도, 그 미소에도 뭔가 마뜩잖은 데가 있었다. 레빈은 심지어 키티의 자세와 눈빛에서도 뭔가 마뜩잖은 것을 보았다. 그러자 다시 그의 눈에서 빛이 꺼졌다. 또다시, 전처럼, 별안간, 어떠한 단계도 거치지 않고, 그는 행복과 평화와 존엄의 절정에서 절망과 분노와 모멸의 나락으로 곤두박질치는 기분이었다. 다시 모든 것과 모든 사람이 그에게 가증스럽게 느껴졌다.\n\n\"좋으실 대로 하십시오, 어머님.\" 그는 다시 한번 주위를 둘러보며 말했다.\n\n\"모노마흐의 관은 무거운 법이지.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 장난스럽게 말했다. 분명 공작 부인과의 대화만이 아니라, 자기가 눈치챈 레빈의 동요의 원인까지 넌지시 빗댄 말이었다.\n* 모노마흐의 관: 러시아 차르의 대관식에 쓰이던 왕관. 푸시킨의 희곡 「보리스 고두노프」의 대사로, 권력에 따르는 무거운 책임을 뜻한다.\n\n\"오늘은 어쩜 이렇게 늦었니, 돌리!\"\n\n모두 다리야 알렉산드로브나를 맞으려고 일어섰다. 바센카만은 잠깐 몸을 일으켰을 뿐, 요즘 젊은이 특유의 부인들에 대한 무례함으로 거의 고개를 숙이지도 않은 채 무언가에 웃으며 다시 이야기를 이어 갔다.\n\n\"마샤 때문에 마음을 졸였어. 잠을 푹 자지 못해서 오늘 영 보채는구나.\" 돌리가 말했다.\n\n바센카가 키티와 시작한 대화는 전날 저녁과 똑같은 흐름으로, 안나에 대해, 그리고 사랑을 세속적인 고려보다 위에 두어야 하는가에 대해 떠들고 있었다. 키티는 그 대화가 싫었고, 화제 자체와 그것이 다루어지는 어조 때문에, 또 그것이 남편에게 미칠 영향을 알기에 마음이 불편했다. 그러나 그녀는 너무 순박하고 천진해서 이 대화를 어떻게 끊어야 할지 몰랐고, 그 젊은이의 너무도 노골적인 찬사가 그녀에게 안겨 주는 피상적인 즐거움을 감출 줄조차 몰랐다. 그녀는 그것을 멈추고 싶었지만, 어찌해야 할지 알 수 없었다. 자기가 무엇을 하든 남편이 지켜볼 것이고, 거기에 가장 나쁜 해석을 붙이리라는 것을 그녀는 알았다. 그리고 실제로, 그녀가 마샤에게 무슨 일이 있느냐고 돌리에게 물었을 때, 바센카가 이 시시한 대화가 끝나기를 기다렸다가 무심히 돌리를 바라보기 시작했을 때, 그 질문은 레빈에게 부자연스럽고 역겨운 위선으로 비쳤다.\n\n\"어때, 오늘 버섯 따러 갈까?\" 돌리가 말했다.\n\n\"좋아요, 꼭 가요, 저도 갈게요.\" 키티는 이렇게 말하고는 얼굴을 붉혔다. 그녀는 예의상 바센카에게 같이 가겠느냐고 묻고 싶었지만 묻지 않았다. \"어디 가요, 코스챠?\" 단호한 걸음으로 자기 곁을 지나치는 남편에게 그녀가 죄지은 듯한 얼굴로 물었다. 이 죄스러운 기색이 그의 온갖 의심을 굳혀 주었다.\n\n\"내가 없는 동안 기계공이 왔어. 아직 못 만나 봤거든.\" 그는 그녀를 보지 않은 채 말했다.\n\n그는 아래층으로 내려갔는데, 미처 서재를 떠나기도 전에 아내가 무모한 속도로 그에게 달려오는 낯익은 발소리를 들었다.\n\n\"무슨 일이야?\" 그가 그녀에게 짧게 말했다. \"우리 바쁜데.\"\n\n\"실례합니다.\" 그녀가 독일인 기계공에게 말했다. \"남편과 잠깐 할 이야기가 있어서요.\"\n\n독일인은 방에서 나가려 했으나, 레빈이 그에게 말했다.\n\n\"신경 쓰지 마십시오.\"\n\n\"기차가 세 시지요?\" 독일인이 물었다. \"늦으면 안 되니까요.\"\n\n레빈은 그에게 대답하지 않고, 직접 아내와 함께 밖으로 나갔다.\n\n\"자, 무슨 말을 하고 싶은 거야?\" 그가 프랑스어로 그녀에게 말했다.\n\n그는 그녀의 얼굴을 보지 않았고, 그런 몸으로 그녀가 온몸을 떨며 가엾고 짓눌린 표정을 짓고 있다는 것을 알려 하지도 않았다.\n\n\"전…… 전 이렇게는 더 못 살겠다는 걸 말하고 싶어요. 이건 너무 비참해요…….\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"하인들이 여기 찬장 옆에 있잖아.\" 그가 화를 내며 말했다. \"소란 피우지 마.\"\n\n\"그럼, 이리로 들어가요!\"\n\n그들은 복도에 서 있었다. 키티는 옆방으로 들어가려 했으나, 거기서는 영국인 가정교사가 타냐에게 수업을 하고 있었다.\n\n\"그럼, 정원으로 나가.\"\n\n정원에서 그들은 길의 풀을 뽑고 있는 농부와 마주쳤다. 그러나 이제 그들은 그 농부가 눈물범벅이 된 그녀의 얼굴과 격앙된 자기 얼굴을 볼 수 있다는 것도, 자기들이 무슨 재앙에서 도망치는 사람들처럼 보인다는 것도 개의치 않은 채, 속내를 털어놓고 오해를 풀어야 한다고, 단둘이 있어야 한다고, 그래서 둘 다 느끼고 있는 이 비참함에서 벗어나야 한다고 느끼며 빠른 걸음으로 걸어갔다.\n\n\"우린 이렇게는 더 못 살아요! 이건 비참해요! 난 비참하고, 당신도 비참해요. 무엇 때문에요?\" 보리수 가로수 길의 굽이진 곳에 있는 외딴 벤치에 마침내 다다랐을 때 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 한 가지만 말해 줘. 그자의 말투에 뭔가 점잖지 못한, 마뜩잖은, 모멸스러우리만치 끔찍한 게 있었어?\" 그가 그날 밤 그녀 앞에 섰던 것과 똑같은 자세로, 주먹을 쥐어 가슴에 댄 채 다시 그녀 앞에 서서 말했다.\n\n\"네.\" 그녀가 떨리는 목소리로 말했다. \"하지만, 코스챠, 내 잘못이 아니라는 거 알잖아요? 아침 내내 어떤 태도를 취하려고 애썼는데…… 그런데 그런 사람들은……. 그 사람은 왜 온 걸까요? 우린 정말 행복했는데!\" 그녀는 자신을 뒤흔드는 흐느낌에 숨을 헐떡이며 말했다.\n\n비록 아무것도 그들을 쫓아오지 않았고, 도망칠 것도 없었으며, 그 벤치에서 그리 즐거운 것이라곤 도무지 찾을 수 없었을 텐데도, 정원사는 그들이 집으로 돌아가는 길에 위안을 얻은 환한 얼굴로 자기 곁을 지나가는 것을 놀라움에 차서 바라보았다.\n\n# Chapter 15\n\n아내를 이층까지 바래다준 뒤, 레빈은 돌리가 머무는 쪽으로 갔다. 한편 다리야 알렉산드로브나도 그날 몹시 속이 상해 있었다. 그녀는 방 안을 이리저리 거닐면서, 구석에 서서 악을 쓰며 우는 어린 계집아이에게 화가 난 목소리로 말하고 있었다.\n\n\"너는 하루 종일 구석에 서 있고, 밥도 혼자 먹어야 하고, 인형도 하나 못 보고, 새 옷도 안 만들어 줄 거야.\" 그녀는 아이를 어떻게 벌해야 할지 몰라 그렇게 말했다.\n\n\"아유, 정말 못된 아이라니까!\" 그녀는 레빈을 돌아보며 말했다. \"대체 어디서 이런 고약한 버릇을 배워 오는지 모르겠어요.\"\n\n\"왜요, 무슨 짓을 했길래요?\" 레빈은 그다지 관심 없이 말했다. 그는 그녀에게 의논할 일이 있어 온 터라, 마침 좋지 않은 때에 와 버린 게 짜증스러웠다.\n\n\"그리샤랑 같이 산딸기 밭에 들어가서, 거기서…… 무슨 짓을 했는지 정말 입에 담을 수도 없어요. 엘리엇 양이 우리랑 같이 있지 않은 게 천만 번 아쉽네요. 이 사람은 도통 아무것도 챙기질 않아요—그냥 기계 같다니까요. Figurez-vous que la petite…?\"\n\n그리고 다리야 알렉산드로브나는 마샤가 저지른 죄를 묘사했다.\n\n* Figurez-vous que la petite: 프랑스어로 \"글쎄, 그 어린 게 말이에요\"라는 뜻.\n\n\"그건 아무것도 증명하지 못해요. 못된 버릇 같은 문제가 결코 아니라, 그냥 장난일 뿐이죠.\" 레빈이 그녀를 안심시켰다.\n\n\"하지만 당신, 뭔가 속상한 일이 있나 봐요? 무슨 일로 왔어요?\" 돌리가 물었다. \"거기서는 무슨 일이 있는 거예요?\"\n\n그리고 그 물음의 어조에서 레빈은 자기가 하려던 말을 꺼내기가 쉬우리라는 것을 느꼈다.\n\"거기엔 안 들어갔어요. 키티랑 둘이서 정원에 있었죠. 우리는…… 스티바가 온 뒤로 벌써 두 번째 다퉜어요.\"\n\n돌리는 영민하고 모든 걸 꿰뚫어 보는 눈으로 그를 바라보았다.\n\n\"자, 솔직하게 말해 봐요. 양심에 손을 얹고요. 그러니까…… 키티가 아니라, 그 신사의 행동에 뭔가 불쾌할 만한—아니, 불쾌한 정도가 아니라 끔찍하고, 남편에게 모욕적일 만한 그런 기색이 있었나요?\"\n\n\"무슨 말이냐면, 그러니까 뭐라고 해야 할까…… 구석에 가만히, 가만히 서 있어!\" 그녀는 엄마 얼굴에서 희미한 미소를 알아채고 몸을 돌리려던 마샤에게 말했다. \"세상 사람들이라면 그가 젊은 남자들이 흔히 하는 대로 행동할 뿐이라고 하겠죠. Il fait la cour à une jeune et jolie femme. 그러니 세상 물정을 아는 남편이라면 오히려 우쭐해해야 할 일이라고요.\"\n* Il fait la cour à une jeune et jolie femme: 프랑스어로 \"그는 젊고 예쁜 여인에게 구애하고 있다\"는 뜻.\n\n\"그래요, 그래요.\" 레빈이 침울하게 말했다. \"하지만 당신도 그걸 눈치챘다는 거죠?\"\n\n\"나뿐만 아니라 스티바도 눈치챘어요. 아침 식사가 끝나자마자 나한테 대놓고 그러더라고요. Je crois que Veslovsky fait un petit brin de cour à Kitty.\"\n* Je crois que Veslovsky fait un petit brin de cour à Kitty: 프랑스어로 \"베슬롭스키가 키티에게 약간 수작을 부리고 있는 것 같아\"라는 뜻.\n\n\"그럼, 됐어요. 이제 마음이 놓이네요. 그를 내보내겠어요.\" 레빈이 말했다.\n\n\"무슨 소리예요! 당신 미쳤어요?\" 돌리가 질겁하며 외쳤다. \"말도 안 돼요, 코스챠, 생각 좀 해 봐요!\" 그녀가 웃으며 말했다. \"이제 패니한테 가도 돼.\" 그녀가 마샤에게 말했다. \"아니, 정 그렇다면 내가 스티바한테 말할게요. 그가 데려가게 하면 되잖아요. 손님이 오기로 했다고 둘러대면 되고요. 어쨌든 그 사람은 이 집과 어울리지 않으니까요.\"\n\n\"아니요, 아니에요. 내가 직접 하겠어요.\"\n\n\"하지만 그와 다투게 될 텐데요?\"\n\n\"전혀요. 오히려 즐거울 것 같은데요.\" 레빈이 말했다. 그의 눈이 진짜 즐거움으로 반짝였다. \"자, 이 아이를 용서해 줘요, 돌리. 다시는 안 그럴 거예요.\" 그는 그 어린 죄인을 두고 말했다. 아이는 패니에게 가지 않고, 머뭇거리며 엄마 앞에 선 채, 엄마와 눈을 맞추려고 눈썹 아래로 올려다보며 기다리고 있었다.\n\n엄마가 아이를 흘끗 보았다. 아이는 흐느껴 울며 엄마 무릎에 얼굴을 파묻었고, 돌리는 가늘고 부드러운 손을 아이의 머리에 얹었다.\n\n\"대체 우리와 저 사람 사이에 무슨 공통점이 있단 말인가?\" 레빈은 생각하며, 베슬롭스키를 찾으러 나갔다.\n\n복도를 지나면서 그는 마차를 정거장으로 갈 채비를 해 두라고 일렀다.\n\n\"어제 용수철이 부러졌습니다.\" 하인이 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 포장마차로 하지. 어서 서둘러. 손님은 어디 계신가?\"\n\n\"손님께서는 방으로 가셨습니다.\"\n\n레빈이 베슬롭스키를 마주친 것은, 그가 트렁크에서 짐을 다 꺼내고 새로 가져온 악보 몇 장을 펼쳐 놓은 다음, 말을 타러 나가려고 각반을 두르고 있던 바로 그 순간이었다.\n\n레빈의 얼굴에 뭔가 심상치 않은 것이 있어서였는지, 아니면 바센카 스스로 자기가 부리던 ce petit brin de cour가 이 가족에게는 어울리지 않는다는 것을 의식해서였는지, 그는 레빈이 들어서자 (사교계의 젊은이로서 그럴 수 있는 정도껏) 다소 당황한 기색을 보였다.\n* ce petit brin de cour: 프랑스어로 \"그 약간의 수작\"이라는 뜻.\n\n\"각반을 차고 말을 타시오?\"\n\n\"네, 이게 훨씬 깔끔하거든요.\" 바센카는 살집 있는 다리를 의자에 올리고, 아래쪽 고리를 채우면서 천진하고 사람 좋은 웃음을 지으며 말했다.\n\n그는 분명 마음씨 좋은 친구였고, 바센카의 얼굴에 떠오른 수줍은 표정을 보자 레빈은 그가 가엾기도 하고, 주인 된 처지에 자기 자신이 부끄럽기도 했다.\n\n탁자 위에는 그날 아침 둘이서 힘을 겨루어 보겠다고 함께 부러뜨린 막대기 토막이 놓여 있었다. 레빈은 그 조각을 손에 쥐고, 어떻게 말을 꺼내야 할지 몰라, 막대기에서 작은 부스러기를 떼어 내며 으스러뜨리기 시작했다.\n\n\"내가…… 말하고 싶었던 건……\" 그는 말을 멈추었다. 그러나 문득 키티며 그동안 일어난 모든 일이 떠올라, 그의 얼굴을 단호하게 똑바로 바라보며 말했다. \"당신을 위해 말을 매어 두라고 일러 놓았소.\"\n\n\"어째서요?\" 바센카가 놀라서 말을 꺼냈다. \"어디로 가라는 말입니까?\"\n\n\"당신이 정거장으로 가도록 말이오.\" 레빈이 침울하게 말했다.\n\n\"당신이 어디 가는 겁니까, 아니면 무슨 일이라도 생겼나요?\"\n\n\"마침 손님이 올 일이 생겼소.\" 레빈이 말했다. 그의 억센 손가락은 쪼개진 막대기의 끝을 점점 더 빠르게 부러뜨려 떼어 냈다. \"아니, 손님이 올 일도 없고, 아무 일도 일어나지 않았소만, 그저 떠나 주기를 부탁하는 거요. 내 무례함이야 마음대로 해석하면 되오.\"\n\n바센카는 몸을 곧추세웠다.\n\n\"이유를 설명해 주시기를 부탁드립니다……\" 그는 마침내 사정을 알아채고는 위엄을 갖추어 말했다.\n\n\"설명할 수 없소.\" 레빈은 떨리는 턱을 억누르려 애쓰며 나직하고 또박또박 말했다. \"그리고 묻지 않는 편이 좋을 거요.\"\n\n쪼개진 끝이 다 떨어져 나가자, 레빈은 굵은 쪽 끝을 손가락으로 움켜쥐고 막대기를 둘로 부러뜨린 다음, 떨어지는 끝을 조심스레 받아 쥐었다.\n\n그날 아침 체조에서 그 위력을 입증해 보인 근육이며, 그 신경질적인 손가락, 번득이는 눈, 나직한 목소리, 부들부들 떨리는 턱을 본 것이, 어떤 말보다도 바센카를 더 확실하게 납득시켰을 것이다. 그는 어깨를 으쓱하고 경멸하듯 웃으며 고개를 숙였다.\n\n\"오블론스키를 만날 수는 있겠지요?\"\n\n그 으쓱거림과 웃음은 레빈을 자극하지 못했다.\n\n\"저 사람으로서야 달리 어쩌겠는가?\" 그는 생각했다.\n\n\"곧 그를 당신에게 보내겠소.\"\n\n\"이게 대체 무슨 미친 짓인가?\" 스테판 아르카지치는, 친구로부터 집에서 쫓겨나는 신세가 되었다는 말을 들은 뒤, 손님이 떠나기를 기다리며 거닐고 있던 레빈을 정원에서 찾아내 말했다. \"Mais c'est ridicule! 자네 무슨 파리한테라도 쏘인 건가? Mais c'est du dernier ridicule! 자네 생각엔, 만약 젊은 남자가……\"\n* Mais c'est ridicule!: 프랑스어로 \"정말 우스꽝스럽군!\"이라는 뜻. * Mais c'est du dernier ridicule!: 프랑스어로 \"이건 정말 더없이 우스꽝스러운 짓이야!\"라는 뜻.\n\n그러나 레빈이 쏘인 자리는 분명 아직도 쓰라렸던 모양이다. 스테판 아르카지치가 그 이유를 길게 늘어놓으려 하자 레빈은 또다시 얼굴이 창백해지더니, 스스로 그의 말을 끊어 버렸다.\n\n\"제발 그 얘긴 들추지 말게! 나도 어쩔 수가 없어. 자네와 그 사람을 이렇게 대하는 게 부끄럽긴 해. 하지만 그가 떠나는 게 그에게 그리 큰 슬픔은 아닐 거라고 생각하네. 그리고 그가 여기 있는 게 나와 내 아내에겐 견디기 힘든 일이었어.\"\n\n\"하지만 그건 그에게 모욕이야! Et puis c'est ridicule.\"\n\n그리고 나로서는 모욕적이기도 하고 괴롭기도 해요! 나는 어떤 잘못도 하지 않았는데, 내가 고통받을 이유가 없잖아요.\n\n\"나에게는 모욕이자 괴로움이라고! 게다가 나는 아무 잘못도 없는데, 내가 고통받을 이유가 없잖나.\"\n\n\"이런, 자네한테서 이런 모습을 보게 될 줄은 몰랐네! On peut être jaloux, mais à ce point, c'est du dernier ridicule!\"\n* On peut être jaloux, mais à ce point, c'est du dernier ridicule!: 프랑스어로 \"질투야 할 수 있다지만, 이 정도까지 가면 그건 더없이 우스꽝스러운 짓이야!\"라는 뜻.\n\n“이건 또 뭐지?” 하인이 집에서 달려나와 마차를 세우자 레빈은 생각했다. 그는 레빈이 까맣게 잊고 있던 기계공이었다. 기계공은 허리를 깊이 숙여 인사하고 베슬롭스키에게 뭔가 말을 하더니 마차에 기어올랐고, 둘은 함께 마차를 몰고 떠났다.\n\n스테판 아르카지이치와 공작 부인은 레빈의 행동에 몹시 언짢아했다. 레빈 자신도 더없이 우스꽝스러울 뿐만 아니라 완전히 잘못했고 수치스럽다고 느꼈다. 하지만 자신과 아내가 어떤 고통을 겪었는지 떠올리며 다음번에는 어떻게 행동해야 할지 스스로에게 물었을 때, 그는 똑같이 다시 행동하리라고 답했다.\n\n이 모든 일에도 불구하고, 그날이 저물어 갈 무렵에는, 레빈의 행동을 용서하지 못하는 공작 부인을 빼고 모두가 유난히 활기차고 기분이 좋아졌다. 마치 벌을 받고 난 아이들처럼, 혹은 지루하고 격식 차린 연회를 끝낸 어른들처럼 말이다. 그래서 저녁 무렵에는, 공작 부인이 없는 자리에서 바센카를 쫓아낸 일이 마치 까마득히 먼 옛일이라도 되는 양 이야깃거리가 되었다. 아버지에게서 우스갯소리를 풀어내는 재주를 물려받은 돌리는, 세 번째, 네 번째로 거듭 이야기를 들려주면서—매번 새로운 우스운 살을 덧붙여 가며—바레니카가 웃다 못해 정신을 못 차리게 만들었다. 손님을 위해 일부러 막 새 구두를 신고 응접실로 들어서는데, 난데없이 마차 굴러가는 소리가 들리더라는 것이었다. 그런데 그 마차에 타고 있는 게 다름 아닌 바센카 본인이지 뭔가. 스코틀랜드식 모자에, 그 악보에, 그 각반에, 죄다 챙겨서 건초더미 위에 떡하니 앉아 있더라는 것이다.\n\n\"그저 마차를 내오라고 하시기만 했어도! 그런데 그게 아니었지 뭐예요! 그러다 '멈춰!' 하는 소리가 들리잖아요. 아, 마음이 누그러졌나 보다 했죠. 내다봤더니, 글쎄 웬 뚱뚱한 독일 사람을 그 옆에 앉히더니 그대로 가 버리지 않겠어요?…… 내 새 구두만 다 헛것이 되고 말았다니까요!……\"\n\n# Chapter 16\n\n다리야 알렉산드로브나는 마음먹은 대로 안나를 만나러 갔다. 동생을 언짢게 하고 레빈이 싫어하는 일을 하는 것이 그녀로서는 마음에 걸렸다. 레빈 부부가 브론스키와 어떤 식으로든 엮이고 싶어 하지 않는 것이 얼마나 옳은지 그녀는 충분히 이해하고 있었다. 그러나 자기는 안나를 만나러 가서, 그녀의 처지가 달라졌어도 자기 마음만은 변할 수 없음을 그녀에게 보여 주어야 한다고 느꼈다. 이번 행차에서 레빈 부부에게 신세를 지지 않으려고 다리야 알렉산드로브나는 마을로 사람을 보내 마차를 끌 말을 빌리려 했다. 그러나 이를 알게 된 레빈이 그녀에게 항의하러 왔다.\n\n\"형수님이 가시는 걸 제가 싫어한다니, 무슨 근거로 그렇게 생각하세요? 설령 제가 싫어한다 해도, 형수님이 제 말을 쓰지 않으시는 게 저로서는 더 싫습니다.\" 그가 말했다. \"확실히 가신다는 말씀도 안 하셨잖아요. 마을에서 말을 빌리는 건 제 기분이 언짢기도 하거니와, 더 중요한 건, 그자들이 일은 맡아 놓고 형수님을 제대로 모셔다드리지 못한다는 겁니다. 저한테 말이 있어요. 그러니 저를 서운하게 만들고 싶지 않으시거든 제 말을 쓰세요.\"\n\n다리야 알렉산드로브나는 승낙할 수밖에 없었고, 정해진 날에 레빈은 형수를 위해 농장 말과 안장 말을 그러모아 네 마리에 갈아탈 말까지 마련해 두었다. 그다지 번듯해 보이는 한 벌은 아니었지만, 다리야 알렉산드로브나를 하루 만에 그 거리 전부를 데려다줄 수 있는 말들이었다. 마침 그 무렵에는 떠나려는 공작 부인을 위한 말과 산파를 위한 말도 필요했던 터라, 레빈으로서는 그 수를 맞추기가 쉽지 않은 일이었다. 그러나 손님을 대접하는 도리상, 자기 집에 머무는 다리야 알렉산드로브나가 말을 빌리도록 내버려 둘 수는 없었다. 더구나 그는 이번 여정에 부르는 값으로 이십 루블이 청구되리라는 것을, 그것이 그녀에게는 만만찮은 문제임을 잘 알고 있었다. 매우 좋지 못한 상태에 놓인 다리야 알렉산드로브나의 금전 사정을 레빈 부부는 자기들 일처럼 마음에 두고 있었다.\n\n레빈의 권유에 따라 다리야 알렉산드로브나는 동트기 전에 길을 나섰다. 길은 좋았고, 마차는 편안했으며, 말들은 신나게 잰걸음을 놓았다. 마부석에는 마부 외에 회계 사무소 서기가 앉아 있었는데, 레빈이 더 든든하라고 마부 옆에 딸려 보낸 사람이었다. 다리야 알렉산드로브나는 꾸벅꾸벅 졸다가, 말을 갈아탈 여인숙에 이르러서야 잠에서 깼다.\n\n레빈이 스비야시스키네로 가던 길에 묵었던 그 부유한 농부의 집에서 차를 마시고, 아낙네들과는 그 집 아이들 이야기를, 노인과는 브론스키 백작 이야기를 나눈 뒤—노인은 백작을 입에 침이 마르도록 칭찬했다—다리야 알렉산드로브나는 열 시에 다시 길을 떠났다. 집에 있을 때는 아이들을 돌보느라 생각에 잠길 겨를이 없었다. 그래서 지금, 네 시간이나 이어진 이 여정 끝에, 전에는 억눌러 두었던 온갖 상념이 떼 지어 머릿속으로 밀려들었고, 그녀는 일찍이 한 번도 그래 본 적 없을 만큼, 그것도 더없이 다양한 각도에서 자기 삶 전체를 곱씹어 보았다. 그 생각들은 그녀 자신에게도 낯설게 느껴졌다. 처음에 그녀는 아이들 생각을 했는데, 공작 부인과 키티가(키티 쪽을 더 믿기는 했다) 돌봐 주겠다고 약속했는데도 그 애들이 마음 놓이지 않았다. '마샤가 못된 장난만 시작하지 않으면, 그리샤가 말에 차이지만 않으면, 릴리가 또 배탈이 나지만 않으면!' 그녀는 생각했다. 그러나 이런 당장의 문제들에 뒤이어 가까운 미래의 문제들이 떠올랐다. 그녀는 다가오는 겨울을 나려면 모스크바에 새 셋집을 구하고, 응접실 가구를 새로 들이고, 큰딸에게 망토를 지어 줘야 한다는 생각을 하기 시작했다. 그러다 더 먼 미래의 문제들이 떠올랐다. 아이들을 어떻게 세상에 내보낼 것인가 하는 문제였다. '딸들은 괜찮아.' 그녀는 생각했다. '하지만 아들들은?'\n\n'내가 그리샤를 가르치는 건 좋은 일이지만, 물론 그건 지금 내 몸이 자유롭기 때문이야, 아이를 배지 않았으니까. 스티바는 물론 기대할 게 못 되지. 마음씨 좋은 친구들의 도움을 받으면 애들을 키울 수야 있겠지. 하지만 또 아이가 들어선다면?……' 그러자 여자에게 내려진 저주가 슬픔 속에서 아이를 낳는 것이라는 말이 얼마나 진실과 거리가 먼지가 문득 그녀의 마음을 쳤다.\n\n'낳는 것 그 자체야 아무것도 아니지. 그렇지만 아이를 배고 지내는 그 몇 달—그게 정말 견딜 수 없는 거야.' 그녀는 마지막 임신과 마지막 아이의 죽음을 떠올리며 생각했다. 그리고 방금 여인숙에서 젊은 아낙과 나눈 대화가 생각났다. 아이가 있느냐는 물음에 그 잘생긴 젊은 아낙은 명랑하게 대답했다.\n\n\"계집애가 하나 있었는데, 하느님이 데려가셨지요. 지난 사순절에 묻었답니다.\"\n\n\"아니, 그래서 많이 슬펐겠네요?\" 다리야 알렉산드로브나가 물었다.\n\n\"슬프긴요? 시아버지한테는 이미 손주가 차고 넘쳐요. 그저 짐만 됐는걸요. 일도 못 하고, 아무것도 못 하고. 그저 발목만 잡혔지요.\"\n\n이 대답은, 그 젊은 아낙의 선량하고 호감 가는 얼굴에도 불구하고 다리야 알렉산드로브나에게는 역겹게 느껴졌었다. 그런데 지금은 그 말이 자꾸만 떠오르는 것을 어쩔 수 없었다. 그 냉소적인 말 속에는 정말이지 한 톨의 진실이 들어 있었다.\n\n'그래, 따지고 보면.' 다리야 알렉산드로브나는 결혼 생활 십오 년 동안의 자기 삶 전체를 돌아보며 생각했다. '임신, 입덧, 멍해지는 정신, 만사에 대한 무관심, 그리고 무엇보다도—추해지는 것. 키티는 저렇게 젊고 예쁜데도, 키티마저 미모를 잃었어. 그리고 나는, 아이를 배면 흉해져, 나도 알아. 출산, 그 고통, 그 끔찍한 고통, 그 마지막 순간…… 그러고 나면 젖먹이기, 잠 못 이루는 밤들, 그 무서운 통증들……'\n\n다리야 알렉산드로브나는 아이를 낳을 때마다 거의 매번 겪었던, 헐어 버린 젖가슴의 통증을 떠올리는 것만으로도 몸서리를 쳤다. '그다음엔 아이들의 병치레, 그 끝없는 조마조마함. 그다음엔 키워 내는 일, 못된 버릇'(그녀는 산딸기밭에서 어린 마샤가 저지른 잘못을 떠올렸다), '교육, 라틴어—전부 너무나 알 수 없고 어려운 일이야. 게다가 이 모든 것 위에, 이 아이들의 죽음이 있지.' 그러자 어머니의 가슴을 늘 갈가리 찢어 놓던 그 잔인한 기억이 다시금 그녀의 상상 앞에 떠올랐다. 후두염으로 죽은 마지막 어린 아기의 죽음, 그 아이의 장례식, 작은 분홍빛 관 앞에서 모두가 보이던 매정한 무관심, 그리고 갈가리 찢긴 자기 가슴, 관 뚜껑—십자가가 수놓인 작은 분홍빛 뚜껑—이 덮이려는 순간 관 속으로 보이던, 관자놀이가 불거진 창백한 작은 이마와 무언가 의아해하듯 벌어진 작은 입을 바라보며 느꼈던 자신의 외로운 비통함이.\n\n'그런데 이 모든 게 다 무엇을 위한 거지? 이 모든 데서 무엇이 나온단 말이야? 나는 한순간의 평온도 누리지 못한 채, 아이를 배거나 아이에게 젖을 먹이거나 하면서, 늘 신경이 곤두서고, 신경질을 부리고, 나 자신도 비참하면서 남들까지 들볶고, 남편에게는 정나미 떨어지는 존재가 되어 내 삶을 허비하고 있어. 그러는 사이 아이들은 불행하게, 제대로 교육도 못 받고, 돈 한 푼 없이 자라고 있고. 지금만 해도 레빈네에서 여름을 나지 않았더라면, 우리가 어떻게 살아갔을지 모르겠어. 물론 코스챠와 키티는 워낙 배려가 깊어서 우리가 그걸 느끼지 않게 해 주지. 하지만 이대로 계속될 수는 없어. 저들도 아이를 낳을 테고, 그러면 우리를 거둬 줄 수 없을 거야. 지금도 저들에게는 짐인걸. 자기 앞가림할 것조차 거의 남지 않은 아버지가 우리를 어떻게 돕겠어? 그러니 나 혼자서는 아이들을 키울 수조차 없고, 남의 도움을 받아 가며 굴욕을 무릅쓰고 키운다 해도 힘에 부칠 거야. 그래, 설령 가장 큰 행운을 가정해서, 아이들이 죽지 않고 내가 그럭저럭 키워 낸다 쳐. 아무리 잘돼 봤자 그저 점잖은 사람들이 될 뿐이야. 내가 바랄 수 있는 건 그게 다야. 그런데 고작 그것을 얻으려고—이 무슨 고통이며, 이 무슨 고생이람!…… 한 사람의 인생이 통째로 망가지는 거지!' 그녀는 다시 그 젊은 농가 아낙이 한 말을 떠올렸고, 다시금 그 생각에 역겨움을 느꼈다. 그러나 그 말 속에 잔인한 진실 한 톨이 들어 있음을 인정하지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n\"이제 얼마나 더 가야 하나, 미하일?\" 다리야 알렉산드로브나는 자기를 두렵게 하는 생각들에서 마음을 돌리려고 회계 사무소 서기에게 물었다.\n\n\"이 마을에서부터 오 베르스타라고들 합니다.\" 마차는 마을 길을 따라 달리다 다리 위로 올라섰다. 다리 위에는 어깨에 곡식 단을 묶을 새끼줄 타래를 멘 농가 아낙들이 떼 지어 명랑하고 시끌벅적하게 수다를 떨고 있었다. 그들은 다리 위에 멈춰 서서 호기심 어린 눈으로 마차를 빤히 쳐다보았다. 다리야 알렉산드로브나 쪽으로 돌린 그 얼굴들은 하나같이 건강하고 행복해 보였고, 그래서 삶을 즐기는 그들이 부러웠다. '저들은 다들 살아가고 있어, 다들 삶을 즐기고 있어.' 다리야 알렉산드로브나는 농가 아낙들을 지나쳐 낡은 마차의 부드러운 용수철에 편안히 몸을 묻은 채 다시 잰걸음으로 언덕을 오르면서도 생각에 잠겨 있었다. '그런데 나는, 말하자면 감옥에서, 나를 죽도록 들볶는 근심의 세계에서 풀려난 지금에야 비로소 잠깐 주위를 둘러보고 있는 거야. 저들은 다들 살아가고 있어. 저 농가 아낙들도, 내 동생 나탈리야도, 바렌카도, 내가 만나러 가는 안나도—다들 그래, 그런데 나만 아니야.\n\n'그런데도 사람들은 안나를 공격하지. 무엇 때문에? 내가 그녀보다 나은 게 뭐가 있어? 나는 그래도 사랑하는 남편이 있어—내가 바라는 만큼 사랑하지는 못해도, 그래도 사랑은 하지. 하지만 안나는 한 번도 자기 남편을 사랑한 적이 없잖아. 그러니 그녀에게 무슨 잘못이 있어? 그녀는 살고 싶은 거야. 하느님이 우리 가슴속에 그런 마음을 넣어 두셨어. 어쩌면 나라도 똑같이 했을지 몰라. 그녀가 그 끔찍한 시절에 모스크바로 나를 찾아왔을 때 그녀 말을 들어준 게 잘한 일이었는지, 오늘까지도 나는 확신이 서지 않아. 그때 나는 남편을 버리고 내 삶을 새로 시작했어야 했어. 진짜로 사랑하고 사랑받을 수도 있었을 텐데. 그런데 지금 이대로가 조금이라도 더 나은가? 나는 그를 존경하지 않아. 그저 그가 내게 필요할 뿐이지.' 그녀는 남편에 대해 생각했다. '그래서 그를 참고 견디는 거야. 이게 조금이라도 더 나은 건가? 그때만 해도 나는 아직 사람들의 찬사를 받을 수 있었어, 아직 미모가 남아 있었으니까.' 다리야 알렉산드로브나는 생각을 이어 갔고, 거울에 자기 모습을 비춰 보고 싶어졌다. 손가방 속에 여행용 손거울이 있어서 그것을 꺼내려 했지만, 마부와 흔들거리는 회계 사무소 서기의 등을 보고는, 둘 중 누구라도 뒤를 돌아보면 부끄러우리라는 생각이 들어 거울을 꺼내지 않았다.\n\n그러나 거울을 들여다보지 않고도 그녀는 지금이라도 아직 늦지 않았다고 생각했다. 그러고는 늘 자기에게 유독 살뜰하던 세르게이 이바노비치를, 또 성홍열을 앓던 자기 아이들을 간호하는 일을 도와주었고 자기를 사랑하던 스티바의 마음씨 좋은 친구 투롭친을 떠올렸다. 그리고 또 한 사람, 아주 젊은 남자가 있었는데, 남편이 농담조로 일러 주기로는 그 사람이 그녀를 두 자매 어느 쪽보다도 더 아름답다고 여긴다는 것이었다. 그리하여 더없이 열정적이고 더없이 가당찮은 연애담이 다리야 알렉산드로브나의 상상 앞에 떠올랐다. '안나는 정말 잘한 거야, 나는 절대로 그녀를 나무라지 않을 거야. 그녀는 행복하고, 또 다른 사람을 행복하게 해 주고 있어. 게다가 그녀는 나처럼 무너져 버린 게 아니라, 십중팔구 여전히 예전 그대로일 거야, 환하고, 영리하고, 온갖 인상에 활짝 열려 있는 모습으로.' 다리야 알렉산드로브나는 그렇게 생각했고, 입가에 짓궂은 미소가 번졌다. 안나의 연애에 대해 곰곰이 생각하다가, 다리야 알렉산드로브나는 그것과 나란히, 자기를 사랑하는 이상적인 남자라는 상상 속의 합성 인물을 상대로, 거의 똑같은 연애담을 자기 몫으로 지어내고 있었던 것이다. 그녀는 안나처럼 그 일 전부를 남편에게 고백했다. 그러자 이 고백에 어쩔 줄 몰라 하며 얼떨떨해하는 스테판 아르카지치의 모습이 그녀를 웃음 짓게 했다.\n\n이런 백일몽에 잠긴 채 그녀는 보즈드비젠스코예로 이어지는 큰길의 모퉁이에 다다랐다.\n\n# Chapter 17\n\n마부가 네 마리 말의 고삐를 당겨 세우고는 오른쪽으로, 농부 몇몇이 짐수레에 걸터앉아 있는 호밀밭 쪽을 둘러보았다. 사무소 서기는 막 뛰어내리려다가 다시 생각을 고쳐먹고는, 대신 농부들에게 명령조로 소리치며 이리 오라고 손짓을 했다. 마차가 달릴 때만 부는 듯하던 바람도 마차가 멈춰 서자 잦아들었다. 김이 모락모락 나는 말들 위로 등에가 달라붙었고, 말들은 성이 나서 그것을 흔들어 떨쳐 냈다. 짐수레 쪽에서 들려오던, 큰 낫에 숫돌을 가는 쇳소리도 멎었다. 농부 하나가 일어나 마차 쪽으로 다가왔다.\n\n\"이런, 어쩌면 그리 굼떠!\" 사무소 서기가, 거칠고 메마른 길의 바퀴 자국 위를 맨발로 천천히 디뎌 오는 농부에게 성난 목소리로 외쳤다. \"어서 좀 와요!\"\n\n머리카락을 둘둘 만 곱슬머리 노인이, 머리에 나무껍질 끈을 동여매고 땀에 젖어 거뭇해진 굽은 등을 한 채로, 걸음을 재촉하며 마차 쪽으로 다가와 햇볕에 그을린 손으로 흙받기를 붙잡았다.\n\n\"보즈드비젠스코예, 영주 저택 말이오? 백작님 댁?\" 그가 되물었다. \"이 길을 끝까지 가시오. 그러고 나서 왼쪽으로 도시오. 가로수 길을 따라 곧장 가면 바로 거기 나옵니다. 그런데 누구를 찾으시오? 백작님 본인이오?\"\n\n\"그래, 다들 집에 있을까요, 영감님?\" 다리아 알렉산드로브나가 막연하게 물었다. 이런 농부에게조차 안나에 대해 어떻게 물어야 할지 몰랐던 것이다.\n\n\"틀림없이 집에 계시지요.\" 농부가 한쪽 맨발에서 다른 쪽 맨발로 무게를 옮기며, 흙먼지 위에 발가락 다섯 개와 발뒤꿈치 자국을 또렷이 남기며 말했다. \"분명 집에 계실 겁니다.\" 그는 이야기를 하고 싶어 안달이 난 듯 거듭 말했다. \"바로 어제도 손님들이 오셨거든요. 손님이 한가득 오셨다니까요. 무슨 일로 오셨소?\" 그는 몸을 돌려, 짐수레에서 그에게 뭐라고 외치는 젊은이를 향해 소리쳤다. \"아! 다들 얼마 전에 여기를 지나 수확 기계를 보러들 가셨소. 지금쯤은 댁에 돌아오셨을 거요. 그런데 댁은 뉘 댁 사람이시오?……\"\n\n\"우리는 멀리서 왔소.\" 마부가 마부석으로 기어오르며 말했다. \"그래, 이제 멀지 않은 거요?\"\n\n\"글쎄, 바로 여기라니까요. 빠져나가시기만 하면……\" 그는 그러는 내내 마차를 붙잡은 채로 말했다.\n\n건강해 보이는, 어깨가 떡 벌어진 젊은이도 다가왔다.\n\n\"뭐예요, 수확 일꾼을 구하는 거예요?\" 그가 물었다.\n\n\"나도 모르네, 이 사람아.\"\n\n\"그러니까 왼쪽으로 가시오, 그러면 바로 거기 나옵니다.\" 농부가 말했는데, 분명히 길손들을 놓아주기 싫어하며 더 이야기를 나누고 싶은 기색이었다.\n\n마부가 말을 출발시켰지만, 이제 막 모퉁이를 돌려는 참에 농부가 외쳤다. \"서요! 어이, 양반! 서요!\" 두 목소리가 외쳤다. 마부가 멈춰 섰다.\n\n\"저기 오십니다! 저기 계세요!\" 농부가 외쳤다. \"저 행차 좀 보시오!\" 그는 말을 타고 길을 따라 오는 네 사람과, 샤라방을 탄 두 사람을 가리키며 말했다.\n\n그들은 기수를 거느린 브론스키와, 말을 탄 베슬롭스키와 안나, 그리고 샤라방에 탄 바르바라 공작 영애와 스비아시스키였다. 그들은 새로 들여온 수확 기계가 어떻게 돌아가는지 보러 나갔던 것이다.\n\n* 샤라방: 여러 사람이 나란히 앉을 수 있도록 좌석을 가로로 배치한 마차.\n마차가 멈춰 섰을 때, 말을 탄 일행은 천천히 걸어오고 있었다. 안나가 베슬롭스키 옆에서 앞장서 있었다. 갈기를 짧게 깎고 꼬리를 짧게 자른, 다부진 영국산 코브를 조용히 걸리고 있는 안나, 높다란 모자 아래로 검은 머리카락을 풀어 흘러내린 아름다운 머리며, 풍만한 어깨며, 검은 승마복에 감싸인 가느다란 허리며, 그 거동의 온갖 여유로움과 우아함이 돌리에게 강한 인상을 남겼다.\n\n처음 잠깐 동안은 안나가 말을 타고 있다는 것이 어울리지 않게 여겨졌다. 다리아 알렉산드로브나의 머릿속에서 부인이 말을 탄다는 관념은 젊은이다운 교태나 경박함에 대한 생각과 결부되어 있었는데, 그녀가 보기에 그런 것은 안나의 처지에는 어울리지 않았다. 하지만 가까이서 그녀를 자세히 뜯어보고 나자, 그녀는 곧바로 안나의 승마를 받아들이게 되었다. 그 우아함에도 불구하고, 안나의 자태며 옷차림이며 동작은 모두가 너무도 단순하고 차분하고 기품이 있어서, 그보다 더 자연스러울 수가 없었다.\n\n안나 옆에는, 더워 보이는 잿빛 군마를 탄 바센카 베슬롭스키가, 리본을 나부끼는 스코틀랜드식 모자를 쓰고, 통통한 두 다리를 앞으로 쭉 뻗은 채로, 누가 봐도 제 모습에 흡족해하며 있었다. 다리아 알렉산드로브나는 그를 알아보고는 흐뭇한 미소를 억누를 수가 없었다. 그 뒤로는 브론스키가, 분명 질주로 달아오른 짙은 적갈색 암말을 타고 오고 있었다. 그는 고삐를 당기며 말을 제어하고 있었다.\n\n그의 뒤로는 기수 차림을 한 자그마한 사내가 따라왔다. 스비아시스키와 바르바라 공작 영애는 큼직한 칠흑같이 검은 속보마가 끄는 새 샤라방을 타고서 말을 탄 일행을 앞질렀다.\n\n낡은 마차 한구석에 잔뜩 웅크린 자그마한 형체에서 돌리를 알아본 순간, 안나의 얼굴이 갑자기 기쁨에 찬 미소로 환하게 빛났다. 그녀는 소리를 지르며 안장 위에서 움찔하더니 말을 질주시켰다. 마차에 다다르자 그녀는 누구의 부축도 없이 뛰어내려, 승마복 자락을 추켜올린 채 돌리를 맞으러 달려갔다.\n\n\"너인 줄 알았으면서도 차마 그렇게 믿지를 못했어. 정말 기뻐! 내가 얼마나 기쁜지 너는 짐작도 못 할 거야!\" 그녀는 한순간 돌리에게 얼굴을 비비며 입을 맞추다가, 다음 순간에는 그녀를 떼어 놓고 미소를 지으며 찬찬히 들여다보면서 말했다.\n\n\"여보, 이 얼마나 반가운 뜻밖의 손님이에요!\" 그녀는 말에서 내려 그들 쪽으로 걸어오는 브론스키를 돌아보며 말했다.\n\n브론스키는 높다란 잿빛 모자를 벗으며 돌리에게 다가갔다.\n\n\"저희가 부인을 뵙게 되어 얼마나 기쁜지 모르실 겁니다.\" 그가 그 말에 각별한 의미를 실으며, 미소 속에 튼튼하고 새하얀 이를 드러내며 말했다.\n\n바센카 베슬롭스키는 말에서 내리지도 않은 채 모자를 벗어, 머리 위로 신이 나서 리본을 흔들며 손님에게 인사를 했다.\n\n\"저분은 바르바라 공작 영애예요.\" 샤라방이 다가오자, 묻는 듯한 돌리의 눈길에 답하여 안나가 말했다.\n\n\"아!\" 다리아 알렉산드로브나가 말했고, 자기도 모르게 그녀의 얼굴에 못마땅한 기색이 드러났다.\n\n바르바라 공작 영애는 그녀의 남편의 고모였고, 그녀는 오래전부터 그녀를 알고 있었지만 그녀를 존경하지는 않았다. 다리아 알렉산드로브나는 바르바라 공작 영애가 평생을 부유한 친척들에게 아첨하며 살아왔다는 것을 알고 있었는데, 그런 그녀가 이제 자기와는 아무 사이도 아닌 브론스키에게 빌붙어 산다는 사실은 자기 남편과의 친척 관계 때문에 돌리에게 굴욕감을 안겨 주었다. 안나는 돌리의 표정을 알아채고 당황했다. 그녀는 얼굴을 붉히며 승마복 자락을 떨어뜨렸고, 거기에 발이 걸려 비틀거렸다.\n\n다리아 알렉산드로브나는 샤라방으로 다가가 바르바라 공작 영애에게 쌀쌀하게 인사를 했다. 스비아시스키도 그녀가 아는 사람이었다. 그는 젊은 아내를 둔 그 별난 친구는 어떻게 지내느냐고 안부를 물었고, 어울리지 않는 말들과 흙받기를 기운 마차로 눈길을 휙 훑고 나서, 부인들에게 샤라방에 옮겨 타시는 게 어떻겠느냐고 권했다.\n\n\"저는 이 마차에 타지요.\" 그가 말했다. \"말이 순하고, 공작 영애께서는 솜씨가 아주 훌륭하시니까요.\"\n\n\"아니에요, 그냥 그대로 계세요.\" 안나가 다가오며 말했다. \"우리는 이 마차로 가겠어요.\" 그러고는 돌리의 팔을 잡고 그녀를 끌고 갔다.\n\n다리아 알렉산드로브나의 눈은 일찍이 본 적 없는 형태의 우아한 마차며, 훌륭한 말들이며, 자기를 둘러싼 우아하고 화려한 사람들에 그만 어찔할 지경이었다. 하지만 무엇보다도 그녀를 놀라게 한 것은, 그토록 잘 알고 또 사랑했던 안나에게 일어난 변화였다. 다른 어떤 여자였더라면, 그처럼 가까이서 살피지 않는 사람이었더라면, 전부터 안나를 알지 못했거나 다리아 알렉산드로브나가 오는 길에 했던 것 같은 생각을 하지 않았더라면, 안나에게서 특별한 무엇도 알아채지 못했을 것이다. 하지만 지금 돌리는, 오로지 사랑의 순간에만 여인들에게서 발견되는, 그리고 지금 안나의 얼굴에서 보이는 그 일시적인 아름다움에 마음을 사로잡혔다. 그녀의 얼굴에 깃든 모든 것이—두 뺨과 턱에 또렷이 팬 보조개며, 입술의 윤곽이며, 그 얼굴 위로 흡사 나풀거리는 듯한 미소며, 두 눈의 광채며, 동작의 우아함과 민첩함이며, 목소리의 충만한 울림이며, 심지어 베슬롭스키가 그녀의 코브를 오른발부터 내딛어 질주하도록 길들이게 한번 타게 해 달라고 청했을 때 일종의 성난 다정함으로 대답하던 그 태도까지—그 모든 것이 유난히 매혹적이었고, 그녀 자신도 그것을 알고서 기뻐하고 있는 듯이 보였다.\n\n두 여자가 마차에 나란히 앉자, 갑자기 둘 다 어색한 기분에 휩싸였다. 안나는 돌리가 자기에게 던지는 골똘하게 묻는 듯한 눈길에 당황했다. 돌리는, 스비아시스키가 \"이 마차\"라고 한 말을 듣고 나니, 안나가 자기와 함께 타고 앉아 있는 이 낡고 더러운 마차가 부끄럽게 느껴지는 것을 어쩔 수 없어 멋쩍었다. 마부 필립과 사무소 서기도 똑같은 기분을 맛보고 있었다. 사무소 서기는 자기 당혹감을 감추려고 부인들을 자리에 앉히는 일에 분주하게 굴었지만, 마부 필립은 시무룩해져서는, 앞으로는 이런 겉치레의 우월함에 기죽지 않으리라 마음을 다잡고 있었다. 그는 칠흑 같은 말을 바라보며 비꼬는 듯한 미소를 지었고, 샤라방을 끄는 저 말쑥한 속보마는 그저 산책용으로나 쓸모가 있지, 더위 속에서 삼십 베르스타를 단숨에 달리지는 못할 거라고 벌써 속으로 단정 짓고 있었다.\n\n농부들은 모두 짐수레에서 일어나, 호기심 어린 즐거운 눈빛으로 두 벗의 만남을 빤히 바라보며 저마다 한마디씩 평을 늘어놓고 있었다.\n\n\"저분들도 좋으신 게지, 오랫동안 못 만났던 게야.\" 머리에 나무껍질 끈을 동여맨 곱슬머리 노인이 말했다.\n\n\"이봐요, 게라심 아저씨, 저 칠흑 같은 말을 지금 끌어다 곡식을 실어 나르면 일이 후딱 끝나겠는걸요!\"\n\n\"저것 좀 봐! 저게 바지 입은 여자인가?\" 그들 중 하나가, 여성용 안장에 앉은 바센카 베슬롭스키를 가리키며 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 사내잖아! 어찌나 멀쑥하게 달리는지 좀 보게!\"\n\n\"어이, 자네들! 그래, 오늘은 잠 못 자게 생겼나 보군?\"\n\n\"오늘 같은 날 잠은 무슨 잠!\" 노인이 해를 곁눈질하며 말했다. \"한낮이 지났어, 저것 좀 봐! 갈고리들 챙겨서 어서들 가세!\"\n\n# Chapter 18\n\n안나는 길에서 묻은 먼지가 주름마다 끼어 있는 돌리의 야위고 근심에 지친 얼굴을 바라보았고, 자기가 생각하는 바를, 그러니까 돌리가 더 야위었다는 말을 막 하려던 참이었다. 하지만 자기 자신은 더 아름다워졌으며, 돌리의 눈이 그것을 말해 주고 있다는 것을 의식한 그녀는 한숨을 쉬고는 자기 이야기를 꺼내기 시작했다.\n\n\"언니는 나를 보면서,\" 그녀가 말했다, \"이런 처지에서 어떻게 내가 행복할 수 있을까 하고 의아해하고 있죠? 그래요! 이런 말을 하기는 부끄럽지만, 나는…… 나는 변명의 여지없이 행복해요. 뭔가 마법 같은 일이 나한테 일어났어요, 꿈처럼요. 무섭고 공포에 사로잡혀 있다가 별안간 잠에서 깨어나니 그 온갖 끔찍한 것들이 다 사라져 버린 그런 꿈 말이에요. 나는 깨어났어요. 그 비참함과 두려움을 지나 살아남았고, 이제는 오래전부터, 특히 이곳에 온 뒤로는, 너무나 행복해요!……\" 그녀는 돌리를 바라보며 무언가를 묻는 듯한 수줍은 미소를 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"나도 정말 기뻐!\" 돌리가 미소 지으며 말했지만, 자기도 모르게 마음먹은 것보다 더 차갑게 말이 나왔다. \"너를 위해 정말 기뻐. 그런데 왜 나한테 편지를 쓰지 않았니?\"\n\n\"왜냐고요?…… 용기가 없었으니까요…… 언니는 내 처지를 잊고 있어요……\"\n\n\"나한테? 용기가 없었다고? 내가 어떻게…… 어떻게 생각하는지 안다면……\"\n\n다리야 알렉산드로브나는 아침에 했던 생각을 표현하고 싶었지만, 어쩐 일인지 지금은 그러는 게 어울리지 않는 것처럼 여겨졌다.\n\n\"하지만 그 얘긴 나중에 하자. 이게 뭐니, 이 건물들은 다 뭐야?\" 그녀는 화제를 바꾸고 싶어, 아카시아와 라일락의 초록빛 울타리 너머로 모습을 드러낸 붉고 푸른 지붕들을 가리키며 물었다. \"아주 작은 도시 같구나.\"\n\n하지만 안나는 대답하지 않았다.\n\n\"아니에요, 아니에요! 언니는 내 처지를 어떻게 보세요, 그걸 어떻게 생각하세요?\" 그녀가 물었다.\n\n\"내 생각엔……\" 다리야 알렉산드로브나가 입을 열려는 바로 그 순간, 바센카 베슬롭스키가 조랑말을 오른쪽 다리부터 내딛게 하여 질주시키며, 짧은 윗도리 차림으로 가죽 안장의 섀미가죽 위에서 묵직하게 위아래로 들썩이면서 그녀들 곁을 지나쳐 달려갔다. \"되네요, 안나 아르카지예브나!\" 그가 소리쳤다.\n\n안나는 그를 거들떠보지도 않았다. 하지만 다리야 알렉산드로브나는 또다시 마차 안에서 그런 긴 이야기를 시작하는 게 어울리지 않는 것처럼 여겨졌고, 그래서 자기 생각을 짧게 잘랐다.\n\n\"난 아무 생각도 안 해,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"하지만 난 늘 너를 사랑했고, 누군가를 사랑한다면 그 사람을 통째로, 있는 그대로 사랑하는 거지, 자기가 바라는 모습대로 사랑하는 게 아니거든……\"\n\n안나는 친구의 얼굴에서 눈을 떼어 눈꺼풀을 내리깔고는(이것은 돌리가 전에 본 적 없는 새 버릇이었다) 그 말의 온전한 의미를 꿰뚫어 보려는 듯 곰곰이 생각에 잠겼다. 그러더니 분명 자기가 바라던 대로 그 말을 해석하고는 돌리를 흘끗 바라보았다.\n\n\"언니한테 무슨 죄가 있다 해도,\" 그녀가 말했다, \"나를 보러 와 준 것과 그 말 한마디로 모두 용서받을 거예요.\"\n\n그리고 돌리는 그녀의 눈에 눈물이 고인 것을 보았다. 그녀는 말없이 안나의 손을 꼭 잡았다.\n\n\"그래, 이 건물들은 다 뭐니? 어쩜 이렇게 많아!\" 잠시 침묵한 뒤 그녀는 다시 물었다.\n\n\"이건 하인들 집이랑 곳간, 마구간이에요.\" 안나가 대답했다. \"그리고 저기서부터 공원이 시작돼요. 다 폐허가 돼 있던 걸 알렉세이가 전부 새로 손봤어요. 그이는 이곳을 무척 좋아하는데, 내가 전혀 기대하지 않았던 건, 그이가 이곳을 돌보는 데 푹 빠져 버렸다는 거예요. 그이는 워낙 풍요로운 천성을 타고났거든요! 무슨 일을 하든 멋지게 해내요. 지루해하기는커녕 열정적인 관심을 쏟으며 일해요. 내가 아는 그이의 기질로 보자면—그런 사람이 신중하고 빈틈없는 일류 경영자가 돼서, 땅을 관리하는 데 한 푼까지 꼼꼼히 따진다니까요. 하지만 그건 그 일에서만 그래요. 수만 단위가 걸린 문제라면 돈 따위는 생각도 안 하거든요.\" 그녀는 여자들이 흔히 자기들만 아는 사랑하는 사람의 은밀한 특성을 이야기할 때 짓는 그런 즐겁고 짓궂은 미소를 띠며 말했다. \"저 큰 건물 보여요? 저게 새 병원이에요. 십만 루블도 넘게 들 것 같아요. 그게 요즘 그이가 빠져 있는 일이죠. 그런데 그게 다 어떻게 시작됐는지 알아요? 농부들이 풀밭인가를 좀 더 싼값에 달라고 부탁했는데 그이가 거절했고, 그래서 내가 그이더러 인색하다고 나무랐죠. 물론 정말 그것 때문은 아니었지만, 이런저런 게 다 합쳐져서, 그이가 자기는 돈에 인색한 게 아니라는 걸 증명하려고, 알겠어요, 이 병원을 짓기 시작한 거예요. C'est une petitesse, 굳이 말하자면 그렇지만, 난 그래서 그이를 더 사랑해요. 자, 이제 곧 집이 보일 거예요. 그이 할아버지가 살던 집인데, 바깥은 하나도 바꾸지 않았어요.\"\n* C'est une petitesse: 프랑스어로 '그건 옹졸한 짓이죠'.\n\n\"어쩜 이렇게 아름답니!\" 돌리가 정원의 오래된 나무들의 가지각색 초록빛 사이로 우뚝 서 있는, 기둥이 늘어선 멋진 집을 자기도 모르게 감탄하며 바라보고 말했다.\n\n\"근사하죠? 그리고 저 집 꼭대기에서 내려다보는 경치가 또 기막혀요.\"\n\n그들은 자갈이 깔리고 꽃들로 화사한 안뜸으로 들어섰는데, 거기서는 일꾼 둘이 화단의 부드러운 흙 둘레에 돌로 가장자리를 두르는 작업을 하고 있었고, 마차는 지붕이 덮인 현관에 멈춰 섰다.\n\n\"아, 다들 벌써 와 있네!\" 안나가 막 계단에서 끌려 나가고 있는 안장 말들을 보며 말했다. \"예쁜 말이죠, 그렇죠? 내 조랑말인데, 내가 제일 좋아하는 거예요. 이리 데려와서 설탕을 좀 가져와요. 백작은 어디 있죠?\" 그녀가 쪼르르 달려 나온 말쑥한 하인 둘에게 물었다. \"아, 저기 오네!\" 그녀는 브론스키가 베슬롭스키와 함께 자기를 맞으러 오는 것을 보며 말했다.\n\n\"공작부인을 어느 방에 모실까요?\" 브론스키가 프랑스어로 안나에게 물었고, 대답도 기다리지 않은 채 다리야 알렉산드로브나에게 다시 한 번 인사하며 이번에는 그녀의 손에 입을 맞추었다. \"큰 발코니 방이 좋을 것 같은데요.\"\n\n\"오, 안 돼요, 거긴 너무 멀어요! 모퉁이 방이 낫겠어요, 그래야 우리가 더 자주 볼 수 있죠. 자, 올라가요.\" 안나는 하인이 가져다준 설탕을 자기가 좋아하는 말에게 주며 말했다.\n\n\"Pardon, j'en ai tout plein les poches.\" 그는 미소를 지으며 조끼 주머니에 손가락을 넣고 대답했다.\n\n* Pardon, j'en ai tout plein les poches: 프랑스어로 '미안합니다, 주머니에 가득 들어 있는걸요'.\n\n\"Mais vous venez trop tard.\" 그녀는 말이 설탕을 받아 먹으면서 침을 묻혀 놓은 자기 손을 손수건으로 문지르며 말했다.\n\n* Mais vous venez trop tard: 프랑스어로 '하지만 당신은 너무 늦게 왔어요'.\n\n안나가 돌리를 돌아보았다. \"좀 머물 수 있죠? 단 하루만요? 그건 안 돼요!\"\n\"돌아가겠다고 약속했고, 또 아이들도……\" 돌리는 마차에서 가방을 꺼내야 하는 데다, 자기 얼굴이 분명 먼지투성이일 거라는 걸 알기에 거북함을 느끼며 말했다.\n\n\"안 돼요, 돌리, 언니!…… 뭐, 두고 보죠. 어서 와요, 어서요!\" 그러고는 안나가 돌리를 자기 방으로 이끌었다.\n\n그 방은 브론스키가 권했던 말쑥한 손님방이 아니라, 안나가 돌리더러 양해해 달라고 했던 바로 그 방이었다. 그런데 양해가 필요하다던 이 방은, 돌리가 그때껏 머물러 본 어느 방보다도 사치스러움으로 가득 차 있었으며, 그 사치스러움은 그녀에게 외국의 최고급 호텔들을 떠올리게 했다.\n\n\"자, 언니, 내가 얼마나 행복한지 몰라요!\" 안나가 승마복 차림 그대로 잠시 돌리 옆에 앉으며 말했다. \"다들 어떻게 지내는지 들려줘요. 스티바는 잠깐 얼굴만 봤는데, 그이는 아이들 얘기를 제대로 들려주지 못해요. 내가 제일 좋아하는 타냐는 어때요? 이제 제법 큰 아가씨가 됐겠죠?\"\n\n\"응, 키가 아주 크단다.\" 다리야 알렉산드로브나는 짤막하게 대답하면서, 자기가 아이들 얘기를 이토록 무덤덤하게 한다는 데에 스스로도 놀랐다. \"우리는 레빈네 집에서 즐겁게 지내고 있어.\" 그녀가 덧붙였다.\n\n\"아, 진작 알았더라면,\" 안나가 말했다, \"언니가 나를 경멸하지 않는다는 걸요!…… 다들 우리한테 왔으면 좋았을 텐데. 알다시피 스티바는 알렉세이의 오랜 친구이자 둘도 없는 벗이거든요.\" 그녀는 이렇게 덧붙이고는 갑자기 얼굴을 붉혔다.\n\n\"그래, 하지만 우리는 다들……\" 돌리가 당황하여 대답했다.\n\n\"하지만 내가 너무 기쁜 나머지 쓸데없는 소리를 하고 있네요. 언니, 한 가지 분명한 건, 언니가 와 줘서 내가 너무나 기쁘다는 거예요!\" 안나가 그녀에게 다시 입을 맞추며 말했다. \"언니는 아직 나를 어떻게 또 어떻게 생각하는지 말해 주지 않았는데, 난 자꾸만 그게 알고 싶어요. 하지만 언니가 나를 있는 그대로 봐 줄 거라 기뻐요. 내가 제일 싫은 건 사람들이 내가 뭔가를 증명하고 싶어 한다고 상상하는 거예요. 난 아무것도 증명하고 싶지 않아요. 난 그저 살고 싶을 뿐이에요, 나 자신 말고는 누구에게도 해를 끼치지 않으면서요. 나한테 그럴 권리는 있는 거죠, 그렇지 않나요? 하지만 그건 큰 이야기니까, 나중에 모든 걸 차근차근 얘기해요. 이제 난 가서 옷을 갈아입고 언니한테 하녀를 보낼게요.\"\n\n# Chapter 19\n\n혼자 남게 되자 다리아 알렉산드로브나는 살림 잘하는 안주인의 눈으로 자기 방을 찬찬히 둘러보았다. 이 집에 들어서면서 본 것이며 집 안을 걸어 지나오며 본 모든 것, 그리고 지금 자기 방에서 보고 있는 모든 것이, 그녀에게 부유함과 호사스러움, 그리고 영국 소설에서나 읽었을 뿐 러시아에서는, 더구나 시골에서는 한 번도 본 적 없는 그 현대 유럽풍 사치의 인상을 안겨 주었다. 벽에 걸린 새 프랑스제 벽포에서부터 바닥 전체를 덮은 양탄자에 이르기까지 모든 것이 새것이었다. 침대에는 스프링 매트리스가 깔려 있었고, 특별한 형태의 긴 베개와 작은 베개들에는 비단 베갯잇이 씌워져 있었다. 대리석 세면대, 화장대, 작은 소파, 탁자들, 벽난로 위의 청동 시계, 창문 커튼, 그리고 portières까지 모두 새것이고 값비싼 것들이었다.\n\n머리를 높이 틀어 올리고 돌리의 것보다 더 멋스러운 가운을 입은 채 시중을 들겠다며 들어온 맵시 있는 하녀도, 방 전체와 마찬가지로 새것이고 값비싼 존재였다. 다리아 알렉산드로브나는 그녀의 단정함과 공손하고 사근사근한 태도가 마음에 들었지만, 그녀 앞에서는 어쩐지 마음이 편치 않았다. 그녀는 하녀가, 그만 실수로 잘못 챙겨 넣은 기운 화장 재킷을 보는 것이 부끄러웠다. 집에서는 그토록 자랑스러워하던 바로 그 기운 자리며 꿰맨 데가 부끄러웠다. 집에서는 화장 재킷 여섯 벌에 야드당 십육 펜스짜리 면포 스물네 야드가 필요하고, 그것이 재단과 바느질 값을 빼고도 삼십 실링이나 된다는 것이 너무도 분명했으며, 그 삼십 실링을 아껴 두었던 것이다. 그러나 하녀 앞에서 그녀는 부끄럽다고까지는 할 수 없어도 적어도 거북함을 느꼈다.\n\n다리아 알렉산드로브나는 여러 해 전부터 알고 지내던 안누슈카가 들어오자 크게 안도했다. 맵시 있는 하녀는 주인마님에게 가라고 불려 갔고, 안누슈카가 다리아 알렉산드로브나 곁에 남았다.\n\n안누슈카는 그 부인이 온 것이 무척이나 기쁜 모양이었고, 쉬지 않고 재잘거리기 시작했다. 돌리는 그녀가 자기 주인마님의 처지에 대해, 특히 백작이 안나 아르카지예브나에게 보이는 사랑과 헌신에 대해 의견을 토로하고 싶어 안달이라는 것을 알아챘지만, 그녀가 그 이야기를 꺼내려 들 때마다 조심스럽게 말을 가로막았다.\n\n\"전 안나 아르카지예브나 마님과 함께 자랐답니다. 제겐 마님이 무엇보다 소중한 분이지요. 뭐, 저희가 이러쿵저러쿵 판단할 일은 아니지만요. 그래도 정말이지, 그토록 사랑이 깊어 보이는 게…….\"\n\n\"수고스럽지만 이제 씻게 물 좀 부어 줘요.\" 다리아 알렉산드로브나가 그녀의 말을 잘랐다.\n\n\"그럼요. 자잘한 것들 빠는 일만 따로 맡은 여자가 둘이나 있답니다만, 빨래는 거의 다 기계로 해요. 백작님은 무슨 일이든 직접 손수 챙기시죠. 아, 정말 대단한 남편이세요!…….\"\n\n돌리는 안나가 들어와 그 등장으로 안누슈카의 수다를 멈추게 해 주자 다행스러웠다.\n\n안나는 아주 수수한 옥양목 가운을 입고 있었다. 돌리는 그 수수한 가운을 유심히 뜯어보았다. 그녀는 그것이 무엇을 의미하는지, 그런 수수함이 어떤 값을 치르고 얻어지는지 알고 있었다.\n\n\"오랜 친구예요.\" 안나가 안누슈카를 두고 말했다.\n\n이제 안나는 당황하지 않았다. 그녀는 완벽하게 차분하고 편안했다. 돌리는 그녀가 자기가 도착했을 때 받았던 인상에서 이제 완전히 벗어났으며, 마치 자기의 더 깊은 감정과 생각이 담긴 칸막이의 문을 닫아 버리기라도 하듯, 그 피상적이고 무심한 어조를 취했다는 것을 알아챘다.\n\n\"그래, 안나, 어린 딸은 어때요?\" 돌리가 물었다.\n\n\"애니 말이에요?\" (그녀는 어린 딸 안나를 이렇게 불렀다.) \"아주 잘 있어요. 놀랄 만큼 잘 자랐답니다. 보고 싶어요? 자, 보여 줄게요. 유모들 때문에 어찌나 골치가 아팠는지 몰라요.\" 그녀가 이야기를 시작했다. \"이탈리아 유모를 두었었죠. 사람은 착한데, 어찌나 멍청하던지! 내보내려고 했지만, 애가 워낙 그 여자한테 익숙해져서 여태 데리고 있어요.\"\n\n\"하지만 어떻게 한 거예요?……\" 돌리는 그 작은 여자아이가 어떤 성을 갖게 될지 물어보려고 했다. 그러나 안나의 얼굴에 별안간 떠오른 찌푸림을 알아차리고는, 질문의 방향을 바꾸었다.\n\n\"어떻게 한 거예요? 벌써 젖을 뗐나요?\"\n\n하지만 안나는 이미 알아들었다.\n\n\"그걸 물어보려던 건 아니죠? 아이의 성을 물어보려던 거였죠. 그렇죠? 그게 알렉세이를 괴롭히고 있어요. 아이에겐 성이 없어요—그러니까, 카레니나죠.\" 안나는 눈꺼풀을 내리깔아 마주 닿은 속눈썹밖에 보이지 않을 정도가 되도록 했다. \"하지만 그건 나중에 다 이야기해요.\" 그녀의 얼굴이 갑자기 환해졌다. \"자, 보여 줄게요. Elle est très gentille. 이제 기어 다닌답니다.\"\n\n* Elle est très gentille: 프랑스어로 '아이가 무척 사랑스러워요'.\n아이 방에서는 온 집 안에서 돌리를 압도했던 호화로움이 그녀를 한층 더 강하게 사로잡았다. 영국에서 주문한 작은 유모차들이며, 걸음마를 배우는 기구들이며, 일부러 기어 다니기 좋게 만든 당구대 모양의 소파며, 그네며 욕조며, 모두가 특별한 양식의 현대식 물건들이었다. 죄다 영국제에다 튼튼하고 솜씨 좋게 만든 것이어서, 누가 봐도 무척 값비싼 것들이었다. 방은 널찍하고, 아주 환하고 천장이 높았다.\n\n그들이 들어갔을 때, 아기는 조그만 속옷 하나만 걸친 채 식탁 앞 작은 팔걸이의자에 앉아, 죽을 먹고 있었는데 그것을 자기 작은 가슴팍에 온통 흘리고 있었다. 아기에게 음식을 먹이는 중이었고, 러시아인 보모는 누가 봐도 아기의 식사를 같이 나눠 먹고 있었다. 유모도 우두머리 보모도 거기에는 없었다. 그들은 옆방에 있었고, 거기서 그들이 서로 소통할 유일한 수단인 그 기묘한 프랑스어로 나누는 대화 소리가 들려왔다.\n\n안나의 목소리를 들은 키 크고 말쑥한 영국인 보모가, 불쾌한 얼굴에 방탕한 표정을 짓고는 금발 곱슬머리를 황급히 흔들며 문으로 걸어 들어왔고, 안나가 나무라지도 않았는데 곧장 자기변명을 늘어놓기 시작했다. 안나가 한마디 할 때마다, 그 영국인 보모는 \"예, 마님\" 하고 황급히 몇 번이고 되풀이했다.\n\n검은 눈썹에 검은 머리카락을 가진 발그레한 아기는, 통통하고 불그스레한 작은 몸뚱이에 살갗이 닭살처럼 팽팽하게 돋아 있었는데, 낯선 사람을 노려보는 그 뾰로통한 표정에도 불구하고 다리야 알렉산드로브나의 마음을 사로잡았다. 그녀는 아기의 건강한 모습이 정말로 부러웠다. 아기가 기어 다니는 모습도 마음에 쏙 들었다. 그녀 자신의 아이들 가운데는 누구도 저렇게 기어 다닌 적이 없었다. 아기를 양탄자 위에 내려놓고 작은 치맛자락을 뒤로 걷어 올려 주자, 그 모습이 놀랄 만큼 사랑스러웠다. 어떤 작은 들짐승처럼 반짝이는 검은 눈으로 어른인 큰 사람들을 둘러보던 아기는, 그들이 자기를 보고 감탄하는 것이 기쁜 게 틀림없이 방긋 웃고는, 다리를 옆으로 짚고 두 팔에 힘껏 힘을 주어 등 전체를 잽싸게 끌어당긴 다음, 작은 팔로 또 한 걸음을 앞으로 내디뎠다.\n\n하지만 아이 방의 전반적인 분위기, 특히 그 영국인 보모가 다리야 알렉산드로브나는 전혀 마음에 들지 않았다. 사람을 꿰뚫어 보는 안나가 어떻게 저토록 호감 안 가고 평판 나빠 보이는 여자를 제 아이의 보모로 둘 수 있었는지, 다리야 알렉산드로브나는 안나처럼 어수선한 가정에는 변변한 보모라면 들어오려 하지 않았으리라는 추측으로만 스스로에게 설명할 수 있었다.\n\n게다가 몇 마디 흘러나온 말로 미루어, 다리야 알렉산드로브나는 안나와 두 보모와 아이 사이에 공통된 삶이라는 것이 없고, 어머니의 방문이 무언가 예외적인 일이라는 것을 단번에 알아챘다. 안나는 아기에게 장난감을 가져다주려 했으나, 그것을 찾지 못했다.\n\n무엇보다 놀라운 것은, 아기가 이가 몇 개 났느냐는 물음에 안나가 틀리게 대답했고, 마지막에 난 두 개에 대해서는 아무것도 모르고 있었다는 사실이었다.\n\n\"가끔은 내가 여기서 이렇게 군더더기 같은 존재라는 게 미안해져요.\" 안나는 아이 방을 나서면서, 문간에 놓인 장난감을 피하느라 치맛자락을 들어 올린 채 말했다. \"첫째 아이 때는 전혀 달랐는데.\"\n\n\"저는 반대일 거라고 생각했어요.\" 다리야 알렉산드로브나가 수줍게 말했다.\n\n\"오, 아니에요! 그런데 말이에요, 내가 세료자를 봤다는 거 알아요?\" 안나는 마치 멀리 있는 무언가를 바라보듯 눈을 가늘게 뜨며 말했다. \"하지만 그건 나중에 이야기해요. 믿기지 않겠지만, 나는 마치 굶주린 거지 여자 앞에 푸짐한 만찬이 차려졌는데 무엇부터 손대야 할지 모르는 꼴이에요. 그 만찬이 바로 당신이고, 또 당신과 나눌 이야기들이에요. 다른 누구와도 결코 나눌 수 없는 그런 이야기들 말이에요. 그러니 어떤 화제부터 먼저 꺼내야 할지 모르겠어요. Mais je ne vous ferai grâce de rien. 당신에게 전부 다 털어놓아야겠어요.\"\n* Mais je ne vous ferai grâce de rien: 프랑스어로 '하지만 무엇 하나 빼놓지 않을 거예요'.\n\n\"아, 우리 집에서 만나게 될 사람들을 대충 그려 줘야겠네요.\" 그녀가 말을 이었다. \"여자들부터 시작할게요. 바르바라 공작 영애—그 사람은 당신도 알고, 그 사람에 대한 당신과 스티바의 생각도 내가 알아요. 스티바는 그 사람이 사는 목적 전부가 카체리나 파블로브나 이모보다 자기가 우월하다는 걸 증명하는 데 있다고 말하죠. 그건 다 사실이에요. 하지만 사람은 마음씨가 좋고, 나는 그 사람에게 무척 고마워하고 있어요. 페테르부르크에서는 나에게 보호자가 절대적으로 필요한 순간이 있었어요. 그때 그 사람이 나타났죠. 하지만 정말로 마음씨가 좋은 사람이에요. 내 처지를 누그러뜨리는 데 큰 도움을 줬어요. 당신은 내 처지의 그 모든 어려움을 다 이해하지 못하는 것 같네요…… 거기 페테르부르크에서요.\" 그녀가 덧붙였다. \"여기서 나는 더없이 편안하고 행복해요. 뭐, 그것도 나중에 이야기해요. 그리고 스비아시스키—그 사람은 군의 귀족회장이고, 아주 좋은 사람인데, 알렉세이에게서 뭔가 얻어 내고 싶어 해요. 알다시피, 우리가 이제 시골에 자리를 잡았으니, 그 사람의 영지를 생각하면 알렉세이가 큰 영향력을 행사할 수 있거든요. 그리고 투시케비치—당신도 그 사람을 본 적 있죠, 알잖아요—베치를 흠모하던 사람이에요. 이제는 차였고, 그래서 우리를 찾아왔어요. 알렉세이 말로는, 그는 보이려고 애쓰는 그대로 받아들여 주면 아주 유쾌한 그런 부류의 사람이에요. 그리고 et puis il est comme il faut, 바르바라 공작 영애가 하는 말이죠. 그리고 베슬로프스키…… 당신도 그 사람을 알아요. 아주 괜찮은 청년이죠.\" 그녀가 말하자, 짓궂은 미소가 그녀의 입가를 휘게 했다. \"그 사람과 레빈 부부 사이의 그 황당한 이야기는 도대체 뭐예요? 베슬로프스키가 알렉세이에게 그 이야기를 했는데, 우리는 믿지 않아요. Il est très gentil et naïf.\" 그녀가 똑같은 미소를 지으며 다시 말했다. \"남자들에게는 할 일이 필요하고, 알렉세이에게는 어울릴 사람들이 필요해요. 그래서 나는 이 사람들을 다 소중히 여기죠. 집을 활기차고 즐겁게 만들어야, 알렉세이가 무슨 색다른 것을 갈망하지 않을 테니까요. 그리고 집사도 보게 될 거예요—독일 사람인데, 아주 좋은 친구이고, 자기 일을 잘 알아요. 알렉세이가 그 사람을 아주 높이 평가하죠. 그리고 의사도 있는데, 젊은 사람이고, 아마 완전한 니힐리스트까지는 아니겠지만, 알다시피, 칼로 음식을 찍어 먹는 그런 부류죠…… 그래도 아주 좋은 의사예요. 그리고 건축가도…… Une petite cour!\"\n* et puis il est comme il faut: 프랑스어로 '게다가 그 사람은 점잖아요'. * Il est très gentil et naïf: 프랑스어로 '그 사람은 무척 상냥하고 순진해요'. * Une petite cour!: 프랑스어로 '하나의 작은 궁정이죠!'.\n\n# Chapter 20\n\n바르바라 공작 부인은 그늘 속에서 자수틀 앞에 앉아 알렉세이 키릴로비치 백작의 안락의자에 씌울 덮개를 만들고 있었는데, 안나가 다리아 알렉산드로브나를 데리고 그 돌 테라스로 나오며 말했다. \"공작 부인, 그토록 보고 싶어 하시던 돌리를 모시고 왔어요. 식사 전에는 아무것도 필요 없다고 하지만, 그래도 점심을 좀 차리라고 일러 주세요. 저는 알렉세이를 찾으러 가서 다들 데려올게요.\"\n\n바르바라 공작 부인은 돌리를 다정하면서도 다소 생색을 내는 태도로 맞이하더니, 곧바로 자기가 안나와 함께 사는 까닭을 설명하기 시작했다. 안나를 키워 준 이모 카체리나 파블로브나보다 늘 안나를 더 아껴 왔으며, 모두가 안나를 저버린 지금 이 가장 힘든 과도기에 안나를 돕는 것이 자신의 의무라고 여긴다는 것이었다.\n\n\"남편이 이혼을 해 줄 테고, 그러면 나는 다시 내 고적한 생활로 돌아갈 거예요. 하지만 지금은 내가 쓸모가 있고, 또 나는 내 의무를 다하고 있죠, 그게 아무리 힘들지라도 말이에요—다른 어떤 사람들과는 다르게요. 그리고 와 주다니 얼마나 고마운지, 얼마나 잘한 일인지! 저 두 사람은 더할 나위 없이 훌륭한 부부처럼 살고 있어요. 저들을 심판하는 건 하느님의 몫이지 우리 몫이 아니에요. 비류조프스키와 아베니예바 부인도 그랬잖아요…… 그리고 삼 니칸드로프와, 바실리예프와 마모노바 부인, 또 리자 넵투노바도…… 그들에 대해서는 아무도 뭐라 하지 않았던가요? 결국엔 모두에게 받아들여지는 걸로 끝났잖아요. 게다가 c’est un intérieur si joli, si comme il faut. Tout-à-fait à l’anglaise. On se réunit le matin au breakfast, et puis on se sépare. 식사 시간 전까지는 다들 제멋대로 지내요. 만찬은 일곱 시고요. 스티바가 당신을 보낸 건 아주 옳은 일이었어요. 그이는 저 두 사람의 지지가 필요하니까요. 알다시피 그이는 어머니와 형을 통해 무슨 일이든 할 수 있잖아요. 게다가 저 두 사람은 좋은 일도 참 많이 해요. 그이가 병원 얘기는 하지 않던가요? Ce sera admirable—전부 파리에서 들여온 거예요.\"\n\n두 사람의 대화는 안나에 의해 중단되었다. 안나는 당구실에서 일행의 남자들을 찾아내 그들을 데리고 테라스로 돌아온 것이었다. 만찬 시간까지는 아직 한참 남아 있었고, 날씨도 더없이 좋아서 다음 두 시간을 보낼 여러 가지 방법이 제안되었다. 보즈드비젠스코예에는 시간을 보내는 방법이 매우 많았는데, 그것들은 모두 포크로프스코예에서 쓰던 방법과는 달랐다.\n\n\"Une partie de lawn-tennis,\" 베슬로프스키가 그 잘생긴 미소를 지으며 제안했다. \"안나 아르카지예브나, 우리 또 짝을 합시다.\"\n\n\"안 돼요, 너무 더워요. 차라리 정원을 거닐다가 보트를 타는 게 낫겠어요. 다리아 알렉산드로브나에게 강기슭을 보여 드리자고요.\" 브론스키가 제안했다.\n\n\"저는 뭐든 좋습니다.\" 스비야시스키가 말했다.\n\n\"돌리가 제일 좋아할 만한 건 산책일 것 같은데—그렇지 않아? 그러고 나서 보트를 타는 거지, 어쩌면.\" 안나가 말했다.\n\n그렇게 정해졌다. 베슬로프스키와 투시케비치는 보트를 준비해 놓고 거기서 기다리겠다고 약속하며 수영장으로 떠났다.\n\n그들은 두 쌍으로 나뉘어 오솔길을 따라 걸었는데, 안나는 스비야시스키와, 돌리는 브론스키와 함께였다. 돌리는 자신이 놓인 이 새로운 환경 속에서 조금 거북하고 불안했다. 추상적으로, 이론적으로 그녀는 안나의 처신을 정당하다고 여길 뿐 아니라 진정으로 찬성하고 있었다. 흠잡을 데 없이 정숙한 여인들, 점잖은 생활의 단조로움에 지친 여인들에게 흔히 그러하듯이, 그녀도 멀찍이 떨어진 자리에서는 불륜의 사랑을 너그러이 봐줄 뿐 아니라 오히려 진심으로 부러워했다. 게다가 그녀는 안나를 진심으로 사랑했다. 하지만 이 낯선 사람들 틈에서, 다리아 알렉산드로브나에게는 너무도 생소한 이 세련된 분위기 속에서 실제로 살아가는 안나를 보고 있자니 마음이 편치 않았다. 그녀가 특히 못마땅했던 것은, 바르바라 공작 부인이 자기가 누리는 편안함을 위해 모든 것을 못 본 척할 태세라는 점이었다.\n\n일반적인 원칙으로서, 추상적으로는 돌리도 안나의 행동을 찬성했다. 그러나 안나가 그런 행동을 한 상대인 그 남자를 직접 보는 것은 그녀로서는 거북한 일이었다. 더구나 그녀는 한 번도 브론스키를 좋아한 적이 없었다. 그녀는 그가 무척 거만하다고 여겼고, 그에게서 자랑할 만한 것이라곤 재산 말고는 아무것도 찾아볼 수 없다고 생각했다. 그런데 본인의 뜻과는 달리, 그의 집인 이곳에서 그는 그 어느 때보다도 그녀를 주눅 들게 했고, 그녀는 그와 함께 있는 것이 편하지 않았다. 그녀가 그와 함께 있으면서 느낀 감정은 자기 화장 가운을 두고 하녀에게 느꼈던 것과 똑같았다. 하녀 앞에서 정확히 부끄럽다기보다 기운 자국 때문에 거북했던 것처럼, 그녀는 그 앞에서 정확히 부끄럽다기보다 자기 자신이 거북했다.\n\n돌리는 마음이 편치 않아 대화의 화제를 찾으려 애썼다. 그의 거만함을 생각하면 집과 정원에 대한 칭찬이 그에게 분명 거북하게 들리리라고 짐작하면서도, 그녀는 그래도 자기가 그의 집을 얼마나 마음에 들어 하는지 말해 주었다.\n\n\"네, 아주 훌륭한 건물이죠. 멋스러운 옛날식이고요.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"계단 앞의 안마당이 정말 마음에 들어요. 늘 저랬나요?\"\n\n\"오, 아니에요!\" 그가 말했고, 그의 얼굴은 기쁨으로 환하게 빛났다. \"지난봄에 저 안마당을 보셨더라면!\"\n\n그러고는 처음에는 다소 머뭇거리며, 그러나 이야기를 이어 갈수록 점점 더 화제에 빠져들면서, 그는 그녀에게 집과 정원의 장식에 관한 갖가지 세부를 일러 주기 시작했다. 자기 집을 개량하고 아름답게 꾸미는 데 많은 공을 들인 만큼, 브론스키는 그 성과를 새로운 사람에게 자랑하고 싶은 욕구를 느꼈으며, 다리아 알렉산드로브나의 칭찬에 진심으로 기뻐하는 것이 분명했다.\n\n\"병원을 한번 보고 싶으시면, 그리고 피곤하지 않으시면, 사실 멀지도 않으니까요. 가 보실까요?\" 그는 그녀가 지루해하지 않는지 확인하려고 그녀의 얼굴을 들여다보며 말했다. \"안나, 당신도 갈래?\" 그가 그녀를 돌아보았다.\n\n\"우리도 가야죠, 그렇죠?\" 그녀가 스비야시스키를 향해 말했다. \"Mais il ne faut pas laisser le pauvre Veslovsky et Tushkevitch se morfondre là dans le bateau. 사람을 보내서 알려 줘야겠어요.\"\n\n\"네, 이게 그이가 여기에 세우고 있는 기념비예요.\" 안나가 앞서 병원 이야기를 할 때 지었던, 모든 걸 다 안다는 듯한 그 짓궂은 미소를 띤 채 돌리를 향해 말했다.\n\n\"오, 정말로 중요한 일이죠!\" 스비야시스키가 말했다. 하지만 자기가 브론스키에게 비위를 맞추려는 게 아님을 보이려고 그는 곧바로 다소 비판적인 말을 덧붙였다.\n\n\"그런데 말입니다, 백작.\" 그가 말했다. \"농민들의 건강을 위해서는 그렇게 많은 일을 하시면서, 학교에는 그토록 관심이 적으시다니 의아하군요.\"\n\n\"C’est devenu tellement commun les écoles,\" 브론스키가 말했다. \"아시다시피 그 때문은 아니고, 그저 어쩌다 보니 그렇게 됐을 뿐이에요. 제 관심이 다른 데로 쏠려 버린 거죠. 그럼 병원은 이쪽입니다.\" 그가 가로수길에서 갈라지는 길을 가리키며 다리아 알렉산드로브나에게 말했다.\n\n부인들은 양산을 펼쳐 들고 옆길로 접어들었다. 몇 굽이를 돌고 작은 문 하나를 지나자, 다리아 알렉산드로브나는 자기 앞 언덕배기에 거의 완성된, 거창해 보이는 커다란 붉은 건물이 서 있는 것을 보았다. 아직 칠하지 않은 철제 지붕이 햇빛을 받아 눈부시게 반짝였다. 완성된 건물 옆에는 또 다른 건물이 비계에 둘러싸인 채 막 짓기 시작한 상태였다. 앞치마를 두른 일꾼들이 비계 위에 서서 벽돌을 쌓고, 통에서 모르타르를 부어 흙손으로 매끈하게 펴 바르고 있었다.\n\n\"댁에서는 일이 정말 빨리 진행되는군요!\" 스비야시스키가 말했다. \"제가 지난번에 왔을 때만 해도 지붕이 없었는데요.\"\n\n\"가을이면 다 준비될 거예요. 안쪽은 거의 다 됐어요.\" 안나가 말했다.\n\n\"그런데 이 새 건물은 뭔가요?\"\n\n\"저건 의사가 묵을 집과 조제실이에요.\" 짧은 윗옷 차림의 건축가가 자기 쪽으로 다가오는 것을 본 브론스키가 대답했다. 그러고는 부인들에게 양해를 구하고 그를 맞으러 갔다.\n\n일꾼들이 석회를 개고 있는 구덩이를 돌아, 그는 건축가와 함께 멈춰 서서 다소 열을 올리며 이야기하기 시작했다.\n\n\"정면이 아직 너무 낮아요.\" 무슨 일이냐고 묻는 안나에게 그가 말했다.\n\n\"제가 기초를 더 높여야 한다고 말했잖아요.\" 안나가 말했다.\n\n\"네, 물론 그렇게 했더라면 훨씬 나았을 겁니다, 안나 아르카지예브나.\" 건축가가 말했다. \"하지만 이제는 너무 늦었어요.\"\n\n\"네, 저는 이 일에 무척 관심이 많아요.\" 안나가 그녀의 건축 지식에 놀라움을 표하는 스비야시스키에게 대답했다. \"이 새 건물은 병원과 조화를 이루었어야 했어요. 그런데 나중에야 생각해 낸 거라, 설계도 없이 짓기 시작한 거죠.\"\n\n브론스키는 건축가와의 이야기를 마치고 부인들에게 합류해, 그들을 병원 안으로 안내했다.\n\n바깥쪽 처마 돌림띠 작업은 아직 진행 중이었고 일층은 칠을 하고 있었지만, 위층은 거의 모든 방이 완성되어 있었다. 폭이 넓은 주철 계단을 올라 층계참에 이른 그들은 첫 번째 큰 방으로 들어섰다. 벽은 대리석처럼 보이도록 회칠이 되어 있었고, 거대한 판유리 창은 이미 끼워져 있었으며, 다만 쪽모이 마룻바닥만 아직 마무리되지 않은 상태였다. 마룻장 한 토막을 대패질하던 목수들이 일손을 멈추고는 머리를 묶고 있던 끈을 풀어 내며 양반들에게 인사를 했다.\n\n\"여기가 접수실입니다.\" 브론스키가 말했다. \"여기에는 책상 하나와 탁자들, 의자들이 놓일 거고, 그 밖에는 아무것도 없어요.\"\n\n\"이쪽이에요. 여기로 들어가요. 창문 가까이는 가지 마세요.\" 페인트가 말랐는지 만져 보며 안나가 말했다. \"알렉세이, 페인트가 벌써 다 말랐어요.\" 그녀가 덧붙였다.\n\n접수실에서 그들은 복도로 나왔다. 여기서 브론스키는 새로운 방식의 환기 장치를 그들에게 보여 주었다. 그다음 대리석 욕조와 비범한 용수철이 달린 침대들을 보여 주었다. 이어서 병실들을 차례차례 보여 주었고, 창고와 침구실을, 그다음에는 새로운 양식의 난방용 스토브를, 그다음에는 필요한 물건들을 복도로 나르면서도 아무 소리를 내지 않는 손수레들을, 그 밖에도 많은 것들을 보여 주었다. 최신 기계 설비에 정통한 스비야시스키는 모든 것을 빠짐없이 높이 평가했다. 돌리는 그저 전에 본 적 없는 것들에 놀라워하며, 그 모든 것을 이해하고 싶은 마음에 하나하나 꼬치꼬치 물었고, 그것은 브론스키에게 큰 만족을 안겨 주었다.\n\n\"네, 제 생각엔 이게 러시아에서 제대로 설비를 갖춘 유일한 병원 사례가 될 것 같습니다.\" 스비야시스키가 말했다.\n\n\"그런데 산실은 안 두실 건가요?\" 돌리가 물었다. \"시골에서는 그게 그토록 절실히 필요한데 말이에요. 저도 자주……\"\n\n평소의 정중함에도 불구하고 브론스키는 그녀의 말을 가로막았다.\n\n\"여기는 산실이 아니라 병자들을 위한 병원이고, 전염성 질환을 빼고는 모든 병을 위한 곳입니다.\" 그가 말했다. \"아! 이것 좀 보세요.\" 그러고는 회복기 환자들을 위해 막 주문해 들여온 환자용 의자 하나를 다리아 알렉산드로브나 쪽으로 굴려 왔다. \"보세요.\" 그는 그 의자에 앉아 그것을 움직이기 시작했다. \"환자가 걷질 못해요—아직 너무 약하거나, 다리에 무슨 문제가 있거나 해서요. 그래도 바람을 쐬어야 하니, 이렇게 움직여서 스스로 굴려 가는 거죠……\"\n\n다리아 알렉산드로브나는 모든 것이 흥미로웠다. 그녀는 모든 것이 무척 마음에 들었지만, 무엇보다도 마음에 든 것은 그 꾸밈없고 순박한 열정을 지닌 브론스키 그 자신이었다. '그래, 정말 좋은 사람, 선량한 사람이야.' 그녀는 그가 하는 말은 듣지 못한 채 그를 바라보며 그의 표정 속으로 파고들면서, 마음속으로 자기를 안나의 자리에 놓아 보았다. 지금 그 열렬한 관심을 지닌 그가 너무도 마음에 들어서, 그녀는 안나가 어떻게 그를 사랑할 수 있었는지 알 것 같았다.\n\n# Chapter 21\n\n\"아니, 공작 부인께서 피곤하실 것 같고, 말에는 흥미가 없으실 테니까요.\" 브론스키는 마차 우리로 더 가 보고 싶어 하는 안나에게 말했다. 그곳에서는 스비아시스키가 새로 들여온 종마를 보고 싶어 했다. \"당신은 가 봐요. 나는 공작 부인을 댁까지 모셔다드리면서 잠깐 이야기를 나누고 올 테니.\" 그가 말했다. \"괜찮으시다면 말입니다.\" 그는 그녀를 돌아보며 덧붙였다.\n\n\"저는 말에 대해서는 아무것도 모르는걸요, 그러니 기꺼이요.\" 다리아 알렉산드로브나가 다소 놀라며 대답했다.\n\n그녀는 브론스키의 얼굴에서 그가 자기에게 무언가를 바라고 있다는 것을 알아챘다. 그녀의 짐작은 틀리지 않았다. 작은 쪽문을 지나 다시 정원으로 들어서자마자, 그는 안나가 간 방향을 살펴보았고, 그녀가 자기들의 말을 듣지도 보지도 못한다는 것을 확인한 뒤 입을 열었다.\n\n\"제가 부인께 드리고 싶은 말씀이 있다는 걸 짐작하고 계시지요.\" 그가 웃음 띤 눈으로 그녀를 바라보며 말했다. \"부인을 안나의 벗으로 여기는 제 생각이 틀리지 않았겠지요.\" 그는 모자를 벗고 손수건을 꺼내 머리를 닦았는데, 그 머리는 점점 벗어지고 있었다.\n\n다리아 알렉산드로브나는 아무 대답도 하지 않고 그저 당황한 채 그를 바라보았다. 그와 단둘이 남게 되자, 그녀는 문득 두려움을 느꼈다. 그의 웃음 띤 눈과 엄숙한 표정이 그녀를 겁먹게 했다.\n\n그가 자기에게 무슨 말을 하려는 것일까, 온갖 갖가지 추측이 머릿속을 스쳐 갔다. \"아이들을 데리고 자기들 집에 와서 지내라고 청하려는 거겠지, 그럼 나는 거절해야 할 테고. 아니면 모스크바에서 안나를 받아 줄 모임을 만들어 달라고……. 아니면 바센카 베슬로프스키와 안나의 관계 때문일까? 아니면 어쩌면 키티 일로, 자기가 잘못했다고 느끼는 걸까?\" 그녀의 짐작은 하나같이 불쾌했지만, 그녀는 그가 정작 무슨 이야기를 하고 싶어 하는지는 알아맞히지 못했다.\n\n\"부인은 안나에게 큰 영향력을 가지고 계시고, 안나도 부인을 무척 좋아합니다.\" 그가 말했다. \"제발 저를 좀 도와주세요.\"\n\n다리아 알렉산드로브나는 보리수 아래에서 햇살을 받아 군데군데 환하게 밝아졌다가 다시 온통 그늘 속으로 잠기곤 하는 그의 활기찬 얼굴을, 조심스러운 물음을 담아 바라보았다. 그녀는 그가 더 말하기를 기다렸지만, 그는 지팡이로 자갈을 긁적이며 말없이 그녀 곁을 걸을 뿐이었다.\n\n\"부인은 우리를 만나러 와 주셨습니다. 안나의 옛 벗들 중에서 유일하게—바르바라 공작 영애는 치지 않습니다—말이지요. 하지만 저는 압니다, 부인이 우리 처지를 정상이라 여겨서가 아니라, 이 처지의 온갖 어려움을 다 헤아리면서도 여전히 그녀를 사랑하고 그녀에게 힘이 되고자 하셨기에 그리하셨다는 것을요. 제가 부인을 옳게 이해한 겁니까?\" 그가 그녀를 돌아보며 물었다.\n\n\"아, 네.\" 다리아 알렉산드로브나가 양산을 내리며 대답했다. \"하지만…….\"\n\n\"아닙니다.\" 그가 말을 가로막았고, 자기 일행을 곤란한 처지에 몰아넣고 있다는 것도 깨닫지 못한 채 무심코 갑자기 걸음을 멈춰 서는 바람에, 그녀도 따라 멈춰 설 수밖에 없었다. \"안나의 처지가 안고 있는 온갖 어려움을 저보다 더 깊고 절실하게 느끼는 사람은 없습니다. 그 점은 부인께서도 잘 이해하실 겁니다, 제게 마음이라는 게 조금이라도 있다고 여겨 주신다면 말이지요. 그 처지는 제 잘못이고, 그래서 제가 그것을 느끼는 겁니다.\"\n\n\"이해해요.\" 다리아 알렉산드로브나는 그가 이 말을 하는 진실함과 단호함에 자기도 모르게 감탄하며 말했다. \"하지만 바로 당신이 스스로 책임을 느끼기 때문에, 그것을 너무 과장하시는 게 아닌가 싶어요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그녀가 사교계에서 처한 입장이 어렵다는 건 저도 충분히 이해할 수 있어요.\"\n\n\"사교계에서 그것은 지옥입니다!\" 그가 어둡게 미간을 찌푸리며 재빨리 내뱉었다. \"그녀가 페테르부르크에서 그 보름 동안 겪은 것보다 더 큰 정신적 고통은 부인께서는 상상도 못 하실 겁니다……. 부디 제 말을 믿어 주십시오.\"\n\n\"네, 하지만 여기서는, 안나도…… 당신도 사교계를 아쉬워하지 않는 한…….\"\n\n\"사교계요!\" 그가 경멸하듯 말했다. \"제가 어떻게 사교계를 아쉬워하겠습니까?\"\n\n\"지금까지는—어쩌면 늘 그럴지도 모르지만—두 분은 행복하고 평온하시잖아요. 안나를 보면 그녀가 행복하다는 게, 더없이 행복하다는 게 보여요. 그녀는 벌써 제게 그토록 많은 이야기를 해 줄 짬이 있었답니다.\" 다리아 알렉산드로브나는 미소를 지으며 말했다. 그러면서도 무심코, 이 말을 하는 바로 그 순간, 안나가 정말로 행복한 것일까 하는 의심이 그녀의 마음속으로 파고들었다.\n\n그러나 브론스키는 그 점에 대해서는 아무런 의심도 없는 듯했다.\n\n\"그래요, 그래요.\" 그가 말했다. \"그녀가 그 모든 고통을 겪은 뒤 되살아났다는 걸 압니다. 그녀는 행복합니다. 그녀는 지금 이 순간 행복하지요. 하지만 저는요?…… 저는 우리 앞에 놓인 것이 두렵습니다……. 죄송합니다, 더 걸으시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"아니요, 괜찮아요.\"\n\n\"그럼, 여기 좀 앉으시지요.\"\n\n다리아 알렉산드로브나는 가로수 길 한쪽 구석의 정원 의자에 앉았다. 그는 그녀를 마주 보고 섰다.\n\n\"저는 그녀가 행복하다는 걸 압니다.\" 그가 거듭 말했고, 그녀가 정말 행복한 것일까 하는 의심은 다리아 알렉산드로브나의 마음속으로 더욱 깊이 가라앉았다. \"하지만 그게 지속될 수 있을까요? 우리가 옳게 행동했는지 그르게 행동했는지는 별개의 문제이지만, 주사위는 이미 던져졌습니다.\" 그가 러시아어에서 프랑스어로 옮겨 가며 말했다. \"그리고 우리는 평생 한데 묶여 있습니다. 우리는 우리가 가장 신성하게 여기는 사랑의 온갖 끈으로 맺어져 있어요. 우리에게는 아이가 있고, 또 다른 아이들도 생길 수 있습니다. 하지만 법이며 우리 처지의 온갖 조건들이, 그녀는 보지 못하고 또 보고 싶어 하지도 않는 수천 가지 골치 아픈 문제들을 일으킵니다. 그것도 충분히 이해할 만한 일이지요. 하지만 저는 그것들을 보지 않을 수가 없습니다. 제 딸은 법적으로 제 딸이 아니라 카레닌의 딸입니다. 저는 이 거짓을 견딜 수가 없어요!\" 그가 거부하듯 힘찬 몸짓을 하며 말하고는, 침울한 물음을 담아 다리아 알렉산드로브나를 바라보았다.\n\n그녀는 아무 대답도 하지 않고 그저 그를 응시했다. 그가 말을 이었다.\n\n\"언젠가 아들이 태어날 수도 있습니다, 제 아들이요. 그런데 그 아이는 법적으로 카레닌이 될 겁니다. 그 아이는 제 이름의 상속자도, 제 재산의 상속자도 되지 못합니다. 우리 가정생활이 아무리 행복하고 아무리 많은 아이가 생긴다 해도, 우리 사이에 진정한 끈은 없을 겁니다. 그 아이들은 카레닌이 될 테니까요. 부인은 이 처지의 쓰라림과 끔찍함을 이해하실 수 있을 겁니다! 저는 이 일을 안나에게 이야기하려고 해 봤습니다. 그것은 그녀를 짜증 나게 합니다. 그녀는 이해하지 못하고, 또 그녀에게 이 모든 것을 솔직하게 말할 수도 없습니다. 자, 이제 다른 면을 봐 주십시오. 저는 행복합니다, 그녀의 사랑 속에서 행복하지요. 하지만 저는 할 일이 있어야 합니다. 저는 할 일을 찾아냈고, 제가 하는 일을 자랑스러워하며, 그것이 궁정과 군대에서 함께했던 옛 동료들의 일거리보다 더 고귀하다고 여깁니다. 그리고 단연코 저는 제가 하고 있는 일을 그들의 일과 바꾸지 않을 겁니다. 저는 이곳에서 일하며, 제 자리에 정착했고, 행복하고 만족스럽습니다. 우리가 행복하기 위해서는 그 이상 아무것도 필요하지 않아요. 저는 이곳에서 하는 제 일을 사랑합니다. Ce n’est pas un pis-aller, 오히려 그 반대로…….\"\n\n* Ce n’est pas un pis-aller: 프랑스어로 '이것은 차선책이 아니다'.\n다리아 알렉산드로브나는 그가 이 대목에 이르러 말을 더듬는 것을 알아챘고, 이 곁가지 이야기를 잘 이해하지 못했다. 하지만 그녀는, 그가 일단 가슴속 깊이 자리한 일들, 안나에게는 말할 수 없는 일들에 대해 이야기를 꺼낸 이상, 이제 모든 것을 거리낌 없이 털어놓고 있으며, 시골에서 그가 하는 일에 관한 문제도 안나와의 관계에 관한 문제와 마찬가지로 그의 가슴 가까이에 놓인 일들과 같은 범주에 속한다는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n\"자, 계속하겠습니다.\" 그가 마음을 가다듬으며 말했다. \"중요한 것은, 제가 일을 하면서, 제가 하는 일이 저와 함께 죽지 않으리라는, 제 뒤를 이을 상속자들이 있으리라는 확신을 갖고 싶다는 겁니다. 그런데 제게는 그것이 없습니다. 자기 아이들이, 자기가 사랑하는 여자의 아이들이 자기 아이가 되지 못하고, 그 아이들을 미워하며 조금도 마음 쓰지 않는 누군가의 것이 되리라는 걸 아는 사람의 처지를 헤아려 보십시오! 끔찍한 일입니다!\"\n\n그는 말을 멈췄는데, 분명 몹시 동요한 듯했다.\n\n\"네, 정말 그래요, 저도 알겠어요. 하지만 안나가 무엇을 할 수 있을까요?\" 다리아 알렉산드로브나가 물었다.\n\n\"네, 바로 그것이 제가 이 대화에서 말하려던 것입니다.\" 그가 애써 마음을 진정시키며 말했다. \"안나는 할 수 있습니다, 그건 그녀에게 달려 있어요……. 황제께 적출 인정을 청원하려고만 해도, 이혼은 반드시 필요합니다. 그리고 그것은 안나에게 달려 있어요. 그녀의 남편은 이혼에 동의했었습니다. 그때 부인의 남편께서 그것을 완전히 매듭지어 놓으셨지요. 그리고 지금도, 제가 알기로, 그는 그것을 거절하지 않을 겁니다. 그에게 편지를 쓰는 일만 남았습니다. 그는 그때 분명히 말했습니다, 그녀가 그 뜻을 밝히기만 하면 거절하지 않겠다고요. 물론,\" 그가 침울하게 말했다, \"그것은 그처럼 냉혹한 인간들만이 부릴 수 있는 바리새인적인 잔인함 가운데 하나입니다. 그는 자기에 대한 어떤 기억이든 그녀에게 얼마나 큰 고통을 주는지 알면서도, 그녀를 알면서도, 그녀에게서 편지를 받아야겠다는 겁니다. 그것이 그녀에게 고통이라는 걸 저는 이해할 수 있습니다. 하지만 이 일은 너무나 중대해서, passer par-dessus toutes ces finesses de sentiment. Il y va du bonheur et de l’existence d’Anne et de ses enfants. 제 이야기는 하지 않겠습니다, 제게도 힘든 일이긴 하지만요, 무척 힘든 일이지만.\" 그가 그것이 자기에게 힘들다는 이유로 누군가를 위협하기라도 하는 듯한 표정으로 말했다. \"그러니, 공작 부인, 저는 부인을 구원의 닻처럼 염치없이 붙들고 있는 겁니다. 그녀가 그에게 편지를 써서 이혼을 청하도록, 부디 저를 도와 설득해 주십시오.\"\n* passer par-dessus toutes ces finesses de sentiment. Il y va du bonheur et de l’existence d’Anne et de ses enfants: 프랑스어로 '이 모든 감정의 섬세함을 넘어서야 한다. 안나와 그 아이들의 행복과 존립이 걸린 문제다'.\n\n\"네, 물론이지요.\" 다리아 알렉산드로브나는 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치와의 마지막 만남을 생생하게 떠올리며 꿈결처럼 말했다. \"네, 물론이에요.\" 그녀는 안나를 생각하며 단호하게 거듭 말했다.\n\n\"그녀에게 부인의 영향력을 행사하셔서, 편지를 쓰게 해 주십시오. 저는 이 일을 그녀에게 이야기하는 게 싫습니다—거의 할 수가 없어요.\"\n\n\"좋아요, 제가 그녀와 이야기해 볼게요. 그런데 어째서 그녀는 스스로 그 생각을 못 하는 걸까요?\" 다리아 알렉산드로브나가 말했다. 그러다 무슨 까닭에선지 그 순간 문득, 눈을 반쯤 감는 안나의 이상한 새 버릇이 떠올랐다. 그리고 안나가 인생의 더 깊은 문제들이 건드려질 때면 꼭 눈꺼풀을 내리깔던 것이 생각났다. \"마치 모든 것을 다 보지 않으려고, 자기 삶에 대해 눈을 반쯤 감아 버리는 것 같아.\" 돌리는 생각했다. \"그래, 정말이지, 나 자신을 위해서도 그녀를 위해서도 그녀와 이야기해 볼 거야.\" 돌리는 그의 감사 어린 눈빛에 답하여 말했다.\n\n그들은 일어나 집을 향해 걸어갔다.\n\n# Chapter 22\n\n안나는 자기보다 먼저 돌리가 집에 와 있는 것을 보고, 그녀의 눈을 가만히 들여다보았다. 마치 브론스키와 나눈 이야기에 대해 묻기라도 하는 듯했지만, 입으로는 아무것도 묻지 않았다.\n\n\"이제 곧 만찬 시간일 거예요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"우린 아직 제대로 얼굴도 못 봤네요. 저녁 시간을 기대하고 있어요. 이제 가서 옷을 갈아입어야겠어요. 형님도 그러시겠지요. 우리 모두 공사장에서 흙탕물을 뒤집어썼으니까요.\"\n\n돌리는 자기 방으로 가면서 우습다는 생각이 들었다. 옷을 갈아입는 것은 불가능했다. 이미 제일 좋은 옷을 입고 있었기 때문이다. 그래도 만찬을 위해 무언가 준비를 한다는 표시는 하고 싶어서, 하녀에게 옷을 솔질해 달라고 부탁하고, 커프스와 넥타이를 바꾸고, 머리에 레이스를 얹었다.\n\n\"제가 할 수 있는 건 이게 다예요.\" 그녀는 다시 한번 더없이 수수한 세 번째 옷을 입고 들어온 안나에게 미소를 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"네, 우린 여기서 너무 격식을 차린답니다.\" 안나가 자신의 화려함을 변명하듯 말했다. \"알렉세이는 형님이 와 주신 걸 무척 기뻐하고 있어요. 그 사람은 좀처럼 무슨 일에 그렇게 기뻐하는 법이 없는데. 형님한테 완전히 마음을 빼앗긴 거예요.\" 그녀가 덧붙였다. \"피곤하진 않으세요?\"\n\n만찬 전까지는 무언가 이야기를 나눌 틈이 없었다. 응접실로 들어가니 이미 바르바라 공작 영애가 와 있었고, 검은 프록코트 차림의 신사들도 있었다. 건축가는 연미복을 입고 있었다. 브론스키는 의사와 집사를 손님에게 소개했다. 건축가는 이미 병원에서 그녀에게 인사를 시킨 터였다.\n\n매끈하게 면도한 둥근 턱과 풀 먹인 흰 크라바트로 번드르르하게 차려입은 뚱뚱한 집사가 만찬 준비가 되었다고 알리자, 부인들이 자리에서 일어났다. 브론스키는 스비야시스키에게 안나 아르카지예브나를 안내해 달라고 청하고, 자기는 돌리에게 팔을 내밀었다. 베슬롭스키가 투시케비치보다 먼저 바르바라 공작 영애에게 팔을 내미는 바람에, 투시케비치는 집사와 의사와 함께 따로 걸어가야 했다.\n\n만찬도, 식당도, 식기도, 시중드는 모양새도, 포도주도, 음식도, 그저 온 집안에 흐르는 현대적 사치의 전반적인 분위기에 어울리는 정도가 아니라, 오히려 그보다 더 호화롭고 더 현대적으로 보였다. 다리야 알렉산드로브나는 자기에게는 낯선 이 사치를 지켜보았고, 살림을 도맡아 꾸려 온 알뜰한 안주인으로서—비록 본 것을 자기 살림에 끌어다 쓸 생각은 꿈에도 하지 않았는데, 이 모든 것이 자기 생활 방식을 훨씬 뛰어넘는 호사스러운 양식이었기 때문이다—구석구석을 뜯어보며 이 모든 것이 어떻게, 또 누구의 손으로 이루어진 것인지 궁금해하지 않을 수 없었다. 바센카 베슬롭스키도, 그녀의 남편도, 심지어 스비야시스키도, 그녀가 아는 다른 많은 사람도, 이런 의문 따위는 결코 품지 않을 것이며, 교양 있는 주인이라면 누구나 손님에게 느끼게 하려고 애쓰는 그것, 곧 자기 집의 잘 정돈된 모든 것이 주인인 자기에게는 아무런 수고도 들이지 않은 채 저절로 그렇게 된 것이라는 말을 선뜻 믿었을 것이다. 다리야 알렉산드로브나는 아이들 아침으로 먹일 죽 한 그릇조차 저절로 생기지 않는다는 것을, 따라서 이토록 복잡하고 웅장한 사치의 양식이 유지되는 곳이라면 누군가 그 운영에 진지하게 마음을 쏟고 있으리라는 것을 잘 알고 있었다. 그리고 알렉세이 키릴로비치가 식탁을 훑어보는 눈길에서, 집사에게 고갯짓을 하는 모습에서, 다리야 알렉산드로브나에게 차가운 수프와 뜨거운 수프 중 하나를 고르라고 권하는 태도에서, 그녀는 이 모든 것이 이 집 주인 본인의 손길로 조직되고 유지되고 있음을 알아차렸다. 이 모든 것이 베슬롭스키에게 기대고 있지 않은 것과 마찬가지로 안나에게 기대고 있지 않다는 것은 분명했다. 그녀도, 스비야시스키도, 공작 영애도, 베슬롭스키도, 모두 똑같이 손님으로서, 가벼운 마음으로 누군가가 마련해 준 것을 누리고 있을 뿐이었다.\n\n안나는 대화를 이끌어 가는 데에서만 안주인이었다. 그것은 작은 식탁에 둘러앉은 안주인에게는 쉽지 않은 대화였다. 집사며 건축가처럼 전혀 다른 세계에 속한 사람들이 함께 있었는데, 그들은 익숙하지 않은 우아함에 압도되지 않으려 애쓰면서도 좌중의 대화에 큰 몫을 떠맡을 수가 없었던 것이다. 하지만 안나는 이 어려운 대화를 늘 그렇듯 능숙하고 자연스럽게 이끌어 갔고, 다리야 알렉산드로브나가 보기에는 실제로 그것을 즐기면서 그렇게 하고 있었다. 대화는 투시케비치와 베슬롭스키가 둘이서만 보트를 탔던 일로 시작되었고, 투시케비치는 페테르부르크의 요트 클럽에서 열린 지난번 보트 경주를 묘사하기 시작했다. 그러나 안나는 첫 번째 틈을 놓치지 않고 곧장 건축가에게 말을 돌려 그를 침묵에서 끌어냈다.\n\n\"니콜라이 이바니치도 놀라워하셨어요.\" 그녀는 스비야시스키를 가리키며 말했다. \"새 건물이 지난번에 오셨을 때보다 얼마나 진척됐는지 보고요. 그런데 저는 매일 그곳에 가는데도, 날마다 그 자라는 속도에 놀란답니다.\"\n\n\"각하와 함께 일하는 건 더없이 좋습니다.\" 건축가가 미소를 지으며 말했다(그는 공손하고 차분했지만 나름의 자긍심도 지니고 있었다). \"지방 관청을 상대하는 것과는 전혀 다른 일이지요. 다른 데서라면 서류 뭉치를 잔뜩 써내야 할 일도, 여기서는 백작님께 말씀드리면 세 마디로 일이 끝나니까요.\"\n\n\"미국식 사업 방식이로군요.\" 스비야시스키가 미소를 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"네, 거기서는 합리적인 방식으로 짓지요…….\"\n\n대화는 미국에서 정치권력이 오용되는 문제로 옮겨 갔지만, 안나는 집사를 대화로 끌어들이려고 재빨리 다른 화제로 돌렸다.\n\n\"형님은 수확기를 본 적 있으세요?\" 그녀가 다리야 알렉산드로브나를 향해 말했다. \"우리가 마주쳤을 때 마침 그걸 보러 갔다 오는 길이었어요. 저도 그날 처음 봤답니다.\"\n\n\"그게 어떻게 작동하는데?\" 돌리가 물었다.\n\n\"꼭 작은 가위 같아요. 널빤지 하나에 작은 가위가 잔뜩 달려 있죠. 이렇게요.\"\n\n안나는 반지로 뒤덮인 아름답고 흰 손으로 나이프와 포크를 집어 들고, 기계가 어떻게 작동하는지 보여 주기 시작했다. 자기 설명으로는 아무것도 이해시킬 수 없다는 것을 분명히 알고 있었지만, 자기 이야기가 듣기 좋고 손이 아름답다는 것을 알기에 그녀는 계속 설명을 이어 갔다.\n\n\"작은 주머니칼에 더 가깝지요.\" 베슬롭스키가 그녀에게서 눈을 떼지 않은 채 장난스럽게 말했다.\n\n안나는 알아챌 듯 말 듯 미소를 지었지만 아무 대답도 하지 않았다. \"카를 표도리치, 정말 작은 가위 같지 않나요?\" 그녀가 집사에게 말했다.\n\n\"오, 야.\" 독일인이 대답했다. \"에스 이스트 아인 간츠 아인파헤스 딩.\" 그러고는 기계의 구조를 설명하기 시작했다.\n\n\"묶기까지 못하는 게 아쉽군요. 빈 박람회에서 철사로 묶는 기계를 봤는데 말이죠.\" 스비야시스키가 말했다. \"그런 게 쓰기에 더 이득일 텐데.\"\n\n\"에스 콤트 드라우프 안…… 데어 프라이스 폼 드라트 무스 아우스게레히네트 베르덴.\" 그러더니 과묵함에서 깨어난 독일인이 브론스키를 향해 돌아섰다. \"다스 레스트 지히 아우스레히넨, 에를라우흐트.\" 독일인은 늘 무언가를 적어 넣는 연필과 수첩이 들어 있는 주머니를 막 더듬으려다가, 자기가 만찬 자리에 있다는 것을 떠올리고 브론스키의 싸늘한 눈길을 알아챘는지 손을 멈추었다. \"추 콤플리치르트, 마흐트 추 필 클로포트.\" 그가 결론지었다.\n\n\"뷘슈트 만 도호츠, 조 하트 만 아우흐 클로포츠.\" 바센카 베슬롭스키가 독일인을 흉내 내며 말했다. \"자도르 랄망.\" 그는 다시 같은 미소를 지으며 안나에게 말을 건넸다.\n\n\"세세.\" 그녀가 장난스러운 엄격함으로 말했다.\n\n\"우린 당신이 들판에 있을 줄 알았어요, 바실리 세묘니치.\" 그녀가 병약해 보이는 의사에게 말했다. \"거기 다녀오셨나요?\"\n\n\"갔었는데, 그만 줄행랑을 쳤지요.\" 의사가 침울한 농담조로 대답했다.\n\n\"그럼 산책 한번 잘하셨겠네요?\"\n\"아주 훌륭했지요!\"\n\n\"그래, 그 할머니는 어떠셨어요? 발진티푸스는 아니었으면 좋겠는데요?\"\n\n\"발진티푸스는 아닙니다만, 상태가 나빠지고 있어요.\"\n\n\"저런, 안됐네요!\" 안나가 말했다. 그리고 이렇게 집안 식구들에게 예의의 도리를 다하고는, 자기 친구들 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸다.\n\n\"그래도 당신 설명만 듣고 기계를 만들어 내라면 꽤 힘든 일이겠는데요, 안나 아르카지예브나.\" 스비야시스키가 농담조로 말했다.\n\n\"어머, 아니에요, 왜요?\" 안나가 미소를 지으며 말했다. 그 미소는 자기가 기계에 대해 늘어놓은 강론에 무언가 매력적인 데가 있고 스비야시스키가 그것을 알아챘다는 것을 자기도 알고 있음을 드러내는 것이었다. 이 새로운 소녀 같은 교태가 돌리에게는 불쾌한 인상을 주었다.\n\n\"그렇지만 안나 아르카지예브나의 건축 지식은 정말 놀라워요.\" 투시케비치가 말했다.\n\n\"정말이지, 어제 안나 아르카지예브나가 초석이며 방습층 이야기를 하시는 걸 들었어요.\" 베슬롭스키가 말했다. \"제가 제대로 말한 건가요?\"\n\n\"그렇게 많이 보고 듣다 보면 놀라울 것도 없어요.\" 안나가 말했다. \"하지만 당신은 집이 무엇으로 지어지는지조차 모르실 거예요, 아마.\"\n\n다리야 알렉산드로브나는 안나가 자기와 베슬롭스키 사이에 오가는 그 야유 섞인 어조를 싫어하면서도, 본의 아니게 거기에 끌려들고 있음을 알아챘다.\n\n이 일에서 브론스키는 레빈과는 전혀 다르게 행동했다. 그는 베슬롭스키의 시시한 수다에 분명 아무런 의미도 두지 않았다. 오히려 그의 농담을 부추겼다.\n\n\"자, 어디 말해 보시지, 베슬롭스키. 돌들은 어떻게 서로 붙어 있는 거요?\"\n\n\"물론 시멘트로 붙어 있지요.\"\n\n\"훌륭해! 그럼 시멘트는 뭐요?\"\n\n\"아, 무슨 풀 같은 거…… 아니, 퍼티지요.\" 베슬롭스키가 말하자 좌중에 웃음이 터졌다.\n\n만찬에 모인 사람들은, 침울한 침묵에 잠겨 있는 의사와 건축가와 집사를 빼고는, 한순간도 멈추지 않는 대화를 이어 갔다. 한 화제를 슬쩍 건드리고 다른 화제를 붙잡으며, 때로는 이 사람 저 사람의 아픈 데를 콕콕 찔러 댔다. 한번은 다리야 알렉산드로브나가 정곡을 찔려 마음에 상처를 입고는, 어찌나 달아올랐던지 얼굴까지 붉어졌고, 나중에 자기가 무언가 지나치거나 불쾌한 말을 하지 않았나 곱씹어 보았다. 스비야시스키가 레빈에 대한 이야기를 꺼내며, 기계라는 것이 러시아 농업에 그저 해롭기만 하다는 그의 기이한 견해를 늘어놓기 시작했다.\n\n\"그 레빈 씨를 알게 되는 기쁨은 누리지 못했습니다만.\" 브론스키가 미소를 지으며 말했다. \"아마 그분은 자기가 비난하는 그 기계들을 한 번도 본 적이 없을 겁니다. 설령 보고 써 봤다 해도, 분명 이상한 방식이었겠지요. 외국산 기계가 아니라 러시아제 모조품 같은 걸 말입니다. 그런 문제에 대해 무슨 견해라는 걸 가질 수가 있겠습니까?\"\n\n\"대체로 터키식 견해지요.\" 베슬롭스키가 미소를 지으며 안나를 향해 돌아서서 말했다.\n\n\"저는 그분의 의견을 변호할 수는 없어요.\" 다리야 알렉산드로브나가 발끈하여 말했다. \"하지만 그분이 대단히 교양 있는 분이라는 건 말할 수 있어요. 만약 그분이 여기 계셨다면 여러분께 어떻게 대답해야 할지 잘 아셨을 거예요. 저는 그렇게 못하지만요.\"\n\n\"저도 그분을 무척 좋아하고, 우린 아주 막역한 사이입니다.\" 스비야시스키가 사람 좋은 미소를 지으며 말했다. \"메 파르동, 일 레 텡 프티 푀 토케. 이를테면 그는 군 자치회나 중재 위원회 같은 게 죄다 쓸모없다고 주장하면서, 무슨 일에든 참여하려 들지 않으니까요.\"\n\n\"그게 우리 러시아 사람들의 무기력이지요.\" 브론스키가 얼음을 채운 유리병의 물을 가늘고 높은 줄기가 달린 섬세한 잔에 따르며 말했다. \"우리는 특권이 우리에게 지우는 의무에 대한 감각이 없어요. 그래서 그런 의무를 인정하기를 거부하는 거지요.\"\n\n\"저는 그분만큼 자기 의무 수행에 엄격한 사람을 알지 못해요.\" 브론스키의 우월한 어조에 화가 난 다리야 알렉산드로브나가 말했다.\n\n\"제 경우를 말씀드리면,\" 브론스키가 말을 이었다. 그는 무슨 까닭에서인지 이 대화에 분명 깊이 자극받은 듯했다. \"이런 저 같은 사람도, 오히려 반대로, 니콜라이 이바니치 덕분에\"(그는 스비야시스키를 가리켰다) \"치안 판사로 뽑히는 영예를 누리게 된 데 대해 무척 감사하고 있습니다. 회기에 출석하고, 말 한 마리를 둘러싼 농민들의 다툼을 판결하는 의무가, 제가 할 수 있는 그 무엇 못지않게 중요한 일이라고 생각합니다. 그리고 만일 저를 군 자치회 의원으로 뽑아 준다면 영예로 여기겠습니다. 그것만이 제가 지주로서 누리는 혜택에 대한 값을 치를 수 있는 길이니까요. 불행히도 사람들은 대지주가 국가에서 마땅히 가져야 할 비중을 이해하지 못합니다.\"\n\n다리야 알렉산드로브나는 그가 자기 식탁에서 자기가 옳다고 그토록 태연하게 확신하는 것을 듣고 묘한 기분이 들었다. 그녀는 정반대를 믿는 레빈도 자기 식탁에서 똑같이 단호하게 자기 의견을 펼치던 것을 떠올렸다. 하지만 그녀는 레빈을 사랑했기에 그의 편이었다.\n\n\"그럼 백작, 이번 선거에서 당신을 믿어도 되겠지요?\" 스비야시스키가 말했다. \"하지만 좀 미리 오셔야 합니다. 8일에는 현장에 계실 수 있도록 말이지요. 저희 집에 묵으시는 영광을 주신다면 좋겠는데요.\"\n\n\"저는 형부 의견에 좀 동감이에요.\" 안나가 말했다. \"그분과 똑같은 근거에서는 아니지만요.\" 그녀가 미소를 지으며 덧붙였다. \"요즘 들어 우리한테 이런 공적 의무가 너무 많아진 게 아닌가 싶어요. 옛날엔 관리가 워낙 많아서 무슨 일이든 일일이 관리를 불러야 했던 것처럼, 이젠 누구나 무슨 공적 의무인지를 떠맡고 있잖아요. 알렉세이는 여기 온 지 이제 여섯 달인데, 벌써 대여섯 군데 공공 단체의 회원이라니까요, 정말이지. 뒤 트랭 크 셀라 바, 이러다간 시간을 죄다 거기에 빼앗기고 말 거예요. 게다가 그런 단체가 그렇게 많아지면, 결국엔 한낱 형식으로 끝나 버리고 말까 봐 걱정이에요. 당신은 몇 군데 회원이세요, 니콜라이 이바니치?\" 그녀가 스비야시스키를 향해 돌아섰다. \"스무 군데도 넘지요, 아마.\"\n\n안나는 가볍게 말했지만, 그 어조에서 짜증을 알아챌 수 있었다. 안나와 브론스키를 주의 깊게 지켜보던 다리야 알렉산드로브나는 그것을 곧바로 간파했다. 그녀는 또한 안나가 그렇게 말하는 동안 브론스키의 얼굴이 이내 진지하고 완고한 표정을 띠는 것을 알아챘다. 이것을 알아챈 데다, 바르바라 공작 영애가 곧장 서둘러 페테르부르크의 지인들 이야기를 꺼내 대화를 바꾸려 한 것, 그리고 브론스키가 정원에서 별다른 맥락도 없이 시골에서의 자기 일에 대해 했던 말을 떠올리며, 돌리는 이 공적 활동의 문제가 안나와 브론스키 사이의 깊은 사적 불화와 얽혀 있음을 짐작했다.\n\n만찬도, 포도주도, 식탁 장식도 모두 매우 훌륭했다. 하지만 그 모든 것이 최근 몇 년간 그녀에게는 아주 낯설어진 격식 차린 만찬과 무도회에서 다리야 알렉산드로브나가 보아 온 것과 똑같았다. 모두 똑같이 비인격적이고 거북한 성격을 띠고 있어서, 평범한 날 친한 사람들끼리의 자그마한 모임에서 그것은 그녀에게 불쾌한 인상을 주었다.\n\n만찬이 끝난 뒤 그들은 테라스에 앉아 있다가, 이어 론 테니스를 치러 나갔다. 선수들은 두 패로 나뉘어, 정성껏 고르고 다진 크로케 경기장 위에 도금한 기둥으로 팽팽하게 친 그물을 사이에 두고 양편으로 갈라섰다. 다리야 알렉산드로브나도 한번 쳐 보려 했지만, 한참 동안 경기 방식을 이해하지 못했고, 마침내 이해했을 무렵에는 너무 지쳐서 바르바라 공작 영애 곁에 앉아 그저 선수들을 구경했다. 그녀의 짝인 투시케비치도 경기를 그만두었지만, 나머지 사람들은 오랫동안 경기를 이어 갔다. 스비야시스키와 브론스키는 둘 다 매우 잘 쳤고 진지했다. 그들은 자기 쪽으로 넘어오는 공을 날카롭게 주시하다가, 서두르거나 서로 방해하는 일 없이, 날렵하게 공으로 달려가 튀어 오르기를 기다렸다가 깔끔하고 정확하게 그물 너머로 되받아쳤다. 베슬롭스키는 다른 사람들보다 못 쳤다. 너무 안달했지만, 흥겨운 기분으로 선수들의 흥을 돋우었다. 그의 웃음소리와 외침이 끊이지 않았다. 그는 다른 남자들처럼 부인들의 허락을 얻어 웃옷을 벗었는데, 흰 셔츠 소매 차림의 단단하고 잘생긴 몸매에, 땀에 젖어 벌건 얼굴과 충동적인 동작이 어우러져, 기억에 또렷이 새겨지는 한 폭의 그림을 이루었다.\n\n그날 밤 다리야 알렉산드로브나는 침대에 누워 눈을 감자마자, 크로케 경기장을 이리저리 날아다니는 바센카 베슬롭스키의 모습이 떠올랐다.\n\n경기를 하는 동안 다리야 알렉산드로브나는 즐겁지 않았다. 바센카 베슬롭스키와 안나 사이에 줄곧 오가던 그 야유 섞인 가벼운 어조도 마음에 들지 않았고, 아이들도 없이 어른들끼리만 모여 아이들 놀이를 하는 것이 통 자연스럽지 않게 느껴졌다. 하지만 모임을 깨지 않으려고, 또 어떻게든 시간을 보내려고, 그녀는 잠시 쉰 뒤 다시 경기에 끼어들어 즐기는 척했다. 그날 하루 종일 그녀는 자기보다 솜씨 좋은 배우들과 함께 극장에서 연기를 하고 있는데, 자기의 서툰 연기가 공연 전체를 망치고 있는 것만 같았다. 그녀는 일이 다 잘 풀리면 이틀쯤 머물 작정으로 왔었다. 그러나 저녁에 경기를 하는 동안, 이튿날 집으로 돌아가기로 마음을 굳혔다. 오는 길에 그토록 진저리를 쳤던 어머니로서의 근심과 걱정거리가, 그것 없이 하루를 보내고 나니 이제는 전혀 다른 빛깔로 다가와, 그녀를 다시 그리로 불러들이는 것이었다.\n\n저녁 차를 마시고 밤에 보트를 탄 뒤, 다리야 알렉산드로브나가 혼자 자기 방으로 가서 옷을 벗고 잠자리를 위해 가느다란 머리카락을 매만지기 시작했을 때, 그녀는 커다란 안도감을 느꼈다.\n\n안나가 곧 자기를 보러 온다는 생각이 그녀에게는 정말이지 거북했다. 그녀는 자기만의 생각에 잠겨 홀로 있고 싶었다.\n\n# Chapter 23\n\n돌리가 막 잠자리에 들려던 참에 안나가 잠옷 차림으로 그녀를 보러 들어왔다. 그날 하루 동안 안나는 마음속에 담아 둔 일들을 몇 번이나 이야기하려 했지만, 그때마다 몇 마디 하다가 멈추곤 했다. \"나중에, 우리 둘만 있을 때 모든 걸 다 이야기해요. 당신한테 하고 싶은 말이 너무 많아요.\" 그녀는 말했다.\n\n이제 둘만 있게 되었는데, 안나는 무슨 이야기를 해야 할지 알 수 없었다. 그녀는 창가에 앉아 돌리를 바라보며, 미리 그렇게도 끝이 없을 것 같던 내밀한 이야깃거리들을 마음속으로 하나하나 더듬어 보았지만, 아무것도 찾을 수 없었다. 그 순간 그녀에게는 모든 것이 이미 다 말해진 듯이 느껴졌다.\n\n\"그래, 키티는 어때요?\" 그녀는 무거운 한숨을 내쉬며, 뉘우치는 듯한 눈길로 돌리를 바라보며 말했다. \"사실대로 말해 줘요, 돌리. 그 애가 나한테 화가 나 있는 거 아니에요?\"\n\n\"화가 나 있다니요? 아, 아니에요!\" 다리야 알렉산드로브나가 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 나를 미워하고, 경멸하겠죠?\"\n\n\"아, 아니에요! 하지만 그런 일은 좀처럼 용서가 안 되는 거잖아요.\"\n\n\"그래요, 그래.\" 안나는 고개를 돌려 열린 창밖을 바라보며 말했다. \"하지만 내 잘못은 아니었어요. 그럼 누구 잘못이죠? 잘못이라는 게 대체 뭘 뜻하는 걸까요? 달리 어떻게 될 수도 있었을까요? 당신은 어떻게 생각해요? 당신이 스티바의 아내가 되지 않는 일이 과연 일어날 수도 있었을까요?\"\n\n\"글쎄, 잘 모르겠어요. 그런데 내가 당신한테 듣고 싶은 건 바로 이거예요…….\"\n\n\"그래요, 그래, 하지만 키티 얘기를 아직 다 못 끝냈잖아요. 그 애는 행복해요? 그 사람이 아주 좋은 사람이라던데.\"\n\n\"아주 좋은 정도가 아니에요. 그보다 더 나은 사람을 본 적이 없어요.\"\n\n\"아, 정말 기뻐요! 너무 기뻐요! 아주 좋은 정도가 아니라니.\" 그녀는 되뇌었다.\n\n돌리가 미소 지었다.\n\n\"하지만 당신 얘기를 들려줘요. 우리 할 이야기가 정말 많잖아요. 그리고 나는 이미……와 이야기를 나눴어요.\" 돌리는 그를 무어라 불러야 할지 몰랐다. 백작이라 부르기도, 알렉세이 키릴로비치라 부르기도 어색했다.\n\n\"알렉세이와요.\" 안나가 말했다. \"무슨 이야기를 나눴는지 알아요. 하지만 나는 당신한테 직접 묻고 싶어요. 나에 대해, 내 삶에 대해 어떻게 생각하는지.\"\n\n\"어떻게 그걸 대뜸 말하겠어요? 나도 정말 잘 모르겠어요.\"\n\n\"아니에요, 그래도 다 말해 줘요…… 당신은 내 삶을 보고 있잖아요. 하지만 한 가지 잊지 말아요. 당신이 우리를 보고 있는 건 여름이고, 당신이 우리한테 와 있어서 우리가 둘만 있는 게 아니라는 걸…… 우리는 이른 봄에 이리로 와서 아주 단둘이 지냈고, 또다시 단둘이 지내게 될 거예요. 나는 그보다 더 좋은 걸 바라지 않아요. 하지만 그 사람 없이 나 혼자 사는 모습을 상상해 봐요, 혼자서, 그렇게 되면…… 모든 걸로 미루어 보아 그런 일이 자주 되풀이될 거예요. 그 사람이 절반은 집을 비울 테니까요.\" 그녀는 자리에서 일어나 돌리 곁으로 바짝 다가와 앉으며 말했다.\n\n\"물론.\" 그녀는 무언가 대답하려는 돌리의 말을 가로막았다. \"물론 나는 그 사람을 억지로 붙들어 두려고 하지 않을 거예요. 사실 붙들어 두지도 않고요. 곧 경마가 열려서, 그 사람의 말들이 출전하니까, 그 사람은 갈 거예요. 나는 아주 기뻐요. 하지만 내 입장을, 내 처지를 한번 생각해 봐요…… 하지만 그런 이야기를 한들 무슨 소용이 있겠어요?\" 그녀는 미소 지었다. \"그래, 그 사람이 당신한테 무슨 이야기를 했어요?\"\n\n\"그분은 내가 나 자신에 대해 말하고 싶은 바로 그것을 이야기했어요. 그래서 그분의 대변자가 되어 주기가 나로서는 쉬워요. 그러니까 어떤 가능성이 있지 않겠느냐는 거…… 당신이 혹시…….\" (다리야 알렉산드로브나는 머뭇거렸다) \"당신의 처지를 바로잡고, 나아지게 할 수 없겠느냐는 거예요…… 내가 이 일을 어떻게 보는지 당신도 알잖아요…… 하지만 그래도, 가능하다면, 당신은 결혼을 해야 해요…….\"\n\n\"이혼 말이죠?\" 안나가 말했다. \"당신 알아요? 페테르부르크에서 나를 보러 온 단 한 사람의 여자가 베치 트베르스카야였어요. 당신도 그 여자를 알죠? Au fond, c’est la femme la plus dépravée qui existe. 그 여자는 투시케비치와 정을 통하면서 자기 남편을 가장 비열한 방식으로 속였어요. 그런데 그 여자가 나한테 그러더군요, 내 처지가 떳떳하지 못한 동안에는 나와 알고 지낼 마음이 없다고요. 내가 비교하려는 거라고는 생각하지 말아요…… 나는 당신을 알아요, 자기. 하지만 그 일이 떠오르는 걸 어쩔 수 없었어요…… 그래, 그 사람이 당신한테 뭐라고 했어요?\" 그녀는 다시 물었다.\n* Au fond, c’est la femme la plus dépravée qui existe: 프랑스어로 '사실 그 여자는 세상에서 가장 타락한 여자예요.'\n\n\"그분은 당신 때문에, 또 자기 자신 때문에 불행하다고 했어요. 어쩌면 당신은 그게 이기심이라고 말할지도 모르지만, 정당하고 고귀한 이기심이잖아요. 그분은 무엇보다 자기 딸을 적자로 인정받게 하고, 당신의 남편이 되어, 당신에 대한 법적인 권리를 갖고 싶어 해요.\"\n\n\"내 처지에 놓인 나만큼 철저하게 노예일 수 있는 아내가, 노예가 어디 있겠어요?\" 그녀는 침울하게 끼어들었다.\n\n\"그분이 가장 바라는 건…… 당신이 괴로워하지 않는 거예요.\"\n\n\"그건 불가능해요. 그래서요?\"\n\n\"그리고, 가장 정당한 바람인데, 그분은 당신의 아이들이 떳떳한 성을 갖기를 바라요.\"\n\n\"무슨 아이들이요?\" 안나는 돌리를 보지 않고, 눈을 반쯤 감은 채 말했다.\n\n\"애니와, 또 앞으로 태어날 아이들 말이에요…….\"\n\n\"그 일이라면 그 사람이 걱정할 필요 없어요. 나는 더 이상 아이를 갖지 않을 테니까.\"\n\n\"당신이 갖지 않으리라는 걸 어떻게 알아요?\"\n\n\"갖지 않을 거예요, 내가 원하지 않으니까.\" 그리고 온갖 격한 감정에도 불구하고, 안나는 돌리의 얼굴에 떠오른 그 순진한 호기심과 놀라움, 경악의 표정을 알아채고는 미소 지었다.\n\n\"병을 앓고 난 뒤에 의사가 나한테 말해 줬어요…….\"\n\n\"설마요!\" 돌리가 눈을 휘둥그레 뜨며 말했다.\n\n그녀에게 이것은, 그로부터 비롯되는 결과와 추론이 너무도 엄청나서, 첫 순간에 느낄 수 있는 거라고는 그 모든 것을 도무지 다 받아들일 수 없다는 것과 그것에 대해 아주, 아주 많이 곱씹어 보아야 하리라는 것뿐인, 그런 발견들 가운데 하나였다.\n\n이 발견은, 지금까지 그녀로서는 도무지 이해할 수 없었던 그 아이 하나둘짜리 가정들에 갑자기 빛을 비추어, 너무도 많은 생각과 성찰, 그리고 서로 모순되는 감정을 불러일으킨 탓에, 그녀는 아무 말도 하지 못한 채 그저 놀라움에 눈을 크게 뜨고 안나를 바라보기만 했다. 이것이야말로 그녀가 꿈꿔 오던 바로 그것이었지만, 막상 그것이 가능하다는 사실을 알게 되자 그녀는 소름이 끼쳤다. 너무도 복잡한 문제에 대한 너무도 간단한 해결책이라는 느낌이 들었던 것이다.\n\"N’est-ce pas immoral?\" 그녀는 잠시 사이를 두었다가 겨우 그 말만 했다.\n\n* N’est-ce pas immoral?: 프랑스어로 '그건 부도덕한 일 아니에요?'\n\n\"왜 그렇죠? 생각해 봐요, 나에게는 두 가지 갈림길밖에 없어요. 아이를 배든가, 다시 말해 환자가 되든가, 아니면 내 남편의, 사실상 내 남편인 그 사람의 벗이자 반려가 되든가.\" 안나는 일부러 가볍고 경박한 어조로 말했다.\n\n\"그래요, 그래.\" 다리야 알렉산드로브나는 자기가 스스로에게 써먹던 바로 그 논리를 들으면서, 그 안에서 예전만큼의 힘을 느끼지 못한 채 말했다.\n\n\"당신에게는, 다른 사람들에게는.\" 안나는 마치 그녀의 생각을 꿰뚫어 보기라도 한 듯이 말했다. \"망설일 까닭이 있을지도 몰라요. 하지만 나에게는…… 한번 생각해 봐요, 나는 그 사람의 아내가 아니에요. 그 사람은 자기가 나를 사랑하는 동안에만 나를 사랑해요. 그러니 내가 어떻게 그 사람의 사랑을 붙들어 두겠어요? 이런 모습으로는 안 돼요!\"\n\n그녀는 흥분의 순간에 흔히 그러하듯 더없이 빠른 동작으로, 자기 허리 앞에서 하얀 두 손을 곡선을 그리며 움직였다. 다리야 알렉산드로브나의 머릿속으로 온갖 생각과 기억이 밀려들었다. '나는,' 그녀는 생각했다. '나는 스티바를 끌어당기던 매력을 지키지 못했어. 그이는 다른 여자들을 찾아 나를 떠났지. 그리고 그이가 나를 배신하며 처음 택했던 여자도, 늘 예쁘고 활기차게 군다고 해서 그이를 붙들어 두지는 못했어. 그이는 그 여자를 버리고 또 다른 여자를 택했지. 그런데 안나가 과연 그런 식으로 브론스키 백작을 끌어당기고 붙들어 둘 수 있을까? 만약 그이가 찾는 게 그런 거라면, 더 매력적이고 사랑스러운 옷차림과 몸가짐을 얼마든지 찾아낼 거야. 그리고 저 여자의 드러난 두 팔이 아무리 희고 아름답다 해도, 검은 곱슬머리 아래 풍만한 몸매와 생기 넘치는 얼굴이 아무리 아름답다 해도, 그이는 더 나은 무언가를 또 찾아내고 말 거야. 꼭 그 역겹고 가엾고 사랑스러운 내 남편이 그러는 것처럼.'\n\n돌리는 아무 대답도 하지 않고, 그저 한숨을 내쉬었다. 안나는 동의하지 않음을 드러내는 그 한숨을 알아채고 말을 이어 갔다. 그녀의 무기고에는 어떤 반박도 할 수 없을 만큼 강력한 다른 논거들이 있었다.\n\n\"그게 옳지 않다고 말하는 거예요? 하지만 한번 생각해 봐요.\" 그녀는 말을 이었다. \"당신은 내 처지를 잊고 있어요. 내가 어떻게 아이들을 바랄 수 있겠어요? 나는 고통을 두고 하는 말이 아니에요, 그건 두렵지 않아요. 다만 생각해 봐요, 내 아이들이 어떤 존재가 될지를. 남의 성을 짊어지고 살아가야 할, 불운한 아이들이에요. 태어났다는 그 사실 하나만으로, 그 아이들은 자기 어머니를, 자기 아버지를, 자기 출생을 부끄러워하지 않을 수 없게 될 거예요.\"\n\n\"하지만 바로 그래서 이혼이 꼭 필요한 거예요.\" 그러나 안나는 그 말을 듣지 못했다. 그녀는 그토록 여러 번 스스로를 납득시켰던 그 모든 논거를 한꺼번에 쏟아 내고 싶었다.\n\n\"내가 이성을 받았는데, 그 이성을 불행한 존재들을 세상에 데려오는 일을 막는 데 쓰지 않는다면, 그게 다 무슨 소용이겠어요!\" 그녀는 돌리를 바라보았지만, 대답을 기다리지 않고 말을 이었다.\n\n\"나는 언제나 그 불행한 아이들에게 못할 짓을 했다고 느낄 거예요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그 아이들이 존재하지 않는다면, 적어도 불행하지는 않을 테니까요. 그런데 그 아이들이 불행하다면, 그건 오로지 나 혼자만의 잘못이 되는 거예요.\"\n\n이것은 다리야 알렉산드로브나가 스스로 곱씹던 바로 그 논거였다. 하지만 그녀는 그 말을 들으면서도 그것을 이해하지 못했다. '어떻게 존재하지도 않는 생명에게 못할 짓을 할 수 있단 말이지?' 그녀는 생각했다. 그러다 문득 한 가지 생각이 떠올랐다. 과연 어떤 경우에든, 사랑하는 그리샤가 아예 태어나지 않았더라면 그 애에게 더 나았을까? 그리고 이것이 그녀에게는 너무도 터무니없고 기이하게 느껴져서, 그녀는 빙빙 도는 미친 생각들의 이 엉킨 실타래를 떨쳐 내려고 고개를 가로저었다.\n\n\"아니에요, 모르겠어요. 그건 옳지 않아요.\" 그녀는 얼굴에 혐오의 표정을 띤 채 그 말만 했다.\n\n\"그래요, 하지만 당신과 나는…… 그걸 잊으면 안 돼요. 게다가,\" 안나는 자기 논거의 풍성함과 돌리가 내놓는 반박의 빈약함에도 불구하고, 여전히 그것이 옳지 않다고 인정하는 듯한 태도로 덧붙였다. \"가장 중요한 점을 잊지 말아요, 지금 나는 당신과 같은 처지에 있지 않다는 걸요. 당신에게 문제는 이거예요. 당신이 더 이상 아이를 갖지 않기를 바라느냐는 것. 그런데 나에게 문제는 이거죠. 내가 아이를 갖기를 바라느냐는 것. 그건 엄청난 차이예요. 내 처지에서는 내가 그걸 바랄 수 없다는 걸 당신도 알아야 해요.\"\n\n다리야 알렉산드로브나는 아무 대답도 하지 않았다. 그녀는 문득 자기가 안나에게서 멀찍이 떨어져 버렸다는 것을, 두 사람 사이에는 결코 의견이 일치할 수 없는, 그래서 차라리 입에 올리지 않는 편이 나은 문제들의 장벽이 가로놓여 있다는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n# Chapter 24\n\n\"그럼 가능하다면 당신의 처지를 합법적인 것으로 만들어야 할 이유가 더더욱 분명해지는 거예요.\" 돌리가 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 가능하다면 말이지.\" 안나가 별안간 전혀 다른 어조로, 가라앉은 슬픈 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"설마 이혼이 불가능하다는 건 아니겠죠? 당신 남편이 이혼에 동의했다고 들었는데요.\"\n\n\"돌리, 나 그 얘기는 하고 싶지 않아.\"\n\n\"오, 그럼 그만두죠.\" 다리아 알렉산드로브나는 안나의 얼굴에 떠오른 고통스러운 표정을 알아채고는 서둘러 말했다. \"다만 내 눈에는 당신이 모든 걸 너무 어둡게만 보는 것 같아서요.\"\n\"내가? 천만에! 난 늘 밝고 행복한걸. 봐, je fais des passions. 베슬로프스키만 해도……\"\n\n* je fais des passions: 프랑스어로 '나는 사람들의 마음을 사로잡지'라는 뜻.\n\n\"그래, 솔직히 말하면 난 베슬로프스키의 그 말투가 마음에 안 들어.\" 다리아 알렉산드로브나가 화제를 바꾸고 싶어 하며 말했다.\n\n\"오, 그건 말도 안 되는 소리야! 그저 알렉세이를 즐겁게 해 줄 뿐, 그게 전부야. 게다가 그는 어린애 같아서 내 손바닥 안에 있는걸. 알겠지만, 난 그를 내 마음대로 다룬다고. 당신네 그리샤하고 똑같은 셈이지…… 돌리!\" 그녀는 불쑥 화제를 바꾸었다. \"당신은 내가 모든 걸 너무 어둡게 본다고 하지. 당신은 이해할 수 없어. 이건 너무나 끔찍해! 난 차라리 아무 시각도 갖지 않으려고 애쓰는 거야.\"\n\n\"하지만 내 생각엔 그래선 안 돼. 할 수 있는 건 다 해 봐야 해.\"\n\n\"하지만 내가 뭘 할 수 있겠어? 아무것도 못 해. 당신은 나더러 알렉세이와 결혼하라고 하면서, 내가 그걸 생각하지 않는다고 하지. 내가 그걸 생각하지 않는다고!\" 그녀는 거듭 말했고, 얼굴에 홍조가 번졌다. 그녀는 가슴을 곧게 펴며 일어나 무겁게 한숨을 쉬었다. 그녀는 가벼운 발걸음으로 방 안을 이리저리 거닐며 이따금 멈춰 섰다. \"내가 그걸 생각하지 않는다고? 그걸 생각하지 않고, 또 그걸 생각한다는 이유로 나 자신을 책망하지 않는 날이, 단 한 시간도 없는데…… 그걸 생각하면 미쳐 버릴 것 같으니까. 미쳐 버릴 것 같단 말이야!\" 그녀는 되풀이했다. \"그걸 생각하면 모르핀 없이는 잠을 잘 수가 없어. 하지만 됐어. 차분하게 얘기하자고. 사람들은 나더러 이혼하라고 하지. 우선, 그이는 내게 이혼을 해 주지 않을 거야. 지금 그이는 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인의 영향력 아래에 있거든.\"\n\n다리아 알렉산드로브나는 의자에 꼿꼿이 앉은 채로 고개를 돌려, 동정 어린 괴로운 얼굴로 안나의 뒤를 좇았다.\n\n\"그래도 시도는 해 봐야 해.\" 그녀가 부드럽게 말했다.\n\n\"내가 시도를 한다고 치자. 그게 무슨 뜻일까?\" 그녀는 천 번도 더 곱씹어 외운 어떤 생각을 입 밖으로 내뱉는 게 분명해 보였다. \"그건 내가, 그이를 미워하면서도 여전히 내가 그이에게 잘못을 저질렀다는 걸 인정하면서—나는 그이가 너그러운 사람이라고 여기거든—그러니까 내가 나 자신을 낮추어 그이에게 편지를 쓴다는 뜻이야…… 좋아, 그렇게 애써 본다고 치자. 내가 그렇게 한다고 치자고. 그럼 나는 모욕적인 거절을 당하거나 동의를 받겠지…… 좋아, 그이의 동의를 받았다고 치자……\" 안나는 그 순간 방의 가장 먼 끝에 가 있었고, 거기서 멈춰 서서 창가의 커튼을 만지작거리며 무언가를 하고 있었다. \"그이의 동의를 받았다고 쳐. 하지만 내…… 내 아들은? 그들은 그 애를 내게 내주지 않을 거야. 그 애는 내가 버린 아버지 곁에서 나를 경멸하며 자라겠지. 알겠어, 난 사랑해…… 똑같이, 그래 똑같이 사랑하는 것 같아, 하지만 둘 다 나 자신보다 더 사랑해—그 두 존재, 세료자와 알렉세이를.\"\n\n그녀는 방 한가운데로 나와 돌리를 마주 보고 섰고, 두 팔을 가슴에 단단히 포갰다. 하얀 실내복을 입은 그녀의 자태는 평소보다 더 위풍당당하고 풍만해 보였다. 그녀는 고개를 숙인 채, 빛나는 젖은 눈으로 눈썹 아래에서 돌리를 바라보았다. 기운 실내복 윗도리에 잠자리 모자를 쓴, 가냘프고 가엾은 작은 모습으로 온몸을 떨며 격정에 사로잡힌 돌리를.\n\n\"내가 사랑하는 건 오직 그 두 존재뿐인데, 한쪽이 다른 한쪽을 밀어내. 난 그 둘을 함께 둘 수가 없어, 그런데 그게 내가 원하는 단 하나뿐인 거야. 그리고 그걸 가질 수 없으니, 나머지는 아무래도 상관없어. 아무것도, 아무것도 상관없다고. 그러니 이건 어떻게든 한쪽으로 끝나 버릴 테고, 그래서 난 이 얘기를 할 수가 없어, 하고 싶지가 않아. 그러니 나를 책망하지 마, 무슨 일이든 나를 판단하지 마. 당신의 그 순결한 마음으로는 내가 겪는 이 모든 고통을 이해할 수 없을 테니까.\" 그녀는 다가와 돌리 곁에 앉았고, 죄지은 듯한 표정으로 돌리의 얼굴을 들여다보며 그 손을 잡았다.\n\n\"무슨 생각을 해? 나에 대해 무슨 생각을 하는 거야? 나를 경멸하지 마. 난 경멸받을 만한 사람이 아니야. 난 그저 불행할 뿐이야. 누군가 불행한 사람이 있다면, 그게 바로 나야.\" 그녀는 또박또박 말하더니, 고개를 돌리며 울음을 터뜨렸다.\n\n홀로 남은 다리아 알렉산드로브나는 기도를 올리고 잠자리에 들었다. 안나와 이야기를 나누는 동안에는 온 마음으로 그녀를 가엾게 여겼지만, 이제는 그녀를 떠올리려 해도 도무지 마음이 가지 않았다. 집과 아이들에 대한 기억이 그녀의 상상 속에 떠올랐는데, 그것은 그녀에게 전혀 새로운, 어떤 독특한 매력과 함께, 일종의 새로운 광채를 띠고 있었다. 그녀 자신의 그 세계가 이제는 너무나 달콤하고 소중하게 느껴져서, 무슨 일이 있어도 그 바깥에서 하루도 더 보내고 싶지 않았고, 그래서 그녀는 반드시 다음 날 돌아가리라 마음을 굳혔다.\n\n그사이 안나는 자기 내실로 돌아가 포도주잔을 집어 들고는 거기에 약을 몇 방울 떨어뜨렸는데, 그 주된 성분은 모르핀이었다. 그것을 단숨에 들이켜고 잠시 가만히 앉아 있던 그녀는, 마음이 진정되고 한결 밝아진 기분으로 침실로 들어갔다.\n\n그녀가 침실로 들어서자 브론스키는 그녀를 유심히 바라보았다. 그는 그녀가 돌리의 방에 그토록 오래 머물렀으니 분명 나누었을 그 대화의 흔적을 찾고 있었다. 그러나 그녀의 절제된 흥분과 어떤 조심스러움이 깃든 표정에서 그가 찾아낼 수 있는 것이라곤, 익숙해졌으면서도 늘 새롭게 그를 매혹하는 그 아름다움과, 그 아름다움에 대한 자각과, 그것이 그에게 영향을 미치기를 바라는 욕망뿐이었다. 그는 그녀에게 무슨 이야기를 나누었느냐고 묻고 싶지 않았지만, 그녀가 자기 입으로 뭔가 말해 주기를 바랐다. 하지만 그녀는 이렇게만 말했다.\n\n\"당신이 돌리를 마음에 들어 해서 정말 기뻐. 마음에 들지, 그렇지?\"\n\n\"오, 알다시피 그녀를 안 지가 오래됐잖아. 무척 마음씨가 고운 사람인 것 같아, mais excessivement terre-à-terre. 그래도 그녀를 보게 되어서 정말 기뻐.\"\n* mais excessivement terre-à-terre: 프랑스어로 '하지만 지나치게 현실적이고 평범하지'라는 뜻.\n\n그는 안나의 손을 잡고 묻는 듯한 눈길로 그녀의 눈을 들여다보았다.\n\n그 눈길을 잘못 해석한 그녀는 그를 향해 미소 지었다. 이튿날 아침, 다리아 알렉산드로브나는 집주인들의 만류에도 불구하고 집으로 돌아갈 채비를 했다. 레빈의 마부가, 결코 새것이라 할 수 없는 외투에 낡은 모자를 쓰고, 짝이 안 맞는 말들과 흙받기를 기운 마차를 끌고, 자갈을 깐 지붕 덮인 현관 진입로로 침울한 결의를 띤 채 들어섰다.\n\n다리아 알렉산드로브나는 바르바라 공작 영애며 손님들과 작별 인사를 나누는 것이 내키지 않았다. 하루를 함께 보내고 나자, 그녀나 집주인들이나 서로 잘 맞지 않으며, 만나지 않는 편이 낫다는 것을 분명히 깨달았던 것이다. 오직 안나만이 서글퍼했다. 그녀는 이제 돌리가 떠나고 나면, 두 사람의 대화로 일깨워졌던 그 감정들을 자기 영혼 속에서 다시 흔들어 깨워 줄 사람이 아무도 없으리라는 것을 알았다. 그 감정들을 흔들어 깨우는 일은 그녀에게 괴로웠지만, 그래도 그녀는 그것이 자기 영혼의 가장 좋은 부분이며, 자기가 살아가는 이 삶 속에서 그 부분이 머잖아 질식해 버리리라는 것을 알고 있었다.\n\n탁 트인 들판으로 마차를 몰아 나오자, 다리아 알렉산드로브나는 기분 좋은 해방감을 느꼈고, 두 사내에게 브론스키의 저택에 머문 것이 어땠는지 물어보고 싶은 마음이 들었다. 그때 마부 필립이 묻지도 않았는데 불쑥 말을 꺼냈다.\n\n\"재산이야 넘쳐 나는지 몰라도, 우리한테 준 거라곤 귀리 세 됫박이 전부였습죠. 닭이 울 무렵엔 낟알 한 톨 안 남고 죄다 동나 버렸어요. 세 됫박이 다 뭡니까? 입에 풀칠도 안 되는걸요! 게다가 요즘은 귀리가 사십오 코페이카까지 떨어졌는데 말이죠. 우리 집에선 어림없는 소리, 누가 오든 배불리 먹을 만큼은 얼마든지 준다고요.\"\n\n\"주인이 구두쇠로군요.\" 회계 사무소 서기가 한마디 거들었다.\n\n\"그래, 그 집 말들은 마음에 들었나?\" 돌리가 물었다.\n\n\"말들이라! 그건 두말할 것도 없습죠. 먹을 것도 좋았고요. 하지만 제겐 어쩐지 그곳이 좀 음울하게 느껴졌습니다요, 다리아 알렉산드로브나. 마님은 어떻게 생각하셨는지 모르겠지만요.\" 그가 잘생기고 선량한 얼굴을 그녀 쪽으로 돌리며 말했다.\n\n\"나도 그렇게 생각했어. 그래, 저녁까지는 집에 닿을 수 있을까?\"\n\n\"아, 닿아야 합죠!\"\n\n집에 도착해 모든 것이 더할 나위 없이 만족스럽고 유난히 정겹다는 걸 알게 된 다리아 알렉산드로브나는, 더없이 활기찬 어조로 식구들에게 자기가 어떻게 도착했는지, 그들이 자기를 얼마나 따뜻하게 맞아 주었는지, 브론스키 부부가 얼마나 호화롭고 고상한 취향 속에서 살고 있는지, 그들이 어떤 여흥을 즐기는지 이야기하기 시작했고, 그들에 대해 험담하는 말은 한마디도 허락하지 않았다.\n\n\"안나와 브론스키를 알아야 해요—난 이제 그이를 더 잘 알게 됐는데—그래야 그들이 얼마나 멋지고 또 얼마나 가슴을 울리는 사람들인지 알 수 있죠.\" 그녀는 이제 더없이 진심 어린 어조로 말했고, 그곳에서 겪었던 막연한 불만과 어색함의 감정은 까맣게 잊어버렸다.\n\n# Chapter 25\n\n브론스키와 안나는 여름 내내, 그리고 겨울의 일부를 시골에서 보냈다. 여전히 똑같은 형편으로 지냈고, 이혼을 얻어 내기 위한 어떤 조치도 취하지 않았다. 어디로든 떠나지 않는다는 것이 두 사람 사이에 암묵적으로 정해진 일이었다. 그러나 둘만 지내는 시간이 길어질수록, 특히 가을에 집에 손님 하나 없이 보낼 때면, 두 사람 모두 이런 생활을 견딜 수 없으며 무언가 바꾸어야만 한다는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n겉으로 보기에 그들의 생활은 더 바랄 것이 없을 만큼 좋았다. 무엇 하나 모자람 없이 풍족하게 갖추고 있었고, 아이도 있었으며, 둘 다 할 일이 있었다. 안나는 손님이 없을 때에도 자기 외모에 똑같이 정성을 쏟았고, 소설이든 유행하는 진지한 문학이든 무척 많이 읽었다. 그녀는 자기가 받아 보는 외국 신문이나 비평지에서 칭찬받는 책이라면 모조리 주문했고, 칩거 중에 읽는 것에만 쏟을 수 있는 그런 집중된 주의력으로 그것들을 읽었다. 게다가 브론스키가 흥미를 두는 주제라면 무엇이든 책과 전문지를 통해 공부했기 때문에, 그는 농업이나 건축에 관한 질문, 때로는 말 사육이나 스포츠에 관한 질문조차 곧장 그녀에게 들고 가곤 했다. 그는 그녀의 지식과 기억력에 놀랐고, 처음에는 그것을 의심하여 사실을 확인해 달라고 청하곤 했다. 그러면 그녀는 그가 묻는 것을 어느 책에선가 찾아내어 그에게 보여 주었다.\n\n병원 건축 역시 그녀의 관심을 끌었다. 그녀는 단순히 돕기만 한 것이 아니라 직접 많은 것을 계획하고 제안했다. 그러나 그녀의 가장 큰 생각은 여전히 자기 자신에 관한 것이었다. 자기가 브론스키에게 얼마나 소중한 존재인지, 그가 포기한 모든 것을 자기가 얼마나 보상해 줄 수 있는지 하는 것이었다. 브론스키는 단지 그를 기쁘게 하려는 것만이 아니라 그를 위해 헌신하려는 이 욕망을, 그것이 그녀 존재의 유일한 목적이 되어 버린 그 욕망을 고맙게 여겼다. 그러나 동시에 그녀가 그를 단단히 붙들어 두려고 쳐 놓은 사랑의 올가미에 지쳐 갔다. 시간이 흐를수록, 그리고 자기가 점점 더 자주 그 올가미에 단단히 걸려 있는 것을 볼수록, 그에게는 거기서 벗어나려는 마음이라기보다 그것들이 자기 자유를 가로막는지 시험해 보고 싶은 마음이 점점 커져 갔다. 이렇게 자유로워지고 싶은 욕망, 회의나 경마를 보러 시내에 가려 할 때마다 소동을 겪지 않았으면 하는 욕망만 없었더라면, 브론스키는 자기 생활에 더할 나위 없이 만족했을 것이다. 그가 떠맡은 역할, 즉 마땅히 러시아 귀족 계급의 한복판이 되어야 할 부류의 한 사람인 부유한 지주라는 역할은 전적으로 그의 취향에 맞았다. 그리고 이제 그 역할로 반년을 보내고 나니 거기서 더 큰 만족을 얻었다. 점점 더 그를 사로잡고 빨아들이던 영지 경영도 대단히 성공적이었다. 병원, 기계, 스위스에서 주문한 소들, 그 밖의 여러 가지에 막대한 비용이 들었음에도, 그는 자기가 재산을 낭비하는 것이 아니라 불려 가고 있다고 확신했다. 수입과 관계되는 모든 일, 목재와 밀과 양모의 판매, 토지의 임대에 있어서 브론스키는 바위처럼 단단했고 가격을 유지하는 법을 잘 알고 있었다. 이 영지와 다른 영지들에서 벌이는 큰 규모의 모든 사업에서 그는 위험이 따르지 않는 가장 단순한 방법을 고수했고, 자질구레한 세부 사항에서는 극도로 신중하고 까다로웠다. 독일인 집사는 늘 처음 견적을 실제 필요한 것보다 훨씬 부풀려 잡은 다음, 그것을 더 싸게 살 수 있으니 그러면 이득을 보는 셈이라고 브론스키를 구슬려 사들이도록 꾀려고 온갖 교활한 술수와 잔꾀를 부렸지만, 브론스키는 넘어가지 않았다. 그는 집사의 말을 듣고 따져 물었으며, 주문하거나 제작할 기구가 아주 최신식이어서 아직 러시아에 알려지지 않았고 사람들을 놀라게 할 만한 것일 때에만 그 제안에 동의했다. 그런 예외를 빼면, 그는 여유가 있을 때에만 지출을 늘리기로 결심했고, 그런 지출을 할 때에는 아주 사소한 세부까지 따져 가며 자기 돈에 합당한 최상의 것을 얻어 내기를 고집했다. 그래서 그가 일을 처리하는 방식만 보아도 그가 재산을 낭비하는 것이 아니라 불려 가고 있음이 분명했다.\n\n10월에는 카신스키 현에서 지방 선거가 있었는데, 그곳에는 브론스키, 스비아시스키, 코즈니셰프, 오블론스키의 영지가 있었고 레빈 땅도 일부 있었다.\n\n이 선거는 그와 관련된 몇 가지 사정으로, 또 거기에 참가하는 사람들 때문에 세간의 이목을 끌고 있었다. 그것을 두고 말이 무성했고 큰 준비가 진행되고 있었다. 평소 선거에 한 번도 나가 본 적 없는 사람들까지 모스크바에서, 페테르부르크에서, 그리고 외국에서 이 선거에 참석하러 오고 있었다. 브론스키는 오래전에 스비아시스키에게 함께 가겠다고 약속해 두었다. 선거를 앞두고, 보즈드비젠스코예에 자주 들르던 스비아시스키가 브론스키를 데리러 마차를 몰고 왔다. 그 전날, 이 예정된 나들이를 두고 브론스키와 안나 사이에는 거의 다툼이 일 뻔했다. 시골에서는 더없이 음울하게 느껴지는, 가장 따분한 가을 날씨였다. 그래서 브론스키는 한판 싸움을 각오하고, 일찍이 그녀에게 그래 본 적 없는 딱딱하고 차가운 표정으로 떠난다는 것을 안나에게 알렸다. 그러나 뜻밖에도 안나는 그 소식을 아주 침착하게 받아들이며, 언제 돌아오느냐고 묻기만 했다. 그는 이 침착함을 어떻게 설명해야 할지 몰라 그녀를 뚫어지게 바라보았다. 그녀는 그의 시선에 미소를 지었다. 그는 그녀가 자기 안으로 움츠러드는 그 방식을 알고 있었고, 그것이 그녀가 자기에게 계획을 알리지 않은 채 무언가를 마음먹었을 때에만 일어난다는 것도 알고 있었다. 그는 이것이 두려웠다. 그러나 소동만큼은 피하고 싶은 마음이 어찌나 간절했던지, 그는 겉모양을 꾸미면서 자기가 믿고 싶은 것을, 즉 그녀의 분별 있음을 반쯤은 진심으로 믿었다.\n\n\"심심하지는 않겠지?\"\n\n\"안 그럴 거예요.\" 안나가 말했다. \"어제 고티에서 책 한 상자를 받았거든요. 아니요, 심심하지 않을 거예요.\"\n\n'저런 투를 취하려는 거군, 그러는 편이 차라리 낫지.' 그는 생각했다. '안 그러면 또 똑같은 일이 끝없이 되풀이될 테니까.'\n\n그리고 그는 그녀에게 솔직한 해명을 청하지 않은 채 선거를 향해 떠났다. 두 사람이 가까워진 이래 그가 충분한 해명 없이 그녀와 헤어진 것은 이번이 처음이었다. 한편으로 이것이 그를 불안하게 했지만, 다른 한편으로는 이렇게 하는 편이 낫다고 느꼈다. '처음에는 이번처럼 무언가 분명치 않은 것을 감추어 두겠지만, 그러다 보면 익숙해질 거야. 어쨌든 나는 그녀를 위해 무엇이든 포기할 수 있어. 하지만 남자로서의 독립만큼은 안 돼.' 그는 생각했다.\n\n# Chapter 26\n\n9월에 레빈은 키티의 출산을 위해 모스크바로 거처를 옮겼다. 모스크바에서 할 일 없이 꼬박 한 달을 보냈을 무렵, 카신 현에 영지를 가지고 있고 다가오는 선거 문제에 큰 관심을 두고 있던 세르게이 이바노비치가 선거에 참석하러 떠날 채비를 했다. 그는 셀레즈뇨프스키 군에서 투표권을 가진 동생에게 함께 가자고 청했다. 게다가 레빈은 카신에서 토지의 후견에 관련된 일과, 외국에 나가 있는 누이를 위해 모종의 상환금을 수령하는, 대단히 중대한 용무를 처리해야 했다.\n\n레빈은 여전히 망설였지만, 그가 모스크바에서 지루해하는 것을 본 키티가 가라고 부추겼고, 제 권한으로 칠 파운드짜리 귀족용 정복을 그에게 맞춰 주었다. 그리고 정복 값으로 치른 그 칠 파운드가, 결국 레빈으로 하여금 떠나기로 마음먹게 한 가장 큰 이유였다. 그는 카신으로 떠났다…….\n\n레빈은 카신에 엿새째 머물면서 날마다 의회에 들렀고, 여전히 질질 끌고 있는 누이의 용무로 분주히 매달려 있었다. 군의 귀족회 의장들은 모두 선거에 매여 있었고, 후견 재판소에 달린 가장 단순한 일조차 처리할 도리가 없었다. 다른 일, 즉 받아야 할 돈의 지급 문제 역시 난관에 부딪혔다. 법적 세부 사항을 둘러싸고 오래 협의한 끝에 마침내 돈을 지급할 채비가 갖춰졌지만, 더없이 친절한 사람인 공증인은 그 지급 명령서를 넘겨줄 수가 없었다. 거기에는 의장의 서명이 있어야 했는데, 의장은 직무를 대리인에게 넘기지도 않은 채 선거에 가 있었기 때문이다. 이 모든 성가신 협의들, 이곳저곳 끝없이 돌아다니는 일, 청원자의 처지가 곤란하다는 것을 충분히 알면서도 그를 도울 힘은 없는 친절하고 훌륭한 사람들과 이야기를 나누는 일—아무런 결실도 맺지 못하는 이 모든 노력은, 꿈속에서 물리적인 힘을 쓰려 할 때 느끼는, 그 굴욕스러운 무력감과 비슷한 비참한 감정을 레빈에게 안겨 주었다. 더없이 친절한 변호사와 이야기를 나눌 때면 그는 이 감정을 자주 느꼈다. 이 변호사는 보아하니 가능한 모든 일을 다 했고, 그를 곤경에서 빼내려고 온 신경을 곤두세웠다. \"이렇게 한번 해 보시지요.\" 그는 한두 번이 아니게 말했다. \"아무개와 아무개를 찾아가 보십시오.\" 그러고는 모든 일을 가로막는 그 치명적인 지점을 우회할 만한 정식 계획을 세워 주었다. 하지만 그는 곧바로 이렇게 덧붙였다. \"어쨌든 시간은 좀 걸릴 테지만, 한번 해 보시는 게 좋겠지요.\" 그래서 레빈은 시도했고, 또 찾아갔다. 모두가 친절하고 정중했지만, 피해 간 듯했던 그 지점은 결국 다시 불쑥 튀어나와 또다시 길을 가로막았다. 특히 견디기 힘든 것은, 자기가 누구와 씨름하고 있는지, 자기 일이 처리되지 않는 것이 누구에게 이득이 되는지를 레빈으로서는 도무지 알 수 없다는 점이었다. 아무도 그것을 모르는 듯했고, 변호사 역시 분명 몰랐다. 마치 철도역 매표소에는 한 줄로만 다가갈 수 있다는 것을 그가 이해하듯, 만약 그 까닭을 이해할 수만 있었다면, 그에게 이토록 짜증스럽고 진저리 나는 일은 아니었을 것이다. 그러나 그의 용무 앞에 가로놓인 장애물들에 대해서는, 어째서 그것들이 존재하는지 아무도 설명해 줄 수 없었다.\n\n그러나 결혼한 이후 레빈은 꽤나 달라져 있었다. 그는 인내심이 있었고, 어째서 모든 일이 이렇게 짜여 있는지 알 수 없을 때면, 전후 사정을 다 알지 못하고서는 판단할 수 없으며 십중팔구 그럴 수밖에 없는 노릇이리라고 스스로에게 일렀고, 안달하지 않으려 애썼다.\n\n선거에 참석하고 거기에 한몫 끼면서도, 그는 이제 함부로 판단하거나 트집 잡지 않으려 했고, 자기가 존경하는 정직하고 훌륭한 사람들이 그토록 진지하고 열렬하게 몰두하는 그 문제를 되도록 온전히 이해하려고 애썼다. 결혼한 이후 레빈에게는 삶의 새롭고 진지한 면면들이 숱하게 드러났는데, 예전에는 그것들을 경박하게 대했던 탓에 아무 중요성도 없어 보였던 것들이었다. 그래서 선거 문제에서도 그는 어떤 진지한 의미를 가정하고 그것을 찾아내려 애썼다.\n\n세르게이 이바노비치는 선거에서 꾀하는 변혁의 의미와 목적을 그에게 설명해 주었다. 법률이 그토록 많은 중요한 공적 직무의 통제권을 손에 쥐여 준 사람, 즉 현 귀족회 의장—피후견인의 후견(바로 지금 레빈을 그토록 애먹이고 있는 그 부서), 현 귀족들이 갹출한 거액의 운용, 여자·남자·군사 고등학교, 그리고 새로운 방식의 국민 교육, 끝으로 군 의회까지—그 현 귀족회 의장인 스네트코프는 구식 귀족이었다. 막대한 재산을 흥청망청 탕진하는 사람, 마음씨는 착하고 제 나름으로는 정직하지만, 현대의 요구에 대해서는 전혀 이해가 없는 사람이었다. 그는 어떤 문제에서든 늘 귀족 편을 들었다. 국민 교육의 보급에 대놓고 적대적이었고, 마땅히 막대한 중요성을 지녀야 할 군 의회에 순전히 당파적인 성격을 입히는 데 성공했다. 필요한 것은 그의 자리에 참신하고 유능하며 완전히 현대적이고 동시대적인 사상을 지닌 인물을 앉히는 일이었고, 또한 귀족에게 주어진 권리로부터—귀족으로서가 아니라 군 의회의 한 요소로서—거기서 끌어낼 수 있는 자치의 권능을 죄다 끄집어내도록 정책을 짜는 일이었다. 모든 면에서 늘 다른 현들을 앞서 이끌어 온 부유한 카신 현에는, 이제 일단 그곳에서 이 정책이 제대로 관철되기만 하면 다른 현들, 나아가 러시아 전체의 본보기가 될 만큼 막강한 세력의 우위가 형성되어 있었다. 그러므로 이 문제 전체가 더없이 중대한 것이었다. 스네트코프 대신 의장으로 스비아시스키를, 아니 그보다는 더 나은 인물로서 전직 대학교수이자 비범한 지성을 갖춘, 세르게이 이바노비치의 절친한 벗인 네뱌돕스키를 선출하자는 안이 제기되었다.\n\n회의는 지사가 개회했는데, 그는 귀족들에게 연설을 하면서 인물에 대한 사사로운 정 때문이 아니라 조국에 대한 봉사와 복리를 위해 공직자들을 선출하라고 촉구했고, 카신 현의 명예로운 귀족들이 지난 모든 선거에서처럼 그들의 의무를 신성하게 여기며 군주의 드높은 신임에 보답하기를 바란다고 했다.\n\n연설을 마치고 나자 지사는 회장을 걸어 나갔고, 귀족들은 떠들썩하고 열렬하게—더러는 흥분하기까지 하며—그를 뒤따라 우르르 몰려갔으며, 그가 모피 외투를 입고 현 귀족회 의장과 다정하게 이야기를 나누는 동안 그를 에워쌌다. 모든 것을 들여다보고 무엇 하나 놓치지 않으려는 레빈도 그 무리 속에 끼여 서 있었는데, 지사가 이렇게 말하는 소리를 들었다. \"마리야 이바노브나에게 제 아내가 보육원에 오지 못해 무척 아쉬워한다고 전해 주십시오.\" 그러고 나서 귀족들은 한껏 들뜬 기분으로 저마다 모피 외투를 골라 입고는 모두 대성당으로 향했다.\n\n대성당에서 레빈은 다른 사람들처럼 손을 들고 부제의 말을 따라 외며, 지사가 그들이 해 주기를 바란 모든 일을 하겠노라고 무시무시한 맹세를 했다. 교회의 예배는 늘 레빈의 마음을 움직였고, 그가 \"십자가에 입을 맞춥니다\"라는 말을 내뱉으며 같은 말을 따라 외는 젊고 늙은 사내들의 무리를 둘러보았을 때, 그는 가슴이 뭉클해지는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n이튿날과 사흗날에는 세르게이 이바노비치의 설명에 따르면 아무 중요성도 없는, 귀족의 재정과 여자 고등학교에 관한 안건이 다뤄졌고, 자기 일을 돌보느라 분주한 레빈은 회의에 참석하지 않았다. 나흗날에는 현 귀족회 의장의 높은 자리에서 의장의 회계 감사가 이루어졌다. 그리고 바로 그때 신파(新派)와 구파(舊派) 사이에 첫 충돌이 일어났다. 회계를 검증하도록 위임받은 위원회가 모든 것이 제대로 되어 있다고 회의에 보고했다. 현 귀족회 의장은 자리에서 일어나 귀족들의 신임에 감사하며 눈물을 흘렸다. 귀족들은 그에게 우렁찬 갈채를 보내며 악수를 나누었다. 그러나 그 순간 세르게이 이바노비치 편의 한 귀족이, 위원회가 그런 검증을 현 귀족회 의장에 대한 모욕이라고 여겨 회계를 검증하지 않았다는 말을 들었다고 했다. 위원회 위원 가운데 한 사람이 경솔하게도 그것을 시인했다. 그러자 매우 젊어 보이지만 매우 악의에 찬 자그마한 신사가, 현 귀족회 의장으로서는 아마 공금 지출에 대해 해명하는 편이 흡족할 터인데, 위원회 위원들의 엉뚱한 배려가 그에게서 이 도덕적 만족을 앗아 가고 있는 것이라고 말하기 시작했다. 그러자 위원회 위원들은 자기들의 시인을 철회하려 했고, 세르게이 이바노비치는 논리적으로 따져 회계를 검증했든가 하지 않았든가 둘 중 하나를 인정할 수밖에 없다는 것을 증명하기 시작하더니, 이 딜레마를 세세하게 펼쳐 보였다. 세르게이 이바노비치에게 반대편의 대변인이 응수했다. 그다음에는 스비아시스키가 발언했고, 그다음에는 그 악의에 찬 신사가 또다시 발언했다. 토론은 오래도록 이어졌으나 아무 결론도 없이 끝났다. 레빈은 그들이 이 주제를 두고 이토록 오래 다투는 것이 의아했는데, 더욱이 세르게이 이바노비치에게 돈이 횡령되었다고 생각하느냐고 물었을 때 세르게이 이바노비치가 이렇게 대답했기에 더 그러했다.\n\n\"오, 아니야! 그는 정직한 사람이지. 하지만 현의 일을 운영하면서 가부장적인 가족 운영 방식의 그 구태의연한 수법은 깨부숴야 해.\"\n\n닷새째 되던 날에는 군 귀족회 의장 선거가 있었다. 몇몇 군에서는 꽤나 험악한 날이었다. 셀레즈뇨프스키 군에서는 스비아시스키가 투표도 없이 만장일치로 선출되었고, 그는 그날 저녁 만찬을 베풀었다.\n\n# Chapter 27\n\n엿새째 되는 날이 도(道) 귀족회장 선거일로 정해졌다.\n\n크고 작은 방마다 온갖 제복을 입은 귀족들로 가득 찼다. 많은 이들이 오로지 그날을 위해서만 모여들었다. 몇 년 동안 서로 만나지 못한 사람들이, 더러는 크림 반도에서, 더러는 페테르부르크에서, 더러는 외국에서 와 귀족회관의 방들에서 마주쳤다. 차르의 초상 아래 놓인 지사(知事)의 탁자 주위에서는 많은 논의가 오갔다.\n\n귀족들은 큰 방에서나 작은 방에서나 진영을 이루어 패를 갈랐고, 서로를 향한 적대적이고 의심에 찬 눈길에서, 외부인이 한 무리에 다가오면 일제히 입을 다무는 침묵에서, 또 몇몇이 서로 귓속말을 주고받으며 저 안쪽 복도로 물러나는 모양새에서, 양편이 서로에게 숨기는 비밀을 품고 있음이 뚜렷이 드러났다. 외관상으로 귀족들은 두 부류로 분명하게 나뉘었다. 구파와 신파였다. 구파는 대개 단추를 목까지 꼭 채운 구식 귀족 제복에 박차와 모자를 갖추었거나, 아니면 저마다의 해군, 기병, 보병, 혹은 관직 제복을 입고 있었다. 나이 든 이들의 제복은 어깨에 견장을 단 구식 자수로 장식되어 있었는데, 마치 입은 사람이 그 옷을 벗어나도록 자라 버린 듯, 영락없이 허리께가 끼고 짧았다. 젊은 축들은 허리가 길고 어깨가 넓은 귀족 제복을 흰 조끼 위에 단추를 풀어 헤쳐 입거나, 검은 깃에 치안판사의 자수 휘장을 단 제복을 입고 있었다. 군중 속을 여기저기서 환하게 밝히던 궁정 제복도 젊은 축의 것이었다.\n\n그러나 노소(老少)의 구분이 당파의 구분과 일치하지는 않았다. 레빈이 보니 젊은이들 가운데 더러는 구파에 속했고, 반대로 가장 나이 든 귀족들 가운데 더러는 스비아시스키와 귓속말을 나누며 명백히 신파의 열성 지지자 노릇을 하고 있었다.\n\n레빈은 사람들이 담배를 피우고 가벼운 음식을 들고 있는 작은 방에서, 제 친구들 가까이에 서서 그들이 하는 말을 귀담아들으며, 무슨 이야기인지 알아내려고 양심껏 자기 머리를 다 짜냈다. 세르게이 이바노비치가 다른 이들이 둘러서는 중심이었다. 그는 그때 스비아시스키와, 같은 당파에 속한 다른 군(郡)의 귀족회장 흘류스토프의 말에 귀를 기울이고 있었다. 흘류스토프는 자기 군을 이끌고 스네트코프에게 출마를 청하러 가는 데 동의하려 들지 않았고, 스비아시스키는 그렇게 하라고 그를 설득하고 있었으며, 세르게이 이바노비치는 그 계획에 찬성하고 있었다. 레빈은 자기들이 끌어내리려는 바로 그 회장에게 어째서 반대파가 출마를 청해야 한다는 것인지 도무지 알 수가 없었다.\n\n마침 술을 마시고 간단히 점심을 들고 온 스테판 아르카지치가 시종(侍從) 제복 차림으로, 가장자리에 무늬를 두른 얇은 무명 손수건에 향수를 뿌려 입술을 닦으며 그들에게 다가왔다.\n\n\"우리는 지금 병력을 배치하는 중이지.\" 그가 구레나룻을 잡아당기며 말했다. \"세르게이 이바노비치!\"\n\n그러고는 대화에 귀를 기울이다가 스비아시스키의 주장을 거들었다.\n\n\"한 군이면 충분하고말고. 스비아시스키가 반대파라는 건 누가 봐도 뻔하니까.\" 그가 말했는데, 레빈을 제외한 모두에게는 명백히 알아들을 만한 말이었다.\n\n\"아니, 코스챠, 너도 여기 있었구나! 너도 넘어온 모양이지, 응?\" 그는 레빈에게 돌아서서 그의 팔에 자기 팔을 끼며 덧붙였다. 레빈은 정말이지 기꺼이 넘어가고 싶었지만, 요점이 무엇인지 도무지 알 수가 없어, 이야기하는 이들에게서 몇 걸음 물러서서 스테판 아르카지치에게 어째서 도 귀족회장에게 출마를 청해야 하는지 자기로서는 이해할 수 없노라고 털어놓았다.\n\n\"O sancta simplicitas!\" 스테판 아르카지치가 말하고는 레빈에게 그것을 짤막하고 분명하게 설명해 주었다. 만약 지난 선거들에서처럼 모든 군이 도 귀족회장에게 출마를 청한다면, 그는 투표 없이 선출될 것이다. 그래서는 안 된다. 지금 여덟 개 군이 그를 추대하기로 합의했는데, 만일 두 개 군이 거부한다면 스네트코프가 아예 출마를 사양해 버릴지도 모르고, 그러면 구파가 자기 당파에서 다른 사람을 뽑을 수 있어, 그들의 계산이 송두리째 어그러질 것이다. 그러나 단 한 군, 곧 스비아시스키의 군만 그를 추대하지 않는다면, 스네트코프는 투표에 부쳐지도록 자신을 내맡길 것이다. 그들 가운데 몇몇은 일부러 그에게 표를 던져 그가 제법 많은 표를 얻게 만들 작정까지 하고 있었는데, 이는 적이 낌새를 채지 못하게 하기 위해서였고, 그래야 상대편 후보가 나설 때 자기들도 그에게 얼마간 표를 줄 수 있기 때문이었다. 레빈은 어느 정도는 알아들었으나 온전히 다는 아니어서 몇 가지를 더 물어보려던 참인데, 별안간 모두가 떠들고 소란을 피우기 시작하더니 큰 방 쪽으로 우르르 몰려갔다.\n\n\"무슨 일이야? 응? 누구를?\" \"자격이 없다고? 누가? 뭐가?\" \"그를 통과시키지 않겠다고?\" \"자격이 없어?\" \"플레로프를 들이지 않겠다는 거야?\" \"어, 그자에게 걸린 혐의 때문에?\" \"아니, 그런 식이라면 아무도 들이지 못하겠군. 이건 사기야!\" \"법이라고!\" 레빈은 사방에서 터져 나오는 외침을 들으며, 저마다 어딘가로 서두르고 무언가를 놓칠까 봐 두려워하는 다른 이들과 함께 큰 방으로 옮겨 갔다. 밀려드는 귀족들 틈에 끼여, 그는 도 귀족회장과 스비아시스키와 다른 지도자들이 무언가를 두고 격렬하게 다투고 있는 높은 탁자 가까이로 다가갔다.\n* O sancta simplicitas!: 라틴어로 '오, 거룩한 단순함이여!'. 순진함을 가벼이 비꼬는 표현.\n\n# Chapter 28\n\n레빈은 꽤 멀찍이 서 있었다. 옆에서 거칠고 쉰 숨을 몰아쉬는 귀족 한 사람과, 두꺼운 장화를 삐걱대는 또 다른 한 사람 때문에 그는 또렷하게 들을 수가 없었다. 그저 귀족 회장의 부드러운 목소리가 희미하게 들리고, 그다음에는 그 심술궂은 신사의 새된 목소리가, 또 그다음에는 스비야시스키의 목소리가 들릴 뿐이었다. 그가 알아들은 바로는, 그들은 법령을 어떻게 해석할 것인가, 그리고 \"재판에 회부될 수 있는\"이라는 말의 정확한 의미가 무엇인가를 두고 다투고 있었다.\n\n세르게이 이바노비치가 탁자로 다가가자 군중이 길을 터 주었다. 세르게이 이바노비치는 그 심술궂은 신사가 말을 끝마치기를 기다렸다가, 가장 좋은 해결책은 법령 자체를 참조하는 것이라고 본다고 말하며 서기에게 법령을 찾아 달라고 청했다. 법령에는, 의견 차이가 있을 경우 반드시 투표를 해야 한다고 적혀 있었다.\n\n세르게이 이바노비치가 법령을 읽고 그 의미를 설명하기 시작했는데, 바로 그때 키가 크고 뚱뚱하며 등이 굽은, 염색한 구레나룻을 기른 한 지주가, 목덜미를 옥죄는 꽉 끼는 제복을 입은 채 그의 말을 가로막았다. 그는 탁자로 다가가 손가락의 반지로 탁자를 두드리며 큰 소리로 외쳤다. \"투표합시다! 표결에 부칩시다! 더 이상 말할 것 없소!\" 그러자 여러 목소리가 한꺼번에 떠들기 시작했고, 반지를 낀 그 키 큰 귀족은 점점 더 격앙되어 점점 더 큰 소리로 외쳤다. 하지만 그가 무슨 말을 하는지는 도무지 알아들을 수 없었다.\n\n그는 세르게이 이바노비치가 제안한 바로 그 방안을 외치고 있었지만, 그가 세르게이 이바노비치와 그 일파를 모두 미워한다는 것은 분명했다. 그리고 이 증오의 감정은 무리 전체로 번져 나가, 반대편에서도 좀 더 점잖은 형태이긴 하나 똑같은 앙심을 불러일으켰다. 고함이 터져 나오고, 한순간 온통 아수라장이 되어, 도(道) 귀족 회장이 조용히 하라고 외쳐야 할 정도였다.\n\n\"투표! 투표! 모든 귀족이 다 알고 있소! 우리는 조국을 위해 피를 흘렸소!…… 군주의 신임을…… 회장의 회계 장부는 검사할 수 없소. 그는 출납계가 아니란 말이오…… 하지만 그게 요점이 아니오…… 투표 좀 합시다! 추잡하기는!……\" 사방에서 격분하고 거친 목소리들이 외쳤다. 눈빛과 얼굴은 그들의 말보다도 더 거칠고 격렬했다. 그것은 더없이 화해할 수 없는 증오를 드러내고 있었다. 레빈은 도대체 무엇이 문제인지 조금도 이해하지 못한 채, 플레로프에 관한 결정을 표결에 부칠 것이냐 말 것이냐를 두고 저토록 격정적으로 다투는 모습에 놀라워했다. 그는 세르게이 이바노비치가 나중에 설명해 준 그 삼단논법을 잊고 있었던 것이다. 즉, 공공의 이익을 위해서는 도 귀족 회장을 몰아내야 하고, 회장을 몰아내려면 다수표를 얻어야 하며, 다수표를 얻으려면 플레로프의 투표권을 확보해야 하고, 플레로프의 투표권을 인정받으려면 법령을 어떻게 해석할지 결정해야 한다는 것이었다.\n\n\"한 표가 문제 전체를 결정할 수도 있어. 그리고 공적인 일에 쓸모 있는 사람이 되고 싶다면 진지하고 일관성이 있어야 하는 거야.\" 세르게이 이바노비치가 결론지어 말했다. 하지만 레빈은 그 모든 것을 잊고 있었고, 자기가 존경하는 이 훌륭한 사람들이 저토록 불쾌하고 악의에 찬 흥분 상태에 빠진 모습을 보는 것이 괴로웠다. 이 괴로운 감정에서 벗어나려고 그는 옆방으로 가 버렸는데, 그곳에는 간이식당의 급사들 말고는 아무도 없었다. 그릇을 씻고 접시며 포도주잔을 정리하느라 분주한 급사들을 보고, 그 차분하고 명랑한 얼굴들을 보면서, 레빈은 마치 답답한 방에서 신선한 공기 속으로 나온 듯 뜻밖의 안도감을 느꼈다. 그는 흐뭇하게 급사들을 바라보며 이리저리 거닐기 시작했다. 특히 마음에 든 것은, 다른 젊은 급사들을 깔보다가 도리어 그들에게 놀림을 받는 회색 구레나룻의 급사 하나가 그들에게 냅킨을 제대로 접는 법을 가르치는 모습이었다. 레빈이 막 그 늙은 급사와 이야기를 나누려던 참에, 도의 모든 귀족을 이름과 부칭으로 알아맞히는 것이 전문인 후견 재판소의 서기, 그 자그마한 노인이 그를 끌고 갔다.\n\n\"이리 좀 오십시오, 콘스탄틴 드미트리예비치.\" 그가 말했다. \"형님께서 찾고 계십니다. 법적 쟁점에 대해 투표하는 중입니다.\"\n\n레빈은 방으로 들어가 흰 공 하나를 받아 들고, 형 세르게이 이바노비치를 따라 탁자로 갔다. 그곳에는 스비야시스키가 의미심장하고도 비꼬는 듯한 얼굴로, 주먹에 수염을 쥔 채 그것을 킁킁대며 서 있었다. 세르게이 이바노비치는 상자 안에 손을 넣어 어딘가에 공을 넣고는, 레빈에게 자리를 내주며 멈춰 섰다. 레빈은 앞으로 나아갔으나, 자기가 무엇을 해야 하는지 까맣게 잊은 채 몹시 당황하여 세르게이 이바노비치에게 물었다. \"이걸 어디에 넣어야 하죠?\" 그는 옆에서 한창 이야기가 오가는 틈을 타 작은 소리로 물었기에, 자기 질문이 들리지 않으리라 기대했다. 하지만 이야기하던 사람들이 말을 멈춘 탓에, 그의 부적절한 질문이 그만 들리고 말았다. 세르게이 이바노비치는 미간을 찌푸렸다.\n\n\"그건 각자가 스스로 결정할 문제야.\" 그가 엄하게 말했다.\n\n몇몇 사람이 미소를 지었다. 레빈은 얼굴이 새빨개진 채 황급히 천 밑으로 손을 밀어 넣고, 오른손에 공이 쥐여 있던 대로 그것을 오른쪽에 넣었다. 다 넣고 나서야 그는 왼손도 마저 밀어 넣었어야 했다는 것을 떠올리고, 너무 늦었지만 그래도 손을 밀어 넣었다. 그러고는 더욱더 당혹감에 사로잡혀 황급히 뒤쪽으로 물러나 버렸다.\n\n\"가입 찬성 백스물여섯 표! 반대 아흔여덟 표!\" 서기의 목소리가 노래하듯 울려 퍼졌는데, 그는 'ㄹ' 발음을 제대로 하지 못했다. 그러자 웃음이 터졌다. 상자 안에서 단추 하나와 호두 두 알이 나온 것이다. 그 귀족에게는 투표권이 인정되었고, 새로운 일파가 승리를 거두었다.\n\n하지만 옛 일파는 자기들이 졌다고 여기지 않았다. 레빈은 그들이 스네트코프에게 출마를 청하는 소리를 들었고, 한 무리의 귀족들이 무언가 말하고 있는 회장을 에워싸고 있는 것을 보았다. 레빈은 가까이 다가갔다. 거기에 대답하며 스네트코프는, 도의 귀족들이 자기에게 보내 준 신뢰며 자기로서는 받을 자격이 없는 애정에 관해 이야기했다. 자기의 유일한 공이라면 십이 년을 봉직해 온 귀족 사회에 대한 헌신뿐이었노라고 말했다. 그는 몇 번이고 같은 말을 되풀이했다. \"저는 진실과 성심을 다해 제 힘이 닿는 한 봉직해 왔습니다. 여러분의 후의를 소중히 여기며 감사드립니다.\" 그러다 별안간 복받쳐 오르는 눈물에 말문이 막혀 방을 나가 버렸다. 그 눈물이 자기에게 가해진 부당함에 대한 느낌에서 나온 것인지, 귀족 사회에 대한 사랑에서 나온 것인지, 아니면 사방이 적들에게 둘러싸였다고 느끼는 자신의 처지가 주는 긴장에서 나온 것인지는 알 수 없었으나, 그의 감정은 회의장 전체로 번져 갔고, 대다수가 마음이 움직였으며, 레빈도 스네트코프에게 애틋한 정을 느꼈다.\n\n문간에서 도 귀족 회장이 레빈과 부딪쳤다.\n\n\"이거 죄송합니다, 실례했습니다.\" 그는 모르는 사람을 대하듯 말했으나, 레빈을 알아보고는 수줍게 미소 지었다. 레빈에게는 그가 무언가 말하고 싶어 하지만 감정에 북받쳐 말을 못 하는 것처럼 보였다. 십자 훈장을 단 제복에 가두리 장식이 달린 흰 바지 차림으로 황급히 걸어가는 그의 얼굴과 그 모습 전체가, 레빈에게는 자기가 궁지에 몰린 것을 아는 어떤 쫓기는 짐승을 떠올리게 했다. 회장의 얼굴에 떠오른 이 표정이 레빈에게 유난히 가슴 아프게 다가온 것은, 바로 그 전날 후견 업무 때문에 그의 집을 찾아갔다가 위풍당당한 그의 모습, 마음씨 곱고 아버지 같은 그 사람을 보았기 때문이었다. 오래된 가구가 놓인 그 큰 저택, 좀 지저분하고 멋스럽다고는 할 수 없으나 공손한 하인들—틀림없이 주인에게 끝까지 붙어 있던 늙은 집안 농노들이었다—레이스 달린 모자에 터키식 숄을 두르고, 딸이 낳은 어여쁜 손녀를 어르고 있던 뚱뚱하고 사람 좋은 아내, 학교에서 돌아와 아버지에게 인사하며 그 큼직한 손에 입을 맞추던 어린 아들, 고등학교 육학년생, 그리고 노인의 진실하고 다정한 말과 몸짓—이 모든 것이 그 전날 레빈에게 본능적인 존경과 동정의 감정을 불러일으켰던 것이다. 이 노인은 지금 레빈에게 가슴 아프고 애처로운 존재였고, 그는 그에게 무언가 기분 좋은 말을 해 주고 싶었다.\n\n\"그러니까 어르신은 틀림없이 다시 우리 회장이 되실 겁니다.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"그럴 것 같진 않소.\" 회장이 겁먹은 표정으로 주위를 둘러보며 말했다. \"나는 지쳤소, 늙었지. 나보다 더 젊고 더 자격 있는 사람들이 있다면, 그들이 봉직하게 합시다.\"\n\n그러고는 회장은 옆문으로 사라졌다.\n\n가장 엄숙한 순간이 다가오고 있었다. 곧바로 선거에 들어갈 참이었다. 양쪽 일파의 우두머리들은 흰 표와 검은 표를 손가락으로 헤아리고 있었다.\n\n플레로프를 둘러싼 논쟁은 새 일파에게 플레로프의 표를 안겨 주었을 뿐 아니라 시간까지 벌어 주어, 그들은 상대편의 술책 때문에 선거에 참여하지 못하게 된 귀족 셋을 데리러 사람을 보낼 수 있었다. 독한 술에 약점이 있는 귀족 둘이 스네트코프 일파에게 술에 취하게 되었고, 또 한 사람은 제복을 빼앗긴 터였다.\n\n이를 알아챈 새 일파는 플레로프에 관한 논쟁이 벌어지는 동안 서둘러 자기들 사람 몇을 썰매에 태워 보내, 옷을 빼앗긴 신사에게 옷을 입히고, 술 취한 사람 하나를 회의장으로 데려오게 했다.\n\n\"한 사람 데려왔습니다, 물을 끼얹어 놨고요.\" 그 심부름을 다녀온 지주가 스비야시스키에게 말했다. \"괜찮아요. 쓸 만할 겁니다.\"\n\n\"너무 취한 건 아니겠지, 쓰러지지 않겠어?\" 스비야시스키가 고개를 저으며 말했다.\n\n\"아닙니다, 아주 멀쩡합니다. 여기서 술만 더 안 주면 됩니다…… 무슨 일이 있어도 그 사람한테 아무것도 주지 말라고 급사한테 일러뒀습니다.\"\n\n# Chapter 29\n\n사람들이 담배를 피우고 간식을 들고 있는 좁은 방은 귀족들로 가득 차 있었다. 흥분은 점점 더 격렬해졌고, 모든 얼굴에 어떤 불안이 드러났다. 흥분이 특히 격렬했던 것은 세세한 사정을 죄다 꿰뚫고 있으며 표 하나하나까지 셈해 둔 각 파벌의 지도자들이었다. 그들은 다가올 전투를 짜는 장군들이었다. 나머지 사람들은 교전을 앞둔 졸병들처럼 싸울 채비를 갖추면서도 그 사이의 시간을 다른 데로 돌려 보내려 했다. 어떤 이들은 술집 앞에 선 채로, 또는 식탁에 앉아 간식을 들었고, 다른 이들은 긴 방을 오락가락하며 담배를 피우고 오랫동안 보지 못한 친구들과 이야기를 나누었다.\n\n레빈은 먹고 싶지도 않았고 담배도 피우지 않았다. 자기 친구들, 그러니까 세르게이 이바노비치며 스테판 아르카지치, 스비야시스키 같은 이들과 어울리고 싶지도 않았는데, 그것은 시종무관 제복을 입은 브론스키가 그들과 함께 열심히 이야기를 나누며 서 있었기 때문이었다. 레빈은 전날 회의에서 이미 그를 보았고, 인사를 나누고 싶지 않아 일부러 그를 피해 왔다. 그는 창가로 가서 앉아 무리들을 둘러보며 주위에서 오가는 말을 들었다. 그는 우울했는데, 무엇보다도 보아하니 다른 사람들은 다들 열의에 차 있고 긴장해 있으며 관심이 깊은데, 옆에는 해군 제복을 입고 입술을 우물거리는, 이도 빠진 늙은이 하나가 앉아 있을 뿐, 자기 혼자만 거기에 아무 관심도 없고 할 일도 없었기 때문이다.\n\n\"그놈은 정말 악당이야! 내가 그렇게 말했는데도 도무지 소용이 없단 말이야. 생각해 보라고! 삼 년이 지나도록 그걸 거둬들이질 못했다니까!\" 어깨가 둥글게 굽은, 키가 작은 시골 양반이 힘차게 내뱉는 소리가 들렸다. 그는 수놓은 깃 위로 포마드를 바른 머리카락을 늘어뜨리고, 이런 자리를 위해 일부러 신은 게 분명한 새 부츠를 신고 있었는데, 말을 할 때마다 굽이 기운차게 또각거렸다. 이 양반은 레빈에게 못마땅한 눈길을 던지더니 홱 등을 돌렸다.\n\n\"그래, 더러운 일이지, 그건 부정할 수 없어.\" 키 작은 양반 하나가 새된 목소리로 맞장구를 쳤다.\n\n이어서 한 무리의 시골 양반들이 뚱뚱한 장군을 둘러싼 채 황급히 레빈 가까이로 다가왔다. 이 사람들은 누가 엿듣지 못하는 데서 이야기할 자리를 찾고 있는 것이 분명했다.\n\n\"감히 내가 제 바지를 훔쳐 가게 했다고 지껄이다니! 제가 술값으로 잡혔겠지, 분명히. 빌어먹을 놈, 공작이라니 가당치도 않아! 그딴 말은 하지 않는 게 좋을 거야, 그 짐승 같은 놈!\"\n\n\"하지만 실례지만! 그자들은 법령을 내세우거든.\" 다른 무리에서 이런 말이 오갔다. \"아내가 귀족으로 등록되어 있어야 한다는 거지.\"\n\n\"오, 자네 법령 따위 엿이나 먹으라지! 나는 진심으로 말하는 거야. 우리는 다 양반들 아닌가? 의심받을 게 없는 사람들이라고.\"\n\n\"가실까요, 각하, fine champagne 한잔 어떠십니까?\"\n\n또 다른 무리는 큰 소리로 뭐라고 외쳐 대는 귀족 하나를 따라가고 있었다. 그는 취한 양반 셋 중 하나였다.\n\n\"내가 마리야 세묘노브나에게는 늘 적당한 임대료로 빌려주라고 충고했지. 그러지 않고는 결코 이문을 남길 수 없으니까.\" 듣기 좋은 목소리로 누군가 말하는 소리가 들렸다. 말하는 이는 회색 구레나룻을 기른 시골 양반으로, 옛 참모 장교의 연대 제복을 입고 있었다. 바로 레빈이 스비야시스키의 집에서 만났던 그 지주였다. 레빈은 그를 단번에 알아보았다. 지주 역시 레빈을 빤히 바라보았고, 둘은 인사를 나누었다.\n\n\"이거 만나 뵙게 되어 정말 반갑소이다! 그렇고말고요! 당신을 아주 잘 기억하고 있지요. 작년에 우리 군 귀족회장이신 니콜라이 이바노비치 댁에서 뵈었지요.\"\n\n\"그래, 당신 땅은 어떻게 돼 가고 있습니까?\" 레빈이 물었다.\n\n\"오, 여전히 그저 똑같습죠, 늘 밑지고 있지요.\" 지주는 체념한 미소를 띠며 대답했지만, 그 표정에는 으레 그래야 마땅하다는 듯한 평온함과 확신이 어려 있었다. \"그런데 어쩐 일로 우리 도(道)까지 오셨소?\" 그가 물었다. \"우리의 coup d'état에 한몫 거들러 오셨소?\" 그는 자신만만하게, 형편없는 발음으로 그 프랑스어를 발음하며 말했다. \"온 러시아가 다 여기 모였지요. 시종(侍從)들에서부터, 장관 빼고는 죄다 와 있다니까요.\" 그는 흰 바지에 궁정 제복을 입고 장군 한 사람과 나란히 걸어가는 스테판 아르카지치의 당당한 모습을 가리켰다.\n\n\"솔직히 고백하자면, 저는 이 지방 선거의 취지를 잘 이해하지 못하겠습니다.\" 레빈이 말했다.\n\n지주가 그를 바라보았다.\n\n\"아니, 이해하고 자시고 할 게 뭐 있겠소? 거기엔 아무 의미도 없어요. 그저 관성의 힘으로만 굴러가는, 다 쓰러져 가는 제도지요. 보시구려, 제복만 봐도 이게 치안판사며 법원 상임위원 따위의 모임이지, 귀족들의 모임이 아니라는 걸 알 수 있잖소.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 왜 오시는 겁니까?\" 레빈이 물었다.\n\n\"습관이지요, 그뿐이오. 게다가 인맥도 유지해야 하고 말이오. 일종의 도덕적 의무인 셈이지요. 그리고 솔직히 말하자면, 제 잇속도 있고요. 사위가 상임위원으로 나서고 싶어 하는데, 넉넉한 사람들이 아니라서 밀어 줘야 하거든요. 그건 그렇고, 저런 양반들은 대체 무엇 하러 오는 걸까요?\" 그는 높은 식탁에서 떠들어 대고 있는, 그 심술궂은 양반을 가리키며 말했다.\n\n\"저들은 새로운 세대의 귀족이지요.\"\n\n\"새롭긴 새롭겠지만, 귀족은 아니지요. 일종의 자산가들이긴 해도, 우리야말로 지주(地主)란 말이오. 귀족으로서 보자면, 저들은 제 목을 제 손으로 베는 짓을 하고 있는 거요.\"\n\n\"하지만 당신은 이게 제 수명을 다한 제도라고 하시지 않습니까.\"\n\n\"그럴지도 모르지요. 하지만 그래도 좀 더 존중하며 다뤄야 마땅한 거요. 가령 스네트코프만 봐도…… 우리가 쓸모가 있을지 없을지는 모르겠지만, 우리는 천 년에 걸쳐 자라난 산물이란 말이오. 정원을 가꾼다 칩시다, 집 앞에 하나 설계한다 칩시다, 그런데 바로 그 자리에 수백 년을 버텨 온 나무가 한 그루 서 있단 말이지요…… 늙고 옹이가 졌을지언정, 화단 자리를 내겠다고 그 늙은 친구를 베어 버리지는 않잖소. 도리어 그 나무를 살리도록 화단을 배치하지요. 그런 나무는 일 년 만에 다시 길러 낼 수 없으니까.\" 그는 조심스럽게 말하고는 곧장 화제를 바꾸었다. \"그래, 당신 땅은 어떻게 돼 가고 있소?\"\n\n\"오, 그리 잘되지는 않습니다. 오 푼 정도 남기지요.\"\n\n\"그래요, 하지만 당신은 제 노동은 셈에 넣지 않는 거요. 당신 자신도 무언가 값어치가 있지 않겠소? 내 경우를 얘기해 드리리다. 내가 땅을 직접 돌보기 시작하기 전에는 관직에서 삼백 파운드의 급료를 받았소. 지금은 관직에 있을 때보다 더 많은 일을 하는데, 당신처럼 땅에서 오 푼을 얻고, 그것만으로도 하느님께 감사하지요. 하지만 제 노동은 거저 던져 넣는 셈이오.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 그게 분명한 손해인데도 왜 하시는 겁니까?\"\n\n\"오, 글쎄, 그냥 하는 거지요! 그럼 어쩌겠소? 습관이고, 또 마땅히 그래야 하는 거라는 걸 아니까. 게다가 말이오,\" 지주는 창틀에 팔꿈치를 괴고 이야기를 이어 갔다. \"내 아들놈은, 이 말은 해 둬야겠는데, 그런 일에 도무지 취미가 없소. 그놈은 영락없이 학자가 될 거요. 그러니 이걸 이어 갈 사람이 없는 셈이지요. 그런데도 하는 거요. 올해만 해도 여기에 과수원을 하나 심었다오.\"\n\n\"그래요, 그래요.\" 레빈이 말했다. \"그건 정말 옳은 말씀입니다. 저도 땅에서 하는 제 일에 진정한 이득의 균형 같은 건 없다고 늘 느끼는데, 그런데도 하게 되지요…… 땅에 대해 느끼는 일종의 의무 같은 거랄까요.\"\n\n\"하지만 내 한마디 하리다.\" 지주가 말을 이었다. \"우리 이웃 중에 장사꾼이 하나 있는데, 그자가 우리 집에 왔었소. 우리는 들판이며 정원을 둘러보았지요. '아니요,' 그자가 그러더군. '스테판 바실리예비치, 다른 건 죄다 잘 건사해 두셨는데, 정원은 방치돼 있네요.' 하지만 사실 정원도 잘 관리돼 있었소. '제 생각에는, 저 보리수를 베어 버리겠어요. 여기 보리수가 수천 그루나 있는데, 한 그루당 좋은 껍질 두 다발은 나오겠어요. 게다가 요즘 그 껍질이 값이 좀 나가거든요. 저라면 죄다 베어 버리겠습니다.'\"\n\n\"그러고는 그걸로 번 돈으로 가축을 늘리거나, 헐값에 땅을 좀 사들여서, 그걸 잘게 쪼개 농부들에게 빌려주겠지요.\" 레빈이 미소 지으며 덧붙였다. 그는 그런 상업적 계산을 한두 번 접해 본 게 아닌 듯했다. \"그래서 한밑천 잡는 거지요. 하지만 당신이나 나나 우리가 가진 걸 지켜서 자식들에게 물려줄 수만 있어도 하느님께 감사해야 할 처지란 말입니다.\"\n\n\"결혼하셨다고 들었는데요?\" 지주가 말했다.\n\n\"네.\" 레빈이 자랑스러운 만족감을 담아 대답했다. \"네, 좀 이상하지요.\" 그가 말을 이었다. \"그러니까 우리는 아무것도 벌어들이지 못하면서 사는 셈이오, 마치 불씨를 지키도록 세워진 옛 베스타 여사제들처럼 말입니다.\"\n\n지주는 흰 콧수염 아래로 빙긋 웃었다.\n\n\"우리 중에도 그런 사람들이 더러 있지요. 우리 친구 니콜라이 이바노비치라든가, 근래에 이곳에 정착한 브론스키 백작 같은 이들 말이오. 그자들은 마치 공장이라도 운영하듯 농사를 꾸려 가려 하지만, 지금까지는 거기에 자본을 탕진하는 것 말고는 아무것도 이루지 못했다오.\"\n\n\"그런데 어째서 우리는 장사꾼들처럼 하지 않는 걸까요? 어째서 우리는 목재로 쓰려고 우리 정원을 베어 버리지 않는 걸까요?\" 자기 머릿속을 스쳤던 생각으로 되돌아가며 레빈이 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 당신이 말했듯이, 불씨를 지키기 위해서지요. 게다가 그건 귀족이 할 일이 아니오. 그리고 귀족으로서 우리가 할 일은 여기 선거에서 하는 게 아니라, 저마다 제 구석에서 하는 거란 말이오. 무엇을 해야 하고 무엇을 해서는 안 되는지에 대한 일종의 계급적 본능 같은 것도 있고요. 농부들도 그렇소, 나는 가끔 그들을 보면 놀라워요. 변변한 농부라면 누구나 가질 수 있는 땅은 죄다 가지려 하지요. 땅이 아무리 나빠도 그걸 부쳐 먹는단 말이오. 게다가 아무 소득도 없이. 그저 손해를 보면서도 말이지요.\"\n\n\"우리가 하는 것과 꼭 같군요.\" 레빈이 말했다. \"정말, 정말 만나 뵈어서 반가웠습니다.\" 그는 스비야시스키가 자기 쪽으로 다가오는 것을 보고 이렇게 덧붙였다.\n\n\"우리는 당신 댁에서 만난 이후로 여기서 처음 만난 거라오.\" 지주가 스비야시스키에게 말했다. \"그래서 이야기도 실컷 나눴소이다.\"\n\n\"그래, 새로운 질서를 한바탕 공격하고 계셨던 겁니까?\" 스비야시스키가 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"그럴 수밖에요.\"\n\n\"속이 좀 후련해지셨나요?\"\n\n# Chapter 30\n\n스비아시스키는 레빈의 팔을 잡고 자기 친구들이 있는 쪽으로 함께 갔다.\n\n이번에는 브론스키를 피할 도리가 없었다. 그는 스테판 아르카지치와 세르게이 이바노비치와 함께 서 있었고, 레빈이 다가가자 그를 똑바로 바라보았다.\n\n\"반갑습니다! 전에 뵌 적이 있는 것 같군요…… 셰르바츠카야 공작 영애 댁에서요.\" 그가 레빈에게 손을 내밀며 말했다.\n\n\"네, 그때 만난 걸 잘 기억합니다.\" 레빈은 이렇게 말하더니, 얼굴이 새빨개진 채 곧바로 돌아서서 형과 이야기를 나누기 시작했다.\n\n브론스키는 엷은 미소를 지으며 스비아시스키와 계속 이야기를 나누었는데, 레빈과 대화를 트고 싶은 마음이 조금도 없는 게 분명했다. 하지만 레빈은 형과 이야기하면서도 자기의 무례함을 무마할 만한 말을 찾으려고 끊임없이 브론스키 쪽을 돌아보았다.\n\n\"지금 우리는 뭘 기다리는 겁니까?\" 레빈이 스비아시스키와 브론스키를 보며 물었다.\n\n\"스네트코프를요. 그가 출마를 거부할지 수락할지 결정해야 하거든요.\" 스비아시스키가 대답했다.\n\n\"그래서, 어떻게 됐답니까, 수락했답니까, 아닙니까?\"\n\n\"바로 그게 문제죠, 이쪽도 저쪽도 아니거든요.\" 브론스키가 말했다.\n\n\"그가 거부하면 그땐 누가 출마하는 겁니까?\" 레빈이 브론스키를 보며 물었다.\n\n\"하겠다는 사람이 하는 거죠.\" 스비아시스키가 말했다.\n\n\"당신이 하시는 겁니까?\" 레빈이 물었다.\n\n\"절대 제가 아닙니다.\" 스비아시스키는 당황한 기색으로, 세르게이 이바노비치 옆에 서 있는 심술궂은 신사에게 놀란 시선을 던지며 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 누구죠? 네뱌도프스키입니까?\" 레빈은 자기가 실수를 저지르고 있다는 걸 느끼며 말했다.\n\n하지만 이건 더 나빴다. 네뱌도프스키와 스비아시스키가 두 후보였던 것이다.\n\n\"저는 어떤 경우에도 절대 하지 않습니다.\" 심술궂은 신사가 대답했다.\n\n이 사람이 바로 네뱌도프스키였다. 스비아시스키가 그를 레빈에게 소개해 주었다.\n\n\"자, 당신도 이게 흥미진진하지요?\" 스테판 아르카지치가 브론스키에게 눈을 찡긋하며 말했다. \"꼭 경마 같단 말이죠. 내기를 걸어도 좋을 정도예요.\"\n\n\"그래, 무척 흥미진진해.\" 브론스키가 말했다. \"일단 일을 시작하면 끝까지 보고 싶어지는 법이지. 이건 싸움이야!\" 그는 눈살을 찌푸리며 억센 턱을 굳게 다물고 말했다.\n\n\"스비아시스키는 정말 유능한 친구야! 모든 걸 그렇게 명확히 꿰뚫어 보니 말이지.\"\n\n\"오, 그렇지!\" 브론스키가 무심하게 맞장구를 쳤다.\n\n침묵이 이어졌고, 그 사이에 브론스키는—무언가를 보긴 봐야 했기에—레빈을, 그의 발을, 그의 제복을 바라보다가 그의 얼굴을 보았고, 자기에게 꽂힌 그 침울한 눈길을 알아채고는 무슨 말이든 하려고 입을 열었다.\n\n\"시골에서 줄곧 사시는데 어째서 치안판사가 아니십니까? 치안판사 제복을 입고 계시지 않군요.\"\n\n\"치안판사란 어리석은 제도라고 생각하기 때문입니다.\" 레빈이 침울하게 대답했다. 그는 첫 대면 때의 무례함을 만회하려고 줄곧 브론스키와 대화를 틀 기회를 노리던 참이었다.\n\n\"저는 그렇게 생각하지 않습니다, 오히려 정반대지요.\" 브론스키가 조용히 놀라며 말했다.\n\n\"그건 장난감 같은 거예요.\" 레빈이 그의 말을 잘랐다. \"우리에겐 치안판사가 필요 없어요. 저는 팔 년 동안 그들과 단 한 번도 엮인 적이 없습니다. 그리고 어쩌다 엮인 일은 그들이 잘못 판결해 버렸지요. 치안판사가 우리 집에서 삼십 베르스타도 넘게 떨어져 있어요. 이 루블짜리 사건 하나 때문에 변호사를 보내야 하는데, 그 변호사 비용이 십오 루블이나 든단 말입니다.\"\n\n그러고는 어느 농부가 방앗간 주인에게서 밀가루를 훔쳤는데, 방앗간 주인이 그 일을 따지자 명예훼손으로 고소를 했다는 이야기를 늘어놓았다. 이 모든 게 전혀 쓸데없고 어리석은 이야기였고, 레빈 자신도 말하면서 그것을 느꼈다.\n\n\"오, 이 친구 정말 별난 사람이에요!\" 스테판 아르카지치가 더없이 부드러운, 편도유처럼 매끄러운 미소를 지으며 말했다. \"자, 가 봅시다. 투표를 하는 것 같으니…….\"\n\n그리고 그들은 흩어졌다.\n\n\"난 이해가 안 가.\" 동생의 서툰 처신을 지켜본 세르게이 이바노비치가 말했다. \"어떻게 사람이 그렇게 정치적 요령이라곤 완전히 없을 수 있는지 이해가 안 돼. 바로 그 점이 우리 러시아 사람들의 결점이지. 도의 귀족 단장은 우리의 적인데, 너는 그와 ami cochon이면서 그더러 출마하라고 청하지 않나. 브론스키 백작만 해도…… 난 그를 친구로 삼는 건 아니야. 그가 만찬에 초대했지만 나는 가지 않을 거야. 하지만 그는 우리 편이거든—그런데 뭐 하러 그를 적으로 만드나? 게다가 너는 네뱌도프스키한테 출마할 거냐고 묻질 않나. 그건 할 짓이 아니야.\"\n* ami cochon: 프랑스어로 '허물없는 친구', 막역한 사이.\n\n\"오, 전 그런 게 도무지 이해가 안 됩니다! 그리고 이게 다 그렇게 부질없는 짓이잖아요.\" 레빈이 침울하게 대답했다.\n\n\"너는 이게 다 부질없는 짓이라고 말하면서도, 막상 그 일에 끼어들기만 하면 엉망으로 만들어 버리잖니.\"\n\n레빈은 대답하지 않았고, 그들은 함께 큰 방으로 걸어 들어갔다.\n\n도의 귀족 단장은 막연하게나마 자기를 노리는 어떤 함정이 준비되고 있다는 것을 분위기로 감지하고 있었고, 모두가 그에게 출마를 권한 것도 아니었지만, 그래도 출마하기로 마음을 굳힌 터였다. 방 안은 온통 고요했다. 서기가 큰 소리로, 친위대 대위 미하일 스테파노비치 스네트코프에 대해 이제 도의 귀족 단장 선출 투표를 실시하겠다고 알렸다.\n\n군의 귀족 단장들이 구슬이 담긴 접시를 들고 자기들 탁자에서 높은 탁자로 걸어갔고, 선거가 시작되었다.\n\n\"오른쪽에 넣어.\" 레빈이 형과 함께 자기 군의 귀족 단장을 따라 탁자로 갔을 때, 스테판 아르카지치가 속삭였다. 하지만 레빈은 이미 그에게 설명해 준 셈법을 잊어버린 상태였고, 스테판 아르카지치가 \"오른쪽\"이라고 말한 게 틀린 게 아닐까 두려웠다. 분명 스네트코프는 적이지 않은가. 그는 다가가면서 구슬을 오른손에 쥐고 있었지만, 자기가 틀렸다고 생각하고는 바로 투표함 앞에서 왼손으로 바꿔 쥐었고, 틀림없이 구슬을 왼쪽에 넣었다. 투표함 옆에 서서 팔꿈치의 움직임만으로 각자가 구슬을 어디에 넣는지 알아내던 그 방면의 전문가는 못마땅한 듯 눈살을 찌푸렸다. 이번에는 그의 통찰력이 소용없었다.\n\n모든 것이 고요했고, 구슬을 세는 소리가 들렸다. 그러고 나서 한 사람의 목소리가 일어나 찬성과 반대의 숫자를 선포했다. 단장은 상당한 다수표로 선출되었다. 사방이 떠들썩해졌고, 사람들이 다투어 문 쪽으로 몰려갔다. 스네트코프가 들어오자 귀족들이 그를 둘러싸고 모여들어 축하의 말을 건넸다.\n\n\"자, 이제 끝난 겁니까?\" 레빈이 세르게이 이바노비치에게 물었다.\n\n\"이제 막 시작된 거죠.\" 스비아시스키가 미소를 지으며 세르게이 이바노비치를 대신해 대답했다. \"다른 후보가 단장보다 더 많은 표를 얻을 수도 있으니까요.\"\n\n레빈은 그 점을 까맣게 잊고 있었다. 이제 그는 여기에 어떤 술책 같은 것이 있다는 것만 떠올릴 수 있을 뿐, 그게 정확히 무엇인지 생각하기에는 너무 지루했다. 그는 의기소침해졌고, 이 군중 속을 벗어나고 싶었다.\n\n아무도 그에게 관심을 두지 않았고, 아무도 그를 필요로 하지 않는 듯 보였기에, 그는 슬그머니 다과가 차려진 작은 방으로 빠져나갔고, 급사들을 보자 또다시 큰 안도감을 느꼈다. 자그마한 노급사가 뭔가 좀 들라고 권했고, 레빈은 그러기로 했다. 콩을 곁들인 커틀릿을 먹고 급사들과 그들의 옛 주인들에 대해 이야기를 나눈 뒤, 레빈은 모든 것이 그렇게도 거북한 그 홀로 돌아가고 싶지 않아 회랑들을 거닐기 시작했다. 회랑에는 멋지게 차려입은 부인들이 가득했는데, 그들은 난간 너머로 몸을 내밀고 아래에서 오가는 말을 한 마디도 놓치지 않으려 애쓰고 있었다. 부인들과 함께 멋쟁이 변호사들과 안경을 쓴 김나지움 교사들과 장교들이 앉거나 서 있었다. 어디서나 사람들은 선거 이야기를, 그리고 단장이 얼마나 애를 태웠는지, 또 토론이 얼마나 훌륭했는지를 이야기하고 있었다. 한 무리에서 레빈은 형을 칭송하는 말을 들었다. 어떤 부인이 한 변호사에게 이렇게 말하고 있었다.\n\n\"코즈니셰프의 연설을 들어서 어찌나 기쁜지요! 저녁 식사를 거를 만한 가치가 있어요. 그분은 정말 탁월해요! 그 모든 게 어찌나 명료하고 또렷한지! 법정에 계신 여러분 중에는 그렇게 말하는 분이 없어요. 유일하게 마이델이 있긴 하지만, 그분도 한참 못 미친답니다.\"\n\n빈자리를 찾은 레빈은 난간 너머로 몸을 기대고 보고 듣기 시작했다.\n\n모든 귀족이 자기 군에 따라 칸막이 뒤에 구획을 나누어 앉아 있었다. 방 한가운데에는 제복을 입은 한 남자가 서서, 크고 높은 목소리로 외쳤다.\n\n\"도의 귀족 단장 후보로 참모 대위 예브게니 이바노비치 아푸흐친을 추천합니다!\" 죽은 듯한 침묵이 이어졌고, 그러더니 힘없는 노인의 목소리가 들렸다. \"사양합니다!\"\n\n\"추밀 고문관 표트르 페트로비치 볼을 추천합니다.\" 목소리가 다시 시작되었다.\n\n\"사양합니다!\" 높은 소년 같은 목소리가 대답했다.\n\n다시 시작되었고, 또다시 \"사양합니다.\" 그렇게 한 시간쯤 이어졌다. 레빈은 난간에 팔꿈치를 괸 채 보고 들었다. 처음에는 의아해하며 그게 무슨 뜻인지 알고 싶었다. 그러다 도저히 알아낼 수 없다는 확신이 들자 지루해지기 시작했다. 그러고는 모든 얼굴에서 보았던 그 온갖 흥분과 적개심을 떠올리며 서글퍼졌다. 그는 가기로 마음먹고 아래층으로 내려갔다. 회랑 입구를 지나면서 그는 지쳐 보이는 눈으로 서성거리는 풀이 죽은 김나지움 학생 하나와 마주쳤다. 계단에서는 한 쌍과 마주쳤는데—높은 굽으로 빠르게 달려 내려가는 부인과 경쾌한 검사보였다.\n\n\"늦지 않았다고 했잖습니까.\" 레빈이 부인을 지나가게 하려고 옆으로 비켜섰을 때, 검사보가 마침 이렇게 말하고 있었다.\n\n레빈은 출구로 가는 계단에 있었고, 외투 번호표를 찾으려고 막 조끼 주머니를 더듬던 참인데, 서기가 그를 따라잡았다.\n\n\"이쪽으로 오시지요, 콘스탄틴 드미트리치. 투표가 진행 중입니다.\"\n\n투표에 부쳐진 후보는, 출마 의사를 그렇게도 한사코 부인하던 그 네뱌도프스키였다. 레빈은 방문으로 다가갔다. 문은 잠겨 있었다. 서기가 노크하자 문이 열렸고, 레빈은 뛰쳐나오는 얼굴이 벌건 두 신사와 마주쳤다.\n\n\"더는 이걸 견딜 수가 없네.\" 얼굴이 벌건 한 신사가 말했다.\n\n그들 뒤로 도의 귀족 단장의 얼굴이 쑥 내밀어졌다. 그의 얼굴은 탈진과 낙담으로 끔찍해 보였다.\n\n\"아무도 내보내지 말라고 했잖나!\" 그가 문지기에게 소리쳤다.\n\n\"한 사람을 들여보낸 겁니다, 각하!\"\n\n\"맙소사!\" 도의 귀족 단장은 무거운 한숨을 내쉬며 고개를 떨군 채, 흰 바지를 입은 두 다리를 비틀거리며 방 한가운데의 높은 탁자로 걸어갔다.\n\n네뱌도프스키는 그들이 계획한 대로 더 많은 다수표를 얻었고, 새로운 도의 귀족 단장이 되었다. 많은 사람이 즐거워했고, 많은 사람이 기뻐하고 행복해했으며, 많은 사람이 황홀경에 빠졌고, 많은 사람이 역겨워하며 불행해했다. 전임 도의 귀족 단장은 감추지 못할 절망에 빠져 있었다. 네뱌도프스키가 방에서 나가자, 군중이 그를 둘러싸고 열광적으로 뒤따랐는데, 그것은 회의를 개회한 지사를 뒤따랐을 때나, 스네트코프가 선출되었을 때 그를 뒤따랐을 때와 꼭 같았다.\n\n# Chapter 31\n\n새로 선출된 귀족 회장과 승리한 당파의 많은 사람들이 그날 브론스키와 함께 만찬을 했다.\n\n브론스키가 선거에 온 것은 한편으로는 시골에서 따분했던 데다 안나에게 자신의 독립적인 권리를 보여 주고 싶었기 때문이고, 또한 군 의회 선거에서 자기를 위해 애써 준 스비야시스키에게 이번 선거에서 지지로 보답하기 위해서이기도 했지만, 무엇보다도 자기가 떠맡은 귀족이자 지주로서의 온갖 의무를 빈틈없이 수행하기 위해서였다. 그러나 그는 선거가 그토록 자기 흥미를 끌고, 그토록 격렬하게 자기를 들뜨게 하며, 자기가 이런 일에 그토록 능숙하리라고는 조금도 예상하지 못했다. 그는 도(道) 귀족 사회에서는 완전히 새로운 인물이었지만 그의 성공은 의심할 여지가 없었고, 자기가 이미 어느 정도 영향력을 얻었다는 그의 짐작은 틀리지 않았다. 이 영향력은 그의 재산과 명성에서, 재무국에 자리를 두고 카신의 번창하는 은행 이사를 맡고 있는 옛 친구 시르코프가 빌려준 시내의 멋진 저택에서, 브론스키가 시골에서 데려온 훌륭한 요리사에서, 그리고 페이지 군단 시절 학우였던—실은 그가 후원하고 보호해 주던 학우였던—지사와의 우정에서 비롯된 것이었다. 그러나 무엇보다도 그의 성공에 가장 크게 기여한 것은 누구를 대하든 솔직하고 한결같은 그의 태도였으니, 그 덕분에 대다수 귀족들은 그가 거만하리라던 기존의 평판을 아주 빠르게 뒤집었다. 그 자신도 의식하고 있었는데, à propos de bottes 그토록 악의에 찬 격분으로 엉뚱한 헛소리를 늘어놓던, 키티 시체르바츠카야와 결혼한 그 변덕스러운 신사를 빼고는, 그가 알게 된 귀족 한 사람 한 사람이 모두 그의 지지자가 되었던 것이다. 그는 자기가 네비예돕스키의 성공을 확보하기 위해 큰일을 해냈다는 것을 똑똑히 알았고, 다른 사람들도 그것을 인정했다. 그리고 이제 자기 식탁에서 네비예돕스키의 당선을 축하하며 그는 자기 후보의 성공을 거둔 흐뭇한 승리감을 맛보고 있었다. 선거 자체가 그를 어찌나 매료했던지, 만약 앞으로 삼 년 안에 결혼하는 데 성공한다면 자기도 출마해 볼까 생각하기 시작했는데, 그것은 마치 기수가 모는 말을 응원해 경주에서 이긴 뒤에 자기가 직접 말을 타고 경주에 나가고 싶어졌던 것과 마찬가지였다.\n* à propos de bottes: 프랑스어로 '뜬금없이, 까닭 없이'라는 뜻.\n\n오늘 그는 자기 기수의 성공을 축하하고 있었다. 브론스키는 식탁 상석에 앉았고, 그의 오른편에는 고위 장군인 젊은 지사가 앉았다. 다른 모든 사람에게 그는 연설로 선거를 엄숙하게 개회한 도의 최고 인물이었으며, 브론스키가 보기에 많은 사람들에게 존경심과 심지어 외경심까지 불러일으키는 사람이었다. 하지만 브론스키에게 그는 어린 카치카 마슬로프였으니—그것이 페이지 군단 시절 그의 별명이었다—그가 수줍어한다고 느낀 브론스키는 그를 mettre à son aise 하려고 애썼다. 왼편에는 젊고 고집스럽고 표독스러운 얼굴을 한 네비예돕스키가 앉았다. 그를 대하는 브론스키의 태도는 소탈하고 정중했다.\n* mettre à son aise: 프랑스어로 '편안하게 해 주다'라는 뜻.\n\n스비야시스키는 자기의 낙선을 아주 가볍게 받아넘겼다. 사실 그것은 그의 눈에는 낙선이 아니었으니, 그가 잔을 손에 들고 네비예돕스키를 향해 몸을 돌리며 직접 말했듯이, 귀족이 마땅히 따라야 할 새로운 흐름의 대표로 이보다 더 나은 사람을 찾을 수는 없었던 것이다. 그러므로 그가 말했듯이, 정직한 사람이라면 누구나 오늘의 성공 편에 서서 그것을 기뻐하고 있었다.\n\n스테판 아르카지치 역시 자기가 즐거운 시간을 보내고 모두가 만족한다는 것이 기뻤다. 이 선거 사건은 멋진 만찬을 위한 좋은 구실이 되었다. 스비야시스키는 회장의 눈물 어린 연설을 익살스럽게 흉내 내면서 네비예돕스키를 향해, 각하께서는 회계를 감사하는 데 눈물 말고 더 복잡한 방법을 골라야 할 것이라고 말했다. 또 다른 귀족은, 회장의 무도회를 위해 양말 신은 하인들을 주문해 두었는데 새 회장이 양말 신은 하인들을 두고 무도회를 열지 않는 한 이제 그들을 도로 돌려보내야 할 것이라고 우스꽝스럽게 늘어놓았다.\n\n만찬 내내 그들은 네비예돕스키를 두고 \"우리 회장\"이라느니 \"각하\"라느니 했다.\n\n이것은 신부를 \"마담\"이라고, 남편의 성을 붙여 부를 때와 똑같은 즐거움으로 하는 말이었다. 네비예돕스키는 이 호칭에 무관심한 척하는 정도가 아니라 경멸하는 척까지 했지만, 그가 더없이 기뻐하고 있으며 그들의 새로운 자유주의적 어조에 어울리지 않는 그 승리감을 드러내지 않으려고 자제하지 않으면 안 된다는 것은 누가 봐도 뻔했다.\n\n만찬이 끝난 뒤 선거 결과에 관심을 가진 사람들에게 몇 통의 전보가 발송되었다. 그리고 한껏 들뜬 기분이던 스테판 아르카지치는 다리아 알렉산드로브나에게 전보를 쳤다. \"네비예돕스키 스무 표 차로 당선. 축하 바람. 사람들에게 알릴 것.\" 그는 \"우리의 기쁨을 그들과 나눠야지.\" 하면서 소리 내어 받아쓰게 했다. 전보를 받은 다리아 알렉산드로브나는 그저 거기에 낭비된 1루블을 두고 한숨지을 뿐, 그것이 만찬 뒤의 일이라는 것을 알아챘다. 그녀는 스티바가 식사 후에 faire jouer le télégraphe 하는 버릇이 있다는 것을 알고 있었다.\n\n* faire jouer le télégraphe: 프랑스어로 '전보를 놀려 대다, 전보를 쳐 대다'라는 뜻.\n훌륭한 만찬과, 러시아 상인에게서가 아니라 외국에서 직접 들여온 포도주를 비롯한 모든 것이 더없이 품위 있고 소탈하며 유쾌했다. 스무 명쯤 되는 일행은 스비야시스키가 좀 더 적극적인 신진 자유주의자들 가운데서 골라낸 사람들로, 다들 생각이 같으면서도 동시에 똑똑하고 교양 있는 자들이었다. 그들은 또 반쯤 농담조로 새 도 귀족 회장과 지사와 은행 이사와 \"우리의 상냥한 주인\"의 건강을 위해 잔을 들었다.\n\n브론스키는 만족스러웠다. 그는 지방에서 이렇게 유쾌한 분위기를 만나게 되리라고는 결코 예상하지 못했다.\n\n만찬이 끝나갈 무렵 분위기는 더욱 활기를 띠었다. 지사는 브론스키에게, 그와 알고 지내고 싶어 안달하는 자기 아내가 준비해 온 세르비아인을 위한 자선 음악회에 와 달라고 청했다.\n\n\"무도회도 있을 거고, 우리 도의 미인도 보실 수 있을 거요. 정말이지 볼 만하지요.\"\n\n\"제 취향은 아닙니다.\" 브론스키가 대답했다. 그는 그 영어 표현이 마음에 들었다. 하지만 그는 미소 지으며 가겠다고 약속했다.\n\n그들이 식탁에서 일어나기 전, 모두가 담배를 피우고 있을 때, 브론스키의 시종이 쟁반에 편지 한 통을 받쳐 들고 그에게 다가왔다.\n\n\"보즈드비젠스코예에서 특별 심부름꾼 편으로 왔습니다.\" 그는 의미심장한 표정으로 말했다.\n\n\"놀랍군! 차장검사 스벤티츠키와 어찌나 닮았는지.\" 손님 가운데 한 사람이 시종을 두고 프랑스어로 말했고, 그러는 동안 브론스키는 얼굴을 찌푸린 채 편지를 읽었다.\n\n편지는 안나에게서 온 것이었다. 그는 편지를 읽기 전부터 그 내용을 알고 있었다. 선거가 닷새 안에 끝나리라 예상했던 그는 금요일에 돌아가겠다고 약속했었다. 오늘이 토요일이니, 그는 그 편지에 정해진 날에 돌아오지 않은 것을 책망하는 말이 담겨 있으리라는 것을 알았다. 그가 전날 저녁에 보낸 편지는 아마 아직 그녀에게 닿지 않았을 터였다.\n\n편지는 그가 예상하던 그대로였지만, 그 형식은 뜻밖이었고 특히 그의 마음에 들지 않았다. \"아니가 몹시 아파요. 의사는 폐렴일지도 모른다고 해요. 나 혼자서는 정신을 못 차리겠어요. 바르바라 공작 영애는 도움이 되기는커녕 방해만 돼요. 그저께도, 어제도 당신을 기다렸는데, 이제 당신이 어디서 무엇을 하고 있는지 알아보려고 사람을 보냅니다. 직접 가고 싶었지만, 당신이 싫어할 것을 알기에 그만두었어요. 어떻게 해야 할지 알 수 있도록 답이라도 좀 보내 줘요.\"\n\n아이가 아픈데도 그녀는 직접 올 생각을 했었다. 딸이 아픈데, 이 적대적인 어조라니.\n\n선거를 둘러싼 천진한 즐거움과, 그가 돌아가야만 하는 이 음울하고 짐스러운 사랑이 그 대조로 브론스키를 후려쳤다. 그러나 그는 가야만 했고, 그날 밤 첫 기차로 집을 향해 떠났다.\n\n# Chapter 32\n\n브론스키가 선거에 떠나기 전, 안나는 그가 집을 떠날 때마다 두 사람 사이에서 끊임없이 되풀이되는 장면이 그를 자기에게 묶어 두기는커녕 오히려 자기에게 냉담하게 만들 뿐이라고 생각했다. 그래서 마음을 다잡아 이별을 의연하게 견디기 위해 할 수 있는 모든 것을 다 하겠다고 결심했다. 그러나 자기가 떠난다는 말을 하러 왔을 때 그가 그녀를 바라본 그 차갑고 매서운 시선이 그녀에게 상처를 입혔고, 그가 출발하기도 전에 그녀의 마음의 평정은 무너지고 말았다.\n\n그가 떠난 뒤 홀로 남아, 자유에 대한 그의 권리를 드러낸 그 시선을 곱씹는 동안, 그녀는 언제나처럼 똑같은 지점에 다다랐다. 바로 자기 자신이 모욕당했다는 느낌이었다. \"그는 자기가 원할 때 원하는 곳으로 떠날 권리가 있어. 그냥 떠나는 게 아니라 나를 버려둘 권리까지. 그에게는 모든 권리가 있고, 나에게는 아무 권리도 없지. 하지만 그걸 알면서도 그렇게 해서는 안 되는 거야. 그런데 그가 뭘 했단 말이야?…… 그는 차갑고 매서운 표정으로 나를 바라봤어. 물론 그건 뭐라고 규정할 수도, 손에 잡을 수도 없는 거지. 하지만 전에는 한 번도 그런 적이 없었고, 그 시선은 많은 걸 뜻해.\" 그녀는 생각했다. \"그 시선은 무관심의 시작을 보여 주는 거야.\"\n\n그리고 비록 냉담함이 시작되고 있음을 확신하면서도, 그녀가 할 수 있는 일은 아무것도 없었고, 그와의 관계를 어떤 식으로도 바꿀 수가 없었다. 예전과 마찬가지로, 오직 사랑과 매력으로만 그를 붙잡을 수 있을 뿐이었다. 그래서 예전과 마찬가지로, 낮에는 분주한 일거리로, 밤에는 모르핀으로, 그가 자기를 더 이상 사랑하지 않게 된다면 어떻게 될까 하는 무서운 생각을 억누를 수밖에 없었다. 사실 한 가지 수단이 더 남아 있긴 했다. 그를 붙잡아 두기 위한 것은 아니었다. 그것을 위해서라면 그녀는 그의 사랑 외에는 아무것도 바라지 않았으니까. 그것은 그에게 더 가까이 다가가, 그가 그녀를 떠날 수 없는 그런 처지에 놓이는 것이었다. 그 수단이란 이혼과 결혼이었다. 그래서 그녀는 그것을 갈망하기 시작했고, 그나 스티바가 그 문제를 처음 꺼내는 순간 거기에 동의하기로 마음을 굳혔다.\n\n그런 생각에 몰두한 채, 그녀는 그가 선거에 가 있어야 할 닷새 동안 그 없이 닷새를 보냈다.\n\n산책, 바르바라 공작 영애와의 대화, 병원 방문, 그리고 무엇보다도 독서, 그러니까 책을 한 권씩 차례차례 읽어 나가는 것이 그녀의 시간을 채웠다. 그러나 엿새째 되던 날, 마부가 그 없이 돌아오자, 그녀는 이제 그에 대한 생각과 그가 그곳에서 무엇을 하고 있을까 하는 생각을 도무지 억누를 수 없음을 느꼈다. 바로 그때 어린 딸이 병이 났다. 안나는 아이를 돌보기 시작했지만, 그것조차 그녀의 마음을 다른 데로 돌려놓지 못했다. 더욱이 병이 심각하지도 않았다. 아무리 애를 써도 그녀는 이 어린아이를 사랑할 수가 없었고, 사랑하는 척하는 것은 그녀의 힘으로는 할 수 없는 일이었다. 그날 저녁 무렵, 여전히 혼자였던 안나는 그에 대한 걱정으로 어찌나 안절부절못했던지 도시로 떠나기로 결심했다. 그러나 다시 생각을 고쳐먹고는, 브론스키가 받게 된 그 모순된 편지를 써서 끝까지 읽어 보지도 않은 채 특별한 심부름꾼을 시켜 부쳐 버렸다. 이튿날 아침 그녀는 그의 편지를 받았고 자기가 보낸 편지를 후회했다. 그녀는 헤어질 때 그가 그녀에게 던진 그 매서운 눈길이 되풀이될까 봐, 특히 아이가 위중한 병이 아니라는 걸 그가 알았을 때 그럴까 봐 두려웠다. 그래도 그녀는 그에게 편지를 쓴 것이 기뻤다. 이 순간 안나는 자기가 그에게 짐스러운 존재라는 것을, 그가 그녀에게 돌아오기 위해 마지못해 자기 자유를 내려놓으리라는 것을 분명히 인정하면서도, 그럼에도 그가 오고 있다는 사실이 기뻤다. 그가 그녀에게 싫증을 내든 말든, 그는 여기 그녀 곁에 있을 것이고, 그러면 그녀는 그를 볼 수 있고, 그가 하는 행동 하나하나를 알 수 있을 테니까.\n\n그녀는 응접실 램프 옆에 앉아 텐의 새 책을 펼쳐 들고 있었다. 책을 읽으면서도 바깥에서 부는 바람 소리에 귀를 기울이며, 매 순간 마차가 도착하기를 기다렸다. 몇 번이나 바퀴 소리가 들리는 것 같았지만, 그때마다 착각이었다. 마침내 그녀는 바퀴 소리가 아니라 마부의 고함 소리와 지붕 덮인 현관 진입로에서 울리는 둔중한 소리를 들었다. 페이션스 카드놀이를 하고 있던 바르바라 공작 영애조차 그것을 확인해 주었고, 안나는 얼굴이 화끈 달아오른 채 일어섰다. 그러나 앞서 두 번 그랬던 것처럼 아래로 내려가는 대신, 그녀는 그 자리에 멈춰 섰다. 그녀는 갑자기 자기가 속임수를 쓴 것이 부끄러워졌지만, 그보다 더, 그가 자기를 어떻게 맞이할까가 두려웠다. 상처받은 자존심의 감정은 이제 모두 사라져 버렸다. 다만 그의 불쾌한 표정이 두려울 뿐이었다. 그녀는 아이가 지난 이틀 동안 완전히 다시 건강해졌다는 것을 떠올렸다. 자기가 편지를 부친 바로 그 순간부터 아이가 좋아진 것에 대해 그녀는 정말로 짜증이 났다. 그러다 그가 떠올랐다. 그가 여기 있다는 것, 그의 손도, 그의 눈도, 그의 모든 것이 여기 있다는 것이. 그녀는 그의 목소리를 들었다. 그리고 모든 것을 잊은 채, 그녀는 기쁨에 차서 그를 맞으러 달려갔다.\n\n\"그래, 아니는 좀 어때?\" 그가 아래에서 머뭇거리며, 자기에게 달려 내려오는 안나를 올려다보며 말했다.\n\n그는 의자에 앉아 있었고, 하인 하나가 그의 따뜻한 덧신을 벗기고 있었다.\n\n\"아, 그 애는 좋아졌어요.\"\n\n\"당신은?\" 그가 몸을 한 번 부르르 떨며 말했다.\n\n그녀는 두 손으로 그의 손을 잡아 자기 허리께로 끌어당기며, 그에게서 한순간도 눈을 떼지 않았다.\n\n\"음, 다행이군.\" 그가 그녀를, 그녀의 머리를, 그를 위해 차려입은 줄 그가 아는 그녀의 옷차림을 차갑게 훑어보며 말했다. 모든 것이 매혹적이었지만, 그를 매혹시킨 게 어디 한두 번이었던가! 그리고 그녀가 그토록 두려워하던 그 엄하고 돌처럼 굳은 표정이 그의 얼굴에 자리 잡았다.\n\n\"음, 다행이야. 그래, 당신은 잘 지냈고?\" 그가 손수건으로 축축한 턱수염을 닦고 그녀의 손에 입을 맞추며 말했다.\n\n\"상관없어.\" 그녀는 생각했다. \"그저 그가 여기 있게만 해 줘. 그가 여기 있는 한 그는 나를 사랑하지 않을 수가 없어, 감히 그러지 못해.\"\n\n저녁은 바르바라 공작 영애가 함께한 가운데 행복하고 즐겁게 흘러갔다. 공작 영애는 그가 없는 동안 안나가 모르핀을 복용해 왔다고 그에게 일러바쳤다.\n\n\"어쩌겠어요? 잠을 잘 수가 없었는걸요…… 온갖 생각이 잠을 방해했어요. 그가 여기 있을 때는 절대 먹지 않아요, 거의 한 번도요.\"\n\n그는 그녀에게 선거 이야기를 들려주었고, 안나는 능숙한 질문으로 그를 그가 가장 기뻐하는 화제, 곧 그 자신의 성공으로 이끌어 가는 법을 알았다. 그녀는 집에서 그가 관심을 가질 만한 모든 것을 그에게 이야기해 주었다. 그리고 그녀가 들려준 이야기는 하나같이 더없이 명랑한 내용이었다.\n\n그러나 저녁이 깊어 두 사람만 남게 되었을 때, 안나는 자기가 그를 완전히 다시 손에 넣었음을 깨닫고는, 편지 때문에 그가 자기에게 던진 그 시선의 괴로운 인상을 지워 버리고 싶었다. 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"솔직히 말해 줘요. 당신은 내 편지를 받고 짜증이 났고, 나를 믿지 않았죠?\"\n\n그 말을 내뱉자마자, 그녀는 그의 감정이 아무리 따뜻하다 해도 그가 그 일에 대해서만큼은 자기를 용서하지 않았음을 느꼈다.\n\n\"그래.\" 그가 말했다. \"편지가 너무 이상했거든. 처음엔 아니가 아프다더니, 그다음엔 당신이 직접 오려고 했다니.\"\n\n\"전부 사실이었어요.\"\n\n\"아, 그걸 의심하는 건 아니야.\"\n\n\"아니, 의심하잖아요. 짜증이 났군요, 보여요.\"\n\n\"단 한순간도 그렇지 않아. 다만 짜증이 나는 건, 그건 사실이야, 당신이 어쩐지 의무라는 게 있다는 걸 인정하려 들지 않는 것 같아서……\"\n\n\"음악회에 갈 의무 말이죠……\"\n\n\"하지만 그 얘긴 하지 말자고.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"왜 얘기하면 안 되죠?\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"난 그저 정말로 중요한 일이 생길 수도 있다는 걸 말하려던 거야. 가령 지금만 해도, 난 집 문제를 처리하러 모스크바에 가야 해…… 아, 안나, 왜 이렇게 신경이 곤두서 있어? 내가 당신 없이는 살 수 없다는 걸 몰라?\"\n\n\"그렇다면,\" 안나가 별안간 목소리를 바꾸며 말했다. \"그건 당신이 이 생활에 진저리가 났다는 뜻이에요…… 그래요, 당신은 하루쯤 왔다가 떠나 버리겠죠, 남자들이 흔히 그러듯이……\"\n\n\"안나, 그건 잔인한 말이야. 난 내 인생 전부를 내던질 각오가 되어 있어.\"\n\n그러나 그녀는 그의 말을 듣고 있지 않았다.\n\n\"당신이 모스크바에 간다면, 나도 갈 거예요. 난 여기 남아 있지 않겠어요. 우린 헤어지거나, 아니면 함께 살아야 해요.\"\n\n\"아니, 알다시피 그게 내 유일한 바람이잖아. 하지만 그러려면……\"\n\n\"우린 이혼을 해야 해요. 내가 그에게 편지를 쓰겠어요. 이대로는 계속 살아갈 수 없다는 걸 알겠어요…… 하지만 난 당신과 함께 모스크바로 가겠어요.\"\n\n\"당신은 마치 나를 협박하듯 말하는군. 하지만 난 당신과 결코 떨어지지 않는 것만큼 간절히 바라는 게 없어.\" 브론스키가 미소를 지으며 말했다.\n\n그러나 이 말을 하는 동안, 그의 눈에서는 단순히 차가운 빛만이 아니라, 박해받아 잔인해진 사람의 앙심에 찬 빛이 번뜩였다.\n\n그녀는 그 눈빛을 보았고, 그 의미를 정확히 알아차렸다.\n\n\"그렇다면 그건 재앙이야!\" 그 시선은 그녀에게 그렇게 말하고 있었다. 그것은 한순간의 인상이었지만, 그녀는 그것을 결코 잊지 못했다.\n\n안나는 남편에게 이혼에 관해 묻는 편지를 썼고, 11월 말 무렵 페테르부르크로 가고 싶어 하던 바르바라 공작 영애와 작별한 뒤, 브론스키와 함께 모스크바로 갔다. 날마다 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 답장을, 그리고 그 뒤에 이어질 이혼을 기다리며, 두 사람은 이제 결혼한 부부처럼 함께 살림을 차렸다.\n\n# PART SEVEN Chapter 1\n\n레빈 부부는 모스크바에 온 지 석 달째였다. 이런 일에 정통한 사람들의 가장 믿을 만한 계산에 따르면 키티가 해산했어야 할 날짜는 이미 한참 지나 있었다. 그러나 그녀는 여전히 멀쩡히 돌아다녔고, 두 달 전보다 그 시기가 조금이라도 가까워졌다는 기미는 어디에도 없었다. 의사도, 산파도, 돌리와 그녀의 어머니도, 그리고 무엇보다도 다가오는 그 일을 두려움 없이는 생각조차 할 수 없는 레빈도 점점 초조해하고 불안해하기 시작했다. 완전히 침착하고 행복한 사람은 오직 키티 하나뿐이었다.\n\n이제 그녀는 장차 태어날 아이에 대한 사랑이라는 새로운 감정이 자기 안에서 싹튼 것을 또렷이 의식하고 있었다. 그 아이는 어느 정도는 이미 실제로 존재하는 것이나 다름없었고, 그녀는 더없이 행복한 마음으로 이 감정에 잠겨 있었다. 이제 아이는 그녀의 일부만은 아니어서, 때로는 그녀와 상관없이 제 나름의 삶을 살았다. 종종 이 별개의 존재가 그녀에게 고통을 주었지만, 동시에 그녀는 낯설고도 새로운 기쁨에 웃음을 터뜨리고 싶어졌다.\n\n그녀가 사랑하는 사람들이 모두 그녀 곁에 있었고, 다들 그녀에게 더없이 다정하고, 더없이 세심하게 보살펴 주었으며, 그녀에게 주어지는 모든 것이 너무도 흐뭇해서, 이 모든 것이 곧 끝나리라는 것을 알지도 느끼지도 못했더라면 이보다 더 낫고 더 즐거운 삶은 바라지도 못했을 정도였다. 이런 생활의 매력을 망치는 것이 딱 하나 있었으니, 그것은 그녀가 사랑하는 모습 그대로의, 그러니까 시골에서의 그 모습 그대로의 남편이 여기에는 없다는 점이었다.\n\n그녀는 시골에서의 그의 평온하고 다정하며 손님을 반기는 태도가 좋았다. 도시에서 그는 끊임없이 불안해하고 경계하는 듯했는데, 누군가가 자기에게, 더더욱 그녀에게 무례하게 굴까 봐 두려워하는 사람 같았다. 시골 집에서는 자기가 분명히 제자리에 있다는 것을 알기에 그는 결코 어디론가 서둘러 떠나려 하지 않았다. 그는 한순간도 빈둥거리는 법이 없었다. 그런데 이 도시에서는 마치 무언가를 놓칠까 봐 겁이라도 내는 사람처럼 끊임없이 부산을 떨었지만, 정작 할 일이라곤 아무것도 없었다. 그래서 그녀는 그가 가여웠다. 그녀는 다른 사람들 눈에는 그가 동정의 대상으로 보이지 않는다는 것을 알았다. 오히려 키티가 사교계에서, 사랑하는 사람을 이따금 그렇게 바라보듯이, 그를 낯선 사람처럼 보려 애쓰며—그가 남들에게 줄 인상을 포착하려고—바라보면, 그가 결코 가여운 모습과는 거리가 멀다는 것을, 훌륭한 교양과 여성을 대하는 다소 구식의 절제된 정중함, 다부진 체격, 그리고 그녀가 보기에 인상적이고 표정이 풍부한 얼굴로 무척 매력적이라는 것을, 질투 어린 두려움마저 느낄 만큼 깜짝 놀라며 깨닫곤 했다. 그러나 그녀는 그를 바깥에서가 아니라 안에서 보았다. 그녀는 여기에서 그가 그 자신이 아니라는 것을 보았고, 그것이 그의 상태를 스스로에게 설명할 수 있는 유일한 방식이었다. 때로 그녀는 그가 도시에서 살지 못하는 것을 마음속으로 나무랐고, 때로는 이곳에서 그가 만족할 만하게 자기 삶을 꾸려 나가기란 정말로 어려운 일이라는 것을 인정했다.\n\n대체 그가 여기서 무엇을 할 수 있었겠는가? 그는 카드놀이를 좋아하지 않았고, 클럽에도 가지 않았다. 오블론스키 같은 부류의 유쾌한 신사들과 시간을 보낸다는 것이—이제 그녀는 그게 무슨 뜻인지 알았다…… 그것은 술을 마시고, 마신 뒤에 또 어디론가 가는 것을 뜻했다. 그런 자리에서 남자들이 어디로 가는지를 그녀는 끔찍한 마음 없이는 생각할 수도 없었다. 그렇다면 사교계에 나가야 할까? 그러나 그녀는 그가 거기서 만족을 찾을 수 있는 길이라곤 젊은 여자들과 어울리는 것을 즐기는 경우뿐이라는 것을 알았고, 그것은 그녀가 바랄 수 없는 일이었다. 그렇다면 집에서 그녀와 그녀의 어머니, 자매들과 함께 있어야 할까? 그러나 늘 똑같은 화제를 두고 끝없이 이어지는 그들의 대화를—노공작이 자매들의 수다를 두고 \"알린나딘\"이라 부르던 그것을—그녀 자신은 아무리 좋아하고 즐긴다 해도, 그것이 그를 지루하게 하리라는 것을 알았다. 그렇다면 그에게 남은 일이 무엇이었겠는가? 자기 책을 계속 써 나가는 것은 실제로 시도해 본 일이었고, 처음에는 도서관에 가서 발췌를 하고 책에 필요한 참고 자료를 찾아보곤 했다. 그러나 그가 그녀에게 말했듯이, 아무것도 하지 않으면 않을수록 무언가를 할 시간이 더 없어졌다. 게다가 그는 여기서 자기 책에 대해 너무 많이 떠들어 댄 탓에, 그것에 관한 자기 생각이 죄다 뒤죽박죽이 되고 흥미마저 잃어버렸다고 푸념했다.\n\n이 도시 생활의 한 가지 이점이라면, 여기 도시에서는 두 사람 사이에 다툼이 좀처럼 일어나지 않는다는 것이었다. 처지가 달라진 탓인지, 아니면 그 점에서 둘 다 더 조심스럽고 분별 있게 된 탓인지, 그들은 시골에서 이사 올 때 그토록 두려워했던 질투로 인한 다툼을 모스크바에서는 한 번도 벌이지 않았다.\n\n다만 그런 관점에서 두 사람에게 무척 중요한 사건이 하나 실제로 일어나기는 했다. 그것은 키티가 브론스키와 마주친 일이었다.\n\n키티의 대모인 노공작 부인 마리야 보리소브나는 늘 그녀를 무척 아꼈는데, 한사코 그녀를 보아야겠다고 우겼다. 키티는 몸 상태 때문에 사교계에는 전혀 나가지 않았지만, 아버지와 함께 그 존경스러운 노부인을 찾아갔고, 거기서 브론스키를 만났다.\n\n이 만남에서 키티가 자책할 만한 것이 딱 하나 있었다면, 사복 차림 속에서 한때 그토록 익숙했던 그의 이목구비를 알아본 그 순간 숨이 막히고, 피가 심장으로 쏠리며, 선명한 홍조가—그녀 자신도 느꼈다—얼굴에 번졌다는 점이었다. 그러나 그것은 단 몇 초간 이어졌을 뿐이었다. 일부러 큰 소리로 브론스키에게 말을 걸기 시작한 아버지의 말이 끝나기도 전에, 그녀는 마리야 보리소브나 공작 부인에게 말하는 그대로 브론스키를 바라보고, 필요하다면 말을 건넬 만반의 준비가 완전히 되어 있었으며, 그뿐 아니라 더없이 미세한 억양과 미소까지도, 그 순간 자기 곁에 보이지 않게 함께 있는 듯 느껴지는 남편의 인정을 받을 만한 방식으로 그렇게 할 수 있었다.\n\n그녀는 그에게 몇 마디를 건넸고, 그가 선거를 두고 \"우리 의회\"라고 부르며 한 농담에도 평온하게 미소까지 지어 보였다. (그 농담을 알아들었다는 것을 보이려면 미소를 지어야 했다.) 그러나 그녀는 곧바로 마리야 보리소브나 공작 부인 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸고, 그가 일어나 가려 할 때까지 단 한 번도 그를 쳐다보지 않았다. 그제야 그를 바라보았지만, 그것은 분명 작별 인사를 하는 사람을 쳐다보지 않는 것이 무례한 일이기 때문일 뿐이었다.\n\n그녀는 아버지가 브론스키와 마주친 일에 대해 자기에게 아무 말도 하지 않은 것이 고마웠다. 그러나 방문을 마친 뒤 평소처럼 산책하는 동안 그녀를 대하는 아버지의 각별한 다정함을 보고, 아버지가 그녀를 흐뭇해한다는 것을 알았다. 그녀는 스스로가 만족스러웠다. 브론스키에 대한 옛 감정의 온갖 기억을 마음 밑바닥 어딘가에 고스란히 간직한 채로도, 그저 겉으로만 그런 척하는 것이 아니라 정말로 그에게 완전히 무심하고 침착할 수 있는 힘이 자기에게 있으리라고는 미처 기대하지 못했던 것이다.\n\n마리야 보리소브나 공작 부인 댁에서 브론스키를 만났다는 이야기를 그녀가 했을 때, 레빈은 그녀보다 훨씬 더 새빨개졌다. 이 이야기를 그에게 꺼내는 것 자체가 그녀에게는 무척 힘들었지만, 만남의 세세한 정황을 계속 이야기하기란 더더욱 힘들었다. 그가 그녀에게 물어보지도 않고 그저 미간을 찌푸린 채 그녀를 빤히 바라보기만 했기 때문이다.\n\n\"당신이 그 자리에 없었던 게 정말 아쉬워요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"당신이 그 방에 없었다는 게 아니라…… 당신이 있었으면 난 그렇게 자연스럽게 굴지 못했을 거예요…… 지금 난 그때보다 훨씬, 정말 훨씬 더 새빨개지고 있는걸요.\" 그녀는 눈에 눈물이 맺힐 만큼 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다. \"그게 아니라, 당신이 문틈으로라도 그 모습을 볼 수 없었던 게 아쉽다는 거예요.\"\n\n그 진실한 눈빛은 그녀가 스스로 만족하고 있다는 것을 레빈에게 말해 주었고, 그녀가 얼굴을 붉히는데도 그는 금세 안심하여 그녀에게 이것저것 묻기 시작했다. 그것이 바로 그녀가 바라던 전부였다. 첫 순간에는 그녀도 얼굴을 붉히지 않을 수 없었지만 그 뒤로는 어쩌다 알게 된 그 누구를 대할 때와 마찬가지로 솔직하고 편안했다는 세세한 대목까지 모두 듣고 나자, 레빈은 다시 무척 행복해져서 그것이 기쁘다고, 이제는 선거 때처럼 어리석게 굴지 않고 다음번에 브론스키를 만나면 되도록 다정하게 대하도록 애쓰겠다고 말했다.\n\n\"만나기 거북한, 거의 적이나 다름없는 사람이 있다고 느끼는 건 정말 비참한 일이지.\" 레빈이 말했다. \"정말, 정말 기뻐.\"\n\n# Chapter 2\n\n\"가요, 제발, 가서 볼 집안에 다녀와요.\" 키티가 남편에게 말했다. 그가 외출하기 전, 열한 시에 그녀를 보러 들어왔을 때였다. \"당신이 클럽에서 식사하는 거 알아요. 아빠가 당신 이름을 적어 두셨거든요. 그런데 오전에는 뭘 할 거예요?\"\n\n\"카타바소프한테나 가려고.\" 레빈이 대답했다.\n\n\"왜 그렇게 일찍요?\"\n\n\"그가 나를 메트로프한테 소개해 주기로 했거든. 내 연구에 대해 그와 이야기하고 싶어서. 페테르부르크에서 온 저명한 학자야.\" 레빈이 말했다.\n\n\"맞다, 당신이 그렇게 칭찬하던 게 그 사람 논문 아니었어요? 그래, 그러고 나서는요?\" 키티가 말했다.\n\n\"법원에 가야 할지도 몰라, 누이 일 때문에.\"\n\n\"그럼 음악회는요?\" 그녀가 물었다.\n\n\"나 혼자서는 거기 안 갈 거야.\"\n\n\"안 가요? 가요, 새로운 곡들이 연주된대요…… 당신이 그렇게 관심 있어 하던 것들이잖아요. 나라면 꼭 가겠어요.\"\n\n\"뭐, 어쨌든 식사 전에는 집에 올게.\" 그가 시계를 보며 말했다.\n\n\"프록코트를 입어요. 곧장 볼라 백작 부인 댁에 다녀올 수 있게요.\"\n\n\"그게 꼭 그렇게 해야 하는 일이야?\"\n\n\"아, 그럼요, 꼭 해야죠! 그분이 우리를 보러 오셨잖아요. 자, 별거 아니에요. 들어가서 앉고, 오 분쯤 날씨 이야기 하다가 일어나서 나오면 돼요.\"\n\n\"아, 당신은 믿지 못할 거야! 내가 이런 일에서 너무 멀어져서 정말이지 부끄러워질 정도라니까. 이건 정말 끔찍한 일이야! 완전히 남남인 사람이 걸어 들어가서, 앉아서, 할 일도 없이 머물며 남의 시간을 축내고 자기도 안절부절못하다가 걸어 나오는 거잖아!\"\n\n키티가 웃었다.\n\n\"아니, 결혼하기 전에는 방문 인사를 다니곤 했을 거 아니에요?\"\n\n\"그래, 다녔지. 하지만 늘 부끄러웠어. 그리고 지금은 그런 일에서 너무 멀어져서, 맙소사! 이런 방문을 하느니 차라리 이틀 내리 저녁을 굶는 게 낫겠어! 정말 부끄럽단 말이야! 나는 줄곧 그들이 짜증스러워하면서 '저 사람은 뭐 하러 온 거야?' 하고 말하는 것만 같다고.\"\n\n\"아니에요, 그렇지 않을 거예요. 내가 장담해요.\" 키티가 웃으며 그의 얼굴을 들여다보며 말했다. 그녀는 그의 손을 잡았다. \"자, 잘 다녀와요…… 제발, 가요.\"\n\n그는 아내의 손에 입을 맞추고 막 나가려던 참이었는데, 그녀가 그를 멈춰 세웠다.\n\n\"코스챠, 나한테 이제 오십 루블밖에 안 남은 거 알아요?\"\n\n\"아, 알았어, 은행에 가서 좀 찾아올게. 얼마나?\" 그가 그녀가 너무도 잘 아는 그 불만스러운 표정을 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 잠깐만요.\" 그녀가 그의 손을 잡았다. \"이 얘기 좀 해요, 나 신경 쓰여서요. 쓸데없는 데는 아무것도 안 쓰는 것 같은데, 돈은 그냥 날아가 버리는 것 같아요. 우리, 어쩐지 살림을 잘 못하나 봐요.\"\n\n\"아니, 괜찮아.\" 그가 살짝 헛기침을 하며 눈썹 아래로 그녀를 바라보며 말했다.\n\n그 헛기침을 그녀는 잘 알았다. 그것은 그녀가 아니라 자기 자신에 대한 극심한 불만의 표시였다. 그가 못마땅해한 것은 분명 돈을 그렇게 많이 쓴 게 아니라, 뭔가 잘못되고 있다는 걸 알면서도 잊고 싶었던 그 일을 새삼 떠올리게 되었다는 점이었다.\n\n\"소콜로프한테 밀을 팔고, 방앗간을 담보로 가불을 받으라고 일러 뒀어. 어쨌든 우리한테 돈은 충분할 거야.\"\n\n\"네, 하지만 전체적으로 봐서 걱정이 돼서요……\"\n\n\"아, 괜찮아, 괜찮다니까.\" 그가 되풀이했다. \"자, 잘 있어, 여보.\"\n\n\"아니에요, 나는 가끔 엄마 말을 들은 게 정말 후회돼요. 시골에 있었으면 얼마나 좋았을까요! 지금은 내가 모두를 괴롭히고 있고, 우리 돈만 낭비하고 있잖아요.\"\n\n\"전혀 그렇지 않아, 전혀. 결혼한 뒤로 단 한 번도 지금보다 형편이 더 나을 수 있었을 거라고 생각한 적 없어……\"\n\n\"정말요?\" 그녀가 그의 눈을 들여다보며 말했다.\n\n그는 아무 생각 없이, 그저 그녀를 위로하려고 그렇게 말했던 것이다. 그러나 그녀를 흘긋 보고, 그 사랑스럽고 진실한 눈이 미심쩍은 듯 자기를 향해 고정되어 있는 것을 보았을 때, 그는 온 마음을 담아 같은 말을 되풀이했다. '내가 정말이지 이 사람을 잊고 있었구나.' 그는 생각했다. 그리고 그는 자기들 앞에 놓인 것, 머지않아 닥쳐올 그 일을 떠올렸다.\n\n\"곧일까? 몸은 어때?\" 그가 그녀의 두 손을 잡으며 속삭였다.\n\n\"너무 자주 곧이려니 생각해서, 이제는 그 생각도 안 하고 아무것도 모르겠어요.\"\n\n\"무섭지는 않고?\"\n\n그녀가 경멸하듯 미소 지었다.\n\n\"조금도 안 무서워요.\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"음, 무슨 일이 생기면 나는 카타바소프 집에 있을게.\"\n\n\"아니에요, 아무 일도 안 생길 거예요. 그런 생각은 하지 마요. 나는 아빠랑 가로수 길로 산책 갈 거예요. 돌리를 만나러 갈 거고요. 식사 전에 당신을 기다릴게요. 아, 맞다! 돌리 형편이 완전히 감당이 안 되게 돼 가는 거 알아요? 온통 빚투성이에다 한 푼도 없대요. 어제 엄마랑 아르세니랑\"—이는 그녀의 언니 남편 리보프였다—\"이야기를 나눴는데, 당신을 그이와 함께 보내서 스티바한테 얘기하게 하기로 정했어요. 정말 견딜 수가 없어요. 아빠한테는 이 얘기를 할 수가 없고요…… 하지만 당신이랑 그이가……\"\n\n\"아니, 우리가 뭘 할 수 있겠어?\" 레빈이 말했다.\n\n\"어차피 아르세니 집에 갈 거잖아요. 그이랑 얘기해 봐요, 우리가 어떻게 정했는지 말해 줄 거예요.\"\n\n\"아, 아르세니가 미리 생각해 둔 거라면 뭐든 다 찬성이야. 가서 그를 만나 볼게. 그건 그렇고, 음악회에 가게 되면 나탈리아랑 같이 갈게. 자, 잘 있어.\"\n\n현관 계단에서 레빈은 그의 늙은 하인 쿠즈마에게 붙들렸다. 쿠즈마는 결혼 전부터 그의 곁에 있었고, 지금은 그들의 시내 살림을 돌보고 있었다.\n\n\"'미녀'가\"—이는 시골에서 데려온 왼쪽 끌채 말이었다—\"편자를 잘못 박아서 다리를 심하게 절뚝거립니다. 나리께서는 어떻게 하면 좋겠습니까?\" 그가 말했다.\n\n모스크바 체류 초기에 레빈은 시골에서 데려온 자기 말들을 부렸다. 그는 이 부분의 비용을 가능한 한 가장 좋고 가장 싸게 처리해 보려고 애썼다. 하지만 자기 말들이 빌린 말보다 오히려 더 비싸게 먹혔고, 그래서 결국 말도 빌려 쓰게 되었다.\n\n\"수의사를 부르게. 멍이 들었는지도 모르니까.\"\n\n\"카체리나 알렉산드로브나 마님께는 어떡할까요?\" 쿠즈마가 물었다.\n\n모스크바 이쪽 끝에서 저쪽 끝으로 가려면 묵직한 마차에 힘센 말 두 필을 매고, 눈 녹은 진창길을 삼 마일이나 달려 거기에 네 시간이나 세워 두면서 매번 오 루블씩 치러야 한다는 사실이, 이제 레빈에게는 처음만큼 충격적으로 다가오지 않았다.\n\n이제는 그것이 아주 당연하게 여겨졌다.\n\n\"마차에 매도록 삯마차업자한테서 한 쌍을 빌려 오게.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"네, 나리.\"\n\n이렇게 도시 생활의 편리함 덕분에 레빈은 시골이라면 그토록 많은 수고와 노력을 요했을 문제를 간단하고 손쉽게 처리하고는, 계단으로 나가 썰매를 불러 올라타고 니키츠키로 향했다. 가는 길에 그는 더 이상 돈 생각을 하지 않고, 자기를 기다리는 페테르부르크 학자—사회학에 관한 글을 쓰는 사람—와의 소개 자리를, 그리고 자기 책에 대해 그에게 무슨 말을 할지를 곰곰이 생각했다.\n\n모스크바 체류 첫 며칠 동안에만 레빈은 시골에 사는 사람에게는 낯선, 비생산적이면서도 어쩔 수 없는, 사방에서 그에게 기대되는 그 지출에 충격을 받았었다. 하지만 이제는 그것에 익숙해져 있었다. 술꾼들에게 일어난다는 그 일이 이 문제에서 그에게 일어난 것이다. 첫 잔은 목구멍에 걸리고, 둘째 잔은 매처럼 날아 내려가고, 셋째 잔부터는 작은 새들처럼 술술 넘어간다는 것 말이다. 하인들과 현관지기의 제복 값을 치르려고 첫 백 루블짜리 지폐를 헐었을 때, 레빈은 이 제복이 누구에게도 쓸모없는 것이라는 생각을 하지 않을 수 없었다—하지만 제복 없이 지내도 되지 않겠냐고 그가 제안했을 때 공작 부인과 키티가 보인 그 경악을 보면 그것은 의심할 여지 없이 필요한 것이었다—그리고 이 제복 값이 일꾼 둘의 여름 한철 품삯, 그러니까 부활절부터 재의 수요일까지 약 삼백 일치 노동, 그것도 매일 이른 아침부터 늦은 저녁까지 이어지는 고된 하루 노동의 대가에 맞먹는다는 생각도 들었고—그 백 루블짜리 지폐는 정말로 목구멍에 걸렸다. 하지만 친척들에게 식사를 대접하느라 헐어 쓴 다음 지폐는, 그것이 이십팔 루블이었고, 이십팔 루블이면 사람들이 끙끙 신음하고 땀 흘리며 베고 묶고 타작하고 까부르고 체질하고 뿌렸을 귀리 아홉 말에 해당한다는 생각을 레빈에게 불러일으키긴 했어도—이 지폐는 더 수월하게 떠나보냈다. 그리고 이제 그가 헐어 쓰는 지폐들은 더 이상 그런 생각을 불러일으키지 않았고, 작은 새들처럼 날아가 버렸다. 돈을 마련하는 데 들인 노동이 그 돈으로 산 것이 주는 즐거움에 걸맞은지 어떤지는, 그가 이미 오래전에 떨쳐 버린 고려 사항이었다. 어떤 곡물을 어느 가격 아래로는 팔 수 없다는 그의 사업적 계산도 잊혀 버렸다. 그가 그토록 오래 버티며 값을 받으려 했던 호밀은, 한 달 전에 받던 값보다 한 말에 오십 코페이카나 싸게 팔려 나갔다. 이런 식으로 지출하면 일 년도 빚 없이 살아갈 수 없으리라는 생각조차, 그것조차 아무런 힘이 없었다. 단 한 가지만이 필수적이었다. 그것이 어디서 나왔는지 캐묻지 않고 은행에 돈이 있어서, 내일 고기를 살 밑천이 있다는 것을 아는 것 말이다. 그리고 이 조건은 지금까지 충족되어 왔다. 그는 언제나 은행에 돈이 있었다. 하지만 이제 은행의 돈이 바닥났고, 그는 다음 몫을 어디서 구해야 할지 도무지 알 수가 없었다. 그리고 바로 이것이, 키티가 돈 이야기를 꺼냈던 그 순간에 그의 마음을 어지럽혔던 것이다. 하지만 그는 그것을 생각할 겨를이 없었다. 그는 카타바소프와, 자기 앞에 놓인 메트로프와의 만남을 생각하며 마차를 몰고 떠났다.\n\n# Chapter 3\n\n레빈은 이번 도시 방문 중에 대학 시절의 옛 친구인 카타바소프 교수를 자주 만났는데, 그는 결혼한 이후로 그를 보지 못했었다. 레빈은 카타바소프의 인생관이 지닌 명료함과 단순함을 좋아했다. 레빈은 카타바소프의 인생관이 그토록 명료한 것은 그의 천성이 빈약하기 때문이라고 생각했고, 카타바소프는 레빈의 사상이 두서없는 것은 그가 지적 단련을 게을리한 탓이라고 생각했다. 그렇지만 레빈은 카타바소프의 명료함을 즐겼고, 카타바소프는 레빈의 다듬어지지 않은 사상의 풍부함을 즐겼으며, 두 사람은 만나서 토론하기를 좋아했다.\n\n레빈은 카타바소프에게 자기 책의 몇 부분을 읽어 준 적이 있었는데, 그는 그것을 마음에 들어 했다. 전날 카타바소프는 공개 강연에서 레빈을 만나, 레빈이 그토록 좋아하던 논문을 쓴 그 유명한 메트로프가 모스크바에 와 있으며, 카타바소프가 레빈의 연구에 대해 들려준 이야기에 그가 큰 흥미를 느꼈고, 내일 열한 시에 만나러 올 것이며, 레빈과 알고 지내게 되어 무척 기쁘리라고 전했다.\n\n\"자네 정말 사람이 달라졌군, 보기 좋아.\" 카타바소프가 작은 응접실에서 레빈을 맞으며 말했다. \"초인종 소리를 듣고 생각했지. 정확한 시각에 올 리가 없는데 설마 그 친구일까!…… 자, 그래 이제 몬테네그로 사람들을 어떻게 생각하나? 타고난 전사들이지.\"\n\n\"왜, 무슨 일이 있었나?\" 레빈이 물었다.\n\n카타바소프는 몇 마디로 전쟁의 가장 최근 소식을 들려주고는, 서재로 들어가 레빈에게 키가 작고 다부진 체격에 호감 가는 인상의 남자를 소개했다. 그가 바로 메트로프였다. 대화는 잠시 정치와, 페테르부르크의 상층부에서 최근 사건들을 어떻게 바라보는가 하는 문제를 건드렸다. 메트로프는 더없이 믿을 만한 출처를 통해 자기에게 전해진 이야기, 곧 이 문제에 대해 차르와 한 장관이 했다는 말을 되풀이했다. 카타바소프 또한 아주 확실한 출처를 통해 차르가 전혀 다른 말을 했다고 들었다. 레빈은 두 가지 말이 모두 나올 수 있었을 법한 정황을 상상해 보려 했고, 그 화제에 대한 대화는 흐지부지되었다.\n\n\"그래, 이 친구가 토지와 관련한 노동자의 자연적 조건에 대해 거의 책 한 권을 써 냈다네.\" 카타바소프가 말했다. \"나야 전문가는 아니지만, 자연과학을 하는 사람으로서 이 친구가 인류를 생물학적 법칙 바깥에 있는 무언가로 다루지 않았다는 점이 마음에 들었지. 오히려 인간이 환경에 의존하고 있음을 보고, 그 의존 속에서 인간 발전의 법칙을 찾고 있더란 말일세.\"\n\n\"그거 아주 흥미롭군요.\" 메트로프가 말했다.\n\n\"제가 정확히 쓰기 시작한 건 농업에 관한 책이었습니다. 그런데 농업의 주된 도구인 노동자를 연구하다 보니,\" 레빈이 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다. \"전혀 예상치 못한 결론에 이르지 않을 수 없었지요.\"\n\n그리고 레빈은 마치 발밑을 더듬어 살피듯 조심스럽게 자기 견해를 펼치기 시작했다. 그는 메트로프가 일반적으로 받아들여지는 정치경제학 이론에 반대하는 논문을 썼다는 것을 알고 있었지만, 자기의 새로운 견해에 대한 그의 공감을 어느 정도까지 기대할 수 있을지는 알 수 없었고, 그 학자의 영리하고 평온한 얼굴에서도 짐작할 수가 없었다.\n\n\"하지만 당신은 러시아 노동자의 특수한 성격을 어디에서 보십니까?\" 메트로프가 말했다. \"이른바 생물학적 특성에서입니까, 아니면 그가 놓인 조건에서입니까?\"\n\n레빈은 이 질문 밑에 자기가 동의하지 않는 어떤 생각이 깔려 있음을 알아차렸다. 하지만 그는 러시아 노동자가 다른 사람들과는 다른, 토지에 대한 아주 특수한 관점을 지니고 있다는 자기 생각을 계속 설명해 나갔다. 그리고 이 주장을 뒷받침하기 위해, 러시아 농민의 이러한 태도는 동방의 광대한 미개척지에 사람들을 정착시키는 것이 자기 사명이라는 의식에서 비롯된 것이라는 게 자기 의견이라고 서둘러 덧붙였다.\n\n\"한 민족의 일반적인 사명을 근거로 어떤 결론을 끌어내다 보면 쉽게 오류에 빠질 수 있습니다.\" 메트로프가 레빈의 말을 가로막으며 말했다. \"노동자의 조건은 언제나 그가 토지와 자본에 대해 맺는 관계에 달려 있는 법이지요.\"\n\n그리고 메트로프는 레빈이 자기 생각을 다 설명하도록 두지 않고, 자기 이론의 특수한 요점을 그에게 펼쳐 보이기 시작했다.\n\n자기 이론의 요점이 어디에 있는지 레빈은 이해하지 못했는데, 굳이 이해하려고 애쓰지 않았기 때문이다. 그는 메트로프가 현행 정치경제학 이론을 공격한 자신의 논문에도 불구하고, 다른 사람들과 마찬가지로 러시아 농민의 처지를 그저 자본, 임금, 지대의 관점에서만 바라보고 있음을 알아차렸다. 사실 메트로프는, 러시아의 동부 지역, 그것도 훨씬 더 넓은 그 지역에서는 지대가 아직 전무하며, 팔천만 러시아 농민의 십분의 구에게 임금이란 그저 자신들을 위해 마련하는 식량의 형태를 띨 뿐이고, 자본이란 것도 아직은 가장 원시적인 도구의 형태로밖에 존재하지 않는다는 사실을 인정하지 않을 수 없었을 것이다. 그럼에도 그는 오로지 그 관점에서만 모든 노동자를 고찰했으니, 비록 여러 점에서 경제학자들과 견해를 달리하고 임금 기금에 대한 자기 나름의 이론을 가지고 있어 그것을 레빈에게 펼쳐 보이기는 했지만 말이다.\n\n레빈은 마지못해 들었고, 처음에는 이의를 제기했다. 그는 메트로프의 말을 끊고 자기 생각을 설명하고 싶었는데, 그의 생각으로는 그렇게 하면 메트로프의 이론을 더 펼쳐 보일 필요가 없어질 것 같았다. 그러나 나중에는 두 사람이 이 문제를 너무도 다르게 바라보고 있어 서로를 결코 이해할 수 없으리라는 확신이 들자, 그의 주장에 반대조차 하지 않고 그저 듣기만 했다. 메트로프가 하는 말이 이제 그에게는 도무지 흥미라곤 없었지만, 그래도 그의 말을 듣는 데서 어떤 만족을 느꼈다. 이런 학식 있는 사람이 자기에게 그토록 열성적으로, 그토록 강렬하게, 또 레빈이 이 주제를 이해하고 있다고 굳게 믿으며, 때로는 그저 넌지시 한마디 던지는 것만으로 주제의 한 측면 전체를 떠올리게 하면서 자기 생각을 설명해 준다는 사실이 그의 허영심을 우쭐하게 했다. 그는 이것을 자기 공으로 돌렸지만, 메트로프가 이미 자기 이론을 가까운 친구들 모두와 거듭거듭 논해 왔으면서도 새로 만나는 사람마다 그것을 유난히 열심히 이야기하며, 대체로 자기가 흥미를 느끼는 주제라면, 설령 아직 자기 자신에게도 분명치 않은 것일지라도, 누구에게든 기꺼이 이야기하고 싶어 한다는 것은 알지 못했다.\n\n\"그런데 우리가 늦겠는데.\" 메트로프가 강론을 마치자마자 카타바소프가 시계를 보며 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 오늘 스빈티치 기념제를 맞아 애호가 협회 모임이 있거든.\" 카타바소프가 레빈의 물음에 답하며 말했다. \"표트르 이바노비치와 나도 가려던 참이지. 내가 그의 동물학 업적에 대해 연설을 하기로 약속했어. 자네도 같이 가세, 아주 흥미로울 거야.\"\n\n\"그래요, 정말 이제 출발할 시간이군요.\" 메트로프가 말했다. \"우리와 함께 가시지요. 그리고 거기서, 원하신다면 우리 집으로 오십시오. 당신 연구를 꼭 듣고 싶습니다.\"\n\n\"오, 아닙니다! 아직 변변치 못합니다, 완성되지도 않았고요. 하지만 모임에는 기꺼이 가겠습니다.\"\n\n\"여보게들, 들었나? 그가 별도 보고서를 제출했다네.\" 다른 방에서 프록코트를 입고 있던 카타바소프가 소리쳤다.\n\n그러자 대학 문제를 둘러싼 대화가 일었는데, 이는 그해 겨울 모스크바에서 매우 중대한 사건이었다. 평의회의 노교수 셋이 젊은 교수들의 의견을 받아들이지 않았던 것이다. 젊은 쪽에서는 별도의 결의안을 등록했다. 이것이 어떤 사람들의 판단으로는 괴이한 짓이었고, 또 어떤 사람들의 판단으로는 더할 나위 없이 단순하고 정당한 처사였으며, 교수들은 두 파로 갈라졌다.\n\n카타바소프가 속한 한쪽 파는 반대편 파에게서 비열한 배신과 변절을 보았고, 반대편 파는 그들에게서 유치함과 권위에 대한 존중의 결여를 보았다. 레빈은 대학에 몸담고 있지는 않았지만, 모스크바에 머무는 동안 이미 여러 번 이 문제에 대해 듣기도 하고 이야기도 했으며, 이 주제에 대해 나름의 의견을 가지고 있었다. 그는 세 사람이 함께 옛 대학 건물로 걸어가는 동안 거리에서 이어진 대화에 끼어들었다.\n\n모임은 이미 시작되어 있었다. 천을 덮은 탁자 둘레에, 카타바소프와 메트로프가 자리를 잡았는데, 거기에는 대여섯 명이 있었고, 그중 한 사람이 원고 위로 바짝 몸을 굽힌 채 무언가를 소리 내어 읽고 있었다. 레빈은 탁자 둘레에 놓여 있던 빈 의자 중 하나에 앉아, 가까이 앉은 학생에게 무엇을 읽고 있는 거냐고 속삭이듯 물었다. 그 학생은 못마땅한 눈초리로 레빈을 흘끔 보며 말했다.\n\n\"전기요.\"\n\n레빈은 그 전기에 흥미가 없었지만, 듣지 않을 수 없었고, 그 저명한 과학자의 생애에 대한 새롭고 흥미로운 사실 몇 가지를 알게 되었다.\n\n낭독자가 끝마치자, 의장은 그에게 감사를 표한 뒤 시인 멘트가 기념제에 부쳐 보내온 시 몇 구절을 읽고, 그 시인에게 감사하는 의미로 몇 마디 말을 했다. 그러고 나서 카타바소프가 우렁차게 울리는 목소리로, 기념제의 주인공인 그 사람의 학문적 업적에 대한 연설문을 읽었다.\n\n카타바소프가 끝마치자, 레빈은 시계를 보았고, 한 시가 지났음을 알고는, 음악회 전에 메트로프에게 자기 책을 읽어 줄 시간이 없겠다고 생각했으며, 사실 이제는 그러고 싶지도 않았다. 낭독이 이어지는 동안 그는 둘이 나눈 대화를 곰곰이 되짚어 보았다. 그는 이제 분명히 알 수 있었으니, 메트로프의 생각에도 어쩌면 가치가 있을지 모르나 자기 생각에도 가치가 있으며, 두 사람의 생각은 각자가 택한 길에서 따로따로 작업할 때라야 비로소 명료해지고 어떤 결실에 이를 수 있을 뿐, 두 사람의 생각을 한데 합친다고 해서 얻을 것은 아무것도 없으리라는 것이었다. 그래서 메트로프의 초대를 거절하기로 마음먹은 레빈은, 모임이 끝날 무렵 그에게 다가갔다. 메트로프는 마침 정치 소식을 이야기하고 있던 의장에게 레빈을 소개했다. 메트로프는 의장에게 이미 레빈에게 들려준 이야기를 했고, 레빈은 그날 아침에 이미 했던 것과 똑같은 소감을 그 소식에 대해 말했지만, 변화를 주려는 마음에 방금 막 떠오른 새로운 의견 하나도 함께 내놓았다. 그 후 대화는 다시 대학 문제로 돌아갔다. 레빈은 이미 그것을 다 들은 터라, 메트로프에게 그의 초대에 응할 수 없어 유감이라고 서둘러 말하고는 작별을 고한 뒤, 마차를 타고 리보프의 집으로 향했다.\n\n# Chapter 4\n\n키티의 언니인 나탈리아의 남편 리보프는 평생을 외국 수도들에서 지냈는데, 그곳에서 교육을 받았고 외교관으로 일했다.\n\n지난 한 해 동안 그는 외교관 일을 그만두었는데, 무슨 \"불미스러운 일\" 때문은 아니었고(그는 누구와도 \"불미스러운 일\"을 겪어 본 적이 없었다), 두 아들에게 가능한 한 최고의 교육을 시키기 위해 모스크바의 궁내부로 자리를 옮겼다.\n\n두 사람의 습관이며 견해가 두드러지게 대조적이었고 리보프가 레빈보다 나이가 많다는 사실에도 불구하고, 그들은 그해 겨울 서로를 자주 만났고 서로에게 깊은 호감을 가지게 되었다.\n\n리보프는 집에 있었고, 레빈은 기별 없이 그에게 들어갔다.\n\n리보프는 허리띠를 맨 실내복을 입고 섀미 가죽 구두를 신은 채 안락의자에 앉아, 푸른 알이 끼워진 코안경을 쓰고 독서대 위에 놓인 책을 읽고 있었으며, 아름다운 손에는 반쯤 타들어 간 담배를 우아하게 몸에서 멀찍이 떼어 들고 있었다.\n\n곱슬곱슬하게 윤이 나는 은빛 머리칼이 한결 더 귀족적인 분위기를 더해 주는 그의 잘생기고 섬세하며 아직도 젊어 보이는 얼굴이, 레빈을 보자 미소로 환하게 밝아졌다.\n\n\"잘됐군! 안 그래도 자네한테 사람을 보내려던 참이었네. 키티는 좀 어떤가? 여기 앉게, 이쪽이 더 편하니까.\" 그는 일어나서 흔들의자를 끌어다 놓았다. \"Journal de St. Pétersbourg에 실린 최근 회람은 읽어 봤나? 내 보기엔 아주 훌륭하던데.\" 그는 가벼운 프랑스어 억양으로 말했다.\n\n* Journal de St. Pétersbourg: 페테르부르크에서 발행되던 프랑스어 신문.\n레빈은 카타바소프에게 들은, 페테르부르크에서 화제가 되고 있다는 이야기를 그에게 전했고, 정치에 관해 잠깐 이야기를 나눈 뒤 메트로프와의 면담과 학회 모임에 관해 말했다. 리보프에게는 그것이 무척 흥미로웠다.\n\n\"바로 그게 내가 자네를 부러워하는 점이라네. 그런 흥미로운 학술 모임에 어울릴 수 있다는 것 말일세.\" 그가 말했다. 그리고 이야기를 하면서 그는 늘 그렇듯이 자기에게 더 편한 프랑스어로 넘어갔다. \"사실 나는 그럴 시간이 없어. 관청 일에다 아이들 때문에 도무지 짬이 안 나거든. 게다가 내가 받은 교육이 너무 부실했다는 걸 인정하는 것도 부끄럽지 않네.\"\n\n\"그건 못 믿겠는데요.\" 레빈은 미소를 지으며 말했다. 그는 언제나 그렇듯이 리보프가 자기 자신을 낮게 평가하는 데에 마음이 움직였는데, 그것은 겸손해 보이거나 겸손하려는 마음에서 꾸민 것이 조금도 아니고 완전히 진심이었던 것이다.\n\n\"오, 정말 그렇다네! 지금 와서야 내가 얼마나 교육을 못 받았는지 느낀다네. 아이들을 가르치려니 정말이지 이것저것 찾아봐야 하고, 사실은 그냥 내가 직접 공부를 해야 할 지경이야. 선생을 두는 것만으로는 충분치 않고, 그들을 감독할 사람이 있어야 하거든. 마치 자네 영지에 일꾼과 함께 감독이 필요한 것처럼 말이지. 내가 뭘 읽고 있는지 보게.\" 그는 책상 위에 놓인 부슬라예프의 「문법」을 가리켰다. \"이게 미샤한테 요구되는 건데, 어찌나 어려운지…… 자, 나한테 좀 설명해 주게…… 여기서 이렇게 말하는데……\"\n\n레빈은 그것이 이해할 수 있는 게 아니라 그냥 가르쳐야 하는 거라고 설명하려 했지만, 리보프는 그의 말에 동의하려 하지 않았다.\n\n\"오, 자넨 그걸 비웃는군!\"\n\n\"천만에요. 제가 형님을 보고 있으면 늘 제 앞에 놓인 과제, 그러니까 자식 교육이라는 걸 배운다니까요. 형님은 상상도 못 하실 거예요.\"\n\n\"뭐, 자네가 배울 게 어디 있겠나.\" 리보프가 말했다.\n\n\"제가 아는 건,\" 레빈이 말했다. \"형님네 아이들보다 더 잘 자란 아이들을 본 적이 없다는 것, 그리고 형님네 아이들보다 더 나은 아이들을 바라지도 않겠다는 것이지요.\"\n\n리보프는 기쁜 빛을 드러내지 않으려고 애쓰는 게 눈에 보였지만, 그의 얼굴은 분명히 미소로 환하게 빛나고 있었다.\n\n\"그저 그 애들이 나보다 나은 사람이 되기만 하면! 그게 내가 바라는 전부라네. 자넨 아직 모를 걸세.\" 그가 말했다. \"나처럼 아이들을 외국에서 제멋대로 굴게 내버려 두었을 때 따르는 그 온갖 수고를 말이야.\"\n\n\"그건 다 따라잡으실 거예요. 그렇게 영리한 아이들인데요. 중요한 건 인격 교육이지요. 그게 바로 제가 형님네 아이들을 보면서 배우는 점이고요.\"\n\n\"인격 교육이라고 했나. 자넨 그게 얼마나 어려운지 상상도 못 할 걸세! 한 가지 성향을 겨우 다스려 놓으면 다른 것들이 불쑥불쑥 튀어나오고, 그러면 싸움이 다시 시작되거든. 종교에 의지하지 않는다면—우리가 그 얘길 나눈 거 기억하지—그 도움 없이는 어떤 아버지도 자기 힘만으로 아이들을 키울 수 없을 거야.\"\n\n레빈이 늘 흥미를 느끼던 이 화제는, 외출 채비를 마친 미인 나탈리아 알렉산드로브나가 들어오는 바람에 끊기고 말았다.\n\n\"여기 계신 줄 몰랐네요.\" 그녀가 말했는데, 하도 많이 들어서 이제는 지겨워진 화제에 관한 이 대화를 끊는 데에 아쉬움이라고는 조금도 없이 오히려 뚜렷한 즐거움을 느끼고 있는 게 분명했다. \"그래, 카체리나는 좀 어때요? 오늘은 댁에서 저녁을 먹을 거예요. 그러니까 말이죠, 아르세니,\" 그녀가 남편 쪽을 돌아보았다. \"당신이 마차를 쓰세요.\"\n\n그러고 나서 부부는 그날의 일정을 의논하기 시작했다. 남편은 공무로 누군가를 만나러 가야 했고, 아내는 음악회와 동방 문제를 다루는 어느 위원회의 공개 모임에 가야 했으므로, 따져 보고 정리해야 할 것이 많았다. 레빈은 자기 자신도 그들 중 한 사람인 양 그들의 계획에 참여해야 했다. 레빈이 나탈리아와 함께 음악회와 모임에 가고, 거기서 마차를 관청으로 보내 아르세니를 데려오게 하고, 그가 그녀를 데리러 와서 카체리나의 집으로 데려가기로, 혹시 그가 일을 다 마치지 못했으면 마차를 돌려보내고 레빈이 그녀와 함께 가기로 정해졌다.\n\n\"이 사람이 날 응석받이로 만든다니까.\" 리보프가 아내에게 말했다. \"우리 애들한테 얼마나 나쁜 데가 많은지 내가 다 아는데, 우리 애들이 훌륭하다고 자꾸 장담을 한단 말이야.\"\n\n\"아르세니는 매사에 극단으로 치닫는다니까요. 내가 늘 하는 말이지만.\" 그의 아내가 말했다. \"완벽함을 찾으려 들면 결코 만족하지 못하는 법이에요. 그리고 아빠 말씀이 맞아요. 우리가 자랄 때는 한쪽 극단이었죠. 부모님은 제일 좋은 방에서 지내시고 우리는 지하실에 처박혀 있었으니까요. 그런데 지금은 정반대예요. 부모는 세탁실에 있고 아이들이 제일 좋은 방을 차지하죠. 요즘 부모는 자기 인생을 살아서는 안 되고, 오로지 자식을 위해서만 존재해야 하나 봐요.\"\n\n\"뭐, 아이들이 그걸 더 좋아한다면 어떻겠소?\" 리보프가 그 아름다운 미소를 지으며 아내의 손을 만지고 말했다. \"당신을 모르는 사람이 보면 친엄마가 아니라 계모인 줄 알겠어.\"\n\n\"아니에요, 무엇이든 극단은 좋지 않아요.\" 나탈리아가 차분하게 말하며 식탁 위의 종이칼을 제자리에 똑바로 놓았다.\n\n\"자, 이리 오너라, 우리 완벽한 아이들.\" 리보프가 방으로 들어온 두 잘생긴 사내아이에게 말했고, 아이들은 레빈에게 인사를 한 뒤, 무언가를 아버지에게 물어보고 싶은 게 분명한 얼굴로 아버지에게 다가갔다.\n\n레빈은 아이들과 이야기를 나누고 싶었고, 그들이 아버지에게 무슨 말을 하는지 듣고 싶었지만, 나탈리아가 그에게 말을 걸기 시작했고, 그러는 사이에 리보프의 동료 관리인 마호틴이 그와 함께 누군가를 만나러 가려고 궁정 제복 차림으로 들어왔으며, 헤르체고비나며, 코르진스카야 공작 부인이며, 시 의회며, 아프락시나 부인의 갑작스러운 죽음에 관한 대화가 끊임없이 이어졌다.\n레빈은 자기에게 맡겨진 부탁까지 잊어버리고 있었다. 그는 현관으로 나가다가 그것을 떠올렸다.\n\n\"아, 카체리나가 형님께 오블론스키 얘기를 좀 해 보라더군요.\" 리보프가 계단에 서서 아내와 레빈을 배웅하고 있을 때 그가 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 그래, maman은 우리 동서들끼리 그 사람을 다그쳐 줬으면 하시지.\" 그가 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다. \"하지만 내가 왜 그래야 하나?\"\n\n* les beaux-frères: 프랑스어로 '처남·매부 사이의 동서'를 뜻함.\n\n\"그럼 좋아요, 내가 다그칠게요.\" 리보프 부인이 하얀 양가죽 망토 차림으로 두 사람이 이야기를 마칠 때까지 기다리고 서 있다가 미소를 지으며 말했다. \"자, 이제 가요.\"\n\n# Chapter 5\n\n오후 음악회에서는 무척 흥미로운 곡 두 개가 연주되었다. 하나는 환상곡 「리어 왕」이었고, 다른 하나는 바흐를 추모하여 바친 사중주였다. 둘 다 새 작품이었고 새로운 양식을 따른 것이어서, 레빈은 그것들에 대한 자기 나름의 견해를 세우고 싶었다. 처제를 그녀의 자리로 안내한 뒤, 그는 기둥에 기대선 채 할 수 있는 한 주의 깊고 성실하게 귀를 기울이려 애썼다. 그는 주의가 흐트러지지 않도록, 그리고 흰 넥타이를 매고 팔을 휘젓는 지휘자—언제나 그의 음악 감상을 무던히도 방해하던—나, 끈을 귀 위로 야무지게 묶은 보닛 차림의 부인들, 그리고 음악 말고는 아무 생각도 하지 않거나 온갖 잡다한 것을 생각하고 있는 그 모든 사람들을 바라봄으로써 자기 인상을 망치지 않으려 했다. 그는 음악 애호가나 말 많은 지인과 마주치지 않으려 애쓰며, 똑바로 앞쪽 바닥을 바라본 채 서서 귀를 기울였다.\n\n그러나 「리어 왕」 환상곡을 들으면 들을수록, 그는 그것에 대한 어떤 분명한 견해를 세우는 일로부터 점점 더 멀어지는 것 같았다. 무언가 어떤 감정의 음악적 표현이 끊임없이 시작되고 준비되는 듯하다가도, 그것은 곧바로 산산조각 나 버리고 새로운 음악적 동기로 부서지거나, 아니면 그저 작곡가의 변덕에 지나지 않는, 지극히 복잡하면서도 서로 단절된 소리들에 불과했다. 그리고 이 단편적인 음악적 표현들은, 때로 아름답기는 했지만 불쾌했다. 그것들이 전혀 예기치 못한 것이었고, 어떤 것에 의해서도 이끌려 나오지 않았기 때문이다. 환희와 비탄과 절망과 애틋함과 환호가 마치 미치광이의 감정처럼 아무런 연관도 없이 잇따랐다. 그리고 그 감정들은, 미치광이의 그것처럼 아주 느닷없이 솟아올랐다.\n\n연주가 진행되는 내내 레빈은 사람들이 춤추는 것을 바라보는 귀머거리 같은 기분이었고, 환상곡이 끝났을 때는 완전한 어리둥절함에 빠진 상태였으며, 보람도 없이 주의를 쥐어짠 탓에 큰 피로를 느꼈다. 사방에서 우레 같은 박수가 울려 퍼졌다. 모두가 일어나 이리저리 움직이며 이야기를 나누기 시작했다. 자신의 당혹감에 다른 사람들의 인상으로 어떤 빛을 비춰 보고 싶었던 레빈은, 음악 전문가를 찾아 이리저리 거닐기 시작했고, 자기가 아는 한 유명한 음악 애호가가 역시 그가 아는 페스초프와 이야기를 나누고 있는 것을 보고 반가워했다.\n\n\"훌륭해!\" 페스초프가 부드러운 저음으로 말하고 있었다. \"안녕하시오, 콘스탄틴 드미트리치? 특히나, 말하자면 조각적이고 입체적이며, 색채가 풍부한 대목은 코델리아의 다가옴이 느껴지는 부분이오. 여인이, das ewig Weibliche가 운명과 갈등을 빚는 그 대목 말이오. 그렇지 않소?\"\n\n* das ewig Weibliche: 독일어로 '영원히 여성적인 것'. 괴테 「파우스트」의 마지막 구절에서 따온 표현.\n\n\"그러니까…… 코델리아가 거기에 무슨 상관이 있다는 거요?\" 환상곡이 리어 왕을 표현하기로 되어 있다는 것을 잊은 채, 레빈이 머뭇거리며 물었다.\n\n\"코델리아가 등장하잖소…… 여기 보시오!\" 페스초프가 손에 든 프로그램의 매끄러운 표면을 손가락으로 톡톡 두드리며 말하고는, 그것을 레빈에게 건넸다.\n\n그제야 레빈은 환상곡의 제목을 떠올렸고, 프로그램 뒷면에 인쇄된, 셰익스피어에서 따온 구절의 러시아어 번역을 서둘러 읽었다.\n\n\"그게 없으면 따라갈 수가 없소.\" 페스초프가 레빈에게 말을 걸며 말했다. 그가 이야기를 나누던 사람이 가 버려서, 더 이상 말을 나눌 상대가 없었던 것이다.\n막간에 레빈과 페스초프는 바그너 악파의 음악이 지닌 장점과 결점을 두고 논쟁에 빠져들었다. 레빈은 바그너와 그의 모든 추종자들의 잘못이 음악을 다른 예술의 영역으로 끌어들이려 한 데 있다고 주장했다. 마치 시가 회화의 예술이 마땅히 해야 할 일인 얼굴 묘사를 시도할 때 잘못되는 것처럼 말이다. 그리고 이런 잘못의 한 예로, 그는 받침대 위에 선 시인의 형상 둘레를 떠도는 어떤 시적 환영들을 대리석에 새긴 조각가를 들었다. \"그 환영들은 환영이기는커녕, 그야말로 사다리에 매달려 있었지 뭐요.\" 레빈이 말했다. 그 비유가 마음에 들었지만, 그는 전에 같은 표현을 쓴 적이 있는지, 그것도 페스초프에게 쓴 적이 있는지 도무지 기억이 나지 않았고, 그 말을 하면서 당황스러움을 느꼈다.\n\n페스초프는 예술은 하나이며, 온갖 종류의 예술과 결합함으로써만 그 최고의 발현에 다다를 수 있다고 주장했다.\n\n두 번째로 연주된 곡은 레빈이 들을 수가 없었다. 옆에 서 있던 페스초프가 거의 내내 그에게 말을 걸며, 그 음악이 단순함을 지나치게 꾸며 내어 가장한다고 비난하고, 그것을 회화에서의 라파엘 전파의 단순함에 견주었던 것이다. 밖으로 나가면서 레빈은 더 많은 지인을 만났고, 그들과 정치며 음악이며 공통의 지인들에 관해 이야기를 나누었다. 그중에는 그가 방문하기로 해 놓고는 까맣게 잊고 있던 볼 백작도 있었다.\n\n\"그럼 어서 다녀와요.\" 레빈이 그 이야기를 하자 르보바 부인이 말했다. \"어쩌면 댁에 없을지도 모르니, 그러면 모임으로 나를 데리러 오면 되겠네요. 그때까지도 거기 있을 테니까요.\"\n\n# Chapter 6\n\n\"어쩌면 집에 없을지도 몰라.\" 레빈은 볼라 백작 부인 댁의 현관으로 들어서며 말했다.\n\n\"계십니다, 들어가시지요.\" 문지기가 단호하게 그의 외투를 벗기며 말했다.\n\n'아, 성가셔라!' 레빈은 한숨을 쉬며 생각했다. 그는 장갑 한 짝을 벗고 모자를 쓰다듬었다. '내가 뭐 하러 왔지? 이 사람들한테 무슨 할 말이 있다고?'\n\n첫 번째 응접실을 지나갈 때 레빈은 출입구에서 볼라 백작 부인과 마주쳤다. 부인은 근심스럽고 엄한 얼굴로 하인에게 무언가 지시를 내리고 있었다. 레빈을 보자 부인은 미소를 지으며 목소리가 들려오는 작은 응접실로 들어오라고 청했다. 그 방에는 백작 부인의 두 딸과, 레빈도 아는 모스크바 출신 대령이 안락의자에 앉아 있었다. 레빈은 다가가 그들에게 인사를 하고는 모자를 무릎에 얹은 채 소파 옆에 앉았다.\n\n\"부인은 어떻게 지내세요? 음악회에는 다녀오셨나요? 저희는 못 갔답니다. 어머니가 장례식에 참석하셔야 했거든요.\"\n\n\"네, 들었습니다…… 정말 갑작스러운 죽음이었지요!\" 레빈이 말했다.\n\n백작 부인이 들어와 소파에 앉더니, 부인도 그의 아내의 안부를 묻고 음악회에 대해 물었다.\n\n레빈은 대답을 한 뒤 아프락시나 부인의 갑작스러운 죽음에 대해 다시 한번 물었다.\n\n\"하지만 그분은 늘 몸이 약하셨잖아요.\"\n\n\"어제 오페라에는 가셨어요?\"\n\n\"네, 갔습니다.\"\n\n\"루카가 아주 훌륭했지요.\"\n\n\"네, 아주 훌륭했어요.\" 그가 말했다. 그리고 그들이 자기를 어떻게 생각하든 자기로서는 아무래도 상관없었으므로, 그는 그 가수의 재능이 지닌 특징에 대해 사람들이 이미 백 번은 들었을 이야기를 되풀이하기 시작했다. 볼라 백작 부인은 듣는 척했다. 그러다 그가 할 말을 충분히 하고 말을 멈추자, 그때까지 잠자코 있던 대령이 입을 열기 시작했다. 대령도 오페라에 대해, 그리고 문화에 대해 이야기했다. 마침내 대령은 투린 댁에서 열 예정인 folle journée에 대해 이야기하고는 웃음을 터뜨리며 요란하게 일어나 자리를 떴다. 레빈도 일어섰지만, 백작 부인의 얼굴을 보고 아직 떠날 때가 아니라는 것을 알아챘다. 이 분쯤은 더 머물러야 했다. 그는 다시 앉았다.\n* folle journée: 프랑스어로 '미친 듯한 하루', 곧 떠들썩한 잔치를 뜻한다.\n\n그러나 이게 얼마나 어리석은 짓인가 하는 생각만 줄곧 하고 있었으므로, 그는 대화의 화젯거리를 찾지 못한 채 잠자코 앉아 있었다.\n\n\"공개 회의에는 안 가세요? 무척 흥미로울 거라고들 하던데요.\" 백작 부인이 말을 꺼냈다.\n\n\"아니요, 처형을 거기서 데려오겠다고 약속을 했답니다.\" 레빈이 말했다.\n\n침묵이 이어졌다. 어머니는 다시 한번 딸과 눈길을 주고받았다.\n\n'자, 이제 때가 된 것 같군.' 레빈은 생각하며 일어났다. 부인들은 그와 악수를 나누고, 부디 자기들 대신 부인에게 mille choses를 전해 달라고 부탁했다.\n\n* mille choses: 프랑스어로 '천 가지 안부', 곧 '안부 인사를 듬뿍'이라는 뜻.\n문지기는 그에게 외투를 건네주며 물었다. \"나리께서는 어디에 묵고 계십니까?\" 그러고는 곧바로 크고 멋지게 장정된 책에 그의 주소를 적어 넣었다.\n\n'물론 난 상관없지만, 그래도 부끄럽고 형편없이 어리석은 짓 같단 말이야.' 레빈은 생각하며, 누구나 다 그렇게 하는 거라는 생각으로 스스로를 위로했다. 그는 처형을 만나 함께 집으로 돌아가기 위해 공개 회의로 마차를 몰았다.\n\n위원회의 공개 회의에는 아주 많은 사람들이, 그것도 거의 최상류 사교계 인사들이 모여 있었다. 레빈은 모두가 입을 모아 무척 흥미롭다고 한 보고가 시작될 때 맞춰 도착했다. 보고 낭독이 끝나자 사람들이 이리저리 움직였고, 레빈은 스비야시스키와 마주쳤다. 그는 그날 저녁 농업협회 모임에 오라고 간곡히 권했는데, 거기서 유명한 강연이 있을 거라고 했다. 또 방금 경마장에서 온 스테판 아르카지치와도, 그 밖의 여러 지인들과도 마주쳤다. 레빈은 회의에 대해, 새로운 환상곡에 대해, 그리고 어느 공판에 대해 갖가지 비평을 듣기도 하고 늘어놓기도 했다. 그러나 슬슬 느껴지기 시작한 정신적 피로 탓이었는지, 그는 공판 이야기를 하다가 실수를 저질렀고, 이 실수를 그는 여러 번 분하게 떠올렸다. 러시아에서 유죄 판결을 받은 한 외국인에 대한 선고를 이야기하면서, 그를 국외 추방으로 처벌하는 것이 얼마나 부당한 일인가에 대해 레빈은 전날 어느 지인과의 대화에서 들은 이야기를 그대로 되풀이했다.\n\n\"제 생각엔 그를 외국으로 보내는 것은 잉어를 물속에 넣어 벌하는 거나 다름없는 것 같습니다.\" 레빈은 말했다. 그러다 문득, 어느 지인에게서 듣고는 마치 자기 생각인 양 말한 이 발상이 사실은 크릴로프의 우화에서 나온 것이며, 그 지인도 어느 신문 기사에서 주워들은 것임을 떠올렸다.\n\n처형과 함께 집으로 돌아와 키티가 기분도 좋고 아주 건강한 것을 확인한 뒤, 레빈은 클럽으로 마차를 몰았다.\n\n# Chapter 7\n\n레빈은 마침 알맞은 때에 클럽에 닿았다. 그가 도착할 무렵 회원들과 손님들이 마차를 타고 속속 들어오고 있었다. 레빈은 아주 오랫동안 클럽에 와 본 적이 없었다. 모스크바에 살며 대학을 졸업하고 사교계에 발을 들이던 시절 이후로는 처음이었다. 그는 클럽을, 그 구조의 외형적인 세부들을 기억하고 있었지만, 옛 시절 그것이 자기에게 주었던 인상만은 까맣게 잊고 있었다. 그러나 넓고 반원형인 마당으로 마차를 몰아 들어가 썰매에서 내려 계단을 오르고, 어깨에 가로로 띠를 두른 수위가 소리 없이 고개를 숙이며 문을 열어 주자마자, 수위실에서 아래층에서 벗어 두는 편이 덜 번거롭다고 여긴 회원들의 외투며 덧신을 보자마자, 양탄자가 깔린 편안한 계단을 오를 때 그를 앞서 울리는 신비로운 종소리를 듣고, 층계참의 조각상을 보고, 맨 위 문 앞의 세 번째 수위—클럽 제복을 입은, 낯익으면서도 나이 든 모습으로 서두름도 지체도 없이 문을 열며 지나가는 손님들을 훑어보던—를 보자마자, 레빈은 클럽의 옛 인상이, 안온함과 편안함과 단정함의 인상이 한꺼번에 되살아나는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n\"모자를 주십시오.\" 모자를 수위실에 맡기는 클럽 규칙을 잊은 레빈에게 수위가 말했다. \"오랜만에 오셨습니다. 공작님께서 어제 성함을 적어 두셨습니다. 스테판 아르카지치 공작님은 아직 안 오셨습니다.\"\n\n수위는 레빈을 알 뿐만 아니라 그의 모든 친분과 관계까지 알고 있어서, 곧바로 그의 가까운 친구들을 입에 올렸다.\n\n칸막이로 나뉜 바깥 홀과, 한 남자가 과일 진열대 앞에 앉아 있는 오른쪽의 칸막이 친 방을 지나면서, 레빈은 천천히 걸어 들어가던 한 노인을 앞질러, 사람들로 가득하고 떠들썩한 식당으로 들어섰다.\n\n그는 거의 다 들어찬 식탁들을 따라 걸으며 손님들을 둘러보았다. 늙은이도 젊은이도, 온갖 부류의 사람들이 보였다. 어떤 이들은 조금 아는 사이였고, 어떤 이들은 가까운 친구였다. 화나거나 근심스러워 보이는 얼굴은 단 하나도 없었다. 모두가 근심과 걱정을 모자와 함께 수위실에 맡겨 두고, 인생의 물질적 축복을 한껏 누릴 채비를 느긋하게 차리고 있는 듯했다. 이곳에는 스비아시스키와 시체르바츠키, 네베돕스키와 늙은 공작, 그리고 브론스키와 세르게이 이바노비치가 있었다.\n\n\"아! 왜 이리 늦었나?\" 공작이 미소 지으며 자기 어깨 너머로 손을 내밀어 그에게 악수를 청했다. \"키티는 어떤가?\" 그는 조끼 단추에 끼워 넣은 냅킨을 매만지며 덧붙였다.\n\n\"잘 있습니다. 셋이서 집에서 식사 중이지요.\"\n\n\"아, '알린-나딘'이로군! 우리 자리엔 빈 데가 없네. 저 식탁으로 가서 얼른 자리를 잡게나.\" 공작은 그렇게 말하고는 몸을 돌려 조심스레 뱀장어 수프 접시를 받아 들었다.\n\n\"레빈, 이쪽이오!\" 조금 떨어진 곳에서 사람 좋은 목소리가 외쳤다. 투롭친이었다. 그는 젊은 장교와 함께 앉아 있었고, 그들 곁에는 뒤집어 놓은 의자 두 개가 있었다. 레빈은 반가운 마음으로 그들에게 다가갔다. 그는 늘 그 마음씨 고운 난봉꾼 투롭친을 좋아했다. 그는 레빈의 마음속에서 구애 시절의 추억과 이어져 있었다. 게다가 지금 이 순간, 지적인 대화의 긴장이 지난 뒤라, 투롭친의 사람 좋은 얼굴은 유독 반가웠다.\n\n\"당신과 오블론스키 자리요. 그가 곧 올 거요.\"\n\n몸을 아주 꼿꼿이 세우고 두 눈이 끊임없이 즐거움으로 반짝이던 그 젊은이는 페테르부르크에서 온 장교 가긴이었다. 투롭친이 두 사람을 소개해 주었다.\n\n\"오블론스키는 늘 늦지요.\"\n\n\"아, 저기 오는군!\"\n\n\"이제 막 왔나?\" 오블론스키가 그들에게 빠른 걸음으로 다가오며 말했다. \"안녕들 하신가. 보드카는 좀 했나? 자, 그럼 이리 오게.\"\n\n레빈은 일어나 그와 함께 온갖 종류의 술과 안주가 차려진 큰 식탁으로 갔다. 스물네 가지나 되는 진미 가운데 입맛에 맞는 것 하나쯤은 찾을 법도 했건만, 스테판 아르카지치는 뭔가 특별한 것을 청했고, 곁에 서 있던 제복 입은 웨이터 하나가 즉시 원하는 것을 가져왔다. 두 사람은 포도주를 한 잔씩 마시고 자기들 식탁으로 돌아왔다.\n\n수프를 먹는 사이에 벌써 가긴에게 샴페인이 나왔고, 그는 웨이터에게 네 잔을 채우라고 일렀다. 레빈은 포도주를 마다하지 않았고 두 번째 병을 청했다. 그는 몹시 배가 고팠으므로 큰 즐거움으로 먹고 마셨으며, 일행의 활기차고 소탈한 대화에 끼는 것은 그보다 더 큰 즐거움이었다. 가긴이 목소리를 낮추어 페테르부르크에서 나온 최신 우스갯소리를 들려주었는데, 그 이야기는 점잖지 못하고 어리석은 것이었지만 어찌나 우스꽝스러웠던지 레빈이 어찌나 큰 소리로 폭소를 터뜨렸던지 곁에 있던 사람들이 돌아볼 정도였다.\n\n\"그건 '내가 도저히 못 견디는 게 바로 그거야!' 하는 거랑 똑같은 식이군. 그 이야기 아나?\" 스테판 아르카지치가 말했다. \"아, 그건 정말 일품이지! 한 병 더.\" 그는 웨이터에게 일러 놓고는 자기 우스갯소리를 풀어놓기 시작했다.\n\n\"표트르 일리치 비놉스키 님이 함께 한잔하시자고 청하십니다.\" 자그마한 늙은 웨이터가 스테판 아르카지치의 말을 끊으며, 거품이 이는 샴페인이 담긴 가느다란 잔 두 개를 가져와 스테판 아르카지치와 레빈에게 건넸다. 스테판 아르카지치는 잔을 받아 들고는, 식탁 저쪽 끝의 붉은 콧수염을 기른 대머리 사내를 향해 미소 지으며 고개를 끄덕였다.\n\n\"저 사람은 누굽니까?\" 레빈이 물었다.\n\n\"언젠가 우리 집에서 만났잖나, 기억 안 나? 사람 좋은 친구지.\"\n\n레빈도 스테판 아르카지치처럼 잔을 받아 들었다.\n\n스테판 아르카지치의 일화도 무척 재미있었다. 레빈도 자기 이야기를 했고, 그것 역시 호응을 얻었다. 그러고 나서 그들은 말 이야기며 경마 이야기, 그날 무엇을 했는지, 브론스키의 아틀라스가 얼마나 멋지게 일등상을 거머쥐었는지를 화제 삼았다. 레빈은 만찬 자리에서 시간이 어떻게 흘러가는지조차 알아채지 못했다.\n\n\"아! 저기들 오는군!\" 만찬이 끝나 갈 무렵 스테판 아르카지치가 의자 등받이에 몸을 기대고 키 큰 근위 장교와 함께 다가오는 브론스키에게 손을 내밀며 말했다. 브론스키의 얼굴 역시 클럽 전체에 감도는 그 기분 좋은 즐거움의 표정으로 환하게 빛났다. 그는 장난스럽게 스테판 아르카지치의 어깨에 팔꿈치를 괴고는 그에게 무어라 속삭이고는, 똑같이 기분 좋은 미소를 머금은 채 레빈에게 손을 내밀었다.\n\n\"만나서 정말 반갑소.\" 그가 말했다. \"선거 때 당신을 찾았는데, 이미 떠났다고들 하더군요.\"\n\n\"네, 바로 그날 떠났습니다. 마침 당신의 말 이야기를 하던 참이었지요. 축하드립니다.\" 레빈이 말했다. \"정말 빠르게 달리더군요.\"\n\n\"그렇소. 당신도 경주마를 가지고 있지 않소?\"\n\n\"아니요, 제 아버지가 가지고 계셨죠. 하지만 기억도 하고 그쪽으로 좀 아는 바도 있습니다.\"\n\n\"어디서 식사했나?\" 스테판 아르카지치가 물었다.\n\n\"우린 기둥 뒤편 둘째 식탁에 있었네.\"\n\n\"우리는 이 친구의 성공을 축하하던 참이오.\" 키 큰 대령이 말했다. \"벌써 두 번째 황실 상이지요. 나도 이 친구가 말에서 누리는 행운만큼만 카드에서 운이 따르면 좋으련만. 자, 귀한 시간을 허비할 게 뭐 있겠소? 나는 '지옥의 영역'으로 가 봐야겠소.\" 대령은 그렇게 덧붙이고는 자리를 떴다.\n\n\"저 친구가 야시빈이오.\" 브론스키가 투롭친의 물음에 답하며, 그들 곁의 빈자리에 앉았다. 그는 건네받은 잔을 비우고 포도주 한 병을 청했다. 클럽의 분위기 탓인지 마신 술 탓인지, 레빈은 브론스키와 가장 좋은 소 품종에 관해 한참 이야기를 나누었고, 이 사내에게 털끝만 한 적대감도 느끼지 않는 것이 무척 기뻤다. 그는 다른 이야기 끝에, 아내에게서 그녀가 마리야 보리소브나 공작 부인 댁에서 그를 만났다는 말을 들었다는 것까지 말해 주었다.\n\n\"아, 마리야 보리소브나 공작 부인, 그분은 참 멋진 분이지!\" 스테판 아르카지치가 말하고는 그 부인에 관한 일화를 들려주어 모두를 웃겼다. 특히 브론스키가 어찌나 천진한 즐거움으로 웃었던지, 레빈은 그에게 완전히 마음이 누그러지는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n\"자, 우린 다 끝났나?\" 스테판 아르카지치가 미소 지으며 일어났다. \"갑시다.\"\n\n# Chapter 8\n\n식탁에서 일어선 레빈은 가긴과 함께 천장이 높은 방을 지나 당구실로 걸어갔다. 걸음을 옮길 때마다 두 팔이 묘하게 가볍고 편안하게 흔들리는 것이 느껴졌다. 큰 방을 가로지르다가 그는 장인과 마주쳤다.\n\n\"어떤가, 우리의 게으름의 전당이 마음에 드는가?\" 공작이 그의 팔을 잡으며 말했다. \"가세, 가자고!\"\n\n\"네, 여기저기 좀 거닐면서 모든 걸 둘러보고 싶었습니다. 흥미롭군요.\"\n\n\"그래, 자네한테는 흥미롭겠지. 하지만 나한테는 그 흥미가 사뭇 달라. 저기 저 작은 노인네들을 좀 보게나.\" 그는 등이 굽고 입술을 쑥 내민 채 부드러운 장화를 신고 발을 끌며 다가오는 한 클럽 회원을 가리키며 말했다. \"그러고는 저들이 태어날 때부터 저런 슐루피크였다고 한번 상상해 보게.\"\n\n\"슐루피크라니요?\"\n\n\"자네는 그 말을 모르는 모양이군. 그게 우리 클럽에서 쓰는 명칭이야. 달걀 굴리기 놀이 알지? 한참을 굴리다 보면 달걀이 슐루피크가 되어 버리거든. 우리도 그래. 클럽에 자꾸자꾸 나오다 보면 결국엔 슐루피크가 되어 버리는 거지. 아, 자네 웃는구먼! 하지만 우리도 자칫 그 꼴이 될까 봐 늘 조심한다네. 자네 체첸스키 공작 아나?\" 공작이 물었다. 레빈은 그의 얼굴을 보고 그가 막 무슨 우스운 이야기를 늘어놓으려 한다는 것을 알아차렸다.\n\n\"아니요, 모릅니다.\"\n\n\"저런! 그래, 체첸스키 공작은 꽤 알려진 인물이야. 하지만 그건 아무래도 좋아. 그는 늘 여기서 당구를 친다네. 불과 삼 년 전만 해도 그는 슐루피크가 아니었고, 기운도 펄펄해서 오히려 남들을 슐루피크라고 부르고 다녔지. 그런데 어느 날 그가 나타났는데, 우리 문지기가... 자네 바실리 알지? 왜, 그 뚱뚱한 친구 말이야. 그는 재담으로 유명하지. 그래서 체첸스키 공작이 그에게 묻기를, '이보게, 바실리, 누가 와 있나? 슐루피크들 좀 와 있나?' 하니까, 그가 '나리께서 세 번째이십니다.' 하더라네. 그래, 이보게, 정말 그랬다니까!\"\n\n이야기를 나누고 마주치는 친구들과 인사를 주고받으며 레빈과 공작은 모든 방을 두루 걸어 다녔다. 이미 테이블들이 차려지고 늘 어울리던 짝패들이 적은 판돈을 걸고 노름을 하고 있는 큰 방, 사람들이 장기를 두고 세르게이 이바노비치가 누군가와 이야기를 나누며 앉아 있던 소파 방, 움푹 들어간 구석의 소파를 둘러싸고 한 무리가 신나게 샴페인을 마시고 있던 당구실—가긴도 그중 하나였다—을 지났다. 그들은 야시빈이 앉아 있는 테이블 하나를 빙 둘러싸고 꽤 많은 사람들이 몰려 있는 \"지옥의 영역\"을 들여다보았다. 소리를 내지 않으려 애쓰며, 갓을 씌운 등불 아래 성난 얼굴을 한 젊은이가 잡지를 하나씩 차례로 넘기고 있고 대머리 장군 하나가 책에 파묻혀 있는 어두운 독서실로 들어갔다. 또한 공작이 지성의 방이라 부르는 곳에도 들어갔는데, 거기서는 세 신사가 최근의 정치 소식을 두고 열띤 토론을 벌이고 있었다.\n\n\"공작님, 어서 오십시오, 다 준비됐습니다.\" 그를 찾으러 온 카드 짝패 중 하나가 말하자, 공작은 그쪽으로 가 버렸다. 레빈은 자리에 앉아 귀를 기울였지만, 아침에 나누었던 온갖 대화를 떠올리자 별안간 끔찍하게 지루해졌다. 그는 부랴부랴 일어나 그토록 즐거웠던 오블론스키와 투롭친을 찾으러 갔다.\n\n투롭친은 당구실에서 술을 마시는 무리 가운데 하나였고, 스테판 아르카지치는 방 저쪽 구석의 문 가까이에서 브론스키와 이야기를 나누고 있었다.\n\n\"그 사람이 따분해서가 아니라, 이 모호하고 불안정한 처지가 문제야.\" 레빈은 이런 말을 얼핏 들었고, 서둘러 자리를 뜨려 했지만 스테판 아르카지치가 그를 불러 세웠다.\n\n\"레빈.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 말했다. 레빈은 그의 눈이 정확히 눈물로 가득 찼다기보다는 촉촉이 젖어 있다는 것을 알아챘는데, 그것은 그가 술을 마셨거나 감동했을 때면 늘 일어나는 일이었다. 지금은 그 두 가지 까닭 모두 때문이었다. \"레빈, 가지 마.\" 그는 그의 팔꿈치 위쪽을 다정하게 꽉 움켜쥐며 말했다. 그를 도무지 보내고 싶지 않은 기색이 역력했다.\n\n\"이 사람은 나의 진정한 벗이야—거의 가장 가까운 벗이지.\" 그는 브론스키에게 말했다. \"자네는 나한테 더욱 가깝고 소중한 사람이 되었고. 그래서 난 자네 둘이 친구가, 그것도 막역한 친구가 되었으면 해. 또 마땅히 그래야 한다고 생각하고. 자네들 둘 다 훌륭한 사람들이니까.\"\n\n\"뭐, 이제 우리한테 남은 거라곤 입맞춤하고 친구가 되는 일밖에 없겠군요.\" 브론스키가 사람 좋게 농을 던지며 손을 내밀었다.\n\n레빈은 내민 손을 얼른 잡고 따뜻하게 마주 쥐었다.\n\n\"정말, 정말 기쁩니다.\" 레빈이 말했다.\n\n\"여보게, 샴페인 한 병.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 말했다.\n\n\"저도 무척 기쁩니다.\" 브론스키가 말했다.\n\n그러나 스테판 아르카지치의 바람에도, 또 그들 자신의 바람에도 불구하고, 그들에게는 나눌 이야깃거리가 없었고, 둘 다 그것을 느꼈다.\n\n\"있잖아, 이 친구가 안나를 한 번도 못 만나 봤다네.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 브론스키에게 말했다. \"그래서 난 무엇보다 이 친구를 데리고 가서 그 사람을 만나게 해 주고 싶어. 가세, 레빈!\"\n\n\"정말입니까?\" 브론스키가 말했다. \"그녀도 당신을 만나면 무척 반가워할 겁니다. 저야 당장 집에 갔으면 합니다만,\" 그가 덧붙였다. \"야시빈이 걱정돼서, 그가 끝낼 때까지 남아 있고 싶군요.\"\n\n\"왜, 그가 잃고 있나?\"\n\n\"계속 잃고 있어서요, 그를 말릴 수 있는 친구는 저밖에 없거든요.\"\n\n\"그럼, 피라미드 한 판 어떤가? 레빈, 자네 칠 텐가? 좋아!\" 스테판 아르카지치가 말했다. \"테이블 준비하게.\" 그가 점수원에게 말했다.\n\n\"진작에 준비됐습니다.\" 점수원이 대답했다. 그는 벌써 공들을 삼각형으로 모아 놓고는 심심풀이로 빨간 공을 이리저리 굴리고 있었다.\n\n\"자, 그럼 시작하지.\"\n\n게임이 끝난 뒤 브론스키와 레빈은 가긴의 테이블에 앉았고, 스테판 아르카지치의 권유로 레빈도 게임에 끼어들었다.\n\n브론스키는 테이블에 앉았고, 끊임없이 그에게 다가오는 친구들에 둘러싸였다. 이따금씩 그는 야시빈을 살피러 \"지옥\"으로 갔다. 레빈은 아침의 정신적 피로 끝에 찾아온 기분 좋은 휴식의 감각을 만끽하고 있었다. 그는 브론스키와의 모든 적대감이 끝난 것이 기뻤고, 평온과 예의와 안락의 감각이 그를 좀처럼 떠나지 않았다.\n\n게임이 끝나자 스테판 아르카지치가 레빈의 팔을 잡았다.\n\n\"자, 그럼 안나한테 가세. 당장? 응? 그 사람 집에 있어. 진작에 자넬 데려가겠다고 약속했거든. 자넨 오늘 저녁을 어디서 보낼 작정이었나?\"\n\n\"아, 딱히 정해 둔 데는 없습니다. 스비야시스키한테 농업 협회에 가겠다고 약속하긴 했지만요. 어떻게든 가시지요.\" 레빈이 말했다.\n\n\"아주 좋아. 가세. 내 마차가 와 있는지 알아봐 주게.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 종업원에게 말했다.\n\n레빈은 테이블로 다가가 자기가 잃은 사십 루블을 치렀다. 계산대 앞에 서 있던 작은 노인 종업원이 무슨 영문인지 어떻게든 알아낸 그의 계산서도 치르고는, 두 팔을 흔들며 모든 방을 지나 출구로 걸어 나갔다.\n\n# Chapter 9\n\n\"오블론스키 댁 마차!\" 문지기가 화난 듯한 굵은 저음으로 외쳤다. 마차가 다가왔고 둘은 올라탔다. 마차가 클럽 정문을 빠져나가는 처음 몇 순간 동안만, 레빈은 여전히 클럽 특유의 휴식과 안락과 나무랄 데 없는 품격의 분위기에 젖어 있었다. 하지만 마차가 거리로 나서자, 울퉁불퉁한 길 위에서 덜컹거리는 것을 느끼고, 맞은편에서 다가오는 썰매꾼의 성난 고함 소리를 듣고, 흐릿한 불빛 속에서 어느 선술집과 가게들의 붉은 차양을 보게 되자, 그 인상은 흩어져 버렸고, 그는 자기 행동을 곰곰이 따져 보며 안나를 만나러 가는 것이 옳은 일인지 스스로 묻기 시작했다. 키티가 뭐라고 할까? 하지만 스테판 아르카지치는 그에게 생각할 틈을 주지 않았고, 마치 그의 의심을 꿰뚫어 보기라도 한 듯 그것을 흩어 버렸다.\n\n\"자네가 그 사람을 알게 되다니, 내가 얼마나 기쁜지 모르겠네.\" 그가 말했다. \"돌리도 진작부터 바라던 일이라네. 르보프도 그녀를 보러 갔고, 자주 들른다네. 내 누이이긴 하네만,\" 스테판 아르카지치가 말을 이었다. \"그래도 주저 없이 말하겠는데, 정말 비범한 여자라네. 자네도 곧 알게 될 거야. 그녀의 처지는 매우 괴로워, 특히 요즘은 더하지.\"\n\n\"왜 특히 요즘이지?\"\n\n\"그녀의 남편과 이혼 문제를 두고 협상을 진행하고 있다네. 그 사람도 동의는 했지만, 아들 문제로 어려움이 좀 있어서, 진작에 정리되었어야 할 일이 벌써 석 달째 질질 끌리고 있다네. 이혼만 끝나면 그녀는 브론스키와 결혼할 걸세. 아무도 믿지 않는, 그저 사람들이 편안하게 사는 것만 가로막는 그런 케케묵은 절차들이 얼마나 어리석은지!\" 스테판 아르카지치가 끼어들었다. \"뭐, 그렇게 되면 그들의 처지도 내 처지나 자네 처지처럼 떳떳해지겠지.\"\n\n\"무엇이 그리 어려운 건가?\" 레빈이 말했다.\n\n\"아, 길고 따분한 이야기일세! 우리네 모든 일이라는 게 다 그렇게 비정상적인 상태에 놓여 있다네. 어쨌든 요점은 이거야. 그녀는 다들 그녀를 아는 모스크바에서 석 달째 이혼을 기다리고 있다네. 어디에도 나다니지 않고, 돌리 말고는 어떤 여자도 만나지 않아. 왜냐하면, 자네 알겠나, 사람들이 마지못해 인심 쓰듯 찾아오는 걸 그녀가 원치 않기 때문이지. 그 바보 같은 바르바라 공작 부인마저, 이걸 예의에 어긋나는 일로 여기고 그녀를 떠나 버렸다네. 자, 보게나, 이런 처지에 놓이면 다른 여자라면 어떤 여자도 제 안에서 헤쳐 나갈 방도를 찾지 못했을 거야. 하지만 자네는 그녀가 자기 삶을 어떻게 꾸려 왔는지, 얼마나 차분하고 얼마나 기품 있는지 보게 될 걸세. 왼쪽으로, 교회 맞은편 반달 모양 거리로!\" 스테판 아르카지치가 창밖으로 몸을 내밀며 외쳤다. \"휴! 어찌나 덥던지!\" 영하 십이 도인데도 그는 이렇게 말하며 풀어 헤친 외투를 더 활짝 젖혔다.\n\n\"하지만 그녀에게는 딸이 있지 않은가. 분명 그 아이를 돌보느라 바쁠 텐데?\" 레빈이 말했다.\n\n\"자네는 모든 여자를 그저 암컷으로만, une couveuse로만 그려 보는 모양이군.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 말했다. \"여자가 바쁘다면 그건 분명 아이들 때문일 거라고 말이지. 아닐세, 내가 알기로 그녀는 딸을 더없이 훌륭하게 기르고 있지만, 그것에 대해서는 입에 올리지 않는다네. 그녀가 바쁜 건, 무엇보다 자기가 쓰는 글 때문이야. 자네가 비웃듯 미소 짓는 게 보이는데, 그건 잘못된 생각일세. 그녀는 동화책을 쓰고 있는데, 그것에 대해 누구에게도 말하지 않는다네. 하지만 내게는 읽어 주었고, 나는 그 원고를 보르쿠예프에게 건넸지... 자네도 알지, 그 출판업자 말이야... 그 사람도 내 생각엔 작가일 거야. 그런 일을 잘 아는 사람인데, 그 사람 말로는 비범한 작품이라더군. 그렇다고 자네는 그녀가 여류 작가라고 생각하나?—전혀 그렇지 않아. 그녀는 무엇보다도 마음이 따뜻한 여자라네, 하지만 자네도 곧 알게 될 거야. 지금은 어린 영국 여자아이 하나를 데리고 있고, 가족 전체를 돌보고 있다네.\"\n* une couveuse: 프랑스어로 '알을 품는 암탉'.\n\n\"오, 무슨 자선 사업 같은 건가?\"\n\n\"이런, 자네는 매사를 가장 나쁜 쪽으로만 보려 드는군. 자선에서 나온 게 아니라, 마음에서 우러나온 일이라네. 그들에게는—그러니까 브론스키에게는—조련사가 하나 있었는데, 영국인이고 자기 분야에서는 일류였지만 술꾼이었지. 그 사람은 완전히 술에 빠져 버려서—진전섬망에 걸려—그 가족이 세상에 내팽개쳐지고 말았다네. 그녀가 그들을 보았고, 도왔고, 점점 더 그들에게 마음을 쓰게 되었지. 그래서 이제는 가족 전체가 그녀의 손에 달려 있다네. 하지만 자네도 알겠지만, 돈으로 돕는 식의 시혜는 아니야. 그녀는 직접 그 집 사내아이들에게 김나지움 진학을 위한 러시아어를 가르치고, 어린 여자아이는 데려와 함께 살고 있다네. 하지만 자네가 직접 그녀를 보게 될 걸세.\"\n\n마차가 안마당으로 들어섰고, 스테판 아르카지치는 썰매들이 늘어선 현관에서 요란하게 초인종을 울렸다.\n\n그리고 문을 열어 준 하인에게 마님이 댁에 계시느냐고 묻지도 않고, 스테판 아르카지치는 곧장 현관 안으로 들어섰다. 레빈은 자기가 옳은 일을 하는 것인지 그른 일을 하는 것인지 점점 더 의심하며 그의 뒤를 따랐다.\n\n거울에 자기 모습을 비춰 본 레빈은 얼굴이 벌겋게 달아오른 것을 알아챘지만, 자기가 취하지 않았다는 것만은 확신하며 양탄자가 깔린 계단을 올라 스테판 아르카지치를 뒤따랐다. 계단 꼭대기에서 스테판 아르카지치는 마치 친한 친구를 대하듯 자기에게 절하는 하인에게 안나 아르카지예브나와 누가 함께 있느냐고 물었고, 보르쿠예프 씨라는 대답을 들었다.\n\n\"어디 있나?\"\n\n\"서재에 계십니다.\"\n\n그리 크지 않고 어두운 색의 판자를 댄 벽으로 둘러싸인 식당을 지나, 스테판 아르카지치와 레빈은 푹신한 양탄자를 밟으며 커다랗고 짙은 갓을 씌운 등불 하나만이 밝히고 있는 어둑한 서재로 걸어 들어갔다. 벽에는 반사판이 달린 또 다른 등이 걸려 한 여인의 커다란 전신 초상화를 비추고 있었는데, 레빈은 그것을 보지 않을 수 없었다. 그것은 안나의 초상화로, 이탈리아에서 미하일로프가 그린 것이었다. 스테판 아르카지치가 격자 칸막이 뒤로 들어가고, 줄곧 이야기하던 남자의 목소리가 멈춘 사이, 레빈은 그 초상화를 응시했다. 환한 빛을 받은 초상화는 액자에서 도드라져 나와 있었고, 그는 도무지 거기서 눈을 뗄 수가 없었다. 그는 자기가 어디에 있는지조차 까맣게 잊었고, 무슨 말이 오가는지도 듣지 못한 채, 그 경이로운 초상화에서 시선을 떼지 못했다. 그것은 한 폭의 그림이 아니라, 살아 있는 매혹적인 여인이었다. 검고 곱슬거리는 머리에, 드러낸 팔과 어깨, 부드러운 솜털로 덮인 입술에는 사색에 잠긴 미소가 어려 있었다. 그녀는 그를 당혹스럽게 하는 눈으로, 의기양양하면서도 부드럽게 그를 바라보고 있었다. 그녀가 살아 있지 않은 것은 오직 살아 있는 여인이 도달할 수 있는 것보다 더 아름다웠기 때문이었다.\n\n\"정말 반가워요!\" 그는 문득 가까이에서, 틀림없이 자기에게 건네는 목소리를, 바로 그 초상화 속에서 그가 감탄하던 여인의 목소리를 들었다. 안나가 격자 칸막이 뒤에서 나와 그를 맞으러 온 것이었다. 레빈은 서재의 어슴푸레한 불빛 속에서 짙은 청색의 광택 나는 드레스를 입은, 바로 그 초상화 속 여인을 보았다. 같은 자세도, 같은 표정도 아니었지만, 화가가 초상화에 담아낸 그 완벽한 아름다움만은 그대로였다. 실제 모습은 덜 눈부셨으나, 그 대신 살아 있는 여인에게는 초상화에 없는 어떤 싱그럽고 매혹적인 무언가가 있었다.\n\n# Chapter 10\n\n그녀는 그를 맞으려고 자리에서 일어났는데, 그를 보는 기쁨을 감추지 않았다. 그녀가 작고 다부진 손을 내밀어 그를 보르쿠예프에게 소개하고, 일감을 든 채 앉아 있던 빨간 머리의 예쁘장한 소녀를 자기 제자라고 가리켜 보이던 그 조용하고 편안한 태도에서, 레빈은 늘 침착하고 자연스러운, 상류 사교계 여인의 몸가짐을 알아보았고 또 그것이 마음에 들었다.\n\n\"정말 기뻐요, 너무나 기뻐요.\" 그녀가 거듭 말했는데, 그녀의 입술에서 흘러나온 이 평범한 말들이 레빈의 귀에는 각별한 의미를 띠고 들렸다. \"오래전부터 당신을 알았고 또 좋아하고 있었답니다. 스티바와의 우정 때문이기도 하고, 또 당신 부인 때문이기도 하지요…… 그분과는 아주 잠깐 알고 지냈을 뿐이지만, 제게는 더없이 아름다운 꽃 같은, 그야말로 한 송이 꽃 같은 인상을 남겼어요. 그런데 그분이 곧 어머니가 된다니!\"\n\n그녀는 이따금 레빈에게서 오빠에게로 시선을 옮겨 가며 서두르지 않고 편안하게 이야기했고, 레빈은 자기가 그녀에게 좋은 인상을 주고 있음을 느꼈으며, 마치 어릴 적부터 그녀를 알아 온 것처럼 곧바로 마음이 놓이고 편안하고 행복해졌다.\n\n\"이반 페트로비치와 저는 알렉세이의 서재에 자리를 잡았답니다.\" 담배를 피워도 되겠느냐는 스테판 아르카지치의 물음에 그녀가 답했다. \"그저 담배를 피울 수 있게 말이에요.\" 그러고는 레빈에게 담배를 피우겠느냐고 묻는 대신 그를 흘긋 보며 자라 등딱지로 만든 담뱃갑을 끌어당겨 궐련 한 개비를 집었다.\n\n\"오늘은 좀 어때?\" 오빠가 그녀에게 물었다.\n\n\"아, 아무렇지도 않아. 늘 그렇듯 신경이 좀.\"\n\n\"그래, 정말 빼어나게 잘 그렸지?\" 레빈이 그림을 유심히 들여다보는 것을 알아챈 스테판 아르카지치가 말했다.\n\n\"이보다 더 훌륭한 초상화는 본 적이 없습니다.\"\n\n\"게다가 놀랄 만큼 닮지 않았습니까?\" 보르쿠예프가 말했다.\n\n레빈은 초상화에서 실물로 시선을 옮겼다. 자기에게 쏠린 그의 눈길을 느끼자 안나의 얼굴이 묘한 광채로 빛났다. 레빈은 얼굴을 붉혔고, 당혹감을 감추려고 요즘 다리아 알렉산드로브나를 만났느냐고 물어보려 했다. 그런데 바로 그 순간 안나가 입을 열었다. \"마침 이반 페트로비치와 바슈첸코프의 근작들에 관해 이야기하던 참이었어요. 그 그림들 보셨나요?\"\n\n\"예, 봤습니다.\" 레빈이 대답했다.\n\n\"아, 죄송해요, 제가 말을 가로막았네요…… 무슨 말씀을 하시려던 거였죠?……\"\n\n레빈은 요즘 돌리를 만났느냐고 물었다.\n\n\"어제 여기 왔었어요. 그리샤 일로 김나지움 사람들에게 잔뜩 화가 나 있더군요. 라틴어 선생이 그 아이에게 부당하게 굴었던 모양이에요.\"\n\n\"예, 그 사람 그림은 봤습니다. 별로 마음에 들지 않았어요.\" 레빈은 그녀가 꺼냈던 화제로 되돌아갔다.\n\n레빈은 이제 그날 아침 내내 그래 왔던 그 순전히 사무적인 태도와는 전혀 다르게 이야기하고 있었다. 그녀와 나누는 대화에서는 한 마디 한 마디가 각별한 의미를 띠었다. 그녀와 이야기하는 것은 즐거웠고, 그녀의 말을 듣는 것은 더더욱 즐거웠다.\n\n안나는 그저 자연스럽고 영리하게 이야기할 뿐만 아니라, 영리하면서도 무심하게, 자기 생각에는 아무런 가치도 두지 않으면서 상대방의 생각에는 큰 무게를 실어 주며 이야기했다.\n\n대화는 예술의 새로운 흐름으로, 어느 프랑스 화가가 그린 새로운 성서 삽화로 옮겨 갔다. 보르쿠예프는 그 화가가 조잡할 정도로 극단까지 밀어붙인 사실주의를 두고 공격했다.\n\n레빈은 프랑스인들이 누구보다도 인습을 멀리까지 밀고 나갔으며, 그래서 그들이 사실주의로의 회귀에서 커다란 가치를 발견하는 거라고 말했다. 거짓말하지 않는다는 사실 그 자체에서 그들은 시를 본다는 것이었다.\n\n레빈은 자기가 한 어떤 영리한 말에서도 이 한마디만큼 큰 기쁨을 느껴 본 적이 없었다. 안나의 얼굴이 단번에 환해졌으니, 그 생각을 곧바로 알아챈 것이었다. 그녀가 웃었다.\n\n\"제가 웃는 건,\" 그녀가 말했다, \"아주 똑 닮은 초상화를 봤을 때처럼 웃는 거예요. 당신이 하신 말씀은 지금의 프랑스 예술을, 회화도 문학도, 정말이지—졸라며 도데며, 완벽하게 짚어 냈어요. 하지만 어쩌면 늘 그런 건지도 모르지요. 사람들이 가공의, 인습적인 전형들로 자기 개념을 빚어내고, 그러다가—온갖 조합combinaisons을 다 만들어 보고 나면—그 가공의 인물들에 싫증이 나서 더 자연스럽고 참된 인물들을 만들어 내기 시작하는 거예요.\"\n\n\"정말이지 옳은 말씀입니다.\" 보르쿠예프가 말했다.\n\n\"그래서 클럽에 다녀온 거예요?\" 그녀가 오빠에게 물었다.\n\n\"그래, 그래, 이런 게 바로 여자야!\" 레빈은 자기 자신도 잊은 채, 그 순간 별안간 완전히 딴판으로 변해 버린 그녀의 사랑스럽고 생기 넘치는 얼굴을 뚫어지게 바라보며 생각했다. 그녀가 오빠에게로 몸을 기울이며 무슨 이야기를 하는지 레빈은 듣지 못했지만, 그녀의 표정 변화에 충격을 받았다. 조금 전 차분함 속에서 그토록 아름다웠던 그녀의 얼굴이, 별안간 묘한 호기심과 분노와 자존심이 어린 표정을 띤 것이다. 하지만 그것은 한순간뿐이었다. 그녀는 무언가를 떠올리기라도 한 듯 눈꺼풀을 내리깔았다.\n\n\"아, 뭐, 그런 건 누구한테도 흥미로운 일이 아니죠.\" 그녀가 말하고는 영국 소녀 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸다.\n\n\"응접실에 차를 내오라고 일러 주렴.\" 그녀가 영어로 말했다.\n\n소녀가 일어나 밖으로 나갔다.\n\n\"그래, 그 애 시험은 어떻게 됐어?\" 스테판 아르카지치가 물었다.\n\n\"훌륭했어! 정말 재능 있는 아이인 데다 마음씨도 곱지.\"\n\n\"그러다가 결국엔 네 친자식보다 그 애를 더 사랑하게 되겠구나.\"\n\n\"꼭 남자다운 말이네. 사랑에는 더하고 덜하고가 없어. 내 딸은 한 가지 사랑으로 사랑하고, 저 아이는 또 다른 사랑으로 사랑하는 거지.\"\n\n\"제가 안나 아르카지예브나께 말씀드렸지요.\" 보르쿠예프가 말했다. \"저 영국 아이에게 쏟는 정성의 백분의 일이라도 러시아 어린이 교육이라는 공적인 문제에 기울이신다면, 위대하고 유익한 일을 하시게 될 거라고요.\"\n\n\"맞아요, 하지만 어쩔 수가 없어요. 저는 그렇게 못 해요. 알렉세이 키릴로비치 백작이 무척 권했답니다.\" (그녀는 알렉세이 키릴로비치 백작이라는 말을 입에 올리며 애원하듯 머뭇거리는 눈빛으로 레빈을 흘긋 보았고, 그는 자기도 모르게 정중하고 안심시키는 눈길로 답했다.) \"그분이 마을 학교를 맡아 보라고 권하셨어요. 저도 몇 번이나 가 봤지요. 아이들은 무척 사랑스러웠지만, 그 일에 마음이 끌리질 않더군요. 정성을 말씀하시는데, 정성은 사랑에 기대는 거예요. 그리고 사랑이란 제멋대로 오는 거라서, 억지로 짜낼 수가 없어요. 저는 이 아이가 좋아진 거고요—왜 그런지는 저도 말로 못 하겠어요.\"\n\n그러고는 그녀가 다시 레빈을 흘긋 보았다. 그녀의 미소와 눈길은—이 모든 것이 그에게, 오직 그에게만 자기 말을 건네고 있으며, 그의 좋은 평가를 소중히 여기고 있고, 또한 두 사람이 서로를 이해하고 있음을 미리부터 확신하고 있다고 말해 주고 있었다.\n\n\"충분히 이해합니다.\" 레빈이 대답했다. \"학교나 그런 류의 기관 일반에 마음을 쏟기란 불가능한 일이고, 바로 그래서 자선 기관들이 늘 그렇게 형편없는 결과를 내는 거라고 생각합니다.\"\n\n그녀는 잠시 입을 다물고 있다가 미소를 지었다.\n\n\"맞아요, 맞아요.\" 그녀가 동의했다. \"저는 도무지 그럴 수가 없었어요. 끔찍한 계집애들로 가득 찬 보육원 하나를 통째로 사랑할 만큼 제 마음이 넉넉하질Je n'ai pas le cœur assez 못해요. 제겐 한 번도 그게 안 됐어요Cela ne m'a jamais réussi. 그런 식으로 사회적 지위une position sociale를 만들어 낸 여자들이 어찌나 많은지요. 그리고 지금은 그 어느 때보다도 더,\" 그녀는 겉으로는 오빠를 향하는 척했지만 그 말이 오직 레빈만을 겨냥한 것임이 분명한, 슬프고 속내를 털어놓는 듯한 표정으로 말했다, \"지금처럼 무언가 할 일이 절실히 필요한 때조차, 저는 그럴 수가 없어요.\" 그러고는 별안간 이맛살을 찌푸리며(레빈은 그녀가 자기 이야기를 한 자신을 향해 찌푸리고 있음을 알아챘다) 화제를 바꾸었다. \"당신에 대해 알고 있어요.\" 그녀가 레빈에게 말했다. \"당신이 공공심이 있는 시민은 아니라는 거 말이에요. 그래서 제가 힘닿는 데까지 당신을 변호해 드렸답니다.\"\n\n\"어떻게 저를 변호하셨는데요?\"\n\n\"아, 당신에게 쏟아지는 공격에 맞춰서요. 그건 그렇고, 차 좀 드시지 않겠어요?\" 그녀는 자리에서 일어나 모로코가죽으로 장정된 책 한 권을 집어 들었다.\n\n\"그건 제게 주십시오, 안나 아르카지예브나.\" 보르쿠예프가 그 책을 가리키며 말했다. \"충분히 다뤄 볼 만한 가치가 있는 작품입니다.\"\n\n\"아, 아니에요, 죄다 어설픈걸요.\"\n\n\"내가 그 사람한테 그 책 얘기를 했어.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 레빈 쪽으로 고갯짓하며 누이에게 말했다.\n\n\"그러지 말았어야지. 내 글 같은 건 리자 메르찰로바가 감옥에서 가져다 나한테 팔던 그 자그마한 바구니며 조각 세공 같은 거야. 그 사람이 그 협회에서 감옥 담당 일을 맡고 있었거든.\" 그녀가 레빈을 향해 몸을 돌렸다. \"그 가엾은 사람들이 만든 그 물건들은 인내심의 기적이었지요.\"\n\n그리하여 레빈은 자기를 그토록 유별나게 끌어당기는 이 여인에게서 새로운 면모를 발견했다. 재치며 우아함이며 아름다움 외에도, 그녀에게는 진실함이 있었다. 그녀는 자기 처지의 온갖 쓰라림을 그에게 숨기려 하지 않았다. 그렇게 말하면서 그녀는 한숨을 내쉬었고, 그녀의 얼굴은 별안간 굳은 표정을 띠며 마치 돌로 변한 것처럼 보였다. 그런 표정을 지을 때 그녀는 어느 때보다도 아름다웠지만, 그 표정은 새로운 것이었다. 화가가 초상화에 담아낸, 행복으로 빛나며 또 행복을 자아내던 그 표정과는 전혀 딴판이었다. 그녀가 오빠의 팔을 잡고 높다란 문 쪽으로 함께 걸어갈 때, 레빈은 초상화와 그녀의 모습을 몇 번이고 번갈아 보았고, 자기 자신도 놀랄 만큼 그녀에 대한 애틋함과 연민을 느꼈다.\n\n그녀는 레빈과 보르쿠예프에게 응접실로 가 있으라 청하고는, 자기는 남아 오빠에게 몇 마디 할 말이 있다고 했다. \"자기 이혼 얘기일까, 브론스키 얘기일까, 아니면 그가 클럽에서 무얼 하고 있는지, 혹은 내 얘기일까?\" 레빈은 궁금해했다. 그리고 그녀가 스테판 아르카지치에게 무슨 이야기를 하고 있을까 하는 물음에 너무나 골똘히 빠진 나머지, 안나 아르카지예브나가 쓴 어린이용 이야기의 장점에 대해 보르쿠예프가 들려주는 말을 거의 듣지 못했다.\n\n차를 마시는 동안에도 흥미로운 화제로 가득한, 그 똑같이 유쾌한 종류의 대화가 이어졌다. 무슨 이야기를 꺼낼지 찾아 헤매는 순간이라곤 단 한 번도 없었다. 오히려 하고 싶은 말을 다 할 시간이 모자랄 지경이어서, 다른 사람들이 무슨 말을 하는지 듣고 싶은 마음에 기꺼이 자기 말을 삼키곤 했다. 그리고 거기서 오간 모든 말은, 그녀뿐 아니라 보르쿠예프며 스테판 아르카지치가 한 말까지도—레빈에게는 그렇게 느껴졌다—그녀의 공감과 비평을 거치며 각별한 의미를 얻었다. 이 흥미로운 대화를 좇으면서도 레빈은 줄곧 그녀에게 감탄하고 있었다—그녀의 아름다움, 지성, 교양, 그리고 동시에 그녀의 솔직함과 진실로 깊은 감정에. 그는 듣고 또 말하면서도, 내내 그녀의 내면 생활을 생각하며 그녀의 감정을 헤아려 보려 애썼다. 지금껏 그토록 가혹하게 그녀를 판단해 왔으면서도, 이제는 어떤 묘한 사고의 사슬을 따라 그녀를 두둔하고 있었고, 또 그녀를 안쓰러워하며, 브론스키가 그녀를 온전히 이해하지 못하는 것은 아닐까 두려워했다. 열한 시가 되어 스테판 아르카지치가 가려고 일어섰을 때(보르쿠예프는 더 일찍 떠난 뒤였다), 레빈은 자기가 이제 막 도착한 것만 같았다. 레빈도 아쉬운 마음으로 일어섰다.\n\n\"안녕히 가세요.\" 그녀가 그의 손을 잡고 마음을 사로잡는 눈길로 그의 얼굴을 들여다보며 말했다. \"얼음이 깨져서que la glace est rompue 정말 기뻐요.\"\n\n그녀는 그의 손을 놓고 눈을 반쯤 감았다.\n\n\"부인께 전해 주세요, 제가 예전과 다름없이 그분을 사랑한다고요. 그리고 만약 그분이 제 처지를 용서하지 못하신다면, 저는 차라리 그분이 영영 그것을 용서하지 않기를 바란다고요. 그걸 용서하려면 제가 겪어 온 일을 똑같이 겪어야 하니, 부디 하느님께서 그분에게는 그것을 면하게 해 주시기를.\"\n\n\"물론입니다, 예, 전하겠습니다……\" 레빈이 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다.\n\n# Chapter 11\n\n\"정말 놀랍고, 다정하고, 불행한 여자야!\" 스테판 아르카지치와 함께 살을 에는 바깥 공기 속으로 나서며 그는 생각했다.\n\n\"그래, 내가 뭐라고 했나?\" 레빈이 완전히 마음을 빼앗긴 것을 보고 스테판 아르카지치가 말했다.\n\n\"맞아.\" 레빈이 꿈꾸듯 말했다. \"비범한 여자야! 머리가 좋다는 게 아니라, 감정의 깊이가 정말 놀랍더군. 그녀가 너무 안됐어!\"\n\n\"자, 이제 신의 가호로 모든 게 곧 해결될 걸세. 그래, 그래, 앞으로는 사람을 너무 모질게 판단하지 말게.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 마차 문을 열며 말했다. \"잘 가게. 우리는 가는 길이 다르니.\"\n\n여전히 안나를, 모든 것을, 그녀와 나눈 대화 속의 가장 단순한 말 한마디까지 떠올리고, 그녀의 표정에 일어난 아주 미세한 변화들까지 되새기며, 점점 더 그녀의 처지 속으로 들어가 그녀에게 연민을 느끼면서 레빈은 집에 다다랐다.\n\n집에서 쿠즈마는 레빈에게 카체리나 알렉산드로브나는 아주 잘 지내고 있으며 그녀의 자매들이 떠난 지 얼마 되지 않았다고 알리고는 편지 두 통을 건넸다. 레빈은 나중에 깜빡할까 봐 현관에서 곧장 그것들을 읽었다. 하나는 그의 집사인 소콜로프에게서 온 것이었다. 소콜로프는 곡물을 팔 수 없는 형편이며, 고작 5루블 반밖에 받지 못하는데 그 이상은 도무지 받아낼 수 없다고 썼다. 다른 편지는 누이에게서 온 것이었다. 그녀는 자기 일이 아직도 해결되지 않았다고 그를 나무랐다.\n\n\"음, 더 받을 수 없다면 5루블 반에라도 팔아야지.\" 레빈은 전에는 늘 그토록 무겁게만 여겨지던 첫 번째 문제를 그 자리에서 놀라우리만치 쉽게 결정해 버렸다. \"여기서는 어쩌면 이렇게 시간이 통째로 잡아먹히는지 놀랍군.\" 두 번째 편지를 생각하며 그는 마음속으로 중얼거렸다. 그는 누이가 부탁한 일을 처리하지 못한 것에 자책감을 느꼈다. \"오늘도 또 법원에 가지 못했어. 하지만 오늘은 정말 시간이 없었지.\" 그리고 내일은 반드시 처리하리라 다짐하며 그는 아내에게로 올라갔다. 방으로 들어가면서 레빈은 그날 하루를 마음속으로 빠르게 되짚어 보았다. 그날의 사건이라는 것은 죄다 대화였다. 그가 듣고 또 끼어들었던 대화들. 그 모든 대화는 만약 그가 집에 혼자 있었다면 결코 입에 올리지 않았을 화제들이었는데, 거기서는 무척 흥미로웠다. 그리고 그 모든 대화는 충분히 옳았으나, 다만 두 군데가 어딘가 옳지 않았다. 하나는 잉어에 관해 그가 했던 말이었고, 다른 하나는 안나에게 느낀 애틋한 연민 속에 무언가 \"온당치 못한\" 데가 있다는 것이었다.\n\n레빈은 아내가 풀이 죽고 시무룩해진 것을 보았다. 세 자매의 만찬은 아주 잘 치러졌지만, 그러고 나서 그들은 그를 기다리고 또 기다렸으며, 다들 따분해졌고, 자매들은 떠났으며, 그녀는 혼자 남겨졌던 것이다.\n\n\"그래서, 당신은 뭘 하고 있었어요?\" 그녀는 의심스러운 빛으로 다소 반짝이는 그의 눈을 똑바로 들여다보며 물었다. 그러나 그가 모든 것을 털어놓는 것을 방해하지 않으려고, 그녀는 그를 면밀히 살피는 기색을 감추고는 그가 저녁을 어떻게 보냈는지 이야기하는 것을 호의적인 미소를 띤 채 들어주었다.\n\n\"음, 브론스키를 만나서 정말 기뻤어. 그 사람 앞에서 아주 편안하고 자연스러웠거든. 알겠지만, 앞으로 그를 만나지 않으려고 애쓰긴 할 거야. 하지만 이 어색함이 다 끝나서 다행이야.\" 그는 말했고, 그를 만나지 않으려고 애쓴다면서 곧장 안나를 찾아갔던 것을 떠올리며 얼굴을 붉혔다. \"우리는 농민들이 술 마시는 이야기를 하지. 농민이 더 많이 마시는지, 우리 계급이 더 많이 마시는지 나는 모르겠어. 농민들은 명절에 마시지만…….\"\n\n그러나 키티는 농민들의 음주 습관을 논하는 데는 조금의 관심도 없었다. 그녀는 그가 얼굴을 붉히는 것을 보았고, 그 까닭을 알고 싶었다.\n\n\"그래서, 그다음엔 어디 갔어요?\"\n\n\"스티바가 안나 아르카지예브나를 보러 가자고 엄청나게 졸라 댔어.\"\n\n이 말을 하면서 레빈은 더욱 얼굴을 붉혔고, 안나를 보러 간 것이 옳은 일이었는지에 대한 그의 의혹은 단번에 결판이 났다. 그는 이제 그래서는 안 되었다는 것을 알았다.\n\n키티의 눈이 묘하게 커지더니 안나의 이름에 번뜩였다. 그러나 그녀는 안간힘을 다해 자제하며 감정을 숨기고 그를 속였다.\n\n\"어머!\" 그녀가 한 말은 그것뿐이었다.\n\n\"내가 간 것에 당신이 화내지 않을 거라고 믿어. 스티바가 부탁했고, 돌리도 바랐던 일이거든.\" 레빈이 말을 이었다.\n\n\"아, 아니에요!\" 그녀가 말했지만, 그는 그녀의 눈 속에서 자기에게 좋을 게 없는 어떤 긴장을 보았다.\n\n\"그녀는 아주 다정하고, 아주, 아주 불행하지만 좋은 여자야.\" 그는 안나에 관해, 그녀가 하는 일들에 관해, 그리고 그녀가 키티에게 전해 달라고 부탁한 말에 관해 이야기하며 말했다.\n\n\"네, 물론, 그분은 정말 가엾은 분이에요.\" 그가 이야기를 마치자 키티가 말했다. \"당신 편지는 누구한테서 온 거예요?\"\n\n그는 말해 주었고, 그녀의 차분한 어조를 믿고는 외투를 갈아입으러 갔다.\n\n돌아와 보니 키티는 여전히 그 안락의자에 앉아 있었다. 그가 다가가자 그녀는 그를 흘끗 보더니 흐느껴 울기 시작했다.\n\n\"왜? 무슨 일이야?\" 그는 무슨 일인지 미리 알면서도 물었다.\n\n\"당신은 그 가증스러운 여자를 사랑하고 있어요. 그 여자가 당신한테 마법을 걸었어요! 당신 눈에서 봤어요. 그래요, 그래! 이게 다 어떻게 되겠어요? 당신은 클럽에서 술을 마시고, 마시고 도박을 하더니, 그러고는 갔잖아요…… 하필이면 그 여자한테로! 안 돼요, 우리는 떠나야 해요…… 나는 내일 떠나겠어요.\"\n\n레빈이 아내를 달래기까지는 오랜 시간이 걸렸다. 마침내 그가 그녀를 진정시킬 수 있었던 것은, 연민의 감정이 마신 술과 겹쳐 자기로서는 도저히 감당이 안 됐고, 안나의 교묘한 영향력에 굴복하고 말았으며, 앞으로는 그녀를 피하겠다고 고백하고 나서였다. 좀 더 진심으로 인정한 것 하나는, 모스크바에서 오로지 대화와 먹고 마시는 일뿐인 생활을 그토록 오래 하다 보니 자기가 타락해 가고 있다는 점이었다. 그들은 새벽 세 시까지 이야기를 나누었다. 세 시가 되어서야 그들은 잠자리에 들 수 있을 만큼 충분히 화해했다.\n\n# Chapter 12\n\n손님들을 배웅한 뒤에도 안나는 자리에 앉지 않고 방 안을 이리저리 거닐기 시작했다. 그녀는 저녁 내내 무의식적으로 레빈의 마음속에 사랑의 감정을 불러일으키려고 온 힘을 다했다—요즈음 들어 그녀는 모든 젊은 남자들에게 그렇게 하곤 했다—그리고 결혼한 데다 양심적인 남자를 상대로 하룻저녁에 가능한 한도 안에서 자기 목적을 이루었음을 알고 있었다. 사실 그녀는 그가 무척 마음에 들었고, 남자의 관점에서 볼 때 브론스키와 레빈 사이에 두드러진 차이가 있음에도, 한 여자로서 그녀는 두 사람에게 공통된 무언가를 보았다. 바로 그것이 키티로 하여금 두 사람을 다 사랑할 수 있게 했던 것이다. 그러나 그가 방을 나서자마자 그녀는 그에 대한 생각을 멈추었다.\n\n한 가지 생각, 오직 한 가지 생각만이 갖가지 형태로 그녀를 쫓아다니며 떨쳐 내기를 거부했다. '내가 다른 사람들에게, 제 집과 아내를 사랑하는 이 남자에게 이토록 영향을 미칠 수 있다면, 어째서 그이는 나에게 이토록 차가운 걸까?…… 차갑다는 건 정확한 말이 아니지, 그이는 나를 사랑해, 그건 내가 알아! 하지만 이제 뭔가 새로운 것이 우리를 갈라놓고 있어. 왜 그이는 저녁 내내 여기 없었을까? 스티바를 시켜 야시빈을 두고 떠날 수 없으니 그의 노름을 지켜봐야 한다고 전하라고 했지. 야시빈이 어린애야? 하지만 그게 사실이라고 치자. 그이는 결코 거짓말을 하지 않으니까. 하지만 그게 사실이라면 거기엔 또 다른 무언가가 있어. 그이는 자기에게 다른 의무가 있다는 걸 나에게 보여 줄 기회를 반긴 거야. 그건 나도 알고, 그것에 따르겠어. 하지만 왜 그걸 나에게 증명하려는 걸까? 그이는 나에 대한 사랑이 자기 자유를 방해해선 안 된다는 걸 보여 주고 싶은 거야. 하지만 나는 증명 따위는 필요 없어, 나는 사랑이 필요해. 그이는 이곳 모스크바에서 내가 겪는 이 삶의 온갖 쓰라림을 이해해야 마땅해. 이게 사는 건가? 나는 살고 있는 게 아니야, 끊임없이 미루어지고 또 미루어지는 어떤 사건을 기다리고 있을 뿐이지. 또다시 답이 없어! 그리고 스티바는 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에게 갈 수 없다고 하고. 나는 다시 편지를 쓸 수도 없어. 나는 아무것도 할 수 없고, 아무것도 시작할 수 없고, 아무것도 바꿀 수 없어. 나는 나를 억누르고, 기다리며, 나 자신을 위한 소일거리를 만들어 내지—영국인 가족이며, 글쓰기며, 독서며—하지만 그건 전부 거짓에 지나지 않아, 전부 모르핀이나 마찬가지야. 그이는 나를 가엾게 여겨야 마땅해.' 그녀는 자기 연민의 눈물이 두 눈에 차오르는 것을 느끼며 그렇게 생각했다.\n\n그녀는 브론스키가 거칠게 초인종을 울리는 소리를 듣고 황급히 눈물을 닦았다—눈물을 닦았을 뿐 아니라 등불 곁에 앉아 책을 펼치고는 침착한 척했다. 그녀는 그가 약속한 대로 집에 돌아오지 않은 데 대해 자기가 언짢아하고 있음을 그에게 보여 주고 싶었다—언짢아하는 것뿐이지, 무슨 일이 있어도 자신의 괴로움을, 그중에서도 특히 자기 연민을 그에게 들켜서는 안 되었다. 자기 자신은 스스로를 가엾게 여길 수 있어도, 그가 그녀를 가엾게 여겨서는 안 되었다. 그녀는 다툼을 원하지 않았고, 다투려 한다며 그를 탓했지만, 무의식적으로 자신을 적대의 태세로 몰아넣었다.\n\n\"음, 지루하지는 않았소?\" 그가 그녀에게 다가오며 들뜨고 기분 좋은 목소리로 말했다. \"노름이라는 게 얼마나 무서운 정념인지!\"\n\n\"아뇨, 지루하지 않았어요. 지루해하지 않는 법은 오래전에 배웠으니까요. 스티바가 왔다 갔고 레빈도요.\"\n\n\"그래, 두 사람이 당신을 보러 오겠다고 했지. 그래, 레빈은 마음에 들었소?\" 그가 그녀 곁에 앉으며 말했다.\n\n\"아주 마음에 들었어요. 떠난 지 얼마 안 됐어요. 야시빈은 뭘 하고 있었나요?\"\n\n\"따고 있었지—만 칠천 루블을. 내가 그를 끌어냈소. 그가 정말로 집으로 향하던 참이었는데, 다시 돌아갔고, 지금은 잃고 있어.\"\n\n\"그럼 당신은 뭐 하러 남아 있었던 거예요?\" 그녀가 갑자기 두 눈을 들어 그를 바라보며 물었다. 그녀의 얼굴 표정은 차갑고 매정했다. \"당신은 스티바한테 야시빈을 끌어내려고 남아 있는 거라고 했잖아요. 그런데 그를 거기 두고 왔고요.\"\n\n차갑게 다툼에 응할 준비가 되었다는 똑같은 표정이 그의 얼굴에도 떠올랐다.\n\n\"우선, 나는 그에게 당신한테 어떤 말을 전해 달라고 부탁한 적이 없소. 그리고 둘째로, 나는 결코 거짓말을 하지 않아. 하지만 무엇보다 핵심은, 내가 남아 있고 싶었고 그래서 남아 있었다는 거요.\" 그가 미간을 찌푸리며 말했다. \"안나, 대체 왜 이러는 거요, 왜 자꾸 이러느냔 말이오?\" 그가 잠시 침묵하다 그녀 쪽으로 몸을 굽히며 말했고, 그녀가 자기 손에 손을 얹어 주기를 바라며 손바닥을 폈다.\n\n그녀는 다정함을 청하는 이 몸짓이 반가웠다. 그러나 어떤 기이한 악의 힘이 그녀로 하여금 자기 감정에 자신을 내맡기지 못하게 했다. 마치 전쟁의 규칙이 그녀에게 항복을 허락하지 않는 것 같았다.\n\n\"물론 당신은 남아 있고 싶었고, 그래서 남아 있었겠죠. 당신은 하고 싶은 건 다 하니까. 하지만 그걸 나한테 왜 말하는 거예요? 무슨 목적으로요?\" 그녀가 점점 더 흥분하며 말했다. \"누가 당신 권리에 시비를 거나요? 하지만 당신은 옳은 사람이 되고 싶은 거죠, 그럼 마음껏 옳은 사람이 되시든가요.\"\n\n그의 손이 오므라들었고, 그는 고개를 돌렸으며, 그의 얼굴에는 한층 더 고집스러운 표정이 떠올랐다.\n\n\"당신에게 이건 고집의 문제예요.\" 그녀가 그를 뚫어지게 바라보다가 자기를 화나게 한 그 표정을 가리킬 알맞은 말을 문득 찾아내고는 말했다. \"그저 고집이죠. 당신에게 이건 나에 대해 우위를 지키느냐 마느냐의 문제지만, 나에게는…….\" 다시금 그녀는 자기 자신이 가엾어져 거의 울음을 터뜨릴 뻔했다. \"당신이 이게 나에게 어떤 건지 안다면! 지금 내가 느끼는 것처럼 당신이 나에게 적대적이라고, 그래요, 적대적이라고 느낄 때, 이게 나에게 무슨 의미인지 당신이 안다면! 바로 이 순간 내가 얼마나 재앙의 벼랑 끝에 서 있다고 느끼는지, 내가 나 자신을 얼마나 두려워하는지 당신이 안다면!\" 그러고는 흐느낌을 감추며 고개를 돌렸다.\n\n\"하지만 대체 무슨 말을 하는 거요?\" 절망에 찬 그녀의 표정에 질겁한 그가 말하고는, 다시 그녀에게로 몸을 굽혀 그녀의 손을 잡고 입을 맞추었다. \"왜 이러는 거요? 내가 우리 집 밖에서 즐길 거리를 찾기라도 한단 말이오? 내가 여자들과 어울리는 걸 피하지 않소?\"\n\n\"그래요, 맞아요! 그게 전부라면 얼마나 좋겠어요!\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"자, 당신 마음을 편안하게 해 주려면 내가 어떻게 해야 하는지 말해 보오. 당신을 행복하게 해 줄 수 있다면 나는 무엇이든 할 준비가 되어 있소.\" 그녀의 절망에 찬 표정에 마음이 움직인 그가 말했다. \"지금처럼 어떤 종류의 괴로움에서든 당신을 구할 수만 있다면 내가 못 할 일이 뭐가 있겠소, 안나!\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"아무것도 아니에요, 아무것도!\" 그녀가 말했다. \"혼자 지내는 생활 탓인지, 신경 탓인지 나도 잘 모르겠어요…… 자, 이 얘기는 그만해요. 경마는 어떻게 됐어요? 아직 얘기 안 해 줬잖아요!\" 그녀는 어쨌든 자기 쪽으로 기운 승리에 대한 의기양양함을 감추려 애쓰며 물었다.\n\n그는 야식을 청하고는 경마 이야기를 들려주기 시작했다. 그러나 그녀는 그의 어조에서, 점점 더 차가워지는 그의 눈에서, 그가 자기의 승리를 용서하지 않았음을, 그녀가 맞서 싸워 온 그 고집의 감정이 그의 안에서 다시 고개를 들었음을 알아챘다. 그는 마치 자기가 항복한 것을 후회하기라도 하듯 전보다 더 그녀에게 차가웠다. 그리고 그녀는 자기에게 승리를 안겨 준 그 말, \"내가 얼마나 재앙의 벼랑 끝에 서 있다고 느끼는지, 내가 나 자신을 얼마나 두려워하는지\"라는 말을 떠올리며, 이 무기가 위험한 것이며, 두 번째로는 쓸 수 없는 것임을 깨달았다. 그리고 그녀는 두 사람을 묶어 주는 사랑 곁에, 그의 마음에서도 몰아낼 수 없고 자기 자신의 마음에서는 더더욱 몰아낼 수 없는 어떤 악한 다툼의 영(靈)이 두 사람 사이에 자라났음을 느꼈다.\n\n# Chapter 13\n\n인간이 익숙해지지 못할 처지란 없다. 더욱이 주위 사람들이 모두 그와 똑같이 살아가는 것을 본다면 더욱 그렇다. 석 달 전이었다면 레빈은 그날 자기가 처한 상황 속에서 태평하게 잠들 수 있으리라고는 믿지 못했을 것이다. 목표도 없고 분별도 없는 삶을 살고, 분수에 넘치게 돈을 쓰고, 과음을 하고(클럽에서 있었던 일을 달리 표현할 길이 없었다), 한때 아내가 사랑했던 남자와 어울리지 않게 가까운 관계를 맺고, 그보다 더 어울리지 않게 타락한 여자라고밖에 부를 수 없는 여인을 찾아가고, 그 여자에게 마음을 빼앗겨 아내를 괴롭게 하고서도—여전히 태평하게 잠들 수 있으리라고는. 하지만 피로와 잠 못 이룬 밤과 마신 술의 영향으로 그의 잠은 깊고 평온했다.\n\n다섯 시에 문 열리는 삐걱 소리가 그를 깨웠다. 그는 벌떡 일어나 주위를 둘러보았다. 키티가 옆자리에 없었다. 그러나 칸막이 뒤에서 불빛이 움직이고 있었고, 그녀의 발소리가 들렸다.\n\n\"무슨 일이야?…… 무슨 일이야?\" 그가 비몽사몽 중에 말했다. \"키티! 무슨 일이야?\"\n\n\"아무것도 아니에요.\" 그녀가 손에 촛불을 들고 칸막이 뒤에서 나오며 말했다. \"몸이 좀 안 좋았어요.\" 그녀는 유난히 달콤하고 의미심장한 미소를 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"뭐? 시작된 거야?\" 그가 겁에 질려 말했다. \"사람을 보내야…….\" 그러고는 황급히 옷을 더듬어 찾았다.\n\n\"아니, 아니에요.\" 그녀가 미소를 지으며 그의 손을 잡았다. \"분명 아무것도 아닐 거예요. 좀 안 좋았을 뿐이에요, 아주 조금. 이제 다 괜찮아요.\"\n\n그러고는 침대로 들어와 촛불을 끄고 누워 가만히 있었다. 그는 마치 그녀가 숨을 죽이고 있기라도 한 듯 그 고요함이 수상쩍게 느껴졌고, 칸막이 뒤에서 나오며 \"아무것도 아니에요\"라고 말할 때의 그 유난히 다정하고 들뜬 표정은 더욱 수상쩍게 느껴졌지만, 너무도 졸린 나머지 곧바로 잠들어 버렸다. 나중에야 그는 그녀의 고른 숨소리를 떠올렸고, 그녀가 미동도 없이 그의 곁에 누워, 한 여인의 삶에서 가장 위대한 사건을 기다리는 동안, 그 사랑스럽고 소중한 가슴속에서 무슨 일이 일어나고 있었을지를 깨달았다. 일곱 시에 그는 그의 어깨에 닿는 그녀의 손길과 부드러운 속삭임에 잠을 깼다. 그녀는 그를 깨운 것을 미안해하는 마음과 그에게 말을 하고 싶은 마음 사이에서 갈등하는 듯했다.\n\n\"코스챠, 놀라지 마세요. 괜찮아요. 그런데 아무래도…… 리자베타 페트로브나를 부르러 보내야 할 것 같아요.\"\n\n촛불이 다시 켜졌다. 그녀는 침대에 일어나 앉아, 지난 며칠 동안 부지런히 뜨고 있던 뜨개질감을 손에 들고 있었다.\n\n\"제발, 놀라지 마세요, 괜찮아요. 저는 조금도 무섭지 않아요.\" 그녀가 그의 겁먹은 얼굴을 보며 말했고, 그의 손을 자기 가슴에 댔다가 입술로 가져갔다.\n\n그는 잠에서 채 깨지도 못한 채 황급히 벌떡 일어났고, 실내복을 걸치면서 그녀에게서 눈을 떼지 못했다. 그러다 멈춰 서서 여전히 그녀를 바라보았다. 가야만 했지만 그는 그녀의 눈에서 자신을 떼어 낼 수가 없었다. 그는 자기가 그녀의 얼굴을 사랑하고, 그녀의 표정을, 그녀의 눈을 안다고 여겼지만, 이런 모습은 한 번도 본 적이 없었다. 어제 그가 그녀에게 안겨 준 괴로움을 떠올리니, 자기 자신이 얼마나 가증스럽고 끔찍하게 느껴지던지. 나이트캡 아래로 부드럽게 곱슬거리는 머리카락이 드리워진 그녀의 발갛게 상기된 얼굴은 기쁨과 용기로 빛나고 있었다.\n\n키티의 성격에는 본래 복잡하거나 꾸민 데가 거의 없었지만, 별안간 모든 가식이 벗겨지고 그녀 영혼의 바로 그 알맹이가 두 눈에 빛나는 지금, 드러난 그 모습에 레빈은 충격을 받았다. 그리고 이 단순함과 영혼의 적나라함 속에서, 그가 그녀에게서 사랑하던 바로 그 여인이 그 어느 때보다 또렷하게 드러났다. 그녀는 미소를 지으며 그를 바라보았다. 그런데 별안간 그녀의 눈썹이 씰룩거렸고, 그녀는 고개를 젖히더니 재빨리 그에게 다가와 그의 손을 움켜쥐고 그에게 바짝 붙어 뜨거운 숨을 그에게 내쉬었다. 그녀는 고통스러워하며, 말하자면 자기 괴로움을 그에게 하소연하고 있었다. 그리고 첫 순간에는 습관처럼, 그에게는 자기가 잘못한 것만 같았다. 그러나 그녀의 눈에는 그녀가 결코 그를 탓하지 않으며, 자기 괴로움 때문에 오히려 그를 사랑한다는 것을 말해 주는 다정함이 어려 있었다. '내가 아니라면, 대체 누가 이 일에 책임이 있단 말인가?' 그는 무의식중에, 벌하기 위해 이 괴로움에 책임 있는 누군가를 찾으며 생각했다. 그러나 책임 있는 자는 아무도 없었다. 그녀는 괴로워하고, 하소연하고, 자기 괴로움 속에서 의기양양해하고, 그것을 기뻐하고, 그것을 사랑하고 있었다. 그는 그녀의 영혼 속에서 무언가 숭고한 일이 이루어지고 있음을 보았다. 그러나 무엇일까? 그는 도무지 알 수 없었다. 그것은 그의 이해를 넘어서는 일이었다.\n\n\"엄마한테는 사람을 보냈어요. 당신은 어서 리자베타 페트로브나를 데리러 가세요…… 코스챠!…… 아무것도 아니에요, 다 지나갔어요.\"\n\n그녀는 그에게서 떨어져 벨을 울렸다.\n\n\"자, 이제 가세요. 파샤가 오고 있어요. 저는 괜찮아요.\"\n\n그리고 레빈은 그녀가 밤중에 가지고 들어왔던 뜨개질감을 다시 집어 들고 또다시 뜨기 시작하는 것을 보고는 깜짝 놀랐다.\n\n레빈이 한쪽 문으로 나가는데, 다른 쪽 문으로 하녀가 들어오는 소리가 들렸다. 그는 문가에 서서, 키티가 하녀에게 정확하게 지시를 내리고 침대 옮기는 일을 거들기 시작하는 소리를 들었다.\n\n그는 옷을 입었고, 말을 마차에 매는 동안—아직 삯마차 썰매는 보이지 않았다—다시 침실로 뛰어 올라갔는데, 발끝으로 가는 것이 아니라 날아가는 것 같은 기분이었다. 하녀 둘이 침실에서 무언가를 조심스럽게 옮기고 있었다.\n\n키티는 빠르게 뜨개질을 하며 이리저리 돌아다니며 지시를 내리고 있었다.\n\n\"나는 의사를 부르러 갈게. 리자베타 페트로브나한테는 사람을 보냈지만, 나도 거기 들렀다 올게. 필요한 거 없어? 그래, 돌리한테 갈까?\"\n\n그녀는 그를 보았지만, 분명 그가 하는 말을 듣고 있지 않았다.\n\n\"네, 네. 다녀오세요.\" 그녀가 미간을 찌푸리고 그에게 손을 흔들며 재빨리 말했다.\n\n그가 막 응접실로 들어섰을 때, 침실에서 별안간 애처로운 신음 소리가 들려왔다가 이내 잦아들었다. 그는 우뚝 멈춰 섰고, 한참 동안 영문을 알 수 없었다.\n\n'그래, 저건 그녀야.' 그는 혼잣말을 하고는 머리를 움켜쥐고 아래층으로 뛰어 내려갔다.\n\n\"주여, 우리에게 자비를 베푸소서! 우리를 용서하소서! 우리를 도우소서!\" 그는 무슨 까닭인지 별안간 입술에 떠오른 말을 되뇌었다. 그리고 신을 믿지 않는 그가 이 말을 입술로만 되뇌는 것이 아니었다. 그 순간 그는, 자기 안에서 스스로 의식하고 있던 온갖 의심도, 심지어 이성으로는 믿을 수 없다는 사실조차도, 그가 신에게 의지하는 것을 조금도 가로막지 못한다는 것을 알았다. 그 모든 것이 이제 먼지처럼 그의 영혼에서 떠나가 버렸다. 자기 자신과 자기 영혼과 자기 사랑이 그분의 손안에 있음을 느끼는 지금, 그분이 아니라면 누구에게 의지한단 말인가?\n\n말은 아직 준비되지 않았지만, 그는 해야 할 일에 자기 육체의 힘과 지력이 유달리 집중되는 것을 느끼며, 말을 기다리지 않고 걸어서 나섰고, 쿠즈마에게 뒤따라오라고 일렀다.\n\n길모퉁이에서 그는 황급히 마차를 모는 야간 마부와 마주쳤다. 작은 썰매 안에는 벨벳 외투를 두른 리자베타 페트로브나가 머리에 두건을 감고 앉아 있었다. \"고맙기도 하지! 고맙기도 해!\" 그는 유난히 진지하다 못해 엄숙하기까지 한 표정을 띤 그녀의 작고 희끄무레한 얼굴을 알아보고는 기쁨에 차 말했다. 마부에게 멈추지 말라고 이르고는, 그녀의 곁을 따라 달렸다.\n\n\"그럼 두 시간이라고요? 그보다 길지는 않고요?\" 그녀가 물었다. \"표트르 드미트리예비치한테 알리시되, 서두르게 하지는 마세요. 그리고 약국에 가서 아편을 좀 사 오세요.\"\n\n\"그럼 잘될 거라고 보시는 거죠? 주여, 우리에게 자비를 베푸시고 우리를 도우소서!\" 레빈은 자기 말이 대문을 나서는 것을 보며 말했다. 쿠즈마 곁의 썰매에 뛰어올라, 그는 의사의 집으로 가자고 일렀다.\n\n# Chapter 14\n\n의사는 아직 일어나지 않았고, 하인은 \"선생님이 늦게 주무셨고 깨우지 말라고 분부하셨지만 곧 일어나실 겁니다.\"라고 말했다. 하인은 램프 등피를 닦고 있었는데 그 일에 무척 몰두해 있는 듯했다. 하인이 자기 램프에만 이토록 집중해 있고 레빈에게 일어난 일에는 무관심하다는 사실이 처음에는 그를 어리둥절하게 했지만, 곧 그 문제를 헤아려 보고는, 아무도 자기 심정을 알지 못하고 또 알아야 할 의무도 없으며, 그렇기에 이 무관심의 벽을 뚫고 목적을 이루려면 더더욱 침착하고 분별 있고 단호하게 행동할 필요가 있다는 것을 깨달았다.\n\n\"서두르지 말고, 어떤 것도 놓치지 말자.\" 레빈은 자기 앞에 놓인 해야 할 모든 일에 대해 점점 더 강해지는 육체적 활력과 주의력을 느끼며 혼잣말을 했다.\n\n의사가 일어나지 않으리라는 것을 확인한 레빈은 여러 가지 방안을 궁리한 끝에 다음과 같이 정했다. 쿠지마는 다른 의사를 부르러 가고, 자기는 약국에 아편을 사러 가며, 돌아왔을 때도 의사가 아직 일어나지 않았으면 하인에게 돈을 찔러주든 힘을 쓰든 무슨 수를 써서라도 의사를 깨우기로 했다.\n\n약국에서는 키만 멀쑥한 점원이 기다리고 서 있는 한 마부에게 줄 가루약 봉지를 봉하고 있었는데, 의사의 하인이 램프 등피를 닦던 것과 똑같은 무신경함으로 그에게 아편을 내주기를 거절했다. 레빈은 허둥대거나 화내지 않으려 애쓰며 의사와 산파의 이름을 대고, 아편이 무엇에 필요한지 설명하며 그를 설득하려 했다. 점원은 독일어로 내주어도 되겠느냐고 물었고, 칸막이 너머에서 그렇게 하라는 대답을 듣자 병 하나와 깔때기를 꺼내 큰 병에서 작은 병으로 천천히 아편을 따르고는 상표를 붙이고, 그러지 말아 달라는 레빈의 부탁에도 아랑곳없이 봉인까지 한 다음, 그것을 포장하려고까지 했다. 이것만은 레빈도 더 참을 수가 없었다. 그는 점원의 손에서 병을 단호히 빼앗아 큰 유리문 쪽으로 달려갔다. 의사는 여전히 일어나지 않았고, 이제 깔개를 깔며 분주한 하인은 그를 깨우려 하지 않았다. 레빈은 천천히 십 루블짜리 지폐를 꺼내, 일 처리에 시간을 끌지 않으면서도 또박또박 말하려고 신경 쓰며, 그에게 지폐를 건네고는 표트르 드미트리치(전에는 그의 눈에 그토록 하찮게 보였던 이 표트르 드미트리치가 지금은 얼마나 위대하고 중요한 인물로 여겨지는지!)가 언제든 오겠다고 약속했으며, 절대 화내지 않을 것이니 지금 당장 깨워야 한다고 설명했다.\n\n하인은 동의했고, 레빈을 대기실로 안내한 뒤 위층으로 올라갔다.\n\n레빈은 문 너머로 의사가 기침을 하고 이리저리 움직이며 세수를 하고 무언가 말하는 소리를 들을 수 있었다. 삼 분이 지났다. 레빈에게는 한 시간도 더 흐른 것만 같았다. 그는 더 이상 기다릴 수 없었다.\n\n\"표트르 드미트리치, 표트르 드미트리치!\" 그는 열린 문가에서 애원하는 목소리로 말했다. \"제발 용서하십시오! 그냥 그대로 좀 나와 주십시오. 벌써 두 시간도 넘게 이러고 있단 말입니다.\"\n\n\"금방, 금방 갑니다!\" 한 목소리가 대답했고, 놀랍게도 레빈은 의사가 말하면서 미소 짓고 있다는 것을 알아챘다.\n\n\"잠깐만이라도요.\"\n\n\"금방 갑니다.\"\n\n의사가 부츠를 신는 동안 다시 이 분이 지났고, 외투를 걸치고 머리를 빗는 동안 또 이 분이 지났다.\n\n\"표트르 드미트리치!\" 레빈이 다시 하소연하는 목소리로 입을 떼려는데, 마침 의사가 옷을 차려입고 준비를 마친 채 들어왔다. \"이 사람들은 양심이라곤 없어.\" 레빈은 생각했다. \"우리는 죽어 가는데 머리나 빗고 있다니!\"\n\n\"안녕하십니까!\" 의사가 그에게 악수를 청하며, 마치 그 침착함으로 그를 약 올리기라도 하듯 말했다. \"서두를 것 없습니다. 자, 어떻습니까?\"\n\n레빈은 되도록 정확하게 말하려 애쓰며 아내의 상태를 시시콜콜한 데까지 죄다 늘어놓기 시작했고, 이야기를 하다 말고 거듭 당장 같이 가 달라고 애원했다.\n\n\"아, 서두르실 것 없습니다. 모르셔서 그러는데요, 분명 제가 필요하지도 않을 겁니다. 그래도 약속을 했으니, 원하신다면 가지요. 하지만 급할 것 없어요. 자, 앉으세요. 커피 한잔 드시겠습니까?\"\n\n레빈은 자기를 놀리는 것이냐고 묻는 듯한 눈으로 그를 빤히 바라보았지만, 의사는 그를 놀릴 생각 따위는 전혀 없었다.\n\n\"압니다, 알아요.\" 의사가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"저도 결혼한 몸이지요. 이런 순간이면 우리 남편들은 참 가엾은 신세랍니다. 제 환자 중에는 이럴 때면 남편이 늘 마구간으로 피신해 버리는 집도 있지요.\"\n\n\"하지만 어떻게 보십니까, 표트르 드미트리치? 다 잘되리라고 생각하십니까?\"\n\n\"모든 정황이 좋은 결과를 가리키고 있습니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 곧장 가시는 거죠?\" 레빈이 커피를 들고 들어오는 하인을 노한 눈으로 쏘아보며 말했다.\n\n\"한 시간 안에요.\"\n\n\"오, 제발 좀!\"\n\n\"아무튼 커피나 좀 마시게 해 주십시오.\"\n\n의사는 커피를 마시기 시작했다. 두 사람 다 말이 없었다.\n\n\"그런데 터키군이 정말로 지고 있더군요. 어제 전보 보셨습니까?\" 의사가 빵을 우물거리며 말했다.\n\n\"아니요, 더는 못 참겠습니다!\" 레빈이 벌떡 일어나며 말했다. \"그럼 십오 분 안에 우리 집으로 와 주시는 거죠.\"\n\n\"삼십 분 안에요.\"\n\n\"명예를 걸고요?\"\n\n레빈이 집에 도착했을 때 마침 공작 부인도 같은 시각에 마차를 타고 왔고, 두 사람은 함께 침실 문으로 올라갔다. 공작 부인의 눈에는 눈물이 고여 있었고 손은 떨리고 있었다. 레빈을 보자 그녀는 그를 끌어안고 울음을 터뜨렸다.\n\n\"자, 어떤가요, 리자베타 페트로브나?\" 그녀가 환하면서도 근심 어린 얼굴로 그들을 맞으러 나온 산파의 손을 잡으며 물었다.\n\n\"잘 진행되고 있어요.\" 산파가 말했다. \"아씨께 좀 누우시라고 권해 주세요. 그게 한결 편하실 거예요.\"\n\n잠에서 깨어 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지 알게 된 순간부터, 레빈은 앞에 닥친 일을 단호히 견뎌 내고, 이런저런 생각이나 예측을 하지 않으며, 아내를 동요시키지 않고 오히려 그녀를 달래며 용기를 북돋워 주리라 마음을 다잡았다. 앞으로 어떻게 될지, 어떻게 끝날지조차 생각하지 않으려 애쓰며, 이런 산고가 보통 얼마나 오래 가는지 물어본 바에 비추어, 레빈은 다섯 시간 동안 굳세게 버티고 감정을 단단히 틀어쥐겠다고 마음속으로 각오를 다졌고, 그것은 자기로서도 해낼 수 있을 것만 같았다. 그러나 의사에게서 돌아와 다시 그녀의 고통을 보자, 그는 점점 더 자주 되뇌기 시작했다. \"주여, 우리를 불쌍히 여기시고, 우리를 도우소서!\" 그는 한숨을 쉬며 고개를 번쩍 쳐들었고, 자기가 이것을 견뎌 내지 못할 것 같다는, 그만 울음을 터뜨리거나 달아나 버릴 것 같다는 두려움에 휩싸이기 시작했다. 그에게는 그것이 그토록 큰 고통이었다. 그런데 이제 겨우 한 시간이 지났을 뿐이었다.\n\n그러나 그 한 시간이 지나고 다시 한 시간이, 두 시간이, 세 시간이 지나고, 그가 자기 고통의 마지막 한계로 정해 둔 다섯 시간이 꼬박 다 지나도록 상황은 여전히 그대로였다. 그리고 그는 견디는 것 말고는 달리 할 수 있는 일이 없었기에 여전히 견디고 있었으며, 매 순간 자기 인내의 한계에 다다랐다고, 동정과 고통으로 심장이 터져 버릴 것만 같다고 느꼈다.\n\n그러나 여전히 분이 지나고 시간이 흐르고, 또 시간이 더 흘러갔으며, 그의 비참함과 공포는 점점 더 커져 더욱더 격렬해졌다.\n\n그 무엇에 대해서도 도무지 아무런 관념을 가질 수 없게 만드는, 삶의 온갖 일상적인 조건이 레빈에게는 더 이상 존재하지 않게 되었다. 그는 시간 감각을 완전히 잃어버렸다. 몇 분—그녀가 그를 부르고 그가 그녀의 축축한 손을 잡으면 그 손이 엄청난 힘으로 그의 손을 움켜쥐었다가 다시 밀쳐 내던 그 몇 분—은 그에게 몇 시간처럼 여겨졌고, 몇 시간은 또 몇 분처럼 여겨졌다. 리자베타 페트로브나가 그에게 칸막이 뒤에서 촛불을 켜 달라고 부탁했을 때, 그는 오후 다섯 시라는 것을 알고는 놀랐다. 아직 아침 열 시밖에 안 되었다고 했더라도 더 놀라지는 않았을 것이다. 그동안 자기가 어디에 있었는지, 그는 무슨 일이 언제 일어났는지만큼이나 알지 못했다. 그는 그녀의 부어오른 얼굴을 보았는데, 어떤 때는 어리둥절하고 고통에 차 있었고 어떤 때는 미소 지으며 그를 안심시키려 애썼다. 그는 늙은 공작 부인도 보았는데, 얼굴이 벌게져 잔뜩 흥분한 채 잿빛 곱슬머리는 헝클어졌고 입술을 깨물며 억지로 눈물을 삼키고 있었다. 그는 돌리와 살찐 담배를 피우는 의사, 그리고 굳고 단호하며 안심시키는 듯한 얼굴을 한 리자베타 페트로브나, 그리고 찌푸린 얼굴로 홀을 오락가락 거니는 늙은 공작도 보았다. 그러나 그들이 왜 들어왔다 나가는지, 그들이 어디에 있는지 그는 알지 못했다. 공작 부인은 의사와 함께 침실에 있다가, 그다음에는 어느새 저녁 식탁이 차려진 서재에 있었고, 그러다 보면 그녀는 거기에 없고 돌리가 있었다. 그러다 레빈은 자기가 어딘가로 심부름을 보내졌던 것을 떠올렸다. 한번은 탁자와 소파를 옮기라는 심부름을 받았다. 그는 그것이 그녀를 위해 해야 할 일이라고 여기며 열심히 했는데, 나중에야 그것이 자기가 잘 자리를 준비하던 것이었음을 알았다. 그다음에는 의사에게 무언가를 물어보러 서재로 보내졌다. 의사는 대답을 하고는 시의회의 부정에 관해 무슨 이야기를 했다. 그다음에는 늙은 공작 부인이 은과 금으로 장식된 성화를 옮기는 것을 도우러 침실로 보내졌고, 공작 부인의 늙은 시녀와 함께 그것에 손이 닿으려고 선반 위로 기어올랐다가 작은 등을 깨뜨렸으며, 늙은 하녀는 등에 대해서도 그의 아내에 대해서도 그를 안심시키려 애썼고, 그는 성화를 들어 키티의 머리맡에 가져다 놓으며 베개 뒤에 조심스럽게 끼워 넣었다. 그러나 이 모든 일이 어디서, 언제, 왜 일어났는지 그는 도무지 알 수 없었다. 그는 늙은 공작 부인이 왜 자기 손을 잡고 가엾다는 듯 그를 바라보며 너무 걱정하지 말라고 당부하는지, 돌리가 왜 무언가 좀 먹으라고 권하며 그를 방 밖으로 데리고 나가는지, 심지어 의사마저 왜 진지하고 측은한 눈길로 그를 바라보며 무슨 물약 같은 것을 한 방울 권하는지 이해하지 못했다.\n\n그가 알고 느끼는 것이라곤, 지금 벌어지고 있는 이 일이 거의 일 년 전 어느 지방 소도시의 여관에서 형 니콜라이의 임종 자리에서 일어났던 일과 같다는 것뿐이었다. 그러나 그것은 슬픔이었고 이것은 기쁨이었다. 하지만 그 슬픔도 이 기쁨도 똑같이 삶의 온갖 일상적인 조건 바깥에 있었다. 그것들은 말하자면 그 일상적인 삶에 난 틈새 같은 것이어서, 그 틈새를 통해 무언가 숭고한 것이 언뜻언뜻 비쳐 들었다. 그리고 이 숭고한 무언가를 응시하는 가운데 영혼은 이전에는 한 번도 헤아려 본 적 없는, 상상할 수도 없는 높이로 고양되었고, 이성은 그 뒤에 처져 도무지 따라잡지 못했다.\n\n\"주여, 우리를 불쌍히 여기시고, 우리를 도우소서!\" 그는 끊임없이 혼자 되뇌었고, 종교로부터 오래, 그리고 보기에는 완전히 멀어졌음에도, 자기가 어린 시절과 첫 청년기에 그랬던 것과 똑같이 미덥고 단순하게 하느님께 돌아서고 있음을 느꼈다.\n\n이 시간 내내 그에게는 뚜렷이 구분되는 두 가지 정신 상태가 있었다. 하나는 그녀에게서 떨어져, 살찐 담배를 하나 또 하나 끊임없이 피워 대고는 가득 찬 재떨이 가장자리에 비벼 끄는 의사와, 돌리와, 늙은 공작과 함께 있을 때였는데, 거기서는 저녁 식사며 정치며 마리야 페트로브나의 병에 관한 이야기가 오갔고, 레빈은 문득 일 분쯤 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지 잊은 채 마치 잠에서 깨어난 것처럼 느꼈다. 다른 하나는 그녀 곁, 그녀의 머리맡에 있을 때였는데, 거기서는 그의 심장이 동정의 고통으로 터질 듯하면서도 여전히 터지지 않았고, 그는 끊임없이 하느님께 기도했다. 그리고 망각의 순간에서 침실로부터 들려오는 비명에 끌려 나올 때마다, 그는 첫 순간에 자기를 덮쳤던 바로 그 기이한 공포에 빠져들었다. 비명 소리를 들을 때마다 그는 벌떡 일어나 변명하러 달려갔고, 가는 도중에 자기 잘못이 아니라는 것을 떠올렸으며, 그녀를 지켜 주고 도와주고 싶어 못 견뎌 했다. 그러나 그녀를 바라보면, 그는 도울 길이 없다는 것을 다시금 깨달았고, 공포에 사로잡혀 기도했다. \"주여, 우리를 불쌍히 여기시고, 우리를 도우소서!\" 그리고 시간이 흐를수록 이 두 가지 상태는 점점 더 격렬해졌다. 그녀에게서 떨어져 그녀를 까맣게 잊은 채 마음이 차분해질수록, 그녀의 고통과 그 고통 앞에서 느끼는 자신의 무력감은 그만큼 더 견딜 수 없는 것이 되었다. 그는 벌떡 일어나 달아나고 싶었지만, 그녀에게로 달려갔다.\n\n때로 그녀가 거듭거듭 그를 부를 때면 그는 그녀를 탓했다. 그러나 참고 견디며 미소 짓는 그녀의 얼굴을 보고, \"당신을 힘들게 하네요.\"라는 말을 들으면, 그는 그 탓을 하느님께 돌렸다. 그러다 하느님을 생각하면, 곧바로 자기를 용서하시고 불쌍히 여겨 주십사 하느님께 빌기 시작했다.\n\n# Chapter 15\n\n그는 시간이 늦은 것인지 이른 것인지 알 수 없었다. 초는 모두 다 타 버렸다. 돌리는 방금 서재에 들렀다가 의사에게 잠시 누워 쉬라고 권한 참이었다. 레빈은 어느 돌팔이 최면술사 이야기를 늘어놓는 의사의 말을 들으며 담배의 재를 바라보고 앉아 있었다. 잠시 잠잠한 시간이 있었고, 그는 망각 속으로 빠져들었다. 그는 지금 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지를 완전히 잊고 있었다. 그는 의사의 잡담을 듣고 그것을 알아들었다. 그때 갑자기 이 세상의 것이 아닌 듯한 비명이 들려왔다. 그 비명이 어찌나 끔찍했던지 레빈은 벌떡 일어나지도 못한 채 숨을 죽이고 겁에 질린 채 의사를 향해 묻는 눈길을 던졌다. 의사는 고개를 한쪽으로 기울이고 귀를 기울이더니 만족스럽다는 듯 미소를 지었다. 모든 것이 너무도 비상해서 레빈에게는 그 무엇도 이상하게 여겨질 수가 없었다. '아마 이래야 하는 모양이지.' 그는 생각하며 여전히 자리에 그대로 앉아 있었다. 이건 누구의 비명일까? 그는 벌떡 일어나 발끝으로 침실까지 달려가, 리자베타 페트로브나와 공작 부인 곁을 비집고 지나 키티의 베개맡에 자리를 잡았다. 비명은 잦아들었지만, 이제 무언가 달라져 있었다. 그것이 무엇인지 그는 보지 못했고 이해하지도 못했으며, 또 보거나 이해하고 싶지도 않았다. 그러나 그는 리자베타 페트로브나의 얼굴을 보고 그것을 알아차렸다. 리자베타 페트로브나의 얼굴은 굳어 있고 창백했으며, 턱이 실룩거리고 두 눈이 키티에게 못 박힌 듯 고정되어 있었음에도 여전히 단호했다. 부어오르고 고통으로 일그러진 키티의 얼굴이, 축축한 이마에 머리카락 한 가닥이 들러붙은 채 그를 향해 돌려지더니 그의 눈을 찾았다. 들어 올린 두 손이 그의 손을 청했다. 차가운 그의 손을 축축한 자신의 손으로 움켜잡고는 그것을 제 얼굴로 끌어다 누르기 시작했다.\n\n\"가지 마요, 가지 마요! 무섭지 않아요, 무섭지 않아요!\" 그녀가 빠르게 말했다. \"엄마, 귀고리를 빼 줘요. 거추장스러워요. 안 무서우세요? 빨리, 빨리요, 리자베타 페트로브나……\"\n\n그녀는 빠르게, 아주 빠르게 말하며 미소를 지으려고 애썼다. 그러나 별안간 그녀의 얼굴이 일그러지더니 그를 밀쳐 냈다.\n\n\"아, 끔찍해요! 죽을 것 같아, 죽을 것 같아! 저리 가요!\" 그녀가 비명을 질렀고, 그는 다시 이 세상의 것이 아닌 듯한 그 비명을 들었다.\n\n레빈은 머리를 움켜쥐고 방에서 뛰쳐나갔다.\n\n\"아무것도 아니에요, 아무것도 아니야, 다 괜찮아요.\" 돌리가 그의 등 뒤로 외쳤다.\n\n그러나 사람들이 뭐라고 하든, 그는 이제 모든 것이 끝났다는 것을 알았다. 그는 옆방에 서서 문기둥에 머리를 기댄 채, 전에는 한 번도 들어 본 적 없는 비명을, 울부짖음을 들었고, 한때 키티였던 존재가 이런 비명을 지르고 있다는 것을 알았다. 그는 이미 오래전부터 아이를 바라는 마음을 그쳤다. 이제 그는 이 아이가 혐오스러웠다. 그는 이제 그녀의 목숨조차 바라지 않았고, 그가 바라는 것은 오직 이 끔찍한 고통의 끝뿐이었다.\n\n\"선생님! 어떻게 된 겁니까? 어떻게 된 거예요? 맙소사!\" 그는 다가오는 의사의 손을 붙들며 말했다.\n\n\"끝입니다.\" 의사가 말했다. 그 말을 하는 의사의 얼굴이 너무도 침통해서, 레빈은 그 끝이라는 말을 그녀의 죽음을 뜻하는 것으로 받아들였다.\n\n제정신이 아닌 채로 그는 침실로 달려 들어갔다. 그가 가장 먼저 본 것은 리자베타 페트로브나의 얼굴이었다. 그 얼굴은 한층 더 찌푸려져 있고 굳어 있었다. 키티의 얼굴은 그가 알아볼 수 없었다. 그 얼굴이 있던 자리에는, 팽팽하게 일그러진 모습과 거기에서 흘러나오는 소리로 보아 무언가 무서운 것이 있을 뿐이었다. 그는 심장이 터질 것만 같은 기분으로 침대의 나무 틀에 머리를 박고 쓰러졌다. 끔찍한 비명은 한순간도 그치지 않았고, 점점 더 끔찍해지더니, 마치 공포의 극한에까지 다다르기라도 한 듯 별안간 멎었다. 레빈은 제 귀를 믿을 수 없었지만, 의심의 여지가 없었다. 비명이 멎었고, 그는 나직한 움직임과 부산함을, 가쁜 숨소리를, 그리고 헐떡이면서도 살아 있고 부드러우며 더없이 행복에 찬 그녀의 목소리가 나직이 \"끝났어요!\"라고 말하는 것을 들었다.\n\n그는 고개를 들었다. 두 손을 기진맥진한 듯 이불 위에 떨군 채, 더없이 사랑스럽고 평온해 보이는 그녀가 말없이 그를 바라보며 미소 지으려 했지만, 그러지 못했다.\n\n그러자 갑자기, 지난 스물두 시간 동안 그가 머물러 있던 그 신비롭고 끔찍한 머나먼 세계로부터, 레빈은 순식간에 옛 일상의 세계로 도로 옮겨진 듯한 느낌을 받았다. 다만 그 세계는 이제 그가 견딜 수 없을 만큼 찬란한 행복의 빛으로 환히 빛나고 있었다. 팽팽하게 당겨졌던 현이 끊어지고, 그가 한 번도 예감하지 못했던 기쁨의 흐느낌과 눈물이 어찌나 격렬하게 솟구쳤던지 온몸이 떨렸고, 한참 동안 그는 말을 할 수가 없었다.\n\n침대 앞에 무릎을 꿇은 채 그는 아내의 손을 입술에 가져다 입을 맞추었고, 그 손은 손가락의 희미한 움직임으로 그의 입맞춤에 응답했다. 그러는 사이, 저기 침대 발치에서는 리자베타 페트로브나의 능숙한 손안에, 등잔불의 가물거리는 빛처럼, 전에는 한 번도 존재한 적 없었으나 이제 똑같은 권리와 똑같은 자기 자신에 대한 소중함을 지니고 제 모습대로 살아가며 창조해 나갈 한 인간 존재의 생명이 놓여 있었다.\n\n\"살아 있어요! 살아 있어! 게다가 사내아이예요! 마음 푹 놓으세요!\" 떨리는 손으로 갓난아기의 등을 두드리며 리자베타 페트로브나가 말하는 소리를 레빈은 들었다.\n\n\"엄마, 정말이에요?\" 키티의 목소리가 말했다.\n\n공작 부인의 흐느낌이 그녀가 해 줄 수 있는 답의 전부였다. 그리고 그 침묵 한가운데로, 어머니의 물음에 대한 틀림없는 대답인 듯, 방 안에서 오가던 나직한 목소리들과는 전혀 다른 목소리가 들려왔다. 그토록 불가해하게 나타난 새로운 인간 존재의 대담하고 떠들썩하며 자기를 주장하는 듯한 울음소리였다.\n\n만약 레빈이 키티가 죽었다는, 그리고 그도 그녀와 함께 죽었다는, 그들의 아이들이 천사들이라는, 그리고 하느님이 그의 앞에 서 계시다는 말을 미리 들었더라도, 그는 그 무엇에도 놀라지 않았을 것이다. 그러나 이제 현실의 세계로 돌아온 그는, 그녀가 살아 있고 무사하다는 것을, 그리고 저토록 필사적으로 울어 대는 존재가 자기 아들이라는 것을 받아들이기 위해 엄청난 정신적 노력을 기울여야만 했다. 키티는 살아 있었고, 그녀의 고통은 끝났다. 그리고 그는 이루 말할 수 없이 행복했다. 그것만은 그도 이해할 수 있었다. 그는 그 점에서 더없이 행복했다. 그런데 이 아기는? 어디서, 무엇 때문에, 누구란 말인가?…… 그는 도무지 그 생각에 익숙해질 수가 없었다. 그것은 그에게 무언가 이질적이고 군더더기 같은, 도저히 익숙해질 수 없는 것으로만 여겨졌다.\n\n# Chapter 16\n\n열 시에 노공작과 세르게이 이바노비치와 스테판 아르카지치가 레빈의 집에 앉아 있었다. 키티의 안부를 묻고 난 다음, 그들은 다른 화제로 넘어가 이야기를 나누고 있었다. 레빈은 그들의 말을 듣고 있었는데, 그들이 이야기하는 동안 자기도 모르게 지난 일들을, 그날 아침까지 있었던 일들을 되짚으며, 바로 그 순간까지 어제의 자기가 어떠했던가를 떠올렸다. 그때 이후로 마치 백 년이라도 흐른 것 같았다. 그는 자신이 다다를 수 없는 높이로 올라가 있는 듯 느꼈고, 함께 이야기하는 사람들에게 상처를 주지 않으려고 일부러 그 높이에서 자신을 끌어내렸다. 그는 이야기를 하면서도 줄곧 아내를, 지금 아내의 상태를, 그리고 그 존재를 믿어 보려고 스스로를 다잡고 있는 아들을 생각했다. 결혼 이후 그에게 전에는 짐작조차 못했던 새로운 가치를 띠게 된 여성이라는 온 세계가, 이제는 너무도 높이 올라가 있어서 그는 그것을 상상으로조차 받아들일 수 없었다. 그는 어제 클럽에서 있었던 만찬 이야기를 들으면서도 생각했다. '지금 그녀에게는 무슨 일이 일어나고 있을까? 자고 있을까? 어떻게 지내고 있을까? 무슨 생각을 하고 있을까? 울고 있을까, 내 아들 드미트리는?' 그러다 대화 도중에, 한 문장 중간에, 그는 벌떡 일어나 방을 나가 버렸다.\n\n\"그녀를 볼 수 있겠으면 내게 전갈을 주게.\" 공작이 말했다.\n\n\"네, 곧 알려 드리겠습니다.\" 레빈은 대답하고는 멈추지 않고 아내의 방으로 갔다.\n\n그녀는 자고 있지 않았다. 어머니와 부드럽게 이야기를 나누며 세례식 계획을 세우고 있었다.\n\n머리를 곱게 빗고, 파란빛이 섞인 맵시 있는 작은 모자를 쓰고, 두 팔을 누비이불 위에 내놓은 채 그녀는 단정하게 매무새를 가다듬고 반듯이 누워 있었다. 그의 눈과 마주치자 그녀의 눈이 그를 자기 쪽으로 끌어당겼다. 전부터 환하던 그녀의 얼굴은 그가 다가가자 더욱 환해졌다. 거기에는 죽은 사람의 얼굴에서 보이는, 이승에서 저승으로 옮겨 가는 듯한 그 변화가 깃들어 있었다. 다만 죽은 이의 얼굴에서 그것이 작별을 뜻한다면, 여기서는 맞이함을 뜻했다. 아이가 태어나던 순간에 느꼈던 것과 같은 감정의 물결이 다시금 그의 가슴으로 밀려들었다. 그녀는 그의 손을 잡고 잠을 좀 잤느냐고 물었다. 그는 대답할 수 없어, 약해지려는 마음과 싸우며 고개를 돌렸다.\n\n\"나는 한숨 잤어요, 코스챠!\" 그녀가 그에게 말했다. \"이제 아주 편안해요.\"\n\n그녀는 그를 바라보다가, 별안간 표정이 바뀌었다.\n\n\"그 애를 내게 줘요.\" 그녀가 아기의 울음소리를 듣고 말했다. \"그 애를 내게 줘요, 리자베타 페트로브나, 아빠가 그 애를 봐야지요.\"\n\n\"그럼요, 아빠가 봐야지요.\" 리자베타 페트로브나는 일어나서 빨갛고 묘하게 생긴, 꿈틀거리는 무언가를 안고 오며 말했다. \"잠깐만요, 우선 깨끗이 해 드려야지요.\" 리자베타 페트로브나는 빨갛게 흔들거리는 것을 침대에 내려놓고는 아기를 풀었다 다시 싸기 시작하면서, 손가락 하나로 들어 올렸다 뒤집었다 하고 무언가를 톡톡 발라 주었다.\n\n레빈은 그 자그맣고 가련한 생명체를 바라보며 자기 마음속에서 그것을 향한 부성애의 흔적이라도 찾아보려고 안간힘을 썼다. 그는 그것에 대해 혐오감 외에는 아무것도 느끼지 못했다. 그런데 아기의 옷이 벗겨지고 아주아주 조그만 손과 발이 언뜻 눈에 들어왔을 때, 샤프란 빛깔에 작은 발가락들까지 달려 있고, 게다가 다른 발가락들과는 다른 작은 엄지발가락까지 있는 것이 보였을 때, 그리고 리자베타 페트로브나가 부드러운 용수철이라도 다루듯 활짝 벌어진 그 조그만 두 손을 오므려 무명옷 속으로 넣는 것을 보았을 때, 그 작은 생명체에 대한 연민이, 그리고 그녀가 그것을 다치게 할까 봐 두려운 마음이 어찌나 사무치게 밀려왔던지, 그는 그녀의 손을 붙들어 막았다.\n\n리자베타 페트로브나가 웃음을 터뜨렸다.\n\n\"무서워 마세요, 무서워 마세요!\"\n\n아기가 단정하게 매무새가 다듬어지고 단단한 인형처럼 변하자, 리자베타 페트로브나는 자기 솜씨가 자랑스럽기라도 한 듯 아기를 어르며 살짝 비켜섰다. 레빈이 아들의 온전한 위용을 볼 수 있도록 말이다.\n\n키티는 아기에게서 눈을 떼지 않은 채 같은 쪽으로 곁눈질을 했다. \"그 애를 내게 줘요! 내게 줘요!\" 그녀는 이렇게 말하며 일어나 앉으려는 시늉까지 했다.\n\n\"무슨 생각을 하시는 거예요, 카체리나 알렉산드로브나, 그렇게 움직이시면 안 돼요! 잠깐만요. 제가 드릴게요. 자, 우리가 얼마나 잘생긴 사내인지 아빠한테 보여 드리는 거예요!\"\n\n그러고서 리자베타 페트로브나는 한 손으로 흔들거리는 머리를 받치고, 다른 팔로 그 이상하고 흐물흐물한 빨간 생명체를 들어 올렸다. 그 머리는 강보 속에 파묻혀 보이지 않았다. 그래도 코도 있고, 비스듬한 눈도 있고, 쩝쩝거리는 입술도 있었다.\n\n\"훌륭한 아기예요!\" 리자베타 페트로브나가 말했다.\n\n레빈은 분한 마음에 한숨을 내쉬었다. 이 훌륭한 아기는 그의 마음속에 혐오와 연민 외에는 아무런 감정도 불러일으키지 못했다. 그가 기대했던 것과는 전혀 다른 감정이었다.\n\n리자베타 페트로브나가 아기를 아직 익숙지 않은 젖가슴에 안겨 주는 동안 그는 고개를 돌렸다.\n\n별안간 웃음소리에 그는 돌아보았다. 아기가 젖을 물고 있었다.\n\n\"자, 이제 됐어요, 그만하면 됐어요!\" 리자베타 페트로브나가 말했지만, 키티는 아기를 놓아주려 하지 않았다. 아기는 그녀의 품에서 잠이 들었다.\n\n\"자, 봐요.\" 키티는 그가 볼 수 있도록 아기를 돌려 보이며 말했다. 늙은이 같던 그 작은 얼굴이 갑자기 더욱더 찡그려지더니 아기가 재채기를 했다.\n\n레빈은 미소를 지으며, 눈물을 가까스로 참은 채 아내에게 입을 맞추고 어두운 방을 나섰다. 그가 이 작은 생명체에게 느낀 것은 그가 기대했던 것과는 전혀 달랐다. 그 감정 속에는 명랑하고 기쁜 데라곤 없었다. 오히려 그것은 새로운 불안의 고통이었다. 그것은 고통에 노출되는 새로운 영역이 생겨났다는 의식이었다. 그리고 처음에 이 감각은 어찌나 괴로웠던지, 이 무력한 생명체가 고통받지나 않을까 하는 염려가 어찌나 강렬했던지, 아기가 재채기했을 때 자기가 느꼈던 그 까닭 모를 기쁨의, 심지어 자랑스러움의 묘한 전율조차 알아채지 못할 지경이었다.\n\n스테판 아르카지치의 형편은 매우 좋지 않았다.\n\n숲의 삼분의 이에 대한 대금은 이미 다 써 버렸고, 나머지 삼분의 일에 해당하는 돈도 거의 전부, 상인에게서 십 퍼센트 할인을 무릅쓰고 미리 당겨 받은 상태였다. 상인은 더 이상 돈을 내주려 하지 않았는데, 특히 다리야 알렉산드로브나가 그해 겨울 처음으로 자기 재산에 대한 권리를 주장하며, 숲의 마지막 삼분의 일에 대한 대금 수령증에 서명하기를 거부했기 때문이었다. 그의 급료는 죄다 살림 비용과, 미룰 수 없는 자질구레한 빚을 갚는 데 들어갔다. 그야말로 돈이라곤 없었다.\n\n이는 불쾌하고 거북한 일이었고, 스테판 아르카지치가 보기에 이런 식으로 계속 갈 수는 없었다. 그의 관점에서 이 처지의 원인은, 자기 급료가 너무 적다는 사실에 있었다. 그가 맡은 자리는 오 년 전만 해도 의심할 여지 없이 아주 좋은 자리였지만, 지금은 더 이상 그렇지 않았다.\n\n은행장인 페트로프는 만 이천을 받았고, 어느 회사의 이사인 스벤티츠키는 만 칠천을 받았으며, 은행을 세운 미틴은 오만을 받았다.\n\n\"내가 졸고 있는 사이에 저들이 나를 빼놓고 가 버린 게 분명해.\" 스테판 아르카지치는 자신에 대해 그렇게 생각했다. 그래서 그는 눈과 귀를 활짝 열어 두기 시작했고, 겨울이 끝나 갈 무렵에는 아주 괜찮은 자리를 하나 발견하여 그것을 공략할 계획을 세웠다. 처음에는 모스크바에서 이모며 삼촌이며 친구들을 통해 손을 쓰다가, 일이 어느 정도 무르익은 봄이 되자 직접 페테르부르크로 갔다. 그것은 오늘날 예전보다 훨씬 많아진, 수입이 일천 루블에서 오만 루블에 이르는 안락하고 짭짤한 자리들 가운데 하나였다. 남부 철도 통합 대리점과 몇몇 은행 회사의 합동 위원회 서기 자리였다. 이런 직책은 모든 그런 임명이 그렇듯, 어마어마한 정력과 갖가지 자질을 요구했기에, 그것들을 한 사람 안에서 모두 갖춘 이를 찾기란 어려웠다. 그리고 모든 자질을 두루 갖춘 사람을 찾을 수 없는 이상, 적어도 그 자리는 부정직한 사람보다는 정직한 사람으로 채워지는 편이 나았다. 그런데 스테판 아르카지치는 그저 흔히 말하는 의미에서의—강조 없이—정직한 사람이었을 뿐 아니라, 모스크바에서 그 단어가 지니는 특별한 의미에서—강조해서 말하는—정직한 사람이었다. 모스크바에서 '정직한' 정치가, '정직한' 작가, '정직한' 신문, '정직한' 기관, '정직한' 노선이라고 말할 때, 그것은 단지 그 사람이나 기관이 부정직하지 않다는 뜻이 아니라, 경우에 따라 당국에 맞서 자기 나름의 입장을 취할 능력이 있다는 뜻이었다.\n\n스테판 아르카지치는 모스크바에서 그런 표현이 통용되는 사교계에 드나들었고, 그곳에서 정직한 사람으로 통했으니, 다른 이들보다 이 자리에 대한 권리가 더 있는 셈이었다.\n\n그 자리는 연간 칠천에서 일만 루블의 수입을 가져다주었고, 오블론스키는 자기 관직을 그대로 둔 채 그것을 맡을 수 있었다. 그 자리는 두 장관과 한 부인, 그리고 두 유대인의 손에 달려 있었는데, 이 사람들과는 이미 길을 닦아 두었음에도 불구하고 스테판 아르카지치는 페테르부르크에서 그들을 직접 만나야 했다. 이 일 말고도 스테판 아르카지치는 누이 안나에게, 카레닌으로부터 이혼 문제에 대한 확실한 대답을 받아 내겠다고 약속한 터였다. 그래서 돌리에게 오십 루블을 꾸어, 페테르부르크로 떠났다.\n\n스테판 아르카지치는 카레닌의 서재에 앉아, 러시아 재정이 만족스럽지 못한 상태에 놓인 원인에 관한 그의 보고를 들으면서, 그가 말을 끝내는 순간 자기 일이나 안나에 관한 이야기를 꺼낼 수 있기만을 기다리고 있었다.\n\n\"그래, 그건 아주 옳은 말씀이야.\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 이제는 그것 없이는 글을 읽지 못하는 코안경을 벗고서 전 처남을 캐묻듯 바라보았을 때, 그가 말했다. \"특정한 경우에는 그게 아주 옳지. 하지만 그래도 우리 시대의 원칙은 자유잖나.\"\n\n\"그래, 하지만 나는 자유의 원칙을 포괄하는 또 다른 원칙을 내세우네.\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 '포괄하는'이라는 말에 힘을 주어 말하고는, 그 주장이 담긴 대목을 읽으려고 다시 코안경을 썼다. 그리고 아름답게 쓰인, 여백이 넓은 원고를 넘기며, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 그 결론 부분을 다시 소리 내어 읽었다.\n\n\"나는 사적 이익을 위해서가 아니라 공공의 복리를 위해, 그리고 하층 계급과 상층 계급을 똑같이 위해 보호 정책을 옹호하는 거네.\" 그는 코안경 너머로 오블론스키를 바라보며 말했다. \"하지만 그자들은 그걸 이해하지 못해. 그자들은 지금 개인적 이해관계에만 골몰하고, 듣기 좋은 말에 휩쓸려 다닌단 말이야.\"\n\n스테판 아르카지치는 카레닌이 그자들이 무엇을 하고 무엇을 생각하는지, 즉 자기 보고를 받아들이지 않으며 러시아의 모든 잘못된 일의 원인인 그 사람들에 대해 이야기를 시작하면, 이야기가 끝나갈 무렵이라는 것을 알았다. 그래서 이번에도 그는 자유무역의 원칙을 선뜻 버리고 전적으로 동의했다. 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 생각에 잠긴 채 원고 장을 넘기며 말을 멈췄다.\n\n\"아, 그건 그렇고,\" 스테판 아르카지치가 말했다. \"부탁이 있는데, 언제 포모르스키를 만나거든 내가 남부 철도와 은행 회사 통합 대리점 합동 위원회 서기라는 그 새 자리를 얻으면 정말 기쁘겠다고 슬쩍 귀띔해 주었으면 하네.\" 스테판 아르카지치는 이제 자기가 탐내는 그 자리의 명칭에 익숙해져서, 막힘없이 술술 읊어 댔다.\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 그 새 위원회의 임무에 관해 그에게 물어보고는 곰곰이 생각했다. 그 새 위원회가 자기가 옹호해 온 견해에 어떤 식으로든 어긋나게 움직이지는 않을지 따져 보고 있었다. 하지만 그 새 위원회의 영향력이라는 것이 매우 복잡한 성격을 띠고 있었고, 자기 견해는 적용 범위가 매우 넓었으므로, 그는 그 자리에서 곧바로 결론을 내릴 수 없었다. 그래서 코안경을 벗으며 말했다.\n\n\"물론 그자에게 말은 해 줄 수 있네. 그런데 정확히 무슨 이유로 그 자리를 얻으려는 건가?\"\n\n\"급료가 좋아서, 구천까지 오르거든. 게다가 내 형편이……\"\n\n\"구천이라!\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 되뇌며 미간을 찌푸렸다. 그 높은 급료 액수는, 그쪽 방면에서 스테판 아르카지치가 노리는 자리가 늘 절약 쪽으로 기우는 자기 개혁안의 주된 방향과 어긋난다는 사실을 곱씹게 만들었다.\n\n\"내 생각으로는, 그리고 이 문제에 관한 견해를 글로도 정리해 두었네만, 오늘날 이런 어마어마한 급료는 우리 재정의 건전하지 못한 경제적 assiette를 보여 주는 증거일세.\"\n\n* assiette: 프랑스어로 '기반, 토대'.\n\"하지만 어쩌겠나?\" 스테판 아르카지치가 말했다. \"은행장이 만을 받는다고 치세. 글쎄, 그만한 값어치가 있는 거고, 기사가 이만을 받는다 해도, 어쨌든 그건 자라나는 분야 아닌가!\"\n\n\"나는 급료란 어떤 상품에 대해 지불되는 값이라고 보네. 그러니 그것은 수요와 공급의 법칙에 부합해야 마땅하지. 만일 그 법칙을 전혀 고려하지 않고 급료가 정해진다면, 가령 똑같이 잘 훈련받고 유능한 두 기사가 함께 학교를 나왔는데 한 사람은 사만을 받고 다른 한 사람은 이천으로 만족하는 경우, 또는 아무런 특별한 자질도 없는 법률가나 경기병이 어마어마한 급료를 받으며 은행 회사 이사로 임명되는 경우를 내가 본다면, 나는 그 급료가 수요와 공급의 법칙에 따라 정해진 것이 아니라 그저 개인적 이해관계를 통해 정해진 것이라고 결론짓네. 그리고 이것은 그 자체로 매우 중대한 폐단이며, 관청의 업무에도 해로운 영향을 미치는 일이지. 내 생각으로는……\"\n\n스테판 아르카지치는 서둘러 매형의 말을 끊었다.\n\n\"그래, 하지만 이건 의심할 여지 없이 유익한 새 기관이 출범하는 거라는 점은 자네도 인정해야 하네. 어쨌든 자라나는 분야 아닌가! 그들이 특히 강조하는 건 일이 정직하게 운영되어야 한다는 점이야.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 힘주어 말했다.\n\n그러나 '정직한'이라는 단어의 모스크바식 의미는 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에게 통하지 않았다.\n\n\"정직성이란 그저 소극적인 자질일 뿐이지.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"뭐, 어쨌든 자네가 포모르스키에게 한마디만 거들어 준다면 내게는 큰 도움이 될 걸세.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 말했다. \"그냥 이야기를 나누는 김에 말이지……\"\n\n\"하지만 그 일은 볼가리노프의 손에 더 많이 달려 있는 것 같네만.\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 말했다.\n\n\"볼가리노프는 자기 쪽에서는 전적으로 동의했네.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다. 스테판 아르카지치가 그 이름이 나오자 낯을 붉힌 것은, 그날 아침 유대인 볼가리노프의 집에 다녀왔는데 그 방문이 불쾌한 기억을 남겼기 때문이었다.\n\n스테판 아르카지치는 자기가 자리를 얻으려는 그 위원회가 새롭고 진정하며 정직한 공공 기관이라고 더없이 확신했지만, 그날 아침 볼가리노프가—틀림없이 의도적으로—다른 청원자들과 함께 그를 두 시간이나 대기실에서 기다리게 했을 때, 그는 별안간 마음이 거북해졌다.\n\n류리크의 후손이자 오블론스키 공작인 자기가 유대인을 만나려고 두 시간이나 기다렸다는 사실 때문에 불편했는지, 아니면 평생 처음으로 관청에 봉직하던 조상들의 본을 따르지 않고 새로운 길로 들어서고 있다는 사실 때문이었는지는 몰라도, 어쨌든 그는 몹시 거북했다. 볼가리노프의 대기실에서 보낸 그 두 시간 동안 스테판 아르카지치는 방 안을 경쾌하게 거닐고, 구레나룻을 매만지고, 다른 청원자들과 이야기를 나누고, 자기 처지에 관한 경구를 지어내면서, 자기가 느끼고 있던 감정을 남들에게는 물론 자기 자신에게까지도 부지런히 감추었다.\n* 류리크: 9세기 러시아 최초의 왕조를 세운 인물.\n\n하지만 그동안 내내 그는 거북하고 화가 났는데, 자기도 그 까닭을 알 수 없었다. 경구를 딱 들어맞게 다듬어 내지 못해서인지, 아니면 다른 무슨 이유에서인지. 마침내 볼가리노프가 그를 지나치게 정중하게, 그러면서도 그의 굴욕에 대한 분명한 승리감을 드러내며 맞이하고는, 그가 청한 부탁을 거의 거절하다시피 했을 때, 스테판 아르카지치는 그 모든 일을 가능한 한 빨리 잊으려고 서둘렀다. 그런데 지금, 그저 그 기억을 떠올리는 것만으로도 그는 얼굴이 붉어졌다.\n\n# Chapter 18\n\n\"이제 한 가지 의논하고 싶은 게 있는데, 자네도 그게 뭔지 알 거야. 안나에 관한 일이지.\" 스테판 아르카지치는 잠시 말을 멈추고 그 불쾌한 느낌을 떨쳐 내며 말했다.\n\n오블론스키가 안나의 이름을 입에 올리자마자, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 얼굴은 완전히 딴판으로 변했다. 거기서 모든 생기가 사라지고, 지치고 죽은 듯이 보였다.\n\n\"나한테서 정확히 뭘 원하는 건가?\" 그는 의자에서 몸을 움직이며 코안경을 탁 접고 말했다.\n\n\"확실한 결말을 보자는 거야, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치. 이 상황을 어떻게든 매듭짓자는 거지. 내가 자네에게 호소하는 건,\" (스테판 아르카지치는 \"상처받은 남편으로서가 아니라\"라고 말하려다가, 이 말로 협상을 그르칠까 두려워 표현을 바꾸었다) \"정치가로서가 아니라\" (이것도 적절치 않게 들렸다) \"그저 한 사람으로서, 마음씨 착한 한 인간으로서, 그리고 한 기독교인으로서일세. 자네는 그녀를 가엾게 여겨야 해.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"그러니까, 정확히 어떤 식으로 말인가?\" 카레닌이 나직이 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 그녀를 가엾게 여겨 달라는 거야. 자네가 나처럼 그녀를 봤더라면!—나는 겨우내 그녀와 함께 지냈거든—자네도 그녀를 가엾게 여겼을 걸세. 그녀의 처지는 끔찍해, 그저 끔찍할 뿐이야!\"\n\n\"내 생각엔,\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 한결 높은, 거의 째지는 목소리로 대꾸했다. \"안나 아르카지예브나는 자기가 바라던 걸 다 가졌을 텐데.\"\n\n\"오, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치, 제발 부탁이니 서로 헐뜯는 짓은 그만두세! 지나간 건 지나간 거야. 그리고 자네도 그녀가 뭘 원하는지, 뭘 기다리는지 알잖나—이혼 말이야.\"\n\n\"하지만 내가 알기로 안나 아르카지예브나는, 내가 아들을 내게 남겨 둘 것을 조건으로 내건다면 이혼을 거부하지. 나는 그런 취지로 답했고, 그 문제는 끝난 걸로 알았네. 나는 그 일을 끝난 걸로 여기고 있어.\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 째지는 소리로 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 제발, 흥분하지 말게!\" 스테판 아르카지치가 매형의 무릎을 만지며 말했다. \"그 문제는 끝나지 않았어. 내가 다시 한번 정리하게 허락해 준다면, 일은 이랬어. 자네들이 갈라설 때, 자네는 더없이 너그러웠어. 그녀에게 뭐든 다 줄 작정이었지—자유든, 이혼이든 말이야. 그녀는 그걸 고맙게 여겼어. 아니, 그렇게 생각하지 말게. 그녀는 정말로 고맙게 여겼어—얼마나 고마워했는지, 첫 순간에는 자기가 자네에게 얼마나 못할 짓을 했는지를 느끼느라, 모든 걸 헤아리지도 못했고 헤아릴 수도 없었지. 그녀는 모든 걸 포기했어. 하지만 경험과 시간이 보여 준 거야, 그녀의 처지가 견딜 수 없고 불가능하다는 걸 말이야.\"\n\n\"안나 아르카지예브나의 삶은 내게 아무런 흥미가 없네.\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 눈썹을 치켜올리며 끼어들었다.\n\n\"그 말은 믿지 않겠네.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 부드럽게 대꾸했다. \"그녀의 처지는 그녀에게 견딜 수 없는 것이고, 누구에게도 아무런 득이 안 돼. 그녀가 자초한 일이라고 자넨 말하겠지. 그녀도 그걸 알고 있고, 자네에게 아무것도 요구하지 않아. 자네에게 감히 요구할 수 없다고 분명히 말해. 하지만 나는, 우리 모두는, 그녀의 친척들은, 그녀를 사랑하는 모든 이들은 자네에게 청하고, 간청하는 거야. 어째서 그녀가 고통받아야 하지? 그걸로 누가 조금이라도 나아지는가?\"\n\n\"미안하지만, 자넨 나를 죄지은 쪽에 세우려는 것 같군.\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 말했다.\n\n\"오, 아니야, 오, 아니야, 전혀 그렇지 않네! 제발 내 말을 이해해 주게.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 다시 그의 손을 만지며 말했다. 이 신체 접촉이 처남의 마음을 누그러뜨려 주리라고 확신하기라도 하는 듯이. \"내가 말하는 건 이것뿐이야. 그녀의 처지는 견딜 수 없는 것이고, 자네가 그걸 덜어 줄 수 있으며, 그렇게 해도 자넨 잃을 게 아무것도 없다는 거지. 내가 자네를 위해 다 처리해 줄 테니, 자네는 신경 쓸 일조차 없을 거야. 자네도 약속했잖나, 그러지 않았나.\"\n\n\"그 약속은 이전에 한 거지. 그리고 나는 아들 문제로 그 일이 매듭지어진 줄 알았네. 게다가 안나 아르카지예브나에게도 충분한 아량이 있으리라고 기대했고…….\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 입술을 씰룩이고 얼굴이 하얗게 질린 채 가까스로 또박또박 말했다.\n\n\"그녀는 모든 걸 자네의 아량에 맡기고 있어. 그녀가 자네에게 빌고, 애원하는 건 단 한 가지야—자기가 처한 이 불가능한 상황에서 자기를 빼내 달라는 거지. 그녀는 이제 아들을 요구하지 않아. 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치, 자넨 선한 사람이야. 잠깐만이라도 그녀의 처지가 되어 보게. 그녀에게 이혼 문제는, 그녀가 처한 상황에서는 죽느냐 사느냐의 문제야. 자네가 한때 그걸 약속하지 않았더라면, 그녀는 자기 처지를 받아들이고 시골에서 계속 살았을 거야. 하지만 자네가 약속했고, 그래서 그녀는 자네에게 편지를 쓰고 모스크바로 옮겨 온 거야. 그러고는 여기 모스크바에서 여섯 달째 지내고 있는데, 어쩌다 마주치는 사람마다 그녀의 가슴을 후벼 파고, 날마다 답을 기다리고 있어. 이건 사형수를 여섯 달 동안 목에 밧줄을 건 채로 두면서, 어쩌면 죽음일지, 어쩌면 사면일지 약속하는 것과 다름없어. 그녀를 가엾게 여겨 주게, 그러면 내가 모든 걸 처리하겠네. Vos scrupules…….\"\n\n\"그 얘기를 하는 게 아니야, 그게 아니라…….\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 넌더리를 내며 말을 가로막았다. \"하지만, 어쩌면 나는 약속할 권리도 없는 걸 약속했는지도 모르지.\"\n\n\"그래서 자네 약속을 무를 셈인가?\"\n\n\"나는 가능한 모든 걸 하기를 거부한 적이 없어. 다만 내가 약속한 것 가운데 얼마나 많은 부분이 가능한지를 생각해 볼 시간이 필요한 거지.\"\n\n\"아니야, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치!\" 오블론스키가 벌떡 일어나며 외쳤다. \"그 말은 믿지 않겠네! 그녀는 불행한 여자가 불행할 수 있는 만큼 불행한데, 자네가 그런 상황에서 거절할 수는…….\"\n\n\"내가 약속한 것 가운데 가능한 만큼은 하겠어. Vous professez d'être libre penseur. 하지만 나는 신앙인으로서, 이토록 중대한 문제에서 기독교 율법에 거슬러 행동할 수는 없네.\"\n\n\"하지만 기독교 사회에서도, 우리들 사이에서도, 내가 알기로는 이혼이 허용되잖나.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 말했다. \"이혼은 우리 교회에서도 인정하고 있어. 그리고 우리는 보고 있지…….\"\n\n\"허용은 되지만, 그런 의미에서는 아니야…….\"\n\n\"알렉세이 알렉산드로비치, 자네답지 않군.\" 오블론스키가 잠시 사이를 두었다가 말했다. \"모든 걸 용서했던 게, (그리고 우리 모두 그 점을 자네에게서 높이 샀던 게) 바로 자네 아니었나? 순전히 기독교적 감정에 이끌려 어떤 희생이라도 치를 각오가 되어 있던 게 말이야. 자네가 직접 말했잖아. 누가 네 속옷을 가지려 하거든 겉옷까지도 주라고, 그런데 지금은…….\"\n\n\"부탁하네.\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 째지는 소리로 말하며, 얼굴이 하얗게 질리고 턱을 씰룩이며 별안간 벌떡 일어섰다. \"부탁이니 이걸 그만두게……이 얘기를……그만둬!\"\n\n\"오, 아니야! 오, 미안하네, 내가 자네에게 상처를 줬다면 용서해 주게.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 멋쩍은 미소를 지으며 손을 내밀었다. \"하지만 나는 그저 심부름꾼처럼, 내게 맡겨진 일을 수행했을 뿐일세.\"\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 그에게 손을 내밀고는 잠시 생각에 잠겼다가 말했다.\n\n\"곰곰이 생각해 보고 길을 찾아봐야겠네. 모레 자네에게 최종적인 답을 주겠어.\" 그는 잠시 헤아려 본 뒤에 말했다.\n\n# Chapter 19\n\n스테판 아르카지치가 막 떠나려는데 코르네이가 들어와 알렸다.\n\n\"세르게이 알렉세이치 도련님이십니다!\"\n\n\"세르게이 알렉세이치가 누구지?\" 스테판 아르카지치는 입을 떼려다가 곧바로 떠올렸다.\n\n\"아, 세료자!\" 그는 소리 내어 말했다. \"세르게이 알렉세이치라니! 나는 무슨 관청 국장이라도 되는 줄 알았네. 안나도 그 애를 만나 달라고 했었지.\" 그는 생각했다.\n\n그러자 헤어질 때 안나가 겁먹고 애처로운 표정으로 그에게 했던 말이 떠올랐다. \"어쨌든 그 애를 보게 될 거예요. 그 애가 정확히 어디 있는지, 누가 돌보고 있는지 알아봐 주세요. 그리고 스티바…… 혹시 가능하다면! 그게 가능할까요?\" 스테판 아르카지치는 그 \"혹시 가능하다면\"이 무슨 뜻인지 알고 있었다—이혼을 그녀가 아들을 데리고 갈 수 있도록 성사시키는 게 가능하다면…… 스테판 아르카지치는 이제 그런 것을 꿈꿔 봐야 소용없다는 것을 알았지만, 그래도 조카를 보게 되어 반가웠다.\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 처남에게 아이 앞에서는 결코 그 어머니 이야기를 하지 않는다는 점을 일러두며, 그녀에 관해서는 단 한마디도 꺼내지 말아 달라고 당부했다.\n\n\"어머니와 그 면회를 한 뒤에 그 애는 몹시 앓았소. 우리도 예상하지 못한 일이었지.\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 말했다. \"정말이지 그 애의 목숨까지 걱정했었소. 하지만 합리적인 치료에다 여름에 바닷물 목욕을 하고 나서 기력을 되찾았고, 이제는 의사의 권유에 따라 학교에 보냈소. 그리고 확실히 학교 친구들과 어울리는 것이 그 애에게 좋은 영향을 미쳐서, 지금은 아주 건강하고 공부도 잘하고 있소.\"\n\n\"참 늠름하게 자랐군! 이제는 세료자가 아니라 어엿한 세르게이 알렉세이치인걸!\" 스테판 아르카지치는 미소를 지으며 말했는데, 파란 윗도리에 긴 바지를 입고 또랑또랑하고 자신감 있는 걸음으로 들어오는, 잘생기고 어깨가 떡 벌어진 소년을 바라보면서였다. 아이는 건강하고 명랑해 보였다. 아이는 낯선 사람을 대하듯 외삼촌에게 절을 했지만, 그를 알아보고는 얼굴을 붉히며 무언가에 기분이 상하고 짜증이 난 듯 황급히 그에게서 돌아섰다. 아이는 아버지에게 다가가 학교에서 받은 성적표를 건넸다.\n\n\"음, 아주 괜찮구나.\" 아버지가 말했다. \"이제 가 보아라.\"\n\n\"전보다 호리호리해지고 키도 컸어. 아이 티를 벗고 소년이 다 됐군. 그게 마음에 드는걸.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 말했다. \"나를 기억하니?\"\n\n아이는 재빨리 외삼촌을 돌아보았다.\n\n\"네, mon oncle.\" 아이는 아버지를 흘끗 쳐다보며 대답하더니, 다시 풀 죽은 표정을 지었다.\n\n* mon oncle: 프랑스어로 '외삼촌'.\n\n\"그래, 요즘은 어떻게 지내니?\" 그는 아이와 이야기를 나누고 싶었지만 무슨 말을 해야 할지 몰라 그렇게 물었다.\n\n아이는 얼굴을 붉히며 아무 대답도 하지 않고 슬그머니 손을 빼냈다. 스테판 아르카지치가 손을 놓아 주기가 무섭게, 아이는 미심쩍은 눈으로 아버지를 흘끗 쳐다보더니, 풀려난 새처럼 방에서 쏜살같이 나가 버렸다.\n\n세료자가 어머니를 마지막으로 본 지 일 년이 지났다. 그 뒤로 아이는 어머니에 대해 아무 소식도 듣지 못했다. 그리고 그 한 해 동안 아이는 학교에 들어갔고, 학교 친구들과 사귀게 되었다. 어머니를 본 뒤 자신을 앓아눕게 만들었던 그 꿈과 기억은 이제 아이의 생각을 차지하지 않았다. 그것들이 되살아날 때면 아이는 그것을 부끄럽고 계집애 같은 것, 소년이자 학생인 자기의 체면에 어울리지 않는 것으로 여기며 애써 몰아냈다. 아이는 아버지와 어머니가 무슨 다툼으로 갈라섰다는 것을 알았고, 자기는 아버지와 함께 남아야 한다는 것을 알았으며, 그 생각에 익숙해지려고 애썼다.\n\n아이는 어머니를 그토록 닮은 외삼촌을 보는 것이 싫었다. 그것이 부끄러워하던 그 기억들을 불러일으켰기 때문이다. 게다가 서재 문 앞에서 기다리는 동안 주워들은 몇 마디 말에서, 그리고 무엇보다 아버지와 외삼촌의 표정에서, 두 사람이 어머니 이야기를 나누고 있었음을 짐작했기에 더더욱 싫었다. 그래서 자기가 함께 살고 의지하고 있는 아버지를 비난하지 않기 위해, 그리고 무엇보다 그토록 치욕스럽게 여기는 감상에 빠지지 않기 위해, 세료자는 자기 마음의 평화를 어지럽히러 온 외삼촌을 쳐다보지 않으려, 그리고 그가 떠올리게 하는 것을 생각하지 않으려 애썼다.\n\n그러나 스테판 아르카지치가 아이를 뒤따라 나오다가 계단에서 그를 보고 불러 세워, 학교에서 쉬는 시간을 어떻게 보내느냐고 묻자, 아버지가 없는 자리에서 세료자는 한결 거리낌 없이 그에게 이야기했다.\n\n\"이제 우리한테는 철도가 있어요.\" 아이는 외삼촌의 물음에 답하며 말했다. \"그러니까 이런 거예요. 보세요. 둘이 의자에 앉아요—그 애들이 승객이에요. 그리고 한 명은 의자 위에 똑바로 서요. 그러면 다들 팔이나 허리띠로 그 애한테 묶여서 모든 방을 가로질러 달리는 거예요—문은 미리 다 열어 두고요. 그런데 차장 노릇 하기가 꽤 힘들어요!\"\n\n\"그게 서 있는 애란 말이지?\" 스테판 아르카지치가 미소를 지으며 물었다.\n\n\"네, 그러려면 배짱도 있어야 하고 머리도 좋아야 해요. 특히 다들 갑자기 멈추거나 누가 넘어질 때는요.\"\n\n\"그래, 그건 정말 중요한 일이겠구나.\" 스테판 아르카지치는 어머니를 닮은 그 초롱초롱한 눈을 서글픈 흥미로 바라보며 말했다. 이제는 어린애 같지도 않고, 더 이상 온전히 천진하지도 않은 눈이었다. 그리고 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에게 안나 이야기를 하지 않겠다고 약속했건만, 그는 자신을 억누를 수 없었다.\n\n\"너, 어머니를 기억하니?\" 그는 불쑥 물었다.\n\n\"아뇨, 기억 안 나요.\" 세료자가 재빨리 말했다. 아이는 새빨갛게 얼굴을 붉혔고, 표정이 어두워졌다. 외삼촌은 그에게서 더 이상 아무 말도 끌어낼 수 없었다. 가정교사는 삼십 분쯤 뒤에 계단에서 제자를 발견했는데, 한참 동안 아이가 화가 난 것인지 울고 있는 것인지 분간할 수 없었다.\n\n\"무슨 일이냐? 넘어지면서 다친 모양이구나?\" 가정교사가 말했다. \"내가 그건 위험한 놀이라고 했잖니. 교장 선생님께 말씀드려야겠다.\"\n\n\"제가 다쳤더라도 아무한테도 들키지 않았을 거예요. 그건 확실해요.\"\n\n\"그럼 대체 무슨 일이냐?\"\n\n\"날 좀 내버려 둬요! 내가 기억하든 안 하든…… 그게 그분이랑 무슨 상관이에요? 내가 왜 기억해야 해요? 날 좀 가만히 내버려 두라고요!\" 아이는 가정교사가 아니라 온 세상을 향해 말했다.\n\n# Chapter 20\n\n스테판 아르카지치는 여느 때처럼 페테르부르크에서 시간을 헛되이 보내지 않았다. 페테르부르크에서는 볼일, 즉 누이의 이혼 문제와 그토록 갈망하던 자리 외에도, 그는 늘 그러하듯 자기 표현대로 모스크바의 곰팡내를 씻어 내고 새로워지고 싶었다.\n\n카페 샹탕과 합승마차가 있다 해도 모스크바는 여전히 고인 늪이었다. 스테판 아르카지치는 늘 그렇게 느꼈다. 모스크바에서 얼마간 살다 보면, 특히 가족과 가까이 지내다 보면, 그는 마음이 가라앉는 것을 의식했다. 변화 없이 모스크바에서 오래 지내다 보면, 그는 아내의 언짢은 기분과 잔소리며, 아이들의 건강과 교육이며, 직무의 자질구레한 세부사항에 대해 어느새 진지하게 안달복달하기 시작하는 지경에 이르렀다. 심지어 빚을 지고 있다는 사실조차 그를 괴롭혔다. 그러나 그저 페테르부르크에 가서 그가 드나드는 그 사교계 모임에 잠시 머무르기만 하면, 그곳에서는 사람들이 모스크바에서처럼 그저 풀처럼 시들시들 연명하는 것이 아니라 진짜로, 정말로 살아가고 있었기에, 그런 온갖 생각들은 불 앞의 밀랍처럼 단숨에 사라져 녹아 버렸다. 아내라고?…… 바로 그날 그는 체첸스키 공작과 이야기를 나누었다. 체첸스키 공작에게는 아내와 가족이 있었고, 사관학교에 다 큰 시동들도 있었으며…… 게다가 또 하나의 사생아 자식들로 이루어진 가족도 있었다. 첫 번째 가족도 무척 좋았지만, 체첸스키 공작은 두 번째 가족에서 더 행복하다고 느꼈다. 그는 큰아들을 두 번째 가족에게 데려가곤 했고, 그것이 아들의 견문을 넓혀 주어 아들에게 좋다고 생각한다고 스테판 아르카지치에게 말했다. 모스크바에서 그런 말을 하면 뭐라고들 했을까?\n\n아이들은? 페테르부르크에서는 아이들이 부모가 인생을 즐기는 것을 가로막지 않았다. 아이들은 학교에서 길러졌고, 모스크바에서, 이를테면 리보프 집안 같은 데서 횡행하던 그 터무니없는 생각—인생의 온갖 호사는 모두 아이들 몫이고 부모에게는 오직 일과 근심만 있을 뿐이라는—같은 것은 흔적도 없었다. 이곳 사람들은 교양 있는 사람이라면 누구나 그래야 하듯 인간은 자기 자신을 위해 살아야 할 의무가 있다는 것을 이해하고 있었다.\n\n직무는? 이곳의 공직 일은 모스크바에서처럼 뻣뻣하고 가망 없는 노역이 아니었다. 이곳에서는 관직 생활에 흥미로운 데가 있었다. 우연한 만남, 베푼 호의, 적절한 한마디, 익살스럽게 흉내 내는 재주만 있으면, 한 사람의 출셋길이 순식간에 열릴 수도 있었다. 스테판 아르카지치가 전날 만난 브랸체프가 바로 그랬는데, 그는 이제 정부의 최고위 관료 가운데 하나였다. 그런 식의 공직 일에는 흥미로운 데가 있었다.\n\n금전 문제를 대하는 페테르부르크의 태도는 특히 스테판 아르카지치의 마음을 달래 주는 효과가 있었다. 사는 꼴을 보아 적어도 오만 루블은 써야 할 바르트냔스키가 그 문제에 관해 전날 흥미로운 말을 한 적이 있었다.\n\n만찬 전에 이야기를 나누다가 스테판 아르카지치는 바르트냔스키에게 말했다.\n\n\"자네는 모르드빈스키와 친한 것 같던데, 부탁 하나 들어주게나. 나를 위해 그 사람한테 한마디 해 주게. 내가 얻고 싶은 자리가 하나 있어—그 대리국의 서기관이라네……\"\n\n\"아, 그렇게 말해 봤자 난 그런 거 다 기억 못 해…… 한데 도대체 무슨 마음으로 철도며 유대인들이랑 엮이려는 건가?…… 어떻게 받아들이든, 그건 천한 일이야.\"\n\n스테판 아르카지치는 그것이 \"전망 있는 사업\"이라고 바르트냔스키에게 말하지 않았다—바르트냔스키는 그 말을 이해하지 못했을 테니까.\n\n\"돈이 필요하다네. 먹고살 게 없어서.\"\n\n\"먹고살고 있잖나, 안 그래?\"\n\n\"그렇지, 하지만 빚을 지고 있다네.\"\n\n\"정말인가? 많이?\" 바르트냔스키가 동정 어린 어조로 물었다.\n\n\"아주 많지. 이만 루블이야.\"\n\n바르트냔스키가 너털웃음을 터뜨렸다.\n\n\"오, 복 받은 사람 같으니!\" 그가 말했다. \"내 빚은 백오십만 루블에 이르고, 가진 건 아무것도 없는데, 그래도 자네가 보다시피 멀쩡히 살고 있잖나!\"\n\n그리고 스테판 아르카지치는 이 견해가 옳다는 것을 말로만이 아니라 실제 사실로도 확인했다. 지바호프는 삼십만 루블의 빚을 지고 한 푼도 가진 게 없었지만 그래도 살았고, 그것도 호사스럽게 살았다! 크리프초프 백작은 모두에게 가망 없는 인간으로 여겨졌지만, 그래도 정부를 둘이나 거느리고 있었다. 페트로프스키는 오백만 루블을 탕진했지만, 여전히 똑같은 방식으로 살았고, 게다가 재무부에서 이만 루블의 봉급을 받는 관리자였다. 그러나 이것 말고도 페테르부르크는 스테판 아르카지치에게 육체적으로도 기분 좋은 영향을 미쳤다. 그것이 그를 젊게 만들었다. 모스크바에서는 이따금 머리에서 흰머리를 발견하고, 만찬 후에는 잠이 들고, 기지개를 켜고, 가쁜 숨을 몰아쉬며 천천히 계단을 오르고, 젊은 여자들과 어울리는 것을 지루해하고, 무도회에서 춤도 추지 않았다. 페테르부르크에서는 늘 열 살은 젊어진 기분이었다.\n\n페테르부르크에서 그가 겪은 일은 전날 막 외국에서 돌아온 예순 살의 표트르 오블론스키 공작이 그에게 들려준 것과 꼭 같았다.\n\n\"우리는 여기서 사는 법을 모른단 말일세.\" 표트르 오블론스키가 말했다. \"여름을 바덴에서 보냈는데, 자네는 믿지 못하겠지만, 내가 아주 젊은이가 된 기분이었어. 예쁜 여자가 언뜻 보이면 내 생각이란 게…… 식사를 하고 포도주를 한 잔 마시면 힘이 넘치고 뭐든 할 수 있을 것 같았지. 러시아 집으로 돌아왔는데—아내도 봐야 했고, 게다가 시골 영지에도 가야 했는데, 거기서, 자네는 도무지 믿지 못할 거야, 보름 만에 실내복을 걸치고 만찬 때 정장 차려입는 일도 그만뒀다네. 예쁜 여자들 생각 따위 남지 않았다는 건 말할 것도 없고. 나는 아주 늙은이가 돼 버렸어. 영혼의 구원이나 생각하는 것 말고는 할 일이 없었지. 그러다 파리로 떠났더니—당장에 더없이 멀쩡해지더군.\"\n\n스테판 아르카지치는 표트르 오블론스키가 말한 그 차이를 똑같이 느꼈다. 모스크바에서는 어찌나 퇴락하는지, 거기서 오래 붙박여 지내야 했다면 정말로 자기 구원을 진지하게 생각하는 지경에 이르렀을지도 몰랐다. 페테르부르크에서는 다시 사교계의 한 인물이 된 기분이었다.\n\n베시 트베르스카야 공작 부인과 스테판 아르카지치 사이에는 오래전부터 다소 묘한 관계가 있었다. 스테판 아르카지치는 늘 농담조로 그녀에게 추파를 던졌고, 역시 농담조로 더없이 점잖지 못한 말을 그녀에게 하곤 했는데, 그녀가 그런 것을 무엇보다 좋아한다는 것을 알았기 때문이다. 카레닌과 이야기를 나눈 다음 날, 스테판 아르카지치는 그녀를 보러 갔고, 어찌나 젊어진 기분이었던지 이 농담 섞인 추파와 객쩍은 수작에서 분별없이 너무 멀리 나가 버려, 어떻게 빠져나와야 할지 알 수 없게 되었다. 공교롭게도 그는 그녀에게 끌리기는커녕 오히려 그녀가 영 마음에 들지 않는다고 여기고 있었기 때문이다. 화제를 바꾸기 어렵게 만든 것은 그가 그녀에게 무척 매력적인 존재였다는 사실이었다. 그래서 그는 먀흐카야 공작 부인이 도착해 둘만의 자리가 중단되자 상당히 안도했다.\n\n\"아, 여기 와 계셨군요!\" 그녀가 그를 보고 말했다. \"그래, 그 가엾은 누이동생은 어떻게 지내나요? 그렇게 쳐다보실 것 없어요.\" 그녀가 덧붙였다. \"그 여자보다 천 배는 더 못된 사람들이 죄다 그녀에게 등을 돌린 뒤로, 나는 그녀가 아주 훌륭한 일을 했다고 생각해 왔어요. 브론스키가 그녀가 페테르부르크에 있을 때 내게 알려 주지 않은 건 도저히 용서할 수 없어요. 알았더라면 보러 갔을 테고, 어디든 함께 다녔을 텐데. 부디 그녀에게 내 안부를 전해 주세요. 자, 그녀 이야기 좀 들려주세요.\"\n\n\"네, 그 애 처지가 무척 힘들지요. 그 애는……\" 스테판 아르카지치는 먀흐카야 공작 부인의 \"그녀 이야기 좀 들려주세요\"라는 말을 순진하게도 액면 그대로 받아들이고 말문을 열었다. 먀흐카야 공작 부인은 늘 그러듯 곧바로 그의 말을 가로막고 자기가 이야기하기 시작했다.\n\n\"그녀는 나만 빼고 다들 하는 짓을 한 거예요—다만 다른 사람들은 그걸 숨기죠. 한데 그녀는 거짓말을 하려 들지 않았고, 그래서 훌륭한 일을 한 거예요. 게다가 당신의 그 정신 나간 매제를 차 버린 건 더 잘한 일이고요. 양해해 주세요. 다들 그 사람이 아주 영리하다고, 정말 대단히 영리하다고 했지만, 그를 바보라고 한 건 나 혼자뿐이었어요. 이제 그가 리디아 이바노브나며 란다우와 그렇게 가까워지고 나니, 다들 그가 미쳤다고들 하네요. 나야 모두와 의견을 같이하기 싫지만, 이번만은 어쩔 수가 없군요.\"\n\n\"오, 제발 설명 좀 해 주세요.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 말했다. \"그게 무슨 뜻인가요? 어제 누이동생 일로 그를 만나서 최종 답변을 달라고 했지요. 그는 아무 답도 주지 않고, 생각해 보겠다고 했어요. 그런데 오늘 아침 답변 대신 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인에게서 오늘 저녁에 와 달라는 초대를 받았답니다.\"\n\n\"아, 그거였군요, 그거예요!\" 먀흐카야 공작 부인이 신이 나서 말했다. \"그들은 란다우에게 무슨 말을 해야 할지 물어보려는 거예요.\"\n\n\"란다우에게 물어본다고요? 무엇 때문에요? 도대체 란다우가 누구입니까, 뭐 하는 사람이에요?\"\n\n\"아니! 쥘 란다우를 모르세요, le fameux Jules Landau, le clairvoyant 말이에요? 그 사람도 미쳤지만, 당신 누이동생의 운명이 그 사람한테 달려 있어요. 시골에서 사니까 이렇게 되는 거예요—세상일을 아무것도 모르잖아요. 란다우는 말이죠, 파리의 어느 가게 점원이었는데, 의사한테 갔다가 그 의사의 대기실에서 잠이 들었어요. 그런데 잠든 채로 환자들 모두에게 충고를 하기 시작한 거예요. 그것도 놀라운 충고였죠! 그러다 유리 멜레딘스키—아시죠, 그 병약한 사람?—그 사람의 아내가 이 란다우 이야기를 듣고, 남편을 보게 했어요. 그러고는 그가 그녀의 남편을 고쳤는데, 내가 보기엔 별로 효험이 있었던 것 같진 않아요. 그 사람은 여전히 예전처럼 비실비실한 인간이니까요. 어쨌든 사람들이 그를 믿고, 데리고 다니다가 러시아까지 데려온 거예요. 여기서는 다들 그에게 우르르 몰려들었고, 그는 모든 사람을 치료하기 시작했죠. 베주보바 백작 부인을 고쳐 주었더니, 부인이 어찌나 그를 마음에 들어 했던지 그를 양자로 삼았답니다.\"\n* le fameux Jules Landau, le clairvoyant: 프랑스어로 '저 유명한 쥘 란다우, 그 천리안'이라는 뜻.\n\n\"양자로 삼았다고요?\"\n\n\"그래요, 아들로요. 그는 이제 더는 란다우가 아니라 베주보프 백작이에요. 하긴 그건 아무래도 상관없는 일이지만요. 다만 리디아가—난 그녀를 무척 좋아하지만, 어딘가 나사가 하나 빠진 사람이에요—이제 이 란다우에게 홀딱 마음을 빼앗겨서, 그녀의 집에서든 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 집에서든 그 사람 없이는 아무것도 결정되지 않아요. 그러니 당신 누이동생의 운명이 이제 란다우, 다시 말해 베주보프 백작의 손에 달려 있다는 거죠.\"\n\n# Chapter 21\n\n바르트냔스키 집에서 훌륭한 만찬을 들고 코냑을 잔뜩 마신 뒤, 스테판 아르카지치는 약속된 시각보다 아주 조금 늦게 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인의 집으로 들어섰다.\n\n\"백작 부인과 함께 또 누가 있나—프랑스인인가?\" 스테판 아르카지치는 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 낯익은 외투와, 어딘지 기묘하고 꽤나 소박해 보이는 죔쇠가 달린 외투를 흘끗 바라보며 현관지기에게 물었다.\n\n\"알렉세이 알렉산드로비치 카레닌 님과 베주보프 백작 님이십니다.\" 현관지기가 근엄하게 대답했다.\n\n'먀흐카야 공작 부인이 제대로 알아맞혔군.' 스테판 아르카지치는 계단을 오르며 생각했다. '신기한걸! 그래도 저 부인과 좋게 지내 두면 나쁘지 않겠어. 영향력이 어마어마하니까. 저 부인이 포모르스키에게 한마디만 해 준다면 그 일은 확실해질 거야.'\n\n바깥은 아직 환했지만, 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인의 작은 응접실에는 블라인드가 내려져 있었고 등불이 켜져 있었다. 등불 아래 둥근 탁자에 앉아 백작 부인과 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 나직이 이야기를 나누고 있었다. 키가 작고 다소 마른, 몹시 창백하고 잘생긴 한 남자가, 여자 같은 엉덩이에 안짱다리를 하고, 가늘게 빛나는 눈에 외투 깃 위로 긴 머리카락을 늘어뜨린 채 방 한쪽 끝에 서서 벽에 걸린 초상화들을 바라보고 있었다. 안주인과 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에게 인사를 건넨 뒤에도, 스테판 아르카지치는 그 낯선 남자를 다시 한 번 흘끗 바라보지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n\"무슈 랑도!\" 백작 부인이 오블론스키에게 깊은 인상을 줄 만큼 부드럽고 조심스럽게 그를 불렀다. 그러고는 두 사람을 서로에게 소개했다.\n\n랑도는 황급히 돌아보더니 다가와, 미소를 지으며 축축하고 생기 없는 손을 스테판 아르카지치가 내민 손에 얹었다가는 곧바로 물러나 다시 초상화들을 바라보기 시작했다. 백작 부인과 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 의미심장하게 서로를 마주 보았다.\n\n\"이렇게 와 주셔서 정말 기뻐요, 더구나 오늘 같은 날에.\" 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인은 스테판 아르카지치에게 카레닌 옆자리를 가리키며 말했다.\n\n\"제가 당신께 저분을 랑도라고 소개했지만,\" 그녀는 프랑스인을 흘끗 보았다가 곧바로 다시 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치를 보며 부드러운 목소리로 말했다. \"사실은 베주보프 백작이세요, 아마 알고 계실 테지만. 다만 저분이 그 칭호를 좋아하지 않으세요.\"\n\n\"네, 그렇게 들었습니다.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 대답했다. \"저분이 베주보바 백작 부인을 완전히 낫게 했다고들 하더군요.\"\n\n\"오늘 그분이 여기 오셨었어요, 가엾기도 하지!\" 백작 부인이 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치를 돌아보며 말했다. \"이 이별이 그분에게는 끔찍한 일이에요. 그분에게는 정말 큰 타격이지요!\"\n\n\"그래서 저분은 정말로 떠나는 겁니까?\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 물었다.\n\n\"네, 파리로 가신답니다. 어제 어떤 목소리를 들으셨대요.\" 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인이 스테판 아르카지치를 바라보며 말했다.\n\n\"아, 목소리를요!\" 오블론스키는, 자기가 열쇠를 쥐고 있지 못한 무언가 기묘한 일이 벌어지고 있거나 벌어질 참인 이 사교 모임에서 될 수 있는 한 신중을 기해야겠다고 느끼며 따라 말했다.\n\n잠시 침묵이 흘렀고, 그런 뒤 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인은 대화의 본론으로 다가가려는 듯, 우아한 미소를 띠며 오블론스키에게 말했다.\n\n\"오래전부터 당신을 알고 있었고, 이렇게 더 가까이 알게 되어 무척 기뻐요. Les amis de nos amis sont nos amis. 하지만 진정한 친구가 되려면 친구의 영적인 상태 속으로 들어가야 하는 법인데, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에 관해서는 당신이 그렇게 하고 있지 못한 것 같아 걱정이에요. 제 말이 무슨 뜻인지 아시겠어요?\" 그녀는 그 우아하고 사색에 잠긴 눈을 들어 올리며 말했다.\n* Les amis de nos amis sont nos amis: 프랑스어로 '우리 친구의 친구는 우리의 친구다'.\n\n\"부분적으로는요, 백작 부인, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 처지를 이해합니다…….\" 오블론스키가 말했다. 두 사람이 무슨 이야기를 하는지 명확히 알지 못했던 그는 두루뭉술한 말로 그치고 싶었다.\n\n\"달라진 것은 그분의 외적인 처지가 아니에요.\" 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인은, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 일어나 랑도에게로 건너가는 모습을 사랑이 담긴 눈으로 좇으며 엄숙하게 말했다. \"그분의 마음이 달라졌어요, 새 마음을 받으셨지요, 그래서 저는 당신이 그분에게 일어난 그 변화를 온전히 헤아리지 못하고 있는 게 아닌가 걱정이에요.\"\n\n\"오, 글쎄요, 대략적인 윤곽은 그 변화를 짐작할 수 있습니다. 저희는 늘 친하게 지내 왔고, 이제는…….\" 스테판 아르카지치는 백작 부인의 표정에 공감 어린 눈빛으로 화답하면서도, 속으로는 그녀가 두 장관 가운데 어느 쪽과 더 가까운지, 그래서 누구를 위해 자기 말을 넣어 달라고 부탁해야 할지를 가늠하며 말했다.\n\n\"그분에게 일어난 변화는 이웃에 대한 사랑을 줄어들게 할 수 없어요. 오히려 그 변화는 그분 마음속의 사랑을 더 강하게 할 뿐이지요. 하지만 당신이 제 말을 이해하지 못하시는 것 같아 걱정이에요. 차라도 좀 드시겠어요?\" 그녀는 쟁반에 차를 받쳐 들고 돌아다니는 하인을 눈짓으로 가리키며 말했다.\n\n\"잘 모르겠습니다, 백작 부인. 물론 그분의 불행은…….\"\n\n\"네, 불행이었지요, 하지만 그분의 마음이 새로워지고 그것으로 가득 찼을 때, 그것은 더없이 높은 행복임이 드러난 불행이었어요.\" 그녀는 사랑이 가득 담긴 눈으로 스테판 아르카지치를 바라보며 말했다.\n\n'두 사람 모두에게 말을 넣어 달라고 부탁해도 되겠는걸.' 스테판 아르카지치는 생각했다.\n\n\"오, 물론입니다, 백작 부인.\" 그가 말했다. \"다만 그런 변화는 너무나 사적인 일이라 누구도, 아무리 가까운 친구라도, 입에 올리고 싶어 하지 않을 것 같습니다만.\"\n\n\"천만에요! 우리는 거리낌 없이 이야기하며 서로 도와야 해요.\"\n\n\"네, 그야 분명 그렇지요, 하지만 신념의 차이라는 게 워낙 크고, 게다가…….\" 오블론스키가 부드러운 미소를 띠며 말했다.\n\n\"거룩한 진리가 문제일 때는 차이라는 게 있을 수 없어요.\"\n\n\"오, 그럼요, 물론이지요. 하지만…….\" 스테판 아르카지치는 당황하여 말을 멈추었다. 그제야 그는 두 사람이 종교 이야기를 하고 있다는 것을 깨달았다.\n\n\"저분이 곧 잠들 것 같습니다.\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 리디아 이바노브나에게 다가가며 의미심장하게 속삭였다.\n\n스테판 아르카지치는 돌아보았다. 랑도는 창가에 앉아 팔꿈치와 의자 등받이에 몸을 기댄 채 고개를 떨구고 있었다. 모든 시선이 자기에게 쏠린 것을 알아채자 그는 고개를 들고 어린아이처럼 천진한 미소를 지었다.\n\n\"신경 쓰지 마세요.\" 리디아 이바노브나가 말하며 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치를 위해 의자를 살짝 가져다 놓았다. \"제가 보기에는…….\" 그녀가 막 입을 떼는데, 하인이 편지를 들고 방으로 들어왔다. 리디아 이바노브나는 쪽지를 재빨리 훑어보고는 양해를 구한 뒤, 놀라울 만큼 빠르게 답장을 써서 하인에게 건네고 다시 탁자로 돌아왔다. \"제가 보기에는,\" 그녀가 말을 이었다. \"모스크바 사람들은, 특히 남자들은 누구보다도 종교에 무관심한 것 같아요.\"\n\n\"오, 그렇지 않습니다, 백작 부인. 저는 모스크바 사람들이 신앙심이 가장 굳건하다는 평판을 얻고 있다고 생각했는데요.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 대답했다.\n\n\"하지만 제가 알아본 바로는, 당신은 안타깝게도 그 무관심한 부류 가운데 한 사람이군요.\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 지친 미소를 띠며 그를 향해 말했다.\n\n\"어떻게 사람이 무관심할 수가 있어요!\" 리디아 이바노브나가 말했다.\n\n\"그 문제에 관해 제가 무관심하다기보다는, 마음을 정하지 못한 채 기다리고 있는 거지요.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 더없이 미안한 듯한 미소를 띠며 말했다. \"저로서는 아직 그런 문제를 따질 때가 오지 않은 것 같습니다.\"\n\n알렉세이 알렉산드로비치와 리디아 이바노브나가 서로를 마주 보았다.\n\n\"우리에게 그때가 왔는지 아닌지는 결코 알 수 없는 법입니다.\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 엄숙하게 말했다. \"우리가 준비됐는지 안 됐는지를 따져서는 안 됩니다. 하느님의 은총은 인간의 헤아림에 따라 움직이지 않으니까요. 때로 그것은 그것을 얻으려 애쓰는 자에게는 오지 않고, 사울처럼 준비되지 않은 자에게 오기도 합니다.\"\n\n\"아니에요, 제 생각엔 그게 아직은 아닐 것 같아요.\" 그동안 프랑스인의 움직임을 지켜보고 있던 리디아 이바노브나가 말했다. 랑도가 일어나 그들에게 다가왔다.\n\n\"제가 들어도 될까요?\" 그가 물었다.\n\n\"오, 그럼요. 방해하고 싶지 않았을 뿐이에요.\" 리디아 이바노브나가 다정하게 그를 바라보며 말했다. \"여기 우리와 함께 앉으세요.\"\n\n\"빛을 가리려면 눈을 감기만 하면 그뿐이지요.\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 말을 이었다.\n\n\"아, 우리가 아는 그 행복을, 우리 마음속에 늘 그분의 임재를 느끼는 그 행복을 당신이 안다면!\" 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인이 황홀한 미소를 띠며 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 사람은 때로 그런 높은 경지에 오르기에 자기가 미천하다고 느낄 수도 있지 않습니까.\" 스테판 아르카지치는 이런 종교적 경지를 인정하는 데 위선이 있음을 의식하면서도, 동시에 포모르스키에게 한마디만 하면 자기가 그토록 탐내는 자리를 마련해 줄 수 있는 사람 앞에서 차마 자기의 자유사상을 털어놓을 수 없어 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"그러니까, 죄가 그분을 가로막는다는 말씀인가요?\" 리디아 이바노브나가 말했다. \"하지만 그것은 잘못된 생각이에요. 믿는 자에게는 죄가 없어요, 그들의 죄는 이미 속죄되었으니까요. Pardon.\" 그녀는 또다시 다른 편지를 들고 들어온 하인을 바라보며 덧붙였다. 그녀는 그것을 읽고 구두로 답했다. \"내일 대공비 전하 댁에서, 라고 전해요.\" \"믿는 자에게 죄란 없는 거예요.\" 그녀가 말을 이었다.\n\n* Pardon: 프랑스어로 '실례합니다'.\n\"네, 하지만 행위가 없는 믿음은 죽은 것이지요.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 교리문답에서 본 구절을 떠올리며, 오직 그 미소로써만 자기 독자성에 매달리며 말했다.\n\n\"바로 그거예요—야고보서의 한 구절이지요.\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 어딘지 책망하는 어조로 리디아 이바노브나를 향해 말했다. 그것은 두 사람이 전에도 한두 번 이야기를 나눈 적이 있는 화제임이 분명했다. \"그 구절을 잘못 해석한 탓에 얼마나 많은 해악이 빚어졌는지! 그 그릇된 해석만큼 사람들을 믿음에서 가로막는 것은 없습니다. '나에게는 행위가 없으니 믿을 수가 없다'는 식이지요, 정작 거기엔 그런 말이 적혀 있지도 않은데 말입니다. 오히려 정반대의 말이 적혀 있어요.\"\n\n\"하느님을 향해 애쓰고, 단식으로 영혼을 구하려 한다는 것,\" 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인이 진저리 나는 듯한 경멸을 담아 말했다. \"그건 우리 수도사들의 투박한 생각이에요…… 하지만 어디에도 그런 말은 없어요. 훨씬 더 단순하고 쉬운 일이지요.\" 그녀는 궁정에서 새로운 분위기에 어쩔 줄 몰라 하는 젊은 시녀들을 격려할 때 짓던 바로 그 격려의 미소를 띠며 오블론스키를 바라보고 덧붙였다.\n\n\"우리는 우리를 위해 고난받으신 그리스도로 말미암아 구원받습니다. 우리는 믿음으로 구원받지요.\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 그녀의 말에 동의하는 눈빛을 보내며 거들었다.\n\n\"Vous comprenez l'anglais?\" 리디아 이바노브나가 물었고, 그렇다는 대답을 듣자 자리에서 일어나 책꽂이를 살펴보기 시작했다.\n\n* Vous comprenez l'anglais?: 프랑스어로 '영어를 이해하세요?'.\n\"이분께 「안전과 행복」이나 「날개 아래서」를 읽어 드리고 싶어요.\" 그녀는 카레닌을 미심쩍게 바라보며 말했다. 그러고는 책을 찾아 다시 제자리에 앉아 그것을 펼쳤다. \"아주 짧아요. 여기엔 믿음에 이르는 길과, 모든 지상의 기쁨을 넘어서서 영혼을 가득 채우는 그 행복이 그려져 있어요. 믿는 자는 혼자가 아니므로 불행할 수 없는 법이지요. 직접 보시게 될 거예요.\" 그녀가 막 자세를 고쳐 앉아 읽으려는데 하인이 또 들어왔다. \"보로즈디나 부인? 내일 두 시에 오시라고 전해요. 그래요.\" 그녀는 책의 그 자리에 손가락을 끼운 채 우아하고 사색에 잠긴 눈으로 앞을 바라보며 말했다. \"참된 믿음이란 그렇게 작용하는 거예요. 마리 사니나를 아세요? 그분의 불행에 대해 들으셨나요? 그분은 외동아이를 잃었어요. 절망에 빠져 있었지요. 그런데 어떻게 됐을까요? 그분은 이 위안자를 찾았고, 지금은 자식의 죽음에 대해 하느님께 감사드린답니다. 믿음이 가져다주는 행복이란 바로 그런 거예요!\"\n\n\"오, 그렇군요, 그건 정말이지…….\" 스테판 아르카지치는 이제 책을 읽게 되어 정신을 추스를 기회가 생긴 것을 다행으로 여기며 말했다. '안 되겠어, 오늘은 부인에게 아무것도 부탁하지 않는 게 좋겠어.' 그는 생각했다. '괜한 실수만 하지 않고 이 자리를 빠져나갈 수 있기만 하면!'\n\n\"당신께는 따분할 거예요.\" 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인이 랑도를 향해 말했다. \"영어를 모르시니까요. 하지만 짧답니다.\"\n\n\"오, 저는 이해할 겁니다.\" 랑도는 여전한 그 미소를 띠며 말하고는 눈을 감았다. 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치와 리디아 이바노브나가 의미심장한 눈빛을 주고받았고, 낭독이 시작되었다.\n\n# Chapter 22\n\n스테판 아르카지치는 난생처음 듣는 이 기묘한 이야기 앞에서 완전히 어쩔 줄을 몰랐다. 페테르부르크의 그 복잡함은 대개 그를 자극하여 모스크바에서의 침체로부터 그를 일깨워 주곤 했다. 하지만 그가 그런 복잡함을 좋아하고 또 이해할 수 있는 것은 자기가 잘 아는, 내 집처럼 편안한 사교 모임에서뿐이었다. 이 낯선 환경 속에서 그는 어리둥절하고 당황스러워 갈피를 잡지 못했다. 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인의 말에 귀를 기울이면서, 그리고 자기에게 고정된 란다우의 아름답고 천진한—아니, 어쩌면 교활한, 어느 쪽인지 단정할 수 없는—눈을 의식하면서, 스테판 아르카지치는 머릿속에서 묘한 무거움을 느끼기 시작했다.\n\n머릿속에서는 도무지 어울리지 않는 생각들이 뒤죽박죽이었다. '마리 사니나는 제 아이가 죽어서 기뻐한다는군…… 지금 담배 한 대 피우면 얼마나 좋을까!…… 구원을 받으려면 그저 믿기만 하면 되는데, 수도사들은 그게 어떻게 이뤄지는지도 모르면서, 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인은 안다는 거지…… 그런데 왜 내 머리는 이렇게 무거울까? 코냑 탓인가, 아니면 이 모든 게 너무 기묘해서 그런 건가? 어쨌든 지금까지는 부적절한 짓은 한 게 없는 것 같은데. 하지만 어쨌든 지금 그 여자에게 부탁을 꺼낼 수는 없겠어. 사람들 말로는 여기선 기도를 시킨다던데. 제발 나한테는 시키지 말아야 할 텐데! 그건 너무 멍청한 짓이 될 거야. 그리고 저 여자가 읽고 있는 건 또 무슨 헛소리람! 하지만 발음 하나는 좋군. 란다우—베주보프—이 사람이 왜 베주보프인 거지?' 그 순간 스테판 아르카지치는 자기 아래턱이 도무지 어쩔 수 없이 하품을 만들어 내고 있음을 알아차렸다. 그는 하품을 가리려 구레나룻을 잡아당기며 몸을 추슬렀다. 하지만 곧이어 자기가 잠에 빠져들고 있으며 막 코를 골 지경에 이르렀음을 깨달았다. 그는 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인의 목소리가 \"잠드셨네요\" 하고 말하던 바로 그 순간에 정신을 차렸다. 스테판 아르카지치는 죄를 짓다 들킨 듯한 기분으로 깜짝 놀라며 움찔했다. 하지만 그 \"잠드셨네요\"라는 말이 자기가 아니라 란다우를 가리킨 것임을 알고는 곧 안심했다. 그 프랑스인이 스테판 아르카지치와 마찬가지로 잠들어 있었던 것이다. 그러나 스테판 아르카지치가 잠든 것은 그들의 기분을 상하게 했을 테지만(하긴 이것도 그렇지 않을지 모른다고, 모든 게 너무도 기묘해 보였으므로 그는 생각했다), 란다우가 잠든 것은 그들을, 특히 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인을 더없이 기쁘게 했다.\n\n\"Mon ami,\" 리디아 이바노브나가 비단 가운 자락이 바스락거리지 않도록 조심스레 붙잡고, 흥분한 나머지 카레닌을 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 아니라 \"mon ami\"라고 부르며 말했다. \"donnez-lui la main. Vous voyez? 쉿!\" 그녀는 다시 들어오는 하인을 향해 쉿 소리를 냈다. \"안 계신다고 해.\"\n* Mon ami: 프랑스어로 '내 친구'. donnez-lui la main. Vous voyez?: '그에게 손을 건네세요. 보이시죠?'\n\n그 프랑스인은 머리를 의자 등받이에 기댄 채 잠들어 있거나 잠든 척하고 있었고, 무릎 위에 놓인 그의 축축한 손은 마치 무언가를 잡으려는 듯 희미하게 움직였다. 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 일어나 조심스레 움직이려 했으나 탁자에 부딪치고는, 다가가 자기 손을 프랑스인의 손에 얹었다. 스테판 아르카지치도 일어나, 자기가 잠들어 있는 것이라면 깨어 보려는 듯 두 눈을 크게 뜨고 먼저 한 사람을, 그다음에 다른 사람을 바라보았다. 모든 게 실제였다. 스테판 아르카지치는 자기 머리가 점점 더 나빠지고 있음을 느꼈다.\n\"Que la personne qui est arrivée la dernière, celle qui demande, qu’elle sorte! Qu’elle sorte!\" 프랑스인이 눈도 뜨지 않은 채 또렷이 말했다.\n\n* '가장 늦게 도착한 사람, 청을 하려는 그 사람을 나가게 하시오! 나가게 하시오!'\n\"Vous m’excuserez, mais vous voyez…… Revenez vers dix heures, encore mieux demain.\"\n\n* '용서하시구려, 하지만 보시다시피…… 열 시쯤 다시 오시오, 아니면 내일이 더 좋겠소.'\n\n\"Qu’elle sorte!\" 프랑스인이 초조하게 되풀이했다.\n\n* '그 사람을 나가게 하시오!'\n\"C’est moi, n’est-ce pas?\" 그리고 그렇다는 대답을 듣고서, 스테판 아르카지치는 리디아 이바노브나에게 청하려던 부탁도 잊고, 누이의 일도 잊고, 아무것도 안중에 없이 오로지 한시바삐 이 자리를 벗어나고 싶은 마음만으로 가득 차, 발끝으로 살금살금 빠져나가서는 페스트가 도는 집에서 도망치듯 거리로 뛰쳐나왔다. 그는 한참 동안 마부와 잡담을 나누고 농담을 던지며 기분을 추스르려 애썼다.\n\n* '저 말이군요, 그렇죠?'\n마지막 막에 맞춰 도착한 프랑스 극장에서, 그리고 그다음 타타르 음식점에서 샴페인을 마신 뒤, 스테판 아르카지치는 늘 익숙하던 분위기 속에서 조금쯤 기운을 되찾았다. 하지만 그래도 그날 저녁 내내 그는 영 자기답지 못한 기분이었다.\n\n자기가 묵고 있던 표트르 오블론스키의 집에 돌아온 스테판 아르카지치는 벳시가 보낸 쪽지를 발견했다. 그녀는 중단되었던 둘의 대화를 무척 마무리하고 싶다며, 다음 날 와 달라고 부탁하는 편지를 써 두었다. 그가 이 쪽지를 채 다 읽고 그 내용에 미간을 찌푸리기도 전에, 아래쪽에서 하인들이 무언가 무거운 것을 나르며 무겁게 쿵쿵거리는 발소리가 들려왔다.\n\n스테판 아르카지치는 보려고 나갔다. 그것은 회춘한 표트르 오블론스키였다. 그는 너무 취해서 계단을 올라오지도 못할 지경이었지만, 스테판 아르카지치를 보자 사람들에게 자기를 두 발로 세워 달라고 하고는, 그에게 매달려 함께 방으로 들어가, 거기서 저녁을 어떻게 보냈는지 늘어놓기 시작하더니 그러다가 잠들어 버렸다.\n\n스테판 아르카지치는 좀처럼 드문 일이지만 몹시 기분이 가라앉아 있었고, 한참 동안 잠을 이루지 못했다. 머릿속에 떠올릴 수 있는 것은 무엇이든 다 역겨웠다. 하지만 그중에서도 가장 역겨운 것은, 마치 무언가 수치스러운 일이라도 되는 양, 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인의 집에서 보낸 그 저녁의 기억이었다.\n\n다음 날 그는 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치로부터 안나의 이혼을 허락할 수 없다는 최종 답변을 받았고, 그 결정이 그 프랑스인이 진짜인지 가짜인지 모를 무아지경 속에서 한 말에 근거하고 있다는 것을 알아차렸다.\n\n# Chapter 23\n\n가정생활에서 어떤 일이든 끝까지 해내려면, 남편과 아내 사이에 완전한 결별이 있거나 아니면 사랑에 찬 화합이 있거나, 둘 중 하나여야 한다. 부부의 관계가 흔들리고 이것도 저것도 아닐 때는 그 어떤 일도 도모할 수가 없다.\n\n많은 가정이 남편과 아내가 둘 다 진절머리를 내면서도 여러 해 동안 같은 자리에 머무는데, 그것은 그저 그들 사이에 완전한 결별도 화합도 없기 때문이다.\n\n브론스키도 안나도 모스크바의 삶이 더위와 먼지 속에서 견딜 수 없다고 느꼈다. 봄 햇살이 지나가고 여름의 작열하는 빛이 뒤따랐으며, 가로수 길의 나무들은 진작부터 잎이 무성했고, 그 잎사귀들은 먼지로 뒤덮여 있었다. 하지만 두 사람은 오래전부터 그러기로 약속했던 보즈드비젠스코예로 돌아가지 않았다. 둘 다 모스크바를 몹시 싫어하면서도 거기 계속 머물렀는데, 요즘 들어 둘 사이에 화합이 없었던 까닭이다.\n\n두 사람을 갈라놓는 그 짜증에는 외적인 원인이 없었고, 화해해 보려는 온갖 노력은 그것을 없애기는커녕 오히려 더 키웠다. 그것은 내면의 짜증이었으니, 그녀의 마음속에서는 그의 사랑이 식었다는 확신에 뿌리를 두었고, 그의 마음속에서는 그녀를 위해 곤란한 처지에 스스로를 빠뜨렸는데도 그녀가 그것을 덜어 주기는커녕 더 곤란하게 만든다는 후회에 뿌리를 두었다. 둘 중 누구도 자기 불만을 온전히 입 밖에 내지는 않았지만, 서로를 잘못했다고 여겼고, 무슨 핑계든 잡아 그것을 상대에게 증명하려 들었다.\n\n그녀의 눈에는 그의 모든 것이, 그의 온갖 습관, 생각, 욕망, 그의 정신적·육체적 기질 전부가 한 가지로 귀결되었다. 여자에 대한 사랑이었다. 그리고 그 사랑은, 그녀가 느끼기에, 마땅히 자기 한 사람에게만 온전히 집중되어야 하는 것이었다. 그 사랑이 줄어들었다. 따라서, 그녀의 추론대로라면, 그는 자기 사랑의 일부를 다른 여자들에게, 혹은 또 다른 한 여자에게 옮긴 것이 분명했다. 그래서 그녀는 질투했다. 그녀는 어떤 특정한 여자를 질투한 것이 아니라 그의 사랑이 줄어든 것을 질투했다. 질투의 대상을 아직 찾지 못한 채, 그녀는 그것을 찾아 두리번거렸다. 아주 사소한 낌새만 보여도 그녀는 질투를 이 대상에서 저 대상으로 옮겼다. 어느 때는 그가 너무도 쉽게 옛 독신 시절의 관계를 다시 맺을 수 있을 천한 여자들을 질투했고, 그다음에는 그가 만날지도 모를 사교계 여자들을 질투했고, 또 그다음에는 그가 결혼하고 싶어 할지도 모를, 그녀를 위해 자기를 버릴지도 모를 상상 속의 처녀를 질투했다. 그리고 이 마지막 형태의 질투가 그녀를 가장 괴롭혔는데, 특히 그가 솔직해진 어느 순간 경솔하게도, 자기 어머니가 자기를 너무 몰라서 뻔뻔하게도 젊은 소로키나 공작 영애와 결혼하라고 설득하려 들었다고 말해 버린 탓이었다.\n\n그리고 그를 질투하는 동안 안나는 그에게 분개했고, 모든 것에서 분개할 거리를 찾아냈다. 자기 처지에서 힘든 일은 무엇이든 그의 탓으로 돌렸다. 모스크바에서 겪은 그 괴로운 불안의 상태도, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 굼뜸과 우유부단함도, 자기의 고독도, 그녀는 모조리 그의 탓으로 여겼다. 그가 그녀를 사랑했다면 자기 처지의 온갖 쓰라림을 알아보았을 테고, 그녀를 거기서 구해 냈을 것이다. 그녀가 시골이 아니라 모스크바에 있는 것도 그의 탓이었다. 그는 그녀가 원하는 대로 시골에 파묻혀 살 수 없는 사람이었다. 그에게는 사교계가 있어야 했고, 그래서 그는 그녀를 이 끔찍한 처지에 빠뜨려 놓고도 그 쓰라림을 보려 하지 않았다. 게다가 그녀가 아들과 영영 떨어지게 된 것도 그의 잘못이었다.\n\n이따금 찾아오는 드문 다정함의 순간조차 그녀를 달래 주지 못했다. 이제 그의 다정함 속에서 그녀는 예전에는 없던 흡족함과 자신만만함의 그늘을 보았고, 그것이 그녀를 격분하게 했다.\n\n해 질 녘이었다. 안나는 혼자였고, 그가 독신 남자들의 만찬에서 돌아오기를 기다리고 있었다. 그녀는 그의 서재(거리의 소음이 가장 덜 들리는 방이었다)를 이리저리 거닐며 어제 다툰 일을 낱낱이 곱씹었다. 다툼에서 똑똑히 기억나는 모진 말들로부터 그 발단이 되었던 것으로 거슬러 올라가, 그녀는 마침내 그 시초에 다다랐다. 한참 동안 그녀는 그토록 악의 없고, 두 사람 모두에게 하찮기 그지없던 대화에서 자기들의 불화가 비롯되었다는 것을 좀처럼 믿을 수가 없었다. 하지만 실제로 그러했다. 모든 것은 그가 여자 고등학교를 비웃으며 그런 것은 쓸데없다고 단언하고, 그녀는 그것을 옹호한 데서 비롯되었다. 그는 여성 교육 일반을 깔보듯 말했고, 안나의 영국인 양녀 한나가 물리학을 조금이라도 알 필요는 전혀 없다고 했다.\n\n이 말에 안나는 짜증이 났다. 그녀는 그 안에서 자기가 하는 일에 대한 멸시의 암시를 보았다. 그래서 그녀는 그가 안긴 아픔을 되갚아 줄 한마디를 떠올렸다. \"당신이 나를, 내 감정을, 나를 사랑하는 사람이라면 누구나 그러듯 이해해 주기를 바라는 건 아니에요. 하지만 그저 약간의 배려는 기대했어요.\" 그녀는 말했다.\n\n그러자 그는 정말로 분에 못 이겨 얼굴을 붉혔고, 무언가 불쾌한 말을 했다. 그녀는 자기가 무어라 대꾸했는지는 기억나지 않았지만, 바로 그 대목에서 그가 자기 또한 그녀에게 상처를 주고 싶다는 분명한 욕망을 품은 채 이렇게 말했던 것은 떠올랐다.\n\n\"당신이 그 여자애한테 그렇게 빠져 있는 것에 나는 아무 흥미도 못 느껴, 그건 사실이야. 자연스럽지 못한 일로 보이니까.\"\n\n그녀가 그토록 고된 삶을 견뎌 내려고 그렇게 애써서 쌓아 올린 세계를 그가 산산이 부수어 버린 그 잔인함, 그녀를 꾸밈이라고, 가식이라고 몰아붙인 그 부당함이 그녀를 들끓게 했다.\n\n\"당신에게는 거칠고 물질적인 것 말고는 아무것도 이해되지도, 자연스럽지도 않다니 참 안됐네요.\" 그녀는 이렇게 말하고 방을 나가 버렸다.\n\n어제저녁 그가 그녀에게 들어왔을 때, 두 사람은 다툼을 입에 올리지 않았지만, 둘 다 다툼이 무마되긴 했어도 끝나지는 않았다고 느꼈다.\n\n오늘 그는 하루 종일 집에 없었고, 그와 사이가 틀어진 채로 지내는 것이 너무도 외롭고 비참하게 느껴진 나머지 그녀는 모든 것을 잊고, 그를 용서하고, 그와 화해하고 싶었다. 그녀는 모든 잘못을 자기에게 돌리고 그를 두둔하고 싶었다.\n\n\"잘못한 건 나야. 난 신경질적이고, 미친 듯이 질투하지. 그이와 화해하고, 우리 시골로 떠나자. 거기서라면 좀 더 마음이 편안해질 거야.\"\n\n\"자연스럽지 못하다니!\" 그녀는 무엇보다 자기를 가장 아프게 찔렀던 그 말을 문득 떠올렸다. 그 말 자체보다도 그것을 내뱉을 때 담겼던, 자기에게 상처를 주려는 의도가 더 아팠다. \"그이가 무슨 뜻으로 한 말인지 알아. 그이 말은—내 친딸도 사랑하지 않으면서 남의 자식을 사랑하는 게 자연스럽지 못하다는 거지. 그이가 아이들에 대한 사랑을, 그이를 위해 내가 희생한 세료자에 대한 내 사랑을 뭘 알겠어? 하지만 나를 상처 주려는 그 마음이라니! 아니, 그이는 다른 여자를 사랑하는 거야, 틀림없이 그래.\"\n\n그리고 마음의 평온을 되찾으려 애쓰는 동안 자기가 예전에도 그토록 자주 돌고 돌았던 바로 그 원을 또다시 돌아, 이전의 격분 상태로 되돌아왔음을 깨닫자, 그녀는 스스로에게 소름이 끼쳤다. \"이게 정말 안 되는 일일까? 내가 나 자신을 다스리는 게 정말 내 힘으로 안 되는 일일까?\" 그녀는 혼잣말을 하고는 다시 처음부터 시작했다. \"그이는 진실하고, 정직하고, 나를 사랑해. 나도 그이를 사랑하고, 며칠 뒤면 이혼이 이루어질 거야. 더 바랄 게 뭐가 있어? 나는 마음의 평온과 신뢰를 원하니, 잘못은 내가 떠안겠어. 그래, 이제 그이가 들어오면, 사실은 잘못이 없었지만 내가 잘못했다고 말하고, 우리는 내일 떠나는 거야.\"\n\n그리고 더는 생각하지 않으려고, 또 짜증에 휩싸이지 않으려고, 그녀는 종을 울려 시골로 가져갈 짐을 꾸리게 트렁크를 올려 오라고 일렀다.\n\n열 시에 브론스키가 들어왔다.\n\n# Chapter 24\n\n\"그래, 즐거웠어요?\" 그녀는 뉘우치는 듯한 온순한 표정으로 그를 맞으러 나오며 물었다.\n\n\"여느 때와 같았지.\" 그녀가 기분 좋은 상태라는 것을 한눈에 알아채고 그가 대답했다. 그는 이제 이런 변화에 익숙해져 있었고, 오늘은 자기 자신도 유난히 기분이 좋았던 터라 그것을 보는 것이 특히 반가웠다.\n\n\"이게 다 뭐지? 이거 잘됐군!\" 그가 복도에 놓인 짐 상자들을 가리키며 말했다.\n\n\"네, 우리 떠나야죠. 마차를 타고 나갔다가 날이 어찌나 좋던지 시골에 가고 싶어졌어요. 당신을 붙잡아 둘 일은 없죠, 그렇죠?\"\n\n\"내가 바라는 게 바로 그거야. 곧 돌아와서 의논합시다. 옷만 갈아입으면 돼. 차나 좀 내오라고 해요.\"\n\n그러고는 자기 방으로 들어갔다.\n\n그가 \"이거 잘됐군\"이라고 말한 투에는 어딘가 모욕적인 데가 있었다. 마치 아이가 떼쓰기를 그쳤을 때 어른이 건네는 말투 같았다. 그리고 그보다 더 모욕적인 것은 뉘우치는 그녀의 어조와 자신만만한 그의 어조 사이의 대조였다. 한순간 그녀 안에서 다시 다투고 싶은 욕망이 솟구쳤지만, 그녀는 안간힘을 써서 그것을 억눌렀고, 전과 다름없이 기분 좋게 브론스키를 맞이했다.\n\n그가 들어오자 그녀는 미리 준비해 둔 말들을 군데군데 되풀이하면서, 하루를 어떻게 보냈는지, 그리고 떠날 계획을 그에게 이야기했다.\n\n\"있잖아요, 거의 영감처럼 떠올랐어요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"여기서 이혼을 기다릴 게 뭐 있어요? 시골에서나 여기서나 마찬가지 아닌가요? 난 더는 못 기다리겠어요! 더 이상 희망을 품고 싶지도 않고, 이혼에 관해 아무 말도 듣고 싶지 않아요. 그 일이 내 삶에 더는 아무 영향도 끼치지 못하도록 마음을 정했어요. 당신도 동의하죠?\"\n\n\"오, 그럼!\" 그가 흥분한 그녀의 얼굴을 불안하게 흘끔 보며 말했다.\n\n\"무얼 했어요? 누가 왔던가요?\" 잠시 침묵하다가 그녀가 물었다.\n\n브론스키는 손님들의 이름을 댔다. \"만찬은 일류였고, 보트 경주도 있었지. 다 그런대로 유쾌했어. 하지만 모스크바에서는 뭔가 ridicule한 것 없이는 무슨 일이든 도무지 해내질 못한다니까. 어떤 부류의 여자가 나타났는데, 스웨덴 여왕에게 수영을 가르치는 교사라더군. 우리한테 자기 솜씨를 한바탕 선보였지.\"\n* ridicule: 프랑스어로 '우스꽝스러운'.\n\n\"어떻게요? 헤엄을 쳤단 말이에요?\" 안나가 미간을 찌푸리며 물었다.\n\n\"우스꽝스러운 빨간 costume de natation 차림으로 말이야. 게다가 늙고 흉측했지. 그래, 우리는 언제 떠날까?\"\n\n\"참 별난 취향이네요! 그래서, 무슨 특별한 방식으로 헤엄을 쳤다는 거예요?\" 안나는 그의 물음에는 대답하지 않고 말했다.\n\n* costume de natation: 프랑스어로 '수영복'.\n\"전혀 별것 없었어. 그러니까 내가 하는 말이, 정말 어처구니없이 시시했다는 거지. 자, 그래서 언제 떠날 생각이야?\"\n\n안나는 어떤 불쾌한 생각을 떨쳐 내려는 듯 고개를 저었다.\n\n\"언제냐고요? 빠르면 빠를수록 좋죠! 내일은 준비가 안 될 거예요. 모레로 해요.\"\n\n\"그래…… 아, 아니, 잠깐만! 모레가 일요일이로군. 그날은 maman을 뵈러 가야 해.\" 브론스키가 당황하며 말했다. 어머니의 이름을 입에 올리자마자 자기를 응시하는 그녀의 의심스러운 눈길을 의식했기 때문이다. 그의 당황은 그녀의 의심을 굳혀 놓았다. 그녀는 확 달아오르며 그에게서 몸을 뺐다. 이제 안나의 상상을 채운 것은 스웨덴 여왕의 수영 교사가 아니라 젊은 소로키나 공작 영애였다. 그녀는 모스크바 근교의 마을에서 브론스카야 백작 부인과 함께 머무르고 있었다.\n* maman: 프랑스어로 '엄마'.\n\n\"내일 못 가나요?\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"글쎄, 안 돼! 내가 거기 가려는 그 일에 필요한 서류와 돈을 내일까지는 마련할 수가 없어.\" 그가 대답했다.\n\n\"그렇다면 아예 가지 말죠.\"\n\n\"아니, 왜 그래?\"\n\n\"난 더 나중에는 안 가요. 월요일 아니면 영영 안 가요!\"\n\n\"왜 그러는데?\" 브론스키가 어이없다는 듯이 말했다. \"그건 아무 의미도 없잖아!\"\n\n\"당신한테야 아무 의미가 없겠죠. 당신은 나한테 조금도 관심이 없으니까. 내 삶을 이해할 마음조차 없으니까. 여기서 내가 마음 쓴 단 한 가지가 한나였어요. 당신은 그게 가식이라고 하죠. 어제도 내가 내 딸은 사랑하지 않으면서 이 영국 여자애를 사랑한다고, 그건 자연스럽지 못한 일이라고 했잖아요. 나한테 자연스러울 수 있는 삶이라는 게 도대체 어떤 건지 알고 싶군요!\"\n\n한순간 그녀는 자기가 무슨 짓을 하고 있는지 똑똑히 보았고, 자기가 결심에서 얼마나 멀어졌는지에 소스라쳤다. 하지만 그것이 자기 자신의 파멸이라는 걸 알면서도, 그녀는 스스로를 억누를 수 없었고, 그가 틀렸다는 것을 그에게 증명하지 않을 수 없었으며, 그에게 굽힐 수 없었다.\n\n\"난 그런 말 한 적 없어. 다만 이 갑작스러운 열정에 공감하지 않는다고 말했을 뿐이야.\"\n\n\"그런데 어쩌면, 그렇게 자신의 솔직함을 자랑하면서도 진실을 말하지 않는 거죠?\"\n\n\"난 자랑한 적도 없고 거짓말한 적도 없어.\" 그는 치미는 분노를 억누르며 천천히 말했다. \"당신이 존중할 줄 모른다면 그건 참으로 유감스러운 일이지…….\"\n\n\"존중이란 사랑이 있어야 할 빈자리를 가리려고 만들어 낸 말이에요. 그리고 당신이 더 이상 날 사랑하지 않는다면, 차라리 그렇다고 말하는 편이 더 낫고 더 정직할 거예요.\"\n\n\"아니, 이건 정말 견딜 수가 없군!\" 브론스키가 의자에서 벌떡 일어나며 소리쳤다. 그러고는 우뚝 멈춰 서서 그녀를 마주 보며 또박또박 힘주어 말했다. \"당신은 무엇 때문에 내 인내심을 시험하는 거지?\" 그는 훨씬 더 많은 말을 할 수도 있었으나 참고 있다는 표정이었다. \"거기에도 한계라는 게 있어.\"\n\n\"그게 무슨 뜻이죠?\" 그녀가 외쳤다. 그의 얼굴 전체에, 특히 잔인하고 위협적인 그의 두 눈에 가득한 노골적인 증오를 공포에 질려 바라보면서.\n\n\"내 말은…….\" 그가 입을 떼다가 자제했다. \"당신이 나한테 원하는 게 뭔지 묻고 싶을 뿐이야.\"\n\n\"내가 뭘 원할 수 있겠어요? 내가 원할 수 있는 건 오직 당신이 지금 생각하고 있는 것처럼 날 버리지 않는 것뿐이에요.\" 그녀는 그가 입 밖에 내지 않은 모든 것을 알아채고 말했다. \"하지만 그건 내가 원하는 게 아니에요. 그건 부차적인 거죠. 난 사랑을 원해요. 그런데 사랑이 없어요. 그러니 모든 게 끝난 거예요.\"\n\n그녀가 문 쪽으로 돌아섰다.\n\n\"멈춰! 머, 멈추라고!\" 브론스키가 음울한 눈썹의 주름을 조금도 바꾸지 않은 채 말했다. 그러면서도 그는 그녀의 손을 붙잡았다. \"도대체 무슨 일로 이러는 거야? 난 떠나는 걸 사흘만 미루자고 했을 뿐인데, 그 말에 당신은 내가 거짓말을 한다느니, 내가 명예롭지 못한 사람이라느니 했잖아.\"\n\n\"그래요, 그리고 다시 말하지만, 나를 위해 모든 걸 희생했다며 나를 나무라는 남자는,\" 그녀가 더 오래전 다툼 때의 말을 떠올리며 말했다. \"그런 남자는 명예롭지 못한 사람보다 더 나빠요. 그는 무정한 사람이에요.\"\n\n\"오, 인내에도 한계가 있어!\" 그가 외치며 황급히 그녀의 손을 놓았다.\n\n'그는 날 미워해, 그건 분명해.' 그녀는 생각했고, 아무 말 없이 뒤도 돌아보지 않은 채 휘청거리는 걸음으로 방을 나섰다. '그는 다른 여자를 사랑해, 그건 더 분명하지.' 자기 방으로 들어가면서 그녀는 혼잣말을 했다. '난 사랑을 원하는데, 사랑이 없어. 그러니 모든 게 끝난 거야.' 그녀는 자기가 했던 말을 되풀이했다. '그래서 끝을 내야만 해.'\n\n'하지만 어떻게?' 그녀는 스스로에게 물으며 거울 앞 낮은 의자에 앉았다.\n\n이제 어디로 갈까, 자기를 키워 준 이모에게로 갈까, 돌리에게로 갈까, 아니면 그저 혼자 외국으로 떠날까 하는 생각, 그가 지금 서재에 혼자서 무얼 하고 있을까 하는 생각, 이것이 마지막 다툼일까 아니면 아직 화해가 가능할까 하는 생각, 페테르부르크의 옛 친구들이 이제 자기를 두고 뭐라고 할까 하는 생각, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 이 일을 어떻게 받아들일까 하는 생각, 그리고 이 파국 뒤에 이제 무슨 일이 벌어질까 하는 온갖 생각이 그녀의 머릿속에 떠올랐다. 하지만 그녀는 그 생각들에 온 마음을 다해 빠져들지 않았다. 그녀의 마음 밑바닥에는 그녀의 관심을 끄는 어떤 어렴풋한 생각 하나가 있었는데, 그녀는 그것을 또렷이 들여다볼 수 없었다. 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치를 다시 떠올리던 그녀는 출산 후 앓았던 때를, 그리고 그 무렵 한시도 자기를 떠나지 않던 그 감정을 떠올렸다. '나는 왜 죽지 않았을까?' 그때의 말과 그때의 감정이 그녀에게 되돌아왔다. 그리고 별안간 그녀는 자기 영혼 속에 무엇이 있는지 알았다. 그래, 모든 것을 풀어 줄 단 하나의 생각은 바로 그것이었다. '그래, 죽는 거야!…… 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치와 세료자의 부끄러움과 치욕도, 나의 끔찍한 수치도, 죽음으로 모두 구원받을 거야. 죽는 거야! 그러면 그는 자책하겠지. 미안해하겠지. 나를 사랑하겠지. 나 때문에 괴로워할 거야.' 자기 자신에 대한 연민의 미소를 어렴풋이 띤 채 그녀는 안락의자에 앉아, 왼손의 반지들을 뺐다 끼웠다 하면서, 자기가 죽은 뒤 그가 느낄 감정들을 여러 각도에서 생생하게 그려 보았다.\n\n가까이 다가오는 발소리—그의 발소리—가 그녀의 주의를 흩뜨렸다. 그녀는 반지를 매만지는 데 몰두한 척, 그를 향해 돌아보지도 않았다.\n\n그가 그녀에게 다가와 손을 잡으며 부드럽게 말했다.\n\n\"안나, 당신이 원한다면 우리 모레 떠납시다. 난 무엇이든 다 동의할게.\"\n\n그녀는 말이 없었다.\n\n\"왜 그래?\" 그가 채근했다.\n\n\"당신도 알잖아요.\" 그녀가 말했고, 바로 그 순간 더는 자제하지 못하고 흐느껴 울음을 터뜨렸다.\n\n\"날 내쳐 버려요!\" 그녀가 흐느낌 사이로 또박또박 말했다. \"난 내일 떠날게요…… 더한 일도 할 거예요. 내가 뭐죠? 부정한 여자예요! 당신 목에 매달린 돌덩이라고요. 난 당신을 비참하게 만들고 싶지 않아요, 그러고 싶지 않아요! 당신을 자유롭게 해 줄게요. 당신은 날 사랑하지 않아요. 당신은 다른 사람을 사랑해요!\"\n\n브론스키는 진정하라고 그녀에게 애원했고, 그녀의 질투에는 근거가 될 만한 구석이 조금도 없다고, 자기는 결코 그녀를 사랑하기를 멈춘 적이 없으며 앞으로도 결코 멈추지 않을 거라고, 그 어느 때보다 더 그녀를 사랑한다고 단언했다.\n\n\"안나, 어쩌자고 당신 자신과 나를 이렇게 괴롭히는 거야?\" 그가 그녀의 두 손에 입 맞추며 말했다. 이제 그의 얼굴에는 다정함이 어려 있었고, 그녀는 그의 목소리에서 눈물의 기척을 느낀 듯했으며, 자기 손에 그 눈물이 젖어 드는 것을 느꼈다. 그러자 순식간에 안나의 절망적인 질투는 절망적인 다정함의 격정으로 바뀌었다. 그녀는 두 팔로 그를 끌어안고 그의 머리에, 목에, 두 손에 입맞춤을 퍼부었다.\n\n# Chapter 25\n\n화해가 완전히 이루어졌다고 느낀 안나는 아침부터 출발 준비에 열심히 매달렸다. 두 사람이 서로 양보하느라 월요일에 떠날지 화요일에 떠날지가 아직 정해지지 않았지만, 안나는 하루 일찍 가든 늦게 가든 전혀 개의치 않는다는 듯이 부지런히 짐을 꾸렸다. 그녀가 자기 방에서 뚜껑이 열린 트렁크 위로 몸을 구부린 채 그 안의 물건들을 꺼내고 있을 때, 외출 차림을 한 그가 평소보다 일찍 그녀를 보러 들어왔다.\n\n\"지금 곧 maman한테 다녀오려고. 어머니가 예고로프 편에 돈을 보내 줄 수 있을 거야. 나는 내일 떠날 채비가 될 거고.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n이렇게 기분이 좋은데도, 그가 어머니를 찾아간다는 생각이 그녀의 가슴을 쿡 찔렀다.\n\n\"아니요, 나는 그때까지 준비가 안 될 거예요.\" 그녀는 말하고는 곧바로 생각했다. '그러니까 내가 바라는 대로 일을 맞출 수도 있었던 거잖아.' \"아니, 당신이 하려던 대로 해요. 식당으로 가세요, 내가 곧 갈 테니까. 그냥 필요 없는 물건들을 추려 내는 것뿐이에요.\" 그녀는 이렇게 말하며 안누슈카의 팔에 안긴 잡동사니 더미 위에 무언가를 더 얹었다.\n\n안나가 식당으로 들어갔을 때 브론스키는 비프스테이크를 먹고 있었다.\n\n\"이 방들이 내게 얼마나 정나미가 떨어졌는지 당신은 믿지 못할 거예요.\" 그녀는 그의 옆에 앉아 커피를 들며 말했다. \"이런 chambres garnies보다 더 끔찍한 건 없어요. 여긴 개성이 없어요, 영혼도 없고. 이 시계들이며, 커튼이며, 무엇보다 이 벽지들—악몽 같아요. 나는 보즈드비젠스코예를 약속의 땅처럼 떠올린다니까요. 말들은 아직 안 보내는 거죠?\"\n* chambres garnies: 프랑스어로 '가구가 갖추어진 셋방'.\n\n\"안 보내, 우리 뒤를 따라올 거야. 당신은 어디 가는데?\"\n\n\"윌슨네에 드레스를 몇 벌 가져다주려고요. 그럼 정말로 내일인 거예요?\" 그녀는 명랑한 목소리로 말했다. 그러나 별안간 그녀의 얼굴이 변했다.\n\n브론스키의 시종이 페테르부르크에서 온 전보의 수령증에 서명해 달라고 청하러 들어왔다. 브론스키가 전보 한 통 받는 일에 별다른 데는 없었지만, 그는 무언가를 그녀에게 숨기고 싶어 안달하는 듯이, 수령증은 서재에 있다고 말하고는 황급히 그녀를 향해 돌아섰다.\n\n\"내일까지는 틀림없이, 다 끝내 놓을게.\"\n\n\"전보가 누구한테서 온 거예요?\" 그녀가 그의 말을 듣지도 않고 물었다.\n\n\"스티바한테서.\" 그가 마지못해 대답했다.\n\n\"왜 나한테 안 보여 줬어요? 스티바와 나 사이에 무슨 비밀이 있을 수 있다고?\"\n\n브론스키는 시종을 다시 불러 전보를 가져오라고 일렀다.\n\n\"보여 주고 싶지 않았어, 스티바가 전보 치는 데 어찌나 열을 올리는지 말이야. 아직 아무것도 정해지지 않았는데 전보는 왜 친담?\"\n\n\"이혼에 관한 거예요?\"\n\n\"응. 그런데 아직 아무것도 얻어 내지 못했다는군. 하루 이틀 안에 확실한 답을 주겠다고 약속했어. 자, 여기 있어, 읽어 봐.\"\n\n안나는 떨리는 손으로 전보를 받아 브론스키가 일러 준 내용을 읽었다. 끝에는 이렇게 덧붙여져 있었다. \"희망은 적음. 그러나 가능한 일이든 불가능한 일이든 다 해 보겠음.\"\n\n\"내가 어제 말했잖아요, 이혼을 언제 받든, 아니면 영영 못 받든 나한테는 전혀 아무 의미가 없다고.\" 그녀는 새빨갛게 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다. \"그걸 나한테 숨길 이유는 눈곱만큼도 없었어요.\" '그러니까 저 사람은 여자들과 주고받은 편지도 나한테 숨길 수 있고, 또 숨기고 있는 거야.' 그녀는 생각했다.\n\n\"야시빈이 오늘 아침에 보이토프와 함께 오기로 했어.\" 브론스키가 말했다. \"퍄프초프한테서 그가 갚을 수 있는 것 이상을 다 땄다더군, 한 육만 정도.\"\n\n\"아니에요.\" 그녀는 그가 이렇게 화제를 돌리는 것으로 자기가 짜증이 났음을 너무도 빤히 드러내는 데에 신경이 곤두서서 말했다. \"당신은 어째서 이 소식이 나한테 그토록 영향을 미칠 거라고, 그래서 그걸 숨기려고까지 해야 한다고 생각한 거예요? 나는 이 일을 따지고 싶지 않다고 말했고, 당신도 나만큼이나 이 일을 대수롭지 않게 여겨 줬으면 했어요.\"\n\n\"나는 명확한 게 좋으니까 이 일에 신경을 쓰는 거야.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"명확함은 형식에 있는 게 아니라 사랑에 있어요.\" 그녀는 점점 더 신경이 곤두서서 말했는데, 그것은 그의 말 때문이 아니라 그가 말하는 그 차분하고 냉정한 어조 때문이었다. \"당신은 그게 뭣 때문에 필요한 거예요?\"\n\n'맙소사! 또 사랑 타령이군.' 그는 미간을 찌푸리며 생각했다.\n\n\"아, 당신도 뭣 때문인지 알잖아. 당신을 위해서, 그리고 앞으로 우리 아이들을 위해서지.\"\n\n\"앞으로 아이들은 없을 거예요.\"\n\n\"그거 참 안타까운 일이군.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"당신은 아이들을 위해서 그걸 원하는 거지, 나는 생각하지 않는 거잖아요?\" 그녀는 그가 \"당신을 위해서, 그리고 아이들을 위해서\"라고 말한 것을 까맣게 잊었거나, 아니면 아예 듣지 못한 채 말했다.\n\n아이를 가질 수 있느냐 없느냐 하는 문제는 이미 오래전부터 그녀에게 다툼과 짜증의 거리였다. 아이를 갖고 싶어 하는 그의 바람을, 그녀는 그가 자기 미모를 소중히 여기지 않는다는 증거로 해석했다.\n\n\"아, 내가 말했잖아, 당신을 위해서라고. 무엇보다도 당신을 위해서라고.\" 그가 고통스러운 듯 미간을 찌푸리며 거듭 말했다. \"왜냐하면 당신이 짜증을 내는 것은 대부분 이 처지가 불명확한 데서 비롯된다고 나는 확신하니까.\"\n\n'그래, 이제 저 사람은 온갖 가식을 다 벗어 버렸고, 나를 향한 차가운 증오를 죄다 드러내고 있어.' 그녀는 그의 말은 듣지도 않고, 그의 눈 속에서 자기를 비웃듯 바라보는 그 차갑고 잔인한 재판관을 공포에 질려 지켜보며 생각했다.\n\n\"그 원인은 그게 아니에요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"게다가 정말이지 내가, 당신 말마따나 짜증을 내는 그 원인이 내가 완전히 당신의 손아귀에 들어 있다는 데에 있다니, 도무지 모르겠네요. 이 처지에 무슨 불명확함이 있다는 거예요? 오히려 그 반대지…….\"\n\n\"당신이 이해하려 들지 않는다니 참으로 유감이군.\" 그가 자기 생각을 끝내 입 밖으로 내고야 말겠다는 듯 고집스럽게 그녀의 말을 가로막았다. \"그 불명확함이란, 당신이 나를 자유로운 몸이라고 상상하는 데 있는 거야.\"\n\n\"그 점이라면 마음을 아주 푹 놓아도 돼요.\" 그녀는 이렇게 말하고는 그에게서 몸을 돌려 커피를 마시기 시작했다.\n\n그녀는 새끼손가락을 따로 세운 채 잔을 들어 입술로 가져갔다. 몇 모금 마시고는 그를 흘끗 보았는데, 그 표정으로 그녀는 그가 자기 손과 자기 몸짓과 자기 입술이 내는 소리에 진저리를 치고 있다는 것을 똑똑히 알아챘다.\n\n\"당신 어머니가 뭐라고 생각하든, 당신을 어떤 사람과 짝지어 주고 싶어 하든, 나는 조금도 관심 없어요.\" 그녀는 떨리는 손으로 잔을 내려놓으며 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 지금 우리가 그 얘길 하고 있는 게 아니잖아.\"\n\n\"아니에요, 우리는 바로 그 얘길 하고 있는 거예요. 그리고 분명히 말해 두겠는데, 매정한 여자는, 그게 늙었든 안 늙었든, 당신 어머니든 그 누구든, 나한테는 아무런 의미도 없고, 나는 그런 여자와 알고 지내는 데 절대 동의할 수 없어요.\"\n\n\"안나, 부탁이니 우리 어머니를 함부로 말하지 말아 줘.\"\n\n\"제 아들의 행복과 명예가 어디에 있는지 마음으로 일러 주지 못하는 여자라면, 그런 여자한테는 마음이라는 게 없는 거예요.\"\n\n\"내가 존경하는 우리 어머니를 함부로 말하지 말아 달라고, 다시 한번 부탁하지.\" 그는 목소리를 높이며 그녀를 매섭게 노려보며 말했다.\n\n그녀는 대답하지 않았다. 그를, 그의 얼굴을, 그의 손을 뚫어지게 바라보며, 그녀는 어제 화해할 때의 온갖 정황과 그의 정열적인 애무를 모조리 떠올렸다. '저런, 바로 저런 애무를 저 사람은 다른 여자들한테도 퍼부었고, 또 퍼부을 테고, 퍼붓고 싶어 안달하겠지!' 그녀는 생각했다.\n\n\"당신은 어머니를 사랑하지 않아요. 그건 다 말뿐이고, 말뿐이고, 또 말뿐이에요!\" 그녀는 증오에 찬 눈으로 그를 바라보며 말했다.\n\n\"설령 그렇다 해도, 당신은…….\"\n\n\"결정을 내려야 한다는 거겠죠, 나는 이미 결정했어요.\" 그녀는 이렇게 말하고는 나가 버리려 했지만, 바로 그 순간 야시빈이 방으로 들어왔다. 안나는 그에게 인사하고 그 자리에 남았다.\n\n영혼 속에 폭풍이 몰아치고, 자기 인생에서 어쩌면 무시무시한 결과를 빚을지도 모르는 전환점에 서 있다고 느끼는 마당에, 어째서 그 순간에 머지않아 어차피 모든 것을 다 알게 될 외부 사람 앞에서 겉치레를 유지해야만 했는지—그녀 자신도 알 수 없었다. 그러나 그녀는 곧 자기 안의 폭풍을 잠재우고 자리에 앉아 손님과 이야기를 나누기 시작했다.\n\n\"그래, 어떻게 지내세요? 빚은 받으셨나요?\" 그녀가 야시빈에게 물었다.\n\n\"아, 그럭저럭이요. 전부 다는 못 받을 것 같습니다만, 절반은 톡톡히 받을 겁니다. 그런데 두 분은 언제 떠나십니까?\" 야시빈은 브론스키를 바라보며, 둘 사이에 다툼이 있었음을 영락없이 알아채고 말했다.\n\n\"모레쯤일 것 같소.\" 브론스키가 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 떠나신다고 하신 지 한참 됐잖습니까.\"\n\n\"하지만 이제는 완전히 정해졌어요.\" 안나는 브론스키의 얼굴을 똑바로 들여다보며 말했는데, 그 눈빛은 그에게 화해 따위의 가능성은 꿈도 꾸지 말라고 일러 주고 있었다.\n\n\"그 운 없는 퍄프초프가 가엾지 않으세요?\" 그녀가 야시빈에게 말을 이었다.\n\n\"제가 그를 가엾어하는지 아닌지, 한 번도 스스로 물어본 적이 없습니다, 안나 아르카지예브나. 보십시오, 제 전 재산은 여기 들어 있고요\"—그는 가슴 안주머니를 가볍게 두드렸다—\"지금 이 순간 저는 부자입니다. 하지만 오늘 클럽에 갔다가 거지꼴이 되어 나올 수도 있지요. 보십시오, 누구든 저와 마주 앉아 노름을 하는 자는—저를 등에 걸칠 셔츠 한 장 없이 발가벗겨 놓으려 들고, 저도 그자한테 그렇게 하려 들죠. 그렇게 우리는 한판 붙는 거고, 거기에 묘미가 있는 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그래도, 만약 당신이 결혼한 몸이라고 쳐 봐요.\" 안나가 말했다. \"당신 부인은 어떻게 되겠어요?\"\n\n야시빈이 껄껄 웃었다.\n\n\"그래서 저는 결혼을 안 했고, 앞으로도 할 생각이 없는 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 헬싱포르스는요?\" 브론스키가 대화에 끼어들며 미소 짓는 안나의 얼굴을 흘끗 보고 말했다. 그의 눈과 마주치자, 안나의 얼굴은 즉각 차갑고 매서운 표정으로 굳어졌는데, 마치 그에게 이렇게 말하는 듯했다. '잊지 않았어. 다 마찬가지야.'\n\n\"정말로 사랑에 빠지셨던 거예요?\" 그녀가 야시빈에게 말했다.\n\n\"오, 맙소사! 그것도 수도 없이요! 하지만 보십시오, 어떤 남자들은 노름을 하긴 하되 rendezvous 시간이 오면 언제든 카드를 내려놓을 수 있게끔만 하지요. 반면에 저는 사랑을 하긴 하되, 저녁 카드 시간에 늦지 않도록만 합니다. 저는 그런 식으로 일을 처리하지요.\"\n* rendezvous: 프랑스어로 '밀회, 만남의 약속'.\n\n\"아니요, 그런 게 아니라, 진짜 사랑 말이에요.\" 그녀는 '헬싱포르스'라고 말하려다가, 브론스키가 쓴 그 단어를 되풀이하고 싶지 않았다.\n\n말을 사기로 한 보이토프가 들어왔다. 안나는 자리에서 일어나 방을 나갔다.\n\n집을 나서기 전, 브론스키는 그녀의 방으로 들어갔다. 그녀는 탁자 위에서 무언가를 찾는 척하려다가, 그렇게 가장하는 것이 부끄러워 차가운 눈으로 그의 얼굴을 똑바로 쳐다보았다.\n\n\"무슨 일이에요?\" 그녀가 프랑스어로 물었다.\n\n\"감베타의 보증서를 가지러, 그놈을 팔았거든.\" 그는 말로 하는 것보다 더 또렷하게 이렇게 말하는 어조로 대답했다. \"이런 일들을 두고 얘기할 시간이 없고, 얘기해 봤자 아무 소용도 없어.\"\n\n'나는 어떤 식으로도 잘못한 게 없어.' 그는 생각했다. '저 여자가 스스로를 벌하겠다면, tant pis pour elle.' 그러나 막 나가려는데 그녀가 무언가를 말한 것 같았고, 별안간 그의 가슴이 그녀에 대한 연민으로 욱신거렸다.\n* tant pis pour elle: 프랑스어로 '그녀에게 안된 일이지만 할 수 없지'.\n\n\"응, 안나?\" 그가 물었다.\n\n\"아무 말도 안 했어요.\" 그녀는 여전히 차갑고 침착하게 대답했다.\n\n'오, 아무것도 아니라면, tant pis로군.' 그는 다시 마음이 싸늘해지는 것을 느끼며 생각하고는, 돌아서서 나갔다. 나가는 길에 그는 거울 속에서 그녀의 얼굴을, 입술을 파르르 떠는 새하얀 얼굴을 언뜻 보았다. 그는 걸음을 멈추고 그녀에게 무언가 위로의 말을 건네고 싶기까지 했지만, 무슨 말을 해야 할지 미처 생각해 내기도 전에 두 다리가 그를 방 밖으로 데리고 나갔다. 그날 하루 종일 그는 집을 떠나 있었고, 밤늦게 돌아왔을 때 하녀는 안나 아르카지예브나가 두통이 있어서 자기 방에 들어오지 말아 달라고 청했다고 그에게 전했다.\n* tant pis: 프랑스어로 '할 수 없지'.\n\n# Chapter 26\n\n이제껏 단 하루도 다툼으로 보낸 적은 없었다. 오늘이 처음이었다. 그리고 이것은 다툼도 아니었다. 이것은 완전한 냉담을 공공연히 인정하는 것이었다. 보증서 때문에 방에 들어왔을 때 그가 자기를 쳐다보았던 것처럼 그렇게 쳐다볼 수가 있단 말인가?—자기를 바라보고, 자기 마음이 절망으로 찢어지고 있는 것을 보면서도, 그 무감각하게 침착한 얼굴로 한마디 말도 없이 나가 버리다니? 그는 단지 자기에게 냉담한 게 아니라, 자기를 미워하고 있었다, 다른 여자를 사랑하기 때문에—그것은 분명했다.\n\n그리고 그가 했던 잔인한 말들을 모조리 떠올리면서, 안나는 그가 분명히 하고 싶어 했고 자기에게 할 수도 있었을 말들까지 덧붙였고, 점점 더 격분해 갔다.\n\n\"당신을 막지 않겠소.\" 그는 이렇게 말할 수도 있었다. \"당신이 원하는 곳 어디든 갈 수 있소. 당신은 남편과 이혼하기를 원치 않았지, 틀림없이 그에게 돌아가려는 거겠지. 그에게 돌아가시오. 돈이 필요하면 내가 주겠소. 몇 루블이나 원하오?\"\n\n잔혹한 남자라면 할 법한 가장 잔인한 말들을 그는 그녀의 상상 속에서 그녀에게 했고, 그녀는 그가 실제로 그 말들을 한 것처럼 그를 용서할 수 없었다.\n\n\"하지만 그는 바로 어제 나를 사랑한다고 맹세하지 않았나, 진실하고 성실한 사람인 그가? 나는 벌써 여러 번 공연히 절망하지 않았던가?\" 그녀는 그 후에 혼잣말을 했다.\n\n그날 하루 종일, 두 시간을 차지한 윌슨의 가게 방문을 빼고는, 안나는 모든 게 끝났는지 아니면 아직 화해할 희망이 있는지, 당장 떠나야 할지 아니면 그를 한 번 더 봐야 할지 망설이며 보냈다. 그녀는 하루 종일 그를 기다렸고, 저녁이 되어 자기 방으로 들어가면서 머리가 아프다는 전갈을 그에게 남긴 채, 혼잣말을 했다. \"하녀가 뭐라고 하든 그가 들어온다면, 그건 그가 아직 나를 사랑한다는 뜻이야. 그렇지 않으면, 그건 모든 게 끝났다는 뜻이고, 그러면 내가 어떻게 할지 결정하겠어!……\"\n\n저녁에 그녀는 그의 마차가 덜커덩거리며 현관 앞에 멈추는 소리, 그가 초인종을 울리는 소리, 그의 발소리, 그리고 그가 하인과 나누는 대화를 들었다. 그는 자기에게 전해진 말을 믿었고, 더 알아보려 하지도 않았으며, 자기 방으로 들어가 버렸다. 그러니 이제 모든 게 끝난 것이었다.\n\n그러자 죽음이, 그의 마음속에 자기에 대한 사랑을 되살리는 유일한 수단으로서, 그를 벌하고 그녀의 마음을 사로잡은 악령이 그를 상대로 벌이고 있는 그 싸움에서 승리를 거두는 유일한 수단으로서, 그녀의 머릿속에 또렷하고 생생하게 떠올랐다.\n\n이제 아무것도 중요하지 않았다. 보즈드비젠스코예로 가든 안 가든, 남편과 이혼을 하든 못 하든—그 모든 것은 중요하지 않았다. 중요한 단 한 가지는 그를 벌하는 것이었다. 그녀가 평소대로 아편을 한 모금 따르면서, 죽으려면 병째로 다 마시기만 하면 된다고 생각하자, 그것이 너무나 간단하고 쉬워 보여서, 그녀는 그가 어떻게 괴로워할지, 그리고 너무 늦어 버렸을 때 어떻게 후회하고 자기를 그리워할지를 즐거이 상상하기 시작했다. 그녀는 다 타들어 가는 촛불 하나의 빛에 의지해 눈을 뜬 채 침대에 누워, 천장의 조각된 처마 장식과 그 일부를 가린 칸막이의 그림자를 바라보면서, 자기가 더 이상 없을 때, 자기가 그에게 한낱 기억일 뿐일 때 그가 어떤 심정일지를 생생하게 그려 보았다. \"어떻게 내가 그녀에게 그토록 잔인한 말을 할 수 있었을까?\" 그는 이렇게 말할 것이다. \"어떻게 내가 그녀에게 아무 말도 없이 방을 나갈 수 있었을까? 그러나 이제 그녀는 없다. 그녀는 우리 곁을 영원히 떠나 버렸다. 그녀는……\" 갑자기 칸막이의 그림자가 흔들리더니, 처마 장식 전체로, 천장 전체로 덮쳐 왔고, 다른 쪽에서 다른 그림자들이 그것을 맞이하러 달려들었으며, 한순간 그림자들은 뒤로 물러났다가, 다시 새로운 빠르기로 앞으로 돌진해 흔들리고 뒤엉키더니, 모든 것이 어둠이 되었다. \"죽음!\" 그녀는 생각했다. 그러자 너무나 큰 공포가 밀려와서, 그녀는 한참 동안 자기가 어디에 있는지 알 수 없었고, 한참 동안 떨리는 손으로 성냥을 찾지 못해, 다 타서 꺼져 버린 초 대신 다른 초에 불을 붙이지 못했다. \"아니, 무엇이든—그저 살고만 싶어! 아니, 나는 그를 사랑해! 아니, 그는 나를 사랑해! 이런 일은 전에도 있었고 지나갈 거야.\" 그녀는 삶으로 돌아온 기쁨의 눈물이 뺨을 타고 흘러내리는 것을 느끼며 말했다. 그리고 공황에서 벗어나려고 그녀는 황급히 그의 방으로 갔다.\n\n그는 거기서 잠들어 있었고, 깊이 자고 있었다. 그녀는 그에게 다가가, 촛불을 그의 얼굴 위로 들어 올린 채 한참 동안 그를 바라보았다. 잠들어 있는 지금, 그녀는 그를 너무나 사랑해서 그를 보고 있노라니 다정한 눈물을 참을 수 없었다. 그러나 그녀는 알고 있었다, 그가 깨어나면 자기가 옳다고 확신한 채 차가운 눈으로 자기를 볼 것이며, 자기 사랑을 그에게 말하기 전에 먼저 그가 자기를 대한 방식이 잘못되었음을 그에게 증명해야 한다는 것을. 그녀는 그를 깨우지 않고 돌아왔고, 아편을 두 번째로 마신 뒤 새벽녘에야 무겁고 어설픈 잠에 빠져들었는데, 그동안 의식을 완전히 잃은 적은 한 번도 없었다.\n\n아침에 그녀는 끔찍한 악몽에 깨어났는데, 그것은 브론스키와 관계를 맺기 전에도 여러 번 꿈에서 되풀이되던 것이었다. 헝클어진 수염을 한 자그마한 노인이 무슨 쇳덩이 위로 몸을 구부린 채 무언가를 하면서 뜻 모를 프랑스 말을 중얼거리고 있었고, 그녀는, 이 악몽에서 늘 그랬듯이(바로 그것이 악몽의 공포를 만들어 내는 것이었다), 이 농부가 자기에게는 아무 관심도 두지 않으면서 그 쇳덩이로 무언가 끔찍한 짓을 하고 있다고—자기 위에서 하고 있다고 느꼈다. 그리고 그녀는 식은땀을 흘리며 깨어났다.\n\n그녀가 일어났을 때, 어제 일이 마치 안개에 가려진 듯 떠올랐다.\n\n\"다툼이 있었지. 여러 번 있었던 바로 그런 일이야. 나는 머리가 아프다고 했고, 그는 나를 보러 들어오지 않았어. 내일 우리는 떠나니까, 나는 그를 만나서 여행 준비를 해야 해.\" 그녀는 혼잣말을 했다. 그리고 그가 서재에 있다는 것을 알고는, 그에게로 내려갔다. 응접실을 지나가는데 마차 한 대가 현관 앞에 멈추는 소리가 들렸고, 창밖을 내다보니 라일락빛 모자를 쓴 젊은 처녀가 마차에서 몸을 내밀고 초인종을 울리는 하인에게 무언가 지시를 하고 있었다. 현관에서 몇 마디 이야기가 오간 뒤, 누군가 위층으로 올라왔고, 응접실을 지나는 브론스키의 발소리가 들렸다. 그는 빠르게 아래층으로 내려갔다. 안나는 다시 창가로 갔다. 그녀는 그가 모자도 쓰지 않은 채 계단으로 나와 마차로 다가가는 것을 보았다. 라일락빛 모자를 쓴 젊은 처녀가 그에게 꾸러미 하나를 건넸다. 브론스키는 미소를 지으며 그녀에게 무언가 말했다. 마차가 떠나갔고, 그는 다시 빠르게 위층으로 뛰어 올라왔다.\n\n그녀의 마음속 모든 것을 뒤덮고 있던 안개가 갑자기 걷혔다. 어제의 감정들이 새로운 격통으로 병든 가슴을 찔렀다. 그녀는 어떻게 자기가 그의 집에서 그와 함께 하루를 통째로 보내며 그렇게까지 스스로를 낮출 수 있었는지 이제 이해할 수 없었다. 그녀는 자기 결심을 알리려고 그의 방으로 들어갔다.\n\n\"소로키나 부인하고 그 딸이었소. 와서 maman한테서 온 돈과 증서를 가져왔지. 어제는 받을 수가 없었거든. 머리는 어떻소, 좀 나아졌소?\" 그는 그녀의 얼굴에 어린 침울하고 엄숙한 표정을 보지도 이해하지도 않으려 하면서 조용히 말했다.\n* maman: 프랑스어로 '어머니'.\n\n그녀는 방 한가운데 선 채, 말없이 그를 뚫어지게 바라보았다. 그는 그녀를 흘끗 보고는 한순간 미간을 찌푸렸다가, 다시 편지를 읽어 나갔다. 그녀는 몸을 돌려 천천히 방을 나갔다. 그는 아직 그녀를 불러 세울 수도 있었지만, 그녀가 문에 이르렀는데도 그는 여전히 말이 없었고, 들리는 소리라고는 그가 편지지를 넘기며 내는 바스락거리는 소리뿐이었다.\n\n\"아, 그런데,\" 그녀가 막 문간에 섰을 때 그가 말했다. \"우리 내일 틀림없이 떠나는 거지, 그렇지?\"\n\n\"당신은 가지만, 나는 안 가요.\" 그녀가 그에게로 돌아서며 말했다.\n\n\"안나, 우리 이렇게 계속할 수는 없잖소……\"\n\n\"당신은 가지만, 나는 안 가요.\" 그녀가 되풀이했다.\n\n\"이건 정말 견딜 수가 없군!\"\n\n\"당신은…… 당신은 이 일을 후회하게 될 거예요.\" 그녀는 이렇게 말하고 나가 버렸다.\n\n이 말이 내뱉어진 그 절망적인 표정에 겁이 난 그는 벌떡 일어나 그녀를 뒤쫓아 가려 했지만, 다시 생각해 보고는 도로 앉아 이를 악물고 얼굴을 찌푸렸다. 무언가 막연한 것에 대한, 그가 보기에는 천박한 이 위협이 그를 격분시켰다. \"나는 모든 걸 다 해 봤어.\" 그는 생각했다. \"남은 건 신경 쓰지 않는 것뿐이야.\" 그러고는 시내로, 다시 어머니에게로 가서 증서에 서명을 받을 채비를 하기 시작했다.\n\n그녀는 서재와 식당 쪽에서 그의 발소리를 들었다. 응접실에서 그는 멈춰 섰다. 그러나 그는 그녀를 보러 들르지 않고, 다만 자기가 없는 동안 보이토프가 오면 말을 내주라는 지시만 했다. 그러고 나서 그녀는 마차가 마련되는 소리를, 문이 열리는 소리를 들었고, 그가 다시 밖으로 나갔다. 그러나 그는 다시 현관으로 들어갔고, 누군가 위층으로 뛰어 올라가고 있었다. 잊고 간 그의 장갑을 가지러 뛰어 올라간 하인이었다. 그녀가 창가로 가 보니, 그는 쳐다보지도 않고 장갑을 받아 들고서, 마부의 등을 툭 치며 그에게 무언가 말하고 있었다. 그러고는 창을 올려다보지도 않은 채 다리를 꼰 평소의 자세로 마차에 자리를 잡고 앉아, 장갑을 끼면서 모퉁이를 돌아 사라져 버렸다.\n\n# Chapter 27\n\n\"그가 가 버렸어! 끝난 거야!\" 안나는 창가에 선 채 혼잣말을 했다. 그러자 이 말에 화답이라도 하듯, 촛불이 꺼졌을 때의 어둠과 그 무서운 꿈에 대한 인상이 하나로 뒤섞여 그녀의 가슴을 차디찬 공포로 가득 채웠다.\n\n\"아니야, 그럴 리 없어!\" 그녀는 소리치며 방을 가로질러 가 종을 울렸다. 이제 그녀는 혼자 있는 것이 너무도 두려워, 하인이 들어오기를 기다리지도 않고 그를 맞으러 나갔다.\n\n\"백작이 어디로 가셨는지 알아보게.\" 그녀가 말했다. 하인은 백작이 마구간으로 갔다고 대답했다.\n\n\"백작 나리께서 마님이 외출하고 싶어 하시면 마차를 곧 돌려보내겠다는 말씀을 남기셨습니다.\"\n\n\"좋아. 잠깐만 기다려. 지금 당장 쪽지를 쓸 테니까. 미하일에게 그 쪽지를 들려서 마구간으로 보내게. 어서 서둘러.\"\n\n그녀는 자리에 앉아 글을 썼다.\n\n\"제가 잘못했어요. 집으로 돌아와요. 제가 설명할게요. 제발 와요! 무서워요.\"\n\n그녀는 쪽지를 봉해 하인에게 건넸다.\n\n이제 그녀는 혼자 남는 것이 두려워, 하인을 따라 방을 나와 아이 방으로 갔다.\n\n\"아니, 이게 아니야, 이 아이가 아니야! 그 애의 파란 눈은, 그 다정하고 수줍은 미소는 어디 있지?\" 통통하고 발그레한 어린 딸아이가 검은 곱슬머리를 하고 있는 것을 보았을 때, 그녀의 머릿속에 가장 먼저 떠오른 생각이었다. 뒤죽박죽 엉킨 생각 속에서 그녀는 아이 방에서 세료자를 보리라고 기대했던 것이다. 식탁에 앉은 어린 딸아이는 고집스럽고 격렬하게 코르크 마개로 식탁을 두드려 대며, 칠흑같이 검은 눈으로 멍하니 어머니를 쳐다보고 있었다. 영국인 보모에게 자기는 아주 잘 지내며 내일 시골로 떠날 거라고 대답한 뒤, 안나는 어린 딸아이 곁에 앉아 코르크 마개를 돌려 아이에게 보여 주기 시작했다. 그러나 아이의 크고 낭랑한 웃음소리와 눈썹을 움직이는 모습이 브론스키를 너무도 생생하게 떠오르게 해서, 그녀는 흐느낌을 억누르며 황급히 일어나 그 자리를 떠났다. \"정말 모든 게 끝난 걸까? 아니야, 그럴 리 없어!\" 그녀는 생각했다. \"그는 돌아올 거야. 하지만 그가 그 여자와 이야기를 나눈 뒤에 지은 그 미소, 그 흥분을 어떻게 설명할 수 있을까? 하지만 그가 설명하지 못한다 해도, 나는 믿겠어. 내가 믿지 않는다면 내게 남은 건 단 한 가지뿐인데, 나는 그렇게 할 수 없어.\"\n\n그녀는 시계를 들여다보았다. 이십 분이 지나 있었다. \"지금쯤 그는 쪽지를 받고 돌아오는 길일 거야. 오래 걸리지 않아, 십 분만 더 있으면 돼…… 하지만 그가 오지 않는다면? 아니야, 그럴 리 없어. 그가 눈물범벅이 된 내 얼굴을 봐서는 안 돼. 가서 씻어야지. 그래, 그래. 내가 머리를 매만졌던가, 안 매만졌던가?\" 그녀는 자문했다. 도무지 기억이 나지 않았다. 그녀는 손으로 머리를 만져 보았다. \"그래, 머리는 매만졌어, 그런데 언제 했는지는 도무지 떠오르지 않아.\" 그녀는 자기 손이 알려 주는 증거를 믿을 수 없어, 정말로 머리를 매만졌는지 확인하려고 체경 앞으로 다가갔다. 분명 머리는 매만져져 있었지만, 언제 했는지는 도통 생각이 나지 않았다. \"저게 누구지?\" 그녀는 거울에 비친, 이상하게 번득이는 눈으로 겁에 질린 듯 자기를 바라보는 부어오른 얼굴을 들여다보며 생각했다. \"아니, 나잖아!\" 그녀는 문득 깨달았고, 주위를 둘러보다가 별안간 그의 입맞춤이 온몸에 느껴지는 듯해, 몸서리를 치며 어깨를 떨었다. 그러다 손을 들어 입술에 대고 입을 맞추었다.\n\n\"이게 뭐람? 이런, 내가 미쳐 가고 있어!\" 그녀는 안누슈카가 방을 정돈하고 있는 침실로 들어갔다.\n\n\"안누슈카.\" 그녀는 그녀 앞에 우뚝 멈춰 서서 말했다. 그러고는 무슨 말을 해야 할지 몰라 하녀를 빤히 바라보았다.\n\n\"다리아 알렉산드로브나를 뵈러 가실 생각이셨잖아요.\" 하녀가 마치 그 마음을 알아채기라도 한 듯 말했다.\n\n\"다리아 알렉산드로브나? 그래, 갈 거야.\"\n\n\"가는 데 십오 분, 오는 데 십오 분. 그는 오고 있어, 곧 여기 올 거야.\" 그녀는 시계를 꺼내 들여다보았다. \"그런데 어떻게 나를 이런 상태로 내버려 둔 채 가 버릴 수가 있지? 나와 화해하지도 않고 어떻게 살아갈 수 있단 말이야?\" 그녀는 창가로 가서 거리를 내다보기 시작했다. 시간으로 보아 이제 그가 돌아왔을 법도 했다. 그러나 자기 계산이 틀렸을지도 모른다는 생각에, 그녀는 다시금 그가 언제 출발했는지를 떠올리며 분 단위로 시간을 헤아리기 시작했다.\n\n그녀가 회중시계와 맞춰 보려고 큰 시계 쪽으로 자리를 옮긴 바로 그 순간, 누군가 마차를 타고 다가왔다. 창밖을 흘끗 내다본 그녀는 그의 마차를 보았다. 그러나 아무도 위층으로 올라오지 않았고, 아래쪽에서 사람들 목소리가 들렸다. 마차를 타고 돌아온 것은 심부름꾼이었다. 그녀는 그에게로 내려갔다.\n\n\"백작님을 만나지 못했습니다. 백작님께서는 아래쪽 시내 길로 떠나셨더군요.\"\n\n\"뭐라고? 뭐라고!……\" 그녀는 발그레하고 사람 좋아 보이는 미하일이 쪽지를 돌려주자 그에게 말했다.\n\n\"그렇다면 그는 이걸 받아 보지도 못한 거잖아!\" 그녀는 생각했다.\n\n\"이 쪽지를 가지고 브론스카야 백작 부인 댁으로 가게, 알겠지? 그리고 곧장 답장을 받아 오게나.\" 그녀가 심부름꾼에게 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 나는, 나는 어떻게 하지?\" 그녀는 생각했다. \"그래, 돌리네 집으로 가는 거야, 정말이지 그러지 않으면 미쳐 버릴 거야. 그래, 전보를 칠 수도 있고.\" 그러고 그녀는 전보를 썼다. \"당신과 꼭 이야기해야 해요. 당장 와요.\" 전보를 부친 뒤 그녀는 옷을 갈아입으러 갔다. 옷을 다 차려입고 모자까지 쓴 다음, 그녀는 통통하고 편안해 보이는 안누슈카의 눈을 다시 한번 들여다보았다. 그 선량한 작은 잿빛 눈에는 틀림없는 연민이 어려 있었다.\n\n\"안누슈카, 나는 어쩌면 좋지?\" 안나는 흐느끼며 맥없이 의자에 주저앉았다.\n\n\"왜 그렇게 마음을 졸이세요, 안나 아르카지예브나? 무슨 별난 일이 일어난 것도 아니잖아요. 바람이라도 좀 쐬고 오시면 기분이 나아지실 거예요.\" 하녀가 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 가야지.\" 안나는 정신을 차리고 일어서며 말했다. \"그리고 내가 없는 동안 전보가 오거든, 다리아 알렉산드로브나 댁으로 보내 줘…… 아니, 내가 직접 돌아올 테니까.\"\n\n\"그래, 생각을 하면 안 돼, 무언가를 해야 해, 어디로든 마차를 타고 나가야 해, 그리고 무엇보다도 이 집에서 벗어나야 해.\" 그녀는 자기 가슴속에서 일어나는 기이한 동요를 공포에 질려 느끼며 말했고, 서둘러 밖으로 나가 마차에 올라탔다.\n\n\"어디로 모실까요?\" 마부석에 오르기 전 표트르가 물었다.\n\n\"즈나멘카의 오블론스키 댁으로.\"\n\n# Chapter 28\n\n햇살이 밝게 빛나는 화창한 날이었다. 아침 내내 가랑비가 내렸는데, 막 갠 지 얼마 되지 않았다. 철제 지붕이며, 길에 깔린 포석이며, 보도의 부싯돌이며, 마차의 바퀴와 가죽이며, 놋쇠와 양철이며—이 모두가 5월의 햇살 속에서 환하게 반짝였다. 오후 세 시, 거리가 가장 활기를 띠는 시각이었다.\n\n탄력 있는 용수철 위에서 거의 흔들리지도 않는 안락한 마차 한구석에 앉아, 잿빛 말들이 경쾌하게 달리는 가운데 끊임없이 덜컹거리는 바퀴 소리와 맑은 공기 속에서 시시각각 바뀌는 인상에 둘러싸인 채, 안나는 지난 며칠의 일들을 되짚어 보았고, 집에서 보였던 것과는 사뭇 다르게 자기 처지를 바라보았다. 이제 죽음에 대한 생각은 더 이상 그토록 끔찍하고 또렷하게 다가오지 않았고, 죽음 자체도 더 이상 그토록 피할 수 없는 것으로 여겨지지 않았다. 이제 그녀는 자기 자신을 그토록 비참하게 낮춘 굴욕에 대해 스스로를 책망했다. '내가 그이에게 용서해 달라고 애원했어. 내가 그이에게 굴복했어. 내가 잘못했다고 인정했어. 무엇 때문에? 내가 그이 없이는 살 수 없단 말인가?' 그러면서 그이 없이 어떻게 살아갈 것인가 하는 물음에는 답을 내리지 못한 채, 그녀는 상점들의 간판을 읽기 시작했다. '사무소 겸 창고. 치과 의사. 그래, 돌리한테 다 털어놓을 거야. 그 애는 브론스키를 싫어하지. 괴롭고 부끄럽겠지만, 그래도 말할 거야. 그 애는 날 사랑하니까, 그 애 충고를 따를 거야. 그이한테 굴복하지 않을 거야. 자기 멋대로 날 길들이게 두지 않겠어. 필리포프, 빵집. 사람들 말로는 저 집 반죽을 페테르부르크로 보낸다지. 모스크바 물이 빵에 그렇게 좋다는군. 아, 미티시첸의 샘물, 그리고 그 팬케이크!'\n\n그러다 그녀는 아주 오래전, 자기가 열일곱 살 처녀였을 때 고모와 함께 트로이차에 갔던 일을 떠올렸다. '그땐 말도 탔지. 손이 빨갰던 게 정말 나였나? 그때는 그렇게 근사하고 손 닿지 않을 것처럼 보였던 게 이제는 하찮아져 버렸는데, 그때 내가 가졌던 것들은 영영 내 손이 닿지 않는 곳으로 가 버렸어! 그때 내가 이런 굴욕을 겪게 되리라고 상상이나 할 수 있었을까? 내 쪽지를 받으면 그이가 얼마나 우쭐대고 흡족해할까! 하지만 본때를 보여 주겠어…… 저 페인트 냄새는 또 왜 저렇게 지독한지! 어째서 사람들은 늘 칠을 하고 짓고 하는 걸까?' Modes et robes, 그녀가 읽었다. 한 남자가 그녀에게 인사를 했다. 안누슈카의 남편이었다. '우리 식객들', 그녀는 브론스키가 그렇게 말했던 것을 떠올렸다. '우리? 어째서 우리지? 정말 끔찍한 건 과거를 뿌리째 뽑아 버릴 수 없다는 거야. 뽑아낼 수는 없어도, 그것에 대한 기억은 감출 수 있지. 그래, 감춰 버릴 거야.' 그러고는 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치와의 과거를, 자기가 어떻게 그 기억을 인생에서 지워 버렸는지를 떠올렸다. '돌리는 내가 두 번째 남편을 떠나는 거라고 생각하겠지, 그러니 분명 내가 잘못한 거라고 여길 거야. 마치 내가 옳고 싶어 안달이라도 난 것처럼! 어쩔 수 없는걸!' 그녀는 그렇게 중얼거렸고, 울고 싶어졌다. 하지만 곧 저 두 처녀가 무엇 때문에 저렇게 웃고 있을까 궁금해졌다. '아마 사랑이겠지. 저 애들은 그게 얼마나 따분하고, 얼마나 천한 것인지 몰라…… 가로수 길과 아이들. 사내아이 셋이 말놀이를 하며 뛰어다니네. 세료자! 나는 모든 것을 잃어 가는데, 그 아이는 되찾지 못하는구나. 그래, 그이가 돌아오지 않으면 나는 모든 걸 잃는 거야. 어쩌면 기차를 놓치는 바람에 지금쯤 돌아와 있을지도 몰라. 또 굴욕을 갈망하고 있구나!' 그녀는 혼잣말을 했다. '아니, 돌리한테 가서 바로 말해야지. 나는 불행해, 이런 꼴을 당해도 싸, 내 잘못이야, 그래도 나는 불행하니까, 도와줘. 이 말들, 이 마차—이 마차 안에 앉아 있는 내가 나 자신에게 얼마나 역겨운지—전부 그이 거야. 하지만 다시는 이것들을 보지 않을 거야.'\n\n돌리에게 어떻게 말할 것인지 그 말들을 곱씹으며, 마음속으로 자기 가슴을 크나큰 비통함으로 몰아세우면서, 안나는 계단을 올라갔다.\n\n\"부인께 손님이 와 계신가요?\" 그녀가 현관에서 물었다.\n\n\"카체리나 알렉산드로브나 레비나 마님이 와 계십니다.\" 하인이 대답했다.\n\n'키티! 브론스키가 사랑했던 그 키티!' 안나는 생각했다. '그이가 사랑을 품고 떠올리는 처녀. 그이는 그 애와 결혼하지 않은 걸 후회하지. 그런데 나는 증오를 품고 떠올리고, 나와 엮인 것을 후회하고 있어.'\n\n안나가 찾아왔을 때 자매는 육아에 관해 상의하고 있었다. 돌리는 둘의 대화를 끊은 손님을 맞으려고 혼자 아래층으로 내려왔다.\n\n\"어머, 그래 아직 안 떠났구나? 내가 너를 찾아가려고 했는데.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"오늘 스티바한테서 편지가 왔어.\"\n\n\"우리도 전보를 받았어요.\" 안나는 키티를 찾아 주위를 둘러보며 대답했다.\n\n\"알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 정확히 뭘 원하는지 도무지 모르겠다고 쓰긴 했는데, 확실한 답을 듣기 전엔 떠나지 않겠대.\"\n\n\"누군가 와 있는 것 같던데요. 편지를 봐도 될까요?\"\n\n\"그래, 키티야.\" 돌리가 당황하며 말했다. \"그 애는 아이 방에 있어. 한동안 많이 아팠거든.\"\n\n\"그렇다고 들었어요. 편지를 봐도 될까요?\"\n\n\"금방 가져올게. 그래도 그이가 거절하는 건 아니야. 오히려 스티바는 희망을 품고 있어.\" 돌리가 문간에 멈춰 서서 말했다.\n\n\"저는 희망이 없어요. 사실 바라지도 않고요.\" 안나가 말했다.\n\n'이게 뭐람? 키티가 나를 만나는 걸 굴욕으로 여기는 걸까?' 혼자 남게 되자 안나는 생각했다. '어쩌면 그 애 말도 맞아. 하지만 그 애가, 브론스키를 사랑했던 그 처녀가 나한테 그런 티를 낼 자격은 없지, 설령 그게 사실이라 해도. 내 처지에서는 점잖은 여자라면 누구도 나를 받아들이지 않으리란 걸 나도 알아. 그이에게 모든 걸 바친 그 첫 순간부터 알고 있었어. 그런데 이게 나한테 돌아온 보답이라니! 아, 내가 그이를 얼마나 미워하는지! 게다가 내가 여긴 뭐하러 온 걸까? 여기 오니 더 비참하고, 더 불행해.' 그녀는 옆방에서 상의하는 자매의 목소리를 들었다. '그래서 이제 내가 돌리한테 무슨 말을 한단 말이야? 내 비참한 꼴을 보여 줘서 키티를 즐겁게 해 주고, 그 애의 동정 어린 시혜를 받으라고? 안 돼. 게다가 돌리는 이해하지도 못할 거야. 말해 봐야 아무 소용없어. 그저 키티를 보는 건 흥미롭겠지, 내가 모든 사람과 모든 것을 얼마나 경멸하는지, 이제 나한테는 아무것도 중요하지 않다는 걸 그 애한테 보여 주는 건.'\n\n돌리가 편지를 들고 들어왔다. 안나는 그것을 읽고는 말없이 돌려주었다.\n\n\"다 알고 있던 내용이에요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"조금도 관심 없어요.\"\n\n\"어머, 왜 그래? 오히려 난 희망이 있는데.\" 돌리가 안나를 캐묻듯 바라보며 말했다. 그녀는 안나가 이토록 묘하게 신경이 곤두선 모습을 본 적이 없었다. \"언제 떠나니?\" 그녀가 물었다.\n\n안나는 눈을 반쯤 감은 채 앞만 똑바로 바라볼 뿐 대답하지 않았다.\n\n\"키티는 왜 나를 피하는 거예요?\" 그녀는 문 쪽을 바라보며 얼굴을 붉히고 말했다.\n\n\"어머, 무슨 그런 소릴! 그 애는 젖을 먹이고 있는데 일이 잘 안 풀려서, 내가 조언을 해 주던 참이야…… 그 애도 무척 반가워해. 곧 내려올 거야.\" 거짓말에 서툰 돌리가 어색하게 말했다. \"그래, 저기 오는구나.\"\n\n안나가 찾아왔다는 말을 듣고 키티는 모습을 보이고 싶지 않았지만, 돌리가 설득했다. 키티는 마음을 다잡고 들어와, 얼굴을 붉히며 안나에게 다가가 악수를 했다.\n\n\"이렇게 뵙게 되어 정말 기뻐요.\" 그녀가 떨리는 목소리로 말했다.\n\n키티는 이 나쁜 여자에 대한 반감과 그녀에게 다정하게 대하고 싶은 마음 사이의 내적 갈등으로 혼란에 빠져 있었다. 하지만 안나의 사랑스럽고 매혹적인 얼굴을 보는 순간, 반감의 감정이 모두 사라져 버렸다.\n\n\"나를 만나고 싶지 않았다 해도 놀라지 않았을 거예요. 나는 어떤 일에도 익숙하니까. 그동안 아팠다고요? 그래요, 좀 변했네요.\" 안나가 말했다.\n\n키티는 안나가 적의 어린 눈으로 자기를 바라보고 있다고 느꼈다. 그녀는 그 적의를, 한때 자기를 시혜하듯 대하던 안나가 이제 자기 앞에서 느낄 수밖에 없는 어색한 처지 탓으로 여겼고, 그녀가 가엾게 느껴졌다.\n\n그들은 키티의 병에 대해, 아기에 대해, 스티바에 대해 이야기했지만, 안나는 어느 것에도 관심이 없다는 게 분명했다.\n\n\"작별 인사를 하러 왔어요.\" 그녀가 자리에서 일어서며 말했다.\n\n\"어머, 언제 떠나는데?\"\n\n하지만 이번에도 대답하지 않고, 안나는 키티에게로 몸을 돌렸다.\n\n\"그래요, 당신을 만나게 되어 정말 기뻐요.\" 그녀가 미소를 지으며 말했다. \"여러 사람한테, 당신 남편한테까지 당신 이야기를 참 많이 들었어요. 그분이 나를 찾아왔었는데, 나는 그분이 무척 마음에 들었어요.\" 그녀는 분명 악의를 품고 말했다. \"그분은 어디 계신가요?\"\n\n\"시골로 돌아갔어요.\" 키티가 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다.\n\n\"그분께 안부 전해 줘요, 꼭이요.\"\n\n\"꼭 전할게요!\" 키티는 동정 어린 눈으로 안나의 눈을 들여다보며 순진하게 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 잘 있어, 돌리.\" 그리고 돌리에게 입을 맞추고 키티와 악수를 한 뒤, 안나는 서둘러 나가 버렸다.\n\n\"여전하시네, 여전히 매력적이셔! 정말 아름다운 분이야!\" 언니와 단둘이 남게 되자 키티가 말했다. \"그런데 어딘가 가여운 데가 있어. 너무나 가여워!\"\n\n\"그래, 오늘 저 애한테 뭔가 심상치 않은 데가 있어.\" 돌리가 말했다. \"내가 같이 현관까지 나갔을 때, 거의 울고 있는 것 같았거든.\"\n\n# Chapter 29\n\n안나는 집에서 나설 때보다 한층 더 나쁜 심정으로 다시 마차에 올랐다. 이전의 고통에 더해, 키티를 만났을 때 그토록 또렷하게 느꼈던, 모욕당하고 버림받은 듯한 그 감각까지 더해진 것이다.\n\n\"어디로 모실까요? 댁으로 가나요?\" 표트르가 물었다.\n\n\"그래, 집으로.\" 그녀는 자기가 어디로 가는지조차 생각하지 않고 대답했다.\n\n'그 사람들은 나를 마치 무섭고 알 수 없고 신기한 것이라도 되는 양 쳐다봤어. 저 사람은 무슨 얘기를 저렇게 열을 올려 떠들어 대는 걸까?' 그녀는 곁을 지나가는 두 남자를 응시하며 생각했다. '자기가 느끼는 걸 누군가에게 말할 수 있기나 한 걸까? 돌리한테 말하려고 했는데, 말하지 않은 게 잘한 일이야. 그 여자는 내 불행을 얼마나 고소해했을까! 겉으로는 감췄겠지만, 마음속 깊은 데서는, 그녀가 나에게 부러워하던 그 행복 때문에 내가 벌을 받는다는 사실에 기뻐했을 거야. 키티는, 그 애는 더 고소해했겠지. 나는 그 애 속을 훤히 들여다볼 수 있어! 그 애는 내가 자기 남편한테 평소보다 더 상냥하게 굴었다는 걸 알아. 그래서 질투하고 나를 미워하는 거야. 게다가 나를 경멸하지. 그 애 눈에 나는 부도덕한 여자야. 내가 정말 부도덕한 여자였다면, 그 애 남편이 나한테 반하게 만들 수도 있었어……마음만 먹었다면 말이야. 사실 마음먹기도 했고.' 저기 자기 자신에게 만족스러워하는 사람이 하나 있군, 하고 그녀는 뚱뚱하고 혈색 좋은 신사가 자기 쪽으로 걸어오는 것을 보며 생각했다. 그는 그녀를 아는 사람으로 착각하고는 번들거리는 대머리 위로 반들반들한 모자를 들어 올렸다가, 곧 자기 실수를 깨달았다. '저 사람은 나를 안다고 생각했지. 글쎄, 저 사람도 이 세상 누구만큼이나 나를 알아. 나 자신도 나를 모르는데. 프랑스 사람들 말마따나 나는 내 욕망만 알 뿐이지. 저 애들은 저 더러운 아이스크림이 먹고 싶은 거고, 그것만큼은 확실히 아는 거야.' 그녀는 아이스크림 장수를 붙잡아 세운 두 사내아이를 바라보며 생각했다. 장수는 머리에서 통을 내려놓고 수건으로 땀에 젖은 얼굴을 닦기 시작했다. '우리는 모두 달콤하고 기분 좋은 걸 원해. 사탕이 아니면 더러운 아이스크림이라도. 키티도 마찬가지야. 브론스키가 아니면 레빈이지. 그 애는 나를 부러워하고, 또 미워해. 우리는 모두 서로 미워해. 나는 키티를, 키티는 나를. 그래, 그게 진실이야. Tiutkin, coiffeur. Je me fais coiffer par Tiutkin…… 그 사람이 오면 이 얘기를 해 줘야지.' 그녀는 그렇게 생각하며 미소 지었다. 하지만 바로 그 순간 그녀는 이제 자기에게는 재미있는 이야기를 들려줄 상대가 아무도 없다는 사실을 떠올렸다. '게다가 재미있는 것도, 즐거운 것도 아무것도 없어, 사실은. 모든 게 다 가증스러워. 저녁 기도를 위해 노래를 부르고 있고, 저 상인은 또 얼마나 정성스레 성호를 긋는지! 마치 뭔가 놓칠까 봐 두려워하기라도 하는 것처럼. 이 교회들이며 이 노래며 이 가식은 다 뭐 때문이지? 그저 우리가 모두 서로 미워한다는 걸 감추기 위해서일 뿐이야, 저기서 저렇게 험악하게 욕설을 퍼붓는 마부들처럼 말이야. 야시빈은 말하지. \"저 사람은 내 셔츠까지 벗겨 가려 하고, 나는 그자의 셔츠를 벗겨 가려 하지.\" 그래, 그게 진실이야!'\n\n* coiffeur(쿠아푀르): 프랑스어로 '미용사'. Je me fais coiffer par Tiutkin: '나는 튜트킨에게 머리를 맡긴다'라는 뜻.\n그녀는 이런 생각들에 너무 깊이 빠져들어 자기 처지에 대한 생각마저 잊어버렸는데, 그때 마차가 그녀의 집 현관 계단 앞에 멈춰 섰다. 자기를 맞으러 달려 나오는 문지기를 보고서야 그녀는 자기가 쪽지와 전보를 보냈다는 사실을 떠올렸다.\n\n\"답장이 왔나?\" 그녀가 물었다.\n\n\"지금 바로 보고 오겠습니다.\" 문지기는 그렇게 대답하고 자기 방을 들여다보더니, 얇은 정사각형 전보 봉투를 꺼내 그녀에게 건넸다. \"열 시 전에는 갈 수 없소.—브론스키.\" 그녀는 읽었다.\n\n\"심부름꾼은 아직 안 돌아왔고?\"\n\n\"네.\" 문지기가 대답했다.\n\n\"그렇다면, 사정이 그러하다면, 내가 어떻게 해야 할지 알겠어.\" 그녀는 이렇게 말하며, 자기 안에서 막연한 분노와 복수에 대한 갈망이 솟구쳐 오르는 것을 느끼면서 위층으로 뛰어올라 갔다. '내가 직접 그이한테 가야지. 영영 떠나기 전에, 그이한테 모든 걸 말해 줄 거야. 그 사람을 미워하는 만큼 누군가를 미워해 본 적이 없어!' 그녀는 생각했다. 옷걸이에 걸린 그의 모자를 보자 그녀는 혐오감에 몸서리쳤다. 그녀는 그의 전보가 자기 전보에 대한 답장이며, 그가 아직 자기 쪽지를 받지 못했다는 것을 헤아리지 못했다. 그녀는 그가 자기 어머니와 소로키나 공작 영애에게 태연히 이야기를 늘어놓으며 자기의 괴로움을 고소해하는 모습을 머릿속에 그려 보았다. '그래, 어서 가야 해.' 그녀는 아직 자기가 어디로 가는지도 모른 채 그렇게 말했다. 그녀는 그 끔찍한 집에서 겪은 감정들로부터 한시바삐 벗어나고 싶었다. 하인들, 벽, 그 집 안의 물건들—모든 것이 그녀 안에 반감과 증오를 불러일으키며 무거운 짐처럼 그녀를 짓눌렀다.\n\n'그래, 역으로 가야지. 그이가 거기 없으면, 거기로 가서 그이를 붙잡아야겠어.' 안나는 신문에 실린 기차 시간표를 들여다보았다. 저녁 기차는 여덟 시 이 분에 떠났다. '그래, 시간 안에 닿을 수 있어.' 그녀는 마차에 다른 말들을 매라고 지시하고, 며칠 동안 필요한 물건들을 여행 가방에 꾸렸다. 그녀는 다시는 이곳으로 돌아오지 않으리라는 것을 알고 있었다.\n\n머릿속에 떠오른 여러 계획 가운데, 그녀는 역에서나 백작 부인 집에서 어떤 일이 벌어진 뒤에는, 니즈니로 가는 길의 첫 번째 도시까지 가서 거기 머물기로 어렴풋이 마음먹었다.\n\n저녁 식사가 식탁에 차려져 있었다. 그녀는 가까이 다가갔지만, 빵과 치즈 냄새만으로도 모든 음식이 역겹게 느껴졌다. 그녀는 마차를 대령하라 이르고 밖으로 나갔다. 이제 집은 길 건너편까지 그림자를 드리우고 있었으나, 환한 저녁이었고 햇살 속은 여전히 따뜻했다. 그녀의 짐을 들고 따라 내려온 안누시카도, 짐을 마차에 싣는 표트르도, 누가 봐도 심기가 불편한 마부도, 모두 그녀에게는 가증스러웠고, 그들의 말과 행동이 그녀를 짜증나게 했다.\n\n\"넌 필요 없어, 표트르.\"\n\n\"그렇지만 표는 어떻게 합니까?\"\n\n\"뭐, 네 마음대로 해, 그건 아무래도 좋아.\" 그녀가 짜증스레 말했다.\n\n표트르는 마부석으로 뛰어올라, 두 손을 허리에 척 짚고는 마부에게 매표소로 가자고 일렀다.\n\n# Chapter 30\n\n\"또 시작이군! 또 모든 게 이해가 돼!\" 마차가 출발해 가볍게 흔들리며 포장도로의 작은 자갈 위를 덜컹덜컹 달리기 시작하자마자, 그리고 다시 하나의 인상이 다른 인상에 빠르게 잇따르자마자, 안나는 혼잣말을 했다.\n\n\"그래, 내가 그렇게 또렷이 떠올렸던 마지막 게 뭐였지?\" 그녀는 그것을 떠올리려 애썼다. \"‘Tiutkin, coiffeur?’—아니, 그게 아니야. 그래, 야시빈이 말한 거였지, 생존을 위한 투쟁과 증오야말로 사람들을 한데 묶어 주는 유일한 거라고. 아니, 너희가 가는 그 여행은 부질없는 거야.\" 그녀는 분명 시골로 소풍을 가는 듯한, 사두마차에 탄 일행에게 마음속으로 말을 걸었다. \"그리고 데려가는 그 개도 너희에게 아무 도움이 안 될 거고. 너희 자신에게서는 달아날 수 없으니까.\" 표트르가 돌아보던 방향으로 눈길을 돌리자, 거의 곤드레만드레 취해 고개를 떨군 공장 직공 하나가 경관에게 끌려가는 모습이 보였다. \"흥, 저 사람은 더 빠른 길을 찾았군.\" 그녀는 생각했다. \"브론스키 백작과 나도 그 행복을 찾지 못했어, 그것에서 그렇게 많은 걸 기대했는데도.\" 그리고 이제 처음으로 안나는, 모든 것을 비추던 그 눈부신 빛을, 지금껏 생각하기를 피해 왔던 그와의 관계로 돌렸다. \"그이가 내게서 찾으려 한 게 뭐였지? 사랑이라기보다는 허영심의 만족이었어.\" 그녀는 그의 말, 그의 표정을 떠올렸다, 그것은 그들 관계 초기의, 비굴한 세터 사냥개를 떠올리게 했다. 그리고 이제 모든 것이 이를 확인해 주었다. \"그래, 그이 안에는 성공의 승리감이 있었어. 물론 사랑도 있었지만, 주된 요소는 성공의 자부심이었지. 그이는 나를 자랑거리로 삼았어. 이제 그건 끝났어. 자랑할 게 아무것도 없는 거야. 자랑할 게 아니라 부끄러워할 거지. 그이는 내게서 가질 수 있는 걸 다 가져갔고, 이제 나는 그이에게 쓸모가 없어. 그이는 나한테 싫증이 났으면서도 내게 비열하게 굴지 않으려고 애쓰는 거야. 어제 그걸 흘렸어—배수의 진을 치려고 이혼과 결혼을 원한다고. 그이는 나를 사랑해, 하지만 어떻게? 영국 사람들 말마따나, 흥취가 가신 거지. 저 사내는 모두가 자기를 우러러봐 주길 바라고 자기 자신에게 아주 만족해하는군.\" 그녀는 승마학교 말을 타고 가는, 얼굴이 벌건 서기를 바라보며 생각했다. \"그래, 이제 내겐 그이를 위한 그런 풍미가 없어. 내가 그이를 떠나면, 마음 깊은 곳에서 그이는 기뻐할 거야.\"\n\n이것은 한낱 추측이 아니었다, 그녀는 그것을 꿰뚫는 빛 속에서 또렷이 보았다, 그 빛은 이제 그녀에게 삶과 인간관계의 의미를 드러내 주었다.\n\n\"내 사랑은 점점 더 격정적이고 이기적으로 자라 가는데, 그이의 사랑은 점점 더 시들고 또 시들어 가, 그래서 우리가 서로 멀어지는 거지.\" 그녀는 계속 생각에 잠겼다. \"그리고 이건 어쩔 도리가 없어. 그이는 내게 전부인데, 나는 점점 더 그이가 온전히 나에게만 자신을 바쳐 주길 바라. 그런데 그이는 점점 더 나에게서 벗어나려 하지. 우리는 사랑이 무르익을 때까지는 서로를 향해 걸어왔고, 그 후로는 거역할 수 없이 서로 다른 방향으로 흘러가고 있어. 그리고 그건 바꿀 수가 없어. 그이는 내가 미치도록 질투한다고 말하고, 나도 스스로 미치도록 질투한다고 말해 왔지, 하지만 그건 사실이 아니야. 나는 질투하는 게 아니라 만족하지 못하는 거야. 하지만…….\" 그녀는 입술을 벌리고, 문득 떠오른 생각에 흥분해 마차 안에서 자리를 고쳐 앉았다. \"내가 정부(情婦) 말고, 그이의 애무 말고는 아무것에도 격정적으로 마음 쓰지 않는 여자 말고 다른 무언가가 될 수만 있다면. 하지만 나는 다른 무언가가 될 수 없고, 되고 싶지도 않아. 그리고 그 욕망으로 나는 그이 안에 혐오를 불러일으키고, 그이는 내 안에 분노를 불러일으키고, 그건 달라질 수가 없어. 그이가 나를 속이지 않으리라는 걸, 소로키나 공작 영애에 대해 아무 꿍꿍이도 없다는 걸, 키티를 사랑하지 않는다는 걸, 나를 버리지 않으리라는 걸 내가 모르겠어! 그걸 다 알지만, 그렇다고 내게 나아지는 건 하나도 없어. 만약 나를 사랑하지도 않으면서, 의무에서 내게 다정하고 친절하게 군다면, 내가 원하는 것은 없이 말이야, 그건 불친절보다 천 배는 더 끔찍해! 그건—지옥이야! 그리고 바로 그런 거야. 이미 오래전부터 그이는 나를 사랑하지 않아. 그리고 사랑이 끝나는 곳에서 증오가 시작되지. 이 거리들은 통 모르겠네. 언덕인 것 같고, 또 집들이, 집들이……. 그리고 집들 안에는 늘 사람들이, 사람들이……. 얼마나 많은지, 끝이 없어, 게다가 모두가 서로를 미워하지! 자, 나를 행복하게 해 주려면 내가 뭘 원하는지 한번 생각해 보자. 어떨까? 내가 이혼하고, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 세료자를 내게 주고, 내가 브론스키와 결혼한다고 치자.\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치를 떠올리자, 그녀는 곧바로 그가 살아서 눈앞에 있는 듯이 비상하게 생생하게 그려 보았다, 그 온순하고 생기 없고 흐리멍덩한 눈, 흰 손등의 푸른 핏줄, 그의 억양과 손가락 마디를 꺾는 소리를, 그리고 그들 사이에 존재했던, 역시 사랑이라 불리던 그 감정을 떠올리며, 그녀는 혐오로 몸서리쳤다. \"좋아, 나는 이혼하고, 브론스키의 아내가 돼. 그래서, 키티가 오늘 나를 바라보던 것처럼 더는 나를 바라보지 않게 될까? 아니야. 그리고 세료자가 내 두 남편에 대해 묻고 의아해하는 걸 그만둘까? 그리고 브론스키와 나 사이에 내가 일깨울 수 있는 새로운 감정이 있을까? 행복은 아니더라도, 비참함에서 벗어난 어떤 편안함이라도 가능할까? 아니, 아니야!\" 그녀는 이제 조금의 망설임도 없이 스스로 대답했다. \"불가능해! 우리는 삶에 의해 갈라졌고, 나는 그이를 불행하게 하고, 그이는 나를 불행하게 하고, 그이도 나도 바꿀 수 없어. 온갖 시도를 다 해 봤지만, 나사가 헐거워져 빠져 버렸어. 아, 아기를 안은 거지 여자네. 저 여자는 내가 자기를 가엾어한다고 여기지. 우리 모두는 서로를 미워하고, 그렇게 자기 자신과 서로를 괴롭히려고 세상에 내던져진 게 아닐까? 학생들이 오네—웃고 있는 세료자?\" 그녀는 생각했다. \"나도 그 애를 사랑한다고 생각했고, 내 자신의 다정함에 곧잘 가슴이 뭉클하곤 했지. 하지만 나는 그 애 없이 살았고, 다른 사랑을 위해 그 애를 포기했고, 그 사랑이 충족될 때까지는 그 맞바꿈을 후회하지 않았어.\" 그리고 그녀는 그 사랑이라는 게 무얼 뜻하는지 혐오스럽게 생각했다. 그리고 이제 자신과 모든 사람의 삶을 보는 그 명료함이 그녀에게는 하나의 쾌감이었다. \"나와 표트르도, 마부 표도르도, 저 상인도, 저 광고판들이 가 보라고 권하는 볼가 강을 따라 사는 모든 사람들도, 어디서나 언제나 다 그런 거야.\" 나지고로드 역의 나지막한 지붕 아래로 마차가 들어서고 짐꾼들이 그녀를 맞으러 달려왔을 때, 그녀는 생각했다.\n\n\"오비랄로프카행 표 한 장이오?\" 표트르가 말했다.\n\n그녀는 어디로 무엇 때문에 가는지를 완전히 잊고 있었고, 큰 노력을 기울여서야 겨우 그 질문을 알아들었다.\n\n\"그래.\" 그녀는 그에게 지갑을 건네며 말하고는, 손에 작은 빨간 가방을 들고 마차에서 내렸다.\n\n군중을 헤치고 일등 대합실로 향하면서, 그녀는 차츰 자기 처지의 온갖 세세한 정황과, 자기가 망설이고 있는 여러 계획들을 떠올렸다. 그리고 다시금 그 오래된 상처 자리마다, 희망이, 그다음에는 절망이 그녀의 괴로워하는, 무섭게 두근거리는 심장의 상처를 독으로 적셨다. 기차를 기다리며 별 모양 소파에 앉아 있는 동안, 그녀는 오가는 사람들을 (그들 모두가 그녀에게는 가증스러웠다) 혐오스럽게 바라보았고, 자기가 역에 어떻게 도착할지, 그에게 어떻게 쪽지를 쓸지, 그에게 무어라고 쓸지, 그리고 그가 지금 이 순간 자기 어머니에게 자기 처지를 어떻게 하소연하고 있을지, 그녀의 고통은 이해하지도 못한 채로, 그리고 자기가 어떻게 방으로 들어갈지, 그에게 무어라고 말할지를 생각했다. 그러다 삶이 아직 행복할 수도 있으리라는 생각이, 자기가 얼마나 비참하게 그를 사랑하고 또 미워하는지가, 그리고 자기 심장이 얼마나 무섭게 뛰고 있는지가 떠올랐다.\n\n# Chapter 31\n\n벨이 울리자, 못생기고 뻔뻔하면서도 동시에 자기가 주는 인상에 신경을 쓰는 젊은 남자 몇이 서둘러 지나갔다. 표트르 역시 제복과 긴 장화 차림으로, 둔하고 짐승 같은 얼굴을 한 채 방을 가로질러 그녀에게 다가와 기차로 데려가려 했다. 시끄럽게 떠들던 남자들이 그녀가 플랫폼에서 곁을 지나가자 입을 다물었고, 그중 하나가 그녀에 대해 다른 남자에게 뭔가를 속삭였다. 분명 천박한 말이었으리라. 그녀는 높은 발판을 딛고 올라가, 한때 흰색이었던 더러운 좌석에 혼자 객실에 앉았다. 그녀의 가방은 옆에 놓여 좌석의 탄력에 따라 위아래로 흔들렸다. 표트르는 멍청한 미소를 지으며 작별의 표시로 창가에서 색띠가 둘린 모자를 들어 올렸고, 뻔뻔한 차장이 문과 빗장을 쾅 닫았다. 버슬을 댄 기괴한 모습의 부인(안나는 마음속으로 그 여자의 옷을 벗겨 보고는 그 흉측함에 소름이 끼쳤다)과 꾸민 듯이 웃어 대는 어린 여자아이가 플랫폼을 뛰어 내려갔다.\n\n\"카체리나 안드레예브나, 그 애가 다 가졌어요, ma tante!\" 여자아이가 소리쳤다.\n\n* ma tante: 프랑스어로 '이모(고모)'.\n\"애조차 흉측하고 가식적이군.\" 안나는 생각했다. 누구의 눈에도 띄지 않으려고 그녀는 얼른 일어나 텅 빈 객실의 맞은편 창가에 자리를 잡았다. 더러운 것을 뒤집어쓴 기형적인 모습의 농부 하나가, 헝클어진 머리카락이 사방으로 삐져나온 모자를 쓰고서 객차 바퀴 쪽으로 몸을 숙이며 그 창 옆을 지나갔다. \"저 흉측한 농부에게 뭔가 낯익은 데가 있어.\" 안나는 생각했다. 그리고 자기 꿈을 떠올리며 공포에 떨면서 맞은편 문 쪽으로 옮겨 갔다. 차장이 문을 열고 한 남자와 그의 아내를 들여보냈다.\n\n\"내리시겠습니까?\"\n\n안나는 아무 대답도 하지 않았다. 차장과 두 동승객은 그녀의 베일 아래 공포에 질린 얼굴을 알아채지 못했다. 그녀는 다시 자기 구석 자리로 돌아가 앉았다. 부부는 맞은편에 자리를 잡고, 유심히 그러나 몰래 그녀의 옷차림을 살폈다. 남편도 아내도 안나에게는 역겹게 느껴졌다. 남편은 그녀에게 담배를 피워도 되겠느냐고 물었는데, 분명 담배를 피우려는 것이 아니라 그녀와 대화를 트려는 속셈이었다. 그녀의 승낙을 얻자 그는 아내에게 프랑스어로 담배를 피우기보다 이야기를 나누고 싶다는 식의 말을 했다. 그들은 오로지 그녀에게 들으라는 듯이 서로에게 시시하고 가식적인 말을 주고받았다. 안나는 그들이 서로에게 진력이 났고 서로를 증오하고 있음을 똑똑히 알아챘다. 그리고 이토록 가련하고 기괴한 인간들이라면 누구라도 증오하지 않을 수 없었으리라.\n\n두 번째 벨이 울렸고, 뒤이어 짐 옮기는 소리, 소음, 고함과 웃음이 따랐다. 누구에게도 기뻐할 거리라곤 전혀 없다는 것이 안나에게는 너무도 분명했기에, 이 웃음소리는 그녀를 괴로울 만큼 짜증나게 했고, 그녀는 그 소리를 듣지 않으려고 귀를 막아 버리고 싶었다. 마침내 세 번째 벨이 울리고, 기적 소리와 증기 새는 소리, 쇠사슬 부딪치는 소리가 났고, 그녀의 객실에 있던 남자가 성호를 그었다. \"저 사람에게 그게 무슨 의미인지 물어보면 재미있겠군.\" 안나는 그를 화난 눈으로 바라보며 생각했다. 그녀는 부인 너머로 창밖을 내다보았는데, 기차 옆을 따라 달리거나 플랫폼에 서 있는 사람들이 빙빙 돌며 스쳐 지나가는 것 같았다. 기차는 레일 이음매에서 일정한 간격으로 덜컹거리며 플랫폼을 지나, 돌담과 신호소를 지나, 다른 기차들을 지나쳐 굴러갔고, 바퀴는 더 부드럽고 고르게 움직이며 레일 위에서 가벼운 쇳소리를 냈다. 창문은 밝은 저녁 햇살로 환해졌고, 가벼운 산들바람이 커튼을 나부끼게 했다. 안나는 동승객들을 잊은 채, 기차의 가벼운 흔들림 속에서 신선한 공기를 들이마시며 다시 생각에 잠겼다.\n\n\"그래, 내가 어디서 멈췄더라? 삶이 비참하지 않을 상황이란 도무지 상상할 수 없다는 것, 우리는 모두 비참해지도록 만들어졌다는 것, 그리고 우리 모두 그 사실을 알면서도 서로를 속이는 수단을 만들어 낸다는 것. 그러면 진실을 보았을 때, 사람은 어떻게 해야 하지?\"\n\n\"그게 바로 인간에게 이성이 주어진 이유잖아요. 자기를 괴롭히는 것에서 벗어나기 위해서요.\" 부인이 프랑스어로 일부러 혀 짧은 소리를 내며 말했는데, 자기가 한 말에 흡족해하는 기색이 역력했다.\n\n그 말은 마치 안나의 생각에 대한 대답인 것 같았다.\n\n\"자기를 괴롭히는 것에서 벗어나기 위해.\" 안나는 되뇌었다. 그리고 두 볼이 불그스레한 남편과 마른 아내를 흘끗 보고는, 병약한 아내는 자기가 이해받지 못한다고 여기고, 남편은 그녀를 속이면서 그녀가 스스로에 대해 품은 그런 생각을 부추기고 있음을 알아차렸다. 안나는 마치 그들 위에 빛을 비추기라도 하듯, 그들의 모든 내력과 영혼의 온갖 구석구석을 다 들여다보는 것 같았다. 하지만 거기에는 흥미로운 것이라곤 아무것도 없었고, 그녀는 자기 생각을 이어 나갔다.\n\n\"그래, 나는 무척 괴로워하고 있어. 바로 그것 때문에 내게 이성이 주어진 거지, 벗어나라고. 그러니 벗어나야 해. 더 이상 볼 것이 없을 때, 그 모든 걸 보는 게 역겨울 때, 어째서 불을 꺼 버리면 안 되는 거지? 그런데 어떻게? 어째서 차장은 발판을 따라 달려갔을까, 어째서 저들은, 저 기차 안의 젊은 남자들은 비명을 질러 대고 있을까? 어째서 저들은 이야기를 나누고, 어째서 웃어 대는 거지? 모두 거짓이야, 모두 허위고, 모두 속임수고, 모두 잔인함이야!……\"\n\n기차가 역에 들어서자 안나는 승객들 무리 속으로 내려서서, 그들이 나병 환자라도 되는 양 그들에게서 떨어져 플랫폼에 서서, 자기가 무엇 하러 여기 왔는지, 무엇을 하려 했는지 떠올리려 애썼다. 전에는 가능해 보였던 모든 일이 이제 와서는 도무지 생각하기 어려웠는데, 특히 그녀를 가만히 내버려 두지 않는 이 흉측한 사람들의 시끄러운 무리 속에서는 더욱 그랬다. 어느 순간 짐꾼들이 그녀에게 달려와 시중을 자청했고, 그다음에는 젊은 남자들이 플랫폼 널빤지에 구두 뒤축을 따각거리고 큰 소리로 떠들며 그녀를 빤히 쳐다보았으며, 그녀와 마주친 사람들은 엉뚱한 쪽으로 비켜 지나갔다. 답장이 없으면 더 멀리 가려 했다는 것을 떠올리고, 그녀는 짐꾼 하나를 붙잡고 자기 마부가 브론스키 백작의 쪽지를 가지고 여기 와 있지 않느냐고 물었다.\n\n\"브론스키 백작이요? 방금 막 브론스키 댁에서 여기로 사람을 보냈습니다, 소로키나 공작 부인과 따님을 마중하러요. 그런데 마부가 어떻게 생겼습니까?\"\n\n그녀가 짐꾼과 이야기를 나누고 있을 바로 그때, 마부 미하일이 멋진 푸른 외투와 사슬 장식을 두르고 얼굴이 불그레하니 명랑한 모습으로, 자기 임무를 그토록 성공적으로 완수한 것을 분명 자랑스러워하며 그녀에게 다가와 편지를 건넸다. 그녀는 편지를 뜯었고, 미처 읽기도 전에 가슴이 아려 왔다.\n\n\"당신의 쪽지가 내게 닿지 못해서 정말 유감이오. 열 시에 집에 돌아가겠소.\" 브론스키는 무심하게 휘갈겨 놓았다……\n\n\"그래, 내가 예상했던 그대로야!\" 그녀는 사악한 미소를 지으며 혼잣말을 했다.\n\n\"좋아, 그럼 자네는 집으로 돌아가게.\" 그녀는 미하일을 향해 나직이 말했다. 심장이 너무 빠르게 뛰어 숨쉬기가 힘들었기에 나직이 말한 것이었다. \"안 돼, 네가 나를 비참하게 만들도록 내버려 두지 않을 거야.\" 그녀는 그를 향해서도, 자기 자신을 향해서도 아닌, 자기를 괴롭히는 그 힘을 향해 위협하듯 생각하며 플랫폼을 따라 걸었다.\n\n플랫폼을 따라 걷던 두 하녀가 고개를 돌려 그녀를 빤히 보며 그녀의 옷차림에 대해 뭐라고 한마디씩 했다. \"진짜네.\" 그들은 그녀가 두른 레이스를 두고 말했다. 젊은 남자들은 그녀를 가만히 내버려 두지 않았다. 그들은 다시 그녀의 얼굴을 들여다보며 곁을 지나갔고, 웃으면서 부자연스러운 목소리로 뭐라고 소리쳤다. 다가온 역장은 그녀에게 기차로 가시느냐고 물었다. 크바스를 파는 소년은 그녀에게서 한순간도 눈을 떼지 않았다. \"맙소사! 나는 어디로 가야 하지?\" 그녀는 플랫폼을 따라 점점 더 멀리 가며 생각했다. 끝에 이르러 그녀는 멈춰 섰다. 안경 쓴 신사를 마중 나온 몇몇 부인과 아이들이 큰 소리로 웃고 떠들다가 멈추고는, 그녀가 다가가자 빤히 쳐다보았다. 그녀는 걸음을 재촉해 그들에게서 멀어져 플랫폼 가장자리로 걸어갔다. 화물 열차가 들어오고 있었다. 플랫폼이 흔들리기 시작했고, 그녀는 자기가 다시 기차 안에 있는 것 같은 느낌이 들었다.\n\n그러다 문득 그녀는 브론스키를 처음 만난 날 기차에 깔려 죽은 남자를 떠올렸고, 자기가 무엇을 해야 하는지 깨달았다. 빠르고 가벼운 걸음으로 그녀는 급수탑에서 레일로 이어지는 계단을 내려가, 다가오는 기차 바로 가까이에 멈춰 섰다.\n\n그녀는 객차의 아랫부분, 나사와 쇠사슬, 천천히 굴러오는 첫 번째 객차의 높다란 주철 바퀴를 바라보며, 앞바퀴와 뒷바퀴 사이의 한가운데를, 그리고 그 한가운데 지점이 정확히 자기 맞은편에 오는 바로 그 순간을 가늠하려 애썼다.\n\n\"저기.\" 그녀는 객차의 그림자 속, 침목을 덮은 모래와 석탄 가루를 들여다보며 혼잣말을 했다. \"저기, 바로 한가운데에, 그러면 나는 그를 벌하고 모든 사람에게서, 그리고 나 자신에게서 벗어날 거야.\"\n\n그녀는 첫 번째 객차가 자기에게 닿는 순간 바퀴 아래로 몸을 던지려 했지만, 손에서 떨어뜨리려던 붉은 가방이 그녀를 지체시켜 너무 늦어 버렸고, 그 순간을 놓치고 말았다. 그녀는 다음 객차를 기다려야 했다. 물에 들어가 처음으로 몸을 담그려 할 때 느끼던 것과 같은 감각이 그녀에게 밀려왔고, 그녀는 성호를 그었다. 그 익숙한 동작은 그녀의 영혼에 처녀 시절과 어린 시절의 온갖 기억을 한꺼번에 되살려 놓았고, 갑자기 그녀의 모든 것을 덮고 있던 어둠이 찢겨 나가면서, 삶이 그 모든 밝고 지난 기쁨과 더불어 한순간 그녀 앞에 솟아올랐다. 하지만 그녀는 두 번째 객차의 바퀴에서 눈을 떼지 않았다. 그리고 바퀴 사이의 공간이 자기 맞은편에 온 바로 그 순간, 그녀는 붉은 가방을 떨어뜨리고, 어깨 사이로 고개를 움츠리고는, 객차 아래로 두 손을 짚으며 쓰러졌고, 마치 곧바로 다시 일어서기라도 할 것처럼 가볍게 무릎을 꿇었다. 그리고 바로 그 순간 그녀는 자기가 하고 있는 일에 공포에 사로잡혔다. \"내가 어디 있는 거지? 내가 무얼 하고 있는 거야? 무엇 때문에?\" 그녀는 일어서려고, 뒤로 몸을 빼려고 애썼다. 하지만 무언가 거대하고 무자비한 것이 그녀의 머리를 내리쳤고 그녀의 등을 짓이기며 굴러갔다. \"주여, 저의 모든 죄를 용서하소서!\" 그녀는 더 이상 발버둥 치는 것이 불가능함을 느끼며 말했다. 무어라 중얼거리는 농부 하나가 그녀의 머리 위쪽에서 쇠를 다루고 있었다. 그리고 근심과 거짓과 슬픔과 악으로 가득 찬 그 책을 읽게 해 주던 빛이, 그 어느 때보다 환하게 타올라, 어둠 속에 있던 모든 것을 그녀에게 밝혀 주고는, 깜박이다가 어두워지기 시작하더니, 영영 꺼져 버렸다.\n\n# PART EIGHT Chapter 1\n\n거의 두 달이 흘렀다. 무더운 여름이 절반쯤 지났지만, 세르게이 이바노비치는 이제야 비로소 모스크바를 떠날 채비를 하고 있었다.\n\n이 기간 동안 세르게이 이바노비치의 삶에 별다른 사건이 없었던 것은 아니다. 일 년 전, 그는 육 년에 걸친 노고의 결실인 자신의 책 『유럽과 러시아의 통치 원리 및 형태에 관한 개관 시론』을 완성했다. 이 책의 몇몇 부분과 서문은 정기 간행물에 발표되었고, 다른 부분들은 세르게이 이바노비치가 자기 모임의 사람들에게 읽어 준 바 있었으므로, 이 저작의 핵심 사상이 대중에게 완전히 새로운 것일 수는 없었다. 그러나 그래도 세르게이 이바노비치는 자기 책이 세상에 나오면 틀림없이 사회에 진지한 인상을 줄 것이며, 설령 사회과학에 혁명을 일으키지는 못하더라도, 적어도 학계에 큰 파란을 불러일으키리라 기대하고 있었다.\n\n지극히 양심적인 교정을 거친 끝에 그 책은 작년에 출간되어 서점들에 배포되었다.\n\n비록 그는 누구에게도 그것에 대해 묻지 않았고, 책이 어떻게 되어 가느냐는 친구들의 물음에는 마지못해 무관심을 가장하며 대답했으며, 책이 얼마나 팔리는지조차 서점에 물어보지 않았지만, 세르게이 이바노비치는 자기 책이 세상과 문단에 일으킬 첫 반응을 긴장된 주의를 곤두세운 채 온통 신경을 곤두세우고 지켜보고 있었다.\n\n그러나 한 주가 지나고, 또 한 주가 지나고, 세 번째 주가 지나도, 사교계에서는 아무런 반응도 감지할 수 없었다. 전문가나 학자인 그의 친구들은 가끔씩—틀림없이 예의상—그 책을 슬쩍 언급했다. 학문적인 주제를 다룬 책에 관심이 없는 나머지 지인들은 그것에 대해 전혀 입에 올리지 않았다. 그리고 사교계는 일반적으로—특히 지금은 다른 일에 정신이 팔려—철저히 무관심했다. 언론에서도 한 달이 다 가도록 그의 책에 관해서는 한마디도 없었다.\n\n세르게이 이바노비치는 서평을 쓰는 데 필요한 시간을 정확하게 계산해 두었지만, 한 달이 지나고, 또 한 달이 지나도 여전히 침묵뿐이었다.\n\n다만 『북방의 딱정벌레』에 목소리를 잃은 가수 드라반티에 관한 익살스러운 글이 실렸는데, 거기서 코즈니셰프의 책을 경멸하듯 언급하며, 그 책이 이미 오래전에 모두에게 간파되어 일반적인 조롱거리가 되었다고 빈정거렸다.\n\n마침내 세 번째 달에 한 진지한 평론지에 비평 기사가 실렸다. 세르게이 이바노비치는 그 글의 필자를 알고 있었다. 골루프초프의 집에서 한 번 만난 적이 있었다.\n\n그 글의 필자는 젊은이로, 병약한 몸이었고, 작가로서는 무척 대담했지만, 교양이 몹시 부족하고 개인적인 교제에서는 수줍음을 탔다.\n\n필자에 대한 절대적인 경멸에도 불구하고, 세르게이 이바노비치는 더없이 정중한 태도로 그 글을 읽기 시작했다. 그 글은 끔찍했다.\n\n비평가는 분명 그 책에 도저히 부여할 수 없는 해석을 부여해 놓았다. 그러나 그는 인용문을 어찌나 교묘하게 골라냈던지, 책을 읽지 않은 사람들에게는(그리고 분명 그 책을 읽은 사람은 거의 없었다) 이 책 전체가 거창한 문구들의 잡탕에 지나지 않으며, 그나마—물음표로 암시되었듯이—적절하게 쓰이지도 않았고, 책의 저자가 그 주제에 대해 아무런 지식도 없는 사람이라는 것이 너무도 명백해 보였다. 그리고 이 모든 것이 어찌나 재치 있게 이루어졌던지, 세르게이 이바노비치 자신도 그런 재치라면 마다하지 않았을 정도였다. 그러나 바로 그 점이 그토록 끔찍했다.\n\n세르게이 이바노비치는 비평가의 논거가 옳은지 세심한 양심을 다해 검증했음에도, 조롱당한 결함과 오류를 곱씹어 보느라 한순간도 멈추지 않았다. 그 대신 자기도 모르게 곧바로 그 글의 필자와 만났던 일이며 나누었던 대화의 세세한 부분들을 하나하나 떠올리기 시작했다.\n\n\"내가 어떤 식으로든 그를 모욕하지 않았던가?\" 세르게이 이바노비치는 생각했다.\n\n그리고 두 사람이 만났을 때 자기가 그 젊은이의 무지를 드러내는 어떤 말을 바로잡아 준 일을 떠올리고는, 세르게이 이바노비치는 그 글을 설명할 실마리를 찾아냈다.\n\n이 글이 나온 뒤로 언론에서도 대화에서도 그 책에 관해서는 죽음 같은 침묵이 이어졌고, 세르게이 이바노비치는 그토록 사랑과 노고를 기울여 매달려 온 육 년의 작업이 아무런 흔적도 남기지 못하고 사라져 버렸음을 깨달았다.\n\n세르게이 이바노비치의 처지는 책을 끝낸 이후로, 지금껏 그의 시간 대부분을 차지해 왔던 그런 문필 작업이 더 이상 없다는 사실 때문에 한층 더 곤란해졌다.\n\n세르게이 이바노비치는 영리하고, 교양 있고, 건강하고, 정력적이었으나, 자기 정력을 어디에 써야 할지 알지 못했다. 응접실에서, 회합에서, 집회에서, 위원회에서—이야기가 가능한 곳이라면 어디서든 나누는 대화가 그의 시간 일부를 차지했다. 그러나 여러 해 도시 생활에 익숙해진 터라, 그는 경험이 부족한 동생이 모스크바에 와 있을 때 그러듯이 자기 정력을 모조리 이야기에 허비하지는 않았다. 아직도 마음껏 쓸 수 있는 여가와 지적 정력이 그에게는 무척 많았다.\n\n그에게는 다행스럽게도, 책의 실패로 그토록 힘겨웠던 이 시기에, 분리파 종파 문제며, 미국과의 동맹 문제며, 사마라의 기근 문제며, 전시회며, 강신술 같은 갖가지 공적 화제들이 대중의 관심 속에서 슬라브 문제로 확연히 대체되었다. 그것은 그때까지만 해도 사교계의 관심을 다소 미적지근하게 끌던 문제였는데, 일찍이 이 주제를 처음 제기한 사람 가운데 하나였던 세르게이 이바노비치는 거기에 온 마음과 영혼을 던져 넣었다.\n\n세르게이 이바노비치가 속한 모임에서는 지금 세르비아 전쟁 외에 달리 화제에 오르거나 글로 쓰이는 것이 없었다. 한가한 무리가 보통 시간을 죽이려고 하던 온갖 일들이 이제는 슬라브 국가들을 위해 행해졌다. 무도회, 음악회, 만찬, 성냥갑, 부인들의 의상, 맥주, 식당—모든 것이 슬라브 민족에 대한 공감을 증언하고 있었다.\n\n이 주제를 두고 사람들이 말하고 쓰는 많은 것들에 대해, 세르게이 이바노비치는 여러 점에서 견해를 달리했다. 그는 슬라브 문제가 사교계에 대상과 소일거리를 제공하며 잇따라 나타나는 그런 유행하는 심심풀이 가운데 하나가 되어 버렸음을 알아보았다. 또한 그는 수많은 사람들이 사리사욕과 자기 과시의 동기에서 이 주제에 매달리고 있음도 알아보았다. 그는 신문들이 오로지 관심을 끌고 서로를 능가하려는 목적 하나로, 군더더기에다 과장된 것들을 잔뜩 게재하고 있음을 알아챘다. 그는 이 전반적인 움직임 속에서 가장 앞으로 나서고 가장 큰 소리로 외쳐 대는 자들이, 실패하여 모욕감에 쓰라려하는 사람들—군대 없는 장군들, 부처에 들지 못한 장관들, 어느 신문에도 소속되지 못한 언론인들, 추종자 없는 당수들—임을 알아보았다. 그는 거기에 경박하고 터무니없는 면이 많다는 것도 알아보았다. 그러나 그는 모든 계층을 하나로 묶는, 공감하지 않을 수 없는 의심할 바 없이 자라나는 열정 또한 보았고 또 인정했다. 같은 그리스도교도이자 같은 슬라브 민족인 사람들이 학살당했다는 사실은 그 수난자들에 대한 공감과 억압자들에 대한 분노를 불러일으켰다. 그리고 위대한 대의를 위해 싸우는 세르비아인들과 몬테네그로인들의 영웅적 행위는 온 국민의 마음속에 말이 아니라 행동으로 자기 형제들을 돕고자 하는 갈망을 낳았다.\n\n그러나 여기에는 세르게이 이바노비치를 기쁘게 하는 또 다른 측면이 있었다. 그것은 바로 여론의 발현이었다. 대중이 자신의 바람을 확연히 표명한 것이다. 세르게이 이바노비치의 말마따나, 민중의 영혼이 표현을 찾아낸 것이다. 그리고 이 대의를 위해 일하면 일할수록, 그것이 거대한 규모를 띠고 한 시대를 열도록 운명 지어진 대의라는 사실이 그에게는 더욱 반박할 수 없는 것으로 여겨졌다.\n\n그는 이 위대한 대의에 봉사하는 일에 온 마음과 영혼을 던졌고, 자기 책에 대해 생각하는 것조차 잊어버렸다. 이제 그의 시간 전부가 그것에 사로잡혀, 자기에게 오는 온갖 편지와 호소에 일일이 답하는 것조차 간신히 해낼 정도였다. 그는 봄 내내, 그리고 여름의 일부까지 그 일에 매달렸고, 시골에 있는 동생의 집으로 떠날 채비를 한 것은 칠월에 이르러서였다.\n\n그는 보름 동안 쉬려는 것이기도 했고, 또한 민중의 한복판에서, 시골의 가장 외진 벽지에서, 자신이 수도와 대도시의 모든 주민들과 마찬가지로 굳게 믿고 있던 그 민중의 정신적 고양을 직접 보고 즐기려는 것이기도 했다. 카타바소프는 레빈의 집에 머물겠다던 약속을 진작부터 지키려 하고 있었으므로, 그와 함께 떠나기로 했다.\n\n# Chapter 2\n\n세르게이 이바노비치와 카타바소프는 마침 그날따라 유난히 붐비고 사람들로 가득한 쿠르스크 선 정거장에 막 도착한 참이었는데, 짐을 들고 뒤따라오는 마부를 찾으려고 주위를 둘러보다가 의용병 일행이 마차 네 대에 나누어 타고 다가오는 것을 보았다. 여인들이 꽃다발을 들고 그들을 맞이했고, 밀려드는 군중을 뒤에 거느린 채 의용병들은 정거장 안으로 들어갔다.\n\n의용병들을 맞이한 여인들 가운데 한 사람이 홀에서 나와 세르게이 이바노비치에게 말을 걸었다.\n\n\"당신도 이분들을 배웅하러 오셨나요?\" 그녀가 프랑스어로 물었다.\n\n\"아닙니다, 공작 부인. 저는 떠나는 길입니다. 동생 집으로 휴가를 가려고요. 부인은 늘 이분들을 배웅하시나요?\" 세르게이 이바노비치가 알아챌 듯 말 듯한 미소를 띠며 말했다.\n\n\"오, 그건 도저히 무리예요!\" 공작 부인이 대답했다. \"우리 쪽에서 벌써 팔백 명이나 보냈다는 게 사실인가요? 말빈스키는 제 말을 믿으려 하질 않더군요.\"\n\n\"팔백 명이 넘습니다. 모스크바에서 직접 보내지 않은 사람들까지 헤아리면 천 명이 넘지요.\" 세르게이 이바노비치가 대답했다.\n\n\"거 보세요! 제가 한 말이 바로 그거예요!\" 부인이 소리쳤다. \"그리고 백만 루블 넘게 모금되었다는 것도 사실이겠죠?\"\n\n\"그렇습니다, 공작 부인.\"\n\n\"오늘 온 전보는 어떻게 보세요? 또다시 터키군을 무찔렀다더군요.\"\n\n\"네, 저도 봤습니다.\" 세르게이 이바노비치가 대답했다. 두 사람은 터키군이 사흘 연속으로 모든 지점에서 격파되어 패주했으며, 내일은 결정적인 교전이 예상된다는 마지막 전보 이야기를 하고 있었다.\n\n\"아, 그런데 말이죠, 아주 훌륭한 젊은이 하나가 가게 해 달라고 청을 넣었는데, 무슨 까닭인지 일을 까다롭게 만들어 놓았더군요. 당신께 부탁드리려 했어요. 저도 아는 청년이거든요. 부디 그 사람 일에 대해 쪽지를 한 장 써 주세요. 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인이 보내는 사람이에요.\"\n\n세르게이 이바노비치는 공작 부인이 그 젊은이에 대해 아는 것을 죄다 물어본 뒤, 일등 대합실로 들어가 휴가 허가를 좌우하는 인물에게 쪽지를 써서 공작 부인에게 건넸다.\n\n\"당신도 그 악명 높은 브론스키 백작이…… 이 기차로 떠난다는 거 아세요?\" 그가 다시 부인을 찾아 편지를 건네주자, 부인은 승리감과 의미가 가득 담긴 미소를 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"떠난다는 얘기는 들었지만, 언제인지는 몰랐습니다. 이 기차로요?\"\n\n\"제가 봤어요. 여기 와 있어요. 어머니만 배웅하러 나왔더군요. 어쨌거나 그로서는 할 수 있는 가장 잘한 일이죠.\"\n\n\"오, 그럼요, 물론입니다.\"\n\n두 사람이 이야기를 나누는 동안 군중이 그들 곁을 지나 식당으로 흘러 들어갔다. 두 사람도 앞으로 나아갔는데, 손에 잔을 든 한 신사가 의용병들을 향해 큰 소리로 연설을 늘어놓는 소리가 들렸다. \"신앙과 인류와 우리 형제들을 위한 봉사에 나서는……\" 신사는 점점 더 목청을 높여 가며 말했다. \"이 위대한 대의에 어머니 모스크바가 축복으로 그대들을 바치노라. 지비오!\" 그는 큰 소리로, 눈물을 글썽이며 끝맺었다.\n* 지비오(Jivio): 세르비아어로 '만세'라는 뜻의 환호.\n\n모두가 \"지비오!\" 하고 외쳤고, 새로운 군중이 홀 안으로 우르르 밀려들어 공작 부인을 하마터면 넘어뜨릴 뻔했다.\n\n\"아, 공작 부인! 그것참 대단했지요!\" 군중 한가운데 불쑥 나타난 스테판 아르카지치가 기쁨에 넘치는 미소로 두 사람을 향해 환하게 웃으며 말했다. \"근사하고 뜨겁게 말하지 않았습니까? 브라보! 그리고 세르게이 이바노비치! 형님이야말로 뭔가 한마디 하셨어야지요. 그저 몇 마디로 저들을 격려하는 말 말입니다. 그런 건 형님이 아주 잘하시잖습니까.\" 그는 부드럽고 공손하면서도 조심스러운 미소를 지으며 덧붙이고는, 세르게이 이바노비치의 팔을 잡아 앞으로 조금 끌었다.\n\n\"아니, 난 막 떠나는 참이야.\"\n\n\"어디로요?\"\n\n\"시골로, 동생 집으로 가는 길이야.\" 세르게이 이바노비치가 대답했다.\n\n\"그럼 제 아내를 보시겠군요. 편지를 써 두긴 했지만, 형님이 먼저 만나실 테죠. 부디 아내에게 사람들이 저를 만났고 영국인들 말마따나 '올 라이트'라고 전해 주세요. 그러면 알아들을 겁니다. 아, 그리고 제가 위원회 간사로 임명되었다고 전해 주시면 고맙겠습니다…… 하지만 아내는 알아들을 거예요! 아시잖습니까, les petites misères de la vie humaine 말입니다.\" 그는 마치 공작 부인에게 변명이라도 하듯 말했다. \"그리고 먀흐카야 공작 부인은요, 리자가 아니라 비비시 말입니다, 소총 천 정과 간호사 열두 명을 보내고 있어요. 제가 말씀드렸던가요?\"\n* les petites misères de la vie humaine: 프랑스어로 '인간 삶의 자질구레한 고충들'.\n\n\"네, 들었습니다.\" 코즈니셰프가 무심하게 대답했다.\n\n\"형님이 떠나신다니 아쉽군요.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 말했다. \"내일 우리는 출발하는 두 사람에게 만찬을 베풀 겁니다. 페테르부르크에서 온 디메르바르트냔스키와 우리 베슬롭스키, 그리샤 말입니다. 둘 다 가거든요. 베슬롭스키는 최근에야 결혼했지요. 정말 멋진 친구 아닙니까! 그렇죠, 공작 부인?\" 그가 부인을 향해 돌아서며 말했다.\n\n공작 부인은 아무 대답도 없이 코즈니셰프를 바라보았다. 하지만 세르게이 이바노비치와 공작 부인이 자기를 떼어 놓고 싶어 하는 기색을 보였는데도 스테판 아르카지치는 조금도 당황하지 않았다. 그는 미소를 지으며 공작 부인의 모자에 달린 깃털을 빤히 바라보다가, 무언가를 주워 들기라도 할 듯 주위를 둘러보았다. 모금함을 든 부인 하나가 다가오는 것을 보고는 그녀를 손짓해 불러 5루블짜리 지폐를 넣었다.\n\n\"호주머니에 돈이 있는 한 이런 모금함을 보고 마음이 동하지 않을 수가 없단 말이에요.\" 그가 말했다. \"그건 그렇고 오늘 전보는 어떻습니까? 그 몬테네그로 사람들, 대단한 친구들이지요!\"\n\n\"설마요!\" 그는 브론스키가 이 기차로 떠난다는 공작 부인의 말에 이렇게 외쳤다. 한순간 스테판 아르카지치의 얼굴이 슬퍼 보였지만, 잠시 후 콧수염을 쓰다듬으며 몸을 흔들흔들 걸어 브론스키가 있는 홀로 들어갈 때쯤에는, 누이의 주검 앞에서 절망에 빠져 흐느꼈던 일을 까맣게 잊은 채 브론스키에게서 오직 영웅이자 옛 친구의 모습만을 보고 있었다.\n\n\"여러 결점이 있긴 해도 저 사람의 공정함만은 인정하지 않을 수 없죠.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 자리를 뜨자마자 공작 부인이 세르게이 이바노비치에게 말했다. \"얼마나 전형적인 러시아적, 슬라브적 천성인지요! 다만 브론스키로서는 저 사람을 보는 게 달갑지 않을 듯해 걱정이에요. 뭐라고 하시든, 저는 저 사람의 운명에 마음이 짠해요. 가시는 길에 저 사람과 얘기 좀 나눠 보세요.\" 공작 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"네, 그렇게 될지도 모르겠습니다. 그리되면요.\"\n\n\"저는 그 사람을 한 번도 좋아한 적이 없어요. 하지만 이번 일로 많은 게 용서되는군요. 그저 자기 혼자 가는 게 아니라, 자기 비용으로 기병 중대까지 데려가니까요.\"\n\n\"네, 저도 그렇게 들었습니다.\"\n종이 울렸다. 모두가 출입구로 몰려들었다. \"저기 저 사람이에요!\" 공작 부인이 브론스키를 가리키며 말했다. 그는 긴 외투를 걸치고 챙 넓은 검은 모자를 쓴 채 어머니를 팔로 부축하며 지나가고 있었다. 오블론스키가 그 곁에서 무언가를 열심히 떠들며 걷고 있었다.\n\n브론스키는 미간을 찌푸린 채, 스테판 아르카지치의 말이 들리지도 않는다는 듯 똑바로 앞만 바라보고 있었다.\n\n아마 오블론스키가 그들을 가리켜 보인 모양인지, 그는 공작 부인과 세르게이 이바노비치가 서 있는 쪽으로 고개를 돌리더니, 아무 말 없이 모자를 들어 올렸다. 고통으로 늙고 수척해진 그의 얼굴은 돌처럼 굳어 보였다.\n\n플랫폼으로 나서며 브론스키는 어머니를 두고 어느 객실 안으로 사라졌다.\n\n플랫폼에서 \"하느님, 차르를 구하소서\"라는 노래가 울려 퍼지더니, 이어 \"우라!\"와 \"지비오!\" 하는 외침이 터져 나왔다. 의용병 가운데 한 사람, 가슴이 움푹 꺼진 키 큰 새파란 젊은이가 특히 눈에 띄었는데, 그는 머리 위로 펠트 모자와 꽃다발을 흔들며 연신 고개를 숙이고 있었다. 이어서 장교 둘이 역시 고개를 숙이며 나왔고, 기름때 묻은 군모를 쓴 커다란 턱수염의 뚱뚱한 사내도 모습을 드러냈다.\n\n# Chapter 3\n\n공작 부인과 작별 인사를 나눈 세르게이 이바노비치는 카타바소프와 합류했다. 두 사람은 발 디딜 틈 없이 가득 찬 객차에 함께 올라탔고, 기차가 출발했다.\n\n차리치노 역에서 기차는 \"그대에게 영광을!\"을 부르는 청년 합창단의 환영을 받았다. 의용병들은 또다시 고개를 숙이고 창밖으로 머리를 내밀었지만, 세르게이 이바노비치는 그들에게 아무런 관심도 두지 않았다. 그는 의용병들과 워낙 많이 얽혀 본 터라 그 유형이 이미 익숙했고, 흥미를 느끼지 못했다. 반면 학문 연구 탓에 그들을 관찰할 기회를 미처 갖지 못했던 카타바소프는 그들에게 큰 흥미를 느꼈고, 세르게이 이바노비치에게 이것저것 물었다.\n\n세르게이 이바노비치는 그에게 직접 이등칸으로 가서 그들과 이야기해 보라고 권했다. 다음 정거장에서 카타바소프는 그 제안대로 했다.\n\n첫 정차역에서 그는 이등칸으로 옮겨가 의용병들과 인사를 텄다. 그들은 객차 한구석에 앉아 큰 소리로 떠들고 있었고, 승객들과 자기가 들어서는 카타바소프의 시선이 자기들에게 쏠려 있다는 것을 분명히 의식하고 있었다. 누구보다도 크게 떠드는 사람은 키가 크고 가슴이 움푹 꺼진 청년이었다. 그는 영락없이 술에 취해, 자기 학교에서 있었던 무슨 이야기를 늘어놓고 있었다. 그 맞은편에는 근위대 제복인 오스트리아식 군용 재킷을 입은 중년 장교가 앉아 있었다. 그는 가슴 꺼진 청년의 이야기를 미소를 띤 채 듣다가 이따금 말을 끊었다. 세 번째 사람은 포병 제복을 입고 그들 옆 상자 위에 앉아 있었다. 네 번째 사람은 잠들어 있었다.\n\n청년과 이야기를 나누면서 카타바소프는 그가 스물두 살도 되기 전에 막대한 재산을 탕진해 버린 부유한 모스크바 상인이라는 것을 알게 되었다. 카타바소프는 그가 마음에 들지 않았다. 사내답지 못하고 계집애 같으며 병약했기 때문이다. 그는 분명, 특히 술을 마신 지금은 더더욱, 자기가 영웅적인 행동을 하고 있다고 확신하며 그것을 더없이 불쾌한 방식으로 떠벌리고 있었다.\n\n두 번째 사람, 그 퇴역 장교 역시 카타바소프에게 불쾌한 인상을 주었다. 그는 무슨 일이든 다 해 본 사람인 듯했다. 철도에서 일했고, 영지 관리인 노릇도 했고, 공장을 차린 적도 있었는데, 그는 굳이 그럴 필요도 없는데 자기가 했던 일들을 죄다 늘어놓았고 학술적인 표현을 아주 엉뚱한 자리에 갖다 붙였다.\n\n반면 세 번째 사람, 그 포병은 카타바소프에게 무척 좋은 인상을 남겼다. 그는 조용하고 겸손한 사내로, 장교의 박식함과 상인의 영웅적인 자기희생에 영락없이 감명받은 듯했고, 자기 자신에 대해서는 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 카타바소프가 그에게 무엇 때문에 세르비아로 가게 되었느냐고 물었을 때, 그는 겸손하게 대답했다.\n\n\"아, 뭐, 다들 가니까요. 세르비아 사람들도 도움이 필요하고요. 그들이 딱해서요.\"\n\n\"그렇지요, 당신네 포병이 특히 거긴 부족하니까요.\" 카타바소프가 말했다.\n\n\"아, 저는 포병에 오래 있지 않았어요. 어쩌면 보병이나 기병으로 보낼지도 모르죠.\"\n\n\"포병이 무엇보다도 필요한 마당에 보병으로요?\" 카타바소프가 말했다. 그는 포병의 겉보기 나이로 미루어 그가 꽤 높은 계급에 올라 있으리라 짐작했던 것이다.\n\n\"포병에 오래 있지 않았어요. 사관후보생으로 퇴역했죠.\" 그가 말하더니, 자기가 어떻게 시험에 떨어졌는지 설명하기 시작했다.\n\n이 모든 것이 한데 어우러져 카타바소프에게 못마땅한 인상을 남겼다. 의용병들이 한잔하러 어느 역에 내렸을 때, 카타바소프는 누군가와 이야기를 나누며 자기의 좋지 못한 인상을 견주어 보고 싶었다. 객차 안에는 군용 외투를 걸친 노인이 하나 있었는데, 그는 그동안 내내 카타바소프와 의용병들의 대화를 듣고 있었다. 둘만 남게 되자 카타바소프는 그에게 말을 걸었다.\n\n\"저기로 떠나는 저 친구들, 참 다양한 신분에서 왔군요.\" 카타바소프는 자기 의견을 드러내고 싶지 않으면서도, 동시에 노인의 생각을 알아내고 싶어서 모호하게 말했다.\n\n노인은 두 차례 출정에 참가한 적이 있는 장교였다. 그는 무엇이 군인을 만드는지 알았고, 그 사람들의 겉모습과 말투로 보아, 또 여행길에 술병에 손을 대는 그 허세로 보아, 그들을 형편없는 군인으로 여겼다. 게다가 그는 어느 군청 소재지에 살고 있었는데, 자기 고장에서 의용병으로 자원한 어떤 병사 이야기를 들려주고 싶어 입이 근질거렸다. 그자는 술꾼에 도둑이라 아무도 일꾼으로 쓰려 하지 않던 자였다. 하지만 지금 같은 여론의 분위기에서는 일반적인 의견에 반하는 견해를 입에 올리는 것이, 특히 의용병들을 나쁘게 비판하는 것이 위험하다는 것을 경험으로 알고 있었던 터라, 그 역시 자기 속내를 드러내지 않은 채 카타바소프를 살폈다.\n\n\"뭐, 저기엔 사람이 필요하니까요.\" 그가 눈으로 웃으며 말했다. 그러고서 둘은 최근의 전쟁 소식 이야기로 넘어갔는데, 최근 소식에 따르면 터키군이 모든 전선에서 패한 까닭에, 이튿날 예정된 교전을 두고 품은 저마다의 의혹을 서로에게 감추었다. 그렇게 두 사람은 어느 쪽도 제 의견을 드러내지 않은 채 헤어졌다.\n\n카타바소프는 자기 객차로 돌아와, 마지못해 위선을 부리며 의용병들을 관찰한 바를 세르게이 이바노비치에게 보고했는데, 그 말대로라면 그들은 훌륭한 친구들인 듯했다.\n\n어느 도시의 큰 역에서 의용병들은 또다시 함성과 노래로 환영받았고, 또다시 모금함을 든 남녀가 나타났으며, 지방 부인들이 의용병들에게 꽃다발을 건네고 그들을 따라 식당칸까지 따라 들어왔다. 하지만 이 모든 것은 모스크바에서보다는 훨씬 작고 미약한 규모였다.\n\n# Chapter 4\n\n기차가 그 지방 도시에 정차해 있는 동안, 세르게이 이바노비치는 식당칸으로 가지 않고 플랫폼을 이리저리 거닐었다.\n\n브론스키의 객실 앞을 처음 지나칠 때 그는 창문에 커튼이 드리워져 있는 것을 알아챘다. 하지만 두 번째로 그 앞을 지날 때, 그는 창가에 앉은 노백작 부인을 보았다. 부인이 코즈니셰프에게 손짓했다.\n\n\"보다시피 나도 가는 길이에요, 쿠르스크까지 저 애를 바래다주려고요.\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"네, 그렇게 들었습니다.\" 세르게이 이바노비치가 그녀의 창가에 서서 안을 들여다보며 말했다. \"참으로 고결한 일이지요!\" 그는 객실 안에 브론스키가 없는 것을 알아채고는 덧붙였다.\n\n\"그래요, 그런 불행을 겪었으니, 저 애가 달리 뭘 할 수 있었겠어요?\"\n\n\"정말 끔찍한 일이었습니다!\" 세르게이 이바노비치가 말했다.\n\n\"아, 내가 무슨 일을 겪었는지! 그러지 말고 들어와요…… 아, 내가 무슨 일을 겪었는지!\" 세르게이 이바노비치가 객실 안으로 들어와 그녀 곁에 앉자 그녀가 거듭 말했다. \"당신은 상상도 못 할 거예요! 그 애는 여섯 주 동안 누구와도 말을 하지 않았고, 내가 사정사정할 때를 빼고는 음식에 손도 대지 않았어요. 단 한 순간도 그 애를 혼자 둘 수가 없었지요. 우리는 그 애가 제 몸에 해를 입힐 만한 것들은 모조리 치워 버렸어요. 우리는 일 층에서 지냈는데, 그래도 아무것도 장담할 수가 없었어요. 물론 당신도 알겠지만, 그 애는 그 여자 때문에 이미 한 번 스스로 총을 쏜 적이 있었어요.\" 그녀가 말했고, 그 일을 떠올리자 노부인의 속눈썹이 파르르 떨렸다. \"그래요, 그런 여자에게는 그게 어울리는 최후였어요. 그 여자가 택한 죽음마저도 천하고 비속했지요.\"\n\n\"우리가 판단할 일은 아니지요, 백작 부인.\" 세르게이 이바노비치가 말했다. \"하지만 부인께서 무척 힘드셨으리라는 건 이해할 수 있습니다.\"\n\n\"아, 그 얘긴 하지 말아요! 나는 영지에 머물고 있었고, 그 애도 나와 함께 있었어요. 그 애한테 쪽지 하나가 전해졌지요. 그 애는 답장을 써서 보냈어요. 우리는 그 여자가 바로 근처 역에 와 있는 줄은 꿈에도 몰랐어요. 저녁 무렵, 막 내 방으로 돌아갔을 때, 우리 마리가 어떤 부인이 기차에 몸을 던졌다고 알려 주더군요. 순간 무언가가 나를 내리치는 것 같았어요. 나는 그게 그 여자라는 걸 알았지요. 내가 맨 처음 한 말은, 저 애한테는 알리지 말라는 거였어요. 하지만 사람들은 이미 그 애한테 말해 버린 뒤였어요. 그 애의 마부가 거기 있었고, 모든 걸 보았던 거예요. 내가 그 애 방으로 뛰어들었을 때, 그 애는 제정신이 아니었어요. 보기에도 무서울 지경이었지요. 그 애는 한마디도 하지 않은 채 그곳으로 말을 몰아 달려가 버렸어요. 거기서 무슨 일이 있었는지는 지금까지도 모르지만, 그 애는 사경을 헤매는 상태로 실려 왔어요. 나는 그 애를 알아보지도 못했을 거예요. Prostration complète, 라고 의사가 말했어요. 그러고는 거의 광기에 가까운 상태가 뒤따랐지요. 아, 그 얘긴 해서 뭘 하겠어요!\" 백작 부인이 손을 내저으며 말했다. \"끔찍한 시간이었어요! 아니, 당신이 뭐라 하든, 그 여자는 나쁜 여자였어요. 대체 그런 절망적인 격정이 무슨 의미가 있단 말인가요? 모두 자기가 무언가 남다른 사람이라는 걸 과시하려던 거였어요. 그래요, 그것만은 해냈지요. 그 여자는 자기 자신을 파멸로 몰아넣었고, 두 명의 선량한 남자, 그러니까 제 남편과 내 불행한 아들까지도 파멸시켰어요.\"\n* Prostration complète: 프랑스어로 '완전한 쇠약(허탈)'.\n\n\"그러면 그 여자의 남편은 어떻게 했습니까?\" 세르게이 이바노비치가 물었다.\n\n\"그 사람이 딸을 데려갔어요. 알렉세이는 처음엔 무엇이든 받아들일 마음이었지요. 지금은 제 자식을 남에게 넘겨 버렸다는 게 그 애를 몹시 괴롭히고 있어요. 하지만 한번 한 말을 거둘 수는 없는 노릇이지요. 카레닌은 장례식에 왔어요. 하지만 우리는 그가 알렉세이와 마주치지 않도록 막으려 애썼어요. 그에게는, 그 여자의 남편에게는, 어쨌든 그게 더 견디기 쉬운 일이었지요. 그 여자가 그를 자유롭게 풀어 준 셈이니까요. 하지만 내 가엾은 아들은 그 여자에게 완전히 자신을 내맡겼어요. 그 애는 모든 걸 내던졌지요, 경력도, 나도. 그런데도 그 여자는 그 애를 가엾게 여기기는커녕, 작정한 듯 그 애를 완전히 파멸시켜 버렸어요. 아니, 당신이 뭐라 하든, 그 여자의 죽음 자체가 비열한 여자의, 신앙심이라곤 없는 여자의 죽음이었어요. 하느님, 용서하소서. 하지만 내 아들의 비참한 꼴을 보고 있으면, 나는 그 여자에 대한 기억을 미워하지 않을 수가 없어요!\"\n\n\"그런데 지금 그 애는 어떻습니까?\"\n\n\"우리에게는 하늘이 내려 준 축복이었어요, 이 세르비아 전쟁 말이에요. 나는 늙어서 그게 옳은지 그른지는 모르지만, 그 애한테는 신의 섭리가 내린 축복처럼 찾아왔어요. 물론 그 애의 어미인 나로서는 끔찍한 일이지요. 게다가 더 나쁜 건, 사람들 말로는, ce n'est pas très bien vu à Pétersbourg 하다는 거예요. 하지만 어쩔 도리가 없어요! 그 애를 다시 일으켜 세울 수 있는 유일한 길이었으니까요. 야시빈이라고, 그 애 친구가 있는데, 카드로 가진 걸 모조리 잃고 세르비아로 가려던 참이었어요. 그가 그 애를 찾아와 함께 가자고 설득했지요. 이제 그 애한테는 그것이 하나의 관심거리가 되었어요. 제발 그 애와 잠깐 이야기를 나눠 줘요. 나는 그 애의 마음을 다른 데로 돌려 주고 싶어요. 그 애는 너무나 의기소침해 있어요. 게다가 운 나쁘게도 치통까지 앓고 있지요. 하지만 당신을 보면 기뻐할 거예요. 부디 그 애와 이야기를 나눠 줘요. 그 애는 저쪽에서 이리저리 거닐고 있어요.\"\n* ce n'est pas très bien vu à Pétersbourg: 프랑스어로 '페테르부르크에서는 그다지 좋게 보지 않는다'.\n\n세르게이 이바노비치는 기꺼이 그러겠노라고 말하고는 역의 반대편으로 건너갔다.\n\n# Chapter 5\n\n플랫폼에 쌓아 올린 짐이 저녁 비스듬한 그림자를 드리운 가운데, 긴 외투에 챙 늘어진 모자를 쓴 브론스키가 두 손을 주머니에 찌른 채, 우리에 갇힌 야수처럼 스무 걸음마다 홱 돌아서며 오락가락 거닐고 있었다. 세르게이 이바노비치는 그에게 다가가면서, 브론스키가 자기를 보았으면서도 못 본 척하고 있다고 느꼈다. 하지만 그런 것은 세르게이 이바노비치에게 조금도 영향을 주지 않았다. 브론스키와 관련해서 그는 그 모든 개인적인 고려를 초월해 있었다.\n\n그 순간 세르게이 이바노비치는 브론스키를 위대한 대의에서 중요한 역할을 맡은 인물로 바라보았고, 코즈니셰프는 그를 격려하고 자기의 찬동을 표하는 것을 자기 의무라고 여겼다. 그는 브론스키에게 다가갔다.\n\n브론스키는 걸음을 멈추고 그를 뚫어지게 바라보다가, 그를 알아보고는 몇 걸음 앞으로 나아가 그를 맞이하며 아주 따뜻하게 악수를 나누었다.\n\n\"아마 저를 만나고 싶지 않으셨겠지요.\" 세르게이 이바노비치가 말했다. \"하지만 제가 뭔가 도움이 될 수는 없을까요?\"\n\n\"제가 만나기를 가장 덜 꺼리는 사람이 있다면 바로 당신입니다.\" 브론스키가 말했다. \"용서하십시오. 그리고 제게는 인생에서 좋아할 만한 게 아무것도 없습니다.\"\n\n\"잘 알겠습니다. 저는 그저 제 도움을 드리고 싶었을 뿐입니다.\" 세르게이 이바노비치가, 틀림없는 고통으로 가득 찬 브론스키의 얼굴을 살피며 말했다. \"리스티치에게—밀란에게 보내는 소개장이 있으면 도움이 되지 않겠습니까?\"\n\n\"아, 아닙니다!\" 브론스키가 그의 말을 가까스로 이해하는 듯한 표정으로 말했다. \"괜찮으시다면, 계속 걸읍시다. 객차 사이는 너무 답답하군요. 소개장이라니요? 아니요, 고맙습니다. 죽음을 맞이하는 데에는 소개장 따위 필요 없습니다. 터키군에게도 마찬가지고요…….\" 그가 입술만으로 짓는 미소를 띠며 말했다. 그의 눈은 여전히 성난 고통의 빛을 간직하고 있었다.\n\n\"그렇지요. 하지만 어쨌든 꼭 필요한 관계를, 당신을 만나려 하는 누군가와 맺는 데에 좀 더 수월할 수도 있습니다. 하지만 그건 당신 뜻대로 하시지요. 저는 당신의 의도를 듣고 무척 기뻤습니다. 의용병들에 대한 비난이 그토록 많았는데, 당신 같은 사람이 그들의 평판을 세상의 평가에서 끌어올려 주니까요.\"\n\n\"한 인간으로서 제 쓸모는,\" 브론스키가 말했다. \"제게 삶이 아무런 가치도 없다는 데에 있지요. 그리고 제게는 그들의 대열을 헤치고 들어가 그들을 짓밟거나 아니면 쓰러질 만큼의 육체적 기력이 있다는 것—그건 저도 압니다. 제 목숨을 바칠 무언가가 있다는 게 기쁩니다. 제 목숨은 그저 쓸모없을 뿐 아니라 혐오스러우니까요. 누구든 가져가도 좋습니다.\" 그러고는 끊임없이 갉아 대는 치통 탓에, 그것이 자연스러운 표정으로 말하는 것조차 가로막아, 그의 턱이 안달하듯 씰룩거렸다.\n\n\"당신은 다른 사람이 될 겁니다, 제가 장담하지요.\" 세르게이 이바노비치가 감동을 느끼며 말했다. \"형제들을 속박에서 해방시키는 것은 죽음도 삶도 바칠 만한 목적입니다. 신께서 당신에게 겉으로는 성공을, 안으로는 평화를 내려 주시기를.\" 그가 이렇게 덧붙이고는 손을 내밀었다. 브론스키는 그가 내민 손을 따뜻하게 잡았다.\n\n\"그렇지요, 하나의 무기로서라면 저도 얼마간 쓸모가 있을지 모릅니다. 하지만 한 인간으로서 저는 폐인입니다.\" 그가 툭 내뱉었다.\n\n입 안의 상아 줄 같은 튼튼한 이가 욱신거리는 통증 때문에 그는 거의 말을 잇지 못했다. 그는 입을 다물었고, 그의 눈은 레일 위를 느리고 매끄럽게 굴러가는 탄수차의 바퀴에 머물렀다.\n\n그런데 별안간 다른 종류의 아픔이, 욱신거리는 통증이 아니라 그의 온 존재를 고뇌에 빠뜨리는 내적인 괴로움이, 한순간 그로 하여금 치통을 잊게 만들었다. 탄수차와 레일을 바라보면서, 불행을 겪은 이후 처음 만난 친구와 나눈 대화의 영향 아래, 그는 문득 그녀를 떠올렸다—그러니까 그가 넋이 나간 사람처럼 철도역의 화물 보관실로 뛰어들었을 때 그녀에게 남아 있던 것을—탁자 위에, 낯선 사람들 틈에서 부끄러운 줄도 모르고 아무렇게나 널브러진 채, 그토록 얼마 전까지만 해도 생명으로 가득했던 피투성이 몸뚱이를. 상하지 않은 머리는 풍성한 머리채의 무게에 짓눌려 뒤로 떨어져 있었고, 관자놀이 주위에는 곱슬곱슬한 머리 타래가 늘어져 있었으며, 아름다운 얼굴에는 붉은, 반쯤 벌어진 입과, 입술에는 애처롭고 여전히 뜬 두 눈에는 무시무시한, 그 기묘하고 굳어 버린 표정이 있었는데, 그 표정은 그들이 다투던 때 그녀가 했던 그 끔찍한 말—그가 이것을 후회하게 될 거라던 그 말을 내뱉는 듯했다.\n\n그리고 그는 그녀가 처음 만났을 때의 모습, 그때도 철도역에서였던, 신비롭고 아름답고 사랑스러우며, 행복을 찾고 또 주려 했던 모습으로, 그가 마지막 순간에 기억하던 잔혹하게 복수심에 불타던 모습이 아닌 그런 모습으로 그녀를 떠올리려 애썼다. 그는 그녀와 함께한 가장 좋았던 순간들을 떠올리려 했지만, 그 순간들은 영원히 더럽혀져 버렸다. 그는 오로지 그녀가 의기양양하게, 결코 지워질 수 없을 전혀 쓸모없는 회한이라는 그 협박을 성공시킨 모습으로밖에 떠올릴 수 없었다. 그는 치통의 의식을 완전히 잃었고, 그의 얼굴은 흐느낌으로 일그러졌다.\n\n말없이 짐 옆을 두 번 오르락내리락한 뒤 그는 평정을 되찾고, 세르게이 이바노비치에게 침착하게 말을 건넸다.\n\n\"어제 이후로 전보는 받지 못하셨습니까? 그렇지요, 세 번째로 격퇴당했지만, 내일은 결정적인 교전이 예상된다고 하더군요.\"\n\n그리고 밀란 왕의 포고문과 그것이 미칠 수도 있는 엄청난 영향에 대해 좀 더 이야기를 나눈 뒤, 두 번째 종소리를 듣고 두 사람은 각자의 객차로 향하며 헤어졌다.\n\n# Chapter 6\n\n세르게이 이바노비치는 자기가 언제 모스크바를 떠날 수 있을지 알 수 없었기에, 마중을 나오라고 동생에게 전보를 치지 않았다. 카타바소프와 세르게이 이바노비치가 역에서 빌린 마차를 타고 길 먼지를 뒤집어써 무어인처럼 까매진 채 포크로프스코예 저택의 현관 앞에 다다랐을 때, 레빈은 집에 없었다. 발코니에서 아버지와 언니와 함께 앉아 있던 키티는 형부를 알아보고 마중하러 뛰어 내려갔다.\n\n\"기별도 안 주시다니, 너무하세요.\" 그녀가 세르게이 이바노비치에게 손을 내밀고 입을 맞추도록 이마를 들이밀며 말했다.\n\n\"썩 잘 달려와서, 자네들을 번거롭게 하지도 않았는걸.\" 세르게이 이바노비치가 대답했다. \"이렇게 지저분해서 손도 못 대겠구나. 너무 바빠서 언제 빠져나올 수 있을지 가늠이 안 됐지. 그래, 너는 여전히 그 평온하고 조용한 행복을 누리고 있구나.\" 그가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"이 잔잔한 후미에서 세상의 물살이 닿지 않는 곳에 말이야. 여기 우리 친구 표도르 바실리예비치도 마침내 여기까지 오는 데 성공했단다.\"\n\n\"하지만 저는 흑인이 아니라서, 씻고 나면 사람 꼴이 될 겁니다.\" 카타바소프가 농을 던지듯 말하며 악수를 청하고 미소를 지었는데, 까만 얼굴 속에서 이가 하얗게 반짝였다.\n\n\"코스챠가 아주 기뻐할 거예요. 농장에 가 있는데, 이제 돌아올 때가 됐어요.\"\n\n\"여전히 농사일로 바쁘시군요. 정말이지 잔잔한 후미야.\" 카타바소프가 말했다. \"우리는 도시에서 세르비아 전쟁 말고는 아무 생각도 없는데. 그래, 우리 친구는 그 일을 어떻게 보고 있습니까? 분명 남들과는 다르게 생각하겠지요.\"\n\n\"아, 글쎄요, 다들 생각하는 거랑 비슷하지 않을까요.\" 키티가 세르게이 이바노비치를 흘끔 돌아보며 약간 당황한 듯 대답했다. \"사람을 보내서 데려오라고 할게요. 아빠도 우리와 함께 계세요. 외국에서 막 돌아오셨거든요.\"\n\n그러고 나서 키티는 레빈을 부르러 사람을 보내고, 손님 한 사람은 자기 방에서, 다른 한 사람은 돌리가 쓰던 방에서 씻도록 채비를 시키고, 점심 식사를 차리라고 지시한 뒤, 임신 기간 내내 빼앗겼던 자유로움과 민첩한 움직임을 만끽하며 발코니로 뛰어나갔다.\n\n\"세르게이 이바노비치하고, 교수인 카타바소프 씨예요.\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"아, 이 더위에 손님이라니 성가시구나.\" 공작이 말했다.\n\n\"아니에요, 아빠, 그분은 정말 좋은 분이고, 코스챠도 무척 좋아해요.\" 키티가 아버지 얼굴에 떠오른 빈정거림을 알아채고는 달래는 듯한 미소를 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 난 아무 말도 안 했다.\"\n\n\"언니가 가서 좀 상대해 줘요.\" 키티가 언니에게 말했다. \"두 분이 역에서 스티바를 봤대요. 아주 건강하더래요. 난 미챠한테 가 봐야겠어요. 하필이면 차 마신 뒤로 아직 젖을 못 먹였거든요. 지금쯤 깨어서 분명 울고 있을 거예요.\" 그러면서 젖이 도는 것을 느끼고는 서둘러 아기방으로 향했다.\n\n이것은 그저 짐작이 아니었다. 아이와의 연결이 아직 너무도 긴밀해서, 그녀는 젖이 도는 것으로 아이의 식욕을 가늠할 수 있었고, 아이가 배고프다는 것을 확실히 알았다.\n\n그녀는 아기방에 닿기도 전에 아이가 울고 있다는 것을 알았다. 그리고 정말로 아이는 울고 있었다. 울음소리를 듣고 그녀는 걸음을 재촉했다. 하지만 빨리 갈수록 아이는 더 크게 울어 댔다. 배고프고 보채는, 우렁차고 건강한 울음소리였다.\n\n\"오래 울었어요, 유모, 아주 오래?\" 키티가 의자에 앉아 아기에게 젖을 물릴 채비를 하며 다급하게 말했다. \"어서 이리 줘요. 아, 유모, 어쩜 이렇게 굼떠요! 자, 모자는 나중에 매 줘요, 어서!\"\n\n아기의 게걸스러운 울음소리가 흐느낌으로 잦아들고 있었다.\n\n\"하지만 그렇게 하시면 안 돼요, 마님.\" 거의 언제나 아기방에 붙어 있는 아가피야 미하일로브나가 말했다. \"똑바로 안아 줘야지요. 아우, 아우!\" 그녀는 어미는 안중에도 없이 아기를 어르며 노래하듯 흥얼거렸다.\n\n유모가 아기를 어미에게 데려왔다. 아가피야 미하일로브나가 다정함에 녹아내리는 얼굴로 그 뒤를 따랐다.\n\n\"이 애가 저를 알아봐요, 알아본다니까요. 하느님께 맹세코, 카체리나 알렉산드로브나 마님, 저를 알아봤어요!\" 아가피야 미하일로브나가 아기의 울음소리 위로 외쳤다.\n\n하지만 키티는 그 말을 듣지 못했다. 아기와 마찬가지로 그녀의 조바심도 점점 커져만 갔다.\n\n두 사람의 조바심이 한동안 일을 더디게 했다. 아기는 젖을 제대로 물지 못해 잔뜩 약이 올랐다.\n\n마침내 절망에 빠진 듯 숨넘어가게 울고 헛되이 빨아 대고 한 끝에, 일이 제대로 풀렸고, 어미와 아이는 동시에 안도감을 느끼며 둘 다 평온 속으로 가라앉았다.\n\n\"아유, 가엾은 것, 땀범벅이 됐네!\" 키티가 아기를 만지며 속삭이듯 말했다.\n\n\"무얼 보고 이 애가 자기를 알아본다고 하는 거예요?\" 그녀는 모자 밑에서, 자기 생각에는, 장난스레 빠끔 내다보는 아기의 눈과 율동적으로 부풀어 오르는 볼, 그리고 손바닥이 발그레한 작은 손을 흔들어 대는 모습을 곁눈질하며 덧붙였다.\n\n\"말도 안 돼요! 누구든 알아본다면, 나를 알아봤겠지요.\" 키티가 아가피야 미하일로브나의 말에 대꾸하며 미소를 지었다.\n\n그녀가 미소 지은 것은, 아이가 자기를 알아볼 리 없다고 말은 했지만, 마음속으로는 아이가 아가피야 미하일로브나뿐 아니라 모든 것을 알고 이해하고 있으며, 다른 누구도 모르는 많은 것까지 알고 이해하고 있다고, 그리고 어미인 자기 자신이 오직 이 아이를 통해서만 배우고 깨닫게 된 그 많은 것까지도 안다고 확신하고 있었기 때문이다. 아가피야 미하일로브나에게도, 유모에게도, 외할아버지에게도, 심지어 아버지에게조차 미챠는 그저 물질적인 보살핌만 필요로 하는 살아 있는 존재였지만, 어미에게 미챠는 이미 오래전부터 도덕적 존재였고, 둘 사이에는 이미 영적인 관계가 한 묶음이나 자리 잡고 있었다.\n\n\"하느님 덕분에 깨어나면, 직접 보시게 될 거예요. 그때 제가 이렇게 해 주면, 글쎄, 그냥 저를 보고 환하게 웃는다니까요, 우리 귀염둥이가! 그저 화창한 날처럼 환하게 빛나지요!\" 아가피야 미하일로브나가 말했다.\n\n\"그래요, 그래요, 그럼 두고 보지요.\" 키티가 속삭였다. \"하지만 이제 그만 나가 봐요, 이 애가 잠들려고 하니까.\"\n\n# Chapter 7\n\n아가피야 미하일로브나는 발끝으로 살금살금 걸어 나갔다. 유모는 블라인드를 내리고, 요람의 모슬린 천장 밑에서 파리 한 마리를 쫓아내고, 창틀에서 버둥대는 호박벌 한 마리를 내쫓고는, 시든 자작나무 가지 하나를 어머니와 아기 위로 흔들며 앉았다.\n\n\"어쩌면 이렇게 덥담! 하느님이 비라도 한 줄기 내려 주시면 좋으련만.\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"네, 네, 쉿—쉿—쉿——\" 키티는 그렇게만 대답하며 살짝 몸을 흔들었고, 미챠가 눈을 떴다 감았다 하며 여전히 힘없이 흔들어 대는, 손목에 살이 접혀 통통한 그 작은 팔을 다정하게 꼭 쥐었다. 그 손이 키티의 마음을 흔들었다. 그 작은 손에 입을 맞추고 싶었지만, 아기가 깰까 봐 두려워 차마 그러지 못했다. 마침내 작은 손이 흔들기를 멈추었고, 눈이 감겼다. 다만 이따금 젖을 빨면서 아기는 길고 곱슬곱슬한 속눈썹을 들어, 어스름 속에서 까맣게 보이는 젖은 눈으로 어머니를 빠끔히 올려다보았다. 유모는 부채질을 멈추고 꾸벅꾸벅 졸고 있었다. 위층에서는 늙은 공작의 쩌렁쩌렁한 목소리와 카타바소프의 킬킬대는 웃음소리가 들려왔다.\n\n'나를 빼놓고 이야기에 빠졌구나.' 키티는 생각했다. '하지만 코스챠가 자리에 없는 건 그래도 속상해. 분명 또 양봉장에 갔을 거야. 거기에 너무 자주 가는 게 안타깝긴 해도, 그래도 난 기뻐. 그이의 마음을 다른 데로 돌려 주니까. 봄에 비하면 이제 훨씬 행복해지고 좋아졌어. 예전엔 어찌나 침울하고 안절부절못하던지 내가 다 겁이 날 정도였지. 그런데 어쩜 그렇게 우스운 사람일까!' 그녀는 미소를 지으며 속삭였다.\n\n그녀는 남편을 괴롭히는 게 무엇인지 알고 있었다. 그것은 그의 불신앙이었다. 만약 누군가 그녀에게, 남편이 믿지 않으니 내세에서 그가 저주받으리라고 생각하느냐고 물었다면, 그녀는 그가 저주받을 거라고 인정할 수밖에 없었을 테지만, 그래도 그의 불신앙이 그녀를 불행하게 만들지는 않았다. 그리고 그녀는, 믿지 않는 자에게는 구원이 있을 수 없다고 고백하면서도, 세상 그 무엇보다 남편의 영혼을 사랑하면서도, 그의 불신앙을 떠올리면 미소가 지어졌고, 그이는 참 우스운 사람이라고 혼자 되뇌었다.\n\n'대체 무엇 때문에 그이는 올 한 해 내내 무슨 철학 같은 걸 그렇게 읽어 대는 걸까?' 그녀는 궁금했다. '만약 그게 다 그 책들에 쓰여 있다면, 그이는 그걸 이해할 수 있을 거야. 만약 그게 다 틀린 거라면, 왜 읽는 걸까? 자기 입으로도 믿고 싶다고 하잖아. 그런데 왜 믿지 못하는 걸까? 분명 너무 많이 생각해서 그런 거야. 그리고 너무 외로워서 그렇게 많이 생각하는 거지. 늘 혼자, 혼자야. 그런 걸 우리한테 다 털어놓을 수도 없고. 이번 손님들, 특히 카타바소프가 와서 그이도 반가워할 것 같아. 그 사람들과 토론하는 걸 좋아하니까.' 그녀는 그렇게 생각하다가, 곧바로 카타바소프를 어디에 묵게 하는 게 더 편할지, 혼자 자게 할지 아니면 세르게이 이바노비치의 방을 같이 쓰게 할지를 궁리하기 시작했다. 그러다 문득 한 가지 생각이 떠올라 그녀는 몸서리쳤고, 그 바람에 미챠까지 움찔하게 만들어 아기가 그녀를 엄하게 흘끗 쳐다보았다. '세탁부가 아직 빨래를 보내지 않은 게 분명한데, 제일 좋은 시트는 죄다 쓰고 있잖아. 내가 챙기지 않으면 아가피야 미하일로브나가 세르게이 이바노비치한테 엉뚱한 시트를 내줄 거야.' 이 생각만으로도 키티의 얼굴로 피가 확 몰렸다.\n\n'그래, 내가 처리해야지.' 그녀는 마음을 정하고는, 다시 아까의 생각으로 돌아가, 무언가 중요한 영적인 문제가 도중에 끊겼다는 것을 떠올리며, 그게 무엇이었는지 기억해 내려 애썼다. '그래, 코스챠, 믿지 않는 사람.' 그녀는 다시 미소 지으며 생각했다.\n\n'뭐, 믿지 않는 사람이면 또 어때! 슈탈 부인처럼 되거나, 그 시절 외국에서 내가 되어 보려고 했던 그런 모습이 되느니, 차라리 늘 그런 사람으로 있는 게 나아. 아니, 그이는 무엇 하나 거짓으로 꾸미는 법이 없을 거야.'\n\n그러자 최근에 있었던 그의 선량함의 한 사례가 그녀의 마음속에 생생히 떠올랐다. 두 주 전에 스테판 아르카지치에게서 돌리에게 뉘우치는 편지가 한 통 왔다. 그는 자기 명예를 지켜 달라고, 빚을 갚을 수 있도록 그녀의 영지를 팔아 달라고 간청했다. 돌리는 절망에 빠져, 남편을 미워하고, 경멸하고, 가엾게 여기고, 헤어지기로 결심했다가, 거절하기로 마음먹었다가, 결국에는 재산의 일부를 팔기로 동의하고 말았다. 그 일이 있은 뒤, 키티는 억누를 수 없는 다정한 미소를 지으며, 남편의 부끄러운 듯 쩔쩔매던 모습을, 그 이야기를 꺼내려고 거듭 어색하게 애쓰던 모습을, 그리고 마침내 돌리의 자존심을 다치게 하지 않으면서 그녀를 도울 유일한 방법을 생각해 낸 끝에, 그녀가—전에는 미처 생각지도 못했던—자기 몫의 재산을 포기하면 어떻겠느냐고 제안하던 그 모습을 떠올렸다.\n\n'그런 사람이 믿지 않는 사람이라고! 그 마음씨로, 누구 하나, 심지어 아이 하나라도 상하게 할까 봐 그토록 두려워하는 사람이! 모든 게 남을 위한 것이고, 자기를 위한 건 하나도 없어. 세르게이 이바노비치는 그저 코스챠가 자기 살림을 관리해 주는 게 당연한 의무라고 여기지. 누이도 마찬가지고. 이제 돌리와 그 아이들도 그이의 보살핌 아래 있어. 날마다 그이를 찾아오는 이 모든 농부들도, 마치 그이가 자기들 시중을 들어야 마땅하다는 듯이 굴잖아.'\n\n\"그래, 그저 네 아빠처럼만, 꼭 그이처럼만 되어 다오.\" 그녀는 미챠를 유모에게 건네주며, 아기의 뺨에 입술을 갖다 대며 말했다.\n\n# Chapter 8\n\n사랑하는 형의 임종 자리에서, 그가 새로운 신념이라고 부르는 것에 비추어 처음으로 삶과 죽음의 문제를 들여다본 이래로—스무 살에서 서른네 살에 이르는 동안 어느새 그의 어린 시절과 젊은 날의 믿음을 대신해 버린 그 신념에 비추어—레빈은 공포에 사로잡혀 있었다. 그것은 죽음에 대한 공포라기보다는, 그것이 어디서 와서 무엇 때문에 어떻게 존재하며 또 무엇인지 전혀 알지 못한 채로 살아가는 삶 자체에 대한 공포였다. 육체의 조직, 그 부패, 물질의 불멸성, 에너지 보존의 법칙, 진화—이런 말들이 그의 옛 믿음의 자리를 빼앗아 버렸다. 이런 말들과 거기에 결부된 관념들은 지적인 목적에는 더없이 훌륭했다. 그러나 삶에는 아무것도 가져다주지 못했고, 레빈은 문득 따뜻한 모피 외투를 모슬린 옷으로 바꿔 입고서 처음으로 추위 속에 나선 사람처럼 느꼈다. 그런 사람은 이성이 아니라 자기 존재 전체로, 자기가 벌거벗은 거나 다름없으며 비참하게 죽고 말 수밖에 없다는 것을 곧장 확신하게 되는 것이다.\n\n그 순간 이후로, 비록 또렷하게 직시하지는 않았고 여전히 예전처럼 살아가기는 했지만, 레빈은 자기가 아무것도 알지 못한다는 데서 오는 이 공포의 감각을 단 한 번도 잃은 적이 없었다.\n\n그는 또한 자기가 새로운 신념이라고 부르는 것이 단순히 무지에 그치는 것이 아니라, 자기에게 필요한 그 어떤 앎도 가능하지 않은 어떤 관념의 전체 질서의 일부라는 것을 어렴풋이 느꼈다.\n\n처음에는 결혼과, 거기에 따라붙은 새로운 기쁨과 의무가 이런 생각들을 완전히 밀어냈다. 그러나 최근 들어, 아내의 출산 후 모스크바에 머물며 할 일 없이 지내는 동안, 해결을 부르짖는 그 물음이 점점 더 자주, 점점 더 집요하게 레빈의 마음을 사로잡았다.\n\n그 물음은 그에게 이렇게 요약되었다. \"내가 그리스도교가 내 삶의 문제들에 내놓는 답들을 받아들이지 않는다면, 나는 어떤 답을 받아들이는 것인가?\" 그리고 그의 신념이라는 무기고 전체를 통틀어, 만족스러운 답을 찾기는커녕 답 비슷한 것조차 도무지 찾아낼 수가 없었다.\n\n그는 마치 장난감 가게와 연장 가게에서 먹을 것을 찾는 사람과 같은 처지였다.\n\n본능적으로, 무의식적으로, 모든 책에서, 모든 대화에서, 마주치는 모든 사람에게서, 그는 이 물음들과 그 해답에 빛을 던져 줄 무언가를 찾고 있었다.\n\n무엇보다도 그를 당혹스럽게 하고 어지럽게 한 것은, 자기 또래에 자기와 같은 부류에 속한 사람들 대다수가 자기와 마찬가지로 옛 믿음을 똑같은 새 신념으로 바꿔 버렸으면서도, 거기서 한탄할 만한 것을 전혀 보지 못한 채 더없이 만족스럽고 평온하게 지낸다는 사실이었다. 그래서 그 근본적인 물음과는 별개로, 레빈은 다른 물음들로도 괴로워했다. 이 사람들은 진실한 것일까? 그는 스스로에게 물었다. 아니면 그저 연기를 하고 있는 것일까? 아니면 그들은 과학이 이 문제들에 내놓는 답을 그와는 다르게, 더 명료한 의미로 이해하고 있는 것일까? 그래서 그는 이 사람들의 견해도, 이런 과학적 설명을 다룬 책들도 부지런히 연구했다.\n\n이 물음들이 그의 마음을 사로잡은 이래로 그가 알아낸 한 가지 사실은, 대학 시절 젊은 날의 친구들에 대한 기억으로 미루어 종교는 이미 그 수명을 다했으며 이제 사실상 존재하지 않는다고 짐작한 것이 완전히 잘못이었다는 점이다. 자기와 가장 가까우면서 삶에서 선량한 사람들은 모두 신자였다. 늙은 공작도, 그가 무척 좋아하는 리보프도, 세르게이 이바노비치도, 모든 여자들도 믿었고, 그의 아내도 그가 아주 어린 시절에 믿었던 것만큼이나 단순하게 믿었으며, 러시아 민중의 백 가운데 아흔아홉이, 그가 그 삶에 가장 깊은 존경을 느끼는 모든 노동하는 사람들이 믿었다.\n\n많은 과학 책을 읽은 뒤 그가 확신하게 된 또 하나의 사실은, 자기와 같은 견해를 가진 사람들이 그 견해 위에 다른 어떤 구조도 세우지 못하며, 답 없이는 살아갈 수 없다고 그가 느끼는 물음들에 대해 아무런 설명도 내놓지 못하고, 그저 그 물음들의 존재를 무시한 채 그에게는 도무지 흥미로울 수 없는 다른 물음들, 이를테면 유기체의 진화라든가 의식에 관한 유물론적 이론 따위를 설명하려 든다는 점이었다.\n\n게다가 아내의 출산 동안 그에게는 무언가 예사롭지 않게 여겨지는 일이 일어났다. 그는, 믿지 않는 그가, 어느새 기도를 하게 되었고, 기도하는 그 순간에는 믿었던 것이다. 그러나 그 순간은 지나가 버렸고, 그는 그 순간의 자기 마음 상태를 나머지 삶에 들어맞게 할 수가 없었다.\n\n그 순간 자기가 진리를 알았고 지금은 틀렸다고 인정할 수는 없었다. 그것에 대해 차분히 생각하기 시작하자마자 모든 것이 산산조각 나 버렸기 때문이다. 그렇다고 그때 자기가 잘못 생각한 것이라고 인정할 수도 없었다. 그때의 영적 상태가 그에게는 소중했고, 그것이 나약함의 증거라고 인정하는 것은 그 순간들을 모독하는 일이 될 터였기 때문이다. 그는 비참하게 자기 자신과 분열되었고, 이 상태에서 벗어나기 위해 온갖 영적인 힘을 한껏 쥐어짰다.\n\n# Chapter 9\n\n이런 의심들이 때로는 약해지고 때로는 강해지면서 그를 들볶고 괴롭혔으나, 결코 그를 떠나지는 않았다. 그는 읽고 생각했고, 더 많이 읽고 더 많이 생각할수록 자기가 좇고 있는 목표에서 점점 더 멀어지는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n근래 모스크바에서, 그리고 시골에서, 유물론자들에게서는 아무런 해답도 찾을 수 없으리라고 확신하게 된 뒤로, 그는 플라톤, 스피노자, 칸트, 셸링, 헤겔, 쇼펜하우어, 그러니까 삶에 대해 비유물론적 설명을 내놓은 철학자들을 꼼꼼히 읽고 또 읽었다.\n\n이들의 사상은 그가 그것을 읽고 있을 때나, 다른 이론들, 특히 유물론자들의 이론을 반박할 논거를 스스로 찾고 있을 때는 풍요롭게 느껴졌다. 그러나 그가 문제들의 해답을 읽기 시작하거나 스스로 찾기 시작하면, 언제나 똑같은 일이 벌어졌다. 정신, 의지, 자유, 본질 같은 모호한 낱말들의 고정된 정의를 따라가면서, 철학자들이 그를 위해 놓아둔 낱말의 덫 속으로 일부러 자기 자신을 빠져들게 하는 동안에는 무언가를 이해하는 것만 같았다. 그러나 이 인위적인 추론의 흐름을 잊어버리고, 삶 그 자체로부터 고정된 정의에 따라 사색하는 동안 자기를 만족시켜 주었던 것으로 돌아서기만 하면, 이 인위적인 구조물 전체가 카드로 지은 집처럼 한순간에 무너져 내렸고, 그 구조물이 이성보다 더 중요한 삶 속의 어떤 것과는 동떨어진 채, 이리저리 자리를 바꾼 낱말들로 지어진 것이었음이 분명해졌다.\n\n한번은 쇼펜하우어를 읽으면서 그의 의지라는 말 자리에 사랑이라는 말을 넣어 보았고, 이틀쯤 이 새로운 철학이 그를 매혹했으나, 거기서 조금 물러서자 그뿐이었다. 그러다 삶 그 자체로부터 그것을 다시 흘끗 바라보려고 돌아서자, 그것 또한 떨어져 나가 버렸고, 따스함이라고는 없는 똑같은 모슬린 옷자락임이 드러났다.\n\n형 세르게이 이바노비치는 그에게 호미아코프의 신학 저작들을 읽어 보라고 권했다. 레빈은 호미아코프 저작의 둘째 권을 읽었는데, 처음에는 그를 거부감 들게 했던 그 우아하고 경구적이며 논쟁적인 문체에도 불구하고, 그 안에서 발견한 교회의 교리에 깊은 인상을 받았다. 그는 우선 신의 진리에 대한 깨달음이 한 인간에게 주어진 것이 아니라, 사랑으로 한데 묶인 사람들의 공동체, 곧 교회에 주어졌다는 생각에 놀랐다. 그를 기쁘게 한 것은, 신비롭고 아득히 먼 신으로, 그리고 창조 따위로 시작하기보다는, 인간의 온갖 믿음을 품고 신을 그 머리에 두었기에 거룩하고 무류(無謬)한, 지금도 살아 존재하는 교회를 믿고, 거기서부터 신에 대한 믿음과 창조와 타락과 구원에 대한 믿음을 받아들이는 편이 얼마나 더 쉬운가 하는 생각이었다. 그러나 그 뒤에 한 가톨릭 저술가가 쓴 교회사를 읽고, 이어서 그리스 정교 저술가가 쓴 교회사를 읽으면서, 그 두 교회가 그 개념 자체로는 무류하면서도 저마다 상대편의 권위를 부정한다는 것을 보게 되자, 호미아코프의 교회 교리는 그에게서 모든 매력을 잃었고, 이 구조물도 철학자들의 구조물처럼 먼지가 되어 부스러졌다.\n\n그해 봄 내내 그는 제정신이 아니었고, 끔찍한 공포의 순간들을 겪었다.\n\n\"내가 무엇이며 왜 여기에 있는지를 모른다면, 산다는 건 불가능해. 그런데 그걸 알 수가 없으니, 결국 살아갈 수가 없는 거지.\" 레빈은 혼잣말을 했다.\n\n\"무한한 시간, 무한한 물질, 무한한 공간 속에서 거품 같은 유기체 하나가 생겨나고, 그 거품은 잠시 머물다가 터져 버려. 그리고 그 거품이 바로 나야.\"\n\n그것은 괴로운 오류였으나, 그 방향으로 흘러온 수백 년에 걸친 인간 사유의 유일한 논리적 귀결이었다.\n\n이것이야말로 인간의 사유가 거의 모든 갈래에 걸쳐 정교하게 다듬어 낸 모든 체계가 그 위에 의지하고 있는 궁극의 믿음이었다. 그것은 널리 퍼진 확신이었고, 레빈은 다른 모든 설명 가운데서 언제 어떻게인지도 모른 채 무의식적으로 그것을, 어쨌든 가장 명료한 것이라 여겨 골라잡고는 자기 것으로 삼아 버렸다.\n\n그러나 그것은 단지 거짓에 그치지 않았다. 그것은 어떤 사악한 힘, 어떤 악하고 가증스러운 힘, 도저히 굴복할 수 없는 그런 힘의 잔인한 비웃음이었다.\n\n그는 이 힘에서 벗어나야 했다. 그리고 벗어날 수단은 누구나 제 손 안에 쥐고 있었다. 악에 대한 이 종속을 끊어 버리기만 하면 되었다. 그리고 그 수단은 하나, 죽음이었다.\n\n그리하여 행복한 아버지요 남편이며 더없이 건강한 레빈은, 목을 매고 싶은 유혹에 빠질까 봐 끈을 감추고, 자기 자신을 쏠까 두려워 총을 들고 나가는 것마저 겁낼 만큼, 몇 번이고 자살에 그토록 가까이 다가갔다.\n\n그러나 레빈은 자기를 쏘지도 않았고, 목을 매지도 않았다. 그는 계속 살아갔다.\n\n# Chapter 10\n\n레빈은 자기가 무엇이며 무엇을 위해 살고 있는지 생각해 보아도 그 물음들에 아무런 답을 찾을 수 없었고, 그래서 절망에 빠지곤 했다. 하지만 그는 그것에 관해 스스로 캐묻기를 그만두었다. 마치 그는 자기가 무엇이며 무엇을 위해 살고 있는지를 다 아는 것 같았는데, 왜냐하면 그는 단호하게, 망설임 없이 행동하고 살아갔기 때문이다. 정말이지 요즘 들어 그는 그 어느 때보다도 훨씬 결단력 있게, 흔들림 없이 살아가고 있었다.\n\n유월 초에 시골로 돌아온 그는 평소의 일상으로도 돌아갔다. 영지 경영, 농부들과 이웃들과의 관계, 살림을 돌보는 일, 그가 관리를 맡고 있던 누이와 형의 재산을 돌보는 일, 아내와 친척들과의 관계, 아이를 돌보는 일, 그리고 그해 봄에 새로 시작한 양봉 취미가 그의 시간을 온통 채웠다.\n\n이런 일들이 지금 그를 사로잡고 있는 것은, 예전에 그랬듯이 어떤 일반적인 원칙으로 그것들을 스스로에게 정당화했기 때문이 아니었다. 오히려 그 반대였다. 공공의 복리를 위한 예전의 노력이 실패로 끝난 것에 실망하고, 자기 자신의 생각과 사방에서 그를 짓누르는 산더미 같은 일거리에 너무 깊이 매여 있던 터라, 그는 공공의 선에 대해 생각하기를 완전히 그만두었고, 그저 자기가 하고 있는 일을 해야만 한다고—달리 어쩔 수 없다고—여겨졌기 때문에 그 모든 일에 매달렸다. 예전에는—거의 어린 시절부터, 그리고 완전한 어른이 될 때까지 점점 더—모두를 위해, 인류를 위해, 러시아를 위해, 마을 전체를 위해 무엇이든 이로운 일을 해 보려 할 때면, 그는 그런 일에 대한 생각 자체는 즐거웠지만 일 자체는 늘 갈피를 잡을 수 없었고, 그때마다 그 일이 절대적으로 필요하다는 온전한 확신을 한 번도 가져 본 적이 없었으며, 처음에는 그토록 위대해 보이던 일이 점점 작아지다가 끝내 아무것도 아닌 것으로 사라져 버리는 것을 늘 느껴 왔다. 그런데 이제, 결혼한 뒤로 점점 더 자기 자신을 위해 사는 쪽으로 자신을 옭아매기 시작하자, 비록 자기가 하고 있는 일에 대한 생각에서 아무런 기쁨도 느끼지 못하면서도, 그 일이 필요하다는 완전한 확신을 느꼈고, 그 일이 예전보다 훨씬 잘 풀려 나가는 것을, 그리고 점점 더 커져 가는 것을 보았다.\n\n이제 그는 자기 의지와 상관없이, 쟁기처럼 점점 더 깊이 흙 속을 파고드는 것 같았고, 그래서 이랑을 비스듬히 틀지 않고서는 빠져나올 수 없게 되어 버렸다.\n\n자기 아버지와 조상들이 살았던 것과 똑같은 가정생활을 하는 것—즉 똑같은 교양의 조건 속에서 사는 것—과 자식들을 똑같이 길러 내는 것은 의심할 여지 없이 필요한 일이었다. 그것은 배고플 때 식사를 하는 것만큼이나 필요한 일이었다. 그리고 이를 위해서는, 마치 저녁 식사를 짓는 일이 필요한 것처럼, 포크롭스코예의 농사 기구가 수입을 내도록 계속 돌아가게 하는 것이 필요했다. 빚을 갚는 것이 필요한 것만큼이나 의심할 여지 없이, 그의 아들이 그것을 유산으로 물려받을 때, 레빈이 자기 할아버지가 짓고 심어 놓은 모든 것에 대해 \"고맙습니다\" 하고 말했던 것처럼 그 아들도 아버지에게 \"고맙습니다\" 하고 말할 수 있도록 재산을 그런 상태로 지켜 두는 것이 필요했다. 그리고 이를 위해서는 땅을 직접 돌보아야지 남에게 빌려주어서는 안 되었고, 가축을 기르고, 밭에 거름을 주고, 나무를 심어야 했다.\n\n세르게이 이바노비치의 일, 누이의 일, 그에게 조언을 구하러 와서 늘 그래 오던 농부들의 일을 돌보지 않는 것은 불가능했다—마치 품에 안고 가던 아이를 내동댕이치는 것이 불가능한 것처럼. 형수와 그 아이들의 편안함을, 그리고 아내와 갓난아이의 편안함을 돌보는 것이 필요했고, 매일 적어도 잠깐씩이라도 그들과 함께 시간을 보내지 않는 것은 불가능했다.\n\n그리고 이 모든 것이, 사냥과 새로 시작한 양봉과 더불어, 레빈의 삶 전체를 채우고 있었는데, 그 삶은 그가 곰곰이 생각하기 시작하면 그에게 아무런 의미도 없는 것이었다.\n\n그러나 자기가 무엇을 해야 하는지를 속속들이 아는 것 외에도, 레빈은 똑같은 방식으로 그 모든 일을 어떻게 해야 하는지를, 그리고 무엇이 나머지보다 더 중요한지를 알고 있었다.\n\n그는 일꾼들을 되도록 싸게 고용해야 한다는 것을 알았다. 하지만 사람들을 계약으로 묶어 두고 현행 임금보다 낮은 값에 미리 삯을 치르는 것은, 비록 그것이 무척 이익이 된다 해도 결코 해서는 안 될 일이었다. 사료가 부족한 때에 농부들에게 짚을 파는 것은, 비록 그들이 가엾게 느껴진다 해도 해도 되는 일이었다. 하지만 술집과 선술집은, 비록 수입의 원천이라 해도 없애 버려야 했다. 나무를 베어 가는 것은 되도록 엄하게 벌해야 했지만, 가축을 그의 밭으로 몰아넣었다고 해서 벌금을 물릴 수는 없었다. 그리고 그것이 산지기를 짜증나게 하고 농부들이 그의 땅에 거리낌 없이 가축을 풀어놓게 만들었지만, 그래도 그는 벌로 그들의 가축을 붙잡아 둘 수는 없었다.\n\n고리대금업자에게 한 달에 십 퍼센트의 이자를 물고 있는 표트르에게는, 그가 그 빚에서 벗어나도록 돈을 빌려 주어야 했다. 하지만 소작료를 내지 않는 농부들을 그냥 봐줄 수도, 그들이 밀린 빚을 쌓아 가도록 내버려 둘 수도 없었다. 마름이 풀밭의 풀을 베지 않아 건초를 썩게 내버려 둔 것을 눈감아 주는 것은 불가능했고, 어린 잡목 숲을 심어 놓은 땅을 베어 내는 것도 마찬가지로 불가능했다. 한창 바쁜 철에 아버지가 죽어 간다는 이유로 집에 가 버린 일꾼을, 아무리 그가 가엾게 느껴진다 해도 너그러이 봐주는 것은 불가능했고, 그가 빈둥거린 그 값비싼 몇 달치 삯은 품삯에서 깎아야 했다. 하지만 아무 쓸모도 없는 늙은 하인들에게 다달이 식량을 주지 않는 것은 불가능했다.\n\n레빈은 집에 돌아가면 무엇보다 먼저 몸이 편치 않은 아내에게 가야 한다는 것을, 그리고 세 시간째 그를 만나려고 기다리고 있던 농부들은 좀 더 기다려도 된다는 것을 알았다. 그는 또한, 벌떼를 거두는 일에서 느끼는 온갖 즐거움에도 불구하고 그 즐거움을 포기하고 노인에게 혼자 벌을 돌보도록 맡겨 둔 채, 자기를 따라 양봉장까지 온 농부들과 이야기를 나누어야 한다는 것도 알았다.\n\n자기가 옳게 행동하는지 그르게 행동하는지 그는 알지 못했고, 자기가 옳다는 것을 증명하려 하기는커녕 요즘 그는 그것에 관한 일체의 생각이나 이야기를 피했다.\n\n이치를 따지는 것은 그를 의심으로 몰아넣었고, 무엇을 해야 하고 무엇을 하지 말아야 하는지 보지 못하게 가로막았다. 그가 생각하지 않고 그저 살아갈 때면, 그는 자기 영혼 속에 결코 그르치지 않는 재판관이 있어, 가능한 두 가지 행동 가운데 어느 것이 더 나은지 어느 것이 더 못한지를 가려 준다는 것을 끊임없이 의식했고, 옳게 행동하지 못하면 곧바로 그것을 알아챘다.\n\n그렇게 그는 자기가 무엇이며 무엇을 위해 사는지 알지 못한 채, 또 그것을 알 가망도 보이지 않는 채 살아갔으며, 이 앎의 결핍에 시달린 나머지 자살을 두려워할 지경에까지 이르렀으면서도, 자기만의 뚜렷한 인생길을 굳건히 닦아 나가고 있었다.\n\n# Chapter 11\n\n세르게이 이바노비치가 포크롭스코예에 온 그날은 레빈에게 가장 괴로운 날 가운데 하나였다. 한 해 가운데 일이 가장 바쁜 철이었으니, 이때가 되면 온 농민이 다른 어떤 삶의 조건에서도 결코 보이지 않는, 자기를 희생하는 비범한 열정을 노동에 쏟아붓는다. 만약 그런 자질을 보이는 사람들 스스로가 그것을 대단하게 여겼다면, 그리고 그것이 해마다 되풀이되지 않았다면, 또 이 격렬한 노동의 결실이 그토록 단순하지만 않았다면, 그 열정은 마땅히 높이 평가받았을 것이다.\n\n호밀과 귀리를 베어 단으로 묶어 나르고, 풀밭의 풀을 베고, 묵정밭을 갈아엎고, 씨앗을 타작하고, 겨울 곡식을 뿌리는 일—이 모든 것이 너무도 단순하고 평범해 보인다. 그러나 이 일들을 죄다 해내려면 마을의 노인부터 어린아이까지 모두가 서너 주 동안 쉼 없이, 평소의 세 배는 더 힘들게, 호밀로 빚은 맥주와 양파와 검은 빵으로 끼니를 때우며, 밤에는 곡식 단을 타작하고 실어 나르고, 하루 스물네 시간 가운데 두세 시간 넘게는 잠도 자지 못하며 일해야 한다. 그리고 이런 일이 해마다 러시아 곳곳에서 되풀이된다.\n\n생애의 대부분을 시골에서, 농민과 더없이 가까운 관계를 맺으며 살아온 레빈은 이 바쁜 철이 되면 늘 사람들 사이에 두루 번지는 이 활력의 고조에 자기도 전염되는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n이른 아침 그는 호밀을 처음으로 뿌리는 밭과, 낟가리로 실려 나가던 귀리밭으로 말을 타고 나갔다가, 아내와 처형이 일어날 무렵 집으로 돌아와 그들과 함께 커피를 마셨다. 그러고는 씨앗으로 쓸 곡식을 마련하려고 새 타작기를 돌리기 시작하는 농장까지 걸어갔다.\n\n그는 서늘한 곳간에 서 있었다. 새 이엉 지붕의, 갓 껍질을 벗긴 사시나무 들보 위에 엮어 놓은 개암나무 가지의 잎 향기가 아직 감돌고 있었다. 그는 열린 문 너머를 바라보았다. 그 문에서는 타작의 메마르고 쓴 먼지가 소용돌이치며 춤추고 있었고, 햇빛 속에 드러난 타작마당의 풀과 헛간에서 들여온 싱싱한 짚이 보였다. 그러다 점점이 무늬 진 머리에 가슴이 흰 제비들이 지저귀며 지붕 밑으로 날아들어, 날개를 파닥이며 문틈에 내려앉는 것을 바라보았고, 다시 어둡고 먼지 자욱한 헛간 안에서 분주히 움직이는 농민들을 바라보았다. 그러면서 그는 이상한 생각에 잠겼다.\n\n\"이 모든 게 왜 이루어지고 있는 거지?\" 그는 생각했다. \"나는 왜 여기 서서 저들을 일하게 만들고 있는 거지? 저들은 다 무엇 때문에 저토록 바쁘게, 내 앞에서 열의를 보이려고 애쓰는 걸까? 내 오랜 친구인 저 노파 마트료나는 무엇 때문에 저렇게 고생하는 걸까? (불이 났을 때 들보가 그녀를 덮쳤을 때 내가 치료해 주었지)\" 그는 곡식을 긁어모으는 비쩍 마른 노파를 바라보며 생각했다. 노파는 햇볕에 검게 그을린 맨발로 울퉁불퉁하고 거친 바닥 위를 힘겹게 옮겨 다니고 있었다. \"그때는 회복했지만, 오늘이든 내일이든 십 년 뒤든 그녀는 죽을 거고, 사람들은 그녀를 묻을 테고, 그녀에게서도, 저 익숙하고 부드러운 손놀림으로 이삭에서 낟알을 털어 내는 붉은 윗옷의 저 야무진 처녀에게서도 아무것도 남지 않겠지. 사람들은 그녀를 묻을 거고, 저 얼룩말도 묻을 거야, 그것도 머지않아.\" 그는 자기 밑에서 돌아가는 바퀴를 끝없이 밟으며 헐떡이고 무겁게 움직이는 말을 바라보며 생각했다. \"그리고 사람들은 그녀도, 곱슬곱슬한 수염에 검불이 가득하고 흰 어깨 위로 셔츠가 찢어진 타작꾼 표도르도 묻을 거야—사람들은 그를 묻겠지. 저자는 곡식 단을 풀고, 지시를 내리고, 여자들에게 소리치고, 돌아가는 바퀴의 가죽끈을 잽싸게 바로잡고 있어. 게다가 저들만이 아니야—사람들은 나도 묻을 거고, 아무것도 남지 않을 거야. 무엇을 위해서지?\"\n\n그는 이런 생각을 하면서도, 동시에 한 시간에 얼마나 타작이 되는지 가늠하려고 시계를 들여다보았다. 그것으로 하루 작업량을 정하려고 알고 싶었던 것이다.\n\n\"곧 한 시인데, 이제 겨우 세 번째 단을 시작하고 있군.\" 레빈은 생각했다. 그는 기계에 곡식을 넣고 있는 사내에게 다가가, 기계의 굉음 너머로 소리쳐서 좀 더 천천히 넣으라고 일렀다. \"한꺼번에 너무 많이 넣잖아, 표도르. 봐—막혀 버려서 일이 안 되는 거야. 고르게 넣으라고.\"\n\n축축한 얼굴에 들러붙은 먼지로 새까매진 표도르가 무어라 대꾸하며 소리쳤지만, 여전히 레빈이 원하지 않는 방식대로 일을 계속했다.\n\n레빈은 기계로 다가가 표도르를 옆으로 비키게 하고는, 직접 곡식을 넣기 시작했다. 머지않아 다가온 농민들의 점심시간까지 일하고 나서, 그는 표도르와 함께 헛간을 나와, 씨앗으로 쓰려고 타작마당에 가지런히 쌓아 놓은 노란 호밀 단 곁에 멈춰 서서 그와 이야기를 나누게 되었다.\n\n표도르는 레빈이 한때 협동조합에 땅을 떼어 주었던 마을에서 좀 떨어진 마을 출신이었다. 지금 그 땅은 전에 문지기로 일하던 사내에게 넘어가 있었다.\n\n레빈은 표도르와 이 땅에 관해 이야기하면서, 같은 마을에 사는, 살림이 넉넉하고 성품도 좋은 농민 플라톤이 내년에 그 땅을 맡지 않겠느냐고 물었다.\n\n\"소작료가 너무 비싸서요, 플라톤한테는 수지가 안 맞을 겁니다, 콘스탄친 드미트리치.\" 농민이 땀에 흠뻑 젖은 셔츠에서 이삭을 떼어 내며 대답했다.\n\n\"하지만 키릴로프는 어떻게 수지를 맞추지?\"\n\n\"미투흐 말입니까!\" (농민은 그 문지기를 경멸하는 어조로 그렇게 불렀다.) \"그 사람이야 틀림없이 수지를 맞출 겁니다, 콘스탄친 드미트리치! 어떻게든 쥐어짜서라도 자기 몫은 챙길걸요! 그 사람은 그리스도교도한테도 인정사정없으니까요. 하지만 포카니치 영감님은\" (그는 늙은 농민 플라톤을 그렇게 불렀다.) \"그 양반이 남의 가죽을 벗겨 먹기야 하겠습니까? 빚이 있는 사람이면 누구든 봐주고요. 마지막 한 푼까지 짜내지도 않습죠. 그 양반도 사람이니까요.\"\n\n\"하지만 왜 남을 봐주는 거지?\"\n\n\"아, 뭐, 그야 당연히, 사람이 다 다르니까요. 어떤 사람은 미투흐처럼 제 욕심만 챙기며 살고, 제 배 채울 생각밖에 안 하지만, 포카니치 영감님은 의로운 분이거든요. 그분은 영혼을 위해 사십니다. 하느님을 잊지 않으세요.\"\n\n\"하느님을 어떻게 생각한다는 거지? 영혼을 위해 산다는 건 또 어떻게 사는 건데?\" 레빈은 거의 소리치다시피 했다.\n\n\"왜, 그야 물론, 진실되게, 하느님의 방식대로지요. 사람이 다 다르니까요. 나리만 봐도, 나리는 남에게 해를 끼치실 분이 아니잖습니까…….\"\n\n\"그래, 그래, 잘 있게!\" 흥분으로 숨이 차오른 레빈은 그렇게 말하고 돌아서서 지팡이를 집어 들고는 집을 향해 빠르게 걸어갔다. 포카니치가 영혼을 위해, 진실되게, 하느님의 방식대로 산다는 농민의 말에, 막연하지만 의미심장한 생각들이 마치 갇혀 있다가 터져 나오는 것 같았다. 그리고 그 모든 생각이 하나의 목표를 향해 치달으며, 그의 머릿속을 소용돌이치며 가득 메워 그 빛으로 그의 눈을 멀게 했다.\n\n# Chapter 12\n\n레빈은 큰길을 따라 성큼성큼 걸었다. 그를 사로잡은 것은 자기 생각이라기보다(아직 그는 그 생각들을 풀어낼 수 없었다)—이전에 한 번도 겪어 본 적 없는 어떤 영혼의 상태였다.\n\n농부가 내뱉은 그 말이 그의 영혼에 전기 충격처럼 작용했고, 끊임없이 그의 마음을 차지하던 그 흩어지고 무력하고 따로 노는 무수한 생각의 떼를 별안간 변형시켜 하나의 전체로 결합시켰다. 그 생각들은 그가 토지에 관해 이야기하는 동안에도 무의식 속에서 그의 마음을 떠나지 않고 있었던 것이다.\n\n그는 자기 영혼 속에 무언가 새로운 것이 있음을 느꼈고, 그것이 무엇인지 아직 알지 못한 채 그 새로운 것을 기쁘게 음미했다.\n\n\"자기 욕구를 위해서가 아니라, 하느님을 위해 산다고? 어떤 하느님을 위해서? 그가 한 말보다 더 터무니없는 말을 할 수 있을까? 그는 자기 욕구를 위해 살아서는 안 된다고 했어. 즉 우리가 이해하는 것, 우리를 끌어당기는 것, 우리가 욕망하는 것을 위해 살아서는 안 되고, 무언가 이해할 수 없는 것, 아무도 이해할 수도 정의할 수도 없는 하느님을 위해 살아야 한다는 거지. 그게 어쨌단 말인가? 표도르의 그 터무니없는 말을 내가 이해하지 못했던가? 그리고 그것을 이해하면서, 내가 그 진실을 의심했던가? 그 말이 어리석다거나 모호하다거나 부정확하다고 생각했던가? 아니, 나는 그를 이해했어, 그가 그 말을 이해하는 것과 똑같이. 나는 그 말을 내가 인생에서 그 무엇을 이해하는 것보다 더 충분하고 명료하게 이해했고, 평생 단 한 번도 그것을 의심한 적이 없으며 또 의심할 수도 없어. 그리고 나뿐만 아니라 모두가, 온 세상이 그것 말고는 아무것도 온전히 이해하지 못하면서, 오직 그것에 대해서만은 의심이 없고 언제나 의견이 일치하지.\n\n그리고 나는 기적을 찾아 헤매며, 나를 납득시켜 줄 기적을 보지 못한다고 불평했어. 물질적인 기적이라면 나를 설득했을 테지. 그런데 여기 기적이, 가능한 단 하나의 기적이, 끊임없이 존재하며 사방에서 나를 둘러싸고 있는 그 기적이 있었는데, 나는 한 번도 그것을 알아채지 못했던 거야!\n\n표도르는 키릴로프가 제 배를 위해 산다고 말하지. 그건 이해할 수 있고 합리적이야. 우리 모두는 합리적 존재로서 제 배를 위해 사는 것 말고는 달리 아무것도 할 수 없으니까. 그런데 별안간 같은 표도르가, 제 배를 위해 살아서는 안 되고 진리를 위해, 하느님을 위해 살아야 한다고 말하니, 한마디 암시만으로도 나는 그를 이해해! 그리고 나와 수백만의 사람들이, 오래전에 살았던 사람들과 지금 살아 있는 사람들이—농부들이며, 마음이 가난한 자들이며, 그것에 관해 생각하고 글을 쓰며 그 모호한 말로 똑같은 것을 말해 온 학자들이—우리 모두는 이 한 가지에 대해서는 의견이 일치해. 즉 우리가 무엇을 위해 살아야 하는가, 무엇이 선인가에 대해서는. 나와 모든 사람은 오직 하나의 확고하고 반박할 수 없는 명료한 앎을 가지고 있고, 그 앎은 이성으로 설명할 수 없어. 그것은 이성 밖에 있고, 원인도 없으며 결과도 가질 수 없어.\n\n선에 원인이 있다면 그것은 선이 아니야. 선에 결과가, 보상이 있다면 그 또한 선이 아니지. 그러니 선은 원인과 결과의 사슬 밖에 있는 거야.\n\n그런데도 나는 그것을 알고 있고, 우리 모두가 그것을 알고 있어.\n\n그보다 더 큰 기적이 어디 있겠는가?\n\n내가 이 모든 것의 해답을 찾은 걸까? 내 고통이 끝날 수 있는 걸까?\" 레빈은 더위도 피로도 느끼지 못한 채 먼지투성이 길을 성큼성큼 걸으며 생각했고, 오랜 고통에서 벗어난 안도감을 느꼈다. 이 감정은 어찌나 달콤한지 그에게는 믿기지 않을 정도였다. 그는 벅찬 감격에 숨이 막혔고 더는 걸어갈 수가 없었다. 그는 길에서 벗어나 숲으로 들어가 사시나무 그늘 아래 베지 않은 풀밭 위에 누웠다. 그는 달아오른 머리에서 모자를 벗고 무성하고 보드라운 숲속 풀밭에 팔꿈치를 괴고 누웠다.\n\n\"그래, 나는 이것을 스스로에게 명확히 하고 이해해야 해.\" 그는 앞에 펼쳐진 밟히지 않은 풀밭을 골똘히 바라보며, 개밀 잎새를 따라 기어가다 길을 막는 미나리아재비 잎사귀를 들어 올리며 나아가는 푸른 딱정벌레의 움직임을 좇으며 생각했다. \"내가 무엇을 발견한 거지?\" 그는 딱정벌레가 지나가도록 미나리아재비 잎사귀를 옆으로 비켜 주고, 딱정벌레가 건너갈 수 있도록 다른 풀잎 하나를 그 위로 비틀어 놓으며 스스로에게 물었다. \"무엇이 나를 기쁘게 하는 걸까? 내가 무엇을 발견한 거지?\n\n나는 아무것도 발견하지 못했어. 나는 다만 내가 이미 알고 있던 것을 알아냈을 뿐이야. 과거에 내게 생명을 주었고 지금도 내게 생명을 주는 그 힘을 이해하게 된 거지. 나는 거짓에서 풀려났고, 주인을 찾았어.\n\n예전에 나는 내 몸 안에서, 이 풀과 이 딱정벌레의 몸 안에서(저런, 저것은 풀에는 관심이 없었군, 날개를 펴고 날아가 버렸어) 물리적, 화학적, 생리적 법칙에 따라 물질의 변형이 일어나고 있다고 말하곤 했지. 그리고 우리 모두 안에서도, 사시나무며 구름이며 안개 자락 안에서와 마찬가지로, 진화의 과정이 진행되고 있다고 말이야. 무엇으로부터의 진화인가? 무엇으로의 진화인가? 영원한 진화와 투쟁이라……. 마치 영원한 것 속에 무슨 경향이나 투쟁 따위가 있을 수 있다는 듯이! 그리고 나는 그 길을 따라 온 힘을 다해 사유했음에도 인생의 의미를, 내 충동과 갈망의 의미를 발견할 수 없다는 데 놀라곤 했어. 이제 나는 내 인생의 의미를 안다고 말해. '하느님을 위해, 내 영혼을 위해 사는 것.' 그리고 이 의미는, 그 명료함에도 불구하고 신비롭고 경이로워. 실로 존재하는 모든 것의 의미가 그러하지. 그래, 교만이야.\" 그는 배를 깔고 돌아누우며 풀잎을 끊지 않으려 조심하며 풀잎으로 올가미를 매기 시작했다.\n\n\"단지 지성의 교만만이 아니라, 지성의 우둔함이지. 그리고 무엇보다도, 그 기만이야. 그래, 지성의 기만. 지성의 속임수와 간교함, 바로 그거야.\" 그는 스스로에게 말했다.\n\n그리고 그는 지난 이 년간 자기 생각이 거쳐 온 모든 과정을 마음속으로 간략히 되짚어 보았다. 그 시작은 사랑하는 형이 절망적으로 병들어 가는 광경 앞에서 죽음을 명료하게 마주한 일이었다.\n\n그때 처음으로, 모든 사람에게, 그리고 자기 자신에게도 고통과 죽음과 망각 외에는 아무것도 예비되어 있지 않다는 것을 깨닫고, 그는 그런 식으로는 삶이 불가능하다고, 그러니 삶을 어떤 악마의 사악한 농지거리로 자기에게 비치지 않도록 해석해 내든지, 아니면 스스로 총을 쏘아 버리든지 해야 한다고 마음먹었다.\n\n그러나 그는 둘 중 어느 것도 하지 않은 채, 계속 살아가고 생각하고 느꼈으며, 심지어 바로 그 무렵 결혼까지 했고, 많은 기쁨을 누렸으며, 자기 인생의 의미를 생각하지 않을 때면 행복하기까지 했다.\n\n이것은 무엇을 의미하는가? 그것은 그가 올바르게 살아왔으나, 그릇되게 생각해 왔음을 의미했다.\n\n그는 (자신도 알지 못한 채) 어머니의 젖과 함께 빨아들인 그 영혼의 진리들에 의지해 살아왔으면서도, 그 진리들을 인정하지 않았을 뿐 아니라 의도적으로 무시하며 생각해 왔던 것이다.\n\n이제 그에게는 자기가 오직 자기를 길러 낸 그 믿음들의 힘으로만 살아갈 수 있다는 것이 명백해졌다.\n\n\"내게 이 믿음들이 없었다면, 내가 내 욕망을 위해서가 아니라 하느님을 위해 살아야 한다는 것을 알지 못했다면, 나는 무엇이 되었을 것이며 내 인생을 어떻게 보냈을까? 나는 도둑질하고 거짓말하고 살인을 저질렀을 거야. 내 인생의 가장 큰 행복을 이루는 그 무엇도 내게는 존재하지 않았을 테지.\" 그리고 상상력을 한껏 펼쳐 보아도, 자기가 무엇을 위해 살고 있는지를 알지 못했다면 자신이 어떤 짐승 같은 존재가 되었을지 그는 도무지 그려 낼 수 없었다.\n\n\"나는 내 물음에 대한 답을 찾고 있었어. 그런데 사유는 내 물음에 답을 줄 수 없었어. 그것은 내 물음과는 차원이 맞지 않으니까. 그 답은 인생 자체가 내게 주었어. 무엇이 옳고 무엇이 그른가에 대한 나의 앎을 통해서 말이야. 그리고 그 앎에 나는 어떤 방식으로도 도달한 것이 아니야. 그것은 모든 사람에게 주어지듯 내게도 주어진 거야. 주어진 것이지, 왜냐하면 나는 어디서도 그것을 얻을 수 없었을 테니까.\n\n내가 그것을 어디서 얻을 수 있었겠는가? 내가 이성으로, 이웃을 사랑하고 억압해서는 안 된다는 것을 알아낼 수 있었을까? 어린 시절에 사람들이 내게 그렇게 말해 주었고, 나는 그것을 기쁘게 믿었어. 왜냐하면 그들은 이미 내 영혼 속에 있던 것을 말해 주었으니까. 그런데 누가 그것을 발견했는가? 이성이 아니야. 이성은 생존을 위한 투쟁을 발견했고, 우리의 욕망을 충족시키는 데 방해가 되는 모든 것을 억압하라고 요구하는 법칙을 발견했어. 그것이 이성의 추론이지. 하지만 이웃을 사랑하는 것은 이성이 결코 발견할 수 없었을 거야. 그것은 비합리적이니까.\"\n\n# Chapter 13\n\n레빈은 얼마 전 돌리와 그녀의 아이들 사이에서 목격한 한 장면을 떠올렸다. 아이들은 어른들 손을 벗어나 촛불에 산딸기를 구워 먹고 주사기로 서로의 입에 우유를 뿜어 대기 시작했다. 어머니는 그 장난을 들켰을 때, 레빈이 보는 앞에서 너희들의 못된 짓이 어른들에게 얼마나 성가신 일을 만드는지, 그리고 그 수고가 모두 너희들을 위한 것이라는 점, 또 컵을 깨뜨리면 차를 따라 마실 그릇이 없어질 거라는 점, 우유를 낭비하면 먹을 것이 없어 굶어 죽게 될 거라는 점을 아이들에게 일깨우기 시작했다.\n\n레빈은 어머니가 하는 말을 듣는 아이들의 그 수동적이고 지친, 믿지 않는 태도에 충격을 받았다. 아이들은 그저 재미난 놀이가 가로막힌 것이 짜증스러울 뿐, 어머니가 하는 말은 한마디도 믿지 않았다. 사실 믿을 수가 없었던 것이, 아이들은 자기들이 늘 누리는 그 모든 것의 어마어마함을 헤아릴 수 없었고, 그래서 자기들이 망가뜨리고 있는 것이 바로 자기들을 살게 해 주는 그것이라는 사실을 떠올릴 수도 없었기 때문이다.\n\n\"이런 건 전부 저절로 생기는 거야.\" 아이들은 생각했다. \"그러니까 재미있을 것도 없고 중요할 것도 없어, 언제나 이래 왔고 앞으로도 늘 이럴 테니까. 게다가 다 늘 똑같단 말이야. 그런 건 우리가 생각할 필요도 없어, 이미 다 갖춰져 있으니까. 하지만 우리는 우리만의 것, 새로운 걸 만들어 내고 싶어. 그래서 컵에 산딸기를 담아 촛불에 구울 생각을 했고, 서로의 입에 우유를 곧장 뿜을 생각을 했지. 그게 재미있고, 새롭고, 컵으로 마시는 것보다 조금도 못할 게 없거든.\"\n\n\"이성의 힘을 빌려 자연의 힘들이 지닌 의미와 인간의 삶의 의미를 찾으려는 우리가, 내가 한 일이 바로 저것과 똑같은 게 아닌가?\" 그는 생각했다.\n\n\"그리고 모든 철학 이론들도 똑같은 짓을 하고 있는 게 아닌가? 인간에게 낯설고 자연스럽지 않은 사유의 길을 통해, 그가 이미 오래전부터 알고 있던 것, 그것 없이는 도무지 살아갈 수조차 없을 만큼 확실하게 알고 있던 것에 대한 앎으로 그를 데려가려 애쓰는 것이? 어느 철학자의 이론이 전개되는 과정을 보면 분명히 드러나지 않는가? 그가 삶의 가장 중요한 의미가 무엇인지를 이미 알고 있다는 것이, 농부 표도르와 꼭 마찬가지로 확신을 가지고, 그보다 조금도 더 명확하지 않게 알고 있으면서, 그저 미심쩍은 지적 경로를 통해 모두가 아는 것으로 되돌아오려 애쓰고 있을 뿐이라는 것이?\n\n\"자, 그럼 아이들을 저희들끼리 내버려 두고 혼자 힘으로 그릇을 만들고, 소에서 우유를 짜고, 그 밖의 일을 하게 해 보라. 그래도 못된 짓을 할까? 아니, 굶어 죽고 말 거다! 자, 그럼 우리를 우리의 욕정과 사념과 함께, 유일한 신, 창조주에 대한 관념도 없이, 무엇이 옳은지에 대한 관념도 없이, 도덕적 악에 대한 관념도 없이 내버려 두어 보라.\n\n\"그 관념들 없이 무엇이든 한번 세워 보라!\n\n\"우리가 그것들을 부수려고만 드는 것은, 우리가 영적으로 이미 공급받고 있기 때문이다. 꼭 저 아이들처럼!\n\n\"농부와 함께 나누는 그 기쁜 앎, 오직 그것만이 내 영혼에 평화를 주는 그 앎을 나는 어디서 얻었는가? 어디서 그것을 얻었단 말인가?\n\n\"신에 대한 관념 속에서 자라난 그리스도교인으로서, 내 평생이 그리스도교가 내게 베푼 영적 축복으로 가득 차 있고, 그것으로 충만하며, 그 축복에 기대어 살아가면서도, 나는 아이들처럼 그것을 이해하지 못한 채, 내가 그것에 기대어 살아가는 바로 그것을 부수고, 아니 부수려 애쓰고 있었다. 그러다 삶의 중대한 순간이 닥치면, 춥고 배고픈 아이들처럼 나는 그분께로 돌아서는데, 어머니가 아이들의 철없는 장난을 꾸짖을 때 아이들이 느끼는 것보다도 더 적게, 나는 함부로 부린 광기 어린 내 철없는 짓들이 내게 죄로 헤아려진다고 느끼지 못한다.\n\n\"그렇다, 내가 아는 것은 이성으로 아는 것이 아니라 내게 주어진 것, 계시된 것이며, 나는 그것을 가슴으로, 교회가 가르치는 가장 중요한 것에 대한 믿음으로 안다.\n\n\"교회! 교회 말이야!\" 레빈은 혼자 거듭 되뇌었다. 그는 몸을 반대편으로 돌려 팔꿈치를 괸 채, 멀리 강을 건너가는 한 무리의 가축들을 바라보기 시작했다.\n\n\"하지만 교회가 가르치는 모든 것을 내가 믿을 수 있을까?\" 그는 자신을 시험하며, 지금 누리는 마음의 평화를 무너뜨릴 수 있는 모든 것을 떠올리며 생각했다. 그는 늘 가장 이상하게 여겨졌고 늘 자기에게 걸림돌이 되어 왔던 교회의 모든 교리를 일부러 되짚어 보았다.\n\n\"창조? 하지만 내가 존재를 어떻게 설명했지? 존재로? 무로? 악마와 죄. 하지만 악을 내가 어떻게 설명하지?…… 속죄는?……\n\n\"하지만 나는 아무것도, 아무것도 알지 못하며, 나와 모든 인간에게 전해진 것 외에는 아무것도 알 수가 없다.\"\n\n그리고 그에게는 교회의 신앙 조항 가운데 단 하나도 가장 중요한 것을, 즉 신에 대한 믿음, 인간의 운명의 유일한 목표인 선에 대한 믿음을 무너뜨릴 수 있는 것은 없는 것처럼 여겨졌다.\n\n교회의 신앙 조항 하나하나의 밑바탕에는, 자기 욕망 대신 진리를 섬기는 데 대한 믿음을 놓을 수 있었다. 그리고 어느 교리도 그 믿음을 흔들어 놓기는커녕, 모든 교리가 저마다 그 위대한 기적, 곧 땅 위에서 끊임없이 드러나는 그 기적을 완성하는 데 꼭 필요한 것처럼 여겨졌다. 그 기적이란, 각각의 사람이, 그리고 수백만의 온갖 부류의 사람들이, 현명한 자와 백치, 노인과 아이—모든 사람, 농부들, 르보프, 키티, 거지와 왕에 이르기까지—똑같은 단 하나의 것을 완벽하게 이해하고, 그것으로써 영혼의 삶, 오직 그것만이 살 가치가 있고 우리에게 유일하게 소중한 그 삶을 세울 수 있게 해 주는 것이었다.\n\n그는 등을 대고 누워, 이제 높고 구름 한 점 없는 하늘을 올려다보았다. \"저것이 무한한 공간이며 둥근 둥근 천장이 아니라는 것을 내가 모르는가? 하지만 아무리 눈을 가늘게 뜨고 시선을 긴장시켜 보아도, 나는 그것이 둥글지 않고 경계가 없다고는 볼 수가 없으며, 무한한 공간에 대해 알고 있음에도, 단단한 푸른 둥근 천장을 볼 때의 내가 의심할 여지 없이 옳고, 그 너머를 보려고 눈을 긴장시킬 때보다 더 옳다.\"\n\n레빈은 생각을 멈추고, 그저 자기 안에서 기쁘고 진지하게 이야기하는 듯한 신비로운 목소리들에 말하자면 귀를 기울일 뿐이었다.\n\n\"이것이 믿음일 수 있을까?\" 그는 자신의 행복을 믿기가 두려워 생각했다. \"나의 하느님, 당신께 감사드립니다!\" 그는 복받쳐 오르는 흐느낌을 삼키며, 두 손으로 두 눈에 그득 고인 눈물을 훔치며 말했다.\n\n# Chapter 14\n\n레빈은 앞을 바라보다 한 무리의 소 떼를 보았고, 이어 보로네이를 채에 맨 자신의 마차와, 그 소 떼 쪽으로 다가가 목동에게 무언가를 말하는 마부를 알아보았다. 그러고는 바퀴가 덜그럭거리는 소리와 윤기 흐르는 말이 바로 곁에서 콧김을 내뿜는 소리를 들었다. 하지만 그는 자기 생각에 어찌나 깊이 잠겨 있었던지, 마부가 무슨 일로 자기를 데리러 왔는지조차 의아해하지 않았다.\n\n마부가 그에게 바짝 마차를 몰고 와 소리쳤을 때에야 비로소 그는 그것을 의식했다. \"마님께서 보내셨습니다요. 동생분이 오셨고, 어떤 신사분도 함께 오셨습니다.\"\n\n레빈은 마차에 올라타 고삐를 잡았다. 잠에서 막 깨어난 사람처럼, 레빈은 한참 동안 정신을 가다듬을 수가 없었다. 그는 마구가 쓸린 엉덩이 사이와 목덜미에 거품이 군데군데 묻어 있는 윤기 나는 말을 멍하니 바라보았고, 곁에 앉은 마부 이반을 멍하니 바라보았으며, 자기가 동생을 기다리고 있었다는 것을 떠올렸고, 아내가 자기가 오래 자리를 비워 불안해하고 있을 거라고 생각했으며, 동생과 함께 온 손님이 누구일지 짐작해 보려 애썼다. 그러자 동생도, 아내도, 알지 못하는 손님도 이제 전과는 사뭇 다르게 느껴졌다. 그는 이제 모든 사람과의 관계가 달라지리라고 생각했다.\n\n\"동생과는 늘 우리 사이에 있던 그 서먹함이 사라질 거야, 다툼도 없을 테고. 키티와는 결코 말다툼하지 않을 거야. 손님과는, 그가 누구든 간에, 다정하고 친절하게 대해야지. 하인들과도, 이반과도, 모든 게 달라질 거야.\"\n\n뻣뻣한 고삐를 당겨, 조바심에 콧김을 내뿜으며 어서 내달리게 해 달라고 조르는 듯한 그 좋은 말을 다잡으면서, 레빈은 곁에 앉은 이반을 돌아보았다. 이반은 할 일 없는 한 손을 어찌해야 할지 몰라 부풀어 오르는 셔츠 자락을 자꾸 눌러 대고 있었고, 레빈은 그와 더불어 이야기를 꺼낼 만한 무언가를 찾아보려 애썼다. 안장 뱃대끈을 너무 높이 당겨 맸다고 말할 뻔했지만, 그건 책망처럼 들릴 듯했고, 그는 다정하고 따뜻한 대화를 갈망하고 있었다. 그 밖에는 아무것도 떠오르지 않았다.\n\n\"나리, 오른쪽으로 붙으셔야 합니다요, 저 그루터기를 조심하시고요.\" 마부가 레빈이 쥔 고삐를 당기며 말했다.\n\n\"제발 건드리지도 말고 나한테 가르치려 들지도 마!\" 이런 참견에 화가 치민 레빈이 말했다. 지금도, 언제나 그랬듯이, 참견은 그를 화나게 했고, 그는 자신의 정신적 상태가 현실과 맞닥뜨리는 순간 곧바로 자기를 변화시킬 수 있으리라던 가정이 얼마나 잘못된 것이었는지를 이내 서글프게 느꼈다.\n\n집에서 채 사백 미터도 못 미친 지점에서 그는 그리샤와 타냐가 자기를 맞으러 달려오는 것을 보았다.\n\n\"코스챠 삼촌! 엄마가 오고 있어요, 할아버지하고, 세르게이 이바노비치하고, 또 다른 사람도요.\" 아이들은 마차로 기어오르며 말했다.\n\n\"그 사람이 누구냐?\"\n\n\"엄청 무서운 사람이에요! 그리고 팔을 이렇게 한다니까요.\" 타냐가 마차에서 일어나 카타바소프를 흉내 내며 말했다.\n\n\"늙은 사람이냐, 젊은 사람이냐?\" 레빈은 웃으며 물었다. 타냐의 시늉이 누군가를 떠올리게 했는데, 그게 누구인지는 알 수 없었다.\n\n\"아, 제발 따분한 사람이 아니어야 할 텐데!\" 레빈은 생각했다.\n\n길이 굽어지는 곳에서 몸을 돌려 그 일행이 다가오는 것을 보자마자, 레빈은 밀짚모자를 쓰고 타냐가 보여 준 그대로 팔을 휘저으며 걸어오는 카타바소프를 알아보았다. 카타바소프는 형이상학을 한 번도 공부한 적 없는 자연과학 저술가들에게서 자기 견해를 끌어다 쓰면서도 형이상학을 논하기를 무척 좋아했고, 모스크바에서 레빈은 근래 그와 여러 차례 논쟁을 벌인 적이 있었다.\n\n그리고 그 논쟁들 가운데 하나, 카타바소프가 자기가 승리를 거두었다고 분명히 여겼던 그 논쟁이, 레빈이 그를 알아보면서 가장 먼저 떠올린 것이었다.\n\n\"아니, 무슨 일이 있어도, 나는 논쟁하지 않을 거고 내 생각을 함부로 입에 올리지도 않을 거야.\" 그는 생각했다.\n\n마차에서 내려 동생과 카타바소프에게 인사한 뒤, 레빈은 아내에 대해 물었다.\n\n\"미챠를 데리고 콜록(집 근처의 작은 숲)에 갔어요. 집 안이 너무 더워서 아이를 거기 데려가 있으려고요.\" 돌리가 말했다. 레빈은 늘 아내에게 아기를 숲으로 데려가지 말라고, 안전하지 않다고 충고해 왔던 터라, 이 말을 듣고 마음이 편치 않았다.\n\n\"애를 데리고 이곳저곳 헤집고 다닌다니까.\" 공작이 미소 지으며 말했다. \"내가 아예 얼음 저장고에 넣어 두라고 권했지.\"\n\n\"양봉장으로 오려고 했어요. 자네가 거기 있을 거라고 생각했거든. 우리도 그리로 가는 길이에요.\" 돌리가 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 너는 뭘 하고 지내냐?\" 세르게이 이바노비치가 나머지 사람들에게서 뒤처져 그의 곁에서 걸으며 말했다.\n\n\"아, 특별한 건 없어요. 늘 그렇듯 땅 일로 바쁘죠.\" 레빈이 대답했다. \"그런데 형은요? 오래 머무를 거예요? 정말 오랫동안 형을 기다렸어요.\"\n\n\"보름만 있을 거야. 모스크바에 할 일이 산더미라서.\"\n\n이 말에 형제의 눈이 마주쳤고, 레빈은 늘 품어 온, 지금은 그 어느 때보다 강렬한, 형과 더 다정하고 더 허물없는 사이가 되고 싶다는 바람에도 불구하고, 형을 마주 보기가 어색하게 느껴졌다. 그는 눈길을 떨구었고 무슨 말을 해야 할지 몰랐다.\n\n세르게이 이바노비치가 반가워할 만한, 그러면서도 그가 모스크바에서 할 일이 있다는 말로 넌지시 비친 세르비아 전쟁이며 슬라브 문제에서 그를 떼어 놓을 만한 화제를 이리저리 더듬던 끝에, 레빈은 세르게이 이바노비치의 책 이야기를 꺼냈다.\n\n\"그래, 형 책에 대한 서평은 좀 나왔어요?\" 그가 물었다.\n\n세르게이 이바노비치는 그 질문의 의도적인 성격에 미소를 지었다.\n\n\"이제 아무도 그런 데 관심이 없어, 나는 누구보다도 더 그렇고.\" 그가 말했다. \"저것 좀 보세요, 다리아 알렉산드로브나, 소나기가 오겠는데요.\" 그는 사시나무 우듬지 위로 보이는 하얀 비구름을 양산으로 가리키며 덧붙였다.\n\n그리고 이 말만으로도 형제 사이에 다시 그 어조가—거의 적대적이지는 않으나 싸늘한 그 어조가—되살아나기에 충분했으니, 그것은 레빈이 그토록 피하고 싶어 하던 바로 그것이었다.\n\n레빈은 카타바소프에게 다가갔다.\n\n\"이렇게 와 주기로 마음먹어 주다니 정말 기쁩니다.\" 그가 그에게 말했다.\n\n\"오래전부터 오려고 했지. 이제 우리 토론 좀 해야지, 그건 두고 봅시다. 스펜서는 읽어 봤나?\"\n\n\"아니요, 끝까지 읽지는 못했어요.\" 레빈이 말했다. \"하지만 이제 그가 필요 없어요.\"\n\n\"그게 무슨 소린가? 그거 흥미로운데. 어째서?\"\n\n\"제 말은, 저는 제가 관심을 두는 문제들의 해답을 그나 그 비슷한 사람들에게서는 결코 찾지 못하리라는 걸 완전히 확신하게 됐다는 거예요. 이제…….\"\n\n그러나 카타바소프의 평온하고 유쾌한 표정이 문득 그의 눈에 들어왔고, 그는 이 대화로 자기가 틀림없이 흐트러뜨리고 있는 자신의 행복한 기분이 어찌나 애틋하던지, 자신의 결심을 떠올리고는 말을 뚝 그쳤다.\n\n\"하지만 그 얘기는 나중에 하죠.\" 그가 덧붙였다. \"양봉장으로 갈 거면, 이쪽이에요, 이 작은 오솔길을 따라서요.\" 그가 모두에게 말했다.\n\n좁은 오솔길을 따라, 한쪽으로는 화사한 삼색제비꽃이 빽빽이 무더기로 덮여 있고 그 사이로 군데군데 키 큰 짙은 초록빛 헬레보어 다발이 솟아 있는, 풀을 베지 않은 작은 풀밭에 이르자, 레빈은 어린 사시나무의 짙고 서늘한 그늘 아래, 벌을 무서워할지도 모를 양봉장 손님들을 위해 일부러 거기 놓아 둔 벤치와 그루터기 몇 개에 손님들을 앉혔고, 자신은 그들을 대접할 빵과 오이와 신선한 꿀을 가지러 오두막으로 갔다.\n\n되도록 동작을 느긋하게 하려고 애쓰며, 점점 더 자주 곁을 스쳐 지나가는 벌들의 윙윙거리는 소리에 귀를 기울이면서, 그는 오솔길을 따라 오두막으로 걸어갔다. 바로 입구에서 벌 한 마리가 그의 수염에 걸려 성난 듯 윙윙댔지만, 그는 조심스럽게 그것을 떼어 냈다. 그늘진 바깥방으로 들어가 못에 걸려 있던 망사 모자를 벽에서 내려 머리에 쓰고 두 손을 주머니에 찔러 넣은 채, 그는 울타리를 두른 양봉장으로 들어갔다. 그곳에는 말끔하게 풀을 벤 공간 한복판에 가지런한 줄을 이루어, 말뚝에 인피로 동여맨 그가 너무도 잘 아는 벌통들이 죽 늘어서 있었으니, 저마다 제 사연을 지닌 오래된 벌통들이었고, 울타리를 따라서는 그해에 새로 들어앉힌 어린 벌 떼의 벌통들이 있었다. 벌통 입구 앞에서는 벌과 수벌이 같은 자리를 빙글빙글 맴도는 것을 보고 있노라면 눈이 어지러웠고, 그 사이로 일벌들이 전리품을 안고 혹은 그것을 찾아 드나들었는데, 언제나 같은 방향으로, 꽃이 핀 보리수가 있는 숲으로 날아갔다가 다시 벌통으로 돌아왔다.\n\n그의 귀는 갖가지 음조의 끊임없는 윙윙거림으로 가득 찼으니, 어떤 때는 부산하게 날아가는 일벌의 윙윙대는 소리였고, 또 어떤 때는 게으른 수벌의 시끄러운 소리였으며, 또 적으로부터 제 재산을 지키려고 침을 쏠 채비를 하는 보초 벌들의 흥분한 윙윙 소리였다. 울타리 저편에서는 늙은 양봉지기가 통에 끼울 테를 깎고 있었는데, 그는 레빈을 보지 못했다. 레빈은 벌통들 한복판에 가만히 서서 그를 부르지 않았다.\n\n그는 이미 자신의 행복한 기분을 짓눌러 버린 평범하고 현실적인 삶의 영향에서 벗어나 홀로 추스를 기회가 생긴 것이 기뻤다. 그는 자기가 벌써 이반에게 성을 내고, 형에게 냉랭함을 드러내고, 카타바소프와 경솔하게 떠들 시간을 가졌다는 것을 떠올렸다.\n\n\"그게 그저 한순간의 기분에 지나지 않았던 걸까, 그래서 흔적도 없이 사라져 버리는 걸까?\" 그는 생각했다. 하지만 바로 그 순간, 그 기분으로 되돌아가면서, 그는 무언가 새롭고 중요한 일이 자기에게 일어났음을 환희에 차서 느꼈다. 현실의 삶은 그가 찾아낸 정신적 평화를 잠시 흐려 놓았을 뿐, 그 평화는 그의 내면에서 여전히 손상되지 않은 채 남아 있었다.\n\n마치 그의 주위를 빙빙 돌며 때로는 위협하고 때로는 주의를 흩뜨리는 벌들이 그가 온전한 육체적 평온을 누리지 못하게 하고, 벌들을 피하려고 동작을 억누르게 만들었듯이, 마차에 올라탄 순간부터 그의 주위로 몰려든 사소한 근심거리들이 그의 정신적 자유를 옭아맸다. 하지만 그것은 그가 그것들 한가운데 있는 동안에만 그러했다. 벌들이 있어도 그의 육체적 힘이 여전히 멀쩡했던 것과 똑같이, 그가 방금 깨닫게 된 정신적 힘 또한 그러했다.\n\n# Chapter 15\n\n“코스챠, 세르게이 이바노비치가 여기 오는 길에 누구랑 같이 왔는지 알아?” 돌리가 아이들에게 오이와 꿀을 나눠 주며 말했다. “브론스키랑 왔대! 그가 세르비아로 간대.”\n\n“그것도 혼자가 아니라 자기 돈을 들여서 기병 중대 하나를 데리고 간다는군.” 카타바소프가 말했다.\n\n“그 사람한테는 딱 어울리는 일이지.” 레빈이 말했다. “그럼 의용병들이 아직도 나가고 있는 겁니까?” 그는 세르게이 이바노비치를 흘끗 바라보며 덧붙였다.\n\n세르게이 이바노비치는 대답하지 않았다. 그는 무딘 칼로, 끈끈한 꿀을 뒤집어쓴 살아 있는 벌 한 마리를 새하얀 벌집이 가득 든 잔에서 조심스럽게 꺼내고 있었다.\n\n“그럼요! 어제 정거장에서 무슨 일이 벌어졌는지 보셨어야 합니다!” 카타바소프가 즙이 흐르는 소리를 내며 오이를 베어 물고 말했다.\n\n“글쎄, 대체 그걸 어떻게 이해해야 하나? 제발, 세르게이 이바노비치, 나한테 좀 설명해 주게. 그 의용병들이 다들 어디로 가는 거고, 누구랑 싸우겠다는 건가?” 노공작이 물었다. 레빈이 없는 사이에 시작된 대화를 다시 이어 가려는 게 분명했다.\n\n“터키군과 싸우러 가는 거지요.” 세르게이 이바노비치가 평온하게 미소 지으며 대답했다. 그러고는 꿀에 절어 거뭇해진 채 힘없이 발버둥치는 벌을 꺼내, 칼로 떠서 단단한 사시나무 잎 위에 올려놓았다.\n\n“하지만 누가 터키에 선전포고를 했단 말인가? 이반 이바노비치 라고조프와 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인이, 스탈 부인의 도움을 받아서 한 건가?”\n\n“아무도 선전포고를 한 건 아닙니다. 다만 사람들이 이웃의 고통에 공감하고 그들을 돕고 싶어 하는 거지요.” 세르게이 이바노비치가 말했다.\n\n“하지만 공작님은 도움 얘기를 하시는 게 아닙니다.” 레빈이 장인을 거들고 나섰다. “전쟁 얘기를 하시는 거지요. 공작님 말씀은 사인(私人)이 정부의 허가 없이 전쟁에 참여할 수는 없다는 겁니다.”\n\n“코스챠, 조심해요, 저거 벌이에요! 정말, 우리를 쏘겠어요!” 돌리가 말벌을 휘저어 쫓으며 말했다.\n\n“그건 벌이 아니라 말벌이오.” 레빈이 말했다.\n\n“자, 그럼, 자네 자신의 이론은 뭔가?” 카타바소프가 레빈에게 미소를 지으며 말했다. 분명히 논쟁을 걸어오는 것이었다. “어째서 사인은 그럴 권리가 없다는 거지?”\n\n“오, 내 이론은 이렇습니다. 전쟁이란 한편으로는 너무나 잔인하고 끔찍하고 짐승 같은 짓이어서, 기독교인은 말할 것도 없고 어떤 사람도 개인적으로 전쟁을 시작하는 책임을 자기 어깨에 짊어질 수 없다는 거죠. 그건 오직 정부만 할 수 있는 일이고, 정부는 그렇게 하도록 부름받았으며, 불가피하게 전쟁으로 내몰리는 겁니다. 다른 한편으로, 정치학도 상식도 우리에게 가르쳐 주잖습니까. 국가의 일에서, 특히 전쟁의 일에서, 사적인 시민은 자기 개인적 의지를 포기해야 한다고 말입니다.”\n\n세르게이 이바노비치와 카타바소프는 반박할 말을 이미 준비하고 있었고, 둘이 동시에 입을 열었다.\n\n“하지만 요점은 말일세, 이보게, 정부가 시민의 의지를 실행하지 않는 경우가 있을 수 있고, 그럴 때는 대중이 자기 의지를 주장한다는 거지.” 카타바소프가 말했다.\n\n그러나 세르게이 이바노비치는 분명 이 대답이 못마땅한 모양이었다. 카타바소프의 말에 그의 눈썹이 찌푸려졌고, 그는 다른 말을 했다.\n\n“자네는 이 문제를 올바른 빛 속에서 보고 있지 않아. 여기서 선전포고가 문제가 되는 게 아니라, 그저 인간적이고 기독교적인 감정의 표현이 문제인 거지. 종교와 혈통에서 우리와 하나인 우리 형제들이 학살당하고 있어. 설령 그들이 우리 형제도 아니고 같은 기독교인도 아니라 그저 아이들이고 여자들이고 노인들이라 해도, 감정이 일어나는 거고, 러시아인들은 그 잔학 행위를 멈추도록 돕겠다고 기꺼이 달려가는 거지. 생각해 보게, 자네가 거리를 가다가 술 취한 사내들이 여자나 아이를 두들겨 패는 걸 본다면 말이야—내 생각엔 자네는 그 사내들한테 전쟁이 선포됐는지 어떤지 따지고 있지는 않을 걸세. 그자들한테 몸을 던져서 희생자를 보호하겠지.”\n\n“하지만 난 그들을 죽이지는 않겠지요.” 레빈이 말했다.\n\n“아니, 자네는 죽일 걸세.”\n\n“모르겠습니다. 그런 광경을 본다면 순간적인 충동에 굴복할지도 모르지요. 하지만 미리 단정할 수는 없습니다. 그리고 슬라브 민족이 억압당하는 경우에는 그런 순간적인 충동 같은 건 없고, 있을 수도 없습니다.”\n\n“자네한테는 없을지 몰라도, 다른 사람들한테는 있어.” 세르게이 이바노비치가 못마땅한 듯 얼굴을 찌푸리며 말했다. “‘하갈의 부정한 자손들’의 멍에 아래서 고통받는 참된 신앙의 슬라브인들에 관한 전설이 민중 사이에 아직도 살아 있어. 민중은 자기 형제들의 고통에 관해 들었고, 입을 연 거지.”\n\n“그럴지도 모르죠.” 레빈이 얼버무리며 말했다. “하지만 저는 그게 보이지 않습니다. 저 자신이 민중의 한 사람인데, 저는 그걸 느끼지 못한단 말이에요.”\n\n“나도 마찬가지일세.” 노공작이 말했다. “난 외국에 머물면서 신문을 읽었는데, 솔직히 말해서 불가리아 잔학 사건이 일어나기 전까지는 어째서 러시아인들이 죄다 갑자기 슬라브 형제들을 그렇게 끔찍이 사랑하게 됐는지 도무지 알 수가 없었어. 나는 그들에게 손톱만큼의 애정도 느끼지 못했으니까. 몹시 마음이 언짢았고, 내가 괴물인가, 아니면 카를스바트 물이 나한테 영향을 미친 건가 생각했지. 그런데 여기 온 뒤로 마음이 놓이더군. 나 말고도 슬라브 형제들이 아니라 오직 러시아에만 관심이 있는 사람들이 있다는 걸 알게 됐거든. 여기 콘스탄친도 그렇지 않나.”\n\n“이런 경우에 개인적인 의견은 아무 의미도 없어요.” 세르게이 이바노비치가 말했다. “러시아 전체가—민중 전체가—그 의지를 표명했을 때는 개인적인 의견의 문제가 아닌 거죠.”\n\n“하지만 미안하지만, 난 그게 보이지 않아. 따지고 보면 민중은 그것에 대해 아무것도 모른다고.” 노공작이 말했다.\n\n“어머, 아빠!…… 어떻게 그런 말씀을 하세요? 지난 일요일 교회에서는요?” 대화를 듣고 있던 돌리가 말했다. “행주 좀 주세요.” 그녀는 미소를 지으며 아이들을 바라보고 있던 노인에게 말했다. “설마 다들 그게……”\n\n“하지만 일요일 교회에서 그게 뭐였느냐? 사제더러 그걸 읽으라고 시킨 거고, 사제가 읽었지. 사람들은 한 마디도 못 알아들었어. 그러고는 교회에서 경건한 목적으로 헌금을 걷는다고 하니까, 글쎄, 다들 동전 몇 닢 꺼내서 냈지. 하지만 그게 무엇을 위한 건지는 말도 못 했단 말이야.”\n\n“민중은 모를 수가 없습니다. 자신의 운명에 대한 감각은 언제나 민중 속에 있고, 지금 같은 순간에는 그 감각이 표현되는 거지요.” 세르게이 이바노비치가 확신에 차서 말하며, 늙은 양봉가를 흘끗 바라보았다.\n\n검은빛이 섞인 희끗한 수염과 숱 많은 은발을 지닌 잘생긴 노인은, 꿀이 든 잔을 들고 꼼짝도 않고 서서, 큰 키의 높이에서 다정하고 평온한 눈으로 신사들을 내려다보고 있었다. 그들의 대화는 분명 한 마디도 이해하지 못하는 데다, 이해하려는 마음도 없어 보였다.\n\n“그렇고말고요, 틀림없습니다.” 그는 세르게이 이바노비치의 말에 의미심장하게 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다.\n\n“자, 그럼, 이 사람한테 물어보십시오. 이 사람은 그것에 대해 아무것도 모르고 아무 생각도 없습니다.” 레빈이 말했다. “미할리치, 전쟁에 대해 들었소?” 그가 노인을 돌아보며 말했다. “교회에서 읽어 준 것 말이오. 그걸 어떻게 생각하오? 우리가 기독교인들을 위해 싸워야 한단 말이오?”\n\n“우리가 뭘 생각하겠습니까? 우리 황제 알렉산드르 니콜라예비치께서 우리를 위해 생각해 주시지요. 정말이지 모든 일에서 우리를 위해 생각해 주십니다. 그분께는 더 잘 보이는 법이니까요. 빵을 좀 더 가져올까요? 도련님께 더 드릴까요?” 그는 다랴 알렉산드로브나를 향해 말하며, 빵 껍질을 다 먹은 그리샤를 가리켰다.\n\n“난 물어볼 필요도 없네.” 세르게이 이바노비치가 말했다. “우리는 정의로운 대의를 위해 모든 걸 버리고, 러시아 방방곡곡에서 와서 자기 생각과 목적을 직접적이고 분명하게 표현하는 사람들을 수백, 수백 명씩 보아 왔고, 지금도 보고 있어. 그들은 동전을 가져오거나 직접 떠나면서 무엇을 위한 것인지 똑바로 말하지. 그게 무슨 뜻이겠나?”\n\n“제 생각엔 그게 이런 뜻입니다.” 점점 흥분하기 시작한 레빈이 말했다. “팔천만 명의 사람들 중에는 언제든, 지금처럼 수백 명이 아니라 수만 명의, 신분을 잃고 빌빌대는 건달들이 있게 마련이라는 거죠. 그자들은 언제든 어디로든—푸가초프의 무리로든, 히바로든, 세르비아로든—기꺼이 떠날 준비가 돼 있는 자들이고요…….”\n\n“수백 명이니 건달이니 하는 게 아니라, 민중의 가장 훌륭한 대표자들이라고 말하지 않나!” 세르게이 이바노비치가 마치 제 재산의 마지막 한 푼을 지키기라도 하는 양 잔뜩 짜증을 내며 말했다. “그럼 헌금은 또 어떻고? 이 경우엔 한 민족 전체가 직접 자기 의지를 표현하고 있는 거지.”\n\n“그 ‘민중’이라는 말이 너무 막연합니다.” 레빈이 말했다. “교구 서기들, 교사들, 그리고 어쩌면 천 명에 한 명꼴인 농민들이나 이게 다 무슨 일인지 알겠지요. 나머지 팔천만 명은 미할리치처럼, 자기 의지를 표현하기는커녕 자기들이 무엇에 대해 의지를 표현해야 하는지 짐작조차 못 하는 사람들이에요. 그런데 우리한테 무슨 권리가 있어서 이게 민중의 의지라고 말한단 말입니까?”\n\n# Chapter 16\n\n세르게이 이바노비치는 논쟁에 단련된 사람이었으므로 곧바로 반박하지 않고, 화제를 그 문제의 다른 측면으로 돌렸다.\n\n\"오, 인민의 정신을 산술적 계산으로 알아내려 한다면야 물론 거기에 다다르기란 무척 어렵지. 게다가 투표는 우리에게 도입되지 않았고 또 도입될 수도 없어. 그건 인민의 의지를 표현하지 못하니까. 하지만 거기에 이르는 다른 길들이 있단 말이야. 그건 공기 중에서 느껴지고, 가슴으로 느껴지지. 편견 없는 사람이라면 누구에게나 분명히 보이는, 저 인민이라는 고요한 대양 속에서 일렁이는 깊은 물결에 대해서는 말하지 않겠어. 좁은 의미의 사회를 한번 보자고. 전에는 서로 적대하던, 교육받은 대중의 가장 다양한 부류들이 하나로 합쳐졌어. 모든 분열은 끝났고, 모든 언론 기관이 똑같은 말을 거듭거듭 되풀이하고 있으며, 모두가 자기들을 사로잡아 한 방향으로 실어 나르는 거대한 격류를 느끼고 있어.\"\n\n\"그래, 모든 신문이 똑같은 말을 하긴 하지.\" 공작이 말했다. \"그건 사실이야. 하지만 폭풍 전에 개구리들이 죄다 똑같이 우는 것도 마찬가지란 말이지. 그것들 때문에 아무 소리도 들리지 않거든.\"\n\n\"개구리든 아니든, 나는 신문 편집자도 아니고 그들을 옹호하고 싶지도 않습니다. 다만 나는 지식인 세계의 만장일치를 말하고 있는 거예요.\" 세르게이 이바노비치가 동생을 향해 말했다. 레빈이 뭐라 대답하려 했지만 늙은 공작이 가로막았다.\n\n\"글쎄, 그 만장일치라는 것 말인데, 그건 또 다른 얘기라고 할 수 있지.\" 공작이 말했다. \"내 사위 스테판 아르카지치, 자네도 알지 않나. 그 친구가 지금 무슨 위원회의 어느 분과인지 뭔지에 자리를 하나 얻었는데, 나도 기억이 안 나. 다만 거기서 할 일이라고는 아무것도 없단 말이지—아니 돌리, 이건 비밀도 아니잖니!—그런데 봉급은 팔천이야. 그 친구한테 한번 물어보게나, 자네 자리가 쓸모가 있느냐고. 그러면 그게 가장 필요한 자리라는 걸 자네한테 증명해 보일 거야. 게다가 그 친구는 정직한 사람이거든. 하지만 팔천 루블의 유용성을 믿지 않을 도리가 없는 거지.\"\n\n\"그렇잖아도 그 자리에 관해 다리아 알렉산드로브나에게 전해 달라고 부탁하더군요.\" 세르게이 이바노비치가 마지못해 말했다. 그는 공작의 말이 때에 맞지 않는다고 느꼈다.\n\n\"언론의 만장일치라는 것도 마찬가지야. 누가 나한테 설명해 줬는데, 전쟁이 나기만 하면 그자들 수입이 두 배가 된다는군. 그러니 어떻게 인민과 슬라브 민족의 운명을…… 그 밖에 온갖 것들을 믿지 않을 수 있겠나?\"\n\n\"신문 중에는 내 마음에 안 드는 게 많긴 하지만, 그건 부당한 말씀입니다.\" 세르게이 이바노비치가 말했다.\n\n\"나는 단 한 가지 조건만 달겠네.\" 늙은 공작이 말을 이었다. \"알퐁스 카르가 프로이센과의 전쟁 전에 기막힌 말을 했지. '당신은 전쟁이 불가피하다고 보시오? 아주 좋소. 그렇다면 전쟁을 주창하는 자는 모조리 특별 선발 전위 연대에 편입시켜, 모든 폭풍의 맨 앞, 모든 공격의 선봉에 세워 그들 전부를 이끌게 합시다!'\"\n\n\"편집자들이 좋은 부대를 만들겠군요!\" 카타바소프가 그 선발된 군단 속에 자기가 아는 편집자들을 그려 보며 큰 소리로 껄껄 웃었다.\n\n\"하지만 그자들은 도망칠 거예요.\" 돌리가 말했다. \"방해만 될 뿐이죠.\"\n\n\"오, 도망친다면 그 뒤에 산탄포나 채찍을 든 카자크 병사들을 세워 두면 되지.\" 공작이 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 그건 농담이고, 죄송하지만 그것도 시원찮은 농담입니다, 공작님.\" 세르게이 이바노비치가 말했다.\n\n\"나는 그게 농담인지 모르겠는데, 그게…….\" 레빈이 말을 꺼내려 했지만 세르게이 이바노비치가 가로막았다.\n\n\"사회의 모든 구성원은 저마다 자기 고유의 일을 하도록 부름을 받은 거야.\" 그가 말했다. \"그리고 사상가들은 여론을 표현할 때 자기 일을 하는 거지. 여론의 순수하고 충만한 표현이야말로 언론의 봉사이며, 동시에 우리를 기쁘게 하는 현상이야. 이십 년 전이었다면 우리는 침묵하고 있었겠지만, 이제 우리는 러시아 인민의 목소리를 들었어. 한 사람처럼 떨쳐 일어나 박해받는 형제들을 위해 자신을 희생할 준비가 된 그 목소리를 말이야. 그건 위대한 진보이며 힘의 증거지.\"\n\n\"하지만 그건 단지 희생하는 게 아니라 터키인을 죽이는 일이잖아요.\" 레빈이 머뭇거리며 말했다. \"인민은 자기들의 영혼을 위해 희생을 하고 또 희생할 준비도 되어 있지만, 살인을 위해서는 아니에요.\" 그는 자기 마음을 사로잡고 있던 생각들과 이 대화를 본능적으로 연결시키며 덧붙였다.\n\n\"영혼을 위해서라고? 그건 자연과학을 하는 사람에게는 참으로 알쏭달쏭한 표현인데, 알겠나? 영혼이라는 게 도대체 어떤 거지?\" 카타바소프가 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"아, 알면서 그러시는군요!\"\n\n\"아니, 맹세코 난 도무지 모르겠는걸!\" 카타바소프가 큰 소리로 웃음을 터뜨리며 말했다.\n\n\"'나는 평화가 아니라 칼을 주러 왔노라'라고 그리스도께서 말씀하셨지.\" 세르게이 이바노비치가 자기 입장에서 응수하며, 늘 레빈을 가장 곤혹스럽게 했던 바로 그 구절을 마치 이해하기에 더없이 쉬운 것이라도 되는 양 간단히 인용했다.\n\n\"그렇고말고, 의심할 여지가 없지.\" 늙은 공작이 다시 한번 되뇌었다. 그는 그들 가까이에 서 있다가 자기 쪽으로 우연히 던져진 시선에 응답한 것이었다.\n\n\"아하, 이보게, 자네 졌네, 완전히 졌어!\" 카타바소프가 유쾌하게 외쳤다.\n\n레빈은 분해서 얼굴이 붉어졌는데, 진 것 때문이 아니라 자제하지 못하고 논쟁에 말려든 것 때문이었다.\n\n'아냐, 난 저 사람들과 논쟁할 수 없어.' 그는 생각했다. '저들은 뚫리지 않는 갑옷을 입고 있는데, 나는 알몸이잖아.'\n\n그는 형과 카타바소프를 설득하는 것이 불가능하다는 것을 알았고, 자기가 그들에게 동의하는 것은 그보다 더 불가능하다는 것을 알았다. 그들이 주창하는 것은 바로 그를 거의 파멸시킬 뻔했던 그 지성의 오만이었다. 그는 몇십 명의 사람들이, 그중에는 자기 형도 끼여 있지만, 수도로 몰려드는 수백 명의 말 잘하는 의용병들에게 들은 이야기를 근거로, 자기들과 신문이 인민의 의지와 감정을 표현하고 있다고, 그것도 복수와 살인으로 표출되는 감정을 표현하고 있다고 말할 권리가 있다는 것을 인정할 수 없었다. 그는 이것을 인정할 수 없었는데, 자기가 살아가는 인민들 속에서도 그런 감정의 표현을 보지 못했고, 자기 자신 안에서도 그것을 찾지 못했기 때문이며(그리고 그는 자신을 러시아 인민을 구성하는 사람들 중 하나로 여기지 않을 수 없었다), 무엇보다도 그 자신이 인민과 마찬가지로 무엇이 공공의 선인지 알지 못하고 또 알 수도 없었기 때문이다. 다만 그는 이 공공의 선이 모든 사람에게 계시된 옳고 그름의 그 법칙을 엄격히 준수함으로써만 이룰 수 있다는 것을 의심의 여지 없이 알고 있었기에, 그 어떤 공공의 목적을 위해서도 전쟁을 바랄 수도, 전쟁을 주창할 수도 없었다. 그는 미할리치가 한 말처럼, 그리고 바랴기를 부르는 전통적인 초청의 말로 자기 감정을 표현한 인민들처럼 말했다. \"공작이 되어 우리를 다스려 주소서. 우리는 기꺼이 완전한 복종을 약속하겠나이다. 모든 노고, 모든 굴욕, 모든 희생을 우리 스스로 떠맡겠나이다. 다만 우리는 판단하지도 결정하지도 않겠나이다.\" 그런데 세르게이 이바노비치의 이야기대로라면, 인민은 그토록 비싼 값을 치르고 사들인 이 특권을 이제 스스로 포기해 버린 셈이었다.\n\n그는 이런 말도 하고 싶었다. 만약 여론이 오류 없는 길잡이라면, 그렇다면 어째서 혁명이나 코뮌은 슬라브 민족을 위한 운동만큼 정당한 것이 못 되느냐고. 하지만 이런 것들은 아무것도 해결할 수 없는 한낱 생각에 지나지 않았다. 의심의 여지 없이 분명한 것은 단 하나—바로 이 순간 이 논의가 세르게이 이바노비치를 짜증나게 하고 있으며, 따라서 그것을 계속하는 것은 잘못이라는 사실이었다. 레빈은 말을 멈추고, 이어 손님들에게 먹구름이 몰려들고 있으니 비가 오기 전에 집으로 돌아가는 편이 낫겠다고 일러 주었다.\n\n# Chapter 17\n\n노공작과 세르게이 이바노비치는 트랩에 올라 떠났고, 나머지 일행은 서둘러 집으로 걸어갔다.\n\n그러나 하얗게 변했다가 다시 검게 변하는 먹구름이 어찌나 빠르게 내려오는지, 그들은 비가 오기 전에 집에 닿으려고 걸음을 재촉해야 했다. 맨 앞의 구름은 그을음을 잔뜩 머금은 연기처럼 낮게 깔리고 새카매서, 보통이 아닌 빠르기로 하늘을 가로질러 내달았다. 집까지는 아직 이백 보쯤 남아 있었는데 이미 한바탕 돌풍이 불어 닥쳤고, 당장이라도 폭우가 쏟아질 것만 같았다.\n\n아이들은 겁에 질리면서도 즐거운 비명을 지르며 앞장서 달려갔다. 다리아 알렉산드로브나는 다리에 휘감기는 치맛자락과 괴롭게 씨름하면서, 걷는 게 아니라 뛰다시피 했고, 두 눈은 아이들에게 고정되어 있었다. 일행의 남자들은 모자를 손으로 누른 채 그녀 곁에서 성큼성큼 큰 걸음으로 걸었다. 그들이 막 현관 계단에 다다랐을 때, 큰 빗방울 하나가 철제 빗물받이 가장자리에 철퍽 떨어졌다. 아이들이, 그리고 그 뒤를 따라 어른들이 즐겁게 떠들며 집 안의 처마 밑으로 뛰어 들어갔다.\n\n\"카체리나 알렉산드로브나는?\" 레빈이 손수건과 무릎 덮개를 들고 현관에서 그들을 맞은 아가피야 미하일로브나에게 물었다.\n\n\"우리는 나리와 함께 계신 줄 알았는데요.\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"미챠는?\"\n\n\"숲 속에 계실 거예요, 유모랑 같이요.\"\n\n레빈은 무릎 덮개를 낚아채 숲을 향해 달려갔다.\n\n그 짧은 사이에 먹구름은 더 멀리 이동해 해를 완전히 가려 버려, 일식이라도 일어난 듯 어두웠다. 마치 제 권리를 우기기라도 하듯 고집스럽게 바람이 레빈을 가로막았고, 보리수의 잎과 꽃을 뜯어내고 흰 자작나무 가지들을 기이하고 흉하게 발가벗기며, 아카시아며 꽃이며 우엉이며 긴 풀이며 키 큰 나무 우듬지며 모든 것을 한쪽으로 비틀어 놓았다. 정원에서 일하던 농가 처녀들이 비명을 지르며 하인 숙소로 피해 달려갔다. 쏟아지는 비는 이미 저 멀리 숲 전체와 가까운 들판 절반에 하얀 장막을 드리웠고, 빠르게 숲을 향해 덮쳐 오고 있었다. 잘게 부서지며 튀어 오르는 빗물의 축축한 냄새가 공기 중에 풍겼다.\n\n레빈은 머리를 앞으로 숙인 채, 덮개를 자기에게서 떼어 내려고 기를 쓰는 바람과 씨름하며 숲으로 다가가고 있었고, 막 떡갈나무 뒤편의 하얀 무언가를 발견한 순간, 별안간 번쩍하는 섬광이 일더니 온 땅이 불타오르는 듯하고 하늘의 천장이 머리 위로 무너져 내리는 것 같았다. 멀어 버린 눈을 뜨고 레빈은 이제 그를 숲에서 갈라놓는 두터운 빗줄기의 장막 너머를 응시했고, 끔찍하게도 가장 먼저 눈에 들어온 것은 숲 한가운데 낯익은 떡갈나무의 푸른 우듬지가 으스스하게 위치를 바꾸는 모습이었다. \"벼락을 맞은 건가?\" 레빈은 미처 생각할 겨를도 없이, 점점 더 빠르게 움직이던 떡갈나무가 다른 나무들 뒤로 사라지는 것을 보았고, 그 거대한 나무가 다른 나무들 위로 쓰러지는 굉음을 들었다.\n\n번개의 섬광, 천둥의 굉음, 그리고 순간적으로 온몸을 훑고 지나간 한기, 이 모든 것이 레빈에게는 하나의 공포감으로 뒤엉켰다.\n\n\"오, 하느님! 하느님! 그들에게는 안 됩니다!\" 그가 말했다.\n\n그리고 방금 쓰러진 떡갈나무에 그들이 깔려 죽지 않게 해 달라는 자기 기도가 얼마나 부질없는 것인지 곧바로 떠올렸음에도, 그는 이 부질없는 기도를 입에 올리는 것 말고는 달리 할 수 있는 일이 없음을 알면서도 그것을 거듭 되뇌었다.\n\n그들이 늘 가던 곳까지 달려갔지만, 거기서 그들을 찾지 못했다.\n\n그들은 숲의 다른 쪽 끝, 늙은 보리수 아래에 있었다. 그들이 그를 부르고 있었다. 검은 옷차림(처음 나섰을 때는 가벼운 여름 옷차림이었다)의 두 사람이 무언가를 굽어보며 서 있었다. 키티와 유모였다. 레빈이 그들에게 닿았을 때 비는 이미 그쳐 가고 있었고 사위가 밝아지기 시작했다. 유모는 옷의 아랫부분이 젖지 않았지만, 키티는 흠뻑 젖어 흠씬 적은 옷이 몸에 들러붙어 있었다. 비는 그쳤건만, 그들은 폭풍우가 닥쳤을 때 서 있던 그 자리에 그대로 서 있었다. 둘 다 초록 양산이 달린 유모차 위로 몸을 굽히고 있었다.\n\n\"살아 있어? 다친 데 없어? 다행이다!\" 그는 흠뻑 젖은 장화로 고인 물을 철벅거리며 그들에게 달려가 말했다.\n\n키티의 발그레하고 젖은 얼굴이 그를 향해 돌려졌고, 그녀는 형태를 잃고 흠뻑 젖은 모자 아래에서 머뭇머뭇 미소를 지었다.\n\n\"부끄러운 줄도 모르나? 어쩌면 이렇게 무모할 수가 있어!\" 그가 아내에게 화를 내며 말했다.\n\n\"정말이지 내 잘못이 아니에요. 우리도 막 가려던 참이었는데, 이 애가 어찌나 칭얼대는지 기저귀를 갈아 줘야 했어요. 우리는 그저…….\" 키티가 자신을 변호하기 시작했다.\n\n미챠는 다친 데 없이 보송보송하게 여전히 곤히 자고 있었다.\n\n\"그래, 다행이군! 내가 무슨 소리를 하는지 모르겠어!\"\n\n그들은 아기의 젖은 물건들을 챙겼다. 유모가 아기를 안아 들고 갔다. 레빈은 아내 곁에서 걸었고, 화를 낸 것을 뉘우치며 유모가 보지 않을 때 아내의 손을 꼭 쥐었다.\n\n# Chapter 18\n\n그날 하루 종일 레빈은, 자기 마음의 가장 윗부분으로만 끼어든 갖가지 잡다한 대화 속에서, 그리고 자기 안에서 기대했던 변화를 찾지 못한 실망에도 불구하고, 내내 가슴이 충만하다는 것을 기쁘게 의식하고 있었다.\n\n비가 온 뒤라 땅이 너무 질척여서 산책을 나갈 수 없었다. 게다가 먹구름이 여전히 지평선 언저리에 걸려 있다가 하늘 가장자리 여기저기에 검고 우레가 칠 듯 모여들곤 했다. 일행은 남은 하루를 모두 집 안에서 보냈다.\n\n더 이상 논쟁은 일어나지 않았다. 오히려 식사가 끝나자 모두가 더없이 다정한 기분에 젖어 있었다.\n\n처음에는 카타바소프가 특유의 기발한 농담으로 부인들을 즐겁게 했는데, 그것은 그를 처음 알게 된 사람들을 늘 흐뭇하게 만드는 것이었다. 그러고 나서 세르게이 이바노비치가 그를 부추겨, 그가 평범한 집파리의 습성과 특징, 그 생태에 관해 관찰한 무척 흥미로운 이야기를 들려주게 했다. 세르게이 이바노비치도 기분이 좋았고, 차를 마실 때 동생이 그를 이끌어 동방 문제의 장래에 대한 견해를 풀어놓게 하자, 그는 어찌나 간결하고 또 훌륭하게 말하던지 모두가 열심히 귀를 기울였다.\n\n다만 키티만은 그 이야기를 끝까지 듣지 못했다. 미차를 목욕시키러 오라는 부름을 받았기 때문이다.\n\n키티가 방을 나간 지 몇 분 뒤, 그녀는 레빈을 육아실로 오라며 사람을 보냈다.\n\n레빈은 마시던 차를 내려놓고, 흥미로운 대화를 아쉬운 마음으로 끊으면서, 동시에 왜 자기를 부르는지 불안하게 궁금해하며—이런 일은 중요한 경우에만 일어났기 때문이다—육아실로 갔다.\n\n러시아와 함께 행동하는 슬라브 민족 사천만 명의 해방이 빚어낼 역사의 새로운 시대에 대한 세르게이 이바노비치의 견해, 그에게는 아주 새로운 그 발상에 그는 무척 흥미를 느꼈고, 또 키티가 자기를 부른 데에 불안한 의구심을 품고 있었지만, 응접실에서 나와 혼자가 되자마자 그의 마음은 곧장 아침의 상념으로 되돌아갔다. 그리고 세계 역사 속에서 슬라브 요소가 갖는 의의에 관한 온갖 이론들이, 자기 영혼 안에서 일어나고 있는 일에 비하면 너무도 하찮게 느껴져서, 그는 단숨에 그 모든 것을 잊고 그날 아침에 잠겨 있던 바로 그 심경으로 되돌아갔다.\n\n그는 다른 때 그랬던 것처럼 생각의 전 과정을 되짚지는 않았다. 그럴 필요가 없었다. 그는 곧바로, 자기를 이끌어 온, 그 상념들과 이어져 있던 그 느낌 속으로 다시 빠져들었고, 그 느낌이 자기 영혼 안에서 전보다 더 강하고 더 또렷해진 것을 발견했다. 위안이 되는 논거를 찾으려던 이전의 시도에서 그래야 했던 것과 달리, 이제는 그 느낌을 되살리기 위해 일련의 생각 전체를 되살릴 필요가 없었다. 오히려 지금은 기쁨과 평화의 느낌이 그 어느 때보다 또렷했고, 생각이 느낌을 따라잡지 못했다.\n\n그는 테라스를 가로질러 걸으며 어두워지는 하늘에 막 돋아난 별 두 개를 바라보았고, 문득 떠올랐다. \"그래, 하늘을 올려다보면서, 내가 보는 저 둥근 천장이 속임수가 아니라고 생각했지. 그러고 나서 뭔가를 생각했는데, 어떤 것과 마주하기를 회피했어.\" 그는 곰곰이 생각했다. \"하지만 그게 무엇이었든, 그걸 반박할 수는 없어! 나는 그저 생각하기만 하면 되고, 그러면 모든 게 분명해질 거야!\"\n\n육아실로 막 들어서려는 순간 그는 자기가 마주하기를 회피했던 것이 무엇인지 떠올렸다. 그것은, 신성의 으뜸가는 증거가 무엇이 옳은지를 드러내 보인 그분의 계시라면, 어째서 이 계시가 오직 기독교 교회에만 국한되어 있는가 하는 것이었다. 마찬가지로 설교하고 선을 행한 불교도며 이슬람교도의 신앙은 이 계시와 어떤 관계가 있는가?\n\n그는 이 물음에 대한 답을 가지고 있는 것 같았다. 그러나 그것을 스스로에게 정리해 표현할 틈도 없이 육아실로 들어섰다.\n\n키티는 소매를 걷어 올린 채 욕조 안의 아기 위로 몸을 굽히고 서 있었다. 남편의 발소리를 듣자 그녀는 그를 향해 돌아서며 미소로 그를 곁으로 불렀다. 한 손으로는 등을 대고 둥둥 떠서 사지를 버둥거리는 통통한 아기를 받치고, 다른 손으로는 아기 위로 해면을 짜고 있었다.\n\n\"이리 와서 좀 봐요, 봐!\" 남편이 다가오자 그녀가 말했다. \"아가피야 미하일로브나 말이 맞아요. 이 애가 우리를 알아본다니까요!\"\n\n그날 미차는 자기 식구들을 모두 알아본다는 틀림없고 부정할 수 없는 신호를 보였던 것이다.\n\n레빈이 욕조에 다가서자마자 실험이 행해졌고, 그것은 완전히 성공이었다. 이 일을 위해 불려 온 요리사가 아기 위로 몸을 굽혔다. 아기는 얼굴을 찌푸리며 못마땅한 듯 고개를 저었다. 키티가 아기에게 몸을 굽히자, 아기는 그녀에게 환한 미소를 지어 보이고는 작은 두 손을 해면에 짚고 짹짹거렸는데, 입술로 어찌나 묘하고 만족스러운 작은 소리를 내던지, 감탄한 것은 키티와 유모만이 아니었다. 레빈도 놀라고 기뻐했다.\n\n아기는 물에 흠뻑 젖은 채 욕조에서 들려 나와 수건에 싸여 물기가 닦였고, 찢어질 듯한 비명을 한 차례 지른 뒤 어머니에게 건네졌다.\n\n\"자, 당신이 이 애를 사랑하기 시작했다니 정말 기뻐요.\" 키티가 아기를 가슴에 안고 늘 앉던 자리에 편안히 자리를 잡은 뒤 남편에게 말했다. \"정말 기뻐요! 그동안 그게 나를 괴롭히기 시작했거든요. 당신은 이 애한테 아무 정도 느껴지지 않는다고 했잖아요.\"\n\n\"아니, 내가 그렇게 말했나? 그저 실망했다고 했을 뿐인데.\"\n\n\"뭐라고요! 이 애한테 실망했다고요?\"\n\n\"이 애한테 실망한 게 아니라, 내 자신의 감정에 실망했다는 거야. 더 많은 걸 기대했거든. 새롭고 황홀한 감정이 뜻밖의 선물처럼 와락 밀려들 거라고 기대했지. 그런데 그 대신에—혐오감, 연민…….\"\n\n그녀는 미차를 목욕시키느라 빼 두었던 반지들을 가느다란 손가락에 다시 끼우면서, 아기 너머로 그를 바라보며 주의 깊게 귀를 기울였다.\n\n\"그리고 무엇보다도, 기쁨보다 두려움과 연민이 훨씬 더 컸다는 거야. 오늘, 폭풍이 칠 때 그렇게 겁에 질리고 나서야, 내가 이 애를 얼마나 사랑하는지 깨달았어.\"\n\n키티의 미소가 환하게 빛났다.\n\n\"많이 무서웠어요?\" 그녀가 말했다. \"나도 그랬어요. 그런데 다 지나고 나니 이제 더 실감이 나네요. 나중에 그 떡갈나무를 보러 갈래요. 카타바소프는 참 좋은 사람이에요! 그리고 오늘 우리 모두 얼마나 즐거운 하루를 보냈는지 몰라요. 그리고 당신, 마음만 먹으면 세르게이 이바노비치한테 참 다정하게 굴잖아요…… 자, 이제 그분들한테 돌아가요. 목욕시키고 나면 여긴 늘 이렇게 덥고 김이 자욱해서요.\"\n\n# Chapter 19\n\n육아실을 나와 다시 혼자가 되자, 레빈은 곧바로 무언가 분명치 않은 점이 남아 있던 그 생각으로 되돌아갔다.\n\n목소리가 들려오는 응접실로 들어가는 대신, 그는 테라스에 멈춰 서서 난간에 팔꿈치를 괴고 하늘을 올려다보았다.\n\n이제 사방이 어두웠고, 그가 바라보고 있는 남쪽에는 구름 한 점 없었다. 폭풍우는 하늘 반대편으로 흘러갔고, 그쪽에서 번개가 번쩍이고 먼 천둥소리가 들려왔다. 레빈은 정원의 보리수에서 단조롭게 떨어지는 물방울 소리에 귀를 기울이며, 자기가 너무도 잘 아는 별들의 삼각형과, 그 한가운데를 지르며 갈래갈래 뻗어 있는 은하수를 바라보았다. 번개가 번쩍일 때마다 은하수도, 심지어 밝은 별들도 사라졌지만, 번개가 잦아들기가 무섭게 별들은 마치 어떤 손이 정성껏 겨냥해 도로 던져 놓기라도 한 듯 제자리에 다시 나타났다.\n\n\"자, 무엇이 나를 이토록 혼란스럽게 하는 걸까?\" 레빈은 자기 어려움의 해답이 영혼 속에 이미 준비되어 있다는 것을 미처 알지 못한 채 그저 미리 느끼면서 혼잣말을 했다. \"그래, 신성이 틀림없고 다툴 여지 없이 드러나는 단 하나의 발현은 선과 악의 법칙이야. 그 법칙은 계시를 통해 세상에 들어왔고, 나는 그것을 내 안에서 느끼지. 그리고 그것을 인정함으로써—내가 스스로 그렇게 만드는 게 아니라, 내가 원하든 원치 않든—나는 교회라 불리는 신자들의 한 몸 안에서 다른 사람들과 하나가 되는 거야. 자, 그런데 유대인, 마호메트교도, 공자교도, 불교도는—이들은 어떻게 되는 거지?\" 그는 직면하기를 두려워했던 물음을 스스로에게 던졌다. \"이 수억의 사람들이 그것 없이는 삶이 아무 의미도 없는 그 지고의 축복을 빼앗긴 채로 살아갈 수 있단 말인가?\" 그는 잠시 곰곰이 생각하다가, 곧바로 자신을 바로잡았다. \"그런데 내가 무엇을 묻고 있는 거지?\" 그는 스스로에게 말했다. \"나는 온 인류의 온갖 다양한 종교들이 신성과 맺는 관계를 묻고 있어. 나는 그 모든 안개 같은 흐릿함을 안고서 신이 온 세상에 보편적으로 발현하는 것을 묻고 있어. 내가 지금 무얼 하고 있는 건가? 나라는 개인에게, 내 마음에게는 의심의 여지없는, 이성으로는 도달할 수 없는 앎이 계시되었는데, 나는 여기서 그 앎을 이성과 말로 표현하려고 고집스럽게 애쓰고 있는 거야.\n\n\"별들이 움직이지 않는다는 걸 내가 모를 리 있나?\" 그는 자작나무의 가장 높은 가지 끝까지 위치를 옮겨 간 저 밝은 별을 바라보며 자문했다. \"하지만 별들의 움직임을 바라보면서 나는 지구의 회전을 머릿속에 그릴 수가 없어. 그러니 별들이 움직인다고 말하는 내가 옳은 거야.\n\n\"그리고 만약 천문학자들이 지구의 그 복잡하고 다양한 온갖 운동을 다 고려했더라면, 무언가를 이해하고 계산해 낼 수 있었을까? 천체들의 거리며 무게며 운동이며 편차에 관해 그들이 도달한 그 모든 경이로운 결론은, 오로지 정지해 있는 지구를 중심으로 한 천체들의 겉보기 운동에, 곧 지금 내 눈앞에 펼쳐져 있는, 오랜 세월 동안 수백만 사람들에게 그러했고 또 언제나 한결같이 그러했으며 또 그러할, 그래서 언제나 믿을 수 있는 바로 그 운동에 근거를 두고 있을 뿐이야. 그리고 천문학자들의 결론이 단 하나의 자오선과 단 하나의 지평선을 기준으로 한 눈에 보이는 하늘의 관측에 근거를 두지 않았다면 헛되고 불확실했을 것처럼, 내 결론도 모든 사람에게 한결같이 그러했고 또 그러할 선의 관념에, 곧 그리스도교도인 내게 계시되었고 또 내 영혼 속에서 언제나 믿을 수 있는 그 관념에 근거를 두지 않았다면 헛되고 불확실했을 거야. 다른 종교들과 그것들이 신성과 맺는 관계의 문제를 결정할 권리도, 결정할 가능성도 내게는 없어.\"\n\n\"어머, 아직 안 들어가셨어요?\" 같은 길을 지나 응접실로 가던 키티의 목소리가 별안간 들려왔다.\n\n\"무슨 일이에요? 무슨 걱정이라도 있으세요?\" 그녀가 별빛 속에서 그의 얼굴을 유심히 들여다보며 물었다.\n\n하지만 번개 한 줄기가 별들을 감추고 그의 얼굴을 드러내지 않았더라면 그녀는 그의 얼굴을 볼 수 없었을 것이다. 그 번갯불 속에서 그녀는 그의 얼굴을 또렷이 보았고, 그가 차분하고 행복해 보이자 그를 향해 미소 지었다.\n\n\"이 사람은 알고 있어.\" 그는 생각했다. \"내가 무슨 생각을 하고 있는지 알고 있어. 이 사람한테 말할까 말까? 그래, 말해야지.\" 그러나 그가 막 입을 열려는 순간, 그녀가 먼저 말을 꺼냈다.\n\n\"코스챠! 부탁 하나만 들어줘요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"구석방에 가서 세르게이 이바노비치를 위해 다 제대로 준비됐는지 좀 봐 줘요. 내가 가기는 좀 그래서요. 새 세면대를 거기 들여놓았는지 봐 주세요.\"\n\n\"좋아, 지금 곧 가 보지.\" 레빈이 일어나 그녀에게 입을 맞추며 말했다.\n\n\"아니야, 이건 말하지 않는 편이 낫겠어.\" 그녀가 그보다 먼저 안으로 들어가자 그는 생각했다. \"이건 오직 나만의 비밀이고, 내게 더없이 중대한 것이며, 말로 옮길 수 있는 게 아니야.\n\n\"이 새로운 감정은 나를 바꾸지도 않았고, 내가 꿈꾸었던 것처럼 나를 갑작스레 행복하고 깨우치게 만들지도 않았어. 내 아이에 대한 감정과 꼭 마찬가지로 말이야. 여기에도 놀라움은 없었어. 믿음이든—아니면 믿음이 아니든—그게 무엇인지 나는 모르지만, 이 감정은 고통을 통해 똑같이 어느새 슬며시 찾아왔고, 내 영혼 속에 단단히 뿌리를 내렸어.\n\n\"나는 예전과 똑같은 모습으로 살아갈 거야. 마부 이반에게 성을 내고, 화를 내며 따지고 들고, 요령 없이 내 의견을 내뱉으면서. 여전히 내 영혼의 지성소와 다른 사람들 사이에는, 심지어 내 아내와의 사이에도 똑같은 벽이 가로놓여 있을 거고, 나는 여전히 내 자신의 두려움 탓에 아내를 나무라고는 그 일을 후회할 거야. 나는 여전히 내가 왜 기도하는지를 이성으로는 이해하지 못할 거고, 그러면서도 여전히 기도할 거야. 하지만 이제 내 삶은, 내게 무슨 일이 일어나든 그것과는 별개로, 내 삶 전체는, 그 한순간 한순간이, 예전처럼 더 이상 무의미하지 않고, 내가 거기에 불어넣을 힘을 지닌 선이라는 긍정적인 의미를 지니게 된 거야.\"";
if(typeof BODIES_SENT!=="undefined") BODIES_SENT["gb-1399"]=[[["Happy families are all alike; every unhappy family is unhappy in its own way.", "행복한 가정은 모두 엇비슷하지만, 불행한 가정은 저마다의 이유로 불행하다."]], [["Everything was in confusion in the Oblonskys’ house.", "오블론스키 집안은 온통 뒤죽박죽이었다."], ["The wife had discovered that the husband was carrying on an intrigue with a French girl, who had been a governess in their family, and she had announced to her husband that she could not go on living in the same house with him.", "아내는 남편이 한때 자기 집에서 가정교사로 있던 프랑스 여자와 부정한 관계를 맺어 왔다는 사실을 알아냈고, 더 이상 한집에서 같이 살 수 없다고 남편에게 선언해 버렸다."], ["This position of affairs had now lasted three days, and not only the husband and wife themselves, but all the members of their family and household, were painfully conscious of it.", "이런 상태가 사흘째 이어지고 있었고, 부부 당사자들뿐 아니라 가족과 하인들까지 모두 그것을 괴롭게 의식하고 있었다."], ["Every person in the house felt that there was no sense in their living together, and that the stray people brought together by chance in any inn had more in common with one another than they, the members of the family and household of the Oblonskys.", "집 안의 모든 사람이 자기들이 한데 모여 사는 데에 아무 의미가 없으며, 어느 여인숙에서 우연히 모인 뜨내기 손님들조차 자기들, 그러니까 오블론스키 집안의 가족이며 하인들보다는 서로 더 통하는 데가 있으리라고 느꼈다."], ["The wife did not leave her own room, the husband had not been at home for three days.", "아내는 자기 방에서 나오지 않았고, 남편은 사흘째 집에 들어오지 않았다. 아이들은 온 집 안을 제멋대로 뛰어다녔다."], ["The children ran wild all over the house; the English governess quarreled with the housekeeper, and wrote to a friend asking her to look out for a new situation for her; the man-cook had walked off the day before just at dinner time; the kitchen-maid, and the coachman had given warning.", "영국인 가정교사는 가정부와 다투고 친구에게 새 일자리를 알아봐 달라는 편지를 썼다. 남자 요리사는 어제 마침 저녁 식사 때 나가 버렸고, 부엌데기와 마부는 그만두겠다고 통고해 왔다."]], [["Three days after the quarrel, Prince Stepan Arkadyevitch Oblonsky—Stiva, as he was called in the fashionable world—woke up at his usual hour, that is, at eight o’clock in the morning, not in his wife’s bedroom, but on the leather-covered sofa in his study.", "다툼이 있은 지 사흘째 되던 날, 스테판 아르카지치 오블론스키 공작—사교계에서 부르는 이름으로는 스티바—은 평소와 같은 시각, 그러니까 아침 여덟 시에 잠에서 깼는데, 아내의 침실이 아니라 서재의 가죽 소파 위에서였다."], ["He turned over his stout, well-cared-for person on the springy sofa, as though he would sink into a long sleep again; he vigorously embraced the pillow on the other side and buried his face in it; but all at once he jumped up, sat up on the sofa, and opened his eyes.", "그는 마치 다시 긴 잠에 빠져들기라도 할 것처럼, 잘 관리된 통통한 몸을 탄력 있는 소파 위에서 뒤척이며 반대편의 베개를 힘껏 끌어안고 거기에 얼굴을 파묻었다. 그러다 별안간 벌떡 일어나 소파에 앉아 눈을 떴다."]], [["“Yes, yes, how was it now?”", "\"그래, 그래, 어떻게 됐더라?\""], ["he thought, going over his dream.", "그는 꿈을 더듬으며 생각했다."], ["“Now, how was it?", "\"그러니까 어떻게 됐지?"], ["To be sure!", "옳지!"], ["Alabin was giving a dinner at Darmstadt; no, not Darmstadt, but something American.", "알라빈이 다름슈타트에서 만찬을 열고 있었어. 아니, 다름슈타트가 아니라 뭔가 미국적인 데였지."], ["Yes, but then, Darmstadt was in America.", "그래, 하지만 그 다름슈타트가 미국에 있었던 거야."], ["Yes, Alabin was giving a dinner on glass tables, and the tables sang, Il mio tesoro—not Il mio tesoro though, but something better, and there were some sort of little decanters on the table, and they were women, too,”", "그래, 알라빈은 유리 식탁에서 만찬을 열고 있었고, 그 식탁들이 노래를 불렀어. Il mio tesoro라고—아니, Il mio tesoro가 아니라 그보다 더 좋은 거였지, 그리고 식탁 위에는 작은 유리병 같은 것들이 있었는데, 그것들이 또 여자들이기도 했지.\""], ["he remembered.", "그는 떠올렸다."]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch’s eyes twinkled gaily, and he pondered with a smile.", "* Il mio tesoro: 이탈리아어로 '나의 보물'."], ["“Yes, it was nice, very nice.", "모차르트 오페라 「돈 조반니」의 아리아."], ["There was a great deal more that was delightful, only there’s no putting it into words, or even expressing it in one’s thoughts awake.”", "스테판 아르카지치의 눈이 즐겁게 반짝였고, 그는 미소를 지으며 곰곰이 생각에 잠겼다. \"그래, 좋았어, 아주 좋았어."], ["And noticing a gleam of light peeping in beside one of the serge curtains, he cheerfully dropped his feet over the edge of the sofa, and felt about with them for his slippers, a present on his last birthday, worked for him by his wife on gold-colored morocco.", "그 밖에도 흐뭇한 게 잔뜩 있었는데, 다만 그건 말로 옮길 수도 없고, 깨어 있는 상태로는 생각으로 표현할 수조차 없단 말이야.\" 그러다 서지 커튼 한쪽 옆으로 한 줄기 빛이 새어 드는 것을 보고는, 기분 좋게 소파 가장자리 너머로 발을 내려뜨려 슬리퍼를 발로 더듬어 찾았다."], ["And, as he had done every day for the last nine years, he stretched out his hand, without getting up, towards the place where his dressing-gown always hung in his bedroom.", "그것은 지난 생일에 아내가 금빛 모로코가죽에 손수 수를 놓아 만들어 준 선물이었다. 그러고는 지난 구 년 동안 매일 그래 왔듯이, 일어나지도 않은 채 침실에서 늘 실내복이 걸려 있던 자리를 향해 손을 뻗었다."], ["And thereupon he suddenly remembered that he was not sleeping in his wife’s room, but in his study, and why: the smile vanished from his face, he knitted his brows.", "그러다 문득 자기가 아내의 방이 아니라 서재에서 자고 있다는 것을, 그리고 그 까닭을 떠올렸다. 미소가 얼굴에서 사라졌고, 그는 미간을 찌푸렸다."]], [["“Ah, ah, ah! Oo!...”", "\"아, 아, 아!"], ["he muttered, recalling everything that had happened.", "어휴!……\" 그는 일어났던 일을 모조리 떠올리며 중얼거렸다."], ["And again every detail of his quarrel with his wife was present to his imagination, all the hopelessness of his position, and worst of all, his own fault.", "그러자 다시 아내와 다툰 일의 온갖 세세한 정황이며, 자기 처지의 절망스러움이며, 무엇보다도 자기 자신의 잘못이 그의 상상 속에 생생하게 되살아났다."]], [["“Yes, she won’t forgive me, and she can’t forgive me.", "\"그래, 그녀는 나를 용서하지 않을 거고, 또 용서할 수도 없어."], ["And the most awful thing about it is that it’s all my fault—all my fault, though I’m not to blame.", "그리고 무엇보다 끔찍한 건 모두 내 탓이라는 거지—전부 내 탓인데, 그렇다고 내가 비난받을 만한 짓을 한 것도 아니야."], ["That’s the point of the whole situation,”", "바로 거기에 이 모든 상황의 핵심이 있는 거지.\""], ["he reflected.", "그는 곰곰이 생각했다."], ["“Oh, oh, oh!”", "\"오, 오, 오!\""], ["he kept repeating in despair, as he remembered the acutely painful sensations caused him by this quarrel.", "그는 이 다툼이 자기에게 안겨 준 견디기 힘든 괴로운 감각을 떠올리며 절망 속에서 거듭 되뇌었다."]], [["Most unpleasant of all was the first minute when, on coming, happy and good-humored, from the theater, with a huge pear in his hand for his wife, he had not found his wife in the drawing-room, to his surprise had not found her in the study either, and saw her at last in her bedroom with the unlucky letter that revealed everything in her hand.", "무엇보다 가장 불쾌했던 것은 첫 순간이었다. 극장에서 행복하고 기분 좋은 얼굴로, 아내에게 줄 커다란 배 하나를 손에 들고 돌아온 그는, 응접실에서 아내를 찾지 못했고, 놀랍게도 서재에서도 찾지 못했으며, 마침내 침실에서 모든 것을 폭로하는 불운한 편지를 손에 든 아내를 보았던 것이다."]], [["She, his Dolly, forever fussing and worrying over household details, and limited in her ideas, as he considered, was sitting perfectly still with the letter in her hand, looking at him with an expression of horror, despair, and indignation.", "그의 돌리, 평소 그가 보기에는 집안일의 자질구레한 일에 늘 부산을 떨고 안달복달하며 생각의 폭도 좁은 그 여자가, 편지를 손에 든 채 꼼짝도 않고 앉아, 공포와 절망과 분노의 표정으로 그를 바라보고 있었다."]], [["“What’s this?", "\"이게 뭐예요?"], ["this?”", "이게?\""], ["she asked, pointing to the letter.", "그녀가 편지를 가리키며 물었다."]], [["And at this recollection, Stepan Arkadyevitch, as is so often the case, was not so much annoyed at the fact itself as at the way in which he had met his wife’s words.", "그리고 이 기억을 떠올리면서, 흔히 그렇듯이 스테판 아르카지치는 그 사실 자체보다도 아내의 말에 자기가 대응했던 방식 때문에 더 괴로웠다."]], [["There happened to him at that instant what does happen to people when they are unexpectedly caught in something very disgraceful.", "그 순간 그에게 일어난 일은, 사람들이 아주 수치스러운 일을 하다가 뜻밖에 들켰을 때 흔히 일어나는 바로 그것이었다."], ["He did not succeed in adapting his face to the position in which he was placed towards his wife by the discovery of his fault.", "그는 자기 잘못이 드러남으로써 아내 앞에 놓이게 된 처지에 자기 얼굴을 알맞게 맞추지 못했다."], ["Instead of being hurt, denying, defending himself, begging forgiveness, instead of remaining indifferent even—anything would have been better than what he did do—his face utterly involuntarily (reflex spinal action, reflected Stepan Arkadyevitch, who was fond of physiology)—utterly involuntarily assumed its habitual, good-humored, and therefore idiotic smile.", "상처받은 표정을 짓거나, 부인하거나, 변명하거나, 용서를 빌거나, 하다못해 무심한 척하거나—그가 실제로 한 행동보다는 무엇이든 더 나았을 것이다—그렇게 하는 대신, 그의 얼굴은 전혀 의지와 상관없이(척수 반사 작용이지, 하고 생리학을 좋아하는 스테판 아르카지치는 생각했다) 전혀 의지와 상관없이 늘 짓던 그 기분 좋은, 그래서 더없이 멍청한 미소를 지어 버린 것이다."]], [["This idiotic smile he could not forgive himself.", "그 멍청한 미소만큼은 자기 자신을 용서할 수가 없었다."], ["Catching sight of that smile, Dolly shuddered as though at physical pain, broke out with her characteristic heat into a flood of cruel words, and rushed out of the room.", "그 미소를 본 순간, 돌리는 육체적 고통이라도 느낀 듯 몸서리를 쳤고, 그녀 특유의 격정으로 모진 말을 쏟아 내고는 방에서 뛰쳐나가 버렸다."], ["Since then she had refused to see her husband.", "그 후로 그녀는 남편 보기를 거부했다."]], [["“It’s that idiotic smile that’s to blame for it all,”", "\"이 모든 게 그 멍청한 미소 탓이야.\""], ["thought Stepan Arkadyevitch.", "스테판 아르카지치는 생각했다."]], [["“But what’s to be done?", "\"하지만 어쩌면 좋지?"], ["What’s to be done?”", "어쩌면 좋단 말이야?\""], ["he said to himself in despair, and found no answer.", "그는 절망에 빠져 혼잣말을 했지만, 아무런 답도 찾지 못했다."]], [["# Chapter 2", "# Chapter 2"]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch was a truthful man in his relations with himself.", "스테판 아르카지치는 자기 자신을 대하는 데 있어서는 정직한 사람이었다."], ["He was incapable of deceiving himself and persuading himself that he repented of his conduct.", "그는 자신을 속이거나 자기 행동을 뉘우치고 있다고 스스로를 설득할 수 없는 사람이었다."], ["He could not at this date repent of the fact that he, a handsome, susceptible man of thirty-four, was not in love with his wife, the mother of five living and two dead children, and only a year younger than himself.", "잘생기고 다정다감한 서른네 살의 남자인 자기가, 살아 있는 아이 다섯과 죽은 아이 둘의 어머니이자 자기보다 겨우 한 살 어린 아내를 더는 사랑하지 않는다는 사실을, 지금 와서 뉘우칠 수는 없었다."], ["All he repented of was that he had not succeeded better in hiding it from his wife.", "그가 뉘우치는 것이라고는 오직 그 사실을 아내에게 더 잘 감추지 못했다는 것뿐이었다."], ["But he felt all the difficulty of his position and was sorry for his wife, his children, and himself.", "그러나 그는 자기 처지의 곤란함을 절절히 느꼈고, 아내와 아이들과 자기 자신이 가여웠다."], ["Possibly he might have managed to conceal his sins better from his wife if he had anticipated that the knowledge of them would have had such an effect on her.", "만약 그 사실이 발각되었을 때 아내가 그토록 충격받으리라는 것을 미리 알았더라면, 어쩌면 자기 죄를 더 잘 숨길 수도 있었을 것이다."], ["He had never clearly thought out the subject, but he had vaguely conceived that his wife must long ago have suspected him of being unfaithful to her, and shut her eyes to the fact.", "그는 이 문제를 한 번도 또렷이 따져 본 적이 없었지만, 아내가 자기의 부정을 진작에 의심하면서도 그 사실에 눈을 감고 있으리라고 막연히 짐작하고 있었다."], ["He had even supposed that she, a worn-out woman no longer young or good-looking, and in no way remarkable or interesting, merely a good mother, ought from a sense of fairness to take an indulgent view.", "게다가 그는, 이제 젊지도 예쁘지도 않고 어디 하나 두드러지거나 흥미로울 것도 없이 그저 좋은 어머니일 뿐인, 지칠 대로 지친 여자인 아내가, 공정함을 생각해서라도 너그럽게 보아 넘겨야 마땅하다고까지 여기고 있었다."], ["It had turned out quite the other way.", "그런데 결과는 전혀 딴판으로 드러났다."]], [["“Oh, it’s awful!", "\"아, 끔찍해!"], ["oh dear, oh dear!", "아아, 끔찍하다고!\""], ["awful!” Stepan Arkadyevitch kept repeating to himself, and he could think of nothing to be done.", "스테판 아르카지치는 혼자 거듭 되뇌었지만, 무엇을 해야 할지 아무 생각도 떠오르지 않았다."], ["“And how well things were going up till now!", "\"게다가 지금까지는 모든 게 얼마나 잘 굴러갔는데!"], ["how well we got on!", "우리는 얼마나 잘 지냈는데 말이야!"], ["She was contented and happy in her children; I never interfered with her in anything; I let her manage the children and the house just as she liked.", "그녀는 아이들 덕에 만족하고 행복해했고, 나는 무슨 일에도 그녀를 간섭하지 않았으며, 아이들이며 집안일이며 모두 제 마음대로 꾸려 가게 내버려 두었어."], ["It’s true it’s bad her having been a governess in our house.", "하긴 하필 그 여자가 우리 집 가정교사였다는 건 좋지 않아."], ["That’s bad!", "그건 나빠!"], ["There’s something common, vulgar, in flirting with one’s governess.", "제 집 가정교사와 시시덕거린다는 건 어딘가 천박하고 상스러운 데가 있지."], ["But what a governess!”", "하지만 그게 또 어떤 가정교사였느냐고!\""], ["(He vividly recalled the roguish black eyes of Mlle. Roland and her smile.) “But after all, while she was in the house, I kept myself in hand.", "(그는 롤랑 양의 짓궂은 검은 눈동자와 그 미소를 생생하게 떠올렸다.) \"하지만 어쨌든 그녀가 우리 집에 있는 동안에는 나도 자제했단 말이야."], ["And the worst of it all is that she’s already ...", "게다가 무엇보다 고약한 건 그녀가 벌써……"], ["it seems as if ill-luck would have it so!", "마치 불운이 일부러 그렇게 만든 것만 같다니까!"], ["Oh, oh!", "아, 아!"], ["But what, what is to be done?”", "하지만 어쩌면, 어쩌면 좋단 말이야?\""]], [["There was no solution, but that universal solution which life gives to all questions, even the most complex and insoluble. That answer is: one must live in the needs of the day—that is, forget oneself. To forget himself in sleep was impossible now, at least till nighttime; he could not go back now to the music sung by the decanter-women; so he must forget himself in the dream of daily life.", "해답이라고는 없었다. 다만 삶이 가장 복잡하고 풀 수 없는 문제까지 포함해 모든 물음에 내려 주는 그 보편적인 해답이 있을 뿐이었다. 그 답이란 이런 것이다. 하루하루의 필요에 따라 살아야 한다는 것—다시 말해 자기 자신을 잊어버려야 한다는 것이다. 잠 속에서 자기를 잊는 것은 이제, 적어도 밤이 되기 전까지는 불가능했다. 유리병 여자들이 부르던 그 음악으로 돌아갈 수도 없었다. 그러니 그는 일상이라는 꿈속에서 자기 자신을 잊어야만 했다."]], [["“Then we shall see,”", "\"그러고 나면 두고 보지.\""], ["Stepan Arkadyevitch said to himself, and getting up he put on a gray dressing-gown lined with blue silk, tied the tassels in a knot, and, drawing a deep breath of air into his broad, bare chest, he walked to the window with his usual confident step, turning out his feet that carried his full frame so easily.", "스테판 아르카지치는 혼자 중얼거리고는, 일어나 푸른 비단으로 안을 댄 회색 실내복을 걸치고 술 장식을 매듭지어 묶었다. 그러고는 넓고 헐벗은 가슴 가득히 깊이 숨을 들이마시며, 그 풍채 좋은 몸을 가볍게 떠받치는 두 발을 바깥으로 벌린 채, 늘 그렇듯 자신감 넘치는 걸음으로 창가로 걸어갔다."], ["He pulled up the blind and rang the bell loudly.", "그는 블라인드를 끌어 올리고 종을 요란하게 울렸다."], ["It was at once answered by the appearance of an old friend, his valet, Matvey, carrying his clothes, his boots, and a telegram.", "곧바로 오랜 벗인 시종 마트베이가 옷가지와 부츠와 전보 한 통을 들고 나타나 그 부름에 응했다."], ["Matvey was followed by the barber with all the necessaries for shaving.", "마트베이 뒤로는 면도에 필요한 도구를 죄다 갖춘 이발사가 따라 들어왔다."]], [["“Are there any papers from the office?”", "\"관청에서 온 서류가 있나?\""], ["asked Stepan Arkadyevitch, taking the telegram and seating himself at the looking-glass.", "스테판 아르카지치가 전보를 받아 들고 거울 앞에 앉으며 물었다."]], [["“On the table,”", "\"탁자 위에 있습니다.\" 마트베이는 캐묻는 듯한 동정 어린 눈길로 주인을 흘끔 쳐다보며 대답했다."], ["replied Matvey, glancing with inquiring sympathy at his master; and, after a short pause, he added with a sly smile, “They’ve sent from the carriage-jobbers.”", "그러고는 잠시 뜸을 들였다가 능청스러운 미소를 지으며 덧붙였다. \"마차 대여점에서 사람이 다녀갔습니다.\""]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch made no reply, he merely glanced at Matvey in the looking-glass.", "스테판 아르카지치는 아무 대꾸도 하지 않고 거울 속으로 마트베이를 흘끗 보기만 했다."], ["In the glance, in which their eyes met in the looking-glass, it was clear that they understood one another.", "거울 속에서 마주친 그 눈길로 보아, 두 사람이 서로를 이해하고 있음이 분명했다."], ["Stepan Arkadyevitch’s eyes asked: “Why do you tell me that?", "스테판 아르카지치의 눈은 이렇게 묻고 있었다. \"왜 그런 말을 나한테 하는 거야?"], ["don’t you know?”", "자네도 모르나?\""]], [["Matvey put his hands in his jacket pockets, thrust out one leg, and gazed silently, good-humoredly, with a faint smile, at his master.", "마트베이는 두 손을 윗옷 주머니에 찔러 넣고 한쪽 다리를 앞으로 내민 채, 옅은 미소를 띠고 사람 좋게 잠자코 주인을 바라보았다."]], [["“I told them to come on Sunday, and till then not to trouble you or themselves for nothing,” he said.", "\"일요일에 다시 오라고, 그때까지는 공연히 나리도 자기네도 번거롭게 하지 말라고 일러 두었습니다.\""], ["He had obviously prepared the sentence beforehand.", "그는 말했다. 분명 미리 준비해 둔 문장이었다."]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch saw Matvey wanted to make a joke and attract attention to himself.", "스테판 아르카지치는 마트베이가 농담을 던져 제 쪽으로 관심을 끌고 싶어 한다는 것을 알아차렸다."], ["Tearing open the telegram, he read it through, guessing at the words, misspelt as they always are in telegrams, and his face brightened.", "그는 전보를 찢어 열고는, 전보에서 늘 그렇듯 틀리게 적힌 단어들을 짐작해 가며 끝까지 읽어 내려갔다. 그러자 그의 얼굴이 환해졌다."]], [["“Matvey, my sister Anna Arkadyevna will be here tomorrow,”", "\"마트베이, 내 누이 안나 아르카지예브나가 내일 이리로 온다네.\""], ["he said, checking for a minute the sleek, plump hand of the barber, cutting a pink path through his long, curly whiskers.", "그는 길고 곱슬곱슬한 구레나룻 사이로 분홍빛 길을 내고 있던 이발사의 매끈하고 통통한 손을 잠시 멈춰 세우며 말했다."]], [["“Thank God!”", "\"하느님 감사합니다!\""], ["said Matvey, showing by this response that he, like his master, realized the significance of this arrival—that is, that Anna Arkadyevna, the sister he was so fond of, might bring about a reconciliation between husband and wife.", "마트베이가 말했다. 이 대답으로 그는, 주인과 마찬가지로 이 방문의 의미를—다시 말해 주인이 그토록 아끼는 누이 안나 아르카지예브나가 부부 사이의 화해를 이끌어 낼지도 모른다는 것을—알고 있음을 드러냈다."]], [["“Alone, or with her husband?”", "\"혼자 오십니까, 아니면 부군과 함께 오십니까?\""], ["inquired Matvey.", "마트베이가 물었다."]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch could not answer, as the barber was at work on his upper lip, and he raised one finger.", "이발사가 윗입술을 손질하고 있던 터라 스테판 아르카지치는 대답할 수 없어 손가락 하나를 들어 올렸다."], ["Matvey nodded at the looking-glass.", "마트베이가 거울을 향해 고개를 끄덕였다."]], [["“Alone.", "\"혼자라네."], ["Is the room to be got ready upstairs?”", "위층에 방을 준비할까요?\""]], [["“Inform Darya Alexandrovna: where she orders.”", "\"다리아 알렉산드로브나께 여쭤서, 그분이 정하시는 대로 하게.\""]], [["“Darya Alexandrovna?”", "\"다리아 알렉산드로브나께요?\""], ["Matvey repeated, as though in doubt.", "마트베이가 미심쩍다는 듯 되물었다."]], [["“Yes, inform her.", "\"그래, 그분께 여쭤."], ["Here, take the telegram; give it to her, and then do what she tells you.”", "자, 전보를 받아 가서 그분께 드리고, 그분이 시키는 대로 하게.\""]], [["“You want to try it on,”", "'한번 떠보시려는 게군.'"], ["Matvey understood, but he only said, “Yes, sir.”", "마트베이는 알아차렸지만, 그저 이렇게만 말했다. \"알겠습니다, 나리.\""]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch was already washed and combed and ready to be dressed, when Matvey, stepping deliberately in his creaky boots, came back into the room with the telegram in his hand.", "스테판 아르카지치가 이미 몸을 씻고 머리를 빗어 옷을 입을 채비를 끝냈을 무렵, 마트베이가 삐걱거리는 부츠를 신고 일부러 천천히 걸어 전보를 손에 든 채 방으로 돌아왔다."], ["The barber had gone.", "이발사는 이미 가고 없었다."]], [["“Darya Alexandrovna told me to inform you that she is going away.", "\"다리아 알렉산드로브나께서, 자기는 떠날 거라고 나리께 전하라 하셨습니다."], ["Let him do—that is you—as he likes,”", "그분—그러니까 나리—께서는 마음대로 하시라고요.\""], ["he said, laughing only with his eyes, and putting his hands in his pockets, he watched his master with his head on one side.", "그는 눈으로만 웃으며 말하고는, 두 손을 주머니에 찔러 넣고 고개를 한쪽으로 갸웃한 채 주인을 지켜보았다."], ["Stepan Arkadyevitch was silent a minute.", "스테판 아르카지치는 잠시 잠자코 있었다."], ["Then a good-humored and rather pitiful smile showed itself on his handsome face.", "이윽고 그의 잘생긴 얼굴에 사람 좋으면서도 다소 가련한 미소가 떠올랐다."]], [["“Eh, Matvey?”", "\"어이, 마트베이?\""], ["he said, shaking his head.", "그가 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 말했다."]], [["“It’s all right, sir; she will come round,”", "\"괜찮습니다, 나리. 마님께서도 마음을 돌리실 겁니다.\""], ["said Matvey.", "마트베이가 말했다."]], [["“Come round?”", "\"마음을 돌린다고?\""]], [["“Yes, sir.”", "\"네, 나리.\""]], [["“Do you think so?", "\"그렇게 생각하나?"], ["Who’s there?”", "거기 누구야?\""], ["asked Stepan Arkadyevitch, hearing the rustle of a woman’s dress at the door.", "문가에서 여자의 옷자락 스치는 소리를 듣고 스테판 아르카지치가 물었다."]], [["“It’s I,”", "\"저예요.\""], ["said a firm, pleasant, woman’s voice, and the stern, pockmarked face of Matrona Philimonovna, the nurse, was thrust in at the doorway.", "단호하고 듣기 좋은 여자의 목소리가 들리더니, 유모 마트료나 필리모노브나의 곰보 자국 난 엄한 얼굴이 문틈으로 쑥 들이밀어졌다."]], [["“Well, what is it, Matrona?”", "\"무슨 일인가, 마트료나?\""], ["queried Stepan Arkadyevitch, going up to her at the door.", "스테판 아르카지치가 문가로 다가가며 물었다."]], [["Although Stepan Arkadyevitch was completely in the wrong as regards his wife, and was conscious of this himself, almost everyone in the house (even the nurse, Darya Alexandrovna’s chief ally) was on his side.", "스테판 아르카지치는 아내에 대해서는 전적으로 잘못한 것이고 그 자신도 그것을 의식하고 있었지만, 집 안의 거의 모든 사람이(다리아 알렉산드로브나의 든든한 한편인 유모마저도) 그의 편이었다."]], [["“Well, what now?”", "\"그래, 무슨 일인가?\""], ["he asked disconsolately.", "그는 풀 죽은 목소리로 물었다."]], [["“Go to her, sir; own your fault again.", "\"마님께 가 보세요, 나리. 다시 한번 잘못을 비세요."], ["Maybe God will aid you.", "어쩌면 하느님께서 도와주실지도 몰라요."], ["She is suffering so, it’s sad to see her; and besides, everything in the house is topsy-turvy.", "마님이 어찌나 괴로워하시는지 차마 보기 딱할 지경이에요. 게다가 집 안이 온통 뒤죽박죽이고요."], ["You must have pity, sir, on the children.", "나리, 아이들을 가엾게 여기셔야지요. 마님께 용서를 비세요, 나리."], ["Beg her forgiveness, sir.", "달리 도리가 없잖아요!"], ["There’s no help for it!", "저지른 일은 감당하셔야지요…….\""], ["One must take the consequences....”", "\"하지만 그 사람이 날 보려고 하지 않아.\""]], [["“But she won’t see me.”", "\"나리는 나리 할 일을 하시면 돼요."], ["“You do your part.", "하느님은 자비로우시니까요."], ["God is merciful; pray to God, sir, pray to God.”", "하느님께 기도하세요, 나리, 하느님께 기도하세요.\""]], [["“Come, that’ll do, you can go,”", "\"자, 그만하면 됐으니 가 보게.\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, blushing suddenly.", "스테판 아르카지치가 별안간 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다."], ["“Well now, do dress me.”", "\"자, 이제 옷을 입혀 주게.\""], ["He turned to Matvey and threw off his dressing-gown decisively.", "그는 마트베이를 향해 돌아서며 단호하게 실내복을 벗어 던졌다."]], [["Matvey was already holding up the shirt like a horse’s collar, and, blowing off some invisible speck, he slipped it with obvious pleasure over the well-groomed body of his master.", "마트베이는 이미 말의 멍에처럼 셔츠를 받쳐 들고 있다가, 보이지도 않는 먼지를 후 불어 내고는, 잘 손질된 주인의 몸 위로 흐뭇한 기색이 역력하게 그것을 미끄러뜨려 입혔다."]], [["# Chapter 3", "# Chapter 3"]], [["When he was dressed, Stepan Arkadyevitch sprinkled some scent on himself, pulled down his shirt-cuffs, distributed into his pockets his cigarettes, pocketbook, matches, and watch with its double chain and seals, and shaking out his handkerchief, feeling himself clean, fragrant, healthy, and physically at ease, in spite of his unhappiness, he walked with a slight swing on each leg into the dining-room, where coffee was already waiting for him, and beside the coffee, letters and papers from the office.", "옷을 다 차려입고 나서 스테판 아르카지치는 향수를 약간 뿌리고, 셔츠 소맷부리를 끌어내리고, 담배와 지갑과 성냥, 그리고 두 겹 줄과 인장이 달린 시계를 주머니에 나누어 넣고는, 손수건을 탁탁 털었다. 그리고 자신이 깨끗하고 향기롭고 건강하며, 불행한 처지에도 불구하고 몸은 더없이 개운하다고 느끼면서, 두 다리를 가볍게 흔드는 걸음걸이로 식당으로 들어갔다. 거기에는 이미 커피가 준비되어 있었고, 커피 옆에는 관청에서 온 편지며 서류가 놓여 있었다."]], [["He read the letters.", "그는 편지를 읽었다."], ["One was very unpleasant, from a merchant who was buying a forest on his wife’s property.", "한 통은 몹시 불쾌한 것으로, 아내의 영지에 있는 숲을 사려는 상인에게서 온 것이었다."], ["To sell this forest was absolutely essential; but at present, until he was reconciled with his wife, the subject could not be discussed.", "이 숲을 파는 일은 반드시 해야만 했다. 하지만 지금으로서는, 아내와 화해하기 전에는 그 문제를 입에 올릴 수가 없었다."], ["The most unpleasant thing of all was that his pecuniary interests should in this way enter into the question of his reconciliation with his wife.", "무엇보다 가장 불쾌한 것은, 자기의 금전적 이해관계가 이런 식으로 아내와의 화해 문제에 끼어든다는 점이었다."], ["And the idea that he might be led on by his interests, that he might seek a reconciliation with his wife on account of the sale of the forest—that idea hurt him.", "그리고 자기가 그 이해관계에 끌려, 숲을 팔기 위해 아내와의 화해를 구할지도 모른다는 생각—그 생각이 그를 아프게 했다."]], [["When he had finished his letters, Stepan Arkadyevitch moved the office-papers close to him, rapidly looked through two pieces of business, made a few notes with a big pencil, and pushing away the papers, turned to his coffee.", "편지를 다 읽고 나서 스테판 아르카지치는 관청 서류를 가까이 끌어당겨 두 건의 사안을 빠르게 훑어보고, 큼직한 연필로 몇 자 적어 넣은 다음, 서류를 밀쳐 놓고 커피로 향했다."], ["As he sipped his coffee, he opened a still damp morning paper, and began reading it.", "커피를 홀짝이면서 그는 아직 축축한 조간신문을 펼쳐 읽기 시작했다."]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch took in and read a liberal paper, not an extreme one, but one advocating the views held by the majority.", "스테판 아르카지치가 받아 보고 읽는 신문은 자유주의 성향의 것이었는데, 극단적인 것이 아니라 다수가 견지하는 견해를 표방하는 것이었다."], ["And in spite of the fact that science, art, and politics had no special interest for him, he firmly held those views on all these subjects which were held by the majority and by his paper, and he only changed them when the majority changed them—or, more strictly speaking, he did not change them, but they imperceptibly changed of themselves within him.", "그리고 학문이며 예술이며 정치가 그에게 특별한 관심거리가 아니었음에도, 그는 이 모든 주제에 대해 다수와 자기 신문이 견지하는 견해를 굳게 지녔고, 다수가 그것을 바꿀 때에야 비로소 자기 견해도 바꾸었다—더 엄밀히 말하면, 그는 그것을 바꾸는 것이 아니라, 그의 내부에서 견해가 어느새 저절로 바뀌어 갔다."]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch had not chosen his political opinions or his views; these political opinions and views had come to him of themselves, just as he did not choose the shapes of his hat and coat, but simply took those that were being worn.", "스테판 아르카지치는 자기의 정치적 견해나 관점을 스스로 고른 것이 아니었다. 이 정치적 견해와 관점은 저절로 그에게 찾아온 것이었으니, 그가 모자와 외투의 모양을 스스로 고르지 않고 그저 남들이 쓰고 입는 것을 따르는 것과 마찬가지였다."], ["And for him, living in a certain society—owing to the need, ordinarily developed at years of discretion, for some degree of mental activity—to have views was just as indispensable as to have a hat.", "그리고 특정한 사교계에서 살아가는 그에게는—철든 나이가 되면 으레 생겨나는, 어느 정도의 지적 활동을 향한 욕구 때문에—견해를 갖는다는 것이 모자를 갖는 것만큼이나 필수적이었다."], ["If there was a reason for his preferring liberal to conservative views, which were held also by many of his circle, it arose not from his considering liberalism more rational, but from its being in closer accordance with his manner of life.", "만일 그가 자기 집단의 많은 사람들도 견지하는 보수주의보다 자유주의를 선호하는 데에 어떤 이유가 있었다면, 그것은 그가 자유주의를 더 합리적이라고 여겨서가 아니라, 그것이 자기 생활 방식에 더 잘 들어맞기 때문이었다."], ["The liberal party said that in Russia everything is wrong, and certainly Stepan Arkadyevitch had many debts and was decidedly short of money.", "자유주의 진영은 러시아에서는 모든 것이 잘못되어 있다고 말했는데, 과연 스테판 아르카지치는 빚이 많았고 돈에 쪼들리는 처지가 분명했다."], ["The liberal party said that marriage is an institution quite out of date, and that it needs reconstruction; and family life certainly afforded Stepan Arkadyevitch little gratification, and forced him into lying and hypocrisy, which was so repulsive to his nature.", "자유주의 진영은 결혼이란 완전히 시대에 뒤떨어진 제도이며 개조가 필요하다고 말했는데, 과연 가정생활은 스테판 아르카지치에게 별다른 만족을 주지 못했고, 그의 천성에 그토록 거슬리는 거짓말과 위선을 강요했다."], ["The liberal party said, or rather allowed it to be understood, that religion is only a curb to keep in check the barbarous classes of the people; and Stepan Arkadyevitch could not get through even a short service without his legs aching from standing up, and could never make out what was the object of all the terrible and high-flown language about another world when life might be so very amusing in this world.", "자유주의 진영은 종교란 야만적인 계층의 사람들을 억누르기 위한 굴레에 지나지 않는다고 말했다—아니, 그렇게 이해되도록 내버려 두었다—그리고 스테판 아르카지치는 짧은 예배조차 서 있느라 다리가 아파 견디지 못했고, 이 세상에서의 삶이 이토록 즐거울 수 있는데 저세상에 관한 그 온갖 무시무시하고 거창한 말들이 다 무엇을 위한 것인지 도무지 알 수 없었다."], ["And with all this, Stepan Arkadyevitch, who liked a joke, was fond of puzzling a plain man by saying that if he prided himself on his origin, he ought not to stop at Rurik and disown the first founder of his family—the monkey.", "게다가 농담을 좋아하는 스테판 아르카지치는, 자기 혈통을 자랑하는 사람을 두고 만약 그가 출신을 자랑한다면 류리크에서 멈출 게 아니라 자기 가문의 최초의 시조인 원숭이를 부정하지 말아야 한다고 말하며, 우직한 사람을 당황하게 만드는 일을 즐겼다."], ["And so Liberalism had become a habit of Stepan Arkadyevitch’s, and he liked his newspaper, as he did his cigar after dinner, for the slight fog it diffused in his brain.", "그리하여 자유주의는 스테판 아르카지치에게 하나의 습관이 되었고, 그는 식후에 피우는 시가를 좋아하듯 자기 신문을 좋아했는데, 그것이 머릿속에 옅은 안개를 퍼뜨려 주기 때문이었다."], ["He read the leading article, in which it was maintained that it was quite senseless in our day to raise an outcry that radicalism was threatening to swallow up all conservative elements, and that the government ought to take measures to crush the revolutionary hydra; that, on the contrary, “in our opinion the danger lies not in that fantastic revolutionary hydra, but in the obstinacy of traditionalism clogging progress,”", "그는 사설을 읽었는데, 거기에는 오늘날 급진주의가 모든 보수적 요소를 집어삼키려 위협하고 있으며 정부가 혁명의 히드라를 짓밟을 조치를 취해야 한다고 외쳐 대는 것은 전혀 분별없는 짓이고, 오히려 \"우리 견해로는 위험은 그 환상적인 혁명의 히드라에 있는 것이 아니라 진보를 가로막는 전통주의의 완고함에 있다\"는 등등의 주장이 펼쳐져 있었다. 그는 또 다른 기사, 곧 재정에 관한 기사도 읽었는데, 거기에는 벤담과 밀이 언급되어 있었고 내각을 겨냥한 몇몇 빈정거림이 슬쩍 끼어 있었다."], ["etc., etc. He read another article, too, a financial one, which alluded to Bentham and Mill, and dropped some innuendoes reflecting on the ministry.", "그는 특유의 명민함으로 빈정거림 하나하나의 속뜻을 알아챘고, 그것이 어디서 나왔으며 누구를 어떤 근거로 겨냥한 것인지를 간파했다."], ["With his characteristic quickwittedness he caught the drift of each innuendo, divined whence it came, at whom and on what ground it was aimed, and that afforded him, as it always did, a certain satisfaction.", "그리고 그것은 늘 그렇듯이 그에게 어떤 만족감을 주었다. 그러나 오늘은 그 만족감이 마트료나 필리모노브나의 충고와 만족스럽지 못한 집안 형편 때문에 씁쓸해져 있었다."], ["But today that satisfaction was embittered by Matrona Philimonovna’s advice and the unsatisfactory state of the household.", "그는 또한 베이스트 백작이 비스바덴으로 떠났다는 소문이 돈다는 것, 더 이상 흰머리가 생기지 않게 해 준다는 것, 경마차를 판다는 것, 어떤 젊은이가 일자리를 구한다는 것 따위도 읽었다."], ["He read, too, that Count Beist was rumored to have left for Wiesbaden, and that one need have no more gray hair, and of the sale of a light carriage, and of a young person seeking a situation; but these items of information did not give him, as usual, a quiet, ironical gratification.", "그러나 이런 정보들은 평소처럼 그에게 조용하고 빈정대는 만족감을 주지 못했다. 신문과 두 잔째의 커피와 버터 바른 빵을 다 비우고 나서 그는 일어나 조끼에 묻은 빵 부스러기를 털어 냈다."], ["Having finished the paper, a second cup of coffee and a roll and butter, he got up, shaking the crumbs of the roll off his waistcoat; and, squaring his broad chest, he smiled joyously: not because there was anything particularly agreeable in his mind—the joyous smile was evoked by a good digestion.", "그리고 넓은 가슴을 쫙 펴며 즐겁게 미소를 지었다. 마음속에 특별히 유쾌한 일이 있어서가 아니라—그 즐거운 미소는 좋은 소화에서 비롯된 것이었다."]], [["But this joyous smile at once recalled everything to him, and he grew thoughtful.", "그러나 이 즐거운 미소가 곧바로 모든 것을 그에게 다시 떠올리게 했고, 그는 생각에 잠겼다."]], [["Two childish voices (Stepan Arkadyevitch recognized the voices of Grisha, his youngest boy, and Tanya, his eldest girl) were heard outside the door.", "문밖에서 아이들의 목소리 둘이 들려왔다(스테판 아르카지치는 막내아들 그리샤와 맏딸 타냐의 목소리임을 알아챘다)."], ["They were carrying something, and dropped it.", "아이들은 무언가를 나르다가 떨어뜨린 것이었다."]], [["“I told you not to sit passengers on the roof,”", "\"지붕 위에 승객을 앉히지 말랬잖아.\""], ["said the little girl in English; “there, pick them up!”", "여자아이가 영어로 말했다. \"자, 어서 주워!\""]], [["“Everything’s in confusion,”", "\"온통 뒤죽박죽이군.\""], ["thought Stepan Arkadyevitch; “there are the children running about by themselves.”", "스테판 아르카지치는 생각했다. \"애들이 저렇게 제멋대로 뛰어다니고 말이야.\""], ["And going to the door, he called them.", "그러고는 문가로 가서 아이들을 불렀다."], ["They threw down the box, that represented a train, and came in to their father.", "아이들은 기차를 본뜬 상자를 내던지고 아버지에게로 들어왔다."]], [["The little girl, her father’s favorite, ran up boldly, embraced him, and hung laughingly on his neck, enjoying as she always did the smell of scent that came from his whiskers.", "아버지가 가장 귀여워하는 여자아이는 대담하게 달려와 그를 끌어안고는, 늘 그러듯 그의 구레나룻에서 풍기는 향수 냄새를 즐기며 웃음 띤 얼굴로 그의 목에 매달렸다."], ["At last the little girl kissed his face, which was flushed from his stooping posture and beaming with tenderness, loosed her hands, and was about to run away again; but her father held her back.", "마침내 여자아이는 허리를 숙인 자세 탓에 발그레해지고 다정함으로 환히 빛나는 그의 얼굴에 입을 맞추고는, 두 손을 풀고 다시 달아나려 했다. 그러나 아버지가 그녀를 붙잡았다."]], [["“How is mamma?”", "\"엄마는 어떠시니?\""], ["he asked, passing his hand over his daughter’s smooth, soft little neck.", "그는 딸아이의 매끄럽고 보드라운 작은 목을 손으로 쓸어 주며 물었다."], ["“Good morning,”", "\"잘 잤니.\""], ["he said, smiling to the boy, who had come up to greet him.", "그는 인사하러 다가온 아들에게 미소를 지으며 말했다."], ["He was conscious that he loved the boy less, and always tried to be fair; but the boy felt it, and did not respond with a smile to his father’s chilly smile.", "그는 자기가 아들을 덜 사랑한다는 것을 의식하고 있었고, 늘 공평하려고 애썼다. 그러나 아들은 그것을 느꼈고, 아버지의 차가운 미소에 미소로 답하지 않았다."]], [["“Mamma?", "\"엄마요?"], ["She is up,”", "일어나셨어요.\""], ["answered the girl.", "여자아이가 대답했다."]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch sighed.", "스테판 아르카지치는 한숨을 쉬었다."], ["“That means that she’s not slept again all night,”", "'그렇다는 건 또 밤새 한숨도 못 잤다는 거지.'"], ["he thought.", "그는 생각했다."]], [["“Well, is she cheerful?”", "\"그래, 기분은 좋으시던?\""]], [["The little girl knew that there was a quarrel between her father and mother, and that her mother could not be cheerful, and that her father must be aware of this, and that he was pretending when he asked about it so lightly.", "여자아이는 아버지와 어머니 사이에 다툼이 있었다는 것을, 그래서 어머니의 기분이 좋을 리 없다는 것을, 또 아버지도 이를 알고 있을 것이며, 이렇게 가볍게 묻는 것은 시치미를 떼는 것임을 알고 있었다."], ["And she blushed for her father.", "그래서 아이는 아버지가 부끄러워졌다."], ["He at once perceived it, and blushed too.", "그는 곧바로 그것을 알아챘고, 그 역시 얼굴이 붉어졌다."]], [["“I don’t know,” she said.", "\"모르겠어요.\""], ["“She did not say we must do our lessons, but she said we were to go for a walk with Miss Hoole to grandmamma’s.”", "아이가 말했다. \"공부하라고는 안 하셨고, 미스 훌이랑 외할머니 댁에 산책 가라고만 하셨어요.\""]], [["“Well, go, Tanya, my darling.", "\"그래, 가렴, 타냐, 우리 귀염둥이."], ["Oh, wait a minute, though,”", "아, 잠깐만 기다려라.\""], ["he said, still holding her and stroking her soft little hand.", "그는 여전히 아이를 붙든 채 보드라운 작은 손을 쓰다듬으며 말했다."]], [["He took off the mantelpiece, where he had put it yesterday, a little box of sweets, and gave her two, picking out her favorites, a chocolate and a fondant.", "그는 어제 벽난로 선반 위에 올려 둔 사탕 상자를 집어 들고, 아이가 좋아하는 것으로 골라 초콜릿 하나와 봉봉 하나를 주었다."]], [["“For Grisha?”", "\"그리샤 거예요?\""], ["said the little girl, pointing to the chocolate.", "여자아이가 초콜릿을 가리키며 물었다."]], [["“Yes, yes.”", "\"그래, 그래.\""], ["And still stroking her little shoulder, he kissed her on the roots of her hair and neck, and let her go.", "그러고는 여전히 작은 어깨를 쓰다듬으며 아이의 머리카락이 난 언저리와 목에 입을 맞추고 보내 주었다."]], [["“The carriage is ready,”", "\"마차가 준비됐습니다.\""], ["said Matvey; “but there’s someone to see you with a petition.”", "마트베이가 말했다. \"그런데 청원하러 온 손님이 있습니다.\""]], [["“Been here long?”", "\"온 지 오래됐나?\""], ["asked Stepan Arkadyevitch.", "스테판 아르카지치가 물었다."]], [["“Half an hour.”", "\"반 시간쯤 됐습니다.\""]], [["“How many times have I told you to tell me at once?”", "\"바로 알리라고 몇 번이나 일렀나?\""]], [["“One must let you drink your coffee in peace, at least,”", "\"적어도 커피는 편히 드시게 해 드려야지요.\""], ["said Matvey, in the affectionately gruff tone with which it was impossible to be angry.", "마트베이가, 화를 낼 수 없게 만드는 그 다정스레 무뚝뚝한 어조로 말했다."]], [["“Well, show the person up at once,”", "\"그래, 어서 그 사람을 들여보내게.\""], ["said Oblonsky, frowning with vexation.", "오블론스키는 짜증스럽게 미간을 찌푸리며 말했다."]], [["The petitioner, the widow of a staff captain Kalinin, came with a request impossible and unreasonable; but Stepan Arkadyevitch, as he generally did, made her sit down, heard her to the end attentively without interrupting her, and gave her detailed advice as to how and to whom to apply, and even wrote her, in his large, sprawling, good and legible hand, a confident and fluent little note to a personage who might be of use to her.", "청원하러 온 사람은 칼리닌이라는 참모 대위의 미망인이었는데, 들어줄 수도 없고 사리에도 맞지 않는 부탁을 가지고 왔다. 그러나 스테판 아르카지치는 늘 그러듯이 그녀를 앉히고, 말을 가로막지 않은 채 끝까지 주의 깊게 들어 주었으며, 어떻게 누구에게 청원해야 하는지 자세히 일러 주었다."], ["Having got rid of the staff captain’s widow, Stepan Arkadyevitch took his hat and stopped to recollect whether he had forgotten anything.", "게다가 그 크고 활달하면서도 보기 좋고 또렷한 글씨로, 그녀에게 도움이 될 만한 인사에게 보내는 자신만만하고 유려한 짧은 편지까지 써 주었다. 참모 대위의 미망인을 보내고 나서 스테판 아르카지치는 모자를 집어 들고는, 뭔가 잊은 것은 없는지 떠올리려고 멈춰 섰다."], ["It appeared that he had forgotten nothing except what he wanted to forget—his wife.", "잊고 싶었던 것—바로 아내 외에는 아무것도 잊은 게 없는 듯했다."]], [["“Ah, yes!”", "\"아, 그렇지!\""], ["He bowed his head, and his handsome face assumed a harassed expression.", "그는 고개를 떨구었고, 그의 잘생긴 얼굴에 괴로운 표정이 떠올랐다. '갈까, 가지 말까!'"], ["“To go, or not to go!”", "그는 혼잣말을 했다."], ["he said to himself; and an inner voice told him he must not go, that nothing could come of it but falsity; that to amend, to set right their relations was impossible, because it was impossible to make her attractive again and able to inspire love, or to make him an old man, not susceptible to love.", "그리고 내면의 목소리는 그에게 가서는 안 된다고, 거기서 나올 것이라곤 거짓밖에 없다고, 둘 사이의 관계를 바로잡고 고치는 것은 불가능하다고 일러 주었다. 왜냐하면 그녀를 다시 매력적이고 사랑을 불러일으킬 수 있게 만들거나, 그를 사랑에 무감한 노인으로 만드는 것이 불가능하기 때문이었다."], ["Except deceit and lying nothing could come of it now; and deceit and lying were opposed to his nature.", "이제는 기만과 거짓 외에 아무것도 나올 수 없었다. 그리고 기만과 거짓은 그의 천성에 어긋났다."]], [["“It must be some time, though: it can’t go on like this,”", "'그래도 언젠가는 해야 해. 이대로 계속 갈 수는 없잖아.'"], ["he said, trying to give himself courage.", "그는 스스로 용기를 내려 애쓰며 말했다."], ["He squared his chest, took out a cigarette, took two whiffs at it, flung it into a mother-of-pearl ashtray, and with rapid steps walked through the drawing-room, and opened the other door into his wife’s bedroom.", "그는 가슴을 쫙 펴고 담배를 꺼내어 두 모금 빨고는 자개 재떨이에 던져 넣고, 빠른 걸음으로 응접실을 가로질러 아내의 침실로 통하는 다른 쪽 문을 열었다."]], [["# Chapter 4", "# Chapter 4"]], [["Darya Alexandrovna, in a dressing jacket, and with her now scanty, once luxuriant and beautiful hair fastened up with hairpins on the nape of her neck, with a sunken, thin face and large, startled eyes, which looked prominent from the thinness of her face, was standing among a litter of all sorts of things scattered all over the room, before an open bureau, from which she was taking something.", "다리아 알렉산드로브나는 실내복 차림으로, 한때는 풍성하고 아름다웠으나 이제는 숱이 적어진 머리를 목덜미에 핀으로 꽂아 올린 채, 야위어 홀쭉해진 얼굴에 그 야윈 탓에 도드라져 보이는 크고 놀란 눈을 하고서, 방 안 곳곳에 온갖 물건이 어지러이 흩어진 가운데, 열린 서랍장 앞에 서서 무언가를 꺼내고 있었다."], ["Hearing her husband’s steps, she stopped, looking towards the door, and trying assiduously to give her features a severe and contemptuous expression.", "남편의 발소리를 듣자 그녀는 동작을 멈추고 문 쪽을 바라보면서, 애써 자기 표정에 엄격하고 경멸하는 빛을 담으려고 했다."], ["She felt she was afraid of him, and afraid of the coming interview.", "그녀는 남편이 두렵고, 다가오는 이 대면이 두렵다는 것을 느꼈다."], ["She was just attempting to do what she had attempted to do ten times already in these last three days—to sort out the children’s things and her own, so as to take them to her mother’s—and again she could not bring herself to do this; but now again, as each time before, she kept saying to herself, “that things cannot go on like this, that she must take some step”", "그녀는 막 지난 사흘 동안 벌써 열 번이나 시도했던 일을, 그러니까 아이들의 물건과 자기 물건을 챙겨 친정 어머니에게 가지고 가려고 추리는 일을 다시 해 보려던 참이었는데, 또다시 차마 그 일을 해낼 수가 없었다. 하지만 지금도, 전에 매번 그랬듯이, 그녀는 \"이대로 살 수는 없으며, 무슨 조치든 취해서\""], ["to punish him, put him to shame, avenge on him some little part at least of the suffering he had caused her.", "그를 벌하고, 그에게 수치를 주고, 그가 자기에게 안겨 준 고통의 적어도 작은 일부만이라도 갚아 주어야 한다고 스스로에게 거듭 다짐하고 있었다."], ["She still continued to tell herself that she should leave him, but she was conscious that this was impossible; it was impossible because she could not get out of the habit of regarding him as her husband and loving him.", "그녀는 여전히 그를 떠나야 한다고 자신에게 말하고 있었지만, 그것이 불가능하다는 것도 의식하고 있었다. 불가능했던 것은, 그를 자기 남편으로 여기고 사랑하는 습관에서 벗어날 수가 없었기 때문이었다."], ["Besides this, she realized that if even here in her own house she could hardly manage to look after her five children properly, they would be still worse off where she was going with them all.", "게다가 그녀는, 비록 여기 자기 집에서조차 다섯 아이를 제대로 돌보기가 버거운 마당에, 아이들을 모두 데리고 가려는 그곳에서는 형편이 한층 더 나빠지리라는 것을 깨달았다."], ["As it was, even in the course of these three days, the youngest was unwell from being given unwholesome soup, and the others had almost gone without their dinner the day before.", "사실 이 사흘 동안만 해도, 막내는 상한 수프를 먹고 탈이 났고, 다른 아이들은 어제 저녁을 거의 굶다시피 했다."], ["She was conscious that it was impossible to go away; but, cheating herself, she went on all the same sorting out her things and pretending she was going.", "그녀는 떠나는 것이 불가능하다는 것을 의식하고 있었지만, 그러면서도 자신을 속이며 여전히 물건을 추리고 떠날 것처럼 가장하고 있었다."]], [["Seeing her husband, she dropped her hands into the drawer of the bureau as though looking for something, and only looked round at him when he had come quite up to her.", "남편을 보자 그녀는 무언가를 찾는 척 서랍장 안으로 손을 떨구었고, 그가 자기에게 바싹 다가왔을 때에야 비로소 그를 돌아보았다."], ["But her face, to which she tried to give a severe and resolute expression, betrayed bewilderment and suffering.", "하지만 엄격하고 단호한 표정을 지으려 애쓰던 그녀의 얼굴은 당혹감과 고통을 드러내고 있었다."]], [["“Dolly!”", "\"돌리!\""], ["he said in a subdued and timid voice.", "그가 가라앉고 머뭇거리는 목소리로 말했다."], ["He bent his head towards his shoulder and tried to look pitiful and humble, but for all that he was radiant with freshness and health.", "그는 고개를 어깨 쪽으로 숙이고 가엾고 비굴해 보이려고 애썼지만, 그럼에도 싱그러움과 건강함으로 환히 빛나고 있었다."], ["In a rapid glance she scanned his figure that beamed with health and freshness.", "그녀는 재빠른 눈길로 건강과 싱그러움으로 빛나는 그의 모습을 훑어보았다."], ["“Yes, he is happy and content!”", "'그래, 저이는 행복하고 만족스럽지!'"], ["she thought; “while I....", "그녀는 생각했다. '그런데 나는……"], ["And that disgusting good nature, which everyone likes him for and praises—I hate that good nature of his,”", "게다가 저 역겨운 호인 기질, 누구나 저이를 좋아하고 칭찬하게 만드는 저 호인 기질—나는 저이의 저 호인 기질이 싫어.'"], ["she thought.", "그녀는 생각했다."], ["Her mouth stiffened, the muscles of the cheek contracted on the right side of her pale, nervous face.", "그녀의 입이 굳어졌고, 창백하고 신경질적인 얼굴의 오른쪽 뺨 근육이 경련하듯 당겨졌다."]], [["“What do you want?”", "\"무슨 일이에요?\""], ["she said in a rapid, deep, unnatural voice.", "그녀가 빠르고 낮고 부자연스러운 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“Dolly!”", "\"돌리!\""], ["he repeated, with a quiver in his voice.", "그가 떨리는 목소리로 다시 불렀다."], ["“Anna is coming today.”", "\"오늘 안나가 와.\""]], [["“Well, what is that to me?", "\"그게 나하고 무슨 상관이죠?"], ["I can’t see her!”", "난 그 사람 못 만나요!\""], ["she cried.", "그녀가 소리쳤다."]], [["“But you must, really, Dolly....”", "\"하지만 정말 만나 줘야 해, 돌리…….\""]], [["“Go away, go away, go away!”", "\"가요, 가요, 가 버려요!\""], ["she shrieked, not looking at him, as though this shriek were called up by physical pain.", "그녀가 그를 쳐다보지도 않고 비명을 질렀는데, 마치 그 비명이 육체적 고통에서 터져 나오기라도 한 것 같았다."]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch could be calm when he thought of his wife, he could hope that she would come round, as Matvey expressed it, and could quietly go on reading his paper and drinking his coffee; but when he saw her tortured, suffering face, heard the tone of her voice, submissive to fate and full of despair, there was a catch in his breath and a lump in his throat, and his eyes began to shine with tears.", "스테판 아르카지치는 아내를 떠올릴 때에는 침착할 수 있었고, 마트베이의 표현대로 그녀가 마음을 돌리리라고 기대할 수 있었으며, 조용히 신문을 읽고 커피를 마실 수도 있었다. 하지만 고통에 짓눌려 괴로워하는 그녀의 얼굴을 보고, 운명에 순응하며 절망으로 가득 찬 그녀의 목소리를 들었을 때, 숨이 턱 막히고 목구멍에 무언가가 치밀어 올랐으며, 눈에는 눈물이 어리기 시작했다."]], [["“My God!", "\"맙소사!"], ["what have I done? Dolly!", "내가 무슨 짓을 한 거지?"], ["For God’s sake!...", "돌리! 제발!……"], ["You know....”", "당신도 알잖아…….\""], ["He could not go on; there was a sob in his throat.", "그는 말을 잇지 못했다. 목구멍에서 흐느낌이 솟구쳤다."]], [["She shut the bureau with a slam, and glanced at him.", "그녀는 서랍장을 쾅 닫고는 그를 흘낏 보았다."]], [["“Dolly, what can I say?...", "\"돌리, 내가 무슨 말을 할 수 있겠어?……"], ["One thing: forgive....", "한 가지만, 용서해 줘……"], ["Remember, cannot nine years of my life atone for an instant....”", "생각해 봐, 내 인생 구 년이 한순간의…… 속죄가 될 수는 없겠어…….\""]], [["She dropped her eyes and listened, expecting what he would say, as it were beseeching him in some way or other to make her believe differently.", "그녀는 눈을 떨군 채, 그가 무슨 말을 할지 기다리며 귀를 기울였는데, 마치 어떻게든 자기를 달리 믿게 해 달라고 그에게 애원하는 듯했다."]], [["“—instant of passion?”", "\"—한순간의 격정 말이야?\""], ["he said, and would have gone on, but at that word, as at a pang of physical pain, her lips stiffened again, and again the muscles of her right cheek worked.", "그가 말하고는 계속하려고 했지만, 그 말에 마치 육체적 고통이 찌르기라도 한 듯 그녀의 입술이 다시 굳어졌고, 오른쪽 뺨의 근육이 다시 실룩거렸다."]], [["“Go away, go out of the room!”", "\"가요, 방에서 나가요!\""], ["she shrieked still more shrilly, “and don’t talk to me of your passion and your loathsomeness.”", "그녀가 한층 더 날카롭게 비명을 질렀다. \"그리고 당신의 격정이니 당신의 그 추잡함이니 하는 얘기는 내게 하지 말아요.\""]], [["She tried to go out, but tottered, and clung to the back of a chair to support herself.", "그녀는 나가려고 했지만 비틀거렸고, 몸을 가누려고 의자 등받이를 붙잡았다."], ["His face relaxed, his lips swelled, his eyes were swimming with tears.", "그의 얼굴이 풀어졌고, 입술이 부풀었으며, 눈에는 눈물이 그렁그렁했다."]], [["“Dolly!”", "\"돌리!\""], ["he said, sobbing now; “for mercy’s sake, think of the children; they are not to blame!", "그가 이제는 흐느끼며 말했다. \"제발, 아이들을 생각해 줘."], ["I am to blame, and punish me, make me expiate my fault.", "아이들에게는 죄가 없잖아! 잘못은 나한테 있어, 나를 벌해, 내 잘못을 속죄하게 해 줘."], ["Anything I can do, I am ready to do anything!", "내가 할 수 있는 일이라면 무엇이든, 무엇이든 할 각오가 돼 있어!"], ["I am to blame, no words can express how much I am to blame!", "잘못은 나한테 있어, 내가 얼마나 잘못했는지는 어떤 말로도 다 표현할 수 없어!"], ["But, Dolly, forgive me!”", "하지만 돌리, 나를 용서해 줘!\""]], [["She sat down.", "그녀는 자리에 앉았다."], ["He listened to her hard, heavy breathing, and he was unutterably sorry for her.", "그는 그녀의 거칠고 무거운 숨소리에 귀를 기울였고, 그녀가 이루 말할 수 없이 가여웠다."], ["She tried several times to begin to speak, but could not.", "그녀는 몇 번이나 말을 꺼내려 했지만 할 수 없었다."], ["He waited.", "그는 기다렸다."]], [["“You remember the children, Stiva, to play with them; but I remember them, and know that this means their ruin,”", "\"당신은 아이들을 떠올릴 때 같이 놀아 주려고 떠올리죠, 스티바. 하지만 나는 아이들을 떠올릴 때 이게 그 애들의 파멸을 뜻한다는 걸 알아요.\""], ["she said—obviously one of the phrases she had more than once repeated to herself in the course of the last few days.", "그녀가 말했다—분명 지난 며칠 동안 스스로에게 여러 번 되뇌던 말 가운데 하나였다."]], [["She had called him “Stiva,” and he glanced at her with gratitude, and moved to take her hand, but she drew back from him with aversion.", "그녀가 그를 \"스티바\"라고 불렀고, 그는 고마운 마음으로 그녀를 흘낏 보고는 그녀의 손을 잡으려고 다가갔지만, 그녀는 혐오감에 그에게서 몸을 움츠렸다."]], [["“I think of the children, and for that reason I would do anything in the world to save them, but I don’t myself know how to save them.", "\"나는 아이들을 생각해요, 그래서 그 애들을 구할 수 있다면 세상 무슨 일이든 하겠어요, 하지만 어떻게 그 애들을 구해야 할지 나 자신도 모르겠어요."], ["By taking them away from their father, or by leaving them with a vicious father—yes, a vicious father....", "아버지에게서 떼어 놓아야 할지, 아니면 부도덕한 아버지 곁에 남겨 두어야 할지—그래요, 부도덕한 아버지……"], ["Tell me, after what ...", "말해 봐요, 그……"], ["has happened, can we live together?", "일이 있고 난 뒤에, 우리가 함께 살 수 있겠어요?"], ["Is that possible?", "그게 가능해요?"], ["Tell me, eh, is it possible?”", "말해 봐요, 네, 그게 가능하냐고요?\""], ["she repeated, raising her voice, “after my husband, the father of my children, enters into a love affair with his own children’s governess?”", "그녀가 목소리를 높이며 거듭 물었다. \"내 남편이, 내 아이들의 아버지가, 제 자식들의 가정교사와 정을 통한 마당에?\""]], [["“But what could I do?", "\"하지만 내가 어쩔 수 있었겠어?"], ["what could I do?”", "내가 어쩔 수 있었겠냐고?\""], ["he kept saying in a pitiful voice, not knowing what he was saying, as his head sank lower and lower.", "그가 자기가 무슨 말을 하는지도 모른 채, 고개를 점점 더 깊이 떨구며 가엾은 목소리로 거듭 말했다."]], [["“You are loathsome to me, repulsive!”", "\"당신은 내게 역겨워요, 혐오스러워요!\""], ["she shrieked, getting more and more heated.", "그녀가 점점 더 격해지며 비명을 질렀다."], ["“Your tears mean nothing!", "\"당신 눈물 따위 아무 의미도 없어요!"], ["You have never loved me; you have neither heart nor honorable feeling!", "당신은 나를 사랑한 적이 한 번도 없어요. 당신에게는 마음도, 명예로운 감정도 없어요!"], ["You are hateful to me, disgusting, a stranger—yes, a complete stranger!”", "당신은 내게 가증스럽고, 역겹고, 남이에요—그래요, 완전한 남이라고요!\""], ["With pain and wrath she uttered the word so terrible to herself—stranger.", "고통과 분노에 차서 그녀는 자기 자신에게도 그토록 끔찍한 말을—남이라는 말을 내뱉었다."]], [["He looked at her, and the fury expressed in her face alarmed and amazed him.", "그는 그녀를 바라보았고, 그녀의 얼굴에 드러난 격분에 놀라며 당황했다."], ["He did not understand how his pity for her exasperated her.", "그는 자기가 그녀를 가엾게 여기는 것이 어째서 그녀를 격분시키는지 이해하지 못했다."], ["She saw in him sympathy for her, but not love.", "그녀는 그에게서 자기에 대한 동정은 보았으나 사랑은 보지 못했다."], ["“No, she hates me.", "'아니, 저 사람은 나를 미워해."], ["She will not forgive me,”", "나를 용서하지 않을 거야.'"], ["he thought.", "그는 생각했다."]], [["“It is awful!", "\"끔찍해!"], ["awful!”", "끔찍하다고!\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["At that moment in the next room a child began to cry; probably it had fallen down.", "바로 그 순간 옆방에서 아이가 울기 시작했다. 아마 넘어진 모양이었다."], ["Darya Alexandrovna listened, and her face suddenly softened.", "다리아 알렉산드로브나는 귀를 기울였고, 그녀의 얼굴이 갑자기 부드러워졌다."]], [["She seemed to be pulling herself together for a few seconds, as though she did not know where she was, and what she was doing, and getting up rapidly, she moved towards the door.", "그녀는 자기가 어디 있는지, 무엇을 하고 있는지조차 모르겠다는 듯, 몇 초 동안 정신을 추스르는 것 같더니, 황급히 일어나 문 쪽으로 움직였다."]], [["“Well, she loves my child,”", "'그래, 저 사람은 내 아이를 사랑하지.' 그는 아이의 울음소리에 그녀의 얼굴이 변하는 것을 알아채고는 생각했다."], ["he thought, noticing the change of her face at the child’s cry, “my child: how can she hate me?”", "'내 아이를 말이야. 그런데 어떻게 나를 미워할 수 있겠어?'"]], [["“Dolly, one word more,”", "\"돌리, 한마디만 더,\""], ["he said, following her.", "그가 그녀를 뒤따르며 말했다."]], [["“If you come near me, I will call in the servants, the children!", "\"내게 가까이 오면 하인들을, 아이들을 부르겠어요!"], ["They may all know you are a scoundrel!", "당신이 비열한 놈이라는 걸 다들 알아도 좋아요!"], ["I am going away at once, and you may live here with your mistress!”", "나는 지금 당장 떠날 거고, 당신은 여기서 당신 정부랑 살면 되잖아요!\""]], [["And she went out, slamming the door.", "그리고 그녀는 문을 쾅 닫으며 나가 버렸다."]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch sighed, wiped his face, and with a subdued tread walked out of the room.", "스테판 아르카지치는 한숨을 쉬고 얼굴을 훔치고는, 가만가만한 걸음으로 방을 나섰다. '마트베이는 그녀가 마음을 돌릴 거라고 하지만, 어떻게?"], ["“Matvey says she will come round; but how?", "도무지 그럴 가망이 조금도 보이질 않아."], ["I don’t see the least chance of it.", "아, 어휴, 어쩌면 이렇게 끔찍할까!"], ["Ah, oh, how horrible it is! And how vulgarly she shouted,”", "게다가 어쩌면 그렇게 천박하게 소리를 질러 댔는지.'"], ["he said to himself, remembering her shriek and the words—“scoundrel” and “mistress.”", "그는 그녀의 비명과 그 말들—\"비열한 놈\"이며 \"정부\"며 하는 말을 떠올리며 혼잣말을 했다."], ["“And very likely the maids were listening!", "'게다가 하녀들이 듣고 있었을지도 몰라!"], ["Horribly vulgar!", "끔찍하게 천박해!"], ["horrible!”", "끔찍해!'"], ["Stepan Arkadyevitch stood a few seconds alone, wiped his face, squared his chest, and walked out of the room.", "스테판 아르카지치는 몇 초 동안 혼자 서서 얼굴을 훔치고는, 가슴을 펴고 방을 나섰다."]], [["It was Friday, and in the dining-room the German watchmaker was winding up the clock.", "금요일이었고, 식당에서는 독일인 시계공이 시계의 태엽을 감고 있었다."], ["Stepan Arkadyevitch remembered his joke about this punctual, bald watchmaker, “that the German was wound up for a whole lifetime himself, to wind up watches,” and he smiled.", "스테판 아르카지치는 이 시간을 정확히 지키는 대머리 시계공에 대해 했던 농담을—\"저 독일인 자신이 시계 태엽을 감기 위해 평생 태엽이 감긴 사람\"이라던 농담을 떠올리고 미소 지었다."], ["Stepan Arkadyevitch was fond of a joke: “And maybe she will come round!", "스테판 아르카지치는 농담을 좋아했다. '그리고 어쩌면 그녀가 마음을 돌릴지도 몰라!"], ["That’s a good expression, ‘come round,’”", "거 참 괜찮은 표현이야, \"마음을 돌린다\"는 거.'"], ["he thought.", "그는 생각했다."], ["“I must repeat that.”", "'저 말은 꼭 한번 써먹어야지.'"]], [["“Matvey!”", "\"마트베이!\""], ["he shouted.", "그가 소리쳤다."], ["“Arrange everything with Darya in the sitting room for Anna Arkadyevna,”", "\"안나 아르카지예브나를 위해 다리야와 함께 응접실을 다 준비해 두게.\""], ["he said to Matvey when he came in.", "마트베이가 들어오자 그는 그에게 말했다."]], [["“Yes, sir.”", "\"알겠습니다, 나리.\""]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch put on his fur coat and went out onto the steps.", "스테판 아르카지치는 모피 외투를 걸치고 현관 계단으로 나섰다."]], [["“You won’t dine at home?”", "\"댁에서 식사하지 않으십니까?\""], ["said Matvey, seeing him off.", "마트베이가 그를 배웅하며 말했다."]], [["“That’s as it happens.", "\"그건 형편 봐서."], ["But here’s for the housekeeping,”", "그런데 자, 이건 살림에 보태게.\""], ["he said, taking ten roubles from his pocketbook.", "그가 지갑에서 십 루블을 꺼내며 말했다."], ["“That’ll be enough.”", "\"이만하면 넉넉할 거야.\""]], [["“Enough or not enough, we must make it do,”", "\"넉넉하든 모자라든, 그걸로 어떻게든 꾸려 봐야지요.\""], ["said Matvey, slamming the carriage door and stepping back onto the steps.", "마트베이가 마차 문을 쾅 닫고 다시 계단 위로 물러서며 말했다."]], [["Darya Alexandrovna meanwhile having pacified the child, and knowing from the sound of the carriage that he had gone off, went back again to her bedroom.", "그동안 다리아 알렉산드로브나는 아이를 달래고 나서, 마차 소리로 그가 떠난 것을 알고는 다시 자기 침실로 돌아갔다."], ["It was her solitary refuge from the household cares which crowded upon her directly she went out from it.", "그곳은 그녀가 나오기만 하면 곧장 밀려드는 집안일의 근심에서 벗어날 수 있는 유일한 피난처였다."], ["Even now, in the short time she had been in the nursery, the English governess and Matrona Philimonovna had succeeded in putting several questions to her, which did not admit of delay, and which only she could answer: “What were the children to put on for their walk?", "지금만 해도, 잠깐 육아실에 다녀온 사이에 영국인 가정교사와 마트료나 필리모노브나는 미룰 수 없는, 그리고 오직 그녀만이 답할 수 있는 몇 가지 질문을 그녀에게 던지는 데 용케 성공했다. \"아이들이 산책 나갈 때 뭘 입혀야 하나요?"], ["Should they have any milk?", "우유를 먹여야 할까요?"], ["Should not a new cook be sent for?”", "새 요리사를 부르러 보내야 하지 않을까요?\""]], [["“Ah, let me alone, let me alone!”", "\"아, 날 좀 내버려 둬요, 좀 내버려 두라고요!\""], ["she said, and going back to her bedroom she sat down in the same place as she had sat when talking to her husband, clasping tightly her thin hands with the rings that slipped down on her bony fingers, and fell to going over in her memory all the conversation.", "그녀가 말하고는 침실로 돌아가, 남편과 이야기할 때 앉아 있던 바로 그 자리에 앉아, 앙상한 손가락에서 흘러내리는 반지를 낀 야윈 두 손을 꼭 맞잡고서, 모든 대화를 기억 속에서 하나하나 되짚어 보기 시작했다."], ["“He has gone!", "'저이는 가 버렸어!"], ["But has he broken it off with her?”", "하지만 그 여자랑은 끝낸 걸까?' 그녀는 생각했다."], ["she thought. “Can it be he sees her?", "'아직도 그 여자를 만나고 있는 건 아닐까?"], ["Why didn’t I ask him!", "왜 물어보지 않았을까!"], ["No, no, reconciliation is impossible.", "아니, 아니야, 화해란 불가능해."], ["Even if we remain in the same house, we are strangers—strangers forever!”", "설령 한집에 남아 있더라도 우리는 남이야—영원히 남이라고!'"], ["She repeated again with special significance the word so dreadful to her.", "그녀는 자기에게 그토록 끔찍한 그 말을 특별한 무게를 담아 다시 되뇌었다."], ["“And how I loved him!", "'내가 저이를 얼마나 사랑했는데!"], ["my God, how I loved him!...", "맙소사, 얼마나 사랑했는데!……"], ["How I loved him!", "얼마나 사랑했는데!"], ["And now don’t I love him?", "그런데 지금은 사랑하지 않는 걸까?"], ["Don’t I love him more than before?", "전보다 더 사랑하고 있는 건 아닐까?"], ["The most horrible thing is,”", "무엇보다 끔찍한 건…….'"], ["she began, but did not finish her thought, because Matrona Philimonovna put her head in at the door.", "그녀는 생각을 시작했지만 끝맺지 못했는데, 마트료나 필리모노브나가 문틈으로 고개를 들이밀었기 때문이었다."]], [["“Let us send for my brother,”", "\"제 오라비를 부르러 보내야겠어요.\""], ["she said; “he can get a dinner anyway, or we shall have the children getting nothing to eat till six again, like yesterday.”", "그녀가 말했다. \"그래도 그이가 끼니는 차려 줄 테니까요, 안 그러면 어제처럼 또 아이들이 여섯 시까지 아무것도 못 먹게 될 거예요.\""]], [["“Very well, I will come directly and see about it.", "\"좋아요, 곧 가서 살펴볼게요."], ["But did you send for some new milk?”", "그런데 새 우유는 가지러 보냈어요?\""]], [["And Darya Alexandrovna plunged into the duties of the day, and drowned her grief in them for a time.", "그리고 다리아 알렉산드로브나는 하루의 일과 속으로 뛰어들어, 한동안 그 속에 자기 슬픔을 가라앉혔다."]], [["# Chapter 5", "# Chapter 5"]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch had learned easily at school, thanks to his excellent abilities, but he had been idle and mischievous, and therefore was one of the lowest in his class.", "스테판 아르카지치는 빼어난 재능 덕분에 학교에서 별 어려움 없이 공부했지만, 게으르고 장난이 심해서 반에서 늘 꼴찌에 가까운 축에 끼었다."], ["But in spite of his habitually dissipated mode of life, his inferior grade in the service, and his comparative youth, he occupied the honorable and lucrative position of president of one of the government boards at Moscow.", "그러나 평소의 방탕한 생활 방식과 관직에서의 낮은 직급, 비교적 젊은 나이에도 불구하고, 그는 모스크바의 어느 관청 국장이라는 명예롭고 수입이 짭짤한 자리를 차지하고 있었다."], ["This post he had received through his sister Anna’s husband, Alexey Alexandrovitch Karenin, who held one of the most important positions in the ministry to whose department the Moscow office belonged.", "이 자리는 누이 안나의 남편인 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치 카레닌을 통해 얻은 것이었는데, 카레닌은 모스크바의 그 관청이 속한 부처에서 가장 중요한 직책 중 하나를 맡고 있었다."], ["But if Karenin had not got his brother-in-law this berth, then through a hundred other personages—brothers, sisters, cousins, uncles, and aunts—Stiva Oblonsky would have received this post, or some other similar one, together with the salary of six thousand absolutely needful for him, as his affairs, in spite of his wife’s considerable property, were in an embarrassed condition.", "그러나 설령 카레닌이 처남에게 이 자리를 마련해 주지 않았더라도, 형제며 자매며 사촌이며 삼촌이며 이모며 고모 같은 수백 명의 다른 인물들을 통해 스티바 오블론스키는 이 자리나 그와 비슷한 다른 자리를, 그에게 절대적으로 필요한 육천 루블의 봉급과 함께 얻었을 것이다. 그도 그럴 것이, 아내의 상당한 재산에도 불구하고 그의 형편은 궁색한 처지였기 때문이다."]], [["Half Moscow and Petersburg were friends and relations of Stepan Arkadyevitch.", "모스크바와 페테르부르크 절반이 스테판 아르카지치의 친구이자 친척이었다."], ["He was born in the midst of those who had been and are the powerful ones of this world.", "그는 이 세상의 권력자였고 또 권력자인 사람들 한가운데서 태어났다."], ["One-third of the men in the government, the older men, had been friends of his father’s, and had known him in petticoats; another third were his intimate chums, and the remainder were friendly acquaintances.", "정부 인사들 가운데 나이 든 사람의 삼분의 일은 그의 아버지의 친구였고 그가 어린 시절부터 그를 알고 지냈으며, 또 다른 삼분의 일은 그의 절친한 단짝이었고, 나머지는 친한 지인이었다."], ["Consequently the distributors of earthly blessings in the shape of places, rents, shares, and such, were all his friends, and could not overlook one of their own set; and Oblonsky had no need to make any special exertion to get a lucrative post.", "따라서 자리며 임대료며 주식이며 하는 형태로 이 세상의 온갖 복을 나눠 주는 사람들이 모두 그의 친구였고, 자기편의 한 사람을 모른 척할 수가 없었다. 그러니 오블론스키는 수입 좋은 자리를 얻기 위해 특별히 애쓸 필요가 전혀 없었다."], ["He had only not to refuse things, not to show jealousy, not to be quarrelsome or take offense, all of which from his characteristic good nature he never did.", "그는 다만 거절하지 않고, 질투하지 않고, 시비를 걸거나 화를 내지만 않으면 되었는데, 타고난 선한 성품 덕분에 그는 그런 짓을 한 적이 없었다. 자기가 바라는 봉급의 자리를 얻지 못할 것이라는 말을 들었다면 그는 어처구니없게 여겼을 것이다."], ["It would have struck him as absurd if he had been told that he would not get a position with the salary he required, especially as he expected nothing out of the way; he only wanted what the men of his own age and standing did get, and he was no worse qualified for performing duties of the kind than any other man.", "더구나 그는 분에 넘치는 것을 바라는 것도 아니었다. 그저 자기 또래에 자기와 같은 지위에 있는 사람들이 으레 얻는 것을 원했을 뿐이고, 그런 종류의 직무를 수행하는 데 다른 누구보다 못한 자격을 갖춘 것도 아니었다."]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch was not merely liked by all who knew him for his good humor, but for his bright disposition, and his unquestionable honesty.", "스테판 아르카지치는 그를 아는 모든 사람에게서 단지 그 좋은 성정 때문만이 아니라 밝은 기질과 의심할 여지 없는 정직함 때문에도 사랑을 받았다."], ["In him, in his handsome, radiant figure, his sparkling eyes, black hair and eyebrows, and the white and red of his face, there was something which produced a physical effect of kindliness and good humor on the people who met him.", "잘생기고 환한 그의 모습, 반짝이는 눈, 검은 머리칼과 눈썹, 희고 붉은 얼굴빛에는 그를 만나는 사람들에게 친절함과 유쾌함의 물리적 효과를 일으키는 무언가가 있었다."], ["“Aha! Stiva!", "\"어이!"], ["Oblonsky!", "스티바!"], ["Here he is!”", "오블론스키!"], ["was almost always said with a smile of delight on meeting him.", "여기 왔구먼!\" 그를 만났을 때 사람들은 거의 언제나 반가운 미소를 지으며 이렇게 말했다."], ["Even though it happened at times that after a conversation with him it seemed that nothing particularly delightful had happened, the next day, and the next, everyone was just as delighted at meeting him again.", "더러 그와 이야기를 나눈 뒤에 특별히 즐거운 일은 아무것도 없었던 것처럼 여겨지는 때가 있어도, 그다음 날이나 그다음다음 날이나 사람들은 그를 다시 만나면 똑같이 반가워했다."]], [["After filling for three years the post of president of one of the government boards at Moscow, Stepan Arkadyevitch had won the respect, as well as the liking, of his fellow-officials, subordinates, and superiors, and all who had had business with him. The principal qualities in Stepan Arkadyevitch which had gained him this universal respect in the service consisted, in the first place, of his extreme indulgence for others, founded on a consciousness of his own shortcomings; secondly, of his perfect liberalism—not the liberalism he read of in the papers, but the liberalism that was in his blood, in virtue of which he treated all men perfectly equally and exactly the same, whatever their fortune or calling might be; and thirdly—the most important point—his complete indifference to the business in which he was engaged, in consequence of which he was never carried away, and never made mistakes.", "모스크바의 어느 관청 국장 자리를 삼 년 동안 지내면서, 스테판 아르카지치는 동료 관리들과 부하들과 상관들, 그리고 그와 업무로 엮인 모든 사람들로부터 호감뿐 아니라 존경까지 얻어 냈다. 관직에서 이런 보편적인 존경을 그에게 가져다준 스테판 아르카지치의 주된 자질은, 첫째로 자기 자신의 결점에 대한 자각에 바탕을 둔, 남에 대한 지극한 너그러움이었다. 둘째로 완벽한 자유주의였는데, 그것은 그가 신문에서 읽은 자유주의가 아니라 그의 핏속에 흐르는 자유주의였다. 그 덕분에 그는 상대가 어떤 재산을 가졌든 어떤 직업에 종사하든 모든 사람을 완벽하게 똑같이, 정확히 동등하게 대했다. 그리고 셋째로—이것이 가장 중요한 점인데—자기가 종사하는 업무에 대한 완벽한 무관심이었다. 그 덕분에 그는 결코 일에 휩쓸리지 않았고, 결코 실수하는 법이 없었다."]], [["On reaching the offices of the board, Stepan Arkadyevitch, escorted by a deferential porter with a portfolio, went into his little private room, put on his uniform, and went into the boardroom.", "관청에 도착한 스테판 아르카지치는 서류 가방을 든 공손한 수위의 안내를 받으며 자기의 작은 집무실로 들어가 제복을 입고 회의실로 들어갔다."], ["The clerks and copyists all rose, greeting him with good-humored deference.", "서기들과 필경사들이 모두 일어나 유쾌하고 공손한 태도로 그를 맞이했다."], ["Stepan Arkadyevitch moved quickly, as ever, to his place, shook hands with his colleagues, and sat down.", "스테판 아르카지치는 여느 때처럼 잰걸음으로 자기 자리로 가서 동료들과 악수를 나누고 자리에 앉았다."], ["He made a joke or two, and talked just as much as was consistent with due decorum, and began work.", "그는 농담을 한두 마디 던지고, 격식에 어긋나지 않을 만큼만 이야기를 나눈 뒤 일을 시작했다."], ["No one knew better than Stepan Arkadyevitch how to hit on the exact line between freedom, simplicity, and official stiffness necessary for the agreeable conduct of business.", "자유로움과 소탈함과 관청 특유의 딱딱함 사이에서, 업무를 기분 좋게 처리하는 데 필요한 정확한 선을 짚어 내는 데 스테판 아르카지치보다 능한 사람은 없었다."], ["A secretary, with the good-humored deference common to everyone in Stepan Arkadyevitch’s office, came up with papers, and began to speak in the familiar and easy tone which had been introduced by Stepan Arkadyevitch.", "스테판 아르카지치의 관청에 있는 모든 사람에게 공통된 유쾌하고 공손한 태도로 서기 한 명이 서류를 들고 다가와, 스테판 아르카지치가 도입한 그 친근하고 편안한 어조로 말을 꺼냈다."]], [["“We have succeeded in getting the information from the government department of Penza.", "\"펜자의 정부 부처에서 정보를 얻는 데 성공했습니다."], ["Here, would you care?...”", "자, 보시겠습니까?……\""]], [["“You’ve got them at last?”", "\"드디어 손에 넣었군?\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, laying his finger on the paper.", "스테판 아르카지치가 서류에 손가락을 짚으며 말했다."], ["“Now, gentlemen....”", "\"자, 여러분…….\""]], [["And the sitting of the board began.", "그리고 회의가 시작되었다."]], [["“If they knew,”", "'저들이 알기나 할까,'"], ["he thought, bending his head with a significant air as he listened to the report, “what a guilty little boy their president was half an hour ago.”", "그는 보고를 들으며 자못 진지한 태도로 고개를 숙인 채 생각했다. '자기들 국장이 삼십 분 전에 얼마나 죄지은 어린애 같았는지를.'"], ["And his eyes were laughing during the reading of the report.", "그리고 보고가 낭독되는 동안 그의 눈은 웃고 있었다."], ["Till two o’clock the sitting would go on without a break, and at two o’clock there would be an interval and luncheon.", "회의는 두 시까지 쉼 없이 이어질 것이고, 두 시가 되면 휴식 시간과 점심 식사가 있을 터였다."]], [["It was not yet two, when the large glass doors of the boardroom suddenly opened and someone came in.", "아직 두 시가 되지 않았는데, 회의실의 커다란 유리문이 갑자기 열리며 누군가 들어왔다."]], [["All the officials sitting on the further side under the portrait of the Tsar and the eagle, delighted at any distraction, looked round at the door; but the doorkeeper standing at the door at once drove out the intruder, and closed the glass door after him.", "황제와 독수리 초상화 아래 안쪽에 앉아 있던 관리들은 어떤 기분 전환거리든 반가운 터라 일제히 문 쪽을 돌아보았다. 그러나 문가에 서 있던 수위가 곧장 침입자를 쫓아내고 그 뒤로 유리문을 닫아 버렸다."]], [["When the case had been read through, Stepan Arkadyevitch got up and stretched, and by way of tribute to the liberalism of the times took out a cigarette in the boardroom and went into his private room.", "안건을 다 검토하고 나자, 스테판 아르카지치는 일어나 기지개를 켜고는, 시대의 자유주의에 대한 경의의 표시로 회의실에서 담배 한 개비를 꺼내 들고 자기 집무실로 들어갔다."], ["Two of the members of the board, the old veteran in the service, Nikitin, and the Kammerjunker Grinevitch, went in with him.", "관청에서 오래 근무한 노련한 베테랑 니키틴과 시종보 그리네비치, 이 두 동료가 그와 함께 들어갔다. * 시종보(Kammerjunker): 러시아 궁정의 하급 시종 관직."]], [["“We shall have time to finish after lunch,”", "\"점심 먹고 마저 끝낼 시간은 있겠어.\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch.", "스테판 아르카지치가 말했다."]], [["“To be sure we shall!”", "\"틀림없이 있고말고요!\""], ["said Nikitin.", "니키틴이 말했다."]], [["“A pretty sharp fellow this Fomin must be,”", "\"이 포민이라는 친구, 제법 약삭빠른 작자인가 봅니다.\""], ["said Grinevitch of one of the persons taking part in the case they were examining.", "그리네비치가 그들이 검토 중인 사건에 연루된 한 인물을 두고 말했다."]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch frowned at Grinevitch’s words, giving him thereby to understand that it was improper to pass judgment prematurely, and made him no reply.", "스테판 아르카지치는 그리네비치의 말에 미간을 찌푸려, 섣불리 판단을 내리는 것은 온당치 못하다는 뜻을 그에게 내비치고는 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았다."]], [["“Who was that came in?”", "\"방금 들어온 게 누군가?\""], ["he asked the doorkeeper.", "그가 수위에게 물었다."]], [["“Someone, your excellency, crept in without permission directly my back was turned.", "\"각하, 누군가가 제가 등을 돌린 사이에 허락도 없이 슬그머니 들어왔습니다."], ["He was asking for you.", "각하를 찾던데요."], ["I told him: when the members come out, then....”", "제가 그랬습죠, 위원님들이 나오시면 그때…….\""]], [["“Where is he?”", "\"그자가 어디 있나?\""]], [["“Maybe he’s gone into the passage, but here he comes anyway.", "\"복도로 나갔는지도 모릅니다만, 마침 저기 오는군요."], ["That is he,”", "저 사람입니다.\""], ["said the doorkeeper, pointing to a strongly built, broad-shouldered man with a curly beard, who, without taking off his sheepskin cap, was running lightly and rapidly up the worn steps of the stone staircase.", "수위가 가리킨 곳에는 곱슬곱슬한 턱수염을 기른, 단단한 체격에 어깨가 떡 벌어진 사내가 있었다. 그는 양가죽 모자도 벗지 않은 채 돌계단의 닳은 디딤돌을 가볍고 빠르게 뛰어 올라오고 있었다."], ["One of the members going down—a lean official with a portfolio—stood out of his way and looked disapprovingly at the legs of the stranger, then glanced inquiringly at Oblonsky.", "계단을 내려가던 위원 한 명—서류 가방을 든 마른 관리—이 그에게 길을 비켜 주며 낯선 사내의 다리를 못마땅한 눈길로 흘끗 보더니 오블론스키를 향해 묻는 듯한 시선을 던졌다."]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch was standing at the top of the stairs.", "스테판 아르카지치는 계단 꼭대기에 서 있었다."], ["His good-naturedly beaming face above the embroidered collar of his uniform beamed more than ever when he recognized the man coming up.", "제복의 수놓인 깃 위로 환하게 빛나던 그의 선량한 얼굴은, 올라오는 사내를 알아본 순간 그 어느 때보다 환하게 빛났다."]], [["“Why, it’s actually you, Levin, at last!”", "\"아니, 정말 자네로군, 레빈, 드디어 왔네그려!\""], ["he said with a friendly mocking smile, scanning Levin as he approached.", "그는 다가오는 레빈을 찬찬히 훑어보며 다정하면서도 놀리는 듯한 미소를 띠고 말했다."], ["“How is it you have deigned to look me up in this den?”", "\"이런 소굴까지 황송하게도 날 찾아 주다니, 어쩐 일인가?\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, and not content with shaking hands, he kissed his friend.", "스테판 아르카지치는 이렇게 말하고는, 악수만으로는 성에 차지 않는 듯 친구에게 입을 맞추었다."], ["“Have you been here long?”", "\"온 지 오래됐나?\""]], [["“I have just come, and very much wanted to see you,”", "\"방금 막 왔네. 자네가 무척 보고 싶었어.\""], ["said Levin, looking shyly and at the same time angrily and uneasily around.", "레빈은 수줍어하면서도 한편으론 화난 듯, 불안한 듯 주위를 둘러보며 말했다."]], [["“Well, let’s go into my room,”", "\"자, 내 방으로 가세.\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, who knew his friend’s sensitive and irritable shyness, and, taking his arm, he drew him along, as though guiding him through dangers.", "친구의 예민하고 신경질적인 수줍음을 잘 아는 스테판 아르카지치는, 마치 위험 속을 헤쳐 그를 인도하기라도 하듯 그의 팔을 잡고 끌고 갔다."]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch was on familiar terms with almost all his acquaintances, and called almost all of them by their Christian names: old men of sixty, boys of twenty, actors, ministers, merchants, and adjutant-generals, so that many of his intimate chums were to be found at the extreme ends of the social ladder, and would have been very much surprised to learn that they had, through the medium of Oblonsky, something in common.", "스테판 아르카지치는 거의 모든 지인들과 흉허물 없는 사이였고, 거의 모든 이를 세례명으로 불렀다. 예순의 노인이든 스무 살의 청년이든, 배우든 장관이든 상인이든 시종무관장이든 가리지 않았다."], ["He was the familiar friend of everyone with whom he took a glass of champagne, and he took a glass of champagne with everyone, and when in consequence he met any of his disreputable chums, as he used in joke to call many of his friends, in the presence of his subordinates, he well knew how, with his characteristic tact, to diminish the disagreeable impression made on them.", "그래서 그의 절친한 단짝들 가운데는 사회적 사다리의 양극단에 자리한 이들이 있었는데, 그들은 오블론스키를 매개로 자기들에게 무언가 공통점이 있다는 사실을 알면 무척 놀랄 터였다. 그는 함께 샴페인 한 잔을 기울인 모든 이의 흉허물 없는 친구였고, 또 누구하고나 샴페인 한 잔을 기울였다."], ["Levin was not a disreputable chum, but Oblonsky, with his ready tact, felt that Levin fancied he might not care to show his intimacy with him before his subordinates, and so he made haste to take him off into his room.", "그러다 부하들 앞에서 그가 농담 삼아 흔히 '못된 단짝들'이라 부르던 친구들 가운데 누구라도 마주치면, 그는 타고난 재치로 그들에게 끼친 불쾌한 인상을 누그러뜨리는 법을 잘 알았다. 레빈은 못된 단짝은 아니었지만, 오블론스키는 특유의 민첩한 재치로, 부하들 앞에서 자기와의 친분을 드러내는 것을 레빈이 달가워하지 않을지도 모른다고 짐작하고는 서둘러 그를 자기 방으로 데리고 들어갔다."]], [["Levin was almost of the same age as Oblonsky; their intimacy did not rest merely on champagne.", "레빈은 오블론스키와 거의 동갑이었다. 그들의 친분은 단지 샴페인에만 기댄 것이 아니었다."], ["Levin had been the friend and companion of his early youth.", "레빈은 그의 어린 시절의 친구이자 동무였다."], ["They were fond of one another in spite of the difference of their characters and tastes, as friends are fond of one another who have been together in early youth.", "두 사람은 성격과 취향의 차이에도 불구하고, 어린 시절을 함께 보낸 친구들이 서로 아끼듯 서로를 아꼈다."], ["But in spite of this, each of them—as is often the way with men who have selected careers of different kinds—though in discussion he would even justify the other’s career, in his heart despised it.", "그러나 그런데도 두 사람은—서로 다른 길을 택한 사람들에게 흔히 그렇듯—이야기를 나눌 때면 상대방의 길을 두둔하면서도 마음속으로는 그것을 경멸했다."], ["It seemed to each of them that the life he led himself was the only real life, and the life led by his friend was a mere phantasm.", "두 사람 각자에게는 자기가 사는 삶만이 유일하게 진짜 삶이고, 친구가 사는 삶은 한낱 허깨비처럼 여겨졌다."], ["Oblonsky could not restrain a slight mocking smile at the sight of Levin.", "오블론스키는 레빈을 보자 살짝 놀리는 듯한 미소를 참을 수 없었다."], ["How often he had seen him come up to Moscow from the country where he was doing something, but what precisely Stepan Arkadyevitch could never quite make out, and indeed he took no interest in the matter.", "시골에서 무언가를 하던 그가 모스크바로 올라오는 것을 그동안 얼마나 자주 보았던가. 그런데 그가 정확히 무엇을 하는지는 스테판 아르카지치도 도무지 알 수 없었고, 사실 그 일에 아무런 관심도 없었다."], ["Levin arrived in Moscow always excited and in a hurry, rather ill at ease and irritated by his own want of ease, and for the most part with a perfectly new, unexpected view of things.", "레빈은 언제나 흥분한 채 서두르며, 다소 거북해하고 또 자기가 거북해하는 것에 짜증을 내며, 대개는 사물에 대한 전혀 새롭고 뜻밖의 견해를 품고 모스크바에 나타났다."], ["Stepan Arkadyevitch laughed at this, and liked it.", "스테판 아르카지치는 이런 점을 보고 웃었지만 또 좋아했다."], ["In the same way Levin in his heart despised the town mode of life of his friend, and his official duties, which he laughed at, and regarded as trifling.", "마찬가지로 레빈도 마음속으로는 친구의 도시적인 생활 방식을, 그리고 그가 비웃으며 하찮게 여기던 친구의 관직 업무를 경멸했다."], ["But the difference was that Oblonsky, as he was doing the same as everyone did, laughed complacently and good-humoredly, while Levin laughed without complacency and sometimes angrily.", "다만 차이가 있다면, 오블론스키는 남들이 다 하는 것을 자기도 하는 터라 흡족하고 유쾌하게 웃었던 반면, 레빈은 흡족함 없이, 때로는 화를 내며 웃었다는 점이다."]], [["“We have long been expecting you,”", "\"우린 오래전부터 자네를 기다리고 있었네.\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, going into his room and letting Levin’s hand go as though to show that here all danger was over.", "스테판 아르카지치가 자기 방으로 들어서며, 이제 모든 위험이 끝났다는 것을 보여 주기라도 하듯 레빈의 손을 놓으며 말했다."], ["“I am very, very glad to see you,”", "\"정말, 정말 반갑네.\" 그가 말을 이었다."], ["he went on. “Well, how are you?", "\"그래, 어떻게 지냈나?"], ["Eh?", "응?"], ["When did you come?”", "언제 왔어?\""]], [["Levin was silent, looking at the unknown faces of Oblonsky’s two companions, and especially at the hand of the elegant Grinevitch, which had such long white fingers, such long yellow filbert-shaped nails, and such huge shining studs on the shirt-cuff, that apparently they absorbed all his attention, and allowed him no freedom of thought.", "레빈은 오블론스키의 두 동료의 낯선 얼굴을, 특히 우아한 그리네비치의 손을 바라보며 입을 다물고 있었다. 그 손은 길고 흰 손가락에, 길고 노르스름한 개암 모양 손톱에, 셔츠 소맷부리에 박힌 어찌나 큼지막하게 번쩍이는 단추가 달려 있었던지, 보아하니 그것이 그의 주의를 온통 빨아들여 자유로이 생각할 여지조차 주지 않는 듯했다."], ["Oblonsky noticed this at once, and smiled.", "오블론스키는 이를 곧바로 알아채고 미소 지었다."]], [["“Ah, to be sure, let me introduce you,” he said. “My colleagues: Philip Ivanitch Nikitin, Mihail Stanislavitch Grinevitch”—and turning to Levin—“a district councilor, a modern district councilman, a gymnast who lifts thirteen stone with one hand, a cattle-breeder and sportsman, and my friend, Konstantin Dmitrievitch Levin, the brother of Sergey Ivanovitch Koznishev.”", "\"아, 참, 소개하지.\" 그가 말했다. \"내 동료들이네. 필립 이바니치 니키틴, 미하일 스타니슬라비치 그리네비치.\" 그러고는 레빈 쪽으로 돌아서서 말했다. \"이쪽은 군 자치회 의원이자 신식 자치회 위원이고, 한 손으로 십삼 스톤을 들어 올리는 운동가요, 가축 사육가에 사냥꾼이며, 내 친구인 콘스탄틴 드미트리예비치 레빈이라네. 세르게이 이바노비치 코즈니셰프의 동생일세.\""]], [["“Delighted,”", "\"반갑습니다.\""], ["said the veteran.", "베테랑이 말했다."]], [["“I have the honor of knowing your brother, Sergey Ivanovitch,”", "\"형님이신 세르게이 이바노비치를 알게 되어 영광입니다.\""], ["said Grinevitch, holding out his slender hand with its long nails.", "그리네비치가 긴 손톱이 달린 가느다란 손을 내밀며 말했다."]], [["Levin frowned, shook hands coldly, and at once turned to Oblonsky.", "레빈은 미간을 찌푸리며 냉랭하게 악수하고는 곧장 오블론스키 쪽으로 돌아섰다."], ["Though he had a great respect for his half-brother, an author well known to all Russia, he could not endure it when people treated him not as Konstantin Levin, but as the brother of the celebrated Koznishev.", "온 러시아에 널리 알려진 작가인 이복형을 그는 무척 존경했지만, 사람들이 자기를 콘스탄틴 레빈이 아니라 그 유명한 코즈니셰프의 동생으로 대하는 것은 견딜 수가 없었다."]], [["“No, I am no longer a district councilor.", "\"아니, 난 이제 자치회 의원이 아닐세."], ["I have quarreled with them all, and don’t go to the meetings any more,”", "그자들과 모조리 사이가 틀어졌고, 더 이상 회의에도 안 나가.\""], ["he said, turning to Oblonsky.", "그가 오블론스키 쪽으로 돌아서며 말했다."]], [["“You’ve been quick about it!”", "\"행동이 빠르군!\""], ["said Oblonsky with a smile.", "오블론스키가 미소 지으며 말했다."], ["“But how?", "\"한데 어쩌다가?"], ["why?”", "왜?\""]], [["“It’s a long story.", "\"긴 얘기야."], ["I will tell you some time,”", "언제 들려주지.\""], ["said Levin, but he began telling him at once.", "레빈은 이렇게 말했지만 곧장 이야기를 시작했다."], ["“Well, to put it shortly, I was convinced that nothing was really done by the district councils, or ever could be,”", "\"뭐, 짧게 말하자면, 자치회로는 실제로 아무것도 이루어지는 게 없고 앞으로도 결코 이루어질 수 없다는 걸 확신하게 됐어.\""], ["he began, as though someone had just insulted him.", "그는 마치 누가 방금 자기를 모욕하기라도 한 양 입을 열었다."], ["“On one side it’s a plaything; they play at being a parliament, and I’m neither young enough nor old enough to find amusement in playthings; and on the other side”", "\"한편으로 그건 장난감이야. 자기들이 무슨 의회라도 되는 양 놀음을 하는 거지."], ["(he stammered) “it’s a means for the coterie of the district to make money.", "난 장난감을 가지고 즐기기엔 그리 젊지도 그리 늙지도 않았어. 또 다른 한편으로는\""], ["Formerly they had wardships, courts of justice, now they have the district council—not in the form of bribes, but in the form of unearned salary,”", "(그는 말을 더듬었다) \"그건 군의 패거리가 돈을 버는 수단이지. 예전엔 후견인이니 재판소니 하는 게 있었는데, 이제는 자치회가 있는 거야—뇌물의 형태가 아니라 일하지 않고 받는 봉급의 형태로 말이야.\""], ["he said, as hotly as though someone of those present had opposed his opinion.", "그는 마치 그 자리에 있는 누군가가 자기 의견에 반대라도 한 양 열을 올리며 말했다."]], [["“Aha!", "\"아하!"], ["You’re in a new phase again, I see—a conservative,”", "또 새로운 국면에 접어들었군그래—보수주의자로 말이야.\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch.", "스테판 아르카지치가 말했다."], ["“However, we can go into that later.”", "\"하지만 그 얘긴 나중에 하지.\""]], [["“Yes, later.", "\"그래, 나중에."], ["But I wanted to see you,”", "한데 난 자네가 보고 싶었어.\""], ["said Levin, looking with hatred at Grinevitch’s hand.", "레빈은 그리네비치의 손을 증오 어린 눈길로 바라보며 말했다."]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch gave a scarcely perceptible smile.", "스테판 아르카지치는 거의 알아챌 수 없는 미소를 지었다."]], [["“How was it you used to say you would never wear European dress again?”", "\"다시는 유럽식 옷을 안 입겠다고 늘 말하던 게 어떻게 된 건가?\""], ["he said, scanning his new suit, obviously cut by a French tailor.", "그는 분명 프랑스 재단사가 지었을 친구의 새 양복을 훑어보며 말했다."], ["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["I see: a new phase.”", "알겠네, 새로운 국면이로군.\""]], [["Levin suddenly blushed, not as grown men blush, slightly, without being themselves aware of it, but as boys blush, feeling that they are ridiculous through their shyness, and consequently ashamed of it and blushing still more, almost to the point of tears.", "레빈은 별안간 얼굴을 붉혔는데, 그것은 어른들이 자기도 모르게 살짝 붉히는 식이 아니라, 자기가 수줍음 때문에 우스꽝스러워 보인다고 느끼고는 그것이 부끄러워 더욱더, 거의 눈물이 날 지경으로 얼굴을 붉히는 소년들의 식이었다."], ["And it was so strange to see this sensible, manly face in such a childish plight, that Oblonsky left off looking at him.", "그리고 이 분별 있고 사내다운 얼굴이 그토록 어린애 같은 곤경에 빠진 모습을 보는 것이 어찌나 묘했던지, 오블론스키는 그를 바라보던 눈길을 거두었다."]], [["“Oh, where shall we meet?", "\"아, 우리 어디서 만날까?"], ["You know I want very much to talk to you,”", "자네하고 꼭 얘기를 나누고 싶은데 말이야.\""], ["said Levin.", "레빈이 말했다."]], [["Oblonsky seemed to ponder.", "오블론스키는 곰곰이 생각하는 듯했다."]], [["“I’ll tell you what: let’s go to Gurin’s to lunch, and there we can talk.", "\"이러면 어떻겠나. 구린 식당에 가서 점심을 먹지."], ["I am free till three.”", "거기서 얘기하면 되겠어. 난 세 시까지 한가하니까.\""]], [["“No,”", "\"아니야.\""], ["answered Levin, after an instant’s thought, “I have got to go on somewhere else.”", "레빈은 잠시 생각하더니 대답했다. \"난 다른 데 또 가 봐야 해.\""]], [["“All right, then, let’s dine together.”", "\"그럼 좋아, 같이 저녁이나 먹세.\""]], [["“Dine together?", "\"같이 저녁을?"], ["But I have nothing very particular, only a few words to say, and a question I want to ask you, and we can have a talk afterwards.”", "하지만 난 특별히 할 얘기가 있는 건 아니고, 그저 몇 마디 하고 싶은 말이랑 묻고 싶은 게 하나 있을 뿐이야. 얘긴 그다음에 나눠도 되고.\""]], [["“Well, say the few words, then, at once, and we’ll gossip after dinner.”", "\"그럼 그 몇 마디를 지금 당장 하게나. 잡담은 저녁 먹고 나서 하면 되지.\""]], [["“Well, it’s this,”", "\"그게 말이야,\""], ["said Levin; “but it’s of no importance, though.”", "레빈이 말했다. \"한데 별로 중요한 건 아니야.\""]], [["His face all at once took an expression of anger from the effort he was making to surmount his shyness.", "수줍음을 떨쳐 내려 안간힘을 쓰느라 그의 얼굴은 별안간 화가 난 듯한 표정을 띠었다."]], [["“What are the Shtcherbatskys doing?", "\"셰르바츠키 댁은 어떻게들 지내나?"], ["Everything as it used to be?”", "다들 예전 그대로인가?\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch, who had long known that Levin was in love with his sister-in-law, Kitty, gave a hardly perceptible smile, and his eyes sparkled merrily.", "레빈이 자기 처제 키티에게 반해 있다는 것을 오래전부터 알고 있던 스테판 아르카지치는 거의 알아챌 수 없는 미소를 짓고는, 그의 눈이 즐겁게 반짝였다."]], [["“You said a few words, but I can’t answer in a few words, because....", "\"몇 마디라고 했지만, 난 몇 마디로는 대답할 수가 없어. 왜냐하면……"], ["Excuse me a minute....”", "잠깐만 실례하지…….\""]], [["A secretary came in, with respectful familiarity and the modest consciousness, characteristic of every secretary, of superiority to his chief in the knowledge of their business; he went up to Oblonsky with some papers, and began, under pretense of asking a question, to explain some objection.", "서기 한 명이 들어왔다. 공손하면서도 흉허물 없는 태도로, 그리고 모든 서기에게 공통된, 업무 지식에서만큼은 상관보다 우월하다는 겸손한 자의식을 풍기며 그는 서류 몇 장을 들고 오블론스키에게 다가가, 질문을 하는 척하며 어떤 이의 사항을 설명하기 시작했다."], ["Stepan Arkadyevitch, without hearing him out, laid his hand genially on the secretary’s sleeve.", "스테판 아르카지치는 그의 말을 끝까지 듣지도 않고 서기의 소맷자락에 다정하게 손을 얹었다."]], [["“No, you do as I told you,”", "\"아니, 내가 말한 대로 하게.\" 그는 미소로 말을 부드럽게 누그러뜨리며 이렇게 말했다."], ["he said, softening his words with a smile, and with a brief explanation of his view of the matter he turned away from the papers, and said: “So do it that way, if you please, Zahar Nikititch.”", "그러고는 그 문제에 대한 자기 견해를 간략히 설명한 뒤 서류에서 손을 떼며 말했다. \"그러니 그렇게 처리해 주게, 자하르 니키티치.\""]], [["The secretary retired in confusion.", "서기는 당황한 채 물러갔다."], ["During the consultation with the secretary Levin had completely recovered from his embarrassment.", "서기와 의논하는 동안 레빈은 거북함에서 완전히 벗어나 있었다."], ["He was standing with his elbows on the back of a chair, and on his face was a look of ironical attention.", "그는 의자 등받이에 두 팔꿈치를 괴고 서서, 얼굴에는 빈정거리는 듯한 주의 깊은 표정을 띠고 있었다."]], [["“I don’t understand it, I don’t understand it,”", "\"난 이해가 안 가, 도무지 이해가 안 가.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“What don’t you understand?”", "\"뭐가 이해가 안 간다는 건가?\""], ["said Oblonsky, smiling as brightly as ever, and picking up a cigarette.", "오블론스키가 여느 때처럼 환하게 웃으며 담배 한 개비를 집어 들었다."], ["He expected some queer outburst from Levin.", "그는 레빈에게서 어떤 기이한 발언이 터져 나오리라 예상하고 있었다."]], [["“I don’t understand what you are doing,”", "\"난 자네가 하는 일이 이해가 안 가.\""], ["said Levin, shrugging his shoulders.", "레빈이 어깨를 으쓱하며 말했다."], ["“How can you do it seriously?”", "\"어떻게 이런 걸 진지하게 할 수가 있나?\""]], [["“Why not?”", "\"왜 못 하겠나?\""]], [["“Why, because there’s nothing in it.”", "\"아니, 거기엔 아무 알맹이가 없으니까.\""]], [["“You think so, but we’re overwhelmed with work.”", "\"자넨 그렇게 생각하겠지만, 우린 일에 파묻혀 산다네.\""]], [["“On paper.", "\"종이 위에서나 그렇지."], ["But, there, you’ve a gift for it,”", "하지만 자네야 그쪽에 타고난 재능이 있으니까.\""], ["added Levin.", "레빈이 덧붙였다."]], [["“That’s to say, you think there’s a lack of something in me?”", "\"그러니까 자네 말은, 나한테 뭔가 부족한 게 있다는 거로군?\""]], [["“Perhaps so,”", "\"그럴지도 모르지.\""], ["said Levin.", "레빈이 말했다."], ["“But all the same I admire your grandeur, and am proud that I’ve a friend in such a great person.", "\"하지만 어쨌든 나는 자네의 위풍당당함에 감탄하고, 그토록 대단한 인물을 친구로 둔 게 자랑스럽네."], ["You’ve not answered my question, though,”", "그런데 자넨 아직 내 질문에 답을 안 했어.\""], ["he went on, with a desperate effort looking Oblonsky straight in the face.", "그는 필사적으로 안간힘을 쓰며 오블론스키의 얼굴을 똑바로 바라보고 말을 이었다."]], [["“Oh, that’s all very well.", "\"오, 그건 다 좋은데 말이야."], ["You wait a bit, and you’ll come to this yourself.", "좀만 기다려 보게, 자네도 언젠가 이렇게 될 테니까."], ["It’s very nice for you to have over six thousand acres in the Karazinsky district, and such muscles, and the freshness of a girl of twelve; still you’ll be one of us one day.", "자네야 카라진스키 군에 육천 에이커가 넘는 땅이 있고, 그런 근육에, 열두 살 소녀 같은 싱싱함이 있으니 좋지. 그래도 언젠가는 자네도 우리 같은 사람이 될 거야."], ["Yes, as to your question, there is no change, but it’s a pity you’ve been away so long.”", "그래, 자네 질문에 답하자면, 달라진 건 없어. 다만 자네가 그렇게 오래 떠나 있었던 게 안타깝군.\""]], [["“Oh, why so?”", "\"오, 어째서?\""], ["Levin queried, panic-stricken.", "레빈이 겁에 질려 캐물었다."]], [["“Oh, nothing,”", "\"오, 아무것도 아니야.\""], ["responded Oblonsky.", "오블론스키가 대답했다."], ["“We’ll talk it over.", "\"그 얘긴 나중에 함께 나누지."], ["But what’s brought you up to town?”", "그런데 자넨 무슨 일로 시내에 올라온 건가?\""]], [["“Oh, we’ll talk about that, too, later on,”", "\"오, 그것도 나중에 얘기하지.\""], ["said Levin, reddening again up to his ears.", "레빈은 다시 귀밑까지 빨개지며 말했다."]], [["“All right.", "\"알았네."], ["I see,”", "알겠어.\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch.", "스테판 아르카지치가 말했다."], ["“I should ask you to come to us, you know, but my wife’s not quite the thing.", "\"있잖아, 우리 집에 들르라고 청하고 싶지만, 아내가 좀 편치 않아서 말이야."], ["But I tell you what; if you want to see them, they’re sure now to be at the Zoological Gardens from four to five.", "그건 그렇고 이러면 어떻겠나. 자네가 그 집 사람들을 보고 싶다면, 지금쯤이면 분명 동물원에 네 시부터 다섯 시까지 있을 거야."], ["Kitty skates.", "키티가 스케이트를 타거든."], ["You drive along there, and I’ll come and fetch you, and we’ll go and dine somewhere together.”", "자넨 그리로 가 있게. 내가 자네를 데리러 가서, 함께 어디 가서 저녁이나 먹지.\""]], [["“Capital.", "\"좋아."], ["So good-bye till then.”", "그럼 그때 봄세.\""]], [["“Now mind, you’ll forget, I know you, or rush off home to the country!”", "\"이봐, 명심하게. 자넨 잊어버리거나, 아니면 시골 집으로 냅다 달아날 게 뻔하니까 말이야."], ["Stepan Arkadyevitch called out laughing.", "내가 자넬 잘 알지!\""], ["“No, truly!”", "스테판 아르카지치가 웃으며 소리쳤다."]], [["And Levin went out of the room, only when he was in the doorway remembering that he had forgotten to take leave of Oblonsky’s colleagues.", "\"아니야, 정말이야!\" 그러고서 레빈은 방을 나섰는데, 문간에 이르러서야 오블론스키의 동료들에게 작별 인사하는 것을 잊었다는 사실을 떠올렸다."]], [["“That gentleman must be a man of great energy,”", "\"저 신사 분은 굉장히 정력적인 사람인가 봅니다.\""], ["said Grinevitch, when Levin had gone away.", "레빈이 떠나자 그리네비치가 말했다."]], [["“Yes, my dear boy,”", "\"그래, 이 사람아.\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, nodding his head, “he’s a lucky fellow!", "스테판 아르카지치가 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다. \"운 좋은 친구지!"], ["Over six thousand acres in the Karazinsky district; everything before him; and what youth and vigor!", "카라진스키 군에 육천 에이커가 넘는 땅을 가졌고, 앞날이 창창한 데다, 그 젊음과 활력이라니!"], ["Not like some of us.”", "우리 같은 사람들하곤 다르지.\""]], [["“You have a great deal to complain of, haven’t you, Stepan Arkadyevitch?”", "\"당신도 불만스러운 게 어디 한둘이겠습니까, 스테판 아르카지치?\""]], [["“Ah, yes, I’m in a poor way, a bad way,”", "\"아, 그래, 난 형편이 말이 아니야, 아주 나빠.\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch with a heavy sigh.", "스테판 아르카지치가 무겁게 한숨을 쉬며 말했다."]], [["# Chapter 6", "# Chapter 6"]], [["When Oblonsky asked Levin what had brought him to town, Levin blushed, and was furious with himself for blushing, because he could not answer, “I have come to make your sister-in-law an offer,”", "오블론스키가 레빈에게 무슨 일로 시내에 왔느냐고 묻자, 레빈은 얼굴을 붉혔고, 그러면서 얼굴을 붉히는 자기 자신에게 화가 났다. 왜냐하면 \"당신 처제에게 청혼하러 왔지요\"라고 대답할 수가 없었기 때문이다."], ["though that was precisely what he had come for.", "사실 바로 그것을 하러 온 것이었는데도 말이다."]], [["The families of the Levins and the Shtcherbatskys were old, noble Moscow families, and had always been on intimate and friendly terms.", "레빈 집안과 시체르바츠키 집안은 둘 다 모스크바의 오랜 귀족 가문이었고, 늘 친밀하고 다정한 사이를 유지해 왔다."], ["This intimacy had grown still closer during Levin’s student days.", "이 친분은 레빈의 대학 시절에 한층 더 두터워졌다."], ["He had both prepared for the university with the young Prince Shtcherbatsky, the brother of Kitty and Dolly, and had entered at the same time with him.", "그는 키티와 돌리의 오빠인 젊은 시체르바츠키 공작과 함께 대학 입시를 준비했고, 그와 같은 시기에 입학했다."], ["In those days Levin used often to be in the Shtcherbatskys’ house, and he was in love with the Shtcherbatsky household.", "그 무렵 레빈은 시체르바츠키 집안에 자주 드나들었고, 그 집안에 푹 빠져 있었다."], ["Strange as it may appear, it was with the household, the family, that Konstantin Levin was in love, especially with the feminine half of the household.", "이상하게 들릴지 모르지만, 콘스탄틴 레빈이 사랑에 빠진 것은 바로 그 가정, 그 가족이었고, 특히 그 집안의 여성들이었다."], ["Levin did not remember his own mother, and his only sister was older than he was, so that it was in the Shtcherbatskys’ house that he saw for the first time that inner life of an old, noble, cultivated, and honorable family of which he had been deprived by the death of his father and mother.", "레빈은 자기 어머니를 기억하지 못했고, 하나뿐인 누이는 그보다 나이가 많았다. 그래서 그가 부모를 여의면서 잃어버린, 유서 깊고 고결하며 교양 있고 명예로운 가정의 그 내밀한 삶을 처음으로 본 곳이 바로 시체르바츠키 집안이었다."], ["All the members of that family, especially the feminine half, were pictured by him, as it were, wrapped about with a mysterious poetical veil, and he not only perceived no defects whatever in them, but under the poetical veil that shrouded them he assumed the existence of the loftiest sentiments and every possible perfection.", "그 집안의 모든 사람, 특히 여성들은 그의 눈에 마치 신비로운 시적 베일에 감싸인 것처럼 비쳤다. 그는 그들에게서 아무런 결점도 보지 못했을 뿐 아니라, 그들을 감싼 그 시적 베일 아래에 가장 고귀한 감정과 온갖 완전함이 깃들어 있으리라 여겼다."], ["Why it was the three young ladies had one day to speak French, and the next English; why it was that at certain hours they played by turns on the piano, the sounds of which were audible in their brother’s room above, where the students used to work; why they were visited by those professors of French literature, of music, of drawing, of dancing; why at certain hours all the three young ladies, with Mademoiselle Linon, drove in the coach to the Tversky boulevard, dressed in their satin cloaks, Dolly in a long one, Natalia in a half-long one, and Kitty in one so short that her shapely legs in tightly-drawn red stockings were visible to all beholders; why it was they had to walk about the Tversky boulevard escorted by a footman with a gold cockade in his hat—all this and much more that was done in their mysterious world he did not understand, but he was sure that everything that was done there was very good, and he was in love precisely with the mystery of the proceedings.", "세 아가씨가 어째서 어느 날은 프랑스어를 쓰고 다음 날은 영어를 써야 하는지, 어째서 정해진 시각이면 번갈아 가며 피아노를 쳐서, 학생들이 공부하던 위층 오빠의 방에까지 그 소리가 들려오는지, 어째서 프랑스 문학과 음악과 그림과 춤을 가르치는 그 선생들이 찾아오는지, 어째서 정해진 시각이면 세 아가씨가 마드무아젤 리농과 함께 새틴 망토를 두르고—돌리는 긴 것을, 나탈리아는 절반쯤 오는 것을, 키티는 너무 짧아서 팽팽하게 당겨 신은 붉은 스타킹을 신은 맵시 있는 다리가 누구의 눈에나 다 보이는 것을 두르고—마차를 타고 트베르스코이 가로수길로 나가는지, 어째서 모자에 금빛 장식을 단 하인의 호위를 받으며 트베르스코이 가로수길을 거닐어야 하는지—이 모든 것과, 그들의 신비로운 세계에서 행해지는 그 밖의 수많은 일을 그는 이해하지 못했다. 하지만 그는 거기서 행해지는 모든 것이 더없이 훌륭하다고 확신했고, 바로 그 모든 행위의 신비로움에 사랑을 느꼈다."]], [["In his student days he had all but been in love with the eldest, Dolly, but she was soon married to Oblonsky.", "대학 시절 그는 맏딸 돌리에게 거의 사랑에 빠질 뻔했지만, 그녀는 곧 오블론스키와 결혼해 버렸다."], ["Then he began being in love with the second.", "그러자 그는 둘째 딸을 사랑하기 시작했다."], ["He felt, as it were, that he had to be in love with one of the sisters, only he could not quite make out which.", "그는 마치 자매 가운데 누군가 한 사람을 사랑해야만 한다고 느끼는 듯했고, 다만 그게 누구인지를 좀처럼 분간하지 못했다."], ["But Natalia, too, had hardly made her appearance in the world when she married the diplomat Lvov.", "그러나 나탈리아 역시 사교계에 모습을 드러내자마자 외교관 르보프와 결혼해 버렸다."], ["Kitty was still a child when Levin left the university.", "레빈이 대학을 떠날 무렵 키티는 아직 어린아이였다."], ["Young Shtcherbatsky went into the navy, was drowned in the Baltic, and Levin’s relations with the Shtcherbatskys, in spite of his friendship with Oblonsky, became less intimate.", "젊은 시체르바츠키는 해군에 입대했다가 발트해에서 익사했고, 오블론스키와의 우정에도 불구하고 시체르바츠키 집안과 레빈의 관계는 점점 소원해졌다."], ["But when early in the winter of this year Levin came to Moscow, after a year in the country, and saw the Shtcherbatskys, he realized which of the three sisters he was indeed destined to love.", "그러나 올해 초겨울, 레빈이 시골에서 일 년을 보낸 뒤 모스크바로 와서 시체르바츠키 집안 사람들을 보았을 때, 그는 자기가 정말로 사랑할 운명인 사람이 세 자매 중 누구인지를 깨달았다."]], [["One would have thought that nothing could be simpler than for him, a man of good family, rather rich than poor, and thirty-two years old, to make the young Princess Shtcherbatskaya an offer of marriage; in all likelihood he would at once have been looked upon as a good match.", "좋은 가문 출신에, 가난하다기보다는 차라리 부유한 편이고, 서른두 살이나 된 그가 젊은 시체르바츠카야 공작 영애에게 청혼하는 것보다 더 간단한 일은 없으리라고 누구나 생각했을 것이다. 십중팔구 그는 단번에 괜찮은 신랑감으로 여겨졌을 것이다."], ["But Levin was in love, and so it seemed to him that Kitty was so perfect in every respect that she was a creature far above everything earthly; and that he was a creature so low and so earthly that it could not even be conceived that other people and she herself could regard him as worthy of her.", "그러나 레빈은 사랑에 빠져 있었고, 그래서 그에게 키티는 모든 면에서 너무도 완벽하여 이 세상 모든 것을 까마득히 초월한 존재로 보였다. 반면 자신은 너무도 비천하고 너무도 세속적인 존재여서, 다른 사람들이며 그녀 자신이 그를 그녀에게 어울리는 사람으로 여길 수 있으리라는 것은 상상조차 할 수 없을 정도였다."]], [["After spending two months in Moscow in a state of enchantment, seeing Kitty almost every day in society, into which he went so as to meet her, he abruptly decided that it could not be, and went back to the country.", "황홀한 상태로 두 달을 모스크바에서 보내며, 그녀를 만나려고 드나든 사교계에서 거의 날마다 키티를 보았던 그는, 불현듯 이건 안 될 일이라고 마음먹고 시골로 돌아가 버렸다."]], [["Levin’s conviction that it could not be was founded on the idea that in the eyes of her family he was a disadvantageous and worthless match for the charming Kitty, and that Kitty herself could not love him.", "이건 안 될 일이라는 레빈의 확신은 이런 생각에 바탕을 두고 있었다. 그녀 가족의 눈에 그는 사랑스러운 키티에게 불리하고 보잘것없는 신랑감이며, 키티 자신도 그를 사랑할 수 없으리라는 것이었다."], ["In her family’s eyes he had no ordinary, definite career and position in society, while his contemporaries by this time, when he was thirty-two, were already, one a colonel, and another a professor, another director of a bank and railways, or president of a board like Oblonsky.", "그녀 가족의 눈에 그는 사교계에서 번듯하고 뚜렷한 경력도 지위도 없는 사람이었던 반면, 그와 동년배인 사람들은 그가 서른두 살이 된 지금 이미 누구는 대령이 되고, 누구는 교수가 되고, 누구는 은행과 철도의 이사가 되거나, 오블론스키처럼 위원회 의장이 되어 있었다."], ["But he (he knew very well how he must appear to others) was a country gentleman, occupied in breeding cattle, shooting game, and building barns; in other words, a fellow of no ability, who had not turned out well, and who was doing just what, according to the ideas of the world, is done by people fit for nothing else.", "그러나 그는(다른 사람들 눈에 자기가 어떻게 보일지 그는 너무도 잘 알았다) 가축을 기르고, 사냥감을 쏘고, 헛간을 짓는 데 매달려 사는 시골 지주였다. 다시 말해 아무 재능도 없고, 변변히 성공하지도 못했으며, 세상의 통념으로 보자면 달리 아무짝에도 쓸모없는 인간들이 하는 바로 그 일을 하고 있는 사내였다."]], [["The mysterious, enchanting Kitty herself could not love such an ugly person as he conceived himself to be, and, above all, such an ordinary, in no way striking person.", "신비롭고 매혹적인 키티가, 스스로 생각하기에 자기처럼 못생긴 사람을, 무엇보다 그토록 평범하고 도무지 눈에 띄는 데라곤 없는 사람을 사랑할 리가 없었다."], ["Moreover, his attitude to Kitty in the past—the attitude of a grown-up person to a child, arising from his friendship with her brother—seemed to him yet another obstacle to love.", "게다가 과거 키티를 대했던 그의 태도—그녀 오빠와의 우정에서 비롯된, 어른이 아이를 대하는 듯한 태도—가 그에게는 사랑을 가로막는 또 하나의 장애물로 여겨졌다."], ["An ugly, good-natured man, as he considered himself, might, he supposed, be liked as a friend; but to be loved with such a love as that with which he loved Kitty, one would need to be a handsome and, still more, a distinguished man.", "못생겼지만 마음씨 좋은 남자는, 그가 스스로를 그렇게 여겼듯, 친구로서는 호감을 살 수 있을지 몰라도, 자기가 키티를 사랑하는 그런 사랑으로 사랑받으려면 잘생긴 남자, 그리고 더 나아가 출중한 남자여야 한다고 그는 생각했다."]], [["He had heard that women often did care for ugly and ordinary men, but he did not believe it, for he judged by himself, and he could not himself have loved any but beautiful, mysterious, and exceptional women.", "여자들이 흔히 못생기고 평범한 남자를 좋아하기도 한다는 말을 그는 들은 적이 있었지만, 그는 그 말을 믿지 않았다. 자기 자신을 기준으로 판단했기 때문이었고, 그 자신은 아름답고 신비롭고 비범한 여자가 아니면 누구도 사랑할 수 없었기 때문이다."]], [["But after spending two months alone in the country, he was convinced that this was not one of those passions of which he had had experience in his early youth; that this feeling gave him not an instant’s rest; that he could not live without deciding the question, would she or would she not be his wife, and that his despair had arisen only from his own imaginings, that he had no sort of proof that he would be rejected.", "그러나 시골에서 홀로 두 달을 보낸 뒤, 그는 확신하게 되었다. 이것은 젊은 시절에 겪었던 그런 한때의 열정 따위가 아니라는 것을, 이 감정이 그에게 한순간의 안식도 주지 않는다는 것을, 그녀가 자기 아내가 될 것인지 아닐 것인지 그 문제를 결정하지 않고서는 도저히 살아갈 수 없다는 것을, 그리고 자기의 절망이라는 것이 오로지 자기 망상에서 비롯되었을 뿐, 거절당하리라는 어떤 증거도 자기에게는 없다는 것을 말이다."], ["And he had now come to Moscow with a firm determination to make an offer, and get married if he were accepted.", "그리하여 그는 이제 청혼하겠다는, 받아들여지면 결혼하겠다는 굳은 결심을 품고 모스크바로 온 것이었다."], ["Or ...", "아니면……"], ["he could not conceive what would become of him if he were rejected.", "만약 거절당한다면 자기가 어떻게 될지는 그도 상상할 수 없었다."]], [["# Chapter 7", "# Chapter 7"]], [["On arriving in Moscow by a morning train, Levin had put up at the house of his elder half-brother, Koznishev.", "아침 기차로 모스크바에 도착한 레빈은 이복형 코즈니셰프의 집에 묵었다."], ["After changing his clothes he went down to his brother’s study, intending to talk to him at once about the object of his visit, and to ask his advice; but his brother was not alone.", "옷을 갈아입은 뒤 그는 곧장 자기가 찾아온 용건을 형과 의논하고 조언을 구할 생각으로 아래층 형의 서재로 내려갔다. 그러나 형은 혼자가 아니었다."], ["With him there was a well-known professor of philosophy, who had come from Harkov expressly to clear up a difference that had arisen between them on a very important philosophical question.", "형 곁에는 어떤 매우 중요한 철학적 문제를 둘러싸고 두 사람 사이에 생겨난 견해 차이를 매듭짓고자 일부러 하리코프에서 찾아온, 이름난 철학 교수가 와 있었다."], ["The professor was carrying on a hot crusade against materialists.", "교수는 유물론자들을 상대로 뜨거운 논전을 벌이는 중이었다."], ["Sergey Koznishev had been following this crusade with interest, and after reading the professor’s last article, he had written him a letter stating his objections.", "세르게이 코즈니셰프는 흥미를 가지고 이 논전을 지켜보아 왔으며, 교수의 마지막 논문을 읽고 나서 자기 반론을 적은 편지를 그에게 써 보냈던 터였다."], ["He accused the professor of making too great concessions to the materialists.", "그는 교수가 유물론자들에게 지나치게 양보하고 있다고 비판했다."], ["And the professor had promptly appeared to argue the matter out.", "그러자 교수는 그 문제를 끝까지 따져 보려고 곧바로 찾아온 것이다."], ["The point in discussion was the question then in vogue: Is there a line to be drawn between psychological and physiological phenomena in man?", "논의의 핵심은 당시 유행하던 바로 그 문제였다. 인간에게 심리적 현상과 생리적 현상 사이에 경계선을 그을 수 있는가?"], ["and if so, where?", "그렇다면 그것은 어디인가?"]], [["Sergey Ivanovitch met his brother with the smile of chilly friendliness he always had for everyone, and introducing him to the professor, went on with the conversation.", "세르게이 이바노비치는 늘 누구에게나 짓는 차갑고 다정한 미소로 동생을 맞이하고는, 그를 교수에게 소개한 뒤 다시 대화를 이어 갔다."]], [["A little man in spectacles, with a narrow forehead, tore himself from the discussion for an instant to greet Levin, and then went on talking without paying any further attention to him.", "좁은 이마에 안경을 쓴 자그마한 남자가 잠시 논의에서 벗어나 레빈에게 인사를 건네고는, 더 이상 그에게는 아무 관심도 두지 않고 이야기를 계속했다."], ["Levin sat down to wait till the professor should go, but he soon began to get interested in the subject under discussion.", "레빈은 교수가 돌아갈 때까지 기다리려고 자리에 앉았는데, 곧 논의되는 주제에 흥미가 일기 시작했다."]], [["Levin had come across the magazine articles about which they were disputing, and had read them, interested in them as a development of the first principles of science, familiar to him as a natural science student at the university.", "레빈은 그들이 논쟁하고 있는 잡지의 논문들을 우연히 접해 읽은 적이 있었는데, 대학에서 자연과학을 공부한 그에게 친숙한, 과학의 제일원리가 전개되는 글로서 흥미를 느꼈던 것이다."], ["But he had never connected these scientific deductions as to the origin of man as an animal, as to reflex action, biology, and sociology, with those questions as to the meaning of life and death to himself, which had of late been more and more often in his mind.", "그러나 그는 동물로서의 인간의 기원이며 반사작용이며 생물학이며 사회학에 관한 이런 과학적 추론들을, 요즘 들어 점점 더 자주 그의 머릿속을 맴돌던 삶과 죽음의 의미에 관한 문제들과 결부시켜 본 적은 한 번도 없었다."]], [["As he listened to his brother’s argument with the professor, he noticed that they connected these scientific questions with those spiritual problems, that at times they almost touched on the latter; but every time they were close upon what seemed to him the chief point, they promptly beat a hasty retreat, and plunged again into a sea of subtle distinctions, reservations, quotations, allusions, and appeals to authorities, and it was with difficulty that he understood what they were talking about.", "형과 교수의 논쟁에 귀를 기울이면서 그는 두 사람이 이런 과학적 문제들을 저 정신적인 문제들과 연결시키고 있으며, 이따금 후자에 거의 닿을 듯하다는 것을 알아차렸다. 그러나 그에게 가장 중요한 지점으로 여겨지는 데 가까이 다가설 때마다 그들은 곧바로 황급히 물러나 다시 미묘한 구별과 단서와 인용과 암시와 권위에의 호소가 넘실대는 바다로 뛰어들었고, 그래서 그는 그들이 무엇에 대해 이야기하는지 가까스로 이해할 따름이었다."]], [["“I cannot admit it,”", "\"나는 그것을 인정할 수 없네.\""], ["said Sergey Ivanovitch, with his habitual clearness, precision of expression, and elegance of phrase.", "세르게이 이바노비치가 늘 그렇듯이 명료하고 정확한 표현과 우아한 어법으로 말했다."], ["“I cannot in any case agree with Keiss that my whole conception of the external world has been derived from perceptions.", "\"나는 외부 세계에 대한 나의 모든 관념이 지각에서 비롯되었다는 카이스의 주장에는 어떤 경우에도 동의할 수 없네."], ["The most fundamental idea, the idea of existence, has not been received by me through sensation; indeed, there is no special sense-organ for the transmission of such an idea.”", "가장 근본적인 관념, 곧 존재라는 관념은 감각을 통해 내게 받아들여진 것이 아니야. 실제로 그런 관념을 전달할 특별한 감각기관 따위는 없으니까.\""]], [["“Yes, but they—Wurt, and Knaust, and Pripasov—would answer that your consciousness of existence is derived from the conjunction of all your sensations, that that consciousness of existence is the result of your sensations.", "\"그렇지요, 하지만 그들은—부르트, 크나우스트, 프리파소프 같은 이들은—이렇게 답할 겁니다. 당신의 존재에 대한 의식은 당신의 모든 감각의 결합에서 비롯된 것이며, 그 존재에 대한 의식이야말로 당신의 감각의 결과라고 말입니다."], ["Wurt, indeed, says plainly that, assuming there are no sensations, it follows that there is no idea of existence.”", "실제로 부르트는, 감각이 없다고 가정하면 결국 존재의 관념도 없다는 결론이 나온다고 분명히 말합니다.\""]], [["“I maintain the contrary,”", "\"나는 그 반대를 주장하네.\""], ["began Sergey Ivanovitch.", "세르게이 이바노비치가 입을 열었다."]], [["But here it seemed to Levin that just as they were close upon the real point of the matter, they were again retreating, and he made up his mind to put a question to the professor.", "그러나 여기서 레빈에게는 그들이 문제의 본질에 막 다가서는 듯하다가 다시 물러나 버리는 것처럼 보였고, 그는 교수에게 한 가지 질문을 던지기로 마음먹었다."]], [["“According to that, if my senses are annihilated, if my body is dead, I can have no existence of any sort?”", "\"그 말에 따르자면, 만약 나의 감각이 사라지고 나의 육체가 죽는다면, 나는 어떤 종류의 존재도 가질 수 없다는 겁니까?\""], ["he queried.", "그가 물었다."]], [["The professor, in annoyance, and, as it were, mental suffering at the interruption, looked round at the strange inquirer, more like a bargeman than a philosopher, and turned his eyes upon Sergey Ivanovitch, as though to ask: What’s one to say to him?", "교수는 짜증이 나서, 마치 그 방해 때문에 정신적 고통이라도 느끼는 듯한 표정으로, 철학자라기보다는 뱃사공에 가까워 보이는 그 낯선 질문자를 돌아보고는, 마치 \"저 사람에게 무슨 말을 해야 하나?\" 하고 묻기라도 하듯이 세르게이 이바노비치에게로 눈길을 돌렸다."], ["But Sergey Ivanovitch, who had been talking with far less heat and one-sidedness than the professor, and who had sufficient breadth of mind to answer the professor, and at the same time to comprehend the simple and natural point of view from which the question was put, smiled and said:", "그러나 교수보다 훨씬 덜 열을 올리고 덜 한쪽으로 치우쳐 이야기해 온 데다, 교수에게 답할 만큼 넉넉한 식견을 갖추고 있으면서도 동시에 그 질문이 나온 단순하고 자연스러운 관점까지 헤아릴 줄 알았던 세르게이 이바노비치는 빙긋 웃으며 말했다."]], [["“That question we have no right to answer as yet.”", "\"그 질문에는 우리가 아직 답할 권리가 없다네.\""]], [["“We have not the requisite data,”", "\"필요한 자료가 우리에게 없으니까요.\""], ["chimed in the professor, and he went back to his argument.", "교수가 맞장구를 치고는 다시 자기 논지로 돌아갔다. \"아니지요.\""], ["“No,”", "그가 말했다."], ["he said; “I would point out the fact that if, as Pripasov directly asserts, perception is based on sensation, then we are bound to distinguish sharply between these two conceptions.”", "\"나는 이 점을 지적하고 싶습니다. 만약 프리파소프가 직접 단언하듯이 지각이 감각에 기초하는 것이라면, 우리는 이 두 개념을 분명하게 구별하지 않을 수 없습니다.\""]], [["Levin listened no more, and simply waited for the professor to go.", "레빈은 더 이상 귀 기울이지 않았고, 그저 교수가 돌아가기만을 기다렸다."]], [["# Chapter 8", "# Chapter 8"]], [["When the professor had gone, Sergey Ivanovitch turned to his brother.", "교수가 가고 나자 세르게이 이바노비치는 동생을 돌아보았다."]], [["“Delighted that you’ve come.", "\"네가 와서 정말 기쁘구나."], ["For some time, is it?", "한동안 머물 거냐?"], ["How’s your farming getting on?”", "농사일은 어떻게 돼 가지?\""]], [["Levin knew that his elder brother took little interest in farming, and only put the question in deference to him, and so he only told him about the sale of his wheat and money matters.", "레빈은 형이 농사일에는 별 관심이 없으면서 단지 자기를 배려해 그렇게 물었을 뿐임을 알았고, 그래서 밀을 판 일과 돈 문제에 대해서만 이야기했다."]], [["Levin had meant to tell his brother of his determination to get married, and to ask his advice; he had indeed firmly resolved to do so.", "레빈은 결혼하기로 결심했다는 것을 형에게 털어놓고 조언을 구하려 했었다. 사실 그렇게 하기로 단단히 마음먹고 있었던 것이다."], ["But after seeing his brother, listening to his conversation with the professor, hearing afterwards the unconsciously patronizing tone in which his brother questioned him about agricultural matters (their mother’s property had not been divided, and Levin took charge of both their shares), Levin felt that he could not for some reason begin to talk to him of his intention of marrying.", "그러나 형을 보고, 형이 교수와 나누는 대화를 듣고, 그러고 나서 형이 농사 문제에 대해 자기에게 물을 때의 무의식적으로 깔보는 듯한 어조를 듣고 나니(어머니의 재산은 아직 나뉘지 않은 채였고, 레빈이 두 사람 몫을 모두 관리하고 있었다), 레빈은 왠지 결혼할 생각을 형에게 꺼낼 수 없을 것 같다는 기분이 들었다."], ["He felt that his brother would not look at it as he would have wished him to.", "형이 그 일을 자기가 바라는 식으로 봐 주지 않으리라는 것이 느껴졌다."]], [["“Well, how is your district council doing?”", "\"그래, 너희 군 자치회는 어떻게 돌아가고 있냐?\""], ["asked Sergey Ivanovitch, who was greatly interested in these local boards and attached great importance to them.", "세르게이 이바노비치가 물었다. 그는 이런 지방 자치 기구에 큰 관심을 두고 있었고 그것에 큰 의미를 부여하고 있었다."]], [["“I really don’t know.”", "\"정말이지 잘 모르겠어요.\""]], [["“What!", "\"뭐라고!"], ["Why, surely you’re a member of the board?”", "아니, 네가 자치회 의원이지 않냐?\""]], [["“No, I’m not a member now; I’ve resigned,”", "\"아뇨, 이제 의원이 아니에요. 사임했거든요.\""], ["answered Levin, “and I no longer attend the meetings.”", "레빈이 대답했다. \"이젠 회의에도 나가지 않아요.\""]], [["“What a pity!”", "\"이런, 안타깝구나!\""], ["commented Sergey Ivanovitch, frowning.", "세르게이 이바노비치가 미간을 찌푸리며 한마디 했다."]], [["Levin in self-defense began to describe what took place in the meetings in his district.", "레빈은 자기변호를 하려고 자기 군의 회의에서 어떤 일이 벌어지는지 설명하기 시작했다."]], [["“That’s how it always is!”", "\"늘 그런 식이지!\" 세르게이 이바노비치가 그의 말을 가로막았다."], ["Sergey Ivanovitch interrupted him.", "\"우리 러시아 사람들은 언제나 그래."], ["“We Russians are always like that.", "어쩌면 그게 정말 우리의 장점일지도 모르지, 자기 결점을 볼 줄 아는 능력 말이야."], ["Perhaps it’s our strong point, really, the faculty of seeing our own shortcomings; but we overdo it, we comfort ourselves with irony which we always have on the tip of our tongues.", "하지만 우린 그걸 도가 지나치게 하는 거야. 늘 혀끝에 매달려 있는 빈정거림으로 스스로를 위로하지."], ["All I say is, give such rights as our local self-government to any other European people—why, the Germans or the English would have worked their way to freedom from them, while we simply turn them into ridicule.”", "내가 하고 싶은 말은 이거야. 우리 지방 자치 같은 권리를 다른 유럽 민족 누구에게라도 줘 봐라—독일인이든 영국인이든 그걸 발판 삼아 자유로 나아갔을 텐데, 우리는 그저 그걸 조롱거리로 만들어 버린단 말이지.\""]], [["“But how can it be helped?”", "\"하지만 어쩌겠어요?\""], ["said Levin penitently.", "레빈이 뉘우치듯 말했다."], ["“It was my last effort.", "\"그게 제 마지막 노력이었어요."], ["And I did try with all my soul.", "정말 온 마음을 다해 해 봤다고요."], ["I can’t.", "안 되더군요."], ["I’m no good at it.”", "전 그런 일에 소질이 없어요.\""]], [["“It’s not that you’re no good at it,”", "\"소질이 없는 게 아니야.\""], ["said Sergey Ivanovitch; “it is that you don’t look at it as you should.”", "세르게이 이바노비치가 말했다. \"네가 그걸 마땅히 봐야 할 대로 보지 않는 거지.\""]], [["“Perhaps not,”", "\"그럴지도 모르죠.\""], ["Levin answered dejectedly.", "레빈이 풀 죽어 대답했다."]], [["“Oh!", "\"아!"], ["do you know brother Nikolay’s turned up again?”", "그런데 너 형 니콜라이가 또 나타났다는 거 아냐?\""]], [["This brother Nikolay was the elder brother of Konstantin Levin, and half-brother of Sergey Ivanovitch; a man utterly ruined, who had dissipated the greater part of his fortune, was living in the strangest and lowest company, and had quarreled with his brothers.", "이 형 니콜라이는 콘스탄틴 레빈의 손위 형이자 세르게이 이바노비치의 이복형으로, 완전히 신세를 망친 사람이었다. 재산의 대부분을 탕진해 버리고, 더없이 괴상하고 천한 무리와 어울려 지냈으며, 형제들과도 사이가 틀어진 상태였다."]], [["“What did you say?”", "\"뭐라고요?\""], ["Levin cried with horror.", "레빈이 경악하며 외쳤다."], ["“How do you know?”", "\"어떻게 아셨어요?\""]], [["“Prokofy saw him in the street.”", "\"프로코피가 거리에서 봤다더라.\""]], [["“Here in Moscow? Where is he?", "\"여기 모스크바에서요?"], ["Do you know?”", "어디 있는데요?"], ["Levin got up from his chair, as though on the point of starting off at once.", "아세요?\" 레빈은 당장이라도 떠날 듯이 의자에서 벌떡 일어났다."]], [["“I am sorry I told you,”", "\"네게 말한 걸 후회한다.\""], ["said Sergey Ivanovitch, shaking his head at his younger brother’s excitement.", "세르게이 이바노비치가 동생의 흥분에 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 말했다."], ["“I sent to find out where he is living, and sent him his IOU to Trubin, which I paid.", "\"그가 어디 사는지 알아보라고 사람을 보냈고, 트루빈에게 진 차용증서를 내가 갚아 주고 그에게 보냈지."], ["This is the answer he sent me.”", "이게 그가 내게 보낸 답장이다.\""]], [["And Sergey Ivanovitch took a note from under a paper-weight and handed it to his brother.", "그러고는 세르게이 이바노비치가 문진 밑에서 쪽지 한 장을 꺼내 동생에게 건넸다."]], [["Levin read in the queer, familiar handwriting: “I humbly beg you to leave me in peace.", "레빈은 그 기묘하고도 낯익은 필체를 읽었다. \"삼가 부탁하건대 나를 좀 가만 내버려 두기를 바라네."], ["That’s the only favor I ask of my gracious brothers.—Nikolay Levin.”", "그것이 너그러운 형제들에게 내가 청하는 유일한 부탁일세.—니콜라이 레빈.\""]], [["Levin read it, and without raising his head stood with the note in his hands opposite Sergey Ivanovitch.", "레빈은 그것을 읽고는 고개도 들지 않은 채, 쪽지를 손에 쥐고 세르게이 이바노비치를 마주한 채 서 있었다."]], [["There was a struggle in his heart between the desire to forget his unhappy brother for the time, and the consciousness that it would be base to do so.", "그의 마음속에서는 불행한 형을 잠시나마 잊고 싶다는 바람과, 그렇게 하는 것은 비열한 짓이라는 자각이 서로 다투고 있었다."]], [["“He obviously wants to offend me,”", "\"저 녀석은 분명 나를 모욕하고 싶어 하는 거야.\" 세르게이 이바노비치가 말을 이었다."], ["pursued Sergey Ivanovitch; “but he cannot offend me, and I should have wished with all my heart to assist him, but I know it’s impossible to do that.”", "\"하지만 나를 모욕할 수는 없지. 나는 진심으로 그를 돕고 싶었지만, 그게 불가능하다는 걸 알아.\""]], [["“Yes, yes,”", "\"그래요, 그래요.\""], ["repeated Levin.", "레빈이 되뇌었다."], ["“I understand and appreciate your attitude to him; but I shall go and see him.”", "\"형이 그를 대하는 태도를 이해하고 또 높이 사요. 하지만 저는 가서 그를 만나 보겠어요.\""]], [["“If you want to, do; but I shouldn’t advise it,”", "\"가고 싶으면 가렴. 하지만 나라면 권하지 않겠다.\""], ["said Sergey Ivanovitch.", "세르게이 이바노비치가 말했다. \"나로서는 네가 그렇게 하는 게 두렵진 않아."], ["“As regards myself, I have no fear of your doing so; he will not make you quarrel with me; but for your own sake, I should say you would do better not to go.", "그가 너를 나와 틀어지게 만들지는 못할 테니까. 다만 너 자신을 위해서 하는 말인데, 가지 않는 편이 나을 거야."], ["You can’t do him any good; still, do as you please.”", "너는 그에게 아무런 도움도 줄 수 없어. 하지만 네 마음대로 하렴.\""]], [["“Very likely I can’t do any good, but I feel—especially at such a moment—but that’s another thing—I feel I could not be at peace.”", "\"제가 아무 도움도 못 줄 가능성이 크겠죠. 하지만 저는 느껴요—특히 이런 순간에는요—아니 그건 또 다른 문제고—마음이 편할 수가 없을 것 같아요.\""]], [["“Well, that I don’t understand,”", "\"글쎄, 그건 나로선 이해가 안 되는구나.\""], ["said Sergey Ivanovitch.", "세르게이 이바노비치가 말했다."], ["“One thing I do understand,”", "\"내가 이해하는 한 가지는,\""], ["he added; “it’s a lesson in humility.", "그가 덧붙였다. \"그게 겸손에 대한 교훈이라는 거야."], ["I have come to look very differently and more charitably on what is called infamous since brother Nikolay has become what he is ...", "형 니콜라이가 지금 같은 사람이 된 뒤로, 나는 이른바 파렴치하다는 것에 대해 아주 다르게, 훨씬 너그럽게 보게 됐어……"], ["you know what he did....”", "그 녀석이 무슨 짓을 했는지 너도 알잖니…….\""]], [["“Oh, it’s awful, awful!”", "\"아, 끔찍해요, 끔찍해!\""], ["repeated Levin.", "레빈이 거듭 말했다."]], [["After obtaining his brother’s address from Sergey Ivanovitch’s footman, Levin was on the point of setting off at once to see him, but on second thought he decided to put off his visit till the evening.", "세르게이 이바노비치의 하인에게서 형의 주소를 받아 낸 레빈은 당장 그를 만나러 떠나려 했지만, 다시 생각한 끝에 방문을 저녁으로 미루기로 했다."], ["The first thing to do to set his heart at rest was to accomplish what he had come to Moscow for.", "마음을 가라앉히기 위해 가장 먼저 해야 할 일은 자기가 모스크바에 온 목적을 이루는 것이었다."], ["From his brother’s Levin went to Oblonsky’s office, and on getting news of the Shtcherbatskys from him, he drove to the place where he had been told he might find Kitty.", "형의 집을 나선 레빈은 오블론스키의 관청으로 갔고, 거기서 셰르바츠키 집안 소식을 들은 뒤, 키티를 만날 수 있을 거라고 들은 장소로 마차를 몰았다."]], [["# Chapter 9", "# Chapter 9"]], [["At four o’clock, conscious of his throbbing heart, Levin stepped out of a hired sledge at the Zoological Gardens, and turned along the path to the frozen mounds and the skating ground, knowing that he would certainly find her there, as he had seen the Shtcherbatskys’ carriage at the entrance.", "네 시에, 가슴이 두근거리는 것을 느끼면서, 레빈은 동물원 앞에서 삯썰매에서 내려 얼어붙은 언덕과 스케이트장으로 이어진 길로 접어들었다. 입구에서 셰르바츠키 집안의 마차를 본 터라, 그곳에 가면 틀림없이 그녀를 만날 수 있으리라는 것을 알고 있었다."]], [["It was a bright, frosty day.", "화창하고 서리가 내린 날이었다."], ["Rows of carriages, sledges, drivers, and policemen were standing in the approach.", "마차와 썰매, 마부와 경관 들이 줄지어 진입로에 서 있었다."], ["Crowds of well-dressed people, with hats bright in the sun, swarmed about the entrance and along the well-swept little paths between the little houses adorned with carving in the Russian style.", "햇빛에 모자를 반짝이며 잘 차려입은 사람들이 무리를 지어 입구 주변과, 러시아풍 조각으로 장식된 작은 집들 사이로 잘 쓸어 놓은 오솔길을 따라 북적였다."], ["The old curly birches of the gardens, all their twigs laden with snow, looked as though freshly decked in sacred vestments.", "잔가지마다 눈을 잔뜩 인 정원의 오래된 곱슬자작나무들은 막 거룩한 제의(祭衣)로 새로 단장한 것처럼 보였다."]], [["He walked along the path towards the skating-ground, and kept saying to himself—“You mustn’t be excited, you must be calm.", "그는 스케이트장으로 이어진 길을 따라 걸으며 자기 자신에게 거듭 말했다. \"흥분하면 안 돼, 침착해야 해."], ["What’s the matter with you?", "도대체 뭐가 문제야?"], ["What do you want?", "뭘 원하는 거야?"], ["Be quiet, stupid,”", "가만히 있어, 멍청아.\""], ["he conjured his heart.", "그는 자기 심장에게 명령했다."], ["And the more he tried to compose himself, the more breathless he found himself.", "그러나 마음을 가라앉히려 애쓰면 애쓸수록 숨이 더 가빠지는 것이었다."], ["An acquaintance met him and called him by his name, but Levin did not even recognize him.", "아는 사람 하나가 그를 만나 이름을 불렀지만, 레빈은 그가 누구인지조차 알아보지 못했다."], ["He went towards the mounds, whence came the clank of the chains of sledges as they slipped down or were dragged up, the rumble of the sliding sledges, and the sounds of merry voices.", "그는 언덕 쪽으로 걸어갔다. 그쪽에서는 썰매가 미끄러져 내려가거나 끌어올려질 때 나는 쇠사슬 소리며, 미끄러지는 썰매의 덜컹거리는 소리며, 즐거운 목소리들이 들려왔다."], ["He walked on a few steps, and the skating-ground lay open before his eyes, and at once, amidst all the skaters, he knew her.", "그가 몇 걸음 더 걷자 스케이트장이 눈앞에 펼쳐졌고, 그 순간 그는 그 모든 스케이트를 타는 사람들 가운데서 단번에 그녀를 알아보았다."]], [["He knew she was there by the rapture and the terror that seized on his heart.", "그녀가 거기 있다는 것을 그는 자기 가슴을 사로잡은 환희와 공포로 알았다."], ["She was standing talking to a lady at the opposite end of the ground.", "그녀는 스케이트장 맞은편 끝에서 어떤 부인과 이야기를 나누며 서 있었다."], ["There was apparently nothing striking either in her dress or her attitude.", "그녀의 옷차림에도 자세에도 눈에 띄는 데는 딱히 없는 듯했다."], ["But for Levin she was as easy to find in that crowd as a rose among nettles.", "그러나 레빈에게는 쐐기풀 속의 장미를 찾아내듯 그 무리 속에서 그녀를 찾아내는 것이 쉬운 일이었다."], ["Everything was made bright by her.", "모든 것이 그녀로 인해 환해졌다."], ["She was the smile that shed light on all round her.", "그녀는 주위 모든 것에 빛을 뿌리는 미소였다."], ["“Is it possible I can go over there on the ice, go up to her?”", "'정말 내가 저 얼음판을 가로질러 저쪽으로 가서, 그녀에게 다가갈 수 있을까?'"], ["he thought.", "그는 생각했다."], ["The place where she stood seemed to him a holy shrine, unapproachable, and there was one moment when he was almost retreating, so overwhelmed was he with terror.", "그녀가 서 있는 자리가 그에게는 다가갈 수 없는 거룩한 성소처럼 여겨졌고, 공포에 그토록 압도된 나머지 하마터면 발길을 돌릴 뻔한 순간도 있었다."], ["He had to make an effort to master himself, and to remind himself that people of all sorts were moving about her, and that he too might come there to skate.", "그는 자신을 다잡으려고 애를 써야 했고, 온갖 부류의 사람들이 그녀 주변을 오가고 있으며 자기도 스케이트를 타러 거기 갈 수 있는 것이라고 스스로에게 일깨워야 했다."], ["He walked down, for a long while avoiding looking at her as at the sun, but seeing her, as one does the sun, without looking.", "그는 마치 태양을 보지 않으려 하듯 오랫동안 그녀를 보지 않으려 애쓰며 내려갔지만, 태양을 그러하듯 보지 않고도 그녀를 보고 있었다."]], [["On that day of the week and at that time of day people of one set, all acquainted with one another, used to meet on the ice.", "한 주 가운데 그 요일, 하루 가운데 그 시각에는 서로 다 아는 사이인 한 무리의 사람들이 얼음판에서 만나곤 했다."], ["There were crack skaters there, showing off their skill, and learners clinging to chairs with timid, awkward movements, boys, and elderly people skating with hygienic motives.", "거기에는 솜씨를 뽐내는 일류 스케이트 선수들도 있었고, 겁먹은 어설픈 동작으로 의자에 매달리는 초보자들도 있었으며, 소년들과, 건강을 위한 목적으로 스케이트를 타는 노인들도 있었다."], ["They seemed to Levin an elect band of blissful beings because they were here, near her.", "레빈에게는 그들이, 그녀 곁 이곳에 있다는 이유만으로, 선택받은 행복한 존재들의 무리처럼 보였다."], ["All the skaters, it seemed, with perfect self-possession, skated towards her, skated by her, even spoke to her, and were happy, quite apart from her, enjoying the capital ice and the fine weather.", "모든 스케이트 타는 사람들은 더없이 침착하게 그녀를 향해 미끄러져 가고, 그녀 곁을 스쳐 가고, 심지어 그녀에게 말을 걸기까지 하면서, 그녀와는 전혀 상관없이 좋은 얼음과 화창한 날씨를 즐기며 행복해하는 듯 보였다."]], [["Nikolay Shtcherbatsky, Kitty’s cousin, in a short jacket and tight trousers, was sitting on a garden seat with his skates on.", "키티의 사촌인 니콜라이 셰르바츠키는 짧은 재킷에 꽉 끼는 바지 차림으로 스케이트를 신은 채 정원 벤치에 앉아 있었다."], ["Seeing Levin, he shouted to him:", "레빈을 보고 그가 소리쳤다."]], [["“Ah, the first skater in Russia!", "\"아, 러시아 제일의 스케이트 선수로군!"], ["Been here long?", "온 지 오래됐나?"], ["First-rate ice—do put your skates on.”", "얼음이 끝내주는걸—얼른 스케이트나 신게.\""]], [["“I haven’t got my skates,”", "\"스케이트를 안 가져왔어.\""], ["Levin answered, marveling at this boldness and ease in her presence, and not for one second losing sight of her, though he did not look at her.", "레빈은 그녀가 있는 자리에서 보이는 이런 대담함과 여유로움에 놀라며, 그녀를 보고 있지는 않으면서도 한순간도 그녀에게서 눈을 떼지 못한 채 대답했다."], ["He felt as though the sun were coming near him.", "그는 마치 태양이 자기에게 가까이 다가오는 듯한 느낌이 들었다."], ["She was in a corner, and turning out her slender feet in their high boots with obvious timidity, she skated towards him.", "그녀는 한쪽 구석에 있었고, 굽 높은 부츠를 신은 날씬한 발을 눈에 띄게 머뭇거리며 바깥쪽으로 돌리면서 그를 향해 미끄러져 왔다."], ["A boy in Russian dress, desperately waving his arms and bowed down to the ground, overtook her.", "러시아풍 옷차림을 한 소년 하나가 두 팔을 미친 듯이 휘저으며 땅에 닿을 듯 몸을 굽힌 채 그녀를 앞질러 갔다."], ["She skated a little uncertainly; taking her hands out of the little muff that hung on a cord, she held them ready for emergency, and looking towards Levin, whom she had recognized, she smiled at him, and at her own fears.", "그녀는 다소 불안하게 미끄러졌다. 끈에 매달린 작은 토시에서 두 손을 빼내 만일에 대비하듯 준비한 채, 자기가 알아본 레빈 쪽을 바라보며 그에게, 그리고 자기 자신의 두려움에 미소를 지었다."], ["When she had got round the turn, she gave herself a push off with one foot, and skated straight up to Shtcherbatsky.", "모퉁이를 돌고 나자 그녀는 한쪽 발로 박차를 가하며 곧장 셰르바츠키에게로 미끄러져 갔다."], ["Clutching at his arm, she nodded smiling to Levin.", "그의 팔을 붙잡으며 그녀는 미소 띤 얼굴로 레빈에게 고갯짓을 했다."], ["She was more splendid than he had imagined her.", "그녀는 그가 상상했던 것보다 더 눈부셨다."]], [["When he thought of her, he could call up a vivid picture of her to himself, especially the charm of that little fair head, so freely set on the shapely girlish shoulders, and so full of childish brightness and good humor.", "그녀를 떠올릴 때면, 그는 그녀의 생생한 모습을, 특히 맵시 있는 소녀다운 어깨 위에 그토록 자유로이 얹혀 있고, 어린아이 같은 밝음과 명랑함으로 가득한 그 작고 고운 머리의 매력을 마음속에 불러낼 수 있었다."], ["The childishness of her expression, together with the delicate beauty of her figure, made up her special charm, and that he fully realized.", "그녀 표정의 어린아이다움이 그 가냘프고 아름다운 자태와 어우러져 그녀만의 독특한 매력을 이루고 있었고, 그는 그것을 충분히 알고 있었다."], ["But what always struck him in her as something unlooked for, was the expression of her eyes, soft, serene, and truthful, and above all, her smile, which always transported Levin to an enchanted world, where he felt himself softened and tender, as he remembered himself in some days of his early childhood.", "그러나 늘 무언가 예기치 못한 것으로 그를 사로잡은 것은, 부드럽고 고요하며 진실한 그녀의 눈빛이었고, 무엇보다도 그녀의 미소였다. 그 미소는 언제나 레빈을 마법의 세계로 데려갔고, 그곳에서 그는 아주 어린 시절의 어느 날들에 자기 모습이 그러했던 것처럼 마음이 누그러지고 다정해지는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["“Have you been here long?”", "\"온 지 오래됐어요?\""], ["she said, giving him her hand.", "그녀가 손을 내밀며 말했다."], ["“Thank you,”", "\"고마워요.\""], ["she added, as he picked up the handkerchief that had fallen out of her muff.", "그녀의 토시에서 떨어진 손수건을 그가 주워 들자 그녀가 덧붙였다."]], [["“I?", "\"나요?"], ["I’ve not long ...", "온 지 얼마 안 됐어요……"], ["yesterday ...", "어제……"], ["I mean today ...", "아니, 오늘이요……"], ["I arrived,”", "도착했어요.\""], ["answered Levin, in his emotion not at once understanding her question.", "레빈은 격앙된 나머지 그녀의 물음을 단번에 알아듣지 못한 채 대답했다."], ["“I was meaning to come and see you,”", "\"댁에 찾아뵈러 가려던 참이었어요.\""], ["he said; and then, recollecting with what intention he was trying to see her, he was promptly overcome with confusion and blushed.", "그가 말했다. 그러고는 무슨 마음으로 그녀를 만나려 했는지를 떠올리고는 곧바로 당혹감에 사로잡혀 얼굴을 붉혔다."]], [["“I didn’t know you could skate, and skate so well.”", "\"스케이트를 탈 줄 아는지, 그것도 이렇게 잘 타는지 몰랐어요.\""]], [["She looked at him earnestly, as though wishing to make out the cause of his confusion.", "그녀는 그의 당혹감의 까닭을 알아내고 싶기라도 한 듯 진지하게 그를 바라보았다."]], [["“Your praise is worth having.", "\"당신이 칭찬해 주니 값진 거네요."], ["The tradition is kept up here that you are the best of skaters,”", "여기서는 당신이 최고의 스케이트 선수라는 게 전설처럼 전해 오거든요.\""], ["she said, with her little black-gloved hand brushing a grain of hoarfrost off her muff.", "그녀는 검은 장갑을 낀 작은 손으로 토시에 내려앉은 서리 알갱이를 털어 내며 말했다."]], [["“Yes, I used once to skate with passion; I wanted to reach perfection.”", "\"네, 한때는 열정적으로 스케이트를 탔어요. 완벽의 경지에 이르고 싶었죠.\""]], [["“You do everything with passion, I think,”", "\"당신은 뭐든 열정적으로 하는 분 같아요.\""], ["she said smiling.", "그녀가 미소 지으며 말했다."], ["“I should so like to see how you skate.", "\"당신이 스케이트 타는 걸 정말 보고 싶어요."], ["Put on skates, and let us skate together.”", "스케이트를 신어요, 그리고 같이 타요.\""]], [["“Skate together!", "'같이 타다니!"], ["Can that be possible?”", "그게 가능할까?'"], ["thought Levin, gazing at her.", "레빈은 그녀를 바라보며 생각했다."]], [["“I’ll put them on directly,”", "\"당장 신을게요.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["And he went off to get skates.", "그러고는 스케이트를 가지러 갔다."]], [["“It’s a long while since we’ve seen you here, sir,”", "\"한참 만에 뵙는군요, 나리.\""], ["said the attendant, supporting his foot, and screwing on the heel of the skate.", "관리인이 그의 발을 받쳐 들고 스케이트 뒤축을 죄며 말했다."], ["“Except you, there’s none of the gentlemen first-rate skaters.", "\"나리 말고는 일류 스케이트 선수인 신사분이 한 분도 안 계시지요."], ["Will that be all right?”", "이만하면 됐습니까?\""], ["said he, tightening the strap.", "그가 끈을 단단히 조이며 물었다."]], [["“Oh, yes, yes; make haste, please,”", "\"오, 그래, 됐네. 제발 좀 서둘러 주게.\""], ["answered Levin, with difficulty restraining the smile of rapture which would overspread his face.", "레빈은 얼굴에 번지려 하는 환희의 미소를 가까스로 억누르며 대답했다."], ["“Yes,”", "'그래.'"], ["he thought, “this now is life, this is happiness!", "그는 생각했다. '이게 바로 인생이야, 이게 바로 행복이야!"], ["Together, she said; let us skate together!", "같이, 라고 그녀가 말했어. 같이 타요, 라고!"], ["Speak to her now?", "지금 그녀에게 말할까?"], ["But that’s just why I’m afraid to speak—because I’m happy now, happy in hope, anyway....", "하지만 바로 그래서 말하기가 두려운 거야—지금 행복하니까, 어쨌든 희망 속에서 행복하니까…… 그러고 나면?……"], ["And then?...", "하지만 해야 해!"], ["But I must!", "해야 해!"], ["I must! I must!", "해야 한단 말이야!"], ["Away with weakness!”", "나약함은 떨쳐 버려!'"]], [["Levin rose to his feet, took off his overcoat, and scurrying over the rough ice round the hut, came out on the smooth ice and skated without effort, as it were, by simple exercise of will, increasing and slackening speed and turning his course.", "레빈은 일어나 외투를 벗고는, 오두막 주변의 울퉁불퉁한 얼음 위를 종종걸음으로 지나 매끄러운 얼음판으로 나와, 그야말로 단순히 의지를 발휘하는 것만으로 힘들이지 않고 미끄러지며 속도를 올렸다 늦추었다 하고 방향을 바꾸었다."], ["He approached with timidity, but again her smile reassured him.", "그는 머뭇거리며 다가갔지만, 다시 한번 그녀의 미소가 그를 안심시켰다."]], [["She gave him her hand, and they set off side by side, going faster and faster, and the more rapidly they moved the more tightly she grasped his hand.", "그녀가 그에게 손을 내밀었고, 두 사람은 나란히 미끄러져 가며 점점 더 빨라졌다. 빨라지면 빨라질수록 그녀는 그의 손을 더 꽉 움켜쥐었다."]], [["“With you I should soon learn; I somehow feel confidence in you,”", "\"당신과 함께라면 금방 배울 것 같아요. 어쩐지 당신을 믿게 되네요.\""], ["she said to him.", "그녀가 그에게 말했다."]], [["“And I have confidence in myself when you are leaning on me,”", "\"그리고 당신이 내게 기대고 있으면 나도 나 자신이 믿어져요.\""], ["he said, but was at once panic-stricken at what he had said, and blushed.", "그는 그렇게 말했지만, 자기가 한 말에 곧바로 겁에 질려 얼굴을 붉혔다."], ["And indeed, no sooner had he uttered these words, when all at once, like the sun going behind a cloud, her face lost all its friendliness, and Levin detected the familiar change in her expression that denoted the working of thought; a crease showed on her smooth brow.", "그리고 정말로, 그가 이 말을 입 밖에 내자마자, 별안간 해가 구름 뒤로 숨듯 그녀의 얼굴에서 다정함이 죄다 사라졌고, 레빈은 그녀의 표정에서 생각이 작동하고 있음을 나타내는 그 낯익은 변화를 알아챘다. 매끈한 이마에 주름이 잡혔다."]], [["“Is there anything troubling you?—though I’ve no right to ask such a question,”", "\"무슨 걱정거리라도 있어요?—이런 걸 물을 자격이 내겐 없지만요.\""], ["he added hurriedly.", "그가 황급히 덧붙였다."]], [["“Oh, why so?...", "\"오, 어째서요?……"], ["No, I have nothing to trouble me,”", "아니에요, 걱정거리 따위 없어요.\""], ["she responded coldly; and she added immediately: “You haven’t seen Mlle.", "그녀는 차갑게 대답했다. 그러고는 곧바로 덧붙였다."], ["Linon, have you?”", "\"리농 양은 아직 못 봤죠?\""]], [["“Not yet.”", "\"아직요.\""]], [["“Go and speak to her, she likes you so much.”", "\"가서 인사드려요, 당신을 무척 좋아하시니까요.\""]], [["“What’s wrong?", "'뭐가 잘못된 거지?"], ["I have offended her. Lord help me!”", "내가 그녀의 마음을 상하게 했어."], ["thought Levin, and he flew towards the old Frenchwoman with the gray ringlets, who was sitting on a bench.", "하느님, 도와주소서!' 레빈은 그렇게 생각하며, 벤치에 앉아 있던, 잿빛 고수머리를 한 늙은 프랑스 여자에게로 날아가듯 다가갔다."], ["Smiling and showing her false teeth, she greeted him as an old friend.", "그녀는 미소를 지으며 의치를 드러낸 채 옛 친구를 대하듯 그를 맞이했다."]], [["“Yes, you see we’re growing up,”", "\"그래요, 보다시피 우리는 자라고 있죠.\""], ["she said to him, glancing towards Kitty, “and growing old.", "그녀는 키티 쪽을 흘끗 바라보며 말했다. \"그리고 늙어 가고요."], ["Tiny bear has grown big now!”", "꼬마 곰이 이젠 다 컸네요!\""], ["pursued the Frenchwoman, laughing, and she reminded him of his joke about the three young ladies whom he had compared to the three bears in the English nursery tale.", "프랑스 여자는 웃으면서 말을 이었고, 그가 세 아가씨를 영국 동화에 나오는 세 마리 곰에 빗대었던 농담을 그에게 일깨워 주었다."], ["“Do you remember that’s what you used to call them?”", "\"그렇게 부르곤 했던 거 기억나요?\""]], [["He remembered absolutely nothing, but she had been laughing at the joke for ten years now, and was fond of it.", "그는 정말이지 아무것도 기억나지 않았지만, 그녀는 이 농담을 두고 십 년째 웃어 오고 있었고, 그 농담을 무척 좋아했다."]], [["“Now, go and skate, go and skate.", "\"자, 가서 타요, 가서 타요."], ["Our Kitty has learned to skate nicely, hasn’t she?”", "우리 키티가 스케이트를 곧잘 타게 됐죠, 안 그래요?\""]], [["When Levin darted up to Kitty her face was no longer stern; her eyes looked at him with the same sincerity and friendliness, but Levin fancied that in her friendliness there was a certain note of deliberate composure.", "레빈이 쏜살같이 키티에게로 다가갔을 때 그녀의 얼굴은 더 이상 굳어 있지 않았다. 그녀의 눈은 여전한 진실함과 다정함으로 그를 바라보았지만, 레빈은 그 다정함 속에 일부러 꾸민 차분함의 기색이 어려 있는 듯한 느낌을 받았다."], ["And he felt depressed.", "그래서 그는 의기소침해졌다."], ["After talking a little of her old governess and her peculiarities, she questioned him about his life.", "그녀의 옛 가정교사와 그녀의 별난 점들에 대해 잠깐 이야기를 나눈 뒤, 그녀는 그에게 그의 생활에 대해 물었다."]], [["“Surely you must be dull in the country in the winter, aren’t you?”", "\"겨울에 시골에 있으면 분명 따분하겠죠, 안 그래요?\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“No, I’m not dull, I am very busy,”", "\"아니요, 따분하지 않아요."], ["he said, feeling that she was holding him in check by her composed tone, which he would not have the force to break through, just as it had been at the beginning of the winter.", "아주 바쁘게 지내거든요.\" 그는 그녀가 그 차분한 어조로 자기를 묶어 두고 있으며, 겨울이 시작될 무렵에 그랬던 것처럼 자기에게는 그것을 뚫고 나갈 힘이 없으리라는 것을 느끼며 말했다."]], [["“Are you going to stay in town long?”", "\"도시에 오래 머물 건가요?\""], ["Kitty questioned him.", "키티가 그에게 물었다."]], [["“I don’t know,”", "\"모르겠어요.\""], ["he answered, not thinking of what he was saying.", "그는 자기가 무슨 말을 하는지도 생각하지 못한 채 대답했다."], ["The thought that if he were held in check by her tone of quiet friendliness he would end by going back again without deciding anything came into his mind, and he resolved to make a struggle against it.", "그녀의 조용히 다정한 어조에 묶여 있다가는 결국 아무것도 결정하지 못한 채 다시 돌아가 버리게 되리라는 생각이 그의 머릿속에 떠올랐고, 그는 그것에 맞서 싸워 보기로 마음먹었다."]], [["“How is it you don’t know?”", "\"어째서 모른다는 거예요?\""]], [["“I don’t know.", "\"모르겠어요."], ["It depends upon you,”", "그건 당신에게 달렸거든요.\""], ["he said, and was immediately horror-stricken at his own words.", "그가 말했고, 자기 말에 곧바로 소스라치게 놀랐다."]], [["Whether it was that she had heard his words, or that she did not want to hear them, she made a sort of stumble, twice struck out, and hurriedly skated away from him. She skated up to Mlle.", "그의 말을 들었기 때문인지, 아니면 그 말을 듣고 싶지 않았기 때문인지, 그녀는 살짝 비틀거리는 듯하더니 두어 번 발을 내딛고는 황급히 그에게서 미끄러져 멀어졌다."], ["Linon, said something to her, and went towards the pavilion where the ladies took off their skates.", "그녀는 리농 양에게로 미끄러져 가 무언가 말을 하고는, 부인들이 스케이트를 벗는 정자 쪽으로 갔다."]], [["“My God!", "'맙소사!"], ["what have I done! Merciful God!", "내가 무슨 짓을 한 거지!"], ["help me, guide me,”", "자비로우신 하느님!"], ["said Levin, praying inwardly, and at the same time, feeling a need of violent exercise, he skated about describing inner and outer circles.", "저를 도우시고, 이끌어 주소서.' 레빈은 마음속으로 기도하며, 동시에 격렬하게 몸을 움직일 필요를 느끼면서 안쪽과 바깥쪽으로 원을 그리며 스케이트를 탔다."]], [["At that moment one of the young men, the best of the skaters of the day, came out of the coffee-house in his skates, with a cigarette in his mouth.", "그 순간 그날 최고의 스케이트 선수인 젊은이 하나가 입에 담배를 문 채 스케이트를 신고 다방에서 나왔다."], ["Taking a run, he dashed down the steps in his skates, crashing and bounding up and down.", "달려와서는 스케이트를 신은 채 우당탕 요란하게 위아래로 튀어 오르며 계단을 내달려 내려갔다."], ["He flew down, and without even changing the position of his hands, skated away over the ice.", "그는 날듯이 내려와서는 두 손의 자세를 바꾸지도 않은 채 얼음판 위를 미끄러져 가 버렸다."]], [["“Ah, that’s a new trick!”", "\"아, 저거 새로운 묘기로군!\""], ["said Levin, and he promptly ran up to the top to do this new trick.", "레빈은 그렇게 말하고는 곧바로 그 새로운 묘기를 해 보려고 꼭대기로 뛰어올라 갔다."]], [["“Don’t break your neck!", "\"목 부러뜨리지 말게!"], ["it needs practice!”", "연습이 필요한 거야!\""], ["Nikolay Shtcherbatsky shouted after him.", "니콜라이 셰르바츠키가 그의 등 뒤에서 소리쳤다."]], [["Levin went to the steps, took a run from above as best he could, and dashed down, preserving his balance in this unwonted movement with his hands.", "레빈은 계단으로 가서 위에서부터 할 수 있는 한 힘껏 달려 내려오며, 두 손으로 이 익숙지 않은 동작 속에서 균형을 잡았다."], ["On the last step he stumbled, but barely touching the ice with his hand, with a violent effort recovered himself, and skated off, laughing.", "마지막 계단에서 그는 비틀거렸지만, 한 손으로 얼음을 살짝 짚었을 뿐 안간힘을 써서 몸을 가누고는 웃으면서 미끄러져 갔다."]], [["“How splendid, how nice he is!”", "'어쩜 저렇게 멋질까, 어쩜 저렇게 좋은 사람일까!'"], ["Kitty was thinking at that time, as she came out of the pavilion with Mlle.", "그때 키티는 리농 양과 함께 정자에서 나오며, 마치 그가 사랑하는 오빠라도 되는 양 조용한 애정이 담긴 미소를 지으며 그를 바라보았다."], ["Linon, and looked towards him with a smile of quiet affection, as though he were a favorite brother.", "'그런데 이게 내 잘못일까, 내가 뭔가 잘못한 게 있을까? 사람들은 교태를 부린다고들 하지."], ["“And can it be my fault, can I have done anything wrong? They talk of flirtation.", "내가 사랑하는 게 그 사람이 아니라는 건 알아."], ["I know it’s not he that I love; but still I am happy with him, and he’s so jolly.", "하지만 그래도 난 그 사람과 있으면 행복하고, 그 사람은 참 유쾌하지. 다만, 왜 그런 말을 했을까?……'"], ["Only, why did he say that?...” she mused.", "그녀는 골똘히 생각에 잠겼다."]], [["Catching sight of Kitty going away, and her mother meeting her at the steps, Levin, flushed from his rapid exercise, stood still and pondered a minute.", "키티가 떠나가는 것을, 그리고 그녀의 어머니가 계단에서 그녀를 맞이하는 것을 본 레빈은, 격렬하게 몸을 움직인 탓에 얼굴이 상기된 채로 멈춰 서서 잠깐 생각에 잠겼다."], ["He took off his skates, and overtook the mother and daughter at the entrance of the gardens.", "그는 스케이트를 벗고, 정원 입구에서 모녀를 따라잡았다."]], [["“Delighted to see you,”", "\"만나서 반가워요.\""], ["said Princess Shtcherbatskaya.", "셰르바츠카야 공작 부인이 말했다."], ["“On Thursdays we are home, as always.”", "\"목요일에는 늘 그렇듯 집에 있답니다.\""]], [["“Today, then?”", "\"그럼 오늘인가요?\""]], [["“We shall be pleased to see you,”", "\"오시면 기쁘겠어요.\""], ["the princess said stiffly.", "공작 부인이 딱딱하게 말했다."]], [["This stiffness hurt Kitty, and she could not resist the desire to smooth over her mother’s coldness.", "이 딱딱함이 키티의 마음을 아프게 했고, 그녀는 어머니의 냉랭함을 누그러뜨리고 싶은 마음을 억누를 수가 없었다."], ["She turned her head, and with a smile said:", "그녀는 고개를 돌리고 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["“Good-bye till this evening.”", "\"그럼 저녁때 뵐게요.\""]], [["At that moment Stepan Arkadyevitch, his hat cocked on one side, with beaming face and eyes, strode into the garden like a conquering hero.", "그 순간 스테판 아르카지치가, 모자를 한쪽으로 비뚜름하게 쓰고 얼굴과 눈을 환하게 빛내며 정복하고 돌아온 영웅처럼 성큼성큼 정원으로 들어섰다."], ["But as he approached his mother-in-law, he responded in a mournful and crestfallen tone to her inquiries about Dolly’s health.", "그러나 장모에게 다가가자 그는 돌리의 건강을 묻는 그녀의 물음에 구슬프고 풀 죽은 어조로 대답했다."], ["After a little subdued and dejected conversation with his mother-in-law, he threw out his chest again, and put his arm in Levin’s.", "장모와 나직하고 침울하게 잠시 이야기를 나눈 뒤, 그는 다시 가슴을 활짝 펴고 레빈의 팔에 자기 팔을 끼었다."]], [["“Well, shall we set off?”", "\"자, 그럼 가 볼까?\""], ["he asked.", "그가 물었다."], ["“I’ve been thinking about you all this time, and I’m very, very glad you’ve come,”", "\"그동안 줄곧 자네 생각을 했네. 자네가 와 줘서 정말, 정말 기쁘네.\""], ["he said, looking him in the face with a significant air.", "그는 의미심장한 표정으로 그의 얼굴을 들여다보며 말했다."]], [["“Yes, come along,”", "\"그래, 가세.\" 레빈은 황홀경에 빠져 대답했다."], ["answered Levin in ecstasy, hearing unceasingly the sound of that voice saying, “Good-bye till this evening,” and seeing the smile with which it was said.", "그의 귀에는 \"그럼 저녁때 뵐게요\"라고 말하던 그 목소리가 끊임없이 들려왔고, 그 말과 함께 짓던 미소가 눈앞에 어른거렸다."]], [["“To the England or the Hermitage?”", "\"앙글레테르로 갈까, 에르미타시로 갈까?\""]], [["“I don’t mind which.”", "\"난 어느 쪽이든 상관없네.\""]], [["“All right, then, the England,”", "\"좋아, 그럼 앙글레테르로.\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, selecting that restaurant because he owed more there than at the Hermitage, and consequently considered it mean to avoid it.", "스테판 아르카지치는 그 식당을 골랐는데, 에르미타시보다 거기에 진 빚이 더 많았기에 그곳을 피하는 것은 비겁한 짓이라고 여겼기 때문이다."], ["“Have you got a sledge?", "\"썰매는 있나?"], ["That’s first-rate, for I sent my carriage home.”", "그것참 잘됐군, 난 마차를 집으로 돌려보냈거든.\""]], [["The friends hardly spoke all the way.", "가는 내내 두 친구는 거의 말이 없었다."], ["Levin was wondering what that change in Kitty’s expression had meant, and alternately assuring himself that there was hope, and falling into despair, seeing clearly that his hopes were insane, and yet all the while he felt himself quite another man, utterly unlike what he had been before her smile and those words, “Good-bye till this evening.”", "레빈은 키티의 표정에 일었던 그 변화가 무엇을 뜻하는지 궁금해하며, 희망이 있다고 자신을 다독였다가 다시 절망에 빠졌다가 하면서, 자기 희망이 미친 짓임을 분명히 알면서도, 그러면서도 줄곧 자기가 그녀의 미소와 \"그럼 저녁때 뵐게요\"라는 그 말 이전의 자기와는 전혀 다른, 완전히 딴사람이 된 것 같다고 느꼈다."]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch was absorbed during the drive in composing the menu of the dinner.", "스테판 아르카지치는 가는 동안 만찬의 메뉴를 짜는 데 골몰해 있었다."]], [["“You like turbot, don’t you?”", "\"자네 가자미 좋아하지, 안 그래?\""], ["he said to Levin as they were arriving.", "도착할 무렵 그가 레빈에게 말했다."]], [["“Eh?”", "\"어?\""], ["responded Levin.", "레빈이 대답했다."], ["“Turbot?", "\"가자미?"], ["Yes, I’m awfully fond of turbot.”", "그래, 난 가자미를 정말이지 무척 좋아하네.\""]], [["# Chapter 10", "# Chapter 10"]], [["When Levin went into the restaurant with Oblonsky, he could not help noticing a certain peculiarity of expression, as it were, a restrained radiance, about the face and whole figure of Stepan Arkadyevitch.", "레빈이 오블론스키와 함께 식당에 들어섰을 때, 그는 스테판 아르카지치의 얼굴과 온몸에서 어딘가 독특한 표정, 말하자면 억눌린 광채 같은 것을 알아채지 않을 수 없었다."], ["Oblonsky took off his overcoat, and with his hat over one ear walked into the dining-room, giving directions to the Tatar waiters, who were clustered about him in evening coats, bearing napkins.", "오블론스키는 외투를 벗고 모자를 한쪽으로 비스듬히 쓴 채 식당 안으로 들어가며, 연미복 차림에 냅킨을 들고 그의 주위로 몰려든 타타르인 종업원들에게 이런저런 지시를 내렸다."], ["Bowing to right and left to the people he met, and here as everywhere joyously greeting acquaintances, he went up to the sideboard for a preliminary appetizer of fish and vodka, and said to the painted Frenchwoman decked in ribbons, lace, and ringlets, behind the counter, something so amusing that even that Frenchwoman was moved to genuine laughter.", "마주치는 사람들에게 좌우로 인사를 건네며, 여기서도 여느 곳에서나 그렇듯 즐겁게 아는 이들과 인사를 나누고는, 그는 생선과 보드카로 입가심을 할 요량으로 곁상 쪽으로 다가갔다. 그러고는 카운터 뒤편에 리본과 레이스와 곱슬머리로 잔뜩 치장하고 화장을 한 프랑스 여자에게 무언가 어찌나 우스운 말을 건넸던지, 그 프랑스 여자조차 진심으로 웃음을 터뜨릴 정도였다."], ["Levin for his part refrained from taking any vodka simply because he felt such a loathing of that Frenchwoman, all made up, it seemed, of false hair, poudre de riz, and vinaigre de toilette.", "레빈으로서는 보드카를 입에 대지 않았는데, 그것은 단지 그 프랑스 여자가 가짜 머리카락과 poudre de riz와 vinaigre de toilette로만 이루어진 것 같아서 진저리가 났기 때문이었다. 그는 마치 더러운 곳에서 벗어나듯 서둘러 그녀에게서 멀어졌다."], ["He made haste to move away from her, as from a dirty place.", "그의 영혼은 온통 키티에 대한 기억으로 가득 차 있었고, 그의 눈에는 승리와 행복의 미소가 빛나고 있었다."], ["His whole soul was filled with memories of Kitty, and there was a smile of triumph and happiness shining in his eyes.", "* poudre de riz: 프랑스어로 쌀가루로 만든 분(粉). vinaigre de toilette: 화장용 향수 식초."]], [["“This way, your excellency, please.", "\"이쪽으로 오시지요, 나리."], ["Your excellency won’t be disturbed here,”", "이 자리시면 나리께서 방해받으실 일이 없습니다.\""], ["said a particularly pertinacious, white-headed old Tatar with immense hips and coat-tails gaping widely behind.", "엉덩이가 어마어마하게 크고 뒤로 옷자락이 활짝 벌어진, 머리가 허연 유난히 끈질긴 타타르인 노인이 말했다."], ["“Walk in, your excellency,”", "\"어서 드시지요, 나리.\""], ["he said to Levin; by way of showing his respect to Stepan Arkadyevitch, being attentive to his guest as well.", "그는 레빈에게도 말했는데, 스테판 아르카지치에 대한 존경의 표시로 그 손님에게도 신경을 쓰는 것이었다."]], [["Instantly flinging a fresh cloth over the round table under the bronze chandelier, though it already had a table cloth on it, he pushed up velvet chairs, and came to a standstill before Stepan Arkadyevitch with a napkin and a bill of fare in his hands, awaiting his commands.", "청동 샹들리에 아래 둥근 식탁에는 이미 식탁보가 깔려 있었지만, 그는 곧바로 새 식탁보를 휙 펼쳐 덮고는 벨벳 의자들을 끌어다 놓았다. 그러고는 냅킨과 차림표를 손에 든 채 스테판 아르카지치 앞에 서서 분부를 기다렸다."]], [["“If you prefer it, your excellency, a private room will be free directly; Prince Golistin with a lady.", "\"원하신다면, 나리, 곧 별실 하나가 빌 겁니다. 골리친 공작이 어떤 부인과 함께 계신데요."], ["Fresh oysters have come in.”", "싱싱한 굴도 들어왔습니다.\""]], [["“Ah!", "\"오!"], ["oysters.”", "굴이라.\""]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch became thoughtful.", "스테판 아르카지치는 생각에 잠겼다."]], [["“How if we were to change our program, Levin?”", "\"우리 계획을 좀 바꿔 보는 게 어떨까, 레빈?\""], ["he said, keeping his finger on the bill of fare.", "그는 차림표 위에 손가락을 댄 채 말했다."], ["And his face expressed serious hesitation.", "그의 얼굴에는 자못 진지한 망설임이 떠올라 있었다."], ["“Are the oysters good?", "\"굴이 괜찮은가?"], ["Mind now.”", "잘 살펴보게.\""]], [["“They’re Flensburg, your excellency.", "\"플렌스부르크산입니다, 나리."], ["We’ve no Ostend.”", "오스텐드산은 없습니다.\""]], [["“Flensburg will do, but are they fresh?”", "\"플렌스부르크산이라도 좋아, 그런데 싱싱한가?\""]], [["“Only arrived yesterday.”", "\"어제 막 들어온 겁니다.\""]], [["“Well, then, how if we were to begin with oysters, and so change the whole program?", "\"그럼, 굴로 시작해서 계획을 통째로 바꾸는 게 어떨까?"], ["Eh?”", "응?\""]], [["“It’s all the same to me.", "\"나는 아무래도 좋아."], ["I should like cabbage soup and porridge better than anything; but of course there’s nothing like that here.”", "무엇보다 양배추 수프하고 죽이 제일 좋겠지만, 물론 여기에 그런 건 없겠지.\""]], [["“Porridge à la Russe, your honor would like?”", "\"러시아식 죽 말씀이십니까, 나리?\""], ["said the Tatar, bending down to Levin, like a nurse speaking to a child.", "타타르인이 마치 아이에게 말하는 유모처럼 레빈에게 몸을 굽히며 말했다."]], [["“No, joking apart, whatever you choose is sure to be good.", "\"아니, 농담은 그만두고, 자네가 고르는 거라면 뭐든 분명 맛있을 거야."], ["I’ve been skating, and I’m hungry.", "스케이트를 탔더니 배가 고프군."], ["And don’t imagine,”", "그렇다고,\""], ["he added, detecting a look of dissatisfaction on Oblonsky’s face, “that I shan’t appreciate your choice.", "그는 오블론스키의 얼굴에 떠오른 불만스러운 기색을 알아채고 덧붙였다. \"자네가 고른 걸 내가 못 알아본다고는 생각하지 말게."], ["I am fond of good things.”", "나도 좋은 음식은 좋아하니까.\""]], [["“I should hope so!", "\"그래야지!"], ["After all, it’s one of the pleasures of life,”", "어쨌든 그것도 인생의 즐거움 가운데 하나니까.\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch.", "스테판 아르카지치가 말했다."], ["“Well, then, my friend, you give us two—or better say three—dozen oysters, clear soup with vegetables....”", "\"그럼, 이봐, 굴을 두 다스, 아니 세 다스가 낫겠군, 그리고 채소를 넣은 맑은 수프……\""]], [["“Printanière,” prompted the Tatar.", "\"프랭타니에르 말씀이시군요.\""], ["But Stepan Arkadyevitch apparently did not care to allow him the satisfaction of giving the French names of the dishes.", "타타르인이 거들었다. 하지만 스테판 아르카지치는 그가 요리의 프랑스 이름을 입에 올리는 만족을 누리도록 내버려 둘 생각이 없는 듯했다."]], [["“With vegetables in it, you know.", "\"채소를 넣은 거 말일세."], ["Then turbot with thick sauce, then ...", "그다음에 진한 소스를 곁들인 넙치, 그러고는……"], ["roast beef; and mind it’s good.", "로스트비프. 잘 만들어야 해."], ["Yes, and capons, perhaps, and then sweets.”", "그래, 거세한 수탉 요리도 좋겠군, 그리고 단것을 내오게.\""]], [["The Tatar, recollecting that it was Stepan Arkadyevitch’s way not to call the dishes by the names in the French bill of fare, did not repeat them after him, but could not resist rehearsing the whole menu to himself according to the bill:—“Soupe printanière, turbot, sauce Beaumarchais, poulard à l’estragon, macédoine de fruits ...", "타타르인은 스테판 아르카지치가 프랑스 차림표의 이름대로 요리를 부르지 않는 버릇이 있다는 것을 떠올리고는, 그를 따라 되풀이하지 않았다."], ["etc.,”", "다만 차림표대로 메뉴 전부를 혼자 되뇌고 싶은 마음만은 참을 수가 없었다."], ["and then instantly, as though worked by springs, laying down one bound bill of fare, he took up another, the list of wines, and submitted it to Stepan Arkadyevitch.", "\"프랭타니에르 수프, 넙치, 보마르셰 소스, 에스트라공을 넣은 영계 요리, 과일 마세두안……\" 그러고는 마치 용수철로 움직이듯 즉시 끈으로 묶은 차림표 하나를 내려놓고 다른 하나, 곧 포도주 목록을 집어 들어 스테판 아르카지치에게 내밀었다."]], [["“What shall we drink?”", "\"무엇을 마실까?\""]], [["“What you like, only not too much.", "\"자네 좋을 대로, 다만 너무 많이는 말고."], ["Champagne,”", "샴페인이 좋겠어.\""], ["said Levin.", "레빈이 말했다."]], [["“What!", "\"뭐!"], ["to start with?", "시작부터?"], ["You’re right though, I dare say.", "하긴 자네 말이 맞아."], ["Do you like the white seal?”", "화이트 실 좋아하나?\""]], [["“Cachet blanc,”", "\"카셰 블랑 말씀이시군요.\""], ["prompted the Tatar.", "타타르인이 거들었다."]], [["“Very well, then, give us that brand with the oysters, and then we’ll see.”", "\"좋아, 그럼 그 상표로 굴과 함께 내오고, 다음은 그때 가서 보지.\""]], [["“Yes, sir.", "\"네, 나리."], ["And what table wine?”", "식탁용 포도주는 무엇으로 할까요?\""]], [["“You can give us Nuits.", "\"뉘를 주게."], ["Oh, no, better the classic Chablis.”", "아니, 고전적인 샤블리가 낫겠군.\""]], [["“Yes, sir.", "\"네, 나리."], ["And your cheese, your excellency?”", "치즈는 어떤 것으로 하시겠습니까, 나리?\""]], [["“Oh, yes, Parmesan.", "\"아, 그래, 파르메산으로."], ["Or would you like another?”", "아니면 자네 다른 게 좋겠나?\""]], [["“No, it’s all the same to me,”", "\"아니, 나는 아무래도 좋아.\""], ["said Levin, unable to suppress a smile.", "레빈이 미소를 참지 못하며 말했다."]], [["And the Tatar ran off with flying coat-tails, and in five minutes darted in with a dish of opened oysters on mother-of-pearl shells, and a bottle between his fingers.", "타타르인은 옷자락을 휘날리며 달려갔다가, 오 분 뒤에는 자개껍데기에 담긴 까 놓은 굴 한 접시를 들고, 손가락 사이에는 병 하나를 끼운 채 쏜살같이 들어왔다."]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch crushed the starchy napkin, tucked it into his waistcoat, and settling his arms comfortably, started on the oysters.", "스테판 아르카지치는 빳빳한 냅킨을 구겨 조끼 사이에 끼우고는, 두 팔을 편하게 자리 잡은 다음 굴을 먹기 시작했다."]], [["“Not bad,”", "\"나쁘지 않군.\""], ["he said, stripping the oysters from the pearly shell with a silver fork, and swallowing them one after another.", "그는 은제 포크로 진주빛 껍데기에서 굴을 떼어 내 하나씩 삼키며 말했다. \"나쁘지 않아.\""], ["“Not bad,” he repeated, turning his dewy, brilliant eyes from Levin to the Tatar.", "그는 촉촉하고 반짝이는 눈을 레빈에게서 타타르인 쪽으로 돌리며 거듭 말했다."]], [["Levin ate the oysters indeed, though white bread and cheese would have pleased him better.", "레빈은 흰 빵과 치즈가 더 좋았겠지만, 그래도 굴을 먹었다. 다만 그는 오블론스키를 바라보며 감탄하고 있었다."], ["But he was admiring Oblonsky. Even the Tatar, uncorking the bottle and pouring the sparkling wine into the delicate glasses, glanced at Stepan Arkadyevitch, and settled his white cravat with a perceptible smile of satisfaction.", "병마개를 따서 거품이 이는 포도주를 가느다란 잔에 따르던 타타르인조차 스테판 아르카지치를 흘끗 바라보고는, 흡족함이 역력한 미소를 띤 채 자기 흰 넥타이를 매만졌다."]], [["“You don’t care much for oysters, do you?”", "\"자네는 굴을 별로 좋아하지 않는 모양이군?\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, emptying his wine-glass, “or you’re worried about something.", "스테판 아르카지치가 포도주잔을 비우며 말했다. \"아니면 뭔가 걱정거리가 있거나."], ["Eh?”", "응?\""]], [["He wanted Levin to be in good spirits.", "그는 레빈이 기분 좋게 지내기를 바랐다."], ["But it was not that Levin was not in good spirits; he was ill at ease.", "하지만 레빈은 기분이 나쁜 것이 아니라, 마음이 편치 않은 것이었다."], ["With what he had in his soul, he felt sore and uncomfortable in the restaurant, in the midst of private rooms where men were dining with ladies, in all this fuss and bustle; the surroundings of bronzes, looking-glasses, gas, and waiters—all of it was offensive to him.", "마음속에 품은 것을 안고 있는 그로서는, 남자들이 부인들과 식사를 하고 있는 별실들 한가운데, 이 온갖 부산함과 법석 속에서 식당에 있는 것이 쓰라리고 거북했다. 청동 장식이며 거울이며 가스등이며 종업원들에 둘러싸인 이 모든 것이 그에게는 거슬렸다."], ["He was afraid of sullying what his soul was brimful of.", "그는 영혼 가득히 차오른 것을 더럽힐까 봐 두려웠던 것이다."]], [["“I?", "\"나?"], ["Yes, I am; but besides, all this bothers me,”", "그래, 걱정이 있긴 해. 하지만 그뿐 아니라 이 모든 게 다 거북해.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“You can’t conceive how queer it all seems to a country person like me, as queer as that gentleman’s nails I saw at your place....”", "\"나 같은 시골 사람한테 이 모든 게 얼마나 기이하게 느껴지는지 자네는 짐작도 못 할 거야. 자네 사무실에서 본 그 신사 손톱만큼이나 기이하단 말이지……\""]], [["“Yes, I saw how much interested you were in poor Grinevitch’s nails,”", "\"그래, 자네가 그 가엾은 그리네비치의 손톱에 얼마나 관심을 가졌는지 보았네.\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, laughing.", "스테판 아르카지치가 웃으며 말했다."]], [["“It’s too much for me,” responded Levin.", "\"나로선 도무지 견딜 수가 없어.\""], ["“Do try, now, and put yourself in my place, take the point of view of a country person.", "레빈이 대답했다. \"한번 내 처지가 되어 시골 사람의 눈으로 봐 보게."], ["We in the country try to bring our hands into such a state as will be most convenient for working with.", "우리 시골에선 일하기 가장 편한 상태로 손을 만들려고 애를 쓰지."], ["So we cut our nails; sometimes we turn up our sleeves.", "그래서 손톱을 깎고, 가끔은 소매를 걷어 올려."], ["And here people purposely let their nails grow as long as they will, and link on small saucers by way of studs, so that they can do nothing with their hands.”", "그런데 여기 사람들은 일부러 손톱을 마음껏 길게 기르고, 단추 대신 작은 접시 같은 걸 매달아서, 손으로는 아무 일도 못 하게 만들어 놓았단 말이야.\""]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch smiled gaily.", "스테판 아르카지치가 유쾌하게 미소 지었다."]], [["“Oh, yes, that’s just a sign that he has no need to do coarse work.", "\"오, 그래, 그건 바로 그가 거친 일을 할 필요가 없다는 표시일 뿐이야."], ["His work is with the mind....”", "그의 일이란 머리로 하는 거니까……\""]], [["“Maybe.", "\"그럴지도 모르지."], ["But still it’s queer to me, just as at this moment it seems queer to me that we country folks try to get our meals over as soon as we can, so as to be ready for our work, while here are we trying to drag out our meal as long as possible, and with that object eating oysters....”", "하지만 그래도 나한테는 기이하게 느껴져. 지금 이 순간에도 기이하게 느껴지는 게, 우리 시골 사람들은 어서 일할 채비를 하려고 식사를 되도록 빨리 끝내려 애쓰는데, 여기서 우리는 식사를 되도록 오래 끌려고 애쓰면서 그러느라 굴을 먹고 있단 말이지……\""]], [["“Why, of course,”", "\"아니, 물론 그렇지.\""], ["objected Stepan Arkadyevitch.", "스테판 아르카지치가 반박했다."], ["“But that’s just the aim of civilization—to make everything a source of enjoyment.”", "\"하지만 그게 바로 문명의 목적이야. 모든 것을 즐거움의 원천으로 만드는 것 말이야.\""]], [["“Well, if that’s its aim, I’d rather be a savage.”", "\"흠, 그게 목적이라면, 난 차라리 야만인이 되겠어.\""]], [["“And so you are a savage.", "\"그러니까 자네는 야만인이야."], ["All you Levins are savages.”", "자네 레빈 집안사람들은 죄다 야만인이라니까.\""]], [["Levin sighed.", "레빈은 한숨을 쉬었다."], ["He remembered his brother Nikolay, and felt ashamed and sore, and he scowled; but Oblonsky began speaking of a subject which at once drew his attention.", "그는 형 니콜라이를 떠올리고는 부끄럽고 쓰라린 마음에 얼굴을 찡그렸다. 하지만 오블론스키가 곧 그의 주의를 끄는 화제를 꺼냈다."]], [["“Oh, I say, are you going tonight to our people, the Shtcherbatskys’, I mean?”", "\"아 참, 오늘 밤 우리 집안 식구들, 그러니까 셰르바츠키 댁에 갈 텐가?\""], ["he said, his eyes sparkling significantly as he pushed away the empty rough shells, and drew the cheese towards him.", "그는 빈 거친 껍데기들을 밀어 놓고 치즈를 끌어당기며, 의미심장하게 눈을 반짝이며 말했다."]], [["“Yes, I shall certainly go,”", "\"그래, 꼭 갈 생각이야.\""], ["replied Levin; “though I fancied the princess was not very warm in her invitation.”", "레빈이 대답했다. \"다만 공작 부인이 초대하면서 그리 살갑지는 않았던 것 같긴 하지만.\""]], [["“What nonsense!", "\"무슨 소리!"], ["That’s her manner....", "그건 그분 방식이야……"], ["Come, boy, the soup!...", "이봐, 수프를 가져오게!……"], ["That’s her manner—grande dame,”", "그게 그분 방식이라니까, grande dame 말이야.\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch.", "스테판 아르카지치가 말했다."], ["“I’m coming, too, but I have to go to the Countess Bonina’s rehearsal.", "\"나도 갈 거야, 다만 그 전에 보니나 백작 부인 댁 예행연습에 가 봐야 해."], ["Come, isn’t it true that you’re a savage?", "자, 자네가 야만인이라는 게 정말 맞지 않은가?"], ["How do you explain the sudden way in which you vanished from Moscow?", "자네가 그렇게 느닷없이 모스크바에서 사라진 걸 어떻게 설명할 텐가?"], ["The Shtcherbatskys were continually asking me about you, as though I ought to know.", "셰르바츠키 댁에서는 마치 내가 다 알고 있어야 한다는 듯이 줄곧 자네에 대해 물었지."], ["The only thing I know is that you always do what no one else does.”", "내가 아는 거라곤, 자네가 늘 남들은 하지 않는 짓만 한다는 것뿐이야.\" * grande dame: 프랑스어로 '귀부인'."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그래.\""], ["said Levin, slowly and with emotion, “you’re right.", "레빈이 천천히, 감정을 실어 말했다. \"자네 말이 맞아."], ["I am a savage.", "난 야만인이지."], ["Only, my savageness is not in having gone away, but in coming now.", "다만 내 야만성은 떠나 버린 데 있는 게 아니라, 지금 이렇게 다시 온 데 있어."], ["Now I have come....”", "이제 나는 왔거든……\""]], [["“Oh, what a lucky fellow you are!”", "\"오, 자네는 얼마나 운 좋은 사람인가!\""], ["broke in Stepan Arkadyevitch, looking into Levin’s eyes.", "스테판 아르카지치가 레빈의 눈을 들여다보며 끼어들었다."]], [["“Why?”", "\"왜?\""]], [["And by his eyes I know a youth in love,’”", "\"'나는 확실한 표징으로 늠름한 준마를 알아보고, 그 눈으로 사랑에 빠진 젊은이를 알아보네.'\""], ["declaimed Stepan Arkadyevitch.", "스테판 아르카지치가 낭송하듯 읊었다."], ["“Everything is before you.”", "\"자네 앞에는 모든 것이 펼쳐져 있어.\""]], [["“Why, is it over for you already?”", "\"왜, 자네한테는 벌써 다 끝났나?\""]], [["“No; not over exactly, but the future is yours, and the present is mine, and the present—well, it’s not all that it might be.”", "\"아니, 딱히 끝난 건 아니지만, 미래는 자네 것이고 현재는 내 것이지. 그리고 그 현재라는 게, 흠, 마땅히 그래야 할 그런 것은 못 되네.\""]], [["“How so?”", "\"어째서?\""]], [["“Oh, things go wrong.", "\"오, 일이 잘 안 풀리고 있어."], ["But I don’t want to talk of myself, and besides I can’t explain it all,”", "하지만 내 얘긴 하고 싶지 않고, 게다가 전부 다 설명할 수도 없어.\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch.", "스테판 아르카지치가 말했다."], ["“Well, why have you come to Moscow, then?...", "\"그래, 그럼 자네는 무슨 일로 모스크바에 온 건가?…… 이봐!"], ["Hi! take away!”", "이건 치워 주게!\""], ["he called to the Tatar.", "그가 타타르인에게 외쳤다."]], [["“You guess?”", "\"짐작하면서?\""], ["responded Levin, his eyes like deep wells of light fixed on Stepan Arkadyevitch.", "레빈이 깊은 빛의 우물 같은 눈을 스테판 아르카지치에게 고정한 채 대답했다."]], [["“I guess, but I can’t be the first to talk about it.", "\"짐작은 하지만, 내가 먼저 그 얘기를 꺼낼 수는 없지."], ["You can see by that whether I guess right or wrong,”", "내가 옳게 짚었는지 그른지는 그걸 보면 알 수 있을 거야.\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, gazing at Levin with a subtle smile.", "스테판 아르카지치가 묘한 미소를 띠고 레빈을 바라보며 말했다."]], [["“Well, and what have you to say to me?”", "\"그래, 그럼 자네는 나한테 무슨 말을 해 주겠나?\""], ["said Levin in a quivering voice, feeling that all the muscles of his face were quivering too.", "레빈이 떨리는 목소리로 말했다. 그는 자기 얼굴의 모든 근육이 떨리고 있음을 느꼈다."], ["“How do you look at the question?”", "\"자네는 이 문제를 어떻게 보나?\""]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch slowly emptied his glass of Chablis, never taking his eyes off Levin.", "스테판 아르카지치는 레빈에게서 눈을 떼지 않은 채, 샤블리잔을 천천히 비웠다."]], [["“I?”", "\"나?"], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, “there’s nothing I desire so much as that—nothing!", "나야말로 그것만큼 바라는 게 없네, 그것 말고는 아무것도! 그건 일어날 수 있는 일 가운데 가장 좋은 일이 될 거야.\""], ["It would be the best thing that could be.”", "스테판 아르카지치가 말했다."]], [["“But you’re not making a mistake?", "\"하지만 자네가 잘못 짚은 건 아니지?"], ["You know what we’re speaking of?”", "우리가 무슨 얘기를 하고 있는지 알고 있나?\""], ["said Levin, piercing him with his eyes.", "레빈이 그를 눈으로 꿰뚫으며 말했다."], ["“You think it’s possible?”", "\"자네는 그게 가능하다고 생각하나?\""]], [["“I think it’s possible.", "\"가능하다고 생각해."], ["Why not possible?”", "왜 가능하지 않겠나?\""]], [["“No!", "\"아니!"], ["do you really think it’s possible?", "자네 정말로 가능하다고 생각하는 거야?"], ["No, tell me all you think!", "아니, 자네가 생각하는 걸 다 말해 주게!"], ["Oh, but if ...", "오, 하지만 만약……"], ["if refusal’s in store for me!...", "만약 거절이 나를 기다리고 있다면!……"], ["Indeed I feel sure....”", "정말이지 그럴 것만 같아……\""]], [["“Why should you think that?”", "\"왜 그렇게 생각하나?\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, smiling at his excitement.", "스테판 아르카지치가 그의 흥분에 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["“It seems so to me sometimes.", "\"가끔 그렇게 느껴져."], ["That will be awful for me, and for her too.”", "그렇게 되면 나한테도, 그녀한테도 끔찍한 일이 될 거야.\""]], [["“Oh, well, anyway there’s nothing awful in it for a girl.", "\"흠, 어쨌든 처녀에게는 그게 끔찍한 일이랄 게 없어."], ["Every girl’s proud of an offer.”", "어떤 처녀든 청혼을 자랑스러워하니까.\""]], [["“Yes, every girl, but not she.”", "\"그래, 어떤 처녀든 그렇겠지, 하지만 그녀는 아니야.\""]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch smiled. He so well knew that feeling of Levin’s, that for him all the girls in the world were divided into two classes: one class—all the girls in the world except her, and those girls with all sorts of human weaknesses, and very ordinary girls: the other class—she alone, having no weaknesses of any sort and higher than all humanity.", "스테판 아르카지치가 미소 지었다. 그는 레빈의 그 마음을 너무도 잘 알고 있었다. 레빈에게는 세상의 모든 처녀가 두 부류로 나뉘었다. 한 부류는 그녀를 제외한 세상의 모든 처녀로, 온갖 인간적 약점을 지닌 지극히 평범한 처녀들이었다. 다른 한 부류는 오직 그녀 하나뿐으로, 어떤 약점도 없고 온 인류보다 높은 존재였다."]], [["“Stay, take some sauce,”", "\"가만, 소스 좀 들게.\""], ["he said, holding back Levin’s hand as it pushed away the sauce.", "그는 소스를 밀어내는 레빈의 손을 붙잡으며 말했다."]], [["Levin obediently helped himself to sauce, but would not let Stepan Arkadyevitch go on with his dinner.", "레빈은 순순히 소스를 덜었지만, 스테판 아르카지치가 계속 식사를 하도록 내버려 두지 않았다."]], [["“No, stop a minute, stop a minute,”", "\"아니, 잠깐만, 잠깐만.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“You must understand that it’s a question of life and death for me.", "\"이건 나한테 사느냐 죽느냐의 문제라는 걸 자네는 알아야 해."], ["I have never spoken to anyone of this.", "난 이걸 누구한테도 말한 적이 없어."], ["And there’s no one I could speak of it to, except you.", "그리고 자네 말고는 이걸 털어놓을 사람이 아무도 없어."], ["You know we’re utterly unlike each other, different tastes and views and everything; but I know you’re fond of me and understand me, and that’s why I like you awfully.", "우리는 서로 완전히 다르다는 거, 취향도 견해도 모든 게 다르다는 거 나도 알아. 하지만 자네가 나를 좋아하고 나를 이해해 준다는 걸 알기에, 그래서 난 자네를 끔찍이 좋아하는 거야."], ["But for God’s sake, be quite straightforward with me.”", "그러니 제발, 나한테 아주 솔직하게 말해 주게.\""]], [["“I tell you what I think,”", "\"내가 생각하는 걸 말해 주겠네.\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, smiling.", "스테판 아르카지치가 미소 지으며 말했다."], ["“But I’ll say more: my wife is a wonderful woman....”", "\"하지만 한마디 더 보태지. 내 아내는 놀라운 여자야……\""], ["Stepan Arkadyevitch sighed, remembering his position with his wife, and, after a moment’s silence, resumed—“She has a gift of foreseeing things.", "스테판 아르카지치는 아내와의 처지를 떠올리며 한숨을 쉬더니, 잠시 잠자코 있다가 다시 말을 이었다. \"그 사람한테는 앞일을 내다보는 재능이 있어."], ["She sees right through people; but that’s not all; she knows what will come to pass, especially in the way of marriages.", "사람 속을 훤히 꿰뚫어 보지. 하지만 그게 다가 아니야."], ["She foretold, for instance, that Princess Shahovskaya would marry Brenteln.", "그 사람은 무슨 일이 일어날지를 알아맞히는데, 특히 혼사에 관해선 더 그래. 이를테면 샤홉스카야 공작 영애가 브렌텔른과 결혼할 거라고 미리 말했지."], ["No one would believe it, but it came to pass.", "아무도 믿지 않았는데, 그대로 됐어."], ["And she’s on your side.”", "그런데 그 사람이 자네 편이야.\""]], [["“How do you mean?”", "\"무슨 뜻이야?\""]], [["“It’s not only that she likes you—she says that Kitty is certain to be your wife.”", "\"그저 자네를 좋아한다는 것뿐이 아니라, 키티가 틀림없이 자네 아내가 될 거라고 말한단 말이야.\""]], [["At these words Levin’s face suddenly lighted up with a smile, a smile not far from tears of emotion.", "이 말에 레빈의 얼굴은 별안간 미소로 환해졌다. 그것은 감격의 눈물에 가까운 미소였다."]], [["“She says that!”", "\"그녀가 그렇게 말했다고!\""], ["cried Levin.", "레빈이 외쳤다."], ["“I always said she was exquisite, your wife.", "\"내가 늘 말했잖아, 자네 아내는 더없이 훌륭한 사람이라고."], ["There, that’s enough, enough said about it,”", "자, 이제 됐어, 이 얘긴 이쯤 하면 충분해.\""], ["he said, getting up from his seat.", "그는 자리에서 일어서며 말했다."]], [["“All right, but do sit down.”", "\"알았어, 그러니 좀 앉게.\""]], [["But Levin could not sit down.", "하지만 레빈은 도무지 앉아 있을 수가 없었다."], ["He walked with his firm tread twice up and down the little cage of a room, blinked his eyelids that his tears might not fall, and only then sat down to the table.", "그는 새장만 한 작은 방을 단단한 걸음걸이로 두 차례 오갔고, 눈물이 떨어지지 않도록 눈을 깜빡거리고는, 그제야 식탁으로 돌아와 앉았다."]], [["“You must understand,”", "\"자네는 이해해야 해.\""], ["said he, “it’s not love.", "그가 말했다. \"이건 사랑이 아니야."], ["I’ve been in love, but it’s not that.", "난 사랑에 빠져 본 적이 있지만, 이건 그게 아니야."], ["It’s not my feeling, but a sort of force outside me has taken possession of me.", "이건 내 감정이 아니라, 내 밖에 있는 어떤 힘 같은 것이 나를 사로잡은 거야. 알겠지만, 나는 떠나 버렸어."], ["I went away, you see, because I made up my mind that it could never be, you understand, as a happiness that does not come on earth; but I’ve struggled with myself, I see there’s no living without it.", "그건 결코 이루어질 수 없는 일이라고, 그러니까 이 땅에서는 찾아오지 않는 행복이라고 마음을 굳혔으니까. 하지만 나 자신과 싸워 본 결과, 그것 없이는 살 수 없다는 걸 알게 됐어."], ["And it must be settled.”", "그러니 결판을 내야 해.\""]], [["“What did you go away for?”", "\"왜 떠났던 건가?\""]], [["“Ah, stop a minute!", "\"아, 잠깐만!"], ["Ah, the thoughts that come crowding on one!", "아, 머릿속으로 밀려드는 온갖 생각들이라니!"], ["The questions one must ask oneself!", "스스로에게 던져야 하는 온갖 물음들이라니!"], ["Listen.", "들어 보게."], ["You can’t imagine what you’ve done for me by what you said.", "자네가 한 그 말로 나한테 어떤 일을 해 줬는지 자네는 상상도 못 할 거야."], ["I’m so happy that I’ve become positively hateful; I’ve forgotten everything.", "난 너무 행복해서 정말이지 밉살스러운 인간이 돼 버렸어. 모든 걸 잊어버렸지."], ["I heard today that my brother Nikolay ...", "오늘 형 니콜라이가…… 알다시피 그가 여기 와 있는데……"], ["you know, he’s here ...", "그 형마저 잊고 있었어."], ["I had even forgotten him.", "형도 행복할 것만 같단 말이야."], ["It seems to me that he’s happy too.", "이건 일종의 광기야. 하지만 한 가지가 끔찍해……"], ["It’s a sort of madness. But one thing’s awful....", "이봐, 자네는 결혼했으니까 그 감정을 알겠지……"], ["Here, you’ve been married, you know the feeling ...", "끔찍한 건, 우리처럼 나이 든 사람, 과거를 지닌 사람이……"], ["it’s awful that we—old—with a past ... not of love, but of sins ...", "사랑이 아니라 죄들로 얼룩진 과거를 지닌 사람이……"], ["are brought all at once so near to a creature pure and innocent; it’s loathsome, and that’s why one can’t help feeling oneself unworthy.”", "순결하고 결백한 존재 곁으로 단숨에 그토록 가까이 끌려간다는 거야. 그건 역겨운 일이고, 그래서 스스로가 그럴 자격이 없다고 느끼지 않을 수 없는 거지.\""]], [["“Oh, well, you’ve not many sins on your conscience.”", "\"오, 흠, 자네 양심에 죄가 그리 많은 건 아니잖나.\""]], [["“Alas!", "\"아아!"], ["all the same,”", "그래도 마찬가지야.\""], ["said Levin, “when with loathing I go over my life, I shudder and curse and bitterly regret it....", "레빈이 말했다. \"혐오감을 안고 내 삶을 돌이켜 보면, 나는 몸서리치고 저주하고 쓰라리게 후회하게 돼……"], ["Yes.”", "그래.\""]], [["“What would you have?", "\"그럼 어쩌겠나?"], ["The world’s made so,”", "세상이 그렇게 생겨먹은걸.\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch.", "스테판 아르카지치가 말했다."]], [["“The one comfort is like that prayer, which I always liked: ‘Forgive me not according to my unworthiness, but according to Thy loving-kindness.’ That’s the only way she can forgive me.”", "\"한 가지 위안이 있다면, 내가 늘 좋아했던 그 기도와 같은 거야. '저의 무가치함이 아니라, 당신의 자비로우심에 따라 저를 용서하소서.' 그녀가 나를 용서할 수 있는 길도 오직 그것뿐이지.\""]], [["Levin emptied his glass, and they were silent for a while.", "레빈은 잔을 비웠고, 둘은 잠시 말이 없었다."]], [["“There’s one other thing I ought to tell you.", "\"한 가지 더 말해 둘 게 있어."], ["Do you know Vronsky?”", "자네, 브론스키를 아나?\""], ["Stepan Arkadyevitch asked Levin.", "스테판 아르카지치가 레빈에게 물었다."]], [["“No, I don’t.", "\"아니, 몰라."], ["Why do you ask?”", "왜 묻는데?\""]], [["“Give us another bottle,”", "\"한 병 더 주게.\""], ["Stepan Arkadyevitch directed the Tatar, who was filling up their glasses and fidgeting round them just when he was not wanted.", "스테판 아르카지치는, 필요하지도 않을 때 잔을 채우며 주위를 부산스레 맴도는 타타르인에게 일렀다."]], [["“Why you ought to know Vronsky is that he’s one of your rivals.”", "\"자네가 브론스키를 알아 둬야 하는 까닭은, 그가 자네의 경쟁자 가운데 한 사람이기 때문이지.\""]], [["“Who’s Vronsky?”", "\"브론스키가 누군데?\""], ["said Levin, and his face was suddenly transformed from the look of childlike ecstasy which Oblonsky had just been admiring to an angry and unpleasant expression.", "레빈이 말했다. 그러자 그의 얼굴은, 방금 전 오블론스키가 흐뭇하게 바라보던 어린아이 같은 환희의 표정에서 별안간 성난, 불쾌한 표정으로 바뀌었다."]], [["“Vronsky is one of the sons of Count Kirill Ivanovitch Vronsky, and one of the finest specimens of the gilded youth of Petersburg.", "\"브론스키는 키릴 이바노비치 브론스키 백작의 아들들 가운데 하나이고, 페테르부르크의 금빛 청년들 중에서도 가장 빼어난 표본 가운데 하나야."], ["I made his acquaintance in Tver when I was there on official business, and he came there for the levy of recruits.", "나는 공무로 트베리에 가 있을 때 그를 알게 됐는데, 그는 신병 모집 때문에 거기 왔지."], ["Fearfully rich, handsome, great connections, an aide-de-camp, and with all that a very nice, good-natured fellow.", "어마어마하게 부자에다, 잘생겼고, 인맥도 넓고, 시종무관인 데다, 게다가 더없이 좋은 사람, 마음씨 고운 친구야."], ["But he’s more than simply a good-natured fellow, as I’ve found out here—he’s a cultivated man, too, and very intelligent; he’s a man who’ll make his mark.”", "한데 그저 마음씨 고운 친구 정도가 아니라는 걸, 여기서 알게 됐어—교양도 갖춘 사람이고, 머리도 아주 좋아. 한자리 할 인물이지.\""]], [["Levin scowled and was dumb.", "레빈은 얼굴을 찌푸리고 입을 다물었다."]], [["“Well, he turned up here soon after you’d gone, and as I can see, he’s over head and ears in love with Kitty, and you know that her mother....”", "\"그래, 그가 자네 떠난 직후에 여기 나타났는데, 내가 보기엔 키티한테 홀딱 반해 있어. 게다가 자네도 알다시피 그 애 어머니가……\""]], [["“Excuse me, but I know nothing,”", "\"미안하지만, 난 아무것도 몰라.\""], ["said Levin, frowning gloomily.", "레빈이 침울하게 미간을 찡그리며 말했다."], ["And immediately he recollected his brother Nikolay and how hateful he was to have been able to forget him.", "그리고 곧바로 형 니콜라이가, 그리고 그 형을 잊고 있을 수 있었던 자신이 얼마나 가증스러운지가 떠올랐다."]], [["“You wait a bit, wait a bit,”", "\"가만, 좀 가만있어 봐.\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, smiling and touching his hand.", "스테판 아르카지치가 미소를 지으며 그의 손을 어루만지듯 짚으며 말했다."], ["“I’ve told you what I know, and I repeat that in this delicate and tender matter, as far as one can conjecture, I believe the chances are in your favor.”", "\"내가 아는 걸 자네한테 말해 준 거고, 다시 한번 말하지만 이 미묘하고 섬세한 문제에서, 짐작이 닿는 한, 내 생각엔 가능성이 자네 쪽에 있어.\""]], [["Levin dropped back in his chair; his face was pale.", "레빈은 의자에 등을 기댔다. 그의 얼굴은 창백했다."]], [["“But I would advise you to settle the thing as soon as may be,”", "\"하지만 이 일은 되도록 빨리 매듭짓는 게 좋겠어.\""], ["pursued Oblonsky, filling up his glass.", "오블론스키는 자기 잔을 채우며 말을 이었다."]], [["“No, thanks, I can’t drink any more,”", "\"아니, 됐어, 더는 못 마시겠어.\""], ["said Levin, pushing away his glass.", "레빈이 잔을 밀어내며 말했다."], ["“I shall be drunk....", "\"취하겠어……."], ["Come, tell me how are you getting on?”", "자, 자네 얘기나 해 봐, 요즘 어떻게 지내?\""], ["he went on, obviously anxious to change the conversation.", "그는 분명 화제를 바꾸고 싶어 하며 말을 이었다."]], [["“One word more: in any case I advise you to settle the question soon.", "\"한마디만 더. 어쨌든 그 문제는 빨리 결판을 짓는 게 좋겠어."], ["Tonight I don’t advise you to speak,”", "오늘 밤엔 말을 꺼내지 않는 게 좋고.\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch.", "스테판 아르카지치가 말했다."], ["“Go round tomorrow morning, make an offer in due form, and God bless you....”", "\"내일 아침에 찾아가서, 격식을 갖춰 청혼을 하게. 그러면 하느님의 가호가 있기를…….\""]], [["“Oh, do you still think of coming to me for some shooting?", "\"아 참, 자네 아직도 나한테 사냥하러 올 생각 있어?"], ["Come next spring, do,”", "내년 봄에 오게, 꼭.\""], ["said Levin.", "레빈이 말했다."]], [["Now his whole soul was full of remorse that he had begun this conversation with Stepan Arkadyevitch.", "이제 그의 온 영혼은 스테판 아르카지치와 이런 이야기를 시작한 데 대한 후회로 가득 찼다."], ["A feeling such as his was profaned by talk of the rivalry of some Petersburg officer, of the suppositions and the counsels of Stepan Arkadyevitch.", "그가 품은 것과 같은 감정은, 어느 페테르부르크 장교와의 경쟁이라느니, 스테판 아르카지치의 추측이며 조언이라느니 하는 이야기로 더럽혀지는 것이었다."]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch smiled.", "스테판 아르카지치는 미소를 지었다."], ["He knew what was passing in Levin’s soul.", "그는 레빈의 영혼 속에서 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지 알고 있었다."]], [["“I’ll come some day,”", "\"언젠가 가겠네.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“But women, my boy, they’re the pivot everything turns upon.", "\"한데 말이야, 여자란 모든 게 그걸 축으로 돌아가는 그런 거야."], ["Things are in a bad way with me, very bad.", "나는 형편이 안 좋아, 아주 안 좋지."], ["And it’s all through women.", "그게 전부 여자 때문이고."], ["Tell me frankly now,”", "자, 이제 솔직하게 말해 봐.\""], ["he pursued, picking up a cigar and keeping one hand on his glass; “give me your advice.”", "그는 시가를 집어 들고 한 손은 잔에 얹은 채 말을 이었다. \"나한테 조언 좀 해 줘.\""]], [["“Why, what is it?”", "\"뭔데, 무슨 일인데?\""]], [["“I’ll tell you.", "\"말해 줄게. 자네가 결혼을 했다고 치자."], ["Suppose you’re married, you love your wife, but you’re fascinated by another woman....”", "아내를 사랑해. 그런데 다른 여자한테 마음을 빼앗긴 거야…….\""]], [["“Excuse me, but I’m absolutely unable to comprehend how ...", "\"미안하지만, 난 도무지 이해가 안 돼, 어떻게……"], ["just as I can’t comprehend how I could now, after my dinner, go straight to a baker’s shop and steal a roll.”", "그건 마치, 지금 저녁을 먹고 나서 곧장 빵집에 가 둥근 빵을 훔칠 수 있다는 걸 이해 못 하는 것과 같아.\""]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch’s eyes sparkled more than usual.", "스테판 아르카지치의 눈이 평소보다 더 반짝였다."]], [["“Why not?", "\"왜 안 돼?"], ["A roll will sometimes smell so good one can’t resist it.”", "빵이란 게 때로 어찌나 좋은 냄새를 풍기는지, 도무지 못 참을 때가 있다고.\""]], [["“Himmlisch ist’s, wenn ich bezwungen\nMeine irdische Begier;", "\"내가 나의 지상의 욕망을 다스렸을 때, 그건 천상의 기쁨이라."]], [["Aber doch wenn’s nich gelungen\nHatt’ ich auch recht hübsch Plaisir!”", "그러나 만약 다스리지 못했다 해도, 나 또한 제법 멋진 즐거움을 누렸으리!\""]], [["As he said this, Stepan Arkadyevitch smiled subtly.", "이렇게 말하면서 스테판 아르카지치는 은근한 미소를 지었다."], ["Levin, too, could not help smiling.", "레빈도 웃지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["“Yes, but joking apart,”", "* 독일어로 된 시. 하인리히 하이네의 풍자시에서 인용된 구절로 알려져 있다."], ["resumed Stepan Arkadyevitch, “you must understand that the woman is a sweet, gentle loving creature, poor and lonely, and has sacrificed everything.", "\"그래, 하지만 농담은 접어 두고.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 다시 말을 이었다."], ["Now, when the thing’s done, don’t you see, can one possibly cast her off?", "\"자넨 이걸 알아야 해. 그 여자는 다정하고, 온화하고, 사랑이 넘치는 존재이고, 가난하고 외로운 데다, 모든 걸 희생했단 말이야."], ["Even supposing one parts from her, so as not to break up one’s family life, still, can one help feeling for her, setting her on her feet, softening her lot?”", "자, 일이 이미 벌어진 마당에, 알겠나, 어떻게 그 여자를 내칠 수가 있겠어? 설령 자기 가정을 깨뜨리지 않으려고 헤어진다 쳐도, 그래도 그 여자를 가엾이 여기고, 두 발로 서게 해 주고, 그 처지를 좀 누그러뜨려 주지 않을 수가 있겠나?\""]], [["“Well, you must excuse me there.", "\"글쎄, 그 점에선 날 좀 봐줘야겠어."], ["You know to me all women are divided into two classes ...", "자네도 알다시피 나한테 여자란 두 부류로 나뉘거든……. 아니, 적어도……"], ["at least no ...", "더 정확히 말하면, 여자가 있고, 그리고……"], ["truer to say: there are women and there are ...", "또…… 나는 매혹적인 타락한 존재라는 걸 본 적도 없고, 앞으로도 못 볼 거야."], ["I’ve never seen exquisite fallen beings, and I never shall see them, but such creatures as that painted Frenchwoman at the counter with the ringlets are vermin to my mind, and all fallen women are the same.”", "한데 저 계산대에 있는, 곱슬머리에 분칠한 프랑스 여자 같은 것들은 내가 보기엔 해충이야. 타락한 여자들은 다 마찬가지지.\""]], [["“But the Magdalen?”", "\"하지만 막달라 마리아는?\""]], [["“Ah, drop that!", "\"아, 그만둬!"], ["Christ would never have said those words if He had known how they would be abused.", "그리스도께서 그 말씀이 어떻게 악용될지 아셨다면, 결코 그런 말씀을 하지 않으셨을 거야."], ["Of all the Gospel those words are the only ones remembered.", "복음서를 통틀어, 사람들이 기억하는 건 그 말씀뿐이지."], ["However, I’m not saying so much what I think, as what I feel.", "어쨌든 난 내가 생각하는 걸 말하는 게 아니라, 내가 느끼는 걸 말하는 거야."], ["I have a loathing for fallen women.", "난 타락한 여자들이 혐오스러워."], ["You’re afraid of spiders, and I of these vermin.", "자넨 거미를 무서워하고, 난 그런 해충을 무서워하는 거지."], ["Most likely you’ve not made a study of spiders and don’t know their character; and so it is with me.”", "자넨 십중팔구 거미를 연구해 본 적이 없어서 그 본성을 모를 거야. 나도 그렇다고.\""]], [["“It’s very well for you to talk like that; it’s very much like that gentleman in Dickens who used to fling all difficult questions over his right shoulder.", "\"자네야 그렇게 말하면 그만이지. 그건 디킨스에 나오는 그 신사하고 아주 비슷해."], ["But to deny the facts is no answer.", "어려운 질문은 죄다 자기 오른쪽 어깨 너머로 휙 던져 버리던 그 사람 말이야."], ["What’s to be done—you tell me that, what’s to be done?", "하지만 사실을 부정하는 건 답이 못 돼."], ["Your wife gets older, while you’re full of life.", "그럼 어떻게 해야 하지—자네가 말해 봐, 어떻게 해야 하느냐고?"], ["Before you’ve time to look round, you feel that you can’t love your wife with love, however much you may esteem her.", "아내는 나이를 먹어 가는데, 자넨 생기가 넘쳐. 미처 둘러볼 새도 없이, 아무리 아내를 존중한다 해도 더는 사랑으로 그 사람을 사랑할 수 없다는 걸 느끼게 돼."], ["And then all at once love turns up, and you’re done for, done for,”", "그러다 별안간 사랑이 나타나면, 자넨 끝장나는 거야, 끝장나는 거라고.\""], ["Stepan Arkadyevitch said with weary despair.", "스테판 아르카지치가 지친 절망감으로 말했다."]], [["Levin half smiled.", "레빈은 살짝 미소를 지었다."]], [["“Yes, you’re done for,”", "\"그래, 끝장나는 거지.\""], ["resumed Oblonsky.", "오블론스키가 다시 말했다."], ["“But what’s to be done?”", "\"하지만 어떻게 해야 하느냐고?\""]], [["“Don’t steal rolls.”", "\"빵을 훔치지 마.\""]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch laughed outright.", "스테판 아르카지치는 소리 내어 웃음을 터뜨렸다."]], [["“Oh, moralist!", "\"오, 도덕군자 나셨네!"], ["But you must understand, there are two women; one insists only on her rights, and those rights are your love, which you can’t give her; and the other sacrifices everything for you and asks for nothing.", "하지만 자넨 이걸 알아야 해, 두 여자가 있어. 하나는 오직 자기 권리만 주장하는데, 그 권리란 게 바로 자네의 사랑이고, 자넨 그걸 줄 수가 없어."], ["What are you to do?", "다른 하나는 자넬 위해 모든 걸 희생하면서도 아무것도 바라지 않아."], ["How are you to act?", "그럼 어떻게 해야 하지?"], ["There’s a fearful tragedy in it.”", "어떻게 처신해야 하느냐고? 거기엔 무시무시한 비극이 있단 말이야.\""]], [["“If you care for my profession of faith as regards that, I’ll tell you that I don’t believe there was any tragedy about it.", "\"그 문제에 관해서 내 신앙 고백이 궁금하다면, 말해 주지. 난 거기에 무슨 비극이 있었다고 믿지 않아."], ["And this is why.", "그리고 그 까닭은 이래."], ["To my mind, love ...", "내가 보기에 사랑이란……"], ["both the sorts of love, which you remember Plato defines in his Banquet, served as the test of men.", "자네도 기억하겠지만, 플라톤이 『향연』에서 정의한 그 두 종류의 사랑 말이야, 그건 사람을 가려내는 시금석 노릇을 해."], ["Some men only understand one sort, and some only the other.", "어떤 사람은 한 종류만 이해하고, 또 어떤 사람은 다른 한 종류만 이해하지."], ["And those who only know the non-platonic love have no need to talk of tragedy.", "그리고 비(非)플라토닉한 사랑만 아는 사람들은 비극을 운운할 필요가 없어."], ["In such love there can be no sort of tragedy.", "그런 사랑엔 어떤 종류의 비극도 있을 수 없으니까."], ["‘I’m much obliged for the gratification, my humble respects’—that’s all the tragedy.", "'즐겁게 해 주셔서 정말 고맙습니다, 그럼 이만 실례합니다'—그게 비극의 전부야."], ["And in platonic love there can be no tragedy, because in that love all is clear and pure, because....”", "그리고 플라토닉한 사랑에도 비극이란 있을 수 없어. 왜냐하면 그 사랑 안에서는 모든 게 맑고 순수하니까, 왜냐하면…….\""]], [["At that instant Levin recollected his own sins and the inner conflict he had lived through.", "바로 그 순간 레빈은 자신의 죄와, 자기가 겪어 온 내면의 갈등을 떠올렸다."], ["And he added unexpectedly:", "그리고 뜻밖에도 이렇게 덧붙였다."]], [["“But perhaps you are right.", "\"하지만 어쩌면 자네 말이 옳을지도 몰라."], ["Very likely ...", "충분히 그럴 수 있지……"], ["I don’t know, I don’t know.”", "모르겠어, 나도 모르겠어.\""]], [["“It’s this, don’t you see,”", "\"이건 말이지, 알겠나.\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, “you’re very much all of a piece.", "스테판 아르카지치가 말했다. \"자넨 한 덩어리로 똘똘 뭉친 사람이야."], ["That’s your strong point and your failing.", "그게 자네의 강점이자 약점이지."], ["You have a character that’s all of a piece, and you want the whole of life to be of a piece too—but that’s not how it is.", "자넨 한 덩어리로 된 성격을 지녀서, 인생 전체도 한 덩어리로 되어 있길 바라는데—세상은 그렇게 돌아가질 않아."], ["You despise public official work because you want the reality to be invariably corresponding all the while with the aim—and that’s not how it is.", "자넨 공직 업무를 멸시하지. 현실이라는 게 늘 한결같이 목적에 딱 들어맞기를 바라니까—그런데 세상은 그렇지가 않거든."], ["You want a man’s work, too, always to have a defined aim, and love and family life always to be undivided—and that’s not how it is.", "자넨 사람의 일도 늘 분명한 목적을 가져야 하고, 사랑과 가정생활도 늘 하나로 나뉘지 않은 채여야 한다고 바라지만—세상은 그렇지가 않아."], ["All the variety, all the charm, all the beauty of life is made up of light and shadow.”", "인생의 온갖 다채로움, 온갖 매력, 온갖 아름다움은 빛과 그림자로 이루어지는 거야.\""]], [["Levin sighed and made no reply.", "레빈은 한숨을 쉬고 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았다."], ["He was thinking of his own affairs, and did not hear Oblonsky.", "그는 자기 자신의 일을 생각하고 있었고, 오블론스키의 말은 귀에 들어오지 않았다."]], [["And suddenly both of them felt that though they were friends, though they had been dining and drinking together, which should have drawn them closer, yet each was thinking only of his own affairs, and they had nothing to do with one another.", "그리고 문득 두 사람은, 자기들이 친구 사이이고, 함께 저녁을 먹고 술을 마셨으니 응당 더 가까워졌어야 하는데도, 저마다 오직 제 일만 생각하고 있을 뿐 서로 아무 상관이 없다는 것을 느꼈다."], ["Oblonsky had more than once experienced this extreme sense of aloofness, instead of intimacy, coming on after dinner, and he knew what to do in such cases.", "오블론스키는 만찬 뒤에 친밀감 대신 이런 지독한 거리감이 밀려드는 것을 여러 번 겪어 본 터라, 그럴 때 어떻게 해야 하는지 알고 있었다."]], [["“Bill!”", "\"계산서!\""], ["he called, and he went into the next room where he promptly came across an aide-de-camp of his acquaintance and dropped into conversation with him about an actress and her protector.", "그는 이렇게 외치고는 옆방으로 갔다. 거기서 곧 안면이 있는 시종무관과 마주쳐, 어느 여배우와 그 후원자에 관한 이야기에 빠져들었다."], ["And at once in the conversation with the aide-de-camp Oblonsky had a sense of relaxation and relief after the conversation with Levin, which always put him to too great a mental and spiritual strain.", "그리고 그 시종무관과 이야기를 나누자마자 오블론스키는, 늘 정신적으로나 영혼으로나 너무 큰 긴장을 강요하던 레빈과의 대화 뒤에 찾아온 느슨함과 안도감을 맛보았다."]], [["When the Tatar appeared with a bill for twenty-six roubles and odd kopecks, besides a tip for himself, Levin, who would another time have been horrified, like anyone from the country, at his share of fourteen roubles, did not notice it, paid, and set off homewards to dress and go to the Shtcherbatskys’ there to decide his fate.", "타타르인이 자기 몫의 팁 말고도 26루블 몇 코페이카짜리 계산서를 들고 나타났을 때, 평소 같으면 시골 사람이 다 그렇듯 자기 몫인 14루블에 기겁했을 레빈은, 그것을 알아차리지도 못한 채 값을 치르고는, 옷을 갈아입고 셰르바츠키 댁으로 가서 자신의 운명을 결정짓기 위해 집으로 향했다."]], [["# Chapter 12", "# Chapter 12"]], [["The young Princess Kitty Shtcherbatskaya was eighteen.", "젊은 공작 영애 키티 셰르바츠카야는 열여덟 살이었다."], ["It was the first winter that she had been out in the world.", "그녀가 사교계에 발을 들인 것은 이번 겨울이 처음이었다."], ["Her success in society had been greater than that of either of her elder sisters, and greater even than her mother had anticipated.", "사교계에서 거둔 성공은 두 언니 그 누구보다도 컸으며, 심지어 어머니가 기대했던 것보다도 더 컸다."], ["To say nothing of the young men who danced at the Moscow balls being almost all in love with Kitty, two serious suitors had already this first winter made their appearance: Levin, and immediately after his departure, Count Vronsky.", "모스크바 무도회에서 춤추던 젊은 남자들이 거의 모두 키티에게 반해 있었다는 것은 말할 것도 없고, 이 첫 겨울에 벌써 진지한 구혼자가 둘이나 나타났다. 레빈, 그리고 그가 떠나자마자 등장한 브론스키 백작이었다."]], [["Levin’s appearance at the beginning of the winter, his frequent visits, and evident love for Kitty, had led to the first serious conversations between Kitty’s parents as to her future, and to disputes between them.", "겨울 초입에 레빈이 나타나, 자주 찾아오며 키티를 향한 사랑을 분명히 드러내자, 키티의 부모는 처음으로 딸의 장래에 관해 진지하게 이야기를 나누게 되었고, 그러다 둘 사이에 다툼이 일었다."], ["The prince was on Levin’s side; he said he wished for nothing better for Kitty.", "공작은 레빈의 편이었다. 그는 키티에게 그보다 더 나은 상대를 바랄 게 없다고 말했다."], ["The princess for her part, going round the question in the manner peculiar to women, maintained that Kitty was too young, that Levin had done nothing to prove that he had serious intentions, that Kitty felt no great attraction to him, and other side issues; but she did not state the principal point, which was that she looked for a better match for her daughter, and that Levin was not to her liking, and she did not understand him.", "한편 공작 부인은 여자 특유의 방식으로 문제를 빙빙 돌려 가며, 키티가 너무 어리다느니, 레빈이 진지한 뜻을 품었다는 것을 입증할 만한 일을 하나도 한 게 없다느니, 키티가 그에게 별다른 호감을 느끼지 못한다느니 하는 이런저런 곁가지 이유를 늘어놓았다. 그러나 정작 핵심은 입에 올리지 않았으니, 그것은 그녀가 딸에게 더 좋은 혼처를 바라고 있으며, 레빈은 자기 마음에 들지 않고 또 그를 도무지 이해할 수 없다는 점이었다."], ["When Levin had abruptly departed, the princess was delighted, and said to her husband triumphantly: “You see I was right.”", "레빈이 돌연 떠나 버리자 공작 부인은 기뻐하며 의기양양하게 남편에게 말했다. \"거 봐요, 내 말이 맞았잖아요.\""], ["When Vronsky appeared on the scene, she was still more delighted, confirmed in her opinion that Kitty was to make not simply a good, but a brilliant match.", "브론스키가 등장하자 그녀는 더더욱 기뻐하며, 키티가 그저 좋은 정도가 아니라 눈부신 혼인을 하게 되리라는 자신의 견해를 굳혔다."]], [["In the mother’s eyes there could be no comparison between Vronsky and Levin.", "어머니의 눈에 브론스키와 레빈은 비교할 거리가 못 되었다."], ["She disliked in Levin his strange and uncompromising opinions and his shyness in society, founded, as she supposed, on his pride and his queer sort of life, as she considered it, absorbed in cattle and peasants.", "그녀는 레빈에게서 그의 괴상하고 타협을 모르는 견해며, 사교계에서의 수줍음을 못마땅해했는데, 그 수줍음은 그녀가 보기에 그의 자존심과, 가축이며 농민들에 파묻혀 사는—그녀가 보기에는—기이한 삶의 방식에서 비롯된 것이었다."], ["She did not very much like it that he, who was in love with her daughter, had kept coming to the house for six weeks, as though he were waiting for something, inspecting, as though he were afraid he might be doing them too great an honor by making an offer, and did not realize that a man, who continually visits at a house where there is a young unmarried girl, is bound to make his intentions clear.", "그녀는 그가 딸을 사랑하면서도 마치 무언가를 기다리기라도 하듯, 청혼을 하면 자기들에게 너무 큰 영광을 베푸는 게 아닌가 두려워하기라도 하듯 살피며 여섯 주 동안이나 집을 드나든 것이 그리 마음에 들지 않았고, 미혼의 처녀가 있는 집을 끊임없이 찾아오는 남자라면 자기 뜻을 분명히 밝혀야 마땅하다는 것을 그가 깨닫지 못하는 것도 못마땅했다."], ["And suddenly, without doing so, he disappeared.", "그런데 그는 그렇게 하지도 않은 채 별안간 종적을 감춰 버린 것이다."], ["“It’s as well he’s not attractive enough for Kitty to have fallen in love with him,”", "\"키티가 반할 만큼 매력적이지 않았던 게 차라리 다행이지.\""], ["thought the mother.", "어머니는 그렇게 생각했다."]], [["Vronsky satisfied all the mother’s desires.", "브론스키는 어머니의 온갖 바람을 만족시켜 주었다."], ["Very wealthy, clever, of aristocratic family, on the highroad to a brilliant career in the army and at court, and a fascinating man.", "대단한 부자에다, 영리하고, 귀족 가문 출신이며, 군에서나 궁정에서나 눈부신 출셋길로 나아가고 있었고, 게다가 매력적인 남자였다."], ["Nothing better could be wished for.", "더 바랄 게 없었다."]], [["Vronsky openly flirted with Kitty at balls, danced with her, and came continually to the house, consequently there could be no doubt of the seriousness of his intentions.", "브론스키는 무도회에서 키티에게 드러내 놓고 추파를 던지며 함께 춤을 추었고 끊임없이 집을 찾아왔으니, 그의 의도가 진지하다는 것은 의심할 여지가 없었다."], ["But, in spite of that, the mother had spent the whole of that winter in a state of terrible anxiety and agitation.", "그러나 그럼에도 어머니는 그 겨울 내내 끔찍한 불안과 동요 속에서 지냈다."]], [["Princess Shtcherbatskaya had herself been married thirty years ago, her aunt arranging the match.", "셰르바츠카야 공작 부인 자신이 결혼한 것은 삼십 년 전 일로, 그녀의 이모가 혼사를 주선했다."], ["Her husband, about whom everything was well known beforehand, had come, looked at his future bride, and been looked at.", "모든 것이 미리 소상히 알려진 남편이 찾아와, 미래의 신부를 보았고, 또 신부의 눈에 비쳤다."], ["The matchmaking aunt had ascertained and communicated their mutual impression.", "중매를 맡은 이모가 두 사람의 상호 인상을 확인하여 전해 주었다."], ["That impression had been favorable.", "그 인상은 호의적이었다."], ["Afterwards, on a day fixed beforehand, the expected offer was made to her parents, and accepted.", "그 후 미리 정해 둔 날에 기다리던 청혼이 부모에게 전해졌고, 받아들여졌다."], ["All had passed very simply and easily.", "모든 것이 아주 간단하고 수월하게 흘러갔다."], ["So it seemed, at least, to the princess.", "적어도 공작 부인에게는 그렇게 여겨졌다."], ["But over her own daughters she had felt how far from simple and easy is the business, apparently so commonplace, of marrying off one’s daughters.", "그러나 자기 딸들 차례가 되자, 겉보기에는 그토록 흔해 빠진 일인 딸을 시집보내는 일이 얼마나 간단하지도 수월하지도 않은 일인지 절감하게 되었다."], ["The panics that had been lived through, the thoughts that had been brooded over, the money that had been wasted, and the disputes with her husband over marrying the two elder girls, Darya and Natalia!", "큰딸 둘, 다리아와 나탈리아를 시집보내며 견뎌 낸 그 숱한 공포며, 골똘히 곱씹던 그 온갖 생각이며, 허비한 돈이며, 남편과 벌인 다툼이라니!"], ["Now, since the youngest had come out, she was going through the same terrors, the same doubts, and still more violent quarrels with her husband than she had over the elder girls.", "이제 막내딸이 사교계에 나오자 그녀는 똑같은 두려움과 똑같은 의혹을, 게다가 언니들 때보다 한층 더 격렬한 부부 싸움을 다시 겪고 있었다."], ["The old prince, like all fathers indeed, was exceedingly punctilious on the score of the honor and reputation of his daughters.", "노공작은 사실 모든 아버지가 그렇듯 딸들의 명예와 평판 문제에 지나치리만큼 까다로웠다."], ["He was irrationally jealous over his daughters, especially over Kitty, who was his favorite.", "그는 딸들을, 특히 그가 가장 아끼던 키티를 두고 분별없이 질투했다."], ["At every turn he had scenes with the princess for compromising her daughter.", "그는 사사건건 딸의 체면을 손상시킨다며 공작 부인과 한바탕씩 했다."], ["The princess had grown accustomed to this already with her other daughters, but now she felt that there was more ground for the prince’s touchiness.", "다른 딸들 때부터 이미 이런 일에 익숙해진 공작 부인이었지만, 이제는 공작이 신경을 곤두세우는 데에 더 그럴 만한 까닭이 있다고 느꼈다."], ["She saw that of late years much was changed in the manners of society, that a mother’s duties had become still more difficult.", "그녀는 최근 몇 년 사이 사교계의 풍속이 많이 달라졌으며, 어머니의 의무가 한층 더 어려워졌다는 것을 알았다."], ["She saw that girls of Kitty’s age formed some sort of clubs, went to some sort of lectures, mixed freely in men’s society; drove about the streets alone, many of them did not curtsey, and, what was the most important thing, all the girls were firmly convinced that to choose their husbands was their own affair, and not their parents’.", "키티 또래의 처녀들이 무슨 모임 같은 것을 만들고, 무슨 강연 같은 데를 다니며, 남자들과 거리낌 없이 어울리고, 혼자서 거리를 마차로 돌아다니는가 하면, 그중 다수가 무릎을 굽혀 인사도 하지 않았다. 그리고 무엇보다 중요한 것은, 처녀들이 하나같이 남편을 고르는 일은 부모가 아니라 자기들 소관이라고 굳게 믿고 있다는 점이었다."], ["“Marriages aren’t made nowadays as they used to be,”", "\"요즘은 결혼이 옛날처럼 이루어지지 않아요.\""], ["was thought and said by all these young girls, and even by their elders.", "이 젊은 처녀들 모두가, 심지어 그들의 윗사람들까지도 그렇게 생각하고 또 말했다."], ["But how marriages were made now, the princess could not learn from anyone.", "그러나 지금은 결혼이 어떻게 이루어지는지, 공작 부인은 누구에게서도 알아낼 수가 없었다."], ["The French fashion—of the parents arranging their children’s future—was not accepted; it was condemned.", "부모가 자식의 장래를 정해 주는 프랑스식은 받아들여지지 않았고, 오히려 비난받았다."], ["The English fashion of the complete independence of girls was also not accepted, and not possible in Russian society.", "처녀들의 완전한 독립이라는 영국식 또한 받아들여지지 않았고, 러시아 사교계에서는 가능하지도 않았다."], ["The Russian fashion of matchmaking by the offices of intermediate persons was for some reason considered unseemly; it was ridiculed by everyone, and by the princess herself.", "중간에 사람을 두어 혼사를 맺는 러시아식은 어찌 된 일인지 점잖지 못한 것으로 여겨졌으니, 모든 사람이, 또 공작 부인 자신도 그것을 비웃었다."], ["But how girls were to be married, and how parents were to marry them, no one knew.", "그러나 처녀들을 어떻게 시집보내야 하는지, 부모가 그들을 어떻게 결혼시켜야 하는지는 아무도 알지 못했다."], ["Everyone with whom the princess had chanced to discuss the matter said the same thing: “Mercy on us, it’s high time in our day to cast off all that old-fashioned business.", "공작 부인이 어쩌다 이 문제를 의논하게 된 사람마다 똑같은 말을 했다. \"이런, 세상에, 우리 시대에는 그 케케묵은 일일랑 다 던져 버릴 때도 됐지요."], ["It’s the young people have to marry; and not their parents; and so we ought to leave the young people to arrange it as they choose.”", "결혼은 젊은 사람들이 하는 거지 부모가 하는 게 아니니까요. 그러니 젊은 사람들이 알아서 정하도록 내버려 둬야지요.\""], ["It was very easy for anyone to say that who had no daughters, but the princess realized that in the process of getting to know each other, her daughter might fall in love, and fall in love with someone who did not care to marry her or who was quite unfit to be her husband.", "딸이 없는 사람이야 그렇게 말하기가 매우 쉬웠지만, 공작 부인은 서로 알아 가는 과정에서 딸이 사랑에 빠질 수도 있으며, 그것도 딸과 결혼할 마음이 없거나 도무지 남편감으로는 어울리지 않는 사람에게 빠질 수도 있다는 것을 잘 알았다."], ["And, however much it was instilled into the princess that in our times young people ought to arrange their lives for themselves, she was unable to believe it, just as she would have been unable to believe that, at any time whatever, the most suitable playthings for children five years old ought to be loaded pistols.", "그리고 요즘 같은 시대에는 젊은이들이 자기 삶을 스스로 꾸려야 한다는 말이 아무리 공작 부인의 머리에 주입되어도, 그녀는 그것을 믿을 수가 없었다. 마치 어느 시대를 막론하고 다섯 살배기 아이에게 가장 알맞은 장난감이 장전된 권총이라는 말을 믿을 수 없었던 것과 똑같이."], ["And so the princess was more uneasy over Kitty than she had been over her elder sisters.", "그래서 공작 부인은 언니들 때보다 키티를 두고 더 불안해했다."]], [["Now she was afraid that Vronsky might confine himself to simply flirting with her daughter.", "이제 그녀는 브론스키가 딸에게 그저 추파를 던지는 데서 그치지나 않을까 두려웠다."], ["She saw that her daughter was in love with him, but tried to comfort herself with the thought that he was an honorable man, and would not do this.", "그녀는 딸이 그를 사랑하고 있음을 알았지만, 그가 명예를 아는 사람이니 그런 짓은 하지 않으리라는 생각으로 스스로를 위로하려 했다."], ["But at the same time she knew how easy it is, with the freedom of manners of today, to turn a girl’s head, and how lightly men generally regard such a crime.", "그러나 동시에 그녀는 오늘날의 자유로운 풍속 속에서 처녀의 마음을 흔들어 놓기가 얼마나 쉬운지, 또 남자들이 흔히 그런 죄를 얼마나 가볍게 여기는지도 알았다."], ["The week before, Kitty had told her mother of a conversation she had with Vronsky during a mazurka.", "일주일 전에 키티는 마주르카를 추는 동안 브론스키와 나눈 대화를 어머니에게 들려준 적이 있었다."], ["This conversation had partly reassured the princess; but perfectly at ease she could not be.", "그 대화는 공작 부인을 어느 정도 안심시켜 주었지만, 그렇다고 완전히 마음을 놓을 수는 없었다."], ["Vronsky had told Kitty that both he and his brother were so used to obeying their mother that they never made up their minds to any important undertaking without consulting her.", "브론스키는 키티에게, 자기와 형은 어머니에게 순종하는 데 워낙 익숙해서 어떤 중요한 일도 어머니와 상의하지 않고는 결심한 적이 없다고 말했던 것이다."], ["“And just now, I am impatiently awaiting my mother’s arrival from Petersburg, as peculiarly fortunate,”", "\"그래서 지금 저는 어머니가 페테르부르크에서 오시기를, 유독 다행스러운 일로 여기며 애타게 기다리고 있답니다.\""], ["he told her.", "그는 그녀에게 그렇게 말했다."]], [["Kitty had repeated this without attaching any significance to the words.", "키티는 그 말에 아무 의미도 두지 않고 그대로 옮겼다."], ["But her mother saw them in a different light.", "그러나 어머니는 그것을 다른 시각으로 보았다."], ["She knew that the old lady was expected from day to day, that she would be pleased at her son’s choice, and she felt it strange that he should not make his offer through fear of vexing his mother.", "그녀는 노부인이 하루하루 오기를 기다리던 참이며, 부인이 아들의 선택을 기뻐하리라는 것을 알았기에, 그가 어머니의 심기를 거스를까 두려워 청혼을 하지 못한다는 게 이상하게 느껴졌다."], ["However, she was so anxious for the marriage itself, and still more for relief from her fears, that she believed it was so.", "하지만 그녀는 그 결혼 자체를, 그리고 그보다 더 자신의 두려움에서 벗어나기를 너무도 간절히 바란 나머지, 정말 그러하리라고 믿어 버렸다."], ["Bitter as it was for the princess to see the unhappiness of her eldest daughter, Dolly, on the point of leaving her husband, her anxiety over the decision of her youngest daughter’s fate engrossed all her feelings.", "남편을 떠나려는 지경에 이른 큰딸 돌리의 불행을 지켜보는 일이 공작 부인에게 아무리 쓰라렸어도, 막내딸의 운명이 어떻게 결정되느냐에 대한 불안이 그녀의 온 마음을 사로잡고 있었다."], ["Today, with Levin’s reappearance, a fresh source of anxiety arose.", "오늘 레빈이 다시 나타나면서 새로운 불안의 씨앗이 돋아났다."], ["She was afraid that her daughter, who had at one time, as she fancied, a feeling for Levin, might, from extreme sense of honor, refuse Vronsky, and that Levin’s arrival might generally complicate and delay the affair so near being concluded.", "그녀는 한때, 그녀 생각으로는, 레빈에게 마음이 있었던 딸이 지나친 명예심 때문에 브론스키를 거절하지나 않을까, 또 레빈의 등장이 거의 매듭지어진 일을 전반적으로 복잡하게 만들고 지연시키지나 않을까 두려웠다."]], [["“Why, has he been here long?”", "\"그래, 그 사람 온 지 오래됐니?\""], ["the princess asked about Levin, as they returned home.", "집으로 돌아오는 길에 공작 부인이 레빈에 관해 물었다."]], [["“He came today, mamma.”", "\"오늘 왔어요, 어머니.\""]], [["“There’s one thing I want to say....”", "\"한 가지 말해 두고 싶은 게…….\""], ["began the princess, and from her serious and alert face, Kitty guessed what it would be.", "공작 부인이 입을 떼자, 진지하고 빈틈없는 어머니의 얼굴에서 키티는 그것이 무슨 말일지 짐작했다."]], [["“Mamma,”", "\"어머니,\""], ["she said, flushing hotly and turning quickly to her, “please, please don’t say anything about that.", "그녀는 얼굴이 화끈 달아오르며 어머니에게 휙 돌아서서 말했다. \"제발, 제발 그 이야기는 아무 말도 마세요."], ["I know, I know all about it.”", "알아요, 다 알고 있어요.\""]], [["She wished for what her mother wished for, but the motives of her mother’s wishes wounded her.", "그녀는 어머니가 바라는 것을 똑같이 바랐지만, 어머니가 그것을 바라는 동기가 그녀의 마음에 상처를 주었다."]], [["“I only want to say that to raise hopes....”", "\"그저 괜한 희망을 품게 하는 건…….\""]], [["“Mamma, darling, for goodness’ sake, don’t talk about it.", "\"어머니, 제발요, 부탁이에요, 그 얘기는 하지 마세요."], ["It’s so horrible to talk about it.”", "그런 얘기를 입에 올리는 게 너무 끔찍해요.\""]], [["“I won’t,”", "\"안 할게.\""], ["said her mother, seeing the tears in her daughter’s eyes; “but one thing, my love; you promised me you would have no secrets from me.", "어머니는 딸의 눈에 맺힌 눈물을 보고 말했다. \"하지만 한 가지만, 얘야."], ["You won’t?”", "너 나한테 아무것도 숨기지 않겠다고 약속했지. 안 그러겠지?\""]], [["“Never, mamma, none,”", "\"절대로요, 어머니, 아무것도요.\""], ["answered Kitty, flushing a little, and looking her mother straight in the face, “but there’s no use in my telling you anything, and I ...", "키티는 살짝 얼굴을 붉히며 어머니의 얼굴을 똑바로 바라보고 대답했다. \"하지만 어머니께 말씀드릴 게 아무것도 없어요."], ["I ...", "그리고 저는……"], ["if I wanted to, I don’t know what to say or how....", "저는…… 설령 말하고 싶어도, 뭐라고 해야 할지, 또 어떻게 해야 할지 모르겠어요……"], ["I don’t know....”", "정말 모르겠어요…….\""]], [["“No, she could not tell an untruth with those eyes,”", "'아니, 저 눈으로 거짓말을 할 수는 없지.'"], ["thought the mother, smiling at her agitation and happiness.", "어머니는 딸의 동요와 행복을 보며 미소 지으면서 생각했다."], ["The princess smiled that what was taking place just now in her soul seemed to the poor child so immense and so important.", "공작 부인은 지금 이 가엾은 아이의 마음속에서 벌어지고 있는 일이 그 아이에게는 그토록 엄청나고 그토록 중대하게 여겨진다는 것에 미소를 지었다."]], [["# Chapter 13", "# Chapter 13"]], [["After dinner, and till the beginning of the evening, Kitty was feeling a sensation akin to the sensation of a young man before a battle.", "만찬이 끝나고 저녁이 시작될 때까지, 키티는 전투를 앞둔 젊은 병사가 느낄 법한 감각과 비슷한 것을 느꼈다."], ["Her heart throbbed violently, and her thoughts would not rest on anything.", "심장이 격렬하게 두근거렸고, 어떤 것에도 생각이 머물러 있지 못했다."]], [["She felt that this evening, when they would both meet for the first time, would be a turning point in her life.", "그녀는 두 사람을 처음으로 한자리에서 만나게 될 오늘 저녁이 자기 인생의 전환점이 되리라고 느꼈다."], ["And she was continually picturing them to herself, at one moment each separately, and then both together.", "그리고 그녀는 끊임없이 두 사람을 마음속에 그려 보았다. 어느 순간엔 각자 따로따로, 그러다 또 둘을 함께."], ["When she mused on the past, she dwelt with pleasure, with tenderness, on the memories of her relations with Levin.", "지난날을 떠올릴 때면, 그녀는 즐겁게, 다정하게, 레빈과의 관계에 얽힌 기억들에 잠겼다. 어린 시절의 추억이며, 레빈과 죽은 오빠 사이의 우정은 그와의 관계에 특별한 시적 정취를 더해 주었다."], ["The memories of childhood and of Levin’s friendship with her dead brother gave a special poetic charm to her relations with him.", "그녀가 확신하고 있는 그의 사랑은 그녀에게 흐뭇하고 기쁜 것이었다. 레빈을 생각하는 일은 그녀에게 즐거웠다."], ["His love for her, of which she felt certain, was flattering and delightful to her; and it was pleasant for her to think of Levin.", "그런데 브론스키에 대한 기억에는 늘 어떤 어색함이 끼어들었다. 그가 더없이 교양 있고 침착한 사람이었는데도, 마치 어딘가 거짓된 음이 섞여 있는 것 같았다."], ["In her memories of Vronsky there always entered a certain element of awkwardness, though he was in the highest degree well-bred and at ease, as though there were some false note—not in Vronsky, he was very simple and nice, but in herself, while with Levin she felt perfectly simple and clear.", "브론스키에게 있는 것이 아니라—그는 무척 소박하고 친절했다—그녀 자신 안에 있는 것이었다. 반면 레빈과 함께 있을 때 그녀는 더없이 소박하고 또렷한 자신을 느꼈다."], ["But, on the other hand, directly she thought of the future with Vronsky, there arose before her a perspective of brilliant happiness; with Levin the future seemed misty.", "그러나 또 한편으로, 브론스키와의 미래를 생각하면 그녀 앞에는 눈부신 행복의 전경이 펼쳐졌다. 레빈과의 미래는 안개에 싸인 듯했다."]], [["When she went upstairs to dress, and looked into the looking-glass, she noticed with joy that it was one of her good days, and that she was in complete possession of all her forces,—she needed this so for what lay before her: she was conscious of external composure and free grace in her movements.", "옷을 갈아입으러 위층으로 올라가 거울을 들여다본 그녀는, 오늘이 자기 모습이 좋은 날 가운데 하나이며, 자신의 모든 힘을 온전히 갖추고 있다는 것을 기쁘게 알아챘다. 앞에 놓인 일을 위해 이런 상태가 그토록 필요했던 것이다. 그녀는 겉으로 드러나는 차분함과 거침없이 우아한 몸놀림을 의식하고 있었다."]], [["At half-past seven she had only just gone down into the drawing-room, when the footman announced, “Konstantin Dmitrievitch Levin.”", "일곱 시 반에 응접실로 막 내려간 그녀에게, 하인이 \"콘스탄틴 드미트리예비치 레빈\"이라고 알렸다."], ["The princess was still in her room, and the prince had not come in.", "공작 부인은 아직 자기 방에 있었고, 공작은 아직 들어오지 않았다."], ["“So it is to be,”", "'드디어 올 것이 왔구나.'"], ["thought Kitty, and all the blood seemed to rush to her heart.", "키티는 생각했고, 온몸의 피가 심장으로 쏠리는 것 같았다."], ["She was horrified at her paleness, as she glanced into the looking-glass.", "거울을 흘끗 들여다본 그녀는 자기 얼굴이 핼쑥한 데에 소스라쳤다."], ["At that moment she knew beyond doubt that he had come early on purpose to find her alone and to make her an offer.", "그 순간 그녀는, 그가 자기를 혼자 만나서 청혼하려고 일부러 일찍 왔다는 것을 의심할 여지 없이 알아차렸다."], ["And only then for the first time the whole thing presented itself in a new, different aspect; only then she realized that the question did not affect her only—with whom she would be happy, and whom she loved—but that she would have that moment to wound a man whom she liked.", "그리고 바로 그때서야 비로소 이 모든 일이 새롭고 다른 면모로 그녀 앞에 떠올랐다. 그제야 그녀는 이 문제가 자기에게만, 그러니까 누구와 함께 행복할지, 누구를 사랑하는지의 문제로만 그치지 않으며, 자기가 좋아하는 한 남자에게 이 순간 상처를 주어야 한다는 것을 깨달았다."], ["And to wound him cruelly. What for?", "그것도 잔인하게 상처를 주어야 한다는 것을."], ["Because he, dear fellow, loved her, was in love with her.", "무엇 때문에? 그 사람이, 그 다정한 사람이 그녀를 사랑하기 때문에, 그녀에게 마음을 빼앗겼기 때문에."], ["But there was no help for it, so it must be, so it would have to be.", "하지만 어쩔 도리가 없었고, 그래야만 했으며, 그렇게 될 수밖에 없었다."]], [["“My God!", "'맙소사!"], ["shall I myself really have to say it to him?”", "내가 정말 직접 그 사람한테 말해야 하는 거야?'"], ["she thought.", "그녀는 생각했다."], ["“Can I tell him I don’t love him? That will be a lie.", "'당신을 사랑하지 않는다고 그 사람한테 말할 수 있을까?"], ["What am I to say to him?", "그건 거짓말이지."], ["That I love someone else?", "대체 뭐라고 말해야 하지?"], ["No, that’s impossible.", "다른 사람을 사랑한다고?"], ["I’m going away, I’m going away.”", "아니, 그건 안 돼. 가야겠어, 가야겠어.'"]], [["She had reached the door, when she heard his step.", "그녀가 문 앞에 이르렀을 때, 그의 발소리가 들렸다. '안 돼!"], ["“No! it’s not honest.", "이건 정직하지 못해."], ["What have I to be afraid of?", "내가 뭘 두려워하는 거지?"], ["I have done nothing wrong.", "난 잘못한 게 없어."], ["What is to be, will be!", "될 일은 되겠지!"], ["I’ll tell the truth.", "사실대로 말하자."], ["And with him one can’t be ill at ease.", "그리고 저 사람 앞에서는 거북해할 까닭이 없어."], ["Here he is,”", "저기 오는구나.'"], ["she said to herself, seeing his powerful, shy figure, with his shining eyes fixed on her.", "그녀는 빛나는 눈으로 자기를 응시하는, 다부지고 수줍은 그의 모습을 보며 혼잣말을 했다."], ["She looked straight into his face, as though imploring him to spare her, and gave her hand.", "그녀는 부디 자기를 봐 달라고 애원하듯 그의 얼굴을 똑바로 들여다보며 손을 내밀었다."]], [["“It’s not time yet; I think I’m too early,”", "\"아직 시간이 안 됐죠. 제가 너무 일찍 온 것 같네요.\""], ["he said glancing round the empty drawing-room.", "그가 텅 빈 응접실을 둘러보며 말했다."], ["When he saw that his expectations were realized, that there was nothing to prevent him from speaking, his face became gloomy.", "자기 기대가 들어맞았다는 것을, 그러니까 말을 꺼내는 데에 아무런 방해도 없다는 것을 알아챘을 때, 그의 얼굴이 어두워졌다."]], [["“Oh, no,”", "\"아, 아니에요.\""], ["said Kitty, and sat down at the table.", "키티가 말하며 탁자 앞에 앉았다."]], [["“But this was just what I wanted, to find you alone,”", "\"하지만 저는 바로 이걸, 당신을 혼자 만나기를 바랐어요.\""], ["he began, not sitting down, and not looking at her, so as not to lose courage.", "그는 용기를 잃지 않으려고 앉지도 않고, 그녀를 보지도 않은 채 말을 꺼냈다."]], [["“Mamma will be down directly.", "\"엄마가 곧 내려오실 거예요."], ["She was very much tired....", "너무 피곤해하셔서……"], ["Yesterday....”", "어제……\""]], [["She talked on, not knowing what her lips were uttering, and not taking her supplicating and caressing eyes off him.", "그녀는 자기 입에서 무슨 말이 나오는지도 모른 채 계속 지껄였고, 애원하듯 어루만지는 듯한 눈길을 그에게서 떼지 못했다."]], [["He glanced at her; she blushed, and ceased speaking.", "그가 그녀를 흘끗 보았다. 그녀는 얼굴을 붉히며 말을 멈췄다."]], [["“I told you I did not know whether I should be here long ...", "\"제가 여기 오래 머물지 어떨지 모르겠다고 말씀드렸죠……"], ["that it depended on you....”", "그게 당신에게 달려 있다고……\""]], [["She dropped her head lower and lower, not knowing herself what answer she should make to what was coming.", "그녀는 다가오는 말에 자기가 무슨 대답을 해야 할지도 모른 채, 점점 더 고개를 떨구었다."]], [["“That it depended on you,”", "\"그게 당신에게 달려 있다고요.\""], ["he repeated.", "그가 되풀이했다."], ["“I meant to say ...", "\"제가 하려던 말은……"], ["I meant to say ...", "제가 하려던 말은……"], ["I came for this ...", "저는 이걸 위해 왔어요……"], ["to be my wife!”", "제 아내가 되어 달라고요!\""], ["he brought out, not knowing what he was saying; but feeling that the most terrible thing was said, he stopped short and looked at her....", "그는 자기가 무슨 말을 하는지도 모른 채 내뱉었다. 하지만 가장 무서운 말은 다 했다고 느끼며 입을 다물고 그녀를 바라보았다."]], [["She was breathing heavily, not looking at him.", "그녀는 그를 보지 않은 채 가쁘게 숨을 쉬고 있었다."], ["She was feeling ecstasy.", "그녀는 황홀감을 느끼고 있었다."], ["Her soul was flooded with happiness.", "그녀의 영혼은 행복으로 흘러넘쳤다."], ["She had never anticipated that the utterance of love would produce such a powerful effect on her.", "사랑의 고백이 자기에게 이토록 강렬한 작용을 일으키리라고는 결코 예상하지 못했다."], ["But it lasted only an instant.", "하지만 그것은 한순간뿐이었다."], ["She remembered Vronsky.", "그녀는 브론스키를 떠올렸다."], ["She lifted her clear, truthful eyes, and seeing his desperate face, she answered hastily:", "그녀는 맑고 진실한 눈을 들어, 절망에 찬 그의 얼굴을 보고는 황급히 대답했다."]], [["“That cannot be ...", "\"그건 안 돼요……"], ["forgive me.”", "용서하세요.\""]], [["A moment ago, and how close she had been to him, of what importance in his life!", "조금 전만 해도 그녀는 그에게 얼마나 가까웠던가, 그의 삶에서 얼마나 소중했던가!"], ["And how aloof and remote from him she had become now!", "그런데 지금 그녀는 그에게서 얼마나 멀어지고 동떨어진 존재가 되어 버렸는가!"]], [["“It was bound to be so,”", "\"그렇게 될 수밖에 없었던 거죠.\""], ["he said, not looking at her.", "그가 그녀를 보지 않은 채 말했다."]], [["He bowed, and was meaning to retreat.", "그는 고개를 숙이고는 물러나려 했다."]], [["# Chapter 14", "# Chapter 14"]], [["But at that very moment the princess came in.", "바로 그 순간 공작 부인이 들어왔다."], ["There was a look of horror on her face when she saw them alone, and their disturbed faces.", "둘만 있는 모습과 흐트러진 두 사람의 얼굴을 본 그녀의 얼굴에는 공포의 빛이 어렸다."], ["Levin bowed to her, and said nothing.", "레빈은 그녀에게 인사를 하고는 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["Kitty did not speak nor lift her eyes.", "키티도 입을 열지 않았고 눈을 들지도 않았다."], ["“Thank God, she has refused him,”", "'다행이야, 거절했구나.'"], ["thought the mother, and her face lighted up with the habitual smile with which she greeted her guests on Thursdays.", "어머니는 그렇게 생각했고, 그녀의 얼굴은 목요일마다 손님들을 맞을 때 짓던 그 익숙한 미소로 환하게 밝아졌다."], ["She sat down and began questioning Levin about his life in the country.", "그녀는 자리에 앉아 레빈에게 시골 생활에 관해 이것저것 묻기 시작했다."], ["He sat down again, waiting for other visitors to arrive, in order to retreat unnoticed.", "그는 눈에 띄지 않게 물러날 작정으로, 다른 손님들이 올 때까지 기다리며 다시 앉았다."]], [["Five minutes later there came in a friend of Kitty’s, married the preceding winter, Countess Nordston.", "오 분쯤 지나자 지난겨울에 결혼한 키티의 친구, 노르드스톤 백작 부인이 들어왔다."]], [["She was a thin, sallow, sickly, and nervous woman, with brilliant black eyes.", "그녀는 야위고 누런 안색에 병약하고 신경질적인 여자였으며, 반짝이는 검은 눈을 가지고 있었다."], ["She was fond of Kitty, and her affection for her showed itself, as the affection of married women for girls always does, in the desire to make a match for Kitty after her own ideal of married happiness; she wanted her to marry Vronsky.", "그녀는 키티를 좋아했고, 결혼한 여자들이 처녀들에게 보이는 애정이 늘 그렇듯이, 그녀의 애정 역시 자신이 그리는 결혼 행복의 이상에 맞추어 키티의 짝을 지어 주고 싶은 욕망으로 드러났다. 그녀는 키티가 브론스키와 결혼하기를 바랐다."], ["Levin she had often met at the Shtcherbatskys’ early in the winter, and she had always disliked him.", "레빈은 초겨울에 쉬체르바츠키 집안에서 자주 마주쳤는데, 그녀는 늘 그를 싫어했다."], ["Her invariable and favorite pursuit, when they met, consisted in making fun of him.", "그와 마주칠 때면 그녀가 변함없이 즐겨 하던 일은 그를 놀려 대는 것이었다."]], [["“I do like it when he looks down at me from the height of his grandeur, or breaks off his learned conversation with me because I’m a fool, or is condescending to me.", "\"나는 그 사람이 자기 위대함의 높이에서 나를 내려다볼 때나, 내가 바보라서 학식 있는 대화를 도중에 끊어 버릴 때나, 나한테 잘난 척 인심 쓰듯 굴 때가 정말 좋아요. 그게 그렇게 좋다니까요."], ["I like that so; to see him condescending!", "그 사람이 잘난 척하는 꼴을 보는 게 말예요!"], ["I am so glad he can’t bear me,”", "그가 나를 못 견뎌 하니 얼마나 기쁜지 몰라요.\""], ["she used to say of him.", "그녀는 그에 관해 그렇게 말하곤 했다."]], [["She was right, for Levin actually could not bear her, and despised her for what she was proud of and regarded as a fine characteristic—her nervousness, her delicate contempt and indifference for everything coarse and earthly.", "그녀가 옳았다. 레빈은 실제로 그녀를 견딜 수 없었고, 그녀가 자랑스러워하며 훌륭한 특징이라고 여기는 것들—그녀의 신경질, 거칠고 세속적인 모든 것에 대한 그녀의 섬세한 경멸과 무관심—때문에 그녀를 경멸했다."]], [["The Countess Nordston and Levin got into that relation with one another not seldom seen in society, when two persons, who remain externally on friendly terms, despise each other to such a degree that they cannot even take each other seriously, and cannot even be offended by each other.", "노르드스톤 백작 부인과 레빈은 사교계에서 드물지 않게 보이는 그런 관계, 곧 겉으로는 우호적인 사이를 유지하는 두 사람이 서로를 너무나도 경멸한 나머지 상대를 진지하게 대할 수조차 없고 서로에게 화를 낼 수조차 없는 그런 관계에 빠져 있었다."]], [["The Countess Nordston pounced upon Levin at once.", "노르드스톤 백작 부인은 곧장 레빈에게 달려들었다."]], [["“Ah, Konstantin Dmitrievitch!", "\"아, 콘스탄틴 드미트리치!"], ["So you’ve come back to our corrupt Babylon,”", "이렇게 우리 타락한 바빌론으로 돌아오셨군요.\""], ["she said, giving him her tiny, yellow hand, and recalling what he had chanced to say early in the winter, that Moscow was a Babylon.", "그녀는 작고 누런 손을 그에게 내밀며, 그가 초겨울에 모스크바는 바빌론이라고 우연히 했던 말을 떠올리게 하며 말했다."], ["“Come, is Babylon reformed, or have you degenerated?”", "\"어디, 바빌론이 개혁되었나요, 아니면 당신이 타락한 건가요?\""], ["she added, glancing with a simper at Kitty.", "그녀는 키티를 향해 짐짓 웃는 얼굴로 흘끗 보며 덧붙였다."]], [["“It’s very flattering for me, countess, that you remember my words so well,”", "\"제 말을 그렇게 잘 기억해 주시다니 저로서는 무척 영광입니다, 백작 부인.\""], ["responded Levin, who had succeeded in recovering his composure, and at once from habit dropped into his tone of joking hostility to the Countess Nordston.", "어느새 평정을 되찾은 레빈이 대답했고, 곧 습관처럼 노르드스톤 백작 부인을 향한 농담 섞인 적대의 어조로 돌아갔다."], ["“They must certainly make a great impression on you.”", "\"분명 제 말이 부인께 깊은 인상을 남긴 모양이군요.\""]], [["“Oh, I should think so!", "\"오, 그럼요!"], ["I always note them all down.", "저는 늘 그걸 빠짐없이 기록해 둔답니다."], ["Well, Kitty, have you been skating again?...”", "그런데 키티, 또 스케이트 타러 갔었니?……\""]], [["And she began talking to Kitty.", "그리고 그녀는 키티와 이야기를 나누기 시작했다."], ["Awkward as it was for Levin to withdraw now, it would still have been easier for him to perpetrate this awkwardness than to remain all the evening and see Kitty, who glanced at him now and then and avoided his eyes.", "레빈이 지금 자리를 뜨는 것이 거북한 일이긴 했지만, 그래도 저녁 내내 남아서 이따금 자기를 흘끗 보다가 눈길을 피하는 키티를 보고 있느니 차라리 그 거북한 짓을 저질러 버리는 편이 더 쉬웠을 것이다."], ["He was on the point of getting up, when the princess, noticing that he was silent, addressed him.", "그가 막 일어서려는 참에, 그가 잠자코 있는 것을 알아챈 공작 부인이 그에게 말을 건넸다."]], [["“Shall you be long in Moscow?", "\"모스크바에 오래 계실 건가요?"], ["You’re busy with the district council, though, aren’t you, and can’t be away for long?”", "그래도 군 의회 일로 바쁘셔서 오래 떠나 계실 수는 없겠죠?\""]], [["“No, princess, I’m no longer a member of the council,”", "\"아닙니다, 공작 부인, 저는 더 이상 의회 위원이 아닙니다.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“I have come up for a few days.”", "\"며칠 일정으로 올라왔습니다.\""]], [["“There’s something the matter with him,”", "'저 사람한테 무슨 일이 있나 봐.'"], ["thought Countess Nordston, glancing at his stern, serious face.", "노르드스톤 백작 부인은 그의 굳고 진지한 얼굴을 흘끗 보며 생각했다."], ["“He isn’t in his old argumentative mood.", "'예전의 그 따지기 좋아하던 기분이 아니네."], ["But I’ll draw him out.", "하지만 내가 끌어내 줘야지."], ["I do love making a fool of him before Kitty, and I’ll do it.”", "키티 앞에서 저 사람을 바보로 만드는 게 얼마나 좋은지 몰라. 꼭 그렇게 하고 말겠어.'"]], [["“Konstantin Dmitrievitch,”", "\"콘스탄틴 드미트리치.\""], ["she said to him, “do explain to me, please, what’s the meaning of it.", "그녀가 그에게 말했다. \"제발 좀 설명해 주세요, 이게 무슨 뜻인지요."], ["You know all about such things.", "당신은 그런 일을 다 아시잖아요."], ["At home in our village of Kaluga all the peasants and all the women have drunk up all they possessed, and now they can’t pay us any rent.", "우리 칼루가 시골 마을에서는 농민들도 아낙네들도 가진 걸 죄다 술로 탕진해 버려서, 이제 우리한테 소작료를 한 푼도 못 낸답니다."], ["What’s the meaning of that?", "그게 무슨 뜻일까요?"], ["You always praise the peasants so.”", "당신은 늘 농민들을 그렇게 칭찬하시잖아요.\""]], [["At that instant another lady came into the room, and Levin got up.", "바로 그때 또 다른 부인이 방으로 들어왔고, 레빈은 일어섰다."]], [["“Excuse me, countess, but I really know nothing about it, and can’t tell you anything,”", "\"죄송합니다만, 백작 부인, 저는 정말 그 일에 대해 아무것도 모르고, 아무 말씀도 드릴 수가 없습니다.\""], ["he said, and looked round at the officer who came in behind the lady.", "그가 말하며, 그 부인의 뒤를 따라 들어온 장교를 돌아보았다."]], [["“That must be Vronsky,”", "'저 사람이 브론스키구나.'"], ["thought Levin, and, to be sure of it, glanced at Kitty.", "레빈은 생각했고, 그것을 확인하려고 키티를 흘끗 보았다."], ["She had already had time to look at Vronsky, and looked round at Levin.", "그녀는 이미 브론스키를 바라본 뒤였고, 다시 레빈을 돌아보았다."], ["And simply from the look in her eyes, that grew unconsciously brighter, Levin knew that she loved that man, knew it as surely as if she had told him so in words.", "그리고 무의식중에 더 환해진 그녀의 눈빛만으로, 레빈은 그녀가 저 사람을 사랑한다는 것을 알았다. 그녀가 말로 그렇게 일러 준 것처럼 확실하게 알았다."], ["But what sort of a man was he?", "하지만 그는 어떤 사람일까?"], ["Now, whether for good or for ill, Levin could not choose but remain; he must find out what the man was like whom she loved.", "이제 좋든 나쁘든 레빈은 남아 있을 수밖에 없었다. 그녀가 사랑하는 그 사람이 어떤 인물인지 알아내야만 했다."]], [["There are people who, on meeting a successful rival, no matter in what, are at once disposed to turn their backs on everything good in him, and to see only what is bad.", "성공한 연적을—그것이 어떤 분야에서든—만나면 곧장 그 사람의 좋은 점은 모조리 외면하고 나쁜 점만 보려 드는 부류의 사람들이 있다."], ["There are people, on the other hand, who desire above all to find in that lucky rival the qualities by which he has outstripped them, and seek with a throbbing ache at heart only what is good.", "반면에 무엇보다도 그 운 좋은 연적에게서 자신을 앞지르게 한 바로 그 자질들을 찾아내려 하고, 가슴이 아릿하게 욱신거리면서도 오로지 좋은 점만을 찾는 부류의 사람들도 있다."], ["Levin belonged to the second class.", "레빈은 두 번째 부류에 속했다."], ["But he had no difficulty in finding what was good and attractive in Vronsky.", "그런데 그는 브론스키에게서 좋고 매력적인 점을 찾는 데 아무런 어려움도 겪지 않았다."], ["It was apparent at the first glance.", "그것은 첫눈에 분명히 드러났다."], ["Vronsky was a squarely built, dark man, not very tall, with a good-humored, handsome, and exceedingly calm and resolute face.", "브론스키는 다부진 체격에 검은 머리를 한, 그리 크지 않은 키의 남자였으며, 선량하고 잘생긴, 더없이 침착하고 단호한 얼굴을 하고 있었다."], ["Everything about his face and figure, from his short-cropped black hair and freshly shaven chin down to his loosely fitting, brand-new uniform, was simple and at the same time elegant.", "짧게 깎은 검은 머리와 갓 면도한 턱부터 헐렁하게 맞는 새 군복에 이르기까지, 그의 얼굴과 풍채의 모든 것은 단순하면서도 동시에 우아했다."], ["Making way for the lady who had come in, Vronsky went up to the princess and then to Kitty.", "브론스키는 들어온 부인에게 길을 비켜 주고 나서 공작 부인에게로, 이어서 키티에게로 다가갔다."]], [["As he approached her, his beautiful eyes shone with a specially tender light, and with a faint, happy, and modestly triumphant smile (so it seemed to Levin), bowing carefully and respectfully over her, he held out his small broad hand to her.", "그녀에게 다가가면서 그의 아름다운 눈은 유난히 다정한 빛으로 빛났고, 희미하면서도 행복하고 겸손하게 의기양양한 미소를 띤 채(레빈에게는 그렇게 보였다) 그녀에게 조심스럽고 정중하게 몸을 숙이며, 작고 넓은 손을 그녀에게 내밀었다."]], [["Greeting and saying a few words to everyone, he sat down without once glancing at Levin, who had never taken his eyes off him.", "그는 모두에게 인사하고 몇 마디 말을 건넨 뒤, 자기에게서 한 번도 눈을 떼지 않던 레빈을 단 한 번도 쳐다보지 않은 채 자리에 앉았다."]], [["“Let me introduce you,”", "\"두 분을 소개하지요.\""], ["said the princess, indicating Levin.", "공작 부인이 레빈을 가리키며 말했다."], ["“Konstantin Dmitrievitch Levin, Count Alexey Kirillovitch Vronsky.”", "\"콘스탄틴 드미트리치 레빈, 알렉세이 키릴로비치 브론스키 백작.\""]], [["Vronsky got up and, looking cordially at Levin, shook hands with him.", "브론스키는 일어나 레빈을 다정하게 바라보며 그와 악수했다."]], [["“I believe I was to have dined with you this winter,”", "\"이번 겨울에 당신과 함께 식사하기로 되어 있었던 것 같은데요.\""], ["he said, smiling his simple and open smile; “but you had unexpectedly left for the country.”", "그가 소박하고 솔직한 미소를 지으며 말했다. \"그런데 당신이 뜻밖에 시골로 떠나 버리셨더군요.\""]], [["“Konstantin Dmitrievitch despises and hates town and us townspeople,”", "\"콘스탄틴 드미트리치는 도시와 우리 같은 도시 사람들을 경멸하고 미워한답니다.\""], ["said Countess Nordston.", "노르드스톤 백작 부인이 말했다."]], [["“My words must make a deep impression on you, since you remember them so well,”", "\"제 말을 그렇게 잘 기억하시는 걸 보니 제 말이 부인께 깊은 인상을 남긴 게 틀림없군요.\""], ["said Levin, and, suddenly conscious that he had said just the same thing before, he reddened.", "레빈은 그렇게 말하고는, 문득 자기가 조금 전에도 똑같은 말을 했다는 것을 깨닫고 얼굴을 붉혔다."]], [["Vronsky looked at Levin and Countess Nordston, and smiled.", "브론스키는 레빈과 노르드스톤 백작 부인을 보며 미소 지었다."]], [["“Are you always in the country?”", "\"항상 시골에 계시나요?\""], ["he inquired.", "그가 물었다."], ["“I should think it must be dull in the winter.”", "\"겨울에는 따분할 것 같은데요.\""]], [["“It’s not dull if one has work to do; besides, one’s not dull by oneself,”", "\"할 일이 있으면 따분하지 않습니다. 게다가 혼자 있어도 따분하지 않고요.\""], ["Levin replied abruptly.", "레빈이 퉁명스럽게 대꾸했다."]], [["“I am fond of the country,”", "\"저는 시골을 좋아합니다.\""], ["said Vronsky, noticing, and affecting not to notice, Levin’s tone.", "브론스키는 레빈의 어조를 알아채고도 짐짓 못 알아챈 척하며 말했다."]], [["“But I hope, count, you would not consent to live in the country always,”", "\"하지만 백작, 늘 시골에서만 사는 것에는 동의하지 않으시겠죠.\""], ["said Countess Nordston.", "노르드스톤 백작 부인이 말했다."]], [["“I don’t know; I have never tried for long.", "\"모르겠습니다. 오래 살아 본 적이 없어서요."], ["I experienced a queer feeling once,” he went on.", "한번은 이상한 기분을 느낀 적이 있습니다.\""], ["“I never longed so for the country, Russian country, with bast shoes and peasants, as when I was spending a winter with my mother in Nice.", "그가 말을 이었다. \"어머니와 함께 니스에서 겨울을 보낼 때만큼 시골이, 짚신과 농민들이 있는 러시아 시골이 그토록 그리웠던 적이 없었습니다."], ["Nice itself is dull enough, you know.", "아시다시피 니스 자체는 꽤 따분하지요."], ["And indeed, Naples and Sorrento are only pleasant for a short time.", "사실 나폴리도 소렌토도 잠깐 동안만 즐거울 뿐입니다."], ["And it’s just there that Russia comes back to me most vividly, and especially the country.", "그리고 바로 거기에서야말로 러시아가, 특히 시골이 가장 생생하게 제게 되살아나더군요."], ["It’s as though....”", "마치……\""]], [["He talked on, addressing both Kitty and Levin, turning his serene, friendly eyes from one to the other, and saying obviously just what came into his head.", "그는 키티와 레빈 양쪽을 향해 이야기하며, 평온하고 다정한 눈길을 이 사람에서 저 사람으로 옮겼고, 머릿속에 떠오르는 대로 거리낌 없이 말하는 것이 분명했다."]], [["Noticing that Countess Nordston wanted to say something, he stopped short without finishing what he had begun, and listened attentively to her.", "노르드스톤 백작 부인이 뭔가 말하고 싶어 하는 것을 알아채자, 그는 꺼낸 이야기를 끝맺지도 않고 뚝 멈춘 채 그녀의 말에 주의 깊게 귀를 기울였다."]], [["The conversation did not flag for an instant, so that the princess, who always kept in reserve, in case a subject should be lacking, two heavy guns—the relative advantages of classical and of modern education, and universal military service—had not to move out either of them, while Countess Nordston had not a chance of chaffing Levin.", "대화는 한순간도 늘어지지 않았으므로, 화제가 떨어질 경우를 대비해 늘 묵직한 대포 두 문—고전 교육과 현대 교육의 상대적 이점, 그리고 국민개병제—을 예비로 마련해 두던 공작 부인은 그중 어느 것도 꺼낼 필요가 없었고, 노르드스톤 백작 부인도 레빈을 놀려 댈 기회를 잡지 못했다."]], [["Levin wanted to, and could not, take part in the general conversation; saying to himself every instant, “Now go,”", "레빈은 다 함께 나누는 대화에 끼고 싶었지만 그러지 못했다. 매 순간 '자, 가자.'"], ["he still did not go, as though waiting for something.", "하고 스스로에게 말하면서도, 무언가를 기다리기라도 하는 듯 여전히 자리를 뜨지 못했다."]], [["The conversation fell upon table-turning and spirits, and Countess Nordston, who believed in spiritualism, began to describe the marvels she had seen.", "대화는 탁자 돌리기와 영혼에 관한 이야기로 흘러갔고, 심령술을 믿는 노르드스톤 백작 부인은 자기가 본 신기한 일들을 늘어놓기 시작했다."]], [["“Ah, countess, you really must take me, for pity’s sake do take me to see them!", "\"아, 백작 부인, 제발 저도 꼭 데려가 주세요, 부탁이니 그것들을 보러 데려가 주세요!"], ["I have never seen anything extraordinary, though I am always on the lookout for it everywhere,”", "저는 어디서나 늘 신기한 것을 찾아 헤매는데도 여태 비범한 것이라곤 본 적이 없거든요.\""], ["said Vronsky, smiling.", "브론스키가 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["“Very well, next Saturday,”", "\"좋아요, 다음 주 토요일에요.\""], ["answered Countess Nordston.", "노르드스톤 백작 부인이 대답했다."], ["“But you, Konstantin Dmitrievitch, do you believe in it?”", "\"그런데 콘스탄틴 드미트리치, 당신은 그걸 믿으세요?\""], ["she asked Levin.", "그녀가 레빈에게 물었다."]], [["“Why do you ask me?", "\"왜 저한테 물으십니까?"], ["You know what I shall say.”", "제가 무슨 말을 할지 다 아시면서요.\""]], [["“But I want to hear your opinion.”", "\"그래도 당신 의견을 듣고 싶어요.\""]], [["“My opinion,”", "\"제 의견은 말이죠,\""], ["answered Levin, “is only that this table-turning simply proves that educated society—so called—is no higher than the peasants.", "레빈이 대답했다. \"그저 이 탁자 돌리기라는 것이 소위 교양 있는 사회라는 게 농민들보다 조금도 나을 게 없다는 것을 증명할 뿐이라는 겁니다."], ["They believe in the evil eye, and in witchcraft and omens, while we....”", "농민들은 흉안이며 마법이며 징조 같은 것을 믿는데, 우리는……\""]], [["“Oh, then you don’t believe in it?”", "\"오, 그럼 당신은 그걸 안 믿는군요?\""]], [["“I can’t believe in it, countess.”", "\"믿을 수가 없습니다, 백작 부인.\""]], [["“But if I’ve seen it myself?”", "\"하지만 제가 직접 봤다면요?\""]], [["“The peasant women too tell us they have seen goblins.”", "\"농민 아낙네들도 도깨비를 봤다고들 하지요.\""]], [["“Then you think I tell a lie?”", "\"그럼 제가 거짓말을 한다는 건가요?\""]], [["And she laughed a mirthless laugh.", "그러고는 그녀가 즐거움 없는 웃음을 웃었다."]], [["“Oh, no, Masha, Konstantin Dmitrievitch said he could not believe in it,”", "\"오, 아니에요, 마샤, 콘스탄틴 드미트리치는 그걸 믿을 수 없다고 말한 거예요.\""], ["said Kitty, blushing for Levin, and Levin saw this, and, still more exasperated, would have answered, but Vronsky with his bright frank smile rushed to the support of the conversation, which was threatening to become disagreeable.", "키티가 레빈을 대신해 얼굴을 붉히며 말했고, 레빈은 이것을 보고 더욱 화가 나서 뭔가 대답하려 했지만, 브론스키가 그 밝고 솔직한 미소로 불쾌해질 위기에 놓인 대화를 구하러 끼어들었다."]], [["“You do not admit the conceivability at all?”", "\"당신은 그것이 가능하다는 것 자체를 전혀 인정하지 않으십니까?\""], ["he queried.", "그가 물었다."], ["“But why not?", "\"하지만 왜죠?"], ["We admit the existence of electricity, of which we know nothing.", "우리는 아무것도 모르는 전기의 존재도 인정하지 않습니까."], ["Why should there not be some new force, still unknown to us, which....”", "어째서 우리가 아직 모르는 어떤 새로운 힘이 있어선 안 되는 거죠, 그것이……\""]], [["“When electricity was discovered,”", "\"전기가 발견되었을 때,\""], ["Levin interrupted hurriedly, “it was only the phenomenon that was discovered, and it was unknown from what it proceeded and what were its effects, and ages passed before its applications were conceived.", "레빈이 황급히 말을 잘랐다. \"발견된 것은 단지 현상뿐이었고, 그것이 무엇에서 비롯되며 그 작용이 무엇인지는 알려지지 않았으며, 그 응용 방법이 고안되기까지는 여러 세대가 흘렀습니다."], ["But the spiritualists have begun with tables writing for them, and spirits appearing to them, and have only later started saying that it is an unknown force.”", "그런데 심령술사들은 탁자가 자기들에게 글을 써 주고 영혼이 자기들 앞에 나타나는 것에서 시작해 놓고는, 나중에야 그것이 미지의 힘이라고 말하기 시작한 겁니다.\""]], [["Vronsky listened attentively to Levin, as he always did listen, obviously interested in his words.", "브론스키는 늘 그러듯이 그의 말에 분명 흥미를 느끼며 주의 깊게 레빈의 말에 귀를 기울였다."]], [["“Yes, but the spiritualists say we don’t know at present what this force is, but there is a force, and these are the conditions in which it acts.", "\"네, 하지만 심령술사들은 현재로서는 이 힘이 무엇인지 모르지만, 어쨌든 힘은 존재하며, 이것이 그 힘이 작용하는 조건이라고 말합니다."], ["Let the scientific men find out what the force consists in.", "그 힘이 무엇으로 이루어졌는지는 과학자들이 밝혀내게 하면 되지요."], ["No, I don’t see why there should not be a new force, if it....”", "아니, 저는 왜 새로운 힘이 있어선 안 되는지 모르겠습니다, 만약 그것이……\""]], [["“Why, because with electricity,”", "\"왜냐하면, 전기의 경우에는,\""], ["Levin interrupted again, “every time you rub tar against wool, a recognized phenomenon is manifested, but in this case it does not happen every time, and so it follows it is not a natural phenomenon.”", "레빈이 다시 말을 잘랐다. \"송진을 양모에 문지를 때마다 매번 확인된 현상이 나타나지만, 이 경우에는 그것이 매번 일어나지 않으니, 따라서 그것은 자연 현상이 아니라는 결론이 나오는 겁니다.\""]], [["Feeling probably that the conversation was taking a tone too serious for a drawing-room, Vronsky made no rejoinder, but by way of trying to change the conversation, he smiled brightly, and turned to the ladies.", "대화가 응접실에서 나누기에는 너무 진지한 어조로 흐른다고 느꼈는지, 브론스키는 더 반박하지 않고, 화제를 바꿔 보려는 듯 밝게 미소 지으며 부인들 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸다."]], [["“Do let us try at once, countess,”", "\"백작 부인, 우리 당장 한번 해 봐요.\""], ["he said; but Levin would finish saying what he thought.", "그가 말했지만, 레빈은 자기 생각을 끝까지 말하려 했다."]], [["“I think,”", "\"제 생각엔 말입니다,\""], ["he went on, “that this attempt of the spiritualists to explain their marvels as some sort of new natural force is most futile.", "그가 말을 이었다. \"심령술사들이 자기들의 신기한 일을 일종의 새로운 자연력으로 설명하려는 이 시도는 더없이 부질없는 짓입니다."], ["They boldly talk of spiritual force, and then try to subject it to material experiment.”", "그들은 대담하게 영적인 힘을 운운하다가, 그것을 물질적인 실험에 종속시키려 드는 거죠.\""]], [["Everyone was waiting for him to finish, and he felt it.", "모두가 그의 말이 끝나기를 기다리고 있었고, 그는 그것을 느꼈다."]], [["“And I think you would be a first-rate medium,”", "\"제 생각엔 당신이야말로 일류 영매가 될 것 같아요.\""], ["said Countess Nordston; “there’s something enthusiastic in you.”", "노르드스톤 백작 부인이 말했다. \"당신에게는 뭔가 열정적인 데가 있거든요.\""]], [["Levin opened his mouth, was about to say something, reddened, and said nothing.", "레빈은 입을 열어 뭔가 말하려다가, 얼굴을 붉히고는 아무 말도 하지 않았다."]], [["“Do let us try table-turning at once, please,”", "\"제발, 당장 탁자 돌리기를 해 봐요.\""], ["said Vronsky.", "브론스키가 말했다."], ["“Princess, will you allow it?”", "\"공작 부인, 허락해 주시겠습니까?\""]], [["And Vronsky stood up, looking for a little table.", "그러고는 브론스키가 자그마한 탁자를 찾으며 일어섰다."]], [["Kitty got up to fetch a table, and as she passed, her eyes met Levin’s.", "키티는 탁자를 가지러 일어났고, 지나가다가 그녀의 눈이 레빈의 눈과 마주쳤다."], ["She felt for him with her whole heart, the more because she was pitying him for suffering of which she was herself the cause.", "그녀는 온 마음으로 그를 가엾게 여겼는데, 자기 자신이 그 원인인 괴로움 때문에 그를 가엾어하는 만큼 더욱 그러했다."], ["“If you can forgive me, forgive me,”", "'용서할 수 있다면, 저를 용서해 주세요.'"], ["said her eyes, “I am so happy.”", "그녀의 눈이 말했다. '저는 정말 행복해요.'"]], [["“I hate them all, and you, and myself,”", "'나는 저들 모두가, 그리고 당신이, 또 나 자신이 밉습니다.'"], ["his eyes responded, and he took up his hat.", "그의 눈이 대답했고, 그는 모자를 집어 들었다."], ["But he was not destined to escape.", "하지만 그는 빠져나갈 운명이 아니었다."], ["Just as they were arranging themselves round the table, and Levin was on the point of retiring, the old prince came in, and after greeting the ladies, addressed Levin.", "사람들이 막 탁자 둘레로 자리를 잡고, 레빈이 막 물러나려는 참에, 노공작이 들어와 부인들에게 인사한 뒤 레빈에게 말을 건넸다."]], [["“Ah!”", "\"아!\""], ["he began joyously.", "그가 반갑게 입을 열었다."], ["“Been here long, my boy?", "\"여기 온 지 오래됐나, 이 사람아?"], ["I didn’t even know you were in town.", "자네가 도시에 와 있는 줄도 몰랐네."], ["Very glad to see you.”", "보게 되어 아주 반갑구먼.\""], ["The old prince embraced Levin, and talking to him did not observe Vronsky, who had risen, and was serenely waiting till the prince should turn to him.", "노공작은 레빈을 끌어안았고, 그와 이야기하느라, 일어서서 자기에게 말을 돌릴 때까지 차분히 기다리고 있던 브론스키를 알아채지 못했다."]], [["Kitty felt how distasteful her father’s warmth was to Levin after what had happened.", "키티는 일어난 일이 있고 난 뒤 아버지의 다정함이 레빈에게 얼마나 불쾌할지를 느꼈다."], ["She saw, too, how coldly her father responded at last to Vronsky’s bow, and how Vronsky looked with amiable perplexity at her father, as though trying and failing to understand how and why anyone could be hostilely disposed towards him, and she flushed.", "그녀는 또 아버지가 마침내 브론스키의 인사에 얼마나 냉랭하게 응했는지, 그리고 브론스키가 어떻게 그리고 왜 누군가가 자기에게 적대적일 수 있는지 이해하려다 이해하지 못하는 듯, 상냥하면서도 어리둥절한 표정으로 자기 아버지를 바라보는지도 보았고, 얼굴을 붉혔다."]], [["“Prince, let us have Konstantin Dmitrievitch,”", "\"공작님, 콘스탄틴 드미트리치를 우리한테 양보해 주세요.\""], ["said Countess Nordston; “we want to try an experiment.”", "노르드스톤 백작 부인이 말했다. \"실험을 한번 해 보려고요.\""]], [["“What experiment?", "\"무슨 실험?"], ["Table-turning?", "탁자 돌리기 말인가?"], ["Well, you must excuse me, ladies and gentlemen, but to my mind it is better fun to play the ring game,”", "글쎄, 신사 숙녀 여러분, 미안하지만 내 생각엔 반지 놀이를 하는 게 더 재미있을 것 같네.\""], ["said the old prince, looking at Vronsky, and guessing that it had been his suggestion.", "노공작은 브론스키를 보며, 그것이 그의 제안이었으리라 짐작하고 말했다."], ["“There’s some sense in that, anyway.”", "\"어쨌든 그쪽이 그래도 뭔가 말이 되니까.\""]], [["Vronsky looked wonderingly at the prince with his resolute eyes, and, with a faint smile, began immediately talking to Countess Nordston of the great ball that was to come off next week.", "브론스키는 단호한 눈으로 의아한 듯 공작을 바라보더니, 희미한 미소를 띠며 곧장 노르드스톤 백작 부인과 다음 주에 열릴 성대한 무도회에 관해 이야기하기 시작했다."]], [["“I hope you will be there?”", "\"당신도 거기 오시겠죠?\""], ["he said to Kitty.", "그가 키티에게 말했다."], ["As soon as the old prince turned away from him, Levin went out unnoticed, and the last impression he carried away with him of that evening was the smiling, happy face of Kitty answering Vronsky’s inquiry about the ball.", "노공작이 그에게서 돌아서자마자, 레빈은 눈에 띄지 않게 밖으로 나갔고, 그날 저녁 그가 가지고 나온 마지막 인상은 무도회에 관한 브론스키의 물음에 답하는 키티의 미소 짓는 행복한 얼굴이었다."]], [["# Chapter 15", "# Chapter 15"]], [["At the end of the evening Kitty told her mother of her conversation with Levin, and in spite of all the pity she felt for Levin, she was glad at the thought that she had received an offer.", "저녁이 끝날 무렵 키티는 어머니에게 레빈과 나눈 대화를 이야기했고, 레빈에게 느끼는 온갖 연민에도 불구하고 자기가 청혼을 받았다는 생각에 기뻤다."], ["She had no doubt that she had acted rightly.", "그녀는 자기가 옳게 처신했다는 데에 의심이 없었다."], ["But after she had gone to bed, for a long while she could not sleep.", "그러나 잠자리에 든 뒤에도 한참 동안 잠들지 못했다."], ["One impression pursued her relentlessly.", "한 가지 인상이 끈질기게 그녀를 따라다녔다."], ["It was Levin’s face, with his scowling brows, and his kind eyes looking out in dark dejection below them, as he stood listening to her father, and glancing at her and at Vronsky.", "그것은 레빈의 얼굴이었다. 미간을 잔뜩 찌푸린 채, 그 아래로 어둡고 침울하게 내다보는 다정한 눈, 그가 그녀의 아버지에게 귀를 기울이며 그녀를, 또 브론스키를 흘끔거리던 그 모습이었다."], ["And she felt so sorry for him that tears came into her eyes.", "그러자 그가 너무도 가엾어 눈물이 핑 돌았다."], ["But immediately she thought of the man for whom she had given him up.", "그러나 곧바로 그녀는 그를 버리고 택한 사내를 떠올렸다."], ["She vividly recalled his manly, resolute face, his noble self-possession, and the good nature conspicuous in everything towards everyone.", "그 사내의 남자다운, 단호한 얼굴, 그 고귀한 침착함, 그리고 모든 사람을 향한 매사에 드러나는 선량함이 생생하게 되살아났다."], ["She remembered the love for her of the man she loved, and once more all was gladness in her soul, and she lay on the pillow, smiling with happiness.", "자기가 사랑하는 그 사내가 자기를 사랑한다는 것을 떠올리자, 다시 한 번 그녀의 영혼은 온통 기쁨으로 차올랐고, 그녀는 베개에 기대 행복하게 미소 지으며 누워 있었다. \"미안해요, 미안해요."], ["“I’m sorry, I’m sorry; but what could I do?", "하지만 나더러 어쩌라고요? 내 잘못이 아니잖아요.\""], ["It’s not my fault,”", "그녀는 혼잣말을 했다."], ["she said to herself; but an inner voice told her something else.", "그러나 마음속의 어떤 목소리는 그녀에게 다른 무언가를 일러 주었다."], ["Whether she felt remorse at having won Levin’s love, or at having refused him, she did not know.", "레빈의 사랑을 얻은 것에 대한 가책인지, 아니면 그를 거절한 것에 대한 가책인지, 그녀는 알 수 없었다. 그러나 그녀의 행복은 의혹에 물들어 버렸다."], ["But her happiness was poisoned by doubts.", "\"주여, 우리를 불쌍히 여기소서."], ["“Lord, have pity on us; Lord, have pity on us; Lord, have pity on us!”", "주여, 우리를 불쌍히 여기소서. 주여, 우리를 불쌍히 여기소서!\""], ["she repeated to herself, till she fell asleep.", "그녀는 잠이 들 때까지 마음속으로 거듭 되뇌었다."]], [["Meanwhile there took place below, in the prince’s little library, one of the scenes so often repeated between the parents on account of their favorite daughter.", "한편 아래층 공작의 작은 서재에서는, 그 애지중지하는 딸 때문에 부부 사이에 그토록 자주 되풀이되던 장면 가운데 하나가 벌어지고 있었다."]], [["“What?", "\"뭐라고?"], ["I’ll tell you what!”", "내가 말해 주지!\""], ["shouted the prince, waving his arms, and at once wrapping his squirrel-lined dressing-gown round him again.", "공작이 두 팔을 휘저으며 소리를 질렀고, 곧장 다람쥐 털을 덧댄 실내복을 다시 몸에 둘렀다."], ["“That you’ve no pride, no dignity; that you’re disgracing, ruining your daughter by this vulgar, stupid matchmaking!”", "\"당신한테는 자존심도, 품위도 없다는 거야. 이런 천박하고 멍청한 중매질로 딸을 망신시키고, 신세를 망쳐 놓고 있다는 거지!\""]], [["“But, really, for mercy’s sake, prince, what have I done?”", "\"하지만, 정말이지, 제발, 여보, 내가 뭘 어쨌다는 거예요?\""], ["said the princess, almost crying.", "공작 부인이 거의 울먹이며 말했다."]], [["She, pleased and happy after her conversation with her daughter, had gone to the prince to say good-night as usual, and though she had no intention of telling him of Levin’s offer and Kitty’s refusal, still she hinted to her husband that she fancied things were practically settled with Vronsky, and that he would declare himself so soon as his mother arrived.", "딸과 대화를 나눈 뒤 흐뭇하고 행복해진 그녀는 평소처럼 잘 자라는 인사를 하러 공작에게 갔고, 레빈의 청혼이며 키티의 거절을 그에게 알릴 생각은 없었으나, 그래도 남편에게 브론스키와는 일이 사실상 정해진 것 같다고, 그의 어머니가 도착하는 대로 그가 청혼할 거라는 듯이 넌지시 비쳤던 것이다."], ["And thereupon, at those words, the prince had all at once flown into a passion, and began to use unseemly language.", "그러자 그 말에 공작은 별안간 격분하여 점잖지 못한 말을 쏟아 내기 시작했다."]], [["“What have you done?", "\"당신이 뭘 어쨌냐고?"], ["I’ll tell you what.", "말해 주지."], ["First of all, you’re trying to catch an eligible gentleman, and all Moscow will be talking of it, and with good reason.", "우선 당신은 신랑감으로 괜찮은 신사를 낚으려 들고 있어. 그러면 모스크바가 온통 그 얘기로 떠들썩해질 거고, 그것도 그럴 만하지."], ["If you have evening parties, invite everyone, don’t pick out the possible suitors.", "야회를 연다면 다들 초대해, 가능성 있는 신랑 후보들만 골라 뽑지 말고."], ["Invite all the young bucks.", "젊은 한량들을 죄다 불러."], ["Engage a piano player, and let them dance, and not as you do things nowadays, hunting up good matches.", "피아노 연주자를 부르고, 춤을 추게 하란 말이야. 요즘 당신이 하는 식으로 좋은 혼처를 찾아 헤매지 말고."], ["It makes me sick, sick to see it, and you’ve gone on till you’ve turned the poor wench’s head.", "보고 있으면 메스꺼워, 메스껍다고. 당신은 그 가엾은 계집애의 머리를 어지럽혀 놓을 때까지 그 짓을 계속해 왔어."], ["Levin’s a thousand times the better man.", "레빈이 천 배는 나은 사내야."], ["As for this little Petersburg swell, they’re turned out by machinery, all on one pattern, and all precious rubbish.", "이 페테르부르크의 멋쟁이 도련님으로 말할 것 같으면, 그런 작자들은 기계로 찍어 내듯 죄다 한 틀에서 나와서, 전부 변변찮은 쓰레기야."], ["But if he were a prince of the blood, my daughter need not run after anyone.”", "하지만 설령 그가 왕족 혈통의 공작이라 한들, 내 딸이 아무 사내한테나 매달릴 필요는 없어.\""]], [["“But what have I done?”", "\"하지만 내가 뭘 어쨌다는 거예요?\""]], [["“Why, you’ve....”", "\"아니, 당신은…….\""], ["The prince was crying wrathfully.", "공작은 노여움에 울부짖다시피 했다."]], [["“I know if one were to listen to you,”", "\"당신 말대로 따른다면,\""], ["interrupted the princess, “we should never marry our daughter.", "공작 부인이 말을 가로막았다. \"우린 영영 딸을 시집보내지 못할 거예요."], ["If it’s to be so, we’d better go into the country.”", "정 그렇다면 차라리 시골로 내려가는 게 낫겠네요.\""]], [["“Well, and we had better.”", "\"그래, 그러는 편이 낫겠지.\""]], [["“But do wait a minute.", "\"하지만 잠깐만요."], ["Do I try and catch them?", "내가 그 사람들을 낚으려 한다고요?"], ["I don’t try to catch them in the least.", "난 그 사람들을 낚으려는 마음이 조금도 없어요."], ["A young man, and a very nice one, has fallen in love with her, and she, I fancy....”", "어떤 젊은이가, 그것도 아주 괜찮은 젊은이가 그 애한테 반했고, 그 애도 내 생각에는…….\""]], [["“Oh, yes, you fancy!", "\"오, 그래, 당신 생각에는!"], ["And how if she really is in love, and he’s no more thinking of marriage than I am!...", "그러다 정말로 그 애가 반해 버렸는데, 그자는 나만큼이나 결혼은 생각도 안 하고 있으면 어쩔 텐가!……"], ["Oh, that I should live to see it!", "오, 내가 이런 꼴을 보려고 살아왔단 말인가!"], ["Ah!", "아!"], ["spiritualism!", "강신술!"], ["Ah!", "아!"], ["Nice!", "멋져라!"], ["Ah!", "아!"], ["the ball!”", "무도회!\""], ["And the prince, imagining that he was mimicking his wife, made a mincing curtsey at each word.", "그러고는 공작은 아내를 흉내 낸답시고, 한 마디 한 마디마다 점잔 빼는 절을 해 보였다."], ["“And this is how we’re preparing wretchedness for Kitty; and she’s really got the notion into her head....”", "\"이런 식으로 우리는 키티에게 불행을 마련해 주고 있는 거야. 게다가 그 애는 진짜로 그런 생각을 머릿속에 박아 넣고 있다고…….\""]], [["“But what makes you suppose so?”", "\"하지만 왜 그렇게 생각하시는 거예요?\""]], [["“I don’t suppose; I know.", "\"생각하는 게 아니라 아는 거야."], ["We have eyes for such things, though women-folk haven’t.", "여자들한테는 없지만 우리한테는 그런 걸 보는 눈이 있거든."], ["I see a man who has serious intentions, that’s Levin: and I see a peacock, like this feather-head, who’s only amusing himself.”", "진지한 속셈을 가진 사내가 누군지 난 보여, 그게 레빈이지. 그리고 이 머리 텅 빈 작자처럼 그저 제 재미나 보려는 공작새 같은 놈도 난 보인단 말이야.\""]], [["“Oh, well, when once you get an idea into your head!...”", "\"오, 참, 당신은 한번 머릿속에 무슨 생각이 박히면!……\""]], [["“Well, you’ll remember my words, but too late, just as with Dolly.”", "\"좋아, 두고 보면 내 말이 떠오를 거야. 하지만 그땐 너무 늦지, 돌리 때처럼 말이야.\""]], [["“Well, well, we won’t talk of it,”", "\"그래요, 그래, 그 얘긴 그만둬요.\""], ["the princess stopped him, recollecting her unlucky Dolly.", "공작 부인은 불운한 돌리를 떠올리며 그의 말을 막았다."]], [["“By all means, and good-night!”", "\"좋아, 그럼 잘 자게!\""]], [["And signing each other with the cross, the husband and wife parted with a kiss, feeling that they each remained of their own opinion.", "그러고서 부부는 서로에게 성호를 그어 주고, 입을 맞추며 헤어졌다. 두 사람 다 저마다 제 생각을 그대로 고집하고 있다는 것을 느끼면서."]], [["The princess had at first been quite certain that that evening had settled Kitty’s future, and that there could be no doubt of Vronsky’s intentions, but her husband’s words had disturbed her. And returning to her own room, in terror before the unknown future, she, too, like Kitty, repeated several times in her heart, “Lord, have pity; Lord, have pity; Lord, have pity.”", "공작 부인은 처음에는 그날 저녁으로 키티의 장래가 정해졌으며, 브론스키의 속셈에는 의심의 여지가 없다고 확신하고 있었으나, 남편의 말에 마음이 어지러워졌다. 그리고 자기 방으로 돌아와, 알 수 없는 미래 앞에서 두려움에 사로잡혀, 그녀 또한 키티처럼 마음속으로 여러 번 되뇌었다. \"주여, 불쌍히 여기소서. 주여, 불쌍히 여기소서. 주여, 불쌍히 여기소서.\""]], [["# Chapter 16", "# Chapter 16"]], [["Vronsky had never had a real home life.", "브론스키는 진정한 가정생활이라는 것을 가져 본 적이 없었다."], ["His mother had been in her youth a brilliant society woman, who had had during her married life, and still more afterwards, many love affairs notorious in the whole fashionable world.", "그의 어머니는 젊은 시절 사교계에서 화려하게 빛나던 여인으로, 결혼 생활 중에도, 그리고 그 후로는 한층 더, 온 상류 사교계에 소문이 자자한 연애 사건을 숱하게 겪은 사람이었다."], ["His father he scarcely remembered, and he had been educated in the Corps of Pages.", "아버지는 거의 기억나지 않았고, 그는 근위 무관 학교에서 교육을 받았다."]], [["Leaving the school very young as a brilliant officer, he had at once got into the circle of wealthy Petersburg army men.", "아주 젊은 나이에 빼어난 장교가 되어 학교를 떠난 그는, 곧장 페테르부르크의 부유한 군인 무리 속으로 들어갔다."], ["Although he did go more or less into Petersburg society, his love affairs had always hitherto been outside it.", "페테르부르크 사교계에 어느 정도 드나들기는 했지만, 그의 연애 사건은 여태껏 늘 그 바깥에서 일어났다."]], [["In Moscow he had for the first time felt, after his luxurious and coarse life at Petersburg, all the charm of intimacy with a sweet and innocent girl of his own rank, who cared for him.", "페테르부르크에서의 사치스럽고 거친 생활을 거친 뒤, 그는 모스크바에서 처음으로 자기와 같은 신분이며 자기를 좋아해 주는, 다정하고 순결한 처녀와 친밀하게 지내는 매력을 온전히 느꼈다."], ["It never even entered his head that there could be any harm in his relations with Kitty.", "키티와의 관계에 무언가 해로운 점이 있을 수 있다는 생각은 그의 머릿속에 떠오른 적조차 없었다."], ["At balls he danced principally with her.", "무도회에서 그는 주로 그녀와 춤을 추었다."], ["He was a constant visitor at their house.", "그는 그녀의 집을 끊임없이 드나들었다."], ["He talked to her as people commonly do talk in society—all sorts of nonsense, but nonsense to which he could not help attaching a special meaning in her case.", "그는 사교계에서 사람들이 흔히 나누는 식으로, 그러니까 온갖 시답잖은 이야기를 그녀에게 건넸지만, 그녀를 상대로 할 때만큼은 그 시답잖은 말에 특별한 의미를 담지 않을 수 없었다."], ["Although he said nothing to her that he could not have said before everybody, he felt that she was becoming more and more dependent upon him, and the more he felt this, the better he liked it, and the tenderer was his feeling for her.", "비록 그가 그녀에게 한 말은 누구 앞에서나 할 수 있는 것뿐이었지만, 그는 그녀가 점점 더 자기에게 기대게 되어 가는 것을 느꼈고, 그렇게 느끼면 느낄수록 그것이 더 좋았으며, 그녀를 향한 마음도 더 애틋해졌다."], ["He did not know that his mode of behavior in relation to Kitty had a definite character, that it is courting young girls with no intention of marriage, and that such courting is one of the evil actions common among brilliant young men such as he was.", "그는 키티에 대한 자기 행동 방식에 분명한 성격이 있다는 것을, 그것이 결혼할 마음도 없으면서 젊은 처녀에게 구애하는 짓이며, 그런 구애야말로 자기 같은 화려한 젊은이들 사이에 흔한 못된 행실 가운데 하나라는 것을 알지 못했다."], ["It seemed to him that he was the first who had discovered this pleasure, and he was enjoying his discovery.", "그에게는 이 즐거움을 자기가 처음으로 발견한 것처럼 여겨졌고, 그는 그 발견을 즐기고 있었다."]], [["If he could have heard what her parents were saying that evening, if he could have put himself at the point of view of the family and have heard that Kitty would be unhappy if he did not marry her, he would have been greatly astonished, and would not have believed it.", "만일 그날 저녁 그녀의 부모가 나눈 이야기를 그가 들을 수 있었다면, 만일 그가 그 가족의 입장에 서서 자기가 키티와 결혼하지 않으면 그녀가 불행해지리라는 말을 들을 수 있었다면, 그는 몹시 놀랐을 것이고, 그 말을 믿지 않았을 것이다."], ["He could not believe that what gave such great and delicate pleasure to him, and above all to her, could be wrong.", "자기에게, 그리고 무엇보다 그녀에게 그토록 크고 섬세한 기쁨을 주는 일이 잘못된 것일 수 있다는 사실을 그는 믿을 수 없었다."], ["Still less could he have believed that he ought to marry.", "자기가 결혼을 해야 한다는 것은 더더욱 믿을 수 없었다."]], [["Marriage had never presented itself to him as a possibility.", "결혼이 그에게 하나의 가능성으로 떠오른 적은 한 번도 없었다."], ["He not only disliked family life, but a family, and especially a husband was, in accordance with the views general in the bachelor world in which he lived, conceived as something alien, repellant, and, above all, ridiculous.", "그는 가정생활을 싫어했을 뿐 아니라, 가족이라는 것, 특히 남편이라는 존재를, 그가 몸담은 독신 세계에 두루 퍼진 견해에 따라, 무언가 낯설고 거슬리며, 무엇보다도 우스꽝스러운 것으로 여겼다."]], [["But though Vronsky had not the least suspicion what the parents were saying, he felt on coming away from the Shtcherbatskys’ that the secret spiritual bond which existed between him and Kitty had grown so much stronger that evening that some step must be taken.", "그러나 브론스키는 부모가 무슨 이야기를 나누는지 조금도 짐작하지 못했지만, 셰르바츠키가를 나서면서 자기와 키티 사이에 존재하는 은밀한 정신적 유대가 그날 저녁 너무도 강해진 나머지, 이제 무언가 조치를 취해야만 한다는 것을 느꼈다."], ["But what step could and ought to be taken he could not imagine.", "그러나 어떤 조치를 취할 수 있고 또 취해야 하는지는 도무지 상상이 가지 않았다."]], [["“What is so exquisite,”", "\"정말 황홀한 건 말이야.\" 그는 셰르바츠키가에서 돌아오며 생각했다."], ["he thought, as he returned from the Shtcherbatskys’, carrying away with him, as he always did, a delicious feeling of purity and freshness, arising partly from the fact that he had not been smoking for a whole evening, and with it a new feeling of tenderness at her love for him—“what is so exquisite is that not a word has been said by me or by her, but we understand each other so well in this unseen language of looks and tones, that this evening more clearly than ever she told me she loves me.", "그는 늘 그렇듯이 청량하고 순결한 기분을 가슴에 안고 돌아오고 있었는데, 그것은 한편으로는 저녁 내내 담배를 피우지 않은 덕분이기도 했고, 거기에 더해 자기를 향한 그녀의 사랑에서 우러난 새로운 애틋함도 섞여 있었다. \"정말 황홀한 건, 나도 그녀도 단 한마디 말도 하지 않았는데, 눈빛과 말투라는 이 보이지 않는 언어로 우리가 서로를 그토록 잘 이해한다는 거야."], ["And how secretly, simply, and most of all, how trustfully!", "오늘 저녁 그녀는 그 어느 때보다 또렷하게 나를 사랑한다고 말했어. 그것도 얼마나 은밀하게, 소박하게, 그리고 무엇보다 얼마나 믿음을 담아서!"], ["I feel myself better, purer.", "나는 내가 더 나아지고, 더 순결해진 것 같은 기분이야."], ["I feel that I have a heart, and that there is a great deal of good in me.", "나에게도 마음이라는 게 있고, 내 안에 좋은 구석이 잔뜩 있다는 걸 느껴."], ["Those sweet, loving eyes!", "그 사랑스럽고 다정한 눈동자라니!"], ["When she said: ‘Indeed I do....’", "그녀가 '정말 그래요……'라고 했을 때 말이야."]], [["“Well, what then?", "\"자, 그래서 어쩌라고?"], ["Oh, nothing.", "아, 아무것도 아니지."], ["It’s good for me, and good for her.”", "나한테도 좋고, 그녀한테도 좋은 일이야.\""], ["And he began wondering where to finish the evening.", "그러고 나서 그는 저녁을 어디서 마무리할지 궁리하기 시작했다."]], [["He passed in review of the places he might go to.", "그는 자기가 갈 만한 곳들을 하나하나 떠올려 보았다."], ["“Club?", "\"클럽?"], ["a game of bezique, champagne with Ignatov?", "베지크 한판에, 이그나토프와 샴페인이나? 아니, 안 가."], ["No, I’m not going.", "Château des Fleurs?"], ["Château des Fleurs; there I shall find Oblonsky, songs, the cancan.", "거기 가면 오블론스키랑, 노래에, 캉캉이 기다리고 있겠지. 아니, 그것도 지긋지긋해."], ["No, I’m sick of it.", "셰르바츠키가가 좋은 게 바로 그 점이야, 내가 점점 나아지고 있다는 거지. 집에 가야겠다.\""], ["That’s why I like the Shtcherbatskys’, that I’m growing better. I’ll go home.”", "그는 곧장 뒤소 호텔의 자기 방으로 가서 야식을 주문하고는 옷을 벗었고, 베개에 머리가 닿기가 무섭게 깊은 잠에 빠져들었다."], ["He went straight to his room at Dussots’ Hotel, ordered supper, and then undressed, and as soon as his head touched the pillow, fell into a sound sleep.", "* Château des Fleurs: 프랑스어로 '꽃의 성관(城館)'. 당시 유흥장을 가리킨다."]], [["# Chapter 17", "# Chapter 17"]], [["Next day at eleven o’clock in the morning Vronsky drove to the station of the Petersburg railway to meet his mother, and the first person he came across on the great flight of steps was Oblonsky, who was expecting his sister by the same train.", "이튿날 아침 열한 시에 브론스키는 어머니를 마중하러 페테르부르크 철도역으로 마차를 몰고 갔는데, 큰 계단에서 가장 먼저 마주친 사람은 같은 기차로 누이를 기다리고 있던 오블론스키였다."]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["your excellency!”", "각하!\""], ["cried Oblonsky, “whom are you meeting?”", "오블론스키가 소리쳤다. \"누구를 마중 나오셨소?\""]], [["“My mother,”", "\"어머니요.\""], ["Vronsky responded, smiling, as everyone did who met Oblonsky.", "오블론스키를 만나는 사람이라면 누구나 그러듯이 브론스키도 미소를 지으며 대답했다."], ["He shook hands with him, and together they ascended the steps.", "그는 오블론스키와 악수를 나누고 함께 계단을 올라갔다."], ["“She is to be here from Petersburg today.”", "\"오늘 페테르부르크에서 오신다더군요.\""]], [["“I was looking out for you till two o’clock last night.", "\"어젯밤 두 시까지 자넬 찾았네."], ["Where did you go after the Shtcherbatskys’?”", "셰르바츠키 댁에서 나와 어디로 갔던 건가?\""]], [["“Home,”", "\"집으로요.\""], ["answered Vronsky.", "브론스키가 대답했다."], ["“I must own I felt so well content yesterday after the Shtcherbatskys’ that I didn’t care to go anywhere.”", "\"솔직히 어제는 셰르바츠키 댁에서 나온 뒤에 어찌나 기분이 흐뭇하던지 어디 다른 데 갈 마음이 안 나더군요.\""]], [["“I know a gallant steed by tokens sure,", "\"준마는 확실한 표징으로 알아보고,"]], [["And by his eyes I know a youth in love,”", "사랑에 빠진 젊은이는 그 눈으로 알아보네,\""]], [["declaimed Stepan Arkadyevitch, just as he had done before to Levin.", "스테판 아르카지치는 전에 레빈에게 했던 것과 똑같이 시를 읊었다."]], [["Vronsky smiled with a look that seemed to say that he did not deny it, but he promptly changed the subject.", "브론스키는 부정하지 않는다는 듯한 표정으로 미소를 지었지만, 곧 화제를 돌렸다."]], [["“And whom are you meeting?”", "\"그러는 당신은 누굴 마중 나오셨소?\""], ["he asked.", "그가 물었다."]], [["“I?", "\"나?"], ["I’ve come to meet a pretty woman,”", "나야 어여쁜 여인을 마중 나왔지.\" 오블론스키가 말했다."], ["said Oblonsky.", "\"설마!\""]], [["“You don’t say so!” “Honi soit qui mal y pense!", "\"Honi soit qui mal y pense!"], ["My sister Anna.”", "내 누이 안나라네.\""]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["that’s Madame Karenina,”", "카레니나 부인 말이군요.\""], ["said Vronsky.", "브론스키가 말했다."]], [["“You know her, no doubt?”", "\"틀림없이 그녀를 알겠지?\""]], [["“I think I do. Or perhaps not ...", "\"아는 것 같기도 하고요."], ["I really am not sure,”", "아니면 모르는 것 같기도 하고……"], ["Vronsky answered heedlessly, with a vague recollection of something stiff and tedious evoked by the name Karenina.", "정말 잘 모르겠네요.\" 브론스키는 카레니나라는 이름이 불러일으키는, 뭔가 뻣뻣하고 따분한 것에 대한 어렴풋한 기억을 떠올리며 무심하게 대답했다."]], [["“But Alexey Alexandrovitch, my celebrated brother-in-law, you surely must know.", "\"하지만 내 유명한 매부 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 자네도 틀림없이 알겠지."], ["All the world knows him.”", "세상 사람들이 다 아는 인물이니까.\""]], [["“I know him by reputation and by sight.", "\"평판으로도 알고 얼굴로도 압니다."], ["I know that he’s clever, learned, religious somewhat....", "영리하고, 학식 있고, 좀 신앙심도 깊은 분이라는 것도 알고요……"], ["But you know that’s not ... not in my line,”", "하지만 그건, 아시다시피 나하고는 not in my line이죠.\""], ["said Vronsky in English.", "브론스키는 영어로 말했다."]], [["“Yes, he’s a very remarkable man; rather a conservative, but a splendid man,”", "\"그래, 그는 아주 비범한 사람이지. 다소 보수적이긴 해도, 훌륭한 사람이야.\""], ["observed Stepan Arkadyevitch, “a splendid man.”", "스테판 아르카지치가 말했다. \"훌륭한 사람이고말고.\""]], [["“Oh, well, so much the better for him,”", "\"아, 그래요, 그분께는 그게 더 잘된 일이겠죠.\""], ["said Vronsky smiling.", "브론스키가 미소를 지으며 말했다."], ["“Oh, you’ve come,”", "\"오, 자네 왔구먼.\""], ["he said, addressing a tall old footman of his mother’s, standing at the door; “come here.”", "그가 문가에 서 있던, 어머니를 모시는 키 큰 늙은 하인에게 말했다. \"이리 오게.\""]], [["Besides the charm Oblonsky had in general for everyone, Vronsky had felt of late specially drawn to him by the fact that in his imagination he was associated with Kitty.", "오블론스키가 누구에게나 풍기는 그 매력 말고도, 브론스키는 요즘 들어 그가 자기 상상 속에서 키티와 연결되어 있다는 사실 때문에 특별히 그에게 끌리고 있었다."]], [["“Well, what do you say?", "\"그래, 어떻게 생각하나?"], ["Shall we give a supper on Sunday for the diva?”", "일요일에 그 diva를 위해 만찬을 열까?\""], ["he said to him with a smile, taking his arm.", "그가 오블론스키의 팔을 잡으며 미소와 함께 말했다."]], [["“Of course.", "\"물론이지."], ["I’m collecting subscriptions.", "내가 회비를 걷고 있다네."], ["Oh, did you make the acquaintance of my friend Levin?”", "아, 그런데 내 친구 레빈하고는 인사를 나눴나?\""], ["asked Stepan Arkadyevitch.", "스테판 아르카지치가 물었다."]], [["“Yes; but he left rather early.”", "\"네, 그런데 일찍 가 버리던걸요.\""]], [["“He’s a capital fellow,”", "\"썩 괜찮은 친구지.\""], ["pursued Oblonsky.", "오블론스키가 말을 이었다."], ["“Isn’t he?”", "\"그렇지 않은가?\""]], [["“I don’t know why it is,”", "\"왜 그런지는 모르겠지만요.\""], ["responded Vronsky, “in all Moscow people—present company of course excepted,”", "브론스키가 대답했다. \"모스크바 사람들은 죄다—물론 지금 여기 계신 분은 빼고요.\""], ["he put in jestingly, “there’s something uncompromising.", "그가 농담조로 끼워 넣었다. \"어딘가 융통성이 없어요."], ["They are all on the defensive, lose their tempers, as though they all want to make one feel something....”", "다들 잔뜩 방어적이고, 욱하기 일쑤고, 마치 모두들 남에게 뭔가를 느끼게 하고 싶어 하는 것 같단 말입니다……\""]], [["“Yes, that’s true, it is so,”", "\"그래, 그건 사실이지, 정말 그렇다네.\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, laughing good-humoredly.", "스테판 아르카지치가 사람 좋게 웃으며 말했다."]], [["“Will the train soon be in?”", "\"기차가 곧 들어옵니까?\""], ["Vronsky asked a railway official.", "브론스키가 철도 직원에게 물었다."]], [["“The train’s signaled,”", "\"신호가 들어왔습니다.\""], ["answered the man.", "그 사내가 대답했다."]], [["The approach of the train was more and more evident by the preparatory bustle in the station, the rush of porters, the movement of policemen and attendants, and people meeting the train.", "기차가 다가오고 있다는 것이 역 안의 채비를 갖추는 분주함과, 짐꾼들이 분주히 오가는 모습과, 경관들과 역무원들의 움직임과, 기차를 맞으러 나온 사람들로 점점 더 뚜렷해졌다."], ["Through the frosty vapor could be seen workmen in short sheepskins and soft felt boots crossing the rails of the curving line.", "서리 낀 수증기 사이로 짧은 양가죽 외투에 부드러운 펠트 장화를 신은 인부들이 굽은 선로를 가로질러 가는 것이 보였다."], ["The hiss of the boiler could be heard on the distant rails, and the rumble of something heavy.", "멀리 떨어진 선로에서 보일러의 쉭쉭거리는 소리와, 뭔가 묵직한 것이 우르릉거리는 소리가 들려왔다."]], [["“No,”", "\"아니야.\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, who felt a great inclination to tell Vronsky of Levin’s intentions in regard to Kitty.", "키티에 대한 레빈의 속내를 브론스키에게 들려주고 싶은 마음이 굴뚝같았던 스테판 아르카지치가 말했다."], ["“No, you’ve not got a true impression of Levin.", "\"아니, 자넨 레빈에 대해 제대로 된 인상을 갖지 못한 거야."], ["He’s a very nervous man, and is sometimes out of humor, it’s true, but then he is often very nice.", "그는 무척 신경이 예민한 사람이라 가끔 기분이 언짢기도 하지, 그건 사실이네만, 또 종종 아주 다정한 사람이라네."], ["He’s such a true, honest nature, and a heart of gold.", "워낙 진실하고 정직한 천성에다, 마음씨가 황금 같은 사람이지."], ["But yesterday there were special reasons,”", "하지만 어제는 특별한 사정이 있었어.\""], ["pursued Stepan Arkadyevitch, with a meaning smile, totally oblivious of the genuine sympathy he had felt the day before for his friend, and feeling the same sympathy now, only for Vronsky.", "스테판 아르카지치는 의미심장한 미소를 지으며 말을 이었는데, 전날 친구에게 느꼈던 진심 어린 동정은 까맣게 잊고서, 이제는 똑같은 동정을 오로지 브론스키에게만 느끼고 있었다."], ["“Yes, there were reasons why he could not help being either particularly happy or particularly unhappy.”", "\"그래, 그가 유난히 행복하거나 유난히 불행할 수밖에 없었던 사정이 있었던 거지.\""]], [["Vronsky stood still and asked directly: “How so?", "브론스키는 우뚝 멈춰 서서 단도직입적으로 물었다. \"어째서요?"], ["Do you mean he made your belle-sœur an offer yesterday?”", "어제 그가 당신의 belle-sœur에게 청혼했다는 말입니까?\""]], [["“Maybe,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch.", "\"어쩌면 그랬을지도.\""], ["“I fancied something of the sort yesterday.", "스테판 아르카지치가 말했다. \"어제 그런 느낌이 들었거든."], ["Yes, if he went away early, and was out of humor too, it must mean it....", "그래, 일찍 가 버린 데다 기분까지 언짢았다면, 그건 분명……"], ["He’s been so long in love, and I’m very sorry for him.”", "그렇게 오랫동안 사랑해 왔으니, 나도 그가 무척 안됐어.\""]], [["“So that’s it!", "\"그런 거였군요!"], ["I should imagine, though, she might reckon on a better match,”", "하지만 제 생각엔, 그녀는 더 나은 혼처를 기대할 수도 있겠는데요.\""], ["said Vronsky, drawing himself up and walking about again, “though I don’t know him, of course,”", "브론스키가 몸을 곧추세우며 다시 거닐기 시작했다. \"물론 저야 그를 모릅니다만.\""], ["he added.", "그가 덧붙였다."], ["“Yes, that is a hateful position!", "\"그래요, 그건 참 곤혹스러운 처지죠!"], ["That’s why most fellows prefer to have to do with Klaras.", "그래서 사내들 대부분은 클라라 같은 여자들을 상대하는 걸 더 좋아하는 거고요."], ["If you don’t succeed with them it only proves that you’ve not enough cash, but in this case one’s dignity’s at stake.", "그런 여자들에게 실패하면 그저 돈이 모자랐다는 것만 드러날 뿐이지만, 이 경우엔 자존심이 걸린 문제니까요."], ["But here’s the train.”", "아, 기차가 들어옵니다.\""]], [["The engine had already whistled in the distance.", "기관차는 벌써 저 멀리서 기적을 울렸다."], ["A few instants later the platform was quivering, and with puffs of steam hanging low in the air from the frost, the engine rolled up, with the lever of the middle wheel rhythmically moving up and down, and the stooping figure of the engine-driver covered with frost.", "몇 순간 뒤 플랫폼이 흔들리기 시작했고, 추위에 낮게 깔린 증기를 뿜어내며 기관차가 들어왔다. 가운데 바퀴의 연결봉이 율동적으로 오르내렸고, 서리를 뒤집어쓴 기관사가 몸을 숙인 채였다."], ["Behind the tender, setting the platform more and more slowly swaying, came the luggage van with a dog whining in it.", "탄수차 뒤로, 플랫폼을 점점 더 느릿느릿 흔들면서 안에서 개 한 마리가 낑낑대는 수하물차가 따라왔다."], ["At last the passenger carriages rolled in, oscillating before coming to a standstill.", "마침내 객차들이 멈춰 서기 전에 좌우로 흔들거리며 들어왔다."]], [["A smart guard jumped out, giving a whistle, and after him one by one the impatient passengers began to get down: an officer of the guards, holding himself erect, and looking severely about him; a nimble little merchant with a satchel, smiling gaily; a peasant with a sack over his shoulder.", "날렵한 차장이 호루라기를 불며 뛰어내렸고, 그 뒤를 이어 성마른 승객들이 하나둘씩 내리기 시작했다. 몸을 꼿꼿이 세운 채 매서운 눈으로 주위를 둘러보는 근위대 장교, 작은 가방을 든 채 유쾌하게 미소 짓는 민첩하고 자그마한 상인, 어깨에 자루를 둘러멘 농부."]], [["Vronsky, standing beside Oblonsky, watched the carriages and the passengers, totally oblivious of his mother.", "오블론스키 곁에 서 있던 브론스키는 어머니에 대해서는 까맣게 잊은 채 객차들과 승객들을 바라보고 있었다."], ["What he had just heard about Kitty excited and delighted him.", "방금 키티에 대해 들은 이야기가 그를 들뜨게 하고 기쁘게 했다."], ["Unconsciously he arched his chest, and his eyes flashed.", "자기도 모르게 그는 가슴을 부풀렸고, 두 눈이 번득였다."], ["He felt himself a conqueror.", "그는 자신이 승리자가 된 듯한 기분이었다."]], [["“Countess Vronskaya is in that compartment,”", "\"브론스카야 백작 부인은 저 칸에 계십니다.\""], ["said the smart guard, going up to Vronsky.", "날렵한 차장이 브론스키에게 다가오며 말했다."]], [["The guard’s words roused him, and forced him to think of his mother and his approaching meeting with her. He did not in his heart respect his mother, and without acknowledging it to himself, he did not love her, though in accordance with the ideas of the set in which he lived, and with his own education, he could not have conceived of any behavior to his mother not in the highest degree respectful and obedient, and the more externally obedient and respectful his behavior, the less in his heart he respected and loved her.", "차장의 말이 그를 일깨워, 어머니와 곧 있을 그녀와의 만남을 떠올리게 했다. 그는 마음속으로 어머니를 존경하지 않았고, 스스로 인정하지는 않았지만 어머니를 사랑하지도 않았다. 다만 그가 몸담은 무리의 통념과 자기가 받은 교육에 비추어 볼 때, 그는 어머니에 대해 더없이 깍듯하고 순종적이지 않은 어떤 태도도 상상할 수 없었으나, 겉으로 순종적이고 깍듯하게 굴면 굴수록 마음속으로는 그만큼 덜 어머니를 존경하고 사랑했다. * Honi soit qui mal y pense: 프랑스어로 '나쁜 마음을 품은 자에게 화 있으라'. 영국 가터 훈장의 표어. * diva: 이탈리아어로 '여가수, 프리마돈나'. * belle-sœur: 프랑스어로 '처제, 시누이' 등 손아래 여자 인척."]], [["# Chapter 18", "# Chapter 18"]], [["Vronsky followed the guard to the carriage, and at the door of the compartment he stopped short to make room for a lady who was getting out.", "브론스키는 차장을 따라 객차로 갔는데, 칸막이 객실 문 앞에서 마침 내리려는 한 부인에게 길을 비켜 주려고 우뚝 멈춰 섰다."]], [["With the insight of a man of the world, from one glance at this lady’s appearance Vronsky classified her as belonging to the best society.", "사교계 인사 특유의 통찰력으로, 브론스키는 이 부인의 모습을 한 번 흘끗 보는 것만으로 그녀를 상류 사회에 속한 사람으로 분류했다."], ["He begged pardon, and was getting into the carriage, but felt he must glance at her once more; not that she was very beautiful, not on account of the elegance and modest grace which were apparent in her whole figure, but because in the expression of her charming face, as she passed close by him, there was something peculiarly caressing and soft.", "그는 양해를 구하고 객차로 들어가려 했지만, 그녀를 한 번 더 봐야겠다는 생각이 들었다. 그녀가 매우 아름다워서도 아니었고, 그녀의 온몸에서 드러나는 우아함과 정숙한 기품 때문도 아니었으며, 그녀가 그의 곁을 스쳐 지나갈 때 그 매혹적인 얼굴 표정에 무언가 유난히 다정하고 부드러운 것이 깃들어 있었기 때문이었다."], ["As he looked round, she too turned her head.", "그가 돌아보는 순간, 그녀도 고개를 돌렸다."], ["Her shining gray eyes, that looked dark from the thick lashes, rested with friendly attention on his face, as though she were recognizing him, and then promptly turned away to the passing crowd, as though seeking someone.", "짙은 속눈썹 때문에 어두워 보이는 그녀의 반짝이는 잿빛 눈은, 마치 그를 알아보기라도 한 듯 다정한 관심을 담고 그의 얼굴에 머물렀다가, 누군가를 찾기라도 하는 듯 곧 지나가는 군중 쪽으로 옮겨 갔다."], ["In that brief look Vronsky had time to notice the suppressed eagerness which played over her face, and flitted between the brilliant eyes and the faint smile that curved her red lips.", "그 짧은 시선 속에서 브론스키는 그녀의 얼굴에 어른거리는, 그리고 빛나는 두 눈과 붉은 입술을 살짝 휘어 놓는 희미한 미소 사이를 오가는 억눌린 생기를 알아챌 수 있었다."], ["It was as though her nature were so brimming over with something that against her will it showed itself now in the flash of her eyes, and now in her smile.", "그녀의 천성이 무언가로 너무도 흘러넘쳐서, 그녀의 의지와는 상관없이 어떤 때는 눈빛의 광채로, 또 어떤 때는 미소로 그것이 저절로 드러나는 것만 같았다."], ["Deliberately she shrouded the light in her eyes, but it shone against her will in the faintly perceptible smile.", "그녀는 일부러 눈 속의 빛을 가렸지만, 그 빛은 그녀의 의지를 거슬러 희미하게 감지되는 미소 속에서 반짝였다."]], [["Vronsky stepped into the carriage.", "브론스키는 객차로 들어섰다."], ["His mother, a dried-up old lady with black eyes and ringlets, screwed up her eyes, scanning her son, and smiled slightly with her thin lips.", "검은 눈에 곱슬머리를 한, 바싹 마른 노부인인 그의 어머니는 눈을 가늘게 뜨고 아들을 찬찬히 살펴보더니 얇은 입술로 살짝 미소 지었다."], ["Getting up from the seat and handing her maid a bag, she gave her little wrinkled hand to her son to kiss, and lifting his head from her hand, kissed him on the cheek.", "그녀는 자리에서 일어나 하녀에게 가방을 건네고, 작고 주름진 손을 아들에게 내밀어 입을 맞추게 하고는, 그의 머리를 손에서 들어 올려 뺨에 입을 맞추었다."]], [["“You got my telegram?", "\"내 전보 받았니?"], ["Quite well?", "잘 지냈고?"], ["Thank God.”", "다행이로구나.\""]], [["“You had a good journey?”", "\"여행은 편안하셨어요?\""], ["said her son, sitting down beside her, and involuntarily listening to a woman’s voice outside the door.", "아들이 그녀 곁에 앉으며 말했는데, 그러면서 자기도 모르게 문밖에서 들려오는 여자 목소리에 귀를 기울이고 있었다."], ["He knew it was the voice of the lady he had met at the door.", "그는 그것이 문 앞에서 마주친 그 부인의 목소리라는 것을 알았다."]], [["“All the same I don’t agree with you,”", "\"그래도 저는 당신 의견에 동의하지 않아요.\""], ["said the lady’s voice.", "그 부인의 목소리가 말했다."]], [["“It’s the Petersburg view, madame.”", "\"그건 페테르부르크식 견해입니다, 부인.\""]], [["“Not Petersburg, but simply feminine,”", "\"페테르부르크식이 아니라, 그냥 여자다운 거예요.\""], ["she responded.", "그녀가 응수했다."]], [["“Well, well, allow me to kiss your hand.”", "\"자, 자, 그럼 손에 입맞추게 허락해 주십시오.\""]], [["“Good-bye, Ivan Petrovitch.", "\"안녕히 가세요, 이반 페트로비치."], ["And could you see if my brother is here, and send him to me?”", "그리고 제 오빠가 와 있는지 좀 봐 주시고, 그이를 제게 보내 주실 수 있겠어요?\""], ["said the lady in the doorway, and stepped back again into the compartment.", "부인은 문간에서 그렇게 말하고는 다시 객실 안으로 들어섰다."]], [["“Well, have you found your brother?”", "\"그래, 오라버니는 찾으셨어요?\""], ["said Countess Vronskaya, addressing the lady.", "브론스카야 백작 부인이 그 부인에게 말을 건넸다."]], [["Vronsky understood now that this was Madame Karenina.", "이제 브론스키는 이 사람이 카레니나 부인이라는 것을 알아차렸다."]], [["“Your brother is here,”", "\"오라버님은 여기 와 계십니다.\""], ["he said, standing up.", "그가 일어서며 말했다."], ["“Excuse me, I did not know you, and, indeed, our acquaintance was so slight,”", "\"실례했습니다, 알아뵙지 못했습니다. 사실 우리가 안면을 튼 것이 워낙 잠깐이라,\""], ["said Vronsky, bowing, “that no doubt you do not remember me.”", "브론스키가 고개를 숙이며 말했다. \"분명 저를 기억하지 못하시겠지요.\""]], [["“Oh, no,”", "\"오, 아니에요.\""], ["said she, “I should have known you because your mother and I have been talking, I think, of nothing but you all the way.”", "그녀가 말했다. \"저는 당신을 알아봤을 거예요."], ["As she spoke she let the eagerness that would insist on coming out show itself in her smile.", "당신 어머님과 제가, 제 생각에는 오는 내내 당신 이야기밖에 안 한 것 같으니까요.\" 그녀는 말하면서, 기어이 비어져 나오려는 그 생기를 미소 속에 드러내 보였다."], ["“And still no sign of my brother.”", "\"그런데도 오빠는 아직 보이지 않네요.\""]], [["“Do call him, Alexey,”", "\"좀 불러 보렴, 알렉세이.\""], ["said the old countess.", "노백작 부인이 말했다."], ["Vronsky stepped out onto the platform and shouted:", "브론스키는 플랫폼으로 나가 외쳤다."]], [["“Oblonsky!", "\"오블론스키!"], ["Here!”", "여기야!\""]], [["Madame Karenina, however, did not wait for her brother, but catching sight of him she stepped out with her light, resolute step. And as soon as her brother had reached her, with a gesture that struck Vronsky by its decision and its grace, she flung her left arm around his neck, drew him rapidly to her, and kissed him warmly. Vronsky gazed, never taking his eyes from her, and smiled, he could not have said why. But recollecting that his mother was waiting for him, he went back again into the carriage.", "그러나 카레니나 부인은 오빠를 기다리지 않고, 그를 발견하자 가볍고도 단호한 걸음으로 객차에서 내렸다. 그리고 오빠가 그녀에게 다가오기가 무섭게, 그녀는 그 결단성과 우아함으로 브론스키를 놀라게 한 동작으로 왼팔을 오빠의 목에 두르고, 그를 재빨리 자기 쪽으로 끌어당겨 따뜻하게 입을 맞추었다. 브론스키는 그녀에게서 눈을 떼지 못한 채 바라보았고, 까닭은 알 수 없었지만 미소를 지었다. 그러나 어머니가 자기를 기다리고 있다는 것을 떠올리고는, 다시 객차 안으로 들어갔다. \"참 사랑스러운 분이지, 그렇지 않니?\" 백작 부인이 카레니나 부인을 두고 말했다. \"남편이 저 사람을 내게 맡겼는데, 함께하게 되어 나도 무척 기뻤단다. 오는 내내 이야기를 나눴지. 그리고 너로 말하자면, 내가 듣자 하니…… vous filez le parfait amour. Tant mieux, mon cher, tant mieux.\""]], [["“She’s very sweet, isn’t she?” said the countess of Madame Karenina. “Her husband put her with me, and I was delighted to have her. We’ve been talking all the way. And so you, I hear ... vous filez le parfait amour. Tant mieux, mon cher, tant mieux.”", "* vous filez le parfait amour. Tant mieux, mon cher, tant mieux: 프랑스어로 '너는 완벽한 사랑을 키워 가고 있다는구나. 잘됐다, 얘야, 잘됐어'라는 뜻."]], [["“I don’t know what you are referring to, maman,”", "\"무슨 말씀을 하시는지 모르겠습니다, maman.\""], ["he answered coldly.", "그가 차갑게 대답했다."], ["“Come, maman, let us go.”", "\"자, maman, 그만 가시지요.\""]], [["Madame Karenina entered the carriage again to say good-bye to the countess.", "카레니나 부인은 백작 부인에게 작별 인사를 하려고 다시 객차로 들어섰다."]], [["“Well, countess, you have met your son, and I my brother,”", "\"자, 백작 부인, 부인께서는 아드님을 만나셨고, 저는 오빠를 만났네요.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“And all my gossip is exhausted.", "\"그리고 제 수다도 이제 다 떨어졌어요."], ["I should have nothing more to tell you.”", "더는 말씀드릴 게 없을 거예요.\""]], [["“Oh, no,”", "\"오, 아니에요.\""], ["said the countess, taking her hand.", "백작 부인이 그녀의 손을 잡으며 말했다."], ["“I could go all around the world with you and never be dull.", "\"나는 당신과 함께라면 온 세상을 돌아다녀도 결코 지루하지 않을 거예요."], ["You are one of those delightful women in whose company it’s sweet to be silent as well as to talk.", "당신은 함께 있으면 말하는 것도 침묵하는 것도 다 즐거운, 그런 사랑스러운 여인들 중 하나예요."], ["Now please don’t fret over your son; you can’t expect never to be parted.”", "자, 부디 아드님 일로 너무 마음 졸이지 말아요. 영영 떨어지지 않고 살 수는 없는 법이니까요.\""]], [["Madame Karenina stood quite still, holding herself very erect, and her eyes were smiling.", "카레니나 부인은 몸을 아주 꼿꼿이 세우고 가만히 서 있었는데, 그녀의 두 눈은 웃고 있었다."]], [["“Anna Arkadyevna,”", "\"안나 아르카지예브나는,\""], ["the countess said in explanation to her son, “has a little son eight years old, I believe, and she has never been parted from him before, and she keeps fretting over leaving him.”", "백작 부인이 아들에게 설명하듯 말했다. \"내 생각에 여덟 살 난 어린 아들이 있는데, 전에는 한 번도 그 아이와 떨어져 본 적이 없어서, 아이를 두고 온 일로 줄곧 마음을 졸이고 있단다.\""]], [["“Yes, the countess and I have been talking all the time, I of my son and she of hers,”", "\"네, 백작 부인과 저는 내내 이야기를 나눴어요. 저는 제 아들 이야기를, 부인은 부인의 아드님 이야기를 했죠.\""], ["said Madame Karenina, and again a smile lighted up her face, a caressing smile intended for him.", "카레니나 부인이 말했고, 다시 그녀의 얼굴에 미소가 환히 떠올랐는데, 그를 향한 다정한 미소였다."]], [["“I am afraid that you must have been dreadfully bored,”", "\"틀림없이 무척 지루하셨겠군요.\""], ["he said, promptly catching the ball of coquetry she had flung him.", "그가 그녀가 던진 교태의 공을 냉큼 받아치며 말했다."], ["But apparently she did not care to pursue the conversation in that strain, and she turned to the old countess.", "그러나 그녀는 그런 어조로 대화를 이어 갈 마음이 없는 듯, 노백작 부인 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸다."]], [["“Thank you so much.", "\"정말 감사했어요."], ["The time has passed so quickly.", "시간이 어찌나 빨리 지났는지요."], ["Good-bye, countess.”", "안녕히 가세요, 백작 부인.\""]], [["“Good-bye, my love,”", "\"잘 가요, 내 사랑.\""], ["answered the countess.", "백작 부인이 대답했다."], ["“Let me have a kiss of your pretty face.", "\"그 예쁜 얼굴에 입맞추게 해 줘요."], ["I speak plainly, at my age, and I tell you simply that I’ve lost my heart to you.”", "내 나이가 되면 거리낌 없이 말하게 되는데, 솔직히 말해서 나는 당신에게 마음을 빼앗겨 버렸어요.\""]], [["Stereotyped as the phrase was, Madame Karenina obviously believed it and was delighted by it.", "상투적인 말이었지만, 카레니나 부인은 그 말을 분명히 믿었고 또 그 말에 기뻐했다."], ["She flushed, bent down slightly, and put her cheek to the countess’s lips, drew herself up again, and with the same smile fluttering between her lips and her eyes, she gave her hand to Vronsky.", "그녀는 얼굴을 붉히고 살짝 몸을 숙여 백작 부인의 입술에 뺨을 댔다가, 다시 몸을 일으켰다. 그리고 입술과 두 눈 사이를 오가는 그 똑같은 미소를 띤 채, 브론스키에게 손을 내밀었다."], ["He pressed the little hand she gave him, and was delighted, as though at something special, by the energetic squeeze with which she freely and vigorously shook his hand.", "그가 그녀가 내민 작은 손을 잡자, 그녀가 거리낌 없이 힘차게 그의 손을 흔드는 그 야무진 악수에 그는 무언가 특별한 일을 만난 듯 기뻤다."], ["She went out with the rapid step which bore her rather fully-developed figure with such strange lightness.", "그녀는 제법 풍만하게 발달한 몸을 그토록 이상하리만치 가볍게 실어 나르는 그 빠른 걸음으로 나가 버렸다."]], [["“Very charming,”", "\"정말 매력적이로구나.\""], ["said the countess.", "백작 부인이 말했다."]], [["That was just what her son was thinking.", "그것은 그녀의 아들이 막 떠올리고 있던 바로 그 생각이었다."], ["His eyes followed her till her graceful figure was out of sight, and then the smile remained on his face.", "그의 눈은 그녀의 우아한 자태가 시야에서 사라질 때까지 그녀를 좇았고, 그러고 나서도 미소가 그의 얼굴에 남아 있었다."], ["He saw out of the window how she went up to her brother, put her arm in his, and began telling him something eagerly, obviously something that had nothing to do with him, Vronsky, and at that he felt annoyed.", "그는 창밖으로, 그녀가 오빠에게 다가가 팔짱을 끼고 무언가를 열심히 이야기하기 시작하는 것을 보았는데, 분명 자기, 그러니까 브론스키와는 아무 상관없는 이야기였고, 그래서 그는 짜증이 났다."]], [["“Well, maman, are you perfectly well?”", "\"그래, maman, 정말 편안하셨어요?\""], ["he repeated, turning to his mother.", "그가 어머니를 향해 고쳐 물었다."]], [["“Everything has been delightful.", "\"모든 게 즐거웠단다."], ["Alexander has been very good, and Marie has grown very pretty.", "알렉산드르는 아주 착했고, 마리는 무척 예뻐졌더구나."], ["She’s very interesting.”", "그 애는 참 흥미로운 아이야.\""]], [["And she began telling him again of what interested her most—the christening of her grandson, for which she had been staying in Petersburg, and the special favor shown her elder son by the Tsar.", "그리고 그녀는 다시, 자기가 가장 마음을 쓰는 일—손자의 세례식, 그리고 그것 때문에 페테르부르크에 머물렀던 일이며, 황제께서 큰아들에게 베푸신 특별한 은총에 대해 그에게 이야기하기 시작했다."]], [["“Here’s Lavrenty,”", "\"라브렌티가 왔네요.\""], ["said Vronsky, looking out of the window; “now we can go, if you like.”", "브론스키가 창밖을 내다보며 말했다. \"이제 원하시면 가실 수 있습니다.\""]], [["The old butler, who had traveled with the countess, came to the carriage to announce that everything was ready, and the countess got up to go.", "백작 부인과 함께 여행해 온 늙은 집사가 모든 준비가 끝났다고 알리려고 객차로 왔고, 백작 부인은 가려고 일어섰다."]], [["“Come; there’s not such a crowd now,”", "\"가시지요. 이제는 그렇게 붐비지도 않습니다.\""], ["said Vronsky.", "브론스키가 말했다."]], [["The maid took a handbag and the lap dog, the butler and a porter the other baggage.", "하녀는 손가방과 애완견을 들었고, 집사와 짐꾼은 나머지 짐을 들었다."], ["Vronsky gave his mother his arm; but just as they were getting out of the carriage several men ran suddenly by with panic-stricken faces.", "브론스키는 어머니에게 팔을 내밀었다. 그런데 그들이 막 객차에서 내리려는 순간, 몇몇 사내들이 공포에 질린 얼굴로 별안간 곁을 달려 지나갔다."], ["The station-master, too, ran by in his extraordinary colored cap.", "역장 또한 그 유난스러운 색깔의 모자를 쓴 채 달려갔다."], ["Obviously something unusual had happened.", "무언가 심상찮은 일이 벌어진 것이 분명했다."], ["The crowd who had left the train were running back again.", "기차에서 내렸던 군중이 다시 되돌아 달려가고 있었다."]], [["“What?...", "\"뭐라고요?……"], ["What?...", "뭐예요?……"], ["Where?...", "어디서요?……"], ["Flung himself!...", "몸을 던졌대!……"], ["Crushed!...”", "깔렸대!……\""], ["was heard among the crowd.", "하는 소리가 군중 사이에서 들려왔다."], ["Stepan Arkadyevitch, with his sister on his arm, turned back.", "누이와 팔짱을 낀 스테판 아르카지치도 발길을 돌렸다."], ["They too looked scared, and stopped at the carriage door to avoid the crowd.", "그들 역시 겁먹은 얼굴로, 군중을 피하려고 객차 문 앞에 멈춰 섰다."]], [["The ladies got in, while Vronsky and Stepan Arkadyevitch followed the crowd to find out details of the disaster.", "부인들은 객차로 들어갔고, 그동안 브론스키와 스테판 아르카지치는 그 참사의 자세한 내막을 알아보려고 군중을 따라갔다."]], [["A guard, either drunk or too much muffled up in the bitter frost, had not heard the train moving back, and had been crushed.", "차장 한 사람이, 술에 취했거나 아니면 매서운 추위에 너무 단단히 몸을 싸맨 탓에, 기차가 뒤로 움직이는 소리를 듣지 못하고 깔려 버린 것이었다."]], [["Before Vronsky and Oblonsky came back the ladies heard the facts from the butler.", "브론스키와 오블론스키가 돌아오기도 전에 부인들은 집사에게서 그 사실을 들었다."]], [["Oblonsky and Vronsky had both seen the mutilated corpse.", "오블론스키와 브론스키는 둘 다 그 짓이겨진 시신을 보았다."], ["Oblonsky was evidently upset.", "오블론스키는 분명 충격을 받은 모습이었다."], ["He frowned and seemed ready to cry.", "그는 미간을 찌푸렸고, 금방이라도 울 것만 같았다."]], [["“Ah, how awful!", "\"아, 어찌나 끔찍한지!"], ["Ah, Anna, if you had seen it!", "아, 안나, 네가 그걸 봤다면!"], ["Ah, how awful!”", "아, 어찌나 끔찍한지!\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["Vronsky did not speak; his handsome face was serious, but perfectly composed.", "브론스키는 말이 없었다. 그의 잘생긴 얼굴은 진지했지만, 더없이 침착했다."]], [["“Oh, if you had seen it, countess,”", "\"오, 백작 부인, 부인께서 그걸 보셨다면,\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch.", "스테판 아르카지치가 말했다."], ["“And his wife was there....", "\"그리고 그 사람의 아내가 거기 있었어요……"], ["It was awful to see her!...", "그 여자를 보는 것은 정말 끔찍했습니다!……"], ["She flung herself on the body.", "그 여자는 시신 위로 몸을 던졌어요."], ["They say he was the only support of an immense family.", "그 사람이 대가족의 유일한 부양자였다더군요."], ["How awful!”", "정말 끔찍해요!\""]], [["“Couldn’t one do anything for her?”", "\"그 여자를 위해 뭐라도 해 줄 수는 없을까요?\""], ["said Madame Karenina in an agitated whisper.", "카레니나 부인이 동요한 목소리로 속삭이듯 말했다."]], [["Vronsky glanced at her, and immediately got out of the carriage.", "브론스키는 그녀를 흘끗 보고는 곧장 객차에서 내렸다."]], [["“I’ll be back directly, maman,”", "\"금방 돌아오겠습니다, maman.\""], ["he remarked, turning round in the doorway.", "그가 문간에서 돌아서며 말했다."]], [["When he came back a few minutes later, Stepan Arkadyevitch was already in conversation with the countess about the new singer, while the countess was impatiently looking towards the door, waiting for her son.", "몇 분 뒤 그가 돌아왔을 때, 스테판 아르카지치는 이미 백작 부인과 새로 나온 가수에 대해 이야기를 나누고 있었고, 백작 부인은 아들을 기다리며 초조하게 문 쪽을 바라보고 있었다."]], [["“Now let us be off,”", "\"자, 이제 출발하시지요.\""], ["said Vronsky, coming in.", "브론스키가 들어오며 말했다."], ["They went out together.", "그들은 함께 나갔다."], ["Vronsky was in front with his mother.", "브론스키가 어머니와 함께 앞장섰다."], ["Behind walked Madame Karenina with her brother.", "그 뒤로 카레니나 부인이 오빠와 함께 걸었다."], ["Just as they were going out of the station the station-master overtook Vronsky.", "그들이 막 역을 나서려는데 역장이 브론스키를 뒤쫓아 왔다."]], [["“You gave my assistant two hundred roubles.", "\"제 부하 직원에게 이백 루블을 주셨더군요."], ["Would you kindly explain for whose benefit you intend them?”", "누구를 위해 쓰실 작정이신지 말씀해 주시겠습니까?\""]], [["“For the widow,”", "\"미망인을 위해서요.\""], ["said Vronsky, shrugging his shoulders.", "브론스키가 어깨를 으쓱하며 말했다."], ["“I should have thought there was no need to ask.”", "\"굳이 물을 것까지야 없을 텐데요.\""]], [["“You gave that?”", "\"자네가 그걸 줬다고?\""], ["cried Oblonsky, behind, and, pressing his sister’s hand, he added: “Very nice, very nice!", "뒤에 있던 오블론스키가 외쳤고, 누이의 손을 꼭 쥐며 덧붙였다. \"정말 훌륭하군, 정말 훌륭해!"], ["Isn’t he a splendid fellow?", "저 친구 멋진 사람 아니냐?"], ["Good-bye, countess.”", "안녕히 가십시오, 백작 부인.\""]], [["And he and his sister stood still, looking for her maid.", "그리고 그와 누이는 하녀를 찾으며 그 자리에 멈춰 섰다."]], [["When they went out the Vronsky’s carriage had already driven away.", "그들이 밖으로 나왔을 때 브론스키의 마차는 이미 떠나고 없었다."], ["People coming in were still talking of what happened.", "안으로 들어오는 사람들은 여전히 방금 일어난 일을 이야기하고 있었다."]], [["“What a horrible death!”", "\"정말 끔찍한 죽음이로군!\""], ["said a gentleman, passing by.", "한 신사가 곁을 지나가며 말했다."], ["“They say he was cut in two pieces.”", "\"두 동강이 났다더군요.\""]], [["“On the contrary, I think it’s the easiest—instantaneous,”", "\"천만에, 내 생각에는 그게 가장 편한 죽음이오. 한순간이니까.\""], ["observed another.", "다른 사람이 한마디 했다."]], [["“How is it they don’t take proper precautions?”", "\"어째서 제대로 된 안전 조치를 안 하는 거야?\""], ["said a third.", "또 다른 사람이 말했다."]], [["Madame Karenina seated herself in the carriage, and Stepan Arkadyevitch saw with surprise that her lips were quivering, and she was with difficulty restraining her tears.", "카레니나 부인은 마차에 자리를 잡고 앉았는데, 스테판 아르카지치는 그녀의 입술이 떨리고 있고, 그녀가 간신히 눈물을 참고 있다는 것을 알아채고 놀랐다."]], [["“What is it, Anna?”", "\"왜 그러니, 안나?\""], ["he asked, when they had driven a few hundred yards.", "마차가 몇백 미터쯤 달렸을 때 그가 물었다."]], [["“It’s an omen of evil,”", "\"불길한 징조예요.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“What nonsense!”", "\"무슨 그런 터무니없는 소리를!\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch.", "스테판 아르카지치가 말했다."], ["“You’ve come, that’s the chief thing.", "\"네가 와 줬다는 것, 그게 가장 중요한 일이야."], ["You can’t conceive how I’m resting my hopes on you.”", "내가 너한테 얼마나 큰 희망을 걸고 있는지 너는 짐작도 못 할 거다.\""]], [["“Have you known Vronsky long?”", "\"브론스키를 안 지는 오래되셨어요?\""], ["she asked.", "그녀가 물었다."]], [["“Yes.", "\"그래."], ["You know we’re hoping he will marry Kitty.”", "너도 알다시피, 우리는 그가 키티와 결혼하기를 바라고 있단다.\""]], [["“Yes?”", "\"그래요?\""], ["said Anna softly.", "안나가 나직이 말했다."], ["“Come now, let us talk of you,”", "\"자, 이제 오빠 이야기를 해요.\""], ["she added, tossing her head, as though she would physically shake off something superfluous oppressing her.", "그녀는 마치 자기를 짓누르는 무언가 거추장스러운 것을 몸으로 떨쳐 내려는 듯 고개를 흔들며 덧붙였다."], ["“Let us talk of your affairs.", "\"오빠 일을 이야기해 봐요."], ["I got your letter, and here I am.”", "편지를 받고 이렇게 왔잖아요.\""]], [["“Yes, all my hopes are in you,”", "\"그래, 내 모든 희망은 너한테 달려 있어.\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch.", "스테판 아르카지치가 말했다."]], [["“Well, tell me all about it.”", "\"자, 그럼 다 이야기해 봐요.\""]], [["And Stepan Arkadyevitch began to tell his story.", "그리고 스테판 아르카지치는 자기 이야기를 풀어놓기 시작했다."]], [["On reaching home Oblonsky helped his sister out, sighed, pressed her hand, and set off to his office.", "집에 다다르자 오블론스키는 누이가 내리는 것을 도와주고, 한숨을 쉬고, 그녀의 손을 꼭 쥐고는, 자기 관청으로 떠났다."]], [["# Chapter 19", "# Chapter 19"]], [["When Anna went into the room, Dolly was sitting in the little drawing-room with a white-headed fat little boy, already like his father, giving him a lesson in French reading.", "안나가 방으로 들어갔을 때, 돌리는 작은 응접실에 머리가 하얗고 통통한, 벌써 아버지를 닮은 사내아이와 함께 앉아 프랑스어 읽기를 가르치고 있었다."], ["As the boy read, he kept twisting and trying to tear off a button that was nearly off his jacket.", "아이는 책을 읽으면서 거의 떨어질 듯 말 듯 한 윗옷 단추를 계속 비틀며 떼어 내려고 했다."], ["His mother had several times taken his hand from it, but the fat little hand went back to the button again.", "어머니가 몇 번이나 단추에서 그 손을 떼어 냈지만, 통통한 작은 손은 다시 단추로 돌아갔다."], ["His mother pulled the button off and put it in her pocket.", "어머니는 단추를 뜯어 자기 호주머니에 넣었다."]], [["“Keep your hands still, Grisha,”", "\"손 좀 가만히 둬라, 그리샤.\""], ["she said, and she took up her work, a coverlet she had long been making.", "그녀는 말하고는, 오래전부터 만들어 오던 침대보를 다시 집어 들었다."], ["She always set to work on it at depressed moments, and now she knitted at it nervously, twitching her fingers and counting the stitches.", "그녀는 늘 마음이 가라앉을 때 그 일에 손을 댔는데, 지금은 손가락을 떨며 코를 세면서 신경질적으로 떴다."], ["Though she had sent word the day before to her husband that it was nothing to her whether his sister came or not, she had made everything ready for her arrival, and was expecting her sister-in-law with emotion.", "비록 그녀는 그 전날 남편에게, 그의 누이가 오든 말든 자기와는 아무 상관이 없다고 전갈을 보냈지만, 정작 그녀를 맞을 채비는 모두 갖추어 놓았고, 마음이 술렁이며 시누이를 기다리고 있었다."]], [["Dolly was crushed by her sorrow, utterly swallowed up by it.", "돌리는 슬픔에 짓눌려, 그 슬픔에 완전히 삼켜져 있었다."], ["Still she did not forget that Anna, her sister-in-law, was the wife of one of the most important personages in Petersburg, and was a Petersburg grande dame.", "그래도 그녀는 시누이인 안나가 페테르부르크에서 가장 중요한 인물 가운데 하나의 아내이며, 페테르부르크의 grande dame라는 사실을 잊지 않았다."], ["And, thanks to this circumstance, she did not carry out her threat to her husband—that is to say, she remembered that her sister-in-law was coming.", "그리고 바로 그 사정 덕분에, 그녀는 남편에게 했던 위협을 실행에 옮기지 않았다. 다시 말해, 시누이가 온다는 것을 기억해 두었던 것이다."], ["“And, after all, Anna is in no wise to blame,”", "\"게다가 어쨌든 안나에게는 아무 잘못도 없잖아.\""], ["thought Dolly.", "돌리는 생각했다."], ["“I know nothing of her except the very best, and I have seen nothing but kindness and affection from her towards myself.”", "\"나는 그녀에 대해 더없이 좋은 것밖에는 아는 게 없고, 그녀가 나를 대하는 태도에서도 친절과 애정밖에는 본 적이 없어.\""], ["It was true that as far as she could recall her impressions at Petersburg at the Karenins’, she did not like their household itself; there was something artificial in the whole framework of their family life.", "사실 페테르부르크에서 카레닌 집안에 머물던 때의 인상을 떠올려 보면, 그 집안 자체가 마음에 들지는 않았다. 그들의 가정생활 전체의 틀에는 어딘가 꾸민 듯한 데가 있었다."], ["“But why should I not receive her?", "\"하지만 내가 왜 그녀를 맞지 못한단 말이야?"], ["If only she doesn’t take it into her head to console me!”", "그저 그녀가 나를 위로하겠다고 마음먹지만 않는다면!\""], ["thought Dolly.", "돌리는 생각했다."], ["“All consolation and counsel and Christian forgiveness, all that I have thought over a thousand times, and it’s all no use.”", "\"온갖 위로와 충고와 기독교적 용서, 그런 건 내가 이미 천 번도 더 곱씹어 봤지만, 다 아무 소용이 없어.\""]], [["All these days Dolly had been alone with her children.", "* grande dame: 프랑스어로 '귀부인'."], ["She did not want to talk of her sorrow, but with that sorrow in her heart she could not talk of outside matters.", "이 며칠 동안 돌리는 아이들과 단둘이서 지냈다. 자기 슬픔에 대해 이야기하고 싶지는 않았지만, 그 슬픔을 가슴에 품은 채로는 바깥의 일들에 대해서도 이야기할 수가 없었다."], ["She knew that in one way or another she would tell Anna everything, and she was alternately glad at the thought of speaking freely, and angry at the necessity of speaking of her humiliation with her, his sister, and of hearing her ready-made phrases of good advice and comfort.", "그녀는 어떤 식으로든 안나에게 모든 것을 털어놓게 되리라는 것을 알았고, 그래서 마음을 터놓고 이야기할 수 있으리라는 생각에 기뻐하다가도, 다른 한편으로는 하필 그의 누이인 그녀에게 자신의 굴욕을 이야기하고, 미리 준비된 듯한 충고와 위로의 말을 들어야 한다는 것에 화가 나기도 하며 마음이 오락가락했다."], ["She had been on the lookout for her, glancing at her watch every minute, and, as so often happens, let slip just that minute when her visitor arrived, so that she did not hear the bell.", "그녀는 안나를 기다리며 줄곧 시계를 들여다보았지만, 흔히 그렇듯이, 손님이 도착한 바로 그 순간을 놓쳐 버려 초인종 소리를 듣지 못했다."]], [["Catching a sound of skirts and light steps at the door, she looked round, and her care-worn face unconsciously expressed not gladness, but wonder.", "문가에서 치맛자락 스치는 소리와 가벼운 발소리가 들리자 그녀는 돌아보았고, 근심에 찌든 그녀의 얼굴에는 저도 모르게 반가움이 아니라 놀라움이 떠올랐다."], ["She got up and embraced her sister-in-law.", "그녀는 일어나 시누이를 껴안았다."]], [["“What, here already!”", "\"어머, 벌써 왔어요!\""], ["she said as she kissed her.", "그녀는 입을 맞추며 말했다."]], [["“Dolly, how glad I am to see you!”", "\"돌리, 만나서 정말 기뻐요!\""]], [["“I am glad, too,”", "\"나도 기뻐요.\""], ["said Dolly, faintly smiling, and trying by the expression of Anna’s face to find out whether she knew.", "돌리는 희미하게 미소 지으며, 안나의 얼굴 표정에서 그녀가 알고 있는지를 알아내려 애쓰며 말했다."], ["“Most likely she knows,”", "\"아마 알고 있을 거야.\""], ["she thought, noticing the sympathy in Anna’s face.", "그녀는 안나의 얼굴에 어린 연민을 알아채고 생각했다."], ["“Well, come along, I’ll take you to your room,”", "\"자, 어서 와요, 방으로 안내할게요.\""], ["she went on, trying to defer as long as possible the moment of confidences.", "그녀는 속내를 털어놓는 순간을 되도록 오래 미루려 애쓰며 말을 이었다."]], [["“Is this Grisha?", "\"얘가 그리샤인가요?"], ["Heavens, how he’s grown!”", "어머나, 정말 많이 컸네요!\""], ["said Anna; and kissing him, never taking her eyes off Dolly, she stood still and flushed a little.", "안나가 말했다. 그러고는 돌리에게서 눈을 떼지 않은 채 아이에게 입을 맞추고는, 가만히 멈춰 서서 얼굴을 살짝 붉혔다."], ["“No, please, let us stay here.”", "\"아니에요, 부탁이니, 우리 그냥 여기 있어요.\""]], [["She took off her kerchief and her hat, and catching it in a lock of her black hair, which was a mass of curls, she tossed her head and shook her hair down.", "그녀는 머릿수건과 모자를 벗었는데, 모자가 곱슬곱슬한 검은 머리카락 한 가닥에 걸리자 고개를 흔들어 머리카락을 풀어 내렸다."]], [["“You are radiant with health and happiness!”", "\"건강과 행복으로 빛나고 있네요!\""], ["said Dolly, almost with envy.", "돌리가 거의 부러움 어린 어조로 말했다."]], [["“I?... Yes,”", "\"내가요?……"], ["said Anna.", "그래요.\""], ["“Merciful heavens, Tanya!", "안나가 말했다. \"어머나, 타냐!"], ["You’re the same age as my Seryozha,”", "우리 세료자랑 동갑이네.\""], ["she added, addressing the little girl as she ran in.", "그녀는 뛰어 들어오는 여자아이를 보고 그렇게 덧붙였다."], ["She took her in her arms and kissed her.", "그녀는 아이를 안아 들고 입을 맞췄다."], ["“Delightful child, delightful!", "\"정말 사랑스러운 아이네, 사랑스러워!"], ["Show me them all.”", "아이들을 모두 보여 줘요.\""]], [["She mentioned them, not only remembering the names, but the years, months, characters, illnesses of all the children, and Dolly could not but appreciate that.", "그녀는 이름뿐만 아니라 나이며 개월 수며 성격이며 앓았던 병까지, 아이들 하나하나를 기억하며 일일이 입에 올렸고, 돌리는 그것을 고맙게 여기지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["“Very well, we will go to them,”", "\"좋아요, 아이들에게 가 봐요.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“It’s a pity Vassya’s asleep.”", "\"바샤가 자고 있는 게 안타깝네요.\""]], [["After seeing the children, they sat down, alone now, in the drawing-room, to coffee.", "아이들을 둘러본 뒤, 두 사람은 이제 단둘이 응접실에 앉아 커피를 마셨다."], ["Anna took the tray, and then pushed it away from her.", "안나는 쟁반을 집어 들었다가, 이내 자기 앞에서 밀쳐 냈다."]], [["“Dolly,”", "\"돌리.\""], ["she said, “he has told me.”", "그녀가 말했다. \"그이가 나한테 다 말했어요.\""]], [["Dolly looked coldly at Anna; she was waiting now for phrases of conventional sympathy, but Anna said nothing of the sort.", "돌리는 안나를 차갑게 바라보았다. 이제 그녀는 판에 박힌 위로의 말이 나오기를 기다리고 있었지만, 안나는 그런 말은 한마디도 하지 않았다."]], [["“Dolly, dear,”", "\"돌리, 그대.\""], ["she said, “I don’t want to speak for him to you, nor to try to comfort you; that’s impossible.", "그녀가 말했다. \"나는 그이를 두둔하려는 것도 아니고, 그대를 위로하려는 것도 아니에요."], ["But, darling, I’m simply sorry, sorry from my heart for you!”", "그런 건 불가능하니까요. 하지만, 그대, 나는 그저 마음이 아파요, 진심으로 그대가 안쓰러워요!\""]], [["Under the thick lashes of her shining eyes tears suddenly glittered.", "반짝이는 그녀의 두 눈, 그 짙은 속눈썹 아래로 갑자기 눈물이 글썽였다."], ["She moved nearer to her sister-in-law and took her hand in her vigorous little hand.", "그녀는 시누이에게 더 가까이 다가앉아, 야무진 작은 손으로 돌리의 손을 잡았다."], ["Dolly did not shrink away, but her face did not lose its frigid expression.", "돌리는 손을 빼지는 않았지만, 그녀의 얼굴은 여전히 차가운 표정을 잃지 않았다."], ["She said:", "그녀는 말했다."]], [["“To comfort me’s impossible.", "\"나를 위로하는 건 불가능해요."], ["Everything’s lost after what has happened, everything’s over!”", "이런 일이 벌어진 뒤로는 모든 게 끝장났어요, 모든 게 다 끝났다고요!\""]], [["And directly she had said this, her face suddenly softened.", "그렇게 말하자마자, 그녀의 얼굴은 갑자기 부드러워졌다."], ["Anna lifted the wasted, thin hand of Dolly, kissed it and said:", "안나는 돌리의 야위고 가느다란 손을 들어 올려 입을 맞추고는 말했다."]], [["“But, Dolly, what’s to be done, what’s to be done?", "\"하지만, 돌리, 어떻게 해야 하죠, 어떻게 해야 좋을까요?"], ["How is it best to act in this awful position—that’s what you must think of.”", "이 끔찍한 상황에서 어떻게 처신하는 것이 가장 나은지—그대가 생각해야 할 건 바로 그거예요.\""]], [["“All’s over, and there’s nothing more,”", "\"다 끝났어요, 그뿐이에요.\""], ["said Dolly.", "돌리가 말했다."], ["“And the worst of all is, you see, that I can’t cast him off: there are the children, I am tied.", "\"그리고 무엇보다 끔찍한 건, 그러니까, 내가 그이를 내칠 수 없다는 거예요. 아이들이 있으니까, 나는 묶여 있는 거죠."], ["And I can’t live with him!", "그렇다고 그이와 함께 살 수도 없어요!"], ["it’s a torture to me to see him.”", "그이를 보는 것 자체가 나에겐 고문이라고요.\""]], [["“Dolly, darling, he has spoken to me, but I want to hear it from you: tell me about it.”", "\"돌리, 그대, 그이가 나한테 이야기하긴 했지만, 나는 그대 입으로 듣고 싶어요. 그 일에 대해 말해 줘요.\""]], [["Dolly looked at her inquiringly.", "돌리는 캐묻는 눈으로 그녀를 바라보았다."]], [["Sympathy and love unfeigned were visible on Anna’s face.", "안나의 얼굴에는 꾸밈없는 연민과 사랑이 드러나 있었다."]], [["“Very well,”", "\"좋아요.\""], ["she said all at once.", "그녀가 불쑥 말했다."], ["“But I will tell you it from the beginning.", "\"하지만 처음부터 다 이야기할게요."], ["You know how I was married.", "내가 어떻게 결혼했는지 알잖아요."], ["With the education mamma gave us I was more than innocent, I was stupid.", "엄마가 우리한테 시킨 그런 교육을 받아서, 나는 순진하다 못해 어리석을 정도였어요."], ["I knew nothing.", "아무것도 몰랐죠."], ["I know they say men tell their wives of their former lives, but Stiva”—she corrected herself—“Stepan Arkadyevitch told me nothing.", "남자들은 아내에게 자기 과거를 이야기한다고들 하지만, 스티바는\"—그녀는 말을 고쳤다—\"스테판 아르카지치는 나에게 아무 말도 하지 않았어요."], ["You’ll hardly believe it, but till now I imagined that I was the only woman he had known.", "믿기 어렵겠지만, 나는 지금까지도 그이가 알았던 여자가 나 하나뿐이라고 생각했어요."], ["So I lived eight years.", "그렇게 팔 년을 살았어요."], ["You must understand that I was so far from suspecting infidelity, I regarded it as impossible, and then—try to imagine it—with such ideas, to find out suddenly all the horror, all the loathsomeness....", "알아 둬요, 나는 부정을 의심하기는커녕, 그런 일은 있을 수 없다고 여겼고, 그런데—한번 상상해 봐요—그런 생각을 품고 있다가, 갑자기 그 모든 끔찍함과 역겨움을 알게 된 거예요……"], ["You must try and understand me.", "나를 좀 이해하려고 해 봐요."], ["To be fully convinced of one’s happiness, and all at once....”", "자기 행복을 철석같이 믿고 있다가, 한순간에……\""], ["continued Dolly, holding back her sobs, “to get a letter ...", "돌리는 흐느낌을 억누르며 말을 이었다. \"편지 한 장을 받았어요……"], ["his letter to his mistress, my governess. No, it’s too awful!”", "그이가 자기 정부에게, 우리 집 가정교사에게 쓴 편지를요."], ["She hastily pulled out her handkerchief and hid her face in it.", "안 돼요, 너무 끔찍해요!\" 그녀는 황급히 손수건을 꺼내 얼굴을 묻었다."], ["“I can understand being carried away by feeling,”", "\"감정에 휩쓸리는 건 이해할 수 있어요.\""], ["she went on after a brief silence, “but deliberately, slyly deceiving me ...", "그녀는 잠시 침묵한 뒤 말을 이었다. \"하지만 일부러, 음흉하게 나를 속이다니……"], ["and with whom?...", "그것도 누구하고?……"], ["To go on being my husband together with her ...", "그 여자랑 함께 지내면서 계속 내 남편 노릇을 하다니……"], ["it’s awful!", "너무 끔찍해요!"], ["You can’t understand....”", "그댄 이해 못 할 거예요……\""]], [["“Oh, yes, I understand!", "\"오, 맞아요, 나는 이해해요!"], ["I understand!", "이해한다고요!"], ["Dolly, dearest, I do understand,”", "돌리, 가엾은 그대, 정말로 이해해요.\""], ["said Anna, pressing her hand.", "안나가 그녀의 손을 꼭 쥐며 말했다."]], [["“And do you imagine he realizes all the awfulness of my position?”", "\"그리고 그이가 내 처지가 얼마나 끔찍한지 알기나 한다고 생각해요?\""], ["Dolly resumed.", "돌리가 다시 말을 이었다."], ["“Not the slightest!", "\"조금도 몰라요!"], ["He’s happy and contented.”", "행복하고 만족스러워하고 있다고요.\""]], [["“Oh, no!”", "\"오, 아니에요!\""], ["Anna interposed quickly.", "안나가 얼른 끼어들었다."], ["“He’s to be pitied, he’s weighed down by remorse....”", "\"그이는 가엾은 사람이에요, 자책으로 짓눌려 있어요……\""]], [["“Is he capable of remorse?”", "\"그이가 자책 같은 걸 할 줄 알기나 해요?\""], ["Dolly interrupted, gazing intently into her sister-in-law’s face.", "돌리가 시누이의 얼굴을 뚫어지게 바라보며 말을 가로막았다."]], [["“Yes.", "\"네."], ["I know him.", "나는 그이를 알아요."], ["I could not look at him without feeling sorry for him.", "그이를 보면 안쓰럽지 않을 수가 없었어요."], ["We both know him.", "우리 둘 다 그이를 알잖아요."], ["He’s good-hearted, but he’s proud, and now he’s so humiliated.", "그이는 마음씨는 착하지만 자존심이 강한데, 지금 너무나도 비참해진 거예요."], ["What touched me most....”", "무엇보다 내 마음을 아프게 한 건……\""], ["(and here Anna guessed what would touch Dolly most) “he’s tortured by two things: that he’s ashamed for the children’s sake, and that, loving you—yes, yes, loving you beyond everything on earth,”", "(그리고 여기서 안나는 무엇이 돌리의 마음을 가장 울릴지 헤아렸다) \"그이는 두 가지 때문에 괴로워하고 있어요. 하나는 아이들을 볼 면목이 없다는 것, 또 하나는 그대를 사랑하면서도—그래요, 그래요, 그대를 이 세상 그 무엇보다 사랑하면서도,\""], ["she hurriedly interrupted Dolly, who would have answered—“he has hurt you, pierced you to the heart.", "그녀는 대답하려는 돌리의 말을 황급히 막았다. \"그대에게 상처를 주고, 가슴을 후벼 팠다는 거예요."], ["‘No, no, she cannot forgive me,’ he keeps saying.”", "'아니야, 아니야, 그 사람은 나를 용서하지 못할 거야.' 그이는 자꾸만 그렇게 말해요.\""]], [["Dolly looked dreamily away beyond her sister-in-law as she listened to her words.", "돌리는 그녀의 말을 들으면서 시누이 너머 먼 곳을 꿈꾸듯 바라보았다."]], [["“Yes, I can see that his position is awful; it’s worse for the guilty than the innocent,”", "\"그래요, 그이의 처지가 끔찍하다는 건 알아요. 죄지은 사람은 죄 없는 사람보다 더 괴로운 법이니까요.\""], ["she said, “if he feels that all the misery comes from his fault.", "그녀가 말했다. \"이 모든 불행이 자기 잘못에서 비롯됐다는 걸 느낀다면 말이에요."], ["But how am I to forgive him, how am I to be his wife again after her?", "하지만 내가 어떻게 그이를 용서하죠, 그 여자가 있고 난 뒤에 내가 어떻게 다시 그이의 아내가 되겠어요?"], ["For me to live with him now would be torture, just because I love my past love for him....”", "지금 그이와 함께 산다는 건 나에겐 고문일 거예요, 바로 내가 그이를 향한 지난날의 사랑을 사랑하고 있기 때문에……\""]], [["And sobs cut short her words.", "그리고 흐느낌이 그녀의 말을 끊었다."], ["But as though of set design, each time she was softened she began to speak again of what exasperated her.", "하지만 마치 일부러 그러기로 작정이라도 한 듯이, 마음이 누그러질 때마다 그녀는 자기를 격분하게 만드는 일에 대해 다시 이야기하기 시작했다."]], [["“She’s young, you see, she’s pretty,”", "\"그 여잔 젊잖아요, 알다시피, 예쁘고요.\""], ["she went on.", "그녀가 말을 이었다."], ["“Do you know, Anna, my youth and my beauty are gone, taken by whom?", "\"알아요, 안나, 내 젊음도 내 아름다움도 다 사라졌어요, 누가 가져갔게요?"], ["By him and his children.", "그이와 그이의 아이들이 가져갔어요."], ["I have worked for him, and all I had has gone in his service, and now of course any fresh, vulgar creature has more charm for him.", "나는 그이를 위해 일했고, 내가 가진 모든 걸 그이에게 바쳤는데, 이제 와서 당연하게도 싱싱하고 천박한 계집이 그이에겐 더 매력적인 거예요."], ["No doubt they talked of me together, or, worse still, they were silent.", "틀림없이 그 둘은 함께 내 이야기를 했거나, 아니 더 나쁘게는, 입을 다물고 있었겠죠."], ["Do you understand?”", "무슨 말인지 알겠어요?\""]], [["Again her eyes glowed with hatred.", "다시 그녀의 두 눈이 증오로 이글거렸다."]], [["“And after that he will tell me....", "\"그러고도 그이는 나한테 말하겠죠…… 뭐라고요!"], ["What! can I believe him?", "내가 그이를 믿을 수 있겠어요?"], ["Never!", "절대로!"], ["No, everything is over, everything that once made my comfort, the reward of my work, and my sufferings....", "안 돼요, 모든 게 끝났어요, 한때 내 위안이었고, 내 노고와 내 고통에 대한 보상이었던 모든 것이……"], ["Would you believe it, I was teaching Grisha just now: once this was a joy to me, now it is a torture.", "믿어지나요, 방금 전에 나는 그리샤를 가르치고 있었어요. 한때는 그게 나에게 기쁨이었는데, 지금은 고문이에요."], ["What have I to strive and toil for?", "내가 무엇을 위해 애쓰고 수고해야 하죠?"], ["Why are the children here?", "무엇 때문에 아이들이 여기 있는 거죠?"], ["What’s so awful is that all at once my heart’s turned, and instead of love and tenderness, I have nothing but hatred for him; yes, hatred.", "정말 끔찍한 건, 한순간에 내 마음이 뒤집혀서, 사랑과 애정 대신 그이에 대한 증오밖에 남지 않았다는 거예요. 그래요, 증오요."], ["I could kill him.”", "그이를 죽여 버릴 수도 있을 것 같아요.\""]], [["“Darling Dolly, I understand, but don’t torture yourself.", "\"가엾은 돌리, 나는 이해해요, 하지만 자신을 괴롭히지 말아요."], ["You are so distressed, so overwrought, that you look at many things mistakenly.”", "그대는 너무 상심하고 너무 흥분한 나머지, 많은 것을 잘못 보고 있어요.\""]], [["Dolly grew calmer, and for two minutes both were silent.", "돌리는 차츰 진정되었고, 두 사람은 잠시 동안 말이 없었다."]], [["“What’s to be done?", "\"어떻게 하면 좋죠?"], ["Think for me, Anna, help me.", "나 대신 생각해 줘요, 안나, 나를 도와줘요."], ["I have thought over everything, and I see nothing.”", "나는 모든 걸 다 생각해 봤지만, 아무것도 안 보여요.\""]], [["Anna could think of nothing, but her heart responded instantly to each word, to each change of expression of her sister-in-law.", "안나도 아무것도 떠오르지 않았지만, 그녀의 마음은 시누이의 말 하나하나에, 표정의 변화 하나하나에 곧바로 응답했다."]], [["“One thing I would say,”", "\"한 가지만 말할게요.\""], ["began Anna.", "안나가 입을 열었다."], ["“I am his sister, I know his character, that faculty of forgetting everything, everything”", "\"나는 그이의 누이라서 그이의 성격을 알아요. 무엇이든, 무엇이든 다 잊어버리는 그 버릇\""], ["(she waved her hand before her forehead), “that faculty for being completely carried away, but for completely repenting too.", "(그녀는 자기 이마 앞에서 손을 내저었다), \"완전히 휩쓸려 버리는 그 버릇, 하지만 또 완전히 뉘우치기도 하는 그 버릇 말이에요."], ["He cannot believe it, he cannot comprehend now how he can have acted as he did.”", "그이는 도무지 믿을 수 없는 거예요, 자기가 어떻게 그런 짓을 저질렀는지 지금으로선 도무지 이해할 수 없는 거예요.\""]], [["“No; he understands, he understood!”", "\"아니에요. 그인 다 알아요, 알고 있었다고요!\""], ["Dolly broke in.", "돌리가 끼어들었다."], ["“But I ...", "\"하지만 나는……"], ["you are forgetting me ...", "그대는 나를 잊고 있어요……"], ["does it make it easier for me?”", "그런다고 내 마음이 편해지나요?\""]], [["“Wait a minute.", "\"잠깐만요."], ["When he told me, I will own I did not realize all the awfulness of your position.", "그이가 나한테 이야기했을 때, 솔직히 말하면 나는 그대 처지가 얼마나 끔찍한지 미처 다 깨닫지 못했어요."], ["I saw nothing but him, and that the family was broken up.", "그이밖에 안 보였고, 한 가정이 깨졌다는 것밖에 안 보였어요."], ["I felt sorry for him, but after talking to you, I see it, as a woman, quite differently.", "그이가 안쓰러웠죠. 하지만 그대와 이야기하고 나니, 같은 여자로서, 전혀 다르게 보여요."], ["I see your agony, and I can’t tell you how sorry I am for you!", "그대의 고통이 보이고, 그대가 얼마나 안쓰러운지 이루 말할 수가 없어요!"], ["But, Dolly, darling, I fully realize your sufferings, only there is one thing I don’t know; I don’t know ...", "하지만, 돌리, 그대, 나는 그대의 괴로움을 충분히 알아요, 다만 한 가지 모르는 게 있어요. 나는 모르겠어요……"], ["I don’t know how much love there is still in your heart for him.", "모르겠어요…… 그대 마음에 아직 그이를 향한 사랑이 얼마나 남아 있는지를요."], ["That you know—whether there is enough for you to be able to forgive him.", "그건 그대만이 알겠죠—그이를 용서할 수 있을 만큼의 사랑이 남아 있는지를요."], ["If there is, forgive him!”", "만약 남아 있다면, 그이를 용서해요!\""]], [["“No,”", "\"아니에요.\""], ["Dolly was beginning, but Anna cut her short, kissing her hand once more.", "돌리가 말을 꺼내려 했지만, 안나는 다시 한번 그녀의 손에 입을 맞추며 그 말을 잘랐다."]], [["“I know more of the world than you do,”", "\"나는 그대보다 세상을 더 많이 알아요.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“I know how men like Stiva look at it.", "\"스티바 같은 남자들이 그걸 어떻게 보는지 나는 알아요."], ["You speak of his talking of you with her. That never happened.", "그대는 그이가 그 여자와 함께 그대 이야기를 했다고 말하지만, 그런 일은 결코 없었어요."], ["Such men are unfaithful, but their home and wife are sacred to them.", "그런 남자들은 부정을 저지르긴 해도, 자기 가정과 아내는 신성하게 여겨요."], ["Somehow or other these women are still looked on with contempt by them, and do not touch on their feeling for their family.", "어찌 된 일인지 그런 여자들은 그들에게는 여전히 경멸의 대상일 뿐이고, 그들이 가족에게 품은 감정에는 손끝 하나 닿지 못해요."], ["They draw a sort of line that can’t be crossed between them and their families.", "그들은 자기들과 가족 사이에 절대 넘을 수 없는 일종의 선을 그어 놓는 거예요."], ["I don’t understand it, but it is so.”", "나는 이해할 수 없지만, 어쨌든 그래요.\""]], [["“Yes, but he has kissed her....”", "\"그래요, 하지만 그이는 그 여자에게 입을 맞췄잖아요……\""]], [["“Dolly, hush, darling.", "\"돌리, 쉿, 그대."], ["I saw Stiva when he was in love with you.", "나는 스티바가 그대를 사랑하던 때를 봤어요. 그이가 나를 찾아와 그대 이야기를 하며 울던 때를, 그대를 향한 그이의 감정이 얼마나 시적이고 고결했는지를 나는 기억해요."], ["I remember the time when he came to me and cried, talking of you, and all the poetry and loftiness of his feeling for you, and I know that the longer he has lived with you the loftier you have been in his eyes.", "그리고 그대와 오래 살면 살수록 그이의 눈에 그대가 점점 더 고결하게 비쳤다는 걸 나는 알아요. 알다시피 우리는 그이가 말끝마다 '돌리는 정말 굉장한 여자야'라고 끼워 넣는 것을 두고 가끔 놀려 대곤 했잖아요."], ["You know we have sometimes laughed at him for putting in at every word: ‘Dolly’s a marvelous woman.’ You have always been a divinity for him, and you are that still, and this has not been an infidelity of the heart....”", "그대는 늘 그이에게 여신 같은 존재였고, 지금도 여전히 그래요. 이건 마음에서 우러난 부정이 아니었어요……\""]], [["“But if it is repeated?”", "\"하지만 만약 또 그런다면요?\""]], [["“It cannot be, as I understand it....”", "\"내가 아는 한, 그럴 수는 없어요……\""]], [["“Yes, but could you forgive it?”", "\"그래요, 하지만 그댄 그런 일을 용서할 수 있겠어요?\""]], [["“I don’t know, I can’t judge....", "\"모르겠어요, 나는 판단할 수가 없어요…… 아니, 할 수 있어요.\""], ["Yes, I can,”", "안나는 잠시 생각하더니 말했다."], ["said Anna, thinking a moment; and grasping the position in her thought and weighing it in her inner balance, she added: “Yes, I can, I can, I can.", "그러고는 그 상황을 머릿속으로 헤아려 자기 내면의 저울에 달아 본 뒤 덧붙였다. \"네, 할 수 있어요, 할 수 있어요, 할 수 있어요."], ["Yes, I could forgive it.", "네, 나라면 용서할 수 있을 거예요."], ["I could not be the same, no; but I could forgive it, and forgive it as though it had never been, never been at all....”", "예전과 똑같을 수는 없겠죠, 그래요. 하지만 용서할 수 있을 거예요, 마치 아예 없었던 일처럼, 처음부터 전혀 없었던 일처럼 용서할 거예요……\""]], [["“Oh, of course,”", "\"오, 물론이죠.\""], ["Dolly interposed quickly, as though saying what she had more than once thought, “else it would not be forgiveness.", "돌리가, 한 번 이상 생각해 본 것을 말하기라도 하듯, 얼른 끼어들었다. \"안 그러면 용서가 아니죠."], ["If one forgives, it must be completely, completely.", "용서한다면, 완전히, 완전히 해야 해요."], ["Come, let us go; I’ll take you to your room,”", "자, 가요. 방으로 안내할게요.\""], ["she said, getting up, and on the way she embraced Anna.", "그녀는 일어서며 말하고는, 가는 길에 안나를 껴안았다."], ["“My dear, how glad I am you came.", "\"그대, 와 줘서 정말 기뻐요."], ["It has made things better, ever so much better.”", "덕분에 한결 나아졌어요, 훨씬 더 나아졌어요.\""]], [["# Chapter 20", "# Chapter 20"]], [["The whole of that day Anna spent at home, that’s to say at the Oblonskys’, and received no one, though some of her acquaintances had already heard of her arrival, and came to call the same day.", "그날 하루 종일 안나는 집에서, 그러니까 오블론스키 집안에서 보냈고, 몇몇 지인은 이미 그녀가 도착했다는 소식을 듣고 그날로 찾아오기도 했지만 아무도 만나지 않았다."], ["Anna spent the whole morning with Dolly and the children.", "안나는 오전 내내 돌리와 아이들과 함께 지냈다."], ["She merely sent a brief note to her brother to tell him that he must not fail to dine at home.", "그녀는 그저 오빠에게, 집에서 꼭 저녁을 먹어야 한다고 짧은 쪽지를 보냈을 뿐이다. \"오세요, 하느님은 자비로우시니까요.\""], ["“Come, God is merciful,” she wrote.", "그녀는 그렇게 적었다."]], [["Oblonsky did dine at home: the conversation was general, and his wife, speaking to him, addressed him as “Stiva,”", "오블론스키는 정말로 집에서 저녁을 먹었다. 대화는 모두 함께 나누는 것이었고, 그의 아내는 그에게 말을 건넬 때 전에는 그러지 않았던 \"스티바\"라는 호칭으로 그를 불렀다."], ["as she had not done before. In the relations of the husband and wife the same estrangement still remained, but there was no talk now of separation, and Stepan Arkadyevitch saw the possibility of explanation and reconciliation.", "부부 사이에는 여전히 똑같은 서먹함이 남아 있었지만, 이제 별거 이야기는 나오지 않았고, 스테판 아르카지치는 해명과 화해의 가능성을 보았다."]], [["Immediately after dinner Kitty came in.", "저녁 식사가 끝나자마자 키티가 들어왔다."], ["She knew Anna Arkadyevna, but only very slightly, and she came now to her sister’s with some trepidation, at the prospect of meeting this fashionable Petersburg lady, whom everyone spoke so highly of.", "그녀는 안나 아르카지예브나를 알기는 했으나 그저 아주 어렴풋이 알 뿐이었고, 모두가 그토록 높이 칭송하는 이 세련된 페테르부르크 부인을 만난다는 생각에 적잖이 가슴을 두근거리며 언니 집을 찾아왔다."], ["But she made a favorable impression on Anna Arkadyevna—she saw that at once.", "그러나 그녀는 안나 아르카지예브나에게 호감을 주었다—키티는 그것을 단번에 알아차렸다."], ["Anna was unmistakably admiring her loveliness and her youth: before Kitty knew where she was she found herself not merely under Anna’s sway, but in love with her, as young girls do fall in love with older and married women.", "안나는 분명 그녀의 사랑스러움과 젊음을 감탄하며 바라보고 있었다. 키티는 자기가 어찌 된 영문인지 깨닫기도 전에, 그저 안나의 매력에 사로잡힌 정도가 아니라 그녀에게 반해 버린 자신을 발견했다."], ["Anna was not like a fashionable lady, nor the mother of a boy of eight years old.", "어린 처녀들이 나이 든 기혼 여인에게 곧잘 반하듯이."], ["In the elasticity of her movements, the freshness and the unflagging eagerness which persisted in her face, and broke out in her smile and her glance, she would rather have passed for a girl of twenty, had it not been for a serious and at times mournful look in her eyes, which struck and attracted Kitty.", "안나는 세련된 부인 같지도, 여덟 살배기 사내아이의 어머니 같지도 않았다. 그녀의 움직임에 깃든 탄력, 그녀의 얼굴에 어려 미소와 눈빛으로 번지는 그 싱그러움과 가실 줄 모르는 생기를 보면, 키티의 마음을 두드리며 끌어당기던 그 진지하고 때로 우수에 잠긴 눈빛만 아니었다면 차라리 스무 살 아가씨로 보였을 것이다."], ["Kitty felt that Anna was perfectly simple and was concealing nothing, but that she had another higher world of interests inaccessible to her, complex and poetic.", "키티는 안나가 더없이 소박하고 아무것도 감추지 않는다는 것을, 그러나 자기로서는 닿을 수 없는, 복잡하고 시적인 또 다른 더 높은 관심의 세계를 지니고 있다는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["After dinner, when Dolly went away to her own room, Anna rose quickly and went up to her brother, who was just lighting a cigar.", "저녁 식사가 끝나고 돌리가 자기 방으로 물러가자, 안나는 잽싸게 일어나 막 시가에 불을 붙이고 있던 오빠에게 다가갔다."]], [["“Stiva,”", "\"스티바,\""], ["she said to him, winking gaily, crossing him and glancing towards the door, “go, and God help you.”", "그녀는 명랑하게 윙크하며, 그에게 성호를 그어 주고는 문 쪽을 흘긋 보며 말했다. \"가, 하느님이 도와주실 거야.\""]], [["He threw down the cigar, understanding her, and departed through the doorway.", "그는 그녀의 뜻을 알아채고 시가를 던져 버린 뒤 문을 지나 나갔다."]], [["When Stepan Arkadyevitch had disappeared, she went back to the sofa where she had been sitting, surrounded by the children.", "스테판 아르카지치가 사라지자, 그녀는 아이들에게 둘러싸인 채 앉아 있던 소파로 돌아갔다."], ["Either because the children saw that their mother was fond of this aunt, or that they felt a special charm in her themselves, the two elder ones, and the younger following their lead, as children so often do, had clung about their new aunt since before dinner, and would not leave her side.", "아이들이 자기 엄마가 이 이모를 좋아한다는 것을 알아서인지, 아니면 그들 스스로 그녀에게서 특별한 매력을 느껴서인지, 위의 두 아이와 그 뒤를 따르는 막내까지, 아이들이 흔히 그러듯, 저녁 식사 전부터 이 새 이모에게 들러붙어 그녀 곁을 떠나려 하지 않았다."], ["And it had become a sort of game among them to sit as close as possible to their aunt, to touch her, hold her little hand, kiss it, play with her ring, or even touch the flounce of her skirt.", "그리고 이모에게 되도록 바싹 붙어 앉아 그녀를 만지고, 그 작은 손을 잡고, 거기에 입을 맞추고, 반지를 가지고 놀거나 심지어 치맛단 주름을 만지작거리는 것이 그들 사이에서는 일종의 놀이가 되어 있었다."]], [["“Come, come, as we were sitting before,”", "\"자, 자, 우리 아까처럼 앉자.\""], ["said Anna Arkadyevna, sitting down in her place.", "안나 아르카지예브나가 제자리에 앉으며 말했다."]], [["And again Grisha poked his little face under her arm, and nestled with his head on her gown, beaming with pride and happiness.", "그러자 그리샤가 다시 그녀의 팔 밑으로 조그만 얼굴을 들이밀고는, 자랑스럽고 행복해서 환하게 빛나는 얼굴로 그녀의 옷자락에 머리를 기댔다."]], [["“And when is your next ball?”", "\"다음 무도회는 언제니?\""], ["she asked Kitty.", "그녀가 키티에게 물었다."]], [["“Next week, and a splendid ball.", "\"다음 주예요, 아주 멋진 무도회죠."], ["One of those balls where one always enjoys oneself.”", "언제나 즐거운 그런 무도회 중 하나예요.\""]], [["“Why, are there balls where one always enjoys oneself?”", "\"아니, 언제나 즐거운 무도회 같은 게 있어?\""], ["Anna said, with tender irony.", "안나가 다정한 비꼼을 섞어 말했다."]], [["“It’s strange, but there are.", "\"이상하지만, 있어요."], ["At the Bobrishtchevs’ one always enjoys oneself, and at the Nikitins’ too, while at the Mezhkovs’ it’s always dull.", "보브리시체프 댁에서는 언제나 즐겁고, 니키틴 댁에서도 그런데, 메시코프 댁에서는 언제나 따분하거든요."], ["Haven’t you noticed it?”", "못 느끼셨어요?\""]], [["“No, my dear, for me there are no balls now where one enjoys oneself,”", "\"아니, 얘야, 나한테는 이제 즐거운 무도회 같은 건 없단다.\""], ["said Anna, and Kitty detected in her eyes that mysterious world which was not open to her.", "안나가 말했고, 키티는 그녀의 눈 속에서 자기에게는 열리지 않은 그 신비로운 세계를 알아챘다."], ["“For me there are some less dull and tiresome.”", "\"나한테는 덜 따분하고 덜 지겨운 무도회가 있을 뿐이지.\""]], [["“How can you be dull at a ball?”", "\"어떻게 무도회에서 따분하실 수가 있어요?\""]], [["“Why should not I be dull at a ball?”", "\"왜 내가 무도회에서 따분하면 안 되는데?\""], ["inquired Anna.", "안나가 물었다."]], [["Kitty perceived that Anna knew what answer would follow.", "키티는 안나가 어떤 대답이 나올지 알고 있다는 것을 알아차렸다."]], [["“Because you always look nicer than anyone.”", "\"언제나 누구보다 아름다우시니까요.\""]], [["Anna had the faculty of blushing.", "안나에게는 얼굴을 붉히는 재주가 있었다."], ["She blushed a little, and said:", "그녀는 살짝 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다."]], [["“In the first place it’s never so; and secondly, if it were, what difference would it make to me?”", "\"첫째로 그건 결코 그렇지가 않고, 둘째로 설령 그렇다 한들 그게 나한테 무슨 상관이겠니?\""]], [["“Are you coming to this ball?”", "\"이번 무도회에 오실 거예요?\""], ["asked Kitty.", "키티가 물었다."]], [["“I imagine it won’t be possible to avoid going.", "\"안 갈 수가 없을 것 같구나."], ["Here, take it,”", "자, 이거 받으렴.\""], ["she said to Tanya, who was pulling the loosely-fitting ring off her white, slender-tipped finger.", "그녀가, 가느다란 손가락 끝에서 헐겁게 끼워진 반지를 빼고 있던 타냐에게 말했다."]], [["“I shall be so glad if you go.", "\"오시면 정말 기쁠 거예요."], ["I should so like to see you at a ball.”", "무도회에서 이모님을 너무 뵙고 싶어요.\""]], [["“Anyway, if I do go, I shall comfort myself with the thought that it’s a pleasure to you ...", "\"어쨌든 내가 가게 되면, 그게 너에게 기쁨이 된다는 생각으로 마음을 달래야겠구나……"], ["Grisha, don’t pull my hair.", "그리샤, 머리 잡아당기지 마라."], ["It’s untidy enough without that,”", "그러지 않아도 충분히 헝클어졌으니까.\""], ["she said, putting up a straying lock, which Grisha had been playing with.", "그녀가 그리샤가 가지고 놀던, 흘러내린 머리카락 한 가닥을 매만지며 말했다."]], [["“I imagine you at the ball in lilac.”", "\"전 이모님이 연보랏빛 옷을 입고 무도회에 계신 모습을 그려 봐요.\""]], [["“And why in lilac precisely?”", "\"왜 하필 연보랏빛이니?\""], ["asked Anna, smiling.", "안나가 미소 지으며 물었다."], ["“Now, children, run along, run along.", "\"자, 얘들아, 어서 가, 어서 가렴."], ["Do you hear?", "들리니?"], ["Miss Hoole is calling you to tea,”", "훌 선생님이 차 마시러 오라고 부르신다.\""], ["she said, tearing the children from her, and sending them off to the dining-room.", "그녀는 아이들을 떼어 내며 식당으로 보냈다."]], [["“I know why you press me to come to the ball.", "\"이모님이 왜 저더러 무도회에 오라고 자꾸 채근하시는지 알아요."], ["You expect a great deal of this ball, and you want everyone to be there to take part in it.”", "이모님은 이 무도회에 큰 기대를 걸고 계셔서, 거기 다들 와서 함께하기를 바라시는 거예요.\""]], [["“How do you know?", "\"어떻게 알았니?"], ["Yes.”", "그래, 맞아.\""]], [["“Oh! what a happy time you are at,”", "\"아, 너는 참 행복한 시절을 보내고 있구나.\""], ["pursued Anna.", "안나가 말을 이었다."], ["“I remember, and I know that blue haze like the mist on the mountains in Switzerland.", "\"나도 기억하고 있단다. 스위스 산을 덮은 안개 같은 그 푸른 아지랑이를 알아."], ["That mist which covers everything in that blissful time when childhood is just ending, and out of that vast circle, happy and gay, there is a path growing narrower and narrower, and it is delightful and alarming to enter the ballroom, bright and splendid as it is....", "어린 시절이 막 끝나 가는 그 더없이 행복한 시절의 모든 것을 감싸는 그 안개 말이야. 행복하고 즐거운 그 드넓은 동그라미에서 점점 좁아져 가는 길 하나가 뻗어 나오고, 그토록 환하고 화려한 무도회장으로 들어서는 일은 황홀하면서도 두렵지……"], ["Who has not been through it?”", "그 시절을 안 겪은 사람이 어디 있겠니?\""]], [["Kitty smiled without speaking.", "키티는 말없이 미소 지었다."], ["“But how did she go through it?", "'하지만 이모는 어떻게 그 시절을 겪었을까?"], ["How I should like to know all her love story!”", "이모의 사랑 이야기를 죄다 알 수만 있다면!'"], ["thought Kitty, recalling the unromantic appearance of Alexey Alexandrovitch, her husband.", "키티는 그녀의 남편인 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 낭만이라곤 없는 모습을 떠올리며 생각했다."]], [["“I know something.", "\"나도 좀 알고 있단다."], ["Stiva told me, and I congratulate you.", "스티바가 얘기해 주었어, 축하한다."], ["I liked him so much,” Anna continued.", "난 그 사람이 무척 마음에 들더라.\""], ["“I met Vronsky at the railway station.”", "안나가 말을 이었다. \"기차역에서 브론스키를 만났어.\""]], [["“Oh, was he there?”", "\"아, 그분이 거기 계셨어요?\""], ["asked Kitty, blushing.", "키티가 얼굴을 붉히며 물었다."], ["“What was it Stiva told you?”", "\"스티바 오빠가 이모님께 무슨 말을 했어요?\""]], [["“Stiva gossiped about it all.", "\"스티바가 그 이야기를 죄다 떠벌렸지."], ["And I should be so glad ...", "그리고 나도 정말 기쁠 거야……"], ["I traveled yesterday with Vronsky’s mother,”", "어제 나는 브론스키의 어머니와 함께 여행했단다.\" 그녀가 말을 이었다."], ["she went on; “and his mother talked without a pause of him, he’s her favorite.", "\"그분 어머니는 쉴 새 없이 아들 이야기를 하시더구나, 그 사람이 어머니가 가장 아끼는 아들이거든."], ["I know mothers are partial, but....”", "어머니들이란 으레 편애하기 마련인 줄은 알지만……\""]], [["“What did his mother tell you?”", "\"그분 어머니가 뭐라고 하셨어요?\""]], [["“Oh, a great deal!", "\"아, 아주 많은 얘기를!"], ["And I know that he’s her favorite; still one can see how chivalrous he is....", "그 사람이 어머니가 가장 아끼는 아들이라는 건 나도 알겠더라."], ["Well, for instance, she told me that he had wanted to give up all his property to his brother, that he had done something extraordinary when he was quite a child, saved a woman out of the water.", "그래도 그가 얼마나 의협심이 강한지 알 수 있었어…… 그러니까 예를 들면, 그가 자기 재산을 전부 형에게 넘겨주려 했다는 거며, 아주 어릴 적에 물에 빠진 여자를 구해 낸 특별한 일을 했다는 거며."], ["He’s a hero, in fact,”", "한마디로 영웅이지.\""], ["said Anna, smiling and recollecting the two hundred roubles he had given at the station.", "안나가 미소 지으며, 그가 역에서 내놓았던 이백 루블을 떠올리며 말했다."]], [["But she did not tell Kitty about the two hundred roubles.", "그러나 그녀는 키티에게 그 이백 루블 이야기는 하지 않았다."], ["For some reason it was disagreeable to her to think of it.", "어쩐 일인지 그 일을 생각하는 것이 불쾌했다."], ["She felt that there was something that had to do with her in it, and something that ought not to have been.", "그녀는 거기에 자기와 관련된 무언가가, 그리고 있어서는 안 될 무언가가 있다고 느꼈다."]], [["“She pressed me very much to go and see her,”", "\"그분 어머니가 한번 찾아와 달라고 무척 채근하셨단다.\""], ["Anna went on; “and I shall be glad to go to see her tomorrow.", "안나가 말을 이었다. \"그래서 나도 내일 기꺼이 찾아뵐 생각이야."], ["Stiva is staying a long while in Dolly’s room, thank God,”", "스티바가 돌리 방에 오래도록 있구나, 다행이지.\""], ["Anna added, changing the subject, and getting up, Kitty fancied, displeased with something.", "안나가 화제를 바꾸며 덧붙이고는 일어섰는데, 키티가 보기에 무언가에 언짢아하는 듯했다."]], [["“No, I’m first! No, I!”", "\"아냐, 내가 먼저야!"], ["screamed the children, who had finished tea, running up to their Aunt Anna.", "아냐, 나라니까!\" 차를 다 마신 아이들이 안나 이모에게 달려오며 소리쳤다."]], [["“All together,”", "\"다 같이.\""], ["said Anna, and she ran laughing to meet them, and embraced and swung round all the throng of swarming children, shrieking with delight.", "안나는 그렇게 말하며 웃음을 터뜨리고 아이들에게 마주 달려가, 기뻐서 꺅꺅 소리 지르며 우글거리는 아이들 무리를 모조리 끌어안고 빙글빙글 돌렸다."]], [["# Chapter 21", "# Chapter 21"]], [["Dolly came out of her room to the tea of the grown-up people.", "돌리는 어른들의 차 모임에 나오려고 방에서 나왔다."], ["Stepan Arkadyevitch did not come out.", "스테판 아르카지치는 나오지 않았다."], ["He must have left his wife’s room by the other door.", "아마도 다른 문으로 아내의 방을 빠져나간 모양이었다."]], [["“I am afraid you’ll be cold upstairs,”", "\"위층은 추울까 봐 걱정이에요.\" 돌리가 안나를 향해 말했다."], ["observed Dolly, addressing Anna; “I want to move you downstairs, and we shall be nearer.”", "\"당신을 아래층으로 옮기고 싶어요. 그러면 우리도 더 가까이 있게 되고요.\""]], [["“Oh, please, don’t trouble about me,”", "\"아, 제발 저 때문에 신경 쓰지 마세요.\""], ["answered Anna, looking intently into Dolly’s face, trying to make out whether there had been a reconciliation or not.", "화해가 이루어졌는지 아닌지 가늠해 보려고 돌리의 얼굴을 뚫어지게 들여다보며 안나가 대답했다."]], [["“It will be lighter for you here,”", "\"여기가 당신한테는 더 밝을 거예요.\""], ["answered her sister-in-law.", "올케가 대답했다."]], [["“I assure you that I sleep everywhere, and always like a marmot.”", "\"정말이지 저는 어디서든 잘 자요, 늘 마멋처럼요.\""]], [["“What’s the question?”", "\"무슨 이야기지?\""], ["inquired Stepan Arkadyevitch, coming out of his room and addressing his wife.", "스테판 아르카지치가 자기 방에서 나오며 아내를 향해 물었다."]], [["From his tone both Kitty and Anna knew that a reconciliation had taken place.", "그의 말투에서 키티도 안나도 화해가 이루어졌음을 알아챘다."]], [["“I want to move Anna downstairs, but we must hang up blinds.", "\"안나를 아래층으로 옮기고 싶은데, 블라인드를 달아야 해요."], ["No one knows how to do it; I must see to it myself,”", "그걸 어떻게 다는지 아무도 모르니, 내가 직접 봐야겠어요.\""], ["answered Dolly addressing him.", "돌리가 그를 향해 대답했다."]], [["“God knows whether they are fully reconciled,”", "'정말로 완전히 화해했는지는 하느님만 아시겠지.'"], ["thought Anna, hearing her tone, cold and composed.", "차갑고 차분한 그녀의 말투를 들으며 안나는 생각했다."]], [["“Oh, nonsense, Dolly, always making difficulties,”", "\"아, 말도 안 돼, 돌리, 당신은 늘 일을 어렵게 만든단 말이야.\""], ["answered her husband.", "남편이 대답했다."], ["“Come, I’ll do it all, if you like....”", "\"자, 당신만 좋다면 내가 다 할게…….\""]], [["“Yes, they must be reconciled,”", "'그래, 두 사람은 분명 화해한 거야.'"], ["thought Anna.", "안나는 생각했다."]], [["“I know how you do everything,”", "\"당신이 무슨 일이든 어떻게 하는지 내가 잘 알죠.\""], ["answered Dolly.", "돌리가 대답했다."], ["“You tell Matvey to do what can’t be done, and go away yourself, leaving him to make a muddle of everything,”", "\"할 수도 없는 일을 마트베이한테 시켜 놓고는 당신은 가 버리고, 결국 그 사람이 죄다 엉망으로 만들게 내버려 두잖아요.\""], ["and her habitual, mocking smile curved the corners of Dolly’s lips as she spoke.", "그렇게 말하면서 늘 그렇듯 빈정대는 미소가 돌리의 입꼬리를 휘게 했다."]], [["“Full, full reconciliation, full,”", "'완전히, 완전히 화해한 거야, 완전히.'"], ["thought Anna; “thank God!”", "안나는 생각했다. '아, 다행이야!'"], ["and rejoicing that she was the cause of it, she went up to Dolly and kissed her.", "그리고 자기가 그 원인이 된 것을 기뻐하며, 그녀는 돌리에게 다가가 입을 맞추었다."]], [["“Not at all.", "\"천만에."], ["Why do you always look down on me and Matvey?”", "당신은 왜 늘 나하고 마트베이를 깔보는 거야?\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, smiling hardly perceptibly, and addressing his wife.", "스테판 아르카지치가 거의 알아챌 수 없을 만큼 희미하게 미소 지으며 아내를 향해 말했다."]], [["The whole evening Dolly was, as always, a little mocking in her tone to her husband, while Stepan Arkadyevitch was happy and cheerful, but not so as to seem as though, having been forgiven, he had forgotten his offense.", "저녁 내내 돌리는 늘 그렇듯 남편에게 다소 빈정대는 말투를 썼고, 스테판 아르카지치는 행복하고 명랑했지만, 용서받았다고 해서 자기 잘못을 잊어버린 것처럼 보일 정도는 아니었다."]], [["At half-past nine o’clock a particularly joyful and pleasant family conversation over the tea-table at the Oblonskys’ was broken up by an apparently simple incident.", "아홉 시 반에 오블론스키 집안의 차 탁자에서 오가던 유난히 즐겁고 화기애애한 가족의 대화는, 겉보기엔 단순한 한 가지 일로 깨지고 말았다."], ["But this simple incident for some reason struck everyone as strange.", "그러나 이 단순한 일은 무슨 까닭에서인지 모두에게 이상하게 느껴졌다."], ["Talking about common acquaintances in Petersburg, Anna got up quickly.", "페테르부르크의 공통 지인들에 관한 이야기를 하다가 안나가 재빨리 일어섰다."]], [["“She is in my album,”", "\"그 여자는 내 앨범에 있어요.\""], ["she said; “and, by the way, I’ll show you my Seryozha,”", "그녀가 말했다. \"그리고 이참에 우리 세료자도 보여 드릴게요.\""], ["she added, with a mother’s smile of pride.", "그녀는 자랑스러운 어머니의 미소를 띠며 덧붙였다."]], [["Towards ten o’clock, when she usually said good-night to her son, and often before going to a ball put him to bed herself, she felt depressed at being so far from him; and whatever she was talking about, she kept coming back in thought to her curly-headed Seryozha.", "평소 열 시쯤이면 그녀는 아들에게 잘 자라는 인사를 했고, 무도회에 가기 전에도 종종 손수 아들을 재우곤 했는데, 지금은 아들과 이토록 멀리 떨어져 있다는 사실에 마음이 울적했다. 그래서 무슨 이야기를 하든 머릿속에서는 자꾸만 곱슬머리의 세료자에게로 되돌아갔다."], ["She longed to look at his photograph and talk of him.", "그녀는 아들의 사진을 보고, 아들에 관해 이야기하고 싶어 견딜 수가 없었다."], ["Seizing the first pretext, she got up, and with her light, resolute step went for her album.", "첫 번째 구실을 잡아채자마자 그녀는 일어나, 그 가볍고 단호한 걸음으로 앨범을 가지러 갔다."], ["The stairs up to her room came out on the landing of the great warm main staircase.", "그녀의 방으로 올라가는 계단은 따뜻하고 큼직한 본계단의 층계참으로 이어져 있었다."]], [["Just as she was leaving the drawing-room, a ring was heard in the hall.", "그녀가 막 응접실을 나서려던 참에 현관에서 초인종 소리가 들렸다."]], [["“Who can that be?”", "\"누굴까요?\""], ["said Dolly.", "돌리가 말했다."]], [["“It’s early for me to be fetched, and for anyone else it’s late,”", "\"저를 데리러 오기엔 이르고, 다른 누군가가 오기엔 늦은 시간이네요.\""], ["observed Kitty.", "키티가 말했다."]], [["“Sure to be someone with papers for me,”", "\"분명 내게 서류를 가져온 사람일 거야.\""], ["put in Stepan Arkadyevitch.", "스테판 아르카지치가 끼어들었다."], ["When Anna was passing the top of the staircase, a servant was running up to announce the visitor, while the visitor himself was standing under a lamp.", "안나가 계단 꼭대기를 지날 때, 하인 하나가 손님이 왔음을 알리려고 뛰어 올라오고 있었고, 그 손님은 등불 아래에 서 있었다."], ["Anna glancing down at once recognized Vronsky, and a strange feeling of pleasure and at the same time of dread of something stirred in her heart.", "아래를 흘끗 내려다본 안나는 단번에 브론스키를 알아보았고, 기쁨과 동시에 무언가에 대한 두려움이라는 야릇한 감정이 그녀의 가슴속에서 일렁였다."], ["He was standing still, not taking off his coat, pulling something out of his pocket.", "그는 외투도 벗지 않고 가만히 서서 주머니에서 무언가를 꺼내고 있었다."], ["At the instant when she was just facing the stairs, he raised his eyes, caught sight of her, and into the expression of his face there passed a shade of embarrassment and dismay.", "그녀가 막 계단을 마주한 순간, 그가 눈을 들어 그녀를 보았고, 그의 얼굴 표정에는 당혹과 낭패의 그늘이 스쳐 지나갔다."], ["With a slight inclination of her head she passed, hearing behind her Stepan Arkadyevitch’s loud voice calling him to come up, and the quiet, soft, and composed voice of Vronsky refusing.", "그녀는 살짝 고개를 숙여 인사하고는 지나쳐 갔는데, 등 뒤에서 스테판 아르카지치가 올라오라고 큰 소리로 부르는 것과, 그것을 거절하는 브론스키의 조용하고 부드럽고 차분한 목소리가 들려왔다."]], [["When Anna returned with the album, he was already gone, and Stepan Arkadyevitch was telling them that he had called to inquire about the dinner they were giving next day to a celebrity who had just arrived.", "안나가 앨범을 가지고 돌아왔을 때 그는 이미 가 버린 뒤였고, 스테판 아르카지치는 그가 막 도착한 어느 명사를 위해 내일 열기로 한 만찬에 관해 물어보러 들렀던 것이라고 이야기하고 있었다."], ["“And nothing would induce him to come up.", "\"그런데 무슨 수를 써도 올라오려 하질 않더라고."], ["What a queer fellow he is!”", "참 별난 친구야!\""], ["added Stepan Arkadyevitch.", "스테판 아르카지치가 덧붙였다."]], [["Kitty blushed.", "키티는 얼굴을 붉혔다."], ["She thought that she was the only person who knew why he had come, and why he would not come up.", "그가 왜 왔는지, 그리고 왜 올라오려 하지 않았는지 아는 사람은 자기뿐이라고 그녀는 생각했다."], ["“He has been at home,”", "'그이는 우리 집에 갔다가,'"], ["she thought, “and didn’t find me, and thought I should be here, but he did not come up because he thought it late, and Anna’s here.”", "그녀는 생각했다, '나를 못 찾았고, 내가 여기 있을 거라고 짐작한 거야. 하지만 시간이 늦었고 안나도 여기 있으니까 올라오지 않은 거지.'"]], [["All of them looked at each other, saying nothing, and began to look at Anna’s album.", "그들은 모두 아무 말 없이 서로를 바라보다가, 안나의 앨범을 보기 시작했다."]], [["There was nothing either exceptional or strange in a man’s calling at half-past nine on a friend to inquire details of a proposed dinner party and not coming in, but it seemed strange to all of them.", "어떤 남자가 아홉 시 반에 친구를 찾아와 예정된 만찬에 관한 세세한 사정을 물어보고는 안으로 들어오지 않았다는 데에 유별나거나 이상할 것은 전혀 없었지만, 그 일은 그들 모두에게 이상하게 여겨졌다."], ["Above all, it seemed strange and not right to Anna.", "무엇보다도 안나에게 그것은 이상하고 온당치 못한 일로 느껴졌다."]], [["# Chapter 22", "# Chapter 22"]], [["The ball was only just beginning as Kitty and her mother walked up the great staircase, flooded with light, and lined with flowers and footmen in powder and red coats.", "무도회는 키티와 어머니가 환한 불빛이 넘쳐흐르고 꽃과 분을 바른 빨간 제복 차림의 하인들이 늘어선 큰 계단을 올라갈 무렵에야 막 시작되고 있었다."], ["From the rooms came a constant, steady hum, as from a hive, and the rustle of movement; and while on the landing between trees they gave last touches to their hair and dresses before the mirror, they heard from the ballroom the careful, distinct notes of the fiddles of the orchestra beginning the first waltz.", "방마다 벌집에서 나는 것 같은 끊임없고 고른 웅성거림과 움직임의 살랑거리는 소리가 흘러나왔고, 나무들 사이의 층계참에서 거울 앞에 서서 머리와 옷매무새에 마지막 손질을 하는 동안, 두 사람은 무도회장에서 오케스트라의 바이올린들이 첫 왈츠를 시작하며 또렷이 신경 써서 켜는 음을 들었다."], ["A little old man in civilian dress, arranging his gray curls before another mirror, and diffusing an odor of scent, stumbled against them on the stairs, and stood aside, evidently admiring Kitty, whom he did not know.", "다른 거울 앞에서 희끗한 곱슬머리를 매만지며 향수 냄새를 풍기던 평복 차림의 작은 노인이 계단에서 두 사람과 부딪치고는 옆으로 비켜서서, 누군지도 모르는 키티를 보고 분명 감탄하고 있었다."], ["A beardless youth, one of those society youths whom the old Prince Shtcherbatsky called “young bucks,” in an exceedingly open waistcoat, straightening his white tie as he went, bowed to them, and after running by, came back to ask Kitty for a quadrille.", "늙은 셰르바츠키 공작이 \"젊은 멋쟁이\"라고 부르는 사교계 청년들 중 하나인 수염 없는 젊은이가, 한껏 깊이 파인 조끼 차림으로 가면서 흰 넥타이를 바로잡으며 두 사람에게 인사하더니, 지나쳤다가 도로 돌아와 키티에게 카드리유를 청했다."], ["As the first quadrille had already been given to Vronsky, she had to promise this youth the second.", "첫 번째 카드리유는 이미 브론스키에게 약속해 두었기에, 그녀는 이 젊은이에게 두 번째 카드리유를 약속해야 했다."], ["An officer, buttoning his glove, stood aside in the doorway, and stroking his mustache, admired rosy Kitty.", "한 장교가 장갑 단추를 채우며 문간에 비켜서서, 콧수염을 쓰다듬으며 발그레한 키티를 바라보고 있었다."]], [["Although her dress, her coiffure, and all the preparations for the ball had cost Kitty great trouble and consideration, at this moment she walked into the ballroom in her elaborate tulle dress over a pink slip as easily and simply as though all the rosettes and lace, all the minute details of her attire, had not cost her or her family a moment’s attention, as though she had been born in that tulle and lace, with her hair done up high on her head, and a rose and two leaves on the top of it.", "비록 그녀의 옷과 머리 모양, 그리고 무도회를 위한 모든 준비가 키티에게 적잖은 수고와 고심을 안겨 주었지만, 이 순간 그녀는 분홍빛 속치마 위에 정교한 튈 드레스를 걸치고, 그 모든 로제트며 레이스며 옷차림의 자잘한 세부가 그녀나 그녀의 가족에게 단 한순간의 신경도 쓰게 한 적이 없다는 듯이, 마치 그 튈과 레이스를 입은 채로, 머리를 높이 틀어 올리고 그 위에 장미 한 송이와 잎새 두 장을 얹은 채로 태어나기라도 한 듯이, 그토록 수월하고 단순하게 무도회장으로 걸어 들어갔다."]], [["When, just before entering the ballroom, the princess, her mother, tried to turn right side out of the ribbon of her sash, Kitty had drawn back a little.", "무도회장에 들어서기 직전, 어머니인 공작 부인이 띠의 리본을 겉으로 바로잡아 주려 하자, 키티는 살짝 몸을 뒤로 뺐다."], ["She felt that everything must be right of itself, and graceful, and nothing could need setting straight.", "그녀는 모든 것이 저절로 제대로 되어 있고 우아하며, 무엇 하나 바로잡을 필요가 없으리라고 느꼈다."]], [["It was one of Kitty’s best days.", "그날은 키티에게 가장 좋은 날 가운데 하루였다."], ["Her dress was not uncomfortable anywhere; her lace berthe did not droop anywhere; her rosettes were not crushed nor torn off; her pink slippers with high, hollowed-out heels did not pinch, but gladdened her feet; and the thick rolls of fair chignon kept up on her head as if they were her own hair.", "드레스는 어디 하나 거북한 데가 없었고, 레이스로 된 베르타는 어디 하나 늘어지지 않았으며, 로제트는 짓눌리거나 뜯겨 나가지 않았고, 높고 안쪽이 옴폭한 굽이 달린 분홍 슬리퍼는 발을 죄지 않고 도리어 발을 즐겁게 했다. 빛깔 고운 가발의 도톰한 타래는 마치 제 머리카락인 듯 머리 위에 잘 얹혀 있었다."], ["All the three buttons buttoned up without tearing on the long glove that covered her hand without concealing its lines.", "손의 선을 가리지 않으면서 손을 덮은 긴 장갑의 단추 세 개는 하나도 뜯기지 않고 다 채워졌다."], ["The black velvet of her locket nestled with special softness round her neck.", "로켓을 단 검은 벨벳 끈은 유난히 부드럽게 그녀의 목을 감싸고 있었다."], ["That velvet was delicious; at home, looking at her neck in the looking-glass, Kitty had felt that that velvet was speaking.", "그 벨벳은 황홀했다. 집에서 거울에 비친 자기 목을 들여다보며, 키티는 그 벨벳이 말을 거는 것만 같았다."], ["About all the rest there might be a doubt, but the velvet was delicious.", "다른 모든 것에는 의심이 갈지 몰라도, 벨벳만큼은 황홀했다."], ["Kitty smiled here too, at the ball, when she glanced at it in the glass.", "무도회장에서도 키티는 거울에 비친 그것을 흘끗 보고 다시 미소를 지었다."], ["Her bare shoulders and arms gave Kitty a sense of chill marble, a feeling she particularly liked.", "드러난 어깨와 팔은 키티에게 차가운 대리석 같은 느낌을 주었는데, 그것은 그녀가 유난히 좋아하는 감각이었다."], ["Her eyes sparkled, and her rosy lips could not keep from smiling from the consciousness of her own attractiveness.", "그녀의 눈이 반짝였고, 자신의 매력을 의식한 탓에 발그레한 입술은 미소를 참을 수가 없었다."], ["She had scarcely entered the ballroom and reached the throng of ladies, all tulle, ribbons, lace, and flowers, waiting to be asked to dance—Kitty was never one of that throng—when she was asked for a waltz, and asked by the best partner, the first star in the hierarchy of the ballroom, a renowned director of dances, a married man, handsome and well-built, Yegorushka Korsunsky.", "그녀가 무도회장에 들어서서 춤 신청을 기다리는, 온통 튈과 리본과 레이스와 꽃으로 뒤덮인 여인들의 무리—키티는 결코 그 무리에 속한 적이 없었다—에 채 닿기도 전에, 그녀에게 왈츠 신청이 들어왔고, 그것도 가장 훌륭한 파트너, 무도회장의 서열에서 으뜸가는 별이며 이름난 무도 지휘자이자 기혼이면서 잘생기고 균형 잡힌 몸매를 가진 예고루시카 코르순스키가 신청한 것이었다."], ["He had only just left the Countess Bonina, with whom he had danced the first half of the waltz, and, scanning his kingdom—that is to say, a few couples who had started dancing—he caught sight of Kitty, entering, and flew up to her with that peculiar, easy amble which is confined to directors of balls.", "그는 바로 조금 전, 보니나 백작 부인과 왈츠의 전반부를 추다가 막 그녀 곁을 떠난 참이었고, 자기 왕국—그러니까 춤을 추기 시작한 몇 쌍의 남녀—을 둘러보다가 들어오는 키티를 발견하고는, 무도회 지휘자들에게만 있는 그 독특하고 가뿐한 종종걸음으로 그녀에게 날아왔다."], ["Without even asking her if she cared to dance, he put out his arm to encircle her slender waist.", "그녀가 춤을 추고 싶은지 묻지도 않은 채, 그는 그녀의 가는 허리를 감으려고 팔을 내밀었다."], ["She looked round for someone to give her fan to, and their hostess, smiling to her, took it.", "그녀가 부채를 맡길 사람을 찾아 두리번거리자, 그녀에게 미소 짓던 안주인이 그것을 받아 주었다."]], [["“How nice you’ve come in good time,”", "\"제때 와 주셔서 참 좋군요.\""], ["he said to her, embracing her waist; “such a bad habit to be late.”", "그가 그녀의 허리를 감싸며 말했다. \"늦는 건 아주 나쁜 버릇이지요.\""], ["Bending her left hand, she laid it on his shoulder, and her little feet in their pink slippers began swiftly, lightly, and rhythmically moving over the slippery floor in time to the music.", "그녀는 왼손을 굽혀 그의 어깨에 얹었고, 분홍 슬리퍼를 신은 자그마한 발이 음악에 맞추어 미끄러운 마룻바닥 위를 재빠르고 가뿐하며 리듬감 있게 움직이기 시작했다."]], [["“It’s a rest to waltz with you,”", "\"당신과 왈츠를 추면 마치 쉬는 것 같군요.\""], ["he said to her, as they fell into the first slow steps of the waltz.", "그들이 왈츠의 첫 느린 스텝으로 접어들 때 그가 말했다."], ["“It’s exquisite—such lightness, precision.”", "\"절묘해요. 이 가벼움, 이 정확함이라니.\""], ["He said to her the same thing he said to almost all his partners whom he knew well.", "그는 잘 아는 거의 모든 파트너에게 하던 말을 그녀에게 그대로 했다."]], [["She smiled at his praise, and continued to look about the room over his shoulder.", "그녀는 그의 칭찬에 미소 지으면서도, 그의 어깨 너머로 계속 방 안을 둘러보았다."], ["She was not like a girl at her first ball, for whom all faces in the ballroom melt into one vision of fairyland.", "그녀는 무도회장의 모든 얼굴이 하나의 동화 같은 환영으로 녹아드는, 첫 무도회에 나온 소녀 같지 않았다."], ["And she was not a girl who had gone the stale round of balls till every face in the ballroom was familiar and tiresome.", "또한 무도회의 진부한 순례를 거듭한 끝에 무도회장의 모든 얼굴이 낯익고 지겨워진 소녀도 아니었다."], ["But she was in the middle stage between these two; she was excited, and at the same time she had sufficient self-possession to be able to observe.", "그녀는 이 둘 사이의 중간 단계에 있었다. 들떠 있으면서도 동시에 관찰할 수 있을 만큼의 침착함을 갖추고 있었던 것이다."], ["In the left corner of the ballroom she saw the cream of society gathered together.", "무도회장의 왼쪽 구석에서 그녀는 사교계의 정수가 한데 모여 있는 것을 보았다."], ["There—incredibly naked—was the beauty Lidi, Korsunsky’s wife; there was the lady of the house; there shone the bald head of Krivin, always to be found where the best people were.", "그곳에는—믿을 수 없으리만치 드러낸 차림으로—코르순스키의 아내인 미인 리디가 있었고, 안주인이 있었으며, 가장 훌륭한 사람들이 모인 곳이라면 늘 나타나는 크리빈의 대머리가 빛나고 있었다."], ["In that direction gazed the young men, not venturing to approach.", "젊은 남자들은 감히 다가서지 못한 채 그쪽을 바라보고 있었다."], ["There, too, she descried Stiva, and there she saw the exquisite figure and head of Anna in a black velvet gown.", "그곳에서 그녀는 스티바도 찾아냈고, 검은 벨벳 드레스를 입은 안나의 절묘한 자태와 두상도 보았다."], ["And he was there.", "그리고 그가 거기 있었다."], ["Kitty had not seen him since the evening she refused Levin.", "키티는 레빈을 거절한 그날 저녁 이후로 그를 보지 못했다."], ["With her long-sighted eyes, she knew him at once, and was even aware that he was looking at her.", "멀리 보는 눈으로 그녀는 단번에 그를 알아보았고, 심지어 그가 자기를 바라보고 있다는 것까지 알아챘다."]], [["“Another turn, eh?", "\"한 바퀴 더 돌까요, 응?"], ["You’re not tired?”", "지치진 않으셨고요?\""], ["said Korsunsky, a little out of breath.", "코르순스키가 약간 숨을 헐떡이며 말했다."]], [["“No, thank you!”", "\"아뇨, 괜찮아요!\""]], [["“Where shall I take you?”", "\"어디로 모실까요?\""]], [["“Madame Karenina’s here, I think ...", "\"카레니나 부인이 저기 계신 것 같은데……"], ["take me to her.”", "그분께 데려다주세요.\""]], [["“Wherever you command.”", "\"분부대로 어디든지요.\""]], [["And Korsunsky began waltzing with measured steps straight towards the group in the left corner, continually saying, “Pardon, mesdames, pardon, pardon, mesdames”; and steering his course through the sea of lace, tulle, and ribbon, and not disarranging a feather, he turned his partner sharply round, so that her slim ankles, in light transparent stockings, were exposed to view, and her train floated out in fan shape and covered Krivin’s knees.", "그리고 코르순스키는 절도 있는 스텝으로 왼쪽 구석의 무리를 향해 곧장 왈츠를 추어 나가면서, \"Pardon, mesdames, pardon, pardon, mesdames\"라고 끊임없이 되뇌었고, 레이스와 튈과 리본의 바다를 헤치고 나아가면서도 깃털 하나 흐트러뜨리지 않은 채 파트너를 홱 돌렸는데, 그 바람에 가벼운 비치는 스타킹에 감싸인 그녀의 가느다란 발목이 드러났고 그녀의 트레인이 부채꼴로 펼쳐지며 크리빈의 무릎을 덮었다."], ["Korsunsky bowed, set straight his open shirt front, and gave her his arm to conduct her to Anna Arkadyevna.", "코르순스키는 절을 하고, 풀어진 셔츠 앞섶을 바로잡고는, 그녀를 안나 아르카지예브나에게로 데려가려고 팔을 내밀었다."], ["Kitty, flushed, took her train from Krivin’s knees, and, a little giddy, looked round, seeking Anna.", "얼굴이 발그레해진 키티는 크리빈의 무릎에서 트레인을 거두어들이고, 약간 어지러워하며 안나를 찾아 주위를 둘러보았다."], ["Anna was not in lilac, as Kitty had so urgently wished, but in a black, low-cut, velvet gown, showing her full throat and shoulders, that looked as though carved in old ivory, and her rounded arms, with tiny, slender wrists.", "안나는 키티가 그토록 간절히 바랐던 라일락 빛깔이 아니라, 풍만한 목과 어깨를 드러낸 검은색의 깊이 파인 벨벳 드레스 차림이었는데, 그 목과 어깨는 마치 오래된 상아로 깎아 놓은 듯했고, 둥근 팔에는 자그맣고 가느다란 손목이 달려 있었다."], ["The whole gown was trimmed with Venetian guipure.", "드레스 전체에 베네치아산 기퓌르 레이스가 둘려 있었다."], ["On her head, among her black hair—her own, with no false additions—was a little wreath of pansies, and a bouquet of the same in the black ribbon of her sash among white lace.", "머리에는, 가발을 덧대지 않은 그녀 자신의 검은 머리카락 사이에, 팬지꽃으로 엮은 작은 화관이 얹혀 있었고, 흰 레이스 사이 띠의 검은 리본에도 같은 꽃다발이 달려 있었다."], ["Her coiffure was not striking.", "그녀의 머리 모양은 눈에 띄지 않았다."], ["All that was noticeable was the little wilful tendrils of her curly hair that would always break free about her neck and temples.", "눈에 띄는 것이라곤 목덜미와 관자놀이 언저리에서 늘 제멋대로 풀려 나오곤 하는 곱슬한 작은 잔머리뿐이었다."], ["Round her well-cut, strong neck was a thread of pearls.", "잘 빠진 단단한 목에는 진주 한 줄이 둘려 있었다."]], [["Kitty had been seeing Anna every day; she adored her, and had pictured her invariably in lilac.", "키티는 날마다 안나를 보아 왔고, 그녀를 흠모했으며, 한결같이 라일락 빛깔을 입은 모습으로 그녀를 그려 왔다."], ["But now seeing her in black, she felt that she had not fully seen her charm.", "그러나 이제 검은 옷을 입은 그녀를 보고서, 키티는 자기가 그녀의 매력을 온전히 보지 못했음을 느꼈다."], ["She saw her now as someone quite new and surprising to her.", "이제 그녀는 안나를 전혀 새롭고 놀라운 사람으로 보게 되었다."], ["Now she understood that Anna could not have been in lilac, and that her charm was just that she always stood out against her attire, that her dress could never be noticeable on her.", "이제 그녀는 안나가 라일락 빛깔을 입을 수 없는 사람이며, 그녀의 매력이란 바로 그녀가 늘 자기 옷차림을 압도하며 도드라진다는 데에, 그녀가 입은 옷이 결코 그녀 위에서 눈에 띌 수 없다는 데에 있음을 깨달았다."], ["And her black dress, with its sumptuous lace, was not noticeable on her; it was only the frame, and all that was seen was she—simple, natural, elegant, and at the same time gay and eager.", "호화로운 레이스가 달린 그녀의 검은 드레스도 그녀 위에서 눈에 띄지 않았다. 그것은 단지 액자일 뿐이었고, 보이는 것이라곤 오로지 그녀—소박하고 자연스럽고 우아하며 동시에 명랑하고 생기 넘치는 그녀뿐이었다."]], [["She was standing holding herself, as always, very erect, and when Kitty drew near the group she was speaking to the master of the house, her head slightly turned towards him.", "그녀는 언제나처럼 몸을 아주 꼿꼿이 세운 채 서 있었고, 키티가 그 무리에 다가갔을 때 그녀는 머리를 약간 그쪽으로 돌린 채 주인에게 이야기하고 있었다."]], [["“No, I don’t throw stones,”", "\"아뇨, 저는 돌을 던지지 않아요.\""], ["she was saying, in answer to something, “though I can’t understand it,”", "무언가에 대답하며 그녀가 말하고 있었다. \"비록 이해할 수는 없지만요.\""], ["she went on, shrugging her shoulders, and she turned at once with a soft smile of protection towards Kitty.", "그녀는 어깨를 으쓱하며 말을 이었고, 곧 부드럽게 감싸 주는 듯한 미소를 띠며 키티 쪽으로 돌아섰다."], ["With a flying, feminine glance she scanned her attire, and made a movement of her head, hardly perceptible, but understood by Kitty, signifying approval of her dress and her looks.", "날렵하고 여성스러운 눈길로 그녀는 키티의 옷차림을 훑어보고는, 거의 알아챌 수 없을 만큼 미세하지만 키티는 알아챈, 그녀의 옷과 용모를 인정한다는 뜻의 고갯짓을 했다."], ["“You came into the room dancing,”", "\"춤을 추며 방으로 들어오시던걸요.\""], ["she added.", "그녀가 덧붙였다."]], [["“This is one of my most faithful supporters,”", "\"이분은 제 가장 충실한 후원자 가운데 한 분이지요.\""], ["said Korsunsky, bowing to Anna Arkadyevna, whom he had not yet seen.", "아직 만난 적이 없던 안나 아르카지예브나에게 절을 하며 코르순스키가 말했다."], ["“The princess helps to make balls happy and successful.", "\"이 영애님은 무도회를 즐겁고 성공적으로 만드는 데 일조하시지요."], ["Anna Arkadyevna, a waltz?”", "안나 아르카지예브나, 왈츠 한 곡 어떠십니까?\""], ["he said, bending down to her.", "그가 그녀에게 몸을 굽히며 말했다."]], [["“Why, have you met?”", "\"아니, 두 분은 서로 아는 사이였소?\""], ["inquired their host.", "주인이 물었다."]], [["“Is there anyone we have not met?", "\"저희가 모르는 사람이 어디 있겠습니까?"], ["My wife and I are like white wolves—everyone knows us,”", "제 아내와 저는 흰 늑대 같은 존재지요. 누구나 다 저희를 알아본답니다.\""], ["answered Korsunsky.", "코르순스키가 대답했다."], ["“A waltz, Anna Arkadyevna?”", "\"왈츠 한 곡, 안나 아르카지예브나?\""]], [["“I don’t dance when it’s possible not to dance,”", "\"안 춰도 될 때면 저는 춤을 추지 않는답니다.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“But tonight it’s impossible,”", "\"하지만 오늘 밤은 안 출 수가 없지요.\""], ["answered Korsunsky.", "코르순스키가 대답했다."]], [["At that instant Vronsky came up.", "바로 그 순간 브론스키가 다가왔다."]], [["“Well, since it’s impossible tonight, let us start,”", "\"그럼, 오늘 밤은 안 출 수가 없다니, 시작하지요.\""], ["she said, not noticing Vronsky’s bow, and she hastily put her hand on Korsunsky’s shoulder.", "그녀는 브론스키의 인사를 알아채지 못한 채 말하고는, 황급히 코르순스키의 어깨에 손을 얹었다."]], [["“What is she vexed with him about?”", "'무엇 때문에 그에게 화가 난 걸까?'"], ["thought Kitty, discerning that Anna had intentionally not responded to Vronsky’s bow.", "안나가 일부러 브론스키의 인사에 응하지 않았다는 것을 알아채고 키티는 생각했다."], ["Vronsky went up to Kitty reminding her of the first quadrille, and expressing his regret that he had not seen her all this time.", "브론스키는 키티에게 다가와 첫 번째 카드리유를 일깨우며, 이렇게 오랫동안 그녀를 보지 못해 유감이었다고 말했다."], ["Kitty gazed in admiration at Anna waltzing, and listened to him.", "키티는 왈츠를 추는 안나를 감탄 어린 눈길로 바라보면서 그의 말을 들었다."], ["She expected him to ask her for a waltz, but he did not, and she glanced wonderingly at him.", "그녀는 그가 왈츠를 청하리라 기대했지만 그는 청하지 않았고, 그녀는 의아한 눈으로 그를 흘끗 보았다."], ["He flushed slightly, and hurriedly asked her to waltz, but he had only just put his arm round her waist and taken the first step when the music suddenly stopped.", "그는 살짝 얼굴을 붉히더니 서둘러 왈츠를 청했지만, 그가 막 그녀의 허리에 팔을 두르고 첫 스텝을 뗀 순간 음악이 별안간 멈춰 버렸다."], ["Kitty looked into his face, which was so close to her own, and long afterwards—for several years after—that look, full of love, to which he made no response, cut her to the heart with an agony of shame.", "키티는 자기 얼굴과 그토록 가까이 있던 그의 얼굴을 들여다보았는데, 그 후로 오랫동안—몇 해가 지나도록—그가 아무런 응답도 하지 않았던, 사랑으로 가득 찬 그 눈길은 수치의 고통으로 그녀의 가슴을 후벼 팠다."]], [["“Pardon!", "\"Pardon!"], ["pardon! Waltz!", "pardon!"], ["waltz!”", "왈츠!"], ["shouted Korsunsky from the other side of the room, and seizing the first young lady he came across he began dancing himself.", "왈츠!\" 코르순스키가 방 저편에서 소리치며, 마주친 첫 번째 아가씨를 붙잡고 자기가 직접 춤을 추기 시작했다."]], [["# Chapter 23", "# Chapter 23"]], [["Vronsky and Kitty waltzed several times round the room.", "브론스키와 키티는 홀을 몇 바퀴 돌며 왈츠를 추었다."], ["After the first waltz Kitty went to her mother, and she had hardly time to say a few words to Countess Nordston when Vronsky came up again for the first quadrille.", "첫 왈츠가 끝난 뒤 키티는 어머니에게로 갔는데, 노르츠톤 백작부인과 몇 마디 나눌 틈도 없이 브론스키가 첫 카드리유를 청하러 다시 다가왔다. 카드리유를 추는 동안에는 별다른 의미 있는 말이 오가지 않았다."], ["During the quadrille nothing of any significance was said: there was disjointed talk between them of the Korsunskys, husband and wife, whom he described very amusingly, as delightful children at forty, and of the future town theater; and only once the conversation touched her to the quick, when he asked her about Levin, whether he was here, and added that he liked him so much.", "두 사람 사이에는 코르순스키 부부에 관한 끊어진 이야기가 오갔는데, 그는 그들을 마흔 살에도 사랑스러운 아이들 같다며 아주 재미나게 묘사했고, 또 앞으로 들어설 시립 극장에 관한 이야기도 했다. 그리고 단 한 번 대화가 그녀의 가슴 한구석을 찔렀는데, 그가 레빈에 관해 그가 여기 와 있는지 물으면서 자기는 그를 무척 좋아한다고 덧붙였을 때였다."], ["But Kitty did not expect much from the quadrille.", "하지만 키티는 카드리유에 큰 기대를 걸지 않았다."], ["She looked forward with a thrill at her heart to the mazurka.", "그녀는 가슴을 두근거리며 마주르카를 고대하고 있었다."], ["She fancied that in the mazurka everything must be decided.", "마주르카에서 모든 것이 결정되리라고 그녀는 상상했다."], ["The fact that he did not during the quadrille ask her for the mazurka did not trouble her.", "그가 카드리유를 추는 동안 마주르카를 청하지 않았다는 사실은 그녀를 불안하게 하지 않았다."], ["She felt sure she would dance the mazurka with him as she had done at former balls, and refused five young men, saying she was engaged for the mazurka.", "그녀는 예전 무도회에서 그랬듯이 그와 마주르카를 추리라고 확신했고, 마주르카는 이미 약속이 되어 있다고 말하며 다섯 명의 젊은이를 거절했다."], ["The whole ball up to the last quadrille was for Kitty an enchanted vision of delightful colors, sounds, and motions.", "마지막 카드리유에 이르기까지 무도회 전체가 키티에게는 즐거운 색채와 소리와 움직임으로 이루어진 황홀한 환영이었다."], ["She only sat down when she felt too tired and begged for a rest.", "그녀는 너무 지쳐서 잠시 쉬게 해 달라고 청할 때만 자리에 앉았다."], ["But as she was dancing the last quadrille with one of the tiresome young men whom she could not refuse, she chanced to be vis-à-vis with Vronsky and Anna.", "그런데 차마 거절할 수 없었던 따분한 젊은이 가운데 한 사람과 마지막 카드리유를 추다가, 그녀는 우연히 브론스키와 안나와 마주 보는 자리에 서게 되었다."], ["She had not been near Anna again since the beginning of the evening, and now again she saw her suddenly quite new and surprising.", "저녁이 시작된 이후로 안나 곁에 다시 가 본 적이 없었던 그녀는, 이제 또다시 안나가 어딘가 완전히 새롭고 놀라운 모습으로 보였다."], ["She saw in her the signs of that excitement of success she knew so well in herself; she saw that she was intoxicated with the delighted admiration she was exciting.", "그녀는 안나에게서 자기 자신이 너무도 잘 아는 그 성공의 흥분의 징후들을 보았다. 자기가 불러일으킨 기쁨에 찬 찬탄에 안나가 취해 있는 것을 보았다."], ["She knew that feeling and knew its signs, and saw them in Anna; saw the quivering, flashing light in her eyes, and the smile of happiness and excitement unconsciously playing on her lips, and the deliberate grace, precision, and lightness of her movements.", "그녀는 그 감정을 알았고 그 징후들을 알았으며, 그것들을 안나에게서 보았다. 그녀의 두 눈에서 떨리며 번득이는 빛을, 입가에 자기도 모르게 어리는 행복과 흥분의 미소를, 그리고 그녀의 동작이 지닌 의도된 우아함과 정확함과 가벼움을 보았다."]], [["“Who?”", "\"누구지?\""], ["she asked herself. “All or one?”", "그녀는 자신에게 물었다."], ["And not assisting the harassed young man she was dancing with in the conversation, the thread of which he had lost and could not pick up again, she obeyed with external liveliness the peremptory shouts of Korsunsky starting them all into the grand rond, and then into the chaîne, and at the same time she kept watch with a growing pang at her heart.", "\"모두에게 그러는 걸까, 아니면 한 사람에게?\" 그러고는 함께 춤추던 시달린 젊은이가 대화의 실마리를 놓쳐 다시 잡지 못하는 것을 도와주지도 않은 채, 그녀는 겉으로만 활기차게, 모두를 grand rond로 이끌었다가 다시 chaîne로 몰아넣는 코르순스키의 단호한 외침에 따랐고, 그러는 동안에도 점점 더 커지는 가슴의 통증을 느끼며 지켜보았다."], ["“No, it’s not the admiration of the crowd has intoxicated her, but the adoration of one.", "\"아니야, 그녀를 취하게 한 건 군중의 찬탄이 아니라 단 한 사람의 흠모야."], ["And that one? can it be he?”", "그런데 그 한 사람이라면?"], ["Every time he spoke to Anna the joyous light flashed into her eyes, and the smile of happiness curved her red lips.", "정말 그 사람일까?\" 그가 안나에게 말을 건넬 때마다 안나의 눈에 기쁨의 빛이 번득였고, 행복의 미소가 그녀의 붉은 입술을 휘게 했다."], ["She seemed to make an effort to control herself, to try not to show these signs of delight, but they came out on her face of themselves.", "그녀는 스스로를 억누르려고, 이 기쁨의 징후들을 드러내지 않으려고 애쓰는 듯했지만, 그것들은 저절로 그녀의 얼굴에 떠올랐다."], ["“But what of him?”", "\"하지만 그는 어떨까?\""], ["Kitty looked at him and was filled with terror.", "키티는 그를 바라보았고 공포에 사로잡혔다."], ["What was pictured so clearly to Kitty in the mirror of Anna’s face she saw in him.", "안나의 얼굴이라는 거울에 그토록 또렷하게 비치던 것을 그녀는 그에게서도 보았다."], ["What had become of his always self-possessed resolute manner, and the carelessly serene expression of his face?", "그의 늘 침착하고 단호하던 태도와, 무심하리만큼 평온하던 얼굴 표정은 어디로 사라진 것일까?"], ["Now every time he turned to her, he bent his head, as though he would have fallen at her feet, and in his eyes there was nothing but humble submission and dread.", "이제 그는 그녀에게로 돌아설 때마다 마치 그녀의 발치에라도 엎드릴 것처럼 고개를 숙였고, 그의 눈에는 겸허한 복종과 두려움밖에 없었다. \"당신을 거스르고 싶지 않습니다.\""], ["“I would not offend you,”", "그의 눈은 매번 이렇게 말하는 듯했다."], ["his eyes seemed every time to be saying, “but I want to save myself, and I don’t know how.”", "\"하지만 나는 나 자신을 구하고 싶고, 그런데 어떻게 해야 할지 모르겠습니다.\""], ["On his face was a look such as Kitty had never seen before.", "그의 얼굴에는 키티가 이전에 한 번도 본 적 없는 표정이 떠올라 있었다."]], [["They were speaking of common acquaintances, keeping up the most trivial conversation, but to Kitty it seemed that every word they said was determining their fate and hers.", "두 사람은 서로 아는 사람들에 관해 더할 나위 없이 하찮은 이야기를 나누고 있었지만, 키티에게는 그들이 주고받는 모든 말이 그들의 운명과 자기 운명을 결정짓고 있는 것처럼 여겨졌다."], ["And strange it was that they were actually talking of how absurd Ivan Ivanovitch was with his French, and how the Eletsky girl might have made a better match, yet these words had all the while consequence for them, and they were feeling just as Kitty did.", "그리고 이상하게도 실제로 그들은 이반 이바노비치가 프랑스어를 쓰는 게 얼마나 우스꽝스러운지, 옐레츠키 집안의 딸이 어떻게 더 나은 혼처를 잡을 수도 있었을지를 이야기하고 있었는데도, 그 말들은 내내 두 사람에게 어떤 의미를 지니고 있었고, 그들도 키티와 똑같이 느끼고 있었다."], ["The whole ball, the whole world, everything seemed lost in fog in Kitty’s soul.", "키티의 마음속에서는 무도회 전체가, 온 세상이, 모든 것이 안개 속으로 사라진 듯했다."], ["Nothing but the stern discipline of her bringing-up supported her and forced her to do what was expected of her, that is, to dance, to answer questions, to talk, even to smile.", "오직 그녀가 받아 온 엄격한 교육의 단련만이 그녀를 지탱해 주며, 사람들이 그녀에게 기대하는 것을, 다시 말해 춤을 추고, 질문에 답하고, 이야기하고, 심지어 미소까지 짓도록 강요했다."], ["But before the mazurka, when they were beginning to rearrange the chairs and a few couples moved out of the smaller rooms into the big room, a moment of despair and horror came for Kitty.", "그러나 마주르카가 시작되기 전, 의자들을 다시 늘어놓기 시작하고 몇 쌍이 작은 방들에서 큰 홀로 나올 무렵, 키티에게 절망과 공포의 순간이 찾아왔다."], ["She had refused five partners, and now she was not dancing the mazurka.", "그녀는 다섯 명의 파트너를 거절했는데, 이제 마주르카를 출 상대가 없었다."], ["She had not even a hope of being asked for it, because she was so successful in society that the idea would never occur to anyone that she had remained disengaged till now.", "마주르카를 청해 올 것이라는 희망조차 없었으니, 그녀가 사교계에서 너무도 인기가 좋았던 탓에 그녀가 지금까지 상대 없이 남아 있으리라는 생각은 누구의 머릿속에도 떠오르지 않을 터였다."], ["She would have to tell her mother she felt ill and go home, but she had not the strength to do this.", "어머니에게 몸이 안 좋다고 말하고 집으로 돌아가야 했지만, 그녀에게는 그럴 기운조차 없었다."], ["She felt crushed.", "그녀는 무너지는 기분이었다."], ["She went to the furthest end of the little drawing-room and sank into a low chair.", "그녀는 작은 응접실의 가장 안쪽으로 가서 낮은 의자에 주저앉았다."], ["Her light, transparent skirts rose like a cloud about her slender waist; one bare, thin, soft, girlish arm, hanging listlessly, was lost in the folds of her pink tunic; in the other she held her fan, and with rapid, short strokes fanned her burning face.", "가볍고 비치는 치맛자락이 그녀의 가느다란 허리를 감싸며 구름처럼 부풀어 올랐다. 맨살이 드러난 가늘고 부드러운 소녀다운 한쪽 팔은 맥없이 늘어진 채 분홍빛 튜닉 주름 속에 묻혀 있었고, 다른 손에는 부채를 들고 짧고 빠른 부채질로 화끈거리는 얼굴을 식히고 있었다."], ["But while she looked like a butterfly, clinging to a blade of grass, and just about to open its rainbow wings for fresh flight, her heart ached with a horrible despair.", "그러나 풀잎에 매달려 무지갯빛 날개를 활짝 펼치고 새로운 비상에 나서려는 나비처럼 보이는 그 순간에도, 그녀의 가슴은 끔찍한 절망으로 아파 왔다."]], [["“But perhaps I am wrong, perhaps it was not so?”", "\"하지만 어쩌면 내가 잘못 본 걸지도 몰라, 어쩌면 그게 아니었을지도?\""], ["And again she recalled all she had seen.", "그러고는 그녀는 자기가 본 모든 것을 다시 떠올렸다."]], [["“Kitty, what is it?”", "\"키티, 무슨 일이니?\""], ["said Countess Nordston, stepping noiselessly over the carpet towards her.", "노르츠톤 백작부인이 카펫 위로 소리 없이 다가서며 말했다."], ["“I don’t understand it.”", "\"도무지 모르겠구나.\""]], [["Kitty’s lower lip began to quiver; she got up quickly.", "키티의 아랫입술이 떨리기 시작했고, 그녀는 얼른 일어섰다."]], [["“Kitty, you’re not dancing the mazurka?”", "\"키티, 너 마주르카 안 추니?\""]], [["“No, no,”", "\"네, 안 춰요.\""], ["said Kitty in a voice shaking with tears.", "키티가 눈물로 떨리는 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“He asked her for the mazurka before me,”", "\"그가 나보다 먼저 그녀에게 마주르카를 청하더구나.\""], ["said Countess Nordston, knowing Kitty would understand who were “he” and “her.”", "노르츠톤 백작부인이 키티가 '그'와 '그녀'가 누구인지 알아들으리라는 것을 알고서 말했다."], ["“She said: ‘Why, aren’t you going to dance it with Princess Shtcherbatskaya?’”", "\"그러니까 그녀가 이러더라. '아니, 시체르바츠카야 공작 영애와 추시는 거 아니었어요?'\""]], [["“Oh, I don’t care!”", "\"아, 전 상관없어요!\""], ["answered Kitty.", "키티가 대답했다."]], [["No one but she herself understood her position; no one knew that she had just refused the man whom perhaps she loved, and refused him because she had put her faith in another.", "그녀 자신 말고는 아무도 그녀의 처지를 이해하지 못했다. 그녀가 어쩌면 사랑하는지도 모를 남자를 방금 거절했다는 것을, 그것도 다른 한 사람을 믿었기에 거절했다는 것을 아는 사람은 아무도 없었다."]], [["Countess Nordston found Korsunsky, with whom she was to dance the mazurka, and told him to ask Kitty.", "노르츠톤 백작부인은 함께 마주르카를 추기로 한 코르순스키를 찾아내어 키티에게 청하라고 말했다."]], [["Kitty danced in the first couple, and luckily for her she had not to talk, because Korsunsky was all the time running about directing the figure.", "키티는 첫 번째 쌍에서 춤을 추었는데, 다행스럽게도 그녀는 말을 할 필요가 없었으니, 코르순스키가 내내 이리저리 뛰어다니며 춤의 대형을 지휘하고 있었기 때문이다."], ["Vronsky and Anna sat almost opposite her.", "브론스키와 안나는 거의 그녀의 맞은편에 앉아 있었다."], ["She saw them with her long-sighted eyes, and saw them, too, close by, when they met in the figures, and the more she saw of them the more convinced was she that her unhappiness was complete.", "그녀는 멀리 보는 눈으로 그들을 바라보았고, 대형 속에서 마주칠 때면 가까이서도 그들을 보았는데, 그들을 보면 볼수록 자기 불행이 완전한 것임을 더욱 확신하게 되었다."], ["She saw that they felt themselves alone in that crowded room.", "그녀는 그들이 사람들로 붐비는 그 홀 안에서 자기들 둘만 있는 것처럼 느낀다는 것을 알아챘다."], ["And on Vronsky’s face, always so firm and independent, she saw that look that had struck her, of bewilderment and humble submissiveness, like the expression of an intelligent dog when it has done wrong.", "그리고 늘 그토록 단단하고 자주적이던 브론스키의 얼굴에서, 그녀의 마음을 쳤던 그 표정을, 잘못을 저지른 영리한 개의 표정 같은 당혹과 겸허한 복종의 빛을 보았다."]], [["Anna smiled, and her smile was reflected by him.", "안나가 미소 지으면 그 미소가 그에게로 반사되었다."], ["She grew thoughtful, and he became serious.", "그녀가 생각에 잠기면 그도 진지해졌다."], ["Some supernatural force drew Kitty’s eyes to Anna’s face.", "어떤 초자연적인 힘이 키티의 눈길을 안나의 얼굴로 끌어당겼다."], ["She was fascinating in her simple black dress, fascinating were her round arms with their bracelets, fascinating was her firm neck with its thread of pearls, fascinating the straying curls of her loose hair, fascinating the graceful, light movements of her little feet and hands, fascinating was that lovely face in its eagerness, but there was something terrible and cruel in her fascination.", "그 수수한 검은 드레스 차림의 그녀는 매혹적이었고, 팔찌를 두른 그 둥근 두 팔도 매혹적이었으며, 진주 한 줄을 두른 그 탄탄한 목도 매혹적이었고, 풀어 헝클어진 머리의 흘러내린 곱슬머리도 매혹적이었으며, 작은 발과 손의 우아하고 가벼운 움직임도 매혹적이었고, 열기에 찬 그 사랑스러운 얼굴도 매혹적이었지만, 그 매혹에는 어딘가 무섭고 잔인한 무언가가 있었다."]], [["Kitty admired her more than ever, and more and more acute was her suffering.", "키티는 그 어느 때보다 그녀에게 감탄했고, 그럴수록 그녀의 고통은 점점 더 날카로워졌다."], ["Kitty felt overwhelmed, and her face showed it.", "키티는 짓눌리는 기분이었고, 그것이 얼굴에 그대로 드러났다."], ["When Vronsky saw her, coming across her in the mazurka, he did not at once recognize her, she was so changed.", "브론스키는 마주르카에서 그녀와 마주쳤을 때, 그녀가 너무도 달라져 있어 한눈에 알아보지 못했다."]], [["“Delightful ball!”", "\"멋진 무도회죠!\""], ["he said to her, for the sake of saying something.", "그는 무슨 말이라도 하려고 그녀에게 건넸다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["she answered.", "그녀가 대답했다."]], [["In the middle of the mazurka, repeating a complicated figure, newly invented by Korsunsky, Anna came forward into the center of the circle, chose two gentlemen, and summoned a lady and Kitty.", "마주르카 중간에, 코르순스키가 새로 고안한 복잡한 대형을 되풀이하던 중, 안나가 원의 한가운데로 나아가 두 신사를 고르고, 한 부인과 키티를 불렀다."], ["Kitty gazed at her in dismay as she went up.", "키티는 다가가며 당혹스러운 눈으로 그녀를 바라보았다."], ["Anna looked at her with drooping eyelids, and smiled, pressing her hand.", "안나는 눈꺼풀을 내리뜬 채 그녀를 바라보며 미소 지었고, 그녀의 손을 꼭 쥐었다."], ["But, noticing that Kitty only responded to her smile by a look of despair and amazement, she turned away from her, and began gaily talking to the other lady.", "하지만 키티가 자기 미소에 절망과 경악의 눈빛으로만 응한다는 것을 알아채고는, 그녀에게서 몸을 돌려 다른 부인과 즐겁게 이야기하기 시작했다."]], [["“Yes, there is something uncanny, devilish and fascinating in her,”", "\"그래, 저 여자에게는 어딘가 섬뜩하고 악마적이며 매혹적인 무언가가 있어.\""], ["Kitty said to herself.", "키티는 속으로 중얼거렸다."]], [["Anna did not mean to stay to supper, but the master of the house began to press her to do so.", "안나는 야식 자리에 남을 생각이 없었지만, 집주인이 남아 달라고 조르기 시작했다."]], [["“Nonsense, Anna Arkadyevna,”", "\"말도 안 됩니다, 안나 아르카지예브나.\""], ["said Korsunsky, drawing her bare arm under the sleeve of his dress coat, “I’ve such an idea for a cotillion!", "코르순스키가 그녀의 맨팔을 자기 연미복 소맷자락 아래로 끌어당기며 말했다. \"코티용을 위해 기막힌 생각이 떠올랐거든요!"], ["Un bijou!”", "Un bijou!\""]], [["And he moved gradually on, trying to draw her along with him.", "그러고는 그녀를 함께 끌고 가려 애쓰며 조금씩 앞으로 나아갔다."], ["Their host smiled approvingly.", "집주인은 흐뭇한 미소를 지었다."]], [["“No, I am not going to stay,”", "\"아니에요, 전 남지 않겠어요.\""], ["answered Anna, smiling, but in spite of her smile, both Korsunsky and the master of the house saw from her resolute tone that she would not stay.", "안나가 미소 지으며 대답했지만, 그 미소에도 불구하고 코르순스키도 집주인도 그녀의 단호한 어조에서 그녀가 남지 않으리라는 것을 알아챘다."]], [["“No; why, as it is, I have danced more at your ball in Moscow than I have all the winter in Petersburg,”", "\"아니요, 이런 식이라면, 전 페테르부르크에서 겨우내 춘 것보다 모스크바 당신 무도회에서 더 많이 춤을 췄는걸요.\""], ["said Anna, looking round at Vronsky, who stood near her.", "안나가 곁에 서 있던 브론스키를 돌아보며 말했다."], ["“I must rest a little before my journey.”", "\"여행을 떠나기 전에 좀 쉬어 둬야 해요.\""]], [["“Are you certainly going tomorrow then?”", "\"그럼 내일 정말로 떠나시는 겁니까?\""], ["asked Vronsky.", "브론스키가 물었다."]], [["“Yes, I suppose so,”", "\"네, 그럴 것 같아요.\""], ["answered Anna, as it were wondering at the boldness of his question; but the irrepressible, quivering brilliance of her eyes and her smile set him on fire as she said it.", "안나는 그의 질문이 대담하다는 듯 대답했다. 하지만 그녀가 그 말을 하는 순간, 억누를 수 없이 떨리며 빛나는 그녀의 두 눈과 미소가 그를 불태웠다."]], [["Anna Arkadyevna did not stay to supper, but went home.", "안나 아르카지예브나는 야식 자리에 남지 않고 집으로 돌아갔다."]], [["# Chapter 24", "# Chapter 24"]], [["“Yes, there is something in me hateful, repulsive,”", "“그래, 내 안에는 뭔가 밉살스럽고 역겨운 데가 있어.”"], ["thought Levin, as he came away from the Shtcherbatskys’, and walked in the direction of his brother’s lodgings.", "레빈은 셰르바츠키가에서 나와 형의 하숙집 쪽으로 걸어가며 생각했다. “게다가 난 다른 사람들과 잘 어울리지도 못해."], ["“And I don’t get on with other people.", "자존심 때문이라고들 하지."], ["Pride, they say. No, I have no pride.", "아니, 난 자존심 같은 거 없어."], ["If I had any pride, I should not have put myself in such a position.”", "자존심이 조금이라도 있었다면 나 자신을 이런 처지에 몰아넣지도 않았겠지.”"], ["And he pictured to himself Vronsky, happy, good-natured, clever, and self-possessed, certainly never placed in the awful position in which he had been that evening.", "그러면서 그는 행복하고 호인이며 영리하고 침착한 브론스키를, 오늘 저녁 자기가 처했던 그 끔찍한 처지에는 결코 빠질 리 없는 그 사람을 떠올렸다."], ["“Yes, she was bound to choose him.", "“그래, 그녀는 그를 택할 수밖에 없었어."], ["So it had to be, and I cannot complain of anyone or anything.", "그렇게 되어야 했던 거고, 난 누구도 무엇도 탓할 수 없어."], ["I am myself to blame.", "내 잘못이지."], ["What right had I to imagine she would care to join her life to mine?", "그녀가 자기 인생을 내 인생에 묶고 싶어 할 거라고 상상할 권리가 내게 어디 있었나?"], ["Who am I and what am I?", "난 누구고 또 뭔데?"], ["A nobody, not wanted by anyone, nor of use to anybody.”", "아무것도 아닌 인간, 누구에게도 필요치 않고 누구에게도 쓸모없는 인간이지.”"], ["And he recalled his brother Nikolay, and dwelt with pleasure on the thought of him.", "그러고는 형 니콜라이를 떠올리며 그 생각에 즐겁게 잠겼다."], ["“Isn’t he right that everything in the world is base and loathsome?", "“세상의 모든 것이 비루하고 혐오스럽다는 형의 말이 옳지 않은가?"], ["And are we fair in our judgment of brother Nikolay?", "그리고 우리가 니콜라이 형을 두고 내리는 판단이 과연 공정할까?"], ["Of course, from the point of view of Prokofy, seeing him in a torn cloak and tipsy, he’s a despicable person.", "물론 프로코피의 눈으로, 찢어진 외투를 걸치고 술에 취한 그를 본다면, 그는 멸시받아 마땅한 인간이겠지."], ["But I know him differently.", "하지만 난 그를 다르게 알아."], ["I know his soul, and know that we are like him.", "그의 영혼을 알고, 우리도 그와 같은 인간이라는 걸 알지."], ["And I, instead of going to seek him out, went out to dinner, and came here.”", "그런데 난 그를 찾아갈 생각은 하지 않고 만찬에 나갔다가 여기로 왔단 말이야.”"], ["Levin walked up to a lamppost, read his brother’s address, which was in his pocketbook, and called a sledge.", "레빈은 가로등 기둥으로 다가가 수첩에 적힌 형의 주소를 읽고 썰매를 불렀다."], ["All the long way to his brother’s, Levin vividly recalled all the facts familiar to him of his brother Nikolay’s life.", "형의 집까지 가는 긴 길 내내 레빈은 형 니콜라이의 삶에 관해 잘 알고 있던 사실들을 생생히 떠올렸다."], ["He remembered how his brother, while at the university, and for a year afterwards, had, in spite of the jeers of his companions, lived like a monk, strictly observing all religious rites, services, and fasts, and avoiding every sort of pleasure, especially women.", "형이 대학에 다닐 때, 그리고 졸업 후 일 년 동안 동료들의 조롱에도 불구하고 수도사처럼 살았던 것을, 모든 종교 의식과 예배와 단식을 엄격히 지키며 온갖 쾌락, 특히 여자를 멀리했던 것을 떠올렸다."], ["And afterwards, how he had all at once broken out: he had associated with the most horrible people, and rushed into the most senseless debauchery.", "그러다 별안간 어떻게 폭발해 버렸는지도—가장 끔찍한 인간들과 어울리며 더없이 무분별한 방탕에 빠져들었던 일을 떠올렸다."], ["He remembered later the scandal over a boy, whom he had taken from the country to bring up, and, in a fit of rage, had so violently beaten that proceedings were brought against him for unlawfully wounding.", "또 시골에서 데려와 키우려던 소년을 둘러싼 추문도 기억났다. 형은 분노의 발작에 사로잡혀 그 아이를 어찌나 심하게 때렸던지 불법 상해죄로 고소까지 당했다."], ["Then he recalled the scandal with a sharper, to whom he had lost money, and given a promissory note, and against whom he had himself lodged a complaint, asserting that he had cheated him.", "그다음에는 사기 도박꾼과 얽힌 추문을 떠올렸다. 형은 그자에게 돈을 잃고 차용증을 써 주었으면서도, 그자가 자기를 속였다고 주장하며 도리어 고소를 했던 것이다."], ["(This was the money Sergey Ivanovitch had paid.) Then he remembered how he had spent a night in the lockup for disorderly conduct in the street.", "(이때 갚아 준 돈이 바로 세르게이 이바노비치가 낸 것이었다.) 또 형이 거리에서 행패를 부린 죄로 유치장에서 하룻밤을 보낸 일도 떠올렸다."], ["He remembered the shameful proceedings he had tried to get up against his brother Sergey Ivanovitch, accusing him of not having paid him his share of his mother’s fortune, and the last scandal, when he had gone to a western province in an official capacity, and there had got into trouble for assaulting a village elder....", "어머니 재산 중 자기 몫을 주지 않았다며 형 세르게이 이바노비치를 걸어 일으키려 했던 그 수치스러운 소송도, 그리고 관리 자격으로 서부 어느 지방에 갔다가 거기서 촌장을 폭행해 곤경에 처했던 마지막 추문도 떠올렸다……."], ["It was all horribly disgusting, yet to Levin it appeared not at all in the same disgusting light as it inevitably would to those who did not know Nikolay, did not know all his story, did not know his heart.", "그 모든 일이 끔찍하게 역겨웠다. 하지만 레빈에게는 그것이, 니콜라이를 모르고 그의 모든 사연을 모르고 그의 마음을 모르는 이들에게 비칠 그 역겨운 빛깔로는 결코 보이지 않았다."]], [["Levin remembered that when Nikolay had been in the devout stage, the period of fasts and monks and church services, when he was seeking in religion a support and a curb for his passionate temperament, everyone, far from encouraging him, had jeered at him, and he, too, with the others.", "레빈은 니콜라이가 독실하던 시절, 단식과 수도사와 교회 예배의 시기, 그러니까 종교에서 자기 격정적 기질을 떠받칠 버팀목이자 그것을 억누를 재갈을 찾던 그 시절에, 누구도 그를 격려하기는커녕 모두가 그를 조롱했고, 자신도 다른 이들과 함께 그랬다는 사실을 떠올렸다."], ["They had teased him, called him Noah, and monk; and, when he had broken out, no one had helped him, but everyone had turned away from him with horror and disgust.", "사람들은 형을 놀려 대며 노아니 수도사니 불렀고, 형이 폭발해 버렸을 때 아무도 그를 돕지 않은 채 모두가 공포와 혐오로 그에게서 등을 돌렸던 것이다."]], [["Levin felt that, in spite of all the ugliness of his life, his brother Nikolay, in his soul, in the very depths of his soul, was no more in the wrong than the people who despised him.", "레빈은 형 니콜라이가, 그의 삶이 아무리 추악하더라도, 그 영혼에서, 그 영혼의 가장 깊은 곳에서는, 자기를 멸시하는 사람들보다 조금도 더 그릇되지 않다고 느꼈다."], ["He was not to blame for having been born with his unbridled temperament and his somehow limited intelligence.", "형이 고삐 풀린 기질과 어딘가 한정된 지성을 지니고 태어난 것은 그의 죄가 아니었다."], ["But he had always wanted to be good.", "그러나 그는 늘 선해지기를 원했다."], ["“I will tell him everything, without reserve, and I will make him speak without reserve, too, and I’ll show him that I love him, and so understand him,”", "‘형한테 숨김없이 다 털어놓을 거야. 그리고 형도 숨김없이 이야기하게 만들 거야."], ["Levin resolved to himself, as, towards eleven o’clock, he reached the hotel of which he had the address.", "내가 형을 사랑하고 그래서 형을 이해한다는 걸 보여 줄 거야.’ 레빈은 열한 시가 다 되어 주소에 적힌 호텔에 이르렀을 때 이렇게 마음먹었다."]], [["“At the top, 12 and 13,”", "“맨 위층, 12호하고 13호입니다.”"], ["the porter answered Levin’s inquiry.", "문지기가 레빈의 물음에 대답했다."]], [["“At home?”", "“계신가요?”"]], [["“Sure to be at home.”", "“계실 게 틀림없습니다.”"]], [["The door of No.", "12호의 문은 반쯤 열려 있었고, 그 새어 나오는 한 줄기 빛 속으로 값싸고 질 나쁜 담배의 짙은 연기와, 레빈이 모르는 어떤 목소리가 흘러나왔다."], ["12 was half open, and there came out into the streak of light thick fumes of cheap, poor tobacco, and the sound of a voice, unknown to Levin; but he knew at once that his brother was there; he heard his cough.", "하지만 그는 형이 그 안에 있음을 단번에 알아챘다. 형의 기침 소리를 들었던 것이다."]], [["As he went in the door, the unknown voice was saying:", "문 안으로 들어서는데 그 낯선 목소리가 이렇게 말하고 있었다."]], [["“It all depends with how much judgment and knowledge the thing’s done.”", "“그게 다 얼마나 분별과 지식을 갖고 일을 하느냐에 달린 거지요.”"]], [["Konstantin Levin looked in at the door, and saw that the speaker was a young man with an immense shock of hair, wearing a Russian jerkin, and that a pockmarked woman in a woolen gown, without collar or cuffs, was sitting on the sofa.", "콘스탄틴 레빈은 문 안을 들여다보았고, 말하는 이가 머리숱이 엄청나게 많고 러시아식 윗도리를 걸친 젊은 남자라는 것을, 그리고 깃도 소맷부리도 없는 모직 가운을 입은 곰보 여자가 소파에 앉아 있는 것을 보았다."], ["His brother was not to be seen.", "형은 보이지 않았다."], ["Konstantin felt a sharp pang at his heart at the thought of the strange company in which his brother spent his life.", "콘스탄틴은 형이 이런 묘한 무리 속에서 인생을 보내고 있다는 생각에 가슴이 날카롭게 저려 왔다."], ["No one had heard him, and Konstantin, taking off his galoshes, listened to what the gentleman in the jerkin was saying.", "아무도 그의 인기척을 듣지 못했고, 콘스탄틴은 덧신을 벗으며 윗도리 차림의 남자가 하는 말에 귀를 기울였다."], ["He was speaking of some enterprise.", "그는 무슨 사업 이야기를 하고 있었다."]], [["“Well, the devil flay them, the privileged classes,”", "“흥, 그놈들, 특권 계급 놈들 따위 악마한테 가죽이나 벗기라지.”"], ["his brother’s voice responded, with a cough.", "형의 목소리가 기침과 함께 대꾸했다."], ["“Masha!", "“마샤!"], ["get us some supper and some wine if there’s any left; or else go and get some.”", "저녁거리 좀 차려 오고, 술 남은 게 있으면 그것도. 없으면 나가서 사 와.”"]], [["The woman rose, came out from behind the screen, and saw Konstantin.", "여자가 일어나 칸막이 뒤에서 나왔다가 콘스탄틴을 보았다."]], [["“There’s some gentleman, Nikolay Dmitrievitch,”", "“손님이 오셨어요, 니콜라이 드미트리예비치.”"], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“Whom do you want?”", "“누굴 찾으시오?”"], ["said the voice of Nikolay Levin, angrily.", "니콜라이 레빈의 목소리가 성난 어조로 물었다."]], [["“It’s I,”", "“나야.”"], ["answered Konstantin Levin, coming forward into the light.", "콘스탄틴 레빈이 빛 속으로 나서며 대답했다."]], [["“Who’s I?” Nikolay’s voice said again, still more angrily.", "“‘나’라니 누구야?”"], ["He could be heard getting up hurriedly, stumbling against something, and Levin saw, facing him in the doorway, the big, scared eyes, and the huge, thin, stooping figure of his brother, so familiar, and yet astonishing in its weirdness and sickliness.", "니콜라이의 목소리가 더욱더 성난 어조로 다시 물었다. 황급히 일어나다 무언가에 부딪치는 소리가 들렸고, 레빈은 문간에서 자기를 마주 보고 선, 크고 겁먹은 눈과, 너무도 낯익으면서도 그 기괴함과 병색이 사람을 깜짝 놀라게 하는, 거대하고 야위고 구부정한 형의 모습을 보았다."]], [["He was even thinner than three years before, when Konstantin Levin had seen him last.", "형은 콘스탄틴 레빈이 마지막으로 보았던 삼 년 전보다도 한층 더 야위어 있었다."], ["He was wearing a short coat, and his hands and big bones seemed huger than ever.", "짧은 외투를 걸치고 있었는데, 손과 굵은 뼈마디가 어느 때보다 더 거대해 보였다."], ["His hair had grown thinner, the same straight mustaches hid his lips, the same eyes gazed strangely and naïvely at his visitor.", "머리숱은 더 줄었고, 똑같은 곧은 콧수염이 입술을 가렸으며, 똑같은 눈이 방문객을 묘하고도 천진하게 바라보았다."]], [["“Ah, Kostya!”", "“아, 코스챠!”"], ["he exclaimed suddenly, recognizing his brother, and his eyes lit up with joy.", "형이 동생을 알아보고 별안간 소리쳤고, 그의 눈이 기쁨으로 빛났다."], ["But the same second he looked round at the young man, and gave the nervous jerk of his head and neck that Konstantin knew so well, as if his neckband hurt him; and a quite different expression, wild, suffering, and cruel, rested on his emaciated face.", "하지만 바로 그 순간 그는 젊은 남자 쪽을 돌아보더니, 콘스탄틴이 너무도 잘 아는, 마치 옷깃이 목을 죄기라도 하는 듯 머리와 목을 신경질적으로 홱 젖히는 동작을 했다. 그러자 사뭇 다른 표정이, 사납고 고통스럽고 잔인한 표정이 그의 수척한 얼굴에 자리 잡았다."]], [["“I wrote to you and Sergey Ivanovitch both that I don’t know you and don’t want to know you.", "“너랑 세르게이 이바노비치 둘 다한테 편지로 썼잖아, 너희를 모르고 알고 싶지도 않다고."], ["What is it you want?”", "뭘 원하는 거야?”"]], [["He was not at all the same as Konstantin had been fancying him.", "형은 콘스탄틴이 그려 보던 모습과는 전혀 딴판이었다."], ["The worst and most tiresome part of his character, what made all relations with him so difficult, had been forgotten by Konstantin Levin when he thought of him, and now, when he saw his face, and especially that nervous twitching of his head, he remembered it all.", "그의 성격에서 가장 고약하고 가장 진저리 나는 부분, 그와의 모든 관계를 그토록 어렵게 만드는 그 부분을, 콘스탄틴 레빈은 형을 떠올릴 때면 까맣게 잊고 있었던 것이다. 그런데 이제 형의 얼굴을, 특히 그 신경질적인 머리 경련을 보자, 그 모든 것이 다시 떠올랐다."]], [["“I didn’t want to see you for anything,”", "“형을 만나는 데 무슨 다른 뜻이 있었던 게 아니야.”"], ["he answered timidly.", "그는 머뭇거리며 대답했다."], ["“I’ve simply come to see you.”", "“그냥 형을 보러 온 거야.”"]], [["His brother’s timidity obviously softened Nikolay.", "동생의 머뭇거리는 태도가 분명히 니콜라이의 마음을 누그러뜨렸다."], ["His lips twitched.", "그의 입술이 씰룩거렸다."]], [["“Oh, so that’s it?” he said.", "“오, 그래서 그런 거였어?”"], ["“Well, come in; sit down.", "그가 말했다. “그럼 들어와, 앉아."], ["Like some supper?", "저녁 좀 먹겠어?"], ["Masha, bring supper for three.", "마샤, 세 사람 분 저녁 차려 와."], ["No, stop a minute.", "아니, 잠깐 기다려."], ["Do you know who this is?”", "너 이 사람이 누군지 아니?”"], ["he said, addressing his brother, and indicating the gentleman in the jerkin: “This is Mr.", "그는 동생을 향해 윗도리 차림의 남자를 가리키며 말했다."], ["Kritsky, my friend from Kiev, a very remarkable man.", "“이 사람은 크리츠키 씨야, 키예프에서부터 알고 지낸 내 친구지, 아주 비범한 사람이야."], ["He’s persecuted by the police, of course, because he’s not a scoundrel.”", "물론 경찰한테 박해를 받고 있지, 악당이 아니니까 말이야.”"]], [["And he looked round in the way he always did at everyone in the room.", "그러고는 늘 그러듯 방 안에 있는 사람들을 둘러보았다."], ["Seeing that the woman standing in the doorway was moving to go, he shouted to her, “Wait a minute, I said.”", "문간에 서 있던 여자가 나가려고 움직이는 것을 보자 그가 소리쳤다. “기다리라고 했잖아.”"], ["And with the inability to express himself, the incoherence that Konstantin knew so well, he began, with another look round at everyone, to tell his brother Kritsky’s story: how he had been expelled from the university for starting a benefit society for the poor students and Sunday schools; and how he had afterwards been a teacher in a peasant school, and how he had been driven out of that too, and had afterwards been condemned for something.", "그러고는 콘스탄틴이 너무도 잘 아는, 자기 생각을 표현하지 못하는 그 횡설수설하는 말투로, 다시 한번 모두를 둘러보면서 동생에게 크리츠키의 이야기를 들려주기 시작했다. 그가 가난한 학생들을 위한 공제회와 일요 학교를 세우려다 대학에서 쫓겨난 일, 그 후 농민 학교 교사가 되었다가 거기서도 내쫓긴 일, 그러고 나서 무슨 일로 유죄 선고까지 받은 일에 대해서였다."]], [["“You’re of the Kiev university?”", "“키예프 대학 출신이십니까?”"], ["said Konstantin Levin to Kritsky, to break the awkward silence that followed.", "그 뒤에 이어진 어색한 침묵을 깨려고 콘스탄틴 레빈이 크리츠키에게 물었다."]], [["“Yes, I was of Kiev,”", "“그렇소, 키예프 출신이오.”"], ["Kritsky replied angrily, his face darkening.", "크리츠키가 얼굴을 어둡게 하며 성난 어조로 대답했다."]], [["“And this woman,”", "“그리고 이 여자는,”"], ["Nikolay Levin interrupted him, pointing to her, “is the partner of my life, Marya Nikolaevna.", "니콜라이 레빈이 그녀를 가리키며 동생의 말을 가로막았다. “내 인생의 반려, 마리야 니콜라예브나야."], ["I took her out of a bad house,”", "내가 나쁜 집에서 데려왔지.”"], ["and he jerked his neck saying this; “but I love her and respect her, and anyone who wants to know me,”", "그는 이 말을 하면서 목을 홱 젖혔다. “하지만 난 이 여자를 사랑하고 존중해."], ["he added, raising his voice and knitting his brows, “I beg to love her and respect her.", "그러니 나를 알고 지내고 싶은 사람은 누구든,” 그는 목소리를 높이고 미간을 찌푸리며 덧붙였다."], ["She’s just the same as my wife, just the same.", "“이 여자를 사랑하고 존중해 줘야 해. 이 여자는 내 아내나 똑같아, 똑같다고."], ["So now you know whom you’ve to do with.", "자, 이제 네가 누굴 상대하는지 알았겠지."], ["And if you think you’re lowering yourself, well, here’s the floor, there’s the door.”", "그리고 이게 네 격을 떨어뜨리는 일이라고 생각된다면, 자, 여기 바닥이 있고 저기 문이 있으니.\""]], [["And again his eyes traveled inquiringly over all of them.", "그러고는 또다시 그의 눈이 캐묻듯 그들 모두를 훑었다."]], [["“Why I should be lowering myself, I don’t understand.”", "“내 격이 왜 떨어진다는 건지 난 모르겠어.”"]], [["“Then, Masha, tell them to bring supper; three portions, spirits and wine....", "“그럼, 마샤, 저녁 가져오라고 해. 세 사람 분, 보드카하고 포도주도……."], ["No, wait a minute....", "아니, 잠깐 기다려……."], ["No, it doesn’t matter....", "아니, 됐어……."], ["Go along.”", "가 봐.”"]], [["# Chapter 25", "# Chapter 25"]], [["“So you see,”", "니콜라이 레빈은 이마를 괴롭게 찌푸리며 몸을 씰룩거렸다."], ["pursued Nikolay Levin, painfully wrinkling his forehead and twitching.", "\"그러니까 알겠지.\" 그가 말을 이었다."]], [["It was obviously difficult for him to think of what to say and do.", "무슨 말을 하고 무엇을 해야 할지 떠올리기가 그에게는 분명 힘겨워 보였다."]], [["“Here, do you see?”...", "\"여기, 보이지?……\""], ["He pointed to some sort of iron bars, fastened together with strings, lying in a corner of the room.", "그는 방 한구석에 끈으로 묶인 채 놓여 있는 무슨 쇠막대 같은 것들을 가리켰다."], ["“Do you see that?", "\"저게 보이나?"], ["That’s the beginning of a new thing we’re going into.", "저게 우리가 새로 시작하려는 일의 출발점이야."], ["It’s a productive association....”", "생산 협동조합이라고…….\""]], [["Konstantin scarcely heard him.", "콘스탄친은 그의 말을 거의 듣지 못했다."], ["He looked into his sickly, consumptive face, and he was more and more sorry for him, and he could not force himself to listen to what his brother was telling him about the association.", "그는 형의 병약하고 폐병 든 얼굴을 들여다보고 있었고, 점점 더 형이 가여워졌으며, 형이 협동조합에 대해 늘어놓는 이야기에 도무지 귀를 기울일 수가 없었다."], ["He saw that this association was a mere anchor to save him from self-contempt.", "그는 이 협동조합이라는 것이 형에게는 자기혐오에서 벗어나기 위한 한낱 닻에 지나지 않는다는 것을 알아차렸다."], ["Nikolay Levin went on talking:", "니콜라이 레빈은 계속 말을 이었다."]], [["“You know that capital oppresses the laborer.", "\"알다시피 자본은 노동자를 억압해."], ["The laborers with us, the peasants, bear all the burden of labor, and are so placed that however much they work they can’t escape from their position of beasts of burden.", "우리네 노동자들, 그러니까 농민들이 노동의 짐을 전부 짊어지고 있는데, 그들은 아무리 일해도 짐을 나르는 짐승 같은 처지에서 벗어날 수 없도록 놓여 있단 말이지."], ["All the profits of labor, on which they might improve their position, and gain leisure for themselves, and after that education, all the surplus values are taken from them by the capitalists.", "노동에서 나오는 모든 이윤, 그걸로 그들이 자기 처지를 개선하고 여가를 얻고 그다음엔 교육까지 받을 수 있을 텐데, 그 모든 잉여 가치를 자본가들이 빼앗아 가지."], ["And society’s so constituted that the harder they work, the greater the profit of the merchants and landowners, while they stay beasts of burden to the end.", "그리고 사회라는 게 그렇게 짜여 있어서, 그들이 열심히 일하면 일할수록 상인과 지주의 이윤만 커지고, 그들은 끝까지 짐 나르는 짐승으로 남는 거야."], ["And that state of things must be changed,”", "그런 상태는 반드시 바뀌어야 해.\""], ["he finished up, and he looked questioningly at his brother.", "그는 이렇게 말을 맺고는 미심쩍은 눈으로 동생을 바라보았다."]], [["“Yes, of course,”", "\"그래, 물론이지.\""], ["said Konstantin, looking at the patch of red that had come out on his brother’s projecting cheekbones.", "콘스탄친은 형의 튀어나온 광대뼈에 돋아난 붉은 반점을 바라보며 말했다."]], [["“And so we’re founding a locksmiths’ association, where all the production and profit and the chief instruments of production will be in common.”", "\"그래서 우리는 자물쇠공 협동조합을 세우려는 거야. 거기서는 모든 생산과 이윤, 그리고 주요한 생산 도구가 공동의 것이 되지.\""]], [["“Where is the association to be?”", "\"그 협동조합은 어디에 두는데?\""], ["asked Konstantin Levin.", "콘스탄친 레빈이 물었다."]], [["“In the village of Vozdrem, Kazan government.”", "\"카잔 현 보즈드렘 마을에.\""]], [["“But why in a village?", "\"하지만 왜 하필 마을에?"], ["In the villages, I think, there is plenty of work as it is.", "내 생각엔 마을엔 지금도 일거리가 넘치는데."], ["Why a locksmiths’ association in a village?”", "왜 마을에 자물쇠공 협동조합이지?\""]], [["“Why?", "\"왜냐고?"], ["Because the peasants are just as much slaves as they ever were, and that’s why you and Sergey Ivanovitch don’t like people to try and get them out of their slavery,”", "농민들이 예나 지금이나 똑같이 노예이기 때문이고, 바로 그래서 형이랑 세르게이 이바노비치는 사람들이 그들을 그 노예 상태에서 끌어내려고 애쓰는 걸 못마땅해하는 거지.\""], ["said Nikolay Levin, exasperated by the objection.", "니콜라이 레빈이 그 반박에 발끈하며 말했다."]], [["Konstantin Levin sighed, looking meanwhile about the cheerless and dirty room.", "콘스탄친 레빈은 한숨을 내쉬며 그 음울하고 더러운 방을 둘러보았다."], ["This sigh seemed to exasperate Nikolay still more.", "이 한숨이 니콜라이를 더욱 발끈하게 만든 듯했다."]], [["“I know your and Sergey Ivanovitch’s aristocratic views.", "\"네놈과 세르게이 이바노비치의 그 귀족적인 관점을 내가 알지."], ["I know that he applies all the power of his intellect to justify existing evils.”", "그 사람이 자기 지성의 온갖 힘을 다해 현존하는 악을 정당화하는 데에 쓰고 있다는 걸 안다고.\""]], [["“No; and what do you talk of Sergey Ivanovitch for?”", "\"아니, 그런데 왜 세르게이 이바노비치 얘기를 꺼내는 거야?\""], ["said Levin, smiling.", "레빈이 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["“Sergey Ivanovitch?", "\"세르게이 이바노비치?"], ["I’ll tell you what for!”", "왜 꺼내냐고 말해 주지!\""], ["Nikolay Levin shrieked suddenly at the name of Sergey Ivanovitch.", "니콜라이 레빈은 세르게이 이바노비치라는 이름에 별안간 소리를 질렀다."], ["“I’ll tell you what for....", "\"왜 그러는지 말해 주마……"], ["But what’s the use of talking?", "하지만 떠들어 봐야 무슨 소용이야?"], ["There’s only one thing....", "다만 한 가지뿐이지……"], ["What did you come to me for?", "넌 뭐 하러 나를 찾아왔어?"], ["You look down on this, and you’re welcome to,—and go away, in God’s name go away!”", "넌 이걸 깔보고 있고, 그래도 좋아—그러니 가 버려, 제발 가 버리라고!\""], ["he shrieked, getting up from his chair.", "그는 의자에서 벌떡 일어나며 소리쳤다."], ["“And go away, and go away!”", "\"가 버려, 가 버리란 말이야!\""]], [["“I don’t look down on it at all,”", "\"난 이걸 전혀 깔보지 않아.\""], ["said Konstantin Levin timidly.", "콘스탄친 레빈이 머뭇거리며 말했다."], ["“I don’t even dispute it.”", "\"반박조차 하지 않잖아.\""]], [["At that instant Marya Nikolaevna came back.", "바로 그 순간 마리야 니콜라예브나가 돌아왔다."], ["Nikolay Levin looked round angrily at her.", "니콜라이 레빈은 성난 눈으로 그녀를 돌아보았다."], ["She went quickly to him, and whispered something.", "그녀는 얼른 그에게 다가가 무어라고 속삭였다."]], [["“I’m not well; I’ve grown irritable,”", "\"내가 몸이 안 좋아서 신경이 곤두서 있었어.\""], ["said Nikolay Levin, getting calmer and breathing painfully; “and then you talk to me of Sergey Ivanovitch and his article.", "니콜라이 레빈이 차분해지면서도 괴롭게 숨을 몰아쉬며 말했다. \"게다가 너는 나한테 세르게이 이바노비치니 그의 논문이니 하는 얘기를 꺼내고."], ["It’s such rubbish, such lying, such self-deception.", "그건 죄다 쓰레기야, 죄다 거짓말이고, 죄다 자기기만이지."], ["What can a man write of justice who knows nothing of it?", "정의에 대해 아무것도 모르는 인간이 정의에 관해 뭘 쓸 수 있겠어?"], ["Have you read his article?”", "너 그 사람 논문 읽어 봤나?\""], ["he asked Kritsky, sitting down again at the table, and moving back off half of it the scattered cigarettes, so as to clear a space.", "그는 크리츠키에게 물으며 다시 탁자에 앉더니, 자리를 비우려고 흩어진 담배를 절반쯤 옆으로 밀어냈다."]], [["“I’ve not read it,”", "\"안 읽었습니다.\""], ["Kritsky responded gloomily, obviously not desiring to enter into the conversation.", "크리츠키는 대화에 끼어들고 싶지 않은 기색이 역력하게 침울하게 대답했다."]], [["“Why not?”", "\"왜 안 읽었어?\""], ["said Nikolay Levin, now turning with exasperation upon Kritsky.", "니콜라이 레빈이 이번엔 발끈하며 크리츠키에게로 돌아앉았다."]], [["“Because I didn’t see the use of wasting my time over it.”", "\"거기에 시간을 허비할 가치가 없다고 봤으니까요.\""]], [["“Oh, but excuse me, how did you know it would be wasting your time?", "\"오, 하지만 실례지만, 그게 시간 낭비라는 걸 어떻게 알았지?"], ["That article’s too deep for many people—that’s to say it’s over their heads.", "그 논문은 많은 사람에게 너무 깊어서—그러니까 그들 머리를 훌쩍 넘어선단 말이야."], ["But with me, it’s another thing; I see through his ideas, and I know where its weakness lies.”", "하지만 나는 다르지. 나는 그의 생각을 꿰뚫어 보고, 그 약점이 어디 있는지도 안다고.\""]], [["Everyone was mute.", "모두가 입을 다물었다."], ["Kritsky got up deliberately and reached his cap.", "크리츠키가 느릿느릿 일어나 모자를 집었다."]], [["“Won’t you have supper?", "\"저녁 안 먹고 가나?"], ["All right, good-bye!", "좋아, 잘 가게!"], ["Come round tomorrow with the locksmith.”", "내일 자물쇠공 데리고 다시 들르라고.\""]], [["Kritsky had hardly gone out when Nikolay Levin smiled and winked.", "크리츠키가 채 나가기도 전에 니콜라이 레빈은 미소를 지으며 눈을 찡긋했다."]], [["“He’s no good either,”", "\"저자도 별 볼 일 없어.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“I see, of course....”", "\"물론 나도 알지…….\""]], [["But at that instant Kritsky, at the door, called him....", "하지만 바로 그 순간 문가에서 크리츠키가 그를 불렀다……"]], [["“What do you want now?”", "\"또 뭘 바라는 거야?\""], ["he said, and went out to him in the passage.", "그는 이렇게 말하며 복도로 그를 따라 나갔다."], ["Left alone with Marya Nikolaevna, Levin turned to her.", "마리야 니콜라예브나와 단둘이 남게 되자 레빈은 그녀에게로 돌아섰다."]], [["“Have you been long with my brother?”", "\"내 형과 오래 함께 지냈소?\""], ["he said to her.", "그가 그녀에게 물었다."]], [["“Yes, more than a year.", "\"네, 일 년 넘게요."], ["Nikolay Dmitrievitch’s health has become very poor.", "니콜라이 드미트리예비치의 건강이 무척 나빠졌어요."], ["Nikolay Dmitrievitch drinks a great deal,”", "니콜라이 드미트리예비치는 술을 너무 많이 마셔요.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“That is ...", "\"그게……"], ["how does he drink?”", "어떻게 마신단 말이오?\""]], [["“Drinks vodka, and it’s bad for him.”", "\"보드카를 마시는데, 몸에 해롭지요.\""]], [["“And a great deal?”", "\"그것도 많이?\""], ["whispered Levin.", "레빈이 속삭였다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["she said, looking timidly towards the doorway, where Nikolay Levin had reappeared.", "그녀는 니콜라이 레빈이 다시 모습을 드러낸 문간 쪽을 머뭇머뭇 바라보며 말했다."]], [["“What were you talking about?”", "\"무슨 얘기를 하고 있었어?\""], ["he said, knitting his brows, and turning his scared eyes from one to the other.", "그가 미간을 찌푸리며 겁먹은 눈을 이쪽저쪽으로 옮기며 말했다."], ["“What was it?”", "\"뭐였어?\""]], [["“Oh, nothing,”", "\"아, 아무것도 아니야.\""], ["Konstantin answered in confusion.", "콘스탄친이 당황하여 대답했다."]], [["“Oh, if you don’t want to say, don’t.", "\"오, 말하기 싫으면 말고."], ["Only it’s no good your talking to her.", "다만 저 여자한테 얘기해 봐야 소용없어."], ["She’s a wench, and you’re a gentleman,”", "저 여잔 계집이고, 넌 신사니까.\""], ["he said with a jerk of the neck.", "그는 목을 홱 젖히며 말했다."], ["“You understand everything, I see, and have taken stock of everything, and look with commiseration on my shortcomings,”", "\"넌 다 이해하고, 다 평가하고, 내 결점들을 가엾다는 눈으로 내려다보지.\""], ["he began again, raising his voice.", "그는 목소리를 높이며 다시 말을 꺼냈다."]], [["“Nikolay Dmitrievitch, Nikolay Dmitrievitch,”", "\"니콜라이 드미트리예비치, 니콜라이 드미트리예비치.\""], ["whispered Marya Nikolaevna, again going up to him.", "마리야 니콜라예브나가 다시 그에게 다가가며 속삭였다."]], [["“Oh, very well, very well!...", "\"오, 알았어, 알았다고!……"], ["But where’s the supper?", "그런데 저녁은 어디 있지?"], ["Ah, here it is,”", "아, 여기 왔군.\""], ["he said, seeing a waiter with a tray.", "그는 쟁반을 든 종업원을 보고 말했다."], ["“Here, set it here,”", "\"자, 여기 놓게.\""], ["he added angrily, and promptly seizing the vodka, he poured out a glassful and drank it greedily.", "그는 성마르게 덧붙이고는, 냉큼 보드카를 집어 한 잔 가득 따라 게걸스럽게 들이켰다."], ["“Like a drink?”", "\"한잔할 텐가?\""], ["he turned to his brother, and at once became better humored.", "그는 동생에게로 돌아섰고, 이내 기분이 한결 누그러졌다."]], [["“Well, enough of Sergey Ivanovitch.", "\"자, 세르게이 이바노비치 얘긴 그만하지."], ["I’m glad to see you, anyway.", "어쨌든 널 보니 반갑구나."], ["After all’s said and done, we’re not strangers.", "이러니저러니 해도 우린 남이 아니잖아."], ["Come, have a drink.", "자, 한잔하자."], ["Tell me what you’re doing,”", "너는 뭘 하고 지내는지 얘기해 봐.\""], ["he went on, greedily munching a piece of bread, and pouring out another glassful.", "그는 빵 한 조각을 게걸스레 씹으면서 또 한 잔을 가득 따르며 말을 이었다."], ["“How are you living?”", "\"어떻게 살고 있어?\""]], [["“I live alone in the country, as I used to.", "\"전처럼 시골에서 혼자 살아."], ["I’m busy looking after the land,”", "땅을 돌보느라 바쁘지.\""], ["answered Konstantin, watching with horror the greediness with which his brother ate and drank, and trying to conceal that he noticed it.", "콘스탄친은 형이 먹고 마시는 그 게걸스러움을 끔찍한 심정으로 지켜보며, 자기가 그걸 알아챘다는 티를 감추려 애쓰며 대답했다."]], [["“Why don’t you get married?”", "\"넌 왜 결혼을 안 해?\""]], [["“It hasn’t happened so,”", "\"그렇게 안 됐어.\""], ["Konstantin answered, reddening a little.", "콘스탄친이 살짝 얼굴을 붉히며 대답했다."]], [["“Why not? For me now ...", "\"왜 안 했어?"], ["everything’s at an end!", "나야 이제…… 다 끝났지!"], ["I’ve made a mess of my life.", "내 인생은 엉망진창으로 만들어 버렸어."], ["But this I’ve said, and I say still, that if my share had been given me when I needed it, my whole life would have been different.”", "하지만 이건 전에도 말했고 지금도 말하는데, 내가 필요로 할 때 내 몫을 받았더라면 내 인생 전체가 달랐을 거라고.\""]], [["Konstantin made haste to change the conversation.", "콘스탄친은 서둘러 화제를 돌렸다."]], [["“Do you know your little Vanya’s with me, a clerk in the countinghouse at Pokrovskoe.”", "\"형의 그 어린 바냐가 나랑 같이 있는 거 알아? 포크롭스코예 회계 사무소의 사무원으로 말이야.\""]], [["Nikolay jerked his neck, and sank into thought.", "니콜라이는 목을 홱 젖히더니 생각에 잠겼다."]], [["“Yes, tell me what’s going on at Pokrovskoe.", "\"그래, 포크롭스코예가 어떤지 말해 봐."], ["Is the house standing still, and the birch trees, and our schoolroom?", "그 집은 여전히 서 있나, 자작나무들이랑 우리 공부방도?"], ["And Philip the gardener, is he living?", "그리고 정원사 필립은, 그자는 살아 있어?"], ["How I remember the arbor and the seat!", "정자랑 그 의자가 어찌나 생각나는지!"], ["Now mind and don’t alter anything in the house, but make haste and get married, and make everything as it used to be again.", "자, 명심해, 집 안의 어떤 것도 바꾸지 말고, 어서 결혼이나 해서 모든 걸 예전처럼 다시 만들어."], ["Then I’ll come and see you, if your wife is nice.”", "그러면 네 아내가 괜찮은 사람이라면 내가 보러 가지.\""]], [["“But come to me now,”", "\"하지만 지금 나한테로 와.\""], ["said Levin.", "레빈이 말했다."], ["“How nicely we would arrange it!”", "\"우리가 얼마나 멋지게 꾸려 나갈지!\""]], [["“I’d come and see you if I were sure I should not find Sergey Ivanovitch.”", "\"세르게이 이바노비치를 마주칠 일이 없다는 걸 확신할 수만 있으면 가서 너를 보겠지.\""]], [["“You wouldn’t find him there.", "\"거기서 그 사람을 마주칠 일 없어."], ["I live quite independently of him.”", "난 그 사람과 완전히 따로 살아.\""]], [["“Yes, but say what you like, you will have to choose between me and him,”", "\"그래, 하지만 네가 뭐라고 하든, 너는 나와 그 사람 중에서 골라야 할 거야.\""], ["he said, looking timidly into his brother’s face.", "그는 머뭇거리며 동생의 얼굴을 들여다보며 말했다."]], [["This timidity touched Konstantin.", "이 머뭇거림이 콘스탄친의 마음을 건드렸다."]], [["“If you want to hear my confession of faith on the subject, I tell you that in your quarrel with Sergey Ivanovitch I take neither side.", "\"이 문제에 대한 내 신념을 듣고 싶다면 말해 주지. 형과 세르게이 이바노비치의 다툼에서 난 어느 편도 들지 않아."], ["You’re both wrong.", "둘 다 틀렸어."], ["You’re more wrong externally, and he inwardly.”", "형은 겉으로 더 틀렸고, 그 사람은 속으로 더 틀렸지.\""]], [["“Ah, ah!", "\"아, 아!"], ["You see that, you see that!”", "너도 그게 보이는구나, 보이는구나!\""], ["Nikolay shouted joyfully.", "니콜라이가 기쁘게 외쳤다."]], [["“But I personally value friendly relations with you more because....”", "\"하지만 나 개인적으로는 형과의 정겨운 관계를 더 소중히 여겨. 왜냐하면…….\""]], [["“Why, why?”", "\"왜, 왜지?\""]], [["Konstantin could not say that he valued it more because Nikolay was unhappy, and needed affection.", "콘스탄친은, 니콜라이가 불행하고 애정을 필요로 하기에 그것을 더 소중히 여긴다고는 차마 말할 수 없었다."], ["But Nikolay knew that this was just what he meant to say, and scowling he took up the vodka again.", "하지만 니콜라이는 동생이 하려던 말이 바로 그것임을 알아챘고, 인상을 찌푸리며 다시 보드카를 집어 들었다."]], [["“Enough, Nikolay Dmitrievitch!”", "\"그만하세요, 니콜라이 드미트리예비치!\""], ["said Marya Nikolaevna, stretching out her plump, bare arm towards the decanter.", "마리야 니콜라예브나가 통통한 맨팔을 술병 쪽으로 뻗으며 말했다."]], [["“Let it be!", "\"내버려 둬!"], ["Don’t insist!", "고집 부리지 마!"], ["I’ll beat you!”", "한 대 때려 줄 테니!\""], ["he shouted.", "그가 소리쳤다."]], [["Marya Nikolaevna smiled a sweet and good-humored smile, which was at once reflected on Nikolay’s face, and she took the bottle.", "마리야 니콜라예브나는 다정하고 너그러운 미소를 지었고, 그 미소는 곧장 니콜라이의 얼굴에도 비쳤다. 그녀는 술병을 거두었다."]], [["“And do you suppose she understands nothing?”", "\"그래, 너는 저 여자가 아무것도 이해 못 한다고 생각하지?\""], ["said Nikolay.", "니콜라이가 말했다."], ["“She understands it all better than any of us.", "\"저 여자는 우리 누구보다도 모든 걸 더 잘 이해해."], ["Isn’t it true there’s something good and sweet in her?”", "저 여자한테 뭔가 착하고 사랑스러운 데가 있는 게 사실 아니냐?\""]], [["“Were you never before in Moscow?”", "\"전에 모스크바에 와 본 적이 없소?\""], ["Konstantin said to her, for the sake of saying something.", "콘스탄친은 무어라도 말하려고 그녀에게 물었다."]], [["“Only you mustn’t be polite and stiff with her.", "\"단, 저 여자한테 예의 차린답시고 딱딱하게 굴진 마."], ["It frightens her.", "저 여잔 그러면 겁먹어."], ["No one ever spoke to her so but the justices of the peace who tried her for trying to get out of a house of ill-fame.", "저 여자한테 그렇게 말을 건넨 건 매음굴에서 빠져나오려 했다고 저 여자를 재판한 치안 판사들뿐이었으니까."], ["Mercy on us, the senselessness in the world!”", "맙소사, 세상에 이런 몰지각함이라니!\""], ["he cried suddenly.", "그가 별안간 외쳤다."], ["“These new institutions, these justices of the peace, rural councils, what hideousness it all is!”", "\"이 새로운 기관들, 이 치안 판사들이며 지방 의회며, 죄다 얼마나 흉측한 노릇인지!\""]], [["And he began to enlarge on his encounters with the new institutions.", "그러고는 그 새로운 기관들과의 충돌담을 늘어놓기 시작했다."]], [["Konstantin Levin heard him, and the disbelief in the sense of all public institutions, which he shared with him, and often expressed, was distasteful to him now from his brother’s lips.", "콘스탄친 레빈은 그의 말을 들었는데, 자기도 그와 공유하며 곧잘 입에 올리던 모든 공공 기관의 의의에 대한 그 불신이, 이제 형의 입에서 나오니 거북하게 느껴졌다."]], [["“In another world we shall understand it all,”", "\"다른 세상에 가면 우린 그걸 죄다 이해하게 될 거야.\""], ["he said lightly.", "그가 가볍게 말했다."]], [["“In another world!", "\"다른 세상!"], ["Ah, I don’t like that other world!", "아, 난 그 다른 세상이 싫어!"], ["I don’t like it,”", "싫다고.\""], ["he said, letting his scared eyes rest on his brother’s eyes.", "그는 겁먹은 눈을 동생의 눈에 머무르게 하며 말했다."], ["“Here one would think that to get out of all the baseness and the mess, one’s own and other people’s, would be a good thing, and yet I’m afraid of death, awfully afraid of death.”", "\"여기서야 자기 것이든 남의 것이든 그 온갖 비루함과 난장판에서 벗어나는 게 좋은 일이겠거니 싶지만, 그래도 난 죽음이 두려워, 죽는 게 끔찍이도 두려워.\""], ["He shuddered.", "그는 몸서리쳤다."], ["“But do drink something.", "\"하지만 뭐라도 좀 마셔."], ["Would you like some champagne?", "샴페인 한잔하겠어?"], ["Or shall we go somewhere?", "아니면 어디 나갈까?"], ["Let’s go to the Gypsies!", "집시들한테 가자고!"], ["Do you know I have got so fond of the Gypsies and Russian songs.”", "내가 집시들이랑 러시아 노래에 어찌나 빠졌는지 너도 알면 좋을 텐데.\""]], [["His speech had begun to falter, and he passed abruptly from one subject to another.", "그의 말은 어눌해지기 시작했고, 그는 이 화제에서 저 화제로 불쑥불쑥 건너뛰었다."], ["Konstantin with the help of Masha persuaded him not to go out anywhere, and got him to bed hopelessly drunk.", "콘스탄친은 마샤의 도움을 받아 그가 어디로도 나가지 않도록 설득했고, 그를 가망 없이 취한 채 침대에 눕혔다."]], [["Masha promised to write to Konstantin in case of need, and to persuade Nikolay Levin to go and stay with his brother.", "마샤는 필요한 경우 콘스탄친에게 편지를 쓰고, 니콜라이 레빈이 동생에게 가서 함께 지내도록 설득하겠다고 약속했다."]], [["# Chapter 26", "# Chapter 26"]], [["In the morning Konstantin Levin left Moscow, and towards evening he reached home.", "아침에 콘스탄틴 레빈은 모스크바를 떠나, 저녁 무렵 집에 도착했다."], ["On the journey in the train he talked to his neighbors about politics and the new railways, and, just as in Moscow, he was overcome by a sense of confusion of ideas, dissatisfaction with himself, shame of something or other.", "기차 안에서 그는 옆자리 사람들과 정치며 새로 놓인 철도에 관해 이야기를 나누었는데, 모스크바에서와 똑같이 생각이 뒤엉킨 듯한 혼란, 자기 자신에 대한 불만, 무언지 모를 부끄러움에 사로잡혔다."], ["But when he got out at his own station, when he saw his one-eyed coachman, Ignat, with the collar of his coat turned up; when, in the dim light reflected by the station fires, he saw his own sledge, his own horses with their tails tied up, in their harness trimmed with rings and tassels; when the coachman Ignat, as he put in his luggage, told him the village news, that the contractor had arrived, and that Pava had calved,—he felt that little by little the confusion was clearing up, and the shame and self-dissatisfaction were passing away.", "그러나 자기 역에 내려 외눈박이 마부 이그나트가 외투 깃을 세운 모습을 보았을 때, 역의 등불이 반사된 어슴푸레한 빛 속에서 고리와 술로 장식된 마구를 채우고 꼬리를 묶은 자기 말들과 자기 썰매를 보았을 때, 그리고 마부 이그나트가 짐을 실으면서 마을 소식을—청부업자가 도착했고 파바가 송아지를 낳았다고—전해 주었을 때, 그는 조금씩 그 혼란이 걷히고 부끄러움과 자기 불만이 사라져 가는 것을 느꼈다. 그저 이그나트와 말들을 보기만 했는데도 그런 느낌이 들었다."], ["He felt this at the mere sight of Ignat and the horses; but when he had put on the sheepskin brought for him, had sat down wrapped up in the sledge, and had driven off pondering on the work that lay before him in the village, and staring at the side-horse, that had been his saddle-horse, past his prime now, but a spirited beast from the Don, he began to see what had happened to him in quite a different light.", "그러나 자기를 위해 가져온 양가죽 외투를 걸치고, 썰매에 몸을 감싸고 앉아, 마을에서 앞으로 해야 할 일을 곰곰이 생각하면서, 한때는 자기 안장말이었으나 이제는 한창때가 지났어도 여전히 기운 넘치는 돈산(産) 곁말을 바라보며 달리기 시작했을 때, 그는 자기에게 일어난 일을 전혀 다른 빛 속에서 보기 시작했다. 그는 자기 자신을 느꼈고, 다른 누군가가 되고 싶지 않았다."], ["He felt himself, and did not want to be anyone else. All he wanted now was to be better than before.", "이제 그가 바라는 것은 그저 전보다 나은 사람이 되는 것뿐이었다."], ["In the first place he resolved that from that day he would give up hoping for any extraordinary happiness, such as marriage must have given him, and consequently he would not so disdain what he really had.", "우선 그는 그날부터 결혼이 분명 가져다주었을 어떤 특별한 행복을 바라기를 그만두기로 결심했고, 따라서 자기가 실제로 가진 것을 그렇게 업신여기지 않으리라 마음먹었다."], ["Secondly, he would never again let himself give way to low passion, the memory of which had so tortured him when he had been making up his mind to make an offer.", "둘째로, 청혼하기로 마음을 굳히던 동안 그를 그토록 괴롭혔던 그 기억, 즉 저열한 욕정에 다시는 자기를 내맡기지 않기로 했다."], ["Then remembering his brother Nikolay, he resolved to himself that he would never allow himself to forget him, that he would follow him up, and not lose sight of him, so as to be ready to help when things should go ill with him.", "그러다 형 니콜라이를 떠올리고는, 결코 형을 잊지 않겠다고, 형을 끝까지 챙기고 시야에서 놓치지 않겠다고, 그래서 형의 형편이 나빠질 때 도울 준비를 해 두겠다고 스스로에게 다짐했다."], ["And that would be soon, he felt.", "그리고 그런 일은 머잖아 닥치리라고 그는 느꼈다."], ["Then, too, his brother’s talk of communism, which he had treated so lightly at the time, now made him think.", "또 그때는 그토록 가볍게 흘려들었던 형의 공산주의 이야기가 이제 와서 그를 생각에 잠기게 했다."], ["He considered a revolution in economic conditions nonsense.", "그는 경제적 조건의 혁명이란 터무니없는 소리라고 여겼다."], ["But he always felt the injustice of his own abundance in comparison with the poverty of the peasants, and now he determined that so as to feel quite in the right, though he had worked hard and lived by no means luxuriously before, he would now work still harder, and would allow himself even less luxury.", "그러나 그는 농민들의 가난에 견주어 자기가 누리는 풍요로움이 부당하다는 것을 늘 느꼈고, 이제 그는 떳떳한 마음을 갖기 위해, 비록 전에도 열심히 일했고 결코 사치스럽게 살지 않았지만, 앞으로는 더욱 열심히 일하고 사치는 한층 더 줄이기로 결심했다."], ["And all this seemed to him so easy a conquest over himself that he spent the whole drive in the pleasantest daydreams.", "그리고 이 모든 것이 그에게는 자기 자신을 이겨 내는 너무도 손쉬운 일처럼 여겨져서, 그는 가는 길 내내 더없이 즐거운 공상에 잠겨 있었다."], ["With a resolute feeling of hope in a new, better life, he reached home before nine o’clock at night.", "새롭고 더 나은 삶에 대한 굳은 희망을 품고, 그는 밤 아홉 시가 되기 전에 집에 도착했다."]], [["The snow of the little quadrangle before the house was lit up by a light in the bedroom windows of his old nurse, Agafea Mihalovna, who performed the duties of housekeeper in his house.", "집 앞 작은 마당의 눈이 그의 집에서 가정부 일을 맡고 있는 늙은 유모 아가피야 미하일로브나의 침실 창에서 새어 나오는 빛에 환히 비쳤다."], ["She was not yet asleep.", "그녀는 아직 잠들지 않았다."], ["Kouzma, waked up by her, came sidling sleepily out onto the steps.", "그녀가 깨운 쿠즈마가 졸린 걸음으로 비척비척 현관 계단으로 나왔다."], ["A setter bitch, Laska, ran out too, almost upsetting Kouzma, and whining, turned round about Levin’s knees, jumping up and longing, but not daring, to put her forepaws on his chest.", "세터 암캐 라스카도 뛰쳐나와 하마터면 쿠즈마를 넘어뜨릴 뻔하면서 낑낑대며 레빈의 무릎 둘레를 맴돌았고, 펄쩍펄쩍 뛰어오르며 앞발을 그의 가슴에 얹고 싶어 했으나 차마 그러지는 못했다."]], [["“You’re soon back again, sir,”", "\"빨리 돌아오셨네요, 나리.\""], ["said Agafea Mihalovna.", "아가피야 미하일로브나가 말했다."]], [["“I got tired of it, Agafea Mihalovna.", "\"지겨워졌어, 아가피야 미하일로브나."], ["With friends, one is well; but at home, one is better,”", "친구들과 있으면 좋긴 한데, 집에 있는 게 더 낫지.\""], ["he answered, and went into his study.", "그가 대답하고는 서재로 들어갔다."]], [["The study was slowly lit up as the candle was brought in.", "촛불이 들어오자 서재가 천천히 밝아졌다."], ["The familiar details came out: the stag’s horns, the bookshelves, the looking-glass, the stove with its ventilator, which had long wanted mending, his father’s sofa, a large table, on the table an open book, a broken ashtray, a manuscript book with his handwriting.", "낯익은 것들이 모습을 드러냈다. 사슴뿔, 책장, 거울, 손볼 데가 생긴 지 오래된 통풍구 달린 난로, 아버지가 쓰던 소파, 커다란 탁자, 그 탁자 위에 펼쳐진 책 한 권, 깨진 재떨이, 그의 필체로 채워진 원고 노트."], ["As he saw all this, there came over him for an instant a doubt of the possibility of arranging the new life, of which he had been dreaming on the road.", "이 모든 것을 보는 순간, 길에서 줄곧 꿈꾸어 온 새 삶을 꾸려 갈 수 있을지 한순간 의심이 그를 덮쳤다. 그의 삶의 이 모든 흔적이 그를 붙잡고 이렇게 말하는 것 같았다."], ["All these traces of his life seemed to clutch him, and to say to him: “No, you’re not going to get away from us, and you’re not going to be different, but you’re going to be the same as you’ve always been; with doubts, everlasting dissatisfaction with yourself, vain efforts to amend, and falls, and everlasting expectation, of a happiness which you won’t get, and which isn’t possible for you.”", "\"안 돼, 너는 우리에게서 벗어나지 못해, 너는 달라지지 못하고, 늘 그래 왔던 그대로일 거야. 의심하고, 자기 자신에 대한 끝없는 불만에 시달리고, 고치려는 헛된 노력을 하고, 또 무너지고, 그러면서 네가 결코 얻지 못할, 너에게는 불가능한 행복을 영원히 기다리면서 말이야.\""]], [["This the things said to him, but another voice in his heart was telling him that he must not fall under the sway of the past, and that one can do anything with oneself.", "물건들은 그에게 이렇게 말했지만, 그의 마음속 또 다른 목소리는 과거에 휘둘려서는 안 된다고, 사람은 자기 자신을 가지고 무엇이든 할 수 있다고 말하고 있었다."], ["And hearing that voice, he went into the corner where stood his two heavy dumbbells, and began brandishing them like a gymnast, trying to restore his confident temper.", "그리고 그 목소리에 귀를 기울이며, 그는 무거운 아령 두 개가 놓인 구석으로 가서 체조 선수처럼 그것들을 휘두르며 자신만만한 기분을 되찾으려 했다."], ["There was a creak of steps at the door.", "문가에서 계단이 삐걱거리는 소리가 났다."], ["He hastily put down the dumbbells.", "그는 황급히 아령을 내려놓았다."]], [["The bailiff came in, and said everything, thank God, was doing well; but informed him that the buckwheat in the new drying machine had been a little scorched.", "집사가 들어와 모든 일이 다행히도 잘되고 있다고 말했지만, 새 건조기에 넣은 메밀이 조금 그슬렸다는 소식을 전했다."], ["This piece of news irritated Levin.", "이 소식에 레빈은 짜증이 났다."], ["The new drying machine had been constructed and partly invented by Levin.", "새 건조기는 레빈이 직접 만들고 부분적으로는 고안하기까지 한 것이었다."], ["The bailiff had always been against the drying machine, and now it was with suppressed triumph that he announced that the buckwheat had been scorched.", "집사는 줄곧 그 건조기를 반대해 왔고, 이제 메밀이 그슬렸노라고 알리는 그의 목소리에는 억눌린 의기양양함이 묻어 있었다."], ["Levin was firmly convinced that if the buckwheat had been scorched, it was only because the precautions had not been taken, for which he had hundreds of times given orders.", "레빈은 만약 메밀이 그슬렸다면 그것은 오로지 자기가 수백 번이나 지시했던 예방 조치를 취하지 않았기 때문임을 굳게 확신했다. 그는 화가 나서 집사를 나무랐다."], ["He was annoyed, and reprimanded the bailiff.", "그러나 중요하고도 기쁜 일이 하나 있었다."], ["But there had been an important and joyful event: Pava, his best cow, an expensive beast, bought at a show, had calved.", "품평회에서 비싼 값에 사 온 그의 가장 좋은 암소 파바가 송아지를 낳은 것이었다."]], [["“Kouzma, give me my sheepskin.", "\"쿠즈마, 내 양가죽 외투를 가져와."], ["And you tell them to take a lantern.", "그리고 자네는 등불을 들라고 일러 주게."], ["I’ll come and look at her,”", "가서 그 녀석을 봐야겠어.\""], ["he said to the bailiff.", "그가 집사에게 말했다."]], [["The cowhouse for the more valuable cows was just behind the house.", "값이 더 나가는 소들을 위한 외양간은 집 바로 뒤에 있었다."], ["Walking across the yard, passing a snowdrift by the lilac tree, he went into the cowhouse.", "마당을 가로질러, 라일락 나무 옆의 눈더미를 지나, 그는 외양간으로 들어갔다."], ["There was the warm, steamy smell of dung when the frozen door was opened, and the cows, astonished at the unfamiliar light of the lantern, stirred on the fresh straw.", "얼어붙은 문을 열자 따뜻하고 김이 서린 거름 냄새가 풍겼고, 등불의 낯선 빛에 놀란 소들이 새로 깐 짚 위에서 몸을 뒤척였다."], ["He caught a glimpse of the broad, smooth, black and piebald back of Hollandka.", "그는 홀란드카의 넓고 매끄러운, 검은색과 얼룩이 섞인 등을 얼핏 보았다."], ["Berkoot, the bull, was lying down with his ring in his lip, and seemed about to get up, but thought better of it, and only gave two snorts as they passed by him.", "황소 베르쿠트는 입술에 코뚜레를 꿴 채 누워 있다가 일어서려는 듯하더니 마음을 고쳐먹고는, 그들이 곁을 지나갈 때 콧김을 두어 번 내뿜을 뿐이었다."], ["Pava, a perfect beauty, huge as a hippopotamus, with her back turned to them, prevented their seeing the calf, as she sniffed her all over.", "더없이 아름다운, 하마처럼 거대한 파바가 등을 돌리고 서서 송아지를 온통 핥아 대는 바람에, 그들은 송아지를 볼 수가 없었다."]], [["Levin went into the pen, looked Pava over, and lifted the red and spotted calf onto her long, tottering legs.", "레빈은 우리 안으로 들어가 파바를 살펴본 뒤, 붉고 얼룩진 송아지를 길고 비틀거리는 다리로 일으켜 세웠다."], ["Pava, uneasy, began lowing, but when Levin put the calf close to her she was soothed, and, sighing heavily, began licking her with her rough tongue.", "파바가 불안해하며 음매 울기 시작했지만, 레빈이 송아지를 가까이 붙여 주자 진정되어, 무겁게 한숨을 내쉬며 거친 혀로 송아지를 핥기 시작했다."], ["The calf, fumbling, poked her nose under her mother’s udder, and stiffened her tail out straight.", "송아지는 더듬더듬 어미의 젖통 밑으로 코를 들이밀고는 꼬리를 빳빳이 곧추세웠다."]], [["“Here, bring the light, Fyodor, this way,”", "\"이리로, 표도르, 불을 가져와, 이쪽으로.\""], ["said Levin, examining the calf.", "레빈이 송아지를 살펴보며 말했다."], ["“Like the mother!", "\"어미를 닮았군!"], ["though the color takes after the father; but that’s nothing.", "빛깔은 아비를 닮았지만, 그건 아무것도 아니지."], ["Very good.", "아주 좋아."], ["Long and broad in the haunch.", "엉덩이가 길고 넓어."], ["Vassily Fedorovitch, isn’t she splendid?”", "바실리 표도로비치, 이 녀석 훌륭하지 않은가?\""], ["he said to the bailiff, quite forgiving him for the buckwheat under the influence of his delight in the calf.", "그는 송아지에 대한 기쁨에 사로잡혀 메밀 일은 완전히 용서한 채 집사에게 말했다."]], [["“How could she fail to be?", "\"훌륭하지 않을 리가 있겠습니까요?"], ["Oh, Semyon the contractor came the day after you left.", "아, 청부업자 세묜이 나리 떠나신 다음 날 왔습니다."], ["You must settle with him, Konstantin Dmitrievitch,”", "그자와 셈을 치르셔야 합니다, 콘스탄틴 드미트리치.\""], ["said the bailiff.", "집사가 말했다."], ["“I did inform you about the machine.”", "\"건조기 일은 제가 분명 말씀드렸지요.\""]], [["This question was enough to take Levin back to all the details of his work on the estate, which was on a large scale, and complicated.", "이 한마디는 레빈을 큰 규모에다 복잡하기 그지없는 영지 일의 온갖 세세한 부분으로 도로 끌어가기에 충분했다."], ["He went straight from the cowhouse to the counting house, and after a little conversation with the bailiff and Semyon the contractor, he went back to the house and straight upstairs to the drawing-room.", "그는 외양간에서 곧장 사무실로 가서, 집사와 청부업자 세묜과 잠시 이야기를 나눈 뒤, 집으로 돌아와 곧장 위층 응접실로 올라갔다."]], [["# Chapter 27", "# Chapter 27"]], [["The house was big and old-fashioned, and Levin, though he lived alone, had the whole house heated and used.", "집은 크고 고풍스러웠는데, 레빈은 혼자 살면서도 집 전체에 난방을 하고 모든 방을 쓰고 있었다."], ["He knew that this was stupid, he knew that it was positively not right, and contrary to his present new plans, but this house was a whole world to Levin.", "그는 이것이 어리석은 일임을 알았고, 분명히 옳지 않으며 지금 자기의 새로운 계획에도 어긋난다는 것을 알았지만, 이 집은 레빈에게 하나의 온전한 세계였다."], ["It was the world in which his father and mother had lived and died.", "그것은 그의 아버지와 어머니가 살다가 세상을 떠난 세계였다."], ["They had lived just the life that to Levin seemed the ideal of perfection, and that he had dreamed of beginning with his wife, his family.", "두 분은 레빈에게는 완벽의 이상으로 여겨지는 바로 그런 삶을 살았고, 그는 아내와 가족과 더불어 그런 삶을 시작하기를 꿈꿔 왔다."]], [["Levin scarcely remembered his mother.", "레빈은 어머니를 거의 기억하지 못했다."], ["His conception of her was for him a sacred memory, and his future wife was bound to be in his imagination a repetition of that exquisite, holy ideal of a woman that his mother had been.", "어머니에 대한 그의 관념은 그에게 신성한 기억이었고, 그의 미래의 아내는 그의 상상 속에서 자기 어머니가 그러했던 여인의 그 절묘하고 거룩한 이상을 되풀이하는 존재일 수밖에 없었다."]], [["He was so far from conceiving of love for woman apart from marriage that he positively pictured to himself first the family, and only secondarily the woman who would give him a family.", "그는 결혼과 동떨어진 여인에 대한 사랑이란 것을 도무지 떠올릴 수 없었기에, 머릿속으로 먼저 가족을 그렸고, 그에게 가족을 안겨 줄 여인은 그저 그다음에 떠올렸다."], ["His ideas of marriage were, consequently, quite unlike those of the great majority of his acquaintances, for whom getting married was one of the numerous facts of social life.", "따라서 결혼에 대한 그의 생각은 그가 아는 대다수의 사람들과는 사뭇 달랐는데, 그들에게 결혼이란 사교 생활의 숱한 일들 가운데 하나일 뿐이었다."], ["For Levin it was the chief affair of life, on which its whole happiness turned.", "레빈에게 결혼은 삶의 으뜸가는 일이었고, 삶의 행복 전부가 거기에 달려 있었다."], ["And now he had to give up that.", "그런데 이제 그는 그것을 단념해야만 했다."]], [["When he had gone into the little drawing-room, where he always had tea, and had settled himself in his armchair with a book, and Agafea Mihalovna had brought him tea, and with her usual, “Well, I’ll stay a while, sir,”", "그가 늘 차를 마시던 작은 응접실로 들어가 책 한 권을 들고 안락의자에 자리를 잡고 앉자, 아가피야 미하일로브나가 차를 가져왔고, 평소처럼 \"그럼 잠깐 앉아 있겠습니다, 나리.\" 하고는 창가의 의자에 앉았다."], ["had taken a chair in the window, he felt that, however strange it might be, he had not parted from his daydreams, and that he could not live without them.", "그때 그는, 아무리 이상하게 여겨질지라도, 자기가 공상에서 결코 헤어 나오지 못했으며 그 공상 없이는 살 수 없다는 것을 느꼈다."], ["Whether with her, or with another, still it would be.", "그녀든 다른 여자든, 어쨌든 그 삶은 펼쳐질 것이었다."], ["He was reading a book, and thinking of what he was reading, and stopping to listen to Agafea Mihalovna, who gossiped away without flagging, and yet with all that, all sorts of pictures of family life and work in the future rose disconnectedly before his imagination.", "그는 책을 읽으며 읽고 있는 내용을 생각했고, 지치지도 않고 수다를 떠는 아가피야 미하일로브나의 말에 귀 기울이려 이따금 멈추기도 했지만, 그러면서도 앞날의 가정생활과 일에 관한 온갖 그림이 두서없이 그의 상상 앞에 떠올랐다."], ["He felt that in the depth of his soul something had been put in its place, settled down, and laid to rest.", "그는 자기 영혼 깊은 곳에서 무언가가 제자리를 찾고, 가라앉고, 잠잠해졌음을 느꼈다."]], [["He heard Agafea Mihalovna talking of how Prohor had forgotten his duty to God, and with the money Levin had given him to buy a horse, had been drinking without stopping, and had beaten his wife till he’d half killed her.", "그는 아가피야 미하일로브나가, 프로호르가 하느님에 대한 도리를 잊고서 레빈이 말을 사라고 준 돈으로 쉴 새 없이 술을 퍼마시고 아내를 반쯤 죽도록 때렸다는 이야기를 늘어놓는 것을 들었다."], ["He listened, and read his book, and recalled the whole train of ideas suggested by his reading.", "그는 귀 기울이면서 책을 읽었고, 그 독서가 불러일으킨 일련의 생각을 모두 떠올렸다."], ["It was Tyndall’s Treatise on Heat.", "그것은 틴들의 『열에 관한 논고』였다."], ["He recalled his own criticisms of Tyndall of his complacent satisfaction in the cleverness of his experiments, and for his lack of philosophic insight.", "그는 자기가 틴들을 두고 비판했던 점들을, 즉 그가 자기 실험의 영리함에 흡족해하는 안일한 만족과 철학적 통찰의 결여를 두고 했던 비판을 떠올렸다. 그러다 별안간 즐거운 생각 하나가 그의 마음속으로 떠올랐다."], ["And suddenly there floated into his mind the joyful thought: “In two years’ time I shall have two Dutch cows; Pava herself will perhaps still be alive, a dozen young daughters of Berkoot and the three others—how lovely!”", "\"이 년만 지나면 나는 네덜란드 암소 두 마리를 갖게 될 거야. 어쩌면 파바도 그때까지 살아 있을지 모르고, 베르쿠트의 어린 딸 열두 마리에다 다른 세 마리까지—얼마나 멋진 일인가!\""]], [["He took up his book again.", "그는 다시 책을 집어 들었다."], ["“Very good, electricity and heat are the same thing; but is it possible to substitute the one quantity for the other in the equation for the solution of any problem?", "\"아주 좋아, 전기와 열은 같은 것이지. 하지만 어떤 문제를 풀기 위한 방정식에서 한 양을 다른 양으로 대체하는 게 가능할까?"], ["No. Well, then what of it?", "아니지."], ["The connection between all the forces of nature is felt instinctively....", "그래, 그렇다면 그게 어쨌다는 거야? 자연의 모든 힘들 사이의 연관은 본능적으로 느껴지는 거지……"], ["It’s particulary nice if Pava’s daughter should be a red-spotted cow, and all the herd will take after her, and the other three, too!", "파바의 딸이 붉은 점박이 암소라면 정말 좋겠고, 그러면 무리 전체가 그놈을 닮을 테고, 다른 세 마리도 그럴 테지!"], ["Splendid!", "굉장해!"], ["To go out with my wife and visitors to meet the herd....", "아내와 손님들과 함께 나가서 가축 떼를 맞이하는 거야…… 아내가 말하겠지."], ["My wife says, ‘Kostya and I looked after that calf like a child.’ ‘How can it interest you so much?’ says a visitor.", "'코스챠와 나는 저 송아지를 자식처럼 보살폈답니다.' '그게 어쩜 그렇게 흥미로우세요?'"], ["‘Everything that interests him, interests me.’ But who will she be?”", "하고 손님이 묻겠지. '그이가 흥미로워하는 건 전부 저도 흥미로워요.'"], ["And he remembered what had happened at Moscow....", "한데 그녀가 누가 될까?\" 그러자 그는 모스크바에서 있었던 일을 떠올렸다……"], ["“Well, there’s nothing to be done....", "\"그래, 어쩔 도리가 없지……"], ["It’s not my fault.", "내 잘못은 아니야."], ["But now everything shall go on in a new way.", "하지만 이제부터는 모든 게 새롭게 흘러갈 거야."], ["It’s nonsense to pretend that life won’t let one, that the past won’t let one.", "삶이 그걸 허락하지 않으리라느니, 과거가 그걸 허락하지 않으리라느니 하는 건 어리석은 소리야."], ["One must struggle to live better, much better.”...", "더 잘 살려고, 훨씬 더 잘 살려고 애써야 해.\"……"], ["He raised his head, and fell to dreaming.", "그는 고개를 들고 공상에 잠겨 들었다."], ["Old Laska, who had not yet fully digested her delight at his return, and had run out into the yard to bark, came back wagging her tail, and crept up to him, bringing in the scent of fresh air, put her head under his hand, and whined plaintively, asking to be stroked.", "늙은 라스카는 그가 돌아온 기쁨을 아직 다 삭이지 못한 채 짖어 대려고 마당으로 뛰쳐나갔다가, 꼬리를 흔들며 돌아와 신선한 바깥 공기 냄새를 풍기며 그에게 슬그머니 다가와, 그의 손 밑에 머리를 들이밀고 쓰다듬어 달라는 듯 애처롭게 낑낑거렸다."]], [["“There, who’d have thought it?”", "\"저것 좀 보세요, 누가 생각이나 했겠어요?\""], ["said Agafea Mihalovna.", "아가피야 미하일로브나가 말했다."], ["“The dog now ...", "\"이제 저 개까지……"], ["why, she understands that her master’s come home, and that he’s low-spirited.”", "글쎄, 주인 나리가 집에 돌아왔다는 걸, 그리고 나리가 기운이 없으시다는 걸 다 안다니까요.\""]], [["“Why low-spirited?”", "\"기운이 없다니, 왜?\""]], [["“Do you suppose I don’t see it, sir?", "\"제가 그걸 못 볼 것 같으세요, 나리?"], ["It’s high time I should know the gentry.", "이제 양반님네 속을 알 때도 됐지요."], ["Why, I’ve grown up from a little thing with them.", "어릴 적부터 이 댁 분들 곁에서 자랐는걸요."], ["It’s nothing, sir, so long as there’s health and a clear conscience.”", "괜찮습니다, 나리, 몸 성하고 양심에 거리낄 게 없으면 그만이지요.\""]], [["Levin looked intently at her, surprised at how well she knew his thought.", "레빈은 그녀가 자기 마음을 얼마나 잘 아는지에 놀라며 그녀를 뚫어지게 바라보았다."]], [["“Shall I fetch you another cup?”", "\"한 잔 더 가져다드릴까요?\""], ["said she, and taking his cup she went out.", "그녀가 이렇게 말하고는 찻잔을 들고 나갔다."]], [["Laska kept poking her head under his hand.", "라스카는 자꾸만 그의 손 밑으로 머리를 들이밀었다."], ["He stroked her, and she promptly curled up at his feet, laying her head on a hindpaw.", "그가 쓰다듬어 주자 개는 곧장 그의 발치에 몸을 웅크리고 뒷발 위에 머리를 얹었다."], ["And in token of all now being well and satisfactory, she opened her mouth a little, smacked her lips, and settling her sticky lips more comfortably about her old teeth, she sank into blissful repose.", "그리고 이제 모든 것이 흡족하고 좋다는 표시로, 개는 입을 살짝 벌려 입맛을 다시고는, 끈적한 입술을 늙은 이빨 둘레에 더 편안하게 자리 잡고서 더없이 행복한 휴식에 빠져들었다."], ["Levin watched all her movements attentively.", "레빈은 개의 모든 움직임을 유심히 지켜보았다."]], [["“That’s what I’ll do,”", "\"나도 저렇게 할 거야.\""], ["he said to himself; “that’s what I’ll do!", "그는 혼잣말로 중얼거렸다. \"나도 저렇게 할 거야!"], ["Nothing’s amiss....", "잘못된 건 아무것도 없어……"], ["All’s well.”", "다 괜찮아.\""]], [["# Chapter 28", "# Chapter 28"]], [["After the ball, early next morning, Anna Arkadyevna sent her husband a telegram that she was leaving Moscow the same day.", "무도회가 끝난 뒤, 이튿날 이른 아침에 안나 아르카지예브나는 남편에게 그날로 모스크바를 떠나겠다는 전보를 보냈다."]], [["“No, I must go, I must go”; she explained to her sister-in-law the change in her plans in a tone that suggested that she had to remember so many things that there was no enumerating them: “no, it had really better be today!”", "\"아니에요, 가야 해요, 가야 해요.\" 그녀는 올케에게 계획이 바뀐 까닭을 설명하면서, 떠올려야 할 일이 너무 많아 일일이 다 헤아릴 수 없다는 투로 말했다. \"아니에요, 역시 오늘이 낫겠어요!\""]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch was not dining at home, but he promised to come and see his sister off at seven o’clock.", "스테판 아르카지치는 집에서 저녁을 먹지 않았지만, 일곱 시에 와서 누이를 배웅하겠다고 약속했다."]], [["Kitty, too, did not come, sending a note that she had a headache.", "키티 역시 오지 않고, 머리가 아프다는 쪽지를 보내왔다."], ["Dolly and Anna dined alone with the children and the English governess.", "돌리와 안나는 아이들과 영국인 가정교사하고만 저녁을 먹었다."], ["Whether it was that the children were fickle, or that they had acute senses, and felt that Anna was quite different that day from what she had been when they had taken such a fancy to her, that she was not now interested in them,—but they had abruptly dropped their play with their aunt, and their love for her, and were quite indifferent that she was going away.", "아이들이 변덕스러워서였는지, 아니면 예민한 감각으로 안나가 그날따라 자기들이 그토록 따랐던 때와는 사뭇 다르며, 이제는 자기들에게 관심이 없다는 것을 느꼈기 때문인지, 아이들은 별안간 고모와의 놀이와 고모에 대한 애정을 뚝 끊어 버리고는 고모가 떠난다는 데에도 도무지 무관심했다."], ["Anna was absorbed the whole morning in preparations for her departure.", "안나는 아침 내내 출발 준비에 골몰했다."], ["She wrote notes to her Moscow acquaintances, put down her accounts, and packed.", "모스크바의 지인들에게 쪽지를 쓰고, 셈을 정리하고, 짐을 꾸렸다."], ["Altogether Dolly fancied she was not in a placid state of mind, but in that worried mood, which Dolly knew well with herself, and which does not come without cause, and for the most part covers dissatisfaction with self.", "통틀어 돌리가 보기에 그녀는 마음이 평온한 상태가 아니라, 돌리 자신도 잘 아는 그 불안한 기분에 빠져 있는 것 같았다. 그것은 까닭 없이 찾아오는 법이 없으며, 대개는 자기 자신에 대한 불만을 감추고 있는 기분이었다."], ["After dinner, Anna went up to her room to dress, and Dolly followed her.", "저녁 식사가 끝난 뒤, 안나는 옷을 갈아입으러 자기 방으로 올라갔고, 돌리도 뒤따라갔다."]], [["“How queer you are today!”", "\"오늘 어쩜 이렇게 이상해요!\""], ["Dolly said to her.", "돌리가 그녀에게 말했다."]], [["“I? Do you think so?", "\"제가요?"], ["I’m not queer, but I’m nasty.", "그렇게 보여요?"], ["I am like that sometimes. I keep feeling as if I could cry.", "이상한 게 아니라, 제가 좀 못된 거예요."], ["It’s very stupid, but it’ll pass off,”", "가끔 이래요. 자꾸 울고 싶어지는 거 있죠."], ["said Anna quickly, and she bent her flushed face over a tiny bag in which she was packing a nightcap and some cambric handkerchiefs.", "참 어리석은 일이지만, 곧 지나갈 거예요.\" 안나가 빠르게 말하며, 잠잘 때 쓰는 모자와 고운 삼베 손수건 몇 장을 챙겨 넣던 작은 가방 위로 상기된 얼굴을 숙였다."], ["Her eyes were particularly bright, and were continually swimming with tears.", "그녀의 눈은 유난히 반짝였고, 줄곧 눈물이 그렁그렁 차올랐다."], ["“In the same way I didn’t want to leave Petersburg, and now I don’t want to go away from here.”", "\"꼭 마찬가지로 페테르부르크를 떠나기도 싫었는데, 이제는 또 여기서 떠나기가 싫네요.\""]], [["“You came here and did a good deed,”", "\"올케는 여기 와서 좋은 일을 했어요.\""], ["said Dolly, looking intently at her.", "돌리가 그녀를 뚫어지게 바라보며 말했다."]], [["Anna looked at her with eyes wet with tears.", "안나는 눈물에 젖은 눈으로 그녀를 바라보았다."]], [["“Don’t say that, Dolly.", "\"그런 말 마요, 돌리."], ["I’ve done nothing, and could do nothing.", "저는 아무것도 하지 않았고, 할 수도 없었어요."], ["I often wonder why people are all in league to spoil me.", "사람들이 어째서 다들 한통속이 되어 저를 추켜세우는지 종종 의아하다니까요."], ["What have I done, and what could I do?", "제가 무얼 했고, 무얼 할 수 있었겠어요?"], ["In your heart there was found love enough to forgive....”", "형님 마음속에 용서할 만큼의 사랑이 있었던 거지요……\""]], [["“If it had not been for you, God knows what would have happened!", "\"올케가 아니었다면, 하느님만이 무슨 일이 벌어졌을지 아시겠지요!"], ["How happy you are, Anna!”", "올케는 참 행복한 사람이에요, 안나!\""], ["said Dolly.", "돌리가 말했다."], ["“Everything is clear and good in your heart.”", "\"올케 마음속은 모든 게 맑고 좋잖아요.\""]], [["“Every heart has its own skeletons, as the English say.”", "\"영국 사람들 말마따나, 마음마다 저마다의 skeleton이 있는 법이에요.\""]], [["“You have no sort of skeleton, have you?", "* skeleton: 영어로 '해골'."], ["Everything is so clear in you.”", "'벽장 속의 해골(skeleton in the closet)'이라는 표현에서 온 말로, 남에게 숨기고 싶은 비밀을 뜻한다."]], [["“I have!”", "\"올케한테는 그런 skeleton 같은 건 없잖아요?"], ["said Anna suddenly, and, unexpectedly after her tears, a sly, ironical smile curved her lips.", "올케는 모든 게 그토록 맑은데.\""]], [["“Come, he’s amusing, anyway, your skeleton, and not depressing,”", "\"있어요!\" 안나가 불쑥 말했다."], ["said Dolly, smiling.", "그러더니 눈물 끝에 뜻밖에도 짓궂고 비꼬는 듯한 미소가 그녀의 입가에 번졌다."]], [["“No, he’s depressing.", "\"어머, 올케의 그 skeleton은 어쨌든 우습기는 해도 우울하진 않은가 봐요.\""], ["Do you know why I’m going today instead of tomorrow?", "돌리가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"아니에요, 우울한걸요."], ["It’s a confession that weighs on me; I want to make it to you,”", "제가 왜 내일이 아니라 오늘 떠나는지 알아요?"], ["said Anna, letting herself drop definitely into an armchair, and looking straight into Dolly’s face.", "마음을 짓누르는 고백이 하나 있는데, 그걸 형님한테 털어놓고 싶어요.\" 안나가 단호하게 안락의자에 몸을 떨구며, 돌리의 얼굴을 똑바로 들여다보며 말했다."]], [["And to her surprise Dolly saw that Anna was blushing up to her ears, up to the curly black ringlets on her neck.", "그러자 놀랍게도 돌리는 안나가 귀밑까지, 목덜미의 곱슬곱슬한 검은 머리칼이 닿는 데까지 새빨개지는 것을 보았다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그래요.\""], ["Anna went on.", "안나가 말을 이었다."], ["“Do you know why Kitty didn’t come to dinner?", "\"키티가 왜 저녁 먹으러 오지 않았는지 알아요?"], ["She’s jealous of me.", "저를 질투하는 거예요."], ["I have spoiled ...", "제가 망쳐 버렸어요……"], ["I’ve been the cause of that ball being a torture to her instead of a pleasure.", "그 애한테 무도회가 즐거움이 아니라 고통이 되게 만든 게 저예요."], ["But truly, truly, it’s not my fault, or only my fault a little bit,”", "하지만 정말이지, 정말이지 제 잘못은 아니에요. 아니면 아주 조금만 제 잘못이거나요.\""], ["she said, daintily drawling the words “a little bit.”", "그녀는 \"아주 조금\"이라는 말을 곱게 늘여 말했다."]], [["“Oh, how like Stiva you said that!”", "\"어머, 그 말투가 어쩜 그렇게 스티바를 닮았어요!\""], ["said Dolly, laughing.", "돌리가 웃으며 말했다."]], [["Anna was hurt.", "안나는 기분이 상했다."]], [["“Oh no, oh no!", "\"아니, 아니에요!"], ["I’m not Stiva,”", "저는 스티바가 아니에요.\""], ["she said, knitting her brows.", "그녀가 미간을 찌푸리며 말했다."], ["“That’s why I’m telling you, just because I could never let myself doubt myself for an instant,”", "\"제가 형님한테 이런 이야기를 하는 건, 바로 단 한순간도 제 자신을 의심하는 걸 견딜 수 없어서예요.\""], ["said Anna.", "안나가 말했다."]], [["But at the very moment she was uttering the words, she felt that they were not true.", "하지만 바로 그 말을 입에 올리는 순간, 그녀는 그것이 사실이 아님을 느꼈다."], ["She was not merely doubting herself, she felt emotion at the thought of Vronsky, and was going away sooner than she had meant, simply to avoid meeting him.", "그녀는 단지 자기 자신을 의심하는 데 그치지 않았다. 브론스키를 떠올리면 가슴이 설렜고, 단지 그와 마주치는 것을 피하려고 본래 마음먹었던 것보다 일찍 떠나는 것이었다."]], [["“Yes, Stiva told me you danced the mazurka with him, and that he....”", "\"그래요, 스티바가 그러던데, 올케가 그 사람하고 마주르카를 췄다고, 그리고 그 사람이……\""]], [["“You can’t imagine how absurdly it all came about.", "\"그게 어쩜 그렇게 어이없게 흘러갔는지 형님은 상상도 못 할 거예요."], ["I only meant to be matchmaking, and all at once it turned out quite differently.", "저는 그저 중매를 서려 했을 뿐인데, 별안간 전혀 딴판으로 되어 버렸지 뭐예요."], ["Possibly against my own will....”", "어쩌면 제 뜻과는 어긋나게……\""]], [["She crimsoned and stopped.", "그녀는 새빨개져서 말을 멈췄다."]], [["“Oh, they feel it directly?”", "\"오, 남자들은 그런 걸 단박에 느끼나 봐요?\""], ["said Dolly.", "돌리가 말했다."]], [["“But I should be in despair if there were anything serious in it on his side,”", "\"하지만 그 사람 쪽에서 뭔가 진지한 게 있다면 저는 절망스러울 거예요.\""], ["Anna interrupted her.", "안나가 그녀의 말을 가로막았다."], ["“And I am certain it will all be forgotten, and Kitty will leave off hating me.”", "\"그리고 저는 다 잊히고, 키티도 저를 미워하길 그만두리라고 확신해요.\""]], [["“All the same, Anna, to tell you the truth, I’m not very anxious for this marriage for Kitty.", "\"그래도 안나, 솔직히 말하면 저는 키티가 이 결혼을 하길 그렇게 바라는 건 아니에요."], ["And it’s better it should come to nothing, if he, Vronsky, is capable of falling in love with you in a single day.”", "그리고 만약 그 사람, 브론스키가 단 하루 만에 올케한테 반할 수 있는 사람이라면, 차라리 아무 일도 없는 편이 나아요.\""]], [["“Oh, heavens, that would be too silly!”", "\"오, 세상에, 그건 너무 어처구니없는 일이죠!\""], ["said Anna, and again a deep flush of pleasure came out on her face, when she heard the idea, that absorbed her, put into words.", "안나가 말했고, 자기를 사로잡고 있던 그 생각이 말로 옮겨지는 것을 듣자, 다시금 기쁨의 짙은 홍조가 그녀의 얼굴에 떠올랐다."], ["“And so here I am going away, having made an enemy of Kitty, whom I liked so much!", "\"그래서 이렇게 제가 떠나는 거예요. 그토록 좋아했던 키티를 적으로 만들어 놓고서요!"], ["Ah, how sweet she is!", "아, 그 애는 어쩜 그렇게 사랑스러운지!"], ["But you’ll make it right, Dolly?", "하지만 형님이 잘 풀어 주실 거죠, 돌리?"], ["Eh?”", "네?\""]], [["Dolly could scarcely suppress a smile.", "돌리는 미소를 가까스로 억눌렀다."], ["She loved Anna, but she enjoyed seeing that she too had her weaknesses.", "그녀는 안나를 사랑했지만, 안나에게도 약점이 있다는 것을 보는 게 즐거웠다."]], [["“An enemy?", "\"적이라고요?"], ["That can’t be.”", "그럴 리가 없어요.\""]], [["“I did so want you all to care for me, as I do for you, and now I care for you more than ever,”", "\"저는 정말이지 형님네 모두가 제가 형님들을 아끼는 만큼 저를 아껴 주길 바랐어요. 그리고 이제는 그 어느 때보다 형님을 아껴요.\""], ["said Anna, with tears in her eyes.", "안나가 눈물을 글썽이며 말했다."], ["“Ah, how silly I am today!”", "\"아, 오늘 제가 어쩜 이렇게 바보 같은지!\""]], [["She passed her handkerchief over her face and began dressing.", "그녀는 손수건으로 얼굴을 닦고 옷을 갈아입기 시작했다."]], [["At the very moment of starting Stepan Arkadyevitch arrived, late, rosy and good-humored, smelling of wine and cigars.", "막 출발하려는 바로 그 순간에, 스테판 아르카지치가 늦게, 발그레한 얼굴로 기분 좋게 술과 시가 냄새를 풍기며 도착했다."]], [["Anna’s emotionalism infected Dolly, and when she embraced her sister-in-law for the last time, she whispered: “Remember, Anna, what you’ve done for me—I shall never forget.", "안나의 감상적인 기분이 돌리에게도 옮아, 마지막으로 올케를 끌어안으며 그녀는 속삭였다. \"기억해요, 안나, 올케가 나를 위해 해 준 일을—나는 결코 잊지 않을 거예요."], ["And remember that I love you, and shall always love you as my dearest friend!”", "그리고 기억해요, 내가 올케를 사랑한다는 걸, 가장 소중한 벗으로 언제까지나 사랑하리라는 걸!\""]], [["“I don’t know why,”", "\"왜 그러는지 모르겠네요.\""], ["said Anna, kissing her and hiding her tears.", "안나가 그녀에게 입을 맞추고 눈물을 감추며 말했다."]], [["“You understood me, and you understand.", "\"형님은 나를 이해했고, 또 이해하고 있어요."], ["Good-bye, my darling!”", "잘 있어요, 내 사랑스러운 형님!\""]], [["# Chapter 29", "# Chapter 29"]], [["“Come, it’s all over, and thank God!”", "\"자, 다 끝났어. 정말 다행이야!\""], ["was the first thought that came to Anna Arkadyevna, when she had said good-bye for the last time to her brother, who had stood blocking up the entrance to the carriage till the third bell rang.", "안나 아르카지예브나가 오라버니에게 마지막 작별 인사를 했을 때 떠오른 첫 번째 생각이었다. 오라버니는 세 번째 종이 울릴 때까지 객차 입구를 가로막은 채 서 있었다."], ["She sat down on her lounge beside Annushka, and looked about her in the twilight of the sleeping-carriage.", "그녀는 안누슈카 옆 소파에 앉아 침대칸의 어스름한 빛 속에서 주위를 둘러보았다."], ["“Thank God!", "\"정말 다행이야!"], ["tomorrow I shall see Seryozha and Alexey Alexandrovitch, and my life will go on in the old way, all nice and as usual.”", "내일이면 세료자와 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치를 만날 테고, 내 삶은 예전처럼 다시 흘러갈 거야. 모든 게 다 좋고, 늘 그렇듯이 평온하게.\""]], [["Still in the same anxious frame of mind, as she had been all that day, Anna took pleasure in arranging herself for the journey with great care.", "그날 하루 종일 그래 왔듯이 여전히 들뜬 마음으로, 안나는 여행 채비를 정성껏 하는 데서 즐거움을 느꼈다."], ["With her little deft hands she opened and shut her little red bag, took out a cushion, laid it on her knees, and carefully wrapping up her feet, settled herself comfortably.", "작고 야무진 손으로 그녀는 작은 빨간 가방을 열었다 닫았다 하며 방석을 꺼내 무릎 위에 올려놓고, 두 발을 꼼꼼히 감싼 뒤 편안하게 자리를 잡았다."], ["An invalid lady had already lain down to sleep.", "병약한 부인 하나는 벌써 자려고 누워 있었다."], ["Two other ladies began talking to Anna, and a stout elderly lady tucked up her feet, and made observations about the heating of the train.", "다른 두 부인이 안나에게 말을 걸기 시작했고, 뚱뚱한 노부인은 발을 끌어당기며 객차의 난방에 대해 한마디 늘어놓았다."], ["Anna answered a few words, but not foreseeing any entertainment from the conversation, she asked Annushka to get a lamp, hooked it onto the arm of her seat, and took from her bag a paper-knife and an English novel.", "안나는 몇 마디 대꾸했지만, 그 대화에서 별다른 재미를 기대할 수 없었으므로 안누슈카에게 등을 가져오라고 부탁해 좌석 팔걸이에 걸고는, 가방에서 종이칼과 영국 소설책을 꺼냈다. 처음에는 독서가 도무지 진척되지 않았다."], ["At first her reading made no progress.", "부산스러운 소동이 그녀를 방해했다."], ["The fuss and bustle were disturbing; then when the train had started, she could not help listening to the noises; then the snow beating on the left window and sticking to the pane, and the sight of the muffled guard passing by, covered with snow on one side, and the conversations about the terrible snowstorm raging outside, distracted her attention.", "그러다 기차가 출발하자 그 소음에 귀를 기울이지 않을 수 없었다. 그다음에는 왼쪽 창문을 때리며 유리창에 들러붙는 눈이며, 한쪽이 온통 눈에 뒤덮인 채 지나가는 둘둘 싸맨 차장의 모습이며, 바깥에 몰아치는 무서운 눈보라에 대한 이야기들이 그녀의 주의를 흐트러뜨렸다."], ["Farther on, it was continually the same again and again: the same shaking and rattling, the same snow on the window, the same rapid transitions from steaming heat to cold, and back again to heat, the same passing glimpses of the same figures in the twilight, and the same voices, and Anna began to read and to understand what she read.", "더 가서도 똑같은 것이 거듭거듭 되풀이되었다. 똑같은 흔들림과 덜컹거림, 똑같이 창문에 부딪치는 눈, 무더운 열기에서 추위로, 다시 열기로 빠르게 옮겨 가는 똑같은 변화, 어스름 속에서 똑같은 모습들이 스쳐 지나가는 똑같은 광경, 그리고 똑같은 목소리들."], ["Annushka was already dozing, the red bag on her lap, clutched by her broad hands, in gloves, of which one was torn.", "그러다 안나는 책을 읽기 시작했고, 읽은 것을 이해하기 시작했다. 안누슈카는 벌써 꾸벅꾸벅 졸고 있었는데, 장갑 낀 넓적한 손으로 무릎 위의 빨간 가방을 움켜쥐고 있었고, 그 장갑 한 짝은 찢어져 있었다."], ["Anna Arkadyevna read and understood, but it was distasteful to her to read, that is, to follow the reflection of other people’s lives.", "안나 아르카지예브나는 읽으며 이해했지만, 읽는다는 것, 그러니까 다른 사람들의 삶이 비친 모습을 좇는다는 것이 그녀에게는 못마땅했다. 그녀에게는 자기 자신이 살고 싶다는 욕망이 너무도 컸다."], ["She had too great a desire to live herself.", "소설의 여주인공이 병자를 간호한다는 대목을 읽으면, 그녀 자신이 병자의 방을 소리 없는 발걸음으로 거닐고 싶어졌다."], ["If she read that the heroine of the novel was nursing a sick man, she longed to move with noiseless steps about the room of a sick man; if she read of a member of Parliament making a speech, she longed to be delivering the speech; if she read of how Lady Mary had ridden after the hounds, and had provoked her sister-in-law, and had surprised everyone by her boldness, she too wished to be doing the same.", "어느 국회의원이 연설을 한다는 대목을 읽으면, 그녀 자신이 그 연설을 하고 싶어졌다. 메리 부인이 사냥개들을 뒤따라 말을 달려 시누이의 부아를 돋우고 그 대담함으로 모두를 놀라게 했다는 대목을 읽으면, 그녀 역시 똑같은 일을 하고 싶어졌다."], ["But there was no chance of doing anything; and twisting the smooth paper-knife in her little hands, she forced herself to read.", "하지만 무언가를 할 가능성은 없었다. 그래서 매끄러운 종이칼을 작은 손으로 만지작거리며, 그녀는 억지로 책을 읽어 나갔다."]], [["The hero of the novel was already almost reaching his English happiness, a baronetcy and an estate, and Anna was feeling a desire to go with him to the estate, when she suddenly felt that he ought to feel ashamed, and that she was ashamed of the same thing.", "소설의 주인공은 어느덧 영국식 행복, 그러니까 준남작 작위와 영지를 거의 손에 넣어 가고 있었고, 안나는 그와 함께 영지로 가고 싶다는 욕망을 느끼고 있었는데, 그때 문득 그가 부끄러움을 느껴야 마땅하다는 생각이, 그리고 그녀 자신이 바로 그 같은 일을 부끄러워하고 있다는 생각이 들었다."], ["But what had he to be ashamed of? “What have I to be ashamed of?”", "하지만 그가 부끄러워해야 할 일이 무엇이란 말인가?"], ["she asked herself in injured surprise.", "\"내가 부끄러워해야 할 일이 뭐가 있다고?\""], ["She laid down the book and sank against the back of the chair, tightly gripping the paper-cutter in both hands.", "그녀는 상처받은 듯 놀라며 스스로에게 물었다. 그녀는 책을 내려놓고 의자 등받이에 기댄 채, 두 손으로 종이칼을 꽉 움켜쥐었다."], ["There was nothing.", "아무것도 없었다."], ["She went over all her Moscow recollections.", "그녀는 모스크바에서의 추억을 하나하나 되짚어 보았다."], ["All were good, pleasant.", "모두 좋고 즐거운 것들이었다."], ["She remembered the ball, remembered Vronsky and his face of slavish adoration, remembered all her conduct with him: there was nothing shameful.", "무도회를 떠올렸고, 브론스키와 노예처럼 흠모하던 그의 얼굴을 떠올렸으며, 그와 함께한 자기 행동을 모조리 떠올렸다. 부끄러울 만한 것은 하나도 없었다."], ["And for all that, at the same point in her memories, the feeling of shame was intensified, as though some inner voice, just at the point when she thought of Vronsky, were saying to her, “Warm, very warm, hot.”", "그런데도 기억 속의 바로 그 대목에 이르면 부끄러운 감정이 더욱 강해졌으니, 마치 어떤 내면의 목소리가, 그녀가 브론스키를 떠올리는 바로 그 순간에 \"따뜻해, 아주 따뜻해, 뜨거워\" 하고 말하기라도 하는 것 같았다."], ["“Well, what is it?”", "\"그래, 대체 뭐야?\""], ["she said to herself resolutely, shifting her seat in the lounge.", "그녀는 단호하게 스스로에게 말하며 소파 위에서 자리를 고쳐 앉았다."], ["“What does it mean?", "\"이게 무슨 뜻이지?"], ["Am I afraid to look it straight in the face?", "내가 이걸 똑바로 마주 보기가 두려운 걸까?"], ["Why, what is it?", "아니, 도대체 뭐란 말이야?"], ["Can it be that between me and this officer boy there exist, or can exist, any other relations than such as are common with every acquaintance?”", "나와 이 젊은 장교 사이에, 누구와도 흔히 있을 법한 그런 사이 말고 다른 어떤 관계가 있다거나, 있을 수 있다는 건 아니겠지?\""], ["She laughed contemptuously and took up her book again; but now she was definitely unable to follow what she read.", "그녀는 경멸하듯 웃고는 다시 책을 집어 들었다. 하지만 이제는 읽은 것을 도무지 좇을 수가 없었다."], ["She passed the paper-knife over the window pane, then laid its smooth, cool surface to her cheek, and almost laughed aloud at the feeling of delight that all at once without cause came over her.", "그녀는 종이칼을 창유리에 갖다 댔다가, 그 매끄럽고 서늘한 면을 뺨에 대 보고는, 아무런 까닭도 없이 별안간 그녀를 사로잡은 기쁨에 거의 소리 내어 웃을 뻔했다."], ["She felt as though her nerves were strings being strained tighter and tighter on some sort of screwing peg.", "그녀는 마치 자기 신경이 어떤 나사못 위에서 점점 더 팽팽하게 죄어지는 현(絃)이 된 것 같은 기분이었다."], ["She felt her eyes opening wider and wider, her fingers and toes twitching nervously, something within oppressing her breathing, while all shapes and sounds seemed in the uncertain half-light to strike her with unaccustomed vividness.", "두 눈이 점점 더 크게 뜨이고, 손가락과 발가락이 신경질적으로 떨리며, 가슴속 무언가가 숨을 짓누르는 것을 느꼈고, 그러는 동안 어슴푸레한 빛 속에서 온갖 형체와 소리가 낯설게 또렷한 모습으로 그녀를 덮쳐 오는 것 같았다."], ["Moments of doubt were continually coming upon her, when she was uncertain whether the train were going forwards or backwards, or were standing still altogether; whether it were Annushka at her side or a stranger.", "끊임없이 의심의 순간들이 그녀를 덮쳤으니, 기차가 앞으로 가는 건지 뒤로 가는 건지, 아니면 아예 멈춰 서 있는 건지, 옆에 있는 것이 안누슈카인지 낯선 사람인지 확신할 수 없었다."], ["“What’s that on the arm of the chair, a fur cloak or some beast?", "\"팔걸이 위에 있는 저건 뭐지, 모피 외투인가 아니면 무슨 짐승인가?"], ["And what am I myself?", "그리고 나 자신은 뭐지?"], ["Myself or some other woman?”", "나인가, 아니면 다른 어떤 여자인가?\""], ["She was afraid of giving way to this delirium.", "그녀는 이 헛것에 자신을 내맡기기가 두려웠다."], ["But something drew her towards it, and she could yield to it or resist it at will.", "하지만 무언가가 그녀를 그쪽으로 끌어당겼고, 그녀는 마음먹기에 따라 그것에 굴복할 수도 저항할 수도 있었다."], ["She got up to rouse herself, and slipped off her plaid and the cape of her warm dress.", "그녀는 정신을 차리려고 일어나 무릎덮개와 따뜻한 옷의 케이프를 벗었다."], ["For a moment she regained her self-possession, and realized that the thin peasant who had come in wearing a long overcoat, with buttons missing from it, was the stoveheater, that he was looking at the thermometer, that it was the wind and snow bursting in after him at the door; but then everything grew blurred again....", "잠시 그녀는 평정을 되찾았고, 단추가 몇 개 떨어져 나간 긴 외투를 입고 들어온 깡마른 농부가 화부(火夫)라는 것을, 그가 온도계를 들여다보고 있다는 것을, 그의 뒤를 따라 문으로 바람과 눈이 몰아쳐 들어왔다는 것을 깨달았다. 하지만 그러고 나자 모든 것이 다시 흐릿해졌다……"], ["That peasant with the long waist seemed to be gnawing something on the wall, the old lady began stretching her legs the whole length of the carriage, and filling it with a black cloud; then there was a fearful shrieking and banging, as though someone were being torn to pieces; then there was a blinding dazzle of red fire before her eyes and a wall seemed to rise up and hide everything.", "허리가 긴 그 농부는 벽에서 무언가를 갉아먹고 있는 것 같았고, 노부인은 객차 끝까지 두 다리를 쭉 뻗기 시작하며 객차 안을 검은 구름으로 가득 채웠다. 그러더니 누군가가 갈가리 찢기기라도 하는 듯한 무서운 비명과 쾅쾅거리는 소리가 났고, 이어 눈앞에서 붉은 불꽃이 눈을 멀게 할 듯 번쩍이더니 벽 하나가 솟아올라 모든 것을 가려 버리는 것 같았다."], ["Anna felt as though she were sinking down.", "안나는 마치 자기가 아래로 가라앉는 것 같은 기분이었다."], ["But it was not terrible, but delightful.", "하지만 그것은 무섭지 않고 오히려 황홀했다."], ["The voice of a man muffled up and covered with snow shouted something in her ear.", "둘둘 싸맨 채 눈을 뒤집어쓴 남자의 목소리가 그녀의 귓가에 무언가를 외쳤다."], ["She got up and pulled herself together; she realized that they had reached a station and that this was the guard.", "그녀는 일어나 정신을 가다듬었다. 기차가 어느 역에 닿았다는 것을, 그리고 이 사람이 차장이라는 것을 깨달았다."], ["She asked Annushka to hand her the cape she had taken off and her shawl, put them on and moved towards the door.", "그녀는 안누슈카에게 벗어 두었던 케이프와 숄을 건네 달라고 부탁해 그것들을 걸치고 문 쪽으로 갔다."]], [["“Do you wish to get out?”", "\"내리시려고요?\""], ["asked Annushka.", "안누슈카가 물었다."]], [["“Yes, I want a little air.", "\"응, 바람을 좀 쐬고 싶어."], ["It’s very hot in here.”", "여긴 너무 더워.\""], ["And she opened the door.", "그러고 그녀는 문을 열었다."], ["The driving snow and the wind rushed to meet her and struggled with her over the door.", "휘몰아치는 눈과 바람이 그녀를 맞으러 달려들어 문을 두고 그녀와 다투었다."], ["But she enjoyed the struggle.", "하지만 그녀는 그 다툼이 즐거웠다."]], [["She opened the door and went out.", "그녀는 문을 열고 밖으로 나갔다."], ["The wind seemed as though lying in wait for her; with gleeful whistle it tried to snatch her up and bear her off, but she clung to the cold door post, and holding her skirt got down onto the platform and under the shelter of the carriages.", "바람은 마치 그녀를 노리고 매복해 있던 것 같았다. 신이 난 듯 휘파람 소리를 내며 그녀를 낚아채 데려가려 했지만, 그녀는 차가운 문기둥에 매달려 치맛자락을 붙든 채 승강장으로, 객차들이 가려 주는 그늘 밑으로 내려섰다."], ["The wind had been powerful on the steps, but on the platform, under the lee of the carriages, there was a lull.", "계단 위에서는 바람이 거셌으나, 승강장 위, 객차들이 바람을 막아 주는 쪽은 잠잠했다."], ["With enjoyment she drew deep breaths of the frozen, snowy air, and standing near the carriage looked about the platform and the lighted station.", "그녀는 얼어붙은 눈 섞인 공기를 즐거이 깊이 들이마시고는, 객차 옆에 서서 승강장과 불 밝힌 역사를 둘러보았다."]], [["# Chapter 30", "# Chapter 30"]], [["The raging tempest rushed whistling between the wheels of the carriages, about the scaffolding, and round the corner of the station.", "사납게 몰아치는 폭풍이 객차의 바퀴 사이로, 비계 주위로, 역 모퉁이를 돌며 휘파람 소리를 내며 휘몰아쳤다."], ["The carriages, posts, people, everything that was to be seen was covered with snow on one side, and was getting more and more thickly covered.", "객차며 기둥이며 사람들이며, 눈에 보이는 모든 것이 한쪽 면에 눈을 뒤집어쓴 채 점점 더 두껍게 덮여 가고 있었다."], ["For a moment there would come a lull in the storm, but then it would swoop down again with such onslaughts that it seemed impossible to stand against it.", "한순간 폭풍이 잠잠해지는가 싶다가도, 이내 다시 도저히 맞서 버틸 수 없을 것만 같은 기세로 들이닥쳤다."], ["Meanwhile men ran to and fro, talking merrily together, their steps crackling on the platform as they continually opened and closed the big doors.", "그러는 동안에도 사람들은 이리저리 뛰어다니며 즐겁게 이야기를 나누었고, 큰 문을 끊임없이 여닫는 통에 플랫폼 위에서 그들의 발소리가 자작자작 울렸다."], ["The bent shadow of a man glided by at her feet, and she heard sounds of a hammer upon iron.", "한 남자의 구부정한 그림자가 그녀의 발치를 스쳐 지나갔고, 쇠를 두드리는 망치 소리가 들려왔다."], ["“Hand over that telegram!”", "\"그 전보 이리 내!\""], ["came an angry voice out of the stormy darkness on the other side.", "반대편의 폭풍 어린 어둠 속에서 성난 목소리가 들려왔다."], ["“This way!", "\"이쪽이오!"], ["No. 28!”", "28호!\""], ["several different voices shouted again, and muffled figures ran by covered with snow.", "다시 여러 목소리가 제각각 외쳤고, 눈을 뒤집어쓴 사람들이 몸을 싸맨 채 뛰어 지나갔다."], ["Two gentlemen with lighted cigarettes passed by her.", "불붙인 담배를 문 신사 둘이 그녀 곁을 지나갔다."], ["She drew one more deep breath of the fresh air, and had just put her hand out of her muff to take hold of the door post and get back into the carriage, when another man in a military overcoat, quite close beside her, stepped between her and the flickering light of the lamp post.", "그녀는 신선한 공기를 한 번 더 깊이 들이마시고는, 객차로 돌아가려고 토시에서 손을 빼 문기둥을 잡으려던 참이었다. 그때 군용 외투를 입은 또 한 남자가 그녀 바로 옆으로 다가와 그녀와 가로등의 깜빡이는 불빛 사이로 들어섰다."], ["She looked round, and the same instant recognized Vronsky’s face.", "그녀가 돌아보자, 바로 그 순간 브론스키의 얼굴을 알아보았다."], ["Putting his hand to the peak of his cap, he bowed to her and asked, Was there anything she wanted? Could he be of any service to her?", "그는 모자챙에 손을 갖다 대며 그녀에게 인사하고는, 무언가 필요한 것이 있는지, 자기가 무엇이든 도와드릴 수 있는지 물었다."], ["She gazed rather a long while at him without answering, and, in spite of the shadow in which he was standing, she saw, or fancied she saw, both the expression of his face and his eyes.", "그녀는 대답도 없이 꽤 오랫동안 그를 바라보았고, 그가 그늘 속에 서 있는데도 그의 얼굴 표정과 두 눈을 보았다, 아니 본 듯한 느낌이 들었다."], ["It was again that expression of reverential ecstasy which had so worked upon her the day before.", "그것은 어제 그녀를 그토록 흔들어 놓았던 바로 그 경건한 황홀의 표정이었다."], ["More than once she had told herself during the past few days, and again only a few moments before, that Vronsky was for her only one of the hundreds of young men, forever exactly the same, that are met everywhere, that she would never allow herself to bestow a thought upon him.", "지난 며칠 동안 그녀는 여러 번, 그리고 불과 몇 분 전에도 다시, 브론스키란 자기에게 어디서나 마주치는, 늘 한결같은 그 수백 명의 젊은이들 가운데 하나일 뿐이며, 결코 그에게 한 가닥 생각조차 허락하지 않으리라고 스스로에게 다짐했었다."], ["But now at the first instant of meeting him, she was seized by a feeling of joyful pride.", "그러나 지금 그를 만난 첫 순간, 그녀는 기쁨에 찬 자랑스러움에 사로잡혔다."], ["She had no need to ask why he had come.", "그가 무엇 때문에 왔는지 물어볼 필요도 없었다."], ["She knew as certainly as if he had told her that he was here to be where she was.", "그가 그녀에게 직접 말이라도 한 듯, 그가 그녀가 있는 곳에 있기 위해 여기 왔다는 것을 그녀는 분명히 알고 있었다."]], [["“I didn’t know you were going.", "\"당신이 떠나는 줄은 몰랐어요."], ["What are you coming for?”", "무엇 때문에 오시는 거죠?\""], ["she said, letting fall the hand with which she had grasped the door post.", "그녀가 문기둥을 잡았던 손을 떨구며 말했다."], ["And irrepressible delight and eagerness shone in her face.", "그리고 억누를 수 없는 기쁨과 열망이 그녀의 얼굴에서 빛났다."]], [["“What am I coming for?”", "\"제가 무엇 때문에 오느냐고요?\""], ["he repeated, looking straight into her eyes.", "그가 그녀의 눈을 똑바로 들여다보며 되뇌었다."], ["“You know that I have come to be where you are,”", "\"제가 당신이 있는 곳에 있으려고 왔다는 걸 당신은 알고 계시지요.\""], ["he said; “I can’t help it.”", "그가 말했다. \"어쩔 수가 없습니다.\""]], [["At that moment the wind, as it were, surmounting all obstacles, sent the snow flying from the carriage roofs, and clanked some sheet of iron it had torn off, while the hoarse whistle of the engine roared in front, plaintively and gloomily.", "바로 그 순간, 바람이 마치 모든 장애물을 넘어서기라도 한 듯 객차 지붕에서 눈을 날려 보냈고, 어딘가에서 뜯어낸 양철판을 덜그럭거렸다. 그러는 사이 기관차의 쉰 기적 소리가 앞쪽에서 구슬프고 음울하게 울부짖었다."], ["All the awfulness of the storm seemed to her more splendid now.", "폭풍의 그 온갖 무시무시함이 이제 그녀에게는 더욱 찬란하게 느껴졌다."], ["He had said what her soul longed to hear, though she feared it with her reason.", "그는 그녀의 영혼이 듣기를 갈망하던 것을, 비록 이성으로는 두려워하던 것을 말해 버린 것이다."], ["She made no answer, and in her face he saw conflict.", "그녀는 아무 대답도 하지 않았고, 그는 그녀의 얼굴에서 갈등을 보았다."]], [["“Forgive me, if you dislike what I said,”", "\"제 말이 마음에 들지 않으셨다면, 용서해 주십시오.\""], ["he said humbly.", "그가 겸손하게 말했다."]], [["He had spoken courteously, deferentially, yet so firmly, so stubbornly, that for a long while she could make no answer.", "그는 정중하게, 공손하게 말했지만, 그러면서도 어찌나 단호하고 끈질기게 말하던지, 그녀는 한참 동안 아무 대답도 할 수 없었다."]], [["“It’s wrong, what you say, and I beg you, if you’re a good man, to forget what you’ve said, as I forget it,”", "\"당신이 하는 말은 옳지 않아요. 그러니 부탁드려요, 당신이 좋은 분이라면, 제가 잊듯이 당신이 한 말을 잊어 주세요.\""], ["she said at last.", "그녀가 마침내 말했다."]], [["“Not one word, not one gesture of yours shall I, could I, ever forget....”", "\"당신의 말 한마디도, 몸짓 하나도 저는 결코 잊지 못할 겁니다, 잊을 수도 없습니다……\""]], [["“Enough, enough!”", "\"그만, 그만하세요!\""], ["she cried trying assiduously to give a stern expression to her face, into which he was gazing greedily.", "그녀는 그가 게걸스레 들여다보고 있는 자기 얼굴에 애써 엄한 표정을 지으려 안간힘을 쓰며 외쳤다."], ["And clutching at the cold door post, she clambered up the steps and got rapidly into the corridor of the carriage.", "그러고는 차가운 문기둥을 움켜쥐고 계단을 기어올라 잰걸음으로 객차의 통로 안으로 들어섰다."], ["But in the little corridor she paused, going over in her imagination what had happened.", "그러나 그 좁은 통로에서 그녀는 걸음을 멈추고, 방금 일어난 일을 머릿속에서 곱씹어 보았다."], ["Though she could not recall her own words or his, she realized instinctively that the momentary conversation had brought them fearfully closer; and she was panic-stricken and blissful at it.", "자기가 한 말도, 그가 한 말도 떠올릴 수는 없었지만, 그녀는 그 순간의 대화가 두 사람을 무섭도록 가깝게 만들어 놓았음을 본능적으로 깨달았다. 그리고 그 일에 그녀는 공포에 사로잡히면서도 더없이 행복했다."], ["After standing still a few seconds, she went into the carriage and sat down in her place.", "몇 초 동안 가만히 서 있다가, 그녀는 객차 안으로 들어가 자기 자리에 앉았다."], ["The overstrained condition which had tormented her before did not only come back, but was intensified, and reached such a pitch that she was afraid every minute that something would snap within her from the excessive tension.", "전에 그녀를 괴롭히던 그 극도로 긴장된 상태가 다시 돌아왔을 뿐 아니라 더욱 심해졌고, 마침내 지나친 긴장으로 자기 안에서 무언가 뚝 끊어지지나 않을까 매 순간 두려울 만큼 극에 달했다."], ["She did not sleep all night.", "그녀는 밤새 한숨도 자지 못했다."], ["But in that nervous tension, and in the visions that filled her imagination, there was nothing disagreeable or gloomy: on the contrary there was something blissful, glowing, and exhilarating.", "그러나 그 신경의 긴장에도, 또 그녀의 상상을 가득 채운 환영들에도 불쾌하거나 음울한 것이라곤 전혀 없었다. 오히려 더없이 행복하고 뜨겁게 타오르며 가슴 벅찬 무언가가 있었다."], ["Towards morning Anna sank into a doze, sitting in her place, and when she waked it was daylight and the train was near Petersburg.", "아침이 가까워질 무렵 안나는 자리에 앉은 채 깜빡 잠이 들었고, 깨어 보니 날은 밝아 있었고 기차는 페테르부르크에 가까이 와 있었다."], ["At once thoughts of home, of husband and of son, and the details of that day and the following came upon her.", "곧바로 집 생각, 남편과 아들 생각, 그리고 그날과 이튿날의 자질구레한 일들이 그녀에게 밀려들었다."]], [["At Petersburg, as soon as the train stopped and she got out, the first person that attracted her attention was her husband.", "페테르부르크에서 기차가 멈추고 그녀가 내리자마자 그녀의 주의를 끈 첫 번째 사람은 남편이었다."], ["“Oh, mercy!", "\"어머나!"], ["why do his ears look like that?”", "저이 귀가 왜 저렇게 보이지?\""], ["she thought, looking at his frigid and imposing figure, and especially the ears that struck her at the moment as propping up the brim of his round hat.", "그녀는 차갑고 위엄 있는 그의 모습을, 특히 그 순간 둥근 모자챙을 떠받치고 있는 것처럼 그녀의 눈에 띈 두 귀를 바라보며 생각했다."], ["Catching sight of her, he came to meet her, his lips falling into their habitual sarcastic smile, and his big, tired eyes looking straight at her.", "그녀를 알아본 그는 마중하러 다가왔는데, 그의 입술은 늘 짓던 그 빈정대는 미소로 일그러졌고, 크고 지친 두 눈은 그녀를 똑바로 바라보고 있었다."], ["An unpleasant sensation gripped at her heart when she met his obstinate and weary glance, as though she had expected to see him different.", "그의 고집스럽고 지친 시선과 마주쳤을 때, 마치 그가 다른 모습이기를 기대하기라도 했던 듯, 불쾌한 감각이 그녀의 가슴을 옥죄었다."], ["She was especially struck by the feeling of dissatisfaction with herself that she experienced on meeting him.", "무엇보다 그녀를 충격에 빠뜨린 것은 그를 만나면서 느낀 자기 자신에 대한 불만이었다."], ["That feeling was an intimate, familiar feeling, like a consciousness of hypocrisy, which she experienced in her relations with her husband.", "그것은 남편과의 관계에서 그녀가 늘 느껴 온, 위선에 대한 자각과도 같은, 은밀하고 익숙한 감정이었다."], ["But hitherto she had not taken note of the feeling, now she was clearly and painfully aware of it.", "그러나 지금까지 그녀는 그 감정을 눈여겨보지 않았는데, 이제는 그것을 또렷하고도 고통스럽게 의식하게 되었다."]], [["“Yes, as you see, your tender spouse, as devoted as the first year after marriage, burned with impatience to see you,”", "\"그래, 보시다시피, 당신의 다정한 남편이, 결혼 첫해처럼 헌신적인 남편이, 당신을 만나고 싶어 안달이 나서 왔소.\""], ["he said in his deliberate, high-pitched voice, and in that tone which he almost always took with her, a tone of jeering at anyone who should say in earnest what he said.", "그가 신중하고 높은 목소리로, 그리고 그가 그녀에게 거의 언제나 쓰는 그 어조로, 그러니까 자기가 하는 말을 진지하게 받아들이는 사람이 있다면 누구든 비웃는 듯한 그 어조로 말했다."]], [["“Is Seryozha quite well?”", "\"세료자는 잘 있나요?\""], ["she asked.", "그녀가 물었다."]], [["“And is this all the reward,”", "\"이게 내 열정에 대한 보답이란 말이오?\""], ["said he, “for my ardor?", "그가 말했다."], ["He’s quite well....”", "\"그 애는 아주 잘 있소……\""]], [["# Chapter 31", "# Chapter 31"]], [["Vronsky had not even tried to sleep all that night.", "브론스키는 그날 밤 내내 잠들려고 시도조차 하지 않았다."], ["He sat in his armchair, looking straight before him or scanning the people who got in and out.", "그는 안락의자에 앉아 정면을 똑바로 응시하거나 타고 내리는 사람들을 훑어보았다."], ["If he had indeed on previous occasions struck and impressed people who did not know him by his air of unhesitating composure, he seemed now more haughty and self-possessed than ever.", "전에도 그가 거리낌 없는 침착한 태도로 자기를 모르는 사람들에게 강한 인상을 남기곤 했다면, 지금은 그 어느 때보다 더 거만하고 자신만만해 보였다."], ["He looked at people as if they were things.", "그는 마치 사람들을 사물 보듯 바라보았다."], ["A nervous young man, a clerk in a law court, sitting opposite him, hated him for that look.", "맞은편에 앉은, 법원 서기인 신경질적인 젊은이는 그 시선 때문에 그를 미워했다."], ["The young man asked him for a light, and entered into conversation with him, and even pushed against him, to make him feel that he was not a thing, but a person.", "젊은이는 그에게 불을 빌려 달라고 청하고는 말을 걸어왔고, 심지어 자기가 사물이 아니라 사람이라는 걸 느끼게 하려고 일부러 그를 밀치기까지 했다."], ["But Vronsky gazed at him exactly as he did at the lamp, and the young man made a wry face, feeling that he was losing his self-possession under the oppression of this refusal to recognize him as a person.", "그러나 브론스키는 램프를 보던 그대로 그를 바라보았고, 젊은이는 자신을 사람으로 인정하지 않는 이 거부의 압박 아래 침착함을 잃어 가는 걸 느끼며 얼굴을 찌푸렸다."]], [["Vronsky saw nothing and no one.", "브론스키에게는 아무것도, 아무도 보이지 않았다."], ["He felt himself a king, not because he believed that he had made an impression on Anna—he did not yet believe that,—but because the impression she had made on him gave him happiness and pride.", "그는 자신을 왕처럼 느꼈는데, 그것은 자기가 안나에게 어떤 인상을 남겼다고 믿어서가 아니라—그는 아직 그것을 믿지 않았다—그녀가 자기에게 남긴 인상이 그에게 행복과 자긍심을 안겨 주었기 때문이었다."]], [["What would come of it all he did not know, he did not even think.", "이 모든 일에서 무엇이 나올지 그는 알지 못했고, 생각조차 하지 않았다."], ["He felt that all his forces, hitherto dissipated, wasted, were centered on one thing, and bent with fearful energy on one blissful goal.", "그는 지금까지 흩어지고 허비되어 온 자기의 모든 힘이 한곳에 모여, 무서운 기세로 하나의 더없이 행복한 목표를 향해 쏠리는 것을 느꼈다."], ["And he was happy at it.", "그리고 그는 그것이 행복했다."], ["He knew only that he had told her the truth, that he had come where she was, that all the happiness of his life, the only meaning in life for him, now lay in seeing and hearing her.", "그가 아는 것이라곤 자기가 그녀에게 진실을 말했다는 것, 그녀가 있는 곳으로 자기가 왔다는 것, 자기 삶의 모든 행복이, 자기에게 삶의 유일한 의미가 이제 그녀를 보고 듣는 데에 있다는 것뿐이었다."], ["And when he got out of the carriage at Bologova to get some seltzer water, and caught sight of Anna, involuntarily his first word had told her just what he thought.", "그리고 볼로고보에서 셀처 물을 마시려고 객차에서 내렸다가 안나를 보았을 때, 그의 첫마디는 자기도 모르게 자기가 생각하던 것을 그대로 그녀에게 전하고 말았다."], ["And he was glad he had told her it, that she knew it now and was thinking of it.", "그리고 그는 그것을 그녀에게 말했다는 것이, 이제 그녀가 그것을 알고 그것을 생각하고 있다는 것이 기뻤다."], ["He did not sleep all night.", "그는 밤새 한숨도 자지 못했다."], ["When he was back in the carriage, he kept unceasingly going over every position in which he had seen her, every word she had uttered, and before his fancy, making his heart faint with emotion, floated pictures of a possible future.", "객차로 돌아온 그는 끊임없이, 그녀를 보았던 모든 모습, 그녀가 내뱉었던 모든 말을 되짚었고, 그의 상상 속에는, 감격으로 그의 심장을 아련하게 하면서, 있을 법한 미래의 그림들이 떠다녔다."]], [["When he got out of the train at Petersburg, he felt after his sleepless night as keen and fresh as after a cold bath.", "페테르부르크에서 기차에서 내렸을 때, 그는 잠 못 이룬 밤을 보냈는데도 마치 찬물로 목욕이라도 한 듯 날카롭고 상쾌한 기분이었다."], ["He paused near his compartment, waiting for her to get out.", "그는 그녀가 내리기를 기다리며 자기 객실 곁에 멈춰 섰다. \"한 번 더.\""], ["“Once more,”", "그는 자기도 모르게 미소를 지으며 혼잣말을 했다."], ["he said to himself, smiling unconsciously, “once more I shall see her walk, her face; she will say something, turn her head, glance, smile, maybe.”", "\"한 번 더 나는 그녀가 걷는 모습을, 그녀의 얼굴을 보게 될 거야. 그녀는 무언가 말하겠지, 고개를 돌리고, 흘끗 보고, 어쩌면 미소를 지을지도 몰라.\""], ["But before he caught sight of her, he saw her husband, whom the station-master was deferentially escorting through the crowd.", "그러나 그녀를 알아보기 전에, 그는 그녀의 남편을 보았다. 역장이 공손하게 그를 군중 사이로 안내하고 있었다."], ["“Ah, yes! The husband.”", "\"아, 그렇지!"], ["Only now for the first time did Vronsky realize clearly the fact that there was a person attached to her, a husband.", "남편.\" 이제 처음으로 브론스키는 그녀에게 딸린 사람이, 남편이 있다는 사실을 분명하게 깨달았다."], ["He knew that she had a husband, but had hardly believed in his existence, and only now fully believed in him, with his head and shoulders, and his legs clad in black trousers; especially when he saw this husband calmly take her arm with a sense of property.", "그녀에게 남편이 있다는 것은 알고 있었지만, 그 존재를 거의 믿지 않았는데, 지금에야 그를, 그의 머리와 어깨를, 검은 바지를 입은 그의 다리를 온전히 믿게 되었다. 특히 이 남편이 소유주 같은 마음으로 침착하게 그녀의 팔을 잡는 것을 보았을 때 그러했다."]], [["Seeing Alexey Alexandrovitch with his Petersburg face and severely self-confident figure, in his round hat, with his rather prominent spine, he believed in him, and was aware of a disagreeable sensation, such as a man might feel tortured by thirst, who, on reaching a spring, should find a dog, a sheep, or a pig, who has drunk of it and muddied the water.", "페테르부르크다운 얼굴에 엄격하고 자신만만한 풍채를 한 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가, 둥근 모자를 쓰고, 다소 튀어나온 등을 한 모습을 보자, 그는 그의 존재를 믿게 되었고, 불쾌한 감각을 느꼈다. 갈증에 시달리던 사람이 샘에 다다랐는데, 거기서 이미 물을 마셔 흙탕으로 만들어 놓은 개나 양이나 돼지를 발견했을 때 느낄 법한 그런 감각이었다."], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch’s manner of walking, with a swing of the hips and flat feet, particularly annoyed Vronsky.", "엉덩이를 흔들고 발을 평평하게 디디며 걷는 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 걸음걸이가 특히 브론스키의 심사를 거슬렀다."], ["He could recognize in no one but himself an indubitable right to love her.", "그는 그녀를 사랑할 의심할 바 없는 권리를 자기 자신 외에는 그 누구에게서도 인정할 수 없었다."], ["But she was still the same, and the sight of her affected him the same way, physically reviving him, stirring him, and filling his soul with rapture.", "그러나 그녀는 여전히 그대로였고, 그녀를 보는 것은 그를 똑같이 동요시켰다. 그를 육체적으로 되살려 내고, 그를 흔들어 놓고, 그의 영혼을 황홀감으로 가득 채웠다."], ["He told his German valet, who ran up to him from the second class, to take his things and go on, and he himself went up to her.", "그는 이등칸에서 달려온 독일인 시종에게 짐을 챙겨 먼저 가라고 이르고는, 자기는 그녀에게 다가갔다."], ["He saw the first meeting between the husband and wife, and noted with a lover’s insight the signs of slight reserve with which she spoke to her husband.", "그는 부부의 첫 만남을 보았고, 연인의 통찰력으로 그녀가 남편에게 말할 때의 가벼운 서먹함의 기색을 알아챘다."], ["“No, she does not love him and cannot love him,”", "\"아니야, 그녀는 그를 사랑하지 않고, 사랑할 수도 없어.\""], ["he decided to himself.", "그는 혼자 단정 지었다."]], [["At the moment when he was approaching Anna Arkadyevna he noticed too with joy that she was conscious of his being near, and looked round, and seeing him, turned again to her husband.", "그가 안나 아르카지예브나에게 다가가던 바로 그 순간, 그는 그녀가 자기가 가까이 있는 것을 의식하고 있다는 것을, 그래서 그녀가 둘레를 둘러보다가 자기를 보고는 다시 남편에게로 고개를 돌렸다는 것을 기쁘게 알아챘다."]], [["“Have you passed a good night?”", "\"밤새 편히 주무셨습니까?\""], ["he asked, bowing to her and her husband together, and leaving it up to Alexey Alexandrovitch to accept the bow on his own account, and to recognize it or not, as he might see fit.", "그는 그녀와 그녀의 남편에게 동시에 인사를 하며 물었고, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 그 인사를 자기 몫으로 받아들이든 말든, 그것을 알은체하든 말든 마음대로 하도록 맡겨 두었다."]], [["“Thank you, very good,”", "\"고마워요, 아주 편히 잤어요.\""], ["she answered.", "그녀가 대답했다."]], [["Her face looked weary, and there was not that play of eagerness in it, peeping out in her smile and her eyes; but for a single instant, as she glanced at him, there was a flash of something in her eyes, and although the flash died away at once, he was happy for that moment.", "그녀의 얼굴은 피곤해 보였고, 미소와 눈빛으로 엿보이던 그 생기 넘치는 활기가 거기엔 없었다. 그러나 그녀가 그를 흘끗 보던 한순간, 그녀의 눈 속에서 무언가가 번뜩였고, 비록 그 번뜩임이 곧 사라지긴 했지만, 그는 그 순간이 행복했다."], ["She glanced at her husband to find out whether he knew Vronsky.", "그녀는 남편이 브론스키를 아는지 알아보려고 그를 흘끗 보았다."], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch looked at Vronsky with displeasure, vaguely recalling who this was.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 이 사람이 누구인지 어렴풋이 떠올리면서 불쾌한 듯 브론스키를 바라보았다."], ["Vronsky’s composure and self-confidence here struck, like a scythe against a stone, upon the cold self-confidence of Alexey Alexandrovitch.", "브론스키의 침착함과 자신감은 여기서, 마치 낫이 돌에 부딪치듯, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 차가운 자신감과 부딪쳤다."]], [["“Count Vronsky,”", "\"브론스키 백작이에요.\""], ["said Anna.", "안나가 말했다."]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["We are acquainted, I believe,”", "우리는 서로 아는 사이인 듯합니다.\""], ["said Alexey Alexandrovitch indifferently, giving his hand.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 무심하게 손을 내밀며 말했다."]], [["“You set off with the mother and you return with the son,”", "\"올 때는 어머니와 함께 떠나셨다가 돌아올 때는 아들과 함께 오셨군요.\""], ["he said, articulating each syllable, as though each were a separate favor he was bestowing.", "그는 마치 한 음절 한 음절이 자기가 베푸는 별개의 호의이기라도 한 듯, 한 음절씩 또박또박 발음하며 말했다."]], [["“You’re back from leave, I suppose?”", "\"휴가에서 돌아오시는 길인가 보군요?\""], ["he said, and without waiting for a reply, he turned to his wife in his jesting tone: “Well, were a great many tears shed at Moscow at parting?”", "그는 이렇게 말하고는, 대답도 기다리지 않고 농담조로 아내에게 돌아섰다. \"그래, 모스크바에서 헤어질 때 눈물깨나 흘렸겠지?\""]], [["By addressing his wife like this he gave Vronsky to understand that he wished to be left alone, and, turning slightly towards him, he touched his hat; but Vronsky turned to Anna Arkadyevna.", "이렇게 아내에게 말을 건넴으로써 그는 자기를 좀 내버려 두기를 바란다는 것을 브론스키에게 알렸고, 그를 향해 살짝 몸을 돌리며 모자에 손을 댔다. 그러나 브론스키는 안나 아르카지예브나에게로 돌아섰다."]], [["“I hope I may have the honor of calling on you,”", "\"댁으로 방문할 영광을 누릴 수 있었으면 합니다.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch glanced with his weary eyes at Vronsky.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 피곤한 눈으로 브론스키를 흘끗 보았다."]], [["“Delighted,”", "\"기쁘게 맞이하지요.\""], ["he said coldly.", "그가 쌀쌀맞게 말했다."], ["“On Mondays we’re at home.", "\"월요일마다 우리는 집에 있습니다."], ["Most fortunate,”", "정말 다행이오.\""], ["he said to his wife, dismissing Vronsky altogether, “that I should just have half an hour to meet you, so that I can prove my devotion,”", "그는 브론스키 따위는 아예 무시하고 아내에게 말했다. \"당신을 마중하러 나올 삼십 분이 마침 있어서 말이오, 그래야 내 헌신을 증명할 수 있을 테니.\""], ["he went on in the same jesting tone.", "그는 여전히 같은 농담조로 말을 이었다."]], [["“You lay too much stress on your devotion for me to value it much,”", "\"당신은 헌신을 너무 강조하시니 내가 그걸 그리 대단하게 여길 수가 없네요.\""], ["she responded in the same jesting tone, involuntarily listening to the sound of Vronsky’s steps behind them.", "그녀도 같은 농담조로 대꾸하면서, 자기도 모르게 뒤에서 들려오는 브론스키의 발소리에 귀를 기울이고 있었다."], ["“But what has it to do with me?”", "\"하지만 그게 나랑 무슨 상관이야?\""], ["she said to herself, and she began asking her husband how Seryozha had got on without her.", "그녀는 속으로 중얼거리고는, 자기가 없는 동안 세료자가 어떻게 지냈는지 남편에게 묻기 시작했다."]], [["“Oh, capitally!", "\"오, 아주 잘 지냈소!"], ["Mariette says he has been very good, And ...", "마리에트 말로는 그 애가 무척 착하게 굴었다는군."], ["I must disappoint you ...", "그리고…… 당신을 실망시켜야겠지만……"], ["but he has not missed you as your husband has.", "그 애는 당신 남편만큼 당신을 그리워하지 않았다오."], ["But once more merci, my dear, for giving me a day.", "하지만 다시 한번 merci, 여보, 하루를 내줘서 말이오."], ["Our dear Samovar will be delighted.”", "우리의 사랑스러운 Samovar가 무척 기뻐할 거요.\""], ["(He used to call the Countess Lidia Ivanovna, well known in society, a samovar, because she was always bubbling over with excitement.) “She has been continually asking after you.", "(그는 사교계에 잘 알려진 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인을, 그녀가 늘 흥분으로 부글부글 끓어오르는 까닭에, 사모바르라고 부르곤 했다.) \"그 부인이 계속 당신 안부를 물었다오."], ["And, do you know, if I may venture to advise you, you should go and see her today.", "그리고 말이오, 내가 감히 조언을 좀 하자면, 당신 오늘 그 부인을 찾아가 보는 게 좋겠소."], ["You know how she takes everything to heart.", "그 부인이 모든 일을 얼마나 가슴에 담아 두는지 당신도 알지 않소."], ["Just now, with all her own cares, she’s anxious about the Oblonskys being brought together.”", "지금도, 자기 일만으로도 벅찰 텐데, 오블론스키 부부를 화해시키는 일로 애를 태우고 있다오.\""]], [["The Countess Lidia Ivanovna was a friend of her husband’s, and the center of that one of the coteries of the Petersburg world with which Anna was, through her husband, in the closest relations.", "* merci: 프랑스어로 '고맙소'. * Samovar: 러시아식 찻물 끓이는 주전자. 사모바르. 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인은 그녀 남편의 친구였고, 안나가 남편을 통해 가장 가깝게 관계 맺고 있던 페테르부르크 사교계의 한 동아리의 중심인물이었다."]], [["“But you know I wrote to her?”", "\"하지만 내가 그분께 편지를 쓴 거 아시잖아요?\""]], [["“Still she’ll want to hear details.", "\"그래도 자세한 이야기를 듣고 싶어 할 거요."], ["Go and see her, if you’re not too tired, my dear.", "너무 피곤하지 않거든 찾아가 보구려, 여보."], ["Well, Kondraty will take you in the carriage, while I go to my committee.", "자, 콘드라티가 당신을 마차로 데려다줄 거요, 나는 위원회에 가야 하니."], ["I shall not be alone at dinner again,”", "또다시 혼자 저녁을 먹지 않아도 되겠군.\""], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch went on, no longer in a sarcastic tone.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 더 이상 빈정대는 어조가 아니게 말을 이었다."], ["“You wouldn’t believe how I’ve missed....”", "\"내가 얼마나 그리웠는지 당신은 믿지 못할 거요…….\""], ["And with a long pressure of her hand and a meaning smile, he put her in her carriage.", "그러고는 그녀의 손을 오래도록 꼭 쥐고 의미심장한 미소를 지으며 그녀를 마차에 태웠다."]], [["# Chapter 32", "# Chapter 32"]], [["The first person to meet Anna at home was her son.", "집에서 안나를 가장 먼저 맞이한 사람은 아들이었다."], ["He dashed down the stairs to her, in spite of the governess’s call, and with desperate joy shrieked: “Mother!", "가정교사가 부르는 소리에도 아랑곳없이 아이는 계단을 뛰어 내려와 그녀에게로 달려왔고, 미친 듯한 기쁨에 차 \"엄마!"], ["mother!”", "엄마!\""], ["Running up to her, he hung on her neck.", "하고 소리쳤다. 그녀에게 달려온 아이는 그녀의 목에 매달렸다."]], [["“I told you it was mother!”", "\"엄마라고 했잖아요!\""], ["he shouted to the governess.", "아이가 가정교사를 향해 외쳤다."], ["“I knew!”", "\"내가 알았다고요!\""]], [["And her son, like her husband, aroused in Anna a feeling akin to disappointment.", "남편이 그랬듯 아들 역시 안나에게 실망과 비슷한 감정을 불러일으켰다."], ["She had imagined him better than he was in reality.", "그녀는 실제보다 더 나은 아이를 상상하고 있었던 것이다."], ["She had to let herself drop down to the reality to enjoy him as he really was.", "그녀는 있는 그대로의 아이를 즐기기 위해 현실로 자신을 끌어내려야 했다."], ["But even as he was, he was charming, with his fair curls, his blue eyes, and his plump, graceful little legs in tightly pulled-up stockings.", "하지만 있는 그대로라 해도 아이는 사랑스러웠다. 금빛 곱슬머리와 푸른 눈, 그리고 팽팽하게 끌어 올린 양말 속의 통통하고 우아한 작은 다리."], ["Anna experienced almost physical pleasure in the sensation of his nearness, and his caresses, and moral soothing, when she met his simple, confiding, and loving glance, and heard his naïve questions.", "아이의 순진하고 신뢰에 찬, 사랑이 담긴 눈길을 마주하고 그 천진한 질문을 들으니, 안나는 아이가 곁에 있다는 느낌과 그 어루만짐, 그리고 마음의 위안 속에서 거의 육체적인 기쁨을 느꼈다."], ["Anna took out the presents Dolly’s children had sent him, and told her son what sort of little girl was Tanya at Moscow, and how Tanya could read, and even taught the other children.", "안나는 돌리네 아이들이 보낸 선물을 꺼냈고, 모스크바의 타냐가 어떤 여자아이인지, 또 타냐가 글을 읽을 줄 알고 다른 아이들까지 가르친다는 이야기를 아들에게 들려주었다."]], [["“Why, am I not so nice as she?”", "\"그럼, 나는 그 누나만큼 착하지 않아요?\""], ["asked Seryozha.", "세료자가 물었다."]], [["“To me you’re nicer than anyone in the world.”", "\"내겐 네가 이 세상 누구보다도 더 착하지.\""]], [["“I know that,”", "\"나도 알아요.\""], ["said Seryozha, smiling.", "세료자가 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["Anna had not had time to drink her coffee when the Countess Lidia Ivanovna was announced.", "안나가 미처 커피를 다 마시기도 전에 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인이 왔다는 전갈이 왔다."], ["The Countess Lidia Ivanovna was a tall, stout woman, with an unhealthily sallow face and splendid, pensive black eyes.", "리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인은 키가 크고 살집이 있는 여자로, 병색이 도는 누런 얼굴에 깊은 생각에 잠긴 듯한 멋진 검은 눈을 하고 있었다."], ["Anna liked her, but today she seemed to be seeing her for the first time with all her defects.", "안나는 그녀를 좋아했지만, 오늘은 그녀의 온갖 결점을 마치 처음 보는 것 같은 기분이었다."]], [["“Well, my dear, so you took the olive branch?”", "\"그래, 여보, 그러니까 화해를 했나요?\""], ["inquired Countess Lidia Ivanovna, as soon as she came into the room.", "리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인이 방에 들어서자마자 물었다. * 화해의 표시인 올리브 가지를 가져왔느냐는 비유적 표현."]], [["“Yes, it’s all over, but it was all much less serious than we had supposed,”", "\"네, 다 끝났어요. 그런데 우리가 생각했던 것보다 훨씬 덜 심각한 일이었어요.\""], ["answered Anna.", "안나가 대답했다."], ["“My belle-sœur is in general too hasty.”", "\"제 belle-sœur가 대체로 너무 성급해서요.\""]], [["But Countess Lidia Ivanovna, though she was interested in everything that did not concern her, had a habit of never listening to what interested her; she interrupted Anna:", "* belle-sœur: 프랑스어로 '올케' 또는 '시누이'. 여기서는 올케 돌리를 가리킨다. 하지만 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인은 자기와 상관없는 모든 일에는 관심을 보이면서도, 정작 자기가 관심을 두는 것은 절대 들으려 하지 않는 버릇이 있었다. 그녀는 안나의 말을 가로막았다."]], [["“Yes, there’s plenty of sorrow and evil in the world.", "\"그래요, 세상엔 슬픔과 악이 넘쳐 나죠."], ["I am so worried today.”", "나는 오늘 정말 속이 상해요.\""]], [["“Oh, why?”", "\"어머, 왜요?\""], ["asked Anna, trying to suppress a smile.", "안나가 미소를 누르려 애쓰며 물었다."]], [["“I’m beginning to be weary of fruitlessly championing the truth, and sometimes I’m quite unhinged by it.", "\"진리를 위해 보람도 없이 싸우는 데 지치기 시작했어요. 가끔은 그것 때문에 마음이 완전히 무너질 지경이에요."], ["The Society of the Little Sisters”", "'작은 자매회'가\""], ["(this was a religiously-patriotic, philanthropic institution) “was going splendidly, but with these gentlemen it’s impossible to do anything,”", "(이것은 종교적·애국적인 자선 단체였다) \"아주 잘 굴러가고 있었는데, 그 양반들하고는 도무지 아무 일도 할 수가 없어요.\""], ["added Countess Lidia Ivanovna in a tone of ironical submission to destiny.", "리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인이 운명에 빈정대듯 순응하는 어조로 덧붙였다."], ["“They pounce on the idea, and distort it, and then work it out so pettily and unworthily.", "\"그들은 어떤 생각에 달려들어서 그것을 왜곡하고, 그러고는 그걸 아주 옹졸하고 천하게 처리해 버려요."], ["Two or three people, your husband among them, understand all the importance of the thing, but the others simply drag it down.", "당신 남편을 포함한 두세 사람만이 이 일의 중요성을 다 이해하고, 나머지는 그냥 그걸 끌어내릴 뿐이에요."], ["Yesterday Pravdin wrote to me....”", "어제 프라브딘이 내게 편지를 보냈는데…….\""]], [["Pravdin was a well-known Panslavist abroad, and Countess Lidia Ivanovna described the purport of his letter.", "프라브딘은 국외에서 잘 알려진 범슬라브주의자였고, 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인은 그의 편지 요지를 설명했다."]], [["Then the countess told her of more disagreements and intrigues against the work of the unification of the churches, and departed in haste, as she had that day to be at the meeting of some society and also at the Slavonic committee.", "그런 다음 백작 부인은 교회 통합 사업에 반대하는 더 많은 불화와 암투에 대해 이야기하고는, 그날 어느 단체 모임에도 가야 하고 또 슬라브 위원회에도 가야 한다며 황급히 떠났다."]], [["“It was all the same before, of course; but why was it I didn’t notice it before?”", "\"물론 전에도 다 마찬가지였지. 그런데 왜 나는 전에는 이걸 알아채지 못했을까?\""], ["Anna asked herself.", "안나는 스스로에게 물었다."], ["“Or has she been very much irritated today?", "\"아니면 오늘 그 사람이 무척 신경이 곤두서 있었던 걸까? 정말 우스운 일이야."], ["It’s really ludicrous; her object is doing good; she a Christian, yet she’s always angry; and she always has enemies, and always enemies in the name of Christianity and doing good.”", "저 사람의 목적은 선을 행하는 거고, 기독교도이면서도 늘 화를 내고 있어. 게다가 늘 적이 있는데, 그것도 언제나 기독교와 선행의 이름으로 만들어진 적이지.\""]], [["After Countess Lidia Ivanovna another friend came, the wife of a chief secretary, who told her all the news of the town.", "리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인이 다녀간 뒤에는 또 다른 친구, 어느 수석 비서관의 아내가 와서 시내의 온갖 소식을 전해 주었다."], ["At three o’clock she too went away, promising to come to dinner.", "세 시에 그녀도 만찬에 오겠다고 약속하며 떠났다."], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch was at the ministry.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 관청에 있었다."], ["Anna, left alone, spent the time till dinner in assisting at her son’s dinner (he dined apart from his parents) and in putting her things in order, and in reading and answering the notes and letters which had accumulated on her table.", "혼자 남은 안나는 만찬 때까지의 시간을 아들의 식사를 거드는 데(아이는 부모와 따로 식사를 했다), 그리고 자기 물건들을 정리하고, 책상 위에 쌓여 있던 쪽지며 편지들을 읽고 답하는 데 보냈다."]], [["The feeling of causeless shame, which she had felt on the journey, and her excitement, too, had completely vanished.", "여행 중에 느꼈던 까닭 모를 부끄러움도, 그 흥분도 이제 완전히 사라지고 없었다."], ["In the habitual conditions of her life she felt again resolute and irreproachable.", "익숙한 삶의 조건 속에서 그녀는 다시금 단호하고 떳떳한 자신을 느꼈다."]], [["She recalled with wonder her state of mind on the previous day.", "그녀는 어제 자신의 마음 상태를 의아하게 떠올렸다. \"그게 뭐였지?"], ["“What was it? Nothing.", "아무것도 아니었어."], ["Vronsky said something silly, which it was easy to put a stop to, and I answered as I ought to have done.", "브론스키가 어리석은 말을 했고, 그건 쉽게 그만두게 할 수 있는 거였고, 나는 마땅히 해야 할 대로 대답했지."], ["To speak of it to my husband would be unnecessary and out of the question.", "그 일을 남편한테 이야기한다는 건 불필요하고 말도 안 되는 짓이야."], ["To speak of it would be to attach importance to what has no importance.”", "그 일을 이야기하는 건 아무 의미도 없는 일에 의미를 부여하는 셈이 될 테니까.\""], ["She remembered how she had told her husband of what was almost a declaration made her at Petersburg by a young man, one of her husband’s subordinates, and how Alexey Alexandrovitch had answered that every woman living in the world was exposed to such incidents, but that he had the fullest confidence in her tact, and could never lower her and himself by jealousy.", "그녀는 페테르부르크에서 남편의 부하 직원 가운데 하나인 한 젊은이가 거의 고백이나 다름없는 말을 했던 일을 남편에게 이야기한 적이 있다는 것, 그리고 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 사교계에 사는 여자라면 누구나 그런 일을 겪게 마련이지만 자신은 그녀의 분별력을 전적으로 믿으며 결코 질투 때문에 그녀와 자신을 깎아내릴 수는 없노라고 대답했던 일을 떠올렸다."], ["“So then there’s no reason to speak of it?", "\"그러니 그 일을 이야기할 까닭이 없지 않은가?"], ["And indeed, thank God, there’s nothing to speak of,”", "게다가 정말이지, 하느님 감사하게도, 이야기할 거리가 아무것도 없어.\""], ["she told herself.", "그녀는 스스로에게 말했다."]], [["# Chapter 33", "# Chapter 33"]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch came back from the meeting of the ministers at four o’clock, but as often happened, he had not time to come in to her.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 네 시에 장관 회의에서 돌아왔지만, 흔히 그렇듯 아내에게 들를 틈이 없었다."], ["He went into his study to see the people waiting for him with petitions, and to sign some papers brought him by his chief secretary.", "그는 청원서를 들고 자기를 기다리는 사람들을 만나고, 수석 비서가 가져온 서류 몇 장에 서명하기 위해 곧장 서재로 들어갔다."], ["At dinner time (there were always a few people dining with the Karenins) there arrived an old lady, a cousin of Alexey Alexandrovitch, the chief secretary of the department and his wife, and a young man who had been recommended to Alexey Alexandrovitch for the service.", "만찬 시각(카레닌가에는 늘 몇몇 손님이 함께 식사하곤 했다)이 되자 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 사촌 노부인과 부서의 수석 비서 부부, 그리고 관직에 들이라고 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에게 추천받은 한 젊은이가 찾아왔다."], ["Anna went into the drawing-room to receive these guests.", "안나는 이 손님들을 맞이하러 응접실로 나갔다."], ["Precisely at five o’clock, before the bronze Peter the First clock had struck the fifth stroke, Alexey Alexandrovitch came in, wearing a white tie and evening coat with two stars, as he had to go out directly after dinner.", "정확히 다섯 시, 표트르 대제 모양의 청동 시계가 다섯 번째 종을 치기 전에 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 흰 넥타이를 매고 별 두 개가 달린 연미복 차림으로 들어왔다. 만찬이 끝나는 대로 곧장 나가야 했기 때문이다."], ["Every minute of Alexey Alexandrovitch’s life was portioned out and occupied.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 삶은 매 순간이 나뉘어 빈틈없이 채워져 있었다."], ["And to make time to get through all that lay before him every day, he adhered to the strictest punctuality.", "그리고 날마다 자기 앞에 놓인 일을 다 해내기 위한 시간을 만들어 내려고, 그는 더없이 엄격한 시간 엄수를 고수했다."], ["“Unhasting and unresting,” was his motto.", "\"서두르지 않되 쉬지도 않는다\"가 그의 좌우명이었다."], ["He came into the dining hall, greeted everyone, and hurriedly sat down, smiling to his wife.", "그는 식당으로 들어와 모두에게 인사를 건넨 뒤, 아내에게 미소를 지으며 서둘러 자리에 앉았다."]], [["“Yes, my solitude is over.", "\"그래, 내 외로운 신세도 끝났군."], ["You wouldn’t believe how uncomfortable”", "혼자 식사하는 게 얼마나 불편한지 당신은 믿지 못할 거요.\""], ["(he laid stress on the word uncomfortable) “it is to dine alone.”", "(그는 '불편하다'는 말에 힘을 주었다.)"]], [["At dinner he talked a little to his wife about Moscow matters, and, with a sarcastic smile, asked her after Stepan Arkadyevitch; but the conversation was for the most part general, dealing with Petersburg official and public news.", "만찬 자리에서 그는 아내와 모스크바 일에 관해 잠깐 이야기를 나누고, 비꼬는 듯한 미소를 지으며 스테판 아르카지치의 안부를 물었다. 그러나 대화는 대체로 일반적인 것이었고, 페테르부르크의 관청과 사교계 소식에 관한 것이었다."], ["After dinner he spent half an hour with his guests, and again, with a smile, pressed his wife’s hand, withdrew, and drove off to the council.", "만찬이 끝난 뒤 그는 손님들과 삼십 분쯤 함께 시간을 보낸 다음, 다시 미소를 지으며 아내의 손을 꼭 쥐고는 자리에서 물러나 마차를 타고 회의장으로 떠났다."], ["Anna did not go out that evening either to the Princess Betsy Tverskaya, who, hearing of her return, had invited her, nor to the theater, where she had a box for that evening.", "안나는 그날 저녁 그녀가 돌아왔다는 소식을 듣고 초대해 준 벳시 트베르스카야 공작부인에게도, 또 그날 저녁 좌석을 잡아 둔 극장에도 가지 않았다."], ["She did not go out principally because the dress she had reckoned upon was not ready.", "그녀가 외출하지 않은 것은 무엇보다 입으려고 했던 옷이 준비되지 않았기 때문이었다."], ["Altogether, Anna, on turning, after the departure of her guests, to the consideration of her attire, was very much annoyed.", "손님들이 떠난 뒤 옷차림을 살펴보게 된 안나는 몹시 화가 났다."], ["She was generally a mistress of the art of dressing well without great expense, and before leaving Moscow she had given her dressmaker three dresses to transform.", "그녀는 대체로 큰돈을 들이지 않고도 옷을 잘 차려입는 솜씨를 지니고 있었고, 모스크바를 떠나기 전에 양재사에게 드레스 세 벌을 고쳐 달라고 맡겨 둔 터였다."], ["The dresses had to be altered so that they could not be recognized, and they ought to have been ready three days before.", "드레스는 알아볼 수 없을 만큼 손을 봐야 했고, 사흘 전에는 완성되어 있어야 마땅했다."], ["It appeared that two dresses had not been done at all, while the other one had not been altered as Anna had intended.", "그런데 알고 보니 두 벌은 아예 손도 대지 않았고, 나머지 한 벌도 안나가 원하던 대로 고쳐지지 않았다."], ["The dressmaker came to explain, declaring that it would be better as she had done it, and Anna was so furious that she felt ashamed when she thought of it afterwards.", "양재사가 와서 변명하며 자기가 한 대로가 더 낫다고 우겨 댔고, 안나는 어찌나 화가 났던지 나중에 그 일을 떠올리면 부끄러울 정도였다."], ["To regain her serenity completely she went into the nursery, and spent the whole evening with her son, put him to bed herself, signed him with the cross, and tucked him up.", "마음의 평온을 온전히 되찾기 위해 그녀는 아이 방으로 가서 저녁 내내 아들과 함께 시간을 보냈고, 직접 아이를 재우며 성호를 그어 주고 이불을 덮어 주었다."], ["She was glad she had not gone out anywhere, and had spent the evening so well.", "그녀는 어디에도 나가지 않고 저녁을 이렇게 잘 보낸 것이 기뻤다."], ["She felt so light-hearted and serene, she saw so clearly that all that had seemed to her so important on her railway journey was only one of the common trivial incidents of fashionable life, and that she had no reason to feel ashamed before anyone else or before herself.", "마음이 어찌나 가볍고 평온하던지, 기차 여행 중에 그토록 중요하게 여겨졌던 일들이 사실은 사교계 생활의 흔하고 사소한 사건 가운데 하나일 뿐이며, 다른 누구 앞에서도 또 자기 자신 앞에서도 부끄러워할 이유가 없다는 것을 또렷이 알 수 있었다."], ["Anna sat down at the hearth with an English novel and waited for her husband.", "안나는 영국 소설책을 들고 난롯가에 앉아 남편을 기다렸다."], ["Exactly at half-past nine she heard his ring, and he came into the room.", "정확히 아홉 시 반에 그녀는 그가 초인종 누르는 소리를 들었고, 그가 방으로 들어왔다."]], [["“Here you are at last!”", "\"이제야 오셨네요!\""], ["she observed, holding out her hand to him.", "그녀는 그에게 손을 내밀며 말했다."]], [["He kissed her hand and sat down beside her.", "그는 아내의 손에 입을 맞추고 그녀 곁에 앉았다."]], [["“Altogether then, I see your visit was a success,”", "\"그래, 결국 당신 방문이 성공적이었던 모양이군.\""], ["he said to her.", "그가 그녀에게 말했다."]], [["“Oh, yes,”", "\"네, 그래요.\""], ["she said, and she began telling him about everything from the beginning: her journey with Countess Vronskaya, her arrival, the accident at the station.", "그녀는 이렇게 대답하고는 처음부터 모든 일을 그에게 이야기하기 시작했다. 브론스카야 백작부인과 함께한 여행이며, 도착한 일이며, 역에서 있었던 사고까지."], ["Then she described the pity she had felt, first for her brother, and afterwards for Dolly.", "그러고 나서 그녀는 처음에는 오라버니에 대해, 그다음에는 돌리에 대해 느꼈던 연민을 묘사했다."]], [["“I imagine one cannot exonerate such a man from blame, though he is your brother,”", "\"그 사람이 당신 오라버니라고는 하지만, 그런 남자를 비난에서 면해 줄 수는 없을 것 같소.\""], ["said Alexey Alexandrovitch severely.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 엄하게 말했다."]], [["Anna smiled.", "안나는 미소를 지었다."], ["She knew that he said that simply to show that family considerations could not prevent him from expressing his genuine opinion.", "그녀는 그가 그 말을 한 것이 단지 가족이라는 사정도 자기 진심을 표명하는 것을 막을 수 없음을 보여 주기 위해서임을 알고 있었다."], ["She knew that characteristic in her husband, and liked it.", "그녀는 남편의 그런 특성을 알고 있었고, 또 그것을 좋아했다."]], [["“I am glad it has all ended so satisfactorily, and that you are back again,”", "\"모든 일이 그렇게 무난하게 끝나고, 당신이 다시 돌아와서 기쁘오.\""], ["he went on.", "그가 말을 이었다."], ["“Come, what do they say about the new act I have got passed in the council?”", "\"그래, 내가 회의에서 통과시킨 새 법안에 대해 사람들이 뭐라고 하던가요?\""]], [["Anna had heard nothing of this act, and she felt conscience-stricken at having been able so readily to forget what was to him of such importance.", "안나는 이 법안에 대해 아무것도 들은 바가 없었고, 그에게 그토록 중요한 일을 이렇게 쉽게 잊어버릴 수 있었다는 것에 양심의 가책을 느꼈다."]], [["“Here, on the other hand, it has made a great sensation,”", "\"여기서는 반대로 그 일이 큰 반향을 일으켰소.\""], ["he said, with a complacent smile.", "그가 흡족한 미소를 지으며 말했다."]], [["She saw that Alexey Alexandrovitch wanted to tell her something pleasant to him about it, and she brought him by questions to telling it.", "안나는 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 그 일에 관해 자기에게 흐뭇한 무언가를 말하고 싶어 한다는 것을 알아챘고, 질문을 던져 그가 그 이야기를 하도록 이끌었다."], ["With the same complacent smile he told her of the ovations he had received in consequence of the act he had passed.", "그는 여전히 흡족한 미소를 지으며 자기가 통과시킨 법안 덕분에 받은 갈채에 대해 그녀에게 이야기했다."]], [["“I was very, very glad.", "\"나는 정말, 정말 기뻤소."], ["It shows that at last a reasonable and steady view of the matter is becoming prevalent among us.”", "마침내 이 문제에 대한 이성적이고 확고한 견해가 우리 사이에 널리 퍼지고 있다는 걸 보여 주는 거니까.\""]], [["Having drunk his second cup of tea with cream, and bread, Alexey Alexandrovitch got up, and was going towards his study.", "크림을 넣은 차 두 잔과 빵을 다 들고 나서, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 자리에서 일어나 서재 쪽으로 가려 했다."]], [["“And you’ve not been anywhere this evening?", "\"그런데 당신은 오늘 저녁 아무 데도 안 갔소?"], ["You’ve been dull, I expect?”", "따분했겠구려?\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“Oh, no!”", "\"아, 아니에요!\""], ["she answered, getting up after him and accompanying him across the room to his study.", "그녀는 그를 따라 일어나 방을 가로질러 서재까지 그를 바래다주며 대답했다."], ["“What are you reading now?”", "\"지금은 무얼 읽고 계세요?\""], ["she asked.", "그녀가 물었다."]], [["“Just now I’m reading Duc de Lille, Poésie des Enfers,”", "\"지금은 뒤크 드 릴의 『Poésie des Enfers』를 읽고 있소.\""], ["he answered.", "그가 대답했다."], ["“A very remarkable book.”", "\"아주 주목할 만한 책이지.\""]], [["Anna smiled, as people smile at the weaknesses of those they love, and, putting her hand under his, she escorted him to the door of the study.", "안나는 사람들이 사랑하는 이의 약점을 보며 짓는 그런 미소를 지으며 그의 팔 밑으로 손을 넣어 서재 문까지 그를 바래다주었다."], ["She knew his habit, that had grown into a necessity, of reading in the evening.", "그녀는 저녁마다 책을 읽는, 이제는 필수가 되어 버린 그의 습관을 알고 있었다."], ["She knew, too, that in spite of his official duties, which swallowed up almost the whole of his time, he considered it his duty to keep up with everything of note that appeared in the intellectual world.", "또한 그녀는 그가 거의 모든 시간을 삼켜 버리는 공무에도 불구하고 지성계에 나타나는 주목할 만한 것이라면 무엇이든 따라잡는 것을 자신의 의무로 여긴다는 것도 알고 있었다."], ["She knew, too, that he was really interested in books dealing with politics, philosophy, and theology, that art was utterly foreign to his nature; but, in spite of this, or rather, in consequence of it, Alexey Alexandrovitch never passed over anything in the world of art, but made it his duty to read everything.", "그녀는 또한 그가 정치와 철학, 신학을 다루는 책에는 진심으로 관심이 있지만 예술은 그의 천성과는 전혀 동떨어진 것임을 알고 있었다. 그런데도, 아니 오히려 그렇기 때문에 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 예술계에서 일어나는 어떤 것도 그냥 지나치는 법이 없었고, 모든 것을 읽는 것을 자신의 의무로 삼았다."], ["She knew that in politics, in philosophy, in theology, Alexey Alexandrovitch often had doubts, and made investigations; but on questions of art and poetry, and, above all, of music, of which he was totally devoid of understanding, he had the most distinct and decided opinions.", "그녀는 정치와 철학, 신학에 관해서는 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 종종 의심을 품고 탐구를 했지만, 예술과 시, 그리고 무엇보다 그가 도무지 이해하지 못하는 음악에 관해서는 더없이 분명하고 단호한 견해를 지니고 있다는 것을 알고 있었다."], ["He was fond of talking about Shakespeare, Raphael, Beethoven, of the significance of new schools of poetry and music, all of which were classified by him with very conspicuous consistency.", "그는 셰익스피어와 라파엘로, 베토벤에 대해, 시와 음악의 새로운 유파가 지니는 의의에 대해 이야기하기를 좋아했는데, 그 모든 것은 그에 의해 매우 두드러진 일관성을 가지고 분류되어 있었다."]], [["“Well, God be with you,”", "\"그럼, 잘 다녀오세요.\""], ["she said at the door of the study, where a shaded candle and a decanter of water were already put by his armchair.", "그녀는 서재 문 앞에서 말했는데, 그곳에는 이미 갓을 씌운 촛불과 물병이 그의 안락의자 옆에 놓여 있었다."], ["“And I’ll write to Moscow.”", "\"나는 모스크바로 편지를 쓸게요.\""]], [["He pressed her hand, and again kissed it.", "그는 아내의 손을 꼭 쥐고는 다시 입을 맞추었다."]], [["“All the same he’s a good man; truthful, good-hearted, and remarkable in his own line,”", "'그래도 저이는 좋은 사람이야. 진실하고, 마음씨가 곱고, 자기 분야에서는 비범하지.'"], ["Anna said to herself going back to her room, as though she were defending him to someone who had attacked him and said that one could not love him.", "안나는 자기 방으로 돌아가며, 마치 그를 비난하면서 그를 사랑할 수 없다고 말한 누군가에게 그를 변호하기라도 하듯 속으로 되뇌었다."], ["“But why is it his ears stick out so strangely?", "'그런데 왜 저이는 귀가 저렇게 이상하게 튀어나와 있을까?"], ["Or has he had his hair cut?”", "아니면 머리를 깎은 건가?'"]], [["Precisely at twelve o’clock, when Anna was still sitting at her writing-table, finishing a letter to Dolly, she heard the sound of measured steps in slippers, and Alexey Alexandrovitch, freshly washed and combed, with a book under his arm, came in to her.", "정확히 열두 시, 안나가 아직 책상에 앉아 돌리에게 쓰는 편지를 마무리하고 있을 때, 그녀는 슬리퍼를 끌며 일정하게 다가오는 발소리를 들었고, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 막 씻고 머리를 빗은 채 책 한 권을 옆구리에 끼고 그녀에게로 들어왔다."]], [["“It’s time, it’s time,”", "\"이제 잘 시간이오, 잘 시간이야.\""], ["said he, with a meaning smile, and he went into their bedroom.", "그가 의미심장한 미소를 지으며 말하고는 침실로 들어갔다."]], [["“And what right had he to look at him like that?”", "'그런데 저 사람한테 무슨 권리가 있어서 그이를 그런 눈으로 본 거지?'"], ["thought Anna, recalling Vronsky’s glance at Alexey Alexandrovitch.", "안나는 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치를 향했던 브론스키의 시선을 떠올리며 생각했다."]], [["Undressing, she went into the bedroom; but her face had none of the eagerness which, during her stay in Moscow, had fairly flashed from her eyes and her smile; on the contrary, now the fire seemed quenched in her, hidden somewhere far away.", "옷을 벗고 그녀는 침실로 들어갔다. 그러나 그녀의 얼굴에는 모스크바에 머무는 동안 두 눈과 미소에서 그토록 환히 빛나던 그 생기가 조금도 남아 있지 않았다. 오히려 지금은 그녀 안의 불이 꺼져 버린 듯, 어딘가 저 멀리에 숨어 버린 듯했다."]], [["# Chapter 34", "# Chapter 34"]], [["When Vronsky went to Moscow from Petersburg, he had left his large set of rooms in Morskaia to his friend and favorite comrade Petritsky.", "브론스키는 페테르부르크에서 모스크바로 떠나면서 모르스카야 거리에 있는 자신의 넓은 셋방을 친구이자 가장 아끼는 동료인 페트리츠키에게 맡겨 두었다."]], [["Petritsky was a young lieutenant, not particularly well-connected, and not merely not wealthy, but always hopelessly in debt.", "페트리츠키는 젊은 중위로, 특별히 좋은 연줄이 있는 것도 아니었고, 부유하기는커녕 늘 가망 없이 빚에 쪼들리는 신세였다."], ["Towards evening he was always drunk, and he had often been locked up after all sorts of ludicrous and disgraceful scandals, but he was a favorite both of his comrades and his superior officers.", "저녁이면 으레 술에 취해 있었고, 온갖 우스꽝스럽고 망신스러운 추문 끝에 영창에 갇히기 일쑤였지만, 그래도 동료들에게나 상관들에게나 사랑받는 인물이었다."], ["On arriving at twelve o’clock from the station at his flat, Vronsky saw, at the outer door, a hired carriage familiar to him.", "정거장에서 열두 시에 자기 셋방에 도착한 브론스키는 바깥문 앞에 낯익은 삯마차 한 대가 세워져 있는 것을 보았다."], ["While still outside his own door, as he rang, he heard masculine laughter, the lisp of a feminine voice, and Petritsky’s voice.", "아직 자기 집 문밖에서 초인종을 누르는 동안, 그는 남자의 웃음소리와 혀짤배기 같은 여자 목소리, 그리고 페트리츠키의 목소리를 들었다."], ["“If that’s one of the villains, don’t let him in!”", "\"그게 그 악당들 중 하나거든, 들이지 마!\""], ["Vronsky told the servant not to announce him, and slipped quietly into the first room.", "브론스키는 하인에게 자기가 왔다는 것을 알리지 말라고 이르고는, 첫 번째 방으로 조용히 들어섰다."], ["Baroness Shilton, a friend of Petritsky’s, with a rosy little face and flaxen hair, resplendent in a lilac satin gown, and filling the whole room, like a canary, with her Parisian chatter, sat at the round table making coffee.", "페트리츠키의 친구인 실톤 남작부인이, 발그레한 작은 얼굴에 아마빛 머리칼을 하고, 연보랏빛 새틴 가운을 눈부시게 차려입은 채, 카나리아처럼 파리 사투리의 수다로 방 안을 가득 채우며, 둥근 탁자에 앉아 커피를 끓이고 있었다."], ["Petritsky, in his overcoat, and the cavalry captain Kamerovsky, in full uniform, probably just come from duty, were sitting each side of her.", "외투를 걸친 페트리츠키와, 정복 차림으로 아마도 막 근무를 마치고 온 듯한 기병 대위 카메롭스키가 그녀의 양옆에 앉아 있었다."]], [["“Bravo!", "\"브라보!"], ["Vronsky!”", "브론스키!\""], ["shouted Petritsky, jumping up, scraping his chair.", "페트리츠키가 의자를 드르륵 끌며 벌떡 일어나 외쳤다."], ["“Our host himself!", "\"집주인이 몸소 납셨군!"], ["Baroness, some coffee for him out of the new coffee pot.", "남작부인, 새 커피포트로 끓인 커피 좀 따라 주십시오."], ["Why, we didn’t expect you!", "아니, 자네가 올 줄은 몰랐는데!"], ["Hope you’re satisfied with the ornament of your study,”", "자네 서재의 장식품이 마음에 드시길 바라네.\""], ["he said, indicating the baroness.", "그는 남작부인을 가리키며 말했다."], ["“You know each other, of course?”", "\"물론 서로 아는 사이지?\""]], [["“I should think so,”", "\"그럼, 알다마다.\""], ["said Vronsky, with a bright smile, pressing the baroness’s little hand.", "브론스키가 환한 미소를 지으며 남작부인의 작은 손을 잡고 말했다."], ["“What next!", "\"무슨 소리야!"], ["I’m an old friend.”", "우리는 오랜 친구인데.\""]], [["“You’re home after a journey,”", "\"여행에서 막 돌아오셨군요.\""], ["said the baroness, “so I’m flying.", "남작부인이 말했다. \"그럼 저는 날아가야겠어요."], ["Oh, I’ll be off this minute, if I’m in the way.”", "아, 제가 방해가 된다면 당장 떠나겠어요.\""]], [["“You’re home, wherever you are, baroness,”", "\"부인은 어디에 계시든 그곳이 집이지요, 남작부인.\""], ["said Vronsky.", "브론스키가 말했다."], ["“How do you do, Kamerovsky?”", "\"잘 지냈나, 카메롭스키?\""], ["he added, coldly shaking hands with Kamerovsky.", "그가 카메롭스키와 차갑게 악수를 나누며 덧붙였다."]], [["“There, you never know how to say such pretty things,”", "\"보세요, 당신은 절대로 저렇게 멋들어진 말을 할 줄 모르잖아요.\""], ["said the baroness, turning to Petritsky.", "남작부인이 페트리츠키를 돌아보며 말했다."]], [["“No; what’s that for?", "\"무슨 소리, 그게 무슨 소용이람?"], ["After dinner I say things quite as good.”", "식사 후라면 나도 저만큼 좋은 말을 한다고.\""]], [["“After dinner there’s no credit in them?", "\"식사 후엔 그런 말에 무슨 값어치가 있겠어요?"], ["Well, then, I’ll make you some coffee, so go and wash and get ready,”", "자, 그럼 제가 커피를 끓여 드릴 테니, 가서 씻고 채비나 하세요.\""], ["said the baroness, sitting down again, and anxiously turning the screw in the new coffee pot.", "남작부인이 다시 자리에 앉으며 새 커피포트의 나사를 조심스럽게 돌리며 말했다."], ["“Pierre, give me the coffee,”", "\"피에르, 커피 좀 줘요.\""], ["she said, addressing Petritsky, whom she called Pierre as a contraction of his surname, making no secret of her relations with him.", "그녀는 페트리츠키에게 말을 건넸는데, 그의 성을 줄여 피에르라고 불렀으며, 그와의 관계를 조금도 숨기지 않았다."], ["“I’ll put it in.”", "\"제가 넣을게요.\""]], [["“You’ll spoil it!”", "\"망쳐 놓을 거면서!\""]], [["“No, I won’t spoil it!", "\"아니, 안 망쳐요!"], ["Well, and your wife?”", "그런데, 부인은요?\""], ["said the baroness suddenly, interrupting Vronsky’s conversation with his comrade.", "남작부인이 갑자기 브론스키와 동료의 대화를 가로막으며 말했다."], ["“We’ve been marrying you here.", "\"여기서 우리가 당신을 장가보내 놓았거든요."], ["Have you brought your wife?”", "부인을 데려오셨나요?\""]], [["“No, baroness.", "\"아닙니다, 남작부인."], ["I was born a Bohemian, and a Bohemian I shall die.”", "저는 보헤미안으로 태어났으니, 보헤미안으로 죽을 겁니다.\""]], [["“So much the better, so much the better.", "\"그게 훨씬 낫지요, 훨씬 나아요."], ["Shake hands on it.”", "그 약속으로 악수해요.\""]], [["And the baroness, detaining Vronsky, began telling him, with many jokes, about her last new plans of life, asking his advice.", "그러고 나서 남작부인은 브론스키를 붙들어 두고, 농담을 잔뜩 섞어 가며 자신의 새로운 인생 계획을 늘어놓기 시작했고, 그에게 조언을 구했다."]], [["“He persists in refusing to give me a divorce!", "\"그 사람이 한사코 이혼을 안 해 주려는 거예요!"], ["Well, what am I to do?”", "자, 제가 어쩌면 좋겠어요?\""], ["(He was her husband.) “Now I want to begin a suit against him.", "(그 사람이란 그녀의 남편이었다.) \"이제 그 사람을 상대로 소송을 걸려고 해요."], ["What do you advise?", "어떻게 하라고 충고해 주시겠어요?"], ["Kamerovsky, look after the coffee; it’s boiling over.", "카메롭스키, 커피 좀 봐줘요, 넘치고 있잖아요."], ["You see, I’m engrossed with business!", "보세요, 저는 사업에 푹 빠져 있다니까요!"], ["I want a lawsuit, because I must have my property.", "소송이 필요한 건, 제 재산을 꼭 찾아야 하기 때문이에요."], ["Do you understand the folly of it, that on the pretext of my being unfaithful to him,”", "그 어처구니없는 짓 좀 이해해 보세요, 제가 자기를 배신했다는 핑계로,\""], ["she said contemptuously, “he wants to get the benefit of my fortune.”", "그녀가 경멸하듯 말했다. \"그 사람은 제 재산을 가로채려 한다니까요.\""]], [["Vronsky heard with pleasure this light-hearted prattle of a pretty woman, agreed with her, gave her half-joking counsel, and altogether dropped at once into the tone habitual to him in talking to such women.", "브론스키는 어여쁜 여자의 이 가벼운 재잘거림을 즐겁게 들으며, 그녀의 말에 맞장구를 치고, 반쯤 농담 섞인 충고를 건넸으며, 이내 그런 부류의 여자들과 이야기할 때면 으레 짓던 그 말투에 완전히 빠져들었다."], ["In his Petersburg world all people were divided into utterly opposed classes.", "그가 속한 페테르부르크 사교계에서는 모든 사람이 서로 정반대되는 두 부류로 나뉘었다."], ["One, the lower class, vulgar, stupid, and, above all, ridiculous people, who believe that one husband ought to live with the one wife whom he has lawfully married; that a girl should be innocent, a woman modest, and a man manly, self-controlled, and strong; that one ought to bring up one’s children, earn one’s bread, and pay one’s debts; and various similar absurdities.", "하나는 천박하고 멍청하며, 무엇보다 우스꽝스러운 하층 부류로, 남편이란 모름지기 합법적으로 결혼한 한 명의 아내와 살아야 한다든가, 처녀는 순결해야 하고 여자는 정숙해야 하며 남자는 사내답고 자제력 있고 강건해야 한다든가, 자식을 길러 내고 제 밥벌이를 하고 빚을 갚아야 한다든가 하는 갖가지 비슷한 어리석은 소리를 믿는 사람들이었다."], ["This was the class of old-fashioned and ridiculous people.", "이것이 구식이고 우스꽝스러운 사람들의 부류였다."], ["But there was another class of people, the real people.", "그러나 또 다른 부류, 진짜 사람들의 부류가 있었다."], ["To this class they all belonged, and in it the great thing was to be elegant, generous, plucky, gay, to abandon oneself without a blush to every passion, and to laugh at everything else.", "그들은 모두 이 부류에 속했고, 거기서 중요한 것은 우아하고 너그럽고 대담하고 쾌활하며, 부끄러운 기색 하나 없이 온갖 정열에 몸을 내맡기고, 그 밖의 모든 것은 비웃어 버리는 일이었다."]], [["For the first moment only, Vronsky was startled after the impression of a quite different world that he had brought with him from Moscow.", "처음 한순간만큼은, 모스크바에서 가지고 온 전혀 다른 세계의 인상이 남아 있던 브론스키도 흠칫 놀랐다."], ["But immediately as though slipping his feet into old slippers, he dropped back into the light-hearted, pleasant world he had always lived in.", "그러나 곧, 낡은 슬리퍼에 발을 꿰어 넣듯이, 그는 늘 살아오던 그 가볍고 유쾌한 세계로 도로 미끄러져 들어갔다."]], [["The coffee was never really made, but spluttered over everyone, and boiled away, doing just what was required of it—that is, providing much cause for much noise and laughter, and spoiling a costly rug and the baroness’s gown.", "커피는 끝내 제대로 끓여지지 못하고 모두에게 튀어 끓어 넘쳐 버렸지만, 정확히 그것에게 요구되던 역할을 해냈다. 다시 말해 한바탕 떠들썩하게 웃고 떠들 거리를 잔뜩 제공하고, 값비싼 양탄자와 남작부인의 가운을 망쳐 놓았던 것이다."]], [["“Well now, good-bye, or you’ll never get washed, and I shall have on my conscience the worst sin a gentleman can commit.", "\"자, 그럼 안녕히, 안 그러면 당신은 영영 씻지도 못할 테고, 신사가 저지를 수 있는 가장 큰 죄가 제 양심에 얹히게 될 테니까요."], ["So you would advise a knife to his throat?”", "그러니까 그 사람 목에 칼을 들이대라고 충고하시는 거죠?\""]], [["“To be sure, and manage that your hand may not be far from his lips.", "\"물론이지요, 그리고 당신 손이 그 사람 입술에서 너무 멀리 있지 않도록 처신하세요."], ["He’ll kiss your hand, and all will end satisfactorily,”", "그 사람이 당신 손에 입을 맞출 테고, 그러면 모든 게 흡족하게 끝날 겁니다.\""], ["answered Vronsky.", "브론스키가 대답했다."]], [["“So at the Français!”", "\"그럼 프랑세 극장에서!\""], ["and, with a rustle of her skirts, she vanished.", "그러고는 치맛자락 스치는 소리와 함께 그녀는 사라졌다."]], [["Kamerovsky got up too, and Vronsky, not waiting for him to go, shook hands and went off to his dressing-room.", "카메롭스키도 일어났고, 브론스키는 그가 나가기를 기다리지 않고 악수를 한 뒤 화장실로 가 버렸다."]], [["While he was washing, Petritsky described to him in brief outlines his position, as far as it had changed since Vronsky had left Petersburg.", "브론스키가 씻는 동안, 페트리츠키는 브론스키가 페테르부르크를 떠난 뒤로 바뀐 자신의 처지를 대략적인 윤곽으로 들려주었다."], ["No money at all.", "돈은 한 푼도 없었다."], ["His father said he wouldn’t give him any and pay his debts.", "아버지는 한 푼도 주지 않을 것이며 빚도 갚아 주지 않겠다고 했다."], ["His tailor was trying to get him locked up, and another fellow, too, was threatening to get him locked up.", "재단사는 그를 영창에 처넣으려 하고, 다른 녀석도 마찬가지로 그를 가둬 버리겠다고 으름장을 놓고 있었다."], ["The colonel of the regiment had announced that if these scandals did not cease he would have to leave.", "연대장은 이런 추문이 그치지 않으면 그가 군을 떠나야 할 거라고 통고했다."], ["As for the baroness, he was sick to death of her, especially since she’d taken to offering continually to lend him money.", "남작부인으로 말하면, 특히 그녀가 끊임없이 돈을 빌려주겠다고 들이대기 시작한 뒤로는 정나미가 떨어질 만큼 지긋지긋했다."], ["But he had found a girl—he’d show her to Vronsky—a marvel, exquisite, in the strict Oriental style, “genre of the slave Rebecca, don’t you know.”", "그러나 그는 한 여자를 찾아냈는데—브론스키에게 보여 주겠다고 했다—기막힌 미인이요, 더없이 빼어나며, 엄격한 동방풍에다, \"이를테면 노예 레베카 같은 분위기지, 자네도 알 거 아닌가.\""], ["He’d had a row, too, with Berkoshov, and was going to send seconds to him, but of course it would come to nothing.", "그는 또 베르코쇼프와도 다툼이 있어, 그에게 결투 입회인을 보낼 작정이었지만, 물론 아무 일도 없이 끝날 것이었다."], ["Altogether everything was supremely amusing and jolly.", "한마디로 모든 것이 더할 나위 없이 재미있고 유쾌했다."], ["And, not letting his comrade enter into further details of his position, Petritsky proceeded to tell him all the interesting news.", "그러고는 페트리츠키는 자기 처지에 관한 시시콜콜한 사정으로 동료를 더는 끌어들이지 않고, 곧장 온갖 흥미로운 소식을 들려주기 시작했다."], ["As he listened to Petritsky’s familiar stories in the familiar setting of the rooms he had spent the last three years in, Vronsky felt a delightful sense of coming back to the careless Petersburg life that he was used to.", "지난 삼 년을 보낸 그 낯익은 방의 낯익은 분위기 속에서 페트리츠키의 익숙한 이야기들을 들으면서, 브론스키는 자기가 늘 몸담아 온 그 태평스러운 페테르부르크 생활로 되돌아온다는 즐거운 감각을 느꼈다."]], [["“Impossible!”", "\"설마!\""], ["he cried, letting down the pedal of the washing basin in which he had been sousing his healthy red neck.", "그는 건강하고 불그레한 목덜미를 적시던 세면기의 페달을 내려놓으며 소리쳤다."], ["“Impossible!”", "\"설마!\""], ["he cried, at the news that Laura had flung over Fertinghof and had made up to Mileev.", "그는 라우라가 페르팅호프를 차 버리고 밀레예프와 눈이 맞았다는 소식에 외쳤다."], ["“And is he as stupid and pleased as ever?", "\"그래, 그 친구는 여전히 멍청하면서도 좋다고 헤벌쭉거리나?"], ["Well, and how’s Buzulukov?”", "그건 그렇고, 부줄루코프는 어떻게 지내?\""]], [["“Oh, there is a tale about Buzulukov—simply lovely!”", "\"오, 부줄루코프에 관해선 정말 끝내주는 이야기가 있지!\""], ["cried Petritsky.", "페트리츠키가 외쳤다."], ["“You know his weakness for balls, and he never misses a single court ball.", "\"그 친구가 무도회라면 사족을 못 쓰는 거 알잖아, 궁정 무도회는 한 번도 빠지는 법이 없거든."], ["He went to a big ball in a new helmet.", "그가 새 투구를 쓰고 큰 무도회에 갔단 말이야."], ["Have you seen the new helmets?", "그 새 투구 본 적 있나?"], ["Very nice, lighter.", "아주 멋지고, 더 가벼워졌어."], ["Well, so he’s standing....", "자, 그래서 그가 서 있는데……"], ["No, I say, do listen.”", "아니, 이봐, 잘 들어 봐.\""]], [["“I am listening,”", "\"듣고 있어.\""], ["answered Vronsky, rubbing himself with a rough towel.", "브론스키가 거친 수건으로 몸을 문지르며 대답했다."]], [["“Up comes the Grand Duchess with some ambassador or other, and, as ill-luck would have it, she begins talking to him about the new helmets.", "\"대공비께서 어느 대사인지 하는 사람과 함께 다가오시는데, 하필이면 운 나쁘게도 그 새 투구 이야기를 그 친구한테 꺼내신 거야."], ["The Grand Duchess positively wanted to show the new helmet to the ambassador.", "대공비께서는 기어이 그 새 투구를 대사에게 보여 주고 싶으셨던 거지."], ["They see our friend standing there.”", "마침 우리 친구가 거기 서 있는 게 보였단 말이야.\""], ["(Petritsky mimicked how he was standing with the helmet.) “The Grand Duchess asked him to give her the helmet; he doesn’t give it to her.", "(페트리츠키는 그가 투구를 들고 서 있던 모습을 흉내 냈다.) \"대공비께서 그 친구한테 투구를 달라고 청하셨는데, 이 친구가 안 드리는 거야."], ["What do you think of that?", "그게 말이 돼?"], ["Well, everyone’s winking at him, nodding, frowning—give it to her, do!", "자, 다들 그 친구한테 눈짓을 하고, 고갯짓을 하고, 눈살을 찌푸리는 거지—어서 드려, 어서!"], ["He doesn’t give it to her.", "그래도 안 드려."], ["He’s mute as a fish.", "벙어리처럼 입을 꾹 다물고 있어."], ["Only picture it!...", "한번 상상해 봐!……"], ["Well, the ...", "그래서, 그……"], ["what’s his name, whatever he was ...", "이름이 뭐더라, 아무튼 그 사람이……"], ["tries to take the helmet from him ...", "그 친구한테서 투구를 빼앗으려고 하는데……"], ["he won’t give it up!...", "안 내놓는 거야!……"], ["He pulls it from him, and hands it to the Grand Duchess.", "그가 억지로 잡아채서 대공비께 바쳤지. '여기, 전하,'"], ["‘Here, your Highness,’ says he, ‘is the new helmet.’ She turned the helmet the other side up, And—just picture it!—plop went a pear and sweetmeats out of it, two pounds of sweetmeats!...", "하고 그가 말했어, '새 투구이옵니다.' 대공비께서 투구를 거꾸로 뒤집으셨는데, 그러자—한번 상상해 봐!—투구에서 배 한 알과 사탕이, 그것도 사탕이 무려 두 파운드나 와르르 쏟아진 거야!……"], ["He’d been storing them up, the darling!”", "이 귀여운 녀석이 거기다 잔뜩 쟁여 두었던 거지!\""]], [["Vronsky burst into roars of laughter.", "브론스키는 와르르 웃음을 터뜨렸다."], ["And long afterwards, when he was talking of other things, he broke out into his healthy laugh, showing his strong, close rows of teeth, when he thought of the helmet.", "그리고 한참 뒤에 다른 이야기를 하다가도, 그 투구가 떠오를 때면 튼튼하고 촘촘한 잇줄을 드러내며 건강한 웃음을 터뜨리곤 했다."]], [["Having heard all the news, Vronsky, with the assistance of his valet, got into his uniform, and went off to report himself.", "소식을 모두 듣고 난 뒤, 브론스키는 시종의 도움을 받아 정복으로 갈아입고 신고를 하러 나섰다."], ["He intended, when he had done that, to drive to his brother’s and to Betsy’s and to pay several visits with a view to beginning to go into that society where he might meet Madame Karenina.", "그 일을 마치면 형의 집과 벳시의 집에 들르고, 카레니나 부인을 만날 수 있을 만한 그 사교계에 발을 들여놓기 위해 몇 군데 방문할 작정이었다."], ["As he always did in Petersburg, he left home not meaning to return till late at night.", "페테르부르크에서 늘 그랬듯이, 그는 밤늦도록 돌아오지 않을 생각으로 집을 나섰다."]], [["# PART TWO Chapter 1", "# PART TWO Chapter 1"]], [["At the end of the winter, in the Shtcherbatskys’ house, a consultation was being held, which was to pronounce on the state of Kitty’s health and the measures to be taken to restore her failing strength.", "겨울이 끝나갈 무렵, 셰르바츠키가에서는 키티의 건강 상태와 쇠약해진 기력을 회복시키기 위해 어떤 조치를 취해야 할지 진단하는 진료 상담이 열렸다."], ["She had been ill, and as spring came on she grew worse.", "키티는 병을 앓고 있었는데, 봄이 다가오면서 상태가 더 나빠졌다."], ["The family doctor gave her cod liver oil, then iron, then nitrate of silver, but as the first and the second and the third were alike in doing no good, and as his advice when spring came was to go abroad, a celebrated physician was called in.", "가족 주치의는 그녀에게 처음에는 간유를, 다음에는 철분제를, 그다음에는 질산은을 주었다. 그러나 첫 번째 것도, 두 번째 것도, 세 번째 것도 한결같이 아무 효험이 없었고, 봄이 오자 그가 외국으로 가라고 조언했으므로, 어느 저명한 의사가 불려 왔다."], ["The celebrated physician, a very handsome man, still youngish, asked to examine the patient.", "그 저명한 의사는 매우 잘생긴, 아직 젊은 축에 드는 남자로, 환자를 진찰하게 해 달라고 청했다."], ["He maintained, with peculiar satisfaction, it seemed, that maiden modesty is a mere relic of barbarism, and that nothing could be more natural than for a man still youngish to handle a young girl naked.", "그는 처녀의 수줍음이란 야만의 잔재에 불과하며, 아직 젊은 축에 드는 남자가 발가벗은 처녀를 다루는 것보다 더 자연스러운 일은 없다고, 보아하니 묘하게 흡족해하며 주장했다."], ["He thought it natural because he did it every day, and felt and thought, as it seemed to him, no harm as he did it and consequently he considered modesty in the girl not merely as a relic of barbarism, but also as an insult to himself.", "그는 매일 그런 일을 하기 때문에 그것을 자연스럽다고 여겼고, 그렇게 하면서도 자기 생각으로는 아무런 잘못된 점을 느끼지도 생각하지도 않았으며, 따라서 처녀의 수줍음을 단지 야만의 잔재로뿐만 아니라 자기 자신에 대한 모욕으로까지 여겼다."]], [["There was nothing for it but to submit, since, although all the doctors had studied in the same school, had read the same books, and learned the same science, and though some people said this celebrated doctor was a bad doctor, in the princess’s household and circle it was for some reason accepted that this celebrated doctor alone had some special knowledge, and that he alone could save Kitty.", "따를 수밖에 다른 도리가 없었다. 모든 의사가 같은 학교에서 공부하고, 같은 책을 읽고, 같은 학문을 익혔으며, 더구나 이 저명한 의사가 형편없는 의사라고 말하는 사람들도 있었지만, 공작 부인의 집안과 그 모임에서는 어쩐 이유에선지 이 저명한 의사만이 어떤 특별한 지식을 갖추고 있으며, 오직 그만이 키티를 구할 수 있다고 받아들여졌던 것이다."], ["After a careful examination and sounding of the bewildered patient, dazed with shame, the celebrated doctor, having scrupulously washed his hands, was standing in the drawing-room talking to the prince.", "수치심에 멍해진 채 어쩔 줄 모르는 환자를 꼼꼼히 진찰하고 청진한 뒤, 그 저명한 의사는 손을 정성껏 씻고 나서 응접실에 서서 공작과 이야기를 나누었다."], ["The prince frowned and coughed, listening to the doctor.", "공작은 의사의 말을 들으며 미간을 찌푸리고 헛기침을 했다."], ["As a man who had seen something of life, and neither a fool nor an invalid, he had no faith in medicine, and in his heart was furious at the whole farce, specially as he was perhaps the only one who fully comprehended the cause of Kitty’s illness.", "세상 물정을 어느 정도 겪었고, 바보도 환자도 아닌 사람으로서 그는 의술을 믿지 않았으며, 마음속으로는 이 모든 익살극에 화가 치밀어 올랐다. 게다가 어쩌면 그만이 키티가 앓는 병의 원인을 온전히 헤아리고 있었기 때문에 더욱 그랬다."], ["“Conceited blockhead!”", "\"건방진 멍청이 같으니!\""], ["he thought, as he listened to the celebrated doctor’s chatter about his daughter’s symptoms.", "그는 자기 딸의 증세를 두고 떠벌리는 저명한 의사의 수다를 들으며 생각했다."], ["The doctor was meantime with difficulty restraining the expression of his contempt for this old gentleman, and with difficulty condescending to the level of his intelligence.", "한편 의사는 이 늙은 양반에 대한 경멸의 빛을 간신히 억누르며, 그의 지능 수준에 맞추어 자세를 낮추느라 애를 먹었다."], ["He perceived that it was no good talking to the old man, and that the principal person in the house was the mother.", "그는 이 노인에게 이야기해 봤자 소용없으며, 이 집안의 중심인물은 어머니라는 것을 알아차렸다."], ["Before her he decided to scatter his pearls.", "그는 그녀 앞에 자기의 진주를 뿌리기로 마음먹었다."], ["At that instant the princess came into the drawing-room with the family doctor.", "바로 그 순간 공작 부인이 가족 주치의와 함께 응접실로 들어왔다."], ["The prince withdrew, trying not to show how ridiculous he thought the whole performance.", "공작은 이 모든 연극을 얼마나 우스꽝스럽게 여기는지 내색하지 않으려 애쓰며 자리를 떴다."], ["The princess was distracted, and did not know what to do.", "공작 부인은 정신이 산란하여 어찌할 바를 몰랐다."], ["She felt she had sinned against Kitty.", "그녀는 자기가 키티에게 죄를 지었다고 느꼈다."]], [["“Well, doctor, decide our fate,”", "\"자, 선생님, 우리 운명을 결정해 주세요.\""], ["said the princess.", "공작 부인이 말했다."], ["“Tell me everything.”", "\"다 말씀해 주세요.\""]], [["“Is there hope?”", "\"희망이 있나요?\""], ["she meant to say, but her lips quivered, and she could not utter the question.", "그녀는 이렇게 묻고 싶었지만, 입술이 떨려 그 질문을 입 밖에 낼 수가 없었다."], ["“Well, doctor?”", "\"어떤가요, 선생님?\""]], [["“Immediately, princess.", "\"곧 말씀드리겠습니다, 부인."], ["I will talk it over with my colleague, and then I will have the honor of laying my opinion before you.”", "제 동료와 상의한 뒤에 제 소견을 부인께 말씀드리는 영광을 누리겠습니다.\""]], [["“So we had better leave you?” “As you please.”", "\"그럼 저희가 자리를 비켜 드리는 게 좋겠지요?\""]], [["The princess went out with a sigh.", "\"좋으실 대로 하십시오.\""]], [["When the doctors were left alone, the family doctor began timidly explaining his opinion, that there was a commencement of tuberculous trouble, but ...", "공작 부인은 한숨을 쉬며 나갔다. 의사들만 남게 되자, 가족 주치의가 머뭇거리며 자기 소견을 설명하기 시작했다."], ["and so on.", "결핵성 질환의 초기가 시작된 것 같지만……"], ["The celebrated doctor listened to him, and in the middle of his sentence looked at his big gold watch.", "하는 식이었다. 저명한 의사는 그의 말을 듣다가, 문장 한가운데서 자기의 커다란 금시계를 들여다보았다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그렇군요.\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["“But....”", "\"하지만…….\""]], [["The family doctor respectfully ceased in the middle of his observations.", "가족 주치의는 자기 소견을 늘어놓다 말고 공손히 입을 다물었다."]], [["“The commencement of the tuberculous process we are not, as you are aware, able to define; till there are cavities, there is nothing definite.", "\"결핵성 진행의 초기는, 아시다시피 우리가 규정할 수 없습니다. 공동이 생기기 전에는 확실한 것이 아무것도 없으니까요."], ["But we may suspect it.", "하지만 의심해 볼 수는 있지요."], ["And there are indications; malnutrition, nervous excitability, and so on.", "그리고 징후들이 있습니다. 영양 부족, 신경의 흥분, 그 밖의 것들 말입니다."], ["The question stands thus: in presence of indications of tuberculous process, what is to be done to maintain nutrition?”", "문제는 이렇게 됩니다. 결핵성 진행의 징후가 있는 상황에서, 영양을 유지하기 위해 무엇을 해야 하는가, 하는 것이지요.\""]], [["“But, you know, there are always moral, spiritual causes at the back in these cases,”", "\"하지만 아시다시피, 이런 경우에는 늘 그 배후에 정신적, 심적 원인이 있게 마련이지요.\""], ["the family doctor permitted himself to interpolate with a subtle smile.", "가족 주치의가 묘한 미소를 띠며 한마디 끼워 넣었다."]], [["“Yes, that’s an understood thing,”", "\"그렇지요, 그건 다 아는 사실입니다.\""], ["responded the celebrated physician, again glancing at his watch.", "저명한 의사가 다시 시계를 흘끗 보며 대꾸했다."], ["“Beg pardon, is the Yausky bridge done yet, or shall I have to drive around?”", "\"실례지만, 야우스키 다리는 이제 다 됐나요, 아니면 빙 둘러 가야 합니까?\""], ["he asked. “Ah!", "그가 물었다."], ["it is.", "\"아!"], ["Oh, well, then I can do it in twenty minutes.", "됐군요. 오, 그렇다면 이십 분이면 갈 수 있겠습니다."], ["So we were saying the problem may be put thus: to maintain nutrition and to give tone to the nerves.", "그래서, 우리가 이야기하던 문제는 이렇게 정리할 수 있겠지요. 영양을 유지하고 신경에 활기를 주는 것 말입니다."], ["The one is in close connection with the other, one must attack both sides at once.”", "이 둘은 서로 긴밀히 연관되어 있으니, 양쪽을 동시에 공략해야 합니다.\""]], [["“And how about a tour abroad?”", "\"그럼 외국 여행은 어떻습니까?\""], ["asked the family doctor.", "가족 주치의가 물었다."]], [["“I’ve no liking for foreign tours.", "\"저는 외국 여행을 좋아하지 않습니다."], ["And take note: if there is an early stage of tuberculous process, of which we cannot be certain, a foreign tour will be of no use.", "그리고 유념하십시오. 만약 결핵성 진행의 초기 단계라면, 그건 우리가 확신할 수 없는 것이지만, 외국 여행은 아무 소용이 없을 겁니다."], ["What is wanted is means of improving nutrition, and not for lowering it.”", "필요한 것은 영양을 개선하는 수단이지, 떨어뜨리는 것이 아니니까요.\""], ["And the celebrated doctor expounded his plan of treatment with Soden waters, a remedy obviously prescribed primarily on the ground that they could do no harm.", "그러고 나서 저명한 의사는 소덴 광천수를 이용한 치료 계획을 설명했는데, 그것은 분명 무엇보다도 해를 끼칠 일이 없다는 근거에서 처방된 요법이었다."]], [["The family doctor listened attentively and respectfully.", "가족 주치의는 주의 깊게, 그리고 공손하게 귀를 기울였다."]], [["“But in favor of foreign travel I would urge the change of habits, the removal from conditions calling up reminiscences.", "\"하지만 외국 여행을 지지하는 입장에서, 저는 생활 습관의 변화와, 옛 기억을 불러일으키는 환경에서 벗어나는 것을 강조하고 싶습니다."], ["And then the mother wishes it,”", "게다가 어머니께서 그것을 바라고 계시고요.\""], ["he added.", "그가 덧붙였다."]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["Well, in that case, to be sure, let them go.", "음, 그렇다면 물론 보내십시오."], ["Only, those German quacks are mischievous....", "다만, 그 독일 돌팔이들이 해롭단 말입니다……"], ["They ought to be persuaded....", "잘 설득해야 할 텐데……"], ["Well, let them go then.”", "뭐, 그럼 보내시지요.\""]], [["He glanced once more at his watch.", "그는 다시 한번 시계를 흘끗 보았다."]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["time’s up already,”", "벌써 시간이 다 됐군요.\""], ["And he went to the door.", "그러고는 문 쪽으로 갔다."], ["The celebrated doctor announced to the princess (a feeling of what was due from him dictated his doing so) that he ought to see the patient once more.", "저명한 의사는 공작 부인에게 환자를 한 번 더 봐야겠다고 알렸다(자기가 마땅히 해야 할 도리라는 느낌이 그렇게 하도록 시킨 것이었다)."]], [["“What!", "\"네?"], ["another examination!”", "또 진찰을 한다고요!\""], ["cried the mother, with horror.", "어머니가 질겁하며 소리쳤다."]], [["“Oh, no, only a few details, princess.”", "\"오, 아닙니다, 그저 몇 가지 세부 사항일 뿐입니다, 부인.\""]], [["“Come this way.”", "\"이쪽으로 오세요.\""]], [["And the mother, accompanied by the doctor, went into the drawing-room to Kitty.", "그리고 어머니는 의사를 데리고 키티가 있는 응접실로 들어갔다."], ["Wasted and flushed, with a peculiar glitter in her eyes, left there by the agony of shame she had been put through, Kitty stood in the middle of the room.", "야위고 얼굴이 달아오른 키티는, 겪어야 했던 수치의 고통이 남겨 놓은 묘한 광채를 두 눈에 띤 채 방 한가운데 서 있었다."], ["When the doctor came in she flushed crimson, and her eyes filled with tears.", "의사가 들어오자 그녀는 얼굴이 새빨개졌고, 두 눈에 눈물이 그렁그렁 고였다."], ["All her illness and treatment struck her as a thing so stupid, ludicrous even!", "자기의 병이며 치료가 모두 너무나 어리석은, 심지어 우스꽝스러운 일로만 여겨졌다!"], ["Doctoring her seemed to her as absurd as putting together the pieces of a broken vase.", "자기를 치료한다는 것이, 깨진 꽃병 조각들을 도로 맞추는 것만큼이나 터무니없게 느껴졌다."], ["Her heart was broken.", "그녀의 마음은 깨져 버렸다."], ["Why would they try to cure her with pills and powders?", "그런데 어째서 알약이며 가루약으로 그녀를 고치려 드는 것일까?"], ["But she could not grieve her mother, especially as her mother considered herself to blame.", "하지만 어머니를 슬프게 할 수는 없었다. 더구나 어머니가 자기 탓이라고 여기고 있었으니 더욱 그랬다."]], [["“May I trouble you to sit down, princess?”", "\"앉으시라고 청해도 될까요, 아가씨?\""], ["the celebrated doctor said to her.", "저명한 의사가 그녀에게 말했다."]], [["He sat down with a smile, facing her, felt her pulse, and again began asking her tiresome questions.", "그는 미소를 지으며 그녀를 마주 보고 앉아, 맥을 짚고는, 다시 따분한 질문들을 던지기 시작했다."], ["She answered him, and all at once got up, furious.", "그녀는 대답하다가, 별안간 화가 치밀어 벌떡 일어섰다."]], [["“Excuse me, doctor, but there is really no object in this.", "\"죄송하지만 선생님, 이건 정말이지 아무 의미가 없어요."], ["This is the third time you’ve asked me the same thing.”", "똑같은 걸 물으시는 게 벌써 세 번째예요.\""]], [["The celebrated doctor did not take offense.", "저명한 의사는 기분 나빠하지 않았다."]], [["“Nervous irritability,”", "\"신경의 과민이로군요.\""], ["he said to the princess, when Kitty had left the room.", "키티가 방에서 나가자 그가 공작 부인에게 말했다."], ["“However, I had finished....”", "\"어쨌든, 저는 끝냈습니다…….\""]], [["And the doctor began scientifically explaining to the princess, as an exceptionally intelligent woman, the condition of the young princess, and concluded by insisting on the drinking of the waters, which were certainly harmless.", "그러고 나서 의사는 공작 부인을 유난히 총명한 부인으로 대하며 젊은 아가씨의 상태를 학문적으로 설명하기 시작했고, 분명 해롭지 않은 광천수를 마실 것을 강조하는 것으로 끝맺었다."], ["At the question: Should they go abroad? the doctor plunged into deep meditation, as though resolving a weighty problem.", "외국으로 보내야 하느냐는 질문에, 의사는 무슨 중대한 문제라도 풀듯이 깊은 명상에 잠겼다."], ["Finally his decision was pronounced: they were to go abroad, but to put no faith in foreign quacks, and to apply to him in any need.", "마침내 그의 결정이 내려졌다. 외국으로 보내되, 외국 돌팔이들은 믿지 말고, 무슨 일이 생기면 자기에게 문의하라는 것이었다."]], [["It seemed as though some piece of good fortune had come to pass after the doctor had gone.", "의사가 떠난 뒤에는 마치 무슨 좋은 일이라도 일어난 것 같았다."], ["The mother was much more cheerful when she went back to her daughter, and Kitty pretended to be more cheerful.", "어머니는 딸에게 돌아가면서 훨씬 명랑해졌고, 키티도 더 명랑한 척했다."], ["She had often, almost always, to be pretending now.", "이제 그녀는 종종, 거의 늘 무언가를 꾸며 내야 했다."]], [["“Really, I’m quite well, mamma.", "\"정말이지 저는 아주 멀쩡해요, 엄마."], ["But if you want to go abroad, let’s go!”", "하지만 외국에 가고 싶으시면, 가요!\""], ["she said, and trying to appear interested in the proposed tour, she began talking of the preparations for the journey.", "그녀는 이렇게 말하고는, 예정된 여행에 관심이 있는 척하려 애쓰며 여행 준비에 대해 이야기하기 시작했다."]], [["# Chapter 2", "# Chapter 2"]], [["Soon after the doctor, Dolly had arrived.", "의사가 다녀간 직후 돌리가 와 있었다."], ["She knew that there was to be a consultation that day, and though she was only just up after her confinement (she had another baby, a little girl, born at the end of the winter), though she had trouble and anxiety enough of her own, she had left her tiny baby and a sick child, to come and hear Kitty’s fate, which was to be decided that day.", "그녀는 그날 진찰이 있으리란 걸 알고 있었기에, 비록 해산한 지 얼마 되지 않아 이제 막 자리에서 일어났음에도(겨울 끝 무렵에 또 아기를, 딸아이를 낳았다), 그리고 자기 나름의 근심과 걱정이 차고 넘쳤음에도, 갓난아기와 병든 아이를 두고서 그날 결정될 키티의 운명을 들으러 온 것이었다."]], [["“Well, well?”", "\"그래, 어떻게 됐어?\""], ["she said, coming into the drawing-room, without taking off her hat.", "그녀는 모자도 벗지 않은 채 응접실로 들어서며 말했다."], ["“You’re all in good spirits.", "\"다들 기분이 좋아 보이네."], ["Good news, then?”", "그럼 좋은 소식이야?\""]], [["They tried to tell her what the doctor had said, but it appeared that though the doctor had talked distinctly enough and at great length, it was utterly impossible to report what he had said.", "다들 의사가 한 말을 그녀에게 전하려 했지만, 의사가 충분히 또렷하게, 그것도 아주 길게 이야기했음에도 그가 한 말을 옮긴다는 것은 전혀 불가능한 일임이 드러났다."], ["The only point of interest was that it was settled they should go abroad.", "관심을 끄는 단 하나의 사실은, 외국으로 떠나기로 정해졌다는 것뿐이었다."]], [["Dolly could not help sighing.", "돌리는 한숨을 내쉬지 않을 수 없었다."], ["Her dearest friend, her sister, was going away.", "더없이 사랑하는 친구이자 동생이 멀리 떠나려 하고 있었다."], ["And her life was not a cheerful one.", "게다가 그녀 자신의 삶도 즐겁다고는 할 수 없었다."], ["Her relations with Stepan Arkadyevitch after their reconciliation had become humiliating.", "화해한 뒤 스테판 아르카지치와의 관계는 굴욕적인 것이 되어 있었다."], ["The union Anna had cemented turned out to be of no solid character, and family harmony was breaking down again at the same point.", "안나가 단단히 이어 붙여 준 결합은 끝내 견고하지 못한 것으로 드러났고, 가정의 화목은 같은 지점에서 다시금 무너져 가고 있었다."], ["There had been nothing definite, but Stepan Arkadyevitch was hardly ever at home; money, too, was hardly ever forthcoming, and Dolly was continually tortured by suspicions of infidelity, which she tried to dismiss, dreading the agonies of jealousy she had been through already.", "뚜렷하게 정해진 것은 없었지만, 스테판 아르카지치는 거의 집에 붙어 있지 않았고, 돈 또한 좀처럼 들어오지 않았으며, 돌리는 부정에 대한 의심으로 끊임없이 시달리면서도 이미 한 번 겪어 낸 질투의 고통이 두려워 그 의심을 떨쳐 버리려 애쓰고 있었다."], ["The first onslaught of jealousy, once lived through, could never come back again, and even the discovery of infidelities could never now affect her as it had the first time.", "한 번 살아 낸 질투의 첫 습격은 두 번 다시 되돌아올 수 없는 것이어서, 이제는 부정을 알아챈다 해도 처음처럼 그녀를 뒤흔들 수는 없었다."], ["Such a discovery now would only mean breaking up family habits, and she let herself be deceived, despising him and still more herself, for the weakness.", "지금 그런 사실을 알게 된들 그것은 가정의 일상을 깨뜨리는 일에 지나지 않을 터였기에, 그녀는 자기가 속아 넘어가는 것을 그대로 내버려 두었고, 그를, 그리고 그 나약함 때문에 자기 자신을 더욱더 경멸했다."], ["Besides this, the care of her large family was a constant worry to her: first, the nursing of her young baby did not go well, then the nurse had gone away, now one of the children had fallen ill.", "이것 말고도 대식구를 건사하는 일이 그녀에게는 끊임없는 걱정거리였다. 처음엔 갓난아기에게 젖을 물리는 일이 순조롭지 않았고, 그다음엔 유모가 나가 버렸으며, 이제는 아이들 가운데 하나가 병이 들었다."]], [["“Well, how are all of you?”", "\"그래, 너희들은 다 어떻게 지내니?\""], ["asked her mother.", "어머니가 물었다."]], [["“Ah, mamma, we have plenty of troubles of our own.", "\"아, 엄마, 우리도 우리대로 걱정이 한가득이에요."], ["Lili is ill, and I’m afraid it’s scarlatina.", "릴리가 아픈데, 성홍열일까 봐 겁이 나요."], ["I have come here now to hear about Kitty, and then I shall shut myself up entirely, if—God forbid—it should be scarlatina.”", "지금은 키티 일을 들으러 온 거지만, 만약—제발 그런 일은 없어야 할 텐데—성홍열이면 저는 아예 집에 틀어박혀 있어야 할 거예요.\""]], [["The old prince too had come in from his study after the doctor’s departure, and after presenting his cheek to Dolly, and saying a few words to her, he turned to his wife:", "의사가 떠난 뒤 늙은 공작도 서재에서 나왔는데, 돌리에게 뺨을 내밀어 입맞춤을 받고 몇 마디 건넨 뒤 아내 쪽으로 돌아섰다."]], [["“How have you settled it?", "\"어떻게 정했소?"], ["you’re going?", "떠나는 거요?"], ["Well, and what do you mean to do with me?”", "그럼 나는 어쩌라는 거지?\""]], [["“I suppose you had better stay here, Alexander,”", "\"당신은 여기 남아 있는 게 좋을 거예요, 알렉산드르.\""], ["said his wife.", "아내가 말했다."]], [["“That’s as you like.”", "\"당신 좋을 대로 하구려.\""]], [["“Mamma, why shouldn’t father come with us?”", "\"엄마, 아버지도 우리랑 같이 가면 안 돼요?\""], ["said Kitty.", "키티가 말했다."], ["“It would be nicer for him and for us too.”", "\"아버지한테도, 우리한테도 그게 더 좋을 텐데요.\""]], [["The old prince got up and stroked Kitty’s hair.", "늙은 공작이 일어나 키티의 머리를 쓰다듬었다."], ["She lifted her head and looked at him with a forced smile.", "그녀는 고개를 들어 억지 미소를 지으며 그를 바라보았다."], ["It always seemed to her that he understood her better than anyone in the family, though he did not say much about her.", "그녀에게는 늘 아버지가 식구들 중 누구보다도 자기를 잘 이해하는 것처럼 느껴졌다. 비록 그가 그녀에 대해 말을 많이 하지는 않았지만 말이다."], ["Being the youngest, she was her father’s favorite, and she fancied that his love gave him insight.", "막내인 그녀는 아버지가 가장 아끼는 딸이었고, 그 사랑이 아버지에게 통찰을 준다고 그녀는 믿었다."], ["When now her glance met his blue kindly eyes looking intently at her, it seemed to her that he saw right through her, and understood all that was not good that was passing within her.", "지금 그녀의 시선이 자기를 뚫어지게 바라보는 아버지의 다정한 파란 눈과 마주쳤을 때, 그녀에게는 아버지가 자기 속을 훤히 들여다보며 마음속에서 일어나는 좋지 못한 것들을 죄다 알아챈 것만 같았다."], ["Reddening, she stretched out towards him expecting a kiss, but he only patted her hair and said:", "얼굴을 붉히며 그녀는 입맞춤을 기대하고 아버지 쪽으로 몸을 내밀었지만, 그는 그저 그녀의 머리를 토닥이며 말했다."]], [["“These stupid chignons!", "\"이 멍청한 시뇽 같으니!"], ["There’s no getting at the real daughter.", "진짜 딸한테는 손이 닿지도 않는구나."], ["One simply strokes the bristles of dead women.", "그저 죽은 여자들 빳빳한 털이나 쓰다듬는 셈이지."], ["Well, Dolinka,”", "자, 돌린카,\""], ["he turned to his elder daughter, “what’s your young buck about, hey?”", "그는 큰딸 쪽으로 돌아섰다, \"네 그 젊은 한량은 요즘 뭘 하고 지내냐, 응?\""]], [["“Nothing, father,”", "\"아무것도요, 아버지.\""], ["answered Dolly, understanding that her husband was meant.", "남편을 두고 하는 말임을 알아챈 돌리가 대답했다."], ["“He’s always out; I scarcely ever see him,”", "\"늘 밖으로만 돌아서, 저는 얼굴 보기도 힘들어요.\""], ["she could not resist adding with a sarcastic smile.", "그녀는 비꼬는 미소를 띠며 덧붙이지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["“Why, hasn’t he gone into the country yet—to see about selling that forest?”", "\"아니, 그 사람 아직도 시골에 안 갔어—그 숲을 파는 일을 보러 말이다?\""]], [["“No, he’s still getting ready for the journey.”", "\"아뇨, 아직 떠날 채비만 하고 있어요.\""]], [["“Oh, that’s it!”", "\"오, 그렇구나!\""], ["said the prince.", "공작이 말했다."], ["“And so am I to be getting ready for a journey too?", "\"그럼 나도 떠날 채비를 해야 한다는 거로군?"], ["At your service,”", "분부대로 하지요.\""], ["he said to his wife, sitting down.", "그는 아내에게 이렇게 말하며 자리에 앉았다. \"그리고 내가 하나 말해 주마, 카탸,\""], ["“And I tell you what, Katia,”", "그는 작은딸을 향해 말을 이었다."], ["he went on to his younger daughter, “you must wake up one fine day and say to yourself: Why, I’m quite well, and merry, and going out again with father for an early morning walk in the frost.", "\"너는 어느 화창한 날 잠에서 깨어 혼잣말을 해야 한다. 어머나, 나는 아주 멀쩡하고 즐거우니, 다시 아버지랑 서리 내린 이른 아침에 산책을 나가야지."], ["Hey?”", "어때, 응?\""]], [["What her father said seemed simple enough, yet at these words Kitty became confused and overcome like a detected criminal.", "아버지가 한 말은 지극히 단순한 것 같았지만, 그 말에 키티는 들킨 죄인처럼 당황하며 어쩔 줄을 몰랐다."], ["“Yes, he sees it all, he understands it all, and in these words he’s telling me that though I’m ashamed, I must get over my shame.”", "'그래, 아버지는 다 알아, 다 이해하셔. 그리고 이 말로, 내가 부끄럽긴 해도 그 부끄러움을 이겨 내야 한다고 말씀하시는 거야.'"], ["She could not pluck up spirit to make any answer.", "그녀는 도무지 무어라 대답할 기운을 낼 수 없었다."], ["She tried to begin, and all at once burst into tears, and rushed out of the room.", "입을 떼려다가 별안간 울음을 터뜨리며 방에서 뛰쳐나가고 말았다."]], [["“See what comes of your jokes!”", "\"당신의 그 농담이 무슨 꼴을 만드는지 좀 봐요!\""], ["the princess pounced down on her husband.", "공작 부인이 남편에게 와락 달려들었다."], ["“You’re always....”", "\"당신은 늘……\""], ["she began a string of reproaches.", "그녀는 줄줄이 비난을 늘어놓기 시작했다."]], [["The prince listened to the princess’s scolding rather a long while without speaking, but his face was more and more frowning.", "공작은 한동안 말없이 공작 부인의 잔소리를 들었지만, 그의 얼굴은 점점 더 험하게 일그러졌다."]], [["“She’s so much to be pitied, poor child, so much to be pitied, and you don’t feel how it hurts her to hear the slightest reference to the cause of it.", "\"저 아이는 너무 가여워요, 불쌍한 것, 너무 가엾다고요. 당신은 그 일의 까닭을 조금만 입에 올려도 저 아이가 얼마나 마음 아파하는지 느끼지도 못하죠."], ["Ah!", "아!"], ["to be so mistaken in people!”", "사람을 그렇게 잘못 볼 수가 있다니!\""], ["said the princess, and by the change in her tone both Dolly and the prince knew she was speaking of Vronsky.", "공작 부인이 말했고, 그 말투가 달라진 것으로 보아 그녀가 브론스키를 두고 하는 말임을 돌리도 공작도 알아챘다."], ["“I don’t know why there aren’t laws against such base, dishonorable people.”", "\"어째서 그런 비열하고 파렴치한 인간들을 처벌하는 법이 없는지 모르겠어요.\""]], [["“Ah, I can’t bear to hear you!”", "\"아, 더는 못 듣겠소!\""], ["said the prince gloomily, getting up from his low chair, and seeming anxious to get away, yet stopping in the doorway.", "공작이 침울하게 말하며 낮은 의자에서 일어났는데, 어서 자리를 뜨고 싶어 하는 듯하면서도 문가에 멈춰 섰다. \"법은 있소, 부인."], ["“There are laws, madam, and since you’ve challenged me to it, I’ll tell you who’s to blame for it all: you and you, you and nobody else.", "그리고 당신이 나한테 따져 물으니, 누구 탓에 이 모든 일이 벌어졌는지 말해 주겠소. 당신이오, 바로 당신, 당신 말고는 아무도 없소."], ["Laws against such young gallants there have always been, and there still are!", "그런 젊은 난봉꾼들을 다스리는 법은 예전에도 늘 있었고, 지금도 있단 말이오!"], ["Yes, if there has been nothing that ought not to have been, old as I am, I’d have called him out to the barrier, the young dandy.", "그래, 만약 있어서는 안 될 일이 없었다면, 이 늙은 나이에도 나는 그 멋쟁이 애송이를 결투장으로 불러냈을 거요."], ["Yes, and now you physic her and call in these quacks.”", "그래, 그런데 당신은 저 아이한테 약이나 먹이고 이런 돌팔이들을 불러들이고 있단 말이지.\""]], [["The prince apparently had plenty more to say, but as soon as the princess heard his tone she subsided at once, and became penitent, as she always did on serious occasions.", "공작은 아직 할 말이 많은 듯했지만, 그 말투를 듣자마자 공작 부인은 곧장 누그러졌고, 심각한 상황이면 늘 그렇듯 뉘우치는 기색이 되었다."]], [["“Alexander, Alexander,”", "\"알렉산드르, 알렉산드르.\""], ["she whispered, moving to him and beginning to weep.", "그녀는 그에게 다가가 울먹이기 시작하며 속삭였다."]], [["As soon as she began to cry the prince too calmed down.", "그녀가 울기 시작하자 공작도 마음이 가라앉았다."], ["He went up to her.", "그는 그녀에게 다가갔다."]], [["“There, that’s enough, that’s enough!", "\"자, 됐소, 됐어!"], ["You’re wretched too, I know.", "당신도 비참하다는 거 알아."], ["It can’t be helped.", "어쩔 수 없는 일이지."], ["There’s no great harm done.", "큰 탈이 난 것도 아니고."], ["God is merciful ...", "하느님은 자비로우시니……"], ["thanks....”", "고맙소……\""], ["he said, not knowing what he was saying, as he responded to the tearful kiss of the princess that he felt on his hand.", "그는 자기 손에 와 닿는 공작 부인의 눈물 어린 입맞춤에 답하며, 자기가 무슨 말을 하는지도 모른 채 중얼거렸다."], ["And the prince went out of the room.", "그러고는 방을 나갔다."]], [["Before this, as soon as Kitty went out of the room in tears, Dolly, with her motherly, family instincts, had promptly perceived that here a woman’s work lay before her, and she prepared to do it.", "이에 앞서, 키티가 울며 방을 나서자마자 돌리는 어머니다운, 한 가정을 꾸리는 사람 특유의 본능으로 여기에 여자가 해야 할 일이 놓여 있음을 곧바로 알아챘고, 그 일을 할 채비를 했다."], ["She took off her hat, and, morally speaking, tucked up her sleeves and prepared for action.", "그녀는 모자를 벗고, 말하자면 소매를 걷어붙이며 행동에 나설 태세를 갖추었다."], ["While her mother was attacking her father, she tried to restrain her mother, so far as filial reverence would allow.", "어머니가 아버지를 몰아붙이는 동안에는 자식으로서 갖춰야 할 공경심이 허락하는 한도 안에서 어머니를 말리려 애썼다."], ["During the prince’s outburst she was silent; she felt ashamed for her mother, and tender towards her father for so quickly being kind again.", "공작이 격분하는 동안에는 잠자코 있었다. 어머니가 부끄러웠고, 그토록 빨리 다시 다정해진 아버지에게는 애틋함을 느꼈다."], ["But when her father left them she made ready for what was the chief thing needful—to go to Kitty and console her.", "그러나 아버지가 자리를 뜨자, 그녀는 무엇보다 꼭 필요한 일, 곧 키티에게 가서 위로해 주는 일을 할 채비를 했다."]], [["“I’d been meaning to tell you something for a long while, mamma: did you know that Levin meant to make Kitty an offer when he was here the last time?", "\"오래전부터 엄마한테 할 말이 있었어요, 엄마. 레빈이 지난번에 여기 왔을 때 키티한테 청혼할 마음이었던 거 알고 계셨어요?"], ["He told Stiva so.”", "그이가 스티바한테 그렇게 말했대요.\""]], [["“Well, what then?", "\"그래, 그래서?"], ["I don’t understand....”", "무슨 소린지 모르겠구나……\""]], [["“So did Kitty perhaps refuse him?...", "\"그래서 키티가 어쩌면 그이를 거절한 게 아닐까요?……"], ["She didn’t tell you so?”", "엄마한테 그런 얘기 안 했어요?\""]], [["“No, she has said nothing to me either of one or the other; she’s too proud.", "\"안 했어, 이 일도 저 일도 나한테는 아무 말이 없었어. 그 애는 자존심이 너무 세니까."], ["But I know it’s all on account of the other.”", "하지만 다 그 다른 사람 때문이라는 건 나도 알아.\""]], [["“Yes, but suppose she has refused Levin, and she wouldn’t have refused him if it hadn’t been for the other, I know.", "\"그래요, 그런데 만약 그 애가 레빈을 거절했고, 그 다른 사람만 아니었다면 거절하지 않았을 거라면요? 저는 그렇다는 걸 알아요."], ["And then, he has deceived her so horribly.”", "그런데 그 사람은 그 애를 그렇게 끔찍하게 속였잖아요.\""]], [["It was too terrible for the princess to think how she had sinned against her daughter, and she broke out angrily.", "자기가 딸에게 얼마나 잘못을 저질렀는지 떠올리는 것은 공작 부인으로서는 너무도 끔찍한 일이었기에, 그녀는 발끈 화를 냈다."]], [["“Oh, I really don’t understand!", "\"오, 난 정말 모르겠다!"], ["Nowadays they will all go their own way, and mothers haven’t a word to say in anything, and then....”", "요즘 애들은 죄다 제멋대로 굴고, 어미는 무슨 일에도 한마디 거들 수가 없으니, 그러다……\""]], [["“Mamma, I’ll go up to her.”", "\"엄마, 제가 그 애한테 올라가 볼게요.\""]], [["“Well, do.", "\"그래, 그러렴."], ["Did I tell you not to?”", "내가 가지 말라고 했니?\""], ["said her mother.", "어머니가 말했다."]], [["# Chapter 3", "# Chapter 3"]], [["When she went into Kitty’s little room, a pretty, pink little room, full of knick-knacks in vieux saxe, as fresh, and pink, and white, and gay as Kitty herself had been two months ago, Dolly remembered how they had decorated the room the year before together, with what love and gaiety.", "키티의 작은 방, 예쁘고 분홍빛이 도는 그 작은 방으로 들어섰을 때—vieux saxe 자기 장식품들로 가득하고, 두 달 전의 키티 자신이 그러했듯이 싱그럽고 분홍빛에 하얗고 발랄한 그 방으로—돌리는 작년에 둘이서 얼마나 큰 애정과 즐거움으로 이 방을 함께 꾸몄던가를 떠올렸다."], ["Her heart turned cold when she saw Kitty sitting on a low chair near the door, her eyes fixed immovably on a corner of the rug.", "문가의 낮은 의자에 앉아 양탄자 한 귀퉁이에 시선을 못 박은 채 꼼짝도 않고 있는 키티를 보았을 때, 돌리의 가슴은 서늘하게 식어 버렸다."], ["Kitty glanced at her sister, and the cold, rather ill-tempered expression of her face did not change.", "키티는 언니를 흘끗 보았지만, 차갑고 다소 심술궂은 그 얼굴 표정은 변하지 않았다. * vieux saxe: 프랑스어로 '옛 작센', 곧 독일 마이센에서 만든 고급 자기를 가리킨다."]], [["“I’m just going now, and I shall have to keep in and you won’t be able to come to see me,”", "\"난 이제 곧 가야 하는데, 그러고 나면 집에만 있어야 해서 너도 날 보러 못 올 거야.\""], ["said Dolly, sitting down beside her.", "돌리가 그녀 곁에 앉으며 말했다."], ["“I want to talk to you.”", "\"너랑 이야기를 좀 하고 싶구나.\""]], [["“What about?”", "\"무슨 이야기요?\""], ["Kitty asked swiftly, lifting her head in dismay.", "키티가 놀란 듯 고개를 들며 재빨리 물었다."]], [["“What should it be, but your trouble?”", "\"네 일 말고 또 뭐가 있겠니?\""]], [["“I have no trouble.”", "\"전 아무 일 없어요.\""]], [["“Nonsense, Kitty.", "\"무슨 소리니, 키티."], ["Do you suppose I could help knowing?", "내가 모르고 있을 거라고 생각하니?"], ["I know all about it.", "난 다 알아."], ["And believe me, it’s of so little consequence....", "그리고 정말이지, 그건 별것도 아니란다……"], ["We’ve all been through it.”", "우리 모두 다 겪은 일이야.\""]], [["Kitty did not speak, and her face had a stern expression.", "키티는 입을 열지 않았고, 그 얼굴에는 굳은 표정이 어려 있었다."]], [["“He’s not worth your grieving over him,”", "\"그 사람은 네가 슬퍼할 만한 가치도 없는 사람이야.\""], ["pursued Darya Alexandrovna, coming straight to the point.", "다리야 알렉산드로브나가 곧장 본론으로 들어가며 말을 이었다."]], [["“No, because he has treated me with contempt,”", "\"아니요, 그 사람이 절 멸시했으니까요.\""], ["said Kitty, in a breaking voice.", "키티가 갈라지는 목소리로 말했다."], ["“Don’t talk of it!", "\"그 얘기는 하지 마세요!"], ["Please, don’t talk of it!”", "제발, 그 얘기는 하지 마세요!\""]], [["“But who can have told you so?", "\"하지만 대체 누가 너한테 그런 말을 할 수 있었겠니?"], ["No one has said that.", "아무도 그런 말 안 했어."], ["I’m certain he was in love with you, and would still be in love with you, if it hadn’t....”", "난 확신해, 그 사람은 널 사랑했고, 만약…… 그런 일만 없었다면 지금도 널 사랑하고 있을 거야……\""]], [["“Oh, the most awful thing of all for me is this sympathizing!”", "\"아, 저한테는 그 무엇보다 끔찍한 게 바로 이런 동정이에요!\""], ["shrieked Kitty, suddenly flying into a passion.", "키티가 갑자기 격분하며 날카롭게 외쳤다."], ["She turned round on her chair, flushed crimson, and rapidly moving her fingers, pinched the clasp of her belt first with one hand and then with the other.", "그녀는 의자 위에서 홱 돌아앉았고, 새빨갛게 얼굴을 붉힌 채 손가락을 재빠르게 움직여 허리띠 죔쇠를 한 손으로 쥐었다가 또 다른 손으로 쥐었다 했다."], ["Dolly knew this trick her sister had of clenching her hands when she was much excited; she knew, too, that in moments of excitement Kitty was capable of forgetting herself and saying a great deal too much, and Dolly would have soothed her, but it was too late.", "돌리는 동생이 몹시 흥분하면 두 손을 꽉 쥐는 이런 버릇이 있다는 것을 알고 있었다. 또한 흥분한 순간이면 키티가 자제력을 잃고 지나치게 많은 말을 쏟아 내곤 한다는 것도 알고 있었기에, 돌리는 그녀를 달래 주고 싶었지만, 이미 너무 늦어 버렸다."]], [["“What, what is it you want to make me feel, eh?”", "\"대체, 언니는 저한테 무얼 느끼게 하고 싶은 거예요, 네?\""], ["said Kitty quickly.", "키티가 빠르게 말했다."], ["“That I’ve been in love with a man who didn’t care a straw for me, and that I’m dying of love for him?", "\"제가 저 따위는 눈곱만큼도 신경 쓰지 않는 남자를 사랑했고, 그 사람을 사랑한 나머지 죽어 가고 있다는 거?"], ["And this is said to me by my own sister, who imagines that ...", "게다가 이런 말을 다름 아닌 제 친언니가 하다니, 그래 놓고 자기가……"], ["that ...", "자기가……"], ["that she’s sympathizing with me!...", "자기가 절 동정한다고 생각하고 있다니!……"], ["I don’t want these condolences and humbug!”", "전 그런 위로니 위선이니 하는 건 바라지 않아요!\""]], [["“Kitty, you’re unjust.”", "\"키티, 너 너무하는구나.\""]], [["“Why are you tormenting me?”", "\"왜 절 괴롭히는 거예요?\""]], [["“But I ...", "\"하지만 난……"], ["quite the contrary ...", "정반대인데……"], ["I see you’re unhappy....”", "네가 불행해하는 게 보여서……\""]], [["But Kitty in her fury did not hear her.", "그러나 격분한 키티에게는 언니의 말이 들리지 않았다."]], [["“I’ve nothing to grieve over and be comforted about.", "\"전 슬퍼할 것도, 위로받을 것도 없어요."], ["I am too proud ever to allow myself to care for a man who does not love me.”", "전 절 사랑하지도 않는 남자한테 마음을 두는 걸 스스로에게 허락할 만큼 그렇게 자존심 없는 사람이 아니에요.\""]], [["“Yes, I don’t say so either....", "\"그래, 나도 그렇다고는 안 해……"], ["Only one thing.", "다만 한 가지만."], ["Tell me the truth,”", "솔직하게 말해 주렴.\""], ["said Darya Alexandrovna, taking her by the hand: “tell me, did Levin speak to you?...”", "다리야 알렉산드로브나가 그녀의 손을 잡으며 말했다. \"말해 봐, 레빈이 너한테 말했니?……\""]], [["The mention of Levin’s name seemed to deprive Kitty of the last vestige of self-control.", "레빈의 이름이 입에 오르자 키티에게 남아 있던 마지막 자제력마저 사라져 버린 듯했다."], ["She leaped up from her chair, and flinging her clasp on the ground, she gesticulated rapidly with her hands and said:", "그녀는 의자에서 벌떡 일어나 죔쇠를 바닥에 내동댕이치더니, 두 손을 빠르게 휘저으며 말했다."]], [["“Why bring Levin in too?", "\"왜 레빈까지 끌어들이는 거예요?"], ["I can’t understand what you want to torment me for.", "언니가 절 왜 이렇게 괴롭히려 드는지 도무지 모르겠어요."], ["I’ve told you, and I say it again, that I have some pride, and never, never would I do as you’re doing—go back to a man who’s deceived you, who has cared for another woman.", "제가 말했고, 다시 말하지만, 전 자존심이라는 게 있어요. 그래서 절대로, 절대로 언니처럼은 하지 않을 거예요—언니를 속인 남자, 다른 여자한테 마음을 둔 남자한테 되돌아가는 짓 따위는요."], ["I can’t understand it!", "전 그걸 이해할 수가 없어요!"], ["You may, but I can’t!”", "언니는 할 수 있을지 몰라도, 전 못 해요!\""]], [["And saying these words she glanced at her sister, and seeing that Dolly sat silent, her head mournfully bowed, Kitty, instead of running out of the room as she had meant to do, sat down near the door, and hid her face in her handkerchief.", "그리고 이 말을 내뱉으며 그녀는 언니를 흘끗 보았는데, 돌리가 슬픈 듯 고개를 떨군 채 말없이 앉아 있는 것을 보고는, 원래 하려던 대로 방에서 뛰쳐나가는 대신 문가에 앉아 손수건에 얼굴을 파묻었다."]], [["The silence lasted for two minutes: Dolly was thinking of herself.", "침묵이 이 분 동안 이어졌다. 돌리는 자기 자신에 대해 생각하고 있었다."], ["That humiliation of which she was always conscious came back to her with a peculiar bitterness when her sister reminded her of it.", "늘 의식하고 있던 그 굴욕이, 동생이 그것을 일깨우자 유난히 쓰라리게 되살아났다."], ["She had not looked for such cruelty in her sister, and she was angry with her.", "그녀는 동생에게서 이런 잔인함을 기대하지 않았기에, 동생에게 화가 났다."], ["But suddenly she heard the rustle of a skirt, and with it the sound of heart-rending, smothered sobbing, and felt arms about her neck.", "그런데 갑자기 치맛자락이 스치는 소리가 들렸고, 그와 함께 가슴이 찢어지는 듯한, 억눌린 흐느낌 소리가 들리더니, 두 팔이 자기 목을 감싸 안는 것이 느껴졌다."], ["Kitty was on her knees before her.", "키티가 그녀 앞에 무릎을 꿇고 있었다."]], [["“Dolinka, I am so, so wretched!”", "\"돌린카, 난, 난 너무, 너무 비참해요!\""], ["she whispered penitently.", "그녀가 뉘우치듯 속삭였다."], ["And the sweet face covered with tears hid itself in Darya Alexandrovna’s skirt.", "그리고 눈물로 뒤덮인 그 사랑스러운 얼굴이 다리야 알렉산드로브나의 치맛자락 속으로 숨어들었다."]], [["As though tears were the indispensable oil, without which the machinery of mutual confidence could not run smoothly between the two sisters, the sisters after their tears talked, not of what was uppermost in their minds, but, though they talked of outside matters, they understood each other.", "마치 눈물이 없어서는 안 될 윤활유여서, 그것 없이는 두 자매 사이에 서로를 믿는 마음의 톱니바퀴가 매끄럽게 돌아가지 못하기라도 하듯, 자매는 눈물을 흘리고 난 뒤 마음속 가장 깊은 곳에 있는 것에 대해서는 말하지 않았지만, 겉도는 이야기를 하면서도 서로를 이해했다."], ["Kitty knew that the words she had uttered in anger about her husband’s infidelity and her humiliating position had cut her poor sister to the heart, but that she had forgiven her.", "키티는 남편의 부정과 굴욕적인 처지에 대해 홧김에 내뱉은 말이 가엾은 언니의 가슴을 도려냈다는 것을, 그러나 언니가 자기를 용서했다는 것을 알았다."], ["Dolly for her part knew all she had wanted to find out.", "돌리는 돌리대로 알고 싶었던 모든 것을 알아냈다."], ["She felt certain that her surmises were correct; that Kitty’s misery, her inconsolable misery, was due precisely to the fact that Levin had made her an offer and she had refused him, and Vronsky had deceived her, and that she was fully prepared to love Levin and to detest Vronsky.", "그녀는 자신의 짐작이 옳다고 확신했다. 키티의 불행이, 그 위로할 길 없는 불행이, 다름 아니라 레빈이 그녀에게 청혼했으나 그녀가 그를 거절했고, 브론스키가 그녀를 속였다는 사실에서 비롯되었으며, 그녀가 이제는 레빈을 사랑하고 브론스키를 혐오할 마음의 준비가 충분히 되어 있다는 것을 확신했다."], ["Kitty said not a word of that; she talked of nothing but her spiritual condition.", "키티는 그것에 대해서는 한마디도 하지 않았다. 그녀는 오로지 자신의 마음 상태에 대해서만 이야기했다."]], [["“I have nothing to make me miserable,”", "\"전 비참해할 만한 게 아무것도 없어요.\" 그녀가 한결 차분해지며 말했다."], ["she said, getting calmer; “but can you understand that everything has become hateful, loathsome, coarse to me, and I myself most of all?", "\"그렇지만 언니, 이해할 수 있어요? 모든 게 제게는 밉고, 역겹고, 천하게 느껴진다는 걸, 그리고 무엇보다 저 자신이 가장 그렇다는 걸요?"], ["You can’t imagine what loathsome thoughts I have about everything.”", "제가 모든 것에 대해 얼마나 역겨운 생각들을 하는지 언니는 상상도 못 할 거예요.\""]], [["“Why, whatever loathsome thoughts can you have?”", "\"아니, 대체 어떤 역겨운 생각을 한다는 거니?\""], ["asked Dolly, smiling.", "돌리가 미소를 지으며 물었다."]], [["“The most utterly loathsome and coarse: I can’t tell you.", "\"더없이 역겹고 천한 생각들이에요. 차마 말도 못 하겠어요."], ["It’s not unhappiness, or low spirits, but much worse.", "그건 불행도, 의기소침도 아니고, 그보다 훨씬 더 나쁜 거예요."], ["As though everything that was good in me was all hidden away, and nothing was left but the most loathsome.", "마치 제 안에 있던 좋은 것은 전부 어딘가로 숨어 버리고, 가장 역겨운 것만 남은 것 같아요."], ["Come, how am I to tell you?”", "자, 이걸 어떻게 말해야 할까요?\""], ["she went on, seeing the puzzled look in her sister’s eyes.", "그녀는 언니의 눈에 어린 어리둥절한 빛을 보며 말을 이었다."], ["“Father began saying something to me just now....", "\"아까 아버지가 저한테 뭔가 말씀하기 시작하셨는데……"], ["It seems to me he thinks all I want is to be married.", "제가 보기엔 아버지는 제가 바라는 게 오로지 시집가는 것뿐이라고 생각하시는 것 같아요."], ["Mother takes me to a ball: it seems to me she only takes me to get me married off as soon as may be, and be rid of me.", "어머니는 절 무도회에 데려가시는데, 제 눈엔 어머니가 절 한시바삐 시집보내서 떼어 버리려고 데려가시는 것처럼만 보여요."], ["I know it’s not the truth, but I can’t drive away such thoughts.", "그게 사실이 아닌 걸 알면서도, 그런 생각을 떨쳐 낼 수가 없어요."], ["Eligible suitors, as they call them—I can’t bear to see them.", "사람들이 신랑감이라 부르는 그런 남자들—전 그 사람들을 보는 것조차 견딜 수가 없어요."], ["It seems to me they’re taking stock of me and summing me up.", "제 눈엔 그 사람들이 절 품평하고 값을 매기는 것처럼만 보이거든요."], ["In old days to go anywhere in a ball dress was a simple joy to me, I admired myself; now I feel ashamed and awkward.", "예전엔 무도회 드레스를 입고 어딜 가는 게 저한테는 그저 단순한 기쁨이었어요, 제 모습에 스스로 흐뭇해했죠. 그런데 지금은 부끄럽고 어색하기만 해요."], ["And then!", "게다가!"], ["The doctor....", "그 의사……"], ["Then....”", "그리고……\""], ["Kitty hesitated; she wanted to say further that ever since this change had taken place in her, Stepan Arkadyevitch had become insufferably repulsive to her, and that she could not see him without the grossest and most hideous conceptions rising before her imagination.", "키티는 머뭇거렸다. 그녀는 자기 안에 이런 변화가 일어난 뒤로 스테판 아르카지치가 견딜 수 없을 만큼 혐오스러워졌으며, 그를 보기만 하면 더없이 추잡하고 끔찍한 상상들이 머릿속에 떠올라 그를 마주 볼 수가 없다는 말까지 하고 싶었다."]], [["“Oh, well, everything presents itself to me, in the coarsest, most loathsome light,”", "\"아, 뭐, 모든 게 제겐 가장 천하고 가장 역겨운 빛깔로 비쳐요.\""], ["she went on.", "그녀가 말을 이었다."], ["“That’s my illness.", "\"그게 제 병이에요."], ["Perhaps it will pass off.”", "어쩌면 지나갈지도 모르죠.\""]], [["“But you mustn’t think about it.”", "\"하지만 그런 건 생각하지 말아야 해.\""]], [["“I can’t help it.", "\"어쩔 수가 없는걸요."], ["I’m never happy except with the children at your house.”", "전 언니 집에서 아이들이랑 있을 때 말고는 도무지 행복하질 않아요.\""]], [["“What a pity you can’t be with me!”", "\"네가 나랑 같이 못 있다니 정말 아쉽구나!\""]], [["“Oh, yes, I’m coming.", "\"아, 아니에요, 갈 거예요."], ["I’ve had scarlatina, and I’ll persuade mamma to let me.”", "전 성홍열을 앓은 적이 있으니까, 엄마를 설득해서 허락받을게요.\""]], [["Kitty insisted on having her way, and went to stay at her sister’s and nursed the children all through the scarlatina, for scarlatina it turned out to be.", "키티는 자기 뜻을 굽히지 않고 끝내 언니 집에 가서 머물렀고, 성홍열을 앓는 내내 아이들을 간호했다. 그것은 정말로 성홍열로 밝혀졌던 것이다."], ["The two sisters brought all the six children successfully through it, but Kitty was no better in health, and in Lent the Shtcherbatskys went abroad.", "두 자매는 여섯 아이 모두를 무사히 그 병에서 회복시켰지만, 키티의 건강은 조금도 나아지지 않았고, 사순절에 셰르바츠키 일가는 외국으로 떠났다."]], [["# Chapter 4", "# Chapter 4"]], [["The highest Petersburg society is essentially one: in it everyone knows everyone else, everyone even visits everyone else.", "페테르부르크 최상류 사교계는 본질적으로 하나다. 그 안에서는 모두가 서로를 알고, 심지어 서로 왕래까지 한다."], ["But this great set has its subdivisions.", "그러나 이 거대한 집단에는 그 나름의 세분된 갈래가 있었다."], ["Anna Arkadyevna Karenina had friends and close ties in three different circles of this highest society.", "안나 아르카지예브나 카레니나는 이 최상류 사교계의 서로 다른 세 무리에 친구와 가까운 인연을 두고 있었다."], ["One circle was her husband’s government official set, consisting of his colleagues and subordinates, brought together in the most various and capricious manner, and belonging to different social strata.", "한 무리는 남편의 관직 동료들로 이루어진 집단으로, 남편의 동료들과 부하들이 더없이 다양하고 변덕스러운 방식으로 한데 모여 있었으며, 각기 다른 사회 계층에 속해 있었다."], ["Anna found it difficult now to recall the feeling of almost awe-stricken reverence which she had at first entertained for these persons.", "안나는 이제 처음에 이 사람들에게 품었던, 거의 경외에 가까운 존경의 감정을 떠올리기가 어려웠다."], ["Now she knew all of them as people know one another in a country town; she knew their habits and weaknesses, and where the shoe pinched each one of them.", "이제 그녀는 시골 소도시 사람들이 서로를 알듯이 그들 모두를 속속들이 알았다. 그들의 습관과 약점을, 그리고 저마다 어디가 가려운지를 다 알았다."], ["She knew their relations with one another and with the head authorities, knew who was for whom, and how each one maintained his position, and where they agreed and disagreed.", "그녀는 그들 서로 간의 관계며 최고위층과의 관계를 알았고, 누가 누구 편인지, 저마다 어떻게 자기 자리를 지켜 내는지, 어디서 의견이 맞고 어디서 갈리는지를 알았다."], ["But the circle of political, masculine interests had never interested her, in spite of countess Lidia Ivanovna’s influence, and she avoided it.", "그러나 정치적이고 남성적인 관심사의 무리는 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인의 영향에도 불구하고 한 번도 그녀의 흥미를 끈 적이 없었기에, 그녀는 그것을 멀리했다."]], [["Another little set with which Anna was in close relations was the one by means of which Alexey Alexandrovitch had made his career.", "안나가 가깝게 지낸 또 다른 작은 무리는, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 출세를 이룬 발판이 된 바로 그 집단이었다."], ["The center of this circle was the Countess Lidia Ivanovna.", "이 무리의 중심에는 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인이 있었다."], ["It was a set made up of elderly, ugly, benevolent, and godly women, and clever, learned, and ambitious men.", "그것은 나이 들고 못생기고 자비롭고 신앙심 깊은 여인들과, 영리하고 학식 있고 야심 찬 남자들로 이루어진 집단이었다."], ["One of the clever people belonging to the set had called it “the conscience of Petersburg society.”", "이 무리에 속한 영리한 사람 가운데 하나가 그것을 \"페테르부르크 사교계의 양심\"이라고 불렀다."], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch had the highest esteem for this circle, and Anna with her special gift for getting on with everyone, had in the early days of her life in Petersburg made friends in this circle also.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 이 무리를 더없이 높이 평가했고, 누구와도 잘 어울리는 특별한 재주를 지닌 안나는 페테르부르크 생활 초기에 이 무리에서도 친구를 사귀었다."], ["Now, since her return from Moscow, she had come to feel this set insufferable.", "그러나 이제, 모스크바에서 돌아온 뒤로 그녀는 이 집단을 견딜 수 없게 느꼈다."], ["It seemed to her that both she and all of them were insincere, and she felt so bored and ill at ease in that world that she went to see the Countess Lidia Ivanovna as little as possible.", "그녀 자신도, 그들 모두도 진실하지 못하다는 생각이 들었고, 그 세계에서 너무도 따분하고 거북했던 탓에 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인을 찾아가는 일을 되도록 줄였다."]], [["The third circle with which Anna had ties was preeminently the fashionable world—the world of balls, of dinners, of sumptuous dresses, the world that hung on to the court with one hand, so as to avoid sinking to the level of the demi-monde.", "안나가 인연을 맺은 세 번째 무리는 무엇보다도 유행을 좇는 사교계, 즉 무도회와 만찬과 호화로운 의상의 세계였으니, 데미몽드 수준으로 전락하지 않으려고 한 손으로 궁정에 매달려 있는 세계였다. 그 유행하는 세계의 사람들은 자기들이 데미몽드를 경멸한다고 믿었지만, 사실 그들의 취향은 단지 비슷한 정도가 아니라 실은 똑같았다."], ["For the demi-monde the members of that fashionable world believed that they despised, though their tastes were not merely similar, but in fact identical.", "이 무리와의 연결은 사촌의 아내인 벳시 트베르스카야 공작 부인을 통해 유지되었는데, 그녀는 연 십이만 루블의 수입이 있었고, 안나가 처음 사교계에 나온 이래로 그녀를 무척 마음에 들어 해 많은 관심을 기울였으며, 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인의 패거리를 비웃으면서 안나를 자기 무리로 끌어들였다."], ["Her connection with this circle was kept up through Princess Betsy Tverskaya, her cousin’s wife, who had an income of a hundred and twenty thousand roubles, and who had taken a great fancy to Anna ever since she first came out, showed her much attention, and drew her into her set, making fun of Countess Lidia Ivanovna’s coterie.", "* 데미몽드(demi-monde): 프랑스어로 '반쪽 사교계'. 부유한 남성의 정부 노릇을 하는 여인들의 세계를 가리킨다."]], [["“When I’m old and ugly I’ll be the same,”", "\"내가 늙고 못생겨지면 나도 그렇게 될 거야.\""], ["Betsy used to say; “but for a pretty young woman like you it’s early days for that house of charity.”", "벳시는 입버릇처럼 말했다. \"하지만 너처럼 예쁘고 젊은 여자한테는 그 자선의 집에 들어가긴 아직 일러.\""]], [["Anna had at first avoided as far as she could Princess Tverskaya’s world, because it necessitated an expenditure beyond her means, and besides in her heart she preferred the first circle.", "안나는 처음에는 트베르스카야 공작 부인의 세계를 되도록 멀리했는데, 그 세계가 자기 형편에 넘치는 지출을 요구했기 때문이고, 게다가 마음속으로는 첫 번째 무리를 더 좋아했기 때문이다."], ["But since her visit to Moscow she had done quite the contrary.", "그러나 모스크바를 다녀온 뒤로 그녀는 정반대로 했다."], ["She avoided her serious-minded friends, and went out into the fashionable world.", "그녀는 진지한 친구들을 피하고 유행하는 사교계로 나갔다."], ["There she met Vronsky, and experienced an agitating joy at those meetings.", "거기서 그녀는 브론스키를 만났고, 그 만남에서 가슴 설레는 기쁨을 느꼈다."], ["She met Vronsky specially often at Betsy’s for Betsy was a Vronsky by birth and his cousin.", "그녀는 특히 벳시의 집에서 브론스키를 자주 만났는데, 벳시는 본래 브론스키 집안 사람으로 그의 사촌이었기 때문이다."], ["Vronsky was everywhere where he had any chance of meeting Anna, and speaking to her, when he could, of his love.", "브론스키는 안나를 만날 가망이 조금이라도 있는 곳이라면 어디든 나타났고, 기회가 닿을 때면 자기 사랑을 그녀에게 이야기했다."], ["She gave him no encouragement, but every time she met him there surged up in her heart that same feeling of quickened life that had come upon her that day in the railway carriage when she saw him for the first time.", "그녀는 그에게 아무런 부추김도 주지 않았지만, 그를 만날 때마다 그녀의 가슴속에는 처음 그를 보았던 그날 열차 안에서 솟아올랐던 바로 그 활기 돋는 생명의 감정이 다시금 차올랐다."], ["She was conscious herself that her delight sparkled in her eyes and curved her lips into a smile, and she could not quench the expression of this delight.", "그녀는 자기 기쁨이 눈 속에서 반짝이고 입술을 미소로 휘어 놓는 것을 스스로 의식했지만, 그 기쁨의 표정을 억누를 수가 없었다."]], [["At first Anna sincerely believed that she was displeased with him for daring to pursue her.", "처음에 안나는 그가 감히 자기를 쫓아다니는 것 때문에 그에게 불쾌함을 느낀다고 진심으로 믿었다."], ["Soon after her return from Moscow, on arriving at a soirée where she had expected to meet him, and not finding him there, she realized distinctly from the rush of disappointment that she had been deceiving herself, and that this pursuit was not merely not distasteful to her, but that it made the whole interest of her life.", "모스크바에서 돌아온 지 얼마 안 되어, 그를 만나리라 기대했던 어느 soirée에 도착했는데 그가 그 자리에 없자, 밀려드는 실망감에서 그녀는 자기가 스스로를 속여 왔다는 것을, 그리고 이 추적이 단지 싫지 않을 뿐 아니라 자기 삶의 모든 흥미를 이룬다는 것을 또렷이 깨달았다."]], [["The celebrated singer was singing for the second time, and all the fashionable world was in the theater.", "* soirée: 프랑스어로 '야회(夜會)', 저녁 모임. 이름난 가수가 두 번째 무대를 노래하고 있었고, 유행하는 사교계 전체가 극장에 모여 있었다."], ["Vronsky, seeing his cousin from his stall in the front row, did not wait till the entr’acte, but went to her box.", "브론스키는 앞줄 일등석에서 사촌을 보고는 막간까지 기다리지 않고 그녀의 칸막이 좌석으로 갔다."]], [["“Why didn’t you come to dinner?”", "\"왜 만찬에 안 왔어요?\""], ["she said to him.", "그녀가 그에게 말했다."], ["“I marvel at the second sight of lovers,”", "\"연인들의 예감이란 참 놀라워.\""], ["she added with a smile, so that no one but he could hear; “she wasn’t there.", "그녀는 그 말고는 아무도 못 듣게 미소를 지으며 덧붙였다. \"그 여자는 거기 없었거든."], ["But come after the opera.”", "그래도 오페라 끝나고 와요.\""]], [["Vronsky looked inquiringly at her. She nodded.", "브론스키는 묻는 듯한 눈길로 그녀를 바라보았다."], ["He thanked her by a smile, and sat down beside her.", "그녀가 고개를 끄덕였다. 그는 미소로 고마움을 표하고는 그녀 곁에 앉았다."]], [["“But how I remember your jeers!”", "\"근데 내가 당신 비웃던 거 어찌나 잘 기억나는지!\""], ["continued Princess Betsy, who took a peculiar pleasure in following up this passion to a successful issue.", "벳시 공작 부인이 말을 이었다. 그녀는 이 연정을 끝까지 따라가 성공으로 이끄는 데서 남다른 즐거움을 느끼고 있었다."], ["“What’s become of all that?", "\"그게 다 어떻게 된 거지?"], ["You’re caught, my dear boy.”", "잡혔잖아, 내 사랑스러운 친구.\""]], [["“That’s my one desire, to be caught,”", "\"바로 그게 내 유일한 소원이죠, 잡히는 것 말입니다.\""], ["answered Vronsky, with his serene, good-humored smile.", "브론스키가 그 평온하고 쾌활한 미소를 지으며 대답했다."], ["“If I complain of anything it’s only that I’m not caught enough, to tell the truth.", "\"내가 무얼 한탄한다면, 솔직히 말해 충분히 잡히지 못한 것뿐이에요."], ["I begin to lose hope.”", "슬슬 희망을 잃기 시작했거든요.\""]], [["“Why, whatever hope can you have?”", "\"아니, 도대체 무슨 희망을 품을 수 있다는 거예요?\""], ["said Betsy, offended on behalf of her friend.", "벳시가 친구를 대신해 발끈하며 말했다."], ["“Entendons nous....”", "\"Entendons nous.......\""], ["But in her eyes there were gleams of light that betrayed that she understood perfectly and precisely as he did what hope he might have.", "그러나 그녀의 눈 속에는, 그가 무슨 희망을 품을 수 있을지 자기도 그와 똑같이 완벽하게 그리고 정확하게 알고 있음을 드러내는 빛이 어른거렸다."]], [["“None whatever,” said Vronsky, laughing and showing his even rows of teeth.", "* Entendons nous: 프랑스어로 '서로 분명히 해 둡시다', '말이 통하게 합시다'."], ["“Excuse me,”", "\"전혀 없어요.\""], ["he added, taking an opera-glass out of her hand, and proceeding to scrutinize, over her bare shoulder, the row of boxes facing them.", "브론스키가 웃으며 가지런한 잇줄을 드러내고 말했다. \"실례.\""], ["“I’m afraid I’m becoming ridiculous.”", "그는 그녀의 손에서 오페라글라스를 받아 들고는, 그녀의 드러난 어깨 너머로 맞은편의 칸막이 좌석 줄을 살피기 시작하며 덧붙였다. \"내가 우스워지고 있는 것 같아서요.\""]], [["He was very well aware that he ran no risk of being ridiculous in the eyes of Betsy or any other fashionable people.", "그는 벳시나 다른 유행하는 사람들의 눈에 자기가 우스워질 위험이 전혀 없음을 잘 알고 있었다."], ["He was very well aware that in their eyes the position of an unsuccessful lover of a girl, or of any woman free to marry, might be ridiculous.", "그들의 눈에 처녀나, 혹은 결혼이 자유로운 어떤 여인의 구애에 실패한 연인의 처지는 우스워 보일 수 있다는 것을 그는 잘 알았다."], ["But the position of a man pursuing a married woman, and, regardless of everything, staking his life on drawing her into adultery, has something fine and grand about it, and can never be ridiculous; and so it was with a proud and gay smile under his mustaches that he lowered the opera-glass and looked at his cousin.", "그러나 유부녀를 쫓으며, 모든 것을 아랑곳하지 않고 그녀를 간통으로 끌어들이는 데 목숨을 거는 남자의 처지에는 무언가 멋지고 웅장한 데가 있어 결코 우스워질 수 없었다. 그리하여 그는 콧수염 아래 자랑스럽고 유쾌한 미소를 띤 채 오페라글라스를 내리고 사촌을 바라보았다."]], [["“But why was it you didn’t come to dinner?”", "\"근데 왜 만찬에 안 왔어요?\""], ["she said, admiring him.", "그녀가 그를 흐뭇하게 바라보며 말했다."]], [["“I must tell you about that.", "\"그 얘기를 해 드려야겠군요."], ["I was busily employed, and doing what, do you suppose?", "나는 바쁘게 일하고 있었는데, 무얼 했는지 짐작이 갑니까?"], ["I’ll give you a hundred guesses, a thousand ... you’d never guess.", "백 번을 맞혀 보세요, 천 번이라도......도저히 못 맞힐 겁니다."], ["I’ve been reconciling a husband with a man who’d insulted his wife.", "자기 아내를 모욕한 남자와 그 남편을 화해시키고 있었어요."], ["Yes, really!”", "정말이에요!\""]], [["“Well, did you succeed?”", "\"그래서, 성공했어요?\""]], [["“Almost.”", "\"거의.\""]], [["“You really must tell me about it,”", "\"그 얘긴 꼭 들려줘야 해요.\""], ["she said, getting up.", "그녀가 일어서며 말했다."], ["“Come to me in the next entr’acte.”", "\"다음 막간에 나한테 와요.\""]], [["“I can’t; I’m going to the French theater.”", "\"안 돼요, 프랑스 극장에 가야 하거든요.\""]], [["“From Nilsson?”", "\"닐손을 두고요?\""], ["Betsy queried in horror, though she could not herself have distinguished Nilsson’s voice from any chorus girl’s.", "벳시가 질겁하며 물었다. 정작 그녀 자신은 닐손의 목소리를 어떤 합창단 여자의 목소리와도 구별하지 못했을 텐데도."]], [["“Can’t help it.", "\"어쩔 수 없어요."], ["I’ve an appointment there, all to do with my mission of peace.”", "거기 약속이 있어서요, 죄다 내 평화 사명과 관련된 일이죠.\""]], [["“‘Blessed are the peacemakers; theirs is the kingdom of heaven,’”", "\"'화평하게 하는 자는 복이 있나니, 천국이 저희 것임이요.'\""], ["said Betsy, vaguely recollecting she had heard some similar saying from someone.", "벳시가 누군가에게서 비슷한 말을 들었던 것을 어렴풋이 떠올리며 말했다."], ["“Very well, then, sit down, and tell me what it’s all about.”", "\"좋아요, 그럼 앉아서 도대체 무슨 일인지 얘기나 해 봐요.\""]], [["And she sat down again.", "그러고는 다시 자리에 앉았다."]], [["# Chapter 5", "# Chapter 5"]], [["“This is rather indiscreet, but it’s so good it’s an awful temptation to tell the story,”", "\"좀 경솔한 얘기지만, 어찌나 재미있는지 들려주고 싶어서 못 견디겠군요.\""], ["said Vronsky, looking at her with his laughing eyes.", "브론스키는 웃음 띤 눈으로 그녀를 바라보며 말했다."], ["“I’m not going to mention any names.”", "\"이름은 일절 대지 않겠습니다.\""]], [["“But I shall guess, so much the better.”", "\"하지만 내가 알아맞힐 텐데요, 그럼 더 좋고요.\""]], [["“Well, listen: two festive young men were driving—”", "\"자, 들어 보세요. 흥에 겨운 두 젊은이가 마차를 몰고 가고 있었지요.\""]], [["“Officers of your regiment, of course?”", "\"물론 당신 연대의 장교들이겠지요?\""]], [["“I didn’t say they were officers,—two young men who had been lunching.”", "\"장교라고는 안 했습니다. 그저 점심을 먹고 난 두 젊은이였지요.\""]], [["“In other words, drinking.”", "\"바꿔 말하면 술을 마셨다는 거군요.\""]], [["“Possibly.", "\"아마도 그렇겠지요."], ["They were driving on their way to dinner with a friend in the most festive state of mind.", "그들은 친구와 저녁 식사를 하러 가는 길이었는데, 더없이 들뜬 기분으로 마차를 몰고 있었습니다."], ["And they beheld a pretty woman in a hired sledge; she overtakes them, looks round at them, and, so they fancy anyway, nods to them and laughs.", "그러다 삯썰매를 탄 어여쁜 여인을 보게 됐지요. 그 여인이 그들을 앞지르더니, 그들 쪽을 돌아보고는, 적어도 그들이 느끼기로는, 고개를 끄덕이며 웃었답니다."], ["They, of course, follow her.", "그들은 물론 그 여인을 뒤쫓았지요."], ["They gallop at full speed.", "전속력으로 내달렸습니다."], ["To their amazement, the fair one alights at the entrance of the very house to which they were going.", "그런데 놀랍게도 그 미인은 다름 아닌 자기들이 가려던 바로 그 집 현관 앞에서 썰매에서 내리는 게 아니겠습니까."], ["The fair one darts upstairs to the top story.", "미인은 맨 위층으로 쏜살같이 올라갔지요."], ["They get a glimpse of red lips under a short veil, and exquisite little feet.”", "그들은 짧은 베일 아래로 드러난 붉은 입술과 더없이 곱고 자그마한 발을 언뜻 보았답니다.\""]], [["“You describe it with such feeling that I fancy you must be one of the two.”", "\"어찌나 실감 나게 묘사하는지, 당신이 그 두 사람 중 하나가 아닌가 싶군요.\""]], [["“And after what you said, just now!", "\"방금 그렇게 말씀하시고서야!"], ["Well, the young men go in to their comrade’s; he was giving a farewell dinner.", "자, 두 젊은이는 친구네 집으로 들어갔지요. 친구는 송별 만찬을 열고 있었습니다."], ["There they certainly did drink a little too much, as one always does at farewell dinners.", "거기서 그들은 분명 술을 좀 과하게 마셨지요, 송별 만찬에서는 늘 그러기 마련이니까요."], ["And at dinner they inquire who lives at the top in that house.", "그러다 식사 도중에 그 집 맨 위층에 누가 사는지를 물었답니다."], ["No one knows; only their host’s valet, in answer to their inquiry whether any ‘young ladies’ are living on the top floor, answered that there were a great many of them about there.", "아무도 모르더군요. 다만 그 집 주인의 시종이, 맨 위층에 '아가씨들'이 사느냐는 그들의 물음에, 그 근방에 그런 여자들이 아주 많다고 대답했지요."], ["After dinner the two young men go into their host’s study, and write a letter to the unknown fair one.", "식사를 마치고 두 젊은이는 주인의 서재로 들어가, 미지의 미인에게 편지를 썼답니다."], ["They compose an ardent epistle, a declaration in fact, and they carry the letter upstairs themselves, so as to elucidate whatever might appear not perfectly intelligible in the letter.”", "열렬한 사연을, 그러니까 사실상 사랑의 고백을 써 내려가고는, 편지에서 어쩌면 영 알아듣기 어려울지도 모를 대목을 직접 설명하려고 그 편지를 손수 위층으로 들고 올라갔지요.\""]], [["“Why are you telling me these horrible stories?", "\"왜 이런 끔찍한 얘기를 들려주시는 거예요?"], ["Well?”", "그래서요?\""]], [["“They ring.", "\"그들이 초인종을 울립니다."], ["A maid-servant opens the door, they hand her the letter, and assure the maid that they’re both so in love that they’ll die on the spot at the door.", "하녀가 문을 열자, 그들은 하녀에게 편지를 건네고는, 둘 다 어찌나 사랑에 빠졌는지 이 문 앞에서 당장 죽어 버릴 지경이라고 우겨 댔지요."], ["The maid, stupefied, carries in their messages.", "어리둥절해진 하녀가 그들의 말을 안에 전합니다."], ["All at once a gentleman appears with whiskers like sausages, as red as a lobster, announces that there is no one living in the flat except his wife, and sends them both about their business.”", "그러자 별안간 소시지처럼 생긴 구레나룻을 기르고 바닷가재처럼 시뻘건 신사가 나타나, 이 집에는 자기 아내 말고는 아무도 살지 않는다고 선언하더니 두 사람을 다 쫓아냈답니다.\""]], [["“How do you know he had whiskers like sausages, as you say?”", "\"그 사람한테 말씀처럼 소시지 같은 구레나룻이 있었다는 걸 어떻게 아세요?\""]], [["“Ah, you shall hear.", "\"아, 들어 보세요."], ["I’ve just been to make peace between them.”", "내가 방금 그들 사이를 화해시키고 오는 길이거든요.\""]], [["“Well, and what then?”", "\"그래서, 그다음엔 어떻게 됐나요?\""]], [["“That’s the most interesting part of the story.", "\"바로 그게 이 이야기에서 가장 흥미로운 대목이지요."], ["It appears that it’s a happy couple, a government clerk and his lady.", "알고 보니 그들은 금실 좋은 부부, 관청 서기와 그 부인이었답니다."], ["The government clerk lodges a complaint, and I became a mediator, and such a mediator!...", "관청 서기가 항의를 제기했고, 내가 중재자로 나섰는데, 그것도 얼마나 대단한 중재자였는지요!……"], ["I assure you Talleyrand couldn’t hold a candle to me.”", "정말이지 탈레랑도 나한테는 댈 게 아닐 겁니다.\""]], [["“Why, where was the difficulty?”", "\"아니, 도대체 뭐가 그리 어려웠는데요?\""]], [["“Ah, you shall hear....", "\"아, 들어 보세요…… 우리는 격식을 갖춰 사과를 했지요."], ["We apologize in due form: we are in despair, we entreat forgiveness for the unfortunate misunderstanding.", "우리는 절망에 빠져 있노라고, 이 불행한 오해를 용서해 달라고 간청했습니다. 소시지 구레나룻을 단 그 관청 서기가 누그러지기 시작하더군요."], ["The government clerk with the sausages begins to melt, but he, too, desires to express his sentiments, and as soon as ever he begins to express them, he begins to get hot and say nasty things, and again I’m obliged to trot out all my diplomatic talents.", "그런데 자기도 속내를 털어놓고 싶어 하더니, 막상 털어놓기 시작하자마자 점점 흥분해서는 험한 말을 쏟아 내는 겁니다. 그래서 나는 또다시 외교적 수완을 죄다 끌어내야 했지요."], ["I allowed that their conduct was bad, but I urged him to take into consideration their heedlessness, their youth; then, too, the young men had only just been lunching together.", "나는 그들의 행동이 잘못되었음을 인정하면서도, 그들의 경솔함과 젊음을 헤아려 달라고 호소했습니다. 게다가 그 젊은이들은 마침 함께 점심을 먹고 온 참이었다고요."], ["‘You understand.", "'아시잖습니까."], ["They regret it deeply, and beg you to overlook their misbehavior.’ The government clerk was softened once more.", "그들은 깊이 뉘우치고 있으며, 자기들의 무례를 너그러이 보아 달라고 빌고 있습니다.' 관청 서기가 다시 한번 마음을 풀더군요."], ["‘I consent, count, and am ready to overlook it; but you perceive that my wife—my wife’s a respectable woman—has been exposed to the persecution, and insults, and effrontery of young upstarts, scoundrels....’ And you must understand, the young upstarts are present all the while, and I have to keep the peace between them.", "'백작님, 좋습니다, 나도 눈감아 줄 용의가 있습니다. 하지만 보세요, 내 아내가, 내 아내는 점잖은 여자입니다만, 그 아내가 어린 풋내기들, 그 망나니들의 추근거림과 모욕과 무례를 당했단 말입니다……'"], ["Again I call out all my diplomacy, and again as soon as the thing was about at an end, our friend the government clerk gets hot and red, and his sausages stand on end with wrath, and once more I launch out into diplomatic wiles.”", "그런데 아시겠지만, 그 풋내기들은 줄곧 그 자리에 있었고, 나는 그들 사이를 화해시켜야 했지요. 나는 또다시 외교술을 죄다 동원했고, 또다시 일이 거의 마무리될 즈음이면 우리의 관청 서기 양반은 흥분해서 시뻘게지고, 그 소시지가 분노로 곤두서고, 그러면 나는 다시금 외교적 술책을 펼쳐야 했답니다.\""]], [["“Ah, he must tell you this story!”", "\"아, 이 사람이 당신한테 이 얘기를 꼭 들려줘야 해요!\""], ["said Betsy, laughing, to a lady who came into her box.", "베치는 자기 칸막이 좌석으로 들어온 부인에게 웃으며 말했다."], ["“He has been making me laugh so.”", "\"어찌나 날 웃겨 댔는지 몰라요.\""]], [["“Well, bonne chance!” she added, giving Vronsky one finger of the hand in which she held her fan, and with a shrug of her shoulders she twitched down the bodice of her gown that had worked up, so as to be duly naked as she moved forward towards the footlights into the light of the gas, and the sight of all eyes.", "\"자, 그럼 bonne chance!\" 그녀는 부채를 든 손의 손가락 하나를 브론스키에게 내밀며 덧붙였다. 그러고는 어깨를 한번 으쓱하며, 위로 말려 올라간 드레스 상의를 아래로 잡아당겼다. 각광 앞으로, 가스등 불빛 속으로, 뭇 시선 앞으로 나아가면서 어울리게끔 어깨를 드러내기 위해서였다. * bonne chance: 프랑스어로 '행운을 빌어요'."]], [["Vronsky drove to the French theater, where he really had to see the colonel of his regiment, who never missed a single performance there.", "브론스키는 프랑스 극장으로 마차를 몰았다. 거기서 정말이지 자기 연대의 대령을 만나야 했는데, 그는 그 극장의 공연이라면 단 한 회도 놓치는 법이 없었다."], ["He wanted to see him, to report on the result of his mediation, which had occupied and amused him for the last three days.", "브론스키는 그를 만나, 지난 사흘 동안 자기를 사로잡고 즐겁게 해 준 그 중재의 결과를 보고하려 했다."], ["Petritsky, whom he liked, was implicated in the affair, and the other culprit was a capital fellow and first-rate comrade, who had lately joined the regiment, the young Prince Kedrov.", "이 사건에는 그가 아끼는 페트리츠키가 연루되어 있었고, 또 다른 장본인은 최근 연대에 들어온, 더없이 훌륭하고 일류 동료인 젊은 케드로프 공작이었다."], ["And what was most important, the interests of the regiment were involved in it too.", "그리고 무엇보다 중요한 것은, 거기에 연대의 이해관계까지 얽혀 있다는 점이었다."]], [["Both the young men were in Vronsky’s company.", "두 젊은이 모두 브론스키의 중대 소속이었다."], ["The colonel of the regiment was waited upon by the government clerk, Venden, with a complaint against his officers, who had insulted his wife.", "연대의 대령에게 관청 서기 벤덴이 찾아와, 자기 아내를 모욕한 장교들을 고발했던 것이다."], ["His young wife, so Venden told the story—he had been married half a year—was at church with her mother, and suddenly overcome by indisposition, arising from her interesting condition, she could not remain standing, she drove home in the first sledge, a smart-looking one, she came across.", "벤덴의 말로는, 결혼한 지 반년밖에 안 된 그의 젊은 아내가 어머니와 함께 교회에 있었는데, 임신한 몸이라 별안간 몸이 불편해져서 더 이상 서 있을 수가 없게 되자, 마침 눈에 띈 산뜻해 보이는 첫 번째 썰매를 잡아타고 집으로 돌아갔다고 한다."], ["On the spot the officers set off in pursuit of her; she was alarmed, and feeling still more unwell, ran up the staircase home.", "바로 그 자리에서 장교들이 그녀를 뒤쫓기 시작했고, 그녀는 겁에 질려 한층 더 속이 안 좋아진 채로 계단을 뛰어 올라가 집으로 들어갔다."], ["Venden himself, on returning from his office, heard a ring at their bell and voices, went out, and seeing the intoxicated officers with a letter, he had turned them out.", "벤덴 본인은 관청에서 돌아오다가 자기 집 초인종 소리와 사람 목소리를 듣고 나가 보았는데, 술 취한 장교들이 편지를 들고 있는 것을 보고는 그들을 쫓아냈다."], ["He asked for exemplary punishment.", "그는 엄중한 처벌을 요구했다."]], [["“Yes, it’s all very well,”", "\"그래, 다 좋은데 말이지,\""], ["said the colonel to Vronsky, whom he had invited to come and see him.", "대령은 자기를 보러 오라고 청한 브론스키에게 말했다."], ["“Petritsky’s becoming impossible.", "\"페트리츠키가 점점 손쓸 수 없게 되어 가는군."], ["Not a week goes by without some scandal.", "무슨 추문 하나 없이 지나가는 주가 없으니."], ["This government clerk won’t let it drop, he’ll go on with the thing.”", "이 관청 서기는 이 일을 그냥 넘기지 않고 계속 물고 늘어질 거야.\""]], [["Vronsky saw all the thanklessness of the business, and that there could be no question of a duel in it, that everything must be done to soften the government clerk, and hush the matter up.", "브론스키는 이 일이 얼마나 보람 없는 것인지, 또 거기에 결투 따위는 있을 수 없는 일이며, 모든 수를 써서 관청 서기를 누그러뜨리고 일을 덮어야 한다는 것을 알았다."], ["The colonel had called in Vronsky just because he knew him to be an honorable and intelligent man, and, more than all, a man who cared for the honor of the regiment.", "대령이 브론스키를 부른 것은 다름 아니라, 그를 명예롭고 영리한 사람으로, 무엇보다 연대의 명예를 소중히 여기는 사람으로 알고 있었기 때문이다."], ["They talked it over, and decided that Petritsky and Kedrov must go with Vronsky to Venden’s to apologize.", "두 사람은 이 일을 의논한 끝에, 페트리츠키와 케드로프가 브론스키와 함께 벤덴의 집으로 가서 사과해야 한다는 결론을 내렸다."], ["The colonel and Vronsky were both fully aware that Vronsky’s name and rank would be sure to contribute greatly to the softening of the injured husband’s feelings.", "대령과 브론스키는 둘 다, 브론스키의 이름과 지위가 모욕당한 남편의 감정을 누그러뜨리는 데 분명 큰 보탬이 되리라는 것을 충분히 알고 있었다."]], [["And these two influences were not in fact without effect; though the result remained, as Vronsky had described, uncertain.", "그리고 실제로 이 두 가지 힘은 효과가 없지 않았다. 다만 브론스키가 묘사한 대로, 그 결과는 여전히 불확실한 채로 남아 있었다."]], [["On reaching the French theater, Vronsky retired to the foyer with the colonel, and reported to him his success, or non-success.", "프랑스 극장에 도착한 브론스키는 대령과 함께 휴게실로 물러나, 자기의 성공인지 실패인지 모를 결과를 보고했다. 대령은 모든 것을 곰곰이 따져 본 끝에, 이 일을 더 이상 끌고 가지 않기로 마음먹었다."], ["The colonel, thinking it all over, made up his mind not to pursue the matter further, but then for his own satisfaction proceeded to cross-examine Vronsky about his interview; and it was a long while before he could restrain his laughter, as Vronsky described how the government clerk, after subsiding for a while, would suddenly flare up again, as he recalled the details, and how Vronsky, at the last half word of conciliation, skillfully manœuvered a retreat, shoving Petritsky out before him.", "그러면서도 자기 자신의 흥을 위해 브론스키에게 그 면담에 대해 꼬치꼬치 캐묻기 시작했다. 그리고 관청 서기가 한동안 가라앉아 있다가도 그 일의 세세한 정황을 떠올리고는 별안간 다시 발끈했다는 것을, 또 브론스키가 화해의 마지막 반 마디 말이 끝나자마자 페트리츠키를 앞으로 떠밀며 능숙하게 후퇴하는 책략을 부렸다는 것을 듣고는, 한참 동안이나 웃음을 참지 못했다."]], [["“It’s a disgraceful story, but killing.", "\"창피한 얘기지만, 정말 배꼽 빠지겠군."], ["Kedrov really can’t fight the gentleman!", "케드로프가 설마 그 양반과 결투를 할 수야 없지!"], ["Was he so awfully hot?”", "그렇게나 펄펄 끓더란 말인가?\""], ["he commented, laughing.", "그는 웃으며 한마디 덧붙였다."], ["“But what do you say to Claire today?", "\"그건 그렇고, 오늘 클레르는 어떻던가?"], ["She’s marvelous,”", "정말 굉장하지,\""], ["he went on, speaking of a new French actress.", "그는 새로 온 프랑스 여배우 이야기로 말을 이었다."], ["“However often you see her, every day she’s different.", "\"아무리 자주 봐도, 날마다 다르단 말이야."], ["It’s only the French who can do that.”", "그건 프랑스 사람들이나 할 수 있는 일이지.\""]], [["# Chapter 6", "# Chapter 6"]], [["Princess Betsy drove home from the theater, without waiting for the end of the last act.", "베치 공작부인은 마지막 막이 끝나기를 기다리지 않고 극장에서 마차를 타고 집으로 돌아왔다."], ["She had only just time to go into her dressing-room, sprinkle her long, pale face with powder, rub it, set her dress to rights, and order tea in the big drawing-room, when one after another carriages drove up to her huge house in Bolshaia Morskaia.", "그녀는 화장실로 들어가 길고 창백한 얼굴에 분을 뿌려 문지르고 옷매무새를 바로잡은 다음 큰 응접실에 차를 내오라고 일러둘 시간밖에 없었는데, 그러는 사이 마차들이 줄지어 볼샤야 모르스카야 거리에 있는 그녀의 거대한 저택으로 들어섰다."], ["Her guests stepped out at the wide entrance, and the stout porter, who used to read the newspapers in the mornings behind the glass door, to the edification of the passers-by, noiselessly opened the immense door, letting the visitors pass by him into the house.", "손님들은 널찍한 현관에서 내렸고, 아침이면 유리문 안쪽에서 신문을 읽으며 지나가는 사람들을 흐뭇하게 해 주던 뚱뚱한 문지기가 소리 없이 그 거대한 문을 열어 방문객들이 자기 옆을 지나 집 안으로 들어가게 했다."]], [["Almost at the same instant the hostess, with freshly arranged coiffure and freshened face, walked in at one door and her guests at the other door of the drawing-room, a large room with dark walls, downy rugs, and a brightly lighted table, gleaming with the light of candles, white cloth, silver samovar, and transparent china tea-things.", "거의 같은 순간에, 새로 머리를 매만지고 얼굴을 산뜻하게 가다듬은 안주인은 응접실의 한쪽 문으로 들어왔고, 손님들은 다른 쪽 문으로 들어왔다. 응접실은 어두운 벽과 보드라운 양탄자가 깔린 큰 방이었고, 촛불과 흰 식탁보, 은빛 사모바르와 투명한 도자기 찻그릇이 어우러진 빛으로 환하게 반짝이는 식탁이 놓여 있었다."]], [["The hostess sat down at the table and took off her gloves.", "안주인은 식탁에 앉아 장갑을 벗었다."], ["Chairs were set with the aid of footmen, moving almost imperceptibly about the room; the party settled itself, divided into two groups: one round the samovar near the hostess, the other at the opposite end of the drawing-room, round the handsome wife of an ambassador, in black velvet, with sharply defined black eyebrows.", "방 안을 거의 눈에 띄지 않게 오가는 하인들의 도움으로 의자들이 놓였고, 일행은 자리를 잡으며 두 무리로 갈라졌다. 하나는 안주인 곁의 사모바르를 둘러싼 무리였고, 다른 하나는 응접실 반대편 끝에서 검은 벨벳을 입고 또렷한 검은 눈썹을 가진 어느 대사의 아름다운 아내를 둘러싼 무리였다."], ["In both groups conversation wavered, as it always does, for the first few minutes, broken up by meetings, greetings, offers of tea, and as it were, feeling about for something to rest upon.", "두 무리 모두에서 대화는 으레 그렇듯 처음 몇 분간은 만남과 인사, 차 권하기로 끊기며 흔들렸고, 마치 무언가 기댈 거리를 더듬어 찾는 듯했다."]], [["“She’s exceptionally good as an actress; one can see she’s studied Kaulbach,”", "\"그 여자는 배우로서 유난히 뛰어나요. 카울바흐를 연구한 게 보여요.\""], ["said a diplomatic attaché in the group round the ambassador’s wife.", "대사 부인을 둘러싼 무리에서 외교관 한 사람이 말했다."], ["“Did you notice how she fell down?...”", "\"그 여자가 쓰러지는 장면 보셨어요?……\""]], [["“Oh, please, don’t let us talk about Nilsson!", "\"아, 제발, 닐손 이야기는 그만둬요!"], ["No one can possibly say anything new about her,”", "누구도 그 여자에 대해 새로운 말을 할 수는 없어요.\""], ["said a fat, red-faced, flaxen-headed lady, without eyebrows and chignon, wearing an old silk dress.", "눈썹도 시뇽도 없이 낡은 비단 드레스를 입은, 뚱뚱하고 얼굴이 붉으며 머리가 아마빛인 부인이 말했다."], ["This was Princess Myakaya, noted for her simplicity and the roughness of her manners, and nicknamed enfant terrible.", "이 사람은 소탈함과 거친 태도로 유명해 enfant terrible이라는 별명으로 불리는 먀흐카야 공작부인이었다."], ["Princess Myakaya, sitting in the middle between the two groups, and listening to both, took part in the conversation first of one and then of the other.", "먀흐카야 공작부인은 두 무리 사이 한가운데에 앉아 양쪽 말을 다 들으며, 먼저 이쪽 대화에 끼었다가 다시 저쪽 대화에 끼곤 했다."], ["“Three people have used that very phrase about Kaulbach to me today already, just as though they had made a compact about it.", "\"오늘 벌써 세 사람이나 카울바흐에 대해 나한테 그 말을 똑같이 했어요. 마치 무슨 약속이라도 한 것처럼요."], ["And I can’t see why they liked that remark so.”", "그런데 왜들 그 말을 그렇게 좋아하는지 통 모르겠어요.\""]], [["The conversation was cut short by this observation, and a new subject had to be thought of again.", "* enfant terrible: 프랑스어로 '무서운 아이', 못 할 말을 거리낌 없이 하는 사람을 뜻한다."]], [["“Do tell me something amusing but not spiteful,”", "이 한마디로 대화가 끊겨, 다시 새 화제를 생각해 내야 했다."], ["said the ambassador’s wife, a great proficient in the art of that elegant conversation called by the English small talk.", "\"뭔가 재미있되 심술궂지 않은 이야기를 해 줘요.\" 영국인들이 small talk이라 부르는 그 우아한 대화술의 명수인 대사 부인이 말했다."], ["She addressed the attaché, who was at a loss now what to begin upon.", "그녀는 이제 무슨 이야기로 시작해야 할지 막막해하는 외교관에게 말을 건넸다."]], [["“They say that that’s a difficult task, that nothing’s amusing that isn’t spiteful,”", "\"그게 어려운 일이라고들 하지요. 심술궂지 않으면 재미있는 게 없다고요.\""], ["he began with a smile.", "그가 미소를 지으며 말문을 열었다."], ["“But I’ll try.", "\"하지만 한번 해 보지요."], ["Get me a subject.", "화제를 주세요."], ["It all lies in the subject.", "모든 건 화제에 달려 있으니까요."], ["If a subject’s given me, it’s easy to spin something round it.", "화제만 주어지면 그걸 두고 뭔가를 엮어 내기는 쉽지요."], ["I often think that the celebrated talkers of the last century would have found it difficult to talk cleverly now.", "저는 지난 세기의 그 유명한 입담꾼들이 지금이라면 재치 있게 말하기가 어려웠으리라고 종종 생각해요."], ["Everything clever is so stale....”", "재치 있는 건 죄다 너무 진부해져서요…….\""]], [["“That has been said long ago,”", "\"그것도 이미 오래전에 나온 말이에요.\""], ["the ambassador’s wife interrupted him, laughing.", "대사 부인이 웃으며 그의 말을 가로막았다."]], [["The conversation began amiably, but just because it was too amiable, it came to a stop again.", "대화는 다정하게 시작되었지만, 바로 그 다정함이 지나친 탓에 다시 멈추고 말았다."], ["They had to have recourse to the sure, never-failing topic—gossip.", "그들은 결코 실패하는 법이 없는 확실한 화제, 즉 험담에 의지할 수밖에 없었다."]], [["“Don’t you think there’s something Louis Quinze about Tushkevitch?”", "\"투시케비치한테 뭔가 루이 캥즈풍이 있다고 생각지 않으세요?\""], ["he said, glancing towards a handsome, fair-haired young man, standing at the table.", "그가 식탁 옆에 서 있는 잘생긴 금발의 젊은이를 흘긋 보며 말했다."]], [["“Oh, yes!", "\"오, 그래요!"], ["He’s in the same style as the drawing-room and that’s why it is he’s so often here.”", "이 응접실하고 같은 양식이지요. 그래서 그렇게 자주 여기 오는 거고요.\""]], [["This conversation was maintained, since it rested on allusions to what could not be talked of in that room—that is to say, of the relations of Tushkevitch with their hostess.", "이 대화는 그 방에서 입에 올릴 수 없는 것에 대한 암시에 기대고 있었기에 이어질 수 있었다. 다시 말해 투시케비치와 안주인의 관계에 대한 암시였다."]], [["Round the samovar and the hostess the conversation had been meanwhile vacillating in just the same way between three inevitable topics: the latest piece of public news, the theater, and scandal.", "한편 사모바르와 안주인을 둘러싼 무리에서도 대화는 똑같은 식으로 피할 수 없는 세 가지 화제 사이에서 흔들리고 있었다. 가장 최근의 시국 소식, 극장, 그리고 추문이었다."], ["It, too, came finally to rest on the last topic, that is, ill-natured gossip.", "이쪽 역시 결국 마지막 화제, 즉 악의에 찬 험담에 가서 멈추었다."]], [["“Have you heard the Maltishtcheva woman—the mother, not the daughter—has ordered a costume in diable rose color?”", "\"몰티시체바 부인 이야기 들으셨어요? 딸이 아니라 어머니 말이에요. 그 여자가 diable rose 색으로 의상을 맞췄대요.\""]], [["“Nonsense!", "\"말도 안 돼요!"], ["No, that’s too lovely!”", "어머, 너무 멋진데요!\""]], [["“I wonder that with her sense—for she’s not a fool, you know—that she doesn’t see how funny she is.”", "\"그 여자만 한 분별력으로—바보는 아니잖아요—자기가 얼마나 우스꽝스러운지 모르다니 신기하네요.\""]], [["Everyone had something to say in censure or ridicule of the luckless Madame Maltishtcheva, and the conversation crackled merrily, like a burning faggot-stack.", "저마다 불운한 몰티시체바 부인을 헐뜯거나 조롱할 말 한마디씩을 보탰고, 대화는 활활 타오르는 장작더미처럼 즐겁게 탁탁거렸다."]], [["The husband of Princess Betsy, a good-natured fat man, an ardent collector of engravings, hearing that his wife had visitors, came into the drawing-room before going to his club.", "판화를 열성적으로 수집하는 인심 좋고 뚱뚱한 사내인 베치 공작부인의 남편은 아내에게 손님이 왔다는 말을 듣고 클럽으로 가기 전에 응접실로 들어왔다."], ["Stepping noiselessly over the thick rugs, he went up to Princess Myakaya.", "그는 두꺼운 양탄자 위를 소리 없이 밟으며 먀흐카야 공작부인에게 다가갔다."]], [["“How did you like Nilsson?”", "\"닐손은 어떠셨어요?\""], ["he asked.", "그가 물었다."]], [["“Oh, how can you steal upon anyone like that!", "\"아니, 어떻게 그렇게 사람한테 몰래 다가오시나요!"], ["How you startled me!”", "깜짝 놀랐잖아요!\""], ["she responded.", "그녀가 대꾸했다."], ["“Please don’t talk to me about the opera; you know nothing about music.", "\"제발 오페라 이야기는 하지 마세요. 음악에 대해선 아무것도 모르시잖아요."], ["I’d better meet you on your own ground, and talk about your majolica and engravings.", "차라리 당신이 잘 아는 분야에서 만나는 게 낫겠어요. 당신의 마욜리카랑 판화 이야기를 합시다."], ["Come now, what treasure have you been buying lately at the old curiosity shops?”", "자, 요즘 고미술상에서 무슨 보물을 사들이셨어요?\""]], [["“Would you like me to show you?", "\"보여 드릴까요?"], ["But you don’t understand such things.”", "하지만 당신은 그런 걸 이해하지 못하실 텐데요.\""]], [["“Oh, do show me!", "\"오, 보여 주세요!"], ["I’ve been learning about them at those—what’s their names?...", "저 그런 거 배우고 있어요, 그—이름이 뭐더라?……"], ["the bankers ...", "그 은행가들 말이에요……"], ["they’ve some splendid engravings.", "그 사람들한테 멋진 판화들이 있어요."], ["They showed them to us.”", "우리한테 보여 줬어요.\""]], [["“Why, have you been at the Schützburgs?”", "\"아니, 슈츠부르크 댁에 가셨었어요?\""], ["asked the hostess from the samovar.", "사모바르 쪽에서 안주인이 물었다."]], [["“Yes, ma chère.", "\"네, ma chère."], ["They asked my husband and me to dinner, and told us the sauce at that dinner cost a hundred pounds,”", "그 사람들이 나하고 남편을 만찬에 초대했는데, 그 만찬에 나온 소스가 백 파운드나 들었다고 하더군요.\""], ["Princess Myakaya said, speaking loudly, and conscious everyone was listening; “and very nasty sauce it was, some green mess.", "먀흐카야 공작부인이 모두가 듣고 있다는 걸 의식하며 큰 소리로 말했다. \"그런데 그 소스가 아주 형편없는, 무슨 푸르죽죽한 죽 같은 거였어요."], ["We had to ask them, and I made them sauce for eighteen pence, and everybody was very much pleased with it.", "우리도 그 사람들을 초대해야 했는데, 나는 일 실링 육 펜스짜리 소스를 만들어 냈더니 다들 아주 좋아하더군요."], ["I can’t run to hundred-pound sauces.”", "나는 백 파운드짜리 소스 같은 건 엄두도 못 내요.\""]], [["“She’s unique!”", "\"정말 둘도 없는 분이라니까요!\""], ["said the lady of the house.", "안주인이 말했다."]], [["“Marvelous!”", "\"기막혀요!\""], ["said someone.", "누군가가 말했다."]], [["The sensation produced by Princess Myakaya’s speeches was always unique, and the secret of the sensation she produced lay in the fact that though she spoke not always appropriately, as now, she said simple things with some sense in them.", "먀흐카야 공작부인의 말이 불러일으키는 반응은 언제나 남다른 것이었는데, 그 반응의 비밀은, 지금처럼 늘 적절하게 말하는 것은 아니어도 그녀가 나름의 의미가 담긴 단순한 것들을 말한다는 데 있었다."], ["In the society in which she lived such plain statements produced the effect of the wittiest epigram.", "그녀가 사는 사교계에서는 그런 솔직한 말이 더없이 재치 있는 경구 같은 효과를 냈다."], ["Princess Myakaya could never see why it had that effect, but she knew it had, and took advantage of it.", "먀흐카야 공작부인은 왜 그런 효과가 나는지 도무지 알 수 없었지만, 그런 효과가 난다는 것만은 알았고 그것을 이용했다."]], [["As everyone had been listening while Princess Myakaya spoke, and so the conversation around the ambassador’s wife had dropped, Princess Betsy tried to bring the whole party together, and turned to the ambassador’s wife.", "먀흐카야 공작부인이 말하는 동안 모두가 귀를 기울였던 탓에 대사 부인을 둘러싼 대화가 끊겼으므로, 베치 공작부인은 일행을 하나로 모으려고 대사 부인 쪽으로 돌아앉았다."]], [["“Will you really not have tea?", "\"정말 차 안 드실 거예요?"], ["You should come over here by us.”", "이쪽 우리 옆으로 오셔야지요.\""]], [["“No, we’re very happy here,”", "\"아니에요, 우리는 여기가 아주 좋아요.\""], ["the ambassador’s wife responded with a smile, and she went on with the conversation that had been begun.", "대사 부인이 미소를 지으며 대답하고는 시작했던 대화를 이어 갔다."]], [["It was a very agreeable conversation.", "그것은 아주 흥미진진한 대화였다."], ["They were criticizing the Karenins, husband and wife.", "그들은 카레닌 부부, 남편과 아내를 도마에 올리고 있었다."]], [["“Anna is quite changed since her stay in Moscow.", "\"안나는 모스크바에 다녀온 뒤로 아주 달라졌어요."], ["There’s something strange about her,”", "뭔가 이상한 데가 있어요.\""], ["said her friend.", "그녀의 친구가 말했다."]], [["“The great change is that she brought back with her the shadow of Alexey Vronsky,”", "\"가장 큰 변화는 그녀가 알렉세이 브론스키라는 그림자를 데리고 돌아왔다는 거지요.\""], ["said the ambassador’s wife.", "대사 부인이 말했다."]], [["“Well, what of it?", "\"그래서 어떻다는 거예요?"], ["There’s a fable of Grimm’s about a man without a shadow, a man who’s lost his shadow.", "그림자 없는 남자, 그림자를 잃어버린 남자에 관한 그림 동화가 있잖아요."], ["And that’s his punishment for something.", "그게 무슨 일에 대한 벌이라죠."], ["I never could understand how it was a punishment.", "나는 그게 어째서 벌인지 도무지 이해할 수 없었어요."], ["But a woman must dislike being without a shadow.”", "하지만 여자라면 그림자가 없는 건 싫겠지요.\""]], [["“Yes, but women with a shadow usually come to a bad end,”", "\"네, 하지만 그림자를 가진 여자는 대개 끝이 좋지 않지요.\""], ["said Anna’s friend.", "안나의 친구가 말했다."]], [["“Bad luck to your tongue!”", "\"그 혓바닥에 불이라도 떨어지라지!\""], ["said Princess Myakaya suddenly.", "먀흐카야 공작부인이 불쑥 말했다."], ["“Madame Karenina’s a splendid woman.", "\"카레니나 부인은 훌륭한 여자예요."], ["I don’t like her husband, but I like her very much.”", "나는 그 남편은 싫지만 그녀는 아주 좋아해요.\""]], [["“Why don’t you like her husband?", "\"왜 그 남편을 싫어하세요?"], ["He’s such a remarkable man,”", "그렇게 뛰어난 사람인데.\""], ["said the ambassador’s wife.", "대사 부인이 말했다."], ["“My husband says there are few statesmen like him in Europe.”", "\"우리 남편 말로는 유럽에 그만한 정치가가 드물대요.\""]], [["“And my husband tells me just the same, but I don’t believe it,”", "\"우리 남편도 똑같은 말을 하지만, 나는 안 믿어요.\""], ["said Princess Myakaya.", "먀흐카야 공작부인이 말했다."], ["“If our husbands didn’t talk to us, we should see the facts as they are.", "\"우리 남편들이 우리한테 그런 말을 안 한다면, 우리는 사실을 있는 그대로 볼 수 있을 거예요."], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch, to my thinking, is simply a fool.", "내 생각에 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 그냥 바보예요."], ["I say it in a whisper ...", "작은 소리로 말하는 거지만요……"], ["but doesn’t it really make everything clear?", "그렇게 보면 모든 게 분명해지지 않아요?"], ["Before, when I was told to consider him clever, I kept looking for his ability, and thought myself a fool for not seeing it; but directly I said, he’s a fool, though only in a whisper, everything’s explained, isn’t it?”", "전에 그 사람을 똑똑하다고 여기라는 말을 들었을 때, 나는 그 사람의 능력을 찾아 헤매면서 그걸 보지 못하는 나 자신을 바보라고 여겼어요. 그런데 비록 작은 소리이긴 해도 일단 그 사람을 바보라고 말하고 나니 모든 게 설명되더라고요, 안 그래요?\""]], [["“How spiteful you are today!”", "\"오늘 어쩜 그렇게 심술궂으세요!\""]], [["“Not a bit.", "\"전혀 아니에요."], ["I’d no other way out of it.", "달리 빠져나갈 길이 없었던 거예요."], ["One of the two had to be a fool.", "둘 중 하나는 바보여야 했으니까요."], ["And, well, you know one can’t say that of oneself.”", "그리고, 뭐, 자기 자신을 두고 그런 말은 할 수 없잖아요.\""]], [["“‘No one is satisfied with his fortune, and everyone is satisfied with his wit.’”", "\"'누구도 자기 운에는 만족하지 않지만, 누구나 자기 재치에는 만족한다.'\""], ["The attaché repeated the French saying.", "외교관이 그 프랑스 격언을 되풀이했다."]], [["“That’s just it, just it,”", "\"바로 그거예요, 바로 그거.\""], ["Princess Myakaya turned to him.", "먀흐카야 공작부인이 그를 돌아보며 말했다."], ["“But the point is that I won’t abandon Anna to your mercies.", "\"하지만 중요한 건, 나는 안나를 당신들 손에 내맡기지 않겠다는 거예요."], ["She’s so nice, so charming.", "그녀는 아주 친절하고 아주 매력적인 사람이에요."], ["How can she help it if they’re all in love with her, and follow her about like shadows?”", "다들 그녀를 사랑하고 그림자처럼 졸졸 따라다니는데, 그게 그녀 탓이겠어요?\""]], [["“Oh, I had no idea of blaming her for it,”", "\"오, 나는 그걸 두고 그녀를 탓할 생각은 없었어요.\""], ["Anna’s friend said in self-defense.", "안나의 친구가 변명하듯 말했다."]], [["“If no one follows us about like a shadow, that’s no proof that we’ve any right to blame her.”", "\"아무도 우리를 그림자처럼 따라다니지 않는다고 해서, 우리한테 그녀를 탓할 권리가 있는 건 아니지요.\""]], [["And having duly disposed of Anna’s friend, the Princess Myakaya got up, and together with the ambassador’s wife, joined the group at the table, where the conversation was dealing with the king of Prussia.", "그리고 안나의 친구를 적절히 눌러 놓은 뒤, 먀흐카야 공작부인은 자리에서 일어나 대사 부인과 함께 식탁 곁의 무리에 합류했는데, 거기서는 프로이센 왕에 관한 이야기가 오가고 있었다."]], [["“What wicked gossip were you talking over there?”", "\"저쪽에서 무슨 고약한 험담들을 하고 있었어요?\""], ["asked Betsy.", "베치가 물었다."]], [["“About the Karenins.", "\"카레닌 부부 이야기요."], ["The princess gave us a sketch of Alexey Alexandrovitch,”", "공작부인이 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치를 한 폭의 그림처럼 묘사해 주셨어요.\""], ["said the ambassador’s wife with a smile, as she sat down at the table.", "대사 부인이 미소를 지으며 식탁에 앉으며 말했다."]], [["“Pity we didn’t hear it!”", "\"우리가 못 들은 게 아쉽네요!\""], ["said Princess Betsy, glancing towards the door.", "베치 공작부인이 문 쪽을 흘긋 보며 말했다."], ["“Ah, here you are at last!”", "\"아, 드디어 오셨군요!\""], ["she said, turning with a smile to Vronsky, as he came in.", "그녀가 들어오는 브론스키를 향해 미소를 지으며 돌아앉으며 말했다."]], [["Vronsky was not merely acquainted with all the persons whom he was meeting here; he saw them all every day; and so he came in with the quiet manner with which one enters a room full of people from whom one has only just parted.", "브론스키는 여기서 만나는 사람들과 단순히 아는 사이인 정도가 아니라, 그들 모두를 날마다 보았다. 그래서 그는 방금 헤어진 사람들로 가득한 방에 들어설 때처럼 차분한 태도로 들어왔다."]], [["“Where do I come from?”", "\"제가 어디서 왔느냐고요?\""], ["he said, in answer to a question from the ambassador’s wife.", "그가 대사 부인의 물음에 답하며 말했다."], ["“Well, there’s no help for it, I must confess.", "\"뭐, 어쩔 수 없이 고백해야겠군요."], ["From the opera bouffe.", "opera bouffe에서 왔습니다."], ["I do believe I’ve seen it a hundred times, and always with fresh enjoyment.", "정말이지 백 번은 본 것 같은데, 볼 때마다 새롭게 즐겁단 말이에요."], ["It’s exquisite!", "일품이지요!"], ["I know it’s disgraceful, but I go to sleep at the opera, and I sit out the opera bouffe to the last minute, and enjoy it.", "부끄러운 일인 줄은 알지만, 저는 오페라에서는 잠이 들어 버리면서도 opera bouffe는 마지막 순간까지 자리에 앉아 즐긴답니다."], ["This evening....”", "오늘 저녁에는…….\""]], [["He mentioned a French actress, and was going to tell something about her; but the ambassador’s wife, with playful horror, cut him short.", "그는 어느 프랑스 여배우의 이름을 대며 그녀에 관한 이야기를 하려 했지만, 대사 부인이 짐짓 질겁하는 시늉을 하며 그의 말을 잘랐다."]], [["“Please don’t tell us about that horror.”", "\"제발 그 끔찍한 이야기는 하지 마세요.\""]], [["“All right, I won’t especially as everyone knows those horrors.”", "\"알겠습니다, 안 하지요. 더구나 그 끔찍한 이야기들은 다들 아실 테니까요.\""]], [["“And we should all go to see them if it were accepted as the correct thing, like the opera,”", "\"그리고 그게 오페라처럼 점잖은 일로 받아들여진다면 우리도 다 보러 갈 거고요.\""], ["chimed in Princess Myakaya.", "먀흐카야 공작부인이 끼어들며 말했다."]], [["# Chapter 7", "# Chapter 7"]], [["Steps were heard at the door, and Princess Betsy, knowing it was Madame Karenina, glanced at Vronsky.", "문가에서 발소리가 들리자 베시 공작부인은 그것이 카레니나 부인임을 알아채고 브론스키를 흘끗 바라보았다."], ["He was looking towards the door, and his face wore a strange new expression.", "그는 문 쪽을 보고 있었고, 그의 얼굴에는 야릇한 새로운 표정이 떠올라 있었다."], ["Joyfully, intently, and at the same time timidly, he gazed at the approaching figure, and slowly he rose to his feet.", "기쁘게, 골똘하게, 그러면서도 동시에 머뭇거리며, 그는 다가오는 자태를 응시했고, 천천히 자리에서 일어섰다."], ["Anna walked into the drawing-room.", "안나가 응접실로 걸어 들어왔다."], ["Holding herself extremely erect, as always, looking straight before her, and moving with her swift, resolute, and light step, that distinguished her from all other society women, she crossed the short space to her hostess, shook hands with her, smiled, and with the same smile looked around at Vronsky.", "늘 그렇듯 몸을 더없이 꼿꼿이 세우고, 똑바로 앞을 바라보며, 다른 모든 사교계 여인들과 그녀를 구별 짓는 그 빠르고 단호하고 가벼운 걸음걸이로, 그녀는 안주인까지의 짧은 거리를 가로질러 그녀와 손을 맞잡고 미소 지었으며, 그 똑같은 미소로 브론스키 쪽을 돌아보았다."], ["Vronsky bowed low and pushed a chair up for her.", "브론스키는 깊이 머리를 숙이고 그녀에게 의자를 밀어 권했다."]], [["She acknowledged this only by a slight nod, flushed a little, and frowned.", "그녀는 가볍게 고개를 까딱하는 것으로만 그에게 답하고는, 얼굴을 살짝 붉히며 미간을 찌푸렸다."], ["But immediately, while rapidly greeting her acquaintances, and shaking the hands proffered to her, she addressed Princess Betsy:", "그러나 곧장 지인들과 빠르게 인사를 나누고 자기에게 내미는 손들을 맞잡으면서 베시 공작부인에게 말을 건넸다."]], [["“I have been at Countess Lidia’s, and meant to have come here earlier, but I stayed on.", "\"리디아 백작부인 댁에 있었어요. 더 일찍 오려고 했는데 그만 눌러앉아 버렸지 뭐예요."], ["Sir John was there.", "거기에 존 경이 와 있었거든요."], ["He’s very interesting.”", "아주 흥미로운 분이에요.\""]], [["“Oh, that’s this missionary?”", "\"오, 그 선교사 말이군요?\""]], [["“Yes; he told us about the life in India, most interesting things.”", "\"네, 그분이 인도에서의 삶에 대해 이야기해 주셨는데, 정말 흥미로운 것들이었어요.\""]], [["The conversation, interrupted by her coming in, flickered up again like the light of a lamp being blown out.", "그녀가 들어오는 바람에 끊겼던 대화가 마치 꺼져 가던 등불의 불빛처럼 다시 너울너울 살아났다."]], [["“Sir John!", "\"존 경!"], ["Yes, Sir John; I’ve seen him.", "그래요, 존 경. 저도 그분을 본 적이 있어요."], ["He speaks well.", "말씀을 잘하시지요."], ["The Vlassieva girl’s quite in love with him.”", "블라시예바 집안 아가씨가 그분에게 홀딱 반해 있어요.\""]], [["“And is it true the younger Vlassieva girl’s to marry Topov?”", "\"그런데 작은 블라시예바 아가씨가 토포프와 결혼한다는 게 사실인가요?\""]], [["“Yes, they say it’s quite a settled thing.”", "\"네, 다들 거의 정해진 일이라고 하더군요.\""]], [["“I wonder at the parents!", "\"부모들이 놀라워요!"], ["They say it’s a marriage for love.”", "사랑해서 하는 결혼이라던데요.\""]], [["“For love?", "\"사랑이라고요?"], ["What antediluvian notions you have!", "어쩜 그렇게 케케묵은 생각을 갖고 계세요!"], ["Can one talk of love in these days?”", "요즘 세상에 사랑을 입에 올릴 수 있나요?\""], ["said the ambassador’s wife.", "대사 부인이 말했다."]], [["“What’s to be done?", "\"어쩌겠습니까?"], ["It’s a foolish old fashion that’s kept up still,”", "아직도 명맥을 잇고 있는 어리석은 옛 풍습이지요.\""], ["said Vronsky.", "브론스키가 말했다."]], [["“So much the worse for those who keep up the fashion.", "\"그 풍습을 따르는 사람들에게나 더 안된 노릇이지요."], ["The only happy marriages I know are marriages of prudence.”", "제가 아는 행복한 결혼이라곤 분별에 따른 결혼뿐이에요.\""]], [["“Yes, but then how often the happiness of these prudent marriages flies away like dust just because that passion turns up that they have refused to recognize,”", "\"그래요, 하지만 그렇게 분별 있는 결혼의 행복이라는 게, 인정하기를 거부했던 바로 그 열정이 불쑥 나타나는 바람에 얼마나 자주 먼지처럼 날아가 버립니까.\""], ["said Vronsky.", "브론스키가 말했다."]], [["“But by marriages of prudence we mean those in which both parties have sown their wild oats already.", "\"하지만 분별에 따른 결혼이라고 할 때, 우리는 양쪽 다 이미 젊은 날의 객기를 다 부려 본 경우를 두고 하는 말이에요."], ["That’s like scarlatina—one has to go through it and get it over.”", "그건 성홍열 같은 거지요. 한 번 앓고 넘겨야 하는 거예요.\""]], [["“Then they ought to find out how to vaccinate for love, like smallpox.”", "\"그렇다면 천연두처럼 사랑에도 예방 접종하는 법을 알아내야겠군요.\""]], [["“I was in love in my young days with a deacon,”", "\"나는 젊은 시절에 어느 부제와 사랑에 빠진 적이 있어요.\""], ["said the Princess Myakaya.", "먀흐카야 공작부인이 말했다."], ["“I don’t know that it did me any good.”", "\"그게 내게 무슨 도움이 됐는지는 모르겠지만요.\""]], [["“No; I imagine, joking apart, that to know love, one must make mistakes and then correct them,”", "\"아니에요, 제 생각엔, 농담은 접어 두고, 사랑을 알려면 실수를 저지르고 그것을 바로잡아야 하는 것 같아요.\""], ["said Princess Betsy.", "베시 공작부인이 말했다."]], [["“Even after marriage?”", "\"결혼한 다음에도요?\""], ["said the ambassador’s wife playfully.", "대사 부인이 장난스럽게 말했다."]], [["“‘It’s never too late to mend.’”", "\"'고치는 데 너무 늦은 때란 없다.'\""], ["The attaché repeated the English proverb.", "외교관이 그 영국 속담을 되뇌었다."]], [["“Just so,”", "\"바로 그거예요.\""], ["Betsy agreed; “one must make mistakes and correct them.", "베시가 맞장구쳤다. \"실수를 저지르고 그것을 바로잡아야 하는 거지요."], ["What do you think about it?”", "당신은 어떻게 생각하세요?\""], ["she turned to Anna, who, with a faintly perceptible resolute smile on her lips, was listening in silence to the conversation.", "그녀는 입가에 어렴풋이 드러나는 단호한 미소를 띤 채 말없이 대화에 귀 기울이고 있던 안나에게 돌아보며 물었다."]], [["“I think,”", "\"제 생각엔,\""], ["said Anna, playing with the glove she had taken off, “I think ...", "안나는 벗어 둔 장갑을 만지작거리며 말했다. \"제 생각엔……"], ["of so many men, so many minds, certainly so many hearts, so many kinds of love.”", "사람이 많으면 많은 만큼 생각도 가지가지이듯, 분명 마음도 가지가지일 테고, 사랑의 종류도 가지가지일 거예요.\""]], [["Vronsky was gazing at Anna, and with a fainting heart waiting for what she would say.", "브론스키는 안나를 응시하며, 가슴이 졸아드는 심정으로 그녀가 무슨 말을 할지 기다리고 있었다."], ["He sighed as after a danger escaped when she uttered these words.", "그녀가 그 말을 입에 올렸을 때, 그는 위기를 모면한 것처럼 한숨을 내쉬었다."]], [["Anna suddenly turned to him.", "안나가 별안간 그에게로 돌아섰다."]], [["“Oh, I have had a letter from Moscow.", "\"아, 모스크바에서 편지가 왔어요."], ["They write me that Kitty Shtcherbatskaya’s very ill.”", "키티 셰르바츠카야가 몹시 아프다고 쓰여 있더군요.\""]], [["“Really?”", "\"정말입니까?\""], ["said Vronsky, knitting his brows.", "브론스키가 미간을 찌푸리며 말했다."]], [["Anna looked sternly at him.", "안나가 그를 엄하게 바라보았다."]], [["“That doesn’t interest you?”", "\"그게 당신에겐 관심거리가 아닌가요?\""]], [["“On the contrary, it does, very much.", "\"그 반대입니다, 아주 관심이 있어요."], ["What was it exactly they told you, if I may know?”", "정확히 뭐라고 알려 왔는지, 여쭤도 될까요?\""], ["he questioned.", "그가 물었다."]], [["Anna got up and went to Betsy.", "안나가 일어나 베시에게로 갔다."]], [["“Give me a cup of tea,”", "\"차 한 잔 주세요.\""], ["she said, standing at her table.", "그녀가 베시의 탁자 앞에 서서 말했다."]], [["While Betsy was pouring out the tea, Vronsky went up to Anna.", "베시가 차를 따르는 동안 브론스키는 안나에게 다가갔다."]], [["“What is it they write to you?”", "\"그쪽에서 뭐라고 써 보냈습니까?\""], ["he repeated.", "그가 거듭 물었다."]], [["“I often think men have no understanding of what’s not honorable though they’re always talking of it,”", "\"남자들은 늘 명예에 대해 입에 올리면서도 정작 명예롭지 못한 게 무엇인지에 대해선 도무지 모르는 것 같아요.\""], ["said Anna, without answering him.", "안나는 그의 물음에는 답하지 않고 말했다."], ["“I’ve wanted to tell you so a long while,”", "\"이 말을 오래전부터 당신에게 하고 싶었어요.\""], ["she added, and moving a few steps away, she sat down at a table in a corner covered with albums.", "그녀는 그렇게 덧붙이고는 몇 걸음 옮겨 가, 앨범들이 놓인 구석의 탁자에 앉았다."]], [["“I don’t quite understand the meaning of your words,”", "\"당신 말씀의 의미를 잘 모르겠습니다.\""], ["he said, handing her the cup.", "그가 찻잔을 건네며 말했다."]], [["She glanced towards the sofa beside her, and he instantly sat down.", "그녀가 자기 옆의 소파 쪽으로 눈길을 던지자, 그가 즉시 그곳에 앉았다."]], [["“Yes, I have been wanting to tell you,”", "\"네, 줄곧 당신에게 말하고 싶었어요.\""], ["she said, not looking at him.", "그녀는 그를 보지 않은 채 말했다."], ["“You behaved wrongly, very wrongly.”", "\"당신은 잘못 처신했어요, 아주 잘못요.\""]], [["“Do you suppose I don’t know that I’ve acted wrongly?", "\"내가 잘못 처신했다는 걸 모를 것 같습니까?"], ["But who was the cause of my doing so?”", "하지만 내가 그렇게 한 게 누구 탓이었지요?\""]], [["“What do you say that to me for?”", "\"무엇 때문에 그런 말을 제게 하는 거죠?\""], ["she said, glancing severely at him.", "그녀가 그를 엄하게 흘끗 보며 말했다."]], [["“You know what for,”", "\"무엇 때문인지 당신도 알지 않습니까.\""], ["he answered boldly and joyfully, meeting her glance and not dropping his eyes.", "그가 그녀의 눈길을 마주하며 눈을 떨어뜨리지도 않은 채, 대담하고 기쁘게 대답했다."]], [["Not he, but she, was confused.", "당황한 것은 그가 아니라 그녀였다."]], [["“That only shows you have no heart,”", "\"그건 당신에게 마음이 없다는 걸 보여 줄 뿐이에요.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["But her eyes said that she knew he had a heart, and that was why she was afraid of him.", "그러나 그녀의 눈은 그에게 마음이 있다는 것을, 그리고 바로 그래서 자기가 그를 두려워한다는 것을 말하고 있었다."]], [["“What you spoke of just now was a mistake, and not love.”", "\"방금 당신이 말한 그것은 실수였지, 사랑이 아니었습니다.\""]], [["“Remember that I have forbidden you to utter that word, that hateful word,”", "\"그 단어를, 그 끔찍한 단어를 입에 올리는 것을 내가 금했다는 걸 잊지 마세요.\""], ["said Anna, with a shudder.", "안나가 몸서리를 치며 말했다."], ["But at once she felt that by that very word “forbidden” she had shown that she acknowledged certain rights over him, and by that very fact was encouraging him to speak of love.", "그러나 그녀는 곧, 바로 그 '금했다'는 말로써 자기가 그에 대해 어떤 권리를 인정하고 있음을 드러냈고, 바로 그 사실로 인해 그가 사랑을 입에 올리도록 부추기고 있음을 느꼈다."], ["“I have long meant to tell you this,”", "\"오래전부터 이 말을 당신에게 하려고 했어요.\""], ["she went on, looking resolutely into his eyes, and hot all over from the burning flush on her cheeks.", "그녀는 단호하게 그의 눈을 들여다보며, 두 뺨에 타오르는 홍조로 온몸이 달아오른 채 말을 이었다."], ["“I’ve come on purpose this evening, knowing I should meet you.", "\"당신을 만나게 되리라는 걸 알고서 오늘 저녁 일부러 온 거예요."], ["I have come to tell you that this must end.", "이 일을 끝내야 한다고 당신에게 말하려고 왔어요."], ["I have never blushed before anyone, and you force me to feel to blame for something.”", "나는 누구 앞에서도 얼굴을 붉혀 본 적이 없는데, 당신은 내가 무언가에 대해 죄책감을 느끼게 만들어요.\""]], [["He looked at her and was struck by a new spiritual beauty in her face.", "그는 그녀를 바라보았고, 그녀의 얼굴에 깃든 새로운 영적인 아름다움에 충격을 받았다."]], [["“What do you wish of me?”", "\"내게 무엇을 바라십니까?\""], ["he said simply and seriously.", "그가 단순하고 진지하게 말했다."]], [["“I want you to go to Moscow and ask for Kitty’s forgiveness,”", "\"모스크바로 가서 키티에게 용서를 구하길 바라요.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“You don’t wish that?”", "\"당신은 그걸 바라지 않잖습니까?\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["He saw she was saying what she forced herself to say, not what she wanted to say.", "그녀가 하고 싶은 말이 아니라 억지로 자신에게 시킨 말을 하고 있음을 그는 알아챘다."]], [["“If you love me, as you say,”", "\"당신 말처럼 정말 나를 사랑한다면,\""], ["she whispered, “do so that I may be at peace.”", "그녀가 속삭였다. \"내가 평안할 수 있도록 그렇게 해 줘요.\""]], [["His face grew radiant.", "그의 얼굴이 환하게 빛났다."]], [["“Don’t you know that you’re all my life to me?", "\"당신이 내겐 삶의 전부라는 걸 모르십니까?"], ["But I know no peace, and I can’t give it to you; all myself—and love ...", "하지만 나는 평안을 모르고, 당신에게 그것을 줄 수도 없습니다. 내 자신 전부를—그리고 사랑을……"], ["yes.", "그래요."], ["I can’t think of you and myself apart.", "나는 당신과 나를 따로 떼어 놓고는 생각할 수가 없어요."], ["You and I are one to me.", "내게 당신과 나는 하나입니다."], ["And I see no chance before us of peace for me or for you.", "그리고 나는 우리 앞에 나에게든 당신에게든 평안의 가능성이 보이지 않아요."], ["I see a chance of despair, of wretchedness ...", "절망의 가능성, 비참함의 가능성이 보이거나……"], ["or I see a chance of bliss, what bliss!...", "아니면 더없는 행복의 가능성, 그 얼마나 큰 행복입니까!……"], ["Can it be there’s no chance of it?”", "그것이 정녕 불가능할 수가 있겠습니까?\""], ["he murmured with his lips; but she heard.", "그가 입술로 중얼거렸지만, 그녀에게는 들렸다."]], [["She strained every effort of her mind to say what ought to be said.", "그녀는 마땅히 해야 할 말을 하려고 온 정신의 힘을 쥐어짰다."], ["But instead of that she let her eyes rest on him, full of love, and made no answer.", "그러나 그러기는커녕 사랑이 가득 담긴 눈길을 그에게 머무르게 한 채 아무런 대답도 하지 않았다."]], [["“It’s come!”", "'왔어!'"], ["he thought in ecstasy.", "그는 황홀경 속에서 생각했다."], ["“When I was beginning to despair, and it seemed there would be no end—it’s come!", "'내가 절망하기 시작하고, 끝이라곤 없을 것 같던 바로 그때—그것이 왔어! 그녀가 나를 사랑해!"], ["She loves me! She owns it!”", "그녀가 그것을 인정하고 있어!'"]], [["“Then do this for me: never say such things to me, and let us be friends,”", "\"그러니 나를 위해 이렇게 해 줘요. 다시는 그런 말을 제게 하지 말고, 우리 친구로 지내요.\""], ["she said in words; but her eyes spoke quite differently.", "그녀가 말로는 그렇게 했지만, 그녀의 눈은 전혀 다른 말을 하고 있었다."]], [["“Friends we shall never be, you know that yourself.", "\"우리는 결코 친구가 될 수 없어요, 당신 자신도 그걸 알지 않습니까."], ["Whether we shall be the happiest or the wretchedest of people—that’s in your hands.”", "우리가 세상에서 가장 행복한 사람이 될지 가장 비참한 사람이 될지—그건 당신 손에 달려 있습니다.\""]], [["She would have said something, but he interrupted her.", "그녀가 무슨 말인가 하려 했지만, 그가 가로막았다."]], [["“I ask one thing only: I ask for the right to hope, to suffer as I do.", "\"나는 단 한 가지만을 청합니다. 희망할 권리를, 지금 이대로 괴로워할 권리를 청해요."], ["But if even that cannot be, command me to disappear, and I disappear.", "하지만 그것조차 안 된다면, 내게 사라지라고 명령하세요, 그러면 사라지겠습니다."], ["You shall not see me if my presence is distasteful to you.”", "내 존재가 당신에게 거슬린다면, 당신은 나를 다시는 보지 않게 될 겁니다.\""]], [["“I don’t want to drive you away.”", "\"당신을 내쫓고 싶진 않아요.\""]], [["“Only don’t change anything, leave everything as it is,”", "\"그저 아무것도 바꾸지 말아 주세요, 모든 걸 지금 그대로 두세요.\""], ["he said in a shaky voice.", "그가 떨리는 목소리로 말했다."], ["“Here’s your husband.”", "\"당신 남편이 오시네요.\""]], [["At that instant Alexey Alexandrovitch did in fact walk into the room with his calm, awkward gait.", "바로 그 순간, 실제로 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 차분하면서도 어색한 걸음걸이로 방 안으로 걸어 들어왔다."]], [["Glancing at his wife and Vronsky, he went up to the lady of the house, and sitting down for a cup of tea, began talking in his deliberate, always audible voice, in his habitual tone of banter, ridiculing someone.", "그는 아내와 브론스키를 흘끗 보고는 안주인에게로 가서, 차 한 잔을 마시려고 자리에 앉아, 늘 또렷이 들리는 신중한 목소리로, 누군가를 비웃는 평소의 농조로 이야기를 시작했다."]], [["“Your Rambouillet is in full conclave,”", "\"댁의 랑부예가 한창 회동 중이로군요.\""], ["he said, looking round at all the party; “the graces and the muses.”", "그가 좌중을 둘러보며 말했다. \"미의 여신들과 시의 여신들이 다 모였어요.\""]], [["But Princess Betsy could not endure that tone of his—“sneering,” as she called it, using the English word, and like a skillful hostess she at once brought him into a serious conversation on the subject of universal conscription.", "그러나 베시 공작부인은 그가 영국 단어를 써 가며 '냉소적'이라고 부른 그런 어조를 견디지 못했고, 능란한 안주인답게 곧바로 그를 국민 개병제라는 주제에 관한 진지한 대화로 끌어들였다."], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch was immediately interested in the subject, and began seriously defending the new imperial decree against Princess Betsy, who had attacked it.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 즉시 그 주제에 흥미를 느꼈고, 그것을 공격했던 베시 공작부인에 맞서 새로운 제국 칙령을 진지하게 옹호하기 시작했다."]], [["Vronsky and Anna still sat at the little table.", "브론스키와 안나는 여전히 작은 탁자에 앉아 있었다."]], [["“This is getting indecorous,”", "\"이건 점점 꼴사나워지는데요.\""], ["whispered one lady, with an expressive glance at Madame Karenina, Vronsky, and her husband.", "한 부인이 카레니나 부인과 브론스키와 그녀의 남편을 의미심장하게 흘끗 보며 속삭였다."]], [["“What did I tell you?”", "\"제가 뭐라고 했어요?\""], ["said Anna’s friend.", "안나의 친구가 말했다."]], [["But not only those ladies, almost everyone in the room, even the Princess Myakaya and Betsy herself, looked several times in the direction of the two who had withdrawn from the general circle, as though that were a disturbing fact.", "그러나 그 부인들뿐 아니라 방 안의 거의 모든 사람들이, 심지어 먀흐카야 공작부인과 베시 자신까지도, 그것이 무언가 거슬리는 사실이라도 되는 양 전체 무리에서 떨어져 나간 그 두 사람 쪽을 여러 차례 바라보았다."], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch was the only person who did not once look in that direction, and was not diverted from the interesting discussion he had entered upon.", "그 방향을 단 한 번도 보지 않은 사람은 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치뿐이었고, 그는 자기가 빠져든 흥미로운 토론에서 한눈을 팔지 않았다."]], [["Noticing the disagreeable impression that was being made on everyone, Princess Betsy slipped someone else into her place to listen to Alexey Alexandrovitch, and went up to Anna.", "베시 공작부인은 모두에게 불쾌한 인상이 새겨지고 있음을 알아채고는, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 말을 들을 사람을 다른 누군가로 슬쩍 바꿔 자기 자리에 들여보낸 뒤, 안나에게로 갔다."]], [["“I’m always amazed at the clearness and precision of your husband’s language,”", "\"나는 당신 남편의 말솜씨가 그토록 명료하고 정확한 데 늘 놀라곤 해요.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“The most transcendental ideas seem to be within my grasp when he’s speaking.”", "\"그분이 말씀하실 때면 더없이 초월적인 관념들조차 내 손에 잡힐 것만 같다니까요.\""]], [["“Oh, yes!”", "\"오, 그래요!\""], ["said Anna, radiant with a smile of happiness, and not understanding a word of what Betsy had said.", "안나는 행복한 미소로 환하게 빛나며 말했지만, 베시가 한 말을 한마디도 알아듣지 못했다."], ["She crossed over to the big table and took part in the general conversation.", "그녀는 큰 탁자 쪽으로 건너가 전체 대화에 끼어들었다."]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch, after staying half an hour, went up to his wife and suggested that they should go home together.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 삼십 분쯤 머문 뒤, 아내에게 다가가 함께 집으로 돌아가자고 권했다."], ["But she answered, not looking at him, that she was staying to supper.", "그러나 그녀는 그를 보지도 않은 채 저녁 식사까지 남아 있겠다고 대답했다."], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch made his bows and withdrew.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 인사를 하고 물러갔다."]], [["The fat old Tatar, Madame Karenina’s coachman, was with difficulty holding one of her pair of grays, chilled with the cold and rearing at the entrance.", "카레니나 부인의 마부인 뚱뚱한 늙은 타타르인은, 추위에 얼어붙어 입구에서 앞발을 쳐드는 그녀의 잿빛 말 한 쌍 중 한 마리를 간신히 붙들고 있었다."], ["A footman stood opening the carriage door.", "하인 하나가 마차 문을 열고 서 있었다."], ["The hall-porter stood holding open the great door of the house.", "문지기는 저택의 큰 문을 활짝 열어 잡고 서 있었다."], ["Anna Arkadyevna, with her quick little hand, was unfastening the lace of her sleeve, caught in the hook of her fur cloak, and with bent head listening to the words Vronsky murmured as he escorted her down.", "안나 아르카지예브나는 자기의 잰 작은 손으로, 모피 외투 갈고리에 걸린 소맷자락의 레이스를 풀어내며, 고개를 숙인 채 자기를 배웅하며 브론스키가 중얼거리는 말에 귀를 기울이고 있었다."]], [["“You’ve said nothing, of course, and I ask nothing,”", "\"당신은 물론 아무 말도 하지 않았고, 나도 아무것도 청하지 않습니다.\" 그가 말하고 있었다."], ["he was saying; “but you know that friendship’s not what I want: that there’s only one happiness in life for me, that word that you dislike so ...", "\"하지만 내가 바라는 게 우정이 아니라는 걸 당신은 알지 않습니까. 내 삶에 행복이라곤 단 하나뿐이라는 걸, 당신이 그토록 싫어하는 그 단어……"], ["yes, love!...”", "그래요, 사랑이라는 걸요!……\""]], [["“Love,”", "\"사랑.\""], ["she repeated slowly, in an inner voice, and suddenly, at the very instant she unhooked the lace, she added, “Why I don’t like the word is that it means too much to me, far more than you can understand,”", "그녀는 속으로 삼키는 목소리로 천천히 되뇌었다가, 레이스를 갈고리에서 막 풀어낸 바로 그 순간 불쑥 덧붙였다. \"내가 그 단어를 싫어하는 건 그것이 내겐 너무 많은 의미를 지니기 때문이에요, 당신이 이해할 수 있는 것보다 훨씬 더요.\""], ["and she glanced into his face.", "그러고는 그의 얼굴을 흘끗 들여다보았다."], ["“Au revoir!”", "\"Au revoir!\""]], [["She gave him her hand, and with her rapid, springy step she passed by the porter and vanished into the carriage.", "그녀는 그에게 손을 내밀고는, 잰 탄력 있는 걸음으로 문지기 곁을 지나 마차 안으로 사라졌다."]], [["Her glance, the touch of her hand, set him aflame.", "그녀의 눈길, 그녀의 손길이 그를 활활 타오르게 했다."], ["He kissed the palm of his hand where she had touched it, and went home, happy in the sense that he had got nearer to the attainment of his aims that evening than during the last two months.", "그는 그녀가 닿았던 자기 손바닥에 입을 맞추고, 그날 저녁 자기가 지난 두 달 동안보다 자기 목표의 달성에 더 가까이 다가갔다는 느낌에 행복해하며 집으로 돌아갔다. * Au revoir: 프랑스어로 '안녕히 가세요, 또 만나요'."]], [["# Chapter 8", "# Chapter 8"]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch had seen nothing striking or improper in the fact that his wife was sitting with Vronsky at a table apart, in eager conversation with him about something.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 자기 아내가 브론스키와 함께 따로 떨어진 탁자에 앉아 무언가에 대해 열심히 이야기를 나누고 있다는 사실에서 별다른 이상함이나 부적절함을 보지 못했다."], ["But he noticed that to the rest of the party this appeared something striking and improper, and for that reason it seemed to him too to be improper.", "하지만 그 자리에 있던 다른 사람들에게는 그것이 무언가 두드러지고 부적절하게 보인다는 것을 알아차렸고, 그 때문에 그에게도 그것이 부적절하게 여겨졌다."], ["He made up his mind that he must speak of it to his wife.", "그는 이 일에 대해 아내에게 말해야겠다고 마음먹었다."]], [["On reaching home Alexey Alexandrovitch went to his study, as he usually did, seated himself in his low chair, opened a book on the Papacy at the place where he had laid the paper-knife in it, and read till one o’clock, just as he usually did.", "집에 도착한 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 평소처럼 서재로 가서 나지막한 안락의자에 앉았고, 페이퍼나이프를 끼워 두었던 자리에서 교황권에 관한 책을 펼쳐 평소처럼 한 시까지 읽었다."], ["But from time to time he rubbed his high forehead and shook his head, as though to drive away something.", "하지만 이따금 그는 무언가를 떨쳐 내려는 듯 넓은 이마를 문지르고 고개를 흔들었다."], ["At his usual time he got up and made his toilet for the night.", "평소와 같은 시각에 그는 일어나 잠자리에 들 채비를 했다."], ["Anna Arkadyevna had not yet come in.", "안나 아르카지예브나는 아직 돌아오지 않았다."], ["With a book under his arm he went upstairs.", "그는 책을 옆구리에 끼고 위층으로 올라갔다."], ["But this evening, instead of his usual thoughts and meditations upon official details, his thoughts were absorbed by his wife and something disagreeable connected with her.", "하지만 이날 저녁에는 평소처럼 공무의 세세한 사항에 대한 생각과 사색에 잠기는 대신, 그의 생각이 온통 아내와 그녀에게 얽힌 무언가 언짢은 일에 사로잡혀 있었다."], ["Contrary to his usual habit, he did not get into bed, but fell to walking up and down the rooms with his hands clasped behind his back.", "그는 평소의 습관과 달리 침대에 들지 않고 두 손을 등 뒤로 맞잡은 채 방들을 이리저리 거닐기 시작했다."], ["He could not go to bed, feeling that it was absolutely needful for him first to think thoroughly over the position that had just arisen.", "그는 잠자리에 들 수가 없었다. 무엇보다 먼저 방금 일어난 상황을 철저히 따져 보는 일이 절대적으로 필요하다고 느꼈기 때문이다."]], [["When Alexey Alexandrovitch had made up his mind that he must talk to his wife about it, it had seemed a very easy and simple matter.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 이 일에 대해 아내와 이야기를 나눠야겠다고 마음먹었을 때만 해도, 그것은 아주 쉽고 간단한 일처럼 여겨졌다."], ["But now, when he began to think over the question that had just presented itself, it seemed to him very complicated and difficult.", "하지만 이제 막 떠오른 그 문제를 곰곰이 생각하기 시작하자, 그것이 그에게 몹시 복잡하고 어렵게 느껴졌다."]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch was not jealous.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 질투하는 사람이 아니었다."], ["Jealousy according to his notions was an insult to one’s wife, and one ought to have confidence in one’s wife.", "그의 생각으로 질투란 아내에 대한 모욕이며, 사람은 자기 아내를 믿어야 마땅했다."], ["Why one ought to have confidence—that is to say, complete conviction that his young wife would always love him—he did not ask himself.", "왜 믿어야 마땅한가—다시 말해 자기의 젊은 아내가 언제까지나 자기를 사랑하리라고 왜 완전히 확신해야 하는가—를 그는 스스로에게 묻지 않았다."], ["But he had no experience of lack of confidence, because he had confidence in her, and told himself that he ought to have it.", "하지만 그는 믿음의 결핍이라는 것을 경험한 적이 없었으니, 아내를 믿고 있었고 또 마땅히 믿어야 한다고 스스로에게 말해 왔기 때문이다."], ["Now, though his conviction that jealousy was a shameful feeling and that one ought to feel confidence, had not broken down, he felt that he was standing face to face with something illogical and irrational, and did not know what was to be done.", "이제 질투가 수치스러운 감정이며 사람은 마땅히 믿음을 품어야 한다는 그의 확신이 무너지지는 않았지만, 그는 자기가 무언가 비논리적이고 불합리한 것과 정면으로 마주하고 있음을 느꼈고, 어찌해야 할지 알지 못했다."], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch was standing face to face with life, with the possibility of his wife’s loving someone other than himself, and this seemed to him very irrational and incomprehensible because it was life itself.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 삶과, 자기 아내가 자기 아닌 다른 누군가를 사랑할 수 있다는 가능성과 정면으로 마주하고 있었으며, 이것이 그에게는 몹시 불합리하고 이해할 수 없는 일로 여겨졌으니, 그것이 바로 삶 그 자체였기 때문이다."], ["All his life Alexey Alexandrovitch had lived and worked in official spheres, having to do with the reflection of life.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 평생을 삶의 반영을 다루는 공무의 영역에서 살고 일해 왔다."], ["And every time he had stumbled against life itself he had shrunk away from it.", "그리고 삶 그 자체에 부딪칠 때마다 그는 그것으로부터 움츠러들었다."], ["Now he experienced a feeling akin to that of a man who, while calmly crossing a precipice by a bridge, should suddenly discover that the bridge is broken, and that there is a chasm below.", "이제 그는 다리를 건너 벼랑을 차분히 지나가다가, 별안간 다리가 끊어져 있고 그 아래에 심연이 입을 벌리고 있음을 발견한 사람의 심정과 비슷한 감정을 맛보았다."], ["That chasm was life itself, the bridge that artificial life in which Alexey Alexandrovitch had lived.", "그 심연은 삶 그 자체였고, 다리는 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 살아온 그 인위적인 삶이었다."], ["For the first time the question presented itself to him of the possibility of his wife’s loving someone else, and he was horrified at it.", "난생처음 자기 아내가 다른 누군가를 사랑할 수도 있다는 의문이 그에게 떠올랐고, 그는 그것에 소스라쳤다."]], [["He did not undress, but walked up and down with his regular tread over the resounding parquet of the dining-room, where one lamp was burning, over the carpet of the dark drawing-room, in which the light was reflected on the big new portrait of himself hanging over the sofa, and across her boudoir, where two candles burned, lighting up the portraits of her parents and woman friends, and the pretty knick-knacks of her writing-table, that he knew so well.", "그는 옷도 벗지 않은 채, 등불 하나가 타고 있는 식당의 울리는 마룻바닥 위를, 소파 위에 걸린 자기의 큼직한 새 초상화에 빛이 반사되는 어두운 응접실의 양탄자 위를, 그리고 두 자루의 양초가 타며 그녀 부모와 여자 친구들의 초상화며 그가 너무도 잘 아는 그녀 책상의 예쁜 자질구레한 장식품들을 비추고 있는 그녀의 내실을 가로질러 일정한 걸음으로 거닐었다. 그는 그녀의 내실을 가로질러 침실 문까지 갔다가 다시 돌아섰다."], ["He walked across her boudoir to the bedroom door, and turned back again.", "걸음을 돌릴 때마다, 특히 불 밝힌 식당의 마룻바닥에서, 그는 멈춰 서서 스스로에게 말했다."], ["At each turn in his walk, especially at the parquet of the lighted dining-room, he halted and said to himself, “Yes, this I must decide and put a stop to; I must express my view of it and my decision.”", "\"그래, 이건 내가 결단을 내려 끝장을 봐야 해. 이에 대한 내 견해와 결정을 밝혀야 해.\""], ["And he turned back again.", "그러고는 다시 돌아섰다."], ["“But express what—what decision?”", "\"하지만 무엇을 밝힌단 말인가—무슨 결정을?\""], ["he said to himself in the drawing-room, and he found no reply.", "응접실에서 그는 스스로에게 물었지만, 아무런 답도 찾지 못했다."], ["“But after all,”", "\"하지만 결국,\""], ["he asked himself before turning into the boudoir, “what has occurred? Nothing.", "그는 내실로 돌아서기 전에 스스로에게 물었다."], ["She was talking a long while with him.", "\"무슨 일이 일어났단 말인가?"], ["But what of that?", "아무 일도 없었어."], ["Surely women in society can talk to whom they please.", "그녀가 그와 오랫동안 이야기를 나눴지. 하지만 그게 어쨌단 말인가?"], ["And then, jealousy means lowering both myself and her,”", "사교계의 여자라면 마음에 드는 누구와도 이야기할 수 있는 법이지."], ["he told himself as he went into her boudoir; but this dictum, which had always had such weight with him before, had now no weight and no meaning at all.", "게다가 질투란 나 자신과 그녀를 둘 다 깎아내리는 짓이야.\" 그녀의 내실로 들어서면서 그는 스스로에게 말했다."], ["And from the bedroom door he turned back again; but as he entered the dark drawing-room some inner voice told him that it was not so, and that if others noticed it that showed that there was something.", "그러나 전에는 그토록 무게를 지녔던 이 격언이 이제는 아무런 무게도 의미도 지니지 못했다. 그리고 침실 문에서 그는 또다시 돌아섰지만, 어두운 응접실로 들어서자 어떤 내면의 목소리가 그렇지 않다고, 다른 이들이 그것을 알아차렸다는 것은 무언가가 있다는 뜻이라고 말했다."], ["And he said to himself again in the dining-room, “Yes, I must decide and put a stop to it, and express my view of it....”", "그래서 식당에서 그는 또다시 스스로에게 말했다. \"그래, 나는 결단을 내려 이를 끝장내야 하고, 이에 대한 내 견해를 밝혀야 해……\""], ["And again at the turn in the drawing-room he asked himself, “Decide how?”", "그리고 또다시 응접실의 모퉁이에서 그는 스스로에게 물었다. \"어떻게 결단을 내린단 말인가?\""], ["And again he asked himself, “What had occurred?”", "그리고 또다시 스스로에게 물었다. \"무슨 일이 일어났던가?\""], ["and answered, “Nothing,”", "그러고는 \"아무 일도 없었어.\""], ["and recollected that jealousy was a feeling insulting to his wife; but again in the drawing-room he was convinced that something had happened.", "하고 답했고, 질투란 아내를 모욕하는 감정임을 떠올렸다. 하지만 응접실에서 그는 또다시 무언가 일이 일어났다고 확신했다."], ["His thoughts, like his body, went round a complete circle, without coming upon anything new.", "그의 생각은 그의 몸과 마찬가지로 새로운 것에 이르지 못한 채 완전한 한 바퀴를 돌고 있었다."], ["He noticed this, rubbed his forehead, and sat down in her boudoir.", "그는 이를 알아차리고 이마를 문지른 뒤 그녀의 내실에 앉았다."]], [["There, looking at her table, with the malachite blotting case lying at the top and an unfinished letter, his thoughts suddenly changed.", "거기서 그녀의 책상을, 그 위에 놓인 공작석 압지함과 쓰다 만 편지를 바라보자, 그의 생각이 별안간 바뀌었다."], ["He began to think of her, of what she was thinking and feeling.", "그는 그녀를, 그녀가 무엇을 생각하고 느끼는지를 떠올리기 시작했다."], ["For the first time he pictured vividly to himself her personal life, her ideas, her desires, and the idea that she could and should have a separate life of her own seemed to him so alarming that he made haste to dispel it.", "난생처음 그는 그녀의 개인적인 삶과 그녀의 생각과 욕망을 또렷이 그려 보았고, 그녀가 그녀만의 별개의 삶을 가질 수 있고 또 가져야 한다는 생각이 그에게는 너무도 섬뜩하게 여겨져서 서둘러 그것을 떨쳐 버렸다."], ["It was the chasm which he was afraid to peep into.", "그것은 그가 들여다보기 두려워한 심연이었다."], ["To put himself in thought and feeling in another person’s place was a spiritual exercise not natural to Alexey Alexandrovitch.", "다른 사람의 처지에 자기 자신을 생각과 감정으로 놓아 보는 일은 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에게는 자연스럽지 않은 정신적 훈련이었다."], ["He looked on this spiritual exercise as a harmful and dangerous abuse of the fancy.", "그는 이 정신적 훈련을 공상을 해롭고 위험하게 남용하는 짓으로 여겼다."]], [["“And the worst of it all,” thought he, “is that just now, at the very moment when my great work is approaching completion”", "'그리고 이 모든 일에서 가장 고약한 건,'"], ["(he was thinking of the project he was bringing forward at the time), “when I stand in need of all my mental peace and all my energies, just now this stupid worry should fall foul of me.", "그는 생각했다. '바로 지금, 나의 위대한 과업이 완성에 가까워지는 바로 이 순간에'(그는 그 무렵 추진하고 있던 사업을 생각하고 있었다) '내게 온갖 정신적 평온과 모든 기력이 필요한 바로 이때에, 하필 이 어리석은 걱정거리가 나를 덮친다는 거야."], ["But what’s to be done?", "하지만 어쩌겠나?"], ["I’m not one of those men who submit to uneasiness and worry without having the force of character to face them.", "나는 그것들과 맞설 의지력도 없이 불안과 걱정에 굴복하는 그런 부류의 사내가 아니야.'"]], [["“I must think it over, come to a decision, and put it out of my mind,”", "\"나는 이 일을 따져 보고, 결단에 이르러, 머릿속에서 떨쳐 내야 해.\""], ["he said aloud.", "그는 소리 내어 말했다."]], [["“The question of her feelings, of what has passed and may be passing in her soul, that’s not my affair; that’s the affair of her conscience, and falls under the head of religion,”", "'그녀의 감정에 관한 문제, 그녀의 마음속에서 무슨 일이 지나갔고 또 지나가고 있을지에 관한 문제, 그건 내 소관이 아니야. 그건 그녀의 양심의 일이고, 종교의 영역에 속하는 거지.'"], ["he said to himself, feeling consolation in the sense that he had found to which division of regulating principles this new circumstance could be properly referred.", "그는 스스로에게 말했고, 이 새로운 상황을 규율하는 원리들 가운데 어느 항목에 마땅히 귀속시킬 수 있을지 찾아냈다는 느낌에서 위안을 얻었다."]], [["“And so,”", "'그러니,'"], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch said to himself, “questions as to her feelings, and so on, are questions for her conscience, with which I can have nothing to do.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 스스로에게 말했다. '그녀의 감정 등에 관한 문제들은 그녀의 양심에 속한 문제들이고, 나는 그것과 아무런 상관이 없어."], ["My duty is clearly defined.", "나의 의무는 명백히 규정되어 있어."], ["As the head of the family, I am a person bound in duty to guide her, and consequently, in part the person responsible; I am bound to point out the danger I perceive, to warn her, even to use my authority.", "한 가정의 가장으로서 나는 의무상 그녀를 인도해야 할 사람이고, 따라서 어느 정도는 책임을 진 사람이지. 나는 내가 감지하는 위험을 지적하고, 그녀에게 경고하고, 심지어 내 권위를 행사할 의무가 있어."], ["I ought to speak plainly to her.”", "나는 그녀에게 분명히 말해야 해.'"], ["And everything that he would say tonight to his wife took clear shape in Alexey Alexandrovitch’s head.", "그리고 그가 오늘 밤 아내에게 할 말이 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 머릿속에서 또렷한 형체를 갖추었다."], ["Thinking over what he would say, he somewhat regretted that he should have to use his time and mental powers for domestic consumption, with so little to show for it, but, in spite of that, the form and contents of the speech before him shaped itself as clearly and distinctly in his head as a ministerial report.", "무엇을 말할지 곰곰이 따져 보면서, 그는 그토록 보잘것없는 성과를 위해 자기의 시간과 정신력을 가정사에 써야 한다는 것이 다소 유감스러웠지만, 그럼에도 불구하고 그가 할 연설의 형식과 내용은 마치 장관 보고서처럼 그의 머릿속에서 또렷하고 분명하게 형체를 갖추어 갔다."]], [["“I must say and express fully the following points: first, exposition of the value to be attached to public opinion and to decorum; secondly, exposition of religious significance of marriage; thirdly, if need be, reference to the calamity possibly ensuing to our son; fourthly, reference to the unhappiness likely to result to herself.” And, interlacing his fingers, Alexey Alexandrovitch stretched them, and the joints of the fingers cracked. This trick, a bad habit, the cracking of his fingers, always soothed him, and gave precision to his thoughts, so needful to him at this juncture.", "'나는 다음의 항목들을 말하고 충분히 밝혀야 해. 첫째, 여론과 예법에 부여해야 할 가치에 대한 설명. 둘째, 결혼의 종교적 의의에 대한 설명. 셋째, 필요하다면, 우리 아들에게 일어날 수도 있는 불행에 대한 언급. 넷째, 그녀 자신에게 닥칠 법한 불행에 대한 언급.' 그러고는 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 손가락을 맞물려 펴 보았고, 손가락 마디에서 우두둑 소리가 났다. 손가락을 우두둑 꺾는 이 버릇은, 나쁜 습관이긴 했지만, 늘 그를 진정시키고 그의 생각에 정확성을 부여해 주었으니, 지금 이 고비에서 그에게 너무도 필요한 것이었다."]], [["There was the sound of a carriage driving up to the front door.", "현관 앞으로 마차가 다가오는 소리가 들렸다."], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch halted in the middle of the room.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 방 한가운데에서 멈춰 섰다."]], [["A woman’s step was heard mounting the stairs.", "여자의 발소리가 계단을 올라오는 것이 들렸다."], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch, ready for his speech, stood compressing his crossed fingers, waiting to see if the crack would not come again.", "연설할 준비를 갖춘 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 맞물린 손가락을 꽉 누른 채 서서, 또 우두둑 소리가 나지 않을까 기다렸다."], ["One joint cracked.", "마디 하나가 우두둑 소리를 냈다."]], [["Already, from the sound of light steps on the stairs, he was aware that she was close, and though he was satisfied with his speech, he felt frightened of the explanation confronting him....", "계단 위로 들려오는 가벼운 발소리만으로도 그는 이미 그녀가 가까이 왔음을 알아차렸고, 자기의 연설에 만족하면서도 자기 앞에 닥친 해명을 두려워하는 마음이 들었다……"]], [["# Chapter 9", "# Chapter 9"]], [["Anna came in with hanging head, playing with the tassels of her hood.", "안나는 고개를 숙인 채, 후드의 술을 만지작거리며 들어왔다. 그녀의 얼굴은 환하게 빛나며 달아올라 있었다."], ["Her face was brilliant and glowing; but this glow was not one of brightness; it suggested the fearful glow of a conflagration in the midst of a dark night.", "하지만 그 빛은 밝음에서 오는 빛이 아니었다. 그것은 어두운 밤 한복판에서 타오르는 화재의 무서운 불빛을 떠올리게 했다."], ["On seeing her husband, Anna raised her head and smiled, as though she had just waked up.", "남편을 보자 안나는 고개를 들고, 마치 방금 잠에서 깬 사람처럼 미소를 지었다."]], [["“You’re not in bed?", "\"아직 안 주무셨어요?"], ["What a wonder!”", "별일이네요!\""], ["she said, letting fall her hood, and without stopping, she went on into the dressing-room.", "그녀는 후드를 떨어뜨리며 이렇게 말하고는, 걸음을 멈추지 않고 화장실로 들어갔다."], ["“It’s late, Alexey Alexandrovitch,”", "\"늦었어요, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치.\""], ["she said, when she had gone through the doorway.", "문을 지나서며 그녀가 말했다."]], [["“Anna, it’s necessary for me to have a talk with you.”", "\"안나, 당신과 이야기를 좀 해야겠소.\""]], [["“With me?”", "\"저와요?\""], ["she said, wonderingly.", "그녀가 의아한 듯 말했다."], ["She came out from behind the door of the dressing-room, and looked at him.", "그녀는 화장실 문 뒤에서 나와 그를 바라보았다. \"왜요, 무슨 일인데요?"], ["“Why, what is it?", "무슨 이야기요?\""], ["What about?” she asked, sitting down.", "그녀가 앉으며 물었다."], ["“Well, let’s talk, if it’s so necessary.", "\"그래요, 정 그렇게 필요하다면 이야기하죠."], ["But it would be better to get to sleep.”", "하지만 자는 편이 더 나을 텐데요.\""]], [["Anna said what came to her lips, and marveled, hearing herself, at her own capacity for lying.", "안나는 입에서 나오는 대로 말하면서, 자기가 하는 말을 들으며 스스로의 거짓말하는 능력에 놀랐다."], ["How simple and natural were her words, and how likely that she was simply sleepy!", "그 말이 얼마나 단순하고 자연스러운지, 그리고 그녀가 그저 졸린 것처럼 보일 법한지!"], ["She felt herself clad in an impenetrable armor of falsehood.", "그녀는 자기가 꿰뚫을 수 없는 거짓의 갑옷으로 무장한 듯한 느낌이 들었다."], ["She felt that some unseen force had come to her aid and was supporting her.", "그녀는 어떤 보이지 않는 힘이 자기를 도우러 와서 떠받쳐 주고 있는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["“Anna, I must warn you,”", "\"안나, 당신에게 경고해 둬야겠소.\""], ["he began.", "그가 입을 열었다."]], [["“Warn me?”", "\"경고요?\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“Of what?”", "\"무엇에 대해서요?\""]], [["She looked at him so simply, so brightly, that anyone who did not know her as her husband knew her could not have noticed anything unnatural, either in the sound or the sense of her words.", "그녀는 너무도 천연덕스럽게, 너무도 환하게 그를 바라보았기에, 남편처럼 그녀를 잘 알지 못하는 사람이라면 그 말의 어조에서도 의미에서도 부자연스러운 점을 전혀 알아채지 못했을 것이다."], ["But to him, knowing her, knowing that whenever he went to bed five minutes later than usual, she noticed it, and asked him the reason; to him, knowing that every joy, every pleasure and pain that she felt she communicated to him at once; to him, now to see that she did not care to notice his state of mind, that she did not care to say a word about herself, meant a great deal.", "하지만 그녀를 잘 아는 그에게는, 그가 평소보다 오 분만 늦게 잠자리에 들어도 그녀가 그것을 알아채고 그 까닭을 묻는다는 것을 아는 그에게는, 그녀가 느끼는 온갖 기쁨이며 즐거움이며 고통을 곧바로 그에게 털어놓는다는 것을 아는 그에게는, 지금 그녀가 그의 심정을 알아채려 하지 않고, 자기 자신에 대해 한마디도 말하려 하지 않는 것을 보는 것이 큰 의미가 있었다."], ["He saw that the inmost recesses of her soul, that had always hitherto lain open before him, were closed against him.", "그는 지금까지 늘 그의 앞에 활짝 열려 있던 그녀 영혼의 가장 깊은 구석들이 그에게 닫혀 버렸음을 알았다."], ["More than that, he saw from her tone that she was not even perturbed at that, but as it were said straight out to him: “Yes, it’s shut up, and so it must be, and will be in future.”", "그뿐만 아니라, 그는 그녀의 말투에서 그녀가 그것을 조금도 동요하지 않고, 오히려 대놓고 그에게 이렇게 말하는 것처럼 느꼈다. \"그래요, 닫혔어요, 그리고 닫혀 있어야만 하고, 앞으로도 그럴 거예요.\""], ["Now he experienced a feeling such as a man might have, returning home and finding his own house locked up.", "이제 그는 집에 돌아왔다가 자기 집이 잠겨 있는 것을 발견한 사람이 느낄 법한 그런 감정을 경험했다."], ["“But perhaps the key may yet be found,”", "\"하지만 어쩌면 아직 열쇠를 찾을 수 있을지도 몰라.\""], ["thought Alexey Alexandrovitch.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 생각했다."]], [["“I want to warn you,”", "\"내가 경고하고 싶은 건,\""], ["he said in a low voice, “that through thoughtlessness and lack of caution you may cause yourself to be talked about in society.", "그가 낮은 목소리로 말했다. \"당신이 경솔하고 조심성이 없어서 사교계에 당신에 관한 입방아거리를 만들 수 있다는 거요."], ["Your too animated conversation this evening with Count Vronsky”", "오늘 저녁 당신이 브론스키 백작과 나눈 너무도 활기찬 대화는,\""], ["(he enunciated the name firmly and with deliberate emphasis) “attracted attention.”", "그는 단호하게 일부러 힘주어 그 이름을 또박또박 발음했다. \"사람들의 주의를 끌었소.\""]], [["He talked and looked at her laughing eyes, which frightened him now with their impenetrable look, and, as he talked, he felt all the uselessness and idleness of his words.", "그는 말하면서 그녀의 웃는 눈을 바라보았는데, 그 눈은 이제 그 꿰뚫을 수 없는 표정으로 그를 두렵게 했다. 그리고 그렇게 말하는 동안, 그는 자기 말이 얼마나 쓸데없고 부질없는지를 절감했다."]], [["“You’re always like that,”", "\"당신은 언제나 그런 식이군요.\""], ["she answered, as though completely misapprehending him, and of all he had said only taking in the last phrase.", "그녀는 마치 그를 완전히 오해한 것처럼, 그리고 그가 한 말 가운데 마지막 구절만 알아들은 것처럼 대답했다."], ["“One time you don’t like my being dull, and another time you don’t like my being lively.", "\"어떤 때는 제가 따분하게 구는 게 마음에 안 든다고 하고, 또 어떤 때는 제가 활기찬 게 마음에 안 든다고 하시네요."], ["I wasn’t dull.", "저는 따분하게 굴지 않았어요."], ["Does that offend you?”", "그게 기분 나쁘세요?\""]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch shivered, and bent his hands to make the joints crack.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 몸을 떨면서, 손가락 마디에서 우두둑 소리를 내려고 손을 구부렸다."]], [["“Oh, please, don’t do that, I do so dislike it,”", "\"오, 제발, 그러지 마세요, 저는 그게 정말 싫어요.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“Anna, is this you?”", "\"안나, 이게 당신이오?\""], ["said Alexey Alexandrovitch, quietly making an effort over himself, and restraining the motion of his fingers.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 가만히 자신을 억누르며, 손가락의 움직임을 멈추고 말했다."]], [["“But what is it all about?”", "\"하지만 도대체 무슨 일이에요?\""], ["she said, with such genuine and droll wonder.", "그녀가 너무도 진심 어린, 그리고 우스꽝스러운 의아함을 띠고 말했다."], ["“What do you want of me?”", "\"저한테 뭘 바라시는 거예요?\""]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch paused, and rubbed his forehead and his eyes.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 말을 멈추고 이마와 눈을 문질렀다."], ["He saw that instead of doing as he had intended—that is to say, warning his wife against a mistake in the eyes of the world—he had unconsciously become agitated over what was the affair of her conscience, and was struggling against the barrier he fancied between them.", "그는 자기가 의도했던 일—다시 말해 세상의 눈에 비칠 실수에 대해 아내에게 경고하는 일—을 하는 대신, 자기도 모르게 그녀의 양심에 속한 문제를 두고 흥분해 버렸고, 두 사람 사이에 있다고 여겨지는 그 장벽과 씨름하고 있음을 깨달았다."]], [["“This is what I meant to say to you,”", "\"내가 당신에게 하려던 말은 이거요.\""], ["he went on coldly and composedly, “and I beg you to listen to it.", "그가 차갑고 침착하게 말을 이었다. \"그러니 부디 들어 주기 바라오."], ["I consider jealousy, as you know, a humiliating and degrading feeling, and I shall never allow myself to be influenced by it; but there are certain rules of decorum which cannot be disregarded with impunity.", "당신도 알다시피 나는 질투를 굴욕적이고 천박한 감정으로 여기며, 결코 거기에 휘둘리도록 나 자신을 내버려 두지 않을 거요. 하지만 함부로 무시했다가는 대가를 치르게 되는 어떤 예법의 규칙이라는 게 있는 법이오."], ["This evening it was not I observed it, but judging by the impression made on the company, everyone observed that your conduct and deportment were not altogether what could be desired.”", "오늘 저녁 그것을 알아챈 건 내가 아니라, 좌중에 끼친 인상으로 미루어 보건대 모두가 알아챘소, 당신의 행동거지가 결코 바람직하다고 할 수 없었다는 걸 말이오.\""]], [["“I positively don’t understand,”", "\"저는 정말 이해가 안 가요.\""], ["said Anna, shrugging her shoulders—“He doesn’t care,”", "안나는 어깨를 으쓱하며 말했다. '이이는 신경도 안 쓰는 거야.'"], ["she thought.", "그녀는 생각했다."], ["“But other people noticed it, and that’s what upsets him.”—“You’re not well, Alexey Alexandrovitch,”", "'하지만 다른 사람들이 알아챘고, 그게 이이를 속상하게 한 거지.' \"당신 몸이 안 좋으신 거예요, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치.\""], ["she added, and she got up, and would have gone towards the door; but he moved forward as though he would stop her.", "그녀는 이렇게 덧붙이고는 일어나서 문 쪽으로 가려 했다. 하지만 그가 그녀를 막기라도 하려는 듯 앞으로 나섰다."]], [["His face was ugly and forbidding, as Anna had never seen him.", "그의 얼굴은 안나가 한 번도 본 적 없을 만큼 흉하고 험상궂었다."], ["She stopped, and bending her head back and on one side, began with her rapid hand taking out her hairpins.", "그녀는 걸음을 멈추고, 고개를 뒤로 젖혀 한쪽으로 기울인 채, 잰 손놀림으로 머리핀을 빼기 시작했다."]], [["“Well, I’m listening to what’s to come,”", "\"좋아요, 그다음에 무슨 말이 나올지 듣고 있어요.\""], ["she said, calmly and ironically; “and indeed I listen with interest, for I should like to understand what’s the matter.”", "그녀가 차분하고 비꼬는 투로 말했다. \"정말이지 흥미롭게 듣고 있다고요, 도대체 무슨 일인지 알고 싶으니까요.\""]], [["She spoke, and marveled at the confident, calm, and natural tone in which she was speaking, and the choice of the words she used.", "그녀는 말하면서, 자기가 말하고 있는 그 확신에 차고 차분하고 자연스러운 어조에, 그리고 자기가 골라 쓰는 말들에 스스로 놀랐다."]], [["“To enter into all the details of your feelings I have no right, and besides, I regard that as useless and even harmful,”", "\"당신 감정의 온갖 세세한 부분까지 파고들 권리는 내게 없소, 게다가 나는 그것이 쓸데없을뿐더러 해롭기까지 하다고 여기오.\""], ["began Alexey Alexandrovitch.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 입을 열었다."], ["“Ferreting in one’s soul, one often ferrets out something that might have lain there unnoticed.", "\"자기 영혼을 들쑤시다 보면, 흔히 거기 그냥 눈에 띄지 않게 묻혀 있어도 좋았을 무언가를 들쑤셔 내곤 하지."], ["Your feelings are an affair of your own conscience; but I am in duty bound to you, to myself, and to God, to point out to you your duties.", "당신의 감정은 당신 양심에 속한 문제요. 하지만 나는 당신에게, 나 자신에게, 그리고 하느님께 당신의 의무를 일러 줘야 할 책무가 있소."], ["Our life has been joined, not by man, but by God.", "우리의 삶은 사람이 아니라 하느님께서 맺어 주신 것이오."], ["That union can only be severed by a crime, and a crime of that nature brings its own chastisement.”", "그 결합은 오직 죄악으로만 끊어질 수 있으며, 그런 종류의 죄악은 그 자체로 응징을 불러오는 법이오.\""]], [["“I don’t understand a word.", "\"한마디도 이해가 안 가요."], ["And, oh dear!", "게다가, 아이고!"], ["how sleepy I am, unluckily,”", "공교롭게도 어찌나 졸린지요.\""], ["she said, rapidly passing her hand through her hair, feeling for the remaining hairpins.", "그녀는 머리에 남은 핀을 더듬으며 재빠르게 손을 머리카락 사이로 훑으며 말했다."]], [["“Anna, for God’s sake don’t speak like that!”", "\"안나, 제발 그런 식으로 말하지 마시오!\""], ["he said gently.", "그가 부드럽게 말했다."], ["“Perhaps I am mistaken, but believe me, what I say, I say as much for myself as for you.", "\"어쩌면 내가 잘못 생각한 것일지도 모르오, 하지만 내 말을 믿어 주시오, 내가 하는 말은 당신을 위해서인 만큼이나 나 자신을 위해서이기도 하오."], ["I am your husband, and I love you.”", "나는 당신의 남편이고, 당신을 사랑하오.\""]], [["For an instant her face fell, and the mocking gleam in her eyes died away; but the word love threw her into revolt again.", "한순간 그녀의 얼굴이 굳어졌고, 눈에 어렸던 조롱의 빛이 사그라들었다. 하지만 사랑이라는 말은 그녀를 다시 반발하게 했다."], ["She thought: “Love?", "그녀는 생각했다."], ["Can he love?", "'사랑?"], ["If he hadn’t heard there was such a thing as love, he would never have used the word.", "이이가 사랑을 할 수 있다고? 사랑이라는 게 있다는 걸 들은 적이 없었다면, 이이는 그런 말을 절대 쓰지 않았을 거야."], ["He doesn’t even know what love is.”", "이이는 사랑이 뭔지조차 몰라.'"]], [["“Alexey Alexandrovitch, really I don’t understand,”", "\"알렉세이 알렉산드로비치, 정말 저는 이해가 안 가요.\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["she said. “Define what it is you find....”", "\"당신이 무엇을 보았다는 건지 분명히 말해 주세요…….\""]], [["“Pardon, let me say all I have to say.", "\"미안하지만, 내가 할 말을 다 하게 해 주시오."], ["I love you.", "나는 당신을 사랑하오."], ["But I am not speaking of myself; the most important persons in this matter are our son and yourself.", "하지만 내 이야기를 하는 게 아니오. 이 문제에서 가장 중요한 사람은 우리 아들과 당신이오."], ["It may very well be, I repeat, that my words seem to you utterly unnecessary and out of place; it may be that they are called forth by my mistaken impression.", "다시 말하지만, 내 말이 당신에게는 전혀 불필요하고 엉뚱하게 들릴 수도 있소. 어쩌면 그 말들이 내 잘못된 인상에서 비롯된 것일 수도 있소."], ["In that case, I beg you to forgive me.", "그렇다면 부디 나를 용서해 주시오."], ["But if you are conscious yourself of even the smallest foundation for them, then I beg you to think a little, and if your heart prompts you, to speak out to me....”", "하지만 만약 당신 스스로 그 말들에 아주 작은 근거라도 있음을 느낀다면, 부디 조금만 생각해 보고, 그리고 마음이 시키거든 나에게 털어놓아 주시오…….\""]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch was unconsciously saying something utterly unlike what he had prepared.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 자기도 모르게 미리 준비해 둔 것과는 전혀 다른 말을 하고 있었다."]], [["“I have nothing to say.", "\"저는 할 말이 없어요."], ["And besides,”", "게다가,\""], ["she said hurriedly, with difficulty repressing a smile, “it’s really time to be in bed.”", "그녀는 미소를 가까스로 억누르며 서둘러 말했다. \"정말이지 이제 잘 시간이에요.\""]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch sighed, and, without saying more, went into the bedroom.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 한숨을 쉬고는, 더 이상 말하지 않고 침실로 들어갔다."]], [["When she came into the bedroom, he was already in bed.", "그녀가 침실로 들어왔을 때, 그는 이미 잠자리에 누워 있었다."], ["His lips were sternly compressed, and his eyes looked away from her.", "그의 입술은 굳게 다물려 있었고, 그의 눈은 그녀를 외면하고 있었다."], ["Anna got into her bed, and lay expecting every minute that he would begin to speak to her again.", "안나는 자기 침대로 들어가, 그가 다시 자기에게 말을 걸어오리라고 매 순간 기다리며 누워 있었다."], ["She both feared his speaking and wished for it.", "그녀는 그가 말하는 것을 두려워하면서도 또한 바랐다."], ["But he was silent.", "하지만 그는 잠자코 있었다."], ["She waited for a long while without moving, and had forgotten about him.", "그녀는 오랫동안 꼼짝도 않고 기다렸고, 그러다 그를 잊어버렸다."], ["She thought of that other; she pictured him, and felt how her heart was flooded with emotion and guilty delight at the thought of him.", "그녀는 그 다른 사람을 생각했다. 그녀는 그를 그려 보았고, 그를 떠올리는 것만으로 자기 가슴이 감정과 죄스러운 환희로 벅차오르는 것을 느꼈다."], ["Suddenly she heard an even, tranquil snore.", "그때 별안간 고르고 평온한 코 고는 소리가 들려왔다."], ["For the first instant Alexey Alexandrovitch seemed, as it were, appalled at his own snoring, and ceased; but after an interval of two breathings the snore sounded again, with a new tranquil rhythm.", "처음 한순간 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 자기 코 고는 소리에 흠칫 놀란 듯 멈추었다. 하지만 두어 번 숨을 쉬는 사이를 두고 나서 코 고는 소리는 새로운 평온한 가락으로 다시 울리기 시작했다."]], [["“It’s late, it’s late,”", "\"늦었어, 늦었어.\""], ["she whispered with a smile.", "그녀는 미소를 띠고 속삭였다."], ["A long while she lay, not moving, with open eyes, whose brilliance she almost fancied she could herself see in the darkness.", "그녀는 오랫동안 꼼짝도 않고 눈을 뜬 채 누워 있었는데, 어둠 속에서도 그 눈의 광채를 자기 스스로 볼 수 있을 것만 같았다."]], [["# Chapter 10", "# Chapter 10"]], [["From that time a new life began for Alexey Alexandrovitch and for his wife.", "그때부터 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치와 그의 아내에게 새로운 삶이 시작되었다."], ["Nothing special happened.", "특별한 일은 아무것도 일어나지 않았다."], ["Anna went out into society, as she had always done, was particularly often at Princess Betsy’s, and met Vronsky everywhere.", "안나는 늘 그래 왔듯이 사교계에 드나들었고, 특히 베시 공작 부인의 집에 자주 갔으며, 어디서나 브론스키와 마주쳤다."], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch saw this, but could do nothing.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치도 이를 보았지만 어찌할 도리가 없었다."], ["All his efforts to draw her into open discussion she confronted with a barrier which he could not penetrate, made up of a sort of amused perplexity.", "그녀를 솔직한 대화로 끌어들이려는 그의 온갖 노력에 그녀는, 무언가 재미있어하는 듯한 어리둥절함으로 이루어진, 그가 도무지 뚫고 들어갈 수 없는 장벽으로 맞섰다."], ["Outwardly everything was the same, but their inner relations were completely changed.", "겉으로는 모든 것이 그대로였지만, 두 사람의 내면적 관계는 완전히 달라져 있었다."], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch, a man of great power in the world of politics, felt himself helpless in this.", "정치의 세계에서 막강한 권력을 지닌 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치도 이 일에서만큼은 자신이 무력하다고 느꼈다."], ["Like an ox with head bent, submissively he awaited the blow which he felt was lifted over him.", "고개를 숙인 황소처럼, 그는 자기 머리 위로 들어 올려졌다고 느껴지는 일격을 순순히 기다렸다."], ["Every time he began to think about it, he felt that he must try once more, that by kindness, tenderness, and persuasion there was still hope of saving her, of bringing her back to herself, and every day he made ready to talk to her.", "그 일을 생각하기 시작할 때마다 그는 한 번 더 노력해 봐야 한다고, 다정함과 부드러움과 설득으로 아직 그녀를 구원할, 그녀를 제정신으로 되돌릴 희망이 남아 있다고 느꼈고, 날마다 그녀와 이야기할 채비를 했다."], ["But every time he began talking to her, he felt that the spirit of evil and deceit, which had taken possession of her, had possession of him too, and he talked to her in a tone quite unlike that in which he had meant to talk.", "그러나 막상 그녀와 이야기를 시작할 때마다, 그녀를 사로잡은 악과 기만의 영(靈)이 자기까지도 사로잡고 있음을 느꼈고, 그래서 그는 본디 말하려던 것과는 전혀 다른 어조로 그녀에게 말하고 말았다."], ["Involuntarily he talked to her in his habitual tone of jeering at anyone who should say what he was saying.", "본의 아니게 그는, 자기가 지금 하고 있는 그런 말을 입에 담는 사람을 비웃을 때 쓰던 그 습관적인 어조로 그녀에게 말했다."], ["And in that tone it was impossible to say what needed to be said to her.", "그리고 그런 어조로는 그녀에게 정작 해야 할 말을 도무지 할 수가 없었다."]], [["# Chapter 11", "# Chapter 11"]], [["That which for Vronsky had been almost a whole year the one absorbing desire of his life, replacing all his old desires; that which for Anna had been an impossible, terrible, and even for that reason more entrancing dream of bliss, that desire had been fulfilled.", "브론스키에게는 거의 일 년 동안 옛 욕망들을 모조리 밀어내며 삶을 통째로 사로잡았던 단 하나의 갈망이었던 것, 안나에게는 불가능하고 끔찍하며, 바로 그렇기에 더욱 황홀한 행복의 꿈이었던 것, 그 갈망이 이루어졌다."], ["He stood before her, pale, his lower jaw quivering, and besought her to be calm, not knowing how or why.", "그는 창백한 얼굴로 아래턱을 떨면서 그녀 앞에 서서, 어떻게 해야 할지도 왜 그래야 하는지도 모른 채, 진정하라고 그녀에게 애원했다."]], [["“Anna!", "\"안나!"], ["Anna!”", "안나!\""], ["he said with a choking voice, “Anna, for pity’s sake!...”", "그가 목멘 소리로 말했다. \"안나, 제발 부탁이오!……\""]], [["But the louder he spoke, the lower she dropped her once proud and gay, now shame-stricken head, and she bowed down and sank from the sofa where she was sitting, down on the floor, at his feet; she would have fallen on the carpet if he had not held her.", "그러나 그가 큰 소리로 말할수록 그녀는, 한때 자랑스럽고 명랑했으나 이제는 수치심에 짓눌린 고개를 점점 더 떨구었고, 몸을 숙이더니 앉아 있던 소파에서 미끄러져 그의 발치 마룻바닥으로 내려앉았다. 그가 붙들지 않았다면 그녀는 양탄자 위에 쓰러졌을 것이다."]], [["“My God!", "\"하느님!"], ["Forgive me!”", "저를 용서해 주세요!\""], ["she said, sobbing, pressing his hands to her bosom.", "그녀는 흐느끼며 그의 두 손을 자기 가슴에 꼭 눌러 댔다."]], [["She felt so sinful, so guilty, that nothing was left her but to humiliate herself and beg forgiveness; and as now there was no one in her life but him, to him she addressed her prayer for forgiveness.", "그녀는 자신이 너무도 죄 많고 너무도 죄스럽게 느껴져, 자신을 낮추고 용서를 비는 것 외에는 아무것도 남아 있지 않았다. 그리고 이제 그녀의 삶에는 그 말고 아무도 없었기에, 그녀는 그에게 용서를 비는 기도를 올렸다."], ["Looking at him, she had a physical sense of her humiliation, and she could say nothing more.", "그를 바라보면서 그녀는 자신의 굴욕을 육체로 느꼈고, 더 이상 아무 말도 할 수 없었다."], ["He felt what a murderer must feel, when he sees the body he has robbed of life.", "그는 살인자가 자기 손으로 목숨을 앗은 시신을 마주했을 때 느낄 법한 감정을 느꼈다."], ["That body, robbed by him of life, was their love, the first stage of their love.", "그가 목숨을 앗은 그 시신은 바로 그들의 사랑, 그들 사랑의 첫 단계였다."], ["There was something awful and revolting in the memory of what had been bought at this fearful price of shame.", "이 끔찍한 수치의 대가로 사들인 것의 기억 속에는 무언가 무섭고 역겨운 데가 있었다."], ["Shame at their spiritual nakedness crushed her and infected him.", "영혼이 발가벗겨진 데 대한 수치심이 그녀를 짓눌렀고, 그에게도 옮아 왔다."], ["But in spite of all the murderer’s horror before the body of his victim, he must hack it to pieces, hide the body, must use what he has gained by his murder.", "그러나 제 희생물의 시신 앞에서 느끼는 온갖 살인자의 공포에도 불구하고, 그는 그 시신을 토막 내고, 시신을 감추고, 그 살인으로 얻은 것을 써먹어야만 한다."]], [["And with fury, as it were with passion, the murderer falls on the body, and drags it and hacks at it; so he covered her face and shoulders with kisses.", "그리하여 마치 격정에 사로잡힌 듯 분노에 차서, 살인자가 시신에 달려들어 그것을 끌고 토막 내듯이, 그는 그녀의 얼굴과 어깨를 입맞춤으로 뒤덮었다."], ["She held his hand, and did not stir.", "그녀는 그의 손을 잡은 채 꼼짝도 하지 않았다."], ["“Yes, these kisses—that is what has been bought by this shame.", "\"그래, 이 입맞춤들—이게 바로 이 수치로 사들인 거야."], ["Yes, and one hand, which will always be mine—the hand of my accomplice.”", "그래, 그리고 이 한 손, 언제까지나 내 것이 될 손—내 공범의 손.\""], ["She lifted up that hand and kissed it.", "그녀는 그 손을 들어 입을 맞추었다."], ["He sank on his knees and tried to see her face; but she hid it, and said nothing.", "그는 무릎을 꿇고 그녀의 얼굴을 보려 했지만, 그녀는 얼굴을 감추며 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["At last, as though making an effort over herself, she got up and pushed him away.", "마침내 그녀는 자신을 다잡으려는 듯 일어나 그를 밀어냈다."], ["Her face was still as beautiful, but it was only the more pitiful for that.", "그녀의 얼굴은 여전히 아름다웠지만, 그래서 더욱 가엾어 보일 뿐이었다."]], [["“All is over,”", "\"모든 게 끝났어요.\""], ["she said; “I have nothing but you.", "그녀가 말했다. \"나에겐 당신밖에 없어요."], ["Remember that.”", "그걸 기억해 줘요.\""]], [["“I can never forget what is my whole life.", "\"내 삶 전부인 것을 어떻게 잊을 수 있겠소."], ["For one instant of this happiness....”", "이 행복의 한순간을 위해서라면……\""]], [["“Happiness!”", "\"행복이라고요!\""], ["she said with horror and loathing and her horror unconsciously infected him.", "그녀는 공포와 혐오에 차서 말했고, 그녀의 그 공포는 자기도 모르게 그에게 옮아 갔다."], ["“For pity’s sake, not a word, not a word more.”", "\"제발, 한마디도, 더는 한마디도 하지 말아요.\""]], [["She rose quickly and moved away from him.", "그녀는 재빨리 일어나 그에게서 멀어졌다."]], [["“Not a word more,”", "\"더는 한마디도.\""], ["she repeated, and with a look of chill despair, incomprehensible to him, she parted from him.", "그녀는 거듭 말했고, 그로서는 이해할 수 없는 싸늘한 절망의 빛을 띤 채 그와 헤어졌다."], ["She felt that at that moment she could not put into words the sense of shame, of rapture, and of horror at this stepping into a new life, and she did not want to speak of it, to vulgarize this feeling by inappropriate words.", "그녀는 그 순간 새로운 삶으로 발을 들여놓는 데 대한 수치심과 환희와 공포의 감정을 말로 옮길 수 없음을 느꼈고, 또 그것을 입에 올리고 싶지도, 어울리지 않는 말로 이 감정을 천박하게 만들고 싶지도 않았다."], ["But later too, and the next day and the third day, she still found no words in which she could express the complexity of her feelings; indeed, she could not even find thoughts in which she could clearly think out all that was in her soul.", "그러나 나중에도, 그다음 날에도, 사흘째 되던 날에도, 그녀는 여전히 자기 감정의 복잡함을 표현할 말을 찾지 못했다. 아니, 자기 영혼 속에 든 모든 것을 또렷이 헤아려 볼 생각조차 떠올릴 수가 없었다."]], [["She said to herself: “No, just now I can’t think of it, later on, when I am calmer.”", "그녀는 혼잣말을 했다. \"아니, 지금은 그걸 생각할 수가 없어, 나중에, 좀 더 차분해지면.\""], ["But this calm for thought never came; every time the thought rose of what she had done and what would happen to her, and what she ought to do, a horror came over her and she drove those thoughts away.", "그러나 생각할 만한 차분함은 끝내 찾아오지 않았다. 자기가 한 일과 자기에게 닥칠 일과 자기가 마땅히 해야 할 일에 대한 생각이 떠오를 때마다 공포가 그녀를 덮쳤고, 그녀는 그 생각들을 떨쳐 내 버렸다."]], [["“Later, later,”", "\"나중에, 나중에.\""], ["she said—“when I am calmer.”", "그녀는 말했다. \"좀 더 차분해지면.\""]], [["But in dreams, when she had no control over her thoughts, her position presented itself to her in all its hideous nakedness.", "그러나 생각을 통제할 수 없는 꿈속에서는, 그녀의 처지가 온갖 추악한 알몸을 드러낸 채 그녀에게 나타났다."], ["One dream haunted her almost every night.", "한 가지 꿈이 거의 매일 밤 그녀를 따라다녔다."], ["She dreamed that both were her husbands at once, that both were lavishing caresses on her.", "그녀는 두 사람 다 동시에 자기 남편이며, 둘 다 자기에게 애무를 퍼붓는 꿈을 꾸었다."], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch was weeping, kissing her hands, and saying, “How happy we are now!”", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 울면서 그녀의 손에 입을 맞추며 \"지금 우리는 정말 행복하군!\" 하고 말했다."], ["And Alexey Vronsky was there too, and he too was her husband.", "그리고 알렉세이 브론스키도 거기 있었고, 그 역시 그녀의 남편이었다."], ["And she was marveling that it had once seemed impossible to her, was explaining to them, laughing, that this was ever so much simpler, and that now both of them were happy and contented.", "그리고 그녀는 한때 이런 일이 불가능하게 여겨졌다는 것이 놀라워, 웃으면서 두 사람에게 이게 훨씬 더 간단한 일이며 이제 둘 다 행복하고 만족스럽다고 설명하고 있었다."], ["But this dream weighed on her like a nightmare, and she awoke from it in terror.", "그러나 이 꿈은 악몽처럼 그녀를 짓눌렀고, 그녀는 공포에 질려 꿈에서 깨어났다."]], [["# Chapter 12", "# Chapter 12"]], [["In the early days after his return from Moscow, whenever Levin shuddered and grew red, remembering the disgrace of his rejection, he said to himself: “This was just how I used to shudder and blush, thinking myself utterly lost, when I was plucked in physics and did not get my remove; and how I thought myself utterly ruined after I had mismanaged that affair of my sister’s that was entrusted to me.", "모스크바에서 돌아온 직후의 며칠 동안, 레빈은 청혼을 거절당한 치욕을 떠올릴 때마다 몸서리를 치고 얼굴이 붉어졌는데, 그럴 때면 스스로에게 이렇게 말하곤 했다. \"물리 시험에서 떨어져 진급을 못 했을 때, 내가 완전히 끝장났다고 생각하며 바로 이렇게 몸서리치고 낯을 붉혔지."], ["And yet, now that years have passed, I recall it and wonder that it could distress me so much.", "그리고 내게 맡겨진 누이의 그 일을 그르쳤을 때도 완전히 망했다고 생각했어. 그런데 세월이 지난 지금 그 일을 떠올리면, 어떻게 그게 나를 그토록 괴롭힐 수 있었는지 의아하단 말이야."], ["It will be the same thing too with this trouble.", "이번 일도 마찬가지일 거야."], ["Time will go by and I shall not mind about this either.”", "시간이 흐르면 이 일도 아무렇지 않게 되겠지.\""]], [["But three months had passed and he had not left off minding about it; and it was as painful for him to think of it as it had been those first days.", "그러나 석 달이 지났는데도 그는 여전히 그 일을 떨쳐 내지 못했다. 그 일을 떠올리는 것은 첫 며칠 동안과 다름없이 괴로웠다."], ["He could not be at peace because after dreaming so long of family life, and feeling himself so ripe for it, he was still not married, and was further than ever from marriage.", "그토록 오랫동안 가정생활을 꿈꿔 왔고, 스스로 그럴 만큼 무르익었다고 느꼈으면서도, 여전히 결혼하지 못한 데다 결혼과는 그 어느 때보다 멀어져 있었기에 그는 마음이 편할 수 없었다."], ["He was painfully conscious himself, as were all about him, that at his years it is not well for man to be alone.", "자기 나이쯤 되면 혼자 지내는 것은 좋지 않다는 것을, 주위 사람들이 다 그러하듯 그 자신도 뼈저리게 느끼고 있었다."], ["He remembered how before starting for Moscow he had once said to his cowman Nikolay, a simple-hearted peasant, whom he liked talking to: “Well, Nikolay!", "모스크바로 떠나기 전, 그는 즐겨 이야기를 나누던 순박한 목동 니콜라이에게 이렇게 말한 적이 있었다. \"그래, 니콜라이!"], ["I mean to get married,”", "나는 장가갈 생각이야.\""], ["and how Nikolay had promptly answered, as of a matter on which there could be no possible doubt: “And high time too, Konstantin Dmitrievitch.”", "그러자 니콜라이는 추호도 의심할 여지가 없는 일이라는 듯 곧바로 대꾸했다. \"암요, 진작 그러셨어야죠, 콘스탄친 드미트리치.\""], ["But marriage had now become further off than ever.", "하지만 이제 결혼은 그 어느 때보다 멀어져 버렸다."], ["The place was taken, and whenever he tried to imagine any of the girls he knew in that place, he felt that it was utterly impossible.", "그 자리는 이미 채워졌고, 자기가 아는 어떤 처녀를 그 자리에 넣어 그려 보려 해도 도무지 불가능하다는 것을 그는 느꼈다."], ["Moreover, the recollection of the rejection and the part he had played in the affair tortured him with shame.", "게다가 거절당한 일과 그 일에서 자기가 했던 역할을 떠올리면 수치심으로 괴로웠다."], ["However often he told himself that he was in no wise to blame in it, that recollection, like other humiliating reminiscences of a similar kind, made him twinge and blush.", "자기에게는 아무 잘못도 없다고 아무리 스스로를 타일러도, 그 기억은 다른 비슷한 굴욕적인 회상들이 그러하듯 그를 움찔하게 하고 낯을 붉히게 했다."], ["There had been in his past, as in every man’s, actions, recognized by him as bad, for which his conscience ought to have tormented him; but the memory of these evil actions was far from causing him so much suffering as those trivial but humiliating reminiscences.", "다른 모든 사람과 마찬가지로 그의 과거에도 스스로 나쁜 짓이라고 인정하는, 양심이 그를 괴롭혀야 마땅한 행동들이 있었다. 그러나 그 못된 행동들에 대한 기억은, 사소하지만 굴욕적인 그 회상들만큼은 그를 괴롭히지 못했다."], ["These wounds never healed.", "그런 상처들은 결코 아물지 않았다."], ["And with these memories was now ranged his rejection and the pitiful position in which he must have appeared to others that evening.", "그리고 이제 그 기억들 틈에 거절당한 일과, 그날 저녁 다른 사람들 눈에 비쳤을 자신의 가련한 처지가 나란히 자리 잡았다."], ["But time and work did their part.", "그러나 시간과 일이 제 몫을 해냈다."], ["Bitter memories were more and more covered up by the incidents—paltry in his eyes, but really important—of his country life.", "쓰라린 기억들은 그의 눈에는 하찮으나 실은 중요한 시골 생활의 자질구레한 사건들에 점점 덮여 갔다."], ["Every week he thought less often of Kitty.", "한 주 한 주가 지날수록 그는 키티 생각을 덜 하게 되었다."], ["He was impatiently looking forward to the news that she was married, or just going to be married, hoping that such news would, like having a tooth out, completely cure him.", "그는 그녀가 결혼했다거나 곧 결혼한다는 소식이 오기를 안달하며 기다렸는데, 그런 소식이 이를 뽑는 것처럼 자기를 완전히 낫게 해 주리라 바랐던 것이다."]], [["Meanwhile spring came on, beautiful and kindly, without the delays and treacheries of spring,—one of those rare springs in which plants, beasts, and man rejoice alike.", "그러는 동안 봄이 찾아왔다. 봄 특유의 지체나 변덕도 없이, 아름답고 다정하게—식물과 짐승과 사람이 다 함께 기뻐하는, 그 드문 봄 중 하나였다."], ["This lovely spring roused Levin still more, and strengthened him in his resolution of renouncing all his past and building up his lonely life firmly and independently.", "이 사랑스러운 봄은 레빈을 한층 더 들뜨게 했고, 자신의 과거를 모두 단념하고 외로운 삶을 굳건하고 독립적으로 세워 나가겠다는 결심을 더욱 단단하게 해 주었다."], ["Though many of the plans with which he had returned to the country had not been carried out, still his most important resolution—that of purity—had been kept by him.", "시골로 돌아오며 세웠던 계획 중 많은 것이 실행되지 못했지만, 그래도 그의 가장 중요한 결심—순결을 지키겠다는 결심만은 지켜졌다."], ["He was free from that shame, which had usually harassed him after a fall; and he could look everyone straight in the face.", "그는 타락한 뒤 늘 자신을 괴롭히던 그 수치심에서 자유로웠고, 누구의 얼굴이든 똑바로 마주 볼 수 있었다."], ["In February he had received a letter from Marya Nikolaevna telling him that his brother Nikolay’s health was getting worse, but that he would not take advice, and in consequence of this letter Levin went to Moscow to his brother’s and succeeded in persuading him to see a doctor and to go to a watering-place abroad.", "2월에 그는 마리야 니콜라예브나에게서 형 니콜라이의 건강이 점점 나빠지는데도 충고를 들으려 하지 않는다는 편지를 받았고, 이 편지 때문에 모스크바의 형에게로 가서 의사를 만나고 외국의 온천지로 떠나도록 설득하는 데 성공했다."], ["He succeeded so well in persuading his brother, and in lending him money for the journey without irritating him, that he was satisfied with himself in that matter.", "형을 자극하지 않으면서 설득하고, 여행 경비까지 빌려주는 데 어찌나 잘 성공했던지, 그는 그 일에서만큼은 스스로에게 만족했다."], ["In addition to his farming, which called for special attention in spring, and in addition to reading, Levin had begun that winter a work on agriculture, the plan of which turned on taking into account the character of the laborer on the land as one of the unalterable data of the question, like the climate and the soil, and consequently deducing all the principles of scientific culture, not simply from the data of soil and climate, but from the data of soil, climate, and a certain unalterable character of the laborer.", "봄에 특별히 신경 써야 할 농사일과 독서에 더해, 레빈은 그해 겨울 농업에 관한 저술을 시작했는데, 그 구상의 핵심은 토양과 기후처럼 농업이라는 문제의 바꿀 수 없는 자료 가운데 하나로 땅에서 일하는 노동자의 성격을 고려에 넣는다는 것, 따라서 과학적 경작의 모든 원리를 단지 토양과 기후의 자료에서만이 아니라 토양과 기후, 그리고 노동자의 어떤 바꿀 수 없는 성격이라는 자료에서 끌어낸다는 것이었다."], ["Thus, in spite of his solitude, or in consequence of his solitude, his life was exceedingly full.", "그리하여 고독함에도 불구하고, 아니 어쩌면 고독한 덕분에 그의 삶은 더없이 충만했다."], ["Only rarely he suffered from an unsatisfied desire to communicate his stray ideas to someone besides Agafea Mihalovna.", "다만 그는 아가피야 미하일로브나 말고 다른 누군가에게 자기가 떠올린 단편적인 생각들을 들려주고 싶은, 채워지지 않는 욕구로 이따금 괴로워할 뿐이었다."], ["With her indeed he not infrequently fell into discussion upon physics, the theory of agriculture, and especially philosophy; philosophy was Agafea Mihalovna’s favorite subject.", "사실 그녀와는 물리학이며 농업 이론, 특히 철학을 두고 적잖이 토론에 빠져들곤 했는데, 철학은 아가피야 미하일로브나가 가장 좋아하는 주제였다."]], [["Spring was slow in unfolding.", "봄은 더디게 피어났다."], ["For the last few weeks it had been steadily fine frosty weather.", "지난 몇 주 동안은 줄곧 맑고 서리 내리는 날씨가 이어졌다."], ["In the daytime it thawed in the sun, but at night there were even seven degrees of frost.", "낮에는 햇볕에 눈이 녹았지만, 밤이면 영하 7도까지 내려갔다."], ["There was such a frozen surface on the snow that they drove the wagons anywhere off the roads.", "눈 위에 얼음이 얼어붙어 단단해진 덕분에 사람들은 길이 아닌 아무 데로나 수레를 몰고 다녔다."], ["Easter came in the snow.", "부활절은 눈 속에서 찾아왔다."], ["Then all of a sudden, on Easter Monday, a warm wind sprang up, storm clouds swooped down, and for three days and three nights the warm, driving rain fell in streams.", "그러다 별안간, 부활절 다음 월요일에 따뜻한 바람이 일고 먹구름이 몰려들더니, 사흘 밤낮 동안 따뜻한 비가 들이치며 줄기차게 쏟아졌다."], ["On Thursday the wind dropped, and a thick gray fog brooded over the land as though hiding the mysteries of the transformations that were being wrought in nature.", "목요일에는 바람이 잦아들었고, 짙은 잿빛 안개가 마치 자연 속에서 벌어지는 변화의 신비를 감추기라도 하듯 대지 위에 자욱이 드리웠다."], ["Behind the fog there was the flowing of water, the cracking and floating of ice, the swift rush of turbid, foaming torrents; and on the following Monday, in the evening, the fog parted, the storm clouds split up into little curling crests of cloud, the sky cleared, and the real spring had come.", "안개 너머에서는 물이 흐르고, 얼음이 쩍쩍 갈라지며 떠다니고, 흙탕물이 거품을 일으키며 빠르게 내달았다. 그리고 그다음 월요일 저녁, 안개가 걷히고 먹구름이 동그랗게 말린 작은 구름 조각들로 흩어지면서 하늘이 맑게 개고, 진짜 봄이 찾아왔다."], ["In the morning the sun rose brilliant and quickly wore away the thin layer of ice that covered the water, and all the warm air was quivering with the steam that rose up from the quickened earth.", "아침에 해가 눈부시게 떠올라 물 위를 덮고 있던 얇은 얼음을 순식간에 녹여 버렸고, 따뜻한 공기는 온통 되살아난 대지에서 피어오르는 아지랑이로 일렁였다."], ["The old grass looked greener, and the young grass thrust up its tiny blades; the buds of the guelder-rose and of the currant and the sticky birch-buds were swollen with sap, and an exploring bee was humming about the golden blossoms that studded the willow.", "묵은 풀은 한결 푸르게 보였고, 어린 풀은 가느다란 싹을 밀어 올렸다. 백당나무와 까치밥나무의 봉오리며 끈적한 자작나무 싹은 물이 올라 부풀었고, 버드나무에 점점이 박힌 황금빛 꽃 둘레로는 먹이를 찾아 나선 벌이 윙윙거렸다."], ["Larks trilled unseen above the velvety green fields and the ice-covered stubble-land; peewits wailed over the low lands and marshes flooded by the pools; cranes and wild geese flew high across the sky uttering their spring calls.", "보드라운 초록빛 들판과 얼음에 덮인 그루터기 밭 위에서는 보이지 않는 종달새들이 지저귀었고, 웅덩이 물이 넘쳐흐른 저지대와 늪지 위에서는 댕기물떼새들이 구슬피 울었다. 두루미와 기러기들은 봄의 울음소리를 내며 하늘 높이 가로질러 날았다."], ["The cattle, bald in patches where the new hair had not grown yet, lowed in the pastures; the bowlegged lambs frisked round their bleating mothers.", "새 털이 아직 다 자라지 않아 여기저기 털이 빠진 가축들이 목초지에서 음매 울었고, 다리가 휜 새끼 양들은 음매거리는 어미 곁을 깡충깡충 뛰어다녔다."], ["Nimble children ran about the drying paths, covered with the prints of bare feet.", "날쌘 아이들은 맨발 자국이 가득 찍힌, 말라 가는 오솔길을 뛰어다녔다."], ["There was a merry chatter of peasant women over their linen at the pond, and the ring of axes in the yard, where the peasants were repairing ploughs and harrows.", "연못가에서는 빨래하는 아낙들의 즐거운 수다 소리가, 마당에서는 농부들이 쟁기와 써레를 손보는 도끼질 소리가 울려 퍼졌다."], ["The real spring had come.", "진짜 봄이 찾아왔다."]], [["# Chapter 13", "# Chapter 13"]], [["Levin put on his big boots, and, for the first time, a cloth jacket, instead of his fur cloak, and went out to look after his farm, stepping over streams of water that flashed in the sunshine and dazzled his eyes, and treading one minute on ice and the next into sticky mud.", "레빈은 큰 장화를 신었고, 모피 외투 대신에 처음으로 천 재킷을 걸친 채, 햇빛에 반짝여 눈이 부신 시냇물 줄기를 건너뛰고, 어느 순간엔 얼음을, 다음 순간엔 끈적한 진창을 밟으며 자기 농장을 둘러보러 나갔다."]], [["Spring is the time of plans and projects.", "봄은 계획과 구상의 시절이다."], ["And, as he came out into the farmyard, Levin, like a tree in spring that knows not what form will be taken by the young shoots and twigs imprisoned in its swelling buds, hardly knew what undertakings he was going to begin upon now in the farm work that was so dear to him.", "농장 마당으로 나서면서, 부풀어 오른 봉오리 속에 갇힌 어린 싹과 가지가 어떤 모양을 띠게 될지 알지 못하는 봄날의 나무처럼, 레빈도 자기에게 그토록 소중한 농사일에서 이제 어떤 일에 착수하게 될지 좀처럼 알 수 없었다."], ["But he felt that he was full of the most splendid plans and projects.", "하지만 그는 자기가 더없이 훌륭한 계획과 구상으로 가득 차 있음을 느꼈다."], ["First of all he went to the cattle.", "우선 그는 가축에게 갔다."], ["The cows had been let out into their paddock, and their smooth sides were already shining with their new, sleek, spring coats; they basked in the sunshine and lowed to go to the meadow.", "소들은 우리 밖 작은 방목장으로 풀려나 있었는데, 매끈한 옆구리는 벌써 새로 돋은 윤기 나는 봄 털로 반들거렸다. 소들은 햇볕을 쬐며 초원으로 나가게 해 달라고 음매 울었다."], ["Levin gazed admiringly at the cows he knew so intimately to the minutest detail of their condition, and gave orders for them to be driven out into the meadow, and the calves to be let into the paddock.", "레빈은 그 상태의 가장 사소한 데까지 속속들이 알고 있는 소들을 흐뭇하게 바라보다가, 소들은 초원으로 몰고 나가고 송아지들은 방목장에 들이라고 일렀다."], ["The herdsman ran gaily to get ready for the meadow.", "목동은 신이 나서 초원에 나갈 채비를 하러 달려갔다."], ["The cowherd girls, picking up their petticoats, ran splashing through the mud with bare legs, still white, not yet brown from the sun, waving brush wood in their hands, chasing the calves that frolicked in the mirth of spring.", "소 치는 처녀들은 치마를 걷어 올리고, 아직 햇볕에 그을지 않아 하얀 맨다리로 진창을 철벅거리며 달렸고, 손에 든 잔가지를 휘두르며 봄의 환희 속에 깡충거리는 송아지들을 뒤쫓았다."]], [["After admiring the young ones of that year, who were particularly fine—the early calves were the size of a peasant’s cow, and Pava’s daughter, at three months old, was as big as a yearling—Levin gave orders for a trough to be brought out and for them to be fed in the paddock.", "그해 태어난 어린 것들에 감탄하고 나서—올해 것들은 유난히 잘 자라서, 일찍 태어난 송아지는 농부의 암소만 했고, 파바의 딸은 생후 석 달 만에 한 살배기만큼이나 컸다—레빈은 여물통을 내오게 하고 방목장에서 송아지들에게 먹이를 주라고 일렀다."], ["But it appeared that as the paddock had not been used during the winter, the hurdles made in the autumn for it were broken.", "그런데 알고 보니 겨울 동안 방목장을 쓰지 않은 탓에, 가을에 만들어 둔 울짱이 부서져 있었다."], ["He sent for the carpenter, who, according to his orders, ought to have been at work at the thrashing machine.", "그는 목수를 부르러 사람을 보냈는데, 목수는 그의 지시대로라면 탈곡기를 손보고 있어야 했다."], ["But it appeared that the carpenter was repairing the harrows, which ought to have been repaired before Lent.", "하지만 알고 보니 목수는 사순절 전에 고쳐 놓았어야 할 써레를 수리하고 있었다."], ["This was very annoying to Levin.", "이것이 레빈에게는 몹시 짜증스러웠다."], ["It was annoying to come upon that everlasting slovenliness in the farm work against which he had been striving with all his might for so many years.", "그토록 여러 해 동안 온 힘을 다해 맞서 싸워 온 농사일의 그 끝없는 게으름을 또다시 마주하는 것이 짜증스러웠다."], ["The hurdles, as he ascertained, being not wanted in winter, had been carried to the cart-horses’ stable; and there broken, as they were of light construction, only meant for feeding calves.", "그가 확인한 바로는, 겨울에는 쓸 데가 없던 울짱이 짐말 마구간으로 옮겨졌고, 거기서 부서졌는데, 그것은 송아지 먹이를 줄 용도로만 가볍게 만든 것이었기 때문이다."], ["Moreover, it was apparent also that the harrows and all the agricultural implements, which he had directed to be looked over and repaired in the winter, for which very purpose he had hired three carpenters, had not been put into repair, and the harrows were being repaired when they ought to have been harrowing the field.", "게다가 겨울 동안 점검하고 수리하라고 지시했고 바로 그 목적으로 목수 셋을 고용했던 써레와 모든 농기구가 손질되지 않았으며, 들판에서 써레질을 하고 있어야 할 때에 써레를 수리하고 있다는 것도 분명히 드러났다."], ["Levin sent for his bailiff, but immediately went off himself to look for him.", "레빈은 관리인을 부르러 사람을 보냈으나, 곧장 직접 그를 찾으러 나섰다."], ["The bailiff, beaming all over, like everyone that day, in a sheepskin bordered with astrachan, came out of the barn, twisting a bit of straw in his hands.", "그날 다른 모두가 그렇듯 온통 환한 얼굴로, 아스트라한 모피로 가장자리를 두른 양가죽 외투를 입은 관리인이, 손에서 짚오라기를 비비 꼬며 헛간에서 나왔다."]], [["“Why isn’t the carpenter at the thrashing machine?”", "\"왜 목수가 탈곡기를 손보고 있지 않은 거지?\""]], [["“Oh, I meant to tell you yesterday, the harrows want repairing.", "\"아, 어제 말씀드리려 했는데요, 써레를 수리해야 합니다."], ["Here it’s time they got to work in the fields.”", "이제 들판에서 일할 때가 됐으니까요.\""]], [["“But what were they doing in the winter, then?”", "\"그럼 겨울 동안에는 뭘 한 거야?\""]], [["“But what did you want the carpenter for?”", "\"그런데 목수가 왜 필요하셨던 겁니까?\""]], [["“Where are the hurdles for the calves’ paddock?”", "\"송아지 방목장 울짱은 어디 있나?\""]], [["“I ordered them to be got ready.", "\"준비해 두라고 일러 놨습니다."], ["What would you have with those peasants!”", "그 농부들을 어쩌겠습니까!\""], ["said the bailiff, with a wave of his hand.", "관리인이 손을 내저으며 말했다."]], [["“It’s not those peasants but this bailiff!”", "\"그 농부들이 아니라 이 관리인이 문제지!\""], ["said Levin, getting angry.", "레빈이 화가 치밀어 말했다."], ["“Why, what do I keep you for?” he cried.", "\"아니, 그럼 내가 자네를 뭐 하러 두고 있는 건가?\""], ["But, bethinking himself that this would not help matters, he stopped short in the middle of a sentence, and merely sighed.", "그가 소리쳤다. 하지만 이래 봤자 사태에 도움이 되지 않으리라는 생각이 들어, 그는 말을 하다 말고 뚝 멈추고는 그저 한숨만 내쉬었다."], ["“Well, what do you say?", "\"자, 어떤가?"], ["Can sowing begin?”", "파종을 시작할 수 있겠나?\""], ["he asked, after a pause.", "그가 잠시 뒤에 물었다."]], [["“Behind Turkin tomorrow or the next day they might begin.”", "\"투르킨 너머는 내일이나 모레쯤 시작할 수 있겠습니다.\""]], [["“And the clover?”", "\"그럼 클로버는?\""]], [["“I’ve sent Vassily and Mishka; they’re sowing.", "\"바실리와 미시카를 보냈습니다. 지금 파종하고 있어요."], ["Only I don’t know if they’ll manage to get through; it’s so slushy.”", "다만 끝까지 해낼 수 있을지는 모르겠습니다. 워낙 질척거려서요.\""]], [["“How many acres?”", "\"몇 에이커나 되지?\""]], [["“Why not sow all?”", "\"왜 전부 파종하지 않는 겁니까?\""], ["cried Levin.", "레빈이 외쳤다."]], [["That they were only sowing the clover on fifteen acres, not on all the forty-five, was still more annoying to him.", "마흔다섯 에이커 전부가 아니라 겨우 열다섯 에이커에만 클로버를 파종하고 있다는 것이 그에게는 더욱더 짜증스러웠다."], ["Clover, as he knew, both from books and from his own experience, never did well except when it was sown as early as possible, almost in the snow.", "책에서나 자기 경험으로나 그가 알기로, 클로버는 가능한 한 일찍, 거의 눈 속에서 파종하지 않으면 결코 잘 자라지 않았다."], ["And yet Levin could never get this done.", "그런데도 레빈은 도무지 이 일을 제대로 해낼 수가 없었다."]], [["“There’s no one to send.", "\"보낼 사람이 없습니다."], ["What would you have with such a set of peasants?", "이런 농부들을 어쩌겠습니까? 셋은 나타나지도 않았어요."], ["Three haven’t turned up. And there’s Semyon....”", "게다가 세묜이라는 작자도 있고요……\""]], [["“Well, you should have taken some men from the thatching.”", "\"그럼 지붕 이는 데서 사람을 좀 빼 왔어야지.\""]], [["“And so I have, as it is.”", "\"이미 그렇게 했습니다, 지금.\""]], [["“Where are the peasants, then?”", "\"그럼 농부들은 어디 있나?\""]], [["“Five are making compôte” (which meant compost), “four are shifting the oats for fear of a touch of mildew, Konstantin Dmitrievitch.”", "\"다섯은 콤포트를 만들고 있고\"(이는 퇴비를 뜻했다), \"넷은 곰팡이가 슬까 봐 귀리를 옮기고 있습니다, 콘스탄친 드미트리치.\""]], [["Levin knew very well that “a touch of mildew” meant that his English seed oats were already ruined.", "곰팡이가 슨다는 말이 곧 자기의 영국산 종자 귀리가 이미 못 쓰게 되었다는 뜻임을 레빈은 너무도 잘 알았다."], ["Again they had not done as he had ordered.", "또다시 그들은 그가 지시한 대로 하지 않은 것이다."]], [["“Why, but I told you during Lent to put in pipes,”", "\"아니, 내가 사순절 동안에 환기관을 설치하라고 했잖나,\""], ["he cried.", "그가 소리쳤다."]], [["“Don’t put yourself out; we shall get it all done in time.”", "\"너무 걱정 마십시오. 제때에 다 해 놓겠습니다.\""]], [["Levin waved his hand angrily, went into the granary to glance at the oats, and then to the stable.", "레빈은 화가 나서 손을 내젓고는 귀리를 살펴보러 곳간으로 들어갔다가, 다시 마구간으로 향했다."], ["The oats were not yet spoiled.", "귀리는 아직 상하지 않았다."], ["But the peasants were carrying the oats in spades when they might simply let them slide down into the lower granary; and arranging for this to be done, and taking two workmen from there for sowing clover, Levin got over his vexation with the bailiff.", "하지만 농부들은 그냥 아래쪽 곳간으로 미끄러뜨려 내려보내면 될 것을 삽으로 귀리를 퍼 나르고 있었다. 그것을 미끄러뜨려 내려보내도록 조처하고, 거기서 일꾼 둘을 빼내 클로버 파종에 보낸 뒤, 레빈은 관리인에 대한 짜증을 가라앉혔다."], ["Indeed, it was such a lovely day that one could not be angry.", "사실 너무도 화창한 날이라 화를 내고 있을 수가 없었다."]], [["“Ignat!”", "\"이그나트!\""], ["he called to the coachman, who, with his sleeves tucked up, was washing the carriage wheels, “saddle me....”", "그는 소매를 걷어 올리고 마차 바퀴를 닦고 있던 마부를 불렀다. \"안장을 얹어 다오……\""]], [["“Well, let it be Kolpik.”", "\"음, 콜피크로 하지.\""]], [["“Yes, sir.”", "\"예, 나리.\""]], [["While they were saddling his horse, Levin again called up the bailiff, who was hanging about in sight, to make it up with him, and began talking to him about the spring operations before them, and his plans for the farm.", "말에 안장을 얹는 동안, 레빈은 눈에 띄는 곳에서 어슬렁대던 관리인을 화해하려는 마음에 다시 불러서는, 앞으로 있을 봄철 작업과 농장에 대한 자기 계획을 이야기하기 시작했다."]], [["The wagons were to begin carting manure earlier, so as to get all done before the early mowing.", "이른 풀베기 전에 모든 일을 끝내려면 짐수레로 거름을 더 일찍 실어 나르기 시작해야 했다."], ["And the ploughing of the further land to go on without a break so as to let it ripen lying fallow.", "그리고 멀리 떨어진 밭을 묵힌 채로 익게 하려면 그곳 갈이질을 쉬지 않고 진행해야 했다."], ["And the mowing to be all done by hired labor, not on half-profits.", "풀베기는 절반 소작으로 하지 말고 전부 품삯 일꾼을 써서 해야 했다."], ["The bailiff listened attentively, and obviously made an effort to approve of his employer’s projects.", "관리인은 주의 깊게 들었고, 분명 고용주의 구상에 찬동하려고 애를 썼다."], ["But still he had that look Levin knew so well that always irritated him, a look of hopelessness and despondency.", "하지만 그는 여전히 레빈이 너무도 잘 아는, 늘 그를 짜증나게 하던 그 표정을, 가망 없음과 낙담의 표정을 짓고 있었다. 그 표정은 이렇게 말하고 있었다."], ["That look said: “That’s all very well, but as God wills.”", "\"다 좋은 말씀입니다만, 하느님 뜻대로 되는 거죠.\""]], [["Nothing mortified Levin so much as that tone.", "이 어조만큼 레빈을 무참하게 만드는 것은 없었다."], ["But it was the tone common to all the bailiffs he had ever had.", "하지만 그것은 그가 거느려 본 모든 관리인에게 공통된 어조였다."], ["They had all taken up that attitude to his plans, and so now he was not angered by it, but mortified, and felt all the more roused to struggle against this, as it seemed, elemental force continually ranged against him, for which he could find no other expression than “as God wills.”", "그들 모두가 그의 계획에 대해 그런 태도를 취해 왔고, 그래서 이제 그는 그것에 화가 나지는 않았지만 무참한 기분이 들었으며, 끊임없이 자기에게 맞서 늘어선, 보아하니 근원적인 듯한 그 힘, \"하느님 뜻대로\"라는 말 외에는 달리 표현할 길이 없는 그 힘에 맞서 싸워야겠다는 마음이 한층 더 끓어올랐다."]], [["“If we can manage it, Konstantin Dmitrievitch,”", "\"우리가 해낼 수 있다면 말입니다, 콘스탄친 드미트리치,\""], ["said the bailiff.", "관리인이 말했다."]], [["“Why ever shouldn’t you manage it?”", "\"아니, 대체 왜 못 해낸단 말인가?\""]], [["“We positively must have another fifteen laborers.", "\"일꾼이 열다섯은 더 있어야 합니다, 꼭이요."], ["And they don’t turn up.", "그런데 나타나질 않아요."], ["There were some here today asking seventy roubles for the summer.”", "오늘 여기 몇 명 왔었는데 여름 한 철에 칠십 루블을 달라더군요.\""]], [["Levin was silent.", "레빈은 잠자코 있었다."], ["Again he was brought face to face with that opposing force.", "또다시 그는 그 맞서는 힘과 정면으로 마주하게 되었다."], ["He knew that however much they tried, they could not hire more than forty—thirty-seven perhaps or thirty-eight—laborers for a reasonable sum.", "아무리 애를 써도 적당한 돈으로는 마흔 명 이상은—어쩌면 서른일곱이나 서른여덟—일꾼을 고용할 수 없다는 것을 그는 알고 있었다."], ["Some forty had been taken on, and there were no more.", "마흔 명쯤 받아들였고, 더는 없었다."], ["But still he could not help struggling against it.", "하지만 그래도 그는 그것에 맞서 발버둥치지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["“Send to Sury, to Tchefirovka; if they don’t come we must look for them.”", "\"수리로, 체피로프카로 사람을 보내게. 그래도 오지 않으면 우리가 찾아 나서야지.\""]], [["“Oh, I’ll send, to be sure,”", "\"아, 보내고말고요, 물론,\""], ["said Vassily Fedorovitch despondently.", "바실리 표도로비치가 풀이 죽어 말했다."], ["“But there are the horses, too, they’re not good for much.”", "\"하지만 말들도 문제입니다, 그놈들도 별 쓸모가 없어요.\""]], [["“We’ll get some more.", "\"몇 마리 더 들이지. 물론 나도 잘 알아,\""], ["I know, of course,”", "레빈이 웃으며 덧붙였다."], ["Levin added laughing, “you always want to do with as little and as poor quality as possible; but this year I’m not going to let you have things your own way.", "\"자네는 늘 되도록 적게, 되도록 질 낮은 걸로 때우려 들지. 하지만 올해는 자네 마음대로 하게 두지 않겠네."], ["I’ll see to everything myself.”", "모든 걸 내가 직접 챙길 거야.\""]], [["“Why, I don’t think you take much rest as it is.", "\"아니, 지금도 별로 쉬지도 못하시는 것 같은데요."], ["It cheers us up to work under the master’s eye....”", "주인 나리 눈앞에서 일하면 저희야 기운이 나지요……\""]], [["“So they’re sowing clover behind the Birch Dale?", "\"그래, 자작나무 골짜기 너머에서 클로버를 파종하고 있다고 했지?"], ["I’ll go and have a look at them,”", "가서 한번 봐야겠어,\""], ["he said, getting on to the little bay cob, Kolpik, who was led up by the coachman.", "그는 마부가 끌고 온 작은 적갈색 조랑말 콜피크에 올라타며 말했다."]], [["“You can’t get across the streams, Konstantin Dmitrievitch,”", "\"시냇물을 건너실 수가 없습니다, 콘스탄친 드미트리치,\""], ["the coachman shouted.", "마부가 외쳤다."]], [["“All right, I’ll go by the forest.”", "\"괜찮아, 숲을 지나서 가지.\""]], [["And Levin rode through the slush of the farmyard to the gate and out into the open country, his good little horse, after his long inactivity, stepping out gallantly, snorting over the pools, and asking, as it were, for guidance.", "그리하여 레빈은 농장 마당의 진창을 지나 문으로, 그리고 탁 트인 들판으로 말을 몰고 나갔다. 그 좋은 작은 말은 오랜 휴식 끝에 씩씩하게 발을 내디뎠고, 물웅덩이를 만날 때마다 콧김을 내뿜으며 마치 길을 일러 달라는 듯했다."], ["If Levin had felt happy before in the cattle pens and farmyard, he felt happier yet in the open country.", "가축 우리와 농장 마당에서 행복을 느꼈다면, 탁 트인 들판에서는 더한층 행복했다."], ["Swaying rhythmically with the ambling paces of his good little cob, drinking in the warm yet fresh scent of the snow and the air, as he rode through his forest over the crumbling, wasted snow, still left in parts, and covered with dissolving tracks, he rejoiced over every tree, with the moss reviving on its bark and the buds swelling on its shoots.", "그 좋은 작은 조랑말의 느릿느릿한 걸음에 맞춰 리듬 있게 몸을 흔들며, 눈과 공기의 따스하면서도 신선한 향기를 들이마시며, 군데군데 아직 남아 녹아 가는 발자국으로 뒤덮인 채 부스러져 사라져 가는 눈 위로 자기 숲을 지나 말을 몰면서, 그는 껍질에 이끼가 되살아나고 가지마다 봉오리가 부풀어 오른 나무 한 그루 한 그루마다 기뻐했다."], ["When he came out of the forest, in the immense plain before him, his grass fields stretched in an unbroken carpet of green, without one bare place or swamp, only spotted here and there in the hollows with patches of melting snow.", "숲에서 나오자, 그의 앞에 펼쳐진 광활한 평원에서 풀밭들이 끊김 없는 초록빛 양탄자처럼 뻗어 있었는데, 헐벗은 자리나 늪 하나 없이, 다만 움푹 팬 곳마다 녹아 가는 눈 무더기가 군데군데 점점이 박혀 있을 뿐이었다."], ["He was not put out of temper even by the sight of the peasants’ horses and colts trampling down his young grass (he told a peasant he met to drive them out), nor by the sarcastic and stupid reply of the peasant Ipat, whom he met on the way, and asked, “Well, Ipat, shall we soon be sowing?”", "그는 농부들의 말과 망아지들이 자기의 어린 풀을 짓밟는 광경을 보고도(그는 마침 마주친 농부에게 그것들을 몰아내라고 일렀다), 또 길에서 마주쳐 \"이봐, 이파트, 곧 파종을 시작할 수 있겠나?\" 하고 물었던 농부 이파트의 비꼬는 듯한 멍청한 대답을 듣고도 기분이 상하지 않았다."], ["“We must get the ploughing done first, Konstantin Dmitrievitch,”", "\"갈이질부터 끝내야지요, 콘스탄친 드미트리치,\""], ["answered Ipat.", "하고 이파트는 대답했다."], ["The further he rode, the happier he became, and plans for the land rose to his mind each better than the last; to plant all his fields with hedges along the southern borders, so that the snow should not lie under them; to divide them up into six fields of arable and three of pasture and hay; to build a cattle yard at the further end of the estate, and to dig a pond and to construct movable pens for the cattle as a means of manuring the land.", "말을 몰아 멀리 갈수록 그는 더 행복해졌고, 땅에 대한 계획이 줄줄이 떠올랐는데 하나하나가 앞의 것보다 더 나았다. 남쪽 경계를 따라 모든 밭에 울타리를 둘러 그 아래에 눈이 쌓이지 않게 하고, 밭을 경작지 여섯과 목초지 및 건초지 셋으로 나누고, 영지의 먼 끝자락에 가축 마당을 짓고, 못을 파고, 땅에 거름을 주는 방편으로 가축용 이동식 우리를 만드는 것이었다."], ["And then eight hundred acres of wheat, three hundred of potatoes, and four hundred of clover, and not one acre exhausted.", "그러고 나서 밀 팔백 에이커, 감자 삼백 에이커, 클로버 사백 에이커를 두되, 단 한 에이커도 지력을 다 소진하지 않게 하는 것이었다."]], [["Absorbed in such dreams, carefully keeping his horse by the hedges, so as not to trample his young crops, he rode up to the laborers who had been sent to sow clover.", "그런 꿈에 잠겨, 어린 작물을 짓밟지 않으려고 말을 울타리 가까이로 조심스레 몰며, 그는 클로버를 파종하러 보낸 일꾼들에게 다가갔다."], ["A cart with the seed in it was standing, not at the edge, but in the middle of the crop, and the winter corn had been torn up by the wheels and trampled by the horse.", "씨앗을 실은 짐수레가 밭 가장자리가 아니라 한가운데에 서 있었고, 겨울 밀이 바퀴에 뜯겨 나가고 말발굽에 짓밟혀 있었다."], ["Both the laborers were sitting in the hedge, probably smoking a pipe together.", "두 일꾼은 울타리에 앉아, 아마도 함께 파이프 담배를 피우고 있는 듯했다."], ["The earth in the cart, with which the seed was mixed, was not crushed to powder, but crusted together or adhering in clods.", "씨앗과 섞인 짐수레 속의 흙은 가루로 으깨진 것이 아니라 딱딱하게 굳었거나 흙덩이로 뭉쳐 들러붙어 있었다."], ["Seeing the master, the laborer, Vassily, went towards the cart, while Mishka set to work sowing.", "주인을 보자 일꾼 바실리는 짐수레 쪽으로 향했고, 미시카는 파종 일을 시작했다."], ["This was not as it should be, but with the laborers Levin seldom lost his temper.", "이것은 마땅한 모습이 아니었으나, 레빈은 일꾼들에게는 좀처럼 화를 내지 않았다."], ["When Vassily came up, Levin told him to lead the horse to the hedge.", "바실리가 다가오자, 레빈은 말을 울타리 쪽으로 끌고 가라고 일렀다."]], [["“It’s all right, sir, it’ll spring up again,”", "\"괜찮습니다, 나리, 다시 돋아날 겁니다,\""], ["responded Vassily.", "바실리가 대꾸했다."]], [["“Please don’t argue,”", "\"제발 말대꾸하지 말고,\""], ["said Levin, “but do as you’re told.”", "레빈이 말했다. \"시키는 대로 하게.\""]], [["“Yes, sir,”", "\"예, 나리,\""], ["answered Vassily, and he took the horse’s head.", "바실리는 대답하며 말 머리를 잡았다."], ["“What a sowing, Konstantin Dmitrievitch,”", "\"이게 무슨 파종입니까, 콘스탄친 드미트리치,\""], ["he said, hesitating; “first rate.", "그가 머뭇거리며 말했다. \"최고지요."], ["Only it’s a work to get about!", "다만 돌아다니는 게 일입니다요!"], ["You drag a ton of earth on your shoes.”", "신발에 흙을 한 짐씩 끌고 다녀야 하니까요.\""]], [["“Why is it you have earth that’s not sifted?”", "\"왜 자네들은 체로 거르지도 않은 흙을 쓰고 있나?\""], ["said Levin.", "레빈이 말했다."]], [["“Well, we crumble it up,”", "\"뭐, 손으로 부숴 가며 합니다,\""], ["answered Vassily, taking up some seed and rolling the earth in his palms.", "바실리는 씨앗을 한 줌 집어 들고 손바닥에 흙을 비벼 굴리며 대답했다."]], [["Vassily was not to blame for their having filled up his cart with unsifted earth, but still it was annoying.", "체로 거르지 않은 흙으로 짐수레를 채운 것은 바실리의 잘못이 아니었지만, 그래도 짜증스러웠다."]], [["Levin had more than once already tried a way he knew for stifling his anger, and turning all that seemed dark right again, and he tried that way now.", "레빈은 이미 여러 번, 화를 가라앉히고 어두워 보이던 모든 것을 다시 제대로 돌려놓는, 자기가 아는 한 가지 방법을 시도해 본 적이 있었는데, 이번에도 그 방법을 시도했다."], ["He watched how Mishka strode along, swinging the huge clods of earth that clung to each foot; and getting off his horse, he took the sieve from Vassily and started sowing himself.", "그는 미시카가 두 발마다 들러붙은 커다란 흙덩이를 휘저으며 성큼성큼 걸어가는 모습을 지켜보다가, 말에서 내려 바실리에게서 체를 받아 들고는 직접 파종을 시작했다."]], [["“Where did you stop?”", "\"어디까지 했지?\""]], [["Vassily pointed to the mark with his foot, and Levin went forward as best he could, scattering the seed on the land.", "바실리가 발로 표시한 자리를 가리켰고, 레빈은 할 수 있는 한 최선을 다해 앞으로 나아가며 씨앗을 땅에 뿌렸다."], ["Walking was as difficult as on a bog, and by the time Levin had ended the row he was in a great heat, and he stopped and gave up the sieve to Vassily.", "걷는 것이 마치 늪 위를 걷는 것만큼이나 힘들어서, 한 고랑을 끝낼 무렵 레빈은 온몸이 후끈 달아올랐고, 그는 멈춰 서서 바실리에게 체를 넘겨주었다."]], [["“Well, master, when summer’s here, mind you don’t scold me for these rows,”", "\"자, 나리, 여름이 오거든 이 고랑들 때문에 저를 나무라지나 마십시오,\""], ["said Vassily.", "바실리가 말했다."]], [["“Eh?”", "\"응?\""], ["said Levin cheerily, already feeling the effect of his method.", "레빈은 벌써 자기 방법의 효과를 느끼며 명랑하게 말했다."]], [["“Why, you’ll see in the summer time.", "\"아니, 여름철이 되면 보실 겁니다."], ["It’ll look different.", "다른 모습이 될 테니까요."], ["Look you where I sowed last spring.", "보십시오, 제가 지난봄에 파종한 데를요."], ["How I did work at it!", "제가 얼마나 공을 들였는지!"], ["I do my best, Konstantin Dmitrievitch, d’ye see, as I would for my own father.", "저는 최선을 다합니다, 콘스탄친 드미트리치, 아시겠지만, 제 친아버지 일이라도 되는 양 말입니다."], ["I don’t like bad work myself, nor would I let another man do it.", "저는 엉성한 일을 좋아하지 않고, 남이 그러는 것도 두고 보지 못합니다."], ["What’s good for the master’s good for us too.", "주인 나리께 좋은 게 저희에게도 좋은 거지요."], ["To look out yonder now,”", "저 멀리를 보면요, 지금,\""], ["said Vassily, pointing, “it does one’s heart good.”", "바실리가 손가락으로 가리키며 말했다. \"마음이 절로 흐뭇해집니다.\""]], [["“It’s a lovely spring, Vassily.”", "\"멋진 봄이군, 바실리.\""]], [["“Why, it’s a spring such as the old men don’t remember the like of.", "\"아니, 이런 봄은 노인네들도 그 비슷한 걸 기억하지 못합니다."], ["I was up home; an old man up there has sown wheat too, about an acre of it.", "제가 고향 집에 올라갔었는데요, 거기 한 노인이 밀도 파종했더군요, 한 에이커쯤요."], ["He was saying you wouldn’t know it from rye.”", "그 양반 말이, 호밀하고 구별이 안 될 정도라더군요.\""]], [["“Have you been sowing wheat long?”", "\"밀을 파종한 지 오래됐나?\""]], [["“Why, sir, it was you taught us the year before last.", "\"아니, 나리, 재작년에 나리께서 가르쳐 주셨잖습니까."], ["You gave me two measures.", "저한테 두 되를 주셨지요."], ["We sold about eight bushels and sowed a rood.”", "여덟 부셸쯤 팔고 사분의 일 에이커를 파종했습니다.\""]], [["“Well, mind you crumble up the clods,”", "\"그래, 흙덩이 잘 부수도록 하고,\""], ["said Levin, going towards his horse, “and keep an eye on Mishka.", "레빈이 자기 말 쪽으로 가며 말했다. \"미시카도 잘 지켜보고."], ["And if there’s a good crop you shall have half a rouble for every acre.”", "작황이 좋으면 에이커마다 반 루블씩 주겠네.\""]], [["“Humbly thankful.", "\"황송합니다."], ["We are very well content, sir, as it is.”", "지금도 저희는 아주 흡족합니다, 나리.\""]], [["Levin got on his horse and rode towards the field where was last year’s clover, and the one which was ploughed ready for the spring corn.", "레빈은 말에 올라 작년에 클로버를 심었던 밭과, 봄 곡식을 위해 갈이질을 해 둔 밭이 있는 쪽으로 말을 몰았다."]], [["The crop of clover coming up in the stubble was magnificent.", "그루터기 사이로 돋아 오르는 클로버 작황은 훌륭했다."], ["It had survived everything, and stood up vividly green through the broken stalks of last year’s wheat.", "그것은 모든 것을 견뎌 내고, 작년 밀의 부러진 줄기들 사이로 선명한 초록빛을 띠며 솟아 있었다."], ["The horse sank in up to the pasterns, and he drew each hoof with a sucking sound out of the half-thawed ground.", "말은 발목까지 푹푹 빠졌고, 반쯤 녹은 땅에서 발굽 하나하나를 쪽쪽 소리를 내며 뽑아 올렸다."], ["Over the ploughland riding was utterly impossible; the horse could only keep a foothold where there was ice, and in the thawing furrows he sank deep in at each step.", "갈아 둔 밭 위로는 말을 타고 가는 것이 도무지 불가능했다. 말은 얼음이 있는 곳에서나 겨우 발을 디딜 수 있었고, 녹아 가는 고랑에서는 걸음마다 깊이 빠져들었다."], ["The ploughland was in splendid condition; in a couple of days it would be fit for harrowing and sowing.", "갈아 둔 밭은 더없이 좋은 상태였다. 이틀이면 써레질과 파종을 하기에 알맞을 것이었다."], ["Everything was capital, everything was cheering.", "모든 것이 더할 나위 없었고, 모든 것이 흐뭇했다."], ["Levin rode back across the streams, hoping the water would have gone down.", "레빈은 물이 빠졌기를 바라며 시냇물을 건너 되돌아갔다."], ["And he did in fact get across, and startled two ducks.", "그리고 실제로 건너는 데 성공했고, 오리 두 마리를 놀라게 했다."], ["“There must be snipe too,”", "\"도요새도 분명 있겠지,\""], ["he thought, and just as he reached the turning homewards he met the forest keeper, who confirmed his theory about the snipe.", "하고 그는 생각했고, 마침 집으로 돌아가는 길목에 이르렀을 때 산지기를 만났는데, 그가 도요새에 관한 그의 짐작을 확인해 주었다."]], [["Levin went home at a trot, so as to have time to eat his dinner and get his gun ready for the evening.", "레빈은 저녁 식사를 할 시간과 저녁 사냥을 위해 총을 손볼 시간을 벌려고, 빠른 걸음으로 집으로 향했다."]], [["# Chapter 14", "# Chapter 14"]], [["As he rode up to the house in the happiest frame of mind, Levin heard the bell ring at the side of the principal entrance of the house.", "더없이 행복한 기분으로 집을 향해 말을 달려가던 레빈은 저택의 정문 옆에서 종이 울리는 소리를 들었다."]], [["“Yes, that’s someone from the railway station,”", "\"그래, 기차역에서 온 누군가야.\""], ["he thought, “just the time to be here from the Moscow train ...", "그는 생각했다. \"모스크바발 기차로 여기 도착할 시간이군……"], ["Who could it be?", "누구일까?"], ["What if it’s brother Nikolay?", "혹시 형 니콜라이가 아닐까?"], ["He did say: ‘Maybe I’ll go to the waters, or maybe I’ll come down to you.’”", "형은 말했지. '온천에 갈지도 모르고, 어쩌면 네게 내려갈지도 모른다'고 말이야.\""], ["He felt dismayed and vexed for the first minute, that his brother Nikolay’s presence should come to disturb his happy mood of spring.", "그는 처음 한순간 형 니콜라이가 나타나 자신의 행복한 봄날의 기분을 흐트러뜨릴까 봐 낙담하고 언짢았다."], ["But he felt ashamed of the feeling, and at once he opened, as it were, the arms of his soul, and with a softened feeling of joy and expectation, now he hoped with all his heart that it was his brother.", "그러나 곧 그런 감정이 부끄러워졌고, 즉시 마음의 두 팔을 활짝 벌리듯 했으며, 부드러워진 기쁨과 기대의 감정으로 이제는 그것이 형이기를 온 마음으로 바랐다."], ["He pricked up his horse, and riding out from behind the acacias he saw a hired three-horse sledge from the railway station, and a gentleman in a fur coat.", "그는 말에 박차를 가했고, 아카시아나무 뒤편에서 빠져나오자 기차역에서 빌려 온 삼두마차 썰매와 모피 외투를 입은 신사가 보였다."], ["It was not his brother.", "형은 아니었다."], ["“Oh, if it were only some nice person one could talk to a little!”", "\"오, 조금이라도 이야기를 나눌 수 있는 괜찮은 사람이기만 하다면!\""], ["he thought.", "그는 생각했다."]], [["“Ah,”", "\"아!\""], ["cried Levin joyfully, flinging up both his hands.", "레빈이 두 손을 번쩍 치켜들며 기쁘게 소리쳤다."], ["“Here’s a delightful visitor!", "\"이렇게 반가운 손님이라니!"], ["Ah, how glad I am to see you!”", "아, 자넬 만나니 정말 기쁘군!\""], ["he shouted, recognizing Stepan Arkadyevitch.", "그는 스테판 아르카지치를 알아보고는 외쳤다."]], [["“I shall find out for certain whether she’s married, or when she’s going to be married,”", "\"그녀가 결혼했는지, 아니면 언제 결혼할 예정인지 확실히 알아낼 수 있겠어.\""], ["he thought.", "그는 생각했다."], ["And on that delicious spring day he felt that the thought of her did not hurt him at all.", "그리고 그 감미로운 봄날에 그는 그녀를 떠올려도 전혀 마음이 아프지 않다는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["“Well, you didn’t expect me, eh?”", "\"이거, 날 만날 줄 몰랐지, 응?\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, getting out of the sledge, splashed with mud on the bridge of his nose, on his cheek, and on his eyebrows, but radiant with health and good spirits.", "콧등이며 뺨이며 눈썹에 진흙이 튄 채로, 그러나 건강과 좋은 기분으로 환하게 빛나며 썰매에서 내린 스테판 아르카지치가 말했다."], ["“I’ve come to see you in the first place,”", "\"첫째로는 자넬 보러 왔고,\""], ["he said, embracing and kissing him, “to have some stand-shooting second, and to sell the forest at Ergushovo third.”", "그는 레빈을 끌어안고 입을 맞추며 말했다. \"둘째로는 사냥을 좀 하려고, 셋째로는 예르구쇼보의 숲을 팔려고 왔지.\""]], [["“Delightful!", "\"반가워!"], ["What a spring we’re having!", "이거 정말 멋진 봄이로군!"], ["How ever did you get along in a sledge?”", "대체 썰매를 타고 어떻게 온 거야?\""]], [["“In a cart it would have been worse still, Konstantin Dmitrievitch,”", "\"수레로 왔으면 더 고생했을 겁니다요, 콘스탄틴 드미트리치.\""], ["answered the driver, who knew him.", "그를 알아본 마부가 대답했다."]], [["“Well, I’m very, very glad to see you,”", "\"그래, 정말이지 자넬 만나서 너무너무 기뻐.\""], ["said Levin, with a genuine smile of childlike delight.", "레빈은 어린아이 같은 진심 어린 기쁨의 미소를 지으며 말했다."]], [["Levin led his friend to the room set apart for visitors, where Stepan Arkadyevitch’s things were carried also—a bag, a gun in a case, a satchel for cigars.", "레빈은 친구를 손님방으로 안내했고, 스테판 아르카지치의 짐도 그리로 옮겨졌다—가방 하나, 케이스에 든 총 한 자루, 시가를 넣는 작은 가방 하나였다."], ["Leaving him there to wash and change his clothes, Levin went off to the counting house to speak about the ploughing and clover.", "그를 거기 남겨 두어 씻고 옷을 갈아입게 하고, 레빈은 경작과 클로버에 대해 의논하러 사무실로 갔다."], ["Agafea Mihalovna, always very anxious for the credit of the house, met him in the hall with inquiries about dinner.", "늘 집안의 체면을 무척 염려하는 아가피야 미하일로브나가 저녁 식사에 관해 물어보려고 현관에서 그를 맞았다."]], [["“Do just as you like, only let it be as soon as possible,”", "\"당신 좋을 대로 하세요, 다만 되도록 빨리 차려 주시고요.\""], ["he said, and went to the bailiff.", "그는 이렇게 말하고 관리인에게 갔다."]], [["When he came back, Stepan Arkadyevitch, washed and combed, came out of his room with a beaming smile, and they went upstairs together.", "그가 돌아오자, 말끔히 씻고 머리를 빗은 스테판 아르카지치가 환한 미소를 지으며 자기 방에서 나왔고, 두 사람은 함께 위층으로 올라갔다."]], [["“Well, I am glad I managed to get away to you!", "\"이야, 자네한테 빠져나올 수 있어서 정말 기뻐!"], ["Now I shall understand what the mysterious business is that you are always absorbed in here.", "이제야 자네가 늘 여기 파묻혀 골몰하는 그 신비로운 일이 뭔지 알 것 같군."], ["No, really, I envy you.", "아니, 정말이지 자네가 부러워."], ["What a house, how nice it all is!", "이 집이며, 이 모든 게 얼마나 멋진지!"], ["So bright, so cheerful!”", "이렇게 밝고, 이렇게 경쾌하다니!\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, forgetting that it was not always spring and fine weather like that day.", "스테판 아르카지치는 늘 그날처럼 봄날의 화창한 날씨인 것은 아니라는 사실을 잊은 채 말했다."], ["“And your nurse is simply charming!", "\"그리고 자네 가정부도 정말 매력적이야!"], ["A pretty maid in an apron might be even more agreeable, perhaps; but for your severe monastic style it does very well.”", "앞치마를 두른 예쁜 하녀라면 어쩌면 더 흐뭇할지도 모르지만, 자네의 엄격한 수도원식 살림살이에는 이쪽이 아주 잘 어울려.\""]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch told him many interesting pieces of news; especially interesting to Levin was the news that his brother, Sergey Ivanovitch, was intending to pay him a visit in the summer.", "스테판 아르카지치는 그에게 흥미로운 소식을 많이 들려주었는데, 그중에서도 레빈에게 특히 흥미로웠던 것은 형 세르게이 이바노비치가 여름에 그를 찾아올 작정이라는 소식이었다."]], [["Not one word did Stepan Arkadyevitch say in reference to Kitty and the Shtcherbatskys; he merely gave him greetings from his wife.", "스테판 아르카지치는 키티와 셰르바츠키 집안에 관해서는 한마디도 하지 않았다. 그저 아내가 안부를 전한다고만 했을 뿐이다."], ["Levin was grateful to him for his delicacy and was very glad of his visitor.", "레빈은 그의 세심한 배려에 고마웠고 그가 찾아와 준 것이 무척 기뻤다."], ["As always happened with him during his solitude, a mass of ideas and feelings had been accumulating within him, which he could not communicate to those about him.", "홀로 지낼 때면 늘 그렇듯이, 그의 안에는 주위 사람들에게 전할 수 없는 숱한 생각과 감정이 쌓여 있었다."], ["And now he poured out upon Stepan Arkadyevitch his poetic joy in the spring, and his failures and plans for the land, and his thoughts and criticisms on the books he had been reading, and the idea of his own book, the basis of which really was, though he was unaware of it himself, a criticism of all the old books on agriculture.", "그래서 이제 그는 스테판 아르카지치에게 봄에 대한 자신의 시적인 기쁨이며, 토지를 두고 겪은 실패와 계획이며, 읽어 온 책들에 대한 생각과 비판이며, 자신이 쓰려는 책에 대한 구상을 쏟아 냈는데, 그 책의 바탕은 사실—본인은 자각하지 못했지만—농업에 관한 모든 옛 서적에 대한 비판이었다."], ["Stepan Arkadyevitch, always charming, understanding everything at the slightest reference, was particularly charming on this visit, and Levin noticed in him a special tenderness, as it were, and a new tone of respect that flattered him.", "늘 매력적이고 아주 사소한 언급만으로도 모든 것을 이해하는 스테판 아르카지치는 이번 방문에서 유독 매력적이었고, 레빈은 그에게서 어떤 각별한 다정함이랄까, 그리고 자신을 우쭐하게 만드는 새로운 존경의 어조를 느꼈다."]], [["The efforts of Agafea Mihalovna and the cook, that the dinner should be particularly good, only ended in the two famished friends attacking the preliminary course, eating a great deal of bread and butter, salt goose and salted mushrooms, and in Levin’s finally ordering the soup to be served without the accompaniment of little pies, with which the cook had particularly meant to impress their visitor.", "저녁 식사를 특별히 잘 차리려던 아가피야 미하일로브나와 요리사의 노력은, 굶주린 두 친구가 전채 요리에 달려들어 버터 바른 빵이며 소금에 절인 거위 고기며 소금에 절인 버섯을 잔뜩 먹어 치우고, 마침내 레빈이 요리사가 손님에게 특별히 강한 인상을 주려고 준비했던 작은 파이도 곁들이지 말고 수프부터 내오라고 시키는 것으로 끝나 버렸다."], ["But though Stepan Arkadyevitch was accustomed to very different dinners, he thought everything excellent: the herb brandy, and the bread, and the butter, and above all the salt goose and the mushrooms, and the nettle soup, and the chicken in white sauce, and the white Crimean wine—everything was superb and delicious.", "그러나 스테판 아르카지치는 전혀 다른 식사에 익숙했음에도 모든 것이 훌륭하다고 여겼다. 약초 보드카며, 빵이며, 버터며, 무엇보다 소금에 절인 거위 고기와 버섯이며, 쐐기풀 수프며, 흰 소스를 끼얹은 닭고기며, 크림산 백포도주까지—모든 것이 더없이 훌륭하고 맛있었다."]], [["“Splendid, splendid!”", "\"근사해, 근사하군!\""], ["he said, lighting a fat cigar after the roast.", "그는 구운 고기를 먹은 뒤 굵은 시가에 불을 붙이며 말했다."], ["“I feel as if, coming to you, I had landed on a peaceful shore after the noise and jolting of a steamer.", "\"자네한테 오니, 마치 증기선의 소음과 흔들림을 뒤로하고 평화로운 기슭에 닿은 기분이야."], ["And so you maintain that the laborer himself is an element to be studied and to regulate the choice of methods in agriculture.", "그러니까 자네 주장은, 노동자 자신이 연구 대상이며 농업에서 방법을 선택하는 기준이 된다는 거지."], ["Of course, I’m an ignorant outsider; but I should fancy theory and its application will have its influence on the laborer too.”", "물론 나야 무지한 외부인이지만, 이론과 그 적용이 노동자에게도 영향을 미치리라고 생각하는데.\""]], [["“Yes, but wait a bit.", "\"그래, 하지만 잠깐만."], ["I’m not talking of political economy, I’m talking of the science of agriculture.", "난 정치경제학을 이야기하는 게 아니라 농업의 과학을 이야기하는 거야."], ["It ought to be like the natural sciences, and to observe given phenomena and the laborer in his economic, ethnographical....”", "그건 자연과학 같아야 하고, 주어진 현상과 노동자를 그의 경제적, 민족지학적인……\""]], [["At that instant Agafea Mihalovna came in with jam.", "바로 그 순간 아가피야 미하일로브나가 잼을 들고 들어왔다."]], [["“Oh, Agafea Mihalovna,”", "\"오, 아가피야 미하일로브나,\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, kissing the tips of his plump fingers, “what salt goose, what herb brandy!...", "스테판 아르카지치가 통통한 손가락 끝에 입을 맞추며 말했다. \"그 거위 고기는 또 어떻고, 그 약초 보드카는 또 어떻소!……"], ["What do you think, isn’t it time to start, Kostya?”", "어때, 슬슬 나설 시간 아닌가, 코스챠?\""], ["he added.", "그가 덧붙였다."]], [["Levin looked out of the window at the sun sinking behind the bare tree-tops of the forest.", "레빈은 창밖으로 숲의 헐벗은 나무 꼭대기 너머로 지고 있는 해를 바라보았다."]], [["“Yes, it’s time,”", "\"그래, 갈 시간이야.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“Kouzma, get ready the trap,”", "\"쿠지마, 마차를 준비해 둬.\""], ["and he ran downstairs.", "그러고는 아래층으로 달려 내려갔다."]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch, going down, carefully took the canvas cover off his varnished gun case with his own hands, and opening it, began to get ready his expensive new-fashioned gun.", "스테판 아르카지치는 아래층으로 내려가, 옻칠한 자신의 총 케이스에서 천 덮개를 손수 조심스럽게 벗기고는, 그것을 열어 값비싸고 신식인 자기 총을 준비하기 시작했다."], ["Kouzma, who already scented a big tip, never left Stepan Arkadyevitch’s side, and put on him both his stockings and boots, a task which Stepan Arkadyevitch readily left him.", "이미 두둑한 팁 냄새를 맡은 쿠지마는 스테판 아르카지치의 곁을 한시도 떠나지 않고 그에게 양말과 장화를 신겨 주었는데, 스테판 아르카지치는 기꺼이 그 일을 그에게 맡겼다."]], [["“Kostya, give orders that if the merchant Ryabinin comes ...", "\"코스챠, 만약 상인 랴비닌이 오거든……"], ["I told him to come today, he’s to be brought in and to wait for me....”", "오늘 오라고 일러 뒀거든, 안으로 들여서 날 기다리게 하라고 일러 두게……\""]], [["“Why, do you mean to say you’re selling the forest to Ryabinin?”", "\"아니, 숲을 랴비닌한테 판다는 말인가?\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"그래."], ["Do you know him?”", "자네 그를 아나?\""]], [["“To be sure I do.", "\"물론 알지. 그와 거래해야 했던 적이 있어."], ["I have had to do business with him, ‘positively and conclusively.’”", "'확실하고도 결정적으로' 말이야.\""]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch laughed.", "스테판 아르카지치가 웃었다."], ["“Positively and conclusively” were the merchant’s favorite words.", "\"확실하고도 결정적으로\"는 그 상인이 즐겨 쓰는 말이었다."]], [["“Yes, it’s wonderfully funny the way he talks.", "\"그래, 그 사람 말투가 정말 우습기 짝이 없지."], ["She knows where her master’s going!”", "이 녀석은 제 주인이 어디 가는지 다 안다니까!\""], ["he added, patting Laska, who hung about Levin, whining and licking his hands, his boots, and his gun.", "그는 끙끙대며 레빈의 손이며 장화며 총을 핥아 대며 그의 주위를 맴도는 라스카를 쓰다듬으며 덧붙였다."]], [["The trap was already at the steps when they went out.", "그들이 나갔을 때 마차는 이미 현관 계단 앞에 와 있었다."]], [["“I told them to bring the trap round; or would you rather walk?”", "\"마차를 돌려 오라고 일러 뒀어. 아니면 걸어가는 게 낫겠나?\""]], [["“No, we’d better drive,”", "\"아니, 타고 가는 게 낫겠어.\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, getting into the trap.", "스테판 아르카지치는 마차에 오르며 말했다."], ["He sat down, tucked the tiger-skin rug round him, and lighted a cigar.", "그는 자리에 앉아 호랑이 가죽 무릎덮개를 몸에 두르고 시가에 불을 붙였다."], ["“How is it you don’t smoke?", "\"자넨 어째서 담배를 안 피우나?"], ["A cigar is a sort of thing, not exactly a pleasure, but the crown and outward sign of pleasure.", "시가란 게 말이야, 딱히 즐거움이라고 할 수는 없어도, 즐거움의 정점이자 겉으로 드러난 표시 같은 거지."], ["Come, this is life!", "자, 이게 바로 인생이야!"], ["How splendid it is!", "얼마나 근사한가!"], ["This is how I should like to live!”", "나도 이렇게 살고 싶다니까!\""]], [["“Why, who prevents you?”", "\"아니, 누가 자넬 말리기라도 하나?\""], ["said Levin, smiling.", "레빈이 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["“No, you’re a lucky man!", "\"아니야, 자넨 운 좋은 사람이야!"], ["You’ve got everything you like.", "자넨 좋아하는 걸 다 가졌잖아."], ["You like horses—and you have them; dogs—you have them; shooting—you have it; farming—you have it.”", "말을 좋아하면—말이 있고, 개를 좋아하면—개가 있고, 사냥을 좋아하면—사냥이 있고, 농사를 좋아하면—농사가 있고.\""]], [["“Perhaps because I rejoice in what I have, and don’t fret for what I haven’t,”", "\"아마도 내가 가진 것에 기뻐하고, 갖지 못한 것을 안달하지 않기 때문이겠지.\""], ["said Levin, thinking of Kitty.", "레빈은 키티를 떠올리며 말했다."]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch comprehended, looked at him, but said nothing.", "스테판 아르카지치는 그 말뜻을 알아채고 그를 바라보았으나, 아무 말도 하지 않았다."]], [["Levin was grateful to Oblonsky for noticing, with his never-failing tact, that he dreaded conversation about the Shtcherbatskys, and so saying nothing about them.", "레빈은 오블론스키가 한결같은 재치로 자신이 셰르바츠키 집안에 관한 이야기를 두려워한다는 것을 눈치채고 그들에 대해 아무 말도 하지 않는 것이 고마웠다."], ["But now Levin was longing to find out what was tormenting him so, yet he had not the courage to begin.", "그러나 이제 레빈은 무엇이 그토록 친구를 괴롭히고 있는지 알아내고 싶었지만, 차마 입을 뗄 용기가 나지 않았다."]], [["“Come, tell me how things are going with you,”", "\"자, 자네 사정은 어떤지 이야기해 보게.\""], ["said Levin, bethinking himself that it was not nice of him to think only of himself.", "레빈은 자기 자신만 생각하는 것이 좋지 않다는 생각이 들어 말했다."]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch’s eyes sparkled merrily.", "스테판 아르카지치의 눈이 즐겁게 반짝였다."]], [["“You don’t admit, I know, that one can be fond of new rolls when one has had one’s rations of bread—to your mind it’s a crime; but I don’t count life as life without love,”", "\"자넨 인정하지 않지, 나도 알아. 빵을 배급받았으면서도 새 롤빵을 좋아할 수 있다는 걸 말이야—자네 생각으론 그게 죄겠지."], ["he said, taking Levin’s question his own way.", "하지만 난 사랑이 없는 삶은 삶으로 치지 않아.\" 그는 레빈의 질문을 자기 식대로 받아들이며 말했다."], ["“What am I to do?", "\"어쩌겠나?"], ["I’m made that way.", "난 그렇게 생겨 먹은걸."], ["And really, one does so little harm to anyone, and gives oneself so much pleasure....”", "게다가 정말이지 누구에게 끼치는 해는 그토록 적고, 자기 자신은 그토록 큰 즐거움을 얻는데……\""]], [["“What!", "\"뭐라고!"], ["is there something new, then?”", "그럼 뭔가 새로운 일이 있다는 건가?\""], ["queried Levin.", "레빈이 물었다."]], [["“Yes, my boy, there is!", "\"그렇다네, 이 친구야, 있고말고!"], ["There, do you see, you know the type of Ossian’s women....", "자, 봐, 자네도 오시안의 여인들 같은 부류를 알 거야……"], ["Women, such as one sees in dreams....", "꿈속에서나 보는 그런 여자들 말이야……"], ["Well, these women are sometimes to be met in reality ...", "그런데 그런 여자들을 이따금 현실에서도 만날 수 있거든……"], ["and these women are terrible.", "그리고 그런 여자들은 무서워."], ["Woman, don’t you know, is such a subject that however much you study it, it’s always perfectly new.”", "여자란, 자네도 알겠지만, 아무리 연구해도 언제나 완전히 새로운 그런 대상이란 말이야.\""]], [["“Well, then, it would be better not to study it.”", "\"그렇다면 차라리 연구하지 않는 편이 낫겠군.\""]], [["“No.", "\"아니야."], ["Some mathematician has said that enjoyment lies in the search for truth, not in the finding it.”", "어느 수학자가 말했지, 즐거움은 진리를 찾는 데 있지 진리를 발견하는 데 있는 게 아니라고.\""]], [["Levin listened in silence, and in spite of all the efforts he made, he could not in the least enter into the feelings of his friend and understand his sentiments and the charm of studying such women.", "레빈은 잠자코 듣고 있었지만, 아무리 애를 써도 친구의 감정 속으로 조금도 들어갈 수 없었고, 그런 여자들을 연구하는 데서 오는 그 정서와 매력을 이해할 수 없었다."]], [["# Chapter 15", "# Chapter 15"]], [["The place fixed on for the stand-shooting was not far above a stream in a little aspen copse.", "총사냥을 하기로 정한 자리는 시냇물 바로 위편, 작은 사시나무 덤불 속이었다."], ["On reaching the copse, Levin got out of the trap and led Oblonsky to a corner of a mossy, swampy glade, already quite free from snow.", "덤불에 다다르자 레빈은 마차에서 내려 오블론스키를 데리고, 이미 눈이 말끔히 녹은 이끼 낀 늪지대 빈터 한구석으로 안내했다."], ["He went back himself to a double birch tree on the other side, and leaning his gun on the fork of a dead lower branch, he took off his full overcoat, fastened his belt again, and worked his arms to see if they were free.", "그러고는 자기는 반대편의 한 쌍으로 갈라진 자작나무 곁으로 돌아가, 죽은 아래쪽 가지가 두 갈래로 벌어진 곳에 총을 기대 세우고, 두툼한 외투를 벗어 다시 허리띠를 졸라매고는, 팔이 자유롭게 움직이는지 휘둘러 보았다."]], [["Gray old Laska, who had followed them, sat down warily opposite him and pricked up her ears.", "그들을 따라온 잿빛 늙은 라스카가 그의 맞은편에 조심스레 앉아 귀를 쫑긋 세웠다."], ["The sun was setting behind a thick forest, and in the glow of sunset the birch trees, dotted about in the aspen copse, stood out clearly with their hanging twigs, and their buds swollen almost to bursting.", "해가 울창한 숲 뒤로 지고 있었고, 노을빛 속에서 사시나무 덤불 사이사이에 흩어져 선 자작나무들이 늘어진 잔가지와 거의 터질 듯 부풀어 오른 눈을 단 채 또렷이 드러났다."]], [["From the thickest parts of the copse, where the snow still remained, came the faint sound of narrow winding threads of water running away.", "아직 눈이 남아 있는 덤불 가장 깊숙한 곳에서는, 좁다랗게 굽이쳐 흘러내리는 가느다란 물줄기의 희미한 소리가 들려왔다."], ["Tiny birds twittered, and now and then fluttered from tree to tree.", "작은 새들이 지저귀며, 이따금 나무에서 나무로 푸드덕 옮겨 다녔다."]], [["In the pauses of complete stillness there came the rustle of last year’s leaves, stirred by the thawing of the earth and the growth of the grass.", "완전한 정적이 잠시 멈출 때마다, 흙이 녹고 풀이 돋아나며 들썩이는 지난해 묵은 잎들의 바스락거리는 소리가 들려왔다."]], [["“Imagine!", "'세상에!"], ["One can hear and see the grass growing!”", "풀이 자라는 소리가 들리고 그게 보이다니!'"], ["Levin said to himself, noticing a wet, slate-colored aspen leaf moving beside a blade of young grass.", "레빈은 어린 풀잎 곁에서 젖은 잿빛 사시나무 잎이 움직이는 것을 보며 속으로 중얼거렸다."], ["He stood, listened, and gazed sometimes down at the wet mossy ground, sometimes at Laska listening all alert, sometimes at the sea of bare tree tops that stretched on the slope below him, sometimes at the darkening sky, covered with white streaks of cloud.", "그는 선 채로 귀를 기울이며, 때로는 젖은 이끼 낀 땅을 내려다보고, 때로는 온 신경을 곤두세워 듣고 있는 라스카를 보고, 때로는 그의 아래 비탈로 펼쳐진 헐벗은 나무 꼭대기들의 바다를 보고, 때로는 흰 구름 줄기로 뒤덮인 채 점점 어두워지는 하늘을 바라보았다."]], [["A hawk flew high over a forest far away with slow sweep of its wings; another flew with exactly the same motion in the same direction and vanished.", "매 한 마리가 느릿느릿 날개를 휘저으며 저 멀리 숲 위를 높이 날아갔고, 또 한 마리가 똑같은 몸짓으로 같은 방향으로 날아가다 사라졌다."], ["The birds twittered more and more loudly and busily in the thicket.", "새들이 덤불 속에서 점점 더 크게, 더 분주하게 지저귀었다."], ["An owl hooted not far off, and Laska, starting, stepped cautiously a few steps forward, and putting her head on one side, began to listen intently.", "멀지 않은 곳에서 부엉이가 울자, 라스카가 흠칫하더니 조심스레 몇 발짝 앞으로 나아가 고개를 한쪽으로 기울이고 골똘히 귀를 기울이기 시작했다."], ["Beyond the stream was heard the cuckoo.", "시냇물 너머에서는 뻐꾸기 소리가 들려왔다."], ["Twice she uttered her usual cuckoo call, and then gave a hoarse, hurried call and broke down.", "뻐꾸기는 평소처럼 두 번 울고는, 쉰 듯한 급한 소리를 한 번 내뱉더니 뚝 그쳤다."]], [["“Imagine! the cuckoo already!”", "\"세상에, 벌써 뻐꾸기가!\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, coming out from behind a bush.", "스테판 아르카지치가 덤불 뒤에서 나오며 말했다."]], [["“Yes, I hear it,”", "\"그래, 들려.\""], ["answered Levin, reluctantly breaking the stillness with his voice, which sounded disagreeable to himself.", "레빈은 마지못해 자기 목소리로 정적을 깨뜨리며 대답했는데, 그 소리가 스스로도 듣기 거북했다."], ["“Now it’s coming!”", "\"이제 날아오겠어!\""]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch’s figure again went behind the bush, and Levin saw nothing but the bright flash of a match, followed by the red glow and blue smoke of a cigarette.", "스테판 아르카지치의 모습이 다시 덤불 뒤로 사라졌고, 레빈에게는 성냥불의 환한 섬광에 이어 담뱃불의 붉은 빛과 푸른 연기만 보였다."]], [["“Tchk!", "\"찰칵!"], ["tchk!”", "찰칵!\""], ["came the snapping sound of Stepan Arkadyevitch cocking his gun.", "스테판 아르카지치가 총의 공이치기를 당기는 소리가 났다."]], [["“What’s that cry?”", "\"저 우는 소리는 뭐지?\""], ["asked Oblonsky, drawing Levin’s attention to a prolonged cry, as though a colt were whinnying in a high voice, in play.", "오블론스키가 마치 망아지가 장난스레 높은 목소리로 히힝거리는 듯한, 길게 늘어지는 울음소리에 레빈의 주의를 돌리며 물었다."]], [["“Oh, don’t you know it?", "\"아, 자네 저것도 모르나?"], ["That’s the hare.", "산토끼야."], ["But enough talking!", "그건 그렇고 말은 그만!"], ["Listen, it’s flying!”", "들어 봐, 날아오고 있어!\""], ["almost shrieked Levin, cocking his gun.", "레빈이 공이치기를 당기며 거의 비명을 지르듯 외쳤다."]], [["They heard a shrill whistle in the distance, and in the exact time, so well known to the sportsman, two seconds later—another, a third, and after the third whistle the hoarse, guttural cry could be heard.", "저 멀리서 날카로운 휘파람 소리가 들렸고, 사냥꾼에게 너무도 익숙한 바로 그 시간 간격에 맞춰 이 초쯤 뒤에 또 한 번, 그리고 세 번째 소리가 났으며, 세 번째 휘파람 뒤에는 쉰 듯한 목쉰 울음소리가 들려왔다."]], [["Levin looked about him to right and to left, and there, just facing him against the dusky blue sky above the confused mass of tender shoots of the aspens, he saw the flying bird.", "레빈은 오른쪽 왼쪽을 두루 둘러보았고, 바로 거기, 부드러운 사시나무 새싹들이 어수선하게 뒤엉킨 무더기 위쪽 어스레한 푸른 하늘을 배경으로, 자기 정면에서 날아오는 새를 보았다. 새는 곧장 그를 향해 날아오고 있었다."], ["It was flying straight towards him; the guttural cry, like the even tearing of some strong stuff, sounded close to his ear; the long beak and neck of the bird could be seen, and at the very instant when Levin was taking aim, behind the bush where Oblonsky stood, there was a flash of red lightning: the bird dropped like an arrow, and darted upwards again.", "무언가 질긴 천이 고르게 찢기는 듯한 목쉰 울음소리가 그의 귓전 가까이에서 울렸고, 새의 긴 부리와 목이 보였다. 그리고 레빈이 막 겨냥하는 바로 그 순간, 오블론스키가 서 있는 덤불 뒤에서 붉은 번개가 번쩍였다."], ["Again came the red flash and the sound of a blow, and fluttering its wings as though trying to keep up in the air, the bird halted, stopped still an instant, and fell with a heavy splash on the slushy ground.", "새는 화살처럼 떨어졌다가 다시 위로 솟구쳤다. 또 한 번 붉은 섬광과 명중하는 소리가 났고, 새는 공중에 떠 있으려는 듯 날개를 푸드덕거리다 멈추더니, 한순간 가만히 있다가 질척한 땅 위로 묵직하게 철퍼덕 떨어졌다."]], [["“Can I have missed it?”", "\"내가 놓친 건가?\""], ["shouted Stepan Arkadyevitch, who could not see for the smoke.", "연기 때문에 앞이 보이지 않는 스테판 아르카지치가 소리쳤다."]], [["“Here it is!”", "\"여기 있어!\""], ["said Levin, pointing to Laska, who with one ear raised, wagging the end of her shaggy tail, came slowly back as though she would prolong the pleasure, and as it were smiling, brought the dead bird to her master.", "레빈이 라스카를 가리키며 말했다. 라스카는 한쪽 귀를 쫑긋 세우고 텁수룩한 꼬리 끝을 흔들며, 마치 그 즐거움을 늘이려는 듯 천천히 돌아왔고, 흡사 미소를 짓는 양 죽은 새를 주인에게 물어다 주었다."], ["“Well, I’m glad you were successful,”", "\"음, 자네가 명중시켜서 기쁘군.\""], ["said Levin, who, at the same time, had a sense of envy that he had not succeeded in shooting the snipe.", "레빈은 그렇게 말했지만, 동시에 자기는 도요새를 쏘아 맞히지 못했다는 부러움도 느꼈다."]], [["“It was a bad shot from the right barrel,”", "\"오른쪽 총신에서 나간 게 형편없는 사격이었어.\""], ["responded Stepan Arkadyevitch, loading his gun.", "스테판 아르카지치가 총을 장전하며 대꾸했다."], ["“Sh...", "\"쉿..."], ["it’s flying!”", "날아온다!\""]], [["The shrill whistles rapidly following one another were heard again.", "날카로운 휘파람 소리가 잇따라 빠르게 다시 들려왔다."], ["Two snipe, playing and chasing one another, and only whistling, not crying, flew straight at the very heads of the sportsmen.", "도요새 두 마리가 장난치며 서로 쫓고, 울지는 않고 휘파람만 불면서, 곧장 사냥꾼들의 머리 위로 날아들었다."], ["There was the report of four shots, and like swallows the snipe turned swift somersaults in the air and vanished from sight.", "네 발의 총성이 울렸고, 도요새들은 제비처럼 공중에서 잽싸게 공중제비를 돌더니 시야에서 사라졌다."]], [["The stand-shooting was capital.", "총사냥은 더없이 훌륭했다."], ["Stepan Arkadyevitch shot two more birds and Levin two, of which one was not found.", "스테판 아르카지치가 두 마리를 더 쏘았고, 레빈도 두 마리를 잡았는데, 그중 한 마리는 찾지 못했다."], ["It began to get dark.", "날이 어두워지기 시작했다."], ["Venus, bright and silvery, shone with her soft light low down in the west behind the birch trees, and high up in the east twinkled the red lights of Arcturus.", "밝고 은빛으로 빛나는 금성이 자작나무들 뒤편 서쪽 하늘 낮은 곳에서 부드러운 빛을 내뿜었고, 동쪽 높은 곳에서는 아르크투루스의 붉은 빛이 깜빡였다."], ["Over his head Levin made out the stars of the Great Bear and lost them again.", "레빈은 머리 위로 큰곰자리의 별들을 알아보았다가 다시 놓쳤다."], ["The snipe had ceased flying; but Levin resolved to stay a little longer, till Venus, which he saw below a branch of birch, should be above it, and the stars of the Great Bear should be perfectly plain.", "도요새는 더 이상 날지 않았다. 하지만 레빈은 자작나무 가지 아래로 보이는 금성이 그 위로 떠오르고 큰곰자리의 별들이 완전히 또렷해질 때까지 조금 더 머물기로 마음먹었다."], ["Venus had risen above the branch, and the ear of the Great Bear with its shaft was now all plainly visible against the dark blue sky, yet still he waited.", "금성은 가지 위로 떠올랐고, 손잡이가 달린 큰곰자리의 국자가 이제 짙푸른 하늘을 배경으로 또렷이 다 보였는데도, 그는 여전히 기다렸다."]], [["“Isn’t it time to go home?”", "\"이제 집에 갈 때가 되지 않았나?\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch.", "스테판 아르카지치가 말했다."]], [["It was quite still now in the copse, and not a bird was stirring.", "이제 덤불 속은 아주 고요했고, 새 한 마리 움직이지 않았다."]], [["“Let’s stay a little while,”", "\"조금만 더 있세.\""], ["answered Levin.", "레빈이 대답했다."]], [["“As you like.”", "\"자네 좋을 대로.\""]], [["They were standing now about fifteen paces from one another.", "이제 그들은 서로 열다섯 걸음쯤 떨어져 서 있었다."]], [["“Stiva!”", "\"스티바!\""], ["said Levin unexpectedly; “how is it you don’t tell me whether your sister-in-law’s married yet, or when she’s going to be?”", "레빈이 불쑥 말했다. \"자네 처제가 벌써 결혼했는지, 아니면 언제 할 건지 어째서 내게 말해 주지 않는 거지?\""]], [["Levin felt so resolute and serene that no answer, he fancied, could affect him.", "레빈은 어떤 대답이 나와도 자기 마음을 흔들지 못하리라 여길 만큼 단호하고 평온한 기분이었다."], ["But he had never dreamed of what Stepan Arkadyevitch replied.", "하지만 스테판 아르카지치가 한 대답은 꿈에도 생각지 못한 것이었다."]], [["“She’s never thought of being married, and isn’t thinking of it; but she’s very ill, and the doctors have sent her abroad.", "\"그 애는 결혼할 생각을 해 본 적도 없고, 지금도 그럴 생각이 없어. 다만 몸이 몹시 아파서 의사들이 외국으로 보냈지."], ["They’re positively afraid she may not live.”", "다들 그 애가 살지 못할까 봐 정말로 걱정하고 있어.\""]], [["“What!”", "\"뭐라고!\""], ["cried Levin.", "레빈이 외쳤다."], ["“Very ill?", "\"몹시 아프다고?"], ["What is wrong with her?", "어디가 아픈 건데?"], ["How has she...?”", "어쩌다 그 애가...?\""]], [["While they were saying this, Laska, with ears pricked up, was looking upwards at the sky, and reproachfully at them.", "그들이 이런 말을 주고받는 동안, 라스카는 귀를 쫑긋 세운 채 하늘을 올려다보고, 또 그들을 나무라듯 바라보고 있었다."]], [["“They have chosen a time to talk,”", "'하필 이런 때에 이야기들을 하다니.'"], ["she was thinking.", "라스카는 생각했다."], ["“It’s on the wing....", "'날고 있잖아..."], ["Here it is, yes, it is.", "저기, 그래, 맞아."], ["They’ll miss it,”", "저것들이 놓치겠어.'"], ["thought Laska.", "라스카는 생각했다."]], [["But at that very instant both suddenly heard a shrill whistle which, as it were, smote on their ears, and both suddenly seized their guns and two flashes gleamed, and two bangs sounded at the very same instant.", "하지만 바로 그 순간 둘은 갑자기 귓전을 때리는 듯한 날카로운 휘파람 소리를 들었고, 둘 다 별안간 총을 움켜쥐었으며, 두 줄기 섬광이 번쩍이고 두 발의 총성이 똑같은 순간에 울렸다."], ["The snipe flying high above instantly folded its wings and fell into a thicket, bending down the delicate shoots.", "높이 날던 도요새가 즉시 날개를 접고 가녀린 새싹들을 휘어뜨리며 덤불 속으로 떨어졌다."]], [["“Splendid!", "\"멋지군!"], ["Together!”", "동시에 맞혔어!\""], ["cried Levin, and he ran with Laska into the thicket to look for the snipe.", "레빈이 외치며, 도요새를 찾으러 라스카와 함께 덤불 속으로 달려갔다."]], [["“Oh, yes, what was it that was unpleasant?”", "'아, 그래, 뭐가 그렇게 불쾌했더라?'"], ["he wondered.", "그는 의아해했다."], ["“Yes, Kitty’s ill....", "'그래, 키티가 아프다지..."], ["Well, it can’t be helped; I’m very sorry,”", "음, 어쩔 수 없지. 정말 안됐어.'"], ["he thought.", "그는 생각했다."]], [["“She’s found it!", "\"찾았구나!"], ["Isn’t she a clever thing?”", "영리한 녀석이지?\""], ["he said, taking the warm bird from Laska’s mouth and packing it into the almost full game bag.", "그는 라스카의 입에서 따뜻한 새를 받아 들어, 거의 가득 찬 사냥 자루에 챙겨 넣으며 말했다."], ["“I’ve got it, Stiva!”", "\"잡았어, 스티바!\""], ["he shouted.", "그가 소리쳤다."]], [["# Chapter 16", "# Chapter 16"]], [["On the way home Levin asked all details of Kitty’s illness and the Shtcherbatskys’ plans, and though he would have been ashamed to admit it, he was pleased at what he heard.", "집으로 돌아오는 길에 레빈은 키티의 병에 대해, 그리고 쉬체르바츠키 집안의 사정에 대해 시시콜콜 캐물었다. 인정하기는 부끄러웠지만, 그는 자기가 들은 이야기에 마음이 흡족했다."], ["He was pleased that there was still hope, and still more pleased that she should be suffering who had made him suffer so much.", "아직 희망이 남아 있다는 사실이 기뻤고, 자신을 그토록 괴롭게 만든 그 여자가 지금 괴로워하고 있다는 사실은 더더욱 기뻤다."], ["But when Stepan Arkadyevitch began to speak of the causes of Kitty’s illness, and mentioned Vronsky’s name, Levin cut him short.", "하지만 스테판 아르카지치가 키티의 병의 원인을 이야기하면서 브론스키의 이름을 입에 올리자, 레빈은 그의 말을 잘라 버렸다."]], [["“I have no right whatever to know family matters, and, to tell the truth, no interest in them either.”", "\"나는 그 집안일을 알 권리가 조금도 없고, 솔직히 말하면 관심도 없어.\""]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch smiled hardly perceptibly, catching the instantaneous change he knew so well in Levin’s face, which had become as gloomy as it had been bright a minute before.", "스테판 아르카지치는 레빈의 얼굴에서 자기가 너무도 잘 아는 그 순간적인 변화를 알아채고는, 거의 알아보기 힘들 만큼 희미하게 미소 지었다. 일 분 전만 해도 환하던 그 얼굴이 어느새 침울하게 흐려져 있었다."]], [["“Have you quite settled about the forest with Ryabinin?”", "\"랴비닌과 숲에 관한 일은 다 매듭지었나?\""], ["asked Levin.", "레빈이 물었다."]], [["“Yes, it’s settled.", "\"그래, 다 끝났어."], ["The price is magnificent; thirty-eight thousand.", "값을 아주 잘 받았지. 삼만 팔천."], ["Eight straight away, and the rest in six years.", "당장 팔천을 받고, 나머지는 육 년에 걸쳐 받기로 했어."], ["I’ve been bothering about it for ever so long.", "그 일로 어찌나 오래 골치를 앓았는지 몰라."], ["No one would give more.”", "그보다 더 쳐줄 사람은 아무도 없었거든.\""]], [["“Then you’ve as good as given away your forest for nothing,”", "\"그렇다면 자네는 숲을 거저 내준 거나 다름없군.\""], ["said Levin gloomily.", "레빈이 침울하게 말했다."]], [["“How do you mean for nothing?”", "\"거저라니, 그게 무슨 소린가?\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch with a good-humored smile, knowing that nothing would be right in Levin’s eyes now.", "스테판 아르카지치는 지금 레빈의 눈에는 무엇 하나 옳게 보일 리 없다는 걸 알면서도 사람 좋은 미소를 지으며 말했다."]], [["“Because the forest is worth at least a hundred and fifty roubles the acre,”", "\"그 숲은 적어도 일 데샤티나에 백오십 루블은 나가거든.\""], ["answered Levin.", "레빈이 대답했다."]], [["“Oh, these farmers!”", "\"오, 이 농사꾼들 좀 보게!\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch playfully.", "스테판 아르카지치가 장난스레 말했다."], ["“Your tone of contempt for us poor townsfolk!...", "\"우리 같은 가련한 도시 사람들을 깔보는 그 말투라니!……"], ["But when it comes to business, we do it better than anyone.", "하지만 막상 일을 처리하는 데는 우리가 누구보다도 더 잘한다네."], ["I assure you I have reckoned it all out,”", "정말이지 내가 다 계산해 봤어.\" 그가 말했다."], ["he said, “and the forest is fetching a very good price—so much so that I’m afraid of this fellow’s crying off, in fact.", "\"그 숲은 아주 좋은 값을 받는 거야. 어찌나 좋은지 사실 나는 저 친구가 도로 무를까 봐 겁이 날 지경이라고."], ["You know it’s not ‘timber,’”", "그게 '재목감'이 아니라는 걸 자네도 알잖나.\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, hoping by this distinction to convince Levin completely of the unfairness of his doubts.", "스테판 아르카지치는 이 구별을 들어 레빈에게 그 의심이 부당하다는 것을 완전히 납득시킬 수 있으리라 기대하며 말했다."], ["“And it won’t run to more than twenty-five yards of fagots per acre, and he’s giving me at the rate of seventy roubles the acre.”", "\"일 데샤티나에서 나오는 장작이라야 스물다섯 사젠을 넘지 않을 텐데, 저 친구는 나한테 일 데샤티나에 칠십 루블씩 쳐주는 거란 말이야.\""]], [["Levin smiled contemptuously.", "레빈은 경멸하는 듯 빙긋 웃었다. '알지.'"], ["“I know,”", "그는 생각했다."], ["he thought, “that fashion not only in him, but in all city people, who, after being twice in ten years in the country, pick up two or three phrases and use them in season and out of season, firmly persuaded that they know all about it.", "'저런 버릇은 이 친구뿐 아니라 모든 도시 사람들한테 있는 거야. 십 년에 두 번쯤 시골에 와 보고는 두세 마디 말을 주워듣고, 그게 맞든 안 맞든 함부로 써먹으면서 자기들이 그 일을 죄다 안다고 굳게 믿는 거지."], ["‘Timber, run to so many yards the acre.’ He says those words without understanding them himself.”", "〈재목감이라느니, 일 데샤티나에 몇 사젠이 나온다느니.〉 저 친구는 자기가 무슨 말을 하는지도 모르면서 그런 말을 늘어놓고 있어.'"]], [["“I wouldn’t attempt to teach you what you write about in your office,”", "\"나라면 자네가 관청에서 쓰는 서류 따위에 대해 가르치려 들지 않을 거야.\""], ["said he, “and if need arose, I should come to you to ask about it.", "그가 말했다. \"필요한 일이 생기면 자네한테 와서 물어보겠지."], ["But you’re so positive you know all the lore of the forest.", "그런데 자네는 숲에 관한 지식이라면 전부 다 안다고 그렇게 자신만만하니."], ["It’s difficult.", "그게 어려운 거라네."], ["Have you counted the trees?”", "자네, 나무를 세어 보기는 했나?\""]], [["“How count the trees?”", "\"나무를 어떻게 세나?\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, laughing, still trying to draw his friend out of his ill-temper.", "스테판 아르카지치는 여전히 친구를 언짢은 기분에서 끌어내려 애쓰며 웃었다."], ["“Count the sands of the sea, number the stars.", "\"바다의 모래알을 세고 별의 수를 헤아리라는 거나 마찬가지지."], ["Some higher power might do it.”", "더 높은 권능을 가진 분이라면 모를까.\""]], [["“Oh, well, the higher power of Ryabinin can.", "\"오, 그렇지, 랴비닌의 더 높은 권능이라면 셀 수 있고말고."], ["Not a single merchant ever buys a forest without counting the trees, unless they get it given them for nothing, as you’re doing now.", "나무를 세지 않고 숲을 사는 상인은 단 하나도 없어. 지금 자네처럼 거저 주는 거라면 또 모를까."], ["I know your forest.", "나는 자네 숲을 잘 알아."], ["I go there every year shooting, and your forest’s worth a hundred and fifty roubles an acre paid down, while he’s giving you sixty by installments.", "해마다 거기 가서 사냥을 하거든. 자네 숲은 현금으로 일 데샤티나에 백오십 루블은 나가는데, 저 친구는 분할로 육십 루블을 쳐주는 거야."], ["So that in fact you’re making him a present of thirty thousand.”", "그러니까 자네는 사실상 저 친구한테 삼만 루블을 선물하는 셈이지.\""]], [["“Come, don’t let your imagination run away with you,”", "\"그만, 상상이 자네를 끌고 가게 내버려 두지 말게나.\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch piteously.", "스테판 아르카지치가 가련한 목소리로 말했다."], ["“Why was it none would give it, then?”", "\"그럼 어째서 아무도 더 쳐주려 하지 않았겠나?\""]], [["“Why, because he has an understanding with the merchants; he’s bought them off.", "\"그야 저 친구가 상인들과 짜고 있으니까 그렇지. 그들을 매수해 버린 거야."], ["I’ve had to do with all of them; I know them.", "나는 그자들 모두와 거래를 해 봤거든."], ["They’re not merchants, you know: they’re speculators.", "잘 알아. 그들은 상인이 아니라, 알겠나, 투기꾼들이야."], ["He wouldn’t look at a bargain that gave him ten, fifteen per cent.", "십 퍼센트나 십오 퍼센트쯤 이익이 남는 흥정 따위는 거들떠보지도 않아."], ["profit, but holds back to buy a rouble’s worth for twenty kopecks.”", "일 루블짜리를 이십 코페이카에 사들이려고 잔뜩 버티고 있는 자들이지.\""]], [["“Well, enough of it!", "\"그래, 그만하면 됐네!"], ["You’re out of temper.”", "자네 기분이 상해 있군.\""]], [["“Not the least,”", "\"전혀 그렇지 않아.\""], ["said Levin gloomily, as they drove up to the house.", "레빈이 침울하게 말했다. 그러는 사이 그들은 집 앞에 다다랐다."]], [["At the steps there stood a trap tightly covered with iron and leather, with a sleek horse tightly harnessed with broad collar-straps.", "현관 계단 앞에는 쇠와 가죽으로 단단히 감싼 마차 한 대가 서 있었고, 거기에는 넓은 멍에 끈으로 단단히 매인 윤기 흐르는 말 한 마리가 묶여 있었다."], ["In the trap sat the chubby, tightly belted clerk who served Ryabinin as coachman.", "마차 안에는 랴비닌의 마부 노릇을 하는, 통통하고 허리띠를 꽉 졸라맨 사무원이 앉아 있었다."], ["Ryabinin himself was already in the house, and met the friends in the hall.", "랴비닌은 이미 집 안에 들어와 있었고, 현관에서 두 친구를 맞이했다."], ["Ryabinin was a tall, thinnish, middle-aged man, with mustache and a projecting clean-shaven chin, and prominent muddy-looking eyes.", "랴비닌은 키가 크고 다소 마른, 중년의 사내였다. 콧수염을 기르고 턱은 말끔히 면도해 앞으로 튀어나와 있었으며, 진흙처럼 탁해 보이는 두 눈이 불거져 있었다."], ["He was dressed in a long-skirted blue coat, with buttons below the waist at the back, and wore high boots wrinkled over the ankles and straight over the calf, with big galoshes drawn over them.", "그는 자락이 긴 푸른색 외투를 입고 있었는데, 뒤쪽 허리 아래에 단추가 달려 있었고, 발목께는 주름이 잡히고 종아리께는 곧게 펴진 높은 장화를 신고 그 위에 커다란 덧신을 끼고 있었다."], ["He rubbed his face with his handkerchief, and wrapping round him his coat, which sat extremely well as it was, he greeted them with a smile, holding out his hand to Stepan Arkadyevitch, as though he wanted to catch something.", "그는 손수건으로 얼굴을 문지른 다음, 가뜩이나 몸에 아주 잘 맞는 외투를 한층 단단히 여미고는, 마치 무언가를 붙잡으려는 듯 스테판 아르카지치에게 손을 내밀며 미소로 그들을 맞았다."]], [["“So here you are,”", "\"아, 여기 와 계셨군요.\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, giving him his hand.", "스테판 아르카지치가 그에게 손을 내밀며 말했다."], ["“That’s capital.”", "\"잘됐습니다.\""]], [["“I did not venture to disregard your excellency’s commands, though the road was extremely bad.", "\"길이 형편없이 나빴지만, 그래도 각하의 분부를 감히 어길 수가 없었습죠."], ["I positively walked the whole way, but I am here at my time.", "정말이지 내내 걸어오다시피 했습니다만, 그래도 약속한 시간에 닿았습니다."], ["Konstantin Dmitrievitch, my respects”; he turned to Levin, trying to seize his hand too.", "콘스탄틴 드미트리치, 안녕하십니까.\" 그는 레빈에게 돌아서며 그의 손까지 붙잡으려 했다."], ["But Levin, scowling, made as though he did not notice his hand, and took out the snipe.", "그러나 레빈은 얼굴을 찌푸린 채 그의 손을 못 본 척하고는 멧도요를 꺼냈다."], ["“Your honors have been diverting yourselves with the chase?", "\"나리들께서는 사냥으로 재미를 보고 오셨군요?"], ["What kind of bird may it be, pray?”", "실례입니다만, 그게 대체 무슨 새입니까요?\""], ["added Ryabinin, looking contemptuously at the snipe: “a great delicacy, I suppose.”", "랴비닌은 멧도요를 경멸하듯 바라보며 덧붙였다. \"아주 별미겠습죠.\""], ["And he shook his head disapprovingly, as though he had grave doubts whether this game were worth the candle.", "그러고는 이 사냥감이 과연 수고할 만한 값어치가 있는지 깊이 의심스럽다는 듯, 못마땅하게 고개를 가로저었다."]], [["“Would you like to go into my study?”", "\"내 서재로 들어가시겠소?\""], ["Levin said in French to Stepan Arkadyevitch, scowling morosely.", "레빈이 침울하게 인상을 쓰며 프랑스어로 스테판 아르카지치에게 말했다."], ["“Go into my study; you can talk there.”", "\"내 서재로 가게. 거기서 이야기하면 되니까.\""]], [["“Quite so, where you please,”", "\"그러시지요, 어디든 좋으실 대로.\""], ["said Ryabinin with contemptuous dignity, as though wishing to make it felt that others might be in difficulties as to how to behave, but that he could never be in any difficulty about anything.", "랴비닌은 마치 남들은 어떻게 처신해야 할지 난처해할지 몰라도, 자기만큼은 어떤 일에도 결코 난처해하는 법이 없다는 걸 일깨우려는 듯, 경멸 섞인 위엄을 풍기며 말했다."]], [["On entering the study Ryabinin looked about, as his habit was, as though seeking the holy picture, but when he had found it, he did not cross himself.", "서재에 들어서자 랴비닌은 늘 하던 버릇대로 성상을 찾기라도 하는 양 사방을 둘러보았으나, 막상 그것을 찾고 나서도 성호를 긋지는 않았다."], ["He scanned the bookcases and bookshelves, and with the same dubious air with which he had regarded the snipe, he smiled contemptuously and shook his head disapprovingly, as though by no means willing to allow that this game were worth the candle.", "그는 책장과 책꽂이들을 훑어보더니, 멧도요를 바라볼 때와 똑같은 미심쩍은 기색으로, 이 사냥감이 수고할 값어치가 있다고는 도무지 인정하지 못하겠다는 듯, 경멸하듯 빙긋 웃고는 못마땅하게 고개를 가로저었다."]], [["“Well, have you brought the money?”", "\"자, 돈은 가져오셨소?\""], ["asked Oblonsky.", "오블론스키가 물었다."], ["“Sit down.”", "\"앉으시지요.\""]], [["“Oh, don’t trouble about the money.", "\"오, 돈일랑 염려 마십시오."], ["I’ve come to see you to talk it over.”", "저는 그 일을 의논하러 뵈러 온 겁니다.\""]], [["“What is there to talk over?", "\"의논할 게 뭐가 있단 말이오?"], ["But do sit down.”", "어쨌든 앉으시오.\""]], [["“I don’t mind if I do,”", "\"앉아도 괜찮으시다면 앉지요.\""], ["said Ryabinin, sitting down and leaning his elbows on the back of his chair in a position of the intensest discomfort to himself.", "랴비닌은 자리에 앉으며 자기로서는 더없이 불편한 자세로 의자 등받이에 팔꿈치를 괴었다."], ["“You must knock it down a bit, prince.", "\"값을 조금 깎아 주셔야겠습니다, 공작님."], ["It would be too bad.", "이대로는 너무 심합니다."], ["The money is ready conclusively to the last farthing.", "돈은 마지막 한 푼까지 빈틈없이 다 준비해 두었습니다."], ["As to paying the money down, there’ll be no hitch there.”", "현금을 치르는 데는 아무 차질도 없을 겁니다.\""]], [["Levin, who had meanwhile been putting his gun away in the cupboard, was just going out of the door, but catching the merchant’s words, he stopped.", "그러는 사이 총을 찬장에 넣고 있던 레빈은 마침 문을 나서려던 참이었는데, 그 상인의 말을 듣고는 걸음을 멈추었다."]], [["“Why, you’ve got the forest for nothing as it is,”", "\"아니, 자네는 지금도 그 숲을 거저 얻는 거나 다름없잖아.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“He came to me too late, or I’d have fixed the price for him.”", "\"저 사람이 나한테 너무 늦게 왔어. 안 그랬으면 내가 값을 정해 줬을 텐데.\""]], [["Ryabinin got up, and in silence, with a smile, he looked Levin down and up.", "랴비닌은 자리에서 일어나, 말없이 미소를 띤 채 레빈을 위아래로 훑어보았다."]], [["“Very close about money is Konstantin Dmitrievitch,”", "\"콘스탄틴 드미트리치께서는 돈에 어찌나 짜신지요.\""], ["he said with a smile, turning to Stepan Arkadyevitch; “there’s positively no dealing with him.", "그는 미소를 지으며 스테판 아르카지치에게 몸을 돌려 말했다. \"도무지 거래라는 걸 할 수가 없는 분입죠."], ["I was bargaining for some wheat of him, and a pretty price I offered too.”", "제가 이분한테서 밀을 흥정한 적이 있는데, 값도 꽤 후하게 쳐 드렸건만요.\""]], [["“Why should I give you my goods for nothing?", "\"내 물건을 왜 자네한테 거저 주겠나?"], ["I didn’t pick it up on the ground, nor steal it either.”", "땅에서 주운 것도 아니고, 훔친 것도 아닌데.\""]], [["“Mercy on us!", "\"아이고, 천만의 말씀입니다요!"], ["nowadays there’s no chance at all of stealing.", "요즘 세상에 훔칠 기회 따위가 어디 있겠습니까."], ["With the open courts and everything done in style, nowadays there’s no question of stealing.", "재판도 공개로 하고 만사가 격식대로 돌아가는 요즘 세상에 훔친다는 건 어림도 없는 얘깁죠."], ["We are just talking things over like gentlemen.", "저희야 그저 신사답게 의논이나 하자는 겁니다."], ["His excellency’s asking too much for the forest.", "각하께서 숲값을 너무 많이 부르셔서요."], ["I can’t make both ends meet over it.", "도무지 수지가 맞질 않습니다."], ["I must ask for a little concession.”", "조금만 양보해 주십사 부탁드릴 수밖에요.\""]], [["“But is the thing settled between you or not?", "\"한데 두 사람 사이에 일이 매듭지어진 거요, 아니오?"], ["If it’s settled, it’s useless haggling; but if it’s not,”", "매듭지어졌다면 흥정해 봐야 소용없는 일이고, 아직 아니라면\""], ["said Levin, “I’ll buy the forest.”", "하고 레빈이 말했다. \"그 숲은 내가 사겠소.\""]], [["The smile vanished at once from Ryabinin’s face.", "랴비닌의 얼굴에서 미소가 단번에 사라졌다."], ["A hawklike, greedy, cruel expression was left upon it.", "그 자리에는 매처럼 탐욕스럽고 잔인한 표정이 남았다."], ["With rapid, bony fingers he unbuttoned his coat, revealing a shirt, bronze waistcoat buttons, and a watch chain, and quickly pulled out a fat old pocketbook.", "그는 뼈만 앙상한 손가락을 재빨리 놀려 외투의 단추를 풀었고, 셔츠와 청동빛 조끼 단추와 시곗줄을 드러내며 두툼하고 낡은 지갑을 잽싸게 꺼냈다."]], [["“Here you are, the forest is mine,”", "\"자, 여기 있습니다. 그 숲은 제 겁니다.\""], ["he said, crossing himself quickly, and holding out his hand.", "그는 재빨리 성호를 긋고 손을 내밀며 말했다."], ["“Take the money; it’s my forest.", "\"돈을 받으십시오. 이건 제 숲입니다."], ["That’s Ryabinin’s way of doing business; he doesn’t haggle over every half-penny,”", "이게 바로 랴비닌이 거래하는 방식입죠. 반 푼 가지고 입씨름하는 법이 없습니다.\""], ["he added, scowling and waving the pocketbook.", "그는 인상을 쓰며 지갑을 흔들어 댔다."]], [["“I wouldn’t be in a hurry if I were you,”", "\"나 같으면 그렇게 서두르지 않겠소.\""], ["said Levin.", "레빈이 말했다."]], [["“Come, really,”", "\"이런, 정말이지.\""], ["said Oblonsky in surprise.", "오블론스키가 놀라서 말했다."], ["“I’ve given my word, you know.”", "\"내가 이미 약속을 했지 않은가, 그래.\""]], [["Levin went out of the room, slamming the door.", "레빈은 문을 쾅 닫고 방을 나가 버렸다."], ["Ryabinin looked towards the door and shook his head with a smile.", "랴비닌은 그 문 쪽을 바라보며 미소 띤 얼굴로 고개를 가로저었다."]], [["“It’s all youthfulness—positively nothing but boyishness.", "\"다 젊은 혈기지요. 그야말로 애송이 티 말고는 아무것도 아닙니다."], ["Why, I’m buying it, upon my honor, simply, believe me, for the glory of it, that Ryabinin, and no one else, should have bought the copse of Oblonsky.", "제가 이걸 사는 건 말입죠, 정말이지 명예를 걸고 말씀드리는데, 그저, 믿어 주십시오, 오블론스키 님의 숲을 다른 누구도 아닌 이 랴비닌이 샀다는 그 영광 하나 때문입니다."], ["And as to the profits, why, I must make what God gives.", "이문으로 말하자면야, 뭐, 하느님이 주시는 대로 챙겨야지요."], ["In God’s name.", "하느님의 이름으로 말입니다."], ["If you would kindly sign the title-deed....”", "죄송합니다만 권리 증서에 서명을 좀 해 주신다면야……\""]], [["Within an hour the merchant, stroking his big overcoat neatly down, and hooking up his jacket, with the agreement in his pocket, seated himself in his tightly covered trap, and drove homewards.", "한 시간이 채 안 되어 그 상인은 큼직한 외투 자락을 매무새 좋게 쓸어내리고 윗도리 단추를 채운 다음, 계약서를 호주머니에 넣고 단단히 감싼 자기 마차에 올라앉아 집으로 떠났다."]], [["“Ugh, these gentlefolks!”", "\"어휴, 저런 양반들이라니!\""], ["he said to the clerk.", "그가 사무원에게 말했다."], ["“They—they’re a nice lot!”", "\"저, 저것들 참 별난 작자들이야!\""]], [["“That’s so,”", "\"그렇습죠.\""], ["responded the clerk, handing him the reins and buttoning the leather apron.", "사무원이 그에게 고삐를 건네주고 가죽 앞가리개의 단추를 채우며 대답했다."], ["“But I can congratulate you on the purchase, Mihail Ignatitch?”", "\"그건 그렇고, 미하일 이그나티치, 이번 거래는 축하드려도 되겠습니까요?\""]], [["“Well, well....”", "\"음, 글쎄다……\""]], [["# Chapter 17", "# Chapter 17"]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch went upstairs with his pocket bulging with notes, which the merchant had paid him for three months in advance.", "스테판 아르카지치는 상인이 석 달치를 미리 치러 준 지폐로 주머니가 불룩해진 채 위층으로 올라갔다."], ["The business of the forest was over, the money in his pocket; their shooting had been excellent, and Stepan Arkadyevitch was in the happiest frame of mind, and so he felt specially anxious to dissipate the ill-humor that had come upon Levin.", "숲에 관한 거래는 끝났고, 돈은 주머니에 들어 있었으며, 사냥도 더할 나위 없이 좋았다. 그래서 스테판 아르카지치는 더없이 행복한 기분이었고, 그런 만큼 레빈에게 덮친 언짢은 기분을 풀어 주고 싶은 마음이 각별했다."], ["He wanted to finish the day at supper as pleasantly as it had been begun.", "그는 하루를 시작할 때처럼 즐겁게 만찬으로 마무리하고 싶었다."]], [["Levin certainly was out of humor, and in spite of all his desire to be affectionate and cordial to his charming visitor, he could not control his mood.", "레빈은 분명 기분이 좋지 않았고, 매력적인 손님에게 다정하고 살갑게 대하고 싶은 온갖 마음에도 불구하고 자기 기분을 다스리지 못했다."], ["The intoxication of the news that Kitty was not married had gradually begun to work upon him.", "키티가 결혼하지 않았다는 소식이 안겨 준 도취감이 차츰 그를 사로잡기 시작했던 것이다."]], [["Kitty was not married, but ill, and ill from love for a man who had slighted her.", "키티는 결혼하지 않았지만, 병이 들었고, 그것도 그녀를 무시한 남자에 대한 사랑으로 병이 든 것이었다."], ["This slight, as it were, rebounded upon him.", "이 무시는 말하자면 그에게로 되튀어 왔다."], ["Vronsky had slighted her, and she had slighted him, Levin.", "브론스키가 그녀를 무시했고, 그녀는 그를, 레빈을 무시했다."], ["Consequently Vronsky had the right to despise Levin, and therefore he was his enemy.", "따라서 브론스키는 레빈을 경멸할 권리가 있었고, 그러므로 그의 적이었다."], ["But all this Levin did not think out.", "그러나 레빈이 이 모든 것을 곰곰이 따져 본 것은 아니었다."], ["He vaguely felt that there was something in it insulting to him, and he was not angry now at what had disturbed him, but he fell foul of everything that presented itself.", "그는 거기에 자기를 모욕하는 무언가가 있다는 것을 어렴풋이 느꼈고, 자기를 어지럽힌 것에 지금 화가 난 것이 아니라, 눈앞에 나타나는 모든 것에 시비를 걸었다."], ["The stupid sale of the forest, the fraud practiced upon Oblonsky and concluded in his house, exasperated him.", "어리석은 숲 매매며, 오블론스키에게 자행되어 자기 집에서 마무리된 그 사기가 그를 분통 터지게 했다."]], [["“Well, finished?”", "\"자, 끝났나?\""], ["he said, meeting Stepan Arkadyevitch upstairs.", "그는 위층에서 스테판 아르카지치를 맞으며 말했다."], ["“Would you like supper?”", "\"야식이라도 들겠나?\""]], [["“Well, I wouldn’t say no to it.", "\"음, 마다하진 않겠네."], ["What an appetite I get in the country!", "시골에 오니 어찌나 식욕이 도는지!"], ["Wonderful!", "놀라워!"], ["Why didn’t you offer Ryabinin something?”", "그런데 자넨 왜 랴비닌에게 뭐라도 권하지 않았나?\""]], [["“Oh, damn him!”", "\"아, 그자는 빌어먹을!\""]], [["“Still, how you do treat him!” said Oblonsky.", "\"그래도 그렇지, 자네 그 사람을 그렇게 대하다니!\""], ["“You didn’t even shake hands with him.", "오블론스키가 말했다. \"악수조차 하지 않더군."], ["Why not shake hands with him?”", "악수 좀 하면 어떤가?\""]], [["“Because I don’t shake hands with a waiter, and a waiter’s a hundred times better than he is.”", "\"나는 웨이터하고는 악수하지 않으니까. 그런데 웨이터가 그자보다 백배는 낫지.\""]], [["“What a reactionist you are, really!", "\"자넨 정말이지 영락없는 반동주의자로군!"], ["What about the amalgamation of classes?”", "그럼 계급의 융합은 어쩌고?\""], ["said Oblonsky.", "오블론스키가 말했다."]], [["“Anyone who likes amalgamating is welcome to it, but it sickens me.”", "\"융합하고 싶은 자는 누구든 마음대로 하라지. 하지만 나는 역겨워.\""]], [["“You’re a regular reactionist, I see.”", "\"보아하니 자넨 천생 반동주의자야.\""]], [["“Really, I have never considered what I am.", "\"정말이지, 내가 뭔지 한 번도 생각해 본 적이 없네."], ["I am Konstantin Levin, and nothing else.”", "나는 콘스탄틴 레빈일 뿐, 그 이상도 이하도 아니야.\""]], [["“And Konstantin Levin very much out of temper,”", "\"게다가 잔뜩 심사가 뒤틀린 콘스탄틴 레빈이지.\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, smiling.", "스테판 아르카지치가 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["“Yes, I am out of temper, and do you know why?", "\"그래, 나는 심사가 뒤틀렸네. 그 이유를 아나?"], ["Because—excuse me—of your stupid sale....”", "미안하지만, 자네의 그 어리석은 매매 때문이야…….\""]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch frowned good-humoredly, like one who feels himself teased and attacked for no fault of his own.", "스테판 아르카지치는 아무 잘못도 없이 놀림받고 공격당한다고 느끼는 사람처럼, 그러나 너그러운 표정으로 눈살을 찌푸렸다."]], [["“Come, enough about it!”", "\"이런, 그만하면 됐네!\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“When did anybody ever sell anything without being told immediately after the sale, ‘It was worth much more’?", "\"누가 무엇을 팔든 거래가 끝난 직후에 '그건 훨씬 더 값이 나갔는데'라는 말을 듣지 않는 경우가 어디 있나?"], ["But when one wants to sell, no one will give anything....", "하지만 막상 팔려고 하면, 아무도 한 푼 주려 들지 않는단 말일세……."], ["No, I see you’ve a grudge against that unlucky Ryabinin.”", "아니, 보아하니 자넨 그 불운한 랴비닌에게 원한이 있나 보군.\""]], [["“Maybe I have. And do you know why?", "\"어쩌면 그럴지도 모르지."], ["You’ll say again that I’m a reactionist, or some other terrible word; but all the same it does annoy and anger me to see on all sides the impoverishing of the nobility to which I belong, and, in spite of the amalgamation of classes, I’m glad to belong.", "그 이유를 아나? 자넨 또 내가 반동주의자라느니, 뭔가 끔찍한 말을 늘어놓겠지."], ["And their impoverishment is not due to extravagance—that would be nothing; living in good style—that’s the proper thing for noblemen; it’s only the nobles who know how to do it.", "하지만 어쨌든 사방에서 내가 속한 귀족 계급이 빈곤해져 가는 꼴을 보는 게 나를 화나게 하고 짜증 나게 해. 게다가 나는 계급의 융합에도 불구하고 이 계급에 속한 게 기쁘단 말이야."], ["Now the peasants about us buy land, and I don’t mind that.", "그리고 그들의 빈곤은 사치 탓이 아니야. 그거라면 아무것도 아니지."], ["The gentleman does nothing, while the peasant works and supplants the idle man.", "격식 있게 사는 거야 귀족에게 합당한 일이고, 그렇게 할 줄 아는 건 오직 귀족뿐이니까."], ["That’s as it ought to be. And I’m very glad for the peasant.", "요즘 우리 주변의 농민들이 땅을 사고 있는데, 나는 그건 개의치 않아."], ["But I do mind seeing the process of impoverishment from a sort of—I don’t know what to call it—innocence.", "신사는 아무 일도 하지 않고, 농민은 일을 해서 게으른 자를 밀어내지. 그건 마땅히 그래야 하는 거야."], ["Here a Polish speculator bought for half its value a magnificent estate from a young lady who lives in Nice.", "나는 농민을 위해 정말 기쁘게 생각하네. 하지만 일종의 — 뭐라 불러야 할지 모르겠지만 — 순진함에서 비롯되는 빈곤의 과정을 보는 건 정말 견디기 힘들어."], ["And there a merchant will get three acres of land, worth ten roubles, as security for the loan of one rouble.", "여기 어떤 폴란드인 투기꾼이 니스에 사는 젊은 아가씨에게서 굉장한 영지를 그 값의 절반에 사들였지."], ["Here, for no kind of reason, you’ve made that rascal a present of thirty thousand roubles.”", "또 저기서는 어떤 상인이 십 루블짜리 땅 삼 에이커를 일 루블 빌려주는 담보로 차지할 거고. 그리고 자넨, 아무 까닭도 없이 그 악당에게 삼만 루블을 선물한 셈이야.\""]], [["“Well, what should I have done?", "\"그럼, 내가 어떻게 했어야 하나?"], ["Counted every tree?”", "나무를 일일이 세기라도 했어야 한단 말인가?\""]], [["“Of course, they must be counted.", "\"물론, 세어 봤어야지."], ["You didn’t count them, but Ryabinin did.", "자넨 세지 않았지만, 랴비닌은 셌어."], ["Ryabinin’s children will have means of livelihood and education, while yours maybe will not!”", "랴비닌의 자식들은 살아갈 밑천과 교육받을 수단을 갖게 될 테고, 자네 자식들은 어쩌면 못 가질지도 모른단 말일세!\""]], [["“Well, you must excuse me, but there’s something mean in this counting.", "\"이거 미안하지만, 그렇게 일일이 세는 데에는 뭔가 치사한 구석이 있어."], ["We have our business and they have theirs, and they must make their profit.", "우리에겐 우리의 일이 있고 그들에겐 그들의 일이 있으니, 그들도 이윤을 남겨야지."], ["Anyway, the thing’s done, and there’s an end of it.", "어쨌든 일은 다 끝났으니, 이걸로 끝이야."], ["And here come some poached eggs, my favorite dish.", "아, 그리고 여기 수란이 나오는군. 내가 제일 좋아하는 요리지."], ["And Agafea Mihalovna will give us that marvelous herb-brandy....”", "아가피야 미하일로브나가 그 기막힌 약초 브랜디도 내올 거고…….\""]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch sat down at the table and began joking with Agafea Mihalovna, assuring her that it was long since he had tasted such a dinner and such a supper.", "스테판 아르카지치는 식탁에 앉아 아가피야 미하일로브나에게 농을 걸며, 이렇게 훌륭한 점심과 야식을 맛본 게 참 오랜만이라고 추어올렸다."]], [["“Well, you do praise it, anyway,”", "\"하긴, 어쨌든 칭찬을 하시니 고맙긴 합니다만,\""], ["said Agafea Mihalovna, “but Konstantin Dmitrievitch, give him what you will—a crust of bread—he’ll eat it and walk away.”", "아가피야 미하일로브나가 말했다. \"콘스탄틴 드미트리치 도련님은, 무얼 드려도 — 빵 한 조각이라도 — 그냥 드시고 일어나 버리신답니다.\""]], [["Though Levin tried to control himself, he was gloomy and silent.", "레빈은 자제하려 애썼지만, 침울하고 말이 없었다."], ["He wanted to put one question to Stepan Arkadyevitch, but he could not bring himself to the point, and could not find the words or the moment in which to put it.", "그는 스테판 아르카지치에게 한 가지 묻고 싶은 것이 있었으나, 좀처럼 그 대목에 다다르지 못했고, 그것을 물을 말도 순간도 찾지 못했다."], ["Stepan Arkadyevitch had gone down to his room, undressed, again washed, and attired in a nightshirt with goffered frills, he had got into bed, but Levin still lingered in his room, talking of various trifling matters, and not daring to ask what he wanted to know.", "스테판 아르카지치는 아래층 자기 방으로 내려가 옷을 벗고 다시 몸을 씻은 다음, 주름 장식이 달린 잠옷을 걸치고 잠자리에 들었는데, 그래도 레빈은 여전히 그의 방에서 어정거리며 이런저런 사소한 이야기를 늘어놓을 뿐, 정작 알고 싶은 것은 감히 묻지 못했다."]], [["“How wonderfully they make this soap,”", "\"이 비누는 정말 기막히게 잘 만들었군.\""], ["he said gazing at a piece of soap he was handling, which Agafea Mihalovna had put ready for the visitor but Oblonsky had not used.", "그는 손에 만지작거리던 비누 한 조각을 들여다보며 말했다. 아가피야 미하일로브나가 손님을 위해 준비해 두었으나 오블론스키가 쓰지 않은 것이었다."], ["“Only look; why, it’s a work of art.”", "\"보게나. 이건 그야말로 예술품이야.\""]], [["“Yes, everything’s brought to such a pitch of perfection nowadays,”", "\"그래, 요즘은 뭐든 그렇게 완벽의 경지에 이르렀지.\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, with a moist and blissful yawn.", "스테판 아르카지치가 촉촉하고 더없이 흐뭇한 하품과 함께 말했다."], ["“The theater, for instance, and the entertainments ...", "\"이를테면 극장이며, 오락거리며……아—아—아!\""], ["a—a—a!” he yawned.", "그는 하품을 했다."], ["“The electric light everywhere ... a—a—a!”", "\"어디든 전등불이고……아—아—아!\""]], [["“Yes, the electric light,”", "\"그래, 전등불이지.\""], ["said Levin.", "레빈이 말했다."], ["“Yes.", "\"그래."], ["Oh, and where’s Vronsky now?”", "아, 그런데 브론스키는 지금 어디 있나?\""], ["he asked suddenly, laying down the soap.", "그는 비누를 내려놓으며 불쑥 물었다."]], [["“Vronsky?”", "\"브론스키?\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, checking his yawn; “he’s in Petersburg.", "스테판 아르카지치가 하품을 멈추며 말했다. \"페테르부르크에 있네."], ["He left soon after you did, and he’s not once been in Moscow since.", "자네가 떠나고 얼마 안 있어 떠났고, 그 뒤로 한 번도 모스크바에 온 적이 없지."], ["And do you know, Kostya, I’ll tell you the truth,”", "그런데 말이야, 코스챠, 솔직히 말하겠네.\""], ["he went on, leaning his elbow on the table, and propping on his hand his handsome ruddy face, in which his moist, good-natured, sleepy eyes shone like stars.", "그는 식탁에 팔꿈치를 괸 채, 촉촉하고 선량하며 졸음이 어린 두 눈이 별처럼 빛나는 잘생긴 불그스름한 얼굴을 손으로 받치며 말을 이었다."], ["“It’s your own fault.", "\"그건 자네 잘못이야."], ["You took fright at the sight of your rival.", "자넨 연적을 보고 겁을 먹어 버렸어."], ["But, as I told you at the time, I couldn’t say which had the better chance.", "하지만 그때도 말했듯이, 나는 어느 쪽이 더 가망이 있는지 알 수 없었네."], ["Why didn’t you fight it out?", "왜 끝까지 싸워 보지 않았나?"], ["I told you at the time that....”", "그때도 자네한테 말하지 않았나…….\""], ["He yawned inwardly, without opening his mouth.", "그는 입을 벌리지 않은 채 속으로 하품을 했다."]], [["“Does he know, or doesn’t he, that I did make an offer?”", "'이 사람은 내가 청혼했던 걸 아는 걸까, 모르는 걸까?'"], ["Levin wondered, gazing at him.", "레빈은 그를 응시하며 의아해했다."], ["“Yes, there’s something humbugging, diplomatic in his face,”", "'그래, 저 얼굴에는 뭔가 능청스럽고 외교적인 데가 있어.'"], ["and feeling he was blushing, he looked Stepan Arkadyevitch straight in the face without speaking.", "그러고는 자기가 얼굴을 붉히고 있음을 느끼며, 아무 말 없이 스테판 아르카지치의 얼굴을 똑바로 바라보았다."]], [["“If there was anything on her side at the time, it was nothing but a superficial attraction,”", "\"그때 그녀 쪽에 무언가 있었다 해도, 그건 한낱 겉으로 끌리는 마음에 지나지 않았어.\""], ["pursued Oblonsky.", "오블론스키가 말을 이었다."], ["“His being such a perfect aristocrat, don’t you know, and his future position in society, had an influence not with her, but with her mother.”", "\"그가 그렇게 완벽한 귀족이라는 점, 알다시피, 그리고 그의 장래 사교계 지위가 영향을 미친 건 그녀가 아니라 그녀 어머니에게였지.\""]], [["Levin scowled.", "레빈은 인상을 찌푸렸다."], ["The humiliation of his rejection stung him to the heart, as though it were a fresh wound he had only just received.", "거절당한 굴욕이, 마치 방금 막 입은 생생한 상처라도 되는 듯, 그의 가슴을 찔렀다."], ["But he was at home, and the walls of home are a support.", "그러나 그는 자기 집에 있었고, 자기 집의 벽은 하나의 버팀목이었다."]], [["“Stay, stay,”", "\"잠깐, 잠깐.\""], ["he began, interrupting Oblonsky.", "그는 오블론스키의 말을 끊으며 입을 열었다."], ["“You talk of his being an aristocrat.", "\"자넨 그가 귀족이라고 말하는군."], ["But allow me to ask what it consists in, that aristocracy of Vronsky or of anybody else, beside which I can be looked down upon?", "그런데 묻겠는데, 도대체 그게 무엇으로 이루어져 있나? 그 브론스키든 누구든, 그 앞에서 내가 멸시당할 만한 그 귀족성이라는 게 말이야?"], ["You consider Vronsky an aristocrat, but I don’t.", "자넨 브론스키를 귀족이라 여기지만, 나는 그렇게 보지 않아."], ["A man whose father crawled up from nothing at all by intrigue, and whose mother—God knows whom she wasn’t mixed up with....", "아비라는 자는 술수를 부려 맨바닥에서 기어 올라왔고, 어미라는 자는 — 하느님만이 아실 일이지, 누구와 얽히지 않았겠나……."], ["No, excuse me, but I consider myself aristocratic, and people like me, who can point back in the past to three or four honorable generations of their family, of the highest degree of breeding (talent and intellect, of course that’s another matter), and have never curried favor with anyone, never depended on anyone for anything, like my father and my grandfather.", "아니, 미안하지만, 나는 나 자신을, 그리고 나 같은 사람들을 귀족이라 여기네. 과거로 거슬러 올라가 서너 대에 걸쳐 명예로운 가문, 최고의 교양을 지닌 가문을 가리킬 수 있고(재능과 지성은, 물론 그건 또 다른 문제지만), 또 우리 아버지나 할아버지처럼 누구에게도 아첨한 적 없고, 무엇 하나 누구에게 기댄 적 없는 사람들 말이야."], ["And I know many such.", "나는 그런 사람을 많이 알고 있네."], ["You think it mean of me to count the trees in my forest, while you make Ryabinin a present of thirty thousand; but you get rents from your lands and I don’t know what, while I don’t and so I prize what’s come to me from my ancestors or been won by hard work....", "자넨 내가 내 숲의 나무를 세는 걸 치사하다고 여기면서, 정작 자네는 랴비닌에게 삼만 루블을 선물하지. 하지만 자넨 영지에서 소작료며 뭔지 모를 수입을 거둬들이는데, 나는 그게 없어."], ["We are aristocrats, and not those who can only exist by favor of the powerful of this world, and who can be bought for twopence halfpenny.”", "그래서 나는 조상에게서 물려받았거나 힘든 노동으로 얻은 것을 소중히 여기는 거야……. 우리야말로 귀족이지, 이 세상 권력자들의 비위를 맞춰야만 존재할 수 있고 동전 두 닢 반에도 매수당하는 그런 자들이 아니란 말이야.\""]], [["“Well, but whom are you attacking?", "\"그래, 한데 자넨 누굴 공격하는 건가?"], ["I agree with you,”", "나는 자네 말에 동의하네.\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, sincerely and genially; though he was aware that in the class of those who could be bought for twopence halfpenny Levin was reckoning him too.", "스테판 아르카지치가 진심으로, 그리고 다정하게 말했다. 다만 그는 동전 두 닢 반에 매수당할 수 있는 부류 안에 레빈이 자기까지 셈하고 있다는 것을 알고 있었다."], ["Levin’s warmth gave him genuine pleasure.", "레빈의 열기가 그에게 진정한 즐거움을 주었던 것이다."], ["“Whom are you attacking?", "\"자넨 누굴 공격하는 거야?"], ["Though a good deal is not true that you say about Vronsky, but I won’t talk about that.", "자네가 브론스키에 대해 한 말 중에 사실이 아닌 게 적지 않지만, 그 얘긴 하지 않겠네."], ["I tell you straight out, if I were you, I should go back with me to Moscow, and....”", "단도직입적으로 말하지. 내가 자네라면, 나하고 같이 모스크바로 돌아가서…….\""]], [["“No; I don’t know whether you know it or not, but I don’t care.", "\"아니. 자네가 그걸 아는지 모르는지 모르겠지만, 나는 상관없어."], ["And I tell you—I did make an offer and was rejected, and Katerina Alexandrovna is nothing now to me but a painful and humiliating reminiscence.”", "그리고 말해 두겠는데 — 나는 청혼을 했고 거절당했어. 카체리나 알렉산드로브나는 이제 나에게 고통스럽고 굴욕적인 추억일 뿐이야.\""]], [["“What ever for?", "\"대체 왜 그런가?"], ["What nonsense!”", "무슨 그런 말도 안 되는 소릴!\""]], [["“But we won’t talk about it.", "\"하지만 그 얘긴 그만하세."], ["Please forgive me, if I’ve been nasty,”", "내가 못되게 굴었다면, 부디 용서해 주게.\""], ["said Levin.", "레빈이 말했다."], ["Now that he had opened his heart, he became as he had been in the morning.", "이제 마음을 털어놓고 나니, 그는 아침에 그랬던 모습으로 돌아갔다."], ["“You’re not angry with me, Stiva?", "\"나한테 화난 건 아니지, 스티바?"], ["Please don’t be angry,”", "제발 화내지 말게.\""], ["he said, and smiling, he took his hand.", "그는 이렇게 말하며 미소 짓고는 그의 손을 잡았다."]], [["“Of course not; not a bit, and no reason to be.", "\"물론 아니지. 조금도, 그럴 까닭도 없네."], ["I’m glad we’ve spoken openly.", "우리가 터놓고 이야기해서 기쁘군."], ["And do you know, stand-shooting in the morning is unusually good—why not go?", "그런데 말이야, 아침에 하는 매복 사냥이 유난히 좋아 — 가지 않겠나?"], ["I couldn’t sleep the night anyway, but I might go straight from shooting to the station.”", "어차피 밤새 잠도 못 잘 텐데, 사냥하고 나서 곧장 역으로 가면 되니까.\""]], [["“Capital.”", "\"그거 좋군.\""]], [["# Chapter 18", "# Chapter 18"]], [["Although all Vronsky’s inner life was absorbed in his passion, his external life unalterably and inevitably followed along the old accustomed lines of his social and regimental ties and interests.", "브론스키의 내면생활은 온통 사랑에 빠져 있었지만, 외적 생활은 변함없이 그리고 어쩔 수 없이 사교계와 연대(聯隊)의 인연이며 관심사라는, 익숙한 옛 노선을 따라 흘러갔다."], ["The interests of his regiment took an important place in Vronsky’s life, both because he was fond of the regiment, and because the regiment was fond of him.", "연대의 일은 브론스키의 삶에서 중요한 자리를 차지했는데, 그가 연대를 좋아하기 때문이기도 했고, 또 연대가 그를 좋아하기 때문이기도 했다."], ["They were not only fond of Vronsky in his regiment, they respected him too, and were proud of him; proud that this man, with his immense wealth, his brilliant education and abilities, and the path open before him to every kind of success, distinction, and ambition, had disregarded all that, and of all the interests of life had the interests of his regiment and his comrades nearest to his heart.", "연대 사람들은 그저 그를 좋아하는 데 그치지 않고 그를 존경하기도 했으며, 그를 자랑스러워했다. 막대한 재산과 빛나는 교양과 재능을 가졌고, 온갖 종류의 성공과 명예와 출세의 길이 눈앞에 열려 있는 이 사내가, 그 모든 것을 마다하고, 삶의 온갖 관심사 가운데 연대와 전우들의 일을 가장 가슴 가까이에 두고 있다는 사실이 자랑스러웠던 것이다."], ["Vronsky was aware of his comrades’ view of him, and in addition to his liking for the life, he felt bound to keep up that reputation.", "브론스키는 전우들이 자기를 어떻게 보는지 잘 알았고, 그 생활을 좋아하는 마음에 더해, 그 평판을 지켜야 한다는 의무감마저 느꼈다."]], [["It need not be said that he did not speak of his love to any of his comrades, nor did he betray his secret even in the wildest drinking bouts (though indeed he was never so drunk as to lose all control of himself).", "그가 전우들 누구에게도 자신의 사랑을 입에 올리지 않았다는 것, 술자리가 아무리 흥청거려도(하긴 그는 자제력을 완전히 잃을 만큼 취한 적이 한 번도 없었다) 비밀을 누설하지 않았다는 것은 말할 필요도 없다."], ["And he shut up any of his thoughtless comrades who attempted to allude to his connection.", "그리고 그는 무심코 자기 관계를 들먹이려는 전우가 있으면 누구든 입을 막아 버렸다."], ["But in spite of that, his love was known to all the town; everyone guessed with more or less confidence at his relations with Madame Karenina.", "하지만 그럼에도 그의 사랑은 도시 전체에 알려져 있었다. 누구나 어느 정도 확신을 가지고 그와 카레니나 부인의 관계를 짐작했다."], ["The majority of the younger men envied him for just what was the most irksome factor in his love—the exalted position of Karenin, and the consequent publicity of their connection in society.", "젊은 축의 대다수는, 정작 그의 사랑에서 가장 성가신 요소였던 바로 그것, 즉 카레닌의 드높은 지위와, 그로 인해 두 사람의 관계가 사교계에 공공연히 드러나게 된 사정을 두고 그를 부러워했다."]], [["The greater number of the young women, who envied Anna and had long been weary of hearing her called virtuous, rejoiced at the fulfillment of their predictions, and were only waiting for a decisive turn in public opinion to fall upon her with all the weight of their scorn.", "안나를 부러워하면서도 그녀가 정숙하다는 말을 듣는 데 오래전부터 진력이 나 있던 젊은 여자들의 다수는, 자기들의 예언이 실현된 것을 기뻐했으며, 여론이 결정적으로 돌아서기만 하면 멸시의 무게를 온통 그녀에게 쏟아부으려고 기다리고 있을 뿐이었다."], ["They were already making ready their handfuls of mud to fling at her when the right moment arrived.", "그들은 때가 무르익으면 그녀에게 던질 진흙을 이미 한 움큼씩 손에 그러쥐고 준비해 두고 있었다."], ["The greater number of the middle-aged people and certain great personages were displeased at the prospect of the impending scandal in society.", "중년층의 대다수와 일부 고관들은 사교계에 닥쳐올 추문의 조짐을 못마땅하게 여겼다. * liaison: 프랑스어로 '정사(情事)·밀통'."]], [["Vronsky’s mother, on hearing of his connection, was at first pleased at it, because nothing to her mind gave such a finishing touch to a brilliant young man as a liaison in the highest society; she was pleased, too, that Madame Karenina, who had so taken her fancy, and had talked so much of her son, was, after all, just like all other pretty and well-bred women,—at least according to the Countess Vronskaya’s ideas.", "브론스키의 어머니는 아들의 관계에 대해 처음 들었을 때는 도리어 흡족해했다. 그녀의 생각으로는 상류 사교계에서의 liaison만큼 빛나는 청년에게 마무리의 광택을 더해 주는 것이 없었기 때문이다."], ["But she had heard of late that her son had refused a position offered him of great importance to his career, simply in order to remain in the regiment, where he could be constantly seeing Madame Karenina.", "또한 그토록 자기 마음에 들었고 자기 아들 이야기를 그토록 많이 했던 카레니나 부인이, 결국 다른 예쁘고 교양 있는 여자들과 다를 바 없었다는 사실도—적어도 브론스카야 백작 부인의 관념으로는 그러했다—흐뭇했다."], ["She learned that great personages were displeased with him on this account, and she changed her opinion.", "하지만 그녀는 최근에, 아들이 출세에 더없이 중요한 자리를 제의받고도, 오로지 카레니나 부인을 늘 만날 수 있는 연대에 남기 위해 그것을 거절했다는 말을 들었다."], ["She was vexed, too, that from all she could learn of this connection it was not that brilliant, graceful, worldly liaison which she would have welcomed, but a sort of Wertherish, desperate passion, so she was told, which might well lead him into imprudence.", "그 일로 고관들이 아들을 못마땅해한다는 것을 알게 되자, 그녀는 생각을 바꾸었다. 게다가 이 관계에 대해 알아낼 수 있는 모든 것으로 미루어, 그것이 자기가 기꺼이 반겼을 그 빛나고 우아하고 세속적인 liaison이 아니라, 듣자 하니 일종의 베르테르식의 절망적인 정열이어서, 아들을 경솔한 짓으로 몰아갈 수도 있다는 점도 그녀를 언짢게 했다."], ["She had not seen him since his abrupt departure from Moscow, and she sent her elder son to bid him come to see her.", "그녀는 아들이 모스크바를 급작스레 떠난 뒤로 한 번도 그를 보지 못했고, 그래서 맏아들을 보내 자기를 보러 오라고 일렀다."]], [["This elder son, too, was displeased with his younger brother.", "이 맏아들 또한 동생을 못마땅하게 여겼다."], ["He did not distinguish what sort of love his might be, big or little, passionate or passionless, lasting or passing (he kept a ballet girl himself, though he was the father of a family, so he was lenient in these matters), but he knew that this love affair was viewed with displeasure by those whom it was necessary to please, and therefore he did not approve of his brother’s conduct.", "그는 동생의 사랑이 어떤 종류인지, 큰 것인지 작은 것인지, 정열적인 것인지 무덤덤한 것인지, 오래갈 것인지 스쳐 지날 것인지 따위는 구분하지 않았다(그 자신도 한 집안의 가장이면서 발레리나 하나를 데리고 살았으니, 이런 문제에는 너그러웠다). 다만 이 연애가 비위를 맞추어야 할 사람들에게 못마땅하게 비치고 있다는 것을 알았기에, 동생의 처신에 찬성하지 않았다."]], [["Besides the service and society, Vronsky had another great interest—horses; he was passionately fond of horses.", "직무와 사교계 말고도 브론스키에게는 또 하나의 큰 관심사가 있었다—말이었다. 그는 말을 미치도록 좋아했다."]], [["That year races and a steeplechase had been arranged for the officers.", "그해에는 장교들을 위한 경마와 장애물 경주가 마련되어 있었다."], ["Vronsky had put his name down, bought a thoroughbred English mare, and in spite of his love affair, he was looking forward to the races with intense, though reserved, excitement....", "브론스키는 출전 신청을 하고, 순종 영국산 암말을 한 마리 사들였으며, 연애 사건이 있는 와중에도 겉으로는 자제하면서도 속으로는 격렬한 흥분 속에서 경마를 손꼽아 기다리고 있었다.……"]], [["These two passions did not interfere with one another.", "이 두 가지 열정은 서로 방해가 되지 않았다."], ["On the contrary, he needed occupation and distraction quite apart from his love, so as to recruit and rest himself from the violent emotions that agitated him.", "오히려 그는 자기를 휘젓는 격렬한 감정에서 기운을 되찾고 휴식을 얻기 위해서라도, 사랑과는 전혀 별개의 몰두할 일과 기분 전환거리가 필요했다."]], [["# Chapter 19", "# Chapter 19"]], [["On the day of the races at Krasnoe Selo, Vronsky had come earlier than usual to eat beefsteak in the common messroom of the regiment.", "크라스노예 셀로에서 경마가 열리는 날, 브론스키는 평소보다 일찍 연대의 공동 식당으로 와서 비프스테이크를 먹었다."], ["He had no need to be strict with himself, as he had very quickly been brought down to the required light weight; but still he had to avoid gaining flesh, and so he eschewed farinaceous and sweet dishes.", "그는 자신에게 그리 엄격할 필요가 없었으니, 요구되는 가벼운 체중까지 이미 아주 빠르게 떨어뜨려 둔 터였다. 그래도 살이 붙는 것은 피해야 했으므로 전분과 단것이 든 요리는 멀리했다."], ["He sat with his coat unbuttoned over a white waistcoat, resting both elbows on the table, and while waiting for the steak he had ordered he looked at a French novel that lay open on his plate.", "그는 흰 조끼 위에 입은 군복 상의의 단추를 풀어 헤친 채로 두 팔꿈치를 식탁에 괴고 앉아, 주문해 둔 스테이크를 기다리는 동안 접시 위에 펼쳐 놓은 프랑스 소설을 들여다보았다."], ["He was only looking at the book to avoid conversation with the officers coming in and out; he was thinking.", "책을 들여다본 것은 그저 들락거리는 장교들과 말을 섞지 않으려는 것이었을 뿐, 그는 생각에 잠겨 있었다."]], [["He was thinking of Anna’s promise to see him that day after the races.", "그는 경마가 끝난 뒤 오늘 만나 주겠다던 안나의 약속을 생각하고 있었다."], ["But he had not seen her for three days, and as her husband had just returned from abroad, he did not know whether she would be able to meet him today or not, and he did not know how to find out.", "그러나 사흘째 그녀를 보지 못했고, 그녀의 남편이 막 외국에서 돌아온 터라 오늘 그녀가 자기를 만날 수 있을지 어떨지 알 수 없었으며, 그것을 알아낼 방도도 알지 못했다."], ["He had had his last interview with her at his cousin Betsy’s summer villa.", "그녀와의 마지막 만남은 사촌 누이 벳시의 여름 별장에서였다."], ["He visited the Karenins’ summer villa as rarely as possible.", "그는 카레닌 부부의 여름 별장에는 되도록 드물게 드나들었다."], ["Now he wanted to go there, and he pondered the question how to do it.", "지금 그는 그곳에 가고 싶었고, 어떻게 하면 좋을지 그 방법을 곰곰이 따져 보았다."]], [["“Of course I shall say Betsy has sent me to ask whether she’s coming to the races.", "\"물론 벳시가 그녀에게 경마에 오는지 물어보라고 나를 보냈다고 하면 되지."], ["Of course, I’ll go,”", "물론, 가야지.\""], ["he decided, lifting his head from the book.", "그는 책에서 고개를 들며 결심했다."], ["And as he vividly pictured the happiness of seeing her, his face lighted up.", "그리고 그녀를 만나는 행복을 생생히 그려 보자, 그의 얼굴이 환해졌다."]], [["“Send to my house, and tell them to have out the carriage and three horses as quick as they can,”", "\"우리 집으로 사람을 보내서, 마차와 말 세 필을 되도록 빨리 준비해 두라고 일러.\""], ["he said to the servant, who handed him the steak on a hot silver dish, and moving the dish up he began eating.", "그는 뜨거운 은쟁반에 스테이크를 받쳐 건네주는 하인에게 말하고는, 쟁반을 끌어당겨 먹기 시작했다."]], [["From the billiard room next door came the sound of balls knocking, of talk and laughter.", "옆방 당구장에서 공이 부딪치는 소리와 이야기하고 웃는 소리가 들려왔다."], ["Two officers appeared at the entrance-door: one, a young fellow, with a feeble, delicate face, who had lately joined the regiment from the Corps of Pages; the other, a plump, elderly officer, with a bracelet on his wrist, and little eyes, lost in fat.", "입구 문가에 두 장교가 나타났다. 하나는 시동 군단에서 최근 연대로 들어온, 가냘프고 섬세한 얼굴을 한 젊은 사내였고, 다른 하나는 손목에 팔찌를 차고 살에 파묻혀 작아 보이는 눈을 한, 통통하고 나이 든 장교였다."]], [["Vronsky glanced at them, frowned, and looking down at his book as though he had not noticed them, he proceeded to eat and read at the same time.", "브론스키는 그들을 흘낏 보고는 눈살을 찌푸렸고, 마치 그들을 보지 못한 양 책으로 시선을 떨군 채 먹으면서 동시에 읽기를 계속했다."]], [["“What?", "\"왜 그래?"], ["Fortifying yourself for your work?”", "자네 일을 위해 기력을 보충하는 중인가?\""], ["said the plump officer, sitting down beside him.", "통통한 장교가 그의 곁에 앉으며 말했다."]], [["“As you see,”", "\"보다시피.\""], ["responded Vronsky, knitting his brows, wiping his mouth, and not looking at the officer.", "브론스키는 미간을 찌푸리고 입을 닦으며, 그 장교 쪽은 보지도 않은 채 대답했다."]], [["“So you’re not afraid of getting fat?”", "\"그럼 살찌는 게 무섭지 않다는 건가?\""], ["said the latter, turning a chair round for the young officer.", "통통한 장교가 젊은 장교를 위해 의자를 돌려 놓으며 말했다."]], [["“What?”", "\"뭐라고?\""], ["said Vronsky angrily, making a wry face of disgust, and showing his even teeth.", "브론스키는 화가 나서, 역겹다는 듯 얼굴을 찡그리며 가지런한 이를 드러낸 채 말했다."]], [["“You’re not afraid of getting fat?”", "\"살찌는 게 무섭지 않냐고?\""]], [["“Waiter, sherry!”", "\"웨이터, 셰리주!\""], ["said Vronsky, without replying, and moving the book to the other side of him, he went on reading.", "브론스키는 대꾸도 하지 않고 그렇게 말하고는, 책을 반대쪽으로 옮겨 놓고 계속 읽어 나갔다."]], [["The plump officer took up the list of wines and turned to the young officer.", "통통한 장교는 술 목록을 집어 들고 젊은 장교 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸다."]], [["“You choose what we’re to drink,”", "\"우리가 뭘 마실지 자네가 골라.\""], ["he said, handing him the card, and looking at him.", "그는 카드를 건네며 그를 바라보고 말했다."]], [["“Rhine wine, please,”", "\"라인 와인으로 부탁드립니다.\""], ["said the young officer, stealing a timid glance at Vronsky, and trying to pull his scarcely visible mustache.", "젊은 장교는 브론스키에게 겁먹은 듯한 시선을 슬쩍 던지며, 거의 보이지도 않는 콧수염을 잡아당기려 애쓰며 말했다."], ["Seeing that Vronsky did not turn round, the young officer got up.", "브론스키가 돌아보지 않는 것을 보고는 젊은 장교가 일어섰다."]], [["“Let’s go into the billiard room,”", "\"당구장으로 갑시다.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["The plump officer rose submissively, and they moved towards the door.", "통통한 장교는 순순히 일어났고, 두 사람은 문 쪽으로 걸어갔다."]], [["At that moment there walked into the room the tall and well-built Captain Yashvin.", "바로 그때 키가 크고 다부진 야시빈 대위가 방으로 들어왔다."], ["Nodding with an air of lofty contempt to the two officers, he went up to Vronsky.", "그는 두 장교에게 거만한 경멸의 기색으로 고갯짓을 하고는 브론스키에게 다가갔다."]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["here he is!”", "여기 있었군!\""], ["he cried, bringing his big hand down heavily on his epaulet.", "그가 큼직한 손을 브론스키의 견장 위로 묵직하게 내리치며 소리쳤다."], ["Vronsky looked round angrily, but his face lighted up immediately with his characteristic expression of genial and manly serenity.", "브론스키는 성난 듯 돌아보았지만, 그의 얼굴은 곧바로 그 특유의 다정하고 사내다운 평온의 표정으로 환해졌다."]], [["“That’s it, Alexey,”", "\"바로 그거야, 알렉세이.\""], ["said the captain, in his loud baritone.", "대위가 큰 바리톤 목소리로 말했다."], ["“You must just eat a mouthful, now, and drink only one tiny glass.”", "\"지금은 딱 한 입만 먹고, 자그마한 잔으로 딱 한 잔만 마셔야 하네.\""]], [["“Oh, I’m not hungry.”", "\"아, 난 배가 안 고파.\""]], [["“There go the inseparables,”", "\"저기 떼려야 뗄 수 없는 단짝들이 가시는군.\""], ["Yashvin dropped, glancing sarcastically at the two officers who were at that instant leaving the room.", "야시빈은 그 순간 방을 나서던 두 장교를 비꼬듯 흘낏 보며 한마디 흘렸다."], ["And he bent his long legs, swathed in tight riding breeches, and sat down in the chair, too low for him, so that his knees were cramped up in a sharp angle.", "그러고는 꽉 끼는 승마 바지로 감싼 긴 다리를 구부려, 자기에게는 너무 낮은 의자에 앉았는데, 그 바람에 무릎이 날카로운 각을 이루며 잔뜩 오므라들었다."]], [["“Why didn’t you turn up at the Red Theater yesterday?", "\"어제 적색 극장엔 왜 안 왔나?"], ["Numerova wasn’t at all bad.", "누메로바가 영 나쁘지 않던데."], ["Where were you?”", "자넨 어디 있었어?\""]], [["“I was late at the Tverskoys’,”", "\"트베르스코이 댁에 늦게까지 있었어.\""], ["said Vronsky.", "브론스키가 말했다."]], [["“Ah!”", "\"아!\""], ["responded Yashvin.", "야시빈이 대꾸했다."]], [["Yashvin, a gambler and a rake, a man not merely without moral principles, but of immoral principles, Yashvin was Vronsky’s greatest friend in the regiment.", "도박꾼이자 난봉꾼이며, 도덕적 원칙이 없을 뿐 아니라 비도덕적 원칙을 지닌 사내인 야시빈은 연대에서 브론스키의 가장 가까운 친구였다."], ["Vronsky liked him both for his exceptional physical strength, which he showed for the most part by being able to drink like a fish, and do without sleep without being in the slightest degree affected by it; and for his great strength of character, which he showed in his relations with his comrades and superior officers, commanding both fear and respect, and also at cards, when he would play for tens of thousands and however much he might have drunk, always with such skill and decision that he was reckoned the best player in the English Club.", "브론스키가 그를 좋아한 것은 그의 남다른 체력 때문이기도 했는데, 그 체력은 주로 술을 물처럼 마셔 대고 잠을 자지 않고도 조금도 영향을 받지 않는 데서 드러났다. 또한 동료들과 상관들과의 관계에서 두려움과 존경을 동시에 자아내며 발휘되는 그의 강한 성격 때문이기도 했고, 카드놀이에서 수만 루블을 걸고 아무리 술을 많이 마셨어도 늘 솜씨 좋고 결단력 있게 게임을 해서 영국 클럽 최고의 선수로 꼽히는 그 모습 때문이기도 했다."], ["Vronsky respected and liked Yashvin particularly because he felt Yashvin liked him, not for his name and his money, but for himself.", "브론스키가 야시빈을 각별히 존경하고 좋아한 것은, 야시빈이 자기를 그 이름이나 돈 때문이 아니라 자기 자신 때문에 좋아한다는 것을 느꼈기 때문이다."], ["And of all men he was the only one with whom Vronsky would have liked to speak of his love.", "그리고 브론스키가 자신의 사랑에 대해 이야기하고 싶은 마음이 드는 유일한 사내가 바로 그였다."], ["He felt that Yashvin, in spite of his apparent contempt for every sort of feeling, was the only man who could, so he fancied, comprehend the intense passion which now filled his whole life.", "야시빈은 온갖 종류의 감정을 겉으로는 경멸하는 듯 보이면서도, 지금 자기 삶 전체를 가득 채운 이 강렬한 열정을 이해할 수 있는 유일한 사내라고 브론스키는 여겼다."], ["Moreover, he felt certain that Yashvin, as it was, took no delight in gossip and scandal, and interpreted his feeling rightly, that is to say, knew and believed that this passion was not a jest, not a pastime, but something more serious and important.", "게다가 그는 야시빈이 험담이나 추문을 즐기지 않으며, 자신의 감정을 올바르게 해석하리라는 것을, 다시 말해 이 열정이 농담도 아니고 한때의 소일거리도 아니며 무언가 더 진지하고 중요한 것임을 알고 믿어 주리라는 것을 확신했다."]], [["Vronsky had never spoken to him of his passion, but he was aware that he knew all about it, and that he put the right interpretation on it, and he was glad to see that in his eyes.", "브론스키는 그에게 자신의 열정에 대해 한 번도 이야기한 적이 없었지만, 그가 그것을 모두 알고 있으며 올바르게 해석하고 있다는 것을 알았고, 그의 눈빛에서 그것을 읽어 내는 것이 기뻤다."]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["yes,”", "그래.\""], ["he said, to the announcement that Vronsky had been at the Tverskoys’; and his black eyes shining, he plucked at his left mustache, and began twisting it into his mouth, a bad habit he had.", "그는 브론스키가 트베르스코이 댁에 있었다는 말에 그렇게 대꾸했다. 그러고는 그 검은 눈을 빛내며, 왼쪽 콧수염을 잡아당겨 입속으로 비틀어 넣기 시작했는데, 그것은 그의 못된 버릇이었다."]], [["“Well, and what did you do yesterday?", "\"그래, 어제 자넨 뭘 했나?"], ["Win anything?”", "뭐 좀 땄어?\""], ["asked Vronsky.", "브론스키가 물었다."]], [["“Eight thousand.", "\"팔천 땄지."], ["But three don’t count; he won’t pay up.”", "하지만 그중 삼천은 안 쳐. 그 친구가 안 갚을 거거든.\""]], [["“Oh, then you can afford to lose over me,”", "\"오, 그럼 자네 나한테는 좀 잃어도 되겠군.\""], ["said Vronsky, laughing.", "브론스키가 웃으며 말했다."], ["(Yashvin had bet heavily on Vronsky in the races.)", "(야시빈은 경마에서 브론스키에게 큰돈을 걸었던 것이다.)"]], [["“No chance of my losing.", "\"내가 잃을 일은 없어."], ["Mahotin’s the only one that’s risky.”", "마호틴 하나만이 위험하지.\""]], [["And the conversation passed to forecasts of the coming race, the only thing Vronsky could think of just now.", "그리고 대화는 다가오는 경마에 대한 예측으로 넘어갔는데, 그것이야말로 지금 브론스키가 생각할 수 있는 유일한 것이었다."]], [["“Come along, I’ve finished,”", "\"가지, 난 다 먹었네.\""], ["said Vronsky, and getting up he went to the door.", "브론스키가 말하고 일어나 문 쪽으로 갔다."], ["Yashvin got up too, stretching his long legs and his long back.", "야시빈도 긴 다리와 긴 등을 쭉 펴며 일어섰다."]], [["“It’s too early for me to dine, but I must have a drink.", "\"식사하기엔 나한텐 너무 이르지만, 한잔은 해야겠어."], ["I’ll come along directly.", "곧 따라가지. 어이, 술!\""], ["Hi, wine!” he shouted, in his rich voice, that always rang out so loudly at drill, and set the windows shaking now.", "그는 훈련 때면 늘 그토록 우렁차게 울리던 그 풍부한 목소리로 외쳤고, 이번에는 그 소리에 창문이 흔들렸다."]], [["“No, all right,”", "\"아니, 됐어.\""], ["he shouted again immediately after.", "그는 곧바로 다시 외쳤다."], ["“You’re going home, so I’ll go with you.”", "\"자네 집에 가는 거니까, 나도 같이 가지.\""]], [["And he walked out with Vronsky.", "그러고는 브론스키와 함께 걸어 나갔다."]], [["# Chapter 20", "# Chapter 20"]], [["Vronsky was staying in a roomy, clean, Finnish hut, divided into two by a partition.", "브론스키는 칸막이로 둘로 나뉜, 널찍하고 깨끗한 핀란드식 오두막에 묵고 있었다."], ["Petritsky lived with him in camp too.", "페트리츠키도 진영에서 그와 함께 지냈다."], ["Petritsky was asleep when Vronsky and Yashvin came into the hut.", "브론스키와 야시빈이 오두막으로 들어왔을 때 페트리츠키는 잠들어 있었다."]], [["“Get up, don’t go on sleeping,”", "\"일어나, 더 자지 말고.\""], ["said Yashvin, going behind the partition and giving Petritsky, who was lying with ruffled hair and with his nose in the pillow, a prod on the shoulder.", "야시빈이 칸막이 뒤로 돌아가, 헝클어진 머리에 베개에 코를 박고 누워 있는 페트리츠키의 어깨를 툭 찌르며 말했다."]], [["Petritsky jumped up suddenly onto his knees and looked round.", "페트리츠키는 별안간 무릎을 꿇고 벌떡 일어나 주위를 둘러보았다."]], [["“Your brother’s been here,”", "\"자네 형이 왔다 갔어.\""], ["he said to Vronsky.", "그가 브론스키에게 말했다."], ["“He waked me up, damn him, and said he’d look in again.”", "\"젠장, 그가 날 깨우더니, 다시 들르겠다고 했어.\""], ["And pulling up the rug he flung himself back on the pillow.", "그러더니 담요를 끌어당겨 다시 베개에 몸을 던졌다."], ["“Oh, do shut up, Yashvin!”", "\"아, 좀 닥쳐, 야시빈!\""], ["he said, getting furious with Yashvin, who was pulling the rug off him.", "그는 담요를 끌어당겨 벗기는 야시빈에게 잔뜩 화를 내며 말했다."], ["“Shut up!”", "\"닥쳐!\""], ["He turned over and opened his eyes.", "그는 몸을 뒤척이며 눈을 떴다."], ["“You’d better tell me what to drink; such a nasty taste in my mouth, that....”", "\"그보다 뭘 마시면 좋을지나 말해 줘. 입안이 이렇게 텁텁하니, 원……\""]], [["“Brandy’s better than anything,”", "\"브랜디가 제일이지.\""], ["boomed Yashvin.", "야시빈이 우렁차게 말했다."], ["“Tereshtchenko!", "\"테레시첸코!"], ["brandy for your master and cucumbers,”", "주인 나리께 브랜디하고 오이를 가져와.\""], ["he shouted, obviously taking pleasure in the sound of his own voice.", "그는 자기 목소리의 울림을 즐기는 게 분명한 어조로 소리쳤다."]], [["“Brandy, do you think? Eh?”", "\"브랜디라고, 그게 좋겠어?"], ["queried Petritsky, blinking and rubbing his eyes.", "응?\" 페트리츠키가 눈을 깜박이며 비비더니 물었다."], ["“And you’ll drink something?", "\"자네도 한잔할 거지?"], ["All right then, we’ll have a drink together!", "좋아, 그럼 같이 마시자고!"], ["Vronsky, have a drink?”", "브론스키, 한잔하겠나?\""], ["said Petritsky, getting up and wrapping the tiger-skin rug round him.", "페트리츠키는 일어서서 호랑이 가죽 담요를 몸에 두르며 말했다."], ["He went to the door of the partition wall, raised his hands, and hummed in French, “There was a king in Thule.”", "그는 칸막이벽의 문 쪽으로 가서 두 손을 들고 프랑스어로 흥얼거렸다. \"툴레에 한 왕이 있었네.\""], ["“Vronsky, will you have a drink?”", "\"브론스키, 한잔하겠나?\""]], [["“Go along,”", "\"됐어, 그만둬.\""], ["said Vronsky, putting on the coat his valet handed to him.", "브론스키가 시종이 건네준 외투를 입으며 말했다."]], [["“Where are you off to?”", "\"어디 가는데?\""], ["asked Yashvin.", "야시빈이 물었다."], ["“Oh, here are your three horses,”", "\"아, 자네 삼두마차가 왔군.\""], ["he added, seeing the carriage drive up.", "마차가 다가오는 것을 보고 그가 덧붙였다."]], [["“To the stables, and I’ve got to see Bryansky, too, about the horses,”", "\"마구간에, 그리고 말 때문에 브랸스키도 만나 봐야 해.\""], ["said Vronsky.", "브론스키가 말했다."]], [["Vronsky had as a fact promised to call at Bryansky’s, some eight miles from Peterhof, and to bring him some money owing for some horses; and he hoped to have time to get that in too.", "브론스키는 사실 페테르고프에서 8마일쯤 떨어진 브랸스키의 집에 들러, 말 값으로 빚진 돈을 가져다주기로 약속해 둔 터였다. 그 일도 처리할 시간이 있기를 바랐다."], ["But his comrades were at once aware that he was not only going there.", "하지만 동료들은 그가 거기만 가려는 게 아니라는 것을 단번에 알아챘다."]], [["Petritsky, still humming, winked and made a pout with his lips, as though he would say: “Oh, yes, we know your Bryansky.”", "페트리츠키는 여전히 흥얼거리며 한쪽 눈을 찡긋하고 입술을 삐죽 내밀었다. 마치 \"그래, 그래, 자네 브랸스키가 어떤 양반인지 우리도 알지\" 하고 말하려는 듯이."]], [["“Mind you’re not late!”", "\"늦지 않게 조심해!\""], ["was Yashvin’s only comment; and to change the conversation: “How’s my roan?", "야시빈은 그 말만 했다. 그러고는 화제를 바꾸려고 \"내 적갈색 말은 어때?"], ["is he doing all right?”", "잘 지내고 있나?\""], ["he inquired, looking out of the window at the middle one of the three horses, which he had sold Vronsky.", "하고 물으며, 창밖으로 자기가 브론스키에게 판, 세 마리 중 가운뎃말을 내다보았다."]], [["“Stop!”", "\"잠깐!\""], ["cried Petritsky to Vronsky as he was just going out.", "브론스키가 막 나가려는데 페트리츠키가 외쳤다."], ["“Your brother left a letter and a note for you.", "\"자네 형이 자네한테 편지하고 쪽지를 남기고 갔어."], ["Wait a bit; where are they?”", "잠깐만, 그게 어디 있더라?\""]], [["Vronsky stopped.", "브론스키는 걸음을 멈췄다."]], [["“Well, where are they?”", "\"그래, 어디 있는데?\""]], [["“Where are they? That’s just the question!”", "\"어디 있느냐, 그게 바로 문제란 말이지!\""], ["said Petritsky solemnly, moving his forefinger upwards from his nose.", "페트리츠키가 집게손가락을 코에서 위로 치켜올리며 엄숙하게 말했다."]], [["“Come, tell me; this is silly!”", "\"이봐, 어서 말해. 이거 우습잖아!\""], ["said Vronsky smiling.", "브론스키가 웃으며 말했다."]], [["“I have not lighted the fire.", "\"불을 안 붙였는데."], ["Here somewhere about.”", "여기 어디쯤 있을 텐데.\""]], [["“Come, enough fooling!", "\"이봐, 장난 그만!"], ["Where is the letter?”", "편지가 어디 있어?\""]], [["“No, I’ve forgotten really.", "\"아니, 정말 깜빡했어."], ["Or was it a dream?", "아니면 꿈이었나?"], ["Wait a bit, wait a bit!", "잠깐, 잠깐만!"], ["But what’s the use of getting in a rage.", "그런데 화낼 게 뭐 있어."], ["If you’d drunk four bottles yesterday as I did you’d forget where you were lying.", "자네도 어제 나처럼 네 병을 들이켰으면 어디 누웠는지조차 잊어버렸을걸."], ["Wait a bit, I’ll remember!”", "잠깐만, 떠올려 볼게!\""]], [["Petritsky went behind the partition and lay down on his bed.", "페트리츠키는 칸막이 뒤로 돌아가 자기 침대에 누웠다."]], [["“Wait a bit!", "\"잠깐!"], ["This was how I was lying, and this was how he was standing.", "내가 이렇게 누워 있었고, 형은 이렇게 서 있었지. 그래, 그래, 그래……"], ["Yes—yes—yes....", "여기 있다!\""], ["Here it is!”—and Petritsky pulled a letter out from under the mattress, where he had hidden it.", "페트리츠키는 매트리스 밑에 숨겨 두었던 편지를 끄집어냈다."]], [["Vronsky took the letter and his brother’s note.", "브론스키는 편지와 형의 쪽지를 받아 들었다."], ["It was the letter he was expecting—from his mother, reproaching him for not having been to see her—and the note was from his brother to say that he must have a little talk with him.", "그것은 그가 예상하던 편지였다. 어머니가 자기를 보러 오지 않았다고 그를 나무라는 편지였고, 쪽지는 형이 잠깐 할 이야기가 있다고 적은 것이었다."], ["Vronsky knew that it was all about the same thing.", "브론스키는 그것이 모두 같은 일에 관한 것임을 알고 있었다."], ["“What business is it of theirs!”", "'그게 그들과 무슨 상관이람!'"], ["thought Vronsky, and crumpling up the letters he thrust them between the buttons of his coat so as to read them carefully on the road.", "브론스키는 생각하고는, 길에서 찬찬히 읽으려고 편지들을 구겨 외투 단추 사이에 끼워 넣었다. 오두막 현관에서 그는 두 장교와 마주쳤다."], ["In the porch of the hut he was met by two officers; one of his regiment and one of another.", "하나는 자기 연대 소속이었고, 하나는 다른 연대 소속이었다."]], [["Vronsky’s quarters were always a meeting place for all the officers.", "브론스키의 숙소는 늘 모든 장교들의 만남의 장소였다."]], [["“Where are you off to?”", "\"어디 가나?\""]], [["“I must go to Peterhof.”", "\"페테르고프에 가야 해.\""]], [["“Has the mare come from Tsarskoe?”", "\"차르스코예에서 암말이 왔나?\""]], [["“Yes, but I’ve not seen her yet.”", "\"응, 하지만 아직 못 봤어.\""]], [["“They say Mahotin’s Gladiator’s lame.”", "\"마호틴의 글라디아토르가 절름거린다던데.\""]], [["“Nonsense!", "\"말도 안 돼!"], ["But however are you going to race in this mud?”", "그런데 자네는 대체 이런 진창에서 어떻게 경마를 할 셈인가?\""], ["said the other.", "다른 장교가 말했다."]], [["“Here are my saviors!”", "\"내 구원자들이 오셨군!\""], ["cried Petritsky, seeing them come in.", "페트리츠키가 그들이 들어오는 것을 보고 외쳤다."], ["Before him stood the orderly with a tray of brandy and salted cucumbers.", "그의 앞에는 브랜디와 소금에 절인 오이를 쟁반에 받쳐 든 당번병이 서 있었다."], ["“Here’s Yashvin ordering me to drink a pick-me-up.”", "\"야시빈이 나더러 해장술을 마시라는군.\""]], [["“Well, you did give it to us yesterday,”", "\"이봐, 어제 자네 때문에 우리가 얼마나 혼났는지 아나.\""], ["said one of those who had come in; “you didn’t let us get a wink of sleep all night.”", "들어온 장교 중 하나가 말했다. \"밤새도록 한숨도 못 자게 했잖아.\""]], [["“Oh, didn’t we make a pretty finish!”", "\"아, 우리 멋들어지게 끝장을 봤지!\""], ["said Petritsky.", "페트리츠키가 말했다."], ["“Volkov climbed onto the roof and began telling us how sad he was.", "\"볼코프가 지붕 위로 기어올라가더니 자기가 얼마나 슬픈지 늘어놓기 시작했지."], ["I said: ‘Let’s have music, the funeral march!’ He fairly dropped asleep on the roof over the funeral march.”", "그래서 내가 '음악을 틀자고, 장송 행진곡으로!' 했더니, 글쎄 그 장송 행진곡을 들으면서 지붕 위에서 그대로 잠들어 버렸지 뭐야.\""]], [["“Drink it up; you positively must drink the brandy, and then seltzer water and a lot of lemon,”", "\"마셔, 자네는 무조건 브랜디를 마셔야 해. 그러고 나서 셀처 탄산수에 레몬을 듬뿍 넣어 마시고.\""], ["said Yashvin, standing over Petritsky like a mother making a child take medicine, “and then a little champagne—just a small bottle.”", "야시빈이 마치 아이에게 약을 먹이는 어머니처럼 페트리츠키 위로 몸을 굽힌 채 말했다. \"그다음에 샴페인을 조금, 딱 작은 병으로 하나만.\""]], [["“Come, there’s some sense in that.", "\"이봐, 그 말엔 일리가 있군."], ["Stop a bit, Vronsky.", "잠깐 있어 봐, 브론스키."], ["We’ll all have a drink.”", "다 같이 한잔하자고.\""]], [["“No; good-bye all of you.", "\"아니, 다들 잘 있게."], ["I’m not going to drink today.”", "난 오늘은 안 마실 거야.\""]], [["“Why, are you gaining weight?", "\"왜, 살이라도 찌고 있나?"], ["All right, then we must have it alone.", "좋아, 그럼 우리끼리 마셔야지."], ["Give us the seltzer water and lemon.”", "셀처 탄산수에 레몬 가져와.\""]], [["“Vronsky!”", "\"브론스키!\""], ["shouted someone when he was already outside.", "그가 이미 밖에 나와 있을 때 누군가가 소리쳤다."]], [["“Well?”", "\"왜?\""]], [["“You’d better get your hair cut, it’ll weigh you down, especially at the top.”", "\"머리나 좀 자르지 그래. 무게에 짓눌리겠어, 특히 정수리 쪽이.\""]], [["Vronsky was in fact beginning, prematurely, to get a little bald.", "브론스키는 사실 때 이르게 머리가 조금씩 벗어지기 시작하던 참이었다."], ["He laughed gaily, showing his even teeth, and pulling his cap over the thin place, went out and got into his carriage.", "그는 고른 치아를 드러내며 유쾌하게 웃고는, 머리숱이 적어진 자리에 모자를 눌러쓰고 밖으로 나가 마차에 올랐다."]], [["“To the stables!”", "\"마구간으로!\""], ["he said, and was just pulling out the letters to read them through, but he thought better of it, and put off reading them so as not to distract his attention before looking at the mare.", "그는 말하고는, 편지들을 다 읽으려고 막 꺼내 들었다가, 다시 생각을 고쳐먹고는, 암말을 보기 전에 주의가 흐트러지지 않도록 읽기를 미루었다."], ["“Later!”", "'나중에!'"]], [["# Chapter 21", "# Chapter 21"]], [["The temporary stable, a wooden shed, had been put up close to the race course, and there his mare was to have been taken the previous day.", "경마장 가까이에 임시 마구간으로 쓸 목조 헛간이 세워졌고, 그의 암말은 전날 그리로 옮겨졌어야 했다."], ["He had not yet seen her there.", "그는 아직 거기서 말을 보지 못했다."]], [["During the last few days he had not ridden her out for exercise himself, but had put her in the charge of the trainer, and so now he positively did not know in what condition his mare had arrived yesterday and was today.", "지난 며칠 동안 그는 직접 말을 끌고 나가 운동을 시키지 않고 조련사에게 맡겨 두었기에, 지금은 자기 암말이 어제 어떤 상태로 도착했는지, 또 오늘은 어떤지 도무지 알지 못했다."], ["He had scarcely got out of his carriage when his groom, the so-called “stable boy,” recognizing the carriage some way off, called the trainer.", "그가 마차에서 채 내리기도 전에, 이른바 '마구간지기 소년'이라 불리는 마부가 멀찍이서 마차를 알아보고 조련사를 불렀다."], ["A dry-looking Englishman, in high boots and a short jacket, clean-shaven, except for a tuft below his chin, came to meet him, walking with the uncouth gait of jockey, turning his elbows out and swaying from side to side.", "긴 장화에 짧은 재킷을 입고, 턱 밑에 수염 한 줌만 남긴 채 말끔히 면도한, 메마른 인상의 영국인이 그를 맞으러 나왔다. 기수 특유의 투박한 걸음걸이로, 팔꿈치를 바깥으로 벌리고 좌우로 몸을 흔들면서."]], [["“Well, how’s Frou-Frou?”", "\"그래, 프루프루는 좀 어떤가?\""], ["Vronsky asked in English.", "브론스키가 영어로 물었다."]], [["“All right, sir,”", "\"괜찮습니다, 나리.\""], ["the Englishman’s voice responded somewhere in the inside of his throat.", "영국인의 목소리가 어딘가 목구멍 깊숙한 데서 흘러나왔다."], ["“Better not go in,”", "\"안 들어가시는 게 낫겠습니다.\""], ["he added, touching his hat.", "그가 모자에 손을 대며 덧붙였다."], ["“I’ve put a muzzle on her, and the mare’s fidgety.", "\"재갈을 물려 놓았는데, 말이 안절부절못합니다."], ["Better not go in, it’ll excite the mare.”", "안 들어가시는 게 낫겠습니다. 들어가시면 말이 흥분합니다.\""]], [["“No, I’m going in.", "\"아니, 들어갈 거야."], ["I want to look at her.”", "한번 보고 싶군.\""]], [["“Come along, then,”", "\"그럼 따라오시지요.\""], ["said the Englishman, frowning, and speaking with his mouth shut, and, with swinging elbows, he went on in front with his disjointed gait.", "영국인이 미간을 찌푸리고 입을 닫은 채 말하더니, 팔꿈치를 흔들며 그 어긋난 걸음걸이로 앞장서 갔다."]], [["They went into the little yard in front of the shed.", "그들은 헛간 앞의 작은 마당으로 들어갔다."], ["A stable boy, spruce and smart in his holiday attire, met them with a broom in his hand, and followed them.", "나들이옷을 차려입어 말쑥하고 멀끔한 마구간지기 소년이 빗자루를 손에 들고 그들을 맞더니 뒤를 따라왔다."], ["In the shed there were five horses in their separate stalls, and Vronsky knew that his chief rival, Gladiator, a very tall chestnut horse, had been brought there, and must be standing among them.", "헛간 안에는 다섯 마리의 말이 칸막이마다 따로 들어 있었고, 브론스키는 자기 최대의 경쟁마인 글라디아토르가, 아주 키가 큰 밤색 말이 그리로 옮겨져 그 사이에 서 있으리라는 것을 알고 있었다."], ["Even more than his mare, Vronsky longed to see Gladiator, whom he had never seen.", "브론스키는 자기 암말보다도 한 번도 본 적 없는 글라디아토르를 더 보고 싶었다."], ["But he knew that by the etiquette of the race course it was not merely impossible for him to see the horse, but improper even to ask questions about him.", "하지만 경마장의 예법으로는 그 말을 보는 것이 불가능할 뿐만 아니라, 그에 관해 묻는 것조차 무례한 일이라는 것을 그는 알고 있었다."], ["Just as he was passing along the passage, the boy opened the door into the second horse-box on the left, and Vronsky caught a glimpse of a big chestnut horse with white legs.", "그가 마침 통로를 지나가는데, 소년이 왼편 두 번째 마구간 칸의 문을 열었고, 브론스키는 흰 다리를 가진 커다란 밤색 말을 언뜻 보았다."], ["He knew that this was Gladiator, but, with the feeling of a man turning away from the sight of another man’s open letter, he turned round and went into Frou-Frou’s stall.", "그는 그것이 글라디아토르임을 알았지만, 남이 펼쳐 놓은 편지에서 눈을 돌리는 사람의 심정으로 몸을 돌려 프루프루의 칸으로 들어갔다."]], [["“The horse is here belonging to Mak... Mak...", "\"여기 있는 이 말은 마크……마크……, 도무지 그 이름을 부를 수가 없네요.\""], ["I never can say the name,” said the Englishman, over his shoulder, pointing his big finger and dirty nail towards Gladiator’s stall.", "영국인이 어깨 너머로, 굵은 손가락과 더러운 손톱을 글라디아토르의 칸 쪽으로 가리키며 말했다."]], [["“Mahotin?", "\"마호틴 말인가?"], ["Yes, he’s my most serious rival,”", "그래, 그가 내 가장 만만찮은 경쟁자지.\""], ["said Vronsky.", "브론스키가 말했다."]], [["“If you were riding him,”", "\"나리께서 저 말을 타신다면,\""], ["said the Englishman, “I’d bet on you.”", "영국인이 말했다, \"저는 나리께 걸겠습니다.\""]], [["“Frou-Frou’s more nervous; he’s stronger,”", "\"프루프루가 더 예민하고, 저쪽이 더 힘이 세지.\""], ["said Vronsky, smiling at the compliment to his riding.", "브론스키가 자기 승마 실력에 대한 칭찬에 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["“In a steeplechase it all depends on riding and on pluck,”", "\"장애물 경마에서는 모든 게 기수의 솜씨와 배짱에 달려 있지요.\""], ["said the Englishman.", "영국인이 말했다."]], [["Of pluck—that is, energy and courage—Vronsky did not merely feel that he had enough; what was of far more importance, he was firmly convinced that no one in the world could have more of this “pluck”", "배짱에 대해서라면—그러니까 기력과 용기 말이다—브론스키는 단지 그것이 충분하다고 느끼는 정도가 아니었다."], ["than he had.", "그보다 훨씬 더 중요한 것은, 세상 누구도 자기보다 이 '배짱'을 더 많이 가질 수는 없으리라고 그가 굳게 확신하고 있다는 점이었다."]], [["“Don’t you think I want more thinning down?”", "\"내가 살을 좀 더 빼야 한다고 보지는 않나?\""]], [["“Oh, no,”", "\"오, 아닙니다.\""], ["answered the Englishman.", "영국인이 대답했다."], ["“Please, don’t speak loud.", "\"제발 큰 소리로 말씀하지 마십시오."], ["The mare’s fidgety,”", "말이 안절부절못합니다.\""], ["he added, nodding towards the horse-box, before which they were standing, and from which came the sound of restless stamping in the straw.", "그가 그들이 바로 앞에 서 있는, 안에서 짚을 밟으며 부산하게 발을 구르는 소리가 들려오는 마구간 칸을 향해 고갯짓하며 덧붙였다."]], [["He opened the door, and Vronsky went into the horse-box, dimly lighted by one little window.", "그가 문을 열자 브론스키는 작은 창 하나로 어슴푸레하게 밝은 마구간 칸으로 들어갔다."], ["In the horse-box stood a dark bay mare, with a muzzle on, picking at the fresh straw with her hoofs.", "칸 안에는 짙은 적갈색 암말이 재갈을 문 채 발굽으로 신선한 짚을 헤집으며 서 있었다."], ["Looking round him in the twilight of the horse-box, Vronsky unconsciously took in once more in a comprehensive glance all the points of his favorite mare.", "칸 안의 어스름 속에서 둘러보던 브론스키는 무심결에 한 번 더, 자기가 아끼는 암말의 모든 특징을 한눈에 죽 훑었다."], ["Frou-Frou was a beast of medium size, not altogether free from reproach, from a breeder’s point of view.", "프루프루는 중간 크기의 짐승으로, 사육가의 눈으로 보면 흠이 전혀 없다고는 할 수 없었다."], ["She was small-boned all over; though her chest was extremely prominent in front, it was narrow.", "온몸의 뼈대가 가늘었고, 가슴은 앞쪽으로 유난히 도드라졌지만 폭이 좁았다."], ["Her hind-quarters were a little drooping, and in her fore-legs, and still more in her hind-legs, there was a noticeable curvature.", "뒷부분은 다소 처져 있었고, 앞다리에도, 그리고 뒷다리에는 한층 더, 눈에 띄는 굽음이 있었다."], ["The muscles of both hind- and fore-legs were not very thick; but across her shoulders the mare was exceptionally broad, a peculiarity specially striking now that she was lean from training.", "뒷다리와 앞다리의 근육은 그리 굵지 않았다. 하지만 어깨 너비만큼은 이 암말이 유달리 넓었는데, 조련으로 살이 빠진 지금에 와서 그 특징이 특히 두드러져 보였다."], ["The bones of her legs below the knees looked no thicker than a finger from in front, but were extraordinarily thick seen from the side.", "무릎 아래 다리뼈는 앞에서 보면 손가락 굵기밖에 안 되는 듯했지만, 옆에서 보면 유독 굵었다."], ["She looked altogether, except across the shoulders, as it were, pinched in at the sides and pressed out in depth.", "어깨 너비를 빼고 보면, 말은 옆구리가 죄어들고 깊이만 짓눌려 늘어난 듯한 모양새였다."], ["But she had in the highest degree the quality that makes all defects forgotten: that quality was blood, the blood that tells, as the English expression has it.", "그러나 모든 결점을 잊게 만드는 자질만큼은 그 어떤 말보다도 높은 정도로 지니고 있었다. 그 자질은 곧 혈통이었다."], ["The muscles stood up sharply under the network of sinews, covered with the delicate, mobile skin, soft as satin, and they were hard as bone.", "영어 표현대로 하자면, 그 자체로 말을 해 주는 혈통이었다. 비단처럼 부드럽고 섬세하며 잘 움직이는 살갗에 덮인 힘줄의 그물 아래로 근육이 또렷하게 솟아 있었고, 그것은 뼈처럼 단단했다."], ["Her clean-cut head, with prominent, bright, spirited eyes, broadened out at the open nostrils, that showed the red blood in the cartilage within.", "또렷하게 빚어진 그 머리에는 도드라지고 빛나며 생기 넘치는 눈이 박혀 있었고, 벌어진 콧구멍에서 넓어졌는데, 그 안쪽 연골에는 붉은 핏기가 비쳤다."], ["About all her figure, and especially her head, there was a certain expression of energy, and, at the same time, of softness.", "온몸에서, 특히 머리에서 어떤 기력의 표정이, 그러면서 동시에 부드러움의 표정이 풍겨 났다."], ["She was one of those creatures which seem only not to speak because the mechanism of their mouth does not allow them to.", "그 말은 입의 구조가 허락하지 않을 뿐, 말을 못 한다는 것만 빼면 거의 사람 같은 그런 짐승의 하나였다."]], [["To Vronsky, at any rate, it seemed that she understood all he felt at that moment, looking at her.", "적어도 브론스키에게는, 자기가 그 순간 말을 바라보며 느끼는 모든 것을 그 말이 다 알아차린 듯이 보였다."]], [["Directly Vronsky went towards her, she drew in a deep breath, and, turning back her prominent eye till the white looked bloodshot, she started at the approaching figures from the opposite side, shaking her muzzle, and shifting lightly from one leg to the other.", "브론스키가 곧장 다가가자, 말은 깊이 숨을 들이쉬더니 도드라진 눈을 뒤로 굴려 흰자위가 충혈된 것처럼 보이도록 한 채, 반대편에서 다가오는 모습에 흠칫 놀라 재갈을 흔들며 이쪽저쪽으로 가볍게 다리를 옮겨 디뎠다."]], [["“There, you see how fidgety she is,”", "\"보십시오, 저렇게 안절부절못하지 않습니까.\""], ["said the Englishman.", "영국인이 말했다."]], [["“There, darling!", "\"자, 착하지!"], ["There!”", "자!\""], ["said Vronsky, going up to the mare and speaking soothingly to her.", "브론스키가 말에게 다가가며 달래듯 말했다."]], [["But the nearer he came, the more excited she grew.", "하지만 그가 가까이 갈수록 말은 더욱 흥분했다."], ["Only when he stood by her head, she was suddenly quieter, while the muscles quivered under her soft, delicate coat.", "그가 말의 머리맡에 섰을 때에야 비로소 말은 갑자기 잠잠해졌고, 다만 부드럽고 섬세한 털가죽 아래로 근육이 떨렸다."], ["Vronsky patted her strong neck, straightened over her sharp withers a stray lock of her mane that had fallen on the other side, and moved his face near her dilated nostrils, transparent as a bat’s wing.", "브론스키는 그 단단한 목을 토닥이고, 날카로운 기갑 위로 반대편으로 흘러내린 갈기 한 가닥을 바로잡아 주었으며, 박쥐 날개처럼 투명하게 벌어진 콧구멍 가까이 얼굴을 가져갔다."], ["She drew a loud breath and snorted out through her tense nostrils, started, pricked up her sharp ear, and put out her strong, black lip towards Vronsky, as though she would nip hold of his sleeve.", "말은 큰 소리로 숨을 들이쉬더니 팽팽해진 콧구멍으로 거칠게 콧김을 뿜었고, 흠칫하더니 날카로운 귀를 쫑긋 세우고, 마치 그의 소맷자락을 물어 잡으려는 듯 억세고 검은 입술을 브론스키 쪽으로 내밀었다."], ["But remembering the muzzle, she shook it and again began restlessly stamping one after the other her shapely legs.", "하지만 재갈이 물려 있다는 것을 떠올린 듯 그것을 흔들더니, 다시 안절부절못하며 맵시 좋은 다리를 차례로 굴러 대기 시작했다."]], [["“Quiet, darling, quiet!”", "\"가만, 착하지, 가만!\""], ["he said, patting her again over her hind-quarters; and with a glad sense that his mare was in the best possible condition, he went out of the horse-box.", "그는 다시 한번 말의 뒷부분을 토닥이며 말했다. 그리고 자기 암말이 더할 나위 없이 좋은 상태라는 흐뭇한 기분을 안고 마구간 칸을 나왔다."]], [["The mare’s excitement had infected Vronsky.", "말의 흥분이 브론스키에게도 옮아붙었다."], ["He felt that his heart was throbbing, and that he, too, like the mare, longed to move, to bite; it was both dreadful and delicious.", "그는 제 심장이 두근거리는 것을, 그리고 자기도 그 말처럼 움직이고 싶고 물어뜯고 싶어진다는 것을 느꼈다. 그것은 끔찍하면서도 황홀한 기분이었다."]], [["“Well, I rely on you, then,”", "\"그럼, 자네만 믿겠네.\""], ["he said to the Englishman; “half-past six on the ground.”", "그가 영국인에게 말했다. \"여섯 시 반에 경마장에서 보세.\""]], [["“All right,”", "\"알겠습니다.\""], ["said the Englishman.", "영국인이 말했다."], ["“Oh, where are you going, my lord?”", "\"오, 어디로 가십니까, 나리?\""], ["he asked suddenly, using the title “my lord,” which he had scarcely ever used before.", "그가 갑자기 그동안 거의 써 본 적이 없는 '나리'라는 호칭을 쓰며 물었다."]], [["Vronsky in amazement raised his head, and stared, as he knew how to stare, not into the Englishman’s eyes, but at his forehead, astounded at the impertinence of his question.", "브론스키는 놀라 고개를 들고, 그 질문의 무례함에 어안이 벙벙한 채로, 자기가 곧잘 그러듯 영국인의 눈이 아니라 이마를 빤히 바라보았다."], ["But realizing that in asking this the Englishman had been looking at him not as an employer, but as a jockey, he answered:", "하지만 이렇게 물으면서 영국인이 자기를 고용주가 아니라 한 사람의 기수로 보고 있었음을 깨닫고는 이렇게 답했다."]], [["“I’ve got to go to Bryansky’s; I shall be home within an hour.”", "\"브랸스키에게 들러야 하네. 한 시간 안에 집에 돌아올 걸세.\""]], [["“How often I’m asked that question today!”", "'오늘 이 질문을 대체 몇 번이나 받는 건지!'"], ["he said to himself, and he blushed, a thing which rarely happened to him.", "그는 속으로 중얼거렸고, 좀처럼 없던 일이지만 얼굴을 붉혔다."], ["The Englishman looked gravely at him; and, as though he, too, knew where Vronsky was going, he added:", "영국인은 그를 진지하게 바라보더니, 마치 자기도 브론스키가 어디로 가는지 안다는 듯 이렇게 덧붙였다."]], [["“The great thing’s to keep quiet before a race,”", "\"경기 전에 무엇보다 중요한 건 침착함을 잃지 않는 겁니다.\""], ["said he; “don’t get out of temper or upset about anything.”", "그가 말했다. \"성을 내거나 무슨 일로든 마음을 흩뜨리지 마십시오.\""]], [["“All right,”", "\"알겠네.\""], ["answered Vronsky, smiling; and jumping into his carriage, he told the man to drive to Peterhof.", "브론스키가 미소 지으며 대답했다. 그러고는 마차에 뛰어올라 페테르고프로 가라고 마부에게 일렀다."]], [["Before he had driven many paces away, the dark clouds that had been threatening rain all day broke, and there was a heavy downpour of rain.", "마차가 몇 걸음 채 나아가기도 전에, 온종일 비를 머금고 위협하던 먹구름이 터지면서 굵은 빗줄기가 쏟아져 내렸다."]], [["“What a pity!”", "'아이고, 야단났군!'"], ["thought Vronsky, putting up the roof of the carriage.", "브론스키는 마차 지붕을 올리며 생각했다."], ["“It was muddy before, now it will be a perfect swamp.”", "'전에도 진창이었는데, 이제 완전히 늪이 되겠어.'"], ["As he sat in solitude in the closed carriage, he took out his mother’s letter and his brother’s note, and read them through.", "닫힌 마차 안에 홀로 앉아, 그는 어머니의 편지와 형의 쪽지를 꺼내어 처음부터 끝까지 읽었다."]], [["Yes, it was the same thing over and over again.", "그래, 또 그 소리, 똑같은 소리의 되풀이였다."], ["Everyone, his mother, his brother, everyone thought fit to interfere in the affairs of his heart.", "모두가, 어머니도, 형도, 모두가 자기 마음의 일에 간섭하는 것을 마땅한 일로 여겼다."], ["This interference aroused in him a feeling of angry hatred—a feeling he had rarely known before.", "이런 간섭은 그의 마음속에 성난 증오의 감정을 불러일으켰다. 전에는 좀처럼 느껴 본 적 없는 감정이었다."], ["“What business is it of theirs?", "'대체 저들과 무슨 상관이람?"], ["Why does everybody feel called upon to concern himself about me?", "어째서 다들 나를 두고 한마디씩 해야 한다고 여기는 거지?"], ["And why do they worry me so?", "또 어째서 이렇게 나를 못살게 구는 걸까?"], ["Just because they see that this is something they can’t understand.", "그저 이게 자기들이 이해할 수 없는 무엇이라는 걸 알기 때문이야."], ["If it were a common, vulgar, worldly intrigue, they would have left me alone.", "만약 이게 흔해 빠진, 천박한 세속의 정사였다면 다들 나를 가만 내버려 두었겠지."], ["They feel that this is something different, that this is not a mere pastime, that this woman is dearer to me than life.", "저들은 이게 뭔가 다르다는 걸, 한낱 심심풀이 놀음이 아니라는 걸, 이 여자가 나에게 목숨보다 소중하다는 걸 느끼고 있어."], ["And this is incomprehensible, and that’s why it annoys them.", "그래서 이해할 수 없는 거고, 그래서 신경에 거슬리는 거지."], ["Whatever our destiny is or may be, we have made it ourselves, and we do not complain of it,”", "우리 운명이 어떻든, 또 앞으로 어떻게 되든, 그건 우리가 스스로 만든 것이고, 우리는 거기에 대해 불평하지 않아.'"], ["he said, in the word we linking himself with Anna.", "그는 우리라는 말 속에 자기와 안나를 한데 묶으며 생각했다."], ["“No, they must needs teach us how to live.", "'아니, 저들은 기어이 우리에게 어떻게 살아야 하는지를 가르치려 들지."], ["They haven’t an idea of what happiness is; they don’t know that without our love, for us there is neither happiness nor unhappiness—no life at all,”", "행복이 무엇인지 손톱만큼도 모르면서. 우리의 사랑이 없다면 우리에게는 행복도 불행도 없다는 걸, 삶 자체가 없다는 걸 모르는 거야.'"], ["he thought.", "그는 생각했다."]], [["He was angry with all of them for their interference just because he felt in his soul that they, all these people, were right.", "그가 저들 모두의 간섭에 화가 난 것은, 바로 저들이, 이 모든 사람이 옳다는 것을 마음속으로 느끼고 있었기 때문이었다."], ["He felt that the love that bound him to Anna was not a momentary impulse, which would pass, as worldly intrigues do pass, leaving no other traces in the life of either but pleasant or unpleasant memories.", "자기를 안나에게 묶어 놓은 사랑이, 세속의 정사가 그러하듯 지나가 버리고 마는, 그래서 두 사람의 삶에 즐겁거나 불쾌한 기억 말고는 아무런 흔적도 남기지 않는 한순간의 충동이 아님을 그는 느끼고 있었다."], ["He felt all the torture of his own and her position, all the difficulty there was for them, conspicuous as they were in the eye of all the world, in concealing their love, in lying and deceiving; and in lying, deceiving, feigning, and continually thinking of others, when the passion that united them was so intense that they were both oblivious of everything else but their love.", "그는 자기와 그녀의 처지가 안고 있는 온갖 고통을, 온 세상의 눈에 그토록 두드러진 두 사람이 사랑을 감추고, 거짓말하고 속이는 일에서 겪는 온갖 어려움을 느끼고 있었다. 그리고 둘을 하나로 묶는 정열이 너무도 격렬하여 둘 다 자기들의 사랑 외에는 모든 것을 잊고 마는 마당에, 거짓말하고 속이고 꾸며 대며 끊임없이 남들을 신경 써야 하는 그 어려움을."]], [["He vividly recalled all the constantly recurring instances of inevitable necessity for lying and deceit, which were so against his natural bent.", "거짓말과 속임수가 불가피하게 끊임없이 되풀이되던 온갖 경우가 그의 머릿속에 생생하게 떠올랐다. 그것은 그의 천성에 너무도 어긋나는 것이었다."], ["He recalled particularly vividly the shame he had more than once detected in her at this necessity for lying and deceit.", "특히 생생하게 떠오른 것은, 거짓말하고 속여야 하는 이 필요 앞에서 그녀가 부끄러워하는 모습을 한두 번이 아니게 알아챘던 일이었다."], ["And he experienced the strange feeling that had sometimes come upon him since his secret love for Anna.", "그리고 안나를 향한 은밀한 사랑이 시작된 뒤로 이따금 그에게 찾아들곤 했던 그 기묘한 감정을 그는 다시 맛보았다."], ["This was a feeling of loathing for something—whether for Alexey Alexandrovitch, or for himself, or for the whole world, he could not have said.", "그것은 무언가에 대한 혐오의 감정이었는데, 그것이 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에 대한 것인지, 자기 자신에 대한 것인지, 아니면 온 세상에 대한 것인지 그로서는 말할 수 없었다."], ["But he always drove away this strange feeling.", "하지만 그는 늘 이 기묘한 감정을 쫓아 버렸다."], ["Now, too, he shook it off and continued the thread of his thoughts.", "지금도 그는 그것을 떨쳐 내고 다시 생각의 실마리를 이어 갔다."]], [["“Yes, she was unhappy before, but proud and at peace; and now she cannot be at peace and feel secure in her dignity, though she does not show it.", "'그래, 그녀는 전에도 불행했지만, 자긍심을 지녔고 마음이 평온했어. 그런데 지금은 겉으로 드러내지는 않아도 마음의 평온도, 자기 존엄에 대한 안도감도 누리지 못해."], ["Yes, we must put an end to it,”", "그래, 우리는 이걸 끝내야 해.'"], ["he decided.", "그는 마음을 정했다."]], [["And for the first time the idea clearly presented itself that it was essential to put an end to this false position, and the sooner the better.", "그리고 처음으로 이 거짓된 처지에 끝장을 내야 한다는, 그것도 빠르면 빠를수록 좋다는 생각이 또렷하게 떠올랐다."], ["“Throw up everything, she and I, and hide ourselves somewhere alone with our love,”", "'모든 걸 내던지고, 그녀와 내가 우리의 사랑과 함께 어딘가 단둘이 숨어 버리는 거야.'"], ["he said to himself.", "그는 혼잣말을 했다."]], [["# Chapter 22", "# Chapter 22"]], [["The rain did not last long, and by the time Vronsky arrived, his shaft-horse trotting at full speed and dragging the trace-horses galloping through the mud, with their reins hanging loose, the sun had peeped out again, the roofs of the summer villas and the old limetrees in the gardens on both sides of the principal streets sparkled with wet brilliance, and from the twigs came a pleasant drip and from the roofs rushing streams of water.", "비는 오래가지 않았고, 브론스키가 도착할 무렵에는, 그의 끌채말이 전속력으로 달리며 고삐를 늘어뜨린 채 진창 속을 질주하는 곁말들을 끌고 오는 동안, 해가 다시 빼꼼 얼굴을 내밀어, 별장들의 지붕이며 큰길 양편 정원에 늘어선 늙은 보리수들이 젖은 채 반짝반짝 빛났고, 잔가지에서는 기분 좋게 물방울이 떨어지고 지붕에서는 물줄기가 콸콸 흘러내렸다."], ["He thought no more of the shower spoiling the race course, but was rejoicing now that—thanks to the rain—he would be sure to find her at home and alone, as he knew that Alexey Alexandrovitch, who had lately returned from a foreign watering place, had not moved from Petersburg.", "그는 소나기가 경마장을 망쳐 버린 것 따위는 더 이상 생각하지 않고, 다만 이 비 덕분에 그녀가 분명 집에 혼자 있으리라는 사실에 기뻐하고 있었다. 그는 최근 외국의 온천지에서 돌아온 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 아직 페테르부르크를 떠나지 않았다는 것을 알고 있었기 때문이다."]], [["Hoping to find her alone, Vronsky alighted, as he always did, to avoid attracting attention, before crossing the bridge, and walked to the house.", "그녀를 혼자 만날 수 있기를 바라며, 브론스키는 늘 그러듯 사람들의 눈에 띄지 않기 위해 다리를 건너기 전에 마차에서 내려 집까지 걸어갔다."], ["He did not go up the steps to the street door, but went into the court.", "그는 길 쪽 현관 계단으로 올라가지 않고 안뜰로 들어갔다."]], [["“Has your master come?”", "\"주인 나리께서는 돌아오셨나?\""], ["he asked a gardener.", "그가 정원사에게 물었다."]], [["“No, sir.", "\"아닙니다, 나리."], ["The mistress is at home.", "마님께서는 집에 계십니다."], ["But will you please go to the front door; there are servants there,”", "하지만 부디 정문으로 가 주십시오. 거기에 하인들이 있습니다.\""], ["the gardener answered.", "정원사가 대답했다."], ["“They’ll open the door.”", "\"그들이 문을 열어 드릴 겁니다.\""]], [["“No, I’ll go in from the garden.”", "\"아니, 정원 쪽으로 들어가겠네.\""]], [["And feeling satisfied that she was alone, and wanting to take her by surprise, since he had not promised to be there today, and she would certainly not expect him to come before the races, he walked, holding his sword and stepping cautiously over the sandy path, bordered with flowers, to the terrace that looked out upon the garden.", "그리고 그녀가 혼자 있다는 사실에 만족하며, 오늘 오겠다고 약속한 적도 없고 그녀가 경마 전에 자기가 오리라고는 분명 기대하지도 않았으니 그녀를 놀래 주고 싶은 마음에, 그는 칼을 손에 쥐고 꽃이 가장자리에 늘어선 모랫길을 조심스레 밟으며 정원이 내다보이는 테라스를 향해 걸어갔다."], ["Vronsky forgot now all that he had thought on the way of the hardships and difficulties of their position.", "브론스키는 이제 자기들 처지의 고난과 어려움에 대해 오는 길에 생각했던 것을 모조리 잊어버렸다."], ["He thought of nothing but that he would see her directly, not in imagination, but living, all of her, as she was in reality.", "그는 다만 곧 그녀를 만나리라는 것, 상상 속에서가 아니라 살아 있는 그녀를, 현실 속의 모습 그대로 온전히 보게 되리라는 것만을 생각했다."], ["He was just going in, stepping on his whole foot so as not to creak, up the worn steps of the terrace, when he suddenly remembered what he always forgot, and what caused the most torturing side of his relations with her, her son with his questioning—hostile, as he fancied—eyes.", "그가 삐걱거리는 소리를 내지 않으려고 발바닥을 온전히 디디며 테라스의 닳은 계단을 올라 막 안으로 들어가려는 순간, 그는 늘 잊고 있다가 문득 떠올리곤 하던 것, 그녀와의 관계에서 가장 고통스러운 측면을 자아내는 것을 별안간 떠올렸다. 그것은 그녀의 아들, 그 무언가를 캐묻는 듯한—그가 보기에는 적대적인—눈빛이었다."]], [["This boy was more often than anyone else a check upon their freedom.", "이 소년은 그 누구보다도 자주 그들의 자유에 제동을 거는 존재였다."], ["When he was present, both Vronsky and Anna did not merely avoid speaking of anything that they could not have repeated before everyone; they did not even allow themselves to refer by hints to anything the boy did not understand.", "아이가 곁에 있을 때면, 브론스키와 안나는 모든 사람 앞에서 되풀이할 수 없는 어떤 것에 대해 말하기를 피하는 정도가 아니라, 아이가 이해하지 못하는 어떤 것을 넌지시 암시하는 것조차 스스로에게 허락하지 않았다."], ["They had made no agreement about this, it had settled itself.", "그들은 이에 대해 아무런 약속도 한 적이 없었으나, 그것은 저절로 그렇게 정해졌다."], ["They would have felt it wounding themselves to deceive the child.", "그들은 아이를 속이는 것이 곧 자기 자신에게 상처를 입히는 일처럼 느껴졌을 것이다."], ["In his presence they talked like acquaintances.", "아이 앞에서 그들은 지인처럼 이야기했다."], ["But in spite of this caution, Vronsky often saw the child’s intent, bewildered glance fixed upon him, and a strange shyness, uncertainty, at one time friendliness, at another, coldness and reserve, in the boy’s manner to him; as though the child felt that between this man and his mother there existed some important bond, the significance of which he could not understand.", "그러나 이런 조심에도 불구하고, 브론스키는 종종 아이의 진지하고 어리둥절한 시선이 자기에게 고정되어 있는 것을, 그리고 그를 대하는 아이의 태도에서 어떤 이상한 수줍음과 망설임, 어느 때는 다정함, 또 어느 때는 차가움과 거리감을 보았다. 마치 이 남자와 어머니 사이에 어떤 중대한 끈이 존재하는데 그 의미를 이해할 수 없다고 아이가 느끼기라도 하는 것 같았다."]], [["As a fact, the boy did feel that he could not understand this relation, and he tried painfully, and was not able to make clear to himself what feeling he ought to have for this man.", "사실 아이는 이 관계를 이해할 수 없다고 느꼈고, 이 남자에게 어떤 감정을 품어야 하는지 괴롭도록 애써 보았으나 스스로에게 분명히 밝힐 수 없었다."], ["With a child’s keen instinct for every manifestation of feeling, he saw distinctly that his father, his governess, his nurse,—all did not merely dislike Vronsky, but looked on him with horror and aversion, though they never said anything about him, while his mother looked on him as her greatest friend.", "모든 감정의 발현에 대한 아이 특유의 예민한 본능으로, 아이는 자기 아버지와 가정교사와 유모가—그들 모두가 브론스키를 그저 싫어하는 정도가 아니라, 비록 그에 대해 결코 아무 말도 하지 않았지만, 공포와 혐오의 눈으로 바라보는 것을, 반면 어머니는 그를 더없이 친한 벗으로 여기는 것을 또렷이 보았다."]], [["“What does it mean?", "\"이게 무슨 뜻일까?"], ["Who is he?", "저 사람은 누구지?"], ["How ought I to love him?", "나는 저 사람을 어떻게 사랑해야 하는 거지?"], ["If I don’t know, it’s my fault; either I’m stupid or a naughty boy,”", "내가 모른다면 그건 내 잘못이야. 내가 멍청하거나 못된 아이인 거야.\""], ["thought the child.", "아이는 그렇게 생각했다."], ["And this was what caused his dubious, inquiring, sometimes hostile, expression, and the shyness and uncertainty which Vronsky found so irksome.", "그리고 바로 이것이 아이의 미심쩍고 캐묻는 듯한, 때로는 적대적인 표정과, 브론스키가 그토록 거북하게 여기던 수줍음과 망설임을 자아낸 것이었다."], ["This child’s presence always and infallibly called up in Vronsky that strange feeling of inexplicable loathing which he had experienced of late.", "이 아이의 존재는 언제나 어김없이 브론스키 안에, 최근 들어 그가 겪어 온 그 이상한, 설명할 수 없는 혐오의 감정을 불러일으켰다."], ["This child’s presence called up both in Vronsky and in Anna a feeling akin to the feeling of a sailor who sees by the compass that the direction in which he is swiftly moving is far from the right one, but that to arrest his motion is not in his power, that every instant is carrying him further and further away, and that to admit to himself his deviation from the right direction is the same as admitting his certain ruin.", "이 아이의 존재는 브론스키와 안나 두 사람 안에, 나침반을 보고서 자기가 빠르게 나아가고 있는 방향이 옳은 방향과는 한참 어긋나 있음을 알면서도 그 움직임을 멈추는 것은 자기 힘으로 어쩔 수 없고, 매 순간이 자기를 점점 더 멀리 데려가고 있으며, 옳은 방향에서 벗어났음을 스스로 인정하는 것은 곧 자신의 확실한 파멸을 인정하는 것과 같다는 사실을 깨달은 뱃사람의 심정과 비슷한 감정을 불러일으켰다."]], [["This child, with his innocent outlook upon life, was the compass that showed them the point to which they had departed from what they knew, but did not want to know.", "이 아이는, 삶에 대한 그 순진한 시선으로, 그들이 알고 있으면서도 알고 싶어 하지 않는 그 지점에서 자기들이 얼마나 멀어졌는지를 가리켜 보이는 나침반이었다."]], [["This time Seryozha was not at home, and she was completely alone.", "이번에는 세료자가 집에 없었고, 그녀는 완전히 혼자였다."], ["She was sitting on the terrace waiting for the return of her son, who had gone out for his walk and been caught in the rain.", "그녀는 산책을 나갔다가 비를 만난 아들이 돌아오기를 기다리며 테라스에 앉아 있었다."], ["She had sent a manservant and a maid out to look for him.", "그녀는 하인 하나와 하녀 하나를 아이를 찾으러 내보낸 터였다."], ["Dressed in a white gown, deeply embroidered, she was sitting in a corner of the terrace behind some flowers, and did not hear him.", "정교하게 수를 놓은 흰 가운을 입고, 그녀는 테라스 한구석 꽃들 뒤에 앉아 있어 그의 기척을 듣지 못했다."], ["Bending her curly black head, she pressed her forehead against a cool watering pot that stood on the parapet, and both her lovely hands, with the rings he knew so well, clasped the pot.", "곱슬곱슬한 검은 머리를 숙인 채, 그녀는 난간 위에 놓인 차가운 물뿌리개에 이마를 댄 채로, 그가 너무도 잘 아는 반지들을 낀 두 아름다운 손으로 그 물뿌리개를 감싸 쥐고 있었다."], ["The beauty of her whole figure, her head, her neck, her hands, struck Vronsky every time as something new and unexpected.", "그녀의 온몸과 머리와 목과 손이 자아내는 아름다움은 매번 브론스키에게 무언가 새롭고 뜻밖의 것으로 다가왔다."], ["He stood still, gazing at her in ecstasy.", "그는 황홀경에 빠져 그녀를 바라보며 가만히 서 있었다."], ["But, directly he would have made a step to come nearer to her, she was aware of his presence, pushed away the watering pot, and turned her flushed face towards him.", "그러나 그가 그녀에게 한 걸음 더 다가서려는 바로 그 순간, 그녀는 그의 존재를 알아채고 물뿌리개를 밀어내며 발그레한 얼굴을 그에게로 돌렸다."]], [["“What’s the matter?", "\"무슨 일이오?"], ["You are ill?”", "어디가 아프오?\""], ["he said to her in French, going up to her.", "그가 다가가며 프랑스어로 말했다."], ["He would have run to her, but remembering that there might be spectators, he looked round towards the balcony door, and reddened a little, as he always reddened, feeling that he had to be afraid and be on his guard.", "그는 그녀에게 달려가고 싶었으나, 보는 눈이 있을지도 모른다는 생각에 발코니 문 쪽을 돌아보고는, 늘 그렇듯 두려워하고 경계해야 한다는 것을 느끼며 살짝 얼굴을 붉혔다."]], [["“No, I’m quite well,”", "\"아뇨, 아주 멀쩡해요.\""], ["she said, getting up and pressing his outstretched hand tightly.", "그녀가 일어서서 그가 내민 손을 꼭 쥐며 말했다."], ["“I did not expect ...", "\"당신이 올 줄은……"], ["thee.”", "몰랐어요.\""]], [["“Mercy!", "\"이런!"], ["what cold hands!”", "손이 어쩜 이렇게 차오!\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“You startled me,”", "\"당신 때문에 놀랐잖아요.\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["she said.", "\"혼자서 세료자를 기다리고 있었어요."], ["“I’m alone, and expecting Seryozha; he’s out for a walk; they’ll come in from this side.”", "산책을 나갔거든요. 이쪽으로 들어올 거예요.\""]], [["But, in spite of her efforts to be calm, her lips were quivering.", "그러나 침착해지려는 노력에도 불구하고, 그녀의 입술은 떨리고 있었다."]], [["“Forgive me for coming, but I couldn’t pass the day without seeing you,”", "\"이렇게 찾아와서 미안하오. 하지만 당신을 보지 못하고는 하루를 보낼 수가 없었소.\""], ["he went on, speaking French, as he always did to avoid using the stiff Russian plural form, so impossibly frigid between them, and the dangerously intimate singular.", "그는 늘 그러듯 프랑스어로 계속 말했다. 두 사람 사이에서는 도저히 견딜 수 없을 만큼 쌀쌀맞게 들리는 딱딱한 러시아어 복수형과, 위험할 만큼 다정한 단수형을 쓰지 않기 위해서였다."]], [["“Forgive you?", "\"미안하다뇨?"], ["I’m so glad!”", "난 정말 기쁜걸요!\""]], [["“But you’re ill or worried,”", "\"하지만 당신은 아프거나 무슨 걱정이 있는 거요.\""], ["he went on, not letting go her hands and bending over her.", "그가 그녀의 손을 놓지 않고 몸을 숙이며 말을 이었다."], ["“What were you thinking of?”", "\"무슨 생각을 하고 있었소?\""]], [["“Always the same thing,”", "\"늘 같은 거죠.\""], ["she said, with a smile.", "그녀가 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["She spoke the truth.", "그녀는 진실을 말한 것이었다."], ["If ever at any moment she had been asked what she was thinking of, she could have answered truly: of the same thing, of her happiness and her unhappiness.", "어느 순간이든 무슨 생각을 하고 있느냐는 질문을 받았다면, 그녀는 진실되게 대답할 수 있었을 것이다. 같은 것, 자신의 행복과 자신의 불행에 대해서라고."], ["She was thinking, just when he came upon her, of this: why was it, she wondered, that to others, to Betsy (she knew of her secret connection with Tushkevitch) it was all easy, while to her it was such torture?", "그가 불쑥 나타났을 때 그녀가 생각하고 있던 것은 바로 이것이었다. 어째서 다른 사람들에게는, 베치(그녀는 베치가 투시케비치와 은밀히 관계를 맺고 있다는 것을 알고 있었다)에게는 이 모든 것이 그토록 쉬운데, 자기에게는 이렇게나 고통스러운 것일까."], ["Today this thought gained special poignancy from certain other considerations.", "오늘은 어떤 다른 정황들 때문에 이 생각이 특히나 가슴을 후벼 팠다."], ["She asked him about the races.", "그녀는 그에게 경마에 대해 물었다."], ["He answered her questions, and, seeing that she was agitated, trying to calm her, he began telling her in the simplest tone the details of his preparations for the races.", "그는 그녀의 물음에 답했고, 그녀가 동요하고 있는 것을 보고는, 그녀를 진정시키려고 더없이 담담한 어조로 경마를 위한 자신의 준비 과정을 자세히 이야기하기 시작했다."]], [["“Tell him or not tell him?”", "'말할까, 말까?'"], ["she thought, looking into his quiet, affectionate eyes.", "그녀는 그의 고요하고 다정한 눈을 들여다보며 생각했다."], ["“He is so happy, so absorbed in his races that he won’t understand as he ought, he won’t understand all the gravity of this fact to us.”", "'저이는 너무나 행복해하고, 너무나 경마에 빠져 있어서, 마땅히 이해해야 할 만큼 이해하지 못할 거야. 이 사실이 우리에게 얼마나 중대한 일인지 깨닫지 못할 거야.'"]], [["“But you haven’t told me what you were thinking of when I came in,”", "\"그런데 당신은 내가 들어왔을 때 무슨 생각을 하고 있었는지 아직 말해 주지 않았소.\""], ["he said, interrupting his narrative; “please tell me!”", "그가 자기 이야기를 끊으며 말했다. \"제발 말해 주오!\""]], [["She did not answer, and, bending her head a little, she looked inquiringly at him from under her brows, her eyes shining under their long lashes.", "그녀는 대답하지 않고 고개를 약간 숙인 채, 긴 속눈썹 아래 빛나는 눈으로 눈썹 밑에서 미심쩍게 그를 바라보았다."], ["Her hand shook as it played with a leaf she had picked.", "그녀가 따 든 잎사귀를 만지작거리는 손이 떨렸다."], ["He saw it, and his face expressed that utter subjection, that slavish devotion, which had done so much to win her.", "그는 그것을 보았고, 그의 얼굴에는 그녀의 마음을 사로잡는 데 그토록 큰 몫을 한 그 온전한 복종, 그 노예 같은 헌신이 떠올랐다."]], [["“I see something has happened.", "\"무슨 일이 있었군."], ["Do you suppose I can be at peace, knowing you have a trouble I am not sharing?", "당신이 나와 나누지 않는 어떤 괴로움을 안고 있는데, 내가 마음 편할 수 있을 것 같소?"], ["Tell me, for God’s sake,”", "제발, 부디 말해 주오.\""], ["he repeated imploringly.", "그가 애원하듯 거듭 말했다."]], [["“Yes, I shan’t be able to forgive him if he does not realize all the gravity of it.", "'그래, 저이가 이 일의 중대함을 모조리 깨닫지 못한다면 난 저이를 용서할 수 없을 거야. 차라리 말하지 말까."], ["Better not tell; why put him to the proof?”", "어째서 저이를 시험에 들게 하는 거지?'"], ["she thought, still staring at him in the same way, and feeling the hand that held the leaf was trembling more and more.", "그녀는 여전히 같은 눈빛으로 그를 응시하며, 잎사귀를 쥔 손이 점점 더 떨리는 것을 느끼면서 생각했다."]], [["“For God’s sake!”", "\"제발 부탁이오!\""], ["he repeated, taking her hand.", "그가 그녀의 손을 잡으며 거듭 말했다."]], [["“Shall I tell you?”", "\"말해 드릴까요?\""]], [["“Yes, yes, yes....”", "\"그래, 그래요, 어서……\""]], [["“I’m with child,”", "\"나 아이를 가졌어요.\""], ["she said, softly and deliberately.", "그녀가 부드럽고 또박또박 말했다."], ["The leaf in her hand shook more violently, but she did not take her eyes off him, watching how he would take it.", "그녀의 손에 든 잎사귀가 더 격하게 떨렸으나, 그녀는 그가 이 말을 어떻게 받아들이는지 지켜보며 그에게서 눈을 떼지 않았다."], ["He turned white, would have said something, but stopped; he dropped her hand, and his head sank on his breast.", "그는 새파랗게 질려 무어라 말하려다가 멈추었고, 그녀의 손을 놓더니 고개를 가슴께로 떨구었다."], ["“Yes, he realizes all the gravity of it,”", "'그래, 저이는 이 일의 중대함을 모조리 깨달은 거야.'"], ["she thought, and gratefully she pressed his hand.", "그녀는 생각하며 고맙다는 듯 그의 손을 꼭 쥐었다."]], [["But she was mistaken in thinking he realized the gravity of the fact as she, a woman, realized it.", "그러나 그가 여자인 그녀가 깨달은 것처럼 이 사실의 중대함을 깨달았으리라고 생각한 것은 그녀의 착각이었다."], ["On hearing it, he felt come upon him with tenfold intensity that strange feeling of loathing of someone.", "그 말을 듣자, 그는 누군가를 향한 그 이상한 혐오의 감정이 열 배의 강도로 자기에게 밀려드는 것을 느꼈다."], ["But at the same time, he felt that the turning-point he had been longing for had come now; that it was impossible to go on concealing things from her husband, and it was inevitable in one way or another that they should soon put an end to their unnatural position.", "그러나 동시에, 그가 그토록 갈망해 온 전환점이 이제 찾아왔다는 것을, 그녀의 남편에게 일들을 계속 숨기는 것은 불가능하며, 어떤 식으로든 머지않아 이 부자연스러운 처지에 종지부를 찍어야 한다는 것이 불가피하다는 것을 느꼈다."], ["But, besides that, her emotion physically affected him in the same way.", "게다가 그것 말고도, 그녀의 격정은 똑같은 방식으로 그에게도 육체적인 영향을 미쳤다."], ["He looked at her with a look of submissive tenderness, kissed her hand, got up, and, in silence, paced up and down the terrace.", "그는 순종적인 다정함이 담긴 눈길로 그녀를 바라보고는, 그녀의 손에 입을 맞추고 일어나 말없이 테라스를 이리저리 거닐었다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그래.\""], ["he said, going up to her resolutely.", "그가 단호하게 그녀에게 다가서며 말했다."], ["“Neither you nor I have looked on our relations as a passing amusement, and now our fate is sealed.", "\"당신도 나도 우리 관계를 한때의 유희로 여긴 적이 없소. 이제 우리의 운명은 정해졌소."], ["It is absolutely necessary to put an end”—he looked round as he spoke—“to the deception in which we are living.”", "우리가 살아가고 있는 이 기만에\"—그는 말하면서 주위를 둘러보았다—\"반드시 종지부를 찍어야만 하오.\""]], [["“Put an end?", "\"종지부를 찍는다고요?"], ["How put an end, Alexey?”", "어떻게 종지부를 찍죠, 알렉세이?\""], ["she said softly.", "그녀가 부드럽게 말했다."]], [["She was calmer now, and her face lighted up with a tender smile.", "그녀는 이제 한결 차분해져, 그녀의 얼굴이 다정한 미소로 환해졌다."]], [["“Leave your husband and make our life one.”", "\"당신 남편을 떠나서 우리의 삶을 하나로 합칩시다.\""]], [["“It is one as it is,”", "\"지금 이대로도 하나인걸요.\""], ["she answered, scarcely audibly.", "그녀가 거의 들리지 않을 만큼 작게 대답했다."]], [["“Yes, but altogether; altogether.”", "\"그래, 하지만 완전히 말이오. 완전히.\""]], [["“But how, Alexey, tell me how?”", "\"하지만 어떻게요, 알렉세이, 어떻게 하면 되는지 말해 줘요.\""], ["she said in melancholy mockery at the hopelessness of her own position.", "그녀가 자기 처지의 가망 없음을 향한 우울한 조소를 담아 말했다."], ["“Is there any way out of such a position?", "\"이런 처지에서 빠져나갈 길이 있긴 한가요?"], ["Am I not the wife of my husband?”", "난 내 남편의 아내가 아닌가요?\""]], [["“There is a way out of every position.", "\"어떤 처지에서든 빠져나갈 길은 있소."], ["We must take our line,”", "우리는 우리의 길을 택해야 하오.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“Anything’s better than the position in which you’re living.", "\"당신이 지금 살아가고 있는 처지보다는 무엇이든 더 나을 거요."], ["Of course, I see how you torture yourself over everything—the world and your son and your husband.”", "물론 나도 당신이 온갖 것을 두고 스스로를 괴롭히고 있다는 걸 알고 있소. 세상이며, 당신 아들이며, 당신 남편이며.\""]], [["“Oh, not over my husband,”", "\"오, 남편을 두고는 아니에요.\""], ["she said, with a quiet smile.", "그녀가 조용한 미소를 띠며 말했다."], ["“I don’t know him, I don’t think of him.", "\"난 그 사람을 몰라요. 그 사람 생각은 하지 않아요."], ["He doesn’t exist.”", "그 사람은 존재하지 않아요.\""]], [["“You’re not speaking sincerely.", "\"당신은 진심으로 말하는 게 아니오."], ["I know you.", "난 당신을 알아."], ["You worry about him too.”", "당신은 그 사람도 걱정하고 있소.\""]], [["“Oh, he doesn’t even know,”", "\"오, 그이는 알지도 못하는걸요.\""], ["she said, and suddenly a hot flush came over her face; her cheeks, her brow, her neck crimsoned, and tears of shame came into her eyes.", "그녀가 말했고, 별안간 그녀의 얼굴에 뜨거운 홍조가 번졌다. 그녀의 뺨과 이마와 목이 새빨개졌고, 수치의 눈물이 그녀의 눈에 차올랐다."], ["“But we won’t talk of him.”", "\"하지만 그 사람 이야기는 하지 말아요.\""]], [["# Chapter 23", "# Chapter 23"]], [["Vronsky had several times already, though not so resolutely as now, tried to bring her to consider their position, and every time he had been confronted by the same superficiality and triviality with which she met his appeal now.", "브론스키는 이미 여러 번, 지금처럼 단호하게는 아니었으나, 그녀에게 두 사람의 처지를 직시하도록 만들려고 애써 왔다. 그러나 그때마다 그는 지금 그녀가 자신의 호소를 받아넘기는 것과 똑같은 피상성과 경박함에 부딪혀 왔다."], ["It was as though there were something in this which she could not or would not face, as though directly she began to speak of this, she, the real Anna, retreated somehow into herself, and another strange and unaccountable woman came out, whom he did not love, and whom he feared, and who was in opposition to him.", "마치 거기에는 그녀가 마주할 수 없거나 마주하려 하지 않는 무언가가 있는 것 같았고, 그녀가 이 문제를 입에 올리기 시작하면 진짜 안나는 어쩐 일인지 자기 안으로 물러나 버리고, 그가 사랑하지 않으며 두려워하고 또 그에게 맞서는, 어딘가 낯설고 알 수 없는 다른 여자가 나오는 것만 같았다."], ["But today he was resolved to have it out.", "그러나 오늘 그는 끝장을 보기로 작정하고 있었다."]], [["“Whether he knows or not,”", "\"그가 알든 모르든,\""], ["said Vronsky, in his usual quiet and resolute tone, “that’s nothing to do with us.", "브론스키는 평소의 차분하고 단호한 어조로 말했다. \"그건 우리와는 상관없는 일이오."], ["We cannot ...", "우리는……"], ["you cannot stay like this, especially now.”", "당신은 이런 식으로 지낼 수 없소, 특히 지금은.\""]], [["“What’s to be done, according to you?”", "\"그럼 당신 생각에는 어떻게 해야 하죠?\""], ["she asked with the same frivolous irony.", "그녀는 여전히 경박한 빈정거림을 담아 물었다."], ["She who had so feared he would take her condition too lightly was now vexed with him for deducing from it the necessity of taking some step.", "그가 자기 처지를 너무 가볍게 받아들일까 봐 그토록 두려워했던 그녀가, 이제는 그가 거기서 어떤 조치를 취해야 한다는 결론을 끌어낸다고 그에게 짜증을 부리고 있었다."]], [["“Tell him everything, and leave him.”", "\"그에게 전부 털어놓고, 그를 떠나시오.\""]], [["“Very well, let us suppose I do that,”", "\"좋아요, 내가 그렇게 한다고 쳐요,\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“Do you know what the result of that would be?", "\"그러면 그 결과가 어떻게 될지 아세요?"], ["I can tell you it all beforehand,”", "내가 미리 전부 말해 줄 수 있어요.\""], ["and a wicked light gleamed in her eyes, that had been so soft a minute before.", "그러자 조금 전까지만 해도 그토록 부드럽던 그녀의 눈에 사악한 빛이 번뜩였다."], ["“‘Eh, you love another man, and have entered into criminal intrigues with him?’”", "\"'아, 당신은 다른 남자를 사랑해서 그와 죄스러운 밀회를 맺었단 말이지?'\""], ["(Mimicking her husband, she threw an emphasis on the word “criminal,” as Alexey Alexandrovitch did.) “‘I warned you of the results in the religious, the civil, and the domestic relation.", "(남편을 흉내 내며 그녀는 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 하던 것처럼 '죄스러운'이라는 말에 힘을 주었다.) \"'나는 종교적으로나, 시민적으로나, 가정적으로나 그 결과를 당신에게 경고했소."], ["You have not listened to me.", "당신은 내 말을 듣지 않았소."], ["Now I cannot let you disgrace my name,—’” “and my son,”", "이제 나는 당신이 내 이름을 더럽히도록 둘 수 없소—'\""], ["she had meant to say, but about her son she could not jest,—“‘disgrace my name, and’—and more in the same style,”", "'그리고 내 아들을'이라고 그녀는 말하려 했지만, 아들에 대해서는 농담할 수가 없었다—\"'내 이름을 더럽히게 둘 수 없고, 또—' 뭐 이런 식으로 더 이어지겠죠.\""], ["she added.", "그녀가 덧붙였다."], ["“In general terms, he’ll say in his official manner, and with all distinctness and precision, that he cannot let me go, but will take all measures in his power to prevent scandal.", "\"한마디로, 그는 그 관청식 말투로, 더할 나위 없이 또렷하고 정확하게, 나를 보내 줄 수 없으며 추문을 막기 위해 자기 권한 안에서 할 수 있는 모든 조치를 취하겠다고 말할 거예요."], ["And he will calmly and punctually act in accordance with his words.", "그러고는 자기가 한 말 그대로 침착하고 빈틈없이 행동하겠죠."], ["That’s what will happen.", "그게 벌어질 일이에요."], ["He’s not a man, but a machine, and a spiteful machine when he’s angry,”", "그는 사람이 아니라 기계예요, 화가 나면 심술궂은 기계라고요.\""], ["she added, recalling Alexey Alexandrovitch as she spoke, with all the peculiarities of his figure and manner of speaking, and reckoning against him every defect she could find in him, softening nothing for the great wrong she herself was doing him.", "그녀는 이렇게 말하면서 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 몸매며 말투의 온갖 특이한 점까지 죄다 떠올렸고, 자기가 그에게 저지르고 있는 크나큰 잘못은 조금도 누그러뜨리지 않은 채, 그에게서 찾아낼 수 있는 결점이란 결점은 모조리 그의 탓으로 헤아렸다."]], [["“But, Anna,”", "\"하지만 안나,\""], ["said Vronsky, in a soft and persuasive voice, trying to soothe her, “we absolutely must, anyway, tell him, and then be guided by the line he takes.”", "브론스키가 그녀를 달래려고 부드럽고 설득하는 목소리로 말했다. \"어쨌든 우리는 무슨 일이 있어도 그에게 말해야 하고, 그런 다음 그가 취하는 태도에 따라 움직여야 하오.\""]], [["“What, run away?”", "\"뭐라고요, 달아나자고요?\""]], [["“And why not run away?", "\"달아나면 왜 안 되오?"], ["I don’t see how we can keep on like this.", "나는 우리가 이런 식으로 계속할 수 있다고 보지 않소."], ["And not for my sake—I see that you suffer.”", "나 자신을 위해서가 아니오—나는 당신이 괴로워하는 걸 보고 있단 말이오.\""]], [["“Yes, run away, and become your mistress,”", "\"그래요, 달아나서 당신의 정부가 되라는 거군요.\""], ["she said angrily.", "그녀가 화를 내며 말했다."]], [["“Anna,”", "\"안나.\""], ["he said, with reproachful tenderness.", "그가 책망하는 듯한 다정함을 담아 말했다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그래요,\""], ["she went on, “become your mistress, and complete the ruin of....”", "그녀가 계속했다. \"당신의 정부가 되고, 그리고 완전히 망쳐 버리는 거죠…….\""]], [["Again she would have said “my son,” but she could not utter that word.", "또다시 그녀는 '내 아들을'이라고 말하려 했지만, 그 말을 입 밖에 낼 수가 없었다."]], [["Vronsky could not understand how she, with her strong and truthful nature, could endure this state of deceit, and not long to get out of it.", "브론스키는 그녀처럼 강하고 진실한 천성을 지닌 여자가 어떻게 이런 기만의 상태를 견디면서 거기서 벗어나기를 갈망하지 않을 수 있는지 이해할 수가 없었다."], ["But he did not suspect that the chief cause of it was the word—son, which she could not bring herself to pronounce.", "그러나 그는 그 주된 원인이 그녀가 차마 입에 올리지 못하는 말—아들이라는 단어에 있다는 것을 짐작하지 못했다."], ["When she thought of her son, and his future attitude to his mother, who had abandoned his father, she felt such terror at what she had done, that she could not face it; but, like a woman, could only try to comfort herself with lying assurances that everything would remain as it always had been, and that it was possible to forget the fearful question of how it would be with her son.", "자기 아들을, 그리고 아버지를 버린 어머니에 대한 아들의 장차의 태도를 떠올릴 때면, 그녀는 자기가 저지른 일에 너무도 큰 공포를 느껴 그것을 마주할 수 없었다. 다만 여자답게, 모든 것이 늘 그래 왔던 그대로 남아 있으리라는, 그리고 아들이 어떻게 될 것인가 하는 무서운 물음을 잊는 것이 가능하리라는 거짓된 다짐으로 스스로를 위로하려 애쓸 뿐이었다."]], [["“I beg you, I entreat you,”", "\"부탁이에요, 간청할게요,\""], ["she said suddenly, taking his hand, and speaking in quite a different tone, sincere and tender, “never speak to me of that!”", "그녀가 그의 손을 잡고 사뭇 다른, 진실하고 다정한 어조로 갑자기 말했다. \"절대로 내게 그 얘기는 하지 말아 줘요!\""]], [["“But, Anna....”", "\"하지만 안나…….\""]], [["“Never.", "\"절대로요."], ["Leave it to me.", "그건 내게 맡겨 둬요."], ["I know all the baseness, all the horror of my position; but it’s not so easy to arrange as you think.", "나는 내 처지의 온갖 비열함과 온갖 끔찍함을 다 알아요. 하지만 그건 당신이 생각하는 것처럼 그렇게 쉽게 정리되는 게 아니에요."], ["And leave it to me, and do what I say.", "그러니 내게 맡기고, 내가 말하는 대로 해 줘요."], ["Never speak to me of it.", "절대로 그 얘기는 내게 하지 말아요."], ["Do you promise me?...", "약속해 주겠어요?……"], ["No, no, promise!...”", "아니, 아니, 약속해요!……\""]], [["“I promise everything, but I can’t be at peace, especially after what you have told me.", "\"무엇이든 약속하겠소. 하지만 나는 마음이 편할 수가 없소, 특히 당신이 방금 내게 한 말을 듣고는."], ["I can’t be at peace, when you can’t be at peace....”", "당신이 편치 못한데 내가 어떻게 편할 수 있겠소…….\""]], [["“I?”", "\"내가요?\""], ["she repeated.", "그녀가 되물었다."], ["“Yes, I am worried sometimes; but that will pass, if you will never talk about this.", "\"그래요, 가끔은 나도 괴로워요. 하지만 당신이 이 얘기를 절대 꺼내지 않는다면 그건 지나갈 거예요."], ["When you talk about it—it’s only then it worries me.”", "당신이 그 얘기를 할 때—오직 그럴 때만 내가 괴로워지는 거니까요.\""]], [["“I don’t understand,”", "\"이해할 수가 없군.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“I know,”", "\"알아요,\""], ["she interrupted him, “how hard it is for your truthful nature to lie, and I grieve for you.", "그녀가 그의 말을 가로막았다. \"당신의 진실한 천성으로는 거짓말하는 게 얼마나 힘든지를요."], ["I often think that you have ruined your whole life for me.”", "그래서 나는 당신이 가여워요. 나는 당신이 나 때문에 당신의 인생 전부를 망쳐 버렸다고 자주 생각해요.\""]], [["“I was just thinking the very same thing,”", "\"나도 방금 똑같은 생각을 하고 있었소,\" 그가 말했다."], ["he said; “how could you sacrifice everything for my sake?", "\"어떻게 당신이 나를 위해 모든 것을 희생할 수 있단 말이오?"], ["I can’t forgive myself that you’re unhappy!”", "당신이 불행하다는 게 나는 나 자신을 용서할 수가 없소!\""]], [["“I unhappy?”", "\"내가 불행하다고요?\""], ["she said, coming closer to him, and looking at him with an ecstatic smile of love.", "그녀가 그에게 더 가까이 다가오며, 사랑의 황홀한 미소를 띠고 그를 바라보며 말했다."], ["“I am like a hungry man who has been given food.", "\"나는 굶주리다가 음식을 받은 사람 같아요."], ["He may be cold, and dressed in rags, and ashamed, but he is not unhappy.", "그는 추위에 떨고, 누더기를 걸치고, 부끄러울지 몰라도, 불행하진 않아요. 내가 불행하다고요?"], ["I unhappy? No, this is my unhappiness....”", "아니에요, 이게 바로 나의 불행이라면…….\""]], [["She could hear the sound of her son’s voice coming towards them, and glancing swiftly round the terrace, she got up impulsively.", "자기 쪽으로 다가오는 아들의 목소리가 들려오자, 그녀는 테라스를 재빨리 둘러보고는 충동적으로 일어섰다."], ["Her eyes glowed with the fire he knew so well; with a rapid movement she raised her lovely hands, covered with rings, took his head, looked a long look into his face, and, putting up her face with smiling, parted lips, swiftly kissed his mouth and both eyes, and pushed him away.", "그녀의 눈은 그가 너무도 잘 아는 그 불꽃으로 타올랐다. 그녀는 재빠른 동작으로 반지를 잔뜩 낀 그 사랑스러운 두 손을 들어 올려 그의 머리를 감싸 쥐고, 그의 얼굴을 오래도록 들여다보다가, 미소를 띤 채 살짝 벌어진 입술로 얼굴을 들어 올려, 잽싸게 그의 입과 두 눈에 입을 맞추고는 그를 밀어냈다."], ["She would have gone, but he held her back.", "그대로 가려 했지만 그가 그녀를 붙들었다."]], [["“When?”", "\"언제?\""], ["he murmured in a whisper, gazing in ecstasy at her.", "그가 황홀하게 그녀를 바라보며 속삭이듯 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Tonight, at one o’clock,”", "\"오늘 밤, 한 시에요.\""], ["she whispered, and, with a heavy sigh, she walked with her light, swift step to meet her son.", "그녀가 속삭였다. 그러고는 무겁게 한숨을 내쉬며, 가볍고 빠른 걸음으로 아들을 맞으러 걸어갔다."]], [["Seryozha had been caught by the rain in the big garden, and he and his nurse had taken shelter in an arbor.", "세료자는 큰 정원에서 비를 만나, 유모와 함께 정자에 몸을 피하고 있었다."]], [["“Well, au revoir,”", "\"그럼, au revoir.\""], ["she said to Vronsky.", "그녀가 브론스키에게 말했다."], ["“I must soon be getting ready for the races.", "\"곧 경마 갈 채비를 해야 해요. 벳시가 데리러 오기로 했거든요.\""], ["Betsy promised to fetch me.”", "브론스키는 회중시계를 보고는 황급히 자리를 떴다."]], [["Vronsky, looking at his watch, went away hurriedly.", "* au revoir: 프랑스어로 '안녕히 가세요, 또 봐요'."]], [["# Chapter 24", "# Chapter 24"]], [["When Vronsky looked at his watch on the Karenins’ balcony, he was so greatly agitated and lost in his thoughts that he saw the figures on the watch’s face, but could not take in what time it was.", "브론스키가 카레닌 집의 발코니에서 시계를 보았을 때, 그는 너무나 흥분한 데다 생각에 잠겨 있어서 문자판의 숫자는 눈에 들어왔으나 몇 시인지 도무지 헤아릴 수가 없었다."], ["He came out on to the highroad and walked, picking his way carefully through the mud, to his carriage.", "그는 큰길로 나와 진흙탕을 조심조심 골라 디디며 마차 쪽으로 걸어갔다."], ["He was so completely absorbed in his feeling for Anna, that he did not even think what o’clock it was, and whether he had time to go to Bryansky’s.", "안나에 대한 자기 감정에 온통 빠져 있던 터라, 지금이 몇 시인지, 브랸스키네 집에 갈 시간이 있는지조차 생각하지 못했다."], ["He had left him, as often happens, only the external faculty of memory, that points out each step one has to take, one after the other.", "흔히 그러하듯, 그에게 남은 것은 기억의 외적 능력뿐이어서, 그것이 다음에 내디뎌야 할 발걸음을 하나씩 하나씩 일러줄 따름이었다."], ["He went up to his coachman, who was dozing on the box in the shadow, already lengthening, of a thick limetree; he admired the shifting clouds of midges circling over the hot horses, and, waking the coachman, he jumped into the carriage, and told him to drive to Bryansky’s.", "그는 벌써 길게 늘어진 그늘 아래, 우거진 보리수 그늘 속 마부석에서 졸고 있는 마부에게 다가갔다. 그는 더운 말들 위를 맴도는 깔따구 떼의 변화무쌍한 구름을 흐뭇하게 바라보다가, 마부를 깨워 마차에 뛰어올라서는 브랸스키네 집으로 가자고 일렀다."], ["It was only after driving nearly five miles that he had sufficiently recovered himself to look at his watch, and realize that it was half-past five, and he was late.", "거의 오 베르스타쯤 달리고 나서야 그는 충분히 정신을 차려 시계를 보았고, 시각이 다섯 시 반이며 자기가 늦었다는 것을 깨달았다."]], [["There were several races fixed for that day: the Mounted Guards’ race, then the officers’ mile-and-a-half race, then the three-mile race, and then the race for which he was entered.", "그날은 경마가 여러 차례 예정되어 있었다. 기마 친위대 경주, 그다음에는 장교들의 일 마일 반 경주, 그다음에는 삼 마일 경주, 그리고 그가 출전하는 경주였다."], ["He could still be in time for his race, but if he went to Bryansky’s he could only just be in time, and he would arrive when the whole of the court would be in their places.", "자기 경주에는 아직 늦지 않을 수 있었지만, 브랸스키네 집에 들렀다가는 간신히 시간을 맞출 뿐이고, 도착할 무렵이면 궁정 사람들은 모두 제자리에 앉아 있을 터였다."], ["That would be a pity.", "그건 유감스러운 일이었다."], ["But he had promised Bryansky to come, and so he decided to drive on, telling the coachman not to spare the horses.", "그러나 그는 브랸스키에게 가겠다고 약속했던 터라, 그대로 마차를 몰기로 하고 마부에게 말을 아끼지 말라고 일렀다."]], [["He reached Bryansky’s, spent five minutes there, and galloped back.", "그는 브랸스키네 집에 도착해 거기서 오 분을 보내고 다시 달려 돌아왔다."], ["This rapid drive calmed him.", "이 빠른 질주가 그를 진정시켰다."], ["All that was painful in his relations with Anna, all the feeling of indefiniteness left by their conversation, had slipped out of his mind.", "안나와의 관계에서 괴로웠던 모든 것, 두 사람의 대화가 남긴 막막한 감정이 죄다 그의 마음에서 빠져나가 버렸다."], ["He was thinking now with pleasure and excitement of the race, of his being anyhow, in time, and now and then the thought of the blissful interview awaiting him that night flashed across his imagination like a flaming light.", "그는 이제 즐거움과 흥분 속에서 경주에 대해, 어쨌든 늦지 않게 도착하리라는 데 대해 생각했고, 이따금 그날 밤 자기를 기다리는 더없이 행복한 밀회의 상상이 타오르는 불빛처럼 머릿속을 스쳐 지나갔다."]], [["The excitement of the approaching race gained upon him as he drove further and further into the atmosphere of the races, overtaking carriages driving up from the summer villas or out of Petersburg.", "다가오는 경주의 흥분이 점점 그를 사로잡았다. 그가 별장지에서, 또는 페테르부르크에서 올라오는 마차들을 앞질러 가며 경마장의 분위기 속으로 자꾸만 깊이 들어갈수록 그러했다."]], [["At his quarters no one was left at home; all were at the races, and his valet was looking out for him at the gate.", "그의 거처에는 아무도 남아 있지 않았다. 다들 경마장에 가 있었고, 하인만 대문에서 그를 기다리고 있었다."], ["While he was changing his clothes, his valet told him that the second race had begun already, that a lot of gentlemen had been to ask for him, and a boy had twice run up from the stables.", "그가 옷을 갈아입는 동안, 하인은 두 번째 경주가 벌써 시작되었으며, 여러 신사분이 그를 찾아왔었고, 마구간에서 소년이 두 번이나 달려왔다고 일러주었다."], ["Dressing without hurry (he never hurried himself, and never lost his self-possession), Vronsky drove to the sheds.", "서두르지 않고 옷을 갈아입은 (그는 결코 서두르는 법이 없었고, 침착함을 잃는 법도 없었다) 브론스키는 마차를 몰아 마구간으로 향했다."], ["From the sheds he could see a perfect sea of carriages, and people on foot, soldiers surrounding the race course, and pavilions swarming with people.", "마구간에서는 마차들의 망망한 바다며, 도보로 온 사람들이며, 경마장을 둘러싼 병사들이며, 사람들로 우글거리는 관람석이 보였다."], ["The second race was apparently going on, for just as he went into the sheds he heard a bell ringing.", "두 번째 경주가 진행 중인 모양으로, 그가 마구간으로 들어서는 바로 그 순간 종소리가 울리는 게 들렸다."], ["Going towards the stable, he met the white-legged chestnut, Mahotin’s Gladiator, being led to the race-course in a blue forage horsecloth, with what looked like huge ears edged with blue.", "마구간 쪽으로 가다가, 그는 푸른 사료용 마포를 두르고 푸른 테를 두른 거대한 귀처럼 보이는 것을 단 채 경마장으로 끌려가는, 흰 다리의 밤색 말 마호틴의 글라디아토르와 마주쳤다."]], [["“Where’s Cord?”", "\"코드는 어디 있나?\""], ["he asked the stable-boy.", "그가 마구간지기 소년에게 물었다."]], [["“In the stable, putting on the saddle.”", "\"마구간에서 안장을 얹고 있습니다.\""]], [["In the open horse-box stood Frou-Frou, saddled ready.", "열린 마방 안에는 안장을 다 채운 프루프루가 서 있었다."], ["They were just going to lead her out.", "막 끌어내려는 참이었다."]], [["“I’m not too late?”", "\"내가 너무 늦진 않았나?\""]], [["“All right!", "\"괜찮습니다!"], ["All right!”", "괜찮아요!\""], ["said the Englishman; “don’t upset yourself!”", "영국인이 말했다. \"마음 졸이지 마십시오!\""]], [["Vronsky once more took in in one glance the exquisite lines of his favorite mare; who was quivering all over, and with an effort he tore himself from the sight of her, and went out of the stable.", "브론스키는 온몸을 부르르 떨고 있는 자기 애마의 절묘한 선을 한 번 더 한눈에 담았고, 애써 그 모습에서 시선을 떼어내고는 마구간을 나섰다."], ["He went towards the pavilions at the most favorable moment for escaping attention.", "그는 주목을 피하기에 더없이 적당한 순간에 관람석 쪽으로 갔다."], ["The mile-and-a-half race was just finishing, and all eyes were fixed on the horse-guard in front and the light hussar behind, urging their horses on with a last effort close to the winning post.", "일 마일 반 경주가 막 끝나가던 참이라, 모든 눈은 결승점 가까이에서 마지막 안간힘으로 말을 몰아대는, 앞선 기마 친위병과 그 뒤의 가벼운 경기병에게 쏠려 있었다."], ["From the center and outside of the ring all were crowding to the winning post, and a group of soldiers and officers of the horse-guards were shouting loudly their delight at the expected triumph of their officer and comrade.", "트랙 안쪽과 바깥쪽에서 사람들이 모조리 결승점으로 몰려들었고, 한 무리의 기마 친위대 병사들과 장교들이 자기네 장교이자 동료의 승리를 기대하며 환희에 차서 큰 소리로 외쳐대고 있었다."], ["Vronsky moved into the middle of the crowd unnoticed, almost at the very moment when the bell rang at the finish of the race, and the tall, mudspattered horse-guard who came in first, bending over the saddle, let go the reins of his panting gray horse that looked dark with sweat.", "브론스키는 경주의 종료를 알리는 종이 울린 바로 그 순간, 키가 크고 진흙을 뒤집어쓴 채 일등으로 들어온 기마 친위병이 안장 위로 몸을 굽히며, 땀에 젖어 거뭇해 보이는 헐떡이는 잿빛 말의 고삐를 늦추는 그 순간에 거의 맞춰, 사람들 사이로 눈에 띄지 않게 끼어들었다."]], [["The horse, stiffening out its legs, with an effort stopped its rapid course, and the officer of the horse-guards looked round him like a man waking up from a heavy sleep, and just managed to smile.", "말은 다리를 뻣뻣이 뻗으며 안간힘으로 그 빠른 질주를 멈추었고, 기마 친위대 장교는 깊은 잠에서 깨어나는 사람처럼 주위를 둘러보더니 간신히 미소를 지었다."], ["A crowd of friends and outsiders pressed round him.", "친구들과 구경꾼들이 그를 빙 둘러쌌다."]], [["Vronsky intentionally avoided that select crowd of the upper world, which was moving and talking with discreet freedom before the pavilions.", "브론스키는 관람석 앞에서 점잖게, 자유로이 거닐며 이야기를 나누는 그 상류 사회의 선택받은 무리를 일부러 피했다."], ["He knew that Madame Karenina was there, and Betsy, and his brother’s wife, and he purposely did not go near them for fear of something distracting his attention.", "그는 그곳에 카레니나 부인이, 또 벳시가, 그리고 형수가 있다는 것을 알았으나, 무언가에 주의를 빼앗길까 두려워 일부러 그들에게 가까이 가지 않았다."], ["But he was continually met and stopped by acquaintances, who told him about the previous races, and kept asking him why he was so late.", "그러나 그는 끊임없이 아는 사람들과 마주쳐 발이 묶였고, 그들은 그에게 앞서의 경주들에 관해 이야기하며 왜 그렇게 늦었느냐고 자꾸 물었다."]], [["At the time when the racers had to go to the pavilion to receive the prizes, and all attention was directed to that point, Vronsky’s elder brother, Alexander, a colonel with heavy fringed epaulets, came up to him.", "기수들이 상을 받으러 관람석으로 가야 할 때가 되어 모든 시선이 그쪽으로 쏠렸을 무렵, 브론스키의 형 알렉산드르가 다가왔다. 묵직한 술 장식을 단 견장을 단 대령이었다."], ["He was not tall, though as broadly built as Alexey, and handsomer and rosier than he; he had a red nose, and an open, drunken-looking face.", "그는 키가 크지는 않았으나 알렉세이만큼 어깨가 떡 벌어졌고, 동생보다 더 잘생기고 혈색이 좋았다. 코가 붉었고, 트인 데다 술에 취한 듯한 얼굴이었다."]], [["“Did you get my note?”", "\"내 쪽지 받았나?\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“There’s never any finding you.”", "\"도무지 너를 찾을 수가 있어야지.\""]], [["Alexander Vronsky, in spite of the dissolute life, and in especial the drunken habits, for which he was notorious, was quite one of the court circle.", "알렉산드르 브론스키는 방탕한 생활, 특히 그가 악명을 떨치던 술버릇에도 불구하고 어엿한 궁정 사람의 한 명이었다."]], [["Now, as he talked to his brother of a matter bound to be exceedingly disagreeable to him, knowing that the eyes of many people might be fixed upon him, he kept a smiling countenance, as though he were jesting with his brother about something of little moment.", "지금 그는 동생에게 몹시 불쾌할 게 분명한 일을 이야기하면서, 여러 사람의 눈이 자기에게 쏠려 있을지 모른다는 것을 알았기에, 마치 동생과 사소한 일로 농담이라도 주고받는 듯 줄곧 웃는 얼굴을 유지했다."]], [["“I got it, and I really can’t make out what you are worrying yourself about,”", "\"받았어요. 그런데 형이 대체 뭘 그렇게 안달하시는지 정말 모르겠네요.\""], ["said Alexey.", "알렉세이가 말했다."]], [["“I’m worrying myself because the remark has just been made to me that you weren’t here, and that you were seen in Peterhof on Monday.”", "\"내가 안달하는 건 말이다, 방금 누가 나한테 네가 여기 없었다고, 월요일에 페테르고프에서 너를 봤다고 말했기 때문이야.\""]], [["“There are matters which only concern those directly interested in them, and the matter you are so worried about is....”", "\"오직 직접 관련된 당사자들만 상관할 일들이 있는 법이고, 형이 그렇게 걱정하는 그 일도……\""]], [["“Yes, but if so, you may as well cut the service....”", "\"그래, 하지만 그렇다면 차라리 군복무를 그만두는 게 낫겠지……\""]], [["“I beg you not to meddle, and that’s all I have to say.”", "\"제발 참견하지 마시기를 부탁드립니다. 제가 할 말은 그뿐이에요.\""]], [["Alexey Vronsky’s frowning face turned white, and his prominent lower jaw quivered, which happened rarely with him.", "알렉세이 브론스키의 찌푸린 얼굴이 하얗게 질렸고, 좀처럼 없는 일이지만 튀어나온 아래턱이 떨렸다."], ["Being a man of very warm heart, he was seldom angry; but when he was angry, and when his chin quivered, then, as Alexander Vronsky knew, he was dangerous.", "그는 마음이 몹시 따뜻한 사람이라 좀체 화를 내지 않았으나, 일단 화가 나서 턱이 떨릴 때면, 알렉산드르 브론스키도 알다시피, 위험한 사람이었다."], ["Alexander Vronsky smiled gaily.", "알렉산드르 브론스키는 명랑하게 웃었다."]], [["“I only wanted to give you Mother’s letter.", "\"난 그저 어머니 편지를 전해주려던 것뿐이야."], ["Answer it, and don’t worry about anything just before the race.", "답장이나 하고, 경주를 코앞에 두고서 아무 걱정도 하지 마라."], ["Bonne chance,”", "Bonne chance.\""], ["he added, smiling and he moved away from him.", "그는 웃으며 이렇게 덧붙이고는 동생에게서 멀어졌다."], ["But after him another friendly greeting brought Vronsky to a standstill.", "그러나 그가 떠나자마자 또 다른 친밀한 인사가 브론스키의 발걸음을 멈춰 세웠다."]], [["“So you won’t recognize your friends!", "* Bonne chance: 프랑스어로 '행운을 빈다'. \"친구도 못 알아보는군!"], ["How are you, mon cher?”", "안녕한가, mon cher?\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, as conspicuously brilliant in the midst of all the Petersburg brilliance as he was in Moscow, his face rosy, and his whiskers sleek and glossy.", "스테판 아르카지치가 말했다. 그는 모스크바에서 그랬듯이 페테르부르크의 온갖 화려함 한가운데서도 유난히 눈부셨고, 얼굴은 발그레했으며, 구레나룻은 매끈하게 윤이 났다."], ["“I came up yesterday, and I’m delighted that I shall see your triumph.", "\"어제 올라왔는데, 자네의 승리를 보게 되어 기쁘이."], ["When shall we meet?”", "우리 언제 만날까?\""]], [["“Come tomorrow to the messroom,”", "* mon cher: 프랑스어로 '여보게', '친애하는 친구'."], ["said Vronsky, and squeezing him by the sleeve of his coat, with apologies, he moved away to the center of the race course, where the horses were being led for the great steeplechase.", "\"내일 장교 식당으로 오게.\" 브론스키는 이렇게 말하고는 미안하다며 그의 외투 소매를 한번 꼭 쥐고서, 대장애물 경주를 위해 말들이 끌려 나오고 있는 경마장 한복판으로 걸어갔다."]], [["The horses who had run in the last race were being led home, steaming and exhausted, by the stable-boys, and one after another the fresh horses for the coming race made their appearance, for the most part English racers, wearing horsecloths, and looking with their drawn-up bellies like strange, huge birds.", "지난 경주에서 달린 말들은 김을 내뿜으며 지쳐 마구간지기 소년들에게 이끌려 돌아가고 있었고, 다가오는 경주에 나설 싱싱한 말들이 하나둘 모습을 드러냈다. 대부분 마포를 두른 영국산 경주마였고, 홀쭉하게 당겨진 배 때문에 기이하고 거대한 새처럼 보였다."], ["On the right was led in Frou-Frou, lean and beautiful, lifting up her elastic, rather long pasterns, as though moved by springs.", "오른편으로 프루프루가 끌려 들어왔다. 야위었으나 아름다웠고, 마치 용수철에 떠밀리듯 탄력 있고 다소 긴 발목을 들어 올리고 있었다."], ["Not far from her they were taking the rug off the lop-eared Gladiator.", "그 멀지 않은 곳에서 사람들이 늘어진 귀의 글라디아토르에게서 마포를 벗기고 있었다."], ["The strong, exquisite, perfectly correct lines of the stallion, with his superb hind-quarters and excessively short pasterns almost over his hoofs, attracted Vronsky’s attention in spite of himself.", "굵직하고 절묘하며 더할 나위 없이 반듯한 수말의 선, 훌륭한 뒷다리와 발굽에 거의 닿을 만큼 지나치게 짧은 발목이 본의 아니게 브론스키의 시선을 끌었다."], ["He would have gone up to his mare, but he was again detained by an acquaintance.", "그는 자기 애마에게 다가가려 했으나, 또다시 아는 사람에게 붙들렸다."]], [["“Oh, there’s Karenin!”", "\"오, 저기 카레닌이 있군!\""], ["said the acquaintance with whom he was chatting.", "그와 잡담을 나누던 지인이 말했다."], ["“He’s looking for his wife, and she’s in the middle of the pavilion.", "\"아내를 찾는 모양인데, 부인은 관람석 한가운데 있어."], ["Didn’t you see her?”", "자네는 못 봤나?\""]], [["“No,”", "\"못 봤네.\""], ["answered Vronsky, and without even glancing round towards the pavilion where his friend was pointing out Madame Karenina, he went up to his mare.", "브론스키는 이렇게 대답하고는, 친구가 카레니나 부인을 가리키는 관람석 쪽으로 눈길 한 번 돌리지 않은 채 자기 애마에게 다가갔다."]], [["Vronsky had not had time to look at the saddle, about which he had to give some direction, when the competitors were summoned to the pavilion to receive their numbers and places in the row at starting.", "브론스키가 무언가 지시를 내려야 할 안장을 미처 살펴볼 새도 없이, 출전자들에게 번호와 출발선의 자리를 받으러 관람석으로 오라는 호출이 떨어졌다."], ["Seventeen officers, looking serious and severe, many with pale faces, met together in the pavilion and drew the numbers.", "진지하고 엄숙한 표정의, 더러는 창백한 얼굴을 한 열일곱 명의 장교가 관람석에 모여 번호를 뽑았다."], ["Vronsky drew the number seven.", "브론스키는 칠 번을 뽑았다."], ["The cry was heard: “Mount!”", "\"탑승!\" 하는 외침이 들렸다."]], [["Feeling that with the others riding in the race, he was the center upon which all eyes were fastened, Vronsky walked up to his mare in that state of nervous tension in which he usually became deliberate and composed in his movements.", "다른 출전자들과 더불어 자기가 모든 시선이 쏠리는 중심이라는 것을 느끼며, 브론스키는 평소 신경이 곤두설 때면 으레 그러하듯 동작이 한결 신중하고 침착해지는 그 상태로 자기 애마에게 걸어갔다."], ["Cord, in honor of the races, had put on his best clothes, a black coat buttoned up, a stiffly starched collar, which propped up his cheeks, a round black hat, and top boots.", "코드는 경주를 기리는 뜻에서 가장 좋은 옷을 차려입었다. 단추를 끝까지 채운 검은 외투, 빳빳하게 풀 먹여 양 볼을 떠받치는 깃, 둥근 검은 모자, 그리고 목이 긴 장화였다."], ["He was calm and dignified as ever, and was with his own hands holding Frou-Frou by both reins, standing straight in front of her.", "그는 여느 때처럼 차분하고 위엄 있게, 프루프루 앞에 똑바로 서서 두 손으로 양쪽 고삐를 잡고 있었다."], ["Frou-Frou was still trembling as though in a fever.", "프루프루는 여전히 열병에 걸린 듯 떨고 있었다."], ["Her eye, full of fire, glanced sideways at Vronsky.", "불꽃이 가득 담긴 그 눈이 곁눈질로 브론스키를 흘낏 보았다."], ["Vronsky slipped his finger under the saddle-girth.", "브론스키는 손가락을 뱃대끈 밑으로 밀어 넣었다."], ["The mare glanced aslant at him, drew up her lip, and twitched her ear.", "암말이 그를 비스듬히 흘겨보더니 입술을 추켜올리고 귀를 쫑긋거렸다."], ["The Englishman puckered up his lips, intending to indicate a smile that anyone should verify his saddling.", "영국인은 누군가 자기가 채운 안장을 검사하더라도 좋다는 뜻으로 미소를 지으려는 양 입술을 오므렸다."]], [["“Get up; you won’t feel so excited.”", "\"올라타십시오. 그러면 그렇게 흥분되지 않으실 겁니다.\""]], [["Vronsky looked round for the last time at his rivals.", "브론스키는 마지막으로 경쟁자들을 둘러보았다."], ["He knew that he would not see them during the race.", "경주 중에는 그들을 보지 못하리라는 것을 알았다."], ["Two were already riding forward to the point from which they were to start.", "둘은 벌써 출발 지점을 향해 말을 몰고 나아가고 있었다."], ["Galtsin, a friend of Vronsky’s and one of his more formidable rivals, was moving round a bay horse that would not let him mount.", "브론스키의 친구이자 더 만만찮은 경쟁자 중 하나인 갈친은 도무지 자기를 태워주지 않으려는 밤색 말 주위를 빙빙 돌고 있었다."], ["A little light hussar in tight riding breeches rode off at a gallop, crouched up like a cat on the saddle, in imitation of English jockeys.", "꽉 끼는 승마 바지를 입은 자그마한 경기병 하나가, 영국 기수를 흉내 내어 고양이처럼 안장 위에 잔뜩 웅크린 채 질주해 나갔다."], ["Prince Kuzovlev sat with a white face on his thoroughbred mare from the Grabovsky stud, while an English groom led her by the bridle.", "쿠조블레프 공작은 그라보프스키 종마장의 순종 암말 위에 하얗게 질린 얼굴로 앉아 있었고, 영국인 마부가 굴레를 잡고 끌어주고 있었다."], ["Vronsky and all his comrades knew Kuzovlev and his peculiarity of “weak nerves” and terrible vanity.", "브론스키와 그의 동료들은 모두 쿠조블레프와 그 특유의 \"약한 신경\"과 끔찍한 허영심을 알고 있었다."], ["They knew that he was afraid of everything, afraid of riding a spirited horse.", "그들은 그가 모든 것을 두려워하며, 사나운 말을 타는 것을 두려워한다는 것을 알았다."], ["But now, just because it was terrible, because people broke their necks, and there was a doctor standing at each obstacle, and an ambulance with a cross on it, and a sister of mercy, he had made up his mind to take part in the race.", "그러나 지금 그는, 바로 그것이 무시무시하기 때문에, 사람들이 목이 부러지고, 장애물마다 의사가 서 있고, 십자가 표시가 된 구급마차와 자비의 수녀가 있기 때문에, 경주에 참가하기로 마음먹은 것이었다."], ["Their eyes met, and Vronsky gave him a friendly and encouraging nod.", "두 사람의 눈이 마주쳤고, 브론스키는 다정하게 격려하는 뜻으로 그에게 고개를 끄덕여 보였다."], ["Only one he did not see, his chief rival, Mahotin on Gladiator.", "다만 한 사람, 그의 가장 큰 경쟁자인 글라디아토르를 탄 마호틴만은 보지 못했다."]], [["“Don’t be in a hurry,” said Cord to Vronsky, “and remember one thing: don’t hold her in at the fences, and don’t urge her on; let her go as she likes.”", "\"서두르지 마십시오.\" 코드가 브론스키에게 말했다. \"그리고 한 가지만 기억하세요. 울타리 앞에서 말을 잡아당기지 말고, 몰아붙이지도 마십시오. 제멋대로 가도록 놓아두세요.\""]], [["“All right, all right,”", "\"알았네, 알았어.\""], ["said Vronsky, taking the reins.", "브론스키는 고삐를 잡으며 말했다."]], [["“If you can, lead the race; but don’t lose heart till the last minute, even if you’re behind.”", "\"가능하면 선두에서 달리십시오. 하지만 뒤처지더라도 마지막 순간까지 낙심하지 마세요.\""]], [["Before the mare had time to move, Vronsky stepped with an agile, vigorous movement into the steel-toothed stirrup, and lightly and firmly seated himself on the creaking leather of the saddle.", "암말이 채 움직일 새도 없이, 브론스키는 날렵하고 힘찬 동작으로 톱니 달린 강철 등자에 발을 디뎠고, 삐걱거리는 가죽 안장 위에 가뿐하면서도 단단하게 올라앉았다."], ["Getting his right foot in the stirrup, he smoothed the double reins, as he always did, between his fingers, and Cord let go.", "오른발을 등자에 끼우고는 늘 하던 대로 손가락 사이로 이중 고삐를 매만졌고, 코드가 손을 놓았다."]], [["As though she did not know which foot to put first, Frou-Frou started, dragging at the reins with her long neck, and as though she were on springs, shaking her rider from side to side.", "마치 어느 발을 먼저 내디뎌야 할지 모르는 듯 프루프루는 긴 목으로 고삐를 잡아당기며 출발했고, 마치 용수철 위에 있는 것처럼 제 기수를 이쪽저쪽으로 흔들어댔다."], ["Cord quickened his step, following him.", "코드는 걸음을 빨리하여 그 뒤를 따랐다."], ["The excited mare, trying to shake off her rider first on one side and then the other, pulled at the reins, and Vronsky tried in vain with voice and hand to soothe her.", "흥분한 암말은 먼저 한쪽으로, 다음엔 다른 쪽으로 기수를 떨쳐내려 애쓰며 고삐를 잡아당겼고, 브론스키는 목소리와 손으로 달래보았으나 헛일이었다."]], [["They were just reaching the dammed-up stream on their way to the starting point.", "그들은 출발 지점으로 가는 길에 둑을 쌓아 막은 개울에 막 다다르고 있었다."], ["Several of the riders were in front and several behind, when suddenly Vronsky heard the sound of a horse galloping in the mud behind him, and he was overtaken by Mahotin on his white-legged, lop-eared Gladiator.", "기수 몇은 앞에, 몇은 뒤에 있었는데, 그때 별안간 브론스키는 자기 뒤에서 진흙탕을 박차며 질주하는 말발굽 소리를 들었고, 흰 다리에 늘어진 귀의 글라디아토르를 탄 마호틴에게 추월당했다."], ["Mahotin smiled, showing his long teeth, but Vronsky looked angrily at him.", "마호틴은 긴 이를 드러내며 웃었으나, 브론스키는 화난 눈으로 그를 바라보았다."], ["He did not like him, and regarded him now as his most formidable rival.", "그는 그를 좋아하지 않았고, 지금은 가장 만만찮은 경쟁자로 여기고 있었다."], ["He was angry with him for galloping past and exciting his mare.", "그는 마호틴이 곁을 스쳐 질주하며 제 암말을 흥분시킨 데 화가 났다."], ["Frou-Frou started into a gallop, her left foot forward, made two bounds, and fretting at the tightened reins, passed into a jolting trot, bumping her rider up and down.", "프루프루는 왼발을 앞세워 질주로 들어서더니, 두 번 껑충 뛰고는 팽팽해진 고삐에 안달하며 덜컹거리는 속보로 옮겨 가, 기수를 위아래로 들썩거리게 했다."], ["Cord, too, scowled, and followed Vronsky almost at a trot.", "코드도 얼굴을 찌푸리며 거의 속보에 가까운 걸음으로 브론스키를 뒤따랐다."]], [["# Chapter 25", "# Chapter 25"]], [["There were seventeen officers in all riding in this race.", "이 경주에는 모두 열일곱 명의 장교가 참가했다."], ["The race course was a large three-mile ring of the form of an ellipse in front of the pavilion.", "경주로는 관람석 앞에 펼쳐진, 타원형으로 둘레가 삼 마일에 이르는 커다란 코스였다."], ["On this course nine obstacles had been arranged: the stream, a big and solid barrier five feet high, just before the pavilion, a dry ditch, a ditch full of water, a precipitous slope, an Irish barricade (one of the most difficult obstacles, consisting of a mound fenced with brushwood, beyond which was a ditch out of sight for the horses, so that the horse had to clear both obstacles or might be killed); then two more ditches filled with water, and one dry one; and the end of the race was just facing the pavilion.", "이 코스에는 아홉 개의 장애물이 마련되어 있었다. 시냇물, 관람석 바로 앞에 놓인 높이 오 피트의 크고 견고한 장벽, 마른 도랑, 물이 가득 찬 도랑, 가파른 비탈, 아일랜드식 방벽(가장 까다로운 장애물 가운데 하나로, 잡목으로 둘러싼 둔덕 뒤에 말의 눈에는 보이지 않는 도랑이 있어서, 말이 두 장애물을 한꺼번에 뛰어넘지 못하면 죽을 수도 있었다), 그다음 물이 가득 찬 도랑 두 개와 마른 도랑 하나가 더 있었고, 경주의 종착점은 바로 관람석 정면이었다."], ["But the race began not in the ring, but two hundred yards away from it, and in that part of the course was the first obstacle, a dammed-up stream, seven feet in breadth, which the racers could leap or wade through as they preferred.", "그러나 경주는 그 둘레에서가 아니라 거기서 이백 야드 떨어진 곳에서 시작되었고, 코스의 그 부분에 첫 번째 장애물, 즉 너비 칠 피트의 둑을 쌓아 만든 시냇물이 있었는데, 경주자들은 그것을 뛰어넘든 헤치고 건너든 마음대로 택할 수 있었다."]], [["Three times they were ranged ready to start, but each time some horse thrust itself out of line, and they had to begin again.", "세 번이나 출발하려고 줄을 맞춰 섰지만, 그때마다 어떤 말이 대열에서 비어져 나와 다시 시작해야 했다."], ["The umpire who was starting them, Colonel Sestrin, was beginning to lose his temper, when at last for the fourth time he shouted “Away!” and the racers started.", "출발 신호를 맡은 심판인 세스트린 대령은 슬슬 화를 내기 시작했고, 마침내 네 번째에 \"출발!\"이라고 외치자 경주자들이 내달렸다."]], [["Every eye, every opera-glass, was turned on the brightly colored group of riders at the moment they were in line to start.", "그들이 출발선에 늘어선 순간, 모든 눈과 모든 오페라글라스가 화려한 색깔의 기수 무리에게로 향했다."]], [["“They’re off!", "\"출발했어!"], ["They’re starting!”", "시작했어!\""], ["was heard on all sides after the hush of expectation.", "숨죽인 기대의 정적이 흐른 뒤, 사방에서 이런 소리가 들렸다."]], [["And little groups and solitary figures among the public began running from place to place to get a better view.", "그리고 관중 가운데 작은 무리들과 외따로 선 사람들이 더 잘 보려고 이곳저곳으로 뛰어다니기 시작했다."], ["In the very first minute the close group of horsemen drew out, and it could be seen that they were approaching the stream in twos and threes and one behind another.", "첫 순간에 빽빽이 모여 있던 기수 무리가 흩어졌고, 그들이 두셋씩 무리를 짓거나 한 사람씩 앞뒤로 늘어선 채 시냇물로 다가가는 것이 보였다."], ["To the spectators it seemed as though they had all started simultaneously, but to the racers there were seconds of difference that had great value to them.", "구경꾼들에게는 그들이 모두 동시에 출발한 것처럼 보였지만, 경주자들에게는 그들에게 큰 의미가 있는 몇 초의 차이가 있었다."]], [["Frou-Frou, excited and over-nervous, had lost the first moment, and several horses had started before her, but before reaching the stream, Vronsky, who was holding in the mare with all his force as she tugged at the bridle, easily overtook three, and there were left in front of him Mahotin’s chestnut Gladiator, whose hind-quarters were moving lightly and rhythmically up and down exactly in front of Vronsky, and in front of all, the dainty mare Diana bearing Kuzovlev more dead than alive.", "흥분한 데다 지나치게 예민해진 프루프루는 첫 순간을 놓쳤고, 몇 마리의 말이 그녀보다 앞서 출발했지만, 시냇물에 이르기 전에 브론스키는 고삐를 잡아당기는 암말을 온 힘을 다해 제어하면서 어렵지 않게 세 마리를 따라잡았고, 그의 앞에는 마호친의 밤색 글라디아토르—그 뒷다리가 브론스키 바로 앞에서 가볍고 율동적으로 오르내렸다—와, 모두의 맨 앞에 거의 죽은 사람처럼 늘어진 쿠조블레프를 태운 우아한 암말 디아나만 남았다."]], [["For the first instant Vronsky was not master either of himself or his mare.", "처음 한순간 브론스키는 자기 자신도 암말도 다스리지 못했다."], ["Up to the first obstacle, the stream, he could not guide the motions of his mare.", "첫 번째 장애물인 시냇물에 이르기까지 그는 암말의 움직임을 이끌 수가 없었다."]], [["Gladiator and Diana came up to it together and almost at the same instant; simultaneously they rose above the stream and flew across to the other side; Frou-Frou darted after them, as if flying; but at the very moment when Vronsky felt himself in the air, he suddenly saw almost under his mare’s hoofs Kuzovlev, who was floundering with Diana on the further side of the stream.", "글라디아토르와 디아나가 거의 같은 순간에 함께 그곳에 다다랐다. 동시에 그들은 시냇물 위로 솟구쳐 건너편으로 날아갔다."], ["(Kuzovlev had let go the reins as he took the leap, and the mare had sent him flying over her head.) Those details Vronsky learned later; at the moment all he saw was that just under him, where Frou-Frou must alight, Diana’s legs or head might be in the way.", "프루프루도 날아오르듯 그 뒤를 좇아 뛰어들었다. 그러나 브론스키가 공중에 떠 있음을 느낀 바로 그 순간, 그는 자기 암말의 발굽 거의 바로 아래에서 디아나와 함께 시냇물 건너편에서 허우적거리는 쿠조블레프를 문득 보았다(쿠조블레프는 도약하는 순간 고삐를 놓쳤고, 암말이 그를 머리 너머로 내동댕이쳤다)."], ["But Frou-Frou drew up her legs and back in the very act of leaping, like a falling cat, and, clearing the other mare, alighted beyond her.", "그 세세한 정황은 나중에 알게 된 것이고, 그 순간 그가 본 것이라곤 바로 자기 아래, 프루프루가 내려앉아야 할 그곳에 디아나의 다리나 머리가 걸리적거릴지도 모른다는 것뿐이었다. 그러나 프루프루는 떨어지는 고양이처럼 도약하는 바로 그 동작 속에서 다리와 등을 끌어당겼고, 다른 암말을 넘어 그녀 너머에 내려앉았다."]], [["“O the darling!”", "'오, 내 사랑!'"], ["thought Vronsky.", "브론스키는 생각했다."]], [["After crossing the stream Vronsky had complete control of his mare, and began holding her in, intending to cross the great barrier behind Mahotin, and to try to overtake him in the clear ground of about five hundred yards that followed it.", "시냇물을 건넌 뒤 브론스키는 암말을 완전히 장악했고, 마호친 뒤를 따라 그 큰 장벽을 넘은 다음 그 뒤로 이어지는 오백 야드가량의 탁 트인 땅에서 그를 따라잡을 작정으로 암말을 제어하기 시작했다."]], [["The great barrier stood just in front of the imperial pavilion.", "그 큰 장벽은 황제의 관람석 바로 앞에 서 있었다."], ["The Tsar and the whole court and crowds of people were all gazing at them—at him, and Mahotin a length ahead of him, as they drew near the “devil,” as the solid barrier was called.", "그 견고한 장벽은 '악마'라고 불렸는데, 그들이—그러니까 그와, 그보다 말 한 마리 길이만큼 앞선 마호친이—그 '악마'로 다가갈 때, 황제와 온 궁정 사람들과 군중이 모두 그들을 바라보고 있었다."], ["Vronsky was aware of those eyes fastened upon him from all sides, but he saw nothing except the ears and neck of his own mare, the ground racing to meet him, and the back and white legs of Gladiator beating time swiftly before him, and keeping always the same distance ahead.", "브론스키는 사방에서 자기에게 쏠린 그 눈길들을 의식했지만, 자기 암말의 귀와 목, 자기를 향해 마주 달려오는 땅, 그리고 그의 앞에서 빠르게 박자를 맞추며 늘 같은 거리를 유지하는 글라디아토르의 등과 흰 다리 말고는 아무것도 보이지 않았다."], ["Gladiator rose, with no sound of knocking against anything.", "글라디아토르가 무엇엔가 부딪치는 소리 하나 없이 솟구쳤다."], ["With a wave of his short tail he disappeared from Vronsky’s sight.", "짧은 꼬리를 한 번 흔들더니 브론스키의 시야에서 사라졌다."]], [["“Bravo!”", "\"브라보!\""], ["cried a voice.", "한 목소리가 외쳤다."]], [["At the same instant, under Vronsky’s eyes, right before him flashed the palings of the barrier.", "같은 순간, 브론스키의 눈앞 바로 그 앞에서 장벽의 말뚝들이 번쩍였다."], ["Without the slightest change in her action his mare flew over it; the palings vanished, and he heard only a crash behind him.", "그의 암말은 동작에 조금의 변화도 없이 그 위로 날아올랐다. 말뚝들이 사라졌고, 그는 그저 뒤에서 무언가 부딪치는 소리만 들었다."], ["The mare, excited by Gladiator’s keeping ahead, had risen too soon before the barrier, and grazed it with her hind hoofs.", "글라디아토르가 앞서 가는 것에 흥분한 암말이 장벽 앞에서 너무 일찍 솟구치는 바람에 뒷발굽으로 그것을 스쳤던 것이다."], ["But her pace never changed, and Vronsky, feeling a spatter of mud in his face, realized that he was once more the same distance from Gladiator.", "그러나 그녀의 속도는 조금도 변하지 않았고, 브론스키는 얼굴에 진흙이 튀는 것을 느끼며 자기가 다시 글라디아토르와 같은 거리에 있음을 깨달았다."], ["Once more he perceived in front of him the same back and short tail, and again the same swiftly moving white legs that got no further away.", "그는 다시 자기 앞에서 똑같은 등과 짧은 꼬리를, 그리고 다시 멀어지지 않는 똑같은 흰 다리가 빠르게 움직이는 것을 보았다."]], [["At the very moment when Vronsky thought that now was the time to overtake Mahotin, Frou-Frou herself, understanding his thoughts, without any incitement on his part, gained ground considerably, and began getting alongside of Mahotin on the most favorable side, close to the inner cord.", "이제 마호친을 따라잡을 때라고 브론스키가 생각한 바로 그 순간, 프루프루 자신이 그의 생각을 알아채고는 그가 부추기지도 않았는데 상당히 거리를 좁혀, 가장 유리한 쪽, 즉 안쪽 줄에 바짝 붙어 마호친과 나란히 가기 시작했다."], ["Mahotin would not let her pass that side.", "마호친은 그쪽으로 그녀를 지나가게 두지 않으려 했다."], ["Vronsky had hardly formed the thought that he could perhaps pass on the outer side, when Frou-Frou shifted her pace and began overtaking him on the other side.", "어쩌면 바깥쪽으로 지나갈 수 있겠다는 생각이 브론스키의 머릿속에 채 떠오르기도 전에, 프루프루가 보폭을 바꾸어 다른 쪽으로 그를 추월하기 시작했다."], ["Frou-Frou’s shoulder, beginning by now to be dark with sweat, was even with Gladiator’s back.", "이제 땀으로 거뭇해지기 시작한 프루프루의 어깨가 글라디아토르의 등과 나란해졌다."], ["For a few lengths they moved evenly.", "그들은 몇 마리 길이만큼 나란히 달렸다."], ["But before the obstacle they were approaching, Vronsky began working at the reins, anxious to avoid having to take the outer circle, and swiftly passed Mahotin just upon the declivity.", "그러나 다가오는 장애물 앞에서 브론스키는 바깥쪽으로 도는 것을 피하려고 마음 졸이며 고삐를 다루기 시작했고, 바로 그 비탈에서 재빨리 마호친을 앞질렀다."], ["He caught a glimpse of his mud-stained face as he flashed by.", "그는 휙 지나가면서 진흙투성이가 된 그의 얼굴을 언뜻 보았다."], ["He even fancied that he smiled.", "심지어 그가 미소 짓는 것처럼 느껴지기도 했다."], ["Vronsky passed Mahotin, but he was immediately aware of him close upon him, and he never ceased hearing the even-thudding hoofs and the rapid and still quite fresh breathing of Gladiator.", "브론스키는 마호친을 앞질렀지만, 곧바로 그가 자기 바로 뒤에 바짝 붙어 있음을 의식했고, 글라디아토르의 고른 발굽 소리와 빠르고도 여전히 제법 생생한 숨소리가 끊임없이 들렸다."]], [["The next two obstacles, the water course and the barrier, were easily crossed, but Vronsky began to hear the snorting and thud of Gladiator closer upon him.", "다음 두 장애물, 수로와 장벽은 어렵지 않게 넘었지만, 브론스키는 글라디아토르의 콧김 소리와 발굽 소리가 더 가까이 들리기 시작했다."], ["He urged on his mare, and to his delight felt that she easily quickened her pace, and the thud of Gladiator’s hoofs was again heard at the same distance away.", "그가 암말을 재촉하자, 기쁘게도 그녀가 어렵지 않게 속도를 높이는 것이 느껴졌고, 글라디아토르의 발굽 소리는 다시 같은 거리에서 들렸다."]], [["Vronsky was at the head of the race, just as he wanted to be and as Cord had advised, and now he felt sure of being the winner.", "브론스키는 자기가 바라던 대로, 또 코드가 충고한 대로 경주의 선두에 섰고, 이제 자신이 승리하리라고 확신했다."], ["His excitement, his delight, and his tenderness for Frou-Frou grew keener and keener.", "그의 흥분, 기쁨, 그리고 프루프루에 대한 애틋함이 점점 더 깊어졌다."], ["He longed to look round again, but he did not dare do this, and tried to be cool and not to urge on his mare so to keep the same reserve of force in her as he felt that Gladiator still kept.", "그는 다시 한번 뒤를 돌아보고 싶었지만 감히 그러지 못했고, 글라디아토르가 여전히 힘을 비축하고 있는 것이 느껴지는 만큼 자기 암말도 그 힘을 그대로 남겨 두기 위해, 침착하게 굴며 암말을 재촉하지 않으려 애썼다."], ["There remained only one obstacle, the most difficult; if he could cross it ahead of the others he would come in first.", "이제 가장 까다로운 장애물 하나만 남아 있었다. 그것을 다른 이들보다 앞서 넘을 수만 있다면 그가 일등으로 들어올 터였다."], ["He was flying towards the Irish barricade, Frou-Frou and he both together saw the barricade in the distance, and both the man and the mare had a moment’s hesitation.", "그는 아일랜드식 방벽을 향해 날아가고 있었고, 프루프루와 그는 함께 멀리 그 방벽을 보았으며, 사람도 암말도 한순간 망설였다."], ["He saw the uncertainty in the mare’s ears and lifted the whip, but at the same time felt that his fears were groundless; the mare knew what was wanted.", "그는 암말의 귀에서 망설임을 보고 채찍을 들었지만, 동시에 자신의 두려움이 근거 없는 것임을 느꼈다. 암말은 무엇이 필요한지 알고 있었다."], ["She quickened her pace and rose smoothly, just as he had fancied she would, and as she left the ground gave herself up to the force of her rush, which carried her far beyond the ditch; and with the same rhythm, without effort, with the same leg forward, Frou-Frou fell back into her pace again.", "그녀는 그가 그려 보았던 그대로 속도를 높이며 매끄럽게 솟구쳤고, 땅을 박차고 오르면서 도약의 힘에 몸을 내맡겨 그 힘으로 도랑 저 멀리까지 건너갔다. 그러고는 똑같은 율동으로, 힘들이지 않고, 똑같은 다리를 앞으로 내디디며 프루프루는 다시 제 보폭을 되찾았다."]], [["“Bravo, Vronsky!”", "\"브라보, 브론스키!\""], ["he heard shouts from a knot of men—he knew they were his friends in the regiment—who were standing at the obstacle.", "그는 한 무리의 사람들에게서 외침이 들렸다. 장애물 곁에 서 있는 그들이 자기 연대 친구들임을 그는 알았다."], ["He could not fail to recognize Yashvin’s voice though he did not see him.", "그는 야슈빈을 보지는 못했지만 그의 목소리를 알아채지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["“O my sweet!”", "'오, 내 사랑!'"], ["he said inwardly to Frou-Frou, as he listened for what was happening behind.", "그는 뒤에서 무슨 일이 일어나는지 귀를 기울이며 프루프루에게 속으로 말했다."], ["“He’s cleared it!”", "'저놈도 넘었군!'"], ["he thought, catching the thud of Gladiator’s hoofs behind him.", "그는 뒤에서 글라디아토르의 발굽 소리를 알아채며 생각했다."], ["There remained only the last ditch, filled with water and five feet wide.", "이제 물이 가득 찬 너비 오 피트의 마지막 도랑만 남았다."], ["Vronsky did not even look at it, but anxious to get in a long way first began sawing away at the reins, lifting the mare’s head and letting it go in time with her paces.", "브론스키는 그것을 쳐다보지도 않았고, 그저 멀찍이 앞서 들어가고 싶은 마음에 암말의 머리를 들어 올렸다 보폭에 맞추어 놓아 주며 고삐를 톱질하듯 다루기 시작했다."], ["He felt that the mare was at her very last reserve of strength; not her neck and shoulders merely were wet, but the sweat was standing in drops on her mane, her head, her sharp ears, and her breath came in short, sharp gasps.", "그는 암말이 마지막 남은 힘까지 쥐어짜고 있음을 느꼈다. 목과 어깨만 젖은 것이 아니라, 갈기와 머리와 뾰족한 귀에 땀이 방울방울 맺혀 있었고, 그녀의 숨은 짧고 거칠게 헐떡였다."], ["But he knew that she had strength left more than enough for the remaining five hundred yards.", "그러나 그는 남은 오백 야드를 달리기에 그녀에게 충분하고도 남는 힘이 있음을 알았다."], ["It was only from feeling himself nearer the ground and from the peculiar smoothness of his motion that Vronsky knew how greatly the mare had quickened her pace.", "다만 자기 몸이 땅에 더 가까워진 느낌과 자기 움직임의 그 야릇한 매끄러움에서, 브론스키는 암말이 얼마나 크게 속도를 높였는지 알 수 있었다."], ["She flew over the ditch as though not noticing it.", "그녀는 그 도랑을 알아채지도 못한 듯 날아넘었다."], ["She flew over it like a bird; but at the same instant Vronsky, to his horror, felt that he had failed to keep up with the mare’s pace, that he had, he did not know how, made a fearful, unpardonable mistake, in recovering his seat in the saddle.", "새처럼 그 위를 날아넘었다. 그러나 같은 순간, 브론스키는 끔찍하게도 자기가 암말의 보폭에 맞추지 못했다는 것을, 안장에서 자세를 다시 잡으면서 어떻게 그랬는지도 모르게 무시무시하고 용서할 수 없는 실수를 저질렀다는 것을 느꼈다."], ["All at once his position had shifted and he knew that something awful had happened.", "별안간 그의 자세가 흐트러졌고, 그는 무언가 끔찍한 일이 일어났음을 알았다."], ["He could not yet make out what had happened, when the white legs of a chestnut horse flashed by close to him, and Mahotin passed at a swift gallop.", "무슨 일이 일어났는지 아직 분간하지 못하는데, 밤색 말의 흰 다리가 그의 바로 곁에서 번쩍이더니 마호친이 빠른 질주로 지나갔다."], ["Vronsky was touching the ground with one foot, and his mare was sinking on that foot.", "브론스키는 한쪽 발로 땅을 딛고 있었고, 그의 암말은 그쪽으로 무너지고 있었다."], ["He just had time to free his leg when she fell on one side, gasping painfully, and, making vain efforts to rise with her delicate, soaking neck, she fluttered on the ground at his feet like a shot bird.", "그는 가까스로 다리를 빼낼 틈이 있었는데, 그녀는 한쪽으로 쓰러지며 고통스럽게 헐떡였고, 가냘프고 흠뻑 젖은 목으로 일어나려 헛되이 애쓰면서 총 맞은 새처럼 그의 발치에서 땅바닥에 푸드덕거렸다."], ["The clumsy movement made by Vronsky had broken her back.", "브론스키가 한 그 서툰 동작이 그녀의 등을 부러뜨린 것이다."], ["But that he only knew much later.", "그러나 그것을 그가 안 것은 한참 뒤의 일이었다."], ["At that moment he knew only that Mahotin had flown swiftly by, while he stood staggering alone on the muddy, motionless ground, and Frou-Frou lay gasping before him, bending her head back and gazing at him with her exquisite eyes.", "그 순간 그가 안 것이라곤, 마호친이 빠르게 휙 지나가 버렸고, 자기는 진흙투성이의 미동도 없는 땅 위에 홀로 비틀거리며 서 있으며, 프루프루가 그 앞에 헐떡이며 누워 고개를 뒤로 젖힌 채 그 아름다운 눈으로 자기를 바라보고 있다는 것뿐이었다."], ["Still unable to realize what had happened, Vronsky tugged at his mare’s reins.", "무슨 일이 일어났는지 여전히 깨닫지 못한 채 브론스키는 암말의 고삐를 잡아당겼다."], ["Again she struggled all over like a fish, and her shoulders setting the saddle heaving, she rose on her front legs but unable to lift her back, she quivered all over and again fell on her side.", "그녀는 다시 물고기처럼 온몸을 버둥거렸고, 어깨로 안장을 들썩이며 앞다리로 일어섰지만 뒷부분을 들지 못한 채 온몸을 떨다가 다시 옆으로 쓰러졌다."], ["With a face hideous with passion, his lower jaw trembling, and his cheeks white, Vronsky kicked her with his heel in the stomach and again fell to tugging at the rein.", "격정으로 얼굴이 흉하게 일그러지고 아래턱이 떨리고 두 뺨이 하얗게 질린 채, 브론스키는 발뒤꿈치로 그녀의 배를 걷어차고는 다시 고삐를 잡아당기기 시작했다."], ["She did not stir, but thrusting her nose into the ground, she simply gazed at her master with her speaking eyes.", "그녀는 꼼짝도 않고, 코를 땅에 처박은 채 말하는 듯한 눈으로 그저 자기 주인을 바라보았다."]], [["“A—a—a!”", "\"아—아—아!\""], ["groaned Vronsky, clutching at his head.", "브론스키는 머리를 움켜쥐며 신음했다."], ["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["what have I done!”", "내가 무슨 짓을 한 거야!\""], ["he cried.", "그가 부르짖었다."], ["“The race lost!", "\"경주를 망쳤어!"], ["And my fault!", "게다가 내 잘못이야!"], ["shameful, unpardonable!", "부끄럽고, 용서받을 수 없어!"], ["And the poor darling, ruined mare!", "그리고 가엾은 내 사랑, 못 쓰게 된 암말!"], ["Ah!", "아!"], ["what have I done!”", "내가 무슨 짓을 한 거야!\""]], [["A crowd of men, a doctor and his assistant, the officers of his regiment, ran up to him.", "한 무리의 사람들, 의사와 그 조수, 그의 연대 장교들이 그에게 달려왔다."], ["To his misery he felt that he was whole and unhurt.", "비참하게도 그는 자기가 멀쩡하고 다친 데 없이 온전하다는 것을 느꼈다."], ["The mare had broken her back, and it was decided to shoot her.", "암말은 등이 부러졌고, 그녀를 쏘아 죽이기로 결정되었다."], ["Vronsky could not answer questions, could not speak to anyone.", "브론스키는 질문에 답할 수도, 누구에게 말을 건넬 수도 없었다."], ["He turned, and without picking up his cap that had fallen off, walked away from the race course, not knowing where he was going.", "그는 몸을 돌려, 떨어진 모자도 줍지 않고 자기가 어디로 가는지도 모른 채 경주로에서 걸어 나왔다."], ["He felt utterly wretched.", "그는 더없이 비참했다."], ["For the first time in his life he knew the bitterest sort of misfortune, misfortune beyond remedy, and caused by his own fault.", "난생처음으로 그는 가장 쓰라린 종류의 불행을, 돌이킬 수 없으며 제 잘못으로 빚어진 불행을 알게 되었다."]], [["Yashvin overtook him with his cap, and led him home, and half an hour later Vronsky had regained his self-possession.", "야슈빈이 모자를 들고 그를 뒤따라와 집으로 데려갔고, 삼십 분쯤 뒤에 브론스키는 마음의 평정을 되찾았다."], ["But the memory of that race remained for long in his heart, the cruelest and bitterest memory of his life.", "그러나 그 경주의 기억은 그의 가슴속에 오래도록 남아, 그의 생애에서 가장 잔혹하고 쓰라린 기억이 되었다."]], [["# Chapter 26", "# Chapter 26"]], [["The external relations of Alexey Alexandrovitch and his wife had remained unchanged.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치와 그의 아내의 표면적인 관계는 변함없이 유지되고 있었다."], ["The sole difference lay in the fact that he was more busily occupied than ever.", "다만 한 가지 차이가 있다면, 그가 그 어느 때보다 분주하게 일에 몰두하고 있다는 사실뿐이었다."], ["As in former years, at the beginning of the spring he had gone to a foreign watering-place for the sake of his health, deranged by the winter’s work that every year grew heavier.", "예년과 마찬가지로 봄이 시작될 무렵 그는 건강을 위해 외국의 온천 휴양지로 떠났다. 해마다 더 무거워지는 겨울철 격무로 망가진 건강을 추스르기 위해서였다."], ["And just as always he returned in July and at once fell to work as usual with increased energy.", "그리고 늘 그래 왔듯이 그는 칠월에 돌아와 곧장 평소처럼, 오히려 더 왕성한 기운으로 일에 매달렸다."], ["As usual, too, his wife had moved for the summer to a villa out of town, while he remained in Petersburg.", "또한 늘 그래 왔듯이 그의 아내는 여름을 나기 위해 교외의 별장으로 거처를 옮겼고, 그는 페테르부르크에 남았다."], ["From the date of their conversation after the party at Princess Tverskaya’s he had never spoken again to Anna of his suspicions and his jealousies, and that habitual tone of his bantering mimicry was the most convenient tone possible for his present attitude to his wife.", "트베르스카야 공작부인 댁의 모임 이후 나누었던 그 대화 이래로, 그는 자신의 의심과 질투에 관해 안나에게 두 번 다시 입을 열지 않았으며, 평소처럼 빈정대듯 흉내 내는 그 말투가 지금 아내를 대하는 그의 태도에 가장 알맞은 어조였다."], ["He was a little colder to his wife.", "그는 아내에게 조금 더 냉랭해졌다."], ["He simply seemed to be slightly displeased with her for that first midnight conversation, which she had repelled.", "다만 그녀가 거부해 버린 그 첫 한밤중의 대화 때문에 그녀에게 가벼운 불만을 품은 것처럼 보일 뿐이었다."], ["In his attitude to her there was a shade of vexation, but nothing more.", "그녀를 대하는 태도에는 어렴풋한 짜증의 기색이 있었지만, 그 이상은 아니었다."], ["“You would not be open with me,”", "\"당신은 내게 솔직하려 하지 않았어.\""], ["he seemed to say, mentally addressing her; “so much the worse for you.", "그는 마음속으로 그녀에게 말하는 듯했다. \"그러니 당신만 더 손해지."], ["Now you may beg as you please, but I won’t be open with you.", "이제 당신이 아무리 빌어도 나는 당신에게 솔직하게 굴지 않을 거야."], ["So much the worse for you!”", "그래 봐야 당신만 손해야!\""], ["he said mentally, like a man who, after vainly attempting to extinguish a fire, should fly in a rage with his vain efforts and say, “Oh, very well then!", "그는 마음속으로 그렇게 말했다. 마치 불을 끄려고 헛되이 애쓰다가 그 헛된 노력에 화가 치밀어 \"아, 그래, 좋아!"], ["you shall burn for this!”", "그럼 다 타 버려라!\""], ["This man, so subtle and astute in official life, did not realize all the senselessness of such an attitude to his wife.", "하고 내뱉는 사람처럼. 공무에서는 그토록 섬세하고 빈틈없던 이 사람이, 아내를 대하는 이런 태도가 얼마나 무의미한지는 도무지 깨닫지 못했다."], ["He did not realize it, because it was too terrible to him to realize his actual position, and he shut down and locked and sealed up in his heart that secret place where lay hid his feelings towards his family, that is, his wife and son.", "그가 그것을 깨닫지 못한 까닭은, 자신의 실제 처지를 깨닫는 것이 너무도 끔찍했기 때문이다. 그래서 그는 자기 가족, 그러니까 아내와 아들을 향한 감정이 숨어 있는 그 은밀한 곳을 마음속 깊이 닫고 잠그고 봉인해 버렸다."], ["He who had been such a careful father, had from the end of that winter become peculiarly frigid to his son, and adopted to him just the same bantering tone he used with his wife.", "그토록 자상한 아버지였던 그는 그해 겨울이 끝날 무렵부터 아들에게 유난히 차가워졌고, 아내에게 쓰던 것과 똑같은 빈정대는 말투를 아들에게도 쓰기 시작했다."], ["“Aha, young man!”", "\"어이, 젊은이!\""], ["was the greeting with which he met him.", "그가 아들을 맞으며 건네는 인사는 이런 식이었다."]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch asserted and believed that he had never in any previous year had so much official business as that year.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 어느 해에도 그해만큼 공무가 많았던 적이 없다고 단언했고, 또 그렇게 믿었다."], ["But he was not aware that he sought work for himself that year, that this was one of the means for keeping shut that secret place where lay hid his feelings towards his wife and son and his thoughts about them, which became more terrible the longer they lay there.", "하지만 그는 자기가 그해 들어 스스로 일거리를 찾고 있다는 사실은 알아차리지 못했다. 그것이 아내와 아들을 향한 감정, 그리고 그들에 관한 생각이 숨어 있는 그 은밀한 곳을 닫아 두기 위한 한 가지 방편이라는 것을—그것들은 그곳에 오래 묻혀 있을수록 더욱 끔찍해졌다—그는 알지 못했다."], ["If anyone had had the right to ask Alexey Alexandrovitch what he thought of his wife’s behavior, the mild and peaceable Alexey Alexandrovitch would have made no answer, but he would have been greatly angered with any man who should question him on that subject.", "만약 누군가가 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에게 아내의 행실을 어떻게 생각하느냐고 물을 권리가 있었다면, 온화하고 평화로운 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 아무 대답도 하지 않았을 것이고, 그 문제로 자기에게 캐묻는 자에게는 누구든 몹시 화를 냈을 것이다."], ["For this reason there positively came into Alexey Alexandrovitch’s face a look of haughtiness and severity whenever anyone inquired after his wife’s health.", "바로 그 때문에 누군가 아내의 안부를 물을 때마다 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 얼굴에는 어김없이 거만하고 준엄한 표정이 떠올랐다."], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch did not want to think at all about his wife’s behavior, and he actually succeeded in not thinking about it at all.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 아내의 행실에 관해 아예 생각하고 싶어 하지 않았고, 실제로 그것에 관해 전혀 생각하지 않는 데 성공하고 있었다."]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch’s permanent summer villa was in Peterhof, and the Countess Lidia Ivanovna used as a rule to spend the summer there, close to Anna, and constantly seeing her.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 여름마다 머무는 별장은 페테르고프에 있었고, 리디아 이바노브나 백작부인은 으레 그곳에서 여름을 보내며 안나의 가까이에서 끊임없이 그녀를 만나곤 했다."], ["That year Countess Lidia Ivanovna declined to settle in Peterhof, was not once at Anna Arkadyevna’s, and in conversation with Alexey Alexandrovitch hinted at the unsuitability of Anna’s close intimacy with Betsy and Vronsky.", "그런데 그해에는 리디아 이바노브나 백작부인이 페테르고프에 자리 잡기를 마다했고, 안나 아르카지예브나에게 한 번도 들르지 않았으며, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치와 이야기를 나누는 자리에서 안나가 베치며 브론스키와 가깝게 지내는 것이 적절치 않다는 점을 넌지시 비쳤다."], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch sternly cut her short, roundly declaring his wife to be above suspicion, and from that time began to avoid Countess Lidia Ivanovna.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 자기 아내가 의심받을 여지가 없는 사람이라고 단호히 못 박으며 그녀의 말을 매몰차게 잘랐고, 그때부터 리디아 이바노브나 백작부인을 피하기 시작했다."], ["He did not want to see, and did not see, that many people in society cast dubious glances on his wife; he did not want to understand, and did not understand, why his wife had so particularly insisted on staying at Tsarskoe, where Betsy was staying, and not far from the camp of Vronsky’s regiment.", "그는 사교계의 많은 이들이 자기 아내에게 미심쩍은 시선을 던지고 있다는 것을 보고 싶어 하지 않았고, 또 보지도 않았다. 그는 아내가 어째서 그토록 한사코 차르스코예에—베치가 머물고 있고, 브론스키 연대의 주둔지에서 멀지 않은 그곳에—머무르겠다고 고집을 부렸는지 이해하고 싶어 하지 않았고, 또 이해하지도 않았다."], ["He did not allow himself to think about it, and he did not think about it; but all the same though he never admitted it to himself, and had no proofs, not even suspicious evidence, in the bottom of his heart he knew beyond all doubt that he was a deceived husband, and he was profoundly miserable about it.", "그는 자기 자신이 그것에 관해 생각하도록 두지 않았고, 그래서 생각하지 않았다. 하지만 그럼에도, 비록 스스로에게 결코 인정하지 않았고 아무 증거도, 심지어 의심스러운 정황조차 없었음에도, 마음 깊은 곳에서 그는 자기가 속은 남편이라는 것을 조금의 의심도 없이 알고 있었고, 그 때문에 깊디깊은 비참함에 잠겨 있었다."]], [["How often during those eight years of happy life with his wife Alexey Alexandrovitch had looked at other men’s faithless wives and other deceived husbands and asked himself: “How can people descend to that?", "아내와 함께 행복하게 산 그 팔 년 동안, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 다른 남자들의 부정한 아내와 속아 넘어간 남편들을 바라보며 얼마나 자주 자문했던가. \"사람들이 어떻게 저 지경까지 떨어질 수 있지?"], ["how is it they don’t put an end to such a hideous position?”", "어째서 저토록 흉측한 처지에 종지부를 찍지 못하는 거지?\""], ["But now, when the misfortune had come upon himself, he was so far from thinking of putting an end to the position that he would not recognize it at all, would not recognize it just because it was too awful, too unnatural.", "하지만 막상 그 불행이 자기 자신에게 닥친 지금, 그는 그 처지에 종지부를 찍을 생각을 하기는커녕 그것을 아예 인정하려 들지 않았다. 그것이 너무도 끔찍하고 너무도 부자연스럽다는 바로 그 이유로 인정하려 들지 않았다."]], [["Since his return from abroad Alexey Alexandrovitch had twice been at their country villa.", "외국에서 돌아온 이래로 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 교외의 별장에 두 번 다녀왔다."], ["Once he dined there, another time he spent the evening there with a party of friends, but he had not once stayed the night there, as it had been his habit to do in previous years.", "한 번은 거기서 만찬을 했고, 또 한 번은 친구들과 어울려 저녁 시간을 보냈지만, 예년의 습관과 달리 단 한 번도 그곳에서 밤을 보내지는 않았다."]], [["The day of the races had been a very busy day for Alexey Alexandrovitch; but when mentally sketching out the day in the morning, he made up his mind to go to their country house to see his wife immediately after dinner, and from there to the races, which all the Court were to witness, and at which he was bound to be present.", "경마가 열리는 날은 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에게 무척 분주한 하루였다. 하지만 아침에 마음속으로 하루 일정을 그려 보면서, 그는 만찬이 끝나는 대로 교외의 집으로 가서 아내를 보고, 거기서 곧장 경마장으로 가기로 마음먹었다."], ["He was going to see his wife, because he had determined to see her once a week to keep up appearances.", "경마는 궁정 사람들이 모두 참관하는 행사였고, 그도 반드시 참석해야만 했다."], ["And besides, on that day, as it was the fifteenth, he had to give his wife some money for her expenses, according to their usual arrangement.", "그가 아내를 보러 가려는 것은, 체면을 지키기 위해 일주일에 한 번은 그녀를 보기로 작정해 두었기 때문이다. 게다가 그날은 십오일이었으므로, 늘 정해 둔 약속대로 아내에게 살림에 쓸 돈을 얼마간 건네주어야 했다."]], [["With his habitual control over his thoughts, though he thought all this about his wife, he did not let his thoughts stray further in regard to her.", "자기 생각을 다스리는 평소의 절제력으로, 그는 아내에 관해 이 모든 것을 생각하면서도 그녀에 관한 생각이 더 멀리 뻗어 나가도록 내버려 두지는 않았다."]], [["That morning was a very full one for Alexey Alexandrovitch.", "그날 아침은 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에게 무척 바쁜 시간이었다."], ["The evening before, Countess Lidia Ivanovna had sent him a pamphlet by a celebrated traveler in China, who was staying in Petersburg, and with it she enclosed a note begging him to see the traveler himself, as he was an extremely interesting person from various points of view, and likely to be useful.", "전날 저녁 리디아 이바노브나 백작부인이 페테르부르크에 머물고 있는, 중국을 여행한 어느 저명한 여행가의 소책자를 보내왔는데, 거기에 쪽지 한 장을 동봉하여 그 여행가를 직접 만나 보라고 청했다. 여러 면에서 대단히 흥미로운 인물이며 유용한 점도 있을 것이라는 이유였다."], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch had not had time to read the pamphlet through in the evening, and finished it in the morning.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 전날 저녁에 그 소책자를 끝까지 읽을 틈이 없어서 아침에 마저 읽었다."], ["Then people began arriving with petitions, and there came the reports, interviews, appointments, dismissals, apportionment of rewards, pensions, grants, notes, the workaday round, as Alexey Alexandrovitch called it, that always took up so much time.", "그러고 나자 청원서를 든 사람들이 찾아오기 시작했고, 이어서 보고와 면담, 임명과 해임, 포상과 연금과 보조금의 배정, 각종 통지 등, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 일상의 다람쥐 쳇바퀴라고 부르는, 늘 그토록 많은 시간을 잡아먹는 일들이 줄지어 이어졌다."], ["Then there was private business of his own, a visit from the doctor and the steward who managed his property.", "그다음에는 자신의 사적인 용무가 있었으니, 의사와 영지를 관리하는 집사의 방문이었다."], ["The steward did not take up much time.", "집사는 그리 많은 시간을 빼앗지 않았다."], ["He simply gave Alexey Alexandrovitch the money he needed together with a brief statement of the position of his affairs, which was not altogether satisfactory, as it had happened that during that year, owing to increased expenses, more had been paid out than usual, and there was a deficit.", "그는 그저 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에게 필요한 돈을 건네주며 살림 형편에 관한 짤막한 보고를 곁들였을 뿐인데, 그 형편이라는 것이 그다지 만족스럽지는 못했다. 그해에는 지출이 늘어나는 바람에 평소보다 더 많은 돈이 나갔고, 그래서 적자가 생겼던 것이다."], ["But the doctor, a celebrated Petersburg doctor, who was an intimate acquaintance of Alexey Alexandrovitch, took up a great deal of time.", "그러나 의사는—알렉세이 알렉산드로비치와 가깝게 지내던 페테르부르크의 저명한 의사였는데—꽤 많은 시간을 잡아먹었다."], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch had not expected him that day, and was surprised at his visit, and still more so when the doctor questioned him very carefully about his health, listened to his breathing, and tapped at his liver.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 그날 그가 오리라고는 예상하지 못했기에 그의 방문에 놀랐고, 의사가 자신의 건강에 관해 아주 세심하게 캐묻고 호흡 소리를 들으며 간을 두드려 보았을 때는 더욱 놀랐다."], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch did not know that his friend Lidia Ivanovna, noticing that he was not as well as usual that year, had begged the doctor to go and examine him.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 자신의 벗 리디아 이바노브나가 그해 그의 건강이 평소만 못한 것을 눈치채고 의사에게 가서 그를 진찰해 달라고 부탁했다는 것을 알지 못했다."], ["“Do this for my sake,”", "\"저를 봐서라도 이렇게 해 주세요.\""], ["the Countess Lidia Ivanovna had said to him.", "리디아 이바노브나 백작부인이 그에게 말했었다."]], [["“I will do it for the sake of Russia, countess,”", "\"러시아를 봐서라도 그렇게 하겠습니다, 백작부인.\""], ["replied the doctor.", "의사가 대답했다."]], [["“A priceless man!”", "\"더없이 귀한 분이세요!\""], ["said the Countess Lidia Ivanovna.", "리디아 이바노브나 백작부인이 말했다."]], [["The doctor was extremely dissatisfied with Alexey Alexandrovitch.", "의사는 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 상태에 대해 몹시 못마땅해했다."], ["He found the liver considerably enlarged, and the digestive powers weakened, while the course of mineral waters had been quite without effect.", "그는 간이 상당히 비대해졌고 소화 기능이 약해졌으며, 광천수 요법은 아무런 효과도 없었음을 알아냈다."], ["He prescribed more physical exercise as far as possible, and as far as possible less mental strain, and above all no worry—in other words, just what was as much out of Alexey Alexandrovitch’s power as abstaining from breathing.", "그는 되도록 운동을 더 많이 하고, 되도록 정신적 긴장을 덜 하며, 무엇보다도 근심을 하지 말 것을 처방했다—다시 말해, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치로서는 숨을 쉬지 않는 것만큼이나 불가능한 일들이었다."], ["Then he withdrew, leaving in Alexey Alexandrovitch an unpleasant sense that something was wrong with him, and that there was no chance of curing it.", "그러고 나서 그는 물러갔고, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에게는 자신에게 무언가 이상이 있다는, 그리고 그것을 고칠 가망이 없다는 불쾌한 느낌만이 남았다."]], [["As he was coming away, the doctor chanced to meet on the staircase an acquaintance of his, Sludin, who was secretary of Alexey Alexandrovitch’s department.", "의사는 돌아가는 길에 계단에서 우연히 아는 사람을 만났는데, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치 부처의 비서관인 슬루딘이었다."], ["They had been comrades at the university, and though they rarely met, they thought highly of each other and were excellent friends, and so there was no one to whom the doctor would have given his opinion of a patient so freely as to Sludin.", "두 사람은 대학 시절의 동무였고, 좀처럼 만나는 일은 없었지만 서로를 높이 평가하며 더없이 가까운 벗으로 지냈다. 그래서 의사가 환자에 관한 자신의 소견을 슬루딘만큼 거리낌 없이 털어놓을 만한 상대는 따로 없었다."]], [["“How glad I am you’ve been seeing him!”", "\"마침 자네가 그분을 진찰해 주었다니 얼마나 다행인지!\""], ["said Sludin.", "슬루딘이 말했다."], ["“He’s not well, and I fancy....", "\"그분이 영 편찮으셔서, 내 짐작에는……"], ["Well, what do you think of him?”", "자, 자네가 보기엔 어떤가?\""]], [["“I’ll tell you,”", "\"내 말해 주지.\""], ["said the doctor, beckoning over Sludin’s head to his coachman to bring the carriage round.", "의사가 슬루딘의 머리 너머로 마부에게 마차를 대라고 손짓하며 말했다."], ["“It’s just this,”", "\"바로 이런 거라네.\""], ["said the doctor, taking a finger of his kid glove in his white hands and pulling it, “if you don’t strain the strings, and then try to break them, you’ll find it a difficult job; but strain a string to its very utmost, and the mere weight of one finger on the strained string will snap it.", "의사는 하얀 손으로 새끼 염소가죽 장갑의 손가락 끝을 잡고 당기면서 말을 이었다. \"줄을 팽팽히 당기지 않은 채로 그것을 끊으려 하면 여간 어려운 일이 아니지."], ["And with his close assiduity, his conscientious devotion to his work, he’s strained to the utmost; and there’s some outside burden weighing on him, and not a light one,”", "하지만 줄을 한껏 팽팽하게 당겨 놓으면, 그 팽팽한 줄 위에 손가락 하나의 무게만 얹어도 그냥 툭 끊어져 버린다네. 그런데 그분은 그 빈틈없는 근면함과 양심적으로 일에 헌신하는 태도 탓에 한껏 팽팽하게 당겨져 있어."], ["concluded the doctor, raising his eyebrows significantly.", "게다가 바깥에서 짓누르는 어떤 부담이, 그것도 가볍지 않은 부담이 있단 말이지.\" 의사는 의미심장하게 눈썹을 치켜올리며 말을 맺었다."], ["“Will you be at the races?”", "\"자네도 경마장에 올 텐가?\""], ["he added, as he sank into his seat in the carriage.", "그는 마차 좌석에 몸을 파묻으며 덧붙였다."]], [["“Yes, yes, to be sure; it does waste a lot of time,”", "\"그래, 그래, 그럼 가고말고. 정말이지 시간을 너무 많이 잡아먹는단 말이야.\""], ["the doctor responded vaguely to some reply of Sludin’s he had not caught.", "의사는 미처 알아듣지 못한 슬루딘의 어떤 대꾸에 막연히 대답했다."]], [["Directly after the doctor, who had taken up so much time, came the celebrated traveler, and Alexey Alexandrovitch, by means of the pamphlet he had only just finished reading and his previous acquaintance with the subject, impressed the traveler by the depth of his knowledge of the subject and the breadth and enlightenment of his view of it.", "그토록 많은 시간을 잡아먹은 의사가 떠난 직후에 그 저명한 여행가가 찾아왔는데, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 방금 막 다 읽은 소책자와 그 주제에 관해 전부터 알고 있던 지식을 동원하여, 그 주제에 대한 깊은 식견과 그것을 바라보는 폭넓고 계몽된 시각으로 여행가에게 깊은 인상을 남겼다."]], [["At the same time as the traveler there was announced a provincial marshal of nobility on a visit to Petersburg, with whom Alexey Alexandrovitch had to have some conversation.", "그 여행가와 같은 시각에 페테르부르크를 방문 중인 어느 지방 귀족 단장의 내방이 알려졌는데, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 그와도 몇 마디 이야기를 나누어야 했다."], ["After his departure, he had to finish the daily routine of business with his secretary, and then he still had to drive round to call on a certain great personage on a matter of grave and serious import.", "그가 돌아간 뒤에는 비서와 함께 그날의 통상 업무를 마무리해야 했고, 그러고도 중대하고 심각한 사안으로 어느 대단한 인물을 찾아가 만나야 했다."], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch only just managed to be back by five o’clock, his dinner-hour, and after dining with his secretary, he invited him to drive with him to his country villa and to the races.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 만찬 시각인 다섯 시에 가까스로 맞춰 돌아왔고, 비서와 함께 식사를 한 뒤 그에게 자기 교외 별장과 경마장에 함께 가자고 청했다."]], [["Though he did not acknowledge it to himself, Alexey Alexandrovitch always tried nowadays to secure the presence of a third person in his interviews with his wife.", "스스로에게 인정하지는 않았지만, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 요즈음 아내와 만나는 자리에는 늘 제삼자가 함께 있도록 애쓰고 있었다."]], [["# Chapter 27", "# Chapter 27"]], [["Anna was upstairs, standing before the looking-glass, and, with Annushka’s assistance, pinning the last ribbon on her gown when she heard carriage wheels crunching the gravel at the entrance.", "안나는 위층에서 거울 앞에 서서 안누슈카의 도움을 받아 가운에 마지막 리본을 꽂고 있다가, 현관 앞 자갈 위로 마차 바퀴가 사각거리는 소리를 들었다."]], [["“It’s too early for Betsy,”", "\"베시가 오기엔 너무 이른데.\""], ["she thought, and glancing out of the window she caught sight of the carriage and the black hat of Alexey Alexandrovitch, and the ears that she knew so well sticking up each side of it.", "그녀는 그렇게 생각하며 창밖을 내다보다가 마차와 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 검은 모자, 그리고 그 모자 양옆으로 삐죽 솟은 익히 아는 귀를 발견했다."], ["“How unlucky!", "\"이런 운도 없지!"], ["Can he be going to stay the night?”", "하룻밤 묵으려는 건 아니겠지?\""], ["she wondered, and the thought of all that might come of such a chance struck her as so awful and terrible that, without dwelling on it for a moment, she went down to meet him with a bright and radiant face; and conscious of the presence of that spirit of falsehood and deceit in herself that she had come to know of late, she abandoned herself to that spirit and began talking, hardly knowing what she was saying.", "그녀는 의아해했고, 그런 우연한 일에서 빚어질 수 있는 온갖 결과를 떠올리자 너무도 끔찍하고 무섭게 느껴진 나머지, 잠시도 거기 마음을 두지 않은 채 밝고 환한 얼굴로 그를 맞으러 내려갔다. 그리고 요즘 들어 자기 안에 깃든 것을 알게 된 그 거짓과 기만의 영(靈)을 의식하면서, 그녀는 그 영에 자신을 내맡기고는 자기가 무슨 말을 하는지도 잘 모른 채 떠들기 시작했다."]], [["“Ah, how nice of you!”", "\"아, 와 주시다니 정말 고마워요!\""], ["she said, giving her husband her hand, and greeting Sludin, who was like one of the family, with a smile.", "그녀는 남편에게 손을 내밀며 말했고, 집안 식구나 다름없는 슬류딘에게도 미소로 인사했다."], ["“You’re staying the night, I hope?”", "\"하룻밤 묵으실 거죠?\""], ["was the first word the spirit of falsehood prompted her to utter; “and now we’ll go together.", "거짓의 영이 그녀에게 일러 준 첫마디였다. \"그럼 같이 나가요."], ["Only it’s a pity I’ve promised Betsy.", "다만 베시한테 약속을 해 둔 게 아쉽네요."], ["She’s coming for me.”", "절 데리러 오기로 했거든요.\""]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch knit his brows at Betsy’s name.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 베시의 이름에 미간을 찌푸렸다."]], [["“Oh, I’m not going to separate the inseparables,”", "\"오, 떼려야 뗄 수 없는 사이를 갈라놓을 생각은 없소.\""], ["he said in his usual bantering tone.", "그는 평소의 농담조로 말했다."], ["“I’m going with Mihail Vassilievitch.", "\"난 미하일 바실리예비치와 함께 가겠소."], ["I’m ordered exercise by the doctors too.", "나도 의사들한테 운동을 하라는 명을 받았거든."], ["I’ll walk, and fancy myself at the springs again.”", "걸으면서, 다시 온천에라도 와 있는 양 상상해 보지.\""]], [["“There’s no hurry,”", "\"서두르실 것 없어요.\""], ["said Anna.", "안나가 말했다."], ["“Would you like tea?” She rang.", "\"차라도 드시겠어요?\""]], [["“Bring in tea, and tell Seryozha that Alexey Alexandrovitch is here.", "그녀가 벨을 울렸다. \"차를 내오고, 세료자한테 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 오셨다고 전해 줘요."], ["Well, tell me, how have you been?", "자, 말씀해 보세요, 그동안 어떻게 지내셨어요?"], ["Mihail Vassilievitch, you’ve not been to see me before.", "미하일 바실리예비치, 전에는 절 보러 와 주신 적이 없었죠."], ["Look how lovely it is out on the terrace,”", "보세요, 테라스 밖이 얼마나 좋은지.\""], ["she said, turning first to one and then to the other.", "그녀는 한 사람에게서 다른 사람에게로 번갈아 몸을 돌리며 말했다."]], [["She spoke very simply and naturally, but too much and too fast.", "그녀는 아주 단순하고 자연스럽게 말했지만, 너무 많이, 너무 빠르게 말했다."], ["She was the more aware of this from noticing in the inquisitive look Mihail Vassilievitch turned on her that he was, as it were, keeping watch on her.", "미하일 바실리예비치가 자기에게 던지는 캐묻는 듯한 눈길에서, 그가 말하자면 자기를 감시하고 있다는 것을 알아차렸기에 그녀는 더욱 그것을 의식했다."]], [["Mihail Vassilievitch promptly went out on the terrace.", "미하일 바실리예비치는 곧장 테라스로 나갔다."]], [["She sat down beside her husband.", "그녀는 남편 곁에 앉았다."]], [["“You don’t look quite well,”", "\"안색이 그리 좋지 않으시네요.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그렇소.\""], ["he said; “the doctor’s been with me today and wasted an hour of my time.", "그가 말했다. \"오늘 의사가 와서 한 시간이나 내 시간을 허비하게 했지."], ["I feel that someone of our friends must have sent him: my health’s so precious, it seems.”", "우리 친구들 중 누가 그를 보낸 게 틀림없다는 생각이 들더군. 내 건강이 어지간히 소중한 모양이오.\""]], [["“No; what did he say?”", "\"아니, 의사가 뭐라고 하던가요?\""]], [["She questioned him about his health and what he had been doing, and tried to persuade him to take a rest and come out to her.", "그녀는 그의 건강과 그동안 한 일들에 대해 물으며, 좀 쉬다가 자기와 함께 나가자고 그를 설득하려 했다."]], [["All this she said brightly, rapidly, and with a peculiar brilliance in her eyes.", "이 모든 말을 그녀는 밝게, 빠르게, 그리고 두 눈에 묘한 광채를 띠며 했다."], ["But Alexey Alexandrovitch did not now attach any special significance to this tone of hers.", "그러나 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 이제 그녀의 그런 어조에 아무런 특별한 의미를 두지 않았다."], ["He heard only her words and gave them only the direct sense they bore.", "그는 오로지 그녀의 말만을 들었고, 그 말이 지닌 직접적인 뜻만을 받아들였다."], ["And he answered simply, though jestingly.", "그래서 그는 농담조이긴 했지만 단순하게 대답했다."], ["There was nothing remarkable in all this conversation, but never after could Anna recall this brief scene without an agonizing pang of shame.", "이 모든 대화에는 별달리 두드러진 점이 없었으나, 그 후로 안나는 두고두고 이 짧은 장면을 떠올릴 때마다 견딜 수 없는 수치심에 가슴이 아렸다."]], [["Seryozha came in preceded by his governess.", "세료자가 가정교사를 앞세우고 들어왔다."], ["If Alexey Alexandrovitch had allowed himself to observe he would have noticed the timid and bewildered eyes with which Seryozha glanced first at his father and then at his mother.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 눈여겨볼 마음만 있었다면, 세료자가 먼저 아버지를, 이어 어머니를 흘끔거리는 그 겁먹고 당황한 눈빛을 알아챘을 것이다."], ["But he would not see anything, and he did not see it.", "그러나 그는 아무것도 보려 하지 않았고, 그래서 보지 못했다."]], [["“Ah, the young man!", "\"아, 우리 젊은이!"], ["He’s grown.", "키가 컸구나."], ["Really, he’s getting quite a man.", "정말이지 어엿한 사내가 다 되었어."], ["How are you, young man?”", "어떻게 지내느냐, 젊은이?\""]], [["And he gave his hand to the scared child.", "그러고는 겁에 질린 아이에게 손을 내밀었다."], ["Seryozha had been shy of his father before, and now, ever since Alexey Alexandrovitch had taken to calling him young man, and since that insoluble question had occurred to him whether Vronsky were a friend or a foe, he avoided his father.", "세료자는 전부터 아버지를 어려워했는데, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 자기를 젊은이라고 부르기 시작한 뒤로, 또 브론스키가 친구인지 적인지 하는 풀리지 않는 의문이 떠오른 뒤로는 아버지를 피했다."], ["He looked round towards his mother as though seeking shelter.", "아이는 마치 피난처라도 찾는 양 어머니 쪽으로 돌아보았다."], ["It was only with his mother that he was at ease.", "아이가 마음 편할 수 있는 사람은 오직 어머니뿐이었다."], ["Meanwhile, Alexey Alexandrovitch was holding his son by the shoulder while he was speaking to the governess, and Seryozha was so miserably uncomfortable that Anna saw he was on the point of tears.", "그러는 동안 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 가정교사와 이야기하며 아들의 어깨를 붙잡고 있었고, 세료자는 너무도 비참하고 거북해서, 안나는 아이가 막 울음을 터뜨리려 한다는 것을 알아차렸다."]], [["Anna, who had flushed a little the instant her son came in, noticing that Seryozha was uncomfortable, got up hurriedly, took Alexey Alexandrovitch’s hand from her son’s shoulder, and kissing the boy, led him out onto the terrace, and quickly came back.", "아들이 들어온 순간 살짝 얼굴을 붉혔던 안나는, 세료자가 거북해하는 것을 알아채고는 황급히 일어나 아들의 어깨에서 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 손을 떼어 내고, 아이에게 입을 맞춘 뒤 테라스로 데리고 나갔다가 얼른 돌아왔다."]], [["“It’s time to start, though,”", "\"그래도 이제 나설 시간이네요.\""], ["said she, glancing at her watch.", "그녀는 시계를 흘끗 보며 말했다."], ["“How is it Betsy doesn’t come?...”", "\"베시는 어째서 안 오는 걸까요?……\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그래.\""], ["said Alexey Alexandrovitch, and getting up, he folded his hands and cracked his fingers.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 말하고는, 일어나서 두 손을 맞잡고 손가락을 우두둑 꺾었다."], ["“I’ve come to bring you some money, too, for nightingales, we know, can’t live on fairy tales,”", "\"당신한테 줄 돈도 가져왔소. 나이팅게일도 옛이야기만으로는 살 수 없는 법이니 말이오.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“You want it, I expect?”", "\"필요하지 않소?\""]], [["“No, I don’t ... yes, I do,”", "\"아니에요, 필요……"], ["she said, not looking at him, and crimsoning to the roots of her hair.", "아니, 필요해요.\" 그녀는 그를 보지 않은 채, 머리끝까지 새빨개지며 말했다."], ["“But you’ll come back here after the races, I suppose?”", "\"그런데 경마가 끝나면 다시 이리로 오시는 거죠?\""]], [["“Oh, yes!”", "\"오, 그럼!\""], ["answered Alexey Alexandrovitch.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 대답했다."], ["“And here’s the glory of Peterhof, Princess Tverskaya,”", "\"그리고 저기 페테르고프의 명물, 트베르스카야 공작부인이 오는군.\""], ["he added, looking out of the window at the elegant English carriage with the tiny seats placed extremely high.", "그는 창밖으로 자그마한 좌석이 유난히 높이 달린 우아한 영국식 마차를 내다보며 덧붙였다."], ["“What elegance!", "\"정말 우아하군!"], ["Charming!", "멋져!"], ["Well, let us be starting too, then.”", "자, 그럼 우리도 나섭시다.\""]], [["Princess Tverskaya did not get out of her carriage, but her groom, in high boots, a cape, and black hat, darted out at the entrance.", "트베르스카야 공작부인은 마차에서 내리지 않았으나, 긴 장화에 케이프, 검은 모자를 쓴 마부가 현관 앞으로 잽싸게 뛰어나왔다."]], [["“I’m going; good-bye!”", "\"전 가요. 안녕히 계세요!\""], ["said Anna, and kissing her son, she went up to Alexey Alexandrovitch and held out her hand to him.", "안나는 그렇게 말하고 아들에게 입을 맞춘 뒤, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에게 다가가 손을 내밀었다."], ["“It was ever so nice of you to come.”", "\"와 주셔서 정말 고마웠어요.\""]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch kissed her hand.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 그녀의 손에 입을 맞췄다."]], [["“Well, au revoir, then!", "\"그럼 au revoir예요!"], ["You’ll come back for some tea; that’s delightful!”", "차 마시러 다시 오시는 거죠. 정말 기뻐요!\""], ["she said, and went out, gay and radiant.", "그녀는 이렇게 말하고는 명랑하고 환한 얼굴로 나갔다."], ["But as soon as she no longer saw him, she was aware of the spot on her hand that his lips had touched, and she shuddered with repulsion.", "그러나 그가 더 이상 보이지 않게 되자, 그녀는 그의 입술이 닿았던 손등의 그 자리를 의식했고, 혐오감에 몸서리를 쳤다. * au revoir: 프랑스어로 '안녕히 가세요, 또 만나요'."]], [["# Chapter 28", "# Chapter 28"]], [["When Alexey Alexandrovitch reached the race-course, Anna was already sitting in the pavilion beside Betsy, in that pavilion where all the highest society had gathered.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 경마장에 도착했을 때, 안나는 이미 베치 옆자리, 최상류 사교계 사람들이 모두 모여 있는 그 관람석에 앉아 있었다."], ["She caught sight of her husband in the distance.", "그녀는 멀리서 남편의 모습을 알아보았다."], ["Two men, her husband and her lover, were the two centers of her existence, and unaided by her external senses she was aware of their nearness.", "남편과 연인, 이 두 남자가 그녀 존재의 두 중심이었고, 그녀는 외적인 감각의 도움 없이도 그들이 가까이 있음을 알아챘다."], ["She was aware of her husband approaching a long way off, and she could not help following him in the surging crowd in the midst of which he was moving.", "그녀는 멀리서부터 남편이 다가오는 것을 느꼈고, 그가 움직이고 있는 출렁이는 인파 속에서 자기도 모르게 그의 모습을 좇았다."], ["She watched his progress towards the pavilion, saw him now responding condescendingly to an ingratiating bow, now exchanging friendly, nonchalant greetings with his equals, now assiduously trying to catch the eye of some great one of this world, and taking off his big round hat that squeezed the tips of his ears.", "그녀는 그가 관람석을 향해 나아가는 모습을, 어떤 때는 아첨 섞인 인사에 거드름을 피우며 답례하고, 어떤 때는 자기와 같은 지위의 사람들과 친근하면서도 무심하게 인사를 주고받고, 또 어떤 때는 이 세상의 어느 거물의 눈에 들려고 애쓰며 귀 끝을 죄는 커다란 둥근 모자를 벗는 모습을 지켜보았다."], ["All these ways of his she knew, and all were hateful to her.", "이 모든 행동거지를 그녀는 알고 있었고, 그 모두가 그녀에게는 가증스러웠다."], ["“Nothing but ambition, nothing but the desire to get on, that’s all there is in his soul,”", "\"오직 야심뿐, 오직 출세하려는 욕망뿐이지, 저이 영혼 속에 있는 거라곤 그게 전부야.\""], ["she thought; “as for these lofty ideals, love of culture, religion, they are only so many tools for getting on.”", "그녀는 생각했다. \"저 고상한 이상이니, 교양에 대한 사랑이니, 종교니 하는 것들도 다 출세를 위한 도구일 뿐이고.\""]], [["From his glances towards the ladies’ pavilion (he was staring straight at her, but did not distinguish his wife in the sea of muslin, ribbons, feathers, parasols and flowers) she saw that he was looking for her, but she purposely avoided noticing him.", "여자들이 있는 관람석 쪽을 향한 그의 시선으로 보아(그는 그녀 쪽을 똑바로 바라보고 있었지만, 모슬린과 리본과 깃털과 양산과 꽃의 바다 속에서 아내를 분간해 내지는 못했다) 그녀는 그가 자기를 찾고 있다는 것을 알았지만, 일부러 그를 알아보지 않은 척했다."]], [["“Alexey Alexandrovitch!”", "\"알렉세이 알렉산드로비치!\""], ["Princess Betsy called to him; “I’m sure you don’t see your wife: here she is.”", "베치 공작부인이 그를 불렀다. \"부인이 안 보이시는 모양인데, 여기 계세요.\""]], [["He smiled his chilly smile.", "그는 차가운 미소를 지었다."]], [["“There’s so much splendor here that one’s eyes are dazzled,”", "\"여긴 화려한 게 너무 많아서 눈이 부실 지경입니다.\""], ["he said, and he went into the pavilion.", "그는 이렇게 말하고는 관람석으로 들어갔다."], ["He smiled to his wife as a man should smile on meeting his wife after only just parting from her, and greeted the princess and other acquaintances, giving to each what was due—that is to say, jesting with the ladies and dealing out friendly greetings among the men.", "그는 방금 헤어졌다가 다시 만난 아내에게 으레 짓는 그런 미소로 아내에게 미소를 짓고, 공작부인과 다른 지인들에게 인사하며 저마다에게 합당한 몫을 나누어 주었다. 다시 말해 부인들에게는 농담을 건네고, 남자들에게는 다정한 인사를 던졌던 것이다."], ["Below, near the pavilion, was standing an adjutant-general of whom Alexey Alexandrovitch had a high opinion, noted for his intelligence and culture.", "관람석 아래쪽 가까이에는 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 높이 평가하는 한 시종무관장이 서 있었는데, 그는 지성과 교양으로 이름난 사람이었다."], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch entered into conversation with him.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 그와 대화를 나누기 시작했다."]], [["There was an interval between the races, and so nothing hindered conversation.", "경주와 경주 사이에 휴식 시간이 있었으므로 대화를 방해하는 것은 아무것도 없었다."], ["The adjutant-general expressed his disapproval of races.", "시종무관장은 경마에 대한 못마땅함을 드러냈다."], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch replied defending them.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 경마를 옹호하며 응수했다."], ["Anna heard his high, measured tones, not losing one word, and every word struck her as false, and stabbed her ears with pain.", "안나는 그의 높고 절제된 어조를 한마디도 놓치지 않고 들었는데, 그 한마디 한마디가 거짓으로 느껴졌고 고통스럽게 그녀의 귀를 찔러 댔다."]], [["When the three-mile steeplechase was beginning, she bent forward and gazed with fixed eyes at Vronsky as he went up to his horse and mounted, and at the same time she heard that loathsome, never-ceasing voice of her husband.", "삼 마일 장애물 경주가 시작되자, 그녀는 몸을 앞으로 기울인 채 브론스키가 말에게 다가가 올라타는 모습을 뚫어지게 바라보았는데, 동시에 그녀는 남편의 그 혐오스럽고 끊임없이 이어지는 목소리를 들었다."], ["She was in an agony of terror for Vronsky, but a still greater agony was the never-ceasing, as it seemed to her, stream of her husband’s shrill voice with its familiar intonations.", "그녀는 브론스키 때문에 공포로 괴로워했지만, 더 큰 괴로움은, 그녀에게는 결코 그치지 않을 듯 들리던, 익숙한 억양이 밴 남편의 새된 목소리의 흐름이었다."]], [["“I’m a wicked woman, a lost woman,”", "\"나는 사악한 여자야, 타락한 여자지.\" 그녀는 생각했다."], ["she thought; “but I don’t like lying, I can’t endure falsehood, while as for him (her husband) it’s the breath of his life—falsehood.", "\"하지만 나는 거짓말을 싫어해, 거짓을 견딜 수가 없어. 그런데 저이(남편)에게는 거짓이 곧 생명의 호흡이야."], ["He knows all about it, he sees it all; what does he care if he can talk so calmly?", "저이는 모든 걸 다 알고, 다 보고 있어. 그러면서 저렇게 태연히 떠들 수 있다면 도대체 무슨 마음을 쓰고 있는 거지?"], ["If he were to kill me, if he were to kill Vronsky, I might respect him.", "차라리 저이가 나를 죽인다면, 브론스키를 죽인다면, 나는 저이를 존경할 수도 있을 거야."], ["No, all he wants is falsehood and propriety,”", "아냐, 저이가 원하는 건 오직 거짓과 체면뿐이야.\""], ["Anna said to herself, not considering exactly what it was she wanted of her husband, and how she would have liked to see him behave.", "안나는 자기가 남편에게 정확히 무엇을 바라는지, 그가 어떻게 행동하기를 원하는지는 헤아리지 못한 채 그렇게 혼잣말을 했다."], ["She did not understand either that Alexey Alexandrovitch’s peculiar loquacity that day, so exasperating to her, was merely the expression of his inward distress and uneasiness.", "그녀는 또한 그날 그토록 그녀의 화를 돋우던 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 유별난 수다가 실은 그의 내면의 고통과 불안의 표현일 뿐이라는 것도 이해하지 못했다."], ["As a child that has been hurt skips about, putting all his muscles into movement to drown the pain, in the same way Alexey Alexandrovitch needed mental exercise to drown the thoughts of his wife that in her presence and in Vronsky’s, and with the continual iteration of his name, would force themselves on his attention.", "다친 아이가 통증을 잊으려고 온몸의 근육을 움직여 가며 깡충거리듯이, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치도 아내에 대한 생각을 잊기 위한 정신적 운동이 필요했던 것이다. 아내가 곁에 있고 브론스키가 곁에 있고 그의 이름이 끊임없이 되풀이되는 가운데, 그 생각들은 억지로라도 그의 주의를 비집고 들어오려 했던 것이다."], ["And it was as natural for him to talk well and cleverly, as it is natural for a child to skip about.", "그리고 그에게는 말을 잘하고 영리하게 하는 것이, 마치 아이가 깡충거리는 것이 자연스러운 만큼이나 자연스러운 일이었다."], ["He was saying:", "그는 이렇게 말하고 있었다."]], [["“Danger in the races of officers, of cavalry men, is an essential element in the race.", "\"장교들, 그러니까 기병들의 경주에 따르는 위험은 경마의 본질적인 요소입니다."], ["If England can point to the most brilliant feats of cavalry in military history, it is simply owing to the fact that she has historically developed this force both in beasts and in men.", "영국이 군사(軍史)에서 가장 빛나는 기병의 위업을 자랑할 수 있는 것은, 단지 영국이 짐승과 사람 양쪽에서 이 힘을 역사적으로 발전시켜 온 덕분이지요."], ["Sport has, in my opinion, a great value, and as is always the case, we see nothing but what is most superficial.”", "스포츠란, 제 생각으로는, 대단한 가치를 지니고 있는데, 늘 그렇듯이 우리는 가장 피상적인 면밖에는 보지 못하는 겁니다.\""]], [["“It’s not superficial,”", "\"피상적이지 않은데요.\""], ["said Princess Tverskaya.", "트베르스카야 공작부인이 말했다."], ["“One of the officers, they say, has broken two ribs.”", "\"장교 하나가 갈비뼈 두 대가 부러졌다잖아요.\""]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch smiled his smile, which uncovered his teeth, but revealed nothing more.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 그 특유의 미소, 이를 드러내기는 하나 그 이상은 아무것도 드러내지 않는 미소를 지었다."]], [["“We’ll admit, princess, that that’s not superficial,”", "\"공작부인, 그게 피상적이지 않다는 건 인정하지요.\" 그가 말했다."], ["he said, “but internal.", "\"내적인 일이니까요."], ["But that’s not the point,”", "하지만 그게 핵심은 아닙니다.\""], ["and he turned again to the general with whom he was talking seriously; “we mustn’t forget that those who are taking part in the race are military men, who have chosen that career, and one must allow that every calling has its disagreeable side.", "그러고는 진지하게 이야기를 나누던 장군 쪽으로 다시 몸을 돌렸다. \"우리는 경주에 참가하는 자들이 그 길을 스스로 택한 군인들이라는 점을 잊어선 안 됩니다."], ["It forms an integral part of the duties of an officer.", "그리고 모든 직업에는 저마다 달갑지 않은 면이 있다는 것도 인정해야지요."], ["Low sports, such as prize-fighting or Spanish bull-fights, are a sign of barbarity.", "그것은 장교의 임무의 한 부분을 이루는 겁니다. 권투니 에스파냐 투우니 하는 저속한 스포츠는 야만의 표지입니다."], ["But specialized trials of skill are a sign of development.”", "하지만 전문적인 기량의 시험은 발전의 표지지요.\""]], [["“No, I shan’t come another time; it’s too upsetting,”", "\"아뇨, 저는 다음번엔 안 올래요. 너무 마음이 흔들려서요.\""], ["said Princess Betsy.", "베치 공작부인이 말했다."], ["“Isn’t it, Anna?”", "\"그렇지 않아, 안나?\""]], [["“It is upsetting, but one can’t tear oneself away,”", "\"마음이 흔들리긴 해도 눈을 뗄 수가 없네요.\""], ["said another lady.", "다른 부인이 말했다."], ["“If I’d been a Roman woman I should never have missed a single circus.”", "\"내가 로마 여인이었다면 원형 경기는 단 한 번도 놓치지 않았을 거예요.\""]], [["Anna said nothing, and keeping her opera-glass up, gazed always at the same spot.", "안나는 아무 말도 하지 않고, 오페라글라스를 든 채 줄곧 한곳만 바라보았다."]], [["At that moment a tall general walked through the pavilion.", "그 순간 키가 큰 장군 하나가 관람석을 가로질러 지나갔다."], ["Breaking off what he was saying, Alexey Alexandrovitch got up hurriedly, though with dignity, and bowed low to the general.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 하던 말을 멈추고, 위엄을 잃지 않으면서도 서둘러 일어나 그 장군에게 깊이 허리를 숙였다."]], [["“You’re not racing?”", "\"경마에 안 나가십니까?\""], ["the officer asked, chaffing him.", "장교가 그를 놀리듯 물었다."]], [["“My race is a harder one,”", "\"제 경주는 그보다 더 힘든 것이지요.\""], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch responded deferentially.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 공손하게 답했다."]], [["And though the answer meant nothing, the general looked as though he had heard a witty remark from a witty man, and fully relished la pointe de la sauce.", "그 대답에는 아무런 뜻도 없었지만, 장군은 마치 재치 있는 사람에게서 재치 있는 말을 들은 듯한 표정을 지으며 la pointe de la sauce를 한껏 음미했다."]], [["“There are two aspects,” Alexey Alexandrovitch resumed: “those who take part and those who look on; and love for such spectacles is an unmistakable proof of a low degree of development in the spectator, I admit, but....”", "* la pointe de la sauce: 프랑스어로 '소스의 묘미', 즉 농담의 핵심·맛을 뜻한다. \"여기엔 두 부류가 있습니다.\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 말을 이었다. \"직접 참가하는 자들과 구경하는 자들이지요. 그리고 그런 구경거리를 좋아한다는 것은 구경꾼의 발전 정도가 낮다는 틀림없는 증거인데, 인정합니다만……\""]], [["“Princess, bets!”", "\"공작부인, 내기요!\""], ["sounded Stepan Arkadyevitch’s voice from below, addressing Betsy.", "아래쪽에서 스테판 아르카지치가 베치를 향해 소리쳤다."], ["“Who’s your favorite?”", "\"어느 쪽이 좋으세요?\""]], [["“Anna and I are for Kuzovlev,”", "\"안나하고 나는 쿠조블레프 편이에요.\""], ["replied Betsy.", "베치가 대답했다."]], [["“I’m for Vronsky.", "\"난 브론스키 편입니다."], ["A pair of gloves?”", "장갑 한 켤레 거실까요?\""]], [["“Done!”", "\"좋아요!\""]], [["“But it is a pretty sight, isn’t it?”", "\"하지만 멋진 광경이긴 하지 않습니까?\""]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch paused while there was talking about him, but he began again directly.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 자기에 대한 이야기가 오가는 동안 입을 다물었다가, 곧바로 다시 말을 시작했다."]], [["“I admit that manly sports do not....”", "\"남자다운 스포츠가……라는 건 인정합니다만.\""], ["he was continuing.", "그는 말을 이어 가고 있었다."]], [["But at that moment the racers started, and all conversation ceased.", "그러나 바로 그 순간 경주마들이 출발했고, 모든 대화가 그쳤다."], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch too was silent, and everyone stood up and turned towards the stream.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치도 입을 다물었고, 모두가 일어나 물길 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸다."], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch took no interest in the race, and so he did not watch the racers, but fell listlessly to scanning the spectators with his weary eyes.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 경주에 관심이 없었으므로 경주마들을 보지 않고, 지친 눈으로 무심하게 관중을 훑어보기 시작했다."], ["His eyes rested upon Anna.", "그의 눈길이 안나에게 멈추었다."]], [["Her face was white and set.", "그녀의 얼굴은 창백하게 굳어 있었다."], ["She was obviously seeing nothing and no one but one man.", "그녀가 오직 한 사람 외에는 아무것도, 아무도 보고 있지 않다는 것은 명백했다."], ["Her hand had convulsively clutched her fan, and she held her breath.", "그녀의 손은 발작적으로 부채를 움켜쥐고 있었고, 그녀는 숨을 멈추고 있었다."], ["He looked at her and hastily turned away, scrutinizing other faces.", "그는 그녀를 바라보다가 황급히 눈을 돌려 다른 얼굴들을 살폈다."]], [["“But here’s this lady too, and others very much moved as well; it’s very natural,”", "\"하지만 이 부인뿐 아니라 다른 사람들도 무척 동요하고 있어. 지극히 자연스러운 일이지.\""], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch told himself.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 혼잣말을 했다."], ["He tried not to look at her, but unconsciously his eyes were drawn to her.", "그는 그녀를 보지 않으려 했지만, 무의식중에 그의 눈은 그녀에게로 끌려갔다."], ["He examined that face again, trying not to read what was so plainly written on it, and against his own will, with horror read on it what he did not want to know.", "그는 그 얼굴에 그토록 또렷이 적혀 있는 것을 읽지 않으려 애쓰며 다시 그 얼굴을 살폈고, 자기 의지와는 반대로, 끔찍한 심정으로 자기가 알고 싶지 않던 것을 그 얼굴에서 읽어 냈다."]], [["The first fall—Kuzovlev’s, at the stream—agitated everyone, but Alexey Alexandrovitch saw distinctly on Anna’s pale, triumphant face that the man she was watching had not fallen.", "첫 번째 낙마, 즉 물길에서의 쿠조블레프의 낙마는 모두를 동요시켰지만, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 안나의 창백하고 의기양양한 얼굴에서 그녀가 지켜보던 그 남자가 떨어지지 않았음을 또렷이 보았다."], ["When, after Mahotin and Vronsky had cleared the worst barrier, the next officer had been thrown straight on his head at it and fatally injured, and a shudder of horror passed over the whole public, Alexey Alexandrovitch saw that Anna did not even notice it, and had some difficulty in realizing what they were talking of about her.", "마호틴과 브론스키가 가장 험한 장애물을 넘은 뒤, 다음 장교가 그 장애물에서 머리부터 곤두박질쳐 치명상을 입어 온 관중 사이로 공포의 전율이 번졌을 때에도, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 안나가 그것을 알아채지조차 못한다는 것을 보았고, 사람들이 자기 주위에서 무슨 이야기를 하고 있는지 알아듣는 데에 애를 먹었다."], ["But more and more often, and with greater persistence, he watched her.", "그러나 그는 점점 더 자주, 점점 더 집요하게 그녀를 지켜보았다."], ["Anna, wholly engrossed as she was with the race, became aware of her husband’s cold eyes fixed upon her from one side.", "경주에 온통 빠져 있던 안나는 옆에서 자기에게 고정된 남편의 차가운 눈길을 알아챘다."]], [["She glanced round for an instant, looked inquiringly at him, and with a slight frown turned away again.", "그녀는 한순간 돌아보더니 의아한 눈으로 그를 바라보다가, 가볍게 미간을 찌푸리며 다시 고개를 돌렸다."]], [["“Ah, I don’t care!”", "\"아, 어찌 되든 상관없어!\""], ["she seemed to say to him, and she did not once glance at him again.", "그녀는 그에게 그렇게 말하는 듯했고, 그 뒤로는 단 한 번도 그를 돌아보지 않았다."]], [["The race was an unlucky one, and of the seventeen officers who rode in it more than half were thrown and hurt.", "이 경주는 불운한 경주여서, 출전한 열일곱 명의 장교 가운데 절반이 넘는 수가 낙마하여 다쳤다."], ["Towards the end of the race everyone was in a state of agitation, which was intensified by the fact that the Tsar was displeased.", "경주가 끝나 갈 무렵에는 모두가 동요에 휩싸였고, 그 동요는 황제께서 언짢아하셨다는 사실로 더욱 깊어졌다."]], [["# Chapter 29", "# Chapter 29"]], [["Everyone was loudly expressing disapprobation, everyone was repeating a phrase someone had uttered—“The lions and gladiators will be the next thing,”", "모두가 큰 소리로 못마땅함을 드러내고 있었고, 누군가 내뱉은 한마디를 모두가 따라 외고 있었으며—\"이러다 사자와 검투사까지 나오겠군\"—모두가 끔찍하다는 듯이 느끼고 있었다."], ["and everyone was feeling horrified; so that when Vronsky fell to the ground, and Anna moaned aloud, there was nothing very out of the way in it.", "그래서 브론스키가 땅에 떨어지고 안나가 큰 소리로 신음을 토했을 때도, 거기에 그리 별스러운 데는 없었다."], ["But afterwards a change came over Anna’s face which really was beyond decorum.", "그러나 그 후 안나의 얼굴에 일어난 변화는 정말이지 예법을 벗어난 것이었다."], ["She utterly lost her head.", "그녀는 완전히 제정신을 잃었다."], ["She began fluttering like a caged bird, at one moment would have got up and moved away, at the next turned to Betsy.", "새장에 갇힌 새처럼 안절부절못하기 시작해, 어느 순간에는 자리에서 일어나 떠나려 하다가도, 다음 순간에는 베시 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸다."]], [["“Let us go, let us go!”", "\"가요, 우리 가요!\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["But Betsy did not hear her.", "그러나 베시는 그녀의 말을 듣지 못했다."], ["She was bending down, talking to a general who had come up to her.", "베시는 몸을 숙이고, 자기에게 다가온 어느 장군과 이야기를 나누고 있었다."]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch went up to Anna and courteously offered her his arm.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 안나에게 다가가 정중하게 자기 팔을 내밀었다."]], [["“Let us go, if you like,”", "\"원한다면 갑시다.\""], ["he said in French, but Anna was listening to the general and did not notice her husband.", "그가 프랑스어로 말했지만, 안나는 장군의 말에 귀를 기울이고 있어서 남편을 알아차리지 못했다."]], [["“He’s broken his leg too, so they say,”", "\"저 사람도 다리가 부러졌다더군요.\""], ["the general was saying.", "장군이 말하고 있었다."], ["“This is beyond everything.”", "\"이건 정말 너무하지 않습니까.\""]], [["Without answering her husband, Anna lifted her opera-glass and gazed towards the place where Vronsky had fallen; but it was so far off, and there was such a crowd of people about it, that she could make out nothing.", "안나는 남편에게 대답도 하지 않은 채 오페라글라스를 들어 올려 브론스키가 떨어진 자리 쪽을 바라보았다. 그러나 너무 멀고, 그 주위에 사람들이 잔뜩 몰려 있어서, 그녀는 아무것도 분간할 수 없었다."], ["She laid down the opera-glass, and would have moved away, but at that moment an officer galloped up and made some announcement to the Tsar.", "그녀는 오페라글라스를 내려놓고 떠나려 했지만, 바로 그 순간 한 장교가 말을 달려와 황제에게 무언가를 알렸다."], ["Anna craned forward, listening.", "안나는 몸을 앞으로 빼고 귀를 기울였다."]], [["“Stiva!", "\"스티바!"], ["Stiva!”", "스티바!\""], ["she cried to her brother.", "그녀가 오빠를 향해 소리쳤다."]], [["But her brother did not hear her.", "그러나 오빠는 그녀의 말을 듣지 못했다."], ["Again she would have moved away.", "그녀는 다시 자리를 뜨려고 했다."]], [["“Once more I offer you my arm if you want to be going,”", "\"가고 싶다면, 한 번 더 내 팔을 빌려주겠소.\""], ["said Alexey Alexandrovitch, reaching towards her hand.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 그녀의 손을 향해 손을 뻗으며 말했다."]], [["She drew back from him with aversion, and without looking in his face answered:", "그녀는 혐오감을 느끼며 그에게서 몸을 뺐고, 그의 얼굴은 쳐다보지도 않은 채 대답했다."]], [["“No, no, let me be, I’ll stay.”", "\"아니요, 아니요, 내버려 두세요, 여기 있을 거예요.\""]], [["She saw now that from the place of Vronsky’s accident an officer was running across the course towards the pavilion.", "그녀는 이제 브론스키가 사고를 당한 자리에서 한 장교가 트랙을 가로질러 관람석 쪽으로 달려오는 것을 보았다."], ["Betsy waved her handkerchief to him.", "베시가 그에게 손수건을 흔들었다."], ["The officer brought the news that the rider was not killed, but the horse had broken its back.", "장교는 기수는 죽지 않았지만 말이 등뼈가 부러졌다는 소식을 가져왔다."]], [["On hearing this Anna sat down hurriedly, and hid her face in her fan.", "이 말을 듣고 안나는 황급히 자리에 앉아 부채로 얼굴을 가렸다."], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch saw that she was weeping, and could not control her tears, nor even the sobs that were shaking her bosom.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 그녀가 울고 있는 것을, 그리고 그녀가 눈물도, 가슴을 들썩이게 하는 흐느낌조차 억누르지 못하고 있다는 것을 보았다."], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch stood so as to screen her, giving her time to recover herself.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 그녀를 가려 주려는 듯이 서서, 그녀에게 마음을 추스를 시간을 주었다."]], [["“For the third time I offer you my arm,”", "\"세 번째로 내 팔을 빌려주겠소.\""], ["he said to her after a little time, turning to her.", "그가 잠시 후 그녀를 돌아보며 말했다."], ["Anna gazed at him and did not know what to say.", "안나는 그를 멍하니 바라보며 무슨 말을 해야 할지 몰랐다."], ["Princess Betsy came to her rescue.", "베시 공작부인이 그녀를 구하러 나섰다."]], [["“No, Alexey Alexandrovitch; I brought Anna and I promised to take her home,”", "\"아니에요, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치. 안나는 제가 데려왔으니 제가 집까지 데려다주기로 했어요.\""], ["put in Betsy.", "베시가 끼어들었다."]], [["“Excuse me, princess,”", "\"실례합니다만, 공작부인.\" 그가 정중하게 미소를 지으면서도 그녀의 얼굴을 아주 단호하게 들여다보며 말했다."], ["he said, smiling courteously but looking her very firmly in the face, “but I see that Anna’s not very well, and I wish her to come home with me.”", "\"보아하니 안나의 몸이 좋지 않은 듯하군요. 나와 함께 집으로 갔으면 합니다.\""]], [["Anna looked about her in a frightened way, got up submissively, and laid her hand on her husband’s arm.", "안나는 겁먹은 듯이 주위를 둘러보고는, 순순히 일어나 남편의 팔에 손을 얹었다."]], [["“I’ll send to him and find out, and let you know,”", "\"그에게 사람을 보내 알아보고 당신에게 알려 줄게요.\""], ["Betsy whispered to her.", "베시가 그녀에게 속삭였다."]], [["As they left the pavilion, Alexey Alexandrovitch, as always, talked to those he met, and Anna had, as always, to talk and answer; but she was utterly beside herself, and moved hanging on her husband’s arm as though in a dream.", "관람석을 나서면서, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 늘 그렇듯 마주치는 사람들과 이야기를 나누었고, 안나도 늘 그렇듯 이야기하고 대답을 해야 했다. 그러나 그녀는 완전히 넋이 나가 있었고, 꿈을 꾸는 듯 남편의 팔에 매달려 움직였다."]], [["“Is he killed or not?", "'그가 죽었을까, 아닐까?"], ["Is it true?", "정말일까?"], ["Will he come or not?", "그가 올까, 안 올까?"], ["Shall I see him today?”", "오늘 그를 볼 수 있을까?'"], ["she was thinking.", "그녀는 생각하고 있었다."]], [["She took her seat in her husband’s carriage in silence, and in silence drove out of the crowd of carriages.", "그녀는 말없이 남편의 마차에 올랐고, 말없이 마차들의 무리에서 빠져나왔다."], ["In spite of all he had seen, Alexey Alexandrovitch still did not allow himself to consider his wife’s real condition.", "자기가 본 모든 것에도 불구하고, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 여전히 아내의 진짜 상태를 헤아려 보려 하지 않았다."], ["He merely saw the outward symptoms.", "그는 겉으로 드러난 증상만 보았을 뿐이다."], ["He saw that she was behaving unbecomingly, and considered it his duty to tell her so.", "그는 그녀가 부적절하게 처신하고 있음을 보았고, 그것을 그녀에게 말해 주는 것이 자기 의무라고 여겼다."], ["But it was very difficult for him not to say more, to tell her nothing but that.", "그러나 그 이상은 말하지 않고, 오직 그것만 말하기란 그에게 무척 어려운 일이었다."], ["He opened his mouth to tell her she had behaved unbecomingly, but he could not help saying something utterly different.", "그는 그녀가 부적절하게 처신했다고 말하려고 입을 열었으나, 전혀 다른 말을 하지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["“What an inclination we all have, though, for these cruel spectacles,”", "\"그래도 우리 모두 이런 잔혹한 구경거리를 얼마나 좋아하는지.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“I observe....”", "\"내가 보기엔……\""]], [["“Eh?", "\"네?"], ["I don’t understand,”", "무슨 말인지 모르겠어요.\""], ["said Anna contemptuously.", "안나가 경멸하듯 말했다."]], [["He was offended, and at once began to say what he had meant to say.", "그는 모욕감을 느꼈고, 곧바로 하려던 말을 하기 시작했다."]], [["“I am obliged to tell you,”", "\"당신에게 할 말이 있소.\""], ["he began.", "그가 입을 뗐다."]], [["“So now we are to have it out,”", "'이제 결판을 내려는 거구나.'"], ["she thought, and she felt frightened.", "그녀는 생각했고, 두려워졌다."]], [["“I am obliged to tell you that your behavior has been unbecoming today,”", "\"오늘 당신의 행동이 부적절했다는 말을 하지 않을 수 없소.\""], ["he said to her in French.", "그가 프랑스어로 그녀에게 말했다."]], [["“In what way has my behavior been unbecoming?”", "\"내 행동이 어떻게 부적절했다는 거죠?\""], ["she said aloud, turning her head swiftly and looking him straight in the face, not with the bright expression that seemed covering something, but with a look of determination, under which she concealed with difficulty the dismay she was feeling.", "그녀는 큰 소리로 말하며 재빨리 고개를 돌려 그의 얼굴을 똑바로 바라보았다. 무언가를 감추는 듯한 그 밝은 표정이 아니라, 자기가 느끼는 당혹감을 가까스로 감춘 결연한 눈빛으로."]], [["“Mind,”", "\"조심하시오.\""], ["he said, pointing to the open window opposite the coachman.", "그가 마부 맞은편의 열린 창문을 가리키며 말했다."]], [["He got up and pulled up the window.", "그는 일어나서 창문을 끌어 올렸다."]], [["“What did you consider unbecoming?”", "\"내 행동에서 뭐가 부적절했다는 거죠?\""], ["she repeated.", "그녀가 되물었다."]], [["“The despair you were unable to conceal at the accident to one of the riders.”", "\"기수 중 한 사람이 사고를 당했을 때 당신이 감추지 못한 그 절망 말이오.\""]], [["He waited for her to answer, but she was silent, looking straight before her.", "그는 그녀가 대답하기를 기다렸지만, 그녀는 입을 다문 채 앞만 똑바로 응시하고 있었다."]], [["“I have already begged you so to conduct yourself in society that even malicious tongues can find nothing to say against you.", "\"나는 이미 당신에게, 아무리 악의에 찬 입들이라도 당신에 대해 흠잡을 거리를 찾지 못하도록 사교계에서 처신하라고 부탁했소."], ["There was a time when I spoke of your inward attitude, but I am not speaking of that now.", "한때는 당신의 내면적 태도에 대해 말한 적이 있었지만, 지금은 그것을 말하는 게 아니오."], ["Now I speak only of your external attitude.", "지금은 오직 당신의 외면적 태도에 대해서만 말하는 거요."], ["You have behaved improperly, and I would wish it not to occur again.”", "당신은 부적절하게 처신했고, 나는 그런 일이 다시는 없기를 바라오.\""]], [["She did not hear half of what he was saying; she felt panic-stricken before him, and was thinking whether it was true that Vronsky was not killed.", "그녀는 그가 하는 말의 절반도 듣지 못했다. 그녀는 그 앞에서 공포에 사로잡혀 있었고, 브론스키가 죽지 않았다는 게 사실인지를 생각하고 있었다."], ["Was it of him they were speaking when they said the rider was unhurt, but the horse had broken its back?", "기수는 다치지 않았는데 말이 등뼈가 부러졌다고 했을 때, 그들이 말한 것이 그였을까?"], ["She merely smiled with a pretense of irony when he finished, and made no reply, because she had not heard what he said.", "그가 말을 마쳤을 때 그녀는 짐짓 비꼬는 척하며 미소 지었을 뿐 아무 대답도 하지 않았다. 그의 말을 듣지 못했기 때문이다."], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch had begun to speak boldly, but as he realized plainly what he was speaking of, the dismay she was feeling infected him too.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 대담하게 말을 시작했지만, 자기가 무엇에 대해 말하고 있는지를 또렷이 깨닫게 되자, 그녀가 느끼고 있던 당혹감이 그에게도 옮아 왔다."], ["He saw the smile, and a strange misapprehension came over him.", "그는 그 미소를 보았고, 기묘한 오해에 사로잡혔다."]], [["“She is smiling at my suspicions.", "'아내가 내 의심을 비웃고 있어."], ["Yes, she will tell me directly what she told me before; that there is no foundation for my suspicions, that it’s absurd.”", "그래, 곧 전에 했던 말을 또 하겠지. 내 의심에는 아무 근거가 없으며, 터무니없는 일이라고.'"]], [["At that moment, when the revelation of everything was hanging over him, there was nothing he expected so much as that she would answer mockingly as before that his suspicions were absurd and utterly groundless.", "모든 것이 폭로될 위기가 그의 머리 위에 드리워진 그 순간, 그가 무엇보다 바란 것은 그녀가 전처럼 비웃듯이 그의 의심이 터무니없고 전혀 근거가 없다고 대답해 주는 것이었다."], ["So terrible to him was what he knew that now he was ready to believe anything.", "그가 알고 있는 것이 너무도 끔찍해서, 이제 그는 무엇이든 믿을 준비가 되어 있었다."], ["But the expression of her face, scared and gloomy, did not now promise even deception.", "그러나 겁에 질리고 침울한 그녀의 표정은 이제 거짓말조차 약속해 주지 않았다."]], [["“Possibly I was mistaken,”", "\"어쩌면 내가 잘못 본 것일 수도 있겠지.\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["“If so, I beg your pardon.”", "\"그렇다면 용서를 빌겠소.\""]], [["“No, you were not mistaken,”", "\"아니요, 당신은 잘못 보지 않았어요.\""], ["she said deliberately, looking desperately into his cold face.", "그녀는 그의 차가운 얼굴을 절망스럽게 바라보며 또박또박 말했다."], ["“You were not mistaken.", "\"당신은 잘못 보지 않았어요."], ["I was, and I could not help being in despair.", "잘못 본 건 나였고, 나는 절망하지 않을 수 없었어요."], ["I hear you, but I am thinking of him.", "당신 말이 들리긴 하지만, 나는 그 사람을 생각하고 있어요."], ["I love him, I am his mistress; I can’t bear you; I’m afraid of you, and I hate you....", "나는 그를 사랑해요, 나는 그의 정부예요. 나는 당신을 견딜 수 없어요."], ["You can do what you like to me.”", "나는 당신이 두렵고, 당신이 미워요…… 나한테 마음대로 하세요.\""]], [["And dropping back into the corner of the carriage, she broke into sobs, hiding her face in her hands.", "그러고는 마차 구석에 몸을 던지듯 기대며, 두 손에 얼굴을 묻고 흐느껴 울기 시작했다."], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch did not stir, and kept looking straight before him.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 꼼짝도 하지 않은 채, 계속 앞만 똑바로 바라보았다."], ["But his whole face suddenly bore the solemn rigidity of the dead, and his expression did not change during the whole time of the drive home.", "그러나 그의 온 얼굴이 갑자기 죽은 사람처럼 엄숙하게 굳어 버렸고, 집으로 돌아오는 내내 그의 표정은 변하지 않았다."], ["On reaching the house he turned his head to her, still with the same expression.", "집에 다다랐을 때, 그는 여전히 그 표정 그대로 그녀에게 고개를 돌렸다."]], [["“Very well!", "\"좋소!"], ["But I expect a strict observance of the external forms of propriety till such time”—his voice shook—“as I may take measures to secure my honor and communicate them to you.”", "하지만 내가 내 명예를 지킬 방도를 마련해서 그것을 당신에게 알릴 때까지는\"—그의 목소리가 떨렸다—\"외면적인 예법만큼은 엄격히 지켜 주기를 바라오.\""]], [["He got out first and helped her to get out.", "그는 먼저 내려서 그녀가 내리는 것을 도왔다."], ["Before the servants he pressed her hand, took his seat in the carriage, and drove back to Petersburg.", "하인들 앞에서 그는 그녀의 손을 꼭 쥐고는, 마차에 올라 페테르부르크로 돌아갔다."], ["Immediately afterwards a footman came from Princess Betsy and brought Anna a note.", "그 직후 베시 공작부인이 보낸 하인이 와서 안나에게 쪽지를 전했다."]], [["“I sent to Alexey to find out how he is, and he writes me he is quite well and unhurt, but in despair.”", "\"그가 어떤지 알아보려고 알렉세이에게 사람을 보냈더니, 아주 멀쩡하고 다친 데도 없지만 절망에 빠져 있다는 답장이 왔어요.\""]], [["“So he will be here,”", "'그러니까 그가 여기로 오겠구나.'"], ["she thought.", "그녀는 생각했다."], ["“What a good thing I told him all!”", "'그에게 다 털어놓길 정말 잘했어!'"]], [["She glanced at her watch.", "그녀는 시계를 흘끗 보았다."], ["She had still three hours to wait, and the memories of their last meeting set her blood in flame.", "아직 세 시간이나 기다려야 했고, 마지막으로 만났던 기억이 그녀의 피를 들끓게 했다."]], [["“My God, how light it is!", "'아, 어쩜 이렇게 환할까!"], ["It’s dreadful, but I do love to see his face, and I do love this fantastic light....", "끔찍하긴 해도, 나는 정말 그의 얼굴을 보는 게 좋아, 그리고 이 환상적인 빛이 정말 좋아……"], ["My husband!", "내 남편!"], ["Oh!", "아!"], ["yes....", "그래……"], ["Well, thank God!", "그래도, 다행이야!"], ["everything’s over with him.”", "그 사람과는 모든 게 끝났으니.'"]], [["# Chapter 30", "# Chapter 30"]], [["In the little German watering-place to which the Shtcherbatskys had betaken themselves, as in all places indeed where people are gathered together, the usual process, as it were, of the crystallization of society went on, assigning to each member of that society a definite and unalterable place.", "쉬체르바츠키 가족이 머물게 된 독일의 작은 온천 휴양지에서도, 사람들이 모여드는 다른 모든 곳에서와 마찬가지로, 말하자면 사교계가 결정(結晶)을 이루어 가는 그 익숙한 과정이 진행되어, 그 사교계의 구성원 각자에게 분명하고 변할 수 없는 자리가 정해지고 있었다."], ["Just as the particle of water in frost, definitely and unalterably, takes the special form of the crystal of snow, so each new person that arrived at the springs was at once placed in his special place.", "서리 속의 물방울이 분명하고 변할 수 없이 눈송이라는 특별한 결정의 형태를 띠게 되듯이, 온천에 새로 도착한 사람도 저마다 곧장 자기만의 특별한 자리에 놓이게 되었다."]], [["Fürst Shtcherbatsky, sammt Gemahlin und Tochter, by the apartments they took, and from their name and from the friends they made, were immediately crystallized into a definite place marked out for them.", "Fürst 쉬체르바츠키는 sammt Gemahlin und Tochter, 그들이 잡은 숙소로 보나, 그들의 이름으로 보나, 그들이 사귄 친구들로 보나, 즉시 그들에게 정해진 분명한 자리에 결정처럼 굳어졌다."]], [["There was visiting the watering-place that year a real German Fürstin, in consequence of which the crystallizing process went on more vigorously than ever.", "* Fürst: 독일어로 '공작'. sammt Gemahlin und Tochter: 독일어로 '부인과 딸을 동반한'."], ["Princess Shtcherbatskaya wished, above everything, to present her daughter to this German princess, and the day after their arrival she duly performed this rite.", "그해 그 휴양지에는 진짜 독일인 Fürstin이 와 있었고, 그 덕분에 결정화 과정은 그 어느 때보다 활발하게 진행되었다."], ["Kitty made a low and graceful curtsey in the very simple, that is to say, very elegant frock that had been ordered her from Paris.", "쉬체르바츠카야 공작 부인은 무엇보다도 자기 딸을 이 독일 공작 부인에게 소개하고 싶어 했고, 도착한 다음 날 격식에 맞게 그 의식을 치렀다."], ["The German princess said, “I hope the roses will soon come back to this pretty little face,”", "키티는 파리에서 주문해 온, 아주 수수한, 다시 말해 아주 우아한 드레스를 입고 낮고 우아하게 무릎을 굽혀 인사했다."], ["and for the Shtcherbatskys certain definite lines of existence were at once laid down from which there was no departing.", "독일 공작 부인은 \"이 예쁘고 작은 얼굴에 곧 장밋빛이 돌아오길 바랍니다\"라고 말했고, 그것으로 쉬체르바츠키 가족에게는 결코 벗어날 수 없는 일정한 존재의 선들이 즉시 그어졌다."], ["The Shtcherbatskys made the acquaintance too of the family of an English Lady Somebody, and of a German countess and her son, wounded in the last war, and of a learned Swede, and of M. Canut and his sister.", "쉬체르바츠키 가족은 어느 영국인 레이디 아무개의 가족과도, 또 독일인 백작 부인과 지난 전쟁에서 부상당한 그녀의 아들과도, 어느 학식 있는 스웨덴 사람과도, 그리고 카뉘 씨와 그의 누이와도 알게 되었다."], ["But yet inevitably the Shtcherbatskys were thrown most into the society of a Moscow lady, Marya Yevgenyevna Rtishtcheva and her daughter, whom Kitty disliked, because she had fallen ill, like herself, over a love affair, and a Moscow colonel, whom Kitty had known from childhood, and always seen in uniform and epaulets, and who now, with his little eyes and his open neck and flowered cravat, was uncommonly ridiculous and tedious, because there was no getting rid of him.", "그러나 그럼에도 쉬체르바츠키 가족은 어쩔 수 없이 모스크바 부인 마리야 예브게니예브나 르티셰바와 그 딸의 무리 속으로 가장 많이 끌려들어 가게 되었다. 키티가 그 딸을 싫어했던 까닭은, 그 딸도 자기처럼 연애 사건으로 병이 났기 때문이었다."], ["When all this was so firmly established, Kitty began to be very much bored, especially as the prince went away to Carlsbad and she was left alone with her mother. She took no interest in the people she knew, feeling that nothing fresh would come of them.", "그리고 키티가 어릴 적부터 알고 지냈고 늘 군복에 견장을 단 모습으로만 보아 온 어느 모스크바 대령의 무리에도 끌려들었는데, 그는 지금 작은 눈에 목을 드러내고 꽃무늬 넥타이를 두른 모습이 유난히 우스꽝스럽고 따분했으며, 도무지 떼어 낼 수가 없었기 때문이었다."], ["Her chief mental interest in the watering-place consisted in watching and making theories about the people she did not know.", "이 모든 것이 그렇게 단단히 자리를 잡자, 키티는 몹시 따분해지기 시작했는데, 특히 공작이 카를스바트로 떠나 버려 어머니와 단둘이 남게 된 뒤로 더욱 그러했다."], ["It was characteristic of Kitty that she always imagined everything in people in the most favorable light possible, especially so in those she did not know.", "그녀는 자기가 아는 사람들에게는 아무 흥미도 느끼지 못했고, 그들에게서 새로운 것이라곤 아무것도 나오지 않으리라고 여겼다. 이 휴양지에서 그녀의 주된 정신적 관심사는 자기가 모르는 사람들을 관찰하며 그들에 관해 이런저런 짐작을 세우는 것이었다."], ["And now as she made surmises as to who people were, what were their relations to one another, and what they were like, Kitty endowed them with the most marvelous and noble characters, and found confirmation of her idea in her observations.", "키티에게는 사람들의 모든 것을 언제나 가능한 한 가장 호의적인 빛 속에서 상상하는 특징이 있었는데, 특히 모르는 사람들에 대해서는 더욱 그러했다. 그래서 지금 그녀는 저 사람들이 누구인지, 서로 어떤 관계인지, 또 어떤 사람들인지 추측하면서, 그들에게 더없이 놀랍고 고귀한 성품을 부여했고, 자신의 관찰 속에서 그 생각을 확인하는 증거를 찾아냈다."]], [["Of these people the one that attracted her most was a Russian girl who had come to the watering-place with an invalid Russian lady, Madame Stahl, as everyone called her.", "이 사람들 가운데 그녀의 마음을 가장 끈 것은, 다들 슈탈 부인이라고 부르는 병약한 러시아 부인과 함께 휴양지에 온 어느 러시아 처녀였다."], ["Madame Stahl belonged to the highest society, but she was so ill that she could not walk, and only on exceptionally fine days made her appearance at the springs in an invalid carriage.", "슈탈 부인은 최상류 사교계에 속했지만, 너무 아파서 걷지도 못했고, 유난히 날이 좋은 날에만 환자용 마차를 타고 온천에 나타났다."], ["But it was not so much from ill-health as from pride—so Princess Shtcherbatskaya interpreted it—that Madame Stahl had not made the acquaintance of anyone among the Russians there.", "그러나 슈탈 부인이 그곳의 러시아 사람들 가운데 누구와도 사귀지 않은 것은, 쉬체르바츠카야 공작 부인이 해석하기로는, 병약함 때문이라기보다는 오만함 때문이었다."], ["The Russian girl looked after Madame Stahl, and besides that, she was, as Kitty observed, on friendly terms with all the invalids who were seriously ill, and there were many of them at the springs, and looked after them in the most natural way.", "그 러시아 처녀는 슈탈 부인을 돌보았고, 그뿐 아니라 키티가 보기에는 온천에 많이 와 있던, 중병에 걸린 환자들 모두와 친하게 지내며 더없이 자연스러운 태도로 그들을 보살폈다."], ["This Russian girl was not, as Kitty gathered, related to Madame Stahl, nor was she a paid attendant.", "키티가 알아낸 바로는, 이 러시아 처녀는 슈탈 부인의 친척도 아니었고 보수를 받는 시중꾼도 아니었다."], ["Madame Stahl called her Varenka, and other people called her “Mademoiselle Varenka.”", "슈탈 부인은 그녀를 바렌카라고 불렀고, 다른 사람들은 그녀를 \"마드무아젤 바렌카\"라고 불렀다."], ["Apart from the interest Kitty took in this girl’s relations with Madame Stahl and with other unknown persons, Kitty, as often happened, felt an inexplicable attraction to Mademoiselle Varenka, and was aware when their eyes met that she too liked her.", "이 처녀가 슈탈 부인이며 다른 낯선 사람들과 맺은 관계에 키티가 느낀 흥미와는 별개로, 키티는 흔히 그러하듯 마드무아젤 바렌카에게 설명할 수 없는 끌림을 느꼈고, 두 사람의 눈이 마주칠 때면 그녀 역시 자기를 좋아한다는 것을 알아챘다."]], [["Of Mademoiselle Varenka one would not say that she had passed her first youth, but she was, as it were, a creature without youth; she might have been taken for nineteen or for thirty.", "마드무아젤 바렌카를 두고 첫 청춘이 지났다고는 할 수 없겠지만, 그녀는 말하자면 청춘이 없는 존재였다. 열아홉 살로 볼 수도 있었고 서른 살로 볼 수도 있었다."], ["If her features were criticized separately, she was handsome rather than plain, in spite of the sickly hue of her face.", "이목구비를 따로따로 뜯어본다면, 병색이 도는 얼굴빛에도 불구하고 못생겼다기보다는 어여뻤다."], ["She would have been a good figure, too, if it had not been for her extreme thinness and the size of her head, which was too large for her medium height.", "또한 지나치게 마른 몸과, 중간쯤 되는 키에 비해 너무 큰 머리만 아니었다면 몸매도 좋았을 것이다."], ["But she was not likely to be attractive to men.", "그러나 그녀가 남자들의 마음을 끌 것 같지는 않았다."], ["She was like a fine flower, already past its bloom and without fragrance, though the petals were still unwithered.", "그녀는 한창때가 이미 지나 향기를 잃었으나 꽃잎만은 아직 시들지 않은, 아름다운 한 송이 꽃 같았다."], ["Moreover, she would have been unattractive to men also from the lack of just what Kitty had too much of—of the suppressed fire of vitality, and the consciousness of her own attractiveness.", "게다가 그녀는, 키티에게는 지나치게 많았던 바로 그것—억눌린 생명력의 불꽃과 자기 매력에 대한 의식—이 없다는 점에서도 남자들의 마음을 끌지 못할 것이었다."]], [["She always seemed absorbed in work about which there could be no doubt, and so it seemed she could not take interest in anything outside it.", "그녀는 언제나 의심의 여지가 없는 어떤 일에 몰두해 있는 듯 보였고, 그래서 그 밖의 무엇에도 흥미를 가질 수 없는 것처럼 보였다."], ["It was just this contrast with her own position that was for Kitty the great attraction of Mademoiselle Varenka.", "키티에게 마드무아젤 바렌카의 커다란 매력은 바로 자기 처지와 이루는 이러한 대조에 있었다."], ["Kitty felt that in her, in her manner of life, she would find an example of what she was now so painfully seeking: interest in life, a dignity in life—apart from the worldly relations of girls with men, which so revolted Kitty, and appeared to her now as a shameful hawking about of goods in search of a purchaser.", "키티는 그녀에게서, 그녀의 삶의 방식에서, 자기가 지금 그토록 괴롭게 찾고 있는 것의 본보기를 발견하리라고 느꼈다. 그것은 삶에 대한 흥미, 삶의 품위였다—키티가 그토록 역겨워했고, 지금은 살 사람을 찾아 물건을 부끄럽게 떠벌리며 파는 짓처럼 여겨지는, 처녀들과 남자들 사이의 세속적인 관계와는 무관한 것이었다."], ["The more attentively Kitty watched her unknown friend, the more convinced she was this girl was the perfect creature she fancied her, and the more eagerly she wished to make her acquaintance.", "키티가 이 낯선 친구를 더 주의 깊게 지켜볼수록, 이 처녀가 자기가 상상하던 그대로 완벽한 존재라는 확신이 더욱 굳어졌고, 그녀와 사귀고 싶은 마음도 더욱 간절해졌다."]], [["The two girls used to meet several times a day, and every time they met, Kitty’s eyes said: “Who are you?", "두 처녀는 하루에도 몇 번씩 마주치곤 했는데, 마주칠 때마다 키티의 눈은 이렇게 말했다."], ["What are you?", "\"당신은 누구죠?"], ["Are you really the exquisite creature I imagine you to be?", "당신은 어떤 사람인가요? 당신은 정말로 내가 상상하는 그 빼어난 사람인가요?"], ["But for goodness’ sake don’t suppose,”", "하지만 제발,\" 그녀의 눈이 덧붙였다."], ["her eyes added, “that I would force my acquaintance on you, I simply admire you and like you.”", "\"내가 당신에게 억지로 사귀자고 들이댄다고는 생각하지 말아 줘요. 나는 그저 당신을 흠모하고 좋아할 뿐이에요.\""], ["“I like you too, and you’re very, very sweet.", "\"나도 당신이 좋아요, 당신은 아주, 아주 사랑스러워요."], ["And I should like you better still, if I had time,”", "시간만 있다면 당신을 더 좋아하게 될 텐데요.\""], ["answered the eyes of the unknown girl.", "낯선 처녀의 눈이 대답했다."], ["Kitty saw indeed, that she was always busy.", "과연 키티는 그녀가 늘 바쁘다는 것을 알 수 있었다."], ["Either she was taking the children of a Russian family home from the springs, or fetching a shawl for a sick lady, and wrapping her up in it, or trying to interest an irritable invalid, or selecting and buying cakes for tea for someone.", "그녀는 어느 러시아 가족의 아이들을 온천에서 집으로 데려가거나, 아픈 부인을 위해 숄을 가져다 감싸 주거나, 신경질적인 환자의 흥미를 끌어 보려 애쓰거나, 누군가를 위해 차에 곁들일 케이크를 골라 사 오곤 했다."]], [["Soon after the arrival of the Shtcherbatskys there appeared in the morning crowd at the springs two persons who attracted universal and unfavorable attention.", "쉬체르바츠키 가족이 도착하고 얼마 지나지 않아, 아침의 온천 인파 속에 모두의 시선을, 그것도 곱지 않은 시선을 끄는 두 사람이 나타났다."], ["These were a tall man with a stooping figure, and huge hands, in an old coat too short for him, with black, simple, and yet terrible eyes, and a pockmarked, kind-looking woman, very badly and tastelessly dressed.", "한 사람은 구부정한 몸에 커다란 손을 가진, 자기 몸에 비해 너무 짧은 낡은 외투를 걸친 키 큰 남자로, 검고 순박하면서도 어딘가 무시무시한 눈을 하고 있었다. 다른 한 사람은 얽은 자국이 있는, 인상이 선해 보이는 여자로, 옷차림은 몹시 형편없고 멋이라곤 없었다."], ["Recognizing these persons as Russians, Kitty had already in her imagination begun constructing a delightful and touching romance about them.", "이들이 러시아 사람임을 알아본 키티는, 벌써 상상 속에서 그들에 관한 즐겁고 가슴 뭉클한 이야기를 지어내기 시작했다."], ["But the princess, having ascertained from the visitors’ list that this was Nikolay Levin and Marya Nikolaevna, explained to Kitty what a bad man this Levin was, and all her fancies about these two people vanished.", "그러나 공작 부인이 투숙객 명단에서 이들이 니콜라이 레빈과 마리야 니콜라예브나라는 것을 알아내고는, 이 레빈이라는 자가 얼마나 못된 사람인지 키티에게 설명해 주자, 이 두 사람에 대한 그녀의 온갖 공상은 사라져 버렸다."], ["Not so much from what her mother told her, as from the fact that it was Konstantin’s brother, this pair suddenly seemed to Kitty intensely unpleasant.", "어머니가 들려준 이야기 때문이라기보다는, 그가 콘스탄틴의 형이라는 사실 때문에, 이 한 쌍은 갑자기 키티에게 몹시 불쾌하게 느껴졌다."], ["This Levin, with his continual twitching of his head, aroused in her now an irrepressible feeling of disgust.", "끊임없이 고개를 씰룩거리는 이 레빈은, 이제 그녀의 안에 억누를 수 없는 혐오감을 불러일으켰다."]], [["It seemed to her that his big, terrible eyes, which persistently pursued her, expressed a feeling of hatred and contempt, and she tried to avoid meeting him.", "집요하게 자기를 좇는 그의 크고 무시무시한 눈이 증오와 경멸의 감정을 드러내는 것만 같아서, 그녀는 그와 마주치는 것을 피하려고 애썼다."]], [["# Chapter 31", "# Chapter 31"]], [["It was a wet day; it had been raining all the morning, and the invalids, with their parasols, had flocked into the arcades.", "비 오는 날이었다. 아침 내내 비가 내렸고, 환자들은 양산을 받쳐 든 채 아케이드로 몰려들었다."]], [["Kitty was walking there with her mother and the Moscow colonel, smart and jaunty in his European coat, bought ready-made at Frankfort.", "키티는 어머니와 모스크바에서 온 대령과 함께 그곳을 거닐고 있었다. 대령은 프랑크푸르트에서 기성품으로 사 입은 유럽풍 외투 차림으로 말쑥하고 멋스러웠다."], ["They were walking on one side of the arcade, trying to avoid Levin, who was walking on the other side.", "그들은 아케이드 한쪽으로 걸으며, 맞은편을 걷고 있는 레빈을 피하려 애썼다."], ["Varenka, in her dark dress, in a black hat with a turn-down brim, was walking up and down the whole length of the arcade with a blind Frenchwoman, and, every time she met Kitty, they exchanged friendly glances.", "바렌카는 어두운 빛깔의 드레스를 입고 챙이 아래로 늘어진 검은 모자를 쓴 채, 눈먼 프랑스 여인과 함께 아케이드의 끝에서 끝까지 오가고 있었고, 키티와 마주칠 때마다 둘은 다정한 눈길을 주고받았다."]], [["“Mamma, couldn’t I speak to her?”", "\"엄마, 저분에게 말을 걸어 보면 안 될까요?\""], ["said Kitty, watching her unknown friend, and noticing that she was going up to the spring, and that they might come there together.", "키티는 알지도 못하는 자기 친구를 바라보며 말했다. 그녀가 샘 쪽으로 가는 것을 알아챘고, 거기서 함께 만날 수도 있겠다 싶었던 것이다."]], [["“Oh, if you want to so much, I’ll find out about her first and make her acquaintance myself,”", "\"오, 정 그러고 싶으면 내가 먼저 저 여자에 대해 알아보고 직접 인사를 트마.\""], ["answered her mother.", "어머니가 대답했다."], ["“What do you see in her out of the way?", "\"저 여자한테서 뭐 특별한 게 보이기라도 하니?"], ["A companion, she must be.", "말동무겠지, 분명."], ["If you like, I’ll make acquaintance with Madame Stahl; I used to know her belle-sœur,”", "네가 원한다면 슈탈 부인하고 인사를 트마. 그 댁 belle-sœur는 내가 알던 사이였단다.\""], ["added the princess, lifting her head haughtily.", "공작 부인은 거만하게 고개를 들며 덧붙였다."]], [["Kitty knew that the princess was offended that Madame Stahl had seemed to avoid making her acquaintance.", "키티는 슈탈 부인이 자신과 인사하기를 피하는 듯 보였다는 사실에 어머니가 기분이 상해 있음을 알았다. 키티는 더 고집부리지 않았다."], ["Kitty did not insist.", "* belle-sœur: 프랑스어로 '시누이' 또는 '올케', '동서'."]], [["“How wonderfully sweet she is!”", "\"정말이지 더할 나위 없이 상냥한 분이에요!\""], ["she said, gazing at Varenka just as she handed a glass to the Frenchwoman.", "마침 바렌카가 프랑스 여인에게 잔을 건네는 모습을 바라보며 키티가 말했다."], ["“Look how natural and sweet it all is.”", "\"보세요, 저 모든 게 얼마나 자연스럽고 사랑스러운지.\""]], [["“It’s so funny to see your engouements,”", "\"네 engouements를 보고 있으면 어찌나 우습던지.\""], ["said the princess.", "공작 부인이 말했다."], ["“No, we’d better go back,”", "\"아니, 우린 돌아가는 게 낫겠다.\""], ["she added, noticing Levin coming towards them with his companion and a German doctor, to whom he was talking very noisily and angrily.", "그녀는 레빈이 일행과 독일인 의사를 데리고 자기들 쪽으로 다가오는 것을 알아채고 덧붙였다. 레빈은 그 의사에게 몹시 시끄럽고 성난 목소리로 뭔가를 떠들어 대고 있었다."]], [["They turned to go back, when suddenly they heard, not noisy talk, but shouting.", "* engouements: 프랑스어로 '심취', '열광'. 그들이 돌아서려는데, 별안간 시끄러운 이야기 소리가 아니라 고함 소리가 들려왔다."], ["Levin, stopping short, was shouting at the doctor, and the doctor, too, was excited.", "레빈이 우뚝 멈춰 서서 의사에게 소리를 지르고 있었고, 의사 또한 흥분해 있었다."], ["A crowd gathered about them.", "그들 주위로 사람들이 모여들었다."], ["The princess and Kitty beat a hasty retreat, while the colonel joined the crowd to find out what was the matter.", "공작 부인과 키티는 황급히 자리를 피했고, 대령은 무슨 일인지 알아보려고 사람들 틈에 끼어들었다."]], [["A few minutes later the colonel overtook them.", "몇 분 뒤 대령이 그들을 따라잡았다."]], [["“What was it?”", "\"무슨 일이었어요?\""], ["inquired the princess.", "공작 부인이 물었다."]], [["“Scandalous and disgraceful!”", "\"수치스럽고 망신스러운 일입니다!\""], ["answered the colonel.", "대령이 대답했다."], ["“The one thing to be dreaded is meeting Russians abroad.", "\"외국에서 가장 두려운 일이라면 러시아 사람을 만나는 것이지요."], ["That tall gentleman was abusing the doctor, flinging all sorts of insults at him because he wasn’t treating him quite as he liked, and he began waving his stick at him.", "그 키 큰 양반이 의사한테 욕설을 퍼붓고 있었습니다. 자기 마음에 꼭 들게 치료해 주지 않는다고 온갖 모욕을 쏟아붓더니, 급기야 지팡이를 휘두르기 시작하더군요."], ["It’s simply a scandal!”", "정말이지 추태가 따로 없습니다!\""]], [["“Oh, how unpleasant!”", "\"아, 정말 불쾌하기도 해라!\""], ["said the princess.", "공작 부인이 말했다."], ["“Well, and how did it end?”", "\"그래서, 어떻게 끝났나요?\""]], [["“Luckily at that point that ...", "\"다행히 그때 그……"], ["the one in the mushroom hat ...", "버섯 모양 모자를 쓴 그분이……"], ["intervened.", "끼어들었지요."], ["A Russian lady, I think she is,”", "러시아 부인인 것 같던데요.\""], ["said the colonel.", "대령이 말했다."]], [["“Mademoiselle Varenka?”", "\"바렌카 양 말씀이세요?\""], ["asked Kitty.", "키티가 물었다."]], [["“Yes, yes.", "\"그래요, 그래."], ["She came to the rescue before anyone; she took the man by the arm and led him away.”", "누구보다 먼저 나서서 도와주더군요. 그 남자의 팔을 잡고는 데리고 가 버렸어요.\""]], [["“There, mamma,”", "\"보세요, 엄마.\""], ["said Kitty; “you wonder that I’m enthusiastic about her.”", "키티가 말했다. \"제가 저분에게 푹 빠진 걸 이상하게 여기시잖아요.\""]], [["The next day, as she watched her unknown friend, Kitty noticed that Mademoiselle Varenka was already on the same terms with Levin and his companion as with her other protégés.", "이튿날, 키티는 알지도 못하는 자기 친구를 바라보다가, 바렌카 양이 이미 다른 protégés들을 대하듯 레빈과 그 일행과도 격의 없이 지내고 있음을 알아챘다."], ["She went up to them, entered into conversation with them, and served as interpreter for the woman, who could not speak any foreign language.", "그녀는 그들에게 다가가 이야기를 나누었고, 외국어라곤 한마디도 못 하는 그 여인을 위해 통역 노릇을 해 주었다. * protégés: 프랑스어로 '돌봐 주는 사람들', '피보호자들'."]], [["Kitty began to entreat her mother still more urgently to let her make friends with Varenka.", "키티는 바렌카와 가까워지게 해 달라고 어머니에게 더욱더 간절히 졸랐다."], ["And, disagreeable as it was to the princess to seem to take the first step in wishing to make the acquaintance of Madame Stahl, who thought fit to give herself airs, she made inquiries about Varenka, and, having ascertained particulars about her tending to prove that there could be no harm though little good in the acquaintance, she herself approached Varenka and made acquaintance with her.", "공작 부인으로서는 잘난 체하기 좋아하는 슈탈 부인에게 먼저 인사를 청하는 듯한 모양새가 마뜩잖았지만, 그래도 바렌카에 대해 알아보았고, 그 교제에 별 이로움은 없을지언정 해로울 것도 없다는 점을 입증하는 자세한 내막을 확인한 뒤에는, 자기가 직접 바렌카에게 다가가 인사를 텄다."]], [["Choosing a time when her daughter had gone to the spring, while Varenka had stopped outside the baker’s, the princess went up to her.", "딸이 샘으로 간 틈을 타, 바렌카가 빵집 앞에 멈춰 서 있을 때, 공작 부인은 그녀에게 다가갔다."]], [["“Allow me to make your acquaintance,”", "\"통성명을 했으면 하는데요.\""], ["she said, with her dignified smile.", "그녀는 위엄 있는 미소를 지으며 말했다."], ["“My daughter has lost her heart to you,”", "\"내 딸이 아가씨한테 홀딱 반했답니다.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“Possibly you do not know me.", "\"혹시 나를 모를 수도 있겠군요."], ["I am....”", "나는……\""]], [["“That feeling is more than reciprocal, princess,”", "\"그 마음이라면 저야말로 그 이상이랍니다, 공작 부인.\""], ["Varenka answered hurriedly.", "바렌카가 다급히 대답했다."]], [["“What a good deed you did yesterday to our poor compatriot!”", "\"어제 그 가엾은 우리 동포에게 얼마나 갸륵한 일을 해 주었던지요!\""], ["said the princess.", "공작 부인이 말했다."]], [["Varenka flushed a little.", "바렌카는 살짝 얼굴을 붉혔다."], ["“I don’t remember.", "\"기억이 안 나는데요."], ["I don’t think I did anything,”", "제가 뭘 했다고는 생각하지 않아요.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“Why, you saved that Levin from disagreeable consequences.”", "\"아니, 그 레빈이라는 분을 곤란한 일에서 구해 주었잖아요.\""]], [["“Yes, sa compagne called me, and I tried to pacify him, he’s very ill, and was dissatisfied with the doctor. I’m used to looking after such invalids.”", "\"네, sa compagne가 저를 부르더군요. 그래서 그분을 달래 보았어요. 그분은 몹시 편찮으셨고, 의사에게 불만이 있으셨거든요. 저는 그런 환자들을 돌보는 데 익숙해서요.\" * sa compagne: 프랑스어로 '그의 동행 여인', '그의 동반자'."]], [["“Yes, I’ve heard you live at Mentone with your aunt—I think—Madame Stahl: I used to know her belle-sœur.”", "\"그렇군요. 아가씨가 멘토네에서 이모와—그러니까—슈탈 부인과 함께 산다고 들었어요. 그 댁 belle-sœur는 내가 알던 사이였답니다.\""]], [["“No, she’s not my aunt.", "\"아니에요, 그분은 제 이모가 아니에요."], ["I call her mamma, but I am not related to her; I was brought up by her,”", "저는 그분을 엄마라고 부르지만, 친척은 아니랍니다. 그분 손에 자랐을 뿐이에요.\""], ["answered Varenka, flushing a little again.", "바렌카가 다시 살짝 얼굴을 붉히며 대답했다."]], [["This was so simply said, and so sweet was the truthful and candid expression of her face, that the princess saw why Kitty had taken such a fancy to Varenka.", "너무도 꾸밈없이 한 말이었고, 진실하고 솔직한 그 얼굴 표정이 어찌나 사랑스럽던지, 공작 부인은 키티가 왜 그토록 바렌카에게 마음을 빼앗겼는지 알 수 있었다."]], [["“Well, and what’s this Levin going to do?”", "\"그래서, 그 레빈이라는 분은 어떻게 한답니까?\""], ["asked the princess.", "공작 부인이 물었다."]], [["“He’s going away,”", "\"떠나신답니다.\""], ["answered Varenka.", "바렌카가 대답했다."]], [["At that instant Kitty came up from the spring beaming with delight that her mother had become acquainted with her unknown friend.", "바로 그때 키티가 샘에서 올라왔다. 어머니가 알지도 못하는 자기 친구와 인사를 텄다는 사실에 기쁨으로 환히 빛나는 얼굴이었다."]], [["“Well, see, Kitty, your intense desire to make friends with Mademoiselle....”", "\"자, 보렴, 키티, 네가 그토록 간절히 사귀고 싶어 하던 그 아가씨가……\""]], [["“Varenka,”", "\"바렌카예요.\""], ["Varenka put in smiling, “that’s what everyone calls me.”", "바렌카가 미소 지으며 끼어들었다. \"다들 저를 그렇게 불러요.\""]], [["Kitty blushed with pleasure, and slowly, without speaking, pressed her new friend’s hand, which did not respond to her pressure, but lay motionless in her hand.", "키티는 기쁨으로 얼굴을 붉혔고, 아무 말 없이 천천히 새 친구의 손을 꼭 잡았다. 그 손은 키티의 손길에 응하지 않고 그저 그녀의 손안에 가만히 놓여 있었다."], ["The hand did not respond to her pressure, but the face of Mademoiselle Varenka glowed with a soft, glad, though rather mournful smile, that showed large but handsome teeth.", "손은 키티의 손길에 응하지 않았지만, 바렌카 양의 얼굴은 다소 애잔하면서도 부드럽고 기쁜 미소로 환하게 빛났고, 그 미소에 크지만 가지런한 이가 드러났다."]], [["“I have long wished for this too,”", "\"저도 오래전부터 이러기를 바랐어요.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“But you are so busy.”", "\"하지만 아가씨는 무척 바쁘잖아요.\""]], [["“Oh, no, I’m not at all busy,”", "\"오, 아니에요, 저는 전혀 바쁘지 않아요.\""], ["answered Varenka, but at that moment she had to leave her new friends because two little Russian girls, children of an invalid, ran up to her.", "바렌카가 대답했지만, 바로 그 순간 그녀는 새 친구들 곁을 떠나야 했다. 어느 환자의 자식인 러시아 소녀 둘이 그녀에게 달려왔기 때문이다."]], [["“Varenka, mamma’s calling!”", "\"바렌카, 엄마가 불러요!\""], ["they cried.", "아이들이 외쳤다."]], [["And Varenka went after them.", "그리고 바렌카는 아이들을 따라갔다."]], [["# Chapter 32", "# Chapter 32"]], [["The particulars which the princess had learned in regard to Varenka’s past and her relations with Madame Stahl were as follows:", "공작 부인이 바렌카의 과거와 슈탈 부인과의 관계에 대해 알아낸 자세한 사정은 다음과 같았다."]], [["Madame Stahl, of whom some people said that she had worried her husband out of his life, while others said it was he who had made her wretched by his immoral behavior, had always been a woman of weak health and enthusiastic temperament.", "슈탈 부인에 대해 어떤 이들은 그녀가 남편을 들볶아 죽음으로 몰아갔다고 했고, 또 어떤 이들은 부도덕한 행실로 그녀를 비참하게 만든 쪽이 남편이었다고 했는데, 그녀는 본래부터 몸이 약하고 격정적인 기질을 지닌 여자였다."], ["When, after her separation from her husband, she gave birth to her only child, the child had died almost immediately, and the family of Madame Stahl, knowing her sensibility, and fearing the news would kill her, had substituted another child, a baby born the same night and in the same house in Petersburg, the daughter of the chief cook of the Imperial Household.", "남편과 헤어진 뒤 하나뿐인 아이를 낳았을 때 그 아이는 거의 곧바로 죽고 말았고, 그녀의 예민한 감수성을 잘 아는 슈탈 부인의 가족은 그 소식이 그녀를 죽게 할까 두려워 다른 아이를, 그러니까 같은 날 밤 페테르부르크의 같은 집에서 태어난 황실 수석 요리사의 딸을 바꿔치기했다."], ["This was Varenka.", "그 아이가 바로 바렌카였다."], ["Madame Stahl learned later on that Varenka was not her own child, but she went on bringing her up, especially as very soon afterwards Varenka had not a relation of her own living.", "슈탈 부인은 나중에 바렌카가 자기 친자식이 아니라는 것을 알았지만, 계속 그녀를 키웠다. 더욱이 얼마 지나지 않아 바렌카에게는 살아 있는 혈육이 하나도 남지 않았기 때문이다."], ["Madame Stahl had now been living more than ten years continuously abroad, in the south, never leaving her couch.", "슈탈 부인은 이제 십 년 넘게 줄곧 외국에서, 남쪽 지방에서 소파에 누운 채 한 번도 일어나지 못하며 지내 왔다."], ["And some people said that Madame Stahl had made her social position as a philanthropic, highly religious woman; other people said she really was at heart the highly ethical being, living for nothing but the good of her fellow creatures, which she represented herself to be.", "어떤 이들은 슈탈 부인이 자선가이자 신앙심 깊은 여인이라는 사교계의 지위를 스스로 만들어 냈다고 했고, 또 어떤 이들은 그녀가 스스로 내세우는 대로 오직 이웃의 행복만을 위해 사는, 진심으로 더없이 윤리적인 인간이라고 했다."], ["No one knew what her faith was—Catholic, Protestant, or Orthodox.", "그녀의 신앙이 무엇인지—가톨릭인지, 신교인지, 정교인지—아무도 알지 못했다."], ["But one fact was indubitable—she was in amicable relations with the highest dignitaries of all the churches and sects.", "그러나 한 가지 사실만은 의심의 여지가 없었으니, 그녀가 모든 교회와 종파의 최고 고위 성직자들과 두루 친밀한 관계를 맺고 있다는 점이었다."]], [["Varenka lived with her all the while abroad, and everyone who knew Madame Stahl knew and liked Mademoiselle Varenka, as everyone called her.", "바렌카는 외국에 머무는 내내 그녀와 함께 살았고, 슈탈 부인을 아는 사람이라면 누구나 모두가 부르는 대로 마드무아젤 바렌카를 알고 좋아했다."]], [["Having learned all these facts, the princess found nothing to object to in her daughter’s intimacy with Varenka, more especially as Varenka’s breeding and education were of the best—she spoke French and English extremely well—and what was of the most weight, brought a message from Madame Stahl expressing her regret that she was prevented by her ill health from making the acquaintance of the princess.", "이 모든 사실을 알고 난 공작 부인은 딸이 바렌카와 가까이 지내는 데에 흠잡을 것이 전혀 없다고 여겼다. 게다가 바렌카의 가정교육과 학식은 더없이 훌륭했으며—프랑스어와 영어를 빼어나게 잘했다—무엇보다 중요한 것은, 슈탈 부인이 건강이 나빠 공작 부인과 인사를 나누지 못해 유감이라는 전갈을 바렌카가 전해 왔다는 점이었다."]], [["After getting to know Varenka, Kitty became more and more fascinated by her friend, and every day she discovered new virtues in her.", "바렌카와 알고 지내게 되면서 키티는 점점 더 친구에게 매료되었고, 날마다 그녀에게서 새로운 미덕을 발견했다."]], [["The princess, hearing that Varenka had a good voice, asked her to come and sing to them in the evening.", "공작 부인은 바렌카가 목소리가 좋다는 말을 듣고, 저녁에 와서 노래를 불러 달라고 청했다."]], [["“Kitty plays, and we have a piano; not a good one, it’s true, but you will give us so much pleasure,”", "\"키티가 피아노를 치고, 우리 집에 피아노도 있어요. 좋은 건 아니지만요, 그래도 우리한테 큰 기쁨을 줄 거예요.\""], ["said the princess with her affected smile, which Kitty disliked particularly just then, because she noticed that Varenka had no inclination to sing.", "공작 부인은 그 꾸민 듯한 미소를 지으며 말했는데, 키티는 바렌카가 노래할 마음이 없다는 것을 알아챘기 때문에 그때만큼은 그 미소가 유난히 거슬렸다."], ["Varenka came, however, in the evening and brought a roll of music with her.", "그래도 바렌카는 저녁에 악보 한 묶음을 들고 왔다."], ["The princess had invited Marya Yevgenyevna and her daughter and the colonel.", "공작 부인은 마리야 예브게니예브나와 그 딸, 그리고 대령을 초대해 놓았다."]], [["Varenka seemed quite unaffected by there being persons present she did not know, and she went directly to the piano.", "바렌카는 모르는 사람들이 있다는 것에 조금도 거리낌이 없어 보였고, 곧장 피아노 쪽으로 갔다."], ["She could not accompany herself, but she could sing music at sight very well.", "그녀는 직접 반주를 하지는 못했지만, 악보를 보고 노래하는 것은 무척 잘했다."], ["Kitty, who played well, accompanied her.", "피아노를 잘 치는 키티가 반주를 맡았다."]], [["“You have an extraordinary talent,”", "\"당신은 놀라운 재능을 지녔군요.\""], ["the princess said to her after Varenka had sung the first song extremely well.", "바렌카가 첫 곡을 더없이 훌륭하게 부르고 나자 공작 부인이 그녀에게 말했다."]], [["Marya Yevgenyevna and her daughter expressed their thanks and admiration.", "마리야 예브게니예브나와 그 딸도 고마움과 감탄을 표했다."]], [["“Look,”", "\"보세요.\""], ["said the colonel, looking out of the window, “what an audience has collected to listen to you.”", "창밖을 내다보며 대령이 말했다. \"당신의 노래를 들으려고 청중이 이렇게나 모였어요.\""], ["There actually was quite a considerable crowd under the windows.", "실제로 창문 아래에는 제법 많은 사람들이 모여 있었다."]], [["“I am very glad it gives you pleasure,”", "\"여러분께 기쁨을 드린다니 저도 무척 기뻐요.\""], ["Varenka answered simply.", "바렌카가 담담하게 대답했다."]], [["Kitty looked with pride at her friend.", "키티는 자랑스러운 마음으로 친구를 바라보았다."], ["She was enchanted by her talent, and her voice, and her face, but most of all by her manner, by the way Varenka obviously thought nothing of her singing and was quite unmoved by their praises.", "그녀는 바렌카의 재능에, 그 목소리에, 그 얼굴에 매료되었지만, 무엇보다 그 태도에, 바렌카가 자기 노래를 대수롭지 않게 여기고 사람들의 칭찬에도 조금도 동요하지 않는 그 모습에 사로잡혔다."], ["She seemed only to be asking: “Am I to sing again, or is that enough?”", "그녀는 그저 이렇게 묻고 있는 것 같았다. \"또 불러야 하나요, 아니면 이만하면 됐나요?\""]], [["“If it had been I,”", "'만약 나였다면,'"], ["thought Kitty, “how proud I should have been!", "키티는 생각했다. '나는 얼마나 우쭐했을까!"], ["How delighted I should have been to see that crowd under the windows!", "창문 아래 모인 저 사람들을 보고 얼마나 기뻐했을까!"], ["But she’s utterly unmoved by it.", "그런데 저 애는 조금도 동요하지 않아."], ["Her only motive is to avoid refusing and to please mamma.", "거절하지 않고 엄마를 기쁘게 해 드리려는 게 유일한 이유야."], ["What is there in her?", "저 애 안에는 뭐가 있는 걸까?"], ["What is it gives her the power to look down on everything, to be calm independently of everything?", "무엇이 저 애에게 모든 것을 내려다보고, 무엇에도 흔들리지 않고 차분할 수 있는 힘을 주는 걸까?"], ["How I should like to know it and to learn it of her!”", "그걸 알고 싶어, 저 애한테서 배우고 싶어!'"], ["thought Kitty, gazing into her serene face.", "키티는 친구의 평온한 얼굴을 들여다보며 생각했다."], ["The princess asked Varenka to sing again, and Varenka sang another song, also smoothly, distinctly, and well, standing erect at the piano and beating time on it with her thin, dark-skinned hand.", "공작 부인이 한 곡 더 불러 달라고 청하자, 바렌카는 또 다른 곡을 역시나 매끄럽고 또렷하게, 훌륭하게 불렀다. 피아노 앞에 꼿꼿이 선 채, 가무잡잡하고 가느다란 손으로 피아노를 두드리며 박자를 맞추면서."]], [["The next song in the book was an Italian one.", "악보집의 다음 곡은 이탈리아 노래였다."], ["Kitty played the opening bars, and looked round at Varenka.", "키티는 첫 소절을 치고는 바렌카를 돌아보았다."]], [["“Let’s skip that,”", "\"그건 건너뛰어요.\""], ["said Varenka, flushing a little.", "바렌카가 살짝 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다."], ["Kitty let her eyes rest on Varenka’s face, with a look of dismay and inquiry.", "키티는 당황과 의아함이 담긴 눈길로 바렌카의 얼굴을 가만히 바라보았다."]], [["“Very well, the next one,”", "\"좋아요, 그럼 다음 곡으로.\""], ["she said hurriedly, turning over the pages, and at once feeling that there was something connected with the song.", "키티는 황급히 말하며 책장을 넘겼고, 곧 그 노래에 무언가 사연이 얽혀 있다는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["“No,”", "\"아니에요.\""], ["answered Varenka with a smile, laying her hand on the music, “no, let’s have that one.”", "바렌카가 미소를 지으며 악보 위에 손을 얹고 대답했다. \"아니, 그걸 불러요.\""], ["And she sang it just as quietly, as coolly, and as well as the others.", "그리고 그녀는 다른 곡들과 똑같이 조용하게, 똑같이 차분하게, 똑같이 훌륭하게 그 노래를 불렀다."]], [["When she had finished, they all thanked her again, and went off to tea.", "노래를 마치자 모두 다시 그녀에게 고마움을 표하고 차를 마시러 갔다."], ["Kitty and Varenka went out into the little garden that adjoined the house.", "키티와 바렌카는 집에 딸린 작은 정원으로 나갔다."]], [["“Am I right, that you have some reminiscences connected with that song?”", "\"내 생각이 맞나요, 그 노래에 무슨 추억이 얽혀 있죠?\""], ["said Kitty.", "키티가 말했다."], ["“Don’t tell me,”", "\"말하지 않아도 돼요.\""], ["she added hastily, “only say if I’m right.”", "그녀는 서둘러 덧붙였다. \"그냥 내 생각이 맞는지만 말해 줘요.\""]], [["“No, why not?", "\"아니, 못 할 게 뭐 있어요?"], ["I’ll tell you simply,”", "그냥 말해 줄게요.\""], ["said Varenka, and, without waiting for a reply, she went on: “Yes, it brings up memories, once painful ones.", "바렌카는 이렇게 말하고는 대답을 기다리지도 않고 이어 갔다. \"맞아요, 그 노래는 추억을 떠올리게 해요."], ["I cared for someone once, and I used to sing him that song.”", "한때는 괴로운 추억이었죠. 옛날에 누군가를 좋아했는데, 그 사람에게 그 노래를 불러 주곤 했어요.\""]], [["Kitty with big, wide-open eyes gazed silently, sympathetically at Varenka.", "키티는 크게 뜬 눈으로 말없이, 안쓰러운 마음으로 바렌카를 바라보았다."]], [["“I cared for him, and he cared for me; but his mother did not wish it, and he married another girl.", "\"나는 그를 좋아했고, 그도 나를 좋아했어요. 그런데 그의 어머니가 원하지 않았고, 그는 다른 여자와 결혼했어요."], ["He’s living now not far from us, and I see him sometimes.", "지금 그 사람은 우리 집에서 멀지 않은 곳에 살고 있어서, 가끔 만나기도 해요."], ["You didn’t think I had a love story too,”", "나한테도 연애 이야기가 있을 줄은 몰랐죠.\""], ["she said, and there was a faint gleam in her handsome face of that fire which Kitty felt must once have glowed all over her.", "그녀가 말했다. 그 아름다운 얼굴에는, 한때 그녀의 온몸을 타오르게 했으리라고 키티가 느낀 그 불꽃이 희미하게 어른거렸다."]], [["“I didn’t think so?", "\"몰랐냐고요?"], ["Why, if I were a man, I could never care for anyone else after knowing you.", "아니, 내가 남자였다면 당신을 알고 난 다음에는 결코 다른 사람을 좋아할 수 없었을 거예요."], ["Only I can’t understand how he could, to please his mother, forget you and make you unhappy; he had no heart.”", "다만 어떻게 그 사람이 어머니를 기쁘게 하려고 당신을 잊고 당신을 불행하게 만들 수 있었는지는 도무지 이해가 안 가요. 그 사람은 심장이 없었던 거예요.\""]], [["“Oh, no, he’s a very good man, and I’m not unhappy; quite the contrary, I’m very happy.", "\"오, 아니에요, 그는 아주 좋은 사람이에요. 그리고 나는 불행하지 않아요."], ["Well, so we shan’t be singing any more now,”", "오히려 정반대로, 아주 행복해요. 자, 그럼 이제 더는 노래하지 않겠네요.\""], ["she added, turning towards the house.", "그녀는 집 쪽으로 돌아서며 덧붙였다."]], [["“How good you are!", "\"당신은 어쩜 그렇게 좋은 사람일까요!"], ["how good you are!”", "정말 좋은 사람이에요!\""], ["cried Kitty, and stopping her, she kissed her.", "키티가 외치며 그녀를 멈춰 세우고 입을 맞추었다."], ["“If I could only be even a little like you!”", "\"내가 당신을 조금이라도 닮을 수 있다면!\""]], [["“Why should you be like anyone?", "\"왜 누구를 닮으려고 해요?"], ["You’re nice as you are,”", "당신은 지금 모습 그대로 사랑스러운데.\""], ["said Varenka, smiling her gentle, weary smile.", "바렌카가 그 부드럽고 지친 미소를 지으며 말했다."]], [["“No, I’m not nice at all.", "\"아니, 난 조금도 사랑스럽지 않아요."], ["Come, tell me....", "자, 말해 줘요……"], ["Stop a minute, let’s sit down,”", "잠깐만요, 우리 앉아요.\""], ["said Kitty, making her sit down again beside her.", "키티는 그녀를 다시 자기 곁에 앉히며 말했다."], ["“Tell me, isn’t it humiliating to think that a man has disdained your love, that he hasn’t cared for it?...”", "\"말해 줘요, 어떤 남자가 당신의 사랑을 무시했다고, 그가 그 사랑에 마음 쓰지 않았다고 생각하면 굴욕스럽지 않아요?……\""]], [["“But he didn’t disdain it; I believe he cared for me, but he was a dutiful son....”", "\"하지만 그는 내 사랑을 무시하지 않았어요. 내 생각엔 그도 나를 좋아했지만, 효성 깊은 아들이었던 거죠……\""]], [["“Yes, but if it hadn’t been on account of his mother, if it had been his own doing?...”", "\"맞아요, 하지만 만약 그의 어머니 때문이 아니라, 그 자신이 한 일이었다면요?……\""], ["said Kitty, feeling she was giving away her secret, and that her face, burning with the flush of shame, had betrayed her already.", "키티는 자기가 비밀을 털어놓고 있다는 것을, 그리고 수치심으로 화끈 달아오른 자기 얼굴이 이미 자신을 드러내 버렸다는 것을 느끼며 말했다."]], [["“In that case he would have done wrong, and I should not have regretted him,”", "\"그랬다면 그가 잘못한 거고, 나는 그를 안타까워하지 않았을 거예요.\" 바렌카가 대답했다."], ["answered Varenka, evidently realizing that they were now talking not of her, but of Kitty.", "그녀는 이제 그들이 자기 이야기가 아니라 키티의 이야기를 하고 있다는 것을 분명히 알아챈 듯했다."]], [["“But the humiliation,”", "\"하지만 그 굴욕은요.\""], ["said Kitty, “the humiliation one can never forget, can never forget,”", "키티가 말했다. \"결코 잊을 수 없는, 결코 잊을 수 없는 그 굴욕은요.\""], ["she said, remembering her look at the last ball during the pause in the music.", "그녀는 지난번 무도회에서 음악이 멈춘 사이에 자기가 지었던 그 눈길을 떠올리며 말했다."]], [["“Where is the humiliation?", "\"굴욕이라니, 어디에 굴욕이 있어요?"], ["Why, you did nothing wrong?”", "당신은 아무 잘못도 안 했잖아요?\""]], [["“Worse than wrong—shameful.”", "\"잘못보다 더 나쁜 거예요. 부끄러운 일이죠.\""]], [["Varenka shook her head and laid her hand on Kitty’s hand.", "바렌카는 고개를 가로젓고 키티의 손 위에 자기 손을 얹었다."]], [["“Why, what is there shameful?”", "\"아니, 부끄러울 게 뭐가 있어요?\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“You didn’t tell a man, who didn’t care for you, that you loved him, did you?”", "\"당신을 좋아하지도 않는 남자한테, 당신이 그를 사랑한다고 말한 건 아니잖아요, 그렇죠?\""]], [["“Of course not; I never said a word, but he knew it. No, no, there are looks, there are ways; I can’t forget it, if I live a hundred years.”", "\"물론 그러지 않았죠. 한마디도 하지 않았어요. 하지만 그는 알고 있었어요. 아니, 아니에요, 눈빛이라는 게 있고, 몸짓이라는 게 있잖아요. 백 년을 산다 해도 그걸 잊을 수 없을 거예요.\""]], [["“Why so?", "\"왜 그래요?"], ["I don’t understand.", "난 이해가 안 가요."], ["The whole point is whether you love him now or not,”", "핵심은 지금 당신이 그를 사랑하느냐 아니냐 하는 거잖아요.\""], ["said Varenka, who called everything by its name.", "무엇이든 있는 그대로 이름 붙여 부르는 바렌카가 말했다."]], [["“I hate him; I can’t forgive myself.”", "\"난 그가 미워요. 나 자신을 용서할 수가 없어요.\""]], [["“Why, what for?”", "\"왜요, 뭣 때문에요?\""]], [["“The shame, the humiliation!”", "\"그 수치심, 그 굴욕 때문이죠!\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["if everyone were as sensitive as you are!”", "다들 당신만큼 예민하다면 어쩌겠어요!\""], ["said Varenka.", "바렌카가 말했다."], ["“There isn’t a girl who hasn’t been through the same.", "\"똑같은 일을 겪어 보지 않은 처녀는 없어요."], ["And it’s all so unimportant.”", "그리고 그건 다 그렇게 대단치 않은 일이에요.\""]], [["“Why, what is important?”", "\"그럼 뭐가 대단한 거예요?\""], ["said Kitty, looking into her face with inquisitive wonder.", "키티가 호기심 어린 놀라움으로 그녀의 얼굴을 들여다보며 말했다."]], [["“Oh, there’s so much that’s important,”", "\"오, 대단한 건 정말 많아요.\""], ["said Varenka, smiling.", "바렌카가 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["“Why, what?”", "\"그게 뭔데요?\""]], [["“Oh, so much that’s more important,”", "\"오, 그보다 훨씬 더 대단한 게 많죠.\""], ["answered Varenka, not knowing what to say.", "바렌카는 뭐라고 말해야 할지 몰라 대답했다."], ["But at that instant they heard the princess’s voice from the window.", "그런데 바로 그 순간 창문에서 공작 부인의 목소리가 들려왔다."], ["“Kitty, it’s cold!", "\"키티, 춥구나!"], ["Either get a shawl, or come indoors.”", "숄을 두르든지, 아니면 안으로 들어오렴.\""]], [["“It really is time to go in!”", "\"정말 들어갈 시간이네요!\""], ["said Varenka, getting up.", "바렌카가 일어서며 말했다."], ["“I have to go on to Madame Berthe’s; she asked me to.”", "\"베르트 부인 댁에도 가 봐야 해요. 와 달라고 했거든요.\""]], [["Kitty held her by the hand, and with passionate curiosity and entreaty her eyes asked her: “What is it, what is this of such importance that gives you such tranquillity?", "키티는 그녀의 손을 잡고, 간절한 호기심과 애원이 담긴 눈으로 그녀에게 물었다. '대체 뭐예요, 당신에게 그런 평온함을 주는 그 대단한 게 뭐냐고요?"], ["You know, tell me!”", "알고 있잖아요, 말해 줘요!'"], ["But Varenka did not even know what Kitty’s eyes were asking her.", "하지만 바렌카는 키티의 눈이 무엇을 묻고 있는지조차 알지 못했다."], ["She merely thought that she had to go to see Madame Berthe too that evening, and to make haste home in time for maman’s tea at twelve o’clock.", "그녀는 그저 그날 저녁 베르트 부인도 만나러 가야 한다는 것과, 열두 시에 있을 maman의 차 시간에 늦지 않게 서둘러 집으로 돌아가야 한다는 것만 생각하고 있었다."], ["She went indoors, collected her music, and saying good-bye to everyone, was about to go.", "그녀는 안으로 들어가 악보를 챙기고, 모두에게 작별 인사를 하고는 막 나가려 했다. * maman: 프랑스어로 '엄마'."]], [["“Allow me to see you home,”", "\"댁까지 바래다드리지요.\""], ["said the colonel.", "대령이 말했다."]], [["“Yes, how can you go alone at night like this?”", "\"그럼요, 이렇게 밤에 어떻게 혼자 가겠어요?\""], ["chimed in the princess.", "공작 부인도 거들었다."], ["“Anyway, I’ll send Parasha.”", "\"어쨌든 파라샤를 딸려 보낼게요.\""]], [["Kitty saw that Varenka could hardly restrain a smile at the idea that she needed an escort.", "키티는 바렌카가 자기에게 동행이 필요하다는 말에 웃음을 간신히 참고 있는 것을 보았다."]], [["“No, I always go about alone and nothing ever happens to me,”", "\"아니에요, 저는 늘 혼자 다니지만 아무 일도 없었는걸요.\""], ["she said, taking her hat.", "그녀가 모자를 집어 들며 말했다."], ["And kissing Kitty once more, without saying what was important, she stepped out courageously with the music under her arm and vanished into the twilight of the summer night, bearing away with her her secret of what was important and what gave her the calm and dignity so much to be envied.", "그리고 키티에게 다시 한번 입을 맞추고는, 무엇이 대단한 것인지 말해 주지 않은 채, 악보를 옆구리에 낀 채 씩씩하게 발을 내디뎌 여름밤의 어스름 속으로 사라졌다. 무엇이 대단한 것인지, 그리고 무엇이 그토록 부러운 평온과 기품을 그녀에게 주는지, 그 비밀을 안고서."]], [["# Chapter 33", "# Chapter 33"]], [["Kitty made the acquaintance of Madame Stahl too, and this acquaintance, together with her friendship with Varenka, did not merely exercise a great influence on her, it also comforted her in her mental distress.", "키티는 슈탈 부인과도 친분을 맺게 되었는데, 이 친분은 바렌카와의 우정과 더불어 그녀에게 커다란 영향을 미쳤을 뿐 아니라 마음의 고통 속에 있던 그녀를 위로해 주기도 했다."], ["She found this comfort through a completely new world being opened to her by means of this acquaintance, a world having nothing in common with her past, an exalted, noble world, from the height of which she could contemplate her past calmly.", "그녀는 이 친분을 통해 자기 앞에 활짝 열린 완전히 새로운 세계 속에서 그 위안을 발견했다. 그것은 자신의 과거와는 아무런 공통점도 없는 세계, 고결하고 숭고한 세계였으며, 그 높은 곳에서 그녀는 자기 과거를 차분히 내려다볼 수 있었다."], ["It was revealed to her that besides the instinctive life to which Kitty had given herself up hitherto there was a spiritual life.", "키티가 그때까지 몸을 맡겨 온 본능적인 삶 말고도 영적인 삶이 존재한다는 것이 그녀에게 드러났다."], ["This life was disclosed in religion, but a religion having nothing in common with that one which Kitty had known from childhood, and which found expression in litanies and all-night services at the Widow’s Home, where one might meet one’s friends, and in learning by heart Slavonic texts with the priest.", "이 삶은 종교 안에서 모습을 드러냈는데, 그것은 키티가 어린 시절부터 알아 온 종교, 그러니까 친구들과 마주칠 수 있는 미망인의 집에서 열리는 기도식과 철야 예배, 사제와 함께 슬라브어 성경 구절을 외우는 일로 표현되던 그 종교와는 아무런 공통점도 없는 종교였다."], ["This was a lofty, mysterious religion connected with a whole series of noble thoughts and feelings, which one could do more than merely believe because one was told to, which one could love.", "이것은 고귀하고 신비로운 종교였으며, 일련의 숭고한 사상과 감정에 연결되어 있어서, 그저 그렇게 하라는 말을 들어서 믿는 것 이상으로, 사랑할 수 있는 종교였다."]], [["Kitty found all this out not from words.", "키티가 이 모든 것을 알게 된 것은 말을 통해서가 아니었다."], ["Madame Stahl talked to Kitty as to a charming child that one looks on with pleasure as on the memory of one’s youth, and only once she said in passing that in all human sorrows nothing gives comfort but love and faith, and that in the sight of Christ’s compassion for us no sorrow is trifling—and immediately talked of other things.", "슈탈 부인은 마치 젊은 시절의 추억을 바라보듯 흐뭇하게 바라보게 되는 사랑스러운 아이를 대하듯 키티에게 이야기했고, 단 한 번 지나가는 말로, 인간의 모든 슬픔 속에서 위안을 주는 것은 오직 사랑과 믿음뿐이며, 우리를 향한 그리스도의 연민 앞에서는 어떤 슬픔도 하찮지 않다고 말했을 뿐, 곧바로 다른 이야기로 넘어갔다."], ["But in every gesture of Madame Stahl, in every word, in every heavenly—as Kitty called it—look, and above all in the whole story of her life, which she heard from Varenka, Kitty recognized that something “that was important,” of which, till then, she had known nothing.", "그러나 슈탈 부인의 모든 몸짓에서, 모든 말에서, 키티가 천상의 것이라 부르던 모든 눈길에서, 그리고 무엇보다 바렌카에게서 들은 그녀의 일생 이야기 전체에서, 키티는 그때까지 전혀 알지 못했던 그 무언가, '중요한 것'을 알아보았다."]], [["Yet, elevated as Madame Stahl’s character was, touching as was her story, and exalted and moving as was her speech, Kitty could not help detecting in her some traits which perplexed her.", "그러나 슈탈 부인의 성품이 아무리 고결하고, 그녀의 일생이 아무리 가슴을 울리며, 그녀의 말이 아무리 숭고하고 감동적이었다 해도, 키티는 그녀에게서 자신을 어리둥절하게 만드는 몇 가지 면모를 발견하지 않을 수 없었다."], ["She noticed that when questioning her about her family, Madame Stahl had smiled contemptuously, which was not in accord with Christian meekness.", "그녀의 가족에 대해 물었을 때 슈탈 부인이 경멸하듯 미소 지었다는 것을 키티는 알아챘는데, 그것은 기독교적인 온유함과는 어울리지 않았다."], ["She noticed, too, that when she had found a Catholic priest with her, Madame Stahl had studiously kept her face in the shadow of the lamp-shade and had smiled in a peculiar way.", "또 슈탈 부인 곁에서 가톨릭 사제를 발견했을 때, 그녀가 일부러 갓 그늘 속에 얼굴을 감추고 묘한 방식으로 미소 지었다는 것도 알아챘다."], ["Trivial as these two observations were, they perplexed her, and she had her doubts as to Madame Stahl.", "이 두 가지 관찰은 사소한 것이었지만 그녀를 어리둥절하게 만들었고, 그녀는 슈탈 부인에 대해 의심을 품게 되었다."], ["But on the other hand Varenka, alone in the world, without friends or relations, with a melancholy disappointment in the past, desiring nothing, regretting nothing, was just that perfection of which Kitty dared hardly dream.", "그러나 다른 한편으로 바렌카는, 이 세상에 홀로 친구도 친척도 없이, 과거에 우울한 환멸을 겪었으면서도 아무것도 바라지 않고 아무것도 후회하지 않는, 바로 키티가 감히 꿈꾸지도 못할 그 완벽함 자체였다."], ["In Varenka she realized that one has but to forget oneself and love others, and one will be calm, happy, and noble.", "바렌카를 통해 그녀는 자기 자신을 잊고 남을 사랑하기만 하면 차분해지고 행복해지며 고결해진다는 것을 깨달았다."], ["And that was what Kitty longed to be.", "그것이 바로 키티가 되고자 갈망하던 것이었다."], ["Seeing now clearly what was the most important, Kitty was not satisfied with being enthusiastic over it; she at once gave herself up with her whole soul to the new life that was opening to her.", "무엇이 가장 중요한 것인지를 이제 분명히 보게 된 키티는 그것에 열광하는 것만으로는 만족하지 못했다. 그녀는 곧바로 자기 앞에 열리고 있는 새로운 삶에 온 영혼을 다해 자신을 내맡겼다."], ["From Varenka’s accounts of the doings of Madame Stahl and other people whom she mentioned, Kitty had already constructed the plan of her own future life.", "바렌카가 들려준 슈탈 부인과 그녀가 언급한 다른 사람들의 행적에 관한 이야기를 통해 키티는 이미 자기 미래의 삶에 대한 계획을 세워 놓고 있었다."], ["She would, like Madame Stahl’s niece, Aline, of whom Varenka had talked to her a great deal, seek out those who were in trouble, wherever she might be living, help them as far as she could, give them the Gospel, read the Gospel to the sick, to criminals, to the dying.", "그녀는 바렌카가 자주 이야기해 준 슈탈 부인의 조카 알린처럼, 자기가 어디에 살든 어려움에 처한 사람들을 찾아내어 할 수 있는 한 그들을 돕고, 그들에게 복음서를 주고, 병자와 죄인과 죽어 가는 이들에게 복음서를 읽어 줄 작정이었다."], ["The idea of reading the Gospel to criminals, as Aline did, particularly fascinated Kitty.", "알린이 그랬듯 죄인들에게 복음서를 읽어 준다는 생각은 특히 키티를 매혹시켰다."], ["But all these were secret dreams, of which Kitty did not talk either to her mother or to Varenka.", "그러나 이 모든 것은 은밀한 꿈이었고, 키티는 그것을 어머니에게도 바렌카에게도 이야기하지 않았다."]], [["While awaiting the time for carrying out her plans on a large scale, however, Kitty, even then at the springs, where there were so many people ill and unhappy, readily found a chance for practicing her new principles in imitation of Varenka.", "그러나 자신의 계획을 큰 규모로 실행에 옮길 때를 기다리는 동안에도, 키티는 그때 이미 병들고 불행한 사람들이 그토록 많은 온천에서 바렌카를 본받아 자신의 새로운 원칙을 실천할 기회를 어렵지 않게 찾아냈다."]], [["At first the princess noticed nothing but that Kitty was much under the influence of her engouement, as she called it, for Madame Stahl, and still more for Varenka.", "처음에 공작 부인은 키티가 그녀의 말마따나 슈탈 부인에 대한, 그리고 더욱이 바렌카에 대한 앙구망에 푹 빠져 있다는 것밖에는 알아채지 못했다."], ["She saw that Kitty did not merely imitate Varenka in her conduct, but unconsciously imitated her in her manner of walking, of talking, of blinking her eyes.", "그녀는 키티가 바렌카의 행동을 본받을 뿐 아니라 무의식적으로 그녀의 걸음걸이며 말투며 눈을 깜박이는 방식까지 흉내 내고 있는 것을 보았다."], ["But later on the princess noticed that, apart from this adoration, some kind of serious spiritual change was taking place in her daughter.", "그러나 나중에 공작 부인은 이러한 숭배 말고도 딸에게서 어떤 진지한 영적 변화가 일어나고 있음을 알아챘다. * 앙구망(engouement): 프랑스어로 '맹목적인 심취', '열중'."]], [["The princess saw that in the evenings Kitty read a French testament that Madame Stahl had given her—a thing she had never done before; that she avoided society acquaintances and associated with the sick people who were under Varenka’s protection, and especially one poor family, that of a sick painter, Petrov.", "공작 부인은 저녁이면 키티가 슈탈 부인에게서 받은 프랑스어 성경을 읽는 것을 보았다. 전에는 한 번도 한 적 없는 일이었다."], ["Kitty was unmistakably proud of playing the part of a sister of mercy in that family.", "또 딸이 사교계의 지인들을 피하고 바렌카의 보살핌을 받는 병자들, 특히 병든 화가 페트로프의 가난한 가족과 어울리는 것을 보았다. 키티가 그 가족에게 자비의 수녀 역할을 하는 것을 자랑스러워한다는 것은 의심할 여지가 없었다."], ["All this was well enough, and the princess had nothing to say against it, especially as Petrov’s wife was a perfectly nice sort of woman, and that the German princess, noticing Kitty’s devotion, praised her, calling her an angel of consolation.", "이 모든 것은 그런대로 괜찮았고, 공작 부인도 그것에 대해 딱히 할 말은 없었다. 더욱이 페트로프의 아내는 더없이 착한 여자였고, 키티의 헌신을 본 독일 공작 부인이 그녀를 위로의 천사라 부르며 칭찬하기까지 했으니 말이다."], ["All this would have been very well, if there had been no exaggeration.", "지나침만 없었다면 이 모든 것은 아주 좋았을 것이다."], ["But the princess saw that her daughter was rushing into extremes, and so indeed she told her.", "그러나 공작 부인은 딸이 극단으로 치닫고 있는 것을 보았고, 그래서 정말로 딸에게 그렇게 말했다."]], [["“Il ne faut jamais rien outrer,”", "\"무슨 일이든 결코 도를 넘어서는 안 된단다.\""], ["she said to her.", "그녀가 딸에게 말했다."]], [["Her daughter made her no reply, only in her heart she thought that one could not talk about exaggeration where Christianity was concerned.", "딸은 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았지만, 마음속으로는 기독교에 관한 일에 지나침을 운운할 수는 없다고 생각했다."], ["What exaggeration could there be in the practice of a doctrine wherein one was bidden to turn the other cheek when one was smitten, and give one’s cloak if one’s coat were taken?", "한쪽 뺨을 맞으면 다른 쪽 뺨을 내밀고, 겉옷을 빼앗기면 속옷마저 내주라고 명하는 교리를 실천하는 데 무슨 지나침이 있을 수 있겠는가?"], ["But the princess disliked this exaggeration, and disliked even more the fact that she felt her daughter did not care to show her all her heart.", "그러나 공작 부인은 이러한 지나침이 못마땅했고, 딸이 자기에게 속마음을 다 보여 주려 하지 않는다는 것이 더더욱 못마땅했다."], ["Kitty did in fact conceal her new views and feelings from her mother.", "키티는 실제로 자신의 새로운 견해와 감정을 어머니에게 숨겼다."], ["She concealed them not because she did not respect or did not love her mother, but simply because she was her mother.", "그녀가 그것을 숨긴 것은 어머니를 존경하지 않거나 사랑하지 않아서가 아니라, 단지 그녀가 자기 어머니였기 때문이다. 그녀는 어머니보다는 차라리 다른 누구에게라도 먼저 그것을 털어놓았을 것이다."], ["She would have revealed them to anyone sooner than to her mother.", "* 무슨 일이든…안 된단다(Il ne faut jamais rien outrer): 프랑스어."]], [["“How is it Anna Pavlovna’s not been to see us for so long?”", "\"안나 파블로브나는 어째서 그렇게 오랫동안 우리를 보러 오지 않는 거니?\""], ["the princess said one day of Madame Petrova.", "어느 날 공작 부인이 페트로바 부인을 두고 말했다."], ["“I’ve asked her, but she seems put out about something.”", "\"내가 청해 봤는데도 뭔가에 기분이 상한 것 같더구나.\""]], [["“No, I’ve not noticed it, maman,”", "\"아니에요, 저는 못 느꼈는데요, maman.\""], ["said Kitty, flushing hotly.", "키티가 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다."]], [["“Is it long since you went to see them?”", "\"그 사람들을 보러 간 지 오래됐니?\""]], [["“We’re meaning to make an expedition to the mountains tomorrow,”", "\"내일 산으로 소풍을 갈 작정이에요.\""], ["answered Kitty.", "키티가 대답했다."]], [["“Well, you can go,”", "\"그래, 다녀오렴.\""], ["answered the princess, gazing at her daughter’s embarrassed face and trying to guess the cause of her embarrassment.", "공작 부인은 딸의 당황한 얼굴을 바라보며 그 당혹감의 원인을 알아내려 애쓰면서 대답했다."]], [["That day Varenka came to dinner and told them that Anna Pavlovna had changed her mind and given up the expedition for the morrow.", "그날 바렌카가 저녁 식사를 하러 와서, 안나 파블로브나가 마음을 바꿔 내일 소풍을 그만두기로 했다고 전했다."], ["And the princess noticed again that Kitty reddened.", "그리고 공작 부인은 키티가 또다시 얼굴을 붉히는 것을 알아챘다."]], [["“Kitty, haven’t you had some misunderstanding with the Petrovs?”", "\"키티, 페트로프네 사람들과 무슨 오해라도 있었던 거니?\""], ["said the princess, when they were left alone.", "둘만 남게 되었을 때 공작 부인이 말했다."], ["“Why has she given up sending the children and coming to see us?”", "\"어째서 그 사람이 아이들을 보내지도 않고 우리를 보러 오지도 않게 된 거지?\""]], [["Kitty answered that nothing had happened between them, and that she could not tell why Anna Pavlovna seemed displeased with her.", "키티는 그들 사이에 아무 일도 없었으며, 안나 파블로브나가 왜 자기에게 언짢아하는 것 같은지 알 수 없다고 대답했다."], ["Kitty answered perfectly truly.", "키티는 더없이 정직하게 대답했다."], ["She did not know the reason Anna Pavlovna had changed to her, but she guessed it.", "그녀는 안나 파블로브나가 자기에게 태도를 바꾼 이유를 알지 못했지만, 짐작은 했다."], ["She guessed at something which she could not tell her mother, which she did not put into words to herself.", "그녀는 어머니에게 말할 수 없는, 자기 자신에게조차 말로 표현하지 못하는 무언가를 짐작했다."], ["It was one of those things which one knows but which one can never speak of even to oneself, so terrible and shameful would it be to be mistaken.", "그것은 알고는 있으나 자기 자신에게조차 결코 입에 담을 수 없는 그런 것들 중 하나였으니, 만약 잘못 짚은 것이라면 너무도 끔찍하고 부끄러운 일일 터였다."]], [["Again and again she went over in her memory all her relations with the family.", "그녀는 그 가족과 맺은 모든 관계를 거듭거듭 기억 속에서 되짚어 보았다."], ["She remembered the simple delight expressed on the round, good-humored face of Anna Pavlovna at their meetings; she remembered their secret confabulations about the invalid, their plots to draw him away from the work which was forbidden him, and to get him out-of-doors; the devotion of the youngest boy, who used to call her “my Kitty,”", "만날 때마다 안나 파블로브나의 둥글고 사람 좋은 얼굴에 떠오르던 순수한 기쁨을 떠올렸다. 환자에 관해 둘이서 나누던 은밀한 의논, 그에게 금지된 일에서 그를 떼어 놓고 바깥바람을 쐬게 하려던 둘의 모의도 떠올렸다."], ["and would not go to bed without her. How nice it all was!", "그녀를 \"나의 키티\"라고 부르며 그녀 없이는 잠자리에 들려고 하지 않던 막내아이의 애정도 떠올렸다."], ["Then she recalled the thin, terribly thin figure of Petrov, with his long neck, in his brown coat, his scant, curly hair, his questioning blue eyes that were so terrible to Kitty at first, and his painful attempts to seem hearty and lively in her presence.", "그 모든 것이 얼마나 좋았던가! 그러고 나서 그녀는 갈색 외투를 걸친 페트로프의 무섭도록 마르고 야윈 모습을, 긴 목과 숱 적은 곱슬머리, 처음에는 키티에게 그토록 무섭게 느껴지던 무언가를 묻는 듯한 푸른 눈, 그녀 앞에서 기운차고 활기차 보이려고 애쓰던 안쓰러운 모습을 떠올렸다."], ["She recalled the efforts she had made at first to overcome the repugnance she felt for him, as for all consumptive people, and the pains it had cost her to think of things to say to him.", "모든 폐병 환자에게 그렇듯 그에게도 느끼던 혐오감을 이겨 내려고 처음에 기울였던 노력과, 그에게 무슨 말을 할까 궁리하느라 들였던 수고를 떠올렸다."], ["She recalled the timid, softened look with which he gazed at her, and the strange feeling of compassion and awkwardness, and later of a sense of her own goodness, which she had felt at it.", "그가 그녀를 바라보던 수줍고 부드러워진 눈길과, 그때 느꼈던 연민과 어색함이라는 묘한 감정, 그리고 나중에는 자기 자신이 착한 사람이라는 느낌까지도 떠올렸다. 그 모든 것이 얼마나 좋았던가!"], ["How nice it all was! But all that was at first.", "그러나 그것은 다 처음의 일이었다."], ["Now, a few days ago, everything was suddenly spoiled.", "그런데 며칠 전, 모든 것이 갑자기 망가져 버렸다."], ["Anna Pavlovna had met Kitty with affected cordiality, and had kept continual watch on her and on her husband.", "안나 파블로브나는 가식적인 다정함으로 키티를 맞이했고, 그녀와 자기 남편을 끊임없이 감시했다."]], [["Could that touching pleasure he showed when she came near be the cause of Anna Pavlovna’s coolness?", "그녀가 가까이 다가갈 때 그가 보이던 그 가슴 뭉클한 기쁨이 안나 파블로브나가 냉랭해진 원인일 수 있을까?"]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그래.\" 그녀는 곰곰이 생각했다."], ["she mused, “there was something unnatural about Anna Pavlovna, and utterly unlike her good nature, when she said angrily the day before yesterday: ‘There, he will keep waiting for you; he wouldn’t drink his coffee without you, though he’s grown so dreadfully weak.’”", "\"그저께 안나 파블로브나가 화난 목소리로 '저것 봐요, 그이는 계속 당신을 기다릴 거예요. 그렇게 끔찍이도 쇠약해졌으면서도 당신 없이는 커피도 안 마시려 들었다니까요'라고 했을 때, 그녀에게는 뭔가 부자연스러운, 그녀의 선량한 천성과는 전혀 어울리지 않는 데가 있었어.\""]], [["“Yes, perhaps, too, she didn’t like it when I gave him the rug.", "\"그래, 어쩌면 내가 그에게 무릎 덮개를 건넸을 때 그녀가 그걸 못마땅해했는지도 몰라."], ["It was all so simple, but he took it so awkwardly, and was so long thanking me, that I felt awkward too.", "그건 정말 아무것도 아닌 일이었는데, 그가 그걸 너무 어색하게 받고 너무 오래도록 내게 고마워하는 바람에 나까지 어색해졌지."], ["And then that portrait of me he did so well.", "게다가 그가 그토록 잘 그려 준 내 초상화도 있고."], ["And most of all that look of confusion and tenderness!", "그리고 무엇보다 그 당황하고 다정한 눈길이!"], ["Yes, yes, that’s it!”", "그래, 그래, 바로 그거야!\""], ["Kitty repeated to herself with horror.", "키티는 공포에 질려 속으로 거듭 되뇌었다."], ["“No, it can’t be, it oughtn’t to be!", "\"아니야, 그럴 리 없어, 그래서는 안 돼!"], ["He’s so much to be pitied!”", "그는 너무도 가엾은 사람인데!\""], ["she said to herself directly after.", "그녀는 곧바로 자기 자신에게 말했다."]], [["This doubt poisoned the charm of her new life.", "이 의심이 그녀의 새로운 삶이 지닌 매력을 망가뜨렸다."]], [["# Chapter 34", "# Chapter 34"]], [["Before the end of the course of drinking the waters, Prince Shtcherbatsky, who had gone on from Carlsbad to Baden and Kissingen to Russian friends—to get a breath of Russian air, as he said—came back to his wife and daughter.", "치료를 위한 온천 음용 요법이 끝나기 전에, 카를스바트를 떠나 바덴과 키싱겐의 러시아 친구들을 찾아갔던—본인 말로는 러시아 공기를 좀 쐬러 갔던—셰르바츠키 공작이 아내와 딸에게로 돌아왔다."]], [["The views of the prince and of the princess on life abroad were completely opposed.", "외국 생활에 대한 공작과 공작 부인의 견해는 완전히 정반대였다."], ["The princess thought everything delightful, and in spite of her established position in Russian society, she tried abroad to be like a European fashionable lady, which she was not—for the simple reason that she was a typical Russian gentlewoman; and so she was affected, which did not altogether suit her.", "공작 부인은 무엇이든 다 즐거워했고, 러시아 사교계에서 확고한 지위를 누리고 있었음에도 외국에서는 유럽풍의 멋쟁이 귀부인처럼 굴려고 애썼다. 그러나 그녀는 그런 부류가 아니었으니—이유는 단순했다, 그녀는 전형적인 러시아 귀부인이었으므로—그래서 그녀의 태도는 어딘가 가식적이었고, 그것이 그녀에게 그리 어울리지도 않았다."], ["The prince, on the contrary, thought everything foreign detestable, got sick of European life, kept to his Russian habits, and purposely tried to show himself abroad less European than he was in reality.", "공작은 그와 반대로, 외국적인 것이라면 무엇이든 질색했고, 유럽 생활에 넌더리를 냈으며, 자신의 러시아식 습관을 고수했고, 외국에서는 일부러 실제보다 덜 유럽적으로 보이려 들었다."]], [["The prince returned thinner, with the skin hanging in loose bags on his cheeks, but in the most cheerful frame of mind.", "공작은 더 야윈 모습으로, 두 뺨의 살가죽이 헐렁한 주머니처럼 늘어진 채 돌아왔지만, 더없이 쾌활한 기분이었다."], ["His good humor was even greater when he saw Kitty completely recovered.", "키티가 완전히 회복한 것을 보자 그의 좋은 기분은 한층 더해졌다."], ["The news of Kitty’s friendship with Madame Stahl and Varenka, and the reports the princess gave him of some kind of change she had noticed in Kitty, troubled the prince and aroused his habitual feeling of jealousy of everything that drew his daughter away from him, and a dread that his daughter might have got out of the reach of his influence into regions inaccessible to him.", "키티가 슈탈 부인과 바렌카와 가깝게 지낸다는 소식, 그리고 공작 부인이 키티에게서 어떤 변화를 알아챘다며 전한 이야기는 공작의 마음을 어지럽혔고, 자기 딸을 자기에게서 떼어 놓는 모든 것에 대한 그의 습관적인 질투심과, 자기 딸이 자신의 영향이 미치지 못하는 곳, 그가 다다를 수 없는 영역으로 빠져나가 버렸을지도 모른다는 두려움을 불러일으켰다."], ["But these unpleasant matters were all drowned in the sea of kindliness and good humor which was always within him, and more so than ever since his course of Carlsbad waters.", "그러나 이런 불쾌한 일들은 그의 안에 늘 자리 잡고 있던, 그리고 카를스바트 온천 요법 이후로 그 어느 때보다 넘쳐흐르던 친절과 명랑함의 바다에 죄다 잠겨 버렸다."]], [["The day after his arrival the prince, in his long overcoat, with his Russian wrinkles and baggy cheeks propped up by a starched collar, set off with his daughter to the spring in the greatest good humor.", "도착한 다음 날, 공작은 긴 외투를 걸치고, 풀 먹인 깃으로 러시아식 주름과 늘어진 뺨을 떠받친 채, 더없이 좋은 기분으로 딸과 함께 온천으로 향했다."]], [["It was a lovely morning: the bright, cheerful houses with their little gardens, the sight of the red-faced, red-armed, beer-drinking German waitresses, working away merrily, did the heart good.", "화창한 아침이었다. 작은 정원이 딸린 밝고 명랑한 집들, 발그레한 얼굴과 발그레한 팔로 맥주를 들이켜며 즐겁게 일하는 독일 여종업원들의 모습은 마음을 흐뭇하게 했다."], ["But the nearer they got to the springs the oftener they met sick people; and their appearance seemed more pitiable than ever among the everyday conditions of prosperous German life.", "그러나 온천에 가까워질수록 병자들과 더 자주 마주쳤고, 그들의 모습은 풍요로운 독일 생활의 일상적인 풍경 속에서 그 어느 때보다 더 가련해 보였다."], ["Kitty was no longer struck by this contrast.", "키티는 더 이상 이런 대조에 충격을 받지 않았다."], ["The bright sun, the brilliant green of the foliage, the strains of the music were for her the natural setting of all these familiar faces, with their changes to greater emaciation or to convalescence, for which she watched.", "그녀에게는 밝은 햇살도, 눈부신 초록빛 나뭇잎도, 흘러나오는 음악 소리도, 그녀가 더 야위어 가는지 아니면 회복되어 가는지 눈여겨보던 그 낯익은 얼굴들의 자연스러운 배경일 뿐이었다."], ["But to the prince the brightness and gaiety of the June morning, and the sound of the orchestra playing a gay waltz then in fashion, and above all, the appearance of the healthy attendants, seemed something unseemly and monstrous, in conjunction with these slowly moving, dying figures gathered together from all parts of Europe.", "그러나 공작에게는 6월 아침의 밝음과 흥겨움도, 당시 유행하던 경쾌한 왈츠를 연주하는 오케스트라 소리도, 무엇보다 건강한 수행원들의 모습도, 유럽 곳곳에서 모여든 이 느릿느릿 움직이며 죽어 가는 형체들과 나란히 놓이니 어딘가 꼴사납고 흉측한 것으로 비쳤다."], ["In spite of his feeling of pride and, as it were, of the return of youth, with his favorite daughter on his arm, he felt awkward, and almost ashamed of his vigorous step and his sturdy, stout limbs.", "가장 아끼는 딸을 팔에 걸치고 느끼는 자랑스러움과, 말하자면 다시 젊어진 듯한 기분에도 불구하고, 그는 어색함을 느꼈고, 자신의 힘찬 걸음걸이와 튼튼하고 우람한 팔다리가 거의 부끄러울 지경이었다."], ["He felt almost like a man not dressed in a crowd.", "마치 군중 속에서 혼자 옷을 제대로 갖춰 입지 못한 사람이 된 듯한 기분이었다."]], [["“Present me to your new friends,”", "\"네 새 친구들을 좀 소개해 다오.\""], ["he said to his daughter, squeezing her hand with his elbow.", "그는 팔꿈치로 딸의 손을 꼭 누르며 말했다."], ["“I like even your horrid Soden for making you so well again.", "\"너를 이렇게 다시 건강하게 해 준 그 끔찍한 조덴까지도 마음에 드는구나."], ["Only it’s melancholy, very melancholy here.", "다만 여긴 우울해, 무척이나 우울하구나."], ["Who’s that?”", "저 사람은 누구냐?\""]], [["Kitty mentioned the names of all the people they met, with some of whom she was acquainted and some not.", "키티는 마주치는 사람들의 이름을 일일이 댔는데, 그중에는 아는 사람도 있었고 모르는 사람도 있었다. 정원 입구에서 그들은 눈먼 여인 베르트 부인과 그녀를 인도하는 사람과 마주쳤고, 공작은 그 늙은 프랑스 여인이 키티의 목소리를 듣자 얼굴이 환해지는 것을 보고 기뻐했다."], ["At the entrance of the garden they met the blind lady, Madame Berthe, with her guide, and the prince was delighted to see the old Frenchwoman’s face light up when she heard Kitty’s voice.", "그녀는 곧장 프랑스식의 과장된 정중함으로 그에게 말을 걸기 시작했고, 이렇게 사랑스러운 딸을 두었다며 그를 칭찬하고, 키티를 면전에서 하늘 끝까지 추켜세우며, 그녀를 보배니 진주니 위안을 주는 천사니 하고 불렀다. \"그렇다면 이 아이는 두 번째 천사로군요.\""], ["She at once began talking to him with French exaggerated politeness, applauding him for having such a delightful daughter, extolling Kitty to the skies before her face, and calling her a treasure, a pearl, and a consoling angel.", "공작이 미소 지으며 말했다. \"마드무아젤 바렌카를 일 번 천사라고 부른다지요.\""]], [["“Well, she’s the second angel, then,”", "\"오! 마드무아젤 바렌카, 그 아이야말로 진짜 천사지요, allez.\""], ["said the prince, smiling. “she calls Mademoiselle Varenka angel number one.”", "베르트 부인이 맞장구를 쳤다."]], [["“Oh! Mademoiselle Varenka, she’s a real angel, allez,”", "* allez: 프랑스어로 '정말이지', '그렇고말고요'"], ["Madame Berthe assented.", "정도의 감탄적 표현."]], [["In the arcade they met Varenka herself.", "아케이드에서 그들은 바렌카와 마주쳤다."], ["She was walking rapidly towards them carrying an elegant red bag.", "그녀는 우아한 빨간 가방을 들고 빠른 걸음으로 그들 쪽으로 다가오고 있었다."]], [["“Here is papa come,”", "\"아빠가 오셨어요.\""], ["Kitty said to her.", "키티가 그녀에게 말했다."]], [["Varenka made—simply and naturally as she did everything—a movement between a bow and a curtsey, and immediately began talking to the prince, without shyness, naturally, as she talked to everyone.", "바렌카는—그녀가 무엇이든 그렇게 하듯 소박하고 자연스럽게—절과 무릎 굽혀 인사하는 동작의 중간쯤 되는 몸짓을 했고, 곧바로 수줍음 없이, 자연스럽게, 누구에게나 말하듯 공작에게 말을 건넸다."]], [["“Of course I know you; I know you very well,”", "\"물론 알고말고요, 아주 잘 알지요.\""], ["the prince said to her with a smile, in which Kitty detected with joy that her father liked her friend.", "공작이 미소를 띠며 그녀에게 말했는데, 키티는 그 미소에서 아버지가 자기 친구를 마음에 들어 한다는 것을 기쁘게 알아차렸다."], ["“Where are you off to in such haste?”", "\"그렇게 서둘러 어디로 가는 길인가요?\""]], [["“Maman’s here,”", "\"엄마가 여기 계세요.\""], ["she said, turning to Kitty.", "그녀가 키티를 돌아보며 말했다."], ["“She has not slept all night, and the doctor advised her to go out.", "\"밤새 한숨도 못 주무셔서, 의사가 바깥바람을 쐬라고 권했거든."], ["I’m taking her her work.”", "손일거리를 갖다 드리는 길이야.\""]], [["“So that’s angel number one?”", "\"그래, 저 아이가 일 번 천사인 거냐?\""], ["said the prince when Varenka had gone on.", "바렌카가 가 버리자 공작이 물었다."]], [["Kitty saw that her father had meant to make fun of Varenka, but that he could not do it because he liked her.", "키티는 아버지가 바렌카를 놀려 줄 작정이었으나, 그녀가 마음에 들어서 그러지 못한다는 것을 알아차렸다."]], [["“Come, so we shall see all your friends,”", "\"자, 그러니 네 친구들을 다 만나 보게 되겠구나.\""], ["he went on, “even Madame Stahl, if she deigns to recognize me.”", "그가 말을 이었다. \"슈탈 부인이 황송하게도 나를 알아봐 준다면 그 부인까지도 말이다.\""]], [["“Why, did you know her, papa?”", "\"아니, 그 부인을 아세요, 아빠?\""], ["Kitty asked apprehensively, catching the gleam of irony that kindled in the prince’s eyes at the mention of Madame Stahl.", "키티가 슈탈 부인이라는 이름이 나오자 공작의 눈에 번뜩이는 아이러니의 기색을 알아채고는 조심스럽게 물었다."]], [["“I used to know her husband, and her too a little, before she’d joined the Pietists.”", "\"그 여자 남편을 알았고, 그 여자도 조금은 알았지, 경건주의자들 무리에 끼기 전에 말이다.\""]], [["“What is a Pietist, papa?”", "\"경건주의자가 뭐예요, 아빠?\""], ["asked Kitty, dismayed to find that what she prized so highly in Madame Stahl had a name.", "슈탈 부인에게서 자기가 그토록 높이 떠받들던 것에 이름이 붙어 있다는 것을 알고 당황한 키티가 물었다."]], [["“I don’t quite know myself.", "\"나도 잘은 몰라."], ["I only know that she thanks God for everything, for every misfortune, and thanks God too that her husband died.", "다만 그 여자가 무슨 일이든, 어떤 불행에 대해서든 하느님께 감사를 드린다는 것, 게다가 남편이 죽은 것에도 하느님께 감사를 드린다는 것만은 안다."], ["And that’s rather droll, as they didn’t get on together.”", "그런데 그게 좀 우습단 말이지, 두 사람은 사이가 좋지 않았으니까.\""]], [["“Who’s that?", "\"저 사람은 누구냐?"], ["What a piteous face!”", "어쩜 저리 가련한 얼굴인지!\""], ["he asked, noticing a sick man of medium height sitting on a bench, wearing a brown overcoat and white trousers that fell in strange folds about his long, fleshless legs.", "그가 갈색 외투에 흰 바지를 입고 벤치에 앉아 있는 중키의 병자를 알아보고 물었다. 그 바지는 길고 살 없는 다리께에서 이상한 주름을 지으며 흘러내려 있었다."], ["This man lifted his straw hat, showed his scanty curly hair and high forehead, painfully reddened by the pressure of the hat.", "그 남자는 밀짚모자를 들어 올리며, 듬성듬성한 곱슬머리와 모자에 눌려 보기 안쓰럽게 붉어진 높은 이마를 드러냈다."]], [["“That’s Petrov, an artist,”", "\"화가 페트로프예요.\""], ["answered Kitty, blushing.", "키티가 얼굴을 붉히며 대답했다."], ["“And that’s his wife,”", "\"그리고 저쪽이 그의 아내고요.\""], ["she added, indicating Anna Pavlovna, who, as though on purpose, at the very instant they approached walked away after a child that had run off along a path.", "그녀가 안나 파블로브나를 가리키며 덧붙였는데, 그 부인은 마치 일부러 그러기라도 하듯, 그들이 다가가는 바로 그 순간에 오솔길을 따라 달아난 아이를 쫓아가 버렸다."]], [["“Poor fellow!", "\"가엾은 사람!"], ["and what a nice face he has!”", "게다가 얼마나 선한 얼굴인지!\""], ["said the prince.", "공작이 말했다."], ["“Why don’t you go up to him?", "\"어째서 가서 인사를 안 하느냐?"], ["He wanted to speak to you.”", "너에게 말을 걸고 싶어 했잖니.\""]], [["“Well, let us go, then,”", "\"그럼, 가 봐요.\""], ["said Kitty, turning round resolutely.", "키티가 단호하게 돌아서며 말했다."], ["“How are you feeling today?”", "\"오늘은 좀 어떠세요?\""], ["she asked Petrov.", "그녀가 페트로프에게 물었다."]], [["Petrov got up, leaning on his stick, and looked shyly at the prince.", "페트로프는 지팡이에 의지해 일어서서는 수줍게 공작을 바라보았다."]], [["“This is my daughter,”", "\"제 딸입니다.\""], ["said the prince.", "공작이 말했다."], ["“Let me introduce myself.”", "\"통성명이나 합시다.\""]], [["The painter bowed and smiled, showing his strangely dazzling white teeth.", "화가는 고개 숙여 인사하고 미소 지었는데, 묘하게 눈부신 흰 이가 드러났다."]], [["“We expected you yesterday, princess,”", "\"어제 오시기를 기다렸는데요, 공작 영애.\""], ["he said to Kitty.", "그가 키티에게 말했다."], ["He staggered as he said this, and then repeated the motion, trying to make it seem as if it had been intentional.", "이 말을 하면서 그는 비틀거렸고, 그러고는 마치 일부러 그런 것처럼 보이려고 같은 동작을 다시 해 보였다."]], [["“I meant to come, but Varenka said that Anna Pavlovna sent word you were not going.”", "\"오려고 했는데, 안나 파블로브나가 안 오신다고 전해 왔다고 바렌카가 그러더라고요.\""]], [["“Not going!”", "\"안 오다니!\""], ["said Petrov, blushing, and immediately beginning to cough, and his eyes sought his wife.", "페트로프가 얼굴을 붉히며 곧 기침을 하기 시작했고, 그의 눈은 아내를 찾았다."], ["“Anita!", "\"아니타!"], ["Anita!”", "아니타!\""], ["he said loudly, and the swollen veins stood out like cords on his thin white neck.", "그가 큰 소리로 불렀고, 가늘고 흰 목에 부풀어 오른 핏줄이 밧줄처럼 돋아났다."]], [["Anna Pavlovna came up.", "안나 파블로브나가 다가왔다."]], [["“So you sent word to the princess that we weren’t going!”", "\"공작 영애께 우리가 안 간다고 전했단 말이오!\""], ["he whispered to her angrily, losing his voice.", "그가 목소리를 잃은 채 화가 나서 그녀에게 속삭였다."]], [["“Good morning, princess,”", "\"안녕하세요, 공작 영애.\""], ["said Anna Pavlovna, with an assumed smile utterly unlike her former manner.", "안나 파블로브나가 전과는 전혀 딴판인 꾸민 미소를 지으며 말했다."], ["“Very glad to make your acquaintance,”", "\"뵙게 되어 무척 기쁩니다.\""], ["she said to the prince.", "그녀가 공작에게 말했다."], ["“You’ve long been expected, prince.”", "\"오래전부터 기다렸답니다, 공작님.\""]], [["“What did you send word to the princess that we weren’t going for?”", "\"공작 영애께 우리가 안 간다고 도대체 왜 전했느냔 말이오?\""], ["the artist whispered hoarsely once more, still more angrily, obviously exasperated that his voice failed him so that he could not give his words the expression he would have liked to.", "화가가 다시 한번, 한층 더 화가 나서 쉰 목소리로 속삭였는데, 목소리가 말을 듣지 않아 자기가 바라는 만큼 말에 표정을 실을 수 없다는 것에 분명 약이 올라 있었다."]], [["“Oh, mercy on us!", "\"어머, 세상에!"], ["I thought we weren’t going,”", "난 우리가 안 가는 줄 알았죠.\""], ["his wife answered crossly.", "아내가 짜증스럽게 대꾸했다."]], [["“What, when....”", "\"아니, 언제 우리가…….\""], ["He coughed and waved his hand.", "그는 기침을 하고는 손을 내저었다."], ["The prince took off his hat and moved away with his daughter.", "공작은 모자를 벗고 딸과 함께 자리를 떴다."]], [["“Ah!", "\"아아!"], ["ah!”", "아아!\""], ["he sighed deeply.", "그가 깊이 한숨을 쉬었다."], ["“Oh, poor things!”", "\"오, 가엾은 사람들 같으니!\""]], [["“Yes, papa,”", "\"네, 아빠.\""], ["answered Kitty.", "키티가 대답했다."], ["“And you must know they’ve three children, no servant, and scarcely any means.", "\"그리고 저분들은 아이가 셋이나 있는데, 하인도 없고, 가진 것도 거의 없다는 걸 아셔야 해요."], ["He gets something from the Academy,”", "그분은 아카데미에서 얼마쯤 받는대요.\""], ["she went on briskly, trying to drown the distress that the queer change in Anna Pavlovna’s manner to her had aroused in her.", "그녀는 안나 파블로브나의 태도가 묘하게 바뀐 것이 자기 마음에 불러일으킨 괴로움을 떨쳐 내려 애쓰며 활기차게 말을 이었다."]], [["“Oh, here’s Madame Stahl,”", "\"오, 저기 슈탈 부인이 계세요.\""], ["said Kitty, indicating an invalid carriage, where, propped on pillows, something in gray and blue was lying under a sunshade.", "키티가 환자용 마차를 가리키며 말했다. 그 안에는 베개에 기댄 채, 회색과 푸른색의 무언가가 양산 아래 누워 있었다."], ["This was Madame Stahl.", "그것이 슈탈 부인이었다."], ["Behind her stood the gloomy, healthy-looking German workman who pushed the carriage.", "그녀 뒤에는 마차를 미는, 침울하고 건강해 보이는 독일인 일꾼이 서 있었다."], ["Close by was standing a flaxen-headed Swedish count, whom Kitty knew by name.", "바로 곁에는 키티가 이름은 알고 있던 아마빛 머리의 스웨덴 백작이 서 있었다."], ["Several invalids were lingering near the low carriage, staring at the lady as though she were some curiosity.", "몇몇 병자들이 그 낮은 마차 가까이서 서성이며, 마치 무슨 구경거리라도 되는 양 그 부인을 빤히 바라보고 있었다."]], [["The prince went up to her, and Kitty detected that disconcerting gleam of irony in his eyes.", "공작이 그녀에게 다가갔고, 키티는 그의 눈에 떠오른 그 사람을 당황케 하는 아이러니의 기색을 알아챘다."], ["He went up to Madame Stahl, and addressed her with extreme courtesy and affability in that excellent French that so few speak nowadays.", "그는 슈탈 부인에게 다가가, 요즈음에는 그토록 구사하는 이가 드문 그 훌륭한 프랑스어로 더없는 정중함과 상냥함으로 그녀에게 말을 건넸다."]], [["“I don’t know if you remember me, but I must recall myself to thank you for your kindness to my daughter,”", "\"저를 기억하실지 모르겠습니다만, 제 딸에게 베풀어 주신 친절에 감사를 드리고자 제 소개를 다시 해야겠군요.\""], ["he said, taking off his hat and not putting it on again.", "그가 모자를 벗고는 다시 쓰지 않은 채 말했다."]], [["“Prince Alexander Shtcherbatsky,”", "\"알렉산드르 셰르바츠키 공작이시군요.\""], ["said Madame Stahl, lifting upon him her heavenly eyes, in which Kitty discerned a look of annoyance.", "슈탈 부인이 그를 향해 천상의 눈을 들어 올리며 말했는데, 키티는 그 눈에서 짜증의 기색을 알아보았다."], ["“Delighted!", "\"반갑습니다!"], ["I have taken a great fancy to your daughter.”", "따님이 무척 마음에 들었답니다.\""]], [["“You are still in weak health?”", "\"여전히 몸이 편찮으십니까?\""]], [["“Yes; I’m used to it,”", "\"네, 이젠 익숙해졌답니다.\""], ["said Madame Stahl, and she introduced the prince to the Swedish count.", "슈탈 부인은 이렇게 말하고는 공작을 스웨덴 백작에게 소개했다."]], [["“You are scarcely changed at all,”", "\"조금도 변하지 않으셨군요.\""], ["the prince said to her.", "공작이 그녀에게 말했다."], ["“It’s ten or eleven years since I had the honor of seeing you.”", "\"당신을 뵙는 영광을 누린 지 십 년인가 십일 년인가 되었습니다만.\""]], [["“Yes; God sends the cross and sends the strength to bear it.", "\"네, 하느님께서는 십자가를 내리시고 또 그것을 짊어질 힘도 내려 주시지요."], ["Often one wonders what is the goal of this life?...", "이따금 이 삶의 목적이 무엇인가 하는 의문이 들기도 한답니다……."], ["The other side!”", "반대쪽이요!\""], ["she said angrily to Varenka, who had rearranged the rug over her feet not to her satisfaction.", "그녀는 발치의 무릎덮개를 마음에 차지 않게 고쳐 놓은 바렌카에게 신경질적으로 말했다."]], [["“To do good, probably,”", "\"아마도 선을 행하는 것이겠지요.\""], ["said the prince with a twinkle in his eye.", "공작이 눈을 반짝이며 말했다."]], [["“That is not for us to judge,”", "\"그건 우리가 판단할 일이 아니지요.\""], ["said Madame Stahl, perceiving the shade of expression on the prince’s face.", "슈탈 부인이 공작의 얼굴에 떠오른 미묘한 표정을 알아채고 말했다."], ["“So you will send me that book, dear count?", "\"그럼 그 책을 보내 주시겠지요, 친애하는 백작님?"], ["I’m very grateful to you,”", "무척 감사드립니다.\""], ["she said to the young Swede.", "그녀가 젊은 스웨덴 사람에게 말했다."]], [["“Ah!”", "\"아!\""], ["cried the prince, catching sight of the Moscow colonel standing near, and with a bow to Madame Stahl he walked away with his daughter and the Moscow colonel, who joined them.", "공작이 가까이 서 있는 모스크바의 대령을 알아보고 소리쳤고, 슈탈 부인에게 인사를 한 뒤 딸과, 그들에게 합류한 모스크바의 대령과 함께 자리를 떴다."]], [["“That’s our aristocracy, prince!”", "\"저런 것이 우리네 귀족이라지요, 공작님!\""], ["the Moscow colonel said with ironical intention.", "모스크바의 대령이 빈정대는 속내를 담아 말했다."], ["He cherished a grudge against Madame Stahl for not making his acquaintance.", "그는 슈탈 부인이 자기와 통성명을 해 주지 않은 것에 앙심을 품고 있었다."]], [["“She’s just the same,”", "\"여전하군요.\""], ["replied the prince.", "공작이 대꾸했다."]], [["“Did you know her before her illness, prince—that’s to say before she took to her bed?”", "\"병이 나기 전에, 그러니까 자리보전을 하기 전에 저 부인을 아셨습니까, 공작님?\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"네."], ["She took to her bed before my eyes,”", "저 여자가 자리보전을 하는 걸 내 눈으로 봤지요.\""], ["said the prince.", "공작이 말했다."]], [["“They say it’s ten years since she has stood on her feet.”", "\"저 부인이 두 발로 선 지가 십 년이 되었다더군요.\""]], [["“She doesn’t stand up because her legs are too short.", "\"저 여자가 일어서지 않는 건 다리가 너무 짧아서요."], ["She’s a very bad figure.”", "몸매가 아주 형편없거든.\""]], [["“Papa, it’s not possible!”", "\"아빠, 그럴 리가 없어요!\""], ["cried Kitty.", "키티가 소리쳤다."]], [["“That’s what wicked tongues say, my darling.", "\"심술궂은 입들이 그렇게들 말한단다, 얘야."], ["And your Varenka catches it too,”", "그리고 네 바렌카까지 덩달아 욕을 먹지.\""], ["he added.", "그가 덧붙였다."], ["“Oh, these invalid ladies!”", "\"오, 이놈의 병약한 부인네들이란!\""]], [["“Oh, no, papa!”", "\"오, 아니에요, 아빠!\""], ["Kitty objected warmly.", "키티가 발끈하며 반박했다."], ["“Varenka worships her.", "\"바렌카는 그분을 우러러 받든다고요."], ["And then she does so much good!", "게다가 그분은 그렇게나 많은 선을 베푸시는걸요!"], ["Ask anyone!", "아무한테나 물어보세요!"], ["Everyone knows her and Aline Stahl.”", "다들 그분을, 알린 슈탈을 안다고요.\""]], [["“Perhaps so,”", "\"그럴지도 모르지.\""], ["said the prince, squeezing her hand with his elbow; “but it’s better when one does good so that you may ask everyone and no one knows.”", "공작이 팔꿈치로 그녀의 손을 꼭 누르며 말했다. \"하지만 선이라는 건 아무한테나 물어도 아무도 모르게끔 행할 때가 더 나은 법이란다.\""]], [["Kitty did not answer, not because she had nothing to say, but because she did not care to reveal her secret thoughts even to her father.", "키티는 대답하지 않았는데, 할 말이 없어서가 아니라 자기 속마음을 아버지에게조차 드러내고 싶지 않았기 때문이었다."], ["But, strange to say, although she had so made up her mind not to be influenced by her father’s views, not to let him into her inmost sanctuary, she felt that the heavenly image of Madame Stahl, which she had carried for a whole month in her heart, had vanished, never to return, just as the fantastic figure made up of some clothes thrown down at random vanishes when one sees that it is only some garment lying there.", "그러나 이상하게도, 아버지의 견해에 휘둘리지 않겠다고, 자기 마음 가장 깊은 성소에 아버지를 들이지 않겠다고 그토록 단단히 마음먹었건만, 그녀는 한 달 내내 가슴속에 품어 왔던 슈탈 부인의 천상의 이미지가, 아무렇게나 벗어 던진 옷가지로 이루어진 환상 속의 형체가 그저 옷자락이 거기 놓여 있을 뿐임을 알아채는 순간 사라져 버리듯, 영영 돌아오지 않을 길로 사라져 버렸음을 느꼈다."], ["All that was left was a woman with short legs, who lay down because she had a bad figure, and worried patient Varenka for not arranging her rug to her liking.", "남은 것이라곤 다리가 짧은 한 여자, 몸매가 형편없어서 자리에 누웠고, 무릎덮개를 자기 마음에 들게 놓아 주지 않았다고 참을성 많은 바렌카를 들볶는 여자뿐이었다."], ["And by no effort of the imagination could Kitty bring back the former Madame Stahl.", "그리고 키티는 아무리 상상력을 짜내어도 예전의 슈탈 부인을 되살려 낼 수가 없었다."]], [["# Chapter 35", "# Chapter 35"]], [["The prince communicated his good humor to his own family and his friends, and even to the German landlord in whose rooms the Shtcherbatskys were staying.", "공작은 자신의 유쾌한 기분을 식구들과 친구들에게, 심지어 셰르바츠키 일가가 머무는 방의 주인인 독일인 집주인에게까지 옮겨 주었다."]], [["On coming back with Kitty from the springs, the prince, who had asked the colonel, and Marya Yevgenyevna, and Varenka all to come and have coffee with them, gave orders for a table and chairs to be taken into the garden under the chestnut tree, and lunch to be laid there.", "키티와 함께 온천에서 돌아온 공작은 대령과 마리야 예브게니예브나, 그리고 바렌카를 모두 불러 함께 커피를 들자고 청해 놓고는, 정원의 밤나무 아래로 탁자와 의자를 내가고 거기에 점심을 차리라고 지시했다."], ["The landlord and the servants, too, grew brisker under the influence of his good spirits.", "집주인과 하인들 역시 그의 유쾌한 기분에 영향을 받아 한결 활기를 띠었다."], ["They knew his open-handedness; and half an hour later the invalid doctor from Hamburg, who lived on the top floor, looked enviously out of the window at the merry party of healthy Russians assembled under the chestnut tree.", "그들은 그의 씀씀이가 후하다는 것을 알고 있었다. 그리고 반 시간 뒤, 맨 위층에 사는 함부르크 출신의 병약한 의사는 밤나무 아래 모여든 건강한 러시아인들의 흥겨운 무리를 창밖으로 부럽게 내다보았다."], ["In the trembling circles of shadow cast by the leaves, at a table, covered with a white cloth, and set with coffeepot, bread-and-butter, cheese, and cold game, sat the princess in a high cap with lilac ribbons, distributing cups and bread-and-butter.", "나뭇잎이 드리우는, 떨리는 그늘의 동그라미 속에서, 하얀 천을 덮고 커피포트며 버터 바른 빵이며 치즈며 차가운 들짐승 고기를 차려 놓은 탁자에 라일락빛 리본을 단 높다란 모자를 쓴 공작 부인이 앉아 잔과 버터 바른 빵을 나누어 주고 있었다."], ["At the other end sat the prince, eating heartily, and talking loudly and merrily.", "맞은편 끝에는 공작이 앉아 맛있게 먹으며 큰 소리로 유쾌하게 떠들었다."], ["The prince had spread out near him his purchases, carved boxes, and knick-knacks, paper-knives of all sorts, of which he bought a heap at every watering-place, and bestowed them upon everyone, including Lieschen, the servant girl, and the landlord, with whom he jested in his comically bad German, assuring him that it was not the water had cured Kitty, but his splendid cookery, especially his plum soup.", "공작은 자기 곁에 사들인 물건들을 펼쳐 놓았는데, 조각이 새겨진 상자들이며 자질구레한 장식품들, 온갖 종류의 편지 칼들이었다. 그는 온천지에 갈 때마다 그것들을 한 무더기씩 사들여서는 모두에게, 하녀인 리셴에게도 집주인에게도 나누어 주었으며, 우스꽝스러울 만큼 서툰 독일어로 집주인에게 농담을 건네며, 키티를 낫게 한 것은 물이 아니라 그의 훌륭한 요리, 특히 자두 수프라고 단언했다."], ["The princess laughed at her husband for his Russian ways, but she was more lively and good-humored than she had been all the while she had been at the waters.", "공작 부인은 남편의 러시아식 행동을 보며 웃었지만, 온천에 머무는 내내 보였던 것보다 훨씬 활기차고 기분이 좋았다."], ["The colonel smiled, as he always did, at the prince’s jokes, but as far as regards Europe, of which he believed himself to be making a careful study, he took the princess’s side.", "대령은 늘 그렇듯 공작의 농담에 미소를 지었으나, 자기가 면밀히 연구하고 있다고 자부하는 유럽에 관해서만큼은 공작 부인 편을 들었다."], ["The simple-hearted Marya Yevgenyevna simply roared with laughter at everything absurd the prince said, and his jokes made Varenka helpless with feeble but infectious laughter, which was something Kitty had never seen before.", "순박한 마리야 예브게니예브나는 공작이 내뱉는 우스꽝스러운 말마다 그저 폭소를 터뜨렸고, 그의 농담은 바렌카를 가냘프지만 사람을 전염시키는 웃음으로 어쩔 줄 모르게 만들었는데, 그것은 키티가 한 번도 본 적 없는 모습이었다."]], [["Kitty was glad of all this, but she could not be light-hearted.", "키티는 이 모든 것이 기뻤지만 마음이 가벼워지지는 않았다."], ["She could not solve the problem her father had unconsciously set her by his good-humored view of her friends, and of the life that had so attracted her.", "그녀는 아버지가 자신의 친구들과 그토록 자신을 끌어당겼던 삶을 유쾌하게 바라봄으로써 무심코 자기에게 던져 놓은 문제를 풀 수가 없었다."], ["To this doubt there was joined the change in her relations with the Petrovs, which had been so conspicuously and unpleasantly marked that morning.", "이 의혹에 더해, 그날 아침 그토록 두드러지고 불쾌하게 드러났던 페트로프 일가와의 관계 변화가 겹쳤다."], ["Everyone was good-humored, but Kitty could not feel good-humored, and this increased her distress.", "모두가 유쾌했지만 키티는 유쾌해질 수 없었고, 이것이 그녀의 괴로움을 더욱 키웠다."], ["She felt a feeling such as she had known in childhood, when she had been shut in her room as a punishment, and had heard her sisters’ merry laughter outside.", "그녀는 어린 시절, 벌을 받느라 방에 갇혀 밖에서 들려오는 언니들의 흥겨운 웃음소리를 들을 때 느꼈던 것과 같은 감정을 느꼈다."]], [["“Well, but what did you buy this mass of things for?”", "\"그런데 이 많은 물건들은 다 뭐 하러 샀어요?\""], ["said the princess, smiling, and handing her husband a cup of coffee.", "공작 부인이 미소 지으며 남편에게 커피를 한 잔 건네며 말했다."]], [["“One goes for a walk, one looks in a shop, and they ask you to buy.", "“산책을 나가서 가게를 들여다보면 사라고 하지요."], ["‘Erlaucht, Durchlaucht?’ Directly they say ‘Durchlaucht,’ I can’t hold out.", "‘엘라우흐트, 두르흘라우흐트?’ 그들이 ‘두르흘라우흐트’라고 하면 나는 버틸 수가 없어요."], ["I lose ten thalers.”", "10탈러를 잃고 말지요.”"]], [["“It’s simply from boredom,”", "\"그건 그냥 심심해서 그런 거예요.\""], ["said the princess.", "공작 부인이 말했다."]], [["“Of course it is.", "\"물론 그렇지."], ["Such boredom, my dear, that one doesn’t know what to do with oneself.”", "어찌나 심심한지, 여보, 도무지 제 몸을 어쩔 줄 모를 지경이니.\""]], [["“How can you be bored, prince?", "\"어떻게 심심하실 수가 있어요, 공작님?"], ["There’s so much that’s interesting now in Germany,”", "요즘 독일엔 흥미로운 게 정말 많은데요.\""], ["said Marya Yevgenyevna.", "마리야 예브게니예브나가 말했다."]], [["“But I know everything that’s interesting: the plum soup I know, and the pea sausages I know.", "\"하지만 난 흥미로운 건 죄다 알고 있소. 자두 수프도 알고, 완두콩 소시지도 알지."], ["I know everything.”", "다 안다고.\""]], [["“No, you may say what you like, prince, there’s the interest of their institutions,”", "\"아니에요, 뭐라고 하셔도, 공작님, 이 나라의 제도들에는 흥미로운 점이 있습니다.\""], ["said the colonel.", "대령이 말했다."]], [["“But what is there interesting about it?", "\"하지만 그게 뭐가 흥미롭다는 거요?"], ["They’re all as pleased as brass halfpence.", "다들 한 푼짜리 동전처럼 흡족해하고 있잖소."], ["They’ve conquered everybody, and why am I to be pleased at that?", "모두를 정복해 놓았으니 말이야, 한데 내가 왜 그걸 기뻐해야 하느냔 말이오? 난 아무도 정복하지 않았소."], ["I haven’t conquered anyone; and I’m obliged to take off my own boots, yes, and put them away too; in the morning, get up and dress at once, and go to the dining-room to drink bad tea!", "게다가 내 손으로 직접 장화를 벗어야 하고, 그래, 그걸 또 직접 치워야 하지. 아침엔 일어나자마자 곧장 옷을 입고 식당으로 나가서 맛없는 차를 마셔야 하고!"], ["How different it is at home!", "집에서는 얼마나 다른가!"], ["You get up in no haste, you get cross, grumble a little, and come round again.", "서두를 것 없이 일어나서, 좀 짜증도 내고, 투덜대다가, 다시 기분이 풀리지."], ["You’ve time to think things over, and no hurry.”", "무엇이든 곰곰이 생각할 시간도 있고, 서두를 것도 없고 말이야.\""]], [["“But time’s money, you forget that,”", "\"하지만 시간은 돈입니다, 그걸 잊으셨군요.\""], ["said the colonel.", "대령이 말했다."]], [["“Time, indeed, that depends!", "\"시간이라, 흥, 그건 경우에 따라 다르지!"], ["Why, there’s time one would give a month of for sixpence, and time you wouldn’t give half an hour of for any money.", "어떤 시간은 한 달치를 육 펜스에 내줘도 좋을 때가 있고, 또 어떤 시간은 반 시간조차 어떤 돈을 줘도 못 내줄 때가 있는 법이오."], ["Isn’t that so, Katinka?", "안 그러냐, 카친카?"], ["What is it?", "왜 그래?"], ["why are you so depressed?”", "왜 그렇게 풀이 죽어 있는 거냐?\""]], [["“I’m not depressed.”", "\"풀이 죽은 거 아니에요.\""]], [["“Where are you off to?", "\"어딜 가려고?"], ["Stay a little longer,”", "좀 더 있다 가지.\""], ["he said to Varenka.", "그가 바렌카에게 말했다."]], [["“I must be going home,”", "\"이제 집에 가 봐야 해요.\""], ["said Varenka, getting up, and again she went off into a giggle.", "바렌카가 일어서며 말하고는 또다시 까르르 웃음을 터뜨렸다."], ["When she had recovered, she said good-bye, and went into the house to get her hat.", "웃음을 가라앉힌 그녀는 작별 인사를 하고 모자를 가지러 집 안으로 들어갔다."]], [["Kitty followed her.", "키티가 그녀를 따라갔다."], ["Even Varenka struck her as different.", "바렌카마저 달리 보였다."], ["She was not worse, but different from what she had fancied her before.", "더 나빠진 것은 아니었지만, 전에 그녀가 그려 보았던 것과는 달랐다."]], [["“Oh, dear!", "\"어머나!"], ["it’s a long while since I’ve laughed so much!”", "이렇게 실컷 웃어 본 게 정말 오랜만이에요!\""], ["said Varenka, gathering up her parasol and her bag.", "바렌카가 양산과 가방을 챙기며 말했다."], ["“How nice he is, your father!”", "\"정말 좋은 분이시네요, 당신 아버님은!\""]], [["Kitty did not speak.", "키티는 말이 없었다."]], [["“When shall I see you again?”", "\"우리 언제 또 만나죠?\""], ["asked Varenka.", "바렌카가 물었다."]], [["“Mamma meant to go and see the Petrovs.", "\"엄마가 페트로프 댁에 가 보려고 하셨어요."], ["Won’t you be there?”", "거기 안 오세요?\""], ["said Kitty, to try Varenka.", "키티가 바렌카의 마음을 떠보려고 말했다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"가요.\""], ["answered Varenka.", "바렌카가 대답했다."], ["“They’re getting ready to go away, so I promised to help them pack.”", "\"그 댁이 떠날 채비를 하고 있어서, 짐 싸는 걸 돕겠다고 약속했거든요.\""]], [["“Well, I’ll come too, then.”", "\"그럼 저도 갈게요.\""]], [["“No, why should you?”", "\"아니, 왜요?\""]], [["“Why not?", "\"왜 안 돼요?"], ["why not?", "왜요?"], ["why not?”", "왜요?\""], ["said Kitty, opening her eyes wide, and clutching at Varenka’s parasol, so as not to let her go.", "키티가 두 눈을 동그랗게 뜨고, 바렌카를 놓치지 않으려는 듯 그녀의 양산을 움켜쥐며 말했다."], ["“No, wait a minute; why not?”", "\"아니, 잠깐만요. 왜 안 되죠?\""]], [["“Oh, nothing; your father has come, and besides, they will feel awkward at your helping.”", "\"아, 아무것도 아니에요. 당신 아버님도 오셨고, 게다가 당신이 거들면 그분들이 거북해하실 거예요.\""]], [["“No, tell me why you don’t want me to be often at the Petrovs’.", "\"아니, 왜 제가 페트로프 댁에 자주 가는 걸 원치 않는지 말해 줘요."], ["You don’t want me to—why not?”", "당신은 제가 그러는 걸 원치 않잖아요. 왜요?\""]], [["“I didn’t say that,”", "\"그런 말 한 적 없어요.\""], ["said Varenka quietly.", "바렌카가 조용히 말했다."]], [["“No, please tell me!”", "\"아니, 제발 말해 줘요!\""]], [["“Tell you everything?”", "\"전부 다 말하라고요?\""], ["asked Varenka.", "바렌카가 물었다."]], [["“Everything, everything!”", "\"전부 다요, 전부!\""], ["Kitty assented.", "키티가 동의했다."]], [["“Well, there’s really nothing of any consequence; only that Mihail Alexeyevitch”", "\"음, 사실 대수로운 건 전혀 없어요. 그저 미하일 알렉세예비치가\""], ["(that was the artist’s name) “had meant to leave earlier, and now he doesn’t want to go away,”", "(화가의 이름이었다) \"원래는 더 일찍 떠나려고 했는데, 이제는 떠나고 싶어 하지 않는다는 것뿐이에요.\""], ["said Varenka, smiling.", "바렌카가 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["“Well, well!”", "\"그래서요, 그래서요!\""], ["Kitty urged impatiently, looking darkly at Varenka.", "키티가 바렌카를 어둡게 쳐다보며 안달이 나서 재촉했다."]], [["“Well, and for some reason Anna Pavlovna told him that he didn’t want to go because you are here.", "\"음, 그런데 무슨 이유에서인지 안나 파블로브나가 그분에게, 당신이 여기 있어서 떠나고 싶어 하지 않는 거라고 말했어요."], ["Of course, that was nonsense; but there was a dispute over it—over you.", "물론 터무니없는 소리였죠. 하지만 그 일로 다툼이 있었어요, 당신을 두고 말이에요."], ["You know how irritable these sick people are.”", "그런 환자들이 얼마나 신경이 예민한지 당신도 알잖아요.\""]], [["Kitty, scowling more than ever, kept silent, and Varenka went on speaking alone, trying to soften or soothe her, and seeing a storm coming—she did not know whether of tears or of words.", "키티는 더욱 얼굴을 찌푸린 채 잠자코 있었고, 바렌카는 혼자서 계속 말을 이어 가며, 그녀를 누그러뜨리거나 달래려 애썼다. 폭풍이 다가오는 것이 느껴졌으나, 그것이 눈물의 폭풍일지 말의 폭풍일지는 알 수 없었다."]], [["“So you’d better not go....", "\"그러니 가지 않는 편이 좋겠어요……"], ["You understand; you won’t be offended?...”", "이해하시죠? 기분 상하지 않으시겠죠?……\""]], [["“And it serves me right!", "\"그래요, 다 제가 잘못한 거예요!"], ["And it serves me right!”", "다 제 탓이라고요!\""], ["Kitty cried quickly, snatching the parasol out of Varenka’s hand, and looking past her friend’s face.", "키티가 바렌카의 손에서 양산을 낚아채며, 친구의 얼굴 너머를 바라보며 빠르게 외쳤다."]], [["Varenka felt inclined to smile, looking at her childish fury, but she was afraid of wounding her.", "바렌카는 그녀의 어린아이 같은 분노를 보며 미소가 나오려 했지만, 그녀에게 상처를 줄까 봐 두려웠다."]], [["“How does it serve you right?", "\"어째서 당신 잘못이라는 거예요?"], ["I don’t understand,”", "이해가 안 돼요.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“It serves me right, because it was all sham; because it was all done on purpose, and not from the heart.", "\"제 탓이에요, 모든 게 가짜였으니까요. 모든 게 일부러 한 거였지, 진심에서 우러난 게 아니었으니까요."], ["What business had I to interfere with outsiders?", "제가 무슨 자격으로 남의 일에 끼어들었을까요?"], ["And so it’s come about that I’m a cause of quarrel, and that I’ve done what nobody asked me to do.", "그러다 결국 제가 다툼의 원인이 되고, 아무도 부탁하지 않은 일을 저질러 버렸어요."], ["Because it was all a sham!", "죄다 가짜였기 때문이에요!"], ["a sham!", "가짜!"], ["a sham!...”", "가짜!……\""]], [["“A sham! with what object?”", "\"가짜라니, 무슨 목적으로요?\""], ["said Varenka gently.", "바렌카가 부드럽게 말했다."]], [["“Oh, it’s so idiotic!", "\"아, 너무 어리석어요!"], ["so hateful!", "너무 혐오스러워요!"], ["There was no need whatever for me....", "제가 나설 필요는 조금도 없었는데……"], ["Nothing but sham!”", "죄다 가짜뿐이었어요!\""], ["she said, opening and shutting the parasol.", "그녀가 양산을 폈다 접었다 하며 말했다."]], [["“But with what object?”", "\"하지만 무슨 목적으로요?\""]], [["“To seem better to people, to myself, to God; to deceive everyone.", "\"남들 눈에, 제 눈에, 하느님 눈에 더 나아 보이려고요. 모두를 속이려고요."], ["No!", "아니에요!"], ["now I won’t descend to that.", "이제 그런 짓까지 하진 않겠어요."], ["I’ll be bad; but anyway not a liar, a cheat.”", "차라리 나쁜 사람이 되겠어요. 어쨌든 거짓말쟁이, 사기꾼은 안 될 거예요.\""]], [["“But who is a cheat?”", "\"그런데 누가 사기꾼이라는 거예요?\""], ["said Varenka reproachfully.", "바렌카가 나무라듯 말했다."], ["“You speak as if....”", "\"마치……\""]], [["But Kitty was in one of her gusts of fury, and she would not let her finish.", "하지만 키티는 격분에 휩싸여 그녀가 말을 맺도록 내버려 두지 않았다."]], [["“I don’t talk about you, not about you at all.", "\"당신 얘기가 아니에요, 당신 얘기는 전혀 아니에요."], ["You’re perfection.", "당신은 완벽한 사람이죠."], ["Yes, yes, I know you’re all perfection; but what am I to do if I’m bad?", "그래요, 그래, 당신이 더없이 완벽하다는 걸 알아요. 하지만 제가 나쁜 사람이라면 어쩌란 말이에요?"], ["This would never have been if I weren’t bad.", "제가 나쁘지 않았다면 이런 일은 절대 없었을 거예요."], ["So let me be what I am.", "그러니 제가 생긴 그대로 살게 내버려 둬요."], ["I won’t be a sham.", "저는 가짜로 살지 않을래요."], ["What have I to do with Anna Pavlovna?", "안나 파블로브나가 저랑 무슨 상관이에요?"], ["Let them go their way, and me go mine.", "그 사람들은 그들 길을 가고, 저는 제 길을 가게 해요."], ["I can’t be different....", "저는 달라질 수가 없어요……"], ["And yet it’s not that, it’s not that.”", "그런데 그게 아니에요, 그게 아니라고요.\""]], [["“What is not that?”", "\"그게 아니라니 뭐가요?\""], ["asked Varenka in bewilderment.", "바렌카가 어리둥절해하며 물었다."]], [["“Everything.", "\"전부 다요."], ["I can’t act except from the heart, and you act from principle.", "저는 진심에서 우러나는 게 아니면 행동할 수가 없는데, 당신은 원칙에 따라 행동하잖아요."], ["I liked you simply, but you most likely only wanted to save me, to improve me.”", "저는 그냥 당신이 좋아서 그랬는데, 당신은 아마 그저 저를 구원하고, 저를 더 낫게 만들고 싶었을 뿐이겠죠.\""]], [["“You are unjust,”", "\"그건 부당해요.\""], ["said Varenka.", "바렌카가 말했다."]], [["“But I’m not speaking of other people, I’m speaking of myself.”", "\"하지만 저는 다른 사람들 얘기를 하는 게 아니에요, 제 자신 얘기를 하는 거예요.\""]], [["“Kitty,”", "\"키티야,\""], ["they heard her mother’s voice, “come here, show papa your necklace.”", "어머니의 목소리가 들렸다. \"이리 와서 아빠한테 네 목걸이 좀 보여 드리렴.\""]], [["Kitty, with a haughty air, without making peace with her friend, took the necklace in a little box from the table and went to her mother.", "키티는 도도한 태도로, 친구와 화해도 하지 않은 채 탁자에서 작은 상자에 든 목걸이를 집어 들고 어머니에게로 갔다."]], [["“What’s the matter?", "\"무슨 일이냐?"], ["Why are you so red?”", "왜 그렇게 얼굴이 빨갛지?\""], ["her mother and father said to her with one voice.", "어머니와 아버지가 한목소리로 그녀에게 물었다."]], [["“Nothing,” she answered.", "\"아무것도 아니에요.\""], ["“I’ll be back directly,”", "그녀가 대답했다. \"금방 돌아올게요.\""], ["and she ran back.", "그러고는 다시 뛰어갔다."]], [["“She’s still here,”", "'아직 거기 있어.'"], ["she thought.", "그녀는 생각했다."], ["“What am I to say to her?", "'뭐라고 말해야 하지? 아, 어쩌면 좋아!"], ["Oh, dear! what have I done, what have I said?", "내가 무슨 짓을 한 거지, 무슨 말을 한 거야?"], ["Why was I rude to her?", "왜 그녀에게 무례하게 굴었을까?"], ["What am I to do?", "어떻게 하지?"], ["What am I to say to her?”", "그녀에게 뭐라고 말해야 하지?'"], ["thought Kitty, and she stopped in the doorway.", "키티는 그렇게 생각하며 문간에 멈춰 섰다."]], [["Varenka in her hat and with the parasol in her hands was sitting at the table examining the spring which Kitty had broken.", "모자를 쓰고 손에 양산을 든 바렌카는 탁자에 앉아 키티가 부러뜨린 양산 살을 살펴보고 있었다."], ["She lifted her head.", "그녀가 고개를 들었다."]], [["“Varenka, forgive me, do forgive me,”", "\"바렌카, 용서해 줘요, 제발 용서해 줘요.\""], ["whispered Kitty, going up to her.", "키티가 그녀에게 다가가며 속삭였다."], ["“I don’t remember what I said.", "\"제가 무슨 말을 했는지 기억도 안 나요."], ["I....”", "저는……\""]], [["“I really didn’t mean to hurt you,”", "\"저도 정말 당신을 아프게 할 생각은 없었어요.\""], ["said Varenka, smiling.", "바렌카가 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["Peace was made.", "화해가 이루어졌다."], ["But with her father’s coming all the world in which she had been living was transformed for Kitty.", "그러나 아버지가 오면서 키티가 살아가던 세계 전체가 그녀에게는 완전히 달라져 버렸다."], ["She did not give up everything she had learned, but she became aware that she had deceived herself in supposing she could be what she wanted to be.", "그녀는 자기가 배운 것을 모두 버리지는 않았지만, 자기가 되고 싶은 사람이 될 수 있으리라 여겼던 것이 스스로를 속인 것이었음을 깨닫게 되었다."], ["Her eyes were, it seemed, opened; she felt all the difficulty of maintaining herself without hypocrisy and self-conceit on the pinnacle to which she had wished to mount.", "그녀의 눈이, 말하자면, 떠진 셈이었다. 그녀는 자기가 오르고자 했던 그 정상에서 위선과 자만 없이 스스로를 지탱한다는 것이 얼마나 어려운 일인지를 절실히 느꼈다."], ["Moreover, she became aware of all the dreariness of the world of sorrow, of sick and dying people, in which she had been living.", "게다가 그녀는 자기가 살아온, 슬픔과 병자와 죽어 가는 사람들로 이루어진 세계의 온갖 적막함을 깨닫게 되었다."], ["The efforts she had made to like it seemed to her intolerable, and she felt a longing to get back quickly into the fresh air, to Russia, to Ergushovo, where, as she knew from letters, her sister Dolly had already gone with her children.", "그것을 좋아하려고 기울였던 노력들이 견딜 수 없게 여겨졌고, 그녀는 어서 빨리 신선한 공기 속으로, 러시아로, 편지를 통해 알고 있듯이 언니 돌리가 이미 아이들과 함께 가 있는 예르구쇼보로 돌아가고 싶은 갈망을 느꼈다."]], [["But her affection for Varenka did not wane.", "그러나 바렌카에 대한 그녀의 애정은 식지 않았다."], ["As she said good-bye, Kitty begged her to come to them in Russia.", "작별 인사를 하면서 키티는 바렌카에게 러시아에 있는 자기들에게로 와 달라고 청했다."]], [["“I’ll come when you get married,”", "\"당신이 결혼하면 갈게요.\""], ["said Varenka.", "바렌카가 말했다."]], [["“I shall never marry.”", "\"저는 절대 결혼 안 할 거예요.\""]], [["“Well, then, I shall never come.”", "\"그럼, 저도 절대 안 가요.\""]], [["“Well, then, I shall be married simply for that.", "\"그럼, 저는 그것만을 위해서라도 결혼할래요."], ["Mind now, remember your promise,”", "자, 이제 약속 잊지 말아요.\""], ["said Kitty.", "키티가 말했다."]], [["The doctor’s prediction was fulfilled.", "의사의 예측은 들어맞았다."], ["Kitty returned home to Russia cured.", "키티는 병이 나아 러시아의 집으로 돌아왔다."], ["She was not so gay and thoughtless as before, but she was serene.", "그녀는 예전처럼 명랑하고 철없지는 않았지만, 평온했다."], ["Her Moscow troubles had become a memory to her.", "모스크바에서의 괴로움은 그녀에게 하나의 추억이 되어 있었다."]], [["# PART THREE Chapter 1", "# PART THREE Chapter 1"]], [["Sergey Ivanovitch Koznishev wanted a rest from mental work, and instead of going abroad as he usually did, he came towards the end of May to stay in the country with his brother.", "세르게이 이바노비치 코즈니셰프는 정신노동에서 벗어나 쉬고 싶었고, 평소처럼 외국으로 떠나는 대신 5월 말경에 동생이 사는 시골로 와서 머물게 되었다."], ["In his judgment the best sort of life was a country life.", "그의 판단으로는 시골 생활이야말로 가장 훌륭한 삶의 방식이었다."], ["He had come now to enjoy such a life at his brother’s.", "이제 그는 동생의 집에서 그런 삶을 누리려고 온 것이었다."], ["Konstantin Levin was very glad to have him, especially as he did not expect his brother Nikolay that summer.", "콘스탄틴 레빈은 형이 와서 무척 기뻤는데, 특히 그해 여름에는 형 니콜라이가 오지 않으리라고 예상했기 때문이다."], ["But in spite of his affection and respect for Sergey Ivanovitch, Konstantin Levin was uncomfortable with his brother in the country.", "그러나 세르게이 이바노비치에 대한 애정과 존경에도 불구하고, 콘스탄틴 레빈은 시골에서 형과 함께 지내는 것이 불편했다."], ["It made him uncomfortable, and it positively annoyed him to see his brother’s attitude to the country.", "시골을 대하는 형의 태도를 보고 있으면 마음이 편치 않았고, 솔직히 짜증이 났다."], ["To Konstantin Levin the country was the background of life, that is of pleasures, endeavors, labor.", "콘스탄틴 레빈에게 시골은 삶의 바탕, 그러니까 즐거움과 노력과 노동의 바탕이었다."], ["To Sergey Ivanovitch the country meant on one hand rest from work, on the other a valuable antidote to the corrupt influences of town, which he took with satisfaction and a sense of its utility.", "세르게이 이바노비치에게 시골은 한편으로는 일에서 벗어난 휴식이었고, 다른 한편으로는 도시의 타락한 영향을 막아 주는 귀중한 해독제였으며, 그는 그것을 만족감과 유익함을 의식하며 받아들였다."], ["To Konstantin Levin the country was good first because it afforded a field for labor, of the usefulness of which there could be no doubt.", "콘스탄틴 레빈에게 시골이 좋은 첫째 이유는 그것이 그 유익함을 의심할 여지 없는 노동의 터전을 마련해 주기 때문이었다."], ["To Sergey Ivanovitch the country was particularly good, because there it was possible and fitting to do nothing.", "세르게이 이바노비치에게 시골이 특히 좋은 이유는 그곳에서는 아무것도 하지 않는 것이 가능하고 또 어울리기 때문이었다."], ["Moreover, Sergey Ivanovitch’s attitude to the peasants rather piqued Konstantin.", "게다가 농민을 대하는 세르게이 이바노비치의 태도는 콘스탄틴의 마음을 적잖이 거슬리게 했다."], ["Sergey Ivanovitch used to say that he knew and liked the peasantry, and he often talked to the peasants, which he knew how to do without affectation or condescension, and from every such conversation he would deduce general conclusions in favor of the peasantry and in confirmation of his knowing them.", "세르게이 이바노비치는 자기가 농민을 잘 알고 또 좋아한다고 말하곤 했고, 자주 농민들과 이야기를 나누었는데, 그것을 꾸밈이나 거들먹거림 없이 해내는 재주가 있었다. 그리고 그런 대화를 나눌 때마다 그는 농민에게 유리한, 또한 자기가 그들을 잘 안다는 사실을 입증해 주는 일반적인 결론을 끌어내곤 했다."], ["Konstantin Levin did not like such an attitude to the peasants.", "콘스탄틴 레빈은 농민을 대하는 그런 태도가 마음에 들지 않았다."], ["To Konstantin the peasant was simply the chief partner in their common labor, and in spite of all the respect and the love, almost like that of kinship, he had for the peasant—sucked in probably, as he said himself, with the milk of his peasant nurse—still as a fellow-worker with him, while sometimes enthusiastic over the vigor, gentleness, and justice of these men, he was very often, when their common labors called for other qualities, exasperated with the peasant for his carelessness, lack of method, drunkenness, and lying.", "콘스탄틴에게 농민이란 그저 함께하는 노동의 가장 중요한 동반자였고, 농민에 대해 거의 혈연에 가까운 온갖 존경과 사랑을 품고 있었음에도—아마도 그 자신이 말하듯, 유모인 농민 여인의 젖과 함께 빨아들인 것이리라—그래도 자기와 함께 일하는 동료로서, 때로는 그들의 활력과 온순함과 정의로움에 감탄하면서도, 함께하는 노동이 다른 자질을 요구할 때면 그들의 부주의함과 두서없음과 술버릇과 거짓말에 매우 자주 화가 치밀었다."], ["If he had been asked whether he liked or didn’t like the peasants, Konstantin Levin would have been absolutely at a loss what to reply.", "만약 누군가 농민을 좋아하느냐 싫어하느냐고 그에게 물었다면, 콘스탄틴 레빈은 무어라 대답해야 할지 도무지 갈피를 잡지 못했을 것이다."], ["He liked and did not like the peasants, just as he liked and did not like men in general. Of course, being a good-hearted man, he liked men rather than he disliked them, and so too with the peasants.", "그는 농민을 좋아하기도 하고 싫어하기도 했으니, 그것은 마치 사람을 일반적으로 좋아하기도 하고 싫어하기도 하는 것과 같았다."], ["But like or dislike “the people” as something apart he could not, not only because he lived with “the people,”", "물론 마음씨 고운 사람인 그는 사람을 싫어하기보다는 좋아하는 편이었고, 농민에 대해서도 마찬가지였다."], ["and all his interests were bound up with theirs, but also because he regarded himself as a part of “the people,” did not see any special qualities or failings distinguishing himself and “the people,”", "그러나 \"민중\"을 무언가 따로 떨어진 것으로 여겨 좋아하거나 싫어하는 것은 그로서는 할 수 없는 일이었으니, 그것은 단지 그가 \"민중\"과 더불어 살았고 그의 모든 관심사가 그들의 것과 얽혀 있었기 때문만이 아니라, 그 자신이 \"민중\"의 일부라고 여겨 자기와 \"민중\"을 구별 짓는 어떤 특별한 자질이나 결점도 보지 못했고, 자기를 그들과 견줄 수도 없었기 때문이다."], ["and could not contrast himself with them. Moreover, although he had lived so long in the closest relations with the peasants, as farmer and arbitrator, and what was more, as adviser (the peasants trusted him, and for thirty miles round they would come to ask his advice), he had no definite views of “the people,”", "더구나 그는 농부로서, 또 중재인으로서, 더 나아가 조언자로서(농민들은 그를 신뢰했고, 삼십 베르스타 사방에서 그에게 조언을 구하러 오곤 했다) 그토록 오랫동안 농민과 더없이 긴밀한 관계를 맺으며 살아왔음에도, \"민중\"에 대한 명확한 견해가 없었으며, 자기가 \"민중\"을 아느냐는 물음에도 그들을 좋아하느냐는 물음 못지않게 답하기 곤란했을 것이다."], ["and would have been as much at a loss to answer the question whether he knew “the people” as the question whether he liked them.", "그에게 농민을 안다고 말하는 것은 사람을 안다고 말하는 것과 매한가지였을 것이다."], ["For him to say he knew the peasantry would have been the same as to say he knew men. He was continually watching and getting to know people of all sorts, and among them peasants, whom he regarded as good and interesting people, and he was continually observing new points in them, altering his former views of them and forming new ones.", "그는 온갖 부류의 사람들을, 그 가운데 농민들을 끊임없이 관찰하며 알아 갔는데, 그들을 선량하고 흥미로운 사람들로 여겼으며, 그들에게서 끊임없이 새로운 점을 발견하고 그들에 대한 이전의 견해를 바꾸어 가며 새로운 견해를 세웠다."], ["With Sergey Ivanovitch it was quite the contrary.", "세르게이 이바노비치는 그와 정반대였다."], ["Just as he liked and praised a country life in comparison with the life he did not like, so too he liked the peasantry in contradistinction to the class of men he did not like, and so too he knew the peasantry as something distinct from and opposed to men generally.", "그가 싫어하는 삶에 견주어 시골 생활을 좋아하고 칭송했듯이, 그는 자기가 싫어하는 계층의 사람들과 대비하여 농민을 좋아했고, 또 그렇게 농민을 사람 일반과 구별되고 대립되는 무언가로서 알고 있었다."], ["In his methodical brain there were distinctly formulated certain aspects of peasant life, deduced partly from that life itself, but chiefly from contrast with other modes of life.", "그의 체계적인 머릿속에는 농민 생활의 어떤 측면들이 명확히 정식화되어 있었는데, 그것은 일부는 그 생활 자체에서, 그러나 주로는 다른 생활 양식과의 대비에서 끌어낸 것이었다."], ["He never changed his opinion of the peasantry and his sympathetic attitude towards them.", "그는 농민에 대한 자기 견해도, 그들을 향한 공감적인 태도도 결코 바꾸지 않았다."]], [["In the discussions that arose between the brothers on their views of the peasantry, Sergey Ivanovitch always got the better of his brother, precisely because Sergey Ivanovitch had definite ideas about the peasant—his character, his qualities, and his tastes.", "농민에 대한 견해를 두고 형제 사이에 벌어지는 논쟁에서 세르게이 이바노비치는 늘 동생을 이겼는데, 그것은 바로 세르게이 이바노비치가 농민에 대해—그 성격이며 자질이며 취향에 대해 명확한 생각을 가지고 있었기 때문이다."], ["Konstantin Levin had no definite and unalterable idea on the subject, and so in their arguments Konstantin was readily convicted of contradicting himself.", "콘스탄틴 레빈은 그 문제에 대해 명확하고 변치 않는 생각이 없었으므로, 논쟁에서 콘스탄틴은 쉽사리 자기모순을 범한 자로 몰리고 말았다."]], [["In Sergey Ivanovitch’s eyes his younger brother was a capital fellow, with his heart in the right place (as he expressed it in French), but with a mind which, though fairly quick, was too much influenced by the impressions of the moment, and consequently filled with contradictions.", "세르게이 이바노비치가 보기에 동생은 마음씨가 올바른(그가 프랑스어로 표현하듯) 훌륭한 녀석이었지만, 그 머리는 제법 빠르긴 해도 순간의 인상에 너무 휘둘려, 그 결과 모순으로 가득 차 있었다."], ["With all the condescension of an elder brother he sometimes explained to him the true import of things, but he derived little satisfaction from arguing with him because he got the better of him too easily.", "형으로서의 온갖 너그러움을 품고 그는 때때로 동생에게 사물의 참된 의미를 설명해 주곤 했지만, 동생과 논쟁하는 데서 얻는 만족은 별로 없었으니, 그를 너무 쉽게 이겨 버렸기 때문이다."]], [["Konstantin Levin regarded his brother as a man of immense intellect and culture, as generous in the highest sense of the word, and possessed of a special faculty for working for the public good.", "콘스탄틴 레빈은 형을 어마어마한 지성과 교양을 갖춘 사람으로, 그 말의 가장 높은 의미에서 관대한 사람으로, 그리고 공익을 위해 일하는 특별한 재능을 지닌 사람으로 여겼다."], ["But in the depths of his heart, the older he became, and the more intimately he knew his brother, the more and more frequently the thought struck him that this faculty of working for the public good, of which he felt himself utterly devoid, was possibly not so much a quality as a lack of something—not a lack of good, honest, noble desires and tastes, but a lack of vital force, of what is called heart, of that impulse which drives a man to choose someone out of the innumerable paths of life, and to care only for that one.", "그러나 마음 깊은 곳에서는, 나이가 들수록, 또 형을 더 속속들이 알아 갈수록, 자기에게는 전혀 없다고 느끼는 그 공익을 위해 일하는 재능이라는 것이 어쩌면 하나의 자질이라기보다는 무언가의 결핍이 아닐까 하는 생각이 점점 더 자주 들었다—선하고 정직하고 고결한 욕망이나 취향의 결핍이 아니라, 생명력의 결핍, 이른바 가슴이라 불리는 것의 결핍, 사람으로 하여금 삶의 헤아릴 수 없는 길들 가운데 하나를 골라 오직 그 하나만을 마음에 두게 만드는 그 충동의 결핍 말이다."], ["The better he knew his brother, the more he noticed that Sergey Ivanovitch, and many other people who worked for the public welfare, were not led by an impulse of the heart to care for the public good, but reasoned from intellectual considerations that it was a right thing to take interest in public affairs, and consequently took interest in them.", "형을 알면 알수록 그는 세르게이 이바노비치를, 그리고 공공의 복리를 위해 일하는 다른 많은 사람들을 더욱 눈여겨보게 되었는데, 그들은 가슴의 충동에 이끌려 공익을 마음에 두는 것이 아니라, 공공의 일에 관심을 갖는 것이 옳다고 지적인 고려에서 추론하여 그 결과 그것에 관심을 갖는 것이었다."], ["Levin was confirmed in this generalization by observing that his brother did not take questions affecting the public welfare or the question of the immortality of the soul a bit more to heart than he did chess problems, or the ingenious construction of a new machine.", "레빈은 형이 공공의 복리에 관한 문제나 영혼불멸의 문제를 체스 문제나 새로운 기계의 정교한 설계만큼도 더 마음에 두지 않는다는 것을 관찰함으로써 이런 일반화에 더욱 확신을 갖게 되었다."]], [["Besides this, Konstantin Levin was not at his ease with his brother, because in summer in the country Levin was continually busy with work on the land, and the long summer day was not long enough for him to get through all he had to do, while Sergey Ivanovitch was taking a holiday.", "이 밖에도 콘스탄틴 레빈은 형과 함께 있는 것이 마음 편치 않았는데, 여름에 시골에서 레빈은 끊임없이 땅에서 하는 일로 바빴고 긴 여름날도 해야 할 일을 다 끝내기에는 모자랐던 반면, 세르게이 이바노비치는 휴가를 보내고 있었기 때문이다."], ["But though he was taking a holiday now, that is to say, he was doing no writing, he was so used to intellectual activity that he liked to put into concise and eloquent shape the ideas that occurred to him, and liked to have someone to listen to him.", "그러나 지금 휴가를 보내고 있다고는 해도, 즉 글을 쓰지 않고 있다고는 해도, 그는 지적인 활동에 너무도 익숙해져 있어서 머릿속에 떠오른 생각들을 간결하고 유려한 형태로 다듬는 것을 좋아했고, 그것을 들어 줄 누군가가 있는 것을 좋아했다."], ["His most usual and natural listener was his brother.", "그의 가장 흔하고 자연스러운 청자는 동생이었다."], ["And so in spite of the friendliness and directness of their relations, Konstantin felt an awkwardness in leaving him alone.", "그래서 두 사람의 관계가 다정하고 거리낌이 없었음에도, 콘스탄틴은 형을 혼자 내버려 두는 것에 어색함을 느꼈다."], ["Sergey Ivanovitch liked to stretch himself on the grass in the sun, and to lie so, basking and chatting lazily.", "세르게이 이바노비치는 햇볕 아래 풀밭에 몸을 쭉 뻗고 누워, 그렇게 누워 볕을 쬐며 느긋하게 이야기 나누기를 좋아했다."]], [["“You wouldn’t believe,”", "\"넌 믿지 못할 거다.\""], ["he would say to his brother, “what a pleasure this rural laziness is to me.", "그는 동생에게 말하곤 했다. \"이 시골의 게으름이 나한테 얼마나 큰 즐거움인지 말이야."], ["Not an idea in one’s brain, as empty as a drum!”", "머릿속에 생각 하나 없이, 텅 빈 북처럼 비어 있다니까!\""]], [["But Konstantin Levin found it dull sitting and listening to him, especially when he knew that while he was away they would be carting dung onto the fields not ploughed ready for it, and heaping it all up anyhow; and would not screw the shares in the ploughs, but would let them come off and then say that the new ploughs were a silly invention, and there was nothing like the old Andreevna plough, and so on.", "그러나 콘스탄틴 레빈은 앉아서 형의 이야기를 듣고 있는 것이 따분했는데, 특히 자기가 자리를 비운 사이에 미처 갈아 두지 않은 밭에 거름을 실어 날라 아무렇게나 무더기로 쌓아 둔다는 것을, 그리고 쟁기에 보습을 단단히 죄어 두지 않아 빠져 버리게 해 놓고는 새 쟁기란 멍청한 발명품이고 옛날 안드레예브나 쟁기만 한 것이 없다는 둥 그런 소리를 한다는 것을 알 때면 더욱 그러했다."]], [["“Come, you’ve done enough trudging about in the heat,”", "\"이봐, 이 더위에 그만큼 쏘다녔으면 됐다.\""], ["Sergey Ivanovitch would say to him.", "세르게이 이바노비치가 그에게 말하곤 했다."]], [["“No, I must just run round to the counting-house for a minute,”", "\"아뇨, 잠깐 사무소에 좀 다녀와야 해서요.\""], ["Levin would answer, and he would run off to the fields.", "레빈은 이렇게 대답하고는 밭으로 달려가곤 했다."]], [["# Chapter 2", "# Chapter 2"]], [["Early in June it happened that Agafea Mihalovna, the old nurse and housekeeper, in carrying to the cellar a jar of mushrooms she had just pickled, slipped, fell, and sprained her wrist.", "6월 초에 이런 일이 있었다. 늙은 유모이자 가정부인 아가피야 미하일로브나가 방금 절인 버섯 항아리를 지하실로 나르다가 미끄러져 넘어지면서 손목을 삐었다."], ["The district doctor, a talkative young medical student, who had just finished his studies, came to see her.", "학업을 막 마친, 말 많은 젊은 의대생인 지역 의사가 그녀를 보러 왔다."], ["He examined the wrist, said it was not broken, was delighted at a chance of talking to the celebrated Sergey Ivanovitch Koznishev, and to show his advanced views of things told him all the scandal of the district, complaining of the poor state into which the district council had fallen.", "그는 손목을 살펴보고는 부러진 데는 없다고 말했고, 저명한 세르게이 이바노비치 코즈니셰프와 이야기할 기회가 생긴 것을 기뻐하며, 자신의 진보적인 견해를 과시하려고 지역의 온갖 추문을 그에게 늘어놓았으며, 지역 의회가 형편없는 지경에 빠졌다고 불평했다."], ["Sergey Ivanovitch listened attentively, asked him questions, and, roused by a new listener, he talked fluently, uttered a few keen and weighty observations, respectfully appreciated by the young doctor, and was soon in that eager frame of mind his brother knew so well, which always, with him, followed a brilliant and eager conversation.", "세르게이 이바노비치는 귀 기울여 듣고 질문을 던졌으며, 새로운 청자에게 자극을 받아 막힘없이 이야기를 펼쳐 몇 마디 예리하고 묵직한 견해를 내놓았는데, 젊은 의사는 그것을 정중하게 경탄했다. 그러더니 그는 동생이 너무도 잘 아는, 빛나고 열띤 대화 뒤에 늘 그에게 찾아오는 그 들뜬 기분 속으로 곧 빠져들었다."], ["After the departure of the doctor, he wanted to go with a fishing rod to the river.", "의사가 떠난 뒤 그는 낚싯대를 들고 강으로 가고 싶어 했다."], ["Sergey Ivanovitch was fond of angling, and was, it seemed, proud of being able to care for such a stupid occupation.", "세르게이 이바노비치는 낚시를 좋아했고, 그토록 어리석은 소일거리에 마음을 쏟을 수 있다는 것을 도리어 자랑스러워하는 듯했다."]], [["Konstantin Levin, whose presence was needed in the plough land and meadows, had come to take his brother in the trap.", "농경지와 풀밭에 그가 있어야 했던 콘스탄틴 레빈은 형을 데리러 마차를 몰고 왔다."]], [["It was that time of the year, the turning-point of summer, when the crops of the present year are a certainty, when one begins to think of the sowing for next year, and the mowing is at hand; when the rye is all in ear, though its ears are still light, not yet full, and it waves in gray-green billows in the wind; when the green oats, with tufts of yellow grass scattered here and there among it, droop irregularly over the late-sown fields; when the early buckwheat is already out and hiding the ground; when the fallow lands, trodden hard as stone by the cattle, are half ploughed over, with paths left untouched by the plough; when from the dry dung-heaps carted onto the fields there comes at sunset a smell of manure mixed with meadow-sweet, and on the low-lying lands the riverside meadows are a thick sea of grass waiting for the mowing, with blackened heaps of the stalks of sorrel among it.", "올해 작황이 확실해지고, 내년 파종을 생각하기 시작하며, 풀베기가 코앞에 다가온 때, 여름의 전환점이었다. 호밀은 이삭이 다 패었으나 그 이삭은 아직 가벼워 여물지 않았고, 잿빛 도는 초록빛 물결을 이루며 바람에 일렁였다. 푸른 귀리는 군데군데 누런 풀잎 다발이 섞인 채 늦게 뿌린 밭 위로 들쭉날쭉 늘어졌고, 일찍 심은 메밀은 벌써 꽃이 피어 땅을 가렸다. 가축들이 돌처럼 단단히 밟아 놓은 휴경지는 절반쯤 갈렸는데, 쟁기가 닿지 않은 길이 그대로 남아 있었다. 밭으로 실어 온 마른 거름 더미에서는 해 질 녘이면 풀거름과 조팝나무 냄새가 뒤섞여 풍겨 왔고, 낮은 지대의 강가 풀밭은 풀베기를 기다리는 짙은 풀의 바다를 이루었으며, 그 사이로 거뭇해진 수영 줄기 더미들이 흩어져 있었다."]], [["It was the time when there comes a brief pause in the toil of the fields before the beginning of the labors of harvest—every year recurring, every year straining every nerve of the peasants.", "해마다 되풀이되며 해마다 농부들의 온 신경을 곤두서게 하는 수확의 노동이 시작되기 직전, 밭일에 잠깐의 쉼이 찾아오는 때였다."], ["The crop was a splendid one, and bright, hot summer days had set in with short, dewy nights.", "작황은 훌륭했고, 짧은 이슬 밤과 함께 밝고 무더운 여름날이 이어지고 있었다."]], [["The brothers had to drive through the woods to reach the meadows.", "형제는 풀밭에 닿으려면 숲을 가로질러 마차를 몰아야 했다."], ["Sergey Ivanovitch was all the while admiring the beauty of the woods, which were a tangled mass of leaves, pointing out to his brother now an old lime tree on the point of flowering, dark on the shady side, and brightly spotted with yellow stipules, now the young shoots of this year’s saplings brilliant with emerald.", "세르게이 이바노비치는 내내 잎이 무성하게 뒤엉킨 숲의 아름다움에 감탄하며, 동생에게 그늘진 쪽은 거뭇하고 누런 턱잎이 환하게 점점이 박힌, 막 꽃을 피우려는 늙은 보리수를 가리켰다가, 다시 에메랄드빛으로 눈부신 올해 묘목의 새순을 가리켰다."], ["Konstantin Levin did not like talking and hearing about the beauty of nature.", "콘스탄틴 레빈은 자연의 아름다움에 대해 이야기하고 듣는 것을 좋아하지 않았다."], ["Words for him took away the beauty of what he saw.", "그에게는 말이 자기가 본 것의 아름다움을 앗아 갔다."], ["He assented to what his brother said, but he could not help beginning to think of other things.", "그는 형의 말에 맞장구를 쳤지만, 자기도 모르게 다른 일들을 생각하기 시작했다."], ["When they came out of the woods, all his attention was engrossed by the view of the fallow land on the upland, in parts yellow with grass, in parts trampled and checkered with furrows, in parts dotted with ridges of dung, and in parts even ploughed.", "숲에서 빠져나오자 그의 모든 주의는 고지대의 휴경지 풍경에 쏠렸는데, 어떤 곳은 풀이 누렇고, 어떤 곳은 밟혀서 이랑으로 격자무늬를 이루고, 어떤 곳은 거름 더미가 점점이 박혀 있고, 또 어떤 곳은 갈리기까지 했다."], ["A string of carts was moving across it.", "짐수레들이 줄지어 그 위를 가로질러 움직이고 있었다."], ["Levin counted the carts, and was pleased that all that were wanted had been brought, and at the sight of the meadows his thoughts passed to the mowing.", "레빈은 수레를 세어 보고는 필요한 만큼 다 실어 왔다는 것에 흐뭇해했고, 풀밭을 바라보자 그의 생각은 풀베기로 옮겨 갔다."], ["He always felt something special moving him to the quick at the hay-making.", "그는 건초 만드는 일에서 늘 무언가 그를 가슴 깊이 움직이는 특별한 것을 느꼈다."], ["On reaching the meadow Levin stopped the horse.", "풀밭에 이르자 레빈은 말을 멈춰 세웠다."]], [["The morning dew was still lying on the thick undergrowth of the grass, and that he might not get his feet wet, Sergey Ivanovitch asked his brother to drive him in the trap up to the willow tree from which the carp was caught.", "아침 이슬이 아직 빽빽한 풀밭 아랫자락에 내려앉아 있어서, 발이 젖지 않도록 세르게이 이바노비치는 잉어를 낚던 버드나무까지 마차로 데려다 달라고 동생에게 청했다."], ["Sorry as Konstantin Levin was to crush down his mowing grass, he drove him into the meadow.", "콘스탄틴 레빈은 풀베기할 풀을 짓밟는 것이 안타까웠지만 형을 풀밭 안으로 몰고 들어갔다."], ["The high grass softly turned about the wheels and the horse’s legs, leaving its seeds clinging to the wet axles and spokes of the wheels.", "키 큰 풀은 바퀴와 말 다리를 부드럽게 감돌며 젖은 차축과 바큇살에 씨앗을 들러붙게 했다."], ["His brother seated himself under a bush, arranging his tackle, while Levin led the horse away, fastened him up, and walked into the vast gray-green sea of grass unstirred by the wind.", "형은 덤불 아래 자리를 잡고 앉아 낚시 도구를 챙겼고, 그동안 레빈은 말을 끌고 가 매어 놓은 뒤 바람 한 점 일지 않는 잿빛 도는 초록빛 풀의 드넓은 바다로 걸어 들어갔다."], ["The silky grass with its ripe seeds came almost to his waist in the dampest spots.", "여문 씨를 단 비단결 같은 풀은 가장 축축한 자리에서는 거의 그의 허리까지 닿았다."]], [["Crossing the meadow, Konstantin Levin came out onto the road, and met an old man with a swollen eye, carrying a skep on his shoulder.", "풀밭을 가로지른 콘스탄틴 레빈은 길로 나섰고, 눈이 부어오른 노인이 어깨에 벌통을 메고 가는 것과 마주쳤다."]], [["“What?", "\"어때요?"], ["taken a stray swarm, Fomitch?”", "도망친 벌 떼를 잡았어요, 포미치?\""], ["he asked.", "그가 물었다."]], [["“No, indeed, Konstantin Dmitrich!", "\"아니요, 천만에요, 콘스탄틴 드미트리치!"], ["All we can do to keep our own!", "우리 것 간수하기도 벅찹니다요!"], ["This is the second swarm that has flown away....", "벌써 두 번째 벌 떼가 날아가 버렸지 뭡니까……"], ["Luckily the lads caught them.", "다행히 애들이 잡았지요."], ["They were ploughing your field.", "마침 나리 밭을 갈고 있었거든요."], ["They unyoked the horses and galloped after them.”", "말을 풀어 놓고 벌 떼를 쫓아 달려갔지요.\""]], [["“Well, what do you say, Fomitch—start mowing or wait a bit?”", "\"그래, 포미치, 어떻게 생각해요—풀베기를 시작할까요, 아니면 좀 기다릴까요?\""]], [["“Eh, well.", "\"글쎄요."], ["Our way’s to wait till St. Peter’s Day.", "우리 식으로는 성 베드로 축일까지 기다리지요."], ["But you always mow sooner.", "하지만 나리는 늘 더 일찍 베시잖습니까."], ["Well, to be sure, please God, the hay’s good.", "뭐, 하느님 덕분에 건초는 좋습니다요."], ["There’ll be plenty for the beasts.”", "가축들 먹일 게 넉넉할 겁니다.\""]], [["“What do you think about the weather?”", "\"날씨는 어떨 것 같소?\""]], [["“That’s in God’s hands.", "\"그야 하느님 손에 달렸지요."], ["Maybe it will be fine.”", "어쩌면 맑을지도 모르고요.\""]], [["Levin went up to his brother.", "레빈은 형에게 다가갔다."]], [["Sergey Ivanovitch had caught nothing, but he was not bored, and seemed in the most cheerful frame of mind.", "세르게이 이바노비치는 한 마리도 잡지 못했지만 지루해하지 않았고, 더없이 명랑한 기분에 잠겨 있는 듯했다."], ["Levin saw that, stimulated by his conversation with the doctor, he wanted to talk.", "레빈은 형이 의사와 나눈 대화에 자극을 받아 이야기를 하고 싶어 한다는 것을 알아챘다."], ["Levin, on the other hand, would have liked to get home as soon as possible to give orders about getting together the mowers for next day, and to set at rest his doubts about the mowing, which greatly absorbed him.", "반면에 레빈은 한시바삐 집으로 돌아가 이튿날 풀 벨 일꾼들을 불러 모으라는 지시를 내리고, 그를 몹시 사로잡고 있던 풀베기에 대한 의구심을 가라앉히고 싶었다."]], [["“Well, let’s be going,”", "\"자, 이제 가시죠.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“Why be in such a hurry?", "\"뭘 그리 서두르느냐?"], ["Let’s stay a little.", "좀 더 있자꾸나."], ["But how wet you are!", "그런데 너 어쩜 그리 흠뻑 젖었니!"], ["Even though one catches nothing, it’s nice.", "한 마리도 못 잡는다 해도 이건 좋은 거란다."], ["That’s the best thing about every part of sport, that one has to do with nature.", "온갖 놀이 중에서 가장 좋은 점이 바로 자연을 상대한다는 거지."], ["How exquisite this steely water is!”", "이 강철빛 물이 얼마나 곱냐!\""], ["said Sergey Ivanovitch.", "세르게이 이바노비치가 말했다."], ["“These riverside banks always remind me of the riddle—do you know it?", "\"이런 강가 둑을 보면 늘 그 수수께끼가 떠오르는구나—너도 아니?"], ["‘The grass says to the water: we quiver and we quiver.’”", "'풀이 물에게 말하기를, 우리는 흔들리고 또 흔들린다.'\""]], [["“I don’t know the riddle,”", "\"그 수수께끼는 모르겠는데요.\""], ["answered Levin wearily.", "레빈이 지친 듯이 대답했다."]], [["# Chapter 3", "# Chapter 3"]], [["“Do you know, I’ve been thinking about you,”", "\"있잖아, 내가 너에 대해 생각해 봤어.\""], ["said Sergey Ivanovitch.", "세르게이 이바노비치가 말했다."], ["“It’s beyond everything what’s being done in the district, according to what this doctor tells me.", "\"그 의사가 내게 들려준 바로는, 군(郡)에서 벌어지고 있는 일이란 게 정말 이만저만이 아니더구나."], ["He’s a very intelligent fellow.", "아주 똑똑한 친구야."], ["And as I’ve told you before, I tell you again: it’s not right for you not to go to the meetings, and altogether to keep out of the district business.", "그리고 전에도 말했지만 다시 한 번 말하마. 네가 회의에 나가지 않고, 군의 일에서 아예 발을 빼고 있는 건 옳지 않아."], ["If decent people won’t go into it, of course it’s bound to go all wrong.", "점잖은 사람들이 거기에 끼어들지 않으면, 당연히 모든 게 엉망이 될 수밖에 없어."], ["We pay the money, and it all goes in salaries, and there are no schools, nor district nurses, nor midwives, nor drugstores—nothing.”", "우리는 돈을 내는데, 그게 전부 봉급으로 나가 버리고, 학교도 없고, 군 간호사도, 산파도, 약방도—아무것도 없어.\""]], [["“Well, I did try, you know,”", "\"음, 나도 해 보긴 했어, 알잖아.\""], ["Levin said slowly and unwillingly.", "레빈이 천천히, 마지못해 말했다."], ["“I can’t!", "\"안 되더라고!"], ["and so there’s no help for it.”", "그러니 어쩔 도리가 없는 거지.\""]], [["“But why can’t you?", "\"하지만 왜 안 된다는 거야?"], ["I must own I can’t make it out.", "솔직히 난 도무지 이해가 안 돼."], ["Indifference, incapacity—I won’t admit; surely it’s not simply laziness?”", "무관심이라거나 무능이라거나—그런 건 인정하지 않겠어. 설마 단순히 게을러서는 아니겠지?\""]], [["“None of those things.", "\"그런 것들은 다 아니야."], ["I’ve tried, and I see I can do nothing,”", "해 봤는데, 내가 아무것도 할 수 없다는 걸 알았을 뿐이야.\""], ["said Levin.", "레빈이 말했다."]], [["He had hardly grasped what his brother was saying.", "그는 형이 하는 말을 거의 알아듣지 못했다."], ["Looking towards the plough land across the river, he made out something black, but he could not distinguish whether it was a horse or the bailiff on horseback.", "강 건너 밭을 바라보던 그는 무언가 검은 것을 알아보았지만, 그게 말인지 말을 탄 관리인인지는 분간할 수 없었다."]], [["“Why is it you can do nothing?", "\"왜 네가 아무것도 할 수 없다는 거지?"], ["You made an attempt and didn’t succeed, as you think, and you give in.", "시도를 해 봤는데 네 생각에 성공하지 못했다고 그냥 포기하다니."], ["How can you have so little self-respect?”", "어떻게 그렇게 자존심이 없을 수 있어?\""]], [["“Self-respect!”", "\"자존심이라니!\""], ["said Levin, stung to the quick by his brother’s words; “I don’t understand.", "형의 말에 정곡을 찔린 레빈이 말했다. \"난 이해가 안 돼."], ["If they’d told me at college that other people understood the integral calculus, and I didn’t, then pride would have come in.", "만약 대학에서 다른 사람들은 다 적분을 이해하는데 나만 이해하지 못한다고 했다면, 그땐 자존심 문제가 끼어들었겠지."], ["But in this case one wants first to be convinced that one has certain qualifications for this sort of business, and especially that all this business is of great importance.”", "하지만 이런 경우엔 무엇보다 먼저 자기가 이런 종류의 일에 어떤 자질을 갖추고 있다는 확신이 있어야 하고, 특히 그 모든 일이 대단히 중요하다는 확신이 있어야 하는 거야.\""]], [["“What!", "\"뭐라고!"], ["do you mean to say it’s not of importance?”", "그러니까 그게 중요하지 않다는 말이냐?\""], ["said Sergey Ivanovitch, stung to the quick too at his brother’s considering anything of no importance that interested him, and still more at his obviously paying little attention to what he was saying.", "세르게이 이바노비치도 정곡을 찔린 듯 말했다. 자기가 관심을 두는 일을 동생이 중요하지 않게 여기는 것이, 그리고 더욱이 자기가 하는 말에 동생이 분명 거의 주의를 기울이지 않는 것이 그를 찔렀던 것이다."]], [["“I don’t think it important; it does not take hold of me, I can’t help it,”", "\"난 그게 중요하다고 생각하지 않아. 도무지 마음에 와닿지가 않는걸."], ["answered Levin, making out that what he saw was the bailiff, and that the bailiff seemed to be letting the peasants go off the ploughed land.", "어쩔 수 없어.\" 레빈이 대답하면서, 자기가 본 것이 관리인이며, 그 관리인이 농부들을 밭에서 내보내고 있는 듯하다는 것을 알아챘다."], ["They were turning the plough over.", "그들은 쟁기를 뒤집어 놓고 있었다."], ["“Can they have finished ploughing?”", "'벌써 밭갈이를 끝낸 건가?'"], ["he wondered.", "그는 궁금했다."]], [["“Come, really though,”", "\"이봐, 그래도 정말이지.\""], ["said the elder brother, with a frown on his handsome, clever face, “there’s a limit to everything.", "형이 잘생기고 영리한 얼굴을 찌푸리며 말했다. \"모든 일에는 한계가 있는 법이야."], ["It’s very well to be original and genuine, and to dislike everything conventional—I know all about that; but really, what you’re saying either has no meaning, or it has a very wrong meaning.", "독창적이고 진실한 것도, 관습적인 모든 걸 싫어하는 것도 좋아—그건 나도 잘 알아. 하지만 정말이지, 네가 하는 말은 아무 의미가 없거나, 아니면 아주 잘못된 의미를 담고 있어."], ["How can you think it a matter of no importance whether the peasant, whom you love as you assert....”", "네가 사랑한다고 주장하는 그 농부가……\""]], [["“I never did assert it,”", "'난 그런 주장을 한 적이 없는데.'"], ["thought Konstantin Levin.", "콘스탄틴 레빈은 생각했다."]], [["“...dies without help?", "\"……도움 없이 죽어 가는 게 중요하지 않은 일이라고 어떻게 생각할 수 있어?"], ["The ignorant peasant-women starve the children, and the people stagnate in darkness, and are helpless in the hands of every village clerk, while you have at your disposal a means of helping them, and don’t help them because to your mind it’s of no importance.”", "무지한 농촌 아낙들은 아이들을 굶기고, 사람들은 어둠 속에 정체되어 마을 서기들의 손아귀에서 속수무책으로 휘둘리는데, 너는 그들을 도울 수단을 손에 쥐고 있으면서도, 네 생각엔 그게 중요하지 않다는 이유로 돕지 않는단 말이지.\""]], [["And Sergey Ivanovitch put before him the alternative: either you are so undeveloped that you can’t see all that you can do, or you won’t sacrifice your ease, your vanity, or whatever it is, to do it.", "그러고는 세르게이 이바노비치가 그 앞에 양자택일을 들이밀었다. 즉, 너는 네가 할 수 있는 모든 일을 보지 못할 만큼 미숙하거나, 아니면 그 일을 하기 위해 네 편안함이나 허영심이나 그 무엇이든 희생하기를 원치 않거나, 둘 중 하나라는 것이었다."]], [["Konstantin Levin felt that there was no course open to him but to submit, or to confess to a lack of zeal for the public good.", "콘스탄틴 레빈은 굴복하거나, 아니면 공익을 위한 열의가 부족함을 자백하는 것 외에는 자기에게 열려 있는 길이 없다고 느꼈다."], ["And this mortified him and hurt his feelings.", "그리고 이것이 그의 자존심을 짓밟았고 그의 감정을 상하게 했다."]], [["“It’s both,”", "\"둘 다야.\""], ["he said resolutely: “I don’t see that it was possible....”", "그가 단호하게 말했다. \"난 그게 가능했다고 보지 않아……\""]], [["“What!", "\"뭐라고!"], ["was it impossible, if the money were properly laid out, to provide medical aid?”", "돈을 제대로 썼다면 의료 지원을 제공하는 게 불가능했단 말이냐?\""]], [["“Impossible, as it seems to me....", "\"내가 보기엔 불가능해……"], ["For the three thousand square miles of our district, what with our thaws, and the storms, and the work in the fields, I don’t see how it is possible to provide medical aid all over.", "우리 군의 그 삼천 평방마일에 걸쳐, 해빙이며 폭풍우며 밭일까지 겹치는데, 어떻게 온 군에 두루 의료 지원을 할 수 있다는 건지 난 모르겠어."], ["And besides, I don’t believe in medicine.”", "게다가 난 의학을 믿지 않아.\""]], [["“Oh, well, that’s unfair ...", "\"오, 그건 공정하지 못한 말이군……"], ["I can quote to you thousands of instances....", "내가 너에게 수천 가지 사례를 들려줄 수도 있어……"], ["But the schools, anyway.”", "하지만 학교는, 어쨌든 학교는 말이야.\""]], [["“Why have schools?”", "\"학교는 뭣 하러 세워?\""]], [["“What do you mean?", "\"무슨 소리야?"], ["Can there be two opinions of the advantage of education?", "교육의 이로움에 대해 두 가지 의견이 있을 수 있겠어?"], ["If it’s a good thing for you, it’s a good thing for everyone.”", "그게 너에게 좋은 거라면, 모두에게도 좋은 거지.\""]], [["Konstantin Levin felt himself morally pinned against a wall, and so he got hot, and unconsciously blurted out the chief cause of his indifference to public business.", "콘스탄틴 레빈은 자기가 도덕적으로 벽에 몰린 듯한 기분이 들었고, 그래서 발끈해서는 공적인 일에 무관심한 자기 마음의 가장 큰 원인을 무심코 내뱉어 버렸다."]], [["“Perhaps it may all be very good; but why should I worry myself about establishing dispensaries which I shall never make use of, and schools to which I shall never send my children, to which even the peasants don’t want to send their children, and to which I’ve no very firm faith that they ought to send them?”", "\"어쩌면 그게 다 아주 좋은 일일지도 모르지. 하지만 내가 결코 이용하지도 않을 진료소를 세우고, 내 아이들은 결코 보내지도 않을, 농부들조차 자기 아이들을 보내고 싶어 하지 않는, 게다가 그들이 거기에 아이들을 보내야 한다는 확고한 믿음도 내겐 없는 그런 학교를 세우는 일에 내가 왜 골머리를 앓아야 하지?\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["Sergey Ivanovitch was for a minute surprised at this unexpected view of the subject; but he promptly made a new plan of attack.", "세르게이 이바노비치는 이 뜻밖의 관점에 잠시 놀랐지만, 곧바로 새로운 공격 계획을 세웠다."], ["He was silent for a little, drew out a hook, threw it in again, and turned to his brother smiling.", "그는 잠시 입을 다물고 낚싯바늘을 끌어올렸다가 다시 던지고는, 미소를 지으며 동생에게로 몸을 돌렸다."]], [["“Come, now....", "\"이봐……"], ["In the first place, the dispensary is needed.", "우선 진료소는 필요해."], ["We ourselves sent for the district doctor for Agafea Mihalovna.”", "우리도 아가피야 미하일로브나를 위해 군 의사를 불러왔잖아.\""]], [["“Oh, well, but I fancy her wrist will never be straight again.”", "\"아, 그렇긴 한데, 내 생각엔 그 사람 손목은 다시는 펴지지 않을 것 같던데.\""]], [["“That remains to be proved....", "\"그건 두고 봐야 알 일이지……"], ["Next, the peasant who can read and write is as a workman of more use and value to you.”", "다음으로, 읽고 쓸 줄 아는 농부는 일꾼으로서 너에게 더 쓸모 있고 가치가 있어.\""]], [["“No, you can ask anyone you like,”", "\"아니, 누구한테든 물어봐.\""], ["Konstantin Levin answered with decision, “the man that can read and write is much inferior as a workman.", "콘스탄틴 레빈이 단호하게 대답했다. \"읽고 쓸 줄 아는 사람은 일꾼으로서는 훨씬 못해."], ["And mending the highroads is an impossibility; and as soon as they put up bridges they’re stolen.”", "그리고 큰길을 보수하는 일은 불가능해. 다리를 놓기가 무섭게 도둑맞으니까.\""]], [["“Still, that’s not the point,”", "\"그래도 그건 요점이 아니야.\""], ["said Sergey Ivanovitch, frowning.", "세르게이 이바노비치가 미간을 찌푸리며 말했다."], ["He disliked contradiction, and still more, arguments that were continually skipping from one thing to another, introducing new and disconnected points, so that there was no knowing to which to reply.", "그는 반박당하는 걸 싫어했고, 더욱이 끊임없이 이것에서 저것으로 건너뛰며 새롭고 연관성도 없는 논점들을 끌어들여 어디에 대답해야 할지 알 수 없게 만드는 논쟁을 싫어했다."], ["“Do you admit that education is a benefit for the people?”", "\"교육이 백성에게 이롭다는 건 인정하니?\""]], [["“Yes, I admit it,”", "\"그래, 인정해.\""], ["said Levin without thinking, and he was conscious immediately that he had said what he did not think.", "레빈이 별생각 없이 말했고, 곧바로 자기가 생각지도 않은 말을 했다는 것을 의식했다."], ["He felt that if he admitted that, it would be proved that he had been talking meaningless rubbish.", "그것을 인정하면, 자기가 줄곧 의미 없는 헛소리를 지껄여 왔다는 게 증명되리라는 것을 그는 느꼈다."], ["How it would be proved he could not tell, but he knew that this would inevitably be logically proved to him, and he awaited the proofs.", "그게 어떻게 증명될지는 알 수 없었지만, 형이 그것을 자기에게 논리적으로 반드시 증명해 보이리라는 것은 알았고, 그래서 그 증명을 기다렸다."]], [["The argument turned out to be far simpler than he had expected.", "논쟁은 그가 예상했던 것보다 훨씬 단순한 것으로 드러났다."]], [["“If you admit that it is a benefit,”", "\"그게 이롭다는 걸 인정한다면,\""], ["said Sergey Ivanovitch, “then, as an honest man, you cannot help caring about it and sympathizing with the movement, and so wishing to work for it.”", "세르게이 이바노비치가 말했다. \"정직한 사람으로서 너는 그것에 마음을 쓰지 않을 수 없고, 그 운동에 공감하지 않을 수 없고, 따라서 그것을 위해 일하고 싶어 하지 않을 수 없는 거지.\""]], [["“But I still do not admit this movement to be just,”", "\"하지만 난 아직도 이 운동이 정당하다고는 인정하지 않아.\""], ["said Konstantin Levin, reddening a little.", "콘스탄틴 레빈이 약간 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다."]], [["“What!", "\"뭐라고!"], ["But you said just now....”", "하지만 방금 네 입으로……\""]], [["“That’s to say, I don’t admit it’s being either good or possible.”", "\"그러니까, 난 그게 좋다거나 가능하다고 인정한 건 아니라는 말이야.\""]], [["“That you can’t tell without making the trial.”", "\"그건 시도해 보지 않고는 알 수 없는 거잖아.\""]], [["“Well, supposing that’s so,”", "\"그래, 그렇다고 치자.\" 레빈은 전혀 그렇게 생각하지 않으면서도 말했다."], ["said Levin, though he did not suppose so at all, “supposing that is so, still I don’t see, all the same, what I’m to worry myself about it for.”", "\"설령 그렇다 쳐도, 그래도 난 여전히 모르겠어. 내가 왜 그 일에 골머리를 앓아야 하는지 말이야.\""]], [["“How so?”", "\"어째서 그렇다는 거지?\""], ["“No; since we are talking, explain it to me from the philosophical point of view,”", "\"아니, 이왕 이야기가 나왔으니, 철학적 관점에서 그걸 나한테 설명해 봐.\""], ["said Levin.", "레빈이 말했다."]], [["“I can’t see where philosophy comes in,”", "\"여기서 철학이 어디서 끼어드는 건지 난 모르겠는데.\""], ["said Sergey Ivanovitch, in a tone, Levin fancied, as though he did not admit his brother’s right to talk about philosophy.", "세르게이 이바노비치가 말했는데, 레빈이 느끼기에 그것은 마치 동생이 철학을 논할 권리가 있다는 걸 인정하지 않는다는 투였다."], ["And that irritated Levin.", "그리고 그것이 레빈을 짜증나게 했다."]], [["“I’ll tell you, then,”", "\"그럼 내가 말해 보지.\""], ["he said with heat, “I imagine the mainspring of all our actions is, after all, self-interest.", "그가 열을 내며 말했다. \"내 생각에 우리 모든 행동의 근본 동력은 결국 사리사욕이야."], ["Now in the local institutions I, as a nobleman, see nothing that could conduce to my prosperity, and the roads are not better and could not be better; my horses carry me well enough over bad ones.", "그런데 난 귀족으로서, 지방 기관에서 내 번영에 도움이 될 만한 건 아무것도 못 봤어. 길은 더 나아지지도 않았고, 더 나아질 수도 없어."], ["Doctors and dispensaries are no use to me.", "내 말들은 나쁜 길에서도 나를 충분히 잘 실어 나르니까."], ["An arbitrator of disputes is no use to me.", "의사나 진료소도 내겐 쓸모가 없어. 분쟁 조정관도 내겐 쓸모가 없어."], ["I never appeal to him, and never shall appeal to him.", "난 그에게 호소한 적도 없고, 앞으로도 결코 호소하지 않을 거야."], ["The schools are no good to me, but positively harmful, as I told you.", "학교도 내겐 아무 도움이 안 될뿐더러, 너한테 말했듯이 도리어 해로워."], ["For me the district institutions simply mean the liability to pay fourpence halfpenny for every three acres, to drive into the town, sleep with bugs, and listen to all sorts of idiocy and loathsomeness, and self-interest offers me no inducement.”", "내게 군 기관이란 그저 삼 에이커마다 사 펜스 반의 세금을 낼 의무를 지고, 읍내까지 마차를 몰고 가서, 빈대와 함께 자고, 온갖 멍청하고 역겨운 소리를 듣는 것을 의미할 뿐이야. 사리사욕은 내게 아무런 유인도 주지 않아.\""]], [["“Excuse me,”", "\"실례지만,\""], ["Sergey Ivanovitch interposed with a smile, “self-interest did not induce us to work for the emancipation of the serfs, but we did work for it.”", "세르게이 이바노비치가 미소를 띠며 끼어들었다. \"농노 해방을 위해 일하도록 우리를 부추긴 건 사리사욕이 아니었지만, 우리는 그 일을 위해 일했지.\""]], [["“No!”", "\"아니!\""], ["Konstantin Levin broke in with still greater heat; “the emancipation of the serfs was a different matter.", "콘스탄틴 레빈이 한층 더 열을 내며 가로막았다. \"농노 해방은 다른 문제였어."], ["There self-interest did come in.", "거기엔 사리사욕이 분명 끼어 있었지."], ["One longed to throw off that yoke that crushed us, all decent people among us.", "우리, 그러니까 우리 가운데 점잖은 사람들 모두를 짓누르던 그 멍에를 벗어 던지기를 갈망했으니까."], ["But to be a town councilor and discuss how many dustmen are needed, and how chimneys shall be constructed in the town in which I don’t live—to serve on a jury and try a peasant who’s stolen a flitch of bacon, and listen for six hours at a stretch to all sorts of jabber from the counsel for the defense and the prosecution, and the president cross-examining my old half-witted Alioshka, ‘Do you admit, prisoner in the dock, the fact of the removal of the bacon?’ ‘Eh?’”", "하지만 시(市)의원이 되어 청소부가 몇 명이나 필요한지, 내가 살지도 않는 도시에서 굴뚝을 어떻게 지을지 따위를 논의하고—배심원으로 나가 베이컨 한 덩이를 훔친 농부를 재판하고, 변호인과 검사가 늘어놓는 온갖 횡설수설을 여섯 시간씩 내리 듣고, 재판장이 내 늙고 모자란 알료시카를 심문하면서 '피고인, 베이컨을 빼돌린 사실을 인정하나?' '예?'\""]], [["Konstantin Levin had warmed to his subject, and began mimicking the president and the half-witted Alioshka: it seemed to him that it was all to the point.", "콘스탄틴 레빈은 자기 이야기에 열이 올라, 재판장과 모자란 알료시카를 흉내 내기 시작했다. 그에게는 그 모든 게 요점에 딱 들어맞는 것처럼 여겨졌다."]], [["But Sergey Ivanovitch shrugged his shoulders.", "하지만 세르게이 이바노비치는 어깨를 으쓱했다."]], [["“Well, what do you mean to say, then?”", "\"그래서, 대체 무슨 말을 하려는 거냐?\""]], [["“I simply mean to say that those rights that touch me ... my interest, I shall always defend to the best of my ability; that when they made raids on us students, and the police read our letters, I was ready to defend those rights to the utmost, to defend my rights to education and freedom. I can understand compulsory military service, which affects my children, my brothers, and myself, I am ready to deliberate on what concerns me; but deliberating on how to spend forty thousand roubles of district council money, or judging the half-witted Alioshka—I don’t understand, and I can’t do it.”", "\"내 말은 그저 이거야. 나와 관련된 권리들…… 내 이익에 관한 것이라면, 난 언제나 힘닿는 데까지 지킬 거라는 거지. 그들이 우리 대학생들을 급습하고 경찰이 우리 편지를 읽었을 때, 난 그 권리들을, 교육과 자유에 대한 내 권리를 끝까지 지킬 각오가 되어 있었어. 내 아이들과 형제들, 그리고 나 자신에게 영향을 미치는 의무 병역도 이해할 수 있어. 나와 관련된 일이라면 기꺼이 숙고할 거야. 하지만 군 의회 돈 사만 루블을 어떻게 쓸지 따위를 숙고하거나, 모자란 알료시카를 재판하는 일은—난 이해할 수도 없고, 할 수도 없어.\""]], [["Konstantin Levin spoke as though the floodgates of his speech had burst open.", "콘스탄틴 레빈은 마치 말의 수문이 활짝 터져 버린 것처럼 이야기했다."], ["Sergey Ivanovitch smiled.", "세르게이 이바노비치가 미소를 지었다."]], [["“But tomorrow it’ll be your turn to be tried; would it have suited your tastes better to be tried in the old criminal tribunal?”", "\"하지만 내일은 네가 재판받을 차례가 될 수도 있어. 그러면 옛날의 형사 재판소에서 재판받는 게 네 취향엔 더 맞았겠니?\""]], [["“I’m not going to be tried.", "\"난 재판받을 일 없어. 난 누구도 죽이지 않을 거고, 그럴 필요도 없으니까."], ["I shan’t murder anybody, and I’ve no need of it.", "자, 그럼 내가 말해 주지.\" 그는 또다시 전혀 요점에서 벗어난 주제로 날아가며 말을 이었다."], ["Well, I tell you what,”", "\"우리 군의 지방 자치니 그 밖의 모든 것들이니 하는 건 말이야, 이를테면 우리가 삼위일체 축일에 땅에 꽂아 놓는 자작나무 가지 같은 거야."], ["he went on, flying off again to a subject quite beside the point, “our district self-government and all the rest of it—it’s just like the birch branches we stick in the ground on Trinity Day, for instance, to look like a copse which has grown up of itself in Europe, and I can’t gush over these birch branches and believe in them.”", "유럽에서 절로 자라난 작은 숲처럼 보이게 하려고 말이지. 그런데 난 그런 자작나무 가지에 호들갑을 떨며 그것들을 믿을 수가 없어.\""]], [["Sergey Ivanovitch merely shrugged his shoulders, as though to express his wonder how the birch branches had come into their argument at that point, though he did really understand at once what his brother meant.", "세르게이 이바노비치는 그저 어깨를 으쓱했을 뿐인데, 마치 자작나무 가지가 어쩌다 그 대목에서 자기들 논쟁에 끼어들게 됐는지 의아하다는 것을 표현하려는 듯했다. 사실 그는 동생이 무슨 말을 하려는지 곧바로 알아챘으면서도 말이다."]], [["“Excuse me, but you know one really can’t argue in that way,”", "\"실례지만, 그런 식으로는 정말 논쟁이 안 된다는 걸 너도 알잖아.\""], ["he observed.", "그가 한마디 했다."]], [["But Konstantin Levin wanted to justify himself for the failing, of which he was conscious, of lack of zeal for the public welfare, and he went on.", "하지만 콘스탄틴 레빈은 자기도 의식하고 있는 그 결점, 즉 공익을 위한 열의가 부족하다는 것을 변명하고 싶어서 말을 이어 갔다."]], [["“I imagine,”", "\"내 생각엔,\""], ["he said, “that no sort of activity is likely to be lasting if it is not founded on self-interest, that’s a universal principle, a philosophical principle,”", "그가 말했다. \"어떤 종류의 활동이든 사리사욕에 기초하지 않으면 오래갈 수 없을 것 같아."], ["he said, repeating the word “philosophical”", "그건 보편적인 원리야, 철학적인 원리지.\""], ["with determination, as though wishing to show that he had as much right as anyone else to talk of philosophy.", "그는 마치 자기도 남들 못지않게 철학을 논할 권리가 있음을 보여 주고 싶다는 듯이, 단호하게 \"철학적인\"이라는 말을 되풀이했다."]], [["Sergey Ivanovitch smiled.", "세르게이 이바노비치가 미소를 지었다."], ["“He too has a philosophy of his own at the service of his natural tendencies,”", "'이 녀석도 제 타고난 성향을 떠받드는 제 나름의 철학을 가지고 있군.'"], ["he thought.", "그는 생각했다."]], [["“Come, you’d better let philosophy alone,” he said.", "\"이봐, 넌 철학은 그냥 가만 놔두는 게 낫겠다.\""], ["“The chief problem of the philosophy of all ages consists just in finding the indispensable connection which exists between individual and social interests.", "그가 말했다. \"동서고금 모든 철학의 핵심 문제는 바로 개인의 이익과 사회의 이익 사이에 존재하는 필수불가결한 연결을 찾아내는 데 있어."], ["But that’s not to the point; what is to the point is a correction I must make in your comparison.", "하지만 그건 요점이 아니고. 요점은 네 비유에서 내가 한 가지 바로잡아야 할 게 있다는 거야."], ["The birches are not simply stuck in, but some are sown and some are planted, and one must deal carefully with them.", "자작나무는 그냥 꽂아 두는 게 아니라, 어떤 건 씨를 뿌리고 어떤 건 심는 거야."], ["It’s only those peoples that have an intuitive sense of what’s of importance and significance in their institutions, and know how to value them, that have a future before them—it’s only those peoples that one can truly call historical.”", "그리고 그것들은 정성껏 다뤄야 하는 거고. 자기네 제도 속에서 무엇이 중요하고 의미 있는지를 직관적으로 감지하고, 그것을 평가할 줄 아는 민족만이 앞날을 가지는 거야—그런 민족만을 진정으로 역사적인 민족이라 부를 수 있는 거지.\""]], [["And Sergey Ivanovitch carried the subject into the regions of philosophical history where Konstantin Levin could not follow him, and showed him all the incorrectness of his view.", "그러고는 세르게이 이바노비치가 그 주제를 콘스탄틴 레빈이 따라갈 수 없는 철학적 역사의 영역으로 끌고 가서는, 동생의 견해가 얼마나 그릇된 것인지를 낱낱이 보여 주었다."]], [["“As for your dislike of it, excuse my saying so, that’s simply our Russian sloth and old serf-owner’s ways, and I’m convinced that in you it’s a temporary error and will pass.”", "\"네가 그걸 싫어하는 것으로 말하자면, 이런 말 해서 미안하다만, 그건 그저 우리 러시아인 특유의 게으름이고 옛 농노주(農奴主)의 습성일 뿐이야. 그리고 난 네 경우에 그게 일시적인 잘못일 뿐이고 곧 지나가리라고 확신해.\""]], [["Konstantin was silent.", "콘스탄틴은 입을 다물었다."], ["He felt himself vanquished on all sides, but he felt at the same time that what he wanted to say was unintelligible to his brother.", "그는 자기가 사방에서 패배했다고 느꼈지만, 동시에 자기가 하고 싶은 말을 형이 알아듣지 못했다고도 느꼈다."], ["Only he could not make up his mind whether it was unintelligible because he was not capable of expressing his meaning clearly, or because his brother would not or could not understand him.", "다만 그것이 자기가 자기 뜻을 명료하게 표현할 능력이 없어서인지, 아니면 형이 자기를 이해하려 하지 않거나 이해할 수 없어서인지는 도무지 마음을 정할 수가 없었다."], ["But he did not pursue the speculation, and without replying, he fell to musing on a quite different and personal matter.", "하지만 그는 그 궁리를 더 좇지 않았고, 대꾸도 하지 않은 채 전혀 다른, 자기 개인의 문제에 대한 상념에 빠져들었다."]], [["Sergey Ivanovitch wound up the last line, untied the horse, and they drove off.", "세르게이 이바노비치는 마지막 낚싯줄을 감아올리고 말을 풀었고, 둘은 마차를 몰고 떠났다."]], [["# Chapter 4", "# Chapter 4"]], [["The personal matter that absorbed Levin during his conversation with his brother was this.", "레빈이 형과 대화를 나누는 동안 그의 마음을 사로잡고 있던 사사로운 문제란 이런 것이었다."], ["Once in a previous year he had gone to look at the mowing, and being made very angry by the bailiff he had recourse to his favorite means for regaining his temper,—he took a scythe from a peasant and began mowing.", "언젠가 지난해에 그는 풀 베는 광경을 보러 나갔다가, 관리인 때문에 잔뜩 화가 나서 자기가 화를 가라앉히는 데 가장 즐겨 쓰는 방법에 의지했다. 그는 한 농부에게서 낫을 받아 풀을 베기 시작했던 것이다."]], [["He liked the work so much that he had several times tried his hand at mowing since.", "그는 그 일이 어찌나 마음에 들었던지 그 후로도 몇 번이나 풀베기에 손을 댔다."], ["He had cut the whole of the meadow in front of his house, and this year ever since the early spring he had cherished a plan for mowing for whole days together with the peasants.", "그는 자기 집 앞 풀밭 전체를 베어 냈고, 올해는 이른 봄부터 농부들과 함께 며칠 내내 풀을 베리라는 계획을 마음에 품어 왔다."], ["Ever since his brother’s arrival, he had been in doubt whether to mow or not.", "형이 찾아온 이래로 그는 풀을 벨지 말지 망설이고 있었다."], ["He was loath to leave his brother alone all day long, and he was afraid his brother would laugh at him about it.", "하루 종일 형을 혼자 내버려 두기가 꺼림칙했고, 형이 그 일을 두고 자기를 비웃지나 않을까 두려웠다."], ["But as he drove into the meadow, and recalled the sensations of mowing, he came near deciding that he would go mowing.", "하지만 풀밭으로 마차를 몰고 들어가 풀 베는 감각을 떠올리자, 그는 풀을 베러 가기로 거의 마음을 굳히게 되었다."], ["After the irritating discussion with his brother, he pondered over this intention again.", "형과 신경을 긁는 토론을 벌인 뒤, 그는 이 의도를 다시 한번 곱씹어 보았다."]], [["“I must have physical exercise, or my temper’ll certainly be ruined,”", "'몸을 움직이는 운동을 해야 해. 안 그러면 내 성미가 분명 엉망이 되고 말 거야.'"], ["he thought, and he determined he would go mowing, however awkward he might feel about it with his brother or the peasants.", "그는 이렇게 생각하며, 형 앞에서나 농부들 앞에서나 아무리 어색하게 느껴질지언정 풀을 베러 가기로 결심했다."]], [["Towards evening Konstantin Levin went to his counting house, gave directions as to the work to be done, and sent about the village to summon the mowers for the morrow, to cut the hay in Kalinov meadow, the largest and best of his grass lands.", "저녁 무렵 콘스탄틴 레빈은 사무실로 가서 해야 할 일에 대한 지시를 내리고, 자기 풀밭 가운데 가장 넓고 가장 좋은 칼리노프 풀밭의 건초를 베기 위해 내일 풀 벨 일꾼들을 불러 모으라고 마을 곳곳에 사람을 보냈다."]], [["“And send my scythe, please, to Tit, for him to set it, and bring it round tomorrow.", "\"그리고 내 낫은 티트에게 보내 날을 세워 두었다가 내일 가져오게 해 주게."], ["I shall maybe do some mowing myself too,”", "어쩌면 나도 풀을 좀 벨지 모르니까.\""], ["he said, trying not to be embarrassed.", "그는 멋쩍어하지 않으려 애쓰며 말했다."]], [["The bailiff smiled and said: “Yes, sir.”", "관리인은 빙긋 웃으며 말했다. \"예, 나리.\""]], [["At tea the same evening Levin said to his brother:", "그날 저녁 차를 마시며 레빈은 형에게 말했다."]], [["“I fancy the fine weather will last.", "\"날씨가 계속 좋을 것 같아요."], ["Tomorrow I shall start mowing.”", "내일 풀을 베기 시작할 거예요.\""]], [["“I’m so fond of that form of field labor,”", "\"나는 그런 종류의 들일이 참 좋더구나.\""], ["said Sergey Ivanovitch.", "세르게이 이바노비치가 말했다."]], [["“I’m awfully fond of it.", "\"저는 그 일을 무척 좋아해요."], ["I sometimes mow myself with the peasants, and tomorrow I want to try mowing the whole day.”", "가끔 농부들과 함께 직접 풀을 베곤 하는데, 내일은 하루 종일 풀을 베어 볼 작정이에요.\""]], [["Sergey Ivanovitch lifted his head, and looked with interest at his brother.", "세르게이 이바노비치는 고개를 들고 흥미로운 눈빛으로 동생을 바라보았다."]], [["“How do you mean?", "\"그게 무슨 말이냐?"], ["Just like one of the peasants, all day long?”", "농부들처럼 하루 종일이란 말이냐?\""]], [["“Yes, it’s very pleasant,”", "\"네, 아주 즐거운 일이거든요.\""], ["said Levin.", "레빈이 말했다."]], [["“It’s splendid as exercise, only you’ll hardly be able to stand it,”", "\"운동으로는 더없이 좋지. 다만 네가 그걸 견뎌 낼 수 있을 것 같지 않구나.\""], ["said Sergey Ivanovitch, without a shade of irony.", "세르게이 이바노비치가 비꼬는 기색이라곤 조금도 없이 말했다."]], [["“I’ve tried it.", "\"해 봤어요."], ["It’s hard work at first, but you get into it.", "처음에는 힘든 일이지만, 점점 몸에 익게 돼요."], ["I dare say I shall manage to keep it up....”", "어떻게든 버텨 낼 수 있을 거예요…….\""]], [["“Really!", "\"정말이냐!"], ["what an idea!", "별 생각을 다 하는구나!"], ["But tell me, how do the peasants look at it?", "그런데 말해 보렴, 농부들은 그걸 어떻게 보느냐?"], ["I suppose they laugh in their sleeves at their master’s being such a queer fish?”", "자기네 주인이 그런 별난 사람이라고 속으로 비웃지 않더냐?\""]], [["“No, I don’t think so; but it’s so delightful, and at the same time such hard work, that one has no time to think about it.”", "\"아니요, 그렇진 않은 것 같아요. 게다가 그건 너무나 즐거우면서도 동시에 너무나 고된 일이라, 그런 걸 생각할 틈이 없거든요.\""]], [["“But how will you do about dining with them?", "\"그런데 그들과 함께 식사하는 건 어떻게 할 작정이냐?"], ["To send you a bottle of Lafitte and roast turkey out there would be a little awkward.”", "너한테 라피트 한 병과 칠면조 구이를 거기까지 보내자면 좀 어색하겠는걸.\""]], [["“No, I’ll simply come home at the time of their noonday rest.”", "\"아니요, 그들이 한낮에 쉴 때 그냥 집으로 돌아오면 돼요.\""]], [["Next morning Konstantin Levin got up earlier than usual, but he was detained giving directions on the farm, and when he reached the mowing grass the mowers were already at their second row.", "이튿날 아침 콘스탄틴 레빈은 평소보다 일찍 일어났지만, 농장 일을 지시하느라 발이 묶였고, 풀 베는 풀밭에 다다랐을 때는 일꾼들이 이미 두 번째 줄을 베고 있었다."]], [["From the uplands he could get a view of the shaded cut part of the meadow below, with its grayish ridges of cut grass, and the black heaps of coats, taken off by the mowers at the place from which they had started cutting.", "높은 지대에서는 아래쪽으로 그늘이 진, 풀이 베어진 풀밭의 한쪽이 내려다보였다. 베어 낸 풀의 잿빛 두둑들과, 일꾼들이 풀을 베기 시작한 자리에 벗어 던진 검은 외투 더미들이 보였다."]], [["Gradually, as he rode towards the meadow, the peasants came into sight, some in coats, some in their shirts mowing, one behind another in a long string, swinging their scythes differently.", "풀밭 쪽으로 말을 몰고 다가가는 동안 차츰 농부들의 모습이 눈에 들어왔다. 어떤 이들은 외투를 입고, 어떤 이들은 셔츠 바람으로 풀을 베며 긴 줄을 이루어 하나씩 뒤따라가면서 저마다 다른 모양새로 낫을 휘두르고 있었다."], ["He counted forty-two of them.", "그는 마흔두 명을 헤아렸다."]], [["They were mowing slowly over the uneven, low-lying parts of the meadow, where there had been an old dam.", "그들은 예전에 둑이 있던, 평탄하지 못한 낮은 풀밭 위에서 천천히 풀을 베어 나가고 있었다."], ["Levin recognized some of his own men.", "레빈은 자기 집 사람 몇몇을 알아보았다."], ["Here was old Yermil in a very long white smock, bending forward to swing a scythe; there was a young fellow, Vaska, who had been a coachman of Levin’s, taking every row with a wide sweep.", "여기에는 아주 긴 흰색 작업복을 입고 몸을 앞으로 숙여 낫을 휘두르는 늙은 예르밀이 있었고, 저기에는 한때 레빈의 마부였던 바스카라는 젊은 친구가 한 줄 한 줄을 큼직하게 후려치며 베고 있었다."], ["Here, too, was Tit, Levin’s preceptor in the art of mowing, a thin little peasant.", "여기에는 또 레빈에게 풀베기 기술을 가르쳐 준 스승 티트가 있었는데, 그는 마르고 자그마한 농부였다."], ["He was in front of all, and cut his wide row without bending, as though playing with the scythe.", "그는 맨 앞에서 마치 낫을 가지고 노는 것처럼 몸을 굽히지도 않고 넓은 줄을 베어 나갔다."]], [["Levin got off his mare, and fastening her up by the roadside went to meet Tit, who took a second scythe out of a bush and gave it to him.", "레빈은 암말에서 내려 길가에 말을 매어 두고 티트를 맞으러 갔다. 티트는 덤불에서 두 번째 낫을 꺼내 그에게 건넸다."]], [["“It’s ready, sir; it’s like a razor, cuts of itself,”", "\"준비됐습니다, 나리. 면도날 같아서 저절로 베어집니다.\""], ["said Tit, taking off his cap with a smile and giving him the scythe.", "티트가 미소를 지으며 모자를 벗고 낫을 건네주며 말했다."]], [["Levin took the scythe, and began trying it.", "레빈은 낫을 받아 들고 시험 삼아 휘둘러 보기 시작했다."], ["As they finished their rows, the mowers, hot and good-humored, came out into the road one after another, and, laughing a little, greeted the master.", "자기 줄을 다 벤 일꾼들이 땀에 젖은 채 기분 좋은 얼굴로 하나씩 길로 나와, 가볍게 웃으며 주인에게 인사를 건넸다."], ["They all stared at him, but no one made any remark, till a tall old man, with a wrinkled, beardless face, wearing a short sheepskin jacket, came out into the road and accosted him.", "그들은 모두 그를 빤히 쳐다보았지만 누구도 아무 말 하지 않았다. 그러다 짧은 양가죽 윗옷을 입은, 주름지고 수염 없는 얼굴의 키 큰 노인이 길로 나와 그에게 말을 걸었다."]], [["“Look’ee now, master, once take hold of the rope there’s no letting it go!”", "\"이것 보십쇼, 나리. 일단 밧줄을 잡았다 하면 놓을 수가 없는 법입니다요!\""], ["he said, and Levin heard smothered laughter among the mowers.", "노인이 말했고, 레빈은 일꾼들 사이에서 새어 나오는 웃음소리를 들었다."]], [["“I’ll try not to let it go,”", "\"놓지 않도록 애써 보겠네.\""], ["he said, taking his stand behind Tit, and waiting for the time to begin.", "레빈은 티트의 뒤에 자리를 잡고 서서, 시작할 때를 기다리며 말했다."]], [["“Mind’ee,”", "\"명심하십쇼.\""], ["repeated the old man.", "노인이 거듭 말했다."]], [["Tit made room, and Levin started behind him.", "티트가 자리를 비켜 주었고, 레빈은 그 뒤에서 시작했다."], ["The grass was short close to the road, and Levin, who had not done any mowing for a long while, and was disconcerted by the eyes fastened upon him, cut badly for the first moments, though he swung his scythe vigorously.", "길에 가까운 곳의 풀은 짧았는데, 오랫동안 풀을 베어 본 적이 없는 데다 자기에게 쏟아지는 시선에 마음이 흐트러진 레빈은, 낫을 힘차게 휘둘렀음에도 처음 얼마간은 서툴게 베었다."], ["Behind him he heard voices:", "등 뒤에서 목소리들이 들려왔다."]], [["“It’s not set right; handle’s too high; see how he has to stoop to it,”", "\"날을 제대로 안 세웠구먼. 자루가 너무 높아."], ["said one.", "저것 봐, 저렇게 몸을 굽혀야 하잖나.\" 한 사람이 말했다."]], [["“Press more on the heel,”", "\"뒤꿈치 쪽을 더 눌러야지.\""], ["said another.", "또 다른 사람이 말했다."]], [["“Never mind, he’ll get on all right,”", "\"괜찮아, 잘하게 될 거야.\""], ["the old man resumed.", "노인이 다시 말을 이었다."]], [["“He’s made a start....", "\"시작은 했구먼……."], ["You swing it too wide, you’ll tire yourself out....", "너무 넓게 휘두르면 금세 지쳐 버려요……."], ["The master, sure, does his best for himself!", "주인 나리도 분명 제 딴엔 애쓰시는 거지!"], ["But see the grass missed out!", "그래도 저기 빠뜨리고 못 벤 풀 좀 보게!"], ["For such work us fellows would catch it!”", "우리 같은 놈들이 저런 식으로 했다간 혼쭐이 났을걸!\""]], [["The grass became softer, and Levin, listening without answering, followed Tit, trying to do the best he could.", "풀이 점점 부드러워졌고, 레빈은 대꾸 없이 귀를 기울이며 티트의 뒤를 따라 최선을 다하려 애썼다."], ["They moved a hundred paces.", "그들은 백 걸음쯤 나아갔다."], ["Tit kept moving on, without stopping, not showing the slightest weariness, but Levin was already beginning to be afraid he would not be able to keep it up: he was so tired.", "티트는 조금도 지친 기색 없이 멈추지 않고 계속 나아갔지만, 레빈은 벌써 버텨 내지 못할까 봐 두려워지기 시작했다. 그만큼 그는 지쳐 있었다."]], [["He felt as he swung his scythe that he was at the very end of his strength, and was making up his mind to ask Tit to stop.", "낫을 휘두르면서 그는 자기 기력이 바닥나 버렸음을 느꼈고, 티트에게 멈춰 달라고 청할 마음을 먹고 있었다."], ["But at that very moment Tit stopped of his own accord, and stooping down picked up some grass, rubbed his scythe, and began whetting it.", "그런데 바로 그 순간 티트가 제 스스로 멈춰서더니, 몸을 굽혀 풀을 한 줌 집어 낫을 문지르고 날을 갈기 시작했다."], ["Levin straightened himself, and drawing a deep breath looked round.", "레빈은 몸을 곧추세우고 깊이 숨을 들이쉬며 주위를 둘러보았다."], ["Behind him came a peasant, and he too was evidently tired, for he stopped at once without waiting to mow up to Levin, and began whetting his scythe.", "그의 뒤에는 한 농부가 따라오고 있었는데, 그 역시 분명 지쳐 있었다. 레빈이 있는 데까지 베어 오기를 기다리지도 않고 그 자리에서 곧장 멈춰 서서 낫을 갈기 시작했기 때문이다."], ["Tit sharpened his scythe and Levin’s, and they went on.", "티트는 자기 낫과 레빈의 낫을 갈았고, 그들은 다시 나아갔다."], ["The next time it was just the same.", "그다음에도 똑같았다."], ["Tit moved on with sweep after sweep of his scythe, not stopping nor showing signs of weariness.", "티트는 멈추지도 지친 기색도 없이 낫을 한 번 한 번 휘두르며 나아갔다."], ["Levin followed him, trying not to get left behind, and he found it harder and harder: the moment came when he felt he had no strength left, but at that very moment Tit stopped and whetted the scythes.", "레빈은 뒤처지지 않으려 애쓰며 그를 따라갔지만, 점점 더 힘에 부쳤다. 더는 기력이 남지 않았다고 느끼는 순간이 찾아왔지만, 바로 그 순간 티트가 멈춰 서서 낫을 갈았다."]], [["So they mowed the first row.", "그렇게 그들은 첫 번째 줄을 베어 냈다."], ["And this long row seemed particularly hard work to Levin; but when the end was reached and Tit, shouldering his scythe, began with deliberate stride returning on the tracks left by his heels in the cut grass, and Levin walked back in the same way over the space he had cut, in spite of the sweat that ran in streams over his face and fell in drops down his nose, and drenched his back as though he had been soaked in water, he felt very happy.", "그리고 이 긴 줄은 레빈에게 유난히 고된 일처럼 여겨졌다. 하지만 끝에 다다라 티트가 낫을 어깨에 걸치고 베어 낸 풀밭에 자기 뒤꿈치가 남긴 자국을 따라 느릿한 걸음으로 되돌아가기 시작했을 때, 그리고 레빈도 자기가 베어 낸 자리를 똑같이 되짚어 걸어가던 그때, 그는 얼굴 위로 줄줄 흘러 코끝에서 방울방울 떨어지는 땀에도, 마치 물에 흠뻑 젖은 듯 등을 적시는 땀에도 아랑곳없이 무척 행복했다."], ["What delighted him particularly was that now he knew he would be able to hold out.", "그를 특히나 기쁘게 한 것은, 이제 자기가 끝까지 버텨 낼 수 있으리라는 것을 알게 되었다는 사실이었다."]], [["His pleasure was only disturbed by his row not being well cut.", "다만 그의 줄이 잘 베어지지 않았다는 점이 그의 즐거움을 흐트러뜨렸다."], ["“I will swing less with my arm and more with my whole body,”", "'팔로 휘두르는 건 줄이고 온몸으로 더 휘둘러야겠어.'"], ["he thought, comparing Tit’s row, which looked as if it had been cut with a line, with his own unevenly and irregularly lying grass.", "그는 줄을 그은 듯 가지런해 보이는 티트의 줄을, 고르지 못하고 들쭉날쭉하게 누워 있는 자기 줄과 견주어 보며 생각했다."]], [["The first row, as Levin noticed, Tit had mowed specially quickly, probably wishing to put his master to the test, and the row happened to be a long one.", "레빈이 알아챈 바로는, 첫 번째 줄을 티트는 유난히 빠르게 베었는데, 아마도 주인을 시험해 보고 싶었던 데다 그 줄이 마침 긴 줄이었기 때문일 것이다."], ["The next rows were easier, but still Levin had to strain every nerve not to drop behind the peasants.", "다음 줄들은 더 수월했지만, 그래도 레빈은 농부들에게 뒤처지지 않으려고 온 신경을 곤두세워야 했다."]], [["He thought of nothing, wished for nothing, but not to be left behind the peasants, and to do his work as well as possible.", "그는 아무것도 생각하지 않았고, 농부들에게 뒤처지지 않는 것과 자기 일을 되도록 잘 해내는 것 외에는 아무것도 바라지 않았다."], ["He heard nothing but the swish of scythes, and saw before him Tit’s upright figure mowing away, the crescent-shaped curve of the cut grass, the grass and flower heads slowly and rhythmically falling before the blade of his scythe, and ahead of him the end of the row, where would come the rest.", "그의 귀에는 낫을 휘두르는 쉭쉭 소리밖에 들리지 않았고, 눈앞에는 풀을 베어 나가는 티트의 꼿꼿한 모습과, 베어 낸 풀이 그리는 초승달 모양의 곡선과, 그의 낫날 앞에서 천천히 율동적으로 쓰러지는 풀과 꽃송이들과, 저 앞에서 쉴 참이 기다리고 있는 줄의 끝이 보일 뿐이었다."]], [["Suddenly, in the midst of his toil, without understanding what it was or whence it came, he felt a pleasant sensation of chill on his hot, moist shoulders.", "문득, 일에 몰두하던 와중에, 그것이 무엇인지 어디서 온 것인지도 모른 채, 그는 뜨겁고 축축한 어깨에 기분 좋은 서늘함을 느꼈다."], ["He glanced at the sky in the interval for whetting the scythes.", "그는 낫을 가는 짬에 하늘을 흘긋 올려다보았다."], ["A heavy, lowering storm cloud had blown up, and big raindrops were falling.", "무겁게 내리깔린 먹구름이 몰려와 굵은 빗방울이 떨어지고 있었다."], ["Some of the peasants went to their coats and put them on; others—just like Levin himself—merely shrugged their shoulders, enjoying the pleasant coolness of it.", "몇몇 농부들은 외투가 있는 데로 가서 그것을 걸쳤고, 또 어떤 이들은—바로 레빈 자신처럼—그저 어깨를 으쓱하며 그 기분 좋은 서늘함을 즐겼다."]], [["Another row, and yet another row, followed—long rows and short rows, with good grass and with poor grass.", "또 한 줄, 그리고 또 한 줄이 이어졌다. 긴 줄과 짧은 줄, 좋은 풀과 시원찮은 풀이 번갈아 나왔다."], ["Levin lost all sense of time, and could not have told whether it was late or early now.", "레빈은 시간 감각을 완전히 잃어버려, 지금이 늦은 시각인지 이른 시각인지조차 알 수 없었다."], ["A change began to come over his work, which gave him immense satisfaction.", "그의 일에 변화가 일기 시작했고, 그것이 그에게 엄청난 만족감을 주었다."], ["In the midst of his toil there were moments during which he forgot what he was doing, and it came all easy to him, and at those same moments his row was almost as smooth and well cut as Tit’s.", "일에 몰두하던 가운데, 자기가 무엇을 하고 있는지 잊어버리는 순간들이 있었는데, 그럴 때는 모든 것이 수월하게 풀렸고, 바로 그 순간들에 그의 줄은 티트의 줄에 못지않게 매끄럽고 가지런하게 베어졌다."], ["But so soon as he recollected what he was doing, and began trying to do better, he was at once conscious of all the difficulty of his task, and the row was badly mown.", "하지만 자기가 무엇을 하고 있는지 떠올리고 더 잘하려고 애쓰기 시작하자마자, 그는 곧바로 자기 일의 온갖 어려움을 의식하게 되었고, 그러면 줄은 엉망으로 베어졌다."]], [["On finishing yet another row he would have gone back to the top of the meadow again to begin the next, but Tit stopped, and going up to the old man said something in a low voice to him.", "또 한 줄을 다 베고서 그는 다음 줄을 시작하러 다시 풀밭 위쪽으로 돌아가려 했지만, 티트가 멈춰 서더니 노인에게 다가가 나직한 목소리로 무언가 말했다. 두 사람은 해를 올려다보았다."], ["They both looked at the sun. “What are they talking about, and why doesn’t he go back?”", "'저들은 무슨 이야기를 하고 있는 거지, 그리고 저 사람은 왜 돌아가지 않는 걸까?'"], ["thought Levin, not guessing that the peasants had been mowing no less than four hours without stopping, and it was time for their lunch.", "농부들이 쉬지 않고 네 시간 가까이 풀을 베어 왔고 이제 점심을 먹을 때라는 것은 짐작도 못 한 채 레빈은 생각했다."]], [["“Lunch, sir,”", "\"점심 드실 때입니다, 나리.\""], ["said the old man.", "노인이 말했다."]], [["“Is it really time?", "\"벌써 그럴 때가 됐나?"], ["That’s right; lunch, then.”", "그래, 그럼 점심을 먹지.\""]], [["Levin gave his scythe to Tit, and together with the peasants, who were crossing the long stretch of mown grass, slightly sprinkled with rain, to get their bread from the heap of coats, he went towards his house.", "레빈은 자기 낫을 티트에게 건네고, 외투 더미에서 빵을 가지러 비에 살짝 적셔진 길게 베어 낸 풀밭을 가로질러 가는 농부들과 함께 자기 집 쪽으로 걸어갔다."], ["Only then he suddenly awoke to the fact that he had been wrong about the weather and the rain was drenching his hay.", "그제야 그는 자기가 날씨를 잘못 짚었으며 비가 자기 건초를 흠뻑 적시고 있다는 사실을 불현듯 깨달았다."]], [["“The hay will be spoiled,”", "\"건초가 못 쓰게 되겠어.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“Not a bit of it, sir; mow in the rain, and you’ll rake in fine weather!”", "\"천만에요, 나리. 비 올 때 풀을 베면 날 갠 뒤에 긁어모으는 법입죠!\""], ["said the old man.", "노인이 말했다."]], [["Levin untied his horse and rode home to his coffee.", "레빈은 말을 풀어 커피를 마시러 집으로 말을 몰았다."], ["Sergey Ivanovitch was only just getting up.", "세르게이 이바노비치는 이제 막 일어나는 참이었다."], ["When he had drunk his coffee, Levin rode back again to the mowing before Sergey Ivanovitch had had time to dress and come down to the dining-room.", "레빈은 커피를 다 마시고서, 세르게이 이바노비치가 옷을 차려입고 식당으로 내려올 새도 없이 다시 풀밭으로 말을 달려 돌아갔다."]], [["# Chapter 5", "# Chapter 5"]], [["After lunch Levin was not in the same place in the string of mowers as before, but stood between the old man who had accosted him jocosely, and now invited him to be his neighbor, and a young peasant, who had only been married in the autumn, and who was mowing this summer for the first time.", "점심을 먹은 뒤 레빈은 풀 베는 사람들의 줄에서 아까와 같은 자리에 서지 않고, 농담을 건네며 자기 옆자리로 와 달라고 청한 노인과, 가을에야 막 장가를 들어 이번 여름 처음으로 풀을 베는 젊은 농부 사이에 섰다."]], [["The old man, holding himself erect, moved in front, with his feet turned out, taking long, regular strides, and with a precise and regular action which seemed to cost him no more effort than swinging one’s arms in walking, as though it were in play, he laid down the high, even row of grass.", "노인은 몸을 꼿꼿이 세운 채 발끝을 바깥으로 벌리고 앞으로 나아가며, 길고 고른 걸음을 내디뎠다. 그러고는 마치 걸을 때 팔을 흔드는 것 이상으로는 아무런 힘도 들지 않는다는 듯, 그저 놀이라도 하는 양 정확하고 규칙적인 동작으로 높고 가지런한 풀 줄을 눕혀 나갔다."], ["It was as though it were not he but the sharp scythe of itself swishing through the juicy grass.", "마치 그가 아니라 날 선 낫이 제 스스로 물기 가득한 풀 사이를 쓱쓱 베어 나가는 것 같았다."]], [["Behind Levin came the lad Mishka.", "레빈의 뒤에는 미시카라는 젊은이가 따라왔다."], ["His pretty, boyish face, with a twist of fresh grass bound round his hair, was all working with effort; but whenever anyone looked at him he smiled.", "갓 벤 풀을 한 가닥 머리에 둘러 묶은 그의 귀엽고 앳된 얼굴은 온통 안간힘으로 일그러져 있었다. 그러나 누군가 자기를 쳐다보기만 하면 그는 빙긋 웃었다."], ["He would clearly have died sooner than own it was hard work for him.", "일이 힘들다고 인정하느니 차라리 죽는 편을 택할 게 분명했다."]], [["Levin kept between them.", "레빈은 두 사람 사이를 지켰다."], ["In the very heat of the day the mowing did not seem such hard work to him.", "한낮의 가장 뜨거운 시각인데도 풀베기가 그에게 그리 고된 일로 느껴지지는 않았다."], ["The perspiration with which he was drenched cooled him, while the sun, that burned his back, his head, and his arms, bare to the elbow, gave a vigor and dogged energy to his labor; and more and more often now came those moments of unconsciousness, when it was possible not to think what one was doing.", "온몸을 흠뻑 적신 땀이 그를 식혀 주었고, 등과 머리와 팔꿈치까지 드러난 두 팔을 태우는 햇살은 그의 노동에 활력과 끈질긴 기운을 불어넣었다. 그리고 이제는 자기가 무엇을 하고 있는지 생각하지 않아도 되는 무의식의 순간이 점점 더 자주 찾아왔다."], ["The scythe cut of itself.", "낫은 저절로 베어 나갔다."], ["These were happy moments.", "그것은 행복한 순간이었다."], ["Still more delightful were the moments when they reached the stream where the rows ended, and the old man rubbed his scythe with the wet, thick grass, rinsed its blade in the fresh water of the stream, ladled out a little in a tin dipper, and offered Levin a drink.", "더욱 즐거운 것은 풀 줄이 끝나는 개울가에 다다랐을 때였다. 노인은 젖은 두꺼운 풀로 낫을 문질러 닦고, 개울의 맑은 물에 칼날을 헹구고는, 양철 국자로 물을 조금 떠서 레빈에게 마시라고 권했다."]], [["“What do you say to my home-brew, eh?", "\"내가 직접 빚은 술 맛이 어떤가, 응?"], ["Good, eh?”", "좋지, 응?\""], ["said he, winking.", "그가 눈을 찡긋하며 말했다."]], [["And truly Levin had never drunk any liquor so good as this warm water with green bits floating in it, and a taste of rust from the tin dipper.", "그리고 실제로 레빈은 푸르스름한 부스러기가 둥둥 떠 있고 양철 국자의 쇠 냄새가 배어든 이 미지근한 물보다 더 좋은 술을 마셔 본 적이 없었다."], ["And immediately after this came the delicious, slow saunter, with his hand on the scythe, during which he could wipe away the streaming sweat, take deep breaths of air, and look about at the long string of mowers and at what was happening around in the forest and the country.", "그러고 나면 곧바로 낫에 손을 얹은 채 천천히 거니는 더없이 달콤한 시간이 이어졌다. 그동안 그는 줄줄 흐르는 땀을 닦아 내고, 깊이 숨을 들이쉬며, 길게 늘어선 풀 베는 사람들과 숲과 들판에서 벌어지는 온갖 광경을 둘러볼 수 있었다."]], [["The longer Levin mowed, the oftener he felt the moments of unconsciousness in which it seemed not his hands that swung the scythe, but the scythe mowing of itself, a body full of life and consciousness of its own, and as though by magic, without thinking of it, the work turned out regular and well-finished of itself.", "레빈이 풀을 오래 벨수록 무의식의 순간은 더 자주 찾아왔다. 그럴 때면 낫을 휘두르는 것은 그의 두 손이 아니라, 제 나름의 생명과 의식으로 가득 찬 낫 자신이 저절로 풀을 베어 나가는 것 같았다."], ["These were the most blissful moments.", "그리고 마치 마법이라도 부린 듯, 일에 대해 생각하지 않는데도 작업은 절로 고르고 깔끔하게 마무리되었다. 그것이 가장 더없이 행복한 순간이었다."]], [["It was only hard work when he had to break off the motion, which had become unconscious, and to think; when he had to mow round a hillock or a tuft of sorrel.", "힘든 것은 오직 그 무의식적으로 된 동작을 끊고 생각을 해야 할 때, 그러니까 작은 흙무더기나 수영 풀숲을 빙 돌아가며 베어야 할 때뿐이었다."], ["The old man did this easily.", "노인은 이런 일을 손쉽게 해냈다."], ["When a hillock came he changed his action, and at one time with the heel, and at another with the tip of his scythe, clipped the hillock round both sides with short strokes.", "흙무더기를 만나면 그는 동작을 바꾸어, 어떤 때는 낫의 뒷부분으로, 또 어떤 때는 낫끝으로, 짧은 동작으로 흙무더기 양쪽을 둘러 가며 깎아 냈다. 그러면서도 그는 계속 주위를 둘러보며 눈에 들어오는 것들을 살폈다."], ["And while he did this he kept looking about and watching what came into his view: at one moment he picked a wild berry and ate it or offered it to Levin, then he flung away a twig with the blade of the scythe, then he looked at a quail’s nest, from which the bird flew just under the scythe, or caught a snake that crossed his path, and lifting it on the scythe as though on a fork showed it to Levin and threw it away.", "어느 순간에는 산딸기를 따서 자기가 먹거나 레빈에게 권했고, 어느 때는 낫날로 나뭇가지를 휙 쳐 내고, 또 어느 때는 메추라기 둥지를 들여다보았는데 거기서 새가 낫 바로 아래로 푸드덕 날아올랐다. 길을 가로지르는 뱀을 잡아 마치 작살에 꿰듯 낫에 들어 올려 레빈에게 보여 주고는 멀리 던져 버리기도 했다."]], [["For both Levin and the young peasant behind him, such changes of position were difficult.", "레빈에게도, 그의 뒤를 따르는 젊은 농부에게도 그런 자세의 변화는 어려웠다."], ["Both of them, repeating over and over again the same strained movement, were in a perfect frenzy of toil, and were incapable of shifting their position and at the same time watching what was before them.", "두 사람 다 같은 긴장된 동작을 거듭하고 또 거듭하면서 완전히 노동의 광란에 빠져 있었기에, 자세를 바꾸는 동시에 눈앞에서 벌어지는 일을 살피는 것은 도무지 할 수 없었다."]], [["Levin did not notice how time was passing.", "레빈은 시간이 어떻게 흘러가는지 알아차리지 못했다."], ["If he had been asked how long he had been working he would have said half an hour—and it was getting on for dinner time.", "만약 누군가 그에게 얼마나 오래 일했느냐고 물었다면, 그는 반 시간쯤이라고 대답했을 것이다. 그런데 어느새 저녁 식사 때가 가까워지고 있었다."], ["As they were walking back over the cut grass, the old man called Levin’s attention to the little girls and boys who were coming from different directions, hardly visible through the long grass, and along the road towards the mowers, carrying sacks of bread dragging at their little hands and pitchers of the sour rye-beer, with cloths wrapped round them.", "베어 낸 풀 위를 걸어 돌아오는 길에, 노인은 사방에서 다가오는 어린 계집아이들과 사내아이들에게 레빈의 주의를 돌렸다. 그 아이들은 키 큰 풀 사이로 거의 보이지 않을 만큼 작은 몸으로, 길을 따라 풀 베는 사람들 쪽으로 오고 있었는데, 작은 손에 빵 자루를 질질 끌고 천으로 둘둘 감싼 시큼한 호밀 맥주 단지를 들고 있었다."]], [["“Look’ee, the little emmets crawling!”", "\"저것 좀 보게, 꼬마 개미들이 기어 오는구먼!\""], ["he said, pointing to them, and he shaded his eyes with his hand to look at the sun.", "그가 아이들을 가리키며 말하고는, 손으로 눈 위를 가린 채 해를 올려다보았다."], ["They mowed two more rows; the old man stopped.", "그들은 두 줄을 더 베었다. 그러자 노인이 멈춰 섰다."]], [["“Come, master, dinner time!”", "\"자, 나리, 저녁 먹을 때요!\""], ["he said briskly.", "그가 활기차게 말했다."], ["And on reaching the stream the mowers moved off across the lines of cut grass towards their pile of coats, where the children who had brought their dinners were sitting waiting for them.", "개울에 다다르자 풀 베는 사람들은 베어 낸 풀 줄을 가로질러 자기들 외투를 쌓아 둔 곳으로 옮겨 갔는데, 거기서는 저녁밥을 가져온 아이들이 앉아 그들을 기다리고 있었다. 농부들은 무리를 지어 모였다."], ["The peasants gathered into groups—those further away under a cart, those nearer under a willow bush.", "멀리 있는 사람들은 짐수레 아래에, 가까이 있는 사람들은 버드나무 덤불 아래에 자리를 잡았다."]], [["Levin sat down by them; he felt disinclined to go away.", "레빈은 그들 곁에 앉았다. 그는 자리를 뜨고 싶지 않았다."]], [["All constraint with the master had disappeared long ago.", "나리 앞이라는 거북함은 진작에 사라지고 없었다."], ["The peasants got ready for dinner.", "농부들은 저녁 먹을 채비를 했다."], ["Some washed, the young lads bathed in the stream, others made a place comfortable for a rest, untied their sacks of bread, and uncovered the pitchers of rye-beer.", "어떤 이들은 몸을 씻었고, 젊은 녀석들은 개울에서 멱을 감았으며, 다른 이들은 쉬기 좋게 자리를 만들고 빵 자루를 풀어 호밀 맥주 단지의 덮개를 벗겼다."], ["The old man crumbled up some bread in a cup, stirred it with the handle of a spoon, poured water on it from the dipper, broke up some more bread, and having seasoned it with salt, he turned to the east to say his prayer.", "노인은 빵을 잘게 부숴 컵에 담고 숟가락 자루로 휘저은 다음, 국자로 물을 부어 빵을 좀 더 부수고 소금으로 간을 한 뒤, 동쪽을 향해 기도를 드렸다."]], [["“Come, master, taste my sop,”", "\"자, 나리, 내가 만든 빵죽을 맛보시오.\""], ["said he, kneeling down before the cup.", "그가 컵 앞에 무릎을 꿇으며 말했다."]], [["The sop was so good that Levin gave up the idea of going home.", "빵죽이 어찌나 맛있던지 레빈은 집으로 돌아갈 생각을 접었다."], ["He dined with the old man, and talked to him about his family affairs, taking the keenest interest in them, and told him about his own affairs and all the circumstances that could be of interest to the old man.", "그는 노인과 함께 저녁을 먹으며 노인의 집안일에 대해 더없이 깊은 관심을 가지고 이야기를 나누었고, 자기 자신의 일이며 노인이 흥미로워할 만한 온갖 사정도 들려주었다."], ["He felt much nearer to him than to his brother, and could not help smiling at the affection he felt for this man.", "그는 자기 형보다도 이 노인에게 훨씬 더 가까움을 느꼈고, 이 사람에게 품은 애정에 절로 미소 짓지 않을 수 없었다."], ["When the old man got up again, said his prayer, and lay down under a bush, putting some grass under his head for a pillow, Levin did the same, and in spite of the clinging flies that were so persistent in the sunshine, and the midges that tickled his hot face and body, he fell asleep at once and only waked when the sun had passed to the other side of the bush and reached him.", "노인이 다시 일어나 기도를 드린 뒤 풀을 베개 삼아 머리에 받치고 덤불 아래 드러눕자, 레빈도 똑같이 했다. 그러고는 햇볕 속에서 그토록 끈질기게 들러붙는 파리떼와 달아오른 얼굴과 몸을 간질이는 각다귀들에도 아랑곳없이 곧바로 잠이 들었고, 해가 덤불 반대편으로 넘어가 그에게 닿았을 때에야 비로소 깨어났다."], ["The old man had been awake a long while, and was sitting up whetting the scythes of the younger lads.", "노인은 진작에 깨어나 일어나 앉은 채 젊은 녀석들의 낫을 갈고 있었다."]], [["Levin looked about him and hardly recognized the place, everything was so changed.", "레빈은 주위를 둘러보았으나 그 장소를 좀처럼 알아보지 못했다."], ["The immense stretch of meadow had been mown and was sparkling with a peculiar fresh brilliance, with its lines of already sweet-smelling grass in the slanting rays of the evening sun.", "모든 것이 너무도 달라져 있었던 것이다. 드넓게 펼쳐진 풀밭은 모조리 베어져, 저무는 저녁 햇살의 비스듬한 빛 속에서 벌써 향긋한 내음을 풍기는 풀 줄들이 늘어선 채 유난히 싱그러운 광채로 반짝였다."], ["And the bushes about the river had been cut down, and the river itself, not visible before, now gleaming like steel in its bends, and the moving, ascending, peasants, and the sharp wall of grass of the unmown part of the meadow, and the hawks hovering over the stripped meadow—all was perfectly new.", "강가의 덤불들은 베여 나갔고, 전에는 보이지 않던 강 자체가 이제 굽이굽이 강철처럼 번득였으며, 움직이며 비탈을 올라오는 농부들과, 아직 베지 않은 풀밭 부분의 날 선 풀 벽과, 헐벗은 풀밭 위를 맴도는 매들까지—모든 것이 완전히 새로웠다."], ["Raising himself, Levin began considering how much had been cut and how much more could still be done that day.", "몸을 일으킨 레빈은 얼마나 베었는지, 그리고 오늘 안에 얼마나 더 할 수 있는지를 헤아리기 시작했다."]], [["The work done was exceptionally much for forty-two men.", "마흔두 사람이 해낸 일치고는 유난히 많았다."], ["They had cut the whole of the big meadow, which had, in the years of serf labor, taken thirty scythes two days to mow.", "그들은 큰 풀밭을 통째로 베어 냈는데, 농노 노동 시절에는 낫 서른 자루로 이틀이 걸리던 일이었다."], ["Only the corners remained to do, where the rows were short.", "이제 줄이 짧은 모퉁이들만 남아 있었다."], ["But Levin felt a longing to get as much mowing done that day as possible, and was vexed with the sun sinking so quickly in the sky.", "그러나 레빈은 오늘 안에 되도록 많은 풀을 베고 싶다는 갈망을 느꼈고, 하늘에서 너무도 빨리 가라앉는 해가 야속하기만 했다."], ["He felt no weariness; all he wanted was to get his work done more and more quickly and as much done as possible.", "그는 조금도 피곤하지 않았다. 오직 자기 일을 점점 더 빨리, 되도록 많이 해내고 싶을 뿐이었다."]], [["“Could you cut Mashkin Upland too?—what do you think?”", "\"마시킨 고지대도 벨 수 있을까요? 어떻게 생각하세요?\""], ["he said to the old man.", "그가 노인에게 물었다."]], [["“As God wills, the sun’s not high.", "\"하느님 뜻대로지요. 해가 아직 안 졌으니."], ["A little vodka for the lads?”", "녀석들한테 보드카나 좀 줄까요?\""]], [["At the afternoon rest, when they were sitting down again, and those who smoked had lighted their pipes, the old man told the men that “Mashkin Upland’s to be cut—there’ll be some vodka.”", "오후 휴식 때 그들이 다시 자리에 앉고, 담배 피우는 자들이 파이프에 불을 붙이자, 노인은 사람들에게 \"마시킨 고지대를 벨 거라네. 그러면 보드카가 좀 나올 게야.\" 하고 일러 주었다."]], [["“Why not cut it?", "\"안 벨 게 뭐 있나?"], ["Come on, Tit!", "가세, 티트!"], ["We’ll look sharp!", "후딱 해치우자고!"], ["We can eat at night.", "밥은 밤에 먹으면 되지."], ["Come on!”", "가세!\""], ["cried voices, and eating up their bread, the mowers went back to work.", "여기저기서 목소리가 터져 나왔고, 풀 베는 사람들은 빵을 다 먹어 치우고 다시 일하러 갔다."]], [["“Come, lads, keep it up!”", "\"자, 다들, 힘내세!\""], ["said Tit, and ran on ahead almost at a trot.", "티트가 말하고는 거의 종종걸음으로 앞장서 달려 나갔다."]], [["“Get along, get along!”", "\"가자고, 가자고!\""], ["said the old man, hurrying after him and easily overtaking him, “I’ll mow you down, look out!”", "노인이 그의 뒤를 서둘러 좇으며 손쉽게 따라잡고는 말했다. \"내가 자네를 베어 넘길 테니, 조심하라고!\""]], [["And young and old mowed away, as though they were racing with one another.", "그리고 젊은이도 늙은이도 마치 서로 경주라도 하듯 풀을 베어 나갔다."], ["But however fast they worked, they did not spoil the grass, and the rows were laid just as neatly and exactly.", "그러나 아무리 빨리 일해도 그들은 풀을 망치지 않았고, 풀 줄은 한결같이 깔끔하고 정확하게 눕혀졌다."], ["The little piece left uncut in the corner was mown in five minutes.", "모퉁이에 베지 않고 남은 작은 부분은 오 분 만에 베어졌다."], ["The last of the mowers were just ending their rows while the foremost snatched up their coats onto their shoulders, and crossed the road towards Mashkin Upland.", "맨 뒤의 사람들이 막 자기 줄을 끝낼 때쯤, 맨 앞의 사람들은 벌써 외투를 어깨에 둘러메고 길을 가로질러 마시킨 고지대 쪽으로 향했다."]], [["The sun was already sinking into the trees when they went with their jingling dippers into the wooded ravine of Mashkin Upland.", "그들이 국자를 절렁이며 마시킨 고지대의 우거진 골짜기로 들어섰을 때, 해는 이미 나무들 사이로 가라앉고 있었다."], ["The grass was up to their waists in the middle of the hollow, soft, tender, and feathery, spotted here and there among the trees with wild heart’s-ease.", "골짜기 한가운데에서는 풀이 허리까지 차올랐는데, 부드럽고 연하며 깃털처럼 보드라운 그 풀밭에는 나무들 사이로 여기저기 들제비꽃이 점점이 박혀 있었다."]], [["After a brief consultation—whether to take the rows lengthwise or diagonally—Prohor Yermilin, also a renowned mower, a huge, black-haired peasant, went on ahead.", "길게 줄을 잡을지 비스듬히 잡을지 잠깐 의논한 뒤, 역시 이름난 풀꾼인 프로호르 예르밀린이라는 덩치 크고 머리 검은 농부가 앞장섰다."], ["He went up to the top, turned back again and started mowing, and they all proceeded to form in line behind him, going downhill through the hollow and uphill right up to the edge of the forest.", "그는 꼭대기까지 올라가 다시 돌아서서 풀을 베기 시작했고, 모두가 그의 뒤로 줄을 지어 골짜기 아래로 내려갔다가 숲 가장자리까지 비탈을 올라갔다."], ["The sun sank behind the forest.", "해가 숲 너머로 가라앉았다."], ["The dew was falling by now; the mowers were in the sun only on the hillside, but below, where a mist was rising, and on the opposite side, they mowed into the fresh, dewy shade.", "어느새 이슬이 내리고 있었다. 풀 베는 사람들은 비탈에서만 햇빛 속에 있었고, 안개가 피어오르는 아래쪽과 맞은편 비탈에서는 싱그러운 이슬 그늘 속으로 베어 들어갔다."], ["The work went rapidly.", "일은 빠르게 진척되었다."], ["The grass cut with a juicy sound, and was at once laid in high, fragrant rows.", "풀은 물기 어린 소리를 내며 베여 곧바로 높고 향긋한 줄로 눕혀졌다."], ["The mowers from all sides, brought closer together in the short row, kept urging one another on to the sound of jingling dippers and clanging scythes, and the hiss of the whetstones sharpening them, and good-humored shouts.", "짧은 줄에 더 바싹 모여든 사방의 풀꾼들은 절렁이는 국자 소리와 쟁쟁거리는 낫 소리, 낫을 가는 숫돌의 쉭쉭 소리, 그리고 흥겨운 외침 속에서 서로를 부추겨 댔다."]], [["Levin still kept between the young peasant and the old man.", "레빈은 여전히 젊은 농부와 노인 사이를 지켰다."], ["The old man, who had put on his short sheepskin jacket, was just as good-humored, jocose, and free in his movements.", "짧은 양가죽 윗도리를 걸친 노인은 한결같이 흥겹고 익살스러우며 동작이 거침없었다."], ["Among the trees they were continually cutting with their scythes the so-called “birch mushrooms,” swollen fat in the succulent grass.", "나무들 사이에서 그들은 물기 많은 풀 속에 통통하게 부풀어 오른 이른바 \"자작나무버섯\"을 낫으로 줄곧 베어 냈다."], ["But the old man bent down every time he came across a mushroom, picked it up and put it in his bosom.", "그러나 노인은 버섯을 만날 때마다 매번 허리를 굽혀 그것을 주워 품에 넣었다."], ["“Another present for my old woman,”", "\"우리 마누라한테 줄 또 하나의 선물이지.\""], ["he said as he did so.", "그는 그러면서 말했다."]], [["Easy as it was to mow the wet, soft grass, it was hard work going up and down the steep sides of the ravine.", "젖어서 부드러운 풀을 베기는 쉬웠지만 골짜기의 가파른 비탈을 오르내리는 것은 고된 일이었다."], ["But this did not trouble the old man.", "그러나 이것이 노인을 괴롭히지는 못했다."], ["Swinging his scythe just as ever, and moving his feet in their big, plaited shoes with firm, little steps, he climbed slowly up the steep place, and though his breeches hanging out below his smock, and his whole frame trembled with effort, he did not miss one blade of grass or one mushroom on his way, and kept making jokes with the peasants and Levin.", "그는 여전히 낫을 휘두르며 크고 엮어 만든 신발을 신은 발을 단단하고 잰걸음으로 옮기면서 가파른 곳을 천천히 올라갔다. 작업복 아래로 비져 나온 바지며 온몸이 안간힘으로 떨렸지만, 그는 가는 길에 풀 한 포기, 버섯 하나도 놓치지 않았고, 농부들과 레빈에게 계속 농담을 건넸다."], ["Levin walked after him and often thought he must fall, as he climbed with a scythe up a steep cliff where it would have been hard work to clamber without anything.", "레빈은 그의 뒤를 따라가며, 맨몸으로도 기어오르기 힘들 가파른 벼랑을 낫까지 들고 오르는 노인이 곧 굴러떨어질 것만 같다고 몇 번이나 생각했다."], ["But he climbed up and did what he had to do.", "그러나 노인은 끝까지 기어올라 자기 할 일을 해냈다."], ["He felt as though some external force were moving him.", "마치 어떤 외부의 힘이 그를 움직이고 있는 것만 같았다."]], [["# Chapter 6", "# Chapter 6"]], [["Mashkin Upland was mown, the last row finished, the peasants had put on their coats and were gaily trudging home.", "마시킨 풀밭은 깎였고, 마지막 줄까지 끝나자 농부들은 외투를 걸쳐 입고 흥겹게 터벅터벅 집으로 향하고 있었다."], ["Levin got on his horse and, parting regretfully from the peasants, rode homewards.", "레빈은 말에 올라, 농부들과 아쉬운 작별을 하고 집을 향해 말을 몰았다. 언덕배기에서 그는 뒤를 돌아보았다."], ["On the hillside he looked back; he could not see them in the mist that had risen from the valley; he could only hear rough, good-humored voices, laughter, and the sound of clanking scythes.", "골짜기에서 피어오른 안개 속에서 그들은 보이지 않았다. 다만 거칠고 유쾌한 목소리며, 웃음소리며, 낫이 부딪치는 소리만 들려올 따름이었다."]], [["Sergey Ivanovitch had long ago finished dinner, and was drinking iced lemon and water in his own room, looking through the reviews and papers which he had only just received by post, when Levin rushed into the room, talking merrily, with his wet and matted hair sticking to his forehead, and his back and chest grimed and moist.", "세르게이 이바노비치는 진작 식사를 마치고, 자기 방에서 얼음을 띄운 레몬수를 마시며 우편으로 방금 받은 평론지와 신문을 훑어보고 있었다. 그때 레빈이 즐겁게 떠들며 방으로 뛰어들었는데, 젖어서 엉겨 붙은 머리카락이 이마에 들러붙어 있었고, 등과 가슴은 땀에 절어 거뭇거뭇했다."]], [["“We mowed the whole meadow!", "\"풀밭을 통째로 다 깎았어!"], ["Oh, it is nice, delicious!", "아, 좋아, 정말 끝내준다고!"], ["And how have you been getting on?”", "형은 어떻게 지냈어?\""], ["said Levin, completely forgetting the disagreeable conversation of the previous day.", "레빈은 전날의 그 거북한 대화 따위는 까맣게 잊고 말했다."]], [["“Mercy!", "\"맙소사!"], ["what do you look like!”", "그 꼴이 뭐냐!\""], ["said Sergey Ivanovitch, for the first moment looking round with some dissatisfaction.", "세르게이 이바노비치가 처음 순간에는 다소 못마땅한 얼굴로 둘러보며 말했다."], ["“And the door, do shut the door!”", "\"그리고 문, 문 좀 닫아!\""], ["he cried.", "그가 소리쳤다."], ["“You must have let in a dozen at least.”", "\"적어도 열댓 마리는 들여보냈을 게다.\""]], [["Sergey Ivanovitch could not endure flies, and in his own room he never opened the window except at night, and carefully kept the door shut.", "세르게이 이바노비치는 파리를 견디지 못했고, 자기 방에서는 밤이 아니면 결코 창문을 열지 않았으며, 문도 늘 조심스럽게 닫아 두었다."]], [["“Not one, on my honor.", "\"한 마리도 안 들어왔어, 정말로."], ["But if I have, I’ll catch them.", "만약 들어왔으면 내가 다 잡을게."], ["You wouldn’t believe what a pleasure it is!", "형은 이게 얼마나 즐거운 일인지 믿지 못할 거야!"], ["How have you spent the day?”", "형은 하루를 어떻게 보냈어?\""]], [["“Very well.", "\"아주 잘 보냈지."], ["But have you really been mowing the whole day?", "그런데 정말 하루 종일 풀을 벴단 말이냐?"], ["I expect you’re as hungry as a wolf.", "늑대처럼 배가 고프겠구나."], ["Kouzma has got everything ready for you.”", "쿠즈마가 네 식사를 다 차려 놨다.\""]], [["“No, I don’t feel hungry even.", "\"아니, 배고픈 줄도 모르겠어."], ["I had something to eat there.", "거기서 뭘 좀 먹었거든."], ["But I’ll go and wash.”", "그래도 일단 가서 씻어야겠어.\""]], [["“Yes, go along, go along, and I’ll come to you directly,”", "\"그래, 가거라, 가, 나도 곧 갈 테니.\""], ["said Sergey Ivanovitch, shaking his head as he looked at his brother.", "세르게이 이바노비치가 동생을 바라보며 고개를 절레절레 흔들고 말했다."], ["“Go along, make haste,”", "\"어서 가, 서둘러.\""], ["he added smiling, and gathering up his books, he prepared to go too.", "그는 미소를 지으며 덧붙이고는, 책들을 챙겨 들고 자기도 일어날 채비를 했다."], ["He, too, felt suddenly good-humored and disinclined to leave his brother’s side.", "그 역시 별안간 기분이 좋아지면서 동생 곁을 떠나기가 싫어졌던 것이다."], ["“But what did you do while it was raining?”", "\"그런데 비가 올 때는 어떻게 했어?\""]], [["“Rain?", "\"비?"], ["Why, there was scarcely a drop.", "아니, 거의 한 방울도 안 왔는걸."], ["I’ll come directly.", "곧 올게."], ["So you had a nice day too?", "그래, 형도 좋은 하루를 보냈다고?"], ["That’s first-rate.”", "그거 잘됐네.\""], ["And Levin went off to change his clothes.", "그러고 레빈은 옷을 갈아입으러 가 버렸다."]], [["Five minutes later the brothers met in the dining-room.", "오 분 뒤 형제는 식당에서 만났다."], ["Although it seemed to Levin that he was not hungry, and he sat down to dinner simply so as not to hurt Kouzma’s feelings, yet when he began to eat the dinner struck him as extraordinarily good.", "레빈은 자기가 배고프지 않은 것 같았고, 그저 쿠즈마의 마음을 상하게 하지 않으려고 식탁 앞에 앉았다. 그런데 막상 먹기 시작하자 음식이 기막히게 맛있게 느껴졌다."], ["Sergey Ivanovitch watched him with a smile.", "세르게이 이바노비치는 미소를 띤 채 그를 지켜보았다."]], [["“Oh, by the way, there’s a letter for you,”", "\"아, 참, 너한테 온 편지가 있다.\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["“Kouzma, bring it down, please.", "\"쿠즈마, 그것 좀 가져오게."], ["And mind you shut the doors.”", "그리고 문 닫는 거 잊지 말고.\""]], [["The letter was from Oblonsky.", "편지는 오블론스키에게서 온 것이었다."], ["Levin read it aloud.", "레빈은 그것을 소리 내어 읽었다."], ["Oblonsky wrote to him from Petersburg: “I have had a letter from Dolly; she’s at Ergushovo, and everything seems going wrong there.", "오블론스키는 페테르부르크에서 그에게 이렇게 써 보냈다. \"돌리에게서 편지를 받았네."], ["Do ride over and see her, please; help her with advice; you know all about it.", "그 사람은 지금 에르구쇼보에 있는데, 거기 일이 죄다 엉망인 모양이야. 제발 한번 가서 그녀를 좀 봐 주게나."], ["She will be so glad to see you.", "조언을 해 줘. 자넨 그쪽 일을 다 알지 않나."], ["She’s quite alone, poor thing.", "자넬 보면 그 사람도 정말 기뻐할 걸세."], ["My mother-in-law and all of them are still abroad.”", "가엾게도 혼자 외톨이로 있다네. 장모님이며 다른 식구들은 아직 다 외국에 있고.\""]], [["“That’s capital!", "\"이거 잘됐어!"], ["I will certainly ride over to her,”", "꼭 그녀에게 다녀와야겠어.\""], ["said Levin.", "레빈이 말했다."], ["“Or we’ll go together.", "\"아니면 같이 가자고."], ["She’s such a splendid woman, isn’t she?”", "정말 훌륭한 여자야, 안 그래?\""]], [["“They’re not far from here, then?”", "\"그럼 여기서 멀지는 않은 거냐?\""]], [["“Twenty-five miles.", "\"이십오 베르스타쯤."], ["Or perhaps it is thirty.", "어쩌면 삼십 베르스타일지도."], ["But a capital road.", "그래도 길이 아주 좋아."], ["Capital, we’ll drive over.”", "끝내주지, 마차로 가면 돼.\""]], [["“I shall be delighted,”", "\"나도 기꺼이 가마.\""], ["said Sergey Ivanovitch, still smiling.", "세르게이 이바노비치가 여전히 미소를 지으며 말했다."], ["The sight of his younger brother’s appearance had immediately put him in a good humor.", "동생의 모습을 보자 단번에 기분이 좋아졌던 것이다."]], [["“Well, you have an appetite!”", "\"이야, 식욕이 대단하구나!\""], ["he said, looking at his dark-red, sunburnt face and neck bent over the plate.", "그가 접시 위로 숙인, 검붉게 햇볕에 그을린 동생의 얼굴과 목덜미를 바라보며 말했다."]], [["“Splendid!", "\"끝내주지!"], ["You can’t imagine what an effectual remedy it is for every sort of foolishness.", "이게 온갖 어리석은 짓에 얼마나 효험 좋은 치료법인지 형은 상상도 못 할 거야."], ["I want to enrich medicine with a new word: Arbeitskur.”", "나는 의학에 새 낱말 하나를 보태고 싶어. Arbeitskur라고 말이지.\""]], [["“Well, but you don’t need it, I should fancy.”", "* Arbeitskur: 독일어로 '노동 요법'이라는 뜻."]], [["“No, but for all sorts of nervous invalids.”", "\"그래, 그렇지만 너한테는 그게 필요 없을 것 같은데.\""]], [["“Yes, it ought to be tried.", "\"내가 아니라, 온갖 신경증 환자들을 위해서 말이야.\""], ["I had meant to come to the mowing to look at you, but it was so unbearably hot that I got no further than the forest.", "\"그래, 한번 시도해 볼 만하지. 나도 네가 풀 베는 모습을 보러 가려고 했는데, 어찌나 견딜 수 없게 더운지 숲까지밖에 못 갔다."], ["I sat there a little, and went on by the forest to the village, met your old nurse, and sounded her as to the peasants’ view of you.", "거기 잠깐 앉았다가, 숲을 따라 마을까지 갔지. 거기서 네 옛 유모를 만나 농부들이 너를 어떻게 보는지 슬쩍 떠봤다."], ["As far as I can make out, they don’t approve of this.", "내가 짐작하기로는, 그들은 그걸 못마땅해해. 유모가 그러더구나."], ["She said: ‘It’s not a gentleman’s work.’ Altogether, I fancy that in the people’s ideas there are very clear and definite notions of certain, as they call it, ‘gentlemanly’ lines of action.", "'그건 나리가 하실 일이 아니지요.' 통틀어 보면, 사람들의 관념 속에는 그들이 말하는 이른바 '나리다운'"], ["And they don’t sanction the gentry’s moving outside bounds clearly laid down in their ideas.”", "처신의 선이라는 게 아주 명확하고 분명하게 자리 잡고 있는 것 같아. 그래서 그들은 양반들이 자기네 관념 속에 또렷이 그어진 경계 밖으로 나가는 걸 인정하지 않는 거지.\""]], [["“Maybe so; but anyway it’s a pleasure such as I have never known in my life.", "\"그럴지도 모르지. 하지만 어쨌든 이건 내가 평생 한 번도 맛본 적 없는 즐거움이야."], ["And there’s no harm in it, you know.", "그리고 거기에 해로울 거라곤 없잖아, 안 그래?"], ["Is there?”", "그렇지?\""], ["answered Levin.", "레빈이 대답했다."], ["“I can’t help it if they don’t like it.", "\"그들이 싫어한다고 해도 난 어쩔 수 없어."], ["Though I do believe it’s all right.", "그래도 난 이게 옳다고 믿어."], ["Eh?”", "응?\""]], [["“Altogether,”", "\"통틀어 말해서,\""], ["pursued Sergey Ivanovitch, “you’re satisfied with your day?”", "세르게이 이바노비치가 말을 이었다. \"넌 오늘 하루에 만족하느냐?\""]], [["“Quite satisfied.", "\"아주 만족해."], ["We cut the whole meadow.", "풀밭을 통째로 다 베었거든."], ["And such a splendid old man I made friends with there!", "게다가 거기서 정말 훌륭한 노인하고 친해졌어!"], ["You can’t fancy how delightful he was!”", "그 노인이 얼마나 유쾌한 사람인지 형은 상상도 못 할 거야!\""]], [["“Well, so you’re content with your day. And so am I.", "\"그래, 그러니 너도 네 하루에 만족하는 거고, 나도 그렇다."], ["First, I solved two chess problems, and one a very pretty one—a pawn opening.", "우선 나는 체스 문제 두 개를 풀었는데, 그중 하나는 아주 멋진 거였어. 폰으로 여는 수였지."], ["I’ll show it you.", "나중에 보여 주마."], ["And then—I thought over our conversation yesterday.”", "그러고 나서는, 어제 우리가 나눈 대화를 곰곰이 생각해 봤다.\""]], [["“Eh!", "\"응?"], ["our conversation yesterday?”", "어제 우리 대화?\""], ["said Levin, blissfully dropping his eyelids and drawing deep breaths after finishing his dinner, and absolutely incapable of recalling what their conversation yesterday was about.", "식사를 마친 레빈이 더없이 흐뭇하게 눈꺼풀을 내리깔고 깊은 숨을 들이쉬며 말했지만, 어제 무슨 대화를 했는지 도무지 떠올릴 수가 없었다."]], [["“I think you are partly right.", "\"네 말도 어느 정도는 옳다고 생각해."], ["Our difference of opinion amounts to this, that you make the mainspring self-interest, while I suppose that interest in the common weal is bound to exist in every man of a certain degree of advancement.", "우리 의견 차이를 정리하자면 이런 거지. 너는 사리사욕을 원동력으로 보는 데 반해, 나는 어느 정도 발전한 단계에 이른 사람이라면 누구에게나 공동의 복리에 대한 관심이 반드시 존재한다고 보는 거야."], ["Possibly you are right too, that action founded on material interest would be more desirable.", "어쩌면 물질적 이익에 바탕을 둔 행동이 더 바람직하다는 네 말도 옳을지 모르지."], ["You are altogether, as the French say, too primesautière a nature; you must have intense, energetic action, or nothing.”", "너는 통틀어 말해, 프랑스 사람들 표현대로 너무 primesautière한 천성이라, 격렬하고 정력적인 행동이 아니면 아예 안 하려고 들거든.\""]], [["Levin listened to his brother and did not understand a single word, and did not want to understand.", "* primesautière: 프랑스어로 '충동적인', '즉흥적인'이라는 뜻. 레빈은 형의 말을 들으면서도 한마디도 이해하지 못했고, 이해하고 싶지도 않았다."], ["He was only afraid his brother might ask him some question which would make it evident he had not heard.", "그는 그저 형이 무슨 질문이라도 던져서 자기가 듣고 있지 않았다는 것이 드러날까 봐 두려울 따름이었다."]], [["“So that’s what I think it is, my dear boy,”", "\"그러니까 내 생각은 그렇다는 거야, 얘야.\""], ["said Sergey Ivanovitch, touching him on the shoulder.", "세르게이 이바노비치가 동생의 어깨를 짚으며 말했다."]], [["“Yes, of course.", "\"응, 물론이지."], ["But, do you know?", "그런데 있잖아?"], ["I won’t stand up for my view,”", "난 내 생각을 고집하지 않을래.\""], ["answered Levin, with a guilty, childlike smile.", "레빈이 죄지은 듯한, 아이 같은 미소를 지으며 대답했다."], ["“Whatever was it I was disputing about?”", "'내가 도대체 무슨 논쟁을 하고 있었더라?'"], ["he wondered.", "그는 생각했다."], ["“Of course, I’m right, and he’s right, and it’s all first-rate.", "'물론 나도 옳고, 형도 옳지. 그러니 다 잘된 거야."], ["Only I must go round to the counting house and see to things.”", "다만 나는 사무소에 들러 일을 좀 살펴봐야 해.'"], ["He got up, stretching and smiling.", "그는 기지개를 켜고 미소를 지으며 일어났다."], ["Sergey Ivanovitch smiled too.", "세르게이 이바노비치도 미소를 지었다."]], [["“If you want to go out, let’s go together,”", "\"나가려거든 같이 가자.\""], ["he said, disinclined to be parted from his brother, who seemed positively breathing out freshness and energy.", "그가 말했다. 그는 싱싱함과 활기를 그야말로 내뿜는 듯한 동생 곁을 떠나기가 싫었다."], ["“Come, we’ll go to the counting house, if you have to go there.”", "\"자, 사무소에 가야 한다면 거기로 가자고.\""]], [["“Oh, heavens!”", "\"아, 이런!\""], ["shouted Levin, so loudly that Sergey Ivanovitch was quite frightened.", "레빈이 어찌나 크게 소리를 질렀는지 세르게이 이바노비치가 깜짝 놀랄 정도였다."]], [["“What, what is the matter?”", "\"왜, 무슨 일이냐?\""]], [["“How’s Agafea Mihalovna’s hand?”", "\"아가피아 미하일로브나 손은 좀 어때?\""], ["said Levin, slapping himself on the head.", "레빈이 자기 머리를 탁 치며 말했다."], ["“I’d positively forgotten her even.”", "\"그 사람을 까맣게 잊고 있었다니.\""]], [["“It’s much better.”", "\"많이 나았다.\""]], [["“Well, anyway I’ll run down to her.", "\"어쨌든 잠깐 내려가 봐야겠어."], ["Before you’ve time to get your hat on, I’ll be back.”", "형이 모자를 쓰기도 전에 돌아올게.\""]], [["And he ran downstairs, clattering with his heels like a spring-rattle.", "그러고 그는 봄철 딸랑이처럼 구두 뒤축을 딸각거리며 아래층으로 달려 내려갔다."]], [["# Chapter 7", "# Chapter 7"]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch had gone to Petersburg to perform the most natural and essential official duty—so familiar to everyone in the government service, though incomprehensible to outsiders—that duty, but for which one could hardly be in government service, of reminding the ministry of his existence—and having, for the due performance of this rite, taken all the available cash from home, was gaily and agreeably spending his days at the races and in the summer villas.", "스테판 아르카지치는 정부 관직에 몸담은 사람이라면 누구나 잘 알지만 바깥 사람들에게는 도무지 이해되지 않는, 더없이 자연스럽고 긴요한 공무를 수행하기 위해 페테르부르크로 떠나 있었다. 그 공무란, 그것 없이는 관직에 머물러 있기조차 어려운 것으로서, 다름 아니라 자신의 존재를 부처(部處)에 상기시키는 일이었다."], ["Meanwhile Dolly and the children had moved into the country, to cut down expenses as much as possible.", "그리고 이 의식을 제대로 치르느라 집 안에 있던 현금을 죄다 챙겨 가지고 떠난 그는, 경마장과 여름 별장들을 오가며 즐겁고 기분 좋게 나날을 보내고 있었다."], ["She had gone to Ergushovo, the estate that had been her dowry, and the one where in spring the forest had been sold.", "한편 돌리와 아이들은 비용을 최대한 줄이기 위해 시골로 옮겨 와 있었다. 그녀가 간 곳은 자기 지참금이었던 영지 예르구쇼보로, 봄에 그곳의 숲을 팔았던 바로 그 영지였다."], ["It was nearly forty miles from Levin’s Pokrovskoe.", "그곳은 레빈의 포크로프스코예에서 사십 마일가량 떨어져 있었다."], ["The big, old house at Ergushovo had been pulled down long ago, and the old prince had had the lodge done up and built on to.", "예르구쇼보의 크고 오래된 본채는 오래전에 헐려 없어졌고, 노공작은 별채를 손보아 증축해 둔 터였다."], ["Twenty years before, when Dolly was a child, the lodge had been roomy and comfortable, though, like all lodges, it stood sideways to the entrance avenue, and faced the south.", "이십 년 전, 돌리가 어린아이였을 무렵에는 이 별채가 넓고 안락했으나, 여느 별채가 다 그렇듯 진입로 가로수길에 옆구리를 대고 남쪽을 향해 서 있었다."], ["But by now this lodge was old and dilapidated.", "그러나 이제 이 별채는 낡고 허물어져 가고 있었다."], ["When Stepan Arkadyevitch had gone down in the spring to sell the forest, Dolly had begged him to look over the house and order what repairs might be needed.", "봄에 스테판 아르카지치가 숲을 팔려고 내려갔을 때, 돌리는 그에게 집을 둘러보고 필요한 수리를 지시해 달라고 부탁했다."], ["Stepan Arkadyevitch, like all unfaithful husbands indeed, was very solicitous for his wife’s comfort, and he had himself looked over the house, and given instructions about everything that he considered necessary.", "스테판 아르카지치는, 사실 부정한 남편들이 다 그렇듯이, 아내의 편안함에 무척 마음을 쓰는 사람이라, 손수 집을 둘러보고 필요하다고 여겨지는 일을 죄다 지시해 두었다."], ["What he considered necessary was to cover all the furniture with cretonne, to put up curtains, to weed the garden, to make a little bridge on the pond, and to plant flowers.", "그가 필요하다고 여긴 일이란, 가구를 죄다 사라사 천으로 씌우고, 커튼을 달고, 정원의 잡초를 뽑고, 연못에 작은 다리를 놓고, 꽃을 심는 것이었다."], ["But he forgot many other essential matters, the want of which greatly distressed Darya Alexandrovna later on.", "그러나 그는 정작 긴요한 다른 일들은 많이 잊어버렸고, 나중에 다리야 알렉산드로브나는 그것이 없어 몹시 애를 먹었다."]], [["In spite of Stepan Arkadyevitch’s efforts to be an attentive father and husband, he never could keep in his mind that he had a wife and children.", "스테판 아르카지치는 자상한 아버지이자 남편이 되려고 애를 썼지만, 자기에게 아내와 자식들이 있다는 사실을 도무지 마음에 담아 두지 못했다."], ["He had bachelor tastes, and it was in accordance with them that he shaped his life.", "그는 독신자의 취향을 지니고 있었고, 그 취향에 맞추어 자기 삶을 꾸렸다."], ["On his return to Moscow he informed his wife with pride that everything was ready, that the house would be a little paradise, and that he advised her most certainly to go.", "모스크바로 돌아온 그는 모든 게 준비되었으며 집이 작은 낙원이 될 거라고, 그러니 꼭 가는 게 좋겠다고 아내에게 자랑스레 알렸다."], ["His wife’s staying away in the country was very agreeable to Stepan Arkadyevitch from every point of view: it did the children good, it decreased expenses, and it left him more at liberty.", "아내가 시골에 가 있는 것은 스테판 아르카지치에게 모든 면에서 매우 흡족한 일이었다. 아이들에게도 이롭고, 비용도 줄고, 자신은 한결 자유로워졌으니 말이다."], ["Darya Alexandrovna regarded staying in the country for the summer as essential for the children, especially for the little girl, who had not succeeded in regaining her strength after the scarlatina, and also as a means of escaping the petty humiliations, the little bills owing to the wood-merchant, the fishmonger, the shoemaker, which made her miserable.", "다리야 알렉산드로브나는 여름을 시골에서 보내는 것이 아이들에게, 특히 성홍열을 앓은 뒤로 기운을 채 회복하지 못한 어린 딸아이에게 꼭 필요하다고 여겼고, 또한 자기를 비참하게 만들던 자잘한 수모들, 그러니까 장작 장수며 생선 장수며 구두장이에게 진 소소한 외상값에서 벗어나는 방편이라고 여겼다."], ["Besides this, she was pleased to go away to the country because she was dreaming of getting her sister Kitty to stay with her there.", "이뿐 아니라 그녀가 시골로 가는 것을 기뻐한 까닭은, 동생 키티를 그곳에 와서 함께 지내게 할 꿈을 꾸고 있었기 때문이다."], ["Kitty was to be back from abroad in the middle of the summer, and bathing had been prescribed for her.", "키티는 한여름께 외국에서 돌아올 예정이었고, 해수욕을 하라는 처방을 받은 터였다."], ["Kitty wrote that no prospect was so alluring as to spend the summer with Dolly at Ergushovo, full of childish associations for both of them.", "키티는 두 사람 모두에게 어린 시절의 추억이 가득한 예르구쇼보에서 돌리와 함께 여름을 보내는 것만큼 마음을 끄는 일은 없다고 편지에 썼다."]], [["The first days of her existence in the country were very hard for Dolly.", "시골 생활의 첫 며칠은 돌리에게 무척 힘들었다."], ["She used to stay in the country as a child, and the impression she had retained of it was that the country was a refuge from all the unpleasantness of the town, that life there, though not luxurious—Dolly could easily make up her mind to that—was cheap and comfortable; that there was plenty of everything, everything was cheap, everything could be got, and children were happy.", "그녀는 어린 시절 시골에서 지냈던 터라, 시골이란 도시의 온갖 불쾌한 일에서 벗어나는 피난처이며, 비록 호사스럽지는 않더라도—돌리는 그쯤은 기꺼이 받아들일 수 있었다—값싸고 편안한 곳이고, 무엇이든 넉넉하고, 무엇이든 값이 싸며, 무엇이든 구할 수 있고, 아이들은 행복하다는 인상을 간직하고 있었다."], ["But now coming to the country as the head of a family, she perceived that it was all utterly unlike what she had fancied.", "그러나 이제 한 집안의 가장으로서 시골에 와 보니, 모든 것이 자기가 그려 왔던 것과는 전혀 딴판임을 알게 되었다."]], [["The day after their arrival there was a heavy fall of rain, and in the night the water came through in the corridor and in the nursery, so that the beds had to be carried into the drawing-room.", "도착한 이튿날 비가 억수같이 쏟아졌고, 밤사이 복도와 아이들 방으로 물이 새어 들어, 침대들을 응접실로 옮겨야 했다. 부엌데기는 도무지 구할 수가 없었다."], ["There was no kitchen maid to be found; of the nine cows, it appeared from the words of the cowherd-woman that some were about to calve, others had just calved, others were old, and others again hard-uddered; there was not butter nor milk enough even for the children.", "젖소 아홉 마리도 소치는 여자의 말로는, 더러는 곧 새끼를 낳을 참이고, 더러는 막 낳은 참이며, 더러는 늙었고, 또 더러는 젖통이 굳어 있어서, 아이들이 먹을 버터도 우유도 넉넉지 않았다."], ["There were no eggs.", "달걀도 없었다."], ["They could get no fowls; old, purplish, stringy cocks were all they had for roasting and boiling.", "닭도 구할 수 없어, 굽거나 삶을 거라곤 늙고 푸르죽죽하고 질긴 수탉들뿐이었다."], ["Impossible to get women to scrub the floors—all were potato-hoeing.", "바닥을 닦을 여자도 구할 수 없었으니—다들 감자밭을 매고 있었던 것이다."], ["Driving was out of the question, because one of the horses was restive, and bolted in the shafts.", "말 한 마리가 사나워서 멍에를 단 채 날뛰는 통에 마차를 타고 나가는 것도 어림없는 일이었다."], ["There was no place where they could bathe; the whole of the river-bank was trampled by the cattle and open to the road; even walks were impossible, for the cattle strayed into the garden through a gap in the hedge, and there was one terrible bull, who bellowed, and therefore might be expected to gore somebody.", "해수욕을 할 만한 곳도 없었으니, 강기슭은 온통 가축들이 밟아 놓은 데다 길 쪽으로 훤히 트여 있었다. 산책조차 할 수 없었던 것이, 가축들이 울타리 틈으로 정원에 들어왔고, 게다가 끔찍한 황소 한 마리가 음매음매 울어 대는 통에 누군가를 들이받을 것만 같았다."], ["There were no proper cupboards for their clothes; what cupboards there were either would not close at all, or burst open whenever anyone passed by them.", "옷을 넣어 둘 변변한 장도 없었다. 있는 장이라곤 아예 닫히지 않거나, 누가 곁을 지나가기만 하면 벌컥 열려 버리는 것들뿐이었다."], ["There were no pots and pans; there was no copper in the washhouse, nor even an ironing-board in the maids’ room.", "냄비도 솥도 없었고, 빨래방에는 구리 가마솥도 없었으며, 하녀들 방에는 다리미질할 판자 하나조차 없었다."]], [["Finding instead of peace and rest all these, from her point of view, fearful calamities, Darya Alexandrovna was at first in despair.", "평온과 휴식 대신 그녀가 보기에 이 모든 끔찍한 재난들을 맞닥뜨리고, 다리야 알렉산드로브나는 처음에는 절망에 빠졌다."], ["She exerted herself to the utmost, felt the hopelessness of the position, and was every instant suppressing the tears that started into her eyes.", "있는 힘을 다해 애를 썼지만 형편이 가망 없음을 느꼈고, 순간순간 눈에 차오르는 눈물을 억눌러야 했다."], ["The bailiff, a retired quartermaster, whom Stepan Arkadyevitch had taken a fancy to and had appointed bailiff on account of his handsome and respectful appearance as a hall-porter, showed no sympathy for Darya Alexandrovna’s woes.", "잘생기고 공손한 현관지기다운 외모 때문에 스테판 아르카지치가 마음에 들어 집사로 앉힌 퇴역 병참 하사관 출신의 그 집사는, 다리야 알렉산드로브나의 시름에 조금도 동정을 보이지 않았다. 그는 \"어쩔 도리가 없습죠, 농사꾼들이 워낙 한심한 작자들이라서요\""], ["He said respectfully, “nothing can be done, the peasants are such a wretched lot,” and did nothing to help her.", "하고 공손히 말할 뿐, 그녀를 돕는 일은 아무것도 하지 않았다."]], [["The position seemed hopeless.", "형편은 가망 없어 보였다."], ["But in the Oblonskys’ household, as in all families indeed, there was one inconspicuous but most valuable and useful person, Marya Philimonovna.", "그러나 오블론스키 집안에도, 사실 어느 집안에나 그렇듯이, 눈에 잘 띄지 않지만 더없이 귀하고 쓸모 있는 사람이 하나 있었으니, 바로 마리야 필리모노브나였다."], ["She soothed her mistress, assured her that everything would come round (it was her expression, and Matvey had borrowed it from her), and without fuss or hurry proceeded to set to work herself.", "그녀는 안주인을 달래며 모든 게 잘 풀릴 거라고 장담했고(이 표현은 그녀의 것이었는데, 마트베이가 그녀에게서 빌려 쓴 것이었다), 부산을 떨거나 서두르는 일 없이 손수 일에 착수했다."], ["She had immediately made friends with the bailiff’s wife, and on the very first day she drank tea with her and the bailiff under the acacias, and reviewed all the circumstances of the position.", "그녀는 곧장 집사의 아내와 친해졌고, 바로 첫날부터 아카시아 나무 아래서 집사 내외와 차를 마시며 형편의 온갖 사정을 함께 짚어 보았다."], ["Very soon Marya Philimonovna had established her club, so to say, under the acacias, and there it was, in this club, consisting of the bailiff’s wife, the village elder, and the counting-house clerk, that the difficulties of existence were gradually smoothed away, and in a week’s time everything actually had come round.", "머지않아 마리야 필리모노브나는 아카시아 나무 아래에 이를테면 자기만의 모임을 꾸렸고, 바로 이 모임, 그러니까 집사의 아내며 마을 촌장이며 회계 사무소 서기로 이루어진 이 모임에서 살림살이의 온갖 어려움이 차츰차츰 풀려 나갔으며, 일주일 만에 정말로 모든 게 잘 풀렸다."], ["The roof was mended, a kitchen maid was found—a crony of the village elder’s—hens were bought, the cows began giving milk, the garden hedge was stopped up with stakes, the carpenter made a mangle, hooks were put in the cupboards, and they ceased to burst open spontaneously, and an ironing-board covered with army cloth was placed across from the arm of a chair to the chest of drawers, and there was a smell of flatirons in the maids’ room.", "지붕이 고쳐졌고, 부엌데기—마을 촌장의 단짝이었다—를 구했으며, 암탉들을 사들였고, 젖소들이 우유를 내기 시작했으며, 정원 울타리는 말뚝으로 틈을 메웠고, 목수가 빨래 짜는 기계를 만들었으며, 장에는 걸쇠를 달아 제멋대로 벌컥 열리는 일이 없어졌고, 군용 천을 씌운 다리미판이 의자 팔걸이에서 서랍장에 걸쳐 놓이자, 하녀들 방에서 다리미 냄새가 풍기게 되었다."]], [["“Just see, now, and you were quite in despair,”", "\"자, 보세요, 그렇게 절망하시더니.\""], ["said Marya Philimonovna, pointing to the ironing-board.", "마리야 필리모노브나가 다리미판을 가리키며 말했다."], ["They even rigged up a bathing-shed of straw hurdles.", "그들은 짚으로 엮은 발로 목욕할 헛간까지 마련했다."], ["Lily began to bathe, and Darya Alexandrovna began to realize, if only in part, her expectations, if not of a peaceful, at least of a comfortable, life in the country.", "릴리가 해수욕을 하기 시작했고, 다리야 알렉산드로브나는 비록 부분적으로나마, 평온하지는 않을지언정 적어도 편안한 시골 생활에 대한 자기 기대가 이루어지는 것을 보게 되었다."], ["Peaceful with six children Darya Alexandrovna could not be.", "여섯 아이를 데리고 다리야 알렉산드로브나가 평온할 수는 없었다."], ["One would fall ill, another might easily become so, a third would be without something necessary, a fourth would show symptoms of a bad disposition, and so on.", "하나가 앓아눕고, 다른 하나가 자칫 그렇게 될 수 있고, 또 하나는 무언가 꼭 필요한 것이 없고, 또 다른 하나는 못된 성미의 기미를 보이는 식이었다."], ["Rare indeed were the brief periods of peace.", "평온한 짧은 시기는 정말이지 드물었다."], ["But these cares and anxieties were for Darya Alexandrovna the sole happiness possible.", "그러나 이런 근심과 걱정이야말로 다리야 알렉산드로브나에게는 누릴 수 있는 유일한 행복이었다."], ["Had it not been for them, she would have been left alone to brood over her husband who did not love her.", "그것들이 없었다면 그녀는 자기를 사랑하지 않는 남편을 곱씹으며 홀로 남겨졌을 것이다."], ["And besides, hard though it was for the mother to bear the dread of illness, the illnesses themselves, and the grief of seeing signs of evil propensities in her children—the children themselves were even now repaying her in small joys for her sufferings.", "게다가 어머니로서 병에 대한 두려움이며, 병 그 자체며, 아이들에게서 못된 기질의 조짐을 보는 슬픔을 견디기가 힘들기는 했어도, 아이들 자신이 지금도 그 고통을 작은 기쁨으로 갚아 주고 있었다."], ["Those joys were so small that they passed unnoticed, like gold in sand, and at bad moments she could see nothing but the pain, nothing but sand; but there were good moments too when she saw nothing but the joy, nothing but gold.", "그 기쁨들은 모래 속의 금처럼 너무도 작아서 눈에 띄지 않은 채 지나가 버렸고, 안 좋은 순간에는 고통밖에, 모래밖에 보이지 않았다. 그러나 좋은 순간도 있어서, 그때는 기쁨밖에, 금밖에 보이지 않았다."]], [["Now in the solitude of the country, she began to be more and more frequently aware of those joys.", "이제 시골의 고적함 속에서 그녀는 점점 더 자주 그 기쁨들을 알아차리게 되었다."], ["Often, looking at them, she would make every possible effort to persuade herself that she was mistaken, that she as a mother was partial to her children.", "아이들을 바라보며 그녀는 자기가 잘못 생각하는 거라고, 어머니라서 제 자식들에게 치우쳐 있는 거라고 스스로를 타이르려 갖은 애를 쓰곤 했다."], ["All the same, she could not help saying to herself that she had charming children, all six of them in different ways, but a set of children such as is not often to be met with, and she was happy in them, and proud of them.", "그러면서도 자기 아이들이 여섯 다 저마다 다른 모습으로 사랑스럽고, 좀처럼 만나 보기 힘든 그런 아이들이며, 그래서 자기는 그들로 하여 행복하고 그들이 자랑스럽다고 스스로에게 말하지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["# Chapter 8", "# Chapter 8"]], [["Towards the end of May, when everything had been more or less satisfactorily arranged, she received her husband’s answer to her complaints of the disorganized state of things in the country.", "5월 말, 모든 일이 그럭저럭 만족스럽게 정리되었을 무렵, 그녀는 시골 살림이 엉망진창이라는 자신의 하소연에 대한 남편의 답장을 받았다."], ["He wrote begging her forgiveness for not having thought of everything before, and promised to come down at the first chance.", "그는 미리 모든 것을 헤아리지 못한 점에 대해 용서를 빌었고, 틈이 나는 대로 곧 내려가겠다고 약속했다."], ["This chance did not present itself, and till the beginning of June Darya Alexandrovna stayed alone in the country.", "그러나 그런 틈은 좀처럼 나지 않았고, 6월 초까지 다리야 알렉산드로브나는 시골에서 홀로 지냈다."]], [["On the Sunday in St. Peter’s week Darya Alexandrovna drove to mass for all her children to take the sacrament.", "성 베드로 주간의 일요일에 다리야 알렉산드로브나는 아이들 모두에게 성찬식을 받게 하려고 마차를 타고 미사에 갔다."], ["Darya Alexandrovna in her intimate, philosophical talks with her sister, her mother, and her friends very often astonished them by the freedom of her views in regard to religion.", "다리야 알렉산드로브나는 언니와 어머니와 친구들과 나누는 은밀하고 철학적인 이야기 속에서 종교에 관한 자유로운 견해로 그들을 자주 놀라게 하곤 했다."], ["She had a strange religion of transmigration of souls all her own, in which she had firm faith, troubling herself little about the dogmas of the Church.", "그녀는 영혼의 윤회라는, 그녀만의 기묘한 종교를 지니고 있었고, 그것을 굳게 믿었으며, 교회의 교리에는 별로 마음을 쓰지 않았다."], ["But in her family she was strict in carrying out all that was required by the Church—and not merely in order to set an example, but with all her heart in it.", "그러나 자기 가족 안에서는 교회가 요구하는 모든 것을 엄격하게 지켰는데, 단지 본보기를 보이기 위해서가 아니라 온 마음을 다해서였다."], ["The fact that the children had not been at the sacrament for nearly a year worried her extremely, and with the full approval and sympathy of Marya Philimonovna she decided that this should take place now in the summer.", "아이들이 거의 일 년 가까이 성찬식을 받지 못했다는 사실이 그녀를 몹시 걱정스럽게 만들었고, 마리야 필리모노브나의 전적인 찬동과 공감을 얻어 그녀는 그것을 이제 여름에 치르기로 결정했다."]], [["For several days before, Darya Alexandrovna was busily deliberating on how to dress all the children.", "며칠 전부터 다리야 알렉산드로브나는 아이들에게 무엇을 입힐지 분주하게 궁리했다."], ["Frocks were made or altered and washed, seams and flounces were let out, buttons were sewn on, and ribbons got ready.", "옷을 새로 짓거나 고치거나 빨았고, 솔기와 주름 장식을 늘렸으며, 단추를 달고 리본을 마련했다."], ["One dress, Tanya’s, which the English governess had undertaken, cost Darya Alexandrovna much loss of temper.", "영국인 가정교사가 맡은 타냐의 옷 하나가 다리야 알렉산드로브나의 속을 단단히 썩였다."], ["The English governess in altering it had made the seams in the wrong place, had taken up the sleeves too much, and altogether spoilt the dress.", "영국인 가정교사는 그 옷을 고치면서 솔기를 엉뚱한 데에 만들고 소매를 너무 많이 잡아 올려서 옷을 아예 망쳐 버렸다."], ["It was so narrow on Tanya’s shoulders that it was quite painful to look at her.", "옷이 타냐의 어깨에 어찌나 끼는지 보고 있기가 안쓰러울 지경이었다."], ["But Marya Philimonovna had the happy thought of putting in gussets, and adding a little shoulder-cape.", "그러나 마리야 필리모노브나가 무를 대고 작은 어깨 망토를 덧붙이자는 좋은 생각을 떠올렸다."], ["The dress was set right, but there was nearly a quarrel with the English governess.", "옷은 제대로 손질되었지만, 영국인 가정교사와 하마터면 다툼이 날 뻔했다."], ["On the morning, however, all was happily arranged, and towards ten o’clock—the time at which they had asked the priest to wait for them for the mass—the children in their new dresses, with beaming faces, stood on the step before the carriage waiting for their mother.", "그렇지만 아침이 되자 모든 것이 다행히 정리되었고, 열 시 무렵—그들이 미사를 위해 사제에게 기다려 달라고 부탁한 시각—새 옷을 입고 환한 얼굴을 한 아이들이 마차 앞 계단에 서서 어머니를 기다리고 있었다."]], [["To the carriage, instead of the restive Raven, they had harnessed, thanks to the representations of Marya Philimonovna, the bailiff’s horse, Brownie, and Darya Alexandrovna, delayed by anxiety over her own attire, came out and got in, dressed in a white muslin gown.", "마차에는 마리야 필리모노브나의 권유 덕분에 사나운 보론 대신 집사의 말 부르니를 매어 놓았고, 자기 옷차림에 대한 걱정으로 늦어진 다리야 알렉산드로브나가 흰 모슬린 가운을 입고 나와 올라탔다."]], [["Darya Alexandrovna had done her hair, and dressed with care and excitement.", "다리야 알렉산드로브나는 머리를 매만지고 정성을 들여 들뜬 마음으로 차려입었다."], ["In the old days she had dressed for her own sake to look pretty and be admired.", "옛날에는 예뻐 보이고 칭찬을 받으려고 자기 자신을 위해 차려입었다."], ["Later on, as she got older, dress became more and more distasteful to her.", "그러다 나이가 들면서 차려입는 일이 점점 더 싫어졌다."], ["She saw that she was losing her good looks.", "자신이 고운 용모를 잃어 가고 있음을 알았기 때문이다."], ["But now she began to feel pleasure and interest in dress again.", "그러나 이제 그녀는 다시 차려입는 일에 기쁨과 흥미를 느끼기 시작했다."], ["Now she did not dress for her own sake, not for the sake of her own beauty, but simply that as the mother of those exquisite creatures she might not spoil the general effect.", "이제는 자기 자신을 위해서가 아니라, 자기 미모를 위해서가 아니라, 단지 그 사랑스러운 아이들의 어머니로서 전체적인 효과를 망치지 않기 위해서 차려입었다."], ["And looking at herself for the last time in the looking-glass she was satisfied with herself.", "그리고 마지막으로 거울에 자신을 비춰 보고 그녀는 만족했다."], ["She looked nice.", "보기 좋았다."], ["Not nice as she would have wished to look nice in old days at a ball, but nice for the object which she now had in view.", "옛날 무도회에서 보이고 싶었던 식의 아름다움은 아니었지만, 이제 그녀가 마음에 둔 목적에는 보기 좋았다."]], [["In the church there was no one but the peasants, the servants and their women-folk.", "교회에는 농부들과 하인들, 그리고 그 아낙네들 말고는 아무도 없었다."], ["But Darya Alexandrovna saw, or fancied she saw, the sensation produced by her children and her.", "그러나 다리야 알렉산드로브나는 자기 아이들과 자기가 불러일으킨 반향을 보았다고, 아니 보았다고 여겼다."], ["The children were not only beautiful to look at in their smart little dresses, but they were charming in the way they behaved.", "아이들은 맵시 있는 작은 옷차림이 보기에 아름다웠을 뿐 아니라 행동거지마저 매력적이었다."], ["Aliosha, it is true, did not stand quite correctly; he kept turning round, trying to look at his little jacket from behind; but all the same he was wonderfully sweet.", "알료샤가 자세를 제대로 못 잡은 것은 사실이었다. 그 애는 자기 짧은 윗도리를 뒤에서 보려고 자꾸만 몸을 돌렸다."], ["Tanya behaved like a grown-up person, and looked after the little ones.", "하지만 그래도 더없이 귀여웠다. 타냐는 어른처럼 행동하며 어린 동생들을 돌보았다."], ["And the smallest, Lily, was bewitching in her naïve astonishment at everything, and it was difficult not to smile when, after taking the sacrament, she said in English, “Please, some more.”", "그리고 가장 어린 릴리는 모든 것에 순진하게 놀라워하는 모습이 사람을 홀릴 듯했고, 성찬을 받고 나서 영어로 \"Please, some more.\"라고 말했을 때는 웃음을 참기가 어려웠다."]], [["On the way home the children felt that something solemn had happened, and were very sedate.", "집으로 돌아오는 길에 아이들은 무언가 엄숙한 일이 일어났다고 느껴 아주 점잖게 굴었다."]], [["Everything went happily at home too; but at lunch Grisha began whistling, and, what was worse, was disobedient to the English governess, and was forbidden to have any tart.", "집에서도 모든 일이 즐겁게 흘러갔다. 그런데 점심 식사 때 그리샤가 휘파람을 불기 시작했고, 더 나쁘게도 영국인 가정교사에게 말을 듣지 않아서 타르트를 먹지 못하게 되었다."], ["Darya Alexandrovna would not have let things go so far on such a day had she been present; but she had to support the English governess’s authority, and she upheld her decision that Grisha should have no tart.", "만일 다리야 알렉산드로브나가 그 자리에 있었더라면 그런 날에 일을 그렇게까지 몰고 가지는 않았을 것이다. 그러나 그녀는 영국인 가정교사의 권위를 받쳐 주어야 했기에, 그리샤에게 타르트를 주지 말라는 그녀의 결정을 지지했다."], ["This rather spoiled the general good humor.", "이 일이 모두의 좋은 기분을 다소 망쳐 놓았다."], ["Grisha cried, declaring that Nikolinka had whistled too, and he was not punished, and that he wasn’t crying for the tart—he didn’t care—but at being unjustly treated.", "그리샤는 울면서 니콜린카도 휘파람을 불었는데 벌을 받지 않았다고, 자기는 타르트 때문에 우는 게 아니라고—그깟 건 아무래도 좋다고—다만 부당한 대우를 받아서 운다고 항변했다."], ["This was really too tragic, and Darya Alexandrovna made up her mind to persuade the English governess to forgive Grisha, and she went to speak to her.", "이건 정말이지 너무 비극적이어서, 다리야 알렉산드로브나는 영국인 가정교사를 설득해 그리샤를 용서하게 하기로 마음먹고 그녀에게 말하러 갔다."], ["But on the way, as she passed the drawing-room, she beheld a scene, filling her heart with such pleasure that the tears came into her eyes, and she forgave the delinquent herself.", "그런데 가는 길에 응접실을 지나다가, 그녀는 어떤 광경을 보았고, 그 광경이 가슴을 기쁨으로 가득 채워 눈물이 고이게 했으며, 그녀 자신이 그 죄인을 용서해 버렸다."]], [["The culprit was sitting at the window in the corner of the drawing-room; beside him was standing Tanya with a plate.", "죄인은 응접실 구석 창가에 앉아 있었다. 그 곁에는 접시를 든 타냐가 서 있었다."], ["On the pretext of wanting to give some dinner to her dolls, she had asked the governess’s permission to take her share of tart to the nursery, and had taken it instead to her brother.", "자기 인형들에게 밥을 먹이고 싶다는 핑계로 그 애는 자기 몫의 타르트를 아이 방으로 가져가게 해 달라고 가정교사의 허락을 받고는, 그 대신 그것을 오빠에게 가져온 것이었다. 그는 여전히 자기가 받은 벌의 부당함을 울면서, 타르트를 먹으며 흐느낌 사이로 자꾸만 말했다."], ["While still weeping over the injustice of his punishment, he was eating the tart, and kept saying through his sobs, “Eat yourself; let’s eat it together ... together.”", "\"너도 먹어, 같이 먹자……함께 먹자.\""]], [["Tanya had at first been under the influence of her pity for Grisha, then of a sense of her noble action, and tears were standing in her eyes too; but she did not refuse, and ate her share.", "타냐는 처음에는 그리샤에 대한 연민에 사로잡혔다가, 그다음에는 자기의 고결한 행동에 대한 감격에 젖었고, 그 애의 눈에도 눈물이 고였다. 하지만 거절하지 않고 자기 몫을 먹었다."]], [["On catching sight of their mother they were dismayed, but, looking into her face, they saw they were not doing wrong.", "어머니를 발견하자 아이들은 당황했지만, 그녀의 얼굴을 들여다보고는 자기들이 잘못하고 있는 게 아님을 알았다."], ["They burst out laughing, and, with their mouths full of tart, they began wiping their smiling lips with their hands, and smearing their radiant faces all over with tears and jam.", "아이들은 웃음을 터뜨렸고, 타르트로 입이 가득 찬 채 미소 짓는 입술을 손으로 닦으며, 환한 얼굴에 눈물과 잼을 온통 칠해 버렸다."]], [["“Mercy!", "\"어쩜!"], ["Your new white frock!", "너희 새 흰 옷!"], ["Tanya!", "타냐!"], ["Grisha!”", "그리샤!\""], ["said their mother, trying to save the frock, but with tears in her eyes, smiling a blissful, rapturous smile.", "어머니는 옷을 구하려 애쓰면서 말했지만, 눈에는 눈물이 고인 채, 더없이 행복하고 황홀한 미소를 짓고 있었다."]], [["The new frocks were taken off, and orders were given for the little girls to have their blouses put on, and the boys their old jackets, and the wagonette to be harnessed; with Brownie, to the bailiff’s annoyance, again in the shafts, to drive out for mushroom picking and bathing.", "새 옷을 벗겼고, 여자아이들에게는 블라우스를, 사내아이들에게는 낡은 윗도리를 입히라는 지시가 내려졌으며, 짐마차를 매라는 명령이 떨어졌다. 부르니를 다시 채에 매니 집사로서는 못마땅했지만, 버섯을 따고 멱을 감으러 나가기 위해서였다."], ["A roar of delighted shrieks arose in the nursery, and never ceased till they had set off for the bathing-place.", "아이 방에서 기뻐 날뛰는 함성이 터져 나왔고, 멱 감는 곳을 향해 출발할 때까지 그칠 줄을 몰랐다."]], [["They gathered a whole basketful of mushrooms; even Lily found a birch mushroom.", "아이들은 한 바구니 가득 버섯을 땄다. 릴리마저 자작나무 버섯 하나를 찾아냈다."], ["It had always happened before that Miss Hoole found them and pointed them out to her; but this time she found a big one quite of herself, and there was a general scream of delight, “Lily has found a mushroom!”", "전에는 늘 미스 훌이 버섯을 발견해 그 애에게 알려 주곤 했는데, 이번에는 그 애가 큼직한 것을 혼자서 찾아냈고, \"릴리가 버섯을 찾았다!\" 하는 환성이 다 함께 터져 나왔다."]], [["Then they reached the river, put the horses under the birch trees, and went to the bathing-place.", "이윽고 그들은 강가에 이르러 자작나무 아래에 말을 매어 두고 멱 감는 곳으로 갔다."], ["The coachman, Terenty, fastened the horses, who kept whisking away the flies, to a tree, and, treading down the grass, lay down in the shade of a birch and smoked his shag, while the never-ceasing shrieks of delight of the children floated across to him from the bathing-place.", "마부 테렌티는 파리를 자꾸만 쫓아 대는 말들을 나무에 묶고는, 풀을 밟아 누이고 자작나무 그늘에 누워 싸구려 담배를 피웠다. 그동안 멱 감는 곳에서 그칠 줄 모르는 아이들의 환성이 그에게로 떠밀려 왔다."]], [["Though it was hard work to look after all the children and restrain their wild pranks, though it was difficult too to keep in one’s head and not mix up all the stockings, little breeches, and shoes for the different legs, and to undo and to do up again all the tapes and buttons, Darya Alexandrovna, who had always liked bathing herself, and believed it to be very good for the children, enjoyed nothing so much as bathing with all the children.", "아이들을 모두 돌보고 그 거친 장난을 말리기란 고된 일이었고, 머릿속에 하나하나 새겨 두어 양말이며 작은 바지며 신발을 각기 다른 다리에 헷갈리지 않게 하고, 온갖 끈과 단추를 풀었다 다시 채우기도 어려운 일이었지만, 본디 멱 감기를 좋아했고 그것이 아이들에게 아주 좋다고 믿어 온 다리야 알렉산드로브나는 아이들 모두와 함께 멱을 감는 일만큼 즐거운 것이 없었다."], ["To go over all those fat little legs, pulling on their stockings, to take in her arms and dip those little naked bodies, and to hear their screams of delight and alarm, to see the breathless faces with wide-open, scared, and happy eyes of all her splashing cherubs, was a great pleasure to her.", "그 통통한 작은 다리들을 일일이 어루만지며 양말을 신기고, 그 작은 알몸들을 두 팔로 안아 물에 담그고, 기쁨과 놀라움이 섞인 비명을 듣고, 첨벙대는 천사 같은 아이들 모두의 크게 뜬 겁먹은 듯 행복한 눈과 숨 가쁜 얼굴을 보는 것이 그녀에게는 더없는 기쁨이었다."]], [["When half the children had been dressed, some peasant women in holiday dress, out picking herbs, came up to the bathing-shed and stopped shyly.", "아이들 절반쯤이 옷을 입었을 때, 약초를 캐러 나온 명절 옷차림의 농가 아낙 몇이 멱 감는 헛간으로 다가와 수줍게 멈춰 섰다."], ["Marya Philimonovna called one of them and handed her a sheet and a shirt that had dropped into the water for her to dry them, and Darya Alexandrovna began to talk to the women.", "마리야 필리모노브나가 그중 하나를 불러 물에 빠졌던 시트와 셔츠를 건네며 말려 달라고 했고, 다리야 알렉산드로브나는 아낙들과 이야기를 나누기 시작했다."], ["At first they laughed behind their hands and did not understand her questions, but soon they grew bolder and began to talk, winning Darya Alexandrovna’s heart at once by the genuine admiration of the children that they showed.", "처음에는 손으로 입을 가리고 웃기만 하며 그녀의 물음을 알아듣지 못했지만, 이내 대담해져서 말을 하기 시작했고, 아이들에 대해 보여 주는 진정 어린 감탄으로 단번에 다리야 알렉산드로브나의 마음을 사로잡았다."]], [["“My, what a beauty!", "\"어머나, 정말 예쁘기도 해라!"], ["as white as sugar,”", "설탕처럼 새하얗네.\""], ["said one, admiring Tanitchka, and shaking her head; “but thin....”", "한 아낙이 고개를 저으며 타니치카에게 감탄해서 말했다. \"그런데 말랐어……\""]], [["“Yes, she has been ill.”", "\"네, 그동안 아팠거든요.\""]], [["“And so they’ve been bathing you too,”", "\"그래서 이 아기도 멱을 감기고 있구먼.\""], ["said another to the baby.", "다른 아낙이 갓난아기를 보고 말했다."]], [["“No; he’s only three months old,”", "\"아니에요, 이 애는 이제 겨우 석 달밖에 안 됐어요.\""], ["answered Darya Alexandrovna with pride.", "다리야 알렉산드로브나가 자랑스럽게 대답했다."]], [["“You don’t say so!”", "\"세상에!\""]], [["“And have you any children?”", "\"댁들도 아이가 있어요?\""]], [["“I’ve had four; I’ve two living—a boy and a girl.", "\"넷을 낳았는데 둘이 살아 있어요. 사내애 하나, 계집애 하나죠."], ["I weaned her last carnival.”", "지난 사육제 때 그 애 젖을 뗐어요.\""]], [["“How old is she?”", "\"몇 살인데요?\""]], [["“Why, two years old.”", "\"두 살이죠, 뭐.\""]], [["“Why did you nurse her so long?”", "\"왜 그렇게 오래 젖을 먹였어요?\""]], [["“It’s our custom; for three fasts....”", "\"우리네 풍습이 그래요. 세 번의 사순절 동안은……\""]], [["And the conversation became most interesting to Darya Alexandrovna.", "그리고 그 대화는 다리야 알렉산드로브나에게 더없이 흥미로워졌다."], ["What sort of time did she have?", "출산 때는 어땠는가?"], ["What was the matter with the boy?", "사내애는 무슨 병이 있었는가?"], ["Where was her husband?", "남편은 어디에 있는가?"], ["Did it often happen?", "그런 일이 자주 있는가?"]], [["Darya Alexandrovna felt disinclined to leave the peasant women, so interesting to her was their conversation, so completely identical were all their interests.", "다리야 알렉산드로브나는 농가 아낙들 곁을 떠나기가 싫었다. 그들과의 대화가 그토록 흥미로웠고, 그들의 관심사가 자기 것과 완전히 똑같았기 때문이다."], ["What pleased her most of all was that she saw clearly what all the women admired more than anything was her having so many children, and such fine ones.", "무엇보다 그녀를 기쁘게 한 것은, 아낙들 모두가 다른 무엇보다 그녀가 그토록 많은, 그것도 그토록 잘난 아이들을 두었다는 사실을 감탄한다는 것을 똑똑히 보았다는 점이었다."], ["The peasant women even made Darya Alexandrovna laugh, and offended the English governess, because she was the cause of the laughter she did not understand.", "농가 아낙들은 다리야 알렉산드로브나를 웃게까지 만들었고, 영국인 가정교사를 언짢게 했는데, 가정교사로서는 알아듣지도 못하는 웃음의 원인이 자기였기 때문이다."], ["One of the younger women kept staring at the Englishwoman, who was dressing after all the rest, and when she put on her third petticoat she could not refrain from the remark, “My, she keeps putting on and putting on, and she’ll never have done!”", "젊은 아낙 하나가 다른 사람들이 다 옷을 입은 뒤에야 옷을 입는 영국 여자를 빤히 쳐다보더니, 그 여자가 세 번째 속치마를 입자 한마디 하지 않을 수 없었다. \"어머나, 저이는 입고 또 입고 하니 도무지 끝이 없겠네!\""], ["she said, and they all went off into roars.", "하고 그녀가 말하자, 모두가 한바탕 웃음을 터뜨렸다."]], [["# Chapter 9", "# Chapter 9"]], [["On the drive home, as Darya Alexandrovna, with all her children round her, their heads still wet from their bath, and a kerchief tied over her own head, was getting near the house, the coachman said, “There’s some gentleman coming: the master of Pokrovskoe, I do believe.”", "다리야 알렉산드로브나가 아이들을 죄다 거느리고, 막 멱을 감고 나와 아직 머리가 젖은 채인 아이들과 함께, 자기 머리에는 수건을 동여매고 집 가까이에 다다랐을 때, 마부가 말했다. \"저기 어떤 나리가 오는데요, 아무래도 포크롭스코예의 주인 같습니다.\""]], [["Darya Alexandrovna peeped out in front, and was delighted when she recognized in the gray hat and gray coat the familiar figure of Levin walking to meet them.", "다리야 알렉산드로브나가 앞쪽을 내다보다가, 회색 모자에 회색 외투를 걸치고 그들을 맞으러 걸어오는 레빈의 낯익은 모습을 알아보고는 기뻐했다."], ["She was glad to see him at any time, but at this moment she was specially glad he should see her in all her glory.", "그녀는 언제든 그를 보면 반가웠지만, 마침 이 순간에는 자신의 위풍당당한 모습을 그가 봐 주는 것이 유난히 기뻤다."], ["No one was better able to appreciate her grandeur than Levin.", "그녀의 위엄을 레빈만큼 잘 알아볼 수 있는 사람도 없었다."]], [["Seeing her, he found himself face to face with one of the pictures of his daydream of family life.", "그녀를 본 레빈은, 자기가 가족과 함께하는 삶에 대해 그리던 백일몽의 한 장면과 정면으로 마주한 듯한 느낌이 들었다."]], [["“You’re like a hen with your chickens, Darya Alexandrovna.”", "\"꼭 병아리를 거느린 암탉 같으십니다, 다리야 알렉산드로브나.\""]], [["“Ah, how glad I am to see you!”", "\"아, 정말 반가워요!\""], ["she said, holding out her hand to him.", "그녀가 그에게 손을 내밀며 말했다."]], [["“Glad to see me, but you didn’t let me know.", "\"저를 보고 반가워하시면서도, 제게 알려 주지는 않으셨군요."], ["My brother’s staying with me.", "제 형이 저와 함께 지내고 있습니다."], ["I got a note from Stiva that you were here.”", "부인이 여기 와 계시다는 걸 스티바가 보낸 쪽지로 알았어요.\""]], [["“From Stiva?”", "\"스티바한테서요?\""], ["Darya Alexandrovna asked with surprise.", "다리야 알렉산드로브나가 놀라서 물었다."]], [["“Yes; he writes that you are here, and that he thinks you might allow me to be of use to you,”", "\"네, 부인이 여기 계시고, 제가 부인께 뭔가 도움을 드려도 좋겠다고 생각한다고 적혀 있더군요.\""], ["said Levin, and as he said it he became suddenly embarrassed, and, stopping abruptly, he walked on in silence by the wagonette, snapping off the buds of the lime trees and nibbling them.", "레빈은 이렇게 말하다가 갑자기 당황스러워졌고, 말을 뚝 그치고는 보리수나무의 새순을 뜯어 잘근잘근 씹으면서 마차 옆을 말없이 걸어갔다."], ["He was embarrassed through a sense that Darya Alexandrovna would be annoyed by receiving from an outsider help that should by rights have come from her own husband.", "그가 당황한 까닭은, 마땅히 남편에게서 받아야 할 도움을 외부 사람에게서 받게 된다면 다리야 알렉산드로브나가 언짢아하리라는 것을 느꼈기 때문이었다."], ["Darya Alexandrovna certainly did not like this little way of Stepan Arkadyevitch’s of foisting his domestic duties on others.", "다리야 알렉산드로브나는 분명히, 자기 집안일을 남에게 떠넘기는 스테판 아르카지치의 이런 버릇을 좋아하지 않았다."], ["And she was at once aware that Levin was aware of this.", "그리고 그녀는 레빈도 그 점을 알고 있다는 것을 곧바로 알아챘다."], ["It was just for this fineness of perception, for this delicacy, that Darya Alexandrovna liked Levin.", "바로 이 섬세한 통찰력, 이 세심함 때문에 다리야 알렉산드로브나는 레빈을 좋아했다."]], [["“I know, of course,”", "\"물론 압니다.\""], ["said Levin, “that that simply means that you would like to see me, and I’m exceedingly glad.", "레빈이 말했다. \"그건 그저 부인이 저를 보고 싶어 하신다는 뜻일 뿐이고, 저는 정말이지 기쁩니다."], ["Though I can fancy that, used to town housekeeping as you are, you must feel in the wilds here, and if there’s anything wanted, I’m altogether at your disposal.”", "다만, 도시 살림에 익숙하신 분이니 이런 시골 한복판에서는 불편하실 거라고 짐작이 가고, 필요한 게 있으시면 저는 무엇이든 부인 뜻대로 하겠습니다.\""]], [["“Oh, no!” said Dolly.", "\"아, 아니에요!\""], ["“At first things were rather uncomfortable, but now we’ve settled everything capitally—thanks to my old nurse,”", "돌리가 말했다. \"처음엔 좀 불편했지만, 지금은 모든 걸 아주 훌륭하게 자리 잡아 놨답니다."], ["she said, indicating Marya Philimonovna, who, seeing that they were speaking of her, smiled brightly and cordially to Levin.", "우리 늙은 유모 덕분이지요.\" 그녀가 마리야 필리모노브나를 가리키며 말하자, 그녀는 자기 이야기를 하고 있다는 걸 알아채고는 레빈에게 환하고 다정하게 미소 지었다."], ["She knew him, and knew that he would be a good match for her young lady, and was very keen to see the matter settled.", "그녀는 레빈을 알고 있었고, 그가 자기 아가씨에게 좋은 짝이 되리라는 것도 알고 있었기에 그 일이 성사되기를 무척 바랐다."]], [["“Won’t you get in, sir, we’ll make room this side!”", "\"안 타시겠어요, 나리? 이쪽으로 자리를 만들어 드릴게요!\""], ["she said to him.", "그녀가 그에게 말했다."]], [["“No, I’ll walk.", "\"아니, 걸어가겠소."], ["Children, who’d like to race the horses with me?”", "얘들아, 누가 나랑 말이랑 달리기 시합할 사람?\""], ["The children knew Levin very little, and could not remember when they had seen him, but they experienced in regard to him none of that strange feeling of shyness and hostility which children so often experience towards hypocritical, grown-up people, and for which they are so often and miserably punished.", "아이들은 레빈을 잘 알지 못했고 그를 언제 봤는지도 기억하지 못했지만, 아이들이 위선적인 어른들에게 흔히 느끼는, 그래서 그 일로 종종 비참하게 벌을 받곤 하는 그 묘한 수줍음이나 적대감 같은 것을 그에게는 조금도 느끼지 않았다."], ["Hypocrisy in anything whatever may deceive the cleverest and most penetrating man, but the least wide-awake of children recognizes it, and is revolted by it, however ingeniously it may be disguised.", "무슨 일에서든 위선은 가장 영리하고 통찰력 있는 사람조차 속일 수 있지만, 아무리 둔한 아이라도 그것을 알아채고, 제아무리 교묘하게 꾸며 댄들 그것에 진저리를 친다."], ["Whatever faults Levin had, there was not a trace of hypocrisy in him, and so the children showed him the same friendliness that they saw in their mother’s face.", "레빈에게 어떤 결점이 있든, 그에게는 위선의 흔적이라곤 없었기에 아이들은 자기 어머니의 얼굴에서 본 것과 똑같은 친근함을 그에게 보였다."], ["On his invitation, the two elder ones at once jumped out to him and ran with him as simply as they would have done with their nurse or Miss Hoole or their mother.", "그의 청에, 큰 두 아이가 곧장 그에게 뛰어내려, 유모나 미스 훌이나 어머니와 함께할 때처럼 거리낌 없이 그와 함께 달렸다."], ["Lily, too, began begging to go to him, and her mother handed her to him; he sat her on his shoulder and ran along with her.", "릴리도 그에게 가겠다고 조르기 시작했고, 어머니가 그 애를 그에게 건네주었다. 그는 아이를 어깨에 앉히고 함께 달렸다."]], [["“Don’t be afraid, don’t be afraid, Darya Alexandrovna!”", "\"걱정 마세요, 걱정 마세요, 다리야 알렉산드로브나!\""], ["he said, smiling good-humoredly to the mother; “there’s no chance of my hurting or dropping her.”", "그가 어머니에게 사람 좋게 미소 지으며 말했다. \"이 애를 다치게 하거나 떨어뜨릴 일은 절대 없으니까요.\""]], [["And, looking at his strong, agile, assiduously careful and needlessly wary movements, the mother felt her mind at rest, and smiled gaily and approvingly as she watched him.", "그러고는 그의 힘차고 날렵하면서도 지나치다 싶을 만큼 조심스럽고 쓸데없이 신중한 동작을 바라보며 어머니는 마음이 놓였고, 그를 지켜보면서 즐겁게, 흐뭇하게 미소 지었다."]], [["Here, in the country, with children, and with Darya Alexandrovna, with whom he was in sympathy, Levin was in a mood not infrequent with him, of childlike light-heartedness that she particularly liked in him.", "이곳 시골에서, 아이들과 함께, 그리고 마음이 통하는 다리야 알렉산드로브나와 함께 있으니, 레빈은 그에게 드물지 않은, 그녀가 그에게서 특히 좋아하는 어린아이 같은 명랑한 기분에 젖어 있었다."], ["As he ran with the children, he taught them gymnastic feats, set Miss Hoole laughing with his queer English accent, and talked to Darya Alexandrovna of his pursuits in the country.", "그는 아이들과 달리면서 체조 묘기를 가르쳐 주었고, 우스꽝스러운 영어 발음으로 미스 훌을 웃겼으며, 다리야 알렉산드로브나에게는 시골에서 하는 자기 일에 대해 이야기했다."]], [["After dinner, Darya Alexandrovna, sitting alone with him on the balcony, began to speak of Kitty.", "저녁 식사 후, 다리야 알렉산드로브나는 발코니에서 그와 단둘이 앉아 키티 이야기를 꺼냈다."]], [["“You know, Kitty’s coming here, and is going to spend the summer with me.”", "\"있잖아요, 키티가 이리로 와서 저랑 여름을 보내기로 했어요.\""]], [["“Really,”", "\"정말입니까?\""], ["he said, flushing, and at once, to change the conversation, he said: “Then I’ll send you two cows, shall I?", "그가 얼굴을 붉히며 말하고는, 곧장 화제를 돌리려고 이렇게 말했다. \"그럼 젖소 두 마리를 보내 드릴까요?"], ["If you insist on a bill you shall pay me five roubles a month; but it’s really too bad of you.”", "굳이 값을 치르시겠다면 한 달에 5루블을 내시면 됩니다. 하지만 그건 정말 너무하신 거예요.\""]], [["“No, thank you.", "\"아니에요, 고마워요."], ["We can manage very well now.”", "지금은 아주 잘 꾸려 나가고 있는걸요.\""]], [["“Oh, well, then, I’ll have a look at your cows, and if you’ll allow me, I’ll give directions about their food.", "\"아, 그럼, 부인 댁 젖소들을 한번 살펴보겠습니다. 그리고 괜찮으시다면 그 녀석들 먹이에 대해 일러 드리지요."], ["Everything depends on their food.”", "모든 게 먹이에 달려 있거든요.\""]], [["And Levin, to turn the conversation, explained to Darya Alexandrovna the theory of cow-keeping, based on the principle that the cow is simply a machine for the transformation of food into milk, and so on.", "그러고서 레빈은 화제를 돌리려고, 젖소란 그저 먹이를 우유로 바꿔 주는 기계일 뿐이라는 원리에 근거한 젖소 사육 이론 따위를 다리야 알렉산드로브나에게 설명했다."]], [["He talked of this, and passionately longed to hear more of Kitty, and, at the same time, was afraid of hearing it.", "그는 이런 이야기를 하면서도, 키티에 대해 더 듣고 싶어 안달이 나면서도 동시에 그것을 듣게 될까 봐 두려웠다."], ["He dreaded the breaking up of the inward peace he had gained with such effort.", "그는 그토록 애써서 얻어 낸 내면의 평화가 깨지는 것이 무서웠다."]], [["“Yes, but still all this has to be looked after, and who is there to look after it?”", "\"네, 하지만 그래도 이 모든 걸 다 돌봐야 하는데, 그걸 돌볼 사람이 어디 있어야지요?\""], ["Darya Alexandrovna responded, without interest.", "다리야 알렉산드로브나가 무심하게 대꾸했다."]], [["She had by now got her household matters so satisfactorily arranged, thanks to Marya Philimonovna, that she was disinclined to make any change in them; besides, she had no faith in Levin’s knowledge of farming.", "그녀는 이제 마리야 필리모노브나 덕분에 집안일을 아주 만족스럽게 정돈해 놓은 터라, 거기에 어떤 변화도 주고 싶지 않았다. 게다가 그녀는 레빈의 농사 지식을 믿지 않았다."], ["General principles, as to the cow being a machine for the production of milk, she looked on with suspicion.", "젖소가 우유를 생산하는 기계라는 따위의 일반 원리를 그녀는 미심쩍게 여겼다."], ["It seemed to her that such principles could only be a hindrance in farm management.", "그런 원리란 농장 운영에 방해만 될 뿐이라고 그녀에게는 여겨졌다."], ["It all seemed to her a far simpler matter: all that was needed, as Marya Philimonovna had explained, was to give Brindle and Whitebreast more food and drink, and not to let the cook carry all the kitchen slops to the laundry maid’s cow.", "그녀에게는 모든 것이 훨씬 더 단순한 일로 보였다. 마리야 필리모노브나가 설명했듯이, 필요한 것이라곤 브린들과 화이트브레스트에게 먹이와 물을 더 주는 것, 그리고 요리사가 부엌의 음식 찌꺼기를 죄다 세탁부의 젖소에게 갖다 주지 못하게 하는 것뿐이었다."], ["That was clear.", "그건 분명한 일이었다."], ["But general propositions as to feeding on meal and on grass were doubtful and obscure.", "하지만 곡물 사료니 풀이니 하는 먹이에 관한 일반론은 미덥지 못하고 모호했다."], ["And, what was most important, she wanted to talk about Kitty.", "그리고 무엇보다 중요한 건, 그녀가 키티 이야기를 하고 싶어 한다는 것이었다."]], [["# Chapter 10", "# Chapter 10"]], [["“Kitty writes to me that there’s nothing she longs for so much as quiet and solitude,”", "\"키티가 편지에 쓰기를, 자기가 무엇보다 간절히 바라는 건 고요함과 혼자 있는 거래요.\""], ["Dolly said after the silence that had followed.", "침묵이 흐른 뒤에 돌리가 말했다."]], [["“And how is she—better?”", "\"그래서 그 애는 좀 어때요, 나아졌나요?\""], ["Levin asked in agitation.", "레빈이 마음 졸이며 물었다."]], [["“Thank God, she’s quite well again.", "\"고맙게도 이제는 아주 건강해졌어요."], ["I never believed her lungs were affected.”", "나는 그 애 폐가 나쁘다는 말을 한 번도 믿은 적이 없었어요.\""]], [["“Oh, I’m very glad!”", "\"오, 정말 기쁩니다!\""], ["said Levin, and Dolly fancied she saw something touching, helpless, in his face as he said this and looked silently into her face.", "레빈이 말했고, 그가 이렇게 말하며 말없이 자기 얼굴을 들여다보는 모습에서 돌리는 무언가 가슴을 찌르는 듯한, 어쩔 줄 모르는 기색을 본 것 같았다."]], [["“Let me ask you, Konstantin Dmitrievitch,”", "\"한 가지 물어봐도 될까요, 콘스탄틴 드미트리치.\""], ["said Darya Alexandrovna, smiling her kindly and rather mocking smile, “why is it you are angry with Kitty?”", "다리아 알렉산드로브나가 다정하면서도 조금은 놀리는 듯한 미소를 지으며 말했다. \"어째서 당신은 키티에게 화가 나 있는 거죠?\""]], [["“I?", "\"제가요?"], ["I’m not angry with her,”", "저는 그 애한테 화가 난 게 아닙니다.\""], ["said Levin.", "레빈이 말했다."]], [["“Yes, you are angry.", "\"아니에요, 화가 나 있어요."], ["Why was it you did not come to see us nor them when you were in Moscow?”", "모스크바에 있었을 때 왜 우리 집에도, 그 애들 집에도 오지 않았던 거죠?\""]], [["“Darya Alexandrovna,”", "\"다리아 알렉산드로브나.\""], ["he said, blushing up to the roots of his hair, “I wonder really that with your kind heart you don’t feel this.", "그가 머리끝까지 새빨개지며 말했다. \"당신처럼 마음씨 고운 분이 이걸 느끼지 못한다는 게 정말 놀랍군요."], ["How it is you feel no pity for me, if nothing else, when you know....”", "다른 건 몰라도, 당신이 알면서도 어떻게 저를 가엾게 여기지 않을 수가 있는지……\""]], [["“What do I know?”", "\"내가 뭘 안다는 거죠?\""]], [["“You know I made an offer and that I was refused,”", "\"제가 청혼을 했고 거절당했다는 걸 당신은 알고 있잖습니까.\""], ["said Levin, and all the tenderness he had been feeling for Kitty a minute before was replaced by a feeling of anger for the slight he had suffered.", "레빈이 말했고, 일 분 전까지 키티에게 느꼈던 온갖 애틋한 감정은 자기가 받은 모욕에 대한 분노로 바뀌어 버렸다."]], [["“What makes you suppose I know?”", "\"내가 안다고 어떻게 단정하는 거죠?\""]], [["“Because everybody knows it....”", "\"누구나 다 아는 일이니까요……\""]], [["“That’s just where you are mistaken; I did not know it, though I had guessed it was so.”", "\"바로 거기서 당신이 잘못 알고 있는 거예요. 나는 그 일을 몰랐어요. 그런 일이 있었으리라고 짐작만 했을 뿐이지.\""]], [["“Well, now you know it.”", "\"그럼, 이제 아셨군요.\""]], [["“All I knew was that something had happened that made her dreadfully miserable, and that she begged me never to speak of it.", "\"내가 알았던 거라곤, 그 애를 끔찍이 비참하게 만든 무슨 일이 있었다는 것, 그리고 그 일에 대해서는 절대 입에 올리지 말아 달라고 나한테 부탁했다는 것뿐이에요."], ["And if she would not tell me, she would certainly not speak of it to anyone else.", "나한테도 말하지 않으려 했으니, 다른 누구에게도 분명 말하지 않았을 거예요."], ["But what did pass between you?", "그런데 당신들 사이에 대체 무슨 일이 있었던 거죠?"], ["Tell me.”", "말해 봐요.\""]], [["“I have told you.”", "\"이미 말씀드렸습니다.\""]], [["“When was it?”", "\"그게 언제였죠?\""]], [["“When I was at their house the last time.”", "\"마지막으로 그 댁에 갔을 때였습니다.\""]], [["“Do you know that,”", "\"이거 아세요?\""], ["said Darya Alexandrovna, “I am awfully, awfully sorry for her.", "다리아 알렉산드로브나가 말했다. \"나는 그 애가 정말, 정말 가여워요."], ["You suffer only from pride....”", "당신은 그저 자존심 때문에 괴로워하는 것뿐이에요……\""]], [["“Perhaps so,”", "\"아마 그럴지도 모르죠.\""], ["said Levin, “but....”", "레빈이 말했다. \"하지만……\""]], [["She interrupted him.", "그녀가 그의 말을 잘랐다."]], [["“But she, poor girl ...", "\"하지만 그 애는, 가엾은 것이……"], ["I am awfully, awfully sorry for her.", "나는 그 애가 정말, 정말 가여워요."], ["Now I see it all.”", "이제야 모든 게 다 보이네요.\""]], [["“Well, Darya Alexandrovna, you must excuse me,”", "\"자, 다리아 알렉산드로브나, 이만 실례하겠습니다.\""], ["he said, getting up.", "그가 일어서며 말했다."], ["“Good-bye, Darya Alexandrovna, till we meet again.”", "\"안녕히 계세요, 다리아 알렉산드로브나, 다음에 또 뵙죠.\""]], [["“No, wait a minute,”", "\"아니, 잠깐만요.\""], ["she said, clutching him by the sleeve.", "그녀가 그의 소매를 붙잡으며 말했다."], ["“Wait a minute, sit down.”", "\"잠깐만, 앉아요.\""]], [["“Please, please, don’t let us talk of this,”", "\"제발, 제발 부탁이니 이 얘기는 그만하시죠.\""], ["he said, sitting down, and at the same time feeling rise up and stir within his heart a hope he had believed to be buried.", "그가 자리에 앉으며 말했다. 그러면서 동시에 묻혀 버렸다고 믿었던 어떤 희망이 가슴속에서 솟아올라 꿈틀거리는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["“If I did not like you,”", "\"내가 당신을 좋아하지 않았더라면,\""], ["she said, and tears came into her eyes; “if I did not know you, as I do know you....”", "그녀가 말했고 눈에는 눈물이 차올랐다. \"내가 지금처럼 당신을 잘 알지 못했더라면……\""]], [["The feeling that had seemed dead revived more and more, rose up and took possession of Levin’s heart.", "죽은 듯했던 그 감정이 점점 더 되살아나 솟구치더니 레빈의 가슴을 온통 사로잡았다."]], [["“Yes, I understand it all now,”", "\"그래요, 이제 모든 게 다 이해돼요.\""], ["said Darya Alexandrovna.", "다리아 알렉산드로브나가 말했다."], ["“You can’t understand it; for you men, who are free and make your own choice, it’s always clear whom you love.", "\"당신은 그걸 이해할 수 없어요. 자유로운 몸으로 스스로 선택하는 당신네 남자들에게는, 자기가 누구를 사랑하는지 늘 분명하니까요."], ["But a girl’s in a position of suspense, with all a woman’s or maiden’s modesty, a girl who sees you men from afar, who takes everything on trust,—a girl may have, and often has, such a feeling that she cannot tell what to say.”", "하지만 여자는 마음을 졸이며 기다리는 처지예요. 여자다운, 처녀다운 온갖 수줍음을 안고서, 당신네 남자들을 멀찍이서 바라보고, 모든 걸 그대로 믿어 버리는 처녀로서—처녀는 무어라 말해야 할지 알 수 없는 그런 감정을 품을 수도 있고, 또 흔히 품기도 하지요.\""]], [["“Yes, if the heart does not speak....”", "\"그렇죠, 마음이 말을 하지 않는다면……\""]], [["“No, the heart does speak; but just consider: you men have views about a girl, you come to the house, you make friends, you criticize, you wait to see if you have found what you love, and then, when you are sure you love her, you make an offer....”", "\"아니에요, 마음은 말을 해요. 하지만 한번 생각해 봐요. 당신네 남자들은 어떤 처녀를 두고 마음을 정하고, 그 집에 드나들고, 친해지고, 따져 보고, 자기가 사랑하는 상대를 찾았는지 두고 보다가, 그러고 나서 그 애를 사랑한다고 확신이 서면 청혼을 하잖아요……\""]], [["“Well, that’s not quite it.”", "\"뭐, 꼭 그렇지만은 않습니다.\""]], [["“Anyway you make an offer, when your love is ripe or when the balance has completely turned between the two you are choosing from.", "\"어쨌든 당신들은 사랑이 무르익었을 때, 아니면 저울질하던 두 사람 사이에서 마음이 완전히 한쪽으로 기울었을 때 청혼을 해요."], ["But a girl is not asked.", "하지만 처녀에게는 아무도 물어봐 주지 않아요."], ["She is expected to make her choice, and yet she cannot choose, she can only answer ‘yes’ or ‘no.’”", "처녀더러 스스로 선택하기를 바라면서도, 정작 처녀는 선택할 수가 없고, 그저 '예'나 '아니오'로 답할 수밖에 없지요.\""]], [["“Yes, to choose between me and Vronsky,”", "'그래, 나와 브론스키 사이에서 고르라는 거지.'"], ["thought Levin, and the dead thing that had come to life within him died again, and only weighed on his heart and set it aching.", "레빈은 생각했고, 그의 안에서 되살아났던 죽은 것이 다시 죽어 버려, 그저 가슴을 짓누르며 쓰라리게 할 뿐이었다."]], [["“Darya Alexandrovna,”", "\"다리아 알렉산드로브나,\""], ["he said, “that’s how one chooses a new dress or some purchase or other, not love.", "그가 말했다. \"그건 새 드레스나 무슨 물건을 고르는 방식이지, 사랑은 아닙니다."], ["The choice has been made, and so much the better....", "선택은 이미 끝났고, 그러는 편이 차라리 나아요……"], ["And there can be no repeating it.”", "다시 되풀이될 수도 없는 일이고요.\""]], [["“Ah, pride, pride!”", "\"아아, 자존심, 그놈의 자존심!\""], ["said Darya Alexandrovna, as though despising him for the baseness of this feeling in comparison with that other feeling which only women know.", "다리아 알렉산드로브나는 오직 여자만이 아는 저 다른 감정에 비하면 이런 감정의 천박함이 한심하다는 듯이 말했다."], ["“At the time when you made Kitty an offer she was just in a position in which she could not answer.", "\"당신이 키티에게 청혼했을 때, 그 애는 마침 대답을 할 수 없는 처지에 있었어요."], ["She was in doubt.", "망설이고 있었던 거죠."], ["Doubt between you and Vronsky.", "당신과 브론스키 사이에서요."], ["Him she was seeing every day, and you she had not seen for a long while.", "그 사람은 날마다 보고 있었고, 당신은 오랫동안 보지 못했으니까요."], ["Supposing she had been older ...", "그 애가 좀 더 나이가 들었더라면……"], ["I, for instance, in her place could have felt no doubt.", "가령 나라면, 그 애 입장에서 아무런 망설임도 느끼지 않았을 거예요."], ["I always disliked him, and so it has turned out.”", "나는 그 사람을 늘 싫어했고, 결국 이렇게 드러난 거잖아요.\""]], [["Levin recalled Kitty’s answer.", "레빈은 키티의 대답을 떠올렸다. 그녀는 이렇게 말했었다."], ["She had said: “No, that cannot be....”", "\"아니에요, 그건 있을 수 없는 일이에요……\""]], [["“Darya Alexandrovna,”", "\"다리아 알렉산드로브나,\""], ["he said dryly, “I appreciate your confidence in me; I believe you are making a mistake.", "그가 무뚝뚝하게 말했다. \"저를 믿어 주시는 마음은 고맙습니다만, 제가 보기엔 당신이 잘못 알고 계신 겁니다."], ["But whether I am right or wrong, that pride you so despise makes any thought of Katerina Alexandrovna out of the question for me,—you understand, utterly out of the question.”", "하지만 제가 옳든 그르든, 당신이 그토록 한심해하는 그 자존심 때문에 카체리나 알렉산드로브나에 대한 어떤 생각도 저로서는 도무지 가당치 않은 일이 되어 버렸습니다—아시겠습니까, 완전히 가당치 않은 일이라고요.\""]], [["“I will only say one thing more: you know that I am speaking of my sister, whom I love as I love my own children.", "\"한 가지만 더 말할게요. 내가 지금 말하고 있는 건 내 친동생, 내 자식들만큼이나 사랑하는 그 애에 관한 얘기라는 걸 알아주세요."], ["I don’t say she cared for you, all I meant to say is that her refusal at that moment proves nothing.”", "그 애가 당신을 마음에 두고 있었다고 말하려는 게 아니에요. 내가 하고 싶은 말은 그저, 그때 그 애가 당신을 거절했다는 것이 아무것도 증명하지 못한다는 거예요.\""]], [["“I don’t know!”", "\"모르겠습니다!\""], ["said Levin, jumping up.", "레빈이 벌떡 일어나며 말했다."], ["“If you only knew how you are hurting me.", "\"당신이 저를 얼마나 아프게 하고 있는지 아신다면."], ["It’s just as if a child of yours were dead, and they were to say to you: He would have been like this and like that, and he might have lived, and how happy you would have been in him.", "이건 마치 당신의 아이가 죽었는데, 사람들이 당신한테 이렇게 말하는 것과 똑같아요. 그 아이는 이렇게도 저렇게도 자랐을 거고, 살아 있었을 수도 있고, 그러면 당신이 그 애로 인해 얼마나 행복했겠느냐고요."], ["But he’s dead, dead, dead!...”", "하지만 그 애는 죽었어요, 죽었다고요, 죽어 버렸단 말입니다!……\""]], [["“How absurd you are!”", "\"당신은 어쩜 그리 엉뚱한지!\""], ["said Darya Alexandrovna, looking with mournful tenderness at Levin’s excitement.", "다리아 알렉산드로브나가 레빈의 흥분한 모습을 애틋하면서도 서글픈 눈길로 바라보며 말했다."], ["“Yes, I see it all more and more clearly,”", "\"그래요, 점점 더 모든 게 또렷이 보이네요.\""], ["she went on musingly.", "그녀가 생각에 잠겨 말을 이었다."], ["“So you won’t come to see us, then, when Kitty’s here?”", "\"그러니까 키티가 와 있을 때는 우리 집에 오지 않겠다는 거죠?\""]], [["“No, I shan’t come.", "\"네, 안 오겠습니다."], ["Of course I won’t avoid meeting Katerina Alexandrovna, but as far as I can, I will try to save her the annoyance of my presence.”", "물론 카체리나 알렉산드로브나와 마주치는 걸 피하겠다는 건 아닙니다만, 할 수 있는 한, 제 존재로 그 애를 불편하게 만드는 일은 없도록 애쓰겠습니다.\""]], [["“You are very, very absurd,”", "\"당신은 정말, 정말 엉뚱한 사람이에요.\""], ["repeated Darya Alexandrovna, looking with tenderness into his face.", "다리아 알렉산드로브나가 그의 얼굴을 애틋하게 들여다보며 거듭 말했다."], ["“Very well then, let it be as though we had not spoken of this.", "\"그럼 좋아요, 우리가 이 얘기를 꺼내지 않은 셈 칠게요."], ["What have you come for, Tanya?”", "무슨 일이니, 타냐?\""], ["she said in French to the little girl who had come in.", "그녀가 방으로 들어온 어린 딸에게 프랑스어로 말했다."]], [["“Where’s my spade, mamma?”", "\"엄마, 내 삽 어디 있어요?\""]], [["“I speak French, and you must too.”", "\"엄마는 프랑스어로 말하고 있잖니, 너도 그래야지.\""]], [["The little girl tried to say it in French, but could not remember the French for spade; the mother prompted her, and then told her in French where to look for the spade.", "어린 딸은 프랑스어로 말해 보려 했지만 삽이 프랑스어로 뭔지 떠올리지 못했다. 어머니가 일러 주었고, 이어서 어디서 삽을 찾으면 되는지 프랑스어로 알려 주었다."], ["And this made a disagreeable impression on Levin.", "그리고 이것이 레빈에게 거북한 인상을 남겼다."]], [["Everything in Darya Alexandrovna’s house and children struck him now as by no means so charming as a little while before.", "이제 다리아 알렉산드로브나의 집이며 아이들 안의 모든 것이, 조금 전과는 달리 도무지 사랑스럽게 느껴지지 않았다."], ["“And what does she talk French with the children for?”", "'대체 왜 아이들과 프랑스어로 말하는 걸까?' 그는 생각했다."], ["he thought; “how unnatural and false it is!", "'얼마나 부자연스럽고 거짓된 일인가!"], ["And the children feel it so: Learning French and unlearning sincerity,”", "아이들도 그걸 느끼고 있어. 프랑스어를 익히면서 진실함을 잃어 가는 거지.'"], ["he thought to himself, unaware that Darya Alexandrovna had thought all that over twenty times already, and yet, even at the cost of some loss of sincerity, believed it necessary to teach her children French in that way.", "그는 속으로 이렇게 생각했지만, 다리아 알렉산드로브나가 이미 그 모든 걸 스무 번도 넘게 곱씹어 보았다는 것, 그리고 진실함을 다소 잃는 대가를 치르더라도 자기 아이들에게 그런 식으로 프랑스어를 가르치는 게 필요하다고 믿고 있다는 것은 알지 못했다."]], [["“But why are you going?", "\"그런데 왜 가시려고요?"], ["Do stay a little.”", "좀 더 계세요.\""]], [["Levin stayed to tea; but his good-humor had vanished, and he felt ill at ease.", "레빈은 차를 마시려고 남았다. 하지만 그의 좋던 기분은 사라져 버렸고, 마음이 영 편치 않았다."]], [["After tea he went out into the hall to order his horses to be put in, and, when he came back, he found Darya Alexandrovna greatly disturbed, with a troubled face, and tears in her eyes.", "차를 마신 뒤 그는 말을 매어 두라고 일러 두려고 현관으로 나갔다가, 돌아와 보니 다리아 알렉산드로브나가 몹시 심란한 얼굴로, 근심에 찬 표정에 눈에는 눈물이 고인 채 있었다."], ["While Levin had been outside, an incident had occurred which had utterly shattered all the happiness she had been feeling that day, and her pride in her children.", "레빈이 밖에 나가 있는 동안, 그날 하루 그녀가 느끼던 행복과 아이들에 대한 자부심을 송두리째 산산조각 내 버린 사건이 일어났던 것이다."], ["Grisha and Tanya had been fighting over a ball.", "그리샤와 타냐가 공을 두고 싸웠다."], ["Darya Alexandrovna, hearing a scream in the nursery, ran in and saw a terrible sight.", "다리아 알렉산드로브나는 아이들 방에서 비명 소리를 듣고 달려갔다가 끔찍한 광경을 보았다."], ["Tanya was pulling Grisha’s hair, while he, with a face hideous with rage, was beating her with his fists wherever he could get at her.", "타냐는 그리샤의 머리채를 잡아당기고 있었고, 그리샤는 분노로 일그러진 흉측한 얼굴로 닥치는 대로 주먹질을 해 대고 있었다."], ["Something snapped in Darya Alexandrovna’s heart when she saw this.", "이 광경을 본 순간 다리아 알렉산드로브나의 가슴속에서 무언가가 툭 끊어졌다."], ["It was as if darkness had swooped down upon her life; she felt that these children of hers, that she was so proud of, were not merely most ordinary, but positively bad, ill-bred children, with coarse, brutal propensities—wicked children.", "마치 그녀의 삶 위로 어둠이 덮쳐 내린 것 같았다. 자기가 그토록 자랑스러워하던 이 아이들이 그저 더없이 평범한 정도가 아니라, 거칠고 잔인한 성정을 지닌, 영락없이 못되고 버르장머리 없는 아이들—심술궂은 아이들이라는 생각이 들었던 것이다."]], [["She could not talk or think of anything else, and she could not speak to Levin of her misery.", "그녀는 다른 어떤 것도 입에 올리거나 생각할 수가 없었고, 자기의 비참한 심정을 레빈에게 털어놓을 수도 없었다."]], [["Levin saw she was unhappy and tried to comfort her, saying that it showed nothing bad, that all children fight; but, even as he said it, he was thinking in his heart: “No, I won’t be artificial and talk French with my children; but my children won’t be like that.", "레빈은 그녀가 괴로워하는 것을 보고 위로하려 애쓰며, 그건 나쁜 게 아니라고, 아이들은 다 싸운다고 말했다. 하지만 그렇게 말하면서도 속으로는 이렇게 생각하고 있었다."], ["All one has to do is not spoil children, not to distort their nature, and they’ll be delightful.", "'아니야, 나는 가식적으로 굴며 내 아이들과 프랑스어로 얘기하지는 않을 거야. 하지만 내 아이들은 저렇지 않을 거야."], ["No, my children won’t be like that.”", "그저 아이들을 망쳐 놓지 않고, 그 천성을 비뚤어지게 하지만 않으면, 아이들은 사랑스러워질 테니까. 그래, 내 아이들은 저렇지 않을 거야.'"]], [["He said good-bye and drove away, and she did not try to keep him.", "그는 작별 인사를 하고 마차를 몰아 떠났고, 그녀는 그를 붙잡으려 하지 않았다."]], [["# Chapter 11", "# Chapter 11"]], [["In the middle of July the elder of the village on Levin’s sister’s estate, about fifteen miles from Pokrovskoe, came to Levin to report on how things were going there and on the hay.", "7월 중순, 포크로프스코예에서 십오 베르스타쯤 떨어진 레빈의 누이 영지의 촌장이 그곳 형편과 건초에 관해 보고하러 레빈을 찾아왔다."], ["The chief source of income on his sister’s estate was from the riverside meadows.", "누이 영지의 주된 수입원은 강가의 풀밭이었다."], ["In former years the hay had been bought by the peasants for twenty roubles the three acres.", "예전에는 농부들이 삼 데샤티나당 이십 루블에 건초를 사들였다."], ["When Levin took over the management of the estate, he thought on examining the grasslands that they were worth more, and he fixed the price at twenty-five roubles the three acres.", "레빈이 영지 관리를 맡게 되었을 때, 그는 초지를 살펴보고 그것이 그 이상의 값어치가 있다고 판단하여 삼 데샤티나당 이십오 루블로 값을 매겼다."], ["The peasants would not give that price, and, as Levin suspected, kept off other purchasers.", "농부들은 그 값을 치르려 하지 않았고, 레빈이 짐작한 대로 다른 매수인들이 접근하지 못하게 막았다."], ["Then Levin had driven over himself, and arranged to have the grass cut, partly by hired labor, partly at a payment of a certain proportion of the crop.", "그러자 레빈은 손수 마차를 몰고 가, 일부는 품팔이 일꾼을 써서, 일부는 수확량의 일정 몫을 품삯으로 치르는 방식으로 풀을 베도록 손을 써 두었다."], ["His own peasants put every hindrance they could in the way of this new arrangement, but it was carried out, and the first year the meadows had yielded a profit almost double.", "그의 농부들은 이 새로운 방식을 두고 갖은 방해를 다 부렸지만, 그래도 그 일은 이루어졌고, 첫해에 풀밭은 거의 두 배에 가까운 이익을 냈다."], ["The previous year—which was the third year—the peasants had maintained the same opposition to the arrangement, and the hay had been cut on the same system.", "지난해—그러니까 세 번째 해—에도 농부들은 그 방식에 똑같이 반대했고, 건초는 같은 방식으로 베어졌다."], ["This year the peasants were doing all the mowing for a third of the hay crop, and the village elder had come now to announce that the hay had been cut, and that, fearing rain, they had invited the counting-house clerk over, had divided the crop in his presence, and had raked together eleven stacks as the owner’s share.", "올해 농부들은 건초 수확량의 삼분의 일을 받는 조건으로 풀베기를 도맡았고, 촌장이 이제 와서 건초를 다 베었으며, 비가 올까 봐 회계 사무소의 서기를 불러다가 그가 보는 앞에서 수확물을 나누었고, 주인 몫으로 열한 더미를 긁어모아 쌓아 두었다고 알리러 온 것이었다."], ["From the vague answers to his question how much hay had been cut on the principal meadow, from the hurry of the village elder who had made the division, not asking leave, from the whole tone of the peasant, Levin perceived that there was something wrong in the division of the hay, and made up his mind to drive over himself to look into the matter.", "주된 풀밭에서 건초를 얼마나 베었느냐는 물음에 대한 모호한 대답에서, 허락도 구하지 않고 분배를 해 버린 촌장의 서두르는 태도에서, 그 농부의 말투 전체에서, 레빈은 건초 분배에 무언가 미심쩍은 구석이 있음을 알아채고는 손수 마차를 몰고 가 그 일을 들여다보기로 마음먹었다."]], [["Arriving for dinner at the village, and leaving his horse at the cottage of an old friend of his, the husband of his brother’s wet-nurse, Levin went to see the old man in his bee-house, wanting to find out from him the truth about the hay.", "마침 만찬 시간에 맞춰 마을에 다다른 레빈은, 옛 친구이자 형의 유모 남편인 노인의 오두막에 말을 맡겨 두고, 건초에 관한 진실을 캐낼 작정으로 그 노인을 만나러 양봉장으로 갔다."], ["Parmenitch, a talkative, comely old man, gave Levin a very warm welcome, showed him all he was doing, told him everything about his bees and the swarms of that year; but gave vague and unwilling answers to Levin’s inquiries about the mowing.", "말 많고 잘생긴 노인 파르메니치는 레빈을 무척 따뜻하게 맞이하며 자기가 하는 일을 죄다 보여 주었고, 자기 벌들과 그해의 분봉에 관해 온갖 이야기를 들려주었다. 그러나 풀베기에 관한 레빈의 물음에는 모호하고 마지못한 대답만 했다."], ["This confirmed Levin still more in his suspicions.", "이것이 레빈의 의심을 한층 더 굳혀 주었다."], ["He went to the hay fields and examined the stacks.", "그는 건초밭으로 가 더미들을 살펴보았다."], ["The haystacks could not possibly contain fifty wagon-loads each, and to convict the peasants Levin ordered the wagons that had carried the hay to be brought up directly, to lift one stack, and carry it into the barn.", "건초 더미가 한 더미당 쉰 짐을 담고 있을 리가 없었다. 농부들의 거짓을 밝혀내기 위해 레빈은 건초를 실어 나른 짐수레들을 곧장 끌고 오게 해서, 더미 하나를 들어 올려 곳간으로 실어 나르라고 일렀다."], ["There turned out to be only thirty-two loads in the stack.", "그 더미에는 서른두 짐밖에 들어 있지 않은 것으로 드러났다."], ["In spite of the village elder’s assertions about the compressibility of hay, and its having settled down in the stacks, and his swearing that everything had been done in the fear of God, Levin stuck to his point that the hay had been divided without his orders, and that, therefore, he would not accept that hay as fifty loads to a stack.", "건초가 잘 눌리고 더미 속에서 가라앉는 법이라는 촌장의 주장에도, 모든 일을 하느님을 두려워하는 마음으로 했다는 맹세에도 아랑곳없이, 레빈은 건초가 자기 지시 없이 분배되었으며 따라서 그 건초를 한 더미당 쉰 짐으로는 인정할 수 없다는 자기 주장을 굽히지 않았다."], ["After a prolonged dispute the matter was decided by the peasants taking these eleven stacks, reckoning them as fifty loads each.", "한참 옥신각신한 끝에, 농부들이 그 열한 더미를 한 더미당 쉰 짐으로 쳐서 가져가는 것으로 일은 매듭지어졌다."], ["The arguments and the division of the haycocks lasted the whole afternoon.", "옥신각신과 건초 더미 분배는 오후 내내 이어졌다."], ["When the last of the hay had been divided, Levin, intrusting the superintendence of the rest to the counting-house clerk, sat down on a haycock marked off by a stake of willow, and looked admiringly at the meadow swarming with peasants.", "마지막 건초까지 다 나누어지자, 레빈은 나머지 감독을 회계 사무소 서기에게 맡기고는 버드나무 말뚝으로 표시해 둔 건초 더미 위에 앉아, 농부들로 북적이는 풀밭을 흐뭇하게 바라보았다."]], [["In front of him, in the bend of the river beyond the marsh, moved a bright-colored line of peasant women, and the scattered hay was being rapidly formed into gray winding rows over the pale green stubble.", "그의 앞쪽, 늪 너머 강이 굽이지는 곳에는 알록달록한 농촌 아낙네들의 행렬이 움직이고 있었고, 흩어져 있던 건초는 연초록 그루터기 위로 잿빛으로 구불구불 이어지는 줄로 빠르게 모아지고 있었다."], ["After the women came the men with pitchforks, and from the gray rows there were growing up broad, high, soft haycocks.", "아낙네들 뒤로는 쇠스랑을 든 남자들이 따랐고, 그 잿빛 줄에서 넓고 높고 부드러운 건초 더미가 자라났다."], ["To the left, carts were rumbling over the meadow that had been already cleared, and one after another the haycocks vanished, flung up in huge forkfuls, and in their place there were rising heavy cartloads of fragrant hay hanging over the horses’ hind-quarters.", "왼편에서는 이미 치워진 풀밭 위로 짐수레들이 덜컹거리며 지나갔고, 건초 더미들은 거대한 쇠스랑 가득 퍼올려져 하나둘 사라졌으며, 그 자리에는 향긋한 건초를 잔뜩 실어 말들의 엉덩이 위로 늘어뜨린 묵직한 짐수레들이 솟아오르고 있었다."]], [["“What weather for haying!", "\"건초 말리기에 딱 좋은 날씨군!"], ["What hay it’ll be!”", "이게 무슨 건초람!\""], ["said an old man, squatting down beside Levin.", "레빈 곁에 쪼그려 앉으며 한 노인이 말했다."], ["“It’s tea, not hay!", "\"이건 건초가 아니라 차라니까!"], ["It’s like scattering grain to the ducks, the way they pick it up!”", "사람들이 거두어들이는 꼴이 꼭 오리한테 곡식 흩뿌려 주는 것 같구먼!\""], ["he added, pointing to the growing haycocks.", "그는 자라나는 건초 더미들을 가리키며 덧붙였다."], ["“Since dinner time they’ve carried a good half of it.”", "\"만찬 때부터 벌써 절반 넘게 실어 날랐다오.\""]], [["“The last load, eh?”", "\"저게 마지막 짐인가?\""], ["he shouted to a young peasant, who drove by, standing in the front of an empty cart, shaking the cord reins.", "그는 빈 수레 앞쪽에 서서 새끼줄 고삐를 흔들며 지나가는 젊은 농부에게 소리쳤다."]], [["“The last, dad!”", "\"마지막 짐이에요, 아버지!\""], ["the lad shouted back, pulling in the horse, and, smiling, he looked round at a bright, rosy-cheeked peasant girl who sat in the cart smiling too, and drove on.", "젊은이가 말을 멈춰 세우며 마주 소리치고는, 빙긋 웃으며 수레에 앉아 역시 웃고 있는 발그레한 뺨의 싱그러운 처녀를 돌아보고는 다시 몰고 갔다."]], [["“Who’s that?", "\"누군가?"], ["Your son?”", "자네 아들인가?\""], ["asked Levin.", "레빈이 물었다."]], [["“My baby,”", "\"제 막내랍니다.\""], ["said the old man with a tender smile.", "노인이 다정한 미소를 지으며 말했다."]], [["“What a fine fellow!”", "\"참 훌륭한 청년이군!\""]], [["“The lad’s all right.”", "\"그 녀석, 괜찮은 놈이지요.\""]], [["“Married already?”", "\"벌써 장가들었나?\""]], [["“Yes, it’s two years last St. Philip’s day.”", "\"예, 지난 성 필립 축일로 두 해째랍니다.\""]], [["“Any children?”", "\"아이는 있고?\""]], [["“Children indeed!", "\"아이라뇨!"], ["Why, for over a year he was innocent as a babe himself, and bashful too,” answered the old man.", "아, 그 녀석은 일 년이 넘도록 제가 갓난애처럼 숫총각인 데다 부끄럼까지 타던걸요.\""], ["“Well, the hay!", "노인이 대답했다."], ["It’s as fragrant as tea!”", "\"원, 이 건초 좀 보시오!"], ["he repeated, wishing to change the subject.", "차처럼 향긋하다니까!\" 그는 화제를 바꾸고 싶어 거듭 말했다."]], [["Levin looked more attentively at Ivan Parmenov and his wife.", "레빈은 이반 파르메노프와 그의 아내를 좀 더 유심히 바라보았다."], ["They were loading a haycock onto the cart not far from him.", "두 사람은 레빈에게서 멀지 않은 곳에서 건초 더미를 수레에 싣고 있었다."], ["Ivan Parmenov was standing on the cart, taking, laying in place, and stamping down the huge bundles of hay, which his pretty young wife deftly handed up to him, at first in armfuls, and then on the pitchfork.", "이반 파르메노프는 수레 위에 서서, 예쁘고 젊은 아내가 처음에는 한 아름씩, 그다음에는 쇠스랑으로 능숙하게 건네 올리는 커다란 건초 다발을 받아 제자리에 놓고 밟아 다졌다."], ["The young wife worked easily, merrily, and dexterously.", "젊은 아내는 가뿐하고 흥겹고 야무지게 일했다."], ["The close-packed hay did not once break away off her fork.", "단단히 뭉친 건초는 그녀의 쇠스랑에서 한 번도 흘러내리지 않았다."], ["First she gathered it together, stuck the fork into it, then with a rapid, supple movement leaned the whole weight of her body on it, and at once with a bend of her back under the red belt she drew herself up, and arching her full bosom under the white smock, with a smart turn swung the fork in her arms, and flung the bundle of hay high onto the cart.", "그녀는 먼저 건초를 그러모은 다음 그 안에 쇠스랑을 꽂고, 빠르고 나긋한 동작으로 온몸의 무게를 거기에 실었다가, 곧바로 붉은 허리띠 아래 등을 굽혀 몸을 일으키고는, 흰 작업복 아래 풍만한 가슴을 활처럼 내밀며 날렵하게 몸을 틀어 두 팔로 쇠스랑을 휘둘러 건초 다발을 수레 위로 높이 던져 올렸다."], ["Ivan, obviously doing his best to save her every minute of unnecessary labor, made haste, opening his arms to clutch the bundle and lay it in the cart.", "이반은 아내가 쓸데없이 힘쓰는 순간을 단 일 분이라도 덜어 주려고 분명 애를 쓰면서, 다발을 붙잡아 수레에 놓으려고 두 팔을 벌린 채 서둘렀다."], ["As she raked together what was left of the hay, the young wife shook off the bits of hay that had fallen on her neck, and straightening the red kerchief that had dropped forward over her white brow, not browned like her face by the sun, she crept under the cart to tie up the load.", "남은 건초를 긁어모으던 젊은 아내는 목덜미에 떨어진 건초 부스러기를 털어 내고는, 햇볕에 그을린 얼굴과는 달리 햇볕을 타지 않은 흰 이마 위로 흘러내린 붉은 머릿수건을 바로잡고서, 짐을 묶으려고 수레 밑으로 기어들어 갔다. 이반은 가로대에 새끼줄을 어떻게 매면 되는지 일러 주었고, 그녀가 뭐라고 한마디 하자 큰 소리로 웃음을 터뜨렸다."], ["Ivan directed her how to fasten the cord to the cross-piece, and at something she said he laughed aloud.", "두 사람의 얼굴 표정에는 힘차고 젊고 갓 깨어난 사랑이 어려 있었다. * 베르스타: 러시아의 거리 단위로 약 1.06킬로미터."], ["In the expressions of both faces was to be seen vigorous, young, freshly awakened love.", "* 데샤티나: 러시아의 토지 면적 단위로 약 1.09헥타르."]], [["# Chapter 12", "# Chapter 12"]], [["The load was tied on.", "짐이 다 묶였다."], ["Ivan jumped down and took the quiet, sleek horse by the bridle.", "이반이 뛰어내려 얌전하고 윤기 흐르는 말을 고삐로 잡았다."], ["The young wife flung the rake up on the load, and with a bold step, swinging her arms, she went to join the women, who were forming a ring for the haymakers’ dance.", "젊은 아내는 갈퀴를 짐 위로 휙 던져 올리고는, 두 팔을 휘저으며 당찬 걸음으로 건초 일꾼들의 춤판을 만들려고 둥글게 모여드는 여인들 쪽으로 갔다."], ["Ivan drove off to the road and fell into line with the other loaded carts.", "이반은 길로 마차를 몰고 나가 짐을 실은 다른 수레들과 줄을 맞추었다."], ["The peasant women, with their rakes on their shoulders, gay with bright flowers, and chattering with ringing, merry voices, walked behind the hay cart.", "여인들은 갈퀴를 어깨에 메고 화사한 꽃으로 곱게 꾸민 채, 낭랑하고 흥겨운 목소리로 재잘대며 건초 수레 뒤를 따라 걸었다."], ["One wild untrained female voice broke into a song, and sang it alone through a verse, and then the same verse was taken up and repeated by half a hundred strong healthy voices, of all sorts, coarse and fine, singing in unison.", "거칠고 다듬어지지 않은 한 여자의 목소리가 노래를 터뜨려 한 소절을 혼자 부르고 나자, 거칠고 고운, 온갖 종류의 건강한 목소리 쉰여 개가 그 같은 소절을 받아 한목소리로 되풀이해 불렀다."]], [["The women, all singing, began to come close to Levin, and he felt as though a storm were swooping down upon him with a thunder of merriment.", "여인들은 모두 노래를 부르며 레빈에게 가까이 다가오기 시작했고, 그는 마치 흥겨움의 천둥소리를 몰고 폭풍이 자기에게 덮쳐 오는 것 같은 기분이 들었다."], ["The storm swooped down, enveloped him and the haycock on which he was lying, and the other haycocks, and the wagon-loads, and the whole meadow and distant fields all seemed to be shaking and singing to the measures of this wild merry song with its shouts and whistles and clapping.", "폭풍이 덮쳐 와 그와 그가 누워 있던 건초 더미를, 그리고 다른 건초 더미들을, 짐을 실은 수레들을, 풀밭 전체와 멀리 떨어진 들판까지를 온통 감쌌고, 모든 것이 고함과 휘파람과 박수가 어우러진 이 거칠고 흥겨운 노래의 가락에 맞추어 흔들리며 노래하는 듯했다."], ["Levin felt envious of this health and mirthfulness; he longed to take part in the expression of this joy of life.", "레빈은 이 건강함과 즐거움이 부러웠다. 그는 이 삶의 기쁨을 표현하는 데에 끼고 싶었다."], ["But he could do nothing, and had to lie and look on and listen.", "그러나 그는 아무것도 할 수 없었고, 그저 누워서 바라보고 귀를 기울일 수밖에 없었다."], ["When the peasants, with their singing, had vanished out of sight and hearing, a weary feeling of despondency at his own isolation, his physical inactivity, his alienation from this world, came over Levin.", "노래하는 농부들이 시야와 귓전에서 사라지고 나자, 자기만 따로 떨어져 있다는 것, 자기 몸이 아무 일도 하지 않고 있다는 것, 자기가 이 세계로부터 동떨어져 있다는 것에 대한 지친 낙담의 감정이 레빈에게 밀려들었다."]], [["Some of the very peasants who had been most active in wrangling with him over the hay, some whom he had treated with contumely, and who had tried to cheat him, those very peasants had greeted him good-humoredly, and evidently had not, were incapable of having any feeling of rancor against him, any regret, any recollection even of having tried to deceive him.", "건초 문제를 놓고 그와 가장 극성스럽게 옥신각신했던 바로 그 농부들 가운데 몇몇, 그가 모욕적으로 대했던 몇몇, 그를 속이려 들었던 몇몇, 바로 그 농부들이 그를 흔쾌히 맞아 주었고, 그들에게는 그에 대한 어떤 원한도, 어떤 후회도, 그를 속이려 했다는 기억조차도 분명 없었으며 또 있을 수가 없었다."], ["All that was drowned in a sea of merry common labor.", "그 모든 것이 흥겨운 공동 노동의 바다 속에 잠겨 버린 것이다."], ["God gave the day, God gave the strength.", "신이 하루를 주었고, 신이 힘을 주었다."], ["And the day and the strength were consecrated to labor, and that labor was its own reward.", "그리고 그 하루와 그 힘은 노동에 바쳐졌으며, 그 노동 자체가 그 보상이었다."], ["For whom the labor?", "누구를 위한 노동인가?"], ["What would be its fruits?", "그것의 결실은 무엇이 될 것인가?"], ["These were idle considerations—beside the point.", "이런 것은 부질없는 생각이었고, 요점에서 벗어난 것이었다."]], [["Often Levin had admired this life, often he had a sense of envy of the men who led this life; but today for the first time, especially under the influence of what he had seen in the attitude of Ivan Parmenov to his young wife, the idea presented itself definitely to his mind that it was in his power to exchange the dreary, artificial, idle, and individualistic life he was leading for this laborious, pure, and socially delightful life.", "레빈은 종종 이런 삶을 동경했고, 종종 이런 삶을 사는 이들에게 부러움을 느꼈다. 그러나 오늘 처음으로, 특히 이반 파르메노프가 자기 젊은 아내를 대하던 태도에서 본 것의 영향으로, 자기가 살고 있는 따분하고 인위적이며 게으르고 개인주의적인 삶을 이 노동으로 가득 차고 순수하며 함께 어울려 즐거운 삶과 맞바꾸는 것이 자기 능력 안에 있다는 생각이 또렷이 그의 머릿속에 떠올랐다."]], [["The old man who had been sitting beside him had long ago gone home; the people had all separated.", "그의 곁에 앉아 있던 노인은 이미 오래전에 집으로 돌아갔다. 사람들은 모두 흩어졌다."], ["Those who lived near had gone home, while those who came from far were gathered into a group for supper, and to spend the night in the meadow.", "가까이 사는 이들은 집으로 갔고, 멀리서 온 이들은 저녁을 먹고 풀밭에서 밤을 지내려고 한 무리로 모여 있었다."], ["Levin, unobserved by the peasants, still lay on the haycock, and still looked on and listened and mused.", "레빈은 농부들 눈에 띄지 않은 채 여전히 건초 더미 위에 누워, 여전히 바라보고 귀 기울이며 사색에 잠겨 있었다."], ["The peasants who remained for the night in the meadow scarcely slept all the short summer night.", "풀밭에서 밤을 지내는 농부들은 짧은 여름밤 내내 거의 잠을 이루지 못했다."], ["At first there was the sound of merry talk and laughing all together over the supper, then singing again and laughter.", "처음에는 저녁을 먹으며 다 함께 흥겹게 이야기하고 웃는 소리가 들렸고, 그다음에는 다시 노래와 웃음이 이어졌다."]], [["All the long day of toil had left no trace in them but lightness of heart.", "고된 노동으로 가득했던 그 긴 하루는 그들에게 마음의 가벼움 말고는 아무런 흔적도 남기지 않았다."], ["Before the early dawn all was hushed.", "이른 새벽이 오기 전 모든 것이 고요해졌다."], ["Nothing was to be heard but the night sounds of the frogs that never ceased in the marsh, and the horses snorting in the mist that rose over the meadow before the morning.", "늪에서 끊임없이 울어 대는 개구리들의 밤 소리와, 아침을 앞두고 풀밭 위로 피어오르는 안개 속에서 말들이 콧김을 내뿜는 소리 말고는 아무것도 들리지 않았다."], ["Rousing himself, Levin got up from the haycock, and looking at the stars, he saw that the night was over.", "레빈은 정신을 차리고 건초 더미에서 일어나 별들을 바라보다가, 밤이 다 지났음을 알았다."]], [["“Well, what am I going to do?", "\"자, 나는 어떻게 해야 하지?"], ["How am I to set about it?”", "어떻게 손을 대야 하나?\""], ["he said to himself, trying to express to himself all the thoughts and feelings he had passed through in that brief night.", "그는 그 짧은 밤 동안 거쳐 온 모든 생각과 감정을 스스로에게 표현해 보려 애쓰며 혼잣말을 했다."], ["All the thoughts and feelings he had passed through fell into three separate trains of thought.", "그가 거쳐 온 모든 생각과 감정은 세 갈래의 서로 다른 사고의 흐름으로 나뉘었다."], ["One was the renunciation of his old life, of his utterly useless education.", "하나는 옛 삶에 대한, 자기의 더할 나위 없이 쓸모없는 교육에 대한 단념이었다."], ["This renunciation gave him satisfaction, and was easy and simple.", "이 단념은 그에게 만족감을 주었고, 쉽고도 단순했다."], ["Another series of thoughts and mental images related to the life he longed to live now.", "또 다른 갈래의 생각과 마음속 이미지들은 이제 그가 살고 싶어 하는 삶과 관련된 것이었다."], ["The simplicity, the purity, the sanity of this life he felt clearly, and he was convinced he would find in it the content, the peace, and the dignity, of the lack of which he was so miserably conscious.", "이 삶의 단순함과 순수함과 건전함을 그는 또렷이 느꼈고, 자기가 그토록 비참하게 결핍을 의식하고 있던 그 만족과 평안과 존엄을 그 안에서 찾을 수 있으리라고 확신했다."], ["But a third series of ideas turned upon the question how to effect this transition from the old life to the new.", "그러나 세 번째 갈래의 생각은 옛 삶에서 새 삶으로의 이 이행을 어떻게 이루어 낼 것인가 하는 문제로 돌아갔다."], ["And there nothing took clear shape for him.", "그리고 거기에서는 아무것도 그에게 또렷한 형태를 갖추지 못했다."], ["“Have a wife?", "\"아내를 얻을까?"], ["Have work and the necessity of work?", "일을, 그리고 일을 해야 할 필연을 가질까?"], ["Leave Pokrovskoe? Buy land?", "포크롭스코예를 떠날까?"], ["Become a member of a peasant community?", "땅을 살까? 농민 공동체의 일원이 될까?"], ["Marry a peasant girl?", "농촌 처녀와 결혼할까?"], ["How am I to set about it?”", "어떻게 손을 대야 하지?\""], ["he asked himself again, and could not find an answer.", "그는 다시 스스로에게 물었지만, 답을 찾을 수 없었다."], ["“I haven’t slept all night, though, and I can’t think it out clearly,”", "\"그래도 밤새 한숨도 못 잤으니, 또렷하게 생각해 낼 수가 없군.\""], ["he said to himself.", "그는 혼잣말을 했다."], ["“I’ll work it out later.", "\"나중에 풀어 봐야지."], ["One thing’s certain, this night has decided my fate.", "한 가지는 분명해, 이 밤이 내 운명을 결정했다는 것."], ["All my old dreams of home life were absurd, not the real thing,”", "가정생활에 대한 내 옛 꿈은 모두 터무니없었어, 진짜가 아니었지.\""], ["he told himself.", "그는 스스로에게 말했다."], ["“It’s all ever so much simpler and better....”", "\"전부 훨씬 더 단순하고 더 나은 거야……\""]], [["“How beautiful!”", "\"어찌 이리 아름다운가!\""], ["he thought, looking at the strange, as it were, mother-of-pearl shell of white fleecy cloudlets resting right over his head in the middle of the sky.", "그는 하늘 한복판 바로 머리 위에 떠 있는, 어쩐지 진주조개 껍데기 같은 희고 보송보송한 작은 구름들을 바라보며 생각했다."], ["“How exquisite it all is in this exquisite night!", "\"이 황홀한 밤에 모든 것이 어찌 이토록 황홀한가!"], ["And when was there time for that cloud-shell to form?", "그런데 저 구름 껍데기가 만들어질 틈이 언제 있었던 거지?"], ["Just now I looked at the sky, and there was nothing in it—only two white streaks.", "방금만 해도 하늘을 보았을 때는 아무것도 없었는데—그저 흰 줄기 두 가닥뿐이었지."], ["Yes, and so imperceptibly too my views of life changed!”", "그래, 내 삶에 대한 생각도 이렇게 알아채지 못하는 사이에 바뀐 거야!\""]], [["He went out of the meadow and walked along the highroad towards the village.", "그는 풀밭을 나와 마을 쪽으로 한길을 따라 걸었다."], ["A slight wind arose, and the sky looked gray and sullen.", "가벼운 바람이 일었고, 하늘은 잿빛으로 음울해 보였다."], ["The gloomy moment had come that usually precedes the dawn, the full triumph of light over darkness.", "흔히 동틀 녘, 빛이 어둠을 완전히 이기기 직전에 찾아오는 그 음울한 순간이 온 것이다."]], [["Shrinking from the cold, Levin walked rapidly, looking at the ground.", "추위에 몸을 움츠리며 레빈은 땅을 내려다본 채 빠르게 걸었다."], ["“What’s that?", "\"저게 뭐지?"], ["Someone coming,”", "누가 오는군.\""], ["he thought, catching the tinkle of bells, and lifting his head.", "그는 방울 소리를 알아채고 고개를 들며 생각했다."], ["Forty paces from him a carriage with four horses harnessed abreast was driving towards him along the grassy road on which he was walking.", "그에게서 마흔 걸음쯤 떨어진 곳에서, 말 네 마리를 나란히 맨 마차 한 대가 그가 걷고 있던 풀이 난 길을 따라 그를 향해 달려오고 있었다."], ["The shaft-horses were tilted against the shafts by the ruts, but the dexterous driver sitting on the box held the shaft over the ruts, so that the wheels ran on the smooth part of the road.", "끌채에 맨 말들은 바큇자국 때문에 끌채 쪽으로 기우뚱했지만, 마부석에 앉은 솜씨 좋은 마부가 끌채를 바큇자국 위로 들어 올려, 바퀴가 길의 평평한 부분으로 굴러가게 했다."]], [["This was all Levin noticed, and without wondering who it could be, he gazed absently at the coach.", "레빈이 알아챈 것은 이것뿐이었고, 그것이 누구일까 궁금해하지도 않은 채 그는 멍하니 마차를 바라보았다."]], [["In the coach was an old lady dozing in one corner, and at the window, evidently only just awake, sat a young girl holding in both hands the ribbons of a white cap.", "마차 안에는 한쪽 구석에서 졸고 있는 노부인이 있었고, 창가에는 분명 막 잠에서 깬 듯한 젊은 처녀가 흰 모자의 리본을 두 손으로 잡고 앉아 있었다."], ["With a face full of light and thought, full of a subtle, complex inner life, that was remote from Levin, she was gazing beyond him at the glow of the sunrise.", "빛과 생각으로 가득하고, 레빈에게서는 멀리 떨어진 어떤 섬세하고 복잡한 내면의 삶으로 가득한 얼굴로, 그녀는 그를 지나쳐 동트는 노을의 빛을 바라보고 있었다."]], [["At the very instant when this apparition was vanishing, the truthful eyes glanced at him.", "이 환영이 막 사라지려는 바로 그 순간, 그 진실한 눈이 그를 흘끗 보았다."], ["She recognized him, and her face lighted up with wondering delight.", "그녀는 그를 알아보았고, 그녀의 얼굴은 놀라움 어린 기쁨으로 환해졌다."]], [["He could not be mistaken.", "그가 잘못 보았을 리 없었다."], ["There were no other eyes like those in the world.", "세상에 그런 눈은 또 없었다."], ["There was only one creature in the world that could concentrate for him all the brightness and meaning of life.", "세상에 그를 위해 삶의 모든 빛과 의미를 하나로 모을 수 있는 존재는 오직 하나뿐이었다."], ["It was she.", "그녀였다."], ["It was Kitty.", "키티였다."], ["He understood that she was driving to Ergushovo from the railway station.", "그는 그녀가 기차역에서 예르구쇼보로 가는 길임을 알아차렸다."], ["And everything that had been stirring Levin during that sleepless night, all the resolutions he had made, all vanished at once.", "그리고 그 잠 못 이룬 밤 내내 레빈을 뒤흔들던 모든 것, 그가 세웠던 모든 결심이 순식간에 사라져 버렸다."], ["He recalled with horror his dreams of marrying a peasant girl.", "그는 농촌 처녀와 결혼하겠다던 자기 꿈을 끔찍한 심정으로 떠올렸다."], ["There only, in the carriage that had crossed over to the other side of the road, and was rapidly disappearing, there only could he find the solution of the riddle of his life, which had weighed so agonizingly upon him of late.", "오직 그곳, 길 반대편으로 건너가 빠르게 사라져 가는 저 마차 안에서만, 오직 그곳에서만 그는 최근 자기를 그토록 고통스럽게 짓눌러 온 자기 삶의 수수께끼에 대한 해답을 찾을 수 있었다."]], [["She did not look out again.", "그녀는 다시 내다보지 않았다."], ["The sound of the carriage-springs was no longer audible, the bells could scarcely be heard.", "마차 용수철 소리는 더 이상 들리지 않았고, 방울 소리도 거의 들리지 않았다."], ["The barking of dogs showed the carriage had reached the village, and all that was left was the empty fields all round, the village in front, and he himself isolated and apart from it all, wandering lonely along the deserted highroad.", "개 짖는 소리로 마차가 마을에 다다랐음을 알 수 있었고, 남은 것이라고는 사방의 텅 빈 들판과, 앞쪽의 마을과, 그 모든 것에서 외따로 떨어져 인적 없는 한길을 홀로 헤매는 그 자신뿐이었다."]], [["He glanced at the sky, expecting to find there the cloud shell he had been admiring and taking as the symbol of the ideas and feelings of that night.", "그는 하늘을 흘끗 올려다보며, 거기에서 자기가 감탄하며 그 밤의 생각과 감정의 상징으로 삼았던 그 구름 껍데기를 찾으려 했다."], ["There was nothing in the sky in the least like a shell.", "하늘에는 껍데기 비슷한 것이라곤 조금도 없었다."], ["There, in the remote heights above, a mysterious change had been accomplished.", "저 멀리 높은 곳에서는 신비로운 변화가 이루어진 뒤였다."], ["There was no trace of shell, and there was stretched over fully half the sky an even cover of tiny and ever tinier cloudlets.", "껍데기의 흔적은 없었고, 하늘의 족히 절반에 걸쳐 점점 더 자잘해지는 작은 구름들이 고르게 덮여 펼쳐져 있었다."], ["The sky had grown blue and bright; and with the same softness, but with the same remoteness, it met his questioning gaze.", "하늘은 푸르고 환하게 변해 있었다. 그리고 그 똑같은 부드러움으로, 그러나 그 똑같은 아득함으로, 하늘은 묻고 있는 그의 시선을 맞이했다."]], [["“No,”", "\"아니야.\""], ["he said to himself, “however good that life of simplicity and toil may be, I cannot go back to it.", "그는 혼잣말을 했다. \"그 단순함과 노동의 삶이 아무리 좋다 한들, 나는 그리로 돌아갈 수 없어."], ["I love her.”", "나는 그녀를 사랑하니까.\""]], [["# Chapter 13", "# Chapter 13"]], [["None but those who were most intimate with Alexey Alexandrovitch knew that, while on the surface the coldest and most reasonable of men, he had one weakness quite opposed to the general trend of his character.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치와 가장 가까운 사람들만이 알고 있었다. 겉으로는 더없이 냉정하고 이성적인 사내였지만, 그에게는 그의 전반적인 성격과는 정반대되는 약점이 하나 있다는 사실을."], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch could not hear or see a child or woman crying without being moved.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 아이나 여자가 우는 것을 듣거나 보면 마음이 흔들리지 않을 수 없었다."], ["The sight of tears threw him into a state of nervous agitation, and he utterly lost all power of reflection.", "눈물을 보면 그는 신경이 곤두서는 동요 상태에 빠졌고, 사고하는 능력을 완전히 잃어버렸다."], ["The chief secretary of his department and his private secretary were aware of this, and used to warn women who came with petitions on no account to give way to tears, if they did not want to ruin their chances.", "그가 속한 부처의 수석 비서관과 개인 비서는 이 사실을 알고 있어서, 청원을 가지고 찾아오는 여자들에게 일을 망치고 싶지 않거든 절대로 눈물을 흘려서는 안 된다고 미리 일러두곤 했다."], ["“He will get angry, and will not listen to you,” they used to say.", "\"그분은 화를 내실 거고, 당신 말을 들어주지 않으실 겁니다.\""], ["And as a fact, in such cases the emotional disturbance set up in Alexey Alexandrovitch by the sight of tears found expression in hasty anger.", "그들은 이렇게 말하곤 했다. 그리고 사실, 그런 경우에 눈물을 보고 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치 안에서 일어나는 감정적 동요는 성급한 분노로 표출되곤 했다."], ["“I can do nothing.", "\"내가 해 줄 수 있는 일이 없소."], ["Kindly leave the room!”", "그만 방에서 나가 주시오!\""], ["he would commonly cry in such cases.", "그런 경우에 그는 흔히 이렇게 소리치곤 했다."]], [["When returning from the races Anna had informed him of her relations with Vronsky, and immediately afterwards had burst into tears, hiding her face in her hands, Alexey Alexandrovitch, for all the fury aroused in him against her, was aware at the same time of a rush of that emotional disturbance always produced in him by tears.", "경마장에서 돌아오는 길에 안나가 자기와 브론스키의 관계를 그에게 털어놓고는, 곧바로 두 손에 얼굴을 묻은 채 울음을 터뜨렸을 때, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 그녀를 향해 끓어오르는 온갖 분노에도 불구하고 동시에 눈물이 언제나 그의 안에 불러일으키는 그 감정적 동요가 밀려드는 것을 느꼈다."], ["Conscious of it, and conscious that any expression of his feelings at that minute would be out of keeping with the position, he tried to suppress every manifestation of life in himself, and so neither stirred nor looked at her.", "그것을 의식하고, 또 그 순간에 자기 감정을 어떤 식으로든 드러내는 것이 이 상황에 어울리지 않으리라는 것을 의식한 그는, 자기 안에서 생명의 모든 표현을 억누르려 애썼고, 그래서 몸을 움직이지도 그녀를 쳐다보지도 않았다."], ["This was what had caused that strange expression of deathlike rigidity in his face which had so impressed Anna.", "안나에게 그토록 깊은 인상을 남겼던, 죽은 사람처럼 굳어 버린 그 기이한 표정이 그의 얼굴에 떠오른 것은 바로 이 때문이었다."]], [["When they reached the house he helped her to get out of the carriage, and making an effort to master himself, took leave of her with his usual urbanity, and uttered that phrase that bound him to nothing; he said that tomorrow he would let her know his decision.", "집에 다다르자 그는 그녀가 마차에서 내리는 것을 도왔고, 스스로를 다잡으려 애쓰며 평소와 같은 정중함으로 그녀에게 작별을 고했다. 그리고 자신을 아무것에도 얽매지 않는 그 문구를 입에 올렸다. 내일 자기 결정을 알려 주겠노라고 그는 말했다."]], [["His wife’s words, confirming his worst suspicions, had sent a cruel pang to the heart of Alexey Alexandrovitch.", "최악의 의심을 확인시켜 준 아내의 말은 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 가슴에 잔인한 통증을 안겨 주었다."], ["That pang was intensified by the strange feeling of physical pity for her set up by her tears.", "그 통증은 그녀의 눈물이 불러일으킨, 그녀에 대한 기묘한 육체적 연민 때문에 한층 심해졌다."], ["But when he was all alone in the carriage Alexey Alexandrovitch, to his surprise and delight, felt complete relief both from this pity and from the doubts and agonies of jealousy.", "그러나 마차 안에 혼자 남게 되자 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 놀랍고도 기쁘게도, 이 연민에서도, 또 질투의 의혹과 고뇌에서도 완전히 벗어나는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["He experienced the sensations of a man who has had a tooth out after suffering long from toothache.", "그는 오랫동안 치통에 시달리다 마침내 이를 뽑아낸 사람과 같은 느낌을 경험했다."], ["After a fearful agony and a sense of something huge, bigger than the head itself, being torn out of his jaw, the sufferer, hardly able to believe in his own good luck, feels all at once that what has so long poisoned his existence and enchained his attention, exists no longer, and that he can live and think again, and take interest in other things besides his tooth.", "무시무시한 고통과, 머리통 자체보다도 더 큰 무언가가 턱에서 뜯겨 나오는 듯한 느낌을 겪고 난 뒤, 환자는 자기에게 찾아온 행운을 좀처럼 믿지 못하다가, 그토록 오랫동안 자기 삶을 좀먹고 주의를 옭아매던 것이 더 이상 존재하지 않으며, 이제 다시 살아갈 수도 생각할 수도 있고, 자기 이 말고 다른 것들에도 관심을 가질 수 있다는 것을 문득 깨닫는다."], ["This feeling Alexey Alexandrovitch was experiencing.", "바로 이 느낌을 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 경험하고 있었다."], ["The agony had been strange and terrible, but now it was over; he felt that he could live again and think of something other than his wife.", "고통은 기이하고 끔찍했지만, 이제 끝났다. 그는 다시 살아갈 수 있고, 아내가 아닌 다른 무언가를 생각할 수 있다는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["“No honor, no heart, no religion; a corrupt woman.", "\"명예도 없고, 마음도 없고, 신앙도 없는, 타락한 여자야."], ["I always knew it and always saw it, though I tried to deceive myself to spare her,”", "나는 늘 그것을 알고 있었고 늘 보고 있었어. 그 여자를 봐주려고 나 자신을 속이려 애쓰긴 했지만.\""], ["he said to himself.", "그는 혼잣말을 했다."], ["And it actually seemed to him that he always had seen it: he recalled incidents of their past life, in which he had never seen anything wrong before—now these incidents proved clearly that she had always been a corrupt woman.", "그리고 정말로 그에게는 자기가 늘 그것을 보아 왔던 것처럼 여겨졌다. 그는 지난 삶의 사건들을 떠올렸는데, 전에는 그 안에서 아무런 잘못된 점도 보지 못했던 것들이었다."], ["“I made a mistake in linking my life to hers; but there was nothing wrong in my mistake, and so I cannot be unhappy.", "그런데 이제 그 사건들은 그녀가 늘 타락한 여자였음을 분명히 증명해 주었다. \"내 삶을 그 여자의 삶에 엮은 것은 실수였어."], ["It’s not I that am to blame,”", "하지만 내 실수에 잘못된 것은 없으니, 나는 불행해질 수 없어."], ["he told himself, “but she.", "비난받아야 할 사람은 내가 아니라,\""], ["But I have nothing to do with her.", "그는 스스로에게 말했다, \"그 여자야. 하지만 나는 그 여자와 아무 상관이 없어."], ["She does not exist for me....”", "그 여자는 나에게 더 이상 존재하지 않아…….\""]], [["Everything relating to her and her son, towards whom his sentiments were as much changed as towards her, ceased to interest him.", "그녀와 관련된 모든 것, 그리고 그녀에 대한 만큼이나 감정이 변해 버린 그녀의 아들과 관련된 모든 것이 그의 흥미를 끌지 못하게 되었다."], ["The only thing that interested him now was the question of in what way he could best, with most propriety and comfort for himself, and thus with most justice, extricate himself from the mud with which she had spattered him in her fall, and then proceed along his path of active, honorable, and useful existence.", "이제 그의 관심을 끄는 유일한 것은, 어떤 방식으로 하면 자기에게 가장 타당하고 가장 마음 편하게, 그리하여 가장 정당하게, 그녀가 자기 추락 속에서 그에게 튀긴 진창에서 빠져나와 능동적이고 명예롭고 유익한 삶의 길을 따라 다시 나아갈 수 있을까 하는 문제였다."]], [["“I cannot be made unhappy by the fact that a contemptible woman has committed a crime.", "\"경멸스러운 여자가 죄를 저질렀다고 해서 내가 불행해질 수는 없어."], ["I have only to find the best way out of the difficult position in which she has placed me.", "나는 그 여자가 나를 몰아넣은 이 어려운 처지에서 벗어날 최선의 방법을 찾기만 하면 돼."], ["And I shall find it,”", "그리고 나는 그것을 찾아낼 거야.\""], ["he said to himself, frowning more and more.", "그는 점점 더 미간을 찌푸리며 스스로에게 말했다."], ["“I’m not the first nor the last.”", "\"내가 처음도 아니고 마지막도 아니지.\""], ["And to say nothing of historical instances dating from the “Fair Helen” of Menelaus, recently revived in the memory of all, a whole list of contemporary examples of husbands with unfaithful wives in the highest society rose before Alexey Alexandrovitch’s imagination.", "그리고 최근 모든 이의 기억 속에 되살아난, 메넬라오스의 '아름다운 헬레네'로 거슬러 올라가는 역사적 사례들은 말할 것도 없고, 상류 사회에서 부정한 아내를 둔 남편들의 현대적 사례들이 줄줄이 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 상상 앞에 떠올랐다."], ["“Daryalov, Poltavsky, Prince Karibanov, Count Paskudin, Dram....", "\"다랴로프, 폴타프스키, 카리바노프 공작, 파스쿠딘 백작, 드람……"], ["Yes, even Dram, such an honest, capable fellow ...", "그래, 드람마저도, 그토록 정직하고 유능한 친구가……"], ["Semyonov, Tchagin, Sigonin,”", "세묘노프, 차긴, 시고닌.\""], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch remembered.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 이렇게 떠올렸다."], ["“Admitting that a certain quite irrational ridicule falls to the lot of these men, yet I never saw anything but a misfortune in it, and always felt sympathy for it,”", "\"어떤 도무지 이치에 맞지 않는 조롱이 이런 남자들의 몫이 된다는 것은 인정하지만, 그래도 나는 거기서 불행 말고 다른 것을 본 적이 없고, 늘 그것에 동정을 느껴 왔어.\""], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch said to himself, though indeed this was not the fact, and he had never felt sympathy for misfortunes of that kind, but the more frequently he had heard of instances of unfaithful wives betraying their husbands, the more highly he had thought of himself.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 스스로에게 말했지만, 실은 그것은 사실이 아니었다. 그는 그런 종류의 불행에 동정을 느낀 적이 한 번도 없었고, 오히려 부정한 아내가 남편을 배신한 사례를 자주 들으면 들을수록 자기 자신을 더 높이 평가해 왔다."], ["“It is a misfortune which may befall anyone.", "\"그것은 누구에게나 닥칠 수 있는 불행이야."], ["And this misfortune has befallen me.", "그리고 그 불행이 나에게 닥친 거지."], ["The only thing to be done is to make the best of the position.”", "할 일은 단 하나, 이 처지에서 최선을 끌어내는 것뿐이야.\" * '아름다운 헬레네': 그리스 신화에서 스파르타 왕 메넬라오스의 아내로, 트로이의 왕자 파리스와 달아나 트로이 전쟁의 원인이 되었다."]], [["And he began passing in review the methods of proceeding of men who had been in the same position that he was in.", "그리고 그는 자기와 같은 처지에 놓였던 남자들이 취한 대처 방식들을 차례로 검토하기 시작했다."]], [["“Daryalov fought a duel....”", "\"다랴로프는 결투를 했지…….\""]], [["The duel had particularly fascinated the thoughts of Alexey Alexandrovitch in his youth, just because he was physically a coward, and was himself well aware of the fact.", "결투는 특히 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 젊은 시절에 그의 생각을 사로잡았는데, 다름 아니라 그가 육체적으로 겁쟁이였고, 또 그 사실을 스스로 잘 알고 있었기 때문이다."], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch could not without horror contemplate the idea of a pistol aimed at himself, and had never made use of any weapon in his life.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 자기를 겨눈 권총을 떠올리기만 해도 공포에 사로잡혔고, 평생 어떤 무기도 다뤄 본 적이 없었다."], ["This horror had in his youth set him pondering on dueling, and picturing himself in a position in which he would have to expose his life to danger.", "이 공포는 젊은 시절 그를 결투에 대해 깊이 생각하게 만들었고, 자기 목숨을 위험에 내맡겨야 하는 처지에 놓인 자기 모습을 상상하게 만들었다. 세상에서 성공과 확고한 지위를 얻은 그는 오래전에 이 감정을 잊어버렸다."], ["Having attained success and an established position in the world, he had long ago forgotten this feeling; but the habitual bent of feeling reasserted itself, and dread of his own cowardice proved even now so strong that Alexey Alexandrovitch spent a long while thinking over the question of dueling in all its aspects, and hugging the idea of a duel, though he was fully aware beforehand that he would never under any circumstances fight one.", "그러나 습관처럼 굳어진 감정의 성향이 다시 고개를 들었고, 자기 자신의 비겁함에 대한 두려움이 지금조차도 어찌나 강했던지, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 결투의 문제를 온갖 측면에서 한참 동안 곱씹으며 결투라는 생각을 끌어안았다. 비록 어떤 상황에서도 자기는 결코 결투 같은 것을 하지 않으리라는 것을 미리 충분히 알고 있었으면서도 말이다."]], [["“There’s no doubt our society is still so barbarous (it’s not the same in England) that very many”—and among these were those whose opinion Alexey Alexandrovitch particularly valued—“look favorably on the duel; but what result is attained by it?", "\"우리 사회가 아직도 너무나 야만적이어서(영국에서는 그렇지 않지) 아주 많은 이들이\"—그리고 그 가운데에는 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 특히 그 견해를 중히 여기는 사람들도 있었다—\"결투를 호의적으로 본다는 데에는 의심의 여지가 없어. 하지만 그것으로 무슨 결과를 얻는단 말인가?"], ["Suppose I call him out,”", "가령 내가 그를 불러낸다 치자.\""], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch went on to himself, and vividly picturing the night he would spend after the challenge, and the pistol aimed at him, he shuddered, and knew that he never would do it—“suppose I call him out.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 혼자 생각을 이어 가다가, 도전장을 던진 뒤에 보내게 될 밤과 자기를 겨눈 권총을 생생하게 떠올리고는 몸서리를 쳤고, 자기는 결코 그러지 못하리라는 것을 알았다. \"가령 내가 그를 불러낸다 치자."], ["Suppose I am taught,”", "가령 내가 사격을 배운다 치자.\""], ["he went on musing, “to shoot; I press the trigger,”", "그는 계속 생각에 잠겼다. \"내가 방아쇠를 당긴다.\""], ["he said to himself, closing his eyes, “and it turns out I have killed him,”", "그는 눈을 감으며 스스로에게 말했다. \"그리고 결국 내가 그를 죽이게 된다.\""], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch said to himself, and he shook his head as though to dispel such silly ideas.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 스스로에게 말하고는, 그런 어리석은 생각을 떨쳐 버리려는 듯 고개를 저었다."], ["“What sense is there in murdering a man in order to define one’s relation to a guilty wife and son?", "\"죄지은 아내와 아들에 대한 내 관계를 규정하기 위해 한 사람을 죽이는 것에 무슨 의미가 있단 말인가?"], ["I should still just as much have to decide what I ought to do with her.", "그렇게 해도 나는 여전히 그 여자를 어떻게 해야 할지 똑같이 결정해야 할 거야."], ["But what is more probable and what would doubtless occur—I should be killed or wounded.", "하지만 더 있음직한 일, 틀림없이 일어날 일은—내가 죽거나 다치는 거지."], ["I, the innocent person, should be the victim—killed or wounded.", "죄 없는 내가 희생자가 되는 거야—죽거나 다치는 거지."], ["It’s even more senseless.", "그건 더더욱 무의미해."], ["But apart from that, a challenge to fight would be an act hardly honest on my side.", "그뿐 아니라, 결투를 신청하는 것은 내 쪽에서 보면 도무지 정직하지 못한 행동일 거야."], ["Don’t I know perfectly well that my friends would never allow me to fight a duel—would never allow the life of a statesman, needed by Russia, to be exposed to danger?", "내 친구들이 결코 나에게 결투를 허락하지 않으리라는 것을—러시아에 필요한 정치가의 목숨을 위험에 내맡기는 것을 결코 허락하지 않으리라는 것을 내가 완벽히 알고 있지 않은가?"], ["Knowing perfectly well beforehand that the matter would never come to real danger, it would amount to my simply trying to gain a certain sham reputation by such a challenge.", "결국 이 일이 실제 위험으로까지 가지 않으리라는 것을 미리 완벽히 알면서, 그런 도전으로 어떤 거짓 명성이나 얻으려 드는 꼴이 될 거야."], ["That would be dishonest, that would be false, that would be deceiving myself and others.", "그건 정직하지 못한 일이고, 거짓된 일이며, 나 자신과 남들을 속이는 일이지."], ["A duel is quite irrational, and no one expects it of me.", "결투는 도무지 이치에 맞지 않고, 아무도 나에게 그것을 기대하지 않아."], ["My aim is simply to safeguard my reputation, which is essential for the uninterrupted pursuit of my public duties.”", "내 목표는 그저 내 명성을 지키는 것뿐인데, 그것은 내 공적인 임무를 중단 없이 수행하는 데에 꼭 필요한 거야.\""], ["Official duties, which had always been of great consequence in Alexey Alexandrovitch’s eyes, seemed of special importance to his mind at this moment.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 눈에 늘 대단히 중요했던 공직의 임무가, 이 순간 그의 마음에는 각별히 중요하게 여겨졌다."], ["Considering and rejecting the duel, Alexey Alexandrovitch turned to divorce—another solution selected by several of the husbands he remembered.", "결투를 검토하고 물리친 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 이혼으로 눈길을 돌렸는데, 그것은 그가 기억하는 남편들 가운데 몇몇이 택한 또 다른 해결책이었다."], ["Passing in mental review all the instances he knew of divorces (there were plenty of them in the very highest society with which he was very familiar), Alexey Alexandrovitch could not find a single example in which the object of divorce was that which he had in view.", "자기가 아는 이혼 사례를 모조리 머릿속으로 검토해 보았지만(그가 아주 잘 아는 바로 그 최상류 사회에는 그런 사례가 수두룩했다), 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 이혼의 목적이 자기가 염두에 둔 것과 같은 사례를 단 하나도 찾을 수 없었다."], ["In all these instances the husband had practically ceded or sold his unfaithful wife, and the very party which, being in fault, had not the right to contract a fresh marriage, had formed counterfeit, pseudo-matrimonial ties with a self-styled husband.", "그 모든 사례에서 남편은 사실상 부정한 아내를 양보하거나 팔아넘긴 셈이었고, 잘못을 저질러 새 결혼을 맺을 권리가 없는 바로 그 당사자가, 자칭 남편이라는 자와 가짜의, 결혼을 흉내 낸 관계를 맺고 있었다."], ["In his own case, Alexey Alexandrovitch saw that a legal divorce, that is to say, one in which only the guilty wife would be repudiated, was impossible of attainment.", "자기 경우에는, 법적인 이혼, 다시 말해 죄지은 아내만을 내치는 이혼은 이루어질 수 없다는 것을 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 알았다."], ["He saw that the complex conditions of the life they led made the coarse proofs of his wife’s guilt, required by the law, out of the question; he saw that a certain refinement in that life would not admit of such proofs being brought forward, even if he had them, and that to bring forward such proofs would damage him in the public estimation more than it would her.", "그들이 영위하는 삶의 복잡한 조건들 때문에 법이 요구하는 아내의 죄에 대한 노골적인 증거를 대는 것은 어림도 없다는 것을 그는 알았다. 그런 삶에 깃든 어떤 세련됨이, 설령 그에게 증거가 있다 한들 그런 증거를 들이대는 것을 용납하지 않으리라는 것을, 그리고 그런 증거를 들이대는 것은 그녀보다 오히려 그를 세간의 평가에서 더 해치리라는 것을 그는 알았다."]], [["An attempt at divorce could lead to nothing but a public scandal, which would be a perfect godsend to his enemies for calumny and attacks on his high position in society.", "이혼을 시도해 봤자 세간의 추문 말고는 아무것도 얻을 게 없을 터였고, 그것은 사교계에서 그의 높은 지위를 헐뜯고 공격하려는 그의 적들에게 더없이 좋은 먹잇감이 될 것이었다."], ["His chief object, to define the position with the least amount of disturbance possible, would not be attained by divorce either.", "가능한 한 가장 적은 동요로 처지를 규정한다는 그의 주된 목표도 이혼으로는 이루어지지 않을 터였다."], ["Moreover, in the event of divorce, or even of an attempt to obtain a divorce, it was obvious that the wife broke off all relations with the husband and threw in her lot with the lover.", "게다가 이혼할 경우, 아니 이혼을 시도하기만 해도, 아내가 남편과 모든 관계를 끊고 정부와 운명을 함께하리라는 것은 뻔한 일이었다."], ["And in spite of the complete, as he supposed, contempt and indifference he now felt for his wife, at the bottom of his heart Alexey Alexandrovitch still had one feeling left in regard to her—a disinclination to see her free to throw in her lot with Vronsky, so that her crime would be to her advantage.", "그리고 그가 생각하기에 지금 아내에게 느끼는 완전한 경멸과 무관심에도 불구하고, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 마음 깊은 곳에는 그녀에 대한 감정이 하나 남아 있었다—그녀가 자유롭게 브론스키와 운명을 함께하여 그녀의 죄가 그녀에게 득이 되는 꼴을 보고 싶지 않다는 것이었다."], ["The mere notion of this so exasperated Alexey Alexandrovitch, that directly it rose to his mind he groaned with inward agony, and got up and changed his place in the carriage, and for a long while after, he sat with scowling brows, wrapping his numbed and bony legs in the fleecy rug.", "이 생각만으로도 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 어찌나 격분했던지, 그 생각이 머릿속에 떠오르기가 무섭게 그는 속에서 끓어오르는 고통에 신음하며 자리에서 일어나 마차 안에서 자리를 옮겼고, 그 후로도 한참 동안 미간을 찌푸린 채 마비된 앙상한 두 다리를 보드라운 무릎 덮개로 감싸고 앉아 있었다."]], [["“Apart from formal divorce, One might still do like Karibanov, Paskudin, and that good fellow Dram—that is, separate from one’s wife,”", "\"정식 이혼이 아니더라도, 카리바노프나 파스쿠딘, 그리고 그 선량한 친구 드람처럼 할 수도 있겠지—그러니까 아내와 별거하는 거야.\""], ["he went on thinking, when he had regained his composure.", "그는 다시 평정을 되찾자 계속 생각해 나갔다."], ["But this step too presented the same drawback of public scandal as a divorce, and what was more, a separation, quite as much as a regular divorce, flung his wife into the arms of Vronsky.", "그러나 이 방안도 이혼과 마찬가지로 세간의 추문이라는 똑같은 결점을 안고 있었고, 더욱이 별거도 정식 이혼과 다름없이 아내를 브론스키의 품으로 던져 넣는 것이었다."], ["“No, it’s out of the question, out of the question!”", "\"안 돼, 그건 말도 안 돼, 말도 안 되는 일이야!\""], ["he said again, twisting his rug about him again.", "그는 또다시 무릎 덮개를 몸에 둘둘 감으며 거듭 말했다."], ["“I cannot be unhappy, but neither she nor he ought to be happy.”", "\"나는 불행해질 수 없지만, 그 여자도 그자도 행복해서는 안 돼.\""]], [["The feeling of jealousy, which had tortured him during the period of uncertainty, had passed away at the instant when the tooth had been with agony extracted by his wife’s words.", "불확실한 시기 내내 그를 괴롭히던 질투의 감정은, 아내의 말로 인해 그 이가 고통스럽게 뽑혀 나간 순간 사라져 버렸다."], ["But that feeling had been replaced by another, the desire, not merely that she should not be triumphant, but that she should get due punishment for her crime.", "그러나 그 감정은 또 다른 감정으로 대체되었으니, 그녀가 의기양양하지 못하게 하는 것은 물론이고, 그녀가 자기 죄에 합당한 벌을 받아야 한다는 욕구였다."], ["He did not acknowledge this feeling, but at the bottom of his heart he longed for her to suffer for having destroyed his peace of mind—his honor.", "그는 이 감정을 인정하지는 않았지만, 마음 깊은 곳에서는 그녀가 자기 마음의 평화를—자기 명예를 파괴한 대가로 고통받기를 갈망했다."], ["And going once again over the conditions inseparable from a duel, a divorce, a separation, and once again rejecting them, Alexey Alexandrovitch felt convinced that there was only one solution,—to keep her with him, concealing what had happened from the world, and using every measure in his power to break off the intrigue, and still more—though this he did not admit to himself—to punish her.", "그리고 결투, 이혼, 별거에서 떼어 낼 수 없는 조건들을 다시 한번 하나하나 짚어 보고, 다시 한번 그것들을 물리친 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 해결책이 단 하나뿐이라는 것을 확신하게 되었다—그녀를 자기 곁에 붙들어 두고, 일어난 일을 세상으로부터 감추며, 자기 능력이 미치는 모든 수단을 동원해 그 밀회를 끊어 버리고, 게다가—비록 이것은 스스로에게 인정하지 않았지만—그녀를 벌하는 것이었다."], ["“I must inform her of my conclusion, that thinking over the terrible position in which she has placed her family, all other solutions will be worse for both sides than an external status quo, and that such I agree to retain, on the strict condition of obedience on her part to my wishes, that is to say, cessation of all intercourse with her lover.”", "\"그 여자가 가족을 몰아넣은 이 끔찍한 처지를 곰곰이 생각해 보건대, 다른 어떤 해결책도 겉으로 드러나는 현 상태를 유지하는 것보다 양쪽 모두에게 더 나쁘리라는 것이 내 결론이며, 따라서 나는 그 여자가 내 뜻에 복종한다는, 다시 말해 정부와의 모든 교제를 끊는다는 엄격한 조건 아래 그 상태를 유지하는 데 동의한다고 그 여자에게 알려야겠어.\""], ["When this decision had been finally adopted, another weighty consideration occurred to Alexey Alexandrovitch in support of it.", "이 결정이 마침내 채택되자, 그것을 뒷받침하는 또 하나의 묵직한 고려 사항이 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에게 떠올랐다."], ["“By such a course only shall I be acting in accordance with the dictates of religion,”", "\"이런 방침을 통해서만 나는 종교의 가르침에 따라 행동하는 것이 될 거야.\""], ["he told himself.", "그는 스스로에게 말했다."], ["“In adopting this course, I am not casting off a guilty wife, but giving her a chance of amendment; and, indeed, difficult as the task will be to me, I shall devote part of my energies to her reformation and salvation.”", "\"이 방침을 택함으로써 나는 죄지은 아내를 내치는 것이 아니라, 그 여자에게 바로잡을 기회를 주는 것이고, 게다가 나에게 아무리 힘든 과업일지라도, 나는 내 힘의 일부를 그 여자의 갱생과 구원에 바칠 거야.\""]], [["Though Alexey Alexandrovitch was perfectly aware that he could not exert any moral influence over his wife, that such an attempt at reformation could lead to nothing but falsity; though in passing through these difficult moments he had not once thought of seeking guidance in religion, yet now, when his conclusion corresponded, as it seemed to him, with the requirements of religion, this religious sanction to his decision gave him complete satisfaction, and to some extent restored his peace of mind.", "비록 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 자기가 아내에게 어떤 도덕적 영향력도 행사할 수 없다는 것을, 그런 갱생의 시도가 거짓 말고는 아무것도 낳지 못하리라는 것을 완벽히 알고 있었지만, 또 이 힘겨운 순간들을 지나오는 동안 종교에서 인도를 구할 생각은 단 한 번도 하지 않았지만, 그래도 지금, 자기 결론이 그가 보기에 종교의 요구와 부합하게 되자, 자기 결정에 대한 이 종교적 승인은 그에게 완전한 만족감을 주었고, 어느 정도는 마음의 평화를 되찾아 주었다."], ["He was pleased to think that, even in such an important crisis in life, no one would be able to say that he had not acted in accordance with the principles of that religion whose banner he had always held aloft amid the general coolness and indifference.", "그는 삶의 이토록 중대한 위기에서조차, 그가 전반적인 냉담과 무관심 속에서도 늘 그 깃발을 높이 치켜들어 온 그 종교의 원칙에 따라 자기가 행동하지 않았다고 그 누구도 말할 수 없으리라는 생각에 흐뭇했다."], ["As he pondered over subsequent developments, Alexey Alexandrovitch did not see, indeed, why his relations with his wife should not remain practically the same as before.", "이후의 전개를 곰곰이 생각해 보며, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 실로 어째서 아내와 자기의 관계가 사실상 예전과 똑같이 유지되어서는 안 되는지 그 까닭을 알 수 없었다."], ["No doubt, she could never regain his esteem, but there was not, and there could not be, any sort of reason that his existence should be troubled, and that he should suffer because she was a bad and faithless wife.", "물론 그녀가 다시는 그의 존경을 되찾을 수 없겠지만, 그녀가 나쁘고 부정한 아내라는 이유로 그의 삶이 어지럽혀지고 그가 고통받아야 할 까닭은 어떤 것도 없었고, 또 있을 수도 없었다."], ["“Yes, time will pass; time, which arranges all things, and the old relations will be reestablished,”", "\"그래, 시간이 흐를 거야. 모든 것을 정돈하는 시간이 흐르면 옛 관계가 다시 세워질 거야.\""], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch told himself; “so far reestablished, that is, that I shall not be sensible of a break in the continuity of my life.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 스스로에게 말했다. \"다시 세워진다는 건, 그러니까 내 삶의 연속성에 균열이 생긴 것을 내가 느끼지 못할 정도로 말이지."], ["She is bound to be unhappy, but I am not to blame, and so I cannot be unhappy.”", "그 여자는 불행할 수밖에 없겠지만, 나는 잘못이 없으니, 나는 불행해질 수 없어.\""]], [["# Chapter 14", "# Chapter 14"]], [["As he neared Petersburg, Alexey Alexandrovitch not only adhered entirely to his decision, but was even composing in his head the letter he would write to his wife.", "페테르부르크에 가까워질수록 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 자신의 결정을 한 치도 흩트리지 않았을 뿐 아니라, 아내에게 쓸 편지를 머릿속으로 짓고 있기까지 했다."], ["Going into the porter’s room, Alexey Alexandrovitch glanced at the letters and papers brought from his office, and directed that they should be brought to him in his study.", "수위실로 들어선 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 관청에서 가져온 편지와 서류들을 한번 훑어보고는, 그것들을 서재로 가져오라고 일렀다."]], [["“The horses can be taken out and I will see no one,”", "\"말은 풀어 두게, 그리고 누구도 만나지 않겠네.\""], ["he said in answer to the porter, with a certain pleasure, indicative of his agreeable frame of mind, emphasizing the words, “see no one.”", "그는 흡족한 기분을 드러내는 어떤 즐거움을 담아 수위에게 대답하면서, \"누구도 만나지 않겠네\"라는 말에 힘을 주었다."]], [["In his study Alexey Alexandrovitch walked up and down twice, and stopped at an immense writing-table, on which six candles had already been lighted by the valet who had preceded him.", "서재에서 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 두어 번 왔다 갔다 하다가, 먼저 와 있던 시종이 벌써 여섯 자루의 양초를 켜 둔 거대한 책상 앞에 멈춰 섰다."], ["He cracked his knuckles and sat down, sorting out his writing appurtenances.", "그는 손가락 마디를 뚝뚝 꺾고 나서 자리에 앉아 필기도구들을 정리했다."], ["Putting his elbows on the table, he bent his head on one side, thought a minute, and began to write, without pausing for a second.", "두 팔꿈치를 책상에 괴고 고개를 한쪽으로 기울인 채 잠시 생각하더니, 한순간도 멈추지 않고 쓰기 시작했다."], ["He wrote without using any form of address to her, and wrote in French, making use of the plural “vous,”", "그는 아내를 부르는 어떤 호칭도 쓰지 않고, 러시아어의 해당 형식만큼 차갑게 들리지 않는 복수형 vous를 사용하여 프랑스어로 써 내려갔다."], ["which has not the same note of coldness as the corresponding Russian form.", "* vous: 프랑스어로 '당신'에 해당하는 존칭·복수형 인칭대명사."]], [["“At our last conversation, I notified you of my intention to communicate to you my decision in regard to the subject of that conversation.", "\"우리가 마지막으로 나눈 대화에서, 나는 그 대화의 주제에 관한 내 결정을 당신에게 알리겠다는 뜻을 밝혔소."], ["Having carefully considered everything, I am writing now with the object of fulfilling that promise.", "모든 것을 신중하게 고려한 끝에, 그 약속을 이행하고자 지금 이 편지를 쓰는 것이오."], ["My decision is as follows.", "내 결정은 다음과 같소."], ["Whatever your conduct may have been, I do not consider myself justified in breaking the ties in which we are bound by a Higher Power.", "당신의 행실이 어떠했든 간에, 나는 우리를 더 높은 권능으로 맺어 준 인연을 끊을 권리가 내게 있다고 보지 않소."], ["The family cannot be broken up by a whim, a caprice, or even by the sin of one of the partners in the marriage, and our life must go on as it has done in the past.", "가정이라는 것은 변덕이나 일시적 기분, 심지어 부부 중 한 사람의 죄로 인해 깨질 수 있는 것이 아니며, 우리의 생활은 지난날과 다름없이 계속되어야만 하오."], ["This is essential for me, for you, and for our son.", "이것은 나를 위해서도, 당신을 위해서도, 그리고 우리 아들을 위해서도 반드시 필요한 일이오."], ["I am fully persuaded that you have repented and do repent of what has called forth the present letter, and that you will cooperate with me in eradicating the cause of our estrangement, and forgetting the past.", "나는 당신이 이 편지를 쓰게 만든 일을 뉘우쳤고 또 지금도 뉘우치고 있으리라고, 그리고 우리가 멀어진 원인을 뿌리 뽑고 과거를 잊는 데 나와 함께 협력하리라고 굳게 믿소."], ["In the contrary event, you can conjecture what awaits you and your son.", "만일 그렇지 않을 경우, 당신과 당신 아들에게 무엇이 닥칠지 짐작할 수 있을 것이오."], ["All this I hope to discuss more in detail in a personal interview.", "이 모든 것에 대해서는 직접 만나 더 자세히 논의하기를 바라오."], ["As the season is drawing to a close, I would beg you to return to Petersburg as quickly as possible, not later than Tuesday.", "계절이 끝나 가고 있으니, 되도록 빨리, 늦어도 화요일까지는 페테르부르크로 돌아와 주기를 청하오."], ["All necessary preparations shall be made for your arrival here.", "당신이 이곳에 도착하는 데 필요한 모든 준비는 갖추어 둘 것이오."], ["I beg you to note that I attach particular significance to compliance with this request.", "이 요청을 따르는 일에 내가 특별한 의미를 두고 있음을 명심해 주기 바라오."]], [["A.", "A."], ["Karenin", "카레닌"]], [["“P.S.—I enclose the money which may be needed for your expenses.”", "추신—당신의 비용에 필요할 돈을 동봉하오.\""]], [["He read the letter through and felt pleased with it, and especially that he had remembered to enclose money: there was not a harsh word, not a reproach in it, nor was there undue indulgence.", "그는 편지를 처음부터 끝까지 읽고는 흡족함을 느꼈고, 특히 돈을 동봉할 것을 잊지 않고 떠올렸다는 점이 마음에 들었다. 그 안에는 모진 말 한마디도, 비난 한 줄도 없었고, 그렇다고 지나친 관용도 없었다."], ["Most of all, it was a golden bridge for return.", "무엇보다도 그것은 돌아올 수 있는 황금 다리였다."], ["Folding the letter and smoothing it with a massive ivory knife, and putting it in an envelope with the money, he rang the bell with the gratification it always afforded him to use the well arranged appointments of his writing-table.", "편지를 접어 묵직한 상아 칼로 매끈하게 누른 뒤 돈과 함께 봉투에 넣고, 그는 잘 정돈된 책상의 비품들을 사용할 때면 늘 느끼는 만족감에 젖어 종을 울렸다."]], [["“Give this to the courier to be delivered to Anna Arkadyevna tomorrow at the summer villa,”", "\"이것을 전령에게 주어 내일 안나 아르카지예브나에게 별장으로 전달하도록 하게.\""], ["he said, getting up.", "그는 일어서며 말했다."]], [["“Certainly, your excellency; tea to be served in the study?”", "\"알겠습니다, 각하. 차는 서재로 가져올까요?\""]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch ordered tea to be brought to the study, and playing with the massive paper-knife, he moved to his easy chair, near which there had been placed ready for him a lamp and the French work on Egyptian hieroglyphics that he had begun.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 차를 서재로 가져오라고 분부하고는, 묵직한 종이칼을 만지작거리며 안락의자 쪽으로 옮겨 갔다. 그 곁에는 등불과, 그가 읽기 시작한 이집트 상형문자에 관한 프랑스 책이 미리 준비되어 있었다."], ["Over the easy chair there hung in a gold frame an oval portrait of Anna, a fine painting by a celebrated artist. Alexey Alexandrovitch glanced at it.", "안락의자 위쪽에는 금테 액자에 든 안나의 타원형 초상화가 걸려 있었는데, 어느 이름난 화가가 그린 훌륭한 그림이었다."], ["The unfathomable eyes gazed ironically and insolently at him.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 그것을 한번 바라보았다."], ["Insufferably insolent and challenging was the effect in Alexey Alexandrovitch’s eyes of the black lace about the head, admirably touched in by the painter, the black hair and handsome white hand with one finger lifted, covered with rings.", "헤아릴 수 없는 두 눈이 비웃듯이, 그리고 뻔뻔하게 그를 응시하고 있었다. 화가가 솜씨 좋게 살려 낸 머리 둘레의 검은 레이스며, 검은 머리카락이며, 반지를 낀 손가락 하나를 치켜든 아름다운 흰 손—그 모든 것이 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 눈에는 참을 수 없을 만큼 뻔뻔하고 도전적으로 비쳤다."], ["After looking at the portrait for a minute, Alexey Alexandrovitch shuddered so that his lips quivered and he uttered the sound “brrr,”", "잠시 초상화를 바라보던 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 입술이 떨릴 만큼 몸서리를 치며 \"브르르\" 하는 소리를 내고는 고개를 돌렸다."], ["and turned away. He made haste to sit down in his easy chair and opened the book.", "그는 서둘러 안락의자에 앉아 책을 펼쳤다."], ["He tried to read, but he could not revive the very vivid interest he had felt before in Egyptian hieroglyphics.", "읽어 보려 했지만, 전에 이집트 상형문자에 대해 느꼈던 그 생생한 흥미를 되살릴 수가 없었다."], ["He looked at the book and thought of something else.", "그는 책을 들여다보면서도 다른 무언가를 생각하고 있었다."], ["He thought not of his wife, but of a complication that had arisen in his official life, which at the time constituted the chief interest of it.", "아내를 생각한 것이 아니라, 당시 그의 공직 생활에서 가장 중요한 관심사를 이루던, 거기서 불거진 어떤 복잡한 사안을 떠올렸다."], ["He felt that he had penetrated more deeply than ever before into this intricate affair, and that he had originated a leading idea—he could say it without self-flattery—calculated to clear up the whole business, to strengthen him in his official career, to discomfit his enemies, and thereby to be of the greatest benefit to the government.", "그는 이 얽히고설킨 문제를 전에 없이 깊이 파고들었다고 느꼈고, 또—자화자찬 없이 말할 수 있건대—이 모든 일을 명료하게 정리하고, 자신의 관직 경력을 굳건히 하고, 정적들을 당혹스럽게 만들며, 그리하여 정부에 더없이 큰 이익을 가져다줄 만한 하나의 주도적인 착상을 떠올렸다고 느꼈다."], ["Directly the servant had set the tea and left the room, Alexey Alexandrovitch got up and went to the writing-table.", "하인이 차를 차려 놓고 방을 나가기가 무섭게, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 일어나 책상으로 갔다."], ["Moving into the middle of the table a portfolio of papers, with a scarcely perceptible smile of self-satisfaction, he took a pencil from a rack and plunged into the perusal of a complex report relating to the present complication.", "흡족한 자기만족의 미소를 좀처럼 알아채기 어려울 만큼 희미하게 띤 채, 그는 서류철을 책상 한가운데로 옮겨 놓고, 필통에서 연필 한 자루를 꺼내 들고는 지금의 복잡한 사안과 관련된 까다로운 보고서를 읽는 데 몰두했다."], ["The complication was of this nature: Alexey Alexandrovitch’s characteristic quality as a politician, that special individual qualification that every rising functionary possesses, the qualification that with his unflagging ambition, his reserve, his honesty, and with his self-confidence had made his career, was his contempt for red tape, his cutting down of correspondence, his direct contact, wherever possible, with the living fact, and his economy.", "그 복잡한 사안의 성격은 이러했다. 정치가로서 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 지닌 특유의 자질, 출세하는 관리라면 누구나 갖추는 그 독특한 개인적 자격, 즉 식을 줄 모르는 야심과 과묵함과 정직함, 그리고 자신감과 더불어 그의 경력을 쌓아 올린 그 자격은, 형식적 관료주의에 대한 경멸, 서신 왕래를 줄이는 태도, 가능한 한 살아 있는 사실과 직접 접촉하려는 자세, 그리고 절약 정신이었다."], ["It happened that the famous Commission of the 2nd of June had set on foot an inquiry into the irrigation of lands in the Zaraisky province, which fell under Alexey Alexandrovitch’s department, and was a glaring example of fruitless expenditure and paper reforms.", "마침 그 유명한 6월 2일 위원회가 자라이스크 현의 토지 관개에 관한 조사에 착수했는데, 이 문제는 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 부서 소관이었고, 헛된 지출과 서류상의 개혁을 보여 주는 노골적인 본보기였다."], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch was aware of the truth of this.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 그것이 사실임을 알고 있었다."], ["The irrigation of these lands in the Zaraisky province had been initiated by the predecessor of Alexey Alexandrovitch’s predecessor.", "자라이스크 현의 이 토지 관개 사업은 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 전임자의 전임자가 시작한 것이었다."], ["And vast sums of money had actually been spent and were still being spent on this business, and utterly unproductively, and the whole business could obviously lead to nothing whatever.", "그리고 실제로 막대한 돈이 이 사업에 쓰였고 지금도 쓰이고 있었지만, 전혀 아무런 성과도 없었으며, 이 사업 전체가 결국 아무런 결실도 맺지 못하리라는 것은 명백했다."], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch had perceived this at once on entering office, and would have liked to lay hands on the Board of Irrigation.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 부임하자마자 이 점을 간파했고, 관개위원회에 손을 대고 싶어 했다."], ["But at first, when he did not yet feel secure in his position, he knew it would affect too many interests, and would be injudicious.", "그러나 처음에는, 아직 자기 자리가 확고하다고 느끼지 못하던 무렵이라, 그 일이 너무 많은 이해관계에 영향을 미치게 되어 경솔한 처사가 되리라는 것을 알았다."], ["Later on he had been engrossed in other questions, and had simply forgotten the Board of Irrigation.", "그 후로는 다른 문제들에 골몰하느라 관개위원회를 그냥 잊어버리고 말았다."], ["It went of itself, like all such boards, by the mere force of inertia.", "그것은 그런 류의 모든 위원회가 그렇듯, 단지 타성의 힘만으로 저절로 굴러갔다."], ["(Many people gained their livelihood by the Board of Irrigation, especially one highly conscientious and musical family: all the daughters played on stringed instruments, and Alexey Alexandrovitch knew the family and had stood godfather to one of the elder daughters.) The raising of this question by a hostile department was in Alexey Alexandrovitch’s opinion a dishonorable proceeding, seeing that in every department there were things similar and worse, which no one inquired into, for well-known reasons of official etiquette.", "(많은 사람이 관개위원회 덕에 생계를 꾸렸는데, 특히 대단히 양심적이고 음악을 좋아하는 한 가족이 그러했다. 그 집 딸들은 모두 현악기를 다루었고, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 그 가족을 알고 있었으며 맏딸 가운데 하나의 대부 노릇을 맡기도 했다.) 적대적인 부서가 이 문제를 들고나온 것은, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 보기에 떳떳치 못한 처사였다."], ["However, now that the glove had been thrown down to him, he had boldly picked it up and demanded the appointment of a special commission to investigate and verify the working of the Board of Irrigation of the lands in the Zaraisky province.", "어느 부서에나 이와 비슷하거나 더한 일들이 있건만, 관료 사회의 잘 알려진 예법상의 이유로 아무도 그런 것을 캐묻지 않았기 때문이다. 그러나 이제 자신에게 도전장이 던져진 이상, 그는 그것을 대담하게 집어 들어, 자라이스크 현 토지 관개위원회의 운영을 조사하고 검증할 특별위원회의 설치를 요구했다."], ["But in compensation he gave no quarter to the enemy either.", "다만 그 대신 적에게도 한 치의 인정을 베풀지 않았다."], ["He demanded the appointment of another special commission to inquire into the question of the Native Tribes Organization Committee.", "그는 토착민족 조직 위원회 문제를 조사할 또 다른 특별위원회의 설치를 요구했다. 토착민족 문제는 6월 2일 위원회에서 우연히 거론되었던 것으로, 토착민족들의 개탄스러운 처지를 이유로 한시도 지체할 수 없는 사안이라 하여 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 적극적으로 밀어붙였던 것이다."], ["The question of the Native Tribes had been brought up incidentally in the Commission of the 2nd of June, and had been pressed forward actively by Alexey Alexandrovitch as one admitting of no delay on account of the deplorable condition of the native tribes.", "위원회에서 이 문제는 여러 부서 사이의 다툼거리가 되었다. 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에게 적대적인 부서는, 토착민족들의 처지가 더없이 번창하고 있으며, 제안된 개편이 도리어 그들의 번영을 망쳐 놓을 수 있고, 만일 무언가 잘못된 점이 있다면 그것은 주로 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 부서가 법으로 정해진 조치들을 시행하지 못한 데서 비롯된 것이라고 주장했다."], ["In the commission this question had been a ground of contention between several departments.", "이제 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 다음과 같은 것을 요구할 작정이었다."], ["The department hostile to Alexey Alexandrovitch proved that the condition of the native tribes was exceedingly flourishing, that the proposed reconstruction might be the ruin of their prosperity, and that if there were anything wrong, it arose mainly from the failure on the part of Alexey Alexandrovitch’s department to carry out the measures prescribed by law.", "첫째, 토착민족들의 처지를 현지에서 조사할 권한을 가진 새 위원회를 구성할 것. 둘째, 만일 토착민족들의 처지가 위원회가 손에 쥔 공문서에 나타난 그대로임이 밝혀진다면, 토착민족들의 개탄스러운 처지를 (1) 정치적, (2) 행정적, (3) 경제적, (4) 민족지학적, (5) 물질적, (6) 종교적 관점에서 조사할 또 다른 새 학술위원회를 임명할 것."], ["Now Alexey Alexandrovitch intended to demand: First, that a new commission should be formed which should be empowered to investigate the condition of the native tribes on the spot; secondly, if it should appear that the condition of the native tribes actually was such as it appeared to be from the official documents in the hands of the committee, that another new scientific commission should be appointed to investigate the deplorable condition of the native tribes from the—(1) political, (2) administrative, (3) economic, (4) ethnographical, (5) material, and (6) religious points of view; thirdly, that evidence should be required from the rival department of the measures that had been taken during the last ten years by that department for averting the disastrous conditions in which the native tribes were now placed; and fourthly and finally, that that department explain why it had, as appeared from the evidence before the committee, from No.", "셋째, 토착민족들이 지금 처한 비참한 상황을 막기 위해 지난 십 년 동안 경쟁 부서가 취해 온 조치들에 관한 증거를 그 부서에 요구할 것. 그리고 넷째이자 마지막으로, 위원회 앞에 제출된 증거, 즉 1863년 12월 5일자 제17,015호와 1864년 6월 7일자 제18,038호에 나타난 바와 같이, 어째서 그 부서가 법령 T…"], ["17,015 and 18,038, from December 5, 1863, and June 7, 1864, acted in direct contravention of the intent of the law T...", "제18조 및 제36조 비고의 취지에 정면으로 어긋나는 행동을 했는지를 그 부서가 해명하도록 할 것."], ["Act 18, and the note to Act 36. A flash of eagerness suffused the face of Alexey Alexandrovitch as he rapidly wrote out a synopsis of these ideas for his own benefit.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 이런 착상들의 요지를 자기 자신을 위해 빠르게 적어 내려가는 동안, 그의 얼굴에 열의의 빛이 번졌다."], ["Having filled a sheet of paper, he got up, rang, and sent a note to the chief secretary of his department to look up certain necessary facts for him.", "종이 한 장을 가득 채우고 나서 그는 일어나 종을 울리고는, 필요한 몇 가지 사실을 찾아봐 달라는 쪽지를 부서의 수석 비서에게 보냈다."], ["Getting up and walking about the room, he glanced again at the portrait, frowned, and smiled contemptuously.", "일어나 방 안을 거닐던 그는 초상화를 다시 한번 바라보고는 눈살을 찌푸리며 경멸하듯 미소 지었다."], ["After reading a little more of the book on Egyptian hieroglyphics, and renewing his interest in it, Alexey Alexandrovitch went to bed at eleven o’clock, and recollecting as he lay in bed the incident with his wife, he saw it now in by no means such a gloomy light.", "이집트 상형문자에 관한 책을 조금 더 읽으며 그 흥미를 되살린 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 열한 시에 잠자리에 들었고, 침대에 누운 채 아내와 있었던 일을 떠올려 보니, 이제는 그것이 결코 그렇게까지 음울하게는 여겨지지 않았다."]], [["# Chapter 15", "# Chapter 15"]], [["Though Anna had obstinately and with exasperation contradicted Vronsky when he told her their position was impossible, at the bottom of her heart she regarded her own position as false and dishonorable, and she longed with her whole soul to change it.", "안나는 브론스키가 둘의 처지가 견딜 수 없는 것이라고 말했을 때, 짜증을 내며 고집스럽게 반박했지만, 마음 깊은 곳에서는 자기 처지가 거짓되고 떳떳하지 못하다고 여기고 있었으며, 온 영혼을 다해 그것을 바꾸고 싶어 했다."], ["On the way home from the races she had told her husband the truth in a moment of excitement, and in spite of the agony she had suffered in doing so, she was glad of it.", "경마장에서 돌아오는 길에 그녀는 격앙된 순간에 남편에게 진실을 털어놓았고, 그렇게 하면서 겪은 고통에도 불구하고 그것을 잘했다고 여겼다."], ["After her husband had left her, she told herself that she was glad, that now everything was made clear, and at least there would be no more lying and deception.", "남편이 그녀를 떠난 뒤, 그녀는 자신에게 잘된 일이라고, 이제 모든 것이 분명해졌으며 적어도 더 이상 거짓과 속임은 없을 거라고 말했다."], ["It seemed to her beyond doubt that her position was now made clear forever.", "이제 자기 처지가 영원히 분명해졌다는 것은 그녀에게 의심의 여지가 없어 보였다."], ["It might be bad, this new position, but it would be clear; there would be no indefiniteness or falsehood about it.", "이 새로운 처지가 나쁠지는 몰라도 분명할 것이며, 거기에는 불확실함이나 거짓이 없을 것이었다."], ["The pain she had caused herself and her husband in uttering those words would be rewarded now by everything being made clear, she thought.", "그 말들을 입에 올리면서 자신과 남편에게 안겨 준 고통은, 이제 모든 것이 분명해짐으로써 보상받으리라고 그녀는 생각했다."], ["That evening she saw Vronsky, but she did not tell him of what had passed between her and her husband, though, to make the position definite, it was necessary to tell him.", "그날 저녁 그녀는 브론스키를 만났지만, 처지를 분명히 하려면 그에게 말해야 했음에도, 자기와 남편 사이에 있었던 일을 그에게 말하지 않았다."]], [["When she woke up next morning the first thing that rose to her mind was what she had said to her husband, and those words seemed to her so awful that she could not conceive now how she could have brought herself to utter those strange, coarse words, and could not imagine what would come of it.", "이튿날 아침 잠에서 깼을 때 가장 먼저 그녀의 머릿속에 떠오른 것은 남편에게 했던 말이었고, 그 말들이 어찌나 끔찍해 보였던지 그녀는 이제 어떻게 자기가 그런 이상하고 거친 말을 입에 올릴 수 있었는지 도무지 이해할 수 없었으며, 그것이 어떤 결과를 가져올지 상상조차 할 수 없었다."], ["But the words were spoken, and Alexey Alexandrovitch had gone away without saying anything.", "하지만 말은 이미 뱉어졌고, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 아무 말 없이 떠나 버렸다."], ["“I saw Vronsky and did not tell him.", "'나는 브론스키를 만나고도 그에게 말하지 않았어."], ["At the very instant he was going away I would have turned him back and told him, but I changed my mind, because it was strange that I had not told him the first minute.", "그가 떠나려던 바로 그 순간 그를 돌려세워 말하려고 했지만, 마음을 바꾸었지. 처음 그 순간에 말하지 않은 게 이상했으니까."], ["Why was it I wanted to tell him and did not tell him?”", "왜 말하고 싶었으면서도 말하지 않은 거지?'"], ["And in answer to this question a burning blush of shame spread over her face.", "그리고 이 물음에 대한 대답으로 부끄러움에 타오르는 홍조가 그녀의 얼굴에 번졌다."], ["She knew what had kept her from it, she knew that she had been ashamed.", "그녀는 무엇이 자기를 가로막았는지 알고 있었다."], ["Her position, which had seemed to her simplified the night before, suddenly struck her now as not only not simple, but as absolutely hopeless.", "자기가 부끄러웠다는 것을 알고 있었다. 전날 밤만 해도 단순해 보였던 그녀의 처지가, 이제 갑자기 단순하기는커녕 완전히 절망적인 것으로 다가왔다."], ["She felt terrified at the disgrace, of which she had not ever thought before.", "그녀는 그전까지 한 번도 생각해 본 적 없는 치욕에 겁이 났다."], ["Directly she thought of what her husband would do, the most terrible ideas came to her mind.", "남편이 어떻게 나올지 생각하자마자, 가장 끔찍한 상상들이 그녀의 머릿속에 떠올랐다."], ["She had a vision of being turned out of the house, of her shame being proclaimed to all the world.", "그녀는 집에서 쫓겨나고, 자기 수치가 온 세상에 공표되는 환영을 보았다."], ["She asked herself where she should go when she was turned out of the house, and she could not find an answer.", "집에서 쫓겨나면 어디로 가야 하나 자문해 보았지만, 답을 찾을 수 없었다."]], [["When she thought of Vronsky, it seemed to her that he did not love her, that he was already beginning to be tired of her, that she could not offer herself to him, and she felt bitter against him for it.", "브론스키를 떠올리자, 그가 자기를 사랑하지 않으며, 벌써 자기에게 싫증을 내기 시작했고, 자기는 그에게 스스로를 내맡길 수도 없다는 생각이 들었고, 그래서 그녀는 그에게 쓰라린 원망을 품었다."], ["It seemed to her that the words that she had spoken to her husband, and had continually repeated in her imagination, she had said to everyone, and everyone had heard them.", "남편에게 했던, 그리고 상상 속에서 끊임없이 되풀이했던 그 말들을, 자기가 모든 사람에게 한 것 같았고, 모든 사람이 그것을 들은 것만 같았다."], ["She could not bring herself to look those of her own household in the face.", "그녀는 차마 집안 식구들의 얼굴을 똑바로 볼 수 없었다."], ["She could not bring herself to call her maid, and still less go downstairs and see her son and his governess.", "차마 하녀를 부를 수도 없었고, 아래층으로 내려가 아들과 그의 가정교사를 보는 것은 더더욱 할 수 없었다."]], [["The maid, who had been listening at her door for a long while, came into her room of her own accord.", "오랫동안 그녀의 방문 앞에서 엿듣고 있던 하녀가 제 발로 방에 들어왔다."], ["Anna glanced inquiringly into her face, and blushed with a scared look.", "안나는 캐묻듯이 그녀의 얼굴을 흘끗 보고는 겁먹은 표정으로 얼굴을 붉혔다."], ["The maid begged her pardon for coming in, saying that she had fancied the bell rang.", "하녀는 종이 울린 줄 알았다며 들어온 것을 용서해 달라고 했다."], ["She brought her clothes and a note.", "그녀는 안나의 옷가지와 쪽지 하나를 가져왔다."], ["The note was from Betsy.", "쪽지는 벳시에게서 온 것이었다."], ["Betsy reminded her that Liza Merkalova and Baroness Shtoltz were coming to play croquet with her that morning with their adorers, Kaluzhsky and old Stremov.", "벳시는 그날 아침 리자 메르칼로바와 시톨츠 남작 부인이 자기네 숭배자인 칼루시스키, 그리고 노인 스트레모프와 함께 크로케를 하러 그녀에게 오기로 했다는 것을 일깨워 주었다."], ["“Come, if only as a study in morals.", "\"그저 풍속 연구 삼아서라도 오세요."], ["I shall expect you,”", "기다리고 있을게요.\""], ["she finished.", "그녀는 그렇게 끝맺었다."]], [["Anna read the note and heaved a deep sigh.", "안나는 쪽지를 읽고 깊은 한숨을 내쉬었다."]], [["“Nothing, I need nothing,”", "\"아무것도, 아무것도 필요 없어.\""], ["she said to Annushka, who was rearranging the bottles and brushes on the dressing table.", "화장대 위의 작은 병들과 솔들을 정리하고 있던 안누시카에게 그녀가 말했다."], ["“You can go.", "\"가도 돼."], ["I’ll dress at once and come down.", "곧 옷을 갈아입고 내려갈 테니까."], ["I need nothing.”", "아무것도 필요 없어.\""]], [["Annushka went out, but Anna did not begin dressing, and sat in the same position, her head and hands hanging listlessly, and every now and then she shivered all over, seemed as though she would make some gesture, utter some word, and sank back into lifelessness again.", "안누시카는 나갔지만, 안나는 옷을 갈아입기 시작하지 않은 채, 머리와 두 손을 맥없이 늘어뜨리고 똑같은 자세로 앉아 있었으며, 이따금 온몸을 부르르 떨면서 무슨 몸짓이라도 하고 무슨 말이라도 할 듯하다가, 다시 생기 없는 상태로 가라앉곤 했다."], ["She repeated continually, “My God!", "그녀는 끊임없이 \"오, 하느님!"], ["my God!”", "하느님!\""], ["But neither “God”", "하고 되뇌었다."], ["nor “my” had any meaning to her.", "그러나 '하느님'도 '나의'도 그녀에게는 아무런 의미가 없었다."], ["The idea of seeking help in her difficulty in religion was as remote from her as seeking help from Alexey Alexandrovitch himself, although she had never had doubts of the faith in which she had been brought up.", "자기 곤경에서 종교에 도움을 구한다는 생각은, 비록 자라면서 받아들인 신앙을 한 번도 의심한 적이 없었음에도, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치 본인에게 도움을 구하는 것만큼이나 그녀에게서 멀었다."], ["She knew that the support of religion was possible only upon condition of renouncing what made up for her the whole meaning of life.", "그녀는 종교의 떠받침이라는 것이 오로지 자기에게 삶의 의미 전부를 이루는 것을 포기한다는 조건에서만 가능하다는 것을 알고 있었다."], ["She was not simply miserable, she began to feel alarm at the new spiritual condition, never experienced before, in which she found herself.", "그녀는 단지 비참한 것만이 아니라, 한 번도 겪어 본 적 없는, 자기가 처한 새로운 영적 상태에 두려움을 느끼기 시작했다."], ["She felt as though everything were beginning to be double in her soul, just as objects sometimes appear double to over-tired eyes.", "그녀는 마치 지나치게 피로한 눈에 사물이 이따금 둘로 보이듯, 자기 영혼 속에서 모든 것이 둘로 갈라지기 시작하는 것 같았다."], ["She hardly knew at times what it was she feared, and what she hoped for.", "그녀는 이따금 자기가 무엇을 두려워하고 무엇을 바라는지조차 거의 알 수 없었다."], ["Whether she feared or desired what had happened, or what was going to happen, and exactly what she longed for, she could not have said.", "이미 일어난 일을 두려워하는 건지 바라는 건지, 아니면 앞으로 일어날 일을 두려워하는 건지 바라는 건지, 또 정확히 무엇을 갈망하는 건지, 그녀로서는 말할 수 없었다."]], [["“Ah, what am I doing!”", "\"아, 내가 무슨 짓을 하고 있는 거야!\""], ["she said to herself, feeling a sudden thrill of pain in both sides of her head.", "그녀는 머리의 양쪽이 별안간 욱신거리는 아픔을 느끼며 혼잣말을 했다."], ["When she came to herself, she saw that she was holding her hair in both hands, each side of her temples, and pulling it.", "정신을 차리고 보니, 자기가 두 손으로 관자놀이 양쪽의 머리카락을 움켜쥐고 잡아당기고 있었다."], ["She jumped up, and began walking about.", "그녀는 벌떡 일어나 방 안을 거닐기 시작했다."]], [["“The coffee is ready, and mademoiselle and Seryozha are waiting,”", "\"커피가 준비됐고, 마드무아젤과 세료자가 기다리고 있습니다.\""], ["said Annushka, coming back again and finding Anna in the same position.", "다시 돌아온 안누시카가 똑같은 자세로 있는 안나를 발견하고 말했다."]], [["“Seryozha?", "\"세료자?"], ["What about Seryozha?”", "세료자가 어쨌다고?\""], ["Anna asked, with sudden eagerness, recollecting her son’s existence for the first time that morning.", "그날 아침 처음으로 아들의 존재를 떠올리며 안나가 별안간 간절하게 물었다."]], [["“He’s been naughty, I think,”", "\"좀 말썽을 부린 것 같아요.\""], ["answered Annushka with a smile.", "안누시카가 미소를 지으며 대답했다."]], [["“In what way?”", "\"어떻게?\""]], [["“Some peaches were lying on the table in the corner room.", "\"구석 방 탁자 위에 복숭아가 몇 개 놓여 있었거든요."], ["I think he slipped in and ate one of them on the sly.”", "도련님이 몰래 들어가서 그중 하나를 슬쩍 먹어 버린 것 같아요.\""]], [["The recollection of her son suddenly roused Anna from the helpless condition in which she found herself.", "아들에 대한 기억이 별안간 안나를 자기가 빠져 있던 무력한 상태에서 끌어올렸다."], ["She recalled the partly sincere, though greatly exaggerated, rôle of the mother living for her child, which she had taken up of late years, and she felt with joy that in the plight in which she found herself she had a support, quite apart from her relation to her husband or to Vronsky.", "그녀는 최근 몇 년 동안 자신이 맡아 온, 아이를 위해 사는 어머니라는, 부분적으로는 진심이지만 크게 과장된 역할을 떠올렸고, 자기가 처한 곤경 속에서, 남편이나 브론스키와의 관계와는 전혀 별개로 자기에게 떠받쳐 줄 것이 있다는 사실을 기쁘게 느꼈다."], ["This support was her son.", "그 떠받침은 바로 아들이었다."], ["In whatever position she might be placed, she could not lose her son.", "어떤 처지에 놓이게 되든, 그녀는 아들을 잃을 수 없었다."], ["Her husband might put her to shame and turn her out, Vronsky might grow cold to her and go on living his own life apart (she thought of him again with bitterness and reproach); she could not leave her son.", "남편이 그녀에게 수치를 안기고 그녀를 쫓아낼지도 모르고, 브론스키가 그녀에게 차가워져 자기만의 삶을 따로 살아갈지도 모르지만(그녀는 다시 쓰라림과 원망 속에서 그를 떠올렸다), 그녀는 아들을 떠날 수 없었다."], ["She had an aim in life.", "그녀에게는 삶의 목표가 있었다."], ["And she must act; act to secure this relation to her son, so that he might not be taken from her.", "그러니 그녀는 행동해야 했다. 아들과의 이 관계를 지키고, 아들을 자기에게서 빼앗기지 않도록 행동해야 했다."], ["Quickly indeed, as quickly as possible, she must take action before he was taken from her.", "정말로 빨리, 되도록 빨리, 아들을 빼앗기기 전에 행동에 나서야 했다."], ["She must take her son and go away.", "아들을 데리고 떠나야 했다."], ["Here was the one thing she had to do now.", "이것이 지금 그녀가 해야 할 단 하나의 일이었다."], ["She needed consolation.", "그녀에게는 위안이 필요했다."], ["She must be calm, and get out of this insufferable position.", "마음을 가라앉히고, 이 견딜 수 없는 처지에서 빠져나와야 했다."], ["The thought of immediate action binding her to her son, of going away somewhere with him, gave her this consolation.", "자기를 아들과 맺어 주는 즉각적인 행동, 아이와 함께 어딘가로 떠난다는 생각이 그녀에게 그 위안을 주었다."]], [["She dressed quickly, went downstairs, and with resolute steps walked into the drawing-room, where she found, as usual, waiting for her, the coffee, Seryozha, and his governess.", "그녀는 재빨리 옷을 갈아입고 아래층으로 내려가, 단호한 걸음으로 응접실로 들어섰다. 거기에는 늘 그렇듯 그녀를 기다리며 커피와 세료자, 그리고 그의 가정교사가 있었다."], ["Seryozha, all in white, with his back and head bent, was standing at a table under a looking-glass, and with an expression of intense concentration which she knew well, and in which he resembled his father, he was doing something to the flowers he carried.", "온통 흰옷을 입은 세료자는 등과 머리를 숙인 채 거울 아래 탁자 앞에 서서, 그녀가 잘 아는, 그리고 그 점에서 아버지를 닮은 강렬한 집중의 표정으로, 손에 든 꽃들에 뭔가를 하고 있었다."]], [["The governess had a particularly severe expression.", "가정교사는 유난히 엄한 표정을 짓고 있었다."], ["Seryozha screamed shrilly, as he often did, “Ah, mamma!”", "세료자는 늘 그러듯 새된 소리로 \"아, 엄마!\""], ["and stopped, hesitating whether to go to greet his mother and put down the flowers, or to finish making the wreath and go with the flowers.", "하고 외치고는 멈춰 서서, 엄마를 맞으러 가서 꽃을 내려놓을지, 아니면 화관 만들기를 마저 끝내고 꽃을 들고 갈지 망설였다."]], [["The governess, after saying good-morning, began a long and detailed account of Seryozha’s naughtiness, but Anna did not hear her; she was considering whether she would take her with her or not.", "가정교사는 아침 인사를 한 뒤 세료자의 말썽에 대해 길고 자세하게 늘어놓기 시작했지만, 안나는 그 말을 듣고 있지 않았다. 그녀는 이 여자를 데려갈지 말지를 궁리하고 있었다."], ["“No, I won’t take her,”", "'아니, 데려가지 않겠어.'"], ["she decided.", "그녀는 결심했다."], ["“I’ll go alone with my child.”", "'내 아이만 데리고 혼자 가겠어.'"]], [["“Yes, it’s very wrong,”", "\"그래, 그건 아주 잘못한 일이야.\""], ["said Anna, and taking her son by the shoulder she looked at him, not severely, but with a timid glance that bewildered and delighted the boy, and she kissed him.", "안나는 그렇게 말하고는 아들의 어깨를 잡고 그를 바라보았는데, 엄하게가 아니라 머뭇거리는 눈길로 바라보았고, 그 눈길은 아이를 어리둥절하게 하면서도 기쁘게 했다. 그러고 나서 그녀는 아들에게 입을 맞추었다."], ["“Leave him to me,”", "\"이 아이는 제게 맡기세요.\""], ["she said to the astonished governess, and not letting go of her son, she sat down at the table, where coffee was set ready for her.", "그녀는 어리둥절해하는 가정교사에게 말하고는, 아들을 놓지 않은 채 자기 앞에 커피가 차려진 탁자에 앉았다."]], [["“Mamma! I ...", "\"엄마!"], ["I ... didn’t....”", "저······ 저······ 안 그랬어요······.\""], ["he said, trying to make out from her expression what was in store for him in regard to the peaches.", "그가 복숭아에 관해 자기에게 무슨 일이 닥칠지 엄마의 표정에서 알아내려 애쓰며 말했다."]], [["“Seryozha,”", "\"세료자.\""], ["she said, as soon as the governess had left the room, “that was wrong, but you’ll never do it again, will you?...", "가정교사가 방에서 나가자마자 그녀가 말했다. \"그건 잘못한 일이야."], ["You love me?”", "하지만 다시는 안 그럴 거지, 그렇지?······ 너 엄마 사랑하지?\""]], [["She felt that the tears were coming into her eyes. “Can I help loving him?”", "그녀는 눈에 눈물이 차오르는 것을 느꼈다."], ["she said to herself, looking deeply into his scared and at the same time delighted eyes.", "'내가 어떻게 이 아이를 사랑하지 않을 수 있겠어?' 그녀는 겁먹은 동시에 기뻐하는 아이의 눈을 깊이 들여다보며 혼잣말을 했다."], ["“And can he ever join his father in punishing me?", "'그리고 이 아이가 언제고 아버지 편에 서서 나를 벌할 수 있을까?"], ["Is it possible he will not feel for me?”", "이 아이가 나를 가엾게 여기지 않을 수 있을까?'"], ["Tears were already flowing down her face, and to hide them she got up abruptly and almost ran out on to the terrace.", "이미 눈물이 얼굴을 타고 흐르고 있었고, 그것을 감추려고 그녀는 불쑥 일어나 거의 뛰다시피 테라스로 나갔다."]], [["After the thunder showers of the last few days, cold, bright weather had set in.", "지난 며칠간의 천둥소나기 뒤로 춥고 맑은 날씨가 찾아와 있었다."], ["The air was cold in the bright sun that filtered through the freshly washed leaves.", "갓 씻긴 잎사귀들 사이로 비쳐 드는 밝은 햇빛 속에서도 공기는 차가웠다."]], [["She shivered, both from the cold and from the inward horror which had clutched her with fresh force in the open air.", "그녀는 추위 때문에, 그리고 탁 트인 바깥에서 새삼 거센 힘으로 그녀를 움켜쥔 내면의 공포 때문에 부르르 떨었다."]], [["“Run along, run along to Mariette,”", "\"가, 어서 마리에트에게로 가렴.\""], ["she said to Seryozha, who had followed her out, and she began walking up and down on the straw matting of the terrace.", "그녀는 자기를 따라 나온 세료자에게 말하고는 테라스의 짚자리 위를 이리저리 거닐기 시작했다."], ["“Can it be that they won’t forgive me, won’t understand how it all couldn’t be helped?”", "'저들이 정말로 나를 용서하지 않을까, 이 모든 게 어쩔 수 없었다는 걸 이해하지 못할까?'"], ["she said to herself.", "그녀는 혼잣말을 했다."]], [["Standing still, and looking at the tops of the aspen trees waving in the wind, with their freshly washed, brightly shining leaves in the cold sunshine, she knew that they would not forgive her, that everyone and everything would be merciless to her now as was that sky, that green.", "가만히 멈춰 서서, 차가운 햇살 속에 갓 씻겨 환하게 반짝이는 잎사귀를 단 사시나무 꼭대기가 바람에 흔들리는 것을 바라보면서, 그녀는 저들이 자기를 용서하지 않으리라는 것을, 이제 모든 사람과 모든 것이 저 하늘과 저 초록처럼 자기에게 무자비하리라는 것을 알았다."], ["And again she felt that everything was split in two in her soul.", "그리고 다시 그녀는 자기 영혼 속에서 모든 것이 둘로 쪼개지는 것을 느꼈다."], ["“I mustn’t, mustn’t think,”", "'생각하면 안 돼, 생각하면 안 돼.'"], ["she said to herself.", "그녀는 혼잣말을 했다."], ["“I must get ready.", "'준비를 해야 해."], ["To go where?", "어디로 가지?"], ["When?", "언제?"], ["Whom to take with me?", "누구를 데리고?"], ["Yes, to Moscow by the evening train.", "그래, 저녁 기차로 모스크바로 가는 거야."], ["Annushka and Seryozha, and only the most necessary things.", "안누시카와 세료자, 그리고 꼭 필요한 것들만."], ["But first I must write to them both.”", "하지만 먼저 두 사람 모두에게 편지를 써야 해.'"], ["She went quickly indoors into her boudoir, sat down at the table, and wrote to her husband:—“After what has happened, I cannot remain any longer in your house.", "그녀는 재빨리 안으로 들어가 자기 내실로 가서 탁자 앞에 앉아 남편에게 편지를 썼다. \"이번 일이 있은 후, 저는 더 이상 당신의 집에 머무를 수 없습니다."], ["I am going away, and taking my son with me.", "저는 떠나며, 아들을 데리고 갑니다."], ["I don’t know the law, and so I don’t know with which of the parents the son should remain; but I take him with me because I cannot live without him.", "저는 법을 모르고, 따라서 아들이 부모 중 누구와 함께 있어야 하는지도 모릅니다. 하지만 저는 그 아이 없이는 살 수 없기에 그를 데리고 갑니다."], ["Be generous, leave him to me.”", "너그럽게, 그 아이를 제게 남겨 주세요.\""]], [["Up to this point she wrote rapidly and naturally, but the appeal to his generosity, a quality she did not recognize in him, and the necessity of winding up the letter with something touching, pulled her up.", "여기까지는 빠르고 자연스럽게 썼지만, 그에게서 인정한 적 없는 자질인 그의 너그러움에 호소한다는 점, 그리고 편지를 무언가 마음을 움직이는 말로 마무리해야 한다는 필요가 그녀를 멈칫하게 했다."], ["“Of my fault and my remorse I cannot speak, because....”", "\"제 잘못과 후회에 대해서는 말할 수 없습니다. 왜냐하면······\""]], [["She stopped again, finding no connection in her ideas.", "그녀는 다시 멈추었다. 생각들 사이에 아무 연결고리도 찾을 수 없었다."], ["“No,”", "'아니야.'"], ["she said to herself, “there’s no need of anything,”", "그녀는 혼잣말을 했다. '아무것도 필요 없어.'"], ["and tearing up the letter, she wrote it again, leaving out the allusion to generosity, and sealed it up.", "그러고는 편지를 찢어 버리고, 너그러움에 대한 언급을 빼고 다시 썼으며, 그것을 봉했다."]], [["Another letter had to be written to Vronsky.", "브론스키에게도 편지를 한 통 써야 했다."], ["“I have told my husband,”", "\"저는 남편에게 말했어요.\""], ["she wrote, and she sat a long while unable to write more.", "그녀는 그렇게 썼고, 그 이상 쓰지 못한 채 오랫동안 앉아 있었다."], ["It was so coarse, so unfeminine.", "그것은 너무나 거칠고, 너무나 여자답지 못했다."], ["“And what more am I to write to him?”", "'그리고 그에게 무슨 말을 더 써야 하지?'"], ["she said to herself.", "그녀는 혼잣말을 했다."], ["Again a flush of shame spread over her face; she recalled his composure, and a feeling of anger against him impelled her to tear the sheet with the phrase she had written into tiny bits.", "다시 부끄러움의 홍조가 그녀의 얼굴에 번졌다. 그녀는 그의 침착함을 떠올렸고, 그에 대한 분노가 치밀어 자기가 써 놓은 그 문장이 적힌 종이를 잘게 찢어 버렸다."], ["“No need of anything,”", "'아무것도 필요 없어.'"], ["she said to herself, and closing her blotting-case she went upstairs, told the governess and the servants that she was going that day to Moscow, and at once set to work to pack up her things.", "그녀는 혼잣말을 하고는 압지첩을 덮고 위층으로 올라가, 가정교사와 하인들에게 자기가 그날 모스크바로 떠난다고 일렀으며, 곧바로 짐을 꾸리는 일에 착수했다."]], [["# Chapter 16", "# Chapter 16"]], [["All the rooms of the summer villa were full of porters, gardeners, and footmen going to and fro carrying out things.", "별장의 모든 방은 짐을 들고 이리저리 오가는 짐꾼과 정원사, 하인들로 가득했다."], ["Cupboards and chests were open; twice they had sent to the shop for cord; pieces of newspaper were tossing about on the floor.", "장롱과 궤짝들이 죄다 열려 있었고, 끈을 사 오라고 가게에 두 번이나 사람을 보냈으며, 신문지 조각들이 바닥에 나뒹굴고 있었다."], ["Two trunks, some bags and strapped-up rugs, had been carried down into the hall.", "트렁크 두 개와 가방 몇 개, 끈으로 묶은 무릎덮개 따위가 현관으로 옮겨져 있었다."], ["The carriage and two hired cabs were waiting at the steps.", "마차 한 대와 세를 낸 삯마차 두 대가 현관 계단 앞에서 기다리고 있었다."], ["Anna, forgetting her inward agitation in the work of packing, was standing at a table in her boudoir, packing her traveling bag, when Annushka called her attention to the rattle of some carriage driving up.", "짐을 꾸리는 일에 내면의 동요를 잊은 채, 안나는 자기 안방의 탁자 앞에 서서 여행용 가방을 챙기고 있었는데, 그때 안누슈카가 어떤 마차가 다가오는 덜컹거리는 소리에 그녀의 주의를 돌렸다."], ["Anna looked out of the window and saw Alexey Alexandrovitch’s courier on the steps, ringing at the front door bell.", "안나는 창밖을 내다보았고, 현관 계단 위에서 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 급사가 현관 초인종을 울리는 것을 보았다."]], [["“Run and find out what it is,”", "\"어서 가서 무슨 일인지 알아보거라.\""], ["she said, and with a calm sense of being prepared for anything, she sat down in a low chair, folding her hands on her knees.", "그녀는 말했다. 그리고 무슨 일에든 마음의 준비가 되어 있다는 차분한 느낌으로, 그녀는 낮은 의자에 앉아 두 손을 무릎 위에 모았다."], ["A footman brought in a thick packet directed in Alexey Alexandrovitch’s hand.", "한 하인이 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 필체로 주소가 적힌 두툼한 봉투를 들고 들어왔다."]], [["“The courier has orders to wait for an answer,”", "\"급사가 답장을 기다리라는 분부를 받았다고 합니다.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“Very well,”", "\"알았다.\""], ["she said, and as soon as he had left the room she tore open the letter with trembling fingers.", "그녀는 말했고, 그가 방에서 나가자마자 떨리는 손가락으로 편지를 뜯어 열었다."], ["A roll of unfolded notes done up in a wrapper fell out of it.", "봉투 안에서 종이로 싼, 접지 않은 지폐 한 뭉치가 떨어졌다."], ["She disengaged the letter and began reading it at the end.", "그녀는 편지를 꺼내 끝부분부터 읽기 시작했다."], ["“Preparations shall be made for your arrival here ...", "\"당신이 이리로 돌아올 수 있도록 준비를 갖추어 두겠소……"], ["I attach particular significance to compliance....” she read.", "이에 따라 주기를 각별히 중요하게 여기는 바이오……\""], ["She ran on, then back, read it all through, and once more read the letter all through again from the beginning.", "그녀는 읽었다. 죽 읽어 내려가다가 다시 거슬러 올라가, 전부 읽고는, 처음부터 한 번 더 편지를 끝까지 읽었다."], ["When she had finished, she felt that she was cold all over, and that a fearful calamity, such as she had not expected, had burst upon her.", "다 읽고 났을 때, 그녀는 온몸이 차갑게 식는 것을, 그리고 자기가 예상치 못했던 무시무시한 재앙이 자기 위로 들이닥쳤음을 느꼈다."]], [["In the morning she had regretted that she had spoken to her husband, and wished for nothing so much as that those words could be unspoken.", "아침에 그녀는 남편에게 말한 것을 후회했고, 그 말들이 입 밖에 나오지 않은 것이 되기를 무엇보다 간절히 바랐다."], ["And here this letter regarded them as unspoken, and gave her what she had wanted.", "그런데 이 편지는 그 말들을 없던 것으로 여기며, 그녀가 원했던 바를 그대로 주고 있었다."], ["But now this letter seemed to her more awful than anything she had been able to conceive.", "하지만 지금 이 편지는 그녀가 상상할 수 있었던 그 무엇보다도 끔찍하게 느껴졌다."]], [["“He’s right!”", "\"그이가 옳아!\" 그녀는 말했다."], ["she said; “of course, he’s always right; he’s a Christian, he’s generous!", "\"당연히, 그이는 늘 옳지. 그이는 기독교인이고, 너그러운 사람이니까!"], ["Yes, vile, base creature!", "그래, 비열하고 천한 인간 같으니!"], ["And no one understands it except me, and no one ever will; and I can’t explain it.", "그리고 나 말고는 아무도 그걸 이해하지 못하고, 앞으로도 영영 못 할 거야. 나로서는 설명할 수도 없어."], ["They say he’s so religious, so high-principled, so upright, so clever; but they don’t see what I’ve seen.", "사람들은 그이가 신앙심이 깊고, 고결한 원칙을 지니고, 정직하고, 영리하다고들 하지. 하지만 그들은 내가 본 것을 보지 못해."], ["They don’t know how he has crushed my life for eight years, crushed everything that was living in me—he has not once even thought that I’m a live woman who must have love.", "그이가 어떻게 팔 년 동안 내 인생을 짓밟았는지, 내 안에 살아 있던 모든 것을 짓이겨 놓았는지 그들은 몰라. 그이는 내가 사랑을 필요로 하는, 살아 있는 여자라는 걸 단 한 번도 생각해 본 적이 없어."], ["They don’t know how at every step he’s humiliated me, and been just as pleased with himself.", "그이가 어떻게 걸음마다 나를 모욕하면서도 그렇게 흐뭇해했는지 그들은 몰라."], ["Haven’t I striven, striven with all my strength, to find something to give meaning to my life?", "나는 안 그랬던가, 내 삶에 의미를 줄 무언가를 찾으려고 온 힘을 다해 애쓰고 또 애쓰지 않았던가?"], ["Haven’t I struggled to love him, to love my son when I could not love my husband?", "남편을 사랑할 수 없을 때 내 아들이라도 사랑하려고, 그이를 사랑하려고 발버둥치지 않았던가?"], ["But the time came when I knew that I couldn’t cheat myself any longer, that I was alive, that I was not to blame, that God has made me so that I must love and live.", "하지만 더 이상 나 자신을 속일 수 없다는 걸, 내가 살아 있다는 걸, 내게 잘못이 없다는 걸, 하느님이 나를 사랑하고 살아가야 하는 존재로 만드셨다는 걸 깨닫는 때가 왔어."], ["And now what does he do?", "그런데 이제 그이는 어떻게 하지?"], ["If he’d killed me, if he’d killed him, I could have borne anything, I could have forgiven anything; but, no, he....", "그이가 나를 죽였다면, 그 사람을 죽였다면, 나는 무엇이든 견딜 수 있었을 거고, 무엇이든 용서할 수 있었을 거야. 하지만 아니, 그이는……"], ["How was it I didn’t guess what he would do?", "어째서 나는 그이가 어떻게 나올지 짐작하지 못했을까?"], ["He’s doing just what’s characteristic of his mean character.", "그이는 그 비열한 성격에 딱 어울리는 짓을 하고 있는 거야."], ["He’ll keep himself in the right, while me, in my ruin, he’ll drive still lower to worse ruin yet....”", "그이는 자기는 옳은 쪽에 머물면서, 파멸한 나를 더더욱 끌어내려 더 끔찍한 파멸로 몰아넣겠지…….\""]], [["She recalled the words from the letter.", "그녀는 편지에 적힌 말들을 떠올렸다."], ["“You can conjecture what awaits you and your son....”", "\"당신과 당신 아들을 무엇이 기다리고 있을지 짐작이 갈 거요…….\""], ["“That’s a threat to take away my child, and most likely by their stupid law he can.", "\"저건 내 아이를 빼앗겠다는 위협이고, 그들의 어리석은 법대로라면 십중팔구 그이가 그렇게 할 수 있겠지."], ["But I know very well why he says it.", "하지만 그이가 왜 그런 말을 하는지 나는 아주 잘 알아."], ["He doesn’t believe even in my love for my child, or he despises it (just as he always used to ridicule it).", "그이는 내가 내 아이를 사랑한다는 것조차 믿지 않거나, 아니면 그걸 경멸하는 거야(늘 그걸 비웃곤 했던 것처럼). 그이는 내 안의 그 감정을 경멸하지."], ["He despises that feeling in me, but he knows that I won’t abandon my child, that I can’t abandon my child, that there could be no life for me without my child, even with him whom I love; but that if I abandoned my child and ran away from him, I should be acting like the most infamous, basest of women.", "하지만 내가 내 아이를 버리지 않으리라는 것을, 내 아이를 버릴 수 없다는 것을, 내 아이가 없으면 내게는, 내가 사랑하는 그 사람과 함께 있더라도, 삶이 있을 수 없다는 것을 그이는 알아. 그리고 만약 내가 내 아이를 버리고 그이에게서 도망친다면, 가장 파렴치하고 가장 천한 여자처럼 행동하는 셈이 되리라는 것을 그이는 알지."], ["He knows that, and knows that I am incapable of doing that.”", "그이는 그것을 알고, 또 내가 그런 짓을 할 수 없는 사람이라는 것도 알아.\""]], [["She recalled another sentence in the letter.", "그녀는 편지에 적힌 또 다른 문장을 떠올렸다."], ["“Our life must go on as it has done in the past....”", "\"우리의 생활은 예전과 다름없이 계속되어야 하오…….\" \"그 생활은 예전에도 충분히 비참했어."], ["“That life was miserable enough in the old days; it has been awful of late.", "요즘은 끔찍했지. 그러니 이제는 어떻게 될까?"], ["What will it be now?", "그이는 그걸 다 알아."], ["And he knows all that; he knows that I can’t repent that I breathe, that I love; he knows that it can lead to nothing but lying and deceit; but he wants to go on torturing me.", "그이는 내가 숨 쉬는 것을, 내가 사랑하는 것을 뉘우칠 수 없다는 걸 알아. 그것이 거짓과 기만 말고는 아무것도 낳을 수 없다는 걸 알면서도, 그이는 계속 나를 괴롭히고 싶어 해."], ["I know him; I know that he’s at home and is happy in deceit, like a fish swimming in the water.", "나는 그이를 알아. 나는 그이가 물속을 헤엄치는 물고기처럼 거짓 속에서 편안하고 행복해한다는 걸 알아."], ["No, I won’t give him that happiness.", "안 돼, 나는 그이에게 그런 행복을 주지 않을 거야."], ["I’ll break through the spiderweb of lies in which he wants to catch me, come what may.", "무슨 일이 있어도, 그이가 나를 옭아매려는 그 거짓의 거미줄을 뚫고 나가겠어."], ["Anything’s better than lying and deceit.”", "거짓과 기만보다는 무엇이든 나아.\""]], [["“But how? My God!", "\"하지만 어떻게?"], ["my God!", "맙소사!"], ["Was ever a woman so miserable as I am?...”", "맙소사! 나만큼 비참한 여자가 또 있었을까?…….\""]], [["“No; I will break through it, I will break through it!”", "\"아니, 나는 뚫고 나갈 거야, 뚫고 나가겠어!\""], ["she cried, jumping up and keeping back her tears.", "그녀는 눈물을 억누르며 벌떡 일어나 소리쳤다."], ["And she went to the writing-table to write him another letter.", "그리고 그에게 또 다른 편지를 쓰려고 책상으로 갔다."], ["But at the bottom of her heart she felt that she was not strong enough to break through anything, that she was not strong enough to get out of her old position, however false and dishonorable it might be.", "하지만 마음 깊은 곳에서, 그녀는 자기가 무언가를 뚫고 나갈 만큼 강하지 못하다는 것을, 그것이 아무리 거짓되고 떳떳하지 못할지라도 자기의 옛 처지에서 벗어날 만큼 강하지 못하다는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["She sat down at the writing-table, but instead of writing she clasped her hands on the table, and, laying her head on them, burst into tears, with sobs and heaving breast like a child crying.", "그녀는 책상 앞에 앉았지만, 쓰는 대신 두 손을 탁자 위에 포개고는 거기에 머리를 묻고, 우는 아이처럼 흐느끼며 가슴을 들썩이면서 울음을 터뜨렸다."], ["She was weeping that her dream of her position being made clear and definite had been annihilated forever.", "그녀는 자기 처지가 분명하고 확실하게 정리되리라던 꿈이 영원히 무너져 버린 것을 두고 울고 있었다."], ["She knew beforehand that everything would go on in the old way, and far worse, indeed, than in the old way.", "그녀는 모든 것이 예전 방식대로, 아니 사실은 예전보다 훨씬 더 나쁘게 흘러가리라는 것을 미리부터 알고 있었다."], ["She felt that the position in the world that she enjoyed, and that had seemed to her of so little consequence in the morning, that this position was precious to her, that she would not have the strength to exchange it for the shameful position of a woman who has abandoned husband and child to join her lover; that however much she might struggle, she could not be stronger than herself.", "그녀가 누리고 있던, 아침에는 그토록 하찮게 여겨졌던 그 사교계에서의 지위가 자기에게 소중하다는 것을, 남편과 아이를 버리고 정부에게로 달아난 여자라는 수치스러운 처지와 그 지위를 맞바꿀 힘이 자기에게 없으리라는 것을, 아무리 발버둥쳐도 자기 자신보다 더 강해질 수는 없으리라는 것을 그녀는 느꼈다."], ["She would never know freedom in love, but would remain forever a guilty wife, with the menace of detection hanging over her at every instant; deceiving her husband for the sake of a shameful connection with a man living apart and away from her, whose life she could never share.", "그녀는 결코 사랑 안에서 자유를 알지 못할 것이며, 매 순간 발각될지 모른다는 위협이 머리 위에 드리운 채, 영원히 죄지은 아내로 남을 터였다. 그녀와 떨어져 따로 사는, 결코 그 삶을 함께할 수 없는 한 남자와의 수치스러운 관계를 위해 남편을 속이면서."], ["She knew that this was how it would be, and at the same time it was so awful that she could not even conceive what it would end in.", "그녀는 일이 이렇게 되리라는 것을 알았고, 동시에 그것이 너무도 끔찍해서 그 끝이 어떻게 될지 짐작조차 할 수 없었다."], ["And she cried without restraint, as children cry when they are punished.", "그래서 그녀는 벌받는 아이가 울듯이 거리낌 없이 울었다."]], [["The sound of the footman’s steps forced her to rouse herself, and, hiding her face from him, she pretended to be writing.", "하인의 발소리에 그녀는 정신을 차릴 수밖에 없었고, 그에게서 얼굴을 가리며 무언가를 쓰는 척했다."]], [["“The courier asks if there’s an answer,”", "\"급사가 답장이 있는지 여쭙니다.\""], ["the footman announced.", "하인이 알렸다."]], [["“An answer?", "\"답장?"], ["Yes,”", "그래.\""], ["said Anna.", "안나가 말했다."], ["“Let him wait.", "\"기다리라고 해라."], ["I’ll ring.”", "내가 종을 울리마.\""]], [["“What can I write?”", "'무슨 말을 써야 하나?'"], ["she thought.", "그녀는 생각했다."], ["“What can I decide upon alone?", "'나 혼자서 무엇을 결정할 수 있단 말인가?"], ["What do I know?", "내가 무엇을 알지?"], ["What do I want?", "내가 무엇을 원하지?"], ["What is there I care for?”", "내게 소중한 게 뭐가 있지?'"], ["Again she felt that her soul was beginning to be split in two.", "다시 그녀는 자기 영혼이 둘로 쪼개지기 시작하는 것을 느꼈다."], ["She was terrified again at this feeling, and clutched at the first pretext for doing something which might divert her thoughts from herself. “I ought to see Alexey”", "이 느낌에 그녀는 또다시 겁이 났고, 자기 자신에게서 생각을 돌릴 수 있는 무언가를 할 첫 번째 구실에 매달렸다."], ["(so she called Vronsky in her thoughts); “no one but he can tell me what I ought to do.", "'알렉세이를 만나야 해(그녀는 마음속으로 브론스키를 그렇게 불렀다). 내가 어떻게 해야 할지 말해 줄 사람은 그이밖에 없어."], ["I’ll go to Betsy’s, perhaps I shall see him there,”", "베치네 집으로 가자, 어쩌면 거기서 그이를 만날 수 있을지도 몰라.' 그녀는 혼잣말을 했다."], ["she said to herself, completely forgetting that when she had told him the day before that she was not going to Princess Tverskaya’s, he had said that in that case he should not go either.", "어제 트베르스카야 공작부인 댁에 가지 않겠다고 그에게 말했을 때, 그렇다면 자기도 가지 않겠다고 그가 했던 말은 까맣게 잊은 채였다. 그녀는 탁자로 가서 남편에게 이렇게 썼다."], ["She went up to the table, wrote to her husband, “I have received your letter.—A.”; and, ringing the bell, gave it to the footman.", "\"당신의 편지를 받았습니다.—A.\" 그러고는 종을 울려 하인에게 그것을 건넸다."]], [["“We are not going,”", "\"우리는 가지 않는다.\""], ["she said to Annushka, as she came in.", "그녀는 안누슈카가 들어오자 말했다."]], [["“Not going at all?”", "\"아예 안 가시는 거예요?\""]], [["“No; don’t unpack till tomorrow, and let the carriage wait.", "\"그래. 내일까지는 짐을 풀지 말고, 마차는 기다리게 해 둬라."], ["I’m going to the princess’s.”", "나는 공작부인 댁에 갈 거야.\""]], [["“Which dress am I to get ready?”", "\"어느 드레스를 준비해 드릴까요?\""]], [["# Chapter 17", "# Chapter 17"]], [["The croquet party to which the Princess Tverskaya had invited Anna was to consist of two ladies and their adorers.", "트베르스카야 공작 부인이 안나를 초대한 크로켓 모임은 부인 둘과 그들을 흠모하는 남자들로 이루어질 예정이었다."], ["These two ladies were the chief representatives of a select new Petersburg circle, nicknamed, in imitation of some imitation, les sept merveilles du monde.", "이 두 부인은 어떤 모방을 또 모방하여 les sept merveilles du monde라고 별명 붙은, 페테르부르크의 선택받은 새 사교계를 대표하는 인물들이었다."], ["These ladies belonged to a circle which, though of the highest society, was utterly hostile to that in which Anna moved.", "이 부인들은 최상류 사교계에 속하면서도 안나가 드나드는 사교계와는 철저히 적대적인 모임의 일원이었다."], ["Moreover, Stremov, one of the most influential people in Petersburg, and the elderly admirer of Liza Merkalova, was Alexey Alexandrovitch’s enemy in the political world.", "게다가 페테르부르크에서 가장 영향력 있는 인물 가운데 하나이자 리자 메르칼로바의 나이 든 숭배자인 스트레모프는 정치계에서 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 적이었다."], ["From all these considerations Anna had not meant to go, and the hints in Princess Tverskaya’s note referred to her refusal.", "이 모든 사정을 고려해 안나는 가지 않을 작정이었고, 트베르스카야 공작 부인의 쪽지에 담긴 암시도 그녀의 거절을 두고 한 말이었다. 그러나 지금 안나는 브론스키를 만날 수 있으리라는 기대로 가고 싶어 안달이 났다."], ["But now Anna was eager to go, in the hope of seeing Vronsky.", "* les sept merveilles du monde: 프랑스어로 '세계의 일곱 가지 불가사의'."]], [["Anna arrived at Princess Tverskaya’s earlier than the other guests.", "안나는 다른 손님들보다 먼저 트베르스카야 공작 부인의 집에 도착했다."]], [["At the same moment as she entered, Vronsky’s footman, with side-whiskers combed out like a Kammerjunker, went in too.", "그녀가 들어서는 바로 그 순간, 시종처럼 빗어 넘긴 구레나룻을 한 브론스키의 하인도 안으로 들어왔다."], ["He stopped at the door, and, taking off his cap, let her pass.", "그는 문 앞에 멈춰 서서 모자를 벗고 그녀에게 길을 내주었다."], ["Anna recognized him, and only then recalled that Vronsky had told her the day before that he would not come.", "안나는 그를 알아보았고, 그제야 어제 브론스키가 오지 않겠다고 말한 것을 떠올렸다. 아마 그가 그것을 알리는 쪽지를 보낸 모양이었다."], ["Most likely he was sending a note to say so.", "* Kammerjunker: 독일어로 궁정 시종을 뜻하는 관직명."]], [["As she took off her outer garment in the hall, she heard the footman, pronouncing his “r’s”", "현관에서 겉옷을 벗는 동안, 그녀는 그 하인이 시종처럼 'r'"], ["even like a Kammerjunker, say, “From the count for the princess,” and hand the note.", "발음을 또렷이 굴리며 \"백작님이 공작 부인께 보내는 것입니다\"라고 말하며 쪽지를 건네는 소리를 들었다."]], [["She longed to question him as to where his master was.", "그녀는 그에게 주인이 어디 있는지 묻고 싶어 견딜 수 없었다."], ["She longed to turn back and send him a letter to come and see her, or to go herself to see him.", "발길을 돌려 그에게 자기를 만나러 와 달라는 편지를 보내거나, 아니면 자기가 직접 그를 만나러 가고 싶어 견딜 수 없었다."], ["But neither the first nor the second nor the third course was possible.", "그러나 첫 번째도, 두 번째도, 세 번째도 불가능했다."], ["Already she heard bells ringing to announce her arrival ahead of her, and Princess Tverskaya’s footman was standing at the open door waiting for her to go forward into the inner rooms.", "벌써 그녀의 도착을 알리는 종소리가 그녀보다 앞서 울리는 것이 들렸고, 트베르스카야 공작 부인의 하인은 그녀가 안쪽 방으로 들어가기를 기다리며 열린 문 앞에 서 있었다."]], [["“The princess is in the garden; they will inform her immediately.", "\"공작 부인께서는 정원에 계십니다. 곧 알려 드리겠습니다."], ["Would you be pleased to walk into the garden?”", "정원으로 드시겠습니까?\""], ["announced another footman in another room.", "다른 방에서 또 다른 하인이 아뢰었다."]], [["The position of uncertainty, of indecision, was still the same as at home—worse, in fact, since it was impossible to take any step, impossible to see Vronsky, and she had to remain here among outsiders, in company so uncongenial to her present mood.", "불확실하고 결단을 내리지 못하는 처지는 집에 있을 때와 똑같았다—아니, 사실 더 나빴다. 아무런 행동도 취할 수 없고, 브론스키를 만날 수도 없으며, 지금 자기 기분에 그토록 맞지 않는 사람들 틈에 끼어 이곳에 남아 있어야 했으니까."], ["But she was wearing a dress that she knew suited her.", "그러나 그녀는 자기에게 잘 어울리는 줄 아는 옷을 입고 있었다."], ["She was not alone; all around was that luxurious setting of idleness that she was used to, and she felt less wretched than at home.", "그녀는 혼자가 아니었고, 사방에는 그녀가 익숙한, 한가로움이 빚어내는 그 호화로운 분위기가 펼쳐져 있었으므로, 집에 있을 때보다는 덜 비참했다."], ["She was not forced to think what she was to do.", "무엇을 해야 할지 굳이 생각할 필요도 없었다."], ["Everything would be done of itself.", "모든 것이 저절로 풀려 갈 터였다."], ["On meeting Betsy coming towards her in a white gown that struck her by its elegance, Anna smiled at her just as she always did.", "우아함으로 그녀를 사로잡는 하얀 드레스 차림으로 마주 걸어오는 벳시를 만나자, 안나는 늘 그러듯 그녀에게 미소를 지었다."], ["Princess Tverskaya was walking with Tushkevitch and a young lady, a relation, who, to the great joy of her parents in the provinces, was spending the summer with the fashionable princess.", "트베르스카야 공작 부인은 투슈케비치, 그리고 친척인 젊은 아가씨와 함께 걷고 있었는데, 그 아가씨는 지방에 있는 부모를 더없이 기쁘게 하며 그 유행을 선도하는 공작 부인과 함께 여름을 보내는 중이었다."]], [["There was probably something unusual about Anna, for Betsy noticed it at once.", "안나에게는 아마 어딘가 예사롭지 않은 데가 있었던 모양이다. 벳시가 단번에 그것을 알아챘으니까."]], [["“I slept badly,”", "\"잠을 설쳤어요.\""], ["answered Anna, looking intently at the footman who came to meet them, and, as she supposed, brought Vronsky’s note.", "안나는 자기들을 맞으러 온, 그리고 그녀 생각에는 브론스키의 쪽지를 가져온 하인을 뚫어지게 바라보며 대답했다."]], [["“How glad I am you’ve come!”", "\"와 줘서 정말 기뻐요!\""], ["said Betsy.", "벳시가 말했다."], ["“I’m tired, and was just longing to have some tea before they come.", "\"난 지쳤어요. 다른 사람들이 오기 전에 차나 좀 마시고 싶던 참이었죠."], ["You might go”—she turned to Tushkevitch—“with Masha, and try the croquet ground over there where they’ve been cutting it.", "당신은\"—그녀가 투슈케비치 쪽으로 돌아섰다—\"마샤랑 같이 가서, 저쪽에 풀을 깎아 둔 크로켓 경기장을 한번 봐 줘요."], ["We shall have time to talk a little over tea; we’ll have a cozy chat, eh?”", "우린 차를 마시면서 잠깐 얘기나 나눌게요. 둘이서 오붓하게 수다나 떨자고요, 응?\""], ["she said in English to Anna, with a smile, pressing the hand with which she held a parasol.", "그녀는 양산을 쥔 손으로 안나의 손을 꼭 누르며 미소 띤 얼굴로 영어로 말했다."]], [["“Yes, especially as I can’t stay very long with you.", "\"좋아요, 그렇잖아도 당신과 오래 있을 수가 없거든요."], ["I’m forced to go on to old Madame Vrede.", "브레데 노부인 댁에 들러야 해서요."], ["I’ve been promising to go for a century,”", "백 년 전부터 가겠다고 약속만 해 놓고 있었지 뭐예요.\""], ["said Anna, to whom lying, alien as it was to her nature, had become not merely simple and natural in society, but a positive source of satisfaction.", "안나가 말했다. 거짓말이란 그녀의 천성에 그토록 낯선 것이었으나, 사교계에서는 그저 쉽고 자연스러운 일이 되었을 뿐 아니라 오히려 흡족함을 안겨 주는 일이 되어 있었다."], ["Why she said this, which she had not thought of a second before, she could not have explained.", "바로 일 초 전까지만 해도 생각조차 하지 않았던 이 말을 왜 했는지, 그녀 자신도 설명할 수 없었으리라."], ["She had said it simply from the reflection that as Vronsky would not be here, she had better secure her own freedom, and try to see him somehow.", "그녀가 이 말을 한 것은 그저, 브론스키가 여기 오지 않을 테니 차라리 자기 자유를 확보해 두었다가 어떻게든 그를 만나 보는 편이 낫겠다는 생각에서였다."], ["But why she had spoken of old Madame Vrede, whom she had to go and see, as she had to see many other people, she could not have explained; and yet, as it afterwards turned out, had she contrived the most cunning devices to meet Vronsky, she could have thought of nothing better.", "하지만 다른 많은 사람을 찾아가야 하듯 찾아가야 하는 브레데 노부인을 왜 입에 올렸는지는 설명할 수 없었으리라. 그러나 나중에 드러난 바로는, 브론스키를 만나기 위해 아무리 교묘한 책략을 꾸몄다 한들 이보다 더 나은 수는 생각해 낼 수 없었을 것이다."]], [["“No.", "\"아니요."], ["I’m not going to let you go for anything,”", "난 무슨 일이 있어도 당신을 보내 주지 않을 거예요.\""], ["answered Betsy, looking intently into Anna’s face.", "벳시가 안나의 얼굴을 뚫어지게 들여다보며 대답했다."], ["“Really, if I were not fond of you, I should feel offended.", "\"정말이지, 내가 당신을 좋아하지 않았다면 모욕감을 느꼈을 거예요."], ["One would think you were afraid my society would compromise you.", "마치 내 곁에 있으면 당신 체면이 깎이기라도 할까 봐 두려워하는 사람처럼 구네요."], ["Tea in the little dining-room, please,”", "작은 식당에 차를 내 줘요.\""], ["she said, half closing her eyes, as she always did when addressing the footman.", "그녀는 하인에게 말을 건넬 때면 늘 그러듯 눈을 반쯤 감으며 말했다."]], [["Taking the note from him, she read it.", "그에게서 쪽지를 받아 든 그녀는 그것을 읽었다."]], [["“Alexey’s playing us false,”", "\"알렉세이가 우릴 골탕 먹이네요.\""], ["she said in French; “he writes that he can’t come,”", "그녀는 프랑스어로 말했다. \"올 수 없다고 쓰여 있어요.\""], ["she added in a tone as simple and natural as though it could never enter her head that Vronsky could mean anything more to Anna than a game of croquet.", "그녀는 브론스키가 안나에게 크로켓 한 판 이상의 어떤 의미를 지닐 수 있으리라고는 머릿속에 떠올릴 수조차 없다는 듯, 더없이 단순하고 자연스러운 어조로 덧붙였다."], ["Anna knew that Betsy knew everything, but, hearing how she spoke of Vronsky before her, she almost felt persuaded for a minute that she knew nothing.", "안나는 벳시가 모든 것을 알고 있다는 것을 알았지만, 자기 앞에서 브론스키를 두고 그녀가 말하는 것을 들으니, 순간 그녀가 아무것도 모른다고 거의 믿을 뻔했다."]], [["“Ah!”", "\"어머!\""], ["said Anna indifferently, as though not greatly interested in the matter, and she went on smiling: “How can you or your friends compromise anyone?”", "안나는 그 일에 별로 관심이 없는 듯 무심하게 말하고는 계속 미소를 지으며 말을 이었다. \"당신이나 당신 친구들이 어떻게 누군가의 체면을 깎을 수가 있겠어요?\""]], [["This playing with words, this hiding of a secret, had a great fascination for Anna, as, indeed, it has for all women.", "이런 말장난, 비밀을 감추는 이 놀이는 안나에게 큰 매력으로 다가왔고, 사실 모든 여자에게 그러하듯이 말이다."], ["And it was not the necessity of concealment, not the aim with which the concealment was contrived, but the process of concealment itself which attracted her.", "그리고 그녀를 끌어당긴 것은 감출 필요성도, 그 감춤이 노리는 목적도 아니라, 감추는 그 과정 자체였다."]], [["“I can’t be more Catholic than the Pope,”", "\"교황보다 더 가톨릭적일 수는 없잖아요.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“Stremov and Liza Merkalova, why, they’re the cream of the cream of society.", "\"스트레모프와 리자 메르칼로바라니, 그 사람들이야말로 사교계의 알짜배기 중의 알짜배기인걸요."], ["Besides, they’re received everywhere, and I”—she laid special stress on the I—“have never been strict and intolerant.", "게다가 어딜 가나 환영받는 사람들이고, 나는\"—그녀는 '나'라는 말에 특별히 힘을 주었다—\"단 한 번도 깐깐하거나 옹졸하게 군 적이 없어요."], ["It’s simply that I haven’t the time.”", "그저 시간이 없을 뿐이죠.\""]], [["“No; you don’t care, perhaps, to meet Stremov?", "\"아니요, 어쩌면 스트레모프를 만나기 싫은 거 아니에요?"], ["Let him and Alexey Alexandrovitch tilt at each other in the committee—that’s no affair of ours.", "그 사람이랑 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 위원회에서 서로 창을 겨누든 말든—그건 우리가 상관할 일이 아니죠."], ["But in the world, he’s the most amiable man I know, and a devoted croquet player.", "하지만 사교계에서는 그 사람이 내가 아는 가장 상냥한 남자랍니다. 게다가 크로켓에 푹 빠져 있고요."], ["You shall see.", "곧 알게 될 거예요."], ["And, in spite of his absurd position as Liza’s lovesick swain at his age, you ought to see how he carries off the absurd position.", "그리고 그 나이에 리자에게 상사병이 든 우스꽝스러운 처지인데도, 그 우스운 처지를 얼마나 멋들어지게 넘기는지 좀 봐야 해요."], ["He’s very nice.", "아주 괜찮은 사람이에요."], ["Sappho Shtoltz you don’t know?", "사포 시톨츠는 모르죠?"], ["Oh, that’s a new type, quite new.”", "오, 그건 새로운 유형이에요, 아주 새롭죠.\""]], [["Betsy said all this, and, at the same time, from her good-humored, shrewd glance, Anna felt that she partly guessed her plight, and was hatching something for her benefit.", "벳시는 이 모든 말을 했고, 동시에 그녀의 쾌활하고 영악한 눈빛에서 안나는 그녀가 자기 곤경을 어느 정도 눈치챘으며, 자기를 위해 무언가를 꾸미고 있다는 것을 느꼈다."], ["They were in the little boudoir.", "두 사람은 작은 내실에 있었다."]], [["“I must write to Alexey though,”", "\"그래도 알렉세이에게는 편지를 써야겠어요.\""], ["and Betsy sat down to the table, scribbled a few lines, and put the note in an envelope.", "그러고는 벳시가 탁자 앞에 앉아 몇 줄 끄적이고는 쪽지를 봉투에 넣었다."]], [["“I’m telling him to come to dinner.", "\"저녁 식사에 오라고 쓰는 거예요."], ["I’ve one lady extra to dinner with me, and no man to take her in.", "식사에 부인 한 명이 더 오는데 모셔 들일 남자가 없거든요."], ["Look what I’ve said, will that persuade him?", "내가 뭐라고 썼는지 봐요, 이러면 그 사람 마음이 움직일까요?"], ["Excuse me, I must leave you for a minute.", "미안하지만 잠깐 자리를 비워야겠어요."], ["Would you seal it up, please, and send it off?”", "이걸 봉해서 보내 주겠어요?\""], ["she said from the door; “I have to give some directions.”", "그녀가 문 쪽에서 말했다. \"지시할 게 좀 있어서요.\""]], [["Without a moment’s thought, Anna sat down to the table with Betsy’s letter, and, without reading it, wrote below: “It’s essential for me to see you.", "안나는 잠깐의 망설임도 없이 벳시의 편지를 들고 탁자 앞에 앉았고, 그것을 읽지도 않은 채 그 아래에 이렇게 썼다. \"당신을 꼭 만나야 해요."], ["Come to the Vrede garden.", "브레데 정원으로 와요."], ["I shall be there at six o’clock.”", "여섯 시에 거기 있을게요.\""], ["She sealed it up, and, Betsy coming back, in her presence handed the note to be taken.", "그녀는 그것을 봉했고, 벳시가 돌아오자 그녀가 보는 앞에서 쪽지를 부치라고 건넸다."]], [["At tea, which was brought them on a little tea-table in the cool little drawing-room, the cozy chat promised by Princess Tverskaya before the arrival of her visitors really did come off between the two women.", "서늘한 작은 응접실에 놓인 작은 찻상으로 차가 들어왔고, 손님들이 오기 전에 트베르스카야 공작 부인이 약속했던 오붓한 수다가 정말로 두 여인 사이에서 벌어졌다."], ["They criticized the people they were expecting, and the conversation fell upon Liza Merkalova.", "그들은 오기로 한 사람들을 도마에 올렸고, 화제는 리자 메르칼로바에게로 옮겨 갔다."]], [["“She’s very sweet, and I always liked her,”", "\"그 사람 참 사랑스러워요, 난 늘 그 사람이 좋았어요.\""], ["said Anna.", "안나가 말했다."]], [["“You ought to like her.", "\"좋아할 만하죠."], ["She raves about you.", "그 사람도 당신한테 푹 빠져 있는걸요."], ["Yesterday she came up to me after the races and was in despair at not finding you.", "어제 경마가 끝나고 나한테 다가오더니 당신을 찾지 못했다고 절망하더라고요."], ["She says you’re a real heroine of romance, and that if she were a man she would do all sorts of mad things for your sake.", "당신이 진짜 소설 속 여주인공 같다면서, 자기가 남자였다면 당신을 위해 온갖 미친 짓도 마다하지 않았을 거라지 뭐예요."], ["Stremov says she does that as it is.”", "스트레모프는 지금도 그 사람이 그러고 있다고 하던데요.\""]], [["“But do tell me, please, I never could make it out,”", "\"그런데 부탁이니 말 좀 해 줘요, 난 도무지 알 수가 없거든요.\""], ["said Anna, after being silent for some time, speaking in a tone that showed she was not asking an idle question, but that what she was asking was of more importance to her than it should have been; “do tell me, please, what are her relations with Prince Kaluzhsky, Mishka, as he’s called?", "안나가 한동안 입을 다물고 있다가, 한가한 질문을 하는 게 아니라 자기가 묻는 것이 마땅한 정도 이상으로 그녀에게 중요한 일임을 드러내는 어조로 말했다. \"부탁이니 말 좀 해 줘요, 그 사람이랑 칼루시스키 공작, 미시카라고 불리는 그 사람 사이는 대체 어떤 관계예요?"], ["I’ve met them so little.", "난 그 사람들을 너무 가끔밖에 못 봐서요."], ["What does it mean?”", "그게 무슨 뜻이에요?\""]], [["Betsy smiled with her eyes, and looked intently at Anna.", "벳시는 눈으로 미소를 지으며 안나를 뚫어지게 바라보았다."]], [["“It’s a new manner,”", "\"그게 새로운 방식이에요.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“They’ve all adopted that manner.", "\"다들 그 방식을 따르고 있죠."], ["They’ve flung their caps over the windmills.", "체면이고 뭐고 다 내던져 버린 거예요."], ["But there are ways and ways of flinging them.”", "하지만 내던지는 데도 이런저런 방식이 있는 법이죠.\""]], [["“Yes, but what are her relations precisely with Kaluzhsky?”", "\"네, 그런데 그 사람이랑 칼루시스키의 관계가 정확히 어떤 거냐고요.\""]], [["Betsy broke into unexpectedly mirthful and irrepressible laughter, a thing which rarely happened with her.", "벳시가 뜻밖에도 유쾌하고 억누를 수 없는 웃음을 터뜨렸는데, 그녀에게는 좀처럼 없는 일이었다."]], [["“You’re encroaching on Princess Myakaya’s special domain now.", "\"이제 미야흐카야 공작 부인의 전문 영역을 넘보고 있군요."], ["That’s the question of an enfant terrible,”", "그건 enfant terrible나 던질 법한 질문이에요.\""], ["and Betsy obviously tried to restrain herself, but could not, and went off into peals of that infectious laughter that people laugh who do not laugh often.", "벳시는 분명 자제하려 애썼지만 그러지 못하고, 자주 웃지 않는 사람들이나 짓는 그 전염성 강한 폭소를 터뜨리고 말았다. \"차라리 그 사람들한테 직접 물어보는 게 낫겠어요.\""], ["“You’d better ask them,”", "그녀가 웃음의 눈물 사이로 간신히 말했다."], ["she brought out, between tears of laughter.", "* enfant terrible: 프랑스어로 '무서운 아이', 거리낌 없이 곤란한 말을 내뱉는 사람을 이르는 말."]], [["“No; you laugh,”", "\"아니요, 당신은 웃지만요.\""], ["said Anna, laughing too in spite of herself, “but I never could understand it.", "안나도 자기도 모르게 따라 웃으며 말했다. \"난 도무지 이해할 수가 없어요."], ["I can’t understand the husband’s rôle in it.”", "거기서 남편이 맡는 역할을 알 수가 없단 말이죠.\""]], [["“The husband?", "\"남편이요?"], ["Liza Merkalova’s husband carries her shawl, and is always ready to be of use.", "리자 메르칼로바의 남편은 그 사람 숄이나 들고 다니고, 늘 도울 채비가 되어 있죠."], ["But anything more than that in reality, no one cares to inquire.", "하지만 실제로 그 이상의 무언가에 대해서는, 아무도 캐물으려 들지 않아요."], ["You know in decent society one doesn’t talk or think even of certain details of the toilet.", "알다시피 점잖은 사교계에서는 몸치장의 어떤 세세한 부분에 대해 말하지도, 생각하지도 않잖아요."], ["That’s how it is with this.”", "이것도 바로 그런 거예요.\""]], [["“Will you be at Madame Rolandak’s fête?”", "\"롤란다크 부인의 잔치에는 갈 거예요?\""], ["asked Anna, to change the conversation.", "안나가 화제를 바꾸려고 물었다."]], [["“I don’t think so,”", "\"안 갈 것 같아요.\""], ["answered Betsy, and, without looking at her friend, she began filling the little transparent cups with fragrant tea.", "벳시가 대답하고는, 친구를 보지도 않은 채 향긋한 차를 작은 투명 잔에 따르기 시작했다."], ["Putting a cup before Anna, she took out a cigarette, and, fitting it into a silver holder, she lighted it.", "안나 앞에 잔을 놓아 준 그녀는 담배 한 개비를 꺼내 은제 물부리에 끼우고 불을 붙였다."]], [["“It’s like this, you see: I’m in a fortunate position,”", "\"이런 거예요, 봐요. 난 운이 좋은 처지에 있죠.\""], ["she began, quite serious now, as she took up her cup.", "그녀가 잔을 들며 이제 자못 진지하게 입을 열었다."], ["“I understand you, and I understand Liza.", "\"난 당신을 이해하고, 리자도 이해해요."], ["Liza now is one of those naïve natures that, like children, don’t know what’s good and what’s bad.", "리자는 어린아이들처럼 무엇이 좋고 무엇이 나쁜지 모르는 그런 순진한 천성을 지닌 사람 중 하나예요."], ["Anyway, she didn’t comprehend it when she was very young.", "어쨌거나 아주 어렸을 때는 그걸 이해하지 못했죠."], ["And now she’s aware that the lack of comprehension suits her.", "그리고 지금은 그렇게 이해하지 못하는 게 자기한테 어울린다는 걸 알아챈 거예요."], ["Now, perhaps, she doesn’t know on purpose,”", "어쩌면 지금은 일부러 모르는 척하는 건지도 모르죠.\""], ["said Betsy, with a subtle smile.", "벳시가 야릇한 미소를 띠며 말했다."], ["“But, anyway, it suits her.", "\"하지만 어쨌든 그게 그 사람한테 어울려요."], ["The very same thing, don’t you see, may be looked at tragically, and turned into a misery, or it may be looked at simply and even humorously.", "알겠어요, 똑같은 일이라도 비극적으로 바라보면 비참한 게 되어 버리지만, 단순하게, 심지어 익살스럽게 바라볼 수도 있는 거예요."], ["Possibly you are inclined to look at things too tragically.”", "어쩌면 당신은 매사를 너무 비극적으로 바라보는 경향이 있는지도 몰라요.\""]], [["“How I should like to know other people just as I know myself!”", "\"다른 사람들을 나 자신처럼 속속들이 알 수 있다면 얼마나 좋을까요!\""], ["said Anna, seriously and dreamily.", "안나가 진지하게, 그리고 꿈꾸듯 말했다."], ["“Am I worse than other people, or better?", "\"난 다른 사람들보다 못한 걸까요, 아니면 나은 걸까요?"], ["I think I’m worse.”", "난 내가 못하다고 생각해요.\""]], [["“Enfant terrible, enfant terrible!”", "\"Enfant terrible, enfant terrible 같으니!\""], ["repeated Betsy.", "벳시가 거듭 말했다."], ["“But here they are.”", "\"어머, 다들 왔네요.\""]], [["# Chapter 18", "# Chapter 18"]], [["They heard the sound of steps and a man’s voice, then a woman’s voice and laughter, and immediately thereafter there walked in the expected guests: Sappho Shtoltz, and a young man beaming with excess of health, the so-called Vaska.", "발소리와 남자의 목소리가 들리고, 이어 여자의 목소리와 웃음소리가 들리더니, 곧바로 기다리던 손님들이 들어왔다. 사포 시톨츠와, 넘쳐 나는 건강미로 환하게 빛나는 젊은 남자, 이른바 바스카였다."], ["It was evident that ample supplies of beefsteak, truffles, and Burgundy never failed to reach him at the fitting hour.", "비프스테이크와 송로버섯과 부르고뉴 포도주가 적당한 시간에 그에게 넉넉히 공급되어 한 번도 끊긴 적이 없으리라는 것이 한눈에 보였다."], ["Vaska bowed to the two ladies, and glanced at them, but only for one second.", "바스카는 두 부인에게 인사를 하고 그들을 흘끗 보았으나, 그것도 단 일 초뿐이었다."], ["He walked after Sappho into the drawing-room, and followed her about as though he were chained to her, keeping his sparkling eyes fixed on her as though he wanted to eat her.", "그는 사포를 따라 응접실로 들어와, 마치 그녀에게 사슬로 묶이기라도 한 듯 그녀의 뒤를 졸졸 따라다니며, 그녀를 잡아먹기라도 할 것처럼 반짝이는 눈을 그녀에게서 떼지 않았다."], ["Sappho Shtoltz was a blonde beauty with black eyes.", "사포 시톨츠는 검은 눈을 가진 금발 미인이었다."], ["She walked with smart little steps in high-heeled shoes, and shook hands with the ladies vigorously like a man.", "그녀는 굽 높은 구두를 신고 멋스러운 종종걸음으로 걸으며, 남자처럼 힘차게 부인들과 악수를 나누었다."]], [["Anna had never met this new star of fashion, and was struck by her beauty, the exaggerated extreme to which her dress was carried, and the boldness of her manners.", "안나는 이 새로운 유행의 별을 한 번도 만난 적이 없었기에, 그녀의 아름다움과, 극단적으로 과장된 옷차림과, 대담한 몸가짐에 깜짝 놀랐다."], ["On her head there was such a superstructure of soft, golden hair—her own and false mixed—that her head was equal in size to the elegantly rounded bust, of which so much was exposed in front.", "그녀의 머리 위에는 진짜와 가짜가 뒤섞인 부드러운 금빛 머리칼이 어찌나 높이 쌓아 올려져 있는지, 머리통이 우아하게 둥글린, 앞이 잔뜩 드러난 가슴팍과 맞먹는 크기였다."], ["The impulsive abruptness of her movements was such that at every step the lines of her knees and the upper part of her legs were distinctly marked under her dress, and the question involuntarily rose to the mind where in the undulating, piled-up mountain of material at the back the real body of the woman, so small and slender, so naked in front, and so hidden behind and below, really came to an end.", "그녀의 움직임은 충동적이고 갑작스러워, 한 걸음 내디딜 때마다 옷 아래로 무릎과 허벅지 윗부분의 윤곽이 또렷이 드러났고, 앞은 그토록 벌거벗고 뒤와 아래로는 그토록 감추어진, 그토록 작고 가냘픈 이 여자의 진짜 몸이, 뒤쪽으로 출렁이며 산처럼 쌓아 올린 옷감 속 어디쯤에서 실제로 끝나는 것인지, 절로 궁금증이 떠올랐다."]], [["Betsy made haste to introduce her to Anna.", "베치는 서둘러 그녀를 안나에게 소개했다."]], [["“Only fancy, we all but ran over two soldiers,”", "\"글쎄, 우리 하마터면 병사 둘을 칠 뻔했지 뭐예요.\""], ["she began telling them at once, using her eyes, smiling and twitching away her tail, which she flung back at one stroke all on one side.", "그녀는 눈을 굴리고 미소를 지으며 단번에 한쪽으로 휙 젖힌 드레스 자락을 흔들면서 곧장 이야기를 늘어놓기 시작했다."], ["“I drove here with Vaska....", "\"바스카랑 같이 여기까지 왔어요……"], ["Ah, to be sure, you don’t know each other.”", "아, 참, 두 분은 서로 모르시지.\""], ["And mentioning his surname she introduced the young man, and reddening a little, broke into a ringing laugh at her mistake—that is, at her having called him Vaska to a stranger.", "그러고는 그의 성을 대며 젊은 남자를 소개하더니, 살짝 얼굴을 붉히며 자신의 실수에—그러니까 낯선 사람 앞에서 그를 바스카라고 불러 버린 것에—까르르 웃음을 터뜨렸다."], ["Vaska bowed once more to Anna, but he said nothing to her.", "바스카는 안나에게 다시 한번 인사를 했으나, 그녀에게는 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["He addressed Sappho: “You’ve lost your bet.", "그는 사포를 향해 말했다. \"내기에 졌어요."], ["We got here first.", "우리가 먼저 도착했잖아요."], ["Pay up,”", "갚으셔야죠.\""], ["said he, smiling.", "그가 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["Sappho laughed still more festively.", "사포는 더욱더 즐겁게 웃었다."]], [["“Not just now,”", "\"지금은 안 돼요.\""], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“Oh, all right, I’ll have it later.”", "\"아, 알았어요, 나중에 받죠.\""]], [["“Very well, very well.", "\"좋아요, 좋아."], ["Oh, yes.”", "아, 참.\""], ["She turned suddenly to Princess Betsy: “I am a nice person ...", "그녀가 갑자기 베치 공작 부인 쪽으로 돌아섰다. \"나 정말 괜찮은 사람이에요……"], ["I positively forgot it ...", "깜빡 잊고 있었지 뭐예요……"], ["I’ve brought you a visitor.", "손님 한 분을 모시고 왔거든요."], ["And here he comes.”", "저기 오시네요.\""], ["The unexpected young visitor, whom Sappho had invited, and whom she had forgotten, was, however, a personage of such consequence that, in spite of his youth, both the ladies rose on his entrance.", "사포가 초대해 놓고는 잊고 있었던 뜻밖의 젊은 손님은, 그러나 그의 젊음에도 불구하고 두 부인이 그의 등장에 자리에서 일어날 만큼 대단한 인물이었다."]], [["He was a new admirer of Sappho’s.", "그는 사포의 새로운 숭배자였다."], ["He now dogged her footsteps, like Vaska.", "그도 이제 바스카처럼 그녀의 발자취를 졸졸 따라다녔다."]], [["Soon after Prince Kaluzhsky arrived, and Liza Merkalova with Stremov.", "곧이어 칼루주스키 공작이 도착했고, 리자 메르칼로바가 스트레모프와 함께 왔다."], ["Liza Merkalova was a thin brunette, with an Oriental, languid type of face, and—as everyone used to say—exquisite enigmatic eyes.", "리자 메르칼로바는 동양적이고 나른한 인상의 얼굴에, 다들 입버릇처럼 말하듯 더없이 빼어나고 수수께끼 같은 눈을 가진, 호리호리한 갈색 머리 여자였다."], ["The tone of her dark dress (Anna immediately observed and appreciated the fact) was in perfect harmony with her style of beauty.", "그녀의 짙은 색 드레스의 색조는(안나는 즉시 그것을 알아채고 높이 평가했다) 그녀의 아름다운 분위기와 완벽하게 어우러져 있었다."], ["Liza was as soft and enervated as Sappho was smart and abrupt.", "사포가 멋스럽고 갑작스러운 만큼이나, 리자는 부드럽고 나른했다."]], [["But to Anna’s taste Liza was far more attractive.", "그러나 안나의 취향에는 리자가 훨씬 더 매력적이었다."], ["Betsy had said to Anna that she had adopted the pose of an innocent child, but when Anna saw her, she felt that this was not the truth.", "베치는 안나에게 리자가 순진한 어린아이 같은 태도를 꾸며 보인다고 말한 적이 있었으나, 안나가 직접 그녀를 보니 그것이 사실이 아니라는 느낌이 들었다."], ["She really was both innocent and corrupt, but a sweet and passive woman.", "그녀는 정말로 순진하면서도 타락한, 그러나 사랑스럽고 수동적인 여자였다."], ["It is true that her tone was the same as Sappho’s; that like Sappho, she had two men, one young and one old, tacked onto her, and devouring her with their eyes.", "그녀의 태도가 사포와 똑같다는 것은 사실이었다. 사포처럼 그녀에게도 하나는 젊고 하나는 늙은, 두 남자가 따라붙어 눈으로 그녀를 집어삼키고 있다는 것도."], ["But there was something in her higher than what surrounded her.", "그러나 그녀에게는 주위를 둘러싼 것들보다 한층 높은 무언가가 있었다."], ["There was in her the glow of the real diamond among glass imitations.", "그녀에게는 유리 모조품들 사이에 섞인 진짜 다이아몬드의 광채가 있었다."], ["This glow shone out in her exquisite, truly enigmatic eyes.", "그 광채는 빼어나고 정말로 수수께끼 같은 그녀의 두 눈에서 뿜어져 나왔다."], ["The weary, and at the same time passionate, glance of those eyes, encircled by dark rings, impressed one by its perfect sincerity.", "짙은 그늘에 둘러싸인 그 눈의, 지치고도 동시에 정열적인 시선은 더없이 진실한 느낌으로 사람을 사로잡았다."], ["Everyone looking into those eyes fancied he knew her wholly, and knowing her, could not but love her.", "그 눈을 들여다보는 사람은 누구나 자기가 그녀를 온전히 안다고 여겼고, 또 그녀를 아는 이상 그녀를 사랑하지 않을 수 없다고 여겼다."], ["At the sight of Anna, her whole face lighted up at once with a smile of delight.", "안나를 보는 순간, 그녀의 온 얼굴이 단번에 반가운 미소로 환해졌다."]], [["“Ah, how glad I am to see you!”", "\"아, 만나서 정말 반가워요!\""], ["she said, going up to her.", "그녀가 다가오며 말했다."], ["“Yesterday at the races all I wanted was to get to you, but you’d gone away.", "\"어제 경마장에서 당신에게 가려고만 했는데, 그새 가 버리셨더군요."], ["I did so want to see you, yesterday especially.", "어제는 특히 더 만나 뵙고 싶었어요."], ["Wasn’t it awful?”", "정말 끔찍하지 않았나요?\""], ["she said, looking at Anna with eyes that seemed to lay bare all her soul.", "그녀는 마치 자기 영혼을 온통 드러내 보이는 듯한 눈으로 안나를 바라보며 말했다."]], [["“Yes; I had no idea it would be so thrilling,”", "\"그래요, 그렇게 손에 땀을 쥐게 할 줄은 미처 몰랐어요.\""], ["said Anna, blushing.", "안나가 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다."]], [["The company got up at this moment to go into the garden.", "바로 그 순간 사람들이 정원으로 나가려고 자리에서 일어났다."]], [["“I’m not going,”", "\"난 안 갈래요.\""], ["said Liza, smiling and settling herself close to Anna.", "리자가 미소 지으며 안나 곁으로 바짝 자리를 옮기면서 말했다."], ["“You won’t go either, will you?", "\"당신도 안 가실 거죠, 그렇죠?"], ["Who wants to play croquet?”", "누가 크로케 따위를 하고 싶겠어요?\""]], [["“Oh, I like it,”", "\"아, 난 그거 좋아하는데요.\""], ["said Anna.", "안나가 말했다."]], [["“There, how do you manage never to be bored by things?", "\"세상에, 어쩌면 당신은 그 무엇에도 지루해하는 법이 없죠?"], ["It’s delightful to look at you.", "당신을 보고 있으면 즐거워요."], ["You’re alive, but I’m bored.”", "당신은 생기가 넘치는데, 난 지루하기만 하니.\""]], [["“How can you be bored?", "\"어떻게 지루할 수가 있어요?"], ["Why, you live in the liveliest set in Petersburg,”", "페테르부르크에서 제일 활기찬 모임에서 지내시면서.\""], ["said Anna.", "안나가 말했다."]], [["“Possibly the people who are not of our set are even more bored; but we—I certainly—are not happy, but awfully, awfully bored.”", "\"어쩌면 우리 모임에 끼지 못한 사람들이 더 지루할지도 모르죠. 하지만 우리는—적어도 나는—행복하지 않아요, 그저 지독하게, 지독하게 지루할 뿐이에요.\""]], [["Sappho smoking a cigarette went off into the garden with the two young men.", "사포는 담배를 피우며 두 젊은 남자와 함께 정원으로 나갔다."], ["Betsy and Stremov remained at the tea-table.", "베치와 스트레모프는 다탁에 남았다."]], [["“What, bored!” said Betsy.", "\"아니, 지루하다니!\""], ["“Sappho says they did enjoy themselves tremendously at your house last night.”", "베치가 말했다. \"사포 말로는 어젯밤 당신 집에서 다들 엄청나게 즐거워했다던데.\""]], [["“Ah, how dreary it all was!”", "\"아, 얼마나 따분했는지 몰라요!\""], ["said Liza Merkalova.", "리자 메르칼로바가 말했다."], ["“We all drove back to my place after the races.", "\"경마가 끝나고 다 같이 우리 집으로 몰려갔죠."], ["And always the same people, always the same.", "그런데 늘 똑같은 사람들, 늘 똑같아요."], ["Always the same thing.", "늘 똑같은 짓이죠."], ["We lounged about on sofas all the evening.", "저녁 내내 소파에 늘어져 빈둥거렸어요."], ["What is there to enjoy in that?", "그런 데서 무슨 즐길 거리가 있겠어요?"], ["No; do tell me how you manage never to be bored?”", "안 돼요, 정말로 당신은 어떻게 지루해하지 않는지 좀 가르쳐 줘요.\""], ["she said, addressing Anna again.", "그녀가 다시 안나를 향해 말했다."], ["“One has but to look at you and one sees, here’s a woman who may be happy or unhappy, but isn’t bored.", "\"당신을 한 번 보기만 해도 알 수 있어요, 여기 행복하거나 불행할 수는 있어도 지루해하지는 않는 여자가 있구나, 하고요."], ["Tell me how you do it?”", "그 비결 좀 말해 줘요?\""]], [["“I do nothing,”", "\"난 아무것도 안 해요.\""], ["answered Anna, blushing at these searching questions.", "이 캐묻는 질문에 얼굴을 붉히며 안나가 대답했다."]], [["“That’s the best way,” Stremov put in.", "\"그게 바로 최고의 방법이죠.\""], ["Stremov was a man of fifty, partly gray, but still vigorous-looking, very ugly, but with a characteristic and intelligent face.", "스트레모프가 끼어들었다. 스트레모프는 머리가 희끗희끗하지만 아직 정정해 보이는 쉰 살의 남자로, 무척 못생겼으나 개성 있고 영리한 얼굴을 하고 있었다."], ["Liza Merkalova was his wife’s niece, and he spent all his leisure hours with her.", "리자 메르칼로바는 그의 아내의 조카딸이었고, 그는 한가한 시간을 죄다 그녀와 함께 보냈다."], ["On meeting Anna Karenina, as he was Alexey Alexandrovitch’s enemy in the government, he tried, like a shrewd man and a man of the world, to be particularly cordial with her, the wife of his enemy.", "정부 내에서 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 정적이었던 그는, 안나 카레니나를 만나자, 영리하고 세상 물정에 밝은 사람답게, 정적의 아내인 그녀에게 유난히 다정하게 굴려고 애썼다."]], [["“‘Nothing,’”", "\"'아무것도 안 한다'는 것.\""], ["he put in with a subtle smile, “that’s the very best way.", "그가 묘한 미소를 지으며 끼어들었다. \"그것이야말로 최고의 방법이죠."], ["I told you long ago,”", "내가 진작에 말했잖아요.\""], ["he said, turning to Liza Merkalova, “that if you don’t want to be bored, you mustn’t think you’re going to be bored.", "그가 리자 메르칼로바를 향해 돌아서며 말했다. \"지루해지고 싶지 않으면, 지루해질 거라고 생각하지 말아야 한다고."], ["It’s just as you mustn’t be afraid of not being able to fall asleep, if you’re afraid of sleeplessness.", "그건 마치 잠을 못 이룰까 봐 걱정한다면 잠들지 못할까 두려워하지 말아야 하는 것과 같죠."], ["That’s just what Anna Arkadyevna has just said.”", "안나 아르카지예브나가 방금 한 말이 바로 그거예요.\""]], [["“I should be very glad if I had said it, for it’s not only clever but true,”", "\"내가 그런 말을 했다면 정말 기쁘겠네요, 영리할 뿐만 아니라 진실이기도 하니까요.\""], ["said Anna, smiling.", "안나가 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["“No, do tell me why it is one can’t go to sleep, and one can’t help being bored?”", "\"아니에요, 그러면 왜 사람이 잠을 이루지 못하고, 또 지루해할 수밖에 없는지 좀 말해 줘요?\""]], [["“To sleep well one ought to work, and to enjoy oneself one ought to work too.”", "\"잘 자려면 일을 해야 하고, 즐기려면 그것도 일을 해야 하는 거죠.\""]], [["“What am I to work for when my work is no use to anybody?", "\"내 일이 아무에게도 쓸모가 없는데 무엇 때문에 일을 해야 하죠?"], ["And I can’t and won’t knowingly make a pretense about it.”", "그렇다고 알면서 그런 척 꾸며 댈 수도 없고, 그러고 싶지도 않아요.\""]], [["“You’re incorrigible,”", "\"당신은 구제 불능이군요.\""], ["said Stremov, not looking at her, and he spoke again to Anna.", "스트레모프가 그녀를 보지도 않은 채 말하고는, 다시 안나에게 말을 걸었다."], ["As he rarely met Anna, he could say nothing but commonplaces to her, but he said those commonplaces as to when she was returning to Petersburg, and how fond Countess Lidia Ivanovna was of her, with an expression which suggested that he longed with his whole soul to please her and show his regard for her and even more than that.", "안나를 만나는 일이 드물었던 터라 그녀에게는 의례적인 말밖에 할 수 없었지만, 그는 언제 페테르부르크로 돌아오느냐, 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인이 그녀를 얼마나 좋아하느냐 따위의 그런 의례적인 말들을, 온 영혼을 다해 그녀를 기쁘게 해 주고 그녀에 대한 존경을, 아니 그 이상의 무언가를 보여 주고 싶어 하는 듯한 표정으로 늘어놓았다."]], [["Tushkevitch came in, announcing that the party were awaiting the other players to begin croquet.", "투시케비치가 들어와, 사람들이 크로케를 시작하려고 나머지 선수들을 기다리고 있다고 알렸다."]], [["“No, don’t go away, please don’t,”", "\"안 돼요, 가지 마세요, 제발요.\""], ["pleaded Liza Merkalova, hearing that Anna was going.", "안나가 가려 한다는 것을 듣고 리자 메르칼로바가 애원했다."], ["Stremov joined in her entreaties.", "스트레모프도 그녀의 간청에 가세했다."]], [["“It’s too violent a transition,” he said, “to go from such company to old Madame Vrede.", "\"이런 자리에서 늙은 브레데 부인에게로 가다니, 너무 격한 전환이지요.\""], ["And besides, you will only give her a chance for talking scandal, while here you arouse none but such different feelings of the highest and most opposite kind,”", "그가 말했다. \"게다가 그분에게는 한낱 험담거리만 안겨 줄 뿐이지만, 여기서는 더없이 고상하고 정반대되는 온갖 감정만을 불러일으키시니까요.\""], ["he said to her.", "그가 그녀에게 말했다."]], [["Anna pondered for an instant in uncertainty.", "안나는 잠시 망설이며 생각에 잠겼다."], ["This shrewd man’s flattering words, the naïve, childlike affection shown her by Liza Merkalova, and all the social atmosphere she was used to,—it was all so easy, and what was in store for her was so difficult, that she was for a minute in uncertainty whether to remain, whether to put off a little longer the painful moment of explanation.", "이 영리한 남자의 아첨하는 말들, 리자 메르칼로바가 보여 주는 순진하고 어린아이 같은 애정, 그리고 그녀가 익숙해져 있던 그 모든 사교계의 분위기—그 모든 것이 너무도 편안했고, 그녀를 기다리고 있는 것은 너무도 괴로운 일이어서, 그녀는 잠시 동안 여기 남을지 말지, 그 고통스러운 해명의 순간을 조금 더 미룰지 말지 망설였다."], ["But remembering what was in store for her alone at home, if she did not come to some decision, remembering that gesture—terrible even in memory—when she had clutched her hair in both hands—she said good-bye and went away.", "그러나 만약 자기가 어떤 결단도 내리지 못한 채 집에 홀로 있을 때 무엇이 기다리고 있을지를 떠올리고, 두 손으로 머리칼을 움켜쥐던 그 몸짓—기억 속에서조차 끔찍한 그 몸짓을 떠올리고는, 작별 인사를 하고 자리를 떴다."]], [["# Chapter 19", "# Chapter 19"]], [["In spite of Vronsky’s apparently frivolous life in society, he was a man who hated irregularity.", "브론스키는 겉으로는 사교계에서 경박한 생활을 하는 듯 보였지만, 사실 질서가 흐트러지는 것을 몹시 싫어하는 사람이었다."], ["In early youth in the Corps of Pages, he had experienced the humiliation of a refusal, when he had tried, being in difficulties, to borrow money, and since then he had never once put himself in the same position again.", "젊은 시절 근위 시동대(侍童隊)에 있을 때, 곤경에 빠져 돈을 빌리려다 거절당하는 굴욕을 겪은 적이 있었는데, 그 후로는 단 한 번도 같은 처지에 자신을 두지 않았다."]], [["In order to keep his affairs in some sort of order, he used about five times a year (more or less frequently, according to circumstances) to shut himself up alone and put all his affairs into definite shape.", "자기 일들을 어느 정도 정리된 상태로 유지하기 위해, 그는 일 년에 다섯 번쯤(형편에 따라 더 잦기도 하고 더 드물기도 했다) 혼자 틀어박혀서 모든 일을 분명한 형태로 정리하곤 했다."], ["This he used to call his day of reckoning or faire la lessive.", "그는 이것을 자신의 결산일, 또는 faire la lessive라고 부르곤 했다."]], [["On waking up the day after the races, Vronsky put on a white linen coat, and without shaving or taking his bath, he distributed about the table moneys, bills, and letters, and set to work.", "* faire la lessive: 프랑스어로 '빨래하기', 즉 깨끗이 정리한다는 뜻. 경마가 있던 다음 날 잠에서 깬 브론스키는 흰 리넨 코트를 걸치고, 면도도 하지 않고 목욕도 하지 않은 채 탁자 위에 돈과 청구서와 편지들을 펼쳐 놓고 일에 착수했다."], ["Petritsky, who knew he was ill-tempered on such occasions, on waking up and seeing his comrade at the writing-table, quietly dressed and went out without getting in his way.", "이런 때 그가 신경이 날카롭다는 것을 잘 아는 페트리츠키는, 잠에서 깨어나 동료가 책상 앞에 앉아 있는 것을 보자 조용히 옷을 챙겨 입고는 그를 방해하지 않고 밖으로 나갔다."]], [["Every man who knows to the minutest details all the complexity of the conditions surrounding him, cannot help imagining that the complexity of these conditions, and the difficulty of making them clear, is something exceptional and personal, peculiar to himself, and never supposes that others are surrounded by just as complicated an array of personal affairs as he is.", "자신을 둘러싼 온갖 조건의 복잡함을 그 미세한 세부까지 속속들이 아는 사람은, 그 조건들의 복잡함과 그것을 정리하는 일의 어려움이 무언가 예외적이고 개인적인 것, 자기에게만 고유한 것이라고 상상하지 않을 수 없으며, 다른 사람들도 자기와 똑같이 복잡하게 얽힌 개인적인 일들에 둘러싸여 있으리라고는 결코 생각하지 못한다."], ["So indeed it seemed to Vronsky.", "브론스키에게도 정말 그렇게 여겨졌다."], ["And not without inward pride, and not without reason, he thought that any other man would long ago have been in difficulties, would have been forced to some dishonorable course, if he had found himself in such a difficult position.", "그래서 내심 적잖은 자부심을 느끼며, 또 근거 없이는 아니게, 그는 다른 어떤 사람이라도 이런 어려운 처지에 놓였더라면 진작에 곤경에 빠졌을 것이고, 무언가 떳떳하지 못한 길로 내몰렸을 것이라고 생각했다."], ["But Vronsky felt that now especially it was essential for him to clear up and define his position if he were to avoid getting into difficulties.", "하지만 브론스키는 곤경에 빠지지 않으려면 지금이야말로 특히 자기 처지를 정리하고 분명히 해 둘 필요가 있다고 느꼈다."]], [["What Vronsky attacked first as being the easiest was his pecuniary position.", "브론스키가 가장 손쉬운 것으로 여겨 제일 먼저 손을 댄 것은 금전 문제였다."], ["Writing out on note paper in his minute hand all that he owed, he added up the amount and found that his debts amounted to seventeen thousand and some odd hundreds, which he left out for the sake of clearness.", "자기가 빚진 모든 것을 작은 글씨로 노트에 적어 내려간 뒤 그 액수를 합산해 보니, 빚이 만 칠천 루블에 몇백 루블이 더 되었는데, 그는 명확함을 위해 몇백 루블은 빼 버렸다."], ["Reckoning up his money and his bank book, he found that he had left one thousand eight hundred roubles, and nothing coming in before the New Year.", "자기 돈과 은행 통장을 따져 보니, 남은 것이 천팔백 루블이었고, 새해가 되기 전에는 들어올 돈이 아무것도 없었다."], ["Reckoning over again his list of debts, Vronsky copied it, dividing it into three classes.", "빚 목록을 다시 한번 따져 본 다음, 브론스키는 그것을 세 가지로 나누어 옮겨 적었다."], ["In the first class he put the debts which he would have to pay at once, or for which he must in any case have the money ready so that on demand for payment there could not be a moment’s delay in paying.", "첫째 항목에는 당장 갚아야 할 빚, 혹은 어쨌든 지불을 요구받았을 때 한순간도 지체 없이 갚을 수 있도록 돈을 마련해 두어야 하는 빚을 넣었다. 그런 빚은 약 사천 루블이었다."], ["Such debts amounted to about four thousand: one thousand five hundred for a horse, and two thousand five hundred as surety for a young comrade, Venovsky, who had lost that sum to a cardsharper in Vronsky’s presence.", "말 값으로 천오백 루블, 그리고 젊은 동료 베노프스키의 보증으로 이천오백 루블이었는데, 베노프스키는 브론스키가 보는 앞에서 어떤 사기 도박꾼에게 그 돈을 잃었던 것이다."], ["Vronsky had wanted to pay the money at the time (he had that amount then), but Venovsky and Yashvin had insisted that they would pay and not Vronsky, who had not played.", "브론스키는 그때 그 돈을 갚으려 했지만(당시에는 그만한 돈이 있었다), 베노프스키와 야쉬빈이 노름을 하지 않은 브론스키가 아니라 자기들이 갚겠다고 우겼다."], ["That was so far well, but Vronsky knew that in this dirty business, though his only share in it was undertaking by word of mouth to be surety for Venovsky, it was absolutely necessary for him to have the two thousand five hundred roubles so as to be able to fling it at the swindler, and have no more words with him.", "거기까지는 좋았지만, 브론스키는 이 더러운 일에서—비록 자기 몫이라곤 입으로 베노프스키의 보증을 서겠다고 한 것뿐이었지만—그 이천오백 루블을 마련해 두어, 그 사기꾼에게 던져 주고는 더 이상 그자와 말을 섞지 않을 수 있어야 한다는 것을 알고 있었다."], ["And so for this first and most important division he must have four thousand roubles.", "그래서 이 첫째이자 가장 중요한 항목을 위해 그는 사천 루블이 필요했다."], ["The second class—eight thousand roubles—consisted of less important debts.", "둘째 항목은—팔천 루블로—덜 중요한 빚이었다."], ["These were principally accounts owing in connection with his race horses, to the purveyor of oats and hay, the English saddler, and so on.", "이것들은 주로 경주마와 관련된 외상이었는데, 귀리와 건초를 대는 상인, 영국인 마구상 따위에게 진 빚이었다."], ["He would have to pay some two thousand roubles on these debts too, in order to be quite free from anxiety.", "마음을 온전히 놓으려면 이 빚들에 대해서도 이천 루블쯤은 갚아야 했다."], ["The last class of debts—to shops, to hotels, to his tailor—were such as need not be considered.", "마지막 항목의 빚은—상점, 호텔, 양복점 따위에 진 것으로—고려하지 않아도 될 만한 것이었다."], ["So that he needed at least six thousand roubles for current expenses, and he only had one thousand eight hundred.", "그러니까 그는 당장 쓸 비용으로 적어도 육천 루블이 필요했는데, 가진 것은 천팔백 루블뿐이었다."], ["For a man with one hundred thousand roubles of revenue, which was what everyone fixed as Vronsky’s income, such debts, one would suppose, could hardly be embarrassing; but the fact was that he was far from having one hundred thousand.", "모두가 브론스키의 수입으로 잡고 있던 십만 루블의 연수입을 가진 사람에게라면, 이런 빚쯤이야 별로 거북할 게 없으리라고 여길 법했다. 하지만 사실 그는 십만 루블을 가지기는커녕 어림도 없었다."], ["His father’s immense property, which alone yielded a yearly income of two hundred thousand, was left undivided between the brothers.", "그것 하나만으로도 해마다 이십만 루블의 수입을 내던 아버지의 막대한 재산은 형제 사이에 나뉘지 않은 채로 남아 있었다."], ["At the time when the elder brother, with a mass of debts, married Princess Varya Tchirkova, the daughter of a Decembrist without any fortune whatever, Alexey had given up to his elder brother almost the whole income from his father’s estate, reserving for himself only twenty-five thousand a year from it.", "형이 빚더미에 올라앉은 채로, 한 푼의 재산도 없는 데카브리스트의 딸인 바랴 치르코바 공작 영애와 결혼했을 때, 알렉세이는 아버지 영지에서 나오는 수입을 거의 전부 형에게 넘겨주고, 자기 몫으로는 거기서 연 이만오천 루블만 남겨 두었다."], ["Alexey had said at the time to his brother that that sum would be sufficient for him until he married, which he probably never would do.", "그때 알렉세이는 형에게, 그 정도면 자기가 결혼할 때까지는 충분할 것이고, 결혼은 아마 영영 하지 않을 것이라고 말했다."], ["And his brother, who was in command of one of the most expensive regiments, and was only just married, could not decline the gift.", "그리고 가장 돈이 많이 드는 연대 중 하나를 지휘하던 데다 막 결혼한 참이던 형은 그 선물을 거절할 수 없었다."], ["His mother, who had her own separate property, had allowed Alexey every year twenty thousand in addition to the twenty-five thousand he had reserved, and Alexey had spent it all.", "자기만의 별도 재산을 가지고 있던 어머니는, 알렉세이가 따로 남겨 둔 이만오천 루블 외에 해마다 이만 루블을 더 보태 주었고, 알렉세이는 그것을 모조리 써 버렸다."], ["Of late his mother, incensed with him on account of his love affair and his leaving Moscow, had given up sending him the money.", "근래에 들어 어머니는 그의 연애 사건과 모스크바를 떠난 일로 그에게 화가 나서 돈을 보내는 것을 그만두었다."], ["And in consequence of this, Vronsky, who had been in the habit of living on the scale of forty-five thousand a year, having only received twenty thousand that year, found himself now in difficulties.", "그래서 한 해에 사만오천 루블 규모로 살아 버릇하던 브론스키는 그해에는 이만 루블밖에 받지 못한 탓에 지금 곤경에 처하게 된 것이었다."], ["To get out of these difficulties, he could not apply to his mother for money.", "이 곤경에서 벗어나기 위해 어머니에게 돈을 청할 수는 없었다."], ["Her last letter, which he had received the day before, had particularly exasperated him by the hints in it that she was quite ready to help him to succeed in the world and in the army, but not to lead a life which was a scandal to all good society.", "전날 받은 어머니의 마지막 편지는, 사교계와 군대에서 출세하도록 그를 도울 마음은 얼마든지 있지만, 점잖은 사회 전체에 추문이 되는 그런 생활을 하도록 도울 마음은 없다는 암시로 그를 유독 격분하게 만들었다."], ["His mother’s attempt to buy him stung him to the quick and made him feel colder than ever to her.", "자기를 돈으로 사들이려는 어머니의 시도가 그의 가슴을 후벼 파, 그 어느 때보다 어머니에게 더 차갑게 느끼게 만들었다."], ["But he could not draw back from the generous word when it was once uttered, even though he felt now, vaguely foreseeing certain eventualities in his intrigue with Madame Karenina, that this generous word had been spoken thoughtlessly, and that even though he were not married he might need all the hundred thousand of income.", "하지만 일단 입 밖에 낸 그 너그러운 말을 이제 와서 거둬들일 수는 없었다. 카레니나 부인과의 밀회가 어떤 결말로 흘러갈지 막연히 예감하면서, 그 너그러운 말이 경솔하게 내뱉어진 것이며, 자기가 결혼하지 않더라도 십만 루블의 수입이 전부 필요할지도 모른다고 지금은 느끼고 있었지만 말이다."], ["But it was impossible to draw back.", "그렇지만 거둬들이는 것은 불가능했다."], ["He had only to recall his brother’s wife, to remember how that sweet, delightful Varya sought, at every convenient opportunity, to remind him that she remembered his generosity and appreciated it, to grasp the impossibility of taking back his gift. It was as impossible as beating a woman, stealing, or lying.", "형수를 떠올리기만 하면, 그 사랑스럽고 유쾌한 바랴가 기회가 닿을 때마다 자기가 그의 너그러움을 기억하고 있으며 고맙게 여기고 있다는 것을 그에게 일깨우려 애쓰던 모습을 떠올리기만 하면, 자기가 준 선물을 도로 거둬들이는 것이 얼마나 불가능한 일인지 절감할 수 있었다."], ["One thing only could and ought to be done, and Vronsky determined upon it without an instant’s hesitation: to borrow money from a money-lender, ten thousand roubles, a proceeding which presented no difficulty, to cut down his expenses generally, and to sell his race horses.", "그것은 여자를 때리거나, 도둑질을 하거나, 거짓말을 하는 것만큼이나 불가능한 일이었다. 할 수 있고 또 해야 할 일은 단 하나뿐이었고, 브론스키는 한순간의 망설임도 없이 그것을 결심했다."], ["Resolving on this, he promptly wrote a note to Rolandak, who had more than once sent to him with offers to buy horses from him.", "고리대금업자에게서 만 루블을 빌리는 일이었는데, 이는 아무런 어려움도 없는 일이었다. 그리고 씀씀이를 전반적으로 줄이고, 경주마를 파는 것이었다."], ["Then he sent for the Englishman and the money-lender, and divided what money he had according to the accounts he intended to pay.", "이렇게 결심하고 나서, 그는 한 번 이상 사람을 보내 자기에게 말을 팔라고 청했던 롤란다크에게 곧장 쪽지를 써 보냈다."], ["Having finished this business, he wrote a cold and cutting answer to his mother.", "그런 다음 영국인과 고리대금업자를 부르러 보내고, 갚으려고 마음먹은 외상에 따라 가진 돈을 나누었다."], ["Then he took out of his notebook three notes of Anna’s, read them again, burned them, and remembering their conversation on the previous day, he sank into meditation.", "이 일을 끝낸 뒤, 그는 어머니에게 차갑고 매정한 답장을 썼다. 그러고 나서 수첩에서 안나의 쪽지 세 장을 꺼내 다시 읽고는 불태워 버렸고, 전날 나눈 대화를 떠올리며 깊은 생각에 잠겼다."]], [["# Chapter 20", "# Chapter 20"]], [["Vronsky’s life was particularly happy in that he had a code of principles, which defined with unfailing certitude what he ought and what he ought not to do.", "브론스키의 삶이 유난히 행복했던 까닭은, 그에게 자신이 무엇을 해야 하고 무엇을 하지 말아야 하는지를 한 치의 어긋남도 없이 확실하게 규정해 주는 원칙의 규범이 있었기 때문이다."], ["This code of principles covered only a very small circle of contingencies, but then the principles were never doubtful, and Vronsky, as he never went outside that circle, had never had a moment’s hesitation about doing what he ought to do.", "이 원칙의 규범은 극히 좁은 범위의 우발적 상황만을 다루었지만, 그래도 그 원칙들은 결코 의심스럽지 않았으며, 브론스키는 그 범위 밖으로 나가는 일이 없었기에 자신이 마땅히 해야 할 일을 하는 데 한순간도 망설인 적이 없었다. 이 원칙들은 불변의 규칙으로 다음과 같은 것들을 정해 놓았다."], ["These principles laid down as invariable rules: that one must pay a cardsharper, but need not pay a tailor; that one must never tell a lie to a man, but one may to a woman; that one must never cheat anyone, but one may a husband; that one must never pardon an insult, but one may give one and so on.", "노름 사기꾼에게는 돈을 갚아야 하지만 재단사에게는 갚지 않아도 된다는 것, 남자에게는 결코 거짓말을 해서는 안 되지만 여자에게는 해도 된다는 것, 누구도 속여서는 안 되지만 남편은 속여도 된다는 것, 모욕은 결코 용서해서는 안 되지만 모욕을 줄 수는 있다는 것, 그 밖에도 이와 비슷한 것들이었다."], ["These principles were possibly not reasonable and not good, but they were of unfailing certainty, and so long as he adhered to them, Vronsky felt that his heart was at peace and he could hold his head up.", "이 원칙들은 어쩌면 합리적이지도 않고 좋은 것도 아니었으나, 한 치의 어긋남도 없이 확실했고, 그것을 지키는 한 브론스키는 자기 마음이 평온하며 떳떳하게 고개를 들 수 있다고 느꼈다."], ["Only quite lately in regard to his relations with Anna, Vronsky had begun to feel that his code of principles did not fully cover all possible contingencies, and to foresee in the future difficulties and perplexities for which he could find no guiding clue.", "다만 아주 최근에 들어서야 안나와의 관계에 관한 한, 브론스키는 자신의 원칙의 규범이 일어날 수 있는 모든 우발적 상황을 온전히 아우르지는 못한다는 것을 느끼기 시작했고, 앞으로 닥칠 어려움과 곤혹스러움 가운데 자신을 이끌어 줄 어떤 실마리도 찾을 수 없는 것들을 예감하기 시작했다."]], [["His present relation to Anna and to her husband was to his mind clear and simple.", "지금 안나와, 그리고 그녀의 남편과 맺고 있는 관계는 그의 마음속에서 분명하고 단순했다."], ["It was clearly and precisely defined in the code of principles by which he was guided.", "그것은 그가 따르는 원칙의 규범 안에 또렷하고 정확하게 규정되어 있었다."]], [["She was an honorable woman who had bestowed her love upon him, and he loved her, and therefore she was in his eyes a woman who had a right to the same, or even more, respect than a lawful wife.", "그녀는 자신의 사랑을 그에게 바친 명예로운 여인이었고, 그도 그녀를 사랑했으며, 따라서 그가 보기에 그녀는 합법적인 아내와 똑같은, 아니 그 이상의 존중을 받을 권리가 있는 여인이었다."], ["He would have had his hand chopped off before he would have allowed himself by a word, by a hint, to humiliate her, or even to fall short of the fullest respect a woman could look for.", "그는 말 한마디로, 암시 하나로 그녀를 모욕하거나, 여인이 기대할 수 있는 가장 온전한 존중에 조금이라도 미치지 못하는 것보다는 차라리 자기 손이 잘리는 편을 택했을 것이다."]], [["His attitude to society, too, was clear.", "사교계에 대한 그의 태도 또한 분명했다."], ["Everyone might know, might suspect it, but no one might dare to speak of it.", "누구나 그것을 알 수도, 짐작할 수도 있었지만, 누구도 감히 그것을 입에 올려서는 안 되었다."], ["If any did so, he was ready to force all who might speak to be silent and to respect the non-existent honor of the woman he loved.", "만약 누군가가 그렇게 한다면, 그는 입을 놀리려는 자들을 모조리 침묵시키고 자신이 사랑하는 여인의 존재하지도 않는 명예를 존중하게 만들 각오가 되어 있었다."]], [["His attitude to the husband was the clearest of all.", "남편에 대한 그의 태도는 그중에서도 가장 분명했다."], ["From the moment that Anna loved Vronsky, he had regarded his own right over her as the one thing unassailable.", "안나가 브론스키를 사랑한 순간부터 그는 그녀에 대한 자신의 권리를 결코 침해할 수 없는 유일한 것으로 여겨 왔다."], ["Her husband was simply a superfluous and tiresome person.", "그녀의 남편은 그저 군더더기 같고 성가신 사람일 뿐이었다."], ["No doubt he was in a pitiable position, but how could that be helped?", "물론 그가 가엾은 처지에 놓인 것은 사실이었지만, 그것을 어찌하겠는가?"], ["The one thing the husband had a right to was to demand satisfaction with a weapon in his hand, and Vronsky was prepared for this at any minute.", "남편에게 권리가 있는 단 한 가지는 손에 무기를 들고 결투를 요구하는 것뿐이었고, 브론스키는 언제라도 거기에 응할 준비가 되어 있었다."]], [["But of late new inner relations had arisen between him and her, which frightened Vronsky by their indefiniteness.", "그러나 최근 들어 그와 그녀 사이에 새로운 내면의 관계가 생겨났고, 그것은 그 불확실함으로 브론스키를 두렵게 했다."], ["Only the day before she had told him that she was with child.", "바로 전날 그녀는 그에게 자신이 아이를 가졌다고 말했던 것이다."], ["And he felt that this fact and what she expected of him called for something not fully defined in that code of principles by which he had hitherto steered his course in life.", "그리고 그는 이 사실과 그녀가 자신에게 기대하는 것이, 그가 지금껏 인생을 살아오면서 길잡이로 삼아 온 그 원칙의 규범 안에 온전히 규정되어 있지 않은 무언가를 요구한다고 느꼈다."], ["And he had been indeed caught unawares, and at the first moment when she spoke to him of her position, his heart had prompted him to beg her to leave her husband.", "그리고 그는 실제로 허를 찔렸던 것이고, 그녀가 자신의 처지에 대해 처음 이야기를 꺼낸 순간, 그의 마음은 남편을 떠나라고 그녀에게 청하라고 충동했다."], ["He had said that, but now thinking things over he saw clearly that it would be better to manage to avoid that; and at the same time, as he told himself so, he was afraid whether it was not wrong.", "그는 그렇게 말했지만, 이제 곰곰이 생각해 보니 그것은 어떻게든 피하는 편이 낫겠다는 것이 분명히 보였다. 그러면서도 동시에, 스스로에게 그렇게 말하면서도, 그것이 잘못된 일은 아닐까 두려웠다."]], [["“If I told her to leave her husband, that must mean uniting her life with mine; am I prepared for that?", "\"만약 그녀에게 남편을 떠나라고 한다면, 그건 그녀의 삶을 내 삶과 하나로 합친다는 뜻이겠지. 나는 그럴 준비가 되어 있나?"], ["How can I take her away now, when I have no money?", "돈도 없는 지금 어떻게 그녀를 데려올 수 있겠어?"], ["Supposing I could arrange....", "설령 어떻게든 마련한다 해도……"], ["But how can I take her away while I’m in the service?", "하지만 내가 복무 중인데 어떻게 그녀를 데려온단 말인가?"], ["If I say that—I ought to be prepared to do it, that is, I ought to have the money and to retire from the army.”", "만약 그렇게 말한다면, 그걸 실행할 준비가 되어 있어야 한다는 거지. 그러니까 돈이 있어야 하고 군대에서 물러나야 한다는 거야.\""]], [["And he grew thoughtful.", "그러고는 생각에 잠겼다."], ["The question whether to retire from the service or not brought him to the other and perhaps the chief though hidden interest of his life, of which none knew but he.", "군에서 물러날 것인가 말 것인가 하는 문제는 그를 또 다른, 그리고 어쩌면 그의 인생에서 가장 중요한, 그러나 감추어진 관심사로 이끌었다. 그것은 그 자신 말고는 아무도 알지 못하는 것이었다."]], [["Ambition was the old dream of his youth and childhood, a dream which he did not confess even to himself, though it was so strong that now this passion was even doing battle with his love.", "야망은 그의 젊은 시절과 어린 시절부터 품어 온 오랜 꿈이었고, 자기 자신에게조차 고백하지 않은 꿈이었지만, 그 꿈이 어찌나 강렬했던지 이제는 이 열망이 그의 사랑과 싸움을 벌이고 있을 정도였다."], ["His first steps in the world and in the service had been successful, but two years before he had made a great mistake.", "사교계와 군무에서 그가 내디딘 첫걸음은 성공적이었으나, 이 년 전 그는 큰 실수를 저질렀다."], ["Anxious to show his independence and to advance, he had refused a post that had been offered him, hoping that this refusal would heighten his value; but it turned out that he had been too bold, and he was passed over.", "자신의 독립성을 보이고 출세하고 싶은 마음에, 그는 제안받은 자리를 거절했고, 그 거절이 자신의 가치를 높여 주리라 기대했다. 그러나 너무 대담했던 것으로 드러났고, 그는 밀려나고 말았다."], ["And having, whether he liked or not, taken up for himself the position of an independent man, he carried it off with great tact and good sense, behaving as though he bore no grudge against anyone, did not regard himself as injured in any way, and cared for nothing but to be left alone since he was enjoying himself.", "그리고 좋든 싫든 독립적인 인간이라는 입지를 스스로 떠맡은 그는, 누구에게도 원한을 품지 않고, 자신이 어떤 식으로든 해를 입었다고 여기지도 않으며, 그저 즐겁게 지내고 있으니 그냥 내버려 두기만을 바란다는 듯이 처신하면서, 대단한 기지와 분별로 그 입지를 능숙하게 감당해 냈다."], ["In reality he had ceased to enjoy himself as long ago as the year before, when he went away to Moscow.", "사실 그는 그보다 훨씬 전부터, 그러니까 모스크바로 떠났던 작년부터 이미 즐거움을 느끼지 못하게 되었다."], ["He felt that this independent attitude of a man who might have done anything, but cared to do nothing, was already beginning to pall, that many people were beginning to fancy that he was not really capable of anything but being a straightforward, good-natured fellow.", "무엇이든 할 수 있었으나 아무것도 하려 하지 않는 사람이라는 이 독립적인 태도가 벌써 시들해지기 시작했다는 것을, 그리고 많은 이들이 그가 솔직하고 사람 좋은 친구라는 것 말고는 사실 아무것도 할 능력이 없다고 여기기 시작했다는 것을 느꼈다."], ["His connection with Madame Karenina, by creating so much sensation and attracting general attention, had given him a fresh distinction which soothed his gnawing worm of ambition for a while, but a week before that worm had been roused up again with fresh force.", "마담 카레니나와의 관계는 그토록 많은 화제를 불러일으키고 사람들의 이목을 끌면서 그에게 새로운 명성을 안겨 주었고, 그것이 한동안 그를 갉아먹던 야망의 벌레를 달래 주었으나, 일주일 전 그 벌레는 다시 새로운 힘으로 깨어났다."], ["The friend of his childhood, a man of the same set, of the same coterie, his comrade in the Corps of Pages, Serpuhovskoy, who had left school with him and had been his rival in class, in gymnastics, in their scrapes and their dreams of glory, had come back a few days before from Central Asia, where he had gained two steps up in rank, and an order rarely bestowed upon generals so young.", "그의 어린 시절 친구이자 같은 무리, 같은 동아리에 속했고, 근위 사관학교의 동기이며, 함께 학교를 마치고 학업에서도, 체조에서도, 짓궂은 장난에서도, 영광의 꿈에서도 그의 경쟁자였던 세르푸호프스코이가, 며칠 전 중앙아시아에서 돌아왔던 것이다. 그곳에서 그는 두 계급이나 진급했고, 그토록 젊은 장군에게는 좀처럼 수여되지 않는 훈장까지 받았다."]], [["As soon as he arrived in Petersburg, people began to talk about him as a newly risen star of the first magnitude.", "페테르부르크에 도착하자마자 사람들은 그를 새로이 떠오른 일등성이라고 입에 올리기 시작했다."], ["A schoolfellow of Vronsky’s and of the same age, he was a general and was expecting a command, which might have influence on the course of political events; while Vronsky, independent and brilliant and beloved by a charming woman though he was, was simply a cavalry captain who was readily allowed to be as independent as ever he liked.", "브론스키의 학교 동기이자 같은 나이였던 그는 이미 장군이 되어 있었고, 정치적 사건의 흐름에 영향을 미칠 수도 있는 지휘권을 기다리고 있었다. 반면 브론스키는 독립적이고 빛나는 인물이며 매혹적인 여인의 사랑을 받고 있긴 했어도, 그저 마음껏 독립적으로 굴도록 선선히 내버려 두어지는 일개 기병 대위에 지나지 않았다."], ["“Of course I don’t envy Serpuhovskoy and never could envy him; but his advancement shows me that one has only to watch one’s opportunity, and the career of a man like me may be very rapidly made.", "\"물론 나는 세르푸호프스코이를 부러워하지 않고, 부러워할 수도 없어. 하지만 그의 출세는 내게 보여 주지."], ["Three years ago he was in just the same position as I am. If I retire, I burn my ships.", "그저 기회를 잘 살피기만 하면 나 같은 사람의 경력도 아주 빠르게 쌓일 수 있다는 걸."], ["If I remain in the army, I lose nothing.", "삼 년 전만 해도 그는 지금의 나와 똑같은 처지였어."], ["She said herself she did not wish to change her position.", "내가 군을 떠난다면, 나는 배수의 진을 치는 셈이지. 군에 남는다면, 나는 잃을 게 없어."], ["And with her love I cannot feel envious of Serpuhovskoy.”", "그녀 스스로도 자기 처지를 바꾸고 싶지 않다고 말했잖아."], ["And slowly twirling his mustaches, he got up from the table and walked about the room.", "그리고 그녀의 사랑이 있으니 나는 세르푸호프스코이를 부러워할 수 없어.\""], ["His eyes shone particularly brightly, and he felt in that confident, calm, and happy frame of mind which always came after he had thoroughly faced his position.", "그러고는 천천히 콧수염을 비비 꼬면서 그는 탁자에서 일어나 방 안을 거닐었다. 그의 두 눈은 유난히 밝게 빛났고, 그는 자신의 처지를 철저히 마주한 뒤에 늘 찾아오는, 그 확신에 차고 차분하며 행복한 마음 상태에 잠겨 있었다."], ["Everything was straight and clear, just as after former days of reckoning.", "모든 것이 곧고 분명했다. 지난날 셈을 따져 보고 난 뒤처럼."], ["He shaved, took a cold bath, dressed and went out.", "그는 면도를 하고, 냉수욕을 하고, 옷을 차려입은 뒤 밖으로 나갔다."]], [["# Chapter 21", "# Chapter 21"]], [["“We’ve come to fetch you.", "\"자넬 데리러 왔네."], ["Your lessive lasted a good time today,”", "오늘 자네 lessive가 꽤 길었더군.\""], ["said Petritsky.", "페트리츠키가 말했다."], ["“Well, is it over?”", "\"그래, 다 끝났나?\""]], [["“It is over,” answered Vronsky, smiling with his eyes only, and twirling the tips of his mustaches as circumspectly as though after the perfect order into which his affairs had been brought any over-bold or rapid movement might disturb it.", "* lessive: 프랑스어로 '세탁'. 여기서는 비유적으로 '말끔히 정리하는 일'을 뜻한다. \"끝났네.\" 브론스키가 눈으로만 미소를 지으며 대답했다. 그러고는 마치 모든 일을 완벽하게 정돈해 놓은 뒤라 너무 과감하거나 성급한 동작 하나로도 그 질서가 흐트러지기라도 할 듯, 조심스럽게 콧수염 끝을 비비 꼬았다."]], [["“You’re always just as if you’d come out of a bath after it,”", "\"자넨 그러고 나면 늘 목욕탕에서 막 나온 사람 같단 말이야.\""], ["said Petritsky.", "페트리츠키가 말했다."], ["“I’ve come from Gritsky’s” (that was what they called the colonel); “they’re expecting you.”", "\"그리츠키 집에서 오는 길이야\"—그들이 연대장을 부르는 별명이었다—\"다들 자넬 기다리고 있어.\""]], [["Vronsky, without answering, looked at his comrade, thinking of something else.", "브론스키는 대꾸도 없이, 다른 무언가를 생각하며 동료를 바라보았다."]], [["“Yes; is that music at his place?”", "\"그래, 저게 그 사람 집에서 들리는 음악인가?\""], ["he said, listening to the familiar sounds of polkas and waltzes floating across to him.", "그가 폴카와 왈츠의 익숙한 가락이 둥둥 떠 와 닿는 것에 귀를 기울이며 말했다."], ["“What’s the fête?”", "\"무슨 잔치라도 있어?\""]], [["“Serpuhovskoy’s come.”", "\"세르푸호프스코이가 왔어.\""]], [["“Aha!”", "\"아하!\""], ["said Vronsky, “why, I didn’t know.”", "브론스키가 말했다. \"이런, 난 몰랐는걸.\""]], [["The smile in his eyes gleamed more brightly than ever.", "그의 눈 속 미소가 그 어느 때보다 환하게 빛났다."]], [["Having once made up his mind that he was happy in his love, that he sacrificed his ambition to it—having anyway taken up this position, Vronsky was incapable of feeling either envious of Serpuhovskoy or hurt with him for not coming first to him when he came to the regiment.", "자기가 사랑 속에서 행복하다고, 그 사랑을 위해 자기 야심을 희생했다고 한번 마음을 정한 이상—어쨌든 이런 입장을 취한 이상, 브론스키는 세르푸호프스코이를 시기하거나, 그가 연대에 와서 자기에게 제일 먼저 들르지 않았다고 해서 서운해할 수가 없었다."], ["Serpuhovskoy was a good friend, and he was delighted he had come.", "세르푸호프스코이는 좋은 친구였고, 그는 그가 와 준 것이 기뻤다."]], [["“Ah, I’m very glad!”", "\"아, 정말 반가운걸!\""]], [["The colonel, Demin, had taken a large country house.", "연대장 데민은 시골에 큰 저택 한 채를 빌려 놓고 있었다."], ["The whole party were in the wide lower balcony.", "일행은 모두 아래층의 넓은 발코니에 있었다."], ["In the courtyard the first objects that met Vronsky’s eyes were a band of singers in white linen coats, standing near a barrel of vodka, and the robust, good-humored figure of the colonel surrounded by officers.", "안마당에 들어선 브론스키의 눈에 맨 먼저 들어온 것은 보드카 통 곁에 늘어선, 흰 무명 외투 차림의 합창단 한 무리와, 장교들에게 둘러싸인 연대장의 건장하고 쾌활한 모습이었다."], ["He had gone out as far as the first step of the balcony and was loudly shouting across the band that played Offenbach’s quadrille, waving his arms and giving some orders to a few soldiers standing on one side.", "연대장은 발코니의 첫 계단까지 나와서, 오펜바흐의 카드리유를 연주하는 악대 너머로 큰 소리를 질러 대며 팔을 휘젓고는, 한쪽에 서 있는 병사 몇몇에게 무어라 지시를 내리고 있었다."], ["A group of soldiers, a quartermaster, and several subalterns came up to the balcony with Vronsky.", "한 무리의 병사들과 병참장교, 그리고 하급 장교 몇이 브론스키와 함께 발코니로 다가왔다."], ["The colonel returned to the table, went out again onto the steps with a tumbler in his hand, and proposed the toast, “To the health of our former comrade, the gallant general, Prince Serpuhovskoy.", "연대장은 식탁으로 돌아갔다가 손에 큰 술잔을 들고 다시 계단으로 나와 건배를 청했다. \"우리의 옛 전우, 용맹한 장군 세르푸호프스코이 공작의 건강을 위하여."], ["Hurrah!”", "만세!\""]], [["The colonel was followed by Serpuhovskoy, who came out onto the steps smiling, with a glass in his hand.", "연대장의 뒤를 이어 세르푸호프스코이가 손에 잔을 들고 미소를 지으며 계단으로 나왔다."]], [["“You always get younger, Bondarenko,”", "\"자네는 갈수록 젊어지는군, 본다렌코.\""], ["he said to the rosy-cheeked, smart-looking quartermaster standing just before him, still youngish looking though doing his second term of service.", "그가 바로 앞에 서 있는, 두 번째 복무 기간을 보내는 중인데도 여전히 젊어 보이는, 발그레한 뺨에 똘똘한 인상의 병참장교에게 말했다."]], [["It was three years since Vronsky had seen Serpuhovskoy.", "브론스키가 세르푸호프스코이를 본 지 삼 년 만이었다."], ["He looked more robust, had let his whiskers grow, but was still the same graceful creature, whose face and figure were even more striking from their softness and nobility than their beauty.", "그는 더 건장해졌고 구레나룻을 길렀지만, 여전히 똑같이 우아한 사람이었다. 그 얼굴과 풍채는 아름답다기보다 부드럽고 기품 있어서 한층 더 눈길을 끌었다."], ["The only change Vronsky detected in him was that subdued, continual radiance of beaming content which settles on the faces of men who are successful and are sure of the recognition of their success by everyone.", "브론스키가 그에게서 발견한 단 하나의 변화는, 성공을 거두고 또 모두가 자기 성공을 인정해 주리라 확신하는 사람들의 얼굴에 자리 잡는, 그 차분하면서도 끊임없이 흘러나오는 흐뭇한 빛이었다."], ["Vronsky knew that radiant air, and immediately observed it in Serpuhovskoy.", "브론스키는 그 빛나는 분위기를 잘 알고 있었고, 세르푸호프스코이에게서 곧바로 그것을 알아보았다."]], [["As Serpuhovskoy came down the steps he saw Vronsky.", "세르푸호프스코이는 계단을 내려오다가 브론스키를 보았다."], ["A smile of pleasure lighted up his face.", "기쁜 미소가 그의 얼굴을 환히 밝혔다."], ["He tossed his head upwards and waved the glass in his hand, greeting Vronsky, and showing him by the gesture that he could not come to him before the quartermaster, who stood craning forward his lips ready to be kissed.", "그는 고개를 위로 젖히고 손에 든 잔을 흔들어 브론스키에게 인사를 건넸고, 그 몸짓으로, 입술을 쑥 내밀고 입맞춤을 기다리며 서 있는 병참장교보다 먼저 그에게 갈 수 없음을 알려 주었다."]], [["“Here he is!”", "\"저기 왔구먼!\""], ["shouted the colonel.", "연대장이 소리쳤다."], ["“Yashvin told me you were in one of your gloomy tempers.”", "\"야슈빈이 그러던데 자네가 잔뜩 우울한 기분에 빠져 있다더군.\""]], [["Serpuhovskoy kissed the moist, fresh lips of the gallant-looking quartermaster, and wiping his mouth with his handkerchief, went up to Vronsky.", "세르푸호프스코이는 늠름한 병참장교의 촉촉하고 싱싱한 입술에 입을 맞추고, 손수건으로 입을 닦으며 브론스키에게 다가갔다."]], [["“How glad I am!”", "\"정말 반갑네!\""], ["he said, squeezing his hand and drawing him on one side.", "그가 브론스키의 손을 꼭 쥐고 한쪽으로 데려가며 말했다."]], [["“You look after him,”", "\"자네가 이 친구를 좀 돌봐 주게.\""], ["the colonel shouted to Yashvin, pointing to Vronsky; and he went down below to the soldiers.", "연대장이 브론스키를 가리키며 야슈빈에게 소리쳤다. 그러고는 아래쪽 병사들에게로 내려갔다."]], [["“Why weren’t you at the races yesterday?", "\"어제는 왜 경마에 안 왔나?"], ["I expected to see you there,”", "거기서 자넬 볼 줄 알았는데.\""], ["said Vronsky, scrutinizing Serpuhovskoy.", "브론스키가 세르푸호프스코이를 찬찬히 살피며 말했다."]], [["“I did go, but late.", "\"가긴 갔는데 늦었어."], ["I beg your pardon,”", "미안하게 됐네.\""], ["he added, and he turned to the adjutant: “Please have this divided from me, each man as much as it runs to.”", "그가 덧붙이고는 부관 쪽으로 돌아섰다. \"이걸 내 몫으로 나눠 주게."], ["And he hurriedly took notes for three hundred roubles from his pocketbook, blushing a little.", "각자 돌아가는 만큼씩 말이야.\" 그러고는 살짝 얼굴을 붉히며 수첩에서 삼백 루블짜리 지폐를 황급히 꺼냈다."]], [["“Vronsky!", "\"브론스키!"], ["Have anything to eat or drink?”", "뭐 좀 먹거나 마시겠나?\""], ["asked Yashvin.", "야슈빈이 물었다."], ["“Hi, something for the count to eat!", "\"어이, 백작님께 먹을 것 좀 갖다 드리게!"], ["Ah, here it is: have a glass!”", "아, 여기 있군. 자, 한잔하게!\""]], [["The fête at the colonel’s lasted a long while.", "연대장 집의 잔치는 오래도록 이어졌다."], ["There was a great deal of drinking.", "술이 어마어마하게 돌았다."], ["They tossed Serpuhovskoy in the air and caught him again several times.", "그들은 세르푸호프스코이를 공중에 던졌다가 다시 받아 내기를 몇 번이고 되풀이했다."], ["Then they did the same to the colonel.", "그다음에는 연대장에게도 똑같이 했다."], ["Then, to the accompaniment of the band, the colonel himself danced with Petritsky.", "그러고 나서는 악대의 반주에 맞춰 연대장이 직접 페트리츠키와 춤을 추었다."], ["Then the colonel, who began to show signs of feebleness, sat down on a bench in the courtyard and began demonstrating to Yashvin the superiority of Russia over Prussia, especially in cavalry attack, and there was a lull in the revelry for a moment.", "이윽고 기운이 빠지기 시작한 연대장은 안마당의 벤치에 앉아, 야슈빈에게 러시아가 특히 기병 돌격에서 프로이센보다 우월하다는 것을 설파하기 시작했고, 한순간 흥청거림이 잦아들었다."], ["Serpuhovskoy went into the house to the bathroom to wash his hands and found Vronsky there; Vronsky was drenching his head with water.", "세르푸호프스코이는 손을 씻으러 집 안의 욕실로 들어갔다가 거기서 브론스키를 만났다. 브론스키는 머리에 물을 끼얹고 있었다."], ["He had taken off his coat and put his sunburnt, hairy neck under the tap, and was rubbing it and his head with his hands.", "그는 윗옷을 벗고 햇볕에 그을린 털 많은 목을 수도꼭지 아래에 댄 채, 손으로 목과 머리를 문지르고 있었다."], ["When he had finished, Vronsky sat down by Serpuhovskoy.", "다 끝내고 나서 브론스키는 세르푸호프스코이 곁에 앉았다."], ["They both sat down in the bathroom on a lounge, and a conversation began which was very interesting to both of them.", "두 사람은 욕실의 긴 의자에 나란히 앉았고, 둘 모두에게 무척 흥미로운 대화가 시작되었다."]], [["“I’ve always been hearing about you through my wife,”", "\"난 늘 아내를 통해 자네 소식을 듣고 있었네.\""], ["said Serpuhovskoy.", "세르푸호프스코이가 말했다."], ["“I’m glad you’ve been seeing her pretty often.”", "\"자네가 그 사람을 꽤 자주 만나 주었다니 기쁘군.\""]], [["“She’s friendly with Varya, and they’re the only women in Petersburg I care about seeing,”", "\"그 사람은 바랴와 친하게 지내지. 그리고 그 두 사람이야말로 페테르부르크에서 내가 만나고 싶은 유일한 여인들이야.\""], ["answered Vronsky, smiling.", "브론스키가 미소 지으며 대답했다."], ["He smiled because he foresaw the topic the conversation would turn on, and he was glad of it.", "그가 미소 지은 것은 대화가 어떤 화제로 흘러갈지 미리 짐작했고, 또 그게 반가웠기 때문이다."]], [["“The only ones?”", "\"유일한 사람들이라고?\""], ["Serpuhovskoy queried, smiling.", "세르푸호프스코이가 미소 지으며 되물었다."]], [["“Yes; and I heard news of you, but not only through your wife,”", "\"그래. 그리고 나도 자네 소식을 들었지."], ["said Vronsky, checking his hint by a stern expression of face.", "하지만 자네 부인을 통해서만은 아니었어.\" 브론스키가 짐짓 엄한 표정으로 그 암시를 눌러 막으며 말했다."], ["“I was greatly delighted to hear of your success, but not a bit surprised.", "\"자네 성공을 듣고 무척 기뻤네. 하지만 조금도 놀라지는 않았어."], ["I expected even more.”", "난 그 이상까지도 기대했으니까.\""]], [["Serpuhovskoy smiled.", "세르푸호프스코이가 미소를 지었다."], ["Such an opinion of him was obviously agreeable to him, and he did not think it necessary to conceal it.", "자기에 대한 그런 평가가 그에게는 분명 흐뭇한 것이었고, 그는 그것을 굳이 감출 필요를 느끼지 못했다."]], [["“Well, I on the contrary expected less—I’ll own frankly.", "\"음, 난 반대로 더 적게 기대했지—솔직히 털어놓자면 말이야."], ["But I’m glad, very glad.", "하지만 기뻐, 아주 기뻐."], ["I’m ambitious; that’s my weakness, and I confess to it.”", "난 야심가야. 그게 내 약점이지, 그건 인정하네.\""]], [["“Perhaps you wouldn’t confess to it if you hadn’t been successful,”", "\"성공하지 못했더라면 자넨 그걸 인정하지 않았을지도 모르지.\""], ["said Vronsky.", "브론스키가 말했다."]], [["“I don’t suppose so,”", "\"그렇진 않을걸세.\""], ["said Serpuhovskoy, smiling again.", "세르푸호프스코이가 다시 미소 지으며 말했다."], ["“I won’t say life wouldn’t be worth living without it, but it would be dull.", "\"그게 없으면 살 가치가 없다고까진 안 하겠네. 그래도 따분하긴 할 거야."], ["Of course I may be mistaken, but I fancy I have a certain capacity for the line I’ve chosen, and that power of any sort in my hands, if it is to be, will be better than in the hands of a good many people I know,”", "물론 내가 틀렸을 수도 있지만, 난 내가 택한 길에 어느 정도 소질이 있다고 생각해. 그리고 내 손에 들어온 권력이라면, 그게 어떤 종류든, 만약 쥐어지게 된다면, 내가 아는 숱한 사람들의 손에 들어가는 것보다는 나을 거라고 보네.\""], ["said Serpuhovskoy, with beaming consciousness of success; “and so the nearer I get to it, the better pleased I am.”", "세르푸호프스코이가 성공을 의식한 환한 빛을 띠며 말했다. \"그러니 거기에 가까워질수록 난 그만큼 더 흐뭇해지는 거지.\""]], [["“Perhaps that is true for you, but not for everyone.", "\"자네한테는 그게 사실일지 몰라도 모두에게 그런 건 아니야."], ["I used to think so too, but here I live and think life worth living not only for that.”", "나도 한때는 그렇게 생각했지. 하지만 지금 이렇게 살아 보니, 인생이란 게 오직 그것만을 위해 살 가치가 있는 건 아니더군.\""]], [["“There it’s out!", "\"이제 나왔구먼!"], ["here it comes!”", "드디어 나왔어!\""], ["said Serpuhovskoy, laughing.", "세르푸호프스코이가 웃으며 말했다."], ["“Ever since I heard about you, about your refusal, I began....", "\"자네 얘기를, 자네가 거절했다는 얘기를 들은 이후로 나는……"], ["Of course, I approved of what you did.", "물론 자네가 한 일은 옳다고 봐."], ["But there are ways of doing everything.", "하지만 무슨 일이든 하는 방식이 있는 법이지."], ["And I think your action was good in itself, but you didn’t do it quite in the way you ought to have done.”", "자네 행동 자체는 훌륭했다고 생각하지만, 자네는 그걸 마땅히 했어야 할 방식대로 한 건 아니었어.\""]], [["“What’s done can’t be undone, and you know I never go back on what I’ve done.", "\"이미 한 일은 돌이킬 수 없어. 그리고 자네도 알다시피 난 내가 한 일을 결코 번복하지 않아."], ["And besides, I’m very well off.”", "게다가 난 지금 아주 만족스럽게 지내고 있고.\""]], [["“Very well off—for the time.", "\"아주 만족스럽다—당분간은 그렇겠지."], ["But you’re not satisfied with that.", "하지만 자넨 그걸로 만족하지 못해. 자네 형한테라면 이런 말 안 하겠네."], ["I wouldn’t say this to your brother.", "그 사람은 착한 어린애야, 여기 우리 주인장처럼 말이지. 저기 또 시작이군!\""], ["He’s a nice child, like our host here.", "그가 \"만세!\" 하고 터지는 함성에 귀를 기울이며 덧붙였다."], ["There he goes!”", "\"그리고 그 사람은 행복하지."], ["he added, listening to the roar of “hurrah!”—“and he’s happy, but that does not satisfy you.”", "하지만 자네는 그런 걸로 만족하지 못해.\""]], [["“I didn’t say it did satisfy me.”", "\"난 그게 날 만족시킨다고 말한 적 없네.\""]], [["“Yes, but that’s not the only thing.", "\"그래, 하지만 그게 전부가 아니지."], ["Such men as you are wanted.”", "자네 같은 사람들이 필요하단 말이야.\""]], [["“By whom?”", "\"누구한테?\""]], [["“By whom?", "\"누구한테냐고?"], ["By society, by Russia.", "사회한테, 러시아한테. 러시아엔 사람이 필요해."], ["Russia needs men; she needs a party, or else everything goes and will go to the dogs.”", "러시아엔 당파가 필요하다고. 안 그러면 모든 게 망가지고, 또 망가져 갈 거야.\""]], [["“How do you mean?", "\"무슨 말인가?"], ["Bertenev’s party against the Russian communists?”", "러시아 공산주의자들에 맞서는 베르테네프의 당파 말인가?\""]], [["“No,”", "\"아니.\""], ["said Serpuhovskoy, frowning with vexation at being suspected of such an absurdity.", "세르푸호프스코이가 그런 터무니없는 짐작을 받은 데 짜증을 내며 미간을 찌푸렸다."], ["“Tout ça est une blague.", "\"Tout ça est une blague."], ["That’s always been and always will be.", "그런 건 늘 있어 왔고 앞으로도 늘 있을 거야."], ["There are no communists.", "공산주의자 따위는 없어."], ["But intriguing people have to invent a noxious, dangerous party.", "하지만 음모를 꾸미는 자들은 유해하고 위험한 당파를 하나 만들어 낼 필요가 있는 거지."], ["It’s an old trick.", "그건 낡은 수법이야."], ["No, what’s wanted is a powerful party of independent men like you and me.”", "아니, 정작 필요한 건 자네나 나처럼 독립적인 사람들의 강력한 당파야.\""]], [["“But why so?” Vronsky mentioned a few men who were in power. “Why aren’t they independent men?”", "* Tout ça est une blague: 프랑스어로 '그건 전부 헛소리야'."]], [["“Simply because they have not, or have not had from birth, an independent fortune; they’ve not had a name, they’ve not been close to the sun and center as we have.", "\"하지만 어째서?\" 브론스키가 권력을 쥐고 있는 몇몇 인물을 거론했다."], ["They can be bought either by money or by favor.", "\"그자들은 왜 독립적인 사람들이 아닌가?\" \"단지 그들이 태생부터 독립적인 재산을 가지지 못했거나 못 가졌기 때문이지."], ["And they have to find a support for themselves in inventing a policy.", "그들은 가문도 없고, 우리처럼 태양과 중심에 가까이 있어 본 적도 없어. 그들은 돈으로든 은혜로든 매수당할 수 있어."], ["And they bring forward some notion, some policy that they don’t believe in, that does harm; and the whole policy is really only a means to a government house and so much income.", "그래서 그들은 어떤 정책을 꾸며 내서 자기들의 버팀목으로 삼아야 하는 거지. 그러고는 자기들도 믿지 않는, 해만 끼치는 어떤 발상이나 정책을 내세우는 거야."], ["Cela n’est pas plus fin que ça, when you get a peep at their cards.", "그 모든 정책이란 게 실은 관저 하나와 얼마간의 수입을 얻기 위한 수단일 뿐이야."], ["I may be inferior to them, stupider perhaps, though I don’t see why I should be inferior to them.", "Cela n’est pas plus fin que ça, 그들의 패를 슬쩍 들여다보면 말이지. 어쩌면 난 그들보다 못할지도, 더 어리석을지도 모르지."], ["But you and I have one important advantage over them for certain, in being more difficult to buy.", "하긴 내가 왜 그들보다 못해야 하는지는 모르겠지만. 그래도 자네와 나는 그들에 비해 한 가지 중요한 이점이 분명 있어."], ["And such men are more needed than ever.”", "우리는 매수하기가 더 어렵다는 거지. 그리고 그런 사람들이야말로 그 어느 때보다 필요하단 말이야.\""]], [["Vronsky listened attentively, but he was not so much interested by the meaning of the words as by the attitude of Serpuhovskoy who was already contemplating a struggle with the existing powers, and already had his likes and dislikes in that higher world, while his own interest in the governing world did not go beyond the interests of his regiment.", "* Cela n’est pas plus fin que ça: 프랑스어로 '그건 그 이상으로 교묘한 게 아니야'. 브론스키는 주의 깊게 들었지만, 그 말의 뜻보다는 세르푸호프스코이의 태도에 더 마음이 끌렸다."], ["Vronsky felt, too, how powerful Serpuhovskoy might become through his unmistakable faculty for thinking things out and for taking things in, through his intelligence and gift of words, so rarely met with in the world in which he moved.", "세르푸호프스코이는 벌써 기존 권력과의 싸움을 구상하고 있었고, 그 더 높은 세계 안에 이미 자기 나름의 호오를 가지고 있었던 반면, 통치 세계에 대한 브론스키 자신의 관심은 자기 연대의 이해관계를 넘어서지 못했다. 또한 브론스키는, 세르푸호프스코이가 자기가 몸담은 세계에서는 좀처럼 만나기 힘든, 사물을 끝까지 사유하고 또 받아들이는 그 의심할 바 없는 능력과 그 지성과 언변의 재능을 통해 얼마나 강력한 인물이 될 수 있을지를 느꼈다."], ["And, ashamed as he was of the feeling, he felt envious.", "그리고 그런 감정이 부끄러웠지만, 그는 시기심을 느꼈다."]], [["“Still I haven’t the one thing of most importance for that,”", "\"그래도 난 거기에 무엇보다 중요한 한 가지가 없네.\""], ["he answered; “I haven’t the desire for power.", "그가 대답했다. \"권력에 대한 욕망이 없어."], ["I had it once, but it’s gone.”", "한때는 있었지만, 이제는 사라졌지.\""]], [["“Excuse me, that’s not true,”", "\"미안하지만, 그건 사실이 아니야.\""], ["said Serpuhovskoy, smiling.", "세르푸호프스코이가 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["“Yes, it is true, it is true ...", "\"아니, 그게 사실이야, 사실이라고……"], ["now!”", "지금은!\""], ["Vronsky added, to be truthful.", "솔직하려고 브론스키가 덧붙였다."]], [["“Yes, it’s true now, that’s another thing; but that now won’t last forever.”", "\"그래, 지금은 사실이지, 그건 또 다른 얘기야. 하지만 그 지금이 영원히 가진 않을 거야.\""]], [["“Perhaps,”", "\"어쩌면.\""], ["answered Vronsky.", "브론스키가 대답했다."]], [["“You say perhaps,”", "\"자네는 어쩌면이라고 하지만.\""], ["Serpuhovskoy went on, as though guessing his thoughts, “but I say for certain.", "세르푸호프스코이가 그의 생각을 알아챈 듯 말을 이었다. \"난 틀림없다고 하겠네."], ["And that’s what I wanted to see you for.", "바로 그래서 내가 자넬 보고 싶었던 거야. 자네 행동은 마땅히 그래야 할 그대로였어."], ["Your action was just what it should have been.", "그건 나도 알아. 하지만 자넨 그걸 계속 끌고 가서는 안 돼."], ["I see that, but you ought not to keep it up.", "난 그저 자네가 내게 carte blanche를 달라는 것뿐이야. 난 자네에게 내 비호를 베풀겠다는 게 아니야……"], ["I only ask you to give me carte blanche.", "하긴, 내가 자넬 비호하지 못할 이유가 또 뭐가 있나?—자네야말로 날 충분히 비호해 주지 않았던가!"], ["I’m not going to offer you my protection ...", "우리 우정은 그런 따위는 다 넘어서는 거라고 믿고 싶군."], ["though, indeed, why shouldn’t I protect you?—you’ve protected me often enough!", "그래.\" 그가 여자처럼 다정한 미소를 그에게 지으며 말했다."], ["I should hope our friendship rises above all that sort of thing. Yes,”", "\"내게 carte blanche를 주고, 연대에서 물러나게."], ["he said, smiling to him as tenderly as a woman, “give me carte blanche, retire from the regiment, and I’ll draw you upwards imperceptibly.”", "그러면 내가 자넬 슬그머니 위로 끌어올려 주겠네.\" * carte blanche: 프랑스어로 '전권', '백지 위임'."]], [["“But you must understand that I want nothing,”", "\"하지만 자네도 알아야 해. 난 아무것도 바라는 게 없어.\""], ["said Vronsky, “except that all should be as it is.”", "브론스키가 말했다. \"그저 모든 게 지금 이대로이기만을 바랄 뿐이지.\""]], [["Serpuhovskoy got up and stood facing him.", "세르푸호프스코이가 일어나 그를 마주 보고 섰다."]], [["“You say that all should be as it is.", "\"자넨 모든 게 지금 이대로이기를 바란다고 하는군."], ["I understand what that means.", "그게 무슨 뜻인지 알아."], ["But listen: we’re the same age, you’ve known a greater number of women perhaps than I have.”", "하지만 들어 보게. 우린 동갑이고, 자넨 아마 나보다 더 많은 여자들을 알았겠지.\""], ["Serpohovskoy’s smile and gestures told Vronsky that he mustn’t be afraid, that he would be tender and careful in touching the sore place.", "세르푸호프스코이의 미소와 몸짓은, 두려워할 것 없다고, 자기가 그 아픈 곳을 건드릴 때 다정하고 조심스럽게 굴겠다고 브론스키에게 일러 주었다. \"하지만 난 결혼한 몸이야."], ["“But I’m married, and believe me, in getting to know thoroughly one’s wife, if one loves her, as someone has said, one gets to know all women better than if one knew thousands of them.”", "그리고 내 말을 믿게나. 누군가가 말했듯, 자기 아내를 사랑한다면, 그 아내 한 사람을 속속들이 알게 됨으로써, 수천 명의 여자를 아는 것보다 모든 여자를 더 잘 알게 되는 법이지.\""]], [["“We’re coming directly!”", "\"곧 가겠소!\""], ["Vronsky shouted to an officer, who looked into the room and called them to the colonel.", "브론스키가 방을 들여다보며 두 사람을 연대장에게 오라고 부르는 장교에게 소리쳤다."]], [["Vronsky was longing now to hear to the end and know what Serpuhovskey would say to him.", "브론스키는 이제 세르푸호프스코이가 무슨 말을 해 줄지 끝까지 듣고 알고 싶어 안달이 났다."]], [["“And here’s my opinion for you.", "\"그리고 이게 자네한테 들려줄 내 의견이야."], ["Women are the chief stumbling block in a man’s career.", "여자란 남자의 출세에서 가장 큰 걸림돌이지."], ["It’s hard to love a woman and do anything.", "여자를 사랑하면서 무언가를 해낸다는 건 어려운 일이야."], ["There’s only one way of having love conveniently without its being a hindrance—that’s marriage.", "사랑을 거추장스러운 방해물로 만들지 않으면서 편하게 누리는 길은 단 하나뿐이지—그게 바로 결혼이야."], ["How, how am I to tell you what I mean?”", "어떻게, 어떻게 내 말뜻을 자네한테 설명하면 좋을까?\""], ["said Serpuhovskoy, who liked similes.", "비유를 좋아하는 세르푸호프스코이가 말했다."], ["“Wait a minute, wait a minute!", "\"잠깐만, 잠깐만! 그래, 꼭 이런 거야."], ["Yes, just as you can only carry a fardeau and do something with your hands, when the fardeau is tied on your back, and that’s marriage.", "fardeau를 등에 묶어 놓아야만 그걸 짊어진 채 두 손으로 무언가를 할 수 있는 것과 같지. 그게 바로 결혼이야."], ["And that’s what I felt when I was married.", "결혼했을 때 내가 느낀 게 바로 그거였어."], ["My hands were suddenly set free.", "갑자기 내 두 손이 자유로워졌거든."], ["But to drag that fardeau about with you without marriage, your hands will always be so full that you can do nothing.", "하지만 결혼도 하지 않고 그 fardeau를 끌고 다니면, 두 손이 늘 꽉 차 있어서 아무것도 할 수가 없게 돼. 마잔코프를 보게, 크루포프를 보게."], ["Look at Mazankov, at Krupov.", "그들은 여자 때문에 출세를 망쳐 버렸지.\""], ["They’ve ruined their careers for the sake of women.”", "* fardeau: 프랑스어로 '짐', '부담'."]], [["“What women!”", "\"무슨 여자들 말인가!\""], ["said Vronsky, recalling the Frenchwoman and the actress with whom the two men he had mentioned were connected.", "브론스키가, 그가 거론한 두 사람과 얽혀 있던 프랑스 여자와 여배우를 떠올리며 말했다."]], [["“The firmer the woman’s footing in society, the worse it is.", "\"여자가 사교계에서 발을 더 단단히 디디고 있을수록 더 나쁜 거야."], ["That’s much the same as—not merely carrying the fardeau in your arms—but tearing it away from someone else.”", "그건 마치 그 fardeau를 두 팔로 짊어지는 것만이 아니라—다른 누군가에게서 빼앗아 오는 것과 다름없는 일이거든.\""]], [["“You have never loved,”", "\"자넨 한 번도 사랑해 본 적이 없군.\""], ["Vronsky said softly, looking straight before him and thinking of Anna.", "브론스키가 똑바로 앞을 바라보며 안나를 생각하면서 나직이 말했다."]], [["“Perhaps.", "\"어쩌면 그럴지도."], ["But you remember what I’ve said to you.", "하지만 내가 자네한테 한 말은 기억해 두게."], ["And another thing, women are all more materialistic than men.", "그리고 또 한 가지, 여자들은 죄다 남자들보다 더 물질적이야."], ["We make something immense out of love, but they are always terre-à-terre.”", "우린 사랑을 가지고 뭔가 어마어마한 걸 만들어 내지만, 그들은 늘 terre-à-terre거든.\""]], [["“Directly, directly!”", "* terre-à-terre: 프랑스어로 '현실적인', '땅에 발붙인'."], ["he cried to a footman who came in.", "\"곧 가겠소, 곧!\" 그가 들어온 하인에게 외쳤다."], ["But the footman had not come to call them again, as he supposed.", "그러나 하인은 그가 생각한 것처럼 두 사람을 다시 부르러 온 게 아니었다."], ["The footman brought Vronsky a note.", "하인은 브론스키에게 쪽지 하나를 가져왔다."]], [["“A man brought it from Princess Tverskaya.”", "\"트베르스카야 공작부인 댁에서 어떤 사람이 가져왔습니다.\""]], [["Vronsky opened the letter, and flushed crimson.", "브론스키는 편지를 열어 보고는 얼굴이 새빨개졌다."]], [["“My head’s begun to ache; I’m going home,”", "\"머리가 아프기 시작했군. 난 집에 가야겠네.\""], ["he said to Serpuhovskoy.", "그가 세르푸호프스코이에게 말했다."]], [["“Oh, good-bye then.", "\"오, 그럼 잘 가게."], ["You give me carte blanche!”", "carte blanche를 내게 주는 거지!\""]], [["“We’ll talk about it later on; I’ll look you up in Petersburg.”", "\"그건 나중에 얘기하세. 페테르부르크에서 자넬 찾아가겠네.\""]], [["# Chapter 22", "# Chapter 22"]], [["It was six o’clock already, and so, in order to be there quickly, and at the same time not to drive with his own horses, known to everyone, Vronsky got into Yashvin’s hired fly, and told the driver to drive as quickly as possible.", "벌써 여섯 시였고, 그래서 빨리 그곳에 가려면서도 동시에 누구나 다 아는 자기 말들을 타고 가지 않으려고, 브론스키는 야시빈이 빌린 마차에 올라타 마부에게 되도록 빨리 몰라고 일렀다."], ["It was a roomy, old-fashioned fly, with seats for four.", "그것은 넷이 앉을 수 있는 좌석이 딸린, 널찍하고 구식인 마차였다."], ["He sat in one corner, stretched his legs out on the front seat, and sank into meditation.", "그는 한쪽 구석에 앉아 두 다리를 앞좌석에 뻗고 깊은 생각에 잠겨들었다."]], [["A vague sense of the order into which his affairs had been brought, a vague recollection of the friendliness and flattery of Serpuhovskoy, who had considered him a man that was needed, and most of all, the anticipation of the interview before him—all blended into a general, joyous sense of life.", "자기 일들이 정리된 어떤 질서에 대한 어렴풋한 느낌, 자기를 꼭 필요한 사람으로 여겨 준 세르푸호프스코이의 호의와 찬사에 대한 어렴풋한 기억, 그리고 무엇보다도 곧 있을 밀회에 대한 기대—이 모든 것이 한데 어우러져 인생에 대한 전반적이고 즐거운 감각으로 녹아들었다."], ["This feeling was so strong that he could not help smiling.", "이 느낌이 어찌나 강렬했던지 그는 미소를 짓지 않을 수 없었다."], ["He dropped his legs, crossed one leg over the other knee, and taking it in his hand, felt the springy muscle of the calf, where it had been grazed the day before by his fall, and leaning back he drew several deep breaths.", "그는 두 다리를 내리고 한쪽 다리를 반대편 무릎 위에 포갠 다음, 손으로 그것을 감싸고는 어제 낙마로 까진 자리, 그 단단한 종아리 근육을 어루만졌다. 그리고 등을 뒤로 기대며 몇 번 깊게 숨을 들이쉬었다."]], [["“I’m happy, very happy!”", "\"행복해, 정말 행복해!\""], ["he said to himself.", "그는 혼잣말로 중얼거렸다."], ["He had often before had this sense of physical joy in his own body, but he had never felt so fond of himself, of his own body, as at that moment.", "전에도 자기 몸이 주는 이런 육체적 기쁨을 자주 느껴 본 적은 있었지만, 그 순간만큼 자기 자신을, 자기 몸을 사랑스럽게 느낀 적은 한 번도 없었다."], ["He enjoyed the slight ache in his strong leg, he enjoyed the muscular sensation of movement in his chest as he breathed.", "그는 튼튼한 다리에서 느껴지는 가벼운 통증을 즐겼고, 숨을 쉴 때마다 가슴에서 느껴지는 움직임의 근육 감각을 즐겼다."], ["The bright, cold August day, which had made Anna feel so hopeless, seemed to him keenly stimulating, and refreshed his face and neck that still tingled from the cold water.", "안나에게 그토록 절망감을 안겨 주었던 밝고 차가운 팔월의 날이 그에게는 짜릿하게 자극적으로 느껴졌고, 차가운 물기로 여전히 얼얼한 그의 얼굴과 목을 산뜻하게 해 주었다."], ["The scent of brilliantine on his whiskers struck him as particularly pleasant in the fresh air.", "콧수염에 바른 브릴리언틴 향기가 상쾌한 공기 속에서 유난히 기분 좋게 다가왔다."], ["Everything he saw from the carriage window, everything in that cold pure air, in the pale light of the sunset, was as fresh, and gay, and strong as he was himself: the roofs of the houses shining in the rays of the setting sun, the sharp outlines of fences and angles of buildings, the figures of passers-by, the carriages that met him now and then, the motionless green of the trees and grass, the fields with evenly drawn furrows of potatoes, and the slanting shadows that fell from the houses, and trees, and bushes, and even from the rows of potatoes—everything was bright like a pretty landscape just finished and freshly varnished.", "마차 창문 너머로 보이는 모든 것이, 그 차갑고 맑은 공기 속의, 노을의 희미한 빛 속의 모든 것이, 그 자신처럼 산뜻하고 즐겁고 강건했다. 지는 햇살을 받아 반짝이는 집들의 지붕, 울타리며 건물 모서리의 날카로운 윤곽선, 행인들의 모습, 이따금 마주치는 마차들, 미동도 없는 나무와 풀의 초록빛, 감자 이랑이 고르게 그어진 밭들, 그리고 집과 나무와 덤불에서, 심지어 감자 이랑에서까지 비스듬히 드리워진 그림자들—모든 것이 막 완성되어 갓 니스를 칠한 아름다운 풍경화처럼 환하게 빛났다."]], [["“Get on, get on!”", "\"어서, 어서!\""], ["he said to the driver, putting his head out of the window, and pulling a three-rouble note out of his pocket he handed it to the man as he looked round.", "그는 창밖으로 고개를 내밀며 마부에게 말했고, 주머니에서 삼 루블짜리 지폐를 꺼내 마부가 뒤를 돌아보는 순간 그것을 건넸다."], ["The driver’s hand fumbled with something at the lamp, the whip cracked, and the carriage rolled rapidly along the smooth highroad.", "마부의 손이 등불 옆에서 무언가를 더듬거리더니, 채찍이 휙 소리를 내며 갈라졌고, 마차는 매끄러운 큰길을 따라 빠르게 굴러갔다."]], [["“I want nothing, nothing but this happiness,”", "\"이 행복 말고는 아무것도, 아무것도 바라지 않아.\""], ["he thought, staring at the bone button of the bell in the space between the windows, and picturing to himself Anna just as he had seen her last time.", "그는 창문 사이 공간에 달린 호출용 뼈 단추를 응시하며 생각했고, 마지막으로 보았던 그대로의 안나를 떠올렸다."], ["“And as I go on, I love her more and more.", "\"그리고 이렇게 다가갈수록, 나는 그녀를 점점 더 사랑하게 돼."], ["Here’s the garden of the Vrede Villa.", "여기가 브레데 별장의 정원이군."], ["Whereabouts will she be?", "그녀는 어디쯤 있을까?"], ["Where? How?", "어디에?"], ["Why did she fix on this place to meet me, and why does she write in Betsy’s letter?”", "어떻게? 왜 하필 이곳을 만날 장소로 정했고, 또 왜 베치의 편지에다 써 보낸 걸까?\""], ["he thought, wondering now for the first time at it.", "그는 이제야 처음으로 그것이 의아하게 여겨졌다."], ["But there was now no time for wonder.", "하지만 이제 의아해할 겨를이 없었다."], ["He called to the driver to stop before reaching the avenue, and opening the door, jumped out of the carriage as it was moving, and went into the avenue that led up to the house.", "그는 가로수길에 이르기 전에 마부에게 멈추라고 일렀고, 문을 열어 아직 움직이는 마차에서 뛰어내려 집으로 이어진 가로수길로 들어섰다."], ["There was no one in the avenue; but looking round to the right he caught sight of her.", "가로수길에는 아무도 없었다. 그러나 오른쪽을 둘러보다가 그녀의 모습을 발견했다."], ["Her face was hidden by a veil, but he drank in with glad eyes the special movement in walking, peculiar to her alone, the slope of the shoulders, and the setting of the head, and at once a sort of electric shock ran all over him.", "그녀의 얼굴은 베일에 가려 있었지만, 그는 그녀만의 독특한 걸음걸이며, 어깨의 기울기며, 머리를 둔 자세를 기쁨에 찬 눈으로 빨아들이듯 바라보았고, 순간 일종의 전기 충격 같은 것이 온몸을 훑고 지나갔다."], ["With fresh force, he felt conscious of himself from the springy motions of his legs to the movements of his lungs as he breathed, and something set his lips twitching.", "그는 새삼스러운 힘으로, 탄력 있게 움직이는 두 다리에서부터 숨을 쉴 때 부풀어 오르는 폐의 움직임에 이르기까지 자기 자신을 의식했고, 무언가가 그의 입술을 실룩이게 했다."]], [["Joining him, she pressed his hand tightly.", "그녀가 그에게 다가와 그의 손을 꼭 쥐었다."]], [["“You’re not angry that I sent for you?", "\"제가 사람을 보내 불러서 화나신 거예요?"], ["I absolutely had to see you,”", "도저히 당신을 만나지 않을 수 없었어요.\""], ["she said; and the serious and set line of her lips, which he saw under the veil, transformed his mood at once.", "그녀가 말했고, 베일 아래로 보이는 그녀의 진지하고 굳게 다문 입매가 그의 기분을 단번에 바꿔 놓았다."]], [["“I angry!", "\"내가 화를 내다니!"], ["But how have you come, where from?”", "그런데 어떻게 왔어요, 어디서 온 거예요?\""]], [["“Never mind,”", "\"그건 상관없어요.\""], ["she said, laying her hand on his, “come along, I must talk to you.”", "그녀가 그의 손에 자기 손을 얹으며 말했다. \"함께 가요, 당신에게 할 말이 있어요.\""]], [["He saw that something had happened, and that the interview would not be a joyous one.", "그는 무언가 일이 벌어졌으며, 이 밀회가 즐거운 것이 되지 않으리라는 걸 알아차렸다."], ["In her presence he had no will of his own: without knowing the grounds of her distress, he already felt the same distress unconsciously passing over him.", "그녀 앞에서 그에게는 자기 의지란 것이 없었다. 그녀가 괴로워하는 까닭을 알지 못한 채로도, 그는 이미 그 똑같은 괴로움이 무의식중에 자기에게로 옮겨 오는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["“What is it?", "\"무슨 일이에요?"], ["what?”", "무슨?\""], ["he asked her, squeezing her hand with his elbow, and trying to read her thoughts in her face.", "그는 팔꿈치로 그녀의 손을 꼭 누르며 물었고, 그녀의 얼굴에서 그 생각을 읽어 내려 애썼다."]], [["She walked on a few steps in silence, gathering up her courage; then suddenly she stopped.", "그녀는 용기를 끌어모으며 말없이 몇 걸음 걸었다. 그러다 별안간 걸음을 멈췄다."]], [["“I did not tell you yesterday,”", "\"어제는 말하지 않았는데.\""], ["she began, breathing quickly and painfully, “that coming home with Alexey Alexandrovitch I told him everything ...", "그녀가 가쁘고 괴롭게 숨을 몰아쉬며 입을 열었다. \"알렉세이 알렉산드로비치와 함께 집으로 돌아오면서 그에게 다 말해 버렸어요……"], ["told him I could not be his wife, that ...", "그의 아내로 살 수 없다고 말했고, 그러니까……"], ["and told him everything.”", "그에게 다 말해 버렸어요.\""]], [["He heard her, unconsciously bending his whole figure down to her as though hoping in this way to soften the hardness of her position for her.", "그는 마치 그렇게 함으로써 그녀의 처지가 안긴 고통을 덜어 주기라도 하려는 듯, 무의식중에 온몸을 그녀에게로 기울인 채 그녀의 말을 들었다."], ["But directly she had said this he suddenly drew himself up, and a proud and hard expression came over his face.", "그러나 그녀가 이 말을 하자마자 그는 갑자기 몸을 곧추세웠고, 그의 얼굴에 자랑스럽고 굳은 표정이 떠올랐다."]], [["“Yes, yes, that’s better, a thousand times better!", "\"그래요, 그래, 그게 더 낫지, 천 배는 더 나아요!"], ["I know how painful it was,”", "얼마나 괴로웠을지 잘 알아요.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["But she was not listening to his words, she was reading his thoughts from the expression of his face.", "하지만 그녀는 그의 말을 듣고 있지 않았다. 그녀는 그의 얼굴 표정에서 그의 속생각을 읽어 내고 있었다."], ["She could not guess that that expression arose from the first idea that presented itself to Vronsky—that a duel was now inevitable.", "그 표정이 브론스키에게 가장 먼저 떠오른 생각—이제 결투를 피할 수 없다는 생각에서 비롯되었다는 것을 그녀는 짐작할 수 없었다."], ["The idea of a duel had never crossed her mind, and so she put a different interpretation on this passing expression of hardness.", "결투라는 생각은 그녀의 머릿속을 한 번도 스친 적이 없었고, 그래서 그녀는 스쳐 지나가는 이 굳은 표정을 다르게 해석했다."]], [["When she got her husband’s letter, she knew then at the bottom of her heart that everything would go on in the old way, that she would not have the strength of will to forego her position, to abandon her son, and to join her lover.", "남편의 편지를 받았을 때, 그녀는 마음 깊은 곳에서 이미 모든 것이 예전 그대로 흘러가리라는 것을, 자기에게는 지금의 처지를 떨치고 아들을 버리고 연인과 결합할 만한 의지력이 없으리라는 것을 알았다."], ["The morning spent at Princess Tverskaya’s had confirmed her still more in this.", "트베르스카야 공작부인 댁에서 보낸 아침나절이 그녀에게 이 생각을 더욱 굳혀 주었다."], ["But this interview was still of the utmost gravity for her.", "하지만 이 밀회는 그녀에게 여전히 더없이 중대한 것이었다."], ["She hoped that this interview would transform her position, and save her.", "그녀는 이 밀회가 자기 처지를 바꿔 놓고 자기를 구원해 주기를 바랐다."], ["If on hearing this news he were to say to her resolutely, passionately, without an instant’s wavering: “Throw up everything and come with me!”", "만약 그가 이 소식을 듣고서 단호하게, 열정적으로, 한순간의 망설임도 없이 그녀에게 \"모든 것을 던져 버리고 나와 함께 갑시다!\""], ["she would give up her son and go away with him.", "하고 말해 준다면, 그녀는 아들을 버리고 그와 함께 떠나갈 것이었다."], ["But this news had not produced what she had expected in him; he simply seemed as though he were resenting some affront.", "하지만 이 소식은 그녀가 그에게서 기대했던 바를 끌어내지 못했다. 그는 그저 무슨 모욕이라도 당해 분개하는 사람처럼 보일 뿐이었다."]], [["“It was not in the least painful to me.", "\"저로서는 조금도 괴롭지 않았어요."], ["It happened of itself,”", "저절로 일어난 일인걸요.\""], ["she said irritably; “and see....”", "그녀가 짜증스럽게 말했다."], ["she pulled her husband’s letter out of her glove.", "\"그리고 보세요……\" 그녀가 장갑 속에서 남편의 편지를 꺼냈다."]], [["“I understand, I understand,”", "\"알아요, 알아.\""], ["he interrupted her, taking the letter, but not reading it, and trying to soothe her.", "그는 편지를 받아 들었으나 읽지는 않은 채 그녀를 달래려 애쓰며 그녀의 말을 가로막았다."], ["“The one thing I longed for, the one thing I prayed for, was to cut short this position, so as to devote my life to your happiness.”", "\"내가 그토록 바라던 단 한 가지, 내가 그토록 기도하던 단 한 가지는 이 처지를 끝장내고 내 삶을 당신의 행복에 바치는 것이었어요.\""]], [["“Why do you tell me that?”", "\"왜 그런 말씀을 하세요?\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“Do you suppose I can doubt it?", "\"제가 그걸 의심할 거라고 생각하세요?"], ["If I doubted....”", "제가 만약 의심했다면……\""]], [["“Who’s that coming?”", "\"저기 오는 게 누구지?\""], ["said Vronsky suddenly, pointing to two ladies walking towards them.", "브론스키가 자기들 쪽으로 걸어오는 두 부인을 가리키며 불쑥 말했다."], ["“Perhaps they know us!”", "\"어쩌면 우리를 아는 사람일지도!\""], ["and he hurriedly turned off, drawing her after him into a side path.", "그러고는 서둘러 방향을 틀어 그녀를 옆길로 이끌었다."]], [["“Oh, I don’t care!”", "\"오, 전 상관없어요!\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["Her lips were quivering.", "그녀의 입술이 떨리고 있었다."], ["And he fancied that her eyes looked with strange fury at him from under the veil.", "그리고 그는 베일 아래로 그녀의 눈이 이상한 분노를 띠고 자기를 바라보는 것 같다는 생각이 들었다."], ["“I tell you that’s not the point—I can’t doubt that; but see what he writes to me.", "\"그게 중요한 게 아니라고 말씀드렸잖아요—그건 의심할 수 없어요. 하지만 그가 제게 뭐라고 썼는지 보세요."], ["Read it.”", "읽어 보세요.\""], ["She stood still again.", "그녀가 다시 걸음을 멈췄다."]], [["Again, just as at the first moment of hearing of her rupture with her husband, Vronsky, on reading the letter, was unconsciously carried away by the natural sensation aroused in him by his own relation to the betrayed husband.", "남편과 결별한 사실을 처음 들었던 그 순간과 똑같이, 브론스키는 편지를 읽으면서 배신당한 남편에 대한 자기 관계가 불러일으킨 자연스러운 감정에 무의식중에 휩쓸렸다."], ["Now while he held his letter in his hands, he could not help picturing the challenge, which he would most likely find at home today or tomorrow, and the duel itself, in which, with the same cold and haughty expression that his face was assuming at this moment he would await the injured husband’s shot, after having himself fired into the air.", "이제 손에 그 편지를 든 채로, 그는 아마도 오늘이나 내일쯤 집에서 받게 될 결투 신청장과, 결투 그 자체를 떠올리지 않을 수 없었다. 그 결투에서, 그는 지금 자기 얼굴에 떠오르고 있는 것과 똑같이 차갑고 오만한 표정으로, 먼저 허공에 한 발을 쏘아 보낸 뒤 모욕당한 남편의 총탄을 기다릴 것이다."], ["And at that instant there flashed across his mind the thought of what Serpuhovskoy had just said to him, and what he had himself been thinking in the morning—that it was better not to bind himself—and he knew that this thought he could not tell her.", "그리고 바로 그 순간, 세르푸호프스코이가 방금 자기에게 했던 말, 그리고 자기가 아침에 스스로 생각했던 것—자기를 얽어매지 않는 편이 낫다는 생각—이 그의 머릿속을 스쳤고, 그는 이 생각만은 그녀에게 말할 수 없다는 것을 알았다."]], [["Having read the letter, he raised his eyes to her, and there was no determination in them.", "편지를 다 읽고 나서 그는 그녀에게로 눈을 들었는데, 그 눈에는 아무런 결단도 담겨 있지 않았다."], ["She saw at once that he had been thinking about it before by himself.", "그녀는 그가 전에 혼자서 이 일을 생각해 보았다는 것을 단번에 알아챘다."], ["She knew that whatever he might say to her, he would not say all he thought.", "그가 무슨 말을 하든, 자기 생각을 전부 말하지는 않으리라는 것을 그녀는 알았다."], ["And she knew that her last hope had failed her.", "그리고 자기의 마지막 희망이 무너져 버렸다는 것을 알았다."], ["This was not what she had been reckoning on.", "이것은 그녀가 기대하고 있던 바가 아니었다."]], [["“You see the sort of man he is,”", "\"그가 어떤 사람인지 아시겠죠.\""], ["she said, with a shaking voice; “he....”", "그녀가 떨리는 목소리로 말했다. \"그는……\""]], [["“Forgive me, but I rejoice at it,”", "\"미안하지만, 나는 이게 기뻐요.\""], ["Vronsky interrupted.", "브론스키가 말을 가로막았다."], ["“For God’s sake, let me finish!”", "\"제발, 끝까지 말하게 해 줘요!\""], ["he added, his eyes imploring her to give him time to explain his words.", "그가 덧붙였고, 그의 눈은 자기 말을 설명할 시간을 달라고 그녀에게 애원하고 있었다."], ["“I rejoice, because things cannot, cannot possibly remain as he supposes.”", "\"내가 기쁜 건, 그가 생각하는 대로 일이 그대로 머물 수는, 도저히 그대로 머물 수는 없기 때문이에요.\""]], [["“Why can’t they?”", "\"왜 그대로 머물 수 없죠?\""], ["Anna said, restraining her tears, and obviously attaching no sort of consequence to what he said.", "안나가 눈물을 억누르며 말했는데, 그가 하는 말에 아무런 의미도 두지 않는 것이 분명했다."], ["She felt that her fate was sealed.", "그녀는 자기 운명이 이미 정해졌다고 느꼈다."]], [["Vronsky meant that after the duel—inevitable, he thought—things could not go on as before, but he said something different.", "브론스키가 말하려던 것은, 그가 보기에 피할 수 없는 결투가 끝난 뒤에는 일이 예전처럼 흘러갈 수 없다는 뜻이었으나, 그는 다른 말을 했다."]], [["“It can’t go on.", "\"이대로 갈 수는 없어요."], ["I hope that now you will leave him.", "이제 당신이 그를 떠나기를 바라요."], ["I hope”—he was confused, and reddened—“that you will let me arrange and plan our life.", "바라건대\"—그는 당황했고 얼굴을 붉혔다—\"내가 우리 삶을 정리하고 계획하도록 맡겨 줘요."], ["Tomorrow....”", "내일……\""], ["he was beginning.", "그가 말을 꺼냈다."]], [["She did not let him go on.", "그녀는 그가 말을 잇도록 두지 않았다."]], [["“But my child!”", "\"하지만 제 아이는요!\""], ["she shrieked.", "그녀가 비명을 질렀다."], ["“You see what he writes!", "\"그가 뭐라고 썼는지 보셨잖아요!"], ["I should have to leave him, and I can’t and won’t do that.”", "저는 아이를 두고 떠나야 할 텐데, 저는 그럴 수 없고, 그러지도 않을 거예요.\""]], [["“But, for God’s sake, which is better?—leave your child, or keep up this degrading position?”", "\"하지만, 제발, 어느 쪽이 더 낫겠어요?—아이를 두고 떠나는 것과, 이 모욕적인 처지를 계속 이어가는 것 중에?\""]], [["“To whom is it degrading?”", "\"누구에게 모욕적이라는 거죠?\""]], [["“To all, and most of all to you.”", "\"모두에게, 그리고 무엇보다도 당신에게요.\""]], [["“You say degrading ...", "\"모욕적이라고 하시는데……"], ["don’t say that.", "그런 말은 하지 마세요."], ["Those words have no meaning for me,”", "그런 말은 제겐 아무 의미도 없어요.\""], ["she said in a shaking voice.", "그녀가 떨리는 목소리로 말했다."], ["She did not want him now to say what was untrue.", "그녀는 이제 그가 진실이 아닌 말을 하기를 바라지 않았다."], ["She had nothing left her but his love, and she wanted to love him.", "그녀에게 남은 것이라곤 그의 사랑뿐이었고, 그녀는 그를 사랑하고 싶었다."], ["“Don’t you understand that from the day I loved you everything has changed for me?", "\"제가 당신을 사랑하게 된 그날부터 제게는 모든 것이 달라졌다는 걸 모르시겠어요?"], ["For me there is one thing, and one thing only—your love.", "제겐 단 한 가지, 오직 단 한 가지밖에 없어요—당신의 사랑이요."], ["If that’s mine, I feel so exalted, so strong, that nothing can be humiliating to me.", "그것이 제 것이라면, 저는 너무도 고양되고 너무도 강해진 기분이라서 그 무엇도 제게 굴욕이 될 수 없어요."], ["I am proud of my position, because ...", "저는 제 처지가 자랑스러워요, 왜냐하면……"], ["proud of being ...", "저는 자랑스러운……"], ["proud....”", "자랑스러운……\""], ["She could not say what she was proud of.", "그녀는 자기가 무엇을 자랑스러워하는지 말할 수 없었다."], ["Tears of shame and despair choked her utterance.", "수치와 절망의 눈물이 그녀의 말문을 막았다."], ["She stood still and sobbed.", "그녀는 우뚝 멈춰 서서 흐느꼈다."]], [["He felt, too, something swelling in his throat and twitching in his nose, and for the first time in his life he felt on the point of weeping.", "그 역시 무언가가 목구멍에서 부풀어 오르고 콧날이 시큰해지는 것을 느꼈고, 난생처음으로 울음이 터질 것만 같은 기분이 들었다."], ["He could not have said exactly what it was touched him so.", "무엇이 자기를 그토록 건드렸는지 그는 정확히 말할 수 없었을 것이다."], ["He felt sorry for her, and he felt he could not help her, and with that he knew that he was to blame for her wretchedness, and that he had done something wrong.", "그는 그녀가 가여웠고, 자기가 그녀를 도울 수 없다는 것을 느꼈으며, 그러면서도 그녀의 비참함이 자기 탓이라는 것을, 자기가 뭔가 잘못을 저질렀다는 것을 알았다."]], [["“Is not a divorce possible?”", "\"이혼은 가능하지 않을까요?\""], ["he said feebly.", "그가 힘없이 말했다."], ["She shook her head, not answering.", "그녀는 대답 없이 고개를 저었다."], ["“Couldn’t you take your son, and still leave him?”", "\"아들을 데리고 와서, 그래도 그를 떠날 수는 없을까요?\""]], [["“Yes; but it all depends on him.", "\"네, 하지만 그건 다 그에게 달렸어요."], ["Now I must go to him,”", "이제 저는 그에게 가 봐야 해요.\""], ["she said shortly.", "그녀가 짧게 말했다."], ["Her presentiment that all would again go on in the old way had not deceived her.", "모든 것이 다시 예전 그대로 흘러가리라는 그녀의 예감은 그녀를 속이지 않았다."]], [["“On Tuesday I shall be in Petersburg, and everything can be settled.”", "\"화요일에 페테르부르크로 가겠어요, 그러면 모든 것을 정리할 수 있을 거예요.\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“But don’t let us talk any more of it.”", "\"하지만 이제 그 일은 더 이상 이야기하지 말아요.\""]], [["Anna’s carriage, which she had sent away, and ordered to come back to the little gate of the Vrede garden, drove up.", "돌려보내면서 브레데 정원의 작은 문으로 다시 오라고 일러두었던 안나의 마차가 다가왔다."], ["Anna said good-bye to Vronsky, and drove home.", "안나는 브론스키에게 작별을 고하고 집으로 향했다."]], [["# Chapter 23", "# Chapter 23"]], [["On Monday there was the usual sitting of the Commission of the 2nd of June.", "월요일에는 6월 2일 위원회의 정례 회의가 있었다."], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch walked into the hall where the sitting was held, greeted the members and the president, as usual, and sat down in his place, putting his hand on the papers laid ready before him.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 회의가 열리는 회의실로 걸어 들어가 늘 그렇듯 위원들과 의장에게 인사한 뒤, 자기 자리에 앉아 미리 준비되어 놓여 있는 서류 위에 손을 얹었다."], ["Among these papers lay the necessary evidence and a rough outline of the speech he intended to make.", "이 서류들 사이에는 필요한 증거 자료와 그가 하려는 연설의 대략적인 윤곽이 들어 있었다."], ["But he did not really need these documents.", "하지만 그에게 이 문서들이 실제로 필요하지는 않았다."], ["He remembered every point, and did not think it necessary to go over in his memory what he would say.", "그는 요점 하나하나를 모두 기억하고 있었으며, 자기가 말할 내용을 머릿속에서 미리 더듬어 볼 필요가 있다고 생각하지 않았다."], ["He knew that when the time came, and when he saw his enemy facing him, and studiously endeavoring to assume an expression of indifference, his speech would flow of itself better than he could prepare it now.", "그는 알고 있었다. 때가 오면, 그리고 자기 앞에 마주 선 적이, 애써 무관심한 표정을 지으려 하는 그 모습을 보게 되면, 자기 연설은 지금 준비할 수 있는 것보다 한결 더 술술 흘러나오리라는 것을."], ["He felt that the import of his speech was of such magnitude that every word of it would have weight.", "그는 자기 연설의 무게가 워낙 막중해서 한 마디 한 마디가 비중을 지니리라고 느꼈다."], ["Meantime, as he listened to the usual report, he had the most innocent and inoffensive air.", "그러는 동안에도 늘 하는 보고를 듣고 있는 그의 태도는 더없이 순진하고 악의 없어 보였다."], ["No one, looking at his white hands, with their swollen veins and long fingers, so softly stroking the edges of the white paper that lay before him, and at the air of weariness with which his head drooped on one side, would have suspected that in a few minutes a torrent of words would flow from his lips that would arouse a fearful storm, set the members shouting and attacking one another, and force the president to call for order.", "부풀어 오른 핏줄과 긴 손가락이 달린 그의 흰 손이 앞에 놓인 흰 종이의 가장자리를 더없이 부드럽게 쓰다듬는 모습이며, 한쪽으로 고개를 떨군 그 나른한 기색을 바라보고서, 몇 분 뒤 그의 입술에서 무서운 폭풍을 불러일으킬, 위원들을 소리치게 하고 서로 공격하게 만들며 의장으로 하여금 정숙을 호소하지 않을 수 없게 할 말의 격류가 쏟아져 나오리라고 의심할 사람은 아무도 없었다."], ["When the report was over, Alexey Alexandrovitch announced in his subdued, delicate voice that he had several points to bring before the meeting in regard to the Commission for the Reorganization of the Native Tribes.", "보고가 끝나자 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 차분하고 섬세한 목소리로, 원주민 부족 재편성 위원회와 관련하여 회의에 제기할 사항이 몇 가지 있다고 밝혔다."], ["All attention was turned upon him.", "모든 시선이 그에게 쏠렸다."], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch cleared his throat, and not looking at his opponent, but selecting, as he always did while he was delivering his speeches, the first person sitting opposite him, an inoffensive little old man, who never had an opinion of any sort in the Commission, began to expound his views.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 헛기침을 한 뒤, 자기 상대를 바라보지 않고, 연설을 할 때면 늘 그러듯이 맞은편에 앉은 첫 번째 사람을, 위원회에서 어떤 종류의 의견도 가져 본 적 없는 악의 없는 자그마한 노인을 골라 자기 견해를 펼치기 시작했다. 그가 기본적이고 근본적인 법률에 관한 대목에 이르자, 그의 상대가 벌떡 일어나 항의하기 시작했다."], ["When he reached the point about the fundamental and radical law, his opponent jumped up and began to protest.", "역시 위원회의 일원이었고 마찬가지로 정곡을 찔려 발끈한 스트레모프가 자신을 변호하기 시작했고, 결국 폭풍 같은 회의가 이어졌다."], ["Stremov, who was also a member of the Commission, and also stung to the quick, began defending himself, and altogether a stormy sitting followed; but Alexey Alexandrovitch triumphed, and his motion was carried, three new commissions were appointed, and the next day in a certain Petersburg circle nothing else was talked of but this sitting.", "그러나 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 승리했고, 그의 동의안은 가결되었으며, 새로운 위원회 세 개가 임명되었다. 다음 날 페테르부르크의 어느 모임에서는 이 회의 말고는 다른 화제가 입에 오르지 않았다."], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch’s success had been even greater than he had anticipated.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 성공은 그가 예상했던 것보다도 더 컸다."]], [["Next morning, Tuesday, Alexey Alexandrovitch, on waking up, recollected with pleasure his triumph of the previous day, and he could not help smiling, though he tried to appear indifferent, when the chief secretary of his department, anxious to flatter him, informed him of the rumors that had reached him concerning what had happened in the Commission.", "이튿날 아침, 화요일에 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 잠에서 깨어 전날의 승리를 흐뭇하게 떠올렸고, 그의 환심을 사려고 안달이 난 부서의 수석 비서관이 위원회에서 일어난 일에 관해 자기 귀에 들어온 소문을 알려 주었을 때, 무관심한 척하려 애썼지만 미소를 짓지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["Absorbed in business with the chief secretary, Alexey Alexandrovitch had completely forgotten that it was Tuesday, the day fixed by him for the return of Anna Arkadyevna, and he was surprised and received a shock of annoyance when a servant came in to inform him of her arrival.", "수석 비서관과 업무에 몰두해 있던 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 그날이 화요일, 곧 자기가 안나 아르카지예브나의 귀가일로 정해 둔 날이라는 것을 까맣게 잊고 있었고, 하인이 들어와 그녀의 도착을 알리자 깜짝 놀라며 짜증의 충격을 받았다."]], [["Anna had arrived in Petersburg early in the morning; the carriage had been sent to meet her in accordance with her telegram, and so Alexey Alexandrovitch might have known of her arrival.", "안나는 아침 일찍 페테르부르크에 도착했다. 그녀의 전보에 따라 마차가 마중 나가 있었으니, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치도 그녀의 도착을 알 수 있었을 터였다."], ["But when she arrived, he did not meet her.", "그러나 그녀가 도착했을 때 그는 그녀를 맞이하러 나오지 않았다."], ["She was told that he had not yet gone out, but was busy with his secretary.", "그녀는 그가 아직 외출하지 않았으며 비서관과 일을 보고 있다는 말을 들었다."], ["She sent word to her husband that she had come, went to her own room, and occupied herself in sorting out her things, expecting he would come to her.", "그녀는 자기가 왔다는 전갈을 남편에게 보낸 뒤 자기 방으로 가서, 그가 자기에게 오리라 기대하며 짐을 정리하는 데 몰두했다."], ["But an hour passed; he did not come.", "그러나 한 시간이 지나도 그는 오지 않았다."], ["She went into the dining-room on the pretext of giving some directions, and spoke loudly on purpose, expecting him to come out there; but he did not come, though she heard him go to the door of his study as he parted from the chief secretary.", "그녀는 몇 가지 지시를 한다는 핑계로 식당으로 가서, 그가 그리로 나오기를 기대하며 일부러 큰 소리로 말했다. 하지만 그가 수석 비서관과 헤어지면서 서재 문으로 가는 소리는 들렸어도, 그는 나오지 않았다."], ["She knew that he usually went out quickly to his office, and she wanted to see him before that, so that their attitude to one another might be defined.", "그녀는 그가 평소 사무실로 서둘러 나간다는 것을 알고 있었기에, 그 전에 그를 만나 두 사람 사이의 태도가 분명히 정해지기를 바랐다."]], [["She walked across the drawing-room and went resolutely to him.", "그녀는 응접실을 가로질러 단호하게 그에게로 갔다."], ["When she went into his study he was in official uniform, obviously ready to go out, sitting at a little table on which he rested his elbows, looking dejectedly before him.", "그녀가 서재로 들어갔을 때 그는 관복을 입고 있었으며, 누가 보아도 외출 준비가 끝난 채로 작은 탁자에 두 팔꿈치를 괴고 앉아 풀 죽은 얼굴로 앞을 바라보고 있었다."], ["She saw him before he saw her, and she saw that he was thinking of her.", "그녀는 그가 자기를 보기 전에 그를 보았고, 그가 자기 생각을 하고 있다는 것을 알아차렸다."]], [["On seeing her, he would have risen, but changed his mind, then his face flushed hotly—a thing Anna had never seen before, and he got up quickly and went to meet her, looking not at her eyes, but above them at her forehead and hair.", "그녀를 보고서 그는 일어서려다가 마음을 바꾸었고, 이내 그의 얼굴이 화끈 달아올랐다. 안나가 한 번도 본 적 없는 일이었다."], ["He went up to her, took her by the hand, and asked her to sit down.", "그러더니 그는 재빨리 일어나 그녀를 맞으러 갔는데, 그녀의 눈이 아니라 그 위쪽의 이마와 머리카락을 바라보았다. 그는 그녀에게 다가가 손을 잡고 자리에 앉으라고 청했다."]], [["“I am very glad you have come,”", "\"당신이 와서 정말 기쁘오.\""], ["he said, sitting down beside her, and obviously wishing to say something, he stuttered.", "그가 그녀 곁에 앉으며 말했다. 무언가 할 말이 있는 게 분명한데도 그는 말을 더듬었다."], ["Several times he tried to begin to speak, but stopped.", "몇 번이나 입을 떼려다가 멈추었다."], ["In spite of the fact that, preparing herself for meeting him, she had schooled herself to despise and reproach him, she did not know what to say to him, and she felt sorry for him.", "그를 만날 채비를 하면서 그를 경멸하고 비난하리라고 스스로를 다잡았건만, 그녀는 그에게 무슨 말을 해야 할지 알 수 없었고, 그가 가엾게 느껴졌다."], ["And so the silence lasted for some time.", "그래서 침묵이 한동안 이어졌다."], ["“Is Seryozha quite well?”", "\"세료자는 잘 있소?\""], ["he said, and not waiting for an answer, he added: “I shan’t be dining at home today, and I have got to go out directly.”", "그가 말하더니, 대답을 기다리지도 않고 덧붙였다. \"오늘은 집에서 식사를 못 하오, 지금 바로 나가 봐야 하니까.\""]], [["“I had thought of going to Moscow,”", "\"저는 모스크바로 갈까 생각했었어요.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“No, you did quite, quite right to come,”", "\"아니, 당신이 온 건 아주, 아주 잘한 일이오.\""], ["he said, and was silent again.", "그가 말하고는 다시 입을 다물었다."]], [["Seeing that he was powerless to begin the conversation, she began herself.", "그가 대화를 시작할 힘이 없다는 것을 알아차린 그녀가 직접 입을 열었다."]], [["“Alexey Alexandrovitch,” she said, looking at him and not dropping her eyes under his persistent gaze at her hair, “I’m a guilty woman, I’m a bad woman, but I am the same as I was, as I told you then, and I have come to tell you that I can change nothing.”", "\"알렉세이 알렉산드로비치.\" 그녀는 그를 바라보며, 자기 머리카락을 집요하게 응시하는 그의 시선 아래서도 눈을 내리깔지 않은 채 말했다. \"저는 죄 지은 여자예요, 저는 나쁜 여자예요. 하지만 저는 그때 당신에게 말씀드린 그대로, 예전 그대로의 저예요. 그리고 저는 아무것도 바꿀 수 없다는 말을 하러 왔어요.\""]], [["“I have asked you no question about that,”", "\"나는 그것에 관해 당신에게 아무것도 묻지 않았소.\""], ["he said, all at once, resolutely and with hatred looking her straight in the face; “that was as I had supposed.”", "그가 불쑥, 단호하게, 증오를 담아 그녀의 얼굴을 똑바로 바라보며 말했다. \"그건 내가 짐작했던 대로였소.\""], ["Under the influence of anger he apparently regained complete possession of all his faculties.", "분노의 영향 아래 그는 분명 자기 능력을 온전히 되찾은 것 같았다. \"하지만 그때 내가 당신에게 말했고 또 편지로도 썼듯이,\""], ["“But as I told you then, and have written to you,”", "그가 가늘고 날카로운 목소리로 말했다."], ["he said in a thin, shrill voice, “I repeat now, that I am not bound to know this.", "\"지금 다시 한번 말하겠소, 나는 이것을 알아야 할 의무가 없소. 나는 그것을 모르는 걸로 하겠소."], ["I ignore it. Not all wives are so kind as you, to be in such a hurry to communicate such agreeable news to their husbands.”", "모든 아내가 당신처럼 친절해서 그렇게 서둘러 남편에게 그런 유쾌한 소식을 전하지는 않으니까.\""], ["He laid special emphasis on the word “agreeable.”", "그는 '유쾌한'이라는 말에 특별히 힘을 주었다."], ["“I shall ignore it so long as the world knows nothing of it, so long as my name is not disgraced.", "\"세상이 그것을 전혀 모르는 한, 내 이름이 더럽혀지지 않는 한, 나는 그것을 모르는 걸로 하겠소."], ["And so I simply inform you that our relations must be just as they have always been, and that only in the event of your compromising me I shall be obliged to take steps to secure my honor.”", "그래서 나는 그저 당신에게 알려 두겠소, 우리 관계는 늘 그래 왔던 그대로여야 하며, 당신이 나를 망신시키는 경우에 한해서만 내 명예를 지키기 위한 조처를 취하지 않을 수 없으리라고.\""]], [["“But our relations cannot be the same as always,”", "\"하지만 우리 관계는 늘 그랬던 그대로일 수 없어요.\""], ["Anna began in a timid voice, looking at him with dismay.", "안나가 겁먹은 목소리로, 당혹스러운 얼굴로 그를 바라보며 입을 열었다."]], [["When she saw once more those composed gestures, heard that shrill, childish, and sarcastic voice, her aversion for him extinguished her pity for him, and she felt only afraid, but at all costs she wanted to make clear her position.", "다시금 그 침착한 몸짓을 보고, 그 날카롭고 어린아이 같고 비아냥거리는 목소리를 듣자, 그에 대한 혐오감이 그를 향한 연민을 꺼 버렸고, 그녀는 오직 두려움만을 느꼈으나, 무슨 수를 써서라도 자기 처지를 분명히 밝히고 싶었다."]], [["“I cannot be your wife while I....”", "\"저는 당신의 아내일 수 없어요, 제가……\""], ["she began.", "그녀가 입을 열었다."]], [["He laughed a cold and malignant laugh.", "그가 차갑고 악의에 찬 웃음을 터뜨렸다."]], [["“The manner of life you have chosen is reflected, I suppose, in your ideas.", "\"당신이 선택한 삶의 방식이, 짐작건대, 당신의 생각에도 반영된 모양이군."], ["I have too much respect or contempt, or both ...", "나는 너무나 큰 존경 혹은 경멸을, 아니면 그 둘 다를……"], ["I respect your past and despise your present ...", "나는 당신의 과거를 존경하고 당신의 현재를 경멸하오……"], ["that I was far from the interpretation you put on my words.”", "그러니까 당신이 내 말에 갖다 붙인 그런 해석과는 거리가 멀었단 말이오.\""]], [["Anna sighed and bowed her head.", "안나는 한숨을 쉬며 고개를 숙였다."]], [["“Though indeed I fail to comprehend how, with the independence you show,”", "\"하긴 정말이지 나로서는 이해할 수가 없소, 당신이 보이는 그 독립성으로,\""], ["he went on, getting hot, “—announcing your infidelity to your husband and seeing nothing reprehensible in it, apparently—you can see anything reprehensible in performing a wife’s duties in relation to your husband.”", "그가 흥분해서 말을 이었다. \"—남편에게 자기 부정을 알리면서 거기서 비난받을 것을 전혀 보지 못하는 듯한 당신이, 어떻게 남편에 대한 아내의 의무를 다하는 데서는 비난받을 무언가를 볼 수 있는지.\""]], [["“Alexey Alexandrovitch!", "\"알렉세이 알렉산드로비치!"], ["What is it you want of me?”", "도대체 저한테 뭘 바라시는 거예요?\""]], [["“I want you not to meet that man here, and to conduct yourself so that neither the world nor the servants can reproach you ... not to see him.", "\"내가 바라는 건 당신이 그 남자를 이 집에서 만나지 않는 것, 그리고 세상도 하인들도 당신을 비난할 수 없도록 처신하는 것이오……"], ["That’s not much, I think.", "그를 만나지 않는 것 말이오."], ["And in return you will enjoy all the privileges of a faithful wife without fulfilling her duties.", "그리 대단한 건 아니라고 생각하오. 그 대가로 당신은 아내의 의무를 다하지 않고도 정숙한 아내의 온갖 특권을 누릴 거요."], ["That’s all I have to say to you.", "당신에게 할 말은 그게 전부요."], ["Now it’s time for me to go.", "이제 나갈 시간이군."], ["I’m not dining at home.”", "집에서 식사는 하지 않겠소.\""], ["He got up and moved towards the door.", "그는 일어나 문 쪽으로 걸어갔다."]], [["Anna got up too.", "안나도 일어났다."], ["Bowing in silence, he let her pass before him.", "그는 말없이 고개를 숙여, 그녀가 자기보다 먼저 지나가도록 길을 비켜 주었다."]], [["# Chapter 24", "# Chapter 24"]], [["The night spent by Levin on the haycock did not pass without result for him.", "레빈이 건초 더미 위에서 보낸 그 밤은 그에게 아무 결과 없이 지나가지 않았다."], ["The way in which he had been managing his land revolted him and had lost all attraction for him.", "그동안 자기 영지를 경영해 온 방식이 그에게 역겹게 느껴졌고, 그것은 그에게 모든 매력을 잃어버렸다."], ["In spite of the magnificent harvest, never had there been, or, at least, never it seemed to him, had there been so many hindrances and so many quarrels between him and the peasants as that year, and the origin of these failures and this hostility was now perfectly comprehensible to him.", "훌륭한 수확에도 불구하고, 그해처럼 그와 농민들 사이에 그토록 많은 방해와 그토록 많은 다툼이 있었던 적은—적어도 그가 보기에는—없었다. 그리고 이 실패들과 이 적대감의 근원이 이제 그에게 완전히 이해되었다."], ["The delight he had experienced in the work itself, and the consequent greater intimacy with the peasants, the envy he felt of them, of their life, the desire to adopt that life, which had been to him that night not a dream but an intention, the execution of which he had thought out in detail—all this had so transformed his view of the farming of the land as he had managed it, that he could not take his former interest in it, and could not help seeing that unpleasant relation between him and the workpeople which was the foundation of it all.", "그가 노동 그 자체에서 맛본 기쁨, 그로 인해 농민들과 한층 가까워진 것, 그가 그들에 대해, 그들의 삶에 대해 느낀 부러움, 그 삶을 받아들이고 싶다는 욕망—그것은 그날 밤 그에게 한낱 꿈이 아니라 하나의 결심이었고, 그 실행을 그는 세세하게 머릿속으로 짜 두기까지 했다—이 모든 것이 그가 경영해 온 농사에 대한 그의 시각을 완전히 뒤바꾸어 놓아서, 그는 더 이상 거기에 예전 같은 흥미를 가질 수 없었고, 또 그 모든 것의 토대를 이루는, 자기와 일꾼들 사이의 그 불쾌한 관계를 보지 않을 수 없었다. 파바 같은 개량종 암소들의 무리, 갈아엎어 비옥하게 만든 온 토지, 울타리로 둘러친 평평한 아홉 개의 밭, 거름을 듬뿍 준 이백사십 에이커, 줄뿌림으로 뿌린 씨앗, 그 밖의 모든 것—이 모든 것은, 만약 그 일이 자기들 자신을 위해, 혹은 자기들과 동료들을 위해—그들과 마음이 통하는 사람들을 위해 행해진 것이었다면 더없이 훌륭한 일이었을 것이다."], ["The herd of improved cows such as Pava, the whole land ploughed over and enriched, the nine level fields surrounded with hedges, the two hundred and forty acres heavily manured, the seed sown in drills, and all the rest of it—it was all splendid if only the work had been done for themselves, or for themselves and comrades—people in sympathy with them.", "그러나 이제 그는 분명히 깨달았다(농업에 관한 책—그 책에서 농사의 주요 요소는 노동자가 되어야 했다—을 집필한 일이 이 점에서 그에게 크게 도움이 되었다). 그가 해 오던 종류의 농사란 그와 노동자들 사이의 잔혹하고 완강한 투쟁에 다름 아니며, 그 투쟁에서 한쪽—그의 쪽—에는 모든 것을 자기가 더 낫다고 여기는 방식으로 바꾸려는 끊임없고 격렬한 노력이 있었고, 다른 쪽에는 사물의 자연스러운 질서가 있었다."], ["But he saw clearly now (his work on a book of agriculture, in which the chief element in husbandry was to have been the laborer, greatly assisted him in this) that the sort of farming he was carrying on was nothing but a cruel and stubborn struggle between him and the laborers, in which there was on one side—his side—a continual intense effort to change everything to a pattern he considered better; on the other side, the natural order of things.", "그리고 이 투쟁에서 그는, 자기 쪽에서는 막대한 힘을 쏟아붓는데도, 다른 쪽에서는 아무런 노력도, 심지어 아무런 의도조차도 없는 채로, 결국 얻어지는 것이라곤 일이 어느 쪽의 마음에도 들지 않는다는 것, 그리고 훌륭한 농기구와 훌륭한 가축과 토지가 누구에게도 이로움 없이 망가진다는 것뿐임을 보았다. 무엇보다 나쁜 것은, 이 일에 쏟아부은 에너지가 단순히 낭비되는 데 그치지 않는다는 점이었다."], ["And in this struggle he saw that with immense expenditure of force on his side, and with no effort or even intention on the other side, all that was attained was that the work did not go to the liking of either side, and that splendid tools, splendid cattle and land were spoiled with no good to anyone.", "이 체계의 의미가 그에게 분명해진 지금, 그는 자기 에너지의 목적이 더없이 보잘것없는 것임을 느끼지 않을 수 없었다. 사실 이 투쟁은 무엇을 위한 것이었던가?"], ["Worst of all, the energy expended on this work was not simply wasted.", "그는 자기 몫의 한 푼 한 푼을 위해 싸우고 있었다(또 그럴 수밖에 없었으니, 노력을 조금만 늦추어도 일꾼들의 품삯을 치를 돈이 없었을 테니까)."], ["He could not help feeling now, since the meaning of this system had become clear to him, that the aim of his energy was a most unworthy one. In reality, what was the struggle about?", "반면에 그들은 그저 자기들 일을 쉽고 기분 좋게, 다시 말해 늘 해 오던 대로 할 수 있도록 싸우고 있을 뿐이었다."], ["He was struggling for every farthing of his share (and he could not help it, for he had only to relax his efforts, and he would not have had the money to pay his laborers’ wages), while they were only struggling to be able to do their work easily and agreeably, that is to say, as they were used to doing it.", "그의 이익을 위해서는 일꾼 하나하나가 가능한 한 열심히 일하고, 그러면서도 정신을 바짝 차려 키질 기계며, 말이 끄는 갈퀴며, 탈곡기를 망가뜨리지 않으려 애쓰고, 자기가 하는 일에 주의를 기울여야 했다. 일꾼이 원하는 것은 가능한 한 즐겁게, 쉬어 가며, 무엇보다도 아무 생각 없이 되는대로 무심하게 일하는 것이었다."], ["It was for his interests that every laborer should work as hard as possible, and that while doing so he should keep his wits about him, so as to try not to break the winnowing machines, the horse rakes, the thrashing machines, that he should attend to what he was doing.", "그해 여름 레빈은 이것을 발걸음마다 보았다. 그는 사람들을 보내 건초로 쓸 클로버를 베게 했는데, 클로버가 풀과 잡초로 뒤덮여 씨앗으로는 쓸모없는, 가장 못한 자리를 골라 지정해 주었다."], ["What the laborer wanted was to work as pleasantly as possible, with rests, and above all, carelessly and heedlessly, without thinking. That summer Levin saw this at every step.", "그런데도 그들은 거듭거듭 가장 좋은 클로버 밭을 베어 버리고는, 집사가 그렇게 시켰다는 핑계로 자기들을 변명하면서, 훌륭한 건초가 될 거라는 장담으로 그를 달래려 했다."], ["He sent the men to mow some clover for hay, picking out the worst patches where the clover was overgrown with grass and weeds and of no use for seed; again and again they mowed the best acres of clover, justifying themselves by the pretense that the bailiff had told them to, and trying to pacify him with the assurance that it would be splendid hay; but he knew that it was owing to those acres being so much easier to mow.", "그러나 그는 그것이 그 밭이 베기에 훨씬 더 수월했기 때문임을 알고 있었다. 그가 건초를 던져 올리려고 건초 기계를 내보내자—그것은 첫 줄에서 망가지고 말았으니, 머리 위로 커다란 날개가 펄럭이는 동안 앞자리에 앉아 있는 것이 농민에게는 따분한 일이었기 때문이다."], ["He sent out a hay machine for pitching the hay—it was broken at the first row because it was dull work for a peasant to sit on the seat in front with the great wings waving above him.", "그러자 그는 이런 말을 들었다. \"걱정 마십쇼, 나리."], ["And he was told, “Don’t trouble, your honor, sure, the womenfolks will pitch it quick enough.”", "아낙네들이 금방 던져 올릴 겁니다요.\" 쟁기는 사실상 쓸모가 없었으니, 일꾼이 쟁기를 돌릴 때 보습을 들어 올릴 생각을 도무지 하지 않고 억지로 돌리는 바람에 말을 혹사하고 땅을 헤집어 놓았기 때문이다."], ["The ploughs were practically useless, because it never occurred to the laborer to raise the share when he turned the plough, and forcing it round, he strained the horses and tore up the ground, and Levin was begged not to mind about it.", "그런데도 레빈은 그런 것에 신경 쓰지 말라는 부탁을 받았다. 말들은 밀밭으로 제멋대로 들어가도록 방치되었으니, 일꾼 가운데 단 한 명도 야간 경비를 맡으려 하지 않았기 때문이다."], ["The horses were allowed to stray into the wheat because not a single laborer would consent to be night-watchman, and in spite of orders to the contrary, the laborers insisted on taking turns for night duty, and Ivan, after working all day long, fell asleep, and was very penitent for his fault, saying, “Do what you will to me, your honor.”", "그리고 그러지 말라는 명령에도 불구하고 일꾼들은 한사코 돌아가며 야간 근무를 서겠다고 고집했고, 이반은 온종일 일한 뒤에 잠들어 버리고는, 자기 잘못을 몹시 뉘우치며 이렇게 말했다. \"저를 어떻게 하시든 나리 좋으실 대로 하십쇼.\""]], [["They killed three of the best calves by letting them into the clover aftermath without care as to their drinking, and nothing would make the men believe that they had been blown out by the clover, but they told him, by way of consolation, that one of his neighbors had lost a hundred and twelve head of cattle in three days.", "그들은 가장 좋은 송아지 세 마리를 죽이고 말았으니, 클로버를 벤 뒤에 다시 자란 풀밭에 송아지들을 풀어 놓으면서 물 먹이는 일을 소홀히 했기 때문이다. 그런데도 송아지들이 클로버 때문에 배가 부풀어 죽었다는 것을 어떤 말로도 사람들에게 믿게 할 수 없었고, 그들은 그를 위로한답시고, 그의 이웃 가운데 한 사람은 사흘 만에 가축 백열두 마리를 잃었다고 일러 주었다."], ["All this happened, not because anyone felt ill-will to Levin or his farm; on the contrary, he knew that they liked him, thought him a simple gentleman (their highest praise); but it happened simply because all they wanted was to work merrily and carelessly, and his interests were not only remote and incomprehensible to them, but fatally opposed to their most just claims.", "이 모든 일은 누군가가 레빈이나 그의 농장에 악의를 품어서 일어난 것이 아니었다. 오히려 그는 그들이 자기를 좋아하고 자기를 소탈한 나리라고(그것이 그들의 최고 찬사였다) 여긴다는 것을 알고 있었다."], ["Long before, Levin had felt dissatisfaction with his own position in regard to the land.", "그저 그 일은, 그들이 원하는 것이라곤 신명 나게 되는대로 일하는 것뿐이고, 그의 이익이란 그들에게 멀고 이해할 수 없는 것일 뿐 아니라 그들의 더없이 정당한 요구와 치명적으로 어긋나는 것이었기에 일어났을 뿐이다."], ["He saw where his boat leaked, but he did not look for the leak, perhaps purposely deceiving himself.", "오래전부터 레빈은 토지에 관한 자기 자신의 처지에 불만을 느껴 왔다. 그는 자기 배가 어디서 물이 새는지 알고 있었지만, 그 새는 곳을 찾으려 하지 않았다."], ["(Nothing would be left him if he lost faith in it.) But now he could deceive himself no longer.", "어쩌면 일부러 자기 자신을 속였는지도 모른다. (만약 그것에 대한 믿음을 잃는다면 그에게 남는 것은 아무것도 없었을 테니까.) 그러나 이제 그는 더 이상 자기 자신을 속일 수 없었다."], ["The farming of the land, as he was managing it, had become not merely unattractive but revolting to him, and he could take no further interest in it.", "그가 경영해 온 방식의 농사는 그에게 단순히 매력 없는 것을 넘어 역겨운 것이 되어 버렸고, 그는 더 이상 거기에 흥미를 가질 수 없었다."]], [["To this now was joined the presence, only twenty-five miles off, of Kitty Shtcherbatskaya, whom he longed to see and could not see.", "이제 여기에 더하여, 불과 이십오 마일 떨어진 곳에 키티 셰르바츠카야가 있다는 사실이 끼어들었다. 그는 그녀를 보고 싶었지만 볼 수가 없었다."], ["Darya Alexandrovna Oblonskaya had invited him, when he was over there, to come; to come with the object of renewing his offer to her sister, who would, so she gave him to understand, accept him now.", "다리야 알렉산드로브나 오블론스카야는 그가 그쪽에 와 있을 때 그를 초대했었다—그녀의 여동생에게 다시 청혼하라는 목적으로 오라고 했고, 그녀가 넌지시 알려 준 바로는 이제 동생이 그를 받아들이리라는 것이었다."], ["Levin himself had felt on seeing Kitty Shtcherbatskaya that he had never ceased to love her; but he could not go over to the Oblonskys’, knowing she was there.", "레빈 자신도 키티 셰르바츠카야를 보고서 자기가 그녀를 사랑하기를 한 번도 멈춘 적이 없음을 느꼈다. 그러나 그녀가 거기 있다는 것을 알면서 오블론스키 집에 갈 수는 없었다."], ["The fact that he had made her an offer, and she had refused him, had placed an insuperable barrier between her and him.", "그가 그녀에게 청혼했고 그녀가 그를 거절했다는 사실이, 그녀와 그 사이에 넘을 수 없는 장벽을 세워 놓았던 것이다."], ["“I can’t ask her to be my wife merely because she can’t be the wife of the man she wanted to marry,”", "\"그녀가 자기가 결혼하고 싶었던 남자의 아내가 될 수 없게 되었다는 이유만으로 내 아내가 되어 달라고 청할 수는 없어.\""], ["he said to himself.", "그는 혼잣말을 했다."], ["The thought of this made him cold and hostile to her.", "이 생각이 그를 그녀에게 차갑고 적대적으로 만들었다."], ["“I should not be able to speak to her without a feeling of reproach; I could not look at her without resentment; and she will only hate me all the more, as she’s bound to.", "\"나는 비난의 감정 없이는 그녀에게 말을 건넬 수 없을 거야. 원망 없이는 그녀를 바라볼 수 없을 테고."], ["And besides, how can I now, after what Darya Alexandrovna told me, go to see them?", "그러면 그녀는 나를 더욱더 미워하겠지, 당연히 그럴 수밖에. 게다가, 다리야 알렉산드로브나가 내게 한 말을 듣고 난 지금, 내가 어떻게 그들을 보러 갈 수 있겠어?"], ["Can I help showing that I know what she told me?", "그녀가 내게 한 말을 내가 알고 있다는 걸 드러내지 않을 수 있겠어?"], ["And me to go magnanimously to forgive her, and have pity on her!", "그러고선 내가 너그럽게 그녀를 용서하고 가엾게 여기러 간다고!"], ["Me go through a performance before her of forgiving, and deigning to bestow my love on her!...", "그녀 앞에서 용서하는 연기를 하고, 황송하게도 내 사랑을 베풀어 준다고!……"], ["What induced Darya Alexandrovna to tell me that?", "대체 무엇 때문에 다리야 알렉산드로브나는 그런 말을 내게 했을까?"], ["By chance I might have seen her, then everything would have happened of itself; but, as it is, it’s out of the question, out of the question!”", "우연히라면 내가 그녀를 만났을지도 모르고, 그랬다면 모든 것이 저절로 일어났을 텐데. 하지만 이렇게 된 이상, 그건 말도 안 돼, 말도 안 되는 일이야!\""]], [["Darya Alexandrovna sent him a letter, asking him for a side-saddle for Kitty’s use.", "다리야 알렉산드로브나가 그에게 편지를 보내, 키티가 쓸 부인용 안장을 청했다."], ["“I’m told you have a side-saddle,”", "\"당신에게 부인용 안장이 있다고 들었어요.\""], ["she wrote to him; “I hope you will bring it over yourself.”", "그녀는 그에게 이렇게 썼다. \"당신이 직접 그것을 가져다주기를 바라요.\""]], [["This was more than he could stand.", "이것은 그가 견딜 수 있는 것 이상이었다."], ["How could a woman of any intelligence, of any delicacy, put her sister in such a humiliating position!", "분별 있고 섬세한 여자라면 어떻게 자기 여동생을 이토록 굴욕적인 처지에 몰아넣을 수 있단 말인가!"], ["He wrote ten notes, and tore them all up, and sent the saddle without any reply.", "그는 짧은 편지 열 통을 썼다가 모조리 찢어 버리고, 아무 답장 없이 안장만 보냈다. 가겠다고 쓰는 것은 불가능했으니, 갈 수가 없었기 때문이다."], ["To write that he would go was impossible, because he could not go; to write that he could not come because something prevented him, or that he would be away, that was still worse.", "무슨 일이 생겨서 못 간다든가, 멀리 가 있겠다고 쓰는 것은 더더욱 나빴다. 그는 답장 없이 안장만 보냈고, 무언가 부끄러운 짓을 했다는 느낌이 들었다."], ["He sent the saddle without an answer, and with a sense of having done something shameful; he handed over all the now revolting business of the estate to the bailiff, and set off next day to a remote district to see his friend Sviazhsky, who had splendid marshes for grouse in his neighborhood, and had lately written to ask him to keep a long-standing promise to stay with him.", "그는 이제 역겨워진 영지의 모든 일을 집사에게 넘기고, 이튿날 멀리 떨어진 군으로 친구 스비야시스키를 만나러 떠났다. 그의 이웃에는 멧닭이 가득한 훌륭한 늪지가 있었고, 그는 최근에 오래전부터 해 둔 약속대로 자기 집에 와서 묵으라는 편지를 레빈에게 보내왔던 것이다."], ["The grouse-marsh, in the Surovsky district, had long tempted Levin, but he had continually put off this visit on account of his work on the estate.", "수로프스키 군에 있는 그 멧닭 늪지는 오래전부터 레빈을 유혹해 왔지만, 그는 영지 일 때문에 이 방문을 계속 미뤄 왔다."], ["Now he was glad to get away from the neighborhood of the Shtcherbatskys, and still more from his farm work, especially on a shooting expedition, which always in trouble served as the best consolation.", "이제 그는 셰르바츠키 집안과의 이웃 관계로부터, 그리고 더더욱 자기 농장 일로부터 벗어나게 된 것이 기뻤다. 특히 사냥 나들이라면 더욱 그러했으니, 그것은 곤경에 처할 때마다 늘 최고의 위안이 되어 주었다."]], [["# Chapter 25", "# Chapter 25"]], [["In the Surovsky district there was no railway nor service of post horses, and Levin drove there with his own horses in his big, old-fashioned carriage.", "수롭스키 군에는 철도도 역마차도 없어서, 레빈은 자기 말들을 매단 크고 구식인 마차를 타고 그곳으로 향했다."]], [["He stopped halfway at a well-to-do peasant’s to feed his horses.", "그는 가는 길의 절반쯤 되는 곳에서 부유한 농부의 집에 들러 말들에게 먹이를 주었다."], ["A bald, well-preserved old man, with a broad, red beard, gray on his cheeks, opened the gate, squeezing against the gatepost to let the three horses pass.", "대머리에 정정한 노인이, 뺨에는 희끗한 기운이 도는 넓고 붉은 수염을 기른 채 대문을 열고는, 세 마리 말이 지나가도록 문기둥에 몸을 바싹 붙였다."], ["Directing the coachman to a place under the shed in the big, clean, tidy yard, with charred, old-fashioned ploughs in it, the old man asked Levin to come into the parlor.", "노인은 마부에게 헛간 아래 자리를 일러 주었는데, 크고 깨끗하고 정돈된 마당에는 그을린 구식 쟁기들이 놓여 있었고, 노인은 레빈에게 거실로 들어오라고 청했다."], ["A cleanly dressed young woman, with clogs on her bare feet, was scrubbing the floor in the new outer room.", "단정하게 차려입은 젊은 여인이 맨발에 나막신을 신고 새로 들인 바깥방 바닥을 닦고 있었다."], ["She was frightened of the dog, that ran in after Levin, and uttered a shriek, but began laughing at her own fright at once when she was told the dog would not hurt her.", "그녀는 레빈을 뒤따라 뛰어든 개를 보고 겁을 먹어 비명을 질렀지만, 개가 해치지 않을 거라는 말을 듣자 곧 자기가 놀란 것이 우스워 웃기 시작했다."], ["Pointing Levin with her bare arm to the door into the parlor, she bent down again, hiding her handsome face, and went on scrubbing.", "그녀는 맨팔로 레빈에게 거실로 들어가는 문을 가리키고는, 다시 몸을 숙여 잘생긴 얼굴을 가린 채 바닥을 계속 닦았다."]], [["“Would you like the samovar?”", "\"사모바르를 올릴까요?\""], ["she asked.", "그녀가 물었다."]], [["“Yes, please.”", "\"네, 부탁합니다.\""]], [["The parlor was a big room, with a Dutch stove, and a screen dividing it into two.", "거실은 큰 방이었는데, 네덜란드식 난로가 있었고 칸막이 하나가 방을 둘로 나누고 있었다."], ["Under the holy pictures stood a table painted in patterns, a bench, and two chairs.", "성화 아래에는 무늬가 그려진 탁자와 긴 의자, 의자 두 개가 놓여 있었다."], ["Near the entrance was a dresser full of crockery.", "입구 가까이에는 그릇이 가득한 찬장이 있었다."], ["The shutters were closed, there were few flies, and it was so clean that Levin was anxious that Laska, who had been running along the road and bathing in puddles, should not muddy the floor, and ordered her to a place in the corner by the door.", "덧창이 닫혀 있어 파리가 거의 없었고, 어찌나 깨끗한지 레빈은 길을 달려오며 웅덩이에서 멱을 감은 라스카가 바닥을 더럽힐까 봐 걱정되어, 문가 구석 자리로 가 있으라고 명했다."], ["After looking round the parlor, Levin went out in the back yard.", "거실을 둘러본 뒤 레빈은 뒷마당으로 나갔다."], ["The good-looking young woman in clogs, swinging the empty pails on the yoke, ran on before him to the well for water.", "나막신을 신은 그 잘생긴 젊은 여인이 멜대에 빈 물통을 흔들흔들 매달고 그보다 앞서 물을 길으러 우물로 달려갔다."]], [["“Look sharp, my girl!”", "\"서둘러라, 얘야!\""], ["the old man shouted after her, good-humoredly, and he went up to Levin.", "노인이 그녀의 뒤에 대고 기분 좋게 소리치고는 레빈에게 다가왔다."], ["“Well, sir, are you going to Nikolay Ivanovitch Sviazhsky?", "\"그런데 나리, 니콜라이 이바노비치 스비야시스키 댁으로 가시는 길입니까?"], ["His honor comes to us too,”", "그분도 우리 집에 들르신답니다.\""], ["he began, chatting, leaning his elbows on the railing of the steps.", "그는 계단 난간에 팔꿈치를 괴고 이야기를 늘어놓기 시작했다."], ["In the middle of the old man’s account of his acquaintance with Sviazhsky, the gates creaked again, and laborers came into the yard from the fields, with wooden ploughs and harrows.", "노인이 스비야시스키와 알고 지낸 사연을 한창 이야기하던 중에 대문이 다시 삐걱거리더니, 일꾼들이 나무 쟁기와 써레를 들고 들에서 마당으로 들어왔다."], ["The horses harnessed to the ploughs and harrows were sleek and fat.", "쟁기와 써레에 매인 말들은 윤기가 흐르고 살이 올라 있었다."], ["The laborers were obviously of the household: two were young men in cotton shirts and caps, the two others were hired laborers in homespun shirts, one an old man, the other a young fellow.", "일꾼들은 한눈에 봐도 그 집 식구들이었다. 둘은 무명 셔츠를 입고 모자를 쓴 젊은이였고, 다른 둘은 손으로 짠 셔츠를 입은 고용 일꾼으로, 하나는 노인이고 다른 하나는 젊은 사내였다."], ["Moving off from the steps, the old man went up to the horses and began unharnessing them.", "노인은 계단에서 물러나 말들에게 다가가 마구를 벗기기 시작했다."]], [["“What have they been ploughing?”", "\"무얼 갈았소?\""], ["asked Levin.", "레빈이 물었다."]], [["“Ploughing up the potatoes.", "\"감자밭을 갈았습니다."], ["We rent a bit of land too.", "우리도 땅을 좀 빌려 부치고 있지요."], ["Fedot, don’t let out the gelding, but take it to the trough, and we’ll put the other in harness.”", "페도트, 거세마는 풀어놓지 말고 여물통으로 끌고 가거라, 다른 놈을 마구에 맬 테니.\""]], [["“Oh, father, the ploughshares I ordered, has he brought them along?”", "\"아버지, 제가 주문한 보습 말이에요, 그자가 가져왔나요?\""], ["asked the big, healthy-looking fellow, obviously the old man’s son.", "한눈에 노인의 아들인 게 분명한 덩치 좋고 건강해 보이는 사내가 물었다."]], [["“There ...", "\"저기……"], ["in the outer room,”", "바깥방에 있다.\""], ["answered the old man, bundling together the harness he had taken off, and flinging it on the ground.", "노인은 벗겨 낸 마구를 한데 뭉쳐 땅바닥에 내던지며 대답했다."], ["“You can put them on, while they have dinner.”", "\"저 사람들 밥 먹는 동안 네가 끼워 놓으려무나.\""]], [["The good-looking young woman came into the outer room with the full pails dragging at her shoulders.", "잘생긴 젊은 여인이 가득 찬 물통을 어깨에 늘어뜨린 채 바깥방으로 들어왔다."], ["More women came on the scene from somewhere, young and handsome, middle-aged, old and ugly, with children and without children.", "어디선가 여인들이 더 나타났는데, 젊고 잘생긴 여자, 중년 여자, 늙고 못생긴 여자, 아이를 데린 여자, 아이 없는 여자가 뒤섞여 있었다."]], [["The samovar was beginning to sing; the laborers and the family, having disposed of the horses, came in to dinner.", "사모바르가 노래하기 시작했다. 말들을 처리한 일꾼들과 식구들이 밥을 먹으러 들어왔다."], ["Levin, getting his provisions out of his carriage, invited the old man to take tea with him.", "레빈은 마차에서 먹을거리를 꺼내 와서는 노인에게 함께 차를 들자고 청했다."]], [["“Well, I have had some today already,”", "\"글쎄요, 오늘은 벌써 좀 마셨습니다만.\""], ["said the old man, obviously accepting the invitation with pleasure.", "노인은 한눈에도 기뻐하며 그 청을 받아들였다."], ["“But just a glass for company.”", "\"그래도 어울리는 셈치고 한 잔만 하지요.\""]], [["Over their tea Levin heard all about the old man’s farming.", "차를 마시는 동안 레빈은 노인의 농사에 관한 이야기를 죄다 들었다."], ["Ten years before, the old man had rented three hundred acres from the lady who owned them, and a year ago he had bought them and rented another three hundred from a neighboring landowner.", "십 년 전에 노인은 땅을 소유한 부인에게서 삼백 에이커를 빌렸고, 일 년 전에는 그 땅을 사들인 데다 이웃 지주에게서 또 삼백 에이커를 빌렸다."], ["A small part of the land—the worst part—he let out for rent, while a hundred acres of arable land he cultivated himself with his family and two hired laborers.", "그 땅의 일부, 그러니까 가장 나쁜 부분은 세를 놓고, 백 에이커의 경작지는 가족과 고용 일꾼 둘과 함께 손수 일구었다."], ["The old man complained that things were doing badly.", "노인은 형편이 나쁘다고 푸념했다."], ["But Levin saw that he simply did so from a feeling of propriety, and that his farm was in a flourishing condition.", "하지만 레빈이 보기에 그것은 그저 예의상 하는 말일 뿐, 그의 농장은 번창하고 있었다."], ["If it had been unsuccessful he would not have bought land at thirty-five roubles the acre, he would not have married his three sons and a nephew, he would not have rebuilt twice after fires, and each time on a larger scale.", "만약 농사가 잘 안 됐다면 에이커당 삼십오 루블에 땅을 사들이지도, 세 아들과 조카를 장가들이지도, 불이 난 뒤 두 번이나, 그것도 매번 더 큰 규모로 집을 다시 짓지도 않았을 것이다."], ["In spite of the old man’s complaints, it was evident that he was proud, and justly proud, of his prosperity, proud of his sons, his nephew, his sons’ wives, his horses and his cows, and especially of the fact that he was keeping all this farming going.", "노인은 푸념을 늘어놓았지만, 그가 자기의 번영을 자랑스러워하며, 그것도 정당하게 자랑스러워하고 있다는 것은 분명했다. 그는 아들들과 조카, 며느리들, 자기 말들과 소들을 자랑스러워했고, 무엇보다 이 모든 농사를 두루 꾸려 가고 있다는 사실을 자랑스러워했다."], ["From his conversation with the old man, Levin thought he was not averse to new methods either.", "노인과 이야기를 나누면서 레빈은 그가 새로운 방식에도 거부감이 없다는 것을 알았다."], ["He had planted a great many potatoes, and his potatoes, as Levin had seen driving past, were already past flowering and beginning to die down, while Levin’s were only just coming into flower.", "그는 감자를 아주 많이 심었는데, 레빈이 지나오며 보았듯이 그의 감자는 이미 꽃이 지고 시들기 시작한 반면, 레빈의 감자는 이제 막 꽃이 피고 있었다."], ["He earthed up his potatoes with a modern plough borrowed from a neighboring landowner.", "그는 이웃 지주에게서 빌린 신식 쟁기로 감자에 흙을 북돋워 주었다."], ["He sowed wheat.", "그는 밀을 뿌렸다."], ["The trifling fact that, thinning out his rye, the old man used the rye he thinned out for his horses, specially struck Levin.", "노인이 호밀을 솎으면서 솎아 낸 호밀을 말 먹이로 쓴다는 사소한 사실이 특히 레빈의 마음을 쳤다."], ["How many times had Levin seen this splendid fodder wasted, and tried to get it saved; but always it had turned out to be impossible.", "레빈은 이 훌륭한 사료가 버려지는 것을 여러 차례 보았고, 그것을 거두려고 애써 봤지만, 번번이 불가능한 것으로 드러났었다."], ["The peasant got this done, and he could not say enough in praise of it as food for the beasts.", "그런데 이 농부는 그것을 해내고 있었고, 그것을 가축 먹이로 칭찬하는 데 입에 침이 마를 줄 몰랐다."]], [["“What have the wenches to do?", "\"계집애들이 무얼 하겠습니까?"], ["They carry it out in bundles to the roadside, and the cart brings it away.”", "그것을 다발로 묶어 길가로 날라 오면, 수레가 실어 오지요.\""]], [["“Well, we landowners can’t manage well with our laborers,”", "\"그런데 우리 지주들은 일꾼들을 데리고 일이 잘 안 풀리는군요.\""], ["said Levin, handing him a glass of tea.", "레빈이 그에게 차 한 잔을 건네며 말했다."]], [["“Thank you,”", "\"고맙습니다.\""], ["said the old man, and he took the glass, but refused sugar, pointing to a lump he had left.", "노인은 잔을 받았지만, 남겨 둔 설탕 덩어리를 가리키며 더는 사양했다."], ["“They’re simple destruction,”", "\"그 작자들은 그저 폐물이지요.\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["“Look at Sviazhsky’s, for instance.", "\"이를테면 스비야시스키 댁을 보십시오."], ["We know what the land’s like—first-rate, yet there’s not much of a crop to boast of.", "그 땅이 어떤 땅인지 우리야 알지요. 일등급인데도 자랑할 만한 수확이 별로 없습니다."], ["It’s not looked after enough—that’s all it is!”", "충분히 돌보질 않아서지요, 그게 다입니다!\""]], [["“But you work your land with hired laborers?”", "\"하지만 노인장도 고용 일꾼들을 데리고 땅을 일구지 않습니까?\""]], [["“We’re all peasants together.", "\"우리야 다 같이 농사꾼 아닙니까."], ["We go into everything ourselves.", "무슨 일이든 직접 나섭니다."], ["If a man’s no use, he can go, and we can manage by ourselves.”", "쓸모없는 사람은 가면 그만이고, 우리끼리 꾸려 갈 수 있지요.\""]], [["“Father, Finogen wants some tar,”", "\"아버지, 피노겐이 타르를 좀 달래요.\""], ["said the young woman in the clogs, coming in.", "나막신을 신은 젊은 여인이 들어오며 말했다."]], [["“Yes, yes, that’s how it is, sir!”", "\"그래, 그래, 그렇다니까요, 나리!\""], ["said the old man, getting up, and crossing himself deliberately, he thanked Levin and went out.", "노인은 일어나 천천히 성호를 그은 뒤, 레빈에게 고맙다는 인사를 하고 나갔다."]], [["When Levin went into the kitchen to call his coachman he saw the whole family at dinner.", "레빈이 마부를 부르러 부엌으로 들어가 보니 온 식구가 식사를 하고 있었다."], ["The women were standing up waiting on them.", "여인들은 선 채로 그들의 시중을 들고 있었다."], ["The young, sturdy-looking son was telling something funny with his mouth full of pudding, and they were all laughing, the woman in the clogs, who was pouring cabbage soup into a bowl, laughing most merrily of all.", "젊고 다부진 아들이 푸딩으로 입을 가득 채운 채 무언가 우스운 이야기를 하고 있었고, 모두 웃고 있었는데, 양배추 수프를 그릇에 따르고 있던 나막신을 신은 여인이 가장 즐겁게 웃고 있었다."]], [["Very probably the good-looking face of the young woman in the clogs had a good deal to do with the impression of well-being this peasant household made upon Levin, but the impression was so strong that Levin could never get rid of it.", "이 농부의 집이 레빈에게 안겨 준 풍족함의 인상에는 나막신을 신은 그 젊은 여인의 잘생긴 얼굴이 적잖이 작용했을 테지만, 그 인상이 어찌나 강렬했던지 레빈은 도무지 그것을 떨쳐 낼 수가 없었다."], ["And all the way from the old peasant’s to Sviazhsky’s he kept recalling this peasant farm as though there were something in this impression that demanded his special attention.", "그리고 노인의 집에서 스비야시스키의 집으로 가는 내내, 마치 이 인상 속에 자기의 특별한 주의를 요구하는 무언가가 있기라도 한 것처럼, 그는 이 농가를 자꾸만 떠올렸다."]], [["# Chapter 26", "# Chapter 26"]], [["Sviazhsky was the marshal of his district.", "스비아시스키는 자기 군(郡)의 귀족 단장이었다."], ["He was five years older than Levin, and had long been married.", "레빈보다 다섯 살 위였고, 결혼한 지 오래되었다."], ["His sister-in-law, a young girl Levin liked very much, lived in his house; and Levin knew that Sviazhsky and his wife would have greatly liked to marry the girl to him.", "그의 집에는 레빈이 무척 좋아하던 젊은 처녀, 곧 그의 처제가 함께 살고 있었다. 그리고 레빈은 스비아시스키와 그 아내가 그 처녀를 자기와 맺어 주고 싶어 한다는 것을 알고 있었다."], ["He knew this with certainty, as so-called eligible young men always know it, though he could never have brought himself to speak of it to anyone; and he knew too that, although he wanted to get married, and although by every token this very attractive girl would make an excellent wife, he could no more have married her, even if he had not been in love with Kitty Shtcherbatskaya, than he could have flown up to the sky.", "이른바 결혼 적령기의 청년들이 늘 그렇듯, 레빈도 그것을 확실히 알고 있었지만, 누구에게도 차마 그 이야기를 입에 올리지는 못했다. 또한 그는 결혼을 하고 싶었고, 모든 정황으로 보아 이 매력적인 처녀가 훌륭한 아내가 되리라는 것도 알았지만, 설령 자기가 키티 셰르바츠카야를 사랑하지 않았더라도 하늘로 날아오르는 것이 불가능한 만큼이나 그 처녀와 결혼하는 일은 불가능하리라는 것 또한 알고 있었다."], ["And this knowledge poisoned the pleasure he had hoped to find in the visit to Sviazhsky.", "그리고 이런 인식이 스비아시스키 집을 방문하면서 기대했던 즐거움에 독을 풀어 놓았다."]], [["On getting Sviazhsky’s letter with the invitation for shooting, Levin had immediately thought of this; but in spite of it he had made up his mind that Sviazhsky’s having such views for him was simply his own groundless supposition, and so he would go, all the same.", "사냥 초대가 담긴 스비아시스키의 편지를 받자마자 레빈은 곧바로 이 점을 떠올렸다. 그러나 그럼에도 그는 스비아시스키가 자기에 대해 그런 생각을 품고 있다는 것이 자신의 근거 없는 억측일 뿐이라고 마음을 고쳐먹고는, 그래도 가기로 했다."], ["Besides, at the bottom of his heart he had a desire to try himself, put himself to the test in regard to this girl.", "게다가 마음 깊은 곳에는 그 처녀에 대해 자기 자신을 시험해 보고, 스스로를 점검해 보고 싶은 욕망이 있었다."], ["The Sviazhskys’ home-life was exceedingly pleasant, and Sviazhsky himself, the best type of man taking part in local affairs that Levin knew, was very interesting to him.", "스비아시스키 집안의 가정생활은 더없이 유쾌했고, 레빈이 아는 한 지방 자치 활동에 참여하는 사람들 중 가장 훌륭한 본보기인 스비아시스키 자신도 그에게는 무척 흥미로운 인물이었다."]], [["Sviazhsky was one of those people, always a source of wonder to Levin, whose convictions, very logical though never original, go one way by themselves, while their life, exceedingly definite and firm in its direction, goes its way quite apart and almost always in direct contradiction to their convictions.", "스비아시스키는 레빈에게 늘 놀라움의 원천이 되는 부류의 사람 중 하나였다. 그들의 신념은 매우 논리적이지만 결코 독창적이지는 않으면서 제 갈 길을 따로 가고, 그들의 삶은 방향이 더없이 분명하고 확고하면서도 그 신념과는 완전히 동떨어져, 거의 언제나 정반대로 흘러간다."], ["Sviazhsky was an extremely advanced man.", "스비아시스키는 지극히 진보적인 사람이었다."], ["He despised the nobility, and believed the mass of the nobility to be secretly in favor of serfdom, and only concealing their views from cowardice.", "그는 귀족을 경멸했고, 귀족 대다수가 속으로는 농노제를 지지하면서 비겁한 탓에 자기 생각을 감추고 있을 뿐이라고 믿었다."], ["He regarded Russia as a ruined country, rather after the style of Turkey, and the government of Russia as so bad that he never permitted himself to criticize its doings seriously, and yet he was a functionary of that government and a model marshal of nobility, and when he drove about he always wore the cockade of office and the cap with the red band.", "그는 러시아를, 차라리 터키에 가까운 망해 버린 나라로 여겼고, 러시아 정부를 어찌나 형편없게 여겼던지 그 정부가 하는 일을 진지하게 비판하는 것조차 스스로에게 결코 허락하지 않았다. 그러면서도 그는 바로 그 정부의 관리였고, 모범적인 귀족 단장이었으며, 마차를 타고 다닐 때면 언제나 관직을 나타내는 모표를 달고 붉은 띠가 둘린 모자를 썼다."], ["He considered human life only tolerable abroad, and went abroad to stay at every opportunity, and at the same time he carried on a complex and improved system of agriculture in Russia, and with extreme interest followed everything and knew everything that was being done in Russia.", "그는 인간의 삶이 외국에서나 그럭저럭 견딜 만하다고 여겼고, 기회만 있으면 외국에 나가 머물렀다. 그러면서도 동시에 러시아에서 복잡하고 개량된 농법을 시행했고, 러시아에서 이루어지는 모든 일을 극진한 관심으로 좇으며 죄다 알고 있었다."], ["He considered the Russian peasant as occupying a stage of development intermediate between the ape and the man, and at the same time in the local assemblies no one was readier to shake hands with the peasants and listen to their opinion.", "그는 러시아 농민이 유인원과 인간 사이의 중간 발달 단계에 머물러 있다고 여겼지만, 동시에 지방 의회에서는 그 누구보다 기꺼이 농민과 악수를 나누고 그들의 의견에 귀를 기울였다."], ["He believed neither in God nor the devil, but was much concerned about the question of the improvement of the clergy and the maintenance of their revenues, and took special trouble to keep up the church in his village.", "그는 신도 악마도 믿지 않았지만, 성직자들의 처우 개선과 그들의 수입 유지 문제에는 큰 관심을 쏟았고, 자기 마을의 교회를 유지하기 위해 각별히 애를 썼다."]], [["On the woman question he was on the side of the extreme advocates of complete liberty for women, and especially their right to labor.", "여성 문제에 관해서는 여성의 완전한 자유, 특히 노동의 권리를 주장하는 극단적 옹호자들 편에 섰다."], ["But he lived with his wife on such terms that their affectionate childless home life was the admiration of everyone, and arranged his wife’s life so that she did nothing and could do nothing but share her husband’s efforts that her time should pass as happily and as agreeably as possible.", "그러면서도 그는 아내와, 자식 없이도 다정한 가정생활로 모두의 부러움을 살 만한 관계를 유지하며 살았고, 아내가 아무 일도 하지 않으면서, 그저 자기 시간을 최대한 행복하고 유쾌하게 보내려는 남편의 노력을 함께 나누는 것 말고는 아무것도 할 수 없도록 아내의 삶을 꾸려 놓았다."]], [["If it had not been a characteristic of Levin’s to put the most favorable interpretation on people, Sviazhsky’s character would have presented no doubt or difficulty to him: he would have said to himself, “a fool or a knave,”", "사람들을 가장 호의적으로 해석하는 것이 레빈의 천성이 아니었더라면, 스비아시스키의 성격이 그에게 아무런 의문이나 곤혹스러움도 안기지 않았을 것이다. \"바보거나 악당이거나\""], ["and everything would have seemed clear. But he could not say “a fool,”", "하고 스스로 단정해 버렸을 테고, 그러면 모든 것이 분명해 보였을 것이다."], ["because Sviazhsky was unmistakably clever, and moreover, a highly cultivated man, who was exceptionally modest over his culture.", "그러나 그는 \"바보\"라고 말할 수 없었으니, 스비아시스키는 의심할 여지 없이 영리한 데다, 더욱이 자기 교양을 유난히 겸손하게 다루는 고도로 교양 있는 사람이었기 때문이다."], ["There was not a subject he knew nothing of.", "그가 전혀 모르는 분야란 없었다."], ["But he did not display his knowledge except when he was compelled to do so.", "그러나 그는 어쩔 수 없이 그래야 할 때를 빼고는 자기 지식을 드러내지 않았다."], ["Still less could Levin say that he was a knave, as Sviazhsky was unmistakably an honest, good-hearted, sensible man, who worked good-humoredly, keenly, and perseveringly at his work; he was held in high honor by everyone about him, and certainly he had never consciously done, and was indeed incapable of doing, anything base.", "\"악당\"이라고 말하는 것은 더더욱 불가능했으니, 스비아시스키는 의심할 여지 없이 정직하고 마음씨 착하며 분별 있는 사람으로, 자기 일을 쾌활하고 열정적이며 끈기 있게 해 나갔기 때문이다. 그는 주위의 모든 사람에게서 높은 존경을 받았고, 분명 한 번도 의식적으로 비열한 짓을 저지른 적이 없었으며, 실로 그런 짓을 할 줄 모르는 사람이었다."]], [["Levin tried to understand him, and could not understand him, and looked at him and his life as at a living enigma.", "레빈은 그를 이해하려 애썼으나 끝내 이해할 수 없었고, 그와 그의 삶을 마치 살아 있는 수수께끼처럼 바라보았다."]], [["Levin and he were very friendly, and so Levin used to venture to sound Sviazhsky, to try to get at the very foundation of his view of life; but it was always in vain.", "레빈과 그는 매우 친한 사이였으므로, 레빈은 곧잘 스비아시스키의 속을 떠보아 그의 인생관의 가장 밑바닥에 다가가려고 시도하곤 했다. 그러나 그것은 언제나 헛수고였다."], ["Every time Levin tried to penetrate beyond the outer chambers of Sviazhsky’s mind, which were hospitably open to all, he noticed that Sviazhsky was slightly disconcerted; faint signs of alarm were visible in his eyes, as though he were afraid Levin would understand him, and he would give him a kindly, good-humored repulse.", "모두에게 친절히 열려 있는 스비아시스키 정신의 바깥 방들을 넘어 그 안으로 파고들려고 할 때마다, 레빈은 스비아시스키가 살짝 당황하는 것을 알아챘다. 마치 레빈이 자기를 이해해 버릴까 봐 두려워하기라도 하듯, 그의 눈에는 희미한 경계의 빛이 어렸고, 그러면 그는 다정하고 쾌활하게 그를 밀어냈다."]], [["Just now, since his disenchantment with farming, Levin was particularly glad to stay with Sviazhsky.", "마침 이즈음, 농사에 환멸을 느낀 터라 레빈은 스비아시스키 집에 머무는 것이 유난히 반가웠다."], ["Apart from the fact that the sight of this happy and affectionate couple, so pleased with themselves and everyone else, and their well-ordered home had always a cheering effect on Levin, he felt a longing, now that he was so dissatisfied with his own life, to get at that secret in Sviazhsky that gave him such clearness, definiteness, and good courage in life.", "자기들이며 다른 모든 사람에게 그토록 만족하는 이 행복하고 다정한 부부와 그 잘 정돈된 가정의 모습이 언제나 레빈의 마음을 밝게 해 주었다는 사실은 차치하더라도, 그는 자기 삶에 그토록 불만스러운 지금, 스비아시스키에게 인생에서 그토록 명료함과 확고함과 굳센 용기를 안겨 주는 그 비밀에 다가가고 싶은 갈망을 느꼈다."], ["Moreover, Levin knew that at Sviazhsky’s he should meet the landowners of the neighborhood, and it was particularly interesting for him just now to hear and take part in those rural conversations concerning crops, laborers’ wages, and so on, which, he was aware, are conventionally regarded as something very low, but which seemed to him just now to constitute the one subject of importance.", "더욱이 레빈은 스비아시스키의 집에서 인근의 지주들을 만나게 되리라는 것을 알고 있었고, 마침 지금은 작물이며 일꾼들의 품삯 따위에 관한 그 시골식 대화를 듣고 거기에 끼어드는 것이 그에게는 각별히 흥미로웠다. 그런 대화가 관습적으로 무언가 아주 천한 것으로 여겨진다는 것을 그도 알고 있었지만, 지금 그에게는 바로 그것이야말로 유일하게 중요한 주제로 여겨졌다."], ["“It was not, perhaps, of importance in the days of serfdom, and it may not be of importance in England.", "'농노제 시절에는 어쩌면 그것이 중요하지 않았는지도 모르고, 영국에서도 중요하지 않을지 모르지."], ["In both cases the conditions of agriculture are firmly established; but among us now, when everything has been turned upside down and is only just taking shape, the question what form these conditions will take is the one question of importance in Russia,”", "두 경우 모두 농업의 조건이 확고하게 자리 잡혀 있으니까. 하지만 모든 것이 뒤집어지고 이제 막 형태를 갖추어 가는 우리에게는, 이 조건들이 어떤 형태를 띠게 될 것인가 하는 문제야말로 러시아에서 유일하게 중요한 문제야.'"], ["thought Levin.", "레빈은 생각했다."]], [["The shooting turned out to be worse than Levin had expected.", "사냥은 레빈이 기대했던 것보다 형편없었다."], ["The marsh was dry and there were no grouse at all.", "늪은 메말랐고, 멧닭은 한 마리도 없었다."], ["He walked about the whole day and only brought back three birds, but to make up for that—he brought back, as he always did from shooting, an excellent appetite, excellent spirits, and that keen, intellectual mood which with him always accompanied violent physical exertion.", "그는 하루 종일 돌아다니고도 새 세 마리밖에 잡지 못했지만, 그 대신 사냥에서 늘 그러했듯이 왕성한 식욕과 더없이 좋은 기분, 그리고 격렬한 육체 활동에 언제나 따라오던 그 예리하고 지적인 상태를 안고 돌아왔다."], ["And while out shooting, when he seemed to be thinking of nothing at all, suddenly the old man and his family kept coming back to his mind, and the impression of them seemed to claim not merely his attention, but the solution of some question connected with them.", "그리고 사냥을 하는 동안, 아무 생각도 하지 않는 것 같다가도 별안간 그 노인과 그 가족이 자꾸만 머릿속에 떠올랐고, 그들에 대한 인상은 단지 그의 주의뿐만 아니라 그들과 얽힌 어떤 문제의 해답까지 요구하는 듯했다."]], [["In the evening at tea, two landowners who had come about some business connected with a wardship were of the party, and the interesting conversation Levin had been looking forward to sprang up.", "저녁 차 시간에는 후견 문제로 찾아온 두 지주가 자리를 함께했고, 레빈이 고대하던 흥미로운 대화가 시작되었다."]], [["Levin was sitting beside his hostess at the tea table, and was obliged to keep up a conversation with her and her sister, who was sitting opposite him.", "레빈은 차 탁자 옆 안주인 곁에 앉아, 그녀와 맞은편에 앉은 그녀의 동생과 대화를 이어 가야 했다."], ["Madame Sviazhskaya was a round-faced, fair-haired, rather short woman, all smiles and dimples.", "스비아시스카야 부인은 둥근 얼굴에 금발의, 키가 다소 작은 여자로, 온통 미소와 보조개로 가득했다."], ["Levin tried through her to get a solution of the weighty enigma her husband presented to his mind; but he had not complete freedom of ideas, because he was in an agony of embarrassment.", "레빈은 그녀를 통해 그녀의 남편이 자기 마음에 던진 묵직한 수수께끼의 해답을 얻으려 했다. 그러나 그는 생각을 마음껏 펼칠 수가 없었으니, 당혹감으로 괴로워하고 있었기 때문이다."], ["This agony of embarrassment was due to the fact that the sister-in-law was sitting opposite to him, in a dress, specially put on, as he fancied, for his benefit, cut particularly open, in the shape of a trapeze, on her white bosom.", "이 당혹스러운 괴로움은, 그의 짐작으로는 그를 위해 일부러 차려입은 듯한 드레스를 입은 처제가 맞은편에 앉아 있다는 사실 때문이었다. 그 드레스는 그녀의 하얀 가슴께가 사다리꼴 모양으로 유난히 깊게 파여 있었다."], ["This quadrangular opening, in spite of the bosom’s being very white, or just because it was very white, deprived Levin of the full use of his faculties.", "가슴이 매우 희었음에도, 아니 오히려 매우 흰 탓에, 이 사각형으로 파인 부분은 레빈에게서 능력을 온전히 발휘할 여유를 앗아 가 버렸다."], ["He imagined, probably mistakenly, that this low-necked bodice had been made on his account, and felt that he had no right to look at it, and tried not to look at it; but he felt that he was to blame for the very fact of the low-necked bodice having been made.", "그는 아마도 잘못된 짐작이겠지만, 이 깊게 파인 상의가 자기 때문에 만들어진 것이라고 상상했고, 자기에게는 그것을 바라볼 권리가 없다고 느껴 보지 않으려 애썼지만, 그 깊게 파인 상의가 만들어진 그 사실 자체가 자기 탓인 것처럼 느꼈다."], ["It seemed to Levin that he had deceived someone, that he ought to explain something, but that to explain it was impossible, and for that reason he was continually blushing, was ill at ease and awkward.", "레빈에게는 자기가 누군가를 속인 것 같았고, 무언가를 해명해야 할 것 같았지만, 그것을 해명하기란 불가능했으며, 그런 까닭에 그는 끊임없이 얼굴을 붉히며 거북하고 어색해했다."], ["His awkwardness infected the pretty sister-in-law too.", "그의 어색함은 그 어여쁜 처제에게도 옮아갔다."], ["But their hostess appeared not to observe this, and kept purposely drawing her into the conversation.", "그러나 안주인은 이를 알아채지 못한 듯, 일부러 자꾸만 동생을 대화 속으로 끌어들였다."]], [["“You say,”", "\"당신은,\""], ["she said, pursuing the subject that had been started, “that my husband cannot be interested in what’s Russian.", "그녀가 앞서 꺼낸 화제를 이어 가며 말했다, \"제 남편이 러시아적인 것에 관심을 가질 수 없다고 하시는군요."], ["It’s quite the contrary; he is always in cheerful spirits abroad, but not as he is here.", "정반대예요. 그이는 외국에서는 언제나 기분이 들떠 있지만, 여기서 같지는 않아요."], ["Here, he feels in his proper place.", "여기서야말로 자기 자리에 있다고 느끼는걸요."], ["He has so much to do, and he has the faculty of interesting himself in everything.", "할 일도 아주 많고, 무엇에든 흥미를 느끼는 재주가 있답니다."], ["Oh, you’ve not been to see our school, have you?”", "아, 우리 학교는 아직 못 보셨죠?\""]], [["“I’ve seen it....", "\"봤습니다……"], ["The little house covered with ivy, isn’t it?”", "담쟁이덩굴로 뒤덮인 그 작은 집, 맞죠?\""]], [["“Yes; that’s Nastia’s work,”", "\"네, 그건 나스티야가 한 일이에요.\""], ["she said, indicating her sister.", "그녀가 동생을 가리키며 말했다."]], [["“You teach in it yourself?”", "\"직접 그 학교에서 가르치십니까?\""], ["asked Levin, trying to look above the open neck, but feeling that wherever he looked in that direction he should see it.", "레빈이 그 파인 목께보다 위쪽을 보려 애쓰면서 물었지만, 그쪽으로 시선을 두면 어디를 보든 그것이 보일 것만 같았다."]], [["“Yes; I used to teach in it myself, and do teach still, but we have a first-rate schoolmistress now.", "\"네, 예전에는 제가 직접 가르쳤고 지금도 가르치지만, 이제는 아주 훌륭한 여선생님이 한 분 계세요."], ["And we’ve started gymnastic exercises.”", "그리고 체조 수업도 시작했답니다.\""]], [["“No, thank you, I won’t have any more tea,”", "\"아니요, 고맙습니다, 차는 이제 됐습니다.\""], ["said Levin, and conscious of doing a rude thing, but incapable of continuing the conversation, he got up, blushing.", "레빈이 말했다. 그러고는 무례한 짓을 하고 있다는 것을 의식하면서도 더는 대화를 이어 갈 수 없어, 얼굴을 붉히며 자리에서 일어났다."], ["“I hear a very interesting conversation,”", "\"저기서 아주 흥미로운 대화가 들리는군요.\""], ["he added, and walked to the other end of the table, where Sviazhsky was sitting with the two gentlemen of the neighborhood.", "그는 이렇게 덧붙이고는, 스비아시스키가 인근의 두 신사와 함께 앉아 있는 탁자 반대편 끝으로 걸어갔다."], ["Sviazhsky was sitting sideways, with one elbow on the table, and a cup in one hand, while with the other hand he gathered up his beard, held it to his nose and let it drop again, as though he were smelling it.", "스비아시스키는 비스듬히 앉아 한쪽 팔꿈치를 탁자에 괸 채 한 손에는 찻잔을 들고, 다른 손으로는 자기 수염을 그러모아 코에 갖다 대었다가 다시 놓곤 했는데, 마치 냄새를 맡는 듯한 시늉이었다."], ["His brilliant black eyes were looking straight at the excited country gentleman with gray whiskers, and apparently he derived amusement from his remarks.", "그의 반짝이는 검은 눈은 회색 구레나룻을 기른, 흥분한 시골 신사를 똑바로 바라보고 있었고, 보아하니 그 신사의 말에서 재미를 느끼는 듯했다."], ["The gentleman was complaining of the peasants.", "그 신사는 농민들에 대해 불평하고 있었다."], ["It was evident to Levin that Sviazhsky knew an answer to this gentleman’s complaints, which would at once demolish his whole contention, but that in his position he could not give utterance to this answer, and listened, not without pleasure, to the landowner’s comic speeches.", "스비아시스키가 이 신사의 불평에 대해, 그의 주장 전체를 단번에 무너뜨릴 답을 알고 있으면서도, 자기 처지로는 그 답을 입 밖에 낼 수 없어, 적잖은 즐거움을 느끼며 그 지주의 우스꽝스러운 연설에 귀를 기울이고 있다는 것이 레빈에게는 분명히 보였다."]], [["The gentleman with the gray whiskers was obviously an inveterate adherent of serfdom and a devoted agriculturist, who had lived all his life in the country.", "회색 구레나룻을 기른 그 신사는 분명 농노제의 골수 신봉자이자, 평생을 시골에서 살아온 열렬한 농사꾼이었다."], ["Levin saw proofs of this in his dress, in the old-fashioned threadbare coat, obviously not his everyday attire, in his shrewd, deep-set eyes, in his idiomatic, fluent Russian, in the imperious tone that had become habitual from long use, and in the resolute gestures of his large, red, sunburnt hands, with an old betrothal ring on the little finger.", "레빈은 그의 옷차림에서, 분명 평상복은 아닌 그 구식의 다 해진 코트에서, 영리하고 움푹한 눈에서, 거침없이 술술 흘러나오는 토속적인 러시아어에서, 오랜 세월 몸에 밴 위압적인 어조에서, 그리고 새끼손가락에 오래된 약혼반지를 낀, 크고 붉으며 햇볕에 그을린 두 손의 단호한 몸짓에서 그 증거를 보았다."]], [["# Chapter 27", "# Chapter 27"]], [["“If I’d only the heart to throw up what’s been set going ...", "\"이미 굴러가기 시작한 일을 내던질 만한 배짱만 있다면야……"], ["such a lot of trouble wasted ...", "그토록 많은 수고를 헛되이 쏟아붓고 나서……"], ["I’d turn my back on the whole business, sell up, go off like Nikolay Ivanovitch ...", "나는 이 모든 일에 등을 돌리고, 죄다 팔아 치우고, 니콜라이 이바노비치처럼 훌쩍 떠나 버릴 텐데……"], ["to hear La Belle Hélène,”", "La Belle Hélène이나 들으러 말이오.\""], ["said the landowner, a pleasant smile lighting up his shrewd old face.", "영리한 노인의 얼굴에 흐뭇한 미소가 번지며 지주가 말했다."]], [["“But you see you don’t throw it up,”", "* La Belle Hélène: 프랑스어로 '아름다운 엘렌'."], ["said Nikolay Ivanovitch Sviazhsky; “so there must be something gained.”", "오펜바흐의 오페레타."]], [["“The only gain is that I live in my own house, neither bought nor hired.", "\"하지만 그렇게 던져 버리지 않으시잖습니까.\" 니콜라이 이바노비치 스비야시스키가 말했다."], ["Besides, one keeps hoping the people will learn sense.", "\"그러니 분명 얻는 게 있는 거지요.\""], ["Though, instead of that, you’d never believe it—the drunkenness, the immorality!", "\"얻는 거라곤 사거나 빌리지 않은 내 집에서 산다는 것뿐이오. 게다가 농민들이 철이 들 거라고 줄곧 바라게 되거든."], ["They keep chopping and changing their bits of land.", "한데 그러기는커녕, 도무지 믿어지지 않을 만큼—그 술주정에다 그 타락이라니!"], ["Not a sight of a horse or a cow.", "자기들 땅뙈기를 노상 쪼개고 바꿔 대지요."], ["The peasant’s dying of hunger, but just go and take him on as a laborer, he’ll do his best to do you a mischief, and then bring you up before the justice of the peace.”", "말이나 소 한 마리 그림자도 안 보여요. 농민은 굶어 죽을 지경이면서도, 막상 일꾼으로 데려다 쓰려고 하면, 어떻게든 당신한테 해코지를 하려 들고, 그러고는 치안판사에게 당신을 고발한단 말이오.\""]], [["“But then you make complaints to the justice too,”", "\"하지만 그러면 당신도 치안판사에게 호소하면 되지요.\""], ["said Sviazhsky.", "스비야시스키가 말했다."]], [["“I lodge complaints?", "\"내가 호소를 한다고요?"], ["Not for anything in the world!", "무슨 일이 있어도 그건 안 할 거요!"], ["Such a talking, and such a to-do, that one would have cause to regret it.", "어찌나 말이 많고 어찌나 소란스러운지, 후회할 일만 생긴다니까."], ["At the works, for instance, they pocketed the advance-money and made off.", "예를 들어 작업장에서는, 선급금을 챙겨서 달아나 버렸지요. 판사가 어떻게 했게요?"], ["What did the justice do? Why, acquitted them.", "아니, 그자들을 무죄로 풀어 줬단 말이오."], ["Nothing keeps them in order but their own communal court and their village elder.", "그자들을 단속할 수 있는 건 자기네 촌락 재판소와 마을 장로뿐이오."], ["He’ll flog them in the good old style!", "그 양반이 옛날 식으로 호되게 매질을 해 주거든!"], ["But for that there’d be nothing for it but to give it all up and run away.”", "그게 아니면 죄다 집어치우고 도망치는 수밖에 없지요.\""]], [["Obviously the landowner was chaffing Sviazhsky, who, far from resenting it, was apparently amused by it.", "지주가 스비야시스키를 놀려 대고 있는 게 분명했지만, 스비야시스키는 분개하기는커녕 도리어 재미있어하는 눈치였다."]], [["“But you see we manage our land without such extreme measures,”", "\"하지만 보세요, 우리는 그런 극단적인 방법 없이도 우리 땅을 잘 꾸려 가고 있잖습니까.\""], ["said he, smiling: “Levin and I and this gentleman.”", "그가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"레빈하고 나, 그리고 여기 이분 말입니다.\""]], [["He indicated the other landowner.", "그는 다른 지주를 가리켰다."]], [["“Yes, the thing’s done at Mihail Petrovitch’s, but ask him how it’s done.", "\"그렇소, 미하일 페트로비치 댁에서는 그렇게 되고 있지요. 한데 어떻게 되고 있는지 한번 물어보시오."], ["Do you call that a rational system?”", "그걸 합리적인 방식이라고 하시오?\""], ["said the landowner, obviously rather proud of the word “rational.”", "지주가 '합리적'이라는 말이 자못 자랑스럽다는 듯이 말했다."]], [["“My system’s very simple,” said Mihail Petrovitch, “thank God. All my management rests on getting the money ready for the autumn taxes, and the peasants come to me, ‘Father, master, help us!’ Well, the peasants are all one’s neighbors; one feels for them. So one advances them a third, but one says: ‘Remember, lads, I have helped you, and you must help me when I need it—whether it’s the sowing of the oats, or the haycutting, or the harvest’; and well, one agrees, so much for each taxpayer—though there are dishonest ones among them too, it’s true.”", "\"내 방식이야 아주 간단하오.\" 미하일 페트로비치가 말했다. \"고맙게도 말이오. 내 살림이라는 게 다 가을 세금 낼 돈을 미리 마련해 두는 데 달려 있는데, 그러면 농민들이 나한테 찾아와서 '아버님, 나리, 우리 좀 도와주십쇼!' 하지요. 뭐, 농민들이야 다 이웃 아니겠소. 안쓰러운 마음이 들지요. 그래서 삼분의 일쯤 선급으로 내어 주면서 이렇게 말하지요. '얘들아, 잊지 마라, 내가 너희를 도와줬으니, 내가 필요로 할 때는 너희가 나를 도와줘야 한다—귀리 파종이든, 풀베기든, 추수든 말이다.' 그래서, 뭐, 그렇게 합의를 보는 거요, 납세자 한 사람당 얼마씩으로—하긴 그중에는 정직하지 못한 작자들도 있긴 하오, 사실이지.\""]], [["Levin, who had long been familiar with these patriarchal methods, exchanged glances with Sviazhsky and interrupted Mihail Petrovitch, turning again to the gentleman with the gray whiskers.", "이런 가부장적 방식에 오래전부터 익숙해 있던 레빈은 스비야시스키와 눈짓을 주고받고는 미하일 페트로비치의 말을 가로막으며, 다시 잿빛 구레나룻의 신사 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸다."]], [["“Then what do you think?”", "\"그러면 어떻게 생각하십니까?\""], ["he asked; “what system is one to adopt nowadays?”", "그가 물었다. \"요즘 같은 시대에는 어떤 방식을 채택해야 할까요?\""]], [["“Why, manage like Mihail Petrovitch, or let the land for half the crop or for rent to the peasants; that one can do—only that’s just how the general prosperity of the country is being ruined.", "\"왜, 미하일 페트로비치처럼 꾸려 가든가, 아니면 수확의 절반을 받기로 하거나 소작료를 받기로 하고 농민들에게 땅을 빌려주든가 하면 되지요. 그거야 할 수 있소—다만 그게 바로 나라 전체의 번영을 망쳐 놓는 길이라오."], ["Where the land with serf-labor and good management gave a yield of nine to one, on the half-crop system it yields three to one.", "농노 노동에 좋은 경영을 곁들였을 때 아홉 배 수확을 내던 땅이, 수확 절반 나누기 방식으로는 세 배밖에 못 내지요."], ["Russia has been ruined by the emancipation!”", "러시아는 그 해방 때문에 망한 거요!\""]], [["Sviazhsky looked with smiling eyes at Levin, and even made a faint gesture of irony to him; but Levin did not think the landowner’s words absurd, he understood them better than he did Sviazhsky.", "스비야시스키는 미소 띤 눈으로 레빈을 바라보며 그에게 슬며시 비꼬는 듯한 몸짓까지 해 보였다. 하지만 레빈은 그 지주의 말이 터무니없다고 여기지 않았고, 오히려 스비야시스키의 말보다 더 잘 이해할 수 있었다."], ["A great deal more of what the gentleman with the gray whiskers said to show in what way Russia was ruined by the emancipation struck him indeed as very true, new to him, and quite incontestable.", "러시아가 어떤 식으로 해방 때문에 망했는지 보여 주려고 잿빛 구레나룻의 신사가 한 말 가운데 많은 부분이, 사실 레빈에게는 매우 옳고, 자신에게는 새로우며, 도무지 반박할 수 없는 것으로 느껴졌다."], ["The landowner unmistakably spoke his own individual thought—a thing that very rarely happens—and a thought to which he had been brought not by a desire of finding some exercise for an idle brain, but a thought which had grown up out of the conditions of his life, which he had brooded over in the solitude of his village, and had considered in every aspect.", "그 지주는 틀림없이 자기 자신의 개인적인 생각을 말하고 있었는데—이는 매우 드물게 일어나는 일이었다—그것도 한가한 머리에 운동거리를 찾아 주려는 욕구에서 나온 생각이 아니라, 자기 삶의 조건들 속에서 자라난 생각, 마을의 고독 속에서 곰곰이 곱씹어 온 생각, 모든 측면에서 따져 본 생각이었다."]], [["“The point is, don’t you see, that progress of every sort is only made by the use of authority,”", "\"요점은 말이오, 아시겠지만, 온갖 종류의 진보라는 게 오직 권위를 사용해야만 이루어진다는 거요.\""], ["he said, evidently wishing to show he was not without culture.", "그가 자기에게 교양이 없지 않음을 보여 주려는 듯이 말했다."], ["“Take the reforms of Peter, of Catherine, of Alexander.", "\"표트르 대제의 개혁이나, 예카테리나, 알렉산드르의 개혁을 보시오."], ["Take European history.", "유럽의 역사를 보시오."], ["And progress in agriculture more than anything else—the potato, for instance, that was introduced among us by force.", "그리고 무엇보다도 농업의 진보를 보란 말이오—예를 들면 감자, 그것도 우리한테는 강제로 들여온 거요."], ["The wooden plough too wasn’t always used.", "나무 쟁기도 늘 쓰던 게 아니었소."], ["It was introduced maybe in the days before the Empire, but it was probably brought in by force.", "그것도 아마 제국 이전 시대에 들여왔겠지만, 십중팔구 강제로 들여왔을 거요."], ["Now, in our own day, we landowners in the serf times used various improvements in our husbandry: drying machines and thrashing machines, and carting manure and all the modern implements—all that we brought into use by our authority, and the peasants opposed it at first, and ended by imitating us.", "자, 우리 시대만 봐도, 농노제 시절에 우리 지주들은 농사에 갖가지 개량법을 썼소. 건조기에 탈곡기에, 거름 나르기며 온갖 근대적 농기구를—그 모든 걸 우리가 우리 권위로 도입했고, 농민들은 처음에는 반대하다가 결국 우리를 흉내 내게 됐지요."], ["Now, by the abolition of serfdom we have been deprived of our authority; and so our husbandry, where it had been raised to a high level, is bound to sink to the most savage primitive condition.", "그런데 농노제가 폐지되면서 우리는 우리 권위를 빼앗겨 버렸소. 그래서 높은 수준까지 끌어올려졌던 우리 농사가 가장 야만적이고 원시적인 상태로 가라앉을 수밖에 없게 된 거요."], ["That’s how I see it.”", "내 눈에는 그렇게 보인단 말이오.\""]], [["“But why so?", "\"하지만 어째서 그렇습니까?"], ["If it’s rational, you’ll be able to keep up the same system with hired labor,”", "그게 합리적인 거라면, 고용 노동을 가지고도 같은 방식을 유지하실 수 있을 텐데요.\""], ["said Sviazhsky.", "스비야시스키가 말했다."]], [["“We’ve no power over them.", "\"우리한테는 그자들을 다스릴 힘이 없소."], ["With whom am I going to work the system, allow me to ask?”", "도대체 누구를 데리고 그 방식을 운영하라는 거요, 한번 물어봅시다?\""]], [["“There it is—the labor force—the chief element in agriculture,”", "'바로 저거야—노동력—농업의 핵심 요소.'"], ["thought Levin.", "레빈은 생각했다."]], [["“With laborers.”", "\"일꾼들을 데리고요.\""]], [["“The laborers won’t work well, and won’t work with good implements.", "\"일꾼들은 일을 제대로 하지도 않고, 좋은 농기구로 일하려고 하지도 않소."], ["Our laborer can do nothing but get drunk like a pig, and when he’s drunk he ruins everything you give him.", "우리 일꾼이 할 줄 아는 거라곤 돼지처럼 술 퍼마시는 것뿐이고, 취하면 당신이 맡긴 걸 죄다 망쳐 놓지요."], ["He makes the horses ill with too much water, cuts good harness, barters the tires of the wheels for drink, drops bits of iron into the thrashing machine, so as to break it.", "말한테 물을 너무 먹여 병들게 하고, 멀쩡한 마구를 잘라 버리고, 바퀴 테를 술과 바꿔 먹고, 탈곡기에 쇳조각을 던져 넣어 망가뜨려 버린단 말이오."], ["He loathes the sight of anything that’s not after his fashion.", "자기 방식에 맞지 않는 건 뭐든 꼴도 보기 싫어하지요."], ["And that’s how it is the whole level of husbandry has fallen.", "그래서 농사 수준 전체가 이렇게 떨어진 거요."], ["Lands gone out of cultivation, overgrown with weeds, or divided among the peasants, and where millions of bushels were raised you get a hundred thousand; the wealth of the country has decreased.", "땅은 묵어 잡초가 무성하거나, 농민들에게 쪼개져 나가고, 수백만 부셸이 나던 곳에서 십만 부셸을 얻게 됐으니, 나라의 부가 줄어든 거요."], ["If the same thing had been done, but with care that....”", "똑같이 했더라도, 조심해서 했다면 그게……\""]], [["And he proceeded to unfold his own scheme of emancipation by means of which these drawbacks might have been avoided.", "그러더니 그는 이런 폐단을 피할 수 있었을 자기 나름의 해방 방안을 펼쳐 보이기 시작했다."]], [["This did not interest Levin, but when he had finished, Levin went back to his first position, and, addressing Sviazhsky, and trying to draw him into expressing his serious opinion:—", "이건 레빈의 흥미를 끌지 못했지만, 그가 말을 마치자 레빈은 처음의 입장으로 돌아가, 스비야시스키를 향해, 그에게서 진지한 의견을 끌어내 보려 애쓰며 말했다."]], [["“That the standard of culture is falling, and that with our present relations to the peasants there is no possibility of farming on a rational system to yield a profit—that’s perfectly true,”", "\"교양의 수준이 떨어지고 있다는 것, 그리고 농민들과의 우리의 현재 관계로는 이익을 낼 수 있는 합리적 방식으로 농사를 지을 가능성이 없다는 것—그건 전적으로 옳습니다.\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“I don’t believe it,”", "\"나는 그렇게 생각하지 않아요.\""], ["Sviazhsky replied quite seriously; “all I see is that we don’t know how to cultivate the land, and that our system of agriculture in the serf days was by no means too high, but too low.", "스비야시스키가 자못 진지하게 대답했다. \"내가 보기에는, 우리가 땅을 일굴 줄 모른다는 것, 그리고 농노 시절 우리 농업 방식이 결코 너무 높았던 게 아니라 오히려 너무 낮았다는 것뿐이오."], ["We have no machines, no good stock, no efficient supervision; we don’t even know how to keep accounts.", "우리한테는 기계도 없고, 좋은 가축도 없고, 효율적인 감독도 없소. 회계를 어떻게 하는지조차 모르지요."], ["Ask any landowner; he won’t be able to tell you what crop’s profitable, and what’s not.”", "아무 지주에게나 물어보시오. 어떤 작물이 수지가 맞고 어떤 게 안 맞는지 말해 줄 수 있는 사람이 없을 거요.\""]], [["“Italian bookkeeping,”", "\"이탈리아식 부기로구려.\""], ["said the gentleman of the gray whiskers ironically.", "잿빛 구레나룻의 신사가 비꼬듯 말했다."], ["“You may keep your books as you like, but if they spoil everything for you, there won’t be any profit.”", "\"장부야 마음대로 적을 수 있겠지만, 그자들이 죄다 망쳐 놓으면 무슨 이익이 있겠소.\""]], [["“Why do they spoil things?", "\"왜 망쳐 놓는다는 거요?"], ["A poor thrashing machine, or your Russian presser, they will break, but my steam press they don’t break.", "형편없는 탈곡기나 댁의 러시아식 압착기야 그자들이 부숴 놓겠지만, 내 증기 압착기는 못 부순단 말이오."], ["A wretched Russian nag they’ll ruin, but keep good dray-horses—they won’t ruin them.", "변변찮은 러시아 조랑말이야 망쳐 놓겠지만, 좋은 짐말은 두면—못 망쳐요."], ["And so it is all round.", "다 그런 식이오."], ["We must raise our farming to a higher level.”", "우리는 우리 농사를 더 높은 수준으로 끌어올려야 하오.\""]], [["“Oh, if one only had the means to do it, Nikolay Ivanovitch!", "\"오, 그럴 수단만 있다면야, 니콜라이 이바노비치!"], ["It’s all very well for you; but for me, with a son to keep at the university, lads to be educated at the high school—how am I going to buy these dray-horses?”", "댁이야 다 좋겠지요. 하지만 나처럼 대학에 다니는 아들을 두고, 김나지움에서 교육시켜야 할 아이들이 있는 사람은—내가 무슨 수로 그런 짐말을 사겠소?\""]], [["“Well, that’s what the land banks are for.”", "\"뭐, 그러라고 토지은행이 있는 거 아닙니까.\""]], [["“To get what’s left me sold by auction?", "\"나한테 남은 걸 경매에 부쳐 팔라고?"], ["No, thank you.”", "아니, 사양하겠소.\""]], [["“I don’t agree that it’s necessary or possible to raise the level of agriculture still higher,”", "\"농업 수준을 더 높이 끌어올리는 게 필요하거나 가능하다는 데에는 저도 동의하지 않습니다.\""], ["said Levin.", "레빈이 말했다."], ["“I devote myself to it, and I have means, but I can do nothing.", "\"저는 거기에 몰두하고 있고, 수단도 있지만, 아무것도 해낼 수가 없어요."], ["As to the banks, I don’t know to whom they’re any good.", "은행으로 말하자면, 그게 누구한테 무슨 도움이 되는지 모르겠습니다."], ["For my part, anyway, whatever I’ve spent money on in the way of husbandry, it has been a loss: stock—a loss, machinery—a loss.”", "적어도 저로서는, 농사에 무슨 돈을 쏟아부었든 간에, 그게 다 손해였어요. 가축도—손해, 기계도—손해였지요.\""]], [["“That’s true enough,”", "\"바로 그렇소.\""], ["the gentleman with the gray whiskers chimed in, positively laughing with satisfaction.", "잿빛 구레나룻의 신사가 만족스러운 듯 그야말로 껄껄 웃으며 맞장구를 쳤다."]], [["“And I’m not the only one,”", "\"그리고 저만 그런 게 아닙니다.\""], ["pursued Levin.", "레빈이 말을 이었다."], ["“I mix with all the neighboring landowners, who are cultivating their land on a rational system; they all, with rare exceptions, are doing so at a loss.", "\"저는 합리적 방식으로 자기 땅을 일구는 이웃 지주들과 두루 어울리는데, 그분들도 드문 예외를 빼고는 다들 손해를 보면서 그렇게 하고 있어요."], ["Come, tell us how does your land do—does it pay?”", "자, 댁의 땅은 어떤지 말씀해 보세요—수지가 맞습니까?\""], ["said Levin, and at once in Sviazhsky’s eyes he detected that fleeting expression of alarm which he had noticed whenever he had tried to penetrate beyond the outer chambers of Sviazhsky’s mind.", "레빈이 말하자, 곧바로 스비야시스키의 눈에서, 스비야시스키 마음의 바깥쪽 방 너머를 들여다보려 할 때마다 그가 알아차리곤 했던 그 스쳐 지나가는 경계의 빛이 보였다."]], [["Moreover, this question on Levin’s part was not quite in good faith.", "게다가 레빈의 이 질문은 그다지 진심에서 나온 것도 아니었다."], ["Madame Sviazhskaya had just told him at tea that they had that summer invited a German expert in bookkeeping from Moscow, who for a consideration of five hundred roubles had investigated the management of their property, and found that it was costing them a loss of three thousand odd roubles.", "스비야시스카야 부인이 방금 차를 마시면서 그에게 말해 주기를, 그들이 그해 여름 모스크바에서 회계에 정통한 독일인 전문가를 불러왔는데, 그 사람이 오백 루블의 사례를 받고 그들 영지의 경영을 조사한 결과, 삼천 루블 남짓한 손해를 보고 있더라는 것이었다."], ["She did not remember the precise sum, but it appeared that the German had worked it out to the fraction of a farthing.", "그녀는 정확한 액수는 기억하지 못했지만, 그 독일인이 그것을 한 푼의 단위까지 계산해 냈던 모양이었다."]], [["The gray-whiskered landowner smiled at the mention of the profits of Sviazhsky’s farming, obviously aware how much gain his neighbor and marshal was likely to be making.", "잿빛 구레나룻의 지주는 스비야시스키 농사의 수익 이야기가 나오자 미소를 지었다. 자기 이웃이자 귀족회장이 얼마나 이득을 보고 있을지를 빤히 안다는 듯이."]], [["“Possibly it does not pay,”", "\"수지가 안 맞을 수도 있지요.\""], ["answered Sviazhsky.", "스비야시스키가 대답했다."], ["“That merely proves either that I’m a bad manager, or that I’ve sunk my capital for the increase of my rents.”", "\"하지만 그건 단지 내가 경영을 잘 못한다는 것이거나, 아니면 내 소작료를 늘리려고 자본을 묻어 두었다는 것을 증명할 뿐이오.\""]], [["“Oh, rent!”", "\"오, 소작료라니!\""], ["Levin cried with horror.", "레빈이 질겁하며 외쳤다. \"소작료라는 건 유럽에서나 있을 수 있어요, 거기서는 거기에 투입된 노동으로 땅이 개량됐으니까요."], ["“Rent there may be in Europe, where land has been improved by the labor put into it, but with us all the land is deteriorating from the labor put into it—in other words they’re working it out; so there’s no question of rent.”", "하지만 우리 땅은 죄다 거기에 투입된 노동으로 황폐해지고 있단 말입니다—다시 말해, 땅의 힘을 짜내고 있는 거지요. 그러니 소작료라는 건 문제가 안 됩니다.\""]], [["“How no rent?", "\"어째서 소작료가 없다는 거요?"], ["It’s a law.”", "그건 법칙인데.\""]], [["“Then we’re outside the law; rent explains nothing for us, but simply muddles us.", "\"그렇다면 우리는 그 법칙 바깥에 있는 거지요. 소작료로는 우리한테 아무것도 설명되지 않고, 그저 우리를 헷갈리게 할 뿐입니다."], ["No, tell me how there can be a theory of rent?...”", "아니, 어떻게 소작료 이론이라는 게 성립할 수 있는지 말씀해 보세요……\""]], [["“Will you have some junket?", "\"준켓 좀 드시겠소?"], ["Masha, pass us some junket or raspberries.”", "마샤, 우리한테 준켓이나 산딸기 좀 건네주구려.\""], ["He turned to his wife.", "그가 아내 쪽을 돌아보며 말했다."], ["“Extraordinarily late the raspberries are lasting this year.”", "\"올해는 산딸기가 유난히 늦게까지 가는구려.\""]], [["And in the happiest frame of mind Sviazhsky got up and walked off, apparently supposing the conversation to have ended at the very point when to Levin it seemed that it was only just beginning.", "* 준켓: 우유에 응유 효소를 넣어 만든 디저트. 그러더니 스비야시스키는 더없이 기분 좋은 표정으로 자리에서 일어나 가 버렸다. 레빈에게는 대화가 이제 막 시작된 것처럼 여겨지던 바로 그 지점에서, 그는 대화가 끝났다고 여기는 모양이었다."]], [["Having lost his antagonist, Levin continued the conversation with the gray-whiskered landowner, trying to prove to him that all the difficulty arises from the fact that we don’t find out the peculiarities and habits of our laborer; but the landowner, like all men who think independently and in isolation, was slow in taking in any other person’s idea, and particularly partial to his own.", "논쟁 상대를 잃은 레빈은 잿빛 구레나룻의 지주와 대화를 계속하면서, 이 모든 어려움이 우리가 우리 일꾼의 특성과 습성을 제대로 알아내지 못하는 데서 비롯한다는 것을 그에게 증명해 보이려 애썼다. 하지만 그 지주는, 홀로 고립된 채 독자적으로 사고하는 모든 사람이 그러하듯, 다른 사람의 생각을 받아들이는 데 더뎠고, 특히 자기 생각에 치우쳐 있었다."], ["He stuck to it that the Russian peasant is a swine and likes swinishness, and that to get him out of his swinishness one must have authority, and there is none; one must have the stick, and we have become so liberal that we have all of a sudden replaced the stick that served us for a thousand years by lawyers and model prisons, where the worthless, stinking peasant is fed on good soup and has a fixed allowance of cubic feet of air.", "그는 러시아 농민이란 돼지 같은 존재이며 돼지 같은 짓을 좋아한다는 것, 그리고 그자들을 그 돼지 같은 행태에서 벗어나게 하려면 권위가 있어야 하는데 그게 없다는 것, 회초리가 있어야 하는데 우리가 너무 자유주의적이 되어 버린 나머지, 천 년 동안 우리에게 도움이 되어 온 회초리를 별안간 변호사와 모범 감옥으로 갈아 치워 버렸고, 거기서는 그 쓸모없고 악취 나는 농민이 좋은 수프를 얻어먹고 일정량의 입방피트짜리 공기까지 배급받는다는 주장을 고집했다."]], [["“What makes you think,”", "\"무엇 때문에 생각하십니까,\""], ["said Levin, trying to get back to the question, “that it’s impossible to find some relation to the laborer in which the labor would become productive?”", "레빈이 다시 본래 문제로 돌아가려 애쓰며 말했다. \"노동이 생산적인 것으로 바뀔 만한 어떤 관계를 일꾼과 맺는 게 불가능하다고 말이죠?\""]], [["“That never could be so with the Russian peasantry; we’ve no power over them,”", "\"러시아 농민을 데리고는 결코 그렇게 될 수 없소. 우리한테는 그자들을 다스릴 힘이 없으니까.\""], ["answered the landowner.", "지주가 대답했다."]], [["“How can new conditions be found?” said Sviazhsky.", "\"어떻게 새로운 조건들을 찾아낼 수 있다는 거요?\""], ["Having eaten some junket and lighted a cigarette, he came back to the discussion.", "스비야시스키가 말했다. 준켓을 좀 먹고 담배에 불을 붙인 그가 다시 토론으로 돌아온 것이었다."], ["“All possible relations to the labor force have been defined and studied,”", "\"노동력과 맺을 수 있는 가능한 관계는 죄다 규정되고 연구됐어요.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“The relic of barbarism, the primitive commune with each guarantee for all, will disappear of itself; serfdom has been abolished—there remains nothing but free labor, and its forms are fixed and ready made, and must be adopted.", "\"야만의 유물인, 만인이 만인을 보증하는 그 원시적 공동체는 저절로 사라질 거요. 농노제는 폐지됐으니—이제 남은 건 자유 노동뿐인데, 그 형태들은 이미 정해지고 다 만들어져 있으니, 그걸 채택하기만 하면 되는 거요."], ["Permanent hands, day-laborers, rammers—you can’t get out of those forms.”", "상시 고용 일꾼, 날품팔이, 다지기 인부—그런 형태들에서 벗어날 수는 없소.\""]], [["“But Europe is dissatisfied with these forms.”", "\"하지만 유럽은 그런 형태들에 만족하지 못하고 있잖습니까.\""]], [["“Dissatisfied, and seeking new ones.", "\"만족하지 못하고, 새로운 걸 찾고 있지요."], ["And will find them, in all probability.”", "그리고 십중팔구 찾아낼 거요.\""]], [["“That’s just what I was meaning,”", "\"바로 그게 제 말이었습니다.\""], ["answered Levin.", "레빈이 대답했다."], ["“Why shouldn’t we seek them for ourselves?”", "\"어째서 우리는 우리 자신을 위해 그걸 찾으면 안 된다는 겁니까?\""]], [["“Because it would be just like inventing afresh the means for constructing railways.", "\"왜냐하면 그건 철도 부설 방법을 새삼스레 다시 발명하는 거나 마찬가지일 테니까요."], ["They are ready, invented.”", "그건 이미 준비돼 있고, 발명돼 있단 말이오.\""]], [["“But if they don’t do for us, if they’re stupid?”", "\"하지만 그게 우리한테 안 맞는다면, 그게 어리석은 거라면요?\""], ["said Levin.", "레빈이 말했다."]], [["And again he detected the expression of alarm in the eyes of Sviazhsky.", "그리고 그는 또다시 스비야시스키의 눈에 어린 경계의 빛을 알아차렸다."]], [["“Oh, yes; we’ll bury the world under our caps!", "\"오, 그렇겠지요. 우리는 모자 하나로 온 세상을 뒤덮어 버릴 테고!"], ["We’ve found the secret Europe was seeking for!", "유럽이 찾아 헤매던 비밀을 우리가 찾아냈다는 거로군! 그런 말이야 나도 다 들어 봤소."], ["I’ve heard all that; but, excuse me, do you know all that’s been done in Europe on the question of the organization of labor?” “No, very little.”", "하지만, 미안하지만, 노동의 조직 문제에 관해 유럽에서 무슨 일이 이루어졌는지 전부 알고 계시오?\""]], [["“That question is now absorbing the best minds in Europe.", "\"아뇨, 아주 조금밖에 모릅니다.\" \"그 문제는 지금 유럽 최고의 지성들을 사로잡고 있소."], ["The Schulze-Delitsch movement....", "슐체델리치 운동이라든지……"], ["And then all this enormous literature of the labor question, the most liberal Lassalle movement ...", "그리고 노동 문제에 관한 이 방대한 문헌들, 더없이 자유주의적인 라살레 운동이라든지……"], ["the Mulhausen experiment?", "뮐하우젠 실험은요?"], ["That’s a fact by now, as you’re probably aware.”", "아마 아시겠지만, 그건 이제 엄연한 사실이 됐소.\""]], [["“I have some idea of it, but very vague.”", "\"어렴풋이는 알지만, 아주 막연합니다.\""]], [["“No, you only say that; no doubt you know all about it as well as I do.", "\"아니, 말씀만 그렇게 하시는 거지요. 분명 나만큼이나 다 알고 계실 텐데."], ["I’m not a professor of sociology, of course, but it interested me, and really, if it interests you, you ought to study it.”", "물론 내가 사회학 교수는 아니지만, 그게 내 흥미를 끌었거든. 정말이지, 댁도 흥미가 있다면, 한번 연구해 보셔야 하오.\""]], [["“But what conclusion have they come to?”", "\"그래서 그들은 어떤 결론에 이르렀습니까?\""]], [["“Excuse me....”", "\"미안하오만……\""]], [["The two neighbors had risen, and Sviazhsky, once more checking Levin in his inconvenient habit of peeping into what was beyond the outer chambers of his mind, went to see his guests out.", "두 이웃이 자리에서 일어섰고, 스비야시스키는 자기 마음의 바깥쪽 방 너머를 들여다보려는 레빈의 그 거북한 버릇을 또 한 번 막으며, 손님들을 배웅하러 나갔다."]], [["# Chapter 28", "# Chapter 28"]], [["Levin was insufferably bored that evening with the ladies; he was stirred as he had never been before by the idea that the dissatisfaction he was feeling with his system of managing his land was not an exceptional case, but the general condition of things in Russia; that the organization of some relation of the laborers to the soil in which they would work, as with the peasant he had met half-way to the Sviazhskys’, was not a dream, but a problem which must be solved.", "레빈은 그날 저녁 부인들과 함께 있는 것이 견딜 수 없이 지루했다. 그런데 자기가 영지 경영 방식에 대해 느끼는 불만이 예외적인 경우가 아니라 러시아의 일반적인 상황이라는 생각, 그리고 자기가 스비아시스키 집으로 오는 길 중간쯤에서 만난 농부의 경우처럼 노동자들과 그들이 일하게 될 토지 사이에 어떤 관계를 조직하는 일이 한낱 꿈이 아니라 반드시 풀어야 할 문제라는 생각에, 그는 일찍이 느껴 본 적 없을 만큼 마음이 들끓었다."], ["And it seemed to him that the problem could be solved, and that he ought to try and solve it.", "그리고 그에게는 그 문제가 풀릴 수 있을 것 같았고, 자기가 그것을 풀어 보아야 한다는 생각이 들었다."]], [["After saying good-night to the ladies, and promising to stay the whole of the next day, so as to make an expedition on horseback with them to see an interesting ruin in the crown forest, Levin went, before going to bed, into his host’s study to get the books on the labor question that Sviazhsky had offered him.", "부인들에게 작별 인사를 하고, 다음 날 하루를 온전히 머물면서 함께 말을 타고 국유림에 있는 흥미로운 폐허를 보러 가기로 약속한 뒤, 레빈은 잠자리에 들기 전에 스비아시스키가 권해 준 노동 문제에 관한 책을 가지러 집주인의 서재로 갔다."], ["Sviazhsky’s study was a huge room, surrounded by bookcases and with two tables in it—one a massive writing-table, standing in the middle of the room, and the other a round table, covered with recent numbers of reviews and journals in different languages, ranged like the rays of a star round the lamp.", "스비아시스키의 서재는 사방이 책장으로 둘러싸인 거대한 방이었고, 그 안에는 탁자가 두 개 있었다. 하나는 방 한가운데 놓인 묵직한 책상이었고, 다른 하나는 둥근 탁자로, 갖가지 언어로 된 최근의 평론지와 잡지가 별빛처럼 램프를 중심으로 펼쳐져 있었다."], ["On the writing-table was a stand of drawers marked with gold lettering, and full of papers of various sorts.", "책상 위에는 금색 글자가 새겨지고 온갖 종류의 서류로 가득 찬 서랍장이 놓여 있었다."]], [["Sviazhsky took out the books, and sat down in a rocking-chair.", "스비아시스키는 책들을 꺼내 들고 흔들의자에 앉았다."]], [["“What are you looking at there?”", "\"거기서 뭘 보고 계십니까?\""], ["he said to Levin, who was standing at the round table looking through the reviews.", "그가 둥근 탁자 옆에 서서 평론지를 훑어보고 있던 레빈에게 물었다."]], [["“Oh, yes, there’s a very interesting article here,”", "\"아, 그래요, 거기 아주 흥미로운 글이 하나 있습니다.\""], ["said Sviazhsky of the review Levin was holding in his hand.", "레빈이 손에 든 평론지를 두고 스비아시스키가 말했다."], ["“It appears,”", "\"그러니까,\""], ["he went on, with eager interest, “that Friedrich was not, after all, the person chiefly responsible for the partition of Poland.", "그는 열렬한 관심을 보이며 말을 이었다. \"결국 폴란드 분할에 대해 주로 책임이 있는 사람이 프리드리히가 아니었다는 거예요."], ["It is proved....”", "그게 증명됐다는군요…….\""]], [["And with his characteristic clearness, he summed up those new, very important, and interesting revelations.", "그러고는 특유의 명료함으로 그 새롭고도 매우 중요하고 흥미로운 사실들을 요약해 주었다."], ["Although Levin was engrossed at the moment by his ideas about the problem of the land, he wondered, as he heard Sviazhsky: “What is there inside of him?", "그 순간 레빈은 토지 문제에 관한 자신의 생각에 빠져 있었지만, 스비아시스키의 말을 들으면서 의아했다. '이 사람 속에는 도대체 뭐가 들어 있는 걸까?"], ["And why, why is he interested in the partition of Poland?”", "그리고 왜, 왜 이 사람은 폴란드 분할에 관심을 갖는 거지?'"], ["When Sviazhsky had finished, Levin could not help asking: “Well, and what then?”", "스비아시스키가 말을 마치자 레빈은 묻지 않을 수 없었다. \"그래서, 그게 어쨌다는 겁니까?\""], ["But there was nothing to follow.", "그러나 뒤따를 것은 아무것도 없었다."], ["It was simply interesting that it had been proved to be so and so.", "그저 그것이 이러저러하다고 증명되었다는 사실이 흥미로울 뿐이었다."], ["But Sviazhsky did not explain, and saw no need to explain why it was interesting to him.", "하지만 스비아시스키는 그것이 왜 자기에게 흥미로운지를 설명하지 않았고, 설명할 필요도 느끼지 못했다."]], [["“Yes, but I was very much interested by your irritable neighbor,”", "\"그렇군요, 그런데 저는 당신의 그 성마른 이웃 분이 무척 흥미로웠습니다.\""], ["said Levin, sighing.", "레빈이 한숨을 쉬며 말했다."], ["“He’s a clever fellow, and said a lot that was true.”", "\"영리한 사람이고, 옳은 말도 많이 하던데요.\""]], [["“Oh, get along with you!", "\"아, 그만하세요!"], ["An inveterate supporter of serfdom at heart, like all of them!”", "그 사람도 마음속으로는 다른 사람들이랑 똑같이 뿌리 깊은 농노제 옹호자라니까요!\""], ["said Sviazhsky.", "스비아시스키가 말했다."]], [["“Whose marshal you are.”", "\"당신이 귀족 단장으로 있는 그 사람들 말이지요.\""]], [["“Yes, only I marshal them in the other direction,”", "\"그렇죠, 다만 저는 그 사람들을 다른 방향으로 이끌고 있을 뿐입니다.\""], ["said Sviazhsky, laughing.", "스비아시스키가 웃으며 말했다."]], [["“I’ll tell you what interests me very much,”", "\"제가 무엇에 큰 관심이 있는지 말씀드리지요.\""], ["said Levin.", "레빈이 말했다."], ["“He’s right that our system, that’s to say of rational farming, doesn’t answer, that the only thing that answers is the money-lender system, like that meek-looking gentleman’s, or else the very simplest....", "\"우리 방식이, 그러니까 합리적 농업이라는 게 효과가 없다는 그 사람 말이 옳아요. 효과가 있는 건 그 온순해 보이는 신사처럼 고리대금 방식이거나, 아니면 아주 단순한 방식뿐이라는 거죠……."], ["Whose fault is it?”", "그게 누구 탓일까요?\""]], [["“Our own, of course.", "\"물론 우리 자신 탓이죠."], ["Besides, it’s not true that it doesn’t answer.", "게다가 효과가 없다는 것도 사실이 아닙니다."], ["It answers with Vassiltchikov.”", "바실치코프네서는 효과가 있어요.\""]], [["“A factory....”", "\"공장이…….\""]], [["“But I really don’t know what it is you are surprised at.", "\"하지만 저는 당신이 도대체 무엇에 놀라는지 정말 모르겠습니다."], ["The people are at such a low stage of rational and moral development, that it’s obvious they’re bound to oppose everything that’s strange to them.", "민중은 이성적·도덕적 발달 단계가 어찌나 낮은지, 자기들에게 낯선 모든 것에 반대할 수밖에 없다는 건 뻔한 일이거든요. 유럽에서는 합리적 방식이 효과가 있는데, 그건 민중이 교육받았기 때문이지요."], ["In Europe, a rational system answers because the people are educated; it follows that we must educate the people—that’s all.”", "그러니 우리도 민중을 교육해야 한다는 결론이 나오는 겁니다. 그게 전부예요.\""]], [["“But how are we to educate the people?”", "\"하지만 어떻게 민중을 교육한단 말입니까?\""]], [["“To educate the people three things are needed: schools, and schools, and schools.”", "\"민중을 교육하려면 세 가지가 필요합니다. 학교, 그리고 학교, 또 학교지요.\""]], [["“But you said yourself the people are at such a low stage of material development: what help are schools for that?”", "\"하지만 당신 스스로 민중이 물질적 발달 단계가 그렇게 낮다고 하지 않았습니까. 그런 데 학교가 무슨 도움이 됩니까?\""]], [["“Do you know, you remind me of the story of the advice given to the sick man—You should try purgative medicine.", "\"있잖아요, 당신은 어떤 병자에게 해 준 충고 이야기를 떠올리게 하는군요. 설사약을 한번 써 보시오."], ["Taken: worse. Try leeches.", "써 봤다, 더 나빠졌다."], ["Tried them: worse.", "거머리를 써 보시오."], ["Well, then, there’s nothing left but to pray to God.", "써 봤다, 더 나빠졌다. 그럼, 이제 남은 건 하느님께 기도하는 것뿐이오."], ["Tried it: worse.", "해 봤다, 더 나빠졌다."], ["That’s just how it is with us.", "우리 처지가 꼭 그래요."], ["I say political economy; you say—worse.", "제가 정치경제학이라고 하면, 당신은 더 나빠진다고 하지요."], ["I say socialism: worse.", "제가 사회주의라고 하면, 더 나빠진다."], ["Education: worse.”", "교육이라고 하면, 더 나빠진다고요.\""]], [["“But how do schools help matters?”", "\"하지만 학교가 어떻게 사정을 나아지게 한단 말입니까?\""]], [["“They give the peasant fresh wants.”", "\"농부에게 새로운 욕구를 주지요.\""]], [["“Well, that’s a thing I’ve never understood,”", "\"음, 그게 바로 제가 도무지 이해할 수 없는 점입니다.\""], ["Levin replied with heat.", "레빈이 열을 올리며 대꾸했다."], ["“In what way are schools going to help the people to improve their material position?", "\"학교가 어떤 방식으로 민중의 물질적 처지를 개선하도록 돕는다는 겁니까?"], ["You say schools, education, will give them fresh wants.", "학교가, 교육이 그들에게 새로운 욕구를 준다고 하셨지요."], ["So much the worse, since they won’t be capable of satisfying them.", "그들은 그 욕구를 채울 능력이 없을 테니, 오히려 더 나빠지는 거예요."], ["And in what way a knowledge of addition and subtraction and the catechism is going to improve their material condition, I never could make out.", "덧셈과 뺄셈, 교리문답에 관한 지식이 어떤 방식으로 그들의 물질적 처지를 개선한다는 건지 저는 도무지 알 수가 없습니다."], ["The day before yesterday, I met a peasant woman in the evening with a little baby, and asked her where she was going.", "그저께 저녁에 어린 아기를 안은 농촌 아낙을 만나서 어디 가느냐고 물었습니다. 점쟁이 노파한테 간다고 하더군요."], ["She said she was going to the wise woman; her boy had screaming fits, so she was taking him to be doctored.", "아이가 발작하듯 비명을 질러서 치료를 받게 하러 데려가는 길이라고요. 제가 물었지요."], ["I asked, ‘Why, how does the wise woman cure screaming fits?’ ‘She puts the child on the hen-roost and repeats some charm....’”", "'아니, 그 점쟁이 노파가 비명 지르는 발작을 어떻게 고친답니까?' 그랬더니, '닭장 홰에 아이를 올려놓고 무슨 주문을 외운대요…….'\""]], [["“Well, you’re saying it yourself!", "\"음, 당신이 직접 그 말을 하고 있군요!"], ["What’s wanted to prevent her taking her child to the hen-roost to cure it of screaming fits is just....”", "그 여자가 비명 발작을 고치겠다고 아이를 닭장 홰로 데려가는 일을 막으려면 필요한 게 바로…….\""], ["Sviazhsky said, smiling good-humoredly.", "스비아시스키가 사람 좋게 웃으며 말했다."]], [["“Oh, no!”", "\"아, 아닙니다!\""], ["said Levin with annoyance; “that method of doctoring I merely meant as a simile for doctoring the people with schools.", "레빈이 짜증스럽게 말했다. \"그 치료법은 그저 학교로 민중을 치료하는 일에 빗댄 비유였을 뿐입니다."], ["The people are poor and ignorant—that we see as surely as the peasant woman sees the baby is ill because it screams.", "민중은 가난하고 무지하지요. 그건 저 아낙이 아이가 비명을 지르니까 병들었다고 확실히 아는 것만큼이나 우리도 확실히 압니다."], ["But in what way this trouble of poverty and ignorance is to be cured by schools is as incomprehensible as how the hen-roost affects the screaming.", "하지만 이 가난과 무지의 병폐를 어떤 방식으로 학교로 고친다는 건지는, 닭장 홰가 비명에 어떤 영향을 미치는지만큼이나 이해가 안 갑니다."], ["What has to be cured is what makes him poor.”", "고쳐야 할 것은 그를 가난하게 만드는 바로 그것이라고요.\""]], [["“Well, in that, at least, you’re in agreement with Spencer, whom you dislike so much.", "\"음, 적어도 그 점에서는 당신이 그렇게도 싫어하는 스펜서와 의견이 같군요."], ["He says, too, that education may be the consequence of greater prosperity and comfort, of more frequent washing, as he says, but not of being able to read and write....”", "그도 교육이란 더 큰 번영과 안락의 결과일 수 있다고, 자기 말로는 더 자주 씻게 된 결과일 수는 있어도 읽고 쓸 수 있게 된 결과는 아니라고 했거든요…….\""]], [["“Well, then, I’m very glad—or the contrary, very sorry, that I’m in agreement with Spencer; only I’ve known it a long while. Schools can do no good; what will do good is an economic organization in which the people will become richer, will have more leisure—and then there will be schools.”", "\"음, 그렇다면 제가 스펜서와 의견이 같다니 무척 기쁘군요. 아니, 그 반대로 무척 유감입니다. 다만 저는 그걸 오래전부터 알고 있었어요. 학교는 아무 도움이 안 됩니다. 도움이 될 것은 민중이 더 부유해지고 더 많은 여가를 갖게 되는 경제 조직입니다. 그렇게 되면 그때 학교도 생기겠지요.\""]], [["“Still, all over Europe now schools are obligatory.”", "\"그래도 지금은 유럽 어디에서나 학교가 의무화되어 있지 않습니까.\""]], [["“And how far do you agree with Spencer yourself about it?”", "\"그러면 당신 자신은 그 점에서 스펜서와 얼마나 의견이 같습니까?\""], ["asked Levin.", "레빈이 물었다."]], [["But there was a gleam of alarm in Sviazhsky’s eyes, and he said smiling:", "그러나 스비아시스키의 눈에는 경계의 빛이 번뜩였고, 그는 미소를 지으며 말했다."]], [["“No; that screaming story is positively capital!", "\"아니, 그 비명 이야기는 정말이지 일품이에요!"], ["Did you really hear it yourself?”", "정말 직접 들으신 겁니까?\""]], [["Levin saw that he was not to discover the connection between this man’s life and his thoughts.", "레빈은 이 사람의 삶과 그의 생각 사이의 연관성을 끝내 알아낼 수 없으리라는 것을 깨달았다."], ["Obviously he did not care in the least what his reasoning led him to; all he wanted was the process of reasoning.", "분명히 그는 자기의 추론이 어떤 결론에 이르는지에는 조금도 관심이 없었다. 그가 원하는 것은 오직 추론하는 과정뿐이었다."], ["And he did not like it when the process of reasoning brought him into a blind alley.", "그리고 그는 추론하는 과정이 자기를 막다른 골목으로 몰아넣을 때면 그것을 싫어했다."], ["That was the only thing he disliked, and avoided by changing the conversation to something agreeable and amusing.", "그것만이 그가 싫어하는 유일한 것이었고, 그는 화제를 기분 좋고 유쾌한 무언가로 바꿈으로써 그것을 피했다."]], [["All the impressions of the day, beginning with the impression made by the old peasant, which served, as it were, as the fundamental basis of all the conceptions and ideas of the day, threw Levin into violent excitement. This dear good Sviazhsky, keeping a stock of ideas simply for social purposes, and obviously having some other principles hidden from Levin, while with the crowd, whose name is legion, he guided public opinion by ideas he did not share; that irascible country gentleman, perfectly correct in the conclusions that he had been worried into by life, but wrong in his exasperation against a whole class, and that the best class in Russia; his own dissatisfaction with the work he had been doing, and the vague hope of finding a remedy for all this—all was blended in a sense of inward turmoil, and anticipation of some solution near at hand.", "그날 받은 모든 인상은, 말하자면 그날의 온갖 개념과 생각의 근본 토대 노릇을 한 그 늙은 농부에게서 받은 인상부터 시작해서, 레빈을 격렬한 흥분으로 몰아넣었다. 그저 사교적 목적을 위해 한 무더기의 생각들을 갖춰 두고 있을 뿐 분명 레빈에게는 숨겨진 다른 어떤 신념을 가지고 있는 이 친절하고 선량한 스비아시스키, 그 무리는 이름이 군단이라 할 만큼 많은 군중을 향해서는 자기도 공감하지 않는 생각들로 여론을 이끄는 이 사람. 삶에 시달린 끝에 다다른 결론에서는 완벽하게 옳았으나 한 계급 전체에, 그것도 러시아 최고의 계급에 대한 격분에서는 그릇된 저 성마른 시골 지주. 자기가 해 온 일에 대한 자신의 불만, 그리고 이 모든 것에 대한 해결책을 찾으리라는 막연한 희망. 이 모든 것이 내면의 동요와 가까운 데 있는 어떤 해결책에 대한 예감 속에 한데 뒤섞였다."]], [["Left alone in the room assigned him, lying on a spring mattress that yielded unexpectedly at every movement of his arm or his leg, Levin did not fall asleep for a long while.", "배정받은 방에 홀로 남아, 팔이나 다리를 움직일 때마다 뜻밖에 푹 꺼지는 스프링 매트리스 위에 누운 레빈은 오랫동안 잠들지 못했다."], ["Not one conversation with Sviazhsky, though he had said a great deal that was clever, had interested Levin; but the conclusions of the irascible landowner required consideration.", "스비아시스키가 영리한 말을 많이 했음에도 그와 나눈 대화 가운데 어느 하나 레빈의 흥미를 끈 것은 없었다. 그러나 성마른 지주의 결론은 깊이 생각해 볼 만했다."], ["Levin could not help recalling every word he had said, and in imagination amending his own replies.", "레빈은 그가 한 말을 한마디 한마디 떠올리며, 상상 속에서 자신의 대답을 고쳐 보지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["“Yes, I ought to have said to him: You say that our husbandry does not answer because the peasant hates improvements, and that they must be forced on him by authority.", "'그래, 그에게 이렇게 말했어야 했어. 당신은 우리 농사가 효과가 없는 건 농부가 개량을 싫어하기 때문이고, 그러니 권위로 그에게 개량을 강요해야 한다고 말하지."], ["If no system of husbandry answered at all without these improvements, you would be quite right.", "만약 이 개량 없이는 어떤 농사 방식도 도무지 효과가 없다면, 당신 말이 전적으로 옳을 거야."], ["But the only system that does answer is where laborer is working in accordance with his habits, just as on the old peasant’s land half-way here.", "하지만 효과가 있는 유일한 방식은, 여기 오는 길 중간의 그 늙은 농부 땅에서처럼 노동자가 자기 습관에 맞게 일하는 경우란 말이지."], ["Your and our general dissatisfaction with the system shows that either we are to blame or the laborers.", "그 방식에 대한 당신과 우리의 전반적인 불만은, 우리에게 잘못이 있거나 노동자들에게 잘못이 있다는 걸 보여 주는 거야."], ["We have gone our way—the European way—a long while, without asking ourselves about the qualities of our labor force.", "우리는 우리 노동력의 특성에 대해서는 자문해 보지도 않은 채, 우리 길을—유럽식 길을—오랫동안 걸어왔어."], ["Let us try to look upon the labor force not as an abstract force, but as the Russian peasant with his instincts, and we shall arrange our system of culture in accordance with that.", "노동력을 추상적인 힘이 아니라 자기 본능을 지닌 러시아 농부로 바라보도록 해 보세. 그러면 우리는 그에 맞게 우리 경작 방식을 짜게 될 테지."], ["Imagine, I ought to have said to him, that you have the same system as the old peasant has, that you have found means of making your laborers take an interest in the success of the work, and have found the happy mean in the way of improvements which they will admit, and you will, without exhausting the soil, get twice or three times the yield you got before.", "그에게 이렇게 말했어야 했어. 당신이 저 늙은 농부와 똑같은 방식을 갖추고 있다고, 당신이 노동자들로 하여금 일의 성공에 관심을 갖게 할 방도를 찾아냈고, 그들이 받아들일 만한 개량의 적절한 중용을 찾아냈다고 상상해 보라고."], ["Divide it in halves, give half as the share of labor, the surplus left you will be greater, and the share of labor will be greater too.", "그러면 당신은 토양을 고갈시키지 않고도 전에 거두던 수확량의 두세 배를 얻게 될 거야. 그걸 절반씩 나눠서 절반을 노동의 몫으로 주면, 당신에게 남는 잉여는 더 커질 테고, 노동의 몫 또한 더 커질 거야."], ["And to do this one must lower the standard of husbandry and interest the laborers in its success.", "그리고 이렇게 하려면 농사의 수준을 낮추고 노동자들이 그 성공에 관심을 갖게 해야 해. 어떻게 이걸 하느냐고?"], ["How to do this?—that’s a matter of detail; but undoubtedly it can be done.”", "그건 세부적인 문제지. 하지만 의심할 바 없이 해낼 수 있어.'"]], [["This idea threw Levin into a great excitement.", "이 생각은 레빈을 큰 흥분으로 몰아넣었다."], ["He did not sleep half the night, thinking over in detail the putting of his idea into practice.", "그는 자기 생각을 실행에 옮기는 방안을 세세히 궁리하느라 밤의 절반을 자지 못했다."], ["He had not intended to go away next day, but he now determined to go home early in the morning.", "그는 다음 날 떠날 생각이 없었지만, 이제 아침 일찍 집으로 돌아가기로 마음을 정했다."], ["Besides, the sister-in-law with her low-necked bodice aroused in him a feeling akin to shame and remorse for some utterly base action.", "게다가 가슴이 깊이 파인 보디스를 입은 그 처형은 그에게 무언가 더없이 천박한 짓을 저지른 듯한 수치심과 회한에 가까운 감정을 불러일으켰다."], ["Most important of all—he must get back without delay: he would have to make haste to put his new project to the peasants before the sowing of the winter wheat, so that the sowing might be undertaken on a new basis.", "무엇보다도 그는 지체 없이 돌아가야만 했다. 겨울밀 파종이 새로운 토대 위에서 이루어질 수 있도록, 파종 전에 서둘러 자신의 새 계획을 농부들에게 제시해야 했던 것이다."], ["He had made up his mind to revolutionize his whole system.", "그는 자신의 전체 방식을 혁신하기로 결심했다."]], [["# Chapter 29", "# Chapter 29"]], [["The carrying out of Levin’s plan presented many difficulties; but he struggled on, doing his utmost, and attained a result which, though not what he desired, was enough to enable him, without self-deception, to believe that the attempt was worth the trouble.", "레빈의 계획을 실행에 옮기는 데에는 여러 어려움이 따랐다. 하지만 그는 안간힘을 다해 분투했고, 비록 바라던 바는 아니었으나, 그 시도가 수고를 들일 만한 가치가 있었다고 자기기만 없이 믿을 수 있을 만큼의 결과는 거두었다."], ["One of the chief difficulties was that the process of cultivating the land was in full swing, that it was impossible to stop everything and begin it all again from the beginning, and the machine had to be mended while in motion.", "가장 큰 어려움 가운데 하나는, 토지 경작 과정이 한창 돌아가고 있어서 모든 것을 멈추고 처음부터 다시 시작하기란 불가능하다는 것, 그러니까 기계가 돌아가는 도중에 그것을 고쳐야 한다는 데 있었다."]], [["When on the evening that he arrived home he informed the bailiff of his plans, the latter with visible pleasure agreed with what he said so long as he was pointing out that all that had been done up to that time was stupid and useless.", "집에 도착한 날 저녁, 그가 관리인에게 자신의 계획을 알렸을 때, 관리인은 지금까지 해 온 모든 일이 어리석고 쓸모없었다고 지적하는 한에서는 눈에 띄게 흡족해하며 그의 말에 동의했다."], ["The bailiff said that he had said so a long while ago, but no heed had been paid him.", "관리인은 자기도 오래전부터 그렇게 말해 왔으나 아무도 귀를 기울이지 않았다고 했다."], ["But as for the proposal made by Levin—to take a part as shareholder with his laborers in each agricultural undertaking—at this the bailiff simply expressed a profound despondency, and offered no definite opinion, but began immediately talking of the urgent necessity of carrying the remaining sheaves of rye the next day, and of sending the men out for the second ploughing, so that Levin felt that this was not the time for discussing it.", "그러나 레빈이 내놓은 제안, 그러니까 각각의 농사일에 일꾼들과 더불어 동업자로서 한몫을 맡자는 제안에 대해서는, 관리인은 그저 깊은 낙담을 드러낼 뿐 분명한 의견을 내놓지 않았고, 곧장 다음 날 남은 호밀 단을 실어 날라야 할 긴급한 필요라든가, 두 번째 갈이를 위해 사람들을 내보내야 한다는 이야기를 꺼내기 시작했다. 그래서 레빈은 지금이 그것을 논할 때가 아니라고 느꼈다."]], [["On beginning to talk to the peasants about it, and making a proposition to cede them the land on new terms, he came into collision with the same great difficulty that they were so much absorbed by the current work of the day, that they had not time to consider the advantages and disadvantages of the proposed scheme.", "농부들에게 그 이야기를 꺼내며 새로운 조건으로 땅을 내주겠다고 제안하기 시작했을 때, 그는 똑같은 커다란 어려움에 부딪혔다. 그들은 그날그날의 당면한 일에 너무 골몰해 있어서, 제안된 방안의 이로움과 해로움을 곰곰이 따져 볼 겨를이 없었던 것이다."]], [["The simple-hearted Ivan, the cowherd, seemed completely to grasp Levin’s proposal—that he should with his family take a share of the profits of the cattle-yard—and he was in complete sympathy with the plan.", "순박한 마음을 가진 목동 이반은 레빈의 제안을, 그러니까 가족과 함께 가축 마당의 수익에서 한몫을 받자는 제안을 온전히 알아듣는 듯했고, 그 방안에 전적으로 공감했다."], ["But when Levin hinted at the future advantages, Ivan’s face expressed alarm and regret that he could not hear all he had to say, and he made haste to find himself some task that would admit of no delay: he either snatched up the fork to pitch the hay out of the pens, or ran to get water or to clear out the dung.", "그러나 레빈이 앞으로의 이로움을 넌지시 비치자, 이반의 얼굴에는 그가 하려는 말을 다 들을 수 없다는 데 대한 불안과 아쉬움이 떠올랐고, 그는 서둘러 도무지 미룰 수 없는 일거리를 스스로 찾아냈다. 우리에서 건초를 끄집어내려고 쇠스랑을 집어 들거나, 물을 길으러 달려가거나, 거름을 치우러 가는 식이었다."]], [["Another difficulty lay in the invincible disbelief of the peasant that a landowner’s object could be anything else than a desire to squeeze all he could out of them.", "또 다른 어려움은, 지주의 목적이라는 게 자기들에게서 짜낼 수 있는 만큼 짜내려는 욕심 말고 다른 무엇일 리 없다는 농부들의 꺾을 수 없는 불신에 있었다."], ["They were firmly convinced that his real aim (whatever he might say to them) would always be in what he did not say to them.", "그들은 (그가 무슨 말을 하든) 그의 진짜 속셈은 늘 그가 말하지 않은 데에 있으리라고 굳게 믿고 있었다."], ["And they themselves, in giving their opinion, said a great deal but never said what was their real object.", "그리고 그들 자신도 의견을 말할 때면 많은 말을 늘어놓으면서도 정작 자기들의 진짜 목적은 결코 말하지 않았다."], ["Moreover (Levin felt that the irascible landowner had been right) the peasants made their first and unalterable condition of any agreement whatever that they should not be forced to any new methods of tillage of any kind, nor to use new implements.", "게다가 (레빈은 그 성마른 지주의 말이 옳았다고 느꼈다) 농부들은 어떤 합의에서든 첫째가는, 그리고 바꿀 수 없는 조건으로, 어떤 종류의 새로운 경작 방식도 강요받지 않을 것, 새로운 농기구도 쓰지 않을 것을 내세웠다."], ["They agreed that the modern plough ploughed better, that the scarifier did the work more quickly, but they found thousands of reasons that made it out of the question for them to use either of them; and though he had accepted the conviction that he would have to lower the standard of cultivation, he felt sorry to give up improved methods, the advantages of which were so obvious.", "그들은 신식 쟁기가 더 잘 갈고 중경기가 일을 더 빨리 해낸다는 데에는 동의하면서도, 그 둘 중 어느 것도 도저히 쓸 수 없게 만드는 수천 가지 이유를 찾아냈다. 그리고 그는 경작 수준을 낮춰야 하리라는 확신을 받아들이기는 했으나, 이로움이 그토록 분명한 개량된 방식을 포기하는 것이 못내 아쉬웠다."], ["But in spite of all these difficulties he got his way, and by autumn the system was working, or at least so it seemed to him.", "그러나 이 모든 어려움에도 불구하고 그는 자기 뜻을 관철했고, 가을 무렵에는 그 제도가 돌아가고 있었다. 적어도 그에게는 그렇게 보였다."]], [["At first Levin had thought of giving up the whole farming of the land just as it was to the peasants, the laborers, and the bailiff on new conditions of partnership; but he was very soon convinced that this was impossible, and determined to divide it up.", "처음에 레빈은 땅의 농사 전체를 있는 그대로 새로운 동업 조건 아래 농부들과 일꾼들과 관리인에게 넘겨줄 생각이었다. 하지만 이내 그것이 불가능하다는 것을 깨닫고, 그것을 나누기로 마음먹었다."], ["The cattle-yard, the garden, hay fields, and arable land, divided into several parts, had to be made into separate lots.", "가축 마당과 정원, 건초밭, 그리고 여러 부분으로 나뉜 경작지는 각각 별개의 몫으로 만들어야 했다."], ["The simple-hearted cowherd, Ivan, who, Levin fancied, understood the matter better than any of them, collecting together a gang of workers to help him, principally of his own family, became a partner in the cattle-yard.", "순박한 마음을 가진 목동 이반은, 레빈이 보기에 그 누구보다도 이 일을 잘 이해하고 있었는데, 주로 자기 가족으로 이루어진 일꾼 패거리를 모아 가축 마당의 동업자가 되었다."], ["A distant part of the estate, a tract of waste land that had lain fallow for eight years, was with the help of the clever carpenter, Fyodor Ryezunov, taken by six families of peasants on new conditions of partnership, and the peasant Shuraev took the management of all the vegetable gardens on the same terms.", "영지의 외진 한쪽, 팔 년 동안 묵혀 둔 황무지 한 자락은 영리한 목수 표도르 라줄료노프의 도움으로 여섯 농가가 새로운 동업 조건 아래 맡았고, 농부 슈라예프는 같은 조건으로 채소밭 전체의 관리를 떠맡았다."], ["The remainder of the land was still worked on the old system, but these three associated partnerships were the first step to a new organization of the whole, and they completely took up Levin’s time.", "나머지 땅은 여전히 옛 방식대로 경작되었지만, 이 세 동업체는 전체를 새롭게 조직하기 위한 첫걸음이었고, 그것들이 레빈의 시간을 완전히 차지했다."]], [["It is true that in the cattle-yard things went no better than before, and Ivan strenuously opposed warm housing for the cows and butter made of fresh cream, affirming that cows require less food if kept cold, and that butter is more profitable made from sour cream, and he asked for wages just as under the old system, and took not the slightest interest in the fact that the money he received was not wages but an advance out of his future share in the profits.", "가축 마당에서는 사실 전보다 나아진 것이 하나도 없었다. 이반은 따뜻한 축사와 신선한 크림으로 만든 버터를 한사코 반대했고, 소는 차게 키워야 먹이가 덜 든다느니, 버터는 시큼한 크림으로 만들어야 더 이문이 남는다느니 우겨 댔으며, 옛 방식 그대로 품삯을 달라고 요구했고, 자기가 받는 돈이 품삯이 아니라 앞으로 받을 수익 몫에서 미리 받는 가불이라는 사실에는 털끝만큼의 관심도 보이지 않았다."]], [["It is true that Fyodor Ryezunov’s company did not plough over the ground twice before sowing, as had been agreed, justifying themselves on the plea that the time was too short.", "표도르 라줄료노프의 동업체는, 사실 합의한 대로 씨를 뿌리기 전에 땅을 두 번 갈지 않았고, 시간이 너무 빠듯했다는 핑계로 자기들을 정당화했다."], ["It is true that the peasants of the same company, though they had agreed to work the land on new conditions, always spoke of the land, not as held in partnership, but as rented for half the crop, and more than once the peasants and Ryezunov himself said to Levin, “If you would take a rent for the land, it would save you trouble, and we should be more free.”", "같은 동업체의 농부들은, 사실 새로운 조건으로 땅을 부치기로 합의해 놓고도 늘 그 땅을 동업으로 맡은 것이 아니라 수확의 절반을 내는 소작으로 빌린 것처럼 말했고, 농부들과 라줄료노프 본인은 한두 번이 아니게 레빈에게 이렇게 말했다. \"땅값을 받으시면, 나리께서도 수고를 더실 테고, 저희도 한결 홀가분할 텐데요.\""], ["Moreover the same peasants kept putting off, on various excuses, the building of a cattleyard and barn on the land as agreed upon, and delayed doing it till the winter.", "게다가 그 농부들은 합의한 대로 땅에 가축 마당과 헛간을 짓는 일을 갖가지 핑계로 자꾸 미루었고, 겨울이 될 때까지 질질 끌었다."]], [["It is true that Shuraev would have liked to let out the kitchen gardens he had undertaken in small lots to the peasants.", "슈라예프는, 사실 자기가 맡은 채소밭을 작은 구획으로 쪼개어 농부들에게 다시 빌려주고 싶어 했다."], ["He evidently quite misunderstood, and apparently intentionally misunderstood, the conditions upon which the land had been given to him.", "그는 분명 땅이 자기에게 주어진 조건을 완전히 오해하고 있었고, 보아하니 일부러 오해하고 있는 것 같았다."]], [["Often, too, talking to the peasants and explaining to them all the advantages of the plan, Levin felt that the peasants heard nothing but the sound of his voice, and were firmly resolved, whatever he might say, not to let themselves be taken in.", "또한 농부들에게 그 방안의 모든 이로움을 이야기하고 설명하는 동안, 레빈은 종종 농부들이 자기 목소리의 소리 말고는 아무것도 듣고 있지 않으며, 그가 무슨 말을 하든 결코 속아 넘어가지 않겠다고 굳게 작정하고 있음을 느꼈다."], ["He felt this especially when he talked to the cleverest of the peasants, Ryezunov, and detected the gleam in Ryezunov’s eyes which showed so plainly both ironical amusement at Levin, and the firm conviction that, if anyone were to be taken in, it would not be he, Ryezunov.", "농부들 가운데 가장 영리한 라줄료노프와 이야기할 때 그는 특히 이것을 느꼈고, 레빈을 향한 비웃는 듯한 즐거움과, 만약 누군가 속아 넘어간다면 그건 자기, 그러니까 라줄료노프는 아닐 것이라는 굳은 확신을 그토록 분명히 드러내는 그의 눈빛을 알아챘다."], ["But in spite of all this Levin thought the system worked, and that by keeping accounts strictly and insisting on his own way, he would prove to them in the future the advantages of the arrangement, and then the system would go of itself.", "그러나 이 모든 것에도 불구하고 레빈은 그 제도가 돌아가고 있다고, 그리고 장부를 엄격하게 기록하고 자기 방식을 끝까지 고집하면, 앞으로 그들에게 이 방식의 이로움을 입증하게 될 것이고, 그러면 그 제도가 저절로 굴러가리라고 생각했다."]], [["These matters, together with the management of the land still left on his hands, and the indoor work over his book, so engrossed Levin the whole summer that he scarcely ever went out shooting.", "이런 일들이, 여전히 그의 손에 남아 있는 토지 경영이며 책을 쓰는 실내 작업과 더불어, 여름 내내 레빈을 어찌나 사로잡았던지 그는 사냥을 거의 나가지 않았다."], ["At the end of August he heard that the Oblonskys had gone away to Moscow, from their servant who brought back the side-saddle.", "팔월 말에 그는 부인용 안장을 돌려주러 온 하인에게서 오블론스키 집안이 모스크바로 떠났다는 소식을 들었다."], ["He felt that in not answering Darya Alexandrovna’s letter he had by his rudeness, of which he could not think without a flush of shame, burned his ships, and that he would never go and see them again.", "그는 다리아 알렉산드로브나의 편지에 답하지 않음으로써, 떠올릴 때마다 부끄러움으로 얼굴이 화끈거리는 그 무례함으로써, 스스로 배수진을 불태워 버렸으며, 다시는 그들을 보러 가지 못하리라고 느꼈다."], ["He had been just as rude with the Sviazhskys, leaving them without saying good-bye.", "스비아시스키 집안에게도 그는 꼭 그만큼 무례하게 굴어, 작별 인사도 없이 떠나 버렸다."], ["But he would never go to see them again either.", "하지만 그들 또한 다시는 보러 가지 않을 것이었다."], ["He did not care about that now.", "이제 그런 것은 아무래도 상관없었다."], ["The business of reorganizing the farming of his land absorbed him as completely as though there would never be anything else in his life.", "자기 땅의 농사를 새로 조직하는 일이, 마치 그의 인생에 다른 무엇도 결코 없을 것처럼 그를 온전히 사로잡았다."], ["He read the books lent him by Sviazhsky, and copying out what he had not got, he read both the economic and socialistic books on the subject, but, as he had anticipated, found nothing bearing on the scheme he had undertaken.", "그는 스비아시스키가 빌려준 책들을 읽었고, 갖고 있지 않은 것은 베껴 쓰면서, 그 주제에 관한 경제학 책과 사회주의 책을 모두 읽었지만, 예상했던 대로 자기가 떠맡은 방안과 관계되는 것은 아무것도 찾아내지 못했다."], ["In the books on political economy—in Mill, for instance, whom he studied first with great ardor, hoping every minute to find an answer to the questions that were engrossing him—he found laws deduced from the condition of land culture in Europe; but he did not see why these laws, which did not apply in Russia, must be general.", "정치경제학 책들에서는, 이를테면 그가 자기를 사로잡은 물음들에 대한 답을 매 순간 찾아내리라는 희망으로 처음에 큰 열정을 가지고 연구했던 밀의 책에서는, 유럽의 토지 경작 상황에서 연역된 법칙들을 발견했다. 하지만 그는 러시아에는 적용되지 않는 그 법칙들이 어째서 보편적이어야 하는지 알 수 없었다."], ["He saw just the same thing in the socialistic books: either they were the beautiful but impracticable fantasies which had fascinated him when he was a student, or they were attempts at improving, rectifying the economic position in which Europe was placed, with which the system of land tenure in Russia had nothing in common.", "그는 사회주의 책들에서도 꼭 같은 것을 보았다. 그것들은 그가 학생 시절에 매혹되었던 아름답지만 실행 불가능한 환상이거나, 아니면 유럽이 처한 경제적 처지를 개선하고 바로잡으려는 시도들이었는데, 러시아의 토지 보유 제도는 그것과 아무런 공통점도 없었다."], ["Political economy told him that the laws by which the wealth of Europe had been developed, and was developing, were universal and unvarying.", "정치경제학은 그에게 유럽의 부가 발전해 왔고 또 발전하고 있는 법칙들이 보편적이고 불변한다고 말했다."], ["Socialism told him that development along these lines leads to ruin.", "사회주의는 그에게 이런 노선을 따른 발전이 파멸로 이어진다고 말했다."], ["And neither of them gave an answer, or even a hint, in reply to the question what he, Levin, and all the Russian peasants and landowners, were to do with their millions of hands and millions of acres, to make them as productive as possible for the common weal.", "그리고 둘 중 어느 것도, 그러니까 그 자신 레빈이, 그리고 모든 러시아 농부들과 지주들이, 수백만의 일손과 수백만 에이커의 땅을 공공의 이익을 위해 되도록 생산적으로 만들려면 어떻게 해야 하는가 하는 물음에는, 답은커녕 실마리조차 주지 못했다."]], [["Having once taken the subject up, he read conscientiously everything bearing on it, and intended in the autumn to go abroad to study land systems on the spot, in order that he might not on this question be confronted with what so often met him on various subjects. Often, just as he was beginning to understand the idea in the mind of anyone he was talking to, and was beginning to explain his own, he would suddenly be told: “But Kauffmann, but Jones, but Dubois, but Michelli? You haven’t read them: they’ve thrashed that question out thoroughly.”", "일단 이 주제를 붙들고 나자, 그는 그것과 관계되는 모든 것을 성실하게 읽었고, 가을에는 외국으로 나가 토지 제도를 현장에서 연구할 작정이었다. 여러 주제에서 그토록 자주 부딪히곤 했던 일을 이 문제에서만큼은 다시 마주치지 않기 위해서였다. 그가 이야기 나누는 상대의 머릿속에 든 생각을 막 이해하기 시작하고 자기 생각을 막 설명하려고 할 때면, 종종 그는 느닷없이 이런 말을 듣곤 했다. \"하지만 카우프만은요? 존스는요? 뒤부아는요? 미첼리는요? 그것들을 안 읽으셨군요. 그 사람들이 그 문제를 철저하게 파헤쳐 놓았는데요.\""]], [["He saw now distinctly that Kauffmann and Michelli had nothing to tell him.", "이제 그는 카우프만과 미첼리가 자기에게 들려줄 것이 아무것도 없다는 것을 분명히 알았다."], ["He knew what he wanted.", "그는 자기가 무엇을 원하는지 알았다."], ["He saw that Russia has splendid land, splendid laborers, and that in certain cases, as at the peasant’s on the way to Sviazhsky’s, the produce raised by the laborers and the land is great—in the majority of cases when capital is applied in the European way the produce is small, and that this simply arises from the fact that the laborers want to work and work well only in their own peculiar way, and that this antagonism is not incidental but invariable, and has its roots in the national spirit.", "그는 러시아에 훌륭한 땅과 훌륭한 일꾼들이 있다는 것을, 그리고 어떤 경우에는, 가령 스비아시스키 집으로 가는 길에 들렀던 농부의 경우처럼, 일꾼들과 땅이 길러 내는 산물이 풍성하다는 것을 알았다. 하지만 자본이 유럽식으로 투입되는 대다수의 경우에는 산물이 빈약하다는 것, 그리고 이것은 단지 일꾼들이 오직 자기들 특유의 방식으로만 일하고 싶어 하고 또 잘하려 한다는 사실에서 비롯된다는 것, 그리고 이 적대는 우연한 것이 아니라 변함없는 것이며 민족정신에 그 뿌리를 두고 있다는 것을 알았다."], ["He thought that the Russian people whose task it was to colonize and cultivate vast tracts of unoccupied land, consciously adhered, till all their land was occupied, to the methods suitable to their purpose, and that their methods were by no means so bad as was generally supposed.", "그는 광대한 미점령지를 개척하고 경작하는 것을 과업으로 삼아 온 러시아 민족이, 자기들의 모든 땅이 점령될 때까지는 그 목적에 알맞은 방식을 의식적으로 고수해 왔으며, 그들의 방식이 흔히 생각되는 것만큼 결코 나쁘지 않다고 생각했다."], ["And he wanted to prove this theoretically in his book and practically on his land.", "그리고 그는 이것을 책 속에서 이론적으로, 또 자기 땅에서 실천적으로 입증하고 싶었다."]], [["# Chapter 30", "# Chapter 30"]], [["At the end of September the timber had been carted for building the cattleyard on the land that had been allotted to the association of peasants, and the butter from the cows was sold and the profits divided.", "9월 말에는 농민 조합에 분배된 토지 위에 가축우리를 짓기 위한 목재가 실려 왔고, 젖소에서 나온 버터가 팔려 그 이익이 분배되었다."], ["In practice the system worked capitally, or, at least, so it seemed to Levin.", "실제로 이 제도는 훌륭하게 돌아갔다. 적어도 레빈에게는 그렇게 보였다."], ["In order to work out the whole subject theoretically and to complete his book, which, in Levin’s daydreams, was not merely to effect a revolution in political economy, but to annihilate that science entirely and to lay the foundation of a new science of the relation of the people to the soil, all that was left to do was to make a tour abroad, and to study on the spot all that had been done in the same direction, and to collect conclusive evidence that all that had been done there was not what was wanted.", "이 주제 전체를 이론적으로 정리하고 자기 책을 완성하기 위해—레빈의 공상 속에서 그 책은 단지 정치경제학에 혁명을 일으키는 데 그치지 않고 그 학문을 송두리째 절멸시키고 민중과 토지의 관계에 관한 새로운 학문의 토대를 놓을 것이었다—이제 남은 일이라곤 외국을 한 바퀴 둘러보며 같은 방향으로 행해진 모든 것을 현장에서 연구하고, 그곳에서 이루어진 모든 것이 정작 필요한 것이 아니었음을 입증할 결정적인 증거를 수집하는 일뿐이었다."], ["Levin was only waiting for the delivery of his wheat to receive the money for it and go abroad.", "레빈은 밀이 인도되어 그 대금을 받고 외국으로 떠나기만을 기다리고 있었다."], ["But the rains began, preventing the harvesting of the corn and potatoes left in the fields, and putting a stop to all work, even to the delivery of the wheat.", "그런데 비가 내리기 시작해 밭에 남아 있던 곡식과 감자의 수확을 가로막았고, 밀의 인도까지 포함한 모든 작업을 중단시켰다."]], [["The mud was impassable along the roads; two mills were carried away, and the weather got worse and worse.", "길마다 진창이 되어 다닐 수가 없었고, 물방앗간 두 곳이 떠내려갔으며, 날씨는 점점 더 나빠졌다."]], [["On the 30th of September the sun came out in the morning, and hoping for fine weather, Levin began making final preparations for his journey.", "9월 30일 아침에 해가 떴고, 날이 갤 것을 기대하며 레빈은 여행을 위한 마지막 준비를 시작했다."], ["He gave orders for the wheat to be delivered, sent the bailiff to the merchant to get the money owing him, and went out himself to give some final directions on the estate before setting off.", "그는 밀을 인도하라고 지시했고, 자기에게 갚을 돈을 받아 오라고 관리인을 상인에게 보냈으며, 출발하기 전에 영지에 몇 가지 마지막 지시를 내리려고 직접 나갔다."]], [["Having finished all his business, soaked through with the streams of water which kept running down the leather behind his neck and his gaiters, but in the keenest and most confident temper, Levin returned homewards in the evening.", "모든 일을 마치고, 목덜미 뒤쪽의 가죽과 각반을 타고 끊임없이 흘러내리는 물줄기에 흠뻑 젖었으면서도 더없이 열정적이고 자신감에 찬 기분으로, 레빈은 저녁 무렵 집으로 돌아왔다. 저녁이 되자 날씨는 그 어느 때보다 더 나빠졌다."], ["The weather had become worse than ever towards evening; the hail lashed the drenched mare so cruelly that she went along sideways, shaking her head and ears; but Levin was all right under his hood, and he looked cheerfully about him at the muddy streams running under the wheels, at the drops hanging on every bare twig, at the whiteness of the patch of unmelted hailstones on the planks of the bridge, at the thick layer of still juicy, fleshy leaves that lay heaped up about the stripped elm-tree.", "우박이 흠뻑 젖은 암말을 어찌나 모질게 후려치는지 말은 고개와 귀를 흔들며 옆걸음으로 나아갔다. 그러나 레빈은 마차의 덮개 아래에서 멀쩡했고, 바퀴 밑으로 흘러가는 진흙탕 물줄기며, 헐벗은 잔가지마다 매달린 물방울이며, 다리의 널빤지 위에 녹지 않고 하얗게 남은 우박 자국이며, 잎이 떨어진 느릅나무 둘레에 수북이 쌓인 채 아직도 물기가 도는 도톰한 잎사귀를 즐겁게 둘러보았다."], ["In spite of the gloominess of nature around him, he felt peculiarly eager.", "그를 둘러싼 자연의 음울함에도 불구하고 그는 유난히 들떠 있는 기분이었다."], ["The talks he had been having with the peasants in the further village had shown that they were beginning to get used to their new position.", "더 먼 마을에서 농민들과 나눈 이야기를 통해 그들이 새로운 처지에 익숙해지기 시작했음을 알 수 있었다."], ["The old servant to whose hut he had gone to get dry evidently approved of Levin’s plan, and of his own accord proposed to enter the partnership by the purchase of cattle.", "그가 몸을 말리려고 들어갔던 오두막의 늙은 하인은 레빈의 계획을 분명히 찬성했고, 자기가 먼저 나서서 가축을 사들여 조합에 가입하겠다고 했다."]], [["“I have only to go stubbornly on towards my aim, and I shall attain my end,”", "\"그저 내 목표를 향해 끈기 있게 나아가기만 하면 돼."], ["thought Levin; “and it’s something to work and take trouble for.", "그러면 끝내 목적을 이루는 거지.\" 레빈은 생각했다."], ["This is not a matter of myself individually; the question of the public welfare comes into it.", "\"그리고 이건 애써 노력하고 수고할 만한 가치가 있는 일이야. 이건 나 개인만의 문제가 아니야."], ["The whole system of culture, the chief element in the condition of the people, must be completely transformed.", "공공의 복리라는 문제가 여기 걸려 있어. 민중의 처지를 좌우하는 가장 중요한 요소인 농경 제도 전체가 완전히 탈바꿈해야 해."], ["Instead of poverty, general prosperity and content; instead of hostility, harmony and unity of interests.", "빈곤 대신 전반적인 번영과 만족을, 적대 대신 화합과 이해의 일치를."], ["In short, a bloodless revolution, but a revolution of the greatest magnitude, beginning in the little circle of our district, then the province, then Russia, the whole world.", "한마디로, 피 흘리지 않는 혁명이지만 더없이 거대한 규모의 혁명이지. 우리 군의 작은 모임에서 시작해서 그다음엔 현, 그다음엔 러시아, 나아가 온 세계로."], ["Because a just idea cannot but be fruitful.", "정의로운 사상은 결실을 맺지 않을 수 없으니까."], ["Yes, it’s an aim worth working for.", "그래, 이건 애써 일할 만한 목표야."], ["And its being me, Kostya Levin, who went to a ball in a black tie, and was refused by the Shtcherbatskaya girl, and who was intrinsically such a pitiful, worthless creature—that proves nothing; I feel sure Franklin felt just as worthless, and he too had no faith in himself, thinking of himself as a whole.", "그리고 그 일을 하는 사람이 검은 넥타이를 매고 무도회에 갔다가 셰르바츠키 집안 딸에게 퇴짜를 맞은, 본질적으로 그토록 가련하고 보잘것없는 인간인 나, 코스챠 레빈이라는 것—그건 아무것도 증명하지 못해. 프랭클린도 분명 똑같이 자신을 보잘것없다고 느꼈을 거야."], ["That means nothing.", "그 사람도 자기 자신을 전체로 놓고 생각하면 스스로를 믿지 못했겠지."], ["And he too, most likely, had an Agafea Mihalovna to whom he confided his secrets.”", "그래도 아무 상관 없어. 그리고 그 사람한테도 십중팔구 비밀을 털어놓을 아가피야 미하일로브나 같은 사람이 있었을 거야.\""]], [["Musing on such thoughts Levin reached home in the darkness.", "이런 생각에 잠긴 채 레빈은 어둠 속에서 집에 다다랐다."]], [["The bailiff, who had been to the merchant, had come back and brought part of the money for the wheat.", "상인에게 다녀온 관리인이 돌아와 밀 대금의 일부를 가져왔다."], ["An agreement had been made with the old servant, and on the road the bailiff had learned that everywhere the corn was still standing in the fields, so that his one hundred and sixty shocks that had not been carried were nothing in comparison with the losses of others.", "늙은 하인과는 합의가 이루어졌고, 관리인은 오는 길에 어디나 곡식이 아직 밭에 서 있다는 것을 알게 되었다. 그래서 미처 거둬들이지 못한 레빈의 곡식 단 백예순 더미는 다른 사람들의 손실에 비하면 아무것도 아니었다."]], [["After dinner Levin was sitting, as he usually did, in an easy chair with a book, and as he read he went on thinking of the journey before him in connection with his book.", "저녁 식사를 마친 뒤 레빈은 여느 때처럼 책을 들고 안락의자에 앉았고, 책을 읽으면서 자기 책과 관련지어 눈앞에 닥친 여행에 관해 계속 생각했다."], ["Today all the significance of his book rose before him with special distinctness, and whole periods ranged themselves in his mind in illustration of his theories.", "오늘은 자기 책의 의의 전체가 유난히 뚜렷하게 떠올랐고, 자기 이론을 뒷받침하는 문장 전체가 머릿속에 줄지어 정렬되었다."], ["“I must write that down,”", "'이걸 적어 두어야겠어.'"], ["he thought.", "그는 생각했다."], ["“That ought to form a brief introduction, which I thought unnecessary before.”", "'이걸로 짤막한 서문을 만들면 되겠어. 전에는 필요 없다고 여겼던 건데.'"], ["He got up to go to his writing-table, and Laska, lying at his feet, got up too, stretching and looking at him as though to inquire where to go.", "그는 책상으로 가려고 일어섰고, 발치에 누워 있던 라스카도 일어나 기지개를 켜며 어디로 가는지 묻기라도 하듯 그를 쳐다보았다."], ["But he had not time to write it down, for the head peasants had come round, and Levin went out into the hall to them.", "그러나 그는 그것을 적어 둘 틈이 없었다. 마을 우두머리 농민들이 찾아와, 레빈이 그들을 맞으러 현관으로 나간 것이다."]], [["After his levee, that is to say, giving directions about the labors of the next day, and seeing all the peasants who had business with him, Levin went back to his study and sat down to work.", "그렇게 알현을 마친 뒤, 그러니까 이튿날의 작업에 관해 지시를 내리고 자기와 볼일이 있는 농민들을 모두 만나고 나서, 레빈은 서재로 돌아와 일을 하려고 자리에 앉았다."]], [["Laska lay under the table; Agafea Mihalovna settled herself in her place with her stocking.", "라스카는 탁자 밑에 누웠고, 아가피야 미하일로브나는 양말 뜨개질감을 들고 제자리에 자리를 잡았다."]], [["After writing for a little while, Levin suddenly thought with exceptional vividness of Kitty, her refusal, and their last meeting.", "잠깐 글을 쓰던 레빈은 별안간 유난히 생생하게 키티를, 그녀의 거절을, 그리고 그들의 마지막 만남을 떠올렸다."], ["He got up and began walking about the room.", "그는 일어나 방 안을 거닐기 시작했다."]], [["“What’s the use of being dreary?”", "\"뭣 하러 그렇게 울적해하세요?\""], ["said Agafea Mihalovna.", "아가피야 미하일로브나가 말했다."], ["“Come, why do you stay on at home?", "\"자, 왜 집에만 붙어 계셔요?"], ["You ought to go to some warm springs, especially now you’re ready for the journey.”", "어디 따뜻한 온천에라도 가셔야지요, 더구나 이렇게 여행 채비도 다 해 두셨는데.\""]], [["“Well, I am going away the day after tomorrow, Agafea Mihalovna; I must finish my work.”", "\"그게, 내일모레는 떠날 거예요, 아가피야 미하일로브나. 일을 끝내야 해서요.\""]], [["“There, there, your work, you say!", "\"원, 원, 일이라고 하시네요!"], ["As if you hadn’t done enough for the peasants!", "마치 농민들을 위해 할 만큼 안 하셨다는 것처럼요! 그래, 지금도 사람들이 '나리께서 그 덕에 황제 폐하한테서 무슨 훈장이라도 받으시겠다'고들 하는걸요."], ["Why, as ’tis, they’re saying, ‘Your master will be getting some honor from the Tsar for it.’ Indeed and it is a strange thing; why need you worry about the peasants?”", "참말 이상한 일이지요. 뭣 하러 농민들 일로 애를 태우셔요?\""]], [["“I’m not worrying about them; I’m doing it for my own good.”", "\"애를 태우는 게 아니에요. 내 자신을 위해서 하는 일이에요.\""]], [["Agafea Mihalovna knew every detail of Levin’s plans for his land.", "아가피야 미하일로브나는 자기 땅에 대한 레빈의 계획을 속속들이 알고 있었다."], ["Levin often put his views before her in all their complexity, and not uncommonly he argued with her and did not agree with her comments.", "레빈은 자주 그 복잡한 견해를 그녀 앞에 펼쳐 놓았고, 그녀와 논쟁을 벌이며 그녀의 의견에 동의하지 않는 일도 드물지 않았다."], ["But on this occasion she entirely misinterpreted what he had said.", "그러나 이번에는 그녀가 그의 말을 완전히 잘못 알아들었다."]], [["“Of one’s soul’s salvation we all know and must think before all else,”", "\"제 영혼의 구원이야 우리 모두가 알고, 또 무엇보다 먼저 생각해야 할 일이지요.\""], ["she said with a sigh.", "그녀가 한숨을 쉬며 말했다."], ["“Parfen Denisitch now, for all he was no scholar, he died a death that God grant everyone of us the like,”", "\"파르펜 데니시치만 해도, 배운 사람은 아니었지만, 하느님이 우리 모두에게 그런 죽음을 내려 주십사 할 만큼 좋은 임종을 맞았는걸요.\""], ["she said, referring to a servant who had died recently.", "그녀는 최근에 죽은 한 하인을 두고 말했다."], ["“Took the sacrament and all.”", "\"성찬도 받고 다 했지요.\""]], [["“That’s not what I mean,”", "\"내 말은 그런 게 아니에요.\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["“I mean that I’m acting for my own advantage.", "\"내 말은 내 이익을 위해 하는 일이라는 거예요."], ["It’s all the better for me if the peasants do their work better.”", "농민들이 일을 더 잘하면 내게도 그만큼 더 좋은 거니까요.\""]], [["“Well, whatever you do, if he’s a lazy good-for-nought, everything’ll be at sixes and sevens.", "\"그래요, 나리께서 뭘 하시든, 그자가 게으른 쓸모없는 놈이면 모든 게 엉망진창이 되고 마는 법이에요."], ["If he has a conscience, he’ll work, and if not, there’s no doing anything.”", "양심이 있는 자면 일을 하고, 없는 자면 아무것도 안 되지요.\""]], [["“Oh, come, you say yourself Ivan has begun looking after the cattle better.”", "\"이런, 당신도 이반이 가축을 더 잘 돌보기 시작했다고 직접 말했잖아요.\""]], [["“All I say is,”", "\"제가 드리는 말씀은 그저,\" 아가피야 미하일로브나가 대답했다."], ["answered Agafea Mihalovna, evidently not speaking at random, but in strict sequence of idea, “that you ought to get married, that’s what I say.”", "어림짐작으로 하는 말이 아니라 분명히 생각의 줄기를 따라 하는 말이었다. \"나리께서 장가를 드셔야 한다는 거예요, 그게 제가 드리는 말씀이지요.\""]], [["Agafea Mihalovna’s allusion to the very subject he had only just been thinking about, hurt and stung him.", "마침 방금 전까지 생각하고 있던 바로 그 화제를 아가피야 미하일로브나가 넌지시 건드리자, 그는 마음이 상하고 따끔했다."], ["Levin scowled, and without answering her, he sat down again to his work, repeating to himself all that he had been thinking of the real significance of that work.", "레빈은 얼굴을 찌푸렸고, 그녀에게 대답도 하지 않은 채 다시 일감 앞에 앉아, 그 일의 진정한 의의에 관해 생각하던 모든 것을 스스로에게 되뇌었다."], ["Only at intervals he listened in the stillness to the click of Agafea Mihalovna’s needles, and recollecting what he did not want to remember, he frowned again.", "다만 이따금 그는 고요 속에서 아가피야 미하일로브나의 뜨개바늘이 부딪치는 소리에 귀를 기울였고, 떠올리고 싶지 않던 것을 떠올리고는 다시 미간을 찌푸렸다."]], [["At nine o’clock they heard the bell and the faint vibration of a carriage over the mud.", "아홉 시에 그들은 초인종 소리와, 진창 위를 달려오는 마차의 희미한 진동을 들었다."]], [["“Well, here’s visitors come to us, and you won’t be dull,”", "\"자, 손님이 오셨네요, 이제 따분하실 일은 없겠어요.\""], ["said Agafea Mihalovna, getting up and going to the door.", "아가피야 미하일로브나가 일어나 문 쪽으로 가며 말했다."], ["But Levin overtook her.", "그러나 레빈이 그녀를 앞질렀다."], ["His work was not going well now, and he was glad of a visitor, whoever it might be.", "이제 그의 일은 잘 풀리지 않고 있었고, 그는 그게 누구든 손님이 온 것이 반가웠다."]], [["# Chapter 31", "# Chapter 31"]], [["Running halfway down the staircase, Levin caught a sound he knew, a familiar cough in the hall.", "계단을 절반쯤 내려가던 레빈은 귀에 익은 소리, 현관에서 들려오는 낯익은 기침 소리를 들었다."], ["But he heard it indistinctly through the sound of his own footsteps, and hoped he was mistaken.", "하지만 자기 발소리에 묻혀 또렷이 들리지 않았기에, 그는 자기가 잘못 들었기를 바랐다."], ["Then he caught sight of a long, bony, familiar figure, and now it seemed there was no possibility of mistake; and yet he still went on hoping that this tall man taking off his fur cloak and coughing was not his brother Nikolay.", "그러다 길쭉하고 앙상한 낯익은 형체가 눈에 들어왔고, 이제는 더 이상 잘못 본 게 아닌 듯했다. 그래도 그는 모피 외투를 벗으며 기침을 해 대는 이 키 큰 사내가 자기 형 니콜라이가 아니기를 여전히 바랐다."]], [["Levin loved his brother, but being with him was always a torture.", "레빈은 형을 사랑했지만, 형과 함께 있는 것은 언제나 고통이었다."], ["Just now, when Levin, under the influence of the thoughts that had come to him, and Agafea Mihalovna’s hint, was in a troubled and uncertain humor, the meeting with his brother that he had to face seemed particularly difficult.", "더구나 지금, 자기에게 떠오른 생각들과 아가피야 미하일로브나의 암시에 영향을 받아 마음이 어지럽고 갈피를 잡지 못하는 상태였던 레빈에게, 형과 마주해야 한다는 것은 유독 견디기 어려운 일로 다가왔다."], ["Instead of a lively, healthy visitor, some outsider who would, he hoped, cheer him up in his uncertain humor, he had to see his brother, who knew him through and through, who would call forth all the thoughts nearest his heart, would force him to show himself fully.", "어수선한 자기 기분을 북돋아 줄 만한, 활기차고 건강한 손님, 그러니까 누군가 바깥사람을 만나기를 바랐건만, 그는 자기를 속속들이 꿰뚫어 보는 형, 마음 깊은 곳에 자리한 온갖 생각들을 불러내고 자신을 남김없이 드러내도록 다그칠 형을 마주해야 했다."], ["And that he was not disposed to do.", "그러나 그는 그러고 싶지 않았다."]], [["Angry with himself for so base a feeling, Levin ran into the hall; as soon as he had seen his brother close, this feeling of selfish disappointment vanished instantly and was replaced by pity.", "이토록 비열한 감정에 스스로 화가 난 레빈은 현관으로 뛰어 내려갔다. 그리고 형을 가까이에서 본 순간, 이기적인 실망의 감정은 순식간에 사라지고 그 자리를 연민이 채웠다."], ["Terrible as his brother Nikolay had been before in his emaciation and sickliness, now he looked still more emaciated, still more wasted.", "형 니콜라이는 전부터 수척하고 병약했지만, 지금은 더욱 야위고 더욱 쇠잔해 보였다."], ["He was a skeleton covered with skin.", "가죽만 남은 해골이나 다름없었다."]], [["He stood in the hall, jerking his long thin neck, and pulling the scarf off it, and smiled a strange and pitiful smile.", "그는 현관에 서서, 가늘고 긴 목을 비틀어 그것을 감은 목도리를 풀며, 기이하고 가련한 미소를 지었다."], ["When he saw that smile, submissive and humble, Levin felt something clutching at his throat.", "순종적이고 비굴한 그 미소를 보았을 때, 레빈은 무언가가 목구멍을 옥죄는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["“You see, I’ve come to you,”", "\"보다시피, 너를 찾아왔다.\""], ["said Nikolay in a thick voice, never for one second taking his eyes off his brother’s face.", "니콜라이가 동생의 얼굴에서 단 한순간도 눈을 떼지 않은 채 탁한 목소리로 말했다."], ["“I’ve been meaning to a long while, but I’ve been unwell all the time.", "\"오래전부터 오려고 했는데, 줄곧 몸이 안 좋았어."], ["Now I’m ever so much better,”", "이젠 훨씬 나아졌단다.\""], ["he said, rubbing his beard with his big thin hands.", "그는 크고 가느다란 손으로 턱수염을 쓸며 말했다."]], [["“Yes, yes!” answered Levin.", "\"그래요, 그래요!\""], ["And he felt still more frightened when, kissing him, he felt with his lips the dryness of his brother’s skin and saw close to him his big eyes, full of a strange light.", "레빈이 대답했다. 그리고 그에게 입을 맞추며 형의 피부가 메마른 것을 입술로 느끼고, 기이한 빛으로 가득한 그 큰 눈을 코앞에서 보았을 때, 그는 더욱 두려워졌다."]], [["A few weeks before, Konstantin Levin had written to his brother that through the sale of the small part of the property, that had remained undivided, there was a sum of about two thousand roubles to come to him as his share.", "몇 주 전에 콘스탄틴 레빈은 형에게, 나누지 않고 남겨 두었던 작은 영지를 처분하여 형의 몫으로 2천 루블가량의 돈이 돌아갈 것이라고 편지를 써 보냈었다."]], [["Nikolay said that he had come now to take this money and, what was more important, to stay a while in the old nest, to get in touch with the earth, so as to renew his strength like the heroes of old for the work that lay before him.", "니콜라이는 지금 이 돈을 받으러 왔으며, 더 중요하게는 옛 둥지에서 얼마간 머물면서 대지와 다시 닿아, 옛 영웅들처럼 앞에 놓인 일을 위해 기력을 새로이 하려고 왔노라고 말했다."], ["In spite of his exaggerated stoop, and the emaciation that was so striking from his height, his movements were as rapid and abrupt as ever.", "지나치게 굽은 등과 큰 키 때문에 더욱 두드러져 보이는 여윈 몸에도 불구하고, 그의 동작은 여전히 빠르고 급작스러웠다."], ["Levin led him into his study.", "레빈은 그를 서재로 안내했다."]], [["His brother dressed with particular care—a thing he never used to do—combed his scanty, lank hair, and, smiling, went upstairs.", "형은 유난히 정성 들여 옷매무새를 가다듬었는데—전에는 한 번도 하지 않던 일이었다—숱이 적고 축 늘어진 머리를 빗고는, 미소를 지으며 위층으로 올라갔다."]], [["He was in the most affectionate and good-humored mood, just as Levin often remembered him in childhood.", "그는 더없이 다정하고 기분 좋은 상태였으니, 레빈이 어린 시절의 형을 떠올릴 때면 흔히 기억하던 바로 그 모습이었다."], ["He even referred to Sergey Ivanovitch without rancor.", "그는 세르게이 이바노비치 이야기까지 원망 없이 꺼냈다."], ["When he saw Agafea Mihalovna, he made jokes with her and asked after the old servants.", "아가피야 미하일로브나를 보았을 때는 그녀에게 농을 건네며 옛 하인들의 안부를 물었다."], ["The news of the death of Parfen Denisitch made a painful impression on him.", "파르펜 데니시치가 죽었다는 소식은 그에게 고통스러운 인상을 남겼다."], ["A look of fear crossed his face, but he regained his serenity immediately.", "두려움의 빛이 그의 얼굴을 스쳤지만, 그는 곧 평정을 되찾았다."]], [["“Of course he was quite old,”", "\"하긴 꽤 늙었으니까.\""], ["he said, and changed the subject.", "그는 이렇게 말하고는 화제를 돌렸다."], ["“Well, I’ll spend a month or two with you, and then I’m off to Moscow.", "\"자, 너랑 한두 달 지내다가 모스크바로 떠날 생각이야. 그거 아니?"], ["Do you know, Myakov has promised me a place there, and I’m going into the service.", "먀코프가 거기 일자리를 약속해 줬거든. 관청에 들어갈 거야."], ["Now I’m going to arrange my life quite differently,”", "이제 내 인생을 완전히 다르게 꾸려 갈 거란다.\""], ["he went on.", "그가 말을 이었다."], ["“You know I got rid of that woman.”", "\"그 여자랑은 헤어졌어, 알지?\""]], [["“Marya Nikolaevna?", "\"마리야 니콜라예브나요?"], ["Why, what for?”", "아니, 왜요?\""]], [["“Oh, she was a horrid woman!", "\"아, 끔찍한 여자였어!"], ["She caused me all sorts of worries.”", "온갖 골칫거리를 다 안겨 줬지.\""], ["But he did not say what the annoyances were.", "하지만 그는 그 골칫거리가 무엇이었는지는 말하지 않았다."], ["He could not say that he had cast off Marya Nikolaevna because the tea was weak, and, above all, because she would look after him, as though he were an invalid.", "차가 묽다는 이유로, 그리고 무엇보다 그녀가 자기를 병자라도 되는 양 돌보려 한다는 이유로 마리야 니콜라예브나를 내쳤다고는 말할 수 없었다."]], [["“Besides, I want to turn over a new leaf completely now.", "\"게다가 이제 나는 완전히 새 출발을 하고 싶어."], ["I’ve done silly things, of course, like everyone else, but money’s the last consideration; I don’t regret it.", "물론 남들처럼 어리석은 짓도 했지만, 돈은 가장 나중 문제야. 그건 후회 안 해."], ["So long as there’s health, and my health, thank God, is quite restored.”", "건강만 있으면 되지, 내 건강은, 다행히도, 완전히 회복됐고 말이야.\""]], [["Levin listened and racked his brains, but could think of nothing to say.", "레빈은 귀를 기울이며 머리를 쥐어짰지만, 무슨 말을 해야 할지 떠오르지 않았다. 니콜라이도 아마 같은 심정이었을 것이다."], ["Nikolay probably felt the same; he began questioning his brother about his affairs; and Levin was glad to talk about himself, because then he could speak without hypocrisy.", "그는 동생에게 일이 어떻게 돼 가는지 묻기 시작했고, 레빈은 자기 이야기를 할 수 있어 기뻤다. 그래야 위선 없이 말할 수 있었기 때문이다."], ["He told his brother of his plans and his doings.", "그는 형에게 자기 계획과 하는 일들을 들려주었다."]], [["His brother listened, but evidently he was not interested by it.", "형은 듣고는 있었지만, 분명 거기에 흥미를 느끼지는 않았다."]], [["These two men were so akin, so near each other, that the slightest gesture, the tone of voice, told both more than could be said in words.", "이 두 사람은 너무도 닮았고, 너무도 가까운 사이여서, 아주 사소한 몸짓이나 목소리의 어조만으로도 말로 다 할 수 있는 것보다 더 많은 것을 서로에게 전했다."]], [["Both of them now had only one thought—the illness of Nikolay and the nearness of his death—which stifled all else.", "이제 두 사람 모두 단 하나의 생각밖에 없었으니—니콜라이의 병과 다가오는 그의 죽음—그것이 다른 모든 것을 짓눌렀다."], ["But neither of them dared to speak of it, and so whatever they said—not uttering the one thought that filled their minds—was all falsehood.", "하지만 둘 중 누구도 감히 그것을 입에 올리지 못했고, 그래서 그들이 무슨 말을 하든—마음을 가득 채운 그 단 하나의 생각을 발설하지 않았으므로—전부 거짓이었다."], ["Never had Levin been so glad when the evening was over and it was time to go to bed.", "저녁이 끝나고 잠자리에 들 시간이 되었을 때만큼 레빈이 기뻤던 적이 없었다."], ["Never with any outside person, never on any official visit had he been so unnatural and false as he was that evening.", "어떤 낯선 사람과 함께든, 어떤 공식적인 방문에서든, 그날 저녁만큼 부자연스럽고 거짓되었던 적은 없었다."], ["And the consciousness of this unnaturalness, and the remorse he felt at it, made him even more unnatural.", "그리고 이 부자연스러움에 대한 의식과 거기서 느끼는 자책이 그를 한층 더 부자연스럽게 만들었다."], ["He wanted to weep over his dying, dearly loved brother, and he had to listen and keep on talking of how he meant to live.", "그는 죽어 가는, 더없이 사랑하는 형을 두고 울고 싶었건만, 자기가 어떻게 살아갈 작정인지 듣고 또 줄곧 이야기해야만 했다."]], [["As the house was damp, and only one bedroom had been kept heated, Levin put his brother to sleep in his own bedroom behind a screen.", "집 안에 습기가 차고 침실 하나만 난방이 되어 있었기에, 레빈은 형을 자기 침실 칸막이 뒤에서 재웠다."]], [["His brother got into bed, and whether he slept or did not sleep, tossed about like a sick man, coughed, and when he could not get his throat clear, mumbled something.", "형은 침대에 누웠는데, 자는지 안 자는지, 병자처럼 몸을 뒤척이고 기침을 해 댔으며, 목을 가다듬지 못할 때면 무어라 웅얼거렸다."], ["Sometimes when his breathing was painful, he said, “Oh, my God!”", "이따금 숨쉬기가 괴로울 때면 \"오, 하느님!\" 하고 말했다."], ["Sometimes when he was choking he muttered angrily, “Ah, the devil!”", "이따금 숨이 막힐 때면 성난 목소리로 \"아, 빌어먹을!\" 하고 중얼거렸다."], ["Levin could not sleep for a long while, hearing him.", "레빈은 그 소리를 들으며 오랫동안 잠들지 못했다."], ["His thoughts were of the most various, but the end of all his thoughts was the same—death.", "그의 생각은 갖가지로 뻗어 나갔지만, 모든 생각의 끝은 한결같았으니—죽음이었다."], ["Death, the inevitable end of all, for the first time presented itself to him with irresistible force.", "만물의 피할 수 없는 종착점인 죽음이, 처음으로 거역할 수 없는 힘으로 그에게 모습을 드러냈다."], ["And death, which was here in this loved brother, groaning half asleep and from habit calling without distinction on God and the devil, was not so remote as it had hitherto seemed to him.", "그리고 이 사랑하는 형 안에 깃든, 비몽사몽간에 신음하며 습관처럼 하느님과 악마를 가리지 않고 불러 대는 그 죽음은, 지금껏 그에게 여겨졌던 것만큼 멀리 있지 않았다."], ["It was in himself too, he felt that.", "그것은 자기 안에도 있었으니, 그는 그것을 느꼈다."], ["If not today, tomorrow, if not tomorrow, in thirty years, wasn’t it all the same!", "오늘이 아니면 내일, 내일이 아니면 삼십 년 후, 결국 다 마찬가지 아니겠는가!"], ["And what was this inevitable death—he did not know, had never thought about it, and what was more, had not the power, had not the courage to think about it.", "그리고 이 피할 수 없는 죽음이 무엇인지—그는 알지 못했고, 한 번도 생각해 본 적이 없었으며, 더구나 그것을 생각할 힘도, 용기도 없었다."]], [["“I work, I want to do something, but I had forgotten it must all end; I had forgotten—death.”", "\"나는 일을 하고, 무언가를 하고 싶어 하면서도, 이 모든 게 끝날 수밖에 없다는 걸 잊고 있었어. 잊고 있었던 거지—죽음을.\""]], [["He sat on his bed in the darkness, crouched up, hugging his knees, and holding his breath from the strain of thought, he pondered.", "그는 어둠 속에서 침대에 앉아, 몸을 웅크려 무릎을 끌어안고, 생각에 골몰한 나머지 숨을 멈춘 채 곰곰이 생각에 잠겼다."], ["But the more intensely he thought, the clearer it became to him that it was indubitably so, that in reality, looking upon life, he had forgotten one little fact—that death will come, and all ends; that nothing was even worth beginning, and that there was no helping it anyway.", "하지만 골똘히 생각하면 할수록, 그것이 의심할 여지 없는 사실임이, 즉 실은 자기가 삶을 바라보면서 한 가지 작은 사실을 잊고 있었다는 것이 더욱 분명해졌다—죽음이 찾아오고, 모든 것이 끝난다는 것, 그 무엇도 시작할 가치조차 없으며, 어차피 어쩔 도리가 없다는 것을."], ["Yes, it was awful, but it was so.", "그래, 그것은 무서운 일이었지만, 사실이 그러했다."]], [["“But I am alive still. Now what’s to be done?", "\"하지만 나는 아직 살아 있어."], ["what’s to be done?”", "그럼 이제 어쩌지?"], ["he said in despair.", "어쩌면 좋단 말이야?\""], ["He lighted a candle, got up cautiously and went to the looking-glass, and began looking at his face and hair.", "그는 절망에 빠져 중얼거렸다. 그는 촛불을 켜고 조심스레 일어나 거울 앞으로 가서, 자기 얼굴과 머리카락을 들여다보기 시작했다."], ["Yes, there were gray hairs about his temples.", "그렇다, 관자놀이 언저리에 흰머리가 보였다."], ["He opened his mouth.", "그는 입을 벌렸다."], ["His back teeth were beginning to decay.", "어금니가 썩기 시작하고 있었다."], ["He bared his muscular arms.", "그는 근육질의 팔을 드러냈다."], ["Yes, there was strength in them.", "그렇다, 거기에는 힘이 있었다."], ["But Nikolay, who lay there breathing with what was left of lungs, had had a strong, healthy body too.", "하지만 저기 누워 얼마 남지 않은 폐로 숨을 쉬고 있는 니콜라이도 한때는 튼튼하고 건강한 몸을 가졌었다."], ["And suddenly he recalled how they used to go to bed together as children, and how they only waited till Fyodor Bogdanitch was out of the room to fling pillows at each other and laugh, laugh irrepressibly, so that even their awe of Fyodor Bogdanitch could not check the effervescing, overbrimming sense of life and happiness.", "그리고 문득 그는 어린 시절 둘이서 함께 잠자리에 들곤 하던 일을, 표도르 보그다니치가 방에서 나가기만을 기다렸다가 서로에게 베개를 던지며 웃어 대던 일을, 표도르 보그다니치에 대한 경외심조차 막을 수 없을 만큼 부글부글 끓어 넘치는 생명과 행복의 감각에 사로잡혀 걷잡을 수 없이 웃어 대던 일을 떠올렸다."], ["“And now that bent, hollow chest ...", "\"그런데 이제 저 굽고 움푹 꺼진 가슴이라니……"], ["and I, not knowing what will become of me, or wherefore....”", "게다가 나는, 내가 어떻게 될지, 무엇을 위해 사는지도 모른 채……\""]], [["“K...ha!", "\"콜록!"], ["K...ha!", "콜록!"], ["Damnation!", "빌어먹을!"], ["Why do you keep fidgeting, why don’t you go to sleep?”", "왜 그렇게 부스럭거리는 거야, 왜 안 자는 거냐?\""], ["his brother’s voice called to him.", "형의 목소리가 그를 불렀다."]], [["“Oh, I don’t know, I’m not sleepy.”", "\"아, 모르겠어요, 잠이 안 와서요.\""]], [["“I have had a good sleep, I’m not in a sweat now.", "\"난 한숨 잘 잤어. 이젠 땀도 안 나."], ["Just see, feel my shirt; it’s not wet, is it?”", "어디, 내 셔츠 좀 만져 봐. 젖지 않았지?\""]], [["Levin felt, withdrew behind the screen, and put out the candle, but for a long while he could not sleep.", "레빈은 만져 보고는, 칸막이 뒤로 물러나 촛불을 껐지만, 오랫동안 잠들지 못했다."], ["The question how to live had hardly begun to grow a little clearer to him, when a new, insoluble question presented itself—death.", "어떻게 살아야 하는가 하는 물음이 이제 막 조금 또렷해지기 시작했을 때, 풀 수 없는 새로운 물음이 그 앞에 닥쳐왔다—죽음이었다."]], [["“Why, he’s dying—yes, he’ll die in the spring, and how help him?", "\"그래, 형은 죽어 가고 있어—그래, 봄이 되면 죽을 거야. 그런데 어떻게 도울 수 있단 말이야?"], ["What can I say to him?", "형에게 무슨 말을 할 수 있겠어?"], ["What do I know about it?", "내가 그것에 대해 뭘 알지?"], ["I’d even forgotten that it was at all.”", "나는 그런 게 있다는 사실조차 잊고 있었는데.\""]], [["# Chapter 32", "# Chapter 32"]], [["Levin had long before made the observation that when one is uncomfortable with people from their being excessively amenable and meek, one is apt very soon after to find things intolerable from their touchiness and irritability.", "레빈은 오래전부터 한 가지를 깨닫고 있었다. 사람들이 지나치게 고분고분하고 온순해서 불편할 때면, 머지않아 바로 그 사람들의 신경질과 짜증 때문에 견딜 수 없어지기 십상이라는 것이었다."], ["He felt that this was how it would be with his brother.", "형의 경우도 그렇게 되리라고 그는 느꼈다."], ["And his brother Nikolay’s gentleness did in fact not last out for long.", "그리고 실제로 형 니콜라이의 온순함은 오래가지 못했다."], ["The very next morning he began to be irritable, and seemed doing his best to find fault with his brother, attacking him on his tenderest points.", "바로 다음 날 아침부터 형은 짜증을 부리기 시작했고, 동생의 가장 아픈 데를 들쑤시며 어떻게든 트집을 잡으려 안달하는 것 같았다."]], [["Levin felt himself to blame, and could not set things right.", "레빈은 자기에게 잘못이 있다고 느꼈지만, 사태를 바로잡을 수가 없었다."], ["He felt that if they had both not kept up appearances, but had spoken, as it is called, from the heart—that is to say, had said only just what they were thinking and feeling—they would simply have looked into each other’s faces, and Konstantin could only have said, “You’re dying, you’re dying!” and Nikolay could only have answered, “I know I’m dying, but I’m afraid, I’m afraid, I’m afraid!”", "그는 만약 두 사람이 겉치레를 하지 않고, 흔히 말하듯 마음에서 우러나오는 대로—그러니까 생각하고 느끼는 것만을 그대로—이야기했더라면, 그저 서로의 얼굴을 들여다보았을 것이고, 콘스탄친은 그저 \"형은 죽어 가고 있어, 죽어 가고 있다고!\"라고 말할 수밖에 없었을 것이며, 니콜라이는 그저 \"내가 죽어 가고 있는 걸 알아, 하지만 무서워, 무서워, 무섭단 말이야!\"라고 대답할 수밖에 없었으리라고 느꼈다."], ["And they could have said nothing more, if they had said only what was in their hearts.", "그리고 마음에 있는 것만을 말했다면 그 이상은 아무 말도 할 수 없었을 것이다."], ["But life like that was impossible, and so Konstantin tried to do what he had been trying to do all his life, and never could learn to do, though, as far as he could observe, many people knew so well how to do it, and without it there was no living at all.", "그러나 그런 식의 삶은 불가능했고, 그래서 콘스탄친은 평생토록 해 보려 애쓰면서도 끝내 배우지 못한 일을, 비록 자기가 관찰한 바로는 많은 사람들이 아주 잘 해내고 또 그것 없이는 도무지 살아갈 수 없는 그 일을 또 해 보려 했다."], ["He tried to say what he was not thinking, but he felt continually that it had a ring of falsehood, that his brother detected him in it, and was exasperated at it.", "그는 생각하지 않는 것을 말하려 애썼지만, 거기에 거짓의 울림이 있다는 것을, 형이 그것을 간파하고 그 때문에 부아를 낸다는 것을 끊임없이 느꼈다."]], [["The third day Nikolay induced his brother to explain his plan to him again, and began not merely attacking it, but intentionally confounding it with communism.", "사흘째 되던 날, 니콜라이는 동생에게 자기 계획을 다시 설명해 보라고 하더니, 그것을 공격하는 데 그치지 않고 일부러 공산주의와 뒤섞어 버리기 시작했다."]], [["“You’ve simply borrowed an idea that’s not your own, but you’ve distorted it, and are trying to apply it where it’s not applicable.”", "\"너는 그저 네 것도 아닌 생각을 빌려 와 놓고는 그걸 왜곡해서, 들어맞지도 않는 데에 갖다 붙이려는 거야.\""]], [["“But I tell you it’s nothing to do with it.", "\"하지만 그것과는 아무 상관도 없다니까."], ["They deny the justice of property, of capital, of inheritance, while I do not deny this chief stimulus.”", "그들은 소유와 자본과 상속의 정당성을 부정하지만, 나는 그 주요한 자극을 부정하지 않아.\""], ["(Levin felt disgusted himself at using such expressions, but ever since he had been engrossed by his work, he had unconsciously come more and more frequently to use words not Russian.) “All I want is to regulate labor.”", "(레빈은 자기가 그런 표현을 쓴다는 것에 스스로 역겨움을 느꼈으나, 일에 몰두하게 된 이래로 그는 자기도 모르게 러시아어가 아닌 말들을 점점 더 자주 쓰게 되었다.) \"내가 바라는 건 노동을 조정하는 것뿐이야.\""]], [["“Which means, you’ve borrowed an idea, stripped it of all that gave it its force, and want to make believe that it’s something new,”", "\"그 말인즉슨, 너는 생각을 하나 빌려다가 거기서 힘을 주던 모든 것을 벗겨 내 버리고는, 그게 뭔가 새로운 것인 양 믿게 하려는 거지.\""], ["said Nikolay, angrily tugging at his necktie.", "니콜라이가 화를 내며 넥타이를 잡아당겼다."]], [["“But my idea has nothing in common....”", "\"하지만 내 생각은 그것과 아무런 공통점도……\""]], [["“That, anyway,”", "\"그건 어쨌든,\""], ["said Nikolay Levin, with an ironical smile, his eyes flashing malignantly, “has the charm of—what’s one to call it?—geometrical symmetry, of clearness, of definiteness.", "니콜라이 레빈은 빈정대는 미소를 지으며 두 눈을 악의에 차게 번득였다. \"뭐라고 불러야 할까—기하학적 대칭, 명료함, 명확함의 매력은 갖추고 있지."], ["It may be a Utopia.", "그건 유토피아일지도 몰라."], ["But if once one allows the possibility of making of all the past a tabula rasa—no property, no family—then labor would organize itself.", "하지만 일단 모든 과거를 tabula rasa로 만들 가능성을 허용한다면—소유도 없고 가족도 없다면—그때는 노동이 스스로 조직될 거야. 그런데 너는 아무것도 얻지 못해…….\""], ["But you gain nothing....”", "* tabula rasa: 라틴어로 '아무것도 쓰여 있지 않은 백지'."]], [["“Why do you mix things up?", "\"왜 자꾸 뒤섞는 거야?"], ["I’ve never been a communist.”", "난 한 번도 공산주의자였던 적이 없어.\""]], [["“But I have, and I consider it’s premature, but rational, and it has a future, just like Christianity in its first ages.”", "\"하지만 나는 공산주의자였고, 그게 시기상조이긴 해도 합리적이며, 초기 시대의 기독교처럼 미래가 있다고 생각해.\""]], [["“All that I maintain is that the labor force ought to be investigated from the point of view of natural science; that is to say, it ought to be studied, its qualities ascertained....”", "\"내가 주장하는 건 단지 노동력을 자연과학의 관점에서 조사해야 한다는 것뿐이야. 그러니까 그것을 연구하고 그 성질을 규명해야 한다는…….\""]], [["“But that’s utter waste of time.", "\"하지만 그건 완전한 시간 낭비야."], ["That force finds a certain form of activity of itself, according to the stage of its development.", "그 힘은 발전 단계에 따라 스스로 어떤 활동 형태를 찾아내거든."], ["There have been slaves first everywhere, then metayers; and we have the half-crop system, rent, and day laborers.", "어디서나 처음에는 노예가 있었고, 그다음에는 소작인이 있었지. 그리고 우리에게는 절반 소작제, 소작료, 날품팔이가 있어."], ["What are you trying to find?”", "너는 대체 뭘 찾으려는 거야?\""]], [["Levin suddenly lost his temper at these words, because at the bottom of his heart he was afraid that it was true—true that he was trying to hold the balance even between communism and the familiar forms, and that this was hardly possible.", "레빈은 이 말에 별안간 화가 치밀었다. 마음 깊은 곳에서 그것이 사실일까 봐—자기가 공산주의와 익숙한 형태들 사이에서 어떻게든 균형을 잡으려 애쓰고 있으며, 그것이 거의 불가능하다는 것이 사실일까 봐 두려웠기 때문이다."]], [["“I am trying to find means of working productively for myself and for the laborers.", "\"나는 나 자신을 위해서나 노동자들을 위해서나 생산적으로 일할 수 있는 방법을 찾으려는 거야."], ["I want to organize....”", "나는 조직하고 싶어…….\""], ["he answered hotly.", "그가 발끈하며 대답했다."]], [["“You don’t want to organize anything; it’s simply just as you’ve been all your life, that you want to be original to pose as not exploiting the peasants simply, but with some idea in view.”", "\"너는 아무것도 조직하고 싶은 게 아니야. 그저 평생토록 그래 온 그대로, 그냥 농민들을 착취하는 게 아니라 뭔가 이상을 품고 그런다는 식으로 독창적인 척하며 폼을 잡고 싶은 거지.\""]], [["“Oh, all right, that’s what you think—and let me alone!”", "\"오, 좋아, 형은 그렇게 생각한단 말이지—그러면 나 좀 내버려 둬!\""], ["answered Levin, feeling the muscles of his left cheek twitching uncontrollably.", "레빈은 왼쪽 뺨의 근육이 걷잡을 수 없이 실룩거리는 것을 느끼며 대답했다."]], [["“You’ve never had, and never have, convictions; all you want is to please your vanity.”", "\"너는 신념이라는 걸 가져 본 적도 없고 지금도 없어. 네가 바라는 건 그저 네 허영심을 만족시키는 것뿐이야.\""]], [["“Oh, very well; then let me alone!”", "\"오, 좋아. 그러면 나 좀 내버려 두라고!\""]], [["“And I will let you alone!", "\"내버려 두고말고!"], ["and it’s high time I did, and go to the devil with you!", "진작 그랬어야 했어. 너 같은 놈은 악마한테나 가 버려!"], ["and I’m very sorry I ever came!”", "내가 여기 온 게 정말 후회스럽다!\""]], [["In spite of all Levin’s efforts to soothe his brother afterwards, Nikolay would listen to nothing he said, declaring that it was better to part, and Konstantin saw that it simply was that life was unbearable to him.", "그 뒤로 레빈이 아무리 형을 달래려 애써도, 니콜라이는 그가 하는 말을 도무지 들으려 하지 않고 헤어지는 편이 낫겠다고 잘라 말했다. 콘스탄친은 그것이 그저 형에게 삶이 견딜 수 없는 것일 뿐임을 알아차렸다."]], [["Nikolay was just getting ready to go, when Konstantin went in to him again and begged him, rather unnaturally, to forgive him if he had hurt his feelings in any way.", "니콜라이가 막 떠날 채비를 하고 있을 때, 콘스탄친은 다시 그에게 가서, 다소 부자연스럽게, 혹시 자기가 어떤 식으로든 형의 마음을 상하게 했다면 용서해 달라고 청했다."]], [["“Ah, generosity!”", "\"아, 관대하시기도 하지!\""], ["said Nikolay, and he smiled.", "니콜라이가 말하며 미소를 지었다."], ["“If you want to be right, I can give you that satisfaction.", "\"네가 옳은 사람이 되고 싶다면, 그 만족감은 줄 수 있어."], ["You’re in the right; but I’m going all the same.”", "네가 옳아. 하지만 나는 그래도 가겠다.\""]], [["It was only just at parting that Nikolay kissed him, and said, looking with sudden strangeness and seriousness at his brother:", "니콜라이가 동생에게 입을 맞춘 것은 헤어지는 바로 그 순간이었다. 그는 별안간 낯설고 진지한 눈빛으로 동생을 바라보며 말했다."]], [["“Anyway, don’t remember evil against me, Kostya!”", "\"어쨌든, 나를 나쁘게 생각하지는 말아 다오, 코스챠!\""], ["and his voice quivered.", "그의 목소리가 떨렸다."], ["These were the only words that had been spoken sincerely between them.", "이것이 두 사람 사이에 오간 유일하게 진심 어린 말이었다."], ["Levin knew that those words meant, “You see, and you know, that I’m in a bad way, and maybe we shall not see each other again.”", "레빈은 그 말이 \"너도 보다시피, 또 알다시피, 나는 처지가 좋지 않고, 어쩌면 우리는 다시 만나지 못할지도 몰라.\"라는 뜻임을 알았다."], ["Levin knew this, and the tears gushed from his eyes.", "레빈은 그것을 알았고, 두 눈에서 눈물이 솟구쳤다."], ["He kissed his brother once more, but he could not speak, and knew not what to say.", "그는 형에게 다시 한번 입을 맞추었지만, 말을 할 수 없었고, 무슨 말을 해야 할지 알지 못했다."]], [["Three days after his brother’s departure, Levin too set off for his foreign tour.", "형이 떠나고 사흘 뒤, 레빈도 외국 여행길에 올랐다."], ["Happening to meet Shtcherbatsky, Kitty’s cousin, in the railway train, Levin greatly astonished him by his depression.", "기차 안에서 우연히 키티의 사촌인 시체르바츠키를 만난 레빈은 자신의 침울함으로 그를 몹시 놀라게 했다."]], [["“What’s the matter with you?”", "\"무슨 일 있어요?\""], ["Shtcherbatsky asked him.", "시체르바츠키가 그에게 물었다."]], [["“Oh, nothing; there’s not much happiness in life.”", "\"아, 아무것도 아니에요. 인생에 별 행복이 없어서요.\""]], [["“Not much?", "\"별로 없다고요?"], ["You come with me to Paris instead of to Mulhausen.", "뮐루즈 대신 나랑 같이 파리로 갑시다."], ["You shall see how to be happy.”", "어떻게 하면 행복해지는지 보여 줄 테니.\""]], [["“No, I’ve done with it all.", "\"아니요, 난 그런 건 다 끝냈어요."], ["It’s time I was dead.”", "이제 죽을 때가 됐죠.\""]], [["“Well, that’s a good one!”", "\"이런, 그것 참 걸작이군요!\""], ["said Shtcherbatsky, laughing; “why, I’m only just getting ready to begin.”", "시체르바츠키가 웃으며 말했다. \"아니, 난 이제 막 시작할 채비를 하고 있는데요.\""]], [["“Yes, I thought the same not long ago, but now I know I shall soon be dead.”", "\"네, 나도 얼마 전엔 그렇게 생각했어요. 하지만 지금은 곧 죽으리란 걸 알아요.\""]], [["Levin said what he had genuinely been thinking of late.", "레빈은 요즘 들어 진정으로 생각해 온 것을 말한 것이었다."], ["He saw nothing but death or the advance towards death in everything.", "그는 모든 것에서 죽음, 아니면 죽음을 향해 다가가는 것밖에는 아무것도 보지 못했다."], ["But his cherished scheme only engrossed him the more.", "그러나 그가 소중히 품은 계획은 오히려 그를 더욱 사로잡았다."], ["Life had to be got through somehow till death did come.", "죽음이 올 때까지는 어떻게든 삶을 살아 내야 했다."], ["Darkness had fallen upon everything for him; but just because of this darkness he felt that the one guiding clue in the darkness was his work, and he clutched it and clung to it with all his strength.", "그에게는 모든 것 위로 어둠이 내려앉았다. 하지만 바로 그 어둠 때문에, 그는 그 어둠 속에서 자기를 인도해 주는 단 하나의 실마리가 자신의 일임을 느꼈고, 그것을 움켜쥐고 온 힘을 다해 매달렸다."]], [["# PART FOUR Chapter 1", "# PART FOUR Chapter 1"]], [["The Karenins, husband and wife, continued living in the same house, met every day, but were complete strangers to one another.", "카레닌 부부는 한집에서 계속 살았고, 날마다 마주쳤지만, 서로에게는 완전히 남남이었다."], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch made it a rule to see his wife every day, so that the servants might have no grounds for suppositions, but avoided dining at home.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 하인들에게 억측의 빌미를 주지 않으려고 날마다 아내를 보는 것을 원칙으로 삼았지만, 집에서 식사하는 일은 피했다."], ["Vronsky was never at Alexey Alexandrovitch’s house, but Anna saw him away from home, and her husband was aware of it.", "브론스키는 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 집에 결코 발을 들이지 않았으나, 안나는 집 밖에서 그를 만났고, 남편도 그 사실을 알고 있었다."]], [["The position was one of misery for all three; and not one of them would have been equal to enduring this position for a single day, if it had not been for the expectation that it would change, that it was merely a temporary painful ordeal which would pass over.", "세 사람 모두에게 그 처지는 비참한 것이었다. 그리고 그 누구도, 이런 상태가 변하리라는 기대가 없었다면, 그저 지나가 버릴 일시적이고 괴로운 시련에 지나지 않는다는 기대가 없었다면, 단 하루도 이런 처지를 견뎌 내지 못했을 것이다."], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch hoped that this passion would pass, as everything does pass, that everyone would forget about it, and his name would remain unsullied.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 이 열정도 다른 모든 것이 지나가듯이 지나가고, 모두가 그 일을 잊어버릴 것이며, 자기 이름은 더럽혀지지 않은 채 남으리라고 기대했다."], ["Anna, on whom the position depended, and for whom it was more miserable than for anyone, endured it because she not merely hoped, but firmly believed, that it would all very soon be settled and come right.", "이 처지가 좌우되고, 또 누구보다도 이 처지로 인해 더 비참했던 안나는, 머지않아 모든 것이 해결되어 바로잡히리라고 그저 기대만 하는 것이 아니라 굳게 믿었기에 그것을 견뎌 냈다."], ["She had not the least idea what would settle the position, but she firmly believed that something would very soon turn up now.", "그녀는 무엇이 이 처지를 해결해 줄지에 대해서는 조금도 짐작하지 못했지만, 이제 곧 뭔가가 일어나리라고 굳게 믿었다."], ["Vronsky, against his own will or wishes, followed her lead, hoped too that something, apart from his own action, would be sure to solve all difficulties.", "브론스키는 제 의지나 바람과는 상관없이 그녀가 이끄는 대로 따랐고, 자기 행동과는 별개로 뭔가가 모든 어려움을 틀림없이 풀어 주리라고 그 역시 기대했다."]], [["In the middle of the winter Vronsky spent a very tiresome week.", "겨울이 한창일 때 브론스키는 무척 진저리 나는 한 주를 보냈다."], ["A foreign prince, who had come on a visit to Petersburg, was put under his charge, and he had to show him the sights worth seeing.", "페테르부르크에 방문차 온 어느 외국 공작이 그의 담당으로 맡겨졌고, 그는 볼 만한 명소들을 그에게 안내해야 했다."], ["Vronsky was of distinguished appearance; he possessed, moreover, the art of behaving with respectful dignity, and was used to having to do with such grand personages—that was how he came to be put in charge of the prince.", "브론스키는 풍채가 당당했고, 게다가 정중한 위엄을 갖추어 처신하는 기술을 지녔으며, 그런 지체 높은 인사들을 상대하는 데 익숙했다. 바로 그래서 그가 공작의 담당을 맡게 된 것이었다."], ["But he felt his duties very irksome.", "하지만 그는 그 임무를 무척 성가시게 여겼다."], ["The prince was anxious to miss nothing of which he would be asked at home, had he seen that in Russia? And on his own account he was anxious to enjoy to the utmost all Russian forms of amusement.", "공작은 집에 돌아가 러시아에서 그것을 보았느냐는 질문을 받았을 때 빠뜨린 것이 하나도 없도록 신경을 곤두세웠고, 또 제 나름대로는 러시아의 온갖 오락거리를 한껏 즐기려고 안달이었다."], ["Vronsky was obliged to be his guide in satisfying both these inclinations.", "브론스키는 이 두 가지 욕구를 만족시켜 주는 안내자 노릇을 해야 했다."], ["The mornings they spent driving to look at places of interest; the evenings they passed enjoying the national entertainments.", "그들은 오전이면 마차를 타고 명소를 구경하러 다녔고, 저녁이면 그 나라 특유의 유흥을 즐기며 보냈다."], ["The prince rejoiced in health exceptional even among princes.", "공작은 공작들 사이에서도 유독 보기 드문 건강을 누렸다."], ["By gymnastics and careful attention to his health he had brought himself to such a point that in spite of his excess in pleasure he looked as fresh as a big glossy green Dutch cucumber.", "체조와 세심한 건강 관리로 그는 쾌락에 탐닉했음에도 불구하고 윤기 흐르는 커다란 초록빛 네덜란드 오이처럼 싱싱해 보일 만큼 제 몸을 만들어 놓았다."], ["The prince had traveled a great deal, and considered one of the chief advantages of modern facilities of communication was the accessibility of the pleasures of all nations.", "공작은 여행을 많이 다녔고, 현대 교통수단의 가장 큰 이점 가운데 하나로 온갖 나라의 쾌락에 손쉽게 다가갈 수 있다는 점을 꼽았다."]], [["He had been in Spain, and there had indulged in serenades and had made friends with a Spanish girl who played the mandolin.", "그는 에스파냐에 가서 세레나데에 흠뻑 빠지고 만돌린을 켜는 에스파냐 처녀와 친분을 맺었다."], ["In Switzerland he had killed chamois.", "스위스에서는 영양을 잡았다."], ["In England he had galloped in a red coat over hedges and killed two hundred pheasants for a bet.", "영국에서는 붉은 외투를 걸치고 울타리를 뛰어넘으며 말을 달렸고, 내기로 꿩 이백 마리를 잡았다."], ["In Turkey he had got into a harem; in India he had hunted on an elephant, and now in Russia he wished to taste all the specially Russian forms of pleasure.", "터키에서는 하렘에 들어갔고, 인도에서는 코끼리를 타고 사냥을 했으며, 이제 러시아에서는 러시아 특유의 온갖 쾌락을 맛보고자 했다."]], [["Vronsky, who was, as it were, chief master of the ceremonies to him, was at great pains to arrange all the Russian amusements suggested by various persons to the prince.", "그에게는 이를테면 의전 책임자나 다름없던 브론스키는, 여러 사람이 공작에게 권하는 러시아의 온갖 유흥을 마련하느라 무던히도 애를 썼다."], ["They had race horses, and Russian pancakes and bear hunts and three-horse sledges, and gypsies and drinking feasts, with the Russian accompaniment of broken crockery.", "그들에게는 경주마가 있었고, 러시아식 팬케이크와 곰 사냥과 삼두마차와 집시들과 그릇을 깨부수는 러시아식 여흥이 곁들여진 술잔치가 있었다."], ["And the prince with surprising ease fell in with the Russian spirit, smashed trays full of crockery, sat with a gypsy girl on his knee, and seemed to be asking—what more, and does the whole Russian spirit consist in just this?", "그리고 공작은 놀랄 만큼 수월하게 러시아의 정취에 빠져들어, 그릇이 가득 담긴 쟁반을 내동댕이치고, 집시 처녀를 무릎에 앉히고는, 마치 이렇게 묻는 듯했다. 더 없는가, 그리고 러시아의 정취란 게 고작 이게 다인가?"]], [["In reality, of all the Russian entertainments the prince liked best French actresses and ballet dancers and white-seal champagne.", "실은 러시아의 온갖 유흥 가운데 공작이 가장 좋아한 것은 프랑스 여배우들과 발레 무용수들, 그리고 화이트실 샴페인이었다."], ["Vronsky was used to princes, but, either because he had himself changed of late, or that he was in too close proximity to the prince, that week seemed fearfully wearisome to him.", "브론스키는 공작들을 상대하는 데 익숙했지만, 근래 자기 자신이 변한 탓인지, 아니면 그 공작과 너무 가까이 붙어 지낸 탓인지, 그 한 주가 그에게는 끔찍하리만치 지겹게 느껴졌다."], ["The whole of that week he experienced a sensation such as a man might have set in charge of a dangerous madman, afraid of the madman, and at the same time, from being with him, fearing for his own reason.", "그 한 주 내내 그는 위험한 미치광이를 떠맡은 사람이 느낄 법한 감각을 맛보았다. 그 미치광이를 두려워하면서, 동시에 그와 함께 지내는 탓에 제 정신마저 잃을까 봐 두려워하는 그런 감각이었다."], ["Vronsky was continually conscious of the necessity of never for a second relaxing the tone of stern official respectfulness, that he might not himself be insulted.", "브론스키는 자기 자신이 모욕당하지 않으려면 단 한순간도 엄격한 공적 정중함의 어조를 늦춰서는 안 된다는 것을 끊임없이 의식하고 있었다."], ["The prince’s manner of treating the very people who, to Vronsky’s surprise, were ready to descend to any depths to provide him with Russian amusements, was contemptuous.", "공작은, 브론스키가 놀랍게도 그에게 러시아식 유흥을 마련해 주려고 어떤 비굴함도 마다하지 않던 바로 그 사람들을 경멸하듯 대했다."], ["His criticisms of Russian women, whom he wished to study, more than once made Vronsky crimson with indignation.", "공작이 연구하고 싶어 했던 러시아 여인들에 대해 늘어놓는 평가는 한두 번이 아니라 브론스키를 분노로 얼굴이 새빨개지게 만들었다."], ["The chief reason why the prince was so particularly disagreeable to Vronsky was that he could not help seeing himself in him.", "공작이 브론스키에게 그토록 유독 못마땅하게 느껴진 가장 큰 이유는, 그 자신을 그에게서 보지 않을 수 없었기 때문이었다."], ["And what he saw in this mirror did not gratify his self-esteem.", "그리고 그 거울에 비친 모습은 그의 자존심을 흡족하게 해 주지 않았다."], ["He was a very stupid and very self-satisfied and very healthy and very well-washed man, and nothing else.", "그는 몹시 멍청하고 몹시 자기만족에 차 있으며 몹시 건강하고 몹시 깔끔하게 씻은 사내일 뿐, 그 이상도 그 이하도 아니었다."], ["He was a gentleman—that was true, and Vronsky could not deny it.", "그가 신사라는 것은 사실이었고, 브론스키도 그것을 부인할 수 없었다."], ["He was equable and not cringing with his superiors, was free and ingratiating in his behavior with his equals, and was contemptuously indulgent with his inferiors.", "그는 윗사람들에게 한결같으면서도 굽실거리지 않았고, 동등한 자들에게는 격의 없고 상냥하게 처신했으며, 아랫사람들에게는 경멸이 섞인 관대함을 보였다."], ["Vronsky was himself the same, and regarded it as a great merit to be so.", "브론스키 자신도 똑같았고, 그렇게 처신하는 것을 큰 미덕으로 여겼다."], ["But for this prince he was an inferior, and his contemptuous and indulgent attitude to him revolted him.", "하지만 이 공작에게는 그가 아랫사람이었고, 자기를 향한 공작의 경멸 섞인 관대한 태도는 그의 비위를 거슬렀다."]], [["“Brainless beef!", "\"멍청한 쇠고기 덩어리 같으니!"], ["can I be like that?”", "내가 저럴 수가 있단 말인가?\""], ["he thought.", "그는 생각했다."]], [["Be that as it might, when, on the seventh day, he parted from the prince, who was starting for Moscow, and received his thanks, he was happy to be rid of his uncomfortable position and the unpleasant reflection of himself.", "어찌 됐든, 이레째 되던 날, 모스크바로 떠나려던 공작과 작별하고 그의 감사 인사를 받았을 때, 그는 불편한 처지와 자기 자신의 불쾌한 반영에서 벗어나게 되어 기뻤다."], ["He said good-bye to him at the station on their return from a bear hunt, at which they had had a display of Russian prowess kept up all night.", "그는 밤새 러시아인의 기개를 마음껏 과시한 곰 사냥에서 돌아오는 길에 역에서 그와 작별 인사를 나누었다."]], [["# Chapter 2", "# Chapter 2"]], [["When he got home, Vronsky found there a note from Anna.", "집에 돌아온 브론스키는 안나에게서 온 쪽지 한 통을 발견했다."], ["She wrote, “I am ill and unhappy.", "그녀는 이렇게 썼다. \"나는 아프고 불행해요."], ["I cannot come out, but I cannot go on longer without seeing you.", "나갈 수가 없지만, 더 이상 당신을 보지 못하고는 견딜 수가 없어요."], ["Come in this evening.", "오늘 저녁에 와 줘요."], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch goes to the council at seven and will be there till ten.”", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 일곱 시에 회의에 가서 열 시까지 거기 있을 거예요.\""], ["Thinking for an instant of the strangeness of her bidding him come straight to her, in spite of her husband’s insisting on her not receiving him, he decided to go.", "남편이 그를 집에 들이지 말라고 못 박았는데도 곧장 자기에게 오라고 하는 그녀의 말이 이상하다고 한순간 생각했지만, 그는 가기로 마음먹었다."]], [["Vronsky had that winter got his promotion, was now a colonel, had left the regimental quarters, and was living alone.", "그해 겨울 브론스키는 진급하여 이제 대령이 되었고, 연대 숙소를 떠나 혼자 살고 있었다."], ["After having some lunch, he lay down on the sofa immediately, and in five minutes memories of the hideous scenes he had witnessed during the last few days were confused together and joined on to a mental image of Anna and of the peasant who had played an important part in the bear hunt, and Vronsky fell asleep.", "점심을 좀 먹고 나서 그는 곧장 소파에 드러누웠고, 오 분 만에 지난 며칠 동안 목격한 끔찍한 장면들의 기억이 뒤엉키며 안나의 모습과, 그 곰 사냥에서 중요한 역할을 했던 농부의 모습과 한데 이어졌다. 그리고 브론스키는 잠이 들었다."], ["He waked up in the dark, trembling with horror, and made haste to light a candle.", "그는 공포에 떨며 어둠 속에서 깨어나, 황급히 촛불을 켰다. \"뭐였지?"], ["“What was it?", "뭐였더라?"], ["What? What was the dreadful thing I dreamed?", "내가 꾼 그 끔찍한 게 뭐였지?"], ["Yes, yes; I think a little dirty man with a disheveled beard was stooping down doing something, and all of a sudden he began saying some strange words in French.", "그래, 그래, 헝클어진 수염을 단 더럽고 자그마한 사내가 몸을 굽히고 뭔가를 하고 있었던 것 같아. 그러다 별안간 프랑스어로 무슨 이상한 말을 하기 시작했어."], ["Yes, there was nothing else in the dream,” he said to himself.", "그래, 꿈에는 그것 말고는 아무것도 없었어.\""], ["“But why was it so awful?”", "그는 혼잣말을 했다."], ["He vividly recalled the peasant again and those incomprehensible French words the peasant had uttered, and a chill of horror ran down his spine.", "\"그런데 왜 그렇게 무서웠던 거지?\" 그는 다시 그 농부와 농부가 내뱉은 그 알 수 없는 프랑스어를 생생하게 떠올렸고, 등줄기를 타고 공포의 한기가 흘러내렸다."]], [["“What nonsense!”", "\"무슨 헛소리람!\""], ["thought Vronsky, and glanced at his watch.", "브론스키는 그렇게 생각하며 시계를 흘끗 보았다."]], [["It was half-past eight already.", "벌써 여덟 시 반이었다."], ["He rang up his servant, dressed in haste, and went out onto the steps, completely forgetting the dream and only worried at being late.", "그는 하인을 부르고 황급히 옷을 차려입은 뒤 현관 계단으로 나섰다. 꿈은 까맣게 잊은 채 늦었다는 것만이 마음에 걸렸다."], ["As he drove up to the Karenins’ entrance he looked at his watch and saw it was ten minutes to nine.", "카레닌 집 입구로 마차를 몰고 다가가며 시계를 보니 아홉 시 십 분 전이었다."], ["A high, narrow carriage with a pair of grays was standing at the entrance.", "잿빛 말 두 필을 맨, 높고 폭이 좁은 마차 한 대가 입구에 서 있었다."], ["He recognized Anna’s carriage.", "그는 안나의 마차임을 알아보았다."], ["“She is coming to me,”", "\"그녀가 나에게 오는 거로군.\""], ["thought Vronsky, “and better she should.", "브론스키는 생각했다. \"차라리 그편이 낫지."], ["I don’t like going into that house.", "나는 저 집에 들어가는 게 싫으니까."], ["But no matter; I can’t hide myself,”", "하지만 상관없어. 숨을 수는 없는 노릇이니.\""], ["he thought, and with that manner peculiar to him from childhood, as of a man who has nothing to be ashamed of, Vronsky got out of his sledge and went to the door.", "그는 그렇게 생각하며, 부끄러울 것이 하나도 없는 사람다운, 어릴 적부터 몸에 밴 그 특유의 태도로 썰매에서 내려 문으로 향했다."], ["The door opened, and the hall-porter with a rug on his arm called the carriage.", "문이 열리고, 팔에 담요를 걸친 현관지기가 마차를 불렀다."], ["Vronsky, though he did not usually notice details, noticed at this moment the amazed expression with which the porter glanced at him.", "브론스키는 평소 세세한 것을 눈여겨보는 편이 아니었지만, 이 순간만큼은 현관지기가 자기를 흘끗 보는 그 놀란 표정을 알아챘다."], ["In the very doorway Vronsky almost ran up against Alexey Alexandrovitch.", "바로 그 문간에서 브론스키는 하마터면 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치와 부딪칠 뻔했다."], ["The gas jet threw its full light on the bloodless, sunken face under the black hat and on the white cravat, brilliant against the beaver of the coat.", "가스등이 검은 모자 아래 핏기 없이 움푹 꺼진 얼굴과, 외투의 비버 모피를 배경으로 환하게 빛나는 흰 넥타이를 환히 비추었다."], ["Karenin’s fixed, dull eyes were fastened upon Vronsky’s face.", "카레닌의 고정된 흐릿한 두 눈이 브론스키의 얼굴에 못 박혔다."], ["Vronsky bowed, and Alexey Alexandrovitch, chewing his lips, lifted his hand to his hat and went on.", "브론스키가 인사를 하자, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 입술을 우물거리며 손을 들어 모자에 가져다 댄 뒤 지나갔다."], ["Vronsky saw him without looking round get into the carriage, pick up the rug and the opera-glass at the window and disappear.", "브론스키는 그가 돌아보지도 않은 채 마차에 올라타, 창가에 놓아둔 담요와 오페라글라스를 집어 들고 사라지는 것을 보았다."], ["Vronsky went into the hall.", "브론스키는 현관으로 들어섰다."], ["His brows were scowling, and his eyes gleamed with a proud and angry light in them.", "그의 미간은 찌푸려져 있었고, 두 눈에는 오만하고 노여운 빛이 번득였다."]], [["“What a position!”", "\"이게 무슨 처지람!\""], ["he thought.", "그는 생각했다."], ["“If he would fight, would stand up for his honor, I could act, could express my feelings; but this weakness or baseness....", "\"저자가 결투라도 하려 든다면, 자기 명예를 지키려 든다면, 나도 행동에 나설 수 있고 내 감정을 드러낼 수 있을 텐데. 하지만 이 나약함이라니, 아니면 이 비열함이라니……"], ["He puts me in the position of playing false, which I never meant and never mean to do.”", "저자는 나를, 내가 한 번도 그러려 한 적 없고 앞으로도 그럴 마음이 없는, 거짓을 꾸미는 처지로 몰아넣고 있어.\""]], [["Vronsky’s ideas had changed since the day of his conversation with Anna in the Vrede garden.", "브레데 정원에서 안나와 이야기를 나눈 그날 이후 브론스키의 생각은 달라져 있었다."], ["Unconsciously yielding to the weakness of Anna—who had surrendered herself up to him utterly, and simply looked to him to decide her fate, ready to submit to anything—he had long ceased to think that their tie might end as he had thought then.", "자기를 송두리째 그에게 내맡기고, 그저 그가 자기 운명을 결정해 주기만 바라며 무엇이든 따를 각오가 되어 있는 안나—그 안나의 나약함에 자기도 모르게 굴복하면서, 그는 그때 생각했던 식으로 둘의 관계가 끝날 수도 있다는 생각을 진작에 접었다."], ["His ambitious plans had retreated into the background again, and feeling that he had got out of that circle of activity in which everything was definite, he had given himself entirely to his passion, and that passion was binding him more and more closely to her.", "야심에 찬 계획들은 다시 뒤편으로 물러났고, 모든 것이 명확했던 그 활동의 영역에서 자기가 벗어났음을 느끼며 그는 자신의 열정에 온전히 몸을 맡겼다. 그리고 그 열정은 그를 점점 더 단단히 그녀에게 묶어 갔다."]], [["He was still in the hall when he caught the sound of her retreating footsteps.", "그가 아직 현관에 있을 때, 그는 멀어져 가는 그녀의 발소리를 들었다."], ["He knew she had been expecting him, had listened for him, and was now going back to the drawing-room.", "그녀가 자기를 기다리며 귀 기울이고 있다가, 이제 응접실로 돌아가는 길임을 그는 알았다."]], [["“No,”", "\"안 돼요.\""], ["she cried, on seeing him, and at the first sound of her voice the tears came into her eyes.", "그를 보자 그녀가 외쳤고, 그 목소리가 처음 새어 나오는 순간 두 눈에 눈물이 차올랐다."], ["“No; if things are to go on like this, the end will come much, much too soon.”", "\"안 돼요. 이런 식으로 계속 간다면, 끝은 너무, 너무 빨리 오고 말 거예요.\""]], [["“What is it, dear one?”", "\"왜 그래요, 여보?\""]], [["“What?", "\"왜냐고요?"], ["I’ve been waiting in agony for an hour, two hours ...", "나는 한 시간, 두 시간이나 괴로움 속에서 기다렸어요……"], ["No, I won’t ...", "아니, 그러지 않을래요……"], ["I can’t quarrel with you.", "당신과 다투지 않을래요."], ["Of course you couldn’t come. No, I won’t.”", "당연히 당신은 올 수 없었겠죠."], ["She laid her two hands on his shoulders, and looked a long while at him with a profound, passionate, and at the same time searching look.", "아니, 그러지 않을래요.\" 그녀는 두 손을 그의 어깨에 얹고, 깊고 격정적이면서도 동시에 무언가를 살피는 눈빛으로 한참 동안 그를 바라보았다."], ["She was studying his face to make up for the time she had not seen him.", "그녀는 그를 보지 못한 동안의 시간을 메우려는 듯 그의 얼굴을 찬찬히 살폈다."], ["She was, every time she saw him, making the picture of him in her imagination (incomparably superior, impossible in reality) fit with him as he really was.", "그녀는 그를 볼 때마다, 자기 상상 속에 그려 놓은 그의 모습(현실에서는 있을 수 없으리만치 비할 데 없이 빼어난 모습)을 실제 그의 모습과 맞춰 보곤 했다."]], [["# Chapter 3", "# Chapter 3"]], [["“You met him?”", "\"그 사람을 만났어요?\""], ["she asked, when they had sat down at the table in the lamplight.", "램프 불빛 아래 식탁에 앉았을 때 그녀가 물었다."], ["“You’re punished, you see, for being late.”", "\"늦게 온 벌이에요, 보세요.\""]], [["“Yes; but how was it?", "\"그래, 그런데 어떻게 된 거지?"], ["Wasn’t he to be at the council?”", "그 사람 회의에 가 있어야 하는 거 아니었어?\""]], [["“He had been and come back, and was going out somewhere again.", "\"갔다가 돌아왔다가, 또 어딘가로 나가는 길이었어요."], ["But that’s no matter.", "하지만 그건 아무래도 좋아요."], ["Don’t talk about it.", "그 얘긴 하지 마요."], ["Where have you been?", "어디 있었어요?"], ["With the prince still?”", "아직도 그 공작이랑?\""]], [["She knew every detail of his existence.", "그녀는 그의 생활을 시시콜콜 다 알고 있었다."], ["He was going to say that he had been up all night and had dropped asleep, but looking at her thrilled and rapturous face, he was ashamed.", "그는 밤을 새우다가 깜빡 잠들어 버렸다고 말하려 했지만, 들뜨고 황홀해하는 그녀의 얼굴을 보자 부끄러웠다."], ["And he said he had had to go to report on the prince’s departure.", "그래서 공작의 출발을 보고하러 가야 했다고 말했다."]], [["“But it’s over now?", "\"하지만 이젠 끝난 거죠?"], ["He is gone?”", "갔나요?\""]], [["“Thank God it’s over!", "\"하느님 감사하게도 끝났지!"], ["You wouldn’t believe how insufferable it’s been for me.”", "그게 나한테 얼마나 견디기 힘들었는지 당신은 믿지 못할 거야.\""]], [["“Why so?", "\"왜요?"], ["Isn’t it the life all of you, all young men, always lead?”", "그건 당신들 모두가, 모든 젊은 남자가 늘 보내는 삶 아닌가요?\""], ["she said, knitting her brows; and taking up the crochet work that was lying on the table, she began drawing the hook out of it, without looking at Vronsky.", "그녀가 미간을 찌푸리며 말했다. 그러고는 탁자 위에 놓여 있던 뜨개질감을 집어 들어, 브론스키를 쳐다보지도 않고 거기서 코바늘을 빼내기 시작했다."]], [["“I gave that life up long ago,”", "\"난 그런 삶을 진작에 그만뒀어.\""], ["said he, wondering at the change in her face, and trying to divine its meaning.", "그가 말했다. 그녀의 얼굴빛이 달라진 것에 의아해하며 그 의미를 헤아리려 애쓰면서."], ["“And I confess,”", "\"그리고 솔직히 말하면,\""], ["he said, with a smile, showing his thick, white teeth, “this week I’ve been, as it were, looking at myself in a glass, seeing that life, and I didn’t like it.”", "그는 미소를 지으며 두툼하고 하얀 이를 드러내며 말했다. \"이번 주에는 마치 거울 속에 비친 나 자신을 들여다보듯 그런 삶을 봤는데, 마음에 들지 않더군.\""]], [["She held the work in her hands, but did not crochet, and looked at him with strange, shining, and hostile eyes.", "그녀는 뜨개질감을 손에 든 채 뜨개질은 하지 않고, 낯설고 번득이는, 적의에 찬 눈으로 그를 바라보았다."]], [["“This morning Liza came to see me—they’re not afraid to call on me, in spite of the Countess Lidia Ivanovna,”", "\"오늘 아침에 리자가 나를 찾아왔어요. 그 사람들은 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인이 뭐라 하든 아랑곳없이 나를 방문하기를 두려워하지 않거든요.\""], ["she put in—“and she told me about your Athenian evening.", "그녀가 끼워 넣듯 말했다. \"그리고 당신네 아테네식 밤 모임에 대해 얘기해 줬어요."], ["How loathsome!”", "얼마나 역겨운지!\""]], [["“I was just going to say....”", "\"마침 그 얘길 하려던 참이었는데…….\""]], [["She interrupted him.", "그녀가 그의 말을 가로막았다."], ["“It was that Thérèse you used to know?”", "\"당신이 알고 지내던 그 테레즈였다면서요?\""]], [["“I was just saying....”", "\"마침 말하려던 거였어…….\""]], [["“How disgusting you are, you men!", "\"당신네 남자들은 어쩜 그렇게 끔찍한지!"], ["How is it you can’t understand that a woman can never forget that,”", "여자는 그런 걸 절대 잊을 수 없다는 걸 어째서 이해하지 못하는 거예요.\""], ["she said, getting more and more angry, and so letting him see the cause of her irritation, “especially a woman who cannot know your life?", "그녀는 점점 더 화를 내며 말했고, 그렇게 자기가 짜증을 내는 원인을 그에게 드러내 보였다. \"더구나 당신의 삶을 알 수도 없는 여자는요?"], ["What do I know?", "내가 뭘 알겠어요?"], ["What have I ever known?”", "내가 여태 뭘 알았겠어요?\""], ["she said, “what you tell me.", "그녀가 말했다. \"당신이 나한테 들려주는 것뿐이죠."], ["And how do I know whether you tell me the truth?...”", "게다가 당신이 진실을 말하는지 아닌지 내가 어떻게 알겠어요?……\""]], [["“Anna, you hurt me.", "\"안나, 당신 나를 아프게 하는군."], ["Don’t you trust me?", "나를 믿지 못하겠어?"], ["Haven’t I told you that I haven’t a thought I wouldn’t lay bare to you?”", "당신한테 털어놓지 못할 생각은 하나도 없다고 내가 말하지 않았던가?\""]], [["“Yes, yes,”", "\"그래요, 그래요.\""], ["she said, evidently trying to suppress her jealous thoughts.", "그녀가 질투 어린 생각을 억누르려 애쓰는 게 역력한 채로 말했다."], ["“But if only you knew how wretched I am!", "\"하지만 내가 얼마나 비참한지 당신이 알기만 한다면!"], ["I believe you, I believe you....", "당신을 믿어요, 믿는다고요……"], ["What were you saying?”", "무슨 말을 하고 있었죠?\""]], [["But he could not at once recall what he had been going to say.", "그러나 그는 자기가 무슨 말을 하려 했었는지 곧바로 떠올릴 수 없었다."], ["These fits of jealousy, which of late had been more and more frequent with her, horrified him, and however much he tried to disguise the fact, made him feel cold to her, although he knew the cause of her jealousy was her love for him.", "요즘 들어 점점 더 잦아진 이런 질투의 발작은 그를 섬뜩하게 했고, 아무리 그 사실을 숨기려 애써도 그녀에 대해 차가워지게 만들었다. 그녀의 질투의 원인이 그를 향한 사랑이라는 것을 알면서도 그랬다."], ["How often he had told himself that her love was happiness; and now she loved him as a woman can love when love has outweighed for her all the good things of life—and he was much further from happiness than when he had followed her from Moscow.", "그녀의 사랑이 행복이라고 그는 얼마나 자주 스스로에게 말해 왔던가. 그리고 이제 그녀는, 사랑이 삶의 온갖 좋은 것들을 그녀에게서 압도해 버렸을 때 한 여자가 사랑할 수 있는 그런 방식으로 그를 사랑하고 있었다."], ["Then he had thought himself unhappy, but happiness was before him; now he felt that the best happiness was already left behind.", "그런데 그는 모스크바에서 그녀를 뒤따라왔을 때보다 행복에서 훨씬 더 멀어져 있었다. 그때 그는 자기가 불행하다고 여겼지만, 행복은 앞에 놓여 있었다."], ["She was utterly unlike what she had been when he first saw her.", "이제 그는 가장 좋은 행복은 이미 뒤에 남겨졌다고 느꼈다."], ["Both morally and physically she had changed for the worse.", "그녀는 그가 처음 보았을 때와는 영 딴판이었다."], ["She had broadened out all over, and in her face at the time when she was speaking of the actress there was an evil expression of hatred that distorted it.", "정신적으로나 육체적으로나 그녀는 나쁜 쪽으로 변해 있었다. 온몸이 펑퍼짐해졌고, 그 여배우 이야기를 하던 순간 그녀의 얼굴에는 그것을 일그러뜨리는 사악한 증오의 표정이 어려 있었다."], ["He looked at her as a man looks at a faded flower he has gathered, with difficulty recognizing in it the beauty for which he picked and ruined it.", "그는, 꺾어 든 시든 꽃을 바라보는 사람이 그것을 꺾어 망쳐 버리게 만든 그 아름다움을 좀처럼 알아보지 못하듯, 그렇게 그녀를 바라보았다."], ["And in spite of this he felt that then, when his love was stronger, he could, if he had greatly wished it, have torn that love out of his heart; but now, when as at that moment it seemed to him he felt no love for her, he knew that what bound him to her could not be broken.", "그러면서도 그는, 사랑이 더 강했던 그때라면 정말로 마음만 먹었다면 그 사랑을 가슴에서 뜯어낼 수도 있었으련만, 지금은 바로 이 순간 그녀에 대한 사랑을 전혀 느끼지 못하는 것만 같으면서도, 자기를 그녀에게 묶어 놓는 것이 끊어질 수 없다는 것을 알고 있다고 느꼈다."]], [["“Well, well, what was it you were going to say about the prince?", "\"자, 자, 그 공작에 대해 무슨 얘기를 하려고 했죠?"], ["I have driven away the fiend,”", "마귀는 내가 쫓아냈어요.\""], ["she added.", "그녀가 덧붙였다."], ["The fiend was the name they had given her jealousy.", "마귀란 그녀의 질투에 두 사람이 붙인 이름이었다."], ["“What did you begin to tell me about the prince?", "\"공작에 대해 나한테 무슨 얘기를 시작했었죠?"], ["Why did you find it so tiresome?”", "왜 그렇게 따분했던 거예요?\""]], [["“Oh, it was intolerable!”", "\"아, 견딜 수가 없었어!\""], ["he said, trying to pick up the thread of his interrupted thought.", "그가 끊긴 생각의 실마리를 다시 붙잡으려 애쓰며 말했다."], ["“He does not improve on closer acquaintance.", "\"그 사람은 가까이서 사귀어 봐도 나아지질 않아."], ["If you want him defined, here he is: a prime, well-fed beast such as takes medals at the cattle shows, and nothing more,”", "한마디로 규정하자면, 이런 거지. 가축 품평회에서 상이나 타 가는 잘 먹은 일등급 짐승, 그 이상도 그 이하도 아니야.\""], ["he said, with a tone of vexation that interested her.", "그가 짜증 섞인 어조로 말했는데, 그녀는 그 어조에 흥미가 일었다."]], [["“No; how so?”", "\"아니에요, 어째서요?\""], ["she replied.", "그녀가 대꾸했다."], ["“He’s seen a great deal, anyway; he’s cultured?”", "\"그래도 그 사람은 견문이 넓잖아요. 교양 있고요?\""]], [["“It’s an utterly different culture—their culture.", "\"그건 전혀 다른 교양이야. 그들만의 교양이지."], ["He’s cultivated, one sees, simply to be able to despise culture, as they despise everything but animal pleasures.”", "교양을 경멸할 수 있기 위해 교양을 쌓은 거야, 동물적 쾌락 말고는 모든 걸 경멸하듯이 말이야. 보면 알 수 있어.\""]], [["“But don’t you all care for these animal pleasures?”", "\"하지만 당신들 모두 그 동물적 쾌락을 좋아하지 않나요?\""], ["she said, and again he noticed a dark look in her eyes that avoided him.", "그녀가 말했고, 그는 다시 그를 피해 가는 그녀의 눈에 어두운 빛이 도는 것을 알아챘다."]], [["“How is it you’re defending him?”", "\"어째서 그 사람을 변호하는 거지?\""], ["he said, smiling.", "그가 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["“I’m not defending him, it’s nothing to me; but I imagine, if you had not cared for those pleasures yourself, you might have got out of them.", "\"변호하는 게 아니에요, 나랑은 아무 상관도 없는 사람인걸요. 다만, 당신이 그런 쾌락을 좋아하지 않았더라면 거기서 빠져나올 수 있었으리라는 생각이 드는 거죠."], ["But if it affords you satisfaction to gaze at Thérèse in the attire of Eve....”", "하지만 이브 차림의 테레즈를 바라보는 게 당신에게 만족을 준다면야…….\""]], [["“Again, the devil again,”", "\"또 마귀가, 또 마귀가.\""], ["Vronsky said, taking the hand she had laid on the table and kissing it.", "브론스키가 그녀가 탁자 위에 올려 둔 손을 잡아 입을 맞추며 말했다."]], [["“Yes; but I can’t help it.", "\"그래요, 하지만 어쩔 수가 없어요."], ["You don’t know what I have suffered waiting for you.", "당신을 기다리며 내가 얼마나 괴로웠는지 당신은 몰라요. 난 질투하는 게 아니라고 믿어요."], ["I believe I’m not jealous.", "질투하는 게 아니에요."], ["I’m not jealous: I believe you when you’re here; but when you’re away somewhere leading your life, so incomprehensible to me....”", "당신이 여기 있을 때는 당신을 믿어요. 하지만 당신이 어딘가 멀리서, 내가 도무지 헤아릴 수 없는 그 삶을 살고 있을 때면…….\""]], [["She turned away from him, pulled the hook at last out of the crochet work, and rapidly, with the help of her forefinger, began working loop after loop of the wool that was dazzling white in the lamplight, while the slender wrist moved swiftly, nervously in the embroidered cuff.", "그녀는 그에게서 몸을 돌리고, 마침내 뜨개질감에서 코바늘을 빼내더니, 집게손가락을 놀려 가며 램프 불빛 아래 눈부시게 새하얀 털실로 한 코, 한 코 빠르게 떠 나가기 시작했다. 그러는 동안 가느다란 손목이 수놓인 소맷부리 안에서 잽싸게, 신경질적으로 움직였다."]], [["“How was it, then?", "\"그래서 어땠어요?"], ["Where did you meet Alexey Alexandrovitch?”", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치를 어디서 만났어요?\""], ["Her voice sounded in an unnatural and jarring tone.", "그녀의 목소리가 부자연스럽고 거슬리는 어조로 울렸다."]], [["“We ran up against each other in the doorway.”", "\"문간에서 딱 마주쳤지.\""]], [["“And he bowed to you like this?”", "\"그래서 이렇게 당신한테 인사하던가요?\""]], [["She drew a long face, and half-closing her eyes, quickly transformed her expression, folded her hands, and Vronsky suddenly saw in her beautiful face the very expression with which Alexey Alexandrovitch had bowed to him.", "그녀는 얼굴을 길게 빼고 눈을 반쯤 감더니, 재빨리 표정을 바꾸고 두 손을 모았다. 그러자 브론스키는 문득 그녀의 아름다운 얼굴에서 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 자기에게 인사하던 바로 그 표정을 보았다."], ["He smiled, while she laughed gaily, with that sweet, deep laugh, which was one of her greatest charms.", "그는 미소 지었고, 그녀는 그 달콤하고 깊은 웃음으로 명랑하게 웃었다. 그것은 그녀의 가장 큰 매력 가운데 하나였다."]], [["“I don’t understand him in the least,”", "\"난 그 사람을 도무지 이해할 수가 없어.\""], ["said Vronsky.", "브론스키가 말했다."], ["“If after your avowal to him at your country house he had broken with you, if he had called me out—but this I can’t understand.", "\"당신네 시골 별장에서 당신이 그에게 고백한 후에 그가 당신과 갈라섰다면, 나한테 결투를 신청했다면 모르겠는데, 이건 정말 이해가 안 돼."], ["How can he put up with such a position?", "어떻게 그런 처지를 견딜 수가 있지?"], ["He feels it, that’s evident.”", "그도 느끼고 있어, 그건 분명한데.\""]], [["“He?”", "\"그가요?\""], ["she said sneeringly.", "그녀가 비웃듯 말했다."], ["“He’s perfectly satisfied.”", "\"그는 더할 나위 없이 만족하고 있어요.\""]], [["“What are we all miserable for, when everything might be so happy?”", "\"모든 게 이토록 행복할 수 있는데, 우리는 다들 무엇 때문에 비참한 걸까?\""]], [["“Only not he.", "\"오직 그 사람만은 아니죠."], ["Don’t I know him, the falsity in which he’s utterly steeped?...", "내가 그를 모를까요, 그 사람이 푹 절어 있는 그 거짓을?……"], ["Could one, with any feeling, live as he is living with me?", "어떤 감정이라도 있는 사람이라면 지금 그 사람처럼 나와 함께 살 수 있을까요?"], ["He understands nothing, and feels nothing.", "그는 아무것도 이해하지 못하고, 아무것도 느끼지 못해요."], ["Could a man of any feeling live in the same house with his unfaithful wife?", "감정이 조금이라도 있는 남자라면 부정한 아내와 한집에서 살 수 있겠어요?"], ["Could he talk to her, call her ‘my dear’?”", "아내한테 말을 걸고, '여보'라고 부를 수 있겠어요?\""]], [["And again she could not help mimicking him: “‘Anna, ma chère; Anna, dear!’”", "그러고는 다시 그녀는 그를 흉내 내지 않을 수 없었다. \"'안나, ma chère, 안나, 여보!'\""]], [["“He’s not a man, not a human being—he’s a doll!", "\"그는 남자가 아니에요, 인간이 아니에요. 인형이에요!"], ["No one knows him; but I know him.", "아무도 그를 모르지만, 난 알아요."], ["Oh, if I’d been in his place, I’d long ago have killed, have torn to pieces a wife like me.", "오, 내가 그의 처지였다면, 나 같은 아내는 진작에 죽여 버리고, 갈가리 찢어 버렸을 거예요."], ["I wouldn’t have said, ‘Anna, ma chère’!", "'안나, ma chère' 따위는 말하지 않았을 거라고요!"], ["He’s not a man, he’s an official machine.", "그는 남자가 아니에요, 관청의 기계예요."], ["He doesn’t understand that I’m your wife, that he’s outside, that he’s superfluous....", "그는 내가 당신의 아내라는 것을, 자기가 바깥 사람이라는 것을, 자기가 군더더기라는 것을 이해하지 못해요……"], ["Don’t let’s talk of him!...”", "그 사람 얘긴 그만해요!……\""]], [["“You’re unfair, very unfair, dearest,”", "* ma chère: 프랑스어로 '내 사랑하는 사람'."], ["said Vronsky, trying to soothe her.", "\"당신은 불공평해, 아주 불공평해, 여보.\""], ["“But never mind, don’t let’s talk of him.", "브론스키가 그녀를 달래려 애쓰며 말했다."], ["Tell me what you’ve been doing?", "\"하지만 괜찮아, 그 사람 얘긴 그만하자."], ["What is the matter?", "당신이 뭘 하고 지냈는지 얘기해 봐."], ["What has been wrong with you, and what did the doctor say?”", "무슨 일이야? 어디가 안 좋았던 거지, 의사는 뭐라고 했어?\""]], [["She looked at him with mocking amusement.", "그녀는 조롱 섞인 즐거움이 어린 눈으로 그를 바라보았다."], ["Evidently she had hit on other absurd and grotesque aspects in her husband and was awaiting the moment to give expression to them.", "그녀는 분명 남편에게서 또 다른 우스꽝스럽고 기괴한 면들을 찾아낸 듯했고, 그것을 입 밖에 낼 순간을 기다리고 있었다."]], [["But he went on:", "그러나 그는 말을 이어 갔다."]], [["“I imagine that it’s not illness, but your condition.", "\"내 생각엔 그건 병이 아니라 당신의 상태인 것 같은데."], ["When will it be?”", "언제쯤이지?\""]], [["The ironical light died away in her eyes, but a different smile, a consciousness of something, he did not know what, and of quiet melancholy, came over her face.", "그녀의 눈에서 비꼬는 빛이 사라졌지만, 다른 미소가, 무언가에 대한—그가 무엇인지 알 수 없는 무언가에 대한—깨달음과 조용한 우수가 그녀의 얼굴에 떠올랐다."]], [["“Soon, soon.", "\"곧, 곧이에요."], ["You say that our position is miserable, that we must put an end to it.", "당신은 우리 처지가 비참하다고, 끝장을 내야 한다고 말하죠."], ["If you knew how terrible it is to me, what I would give to be able to love you freely and boldly!", "자유롭고 떳떳하게 당신을 사랑할 수 있다면, 그게 나한테 얼마나 간절한지, 그러지 못하는 게 나한테 얼마나 끔찍한지 당신이 알기만 한다면!"], ["I should not torture myself and torture you with my jealousy....", "그러면 내 질투로 나 자신을 괴롭히고 당신을 괴롭히지도 않을 텐데……"], ["And it will come soon, but not as we expect.”", "그리고 그건 곧 올 거예요, 하지만 우리가 기대하는 식은 아니에요.\""]], [["And at the thought of how it would come, she seemed so pitiable to herself that tears came into her eyes, and she could not go on.", "그리고 그것이 어떻게 올지를 생각하니 자신이 너무도 가엾게 느껴져 눈에 눈물이 차올랐고, 그녀는 말을 이어 가지 못했다."], ["She laid her hand on his sleeve, dazzling and white with its rings in the lamplight.", "그녀는 램프 불빛 속에서 반지들과 함께 눈부시게 새하얀 손을 그의 소매에 얹었다."]], [["“It won’t come as we suppose.", "\"그건 우리가 짐작하는 식으로 오지 않을 거예요."], ["I didn’t mean to say this to you, but you’ve made me.", "당신한테 이 말을 하려던 건 아니었는데, 당신이 하게 만들었어요."], ["Soon, soon, all will be over, and we shall all, all be at peace, and suffer no more.”", "곧, 곧, 모든 게 끝나고, 우리 모두, 우리 모두 평안해질 거예요, 더는 괴로워하지 않을 거예요.\""]], [["“I don’t understand,”", "\"무슨 말인지 모르겠어.\""], ["he said, understanding her.", "그는 그녀의 말을 이해하면서도 그렇게 말했다."]], [["“You asked when?", "\"언제냐고 물었죠?"], ["Soon.", "곧이에요."], ["And I shan’t live through it.", "그리고 난 그걸 견디고 살아남지 못할 거예요."], ["Don’t interrupt me!”", "내 말 가로막지 마요!\""], ["and she made haste to speak.", "그녀가 서둘러 말했다. \"난 알아요."], ["“I know it; I know for certain.", "확실히 알아요. 난 죽을 거예요."], ["I shall die; and I’m very glad I shall die, and release myself and you.”", "그리고 내가 죽어서 나 자신과 당신을 풀어 주게 되어서 무척 기뻐요.\""]], [["Tears dropped from her eyes; he bent down over her hand and began kissing it, trying to hide his emotion, which, he knew, had no sort of grounds, though he could not control it.", "그녀의 눈에서 눈물이 떨어졌다. 그는 그녀의 손 위로 몸을 숙여 거기에 입을 맞추기 시작했다. 아무런 근거도 없다는 것을 알면서도 제어할 수 없는 자신의 감정을 감추려 애쓰면서."]], [["“Yes, it’s better so,”", "\"그래요, 그편이 나아요.\""], ["she said, tightly gripping his hand.", "그녀가 그의 손을 꽉 움켜쥐며 말했다."], ["“That’s the only way, the only way left us.”", "\"그게 유일한 길이에요, 우리에게 남은 유일한 길이에요.\""]], [["He had recovered himself, and lifted his head.", "그는 마음을 추스르고 고개를 들었다."]], [["“How absurd!", "\"얼마나 터무니없는 소리야!"], ["What absurd nonsense you are talking!”", "당신 지금 얼마나 어이없는 헛소리를 하고 있는 거지!\""]], [["“No, it’s the truth.”", "\"아니에요, 그건 진실이에요.\""]], [["“What, what’s the truth?”", "\"뭐가, 뭐가 진실이라는 거야?\""]], [["“That I shall die.", "\"내가 죽으리라는 거요."], ["I have had a dream.”", "꿈을 꿨어요.\""]], [["“A dream?”", "\"꿈?\""], ["repeated Vronsky, and instantly he recalled the peasant of his dream.", "브론스키가 되뇌었고, 그 순간 자기 꿈에 나온 농부가 떠올랐다."]], [["“Yes, a dream,”", "\"그래요, 꿈이에요.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“It’s a long while since I dreamed it.", "\"꽤 오래전에 꾼 꿈이에요. 꿈속에서 난 침실로 뛰어 들어갔어요."], ["I dreamed that I ran into my bedroom, that I had to get something there, to find out something; you know how it is in dreams,”", "거기서 뭔가를 가져와야 했고, 뭔가를 알아내야 했죠. 꿈에서란 게 어떤지 당신도 알잖아요.\""], ["she said, her eyes wide with horror; “and in the bedroom, in the corner, stood something.”", "그녀가 공포로 눈을 크게 뜨고 말했다. \"그런데 침실 구석에 뭔가가 서 있었어요.\""]], [["“Oh, what nonsense!", "\"오, 무슨 헛소리야!"], ["How can you believe....”", "어떻게 그런 걸 믿을 수가…….\""]], [["But she would not let him interrupt her.", "그러나 그녀는 그가 말을 가로막게 두지 않았다."], ["What she was saying was too important to her.", "자기가 하는 말이 그녀에게는 너무도 중요했다."]], [["“And the something turned round, and I saw it was a peasant with a disheveled beard, little, and dreadful looking.", "\"그런데 그 뭔가가 돌아섰고, 보니까 그건 농부였어요. 수염이 텁수룩하고, 키가 작고, 끔찍하게 생긴."], ["I wanted to run away, but he bent down over a sack, and was fumbling there with his hands....”", "난 달아나려고 했는데, 그가 자루 위로 몸을 숙이고 거기서 두 손으로 뭔가를 더듬거리고 있었어요…….\""]], [["She showed how he had moved his hands.", "그녀는 그가 어떻게 손을 움직였는지 흉내 내 보였다."], ["There was terror in her face.", "그녀의 얼굴에는 공포가 어려 있었다."], ["And Vronsky, remembering his dream, felt the same terror filling his soul.", "그리고 브론스키는 자기 꿈을 떠올리며 똑같은 공포가 자기 영혼을 채우는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["“He was fumbling and kept talking quickly, quickly in French, you know: Il faut le battre, le fer, le broyer, le pétrir....", "\"그는 더듬거리면서 프랑스어로 계속 빠르게, 빠르게 중얼거렸어요. Il faut le battre, le fer, le broyer, le pétrir……라고요."], ["And in my horror I tried to wake up, and woke up ...", "그래서 난 무서워서 깨어나려고 했고, 깨어났어요…… 그런데 꿈속에서 깨어난 거였어요."], ["but woke up in the dream.", "그러고는 그게 무슨 뜻인지 자문하기 시작했죠."], ["And I began asking myself what it meant.", "그러자 코르네이가 나한테 말하는 거예요."], ["And Korney said to me: ‘In childbirth you’ll die, ma’am, you’ll die....’ And I woke up.”", "'해산하다가 죽으실 거예요, 마님, 죽으실 거예요……'라고. 그러고서 난 잠에서 깼어요.\""]], [["“What nonsense, what nonsense!”", "\"무슨 헛소리야, 무슨 헛소리.\""], ["said Vronsky; but he felt himself that there was no conviction in his voice.", "브론스키가 말했지만, 자기 목소리에 아무런 확신도 실려 있지 않다는 것을 스스로도 느꼈다."]], [["“But don’t let’s talk of it. Ring the bell, I’ll have tea. And stay a little now; it’s not long I shall....”", "* Il faut le battre, le fer, le broyer, le pétrir: 프랑스어로 '쇠를 두드려야 한다, 그것을 빻고, 그것을 반죽해야 한다'."]], [["But all at once she stopped.", "\"하지만 그 얘긴 그만해요. 종을 울려 줘요, 차를 마실래요."], ["The expression of her face instantaneously changed.", "그리고 이제 잠깐만 더 있어 줘요. 내가 오래…….\""], ["Horror and excitement were suddenly replaced by a look of soft, solemn, blissful attention.", "그러나 그녀는 별안간 말을 멈췄다. 그녀의 얼굴 표정이 순식간에 달라졌다."], ["He could not comprehend the meaning of the change.", "공포와 흥분이 갑자기 부드럽고 엄숙하며 더없이 행복한 주의(注意)의 표정으로 바뀌었다."], ["She was listening to the stirring of the new life within her.", "그는 그 변화의 의미를 이해할 수 없었다. 그녀는 자기 안에서 꿈틀거리는 새 생명의 움직임에 귀 기울이고 있었던 것이다."]], [["# Chapter 4", "# Chapter 4"]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch, after meeting Vronsky on his own steps, drove, as he had intended, to the Italian opera.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 자기 집 현관 계단에서 브론스키와 마주친 뒤, 애초 작정한 대로 이탈리아 오페라 극장으로 마차를 몰았다."], ["He sat through two acts there, and saw everyone he had wanted to see.", "그곳에서 두 막을 끝까지 보았고, 보고 싶었던 사람들을 모두 만났다."], ["On returning home, he carefully scrutinized the hat stand, and noticing that there was not a military overcoat there, he went, as usual, to his own room.", "집으로 돌아와서는 모자걸이를 주의 깊게 살폈는데, 거기에 군용 외투가 걸려 있지 않은 것을 확인하고는 여느 때처럼 자기 방으로 갔다."], ["But, contrary to his usual habit, he did not go to bed, he walked up and down his study till three o’clock in the morning.", "그러나 평소의 습관과 달리 잠자리에 들지 않고 새벽 세 시까지 서재를 이리저리 거닐었다."], ["The feeling of furious anger with his wife, who would not observe the proprieties and keep to the one stipulation he had laid on her, not to receive her lover in her own home, gave him no peace.", "아내에 대한 격렬한 분노의 감정이 그를 가만두지 않았다. 아내는 예법을 지키지 않았고, 그가 단 하나 내건 조건, 곧 자기 집에서 정부를 맞아들이지 말라는 그 조건마저 지키지 않았던 것이다."], ["She had not complied with his request, and he was bound to punish her and carry out his threat—obtain a divorce and take away his son.", "아내는 그의 요구를 따르지 않았고, 그는 아내를 벌하고 위협한 대로 실행해야만 했다. 이혼하고 아들을 데려가야 했다."], ["He knew all the difficulties connected with this course, but he had said he would do it, and now he must carry out his threat.", "그는 이 일에 따르는 온갖 난관을 잘 알고 있었지만, 자기가 그렇게 하겠다고 말했으니 이제 그 위협을 실행에 옮겨야 했다."], ["Countess Lidia Ivanovna had hinted that this was the best way out of his position, and of late the obtaining of divorces had been brought to such perfection that Alexey Alexandrovitch saw a possibility of overcoming the formal difficulties.", "리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인은 그것이 그의 처지에서 벗어나는 가장 좋은 길이라고 넌지시 일러 준 바 있었고, 더구나 근래에는 이혼 수속이 워낙 완벽하게 다듬어진 터라 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 형식상의 난관을 극복할 가능성이 있다고 보았다."], ["Misfortunes never come singly, and the affairs of the reorganization of the native tribes, and of the irrigation of the lands of the Zaraisky province, had brought such official worries upon Alexey Alexandrovitch that he had been of late in a continual condition of extreme irritability.", "불행은 결코 홀로 오는 법이 없으니, 토착 부족들의 재편 문제며 자라이스크 현의 토지 관개 문제까지 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에게 어찌나 많은 공무상의 근심을 안겨 주었던지, 그는 근래 들어 줄곧 극도로 신경이 곤두선 상태였다."]], [["He did not sleep the whole night, and his fury, growing in a sort of vast, arithmetical progression, reached its highest limits in the morning.", "그는 밤새 한숨도 자지 못했고, 그의 분노는 일종의 거대한 산술급수처럼 불어나 아침이 되자 절정에 이르렀다."], ["He dressed in haste, and as though carrying his cup full of wrath, and fearing to spill any over, fearing to lose with his wrath the energy necessary for the interview with his wife, he went into her room directly he heard she was up.", "그는 서둘러 옷을 입었고, 마치 분노로 가득 찬 잔을 받쳐 들고 한 방울도 흘리지 않으려는 듯, 그 분노와 함께 아내와의 담판에 필요한 기력마저 잃지 않으려는 듯, 아내가 일어났다는 소리를 듣자마자 곧장 그녀의 방으로 들어갔다."]], [["Anna, who had thought she knew her husband so well, was amazed at his appearance when he went in to her.", "남편을 잘 안다고 여겨 왔던 안나는 그가 방으로 들어서는 순간 그 모습에 깜짝 놀랐다."], ["His brow was lowering, and his eyes stared darkly before him, avoiding her eyes; his mouth was tightly and contemptuously shut.", "그의 이마는 잔뜩 찌푸려져 있었고, 두 눈은 그녀의 눈길을 피한 채 음울하게 앞을 노려보고 있었으며, 입은 경멸하듯 굳게 다물려 있었다."], ["In his walk, in his gestures, in the sound of his voice there was a determination and firmness such as his wife had never seen in him.", "그의 걸음걸이에도, 몸짓에도, 목소리에도, 아내가 여태껏 그에게서 한 번도 본 적 없는 단호함과 결연함이 깃들어 있었다."], ["He went into her room, and without greeting her, walked straight up to her writing-table, and taking her keys, opened a drawer.", "그는 그녀의 방으로 들어와 인사도 없이 곧장 그녀의 책상으로 다가가더니, 열쇠를 집어 서랍을 열었다."]], [["“What do you want?”", "\"뭘 찾으시는 거예요?\""], ["she cried.", "그녀가 소리쳤다."]], [["“Your lover’s letters,”", "\"당신 정부의 편지들이오.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“They’re not here,”", "\"거긴 없어요.\""], ["she said, shutting the drawer; but from that action he saw he had guessed right, and roughly pushing away her hand, he quickly snatched a portfolio in which he knew she used to put her most important papers.", "그녀는 서랍을 닫으며 말했다. 하지만 그 행동을 보고 그는 자기가 제대로 짚었음을 알아챘고, 그녀의 손을 거칠게 밀쳐 내면서 그녀가 가장 중요한 서류들을 넣어 두곤 한다는 것을 아는 손가방을 잽싸게 낚아챘다."], ["She tried to pull the portfolio away, but he pushed her back.", "그녀가 그 손가방을 잡아당기려 했지만, 그는 그녀를 떠밀었다."]], [["“Sit down!", "\"앉아요!"], ["I have to speak to you,”", "당신한테 할 말이 있소.\""], ["he said, putting the portfolio under his arm, and squeezing it so tightly with his elbow that his shoulder stood up.", "그는 손가방을 겨드랑이에 끼고 팔꿈치로 어찌나 세게 눌렀던지 어깨가 위로 솟아올랐다."], ["Amazed and intimidated, she gazed at him in silence.", "그녀는 놀라고 겁에 질린 채 말없이 그를 바라보았다."]], [["“I told you that I would not allow you to receive your lover in this house.”", "\"이 집에서 당신 정부를 맞아들이는 일은 용납하지 않겠다고 내가 말했을 텐데.\""]], [["“I had to see him to....”", "\"그이를 만나야 했어요, 그건…….\""]], [["She stopped, not finding a reason.", "그녀는 이유를 찾지 못해 말을 멈췄다."]], [["“I do not enter into the details of why a woman wants to see her lover.”", "\"여자가 무엇 때문에 정부를 만나고 싶어 하는지, 그 자세한 사정까지 내가 따지지는 않겠소.\""]], [["“I meant, I only....”", "\"제 말은, 그저…….\""], ["she said, flushing hotly.", "그녀가 얼굴을 화끈 붉히며 말했다."], ["This coarseness of his angered her, and gave her courage.", "그의 이런 천박함이 그녀를 화나게 했고, 동시에 용기를 주었다."], ["“Surely you must feel how easy it is for you to insult me?”", "\"당신은 나를 모욕하는 게 얼마나 쉬운 일인지 정녕 모르세요?\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“An honest man and an honest woman may be insulted, but to tell a thief he’s a thief is simply la constatation d’un fait.”", "\"정직한 남자, 정직한 여자라면 모욕이라 할 수 있겠지. 하지만 도둑한테 도둑이라고 말하는 건 그저 la constatation d'un fait일 뿐이오.\""]], [["“This cruelty is something new I did not know in you.”", "* la constatation d'un fait: 프랑스어로 '사실의 확인'."]], [["“You call it cruelty for a husband to give his wife liberty, giving her the honorable protection of his name, simply on the condition of observing the proprieties: is that cruelty?”", "\"이런 잔인함은 내가 몰랐던 당신의 새로운 면이네요.\" \"남편이 아내에게 자유를 주고, 제 이름이라는 떳떳한 보호막까지 내주면서, 단지 예법을 지키라는 조건 하나를 다는 것을 두고 당신은 잔인하다 하는 거요? 그게 잔인한 거요?\""]], [["“It’s worse than cruel—it’s base, if you want to know!”", "\"잔인한 것보다 더 나빠요. 알고 싶다면 말해 주죠, 그건 비열한 거예요!\""], ["Anna cried, in a rush of hatred, and getting up, she was going away.", "안나가 증오를 터뜨리며 외쳤고, 일어나 나가려 했다."]], [["“No!”", "\"안 돼!\""], ["he shrieked, in his shrill voice, which pitched a note higher than usual even, and his big hands clutching her by the arm so violently that red marks were left from the bracelet he was squeezing, he forcibly sat her down in her place.", "그가 여느 때보다 한 음정이나 높아진 날카로운 목소리로 새된 비명을 질렀고, 큼직한 두 손으로 어찌나 거칠게 그녀의 팔을 움켜쥐었던지 그가 짓누른 팔찌 자국이 벌겋게 남았다. 그는 그녀를 억지로 제자리에 도로 앉혔다."]], [["“Base!", "\"비열하다고!"], ["If you care to use that word, what is base is to forsake husband and child for a lover, while you eat your husband’s bread!”", "굳이 그 말을 쓰겠다면, 정작 비열한 건 남편의 빵을 먹으면서 정부 때문에 남편과 자식을 저버리는 짓이지!\""]], [["She bowed her head.", "그녀는 고개를 숙였다."], ["She did not say what she had said the evening before to her lover, that he was her husband, and her husband was superfluous; she did not even think that.", "그녀는 전날 저녁 정부에게 했던 말, 곧 그이가 자기 남편이고 당신은 군더더기일 뿐이라는 말을 입에 올리지 않았다. 그런 생각조차 하지 않았다."], ["She felt all the justice of his words, and only said softly:", "그녀는 그의 말이 모두 옳다고 느꼈고, 그저 나직이 말했다."]], [["“You cannot describe my position as worse than I feel it to be myself; but what are you saying all this for?”", "\"내 처지를 두고 나 자신이 느끼는 것보다 더 나쁘게 그릴 수는 없을 거예요. 그런데 이런 말씀을 왜 다 하시는 거죠?\""]], [["“What am I saying it for?", "\"왜 하느냐고? 무엇 때문에?\""], ["what for?” he went on, as angrily.", "그가 여전히 노기 띤 목소리로 말을 이었다."], ["“That you may know that since you have not carried out my wishes in regard to observing outward decorum, I will take measures to put an end to this state of things.”", "\"겉으로나마 체면을 지키라는 내 뜻을 당신이 따르지 않았으니, 이런 상황을 끝내기 위한 조치를 내가 취하겠다는 걸 알리려는 거요.\""]], [["“Soon, very soon, it will end, anyway,”", "\"어차피 곧, 아주 곧 끝날 거예요.\""], ["she said; and again, at the thought of death near at hand and now desired, tears came into her eyes.", "그녀는 말했다. 그리고 다시금, 가까이 다가온, 이제는 도리어 바라게 된 죽음을 떠올리자 눈에 눈물이 차올랐다."]], [["“It will end sooner than you and your lover have planned!", "\"당신과 당신 정부가 꾸민 것보다 더 빨리 끝나게 될 거요!"], ["If you must have the satisfaction of animal passion....”", "짐승 같은 욕정의 쾌락을 기어이 채워야겠다면…….\""]], [["“Alexey Alexandrovitch!", "\"알렉세이 알렉산드로비치!"], ["I won’t say it’s not generous, but it’s not like a gentleman to strike anyone who’s down.”", "너그럽지 못하다고는 안 하겠어요. 하지만 쓰러진 사람을 때리는 건 신사다운 짓이 아니에요.\""]], [["“Yes, you only think of yourself!", "\"그래, 당신은 자기 생각밖에 안 하지!"], ["But the sufferings of a man who was your husband have no interest for you.", "한때 당신 남편이었던 사람의 고통 따위엔 관심도 없고."], ["You don’t care that his whole life is ruined, that he is thuff ...", "그자의 인생 전부가 망가졌다는 것도, 그자가 고통스러… 고통스러…….\""], ["thuff....”", "그래 봤자 당신은 아무렇지도 않겠지."]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch was speaking so quickly that he stammered, and was utterly unable to articulate the word “suffering.”", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 어찌나 빨리 말을 쏟아 냈던지 말을 더듬었고, '고통스럽다'라는 단어를 도무지 제대로 발음하지 못했다."], ["In the end he pronounced it “thuffering.”", "결국 그는 그것을 '고통스러읍다'라고 내뱉었다."], ["She wanted to laugh, and was immediately ashamed that anything could amuse her at such a moment.", "그녀는 웃음이 터질 것 같았고, 이런 순간에 무언가가 자기를 웃길 수 있다는 사실에 곧바로 부끄러워졌다."], ["And for the first time, for an instant, she felt for him, put herself in his place, and was sorry for him.", "그리고 처음으로, 한순간, 그녀는 그의 심정을 헤아렸고, 그의 처지에 자신을 놓아 보았으며, 그가 가엾어졌다."], ["But what could she say or do? Her head sank, and she sat silent.", "하지만 그녀가 무슨 말을, 무슨 행동을 할 수 있었겠는가?"], ["He too was silent for some time, and then began speaking in a frigid, less shrill voice, emphasizing random words that had no special significance.", "그녀는 고개를 떨군 채 말없이 앉아 있었다. 그도 한동안 말이 없다가, 이내 차갑고 덜 날카로운 목소리로, 별다른 의미도 없는 말들을 되는대로 강조해 가며 말하기 시작했다."]], [["“I came to tell you....”", "\"당신한테 말하러 왔소…….\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["She glanced at him.", "그녀는 그를 흘끗 보았다."], ["“No, it was my fancy,”", "'아니야, 내 착각이었어.'"], ["she thought, recalling the expression of his face when he stumbled over the word “suffering.”", "그녀는 그가 '고통스럽다'라는 단어에서 더듬거릴 때의 얼굴 표정을 떠올리며 생각했다."], ["“No; can a man with those dull eyes, with that self-satisfied complacency, feel anything?”", "'아니, 저렇게 흐리멍덩한 눈에, 저런 자기만족에 젖은 사람이 무얼 느낄 수 있겠어?'"]], [["“I cannot change anything,”", "\"나는 아무것도 바꿀 수 없어요.\""], ["she whispered.", "그녀가 속삭이듯 말했다."]], [["“I have come to tell you that I am going tomorrow to Moscow, and shall not return again to this house, and you will receive notice of what I decide through the lawyer into whose hands I shall intrust the task of getting a divorce.", "\"당신한테 말해 두러 왔소. 나는 내일 모스크바로 떠날 것이고, 다시는 이 집으로 돌아오지 않을 거요."], ["My son is going to my sister’s,”", "그리고 이혼을 처리하는 일을 맡길 변호사를 통해 내가 결정한 바를 통보받게 될 거요."], ["said Alexey Alexandrovitch, with an effort recalling what he had meant to say about his son.", "내 아들은 내 누이에게 보낼 거요.\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 아들에 관해 하려 했던 말을 가까스로 떠올리며 말했다."]], [["“You take Seryozha to hurt me,”", "\"당신은 나를 괴롭히려고 세료자를 데려가는 거예요.\""], ["she said, looking at him from under her brows.", "그녀가 이맛살 아래로 그를 올려다보며 말했다."], ["“You do not love him....", "\"당신은 그 애를 사랑하지 않잖아요……"], ["Leave me Seryozha!”", "세료자만은 내게 남겨 줘요!\""]], [["“Yes, I have lost even my affection for my son, because he is associated with the repulsion I feel for you.", "\"그래, 나는 아들에 대한 정마저 잃었소. 그 애가 당신에게 느끼는 혐오와 얽혀 있으니까."], ["But still I shall take him.", "하지만 그래도 나는 그 애를 데려가겠소."], ["Good-bye!”", "잘 있으시오!\""]], [["And he was going away, but now she detained him.", "그러고는 그가 나가려 했지만, 이번에는 그녀가 그를 붙잡았다."]], [["“Alexey Alexandrovitch, leave me Seryozha!”", "\"알렉세이 알렉산드로비치, 세료자만은 내게 남겨 줘요!\""], ["she whispered once more.", "그녀가 다시 한번 속삭였다."], ["“I have nothing else to say.", "\"더는 할 말이 없어요. 내가……"], ["Leave Seryozha till my ...", "곧 몸을 풀 때까지만 세료자를 남겨 줘요."], ["I shall soon be confined; leave him!”", "그 애를 남겨 줘요!\""]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch flew into a rage, and, snatching his hand from her, he went out of the room without a word.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 발끈 화를 내며 그녀에게서 손을 홱 빼더니, 한마디 말도 없이 방을 나가 버렸다."]], [["# Chapter 5", "# Chapter 5"]], [["The waiting-room of the celebrated Petersburg lawyer was full when Alexey Alexandrovitch entered it.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 페테르부르크의 저명한 변호사 사무실 대기실에 들어섰을 때, 그곳은 사람들로 가득 차 있었다."], ["Three ladies—an old lady, a young lady, and a merchant’s wife—and three gentlemen—one a German banker with a ring on his finger, the second a merchant with a beard, and the third a wrathful-looking government clerk in official uniform, with a cross on his neck—had obviously been waiting a long while already.", "여자 셋—노부인 한 명, 젊은 여자 한 명, 상인의 아내 한 명—과 신사 셋—손가락에 반지를 낀 독일인 은행가 한 명, 턱수염을 기른 상인 한 명, 그리고 목에 십자가를 단, 관복을 입은 성난 얼굴의 관리 한 명—이 누가 봐도 이미 오랫동안 기다려 온 모양이었다."], ["Two clerks were writing at tables with scratching pens.", "서기 둘이 책상에서 사각거리는 펜으로 무언가를 쓰고 있었다."], ["The appurtenances of the writing-tables, about which Alexey Alexandrovitch was himself very fastidious, were exceptionally good.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치 자신이 책상 비품에 대해서는 무척 까다로운 편이었는데, 이곳의 비품은 유난히 훌륭했다."], ["He could not help observing this.", "그는 그것을 알아보지 않을 수 없었다."], ["One of the clerks, without getting up, turned wrathfully to Alexey Alexandrovitch, half closing his eyes.", "서기 하나가 일어서지도 않은 채 눈을 반쯤 감으며 성난 얼굴로 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치 쪽으로 돌아앉았다."], ["“What are you wanting?”", "\"무슨 일로 오셨소?\""]], [["He replied that he had to see the lawyer on some business.", "그는 어떤 용무로 변호사를 만나야 한다고 대답했다."]], [["“He is engaged,”", "\"바쁘십니다.\""], ["the clerk responded severely, and he pointed with his pen at the persons waiting, and went on writing.", "서기가 무뚝뚝하게 대꾸하고는 펜으로 기다리는 사람들을 가리키더니, 다시 글을 써 내려갔다."]], [["“Can’t he spare time to see me?”", "\"나를 만날 시간을 좀 내줄 수 없겠소?\""], ["said Alexey Alexandrovitch.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 말했다."]], [["“He has no time free; he is always busy.", "\"빈 시간이 없으십니다. 늘 바쁘시니까요."], ["Kindly wait your turn.”", "차례를 기다려 주십시오.\""]], [["“Then I must trouble you to give him my card,”", "\"그렇다면 수고스럽겠지만 이 명함을 그분께 전해 주시오.\""], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch said with dignity, seeing the impossibility of preserving his incognito.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 자기 신분을 감추는 것이 불가능함을 깨닫고 위엄 있게 말했다."]], [["The clerk took the card and, obviously not approving of what he read on it, went to the door.", "서기는 명함을 받아 들고, 거기 적힌 것이 마뜩잖은 기색이 분명한 채로 문 쪽으로 갔다."]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch was in principle in favor of the publicity of legal proceedings, though for some higher official considerations he disliked the application of the principle in Russia, and disapproved of it, as far as he could disapprove of anything instituted by authority of the Emperor.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 원칙적으로 재판 공개에 찬성하는 편이었지만, 더 높은 관료적 견지에서 그 원칙을 러시아에 적용하는 것은 탐탁지 않게 여겼고, 황제의 권위로 제정된 것을 그가 못마땅해할 수 있는 한도 내에서 그것에 반대했다."], ["His whole life had been spent in administrative work, and consequently, when he did not approve of anything, his disapproval was softened by the recognition of the inevitability of mistakes and the possibility of reform in every department.", "그의 평생은 행정 업무로 점철되어 왔고, 따라서 그가 무언가를 못마땅하게 여길 때면 그 불만은 어느 부서에나 실수란 불가피하며 개혁의 여지가 있다는 인식으로 누그러지곤 했다."], ["In the new public law courts he disliked the restrictions laid on the lawyers conducting cases.", "그는 새로운 공개 법정에서 사건을 맡은 변호사들에게 가해지는 제약을 못마땅하게 여겼다."], ["But till then he had had nothing to do with the law courts, and so had disapproved of their publicity simply in theory; now his disapprobation was strengthened by the unpleasant impression made on him in the lawyer’s waiting room.", "하지만 그때까지 그는 법정과 아무 관계도 없었고, 그래서 단지 이론적으로만 재판 공개에 반대해 온 셈이었다. 그런데 이제 그 불만은 변호사 대기실에서 받은 불쾌한 인상으로 한층 강해졌다."]], [["“Coming immediately,”", "\"곧 들어오시랍니다.\""], ["said the clerk; and two minutes later there did actually appear in the doorway the large figure of an old solicitor who had been consulting with the lawyer himself.", "서기가 말했다. 그리고 이 분쯤 지나자 정말로 문간에 변호사와 직접 상담하고 나온 어느 늙은 사무 변호사의 커다란 형체가 나타났다."]], [["The lawyer was a little, squat, bald man, with a dark, reddish beard, light-colored long eyebrows, and an overhanging brow.", "변호사는 작고 땅딸막한 대머리 사내로, 거무스름한 불그스레한 턱수염에 연한 빛깔의 긴 눈썹, 툭 튀어나온 이마를 가지고 있었다."], ["He was attired as though for a wedding, from his cravat to his double watch-chain and varnished boots.", "그는 크라바트에서부터 이중으로 늘어뜨린 회중시곗줄과 반들반들 윤이 나는 부츠에 이르기까지, 마치 결혼식에라도 가는 차림이었다."], ["His face was clever and manly, but his dress was dandified and in bad taste.", "얼굴은 영리하고 남자다웠지만, 옷차림은 멋쟁이연하며 천박한 데가 있었다."]], [["“Pray walk in,”", "\"자, 들어오시지요.\""], ["said the lawyer, addressing Alexey Alexandrovitch; and, gloomily ushering Karenin in before him, he closed the door.", "변호사가 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에게 말하고는, 카레닌을 앞세워 침울하게 안으로 들이고 문을 닫았다."]], [["“Won’t you sit down?”", "\"앉으시지 않겠습니까?\""], ["He indicated an armchair at a writing-table covered with papers.", "그는 서류로 뒤덮인 책상 옆 안락의자를 가리켰다."], ["He sat down himself, and, rubbing his little hands with short fingers covered with white hairs, he bent his head on one side.", "그 자신도 자리에 앉아, 흰 털이 덮인 짤막한 손가락의 작은 두 손을 비비며 고개를 한쪽으로 갸웃했다."], ["But as soon as he was settled in this position a moth flew over the table.", "그러나 그가 이런 자세로 막 자리를 잡자마자 나방 한 마리가 책상 위로 날아들었다."], ["The lawyer, with a swiftness that could never have been expected of him, opened his hands, caught the moth, and resumed his former attitude.", "변호사는 도무지 그에게서 기대할 수 없을 만큼 날쌘 동작으로 두 손을 펴 나방을 잡고는 원래의 자세로 돌아갔다."]], [["“Before beginning to speak of my business,”", "\"제 용무를 말씀드리기에 앞서,\""], ["said Alexey Alexandrovitch, following the lawyer’s movements with wondering eyes, “I ought to observe that the business about which I have to speak to you is to be strictly private.”", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 놀란 눈으로 변호사의 동작을 좇으며 말했다. \"선생께 의논드릴 용무가 철저히 비밀에 부쳐져야 한다는 점을 말씀드려야겠습니다.\""]], [["The lawyer’s overhanging reddish mustaches were parted in a scarcely perceptible smile.", "변호사의 툭 튀어나온 불그스레한 콧수염이 거의 알아챌 수 없는 미소로 양옆으로 벌어졌다."]], [["“I should not be a lawyer if I could not keep the secrets confided to me.", "\"제게 맡겨진 비밀을 지키지 못한다면 변호사라 할 수 없겠지요."], ["But if you would like proof....”", "하지만 증거를 원하신다면……\""]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch glanced at his face, and saw that the shrewd, gray eyes were laughing, and seemed to know all about it already.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 그의 얼굴을 흘끗 보았고, 그 약삭빠른 잿빛 눈이 웃고 있으며, 이미 모든 것을 알고 있는 듯하다는 것을 알아챘다."]], [["“You know my name?”", "\"내 이름을 아시오?\""], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch resumed.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 말을 이었다."]], [["“I know you and the good”—again he caught a moth—“work you are doing, like every Russian,”", "\"모든 러시아인이 그러하듯, 저도 선생을, 그리고 선생께서 하시는 훌륭한\"—그는 또 나방 한 마리를 잡았다—\"일을 알고 있습니다.\""], ["said the lawyer, bowing.", "변호사가 머리를 숙이며 말했다."]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch sighed, plucking up his courage.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 한숨을 쉬며 용기를 끌어모았다."], ["But having once made up his mind he went on in his shrill voice, without timidity—or hesitation, accentuating here and there a word.", "그러나 일단 마음을 정하자 그는 주눅 들거나 머뭇거리지 않고, 군데군데 한 단어씩 힘주어 강조해 가며 날카로운 목소리로 말을 이었다."]], [["“I have the misfortune,”", "\"불행하게도,\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 입을 열었다."], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch began, “to have been deceived in my married life, and I desire to break off all relations with my wife by legal means—that is, to be divorced, but to do this so that my son may not remain with his mother.”", "\"저는 결혼 생활에서 기만을 당했고, 법적인 수단으로 아내와의 모든 관계를 끊고자 합니다. 다시 말해 이혼을 하되, 제 아들이 어미 곁에 남지 않도록 하는 식으로 말입니다.\""]], [["The lawyer’s gray eyes tried not to laugh, but they were dancing with irrepressible glee, and Alexey Alexandrovitch saw that it was not simply the delight of a man who has just got a profitable job: there was triumph and joy, there was a gleam like the malignant gleam he saw in his wife’s eyes.", "변호사의 잿빛 눈은 애써 웃지 않으려 했으나, 억누를 수 없는 환희로 춤추고 있었고, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 그것이 그저 돈벌이가 될 일감을 막 얻은 사람의 기쁨만은 아님을 알아챘다. 거기에는 승리감과 환희가 있었고, 그가 아내의 눈에서 보았던 악의에 찬 번득임과 같은 번득임이 있었다."]], [["“You desire my assistance in securing a divorce?”", "\"이혼을 성사시키는 데 제 도움을 원하신다는 말씀입니까?\""]], [["“Yes, precisely so; but I ought to warn you that I may be wasting your time and attention.", "\"그렇소, 바로 그렇소. 다만 미리 말해 두어야겠는데, 어쩌면 내가 선생의 시간과 수고를 헛되이 할지도 모르겠소."], ["I have come simply to consult you as a preliminary step.", "나는 그저 예비적인 단계로 의논하러 온 것이오."], ["I want a divorce, but the form in which it is possible is of great consequence to me.", "나는 이혼을 원하지만, 그것이 어떤 형식으로 가능한지가 내게는 매우 중요하오."], ["It is very possible that if that form does not correspond with my requirements I may give up a legal divorce.”", "만일 그 형식이 내 요구에 부합하지 않는다면, 법적 이혼을 단념할 수도 있소.\""]], [["“Oh, that’s always the case,”", "\"오, 늘 그런 법이지요.\""], ["said the lawyer, “and that’s always for you to decide.”", "변호사가 말했다. \"그리고 그것은 늘 선생께서 결정하실 일입니다.\""]], [["He let his eyes rest on Alexey Alexandrovitch’s feet, feeling that he might offend his client by the sight of his irrepressible amusement.", "그는 억누를 수 없는 즐거운 기색을 내보였다가 의뢰인의 기분을 상하게 할까 봐, 시선을 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 발에 내려놓았다."], ["He looked at a moth that flew before his nose, and moved his hands, but did not catch it from regard for Alexey Alexandrovitch’s position.", "그는 코앞으로 날아든 나방을 바라보며 손을 움직였으나, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 처지를 헤아려 잡지는 않았다."]], [["“Though in their general features our laws on this subject are known to me,”", "\"이 문제에 관한 우리 법률은 대략적인 윤곽이라면 제가 알고 있습니다만,\""], ["pursued Alexey Alexandrovitch, “I should be glad to have an idea of the forms in which such things are done in practice.”", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 말을 이었다. \"그런 일들이 실제로 어떤 형식을 거쳐 이루어지는지 짐작이라도 했으면 합니다.\""]], [["“You would be glad,”", "\"그러니까,\""], ["the lawyer, without lifting his eyes, responded, adopting, with a certain satisfaction, the tone of his client’s remarks, “for me to lay before you all the methods by which you could secure what you desire?”", "변호사는 눈을 들지 않은 채, 흡족한 기색으로 의뢰인의 어투를 따라 가며 대꾸했다. \"선생께서 원하시는 바를 이룰 수 있는 모든 방법을 제가 앞에 펼쳐 보여 드리기를 바라신다는 말씀이군요?\""]], [["And on receiving an assuring nod from Alexey Alexandrovitch, he went on, stealing a glance now and then at Alexey Alexandrovitch’s face, which was growing red in patches.", "그리고 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 그렇다는 듯 고개를 끄덕이자, 그는 군데군데 붉게 물들어 가는 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 얼굴을 이따금 슬쩍슬쩍 훔쳐보며 말을 이었다."]], [["“Divorce by our laws,”", "\"우리 법률에 따르면 이혼은,\""], ["he said, with a slight shade of disapprobation of our laws, “is possible, as you are aware, in the following cases....", "그는 우리 법률에 대한 미미한 못마땅함의 기색을 띠며 말했다. \"아시다시피 다음과 같은 경우에 가능합니다……"], ["Wait a little!”", "잠깐만!\""], ["he called to a clerk who put his head in at the door, but he got up all the same, said a few words to him, and sat down again. “...", "그는 문틈으로 머리를 들이민 서기에게 외치고는, 그래도 자리에서 일어나 그에게 몇 마디 건넨 뒤 다시 앉았다."], ["In the following cases: physical defect in the married parties, desertion without communication for five years,”", "\"……다음과 같은 경우입니다. 부부 가운데 한쪽의 신체적 결함, 연락 없이 오 년간 지속된 유기,\""], ["he said, crooking a short finger covered with hair, “adultery”", "그는 털 덮인 짤막한 손가락 하나를 꼬부리며 말했다. \"간통\""], ["(this word he pronounced with obvious satisfaction), “subdivided as follows”", "(이 단어를 그는 분명한 만족감을 담아 발음했다) \"이는 다음과 같이 세분됩니다\" (그는 세 경우와 그 세부 항목이 도무지 한데 묶일 수 없는 것임에도 살진 손가락을 계속 꼬부려 나갔다)."], ["(he continued to crook his fat fingers, though the three cases and their subdivisions could obviously not be classified together): “physical defect of the husband or of the wife, adultery of the husband or of the wife.”", "\"남편 또는 아내의 신체적 결함, 남편 또는 아내의 간통.\" 이제 손가락을 다 써 버린 그는 꼬부린 손가락을 모두 펴며 말을 이었다."], ["As by now all his fingers were used up, he uncrooked all his fingers and went on: “This is the theoretical view; but I imagine you have done me the honor to apply to me in order to learn its application in practice.", "\"이상이 이론적인 견해입니다. 하지만 선생께서 영광스럽게도 저를 찾아오신 것은 그 실제 적용을 알고자 하심이리라 짐작됩니다."], ["And therefore, guided by precedents, I must inform you that in practice cases of divorce may all be reduced to the following—there’s no physical defect, I may assume, nor desertion?...”", "그러니 선례에 비추어 말씀드리자면, 실제로 이혼 사건은 모두 다음의 경우로 귀결될 수 있습니다. 신체적 결함도, 유기도 없는 것으로 보아도 되겠지요……?\""]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch bowed his head in assent.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 그렇다는 뜻으로 고개를 숙였다."]], [["“—May be reduced to the following: adultery of one of the married parties, and the detection in the fact of the guilty party by mutual agreement, and failing such agreement, accidental detection.", "\"……그렇다면 다음의 경우로 귀결됩니다. 부부 가운데 한쪽의 간통, 그리고 쌍방 합의에 의한 유죄 당사자의 현장 적발, 그런 합의가 이루어지지 않을 경우에는 우발적인 적발입니다."], ["It must be admitted that the latter case is rarely met with in practice,”", "다만 후자의 경우는 실제로는 드물다는 점을 인정해야겠지요.\""], ["said the lawyer, and stealing a glance at Alexey Alexandrovitch he paused, as a man selling pistols, after enlarging on the advantages of each weapon, might await his customer’s choice.", "변호사는 이렇게 말하고는 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치를 슬쩍 훔쳐보며, 마치 권총을 파는 사람이 각 무기의 장점을 늘어놓은 뒤 손님의 선택을 기다리듯 말을 멈추었다."], ["But Alexey Alexandrovitch said nothing, and therefore the lawyer went on: “The most usual and simple, the sensible course, I consider, is adultery by mutual consent.", "그러나 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 아무 말도 하지 않았으므로, 변호사는 말을 이었다. \"제 생각에 가장 흔하고 단순하며 분별 있는 방법은 쌍방 합의에 의한 간통입니다."], ["I should not permit myself to express it so, speaking with a man of no education,”", "교양 없는 사람을 상대로라면 이렇게 표현하지는 않겠습니다만,\" 그가 말했다."], ["he said, “but I imagine that to you this is comprehensible.”", "\"선생께라면 이 정도는 이해하시리라 짐작됩니다.\""]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch was, however, so perturbed that he did not immediately comprehend all the good sense of adultery by mutual consent, and his eyes expressed this uncertainty; but the lawyer promptly came to his assistance.", "그러나 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 너무도 혼란스러운 나머지, 쌍방 합의에 의한 간통이라는 것의 온갖 합리성을 단번에 이해하지 못했고, 그의 눈은 그 어리둥절함을 드러냈다. 하지만 변호사가 곧바로 그를 도왔다."]], [["“People cannot go on living together—here you have a fact.", "\"사람들이 더 이상 함께 살 수 없다—여기에 한 가지 사실이 있는 셈입니다."], ["And if both are agreed about it, the details and formalities become a matter of no importance.", "그리고 양쪽이 그 점에 합의한다면, 세부 사항이나 절차 따위는 중요치 않게 됩니다."], ["And at the same time this is the simplest and most certain method.”", "동시에 이것이야말로 가장 단순하고 가장 확실한 방법이지요.\""]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch fully understood now.", "이제 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 충분히 이해했다."], ["But he had religious scruples, which hindered the execution of such a plan.", "그러나 그에게는 그런 계획을 실행하는 데 걸림돌이 되는 종교적 양심의 가책이 있었다."]], [["“That is out of the question in the present case,”", "\"이번 경우에는 그것이 불가능하오.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“Only one alternative is possible: undesigned detection, supported by letters which I have.”", "\"단 하나의 대안만이 가능하오. 내가 가지고 있는 편지들로 뒷받침되는, 의도치 않은 적발 말이오.\""]], [["At the mention of letters the lawyer pursed up his lips, and gave utterance to a thin little compassionate and contemptuous sound.", "편지 이야기가 나오자 변호사는 입술을 오므리며, 가늘고 작은, 측은해하면서도 경멸하는 듯한 소리를 냈다."]], [["“Kindly consider,”", "\"부디 헤아려 주십시오.\" 그가 입을 열었다."], ["he began, “cases of that kind are, as you are aware, under ecclesiastical jurisdiction; the reverend fathers are fond of going into the minutest details in cases of that kind,”", "\"그런 종류의 사건은 아시다시피 교회의 관할에 속합니다. 그리고 신부님들은 그런 종류의 사건이라면 사소하기 그지없는 세부 사항까지 파고들기를 즐기시지요.\""], ["he said with a smile, which betrayed his sympathy with the reverend fathers’ taste.", "그는 신부들의 그런 취향에 공감하는 기색을 내비치며 미소 지었다."], ["“Letters may, of course, be a partial confirmation; but detection in the fact there must be of the most direct kind, that is, by eyewitnesses.", "\"물론 편지가 부분적인 증거는 될 수 있습니다. 하지만 현장 적발이 가장 직접적인 방식으로, 그러니까 목격자에 의해 이루어져야만 합니다."], ["In fact, if you do me the honor to intrust your confidence to me, you will do well to leave me the choice of the measures to be employed.", "사실, 영광스럽게도 제게 일을 맡겨 주신다면, 어떤 방법을 쓸지는 제 선택에 맡겨 두시는 편이 좋습니다."], ["If one wants the result, one must admit the means.”", "결과를 원하신다면, 수단도 받아들이셔야 하니까요.\""]], [["“If it is so....”", "\"만일 그렇다면……\""], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch began, suddenly turning white; but at that moment the lawyer rose and again went to the door to speak to the intruding clerk.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 별안간 새파랗게 질리며 입을 열었다. 그러나 바로 그 순간 변호사가 일어서더니, 또다시 끼어든 서기에게 말을 하러 문 쪽으로 갔다."]], [["“Tell her we don’t haggle over fees!”", "\"수임료를 흥정하지 않는다고 그 부인께 전하게!\""], ["he said, and returned to Alexey Alexandrovitch.", "그가 말하고는 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에게 돌아왔다."]], [["On his way back he caught unobserved another moth.", "돌아오는 길에 그는 들키지 않게 나방 한 마리를 또 잡았다."], ["“Nice state my rep curtains will be in by the summer!”", "'여름쯤이면 내 렙 커튼이 꼴이 말이 아니겠군!'"], ["he thought, frowning.", "그는 인상을 찌푸리며 생각했다."]], [["“And so you were saying?...” he said.", "* 렙: 골이 진 직물."]], [["“I will communicate my decision to you by letter,”", "\"그래, 무슨 말씀을 하시던 참이었지요……?\" 그가 말했다."], ["said Alexey Alexandrovitch, getting up, and he clutched at the table.", "\"내 결정은 편지로 알려 드리겠소.\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 일어서며 말하고는 책상을 움켜잡았다."], ["After standing a moment in silence, he said: “From your words I may consequently conclude that a divorce may be obtained?", "잠시 말없이 서 있다가 그가 말했다. \"그러니까 선생의 말씀으로 미루어, 이혼은 가능하다고 결론지어도 되겠소?"], ["I would ask you to let me know what are your terms.”", "선생의 조건이 어떻게 되는지 알려 주셨으면 하오.\""]], [["“It may be obtained if you give me complete liberty of action,”", "\"제게 행동의 완전한 자유를 주신다면 가능합니다.\""], ["said the lawyer, not answering his question.", "변호사는 그의 질문에는 대답하지 않은 채 말했다."], ["“When can I reckon on receiving information from you?”", "\"선생의 기별은 언제쯤 받을 수 있다고 보면 되겠습니까?\""], ["he asked, moving towards the door, his eyes and his varnished boots shining.", "그가 눈과 반들거리는 부츠를 빛내며 문 쪽으로 다가가며 물었다."]], [["“In a week’s time.", "\"일주일 안에."], ["Your answer as to whether you will undertake to conduct the case, and on what terms, you will be so good as to communicate to me.”", "이 사건을 맡으실지, 또 어떤 조건으로 하실지에 대한 선생의 답은 부디 내게 알려 주시오.\""]], [["“Very good.”", "\"잘 알겠습니다.\""]], [["The lawyer bowed respectfully, let his client out of the door, and, left alone, gave himself up to his sense of amusement.", "변호사는 정중히 머리를 숙이고 의뢰인을 문밖으로 내보낸 뒤, 혼자 남게 되자 즐거운 기분에 흠뻑 빠져들었다."], ["He felt so mirthful that, contrary to his rules, he made a reduction in his terms to the haggling lady, and gave up catching moths, finally deciding that next winter he must have the furniture covered with velvet, like Sigonin’s.", "어찌나 유쾌했던지, 그는 자기 원칙을 어기면서까지 흥정을 하던 그 부인에게 수임료를 깎아 주었고, 나방 잡기도 그만두었으며, 마침내 다음 겨울에는 시고닌의 집처럼 가구에 벨벳을 씌워야겠다고 마음먹었다."]], [["# Chapter 6", "# Chapter 6"]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch had gained a brilliant victory at the sitting of the Commission of the 17th of August, but in the sequel this victory cut the ground from under his feet.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 8월 17일 위원회 회의에서 눈부신 승리를 거두었지만, 그 결과 이 승리는 도리어 그의 발밑을 허물어 버렸다."], ["The new commission for the inquiry into the condition of the native tribes in all its branches had been formed and despatched to its destination with an unusual speed and energy inspired by Alexey Alexandrovitch.", "모든 부문에 걸쳐 토착 부족들의 상태를 조사하기 위한 새 위원회가 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 불어넣은 보기 드문 신속함과 열의로 구성되어 목적지로 파견되었다."], ["Within three months a report was presented.", "석 달 만에 보고서가 제출되었다."], ["The condition of the native tribes was investigated in its political, administrative, economic, ethnographic, material, and religious aspects.", "토착 부족들의 상태가 정치적, 행정적, 경제적, 인종지학적, 물질적, 종교적 측면에서 조사되었다."], ["To all these questions there were answers admirably stated, and answers admitting no shade of doubt, since they were not a product of human thought, always liable to error, but were all the product of official activity.", "이 모든 질문에는 훌륭하게 진술된 답변들이 갖추어져 있었고, 그 답변들은 한 점의 의심도 허용하지 않았는데, 그것이 늘 오류를 범하기 마련인 인간의 사고에서 나온 산물이 아니라 모두가 관청 활동의 산물이었기 때문이다."], ["The answers were all based on official data furnished by governors and heads of churches, and founded on the reports of district magistrates and ecclesiastical superintendents, founded in their turn on the reports of parochial overseers and parish priests; and so all of these answers were unhesitating and certain.", "답변들은 모두 지사들과 교회 수장들이 제공한 공식 자료에 근거했고, 그것은 다시 군 치안판사와 교회 감독관의 보고에 근거했으며, 그 보고는 또다시 교구 감독관과 교구 사제의 보고에 근거한 것이었다. 그리하여 이 모든 답변은 망설임 없이 단정적이었다."], ["All such questions as, for instance, of the cause of failure of crops, of the adherence of certain tribes to their ancient beliefs, etc.—questions which, but for the convenient intervention of the official machine, are not, and cannot be solved for ages—received full, unhesitating solution.", "예컨대 흉작의 원인이라든가, 어떤 부족들이 자기네 오랜 신앙을 고집하는 이유 같은—관청이라는 기계가 편리하게 개입하지 않는 한 수백 년이 지나도 풀리지 않고 풀릴 수도 없는—온갖 질문들이 충실하고 망설임 없는 해답을 얻었다."], ["And this solution was in favor of Alexey Alexandrovitch’s contention.", "그리고 이 해답은 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 주장에 유리한 것이었다."], ["But Stremov, who had felt stung to the quick at the last sitting, had, on the reception of the commission’s report, resorted to tactics which Alexey Alexandrovitch had not anticipated.", "그러나 지난 회의에서 뼛속까지 찔린 듯한 모욕을 느꼈던 스트레모프는 위원회 보고서를 받자 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 미처 예상하지 못한 책략을 썼다."], ["Stremov, carrying with him several members, went over to Alexey Alexandrovitch’s side, and not contenting himself with warmly defending the measure proposed by Karenin, proposed other more extreme measures in the same direction.", "스트레모프는 몇몇 위원을 거느리고 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치 편으로 넘어왔고, 카레닌이 제안한 조치를 열렬히 옹호하는 데 만족하지 않고 같은 방향으로 한층 극단적인 조치들을 제안했다."], ["These measures, still further exaggerated in opposition to what was Alexey Alexandrovitch’s fundamental idea, were passed by the commission, and then the aim of Stremov’s tactics became apparent.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 근본 취지에 어긋나게 더욱 과장된 이 조치들은 위원회에서 통과되었고, 그제야 스트레모프 책략의 목적이 드러났다."], ["Carried to an extreme, the measures seemed at once to be so absurd that the highest authorities, and public opinion, and intellectual ladies, and the newspapers, all at the same time fell foul of them, expressing their indignation both with the measures and their nominal father, Alexey Alexandrovitch.", "극단으로 치달은 그 조치들은 대번에 너무도 터무니없어 보여서, 최고 당국이며 여론이며 지성을 갖춘 부인들이며 신문들이 일제히 들고일어나 그 조치들과 그것들의 명목상 아버지인 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에게 분노를 쏟아 냈다."], ["Stremov drew back, affecting to have blindly followed Karenin, and to be astounded and distressed at what had been done.", "스트레모프는 마치 자기는 카레닌을 맹목적으로 따랐을 뿐이고, 벌어진 일에 깜짝 놀라 괴로워하기라도 하는 듯이 꾸미며 뒤로 물러났다."], ["This meant the defeat of Alexey Alexandrovitch.", "이것은 곧 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 패배를 뜻했다."], ["But in spite of failing health, in spite of his domestic griefs, he did not give in.", "그러나 그는 쇠약해진 건강에도, 가정의 비탄에도 굴하지 않았다."], ["There was a split in the commission.", "위원회에는 분열이 생겼다."], ["Some members, with Stremov at their head, justified their mistake on the ground that they had put faith in the commission of revision, instituted by Alexey Alexandrovitch, and maintained that the report of the commission was rubbish, and simply so much waste paper.", "스트레모프를 우두머리로 하는 일부 위원들은, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 설립한 조사 위원회를 믿었던 것이라며 자기들의 실수를 변명했고, 그 위원회의 보고서는 쓰레기에 불과하며 그저 한 무더기의 휴지 조각일 뿐이라고 주장했다."], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch, with a following of those who saw the danger of so revolutionary an attitude to official documents, persisted in upholding the statements obtained by the revising commission.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 공식 문서에 대한 그처럼 혁명적인 태도가 안고 있는 위험을 알아챈 추종자들을 거느리고, 조사 위원회가 얻어 낸 진술들을 계속 옹호했다."], ["In consequence of this, in the higher spheres, and even in society, all was chaos, and although everyone was interested, no one could tell whether the native tribes really were becoming impoverished and ruined, or whether they were in a flourishing condition.", "그 결과 상층부에서도, 심지어 사교계에서도 모든 것이 혼돈에 빠졌고, 누구나 관심을 가졌지만 토착 부족들이 정말로 빈곤해지고 몰락해 가는지 아니면 번영하는 상태에 있는지 아무도 알 수 없었다."], ["The position of Alexey Alexandrovitch, owing to this, and partly owing to the contempt lavished on him for his wife’s infidelity, became very precarious.", "이로 인해, 또 한편으로는 아내의 부정 때문에 그에게 퍼부어진 멸시로 인해, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 처지는 매우 위태로워졌다."], ["And in this position he took an important resolution.", "그리고 이런 처지에서 그는 중대한 결심을 했다."], ["To the astonishment of the commission, he announced that he should ask permission to go himself to investigate the question on the spot.", "위원회가 놀란 가운데, 그는 직접 현지로 가서 이 문제를 조사하겠다고 허가를 청하겠노라 선언했다."], ["And having obtained permission, Alexey Alexandrovitch prepared to set off to these remote provinces.", "그리고 허가를 얻어 낸 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 이 머나먼 지방으로 떠날 채비를 했다."]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch’s departure made a great sensation, the more so as just before he started he officially returned the posting-fares allowed him for twelve horses, to drive to his destination.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 출발은 커다란 화제를 불러일으켰는데, 출발 직전 그가 목적지까지 가는 데 쓰라고 책정된 말 열두 필분의 역마 비용을 공식적으로 반납했기 때문에 더욱 그러했다."]], [["“I think it very noble,”", "\"참으로 고결하다고 생각해요.\""], ["Betsy said about this to the Princess Myakaya.", "베치가 이 일을 두고 먀흐카야 공작 부인에게 말했다."], ["“Why take money for posting-horses when everyone knows that there are railways everywhere now?”", "\"이제 어디에나 철도가 있다는 걸 누구나 아는 마당에 무엇하러 역마 비용을 받겠어요?\""]], [["But Princess Myakaya did not agree, and the Princess Tverskaya’s opinion annoyed her indeed.", "그러나 먀흐카야 공작 부인은 동의하지 않았고, 트베르스카야 공작 부인의 의견은 오히려 그녀의 비위를 거슬렀다."]], [["“It’s all very well for you to talk,”", "\"댁이야 그렇게 말하기 좋겠지요.\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["said she, “when you have I don’t know how many millions; but I am very glad when my husband goes on a revising tour in the summer.", "\"몇백만 루블이나 가졌는지 모를 정도니까. 하지만 나는 우리 남편이 여름에 시찰을 나가면 정말 기뻐요."], ["It’s very good for him and pleasant traveling about, and it’s a settled arrangement for me to keep a carriage and coachman on the money.”", "그이한테도 좋고 여기저기 돌아다니는 게 즐거운 일이거든요. 게다가 그 돈으로 마차와 마부를 두기로 우리끼리 정해 둔 거고요.\""]], [["On his way to the remote provinces Alexey Alexandrovitch stopped for three days at Moscow.", "머나먼 지방으로 가는 길에 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 모스크바에 사흘간 머물렀다."]], [["The day after his arrival he was driving back from calling on the governor-general.", "도착한 이튿날 그는 총독을 방문하고 마차로 돌아오는 중이었다."], ["At the crossroads by Gazetoy Place, where there are always crowds of carriages and sledges, Alexey Alexandrovitch suddenly heard his name called out in such a loud and cheerful voice that he could not help looking round.", "언제나 마차와 썰매가 붐비는 가제트니 광장 부근의 사거리에서, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 불현듯 누군가가 자기 이름을 어찌나 크고 명랑한 목소리로 부르는지 돌아보지 않을 수 없었다."], ["At the corner of the pavement, in a short, stylish overcoat and a low-crowned fashionable hat, jauntily askew, with a smile that showed a gleam of white teeth and red lips, stood Stepan Arkadyevitch, radiant, young, and beaming.", "보도 모퉁이에, 짧고 멋스러운 외투에 챙 낮은 유행하는 모자를 한쪽으로 비스듬히 멋들어지게 쓰고서, 하얀 이와 붉은 입술을 반짝 드러내며 미소 짓는 스테판 아르카지치가 환하게 빛을 내는 젊고 발랄한 모습으로 서 있었다."], ["He called him vigorously and urgently, and insisted on his stopping.", "그는 기운차고 다급하게 그를 부르며 멈추라고 졸랐다."], ["He had one arm on the window of a carriage that was stopping at the corner, and out of the window were thrust the heads of a lady in a velvet hat, and two children.", "그는 한 팔을 모퉁이에 멈춰 선 마차의 창에 걸쳐 두고 있었고, 그 창 밖으로 벨벳 모자를 쓴 부인과 두 아이의 머리가 내밀어져 있었다."], ["Stepan Arkadyevitch was smiling and beckoning to his brother-in-law.", "스테판 아르카지치는 미소 지으며 매부에게 손짓을 했다."], ["The lady smiled a kindly smile too, and she too waved her hand to Alexey Alexandrovitch.", "부인도 다정한 미소를 지으며 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치를 향해 손을 흔들었다."], ["It was Dolly with her children.", "아이들을 데리고 나온 돌리였다."]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch did not want to see anyone in Moscow, and least of all his wife’s brother.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 모스크바에서 아무도 만나고 싶지 않았고, 그중에서도 아내의 오빠는 더더욱 만나고 싶지 않았다."], ["He raised his hat and would have driven on, but Stepan Arkadyevitch told his coachman to stop, and ran across the snow to him.", "그는 모자를 들어 보이고 그대로 지나치려 했지만, 스테판 아르카지치는 마부에게 멈추라 이르고 눈 위를 가로질러 그에게 달려왔다."]], [["“Well, what a shame not to have let us know!", "\"이런, 알리지도 않다니 너무하잖나!"], ["Been here long?", "온 지 오래됐나?"], ["I was at Dussots’ yesterday and saw ‘Karenin’ on the visitors’ list, but it never entered my head that it was you,”", "어제 뒤소 호텔에 들렀다가 방문객 명단에서 '카레닌'을 봤는데, 그게 자네일 줄은 꿈에도 몰랐지.\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, sticking his head in at the window of the carriage, “or I should have looked you up.", "스테판 아르카지치가 마차 창 안으로 머리를 들이밀며 말했다. \"알았더라면 찾아갔을 텐데."], ["I am glad to see you!”", "만나서 반갑네!\""], ["he said, knocking one foot against the other to shake the snow off.", "그가 눈을 털어 내려고 한쪽 발을 다른 발에 부딪치며 말했다."], ["“What a shame of you not to let us know!”", "\"알리지도 않다니, 정말 너무하지 않나!\""], ["he repeated.", "그는 거듭 말했다."]], [["“I had no time; I am very busy,”", "\"시간이 없었네. 무척 바빠서.\""], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch responded dryly.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 무뚝뚝하게 대꾸했다."]], [["“Come to my wife, she does so want to see you.”", "\"내 아내한테 가 보게. 자네를 그렇게 보고 싶어 하니.\""]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch unfolded the rug in which his frozen feet were wrapped, and getting out of his carriage made his way over the snow to Darya Alexandrovna.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 얼어붙은 발을 감싸고 있던 무릎덮개를 펼쳐 내고, 마차에서 내려 눈을 밟으며 다리야 알렉산드로브나에게로 갔다."]], [["“Why, Alexey Alexandrovitch, what are you cutting us like this for?”", "\"아니, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치, 어쩌자고 이렇게 우리를 모른 척하시는 거예요?\""], ["said Dolly, smiling.", "돌리가 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["“I was very busy.", "\"무척 바빴습니다."], ["Delighted to see you!”", "만나서 반갑습니다!\""], ["he said in a tone clearly indicating that he was annoyed by it.", "그는 그것이 성가시다는 기색이 역력한 어조로 말했다."], ["“How are you?”", "\"어떻게 지내십니까?\""]], [["“Tell me, how is my darling Anna?”", "\"말씀해 주세요, 우리 사랑스러운 안나는 어떻게 지내나요?\""]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch mumbled something and would have gone on.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 무언가 우물거리고는 그대로 가려 했다."], ["But Stepan Arkadyevitch stopped him.", "그러나 스테판 아르카지치가 그를 붙잡았다."]], [["“I tell you what we’ll do tomorrow.", "\"내일 이렇게 하세."], ["Dolly, ask him to dinner.", "돌리, 식사하자고 청해 봐."], ["We’ll ask Koznishev and Pestsov, so as to entertain him with our Moscow celebrities.”", "코즈니셰프와 페스초프를 불러서 우리 모스크바의 명사들로 대접해 드리자고.\""]], [["“Yes, please, do come,”", "\"네, 부디 오세요.\""], ["said Dolly; “we will expect you at five, or six o’clock, if you like.", "돌리가 말했다. \"다섯 시에, 아니면 원하시면 여섯 시에 기다릴게요."], ["How is my darling Anna?", "우리 사랑스러운 안나는 어떻게 지내나요?"], ["How long....”", "얼마나 오래…….\""]], [["“She is quite well,”", "\"아주 잘 지냅니다.\""], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch mumbled, frowning.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 미간을 찌푸리며 우물거렸다."], ["“Delighted!”", "\"반갑습니다!\""], ["and he moved away towards his carriage.", "그러고는 자기 마차 쪽으로 걸음을 옮겼다."]], [["“You will come?”", "\"오실 거죠?\""], ["Dolly called after him.", "돌리가 그의 뒤에 대고 외쳤다."]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch said something which Dolly could not catch in the noise of the moving carriages.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 무어라 말했지만, 오가는 마차들의 소음에 묻혀 돌리는 알아듣지 못했다."]], [["“I shall come round tomorrow!”", "\"내일 들르겠네!\""], ["Stepan Arkadyevitch shouted to him.", "스테판 아르카지치가 그에게 소리쳤다."]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch got into his carriage, and buried himself in it so as neither to see nor be seen.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 자기 마차에 올라타, 누구도 보지 않고 누구에게도 보이지 않으려는 듯 그 안에 몸을 파묻었다."]], [["“Queer fish!”", "\"별난 친구로군!\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch to his wife, and glancing at his watch, he made a motion of his hand before his face, indicating a caress to his wife and children, and walked jauntily along the pavement.", "스테판 아르카지치가 아내에게 말하고는, 시계를 흘긋 본 뒤 아내와 아이들에게 애정을 표하는 양 얼굴 앞에서 손짓을 해 보이고는 보도를 따라 경쾌하게 걸어갔다."]], [["“Stiva!", "\"스티바!"], ["Stiva!”", "스티바!\""], ["Dolly called, reddening.", "돌리가 얼굴을 붉히며 불렀다."]], [["He turned round.", "그가 돌아보았다."]], [["“I must get coats, you know, for Grisha and Tanya.", "\"그리샤랑 타냐 외투를 사야 해요, 알잖아요."], ["Give me the money.”", "돈을 줘요.\""]], [["“Never mind; you tell them I’ll pay the bill!”", "\"걱정 말아. 청구서는 내가 치를 테니 가게에 그렇게 말해 둬!\""], ["and he vanished, nodding genially to an acquaintance who drove by.", "그러고는 마차로 지나가는 아는 사람에게 상냥하게 고개를 끄덕여 보이며 사라져 버렸다."]], [["# Chapter 7", "# Chapter 7"]], [["The next day was Sunday.", "다음 날은 일요일이었다."], ["Stepan Arkadyevitch went to the Grand Theater to a rehearsal of the ballet, and gave Masha Tchibisova, a pretty dancing-girl whom he had just taken under his protection, the coral necklace he had promised her the evening before, and behind the scenes in the dim daylight of the theater, managed to kiss her pretty little face, radiant over her present.", "스테판 아르카지치는 볼쇼이 극장의 발레 리허설을 보러 갔고, 얼마 전부터 자기가 후견을 맡게 된 어여쁜 무용수 마샤 치비소바에게 전날 저녁 약속했던 산호 목걸이를 건넸으며, 무대 뒤편 극장의 어슴푸레한 한낮의 빛 속에서 선물을 받고 환하게 빛나는 그녀의 어여쁜 작은 얼굴에 입을 맞추는 데 성공했다. 목걸이 선물 말고도 그는 발레가 끝난 뒤 만날 약속을 그녀와 잡고 싶었다."], ["Besides the gift of the necklace he wanted to arrange with her about meeting after the ballet.", "발레 시작 무렵에는 올 수 없다고 설명한 뒤, 그는 마지막 막에 와서 그녀를 데리고 저녁 식사를 하러 가겠다고 약속했다."], ["After explaining that he could not come at the beginning of the ballet, he promised he would come for the last act and take her to supper.", "극장에서 나온 스테판 아르카지치는 오호트니 거리로 마차를 몰아 가서 직접 만찬에 쓸 생선과 아스파라거스를 골랐고, 열두 시에는 뒤소 호텔에 도착했다. 그곳에서 그는 세 사람을 만나야 했는데, 다행히도 모두 같은 호텔에 묵고 있었다."], ["From the theater Stepan Arkadyevitch drove to Ohotny Row, selected himself the fish and asparagus for dinner, and by twelve o’clock was at Dussots’, where he had to see three people, luckily all staying at the same hotel: Levin, who had recently come back from abroad and was staying there; the new head of his department, who had just been promoted to that position, and had come on a tour of revision to Moscow; and his brother-in-law, Karenin, whom he must see, so as to be sure of bringing him to dinner.", "최근 외국에서 돌아와 그곳에 묵고 있는 레빈, 막 그 자리로 승진하여 모스크바로 시찰을 온 그의 부서의 새 책임자, 그리고 처남인 카레닌이었다. 카레닌은 만찬에 꼭 데려오기 위해 반드시 만나 두어야 했다."]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch liked dining, but still better he liked to give a dinner, small, but very choice, both as regards the food and drink and as regards the selection of guests.", "스테판 아르카지치는 만찬을 즐기는 것을 좋아했지만, 그보다 더 좋아한 것은 만찬을 베푸는 일이었다. 규모는 작지만 음식과 술은 물론 손님 선정에 이르기까지 아주 까다롭게 고른 만찬이었다."], ["He particularly liked the program of that day’s dinner.", "그는 그날 만찬의 차림표가 특히 마음에 들었다."], ["There would be fresh perch, asparagus, and la pièce de resistance—first-rate, but quite plain, roast beef, and wines to suit: so much for the eating and drinking.", "싱싱한 농어, 아스파라거스, 그리고 la pièce de resistance—더할 나위 없이 훌륭하면서도 아주 소박한 로스트비프, 거기에 어울리는 포도주들이 나올 것이다."], ["Kitty and Levin would be of the party, and that this might not be obtrusively evident, there would be a girl cousin too, and young Shtcherbatsky, and la pièce de resistance among the guests—Sergey Koznishev and Alexey Alexandrovitch.", "먹고 마실 것은 그쯤으로 됐다. 키티와 레빈도 자리에 함께할 텐데, 그게 너무 빤히 드러나지 않도록 하기 위해 처녀 사촌 한 명과 젊은 셰르바츠키도 부르고, 손님들 가운데 la pièce de resistance에 해당하는 인물로 세르게이 코즈니셰프와 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치를 부를 것이다."], ["Sergey Ivanovitch was a Moscow man, and a philosopher; Alexey Alexandrovitch a Petersburger, and a practical politician.", "세르게이 이바노비치는 모스크바 사람이자 철학자였고, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 페테르부르크 사람이자 실무에 밝은 정치가였다."], ["He was asking, too, the well-known eccentric enthusiast, Pestsov, a liberal, a great talker, a musician, an historian, and the most delightfully youthful person of fifty, who would be a sauce or garnish for Koznishev and Karenin.", "그는 또한 잘 알려진 괴짜 열정가 페스초프도 부를 생각이었다. 자유주의자이자 굉장한 수다쟁이에 음악가요 역사가이며, 쉰 살이면서도 더없이 유쾌하고 젊은 사람인 그는, 코즈니셰프와 카레닌에게 곁들이는 양념이나 고명 노릇을 할 것이다."], ["He would provoke them and set them off.", "그가 두 사람을 자극하여 서로 맞붙게 할 것이다."]], [["The second installment for the forest had been received from the merchant and was not yet exhausted; Dolly had been very amiable and good-humored of late, and the idea of the dinner pleased Stepan Arkadyevitch from every point of view.", "숲을 판 두 번째 대금이 상인에게서 들어왔는데 아직 다 쓰지 않았고, 돌리는 요즘 들어 무척 상냥하고 기분이 좋았으며, 만찬에 대한 생각은 어느 모로 보나 스테판 아르카지치를 흐뭇하게 했다."], ["He was in the most light-hearted mood.", "그는 더할 나위 없이 들뜬 기분이었다."], ["There were two circumstances a little unpleasant, but these two circumstances were drowned in the sea of good-humored gaiety which flooded the soul of Stepan Arkadyevitch.", "조금 불쾌한 정황이 두 가지 있긴 했지만, 그 두 가지 정황은 스테판 아르카지치의 영혼에 넘쳐흐르는 유쾌한 명랑함의 바다에 잠겨 버렸다. 그 두 가지 정황이란 이러했다."], ["These two circumstances were: first, that on meeting Alexey Alexandrovitch the day before in the street he had noticed that he was cold and reserved with him, and putting the expression of Alexey Alexandrovitch’s face and the fact that he had not come to see them or let them know of his arrival with the rumors he had heard about Anna and Vronsky, Stepan Arkadyevitch guessed that something was wrong between the husband and wife.", "첫째는, 전날 거리에서 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치를 만났을 때 그가 자기를 냉랭하고 서먹하게 대하는 것을 알아챘다는 점이었다. 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 표정과, 그가 그들을 찾아오지도 도착을 알려 오지도 않았다는 사실을, 안나와 브론스키에 관해 들은 소문과 견주어 보고서, 스테판 아르카지치는 그 부부 사이에 뭔가 어긋난 일이 있다고 짐작했다."]], [["That was one disagreeable thing.", "그것이 한 가지 거북한 일이었다."], ["The other slightly disagreeable fact was that the new head of his department, like all new heads, had the reputation already of a terrible person, who got up at six o’clock in the morning, worked like a horse, and insisted on his subordinates working in the same way.", "또 한 가지 조금 거북한 사실은, 그의 부서의 새 책임자가 모든 새 책임자들이 그렇듯 벌써부터 무시무시한 인물이라는 평판을 얻고 있다는 점이었다. 그는 아침 여섯 시에 일어나 말처럼 일하고, 부하 직원들에게도 똑같이 일하라고 다그친다는 것이었다."], ["Moreover, this new head had the further reputation of being a bear in his manners, and was, according to all reports, a man of a class in all respects the opposite of that to which his predecessor had belonged, and to which Stepan Arkadyevitch had hitherto belonged himself.", "게다가 이 새 책임자는 태도가 곰처럼 무뚝뚝하다는 평판까지 있었고, 모든 소문에 따르면 그는 전임자가 속해 있던, 그리고 스테판 아르카지치 자신이 지금껏 속해 있던 부류와는 모든 면에서 정반대인 부류의 사람이었다."], ["On the previous day Stepan Arkadyevitch had appeared at the office in a uniform, and the new chief had been very affable and had talked to him as to an acquaintance.", "전날 스테판 아르카지치는 제복 차림으로 관청에 나타났는데, 새 책임자는 무척 상냥하게 굴며 마치 아는 사이처럼 그와 이야기를 나누었다."], ["Consequently Stepan Arkadyevitch deemed it his duty to call upon him in his non-official dress.", "그래서 스테판 아르카지치는 평복 차림으로 그를 찾아가는 것이 도리라고 여겼다."], ["The thought that the new chief might not tender him a warm reception was the other unpleasant thing.", "새 책임자가 자기를 따뜻하게 맞아 주지 않을지도 모른다는 생각, 그것이 또 하나의 불쾌한 일이었다."], ["But Stepan Arkadyevitch instinctively felt that everything would come round all right.", "하지만 스테판 아르카지치는 모든 것이 잘 풀리리라는 것을 본능적으로 느꼈다."], ["“They’re all people, all men, like us poor sinners; why be nasty and quarrelsome?”", "'다들 사람이지, 다들 우리처럼 가엾은 죄인들이야. 무엇 때문에 심술궂게 다투겠어?'"], ["he thought as he went into the hotel.", "그는 호텔로 들어가며 이렇게 생각했다."]], [["“Good-day, Vassily,”", "\"안녕한가, 바실리.\""], ["he said, walking into the corridor with his hat cocked on one side, and addressing a footman he knew; “why, you’ve let your whiskers grow!", "모자를 한쪽으로 비스듬히 쓴 채 복도로 들어서며, 그는 안면이 있는 하인에게 말을 걸었다. \"아니, 구레나룻을 길렀군!"], ["Levin, number seven, eh?", "레빈, 7호실이지, 그렇지?"], ["Take me up, please.", "좀 데려다주게."], ["And find out whether Count Anitchkin” (this was the new head) “is receiving.”", "그리고 아니치킨 백작이\"—이 사람이 새 책임자였다—\"손님을 받고 있는지 알아봐 주게.\""]], [["“Yes, sir,”", "\"예, 나리.\""], ["Vassily responded, smiling.", "바실리가 미소 지으며 대답했다."], ["“You’ve not been to see us for a long while.”", "\"오랫동안 저희를 찾아 주시지 않았습니다.\""]], [["“I was here yesterday, but at the other entrance.", "\"어제 왔었네, 다른 입구로 들어갔지."], ["Is this number seven?”", "여기가 7호실인가?\""]], [["Levin was standing with a peasant from Tver in the middle of the room, measuring a fresh bearskin, when Stepan Arkadyevitch went in.", "스테판 아르카지치가 들어섰을 때, 레빈은 트베리에서 온 농부와 함께 방 한가운데 서서 갓 벗긴 곰 가죽의 치수를 재고 있었다."]], [["“What!", "\"뭐야!"], ["you killed him?”", "자네가 곰을 잡은 건가?\""], ["cried Stepan Arkadyevitch.", "스테판 아르카지치가 소리쳤다."], ["“Well done!", "\"잘했네!"], ["A she-bear?", "암곰이야?"], ["How are you, Arhip!”", "잘 있었나, 아르히프!\""]], [["He shook hands with the peasant and sat down on the edge of a chair, without taking off his coat and hat.", "그는 농부와 악수를 나누고는, 외투와 모자도 벗지 않은 채 의자 끝에 걸터앉았다."]], [["“Come, take off your coat and stay a little,”", "\"이봐, 외투를 벗고 좀 앉아 있게나.\""], ["said Levin, taking his hat.", "레빈이 그의 모자를 받으며 말했다."]], [["“No, I haven’t time; I’ve only looked in for a tiny second,”", "\"아니, 시간이 없어. 그저 잠깐 들렀을 뿐이야.\""], ["answered Stepan Arkadyevitch.", "스테판 아르카지치가 대답했다."], ["He threw open his coat, but afterwards did take it off, and sat on for a whole hour, talking to Levin about hunting and the most intimate subjects.", "그는 외투 앞섶을 풀어 헤쳤다가 이내 아예 벗어 버리고는, 한 시간 내내 앉아 레빈과 사냥이며 더없이 은밀한 화제들에 대해 이야기를 나누었다."]], [["“Come, tell me, please, what you did abroad?", "\"자, 어디 말해 보게, 외국에서 뭘 했나?"], ["Where have you been?”", "어디어디 가 봤어?\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, when the peasant had gone.", "농부가 나가자 스테판 아르카지치가 물었다."]], [["“Oh, I stayed in Germany, in Prussia, in France, and in England—not in the capitals, but in the manufacturing towns, and saw a great deal that was new to me.", "\"아, 독일, 프로이센, 프랑스, 영국에 머물렀지. 수도가 아니라 공업 도시들에 말이야."], ["And I’m glad I went.”", "그래서 내게는 새로운 것들을 아주 많이 봤어. 가길 잘했다 싶네.\""]], [["“Yes, I knew your idea of the solution of the labor question.”", "\"그래, 노동 문제 해결에 대한 자네 생각은 알고 있지.\""]], [["“Not a bit: in Russia there can be no labor question.", "\"천만에. 러시아에는 노동 문제란 게 있을 수 없어."], ["In Russia the question is that of the relation of the working people to the land; though the question exists there too—but there it’s a matter of repairing what’s been ruined, while with us....”", "러시아의 문제는 일하는 사람들과 토지의 관계 문제야. 그곳에도 그런 문제가 있긴 하지만, 거기서는 망가진 것을 고치는 일이고, 우리에게는…….\""]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch listened attentively to Levin.", "스테판 아르카지치는 레빈의 말에 귀를 기울였다."]], [["“Yes, yes!”", "\"그래, 그래!\""], ["he said, “it’s very possible you’re right.", "그가 말했다. \"자네 말이 옳을 수도 있겠지."], ["But I’m glad you’re in good spirits, and are hunting bears, and working, and interested.", "하지만 자네가 활기차고, 곰 사냥도 하고, 일도 하고, 뭔가에 관심을 갖고 있다니 기쁘군."], ["Shtcherbatsky told me another story—he met you—that you were in such a depressed state, talking of nothing but death....”", "셰르바츠키한테서 다른 얘기를 들었는데—그 친구가 자넬 만났다더군—자네가 영 우울한 상태로, 줄곧 죽음 얘기만 하더라고…….\""]], [["“Well, what of it?", "\"그래서 어떻단 말인가?"], ["I’ve not given up thinking of death,”", "난 죽음에 대한 생각을 버린 게 아니야.\""], ["said Levin.", "레빈이 말했다."], ["“It’s true that it’s high time I was dead; and that all this is nonsense.", "\"내가 죽을 때가 됐다는 것도, 이 모든 게 다 부질없다는 것도 사실이지. 진심으로 하는 말이야."], ["It’s the truth I’m telling you.", "난 내 생각과 내 일을 정말로 끔찍이 소중히 여겨."], ["I do value my idea and my work awfully; but in reality only consider this: all this world of ours is nothing but a speck of mildew, which has grown up on a tiny planet.", "하지만 실은 한번 생각해 보게. 우리의 이 온 세상이라는 것도 결국 자그마한 행성 위에 자라난 한 점 곰팡이에 지나지 않아."], ["And for us to suppose we can have something great—ideas, work—it’s all dust and ashes.”", "그런데 우리가 뭔가 위대한 것을—사상이니 일이니 하는 것을—가질 수 있다고 여기는 건, 죄다 먼지요 재나 다름없어.\""]], [["“But all that’s as old as the hills, my boy!”", "\"하지만 그건 다 케케묵은 얘기 아닌가, 이 사람아!\""]], [["“It is old; but do you know, when you grasp this fully, then somehow everything becomes of no consequence.", "\"케케묵은 얘기지. 하지만 알겠나, 이걸 온전히 깨닫고 나면 어쩐지 모든 게 다 아무 의미도 없어져 버려."], ["When you understand that you will die tomorrow, if not today, and nothing will be left, then everything is so unimportant!", "오늘은 아니라도 내일이면 죽고, 아무것도 남지 않으리란 걸 깨닫고 나면, 모든 게 정말 하찮아지지!"], ["And I consider my idea very important, but it turns out really to be as unimportant too, even if it were carried out, as doing for that bear.", "난 내 생각이 무척 중요하다고 여기지만, 실은 그것이 설령 이루어진다 해도 저 곰을 잡는 일만큼이나 하찮은 것으로 드러나거든."], ["So one goes on living, amusing oneself with hunting, with work—anything so as not to think of death!”", "그러니 사람은 살아가는 거야, 사냥으로, 일로 즐기면서—죽음을 생각하지 않으려고 뭐든 하면서 말이야!\""]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch smiled a subtle affectionate smile as he listened to Levin.", "스테판 아르카지치는 레빈의 말을 들으며 은근하고 다정한 미소를 지었다."]], [["“Well, of course!", "\"자, 그것 보게!"], ["Here you’ve come round to my point.", "이제 자네가 내 생각 쪽으로 돌아온 거야."], ["Do you remember you attacked me for seeking enjoyment in life?", "자네가 인생에서 즐거움을 좇는다고 날 몰아세웠던 거 기억나나?"], ["Don’t be so severe, O moralist!”", "그렇게 모질게 굴지 말게, 오, 도덕가 선생!\""]], [["“No; all the same, what’s fine in life is....”", "\"아니, 그래도 인생에서 멋진 건…….\""], ["Levin hesitated—“oh, I don’t know.", "레빈이 머뭇거렸다. \"아, 나도 모르겠어."], ["All I know is that we shall soon be dead.”", "내가 아는 건 우리가 머지않아 죽으리라는 것뿐이야.\""]], [["“Why so soon?”", "\"어째서 그렇게 빨리?\""]], [["“And do you know, there’s less charm in life, when one thinks of death, but there’s more peace.”", "\"그리고 알겠나, 죽음을 생각하면 인생에 매력은 덜해지지만, 그 대신 평온은 더해지는 거야.\""]], [["“On the contrary, the finish is always the best.", "\"천만에, 끝마무리야말로 늘 가장 좋은 거지."], ["But I must be going,”", "하지만 난 이만 가 봐야겠네.\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, getting up for the tenth time.", "스테판 아르카지치가 열 번째로 일어서며 말했다."]], [["“Oh, no, stay a bit!”", "\"아, 안 돼, 좀 더 있게나!\""], ["said Levin, keeping him.", "레빈이 그를 붙잡으며 말했다."], ["“Now, when shall we see each other again?", "\"그래, 우리 언제 다시 보지?"], ["I’m going tomorrow.”", "난 내일 떠나네.\""]], [["“I’m a nice person!", "\"내가 참 좋은 사람이지!"], ["Why, that’s just what I came for!", "아니, 바로 그 일 때문에 온 건데!"], ["You simply must come to dinner with us today.", "자네 오늘 우리 집 만찬에 꼭 와야 하네."], ["Your brother’s coming, and Karenin, my brother-in-law.”", "자네 형도 오고, 내 처남 카레닌도 와.\""]], [["“You don’t mean to say he’s here?”", "\"설마 그가 여기 와 있다는 건 아니겠지?\""], ["said Levin, and he wanted to inquire about Kitty.", "레빈이 말하며 키티에 대해 물어보고 싶어졌다."], ["He had heard at the beginning of the winter that she was at Petersburg with her sister, the wife of the diplomat, and he did not know whether she had come back or not; but he changed his mind and did not ask.", "그는 겨울 초입에 그녀가 외교관의 아내인 언니와 함께 페테르부르크에 가 있다는 말을 들었고, 그녀가 돌아왔는지 어떤지는 알지 못했다. 하지만 그는 마음을 고쳐먹고 묻지 않았다."], ["“Whether she’s coming or not, I don’t care,”", "'그녀가 오든 말든, 내 알 바 아니지.'"], ["he said to himself.", "그는 속으로 중얼거렸다."]], [["“So you’ll come?”", "\"그래서 오는 거지?\""]], [["“Of course.”", "\"물론이야.\""]], [["“At five o’clock, then, and not evening dress.”", "\"그럼 다섯 시일세, 정장은 입지 말고.\""]], [["And Stepan Arkadyevitch got up and went down below to the new head of his department.", "그러고 나서 스테판 아르카지치는 일어나 아래층으로 그의 부서의 새 책임자를 만나러 내려갔다."], ["Instinct had not misled Stepan Arkadyevitch.", "스테판 아르카지치의 본능은 그를 잘못 인도하지 않았다."], ["The terrible new head turned out to be an extremely amenable person, and Stepan Arkadyevitch lunched with him and stayed on, so that it was four o’clock before he got to Alexey Alexandrovitch.", "그 무시무시한 새 책임자는 더없이 싹싹한 사람으로 드러났고, 스테판 아르카지치는 그와 함께 점심을 먹고 더 머무는 바람에, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에게 도착한 것은 네 시가 되어서였다."]], [["# Chapter 8", "# Chapter 8"]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch, on coming back from church service, had spent the whole morning indoors.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 예배에서 돌아온 후 오전 내내 집 안에 머물렀다."], ["He had two pieces of business before him that morning; first, to receive and send on a deputation from the native tribes which was on its way to Petersburg, and now at Moscow; secondly, to write the promised letter to the lawyer.", "그날 아침 그에게는 두 가지 처리할 일이 있었다. 첫째는 페테르부르크로 향하는 길에 지금 모스크바에 와 있는 원주민 부족의 사절단을 맞아들였다가 보내는 일이었고, 둘째는 변호사에게 약속한 편지를 쓰는 일이었다."], ["The deputation, though it had been summoned at Alexey Alexandrovitch’s instigation, was not without its discomforting and even dangerous aspect, and he was glad he had found it in Moscow.", "이 사절단은 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 발의로 소집되긴 했으나 불편한, 심지어 위험한 측면이 없지 않았으므로, 그는 그들을 모스크바에서 만나게 된 것이 다행스러웠다."], ["The members of this deputation had not the slightest conception of their duty and the part they were to play.", "이 사절단의 구성원들은 자기들의 임무며 자기들이 맡아야 할 역할에 대해 조금도 이해하지 못하고 있었다."], ["They naïvely believed that it was their business to lay before the commission their needs and the actual condition of things, and to ask assistance of the government, and utterly failed to grasp that some of their statements and requests supported the contention of the enemy’s side, and so spoiled the whole business.", "그들은 위원회 앞에 자기들의 곤궁과 실제 상황을 늘어놓고 정부의 지원을 요청하는 것이 자기들의 소임이라고 순진하게 믿었으며, 그들의 진술과 요청 가운데 어떤 것들은 적대 측의 주장을 뒷받침하여 결국 일 전체를 그르치게 된다는 것을 전혀 깨닫지 못했다."], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch was busily engaged with them for a long while, drew up a program for them from which they were not to depart, and on dismissing them wrote a letter to Petersburg for the guidance of the deputation.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 오랫동안 그들과 분주하게 씨름하며, 그들이 벗어나서는 안 될 지침을 마련해 주었고, 그들을 돌려보내면서 사절단을 지도하기 위해 페테르부르크로 편지 한 통을 써 보냈다."], ["He had his chief support in this affair in the Countess Lidia Ivanovna.", "이 일에서 그가 가장 크게 의지한 사람은 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인이었다."], ["She was a specialist in the matter of deputations, and no one knew better than she how to manage them, and put them in the way they should go.", "그녀는 사절단 문제의 전문가였으며, 그들을 어떻게 다루고 어떻게 가야 할 길로 이끌어야 하는지를 그녀보다 더 잘 아는 사람은 없었다."], ["Having completed this task, Alexey Alexandrovitch wrote the letter to the lawyer.", "이 과업을 마친 후 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 변호사에게 편지를 썼다."], ["Without the slightest hesitation he gave him permission to act as he might judge best.", "그는 조금도 망설이지 않고 변호사가 최선이라 판단하는 대로 행동해도 좋다는 허락을 내렸다."], ["In the letter he enclosed three of Vronsky’s notes to Anna, which were in the portfolio he had taken away.", "그는 편지 안에, 자기가 가져온 서류첩에 들어 있던 브론스키가 안나에게 보낸 쪽지 세 통을 동봉했다."]], [["Since Alexey Alexandrovitch had left home with the intention of not returning to his family again, and since he had been at the lawyer’s and had spoken, though only to one man, of his intention, since especially he had translated the matter from the world of real life to the world of ink and paper, he had grown more and more used to his own intention, and by now distinctly perceived the feasibility of its execution.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 다시는 가족에게로 돌아가지 않을 작정으로 집을 떠났고, 변호사를 찾아가 비록 한 사람에게뿐이긴 했으나 자기 의도를 말했으며, 특히 그 문제를 실생활의 세계에서 잉크와 종이의 세계로 옮겨 놓았으므로, 그는 자기 의도에 점점 더 익숙해졌고 이제는 그것을 실행하는 일이 충분히 가능하다는 것을 또렷이 느끼게 되었다."]], [["He was sealing the envelope to the lawyer, when he heard the loud tones of Stepan Arkadyevitch’s voice.", "그가 변호사에게 보낼 봉투를 봉하고 있을 때, 스테판 아르카지치의 우렁찬 목소리가 들려왔다."], ["Stepan Arkadyevitch was disputing with Alexey Alexandrovitch’s servant, and insisting on being announced.", "스테판 아르카지치는 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 하인과 다투며 자기가 왔음을 전하라고 우기고 있었다."]], [["“No matter,”", "'상관없지.'"], ["thought Alexey Alexandrovitch, “so much the better.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 생각했다. '오히려 잘됐어."], ["I will inform him at once of my position in regard to his sister, and explain why it is I can’t dine with him.”", "처남에게 그의 누이에 대한 내 입장을 당장 알리고, 왜 내가 그와 함께 식사할 수 없는지 설명해 줘야지.'"]], [["“Come in!”", "\"들어오게!\""], ["he said aloud, collecting his papers, and putting them in the blotting-paper.", "그는 서류를 챙겨 압지 사이에 끼워 넣으며 소리 내어 말했다."]], [["“There, you see, you’re talking nonsense, and he’s at home!”", "\"보게, 자네가 헛소리를 한 거잖아, 댁에 계시지 않은가!\""], ["responded Stepan Arkadyevitch’s voice, addressing the servant, who had refused to let him in, and taking off his coat as he went, Oblonsky walked into the room.", "안으로 들이기를 거부한 하인을 향해 스테판 아르카지치의 목소리가 응수했고, 오블론스키는 들어오면서 외투를 벗으며 방 안으로 걸어 들어왔다."], ["“Well, I’m awfully glad I’ve found you!", "\"이야, 자네를 만나게 되어 정말 기쁘군!"], ["So I hope....”", "그래서 말인데, 부디……\""], ["Stepan Arkadyevitch began cheerfully.", "스테판 아르카지치가 명랑하게 입을 열었다."]], [["“I cannot come,”", "\"갈 수가 없네.\""], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch said coldly, standing and not asking his visitor to sit down.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 선 채로 손님에게 앉으라는 말도 하지 않고 차갑게 말했다."]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch had thought to pass at once into those frigid relations in which he ought to stand with the brother of a wife against whom he was beginning a suit for divorce.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는, 이혼 소송을 막 시작하려는 아내의 오빠와 자기가 마땅히 유지해야 할 그 냉랭한 관계로 곧장 들어서려 했다."], ["But he had not taken into account the ocean of kindliness brimming over in the heart of Stepan Arkadyevitch.", "그러나 그는 스테판 아르카지치의 가슴속에 넘쳐흐르는 너그러움의 바다를 미처 계산에 넣지 못했다."]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch opened wide his clear, shining eyes.", "스테판 아르카지치는 맑고 빛나는 두 눈을 크게 떴다."]], [["“Why can’t you?", "\"왜 못 온다는 건가?"], ["What do you mean?”", "무슨 말이야?\""], ["he asked in perplexity, speaking in French.", "그는 어리둥절하여, 프랑스어로 물었다."], ["“Oh, but it’s a promise.", "\"아니, 약속이잖아."], ["And we’re all counting on you.”", "우리 모두 자네만 기다리고 있는데.\""]], [["“I want to tell you that I can’t dine at your house, because the terms of relationship which have existed between us must cease.”", "\"자네 집에서 식사할 수 없다는 걸 말해 두려 하네. 우리 사이에 존재해 온 친족 관계가 끊어져야 하기 때문이지.\""]], [["“How?", "\"어떻게?"], ["How do you mean?", "무슨 뜻이야?"], ["What for?”", "무엇 때문에?\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch with a smile.", "스테판 아르카지치가 미소를 지으며 말했다."]], [["“Because I am beginning an action for divorce against your sister, my wife.", "\"내가 자네 누이, 그러니까 내 아내를 상대로 이혼 소송을 시작하려 하기 때문이네."], ["I ought to have....”", "진작에……\""]], [["But, before Alexey Alexandrovitch had time to finish his sentence, Stepan Arkadyevitch was behaving not at all as he had expected.", "그러나 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 말을 채 끝내기도 전에, 스테판 아르카지치는 그가 예상했던 것과는 전혀 다르게 행동했다."], ["He groaned and sank into an armchair.", "그는 신음을 내뱉으며 안락의자에 털썩 주저앉았다."]], [["“No, Alexey Alexandrovitch!", "\"아니, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치!"], ["What are you saying?”", "무슨 말을 하는 건가?\""], ["cried Oblonsky, and his suffering was apparent in his face.", "오블론스키가 외쳤고, 그의 괴로움이 얼굴에 역력히 드러났다."]], [["“It is so.”", "\"사실이네.\""]], [["“Excuse me, I can’t, I can’t believe it!”", "\"미안하지만, 난 믿을 수가 없어, 믿을 수가 없네!\""]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch sat down, feeling that his words had not had the effect he anticipated, and that it would be unavoidable for him to explain his position, and that, whatever explanations he might make, his relations with his brother-in-law would remain unchanged.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는, 자기 말이 예상했던 효과를 내지 못했다는 것을, 그리고 자기 입장을 설명하는 일이 불가피하리라는 것을, 또한 어떤 설명을 한다 해도 처남과의 관계는 변함없이 그대로 남으리라는 것을 느끼며 자리에 앉았다."]], [["“Yes, I am brought to the painful necessity of seeking a divorce,”", "\"그래, 나는 이혼을 청해야 하는 괴로운 처지에 몰리고 말았네.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“I will say one thing, Alexey Alexandrovitch.", "\"한 가지만 말하겠네, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치."], ["I know you for an excellent, upright man; I know Anna—excuse me, I can’t change my opinion of her—for a good, an excellent woman; and so, excuse me, I cannot believe it.", "나는 자네가 더없이 훌륭하고 정직한 사람임을 알고 있네. 또 안나를 — 미안하지만 그녀에 대한 내 생각은 바꿀 수 없어 — 착하고 더없이 훌륭한 여자로 알고 있어."], ["There is some misunderstanding,”", "그러니, 미안하지만 나는 믿을 수가 없네. 뭔가 오해가 있는 거야.\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“Oh, if it were merely a misunderstanding!...”", "\"오, 그것이 한낱 오해이기만 하다면!……\""]], [["“Pardon, I understand,”", "\"잠깐, 알겠네.\""], ["interposed Stepan Arkadyevitch.", "스테판 아르카지치가 끼어들었다."], ["“But of course....", "\"하지만 물론이지……"], ["One thing: you must not act in haste.", "한 가지, 자네 서둘러서는 안 되네."], ["You must not, you must not act in haste!”", "그러면 안 돼, 절대 서둘러서는 안 돼!\""]], [["“I am not acting in haste,”", "\"나는 서두르는 게 아니네.\""], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch said coldly, “but one cannot ask advice of anyone in such a matter.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 차갑게 말했다. \"하지만 이런 일에서는 누구에게도 조언을 구할 수가 없는 법이지."], ["I have quite made up my mind.”", "나는 이미 결심을 굳혔네.\""]], [["“This is awful!”", "\"이건 끔찍한 일이야!\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch.", "스테판 아르카지치가 말했다."], ["“I would do one thing, Alexey Alexandrovitch.", "\"한 가지만 하겠네, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치."], ["I beseech you, do it!”", "부디 그렇게 해 주게!\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“No action has yet been taken, if I understand rightly.", "\"내가 제대로 이해했다면, 아직 아무런 조처도 취해지지 않았어."], ["Before you take advice, see my wife, talk to her.", "조언을 구하기 전에, 내 아내를 만나 그녀와 이야기를 나눠 보게."], ["She loves Anna like a sister, she loves you, and she’s a wonderful woman.", "그녀는 안나를 친자매처럼 사랑하고, 자네도 사랑하며, 또 놀라운 여자라네."], ["For God’s sake, talk to her!", "제발, 그녀와 이야기해 주게!"], ["Do me that favor, I beseech you!”", "부디 그 부탁을 들어주게, 간청하네!\""]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch pondered, and Stepan Arkadyevitch looked at him sympathetically, without interrupting his silence.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 곰곰이 생각에 잠겼고, 스테판 아르카지치는 그의 침묵을 방해하지 않으며 동정 어린 눈으로 그를 바라보았다."]], [["“You will go to see her?”", "\"그녀를 만나러 가 주겠나?\""]], [["“I don’t know.", "\"모르겠네."], ["That was just why I have not been to see you.", "바로 그 때문에 자네를 찾아가지 않았던 거야."], ["I imagine our relations must change.”", "우리 관계도 달라질 수밖에 없으리라 생각하네.\""]], [["“Why so?", "\"왜 그렇지?"], ["I don’t see that.", "나는 그렇게 보지 않네."], ["Allow me to believe that apart from our connection you have for me, at least in part, the same friendly feeling I have always had for you ...", "우리의 인척 관계와는 별개로, 자네가 적어도 얼마간은 내가 늘 자네에게 품어 온 것과 똑같은 우정을…… 그리고 진심 어린 존경을 나에게 품고 있다고 믿게 해 주게.\""], ["and sincere esteem,” said Stepan Arkadyevitch, pressing his hand.", "스테판 아르카지치는 그의 손을 잡으며 말했다."], ["“Even if your worst suppositions were correct, I don’t—and never would—take on myself to judge either side, and I see no reason why our relations should be affected.", "\"설령 자네의 최악의 추측이 옳다 하더라도, 나는 어느 한쪽을 판단하는 일을 떠맡지 않을 거고, 또 결코 그러지 않을 거야. 그러니 우리 관계가 영향을 받아야 할 이유를 나는 모르겠네."], ["But now, do this, come and see my wife.”", "자, 그러니 이렇게 해 주게, 와서 내 아내를 만나 주게.\""]], [["“Well, we look at the matter differently,”", "\"글쎄, 우리는 이 문제를 다르게 보고 있군.\""], ["said Alexey Alexandrovitch coldly.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 차갑게 말했다."], ["“However, we won’t discuss it.”", "\"하지만, 그 이야기는 그만하세.\""]], [["“No; why shouldn’t you come today to dine, anyway?", "\"아니, 어쨌든 오늘 식사하러 오지 못할 까닭이 뭔가?"], ["My wife’s expecting you.", "내 아내가 자네를 기다리고 있네."], ["Please, do come.", "부디, 와 주게."], ["And, above all, talk it over with her.", "그리고 무엇보다, 그녀와 그 일을 의논해 보게."], ["She’s a wonderful woman.", "그녀는 놀라운 여자야."], ["For God’s sake, on my knees, I implore you!”", "제발, 무릎이라도 꿇고 자네에게 간청하네!\""]], [["“If you so much wish it, I will come,”", "\"자네가 그토록 원한다면, 가겠네.\""], ["said Alexey Alexandrovitch, sighing.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 한숨을 쉬며 말했다."]], [["And, anxious to change the conversation, he inquired about what interested them both—the new head of Stepan Arkadyevitch’s department, a man not yet old, who had suddenly been promoted to so high a position.", "그러고는 화제를 돌리고 싶어, 그는 두 사람 모두에게 흥미로운 일에 대해 물었다. 그것은 스테판 아르카지치가 속한 부서의 새 수장에 관한 것으로, 아직 늙지 않은 한 사내가 별안간 그토록 높은 자리로 승진한 일이었다."]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch had previously felt no liking for Count Anitchkin, and had always differed from him in his opinions.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 전부터 아니치킨 백작에게 호감을 느끼지 못했고, 늘 그와 의견을 달리해 왔다."], ["But now, from a feeling readily comprehensible to officials—that hatred felt by one who has suffered a defeat in the service for one who has received a promotion, he could not endure him.", "그러나 이제는, 관료들이라면 쉽게 이해할 만한 감정에서 — 즉 관직에서 패배를 겪은 자가 승진한 자에게 느끼는 그 증오심에서 — 그를 견딜 수가 없었다."]], [["“Well, have you seen him?”", "\"그래, 그를 만나 봤나?\""], ["said Alexey Alexandrovitch with a malignant smile.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 악의 어린 미소를 지으며 말했다."]], [["“Of course; he was at our sitting yesterday.", "\"물론이지. 어제 우리 회의에 나왔더군."], ["He seems to know his work capitally, and to be very energetic.”", "일을 아주 훌륭하게 파악하고 있는 것 같고, 무척 정력적이야.\""]], [["“Yes, but what is his energy directed to?”", "\"그래, 한데 그 정력이 어디로 향하고 있나?\""], ["said Alexey Alexandrovitch.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 말했다."], ["“Is he aiming at doing anything, or simply undoing what’s been done?", "\"그는 무언가 이루어 내려는 건가, 아니면 그저 이미 해 놓은 것을 허물려는 건가?"], ["It’s the great misfortune of our government—this paper administration, of which he’s a worthy representative.”", "그 종이뭉치 행정이야말로 우리 정부의 크나큰 불행인데, 그가 바로 그것의 가장 합당한 대표자란 말이지.\""]], [["“Really, I don’t know what fault one could find with him.", "\"정말이지, 그에게서 무슨 흠을 잡을 수 있을지 난 모르겠네."], ["His policy I don’t know, but one thing—he’s a very nice fellow,”", "그의 정책이야 나도 모르지만, 한 가지는 분명하지 — 그는 아주 좋은 친구야.\""], ["answered Stepan Arkadyevitch.", "스테판 아르카지치가 대답했다."], ["“I’ve just been seeing him, and he’s really a capital fellow.", "\"방금 그를 만나고 오는 길인데, 정말 훌륭한 친구더군."], ["We lunched together, and I taught him how to make, you know that drink, wine and oranges.", "함께 점심을 들었고, 내가 그에게 그 음료, 자네도 알지, 포도주에 오렌지를 넣어 만드는 거 말이야, 그걸 만드는 법을 가르쳐 줬지."], ["It’s so cooling.", "어찌나 시원한지."], ["And it’s a wonder he didn’t know it.", "그런데 그가 그걸 모르고 있었다니 놀랍더군."], ["He liked it awfully.", "무척 마음에 들어 했어."], ["No, really he’s a capital fellow.”", "아니, 정말이지 그는 훌륭한 친구야.\""]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch glanced at his watch.", "스테판 아르카지치가 시계를 흘끗 보았다."]], [["“Why, good heavens, it’s four already, and I’ve still to go to Dolgovushin’s!", "\"이런, 맙소사, 벌써 네 시잖아, 게다가 돌고부신네에도 가야 하는데!"], ["So please come round to dinner.", "그러니 부디 식사하러 들러 주게."], ["You can’t imagine how you will grieve my wife and me.”", "자네가 나와 내 아내를 얼마나 슬프게 할지 자네는 상상도 못 할 거야.\""]], [["The way in which Alexey Alexandrovitch saw his brother-in-law out was very different from the manner in which he had met him.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 처남을 배웅하는 모습은, 그를 맞이했던 태도와는 사뭇 달랐다."]], [["“I’ve promised, and I’ll come,”", "\"약속했으니, 가겠네.\""], ["he answered wearily.", "그가 지친 듯이 대답했다."]], [["“Believe me, I appreciate it, and I hope you won’t regret it,”", "\"정말 고맙네, 자네가 후회하지 않으리라 믿어.\""], ["answered Stepan Arkadyevitch, smiling.", "스테판 아르카지치가 미소를 지으며 대답했다."]], [["And, putting on his coat as he went, he patted the footman on the head, chuckled, and went out.", "그러고는 걸어가면서 외투를 걸치고, 하인의 머리를 토닥이며 빙긋 웃고는 밖으로 나갔다."]], [["“At five o’clock, and not evening dress, please,”", "\"다섯 시야, 그리고 정장은 입지 말고 와 주게.\""], ["he shouted once more, turning at the door.", "그는 문간에서 돌아서며 다시 한번 소리쳤다."]], [["# Chapter 9", "# Chapter 9"]], [["It was past five, and several guests had already arrived, before the host himself got home.", "다섯 시가 지나 손님 몇몇이 이미 도착했을 무렵에야 주인이 집에 돌아왔다."], ["He went in together with Sergey Ivanovitch Koznishev and Pestsov, who had reached the street door at the same moment.", "그는 세르게이 이바노비치 코즈니셰프와 페스초프와 함께 들어왔는데, 그 두 사람은 같은 순간에 현관 앞에 다다른 참이었다."], ["These were the two leading representatives of the Moscow intellectuals, as Oblonsky had called them.", "오블론스키가 부르는 표현대로 하자면, 이들은 모스크바 지식인을 대표하는 두 거물이었다."], ["Both were men respected for their character and their intelligence.", "둘 다 인품과 지성으로 존경받는 사람들이었다."], ["They respected each other, but were in complete and hopeless disagreement upon almost every subject, not because they belonged to opposite parties, but precisely because they were of the same party (their enemies refused to see any distinction between their views); but, in that party, each had his own special shade of opinion.", "그들은 서로를 존중했지만, 거의 모든 주제에서 완전하고 절망적으로 의견이 엇갈렸는데, 그것은 그들이 서로 반대 진영에 속해서가 아니라 바로 같은 진영에 속해 있었기 때문이었다(그들의 적들은 두 사람의 견해에서 어떤 차이도 인정하려 들지 않았다). 다만 그 같은 진영 안에서도 저마다 자기만의 독특한 색채를 띤 의견을 지니고 있었다."], ["And since no difference is less easily overcome than the difference of opinion about semi-abstract questions, they never agreed in any opinion, and had long, indeed, been accustomed to jeer without anger, each at the other’s incorrigible aberrations.", "그리고 반쯤 추상적인 문제에 관한 의견 차이만큼 좀처럼 극복하기 어려운 차이는 없는 법이라, 두 사람은 어떤 의견에서도 결코 일치하지 못했고, 실은 오래전부터 서로 상대방의 고질적인 빗나감을 화내지 않고 비웃는 데 익숙해져 있었다."]], [["They were just going in at the door, talking of the weather, when Stepan Arkadyevitch overtook them.", "날씨 이야기를 하며 막 문으로 들어서려던 참에 스테판 아르카지치가 그들을 따라잡았다."], ["In the drawing-room there were already sitting Prince Alexander Dmitrievitch Shtcherbatsky, young Shtcherbatsky, Turovtsin, Kitty, and Karenin.", "응접실에는 이미 알렉산드르 드미트리예비치 셰르바츠키 공작, 젊은 셰르바츠키, 투롭친, 키티, 그리고 카레닌이 앉아 있었다."]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch saw immediately that things were not going well in the drawing-room without him.", "스테판 아르카지치는 자기가 없는 사이 응접실의 분위기가 신통치 않게 흘러가고 있음을 단번에 알아챘다."], ["Darya Alexandrovna, in her best gray silk gown, obviously worried about the children, who were to have their dinner by themselves in the nursery, and by her husband’s absence, was not equal to the task of making the party mix without him.", "가장 좋은 회색 비단 드레스를 입은 다리아 알렉산드로브나는 보육실에서 따로 저녁을 먹기로 된 아이들 걱정에, 그리고 남편의 부재에 마음을 쓰느라, 남편 없이 손님들을 어우러지게 만드는 일을 감당하지 못하고 있었다."], ["All were sitting like so many priests’ wives on a visit (so the old prince expressed it), obviously wondering why they were there, and pumping up remarks simply to avoid being silent.", "모두가 마치 방문 온 사제의 아내들처럼(노공작의 표현이었다) 앉아서, 자기들이 왜 여기 와 있는지 못내 의아해하며, 그저 침묵을 면하려고 억지로 말을 짜내고 있었다."], ["Turovtsin—good, simple man—felt unmistakably a fish out of water, and the smile with which his thick lips greeted Stepan Arkadyevitch said, as plainly as words: “Well, old boy, you have popped me down in a learned set!", "선량하고 소박한 사람인 투롭친은 영락없이 물 밖에 나온 물고기 신세였고, 그의 두툼한 입술이 스테판 아르카지치를 맞이하며 짓는 미소는 마치 말처럼 또렷이 이렇게 말하고 있었다. \"이봐, 친구, 자네가 나를 학자들 틈에 처박아 놓았군!"], ["A drinking party now, or the Château des Fleurs, would be more in my line!”", "지금 같아선 술자리나 Château des Fleurs가 내 취향에 더 맞을 텐데 말이야!\""], ["The old prince sat in silence, his bright little eyes watching Karenin from one side, and Stepan Arkadyevitch saw that he had already formed a phrase to sum up that politician of whom guests were invited to partake as though he were a sturgeon.", "노공작은 잠자코 앉아 작고 반짝이는 눈으로 한쪽에서 카레닌을 지켜보고 있었고, 스테판 아르카지치는 노인이 이미, 마치 철갑상어라도 되는 양 손님들이 맛보도록 초대된 그 정치가를 한마디로 요약할 문구를 머릿속에 만들어 두었음을 알아챘다."], ["Kitty was looking at the door, calling up all her energies to keep her from blushing at the entrance of Konstantin Levin.", "키티는 문 쪽을 바라보며, 콘스탄틴 레빈이 들어설 때 얼굴이 붉어지지 않도록 온 힘을 그러모으고 있었다."], ["Young Shtcherbatsky, who had not been introduced to Karenin, was trying to look as though he were not in the least conscious of it.", "카레닌에게 소개받지 못한 젊은 셰르바츠키는 그 사실을 조금도 의식하지 않는 척하려 애쓰고 있었다."], ["Karenin himself had followed the Petersburg fashion for a dinner with ladies and was wearing evening dress and a white tie.", "카레닌 본인은 부인들과 함께하는 만찬에 어울리는 페테르부르크식 격식을 따라 야회복에 흰 넥타이 차림이었다."], ["Stepan Arkadyevitch saw by his face that he had come simply to keep his promise, and was performing a disagreeable duty in being present at this gathering.", "스테판 아르카지치는 그의 얼굴을 보고, 그가 그저 약속을 지키려고 왔을 뿐이며 이 모임에 참석하는 것을 달갑잖은 의무로 여기고 있음을 알아챘다."], ["He was indeed the person chiefly responsible for the chill benumbing all the guests before Stepan Arkadyevitch came in.", "실은 스테판 아르카지치가 들어오기 전까지 모든 손님을 얼어붙게 만든 그 한기의 주된 책임자가 바로 그였다."]], [["On entering the drawing-room Stepan Arkadyevitch apologized, explaining that he had been detained by that prince, who was always the scapegoat for all his absences and unpunctualities, and in one moment he had made all the guests acquainted with each other, and, bringing together Alexey Alexandrovitch and Sergey Koznishev, started them on a discussion of the Russification of Poland, into which they immediately plunged with Pestsov.", "응접실에 들어선 스테판 아르카지치는, 자기가 자리를 비우거나 시간에 늦을 때마다 늘 희생양이 되곤 하는 바로 그 공작에게 붙들려 있었다고 변명하며 사과하더니, 순식간에 모든 손님을 서로 인사시켰고, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치와 세르게이 코즈니셰프를 한자리에 모아 폴란드의 러시아화를 주제로 토론을 붙여 놓았는데, 두 사람은 페스초프까지 끌어들여 곧장 거기에 몰두했다."], ["Slapping Turovtsin on the shoulder, he whispered something comic in his ear, and set him down by his wife and the old prince.", "그는 투롭친의 어깨를 툭 치고 귀에다 뭔가 우스운 말을 속삭이고는 아내와 노공작 곁에 앉혀 놓았다."], ["Then he told Kitty she was looking very pretty that evening, and presented Shtcherbatsky to Karenin.", "그러고는 키티에게 오늘 저녁 무척 예뻐 보인다고 말하고, 셰르바츠키를 카레닌에게 소개했다."], ["In a moment he had so kneaded together the social dough that the drawing-room became very lively, and there was a merry buzz of voices.", "그는 순식간에 사교의 반죽을 잘도 주물러 놓아, 응접실은 무척 활기를 띠게 되었고 흥겨운 목소리들이 웅성거렸다."], ["Konstantin Levin was the only person who had not arrived.", "도착하지 않은 사람은 콘스탄틴 레빈뿐이었다."], ["But this was so much the better, as going into the dining-room, Stepan Arkadyevitch found to his horror that the port and sherry had been procured from Depré, and not from Levy, and, directing that the coachman should be sent off as speedily as possible to Levy’s, he was going back to the drawing-room.", "그러나 차라리 그편이 나았는데, 식당으로 들어간 스테판 아르카지치가 포트와인과 셰리주를 레비가 아니라 데프레에서 들여왔다는 것을 알고 질겁한 까닭이었다. 그는 마부를 되도록 빨리 레비의 가게로 보내라고 지시한 뒤 응접실로 돌아가려던 참이었다."]], [["In the dining-room he was met by Konstantin Levin.", "식당에서 그는 콘스탄틴 레빈과 마주쳤다."]], [["“I’m not late?”", "\"나 늦지 않았나?\""]], [["“You can never help being late!”", "\"자네가 늦지 않을 수가 있나!\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, taking his arm.", "스테판 아르카지치가 그의 팔을 붙들며 말했다."]], [["“Have you a lot of people?", "\"사람들 많이 왔나?"], ["Who’s here?”", "누가 와 있지?\""], ["asked Levin, unable to help blushing, as he knocked the snow off his cap with his glove.", "레빈이 장갑으로 모자에 묻은 눈을 털어내며, 어쩔 수 없이 얼굴을 붉히면서 물었다."]], [["“All our own set.", "\"다 우리 식구들이지."], ["Kitty’s here.", "키티도 와 있어."], ["Come along, I’ll introduce you to Karenin.”", "자, 가세, 카레닌에게 소개해 줄 테니.\""]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch, for all his liberal views, was well aware that to meet Karenin was sure to be felt a flattering distinction, and so treated his best friends to this honor.", "스테판 아르카지치는 자유주의적인 견해를 지녔으면서도, 카레닌과 만나는 것이 분명 영광스러운 대접으로 여겨지리라는 것을 잘 알고 있었기에, 가장 가까운 친구들에게 이 영예를 베풀곤 했다."], ["But at that instant Konstantin Levin was not in a condition to feel all the gratification of making such an acquaintance.", "그러나 그 순간 콘스탄틴 레빈은 그런 사람과 알게 되는 기쁨을 온전히 느낄 만한 상태가 아니었다."], ["He had not seen Kitty since that memorable evening when he met Vronsky, not counting, that is, the moment when he had had a glimpse of her on the highroad.", "그는 브론스키를 만났던 그 잊을 수 없는 저녁 이후로 키티를 본 적이 없었다. 큰길에서 그녀를 언뜻 본 그 순간을 빼면 말이다."], ["He had known at the bottom of his heart that he would see her here today.", "그는 마음 깊은 곳에서 오늘 여기서 그녀를 만나게 되리라는 것을 알고 있었다."], ["But to keep his thoughts free, he had tried to persuade himself that he did not know it.", "그러나 마음을 자유롭게 두려고, 자기는 그것을 모른다고 스스로를 설득하려 애썼다."], ["Now when he heard that she was here, he was suddenly conscious of such delight, and at the same time of such dread, that his breath failed him and he could not utter what he wanted to say.", "이제 그녀가 여기 있다는 말을 듣자, 그는 별안간 너무도 큰 기쁨과 동시에 너무도 큰 두려움을 느껴 숨이 멎고 하려던 말을 입 밖에 낼 수 없었다."]], [["“What is she like, what is she like?", "'그녀는 어떤 모습일까, 어떤 모습일까?"], ["Like what she used to be, or like what she was in the carriage?", "예전 그대로일까, 아니면 마차 안에 있던 그 모습일까?"], ["What if Darya Alexandrovna told the truth?", "다리아 알렉산드로브나가 한 말이 정말이라면?"], ["Why shouldn’t it be the truth?”", "정말이지 못할 게 뭐람?'"], ["he thought.", "그는 생각했다."]], [["“Oh, please, introduce me to Karenin,”", "\"아, 부탁이네, 카레닌에게 소개해 주게.\""], ["he brought out with an effort, and with a desperately determined step he walked into the drawing-room and beheld her.", "그는 힘겹게 말을 꺼내고는, 필사적으로 마음을 다잡은 걸음걸이로 응접실로 들어가 그녀를 보았다."]], [["She was not the same as she used to be, nor was she as she had been in the carriage; she was quite different.", "그녀는 예전 그대로의 모습도 아니었고, 마차 안에서 보았던 그 모습도 아니었다. 전혀 다른 모습이었다."]], [["She was scared, shy, shame-faced, and still more charming from it.", "그녀는 겁먹고, 수줍어하고, 부끄러워했으며, 그래서 더욱 매력적이었다."], ["She saw him the very instant he walked into the room.", "그녀는 그가 방 안에 들어선 바로 그 순간 그를 보았다."], ["She had been expecting him.", "그녀는 그를 기다리고 있었던 것이다."], ["She was delighted, and so confused at her own delight that there was a moment, the moment when he went up to her sister and glanced again at her, when she, and he, and Dolly, who saw it all, thought she would break down and would begin to cry.", "그녀는 기뻤고, 자기 자신의 기쁨에 너무도 당황한 나머지, 한순간—그가 언니에게 다가가 다시 그녀를 흘긋 바라본 그 순간—그녀도, 그도, 그리고 그 모든 것을 지켜본 돌리도, 그녀가 무너져 울음을 터뜨릴 것이라 생각했다."], ["She crimsoned, turned white, crimsoned again, and grew faint, waiting with quivering lips for him to come to her.", "그녀는 새빨개졌다가, 새하얘졌다가, 다시 새빨개졌고, 떨리는 입술로 그가 다가오기를 기다리며 아득해졌다."], ["He went up to her, bowed, and held out his hand without speaking.", "그가 다가가 고개를 숙이고 말없이 손을 내밀었다."], ["Except for the slight quiver of her lips and the moisture in her eyes that made them brighter, her smile was almost calm as she said:", "살짝 떨리는 입술과 눈을 더 빛나게 하는 물기를 빼면, 그녀의 미소는 거의 차분했고, 그녀는 이렇게 말했다."]], [["“How long it is since we’ve seen each other!”", "\"우리가 못 본 지 정말 오래됐네요!\""], ["and with desperate determination she pressed his hand with her cold hand.", "그러고는 필사적으로 마음을 다잡으며 차가운 손으로 그의 손을 꼭 잡았다."]], [["“You’ve not seen me, but I’ve seen you,”", "\"당신은 못 보셨겠지만, 저는 봤어요.\""], ["said Levin, with a radiant smile of happiness.", "레빈이 행복으로 환하게 빛나는 미소를 지으며 말했다."], ["“I saw you when you were driving from the railway station to Ergushovo.”", "\"당신이 기차역에서 예르구쇼보로 가는 길에 마차를 타고 가시는 걸 봤습니다.\""]], [["“When?”", "\"언제요?\""], ["she asked, wondering.", "그녀가 의아해하며 물었다."]], [["“You were driving to Ergushovo,”", "\"예르구쇼보로 가시는 길이었어요.\""], ["said Levin, feeling as if he would sob with the rapture that was flooding his heart.", "레빈은 가슴을 가득 채우는 환희에 흐느낌이 터질 것만 같은 기분으로 말했다."], ["“And how dared I associate a thought of anything not innocent with this touching creature?", "'그런데 내가 어찌 감히 이 가슴 뭉클한 사람에게 순결하지 못한 생각을 결부시킬 수 있었단 말인가?"], ["And, yes, I do believe it’s true what Darya Alexandrovna told me,”", "그래, 그래, 나는 다리아 알렉산드로브나가 내게 한 말이 정말이라고 믿어.'"], ["he thought.", "그는 생각했다."]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch took him by the arm and led him away to Karenin.", "스테판 아르카지치가 그의 팔을 붙들고 카레닌에게로 데려갔다."]], [["“Let me introduce you.”", "\"소개하지.\""], ["He mentioned their names.", "그는 두 사람의 이름을 말했다."]], [["“Very glad to meet you again,”", "\"다시 만나게 되어 무척 반갑습니다.\""], ["said Alexey Alexandrovitch coldly, shaking hands with Levin.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 레빈과 악수하며 차갑게 말했다."]], [["“You are acquainted?”", "\"두 분이 아는 사이인가?\""], ["Stepan Arkadyevitch asked in surprise.", "스테판 아르카지치가 놀라 물었다."]], [["“We spent three hours together in the train,”", "\"기차 안에서 세 시간을 함께 보냈지요.\" 레빈이 미소 지으며 말했다."], ["said Levin smiling, “but got out, just as in a masquerade, quite mystified—at least I was.”", "\"그런데 가장무도회에서처럼 영문도 모른 채 내렸어요. 적어도 저는 그랬습니다.\""]], [["“Nonsense! Come along, please,”", "\"무슨 소리, 자, 어서들 오게.\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, pointing in the direction of the dining-room.", "스테판 아르카지치가 식당 쪽을 가리키며 말했다."]], [["The men went into the dining-room and went up to a table, laid with six sorts of spirits and as many kinds of cheese, some with little silver spades and some without, caviar, herrings, preserves of various kinds, and plates with slices of French bread.", "남자들은 식당으로 들어가 식탁으로 다가갔다. 식탁에는 여섯 가지 독한 술과 그만큼 여러 종류의 치즈가, 어떤 것은 작은 은제 칼을 곁들이고 어떤 것은 그냥, 그리고 캐비아, 청어, 갖가지 절임, 프랑스 빵 조각을 담은 접시들이 차려져 있었다."]], [["The men stood round the strong-smelling spirits and salt delicacies, and the discussion of the Russification of Poland between Koznishev, Karenin, and Pestsov died down in anticipation of dinner.", "남자들은 독한 냄새를 풍기는 술과 짭짤한 진미들 주위에 둘러섰고, 코즈니셰프와 카레닌과 페스초프 사이에 오가던 폴란드의 러시아화에 관한 토론은 만찬을 기다리며 사그라들었다."]], [["Sergey Ivanovitch was unequaled in his skill in winding up the most heated and serious argument by some unexpected pinch of Attic salt that changed the disposition of his opponent.", "세르게이 이바노비치는 가장 뜨겁고 진지한 논쟁이라도 상대방의 기분을 바꾸어 놓는 뜻밖의 아티카식 재치 한 자밤으로 마무리하는 솜씨가 따를 자가 없었다."], ["He did this now.", "그는 이번에도 그렇게 했다."]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch had been maintaining that the Russification of Poland could only be accomplished as a result of larger measures which ought to be introduced by the Russian government.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 폴란드의 러시아화가 오로지 러시아 정부가 도입해야 할 더 큰 규모의 조치들의 결과로만 이루어질 수 있다고 주장해 왔다."]], [["Pestsov insisted that one country can only absorb another when it is the more densely populated.", "페스초프는 한 나라가 다른 나라를 흡수할 수 있는 것은 그 나라가 인구밀도가 더 높을 때뿐이라고 우겼다."]], [["Koznishev admitted both points, but with limitations.", "코즈니셰프는 두 견해를 모두 인정하면서도 단서를 달았다."], ["As they were going out of the drawing-room to conclude the argument, Koznishev said, smiling:", "논쟁을 마무리하려고 응접실에서 나오던 길에 코즈니셰프가 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["“So, then, for the Russification of our foreign populations there is but one method—to bring up as many children as one can.", "\"그러니까 우리 이민족 주민들의 러시아화를 위한 방법은 단 하나뿐이군요—될 수 있는 한 아이를 많이 낳는 것 말입니다."], ["My brother and I are terribly in fault, I see.", "보아하니 내 동생과 나는 죄가 막심하군요."], ["You married men, especially you, Stepan Arkadyevitch, are the real patriots: what number have you reached?”", "결혼한 분들, 특히 스테판 아르카지치 당신 같은 분들이야말로 진정한 애국자지요."], ["he said, smiling genially at their host and holding out a tiny wine-glass to him.", "그래, 자녀를 몇이나 두셨소?\" 그는 주인을 향해 다정하게 웃으며 작은 술잔을 내밀었다."]], [["Everyone laughed, and Stepan Arkadyevitch with particular good humor.", "모두가 웃었고, 스테판 아르카지치는 유달리 흐뭇해했다."]], [["“Oh, yes, that’s the best method!”", "\"아, 그렇고말고요, 그게 최고의 방법이지요!\""], ["he said, munching cheese and filling the wine-glass with a special sort of spirit.", "그는 치즈를 우물거리며 특별한 종류의 술을 잔에 채우면서 말했다."], ["The conversation dropped at the jest.", "그 농담으로 대화가 끊겼다."]], [["“This cheese is not bad.", "\"이 치즈 괜찮은데."], ["Shall I give you some?”", "좀 줄까?\""], ["said the master of the house.", "주인이 말했다."], ["“Why, have you been going in for gymnastics again?”", "\"어럽쇼, 또 체조를 하기 시작했나?\""], ["he asked Levin, pinching his muscle with his left hand.", "그는 왼손으로 레빈의 근육을 꼬집으며 물었다."], ["Levin smiled, bent his arm, and under Stepan Arkadyevitch’s fingers the muscles swelled up like a sound cheese, hard as a knob of iron, through the fine cloth of the coat.", "레빈이 미소 지으며 팔을 굽히자, 스테판 아르카지치의 손가락 아래에서 근육이 단단한 치즈처럼, 쇠뭉치처럼 딴딴하게 솟아올라 코트의 고운 천을 밀어 올렸다."]], [["“What biceps!", "\"이 이두근 좀 보게!"], ["A perfect Samson!”", "영락없는 삼손이군!\""]], [["“I imagine great strength is needed for hunting bears,”", "\"곰 사냥에는 굉장한 힘이 필요할 것 같군요.\""], ["observed Alexey Alexandrovitch, who had the mistiest notions about the chase.", "사냥에 관해서는 더없이 어렴풋한 관념밖에 없는 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 말했다."], ["He cut off and spread with cheese a wafer of bread fine as a spider-web.", "그는 거미줄처럼 얇은 빵 조각을 잘라 치즈를 발랐다."]], [["Levin smiled.", "레빈이 미소 지었다."]], [["“Not at all.", "\"천만에요."], ["Quite the contrary; a child can kill a bear,”", "오히려 그 반대지요. 곰은 아이라도 죽일 수 있습니다.\""], ["he said, with a slight bow moving aside for the ladies, who were approaching the table.", "그는 식탁으로 다가오는 부인들에게 살짝 고개 숙이며 비켜서면서 말했다."]], [["“You have killed a bear, I’ve been told!”", "\"곰을 잡으셨다면서요!\""], ["said Kitty, trying assiduously to catch with her fork a perverse mushroom that would slip away, and setting the lace quivering over her white arm.", "키티가 자꾸만 미끄러져 달아나는 짓궂은 버섯을 포크로 잡으려 부지런히 애쓰며, 그 바람에 하얀 팔 위로 레이스를 떨리게 하면서 말했다."], ["“Are there bears on your place?”", "\"댁의 영지에 곰이 있나요?\""], ["she added, turning her charming little head to him and smiling.", "그녀가 사랑스러운 작은 머리를 그에게로 돌리며 미소 띤 채 덧붙였다."]], [["There was apparently nothing extraordinary in what she said, but what unutterable meaning there was for him in every sound, in every turn of her lips, her eyes, her hand as she said it!", "그녀가 한 말에는 겉보기에 별다른 것이 없었지만, 그녀가 그 말을 할 때의 모든 소리, 입술의 모든 움직임, 두 눈, 그 손에 그에게는 말로 다 할 수 없는 의미가 담겨 있었다!"], ["There was entreaty for forgiveness, and trust in him, and tenderness—soft, timid tenderness—and promise and hope and love for him, which he could not but believe in and which choked him with happiness.", "거기에는 용서를 구하는 간청과, 그에 대한 믿음과, 다정함—부드럽고 수줍은 다정함—과, 약속과, 희망과, 그를 향한 사랑이 담겨 있어, 그는 그것을 믿지 않을 수 없었고 행복에 숨이 막혔다."]], [["“No, we’ve been hunting in the Tver province.", "\"아니요, 우리는 트베리 지방에서 사냥을 했어요."], ["It was coming back from there that I met your beau-frère in the train, or your beau-frère’s brother-in-law,”", "거기서 돌아오는 길에 기차에서 당신의 beau-frère, 아니 당신의 beau-frère의 처남을 만났지요.\""], ["he said with a smile.", "그가 미소 지으며 말했다."], ["“It was an amusing meeting.”", "\"재미있는 만남이었어요.\""]], [["And he began telling with droll good-humor how, after not sleeping all night, he had, wearing an old fur-lined, full-skirted coat, got into Alexey Alexandrovitch’s compartment.", "그러고는 한숨도 못 잔 채 모피를 안에 댄 낡고 자락이 넓은 외투를 입고 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 객실에 들어가게 된 경위를, 익살스럽고 유쾌하게 들려주기 시작했다."]], [["“The conductor, forgetting the proverb, would have chucked me out on account of my attire; but thereupon I began expressing my feelings in elevated language, and ... you, too,” he said, addressing Karenin and forgetting his name, “at first would have ejected me on the ground of the old coat, but afterwards you took my part, for which I am extremely grateful.”", "* beau-frère: 프랑스어로 '매부, 자형, 처남, 시아주버니' 등 인척 남자를 두루 이르는 말. \"차장은 속담은 잊은 채 내 옷차림을 보고 나를 내쫓으려 했지요. 그래서 내가 고상한 말투로 내 심정을 늘어놓기 시작했더니… 당신도 말입니다.\" 그는 카레닌을 향해 말하면서 그의 이름을 잊어버린 채 말했다. \"처음엔 그 낡은 외투를 이유로 나를 내치려 하셨지만, 나중엔 내 편을 들어 주셨지요. 정말 고맙게 생각합니다.\""]], [["“The rights of passengers generally to choose their seats are too ill-defined,”", "\"대개 승객들이 자리를 고를 권리란 너무 모호하게 정해져 있지요.\""], ["said Alexey Alexandrovitch, rubbing the tips of his fingers on his handkerchief.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 손수건으로 손가락 끝을 닦으며 말했다."]], [["“I saw you were in uncertainty about me,”", "\"당신이 나를 두고 갸웃거리시는 걸 봤습니다.\""], ["said Levin, smiling good-naturedly, “but I made haste to plunge into intellectual conversation to smooth over the defects of my attire.”", "레빈이 사람 좋게 미소 지으며 말했다. \"그래서 내 옷차림의 결함을 무마하려고 서둘러 지적인 대화에 뛰어들었지요.\""], ["Sergey Ivanovitch, while he kept up a conversation with their hostess, had one ear for his brother, and he glanced askance at him.", "세르게이 이바노비치는 안주인과 대화를 이어 가면서도 한쪽 귀로는 동생의 말을 듣고 있다가 곁눈질로 그를 흘긋 바라보았다."], ["“What is the matter with him today?", "'쟤가 오늘 왜 저러지?"], ["Why such a conquering hero?”", "어쩌자고 저렇게 개선장군 같담?'"], ["he thought.", "그는 생각했다."], ["He did not know that Levin was feeling as though he had grown wings.", "그는 레빈이 마치 날개라도 돋친 듯한 기분이라는 것을 알지 못했다."], ["Levin knew she was listening to his words and that she was glad to listen to him.", "레빈은 그녀가 자기 말에 귀 기울이고 있으며 자기 말을 즐겨 듣고 있다는 것을 알고 있었다."], ["And this was the only thing that interested him.", "그리고 그것만이 그의 유일한 관심사였다."], ["Not in that room only, but in the whole world, there existed for him only himself, with enormously increased importance and dignity in his own eyes, and she.", "이 방 안에서만이 아니라 온 세상에서 그에게 존재하는 것은, 자기 눈에 한없이 커진 중요성과 위엄을 띤 자기 자신과, 그녀뿐이었다."], ["He felt himself on a pinnacle that made him giddy, and far away down below were all those nice excellent Karenins, Oblonskys, and all the world.", "그는 어지러울 만큼 높은 봉우리 위에 서 있는 듯한 기분이었고, 저 아래 까마득한 곳에 그 모든 친절하고 훌륭한 카레닌, 오블론스키 일가, 그리고 온 세상이 있었다."]], [["Quite without attracting notice, without glancing at them, as though there were no other places left, Stepan Arkadyevitch put Levin and Kitty side by side.", "누구의 주의도 끌지 않고, 그들을 바라보지도 않은 채, 마치 다른 자리는 남아 있지 않다는 듯이, 스테판 아르카지치는 레빈과 키티를 나란히 앉혔다."]], [["“Oh, you may as well sit there,”", "\"아, 자네는 거기 앉으면 되겠군.\""], ["he said to Levin.", "그가 레빈에게 말했다."]], [["The dinner was as choice as the china, in which Stepan Arkadyevitch was a connoisseur.", "만찬은 스테판 아르카지치가 정통한 분야인 도자기만큼이나 정선된 것이었다."], ["The soupe Marie-Louise was a splendid success; the tiny pies eaten with it melted in the mouth and were irreproachable.", "soupe Marie-Louise는 훌륭하게 성공했고, 그것과 함께 먹는 자그마한 파이는 입에서 살살 녹았으며 나무랄 데가 없었다."], ["The two footmen and Matvey, in white cravats, did their duty with the dishes and wines unobtrusively, quietly, and swiftly.", "흰 넥타이를 맨 두 하인과 마트베이는 요리와 술 시중을 눈에 띄지 않게, 조용하고 날렵하게 들었다."], ["On the material side the dinner was a success; it was no less so on the immaterial.", "물질적인 면에서 만찬은 성공이었고, 비물질적인 면에서도 그에 못지않게 성공이었다."], ["The conversation, at times general and at times between individuals, never paused, and towards the end the company was so lively that the men rose from the table, without stopping speaking, and even Alexey Alexandrovitch thawed.", "때로는 다 함께, 때로는 개인끼리 오가는 대화는 한순간도 끊이지 않았고, 끝 무렵에는 좌중이 어찌나 활기를 띠던지 남자들은 이야기를 멈추지도 않은 채 식탁에서 일어났으며, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치마저 마음이 누그러졌다."]], [["# Chapter 10", "# Chapter 10"]], [["Pestsov liked thrashing an argument out to the end, and was not satisfied with Sergey Ivanovitch’s words, especially as he felt the injustice of his view.", "페스초프는 논쟁을 끝까지 물고 늘어지기를 좋아했고, 세르게이 이바노비치의 말에 만족하지 못했다. 자기 견해가 옳지 못하다는 것을 느꼈기에 더욱 그러했다."]], [["“I did not mean,”", "\"제가 말씀드린 건,\" 그는 수프를 들면서 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치를 향해 말했다."], ["he said over the soup, addressing Alexey Alexandrovitch, “mere density of population alone, but in conjunction with fundamental ideas, and not by means of principles.”", "\"단순한 인구 밀도만이 아니라, 그것이 근본적인 사상과 결합한 경우를 말하는 겁니다. 원리에 의해서가 아니라요.\""]], [["“It seems to me,”", "\"제가 보기엔,\""], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch said languidly, and with no haste, “that that’s the same thing.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 나른하게, 서두르는 기색 없이 말했다. \"그게 결국 같은 얘기 같습니다만."], ["In my opinion, influence over another people is only possible to the people which has the higher development, which....”", "제 생각에 다른 민족에게 영향을 미치는 일은 더 높이 발전한 민족만이 할 수 있는 것이고, 그 민족이…….\""]], [["“But that’s just the question,”", "\"하지만 바로 그게 문제죠.\""], ["Pestsov broke in in his bass.", "페스초프가 굵은 저음으로 끼어들었다."], ["He was always in a hurry to speak, and seemed always to put his whole soul into what he was saying.", "그는 늘 말하기를 서둘렀고, 언제나 자기가 하는 말에 온 영혼을 쏟아붓는 것 같았다."], ["“In what are we to make higher development consist?", "\"도대체 무엇을 더 높은 발전이라고 봐야 합니까?"], ["The English, the French, the Germans, which is at the highest stage of development?", "영국인, 프랑스인, 독일인, 이 중 어느 쪽이 발전의 가장 높은 단계에 있나요?"], ["Which of them will nationalize the other?", "어느 민족이 다른 민족을 동화시키겠습니까?"], ["We see the Rhine provinces have been turned French, but the Germans are not at a lower stage!”", "라인란트 지방이 프랑스화된 것을 우리는 알고 있지만, 그렇다고 독일인이 더 낮은 단계에 있는 건 아니지 않습니까!\""], ["he shouted.", "그가 소리쳤다."], ["“There is another law at work there.”", "\"거기엔 다른 법칙이 작용하고 있는 겁니다.\""]], [["“I fancy that the greater influence is always on the side of true civilization,”", "\"제 생각엔 더 큰 영향력은 언제나 참된 문명 쪽에 있는 것 같습니다.\""], ["said Alexey Alexandrovitch, slightly lifting his eyebrows.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 눈썹을 살짝 치켜올리며 말했다."]], [["“But what are we to lay down as the outward signs of true civilization?”", "\"그렇다면 참된 문명의 외적인 표지로 무엇을 내세워야 합니까?\""], ["said Pestsov.", "페스초프가 말했다."]], [["“I imagine such signs are generally very well known,”", "\"그런 표지는 대체로 아주 잘 알려져 있다고 봅니다만.\""], ["said Alexey Alexandrovitch.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 말했다."]], [["“But are they fully known?”", "\"하지만 그게 온전히 알려져 있을까요?\""], ["Sergey Ivanovitch put in with a subtle smile.", "세르게이 이바노비치가 은근한 미소를 지으며 끼어들었다."], ["“It is the accepted view now that real culture must be purely classical; but we see most intense disputes on each side of the question, and there is no denying that the opposite camp has strong points in its favor.”", "\"지금은 진정한 교양이 순전히 고전적인 것이어야 한다는 견해가 받아들여지고 있지만, 우리는 이 문제를 두고 양쪽 모두에서 격렬한 논쟁이 벌어지는 것을 보고 있습니다. 그리고 반대편 진영에도 유리한 강점이 있다는 것을 부인할 수는 없죠.\""]], [["“You are for classics, Sergey Ivanovitch.", "\"세르게이 이바노비치, 당신은 고전 쪽이시군요."], ["Will you take red wine?”", "적포도주 한잔하시겠습니까?\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch.", "스테판 아르카지치가 말했다."]], [["“I am not expressing my own opinion of either form of culture,”", "\"저는 어느 쪽 교양에 대해서도 제 개인적인 견해를 표명하는 게 아닙니다.\""], ["Sergey Ivanovitch said, holding out his glass with a smile of condescension, as to a child.", "세르게이 이바노비치가 아이에게 하듯 너그러운 미소를 지으며 잔을 내밀었다."], ["“I only say that both sides have strong arguments to support them,”", "\"다만 양쪽 다 그것을 뒷받침할 강력한 논거를 가지고 있다는 것을 말하는 것뿐이죠.\""], ["he went on, addressing Alexey Alexandrovitch.", "그가 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치를 향해 말을 이었다."], ["“My sympathies are classical from education, but in this discussion I am personally unable to arrive at a conclusion.", "\"제 호감은 받은 교육 덕분에 고전 쪽으로 기울어 있습니다만, 이 논의에서 저는 개인적으로 결론에 이르지 못하겠습니다."], ["I see no distinct grounds for classical studies being given a preeminence over scientific studies.”", "고전 연구가 과학 연구보다 우위를 점해야 할 뚜렷한 근거를 찾을 수가 없거든요.\""]], [["“The natural sciences have just as great an educational value,”", "\"자연과학도 그에 못지않은 교육적 가치를 가지고 있습니다.\""], ["put in Pestsov.", "페스초프가 끼어들었다."], ["“Take astronomy, take botany, or zoology with its system of general principles.”", "\"천문학을 보세요, 식물학을 보세요, 아니면 일반 원리의 체계를 갖춘 동물학을 보세요.\""]], [["“I cannot quite agree with that,” responded Alexey Alexandrovitch.", "\"저는 거기에 전적으로 동의하기는 어렵습니다.\""], ["“It seems to me that one must admit that the very process of studying the forms of language has a peculiarly favorable influence on intellectual development.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 대꾸했다. \"언어의 형태를 연구하는 과정 자체가 지적 발달에 유난히 이로운 영향을 미친다는 점은 인정해야 한다고 봅니다."], ["Moreover, it cannot be denied that the influence of the classical authors is in the highest degree moral, while, unfortunately, with the study of the natural sciences are associated the false and noxious doctrines which are the curse of our day.”", "게다가 고전 작가들의 영향이 더없이 도덕적이라는 점도 부인할 수 없죠. 반면 안타깝게도 자연과학 연구에는 우리 시대의 저주라 할 그릇되고 해로운 학설들이 결부되어 있습니다.\""]], [["Sergey Ivanovitch would have said something, but Pestsov interrupted him in his rich bass.", "세르게이 이바노비치가 무언가 말하려 했지만, 페스초프가 그 풍부한 저음으로 그의 말을 가로막았다."], ["He began warmly contesting the justice of this view.", "그는 이 견해의 정당함에 대해 열을 올리며 반박하기 시작했다."], ["Sergey Ivanovitch waited serenely to speak, obviously with a convincing reply ready.", "세르게이 이바노비치는 분명 설득력 있는 답변을 준비해 둔 채, 침착하게 말할 차례를 기다렸다."]], [["“But,” said Sergey Ivanovitch, smiling subtly, and addressing Karenin, “One must allow that to weigh all the advantages and disadvantages of classical and scientific studies is a difficult task, and the question which form of education was to be preferred would not have been so quickly and conclusively decided if there had not been in favor of classical education, as you expressed it just now, its moral—disons le mot—anti-nihilist influence.”", "\"하지만,\" 세르게이 이바노비치가 은근한 미소를 지으며 카레닌을 향해 말했다. \"고전 연구와 과학 연구의 모든 장단점을 저울질하는 것이 어려운 일이라는 점은 인정해야겠지요. 그리고 어느 형태의 교육을 선호해야 하는가 하는 문제도, 방금 당신이 표현하신 대로 고전 교육 편에 그 도덕적인—disons le mot—반(反)니힐리즘적 영향이 없었더라면 이렇게 빠르고 단호하게 결정되지는 않았을 겁니다.\" * disons le mot: 프랑스어로 '터놓고 말하자면'."]], [["“Undoubtedly.”", "\"틀림없이 그렇죠.\""]], [["“If it had not been for the distinctive property of anti-nihilistic influence on the side of classical studies, we should have considered the subject more, have weighed the arguments on both sides,”", "\"만약 고전 연구 편에 반니힐리즘적 영향이라는 그 독특한 성질이 없었더라면, 우리는 이 문제를 더 깊이 고려하고 양쪽 논거를 저울질했을 겁니다.\""], ["said Sergey Ivanovitch with a subtle smile, “we should have given elbow-room to both tendencies.", "세르게이 이바노비치가 은근한 미소를 지으며 말했다. \"양쪽 경향 모두에 활동할 여지를 주었겠지요."], ["But now we know that these little pills of classical learning possess the medicinal property of anti-nihilism, and we boldly prescribe them to our patients....", "하지만 지금 우리는 이 고전 학문이라는 작은 알약이 반니힐리즘이라는 약효를 지니고 있다는 것을 알기에, 대담하게 그것을 환자들에게 처방하는 겁니다……."], ["But what if they had no such medicinal property?”", "그런데 만약 그 알약에 그런 약효가 없다면 어떻게 되겠습니까?\""], ["he wound up humorously.", "그가 익살스럽게 말을 맺었다."]], [["At Sergey Ivanovitch’s little pills, everyone laughed; Turovtsin in especial roared loudly and jovially, glad at last to have found something to laugh at, all he ever looked for in listening to conversation.", "세르게이 이바노비치의 작은 알약 이야기에 모두 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 특히 투롭친은 크고 유쾌하게 껄껄 웃어 댔는데, 마침내 웃을 거리를 찾아 기뻐하는 것이었다. 그가 대화에 귀 기울이며 늘 바라던 것은 오직 그것뿐이었다."]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch had not made a mistake in inviting Pestsov.", "스테판 아르카지치는 페스초프를 초대한 것이 잘못이 아니었다."], ["With Pestsov intellectual conversation never flagged for an instant.", "페스초프가 있으면 지적인 대화가 한순간도 끊기는 법이 없었다."], ["Directly Sergey Ivanovitch had concluded the conversation with his jest, Pestsov promptly started a new one.", "세르게이 이바노비치가 농담으로 대화를 마무리하자마자 페스초프는 곧바로 새로운 이야기를 꺼냈다."]], [["“I can’t agree even,” said he, “that the government had that aim.", "\"저는 정부가 그런 목적을 가졌다는 데에도 동의할 수 없습니다.\""], ["The government obviously is guided by abstract considerations, and remains indifferent to the influence its measures may exercise.", "그가 말했다. \"정부는 분명 추상적인 고려에 따라 움직이고, 자기들의 조치가 어떤 영향을 미치는지에는 무관심합니다."], ["The education of women, for instance, would naturally be regarded as likely to be harmful, but the government opens schools and universities for women.”", "예를 들어 여성 교육은 당연히 해로운 것으로 여겨질 만한데도, 정부는 여성을 위한 학교와 대학을 열고 있지 않습니까.\""]], [["And the conversation at once passed to the new subject of the education of women.", "그러자 대화는 곧바로 여성 교육이라는 새로운 주제로 넘어갔다."]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch expressed the idea that the education of women is apt to be confounded with the emancipation of women, and that it is only so that it can be considered dangerous.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 여성 교육이 여성 해방과 혼동되기 쉬우며, 오직 그 점에서만 위험하다고 여겨질 수 있다는 견해를 밝혔다."]], [["“I consider, on the contrary, that the two questions are inseparably connected together,”", "\"저는 오히려 그 두 문제가 떼려야 뗄 수 없게 얽혀 있다고 봅니다.\""], ["said Pestsov; “it is a vicious circle.", "페스초프가 말했다. \"그건 악순환이죠."], ["Woman is deprived of rights from lack of education, and the lack of education results from the absence of rights.", "여성은 교육이 부족해서 권리를 빼앗기고, 권리가 없기 때문에 교육이 부족한 겁니다."], ["We must not forget that the subjection of women is so complete, and dates from such ages back that we are often unwilling to recognize the gulf that separates them from us,”", "여성에 대한 예속이 너무나 철저하고, 또 너무 먼 옛날부터 이어져 온 것이라, 우리는 종종 그들과 우리를 갈라놓는 심연을 인정하려 들지 않는다는 점을 잊어서는 안 됩니다.\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“You said rights,”", "\"방금 권리라고 하셨는데,\""], ["said Sergey Ivanovitch, waiting till Pestsov had finished, “meaning the right of sitting on juries, of voting, of presiding at official meetings, the right of entering the civil service, of sitting in parliament....”", "세르게이 이바노비치가 페스초프의 말이 끝나기를 기다렸다가 말했다. \"그건 배심원으로 앉을 권리, 투표할 권리, 공식 회의를 주재할 권리, 관청에 들어갈 권리, 의회에 의석을 둘 권리를 말씀하시는 거겠죠…….\""]], [["“Undoubtedly.”", "\"틀림없이 그렇습니다.\""]], [["“But if women, as a rare exception, can occupy such positions, it seems to me you are wrong in using the expression ‘rights.’ It would be more correct to say duties.", "\"하지만 여성이 드문 예외로 그런 자리를 차지할 수 있다 하더라도, '권리'라는 표현을 쓰시는 건 잘못인 것 같습니다. 의무라고 하는 편이 더 정확하겠지요."], ["Every man will agree that in doing the duty of a juryman, a witness, a telegraph clerk, we feel we are performing duties.", "배심원이나 증인이나 전신국 직원의 직무를 수행할 때, 우리가 의무를 다하고 있다고 느낀다는 데에는 누구나 동의할 겁니다."], ["And therefore it would be correct to say that women are seeking duties, and quite legitimately.", "그러니 여성들이 의무를 찾고 있다고 말하는 편이 정확하겠고, 그건 지극히 정당한 일이죠."], ["And one can but sympathize with this desire to assist in the general labor of man.”", "그리고 인간 전체의 노동을 돕고자 하는 그 바람에는 공감할 수밖에 없습니다.\""]], [["“Quite so,”", "\"바로 그렇습니다.\""], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch assented.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 동의했다."], ["“The question, I imagine, is simply whether they are fitted for such duties.”", "\"문제는 그저 그들이 그런 직무에 적합한가 하는 것이겠죠.\""]], [["“They will most likely be perfectly fitted,”", "\"교육이 그들 사이에 두루 퍼지게 되면,\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, “when education has become general among them.", "스테판 아르카지치가 말했다. \"아마 완벽하게 적합해질 겁니다."], ["We see this....”", "우리는 그것을 보고 있죠…….\""]], [["“How about the proverb?”", "\"그 속담은 어쩌고?\""], ["said the prince, who had a long while been intent on the conversation, his little comical eyes twinkling.", "한참 동안 대화에 골똘히 귀 기울이고 있던 공작이 그 작고 익살스러운 눈을 반짝이며 말했다."], ["“I can say it before my daughter: her hair is long, because her wit is....”", "\"내 딸 앞에서도 할 수 있는 말이지. 머리카락이 길면, 지혜는…….\""]], [["“Just what they thought of the negroes before their emancipation!”", "\"흑인들도 해방되기 전엔 꼭 그렇게 여겨졌죠!\""], ["said Pestsov angrily.", "페스초프가 화를 내며 말했다."]], [["“What seems strange to me is that women should seek fresh duties,”", "\"제겐 여성들이 새로운 의무를 찾으려 한다는 게 이상하게 느껴집니다.\""], ["said Sergey Ivanovitch, “while we see, unhappily, that men usually try to avoid them.”", "세르게이 이바노비치가 말했다. \"안타깝게도 남자들은 대개 그것을 피하려 드는 걸 보고 있으니 말입니다.\""]], [["“Duties are bound up with rights—power, money, honor; those are what women are seeking,”", "\"의무에는 권리가 따라붙죠—권력, 돈, 명예. 여성들이 찾는 건 바로 그것들입니다.\""], ["said Pestsov.", "페스초프가 말했다."]], [["“Just as though I should seek the right to be a wet-nurse and feel injured because women are paid for the work, while no one will take me,”", "\"꼭 내가 유모가 될 권리를 찾아 나서서, 여자들은 그 일로 돈을 받는데 나는 아무도 안 써 준다고 억울해하는 격이군.\""], ["said the old prince.", "노공작이 말했다."]], [["Turovtsin exploded in a loud roar of laughter and Sergey Ivanovitch regretted that he had not made this comparison.", "투롭친이 큰 소리로 웃음을 터뜨렸고, 세르게이 이바노비치는 자기가 그 비유를 하지 못한 것을 아쉬워했다."], ["Even Alexey Alexandrovitch smiled.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치조차 미소를 지었다."]], [["“Yes, but a man can’t nurse a baby,”", "\"맞습니다, 하지만 남자는 아기에게 젖을 먹일 수가 없죠.\""], ["said Pestsov, “while a woman....”", "페스초프가 말했다. \"반면 여자는…….\""]], [["“No, there was an Englishman who did suckle his baby on board ship,”", "\"아니, 배 위에서 제 아기에게 젖을 물린 영국인이 있었다오.\""], ["said the old prince, feeling this freedom in conversation permissible before his own daughters.", "노공작이 자기 딸들 앞에서 이런 자유로운 대화쯤은 허용된다고 여기며 말했다."]], [["“There are as many such Englishmen as there would be women officials,”", "\"그런 영국인은 여성 관리만큼이나 많겠지요.\""], ["said Sergey Ivanovitch.", "세르게이 이바노비치가 말했다."]], [["“Yes, but what is a girl to do who has no family?”", "\"맞아요, 하지만 가족이 없는 처녀는 어떻게 해야 합니까?\""], ["put in Stepan Arkadyevitch, thinking of Masha Tchibisova, whom he had had in his mind all along, in sympathizing with Pestsov and supporting him.", "스테판 아르카지치가 끼어들었다. 페스초프에게 공감하며 그를 거드는 내내 그는 마샤 치비소바를 마음에 두고 있었던 것이다."]], [["“If the story of such a girl were thoroughly sifted, you would find she had abandoned a family—her own or a sister’s, where she might have found a woman’s duties,”", "\"그런 처녀의 사연을 낱낱이 들춰 보면, 그 여자가 자기 가족이든 언니의 가족이든 어떤 가정을 버렸다는 걸 알게 될 거예요. 거기서 여자로서의 의무를 찾을 수 있었을 텐데 말이죠.\""], ["Darya Alexandrovna broke in unexpectedly in a tone of exasperation, probably suspecting what sort of girl Stepan Arkadyevitch was thinking of.", "다리야 알렉산드로브나가 뜻밖에도 분에 찬 어조로 불쑥 끼어들었다. 아마 스테판 아르카지치가 어떤 처녀를 떠올리고 있는지 눈치챈 모양이었다."]], [["“But we take our stand on principle as the ideal,”", "\"하지만 우리는 이상으로서 원칙에 입각해 이야기하는 겁니다.\""], ["replied Pestsov in his mellow bass.", "페스초프가 부드러운 저음으로 대답했다."], ["“Woman desires to have rights, to be independent, educated.", "\"여성은 권리를 갖기를, 독립하기를, 교육받기를 원합니다."], ["She is oppressed, humiliated by the consciousness of her disabilities.”", "그녀는 자신의 무력함을 의식하며 억압당하고 모욕당하고 있죠.\""]], [["“And I’m oppressed and humiliated that they won’t engage me at the Foundling,”", "\"나도 양육원에서 날 안 써 준다고 억압당하고 모욕당하고 있다오.\""], ["the old prince said again, to the huge delight of Turovtsin, who in his mirth dropped his asparagus with the thick end in the sauce.", "노공작이 다시 말했고, 투롭친은 그 말에 크게 기뻐하며 웃다가 아스파라거스를 굵은 쪽이 소스에 빠지도록 떨어뜨렸다."]], [["# Chapter 11", "# Chapter 11"]], [["Everyone took part in the conversation except Kitty and Levin.", "키티와 레빈을 빼고는 모두가 대화에 끼어들었다."], ["At first, when they were talking of the influence that one people has on another, there rose to Levin’s mind what he had to say on the subject.", "처음에 한 민족이 다른 민족에게 미치는 영향에 관한 이야기가 나왔을 때, 레빈의 머릿속에는 그 주제에 대해 자기가 할 말이 떠올랐다."], ["But these ideas, once of such importance in his eyes, seemed to come into his brain as in a dream, and had now not the slightest interest for him.", "하지만 한때 그의 눈에 그토록 중요하게 여겨지던 이 생각들이 이제는 마치 꿈속에서처럼 머릿속에 떠오를 뿐, 더 이상 그에게 조금의 흥미도 주지 못했다."], ["It even struck him as strange that they should be so eager to talk of what was of no use to anyone.", "누구에게도 쓸모없는 이야기를 저토록 열심히 떠드는 것이 그에게는 오히려 이상하게 여겨질 정도였다."], ["Kitty, too, should, one would have supposed, have been interested in what they were saying of the rights and education of women.", "키티 역시 여성의 권리와 교육에 관해 그들이 나누는 이야기에 관심을 가질 법도 했다."], ["How often she had mused on the subject, thinking of her friend abroad, Varenka, of her painful state of dependence, how often she had wondered about herself what would become of her if she did not marry, and how often she had argued with her sister about it!", "그녀는 외국에 있는 친구 바렌카와 그녀의 고통스러운 종속 상태를 떠올리며 그 주제에 대해 얼마나 자주 골똘히 생각했던가. 결혼하지 않으면 자기는 어떻게 될까 하고 스스로에 대해 얼마나 자주 궁금해했으며, 그 일을 두고 언니와 얼마나 자주 다투었던가!"], ["But it did not interest her at all.", "그런데도 지금은 그것이 전혀 그녀의 흥미를 끌지 못했다."], ["She and Levin had a conversation of their own, yet not a conversation, but some sort of mysterious communication, which brought them every moment nearer, and stirred in both a sense of glad terror before the unknown into which they were entering.", "그녀와 레빈은 그들만의 대화를 나누고 있었는데, 그것은 대화라기보다 어떤 신비로운 교감 같은 것이어서, 매 순간 두 사람을 더 가까이 끌어당겼고, 그들이 막 들어서고 있는 미지의 세계 앞에서 두 사람 모두의 마음속에 기쁜 두려움을 불러일으켰다."]], [["At first Levin, in answer to Kitty’s question how he could have seen her last year in the carriage, told her how he had been coming home from the mowing along the highroad and had met her.", "처음에 레빈은 작년에 어떻게 마차 안의 자기를 볼 수 있었느냐는 키티의 물음에, 풀베기를 마치고 큰길을 따라 집으로 돌아오다가 그녀와 마주쳤다고 이야기했다."]], [["“It was very, very early in the morning.", "\"아주, 아주 이른 아침이었어요."], ["You were probably only just awake.", "당신은 아마 막 잠에서 깬 참이었을 거예요."], ["Your mother was asleep in the corner.", "어머니는 한쪽 구석에서 주무시고 계셨고요."], ["It was an exquisite morning.", "정말 더없이 아름다운 아침이었죠."], ["I was walking along wondering who it could be in a four-in-hand?", "나는 길을 걸으면서 네 마리 말이 끄는 저 마차에 탄 사람이 누굴까 생각하고 있었어요."], ["It was a splendid set of four horses with bells, and in a second you flashed by, and I saw you at the window—you were sitting like this, holding the strings of your cap in both hands, and thinking awfully deeply about something,”", "방울을 단 멋진 네 필의 말이었는데, 한순간에 당신이 휙 지나갔고, 나는 창가에 있는 당신을 보았죠—당신은 이렇게 앉아 두 손으로 모자 끈을 잡은 채 무언가를 무척이나 깊이 생각하고 있었어요.\""], ["he said, smiling.", "그는 미소 지으며 말했다."], ["“How I should like to know what you were thinking about then!", "\"그때 당신이 무슨 생각을 하고 있었는지 정말 알고 싶군요!"], ["Something important?”", "뭔가 중요한 것이었나요?\""]], [["“Wasn’t I dreadfully untidy?”", "'내가 그때 형편없이 단정치 못한 모습이지 않았을까?'"], ["she wondered, but seeing the smile of ecstasy these reminiscences called up, she felt that the impression she had made had been very good.", "그녀는 궁금했지만, 그 추억이 불러일으킨 그의 황홀한 미소를 보고는 자기가 남긴 인상이 매우 좋았음을 느꼈다."], ["She blushed and laughed with delight; “Really I don’t remember.”", "그녀는 얼굴을 붉히며 기쁨에 차 웃었다. \"정말이지 기억이 안 나요.\""]], [["“How nicely Turovtsin laughs!”", "\"투로프친이 웃는 모습이 참 보기 좋네요!\""], ["said Levin, admiring his moist eyes and shaking chest.", "레빈이 그의 촉촉한 눈과 들썩이는 가슴을 흐뭇하게 바라보며 말했다."]], [["“Have you known him long?”", "\"그분을 안 지 오래되셨어요?\""], ["asked Kitty.", "키티가 물었다."]], [["“Oh, everyone knows him!”", "\"오, 모두가 그를 알죠!\""]], [["“And I see you think he’s a horrid man?”", "\"그런데 보아하니 그분을 형편없는 사람이라고 생각하시는 것 같네요?\""]], [["“Not horrid, but nothing in him.”", "\"형편없다기보다는, 그저 알맹이가 없는 사람이죠.\""]], [["“Oh, you’re wrong!", "\"오, 그건 틀렸어요!"], ["And you must give up thinking so directly!”", "그런 생각은 당장 버리셔야 해요!\""], ["said Kitty.", "키티가 말했다."], ["“I used to have a very poor opinion of him too, but he, he’s an awfully nice and wonderfully good-hearted man.", "\"저도 한때 그분을 아주 보잘것없게 여겼지만, 그분은, 그분은 정말로 좋은 사람이고 더없이 마음씨가 고운 분이에요."], ["He has a heart of gold.”", "황금 같은 마음을 지녔답니다.\""]], [["“How could you find out what sort of heart he has?”", "\"그가 어떤 마음을 지녔는지 어떻게 알아냈죠?\""]], [["“We are great friends.", "\"우리는 아주 친한 사이거든요."], ["I know him very well.", "저는 그분을 아주 잘 알아요."], ["Last winter, soon after ...", "지난겨울, 그러니까..."], ["you came to see us,”", "당신이 우리 집에 다녀가신 직후에,\""], ["she said, with a guilty and at the same time confiding smile, “all Dolly’s children had scarlet fever, and he happened to come and see her.", "그녀는 죄지은 듯하면서도 동시에 속을 털어놓는 미소를 지으며 말했다. \"돌리의 아이들이 모두 성홍열에 걸렸는데, 마침 그분이 언니를 보러 왔어요."], ["And only fancy,”", "그런데 글쎄,\""], ["she said in a whisper, “he felt so sorry for her that he stayed and began to help her look after the children.", "그녀가 속삭이듯 말했다. \"그분은 언니를 너무도 안쓰러워한 나머지 그대로 머물면서 아이들 돌보는 일을 거들기 시작했어요."], ["Yes, and for three weeks he stopped with them, and looked after the children like a nurse.”", "네, 그렇게 삼 주 동안이나 함께 지내면서 보모처럼 아이들을 돌봐 주었답니다.\""]], [["“I am telling Konstantin Dmitrievitch about Turovtsin in the scarlet fever,”", "\"투로프친이 성홍열 때 한 일을 콘스탄틴 드미트리치께 이야기하는 중이야.\""], ["she said, bending over to her sister.", "그녀가 언니 쪽으로 몸을 기울이며 말했다."]], [["“Yes, it was wonderful, noble!”", "\"그래, 정말 훌륭했어, 고귀한 일이었지!\""], ["said Dolly, glancing towards Turovtsin, who had become aware they were talking of him, and smiling gently to him.", "돌리가 투로프친 쪽을 흘긋 바라보며 말했다. 그는 사람들이 자기 이야기를 하고 있음을 알아채고는 그녀에게 부드럽게 미소를 지었다."], ["Levin glanced once more at Turovtsin, and wondered how it was he had not realized all this man’s goodness before.", "레빈은 다시 한 번 투로프친을 바라보며, 어째서 자기는 진작 이 사람의 선함을 알아보지 못했을까 의아해했다."]], [["“I’m sorry, I’m sorry, and I’ll never think ill of people again!”", "\"미안합니다, 미안해요, 다시는 사람을 나쁘게 생각하지 않겠어요!\""], ["he said gaily, genuinely expressing what he felt at the moment.", "그는 그 순간 느낀 바를 진심으로 드러내며 명랑하게 말했다."]], [["# Chapter 12", "# Chapter 12"]], [["Connected with the conversation that had sprung up on the rights of women there were certain questions as to the inequality of rights in marriage improper to discuss before the ladies.", "여자의 권리를 둘러싸고 일어난 대화와 연결되어, 결혼 생활에서의 권리 불평등에 관한 몇 가지 문제가 있었는데, 부인들 앞에서 논하기에는 적절치 않은 것들이었다."], ["Pestsov had several times during dinner touched upon these questions, but Sergey Ivanovitch and Stepan Arkadyevitch carefully drew him off them.", "페스초프는 만찬 동안 여러 차례 이 문제들을 건드렸지만, 세르게이 이바노비치와 스테판 아르카지치는 조심스럽게 그를 거기에서 떼어 놓았다."]], [["When they rose from the table and the ladies had gone out, Pestsov did not follow them, but addressing Alexey Alexandrovitch, began to expound the chief ground of inequality.", "식탁에서 일어나 부인들이 나가 버리자, 페스초프는 그들을 따라가지 않고 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치를 향해 불평등의 주된 근거를 설명하기 시작했다."], ["The inequality in marriage, in his opinion, lay in the fact that the infidelity of the wife and the infidelity of the husband are punished unequally, both by the law and by public opinion.", "그의 견해로는, 결혼 생활에서의 불평등이란 아내의 부정과 남편의 부정이 법으로나 여론으로나 불평등하게 처벌받는다는 사실에 있었다."], ["Stepan Arkadyevitch went hurriedly up to Alexey Alexandrovitch and offered him a cigar.", "스테판 아르카지치는 급히 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에게 다가가 시가를 권했다."]], [["“No, I don’t smoke,”", "\"아니, 난 담배를 피우지 않네.\""], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch answered calmly, and as though purposely wishing to show that he was not afraid of the subject, he turned to Pestsov with a chilly smile.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 침착하게 대답했고, 일부러 그 주제를 두려워하지 않음을 보이려는 듯, 차가운 미소를 띠며 페스초프를 향해 돌아섰다."]], [["“I imagine that such a view has a foundation in the very nature of things,”", "\"내 생각엔 그런 견해는 사물의 본성 그 자체에 근거를 두고 있는 것 같소.\""], ["he said, and would have gone on to the drawing-room.", "그는 그렇게 말하고는 응접실로 가려 했다."], ["But at this point Turovtsin broke suddenly and unexpectedly into the conversation, addressing Alexey Alexandrovitch.", "그런데 이때 투롭친이 별안간 뜻밖에도 대화에 끼어들어 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에게 말을 걸었다."]], [["“You heard, perhaps, about Pryatchnikov?”", "\"혹시 프라치니코프에 관한 얘기 들으셨소?\""], ["said Turovtsin, warmed up by the champagne he had drunk, and long waiting for an opportunity to break the silence that had weighed on him.", "투롭친이 말했는데, 그는 마신 샴페인으로 들떠 있었고, 자기를 짓누르던 침묵을 깨뜨릴 기회를 오래 기다려 온 터였다."], ["“Vasya Pryatchnikov,”", "\"바샤 프라치니코프 말이오.\""], ["he said, with a good-natured smile on his damp, red lips, addressing himself principally to the most important guest, Alexey Alexandrovitch, “they told me today he fought a duel with Kvitsky at Tver, and has killed him.”", "그는 축축한 붉은 입술에 사람 좋은 미소를 띠며, 주로 가장 중요한 손님인 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치를 향해 말했다. \"오늘 들은 얘긴데, 트베리에서 크비츠키랑 결투를 벌여서 그를 죽였다지 뭐요.\""]], [["Just as it always seems that one bruises oneself on a sore place, so Stepan Arkadyevitch felt now that the conversation would by ill luck fall every moment on Alexey Alexandrovitch’s sore spot.", "마치 아픈 자리를 자꾸 부딪치게 되는 것처럼 느껴지듯, 스테판 아르카지치는 지금 이 대화가 운 나쁘게도 매 순간 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 아픈 곳으로 떨어진다고 느꼈다."], ["He would again have got his brother-in-law away, but Alexey Alexandrovitch himself inquired, with curiosity:", "그는 또다시 처남을 떼어 놓으려 했지만, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치 자신이 호기심을 보이며 물었다."]], [["“What did Pryatchnikov fight about?”", "\"프라치니코프는 뭣 때문에 결투를 한 거요?\""]], [["“His wife.", "\"아내 때문이오."], ["Acted like a man, he did!", "사내답게 굴었지 뭐요!"], ["Called him out and shot him!”", "결투를 신청해서 쏘아 버렸으니!\""]], [["“Ah!”", "\"아!\""], ["said Alexey Alexandrovitch indifferently, and lifting his eyebrows, he went into the drawing-room.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 무심하게 말하고는, 눈썹을 치켜올린 채 응접실로 들어갔다."]], [["“How glad I am you have come,”", "\"와 주셔서 정말 기뻐요.\""], ["Dolly said with a frightened smile, meeting him in the outer drawing-room.", "돌리가 바깥 응접실에서 그를 맞으며 겁먹은 미소로 말했다."], ["“I must talk to you.", "\"당신과 이야기를 좀 해야겠어요."], ["Let’s sit here.”", "여기 앉아요.\""]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch, with the same expression of indifference, given him by his lifted eyebrows, sat down beside Darya Alexandrovna, and smiled affectedly.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 치켜올린 눈썹이 만들어 낸 그 무심한 표정 그대로, 다리아 알렉산드로브나 곁에 앉아 어색하게 미소 지었다."]], [["“It’s fortunate,”", "\"잘됐군요.\""], ["said he, “especially as I was meaning to ask you to excuse me, and to be taking leave.", "그가 말했다. \"마침 양해를 구하고 작별 인사를 드리려던 참이었으니까요."], ["I have to start tomorrow.”", "내일 떠나야 합니다.\""]], [["Darya Alexandrovna was firmly convinced of Anna’s innocence, and she felt herself growing pale and her lips quivering with anger at this frigid, unfeeling man, who was so calmly intending to ruin her innocent friend.", "다리아 알렉산드로브나는 안나가 결백하다고 굳게 믿고 있었으며, 자기의 죄 없는 친구를 이토록 태연히 파멸시키려 드는 이 냉정하고 무정한 남자에 대한 분노로 자기 얼굴이 창백해지고 입술이 떨리는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["“Alexey Alexandrovitch,”", "\"알렉세이 알렉산드로비치.\""], ["she said, with desperate resolution looking him in the face, “I asked you about Anna, you made me no answer.", "그녀가 필사적인 결의로 그의 얼굴을 똑바로 바라보며 말했다. \"제가 안나에 대해 여쭈었는데, 당신은 아무 대답도 해 주지 않으셨어요."], ["How is she?”", "그 애는 어떻게 지내나요?\""]], [["“She is, I believe, quite well, Darya Alexandrovna,”", "\"잘 지내는 것 같습니다, 다리아 알렉산드로브나.\""], ["replied Alexey Alexandrovitch, not looking at her.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 그녀를 보지도 않은 채 대답했다."]], [["“Alexey Alexandrovitch, forgive me, I have no right ...", "\"알렉세이 알렉산드로비치, 용서하세요, 제겐 그럴 권리가 없지만……"], ["but I love Anna as a sister, and esteem her; I beg, I beseech you to tell me what is wrong between you?", "그래도 저는 안나를 자매처럼 사랑하고 또 존경해요. 부탁드려요, 간곡히 부탁드리니, 두 분 사이에 무슨 일이 있었는지 말씀해 주세요."], ["what fault do you find with her?”", "그 애의 어떤 잘못을 탓하시는 건가요?\""]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch frowned, and almost closing his eyes, dropped his head.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 미간을 찌푸리고는 눈을 거의 감다시피 한 채 고개를 떨구었다."]], [["“I presume that your husband has told you the grounds on which I consider it necessary to change my attitude to Anna Arkadyevna?”", "\"내가 안나 아르카지예브나에 대한 태도를 바꿀 필요가 있다고 여기게 된 근거에 대해서는 부인의 남편 되시는 분이 이미 말씀하셨으리라 생각합니다만?\""], ["he said, not looking her in the face, but eyeing with displeasure Shtcherbatsky, who was walking across the drawing-room.", "그는 그녀의 얼굴을 보지 않은 채, 응접실을 가로질러 걸어가는 셰르바츠키를 못마땅한 눈길로 바라보며 말했다."]], [["“I don’t believe it, I don’t believe it, I can’t believe it!”", "\"믿을 수 없어요, 믿을 수 없어요, 그럴 리 없어요!\""], ["Dolly said, clasping her bony hands before her with a vigorous gesture.", "돌리는 앙상한 두 손을 앞으로 모아 힘껏 맞잡으며 말했다."], ["She rose quickly, and laid her hand on Alexey Alexandrovitch’s sleeve.", "그녀는 재빨리 일어나 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 소매에 손을 얹었다."], ["“We shall be disturbed here.", "\"여기서는 방해를 받겠어요."], ["Come this way, please.”", "이쪽으로 오세요, 제발.\""]], [["Dolly’s agitation had an effect on Alexey Alexandrovitch.", "돌리의 동요는 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에게 영향을 미쳤다."], ["He got up and submissively followed her to the schoolroom.", "그는 일어나 순순히 그녀를 따라 공부방으로 갔다."], ["They sat down to a table covered with an oilcloth cut in slits by penknives.", "두 사람은 주머니칼로 군데군데 베인 자국이 난 방수포가 덮인 탁자에 마주 앉았다."]], [["“I don’t, I don’t believe it!”", "\"믿어지지 않아요, 믿어지지 않아요!\""], ["Dolly said, trying to catch his glance that avoided her.", "돌리가 그녀를 피하는 그의 눈길을 붙잡으려 애쓰며 말했다."]], [["“One cannot disbelieve facts, Darya Alexandrovna,”", "\"사실을 믿지 않을 수는 없는 노릇이지요, 다리아 알렉산드로브나.\""], ["said he, with an emphasis on the word “facts.”", "그가 '사실'이라는 말에 힘을 주며 말했다."]], [["“But what has she done?”", "\"하지만 그 애가 무얼 했단 말인가요?\""], ["said Darya Alexandrovna.", "다리아 알렉산드로브나가 말했다."], ["“What precisely has she done?”", "\"도대체 무얼 했는데요?\""]], [["“She has forsaken her duty, and deceived her husband.", "\"제 의무를 저버리고 남편을 속였습니다."], ["That’s what she has done,”", "그게 그 사람이 한 짓입니다.\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“No, no, it can’t be!", "\"아니, 아니에요, 그럴 리 없어요!"], ["No, for God’s sake, you are mistaken,”", "아니에요, 제발, 당신이 잘못 아신 거예요.\""], ["said Dolly, putting her hands to her temples and closing her eyes.", "돌리가 두 손을 관자놀이에 갖다 대고 눈을 감으며 말했다."]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch smiled coldly, with his lips alone, meaning to signify to her and to himself the firmness of his conviction; but this warm defense, though it could not shake him, reopened his wound.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 자기 확신의 굳건함을 그녀에게도 자기 자신에게도 알리려는 듯, 입술만으로 차갑게 미소 지었다. 그러나 이 뜨거운 변호는 그를 흔들어 놓지는 못했어도 그의 상처를 다시 헤집었다."], ["He began to speak with greater heat.", "그는 한층 격앙된 어조로 말하기 시작했다."]], [["“It is extremely difficult to be mistaken when a wife herself informs her husband of the fact—informs him that eight years of her life, and a son, all that’s a mistake, and that she wants to begin life again,”", "\"아내가 직접 남편에게 그 사실을 알릴 때—제 인생의 팔 년 세월도, 아들도, 그 모든 게 다 잘못이었으며, 인생을 새로 시작하고 싶다고 알릴 때, 잘못 안다는 건 지극히 어려운 일이지요.\""], ["he said angrily, with a snort.", "그는 화가 나서 콧방귀를 뀌며 말했다."]], [["“Anna and sin—I cannot connect them, I cannot believe it!”", "\"안나와 죄라니—저는 그 둘을 연결시킬 수가 없어요, 믿을 수가 없어요!\""]], [["“Darya Alexandrovna,”", "\"다리아 알렉산드로브나.\" 그가 말했다."], ["he said, now looking straight into Dolly’s kindly, troubled face, and feeling that his tongue was being loosened in spite of himself, “I would give a great deal for doubt to be still possible.", "이제 그는 돌리의 다정하고 근심 어린 얼굴을 똑바로 바라보았고, 자기 의지와는 상관없이 혀가 풀려 가는 것을 느꼈다. \"의심이라도 아직 가능하다면 저는 무엇이든 내놓겠습니다."], ["When I doubted, I was miserable, but it was better than now.", "의심하던 때 저는 비참했지만, 그래도 지금보다는 나았어요."], ["When I doubted, I had hope; but now there is no hope, and still I doubt of everything.", "의심하던 때는 희망이 있었지요. 그러나 지금은 희망이 없는데도 여전히 모든 것을 의심합니다."], ["I am in such doubt of everything that I even hate my son, and sometimes do not believe he is my son.", "모든 것을 어찌나 의심하는지, 저는 제 아들마저 미워하고, 때로는 그 애가 제 아들이라는 것조차 믿지 못합니다."], ["I am very unhappy.”", "저는 정말 불행한 사람입니다.\""]], [["He had no need to say that.", "그는 그런 말을 할 필요가 없었다."], ["Darya Alexandrovna had seen that as soon as he glanced into her face; and she felt sorry for him, and her faith in the innocence of her friend began to totter.", "다리아 알렉산드로브나는 그가 자기 얼굴을 들여다본 순간 그것을 보아 버렸다. 그래서 그녀는 그가 가엾어졌고, 친구의 결백에 대한 믿음이 흔들리기 시작했다."]], [["“Oh, this is awful, awful!", "\"오, 이건 끔찍해요, 끔찍해요!"], ["But can it be true that you are resolved on a divorce?”", "그런데 정말로 이혼을 결심하셨다는 게 사실인가요?\""]], [["“I am resolved on extreme measures.", "\"극단적인 조치를 결심했습니다."], ["There is nothing else for me to do.”", "제게는 달리 할 수 있는 일이 없어요.\""]], [["“Nothing else to do, nothing else to do....”", "\"달리 할 일이 없다, 달리 할 일이 없다고요……\""], ["she replied, with tears in her eyes.", "그녀가 눈물을 글썽이며 대답했다."], ["“Oh no, don’t say nothing else to do!”", "\"오, 아니에요, 달리 할 일이 없다는 말씀은 하지 마세요!\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“What is horrible in a trouble of this kind is that one cannot, as in any other—in loss, in death—bear one’s trouble in peace, but that one must act,”", "\"이런 종류의 괴로움에서 끔찍한 것은, 다른 어떤 경우처럼—상실이나 죽음에서처럼—자기 괴로움을 가만히 견딜 수 없고, 반드시 행동해야 한다는 점입니다.\""], ["said he, as though guessing her thought.", "그가 그녀의 생각을 헤아리기라도 한 듯 말했다."], ["“One must get out of the humiliating position in which one is placed; one can’t live à trois.”", "\"자기가 놓인 굴욕적인 처지에서 벗어나야만 하는 거지요. 셋이서 함께 살 수는 없으니까요.\""]], [["“I understand, I quite understand that,”", "* à trois: 프랑스어로 '셋이서'."], ["said Dolly, and her head sank.", "\"이해해요, 충분히 이해해요.\""], ["She was silent for a little, thinking of herself, of her own grief in her family, and all at once, with an impulsive movement, she raised her head and clasped her hands with an imploring gesture.", "돌리는 말하며 고개를 떨구었다. 그녀는 잠시 잠자코, 자기 자신의 일을, 가정에서 겪은 자기 자신의 슬픔을 생각하다가, 별안간 충동적인 몸짓으로 고개를 들어 애원하는 동작으로 두 손을 맞잡았다."], ["“But wait a little!", "\"하지만 조금만 기다려 보세요!"], ["You are a Christian.", "당신은 기독교인이잖아요."], ["Think of her!", "그 애를 생각해 보세요!"], ["What will become of her, if you cast her off?”", "당신이 그 애를 내쳐 버리면, 그 애는 어떻게 되겠어요?\""]], [["“I have thought, Darya Alexandrovna, I have thought a great deal,”", "\"저도 생각해 봤습니다, 다리아 알렉산드로브나, 아주 많이 생각해 봤어요.\""], ["said Alexey Alexandrovitch.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 말했다."], ["His face turned red in patches, and his dim eyes looked straight before him.", "그의 얼굴은 군데군데 붉게 물들었고, 흐릿한 두 눈은 앞쪽을 똑바로 바라보았다."], ["Darya Alexandrovna at that moment pitied him with all her heart.", "그 순간 다리아 알렉산드로브나는 진심으로 그가 가엾었다."], ["“That was what I did indeed when she herself made known to me my humiliation; I left everything as of old.", "\"그 사람이 직접 제 굴욕을 제게 알렸을 때 저는 정말이지 그렇게 했습니다. 모든 것을 예전 그대로 두었지요."], ["I gave her a chance to reform, I tried to save her.", "저는 그 사람에게 고칠 기회를 주었고, 구해 보려고 애썼습니다."], ["And with what result?", "그런데 그 결과가 어땠습니까?"], ["She would not regard the slightest request—that she should observe decorum,”", "그 사람은 가장 사소한 부탁조차—체면만은 지켜 달라는 부탁조차 들어주지 않았어요.\""], ["he said, getting heated.", "그가 점점 격해지며 말했다."], ["“One may save anyone who does not want to be ruined; but if the whole nature is so corrupt, so depraved, that ruin itself seems to be her salvation, what’s to be done?”", "\"파멸하기를 원하지 않는 사람은 누구든 구할 수 있습니다. 그러나 천성 전체가 너무도 부패하고 타락해서 파멸 그 자체가 도리어 구원으로 여겨질 지경이라면, 무엇을 어찌하겠습니까?\""]], [["“Anything, only not divorce!”", "\"무엇이든 좋아요, 다만 이혼만은 안 돼요!\""], ["answered Darya Alexandrovna\n“But what is anything?”", "다리아 알렉산드로브나가 대답했다."]], [["“No, it is awful! She will be no one’s wife, she will be lost!”", "\"하지만 무엇이든이라니, 그게 뭡니까?\""]], [["“What can I do?”", "\"안 돼요, 그건 끔찍해요!"], ["said Alexey Alexandrovitch, raising his shoulders and his eyebrows.", "그 애는 누구의 아내도 아닌 신세가 되어 버릴 거예요, 그 애는 파멸하고 말 거예요!\" \"제가 무얼 할 수 있겠습니까?\""], ["The recollection of his wife’s last act had so incensed him that he had become frigid, as at the beginning of the conversation.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 어깨와 눈썹을 치켜올리며 말했다. 아내의 마지막 행동을 떠올리자 그는 어찌나 화가 났던지 대화를 시작할 때처럼 다시 냉랭해졌다."], ["“I am very grateful for your sympathy, but I must be going,”", "\"동정해 주셔서 정말 감사합니다만, 이제 가 봐야겠습니다.\""], ["he said, getting up.", "그가 일어나며 말했다."]], [["“No, wait a minute.", "\"아니에요, 잠깐만요."], ["You must not ruin her.", "그 애를 파멸시켜서는 안 돼요."], ["Wait a little; I will tell you about myself.", "조금만 기다려 보세요. 제 이야기를 들려드릴게요."], ["I was married, and my husband deceived me; in anger and jealousy, I would have thrown up everything, I would myself....", "저는 결혼했고, 제 남편은 저를 속였어요. 분노와 질투에 사로잡혀 저는 모든 것을 내던지려 했고, 저 스스로……"], ["But I came to myself again; and who did it?", "그렇지만 다시 정신을 차렸어요. 그런데 누가 그렇게 해 주었을까요?"], ["Anna saved me.", "안나가 저를 구해 주었어요."], ["And here I am living on.", "그래서 저는 이렇게 살아가고 있는 거예요."], ["The children are growing up, my husband has come back to his family, and feels his fault, is growing purer, better, and I live on....", "아이들은 자라고, 남편은 가정으로 돌아왔고, 자기 잘못을 느끼며 점점 더 맑아지고 나아지고 있어요. 그리고 저는 이렇게 살아가고 있고요……"], ["I have forgiven it, and you ought to forgive!”", "저는 용서했어요, 그러니 당신도 용서하셔야 해요!\""]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch heard her, but her words had no effect on him now.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 그녀의 말을 들었지만, 이제 그 말은 그에게 아무런 영향도 미치지 못했다."], ["All the hatred of that day when he had resolved on a divorce had sprung up again in his soul.", "이혼을 결심하던 그날의 그 모든 증오가 그의 영혼 속에서 다시 솟구쳐 올랐다."], ["He shook himself, and said in a shrill, loud voice:", "그는 몸을 부르르 떨더니 날카롭고 큰 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“Forgive I cannot, and do not wish to, and I regard it as wrong.", "\"용서할 수 없고, 용서하고 싶지도 않으며, 그것을 옳지 않은 일이라 여깁니다."], ["I have done everything for this woman, and she has trodden it all in the mud to which she is akin.", "저는 그 여자를 위해 모든 것을 다했는데, 그 여자는 그 모든 것을 자기와 어울리는 진창 속에 짓밟아 버렸어요."], ["I am not a spiteful man, I have never hated anyone, but I hate her with my whole soul, and I cannot even forgive her, because I hate her too much for all the wrong she has done me!”", "저는 악의에 찬 사람이 아니고, 누구도 미워해 본 적이 없습니다만, 그 여자만은 영혼을 다해 미워하며, 그 여자를 용서조차 할 수 없습니다. 그 여자가 제게 저지른 모든 잘못 때문에 그 여자를 너무나도 미워하니까요!\""], ["he said, with tones of hatred in his voice.", "그가 증오에 찬 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“Love those that hate you....”", "\"너희를 미워하는 자들을 사랑하라……\""], ["Darya Alexandrovna whispered timorously.", "다리아 알렉산드로브나가 겁먹은 듯 속삭였다."]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch smiled contemptuously.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 경멸하듯 미소 지었다."], ["That he knew long ago, but it could not be applied to his case.", "그것은 그가 오래전부터 알던 말이었지만, 자기 경우에는 적용될 수 없었다."]], [["“Love those that hate you, but to love those one hates is impossible.", "\"너희를 미워하는 자들을 사랑하라고는 하지만, 자기가 미워하는 자를 사랑하기란 불가능합니다."], ["Forgive me for having troubled you.", "폐를 끼쳐 죄송합니다."], ["Everyone has enough to bear in his own grief!”", "누구나 자기 자신의 슬픔만으로도 견뎌 내야 할 짐이 충분하니까요!\""], ["And regaining his self-possession, Alexey Alexandrovitch quietly took leave and went away.", "그러고는 평정을 되찾은 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 조용히 작별 인사를 하고 떠나갔다."]], [["# Chapter 13", "# Chapter 13"]], [["When they rose from table, Levin would have liked to follow Kitty into the drawing-room; but he was afraid she might dislike this, as too obviously paying her attention.", "식탁에서 일어났을 때, 레빈은 키티를 따라 응접실로 들어가고 싶었지만, 너무 노골적으로 그녀에게 마음을 쓰는 것처럼 보여 그녀가 싫어할까 봐 두려웠다."], ["He remained in the little ring of men, taking part in the general conversation, and without looking at Kitty, he was aware of her movements, her looks, and the place where she was in the drawing-room.", "그는 사내들의 작은 무리 속에 남아 전체 대화에 끼어 있었고, 키티를 쳐다보지 않으면서도 그녀의 움직임이며 시선이며 응접실 안에서 그녀가 있는 자리를 알아차리고 있었다."]], [["He did at once, and without the smallest effort, keep the promise he had made her—always to think well of all men, and to like everyone always.", "그는 곧바로, 조금의 노력도 들이지 않고, 그녀에게 한 약속을 지켰다. 늘 모든 사람을 좋게 생각하고 언제나 누구든 좋아하겠다는 약속이었다."], ["The conversation fell on the village commune, in which Pestsov saw a sort of special principle, called by him the “choral”", "대화는 농촌 공동체로 흘러갔는데, 페스초프는 거기서 일종의 특별한 원리, 그가 \"합창적\" 원리라고 부르는 것을 보았다."], ["principle. Levin did not agree with Pestsov, nor with his brother, who had a special attitude of his own, both admitting and not admitting the significance of the Russian commune.", "레빈은 페스초프의 생각에도, 또 러시아 공동체의 의의를 인정하면서도 인정하지 않는 자기만의 독특한 태도를 지닌 형의 생각에도 동의하지 않았다."], ["But he talked to them, simply trying to reconcile and soften their differences.", "하지만 그는 그들과 이야기를 나누며, 그저 그들의 견해 차이를 화해시키고 누그러뜨리려 애썼다."], ["He was not in the least interested in what he said himself, and even less so in what they said; all he wanted was that they and everyone should be happy and contented.", "그는 자기가 하는 말에도 조금도 흥미가 없었고, 그들이 하는 말에는 더더욱 흥미가 없었다. 그가 바라는 것은 오직 그들이며 모든 사람이 행복하고 만족스러워하는 것뿐이었다."], ["He knew now the one thing of importance; and that one thing was at first there, in the drawing-room, and then began moving across and came to a standstill at the door.", "그는 이제 중요한 단 한 가지를 알고 있었다. 그리고 그 한 가지는 처음에는 저쪽 응접실에 있었으나, 이윽고 움직이기 시작해 문가에 와서 멈춰 섰다."], ["Without turning round he felt the eyes fixed on him, and the smile, and he could not help turning round.", "돌아보지 않고도 그는 자기에게 못 박힌 시선과 미소를 느꼈고, 돌아보지 않을 수 없었다."], ["She was standing in the doorway with Shtcherbatsky, looking at him.", "그녀는 셰르바츠키와 함께 문간에 서서 그를 바라보고 있었다."]], [["“I thought you were going towards the piano,”", "\"피아노 쪽으로 가시는 줄 알았어요.\""], ["said he, going up to her.", "그는 그녀에게 다가가며 말했다."], ["“That’s something I miss in the country—music.”", "\"음악, 그게 시골에서 제가 아쉬워하는 거랍니다.\""]], [["“No; we only came to fetch you and thank you,”", "\"아니에요. 그냥 당신을 모시러, 또 와 주셔서 감사하다는 인사를 드리러 온 거예요.\""], ["she said, rewarding him with a smile that was like a gift, “for coming.", "그녀는 선물 같은 미소로 그에게 보답하며 말했다."], ["What do they want to argue for?", "\"다들 뭣 때문에 그렇게 논쟁을 하는 걸까요?"], ["No one ever convinces anyone, you know.”", "아무도 누구를 설득하지 못하는데 말이에요.\""]], [["“Yes; that’s true,”", "\"그래요, 맞는 말입니다.\""], ["said Levin; “it generally happens that one argues warmly simply because one can’t make out what one’s opponent wants to prove.”", "레빈이 말했다. \"보통은 그냥 상대가 뭘 증명하려는지 도무지 알아낼 수 없기 때문에 열을 올려 논쟁을 하게 되지요.\""]], [["Levin had often noticed in discussions between the most intelligent people that after enormous efforts, and an enormous expenditure of logical subtleties and words, the disputants finally arrived at being aware that what they had so long been struggling to prove to one another had long ago, from the beginning of the argument, been known to both, but that they liked different things, and would not define what they liked for fear of its being attacked.", "레빈은 가장 영리한 사람들 사이의 토론에서도 흔히 이런 일이 벌어진다는 것을 자주 보아 왔다. 엄청난 노력을 기울이고, 논리적 정교함과 말을 어마어마하게 쏟아부은 끝에, 논쟁하던 이들은 마침내 자기들이 그토록 오랫동안 서로에게 증명하려 애써 온 것이 사실은 논쟁이 시작될 때부터 양쪽 모두 이미 오래전에 알고 있던 것이며, 다만 서로 좋아하는 것이 다를 뿐인데, 그 좋아하는 것이 공격당할까 두려워 그것을 분명히 밝히지 않으려 한다는 사실을 깨닫게 된다는 것이다."], ["He had often had the experience of suddenly in a discussion grasping what it was his opponent liked and at once liking it too, and immediately he found himself agreeing, and then all arguments fell away as useless.", "그는 토론 도중에 문득 상대가 좋아하는 것이 무엇인지 알아채고 자기도 곧장 그것을 좋아하게 되어, 그러자 즉시 동의하게 되고 모든 논쟁이 부질없는 것이 되어 무너져 내리는 경험을 자주 했다."], ["Sometimes, too, he had experienced the opposite, expressing at last what he liked himself, which he was devising arguments to defend, and, chancing to express it well and genuinely, he had found his opponent at once agreeing and ceasing to dispute his position.", "또 때로는 그 반대의 경험도 했다. 자기가 좋아하는 것을, 그것을 옹호하려고 온갖 논거를 궁리하던 그것을 마침내 입 밖에 냈는데, 우연히 그것을 잘 그리고 진솔하게 표현하게 되면, 상대가 곧바로 동의하며 자기 입장을 두고 더는 다투지 않게 되는 것이었다."], ["He tried to say this.", "그는 이 이야기를 하려고 했다."]], [["She knitted her brow, trying to understand.", "그녀는 이해하려 애쓰며 미간을 찌푸렸다."], ["But directly he began to illustrate his meaning, she understood at once.", "하지만 그가 자기 뜻을 예를 들어 설명하기 시작하자, 그녀는 곧바로 알아들었다."]], [["“I know: one must find out what he is arguing for, what is precious to him, then one can....”", "\"알겠어요. 그가 무엇 때문에 논쟁하는지, 그에게 소중한 게 무엇인지 먼저 알아내야 한다는 거죠. 그러면 그제야……\""]], [["She had completely guessed and expressed his badly expressed idea.", "그녀는 그가 서툴게 표현한 생각을 완전히 알아맞히고 또렷이 말로 옮겼다."], ["Levin smiled joyfully; he was struck by this transition from the confused, verbose discussion with Pestsov and his brother to this laconic, clear, almost wordless communication of the most complex ideas.", "레빈은 기쁨에 차 미소 지었다. 페스초프며 형과 나눈 그 혼란스럽고 장황한 토론에서, 가장 복잡한 생각이 이렇듯 간결하고 명료하게, 거의 말 없이도 전해지는 소통으로 옮겨 온 것에 그는 놀랐다."]], [["Shtcherbatsky moved away from them, and Kitty, going up to a card-table, sat down, and, taking up the chalk, began drawing diverging circles over the new green cloth.", "셰르바츠키가 그들에게서 멀어졌고, 키티는 카드 탁자로 다가가 자리에 앉더니 분필을 집어 들고 새 초록색 천 위에 갈라지며 퍼져 나가는 원들을 그리기 시작했다."]], [["They began again on the subject that had been started at dinner—the liberty and occupations of women.", "그들은 만찬 자리에서 시작되었던 화제로 다시 돌아갔다. 여성의 자유와 일거리에 관한 이야기였다."], ["Levin was of the opinion of Darya Alexandrovna that a girl who did not marry should find a woman’s duties in a family.", "레빈은 결혼하지 않은 처녀라면 한 가정에서 여자로서의 의무를 찾아야 한다는 다리야 알렉산드로브나의 의견에 동의했다."], ["He supported this view by the fact that no family can get on without women to help; that in every family, poor or rich, there are and must be nurses, either relations or hired.", "그는 여자들의 도움 없이는 어떤 가정도 꾸려 나갈 수 없다는 사실, 가난하든 부유하든 모든 가정에는 친척이든 고용인이든 유모가 있고 또 있어야만 한다는 사실로 이 견해를 뒷받침했다."]], [["“No,”", "\"아니에요.\" 키티가 얼굴을 붉히면서도 그 진실한 눈으로 한층 더 대담하게 그를 바라보며 말했다."], ["said Kitty, blushing, but looking at him all the more boldly with her truthful eyes; “a girl may be so circumstanced that she cannot live in the family without humiliation, while she herself....”", "\"어떤 처녀는 그 가정 안에서 굴욕을 겪지 않고는 살 수 없는 처지에 놓일 수도 있어요. 그런데 그녀 자신은……\""]], [["At the hint he understood her.", "그 말의 암시만으로 그는 그녀를 알아들었다."]], [["“Oh, yes,”", "\"오, 그래요.\" 그가 말했다."], ["he said.", "\"그래요, 그래요, 그래요."], ["“Yes, yes, yes—you’re right; you’re right!”", "당신 말이 맞아요. 맞아요!\""]], [["And he saw all that Pestsov had been maintaining at dinner of the liberty of woman, simply from getting a glimpse of the terror of an old maid’s existence and its humiliation in Kitty’s heart; and loving her, he felt that terror and humiliation, and at once gave up his arguments.", "그리고 그는, 만찬 자리에서 페스초프가 여성의 자유에 대해 주장하던 모든 것을, 그저 노처녀의 삶이 지닌 공포와 그 굴욕을 키티의 마음속에서 언뜻 엿보는 것만으로 다 이해하게 되었다. 그리고 그녀를 사랑하는 까닭에, 그는 그 공포와 굴욕을 느꼈고 곧바로 자기 논거를 거두어들였다."]], [["A silence followed.", "침묵이 뒤따랐다."], ["She was still drawing with the chalk on the table.", "그녀는 여전히 분필로 탁자에 무언가를 그리고 있었다."], ["Her eyes were shining with a soft light.", "그녀의 눈은 부드러운 빛으로 반짝였다."], ["Under the influence of her mood he felt in all his being a continually growing tension of happiness.", "그녀의 기분에 이끌려, 그는 자기 온몸에서 행복의 긴장이 끊임없이 부풀어 오르는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["“Ah!", "\"어머!"], ["I’ve scribbled all over the table!”", "탁자를 온통 휘갈겨 놓았네요!\""], ["she said, and, laying down the chalk, she made a movement as though to get up.", "그녀가 말하고는 분필을 내려놓고 일어서려는 듯한 동작을 했다."]], [["“What!", "\"뭐라고!"], ["shall I be left alone—without her?”", "그럼 나 혼자 남겨지는 건가, 그녀 없이?\""], ["he thought with horror, and he took the chalk.", "그는 공포에 사로잡혀 생각하며 분필을 집어 들었다."], ["“Wait a minute,”", "\"잠깐만요.\""], ["he said, sitting down to the table.", "그는 탁자 앞에 앉으며 말했다."], ["“I’ve long wanted to ask you one thing.”", "\"오래전부터 당신에게 한 가지 물어보고 싶은 게 있었어요.\""]], [["He looked straight into her caressing, though frightened eyes.", "그는 다정하지만 겁먹은 그녀의 두 눈을 똑바로 들여다보았다."]], [["“Please, ask it.”", "\"물어보세요, 어서요.\""]], [["“Here,”", "\"여기요.\""], ["he said; and he wrote the initial letters, w, y, t, m, i, c, n, b, d, t, m, n, o, t.", "그가 말하고는 머리글자들을 적어 나갔다. ㄱ, ㄷ, ㅂ, ㅅ, ㅇ, ㅅ, ㅇ, ㄱ, ㅇ, ㄱ, ㅇ, ㄱ, ㅇ, ㄴ, ㄱㅇ."], ["These letters meant, “When you told me it could never be, did that mean never, or then?”", "이 글자들은 이런 뜻이었다. \"그게 도무지 불가능한 일이라고 당신이 내게 말했을 때, 그건 영영 그렇다는 거였나요, 아니면 그때 그랬다는 건가요?\""], ["There seemed no likelihood that she could make out this complicated sentence; but he looked at her as though his life depended on her understanding the words.", "그녀가 이렇게 복잡한 문장을 알아낼 가능성은 전혀 없어 보였다. 하지만 그는 마치 그녀가 그 글자들을 이해하는지에 자기 목숨이 걸리기라도 한 듯 그녀를 바라보았다."], ["She glanced at him seriously, then leaned her puckered brow on her hands and began to read.", "그녀는 그를 진지하게 흘끗 쳐다보더니, 찌푸린 이마를 두 손에 기대고 읽기 시작했다."], ["Once or twice she stole a look at him, as though asking him, “Is it what I think?”", "한두 번 그녀는 마치 \"이게 내가 생각하는 그건가요?\" 하고 묻듯이 그를 몰래 쳐다보았다."]], [["“I understand,”", "\"알겠어요.\""], ["she said, flushing a little.", "그녀가 살짝 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다."]], [["“What is this word?”", "\"이 글자는 뭐죠?\""], ["he said, pointing to the n that stood for never.", "그는 \"영영\"을 뜻하는 ㅇ을 가리키며 말했다."]], [["“It means never,”", "\"그건 영영이라는 뜻이에요.\""], ["she said; “but that’s not true!”", "그녀가 말했다. \"하지만 그건 사실이 아니에요!\""]], [["He quickly rubbed out what he had written, gave her the chalk, and stood up.", "그는 자기가 쓴 것을 재빨리 지우고 그녀에게 분필을 건넨 뒤 일어섰다. 그녀는 적었다."], ["She wrote, t, i, c, n, a, d.", "ㄱ, ㄸ, ㄴ, ㄷ, ㄷㄹ, ㄷ."]], [["Dolly was completely comforted in the depression caused by her conversation with Alexey Alexandrovitch when she caught sight of the two figures: Kitty with the chalk in her hand, with a shy and happy smile looking upwards at Levin, and his handsome figure bending over the table with glowing eyes fastened one minute on the table and the next on her.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치와 나눈 대화로 가라앉아 있던 우울한 기분에서, 돌리는 그 두 사람의 모습을 보고는 완전히 위안을 얻었다. 분필을 손에 든 키티는 수줍고도 행복한 미소를 지으며 레빈을 올려다보고 있었고, 그의 잘생긴 모습은 탁자 위로 몸을 숙인 채 이글거리는 눈으로 한 순간은 탁자를, 다음 순간은 그녀를 응시하고 있었다."], ["He was suddenly radiant: he had understood.", "그는 별안간 환하게 빛났다. 알아낸 것이다."], ["It meant, “Then I could not answer differently.”", "그것은 \"그때는 달리 대답할 수 없었어요\"라는 뜻이었다."]], [["He glanced at her questioningly, timidly. “Only then?”", "그는 머뭇거리며 미심쩍은 눈으로 그녀를 흘끗 보았다."]], [["“Yes,” her smile answered.", "\"그때만 그랬다는 건가요?\""]], [["“And n...", "\"네.\""], ["and now?” he asked.", "그녀의 미소가 대답했다."]], [["“Well, read this. I’ll tell you what I should like—should like so much!” she wrote the initial letters, i, y, c, f, a, f, w, h. This meant, “If you could forget and forgive what happened.”", "\"그럼 지…… 그럼 지금은요?\" 그가 물었다. \"자, 이걸 읽어 보세요. 제가 바라는 걸 말씀드릴게요. 정말 간절히 바라는 걸요!\" 그녀는 머리글자들을 적었다. ㅈ, ㄴ, ㅇ, ㅇ, ㅇ, ㅇ, ㅈㅇ, ㅁ. 이것은 \"지난 일을 잊고 용서해 주실 수 있다면\"이라는 뜻이었다."]], [["He snatched the chalk with nervous, trembling fingers, and breaking it, wrote the initial letters of the following phrase, “I have nothing to forget and to forgive; I have never ceased to love you.”", "그는 떨리는 손가락으로 신경질적으로 분필을 낚아채 그것을 부러뜨리면서, 다음 문장의 머리글자들을 적었다. \"저는 잊거나 용서할 것이 아무것도 없어요. 저는 단 한 번도 당신을 사랑하기를 멈춘 적이 없습니다.\""]], [["She glanced at him with a smile that did not waver.", "그녀는 흔들림 없는 미소로 그를 흘끗 보았다."]], [["“I understand,”", "\"알겠어요.\""], ["she said in a whisper.", "그녀가 속삭이듯 말했다."]], [["He sat down and wrote a long phrase.", "그는 자리에 앉아 긴 문장을 적었다."], ["She understood it all, and without asking him, “Is it this?”", "그녀는 그것을 모두 이해했고, 그에게 \"이게 맞나요?\""], ["took the chalk and at once answered.", "하고 묻지도 않은 채 분필을 집어 곧바로 답을 적었다."]], [["For a long while he could not understand what she had written, and often looked into her eyes.", "오랫동안 그는 그녀가 적은 것을 이해하지 못했고, 자꾸만 그녀의 눈을 들여다보았다."], ["He was stupefied with happiness.", "그는 행복에 멍해져 있었다."], ["He could not supply the word she had meant; but in her charming eyes, beaming with happiness, he saw all he needed to know.", "그녀가 뜻한 단어를 채워 넣을 수가 없었다. 하지만 행복으로 빛나는 그녀의 사랑스러운 눈동자 속에서, 그는 자기가 알아야 할 모든 것을 보았다."], ["And he wrote three letters.", "그래서 그는 세 글자를 적었다."], ["But he had hardly finished writing when she read them over her arm, and herself finished and wrote the answer, “Yes.”", "그런데 미처 다 적기도 전에 그녀는 그의 팔 너머로 그것을 읽고, 손수 마저 채워 답을 적었다. \"네.\""]], [["“You’re playing secrétaire?”", "\"비밀 편지 놀이라도 하는 게냐?\""], ["said the old prince.", "노공작이 말했다."], ["“But we must really be getting along if you want to be in time at the theater.”", "\"한데 극장에 늦지 않으려면 정말 슬슬 가 봐야겠구나.\""]], [["Levin got up and escorted Kitty to the door.", "레빈은 일어나 키티를 문까지 바래다주었다."]], [["In their conversation everything had been said; it had been said that she loved him, and that she would tell her father and mother that he would come tomorrow morning.", "그들의 대화에서는 모든 것이 다 이야기된 셈이었다. 그녀가 그를 사랑한다는 것도, 그리고 그가 내일 아침 찾아올 것이라고 그녀가 아버지와 어머니에게 말하리라는 것도, 모두 이야기된 것이다."]], [["# Chapter 14", "# Chapter 14"]], [["When Kitty had gone and Levin was left alone, he felt such uneasiness without her, and such an impatient longing to get as quickly, as quickly as possible, to tomorrow morning, when he would see her again and be plighted to her forever, that he felt afraid, as though of death, of those fourteen hours that he had to get through without her.", "키티가 떠나고 레빈만 홀로 남게 되자, 그는 그녀가 없는 데서 견디기 힘든 불안과, 그녀를 다시 보고 영원히 그녀와 약혼하게 될 내일 아침으로 어떻게든 한시바삐, 한시바삐 다다르고 싶은 초조한 갈망을 느꼈고, 그녀 없이 보내야 할 그 열네 시간이 마치 죽음처럼 두렵게 느껴졌다."], ["It was essential for him to be with someone to talk to, so as not to be left alone, to kill time.", "그에게는 혼자 남지 않도록, 시간을 죽이도록, 누군가와 함께 이야기를 나누는 것이 꼭 필요했다."], ["Stepan Arkadyevitch would have been the companion most congenial to him, but he was going out, he said, to a soirée, in reality to the ballet.", "스테판 아르카지치야말로 가장 마음에 맞는 길동무였을 테지만, 그는 야회에 간다고 했고, 실은 발레를 보러 가는 길이었다."], ["Levin only had time to tell him he was happy, and that he loved him, and would never, never forget what he had done for him.", "레빈은 다만 그에게 자기가 행복하다고, 그를 사랑한다고, 그가 자기를 위해 해 준 일을 결코, 결코 잊지 않겠다고 말할 짬밖에 없었다."], ["The eyes and the smile of Stepan Arkadyevitch showed Levin that he comprehended that feeling fittingly.", "스테판 아르카지치의 눈과 미소는, 그가 그 감정을 알맞게 이해하고 있음을 레빈에게 보여 주었다."]], [["“Oh, so it’s not time to die yet?”", "\"오, 그럼 아직 죽을 때는 아닌 거로군?\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, pressing Levin’s hand with emotion.", "스테판 아르카지치가 감격에 겨워 레빈의 손을 꼭 쥐며 말했다."]], [["“N-n-no!”", "\"아-아-니!\""], ["said Levin.", "레빈이 말했다."]], [["Darya Alexandrovna too, as she said good-bye to him, gave him a sort of congratulation, saying, “How glad I am you have met Kitty again!", "다리아 알렉산드로브나 역시 그에게 작별 인사를 하면서, \"키티를 다시 만나게 되셔서 정말 기뻐요! 오랜 친구는 소중히 여겨야 하는 법이죠.\""], ["One must value old friends.”", "하고 일종의 축하 같은 말을 건넸다."], ["Levin did not like these words of Darya Alexandrovna’s.", "레빈은 다리아 알렉산드로브나의 이런 말이 마음에 들지 않았다."], ["She could not understand how lofty and beyond her it all was, and she ought not to have dared to allude to it.", "그녀로서는 이 모든 것이 얼마나 숭고하고 자기 따위가 헤아릴 수 없는 것인지 이해할 수 없을 테고, 또 감히 그것에 대해 입을 떼서도 안 되는 것이었다."], ["Levin said good-bye to them, but, not to be left alone, he attached himself to his brother.", "레빈은 그들에게 작별을 고했지만, 혼자 남지 않으려고 형에게 들러붙었다."]], [["“Where are you going?”", "\"어디 가세요?\""]], [["“I’m going to a meeting.”", "\"회의에 가는 길이다.\""]], [["“Well, I’ll come with you.", "\"그럼 저도 같이 가요."], ["May I?”", "그래도 돼요?\""]], [["“What for?", "\"뭐 하러?"], ["Yes, come along,”", "그래, 따라오너라.\""], ["said Sergey Ivanovitch, smiling.", "세르게이 이바노비치가 웃으며 말했다."], ["“What is the matter with you today?”", "\"너 오늘 무슨 일 있느냐?\""]], [["“With me?", "\"저요?"], ["Happiness is the matter with me!”", "행복이 무슨 일이죠!\""], ["said Levin, letting down the window of the carriage they were driving in.", "레빈이 그들이 타고 가던 마차의 창문을 내리며 말했다."], ["“You don’t mind?—it’s so stifling.", "\"괜찮으시죠?—너무 답답해서요."], ["It’s happiness is the matter with me!", "행복이 무슨 일이라고요!"], ["Why is it you have never married?”", "형은 왜 한 번도 결혼을 안 하셨어요?\""]], [["Sergey Ivanovitch smiled.", "세르게이 이바노비치가 미소 지었다."]], [["“I am very glad, she seems a nice gi....”", "\"나야 아주 기쁘다, 그 아가씨도 참한 처녀…\""], ["Sergey Ivanovitch was beginning.", "세르게이 이바노비치가 말을 시작했다."]], [["“Don’t say it!", "\"그 말씀은 마세요!"], ["don’t say it!”", "마세요!\""], ["shouted Levin, clutching at the collar of his fur coat with both hands, and muffling him up in it.", "레빈이 두 손으로 형의 모피 외투 깃을 움켜쥐고 그 속에 얼굴을 파묻게 하며 소리쳤다."], ["“She’s a nice girl” were such simple, humble words, so out of harmony with his feeling.", "\"참한 처녀\"라는 말은 너무도 단순하고 너무도 보잘것없는 말이어서, 그의 감정과는 도무지 어울리지 않았다."]], [["Sergey Ivanovitch laughed outright a merry laugh, which was rare with him.", "세르게이 이바노비치가, 그에게는 드문 일인, 유쾌하게 큰 소리로 껄껄 웃었다."], ["“Well, anyway, I may say that I’m very glad of it.”", "\"어쨌든, 내가 이 일을 무척 기뻐한다는 것만은 말해도 되겠지.\""]], [["“That you may do tomorrow, tomorrow and nothing more!", "\"그건 내일, 내일 하시고 더는 안 돼요!"], ["Nothing, nothing, silence,”", "아무것도, 아무것도, 입 다무세요.\""], ["said Levin, and muffling him once more in his fur coat, he added: “I do like you so!", "레빈은 이렇게 말하고 다시 한번 형을 모피 외투 속에 파묻게 하며 덧붙였다."], ["Well, is it possible for me to be present at the meeting?”", "\"전 형을 정말 좋아해요! 그런데, 제가 회의에 참석해도 될까요?\""]], [["“Of course it is.”", "\"물론 되고말고.\""]], [["“What is your discussion about today?”", "\"오늘은 무슨 토론을 하시는데요?\""], ["asked Levin, never ceasing smiling.", "레빈이 미소를 거두지 못한 채 물었다."]], [["They arrived at the meeting.", "그들은 회의장에 도착했다."], ["Levin heard the secretary hesitatingly read the minutes which he obviously did not himself understand; but Levin saw from this secretary’s face what a good, nice, kind-hearted person he was.", "레빈은 서기가 분명 자기도 이해하지 못하는 의사록을 머뭇거리며 읽어 내려가는 소리를 들었다. 하지만 레빈은 그 서기의 얼굴에서 그가 얼마나 선량하고 호감 가고 마음 따뜻한 사람인지를 알아보았다."], ["This was evident from his confusion and embarrassment in reading the minutes.", "의사록을 읽으며 어쩔 줄 몰라 하고 쩔쩔매는 모습에서 그것이 뚜렷이 드러났다."], ["Then the discussion began.", "그러고 나서 토론이 시작되었다."], ["They were disputing about the misappropriation of certain sums and the laying of certain pipes, and Sergey Ivanovitch was very cutting to two members, and said something at great length with an air of triumph; and another member, scribbling something on a bit of paper, began timidly at first, but afterwards answered him very viciously and delightfully.", "그들은 어떤 금액의 유용과 어떤 배관 설치 문제를 두고 다투었고, 세르게이 이바노비치는 두 위원에게 매우 신랄하게 굴며 의기양양한 태도로 무언가를 장황하게 늘어놓았다. 그러자 다른 위원 하나가 종잇조각에 무언가를 끄적이더니, 처음에는 머뭇거리며 시작했으나 이내 더없이 표독스럽고도 통쾌하게 그에게 응수했다."], ["And then Sviazhsky (he was there too) said something too, very handsomely and nobly.", "그다음에는 스비야시스키(그도 그 자리에 있었다)가 매우 훌륭하고 점잖게 한마디 거들었다."], ["Levin listened to them, and saw clearly that these missing sums and these pipes were not anything real, and that they were not at all angry, but were all the nicest, kindest people, and everything was as happy and charming as possible among them.", "레빈은 그들의 말에 귀를 기울이면서, 이 사라진 금액이며 이 배관이며 하는 것들이 조금도 실질적인 게 아니라는 것을, 그리고 그들이 전혀 화가 나 있지 않으며 모두가 더없이 호감 가고 마음씨 고운 사람들이라는 것을, 그래서 그들 사이의 모든 것이 더없이 행복하고 매력적이라는 것을 분명히 알아챘다."], ["They did no harm to anyone, and were all enjoying it.", "그들은 누구에게도 해를 끼치지 않았고, 다들 그것을 즐기고 있었다."], ["What struck Levin was that he could see through them all today, and from little, almost imperceptible signs knew the soul of each, and saw distinctly that they were all good at heart.", "레빈을 놀라게 한 것은, 오늘 자기가 그들 모두를 꿰뚫어 볼 수 있다는 것, 작고 거의 알아챌 수 없는 징후들로부터 그들 하나하나의 영혼을 알아내고, 그들 모두가 마음속으로 선량한 사람들임을 똑똑히 본다는 것이었다."], ["And Levin himself in particular they were all extremely fond of that day.", "게다가 그날 그들은 특히 레빈 자신을 몹시도 좋아했다."], ["That was evident from the way they spoke to him, from the friendly, affectionate way even those he did not know looked at him.", "그것은 그들이 그에게 말을 거는 태도에서, 심지어 그가 모르는 사람들조차 다정하고 살가운 눈길로 그를 바라보는 데서 분명히 드러났다."]], [["“Well, did you like it?”", "\"어떠냐, 마음에 들었느냐?\""], ["Sergey Ivanovitch asked him.", "세르게이 이바노비치가 그에게 물었다."]], [["“Very much.", "\"아주요."], ["I never supposed it was so interesting!", "이렇게 흥미로운 줄은 몰랐어요!"], ["Capital!", "훌륭해요!"], ["Splendid!”", "멋져요!\""]], [["Sviazhsky went up to Levin and invited him to come round to tea with him.", "스비야시스키가 레빈에게 다가와 자기 집에 차를 마시러 들르라고 청했다."], ["Levin was utterly at a loss to comprehend or recall what it was he had disliked in Sviazhsky, what he had failed to find in him.", "레빈은 자기가 도대체 스비야시스키의 무엇을 마음에 들어 하지 않았던 건지, 그에게서 무엇을 찾지 못했던 건지 도무지 헤아릴 수도 떠올릴 수도 없었다."], ["He was a clever and wonderfully good-hearted man.", "그는 영리하고 놀랍도록 마음씨 고운 사람이었다."]], [["“Most delighted,”", "\"더없이 기쁩니다.\""], ["he said, and asked after his wife and sister-in-law.", "레빈은 이렇게 말하고는 그의 아내와 처제의 안부를 물었다."], ["And from a queer association of ideas, because in his imagination the idea of Sviazhsky’s sister-in-law was connected with marriage, it occurred to him that there was no one to whom he could more suitably speak of his happiness, and he was very glad to go and see them.", "그러다 묘한 연상 작용으로—그의 상상 속에서 스비야시스키의 처제라는 생각이 결혼과 결부되어 있었기 때문에—자기 행복에 대해 이야기하기에 그들보다 더 알맞은 상대는 없으리라는 생각이 떠올랐고, 그는 그들을 찾아가게 된 것이 무척 기뻤다."]], [["Sviazhsky questioned him about his improvements on his estate, presupposing, as he always did, that there was no possibility of doing anything not done already in Europe, and now this did not in the least annoy Levin.", "스비야시스키는 그의 영지 개량에 관해 이것저것 물었는데, 늘 그렇듯 유럽에서 이미 행해지지 않은 일이라면 아무것도 할 수 없다는 것을 전제로 하고 있었다. 그런데 이제 그것이 레빈을 조금도 짜증나게 하지 않았다."], ["On the contrary, he felt that Sviazhsky was right, that the whole business was of little value, and he saw the wonderful softness and consideration with which Sviazhsky avoided fully expressing his correct view.", "오히려 그는 스비야시스키가 옳으며, 그 모든 일이 별 가치가 없다고 느꼈고, 스비야시스키가 자신의 올바른 견해를 끝까지 드러내기를 피하는 그 놀라운 부드러움과 배려를 알아보았다."], ["The ladies of the Sviazhsky household were particularly delightful.", "스비야시스키 집안의 부인들은 유난히도 사랑스러웠다."], ["It seemed to Levin that they knew all about it already and sympathized with him, saying nothing merely from delicacy.", "레빈에게는 그들이 이미 모든 것을 알고 그를 가엾이 여기면서도, 다만 섬세한 마음에서 아무 말도 하지 않는 것처럼 여겨졌다."], ["He stayed with them one hour, two, three, talking of all sorts of subjects but the one thing that filled his heart, and did not observe that he was boring them dreadfully, and that it was long past their bedtime.", "그는 한 시간, 두 시간, 세 시간을 그들과 함께 머물며 자기 가슴을 가득 채운 단 한 가지만 빼고 온갖 주제에 대해 이야기를 나누었고, 자기가 그들을 끔찍이도 지루하게 만들고 있다는 것도, 그들이 잠자리에 들 시각이 한참 지났다는 것도 알아채지 못했다."]], [["Sviazhsky went with him into the hall, yawning and wondering at the strange humor his friend was in.", "스비야시스키는 하품을 하고 친구가 빠져 있는 이상한 기분을 의아해하며 현관까지 그를 배웅했다."], ["It was past one o’clock.", "한 시가 지나 있었다."], ["Levin went back to his hotel, and was dismayed at the thought that all alone now with his impatience he had ten hours still left to get through.", "레빈은 호텔로 돌아왔고, 이제 자기 초조함과 함께 홀로 남아 여전히 보내야 할 시간이 열 시간이나 남았다는 생각에 아연실색했다."], ["The servant, whose turn it was to be up all night, lighted his candles, and would have gone away, but Levin stopped him.", "밤새 자지 않고 당번을 서던 하인이 그의 양초에 불을 밝히고는 물러가려 했지만, 레빈이 그를 붙잡았다."], ["This servant, Yegor, whom Levin had noticed before, struck him as a very intelligent, excellent, and, above all, good-hearted man.", "전에도 눈여겨본 적이 있던 이 하인 예고르는, 레빈에게 무척 영리하고 훌륭하며, 무엇보다도 마음씨 고운 사람으로 다가왔다."]], [["“Well, Yegor, it’s hard work not sleeping, isn’t it?”", "\"그런데 예고르, 잠을 못 자는 게 힘든 일이지, 안 그런가?\""]], [["“One’s got to put up with it!", "\"참아 내야지요!"], ["It’s part of our work, you see.", "보시다시피 저희 일이 다 그런걸요."], ["In a gentleman’s house it’s easier; but then here one makes more.”", "점잖은 댁에서야 일이 더 수월하지만, 그래도 여기서는 벌이가 더 낫지요.\""]], [["It appeared that Yegor had a family, three boys and a daughter, a sempstress, whom he wanted to marry to a cashier in a saddler’s shop.", "알고 보니 예고르에게는 가족이 있었는데, 아들 셋과 삯바느질하는 딸 하나였고, 그는 그 딸을 마구점 회계원에게 시집보내고 싶어 했다."]], [["Levin, on hearing this, informed Yegor that, in his opinion, in marriage the great thing was love, and that with love one would always be happy, for happiness rests only on oneself.", "이 말을 듣고서 레빈은 예고르에게, 자기 생각에는 결혼에서 가장 중요한 것이 사랑이며, 사랑만 있으면 언제나 행복할 수 있는데, 그것은 행복이란 오로지 자기 자신에게 달린 것이기 때문이라고 일러 주었다."]], [["Yegor listened attentively, and obviously quite took in Levin’s idea, but by way of assent to it he enunciated, greatly to Levin’s surprise, the observation that when he had lived with good masters he had always been satisfied with his masters, and now was perfectly satisfied with his employer, though he was a Frenchman.", "예고르는 주의 깊게 귀를 기울였고, 레빈의 생각을 분명히 잘 알아들었지만, 그것에 동의하는 뜻으로, 레빈을 몹시도 놀라게 한 한마디를 했으니, 좋은 주인들을 모실 때면 늘 그 주인들에게 만족했고, 지금도 비록 프랑스 사람이긴 하지만 자기 고용주에게 더없이 만족하고 있다는 것이었다."]], [["“Wonderfully good-hearted fellow!”", "'놀랍도록 마음씨 고운 친구로군!'"], ["thought Levin.", "레빈은 생각했다."]], [["“Well, but you yourself, Yegor, when you got married, did you love your wife?”", "\"그런데 예고르 자네는, 결혼할 때 자네 아내를 사랑했는가?\""]], [["“Ay!", "\"아이고!"], ["and why not?”", "안 할 까닭이 있겠습니까?\""], ["responded Yegor.", "예고르가 대답했다."]], [["And Levin saw that Yegor too was in an excited state and intending to express all his most heartfelt emotions.", "그리고 레빈은 예고르 역시 흥분한 상태로 자기 마음속 깊은 감정을 죄다 털어놓으려 한다는 것을 알아챘다."]], [["“My life, too, has been a wonderful one.", "\"제 인생도 참 별난 것이었습죠."], ["From a child up....”", "어릴 적부터…\""], ["he was beginning with flashing eyes, apparently catching Levin’s enthusiasm, just as people catch yawning.", "그는 사람들이 하품을 옮듯 분명 레빈의 열정에 전염된 듯, 눈을 반짝이며 말을 꺼냈다."]], [["But at that moment a ring was heard.", "하지만 바로 그 순간 초인종 소리가 들렸다."], ["Yegor departed, and Levin was left alone.", "예고르는 가 버렸고, 레빈은 홀로 남았다."], ["He had eaten scarcely anything at dinner, had refused tea and supper at Sviazhsky’s, but he was incapable of thinking of supper.", "그는 저녁 식사 때 거의 아무것도 먹지 않았고, 스비야시스키 집에서도 차와 야식을 마다했지만, 야식 생각조차 할 수 없었다."], ["He had not slept the previous night, but was incapable of thinking of sleep either.", "그는 지난밤에 잠을 자지 못했으면서도, 잠 생각조차 할 수 없었다."], ["His room was cold, but he was oppressed by heat.", "방은 추웠으나, 그는 열기에 짓눌렸다."], ["He opened both the movable panes in his window and sat down to the table opposite the open panes.", "그는 창문의 여닫이 환기창을 두 짝 다 열어젖히고, 열린 환기창 맞은편 탁자에 가 앉았다."], ["Over the snow-covered roofs could be seen a decorated cross with chains, and above it the rising triangle of Charles’s Wain with the yellowish light of Capella.", "눈 덮인 지붕들 너머로 사슬이 달린 장식 십자가가 보였고, 그 위로는 카펠라의 누르스름한 빛과 함께 큰곰자리의 삼각형이 솟아오르고 있었다."], ["He gazed at the cross, then at the stars, drank in the fresh freezing air that flowed evenly into the room, and followed as though in a dream the images and memories that rose in his imagination.", "그는 십자가를, 그다음에는 별들을 바라보았고, 방 안으로 고르게 흘러드는 차고 신선한 공기를 들이마시며, 꿈속에서처럼 자기 상상 속에 떠오르는 형상들과 추억들을 좇았다."], ["At four o’clock he heard steps in the passage and peeped out at the door.", "네 시에 그는 복도에서 발소리를 듣고 문틈으로 내다보았다."], ["It was the gambler Myaskin, whom he knew, coming from the club.", "그가 아는 노름꾼 먀스킨이 클럽에서 돌아오는 길이었다."], ["He walked gloomily, frowning and coughing.", "그는 침울하게, 미간을 찌푸리고 기침을 하며 걷고 있었다."], ["“Poor, unlucky fellow!”", "'가엾고 불운한 친구로군!'"], ["thought Levin, and tears came into his eyes from love and pity for this man.", "레빈은 생각했고, 이 사람에 대한 사랑과 연민에서 눈에 눈물이 고였다."], ["He would have talked with him, and tried to comfort him, but remembering that he had nothing but his shirt on, he changed his mind and sat down again at the open pane to bathe in the cold air and gaze at the exquisite lines of the cross, silent, but full of meaning for him, and the mounting lurid yellow star.", "그는 그와 이야기를 나누며 위로해 주고 싶었지만, 자기가 셔츠밖에 걸치지 않았다는 것을 떠올리고는 마음을 고쳐먹고, 다시 열린 환기창 곁에 앉아 찬 공기에 몸을 담그고서, 말이 없으나 자기에게는 의미로 가득 찬 십자가의 빼어난 선과, 떠오르는 음산한 노란 별을 바라보았다."], ["At seven o’clock there was a noise of people polishing the floors, and bells ringing in some servants’ department, and Levin felt that he was beginning to get frozen.", "일곱 시에 마룻바닥을 닦는 사람들의 소리와 어느 하인 부서에서 종이 울리는 소리가 들려왔고, 레빈은 자기 몸이 얼어붙기 시작한다는 것을 느꼈다."], ["He closed the pane, washed, dressed, and went out into the street.", "그는 환기창을 닫고, 세수를 하고, 옷을 입고, 거리로 나갔다."]], [["# Chapter 15", "# Chapter 15"]], [["The streets were still empty.", "거리는 여전히 텅 비어 있었다."], ["Levin went to the house of the Shtcherbatskys.", "레빈은 셰르바츠키가로 갔다."], ["The visitors’ doors were closed and everything was asleep.", "손님용 현관문은 닫혀 있었고 모든 것이 잠들어 있었다."], ["He walked back, went into his room again, and asked for coffee.", "그는 발길을 돌려 다시 자기 방으로 들어와 커피를 청했다."], ["The day servant, not Yegor this time, brought it to him.", "낮 담당 하인이, 이번에는 예고르가 아니었는데, 커피를 가져왔다."], ["Levin would have entered into conversation with him, but a bell rang for the servant, and he went out.", "레빈은 그와 이야기를 나누려 했지만 하인을 부르는 종이 울렸고, 그는 나가 버렸다."], ["Levin tried to drink coffee and put some roll in his mouth, but his mouth was quite at a loss what to do with the roll.", "레빈은 커피를 마시려 했고 빵을 좀 입에 넣어 보았지만, 그의 입은 그 빵을 어떻게 해야 할지 도무지 갈피를 잡지 못했다."], ["Levin, rejecting the roll, put on his coat and went out again for a walk.", "레빈은 빵을 내려놓고 외투를 걸친 뒤 다시 산책을 나섰다."], ["It was nine o’clock when he reached the Shtcherbatskys’ steps the second time.", "두 번째로 셰르바츠키가의 계단에 이르렀을 때는 아홉 시였다."], ["In the house they were only just up, and the cook came out to go marketing.", "집 안에서는 사람들이 막 일어나는 참이었고, 요리사가 장을 보러 나왔다."], ["He had to get through at least two hours more.", "적어도 두 시간은 더 견뎌야 했다."]], [["All that night and morning Levin lived perfectly unconsciously, and felt perfectly lifted out of the conditions of material life.", "그날 밤과 아침 내내 레빈은 완전히 무의식 상태로 지냈고, 물질적인 삶의 조건에서 완전히 들어 올려진 듯한 기분이었다."], ["He had eaten nothing for a whole day, he had not slept for two nights, had spent several hours undressed in the frozen air, and felt not simply fresher and stronger than ever, but felt utterly independent of his body; he moved without muscular effort, and felt as if he could do anything.", "그는 온종일 아무것도 먹지 않았고, 이틀 밤을 자지 못했으며, 얼어붙은 공기 속에서 옷을 벗은 채로 몇 시간을 보냈는데도, 그저 어느 때보다 상쾌하고 강해진 정도가 아니라 자기 육체에서 완전히 자유로워진 느낌이었다. 그는 근육의 힘을 들이지 않고도 움직였고, 무엇이든 할 수 있을 것만 같았다."], ["He was convinced he could fly upwards or lift the corner of the house, if need be.", "필요하다면 위로 날아오를 수도, 집 한 귀퉁이를 들어 올릴 수도 있으리라고 확신했다."], ["He spent the remainder of the time in the street, incessantly looking at his watch and gazing about him.", "그는 남은 시간을 거리에서 보내며, 끊임없이 시계를 들여다보고 주위를 둘러보았다."]], [["And what he saw then, he never saw again after.", "그리고 그때 본 것을, 그는 그 후로 다시는 보지 못했다."], ["The children especially going to school, the bluish doves flying down from the roofs to the pavement, and the little loaves covered with flour, thrust out by an unseen hand, touched him.", "특히 학교에 가는 아이들이며, 지붕에서 보도로 날아 내려오는 푸르스름한 비둘기들이며, 보이지 않는 손에 밀려 나온, 밀가루를 뒤집어쓴 작은 빵들이 그의 마음을 울렸다."], ["Those loaves, those doves, and those two boys were not earthly creatures.", "그 빵들, 그 비둘기들, 그리고 그 두 소년은 지상의 존재가 아니었다."], ["It all happened at the same time: a boy ran towards a dove and glanced smiling at Levin; the dove, with a whir of her wings, darted away, flashing in the sun, amid grains of snow that quivered in the air, while from a little window there came a smell of fresh-baked bread, and the loaves were put out.", "모든 것이 한꺼번에 일어났다. 한 소년이 비둘기를 향해 달려가며 레빈을 보고 미소 지었고, 비둘기는 날개를 푸드덕거리며 날아올라, 공중에서 떨리는 눈 알갱이들 사이로 햇빛에 반짝이며 쏜살같이 사라졌으며, 그러는 사이 작은 창문에서는 갓 구운 빵 냄새가 풍겨 왔고, 빵들이 진열되었다."], ["All of this together was so extraordinarily nice that Levin laughed and cried with delight.", "이 모든 것이 한데 어우러져 어찌나 비할 데 없이 멋졌던지 레빈은 기뻐서 웃다가 울었다."], ["Going a long way round by Gazetny Place and Kislovka, he went back again to the hotel, and putting his watch before him, he sat down to wait for twelve o’clock.", "가제트니 광장과 키슬로프카를 돌아 멀리 에둘러서 그는 다시 호텔로 돌아왔고, 시계를 앞에 놓고 열두 시가 되기를 기다리며 앉아 있었다."], ["In the next room they were talking about some sort of machines, and swindling, and coughing their morning coughs.", "옆방에서는 사람들이 무슨 기계니, 사기니 하는 이야기를 하며 아침 기침을 콜록거리고 있었다."], ["They did not realize that the hand was near twelve.", "그들은 시곗바늘이 열두 시에 가까워졌다는 것을 알지 못했다."], ["The hand reached it.", "시곗바늘이 열두 시에 닿았다."], ["Levin went out onto the steps.", "레빈은 계단으로 나갔다."], ["The sledge-drivers clearly knew all about it.", "썰매꾼들은 분명히 그 일에 대해 다 알고 있었다."], ["They crowded round Levin with happy faces, quarreling among themselves, and offering their services.", "그들은 행복한 얼굴로 레빈을 둘러싸고는, 자기들끼리 다투며 서로 자기를 태워 달라고 했다."], ["Trying not to offend the other sledge drivers, and promising to drive with them too, Levin took one and told him to drive to the Shtcherbatskys’.", "다른 썰매꾼들의 기분을 상하게 하지 않으려고, 그들 썰매도 타겠노라 약속하면서 레빈은 한 사람을 골라 셰르바츠키가로 가자고 일렀다."], ["The sledge-driver was splendid in a white shirt-collar sticking out over his overcoat and into his strong, full-blooded red neck.", "썰매꾼은 외투 위로, 그리고 억세고 혈색 좋은 붉은 목으로 삐죽 솟은 흰 셔츠 깃 때문에 멋져 보였다."], ["The sledge was high and comfortable, and altogether such a one as Levin never drove in after, and the horse was a good one, and tried to gallop but didn’t seem to move.", "썰매는 높고 편안했으며, 도무지 그 후로는 레빈이 다시 타 본 적 없는 그런 썰매였고, 말도 좋은 놈이어서 질주하려 애썼지만 도무지 나아가지 못하는 듯했다."], ["The driver knew the Shtcherbatskys’ house, and drew up at the entrance with a curve of his arm and a “Wo!”", "마부는 셰르바츠키가의 집을 알고 있었고, 팔을 곡선으로 휘두르며 \"워!\""], ["especially indicative of respect for his fare.", "하고, 특히나 자기 손님에 대한 존경을 드러내는 소리를 내면서 현관 앞에 멈춰 섰다."], ["The Shtcherbatskys’ hall-porter certainly knew all about it.", "셰르바츠키가의 수위도 분명히 그 일에 대해 다 알고 있었다."], ["This was evident from the smile in his eyes and the way he said:", "그의 눈에 어린 미소와 그가 건넨 말투에서 그것이 분명히 드러났다."]], [["“Well, it’s a long while since you’ve been to see us, Konstantin Dmitrievitch!”", "\"아니, 오랜만에 오셨군요, 콘스탄친 드미트리예비치!\""]], [["Not only he knew all about it, but he was unmistakably delighted and making efforts to conceal his joy.", "그는 모든 것을 알고 있었을 뿐만 아니라, 틀림없이 기뻐하며 그 기쁨을 감추려 애쓰고 있었다."], ["Looking into his kindly old eyes, Levin realized even something new in his happiness.", "레빈은 그 다정한 노인의 눈을 들여다보며 자기 행복에서 무언가 새로운 것까지 깨달았다."]], [["“Are they up?”", "\"다들 일어나셨나?\""]], [["“Pray walk in!", "\"들어가시지요!"], ["Leave it here,”", "그건 여기 두세요.\""], ["said he, smiling, as Levin would have come back to take his hat.", "레빈이 모자를 가지러 돌아오려 하자, 그가 미소 지으며 말했다."], ["That meant something.", "그 말에는 어떤 뜻이 담겨 있었다."]], [["“To whom shall I announce your honor?”", "\"어느 분께 나리의 방문을 전할까요?\""], ["asked the footman.", "하인이 물었다."]], [["The footman, though a young man, and one of the new school of footmen, a dandy, was a very kind-hearted, good fellow, and he too knew all about it.", "그 하인은 젊은이로, 새로운 부류의 멋쟁이 하인이었지만, 마음씨가 무척 곱고 착한 사람이었으며, 그 역시 모든 것을 알고 있었다."]], [["“The princess ... the prince ...", "\"공작 부인……공작님……아가씨께……\""], ["the young princess....” said Levin.", "레빈이 말했다."]], [["The first person he saw was Mademoiselle Linon.", "그가 처음 본 사람은 마드무아젤 리농이었다."], ["She walked across the room, and her ringlets and her face were beaming.", "그녀는 방을 가로질러 걸어왔는데, 그녀의 곱슬머리와 얼굴이 환하게 빛나고 있었다."], ["He had only just spoken to her, when suddenly he heard the rustle of a skirt at the door, and Mademoiselle Linon vanished from Levin’s eyes, and a joyful terror came over him at the nearness of his happiness.", "그녀와 막 말을 나누기 시작했을 때, 갑자기 문가에서 치맛자락 스치는 소리가 들렸고, 마드무아젤 리농은 레빈의 눈앞에서 사라졌으며, 행복이 가까이 다가온 데서 오는 기쁨에 찬 두려움이 그를 사로잡았다."], ["Mademoiselle Linon was in great haste, and leaving him, went out at the other door.", "마드무아젤 리농은 몹시 서두르며 그를 남겨 두고 다른 문으로 나갔다."], ["Directly she had gone out, swift, swift light steps sounded on the parquet, and his bliss, his life, himself—what was best in himself, what he had so long sought and longed for—was quickly, so quickly approaching him.", "그녀가 나가자마자 빠르고도 빠른 가벼운 발소리가 마룻바닥에서 울렸고, 그의 더없는 기쁨이, 그의 삶이, 그 자신이—그 자신 안에서 가장 좋은 것이, 그가 그토록 오래 찾고 갈망해 온 것이—빠르게, 그토록 빠르게 그에게 다가오고 있었다."], ["She did not walk, but seemed, by some unseen force, to float to him.", "그녀는 걷는 것이 아니라 어떤 보이지 않는 힘에 이끌려 그에게로 떠오는 듯했다."], ["He saw nothing but her clear, truthful eyes, frightened by the same bliss of love that flooded his heart.", "그는 그녀의 맑고 진실한 눈 말고는 아무것도 보지 못했는데, 그 눈은 그의 가슴을 가득 채운 것과 똑같은 사랑의 환희로 겁에 질려 있었다."], ["Those eyes were shining nearer and nearer, blinding him with their light of love.", "그 눈은 점점 더 가까이 빛나며 사랑의 빛으로 그를 눈멀게 했다."], ["She stopped still close to him, touching him.", "그녀는 그에게 바싹 다가와, 닿을 듯이 멈춰 섰다."], ["Her hands rose and dropped onto his shoulders.", "그녀의 두 손이 들렸다가 그의 어깨 위로 내려앉았다."]], [["She had done all she could—she had run up to him and given herself up entirely, shy and happy.", "그녀는 할 수 있는 모든 것을 했다—그에게로 달려와 수줍어하면서도 행복하게 자기 자신을 온전히 내맡겼다."], ["He put his arms round her and pressed his lips to her mouth that sought his kiss.", "그는 두 팔로 그녀를 감싸 안고, 자기 입맞춤을 구하는 그녀의 입술에 자기 입술을 포갰다."]], [["She too had not slept all night, and had been expecting him all the morning.", "그녀 역시 밤새도록 잠을 이루지 못했고, 아침 내내 그를 기다리고 있었다."]], [["Her mother and father had consented without demur, and were happy in her happiness.", "그녀의 어머니와 아버지는 두말없이 승낙했고, 그녀의 행복 속에서 행복해했다."], ["She had been waiting for him.", "그녀는 그를 기다려 왔다."], ["She wanted to be the first to tell him her happiness and his.", "그녀는 자기의 행복이자 그의 행복을 가장 먼저 그에게 전하고 싶었다."], ["She had got ready to see him alone, and had been delighted at the idea, and had been shy and ashamed, and did not know herself what she was doing.", "그녀는 그를 혼자 만날 채비를 했고, 그 생각에 들떴으며, 수줍고 부끄러웠고, 자기가 무얼 하고 있는지 스스로도 알지 못했다."], ["She had heard his steps and voice, and had waited at the door for Mademoiselle Linon to go.", "그녀는 그의 발소리와 목소리를 듣고는, 문가에서 마드무아젤 리농이 나가기를 기다렸다."], ["Mademoiselle Linon had gone away.", "마드무아젤 리농이 나가 버렸다."], ["Without thinking, without asking herself how and what, she had gone up to him, and did as she was doing.", "어떻게 무엇을 할지 자신에게 묻지도, 생각하지도 않은 채, 그녀는 그에게 다가가 지금 하고 있는 그대로 했다."]], [["“Let us go to mamma!”", "\"엄마한테 가요!\""], ["she said, taking him by the hand.", "그녀가 그의 손을 잡으며 말했다."], ["For a long while he could say nothing, not so much because he was afraid of desecrating the loftiness of his emotion by a word, as that every time he tried to say something, instead of words he felt that tears of happiness were welling up.", "그는 한참 동안 아무 말도 하지 못했는데, 한마디 말로 자기 감정의 숭고함을 더럽힐까 두려웠다기보다는, 무언가를 말하려 할 때마다 말 대신 행복의 눈물이 차오르는 것을 느꼈기 때문이었다."], ["He took her hand and kissed it.", "그는 그녀의 손을 잡아 입을 맞췄다."]], [["“Can it be true?”", "\"이게 정말일까?\""], ["he said at last in a choked voice.", "그가 마침내 목멘 소리로 말했다."], ["“I can’t believe you love me, dear!”", "\"당신이 날 사랑한다는 게 믿기지 않아, 여보!\""]], [["She smiled at that “dear,” and at the timidity with which he glanced at her.", "그녀는 그 \"여보\"라는 말에, 그리고 그가 그녀를 바라보는 그 수줍음에 미소 지었다."]], [["“Yes!”", "\"네!\""], ["she said significantly, deliberately.", "그녀가 의미심장하게, 또박또박 말했다."], ["“I am so happy!”", "\"난 정말 행복해요!\""]], [["Not letting go his hands, she went into the drawing-room.", "그의 두 손을 놓지 않은 채, 그녀는 응접실로 들어갔다."], ["The princess, seeing them, breathed quickly, and immediately began to cry and then immediately began to laugh, and with a vigorous step Levin had not expected, ran up to him, and hugging his head, kissed him, wetting his cheeks with her tears.", "공작 부인은 그들을 보더니 가쁘게 숨을 몰아쉬며 이내 울기 시작했다가 곧바로 웃기 시작했고, 레빈이 예상치 못한 힘찬 걸음으로 그에게 달려와 그의 머리를 끌어안고 입을 맞추며 눈물로 그의 두 뺨을 적셨다."]], [["“So it is all settled!", "\"그래, 다 정해졌구나! 기쁘구나."], ["I am glad. Love her.", "이 아이를 사랑해 주게."], ["I am glad.... Kitty!”", "정말 기뻐……키티!\""]], [["“You’ve not been long settling things,”", "\"일을 정하는 데 오래 걸리지 않았군.\""], ["said the old prince, trying to seem unmoved; but Levin noticed that his eyes were wet when he turned to him.", "노공작이 짐짓 아무렇지 않은 척하며 말했다. 하지만 레빈은 그가 자기 쪽으로 돌아섰을 때 그의 눈이 젖어 있는 것을 알아챘다."]], [["“I’ve long, always wished for this!”", "\"난 오래전부터, 늘 이걸 바라 왔다네!\""], ["said the prince, taking Levin by the arm and drawing him towards himself.", "공작이 레빈의 팔을 잡아 자기 쪽으로 끌어당기며 말했다."], ["“Even when this little feather-head fancied....”", "\"이 가벼운 머리의 아이가 그…… 생각을 품고 있을 때조차도……\""]], [["“Papa!”", "\"아빠!\""], ["shrieked Kitty, and shut his mouth with her hands.", "키티가 비명을 지르며 두 손으로 그의 입을 막았다."]], [["“Well, I won’t!”", "\"그래, 안 하마!\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“I’m very, very ...", "\"난 아주, 아주……"], ["plea...", "기……"], ["Oh, what a fool I am....”", "아, 내가 참 바보로구나……\""]], [["He embraced Kitty, kissed her face, her hand, her face again, and made the sign of the cross over her.", "그는 키티를 끌어안고, 그녀의 얼굴에, 손에, 다시 얼굴에 입을 맞추고는, 그녀에게 성호를 그어 주었다."]], [["And there came over Levin a new feeling of love for this man, till then so little known to him, when he saw how slowly and tenderly Kitty kissed his muscular hand.", "그리고 키티가 그의 억센 손에 얼마나 천천히, 다정하게 입맞추는지를 보았을 때, 그때까지 거의 알지 못했던 이 사람에 대한 새로운 사랑의 감정이 레빈에게 밀려왔다."]], [["# Chapter 16", "# Chapter 16"]], [["The princess sat in her armchair, silent and smiling; the prince sat down beside her.", "공작 부인은 안락의자에 앉아 말없이 미소 짓고 있었고, 공작은 그 곁에 앉았다."], ["Kitty stood by her father’s chair, still holding his hand.", "키티는 아버지의 의자 옆에 서서 여전히 그의 손을 잡고 있었다."], ["All were silent.", "모두가 입을 다물고 있었다."]], [["The princess was the first to put everything into words, and to translate all thoughts and feelings into practical questions.", "모든 것을 말로 옮기고, 모든 생각과 감정을 실질적인 질문으로 바꾼 것은 공작 부인이 처음이었다."], ["And all equally felt this strange and painful for the first minute.", "그리고 첫 순간만큼은 모두가 똑같이 이 일을 이상하고 괴롭게 느꼈다."]], [["“When is it to be?", "\"언제로 할까요?"], ["We must have the benediction and announcement.", "약혼식과 발표를 해야겠죠."], ["And when’s the wedding to be?", "그리고 결혼식은 언제로 하죠?"], ["What do you think, Alexander?”", "어떻게 생각하세요, 알렉산드르?\""]], [["“Here he is,”", "\"이 사람한테 물어봐요.\""], ["said the old prince, pointing to Levin—“he’s the principal person in the matter.”", "노공작이 레빈을 가리키며 말했다. \"이 일의 당사자이니.\""]], [["“When?”", "\"언제냐고요?\""], ["said Levin blushing.", "레빈이 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다."], ["“Tomorrow.", "\"내일요."], ["If you ask me, I should say, the benediction today and the wedding tomorrow.”", "제 생각을 묻는다면, 약혼식은 오늘, 결혼식은 내일이라고 하겠습니다.\""]], [["“Come, mon cher, that’s nonsense!”", "\"이런, mon cher, 그건 말도 안 돼요!\""]], [["“Well, in a week.”", "\"그럼, 일주일 안에요.\""]], [["“He’s quite mad.” “No, why so?”", "\"이 사람 아주 정신이 나갔네.\""]], [["“Well, upon my word!”", "\"아니, 왜요?\""], ["said the mother, smiling, delighted at this haste.", "\"세상에, 정말!\" 어머니가 이 성급함에 흐뭇해하며 미소 지었다."], ["“How about the trousseau?”", "\"혼수는 어쩌고요?\""]], [["“Will there really be a trousseau and all that?”", "* mon cher: 프랑스어로 '여보게'."], ["Levin thought with horror.", "\"정말 혼수다 뭐다 그런 게 다 필요한가?\""], ["“But can the trousseau and the benediction and all that—can it spoil my happiness?", "레빈은 섬뜩한 기분으로 생각했다. \"하지만 혼수며 약혼식이며 그런 것들이—그게 내 행복을 망칠 수 있을까?"], ["Nothing can spoil it!”", "아무것도 내 행복을 망칠 수는 없어!\""], ["He glanced at Kitty, and noticed that she was not in the least, not in the very least, disturbed by the idea of the trousseau.", "그는 키티를 흘끗 보았고, 그녀가 혼수 생각에 조금도, 정말 조금도 동요하지 않는다는 것을 알아챘다."], ["“Then it must be all right,”", "\"그렇다면 다 괜찮은 거지.\""], ["he thought.", "그는 생각했다."]], [["“Oh, I know nothing about it; I only said what I should like,”", "\"아, 저는 그런 건 아무것도 모릅니다. 그저 제가 바라는 바를 말씀드렸을 뿐이에요.\""], ["he said apologetically.", "그가 미안한 듯이 말했다."]], [["“We’ll talk it over, then.", "\"그럼 그건 우리끼리 의논해 보자꾸나."], ["The benediction and announcement can take place now.", "약혼식과 발표는 지금 해도 되겠어."], ["That’s very well.”", "그게 아주 좋겠다.\""]], [["The princess went up to her husband, kissed him, and would have gone away, but he kept her, embraced her, and, tenderly as a young lover, kissed her several times, smiling.", "공작 부인이 남편에게 다가가 입을 맞추고는 자리를 뜨려 했지만, 그가 그녀를 붙잡아 끌어안고는 젊은 연인처럼 다정하게 미소 지으며 여러 번 입을 맞추었다."], ["The old people were obviously muddled for a moment, and did not quite know whether it was they who were in love again or their daughter.", "두 노인은 한순간 분명히 어리둥절해져서, 다시 사랑에 빠진 것이 자기들인지 딸인지 잘 분간하지 못하는 것 같았다."], ["When the prince and the princess had gone, Levin went up to his betrothed and took her hand.", "공작과 공작 부인이 나가자, 레빈은 약혼녀에게 다가가 그녀의 손을 잡았다."], ["He was self-possessed now and could speak, and he had a great deal he wanted to tell her.", "이제 그는 마음을 가라앉히고 말을 할 수 있었고, 그녀에게 하고 싶은 말도 무척 많았다."], ["But he said not at all what he had to say.", "하지만 그는 정작 해야 할 말은 전혀 하지 않았다."]], [["“How I knew it would be so!", "\"이렇게 될 줄 나는 어떻게 알았을까요!"], ["I never hoped for it; and yet in my heart I was always sure,”", "한 번도 바란 적은 없었어요. 그런데도 마음속으로는 늘 확신하고 있었어요.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“I believe that it was ordained.”", "\"이건 미리 정해진 일이었다고 믿어요.\""]], [["“And I!”", "\"저도요!\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“Even when....”", "\"그때조차도…….\""], ["She stopped and went on again, looking at him resolutely with her truthful eyes, “Even when I thrust from me my happiness.", "그녀는 말을 멈추었다가, 진실한 눈으로 그를 단호하게 바라보며 다시 이어 갔다. \"제가 제 행복을 밀쳐 냈던 그때조차도요."], ["I always loved you alone, but I was carried away.", "저는 언제나 당신만을 사랑했지만, 한순간 마음이 휩쓸렸어요."], ["I ought to tell you....", "당신에게 말해야겠어요……"], ["Can you forgive that?”", "그걸 용서해 주실 수 있어요?\""]], [["“Perhaps it was for the best.", "\"어쩌면 그게 오히려 잘된 일인지도 몰라요."], ["You will have to forgive me so much.", "당신은 나를 훨씬 더 많이 용서해 줘야 할 테니까요."], ["I ought to tell you....”", "나도 당신에게 말해야 할 게…….\""]], [["This was one of the things he had meant to speak about.", "이것은 그가 말하려고 마음먹었던 일들 가운데 하나였다."], ["He had resolved from the first to tell her two things—that he was not chaste as she was, and that he was not a believer.", "그는 처음부터 그녀에게 두 가지를 털어놓기로 결심하고 있었다—자기가 그녀처럼 순결하지 못하다는 것, 그리고 자기가 신앙이 없는 사람이라는 것."], ["It was agonizing, but he considered he ought to tell her both these facts.", "그것은 괴로운 일이었지만, 그는 이 두 가지 사실을 모두 그녀에게 말해야 한다고 여겼다."]], [["“No, not now, later!”", "\"아니, 지금은 아니에요, 나중에!\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“Very well, later, but you must certainly tell me.", "\"좋아요, 나중에요. 하지만 꼭 말해 줘야 해요."], ["I’m not afraid of anything.", "저는 아무것도 두렵지 않아요."], ["I want to know everything.", "모든 걸 알고 싶어요."], ["Now it is settled.”", "이제 다 정해졌어요.\""]], [["He added: “Settled that you’ll take me whatever I may be—you won’t give me up?", "그가 덧붙였다. \"당신은 내가 어떤 사람이든 나를 받아 주겠다고 정한 거죠—나를 버리지 않을 거죠?"], ["Yes?”", "그렇죠?\""]], [["“Yes, yes.”", "\"네, 그럼요.\""]], [["Their conversation was interrupted by Mademoiselle Linon, who with an affected but tender smile came to congratulate her favorite pupil.", "마드무아젤 리농이 들어와 그들의 대화가 끊겼다. 그녀는 꾸민 듯하지만 다정한 미소를 띠고 가장 아끼는 제자를 축하하러 온 것이었다."], ["Before she had gone, the servants came in with their congratulations.", "그녀가 가기도 전에 하인들이 들어와 축하의 말을 건넸다."], ["Then relations arrived, and there began that state of blissful absurdity from which Levin did not emerge till the day after his wedding.", "이어서 친척들이 도착했고, 레빈이 결혼식 다음 날에야 비로소 헤어날 수 있었던 그 행복에 겨운 야단법석이 시작되었다."], ["Levin was in a continual state of awkwardness and discomfort, but the intensity of his happiness went on all the while increasing.", "레빈은 끊임없이 어색하고 거북한 상태에 놓여 있었지만, 그 와중에도 행복의 강도는 줄곧 더해만 갔다."], ["He felt continually that a great deal was being expected of him—what, he did not know; and he did everything he was told, and it all gave him happiness.", "그는 사람들이 자기에게 무언가 많은 것을 기대하고 있다는 것을 끊임없이 느꼈다—그게 무엇인지는 몰랐지만. 그래서 그는 시키는 대로 무엇이든 다 했고, 그 모든 것이 그에게 행복을 가져다주었다."], ["He had thought his engagement would have nothing about it like others, that the ordinary conditions of engaged couples would spoil his special happiness; but it ended in his doing exactly as other people did, and his happiness being only increased thereby and becoming more and more special, more and more unlike anything that had ever happened.", "그는 자기 약혼만큼은 다른 이들의 것과는 전혀 다를 것이라고, 약혼한 남녀에게 으레 따르는 평범한 절차들이 자기만의 특별한 행복을 망쳐 버리리라고 생각했었다. 하지만 결국 그는 다른 사람들이 하는 것과 똑같이 하게 되었고, 그의 행복은 그로 인해 오히려 더 커지기만 했으며, 점점 더 특별해지고, 점점 더 지금껏 일어난 그 어떤 일과도 닮지 않은 것이 되어 갔다."]], [["“Now we shall have sweetmeats to eat,”", "\"이제 우리 사탕과자를 먹어야겠어요.\""], ["said Mademoiselle Linon—and Levin drove off to buy sweetmeats.", "마드무아젤 리농이 말하자—레빈은 사탕과자를 사러 마차를 몰고 나갔다."]], [["“Well, I’m very glad,”", "\"음, 정말 기쁘네요.\""], ["said Sviazhsky.", "스비야시스키가 말했다."], ["“I advise you to get the bouquets from Fomin’s.”", "\"꽃다발은 포민 가게에서 사라고 권하고 싶습니다.\""]], [["“Oh, are they wanted?”", "\"아, 그것도 필요한가요?\""], ["And he drove to Fomin’s.", "그러고는 그는 포민 가게로 마차를 몰았다."]], [["His brother offered to lend him money, as he would have so many expenses, presents to give....", "그의 형은 그가 비용이 많이 들 테고, 선물도 줘야 할 테니 돈을 빌려주겠다고 했다……."]], [["“Oh, are presents wanted?”", "\"아, 선물도 필요한가요?\""], ["And he galloped to Foulde’s.", "그러고는 그는 풀데 가게로 달려갔다."]], [["And at the confectioner’s, and at Fomin’s, and at Foulde’s he saw that he was expected; that they were pleased to see him, and prided themselves on his happiness, just as everyone whom he had to do with during those days.", "그리고 사탕과자 가게에서도, 포민 가게에서도, 풀데 가게에서도 그는 사람들이 자기를 기다리고 있었음을, 자기를 보고 반가워하며 자기의 행복을 자랑스러워한다는 것을 알았다. 그 며칠 동안 그가 상대해야 했던 모든 사람이 그러했듯이."], ["What was extraordinary was that everyone not only liked him, but even people previously unsympathetic, cold, and callous, were enthusiastic over him, gave way to him in everything, treated his feeling with tenderness and delicacy, and shared his conviction that he was the happiest man in the world because his betrothed was beyond perfection.", "무엇보다 놀라운 것은, 모두가 그를 좋아할 뿐 아니라, 전에는 무뚝뚝하고 차갑고 매정하던 사람들조차 그에게 열광하며 무슨 일에서든 그에게 양보하고, 그의 감정을 다정하고 섬세하게 대해 주었으며, 그의 약혼녀가 완벽함 그 이상이기에 그가 세상에서 가장 행복한 사람이라는 그의 확신을 함께 나눈다는 것이었다."], ["Kitty too felt the same thing.", "키티 역시 똑같이 느꼈다."], ["When Countess Nordston ventured to hint that she had hoped for something better, Kitty was so angry and proved so conclusively that nothing in the world could be better than Levin, that Countess Nordston had to admit it, and in Kitty’s presence never met Levin without a smile of ecstatic admiration.", "노르드스톤 백작 부인이 좀 더 나은 상대를 바랐었노라고 넌지시 비치자, 키티는 어찌나 화를 냈던지, 세상에 레빈보다 더 나은 사람은 있을 수 없다는 것을 너무도 확고하게 증명해 보였고, 결국 노르드스톤 백작 부인도 그것을 인정할 수밖에 없었으며, 키티가 있는 자리에서는 황홀하게 감탄하는 미소 없이는 레빈을 만나는 일이 없었다."]], [["The confession he had promised was the one painful incident of this time.", "그가 약속했던 고백은 이 시기의 유일하게 괴로운 사건이었다."], ["He consulted the old prince, and with his sanction gave Kitty his diary, in which there was written the confession that tortured him.", "그는 노공작과 의논한 끝에, 그의 허락을 받아 키티에게 자기 일기를 건넸다. 거기에는 그를 괴롭히던 고백이 적혀 있었다."], ["He had written this diary at the time with a view to his future wife.", "그는 미래의 아내를 염두에 두고 그 무렵 이 일기를 써 두었던 것이다."], ["Two things caused him anguish: his lack of purity and his lack of faith.", "두 가지가 그를 고통스럽게 했다. 자기에게 순결함이 없다는 것과 신앙이 없다는 것이었다."], ["His confession of unbelief passed unnoticed.", "그가 불신앙을 고백한 것은 별다른 주목을 받지 못하고 넘어갔다."], ["She was religious, had never doubted the truths of religion, but his external unbelief did not affect her in the least.", "그녀는 신앙심이 깊었고, 종교의 진리들을 한 번도 의심한 적이 없었지만, 그의 겉으로 드러난 불신앙은 그녀에게 조금도 영향을 미치지 못했다."], ["Through love she knew all his soul, and in his soul she saw what she wanted, and that such a state of soul should be called unbelieving was to her a matter of no account.", "사랑을 통해 그녀는 그의 영혼을 속속들이 알았고, 그 영혼 속에서 자기가 바라는 것을 보았으며, 그러한 영혼의 상태를 불신앙이라 부른다는 것 따위는 그녀에게 아무런 의미도 없었다."], ["The other confession set her weeping bitterly.", "그러나 다른 한 가지 고백은 그녀를 서럽게 울게 만들었다."]], [["Levin, not without an inner struggle, handed her his diary.", "레빈은 내면의 갈등 끝에 그녀에게 일기를 건넸다."], ["He knew that between him and her there could not be, and should not be, secrets, and so he had decided that so it must be.", "그는 자기와 그녀 사이에 비밀이 있을 수 없고, 또 있어서도 안 된다는 것을 알았기에, 반드시 그래야 한다고 마음먹었던 것이다."], ["But he had not realized what an effect it would have on her, he had not put himself in her place.", "하지만 그것이 그녀에게 어떤 영향을 미칠지는 미처 깨닫지 못했고, 그녀의 입장이 되어 생각해 보지 못했다."], ["It was only when the same evening he came to their house before the theater, went into her room and saw her tear-stained, pitiful, sweet face, miserable with suffering he had caused and nothing could undo, he felt the abyss that separated his shameful past from her dovelike purity, and was appalled at what he had done.", "바로 그날 저녁 극장에 가기 전에 그가 그녀의 집에 들러 방으로 들어갔을 때, 자기가 안겨 주었으나 무엇으로도 돌이킬 수 없는 그 괴로움에 비참해진, 눈물범벅이 된 가련하고 사랑스러운 그녀의 얼굴을 보고 나서야, 그는 자기의 수치스러운 과거와 그녀의 비둘기 같은 순결함을 갈라놓는 심연을 느꼈고, 자기가 저지른 일에 소스라치게 놀랐다."]], [["“Take them, take these dreadful books!”", "\"가져가요, 이 끔찍한 책을 가져가 버려요!\""], ["she said, pushing away the notebooks lying before her on the table.", "그녀가 탁자 위 자기 앞에 놓인 공책들을 밀어내며 말했다."], ["“Why did you give them me?", "\"왜 이걸 저에게 주셨어요?"], ["No, it was better anyway,”", "아니, 그래도 이게 나았어요.\""], ["she added, touched by his despairing face.", "그녀는 절망에 찬 그의 얼굴에 마음이 움직여 이렇게 덧붙였다."], ["“But it’s awful, awful!”", "\"하지만 끔찍해요, 끔찍해요!\""]], [["His head sank, and he was silent.", "그는 고개를 떨구었고, 입을 다물었다."], ["He could say nothing.", "아무 말도 할 수 없었다."]], [["“You can’t forgive me,”", "\"당신은 나를 용서할 수 없겠죠.\""], ["he whispered.", "그가 속삭였다."]], [["“Yes, I forgive you; but it’s terrible!”", "\"네, 용서해요. 하지만 너무 끔찍해요!\""]], [["But his happiness was so immense that this confession did not shatter it, it only added another shade to it.", "그러나 그의 행복은 너무도 거대했기에 이 고백도 그것을 산산이 부수지는 못했고, 다만 거기에 또 다른 그늘 하나를 더했을 뿐이었다."], ["She forgave him; but from that time more than ever he considered himself unworthy of her, morally bowed down lower than ever before her, and prized more highly than ever his undeserved happiness.", "그녀는 그를 용서했다. 하지만 그때부터 그는 그 어느 때보다도 자기가 그녀에게 어울리지 않는 사람이라고 여겼고, 그녀 앞에서 전보다 더 깊이 도덕적으로 고개를 숙였으며, 분에 넘치는 자기의 행복을 그 어느 때보다도 소중히 여기게 되었다."]], [["# Chapter 17", "# Chapter 17"]], [["Unconsciously going over in his memory the conversations that had taken place during and after dinner, Alexey Alexandrovitch returned to his solitary room.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 만찬 도중과 그 후에 오갔던 대화를 무심결에 기억 속에서 되짚으며 자신의 호젓한 방으로 돌아왔다."], ["Darya Alexandrovna’s words about forgiveness had aroused in him nothing but annoyance.", "다리야 알렉산드로브나가 용서에 관해 한 말은 그에게 짜증 외에는 아무것도 불러일으키지 못했다."], ["The applicability or non-applicability of the Christian precept to his own case was too difficult a question to be discussed lightly, and this question had long ago been answered by Alexey Alexandrovitch in the negative.", "그리스도교의 가르침이 자기 경우에 적용될 수 있는가 없는가 하는 것은 가볍게 논할 만한 문제가 아니었고, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 오래전에 이미 그 문제를 부정적으로 결론지어 둔 터였다."], ["Of all that had been said, what stuck most in his memory was the phrase of stupid, good-natured Turovtsin—“Acted like a man, he did!", "오갔던 모든 말 가운데 그의 기억에 가장 또렷이 박힌 것은 멍청하지만 사람 좋은 투롭친이 한 말이었다. \"사내답게 처신했지, 암!"], ["Called him out and shot him!”", "결투를 신청해 쏴 죽였다니까!\""], ["Everyone had apparently shared this feeling, though from politeness they had not expressed it.", "다들 그 감정에 공감하는 듯했으나, 예의를 차리느라 입 밖에 내지 않았을 뿐이었다."]], [["“But the matter is settled, it’s useless thinking about it,”", "\"하지만 이미 끝난 일이야, 생각해 봐야 소용없어.\""], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch told himself.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 스스로에게 일렀다."], ["And thinking of nothing but the journey before him, and the revision work he had to do, he went into his room and asked the porter who escorted him where his man was.", "그리고 자기 앞에 놓인 여정과 처리해야 할 검토 작업만을 떠올리며 방으로 들어가, 자신을 안내한 문지기에게 하인이 어디 있느냐고 물었다."], ["The porter said that the man had only just gone out.", "문지기는 하인이 방금 막 나갔다고 대답했다."], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch ordered tea to be sent him, sat down to the table, and taking the guidebook, began considering the route of his journey.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 차를 가져오라 이르고 탁자 앞에 앉아 안내서를 집어 들고는 여정의 경로를 살피기 시작했다."]], [["“Two telegrams,”", "\"전보가 두 통 왔습니다.\""], ["said his manservant, coming into the room.", "하인이 방으로 들어오며 말했다."], ["“I beg your pardon, your excellency; I’d only just that minute gone out.”", "\"용서하십시오, 각하. 방금 막 잠깐 나갔던 참이라.\""]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch took the telegrams and opened them.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 전보를 받아 펼쳤다."], ["The first telegram was the announcement of Stremov’s appointment to the very post Karenin had coveted.", "첫 번째 전보는 카레닌이 그토록 탐내던 바로 그 자리에 스트레모프가 임명되었다는 통지였다."], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch flung the telegram down, and flushing a little, got up and began to pace up and down the room.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 전보를 내던지고 얼굴을 약간 붉히며 일어나 방 안을 이리저리 거닐기 시작했다."], ["“Quos vult perdere dementat,”", "\"Quos vult perdere dementat.\""], ["he said, meaning by quos the persons responsible for this appointment.", "그는 quos라는 말로 이번 임명을 책임진 자들을 가리키며 중얼거렸다."], ["He was not so much annoyed that he had not received the post, that he had been conspicuously passed over; but it was incomprehensible, amazing to him that they did not see that the wordy phrase-monger Stremov was the last man fit for it.", "그가 그 자리를 얻지 못한 것, 보란 듯이 제쳐진 것이 그토록 분했던 것은 아니었다. 다만 말만 번지르르하게 늘어놓는 그 허풍쟁이 스트레모프야말로 그 자리에 가장 어울리지 않는 인물임을 저들이 보지 못한다는 사실이 그로서는 이해할 수도 없고 놀라울 따름이었다."], ["How could they fail to see how they were ruining themselves, lowering their prestige by this appointment?", "이번 임명으로 저들이 어떻게 스스로를 망치고 자신들의 prestige를 떨어뜨리고 있는지 어떻게 보지 못한단 말인가?"]], [["“Something else in the same line,”", "\"또 그 비슷한 거겠지.\""], ["he said to himself bitterly, opening the second telegram.", "그는 두 번째 전보를 펼치며 씁쓸하게 혼잣말을 했다."], ["The telegram was from his wife.", "전보는 아내가 보낸 것이었다."], ["Her name, written in blue pencil, “Anna,” was the first thing that caught his eye.", "푸른 연필로 쓴 그녀의 이름, \"안나\"가 가장 먼저 그의 눈에 들어왔다."], ["“I am dying; I beg, I implore you to come.", "\"저는 죽어 가고 있습니다. 부디, 간곡히 청하오니 와 주세요."], ["I shall die easier with your forgiveness,” he read.", "당신의 용서를 받는다면 더 편히 죽을 수 있겠습니다.\""], ["He smiled contemptuously, and flung down the telegram.", "그는 이렇게 읽었다. 그는 경멸하듯 미소 짓고 전보를 내던졌다."], ["That this was a trick and a fraud, of that, he thought for the first minute, there could be no doubt.", "이것이 속임수이자 사기라는 것, 그 점에는 처음 한순간 의심의 여지가 없다고 그는 생각했다."]], [["“There is no deceit she would stick at.", "\"저 여자가 못 할 거짓말은 없지."], ["She was near her confinement.", "해산이 가까웠으니."], ["Perhaps it is the confinement.", "어쩌면 해산일지도 몰라."], ["But what can be their aim?", "하지만 저들의 목적이 뭘까?"], ["To legitimize the child, to compromise me, and prevent a divorce,”", "아이를 적자로 만들고, 나를 곤경에 빠뜨려 이혼을 막으려는 거겠지.\""], ["he thought.", "그는 생각했다."], ["“But something was said in it: I am dying....”", "\"그런데 거기 무슨 말이 있었지."], ["He read the telegram again, and suddenly the plain meaning of what was said in it struck him.", "저는 죽어 가고 있습니다…….\" 그는 전보를 다시 읽었고, 거기 적힌 말의 단순한 의미가 문득 그의 가슴을 쳤다."]], [["“And if it is true?”", "\"만약 사실이라면?\""], ["he said to himself.", "그는 스스로에게 물었다."], ["“If it is true that in the moment of agony and nearness to death she is genuinely penitent, and I, taking it for a trick, refuse to go?", "\"만약 고통과 죽음이 임박한 순간에 그녀가 진심으로 뉘우치고 있는데, 내가 그것을 속임수로 여기고 가기를 거부한다면?"], ["That would not only be cruel, and everyone would blame me, but it would be stupid on my part.”", "그건 잔인할 뿐만 아니라 모두가 나를 비난할 테고, 게다가 나로서는 어리석은 짓이기도 하겠지.\""]], [["“Piotr, call a coach; I am going to Petersburg,”", "\"표트르, 마차를 불러 다오. 페테르부르크로 가겠다.\""], ["he said to his servant.", "그는 하인에게 말했다."]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch decided that he would go to Petersburg and see his wife.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 페테르부르크로 가서 아내를 보기로 결심했다."], ["If her illness was a trick, he would say nothing and go away again.", "그녀의 병이 속임수라면 아무 말 없이 그대로 떠나오리라."], ["If she was really in danger, and wished to see him before her death, he would forgive her if he found her alive, and pay her the last duties if he came too late.", "그녀가 정말로 위독하여 죽기 전에 자기를 보고 싶어 하는 것이라면, 살아 있는 그녀를 보게 될 경우 용서해 주고, 너무 늦게 도착하면 마지막 도리를 다하리라."]], [["All the way he thought no more of what he ought to do.", "가는 내내 그는 어떻게 처신해야 할지에 대해서는 더 이상 생각하지 않았다."]], [["With a sense of weariness and uncleanness from the night spent in the train, in the early fog of Petersburg Alexey Alexandrovitch drove through the deserted Nevsky and stared straight before him, not thinking of what was awaiting him.", "기차에서 밤을 보낸 탓에 피로와 불결한 느낌에 젖은 채, 페테르부르크의 이른 안개 속에서 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 인적 없는 네프스키 거리를 지나며 자기를 기다리고 있는 것은 생각지 않은 채 똑바로 앞만 바라보았다."], ["He could not think about it, because in picturing what would happen, he could not drive away the reflection that her death would at once remove all the difficulty of his position.", "그는 그것을 생각할 수가 없었다. 앞으로 벌어질 일을 그려 보노라면, 그녀가 죽음으로써 자기 처지의 온갖 곤란이 단번에 사라지리라는 생각을 떨쳐 낼 수가 없었기 때문이다."], ["Bakers, closed shops, night-cabmen, porters sweeping the pavements flashed past his eyes, and he watched it all, trying to smother the thought of what was awaiting him, and what he dared not hope for, and yet was hoping for.", "빵집들, 닫힌 가게들, 밤 마부들, 보도를 쓰는 문지기들이 그의 눈앞을 스쳐 지나갔고, 그는 자기를 기다리는 것, 차마 바랄 수 없으면서도 바라고 있는 그것에 대한 생각을 억누르려 애쓰며 그 모든 것을 바라보았다."], ["He drove up to the steps.", "그는 마차를 몰아 현관 계단 앞에 댔다."], ["A sledge and a carriage with the coachman asleep stood at the entrance.", "입구에는 마부가 잠든 채로 썰매 한 대와 마차 한 대가 서 있었다."], ["As he went into the entry, Alexey Alexandrovitch, as it were, got out his resolution from the remotest corner of his brain, and mastered it thoroughly.", "현관으로 들어서며 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 마치 머릿속 가장 깊숙한 구석에서 자신의 결심을 끄집어내듯 하여 그것을 단단히 다잡았다."], ["Its meaning ran: “If it’s a trick, then calm contempt and departure.", "그 결심의 내용은 이러했다. \"속임수라면 차분한 경멸과 떠남."], ["If truth, do what is proper.”", "사실이라면 마땅한 도리를 다할 것.\""]], [["The porter opened the door before Alexey Alexandrovitch rang.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 벨을 울리기도 전에 문지기가 문을 열었다."], ["The porter, Kapitonitch, looked queer in an old coat, without a tie, and in slippers.", "문지기 카피토니치는 낡은 외투에 넥타이도 매지 않고 슬리퍼를 신은 우스꽝스러운 차림이었다."]], [["“How is your mistress?”", "\"마님은 어떠신가?\""]], [["“A successful confinement yesterday.”", "\"어제 무사히 해산하셨습니다.\""]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch stopped short and turned white.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 우뚝 멈춰 섰고 얼굴이 하얗게 질렸다."], ["He felt distinctly now how intensely he had longed for her death.", "자기가 얼마나 간절히 그녀의 죽음을 바라 왔는지를 이제 또렷이 느꼈다."]], [["“And how is she?”", "\"그래서 지금은 어떠신가?\""]], [["Korney in his morning apron ran downstairs.", "코르네이가 아침 앞치마를 두른 채 아래층으로 달려 내려왔다."]], [["“Very ill,” he answered.", "\"매우 위중하십니다.\""], ["“There was a consultation yesterday, and the doctor’s here now.”", "그가 대답했다. \"어제 진찰이 있었고, 지금은 의사 선생님이 와 계십니다.\""]], [["“Take my things,”", "\"내 짐을 받아라.\""], ["said Alexey Alexandrovitch, and feeling some relief at the news that there was still hope of her death, he went into the hall.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 말했고, 아직 그녀가 죽을 희망이 남아 있다는 소식에 얼마간 안도를 느끼며 현관으로 들어섰다."]], [["On the hatstand there was a military overcoat.", "모자걸이에는 군용 외투가 걸려 있었다."], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch noticed it and asked:", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 그것을 알아채고 물었다."]], [["“Who is here?”", "\"누가 와 있느냐?\""]], [["“The doctor, the midwife, and Count Vronsky.”", "\"의사 선생님과 산파, 그리고 브론스키 백작님입니다.\""]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch went into the inner rooms.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 안쪽 방들로 들어갔다."]], [["In the drawing-room there was no one; at the sound of his steps there came out of her boudoir the midwife in a cap with lilac ribbons.", "응접실에는 아무도 없었다. 그의 발소리에 그녀의 내실에서 연보랏빛 리본이 달린 모자를 쓴 산파가 나왔다."]], [["She went up to Alexey Alexandrovitch, and with the familiarity given by the approach of death took him by the arm and drew him towards the bedroom.", "산파는 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에게 다가가, 죽음이 임박했을 때 생기는 그 친밀함으로 그의 팔을 잡고 침실 쪽으로 이끌었다."]], [["“Thank God you’ve come!", "\"오셔서 정말 다행이에요!"], ["She keeps on about you and nothing but you,”", "마님이 줄곧 당신 말씀만, 오직 당신 말씀만 하고 계세요.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“Make haste with the ice!”", "\"얼음을 어서!\""], ["the doctor’s peremptory voice said from the bedroom.", "침실에서 의사의 단호한 목소리가 들려왔다."]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch went into her boudoir.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 그녀의 내실로 들어갔다."]], [["At the table, sitting sideways in a low chair, was Vronsky, his face hidden in his hands, weeping.", "탁자 곁, 낮은 의자에 비스듬히 앉아 두 손에 얼굴을 묻고 흐느끼고 있는 사람은 브론스키였다."], ["He jumped up at the doctor’s voice, took his hands from his face, and saw Alexey Alexandrovitch.", "그는 의사의 목소리에 벌떡 일어나 얼굴에서 손을 떼었다가 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치를 보았다."], ["Seeing the husband, he was so overwhelmed that he sat down again, drawing his head down to his shoulders, as if he wanted to disappear; but he made an effort over himself, got up and said:", "남편을 본 그는 너무도 당황한 나머지 다시 주저앉으며, 사라지고 싶다는 듯 머리를 어깨 사이로 움츠렸다. 그러나 가까스로 마음을 다잡아 일어서며 말했다."]], [["“She is dying.", "\"부인은 죽어 가고 있습니다."], ["The doctors say there is no hope.", "의사들 말로는 가망이 없다고 합니다."], ["I am entirely in your power, only let me be here ...", "저는 전적으로 당신 처분에 맡기겠습니다. 다만 제가 여기 있게만 해 주십시오……"], ["though I am at your disposal.", "저는 당신 뜻대로 하겠습니다."], ["I....”", "저는…….\""]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch, seeing Vronsky’s tears, felt a rush of that nervous emotion always produced in him by the sight of other people’s suffering, and turning away his face, he moved hurriedly to the door, without hearing the rest of his words.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 브론스키의 눈물을 보자, 타인의 고통을 볼 때면 늘 솟구치던 그 신경질적인 격정이 밀려오는 것을 느꼈고, 얼굴을 돌린 채 그의 나머지 말은 듣지도 않고 서둘러 문 쪽으로 옮겨 갔다."], ["From the bedroom came the sound of Anna’s voice saying something.", "침실에서 안나가 뭐라고 말하는 목소리가 들려왔다."], ["Her voice was lively, eager, with exceedingly distinct intonations.", "그녀의 목소리는 생기에 차 있었고, 열에 들떠 있었으며, 더없이 또렷한 억양을 띠고 있었다."], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch went into the bedroom, and went up to the bed.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 침실로 들어가 침대로 다가갔다."], ["She was lying turned with her face towards him.", "그녀는 얼굴을 그쪽으로 향한 채 누워 있었다."], ["Her cheeks were flushed crimson, her eyes glittered, her little white hands thrust out from the sleeves of her dressing gown were playing with the quilt, twisting it about.", "두 뺨은 새빨갛게 달아올라 있었고 두 눈은 반짝였으며, 실내복 소매 밖으로 내민 작고 흰 두 손은 누비이불을 만지작거리며 비비 꼬고 있었다."], ["It seemed as though she were not only well and blooming, but in the happiest frame of mind.", "그녀는 건강하고 생기에 넘칠 뿐만 아니라 더없이 행복한 심정에 잠긴 듯 보였다."], ["She was talking rapidly, musically, and with exceptionally correct articulation and expressive intonation.", "그녀는 빠르고 음악적으로, 유난히 정확한 발음과 풍부한 억양으로 말하고 있었다."]], [["“For Alexey—I am speaking of Alexey Alexandrovitch (what a strange and awful thing that both are Alexey, isn’t it?)—Alexey would not refuse me.", "\"알렉세이라면, 저는 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치를 두고 하는 말이에요(둘 다 알렉세이라니, 참 이상하고 끔찍한 일이죠, 그렇지 않아요?), 알렉세이라면 제 청을 거절하지 않을 거예요."], ["I should forget, he would forgive....", "저는 잊을 거고, 그이는 용서할 거예요……"], ["But why doesn’t he come?", "그런데 왜 안 오는 걸까요?"], ["He’s so good he doesn’t know himself how good he is.", "그이는 너무 착해서 자기가 얼마나 착한지조차 몰라요."], ["Ah, my God, what agony!", "아, 하느님, 이 고통이라니!"], ["Give me some water, quick!", "물 좀 줘요, 어서!"], ["Oh, that will be bad for her, my little girl!", "아, 그러면 우리 아기에게, 우리 딸에게 안 좋을 텐데!"], ["Oh, very well then, give her to a nurse.", "아, 그럼 좋아요, 아기를 유모에게 맡겨요."], ["Yes, I agree, it’s better in fact.", "그래요, 동의해요, 사실 그게 나아요."], ["He’ll be coming; it will hurt him to see her.", "그이가 올 거예요. 아기를 보면 마음 아파할 거예요."], ["Give her to the nurse.”", "아기를 유모에게 맡겨요.\""]], [["“Anna Arkadyevna, he has come.", "\"안나 아르카지예브나, 그분이 오셨어요."], ["Here he is!”", "여기 계세요!\""], ["said the midwife, trying to attract her attention to Alexey Alexandrovitch.", "산파가 안나의 주의를 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에게 돌리려 애쓰며 말했다."]], [["“Oh, what nonsense!”", "\"아, 무슨 말도 안 되는 소리예요!\""], ["Anna went on, not seeing her husband.", "안나는 남편을 보지 못한 채 계속 말했다."], ["“No, give her to me; give me my little one!", "\"아니, 그 애를 내게 줘요. 내 아기를 내게 줘요!"], ["He has not come yet.", "그이는 아직 안 왔어요."], ["You say he won’t forgive me, because you don’t know him.", "당신은 그이가 나를 용서하지 않을 거라고 하지만, 그건 당신이 그이를 모르기 때문이에요."], ["No one knows him.", "아무도 그이를 몰라요."], ["I’m the only one, and it was hard for me even.", "오직 나만 알아요, 그리고 나조차도 그건 힘든 일이었어요. 그이의 눈을 나는 잘 알아야 해요."], ["His eyes I ought to know—Seryozha has just the same eyes—and I can’t bear to see them because of it.", "세료자도 꼭 그 눈을 가졌죠. 그래서 나는 그 눈을 차마 볼 수가 없어요."], ["Has Seryozha had his dinner?", "세료자는 저녁을 먹었나요?"], ["I know everyone will forget him.", "다들 그 애를 잊어버릴 거라는 거 알아요."], ["He would not forget.", "그이라면 잊지 않을 텐데."], ["Seryozha must be moved into the corner room, and Mariette must be asked to sleep with him.”", "세료자를 구석 방으로 옮기고, 마리에트한테 그 애와 같이 자 달라고 해야 해요.\""]], [["All of a sudden she shrank back, was silent; and in terror, as though expecting a blow, as though to defend herself, she raised her hands to her face.", "별안간 그녀는 움찔 뒤로 물러서며 입을 다물었다. 그리고 마치 매를 맞을 것을 예상하는 듯, 자신을 지키려는 듯 공포에 질려 두 손을 들어 얼굴을 가렸다."], ["She had seen her husband.", "그녀가 남편을 본 것이다."]], [["“No, no!” she began.", "\"아니야, 아니야!\""], ["“I am not afraid of him; I am afraid of death.", "그녀가 입을 열었다. \"나는 그이가 무섭지 않아요."], ["Alexey, come here.", "나는 죽음이 무서운 거예요."], ["I am in a hurry, because I’ve no time, I’ve not long left to live; the fever will begin directly and I shall understand nothing more.", "알렉세이, 이리 와요. 나는 서둘러야 해요, 시간이 없으니까, 살날이 얼마 안 남았으니까."], ["Now I understand, I understand it all, I see it all!”", "곧 열이 오르기 시작하면 나는 아무것도 알아듣지 못할 테니까. 지금은 알아요, 다 알아요, 다 보여요!\""]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch’s wrinkled face wore an expression of agony; he took her by the hand and tried to say something, but he could not utter it; his lower lip quivered, but he still went on struggling with his emotion, and only now and then glanced at her.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 주름진 얼굴은 고통스러운 표정을 띠었다. 그는 그녀의 손을 잡고 뭐라고 말하려 했으나 입 밖에 낼 수가 없었다."], ["And each time he glanced at her, he saw her eyes gazing at him with such passionate and triumphant tenderness as he had never seen in them.", "아랫입술이 떨렸지만 그는 여전히 자신의 감정과 싸우며 이따금씩 그녀를 흘끗거릴 뿐이었다. 그리고 그녀를 흘끗거릴 때마다, 그는 그녀의 눈이 일찍이 본 적 없는 그토록 열정적이고 의기양양한 다정함으로 자기를 응시하고 있는 것을 보았다."]], [["“Wait a minute, you don’t know ...", "\"잠깐만요, 당신은 몰라요……"], ["stay a little, stay!...”", "조금만 있어 줘요, 있어 줘요!……\""], ["She stopped, as though collecting her ideas.", "그녀는 마치 생각을 가다듬는 듯 말을 멈추었다."], ["“Yes,”", "\"그래요.\""], ["she began; “yes, yes, yes.", "그녀가 입을 열었다. \"그래요, 그래요, 그래요."], ["This is what I wanted to say.", "이게 바로 내가 하고 싶었던 말이에요."], ["Don’t be surprised at me.", "나를 보고 놀라지 말아요."], ["I’m still the same....", "나는 여전히 똑같아요……"], ["But there is another woman in me, I’m afraid of her: she loved that man, and I tried to hate you, and could not forget about her that used to be.", "하지만 내 안에는 또 다른 여자가 있어요, 나는 그 여자가 무서워요. 그 여자는 그 사람을 사랑했고, 나는 당신을 미워하려 애썼지만 예전의 그 여자를 잊을 수가 없었어요."], ["I’m not that woman.", "나는 그 여자가 아니에요."], ["Now I’m my real self, all myself.", "지금의 나는 진짜 나예요, 온전한 나예요."], ["I’m dying now, I know I shall die, ask him.", "나는 지금 죽어 가요, 죽으리라는 걸 알아요, 그이에게 물어봐요."], ["Even now I feel—see here, the weights on my feet, on my hands, on my fingers.", "지금도 느껴져요, 보세요, 여기 내 발에, 내 손에, 내 손가락에 추가 매달려 있어요."], ["My fingers—see how huge they are!", "내 손가락, 보세요, 얼마나 큰지!"], ["But this will soon all be over....", "하지만 이 모든 게 곧 끝날 거예요……"], ["Only one thing I want: forgive me, forgive me quite.", "오직 한 가지만 바라요, 나를 용서해 줘요, 나를 완전히 용서해 줘요."], ["I’m terrible, but my nurse used to tell me; the holy martyr—what was her name?", "나는 끔찍한 여자예요, 하지만 내 유모가 늘 들려주었던 거룩한 순교자, 그 여자 이름이 뭐였더라?"], ["She was worse.", "그 여자는 더 나빴어요."], ["And I’ll go to Rome; there’s a wilderness, and there I shall be no trouble to anyone, only I’ll take Seryozha and the little one....", "나는 로마로 갈 거예요. 거기 황야가 있어요, 거기서라면 나는 누구에게도 폐가 되지 않을 거예요, 다만 세료자와 어린것만 데려갈래요……"], ["No, you can’t forgive me!", "아니, 당신은 나를 용서할 수 없어요!"], ["I know, it can’t be forgiven!", "알아요, 용서받을 수 없는 일이라는 거!"], ["No, no, go away, you’re too good!”", "아니, 아니, 가요, 당신은 너무 착해요!\""], ["She held his hand in one burning hand, while she pushed him away with the other.", "그녀는 불덩이 같은 한 손으로는 그의 손을 잡은 채, 다른 손으로는 그를 밀어냈다."]], [["The nervous agitation of Alexey Alexandrovitch kept increasing, and had by now reached such a point that he ceased to struggle with it.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 신경질적인 동요는 점점 커져 갔고, 이제는 그가 더 이상 그것과 싸우기를 멈출 지경에 이르렀다."], ["He suddenly felt that what he had regarded as nervous agitation was on the contrary a blissful spiritual condition that gave him all at once a new happiness he had never known.", "그는 자신이 신경질적인 동요라고 여겼던 것이 오히려 일찍이 알지 못했던 새로운 행복을 단번에 안겨 주는 더없이 복된 정신 상태임을 문득 느꼈다."], ["He did not think that the Christian law that he had been all his life trying to follow, enjoined on him to forgive and love his enemies; but a glad feeling of love and forgiveness for his enemies filled his heart.", "그는 평생 따르려 애써 온 그리스도교의 율법이 자신에게 원수를 용서하고 사랑하라 명한다는 것을 생각한 것이 아니었다. 다만 원수를 향한 사랑과 용서의 기쁜 감정이 그의 가슴을 가득 채웠다."], ["He knelt down, and laying his head in the curve of her arm, which burned him as with fire through the sleeve, he sobbed like a little child.", "그는 무릎을 꿇었고, 소매 너머로 불처럼 그를 데우는 그녀의 팔 안쪽 굽이에 머리를 묻고 어린아이처럼 흐느꼈다."], ["She put her arm around his head, moved towards him, and with defiant pride lifted up her eyes.", "그녀는 한 팔로 그의 머리를 감싸 안고 그에게로 몸을 기울이며, 도전적인 긍지로 눈을 치떴다."]], [["“That is he.", "\"바로 저이예요."], ["I knew him!", "나는 저이를 알아봤어요!"], ["Now, forgive me, everyone, forgive me!...", "자, 모두 나를 용서해 줘요, 나를 용서해 줘요!……"], ["They’ve come again; why don’t they go away?...", "또 왔네, 왜 가지 않는 거지?……"], ["Oh, take these cloaks off me!”", "아, 이 망토들을 내게서 벗겨 줘요!\""]], [["The doctor unloosed her hands, carefully laying her on the pillow, and covered her up to the shoulders.", "의사는 그녀의 두 손을 풀어 조심스럽게 베개 위에 누이고는 어깨까지 이불을 덮어 주었다."], ["She lay back submissively, and looked before her with beaming eyes.", "그녀는 순순히 누워 빛나는 눈으로 앞을 바라보았다."]], [["“Remember one thing, that I needed nothing but forgiveness, and I want nothing more....", "\"한 가지만 기억해 줘요, 나는 용서 외에는 아무것도 필요하지 않았고, 더 바라는 것도 없어요……"], ["Why doesn’t he come?”", "왜 저이는 오지 않죠?\""], ["she said, turning to the door towards Vronsky.", "그녀는 브론스키 쪽으로, 문 쪽으로 돌아서며 말했다."], ["“Do come, do come!", "\"이리 와요, 어서 와요!"], ["Give him your hand.”", "이이에게 손을 줘요.\""]], [["Vronsky came to the side of the bed, and seeing Anna, again hid his face in his hands.", "브론스키는 침대 곁으로 다가왔으나, 안나를 보자 다시 두 손에 얼굴을 묻었다."]], [["“Uncover your face—look at him!", "\"얼굴에서 손을 떼고 이이를 봐요!"], ["He’s a saint,”", "이이는 성자예요.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“Oh!", "\"아!"], ["uncover your face, do uncover it!”", "얼굴을 드러내요, 어서 드러내요!\""], ["she said angrily.", "그녀가 화를 내며 말했다."], ["“Alexey Alexandrovitch, do uncover his face!", "\"알렉세이 알렉산드로비치, 저이의 얼굴을 드러내 줘요!"], ["I want to see him.”", "저이를 보고 싶어요.\""]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch took Vronsky’s hands and drew them away from his face, which was awful with the expression of agony and shame upon it.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 브론스키의 두 손을 잡아 얼굴에서 떼어 냈다. 그 얼굴은 고통과 수치의 표정으로 처참했다."]], [["“Give him your hand.", "\"이이에게 손을 줘요."], ["Forgive him.”", "이이를 용서해요.\""]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch gave him his hand, not attempting to restrain the tears that streamed from his eyes.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 두 눈에서 흘러내리는 눈물을 막으려 하지도 않은 채 그에게 손을 내밀었다."]], [["“Thank God, thank God!”", "\"하느님 감사합니다, 하느님 감사합니다!\""], ["she said, “now everything is ready.", "그녀가 말했다. \"이제 모든 게 준비됐어요."], ["Only to stretch my legs a little.", "다리만 좀 펴면 돼요."], ["There, that’s capital.", "자, 됐어, 아주 좋아."], ["How badly these flowers are done—not a bit like a violet,”", "이 꽃들은 정말 형편없이 만들었네, 제비꽃이랑은 조금도 안 닮았어.\""], ["she said, pointing to the hangings.", "그녀는 휘장을 가리키며 말했다."], ["“My God, my God!", "\"하느님, 하느님!"], ["when will it end?", "이게 언제 끝나려나?"], ["Give me some morphine.", "모르핀을 줘요."], ["Doctor, give me some morphine!", "의사 선생님, 모르핀을 주세요!"], ["Oh, my God, my God!”", "아, 하느님, 하느님!\""]], [["And she tossed about on the bed.", "그리고 그녀는 침대 위에서 몸부림쳤다."]], [["The doctors said that it was puerperal fever, and that it was ninety-nine chances in a hundred it would end in death.", "의사들은 산욕열이라며, 백 중 아흔아홉은 죽음으로 끝날 것이라고 했다."], ["The whole day long there was fever, delirium, and unconsciousness.", "온종일 열과 헛소리와 의식 불명이 이어졌다."], ["At midnight the patient lay without consciousness, and almost without pulse.", "자정 무렵 환자는 의식도 없이, 맥박도 거의 없이 누워 있었다."]], [["The end was expected every minute.", "이제나저제나 임종이 닥칠 것으로 예상되었다."]], [["Vronsky had gone home, but in the morning he came to inquire, and Alexey Alexandrovitch meeting him in the hall, said: “Better stay, she might ask for you,”", "브론스키는 집으로 돌아갔으나, 아침에 안부를 물으러 다시 왔고, 현관에서 그를 만난 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 \"그냥 계시는 게 낫겠습니다, 그녀가 당신을 찾을지도 모르니\""], ["and himself led him to his wife’s boudoir.", "하고는 직접 그를 아내의 내실로 데려갔다."], ["Towards morning, there was a return again of excitement, rapid thought and talk, and again it ended in unconsciousness.", "동틀 무렵, 다시 흥분과 빠른 생각과 말이 돌아왔고, 또다시 의식 불명으로 끝났다."], ["On the third day it was the same thing, and the doctors said there was hope.", "사흘째 되는 날도 똑같았으나, 의사들은 희망이 있다고 말했다."], ["That day Alexey Alexandrovitch went into the boudoir where Vronsky was sitting, and closing the door sat down opposite him.", "그날 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 브론스키가 앉아 있는 내실로 들어가 문을 닫고 그의 맞은편에 앉았다."]], [["“Alexey Alexandrovitch,”", "\"알렉세이 알렉산드로비치.\""], ["said Vronsky, feeling that a statement of the position was coming, “I can’t speak, I can’t understand.", "브론스키는 이제 처지에 대한 어떤 선언이 나오리라는 것을 느끼며 말했다. \"저는 말을 할 수도 없고, 이해할 수도 없습니다."], ["Spare me!", "저를 가만 내버려 두십시오!"], ["However hard it is for you, believe me, it is more terrible for me.”", "당신에게 아무리 힘든 일이라 한들, 믿어 주십시오, 저에게는 그것이 더 끔찍합니다.\""]], [["He would have risen; but Alexey Alexandrovitch took him by the hand and said:", "그는 일어서려 했으나, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 그의 손을 잡고 말했다."]], [["“I beg you to hear me out; it is necessary.", "\"제 말을 끝까지 들어 주시기를 부탁드립니다."], ["I must explain my feelings, the feelings that have guided me and will guide me, so that you may not be in error regarding me.", "그래야만 합니다. 저는 제 감정을, 저를 이끌어 온 그리고 앞으로도 이끌어 갈 그 감정을 설명해야겠습니다."], ["You know I had resolved on a divorce, and had even begun to take proceedings.", "그래야 당신이 저에 대해 오해하지 않을 테니까요. 아시다시피 저는 이혼을 결심했고, 절차까지 밟기 시작했습니다."], ["I won’t conceal from you that in beginning this I was in uncertainty, I was in misery; I will confess that I was pursued by a desire to revenge myself on you and on her.", "숨기지 않겠습니다, 이 일을 시작할 때 저는 마음을 정하지 못하고 괴로워했습니다. 고백하건대 당신과 그녀에게 복수하고 싶은 마음에 사로잡혀 있었습니다."], ["When I got the telegram, I came here with the same feelings; I will say more, I longed for her death.", "전보를 받았을 때도 같은 심정으로 이리로 왔습니다. 더 말씀드리자면, 저는 그녀의 죽음을 바랐습니다."], ["But....”", "그런데…….\""], ["He paused, pondering whether to disclose or not to disclose his feeling to him.", "그는 자신의 감정을 그에게 털어놓을지 말지 망설이며 말을 멈추었다."], ["“But I saw her and forgave her.", "\"그런데 저는 그녀를 보았고, 그녀를 용서했습니다."], ["And the happiness of forgiveness has revealed to me my duty.", "그리고 용서의 행복이 저에게 제 도리를 일깨워 주었습니다."], ["I forgive completely.", "저는 완전히 용서합니다."], ["I would offer the other cheek, I would give my cloak if my coat be taken.", "한쪽 뺨을 맞으면 다른 쪽 뺨도 내밀겠고, 겉옷을 빼앗기면 속옷까지 내주겠습니다."], ["I pray to God only not to take from me the bliss of forgiveness!”", "저는 다만 하느님께 용서의 더없는 행복을 제게서 거두지 마시기를 기도할 따름입니다!\""]], [["Tears stood in his eyes, and the luminous, serene look in them impressed Vronsky.", "그의 두 눈에는 눈물이 고여 있었고, 그 안에 어린 맑고 평온한 빛이 브론스키에게 깊은 인상을 남겼다."]], [["“This is my position: you can trample me in the mud, make me the laughing-stock of the world, I will not abandon her, and I will never utter a word of reproach to you,”", "\"이것이 제 입장입니다. 당신은 저를 진창에 짓밟을 수도 있고, 세상의 웃음거리로 만들 수도 있겠지요."], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch went on.", "그래도 저는 그녀를 버리지 않을 것이며, 당신에게 단 한 마디 비난의 말도 하지 않겠습니다.\""], ["“My duty is clearly marked for me; I ought to be with her, and I will be.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 말을 이었다. \"제 도리는 제게 분명히 정해져 있습니다."], ["If she wishes to see you, I will let you know, but now I suppose it would be better for you to go away.”", "저는 그녀 곁에 있어야 하고, 그렇게 할 것입니다. 그녀가 당신을 보고 싶어 하면 알려 드리겠습니다만, 지금은 당신이 떠나 주시는 편이 나을 것 같습니다.\""]], [["He got up, and sobs cut short his words.", "그는 일어섰고, 흐느낌이 그의 말을 끊었다."], ["Vronsky too was getting up, and in a stooping, not yet erect posture, looked up at him from under his brows.", "브론스키도 일어서고 있었는데, 아직 똑바로 펴지 못한 구부정한 자세로 눈썹 아래에서 그를 올려다보았다."], ["He did not understand Alexey Alexandrovitch’s feeling, but he felt that it was something higher and even unattainable for him with his view of life.", "그는 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 감정을 이해하지는 못했지만, 그것이 자신의 인생관으로는 다다를 수조차 없는 무언가 더 높은 것임을 느꼈다."]], [["# Chapter 18", "# Chapter 18"]], [["After the conversation with Alexey Alexandrovitch, Vronsky went out onto the steps of the Karenins’ house and stood still, with difficulty remembering where he was, and where he ought to walk or drive.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치와 이야기를 나눈 뒤, 브론스키는 카레닌 가의 현관 계단으로 나와 우뚝 멈춰 섰다. 자기가 어디에 있는지, 어디로 걸어가야 할지 마차를 타야 할지조차 가까스로 떠올리며."], ["He felt disgraced, humiliated, guilty, and deprived of all possibility of washing away his humiliation.", "그는 명예가 더럽혀지고, 모욕당하고, 죄지은 사람이 된 데다, 그 치욕을 씻어 낼 그 어떤 가능성마저 빼앗긴 듯한 기분이었다."], ["He felt thrust out of the beaten track along which he had so proudly and lightly walked till then.", "그는 그때까지 그토록 자랑스럽고 가뿐하게 걸어왔던 익숙한 길에서 밀려나 버린 것만 같았다."], ["All the habits and rules of his life that had seemed so firm, had turned out suddenly false and inapplicable.", "그토록 견고하게만 보였던 그의 삶의 모든 습관과 규범이 별안간 거짓되고 쓸모없는 것으로 드러났다."], ["The betrayed husband, who had figured till that time as a pitiful creature, an incidental and somewhat ludicrous obstacle to his happiness, had suddenly been summoned by her herself, elevated to an awe-inspiring pinnacle, and on the pinnacle that husband had shown himself, not malignant, not false, not ludicrous, but kind and straightforward and large.", "그때까지 가련한 존재이자 자기 행복에 우연히 끼어든 다소 우스꽝스러운 장애물로만 여겨졌던 배신당한 남편이, 다른 누구도 아닌 그녀 자신에 의해 별안간 불려 나와 경외감을 불러일으키는 높은 자리로 끌어올려졌고, 그 자리에서 그 남편은 악의에 차 있지도, 거짓되지도, 우스꽝스럽지도 않은, 친절하고 솔직하고 도량 넓은 사람으로 자신을 드러냈다."], ["Vronsky could not but feel this, and the parts were suddenly reversed.", "브론스키는 이것을 느끼지 않을 수 없었으며, 두 사람의 역할이 별안간 뒤바뀌었다."], ["Vronsky felt his elevation and his own abasement, his truth and his own falsehood.", "브론스키는 상대의 높음과 자기 자신의 비천함을, 상대의 진실과 자기 자신의 거짓을 느꼈다."], ["He felt that the husband was magnanimous even in his sorrow, while he had been base and petty in his deceit.", "그는 그 남편이 슬픔 속에서도 관대한 데 반해, 자기는 기만 속에서 비열하고 옹졸했음을 느꼈다."], ["But this sense of his own humiliation before the man he had unjustly despised made up only a small part of his misery.", "그러나 부당하게 멸시했던 그 사람 앞에서 느끼는 이 굴욕감은 그의 비참함의 작은 일부에 지나지 않았다."], ["He felt unutterably wretched now, for his passion for Anna, which had seemed to him of late to be growing cooler, now that he knew he had lost her forever, was stronger than ever it had been.", "그는 지금 말로 다 할 수 없을 만큼 비참했으니, 요즈음 들어 식어 가는 것만 같았던 안나를 향한 그의 정열이, 그녀를 영영 잃었다는 것을 알게 된 지금에 와서 그 어느 때보다 더 강렬해졌기 때문이다."], ["He had seen all of her in her illness, had come to know her very soul, and it seemed to him that he had never loved her till then.", "그는 그녀가 앓는 동안 그녀의 모든 것을 보았고, 그녀의 영혼까지 알게 되었으며, 그때까지 한 번도 그녀를 사랑한 적이 없었던 것만 같았다."], ["And now when he had learned to know her, to love her as she should be loved, he had been humiliated before her, and had lost her forever, leaving with her nothing of himself but a shameful memory.", "그런데 이제 그녀를 알게 되고, 마땅히 사랑해야 할 방식으로 그녀를 사랑하게 되었을 때, 그는 그녀 앞에서 모욕을 당했고, 그녀에게 부끄러운 기억 말고는 자기 자신의 그 무엇도 남기지 못한 채 그녀를 영영 잃어버린 것이다."], ["Most terrible of all had been his ludicrous, shameful position when Alexey Alexandrovitch had pulled his hands away from his humiliated face.", "무엇보다 끔찍했던 것은 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 그의 굴욕에 찬 얼굴에서 그의 두 손을 떼어 냈을 때의 그 우스꽝스럽고 수치스러운 처지였다."], ["He stood on the steps of the Karenins’ house like one distraught, and did not know what to do.", "그는 넋이 나간 사람처럼 카레닌 가의 현관 계단에 서서, 무엇을 해야 할지 알지 못했다."]], [["“A sledge, sir?”", "\"썰매를 부를까요, 나리?\""], ["asked the porter.", "문지기가 물었다."]], [["“Yes, a sledge.”", "\"그래, 썰매를.\""]], [["On getting home, after three sleepless nights, Vronsky, without undressing, lay down flat on the sofa, clasping his hands and laying his head on them.", "사흘 밤을 뜬눈으로 지새운 끝에 집에 돌아온 브론스키는 옷도 벗지 않은 채 소파 위에 반듯이 드러누워, 두 손을 깍지 껴 머리를 그 위에 얹었다."], ["His head was heavy.", "머리가 무거웠다."], ["Images, memories, and ideas of the strangest description followed one another with extraordinary rapidity and vividness.", "더없이 기이한 형상들과 기억들과 상념들이 놀라운 속도와 선명함으로 잇따라 떠올랐다."], ["First it was the medicine he had poured out for the patient and spilt over the spoon, then the midwife’s white hands, then the queer posture of Alexey Alexandrovitch on the floor beside the bed.", "처음에는 환자에게 따라 주다가 숟가락 위로 흘린 약이, 그다음에는 산파의 하얀 두 손이, 그다음에는 침대 옆 바닥에 엎드린 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 기묘한 자세가 떠올랐다."]], [["“To sleep!", "\"잠을 자자!"], ["To forget!”", "잊어버리자!\""], ["he said to himself with the serene confidence of a healthy man that if he is tired and sleepy, he will go to sleep at once.", "그는 건강한 사람 특유의, 피곤하고 졸리면 곧바로 잠들 수 있다는 차분한 확신을 품고 스스로에게 말했다."], ["And the same instant his head did begin to feel drowsy and he began to drop off into forgetfulness.", "그러자 그 순간 그의 머리가 정말로 나른해지기 시작했고, 그는 망각 속으로 빠져들기 시작했다."], ["The waves of the sea of unconsciousness had begun to meet over his head, when all at once—it was as though a violent shock of electricity had passed over him.", "무의식의 바다에서 일어난 물결이 그의 머리 위로 막 한데 모이려는 찰나, 별안간 마치 격렬한 전기 충격이 그의 몸을 훑고 지나가기라도 한 것 같았다."], ["He started so that he leaped up on the springs of the sofa, and leaning on his arms got in a panic onto his knees.", "그는 어찌나 놀랐던지 소파의 스프링 위에서 펄쩍 뛰어올라, 두 팔을 짚고 허둥지둥 무릎을 꿇은 자세가 되었다."], ["His eyes were wide open as though he had never been asleep.", "그의 두 눈은 마치 한 번도 잠든 적이 없었다는 듯 활짝 열려 있었다."], ["The heaviness in his head and the weariness in his limbs that he had felt a minute before had suddenly gone.", "조금 전까지 느꼈던 머릿속의 무거움과 팔다리의 노곤함이 별안간 사라져 버렸다."]], [["“You may trample me in the mud,”", "\"나를 진창에 짓밟아도 좋소.\""], ["he heard Alexey Alexandrovitch’s words and saw him standing before him, and saw Anna’s face with its burning flush and glittering eyes, gazing with love and tenderness not at him but at Alexey Alexandrovitch; he saw his own, as he fancied, foolish and ludicrous figure when Alexey Alexandrovitch took his hands away from his face.", "그는 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 말을 듣고, 그가 자기 앞에 서 있는 모습을 보았으며, 타오르는 듯한 홍조와 반짝이는 눈으로, 그가 아니라 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치를 사랑과 다정함으로 바라보는 안나의 얼굴을 보았다. 그는 또 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 그의 얼굴에서 두 손을 떼어 냈을 때, 그가 상상하기에 어리석고 우스꽝스러웠던 자기 자신의 모습을 보았다."], ["He stretched out his legs again and flung himself on the sofa in the same position and shut his eyes.", "그는 다시 다리를 쭉 뻗고는 아까와 똑같은 자세로 소파에 몸을 던지고 눈을 감았다."]], [["“To sleep!", "\"잠을 자자!"], ["To forget!”", "잊어버리자!\""], ["he repeated to himself.", "그는 스스로에게 거듭 되뇌었다."], ["But with his eyes shut he saw more distinctly than ever Anna’s face as it had been on the memorable evening before the races.", "그러나 눈을 감으니, 경마가 있던 날 전날 저녁의, 그 잊을 수 없는 저녁의 안나의 얼굴이 그 어느 때보다 또렷하게 보였다."]], [["“That is not and will not be, and she wants to wipe it out of her memory.", "\"그건 지나간 일이고 다시는 없을 일이야, 그리고 그녀는 그걸 기억에서 지워 버리고 싶어 해."], ["But I cannot live without it.", "하지만 나는 그것 없이는 살 수가 없어."], ["How can we be reconciled?", "우리가 어떻게 화해할 수 있겠어?"], ["how can we be reconciled?”", "어떻게 화해할 수 있단 말이야?\""], ["he said aloud, and unconsciously began to repeat these words.", "그는 소리 내어 말했고, 자기도 모르게 이 말을 되풀이하기 시작했다."], ["This repetition checked the rising up of fresh images and memories, which he felt were thronging in his brain.", "이렇게 되풀이하니, 머릿속으로 몰려드는 것이 느껴지던 새로운 형상들과 기억들이 솟구쳐 오르는 것이 멎었다."], ["But repeating words did not check his imagination for long.", "그러나 말을 되풀이하는 것으로 그의 상상이 오래 멎어지지는 않았다."], ["Again in extraordinarily rapid succession his best moments rose before his mind, and then his recent humiliation.", "다시 놀랍도록 빠르게 잇따라, 그의 가장 행복했던 순간들이 그의 마음 앞에 떠올랐고, 그다음에는 최근의 굴욕이 떠올랐다."], ["“Take away his hands,”", "\"그의 두 손을 떼어 내요.\""], ["Anna’s voice says.", "안나의 목소리가 말한다."], ["He takes away his hands and feels the shamestruck and idiotic expression of his face.", "그는 두 손을 떼어 내고, 수치심에 사로잡힌 자기 얼굴의 멍청한 표정을 느낀다."]], [["He still lay down, trying to sleep, though he felt there was not the smallest hope of it, and kept repeating stray words from some chain of thought, trying by this to check the rising flood of fresh images.", "그는 잠들 가망이 조금도 없다는 것을 느끼면서도 여전히 누운 채 잠을 청하려 애썼고, 어떤 상념의 사슬에서 떨어져 나온 토막 난 말들을 거듭 되뇌며 그것으로 솟구쳐 오르는 새로운 형상들의 물결을 막아 보려 했다."], ["He listened, and heard in a strange, mad whisper words repeated: “I did not appreciate it, did not make enough of it.", "그는 귀를 기울였고, 기이하고 광기 어린 속삭임으로 거듭되는 말을 들었다. \"난 그걸 소중히 여기지 않았어, 충분히 누리지 못했어."], ["I did not appreciate it, did not make enough of it.”", "난 그걸 소중히 여기지 않았어, 충분히 누리지 못했어.\""]], [["“What’s this?", "\"이게 뭐지?"], ["Am I going out of my mind?”", "내가 미쳐 가는 건가?\""], ["he said to himself. “Perhaps.", "그는 스스로에게 말했다."], ["What makes men go out of their minds; what makes men shoot themselves?”", "\"어쩌면 그런지도. 무엇이 사람을 미치게 하지, 무엇이 사람을 권총으로 자살하게 하지?\""], ["he answered himself, and opening his eyes, he saw with wonder an embroidered cushion beside him, worked by Varya, his brother’s wife.", "그는 스스로에게 답했고, 눈을 떴다가 자기 곁에 형수 바랴가 수놓아 만든 쿠션이 놓여 있는 것을 보고 놀랐다."], ["He touched the tassel of the cushion, and tried to think of Varya, of when he had seen her last.", "그는 쿠션의 술을 만지며 바랴를 떠올리려, 그녀를 마지막으로 본 게 언제였는지 떠올리려 애썼다."], ["But to think of anything extraneous was an agonizing effort.", "그러나 다른 무언가를 생각한다는 것은 고통스러운 노력이었다."], ["“No, I must sleep!”", "\"안 돼, 잠을 자야 해!\""], ["He moved the cushion up, and pressed his head into it, but he had to make an effort to keep his eyes shut.", "그는 쿠션을 끌어다 거기에 머리를 파묻었지만, 눈을 감고 있으려면 애를 써야만 했다."], ["He jumped up and sat down.", "그는 벌떡 일어나 앉았다."], ["“That’s all over for me,”", "\"나에게는 모든 게 끝났어.\""], ["he said to himself.", "그는 스스로에게 말했다."], ["“I must think what to do.", "\"어떻게 해야 할지 생각해야 해."], ["What is left?”", "무엇이 남았지?\""], ["His mind rapidly ran through his life apart from his love of Anna.", "그의 마음은 안나에 대한 사랑을 빼고 자기 삶을 빠르게 훑어 내려갔다."]], [["“Ambition?", "\"공명심?"], ["Serpuhovskoy?", "세르푸호프스코이?"], ["Society?", "사교계?"], ["The court?”", "궁정?\""], ["He could not come to a pause anywhere.", "그는 어디에서도 멈춰 설 수 없었다."], ["All of it had had meaning before, but now there was no reality in it.", "그 모든 것이 전에는 의미가 있었지만, 이제는 그 어떤 것에도 실체가 없었다."], ["He got up from the sofa, took off his coat, undid his belt, and uncovering his hairy chest to breathe more freely, walked up and down the room.", "그는 소파에서 일어나 외투를 벗고 허리띠를 풀고는, 더 자유롭게 숨을 쉬려고 털 난 가슴을 드러낸 채 방 안을 이리저리 거닐었다. \"사람들은 이렇게 미쳐 가는 거지.\""], ["“This is how people go mad,”", "그는 거듭 되뇌었다."], ["he repeated, “and how they shoot themselves ...", "\"그리고 이렇게 권총으로 자살하는 거야……"], ["to escape humiliation,”", "치욕에서 벗어나려고.\""], ["he added slowly.", "그는 천천히 덧붙였다."]], [["He went to the door and closed it, then with fixed eyes and clenched teeth he went up to the table, took a revolver, looked round him, turned it to a loaded barrel, and sank into thought.", "그는 문가로 가서 문을 닫고는, 눈을 한곳에 고정한 채 이를 악물고 탁자로 다가가 권총을 집어 들고 주위를 둘러보더니, 총알이 든 약실로 돌려놓고 생각에 잠겼다."], ["For two minutes, his head bent forward with an expression of an intense effort of thought, he stood with the revolver in his hand, motionless, thinking.", "두 시간 가까이—아니, 이 분 동안 그는 강렬하게 생각에 몰두하는 표정으로 머리를 앞으로 숙이고, 손에 권총을 든 채 꼼짝 않고 서서 생각에 잠겨 있었다."]], [["“Of course,”", "\"물론이지.\""], ["he said to himself, as though a logical, continuous, and clear chain of reasoning had brought him to an indubitable conclusion.", "그는 마치 논리적이고 연속적이며 명료한 추론의 사슬이 자기를 의심할 여지 없는 결론으로 이끌기라도 한 것처럼 스스로에게 말했다."], ["In reality this “of course,” that seemed convincing to him, was simply the result of exactly the same circle of memories and images through which he had passed ten times already during the last hour—memories of happiness lost forever.", "실은 그에게 그토록 설득력 있게 여겨진 이 \"물론이지\"는, 지난 한 시간 동안 그가 이미 열 번이나 거쳐 온 바로 그 똑같은 기억과 형상의 순환—영영 잃어버린 행복에 대한 기억—이 낳은 결과에 지나지 않았다."], ["There was the same conception of the senselessness of everything to come in life, the same consciousness of humiliation.", "거기에는 앞으로 살아갈 삶의 모든 것이 무의미하다는 똑같은 생각이, 굴욕에 대한 똑같은 의식이 있었다."], ["Even the sequence of these images and emotions was the same.", "심지어 이 형상들과 감정들의 순서마저 똑같았다."]], [["“Of course,”", "\"물론이지.\""], ["he repeated, when for the third time his thought passed again round the same spellbound circle of memories and images, and pulling the revolver to the left side of his chest, and clutching it vigorously with his whole hand, as it were, squeezing it in his fist, he pulled the trigger.", "그의 생각이 세 번째로 다시 그 똑같은 주술에 걸린 기억과 형상의 순환을 한 바퀴 돌았을 때, 그는 거듭 되뇌었다. 그러고는 권총을 가슴 왼편으로 가져가, 마치 주먹 안에 그것을 꽉 쥐어 짜내려는 것처럼 온 손으로 힘껏 움켜쥐고 방아쇠를 당겼다."], ["He did not hear the sound of the shot, but a violent blow on his chest sent him reeling.", "그는 총소리를 듣지 못했지만, 가슴을 강타한 격렬한 충격에 비틀거렸다."], ["He tried to clutch at the edge of the table, dropped the revolver, staggered, and sat down on the ground, looking about him in astonishment.", "그는 탁자 가장자리를 붙잡으려다 권총을 떨어뜨리고는 휘청거리다가 바닥에 주저앉아, 어리둥절한 표정으로 주위를 둘러보았다."], ["He did not recognize his room, looking up from the ground, at the bent legs of the table, at the wastepaper basket, and the tiger-skin rug.", "그는 바닥에서 올려다보며 탁자의 굽은 다리며, 휴지통이며, 호랑이 가죽 깔개를 보면서도 자기 방을 알아보지 못했다."], ["The hurried, creaking steps of his servant coming through the drawing-room brought him to his senses.", "응접실을 가로질러 다가오는 하인의 다급하고 삐걱거리는 발소리가 그를 제정신으로 돌아오게 했다."], ["He made an effort at thought, and was aware that he was on the floor; and seeing blood on the tiger-skin rug and on his arm, he knew he had shot himself.", "그는 애써 생각해 보았고, 자기가 바닥에 쓰러져 있다는 것을 깨달았다. 그리고 호랑이 가죽 깔개와 자기 팔 위에 묻은 피를 보고는, 자기가 권총으로 자살을 기도했다는 것을 알았다."]], [["“Idiotic!", "\"멍청하긴!"], ["Missed!”", "빗나갔어!\""], ["he said, fumbling after the revolver.", "그는 권총을 더듬어 찾으며 말했다."], ["The revolver was close beside him—he sought further off.", "권총은 바로 그의 곁에 있었지만, 그는 더 먼 곳을 뒤졌다."], ["Still feeling for it, he stretched out to the other side, and not being strong enough to keep his balance, fell over, streaming with blood.", "여전히 그것을 더듬으며 그는 반대편으로 손을 뻗었고, 균형을 잡을 만큼 힘이 남아 있지 않아 피를 줄줄 흘리며 옆으로 쓰러졌다."]], [["The elegant, whiskered manservant, who used to be continually complaining to his acquaintances of the delicacy of his nerves, was so panic-stricken on seeing his master lying on the floor, that he left him losing blood while he ran for assistance.", "평소 아는 사람들에게 자기 신경이 예민하다고 끊임없이 하소연하던 멋쟁이 구레나룻 하인은, 주인이 바닥에 쓰러져 있는 것을 보고 어찌나 공포에 사로잡혔던지, 피를 흘리는 그를 내버려 둔 채 도움을 청하러 뛰어갔다."], ["An hour later Varya, his brother’s wife, had arrived, and with the assistance of three doctors, whom she had sent for in all directions, and who all appeared at the same moment, she got the wounded man to bed, and remained to nurse him.", "한 시간 뒤 형수 바랴가 도착했고, 그녀가 사방으로 사람을 보내 부른, 모두 동시에 나타난 세 명의 의사의 도움을 받아 부상당한 그를 침대에 눕히고, 곁에 남아 그를 간호했다."]], [["# Chapter 19", "# Chapter 19"]], [["The mistake made by Alexey Alexandrovitch in that, when preparing for seeing his wife, he had overlooked the possibility that her repentance might be sincere, and he might forgive her, and she might not die—this mistake was two months after his return from Moscow brought home to him in all its significance.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 아내를 만날 준비를 하면서 그녀의 뉘우침이 진실할 수도 있고, 자신이 그녀를 용서할 수도 있으며, 그녀가 죽지 않을 수도 있다는 가능성을 간과하는 실수를 저질렀는데, 그 실수가 지닌 의미는 모스크바에서 돌아온 지 두 달이 지나서야 비로소 그에게 온전히 와닿았다."], ["But the mistake made by him had arisen not simply from his having overlooked that contingency, but also from the fact that until that day of his interview with his dying wife, he had not known his own heart.", "그러나 그가 저지른 실수는 단지 그 가능성을 간과한 데서 비롯된 것만이 아니라, 죽어 가는 아내를 만나던 그날까지 자기 자신의 마음을 알지 못했다는 사실에서 비롯된 것이기도 했다."], ["At his sick wife’s bedside he had for the first time in his life given way to that feeling of sympathetic suffering always roused in him by the sufferings of others, and hitherto looked on by him with shame as a harmful weakness.", "병든 아내의 머리맡에서 그는 생애 처음으로, 늘 다른 사람의 고통이 그의 안에서 불러일으켰으나 지금껏 해로운 약점이라 여기며 부끄러워하던 그 동정의 괴로움에 자신을 내맡겼다."], ["And pity for her, and remorse for having desired her death, and most of all, the joy of forgiveness, made him at once conscious, not simply of the relief of his own sufferings, but of a spiritual peace he had never experienced before.", "그리고 아내에 대한 연민과, 그녀가 죽기를 바랐던 데 대한 자책과, 무엇보다도 용서의 기쁨이, 자기 고통의 완화뿐 아니라 이제껏 한 번도 경험해 보지 못한 영적 평화를 그에게 단번에 깨닫게 했다."], ["He suddenly felt that the very thing that was the source of his sufferings had become the source of his spiritual joy; that what had seemed insoluble while he was judging, blaming, and hating, had become clear and simple when he forgave and loved.", "그는 자기 고통의 원천이었던 바로 그것이 자기 영적 기쁨의 원천이 되었다는 것을, 자신이 심판하고 비난하고 미워하는 동안에는 풀 수 없을 것처럼 보였던 것이 용서하고 사랑하자 명료하고 단순해졌다는 것을 문득 느꼈다."]], [["He forgave his wife and pitied her for her sufferings and her remorse.", "그는 아내를 용서하고 그녀의 고통과 뉘우침을 가엾게 여겼다."], ["He forgave Vronsky, and pitied him, especially after reports reached him of his despairing action.", "그는 브론스키를 용서했고, 특히 그의 절망적인 행동에 대한 소식이 그에게 전해진 뒤로는 그를 가엾게 여겼다."], ["He felt more for his son than before.", "그는 아들에게도 전보다 더 깊은 정을 느꼈다."], ["And he blamed himself now for having taken too little interest in him.", "그리고 이제는 아들에게 너무 관심을 두지 않았던 자신을 책망했다."], ["But for the little newborn baby he felt a quite peculiar sentiment, not of pity, only, but of tenderness.", "그러나 갓 태어난 작은 아기에 대해서는 연민뿐 아니라 애틋함까지 깃든, 사뭇 독특한 감정을 느꼈다."], ["At first, from a feeling of compassion alone, he had been interested in the delicate little creature, who was not his child, and who was cast on one side during her mother’s illness, and would certainly have died if he had not troubled about her, and he did not himself observe how fond he became of her.", "처음에는 오직 측은한 마음에서, 자기 아이도 아니고 어미의 병중에 한쪽으로 내팽개쳐졌으며 그가 마음을 쓰지 않았다면 틀림없이 죽고 말았을 그 가냘픈 작은 생명에게 관심을 가졌는데, 자신이 그 아이를 얼마나 좋아하게 되었는지는 스스로도 알아차리지 못했다."], ["He would go into the nursery several times a day, and sit there for a long while, so that the nurses, who were at first afraid of him, got quite used to his presence.", "그는 하루에도 몇 번씩 육아실에 들어가 오래도록 거기 앉아 있곤 했고, 그래서 처음에는 그를 두려워하던 유모들도 그가 있는 데에 아주 익숙해졌다."], ["Sometimes for half an hour at a stretch he would sit silently gazing at the saffron-red, downy, wrinkled face of the sleeping baby, watching the movements of the frowning brows, and the fat little hands, with clenched fingers, that rubbed the little eyes and nose.", "때로는 내리 반 시간씩 잠든 아기의 사프란빛이 도는 붉고 솜털 보송한 주름진 얼굴을 말없이 들여다보며, 찌푸려진 눈썹의 움직임이며 작은 눈과 코를 비비는, 손가락을 꼭 오므린 통통한 작은 손을 지켜보곤 했다."], ["At such moments particularly, Alexey Alexandrovitch had a sense of perfect peace and inward harmony, and saw nothing extraordinary in his position, nothing that ought to be changed.", "특히 그런 순간에 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 완전한 평화와 내면의 조화를 느꼈고, 자기 처지에서 비범한 것이라곤, 바꿔야 할 것이라곤 아무것도 보지 못했다."]], [["But as time went on, he saw more and more distinctly that however natural the position now seemed to him, he would not long be allowed to remain in it.", "그러나 시간이 흐를수록, 지금의 처지가 자기에게 아무리 자연스럽게 보일지라도 자신이 그 안에 오래 머무는 것은 허락되지 않으리라는 것을 점점 더 또렷이 깨달았다."], ["He felt that besides the blessed spiritual force controlling his soul, there was another, a brutal force, as powerful, or more powerful, which controlled his life, and that this force would not allow him that humble peace he longed for.", "그는 자기 영혼을 다스리는 축복받은 영적인 힘 외에, 또 다른 힘이, 그만큼 강하거나 그보다 더 강한 잔혹한 힘이 자기 삶을 지배하고 있으며, 그 힘은 그가 갈망하는 그 겸허한 평화를 그에게 허락하지 않으리라는 것을 느꼈다."], ["He felt that everyone was looking at him with inquiring wonder, that he was not understood, and that something was expected of him.", "그는 모두가 캐묻는 듯한 의아함으로 자신을 바라보고 있다는 것을, 사람들이 자기를 이해하지 못하며 자기에게 무언가를 기대하고 있다는 것을 느꼈다."], ["Above all, he felt the instability and unnaturalness of his relations with his wife.", "무엇보다도 그는 아내와의 관계가 불안정하고 부자연스럽다는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["When the softening effect of the near approach of death had passed away, Alexey Alexandrovitch began to notice that Anna was afraid of him, ill at ease with him, and could not look him straight in the face.", "죽음이 임박하면서 마음을 누그러뜨렸던 영향이 사라지자, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 안나가 자기를 두려워하고, 자기 앞에서 거북해하며, 자기 얼굴을 똑바로 쳐다보지 못한다는 것을 알아차리기 시작했다."], ["She seemed to be wanting, and not daring, to tell him something; and as though foreseeing their present relations could not continue, she seemed to be expecting something from him.", "그녀는 그에게 무언가 말하고 싶어 하면서도 차마 그러지 못하는 것 같았다. 그리고 지금의 관계가 계속될 수 없으리라는 것을 예감하기라도 하듯, 그에게서 무언가를 기다리고 있는 것 같았다."]], [["Towards the end of February it happened that Anna’s baby daughter, who had been named Anna too, fell ill.", "2월 말 무렵, 역시 안나라는 이름이 붙은 안나의 어린 딸이 병에 걸리는 일이 있었다."], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch was in the nursery in the morning, and leaving orders for the doctor to be sent for, he went to his office.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 아침에 육아실에 들렀고, 의사를 부르라고 일러둔 뒤 관청으로 갔다."], ["On finishing his work, he returned home at four.", "일을 마치고 그는 네 시에 집으로 돌아왔다."], ["Going into the hall he saw a handsome groom, in a braided livery and a bear fur cape, holding a white fur cloak.", "현관으로 들어서던 그는 장식 줄이 달린 제복에 곰털 망토를 두른 잘생긴 마부가 흰 모피 외투를 들고 서 있는 것을 보았다."]], [["“Who is here?”", "\"누가 와 있나?\""], ["asked Alexey Alexandrovitch.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 물었다."]], [["“Princess Elizaveta Federovna Tverskaya,”", "\"옐리자베타 표도로브나 트베르스카야 공작 부인이십니다.\""], ["the groom answered, and it seemed to Alexey Alexandrovitch that he grinned.", "마부가 대답했는데, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에게는 그가 히죽 웃은 것처럼 보였다."]], [["During all this difficult time Alexey Alexandrovitch had noticed that his worldly acquaintances, especially women, took a peculiar interest in him and his wife.", "이 힘겨운 시기 내내 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 사교계의 지인들, 특히 여자들이 자신과 아내에게 묘한 관심을 보인다는 것을 알아차렸다."], ["All these acquaintances he observed with difficulty concealing their mirth at something; the same mirth that he had perceived in the lawyer’s eyes, and just now in the eyes of this groom.", "그는 이 모든 지인들이 무언가를 두고 즐거워하는 기색을 애써 감추는 것을 지켜보았는데, 그것은 변호사의 눈에서 보았던, 그리고 방금 이 마부의 눈에서 본 것과 똑같은 즐거움이었다."], ["Everyone seemed, somehow, hugely delighted, as though they had just been at a wedding.", "모두가 어쩐지 결혼식에라도 다녀온 것처럼 더없이 들떠 있는 듯했다."], ["When they met him, with ill-disguised enjoyment they inquired after his wife’s health.", "그를 만나면 사람들은 잘 감추지 못한 흥겨움으로 아내의 안부를 물었다."], ["The presence of Princess Tverskaya was unpleasant to Alexey Alexandrovitch from the memories associated with her, and also because he disliked her, and he went straight to the nursery.", "트베르스카야 공작 부인이 와 있다는 것은 그녀와 얽힌 기억 때문에, 또 그가 그녀를 싫어하기 때문에 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에게는 불쾌한 일이었고, 그래서 그는 곧장 육아실로 갔다."], ["In the day nursery Seryozha, leaning on the table with his legs on a chair, was drawing and chatting away merrily.", "낮 육아실에서는 세료자가 다리를 의자에 올려놓고 탁자에 기댄 채 그림을 그리며 즐겁게 재잘대고 있었다."], ["The English governess, who had during Anna’s illness replaced the French one, was sitting near the boy knitting a shawl.", "안나가 앓는 동안 프랑스인 가정교사를 대신하게 된 영국인 가정교사가 아이 곁에 앉아 숄을 뜨고 있었다."], ["She hurriedly got up, curtseyed, and pulled Seryozha.", "그녀는 황급히 일어나 무릎을 굽혀 인사하고는 세료자를 끌어당겼다."]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch stroked his son’s hair, answered the governess’s inquiries about his wife, and asked what the doctor had said of the baby.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 아들의 머리를 쓰다듬고, 아내의 안부를 묻는 가정교사의 물음에 답한 뒤, 의사가 아기에 대해 뭐라고 했는지 물었다."]], [["“The doctor said it was nothing serious, and he ordered a bath, sir.”", "\"의사 선생님께서는 심각한 게 아니라 하시면서 목욕을 시키라고 하셨습니다, 나리.\""]], [["“But she is still in pain,”", "\"하지만 아직 아파하고 있는데.\""], ["said Alexey Alexandrovitch, listening to the baby’s screaming in the next room.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 옆방에서 들려오는 아기의 울음소리에 귀를 기울이며 말했다."]], [["“I think it’s the wet-nurse, sir,”", "\"제 생각엔 유모 탓인 것 같습니다, 나리.\""], ["the Englishwoman said firmly.", "영국 여자가 단호하게 말했다."]], [["“What makes you think so?”", "\"어째서 그렇게 생각하지?\""], ["he asked, stopping short.", "그가 걸음을 멈추며 물었다."]], [["“It’s just as it was at Countess Paul’s, sir.", "\"파울 백작 부인 댁에서도 꼭 이랬거든요, 나리."], ["They gave the baby medicine, and it turned out that the baby was simply hungry: the nurse had no milk, sir.”", "아기에게 약을 먹였는데, 알고 보니 아기가 그저 배가 고팠던 거였어요. 유모에게 젖이 없었던 거죠, 나리.\""]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch pondered, and after standing still a few seconds he went in at the other door.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 생각에 잠겼고, 몇 초 동안 가만히 서 있다가 다른 쪽 문으로 들어갔다."], ["The baby was lying with its head thrown back, stiffening itself in the nurse’s arms, and would not take the plump breast offered it; and it never ceased screaming in spite of the double hushing of the wet-nurse and the other nurse, who was bending over her.", "아기는 머리를 뒤로 젖히고 유모의 팔 안에서 몸을 뻣뻣하게 곧추세운 채, 내미는 통통한 젖을 물려 하지 않았다. 그리고 유모와 그 위로 몸을 굽힌 또 다른 보모가 함께 어르며 달래는데도 울음을 그치지 않았다."]], [["“Still no better?”", "\"아직도 나아지지 않았나?\""], ["said Alexey Alexandrovitch.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 말했다."]], [["“She’s very restless,”", "\"몹시 보채는군요.\""], ["answered the nurse in a whisper.", "보모가 속삭이듯 대답했다."]], [["“Miss Edwarde says that perhaps the wet-nurse has no milk,”", "\"에드워드 양 말로는 유모에게 젖이 없는지도 모른다던데.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“I think so too, Alexey Alexandrovitch.”", "\"저도 그렇게 생각합니다, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치.\""]], [["“Then why didn’t you say so?”", "\"그럼 왜 진작 말하지 않았나?\""]], [["“Who’s one to say it to?", "\"누구한테 말씀드리겠어요?"], ["Anna Arkadyevna still ill....”", "안나 아르카지예브나께서는 아직도 편찮으신데……\""], ["said the nurse discontentedly.", "보모가 못마땅한 듯이 말했다."]], [["The nurse was an old servant of the family.", "보모는 이 집안의 오랜 하인이었다."], ["And in her simple words there seemed to Alexey Alexandrovitch an allusion to his position.", "그리고 그녀의 소박한 말 속에서 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 자기 처지를 빗댄 암시를 느꼈다."]], [["The baby screamed louder than ever, struggling and sobbing.", "아기는 발버둥 치며 흐느끼더니 그 어느 때보다 더 크게 울어 댔다."], ["The nurse, with a gesture of despair, went to it, took it from the wet-nurse’s arms, and began walking up and down, rocking it.", "보모는 절망스러운 몸짓으로 아기에게 다가가 유모의 팔에서 받아 안고는 어르며 이리저리 걷기 시작했다."]], [["“You must ask the doctor to examine the wet-nurse,”", "\"의사한테 유모를 진찰해 보라고 해야겠군.\""], ["said Alexey Alexandrovitch.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 말했다."], ["The smartly dressed and healthy-looking nurse, frightened at the idea of losing her place, muttered something to herself, and covering her bosom, smiled contemptuously at the idea of doubts being cast on her abundance of milk.", "말쑥하게 차려입은 건강해 보이는 유모는 일자리를 잃을지 모른다는 생각에 겁을 먹고 무어라 혼잣말로 중얼거리더니, 가슴을 가리고는 자기 젖이 넘치도록 풍부하다는 것을 의심받는다는 데에 경멸스럽다는 듯 웃었다."], ["In that smile, too, Alexey Alexandrovitch saw a sneer at his position.", "그 웃음에서도 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 자기 처지에 대한 비웃음을 보았다."]], [["“Luckless child!”", "\"가엾은 것!\""], ["said the nurse, hushing the baby, and still walking up and down with it.", "유모는 아기를 어르며, 여전히 아기를 안고 이리저리 걸으며 말했다."]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch sat down, and with a despondent and suffering face watched the nurse walking to and fro.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 자리에 앉아, 낙담하고 고통스러운 얼굴로 보모가 왔다 갔다 하는 모습을 지켜보았다."]], [["When the child at last was still, and had been put in a deep bed, and the nurse, after smoothing the little pillow, had left her, Alexey Alexandrovitch got up, and walking awkwardly on tiptoe, approached the baby.", "마침내 아기가 잠잠해지고 푹신한 침대에 뉘어졌으며, 보모가 작은 베개를 매만진 뒤 그 곁을 떠나자, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 일어나 어색하게 발끝으로 걸어 아기에게 다가갔다."], ["For a minute he was still, and with the same despondent face gazed at the baby; but all at once a smile, that moved his hair and the skin of his forehead, came out on his face, and he went as softly out of the room.", "한동안 그는 가만히 서서 여전히 낙담한 얼굴로 아기를 들여다보았다. 그러나 별안간, 머리카락과 이마의 살갗을 움직이는 미소가 그의 얼굴에 떠올랐고, 그는 발소리를 죽이며 방을 나섰다."]], [["In the dining-room he rang the bell, and told the servant who came in to send again for the doctor.", "식당에서 그는 종을 울려, 들어온 하인에게 다시 의사를 부르러 보내라고 일렀다."], ["He felt vexed with his wife for not being anxious about this exquisite baby, and in this vexed humor he had no wish to go to her; he had no wish, either, to see Princess Betsy.", "그는 이 사랑스러운 아기를 두고 마음 졸이지 않는 아내가 못마땅했고, 이렇게 못마땅한 기분에 그녀에게 가고 싶지 않았다. 베치 공작 부인을 마주하고 싶지도 않았다."], ["But his wife might wonder why he did not go to her as usual; and so, overcoming his disinclination, he went towards the bedroom.", "그러나 아내가 자기가 평소처럼 들르지 않는 것을 의아하게 여길지도 몰랐다. 그래서 그는 내키지 않는 마음을 누르고 침실 쪽으로 갔다."], ["As he walked over the soft rug towards the door, he could not help overhearing a conversation he did not want to hear.", "부드러운 양탄자를 밟으며 문 쪽으로 다가가던 그는, 듣고 싶지 않은 대화를 본의 아니게 엿듣고 말았다."]], [["“If he hadn’t been going away, I could have understood your answer and his too.", "\"그가 떠나지 않는다면 당신 대답도, 그의 대답도 이해할 수 있어요."], ["But your husband ought to be above that,”", "하지만 당신 남편이라면 그쯤은 초연해야지요.\""], ["Betsy was saying.", "베치가 말하고 있었다."]], [["“It’s not for my husband; for myself I don’t wish it.", "\"내 남편을 위해서가 아니에요. 나 자신을 위해 그걸 원하지 않는 거예요."], ["Don’t say that!”", "그런 말 하지 말아요!\""], ["answered Anna’s excited voice.", "안나의 흥분한 목소리가 대답했다."]], [["“Yes, but you must care to say good-bye to a man who has shot himself on your account....”", "\"하지만 당신 때문에 스스로 총을 쏜 남자에게 작별 인사쯤은 하고 싶을 텐데요……\""]], [["“That’s just why I don’t want to.”", "\"바로 그래서 그러고 싶지 않은 거예요.\""]], [["With a dismayed and guilty expression, Alexey Alexandrovitch stopped and would have gone back unobserved.", "당황하고 죄스러운 표정으로 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 걸음을 멈추었고, 눈에 띄지 않게 되돌아가려 했다."], ["But reflecting that this would be undignified, he turned back again, and clearing his throat, he went up to the bedroom.", "그러나 그것이 품위 없는 짓이라는 데에 생각이 미치자, 다시 발길을 돌려 헛기침을 하고는 침실로 들어갔다."], ["The voices were silent, and he went in.", "목소리들이 잦아들었고, 그는 안으로 들어섰다."]], [["Anna, in a gray dressing gown, with a crop of short clustering black curls on her round head, was sitting on a settee.", "안나는 회색 실내복 차림으로, 둥근 머리에 짧게 무리 지어 곱슬거리는 검은 머리카락을 한 채 긴 의자에 앉아 있었다."], ["The eagerness died out of her face, as it always did, at the sight of her husband; she dropped her head and looked round uneasily at Betsy.", "늘 그렇듯 남편을 보자 그녀의 얼굴에서 생기가 사라졌다. 그녀는 고개를 떨구고 불안하게 베치를 돌아보았다."], ["Betsy, dressed in the height of the latest fashion, in a hat that towered somewhere over her head like a shade on a lamp, in a blue dress with violet crossway stripes slanting one way on the bodice and the other way on the skirt, was sitting beside Anna, her tall flat figure held erect.", "최신 유행의 절정을 따라 차려입은 베치는 어쩐지 머리 위로 등갓처럼 우뚝 솟은 모자를 쓰고, 몸통 부분에는 한 방향으로, 치마 부분에는 다른 방향으로 비스듬히 보라색 줄무늬가 가로지른 푸른 드레스를 입은 채, 크고 납작한 몸을 꼿꼿이 세우고 안나 곁에 앉아 있었다."], ["Bowing her head, she greeted Alexey Alexandrovitch with an ironical smile.", "고개를 숙이며 그녀는 빈정대는 미소로 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치를 맞았다."]], [["“Ah!”", "\"어머!\""], ["she said, as though surprised.", "그녀는 놀란 듯이 말했다."], ["“I’m very glad you’re at home.", "\"집에 계시다니 정말 반갑네요."], ["You never put in an appearance anywhere, and I haven’t seen you ever since Anna has been ill.", "어디에도 통 모습을 비치지 않으시니, 안나가 앓기 시작한 뒤로는 한 번도 뵙질 못했어요."], ["I have heard all about it—your anxiety.", "다 들었답니다, 당신이 얼마나 마음 졸였는지를요."], ["Yes, you’re a wonderful husband!”", "그래요, 당신은 훌륭한 남편이세요!\""], ["she said, with a meaning and affable air, as though she were bestowing an order of magnanimity on him for his conduct to his wife.", "그녀는 마치 아내를 대하는 그의 처신에 너그러움의 훈장이라도 수여하듯, 의미심장하면서도 상냥한 태도로 말했다."]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch bowed frigidly, and kissing his wife’s hand, asked how she was.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 차갑게 고개를 숙이고, 아내의 손에 입을 맞춘 뒤 몸은 좀 어떠냐고 물었다."]], [["“Better, I think,”", "\"나아진 것 같아요.\""], ["she said, avoiding his eyes.", "그녀는 그의 눈길을 피하며 말했다."]], [["“But you’ve rather a feverish-looking color,”", "\"하지만 안색이 좀 열이 있어 보이는군.\""], ["he said, laying stress on the word “feverish.”", "그는 '열이 있다'는 말에 힘주어 말했다."]], [["“We’ve been talking too much,” said Betsy.", "\"우리가 얘기를 너무 많이 했나 봐요.\""], ["“I feel it’s selfishness on my part, and I am going away.”", "베치가 말했다. \"제 이기심 탓인 것 같으니, 이만 가 볼게요.\""]], [["She got up, but Anna, suddenly flushing, quickly caught at her hand.", "그녀가 일어섰지만, 안나는 별안간 얼굴을 붉히며 재빨리 그녀의 손을 붙잡았다."]], [["“No, wait a minute, please.", "\"아니, 잠깐만 있어 줘요, 제발."], ["I must tell you ... no, you.”", "당신께 말씀드려야 할 게 있어요……"], ["she turned to Alexey Alexandrovitch, and her neck and brow were suffused with crimson.", "아니, 당신께요.\" 그녀는 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치를 향해 돌아섰고, 목과 이마가 새빨갛게 물들었다."], ["“I won’t and can’t keep anything secret from you,”", "\"당신께는 아무것도 숨기지 않을 거고, 숨길 수도 없어요.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch cracked his fingers and bowed his head.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 손가락을 우두둑 꺾고 고개를 숙였다."]], [["“Betsy’s been telling me that Count Vronsky wants to come here to say good-bye before his departure for Tashkend.”", "\"베치 말로는 브론스키 백작이 타슈켄트로 떠나기 전에 작별 인사를 하러 여기 오고 싶어 한다는군요.\""], ["She did not look at her husband, and was evidently in haste to have everything out, however hard it might be for her.", "그녀는 남편을 쳐다보지 않았고, 자기에게 아무리 힘든 일일지라도 모든 것을 다 털어놓으려고 서두르는 게 분명했다."], ["“I told her I could not receive him.”", "\"나는 그를 맞을 수 없다고 했어요.\""]], [["“You said, my dear, that it would depend on Alexey Alexandrovitch,”", "\"그건 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치께 달려 있다고 하셨잖아요.\""], ["Betsy corrected her.", "베치가 그녀의 말을 바로잡았다."]], [["“Oh, no, I can’t receive him; and what object would there....”", "\"아니에요, 나는 그를 맞을 수 없어요. 게다가 그게 무슨 소용이……\""], ["She stopped suddenly, and glanced inquiringly at her husband (he did not look at her).", "그녀는 갑자기 말을 멈추고 캐묻는 듯이 남편을 흘끗 보았다(그는 그녀를 쳐다보지 않았다)."], ["“In short, I don’t wish it....”", "\"한마디로, 나는 그걸 원하지 않아요……\""]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch advanced and would have taken her hand.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 다가가 그녀의 손을 잡으려 했다."]], [["Her first impulse was to jerk back her hand from the damp hand with big swollen veins that sought hers, but with an obvious effort to control herself she pressed his hand.", "그녀의 첫 충동은 굵게 부풀어 오른 핏줄이 도드라진 축축한 손이 자기 손을 찾아오자 그것을 홱 빼는 것이었지만, 그녀는 애써 자제하며 그의 손을 꼭 쥐었다."]], [["“I am very grateful to you for your confidence, but....”", "\"당신이 이렇게 믿고 말해 주니 정말 고맙소, 하지만……\""], ["he said, feeling with confusion and annoyance that what he could decide easily and clearly by himself, he could not discuss before Princess Tverskaya, who to him stood for the incarnation of that brute force which would inevitably control him in the life he led in the eyes of the world, and hinder him from giving way to his feeling of love and forgiveness.", "그는 혼자서라면 쉽고 명료하게 결정할 수 있는 일을 트베르스카야 공작 부인 앞에서는 의논할 수 없다는 것을 당혹스럽고도 짜증스럽게 느끼며 말했다. 그에게 그녀는, 세상의 눈앞에서 영위하는 삶 속에서 자신을 필연적으로 지배하며, 사랑과 용서의 감정에 자신을 내맡기지 못하게 가로막는 그 잔혹한 힘의 화신이었던 것이다."], ["He stopped short, looking at Princess Tverskaya.", "그는 트베르스카야 공작 부인을 바라보며 말을 멈추었다."]], [["“Well, good-bye, my darling,”", "\"그럼, 잘 있어요, 내 사랑.\""], ["said Betsy, getting up.", "베치가 일어서며 말했다."], ["She kissed Anna, and went out.", "그녀는 안나에게 입을 맞추고 밖으로 나갔다."], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch escorted her out.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 그녀를 배웅했다."]], [["“Alexey Alexandrovitch!", "\"알렉세이 알렉산드로비치!"], ["I know you are a truly magnanimous man,”", "저는 당신이 진정으로 너그러운 분이라는 걸 알아요.\""], ["said Betsy, stopping in the little drawing-room, and with special warmth shaking hands with him once more.", "베치는 작은 응접실에서 걸음을 멈추고, 유난히 따뜻하게 그와 다시 한번 악수하며 말했다."], ["“I am an outsider, but I so love her and respect you that I venture to advise.", "\"저는 남이지만, 그녀를 무척 사랑하고 당신을 존경하기에 감히 충고드리는 거예요."], ["Receive him.", "그를 맞아 주세요."], ["Alexey Vronsky is the soul of honor, and he is going away to Tashkend.”", "알렉세이 브론스키는 더없이 명예로운 사람이고, 타슈켄트로 떠난답니다.\""]], [["“Thank you, princess, for your sympathy and advice.", "\"공작 부인, 호의와 충고에 감사드립니다."], ["But the question of whether my wife can or cannot see anyone she must decide herself.”", "하지만 제 아내가 누구를 만날 수 있느냐 없느냐 하는 문제는 그녀 자신이 결정해야 합니다.\""]], [["He said this from habit, lifting his brows with dignity, and reflected immediately that whatever his words might be, there could be no dignity in his position.", "그는 위엄 있게 눈썹을 치켜올리며 습관처럼 이렇게 말했지만, 곧바로 자기 말이 어떻든 간에 자기 처지에는 위엄이라곤 있을 수 없다는 데에 생각이 미쳤다."], ["And he saw this by the suppressed, malicious, and ironical smile with which Betsy glanced at him after this phrase.", "그리고 그는 이 말이 끝난 뒤 베치가 자신을 흘끗 보며 지은, 억눌린 짓궂고 빈정대는 미소에서 그것을 알아차렸다."]], [["# Chapter 20", "# Chapter 20"]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch took leave of Betsy in the drawing-room, and went to his wife.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 응접실에서 베시와 작별 인사를 나누고 아내에게로 갔다."], ["She was lying down, but hearing his steps she sat up hastily in her former attitude, and looked in a scared way at him.", "그녀는 누워 있었지만 그의 발걸음 소리를 듣고는 아까의 자세로 황급히 몸을 일으켜 앉으며 겁먹은 듯 그를 바라보았다."], ["He saw she had been crying.", "그는 그녀가 울고 있었다는 것을 알아챘다."]], [["“I am very grateful for your confidence in me.”", "\"나에 대한 당신의 신뢰에 진심으로 감사하오.\""], ["He repeated gently in Russian the phrase he had said in Betsy’s presence in French, and sat down beside her.", "그는 베시 앞에서 프랑스어로 했던 말을 러시아어로 부드럽게 되풀이하고는 그녀 곁에 앉았다."], ["When he spoke to her in Russian, using the Russian “thou” of intimacy and affection, it was insufferably irritating to Anna.", "그가 러시아어로, 그것도 친밀함과 애정을 담은 러시아어의 '너'를 쓰며 말을 건넬 때면, 안나는 견딜 수 없이 짜증이 났다."], ["“And I am very grateful for your decision.", "\"그리고 당신의 결정에도 진심으로 감사하오."], ["I, too, imagine that since he is going away, there is no sort of necessity for Count Vronsky to come here.", "나 역시 그가 떠나는 마당에 브론스키 백작이 이리로 올 필요 따위는 전혀 없으리라 생각하오."], ["However, if....”", "하지만, 만약……\""]], [["“But I’ve said so already, so why repeat it?”", "\"하지만 그건 이미 말했잖아요."], ["Anna suddenly interrupted him with an irritation she could not succeed in repressing.", "그런데 왜 또 되풀이하시는 거예요?\" 안나는 도무지 억누를 수 없는 짜증을 드러내며 불쑥 그의 말을 가로막았다."], ["“No sort of necessity,”", "'필요 따위는 전혀 없다니.'"], ["she thought, “for a man to come and say good-bye to the woman he loves, for whom he was ready to ruin himself, and has ruined himself, and who cannot live without him.", "그녀는 생각했다. '사랑하는 여자, 그를 위해 자기 자신을 망칠 각오까지 했고 실제로 망쳐 버린 여자, 그가 없이는 살 수도 없는 여자에게 작별을 고하러 오는 데에 아무런 필요도 없다니."], ["No sort of necessity!”", "필요 따위는 전혀 없다니!'"], ["she compressed her lips, and dropped her burning eyes to his hands with their swollen veins.", "그녀는 입술을 꼭 다물고, 핏줄이 불거진 그의 손으로 타오르는 듯한 눈길을 떨어뜨렸다."], ["They were rubbing each other.", "그 두 손은 서로를 비비고 있었다."]], [["“Let us never speak of it,”", "\"우리 이 일에 대해서는 두 번 다시 말하지 말아요.\""], ["she added more calmly.", "그녀는 좀 더 차분하게 덧붙였다."]], [["“I have left this question to you to decide, and I am very glad to see....”", "\"이 문제는 당신이 결정하도록 맡겼고, 또 이렇게 보게 되어 무척 기쁘오……\""], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch was beginning.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 입을 떼기 시작했다."]], [["“That my wish coincides with your own,”", "\"제 바람이 당신 바람과 일치한다는 것을 말이죠.\""], ["she finished quickly, exasperated at his talking so slowly while she knew beforehand all he would say.", "그녀는 그가 무슨 말을 할지 미리 다 알면서도 그토록 느릿느릿 말하는 것에 부아가 치밀어 얼른 말을 맺어 버렸다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그렇소.\""], ["he assented; “and Princess Tverskaya’s interference in the most difficult private affairs is utterly uncalled for.", "그가 수긍했다. \"그리고 트베르스카야 공작 부인이 이토록 까다로운 사사로운 일에 끼어드는 것은 전혀 당치 않은 일이오."], ["She especially....”", "특히 그 여자는……\""]], [["“I don’t believe a word of what’s said about her,”", "\"그 사람에 대해 사람들이 하는 말은 한마디도 믿지 않아요.\""], ["said Anna quickly.", "안나가 얼른 말했다."], ["“I know she really cares for me.”", "\"그 사람이 진심으로 저를 아낀다는 걸 저는 알아요.\""]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch sighed and said nothing.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 한숨을 쉬고 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["She played nervously with the tassel of her dressing-gown, glancing at him with that torturing sensation of physical repulsion for which she blamed herself, though she could not control it.", "그녀는 신경질적으로 실내복의 술을 만지작거리며, 스스로도 어쩌지 못해 자책하면서도 어쩔 수 없는 그 육체적 혐오의 괴로운 감각에 사로잡힌 채 그를 흘끔거렸다."], ["Her only desire now was to be rid of his oppressive presence.", "지금 그녀의 유일한 바람은 그의 짓누르는 듯한 존재로부터 벗어나는 것뿐이었다."]], [["“I have just sent for the doctor,”", "\"방금 의사를 부르러 사람을 보냈소.\""], ["said Alexey Alexandrovitch.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 말했다."]], [["“I am very well; what do I want the doctor for?”", "\"저는 멀쩡해요. 의사가 왜 필요하죠?\""]], [["“No, the little one cries, and they say the nurse hasn’t enough milk.”", "\"아니, 아기가 우는데, 유모의 젖이 모자란다고들 하더군.\""]], [["“Why didn’t you let me nurse her, when I begged to?", "\"제가 그렇게 사정했을 때 왜 직접 젖을 먹이지 못하게 하셨어요?"], ["Anyway”", "어쨌든\""], ["(Alexey Alexandrovitch knew what was meant by that “anyway”), “she’s a baby, and they’re killing her.”", "(알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 그 '어쨌든'이 무엇을 뜻하는지 알고 있었다.) \"저 애는 갓난아이인데, 사람들이 저 애를 죽이고 있는 거예요.\""], ["She rang the bell and ordered the baby to be brought her.", "그녀는 종을 울려 아기를 데려오라고 일렀다."], ["“I begged to nurse her, I wasn’t allowed to, and now I’m blamed for it.”", "\"제가 젖을 먹이게 해 달라고 빌었지만 허락받지 못했고, 그런데 이제 와서 그것 때문에 비난을 받는군요.\""]], [["“I don’t blame....”", "\"나는 비난하는 게 아니라……\""]], [["“Yes, you do blame me!", "\"아니에요, 당신은 저를 비난하고 있어요!"], ["My God!", "오, 하느님!"], ["why didn’t I die!”", "저는 왜 죽지 않았을까요!\""], ["And she broke into sobs.", "그러고서 그녀는 흐느껴 울기 시작했다."], ["“Forgive me, I’m nervous, I’m unjust,”", "\"용서하세요, 제가 신경이 곤두서서, 부당하게 굴고 있어요.\""], ["she said, controlling herself, “but do go away....”", "그녀가 마음을 다잡으며 말했다. \"그러니 제발 나가 주세요……\""]], [["“No, it can’t go on like this,”", "\"안 되겠어, 이대로 계속될 수는 없어.\""], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch said to himself decidedly as he left his wife’s room.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 아내의 방을 나서며 단호하게 혼잣말을 했다."]], [["Never had the impossibility of his position in the world’s eyes, and his wife’s hatred of him, and altogether the might of that mysterious brutal force that guided his life against his spiritual inclinations, and exacted conformity with its decrees and change in his attitude to his wife, been presented to him with such distinctness as that day.", "세상의 눈에 비친 자기 처지의 가망 없음과, 자기를 향한 아내의 증오와, 무엇보다 자기의 정신적 성향을 거스르며 자기 삶을 끌고 가면서 그 명령에 순응할 것과 아내를 대하는 태도를 바꿀 것을 강요하는 그 알 수 없는 잔혹한 힘의 위력이, 그날만큼 또렷하게 그에게 다가온 적은 없었다."], ["He saw clearly that all the world and his wife expected of him something, but what exactly, he could not make out.", "그는 온 세상과 자기 아내가 자기에게서 무언가를 기대하고 있음을 분명히 알았지만, 그것이 정확히 무엇인지는 도무지 헤아릴 수가 없었다."], ["He felt that this was rousing in his soul a feeling of anger destructive of his peace of mind and of all the good of his achievement.", "그는 이것이 자기 영혼 속에, 마음의 평화와 자기가 이룬 모든 좋은 것을 파괴하는 분노의 감정을 불러일으키고 있음을 느꼈다."], ["He believed that for Anna herself it would be better to break off all relations with Vronsky; but if they all thought this out of the question, he was even ready to allow these relations to be renewed, so long as the children were not disgraced, and he was not deprived of them nor forced to change his position.", "그는 안나 자신을 위해서도 브론스키와의 관계를 모조리 끊는 편이 더 나으리라고 믿었다. 하지만 사람들 모두가 그것을 도저히 있을 수 없는 일이라 여긴다면, 아이들이 수치를 당하지 않고, 자기가 아이들을 빼앗기거나 자기 처지를 바꾸도록 강요당하지만 않는다면, 그는 그 관계가 다시 이어지도록 내버려 둘 각오마저 되어 있었다."], ["Bad as this might be, it was anyway better than a rupture, which would put her in a hopeless and shameful position, and deprive him of everything he cared for.", "그것이 아무리 나쁘다 한들, 어쨌든 파국보다는 나았다. 파국은 그녀를 가망도 없고 수치스러운 처지에 빠뜨리고, 그에게서 그가 소중히 여기는 모든 것을 앗아 갈 터였다."], ["But he felt helpless; he knew beforehand that everyone was against him, and that he would not be allowed to do what seemed to him now so natural and right, but would be forced to do what was wrong, though it seemed the proper thing to them.", "그러나 그는 무력함을 느꼈다. 그는 모두가 자기에게 맞서고 있다는 것을, 그리고 지금 자기에게 그토록 자연스럽고 옳게 여겨지는 일을 하도록 허락받지 못하고, 도리어 잘못된 일을, 그러나 그들에게는 마땅해 보이는 그 일을 하도록 강요당하리라는 것을 미리부터 알고 있었다."]], [["# Chapter 21", "# Chapter 21"]], [["Before Betsy had time to walk out of the drawing-room, she was met in the doorway by Stepan Arkadyevitch, who had just come from Yeliseev’s, where a consignment of fresh oysters had been received.", "베치가 미처 응접실에서 나서기도 전에, 그녀는 문간에서 스테판 아르카지치와 마주쳤다. 그는 막 옐리세예프 상점에서 오는 길이었는데, 거기에 신선한 굴 한 상자가 들어와 있었던 것이다."]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["princess!", "공작 부인!"], ["what a delightful meeting!”", "이렇게 반가운 만남이라니!\""], ["he began.", "그가 운을 뗐다."], ["“I’ve been to see you.”", "\"마침 부인을 뵈러 가던 참이었어요.\""]], [["“A meeting for one minute, for I’m going,”", "\"잠깐 동안만 뵐 수 있겠네요, 막 나가려던 참이라서요.\""], ["said Betsy, smiling and putting on her glove.", "베치가 미소를 지으며 장갑을 끼면서 말했다."]], [["“Don’t put on your glove yet, princess; let me kiss your hand.", "\"아직 장갑은 끼지 마세요, 공작 부인. 손에 입 맞추게 해 주세요."], ["There’s nothing I’m so thankful to the revival of the old fashions for as the kissing the hand.”", "옛 풍습이 되살아난 것 중에 손에 입 맞추는 것만큼 고마운 게 없답니다.\""], ["He kissed Betsy’s hand.", "그는 베치의 손에 입을 맞췄다."], ["“When shall we see each other?”", "\"언제 또 뵐 수 있을까요?\""]], [["“You don’t deserve it,”", "\"그럴 자격이 없으신데요.\""], ["answered Betsy, smiling.", "베치가 미소 지으며 대꾸했다."]], [["“Oh, yes, I deserve a great deal, for I’ve become a most serious person.", "\"오, 아니죠, 저는 자격이 충분합니다. 아주 진지한 사람이 되었거든요."], ["I don’t only manage my own affairs, but other people’s too,”", "제 일만 처리하는 게 아니라 남의 일까지 챙기고 있답니다.\""], ["he said, with a significant expression.", "그가 의미심장한 표정으로 말했다."]], [["“Oh, I’m so glad!”", "\"오, 정말 기뻐요!\""], ["answered Betsy, at once understanding that he was speaking of Anna.", "베치는 그가 안나 이야기를 하고 있다는 것을 곧바로 알아채고 대답했다."], ["And going back into the drawing-room, they stood in a corner.", "그러고는 두 사람은 응접실로 다시 들어가 한쪽 구석에 섰다."], ["“He’s killing her,”", "\"그이가 그녀를 죽이고 있어요.\""], ["said Betsy in a whisper full of meaning.", "베치가 의미심장하게 가득 찬 목소리로 속삭였다."], ["“It’s impossible, impossible....”", "\"이건 안 될 일이에요, 안 될 일이라고요…….\""]], [["“I’m so glad you think so,”", "\"그렇게 생각해 주시니 정말 기쁩니다.\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, shaking his head with a serious and sympathetically distressed expression, “that’s what I’ve come to Petersburg for.”", "스테판 아르카지치가 진지하고 동정 어린 괴로운 표정으로 고개를 저으며 말했다. \"바로 그 일 때문에 페테르부르크에 온 거니까요.\""]], [["“The whole town’s talking of it,”", "\"온 도시가 그 이야기뿐이에요.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“It’s an impossible position.", "\"있을 수 없는 처지예요."], ["She pines and pines away.", "그녀는 나날이 시들어 가고 있어요."], ["He doesn’t understand that she’s one of those women who can’t trifle with their feelings.", "그이는 그녀가 자기 감정을 가지고 장난칠 수 없는 그런 여자라는 걸 이해하지 못해요."], ["One of two things: either let him take her away, act with energy, or give her a divorce.", "둘 중 하나죠. 그이가 그녀를 데리고 떠나든가, 단호하게 행동하든가, 아니면 이혼을 해 주든가."], ["This is stifling her.”", "이러다간 그녀가 숨이 막혀 죽고 말 거예요.\""]], [["“Yes, yes ...", "\"그래요, 그래요……"], ["just so....”", "바로 그겁니다…….\""], ["Oblonsky said, sighing.", "오블론스키가 한숨을 쉬며 말했다."], ["“That’s what I’ve come for.", "\"그것 때문에 온 거예요."], ["At least not solely for that ...", "적어도 그것만을 위해서 온 건 아니지만요……"], ["I’ve been made a Kammerherr; of course, one has to say thank you.", "시종보로 임명되었거든요. 물론 감사 인사를 하러 가야지요."], ["But the chief thing was having to settle this.”", "하지만 가장 중요한 건 이 일을 매듭짓는 거였습니다.\""]], [["“Well, God help you!”", "\"그럼, 부디 잘되시길 빌어요!\""], ["said Betsy.", "베치가 말했다."]], [["After accompanying Betsy to the outside hall, once more kissing her hand above the glove, at the point where the pulse beats, and murmuring to her such unseemly nonsense that she did not know whether to laugh or be angry, Stepan Arkadyevitch went to his sister.", "스테판 아르카지치는 베치를 현관까지 배웅하면서 다시 한번 장갑 위쪽, 맥박이 뛰는 자리에 입을 맞추고, 그녀가 웃어야 할지 화를 내야 할지 모를 만큼 점잖지 못한 농지거리를 속삭인 다음, 누이에게로 갔다."], ["He found her in tears.", "그는 그녀가 눈물에 젖어 있는 것을 보았다."]], [["Although he happened to be bubbling over with good spirits, Stepan Arkadyevitch immediately and quite naturally fell into the sympathetic, poetically emotional tone which harmonized with her mood.", "비록 기분이 한껏 들떠 있었지만, 스테판 아르카지치는 곧장 아주 자연스럽게 그녀의 기분에 어울리는, 동정 어리고 시적으로 감상적인 어조로 빠져들었다."], ["He asked her how she was, and how she had spent the morning.", "그는 그녀에게 어떻게 지내는지, 아침나절을 어떻게 보냈는지 물었다."]], [["“Very, very miserably.", "\"아주, 아주 비참했어요."], ["Today and this morning and all past days and days to come,”", "오늘도, 오늘 아침도, 지나간 모든 날도, 앞으로 다가올 모든 날도요.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“I think you’re giving way to pessimism.", "\"내가 보기엔 네가 비관에 빠져 있는 것 같구나."], ["You must rouse yourself, you must look life in the face.", "기운을 차려야지, 인생을 똑바로 바라봐야 해."], ["I know it’s hard, but....”", "힘든 건 알지만…….\""]], [["“I have heard it said that women love men even for their vices,”", "\"여자들은 남자의 결점까지도 사랑한다는 말을 들은 적이 있어요.\""], ["Anna began suddenly, “but I hate him for his virtues.", "안나가 불쑥 입을 열었다. \"하지만 저는 그이의 미덕 때문에 그이가 미워요."], ["I can’t live with him.", "그이와는 같이 살 수 없어요."], ["Do you understand?", "아시겠어요?"], ["the sight of him has a physical effect on me, it makes me beside myself.", "그이를 보기만 해도 몸에 반응이 일어나요, 제정신이 아니게 돼요."], ["I can’t, I can’t live with him.", "그이와는 살 수 없어요, 살 수 없다고요."], ["What am I to do?", "어떻게 해야 하죠?"], ["I have been unhappy, and used to think one couldn’t be more unhappy, but the awful state of things I am going through now, I could never have conceived.", "저는 불행했어요, 그리고 이보다 더 불행할 수는 없으리라고 생각하곤 했어요. 그런데 지금 제가 겪고 있는 이 끔찍한 처지는 도저히 상상조차 할 수 없는 거였어요."], ["Would you believe it, that knowing he’s a good man, a splendid man, that I’m not worth his little finger, still I hate him.", "믿어지세요? 그이가 좋은 사람이라는 걸, 훌륭한 사람이라는 걸, 제가 그이의 새끼손가락만큼의 가치도 없다는 걸 알면서도, 그래도 그이가 미워요."], ["I hate him for his generosity.", "그이의 너그러움 때문에 그이가 미운 거예요."], ["And there’s nothing left for me but....”", "그러니 제게 남은 건 오직…….\""]], [["She would have said death, but Stepan Arkadyevitch would not let her finish.", "그녀는 죽음이라고 말하려 했지만, 스테판 아르카지치는 그 말을 끝맺도록 내버려 두지 않았다."]], [["“You are ill and overwrought,”", "\"넌 병들어 있고 신경이 곤두서 있어.\""], ["he said; “believe me, you’re exaggerating dreadfully.", "그가 말했다. \"내 말 믿으렴, 넌 지금 끔찍하게 과장하고 있어."], ["There’s nothing so terrible in it.”", "그렇게까지 무서운 일은 아니야.\""]], [["And Stepan Arkadyevitch smiled.", "그러면서 스테판 아르카지치는 미소를 지었다."], ["No one else in Stepan Arkadyevitch’s place, having to do with such despair, would have ventured to smile (the smile would have seemed brutal); but in his smile there was so much of sweetness and almost feminine tenderness that his smile did not wound, but softened and soothed.", "그런 절망을 마주한 처지에서 스테판 아르카지치 자리에 있었다면 누구도 감히 미소 지을 엄두를 내지 못했을 것이다(그 미소는 잔인하게 보였을 테니까). 하지만 그의 미소에는 더없이 다정하고 거의 여성적이라 할 만한 부드러움이 가득 담겨 있어서, 그 미소는 상처를 주기는커녕 마음을 누그러뜨리고 가라앉혀 주었다."], ["His gentle, soothing words and smiles were as soothing and softening as almond oil.", "그의 부드럽고 위로하는 말과 미소는 아몬드 기름처럼 사람을 진정시키고 누그러뜨려 주었다."], ["And Anna soon felt this.", "안나도 곧 그것을 느꼈다."]], [["“No, Stiva,”", "\"아니에요, 스티바.\""], ["she said, “I’m lost, lost!", "그녀가 말했다. \"저는 끝장났어요, 끝장났다고요!"], ["worse than lost!", "끝장난 것보다 더 나빠요!"], ["I can’t say yet that all is over; on the contrary, I feel that it’s not over.", "아직 모든 게 끝났다고 말할 수는 없어요. 오히려 끝나지 않았다는 게 느껴져요."], ["I’m an overstrained string that must snap.", "저는 끊어질 수밖에 없는 팽팽하게 당겨진 줄이에요."], ["But it’s not ended yet ...", "하지만 아직 끝나지 않았어요……"], ["and it will have a fearful end.”", "그리고 무서운 결말을 맞게 될 거예요.\""]], [["“No matter, we must let the string be loosened, little by little.", "\"상관없어, 줄을 조금씩, 조금씩 느슨하게 풀어 주면 되는 거야."], ["There’s no position from which there is no way of escape.”", "빠져나갈 길이 없는 처지란 없는 법이니까.\""]], [["“I have thought, and thought.", "\"생각하고, 또 생각했어요."], ["Only one....”", "오직 하나뿐이에요…….\""]], [["Again he knew from her terrified eyes that this one way of escape in her thought was death, and he would not let her say it.", "그녀의 겁에 질린 눈빛에서, 그는 그녀가 생각하는 그 하나뿐인 탈출구가 죽음이라는 것을 다시금 알아챘고, 그녀가 그 말을 하도록 내버려 두지 않았다."]], [["“Not at all,” he said.", "\"전혀 아니야.\""], ["“Listen to me.", "그가 말했다."], ["You can’t see your own position as I can.", "\"내 말 들어 봐. 넌 네 처지를 나만큼 볼 수가 없어."], ["Let me tell you candidly my opinion.”", "내 솔직한 생각을 말해 주마.\""], ["Again he smiled discreetly his almond-oil smile.", "그는 다시 신중하게 아몬드 기름 같은 미소를 지었다."], ["“I’ll begin from the beginning.", "\"처음부터 이야기를 시작하지."], ["You married a man twenty years older than yourself.", "넌 너보다 스무 살이나 많은 남자와 결혼했어."], ["You married him without love and not knowing what love was.", "사랑 없이, 사랑이 뭔지도 모른 채 결혼한 거야."], ["It was a mistake, let’s admit.”", "그게 잘못이었다고, 그건 인정하자.\""]], [["“A fearful mistake!”", "\"끔찍한 잘못이었죠!\""], ["said Anna.", "안나가 말했다."]], [["“But I repeat, it’s an accomplished fact.", "\"하지만 거듭 말하건대, 그건 이미 끝난 일이야."], ["Then you had, let us say, the misfortune to love a man not your husband.", "그러고 나서, 말하자면, 넌 남편이 아닌 남자를 사랑하는 불행을 겪었어."], ["That was a misfortune; but that, too, is an accomplished fact.", "그건 불행이었지. 하지만 그것 역시 이미 끝난 일이야."], ["And your husband knew it and forgave it.”", "그리고 네 남편은 그걸 알면서도 용서했어.\""], ["He stopped at each sentence, waiting for her to object, but she made no answer.", "그는 그녀가 반박하기를 기다리며 한 문장마다 말을 멈추었지만, 그녀는 아무 대답도 하지 않았다."], ["“That’s so.", "\"바로 그거야."], ["Now the question is: can you go on living with your husband?", "이제 문제는 이거지. 넌 네 남편과 계속 살아갈 수 있겠니?"], ["Do you wish it?", "네가 그걸 바라니?"], ["Does he wish it?”", "그이가 그걸 바라고?\""]], [["“I know nothing, nothing.”", "\"저는 아무것도 몰라요, 아무것도요.\""]], [["“But you said yourself that you can’t endure him.”", "\"하지만 그이를 견딜 수 없다고 네 입으로 말했잖니.\""]], [["“No, I didn’t say so. I deny it.", "\"아니에요, 그렇게 말하지 않았어요."], ["I can’t tell, I don’t know anything about it.”", "그 말은 부정하겠어요. 모르겠어요, 저는 그것에 대해 아무것도 모르겠어요.\""]], [["“Yes, but let....”", "\"그래, 하지만 어쨌든…….\""]], [["“You can’t understand.", "\"오빠는 이해 못 해요."], ["I feel I’m lying head downwards in a sort of pit, but I ought not to save myself.", "저는 마치 어떤 구덩이 속에 머리를 거꾸로 처박고 있는 기분이에요, 그런데도 저 자신을 구해서는 안 될 것 같아요."], ["And I can’t....”", "그리고 그럴 수도 없고요…….\""]], [["“Never mind, we’ll slip something under and pull you out.", "\"걱정 마라, 우리가 밑에 뭔가를 받쳐 너를 끌어 올려 줄 테니까."], ["I understand you: I understand that you can’t take it on yourself to express your wishes, your feelings.”", "난 너를 이해해. 네가 네 바람을, 네 감정을 스스로 입 밖에 낼 수 없다는 걸 이해한다고.\""]], [["“There’s nothing, nothing I wish ...", "\"바라는 건 아무것도 없어요, 아무것도요……"], ["except for it to be all over.”", "그저 모든 게 끝나기만을 바랄 뿐이에요.\""]], [["“But he sees this and knows it.", "\"하지만 그이도 이걸 보고 있고 알고 있어."], ["And do you suppose it weighs on him any less than on you?", "그리고 이게 너한테보다 그이한테 덜 무겁게 지워진다고 생각하니?"], ["You’re wretched, he’s wretched, and what good can come of it?", "너도 비참하고, 그이도 비참한데, 거기서 무슨 좋은 게 나올 수 있겠어?"], ["while divorce would solve the difficulty completely.”", "반면에 이혼은 그 어려움을 완전히 해결해 줄 거야.\""], ["With some effort Stepan Arkadyevitch brought out his central idea, and looked significantly at her.", "스테판 아르카지치는 다소 애를 쓰며 자신의 핵심 생각을 꺼내 놓고, 의미심장하게 그녀를 바라보았다."]], [["She said nothing, and shook her cropped head in dissent.", "그녀는 아무 말도 하지 않고, 짧게 깎은 머리를 저어 반대의 뜻을 나타냈다."], ["But from the look in her face, that suddenly brightened into its old beauty, he saw that if she did not desire this, it was simply because it seemed to her unattainable happiness.", "하지만 그녀의 얼굴 표정에서, 갑자기 예전의 아름다움으로 환하게 빛난 그 표정에서, 그는 그녀가 이것을 바라지 않는 것이 단지 그것이 이룰 수 없는 행복처럼 여겨지기 때문임을 알아차렸다."]], [["“I’m awfully sorry for you!", "\"네가 너무나 안쓰럽구나!"], ["And how happy I should be if I could arrange things!”", "그리고 내가 일을 잘 풀어낼 수만 있다면 얼마나 기쁠까!\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, smiling more boldly.", "스테판 아르카지치가 한층 대담하게 미소 지으며 말했다."], ["“Don’t speak, don’t say a word!", "\"말하지 마, 한 마디도 하지 마!"], ["God grant only that I may speak as I feel.", "그저 내가 느끼는 대로 말할 수 있게 해 주십사 하느님께 빌 뿐이야."], ["I’m going to him.”", "그이에게 가 보마.\""]], [["Anna looked at him with dreamy, shining eyes, and said nothing.", "안나는 꿈꾸는 듯 반짝이는 눈으로 그를 바라볼 뿐, 아무 말도 하지 않았다."]], [["# Chapter 22", "# Chapter 22"]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch, with the same somewhat solemn expression with which he used to take his presidential chair at his board, walked into Alexey Alexandrovitch’s room.", "스테판 아르카지치는 이사회에서 의장석에 앉을 때 짓던 것과 똑같이 다소 엄숙한 표정을 한 채 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 방으로 들어섰다."], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch was walking about his room with his hands behind his back, thinking of just what Stepan Arkadyevitch had been discussing with his wife.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 뒷짐을 진 채 방 안을 거닐며, 바로 스테판 아르카지치가 자기 아내와 의논하던 것과 똑같은 일을 생각하고 있었다."]], [["“I’m not interrupting you?”", "\"방해가 되지 않습니까?\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, on the sight of his brother-in-law becoming suddenly aware of a sense of embarrassment unusual with him.", "매부를 본 순간 평소 자기답지 않은 어떤 거북함을 문득 의식하게 된 스테판 아르카지치가 말했다."], ["To conceal this embarrassment he took out a cigarette case he had just bought that opened in a new way, and sniffing the leather, took a cigarette out of it.", "이 거북함을 감추려고 그는 얼마 전에 새로 산, 색다른 방식으로 열리는 담뱃갑을 꺼내어 가죽 냄새를 맡고는 그 안에서 담배 한 개비를 꺼냈다."]], [["“No.", "\"아니요."], ["Do you want anything?”", "무슨 볼일이라도 있습니까?\""], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch asked without eagerness.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 별다른 열의 없이 물었다."]], [["“Yes, I wished ...", "\"네, 저는……"], ["I wanted ...", "저는 하고 싶은……"], ["yes, I wanted to talk to you,”", "그래요, 당신과 이야기를 좀 하고 싶었습니다.\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, with surprise aware of an unaccustomed timidity.", "평소답지 않은 소심함을 깨닫고는 스스로 놀라며 스테판 아르카지치가 말했다."]], [["This feeling was so unexpected and so strange that he did not believe it was the voice of conscience telling him that what he was meaning to do was wrong.", "이 느낌은 너무도 뜻밖이고 너무도 이상해서, 그는 자기가 하려는 일이 옳지 않다고 말해 주는 양심의 소리라고는 믿지 못했다."]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch made an effort and struggled with the timidity that had come over him.", "스테판 아르카지치는 애써 마음을 다잡으며 자신을 덮친 소심함과 싸웠다."]], [["“I hope you believe in my love for my sister and my sincere affection and respect for you,”", "\"제가 누이를 사랑하는 마음과, 당신을 향한 진심 어린 애정과 존경을 당신이 믿어 주시리라 생각합니다.\""], ["he said, reddening.", "그는 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다."]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch stood still and said nothing, but his face struck Stepan Arkadyevitch by its expression of an unresisting sacrifice.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 가만히 선 채 아무 말도 하지 않았으나, 그 얼굴에 어린 저항 없는 희생의 표정이 스테판 아르카지치의 가슴을 쳤다."]], [["“I intended ...", "\"저는……"], ["I wanted to have a little talk with you about my sister and your mutual position,”", "누이와 두 분 사이의 처지에 대해 당신과 잠깐 이야기를 나누고 싶었습니다.\""], ["he said, still struggling with an unaccustomed constraint.", "그는 여전히 익숙지 않은 어색함과 싸우며 말했다."]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch smiled mournfully, looked at his brother-in-law, and without answering went up to the table, took from it an unfinished letter, and handed it to his brother-in-law.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 서글프게 미소를 짓고 매부를 바라보았으나, 대답은 하지 않고 탁자로 다가가더니 거기서 미처 끝맺지 못한 편지 한 통을 집어 매부에게 건넸다."]], [["“I think unceasingly of the same thing.", "\"저도 끊임없이 같은 일만 생각하고 있습니다."], ["And here is what I had begun writing, thinking I could say it better by letter, and that my presence irritates her,”", "이게 제가 쓰기 시작한 것인데, 직접 마주하는 것이 그녀를 자극하니 편지로 하는 편이 더 낫겠다 싶어서요.\""], ["he said, as he gave him the letter.", "그는 편지를 건네며 말했다."]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch took the letter, looked with incredulous surprise at the lusterless eyes fixed so immovably on him, and began to read.", "스테판 아르카지치는 편지를 받아 들고, 자기에게 그토록 꼼짝없이 고정된 광채 없는 두 눈을 믿기지 않는다는 듯 놀라워하며 바라보다가 읽기 시작했다."]], [["“I see that my presence is irksome to you.", "\"내가 곁에 있는 것이 당신에게 괴로운 일이라는 걸 알겠소."], ["Painful as it is to me to believe it, I see that it is so, and cannot be otherwise.", "나로서는 그것을 믿기가 고통스럽지만, 사실이 그렇고 달리 어쩔 수 없다는 것을 압니다."], ["I don’t blame you, and God is my witness that on seeing you at the time of your illness I resolved with my whole heart to forget all that had passed between us and to begin a new life.", "나는 당신을 탓하지 않으며, 당신이 앓던 때에 당신을 보고는 우리 사이에 있었던 모든 일을 잊고 새로운 삶을 시작하기로 온 마음을 다해 결심했음을 하느님이 증인이 되어 주실 것이오."], ["I do not regret, and shall never regret, what I have done; but I have desired one thing—your good, the good of your soul—and now I see I have not attained that.", "나는 내가 한 일을 후회하지 않으며 앞으로도 결코 후회하지 않을 것이오. 다만 나는 한 가지만을, 곧 당신의 행복, 당신 영혼의 행복을 바랐는데, 이제 보니 나는 그것을 이루지 못했소."], ["Tell me yourself what will give you true happiness and peace to your soul.", "무엇이 당신에게 진정한 행복과 영혼의 평화를 줄 수 있을지 당신 입으로 직접 말해 주시오."], ["I put myself entirely in your hands, and trust to your feeling of what’s right.”", "나는 나 자신을 온전히 당신 손에 맡기며, 무엇이 옳은가에 대한 당신의 감정을 믿겠소.\""]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch handed back the letter, and with the same surprise continued looking at his brother-in-law, not knowing what to say.", "스테판 아르카지치는 편지를 돌려주고는, 무슨 말을 해야 할지 모른 채 여전히 똑같은 놀라움으로 매부를 계속 바라보았다."], ["This silence was so awkward for both of them that Stepan Arkadyevitch’s lips began twitching nervously, while he still gazed without speaking at Karenin’s face.", "이 침묵이 두 사람 모두에게 너무도 어색하여, 스테판 아르카지치는 여전히 아무 말 없이 카레닌의 얼굴을 응시하는 동안 입술이 신경질적으로 씰룩거리기 시작했다."]], [["“That’s what I wanted to say to her,”", "\"이게 제가 그녀에게 하고 싶었던 말입니다.\""], ["said Alexey Alexandrovitch, turning away.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 몸을 돌리며 말했다."]], [["“Yes, yes....”", "\"네, 네…….\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, not able to answer for the tears that were choking him.", "목이 메는 눈물 때문에 대답을 못 하고 스테판 아르카지치가 말했다."]], [["“Yes, yes, I understand you,”", "\"네, 네, 당신 마음 이해합니다.\""], ["he brought out at last.", "그가 마침내 입을 뗐다."]], [["“I want to know what she would like,”", "\"저는 그녀가 무엇을 원하는지 알고 싶습니다.\""], ["said Alexey Alexandrovitch.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 말했다."]], [["“I am afraid she does not understand her own position.", "\"제 생각에는 그녀가 자기 처지를 제대로 이해하지 못하는 것 같습니다."], ["She is not a judge,”", "그녀는 판단을 내릴 사람이 못 됩니다.\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, recovering himself.", "스테판 아르카지치가 마음을 추스르며 말했다."], ["“She is crushed, simply crushed by your generosity.", "\"그녀는 당신의 너그러움에 짓눌려 있습니다, 그저 짓눌려 있어요."], ["If she were to read this letter, she would be incapable of saying anything, she would only hang her head lower than ever.”", "만일 이 편지를 읽는다면, 그녀는 아무 말도 못 하고 그저 그 어느 때보다 고개를 더 깊이 떨굴 뿐일 겁니다.\""]], [["“Yes, but what’s to be done in that case?", "\"그래요, 하지만 그렇다면 어떻게 해야 합니까?"], ["how explain, how find out her wishes?”", "어떻게 설명하고, 어떻게 그녀의 바람을 알아냅니까?\""]], [["“If you will allow me to give my opinion, I think that it lies with you to point out directly the steps you consider necessary to end the position.”", "\"제 의견을 말씀드려도 된다면, 이 처지를 끝내기 위해 필요하다고 생각되는 절차를 곧장 짚어 주시는 것이 당신께 달린 일이라고 봅니다.\""]], [["“So you consider it must be ended?”", "\"그러니까 당신은 이것이 반드시 끝나야 한다고 보는 겁니까?\""], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch interrupted him.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 그의 말을 가로막았다."], ["“But how?”", "\"하지만 어떻게요?\""], ["he added, with a gesture of his hands before his eyes not usual with him.", "그는 평소 자기답지 않게 두 손을 눈앞으로 가져가는 동작을 하며 덧붙였다."], ["“I see no possible way out of it.”", "\"저는 빠져나갈 길이 도무지 보이지 않습니다.\""]], [["“There is some way of getting out of every position,”", "\"어떤 처지든 빠져나갈 길은 있는 법입니다.\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, standing up and becoming more cheerful.", "스테판 아르카지치가 일어서며 한결 활기를 띠고 말했다."], ["“There was a time when you thought of breaking off....", "\"당신이 헤어질 생각을 했던 때가 있지 않았습니까……"], ["If you are convinced now that you cannot make each other happy....”", "이제 서로를 행복하게 해 줄 수 없다고 확신하신다면……\""]], [["“Happiness may be variously understood.", "\"행복은 여러 가지로 이해될 수 있지요."], ["But suppose that I agree to everything, that I want nothing: what way is there of getting out of our position?”", "하지만 가령 제가 모든 것에 동의하고 아무것도 바라지 않는다고 칩시다. 그렇다 한들 우리 처지에서 빠져나갈 길이 어디 있습니까?\""]], [["“If you care to know my opinion,”", "\"제 의견이 궁금하시다면,\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch with the same smile of softening, almond-oil tenderness with which he had been talking to Anna.", "스테판 아르카지치는 안나에게 이야기할 때와 똑같이 부드럽게 녹아드는, 편도유 같은 다정한 미소를 지으며 말했다."], ["His kindly smile was so winning that Alexey Alexandrovitch, feeling his own weakness and unconsciously swayed by it, was ready to believe what Stepan Arkadyevitch was saying.", "그의 다정한 미소가 어찌나 마음을 끄는지, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 자기 자신의 나약함을 느끼며 자기도 모르게 그것에 휩쓸려, 스테판 아르카지치가 하는 말을 믿을 마음의 준비가 되어 있었다."]], [["“She will never speak out about it.", "\"그녀는 결코 그것을 입 밖에 내지 않을 겁니다."], ["But one thing is possible, one thing she might desire,”", "하지만 한 가지는 가능하고, 한 가지는 그녀가 바랄 만한 일이지요.\""], ["he went on, “that is the cessation of your relations and all memories associated with them.", "그가 말을 이었다. \"그건 바로 두 분의 관계와 그에 얽힌 모든 기억을 끝내는 것입니다."], ["To my thinking, in your position what’s essential is the formation of a new attitude to one another.", "제 생각에 당신들의 처지에서 본질적인 것은 서로에 대한 새로운 태도를 세우는 일입니다."], ["And that can only rest on a basis of freedom on both sides.”", "그리고 그것은 오직 양쪽 모두의 자유라는 토대 위에서만 가능합니다.\""]], [["“Divorce,”", "\"이혼이로군요.\""], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch interrupted, in a tone of aversion.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 혐오감 어린 어조로 가로막았다."]], [["“Yes, I imagine that divorce—yes, divorce,”", "\"네, 제 생각에는 이혼이…… 그래요, 이혼이지요.\""], ["Stepan Arkadyevitch repeated, reddening.", "스테판 아르카지치가 얼굴을 붉히며 거듭 말했다."], ["“That is from every point of view the most rational course for married people who find themselves in the position you are in.", "\"그것이 당신들 같은 처지에 놓인 부부에게는 어느 모로 보나 가장 합리적인 길입니다."], ["What can be done if married people find that life is impossible for them together?", "부부가 함께 사는 것이 불가능하다는 걸 알게 되었을 때 달리 무엇을 할 수 있겠습니까?"], ["That may always happen.”", "그런 일은 언제든 일어날 수 있는 거니까요.\""]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch sighed heavily and closed his eyes.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 무겁게 한숨을 내쉬며 눈을 감았다."]], [["“There’s only one point to be considered: is either of the parties desirous of forming new ties?", "\"고려해야 할 점은 단 하나뿐입니다. 두 사람 중 어느 한쪽이 새로운 인연을 맺기를 바라는가 하는 것이지요."], ["If not, it is very simple,”", "만약 그렇지 않다면 아주 간단합니다.\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, feeling more and more free from constraint.", "스테판 아르카지치는 점점 더 거북함에서 벗어나는 것을 느끼며 말했다."]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch, scowling with emotion, muttered something to himself, and made no answer.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 격한 감정으로 얼굴을 찌푸린 채 혼자 무어라 중얼거렸을 뿐 아무 대답도 하지 않았다."], ["All that seemed so simple to Stepan Arkadyevitch, Alexey Alexandrovitch had thought over thousands of times.", "스테판 아르카지치에게는 그토록 간단해 보이는 그 모든 것을, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 수천 번이나 곱씹어 왔다."], ["And, so far from being simple, it all seemed to him utterly impossible.", "그리고 그에게는 간단하기는커녕 그 모든 것이 도무지 불가능해 보였다."], ["Divorce, the details of which he knew by this time, seemed to him now out of the question, because the sense of his own dignity and respect for religion forbade his taking upon himself a fictitious charge of adultery, and still more suffering his wife, pardoned and beloved by him, to be caught in the fact and put to public shame.", "이제는 그 세세한 절차까지 다 알게 된 이혼이, 그에게는 이제 말도 안 되는 일로 여겨졌다. 자신의 존엄에 대한 의식과 종교에 대한 존경이, 거짓으로 간통의 죄를 자기에게 뒤집어쓰는 것을, 더더욱 자기가 용서하고 사랑하는 아내가 현장에서 발각되어 세상의 망신을 당하게 두는 것을 금했기 때문이다."], ["Divorce appeared to him impossible also on other still more weighty grounds.", "이혼은 그 밖에도 한층 더 무거운 다른 이유들 때문에 그에게는 불가능해 보였다."]], [["What would become of his son in case of a divorce?", "이혼할 경우 아들은 어떻게 되겠는가?"], ["To leave him with his mother was out of the question.", "아들을 어미에게 맡긴다는 것은 말도 안 되는 일이었다."], ["The divorced mother would have her own illegitimate family, in which his position as a stepson and his education would not be good.", "이혼한 어미는 자기만의 사생아 가정을 꾸릴 테고, 그 안에서 의붓아들이라는 아이의 처지며 양육은 좋을 리가 없었다. 그렇다면 자기가 데리고 있을까?"], ["Keep him with him? He knew that would be an act of vengeance on his part, and that he did not want.", "그것은 자기 쪽의 복수가 되리라는 것을 그는 알았고, 그건 그가 바라는 바가 아니었다."], ["But apart from this, what more than all made divorce seem impossible to Alexey Alexandrovitch was, that by consenting to a divorce he would be completely ruining Anna.", "하지만 이것은 차치하더라도, 무엇보다도 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에게 이혼을 불가능하게 보이게 한 것은, 이혼에 동의함으로써 자기가 안나를 완전히 파멸시키게 되리라는 점이었다."], ["The saying of Darya Alexandrovna at Moscow, that in deciding on a divorce he was thinking of himself, and not considering that by this he would be ruining her irrevocably, had sunk into his heart.", "모스크바에서 다리아 알렉산드로브나가 한 말, 즉 그가 이혼을 결정하는 것은 자기 자신만 생각하는 것이며 이로써 그녀를 돌이킬 수 없이 파멸시키게 된다는 것을 헤아리지 않는 것이라던 그 말이 그의 가슴에 깊이 박혀 있었다."], ["And connecting this saying with his forgiveness of her, with his devotion to the children, he understood it now in his own way.", "그리고 이 말을 그녀를 향한 자신의 용서와, 아이들에 대한 자신의 헌신과 연결시켜, 그는 이제 그것을 자기 나름의 방식으로 이해했다."], ["To consent to a divorce, to give her her freedom, meant in his thoughts to take from himself the last tie that bound him to life—the children whom he loved; and to take from her the last prop that stayed her on the path of right, to thrust her down to her ruin.", "이혼에 동의하여 그녀에게 자유를 주는 것은, 그의 생각으로는 자기를 삶에 붙잡아 두는 마지막 끈—그가 사랑하는 아이들—을 자기 자신에게서 빼앗는 것이요, 그녀를 올바른 길 위에 머물게 하는 마지막 버팀목을 그녀에게서 빼앗아 그녀를 파멸로 떠미는 것이었다."], ["If she were divorced, he knew she would join her life to Vronsky’s, and their tie would be an illegitimate and criminal one, since a wife, by the interpretation of the ecclesiastical law, could not marry while her husband was living.", "만일 그녀가 이혼한다면, 그녀가 자기 삶을 브론스키의 삶과 합치리라는 것을 그는 알았고, 그들의 결합은 부정하고 죄스러운 것이 될 터였다. 교회법의 해석에 따르면 아내는 남편이 살아 있는 동안에는 재혼할 수 없기 때문이었다."], ["“She will join him, and in a year or two he will throw her over, or she will form a new tie,”", "\"그녀는 그와 합치겠지, 그리고 한두 해 뒤면 그는 그녀를 내팽개치거나, 아니면 그녀가 새로운 인연을 맺겠지.\""], ["thought Alexey Alexandrovitch.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 생각했다."], ["“And I, by agreeing to an unlawful divorce, shall be to blame for her ruin.”", "\"그러면 나는 부정한 이혼에 동의함으로써 그녀의 파멸에 책임을 지게 되는 거야.\""], ["He had thought it all over hundreds of times, and was convinced that a divorce was not at all simple, as Stepan Arkadyevitch had said, but was utterly impossible.", "그는 이 모든 것을 수백 번이나 곱씹었고, 스테판 아르카지치가 말한 것처럼 이혼이 결코 간단한 것이 아니라 도무지 불가능한 일임을 확신하고 있었다. 그는 스테판 아르카지치가 자기에게 하는 말을 한마디도 믿지 않았다."], ["He did not believe a single word Stepan Arkadyevitch said to him; to every word he had a thousand objections to make, but he listened to him, feeling that his words were the expression of that mighty brutal force which controlled his life and to which he would have to submit.", "그 한마디 한마디마다 그는 수천 가지 반박할 거리가 있었지만, 그래도 그의 말에 귀를 기울였다. 그 말들이 자기 삶을 지배하며 자기가 끝내 굴복해야만 할 그 거대하고 야만적인 힘의 표현임을 느꼈기 때문이다."]], [["“The only question is on what terms you agree to give her a divorce.", "\"문제는 단지 어떤 조건으로 그녀에게 이혼을 허락하실 것인가 하는 것뿐입니다."], ["She does not want anything, does not dare ask you for anything, she leaves it all to your generosity.”", "그녀는 아무것도 바라지 않고, 감히 당신께 아무것도 청하지 못하며, 모든 것을 당신의 너그러움에 맡기고 있습니다.\""]], [["“My God, my God!", "\"맙소사, 맙소사!"], ["what for?”", "무엇 때문에?\""], ["thought Alexey Alexandrovitch, remembering the details of divorce proceedings in which the husband took the blame on himself, and with just the same gesture with which Vronsky had done the same, he hid his face for shame in his hands.", "남편이 죄를 스스로 뒤집어쓰는 이혼 소송의 세세한 절차를 떠올리며 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 생각했다. 그러고는 브론스키가 똑같은 짓을 할 때 했던 것과 똑같은 동작으로, 부끄러움에 두 손에 얼굴을 파묻었다."]], [["“You are distressed, I understand that.", "\"괴로우신 거 압니다."], ["But if you think it over....”", "하지만 곰곰이 생각해 보시면……\""]], [["“Whosoever shall smite thee on thy right cheek, turn to him the other also; and if any man take away thy coat, let him have thy cloak also,”", "\"누구든지 네 오른뺨을 치거든 왼뺨마저 돌려 대고, 또 누가 네 속옷을 가지려 하거든 겉옷까지도 내주어라.\""], ["thought Alexey Alexandrovitch.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 생각했다."]], [["“Yes, yes!”", "\"네, 네!\""], ["he cried in a shrill voice.", "그가 날카로운 목소리로 외쳤다."], ["“I will take the disgrace on myself, I will give up even my son, but ...", "\"치욕은 제가 떠맡겠습니다, 아들까지도 포기하겠습니다, 하지만……"], ["but wouldn’t it be better to let it alone?", "하지만 그냥 그대로 두는 편이 낫지 않겠습니까?"], ["Still you may do as you like....”", "어쨌든 당신 좋을 대로 하십시오……\""]], [["And turning away so that his brother-in-law could not see him, he sat down on a chair at the window.", "그러고는 매부가 자기를 보지 못하도록 몸을 돌려 창가의 의자에 앉았다."], ["There was bitterness, there was shame in his heart, but with bitterness and shame he felt joy and emotion at the height of his own meekness.", "그의 마음속에는 쓰라림이 있었고 부끄러움이 있었지만, 그 쓰라림과 부끄러움과 더불어 그는 자기 자신의 온순함의 절정에서 기쁨과 감동을 느꼈다."]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch was touched.", "스테판 아르카지치는 마음이 뭉클했다."], ["He was silent for a space.", "그는 잠시 말이 없었다."]], [["“Alexey Alexandrovitch, believe me, she appreciates your generosity,”", "\"알렉세이 알렉산드로비치, 제 말을 믿으십시오, 그녀는 당신의 너그러움을 고맙게 여기고 있습니다.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“But it seems it was the will of God,”", "\"하지만 이건 아무래도 하느님의 뜻이었던 모양입니다.\""], ["he added, and as he said it felt how foolish a remark it was, and with difficulty repressed a smile at his own foolishness.", "그가 덧붙였는데, 말을 하면서 그것이 얼마나 어리석은 말인지를 느끼고는 자기 자신의 어리석음에 터져 나오는 웃음을 간신히 억눌렀다."]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch would have made some reply, but tears stopped him.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 무슨 대답이라도 하려 했지만, 눈물이 그를 가로막았다."]], [["“This is an unhappy fatality, and one must accept it as such.", "\"이것은 불행한 숙명이며, 그런 것으로 받아들여야만 합니다."], ["I accept the calamity as an accomplished fact, and am doing my best to help both her and you,”", "저는 이 재앙을 이미 이루어진 사실로 받아들이고 있고, 그녀와 당신 두 분 모두를 돕기 위해 최선을 다하고 있습니다.\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch.", "스테판 아르카지치가 말했다."]], [["When he went out of his brother-in-law’s room he was touched, but that did not prevent him from being glad he had successfully brought the matter to a conclusion, for he felt certain Alexey Alexandrovitch would not go back on his words.", "매부의 방을 나설 때 그는 마음이 뭉클했으나, 그렇다고 일을 성공적으로 매듭지었다는 데 대한 기쁨이 가시지는 않았다. 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 자기 말을 번복하지 않으리라고 확신했기 때문이다."], ["To this satisfaction was added the fact that an idea had just struck him for a riddle turning on his successful achievement, that when the affair was over he would ask his wife and most intimate friends.", "이 만족감에 더하여, 마침 이 일을 성공적으로 해낸 것을 소재로 한 수수께끼 하나가 그의 머리에 떠올랐다는 사실까지 보태졌다. 일이 다 끝나면 그것을 아내와 가장 가까운 친구들에게 내볼 작정이었다."], ["He put this riddle into two or three different ways.", "그는 이 수수께끼를 두세 가지 다른 방식으로 만들어 보았다."], ["“But I’ll work it out better than that,”", "\"아니, 이것보다는 더 잘 다듬어야겠어.\""], ["he said to himself with a smile.", "그는 미소를 지으며 혼잣말했다."]], [["# Chapter 23", "# Chapter 23"]], [["Vronsky’s wound had been a dangerous one, though it did not touch the heart, and for several days he had lain between life and death.", "브론스키의 상처는 위험한 것이었다. 심장은 다치지 않았지만, 며칠 동안 그는 삶과 죽음 사이를 오갔다."], ["The first time he was able to speak, Varya, his brother’s wife, was alone in the room.", "그가 처음으로 말을 할 수 있게 되었을 때, 방 안에는 그의 형수인 바랴 혼자 있었다."]], [["“Varya,”", "\"바랴.\""], ["he said, looking sternly at her, “I shot myself by accident.", "그가 그녀를 엄하게 바라보며 말했다. \"나는 실수로 총을 쏜 거예요."], ["And please never speak of it, and tell everyone so.", "그러니 부디 그 일에 대해서는 절대 말하지 말고, 모두에게도 그렇게 전해 줘요."], ["Or else it’s too ridiculous.”", "그러지 않으면 너무 우스꽝스러우니까.\""]], [["Without answering his words, Varya bent over him, and with a delighted smile gazed into his face.", "그의 말에는 아무 대꾸도 하지 않고, 바랴는 그에게 몸을 숙인 채 기쁨에 찬 미소를 지으며 그의 얼굴을 들여다보았다."], ["His eyes were clear, not feverish; but their expression was stern.", "그의 눈은 맑았고, 열에 들뜨지도 않았다. 다만 그 표정만은 엄했다."]], [["“Thank God!”", "\"하느님, 감사합니다!\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“You’re not in pain?”", "\"아프지는 않아요?\""]], [["“A little here.”", "\"여기가 조금.\""], ["He pointed to his breast.", "그는 자기 가슴을 가리켰다."]], [["“Then let me change your bandages.”", "\"그럼 붕대를 갈아 줄게요.\""]], [["In silence, stiffening his broad jaws, he looked at her while she bandaged him up.", "그는 말없이 넓은 턱을 굳힌 채, 그녀가 붕대를 감는 동안 그녀를 바라보았다."], ["When she had finished he said:", "그녀가 다 마치자 그가 말했다."]], [["“I’m not delirious.", "\"나는 헛소리를 하는 게 아니에요."], ["Please manage that there may be no talk of my having shot myself on purpose.”", "부디 내가 일부러 총을 쐈다는 말이 나돌지 않도록 해 줘요.\""]], [["“No one does say so.", "\"아무도 그런 말 안 해요."], ["Only I hope you won’t shoot yourself by accident any more,”", "다만 다시는 실수로 총을 쏘는 일이 없기를 바랄 뿐이에요.\""], ["she said, with a questioning smile.", "그녀가 묻는 듯한 미소를 띠며 말했다."]], [["“Of course I won’t, but it would have been better....”", "\"물론 그러지 않을 거예요. 하지만 차라리 그편이 나았을 텐데…….\""]], [["And he smiled gloomily.", "그러고는 음울하게 미소 지었다."]], [["In spite of these words and this smile, which so frightened Varya, when the inflammation was over and he began to recover, he felt that he was completely free from one part of his misery.", "바랴를 그토록 두렵게 했던 이 말과 이 미소에도 불구하고, 염증이 가라앉고 회복되기 시작하자 그는 자기 고통의 한 부분에서 완전히 벗어났음을 느꼈다."], ["By his action he had, as it were, washed away the shame and humiliation he had felt before.", "그 행동으로 그는 이전에 느꼈던 수치와 굴욕을 말하자면 씻어 낸 셈이었다."], ["He could now think calmly of Alexey Alexandrovitch.", "이제 그는 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치를 차분히 생각할 수 있었다."], ["He recognized all his magnanimity, but he did not now feel himself humiliated by it.", "그는 그 사람의 너그러움을 모두 인정했지만, 이제는 그 때문에 굴욕감을 느끼지는 않았다."], ["Besides, he got back again into the beaten track of his life.", "게다가 그는 다시 자기 삶의 익숙한 궤도로 돌아왔다."], ["He saw the possibility of looking men in the face again without shame, and he could live in accordance with his own habits.", "그는 부끄러움 없이 다시 사람들의 얼굴을 마주 볼 수 있게 되었고, 자기 습관대로 살아갈 수 있었다."], ["One thing he could not pluck out of his heart, though he never ceased struggling with it, was the regret, amounting to despair, that he had lost her forever.", "다만 그가 끊임없이 맞서 싸웠으면서도 가슴에서 도려내지 못한 한 가지는, 그녀를 영원히 잃었다는 절망에 가까운 회한이었다."], ["That now, having expiated his sin against the husband, he was bound to renounce her, and never in future to stand between her with her repentance and her husband, he had firmly decided in his heart; but he could not tear out of his heart his regret at the loss of her love, he could not erase from his memory those moments of happiness that he had so little prized at the time, and that haunted him in all their charm.", "이제 남편에 대한 자기 죄를 속죄한 이상 그녀를 단념해야 하며, 앞으로는 결코 그녀와 그녀의 참회와 그녀의 남편 사이에 끼어들지 않겠다고 그는 마음속으로 굳게 다짐했다. 하지만 그녀의 사랑을 잃은 회한만큼은 가슴에서 뜯어낼 수 없었고, 그 당시에는 그토록 하찮게 여겼으나 이제는 온갖 매혹으로 그를 사로잡는 그 행복의 순간들을 기억에서 지울 수가 없었다."]], [["Serpuhovskoy had planned his appointment at Tashkend, and Vronsky agreed to the proposition without the slightest hesitation.", "세르푸호프스코이가 그를 타슈켄트에 임명되도록 손을 써 두었고, 브론스키는 조금의 망설임도 없이 그 제안을 받아들였다."], ["But the nearer the time of departure came, the bitterer was the sacrifice he was making to what he thought his duty.", "그러나 출발할 날이 가까워질수록, 의무라고 여기는 것에 바치는 그 희생은 더욱 쓰라리게 다가왔다."]], [["His wound had healed, and he was driving about making preparations for his departure for Tashkend.", "상처가 아물자, 그는 타슈켄트로 떠날 채비를 하느라 여기저기 마차를 몰고 다녔다."]], [["“To see her once and then to bury myself, to die,”", "'그녀를 한 번만 보고, 그런 다음 나 자신을 묻어 버리는 거야, 죽어 버리는 거지.'"], ["he thought, and as he was paying farewell visits, he uttered this thought to Betsy.", "그는 생각했다. 그리고 작별 인사차 방문을 다니던 중 이 생각을 베치에게 털어놓았다."], ["Charged with this commission, Betsy had gone to Anna, and brought him back a negative reply.", "그 부탁을 맡은 베치는 안나를 찾아갔다가, 그에게 거절의 답을 가지고 돌아왔다."]], [["“So much the better,”", "'차라리 잘됐어.'"], ["thought Vronsky, when he received the news.", "그 소식을 받았을 때 브론스키는 생각했다."], ["“It was a weakness, which would have shattered what strength I have left.”", "'그건 약한 마음이었고, 내게 남아 있는 힘마저 산산조각 냈을 테니까.'"]], [["Next day Betsy herself came to him in the morning, and announced that she had heard through Oblonsky as a positive fact that Alexey Alexandrovitch had agreed to a divorce, and that therefore Vronsky could see Anna.", "이튿날 아침 베치가 직접 그를 찾아와, 오블론스키를 통해 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 이혼에 동의했다는 사실을 확실히 들었으며, 따라서 브론스키가 안나를 만날 수 있게 되었다고 알려 주었다."]], [["Without even troubling himself to see Betsy out of his flat, forgetting all his resolutions, without asking when he could see her, where her husband was, Vronsky drove straight to the Karenins’.", "베치를 집 밖까지 배웅하는 수고조차 잊은 채, 자기가 했던 모든 결심을 잊은 채, 언제 그녀를 만날 수 있는지, 그녀의 남편은 어디 있는지도 묻지 않은 채, 브론스키는 곧장 카레닌 집으로 마차를 몰았다."], ["He ran up the stairs seeing no one and nothing, and with a rapid step, almost breaking into a run, he went into her room.", "그는 아무것도 누구도 보지 않으며 계단을 뛰어올랐고, 거의 달음박질에 가까운 빠른 걸음으로 그녀의 방으로 들어섰다."], ["And without considering, without noticing whether there was anyone in the room or not, he flung his arms round her, and began to cover her face, her hands, her neck with kisses.", "그리고 방 안에 누가 있는지 없는지 헤아리지도 알아차리지도 않은 채, 그는 그녀를 두 팔로 와락 끌어안고는 그녀의 얼굴, 두 손, 목에 입맞춤을 퍼붓기 시작했다."]], [["Anna had been preparing herself for this meeting, had thought what she would say to him, but she did not succeed in saying anything of it; his passion mastered her.", "안나는 이 만남에 대비해 그에게 무슨 말을 할지 생각해 두었지만, 그중 어느 것도 입에 올리지 못했다. 그의 열정이 그녀를 사로잡아 버린 것이다."], ["She tried to calm him, to calm herself, but it was too late.", "그녀는 그를 진정시키고 자기 자신도 진정시키려 애썼지만, 이미 너무 늦었다."], ["His feeling infected her.", "그의 감정이 그녀에게 옮아왔다."], ["Her lips trembled so that for a long while she could say nothing.", "그녀의 입술이 어찌나 떨렸던지 한참 동안 그녀는 아무 말도 할 수 없었다."]], [["“Yes, you have conquered me, and I am yours,”", "\"그래요, 당신은 나를 정복했어요, 나는 당신의 것이에요.\""], ["she said at last, pressing his hands to her bosom.", "마침내 그녀가 그의 두 손을 자기 가슴에 꼭 누르며 말했다."]], [["“So it had to be,”", "\"이렇게 될 수밖에 없었어요.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“So long as we live, it must be so.", "\"우리가 살아 있는 한, 이렇게 되어야 했던 거예요."], ["I know it now.”", "이제 나는 그걸 알겠어요.\""]], [["“That’s true,”", "\"맞아요.\""], ["she said, getting whiter and whiter, and embracing his head.", "그녀가 점점 더 창백해지며 그의 머리를 끌어안고 말했다."], ["“Still there is something terrible in it after all that has happened.”", "\"하지만 그래도 이 모든 일이 있고 난 뒤에 보면, 거기엔 뭔가 끔찍한 게 있어요.\""]], [["“It will all pass, it will all pass; we shall be so happy.", "\"다 지나갈 거예요, 다 지나갈 거예요. 우리는 무척 행복해질 거예요."], ["Our love, if it could be stronger, will be strengthened by there being something terrible in it,”", "우리 사랑은, 더 강해질 수 있다면, 그 안에 뭔가 끔찍한 것이 있음으로 해서 더 강해질 거예요.\""], ["he said, lifting his head and parting his strong teeth in a smile.", "그가 고개를 들고, 튼튼한 이를 드러내며 미소 지은 채 말했다."]], [["And she could not but respond with a smile—not to his words, but to the love in his eyes.", "그러자 그녀도 미소로 답하지 않을 수 없었다. 그의 말이 아니라 그의 눈에 어린 사랑에 대한 답이었다."], ["She took his hand and stroked her chilled cheeks and cropped head with it.", "그녀는 그의 손을 잡아, 그것으로 차갑게 식은 자기 두 뺨과 짧게 깎은 머리를 쓰다듬었다."]], [["“I don’t know you with this short hair.", "\"이렇게 머리가 짧아진 당신이 낯설어요."], ["You’ve grown so pretty.", "정말 예뻐졌어요. 꼭 소년 같아요."], ["A boy. But how pale you are!”", "그런데 어쩜 이렇게 창백할까요!\""]], [["“Yes, I’m very weak,”", "\"그래요, 나는 아주 약해졌어요.\""], ["she said, smiling.", "그녀가 미소 지으며 말했다."], ["And her lips began trembling again.", "그리고 그녀의 입술이 다시 떨리기 시작했다."]], [["“We’ll go to Italy; you will get strong,”", "\"우리 이탈리아로 가요. 거기서 당신은 건강해질 거예요.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“Can it be possible we could be like husband and wife, alone, your family with you?”", "\"우리가 정말 부부처럼, 단둘이서, 당신의 가족과 함께 있을 수 있을까요?\""], ["she said, looking close into his eyes.", "그녀가 그의 눈을 가까이 들여다보며 말했다."]], [["“It only seems strange to me that it can ever have been otherwise.”", "\"내겐 오히려 그렇지 않은 적이 있었다는 게 이상하게만 느껴져요.\""]], [["“Stiva says that he has agreed to everything, but I can’t accept his generosity,”", "\"스티바 말로는 그이가 모든 것에 동의했다고 하지만, 나는 그이의 너그러움을 받아들일 수가 없어요.\""], ["she said, looking dreamily past Vronsky’s face.", "그녀가 브론스키의 얼굴 너머를 꿈꾸듯 바라보며 말했다."], ["“I don’t want a divorce; it’s all the same to me now.", "\"나는 이혼을 원하지 않아요. 이제 내게는 다 마찬가지예요."], ["Only I don’t know what he will decide about Seryozha.”", "다만 그이가 세료자를 두고 무슨 결정을 내릴지 모르겠어요.\""]], [["He could not conceive how at this moment of their meeting she could remember and think of her son, of divorce.", "그는 두 사람이 만나는 이 순간에 어떻게 그녀가 아들을, 이혼을 떠올리고 생각할 수 있는지 이해할 수 없었다."], ["What did it all matter?", "그게 다 무슨 상관이란 말인가?"]], [["“Don’t speak of that, don’t think of it,”", "\"그런 이야긴 하지 말아요, 그런 생각 하지 말아요.\""], ["he said, turning her hand in his, and trying to draw her attention to him; but still she did not look at him.", "그가 그녀의 손을 자기 손안에서 돌려 쥐며, 그녀의 주의를 자기에게 끌려 애쓰며 말했다. 하지만 그녀는 여전히 그를 바라보지 않았다."]], [["“Oh, why didn’t I die!", "\"아, 왜 나는 죽지 않았을까!"], ["it would have been better,”", "차라리 그편이 나았을 텐데.\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["she said, and silent tears flowed down both her cheeks; but she tried to smile, so as not to wound him.", "그러자 소리 없는 눈물이 그녀의 두 뺨을 타고 흘러내렸다. 하지만 그녀는 그에게 상처를 주지 않으려고 미소 지으려 애썼다."]], [["To decline the flattering and dangerous appointment at Tashkend would have been, Vronsky had till then considered, disgraceful and impossible.", "타슈켄트의 그 명예롭고도 위험한 임명을 거절하는 것은, 그때까지만 해도 브론스키가 생각하기에 수치스럽고 불가능한 일이었다."], ["But now, without an instant’s consideration, he declined it, and observing dissatisfaction in the most exalted quarters at this step, he immediately retired from the army.", "그러나 이제 그는 한순간도 망설이지 않고 그것을 거절했고, 이 처사에 대해 최고위층에서 불만을 품는 것을 보고는 곧바로 군에서 물러났다."]], [["A month later Alexey Alexandrovitch was left alone with his son in his house at Petersburg, while Anna and Vronsky had gone abroad, not having obtained a divorce, but having absolutely declined all idea of one.", "한 달 뒤 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 페테르부르크의 자기 집에 아들과 단둘이 남았고, 한편 안나와 브론스키는 이혼을 얻어 내지도 않은 채, 오히려 이혼이라는 생각 자체를 완전히 거부한 채 외국으로 떠났다."]], [["# PART FIVE Chapter 1", "# PART FIVE Chapter 1"]], [["Princess Shtcherbatskaya considered that it was out of the question for the wedding to take place before Lent, just five weeks off, since not half the trousseau could possibly be ready by that time.", "시쳬르바츠카야 공작 부인은 결혼식을 사순절 전에, 그러니까 겨우 다섯 주밖에 남지 않은 그때 치르는 것은 도무지 말이 안 된다고 여겼다. 그때까지 혼수의 절반도 채 준비할 수 없을 게 분명했기 때문이다."], ["But she could not but agree with Levin that to fix it for after Lent would be putting it off too late, as an old aunt of Prince Shtcherbatsky’s was seriously ill and might die, and then the mourning would delay the wedding still longer.", "그렇지만 사순절 후로 미루면 너무 늦어진다는 레빈의 말에는 동의하지 않을 수 없었다. 시쳬르바츠키 공작의 늙은 고모가 위중해서 죽을지도 모르는데, 그렇게 되면 상중이라 결혼식이 더더욱 늦어질 터였기 때문이다."], ["And therefore, deciding to divide the trousseau into two parts—a larger and smaller trousseau—the princess consented to have the wedding before Lent.", "그래서 부인은 혼수를 큰 혼수와 작은 혼수, 두 부분으로 나누기로 결정하고 사순절 전에 결혼식을 치르는 데 동의했다."], ["She determined that she would get the smaller part of the trousseau all ready now, and the larger part should be made later, and she was much vexed with Levin because he was incapable of giving her a serious answer to the question whether he agreed to this arrangement or not.", "그녀는 작은 부분의 혼수는 지금 당장 모두 준비하고, 큰 부분은 나중에 마련하기로 마음먹었다. 그러면서 레빈에게 몹시 화가 났는데, 이 방안에 동의하느냐 마느냐는 질문에 그가 진지한 대답 하나 내놓지 못했기 때문이었다."], ["The arrangement was the more suitable as, immediately after the wedding, the young people were to go to the country, where the more important part of the trousseau would not be wanted.", "이 방안은 더없이 적절했다. 결혼식 직후 두 젊은이는 시골로 내려갈 예정이었고, 그곳에서는 더 중요한 부분의 혼수가 필요 없을 테니 말이다."]], [["Levin still continued in the same delirious condition in which it seemed to him that he and his happiness constituted the chief and sole aim of all existence, and that he need not now think or care about anything, that everything was being done and would be done for him by others.", "레빈은 여전히 자기와 자기 행복이야말로 모든 존재의 주된, 유일한 목적이며, 이제는 아무것도 생각하거나 신경 쓸 필요가 없고, 모든 일이 다른 사람들에 의해 진행되고 있고 또 진행될 거라고 여겨지는 그 도취 상태에 계속 빠져 있었다."], ["He had not even plans and aims for the future, he left its arrangement to others, knowing that everything would be delightful.", "그는 미래에 대한 계획이나 목적조차 없었고, 모든 것이 멋지게 풀릴 것을 알기에 그 채비를 남들에게 맡겼다."], ["His brother Sergey Ivanovitch, Stepan Arkadyevitch, and the princess guided him in doing what he had to do.", "그의 형 세르게이 이바노비치와 스테판 아르카지치, 그리고 공작 부인이 그가 해야 할 일을 하도록 그를 이끌었다."], ["All he did was to agree entirely with everything suggested to him.", "그가 한 일이라곤 자기에게 제안된 모든 것에 전적으로 동의하는 것뿐이었다."], ["His brother raised money for him, the princess advised him to leave Moscow after the wedding.", "형은 그를 위해 돈을 마련했고, 공작 부인은 결혼식 후에 모스크바를 떠나라고 충고했다."], ["Stepan Arkadyevitch advised him to go abroad.", "스테판 아르카지치는 외국으로 나가라고 충고했다."], ["He agreed to everything.", "그는 무엇에든 동의했다."], ["“Do what you choose, if it amuses you.", "'재미있다면 마음대로 하라지."], ["I’m happy, and my happiness can be no greater and no less for anything you do,” he thought.", "난 행복하고, 내 행복은 너희가 무얼 하든 더 커지지도 작아지지도 않을 테니까.'"], ["When he told Kitty of Stepan Arkadyevitch’s advice that they should go abroad, he was much surprised that she did not agree to this, and had some definite requirements of her own in regard to their future.", "그는 그렇게 생각했다. 스테판 아르카지치가 외국으로 나가는 게 좋겠다고 한 충고를 키티에게 전했을 때, 그녀가 거기에 동의하지 않고 자기들의 미래에 관해 나름의 분명한 요구를 가지고 있다는 사실에 그는 무척 놀랐다."], ["She knew Levin had work he loved in the country.", "그녀는 레빈이 시골에서 사랑하는 일을 한다는 것을 알았다."], ["She did not, as he saw, understand this work, she did not even care to understand it.", "그가 보기에 그녀는 그 일을 이해하지 못했고, 이해하려고 애쓰지도 않았다."], ["But that did not prevent her from regarding it as a matter of great importance.", "그래도 그녀가 그 일을 대단히 중요한 것으로 여기는 데에는 아무 지장이 없었다."], ["And then she knew their home would be in the country, and she wanted to go, not abroad where she was not going to live, but to the place where their home would be.", "그리고 그녀는 자기들의 보금자리가 시골에 있으리란 것을 알았기에, 살지도 않을 외국이 아니라 자기들의 보금자리가 들어설 곳으로 가고 싶어 했다."], ["This definitely expressed purpose astonished Levin.", "이렇게 분명하게 표현된 의향이 레빈을 놀라게 했다."], ["But since he did not care either way, he immediately asked Stepan Arkadyevitch, as though it were his duty, to go down to the country and to arrange everything there to the best of his ability with the taste of which he had so much.", "하지만 그는 어느 쪽이든 상관없었기에, 마치 그것이 자기 의무라도 되는 양 스테판 아르카지치에게 시골로 내려가 그가 그토록 풍부하게 지닌 그 안목으로 거기 모든 것을 최선껏 정돈해 달라고 곧장 부탁했다."]], [["“But I say,”", "\"그런데 말이야,\""], ["Stepan Arkadyevitch said to him one day after he had come back from the country, where he had got everything ready for the young people’s arrival, “have you a certificate of having been at confession?”", "어느 날 스테판 아르카지치가 두 젊은이의 도착을 위해 모든 것을 준비해 두고 시골에서 돌아온 뒤 그에게 말했다. \"고해성사를 받았다는 증명서가 있나?\""]], [["“No.", "\"아니."], ["But what of it?”", "그게 뭐 어때서?\""]], [["“You can’t be married without it.”", "\"그게 없으면 결혼식을 올릴 수 없어.\""]], [["“Aïe, aïe, aïe!” cried Levin.", "\"아이, 아이, 아이!\""], ["“Why, I believe it’s nine years since I’ve taken the sacrament!", "레빈이 소리쳤다. \"아니, 성찬을 받은 지 한 구 년쯤 된 것 같은데!"], ["I never thought of it.”", "그건 생각도 못 했군.\""]], [["“You’re a pretty fellow!”", "\"참 잘난 친구로군!\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch laughing, “and you call me a Nihilist!", "스테판 아르카지치가 웃으며 말했다. \"그러면서 나더러 허무주의자라니!"], ["But this won’t do, you know.", "하지만 이건 안 될 일이야, 알겠나."], ["You must take the sacrament.”", "자넨 성찬을 받아야 해.\""]], [["“When?", "\"언제?"], ["There are four days left now.”", "이제 나흘밖에 안 남았는데.\""]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch arranged this also, and Levin had to go to confession.", "스테판 아르카지치는 이 일도 처리해 주었고, 레빈은 고해성사를 하러 가야 했다."], ["To Levin, as to any unbeliever who respects the beliefs of others, it was exceedingly disagreeable to be present at and take part in church ceremonies.", "다른 사람들의 믿음을 존중하는 여느 비신자와 마찬가지로, 레빈에게도 교회 의식에 참석하고 거기에 동참하는 일은 지극히 거북했다."], ["At this moment, in his present softened state of feeling, sensitive to everything, this inevitable act of hypocrisy was not merely painful to Levin, it seemed to him utterly impossible.", "지금 이 순간, 모든 것에 예민해진 부드러운 감정 상태에 있는 레빈에게 이 불가피한 위선의 행위는 그저 괴롭기만 한 것이 아니라 도무지 불가능한 일로 여겨졌다."], ["Now, in the heyday of his highest glory, his fullest flower, he would have to be a liar or a scoffer.", "지금, 더없이 영광스러운 전성기, 활짝 핀 절정에서 그는 거짓말쟁이나 비웃는 자가 되어야 했다."], ["He felt incapable of being either.", "그는 둘 중 어느 쪽도 될 수 없을 것 같았다."], ["But though he repeatedly plied Stepan Arkadyevitch with questions as to the possibility of obtaining a certificate without actually communicating, Stepan Arkadyevitch maintained that it was out of the question.", "하지만 실제로 성찬을 받지 않고도 증명서를 얻을 수 있는지를 스테판 아르카지치에게 거듭 캐물었으나, 스테판 아르카지치는 그건 말도 안 되는 일이라고 못 박았다."]], [["“Besides, what is it to you—two days?", "\"게다가 그게 자네한테 뭐 대수인가—이틀이잖나?"], ["And he’s an awfully nice clever old fellow.", "그리고 그 양반은 정말 친절하고 영리한 노인이야."], ["He’ll pull the tooth out for you so gently, you won’t notice it.”", "자네가 알아차리지도 못할 만큼 살살 이를 뽑아 줄 거야.\""]], [["Standing at the first litany, Levin attempted to revive in himself his youthful recollections of the intense religious emotion he had passed through between the ages of sixteen and seventeen.", "첫 기도가 진행되는 동안 서 있으면서 레빈은 자기가 열여섯에서 열일곱 살 사이에 거쳐 갔던 강렬한 종교적 감정에 대한 젊은 날의 기억을 자기 안에 되살려 보려 애썼다."]], [["But he was at once convinced that it was utterly impossible to him.", "하지만 그는 그것이 자기에게 도무지 불가능하다는 것을 곧바로 깨달았다."], ["He attempted to look at it all as an empty custom, having no sort of meaning, like the custom of paying calls.", "그는 이 모든 것을, 방문 예절 같은 풍습처럼 아무런 의미도 없는 텅 빈 관습으로 바라보려 했다."], ["But he felt that he could not do that either.", "하지만 그는 그것도 할 수 없다고 느꼈다."], ["Levin found himself, like the majority of his contemporaries, in the vaguest position in regard to religion.", "레빈은 자기 동시대인의 대다수가 그렇듯 종교에 관해 더없이 모호한 처지에 놓여 있는 자신을 발견했다."], ["Believe he could not, and at the same time he had no firm conviction that it was all wrong.", "그는 믿을 수 없었고, 동시에 그 모든 것이 잘못되었다는 확고한 신념도 없었다."], ["And consequently, not being able to believe in the significance of what he was doing nor to regard it with indifference as an empty formality, during the whole period of preparing for the sacrament he was conscious of a feeling of discomfort and shame at doing what he did not himself understand, and what, as an inner voice told him, was therefore false and wrong.", "그래서 자기가 하는 일의 의미를 믿을 수도, 그것을 텅 빈 형식으로 여겨 무심하게 대할 수도 없었기에, 성찬을 준비하는 내내 그는 스스로도 이해하지 못하는, 그리고 내면의 목소리가 일러 주듯 바로 그렇기에 거짓되고 그릇된 일을 하고 있다는 불편함과 부끄러움을 느꼈다."]], [["During the service he would first listen to the prayers, trying to attach some meaning to them not discordant with his own views; then feeling that he could not understand and must condemn them, he tried not to listen to them, but to attend to the thoughts, observations, and memories which floated through his brain with extreme vividness during this idle time of standing in church.", "예배가 진행되는 동안 그는 처음에는 기도에 귀를 기울이며 자기 견해와 어긋나지 않는 어떤 의미를 거기에 갖다 붙이려 애썼다. 그러다 그것을 이해할 수 없고 비난할 수밖에 없다고 느끼자, 기도에 귀 기울이지 않으려 했고, 교회에 서 있는 이 한가한 시간 동안 극도로 생생하게 머릿속을 떠다니는 생각과 관찰과 기억에 마음을 두려 했다."]], [["He had stood through the litany, the evening service and the midnight service, and the next day he got up earlier than usual, and without having tea went at eight o’clock in the morning to the church for the morning service and the confession.", "그는 기도와 저녁 예배, 자정 예배를 내내 서서 견뎌 냈고, 이튿날에는 평소보다 일찍 일어나 차도 마시지 않은 채 아침 여덟 시에 아침 예배와 고해성사를 위해 교회로 갔다."]], [["There was no one in the church but a beggar soldier, two old women, and the church officials.", "교회 안에는 구걸하는 병사 하나, 노파 둘, 그리고 교회 직원들 말고는 아무도 없었다."], ["A young deacon, whose long back showed in two distinct halves through his thin undercassock, met him, and at once going to a little table at the wall read the exhortation. During the reading, especially at the frequent and rapid repetition of the same words, “Lord, have mercy on us!”", "얇은 속법의 사이로 긴 등이 또렷이 둘로 나뉘어 보이는 젊은 부제가 그를 맞이하더니, 곧장 벽 쪽의 작은 탁자로 가서 권면의 글을 읽었다."], ["which resounded with an echo, Levin felt that thought was shut and sealed up, and that it must not be touched or stirred now or confusion would be the result; and so standing behind the deacon he went on thinking of his own affairs, neither listening nor examining what was said.", "그 낭독이 진행되는 동안, 특히 메아리치며 울려 퍼지는 \"주여, 우리를 불쌍히 여기소서!\"라는 똑같은 말이 빈번하고 빠르게 되풀이될 때, 레빈은 생각이 닫히고 봉인되어 버린 듯한, 그래서 지금은 그것을 건드리거나 휘저어서는 안 되며 그러면 혼란이 빚어질 것 같은 느낌을 받았다. 그래서 부제 뒤에 서서 그는 듣지도, 무슨 말이 오가는지 살피지도 않은 채 자기 자신의 일을 계속 생각했다."], ["“It’s wonderful what expression there is in her hand,”", "'그녀 손에 담긴 그 표정은 정말 놀랍단 말이야.'"], ["he thought, remembering how they had been sitting the day before at a corner table.", "어제 모퉁이 탁자에 둘이 앉아 있던 일을 떠올리며 그는 생각했다."], ["They had nothing to talk about, as was almost always the case at this time, and laying her hand on the table she kept opening and shutting it, and laughed herself as she watched her action.", "이맘때면 거의 늘 그랬듯이 그들은 별달리 나눌 이야기가 없었고, 그녀는 탁자 위에 손을 올려놓은 채 그것을 폈다 쥐었다 하면서 자기 동작을 지켜보며 혼자 웃었다."], ["He remembered how he had kissed it and then had examined the lines on the pink palm.", "그는 자기가 그 손에 입을 맞추고 나서 분홍빛 손바닥의 손금을 들여다보았던 일을 떠올렸다."], ["“Have mercy on us again!”", "'다시 우리를 불쌍히 여기소서!'"], ["thought Levin, crossing himself, bowing, and looking at the supple spring of the deacon’s back bowing before him.", "레빈은 성호를 긋고 절을 하며, 자기 앞에서 절을 하는 부제의 등이 유연하게 펴졌다 굽혀지는 모습을 바라보면서 생각했다."], ["“She took my hand then and examined the lines.", "'그때 그녀는 내 손을 잡고 손금을 들여다봤지."], ["‘You’ve got a splendid hand,’ she said.”", "「당신 손은 정말 멋져요」 하고 그녀는 말했어.'"], ["And he looked at his own hand and the short hand of the deacon.", "그러고는 자기 손과 부제의 짧은 손을 바라보았다."], ["“Yes, now it will soon be over,”", "'그래, 이제 곧 끝나겠지.'"], ["he thought.", "그는 생각했다."], ["“No, it seems to be beginning again,”", "'아니, 또 시작되는 모양이군.'"], ["he thought, listening to the prayers.", "기도에 귀 기울이며 그는 생각했다."], ["“No, it’s just ending: there he is bowing down to the ground.", "'아니, 막 끝나는군. 저기 그가 땅에 닿도록 절을 하잖아."], ["That’s always at the end.”", "저건 늘 끝에 하는 거지.'"]], [["The deacon’s hand in a plush cuff accepted a three-rouble note unobtrusively, and the deacon said he would put it down in the register, and his new boots creaking jauntily over the flagstones of the empty church, he went to the altar.", "플러시 천 소매 속의 부제 손이 눈에 띄지 않게 삼 루블짜리 지폐를 받았고, 부제는 그것을 장부에 적어 두겠다고 말하더니, 텅 빈 교회의 포석 위로 새 부츠를 경쾌하게 삐걱이며 제단으로 갔다."], ["A moment later he peeped out thence and beckoned to Levin.", "잠시 후 그는 거기서 고개를 내밀어 레빈에게 손짓했다."], ["Thought, till then locked up, began to stir in Levin’s head, but he made haste to drive it away.", "그때까지 잠겨 있던 생각이 레빈의 머릿속에서 꿈틀거리기 시작했지만, 그는 서둘러 그것을 쫓아냈다."], ["“It will come right somehow,”", "'어떻게든 잘되겠지.'"], ["he thought, and went towards the altar-rails.", "그는 그렇게 생각하며 제단 난간 쪽으로 갔다."], ["He went up the steps, and turning to the right saw the priest.", "그가 계단을 올라 오른쪽으로 돌자 사제가 보였다."], ["The priest, a little old man with a scanty grizzled beard and weary, good-natured eyes, was standing at the altar-rails, turning over the pages of a missal.", "희끗희끗한 성긴 수염에 지치고 선량한 눈을 가진 자그마한 노인인 사제는 제단 난간 앞에 서서 미사 경본의 책장을 넘기고 있었다."], ["With a slight bow to Levin he began immediately reading prayers in the official voice.", "사제는 레빈에게 가볍게 고개를 숙이고는 곧바로 격식 차린 목소리로 기도문을 읽기 시작했다."], ["When he had finished them he bowed down to the ground and turned, facing Levin.", "기도를 마치자 그는 땅에 닿도록 절을 하고 몸을 돌려 레빈을 마주 보았다."]], [["“Christ is present here unseen, receiving your confession,”", "\"그리스도께서 보이지 않게 여기 임하시어 그대의 고해를 받으십니다.\""], ["he said, pointing to the crucifix.", "십자가상을 가리키며 그가 말했다."], ["“Do you believe in all the doctrines of the Holy Apostolic Church?”", "\"그대는 거룩한 사도 교회의 모든 교리를 믿습니까?\""], ["the priest went on, turning his eyes away from Levin’s face and folding his hands under his stole.", "사제는 레빈의 얼굴에서 눈을 돌리고 영대 아래로 두 손을 모으며 말을 이었다."]], [["“I have doubted, I doubt everything,”", "\"저는 의심해 왔고, 모든 것을 의심합니다.\""], ["said Levin in a voice that jarred on himself, and he ceased speaking.", "레빈은 스스로도 거슬리는 목소리로 말하고는 입을 다물었다."]], [["The priest waited a few seconds to see if he would not say more, and closing his eyes he said quickly, with a broad, Vladimirsky accent:", "사제는 그가 더 말하지 않을지 보려고 몇 초 기다렸다가, 눈을 감고 넓적한 블라디미르 사투리로 빠르게 말했다."]], [["“Doubt is natural to the weakness of mankind, but we must pray that God in His mercy will strengthen us.", "\"의심은 인간의 나약함에 따른 자연스러운 일입니다만, 우리는 하느님께서 자비로 우리를 굳세게 해 주시길 기도해야 합니다."], ["What are your special sins?”", "그대의 특별한 죄는 무엇입니까?\""], ["he added, without the slightest interval, as though anxious not to waste time.", "그는 시간을 허비하지 않으려는 듯 조금의 틈도 두지 않고 덧붙였다."]], [["“My chief sin is doubt.", "\"저의 가장 큰 죄는 의심입니다."], ["I have doubts of everything, and for the most part I am in doubt.”", "저는 모든 것을 의심하고, 대개 의심 속에 있습니다.\""]], [["“Doubt is natural to the weakness of mankind,”", "\"의심은 인간의 나약함에 따른 자연스러운 일입니다.\""], ["the priest repeated the same words.", "사제는 똑같은 말을 되풀이했다."], ["“What do you doubt about principally?”", "\"그대는 주로 무엇을 의심합니까?\""]], [["“I doubt of everything.", "\"저는 모든 것을 의심합니다."], ["I sometimes even have doubts of the existence of God,”", "때로는 하느님의 존재마저 의심합니다.\""], ["Levin could not help saying, and he was horrified at the impropriety of what he was saying.", "레빈은 그렇게 말하지 않을 수 없었고, 자기가 하는 말의 부적절함에 소스라쳤다."], ["But Levin’s words did not, it seemed, make much impression on the priest.", "하지만 레빈의 말은 사제에게 별 인상을 남기지 못한 듯했다."]], [["“What sort of doubt can there be of the existence of God?”", "\"하느님의 존재에 무슨 의심이 있을 수 있겠습니까?\""], ["he said hurriedly, with a just perceptible smile.", "사제는 어렴풋이 알아챌 만한 미소를 띠고 서둘러 말했다."]], [["Levin did not speak.", "레빈은 입을 열지 않았다."]], [["“What doubt can you have of the Creator when you behold His creation?”", "\"그대가 창조주의 창조물을 바라보면서 어찌 그분을 의심할 수 있습니까?\""], ["the priest went on in the rapid customary jargon.", "사제는 익숙한 빠른 말투로 말을 이었다."], ["“Who has decked the heavenly firmament with its lights?", "\"누가 하늘의 궁창을 빛으로 꾸몄습니까?"], ["Who has clothed the earth in its beauty?", "누가 대지에 아름다움의 옷을 입혔습니까?"], ["How explain it without the Creator?”", "창조주 없이 그것을 어떻게 설명하겠습니까?\""], ["he said, looking inquiringly at Levin.", "그는 캐묻는 듯한 눈으로 레빈을 바라보며 말했다."]], [["Levin felt that it would be improper to enter upon a metaphysical discussion with the priest, and so he said in reply merely what was a direct answer to the question.", "레빈은 사제와 형이상학적 논쟁에 들어가는 것은 부적절하리라고 느꼈고, 그래서 그저 질문에 대한 직접적인 답만을 대답으로 내놓았다."]], [["“I don’t know,”", "\"모르겠습니다.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“You don’t know!", "\"모른다고요!"], ["Then how can you doubt that God created all?”", "그런데 어떻게 하느님께서 만물을 창조하셨다는 것을 의심할 수 있습니까?\""], ["the priest said, with good-humored perplexity.", "사제는 선량한 당혹감을 띠고 말했다."]], [["“I don’t understand it at all,”", "\"저는 그것을 전혀 이해하지 못하겠습니다.\""], ["said Levin, blushing, and feeling that his words were stupid, and that they could not be anything but stupid in such a position.", "레빈은 얼굴을 붉히며 말했고, 자기 말이 어리석으며 이런 상황에서는 어리석을 수밖에 없다고 느꼈다."]], [["“Pray to God and beseech Him.", "\"하느님께 기도하고 그분께 간구하십시오."], ["Even the holy fathers had doubts, and prayed to God to strengthen their faith.", "거룩한 교부들조차 의심을 품었고, 자기 믿음을 굳세게 해 달라고 하느님께 기도했습니다."], ["The devil has great power, and we must resist him.", "악마는 큰 힘을 가졌으니, 우리는 그에게 맞서야 합니다."], ["Pray to God, beseech Him.", "하느님께 기도하십시오, 그분께 간구하십시오."], ["Pray to God,”", "하느님께 기도하십시오.\""], ["he repeated hurriedly.", "그가 서둘러 되풀이했다."]], [["The priest paused for some time, as though meditating.", "사제는 무언가 명상이라도 하듯 한동안 말을 멈추었다."]], [["“You’re about, I hear, to marry the daughter of my parishioner and son in the spirit, Prince Shtcherbatsky?”", "\"내가 듣기로, 그대는 내 교구민이자 영적인 아들인 시쳬르바츠키 공작의 따님과 결혼하려 한다지요?\""], ["he resumed, with a smile.", "그가 미소를 지으며 다시 말을 이었다."], ["“An excellent young lady.”", "\"참으로 훌륭한 아가씨입니다.\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["answered Levin, blushing for the priest.", "레빈은 사제 대신 얼굴을 붉히며 대답했다."], ["“What does he want to ask me about this at confession for?”", "'도대체 고해성사에서 이 일을 왜 나한테 물어보는 거지?'"], ["he thought.", "그는 생각했다."]], [["And, as though answering his thought, the priest said to him:", "그러자 마치 그의 생각에 답이라도 하듯 사제가 그에게 말했다."]], [["“You are about to enter into holy matrimony, and God may bless you with offspring.", "\"그대는 거룩한 혼인에 들어서려 하고, 하느님께서 그대에게 자식을 점지하실지도 모릅니다."], ["Well, what sort of bringing-up can you give your babes if you do not overcome the temptation of the devil, enticing you to infidelity?”", "그런데 그대가 불신앙으로 그대를 꾀어내는 악마의 유혹을 이겨 내지 못한다면, 그대의 아기들을 어찌 길러 내겠습니까?\""], ["he said, with gentle reproachfulness.", "그가 부드러운 책망을 담아 말했다."], ["“If you love your child as a good father, you will not desire only wealth, luxury, honor for your infant; you will be anxious for his salvation, his spiritual enlightenment with the light of truth. Eh?", "\"그대가 좋은 아버지로서 자기 아이를 사랑한다면, 그 어린것에게 부와 사치와 명예만을 바라지는 않을 것입니다."], ["What answer will you make him when the innocent babe asks you: ‘Papa!", "그대는 그 아이의 구원을, 진리의 빛으로 영적으로 깨우치는 일을 염려할 것입니다. 그렇지요?"], ["who made all that enchants me in this world—the earth, the waters, the sun, the flowers, the grass?’ Can you say to him: ‘I don’t know’?", "죄 없는 아기가 그대에게 '아빠! 이 세상에서 나를 황홀하게 하는 이 모든 것—땅과 물과 해와 꽃과 풀을 누가 만들었어요?'"], ["You cannot but know, since the Lord God in His infinite mercy has revealed it to us.", "하고 물으면 그대는 무어라 답하겠습니까? 그 아이에게 '난 모른단다'라고 말할 수 있겠습니까?"], ["Or your child will ask you: ‘What awaits me in the life beyond the tomb?’ What will you say to him when you know nothing?", "주 하느님께서 무한한 자비로 우리에게 그것을 계시하셨으니, 그대는 모를 수가 없습니다. 혹은 그대의 아이가 '무덤 너머의 삶에서는 무엇이 나를 기다리고 있나요?'"], ["How will you answer him?", "하고 물을 것입니다."], ["Will you leave him to the allurements of the world and the devil?", "그대가 아무것도 모를 때 그 아이에게 무어라 말하겠습니까? 어떻게 답하겠습니까?"], ["That’s not right,”", "그 아이를 세상과 악마의 유혹에 내맡길 것입니까?"], ["he said, and he stopped, putting his head on one side and looking at Levin with his kindly, gentle eyes.", "그건 옳지 않습니다.\" 그는 그렇게 말하고는 멈추어, 고개를 한쪽으로 기울이고 자애롭고 부드러운 눈으로 레빈을 바라보았다."]], [["Levin made no answer this time, not because he did not want to enter upon a discussion with the priest, but because, so far, no one had ever asked him such questions, and when his babes did ask him those questions, it would be time enough to think about answering them.", "이번에 레빈은 아무 대답도 하지 않았는데, 사제와 논쟁에 들어가고 싶지 않아서가 아니라, 지금껏 아무도 그에게 그런 질문을 한 적이 없었고, 또 자기 아기들이 정말로 그런 질문을 하게 되면 그때 가서 답을 생각해도 충분하리라 여겼기 때문이었다."]], [["“You are entering upon a time of life,”", "\"그대는,\" 사제가 말을 이었다."], ["pursued the priest, “when you must choose your path and keep to it.", "\"자기 길을 택하여 그것을 지켜 나가야 할 인생의 시기에 들어서고 있습니다."], ["Pray to God that He may in His mercy aid you and have mercy on you!”", "하느님께서 자비로 그대를 도우시고 그대를 불쌍히 여기시길 기도하십시오!\""], ["he concluded.", "그가 끝맺었다."], ["“Our Lord and God, Jesus Christ, in the abundance and riches of His loving-kindness, forgives this child....”", "\"우리 주 하느님이신 예수 그리스도께서 풍성하고 넉넉한 사랑과 자비로 이 자식을 용서하시니……\""], ["and, finishing the prayer of absolution, the priest blessed him and dismissed him.", "그러고는 사죄의 기도를 마치며 사제는 그를 축복하고 돌려보냈다."]], [["On getting home that day, Levin had a delightful sense of relief at the awkward position being over and having been got through without his having to tell a lie.", "그날 집에 돌아오자 레빈은 거북한 상황이 끝났고, 거짓말을 하지 않고도 그것을 무사히 넘겼다는 데에 흐뭇한 안도감을 느꼈다."], ["Apart from this, there remained a vague memory that what the kind, nice old fellow had said had not been at all so stupid as he had fancied at first, and that there was something in it that must be cleared up.", "그 외에도, 그 친절하고 다정한 노인이 한 말이 처음에 짐작했던 만큼 그렇게 어리석지는 않았으며, 거기에는 풀어내야 할 무언가가 있다는 어렴풋한 기억이 남아 있었다."]], [["“Of course, not now,”", "'물론 지금은 아니지.'"], ["thought Levin, “but some day later on.”", "레빈은 생각했다. '하지만 언젠가 나중에는.'"], ["Levin felt more than ever now that there was something not clear and not clean in his soul, and that, in regard to religion, he was in the same position which he perceived so clearly and disliked in others, and for which he blamed his friend Sviazhsky.", "레빈은 이제 그 어느 때보다도 자기 영혼 속에 무언가 맑지 못하고 깨끗하지 못한 데가 있으며, 종교에 관한 한 자기가 남들에게서 그토록 또렷이 알아보고 싫어했던, 그리고 친구 스비아시스키를 두고 나무랐던 바로 그 처지에 자기 자신이 놓여 있음을 느꼈다."]], [["Levin spent that evening with his betrothed at Dolly’s, and was in very high spirits.", "레빈은 그날 저녁을 돌리의 집에서 약혼녀와 함께 보냈고, 기분이 무척 좋았다."], ["To explain to Stepan Arkadyevitch the state of excitement in which he found himself, he said that he was happy like a dog being trained to jump through a hoop, who, having at last caught the idea, and done what was required of him, whines and wags its tail, and jumps up to the table and the windows in its delight.", "자기가 빠져 있는 들뜬 상태를 스테판 아르카지치에게 설명하면서, 그는 자기가 마치 둥근 고리를 통과하도록 훈련받는 개와 같은 행복을 느낀다고 말했다. 마침내 요령을 깨치고 요구받은 것을 해낸 그 개가, 기뻐서 낑낑대며 꼬리를 흔들고, 좋아라 탁자와 창문으로 뛰어오르는 것처럼 말이다."]], [["# Chapter 2", "# Chapter 2"]], [["On the day of the wedding, according to the Russian custom (the princess and Darya Alexandrovna insisted on strictly keeping all the customs), Levin did not see his betrothed, and dined at his hotel with three bachelor friends, casually brought together at his rooms.", "결혼식 날, 러시아의 관습에 따라(공작 부인과 다리야 알렉산드로브나는 모든 관습을 엄격히 지켜야 한다고 고집했다) 레빈은 신부를 보지 않았고, 자기 방으로 우연히 모인 세 명의 독신 친구들과 함께 호텔에서 저녁을 먹었다."], ["These were Sergey Ivanovitch, Katavasov, a university friend, now professor of natural science, whom Levin had met in the street and insisted on taking home with him, and Tchirikov, his best man, a Moscow conciliation-board judge, Levin’s companion in his bear-hunts.", "그들은 세르게이 이바노비치, 그리고 대학 동창이자 지금은 자연과학 교수인 카타바소프였는데, 레빈은 거리에서 그를 만나 기어이 자기 집으로 데려온 터였다. 또 한 사람은 들러리인 치리코프로, 모스크바 화해 조정관이자 레빈과 함께 곰 사냥을 다니던 동료였다."], ["The dinner was a very merry one: Sergey Ivanovitch was in his happiest mood, and was much amused by Katavasov’s originality.", "저녁 식사는 무척 흥겨웠다. 세르게이 이바노비치는 더없이 기분 좋은 상태였고, 카타바소프의 독특함을 무척 재미있어했다."], ["Katavasov, feeling his originality was appreciated and understood, made the most of it.", "카타바소프는 자기의 독특함이 인정받고 이해받고 있다고 느끼자 그것을 한껏 발휘했다."], ["Tchirikov always gave a lively and good-humored support to conversation of any sort.", "치리코프는 어떤 종류의 대화든 늘 생기 있고 유쾌하게 거들었다."]], [["“See, now,”", "\"자, 보십시오.\""], ["said Katavasov, drawling his words from a habit acquired in the lecture-room, “what a capable fellow was our friend Konstantin Dmitrievitch.", "강의실에서 익힌 버릇대로 말을 길게 늘이며 카타바소프가 말했다. \"우리 친구 콘스탄틴 드미트리예비치가 얼마나 재능 있는 사람이었는지 말입니다."], ["I’m not speaking of present company, for he’s absent.", "지금 이 자리에 있는 사람을 두고 하는 말은 아닙니다, 그는 여기 없으니까요."], ["At the time he left the university he was fond of science, took an interest in humanity; now one-half of his abilities is devoted to deceiving himself, and the other to justifying the deceit.”", "대학을 떠날 무렵에 그는 학문을 사랑했고 인류에 관심을 가졌지요. 그런데 지금은 자기 재능의 절반은 스스로를 속이는 데 바치고, 나머지 절반은 그 기만을 정당화하는 데 바치고 있단 말입니다.\""]], [["“A more determined enemy of matrimony than you I never saw,”", "\"자네만큼 결혼을 단호하게 적대시하는 사람은 본 적이 없네.\""], ["said Sergey Ivanovitch.", "세르게이 이바노비치가 말했다."]], [["“Oh, no, I’m not an enemy of matrimony.", "\"아니, 천만에요, 저는 결혼의 적이 아닙니다."], ["I’m in favor of division of labor.", "저는 분업에 찬성하는 거지요."], ["People who can do nothing else ought to rear people while the rest work for their happiness and enlightenment.", "아무것도 할 수 없는 사람들은 사람을 길러 내야 하고, 나머지 사람들은 그들의 행복과 계몽을 위해 일해야 한다는 겁니다."], ["That’s how I look at it.", "저는 그렇게 봅니다."], ["To muddle up two trades is the error of the amateur; I’m not one of their number.”", "두 가지 일을 뒤죽박죽 섞는 건 아마추어의 잘못이지요. 저는 그런 부류가 아닙니다.\""]], [["“How happy I shall be when I hear that you’re in love!”", "\"자네가 사랑에 빠졌다는 소식을 들으면 내가 얼마나 기쁠까!\""], ["said Levin.", "레빈이 말했다."], ["“Please invite me to the wedding.”", "\"그땐 꼭 결혼식에 초대해 주게.\""]], [["“I’m in love now.”", "\"전 지금 사랑에 빠져 있는데요.\""]], [["“Yes, with a cuttlefish! You know,”", "\"그래, 오징어와 말이지!\""], ["Levin turned to his brother, “Mihail Semyonovitch is writing a work on the digestive organs of the....”", "레빈이 형을 돌아보며 말했다. \"있잖아, 미하일 세묘노비치가 지금 소화 기관에 관한 논문을 쓰고 있거든, 그러니까……\""]], [["“Now, make a muddle of it!", "\"자, 또 뒤죽박죽으로 만드시는군!"], ["It doesn’t matter what about.", "무엇에 관한 거든 상관없습니다."], ["And the fact is, I certainly do love cuttlefish.”", "사실 저는 분명히 오징어를 사랑하니까요.\""]], [["“But that’s no hindrance to your loving your wife.”", "\"하지만 그게 아내를 사랑하는 데 방해가 되지는 않잖나.\""]], [["“The cuttlefish is no hindrance.", "\"오징어는 방해가 안 되지요."], ["The wife is the hindrance.”", "방해가 되는 건 아내입니다.\" \"어째서 그렇지?\""]], [["“Why so?” “Oh, you’ll see!", "\"오, 곧 알게 되실 겁니다!"], ["You care about farming, hunting,—well, you’d better look out!”", "당신은 농사니 사냥이니 하는 것에 마음을 쓰시지요. 그러니 조심하시는 게 좋을 겁니다!\""]], [["“Arhip was here today; he said there were a lot of elks in Prudno, and two bears,”", "\"오늘 아르히프가 다녀갔는데, 프루드노에 큰 사슴이 잔뜩 있고 곰이 두 마리 있다더군요.\""], ["said Tchirikov.", "치리코프가 말했다."]], [["“Well, you must go and get them without me.”", "\"그럼 자네들끼리 가서 잡게나, 나 없이.\""]], [["“Ah, that’s the truth,”", "\"아, 그게 맞는 말이네.\""], ["said Sergey Ivanovitch.", "세르게이 이바노비치가 말했다."], ["“And you may say good-bye to bear-hunting for the future—your wife won’t allow it!”", "\"그리고 앞으로 곰 사냥과는 작별 인사를 해 두는 게 좋을 거야. 자네 아내가 허락하지 않을 테니!\""]], [["Levin smiled.", "레빈은 미소 지었다."], ["The picture of his wife not letting him go was so pleasant that he was ready to renounce the delights of looking upon bears forever.", "아내가 자기를 못 가게 한다는 그림이 너무도 흐뭇해서, 그는 곰을 보는 즐거움쯤은 영영 단념해도 좋다는 마음이었다."]], [["“Still, it’s a pity they should get those two bears without you.", "\"그래도 자네 없이 그 곰 두 마리를 잡아 버린다니 안된 일이군."], ["Do you remember last time at Hapilovo?", "지난번 하필로보에서 있었던 일 기억나나?"], ["That was a delightful hunt!”", "그땐 정말 신나는 사냥이었지!\""], ["said Tchirikov.", "치리코프가 말했다."]], [["Levin had not the heart to disillusion him of the notion that there could be something delightful apart from her, and so said nothing.", "레빈은 그녀와 떨어진 곳에 무언가 즐거운 것이 있을 수 있다는 그 생각을 깨뜨려 줄 마음이 차마 들지 않아, 아무 말도 하지 않았다."]], [["“There’s some sense in this custom of saying good-bye to bachelor life,”", "\"독신 생활과 작별을 고하는 이런 관습에는 일리가 있어.\""], ["said Sergey Ivanovitch.", "세르게이 이바노비치가 말했다."], ["“However happy you may be, you must regret your freedom.”", "\"아무리 행복하다 해도 자기 자유는 아쉬울 수밖에 없으니까.\""]], [["“And confess there is a feeling that you want to jump out of the window, like Gogol’s bridegroom?”", "\"그리고 고골의 신랑처럼 창밖으로 뛰쳐나가고 싶은 기분이 든다는 것도 솔직히 인정해야겠지?\""]], [["“Of course there is, but it isn’t confessed,”", "\"물론 그런 기분이 들지요, 다만 인정하지 않을 뿐입니다.\""], ["said Katavasov, and he broke into loud laughter.", "카타바소프가 이렇게 말하고는 큰 소리로 웃음을 터뜨렸다."]], [["“Oh, well, the window’s open.", "\"오, 그래요, 창문이 열려 있군요."], ["Let’s start off this instant to Tver!", "지금 당장 트베리로 떠납시다!"], ["There’s a big she-bear; one can go right up to the lair.", "거기 큰 암곰이 있는데, 굴까지 곧장 다가갈 수 있어요."], ["Seriously, let’s go by the five o’clock!", "진지하게 말하는데, 다섯 시 기차로 갑시다!"], ["And here let them do what they like,”", "그리고 여기 일은 저들이 알아서 하라지요.\""], ["said Tchirikov, smiling.", "치리코프가 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["“Well, now, on my honor,”", "\"자, 그러니까, 내 명예를 걸고 말하는데,\""], ["said Levin, smiling, “I can’t find in my heart that feeling of regret for my freedom.”", "레빈이 미소 지으며 말했다. \"내 마음속에서는 자유를 아쉬워하는 그런 감정을 도무지 찾을 수가 없네.\""]], [["“Yes, there’s such a chaos in your heart just now that you can’t find anything there,”", "\"그래, 지금 자네 마음속이 어찌나 뒤죽박죽인지 거기서 아무것도 찾을 수가 없는 거지.\""], ["said Katavasov.", "카타바소프가 말했다."], ["“Wait a bit, when you set it to rights a little, you’ll find it!”", "\"조금만 기다려 보게, 마음을 좀 정리하고 나면 찾게 될 테니!\""]], [["“No; if so, I should have felt a little, apart from my feeling” (he could not say love before them) “and happiness, a certain regret at losing my freedom....", "\"아니, 만약 그렇다면, 내 감정과(그는 그들 앞에서 사랑이라고는 말할 수 없었다) 행복과는 별개로, 자유를 잃는 데 대한 어떤 아쉬움을 조금이라도 느꼈어야 마땅하잖나……"], ["On the contrary, I am glad at the very loss of my freedom.”", "그런데 오히려 나는 자유를 잃는 바로 그것이 기쁘단 말이야.\""]], [["“Awful!", "\"끔찍하군!"], ["It’s a hopeless case!”", "가망 없는 경우야!\""], ["said Katavasov.", "카타바소프가 말했다."], ["“Well, let’s drink to his recovery, or wish that a hundredth part of his dreams may be realized—and that would be happiness such as never has been seen on earth!”", "\"자, 그럼 그의 회복을 위해 건배하든가, 아니면 그의 꿈이 백분의 일이라도 이루어지기를 빌어 줍시다. 그렇게만 된다면 이 땅에서 일찍이 본 적 없는 행복일 테니까요!\""]], [["Soon after dinner the guests went away to be in time to be dressed for the wedding.", "저녁 식사가 끝나고 얼마 지나지 않아 손님들은 결혼식을 위해 옷을 갈아입을 시간에 맞추려고 자리를 떴다."]], [["When he was left alone, and recalled the conversation of these bachelor friends, Levin asked himself: had he in his heart that regret for his freedom of which they had spoken?", "혼자 남은 레빈은 그 독신 친구들과 나눈 대화를 떠올리며 스스로에게 물었다. 과연 자기 마음속에 그들이 말한 그 자유에 대한 아쉬움이 있는가?"], ["He smiled at the question.", "그는 그 물음에 미소 지었다."], ["“Freedom!", "'자유라니!"], ["What is freedom for?", "자유가 다 무슨 소용이야? 행복은 오로지 그녀를 사랑하고, 그녀가 바라는 것을 바라고, 그녀가 생각하는 것을 생각하는 데에만 있어."], ["Happiness is only in loving and wishing her wishes, thinking her thoughts, that is to say, not freedom at all—that’s happiness!”", "그러니까 그건 전혀 자유가 아닌 거지. 바로 그게 행복이야!'"]], [["“But do I know her ideas, her wishes, her feelings?”", "'하지만 내가 그녀의 생각을, 그녀의 바람을, 그녀의 감정을 알기나 하는가?'"], ["some voice suddenly whispered to him.", "어떤 목소리가 별안간 그에게 속삭였다."], ["The smile died away from his face, and he grew thoughtful.", "미소가 그의 얼굴에서 사라졌고, 그는 생각에 잠겼다."], ["And suddenly a strange feeling came upon him.", "그러다 문득 이상한 감정이 그를 덮쳐 왔다."], ["There came over him a dread and doubt—doubt of everything.", "두려움과 의심이, 모든 것에 대한 의심이 그를 사로잡았다."]], [["“What if she does not love me?", "'만약 그녀가 나를 사랑하지 않는다면?"], ["What if she’s marrying me simply to be married?", "만약 그녀가 그저 결혼을 하기 위해 나와 결혼하는 거라면?"], ["What if she doesn’t see herself what she’s doing?”", "만약 그녀가 자기가 무슨 짓을 하고 있는지조차 모르고 있다면?'"], ["he asked himself.", "그는 스스로에게 물었다."], ["“She may come to her senses, and only when she is being married realize that she does not and cannot love me.”", "'그녀는 정신을 차리고서, 막상 결혼을 하는 순간에야 자기가 나를 사랑하지 않으며 사랑할 수도 없다는 걸 깨달을지도 몰라.'"], ["And strange, most evil thoughts of her began to come to him.", "그러자 그녀에 대한 이상하고 더없이 사악한 생각들이 그에게 떠오르기 시작했다."], ["He was jealous of Vronsky, as he had been a year ago, as though the evening he had seen her with Vronsky had been yesterday.", "그는 일 년 전과 마찬가지로, 마치 그녀가 브론스키와 함께 있는 것을 본 그날 저녁이 바로 어제 일이기라도 한 듯이 브론스키에게 질투를 느꼈다."], ["He suspected she had not told him everything.", "그는 그녀가 자기에게 모든 것을 다 말하지 않았으리라고 의심했다."]], [["He jumped up quickly.", "그는 벌떡 일어섰다."], ["“No, this can’t go on!”", "'안 돼, 이대로는 안 돼!'"], ["he said to himself in despair.", "그는 절망에 빠져 스스로에게 말했다."], ["“I’ll go to her; I’ll ask her; I’ll say for the last time: we are free, and hadn’t we better stay so?", "'그녀에게 가야겠어. 물어봐야겠어."], ["Anything’s better than endless misery, disgrace, unfaithfulness!”", "마지막으로 이렇게 말하겠어. 우리는 자유로운 몸이니, 차라리 이대로 있는 편이 낫지 않겠느냐고."], ["With despair in his heart and bitter anger against all men, against himself, against her, he went out of the hotel and drove to her house.", "끝없는 비참함과 치욕과 부정함보다는 무엇이든 나으니까!' 마음속에 절망을 품고, 모든 사람에 대한, 자기 자신에 대한, 그녀에 대한 쓰디쓴 분노를 안은 채 그는 호텔을 나와 그녀의 집으로 마차를 몰았다."]], [["He found her in one of the back rooms.", "그는 안쪽 방 가운데 한 곳에서 그녀를 찾아냈다."], ["She was sitting on a chest and making some arrangements with her maid, sorting over heaps of dresses of different colors, spread on the backs of chairs and on the floor.", "그녀는 궤짝 위에 앉아 하녀와 무언가를 정리하며, 의자 등받이와 바닥에 펼쳐 놓은 갖가지 색깔의 드레스 더미를 가려내고 있었다."]], [["“Ah!”", "\"아!\""], ["she cried, seeing him, and beaming with delight.", "그를 보자 그녀는 기쁨으로 환히 빛나며 소리쳤다."], ["“Kostya!", "\"코스챠!"], ["Konstantin Dmitrievitch!” (These latter days she used these names almost alternately.) “I didn’t expect you!", "콘스탄틴 드미트리예비치!\"(요즘 며칠 동안 그녀는 이 두 이름을 거의 번갈아 부르고 있었다.) \"당신이 올 줄은 몰랐어요!"], ["I’m going through my wardrobe to see what’s for whom....”", "어느 게 누구 줄 건지 보려고 옷장을 정리하고 있던 참이에요……\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["that’s very nice!”", "그것 참 좋은 일이군.\""], ["he said gloomily, looking at the maid.", "그는 하녀를 바라보며 침울하게 말했다."]], [["“You can go, Dunyasha, I’ll call you presently,”", "\"두냐샤, 가도 좋아, 곧 부를게.\""], ["said Kitty.", "키티가 말했다."], ["“Kostya, what’s the matter?”", "\"코스챠, 무슨 일이에요?\""], ["she asked, definitely adopting this familiar name as soon as the maid had gone out.", "하녀가 나가자마자 그녀는 이 다정한 이름을 분명하게 골라 부르며 물었다."], ["She noticed his strange face, agitated and gloomy, and a panic came over her.", "그녀는 동요하고 침울한 그의 이상한 얼굴을 알아챘고, 공포가 그녀를 덮쳤다."]], [["“Kitty!", "\"키티!"], ["I’m in torture.", "나는 고통스러워."], ["I can’t suffer alone,”", "혼자서는 견딜 수가 없어.\""], ["he said with despair in his voice, standing before her and looking imploringly into her eyes.", "그는 절망이 담긴 목소리로 그녀 앞에 선 채 애원하듯 그녀의 눈을 들여다보며 말했다."], ["He saw already from her loving, truthful face, that nothing could come of what he had meant to say, but yet he wanted her to reassure him herself.", "그는 이미 그녀의 사랑스럽고 진실한 얼굴에서, 자기가 하려던 말이 아무런 결과도 낳지 못하리라는 것을 알았지만, 그래도 그녀가 직접 자기를 안심시켜 주기를 바랐다."], ["“I’ve come to say that there’s still time.", "\"아직 시간이 있다는 걸 말하러 왔어."], ["This can all be stopped and set right.”", "이 모든 걸 멈추고 바로잡을 수 있어.\""]], [["“What?", "\"뭐라고요?"], ["I don’t understand.", "무슨 말인지 모르겠어요."], ["What is the matter?”", "무슨 일인데요?\""]], [["“What I have said a thousand times over, and can’t help thinking ...", "\"내가 천 번도 더 말했고 또 생각하지 않을 수 없는 것……"], ["that I’m not worthy of you.", "내가 당신에게 어울리지 않는다는 거야."], ["You couldn’t consent to marry me.", "당신이 나와 결혼하기로 한 건 동의할 수 없는 일이었어."], ["Think a little.", "좀 생각해 봐."], ["You’ve made a mistake.", "당신은 잘못 생각한 거야."], ["Think it over thoroughly.", "곰곰이 다시 생각해 봐."], ["You can’t love me....", "당신은 나를 사랑할 수 없어……"], ["If ...", "만약……"], ["better say so,”", "차라리 그렇다고 말해 줘.\""], ["he said, not looking at her.", "그는 그녀를 보지 않은 채 말했다."], ["“I shall be wretched.", "\"나는 비참해지겠지."], ["Let people say what they like; anything’s better than misery....", "사람들이 뭐라고 하든 내버려 둬. 비참함보다는 무엇이든 나으니까……"], ["Far better now while there’s still time....”", "아직 시간이 있는 지금이 훨씬 나아……\""]], [["“I don’t understand,”", "\"무슨 말인지 모르겠어요.\""], ["she answered, panic-stricken; “you mean you want to give it up ...", "그녀는 공포에 질려 대답했다. \"그러니까 그만두고 싶다는……"], ["don’t want it?”", "원하지 않는다는 말인가요?\""]], [["“Yes, if you don’t love me.”", "\"그래, 당신이 나를 사랑하지 않는다면.\""]], [["“You’re out of your mind!”", "\"당신 제정신이 아니에요!\""], ["she cried, turning crimson with vexation.", "그녀는 분해서 새빨개지며 소리쳤다."], ["But his face was so piteous, that she restrained her vexation, and flinging some clothes off an armchair, she sat down beside him.", "하지만 그의 얼굴이 너무도 가여워서, 그녀는 분한 마음을 억누르고 안락의자에서 옷가지 몇 벌을 치우더니 그의 곁에 앉았다."], ["“What are you thinking?", "\"무슨 생각을 하고 있는 거예요?"], ["tell me all.”", "다 말해 봐요.\""]], [["“I am thinking you can’t love me.", "\"당신이 나를 사랑할 수 없을 거라고 생각하고 있어."], ["What can you love me for?”", "무엇 때문에 나를 사랑할 수 있겠어?\""]], [["“My God!", "\"맙소사!"], ["what can I do?...”", "내가 어떻게 해야 하나요?……\""], ["she said, and burst into tears.", "그녀는 이렇게 말하고는 울음을 터뜨렸다."]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["what have I done?”", "내가 무슨 짓을 한 거지?\""], ["he cried, and kneeling before her, he fell to kissing her hands.", "그가 소리치며 그녀 앞에 무릎을 꿇고 그녀의 두 손에 입을 맞추기 시작했다."]], [["When the princess came into the room five minutes later, she found them completely reconciled.", "오 분 뒤 공작 부인이 방에 들어왔을 때, 두 사람은 완전히 화해해 있었다."], ["Kitty had not simply assured him that she loved him, but had gone so far—in answer to his question, what she loved him for—as to explain what for.", "키티는 단순히 그를 사랑한다고 안심시켰을 뿐 아니라, 무엇 때문에 자기를 사랑하느냐는 그의 물음에 무엇 때문인지를 설명하는 데까지 나아갔다."], ["She told him that she loved him because she understood him completely, because she knew what he would like, and because everything he liked was good.", "그녀는 자기가 그를 완전히 이해하기 때문에, 그가 무엇을 좋아할지 알기 때문에, 그리고 그가 좋아하는 모든 것이 다 좋기 때문에 그를 사랑한다고 말했다."], ["And this seemed to him perfectly clear.", "그리고 이 말이 그에게는 더없이 분명하게 여겨졌다."], ["When the princess came to them, they were sitting side by side on the chest, sorting the dresses and disputing over Kitty’s wanting to give Dunyasha the brown dress she had been wearing when Levin proposed to her, while he insisted that that dress must never be given away, but Dunyasha must have the blue one.", "공작 부인이 그들에게 다가왔을 때, 두 사람은 궤짝 위에 나란히 앉아 드레스를 가려내며 다투고 있었는데, 키티는 레빈이 청혼하던 날 입고 있던 갈색 드레스를 두냐샤에게 주려 했고, 그는 그 드레스만큼은 절대 남에게 줘서는 안 되며 두냐샤에게는 파란 드레스를 주어야 한다고 우기고 있었다."]], [["“How is it you don’t see?", "\"어째서 모르는 거예요?"], ["She’s a brunette, and it won’t suit her....", "그 애는 살결이 검어서 그 옷이 어울리지 않을 거예요……"], ["I’ve worked it all out.”", "내가 다 생각해 뒀어요.\""]], [["Hearing why he had come, the princess was half humorously, half seriously angry with him, and sent him home to dress and not to hinder Kitty’s hair-dressing, as Charles the hair-dresser was just coming.", "그가 무엇 때문에 왔는지 듣고서 공작 부인은 반은 우스워하고 반은 진지하게 그에게 화를 내며, 마침 머리 손질사 샤를이 오는 참이니 키티의 머리 손질을 방해하지 말고 집으로 가서 옷을 갈아입으라고 그를 돌려보냈다."]], [["“As it is, she’s been eating nothing lately and is losing her looks, and then you must come and upset her with your nonsense,”", "\"안 그래도 저 애가 요즘 통 먹질 않아서 얼굴이 상하고 있는데, 자네까지 와서 그런 헛소리로 애를 뒤집어 놓다니.\""], ["she said to him.", "그녀가 그에게 말했다."], ["“Get along with you, my dear!”", "\"어서 가게나, 이 사람아!\""]], [["Levin, guilty and shamefaced, but pacified, went back to his hotel.", "레빈은 죄스럽고 부끄러웠지만 마음이 진정되어 호텔로 돌아갔다."], ["His brother, Darya Alexandrovna, and Stepan Arkadyevitch, all in full dress, were waiting for him to bless him with the holy picture.", "그의 형과 다리야 알렉산드로브나, 그리고 스테판 아르카지치가 모두 정장을 갖춰 입고서, 성화로 그를 축복해 주려고 그를 기다리고 있었다."], ["There was no time to lose.", "지체할 시간이 없었다."], ["Darya Alexandrovna had to drive home again to fetch her curled and pomaded son, who was to carry the holy pictures after the bride.", "다리야 알렉산드로브나는 신부 뒤에서 성화를 들고 갈 아들을 데려오기 위해 다시 집으로 마차를 몰고 가야 했는데, 아이는 머리를 곱슬곱슬하게 말고 포마드를 바른 상태였다."], ["Then a carriage had to be sent for the best man, and another that would take Sergey Ivanovitch away would have to be sent back....", "그러고 나서 들러리를 데리러 마차 한 대를 보내야 했고, 세르게이 이바노비치를 태우고 갈 또 다른 마차는 다시 돌려보내야 했다……"], ["Altogether there were a great many most complicated matters to be considered and arranged.", "이래저래 따지고 정리해야 할 복잡한 일들이 무척 많았다."], ["One thing was unmistakable, that there must be no delay, as it was already half-past six.", "한 가지 분명한 것은, 이미 여섯 시 반이니 지체해서는 안 된다는 것이었다."]], [["Nothing special happened at the ceremony of benediction with the holy picture.", "성화로 축복하는 의식에서는 별다른 일이 일어나지 않았다."], ["Stepan Arkadyevitch stood in a comically solemn pose beside his wife, took the holy picture, and telling Levin to bow down to the ground, he blessed him with his kindly, ironical smile, and kissed him three times; Darya Alexandrovna did the same, and immediately was in a hurry to get off, and again plunged into the intricate question of the destinations of the various carriages.", "스테판 아르카지치는 우스꽝스러울 만큼 엄숙한 자세로 아내 곁에 서서 성화를 들고는, 레빈에게 땅에 닿도록 절하라고 이르고서 다정하면서도 빈정거리는 듯한 미소를 띤 채 그를 축복하고 세 번 입을 맞추었다. 다리야 알렉산드로브나도 똑같이 하고는 곧바로 서둘러 떠나려 했고, 다시 여러 마차의 행선지라는 그 복잡한 문제 속으로 빠져들었다."]], [["“Come, I’ll tell you how we’ll manage: you drive in our carriage to fetch him, and Sergey Ivanovitch, if he’ll be so good, will drive there and then send his carriage.”", "\"자, 어떻게 할지 말해 줄게. 자네는 우리 마차를 타고 가서 그를 데려오고, 세르게이 이바노비치가 친절을 베풀어 주신다면 거기까지 가셨다가 마차를 돌려보내 주시면 돼.\""]], [["“Of course; I shall be delighted.”", "\"물론이지, 기꺼이 그렇게 하겠네.\""]], [["“We’ll come on directly with him.", "\"우리는 곧장 그와 함께 갈 거야."], ["Are your things sent off?”", "자네 짐은 다 보냈나?\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch.", "스테판 아르카지치가 말했다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"응.\""], ["answered Levin, and he told Kouzma to put out his clothes for him to dress.", "레빈이 대답하고는, 쿠지마에게 자기가 갈아입을 옷을 꺼내 놓으라고 일렀다."]], [["# Chapter 3", "# Chapter 3"]], [["A crowd of people, principally women, was thronging round the church lighted up for the wedding.", "결혼식을 위해 환하게 불을 밝힌 교회 주위로 사람들이, 주로 여자들이 떼를 지어 몰려들고 있었다."], ["Those who had not succeeded in getting into the main entrance were crowding about the windows, pushing, wrangling, and peeping through the gratings.", "정문으로 들어가지 못한 이들은 창가로 몰려가 서로 밀치고 다투며 창살 틈으로 안을 들여다보았다."]], [["More than twenty carriages had already been drawn up in ranks along the street by the police.", "이미 스무 대가 넘는 마차들이 경찰의 통제에 따라 거리를 따라 줄지어 늘어서 있었다."], ["A police officer, regardless of the frost, stood at the entrance, gorgeous in his uniform.", "한 경관이 추위에도 아랑곳없이 화려한 제복 차림으로 입구에 서 있었다."], ["More carriages were continually driving up, and ladies wearing flowers and carrying their trains, and men taking off their helmets or black hats kept walking into the church.", "마차들은 끊임없이 들어왔고, 꽃을 단 채 드레스 자락을 받쳐 든 부인들과 투구나 검은 모자를 벗는 신사들이 줄지어 교회 안으로 걸어 들어왔다."], ["Inside the church both lusters were already lighted, and all the candles before the holy pictures.", "교회 안에는 이미 양쪽 샹들리에에 불이 들어와 있었고, 성화 앞의 촛불도 모조리 켜져 있었다."], ["The gilt on the red ground of the holy picture-stand, and the gilt relief on the pictures, and the silver of the lusters and candlesticks, and the stones of the floor, and the rugs, and the banners above in the choir, and the steps of the altar, and the old blackened books, and the cassocks and surplices—all were flooded with light.", "성화대의 붉은 바탕에 입혀진 금박이며, 성화에 새겨진 금빛 부조며, 샹들리에와 촛대의 은빛이며, 바닥의 돌이며, 양탄자며, 위쪽 성가대석의 깃발이며, 제단의 계단이며, 검게 변한 낡은 책들이며, 사제복과 흰 제의까지—모든 것이 빛으로 흘러넘쳤다."], ["On the right side of the warm church, in the crowd of frock coats and white ties, uniforms and broadcloth, velvet, satin, hair and flowers, bare shoulders and arms and long gloves, there was discreet but lively conversation that echoed strangely in the high cupola.", "따뜻한 교회의 오른편에서는 프록코트와 흰 넥타이, 제복과 고급 나사천, 벨벳, 새틴, 머리 장식과 꽃, 드러난 어깨와 팔과 긴 장갑이 뒤섞인 무리 속에서, 높은 둥근 천장에 묘하게 울리는 조심스럽지만 활기찬 대화 소리가 오갔다."], ["Every time there was heard the creak of the opened door the conversation in the crowd died away, and everybody looked round expecting to see the bride and bridegroom come in.", "문이 열리며 삐걱대는 소리가 들릴 때마다 사람들 사이의 대화는 잦아들었고, 모두가 신랑 신부가 들어오기를 기대하며 돌아보았다."], ["But the door had opened more than ten times, and each time it was either a belated guest or guests, who joined the circle of the invited on the right, or a spectator, who had eluded or softened the police officer, and went to join the crowd of outsiders on the left.", "그러나 문은 열 번도 넘게 열렸지만, 그때마다 들어온 것은 오른편 초대객 무리에 합류하는 뒤늦은 손님이거나, 경관을 따돌리거나 구슬려 왼편의 구경꾼 무리에 끼어드는 구경꾼이었다."], ["Both the guests and the outside public had by now passed through all the phases of anticipation.", "초대객들도 바깥의 구경꾼들도 이제는 기다림의 온갖 단계를 다 거친 뒤였다."]], [["At first they imagined that the bride and bridegroom would arrive immediately, and attached no importance at all to their being late.", "처음에 사람들은 신랑 신부가 곧 도착하리라 생각하며 그들이 늦는 데에 전혀 의미를 두지 않았다."], ["Then they began to look more and more often towards the door, and to talk of whether anything could have happened.", "그러다 점점 더 자주 문 쪽을 바라보며 무슨 일이라도 생긴 게 아닌지 이야기하기 시작했다."], ["Then the long delay began to be positively discomforting, and relations and guests tried to look as if they were not thinking of the bridegroom but were engrossed in conversation.", "그러다 그 긴 지체가 정말이지 불편해지기 시작했고, 친척들과 손님들은 신랑 생각은 하지 않는 척, 대화에 푹 빠진 척하려고 애썼다."]], [["The head deacon, as though to remind them of the value of his time, coughed impatiently, making the window-panes quiver in their frames.", "수석 부제는 자기 시간의 소중함을 일깨우기라도 하려는 듯 못마땅하게 기침을 했고, 그 바람에 창문 유리가 틀 안에서 떨렸다."], ["In the choir the bored choristers could be heard trying their voices and blowing their noses.", "성가대석에서는 지루해진 성가대원들이 목소리를 가다듬고 코를 푸는 소리가 들렸다."], ["The priest was continually sending first the beadle and then the deacon to find out whether the bridegroom had not come, more and more often he went himself, in a lilac vestment and an embroidered sash, to the side door, expecting to see the bridegroom.", "사제는 신랑이 도착하지 않았는지 알아보라며 끊임없이 처음엔 교회지기를, 그다음엔 부제를 보냈고, 점점 더 자주 직접 라일락빛 제의에 수놓은 띠를 두른 채 옆문으로 가서 신랑이 보이기를 기대했다."], ["At last one of the ladies, glancing at her watch, said, “It really is strange, though!”", "마침내 부인들 가운데 한 사람이 시계를 보며 \"정말이지, 이상하네요!\""], ["and all the guests became uneasy and began loudly expressing their wonder and dissatisfaction.", "하고 말하자, 손님들 모두가 안절부절못하며 큰 소리로 놀라움과 불만을 토로하기 시작했다."], ["One of the bridegroom’s best men went to find out what had happened.", "신랑의 들러리 가운데 한 사람이 무슨 일이 생긴 건지 알아보러 갔다."], ["Kitty meanwhile had long ago been quite ready, and in her white dress and long veil and wreath of orange blossoms she was standing in the drawing-room of the Shtcherbatskys’ house with her sister, Madame Lvova, who was her bridal-mother.", "한편 키티는 이미 오래전에 채비를 다 마치고, 흰 드레스에 긴 면사포를 쓰고 오렌지꽃 화관을 두른 채, 신부의 어머니 역할을 맡은 언니 리보바 부인과 함께 셰르바츠키 가의 응접실에 서 있었다."], ["She was looking out of the window, and had been for over half an hour anxiously expecting to hear from the best man that her bridegroom was at the church.", "그녀는 창밖을 내다보며, 신랑이 교회에 도착했다는 소식을 들러리한테서 듣기를 반 시간이 넘도록 애타게 기다리고 있었다."]], [["Levin meanwhile, in his trousers, but without his coat and waistcoat, was walking to and fro in his room at the hotel, continually putting his head out of the door and looking up and down the corridor.", "한편 레빈은 바지만 입은 채 코트와 조끼는 걸치지 않고 호텔 방 안을 이리저리 서성이며, 연신 문밖으로 머리를 내밀어 복도의 이쪽저쪽을 살폈다."], ["But in the corridor there was no sign of the person he was looking for and he came back in despair, and frantically waving his hands addressed Stepan Arkadyevitch, who was smoking serenely.", "그러나 복도에는 그가 찾는 사람의 그림자도 보이지 않았고, 그는 절망에 빠져 돌아와서는 미친 듯이 두 손을 내저으며, 태연하게 담배를 피우고 있는 스테판 아르카지치를 향해 말을 쏟아 냈다."]], [["“Was ever a man in such a fearful fool’s position?”", "\"이런 끔찍한 바보 꼴이 된 사람이 또 있었을까?\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“Yes, it is stupid,”", "\"그래, 한심한 일이지.\""], ["Stepan Arkadyevitch assented, smiling soothingly.", "스테판 아르카지치는 달래듯 미소 지으며 동의했다."], ["“But don’t worry, it’ll be brought directly.”", "\"하지만 걱정 마, 곧 가져올 테니까.\""]], [["“No, what is to be done!”", "\"아니, 어쩌면 좋아!\""], ["said Levin, with smothered fury.", "레빈이 억눌린 분노로 말했다."], ["“And these fools of open waistcoats!", "\"게다가 이 빌어먹을 트인 조끼라니!"], ["Out of the question!”", "도저히 안 돼!\""], ["he said, looking at the crumpled front of his shirt.", "그는 구겨진 셔츠 앞섶을 바라보며 말했다."], ["“And what if the things have been taken on to the railway station!”", "\"그것들이 정거장으로 실려 가 버렸으면 어쩌지!\""], ["he roared in desperation.", "그가 절망에 차서 고함쳤다."]], [["“Then you must put on mine.”", "\"그럼 내 걸 입어야지.\""]], [["“I ought to have done so long ago, if at all.”", "\"그렇게 할 거였으면 진작에 그랬어야지.\""]], [["“It’s not nice to look ridiculous....", "\"우스꽝스러운 꼴을 보이는 건 좋지 않아……"], ["Wait a bit!", "잠깐만!"], ["it will come round.”", "잘 해결될 거야.\""]], [["The point was that when Levin asked for his evening suit, Kouzma, his old servant, had brought him the coat, waistcoat, and everything that was wanted.", "사정은 이러했다. 레빈이 예복을 가져오라고 하자, 그의 늙은 하인 쿠지마가 코트와 조끼와 필요한 것들을 다 가져왔던 것이다."]], [["“But the shirt!”", "\"그런데 셔츠는!\""], ["cried Levin.", "레빈이 외쳤다."]], [["“You’ve got a shirt on,”", "\"셔츠는 입고 계시잖습니까.\""], ["Kouzma answered, with a placid smile.", "쿠지마가 차분한 미소를 띠며 대답했다."]], [["Kouzma had not thought of leaving out a clean shirt, and on receiving instructions to pack up everything and send it round to the Shtcherbatskys’ house, from which the young people were to set out the same evening, he had done so, packing everything but the dress suit.", "쿠지마는 깨끗한 셔츠를 따로 챙겨 두어야겠다는 생각을 미처 하지 못했고, 모든 것을 꾸려서 그날 저녁 신랑 신부가 함께 출발하기로 한 셰르바츠키 가로 보내라는 지시를 받자, 그대로 따라서 예복만 빼고 모든 것을 꾸려 보냈던 것이다."], ["The shirt worn since the morning was crumpled and out of the question with the fashionable open waistcoat.", "아침부터 입고 있던 셔츠는 구겨져서 유행에 맞는 트인 조끼와는 도저히 어울리지 않았다."], ["It was a long way to send to the Shtcherbatskys’.", "셰르바츠키 가까지는 사람을 보내기에 거리가 멀었다."], ["They sent out to buy a shirt.", "그들은 셔츠를 사 오라고 사람을 내보냈다."], ["The servant came back; everything was shut up—it was Sunday.", "하인이 돌아왔는데, 모든 가게가 문을 닫았다고 했다—일요일이었던 것이다."], ["They sent to Stepan Arkadyevitch’s and brought a shirt—it was impossibly wide and short.", "스테판 아르카지치 집으로 사람을 보내 셔츠를 가져왔지만—말도 안 되게 통이 넓고 길이가 짧았다."], ["They sent finally to the Shtcherbatskys’ to unpack the things.", "결국 그들은 셰르바츠키 가로 사람을 보내 짐을 풀게 했다."], ["The bridegroom was expected at the church while he was pacing up and down his room like a wild beast in a cage, peeping out into the corridor, and with horror and despair recalling what absurd things he had said to Kitty and what she might be thinking now.", "신랑은 교회에서 기다림을 받고 있는데, 정작 그는 우리에 갇힌 들짐승처럼 방 안을 서성이며 복도를 기웃거렸고, 자기가 키티에게 무슨 터무니없는 말을 했는지, 그리고 지금 그녀가 무슨 생각을 하고 있을지 떠올리며 공포와 절망에 빠져 있었다."]], [["At last the guilty Kouzma flew panting into the room with the shirt.", "마침내 죄지은 쿠지마가 셔츠를 들고 헐떡이며 방으로 날아들었다."]], [["“Only just in time.", "\"아슬아슬하게 댔습니다."], ["They were just lifting it into the van,”", "막 짐칸에 싣고 있던 참이었어요.\""], ["said Kouzma.", "쿠지마가 말했다."]], [["Three minutes later Levin ran full speed into the corridor, not looking at his watch for fear of aggravating his sufferings.", "삼 분 뒤 레빈은 괴로움을 더할까 두려워 시계도 보지 않은 채 전속력으로 복도로 뛰쳐나갔다."]], [["“You won’t help matters like this,”", "\"이런다고 일이 해결되진 않아.\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch with a smile, hurrying with more deliberation after him.", "스테판 아르카지치가 미소를 띠며 좀 더 느긋한 걸음으로 그의 뒤를 서둘러 따라가며 말했다."], ["“It will come round, it will come round ...", "\"잘될 거야, 잘될 거라니까……"], ["I tell you.”", "내 말 믿어.\""]], [["# Chapter 4", "# Chapter 4"]], [["“They’ve come!”", "\"왔다!\""], ["“Here he is!”", "\"신랑이다!\""], ["“Which one?”", "\"어느 쪽이야?\""], ["“Rather young, eh?”", "\"꽤 젊네, 그렇지?\""], ["“Why, my dear soul, she looks more dead than alive!”", "\"어머, 세상에, 신부 얼굴이 산 사람보다 죽은 사람 같지 않니!\""], ["were the comments in the crowd, when Levin, meeting his bride in the entrance, walked with her into the church.", "레빈이 입구에서 신부를 맞아 함께 교회 안으로 들어설 때, 군중 사이에서 이런 말들이 오갔다."]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch told his wife the cause of the delay, and the guests were whispering it with smiles to one another.", "스테판 아르카지치는 아내에게 늦어진 까닭을 일러 주었고, 하객들은 미소를 지으며 그 이야기를 서로 소곤거렸다."], ["Levin saw nothing and no one; he did not take his eyes off his bride.", "레빈에게는 아무것도, 아무도 보이지 않았다. 그는 신부에게서 눈을 떼지 않았다."]], [["Everyone said she had lost her looks dreadfully of late, and was not nearly so pretty on her wedding day as usual; but Levin did not think so. He looked at her hair done up high, with the long white veil and white flowers and the high, stand-up, scalloped collar, that in such a maidenly fashion hid her long neck at the sides and only showed it in front, her strikingly slender figure, and it seemed to him that she looked better than ever—not because these flowers, this veil, this gown from Paris added anything to her beauty; but because, in spite of the elaborate sumptuousness of her attire, the expression of her sweet face, of her eyes, of her lips was still her own characteristic expression of guileless truthfulness.", "사람들은 그녀가 요즘 들어 부쩍 야위었으며, 결혼식 날인데도 평소만큼 예쁘지 않다고들 했다. 하지만 레빈은 그렇게 생각하지 않았다. 그는 높이 틀어 올린 그녀의 머리며, 긴 흰 베일과 흰 꽃이며, 옆에서는 처녀답게 긴 목을 가리고 앞에서만 드러내는 높직한 가리비 무늬 깃이며, 놀랍도록 가냘픈 자태를 바라보았다. 그에게는 그녀가 그 어느 때보다 아름다워 보였다. 이 꽃이며 이 베일이며 파리에서 온 이 드레스가 그녀의 아름다움에 무언가를 보태서가 아니라, 공들여 차려입은 화려한 의상에도 불구하고 그녀의 사랑스러운 얼굴이며 눈이며 입술의 표정이 여전히 거짓 없는 진실함이라는 그녀만의 표정 그대로였기 때문이다."]], [["“I was beginning to think you meant to run away,”", "\"당신이 도망쳐 버린 줄 알았어요.\""], ["she said, and smiled to him.", "그녀가 그에게 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["“It’s so stupid, what happened to me, I’m ashamed to speak of it!”", "\"나한테 일어난 일이 너무 어처구니없어서, 말하기도 부끄러워요!\""], ["he said, reddening, and he was obliged to turn to Sergey Ivanovitch, who came up to him.", "그는 얼굴을 붉히며 말했고, 마침 다가온 세르게이 이바노비치에게로 몸을 돌려야 했다."]], [["“This is a pretty story of yours about the shirt!”", "\"셔츠 이야기는 참 가관이더군!\""], ["said Sergey Ivanovitch, shaking his head and smiling.", "세르게이 이바노비치가 고개를 가로저으며 미소 지었다."]], [["“Yes, yes!”", "\"네, 네!\""], ["answered Levin, without an idea of what they were talking about.", "레빈은 무슨 이야기를 하는지도 모른 채 대답했다."]], [["“Now, Kostya, you have to decide,”", "\"자, 코스챠, 자네가 결정해야 해.\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch with an air of mock dismay, “a weighty question.", "스테판 아르카지치가 짐짓 난처한 척하며 말했다. \"중대한 문제야."], ["You are at this moment just in the humor to appreciate all its gravity.", "지금 이 순간 자네야말로 그 무게를 제대로 알아줄 기분일 테지."], ["They ask me, are they to light the candles that have been lighted before or candles that have never been lighted?", "사람들이 내게 묻는단 말이야, 전에 한 번 켰던 양초를 켜야 하느냐, 아니면 한 번도 안 켠 새 양초를 켜야 하느냐고."], ["It’s a matter of ten roubles,”", "십 루블이 걸린 문제야.\""], ["he added, relaxing his lips into a smile.", "그는 입가를 풀고 미소 지으며 덧붙였다."], ["“I have decided, but I was afraid you might not agree.”", "\"난 이미 결정했지만, 자네가 동의하지 않을까 봐 걱정이었어.\""]], [["Levin saw it was a joke, but he could not smile.", "레빈은 그것이 농담임을 알았지만 미소 지을 수 없었다."]], [["“Well, how’s it to be then?—unlighted or lighted candles?", "\"자, 그래서 어떻게 할까? 안 켠 양초야, 켰던 양초야?"], ["that’s the question.”", "그게 문제야.\""]], [["“Yes, yes, unlighted.”", "\"네, 네, 안 켠 걸로.\""]], [["“Oh, I’m very glad.", "\"오, 정말 다행이군."], ["The question’s decided!”", "문제가 해결됐어!\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, smiling.", "스테판 아르카지치가 미소 지으며 말했다."], ["“How silly men are, though, in this position,”", "레빈이 멍하니 그를 바라보다가 다시 신부 곁으로 물러나자, 그는 치리코프에게 말했다."], ["he said to Tchirikov, when Levin, after looking absently at him, had moved back to his bride.", "\"그런데 이런 처지에 놓이면 사내들이 어쩜 이렇게 멍청해지는지.\""]], [["“Kitty, mind you’re the first to step on the carpet,”", "\"키티, 카펫에 네가 제일 먼저 발을 디뎌야 한다는 거 잊지 마.\""], ["said Countess Nordston, coming up.", "노르드스톤 백작 부인이 다가오며 말했다."], ["“You’re a nice person!”", "\"당신은 참 좋은 사람이에요!\""], ["she said to Levin.", "그녀가 레빈에게 말했다."]], [["“Aren’t you frightened, eh?”", "\"안 무섭니, 응?\""], ["said Marya Dmitrievna, an old aunt.", "늙은 숙모 마리야 드미트리예브나가 말했다."]], [["“Are you cold?", "\"춥니?"], ["You’re pale.", "얼굴이 창백하구나."], ["Stop a minute, stoop down,”", "잠깐만, 고개를 좀 숙여 봐.\""], ["said Kitty’s sister, Madame Lvova, and with her plump, handsome arms she smilingly set straight the flowers on her head.", "키티의 언니 리보바 부인이 말하며 통통하고 어여쁜 두 팔로 미소 지으며 동생 머리 위의 꽃을 매만져 주었다."]], [["Dolly came up, tried to say something, but could not speak, cried, and then laughed unnaturally.", "돌리가 다가와 무슨 말을 하려 했지만 말이 나오지 않았고, 울다가 부자연스럽게 웃었다."]], [["Kitty looked at all of them with the same absent eyes as Levin.", "키티는 레빈과 똑같이 멍한 눈으로 그들 모두를 바라보았다."]], [["Meanwhile the officiating clergy had got into their vestments, and the priest and deacon came out to the lectern, which stood in the forepart of the church.", "그러는 사이 집전하는 성직자들이 제의를 갖춰 입었고, 사제와 부제가 교회 앞쪽에 놓인 성서대로 나왔다."], ["The priest turned to Levin saying something.", "사제가 레빈을 향해 무어라 말했다."], ["Levin did not hear what the priest said.", "레빈은 사제가 무슨 말을 하는지 듣지 못했다."]], [["“Take the bride’s hand and lead her up,”", "\"신부의 손을 잡고 모시고 오십시오.\""], ["the best man said to Levin.", "들러리가 레빈에게 말했다."]], [["It was a long while before Levin could make out what was expected of him.", "레빈이 자기에게 무엇이 요구되는지 알아차리기까지는 한참이 걸렸다."], ["For a long time they tried to set him right and made him begin again—because he kept taking Kitty by the wrong arm or with the wrong arm—till he understood at last that what he had to do was, without changing his position, to take her right hand in his right hand.", "그가 자꾸 키티의 엉뚱한 팔을 잡거나 엉뚱한 팔로 잡았기 때문에, 사람들은 오랫동안 그를 바로잡아 주고 다시 시작하게 했다. 마침내 그는 자기 위치를 바꾸지 않은 채 오른손으로 그녀의 오른손을 잡아야 한다는 것을 깨달았다."], ["When at last he had taken the bride’s hand in the correct way, the priest walked a few paces in front of them and stopped at the lectern.", "마침내 그가 올바른 방식으로 신부의 손을 잡자, 사제가 그들 앞으로 몇 걸음 걸어가 성서대 앞에 멈춰 섰다."], ["The crowd of friends and relations moved after them, with a buzz of talk and a rustle of skirts.", "친구들과 친척들의 무리가 두런거리는 말소리와 치맛자락 스치는 소리를 내며 그들을 뒤따라 움직였다."], ["Someone stooped down and pulled out the bride’s train.", "누군가 몸을 숙여 신부의 끌리는 옷자락을 펴 주었다."], ["The church became so still that the drops of wax could be heard falling from the candles.", "교회 안이 어찌나 고요해졌던지 양초에서 밀랍 방울이 떨어지는 소리까지 들릴 정도였다."]], [["The little old priest in his ecclesiastical cap, with his long silvery-gray locks of hair parted behind his ears, was fumbling with something at the lectern, putting out his little old hands from under the heavy silver vestment with the gold cross on the back of it.", "성직자용 모자를 쓴 자그마한 노사제는, 귀 뒤로 가른 긴 은회색 머리채를 늘어뜨린 채, 등에 금빛 십자가가 새겨진 묵직한 은빛 제의 아래로 자그마한 늙은 두 손을 내밀어 성서대에서 무언가를 더듬거리고 있었다."]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch approached him cautiously, whispered something, and making a sign to Levin, walked back again.", "스테판 아르카지치가 조심스레 그에게 다가가 무어라 속삭이고는, 레빈에게 신호를 보낸 뒤 다시 제자리로 걸어왔다."]], [["The priest lighted two candles, wreathed with flowers, and holding them sideways so that the wax dropped slowly from them he turned, facing the bridal pair.", "사제는 꽃으로 두른 양초 두 자루에 불을 붙이고는, 밀랍이 천천히 떨어지도록 비스듬히 든 채 신랑 신부를 향해 몸을 돌렸다."], ["The priest was the same old man that had confessed Levin.", "그 사제는 전에 레빈의 고해를 받았던 바로 그 노인이었다."], ["He looked with weary and melancholy eyes at the bride and bridegroom, sighed, and putting his right hand out from his vestment, blessed the bridegroom with it, and also with a shade of solicitous tenderness laid the crossed fingers on the bowed head of Kitty.", "그는 지치고 우수에 잠긴 눈으로 신부와 신랑을 바라보고는 한숨을 쉰 뒤, 제의 아래로 오른손을 내밀어 신랑을 축복하고, 또한 자애로운 다정함을 살짝 머금은 채 십자로 모은 손가락을 키티의 숙인 머리 위에 얹었다."], ["Then he gave them the candles, and taking the censer, moved slowly away from them.", "그러고는 그들에게 양초를 건네고, 향로를 들어 그들에게서 천천히 멀어졌다."]], [["“Can it be true?”", "'정말일까?'"], ["thought Levin, and he looked round at his bride.", "레빈은 생각하며 신부를 돌아보았다."], ["Looking down at her he saw her face in profile, and from the scarcely perceptible quiver of her lips and eyelashes he knew she was aware of his eyes upon her.", "그녀를 내려다보다가 옆얼굴을 보게 되었는데, 입술과 속눈썹이 거의 알아챌 수 없을 만큼 떨리는 것을 보고 그녀가 자기 시선을 의식하고 있음을 알았다."], ["She did not look round, but the high scalloped collar, that reached her little pink ear, trembled faintly.", "그녀는 돌아보지 않았지만, 그녀의 작고 분홍빛인 귀에까지 닿는 높직한 가리비 무늬 깃이 희미하게 떨렸다."], ["He saw that a sigh was held back in her throat, and the little hand in the long glove shook as it held the candle.", "그는 그녀의 목구멍에서 한숨이 참아지는 것을, 긴 장갑 속의 작은 손이 양초를 든 채 떨리는 것을 보았다."]], [["All the fuss of the shirt, of being late, all the talk of friends and relations, their annoyance, his ludicrous position—all suddenly passed away and he was filled with joy and dread.", "셔츠 소동이며, 늦은 것이며, 친구들과 친척들의 온갖 말이며, 그들의 짜증이며, 자신의 우스꽝스러운 처지며—이 모든 것이 별안간 사라지고, 그는 기쁨과 두려움으로 가득 찼다."]], [["The handsome, stately head-deacon wearing a silver robe and his curly locks standing out at each side of his head, stepped smartly forward, and lifting his stole on two fingers, stood opposite the priest.", "은빛 제의를 걸치고 곱슬머리를 머리 양옆으로 뻗친 잘생기고 위엄 있는 수석 부제가 날렵하게 앞으로 나서, 두 손가락으로 영대를 받쳐 들고 사제 맞은편에 섰다."]], [["“Blessed be the name of the Lord,”", "\"주님의 이름이 찬양받으소서.\""], ["the solemn syllables rang out slowly one after another, setting the air quivering with waves of sound.", "엄숙한 음절들이 하나하나 천천히 울려 퍼지며, 소리의 물결로 공기를 떨게 했다."]], [["“Blessed is the name of our God, from the beginning, is now, and ever shall be,”", "\"우리 하느님의 이름이 태초로부터 찬양받으시며, 지금도 그러하시고 영원토록 그러하시리이다.\""], ["the little old priest answered in a submissive, piping voice, still fingering something at the lectern.", "자그마한 노사제가 순종하는 듯한 가느다란 목소리로, 여전히 성서대에서 무언가를 더듬거리며 응답했다."], ["And the full chorus of the unseen choir rose up, filling the whole church, from the windows to the vaulted roof, with broad waves of melody.", "그러자 보이지 않는 성가대의 충만한 합창이 솟아올라, 창문부터 둥근 천장에 이르기까지 온 교회를 넓은 선율의 물결로 가득 채웠다."], ["It grew stronger, rested for an instant, and slowly died away.", "그 소리는 점점 강해지다가 한순간 멎었고, 천천히 잦아들었다."]], [["They prayed, as they always do, for peace from on high and for salvation, for the Holy Synod, and for the Tsar; they prayed, too, for the servants of God, Konstantin and Ekaterina, now plighting their troth.", "그들은 늘 그러하듯 높은 곳에서 내려오는 평화와 구원을 위하여, 거룩한 종교 회의를 위하여, 차르를 위하여 기도했다. 또한 이제 서로 백년가약을 맺는 하느님의 종 콘스탄틴과 예카테리나를 위하여 기도했다."]], [["“Vouchsafe to them love made perfect, peace and help, O Lord, we beseech Thee,”", "\"주여, 비옵나니 그들에게 온전한 사랑과 평화와 도우심을 베푸소서.\""], ["the whole church seemed to breathe with the voice of the head deacon.", "온 교회가 수석 부제의 목소리에 실려 숨을 쉬는 듯했다."]], [["Levin heard the words, and they impressed him.", "레빈은 그 말을 듣고 마음 깊이 새겼다."], ["“How did they guess that it is help, just help that one wants?”", "'저들은 어떻게 사람이 원하는 게 바로 도움이라는 걸, 다른 게 아니라 도움이라는 걸 알았을까?'"], ["he thought, recalling all his fears and doubts of late.", "그는 요즘 들어 느꼈던 온갖 두려움과 의혹을 떠올리며 생각했다."], ["“What do I know? what can I do in this fearful business,”", "'도움 없이 이 무서운 일에서 내가 뭘 알고, 뭘 할 수 있단 말인가.'"], ["he thought, “without help?", "그는 생각했다."], ["Yes, it is help I want now.”", "'그래, 내가 지금 원하는 건 도움이야.'"]], [["When the deacon had finished the prayer for the Imperial family, the priest turned to the bridal pair with a book: “Eternal God, that joinest together in love them that were separate,”", "부제가 황실을 위한 기도를 마치자, 사제가 책을 들고 신랑 신부를 향해 돌아섰다. \"갈라져 있던 자들을 사랑으로 하나 되게 하시는 영원하신 하느님이시여.\""], ["he read in a gentle, piping voice: “who hast ordained the union of holy wedlock that cannot be set asunder, Thou who didst bless Isaac and Rebecca and their descendants, according to Thy Holy Covenant; bless Thy servants, Konstantin and Ekaterina, leading them in the path of all good works.", "그는 부드럽고 가느다란 목소리로 읽었다. \"갈라놓을 수 없는 거룩한 혼인의 결합을 정하셨고, 당신의 거룩한 언약을 따라 이삭과 리브가와 그들의 후손을 축복하신 주여, 당신의 종 콘스탄틴과 예카테리나를 축복하시어 그들을 온갖 선한 일의 길로 이끄소서."], ["For gracious and merciful art Thou, our Lord, and glory be to Thee, the Father, the Son, and the Holy Ghost, now and ever shall be.”", "자비롭고 인자하신 주여, 성부와 성자와 성령께 지금도 영원토록 영광이 있을지어다.\""]], [["“Amen!”", "\"아멘!\""], ["the unseen choir sent rolling again upon the air.", "보이지 않는 성가대가 다시금 공중에 그 소리를 굴려 보냈다."]], [["“‘Joinest together in love them that were separate.’ What deep meaning in those words, and how they correspond with what one feels at this moment,”", "'갈라져 있던 자들을 사랑으로 하나 되게 하신다.' 그 말 속에 얼마나 깊은 뜻이 있는가, 그리고 이 순간 느끼는 감정과 얼마나 잘 들어맞는가.'"], ["thought Levin.", "레빈은 생각했다."], ["“Is she feeling the same as I?”", "'그녀도 나와 같은 걸 느끼고 있을까?'"]], [["And looking round, he met her eyes, and from their expression he concluded that she was understanding it just as he was.", "그리고 돌아보다가 그녀의 눈과 마주쳤고, 그 눈빛에서 그녀도 자기와 똑같이 그것을 이해하고 있다고 결론지었다. 하지만 이는 착각이었다."], ["But this was a mistake; she almost completely missed the meaning of the words of the service; she had not heard them, in fact.", "그녀는 예식의 말뜻을 거의 완전히 놓치고 있었다. 사실 그녀는 그 말을 듣지조차 못했다."], ["She could not listen to them and take them in, so strong was the one feeling that filled her breast and grew stronger and stronger.", "그녀의 가슴을 채우고 점점 더 강해지는 그 한 가지 감정이 너무도 강렬해서, 그녀는 그것을 듣고 받아들일 수 없었다."], ["That feeling was joy at the completion of the process that for the last month and a half had been going on in her soul, and had during those six weeks been a joy and a torture to her.", "그 감정은 지난 한 달 반 동안 그녀의 영혼 속에서 진행되어 온 과정이, 그 여섯 주 내내 그녀에게 기쁨이자 고통이었던 그 과정이 완결된 데 대한 기쁨이었다."], ["On the day when in the drawing-room of the house in Arbaty Street she had gone up to him in her brown dress, and given herself to him without a word—on that day, at that hour, there took place in her heart a complete severance from all her old life, and a quite different, new, utterly strange life had begun for her, while the old life was actually going on as before.", "아르바티 거리 저택의 응접실에서 갈색 드레스를 입고 그에게 다가가 말없이 자신을 내맡겼던 그날, 바로 그 시각, 그녀의 마음속에서는 옛 삶 전체와의 완전한 단절이 일어났고, 전혀 다른, 새로운, 더없이 낯선 삶이 그녀에게 시작되었다. 그러면서도 옛 삶은 실제로는 예전처럼 이어지고 있었다."], ["Those six weeks had for her been a time of the utmost bliss and the utmost misery.", "그 여섯 주는 그녀에게 더없는 행복이자 더없는 비참함의 시간이었다."], ["All her life, all her desires and hopes were concentrated on this one man, still uncomprehended by her, to whom she was bound by a feeling of alternate attraction and repulsion, even less comprehended than the man himself, and all the while she was going on living in the outward conditions of her old life.", "그녀의 온 삶이, 온갖 욕망과 희망이 이 한 남자에게 집중되어 있었다. 그녀는 아직 그를 다 이해하지 못했고, 그를 향한 끌림과 밀어냄이 번갈아 일어나는 감정—그 남자 자신보다도 더 이해할 수 없는 감정—으로 그에게 묶여 있었으며, 그러는 내내 옛 삶의 외적 조건 속에서 계속 살아가고 있었다."], ["Living the old life, she was horrified at herself, at her utter insurmountable callousness to all her own past, to things, to habits, to the people she had loved, who loved her—to her mother, who was wounded by her indifference, to her kind, tender father, till then dearer than all the world.", "옛 삶을 살면서 그녀는 자기 자신에게, 자신의 모든 과거에 대해, 사물에 대해, 습관에 대해, 자기가 사랑했고 자기를 사랑해 준 사람들에 대해—자신의 무관심에 상처받은 어머니에 대해, 그때까지 세상 누구보다 소중했던 다정하고 자상한 아버지에 대해—느끼는 도무지 넘어설 수 없는 냉담함에 소스라치게 놀랐다."], ["At one moment she was horrified at this indifference, at another she rejoiced at what had brought her to this indifference.", "어느 순간에는 이 냉담함에 소스라치게 놀랐고, 또 어느 순간에는 자기를 이 냉담함으로 이끈 것을 기뻐했다."], ["She could not frame a thought, not a wish apart from life with this man; but this new life was not yet, and she could not even picture it clearly to herself.", "그녀는 이 남자와의 삶을 떠나서는 어떤 생각도, 어떤 소망도 떠올릴 수 없었다. 하지만 이 새로운 삶은 아직 오지 않았고, 그녀는 그것을 또렷이 그려 볼 수조차 없었다."], ["There was only anticipation, the dread and joy of the new and the unknown.", "오직 기대만이, 새롭고 알 수 없는 것에 대한 두려움과 기쁨만이 있을 뿐이었다."], ["And now behold—anticipation and uncertainty and remorse at the abandonment of the old life—all was ending, and the new was beginning.", "그리고 이제 보라—기대와 불확실함과 옛 삶을 버리는 데 대한 회한, 그 모든 것이 끝나가고 새것이 시작되고 있었다."], ["This new life could not but have terrors for her inexperience; but, terrible or not, the change had been wrought six weeks before in her soul, and this was merely the final sanction of what had long been completed in her heart.", "이 새로운 삶이 미숙한 그녀에게 두려움을 안기지 않을 수 없었지만, 두렵든 아니든 그 변화는 여섯 주 전에 이미 그녀의 영혼 속에서 이루어졌으며, 이것은 그저 오래전에 그녀의 마음속에서 완결된 것에 대한 마지막 추인일 뿐이었다."]], [["Turning again to the lectern, the priest with some difficulty took Kitty’s little ring, and asking Levin for his hand, put it on the first joint of his finger.", "다시 성서대를 향해 돌아선 사제는 다소 힘겹게 키티의 작은 반지를 집어 들고, 레빈에게 손을 달라고 한 뒤 그의 손가락 첫 마디에 끼웠다."], ["“The servant of God, Konstantin, plights his troth to the servant of God, Ekaterina.”", "\"하느님의 종 콘스탄틴이 하느님의 종 예카테리나에게 백년가약을 맺나이다.\""], ["And putting his big ring on Kitty’s touchingly weak, pink little finger, the priest said the same thing.", "그러고는 키티의 가련하리만치 가냘프고 분홍빛인 작은 손가락에 그의 큰 반지를 끼우며, 사제는 같은 말을 했다."]], [["And the bridal pair tried several times to understand what they had to do, and each time made some mistake and were corrected by the priest in a whisper.", "신랑 신부는 자기들이 무엇을 해야 하는지 여러 번 알아내려 애썼지만, 그때마다 무언가 실수를 했고 사제가 속삭이며 바로잡아 주었다."], ["At last, having duly performed the ceremony, having signed the rings with the cross, the priest handed Kitty the big ring, and Levin the little one.", "마침내 예식을 제대로 마치고 반지를 십자로 축성한 뒤, 사제는 키티에게 큰 반지를, 레빈에게 작은 반지를 건넸다."], ["Again they were puzzled, and passed the rings from hand to hand, still without doing what was expected.", "그들은 또다시 어리둥절해하며 반지를 손에서 손으로 주고받았지만, 여전히 요구되는 대로 하지 못했다."]], [["Dolly, Tchirikov, and Stepan Arkadyevitch stepped forward to set them right.", "돌리와 치리코프와 스테판 아르카지치가 그들을 바로잡아 주려고 앞으로 나섰다."], ["There was an interval of hesitation, whispering, and smiles; but the expression of solemn emotion on the faces of the betrothed pair did not change: on the contrary, in their perplexity over their hands they looked more grave and deeply moved than before, and the smile with which Stepan Arkadyevitch whispered to them that now they would each put on their own ring died away on his lips.", "잠시 망설임과 소곤거림과 미소가 오갔지만, 약혼한 두 사람의 얼굴에 어린 엄숙한 감동의 표정은 변하지 않았다. 오히려 손을 두고 쩔쩔매는 그 와중에 그들은 전보다 더 진지하고 깊이 감동한 듯 보였고, 이제 각자 자기 반지를 끼우면 된다고 그들에게 속삭이던 스테판 아르카지치의 미소는 입가에서 사라져 버렸다."], ["He had a feeling that any smile would jar on them.", "그는 어떤 미소든 그들에게 거슬릴 것만 같은 느낌이 들었다."]], [["“Thou who didst from the beginning create male and female,”", "\"태초에 남자와 여자를 지으신 주여.\""], ["the priest read after the exchange of rings, “from Thee woman was given to man to be a helpmeet to him, and for the procreation of children.", "사제는 반지를 주고받은 뒤 읽었다. \"당신으로부터 여자가 남자에게 돕는 배필이 되고 자손을 낳도록 주어졌나이다."], ["O Lord, our God, who hast poured down the blessings of Thy Truth according to Thy Holy Covenant upon Thy chosen servants, our fathers, from generation to generation, bless Thy servants Konstantin and Ekaterina, and make their troth fast in faith, and union of hearts, and truth, and love....”", "우리 주 하느님이시여, 당신의 거룩한 언약을 따라 당신이 택하신 종들, 우리 조상들에게 대대로 당신의 진리의 복을 부어 주신 주여, 당신의 종 콘스탄틴과 예카테리나를 축복하시어, 그들의 가약을 믿음과 마음의 하나 됨과 진리와 사랑 안에 굳건히 하소서…….\""]], [["Levin felt more and more that all his ideas of marriage, all his dreams of how he would order his life, were mere childishness, and that it was something he had not understood hitherto, and now understood less than ever, though it was being performed upon him.", "레빈은 결혼에 대한 자신의 온갖 생각이, 어떻게 삶을 꾸려 가겠다던 온갖 꿈이 그저 어린아이 같은 것이었음을, 그리고 그것이 여태껏 자기가 이해하지 못했고 지금은 그 어느 때보다 더 이해할 수 없는 무언가임을, 비록 그 일이 자기에게 행해지고 있는데도 그러함을 점점 더 절실히 느꼈다."], ["The lump in his throat rose higher and higher, tears that would not be checked came into his eyes.", "목구멍에 치미는 덩어리가 점점 더 높이 솟아올랐고, 억누를 수 없는 눈물이 그의 눈에 고였다."]], [["# Chapter 5", "# Chapter 5"]], [["In the church there was all Moscow, all the friends and relations; and during the ceremony of plighting troth, in the brilliantly lighted church, there was an incessant flow of discreetly subdued talk in the circle of gaily dressed women and girls, and men in white ties, frockcoats, and uniforms.", "교회 안에는 온 모스크바가, 모든 친구들과 친척들이 모여 있었다. 그리고 약혼 의식이 거행되는 동안, 환하게 불을 밝힌 교회 안에서는 화려하게 차려입은 부인들과 아가씨들, 그리고 흰 넥타이에 프록코트나 제복을 입은 남자들의 무리 사이로 조심스레 낮춘 이야기 소리가 끊임없이 흘러나왔다."], ["The talk was principally kept up by the men, while the women were absorbed in watching every detail of the ceremony, which always means so much to them.", "이야기는 주로 남자들이 이어 갔고, 여자들은 그네들에게 늘 그토록 의미가 큰 의식의 자질구레한 부분 하나하나를 지켜보는 데 온통 정신을 쏟고 있었다."]], [["In the little group nearest to the bride were her two sisters: Dolly, and the other one, the self-possessed beauty, Madame Lvova, who had just arrived from abroad.", "신부에게 가장 가까운 작은 무리에는 그녀의 두 언니가 있었다. 돌리와, 또 한 사람, 침착하고 아름다운 르보바 부인이었는데, 그녀는 막 외국에서 돌아온 참이었다."]], [["“Why is it Marie’s in lilac, as bad as black, at a wedding?”", "\"왜 마리는 결혼식에 검은색이나 다름없는 연보라색을 입었을까?\""], ["said Madame Korsunskaya.", "코르순스카야 부인이 말했다."]], [["“With her complexion, it’s the one salvation,”", "\"그 사람 안색에는 그게 유일한 구원이거든요.\""], ["responded Madame Trubetskaya.", "트루베츠카야 부인이 대꾸했다."], ["“I wonder why they had the wedding in the evening?", "\"그런데 왜 저녁에 식을 올렸을까요?"], ["It’s like shop-people....”", "꼭 장사꾼들 같잖아요…….\""]], [["“So much prettier.", "\"그게 훨씬 더 예쁜걸요."], ["I was married in the evening too....”", "나도 저녁에 결혼했답니다…….\""], ["answered Madame Korsunskaya, and she sighed, remembering how charming she had been that day, and how absurdly in love her husband was, and how different it all was now.", "코르순스카야 부인이 대답하며 한숨을 내쉬었다. 그날 자기가 얼마나 매력적이었는지, 남편이 얼마나 우스꽝스러울 만큼 자기에게 빠져 있었는지, 그리고 지금은 모든 것이 얼마나 달라졌는지를 떠올리면서."]], [["“They say if anyone’s best man more than ten times, he’ll never be married.", "\"누구든 들러리를 열 번 넘게 서면 영영 결혼을 못 한대요."], ["I wanted to be for the tenth time, but the post was taken,”", "나는 열 번째로 서고 싶었는데, 그 자리를 빼앗겨 버렸지 뭡니까.\""], ["said Count Siniavin to the pretty Princess Tcharskaya, who had designs on him.", "시니아빈 백작이 자기에게 마음을 두고 있던 예쁜 차르스카야 공작 영애에게 말했다."]], [["Princess Tcharskaya only answered with a smile.", "차르스카야 공작 영애는 그저 미소로 대답할 뿐이었다."], ["She looked at Kitty, thinking how and when she would stand with Count Siniavin in Kitty’s place, and how she would remind him then of his joke today.", "그녀는 키티를 바라보면서, 자기가 어떻게, 또 언제 키티의 자리에 시니아빈 백작과 나란히 서게 될지, 그리고 그때 그에게 오늘의 이 농담을 어떻게 일깨워 줄지를 생각했다."]], [["Shtcherbatsky told the old maid of honor, Madame Nikolaeva, that he meant to put the crown on Kitty’s chignon for luck.", "시체르바츠키는 늙은 궁녀 니콜라예바 부인에게, 행운을 빌어 주려고 키티의 시뇽 위에 관을 씌워 줄 작정이라고 말했다."]], [["“She ought not to have worn a chignon,”", "\"시뇽을 하지 말았어야 했어요.\""], ["answered Madame Nikolaeva, who had long ago made up her mind that if the elderly widower she was angling for married her, the wedding should be of the simplest.", "니콜라예바 부인이 대답했다. 그녀는 자기가 노리고 있는 그 나이 든 홀아비가 자기와 결혼하게 된다면 식은 더없이 소박하게 치러야겠다고 오래전부터 마음먹고 있었다."], ["“I don’t like such grandeur.”", "\"난 그런 화려한 게 싫어요.\""]], [["Sergey Ivanovitch was talking to Darya Dmitrievna, jestingly assuring her that the custom of going away after the wedding was becoming common because newly married people always felt a little ashamed of themselves.", "세르게이 이바노비치는 다리야 드미트리예브나에게, 결혼식이 끝나면 곧장 떠나 버리는 풍습이 점점 흔해지는 건 갓 결혼한 사람들이 늘 스스로를 조금 부끄러워하기 때문이라며 농담조로 장담하고 있었다."]], [["“Your brother may feel proud of himself.", "\"동생분은 자랑스러워해도 되겠어요."], ["She’s a marvel of sweetness.", "신부는 더없이 사랑스러운 보물이잖아요."], ["I believe you’re envious.”", "당신은 부러운 거 아니에요?\""]], [["“Oh, I’ve got over that, Darya Dmitrievna,”", "\"오, 그런 마음은 이미 다 지나갔답니다, 다리야 드미트리예브나.\""], ["he answered, and a melancholy and serious expression suddenly came over his face.", "그가 대답했고, 우울하고 진지한 표정이 별안간 그의 얼굴에 떠올랐다."]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch was telling his sister-in-law his joke about divorce.", "스테판 아르카지치는 손위 처형에게 이혼에 관한 자기 농담을 늘어놓고 있었다."]], [["“The wreath wants setting straight,”", "\"화관을 똑바로 고쳐야겠어요.\""], ["she answered, not hearing him.", "그녀는 그의 말을 듣지도 않은 채 대답했다."]], [["“What a pity she’s lost her looks so,”", "\"저렇게 미모가 상해 버리다니 안됐어요.\""], ["Countess Nordston said to Madame Lvova.", "노르드스톤 백작 부인이 르보바 부인에게 말했다."], ["“Still he’s not worth her little finger, is he?”", "\"그래도 저 남자는 신부의 새끼손가락만 한 가치도 없잖아요, 그렇죠?\""]], [["“Oh, I like him so—not because he’s my future beau-frère,”", "\"오, 난 저이가 참 좋아요. 장차 내 beau-frère가 될 사람이라서가 아니라요.\""], ["answered Madame Lvova.", "르보바 부인이 대답했다."], ["“And how well he’s behaving!", "\"그리고 저이가 얼마나 처신을 잘하는지 보세요!"], ["It’s so difficult, too, to look well in such a position, not to be ridiculous.", "저런 자리에서 우스꽝스러워지지 않고 의젓하게 보이는 게 어디 쉬운 일인가요. 그런데 저이는 우스꽝스럽지도, 거드름을 피우지도 않잖아요."], ["And he’s not ridiculous, and not affected; one can see he’s moved.”", "마음이 움직이고 있다는 게 보인다니까요.\" * beau-frère: 프랑스어로 '매부, 동서'."]], [["“You expected it, I suppose?”", "\"이렇게 될 줄 짐작하고 계셨겠죠?\""]], [["“Almost.", "\"거의요."], ["She always cared for him.”", "저 애는 늘 저이를 마음에 두고 있었으니까요.\""]], [["“Well, we shall see which of them will step on the rug first.", "\"자, 그럼 둘 중 누가 먼저 깔개를 밟는지 두고 보자고요."], ["I warned Kitty.”", "난 키티한테 미리 일러두었어요.\""]], [["“It will make no difference,”", "\"그래 봤자 별 차이 없을걸요.\" 르보바 부인이 말했다."], ["said Madame Lvova; “we’re all obedient wives; it’s in our family.”", "\"우리는 다 고분고분한 아내들이잖아요. 그게 우리 집안 내력이거든요.\""]], [["“Oh, I stepped on the rug before Vassily on purpose.", "\"오, 난 일부러 바실리보다 먼저 깔개를 밟았는데."], ["And you, Dolly?”", "당신은요, 돌리?\""]], [["Dolly stood beside them; she heard them, but she did not answer.", "돌리는 그들 곁에 서 있었다. 그녀는 그 말을 들었지만 대답하지 않았다."], ["She was deeply moved.", "그녀는 깊이 마음이 움직인 상태였다."], ["The tears stood in her eyes, and she could not have spoken without crying.", "두 눈에 눈물이 고여, 울지 않고서는 한마디도 할 수 없었을 것이다."], ["She was rejoicing over Kitty and Levin; going back in thought to her own wedding, she glanced at the radiant figure of Stepan Arkadyevitch, forgot all the present, and remembered only her own innocent love.", "그녀는 키티와 레빈을 보며 기뻐하고 있었다. 생각 속에서 자기 자신의 결혼식으로 되돌아가, 환하게 빛나는 스테판 아르카지치의 모습을 흘끗 바라보다가, 지금의 모든 것을 잊고 오직 자기의 순결했던 사랑만을 떠올렸다."], ["She recalled not herself only, but all her women-friends and acquaintances.", "그녀는 자기 자신만이 아니라, 모든 여자 친구들과 아는 여인들까지 떠올렸다."], ["She thought of them on the one day of their triumph, when they had stood like Kitty under the wedding crown, with love and hope and dread in their hearts, renouncing the past, and stepping forward into the mysterious future.", "그네들의 영광스러운 그 하루를, 그네들이 키티처럼 결혼 화관 아래 서서, 가슴속에 사랑과 희망과 두려움을 품은 채 과거를 등지고 신비로운 미래를 향해 발을 내딛던 그 순간을 떠올렸다."], ["Among the brides that came back to her memory, she thought too of her darling Anna, of whose proposed divorce she had just been hearing.", "기억 속에 되살아난 신부들 가운데, 그녀는 사랑하는 안나도 떠올렸다. 안나가 이혼을 제기하려 한다는 이야기를 그녀는 막 전해 들은 참이었다."], ["And she had stood just as innocent in orange flowers and bridal veil.", "안나도 오렌지꽃과 면사포를 두르고 꼭 저렇게 순결하게 서 있었더랬다. 그런데 지금은?"], ["And now? “It’s terribly strange,”", "\"정말 끔찍하게도 이상한 일이야.\""], ["she said to herself.", "그녀는 혼잣말을 했다."], ["It was not merely the sisters, the women-friends and female relations of the bride who were following every detail of the ceremony.", "의식의 자질구레한 부분 하나하나를 좇고 있는 것은 비단 신부의 언니들이며 여자 친구들이며 여자 친척들만이 아니었다."], ["Women who were quite strangers, mere spectators, were watching it excitedly, holding their breath, in fear of losing a single movement or expression of the bride and bridegroom, and angrily not answering, often not hearing, the remarks of the callous men, who kept making joking or irrelevant observations.", "전혀 모르는 여인들, 그저 구경 온 사람들까지도 신랑 신부의 동작이나 표정을 단 하나라도 놓칠세라 숨을 죽인 채 들떠서 지켜보고 있었고, 줄곧 농담이나 엉뚱한 소리를 늘어놓는 무신경한 남자들의 말에는 화를 내며 대꾸도 않거나, 흔히 아예 듣지도 않았다."]], [["“Why has she been crying?", "\"왜 저 신부는 울고 있는 거야?"], ["Is she being married against her will?”", "마음에도 없는 결혼을 하는 건가?\""]], [["“Against her will to a fine fellow like that?", "\"저렇게 멋진 사내한테 마음에도 없이 시집을 가?"], ["A prince, isn’t he?”", "공작이라잖아, 안 그래?\""]], [["“Is that her sister in the white satin?", "\"흰 새틴을 입은 저 사람이 신부 언니인가?"], ["Just listen how the deacon booms out, ‘And fearing her husband.’”", "부제가 어떻게 우렁차게 외치는지 좀 들어 봐, '그리고 남편을 두려워하며'라고 말이야.\""]], [["“Are the choristers from Tchudovo?”", "\"성가대는 추도보에서 온 사람들인가?\""]], [["“No, from the Synod.”", "\"아니, 종무원에서 왔대.\""]], [["“I asked the footman.", "\"하인한테 물어봤어."], ["He says he’s going to take her home to his country place at once.", "신랑이 곧장 신부를 자기 시골 영지로 데려갈 거라더군."], ["Awfully rich, they say.", "엄청난 부자라던데."], ["That’s why she’s being married to him.”", "그래서 신부가 저이한테 시집가는 거지.\""]], [["“No, they’re a well-matched pair.”", "\"아니야, 저 둘은 잘 어울리는 한 쌍이야.\""]], [["“I say, Marya Vassilievna, you were making out those fly-away crinolines were not being worn.", "\"이봐요, 마리야 바실리예브나, 당신은 저렇게 활짝 퍼지는 크리놀린은 이제 안 입는다고 우기지 않았어요."], ["Just look at her in the puce dress—an ambassador’s wife they say she is—how her skirt bounces out from side to side!”", "저기 적갈색 드레스 입은 여자 좀 봐요. 대사 부인이라던데, 치마가 양옆으로 어떻게 출렁거리는지 좀 보라고요!\""]], [["“What a pretty dear the bride is—like a lamb decked with flowers!", "\"신부가 정말 예쁘고 사랑스럽기도 해라. 꼭 꽃으로 단장한 양 같잖아!"], ["Well, say what you will, we women feel for our sister.”", "뭐, 뭐라고들 하든, 우리 여자들은 같은 여자 처지가 안타까운 법이지.\""]], [["Such were the comments in the crowd of gazing women who had succeeded in slipping in at the church doors.", "교회 문 안으로 용케 끼어 들어온, 눈이 휘둥그레진 여인네들 무리에서 오가는 이야기들이 이러했다."]], [["# Chapter 6", "# Chapter 6"]], [["When the ceremony of plighting troth was over, the beadle spread before the lectern in the middle of the church a piece of pink silken stuff, the choir sang a complicated and elaborate psalm, in which the bass and tenor sang responses to one another, and the priest turning round pointed the bridal pair to the pink silk rug.", "약혼의 예식이 끝나자, 교회 사찰이 한가운데에 놓인 성서대 앞에 분홍빛 비단 천을 펼쳐 놓았고, 성가대는 복잡하고 정교한 시편을 불렀다. 거기서는 베이스와 테너가 서로 화답을 주고받았으며, 사제는 몸을 돌려 신랑 신부에게 분홍빛 비단 깔개를 가리켰다."], ["Though both had often heard a great deal about the saying that the one who steps first on the rug will be the head of the house, neither Levin nor Kitty were capable of recollecting it, as they took the few steps towards it.", "깔개에 먼저 발을 디딘 사람이 집안의 가장이 된다는 말을 두 사람 모두 여러 차례 익히 들어 왔건만, 레빈도 키티도 그 깔개를 향해 몇 걸음을 옮기는 동안 그것을 떠올릴 겨를이 없었다."], ["They did not hear the loud remarks and disputes that followed, some maintaining he had stepped on first, and others that both had stepped on together.", "두 사람은 그 뒤로 이어진 큰 목소리의 말다툼도 듣지 못했는데, 어떤 이들은 그가 먼저 발을 디뎠다고 우겼고 또 어떤 이들은 둘이 동시에 디뎠다고 우겼다."]], [["After the customary questions, whether they desired to enter upon matrimony, and whether they were pledged to anyone else, and their answers, which sounded strange to themselves, a new ceremony began.", "혼인할 뜻이 있는지, 다른 누군가와 약혼한 바가 없는지 묻는 관례적인 질문이 오가고, 스스로 들어도 낯설게만 들리는 대답을 마치자, 새로운 예식이 시작되었다."], ["Kitty listened to the words of the prayer, trying to make out their meaning, but she could not.", "키티는 기도문의 말에 귀를 기울이며 그 뜻을 헤아려 보려 했으나 도무지 알아들을 수가 없었다."], ["The feeling of triumph and radiant happiness flooded her soul more and more as the ceremony went on, and deprived her of all power of attention.", "예식이 진행될수록 승리감과 환한 행복감이 점점 더 그녀의 마음을 가득 채웠고, 그 바람에 무언가에 집중할 힘을 모조리 앗아 가 버렸다."]], [["They prayed: “Endow them with continence and fruitfulness, and vouchsafe that their hearts may rejoice looking upon their sons and daughters.”", "사람들은 이렇게 기도했다. \"그들에게 절제와 다산을 내려 주시고, 그들의 마음이 아들딸을 바라보며 기뻐할 수 있게 해 주소서.\""], ["They alluded to God’s creation of a wife from Adam’s rib “and for this cause a man shall leave father and mother, and cleave unto his wife, and they two shall be one flesh,”", "그들은 하느님이 아담의 갈빗대에서 아내를 지으셨다는 이야기를, \"이러므로 남자가 부모를 떠나 그 아내와 합하여 둘이 한 몸을 이룰지로다\", 또 \"이 비밀이 크도다\"를 인용했다."], ["and that “this is a great mystery”; they prayed that God would make them fruitful and bless them, like Isaac and Rebecca, Joseph, Moses and Zipporah, and that they might look upon their children’s children.", "그들은 하느님이 이삭과 레베카, 요셉, 모세와 시포라처럼 그들을 다산하게 하시고 축복해 주시기를, 그들이 자손의 자손을 바라볼 수 있기를 기도했다."], ["“That’s all splendid,”", "'이게 다 멋진 일이야.'"], ["thought Kitty, catching the words, “all that’s just as it should be,”", "키티는 그 말들을 붙들며 생각했다. '전부 마땅히 그래야 하는 대로야.'"], ["and a smile of happiness, unconsciously reflected in everyone who looked at her, beamed on her radiant face.", "그러자 그녀를 바라보는 모든 사람에게 저도 모르게 비치는 행복의 미소가 그녀의 환한 얼굴 위로 빛났다."]], [["“Put it on quite,”", "\"끝까지 씌우세요.\""], ["voices were heard urging when the priest had put on the wedding crowns and Shtcherbatsky, his hand shaking in its three-button glove, held the crown high above her head.", "사제가 결혼 관을 씌우고, 셰르바츠키가 세 단추짜리 장갑 낀 손을 떨면서 관을 그녀의 머리 위로 높이 들고 있을 때, 재촉하는 목소리들이 들려왔다."]], [["“Put it on!”", "\"씌워요!\""], ["she whispered, smiling.", "그녀가 미소를 지으며 속삭였다."]], [["Levin looked round at her, and was struck by the joyful radiance on her face, and unconsciously her feeling infected him.", "레빈은 그녀를 돌아보았고, 그녀의 얼굴에 어린 기쁨의 광채에 마음을 빼앗겼다. 그녀의 감정이 저도 모르게 그에게도 옮아 왔다."], ["He too, like her felt glad and happy.", "그도 그녀처럼 즐겁고 행복했다."]], [["They enjoyed hearing the epistle read, and the roll of the head deacon’s voice at the last verse, awaited with such impatience by the outside public.", "두 사람은 서한이 낭독되는 것을 듣는 것이 즐거웠고, 바깥의 구경꾼들이 그토록 애타게 기다리던 마지막 구절에서 부사제의 목소리가 우렁차게 울려 퍼지는 것도 즐거웠다."], ["They enjoyed drinking out of the shallow cup of warm red wine and water, and they were still more pleased when the priest, flinging back his stole and taking both their hands in his, led them round the lectern to the accompaniment of bass voices chanting “Glory to God.”", "따뜻하게 데운 붉은 포도주와 물을 담은 얕은 잔으로 마시는 것이 즐거웠고, 사제가 영대를 뒤로 젖히고 두 사람의 손을 자기 손에 맞잡은 채 \"하느님께 영광\"을 노래하는 베이스 목소리에 맞추어 성서대를 빙 돌게 했을 때는 더욱 기뻤다."]], [["Shtcherbatsky and Tchirikov, supporting the crowns and stumbling over the bride’s train, smiling too and seeming delighted at something, were at one moment left behind, at the next treading on the bridal pair as the priest came to a halt.", "셰르바츠키와 치리코프는 관을 받쳐 들고 신부의 끌리는 옷자락에 걸려 비틀거리면서도 역시 미소를 지으며 무언가에 기뻐하는 듯했는데, 어느 순간엔 뒤처졌다가, 또 어느 순간엔 사제가 걸음을 멈추는 바람에 신랑 신부의 발을 밟기도 했다."], ["The spark of joy kindled in Kitty seemed to have infected everyone in the church.", "키티에게 일었던 기쁨의 불꽃이 교회 안의 모든 이에게 옮아 붙은 듯했다."], ["It seemed to Levin that the priest and the deacon too wanted to smile just as he did.", "레빈에게는 사제와 부사제조차 자기처럼 미소 짓고 싶어 하는 것처럼 보였다."]], [["Taking the crowns off their heads the priest read the last prayer and congratulated the young people.", "사제는 두 사람의 머리에서 관을 벗기고 마지막 기도문을 읽은 뒤 젊은 부부를 축하했다."], ["Levin looked at Kitty, and he had never before seen her look as she did.", "레빈은 키티를 바라보았는데, 지금껏 그녀의 그런 모습을 본 적이 없었다."], ["She was charming with the new radiance of happiness in her face.", "얼굴에 어린 새로운 행복의 광채로 그녀는 매혹적이었다."], ["Levin longed to say something to her, but he did not know whether it was all over.", "레빈은 그녀에게 무언가 말하고 싶었지만, 모든 것이 다 끝난 것인지 알 수 없었다."], ["The priest got him out of his difficulty.", "사제가 그를 그 난처함에서 구해 주었다."], ["He smiled his kindly smile and said gently, “Kiss your wife, and you kiss your husband,”", "그는 다정한 미소를 지으며 부드럽게 말했다. \"아내에게 입을 맞추세요, 그리고 그대는 남편에게 입을 맞추세요.\""], ["and took the candles out of their hands.", "그러고는 두 사람의 손에서 초를 거두어 갔다."]], [["Levin kissed her smiling lips with timid care, gave her his arm, and with a new strange sense of closeness, walked out of the church.", "레빈은 머뭇거리는 조심스러움으로 그녀의 미소 짓는 입술에 입을 맞추고, 그녀에게 팔을 내주고는, 낯설고도 새로운 친밀감을 느끼며 교회 밖으로 걸어 나왔다."], ["He did not believe, he could not believe, that it was true.", "그는 그것이 사실이라는 것을 믿지 못했고, 믿을 수가 없었다."], ["It was only when their wondering and timid eyes met that he believed in it, because he felt that they were one.", "두 사람의 놀라움에 찬 머뭇거리는 눈길이 마주쳤을 때에야 비로소 그는 그것을 믿었는데, 두 사람이 하나가 되었음을 느꼈기 때문이었다."]], [["After supper, the same night, the young people left for the country.", "그날 밤, 만찬이 끝난 뒤 젊은 부부는 시골로 떠났다."]], [["# Chapter 7", "# Chapter 7"]], [["Vronsky and Anna had been traveling for three months together in Europe.", "브론스키와 안나는 석 달째 함께 유럽을 여행하고 있었다."], ["They had visited Venice, Rome, and Naples, and had just arrived at a small Italian town where they meant to stay some time.", "그들은 베네치아와 로마, 나폴리를 둘러보았고, 얼마간 머물 작정인 이탈리아의 작은 도시에 막 도착한 참이었다."], ["A handsome head waiter, with thick pomaded hair parted from the neck upwards, an evening coat, a broad white cambric shirt front, and a bunch of trinkets hanging above his rounded stomach, stood with his hands in the full curve of his pockets, looking contemptuously from under his eyelids while he gave some frigid reply to a gentleman who had stopped him.", "잘생긴 수석 웨이터는 목덜미부터 위로 가르마를 탄 포마드 바른 숱 많은 머리에 연미복을 입고, 넓은 흰색 캠브릭 셔츠 가슴팍을 드러낸 채, 불룩한 배 위로 자질구레한 장식들을 한 다발 늘어뜨리고서, 두 손을 깊숙한 주머니 곡선 속에 찔러 넣고 서서, 눈꺼풀 아래로 깔보는 시선을 던지며 자기를 붙잡은 신사에게 냉랭하게 대꾸하고 있었다."], ["Catching the sound of footsteps coming from the other side of the entry towards the staircase, the head waiter turned round, and seeing the Russian count, who had taken their best rooms, he took his hands out of his pockets deferentially, and with a bow informed him that a courier had been, and that the business about the palazzo had been arranged.", "입구 반대편에서 계단 쪽으로 다가오는 발소리를 알아채고 수석 웨이터가 몸을 돌렸는데, 가장 좋은 방을 잡은 러시아 백작을 보자 공손히 주머니에서 손을 빼고는, 허리를 굽히며 사람을 보냈던 일이 있었고 그 palazzo에 관한 일이 잘 처리되었음을 알렸다."], ["The steward was prepared to sign the agreement.", "관리인이 계약서에 서명할 준비가 되어 있다는 것이었다."]], [["“Ah! I’m glad to hear it,” said Vronsky. “Is madame at home or not?”", "* palazzo: 이탈리아어로 '대저택, 궁전'."]], [["“Madame has been out for a walk but has returned now,”", "\"아! 그것참 반가운 소식이군.\""], ["answered the waiter.", "브론스키가 말했다. \"마담은 집에 계신가, 안 계신가?\""]], [["Vronsky took off his soft, wide-brimmed hat and passed his handkerchief over his heated brow and hair, which had grown half over his ears, and was brushed back covering the bald patch on his head.", "\"마담께서는 산책을 나가셨다가 지금 돌아오셨습니다.\" 웨이터가 대답했다."], ["And glancing casually at the gentleman, who still stood there gazing intently at him, he would have gone on.", "브론스키는 챙이 넓은 부드러운 모자를 벗고, 손수건으로 달아오른 이마와, 귀 절반까지 자라 머리의 벗겨진 부분을 가리며 뒤로 빗어 넘긴 머리카락을 닦았다. 그러고는 여전히 그 자리에 서서 자기를 뚫어지게 바라보는 신사를 무심히 흘끗 보고 그대로 지나치려 했다."]], [["“This gentleman is a Russian, and was inquiring after you,”", "\"이 신사분이 러시아 분인데, 백작님을 찾으셨습니다.\""], ["said the head waiter.", "수석 웨이터가 말했다."]], [["With mingled feelings of annoyance at never being able to get away from acquaintances anywhere, and longing to find some sort of diversion from the monotony of his life, Vronsky looked once more at the gentleman, who had retreated and stood still again, and at the same moment a light came into the eyes of both.", "어딜 가든 아는 사람들에게서 벗어날 수 없다는 데 대한 짜증과, 단조로운 생활에서 무언가 기분 전환거리를 찾고 싶다는 갈망이 뒤섞인 채, 브론스키는 한 발 물러나 다시 가만히 서 있는 신사를 한 번 더 바라보았는데, 바로 그 순간 두 사람의 눈에 동시에 빛이 떠올랐다."]], [["“Golenishtchev!”", "\"골레니셰프!\""]], [["“Vronsky!”", "\"브론스키!\""]], [["It really was Golenishtchev, a comrade of Vronsky’s in the Corps of Pages.", "정말로 골레니셰프였다. 사관학교 시절 브론스키의 동기였다."], ["In the corps Golenishtchev had belonged to the liberal party; he left the corps without entering the army, and had never taken office under the government.", "학교에서 골레니셰프는 자유주의 파에 속했고, 졸업 후 군대에 들어가지 않았으며, 정부 기관에서 관직을 맡은 적도 없었다."], ["Vronsky and he had gone completely different ways on leaving the corps, and had only met once since.", "브론스키와 그는 학교를 나온 뒤 완전히 다른 길을 걸었고, 그동안 단 한 번 마주쳤을 뿐이었다."]], [["At that meeting Vronsky perceived that Golenishtchev had taken up a sort of lofty, intellectually liberal line, and was consequently disposed to look down upon Vronsky’s interests and calling in life.", "그 만남에서 브론스키는 골레니셰프가 일종의 고고하고 지적으로 자유주의적인 노선을 택했으며, 그래서 브론스키의 관심사며 인생의 직분을 깔보는 경향이 있다는 것을 알아챘다."], ["Hence Vronsky had met him with the chilling and haughty manner he so well knew how to assume, the meaning of which was: “You may like or dislike my way of life, that’s a matter of the most perfect indifference to me; you will have to treat me with respect if you want to know me.”", "그래서 브론스키는 그를, 자기가 능숙하게 지을 줄 아는 차갑고 거만한 태도로 대했는데, 그 태도가 의미하는 바는 이런 것이었다. \"자네가 내 삶의 방식을 좋아하든 싫어하든, 그건 나에게 더없이 아무래도 좋은 일이야."], ["Golenishtchev had been contemptuously indifferent to the tone taken by Vronsky.", "나를 알고 싶다면 나를 존중해야 할 걸세.\" 골레니셰프는 브론스키가 취한 그 태도에 경멸 어린 무관심으로 응했다."], ["This second meeting might have been expected, one would have supposed, to estrange them still more.", "이 두 번째 만남은, 당연히 두 사람을 더욱 멀어지게 했으리라 짐작할 만했다."], ["But now they beamed and exclaimed with delight on recognizing one another.", "하지만 지금 그들은 서로를 알아보고 기쁨에 차서 환하게 빛나며 소리쳤다."], ["Vronsky would never have expected to be so pleased to see Golenishtchev, but probably he was not himself aware how bored he was.", "브론스키는 골레니셰프를 만나 이토록 기쁘리라고는 결코 예상하지 못했지만, 아마 자기가 얼마나 따분해하고 있었는지 스스로도 몰랐던 것이리라."], ["He forgot the disagreeable impression of their last meeting, and with a face of frank delight held out his hand to his old comrade.", "그는 지난 만남의 불쾌한 인상을 잊고, 꾸밈없이 기뻐하는 얼굴로 옛 동기에게 손을 내밀었다."], ["The same expression of delight replaced the look of uneasiness on Golenishtchev’s face.", "골레니셰프의 얼굴에서도 불안한 빛이 사라지고 같은 기쁨의 표정이 자리 잡았다."]], [["“How glad I am to meet you!”", "\"이렇게 만나다니 정말 반갑네!\""], ["said Vronsky, showing his strong white teeth in a friendly smile.", "브론스키가 다정한 미소로 희고 튼튼한 이를 드러내며 말했다."]], [["“I heard the name Vronsky, but I didn’t know which one.", "\"브론스키라는 이름은 들었는데, 어느 브론스키인지를 몰랐지 뭔가."], ["I’m very, very glad!”", "정말, 정말 반가워!\""]], [["“Let’s go in.", "\"안으로 들어가세."], ["Come, tell me what you’re doing.”", "자, 자네가 무슨 일을 하고 지내는지 얘기해 주게.\""]], [["“I’ve been living here for two years.", "\"여기서 산 지 이 년 됐어."], ["I’m working.”", "일을 하고 있지.\""]], [["“Ah!”", "\"아!\""], ["said Vronsky, with sympathy; “let’s go in.”", "브론스키가 공감을 담아 말했다. \"들어가세.\""], ["And with the habit common with Russians, instead of saying in Russian what he wanted to keep from the servants, he began to speak in French.", "그러고는 러시아인들 사이에 흔한 버릇대로, 하인들에게 들키고 싶지 않은 말을 러시아어로 하는 대신 프랑스어로 말하기 시작했다."]], [["“Do you know Madame Karenina?", "\"마담 카레니나를 아나?"], ["We are traveling together.", "우리는 함께 여행하는 중이야."], ["I am going to see her now,”", "지금 그녀를 만나러 가는 길이지.\""], ["he said in French, carefully scrutinizing Golenishtchev’s face.", "그는 프랑스어로 말하며 골레니셰프의 얼굴을 주의 깊게 살폈다."]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["I did not know”", "몰랐는걸.\""], ["(though he did know), Golenishtchev answered carelessly.", "(사실은 알고 있었지만.) 골레니셰프가 아무렇지 않게 대답했다."], ["“Have you been here long?”", "\"여기 온 지 오래됐나?\""], ["he added.", "그가 덧붙였다."]], [["“Four days,”", "\"나흘 됐네.\""], ["Vronsky answered, once more scrutinizing his friend’s face intently.", "브론스키가 대답하며 다시 한번 친구의 얼굴을 유심히 살폈다."]], [["“Yes, he’s a decent fellow, and will look at the thing properly,”", "'그래, 점잖은 친구야. 이 일을 제대로 볼 줄 알 거야.'"], ["Vronsky said to himself, catching the significance of Golenishtchev’s face and the change of subject.", "브론스키는 골레니셰프의 표정에 담긴 뜻과 화제를 바꾸는 태도를 알아채고 속으로 생각했다."], ["“I can introduce him to Anna, he looks at it properly.”", "'안나에게 소개해도 되겠어. 제대로 받아들이는군.'"]], [["During those three months that Vronsky had spent abroad with Anna, he had always on meeting new people asked himself how the new person would look at his relations with Anna, and for the most part, in men, he had met with the “proper”", "브론스키가 안나와 함께 외국에서 보낸 그 석 달 동안, 그는 새로운 사람을 만날 때마다 그 사람이 안나와 자기의 관계를 어떻게 볼지 늘 스스로에게 물었고, 남자들의 경우 대개 '제대로'"], ["way of looking at it.", "보는 방식을 만났다."], ["But if he had been asked, and those who looked at it “properly”", "하지만 만약 그에게, 그리고 그것을 '제대로'"], ["had been asked, exactly how they did look at it, both he and they would have been greatly puzzled to answer.", "본다는 사람들에게 정확히 어떻게 보느냐고 물었다면, 그도 그들도 대답하기에 몹시 난처했을 것이다."]], [["In reality, those who in Vronsky’s opinion had the “proper”", "사실 브론스키가 보기에 '제대로'"], ["view had no sort of view at all, but behaved in general as well-bred persons do behave in regard to all the complex and insoluble problems with which life is encompassed on all sides; they behaved with propriety, avoiding allusions and unpleasant questions.", "된 시각을 가진 자들은 실은 아무런 시각도 가지고 있지 않았고, 다만 삶을 사방에서 에워싸는 온갖 복잡하고 풀리지 않는 문제들에 대해 교양 있는 사람들이 으레 하는 식으로 처신했을 뿐이었다. 그들은 점잖게 굴며 넌지시 비추는 말이나 불쾌한 질문을 피했다."], ["They assumed an air of fully comprehending the import and force of the situation, of accepting and even approving of it, but of considering it superfluous and uncalled for to put all this into words.", "그들은 상황의 의미와 무게를 충분히 이해하고, 그것을 받아들이며 심지어 인정하기까지 하지만, 그것을 굳이 말로 옮기는 것은 군더더기이고 쓸데없는 짓이라 여기는 듯한 태도를 취했다."]], [["Vronsky at once divined that Golenishtchev was of this class, and therefore was doubly pleased to see him.", "브론스키는 골레니셰프가 이 부류에 속한다는 것을 곧장 알아차렸고, 그래서 그를 보는 것이 갑절로 기뻤다."], ["And in fact, Golenishtchev’s manner to Madame Karenina, when he was taken to call on her, was all that Vronsky could have desired.", "그리고 사실 골레니셰프를 데려가 마담 카레니나를 방문하게 했을 때, 그가 그녀를 대하는 태도는 브론스키가 바랄 수 있는 그대로였다."], ["Obviously without the slightest effort he steered clear of all subjects which might lead to embarrassment.", "그는 분명 조금의 애도 쓰지 않고 곤란을 불러올 만한 모든 화제를 매끄럽게 피해 갔다."]], [["He had never met Anna before, and was struck by her beauty, and still more by the frankness with which she accepted her position.", "그는 안나를 만난 적이 한 번도 없었기에, 그녀의 아름다움에 놀랐고, 자기 처지를 받아들이는 그 솔직함에는 더욱 놀랐다."], ["She blushed when Vronsky brought in Golenishtchev, and he was extremely charmed by this childish blush overspreading her candid and handsome face.", "브론스키가 골레니셰프를 데리고 들어오자 그녀는 얼굴을 붉혔는데, 그 꾸밈없고 아름다운 얼굴에 번지는 그 어린아이 같은 홍조에 그는 더없이 매료되었다."], ["But what he liked particularly was the way in which at once, as though on purpose that there might be no misunderstanding with an outsider, she called Vronsky simply Alexey, and said they were moving into a house they had just taken, what was here called a palazzo.", "하지만 그가 특히 마음에 들어 한 것은, 마치 외부 사람과의 사이에 오해가 없도록 일부러 그러는 것처럼, 그녀가 곧바로 브론스키를 그저 알렉세이라고 부르며, 자기들이 여기서 palazzo라 불리는, 막 빌린 집으로 옮겨 가는 중이라고 말하던 그 방식이었다."], ["Golenishtchev liked this direct and simple attitude to her own position.", "골레니셰프는 자기 처지에 대한 그녀의 이 솔직하고 단순한 태도가 마음에 들었다."], ["Looking at Anna’s manner of simple-hearted, spirited gaiety, and knowing Alexey Alexandrovitch and Vronsky, Golenishtchev fancied that he understood her perfectly.", "안나의 천진하고 활기찬 명랑함을 바라보며, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치와 브론스키를 알기에, 골레니셰프는 그녀를 완전히 이해했다고 자부했다."], ["He fancied that he understood what she was utterly unable to understand: how it was that, having made her husband wretched, having abandoned him and her son and lost her good name, she yet felt full of spirits, gaiety, and happiness.", "그는 그녀 자신은 도무지 이해할 수 없는 것을 자기는 이해한다고 자부했다. 즉, 남편을 비참하게 만들고, 그와 아들을 버리고 명예를 잃었으면서도, 어떻게 그녀가 여전히 생기와 명랑함과 행복으로 가득 차 있을 수 있는지를 말이다."]], [["“It’s in the guide-book,”", "\"그건 안내서에도 나와 있지.\""], ["said Golenishtchev, referring to the palazzo Vronsky had taken.", "골레니셰프가 브론스키가 빌린 palazzo를 두고 말했다."], ["“There’s a first-rate Tintoretto there.", "\"거기에 일류 틴토레토 작품이 있어."], ["One of his latest period.”", "그의 후기 작품 중 하나지.\""]], [["“I tell you what: it’s a lovely day, let’s go and have another look at it,”", "\"이러면 어떨까. 날씨도 화창하니, 가서 한 번 더 둘러보세.\""], ["said Vronsky, addressing Anna.", "브론스키가 안나를 향해 말했다."]], [["“I shall be very glad to; I’ll go and put on my hat.", "\"아주 좋아요. 가서 모자를 쓰고 올게요."], ["Would you say it’s hot?”", "날씨가 덥다고 생각하세요?\""], ["she said, stopping short in the doorway and looking inquiringly at Vronsky.", "그녀가 문간에서 우뚝 멈춰 서서 묻는 듯한 눈빛으로 브론스키를 바라보며 말했다."], ["And again a vivid flush overspread her face.", "그러자 다시 한번 선명한 홍조가 그녀의 얼굴에 번졌다."]], [["Vronsky saw from her eyes that she did not know on what terms he cared to be with Golenishtchev, and so was afraid of not behaving as he would wish.", "브론스키는 그녀의 눈빛에서, 그녀가 자기와 골레니셰프 사이가 어떤 관계인지 몰라서 자기가 바라는 대로 처신하지 못할까 봐 두려워한다는 것을 읽었다."]], [["He looked a long, tender look at her.", "그는 그녀를 오래도록 다정하게 바라보았다."]], [["“No, not very,”", "\"아니, 그리 덥지 않아.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["And it seemed to her that she understood everything, most of all, that he was pleased with her; and smiling to him, she walked with her rapid step out at the door.", "그러자 그녀에게는 모든 것이, 무엇보다도 그가 자기에게 만족하고 있다는 것이 이해되는 듯했다. 그녀는 그에게 미소 지으며 빠른 걸음으로 문밖으로 걸어 나갔다."]], [["The friends glanced at one another, and a look of hesitation came into both faces, as though Golenishtchev, unmistakably admiring her, would have liked to say something about her, and could not find the right thing to say, while Vronsky desired and dreaded his doing so.", "두 친구는 서로를 흘끗 보았는데, 두 사람의 얼굴에 망설이는 빛이 떠올랐다. 마치 골레니셰프는, 분명 그녀에게 감탄하고 있어서 그녀에 대해 뭔가 말하고 싶지만 알맞은 말을 찾지 못하는 듯했고, 브론스키는 그가 그러기를 바라면서도 동시에 두려워하는 듯했다."]], [["“Well then,”", "\"그래, 그런데,\""], ["Vronsky began to start a conversation of some sort; “so you’re settled here?", "브론스키가 무언가 대화를 시작하려 운을 뗐다. \"그러니까 자네 여기에 자리를 잡은 건가?"], ["You’re still at the same work, then?”", "여전히 같은 일을 하고 있고?\""], ["he went on, recalling that he had been told Golenishtchev was writing something.", "그는 골레니셰프가 무언가를 쓰고 있다는 말을 들었던 것을 떠올리며 말을 이었다."]], [["“Yes, I’m writing the second part of the Two Elements,”", "\"그래, 「두 가지 요소」의 제2부를 쓰고 있어.\""], ["said Golenishtchev, coloring with pleasure at the question—“that is, to be exact, I am not writing it yet; I am preparing, collecting materials.", "골레니셰프가 그 질문에 기뻐서 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다. \"그러니까, 정확히 말하면 아직 쓰는 건 아니고, 준비하면서 자료를 모으는 중이지."], ["It will be of far wider scope, and will touch on almost all questions.", "훨씬 더 폭넓은 내용이 될 거고, 거의 모든 문제를 다루게 될 거야."], ["We in Russia refuse to see that we are the heirs of Byzantium,”", "우리 러시아인들은 우리가 비잔티움의 후예라는 사실을 인정하려 들지 않거든.\""], ["and he launched into a long and heated explanation of his views.", "그러더니 그는 자기 견해를 길고 열띠게 설명하기 시작했다."]], [["Vronsky at the first moment felt embarrassed at not even knowing of the first part of the Two Elements, of which the author spoke as something well known.", "브론스키는 처음 한순간, 저자가 잘 알려진 것인 양 말하는 「두 가지 요소」의 제1부조차 모른다는 데 당황했다."], ["But as Golenishtchev began to lay down his opinions and Vronsky was able to follow them even without knowing the Two Elements, he listened to him with some interest, for Golenishtchev spoke well.", "하지만 골레니셰프가 자기 견해를 늘어놓기 시작하자 브론스키는 「두 가지 요소」를 모르고도 그 이야기를 따라갈 수 있었기에, 어느 정도 흥미를 느끼며 귀를 기울였다. 골레니셰프가 말을 잘했기 때문이다."], ["But Vronsky was startled and annoyed by the nervous irascibility with which Golenishtchev talked of the subject that engrossed him.", "그러나 골레니셰프가 자기를 사로잡은 그 주제를 이야기할 때 보이는 신경질적인 짜증에 브론스키는 놀랐고 거슬렸다."], ["As he went on talking, his eyes glittered more and more angrily; he was more and more hurried in his replies to imaginary opponents, and his face grew more and more excited and worried.", "말을 이어 갈수록 그의 눈은 점점 더 성난 듯 번득였고, 가상의 적수들에게 대꾸하는 말투가 점점 더 다급해졌으며, 얼굴은 점점 더 흥분하고 근심스러워졌다."], ["Remembering Golenishtchev, a thin, lively, good-natured and well-bred boy, always at the head of the class, Vronsky could not make out the reason of his irritability, and he did not like it.", "마르고 활기차며 선량하고 교양 있던, 늘 학급에서 일등을 하던 소년 골레니셰프를 떠올리며, 브론스키는 그의 짜증의 까닭을 도무지 헤아릴 수 없었고, 그것이 마음에 들지 않았다."], ["What he particularly disliked was that Golenishtchev, a man belonging to a good set, should put himself on a level with some scribbling fellows, with whom he was irritated and angry.", "특히 그가 거슬려 한 것은, 좋은 가문 출신인 골레니셰프가 자기를 짜증나게 하고 화나게 하는 어떤 글쟁이들과 같은 수준으로 자신을 끌어내린다는 점이었다. 그럴 만한 가치가 있는 일인가?"], ["Was it worth it? Vronsky disliked it, yet he felt that Golenishtchev was unhappy, and was sorry for him.", "브론스키는 그것이 못마땅했지만, 그러면서도 골레니셰프가 불행하다는 것을 느꼈고, 그가 가여웠다."], ["Unhappiness, almost mental derangement, was visible on his mobile, rather handsome face, while without even noticing Anna’s coming in, he went on hurriedly and hotly expressing his views.", "안나가 들어오는 것조차 알아채지 못한 채 다급하고 열띠게 자기 견해를 늘어놓는 그의 표정 풍부한, 제법 잘생긴 얼굴에는 불행이, 거의 정신적 착란에 가까운 것이 드러나 있었다."]], [["When Anna came in in her hat and cape, and her lovely hand rapidly swinging her parasol, and stood beside him, it was with a feeling of relief that Vronsky broke away from the plaintive eyes of Golenishtchev which fastened persistently upon him, and with a fresh rush of love looked at his charming companion, full of life and happiness.", "안나가 모자를 쓰고 망토를 두른 채 사랑스러운 손으로 양산을 빠르게 흔들며 들어와 그의 곁에 섰을 때, 브론스키는 끈질기게 자기에게 들러붙는 골레니셰프의 애처로운 눈길에서 벗어나는 안도감을 느끼며, 새롭게 차오르는 사랑으로 생기와 행복이 가득한 매혹적인 동반자를 바라보았다."], ["Golenishtchev recovered himself with an effort, and at first was dejected and gloomy, but Anna, disposed to feel friendly with everyone as she was at that time, soon revived his spirits by her direct and lively manner.", "골레니셰프는 애써 정신을 추슬렀고, 처음에는 풀이 죽어 침울했지만, 그 무렵 누구에게나 다정하게 대하려는 마음이었던 안나가 솔직하고 활기찬 태도로 곧 그의 기분을 되살려 주었다."], ["After trying various subjects of conversation, she got him upon painting, of which he talked very well, and she listened to him attentively.", "이런저런 화제를 시도해 본 끝에 그녀는 그를 회화 이야기로 끌어들였고, 그는 그 이야기를 아주 잘했으며, 그녀는 주의 깊게 귀를 기울였다."], ["They walked to the house they had taken, and looked over it.", "그들은 빌린 집까지 걸어가 그곳을 둘러보았다."]], [["“I am very glad of one thing,”", "\"한 가지 정말 기쁜 게 있어요.\""], ["said Anna to Golenishtchev when they were on their way back, “Alexey will have a capital atelier.", "돌아오는 길에 안나가 골레니셰프에게 말했다. \"알렉세이가 훌륭한 atelier를 갖게 됐거든요."], ["You must certainly take that room,”", "당신은 꼭 저 방을 쓰셔야 해요.\""], ["she said to Vronsky in Russian, using the affectionately familiar form as though she saw that Golenishtchev would become intimate with them in their isolation, and that there was no need of reserve before him.", "그녀는 브론스키에게 러시아어로, 골레니셰프가 자기들의 고립된 생활 속에서 가까운 사이가 되리라는 것을, 그래서 그 앞에서 거리낄 필요가 없다는 것을 알아차린 듯, 다정하고 허물없는 말투를 쓰며 말했다. * atelier: 프랑스어로 '화실, 작업실'."]], [["“Do you paint?”", "\"그림을 그리시나요?\""], ["said Golenishtchev, turning round quickly to Vronsky.", "골레니셰프가 브론스키 쪽으로 잽싸게 돌아서며 물었다."]], [["“Yes, I used to study long ago, and now I have begun to do a little,”", "\"네, 오래전에 배운 적이 있고, 이제 조금씩 다시 손을 대기 시작했어요.\""], ["said Vronsky, reddening.", "브론스키가 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다."]], [["“He has great talent,”", "\"그이는 대단한 재능이 있어요.\""], ["said Anna with a delighted smile.", "안나가 기쁨에 찬 미소를 지으며 말했다."], ["“I’m no judge, of course.", "\"물론 제가 판단할 처지는 아니지만요."], ["But good judges have said the same.”", "안목 있는 분들이 그렇게 말씀하셨답니다.\""]], [["# Chapter 8", "# Chapter 8"]], [["Anna, in that first period of her emancipation and rapid return to health, felt herself unpardonably happy and full of the joy of life.", "해방을 맞은 첫 시기, 빠르게 건강을 회복해 가던 안나는 자신이 용서받을 수 없을 만큼 행복하며 삶의 기쁨으로 가득 차 있다고 느꼈다."], ["The thought of her husband’s unhappiness did not poison her happiness.", "남편의 불행에 대한 생각도 그녀의 행복을 망치지는 못했다."], ["On one side that memory was too awful to be thought of.", "한편으로 그 기억은 떠올리기에 너무나 끔찍했다."], ["On the other side her husband’s unhappiness had given her too much happiness to be regretted.", "다른 한편으로 남편의 불행은, 후회하기에는 그녀에게 너무 많은 행복을 가져다주었다."], ["The memory of all that had happened after her illness: her reconciliation with her husband, its breakdown, the news of Vronsky’s wound, his visit, the preparations for divorce, the departure from her husband’s house, the parting from her son—all that seemed to her like a delirious dream, from which she had waked up alone with Vronsky abroad.", "자신이 앓은 뒤 일어난 모든 일들의 기억—남편과의 화해, 그 화해의 파탄, 브론스키가 부상당했다는 소식, 그의 방문, 이혼 준비, 남편의 집에서 나온 일, 아들과의 이별—그 모든 것이 그녀에게는 마치 헛소리 같은 꿈처럼 여겨졌고, 그녀는 외국에서 브론스키와 단둘이 그 꿈에서 깨어난 것 같았다."], ["The thought of the harm caused to her husband aroused in her a feeling like repulsion, and akin to what a drowning man might feel who has shaken off another man clinging to him. That man did drown.", "남편에게 끼친 해악에 대한 생각은 그녀에게 일종의 혐오감 같은 것을, 물에 빠진 사람이 자기에게 매달린 다른 사람을 떨쳐 냈을 때 느낄 법한 감정과 비슷한 것을 불러일으켰다."], ["It was an evil action, of course, but it was the sole means of escape, and better not to brood over these fearful facts.", "그 사람은 정말로 물에 빠져 죽었다. 물론 그것은 악한 행동이었지만, 그것만이 유일한 살길이었으니, 그 무서운 사실들을 곱씹지 않는 편이 나았다."]], [["One consolatory reflection upon her conduct had occurred to her at the first moment of the final rupture, and when now she recalled all the past, she remembered that one reflection.", "마지막 결별의 첫 순간에 그녀는 자신의 행동에 대해 한 가지 위안이 되는 생각을 떠올렸고, 이제 지나간 모든 일을 되돌아볼 때면 그 한 가지 생각이 떠올랐다."], ["“I have inevitably made that man wretched,”", "\"나는 어쩔 수 없이 그 사람을 비참하게 만들었어.\""], ["she thought; “but I don’t want to profit by his misery.", "그녀는 생각했다. \"하지만 나는 그의 불행으로 이득을 보고 싶지는 않아."], ["I too am suffering, and shall suffer; I am losing what I prized above everything—I am losing my good name and my son.", "나도 괴로워하고 있고, 또 앞으로도 괴로워할 거야. 나는 무엇보다 소중히 여기던 것을 잃고 있어—나의 명예와 아들을 잃고 있는 거야."], ["I have done wrong, and so I don’t want happiness, I don’t want a divorce, and shall suffer from my shame and the separation from my child.”", "내가 잘못을 저질렀으니, 나는 행복을 원치 않고, 이혼도 원치 않으며, 나의 치욕과 아이와의 이별로 괴로워할 거야.\""], ["But, however sincerely Anna had meant to suffer, she was not suffering.", "그러나 안나가 아무리 진심으로 괴로워하려 했어도, 그녀는 괴롭지 않았다."], ["Shame there was not.", "치욕도 없었다."], ["With the tact of which both had such a large share, they had succeeded in avoiding Russian ladies abroad, and so had never placed themselves in a false position, and everywhere they had met people who pretended that they perfectly understood their position, far better indeed than they did themselves.", "둘 다 풍부하게 지닌 그 재치 덕분에 그들은 외국에서 러시아 부인들을 피하는 데 성공했고, 그래서 한 번도 거짓된 처지에 자신들을 놓이게 한 적이 없었으며, 가는 곳마다 자기들의 처지를 완벽히 이해하는 척하는 사람들을, 사실 당사자인 자기들보다 훨씬 더 잘 이해하는 척하는 사람들을 만났다."], ["Separation from the son she loved—even that did not cause her anguish in these early days.", "사랑하는 아들과의 이별—그것조차도 이 초기 시절에는 그녀에게 고통을 주지 않았다."], ["The baby girl—his child—was so sweet, and had so won Anna’s heart, since she was all that was left her, that Anna rarely thought of her son.", "그의 아이인 갓난 딸은 너무나 사랑스러웠고, 자기에게 남은 전부라는 사실 때문에 안나의 마음을 온통 사로잡았기에, 안나는 좀처럼 아들 생각을 하지 않았다."]], [["The desire for life, waxing stronger with recovered health, was so intense, and the conditions of life were so new and pleasant, that Anna felt unpardonably happy.", "회복된 건강과 더불어 점점 강해지는 삶에 대한 욕망은 너무나 강렬했고, 삶의 조건들은 너무나 새롭고 즐거웠기에, 안나는 용서받을 수 없을 만큼 행복하다고 느꼈다."], ["The more she got to know Vronsky, the more she loved him.", "브론스키를 알아 가면 갈수록 그녀는 그를 더욱 사랑했다."], ["She loved him for himself, and for his love for her.", "그녀는 그 자체로서의 그를 사랑했고, 그가 자기를 사랑한다는 사실 때문에 그를 사랑했다."], ["Her complete ownership of him was a continual joy to her.", "그를 온전히 소유하고 있다는 것은 그녀에게 끊임없는 기쁨이었다."], ["His presence was always sweet to her.", "그가 곁에 있다는 것은 언제나 그녀에게 달콤했다."], ["All the traits of his character, which she learned to know better and better, were unutterably dear to her.", "점점 더 잘 알게 된 그의 성격의 온갖 면면이 그녀에게는 말로 다 할 수 없이 사랑스러웠다."], ["His appearance, changed by his civilian dress, was as fascinating to her as though she were some young girl in love.", "평복으로 바뀐 그의 모습은, 마치 그녀가 사랑에 빠진 어린 처녀이기라도 한 듯 그녀를 매혹했다."], ["In everything he said, thought, and did, she saw something particularly noble and elevated.", "그가 말하고 생각하고 행하는 모든 것에서 그녀는 무언가 유난히 고귀하고 드높은 것을 보았다."], ["Her adoration of him alarmed her indeed; she sought and could not find in him anything not fine.", "그를 향한 자신의 흠모는 사실 그녀를 불안하게 했다."], ["She dared not show him her sense of her own insignificance beside him.", "그녀는 그에게서 훌륭하지 않은 점을 찾아보려 했지만 찾을 수 없었다."], ["It seemed to her that, knowing this, he might sooner cease to love her; and she dreaded nothing now so much as losing his love, though she had no grounds for fearing it.", "그녀는 감히 그 앞에서 자기가 보잘것없는 존재라는 느낌을 그에게 내보일 수 없었다. 이를 알게 되면 그가 더 빨리 자기를 사랑하지 않게 될지도 모른다고 여겼다."], ["But she could not help being grateful to him for his attitude to her, and showing that she appreciated it.", "그리고 이제 그녀는 그의 사랑을 잃는 것만큼 두려운 것이 없었다. 그것을 두려워할 근거는 전혀 없었지만 말이다."], ["He, who had in her opinion such a marked aptitude for a political career, in which he would have been certain to play a leading part—he had sacrificed his ambition for her sake, and never betrayed the slightest regret.", "그러나 그녀는 자기를 대하는 그의 태도에 감사하지 않을 수 없었고, 그것을 고맙게 여긴다는 것을 내보이지 않을 수 없었다. 그녀가 보기에 정계에서 두각을 나타낼 그토록 분명한 자질을 갖춰, 틀림없이 주도적인 역할을 했을 그가—그는 그녀를 위해 자신의 야망을 희생했으며, 단 한 번도 조금의 후회조차 내비치지 않았다."], ["He was more lovingly respectful to her than ever, and the constant care that she should not feel the awkwardness of her position never deserted him for a single instant.", "그는 그 어느 때보다 다정하고 정중하게 그녀를 대했고, 그녀가 자기 처지의 어색함을 느끼지 않게 하려는 한결같은 배려를 한순간도 거두지 않았다."], ["He, so manly a man, never opposed her, had indeed, with her, no will of his own, and was anxious, it seemed, for nothing but to anticipate her wishes.", "그토록 남자다운 사내였던 그는 결코 그녀에게 반대하는 법이 없었고, 그녀 앞에서는 자기 의지라고 할 것이 전혀 없는 듯했으며, 오직 그녀의 바람을 미리 헤아리는 것 말고는 아무것도 바라지 않는 것 같았다."], ["And she could not but appreciate this, even though the very intensity of his solicitude for her, the atmosphere of care with which he surrounded her, sometimes weighed upon her.", "그리고 그녀는 이를 고맙게 여기지 않을 수 없었다. 비록 그녀를 향한 바로 그 지극한 정성과, 그녀를 에워싼 보살핌의 분위기가 때로 그녀를 짓누르기도 했지만 말이다."]], [["Vronsky, meanwhile, in spite of the complete realization of what he had so long desired, was not perfectly happy.", "한편 브론스키는 그토록 오래 갈망해 온 것을 완전히 이루었음에도 온전히 행복하지는 않았다."], ["He soon felt that the realization of his desires gave him no more than a grain of sand out of the mountain of happiness he had expected.", "그는 자신의 욕망이 실현되었다 해도 그것이 자기가 기대했던 행복의 산더미에서 모래 한 알밖에 주지 못한다는 것을 곧 깨달았다."], ["It showed him the mistake men make in picturing to themselves happiness as the realization of their desires.", "그것은 사람들이 행복을 자기 욕망의 실현으로 그려 보는 데서 저지르는 오류를 그에게 일깨워 주었다."], ["For a time after joining his life to hers, and putting on civilian dress, he had felt all the delight of freedom in general of which he had known nothing before, and of freedom in his love,—and he was content, but not for long.", "자신의 삶을 그녀의 삶과 하나로 합치고 평복을 입은 후 한동안 그는, 전에는 전혀 몰랐던 자유 전반의 온갖 기쁨과 사랑 속에서의 자유의 기쁨을 만끽했고—그는 만족했지만, 그것은 오래가지 않았다."], ["He was soon aware that there was springing up in his heart a desire for desires—ennui.", "그는 곧 자기 마음속에서 욕망을 향한 욕망, 곧 권태가 솟아나고 있음을 알아차렸다."], ["Without conscious intention he began to clutch at every passing caprice, taking it for a desire and an object.", "그는 별다른 의도도 없이 스쳐 가는 온갖 변덕을 욕망이자 목표로 여기며 거기에 매달리기 시작했다."], ["Sixteen hours of the day must be occupied in some way, since they were living abroad in complete freedom, outside the conditions of social life which filled up time in Petersburg.", "그들은 페테르부르크에서 시간을 채워 주던 사교 생활의 조건 바깥에서, 외국에서 완전한 자유를 누리며 살고 있었기에, 하루 열여섯 시간을 어떻게든 채워야만 했다."], ["As for the amusements of bachelor existence, which had provided Vronsky with entertainment on previous tours abroad, they could not be thought of, since the sole attempt of the sort had led to a sudden attack of depression in Anna, quite out of proportion with the cause—a late supper with bachelor friends.", "이전의 외국 여행에서 브론스키에게 즐거움을 주었던 독신 시절의 오락들로 말하자면, 그것은 생각조차 할 수 없었다. 그런 종류의 단 한 번의 시도—독신 친구들과의 늦은 밤 만찬—가 그 원인에 전혀 어울리지 않게 안나에게 느닷없는 우울증을 불러일으켰기 때문이다."], ["Relations with the society of the place—foreign and Russian—were equally out of the question owing to the irregularity of their position.", "그곳 사교계—외국인이든 러시아인이든—와의 교제 역시 그들 처지의 불안정함 때문에 마찬가지로 불가능했다."], ["The inspection of objects of interest, apart from the fact that everything had been seen already, had not for Vronsky, a Russian and a sensible man, the immense significance Englishmen are able to attach to that pursuit.", "흥미로운 명소들을 둘러보는 일은, 이미 모든 것을 다 보았다는 사실은 차치하더라도, 러시아인이자 분별 있는 사내인 브론스키에게는 영국인들이 그 일에 부여할 수 있는 그런 엄청난 의미가 없었다."]], [["And just as the hungry stomach eagerly accepts every object it can get, hoping to find nourishment in it, Vronsky quite unconsciously clutched first at politics, then at new books, and then at pictures.", "그리고 굶주린 위장이 영양분을 얻으리라는 기대로 손에 닿는 모든 것을 게걸스럽게 받아들이듯, 브론스키도 자기도 모르게 처음에는 정치에, 다음에는 신간 서적에, 그다음에는 그림에 매달렸다."]], [["As he had from a child a taste for painting, and as, not knowing what to spend his money on, he had begun collecting engravings, he came to a stop at painting, began to take interest in it, and concentrated upon it the unoccupied mass of desires which demanded satisfaction.", "그는 어려서부터 회화에 취미가 있었고, 돈을 어디에 쓸지 몰라 판화를 수집하기 시작했던 터라, 회화에 멈춰 서서 그것에 흥미를 갖기 시작했으며, 충족을 요구하던 미처 다 쓰지 못한 욕망의 덩어리를 거기에 쏟아부었다."]], [["He had a ready appreciation of art, and probably, with a taste for imitating art, he supposed himself to have the real thing essential for an artist, and after hesitating for some time which style of painting to select—religious, historical, realistic, or genre painting—he set to work to paint.", "그는 예술을 곧잘 감상할 줄 알았고, 아마도 예술을 모방하는 데 취미가 있었기에 자기에게 화가에게 필요한 진짜 자질이 있다고 여겼다. 그는 어떤 화풍을 택할지—종교화, 역사화, 사실주의, 아니면 풍속화—얼마간 망설인 끝에 그림을 그리기 시작했다."], ["He appreciated all kinds, and could have felt inspired by anyone of them; but he had no conception of the possibility of knowing nothing at all of any school of painting, and of being inspired directly by what is within the soul, without caring whether what is painted will belong to any recognized school.", "그는 온갖 화풍을 감상할 줄 알았고 그 어느 것에서든 영감을 받을 수 있었지만, 어떤 화파에 대해서도 아무것도 모를 수 있다는 것, 그리고 그려진 것이 어느 공인된 화파에 속하는지 따지지 않고 영혼 안에 있는 것으로부터 직접 영감을 받을 수 있다는 것은 전혀 생각하지 못했다."], ["Since he knew nothing of this, and drew his inspiration, not directly from life, but indirectly from life embodied in art, his inspiration came very quickly and easily, and as quickly and easily came his success in painting something very similar to the sort of painting he was trying to imitate.", "이를 전혀 몰랐기에, 그리고 삶에서 직접 영감을 끌어내지 않고 예술 속에 구현된 삶에서 간접적으로 영감을 끌어냈기에, 그의 영감은 아주 빠르고 손쉽게 찾아왔고, 그가 모방하려던 그림과 매우 비슷한 무언가를 그려 내는 성공도 그만큼 빠르고 손쉽게 찾아왔다."]], [["More than any other style he liked the French—graceful and effective—and in that style he began to paint Anna’s portrait in Italian costume, and the portrait seemed to him, and to everyone who saw it, extremely successful.", "다른 어떤 화풍보다도 그는 우아하고 효과적인 프랑스풍을 좋아했고, 그 화풍으로 이탈리아 의상을 입은 안나의 초상화를 그리기 시작했는데, 그 초상화는 그 자신에게도, 그것을 본 모든 사람에게도 더없이 성공적으로 보였다."]], [["# Chapter 9", "# Chapter 9"]], [["The old neglected palazzo, with its lofty carved ceilings and frescoes on the walls, with its floors of mosaic, with its heavy yellow stuff curtains on the windows, with its vases on pedestals, and its open fireplaces, its carved doors and gloomy reception rooms, hung with pictures—this palazzo did much, by its very appearance after they had moved into it, to confirm in Vronsky the agreeable illusion that he was not so much a Russian country gentleman, a retired army officer, as an enlightened amateur and patron of the arts, himself a modest artist who had renounced the world, his connections, and his ambition for the sake of the woman he loved.", "낡고 방치된 그 저택은 높다란 조각 천장과 벽에 그려진 프레스코화, 모자이크로 된 바닥, 창문에 드리운 묵직한 노란 천 커튼, 받침대 위에 놓인 화병들, 활짝 열린 벽난로, 조각이 새겨진 문과 그림이 걸린 음울한 응접실을 갖추고 있었는데, 이 저택은 그들이 그리로 옮겨 온 뒤로 그 외관만으로도, 자기가 러시아의 시골 지주이자 퇴역 장교라기보다는 오히려 계몽된 예술 애호가이자 후원자이며, 사랑하는 여인을 위해 세상과 인맥과 야망을 모두 단념한 겸손한 예술가라는, 브론스키의 그 흐뭇한 환상을 굳히는 데 크게 한몫했다."]], [["The pose chosen by Vronsky with their removal into the palazzo was completely successful, and having, through Golenishtchev, made acquaintance with a few interesting people, for a time he was satisfied.", "저택으로 옮겨 오면서 브론스키가 택한 자세는 완벽하게 성공적이었고, 골레니셰프를 통해 흥미로운 사람들 몇과 알고 지내게 되면서 그는 한동안 만족스러웠다."], ["He painted studies from nature under the guidance of an Italian professor of painting, and studied mediæval Italian life.", "그는 이탈리아인 회화 교수의 지도를 받아 실물을 보고 습작을 그렸고, 중세 이탈리아의 생활을 연구했다."], ["Mediæval Italian life so fascinated Vronsky that he even wore a hat and flung a cloak over his shoulder in the mediæval style, which, indeed, was extremely becoming to him.", "중세 이탈리아의 생활에 브론스키는 어찌나 매료되었던지, 중세풍으로 모자를 쓰고 어깨에 망토를 걸치고 다니기까지 했는데, 사실 그것은 그에게 무척이나 잘 어울렸다."]], [["“Here we live, and know nothing of what’s going on,”", "\"우리는 여기 살면서 바깥에서 무슨 일이 벌어지는지 아무것도 모르고 있군요.\""], ["Vronsky said to Golenishtchev as he came to see him one morning.", "어느 날 아침 골레니셰프를 찾아온 브론스키가 그에게 말했다."], ["“Have you seen Mihailov’s picture?”", "\"미하일로프의 그림 보셨습니까?\""], ["he said, handing him a Russian gazette he had received that morning, and pointing to an article on a Russian artist, living in the very same town, and just finishing a picture which had long been talked about, and had been bought beforehand.", "그가 그날 아침 받은 러시아 신문을 건네며 어느 러시아 화가에 관한 기사를 가리키며 말했다. 바로 같은 도시에 살고 있으며, 오래전부터 화제가 되어 미리 팔리기까지 한 그림을 이제 막 완성하고 있는 화가였다."], ["The article reproached the government and the academy for letting so remarkable an artist be left without encouragement and support.", "그 기사는 이토록 비범한 화가를 격려도 후원도 없이 내버려 두고 있다며 정부와 아카데미를 책망하고 있었다."]], [["“I’ve seen it,”", "\"봤습니다.\""], ["answered Golenishtchev.", "골레니셰프가 대답했다."], ["“Of course, he’s not without talent, but it’s all in a wrong direction.", "\"물론 그에게 재능이 없는 건 아니지만, 죄다 잘못된 방향이지요."], ["It’s all the Ivanov-Strauss-Renan attitude to Christ and to religious painting.”", "그리스도와 종교화를 대하는 태도가 죄다 이바노프-슈트라우스-르낭 식이란 말입니다.\""]], [["“What is the subject of the picture?”", "\"그림의 주제가 뭔가요?\""], ["asked Anna.", "안나가 물었다."]], [["“Christ before Pilate.", "\"빌라도 앞에 선 그리스도입니다."], ["Christ is represented as a Jew with all the realism of the new school.”", "그리스도를 새 화파 특유의 사실주의로 한 사람의 유대인으로 표현했지요.\""]], [["And the question of the subject of the picture having brought him to one of his favorite theories, Golenishtchev launched forth into a disquisition on it.", "그림의 주제에 관한 질문이 그를 자기가 좋아하는 이론 가운데 하나로 끌어들이자, 골레니셰프는 그것에 대한 장광설을 늘어놓기 시작했다."]], [["“I can’t understand how they can fall into such a gross mistake.", "\"어떻게 그들이 그토록 큰 잘못에 빠질 수 있는지 도무지 이해할 수가 없어요."], ["Christ always has His definite embodiment in the art of the great masters.", "그리스도는 위대한 거장들의 예술 속에 언제나 그 분명한 형상을 지니고 있단 말입니다."], ["And therefore, if they want to depict, not God, but a revolutionist or a sage, let them take from history a Socrates, a Franklin, a Charlotte Corday, but not Christ.", "그러니 만일 그들이 신이 아니라 혁명가나 현자를 그리고 싶다면, 역사에서 소크라테스나 프랭클린이나 샤를로트 코르데를 가져오면 되지, 그리스도를 가져올 일이 아니지요."], ["They take the very figure which cannot be taken for their art, and then....”", "그들은 하필이면 자기들 예술로는 취할 수 없는 형상을 취해 놓고는, 그러고 나서…….\""]], [["“And is it true that this Mihailov is in such poverty?”", "\"그런데 이 미하일로프가 그렇게 가난하다는 게 사실인가요?\""], ["asked Vronsky, thinking that, as a Russian Mæcenas, it was his duty to assist the artist regardless of whether the picture were good or bad.", "브론스키가 물었다. 그는 러시아의 한 메케나스로서 그림이 좋든 나쁘든 상관없이 그 화가를 도와주는 것이 자기 의무라고 생각하고 있었다."]], [["“I should say not.", "\"그렇지는 않을 겁니다."], ["He’s a remarkable portrait-painter.", "그는 비범한 초상화가니까요."], ["Have you ever seen his portrait of Madame Vassiltchikova?", "바실치코바 부인의 초상을 보신 적 있습니까?"], ["But I believe he doesn’t care about painting any more portraits, and so very likely he is in want.", "그런데 제 생각엔 그가 더 이상 초상화를 그리는 데에 관심이 없는 것 같으니, 어쩌면 정말로 궁핍할지도 모르지요."], ["I maintain that....”", "제가 주장하는 바는…….\""]], [["“Couldn’t we ask him to paint a portrait of Anna Arkadyevna?”", "\"우리가 그에게 안나 아르카지예브나의 초상을 그려 달라고 부탁할 수는 없을까요?\""], ["said Vronsky.", "브론스키가 말했다."]], [["“Why mine?”", "\"왜 제 초상을 그려요?\""], ["said Anna.", "안나가 말했다."], ["“After yours I don’t want another portrait. Better have one of Annie”", "\"당신이 그려 준 것이 있는데 다른 초상은 필요 없어요."], ["(so she called her baby girl). “Here she is,”", "차라리 아니의 초상을 그리게 하는 편이 낫겠어요.\""], ["she added, looking out of the window at the handsome Italian nurse, who was carrying the child out into the garden, and immediately glancing unnoticed at Vronsky.", "(그녀는 자기 어린 딸을 그렇게 불렀다.) \"마침 저기 있네요.\" 그녀는 창밖으로, 아이를 정원으로 데리고 나가는 아름다운 이탈리아인 유모를 내다보며 덧붙였고, 곧바로 들키지 않게 슬쩍 브론스키를 흘끔거렸다."], ["The handsome nurse, from whom Vronsky was painting a head for his picture, was the one hidden grief in Anna’s life.", "그 아름다운 유모는, 브론스키가 자기 그림에 쓸 두상을 그리는 모델이기도 했는데, 안나의 삶에 숨겨진 단 하나의 괴로움이었다."], ["He painted with her as his model, admired her beauty and mediævalism, and Anna dared not confess to herself that she was afraid of becoming jealous of this nurse, and was for that reason particularly gracious and condescending both to her and her little son.", "그는 그녀를 모델 삼아 그림을 그렸고, 그녀의 아름다움과 중세풍을 찬미했으며, 안나는 자기가 이 유모에게 질투를 느끼게 될까 봐 두려워하고 있다는 것을 스스로에게도 차마 인정하지 못했고, 바로 그 때문에 유모와 그녀의 어린 아들에게 유난히 너그럽고 깍듯하게 대했다."], ["Vronsky, too, glanced out of the window and into Anna’s eyes, and, turning at once to Golenishtchev, he said:", "브론스키 역시 창밖을 내다보았다가 안나의 눈을 들여다보고는, 곧장 골레니셰프 쪽으로 돌아서며 말했다."]], [["“Do you know this Mihailov?”", "\"이 미하일로프를 아십니까?\""]], [["“I have met him.", "\"만난 적은 있습니다."], ["But he’s a queer fish, and quite without breeding.", "그런데 괴짜인 데다 교양이라곤 도통 없는 사람이에요."], ["You know, one of those uncouth new people one’s so often coming across nowadays, one of those free-thinkers you know, who are reared d’emblée in theories of atheism, scepticism, and materialism.", "아시잖습니까, 요즘 그렇게 자주 마주치게 되는 그 거칠고 새로운 부류, 그러니까 무신론과 회의론과 유물론의 이론 속에서 d'emblée 길러진 자유사상가 부류 말입니다."], ["In former days,”", "옛날에는,\""], ["said Golenishtchev, not observing, or not willing to observe, that both Anna and Vronsky wanted to speak, “in former days the free-thinker was a man who had been brought up in ideas of religion, law, and morality, and only through conflict and struggle came to free-thought; but now there has sprung up a new type of born free-thinkers who grow up without even having heard of principles of morality or of religion, of the existence of authorities, who grow up directly in ideas of negation in everything, that is to say, savages.", "골레니셰프는 안나도 브론스키도 무언가 말하고 싶어 한다는 것을 알아채지 못하거나 알아채려 하지 않은 채 말을 이어 갔다. \"옛날에는 자유사상가란 종교와 법과 도덕의 관념 속에서 자라난 사람이 오로지 갈등과 투쟁을 통해서만 자유사상에 이른 사람이었지요."], ["Well, he’s of that class.", "그런데 이제는 새로운 유형의 타고난 자유사상가들이 생겨났단 말입니다."], ["He’s the son, it appears, of some Moscow butler, and has never had any sort of bringing-up.", "도덕이나 종교의 원리에 대해, 권위라는 것의 존재에 대해 들어 본 적조차 없이 자라난 사람들, 만사를 부정하는 관념 속에서 곧바로 자라난 사람들, 한마디로 야만인들이지요."], ["When he got into the academy and made his reputation he tried, as he’s no fool, to educate himself.", "자, 그가 바로 그런 부류입니다. 듣자 하니 모스크바의 어느 집사의 아들이라는데, 변변한 가정교육이라곤 받아 본 적이 없답니다."], ["And he turned to what seemed to him the very source of culture—the magazines.", "아카데미에 들어가 명성을 얻고 나서, 그는 바보가 아닌지라 스스로 교육해 보려고 애를 썼지요."], ["In old times, you see, a man who wanted to educate himself—a Frenchman, for instance—would have set to work to study all the classics and theologians and tragedians and historians and philosophers, and, you know, all the intellectual work that came in his way.", "그러고는 자기에게 교양의 바로 그 원천으로 보이는 것에 눈을 돌렸는데, 그게 잡지였단 말입니다. 보세요, 옛날에는 스스로 교육하고자 하는 사람이라면, 이를테면 프랑스인이라면, 모든 고전과 신학자와 비극 작가와 역사가와 철학자, 그러니까 자기 앞에 놓인 온갖 지적 업적을 다 공부하기 시작했을 겁니다."], ["But in our day he goes straight for the literature of negation, very quickly assimilates all the extracts of the science of negation, and he’s ready.", "그런데 요즘 사람은 곧장 부정의 문헌으로 직행해서, 부정의 학문에서 발췌한 것들을 아주 빠르게 흡수하고는, 그걸로 준비가 다 됐다고 하지요. 그뿐만이 아닙니다."], ["And that’s not all—twenty years ago he would have found in that literature traces of conflict with authorities, with the creeds of the ages; he would have perceived from this conflict that there was something else; but now he comes at once upon a literature in which the old creeds do not even furnish matter for discussion, but it is stated baldly that there is nothing else—evolution, natural selection, struggle for existence—and that’s all.", "이십 년 전이라면 그 문헌 속에서 권위와의, 시대의 신조들과의 갈등의 흔적을 발견했을 테고, 그 갈등을 통해 다른 무언가가 있다는 것을 깨달았을 겁니다. 그런데 지금은 옛 신조들이 논의의 거리조차 되지 못하고, 다른 건 아무것도 없다고, 진화와 자연선택과 생존경쟁뿐이라고 노골적으로 단언하는 문헌과 곧장 맞닥뜨리게 되지요."], ["In my article I’ve....”", "그게 전부예요. 제 논문에서 저는…….\""]], [["“I tell you what,”", "\"제가 한 말씀 드리죠.\" 안나가 말했다."], ["said Anna, who had for a long while been exchanging wary glances with Vronsky, and knew that he was not in the least interested in the education of this artist, but was simply absorbed by the idea of assisting him, and ordering a portrait of him; “I tell you what,”", "그녀는 한참 동안 브론스키와 조심스러운 눈짓을 주고받으며, 그가 이 화가의 교육 문제 따위에는 조금도 관심이 없고 단지 그를 돕고 그에게 초상화를 주문하려는 생각에 골몰해 있다는 것을 알고 있었다. \"제가 한 말씀 드릴게요.\""], ["she said, resolutely interrupting Golenishtchev, who was still talking away, “let’s go and see him!”", "그녀는 여전히 떠들어 대고 있는 골레니셰프의 말을 단호하게 가로막으며 말했다. \"그를 만나러 가요!\""]], [["Golenishtchev recovered his self-possession and readily agreed.", "골레니셰프는 평정을 되찾고 선뜻 동의했다."], ["But as the artist lived in a remote suburb, it was decided to take the carriage.", "다만 그 화가가 멀리 떨어진 변두리에 살고 있었으므로, 마차를 타고 가기로 했다."]], [["An hour later Anna, with Golenishtchev by her side and Vronsky on the front seat of the carriage, facing them, drove up to a new ugly house in the remote suburb.", "한 시간 뒤, 안나는 곁에 골레니셰프를, 그리고 맞은편 앞자리에 브론스키를 앉히고서, 멀리 떨어진 변두리의 어느 새로 지은 흉한 집 앞에 마차를 댔다."], ["On learning from the porter’s wife, who came out to them, that Mihailov saw visitors at his studio, but that at that moment he was in his lodging only a couple of steps off, they sent her to him with their cards, asking permission to see his picture.", "그들에게 나온 문지기의 아내에게서, 미하일로프가 화실에서는 방문객을 맞지만 지금은 바로 몇 걸음 떨어진 자기 거처에 있다는 말을 듣고, 그들은 그녀에게 명함을 들려 보내며 그림을 볼 수 있게 허락해 달라고 청했다."]], [["# Chapter 10", "# Chapter 10"]], [["The artist Mihailov was, as always, at work when the cards of Count Vronsky and Golenishtchev were brought to him.", "화가 미하일로프는 브론스키 백작과 골레니셰프의 명함이 그에게 전해졌을 때, 늘 그렇듯이 작업 중이었다."], ["In the morning he had been working in his studio at his big picture.", "아침에 그는 자기 아틀리에에서 큰 그림에 매달려 있었다."], ["On getting home he flew into a rage with his wife for not having managed to put off the landlady, who had been asking for money.", "집에 돌아오자, 돈을 달라고 졸라 대던 집주인 여자를 잘 돌려보내지 못했다며 아내에게 화를 냈다."]], [["“I’ve said it to you twenty times, don’t enter into details.", "\"내가 스무 번도 더 말했잖아, 자질구레한 얘기에 끼어들지 말라고."], ["You’re fool enough at all times, and when you start explaining things in Italian you’re a fool three times as foolish,”", "당신은 어느 때나 충분히 멍청한데, 이탈리아 말로 이러쿵저러쿵 설명하기 시작하면 멍청함이 세 배는 더 멍청해진다고.\""], ["he said after a long dispute.", "그는 한참 옥신각신한 끝에 말했다."]], [["“Don’t let it run so long; it’s not my fault.", "\"그렇게 질질 끌게 두지 말았어야죠. 내 잘못이 아니에요."], ["If I had the money....”", "나한테 돈만 있었어도……\""]], [["“Leave me in peace, for God’s sake!”", "\"제발 좀 나를 가만히 내버려 둬!\""], ["Mihailov shrieked, with tears in his voice, and, stopping his ears, he went off into his working room, the other side of a partition wall, and closed the door after him.", "미하일로프는 목소리에 울음을 섞어 비명을 질렀고, 두 귀를 틀어막은 채 칸막이벽 너머 자기 작업실로 가 버리더니 문을 닫았다. \"멍청한 여자 같으니!\""], ["“Idiotic woman!” he said to himself, sat down to the table, and, opening a portfolio, he set to work at once with peculiar fervor at a sketch he had begun.", "그는 혼잣말로 중얼거리며 탁자 앞에 앉아, 화첩을 펼쳐 들고는 진작 시작해 둔 밑그림 하나에 곧장 유난한 열기로 매달리기 시작했다."]], [["Never did he work with such fervor and success as when things went ill with him, and especially when he quarreled with his wife.", "그는 일이 잘 풀리지 않을 때, 특히 아내와 다투었을 때만큼 그토록 열렬하고 성공적으로 작업한 적이 없었다. \"오!"], ["“Oh! damn them all!”", "다들 빌어먹으라지!\""], ["he thought as he went on working.", "그는 계속 작업하면서 생각했다."], ["He was making a sketch for the figure of a man in a violent rage.", "그는 격렬한 분노에 휩싸인 남자의 모습을 밑그림으로 그리는 중이었다."], ["A sketch had been made before, but he was dissatisfied with it.", "전에 한 번 밑그림을 그려 두었지만 그것이 마음에 들지 않았다."], ["“No, that one was better ...", "\"아니야, 그게 더 나았는데……"], ["where is it?”", "그게 어디 있더라?\""], ["He went back to his wife, and scowling, and not looking at her, asked his eldest little girl, where was that piece of paper he had given them?", "그는 아내에게로 돌아가서, 얼굴을 찌푸린 채 아내 쪽은 쳐다보지도 않고, 자기가 아이들에게 준 그 종이가 어디 있느냐고 큰딸에게 물었다."], ["The paper with the discarded sketch on it was found, but it was dirty, and spotted with candle-grease.", "버려진 밑그림이 그려진 종이를 찾아냈는데, 더럽고 양초 기름 얼룩이 점점이 묻어 있었다."], ["Still, he took the sketch, laid it on his table, and, moving a little away, screwing up his eyes, he fell to gazing at it.", "그래도 그는 그 밑그림을 집어 탁자 위에 놓고, 조금 물러서서 눈을 가늘게 뜬 채 그것을 응시하기 시작했다."], ["All at once he smiled and gesticulated gleefully.", "별안간 그는 미소를 지으며 신이 나서 손짓을 했다."]], [["“That’s it!", "\"바로 그거야!"], ["that’s it!”", "바로 그거라고!\""], ["he said, and, at once picking up the pencil, he began rapidly drawing.", "그는 이렇게 말하고는 곧장 연필을 집어 들어 빠르게 그리기 시작했다."], ["The spot of tallow had given the man a new pose.", "그 양초 기름 얼룩이 남자에게 새로운 자세를 부여한 것이었다."]], [["He had sketched this new pose, when all at once he recalled the face of a shopkeeper of whom he had bought cigars, a vigorous face with a prominent chin, and he sketched this very face, this chin on to the figure of the man.", "그가 이 새로운 자세를 밑그림으로 그려 냈을 때, 별안간 자기에게 시가를 팔았던 어느 가게 주인의 얼굴이, 툭 튀어나온 턱을 가진 그 활기찬 얼굴이 떠올랐고, 그는 바로 그 얼굴을, 그 턱을 남자의 모습에 옮겨 그렸다."], ["He laughed aloud with delight.", "그는 기쁨에 겨워 소리 내어 웃었다."], ["The figure from a lifeless imagined thing had become living, and such that it could never be changed.", "그 모습은 생명 없이 상상으로 빚어낸 것에서 살아 있는 것으로, 다시는 바꿀 수 없는 그런 것으로 변모해 있었다."], ["That figure lived, and was clearly and unmistakably defined.", "그 모습은 살아 있었고, 또렷하고 틀림없이 규정되어 있었다."], ["The sketch might be corrected in accordance with the requirements of the figure, the legs, indeed, could and must be put differently, and the position of the left hand must be quite altered; the hair too might be thrown back.", "밑그림은 그 모습의 요구에 따라 손볼 수도 있었다. 다리는 사실 다르게 놓을 수 있고 또 그래야만 했으며, 왼손의 위치도 완전히 바꿔야 했다."], ["But in making these corrections he was not altering the figure but simply getting rid of what concealed the figure.", "머리카락도 뒤로 넘길 수 있었다. 하지만 이런 수정을 하면서도 그는 모습 자체를 바꾸는 것이 아니라 그저 그 모습을 가리고 있는 것을 걷어 낼 따름이었다."], ["He was, as it were, stripping off the wrappings which hindered it from being distinctly seen.", "말하자면 그는 그 모습이 또렷이 보이는 것을 방해하는 껍데기를 벗겨 내고 있었다."], ["Each new feature only brought out the whole figure in all its force and vigor, as it had suddenly come to him from the spot of tallow.", "새로운 특징 하나하나가 그저, 양초 기름 얼룩에서 그에게 별안간 다가왔던 그대로, 그 모습 전체를 온전한 힘과 활기 속에서 드러내 줄 뿐이었다."], ["He was carefully finishing the figure when the cards were brought him.", "그가 그 모습을 꼼꼼히 마무리하고 있을 때 명함이 그에게 전해진 것이었다."]], [["“Coming, coming!”", "\"갑니다, 가요!\""]], [["He went in to his wife.", "그는 아내에게로 들어갔다."]], [["“Come, Sasha, don’t be cross!”", "\"이봐, 사샤, 화내지 마!\""], ["he said, smiling timidly and affectionately at her.", "그는 수줍은 듯 다정하게 아내에게 미소 지으며 말했다."], ["“You were to blame.", "\"당신 잘못이었어."], ["I was to blame.", "내 잘못이었지."], ["I’ll make it all right.”", "내가 다 잘 풀어 볼게.\""], ["And having made peace with his wife he put on an olive-green overcoat with a velvet collar and a hat, and went towards his studio.", "그렇게 아내와 화해한 뒤 그는 벨벳 깃이 달린 올리브색 외투를 걸치고 모자를 쓴 채 아틀리에로 향했다."], ["The successful figure he had already forgotten.", "잘 그려진 그 모습은 그가 벌써 잊어버린 뒤였다."], ["Now he was delighted and excited at the visit of these people of consequence, Russians, who had come in their carriage.", "이제 그는 마차를 타고 찾아온, 이 지체 높은 사람들, 러시아인들의 방문에 기뻐 들떠 있었다."]], [["Of his picture, the one that stood now on his easel, he had at the bottom of his heart one conviction—that no one had ever painted a picture like it.", "지금 자기 이젤 위에 놓여 있는 그림에 대해, 그는 마음 깊은 곳에 한 가지 확신을 품고 있었다. 그 누구도 이런 그림을 그린 적이 없다는 확신이었다."], ["He did not believe that his picture was better than all the pictures of Raphael, but he knew that what he tried to convey in that picture, no one ever had conveyed.", "그는 자기 그림이 라파엘로의 모든 그림보다 낫다고 믿지는 않았지만, 자기가 그 그림에서 전하고자 한 것을 그 누구도 전한 적이 없다는 것만은 알고 있었다."], ["This he knew positively, and had known a long while, ever since he had begun to paint it.", "이것을 그는 확실히, 그것도 오래전부터, 그 그림을 그리기 시작한 바로 그때부터 알고 있었다."], ["But other people’s criticisms, whatever they might be, had yet immense consequence in his eyes, and they agitated him to the depths of his soul.", "그렇지만 다른 사람들의 평가는, 그것이 어떤 것이든, 그의 눈에는 여전히 엄청난 무게를 지녔고, 그의 영혼 밑바닥까지 뒤흔들어 놓았다."], ["Any remark, the most insignificant, that showed that the critic saw even the tiniest part of what he saw in the picture, agitated him to the depths of his soul.", "어떤 말이든, 아무리 하찮은 말이라도, 그 비평가가 자기가 그림 속에서 본 것 중 아주 작은 일부라도 보았음을 드러내는 말이면 그의 영혼 밑바닥까지 뒤흔들었다."], ["He always attributed to his critics a more profound comprehension than he had himself, and always expected from them something he did not himself see in the picture.", "그는 늘 자기 비평가들에게 자기 자신보다 더 깊은 이해력이 있으리라고 여겼고, 늘 그들에게서 자기가 그림 속에서 미처 보지 못한 무언가를 기대했다."], ["And often in their criticisms he fancied that he had found this.", "그리고 종종 그들의 비평 속에서 자기가 바로 그것을 찾아냈다고 믿곤 했다."]], [["He walked rapidly to the door of his studio, and in spite of his excitement he was struck by the soft light on Anna’s figure as she stood in the shade of the entrance listening to Golenishtchev, who was eagerly telling her something, while she evidently wanted to look round at the artist.", "그는 아틀리에 문으로 빠르게 걸어갔는데, 들뜬 와중에도 입구의 그늘에 선 안나의 모습에 비치는 부드러운 빛에 마음이 사로잡혔다. 그녀는 무언가를 열심히 이야기하는 골레니셰프의 말에 귀를 기울이면서도, 분명 화가 쪽을 돌아보고 싶어 하는 기색이었다."], ["He was himself unconscious how, as he approached them, he seized on this impression and absorbed it, as he had the chin of the shopkeeper who had sold him the cigars, and put it away somewhere to be brought out when he wanted it.", "그가 그들에게 다가가면서 어떻게 이 인상을 붙잡아, 자기에게 시가를 팔았던 가게 주인의 턱을 그랬던 것처럼 빨아들였는지, 그리고 필요할 때 꺼내 쓰려고 그것을 어딘가에 챙겨 두었는지, 그 자신은 의식하지 못했다."], ["The visitors, not agreeably impressed beforehand by Golenishtchev’s account of the artist, were still less so by his personal appearance.", "손님들은 골레니셰프가 그 화가에 대해 늘어놓은 이야기에 미리부터 호감을 갖지 못한 터였지만, 그의 외모에는 더더욱 호감이 가지 않았다."], ["Thick-set and of middle height, with nimble movements, with his brown hat, olive-green coat and narrow trousers—though wide trousers had been a long while in fashion,—most of all, with the ordinariness of his broad face, and the combined expression of timidity and anxiety to keep up his dignity, Mihailov made an unpleasant impression.", "다부진 체격에 중키, 민첩한 동작, 갈색 모자에 올리브색 외투, 그리고 통 넓은 바지가 유행한 지 한참이나 되었는데도 그가 입고 있는 좁은 바지, 무엇보다도 넓적한 얼굴의 그 평범함, 거기에 수줍음과 위신을 지키려는 안달이 뒤섞인 표정까지, 미하일로프는 유쾌하지 못한 인상을 주었다."]], [["“Please step in,”", "\"들어오시지요.\""], ["he said, trying to look indifferent, and going into the passage he took a key out of his pocket and opened the door.", "그는 무심한 표정을 지으려 애쓰며 말하고는, 복도로 들어가 주머니에서 열쇠를 꺼내 문을 열었다."]], [["# Chapter 11", "# Chapter 11"]], [["On entering the studio, Mihailov once more scanned his visitors and noted down in his imagination Vronsky’s expression too, and especially his jaws.", "화실로 들어서면서 미하일로프는 다시 한번 방문객들을 훑어보았고, 상상 속에 브론스키의 표정 또한, 특히 그 턱을 새겨 두었다."], ["Although his artistic sense was unceasingly at work collecting materials, although he felt a continually increasing excitement as the moment of criticizing his work drew nearer, he rapidly and subtly formed, from imperceptible signs, a mental image of these three persons.", "그의 예술적 감각은 끊임없이 재료를 모으느라 쉴 새 없이 작동했고, 자기 작품이 비평받을 순간이 다가올수록 점점 더 커지는 흥분을 느꼈지만, 그는 눈에 띄지 않는 단서들로부터 이 세 사람에 대한 심상을 재빠르고 섬세하게 빚어냈다."]], [["That fellow (Golenishtchev) was a Russian living here.", "저 사내(골레니셰프)는 이곳에 사는 러시아인이었다."], ["Mihailov did not remember his surname nor where he had met him, nor what he had said to him.", "미하일로프는 그의 성도, 어디서 그를 만났는지도, 그에게 무슨 말을 했는지도 기억하지 못했다."], ["He only remembered his face as he remembered all the faces he had ever seen; but he remembered, too, that it was one of the faces laid by in his memory in the immense class of the falsely consequential and poor in expression.", "그저 자기가 본 모든 얼굴을 기억하듯 그의 얼굴을 기억할 뿐이었다. 하지만 그는 그 얼굴이 자기 기억 속에서 거짓되게 거드름을 부리며 표정이 빈곤한 부류라는 거대한 범주에 분류해 둔 얼굴 가운데 하나라는 것도 기억했다."], ["The abundant hair and very open forehead gave an appearance of consequence to the face, which had only one expression—a petty, childish, peevish expression, concentrated just above the bridge of the narrow nose.", "풍성한 머리카락과 몹시 훤한 이마가 얼굴에 거드름의 분위기를 주었지만, 그 얼굴에는 좁은 콧날 바로 위에 집중된, 옹졸하고 어린애 같으며 짜증스러운 표정 하나밖에 없었다."], ["Vronsky and Madame Karenina must be, Mihailov supposed, distinguished and wealthy Russians, knowing nothing about art, like all those wealthy Russians, but posing as amateurs and connoisseurs.", "브론스키와 마담 카레니나는, 미하일로프가 짐작하기로, 모든 부유한 러시아인이 그렇듯 예술에 대해서는 아무것도 모르면서 애호가요 감식가인 척하는, 지체 높고 부유한 러시아인임이 분명했다."], ["“Most likely they’ve already looked at all the antiques, and now they’re making the round of the studios of the new people, the German humbug, and the cracked Pre-Raphaelite English fellow, and have only come to me to make the point of view complete,”", "\"필시 저들은 골동품은 이미 다 둘러보았을 거고, 이제 신진들의 화실을, 그러니까 그 독일 사기꾼이며 머리가 좀 돈 라파엘 전파의 영국인 화가 같은 자들을 한 바퀴 돌고 나서, 시각을 완비하려고 내게도 들른 것이겠지.\""], ["he thought.", "그는 생각했다."], ["He was well acquainted with the way dilettanti have (the cleverer they were the worse he found them) of looking at the works of contemporary artists with the sole object of being in a position to say that art is a thing of the past, and that the more one sees of the new men the more one sees how inimitable the works of the great old masters have remained.", "그는 호사가들이(더 영리할수록 그에게는 더 고약하게 느껴졌다) 동시대 화가들의 작품을 바라보는 방식을 익히 알고 있었다. 오로지 예술은 한물간 것이라고, 신진들을 보면 볼수록 위대한 옛 거장들의 작품이 얼마나 흉내 낼 수 없는 것인지 알게 된다고 말할 수 있는 처지에 서기 위해서만 작품을 보는 것이었다."], ["He expected all this; he saw it all in their faces, he saw it in the careless indifference with which they talked among themselves, stared at the lay figures and busts, and walked about in leisurely fashion, waiting for him to uncover his picture.", "그는 이 모든 것을 예상하고 있었다. 그는 그것을 그들의 얼굴에서 보았고, 그들이 자기들끼리 무심하게 이야기를 주고받으며 모형 인형이며 흉상을 멍하니 바라보고, 그가 그림을 가릴 것을 벗겨 주기를 기다리며 느긋하게 거니는 그 태평한 무관심에서 보았다."], ["But in spite of this, while he was turning over his studies, pulling up the blinds and taking off the sheet, he was in intense excitement, especially as, in spite of his conviction that all distinguished and wealthy Russians were certain to be beasts and fools, he liked Vronsky, and still more Anna.", "그러나 그럼에도, 습작들을 뒤적이고 차양을 걷어 올리며 덮개를 벗기는 동안, 그는 극심한 흥분에 휩싸여 있었다. 지체 높고 부유한 러시아인이라면 죄다 짐승이요 멍청이임이 틀림없다는 확신에도 불구하고, 그는 브론스키가 마음에 들었고, 안나는 더더욱 마음에 들었기 때문이었다."]], [["“Here, if you please,”", "\"여기, 보십시오.\""], ["he said, moving on one side with his nimble gait and pointing to his picture, “it’s the exhortation to Pilate.", "그는 날렵한 걸음으로 한쪽으로 비켜서며 자기 그림을 가리키며 말했다. \"빌라도에게 권고하는 장면입니다."], ["Matthew, chapter xxvii,”", "마태복음 27장이지요.\""], ["he said, feeling his lips were beginning to tremble with emotion.", "그는 감정에 북받쳐 입술이 떨리기 시작하는 것을 느끼며 말했다."], ["He moved away and stood behind them.", "그러고는 물러서서 그들 뒤에 가서 섰다."]], [["For the few seconds during which the visitors were gazing at the picture in silence Mihailov too gazed at it with the indifferent eye of an outsider.", "방문객들이 말없이 그림을 응시하던 그 몇 초 동안, 미하일로프 또한 무관한 외부인의 무심한 눈으로 그것을 바라보았다."], ["For those few seconds he was sure in anticipation that a higher, juster criticism would be uttered by them, by those very visitors whom he had been so despising a moment before.", "그 몇 초 동안 그는 조금 전까지만 해도 그토록 경멸하던 바로 그 방문객들에게서 더 높고 더 정당한 비평이 나오리라고 미리 확신했다. 그는 그 그림을 그려 온 삼 년 동안 그것에 대해 생각했던 모든 것을 잊어버렸다."], ["He forgot all he had thought about his picture before during the three years he had been painting it; he forgot all its qualities which had been absolutely certain to him—he saw the picture with their indifferent, new, outside eyes, and saw nothing good in it.", "그에게 절대적으로 확실했던 그것의 온갖 미덕을 잊어버렸다. 그는 그들의 무심하고 새롭고 외부적인 눈으로 그림을 보았고, 거기서 아무런 좋은 점도 보지 못했다."], ["He saw in the foreground Pilate’s irritated face and the serene face of Christ, and in the background the figures of Pilate’s retinue and the face of John watching what was happening.", "그는 전경에서 빌라도의 짜증 난 얼굴과 그리스도의 평온한 얼굴을, 배경에서 빌라도의 수행원들의 형상과 벌어지는 일을 지켜보는 요한의 얼굴을 보았다."], ["Every face that, with such agony, such blunders and corrections had grown up within him with its special character, every face that had given him such torments and such raptures, and all these faces so many times transposed for the sake of the harmony of the whole, all the shades of color and tones that he had attained with such labor—all of this together seemed to him now, looking at it with their eyes, the merest vulgarity, something that had been done a thousand times over.", "그토록 고뇌하며, 그토록 헤매고 고치며 그 안에서 저마다의 독특한 성격을 띠고 자라난 모든 얼굴, 그에게 그토록 큰 고통과 환희를 안겨 준 모든 얼굴, 전체의 조화를 위해 그토록 여러 번 자리를 옮긴 이 모든 얼굴, 그토록 애써 도달한 온갖 색조와 색감—이 모든 것이 한데 어우러져, 그들의 눈으로 바라보니 이제 그에게는 더없이 천박한 것, 천 번도 넘게 그려진 것처럼 보였다."], ["The face dearest to him, the face of Christ, the center of the picture, which had given him such ecstasy as it unfolded itself to him, was utterly lost to him when he glanced at the picture with their eyes.", "그에게 가장 소중한 얼굴, 그림의 중심인 그리스도의 얼굴, 그에게 펼쳐지면서 그토록 큰 황홀을 안겨 준 그 얼굴은, 그들의 눈으로 그림을 흘끗 보자 그에게서 완전히 사라져 버렸다."], ["He saw a well-painted (no, not even that—he distinctly saw now a mass of defects) repetition of those endless Christs of Titian, Raphael, Rubens, and the same soldiers and Pilate.", "그는 티치아노, 라파엘로, 루벤스의 그 끝없는 그리스도들과 똑같은 병사들과 빌라도를 잘 그려 낸(아니, 그것조차 아니었다—이제 그는 결함 덩어리를 또렷이 보았다) 반복을 보았다."], ["It was all common, poor, and stale, and positively badly painted—weak and unequal.", "그것은 모두 흔하고 빈약하고 진부했으며, 명백히 형편없이 그려진—약하고 고르지 못한 것이었다."], ["They would be justified in repeating hypocritically civil speeches in the presence of the painter, and pitying him and laughing at him when they were alone again.", "그들이 화가 앞에서 위선적으로 정중한 말을 늘어놓다가, 다시 자기들끼리 남게 되면 그를 가엾어하고 비웃는다 해도 그것은 마땅한 일이었다."]], [["The silence (though it lasted no more than a minute) became too intolerable to him.", "침묵이(채 일 분도 지속되지 않았건만) 그에게는 견딜 수 없을 만큼 괴로워졌다."], ["To break it, and to show he was not agitated, he made an effort and addressed Golenishtchev.", "침묵을 깨고, 자기가 동요하지 않았음을 보이려고, 그는 애써 골레니셰프에게 말을 건넸다."]], [["“I think I’ve had the pleasure of meeting you,”", "\"제가 전에 뵌 적이 있는 것 같습니다만.\""], ["he said, looking uneasily first at Anna, then at Vronsky, in fear of losing any shade of their expression.", "그는 그들 표정의 어떤 미묘한 결도 놓칠까 두려워하며 불안하게 처음엔 안나를, 다음엔 브론스키를 바라보며 말했다."]], [["“To be sure!", "\"물론이지요!"], ["We met at Rossi’s, do you remember, at that soirée when that Italian lady recited—the new Rachel?”", "로시 댁에서 만났잖습니까, 기억하시나요, 그 이탈리아 부인이—새로운 라셸이라던 그 여인이—낭송하던 그 soirée 자리에서요?\""], ["Golenishtchev answered easily, removing his eyes without the slightest regret from the picture and turning to the artist.", "골레니셰프는 조금의 아쉬움도 없이 그림에서 시선을 거두며 화가 쪽으로 돌아서서는 편안하게 대답했다. * soirée: 프랑스어로 '저녁 모임, 야회'."]], [["Noticing, however, that Mihailov was expecting a criticism of the picture, he said:", "그러나 미하일로프가 그림에 대한 비평을 기다리고 있음을 알아채고서 그는 말했다."]], [["“Your picture has got on a great deal since I saw it last time; and what strikes me particularly now, as it did then, is the figure of Pilate.", "\"당신 그림은 지난번에 봤을 때보다 훨씬 진척되었군요. 그리고 지금도 그때처럼 제게 특히 인상적인 것은 빌라도의 형상입니다."], ["One so knows the man: a good-natured, capital fellow, but an official through and through, who does not know what it is he’s doing.", "그 인물을 정말 잘 알겠어요. 사람 좋고 더없이 훌륭한 사내지만, 뼛속까지 관리라 자기가 무슨 일을 하는지 모르는 자 말입니다."], ["But I fancy....”", "하지만 제 생각엔…….\""]], [["All Mihailov’s mobile face beamed at once; his eyes sparkled.", "미하일로프의 변화무쌍한 얼굴이 한순간에 환하게 빛났다."], ["He tried to say something, but he could not speak for excitement, and pretended to be coughing.", "그의 눈이 반짝였다. 그는 무언가 말하려 했지만 흥분 때문에 말이 나오지 않아 기침을 하는 척했다."], ["Low as was his opinion of Golenishtchev’s capacity for understanding art, trifling as was the true remark upon the fidelity of the expression of Pilate as an official, and offensive as might have seemed the utterance of so unimportant an observation while nothing was said of more serious points, Mihailov was in an ecstasy of delight at this observation.", "골레니셰프의 예술 이해 능력에 대한 그의 평가가 아무리 낮았다 해도, 빌라도를 관리로서 표현한 충실함에 관한 그 옳은 지적이 아무리 사소했다 해도, 더 진지한 점에 대해서는 아무 말도 없는 마당에 그처럼 하찮은 관찰을 입에 올린 것이 아무리 거슬릴 만했다 해도, 미하일로프는 이 관찰에 황홀한 기쁨에 휩싸였다."], ["He had himself thought about Pilate’s figure just what Golenishtchev said.", "그 자신이 빌라도의 형상에 대해 골레니셰프가 말한 바로 그대로 생각하고 있었던 것이다."], ["The fact that this reflection was but one of millions of reflections, which as Mihailov knew for certain would be true, did not diminish for him the significance of Golenishtchev’s remark.", "이 견해가 미하일로프가 확실히 알기로 진실일 수백만 가지 견해 가운데 하나에 불과하다는 사실도, 그에게 골레니셰프의 지적이 갖는 의미를 깎아내리지는 못했다."], ["His heart warmed to Golenishtchev for this remark, and from a state of depression he suddenly passed to ecstasy.", "그의 마음은 이 지적 덕분에 골레니셰프에게 따뜻해졌고, 그는 침울한 상태에서 갑자기 황홀로 옮겨 갔다."], ["At once the whole of his picture lived before him in all the indescribable complexity of everything living.", "단숨에 그의 그림 전체가, 살아 있는 모든 것의 형언할 수 없는 복잡함을 고스란히 띤 채 그의 앞에 살아났다."], ["Mihailov again tried to say that that was how he understood Pilate, but his lips quivered intractably, and he could not pronounce the words.", "미하일로프는 다시 빌라도를 자기가 그렇게 이해했노라고 말하려 했지만, 입술이 도무지 말을 듣지 않고 떨려서 그 말을 발음할 수 없었다."], ["Vronsky and Anna too said something in that subdued voice in which, partly to avoid hurting the artist’s feelings and partly to avoid saying out loud something silly—so easily said when talking of art—people usually speak at exhibitions of pictures.", "브론스키와 안나 또한 무언가를 그 낮은 목소리로 말했는데, 사람들은 한편으로는 화가의 감정을 상하게 하지 않으려고, 또 한편으로는 예술을 이야기할 때 그토록 쉽게 튀어나오는 어리석은 말을 큰 소리로 내뱉지 않으려고 그림 전시회에서 흔히 그런 목소리로 말하곤 했다."], ["Mihailov fancied that the picture had made an impression on them too.", "미하일로프는 그림이 그들에게도 인상을 남겼다고 짐작했다."], ["He went up to them.", "그는 그들에게 다가갔다."]], [["“How marvelous Christ’s expression is!”", "\"그리스도의 표정이 정말 경이로워요!\""], ["said Anna.", "안나가 말했다."], ["Of all she saw she liked that expression most of all, and she felt that it was the center of the picture, and so praise of it would be pleasant to the artist.", "그녀가 본 모든 것 가운데 그녀는 그 표정이 가장 마음에 들었고, 그것이 그림의 중심임을 느꼈으므로, 그 표정에 대한 칭찬이 화가에게 흐뭇하리라고 생각했다."], ["“One can see that He is pitying Pilate.”", "\"그분이 빌라도를 가엾어하고 있다는 걸 알 수 있어요.\""]], [["This again was one of the million true reflections that could be found in his picture and in the figure of Christ.", "이 또한 그의 그림에서, 그리고 그리스도의 형상에서 찾아낼 수 있는 수백만 가지 옳은 견해 가운데 하나였다."], ["She said that He was pitying Pilate.", "그녀는 그분이 빌라도를 가엾어하고 있다고 말했다."], ["In Christ’s expression there ought to be indeed an expression of pity, since there is an expression of love, of heavenly peace, of readiness for death, and a sense of the vanity of words.", "그리스도의 표정에는 마땅히 연민의 표정이 있어야 했다. 거기에는 사랑의 표정, 천상의 평화의 표정, 죽음을 받아들일 각오의 표정, 그리고 말의 헛됨에 대한 감각이 있기 때문이다."], ["Of course there is the expression of an official in Pilate and of pity in Christ, seeing that one is the incarnation of the fleshly and the other of the spiritual life.", "물론 빌라도에게는 관리의 표정이, 그리스도에게는 연민의 표정이 있다. 하나는 육신의 화신이고 다른 하나는 영적 삶의 화신이니 말이다."], ["All this and much more flashed into Mihailov’s thoughts.", "이 모든 것과 그 밖의 더 많은 것이 미하일로프의 머릿속에 번득였다."]], [["“Yes, and how that figure is done—what atmosphere! One can walk round it,”", "\"그래요, 그리고 저 형상이 어찌나 잘 그려졌는지—저 분위기라니!"], ["said Golenishtchev, unmistakably betraying by this remark that he did not approve of the meaning and idea of the figure.", "그 둘레를 빙 돌아 걸을 수도 있겠어요.\" 골레니셰프가 말했는데, 이 말로 그가 그 형상의 의미와 사상에는 동의하지 않음을 여지없이 드러냈다."]], [["“Yes, there’s a wonderful mastery!”", "\"그래요, 놀라운 솜씨예요!\""], ["said Vronsky.", "브론스키가 말했다."], ["“How those figures in the background stand out!", "\"저 배경의 형상들이 어쩌면 저렇게 도드라지는지!"], ["There you have technique,”", "저것이야말로 기법이라는 거지요.\""], ["he said, addressing Golenishtchev, alluding to a conversation between them about Vronsky’s despair of attaining this technique.", "그는 그 기법에 도달하지 못하는 자신의 절망에 대해 둘이서 나눈 대화를 빗대어 골레니셰프를 향해 말했다."]], [["“Yes, yes, marvelous!”", "\"그래요, 그래요, 경이롭습니다!\""], ["Golenishtchev and Anna assented.", "골레니셰프와 안나가 맞장구를 쳤다."], ["In spite of the excited condition in which he was, the sentence about technique had sent a pang to Mihailov’s heart, and looking angrily at Vronsky he suddenly scowled.", "흥분한 상태였음에도, 기법에 관한 그 말은 미하일로프의 가슴을 찌르듯 아프게 했고, 그는 브론스키를 성난 눈으로 바라보며 갑자기 인상을 찌푸렸다."], ["He had often heard this word technique, and was utterly unable to understand what was understood by it.", "그는 이 기법이라는 말을 자주 들었지만, 그것이 무엇을 뜻하는지 도무지 이해할 수가 없었다."], ["He knew that by this term was understood a mechanical facility for painting or drawing, entirely apart from its subject.", "이 용어로 사람들이 그림의 주제와는 전혀 무관한, 그리거나 칠하는 기계적 숙련을 뜻한다는 것을 그는 알고 있었다."], ["He had noticed often that even in actual praise technique was opposed to essential quality, as though one could paint well something that was bad.", "그는 심지어 칭찬을 할 때조차 기법이 본질적 가치와 대립되곤 한다는 것을, 마치 형편없는 무언가를 잘 그릴 수도 있다는 듯이 여겨진다는 것을 종종 알아챘다."], ["He knew that a great deal of attention and care was necessary in taking off the coverings, to avoid injuring the creation itself, and to take off all the coverings; but there was no art of painting—no technique of any sort—about it.", "그는 덮개를 벗겨 내는 데 많은 주의와 정성이 필요하다는 것을, 창작물 자체를 손상시키지 않으면서 모든 덮개를 다 벗겨 내야 한다는 것을 알고 있었다. 그러나 거기에 그림을 그리는 기술이란—그 어떤 기법이란—없었다."], ["If to a little child or to his cook were revealed what he saw, it or she would have been able to peel the wrappings off what was seen.", "만약 어린아이나 자기 집 요리사에게 자기가 본 것이 드러난다면, 그 아이나 그 여자도 본 것에서 포장을 벗겨 낼 수 있을 것이었다."], ["And the most experienced and adroit painter could not by mere mechanical facility paint anything if the lines of the subject were not revealed to him first.", "그리고 가장 노련하고 솜씨 좋은 화가라도 주제의 선들이 먼저 자기에게 드러나지 않는다면 단순한 기계적 숙련만으로는 아무것도 그릴 수 없었다."], ["Besides, he saw that if it came to talking about technique, it was impossible to praise him for it.", "게다가 그는, 기법을 이야기하는 거라면 그 점에서 자기를 칭찬하기란 불가능하다는 것을 알았다."], ["In all he had painted and repainted he saw faults that hurt his eyes, coming from want of care in taking off the wrappings—faults he could not correct now without spoiling the whole.", "자기가 그리고 또 그린 모든 것에서 그는 눈에 거슬리는 결함들을, 포장을 벗겨 낼 때 정성이 부족해서 생긴, 이제 와서는 전체를 망치지 않고서는 고칠 수 없는 결함들을 보았다."], ["And in almost all the figures and faces he saw, too, remnants of the wrappings not perfectly removed that spoiled the picture.", "그리고 거의 모든 형상과 얼굴에서 그는 또한 완전히 제거되지 못해 그림을 망치는 포장의 잔재를 보았다."]], [["“One thing might be said, if you will allow me to make the remark....”", "\"한 가지 말씀드리고 싶은 게 있는데요, 한마디 해도 좋다면…….\""], ["observed Golenishtchev.", "골레니셰프가 의견을 냈다."]], [["“Oh, I shall be delighted, I beg you,”", "\"오, 기꺼이 듣겠습니다, 어서 말씀하세요.\""], ["said Mihailov with a forced smile.", "미하일로프가 억지웃음을 지으며 말했다."]], [["“That is, that you make Him the man-god, and not the God-man.", "\"그러니까, 당신은 그분을 신인(神人)이 아니라 인신(人神)으로 그렸다는 겁니다."], ["But I know that was what you meant to do.”", "하지만 그게 바로 당신이 의도한 바라는 것은 압니다.\""]], [["“I cannot paint a Christ that is not in my heart,”", "\"제 마음속에 없는 그리스도는 그릴 수 없습니다.\""], ["said Mihailov gloomily.", "미하일로프가 침울하게 말했다."]], [["“Yes; but in that case, if you will allow me to say what I think....", "\"그래요. 하지만 그렇다면, 제 생각을 말해도 좋다면……"], ["Your picture is so fine that my observation cannot detract from it, and, besides, it is only my personal opinion.", "당신 그림은 너무나 훌륭해서 제 견해 따위가 그것을 깎아내릴 수는 없고, 게다가 이건 제 개인적인 의견일 뿐입니다."], ["With you it is different.", "당신은 다르지요."], ["Your very motive is different.", "당신의 동기 자체가 다릅니다."], ["But let us take Ivanov.", "하지만 이바노프를 예로 들어 봅시다."], ["I imagine that if Christ is brought down to the level of an historical character, it would have been better for Ivanov to select some other historical subject, fresh, untouched.”", "제 생각에는, 만약 그리스도가 역사적 인물의 수준으로 끌어내려지는 거라면, 이바노프로서는 다른 어떤 역사적 주제를, 신선하고 손대지 않은 주제를 택하는 편이 더 나았을 겁니다.\""]], [["“But if this is the greatest subject presented to art?”", "\"하지만 이것이 예술에 주어진 가장 위대한 주제라면요?\""]], [["“If one looked one would find others.", "\"찾아본다면 다른 것들도 찾을 수 있겠지요."], ["But the point is that art cannot suffer doubt and discussion.", "하지만 요점은 예술이 의심과 논의를 견뎌 낼 수 없다는 겁니다."], ["And before the picture of Ivanov the question arises for the believer and the unbeliever alike, ‘Is it God, or is it not God?’ and the unity of the impression is destroyed.”", "그런데 이바노프의 그림 앞에서는 신자에게나 불신자에게나 똑같이 '저것이 신인가, 아니면 신이 아닌가?' 하는 의문이 일어나고, 그러면 인상의 통일성이 깨져 버립니다.\""]], [["“Why so?", "\"어째서 그렇지요?"], ["I think that for educated people,”", "제 생각에 교양 있는 사람에게는,\""], ["said Mihailov, “the question cannot exist.”", "미하일로프가 말했다, \"그런 의문이 있을 수 없습니다.\""]], [["Golenishtchev did not agree with this, and confounded Mihailov by his support of his first idea of the unity of the impression being essential to art.", "골레니셰프는 이에 동의하지 않았고, 인상의 통일성이 예술에 필수적이라는 자신의 첫 견해를 거듭 옹호하여 미하일로프를 당혹스럽게 했다."]], [["Mihailov was greatly perturbed, but he could say nothing in defense of his own idea.", "미하일로프는 몹시 마음이 어지러웠지만, 자기 견해를 옹호하여 아무 말도 할 수 없었다."]], [["# Chapter 12", "# Chapter 12"]], [["Anna and Vronsky had long been exchanging glances, regretting their friend’s flow of cleverness.", "안나와 브론스키는 벌써부터 서로 눈짓을 주고받으며, 친구가 늘어놓는 그 영리한 말들의 흐름을 안타까워하고 있었다."], ["At last Vronsky, without waiting for the artist, walked away to another small picture.", "마침내 브론스키는 화가를 기다리지도 않고 다른 작은 그림 쪽으로 걸어가 버렸다."]], [["“Oh, how exquisite!", "\"오, 얼마나 절묘한지!"], ["What a lovely thing!", "정말 사랑스러운 작품이군!"], ["A gem!", "보석 같아!"], ["How exquisite!”", "어쩌면 이렇게 절묘할까!\""], ["they cried with one voice.", "두 사람이 한목소리로 외쳤다."]], [["“What is it they’re so pleased with?”", "'대체 뭐가 저렇게 좋다는 거지?'"], ["thought Mihailov.", "미하일로프는 생각했다."], ["He had positively forgotten that picture he had painted three years ago.", "그는 삼 년 전에 그린 그 그림을 완전히 잊고 있었다."], ["He had forgotten all the agonies and the ecstasies he had lived through with that picture when for several months it had been the one thought haunting him day and night.", "몇 달 동안 밤낮으로 그를 사로잡았던 단 하나의 생각이었을 때, 그 그림과 함께 겪었던 온갖 고뇌와 환희를 그는 깡그리 잊고 있었던 것이다."], ["He had forgotten, as he always forgot, the pictures he had finished.", "늘 그래 왔듯이, 그는 완성한 그림들을 잊어버렸다."], ["He did not even like to look at it, and had only brought it out because he was expecting an Englishman who wanted to buy it.", "그는 그 그림을 들여다보는 것조차 좋아하지 않았으며, 그것을 사고 싶어 하는 어느 영국인이 오기를 기다리고 있었기에 마지못해 꺼내 두었을 따름이었다."]], [["“Oh, that’s only an old study,”", "\"아, 그건 그냥 오래된 습작입니다.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“How fine!”", "\"얼마나 훌륭한지!\""], ["said Golenishtchev, he too, with unmistakable sincerity, falling under the spell of the picture.", "골레니셰프도 의심할 여지 없이 진지한 태도로, 역시 그림의 마력에 사로잡혀 말했다."]], [["Two boys were angling in the shade of a willow-tree.", "버드나무 그늘 아래에서 두 소년이 낚시를 하고 있었다."], ["The elder had just dropped in the hook, and was carefully pulling the float from behind a bush, entirely absorbed in what he was doing.", "형으로 보이는 아이는 막 낚싯바늘을 던져 넣고, 자기 일에 온통 몰두한 채 덤불 뒤에서 조심스럽게 찌를 끌어당기고 있었다."], ["The other, a little younger, was lying in the grass leaning on his elbows, with his tangled, flaxen head in his hands, staring at the water with his dreamy blue eyes.", "다른 아이는, 형보다 조금 어려 보이는데, 풀밭에 엎드려 팔꿈치를 괴고, 헝클어진 아마빛 머리를 두 손에 받친 채, 꿈꾸는 듯한 파란 눈으로 물을 바라보고 있었다."], ["What was he thinking of?", "무슨 생각을 하고 있던 것일까?"]], [["The enthusiasm over this picture stirred some of the old feeling for it in Mihailov, but he feared and disliked this waste of feeling for things past, and so, even though this praise was grateful to him, he tried to draw his visitors away to a third picture.", "이 그림에 쏟아지는 열광은 미하일로프의 마음속에 그 그림에 대한 옛 감정을 얼마쯤 흔들어 깨웠지만, 그는 지난 일에 대한 감정을 이렇게 헛되이 소모하는 것을 두려워하고 또 싫어했기에, 비록 그 칭찬이 고맙기는 했어도 손님들을 세 번째 그림 쪽으로 이끌어 가려고 애썼다."]], [["But Vronsky asked whether the picture was for sale.", "그러나 브론스키는 그 그림이 파는 것이냐고 물었다."], ["To Mihailov at that moment, excited by visitors, it was extremely distasteful to speak of money matters.", "손님들 때문에 들떠 있던 미하일로프에게, 그 순간 돈 이야기를 하는 것은 더없이 거북한 일이었다."]], [["“It is put up there to be sold,”", "\"팔려고 거기 내놓은 겁니다.\""], ["he answered, scowling gloomily.", "그는 음울하게 미간을 찌푸리며 대답했다."]], [["When the visitors had gone, Mihailov sat down opposite the picture of Pilate and Christ, and in his mind went over what had been said, and what, though not said, had been implied by those visitors.", "손님들이 떠나자 미하일로프는 빌라도와 그리스도를 그린 그림 맞은편에 앉아, 그들이 했던 말과, 입 밖에 내지는 않았으나 그 손님들이 은연중에 내비친 것들을 머릿속으로 되짚어 보았다."], ["And, strange to say, what had had such weight with him, while they were there and while he mentally put himself at their point of view, suddenly lost all importance for him.", "그런데 이상하게도, 그들이 거기 있는 동안, 그리고 그가 마음속으로 그들의 관점에 자기를 세워 두고 있는 동안 그토록 무게를 지녔던 것들이, 별안간 그에게는 아무 의미도 없는 것이 되어 버렸다."], ["He began to look at his picture with all his own full artist vision, and was soon in that mood of conviction of the perfectibility, and so of the significance, of his picture—a conviction essential to the most intense fervor, excluding all other interests—in which alone he could work.", "그는 자기만의 온전한 화가의 눈으로 그림을 바라보기 시작했고, 이내 자기 그림이 완성에 이를 수 있다는, 그리하여 의미를 지닌다는 그 확신의 상태에 빠져들었다. 그것은 다른 모든 관심사를 배제하는 가장 격렬한 열정에 꼭 필요한 확신이었으며, 오직 그 안에서만 그는 일할 수 있었다."]], [["Christ’s foreshortened leg was not right, though.", "그렇지만 단축법으로 그린 그리스도의 다리가 어딘지 잘못되어 있었다."], ["He took his palette and began to work.", "그는 팔레트를 집어 들고 작업을 시작했다."], ["As he corrected the leg he looked continually at the figure of John in the background, which his visitors had not even noticed, but which he knew was beyond perfection.", "다리를 고치면서 그는 배경에 있는 요한의 모습을 끊임없이 바라보았는데, 손님들은 그것을 눈여겨보지도 않았으나 그는 그것이 완성의 경지를 넘어섰음을 알고 있었다."], ["When he had finished the leg he wanted to touch that figure, but he felt too much excited for it.", "다리를 다 손보고 나자 그는 그 모습에 손을 대고 싶었으나, 그러기에는 마음이 너무 들떠 있음을 느꼈다."], ["He was equally unable to work when he was cold and when he was too much affected and saw everything too much.", "그는 차갑게 식어 있을 때나, 너무 깊이 감정에 휩쓸려 모든 것이 지나치게 또렷이 보일 때나 똑같이 일을 할 수가 없었다."], ["There was only one stage in the transition from coldness to inspiration, at which work was possible.", "차가움에서 영감으로 옮겨 가는 과정에는 작업이 가능한 단 하나의 단계가 있을 뿐이었다."], ["Today he was too much agitated.", "오늘은 너무 들떠 있었다."], ["He would have covered the picture, but he stopped, holding the cloth in his hand, and, smiling blissfully, gazed a long while at the figure of John.", "그는 그림을 덮어 버리려다가 천을 손에 쥔 채 멈춰 서서, 더없이 행복하게 미소 지으며 오랫동안 요한의 모습을 바라보았다."], ["At last, as it were regretfully tearing himself away, he dropped the cloth, and, exhausted but happy, went home.", "마침내 아쉬워하듯 몸을 떼어 내며 천을 떨어뜨리고는, 지쳤으나 행복한 마음으로 집으로 돌아갔다."]], [["Vronsky, Anna, and Golenishtchev, on their way home, were particularly lively and cheerful.", "브론스키와 안나, 골레니셰프는 집으로 돌아가는 길에 유난히 활기차고 명랑했다."], ["They talked of Mihailov and his pictures.", "그들은 미하일로프와 그의 그림들에 대해 이야기했다."], ["The word talent, by which they meant an inborn, almost physical, aptitude apart from brain and heart, and in which they tried to find an expression for all the artist had gained from life, recurred particularly often in their talk, as though it were necessary for them to sum up what they had no conception of, though they wanted to talk of it.", "talent라는 말이 그들의 대화에 유독 자주 되풀이되었는데, 그들은 그것으로 머리나 가슴과는 별개인, 타고난, 거의 육체적이라 할 소질을 뜻했고, 그 말 속에서 그 화가가 삶으로부터 얻은 모든 것을 표현해 보려 했다. 마치 자기들이 그것에 대해 이야기하고 싶어 하면서도 정작 아무런 개념도 갖고 있지 못한 그 무언가를 어떻게든 정리해야 한다는 듯이 말이다."], ["They said that there was no denying his talent, but that his talent could not develop for want of education—the common defect of our Russian artists.", "그들은 그의 재능을 부인할 수 없으나, 그 재능이 교양의 부족 탓에 꽃피우지 못한다고, 그것이 우리 러시아 화가들에게 흔한 결함이라고 말했다."], ["But the picture of the boys had imprinted itself on their memories, and they were continually coming back to it.", "그러나 두 소년을 그린 그림은 그들의 기억 속에 또렷이 새겨져, 그들은 끊임없이 그 그림으로 되돌아왔다."], ["“What an exquisite thing!", "\"정말 절묘한 작품이야!"], ["How he has succeeded in it, and how simply!", "그 사람이 어쩌면 그렇게, 또 그토록 단순하게 그걸 해냈을까!"], ["He doesn’t even comprehend how good it is.", "그 사람은 그게 얼마나 훌륭한지 알지도 못한다니까. 그래, 이 기회를 놓쳐선 안 돼."], ["Yes, I mustn’t let it slip; I must buy it,”", "꼭 사야겠어.\" 브론스키가 말했다."], ["said Vronsky.", "* talent: 영어로 '재능'."]], [["# Chapter 13", "# Chapter 13"]], [["Mihailov sold Vronsky his picture, and agreed to paint a portrait of Anna.", "미하일로프는 브론스키에게 그림을 팔았고, 안나의 초상화를 그리는 데에도 동의했다."], ["On the day fixed he came and began the work.", "정해진 날에 그는 와서 작업을 시작했다."]], [["From the fifth sitting the portrait impressed everyone, especially Vronsky, not only by its resemblance, but by its characteristic beauty.", "다섯 번째 포즈를 취한 자리부터 그 초상화는 모든 사람에게, 특히 브론스키에게 깊은 인상을 주었는데, 닮은 것뿐 아니라 그녀 특유의 아름다움 때문이었다."], ["It was strange how Mihailov could have discovered just her characteristic beauty.", "미하일로프가 어떻게 바로 그녀 특유의 아름다움을 찾아낼 수 있었는지 신기할 따름이었다."], ["“One needs to know and love her as I have loved her to discover the very sweetest expression of her soul,”", "\"그녀 영혼의 가장 사랑스러운 표정을 찾아내려면, 내가 그녀를 사랑해 온 것처럼 그녀를 알고 사랑해야만 해.\""], ["Vronsky thought, though it was only from this portrait that he had himself learned this sweetest expression of her soul.", "브론스키는 그렇게 생각했지만, 정작 그 자신이 그녀 영혼의 가장 사랑스러운 표정을 알게 된 것도 다름 아닌 이 초상화를 통해서였다."], ["But the expression was so true that he, and others too, fancied they had long known it.", "그러나 그 표정이 어찌나 진실했던지, 그도 다른 사람들도 그것을 오래전부터 알고 있었다고 여길 정도였다."]], [["“I have been struggling on for ever so long without doing anything,”", "\"나는 끝없이 오랫동안 끙끙대면서도 아무것도 해내지 못했는데,\""], ["he said of his own portrait of her, “and he just looked and painted it.", "그는 자기가 그린 그녀의 초상화를 두고 말했다. \"저 사람은 그저 한번 보고는 그려 버렸어."], ["That’s where technique comes in.”", "바로 거기서 기법이 빛을 발하는 거지.\""]], [["“That will come,”", "\"그것도 차차 갖춰질 겁니다.\""], ["was the consoling reassurance given him by Golenishtchev, in whose view Vronsky had both talent, and what was most important, culture, giving him a wider outlook on art.", "골레니셰프가 위로하며 안심시키듯 그에게 말했다. 그가 보기에 브론스키에게는 재능이 있었고, 무엇보다 중요한 것으로, 예술에 대한 더 넓은 안목을 갖게 해 주는 교양이 있었다."], ["Golenishtchev’s faith in Vronsky’s talent was propped up by his own need of Vronsky’s sympathy and approval for his own articles and ideas, and he felt that the praise and support must be mutual.", "브론스키의 재능에 대한 골레니셰프의 믿음은, 자기 자신의 글과 사상에 대한 브론스키의 공감과 인정을 바라는 그 자신의 필요에 떠받쳐지고 있었고, 그는 칭찬과 지지가 서로 주고받는 것이어야 한다고 느꼈다."]], [["In another man’s house, and especially in Vronsky’s palazzo, Mihailov was quite a different man from what he was in his studio.", "남의 집에서, 특히 브론스키의 palazzo에서 미하일로프는 자기 화실에 있을 때와는 완전히 다른 사람이었다."], ["He behaved with hostile courtesy, as though he were afraid of coming closer to people he did not respect.", "그는 적의 어린 예의를 갖추고 행동했는데, 마치 자기가 존경하지 않는 사람들에게 가까이 다가가기를 두려워하는 듯했다."], ["He called Vronsky “your excellency,” and notwithstanding Anna’s and Vronsky’s invitations, he would never stay to dinner, nor come except for the sittings.", "그는 브론스키를 \"각하\"라고 불렀고, 안나와 브론스키가 아무리 청해도 결코 만찬에 머무는 법이 없었으며, 포즈를 취하는 일이 아니고는 오지 않았다."], ["Anna was even more friendly to him than to other people, and was very grateful for her portrait.", "안나는 다른 사람들에게보다 그에게 더 다정하게 대했고, 자기 초상화에 대해 무척 고마워했다."], ["Vronsky was more than cordial with him, and was obviously interested to know the artist’s opinion of his picture.", "브론스키는 그에게 더없이 살가웠고, 화가가 자기 그림을 어떻게 보는지 알고 싶어 하는 기색이 역력했다."], ["Golenishtchev never let slip an opportunity of instilling sound ideas about art into Mihailov.", "골레니셰프는 미하일로프에게 예술에 관한 건전한 견해를 불어넣을 기회를 결코 놓치지 않았다."], ["But Mihailov remained equally chilly to all of them.", "그러나 미하일로프는 그들 모두에게 한결같이 쌀쌀했다."], ["Anna was aware from his eyes that he liked looking at her, but he avoided conversation with her.", "안나는 그가 자기를 바라보기를 좋아한다는 것을 그의 눈에서 알아챘지만, 그는 그녀와 대화하는 것을 피했다."], ["Vronsky’s talk about his painting he met with stubborn silence, and he was as stubbornly silent when he was shown Vronsky’s picture.", "브론스키가 자기 그림에 대해 늘어놓는 말에 그는 고집스러운 침묵으로 응했고, 브론스키의 그림을 보여 주었을 때도 똑같이 고집스럽게 침묵했다."], ["He was unmistakably bored by Golenishtchev’s conversation, and he did not attempt to oppose him.", "그는 골레니셰프의 이야기에 분명히 따분해했지만, 그에게 반박하려고 하지는 않았다."]], [["Altogether Mihailov, with his reserved and disagreeable, as it were, hostile attitude, was quite disliked by them as they got to know him better; and they were glad when the sittings were over, and they were left with a magnificent portrait in their possession, and he gave up coming.", "요컨대 미하일로프는, 마음을 닫은 채 못마땅해하는, 말하자면 적의 어린 태도 탓에, 그들이 그를 더 잘 알게 될수록 영 미움을 샀다. 그래서 그들은 포즈를 취하는 일이 다 끝나고 멋진 초상화를 손에 넣게 되어 그가 더는 찾아오지 않게 되자 흐뭇해했다."], ["Golenishtchev was the first to give expression to an idea that had occurred to all of them, which was that Mihailov was simply jealous of Vronsky.", "그들 모두에게 떠올랐던 한 가지 생각을 처음으로 입 밖에 낸 사람은 골레니셰프였는데, 그것은 미하일로프가 그저 브론스키를 질투하고 있을 뿐이라는 것이었다."]], [["“Not envious, let us say, since he has talent; but it annoys him that a wealthy man of the highest society, and a count, too (you know they all detest a title), can, without any particular trouble, do as well, if not better, than he who has devoted all his life to it.", "\"부러워한다고는 하지 맙시다, 그에게는 재능이 있으니까요. 다만 거슬리는 거지요."], ["And more than all, it’s a question of culture, which he is without.”", "최상류 사교계의 부유한 사람이, 게다가 백작이기까지 한 사람이(아시다시피 저들은 작위라면 다들 질색하잖습니까) 별다른 수고도 들이지 않고, 평생을 그 일에 바쳐 온 자기만큼은, 아니 어쩌면 자기보다 더 잘해 낼 수 있다는 게 말입니다. 그리고 무엇보다도, 그건 교양의 문제인데, 저 사람에게는 그게 없거든요.\""]], [["Vronsky defended Mihailov, but at the bottom of his heart he believed it, because in his view a man of a different, lower world would be sure to be envious.", "브론스키는 미하일로프를 두둔했지만, 마음 깊은 곳에서는 그 말을 믿었다. 그가 보기에 다른 세계, 더 낮은 세계에 속한 사람이라면 틀림없이 부러워할 게 분명했으니까."]], [["Anna’s portrait—the same subject painted from nature both by him and by Mihailov—ought to have shown Vronsky the difference between him and Mihailov; but he did not see it.", "안나의 초상화—그와 미하일로프가 둘 다 실물을 보고 그린 똑같은 대상—는 브론스키에게 자기와 미하일로프 사이의 차이를 보여 주었어야 마땅했으나, 그는 그것을 보지 못했다."], ["Only after Mihailov’s portrait was painted he left off painting his portrait of Anna, deciding that it was now not needed.", "다만 미하일로프의 초상화가 완성되고 나서, 그는 이제 더는 필요 없다고 판단하고 안나의 초상화를 그리는 일을 그만두었다."], ["His picture of mediæval life he went on with.", "중세 생활을 담은 그의 그림은 계속 그려 나갔다."], ["And he himself, and Golenishtchev, and still more Anna, thought it very good, because it was far more like the celebrated pictures they knew than Mihailov’s picture.", "그리고 그 자신도, 골레니셰프도, 그리고 안나는 더더욱, 그것을 아주 훌륭하다고 여겼는데, 그것이 미하일로프의 그림보다 자기들이 아는 그 유명한 그림들과 훨씬 더 비슷했기 때문이다."]], [["Mihailov meanwhile, although Anna’s portrait greatly fascinated him, was even more glad than they were when the sittings were over, and he had no longer to listen to Golenishtchev’s disquisitions upon art, and could forget about Vronsky’s painting.", "한편 미하일로프는, 안나의 초상화가 그를 크게 매료시키긴 했지만, 포즈를 취하는 일이 끝나고 더는 예술에 관한 골레니셰프의 일장연설을 듣지 않아도 되고 브론스키의 그림에 대해 잊어버릴 수 있게 되자, 그들보다 한층 더 기뻐했다."], ["He knew that Vronsky could not be prevented from amusing himself with painting; he knew that he and all dilettanti had a perfect right to paint what they liked, but it was distasteful to him.", "그는 브론스키가 그림으로 소일하는 것을 막을 수 없다는 것을 알았고, 브론스키도 모든 호사가도 자기가 좋아하는 것을 그릴 완전한 권리가 있다는 것을 알았지만, 그것이 그에게는 거슬렸다."], ["A man could not be prevented from making himself a big wax doll, and kissing it.", "사람이 자기 손으로 커다란 밀랍 인형을 만들어 거기에 입을 맞추는 것을 막을 수는 없는 노릇이다."], ["But if the man were to come with the doll and sit before a man in love, and begin caressing his doll as the lover caressed the woman he loved, it would be distasteful to the lover.", "그러나 그 사람이 그 인형을 들고 와서 사랑에 빠진 남자 앞에 앉아, 사랑하는 남자가 사랑하는 여자를 어루만지듯 자기 인형을 어루만지기 시작한다면, 그것은 사랑에 빠진 남자에게 거슬리는 일일 것이다."], ["Just such a distasteful sensation was what Mihailov felt at the sight of Vronsky’s painting: he felt it both ludicrous and irritating, both pitiable and offensive.", "미하일로프가 브론스키의 그림을 보면서 느낀 것이 바로 그런 거슬리는 감각이었다. 그는 그것을 우습기도 하고 짜증스럽기도 하며, 가엾기도 하고 모욕적이기도 하다고 느꼈다."]], [["Vronsky’s interest in painting and the Middle Ages did not last long.", "회화와 중세에 대한 브론스키의 관심은 오래가지 못했다."], ["He had enough taste for painting to be unable to finish his picture.", "그는 자기 그림을 끝내지 못할 만큼은 회화에 대한 안목을 가지고 있었다."], ["The picture came to a standstill.", "그림은 멈춰 서고 말았다."], ["He was vaguely aware that its defects, inconspicuous at first, would be glaring if he were to go on with it.", "그는 처음에는 눈에 띄지 않던 그 결함들이, 만약 계속 그려 나간다면 두드러지게 드러나리라는 것을 어렴풋이 알고 있었다."], ["The same experience befell him as Golenishtchev, who felt that he had nothing to say, and continually deceived himself with the theory that his idea was not yet mature, that he was working it out and collecting materials.", "그에게도 골레니셰프와 똑같은 일이 닥친 셈이었는데, 골레니셰프는 자기에게 할 말이 없다는 것을 느끼면서도, 자기 사상이 아직 무르익지 않았을 뿐이며 그것을 다듬어 가며 자료를 모으는 중이라는 이론으로 끊임없이 자신을 속이고 있었다."], ["This exasperated and tortured Golenishtchev, but Vronsky was incapable of deceiving and torturing himself, and even more incapable of exasperation.", "이것이 골레니셰프를 화나게 하고 괴롭혔지만, 브론스키는 자기 자신을 속이거나 괴롭힐 줄 모르는 사람이었고, 화를 내는 것은 더더욱 할 줄 몰랐다."], ["With his characteristic decision, without explanation or apology, he simply ceased working at painting.", "그는 그 특유의 결단력으로, 아무런 해명도 변명도 없이, 그저 그림 작업을 그만두어 버렸다."]], [["But without this occupation, the life of Vronsky and of Anna, who wondered at his loss of interest in it, struck them as intolerably tedious in an Italian town.", "그러나 이 일거리가 없어지자, 브론스키의 삶도, 그가 그림에 흥미를 잃은 것을 의아해하던 안나의 삶도, 이탈리아의 한 도시에서 견디기 힘들 만큼 따분하게 느껴졌다."], ["The palazzo suddenly seemed so obtrusively old and dirty, the spots on the curtains, the cracks in the floors, the broken plaster on the cornices became so disagreeably obvious, and the everlasting sameness of Golenishtchev, and the Italian professor and the German traveler became so wearisome, that they had to make some change.", "그 palazzo는 갑자기 거슬리도록 낡고 지저분해 보였고, 커튼의 얼룩이며 마룻바닥의 갈라진 틈이며 처마 돌림띠의 부서진 회반죽이 어찌나 보기 싫게 도드라지는지, 골레니셰프의 한결같은 똑같음이며 이탈리아인 교수와 독일인 여행자가 어찌나 진력나는지, 그들은 뭔가 변화를 주지 않을 수 없었다."], ["They resolved to go to Russia, to the country.", "그들은 러시아로, 시골로 가기로 결심했다."], ["In Petersburg Vronsky intended to arrange a partition of the land with his brother, while Anna meant to see her son.", "페테르부르크에서 브론스키는 형과 땅을 나누는 일을 처리할 작정이었고, 안나는 아들을 만날 생각이었다."], ["The summer they intended to spend on Vronsky’s great family estate.", "여름은 브론스키의 큰 영지에서 보낼 생각이었다."]], [["# Chapter 14", "# Chapter 14"]], [["Levin had been married three months.", "레빈은 결혼한 지 석 달이 되었다."], ["He was happy, but not at all in the way he had expected to be.", "그는 행복했지만, 결코 그가 기대했던 방식으로는 아니었다."], ["At every step he found his former dreams disappointed, and new, unexpected surprises of happiness.", "발걸음을 옮길 때마다 그는 예전의 꿈들이 어그러지는 것을 발견했고, 또 행복의 새롭고 뜻하지 않은 놀라움들을 발견했다."], ["He was happy; but on entering upon family life he saw at every step that it was utterly different from what he had imagined.", "그는 행복했다. 그러나 가정생활에 들어서면서 발걸음을 옮길 때마다 그것이 자기가 상상했던 것과는 완전히 다르다는 것을 깨달았다."], ["At every step he experienced what a man would experience who, after admiring the smooth, happy course of a little boat on a lake, should get himself into that little boat.", "발걸음을 옮길 때마다 그는, 호수 위를 미끄러지듯 행복하게 나아가는 작은 배를 감탄하며 바라보다가 막상 그 작은 배에 올라탄 사람이 겪을 법한 것을 겪었다."], ["He saw that it was not all sitting still, floating smoothly; that one had to think too, not for an instant to forget where one was floating; and that there was water under one, and that one must row; and that his unaccustomed hands would be sore; and that it was only to look at it that was easy; but that doing it, though very delightful, was very difficult.", "그는 가만히 앉아 매끄럽게 떠가기만 하면 되는 게 아니라는 것을 알게 되었다. 생각도 해야 하고, 자기가 어디로 떠가고 있는지 한순간도 잊어서는 안 되며, 발밑에는 물이 있고, 노를 저어야 하고, 익숙지 않은 손은 쓰라릴 것이며, 그저 바라보기만 하는 게 쉬웠을 뿐, 막상 그것을 하는 일은 무척 즐겁기는 해도 무척 어렵다는 것을 알게 되었다."]], [["As a bachelor, when he had watched other people’s married life, seen the petty cares, the squabbles, the jealousy, he had only smiled contemptuously in his heart.", "총각 시절, 다른 사람들의 결혼 생활을 지켜보며 그 자질구레한 근심거리며 다툼이며 질투를 보았을 때, 그는 마음속으로 그저 경멸하듯 비웃었을 뿐이었다."], ["In his future married life there could be, he was convinced, nothing of that sort; even the external forms, indeed, he fancied, must be utterly unlike the life of others in everything.", "그는 자기의 앞으로의 결혼 생활에는 그런 것이라곤 결코 있을 수 없으리라고 확신했다. 게다가 외적인 형식조차도 모든 면에서 다른 사람들의 삶과는 완전히 다를 것이라고 그는 상상했다."], ["And all of a sudden, instead of his life with his wife being made on an individual pattern, it was, on the contrary, entirely made up of the pettiest details, which he had so despised before, but which now, by no will of his own, had gained an extraordinary importance that it was useless to contend against.", "그런데 별안간, 아내와의 삶이 어떤 독자적인 양식으로 이루어지기는커녕, 오히려 전에 그가 그토록 멸시했던 바로 그 자질구레하기 그지없는 세부적인 일들로 온통 이루어지고 말았다. 그리고 그 세부적인 일들은 이제, 그의 의지와는 아무 상관없이, 맞서 싸워 봐야 소용없는 엄청난 중요성을 띠게 되었다."], ["And Levin saw that the organization of all these details was by no means so easy as he had fancied before.", "그리고 레빈은 이 모든 세부적인 일들을 꾸려 나가는 것이 자기가 전에 상상했던 것처럼 결코 쉽지 않다는 것을 알게 되었다."], ["Although Levin believed himself to have the most exact conceptions of domestic life, unconsciously, like all men, he pictured domestic life as the happiest enjoyment of love, with nothing to hinder and no petty cares to distract.", "레빈은 자기가 가정생활에 대해 더없이 정확한 관념을 지니고 있다고 믿었지만, 모든 남자가 그렇듯, 그도 무의식중에 가정생활을 방해하는 것도 없고 마음을 흩뜨리는 자질구레한 근심거리도 없이 사랑을 가장 행복하게 누리는 것으로 그려 보았던 것이다."], ["He ought, as he conceived the position, to do his work, and to find repose from it in the happiness of love.", "그가 그 처지를 머릿속에 그린 바로는, 그는 자기 일을 하고 사랑의 행복 속에서 그 일로부터의 안식을 찾아야 했다."], ["She ought to be beloved, and nothing more.", "아내는 사랑받아야 하고, 그것이면 충분했다."], ["But, like all men, he forgot that she too would want work.", "그러나 모든 남자가 그렇듯, 그는 아내도 일을 원하리라는 것을 잊고 있었다."], ["And he was surprised that she, his poetic, exquisite Kitty, could, not merely in the first weeks, but even in the first days of their married life, think, remember, and busy herself about tablecloths, and furniture, about mattresses for visitors, about a tray, about the cook, and the dinner, and so on.", "그래서 그는 자기의 시적이고 우아한 키티가, 결혼 생활의 첫 몇 주뿐 아니라 첫 며칠부터도, 식탁보며 가구며 손님용 매트리스며 쟁반이며 요리사며 저녁 식사며 따위에 대해 생각하고 기억하고 부산을 떨 수 있다는 데에 놀랐다."], ["While they were still engaged, he had been struck by the definiteness with which she had declined the tour abroad and decided to go into the country, as though she knew of something she wanted, and could still think of something outside her love.", "아직 약혼 중이었을 때에도, 그녀가 마치 자기가 무엇을 원하는지 알고 있고 또 사랑 바깥의 무언가를 여전히 생각할 수 있기라도 한 것처럼, 외국 여행을 단호하게 거절하고 시골로 가기로 결정한 그 명확함에 그는 충격을 받았었다."], ["This had jarred upon him then, and now her trivial cares and anxieties jarred upon him several times.", "그때도 이 일이 그의 마음을 거슬렀었는데, 이제는 그녀의 사소한 근심과 걱정들이 여러 차례 그의 마음을 거슬렀다."], ["But he saw that this was essential for her.", "그러나 그는 이것이 그녀에게 꼭 필요한 일임을 알게 되었다."], ["And, loving her as he did, though he did not understand the reason of them, and jeered at these domestic pursuits, he could not help admiring them.", "그리고 그는 그녀를 사랑했으므로, 비록 그 까닭을 이해하지는 못하고 이 살림살이에 매달리는 일을 비웃기는 했어도, 그것에 감탄하지 않을 수 없었다."], ["He jeered at the way in which she arranged the furniture they had brought from Moscow; rearranged their room; hung up curtains; prepared rooms for visitors; a room for Dolly; saw after an abode for her new maid; ordered dinner of the old cook; came into collision with Agafea Mihalovna, taking from her the charge of the stores.", "그는 그녀가 모스크바에서 가져온 가구를 배치하는 방식이며, 방을 새로 꾸미고, 커튼을 달고, 손님용 방을 마련하고, 돌리를 위한 방을 준비하고, 자기의 새 하녀가 머물 곳을 돌보고, 늙은 요리사에게 저녁 식사를 주문하고, 아가피야 미하일로브나에게서 식료품 관리를 넘겨받으려고 그녀와 부딪치는 모습을 비웃었다."], ["He saw how the old cook smiled, admiring her, and listening to her inexperienced, impossible orders, how mournfully and tenderly Agafea Mihalovna shook her head over the young mistress’s new arrangements.", "그는 늙은 요리사가 그녀에게 감탄하며 미소를 짓고 그녀의 미숙하고 터무니없는 지시에 귀 기울이는 모습을, 아가피야 미하일로브나가 젊은 안주인의 새로운 방식을 두고 얼마나 구슬프고 다정하게 고개를 젓는지를 보았다."], ["He saw that Kitty was extraordinarily sweet when, laughing and crying, she came to tell him that her maid, Masha, was used to looking upon her as her young lady, and so no one obeyed her.", "그는 키티가 웃다 울다 하며 자기의 하녀 마샤가 그녀를 여전히 아가씨로 여기는 데에 익숙해서 아무도 자기 말을 따르지 않는다고 그에게 하소연하러 왔을 때 그녀가 유난히 사랑스럽다고 느꼈다."], ["It seemed to him sweet, but strange, and he thought it would have been better without this.", "그것이 그에게는 사랑스럽게, 그러나 묘하게 여겨졌고, 그는 이런 일이 없었더라면 더 좋았을 거라고 생각했다."]], [["He did not know how great a sense of change she was experiencing; she, who at home had sometimes wanted some favorite dish, or sweets, without the possibility of getting either, now could order what she liked, buy pounds of sweets, spend as much money as she liked, and order any puddings she pleased.", "그는 그녀가 얼마나 큰 변화를 겪고 있는지 알지 못했다. 친정에 있을 때는 때때로 좋아하는 음식이나 단것이 먹고 싶어도 그 어느 것도 손에 넣을 수 없었던 그녀가, 이제는 마음에 드는 것을 주문할 수 있고, 단것을 몇 파운드씩 사들이고, 원하는 만큼 돈을 쓰고, 좋아하는 푸딩이라면 무엇이든 주문할 수 있게 된 것이다."]], [["She was dreaming with delight now of Dolly’s coming to them with her children, especially because she would order for the children their favorite puddings and Dolly would appreciate all her new housekeeping.", "그녀는 이제 돌리가 아이들을 데리고 자기들 집에 올 것을 기쁨에 차서 꿈꾸고 있었는데, 특히 아이들에게 그 아이들이 좋아하는 푸딩을 주문해 줄 수 있고 돌리가 자기의 새로운 살림살이를 모두 알아봐 줄 것이기 때문이었다."], ["She did not know herself why and wherefore, but the arranging of her house had an irresistible attraction for her.", "그녀 자신도 왜, 무엇 때문인지 알지 못했지만, 집을 꾸리는 일이 그녀에게는 거역할 수 없는 매력을 지니고 있었다."], ["Instinctively feeling the approach of spring, and knowing that there would be days of rough weather too, she built her nest as best she could, and was in haste at the same time to build it and to learn how to do it.", "본능적으로 봄이 다가오는 것을 느끼고, 또 사나운 날씨가 닥치는 날들도 있으리라는 것을 알았기에, 그녀는 할 수 있는 한 정성껏 자기의 둥지를 지었고, 동시에 그것을 짓는 일과 짓는 법을 익히는 일을 서둘렀다."]], [["This care for domestic details in Kitty, so opposed to Levin’s ideal of exalted happiness, was at first one of the disappointments; and this sweet care of her household, the aim of which he did not understand, but could not help loving, was one of the new happy surprises.", "레빈의 드높은 행복이라는 이상에 그토록 어긋나는 키티의 이 살림살이에 대한 정성은, 처음에는 실망거리 가운데 하나였다. 그러나 그 목적을 그가 이해하지는 못해도 사랑하지 않을 수 없었던 살림에 대한 이 사랑스러운 정성은, 새롭고 행복한 놀라움 가운데 하나였다."]], [["Another disappointment and happy surprise came in their quarrels.", "또 다른 실망거리이자 행복한 놀라움은 그들의 다툼 속에서 찾아왔다."], ["Levin could never have conceived that between him and his wife any relations could arise other than tender, respectful and loving, and all at once in the very early days they quarreled, so that she said he did not care for her, that he cared for no one but himself, burst into tears, and wrung her arms.", "레빈은 자기와 아내 사이에 다정하고 공손하고 사랑이 넘치는 것 말고 다른 어떤 관계가 생길 수 있으리라고는 결코 상상조차 할 수 없었는데, 결혼 생활 초기에 별안간 두 사람이 다투었고, 그래서 그녀는 그가 자기를 아끼지 않으며 자기 자신밖에 모른다고 말하고는 울음을 터뜨리며 두 팔을 비틀었다."]], [["This first quarrel arose from Levin’s having gone out to a new farmhouse and having been away half an hour too long, because he had tried to get home by a short cut and had lost his way.", "이 첫 번째 다툼은 레빈이 새로 지은 농가에 나갔다가 지름길로 집에 돌아오려다 길을 잃는 바람에 삼십 분이나 더 늦게 돌아온 데서 비롯되었다."], ["He drove home thinking of nothing but her, of her love, of his own happiness, and the nearer he drew to home, the warmer was his tenderness for her.", "그는 오직 그녀만을, 그녀의 사랑을, 자기 자신의 행복을 생각하며 집으로 마차를 몰았고, 집에 가까워질수록 그녀에 대한 애정은 더욱 뜨거워졌다."], ["He ran into the room with the same feeling, with an even stronger feeling than he had had when he reached the Shtcherbatskys’ house to make his offer.", "그는 청혼하러 셰르바츠키 집에 다다랐을 때 느꼈던 것과 똑같은, 아니 그보다 더 강렬한 감정을 품고 방으로 뛰어 들어갔다."], ["And suddenly he was met by a lowering expression he had never seen in her.", "그런데 별안간 그녀에게서 한 번도 본 적 없는 음울한 표정과 마주쳤다."], ["He would have kissed her; she pushed him away.", "그가 입을 맞추려 하자 그녀가 그를 밀쳐 냈다."]], [["“What is it?”", "\"무슨 일이야?\""]], [["“You’ve been enjoying yourself,”", "\"즐기다 오셨군요.\""], ["she began, trying to be calm and spiteful.", "그녀는 차분하면서도 앙심을 담으려 애쓰며 입을 열었다."], ["But as soon as she opened her mouth, a stream of reproach, of senseless jealousy, of all that had been torturing her during that half hour which she had spent sitting motionless at the window, burst from her.", "그러나 입을 열기가 무섭게, 비난과 터무니없는 질투와, 그녀가 창가에 꼼짝 않고 앉아 보낸 그 삼십 분 동안 그녀를 괴롭혔던 그 모든 것이 봇물처럼 쏟아져 나왔다."], ["It was only then, for the first time, that he clearly understood what he had not understood when he led her out of the church after the wedding.", "그제야, 비로소 처음으로, 그는 결혼식이 끝난 뒤 교회에서 그녀를 데리고 나올 때 이해하지 못했던 것을 분명히 이해하게 되었다."], ["He felt now that he was not simply close to her, but that he did not know where he ended and she began.", "그는 이제 자기가 단지 그녀와 가까운 것이 아니라, 자기가 어디서 끝나고 그녀가 어디서 시작되는지 알 수 없다는 것을 느꼈다."], ["He felt this from the agonizing sensation of division that he experienced at that instant.", "그는 그 순간 겪은, 갈라지는 듯한 고통스러운 감각에서 이것을 느꼈다."], ["He was offended for the first instant, but the very same second he felt that he could not be offended by her, that she was himself.", "그는 처음 한순간 모욕감을 느꼈지만, 바로 그 같은 순간 자기가 그녀에게 모욕감을 느낄 수 없다는 것을, 그녀가 곧 자기 자신이라는 것을 느꼈다."], ["He felt for the first moment as a man feels when, having suddenly received a violent blow from behind, he turns round, angry and eager to avenge himself, to look for his antagonist, and finds that it is he himself who has accidentally struck himself, that there is no one to be angry with, and that he must put up with and try to soothe the pain.", "그는 처음 한순간, 뒤에서 별안간 거센 일격을 맞고 화가 나서 복수하려고, 자기를 친 자를 찾으려고 돌아섰다가, 우연히 자기 자신을 친 것이 다름 아닌 자기 자신이며 화낼 상대가 아무도 없고 그 아픔을 참고 달래려 애쓸 수밖에 없다는 것을 깨달은 사람이 느낄 법한 것을 느꼈다."]], [["Never afterwards did he feel it with such intensity, but this first time he could not for a long while get over it.", "그 뒤로 그는 결코 그것을 그토록 강렬하게 느낀 적이 없었지만, 이 첫 번째만큼은 오랫동안 떨쳐 낼 수 없었다."], ["His natural feeling urged him to defend himself, to prove to her she was wrong; but to prove her wrong would mean irritating her still more and making the rupture greater that was the cause of all his suffering.", "그의 본능적인 감정은 자기를 방어하라고, 그녀가 틀렸음을 증명해 보이라고 그를 부추겼다. 그러나 그녀가 틀렸음을 증명하는 것은 그녀를 더욱 자극하여, 그의 온갖 괴로움의 원인인 그 균열을 더 크게 벌리는 일이 되고 말 것이었다."], ["One habitual feeling impelled him to get rid of the blame and to pass it on to her.", "한 습관적인 감정은 그에게 책임을 벗어 던지고 그것을 그녀에게 떠넘기라고 몰아붙였다."], ["Another feeling, even stronger, impelled him as quickly as possible to smooth over the rupture without letting it grow greater.", "또 다른, 한층 더 강한 감정은 그에게 그 균열이 더 커지지 않도록 되도록 빨리 그것을 봉합하라고 몰아붙였다."], ["To remain under such undeserved reproach was wretched, but to make her suffer by justifying himself was worse still.", "받을 까닭 없는 비난 아래 머무는 것은 비참한 일이었지만, 자기를 변호하여 그녀를 괴롭게 하는 것은 그보다 더 나빴다."], ["Like a man half-awake in an agony of pain, he wanted to tear out, to fling away the aching place, and coming to his senses, he felt that the aching place was himself.", "고통에 몸부림치며 반쯤 잠에서 깬 사람처럼, 그는 그 쑤시는 자리를 뜯어내어 멀리 내던지고 싶었지만, 정신을 차리고 보니 그 쑤시는 자리가 바로 자기 자신임을 느꼈다."], ["He could do nothing but try to help the aching place to bear it, and this he tried to do.", "그는 그 쑤시는 자리가 아픔을 견디도록 돕는 것밖에 달리 할 수 있는 일이 없었고, 바로 그것을 하려고 애썼다."]], [["They made peace.", "그들은 화해했다."], ["She, recognizing that she was wrong, though she did not say so, became tenderer to him, and they experienced new, redoubled happiness in their love.", "그녀는 입 밖으로 말하지는 않았어도 자기가 틀렸음을 인정하고 그에게 더 다정해졌으며, 두 사람은 그들의 사랑 속에서 새롭고 갑절로 커진 행복을 맛보았다."], ["But that did not prevent such quarrels from happening again, and exceedingly often too, on the most unexpected and trivial grounds.", "그러나 그렇다고 해서 이런 다툼이 다시 일어나지 않는 것은 아니었고, 그것도 더없이 뜻밖의 사소한 이유로 무척 자주 일어났다."], ["These quarrels frequently arose from the fact that they did not yet know what was of importance to each other and that all this early period they were both often in a bad temper.", "이런 다툼은 그들이 서로에게 무엇이 중요한지 아직 알지 못한다는 사실에서, 그리고 이 초기에 두 사람 다 곧잘 언짢은 기분에 빠지곤 했다는 사실에서 자주 비롯되었다."], ["When one was in a good temper, and the other in a bad temper, the peace was not broken; but when both happened to be in an ill-humor, quarrels sprang up from such incomprehensibly trifling causes, that they could never remember afterwards what they had quarreled about.", "한쪽이 기분이 좋고 다른 한쪽이 언짢을 때는 평화가 깨지지 않았다. 그러나 두 사람이 다 같이 언짢은 기분일 때는, 나중에는 도대체 무엇 때문에 다투었는지 결코 기억해 낼 수조차 없을 만큼 영문 모를 사소한 이유에서 다툼이 솟아났다."], ["It is true that when they were both in a good temper their enjoyment of life was redoubled.", "두 사람이 다 기분이 좋을 때면 그들의 삶의 기쁨이 갑절이 되는 것은 사실이었다."], ["But still this first period of their married life was a difficult time for them.", "그러나 그래도 결혼 생활의 이 첫 시기는 그들에게 힘겨운 시간이었다."]], [["During all this early time they had a peculiarly vivid sense of tension, as it were, a tugging in opposite directions of the chain by which they were bound.", "이 초기 동안 내내 그들은 유난히 생생하게, 말하자면 그들을 묶어 놓은 사슬이 서로 반대 방향으로 잡아당겨지는 듯한 긴장감을 느꼈다."], ["Altogether their honeymoon—that is to say, the month after their wedding—from which from tradition Levin expected so much, was not merely not a time of sweetness, but remained in the memories of both as the bitterest and most humiliating period in their lives.", "전통에 따라 레빈이 그토록 많은 것을 기대했던 그들의 신혼여행, 즉 결혼 후의 한 달은, 결코 달콤한 시간이 아니었을 뿐 아니라 두 사람의 기억 속에 그들 생애에서 가장 쓰라리고 가장 굴욕적인 시기로 남았다."], ["They both alike tried in later life to blot out from their memories all the monstrous, shameful incidents of that morbid period, when both were rarely in a normal frame of mind, both were rarely quite themselves.", "두 사람은 똑같이, 훗날의 삶에서 그 병적인 시기의 온갖 끔찍하고 부끄러운 사건들을, 둘 다 좀처럼 정상적인 마음 상태가 아니었고 둘 다 좀처럼 온전히 제정신이 아니었던 그 시기의 일들을 기억에서 지워 내려고 애썼다."]], [["It was only in the third month of their married life, after their return from Moscow, where they had been staying for a month, that their life began to go more smoothly.", "그들의 삶이 한결 순조롭게 흘러가기 시작한 것은, 한 달간 머물렀던 모스크바에서 돌아온 뒤, 결혼 생활의 석 달째에 이르러서였다."]], [["# Chapter 15", "# Chapter 15"]], [["They had just come back from Moscow, and were glad to be alone.", "그들은 막 모스크바에서 돌아온 참이었고, 둘만 있게 되어 기뻤다."], ["He was sitting at the writing-table in his study, writing.", "그는 서재의 책상 앞에 앉아 글을 쓰고 있었다."], ["She, wearing the dark lilac dress she had worn during the first days of their married life, and put on again today, a dress particularly remembered and loved by him, was sitting on the sofa, the same old-fashioned leather sofa which had always stood in the study in Levin’s father’s and grandfather’s days.", "그녀는 결혼 생활 첫 며칠 동안 입었다가 오늘 다시 꺼내 입은, 그가 특히 기억하고 사랑하는 짙은 라일락빛 드레스 차림으로, 레빈의 아버지와 할아버지 시절부터 줄곧 서재에 놓여 있던 그 낡은 가죽 소파에 앉아 있었다. 그녀는 broderie anglaise를 수놓고 있었다."], ["She was sewing at broderie anglaise. He thought and wrote, never losing the happy consciousness of her presence.", "그는 그녀가 곁에 있다는 행복한 의식을 한순간도 잃지 않은 채 생각하며 글을 썼다."], ["His work, both on the land and on the book, in which the principles of the new land system were to be laid down, had not been abandoned; but just as formerly these pursuits and ideas had seemed to him petty and trivial in comparison with the darkness that overspread all life, now they seemed as unimportant and petty in comparison with the life that lay before him suffused with the brilliant light of happiness.", "토지에 관한 일이든, 새로운 토지 제도의 원칙을 정리하려는 저서에 관한 일이든 그는 손을 놓지 않았다. 하지만 예전에 이런 일과 사상이 온 인생을 뒤덮은 어둠에 비하면 자질구레하고 하찮게 여겨졌던 것과 꼭 마찬가지로, 이제 그것들은 행복의 찬란한 빛으로 가득 찬, 자기 앞에 펼쳐진 삶에 비하면 사소하고 하찮게 여겨졌다."], ["He went on with his work, but he felt now that the center of gravity of his attention had passed to something else, and that consequently he looked at his work quite differently and more clearly.", "그는 일을 계속해 나갔지만, 이제 자기 관심의 무게중심이 다른 무언가로 옮겨 갔으며, 그래서 자기 일을 전혀 다른 눈으로 한층 또렷하게 바라보고 있다고 느꼈다. 예전에 이 일은 그에게 삶으로부터의 도피였다."], ["Formerly this work had been for him an escape from life.", "예전에 그는 이 일이 없으면 자기 삶이 너무 음울하리라고 느꼈다."], ["Formerly he had felt that without this work his life would be too gloomy.", "이제 이런 일은 삶이 한결같이 너무 밝기만 하지 않도록 그에게 필요한 것이었다."], ["Now these pursuits were necessary for him that life might not be too uniformly bright.", "원고를 집어 들어 자기가 써 놓은 것을 끝까지 읽어 보며, 그는 이 일이 매달릴 만한 가치가 있음을 흐뭇하게 깨달았다."], ["Taking up his manuscript, reading through what he had written, he found with pleasure that the work was worth his working at.", "옛 생각들 가운데 많은 것이 그에게는 군더더기로, 극단적으로 보였지만, 머릿속에서 전체를 다시 훑어보니 많은 빈자리가 또렷하게 떠올랐다. 그는 지금 러시아 농업의 현재의 참담한 상태가 어디서 비롯되었는가를 다루는 새 장을 쓰고 있었다."], ["Many of his old ideas seemed to him superfluous and extreme, but many blanks became distinct to him when he reviewed the whole thing in his memory. He was writing now a new chapter on the causes of the present disastrous condition of agriculture in Russia.", "그는 러시아의 빈곤이 단지 토지 소유의 비정상적인 분배와 잘못된 방향의 개혁에서만 생기는 것이 아니라, 최근 몇 년 동안 이 결과에 한몫한 것은 러시아에 비정상적으로 접목된 외래 문명, 특히 철도 같은 교통수단의 편의이며, 이것이 도시로의 집중과 사치의 발달, 그에 따른 제조업과 신용 및 거기에 뒤따르는 투기의 발달을 불러왔고, 이 모든 것이 농업에 해를 끼쳤다고 주장했다."], ["He maintained that the poverty of Russia arises not merely from the anomalous distribution of landed property and misdirected reforms, but that what had contributed of late years to this result was the civilization from without abnormally grafted upon Russia, especially facilities of communication, as railways, leading to centralization in towns, the development of luxury, and the consequent development of manufactures, credit and its accompaniment of speculation—all to the detriment of agriculture.", "그가 보기에 한 국가에서 부가 정상적으로 발전한다면 이 모든 현상은 농업에 상당한 노동이 투입되어 그것이 규칙적인, 적어도 일정한 조건 아래 놓이게 되었을 때에야 비로소 생겨나야 했다. 한 나라의 부는 비례적으로, 특히 부의 다른 원천들이 농업을 앞지르지 않는 방식으로 늘어나야 했다."], ["It seemed to him that in a normal development of wealth in a state all these phenomena would arise only when a considerable amount of labor had been put into agriculture, when it had come under regular, or at least definite, conditions; that the wealth of a country ought to increase proportionally, and especially in such a way that other sources of wealth should not outstrip agriculture; that in harmony with a certain stage of agriculture there should be means of communication corresponding to it, and that in our unsettled condition of the land, railways, called into being by political and not by economic needs, were premature, and instead of promoting agriculture, as was expected of them, they were competing with agriculture and promoting the development of manufactures and credit, and so arresting its progress; and that just as the one-sided and premature development of one organ in an animal would hinder its general development, so in the general development of wealth in Russia, credit, facilities of communication, manufacturing activity, indubitably necessary in Europe, where they had arisen in their proper time, had with us only done harm, by throwing into the background the chief question calling for settlement—the question of the organization of agriculture.", "농업의 일정한 단계와 조화를 이루어 거기에 상응하는 교통수단이 있어야 했고, 토지가 안정되지 못한 우리네 형편에서는 경제적 필요가 아니라 정치적 필요에 의해 생겨난 철도가 시기상조였으며, 사람들이 기대했던 것처럼 농업을 촉진하기는커녕 오히려 농업과 경쟁하면서 제조업과 신용의 발달을 부추겨 농업의 진보를 가로막고 있었다. 그리고 동물의 한 기관이 한쪽으로 치우쳐 너무 일찍 발달하면 전체의 발달을 저해하듯이, 러시아의 전반적인 부의 발달에서도 신용과 교통의 편의와 제조 활동이—유럽에서는 제때 생겨나 의심할 바 없이 필요한 것이었지만—우리에게는 해결을 요하는 가장 중대한 문제, 곧 농업의 조직화 문제를 뒤로 밀어내 버림으로써 오로지 해만 끼쳤다는 것이었다."]], [["While he was writing his ideas she was thinking how unnaturally cordial her husband had been to young Prince Tcharsky, who had, with great want of tact, flirted with her the day before they left Moscow.", "그가 자기 생각을 써 나가는 동안, 그녀는 모스크바를 떠나기 전날 큰 무례를 무릅쓰고 자기에게 추근거렸던 젊은 차르스키 공작에게 남편이 어쩌면 그렇게 부자연스럽게 살갑게 굴었는지를 생각하고 있었다."], ["“He’s jealous,”", "\"질투하는 거야.\""], ["she thought.", "그녀는 생각했다."], ["“Goodness!", "\"어머나!"], ["how sweet and silly he is!", "어쩜 저렇게 사랑스럽고 어리석을까!"], ["He’s jealous of me!", "나를 두고 질투하다니!"], ["If he knew that I think no more of them than of Piotr the cook,”", "내가 그 사람들을 요리사 표트르만큼도 여기지 않는다는 걸 안다면.\""], ["she thought, looking at his head and red neck with a feeling of possession strange to herself.", "그녀는 스스로도 낯선 소유의 감정으로 그의 머리와 붉은 목덜미를 바라보며 생각했다."], ["“Though it’s a pity to take him from his work (but he has plenty of time!), I must look at his face; will he feel I’m looking at him?", "\"일하는 걸 방해하기는 좀 안됐지만(그래도 시간이야 얼마든지 있잖아!), 저 사람 얼굴을 봐야겠어. 내가 보고 있는 걸 느낄까?"], ["I wish he’d turn round ...", "좀 돌아봐 주면 좋겠는데……"], ["I’ll will him to!”", "내가 돌아보게 만들어야지!\""], ["and she opened her eyes wide, as though to intensify the influence of her gaze.", "그러고는 자기 시선의 힘을 더 강하게 하려는 듯 두 눈을 크게 떴다."]], [["“Yes, they draw away all the sap and give a false appearance of prosperity,”", "\"그래, 그것들이 모든 수액을 빨아들이고 거짓된 번영의 외양만 만들어 내는 거야.\""], ["he muttered, stopping to write, and, feeling that she was looking at him and smiling, he looked round.", "그는 글쓰기를 멈추고 중얼거렸다. 그러다 그녀가 자기를 바라보며 미소 짓고 있음을 느끼고는 뒤를 돌아보았다."]], [["“Well?”", "\"왜?\""], ["he queried, smiling, and getting up.", "그가 미소를 지으며 묻고는 자리에서 일어섰다."]], [["“He looked round,”", "\"돌아봤어.\""], ["she thought.", "그녀는 생각했다."]], [["“It’s nothing; I wanted you to look round,”", "\"아무것도 아니에요. 당신이 돌아봤으면 했어요.\""], ["she said, watching him, and trying to guess whether he was vexed at being interrupted or not.", "그녀는 그를 지켜보며, 방해받은 것을 그가 언짢아하는지 아닌지 가늠해 보려 애쓰면서 말했다."]], [["“How happy we are alone together!—I am, that is,”", "\"우리 둘만 있으니 얼마나 행복한지! 적어도 나는 그래.\""], ["he said, going up to her with a radiant smile of happiness.", "그가 행복으로 환히 빛나는 미소를 띠고 그녀에게 다가가며 말했다."]], [["“I’m just as happy.", "\"나도 꼭 그만큼 행복해요."], ["I’ll never go anywhere, especially not to Moscow.”", "어디에도 절대 가지 않을 거예요, 특히 모스크바에는요.\""]], [["“And what were you thinking about?”", "\"그래, 무슨 생각을 하고 있었어?\""]], [["“I?", "\"나요?"], ["I was thinking....", "생각하고 있었어요……"], ["No, no, go along, go on writing; don’t break off,”", "아니에요, 아니에요, 어서 가요, 어서 글을 써요. 그만두지 말아요.\""], ["she said, pursing up her lips, “and I must cut out these little holes now, do you see?”", "그녀가 입술을 오므리며 말했다. \"그리고 나는 이제 이 작은 구멍들을 도려내야 해요, 보이죠?\""]], [["She took up her scissors and began cutting them out.", "그녀는 가위를 집어 들고 그것들을 도려내기 시작했다."]], [["“No; tell me, what was it?”", "\"안 돼, 말해 봐, 무슨 생각이었어?\""], ["he said, sitting down beside her and watching the tiny scissors moving round.", "그가 그녀 곁에 앉아 작은 가위가 빙글빙글 움직이는 것을 지켜보며 말했다."]], [["“Oh!", "\"아!"], ["what was I thinking about?", "내가 무슨 생각을 하고 있었더라?"], ["I was thinking about Moscow, about the back of your head.”", "모스크바를 생각하고 있었어요, 당신 뒤통수를요.\""]], [["“Why should I, of all people, have such happiness!", "\"왜 하필이면 내가, 이런 행복을 누려야 하는 걸까!"], ["It’s unnatural, too good,”", "부자연스럽잖아, 너무 좋아서 말이야.\""], ["he said, kissing her hand.", "그가 그녀의 손에 입을 맞추며 말했다."]], [["“I feel quite the opposite; the better things are, the more natural it seems to me.”", "\"나는 정반대로 느껴요. 좋은 일일수록 더 자연스럽게 여겨지는걸요.\""]], [["“And you’ve got a little curl loose,”", "\"그리고 당신 머리카락 한 가닥이 살짝 풀렸어.\""], ["he said, carefully turning her head round.", "그가 조심스럽게 그녀의 머리를 돌리며 말했다."]], [["“A little curl, oh yes.", "\"머리카락 한 가닥, 아 그래요."], ["No, no, we are busy at our work!”", "안 돼요, 안 돼, 우리 일하는 중이잖아요!\""]], [["Work did not progress further, and they darted apart from one another like culprits when Kouzma came in to announce that tea was ready.", "일은 더 진척되지 않았고, 쿠즈마가 차가 준비됐다고 알리러 들어오자 두 사람은 죄지은 사람들처럼 서로에게서 떨어졌다."]], [["“Have they come from the town?”", "\"시내에서 사람들이 왔나?\""], ["Levin asked Kouzma.", "레빈이 쿠즈마에게 물었다."]], [["“They’ve just come; they’re unpacking the things.”", "\"방금 도착했습니다. 짐을 풀고 있습니다.\""]], [["“Come quickly,”", "\"빨리 와요.\""], ["she said to him as she went out of the study, “or else I shall read your letters without you.”", "그녀가 서재를 나서며 그에게 말했다. \"안 그러면 당신 없이 나 혼자 편지를 읽어 버릴 거예요.\""]], [["Left alone, after putting his manuscripts together in the new portfolio bought by her, he washed his hands at the new washstand with the elegant fittings, that had all made their appearance with her.", "혼자 남은 그는 원고를 그녀가 사 준 새 서류첩에 한데 모아 넣은 뒤, 우아한 부속품이 달린 새 세면대에서 손을 씻었다. 이 모든 것이 그녀와 함께 나타난 물건이었다."], ["Levin smiled at his own thoughts, and shook his head disapprovingly at those thoughts; a feeling akin to remorse fretted him.", "레빈은 자기 생각에 미소 지었고, 그러다 그 생각이 못마땅하다는 듯 고개를 저었다. 후회에 가까운 감정이 그를 괴롭혔다."], ["There was something shameful, effeminate, Capuan, as he called it to himself, in his present mode of life.", "지금의 생활 방식에는 무언가 부끄럽고, 사내답지 못하고, 그가 스스로 부르는 표현대로 카푸아적인 데가 있었다."], ["“It’s not right to go on like this,”", "\"이렇게 계속 지내는 건 옳지 않아.\""], ["he thought.", "그는 생각했다."], ["“It’ll soon be three months, and I’m doing next to nothing.", "\"곧 석 달이 되는데, 나는 거의 아무것도 한 게 없어."], ["Today, almost for the first time, I set to work seriously, and what happened?", "오늘에야 거의 처음으로 진지하게 일에 매달렸는데, 그래서 어떻게 됐지?"], ["I did nothing but begin and throw it aside.", "시작만 했다가 내팽개친 것 말고는 한 게 없잖아."], ["Even my ordinary pursuits I have almost given up.", "평소에 하던 일조차 거의 손을 놓아 버렸어."], ["On the land I scarcely walk or drive about at all to look after things.", "영지에서는 일을 살피러 걷거나 마차를 몰고 돌아보는 일이 거의 없지."], ["Either I am loath to leave her, or I see she’s dull alone.", "그녀를 두고 떠나기가 싫거나, 아니면 그녀가 혼자 있으면 따분해하는 게 눈에 보이거든."], ["And I used to think that, before marriage, life was nothing much, somehow didn’t count, but that after marriage, life began in earnest.", "그리고 나는 결혼 전에는 삶이 별것 아니고, 어쩐지 셈에 들지 않으며, 결혼 후에야 삶이 본격적으로 시작된다고 생각하곤 했지."], ["And here almost three months have passed, and I have spent my time so idly and unprofitably.", "그런데 여기 거의 석 달이 지나도록, 나는 시간을 이토록 빈둥거리며 헛되이 보내 버렸어."], ["No, this won’t do; I must begin.", "안 돼, 이래선 안 돼. 시작해야 해."], ["Of course, it’s not her fault.", "물론 그녀 탓은 아니야."], ["She’s not to blame in any way.", "그녀는 어떤 식으로든 비난받을 게 없어."], ["I ought myself to be firmer, to maintain my masculine independence of action; or else I shall get into such ways, and she’ll get used to them too....", "내가 좀 더 단단해져서 사내다운 행동의 독립성을 지켜야 하는 거지. 안 그러면 나는 이런 버릇이 들어 버릴 테고, 그녀도 거기에 익숙해질 거야……"], ["Of course she’s not to blame,”", "물론 그녀 탓은 아니야.\""], ["he told himself.", "그는 스스로에게 일렀다."]], [["But it is hard for anyone who is dissatisfied not to blame someone else, and especially the person nearest of all to him, for the ground of his dissatisfaction.", "* 카푸아적: 고대 이탈리아의 도시 카푸아는 사치와 향락에 빠져 군대를 무력하게 만든 곳으로 알려져, '안일과 방종에 젖은'이라는 뜻으로 쓰인다. 하지만 불만에 찬 사람이 자기 불만의 근거를 두고 누군가 다른 사람을, 특히 자기와 가장 가까운 사람을 탓하지 않기란 어려운 법이다."], ["And it vaguely came into Levin’s mind that she herself was not to blame (she could not be to blame for anything), but what was to blame was her education, too superficial and frivolous.", "그래서 레빈의 머릿속에는 그녀 자신은 탓할 게 없지만(그녀는 무엇에 대해서도 탓할 수 없었다), 탓할 것은 너무 피상적이고 경박한 그녀의 교육이라는 생각이 어렴풋이 떠올랐다.(\"그 멍청한 차르스키."], ["(“That fool Tcharsky: she wanted, I know, to stop him, but didn’t know how to.”) “Yes, apart from her interest in the house (that she has), apart from dress and broderie anglaise, she has no serious interests.", "그녀가 그를 말리고 싶어 했지만 어떻게 해야 할지 몰랐던 거야, 나는 알아.\") \"그래, 집안일에 대한 관심(그건 있지)을 빼면, 옷과 broderie anglaise를 빼면, 그녀에게는 진지한 관심사가 없어."], ["No interest in her work, in the estate, in the peasants, nor in music, though she’s rather good at it, nor in reading.", "자기 일에도, 영지에도, 농민들에게도 관심이 없고, 음악에도—꽤 잘하는데도—관심이 없고, 독서에도 관심이 없지."], ["She does nothing, and is perfectly satisfied.”", "아무것도 하지 않으면서, 더없이 만족해하고 있어.\""], ["Levin, in his heart, censured this, and did not as yet understand that she was preparing for that period of activity which was to come for her when she would at once be the wife of her husband and mistress of the house, and would bear, and nurse, and bring up children.", "레빈은 마음속으로 이것을 못마땅하게 여겼지만, 그녀가 머지않아 자기에게 닥쳐올 그 활동의 시기를 위해 준비하고 있다는 것을 아직 이해하지 못했다. 그때가 되면 그녀는 남편의 아내이자 집안의 안주인이 되는 동시에, 아이를 낳고 젖을 먹이고 길러 낼 터였다."], ["He knew not that she was instinctively aware of this, and preparing herself for this time of terrible toil, did not reproach herself for the moments of carelessness and happiness in her love that she enjoyed now while gaily building her nest for the future.", "그는 그녀가 이것을 본능적으로 알고서, 그 무서운 노고의 시기에 대비하고 있으며, 미래를 위해 즐겁게 보금자리를 짓고 있는 지금 자기가 누리는 사랑 속의 무심하고 행복한 순간들에 대해 스스로를 책망하지 않는다는 것을 알지 못했다."]], [["# Chapter 16", "# Chapter 16"]], [["When Levin went upstairs, his wife was sitting near the new silver samovar behind the new tea service, and, having settled old Agafea Mihalovna at a little table with a full cup of tea, was reading a letter from Dolly, with whom they were in continual and frequent correspondence.", "레빈이 위층으로 올라갔을 때, 그의 아내는 새로 들인 은제 사모바르 옆, 새 다기 세트 뒤에 앉아, 늙은 아가피야 미하일로브나를 작은 탁자 앞에 앉히고 차를 가득 따른 잔을 권한 뒤, 끊임없이 자주 편지를 주고받는 돌리에게서 온 편지를 읽고 있었다."]], [["“You see, your good lady’s settled me here, told me to sit a bit with her,”", "\"보세요, 마님이 저를 여기 앉혀 놓고 잠깐 함께 있어 달라고 하셨답니다.\""], ["said Agafea Mihalovna, smiling affectionately at Kitty.", "아가피야 미하일로브나가 키티를 향해 다정하게 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["In these words of Agafea Mihalovna, Levin read the final act of the drama which had been enacted of late between her and Kitty.", "아가피야 미하일로브나의 이 말에서 레빈은 요즘 그녀와 키티 사이에서 벌어졌던 드라마의 마지막 막을 읽어 냈다."], ["He saw that, in spite of Agafea Mihalovna’s feelings being hurt by a new mistress taking the reins of government out of her hands, Kitty had yet conquered her and made her love her.", "새 안주인이 살림의 고삐를 자기 손에서 빼앗아 갔다는 데에 아가피야 미하일로브나의 마음이 상했음에도, 키티가 끝내 그녀를 정복하여 자기를 사랑하게 만들었다는 것을 그는 알아챘다."]], [["“Here, I opened your letter too,”", "\"여기, 당신 편지도 제가 뜯어 봤어요.\""], ["said Kitty, handing him an illiterate letter.", "키티가 글씨가 서툰 편지 한 통을 그에게 건네며 말했다."], ["“It’s from that woman, I think, your brother’s....”", "\"그 여자한테서 온 것 같아요, 당신 형의…….\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“I did not read it through.", "\"끝까지 읽지는 않았어요."], ["This is from my people and from Dolly.", "이건 친정 식구들하고 돌리한테서 온 거예요."], ["Fancy!", "글쎄!"], ["Dolly took Tanya and Grisha to a children’s ball at the Sarmatskys’: Tanya was a French marquise.”", "돌리가 타냐와 그리샤를 사르마츠키 집안의 아이들 무도회에 데려갔다지 뭐예요. 타냐는 프랑스 후작 부인 차림이었대요.\""]], [["But Levin did not hear her.", "그러나 레빈은 그녀의 말을 듣고 있지 않았다."], ["Flushing, he took the letter from Marya Nikolaevna, his brother’s former mistress, and began to read it.", "얼굴을 붉히며 그는 형의 옛 정부인 마리야 니콜라예브나에게서 온 편지를 받아 들고 읽기 시작했다."], ["This was the second letter he had received from Marya Nikolaevna.", "마리야 니콜라예브나에게서 받은 두 번째 편지였다."], ["In the first letter, Marya Nikolaevna wrote that his brother had sent her away for no fault of hers, and, with touching simplicity, added that though she was in want again, she asked for nothing, and wished for nothing, but was only tormented by the thought that Nikolay Dmitrievitch would come to grief without her, owing to the weak state of his health, and begged his brother to look after him.", "첫 번째 편지에서 마리야 니콜라예브나는 형이 자기 잘못도 없는데 자기를 내쫓았다고 썼고, 가슴 뭉클할 만큼 소박한 어조로, 자기는 다시 궁핍한 처지가 되었으나 아무것도 청하지 않고 아무것도 바라지 않으며, 다만 니콜라이 드미트리예비치가 허약한 건강 탓에 자기 없이는 화를 입을까 하는 생각에 괴로울 뿐이라며, 형을 돌봐 달라고 그의 동생에게 부탁한다고 덧붙였다."], ["Now she wrote quite differently.", "그런데 이번에는 사뭇 다른 내용을 썼다."], ["She had found Nikolay Dmitrievitch, had again made it up with him in Moscow, and had moved with him to a provincial town, where he had received a post in the government service.", "그녀는 니콜라이 드미트리예비치를 찾아내어 모스크바에서 다시 그와 화해했고, 그가 관청에 일자리를 얻은 어느 지방 도시로 그와 함께 이사했다."], ["But that he had quarreled with the head official, and was on his way back to Moscow, only he had been taken so ill on the road that it was doubtful if he would ever leave his bed again, she wrote.", "그러나 그가 상관과 다투고 다시 모스크바로 돌아오는 길이었는데, 도중에 너무 심하게 병이 들어 다시는 자리에서 일어나지 못할 것 같다고 그녀는 썼다."], ["“It’s always of you he has talked, and, besides, he has no more money left.”", "\"그분은 늘 도련님 이야기만 하셨어요. 게다가 이젠 돈도 한 푼 남지 않았답니다.\""]], [["“Read this; Dolly writes about you,”", "\"이것 좀 읽어 봐요. 돌리가 당신 얘기를 썼어요.\""], ["Kitty was beginning, with a smile; but she stopped suddenly, noticing the changed expression on her husband’s face.", "키티가 미소를 지으며 말을 꺼냈다. 그러나 그녀는 남편의 얼굴에 떠오른 달라진 표정을 알아채고는 별안간 입을 다물었다."]], [["“What is it?", "\"왜 그래요?"], ["What’s the matter?”", "무슨 일이에요?\""]], [["“She writes to me that Nikolay, my brother, is at death’s door.", "\"형 니콜라이가 죽음의 문턱에 있다는 편지요."], ["I shall go to him.”", "형한테 가 봐야겠소.\""]], [["Kitty’s face changed at once.", "키티의 얼굴이 단번에 바뀌었다."], ["Thoughts of Tanya as a marquise, of Dolly, all had vanished.", "후작 부인 차림의 타냐도, 돌리도, 모두 사라져 버렸다."]], [["“When are you going?”", "\"언제 떠나실 거예요?\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 물었다."]], [["“Tomorrow.”", "\"내일.\""]], [["“And I will go with you, can I?”", "\"저도 같이 가겠어요. 그래도 되죠?\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“Kitty!", "\"키티!"], ["What are you thinking of?”", "무슨 생각을 하는 거요?\""], ["he said reproachfully.", "그가 나무라듯 말했다."]], [["“How do you mean?”", "\"무슨 뜻이에요?\""], ["offended that he should seem to take her suggestion unwillingly and with vexation.", "그가 자기 제안을 마지못해, 못마땅한 기색으로 받아들이는 것 같아 그녀는 마음이 상했다."], ["“Why shouldn’t I go?", "\"내가 가면 안 될 이유가 뭐예요?"], ["I shan’t be in your way.", "방해되진 않을 거예요."], ["I....”", "난…….\""]], [["“I’m going because my brother is dying,”", "\"내가 가는 건 형이 죽어 가고 있기 때문이오.\""], ["said Levin.", "레빈이 말했다."], ["“Why should you....”", "\"당신이 왜…….\""]], [["“Why?", "\"왜냐고요?"], ["For the same reason as you.”", "당신과 똑같은 이유죠.\""]], [["“And, at a moment of such gravity for me, she only thinks of her being dull by herself,”", "'나에게 이토록 중대한 순간에, 저 사람은 혼자 있으면 따분하리라는 것만 생각하는군.'"], ["thought Levin.", "레빈은 생각했다."], ["And this lack of candor in a matter of such gravity infuriated him.", "그리고 이토록 중대한 일에 솔직하지 못한 그 태도가 그를 격분하게 했다."]], [["“It’s out of the question,”", "\"말도 안 되는 소리요.\""], ["he said sternly.", "그가 매섭게 말했다."]], [["Agafea Mihalovna, seeing that it was coming to a quarrel, gently put down her cup and withdrew.", "다툼으로 번지고 있음을 알아챈 아가피야 미하일로브나는 잔을 살그머니 내려놓고 물러갔다."], ["Kitty did not even notice her.", "키티는 그녀가 나가는 것조차 알아채지 못했다."], ["The tone in which her husband had said the last words wounded her, especially because he evidently did not believe what she had said.", "남편이 마지막 말을 내뱉은 그 어조가, 특히 그가 자기 말을 분명 믿지 않는다는 사실이 그녀에게 상처를 주었다."]], [["“I tell you, that if you go, I shall come with you; I shall certainly come,”", "\"말해 두는데, 당신이 가면 나도 같이 갈 거예요. 꼭 갈 거라고요.\""], ["she said hastily and wrathfully.", "그녀가 다급하고 노여운 어조로 말했다."], ["“Why out of the question?", "\"왜 말도 안 된다는 거예요?"], ["Why do you say it’s out of the question?”", "어째서 말도 안 된다고 하는 거죠?\""]], [["“Because it’ll be going God knows where, by all sorts of roads and to all sorts of hotels.", "\"하느님만 아는 곳으로, 온갖 길을 거쳐 온갖 여관을 전전해야 할 텐데."], ["You would be a hindrance to me,”", "당신은 나한테 짐이 될 거요.\""], ["said Levin, trying to be cool.", "레빈이 애써 침착하게 말했다."]], [["“Not at all.", "\"전혀요."], ["I don’t want anything.", "난 아무것도 바라지 않아요."], ["Where you can go, I can....”", "당신이 갈 수 있는 곳이면 나도…….\""]], [["“Well, for one thing then, because this woman’s there whom you can’t meet.”", "\"그럼 우선 한 가지만 말하지. 거기엔 당신이 만나서는 안 될 그 여자가 있소.\""]], [["“I don’t know and don’t care to know who’s there and what.", "\"거기에 누가 있는지, 무엇이 있는지 난 몰라요, 알고 싶지도 않아요."], ["I know that my husband’s brother is dying and my husband is going to him, and I go with my husband too....”", "내가 아는 건 내 남편의 형이 죽어 가고 있고 내 남편이 그분께 간다는 것, 그러니 나도 내 남편과 함께 간다는 것뿐이에요…….\""]], [["“Kitty!", "\"키티!"], ["Don’t get angry.", "화내지 마."], ["But just think a little: this is a matter of such importance that I can’t bear to think that you should bring in a feeling of weakness, of dislike to being left alone.", "하지만 조금만 생각해 봐. 이건 너무나 중대한 일이라, 당신이 거기에 약한 마음이라든지, 혼자 남겨지기 싫은 마음 따위를 끌어들인다고 생각하면 견딜 수가 없어."], ["Come, you’ll be dull alone, so go and stay at Moscow a little.”", "자, 당신은 혼자 있으면 따분할 테니, 모스크바에 가서 잠깐 머물러요.\""]], [["“There, you always ascribe base, vile motives to me,”", "\"거봐요, 당신은 늘 나한테 비열하고 천한 동기를 갖다 붙이는군요.\""], ["she said with tears of wounded pride and fury.", "그녀가 상처받은 자존심과 분노의 눈물을 흘리며 말했다."], ["“I didn’t mean, it wasn’t weakness, it wasn’t ...", "\"난 그런 뜻이 아니었어요. 약한 마음 때문도 아니었고, 그게……."], ["I feel that it’s my duty to be with my husband when he’s in trouble, but you try on purpose to hurt me, you try on purpose not to understand....”", "난 남편이 곤경에 빠졌을 때 곁에 있는 게 아내의 도리라고 느끼는데, 당신은 일부러 날 아프게 하려 들고, 일부러 이해하지 않으려 하는군요…….\""]], [["“No; this is awful!", "\"아니, 이건 끔찍하군!"], ["To be such a slave!”", "이렇게 노예 노릇을 하다니!\""], ["cried Levin, getting up, and unable to restrain his anger any longer.", "더는 분노를 억누르지 못한 레빈이 벌떡 일어나며 외쳤다."], ["But at the same second he felt that he was beating himself.", "그러나 바로 그 순간 그는 자기가 스스로를 매질하고 있음을 느꼈다."]], [["“Then why did you marry?", "\"그럼 왜 결혼했어요?"], ["You could have been free.", "자유로울 수도 있었잖아요."], ["Why did you, if you regret it?”", "후회할 거라면 대체 왜 한 거예요?\""], ["she said, getting up and running away into the drawing-room.", "그녀가 자리에서 일어나 응접실로 달아나며 말했다."]], [["When he went to her, she was sobbing.", "그가 그녀에게 다가갔을 때, 그녀는 흐느끼고 있었다."]], [["He began to speak, trying to find words not to dissuade but simply to soothe her.", "그는 말을 꺼내며, 설득하기 위해서가 아니라 그저 그녀를 달래기 위한 말을 찾으려 애썼다."], ["But she did not heed him, and would not agree to anything.", "그러나 그녀는 그의 말에 귀 기울이지 않았고, 어떤 것에도 응하려 하지 않았다."], ["He bent down to her and took her hand, which resisted him.", "그는 그녀에게 몸을 숙여, 뿌리치려는 그녀의 손을 잡았다."], ["He kissed her hand, kissed her hair, kissed her hand again—still she was silent.", "그는 그녀의 손에 입을 맞추고, 머리카락에 입을 맞추고, 다시 손에 입을 맞추었다. 그래도 그녀는 잠자코 있었다."], ["But when he took her face in both his hands and said “Kitty!” she suddenly recovered herself, and began to cry, and they were reconciled.", "그러나 그가 두 손으로 그녀의 얼굴을 감싸 쥐고 \"키티!\"라고 부르자, 그녀는 별안간 마음을 가라앉히고는 울음을 터뜨렸고, 두 사람은 화해했다."]], [["It was decided that they should go together the next day.", "이튿날 함께 떠나기로 결정되었다."], ["Levin told his wife that he believed she wanted to go simply in order to be of use, agreed that Marya Nikolaevna’s being with his brother did not make her going improper, but he set off at the bottom of his heart dissatisfied both with her and with himself.", "레빈은 그녀가 단지 도움이 되고 싶어서 가려는 것이라고 믿는다고 아내에게 말했고, 마리야 니콜라예브나가 형 곁에 있다고 해서 그녀가 가는 것이 부적절해지는 건 아니라는 데에도 동의했지만, 마음 깊은 곳에서는 그녀에게도 자기 자신에게도 불만을 품은 채 길을 나섰다."], ["He was dissatisfied with her for being unable to make up her mind to let him go when it was necessary (and how strange it was for him to think that he, so lately hardly daring to believe in such happiness as that she could love him—now was unhappy because she loved him too much!), and he was dissatisfied with himself for not showing more strength of will.", "정작 필요한 순간에 그를 혼자 보내겠다는 결심을 하지 못하는 그녀가 그는 불만스러웠고(그리고 그녀가 자기를 사랑할 수 있으리라는 그런 행복을 얼마 전까지만 해도 감히 믿지 못했던 그가, 이제는 그녀가 자기를 너무 사랑하기 때문에 불행하다고 생각하다니, 얼마나 이상한 일인가!), 좀 더 강한 의지를 보이지 못한 자기 자신에게도 불만스러웠다."], ["Even greater was the feeling of disagreement at the bottom of his heart as to her not needing to consider the woman who was with his brother, and he thought with horror of all the contingencies they might meet with.", "형 곁에 있는 그 여자를 그녀가 신경 쓸 필요가 없다는 점에 대해서는, 마음 깊은 곳의 거부감이 한층 더 컸으며, 그는 그들이 맞닥뜨릴지도 모를 온갖 상황을 떠올리며 몸서리쳤다."], ["The mere idea of his wife, his Kitty, being in the same room with a common wench, set him shuddering with horror and loathing.", "자기 아내, 자기 키티가 천한 계집과 같은 방에 있게 된다는 생각만으로도 그는 공포와 혐오에 몸을 떨었다."]], [["# Chapter 17", "# Chapter 17"]], [["The hotel of the provincial town where Nikolay Levin was lying ill was one of those provincial hotels which are constructed on the newest model of modern improvements, with the best intentions of cleanliness, comfort, and even elegance, but owing to the public that patronizes them, are with astounding rapidity transformed into filthy taverns with a pretension of modern improvement that only makes them worse than the old-fashioned, honestly filthy hotels.", "니콜라이 레빈이 병으로 누워 있던 그 지방 도시의 호텔은, 청결과 안락과 심지어 우아함까지 갖추겠다는 더없이 좋은 의도로 최신 개량 양식에 따라 지어진 지방 호텔 가운데 하나였으나, 그곳을 드나드는 손님들 탓에 놀라울 만큼 빠르게 더러운 선술집으로 변해 버렸고, 그러면서도 최신 개량의 허울을 내세운 탓에 차라리 정직하게 더러운 옛날식 호텔보다 더 나빠진 그런 곳이었다."], ["This hotel had already reached that stage, and the soldier in a filthy uniform smoking in the entry, supposed to stand for a hall-porter, and the cast-iron, slippery, dark, and disagreeable staircase, and the free and easy waiter in a filthy frock coat, and the common dining-room with a dusty bouquet of wax flowers adorning the table, and filth, dust, and disorder everywhere, and at the same time the sort of modern up-to-date self-complacent railway uneasiness of this hotel, aroused a most painful feeling in Levin after their fresh young life, especially because the impression of falsity made by the hotel was so out of keeping with what awaited them.", "이 호텔은 이미 그 단계에 이르러 있었으니, 현관에서 담배를 피우며 문지기 노릇을 한다는 더러운 군복 차림의 병사며, 무쇠로 만든 미끄럽고 어둡고 불쾌한 계단이며, 더러운 프록코트를 걸친 데면데면한 보이며, 먼지 앉은 밀랍 꽃다발이 식탁을 장식하고 있는 공동 식당이며, 어디에나 깔린 오물과 먼지와 어수선함이며, 그러면서도 동시에 풍기는 어딘가 현대적이고 최신식인, 철도 같은 자기만족적 불안감이, 싱그럽고 젊은 그들의 삶을 겪은 뒤의 레빈에게 더없이 괴로운 감정을 불러일으켰는데, 특히 이 호텔이 자아내는 거짓스러운 인상이 그들을 기다리고 있는 것과 너무도 어울리지 않았기 때문이었다."]], [["As is invariably the case, after they had been asked at what price they wanted rooms, it appeared that there was not one decent room for them; one decent room had been taken by the inspector of railroads, another by a lawyer from Moscow, a third by Princess Astafieva from the country.", "언제나 그렇듯이, 어느 정도 가격의 방을 원하느냐는 물음을 받고 난 뒤에야, 그들에게 내줄 변변한 방이 하나도 없다는 사실이 드러났다. 변변한 방 하나는 철도 감독관이, 또 하나는 모스크바에서 온 변호사가, 세 번째 방은 시골에서 온 아스타피예바 공작 부인이 차지하고 있었다."], ["There remained only one filthy room, next to which they promised that another should be empty by the evening.", "남은 것은 더러운 방 하나뿐이었고, 그 옆방이 저녁쯤이면 비게 될 거라고 약속했다."], ["Feeling angry with his wife because what he had expected had come to pass, which was that at the moment of arrival, when his heart throbbed with emotion and anxiety to know how his brother was getting on, he should have to be seeing after her, instead of rushing straight to his brother, Levin conducted her to the room assigned them.", "자기가 예상했던 일이 그대로 벌어진 것에 아내에게 화가 났는데, 그것은 곧 도착하는 순간, 형의 안부를 알고 싶은 마음에 가슴이 격정과 불안으로 두근거리던 바로 그 순간에, 형에게 곧장 달려가는 대신 아내를 챙겨야만 한다는 것이었고, 레빈은 그녀를 정해진 방으로 데려다주었다."]], [["“Go, do go!”", "\"가요, 어서 가요!\""], ["she said, looking at him with timid and guilty eyes.", "그녀가 겁먹은, 죄지은 듯한 눈으로 그를 바라보며 말했다."]], [["He went out of the door without a word, and at once stumbled over Marya Nikolaevna, who had heard of his arrival and had not dared to go in to see him.", "그는 말없이 문을 나서다가, 곧장 마리야 니콜라예브나와 부딪힐 뻔했다. 그녀는 그가 도착했다는 말을 듣고도 감히 그를 만나러 들어가지 못하고 있던 참이었다."], ["She was just the same as when he saw her in Moscow; the same woolen gown, and bare arms and neck, and the same good-naturedly stupid, pockmarked face, only a little plumper.", "그녀는 모스크바에서 보았을 때와 똑같았다. 똑같은 모직 원피스에, 드러난 팔과 목, 그리고 사람 좋게 멍청한, 곰보 자국이 있는 똑같은 얼굴이었으나, 다만 조금 더 통통해져 있었다."]], [["“Well, how is he?", "\"그래, 형은 어때요?"], ["how is he?”", "어떻습니까?\""]], [["“Very bad.", "\"아주 안 좋아요."], ["He can’t get up.", "일어나지도 못해요."], ["He has kept expecting you.", "줄곧 당신을 기다리셨어요."], ["He....", "그분이……"], ["Are you ...", "사모님과……"], ["with your wife?”", "함께 오셨나요?\""]], [["Levin did not for the first moment understand what it was confused her, but she immediately enlightened him.", "레빈은 처음 한순간 무엇이 그녀를 당황하게 했는지 알아채지 못했으나, 그녀가 곧 그를 일깨워 주었다."]], [["“I’ll go away.", "\"전 비켜 있을게요."], ["I’ll go down to the kitchen,”", "부엌으로 내려가 있겠어요.\""], ["she brought out.", "그녀가 말을 꺼냈다."], ["“Nikolay Dmitrievitch will be delighted.", "\"니콜라이 드미트리예비치도 기뻐하실 거예요."], ["He heard about it, and knows your lady, and remembers her abroad.”", "그분도 소식을 들으셨고, 사모님을 아세요, 외국에서 뵌 걸 기억하시거든요.\""]], [["Levin realized that she meant his wife, and did not know what answer to make.", "레빈은 그녀가 자기 아내를 두고 하는 말임을 깨달았으나, 무어라 대답해야 할지 알 수 없었다."]], [["“Come along, come along to him!”", "\"같이 가요, 형에게 같이 갑시다!\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["But as soon as he moved, the door of his room opened and Kitty peeped out.", "그러나 그가 움직이기가 무섭게, 그의 방문이 열리고 키티가 빼꼼히 내다보았다."], ["Levin crimsoned both from shame and anger with his wife, who had put herself and him in such a difficult position; but Marya Nikolaevna crimsoned still more.", "레빈은 자기와 자기를 이토록 난처한 처지에 빠뜨린 아내에 대한 수치심과 분노로 얼굴이 붉어졌으나, 마리야 니콜라예브나는 더욱 새빨개졌다."], ["She positively shrank together and flushed to the point of tears, and clutching the ends of her apron in both hands, twisted them in her red fingers without knowing what to say and what to do.", "그녀는 그야말로 몸을 잔뜩 움츠리며 눈물이 핑 돌 만큼 얼굴을 붉혔고, 두 손으로 앞치마 자락을 움켜쥔 채 무슨 말을 하고 무얼 해야 할지 모르는 채로 붉은 손가락으로 그것을 비비 꼬았다."]], [["For the first instant Levin saw an expression of eager curiosity in the eyes with which Kitty looked at this awful woman, so incomprehensible to her; but it lasted only a single instant.", "처음 한순간 레빈은, 자기로서는 도무지 이해할 수 없는 이 끔찍한 여자를 바라보는 키티의 눈에서 강렬한 호기심의 표정을 보았으나, 그것은 단 한순간뿐이었다."]], [["“Well!", "\"그래요!"], ["how is he?”", "형님은 어떠세요?\""], ["she turned to her husband and then to her.", "그녀가 남편을 향해, 그러고는 그녀를 향해 물었다."]], [["“But one can’t go on talking in the passage like this!”", "\"하지만 이렇게 복도에서 계속 이야기를 할 수는 없잖아!\""], ["Levin said, looking angrily at a gentleman who walked jauntily at that instant across the corridor, as though about his affairs.", "레빈은 마침 그때 마치 제 볼일이라도 보러 가는 듯 복도를 경쾌하게 가로질러 가는 한 신사를 화난 눈으로 바라보며 말했다."]], [["“Well then, come in,”", "\"그럼 이리 들어와요.\""], ["said Kitty, turning to Marya Nikolaevna, who had recovered herself, but noticing her husband’s face of dismay, “or go on; go, and then come for me,”", "키티가 정신을 차린 마리야 니콜라예브나를 향해 말했으나, 남편의 낭패한 얼굴을 보고는, \"아니면 그냥 가세요. 가셨다가 저를 데리러 와요.\""], ["she said, and went back into the room.", "그녀가 말하고는 방으로 돌아갔다."]], [["Levin went to his brother’s room.", "레빈은 형의 방으로 갔다."], ["He had not in the least expected what he saw and felt in his brother’s room.", "형의 방에서 자기가 보고 느낀 것을 그는 조금도 예상하지 못했었다."], ["He had expected to find him in the same state of self-deception which he had heard was so frequent with the consumptive, and which had struck him so much during his brother’s visit in the autumn.", "그는 형이, 폐병 환자에게 흔하다고 들었고 또 지난가을 형이 찾아왔을 때 자기에게 그토록 강한 인상을 남겼던, 그 똑같은 자기기만의 상태에 있으리라 예상했었다."], ["He had expected to find the physical signs of the approach of death more marked—greater weakness, greater emaciation, but still almost the same condition of things.", "그는 죽음이 다가오는 신체적 징후가 더 뚜렷해지긴 했어도—더 쇠약해지고 더 야위었어도—거의 같은 상태이리라 예상했었다."], ["He had expected himself to feel the same distress at the loss of the brother he loved and the same horror in face of death as he had felt then, only in a greater degree.", "그는 자기가 사랑하는 형을 잃는 데서 오는 그때와 똑같은 비통함과, 죽음 앞에서의 그때와 똑같은 공포를, 다만 더 큰 정도로 느끼리라 예상했었다."], ["And he had prepared himself for this; but he found something utterly different.", "그래서 그는 거기에 대비해 마음의 준비를 해 두었었다. 그러나 그가 발견한 것은 전혀 다른 것이었다."]], [["In a little dirty room with the painted panels of its walls filthy with spittle, and conversation audible through the thin partition from the next room, in a stifling atmosphere saturated with impurities, on a bedstead moved away from the wall, there lay covered with a quilt, a body.", "벽의 칠한 판자가 가래침으로 더러워지고, 얇은 칸막이 너머 옆방의 이야기 소리가 들려오는, 온갖 더러운 냄새로 숨 막히게 찌든 작고 지저분한 방 안, 벽에서 떼어 놓은 침대 위에, 누비이불을 덮은 채 한 육체가 누워 있었다."], ["One arm of this body was above the quilt, and the wrist, huge as a rake-handle, was attached, inconceivably it seemed, to the thin, long bone of the arm smooth from the beginning to the middle.", "그 육체의 한 팔은 이불 밖으로 나와 있었고, 갈퀴 자루만큼 거대한 손목이, 도무지 믿기지 않게도, 처음부터 가운데까지 매끈한 가늘고 긴 팔뼈에 붙어 있었다."], ["The head lay sideways on the pillow.", "머리는 베개 위에 옆으로 뉘어 있었다."], ["Levin could see the scanty locks wet with sweat on the temples and tense, transparent-looking forehead.", "레빈은 관자놀이와 팽팽하게 당겨진 투명해 보이는 이마에 땀으로 젖어 들러붙은 성긴 머리카락을 볼 수 있었다."]], [["“It cannot be that that fearful body was my brother Nikolay?”", "'저 무시무시한 육체가 내 형 니콜라이일 리가 없어.'"], ["thought Levin.", "레빈은 생각했다."], ["But he went closer, saw the face, and doubt became impossible.", "그러나 그는 더 가까이 다가가 얼굴을 보았고, 의심은 더 이상 불가능해졌다."], ["In spite of the terrible change in the face, Levin had only to glance at those eager eyes raised at his approach, only to catch the faint movement of the mouth under the sticky mustache, to realize the terrible truth that this death-like body was his living brother.", "얼굴이 끔찍하게 변해 버렸음에도, 레빈은 자기가 다가가자 들어 올린 그 형형한 눈을 한 번 바라보기만 해도, 끈적이는 콧수염 아래 입가의 희미한 움직임을 한 번 포착하기만 해도, 이 시체 같은 육체가 살아 있는 자기 형이라는 끔찍한 진실을 깨달을 수 있었다."]], [["The glittering eyes looked sternly and reproachfully at his brother as he drew near.", "번득이는 눈은 그가 다가가는 동안 형제를 엄하고 책망하는 듯한 눈빛으로 바라보았다."], ["And immediately this glance established a living relationship between living men.", "그리고 그 눈길이 곧바로 살아 있는 사람들 사이에 살아 있는 관계를 맺어 주었다."], ["Levin immediately felt the reproach in the eyes fixed on him, and felt remorse at his own happiness.", "레빈은 곧바로 자기에게 박힌 눈 속의 책망을 느꼈고, 자기 자신의 행복에 대한 회한을 느꼈다."]], [["When Konstantin took him by the hand, Nikolay smiled.", "콘스탄틴이 그의 손을 잡자, 니콜라이는 미소를 지었다."], ["The smile was faint, scarcely perceptible, and in spite of the smile the stern expression of the eyes was unchanged.", "미소는 희미했고 거의 알아챌 수 없을 정도였으며, 그 미소에도 불구하고 눈의 엄한 표정은 변함이 없었다."]], [["“You did not expect to find me like this,”", "\"나를 이런 꼴로 보게 될 줄은 몰랐겠지.\""], ["he articulated with effort.", "그가 힘겹게 말을 내뱉었다."]], [["“Yes ... no,”", "\"그래……"], ["said Levin, hesitating over his words.", "아니.\" 레빈이 말을 더듬으며 말했다."], ["“How was it you didn’t let me know before, that is, at the time of my wedding?", "\"어쩌자고 진작, 그러니까 내 결혼식 때라도 알려 주지 않았어?"], ["I made inquiries in all directions.”", "사방으로 수소문을 했는데.\""]], [["He had to talk so as not to be silent, and he did not know what to say, especially as his brother made no reply, and simply stared without dropping his eyes, and evidently penetrated to the inner meaning of each word.", "그는 침묵하지 않으려고 말을 해야 했으나, 무슨 말을 해야 할지 알 수 없었으니, 특히 형이 아무 대꾸도 없이 그저 눈을 떼지 않고 빤히 쳐다보며, 분명 말 한마디 한마디의 속뜻까지 꿰뚫어 보고 있었기 때문이었다."], ["Levin told his brother that his wife had come with him.", "레빈은 형에게 아내가 함께 왔다고 말했다."], ["Nikolay expressed pleasure, but said he was afraid of frightening her by his condition.", "니콜라이는 기쁜 기색을 보였으나, 자기 몰골로 그녀를 놀라게 할까 봐 두렵다고 말했다."], ["A silence followed.", "침묵이 뒤따랐다."], ["Suddenly Nikolay stirred, and began to say something.", "갑자기 니콜라이가 몸을 움직이며 무언가 말을 꺼내기 시작했다."], ["Levin expected something of peculiar gravity and importance from the expression of his face, but Nikolay began speaking of his health.", "레빈은 그 얼굴 표정으로 보아 무언가 각별히 무겁고 중요한 말을 기대했으나, 니콜라이는 자기 건강에 대해 이야기하기 시작했다."], ["He found fault with the doctor, regretting he had not a celebrated Moscow doctor.", "그는 의사를 탓하며, 유명한 모스크바 의사를 두지 못한 것을 아쉬워했다."], ["Levin saw that he still hoped.", "레빈은 형이 아직도 희망을 품고 있음을 보았다."]], [["Seizing the first moment of silence, Levin got up, anxious to escape, if only for an instant, from his agonizing emotion, and said that he would go and fetch his wife.", "침묵의 첫 순간을 붙잡아, 레빈은 잠시만이라도 자기를 괴롭히는 감정에서 벗어나고 싶은 마음에 자리에서 일어나, 가서 아내를 데려오겠다고 말했다."]], [["“Very well, and I’ll tell her to tidy up here.", "\"좋아, 그럼 그 애한테 여기 좀 치우라고 일러야겠다."], ["It’s dirty and stinking here, I expect.", "여긴 더럽고 냄새가 날 테니까."], ["Marya!", "마리야!"], ["clear up the room,”", "방 좀 치워.\""], ["the sick man said with effort.", "병자가 힘겹게 말했다."], ["“Oh, and when you’ve cleared up, go away yourself,”", "\"아, 그리고 다 치우거든 너는 나가 있어.\""], ["he added, looking inquiringly at his brother.", "그가 형을 캐묻듯 바라보며 덧붙였다."]], [["Levin made no answer.", "레빈은 아무 대답도 하지 않았다."], ["Going out into the corridor, he stopped short.", "복도로 나오다가 그는 우뚝 멈춰 섰다."], ["He had said he would fetch his wife, but now, taking stock of the emotion he was feeling, he decided that he would try on the contrary to persuade her not to go in to the sick man.", "그는 아내를 데려오겠다고 말했으나, 이제 자기가 느끼는 감정을 헤아려 보고는, 오히려 그녀가 병자에게 들어가지 않도록 설득해 보리라 마음먹었다."], ["“Why should she suffer as I am suffering?”", "'내가 괴로워하는 것처럼 저 사람까지 괴로워해야 할 이유가 어디 있어?'"], ["he thought.", "그는 생각했다."]], [["“Well, how is he?”", "\"그래, 형님은 어떠세요?\""], ["Kitty asked with a frightened face.", "키티가 겁에 질린 얼굴로 물었다."]], [["“Oh, it’s awful, it’s awful!", "\"아, 끔찍해, 끔찍하다고!"], ["What did you come for?”", "당신은 뭐하러 따라온 거야?\""], ["said Levin.", "레빈이 말했다."]], [["Kitty was silent for a few seconds, looking timidly and ruefully at her husband; then she went up and took him by the elbow with both hands.", "키티는 몇 초간 잠자코, 겁먹은 듯 안쓰러운 눈으로 남편을 바라보았다. 그러더니 다가와 두 손으로 그의 팔꿈치를 잡았다."]], [["“Kostya!", "\"코스챠!"], ["take me to him; it will be easier for us to bear it together.", "저를 형님에게 데려가 줘요. 함께라면 우리가 더 견디기 쉬울 거예요."], ["You only take me, take me to him, please, and go away,”", "저를 데려가 줘요, 형님에게 데려다만 주고 나가 있으면 돼요.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“You must understand that for me to see you, and not to see him, is far more painful.", "\"당신은 보면서 그분은 못 보는 게 저로선 훨씬 더 괴롭다는 걸 이해해 줘야 해요."], ["There I might be a help to you and to him.", "거기 가면 저는 당신에게도 그분에게도 도움이 될 수 있을 거예요."], ["Please, let me!”", "제발, 가게 해 줘요!\""], ["she besought her husband, as though the happiness of her life depended on it.", "그녀는 마치 자기 인생의 행복이 거기에 달려 있기라도 한 듯 남편에게 간청했다."]], [["Levin was obliged to agree, and regaining his composure, and completely forgetting about Marya Nikolaevna by now, he went again in to his brother with Kitty.", "레빈은 동의하지 않을 수 없었고, 평정을 되찾고 이제는 마리야 니콜라예브나에 대해서는 까맣게 잊은 채, 키티와 함께 다시 형에게 들어갔다."]], [["Stepping lightly, and continually glancing at her husband, showing him a valorous and sympathetic face, Kitty went into the sick-room, and, turning without haste, noiselessly closed the door.", "키티는 가벼운 걸음으로, 남편에게 용감하고 다정한 얼굴을 보이며 끊임없이 그를 흘끔거리면서 병실로 들어갔고, 서두르지 않고 돌아서서 소리 없이 문을 닫았다."], ["With inaudible steps she went quickly to the sick man’s bedside, and going up so that he had not to turn his head, she immediately clasped in her fresh young hand the skeleton of his huge hand, pressed it, and began speaking with that soft eagerness, sympathetic and not jarring, which is peculiar to women.", "들리지 않는 발걸음으로 그녀는 재빨리 병자의 침대맡으로 다가가, 그가 고개를 돌리지 않아도 되도록 가까이 가서, 곧바로 자기 싱그럽고 젊은 손으로 뼈만 남은 그의 거대한 손을 감싸 쥐고 가볍게 누르며, 여자 특유의 그 부드러운 열의로, 동정 어리되 거슬리지 않는 말투로 이야기를 시작했다."]], [["“We have met, though we were not acquainted, at Soden,”", "\"우리, 서로 모르는 사이였지만 소덴에서 만난 적이 있어요.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“You never thought I was to be your sister?”", "\"제가 제수가 될 줄은 미처 생각 못 하셨죠?\""]], [["“You would not have recognized me?”", "\"나를 알아보지 못했을 텐데요?\""], ["he said, with a radiant smile at her entrance.", "그가 그녀가 들어선 것에 환하게 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["“Yes, I should.", "\"아니요, 알아봤을 거예요."], ["What a good thing you let us know!", "우리에게 알려 주시길 정말 잘하셨어요!"], ["Not a day has passed that Kostya has not mentioned you, and been anxious.”", "코스챠가 아주버님 이야기를 하지 않은 날이 하루도 없었고, 늘 걱정했답니다.\""]], [["But the sick man’s interest did not last long.", "그러나 병자의 관심은 오래가지 않았다."]], [["Before she had finished speaking, there had come back into his face the stern, reproachful expression of the dying man’s envy of the living.", "그녀가 말을 채 마치기도 전에, 그의 얼굴에는 산 자를 시샘하는 죽어 가는 사람 특유의 그 엄하고 책망하는 듯한 표정이 다시 떠올랐다."]], [["“I am afraid you are not quite comfortable here,”", "\"여기가 영 불편하실 것 같아요.\""], ["she said, turning away from his fixed stare, and looking about the room.", "그녀가 그의 붙박인 시선을 피해 방 안을 둘러보며 말했다. \"다른 방을 알아봐야겠어요.\""], ["“We must ask about another room,”", "그녀가 남편에게 말했다."], ["she said to her husband, “so that we might be nearer.”", "\"그래야 우리가 더 가까이 있을 수 있을 테니까요.\""]], [["# Chapter 18", "# Chapter 18"]], [["Levin could not look calmly at his brother; he could not himself be natural and calm in his presence.", "레빈은 형을 차분히 바라볼 수가 없었다. 형 앞에서 자신도 자연스럽고 침착하게 굴 수 없었다."], ["When he went in to the sick man, his eyes and his attention were unconsciously dimmed, and he did not see and did not distinguish the details of his brother’s position.", "그가 병자에게 들어설 때면 자기도 모르게 눈도 주의도 흐려졌고, 형의 처지에 관한 세세한 정황은 보이지도, 분간되지도 않았다."], ["He smelt the awful odor, saw the dirt, disorder, and miserable condition, and heard the groans, and felt that nothing could be done to help.", "끔찍한 냄새를 맡았고, 더러움과 어수선함과 비참한 상태를 보았으며, 신음을 들었고, 어떻게 해도 도울 길이 없다고 느꼈다."], ["It never entered his head to analyze the details of the sick man’s situation, to consider how that body was lying under the quilt, how those emaciated legs and thighs and spine were lying huddled up, and whether they could not be made more comfortable, whether anything could not be done to make things, if not better, at least less bad.", "병자의 상태를 세세히 따져 본다든지, 저 몸이 이불 아래에 어떻게 누워 있는지, 저 비쩍 마른 다리며 넓적다리며 등뼈가 어떻게 웅크린 채 놓여 있는지, 그것들을 좀 더 편하게 해 줄 수는 없는지, 사정을 더 낫게는 못 하더라도 적어도 덜 나쁘게라도 해 줄 무언가가 있지는 않은지 헤아려 본다는 것은 그의 머릿속에 떠오르지조차 않았다."], ["It made his blood run cold when he began to think of all these details.", "이 모든 세세한 것들을 생각하기 시작하면 피가 차갑게 식었다."], ["He was absolutely convinced that nothing could be done to prolong his brother’s life or to relieve his suffering.", "그는 형의 생명을 늘리거나 그 고통을 덜어 줄 길은 전혀 없다고 절대적으로 확신했다."], ["But a sense of his regarding all aid as out of the question was felt by the sick man, and exasperated him.", "그러나 그가 어떤 도움도 가망 없는 것으로 여긴다는 느낌은 병자에게도 전해졌고, 그것이 병자를 격분시켰다."], ["And this made it still more painful for Levin.", "그래서 레빈은 더욱더 괴로웠다."], ["To be in the sick-room was agony to him, not to be there still worse.", "병실 안에 있는 것이 그에게는 고문이었고, 거기 있지 않는 것은 더한 고문이었다."], ["And he was continually, on various pretexts, going out of the room, and coming in again, because he was unable to remain alone.", "그는 혼자 남아 있을 수가 없어, 끊임없이 갖가지 핑계를 대며 방을 나갔다가 다시 들어오곤 했다."]], [["But Kitty thought, and felt, and acted quite differently.", "그러나 키티는 생각하고 느끼고 행동하는 방식이 사뭇 달랐다."], ["On seeing the sick man, she pitied him.", "그녀는 병자를 보자 그가 가여웠다."], ["And pity in her womanly heart did not arouse at all that feeling of horror and loathing that it aroused in her husband, but a desire to act, to find out all the details of his state, and to remedy them.", "그리고 여자다운 그녀의 마음속에서 연민은 남편에게 불러일으킨 것과 같은 그런 공포와 혐오의 감정을 조금도 불러일으키지 않았다. 도리어 무언가를 하고 싶은 마음, 그의 상태를 세세한 데까지 다 알아내어 손을 쓰고 싶은 마음을 불러일으켰다."], ["And since she had not the slightest doubt that it was her duty to help him, she had no doubt either that it was possible, and immediately set to work.", "그리고 그를 돕는 것이 자기 의무임을 조금도 의심하지 않았기에, 그것이 가능하다는 것 또한 의심하지 않았고, 곧바로 일에 착수했다."], ["The very details, the mere thought of which reduced her husband to terror, immediately engaged her attention.", "그저 생각만으로도 남편을 공포에 빠뜨렸던 바로 그 세세한 일들이 곧장 그녀의 주의를 사로잡았다."], ["She sent for the doctor, sent to the chemist’s, set the maid who had come with her and Marya Nikolaevna to sweep and dust and scrub; she herself washed up something, washed out something else, laid something under the quilt.", "그녀는 의사를 부르러 사람을 보내고, 약국에도 사람을 보냈으며, 자기와 함께 온 하녀와 마리야 니콜라예브나에게 쓸고 닦고 문지르게 했다. 그녀 자신도 무언가를 씻고, 또 무언가를 헹구고, 이불 밑에 무언가를 깔아 주었다."], ["Something was by her directions brought into the sick-room, something else was carried out.", "그녀의 지시에 따라 무언가가 병실로 들여와지고, 또 다른 무언가는 밖으로 내가졌다."], ["She herself went several times to her room, regardless of the men she met in the corridor, got out and brought in sheets, pillow cases, towels, and shirts.", "그녀 자신도 복도에서 마주치는 남자들에 아랑곳하지 않고 몇 번이나 자기 방을 드나들며 시트며 베갯잇이며 수건이며 셔츠를 꺼내 들고 왔다."]], [["The waiter, who was busy with a party of engineers dining in the dining hall, came several times with an irate countenance in answer to her summons, and could not avoid carrying out her orders, as she gave them with such gracious insistence that there was no evading her.", "식당에서 식사하는 기사들의 일행을 시중드느라 바쁜 종업원은 그녀의 부름에 못마땅한 낯빛으로 몇 번이나 왔으나, 그녀가 어찌나 정중하면서도 단호하게 분부를 내리는지 빠져나갈 길이 없어 그녀의 명령을 따르지 않을 수 없었다."], ["Levin did not approve of all this; he did not believe it would be of any good to the patient.", "레빈은 이 모든 것이 못마땅했다. 그것이 환자에게 무슨 도움이 되리라고 믿지 않았다."], ["Above all, he feared the patient would be angry at it.", "무엇보다도 환자가 거기에 화를 낼까 봐 두려웠다."], ["But the sick man, though he seemed and was indifferent about it, was not angry, but only abashed, and on the whole as it were interested in what she was doing with him.", "그러나 병자는 겉으로도 그러했고 실제로도 무심한 듯했지만, 화를 내지 않았다. 다만 멋쩍어할 뿐이었고, 대체로 그녀가 자기를 데리고 하는 일에 흥미를 느끼는 것 같기도 했다."], ["Coming back from the doctor to whom Kitty had sent him, Levin, on opening the door, came upon the sick man at the instant when, by Kitty’s directions, they were changing his linen.", "키티가 보냈던 의사에게 다녀온 레빈이 문을 열다가, 마침 키티의 지시로 병자의 속옷을 갈아입히고 있는 바로 그 순간과 맞닥뜨렸다."], ["The long white ridge of his spine, with the huge, prominent shoulder blades and jutting ribs and vertebrae, was bare, and Marya Nikolaevna and the waiter were struggling with the sleeve of the night shirt, and could not get the long, limp arm into it.", "거대하게 도드라진 어깻죽지뼈며 불거진 갈비뼈와 등뼈가 달린, 길고 하얀 등줄기가 드러나 있었고, 마리야 니콜라예브나와 종업원은 잠옷 소매와 씨름하며 길고 축 늘어진 팔을 거기에 끼워 넣지 못하고 있었다."], ["Kitty, hurriedly closing the door after Levin, was not looking that way; but the sick man groaned, and she moved rapidly towards him.", "레빈이 들어온 뒤 황급히 문을 닫은 키티는 그쪽을 보고 있지 않았다. 그러나 병자가 신음을 내자 그녀는 잽싸게 그에게 다가갔다."]], [["“Make haste,”", "\"서둘러요.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“Oh, don’t you come,”", "\"아, 당신은 오지 마.\""], ["said the sick man angrily.", "병자가 화난 듯이 말했다."], ["“I’ll do it my myself....”", "\"내가 직접 할 테니…….\""]], [["“What say?”", "\"뭐라고 하셨어요?\""], ["queried Marya Nikolaevna.", "마리야 니콜라예브나가 물었다."], ["But Kitty heard and saw he was ashamed and uncomfortable at being naked before her.", "그러나 키티는 그가 자기 앞에서 벌거벗은 채 있는 것을 부끄러워하고 거북해한다는 것을 듣고 또 보았다."]], [["“I’m not looking, I’m not looking!”", "\"안 봐요, 안 봐.\""], ["she said, putting the arm in.", "그녀가 팔을 끼워 넣으며 말했다."], ["“Marya Nikolaevna, you come this side, you do it,”", "\"마리야 니콜라예브나, 당신은 이쪽으로 와서 해 줘요.\""], ["she added.", "그녀가 덧붙였다."]], [["“Please go for me, there’s a little bottle in my small bag,”", "\"미안하지만 좀 다녀와 줘요, 내 작은 가방 안에 자그마한 병이 하나 있어요.\" 그녀가 남편을 향해 돌아서며 말했다."], ["she said, turning to her husband, “you know, in the side pocket; bring it, please, and meanwhile they’ll finish clearing up here.”", "\"옆 주머니에 있는 거, 알죠. 그걸 가져다줘요, 그동안 여기는 이 사람들이 마저 정리할 테니까.\""]], [["Returning with the bottle, Levin found the sick man settled comfortably and everything about him completely changed.", "병을 가지고 돌아온 레빈은 병자가 편안히 자리를 잡고, 그를 둘러싼 모든 것이 완전히 바뀌어 있는 것을 보았다."], ["The heavy smell was replaced by the smell of aromatic vinegar, which Kitty with pouting lips and puffed-out, rosy cheeks was squirting through a little pipe.", "그 무거운 냄새는 향긋한 식초 냄새로 바뀌어 있었는데, 키티가 입술을 오므리고 발그레한 두 뺨을 부풀린 채 작은 관으로 그것을 뿜어내고 있었다."], ["There was no dust visible anywhere, a rug was laid by the bedside.", "어디에도 먼지 한 점 보이지 않았고, 침대 곁에는 깔개가 놓여 있었다."], ["On the table stood medicine bottles and decanters tidily arranged, and the linen needed was folded up there, and Kitty’s broderie anglaise.", "탁자 위에는 약병과 물병이 가지런히 정돈되어 있었고, 필요한 속옷들이 개켜져 있었으며, 키티의 broderie anglaise도 놓여 있었다."], ["On the other table by the patient’s bed there were candles and drink and powders.", "환자의 침대 곁 다른 탁자에는 양초와 마실 것과 가루약이 놓여 있었다."], ["The sick man himself, washed and combed, lay in clean sheets on high raised pillows, in a clean night-shirt with a white collar about his astoundingly thin neck, and with a new expression of hope looked fixedly at Kitty.", "씻기고 머리를 빗긴 병자 자신은 깨끗한 시트 위에, 높이 괸 베개에 기대 누워 있었으니, 놀랍도록 야윈 목둘레에 하얀 깃이 달린 깨끗한 잠옷을 입은 채, 새로운 희망의 표정을 띠고 키티를 뚫어지게 바라보았다."]], [["The doctor brought by Levin, and found by him at the club, was not the one who had been attending Nikolay Levin, as the patient was dissatisfied with him.", "* broderie anglaise: 프랑스어로 '영국식 자수'. 구멍을 뚫어 가장자리를 감친 흰 무명 자수."], ["The new doctor took up a stethoscope and sounded the patient, shook his head, prescribed medicine, and with extreme minuteness explained first how to take the medicine and then what diet was to be kept to.", "레빈이 클럽에서 찾아 데려온 의사는, 그동안 니콜라이 레빈을 진찰해 온 그 의사가 아니었다. 환자가 그 의사에게 불만이 있었기 때문이다."], ["He advised eggs, raw or hardly cooked, and seltzer water, with warm milk at a certain temperature.", "새 의사는 청진기를 들고 환자의 가슴 소리를 들어 보더니 고개를 가로젓고 약을 처방했으며, 더없이 세세하게 먼저 약을 먹는 법을, 그다음에는 어떤 식이요법을 지켜야 하는지를 일러 주었다."], ["When the doctor had gone away the sick man said something to his brother, of which Levin could distinguish only the last words: “Your Katya.”", "날달걀이나 살짝 익힌 달걀과 셀처 탄산수, 그리고 일정한 온도로 데운 따뜻한 우유를 권했다. 의사가 가고 나자 병자가 동생에게 무언가 말했는데, 레빈이 알아들은 것이라고는 마지막 말뿐이었다."], ["By the expression with which he gazed at her, Levin saw that he was praising her.", "\"네 카탸 말이야.\" 형이 그녀를 바라보는 표정으로 보아, 레빈은 형이 그녀를 칭찬하고 있음을 알았다."], ["He called indeed to Katya, as he called her.", "사실 형은 카탸를, 자기가 부르는 그 이름으로 불렀다."]], [["“I’m much better already,”", "\"벌써 훨씬 좋아졌어.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“Why, with you I should have got well long ago.", "\"그래, 네가 곁에 있었더라면 난 진작 다 나았을 텐데."], ["How nice it is!”", "정말 좋구나!\""], ["he took her hand and drew it towards his lips, but as though afraid she would dislike it he changed his mind, let it go, and only stroked it.", "그는 그녀의 손을 잡아 자기 입술로 끌어당겼으나, 그녀가 싫어할까 봐 두려운 듯 마음을 바꾸어 손을 놓고는 그저 쓰다듬기만 했다."], ["Kitty took his hand in both hers and pressed it.", "키티는 두 손으로 그의 손을 잡아 꼭 눌렀다."]], [["“Now turn me over on the left side and go to bed,”", "\"이제 나를 왼쪽으로 돌려 눕히고 자러들 가.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["No one could make out what he said but Kitty; she alone understood.", "키티 말고는 아무도 그가 하는 말을 알아듣지 못했다. 그녀만이 이해했다."], ["She understood because she was all the while mentally keeping watch on what he needed.", "그가 무엇을 필요로 하는지 줄곧 마음속으로 살피고 있었기에 알아들은 것이다."]], [["“On the other side,”", "\"반대편으로요.\""], ["she said to her husband, “he always sleeps on that side.", "그녀가 남편에게 말했다. \"형님은 늘 저쪽으로 주무세요."], ["Turn him over, it’s so disagreeable calling the servants.", "돌려 눕혀 드려요, 하인들을 부르는 건 너무 번거로우니까. 나는 힘이 모자라요."], ["I’m not strong enough.", "당신은 할 수 있죠?\""], ["Can you?” she said to Marya Nikolaevna.", "그녀가 마리야 니콜라예브나에게 말했다."]], [["“I’m afraid not,”", "\"아무래도 못 할 것 같아요.\""], ["answered Marya Nikolaevna.", "마리야 니콜라예브나가 대답했다."]], [["Terrible as it was to Levin to put his arms round that terrible body, to take hold of that under the quilt, of which he preferred to know nothing, under his wife’s influence he made his resolute face that she knew so well, and putting his arms into the bed took hold of the body, but in spite of his own strength he was struck by the strange heaviness of those powerless limbs.", "저 끔찍한 몸뚱이를 두 팔로 끌어안는 것이, 차라리 아무것도 모르고 싶었던 이불 밑의 그것을 붙드는 것이 레빈에게는 끔찍했지만, 아내의 영향 아래 그는 그녀가 익히 아는 그 단호한 얼굴을 짓고는, 두 팔을 침대 안으로 넣어 몸을 붙들었다. 그러나 자기 딴에는 힘깨나 쓰는데도, 그 맥없는 사지의 기이한 무거움에 그는 놀라고 말았다."], ["While he was turning him over, conscious of the huge emaciated arm about his neck, Kitty swiftly and noiselessly turned the pillow, beat it up and settled in it the sick man’s head, smoothing back his hair, which was sticking again to his moist brow.", "형을 돌려 눕히는 동안, 자기 목을 휘감은 거대하게 야윈 팔을 느끼고 있을 때, 키티는 잽싸고 소리 없이 베개를 뒤집어 두드려 부풀린 다음 병자의 머리를 그 위에 누이고, 축축한 이마에 다시 들러붙은 그의 머리카락을 쓸어 넘겼다."]], [["The sick man kept his brother’s hand in his own.", "병자는 동생의 손을 자기 손으로 붙들고 있었다."], ["Levin felt that he meant to do something with his hand and was pulling it somewhere.", "레빈은 형이 자기 손으로 무언가를 하려 하며 어딘가로 끌어당기고 있음을 느꼈다."], ["Levin yielded with a sinking heart: yes, he drew it to his mouth and kissed it.", "레빈은 가슴이 내려앉은 채 손을 내맡겼다. 그러자 형은 그것을 자기 입으로 끌어당겨 입을 맞추었다."], ["Levin, shaking with sobs and unable to articulate a word, went out of the room.", "흐느낌에 몸을 떨며 한마디도 내뱉지 못한 채, 레빈은 방을 나갔다."]], [["# Chapter 19", "# Chapter 19"]], [["“Thou hast hid these things from the wise and prudent, and hast revealed them unto babes.”", "\"주께서 이것을 지혜롭고 슬기 있는 자들에게는 숨기시고 어린아이들에게는 나타내셨나이다.\""], ["So Levin thought about his wife as he talked to her that evening.", "그날 저녁 아내와 이야기를 나누며 레빈은 그녀를 두고 그렇게 생각했다."]], [["Levin thought of the text, not because he considered himself “wise and prudent.”", "레빈이 이 구절을 떠올린 것은 자기 자신을 \"지혜롭고 슬기 있는 자\"라고 여겼기 때문이 아니었다."], ["He did not so consider himself, but he could not help knowing that he had more intellect than his wife and Agafea Mihalovna, and he could not help knowing that when he thought of death, he thought with all the force of his intellect.", "그는 스스로를 그렇게 생각하지 않았지만, 자기가 아내나 아가피야 미하일로브나보다 지력이 뛰어나다는 것을 모를 수 없었고, 또 죽음에 대해 생각할 때면 자기 지력을 총동원해서 생각한다는 것도 모를 수 없었다."], ["He knew too that the brains of many great men, whose thoughts he had read, had brooded over death and yet knew not a hundredth part of what his wife and Agafea Mihalovna knew about it.", "그는 또한, 자기가 그 사상을 읽어 본 수많은 위대한 인물들의 두뇌가 죽음을 두고 깊이 골몰했음에도, 죽음에 관해 자기 아내와 아가피야 미하일로브나가 아는 것의 백분의 일도 알지 못한다는 것도 알고 있었다."], ["Different as those two women were, Agafea Mihalovna and Katya, as his brother Nikolay had called her, and as Levin particularly liked to call her now, they were quite alike in this.", "아가피야 미하일로브나와, 형 니콜라이가 부르던 이름이자 이제 레빈도 특히 즐겨 부르게 된 카탸, 이 두 여인은 서로 그렇게 다르면서도 이 점에서는 완전히 똑같았다."], ["Both knew, without a shade of doubt, what sort of thing life was and what was death, and though neither of them could have answered, and would even not have understood the questions that presented themselves to Levin, both had no doubt of the significance of this event, and were precisely alike in their way of looking at it, which they shared with millions of people.", "둘 다 추호의 의심도 없이 삶이란 어떤 것이며 죽음이란 무엇인지를 알고 있었고, 비록 둘 중 누구도 레빈에게 떠오른 물음들에 답할 수 없었을뿐더러 그 물음을 이해조차 못 했겠지만, 둘 다 이 사건의 의미에 대해 아무 의심이 없었으며, 수백만 사람들과 함께 나누는 그 시선으로 죽음을 바라보는 방식이 정확히 똑같았다."], ["The proof that they knew for a certainty the nature of death lay in the fact that they knew without a second of hesitation how to deal with the dying, and were not frightened of them.", "그들이 죽음의 본질을 확실히 알고 있다는 증거는, 그들이 한순간의 망설임도 없이 죽어 가는 사람을 어떻게 대해야 할지 알았고 또 그들을 무서워하지 않았다는 사실에 있었다."], ["Levin and other men like him, though they could have said a great deal about death, obviously did not know this since they were afraid of death, and were absolutely at a loss what to do when people were dying.", "레빈과 그와 같은 다른 사람들은, 죽음에 관해 많은 것을 말할 수는 있었지만, 죽음을 두려워하고 사람들이 죽어 갈 때 무엇을 해야 할지 도무지 알지 못한다는 점에서 분명 이를 알지 못했다."], ["If Levin had been alone now with his brother Nikolay, he would have looked at him with terror, and with still greater terror waited, and would not have known what else to do.", "만약 지금 레빈이 형 니콜라이와 단둘이 있었다면, 그는 공포에 질려 형을 바라보며, 더한 공포 속에서 무언가를 기다렸을 것이고, 그 밖에 무엇을 해야 할지 알지 못했을 것이다."]], [["More than that, he did not know what to say, how to look, how to move.", "그뿐만 아니라, 그는 무슨 말을 해야 할지, 어떤 표정을 지어야 할지, 어떻게 움직여야 할지조차 알지 못했다."], ["To talk of outside things seemed to him shocking, impossible, to talk of death and depressing subjects—also impossible.", "엉뚱한 일을 이야기하는 것은 그에게 충격적이고 불가능해 보였고, 죽음이나 우울한 주제를 이야기하는 것도—마찬가지로 불가능했다."], ["To be silent, also impossible.", "침묵하는 것 역시 불가능했다."], ["“If I look at him he will think I am studying him, I am afraid; if I don’t look at him, he’ll think I’m thinking of other things.", "'내가 형을 쳐다보면, 형은 내가 자기를 관찰한다고, 내가 무서워한다고 생각하겠지. 쳐다보지 않으면, 내가 딴생각을 한다고 여길 거야."], ["If I walk on tiptoe, he will be vexed; to tread firmly, I’m ashamed.”", "발끝으로 걸으면 형은 언짢아할 테고, 쿵쿵 걸으면 내가 부끄러워.'"], ["Kitty evidently did not think of herself, and had no time to think about herself: she was thinking about him because she knew something, and all went well.", "키티는 분명 자기 자신을 생각하지 않았고, 자기를 생각할 겨를도 없었다. 그녀는 무언가를 알고 있었기에 형을 생각했고, 그래서 모든 일이 잘 풀렸다."], ["She told him about herself even and about her wedding, and smiled and sympathized with him and petted him, and talked of cases of recovery and all went well; so then she must know.", "그녀는 형에게 자기 이야기며 자기 결혼식 이야기까지 들려주었고, 미소를 지으며 형을 안쓰러워하고 다정하게 어루만졌으며, 회복한 사례들을 이야기했고, 그렇게 모든 것이 잘 풀려 나갔다."], ["The proof that her behavior and Agafea Mihalovna’s was not instinctive, animal, irrational, was that apart from the physical treatment, the relief of suffering, both Agafea Mihalovna and Kitty required for the dying man something else more important than the physical treatment, and something which had nothing in common with physical conditions.", "그러니 그녀는 알고 있는 게 틀림없었다. 그녀와 아가피야 미하일로브나의 행동이 본능적이고 동물적이며 비이성적인 것이 아니라는 증거는, 신체적 처치와 고통의 완화 말고도 아가피야 미하일로브나와 키티 둘 다 죽어 가는 사람에게 신체적 처치보다 더 중요한 무언가를, 신체적 조건과는 아무 상관없는 무언가를 필요로 했다는 데 있었다."], ["Agafea Mihalovna, speaking of the man just dead, had said: “Well, thank God, he took the sacrament and received absolution; God grant each one of us such a death.”", "아가피야 미하일로브나는 방금 죽은 사람을 두고 이렇게 말한 적이 있었다. \"그래도, 다행이지요, 그분이 성찬을 받고 죄 사함도 받았으니."], ["Katya in just the same way, besides all her care about linen, bedsores, drink, found time the very first day to persuade the sick man of the necessity of taking the sacrament and receiving absolution.", "하느님께서 우리 모두에게 그런 죽음을 내려 주시기를.\" 카탸도 똑같이, 시트며 욕창이며 마실 것에 온갖 신경을 쓰는 와중에도, 바로 첫날에 짬을 내어 병자에게 성찬을 받고 죄 사함을 받아야 한다고 설득했다."]], [["On getting back from the sick-room to their own two rooms for the night, Levin sat with hanging head not knowing what to do.", "밤을 보내려고 병실에서 자기들 방 두 칸으로 돌아온 레빈은, 무엇을 해야 할지 모른 채 고개를 떨구고 앉아 있었다."], ["Not to speak of supper, of preparing for bed, of considering what they were going to do, he could not even talk to his wife; he was ashamed to.", "저녁 식사며 잠자리 준비며 앞으로 무엇을 할지 궁리하는 것은 말할 것도 없고, 아내에게 말을 거는 것조차 할 수 없었다."], ["Kitty, on the contrary, was more active than usual.", "부끄러웠던 것이다. 반대로 키티는 평소보다 더 활기찼다."], ["She was even livelier than usual.", "평소보다 더 생기마저 넘쳤다."], ["She ordered supper to be brought, herself unpacked their things, and herself helped to make the beds, and did not even forget to sprinkle them with Persian powder.", "그녀는 저녁 식사를 가져오게 하고, 손수 짐을 풀었으며, 침대를 정돈하는 것도 거들었고, 페르시아 가루를 뿌리는 일조차 잊지 않았다."], ["She showed that alertness, that swiftness of reflection which comes out in men before a battle, in conflict, in the dangerous and decisive moments of life—those moments when a man shows once and for all his value, and that all his past has not been wasted but has been a preparation for these moments.", "그녀는 전투를 앞둔 사내들에게서, 다툼 속에서, 삶의 위태롭고 결정적인 순간들에서 드러나는 그 민첩함, 그 재빠른 판단력을 보여 주었다—사람이 단번에 자기 가치를 드러내고, 자기의 모든 과거가 헛되지 않았으며 바로 이 순간들을 위한 준비였음을 보여 주는 그런 순간들 말이다."]], [["Everything went rapidly in her hands, and before it was twelve o’clock all their things were arranged cleanly and tidily in her rooms, in such a way that the hotel rooms seemed like home: the beds were made, brushes, combs, looking-glasses were put out, table napkins were spread.", "그녀의 손에서는 모든 일이 척척 진행되어, 열두 시가 되기도 전에 모든 물건이 그녀의 방에 깨끗하고 단정하게 정돈되었고, 호텔 방이 마치 집처럼 보일 정도였다. 침대가 정돈되었고, 솔과 빗과 거울이 꺼내져 놓였으며, 식탁용 냅킨이 펼쳐졌다."]], [["Levin felt that it was unpardonable to eat, to sleep, to talk even now, and it seemed to him that every movement he made was unseemly.", "레빈은 지금 같은 때에 먹고 자고 말하는 것조차 용서받을 수 없는 일이라고 느꼈고, 자기가 하는 동작 하나하나가 부적절하게 여겨졌다."], ["She arranged the brushes, but she did it all so that there was nothing shocking in it.", "그녀는 솔을 정리했지만, 그 모든 것을 충격적인 데가 조금도 없게 해냈다."]], [["They could neither of them eat, however, and for a long while they could not sleep, and did not even go to bed.", "그러나 두 사람은 아무것도 먹지 못했고, 오랫동안 잠을 이루지도 못했으며, 잠자리에 들지조차 않았다."]], [["“I am very glad I persuaded him to receive extreme unction tomorrow,”", "\"내일 형님께 종부성사를 받으시도록 설득해서 정말 기뻐요.\""], ["she said, sitting in her dressing jacket before her folding looking-glass, combing her soft, fragrant hair with a fine comb.", "그녀가 접이식 거울 앞에 잠옷 윗도리 차림으로 앉아, 부드럽고 향기로운 머리카락을 가는 빗으로 빗으며 말했다."], ["“I have never seen it, but I know, mamma has told me, there are prayers said for recovery.”", "\"저는 한 번도 본 적은 없지만, 알아요, 엄마가 말씀해 주셨거든요, 회복을 비는 기도가 있다는 걸요.\""]], [["“Do you suppose he can possibly recover?”", "\"형이 회복할 수 있다고 생각해?\""], ["said Levin, watching a slender tress at the back of her round little head that was continually hidden when she passed the comb through the front.", "레빈이, 그녀가 앞머리를 빗어 넘길 때마다 끊임없이 가려지곤 하는, 동그란 작은 머리 뒤편의 가느다란 머리채를 바라보며 말했다."]], [["“I asked the doctor; he said he couldn’t live more than three days.", "\"의사한테 물어봤어요. 사흘 넘게는 못 사실 거라고 하더군요."], ["But can they be sure?", "하지만 그분들이 어떻게 확신하겠어요?"], ["I’m very glad, anyway, that I persuaded him,”", "어쨌든, 형님을 설득해서 정말 기뻐요.\""], ["she said, looking askance at her husband through her hair.", "그녀가 머리카락 사이로 남편을 곁눈질하며 말했다."], ["“Anything is possible,”", "\"무슨 일이든 일어날 수 있는 거니까요.\""], ["she added with that peculiar, rather sly expression that was always in her face when she spoke of religion.", "그녀는 종교 이야기를 할 때면 늘 얼굴에 떠오르던 그 독특하고 다소 짓궂은 표정을 지으며 덧붙였다."]], [["Since their conversation about religion when they were engaged neither of them had ever started a discussion of the subject, but she performed all the ceremonies of going to church, saying her prayers, and so on, always with the unvarying conviction that this ought to be so. In spite of his assertion to the contrary, she was firmly persuaded that he was as much a Christian as she, and indeed a far better one; and all that he said about it was simply one of his absurd masculine freaks, just as he would say about her broderie anglaise that good people patch holes, but that she cut them on purpose, and so on.", "약혼 시절에 종교에 관해 나눈 그 대화 이후로 두 사람 중 누구도 이 주제로 논쟁을 시작한 적이 없었지만, 그녀는 교회에 가고 기도를 드리는 등의 온갖 의례를, 마땅히 그래야 한다는 변함없는 확신을 가지고 빠짐없이 행했다. 그가 아니라고 주장하는데도, 그녀는 그가 자기만큼이나 그리스도교도이며 사실은 훨씬 더 나은 그리스도교도라고 굳게 믿고 있었다. 그리고 그가 이에 관해 하는 모든 말은 그저 사내다운 터무니없는 변덕 가운데 하나일 뿐이라고 여겼는데, 그것은 마치 그가 그녀의 broderie anglaise를 두고 선량한 사람들은 구멍을 기우는 법인데 그녀는 일부러 구멍을 낸다는 둥 하는 말과 같은 것이었다. * broderie anglaise: 프랑스어로 '영국식 자수'. 천에 구멍을 뚫어 그 둘레를 감치는 방식의 흰 자수."]], [["“Yes, you see this woman, Marya Nikolaevna, did not know how to manage all this,”", "\"그래, 봐, 그 여자, 마리야 니콜라예브나는 이 모든 걸 어떻게 처리해야 할지 몰랐어.\""], ["said Levin.", "레빈이 말했다."], ["“And ...", "\"그리고……"], ["I must own I’m very, very glad you came.", "솔직히 말해서 당신이 와 줘서 정말, 정말 기뻐."], ["You are such purity that....”", "당신은 너무나 순수해서……\""], ["He took her hand and did not kiss it (to kiss her hand in such closeness to death seemed to him improper); he merely squeezed it with a penitent air, looking at her brightening eyes.", "그는 그녀의 손을 잡았지만 입을 맞추지는 않았다(죽음이 이토록 가까운 데서 그녀의 손에 입을 맞추는 것은 부적절해 보였다). 그는 그저 뉘우치는 듯한 태도로 손을 꼭 쥐며, 환하게 빛나는 그녀의 눈을 바라보았다."]], [["“It would have been miserable for you to be alone,”", "\"혼자 계셨다면 당신은 비참했을 거예요.\""], ["she said, and lifting her hands which hid her cheeks flushing with pleasure, twisted her coil of hair on the nape of her neck and pinned it there. “No,”", "그녀는 이렇게 말하고, 기쁨으로 발그레해진 두 뺨을 가리던 손을 들어 올려, 목덜미의 땋은 머리를 비틀어 올리고 거기에 핀을 꽂았다."], ["she went on, “she did not know how....", "\"아니에요,\" 그녀가 말을 이었다."], ["Luckily, I learned a lot at Soden.”", "\"그 여자는 어찌할 줄을 몰랐어요…… 다행히 저는 소덴에서 많이 배웠거든요.\""]], [["“Surely there are not people there so ill?” “Worse.”", "\"설마 거기에 그렇게 위중한 사람들이 있는 건 아니겠지?\""]], [["“What’s so awful to me is that I can’t see him as he was when he was young.", "\"더한 사람들도 있어요.\" \"나한테 가장 끔찍한 건, 형이 젊었을 때의 모습으로 형을 볼 수가 없다는 거야."], ["You would not believe how charming he was as a youth, but I did not understand him then.”", "젊었을 적에 형이 얼마나 매력적이었는지 당신은 믿지 못할 거야. 그런데 그때 나는 형을 이해하지 못했어.\""]], [["“I can quite, quite believe it.", "\"저는 정말, 정말 믿어요."], ["How I feel that we might have been friends!”", "우리가 친구가 될 수도 있었을 텐데, 그런 느낌이 들어요!\""], ["she said; and, distressed at what she had said, she looked round at her husband, and tears came into her eyes.", "그녀가 말했다. 그러고는 자기가 한 말에 마음이 아파, 남편을 돌아보았고, 두 눈에 눈물이 고였다."]], [["“Yes, might have been,”", "\"그래, 그럴 수도 있었을 텐데.\""], ["he said mournfully.", "그가 슬프게 말했다."], ["“He’s just one of those people of whom they say they’re not for this world.”", "\"형은 사람들이 이 세상 사람이 아니라고 말하는, 바로 그런 부류의 사람들 중 하나야.\""]], [["“But we have many days before us; we must go to bed,”", "\"하지만 우리 앞에는 많은 날이 남아 있어요. 이제 자야 해요.\""], ["said Kitty, glancing at her tiny watch.", "키티가 자기의 자그마한 시계를 흘긋 보며 말했다."]], [["# Chapter 20", "# Chapter 20"]], [["The next day the sick man received the sacrament and extreme unction.", "다음 날 병자는 성찬식과 종부성사를 받았다."], ["During the ceremony Nikolay Levin prayed fervently.", "의식이 진행되는 동안 니콜라이 레빈은 열렬히 기도했다."], ["His great eyes, fastened on the holy image that was set out on a card-table covered with a colored napkin, expressed such passionate prayer and hope that it was awful to Levin to see it.", "색깔 있는 냅킨이 덮인 카드 탁자 위에 세워 둔 성상을 응시하는 그의 커다란 두 눈에는 어찌나 간절한 기도와 희망이 어려 있던지, 레빈으로서는 그것을 보고 있기가 두려울 지경이었다."], ["Levin knew that this passionate prayer and hope would only make him feel more bitterly parting from the life he so loved.", "레빈은 이 간절한 기도와 희망이 그토록 사랑하던 삶과 헤어지는 일을 형에게 더 쓰라리게 느끼도록 만들 뿐이라는 것을 알고 있었다. 레빈은 형을 알았고 형의 지성이 작동하는 방식을 알았다."], ["Levin knew his brother and the workings of his intellect: he knew that his unbelief came not from life being easier for him without faith, but had grown up because step by step the contemporary scientific interpretation of natural phenomena crushed out the possibility of faith; and so he knew that his present return was not a legitimate one, brought about by way of the same working of his intellect, but simply a temporary, interested return to faith in a desperate hope of recovery.", "그의 불신앙이 신앙 없이 사는 편이 그에게 더 편해서가 아니라, 자연 현상에 대한 동시대의 과학적 해석이 한 걸음 한 걸음 신앙의 가능성을 짓밟아 없애 버렸기 때문에 자라난 것임을 그는 알고 있었다. 그래서 그는 형이 지금 신앙으로 되돌아온 것이 동일한 지성의 작동을 거쳐 이루어진 정당한 회귀가 아니라, 회복을 바라는 절망적인 희망 속에서 일시적으로, 이해타산적으로 신앙으로 돌아온 것임을 알고 있었다."], ["Levin knew too that Kitty had strengthened his hope by accounts of the marvelous recoveries she had heard of.", "레빈은 또한 키티가 자신이 들어 본 기적적인 회복 사례들을 이야기해 줌으로써 형의 희망을 더 부추겼다는 것도 알고 있었다."], ["Levin knew all this; and it was agonizingly painful to him to behold the supplicating, hopeful eyes and the emaciated wrist, lifted with difficulty, making the sign of the cross on the tense brow, and the prominent shoulders and hollow, gasping chest, which one could not feel consistent with the life the sick man was praying for.", "레빈은 이 모든 것을 알았다. 그래서 애원하듯 희망에 찬 두 눈과, 힘겹게 들어 올려 긴장된 이마에 십자를 긋는 앙상한 손목과, 불거진 어깨와 헐떡이는 움푹 꺼진 가슴—병자가 빌고 있는 그 삶과는 도무지 어울려 보이지 않는—을 바라보는 것이 그에게는 견딜 수 없이 괴로웠다."], ["During the sacrament Levin did what he, an unbeliever, had done a thousand times.", "성찬식이 진행되는 동안 레빈은, 불신자인 그가 수천 번도 더 해 왔던 그 일을 했다. 그는 하느님을 향해 이렇게 말했다."], ["He said, addressing God, “If Thou dost exist, make this man to recover” (of course this same thing has been repeated many times), “and Thou wilt save him and me.”", "\"당신이 존재하신다면, 이 사람을 회복시켜 주소서\"(물론 이와 똑같은 말은 이미 여러 번 되풀이된 것이었다) \"그러면 당신은 그도 저도 구원하시는 겁니다.\""]], [["After extreme unction the sick man became suddenly much better.", "종부성사를 받은 뒤 병자는 별안간 훨씬 좋아졌다."], ["He did not cough once in the course of an hour, smiled, kissed Kitty’s hand, thanking her with tears, and said he was comfortable, free from pain, and that he felt strong and had an appetite.", "한 시간 내내 한 번도 기침을 하지 않았고, 미소를 지었으며, 키티의 손에 입을 맞추고는 눈물을 흘리며 고맙다고 했고, 편안하고 아프지 않으며 기운이 나고 입맛도 돈다고 말했다."], ["He even raised himself when his soup was brought, and asked for a cutlet as well.", "수프가 들어왔을 때는 몸을 일으키기까지 했고 커틀릿도 달라고 했다."], ["Hopelessly ill as he was, obvious as it was at the first glance that he could not recover, Levin and Kitty were for that hour both in the same state of excitement, happy, though fearful of being mistaken.", "회복할 가망이 전혀 없는 상태였고, 한눈에 봐도 그가 낫지 못하리라는 것이 분명했건만, 레빈과 키티는 그 한 시간 동안 둘 다 똑같은 흥분 상태에 빠져, 행복해하면서도 혹시 잘못 생각한 게 아닐까 두려워했다."]], [["“Is he better?” “Yes, much.”", "\"형 좀 나아졌어요?\""]], [["“It’s wonderful.”", "\"그래요, 많이요.\""]], [["“There’s nothing wonderful in it.”", "\"놀라운 일이에요.\" \"놀라울 것도 없죠.\""]], [["“Anyway, he’s better,”", "\"어쨌든 나아졌어요.\""], ["they said in a whisper, smiling to one another.", "두 사람은 서로에게 미소를 지으며 속삭이듯 말했다."]], [["This self-deception was not of long duration.", "이 자기기만은 오래가지 않았다."], ["The sick man fell into a quiet sleep, but he was waked up half an hour later by his cough.", "병자는 조용히 잠이 들었으나, 삼십 분 뒤 기침 때문에 깨어났다."], ["And all at once every hope vanished in those about him and in himself.", "그러자 단번에 주위 사람들에게서도, 그 자신에게서도 모든 희망이 사라져 버렸다."], ["The reality of his suffering crushed all hopes in Levin and Kitty and in the sick man himself, leaving no doubt, no memory even of past hopes.", "그가 겪는 고통의 현실은 레빈에게서도, 키티에게서도, 병자 자신에게서도 모든 희망을 짓밟아 버려, 의심의 여지도, 지난 희망의 기억조차도 남기지 않았다."]], [["Without referring to what he had believed in half an hour before, as though ashamed even to recall it, he asked for iodine to inhale in a bottle covered with perforated paper.", "그는 삼십 분 전에 자기가 믿었던 것을, 마치 떠올리기조차 부끄럽다는 듯 한마디도 언급하지 않은 채, 구멍이 뚫린 종이로 감싼 병에 든 요오드를 흡입하게 해 달라고 했다."], ["Levin gave him the bottle, and the same look of passionate hope with which he had taken the sacrament was now fastened on his brother, demanding from him the confirmation of the doctor’s words that inhaling iodine worked wonders.", "레빈이 병을 건네주자, 성찬을 받을 때 떠올렸던 그 간절한 희망의 표정이 이번에는 동생을 향해 못 박힌 채, 요오드를 흡입하면 기적이 일어난다던 의사의 말이 사실임을 확인해 달라고 그에게 요구했다."]], [["“Is Katya not here?”", "\"카탸는 여기 없지?\""], ["he gasped, looking round while Levin reluctantly assented to the doctor’s words.", "그는 헐떡이며 주위를 둘러보았고, 레빈은 마지못해 의사의 말에 동의했다. \"없어."], ["“No; so I can say it....", "그러니 말할 수 있겠군……"], ["It was for her sake I went through that farce.", "그 코미디를 한 건 그 애를 위해서였어."], ["She’s so sweet; but you and I can’t deceive ourselves.", "그 앤 참 다정하지. 하지만 너와 나는 우리 자신을 속일 수가 없어."], ["This is what I believe in,”", "내가 믿는 건 바로 이거야.\""], ["he said, and, squeezing the bottle in his bony hand, he began breathing over it.", "그는 이렇게 말하고는, 앙상한 손으로 병을 움켜쥐고 그 위로 숨을 들이쉬기 시작했다."]], [["At eight o’clock in the evening Levin and his wife were drinking tea in their room when Marya Nikolaevna ran in to them breathlessly.", "저녁 여덟 시에 레빈과 그의 아내가 방에서 차를 마시고 있는데 마리야 니콜라예브나가 숨을 헐떡이며 그들에게 뛰어 들어왔다."], ["She was pale, and her lips were quivering.", "그녀는 창백했고 입술이 떨리고 있었다."], ["“He is dying!”", "\"돌아가시려고 해요!\""], ["she whispered.", "그녀가 속삭였다."], ["“I’m afraid will die this minute.”", "\"당장이라도 돌아가실까 봐 무서워요.\""]], [["Both of them ran to him.", "두 사람은 그에게로 달려갔다."], ["He was sitting raised up with one elbow on the bed, his long back bent, and his head hanging low.", "그는 한쪽 팔꿈치를 침대에 짚고 몸을 일으킨 채, 길쭉한 등을 굽히고 고개를 낮게 떨어뜨리고 앉아 있었다."]], [["“How do you feel?”", "\"좀 어때요?\""], ["Levin asked in a whisper, after a silence.", "레빈이 잠시 침묵한 뒤 속삭이듯 물었다."]], [["“I feel I’m setting off,”", "\"이제 떠나는 것 같아.\""], ["Nikolay said with difficulty, but with extreme distinctness, screwing the words out of himself.", "니콜라이는 힘겹게, 그러나 더없이 또렷하게, 한 마디 한 마디를 자기 안에서 짜내듯 말했다."], ["He did not raise his head, but simply turned his eyes upwards, without their reaching his brother’s face.", "그는 고개를 들지 않고 그저 눈만 위로 치떴는데, 그 눈길은 동생의 얼굴까지 닿지 못했다. \"카탸, 나가 있어라!\""], ["“Katya, go away!” he added.", "그가 덧붙였다."]], [["Levin jumped up, and with a peremptory whisper made her go out.", "레빈은 벌떡 일어나 단호한 속삭임으로 그녀를 나가게 했다."]], [["“I’m setting off,”", "\"이제 떠나.\""], ["he said again.", "그가 다시 말했다."]], [["“Why do you think so?”", "\"왜 그렇게 생각해요?\""], ["said Levin, so as to say something.", "레빈은 무슨 말이든 해야 했기에 물었다."]], [["“Because I’m setting off,”", "\"내가 떠나니까.\""], ["he repeated, as though he had a liking for the phrase.", "그는 마치 그 말이 마음에 든다는 듯 되풀이했다."], ["“It’s the end.”", "\"이게 끝이야.\""]], [["Marya Nikolaevna went up to him.", "마리야 니콜라예브나가 그에게 다가갔다."]], [["“You had better lie down; you’d be easier,”", "\"누워 계시는 게 좋겠어요. 그러면 한결 편하실 거예요.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“I shall lie down soon enough,”", "\"이제 곧 누울 텐데 뭐.\""], ["he pronounced slowly, “when I’m dead,”", "그는 천천히 말했다. \"내가 죽으면 말이야.\""], ["he said sarcastically, wrathfully.", "그가 빈정거리듯, 화난 듯 말했다."], ["“Well, you can lay me down if you like.”", "\"뭐, 좋다면 눕혀 주든지.\""]], [["Levin laid his brother on his back, sat down beside him, and gazed at his face, holding his breath.", "레빈은 형을 반듯이 눕히고 그 곁에 앉아, 숨을 죽인 채 형의 얼굴을 바라보았다."], ["The dying man lay with closed eyes, but the muscles twitched from time to time on his forehead, as with one thinking deeply and intensely.", "죽어 가는 사람은 눈을 감은 채 누워 있었으나, 깊고 격렬하게 생각에 잠긴 사람처럼 이따금 이마의 근육이 씰룩거렸다."], ["Levin involuntarily thought with him of what it was that was happening to him now, but in spite of all his mental efforts to go along with him he saw by the expression of that calm, stern face that for the dying man all was growing clearer and clearer that was still as dark as ever for Levin.", "레빈은 자기도 모르게 형과 함께 지금 형에게 일어나고 있는 일이 무엇인지를 생각했지만, 형을 따라가려고 온갖 정신적 노력을 기울였음에도, 그 고요하고 준엄한 얼굴의 표정을 보고 알 수 있었다. 레빈에게는 여전히 그 어느 때처럼 캄캄한 것이, 죽어 가는 사람에게는 점점 더 또렷해지고 있다는 것을."]], [["“Yes, yes, so,”", "\"그래, 그래, 그렇구나.\""], ["the dying man articulated slowly at intervals.", "죽어 가는 사람이 사이를 두고 천천히 또렷이 말했다."], ["“Wait a little.”", "\"조금만 기다려.\""], ["He was silent.", "그는 잠잠해졌다."], ["“Right!”", "\"옳지!\""], ["he pronounced all at once reassuringly, as though all were solved for him.", "그는 마치 모든 것이 풀리기라도 한 듯, 별안간 안심한 어조로 내뱉었다."], ["“O Lord!”", "\"오, 주여!\""], ["he murmured, and sighed deeply.", "그는 중얼거리고는 깊이 한숨을 내쉬었다."]], [["Marya Nikolaevna felt his feet.", "마리야 니콜라예브나가 그의 발을 만져 보았다."], ["“They’re getting cold,”", "\"차가워지고 있어요.\""], ["she whispered.", "그녀가 속삭였다."]], [["For a long while, a very long while it seemed to Levin, the sick man lay motionless.", "오랫동안, 레빈에게는 아주 오랜 시간처럼 느껴졌으나, 병자는 꼼짝도 않고 누워 있었다."], ["But he was still alive, and from time to time he sighed.", "하지만 그는 여전히 살아 있었고, 이따금 한숨을 내쉬었다."], ["Levin by now was exhausted from mental strain.", "이제 레빈은 정신적 긴장으로 기진맥진해 있었다."], ["He felt that, with no mental effort, could he understand what it was that was right.", "그는 정신적 노력을 기울이지 않고서는 무엇이 옳은지 이해할 수 없을 것 같았다."], ["He could not even think of the problem of death itself, but with no will of his own thoughts kept coming to him of what he had to do next; closing the dead man’s eyes, dressing him, ordering the coffin.", "그는 죽음이라는 문제 자체를 생각할 수조차 없었고, 자기 의지와는 무관하게 다음에 해야 할 일에 대한 생각이 자꾸만 떠올랐다. 죽은 사람의 눈을 감겨 주는 일, 옷을 입히는 일, 관을 주문하는 일."], ["And, strange to say, he felt utterly cold, and was not conscious of sorrow nor of loss, less still of pity for his brother.", "그리고 이상하게도, 그는 완전히 냉랭한 기분이었고, 슬픔도 상실감도 느끼지 못했으며, 형에 대한 연민은 더더욱 느끼지 못했다."], ["If he had any feeling for his brother at that moment, it was envy for the knowledge the dying man had now that he could not have.", "그 순간 형에 대해 어떤 감정이 있었다면, 그것은 죽어 가는 사람이 지금 가지고 있되 자기는 가질 수 없는 그 앎에 대한 부러움이었다."]], [["A long time more he sat over him so, continually expecting the end.", "그는 그렇게 한참 더, 끊임없이 마지막 순간을 기다리며 형 곁에 앉아 있었다."], ["But the end did not come.", "그러나 마지막은 오지 않았다."], ["The door opened and Kitty appeared.", "문이 열리고 키티가 나타났다."], ["Levin got up to stop her.", "레빈은 그녀를 막으려고 일어났다."], ["But at the moment he was getting up, he caught the sound of the dying man stirring.", "하지만 일어나는 순간, 그는 죽어 가는 사람이 몸을 움직이는 소리를 들었다."]], [["“Don’t go away,”", "\"가지 마라.\""], ["said Nikolay and held out his hand.", "니콜라이가 말하며 손을 내밀었다."], ["Levin gave him his, and angrily waved to his wife to go away.", "레빈은 자기 손을 내주고는, 화난 듯 아내에게 나가라고 손짓했다."]], [["With the dying man’s hand in his hand, he sat for half an hour, an hour, another hour.", "죽어 가는 사람의 손을 자기 손에 쥔 채, 그는 삼십 분, 한 시간, 또 한 시간을 앉아 있었다."], ["He did not think of death at all now.", "이제 그는 죽음에 대해서는 전혀 생각하지 않았다."], ["He wondered what Kitty was doing; who lived in the next room; whether the doctor lived in a house of his own.", "그는 키티가 무얼 하고 있을까, 옆방에는 누가 사나, 의사는 제 집을 가지고 있을까 따위를 궁금해했다."], ["He longed for food and for sleep.", "그는 음식과 잠을 갈망했다."], ["He cautiously drew away his hand and felt the feet.", "그는 조심스럽게 손을 빼서 발을 만져 보았다."], ["The feet were cold, but the sick man was still breathing.", "발은 차가웠으나 병자는 여전히 숨을 쉬고 있었다."], ["Levin tried again to move away on tiptoe, but the sick man stirred again and said: “Don’t go.”", "레빈은 다시 발끝으로 살그머니 물러나려 했지만, 병자는 또 몸을 움직이며 말했다. \"가지 마라.\""]], [["The dawn came; the sick man’s condition was unchanged.", "날이 밝아 왔다. 병자의 상태는 변함이 없었다."], ["Levin stealthily withdrew his hand, and without looking at the dying man, went off to his own room and went to sleep.", "레빈은 슬그머니 손을 빼고, 죽어 가는 사람을 쳐다보지도 않은 채 자기 방으로 가서 잠이 들었다."], ["When he woke up, instead of news of his brother’s death which he expected, he learned that the sick man had returned to his earlier condition.", "그가 깨어났을 때, 기대했던 형의 부고 대신, 병자가 이전 상태로 되돌아갔다는 소식을 들었다."], ["He had begun sitting up again, coughing, had begun eating again, talking again, and again had ceased to talk of death, again had begun to express hope of his recovery, and had become more irritable and more gloomy than ever.", "그는 다시 일어나 앉기 시작했고, 기침을 했고, 다시 음식을 먹기 시작했으며, 다시 말을 하기 시작했고, 다시 죽음에 대한 이야기를 그쳤으며, 다시 회복에 대한 희망을 입에 올리기 시작했고, 어느 때보다도 더 짜증을 부리고 더 침울해졌다."], ["No one, neither his brother nor Kitty, could soothe him.", "누구도, 동생도 키티도 그를 달랠 수 없었다."], ["He was angry with everyone, and said nasty things to everyone, reproached everyone for his sufferings, and insisted that they should get him a celebrated doctor from Moscow.", "그는 모두에게 화를 냈고, 모두에게 험한 말을 했으며, 자기 고통의 책임을 모두에게 돌렸고, 모스크바에서 유명한 의사를 데려와야 한다고 우겨 댔다."], ["To all inquiries made him as to how he felt, he made the same answer with an expression of vindictive reproachfulness, “I’m suffering horribly, intolerably!”", "좀 어떠냐는 온갖 물음에 그는 앙심에 찬 비난의 표정으로 똑같은 대답을 했다. \"끔찍하게 아파, 견딜 수가 없어!\""]], [["The sick man was suffering more and more, especially from bedsores, which it was impossible now to remedy, and grew more and more angry with everyone about him, blaming them for everything, and especially for not having brought him a doctor from Moscow.", "병자는 점점 더 심하게, 특히 이제는 어떻게 손쓸 수도 없는 욕창 때문에 괴로워했고, 주위의 모든 사람에게 점점 더 화를 냈으며, 모든 일을, 특히 모스크바에서 의사를 데려오지 않은 것을 그들 탓으로 돌렸다."], ["Kitty tried in every possible way to relieve him, to soothe him; but it was all in vain, and Levin saw that she herself was exhausted both physically and morally, though she would not admit it.", "키티는 가능한 모든 방법으로 그를 편하게 해 주고 달래려 했으나, 모두 헛수고였고, 레빈은 그녀가 인정하려 들지 않을 뿐, 육체적으로나 정신적으로나 기진맥진해 있다는 것을 알았다."], ["The sense of death, which had been evoked in all by his taking leave of life on the night when he had sent for his brother, was broken up.", "동생을 부르러 보냈던 그날 밤, 그가 삶과 작별함으로써 모두의 마음에 불러일으켜졌던 죽음의 감각은 흩어져 버렸다."], ["Everyone knew that he must inevitably die soon, that he was half dead already.", "누구나 그가 머지않아 죽을 수밖에 없다는 것을, 이미 반쯤 죽어 있다는 것을 알았다."], ["Everyone wished for nothing but that he should die as soon as possible, and everyone, concealing this, gave him medicines, tried to find remedies and doctors, and deceived him and themselves and each other.", "누구나 그가 한시라도 빨리 죽기만을 바랐고, 그러면서도 모두 그 마음을 감춘 채 그에게 약을 주고, 치료법과 의사를 찾으려 애쓰며, 그를 속이고 자기 자신을 속이고 서로를 속였다."], ["All this was falsehood, disgusting, irreverent deceit.", "이 모든 것은 거짓이었고, 역겹고 불경한 기만이었다."], ["And owing to the bent of his character, and because he loved the dying man more than anyone else did, Levin was most painfully conscious of this deceit.", "그리고 그의 성격상, 또 그가 누구보다도 죽어 가는 사람을 사랑했기에, 레빈은 이 기만을 누구보다도 고통스럽게 의식했다."]], [["Levin, who had long been possessed by the idea of reconciling his brothers, at least in face of death, had written to his brother, Sergey Ivanovitch, and having received an answer from him, he read this letter to the sick man.", "오래전부터 형제들을, 적어도 죽음을 앞두고서나마 화해시켜야겠다는 생각에 사로잡혀 있던 레빈은 형 세르게이 이바노비치에게 편지를 썼고, 그에게서 답장을 받자 그 편지를 병자에게 읽어 주었다."], ["Sergey Ivanovitch wrote that he could not come himself, and in touching terms he begged his brother’s forgiveness.", "세르게이 이바노비치는 자기가 직접 올 수는 없다고 쓰면서, 가슴을 울리는 말로 아우의 용서를 빌었다."]], [["The sick man said nothing.", "병자는 아무 말도 하지 않았다."]], [["“What am I to write to him?”", "\"형에게 뭐라고 답장을 써야 할까요?\""], ["said Levin.", "레빈이 말했다."], ["“I hope you are not angry with him?”", "\"형한테 화가 난 건 아니죠?\""]], [["“No, not the least!”", "\"아니, 조금도!\""], ["Nikolay answered, vexed at the question.", "니콜라이가 그 물음에 짜증을 내며 대답했다."], ["“Tell him to send me a doctor.”", "\"나한테 의사나 보내 달라고 해.\""]], [["Three more days of agony followed; the sick man was still in the same condition.", "사흘 더 고통의 날들이 이어졌다. 병자는 여전히 똑같은 상태였다."], ["The sense of longing for his death was felt by everyone now at the mere sight of him, by the waiters and the hotel-keeper and all the people staying in the hotel, and the doctor and Marya Nikolaevna and Levin and Kitty.", "이제는 그를 보기만 해도 모두가 그의 죽음을 바라는 마음을 느꼈다. 종업원들도, 호텔 주인도, 호텔에 묵는 모든 사람도, 의사도, 마리야 니콜라예브나도, 레빈도, 키티도 그러했다."], ["The sick man alone did not express this feeling, but on the contrary was furious at their not getting him doctors, and went on taking medicine and talking of life.", "오직 병자만이 그런 감정을 드러내지 않았고, 오히려 그들이 의사를 데려오지 않는다고 격분하며, 계속 약을 먹고 삶에 대해 이야기했다."], ["Only at rare moments, when the opium gave him an instant’s relief from the never-ceasing pain, he would sometimes, half asleep, utter what was ever more intense in his heart than in all the others: “Oh, if it were only the end!”", "다만 드물게, 아편이 끊임없는 고통으로부터 잠시나마 그를 놓아 주는 순간이면, 그는 반쯤 잠든 채, 다른 누구의 마음보다도 자기 마음속에서 더 강렬했던 것을 이따금 입 밖에 냈다. \"아, 차라리 끝이라면!\""], ["or: “When will it be over?”", "또는 \"언제나 끝이 날까?\""]], [["His sufferings, steadily growing more intense, did their work and prepared him for death.", "꾸준히 더 심해지는 그의 고통은 제 할 일을 다 하며 그를 죽음으로 준비시켜 갔다."], ["There was no position in which he was not in pain, there was not a minute in which he was unconscious of it, not a limb, not a part of his body that did not ache and cause him agony.", "아프지 않은 자세가 없었고, 고통을 의식하지 않는 순간이 없었으며, 쑤시지 않고 그를 괴롭히지 않는 팔다리나 신체 부위가 한 군데도 없었다."], ["Even the memories, the impressions, the thoughts of this body awakened in him now the same aversion as the body itself.", "이제는 이 육체에 대한 기억이며 인상이며 생각마저도 육체 그 자체와 똑같은 혐오감을 그의 안에 불러일으켰다."], ["The sight of other people, their remarks, his own reminiscences, everything was for him a source of agony.", "다른 사람들의 모습, 그들의 말, 자기 자신의 회상, 그 모든 것이 그에게는 고통의 원천이었다."], ["Those about him felt this, and instinctively did not allow themselves to move freely, to talk, to express their wishes before him.", "주위 사람들은 이를 느꼈고, 본능적으로 그의 앞에서는 자유로이 움직이거나 말하거나 자기 바람을 드러내지 않으려 했다."], ["All his life was merged in the one feeling of suffering and desire to be rid of it.", "그의 삶 전부가 고통이라는, 그리고 그것에서 벗어나고 싶다는 단 하나의 감정으로 녹아들어 있었다."]], [["There was evidently coming over him that revulsion that would make him look upon death as the goal of his desires, as happiness.", "그에게는 분명, 죽음을 자기 욕망의 목표로, 행복으로 바라보게 만드는 그 거부감이 차오르고 있었다."], ["Hitherto each individual desire, aroused by suffering or privation, such as hunger, fatigue, thirst, had been satisfied by some bodily function giving pleasure.", "지금까지는 고통이나 결핍—이를테면 배고픔, 피로, 갈증 같은 것—으로 일어난 개개의 욕망이 쾌감을 주는 어떤 신체 기능으로 충족되어 왔다."], ["But now no physical craving or suffering received relief, and the effort to relieve them only caused fresh suffering.", "그러나 이제는 어떤 육체적 욕구도 고통도 풀리지 않았고, 그것들을 풀어 보려는 노력은 도리어 새로운 고통만을 일으켰다."], ["And so all desires were merged in one—the desire to be rid of all his sufferings and their source, the body.", "그래서 모든 욕망은 하나로—자기의 모든 고통과 그 근원인 육체에서 벗어나고 싶다는 욕망으로 녹아들었다."], ["But he had no words to express this desire of deliverance, and so he did not speak of it, and from habit asked for the satisfaction of desires which could not now be satisfied.", "그러나 그에게는 이 해방의 욕망을 표현할 말이 없었고, 그래서 그것에 대해서는 말하지 않은 채, 습관대로 이제는 충족될 수 없는 욕망들의 충족을 청했다."], ["“Turn me over on the other side,”", "\"나를 다른 쪽으로 돌려 줘.\""], ["he would say, and immediately after he would ask to be turned back again as before.", "그는 이렇게 말하고는, 곧바로 다시 전처럼 돌려 달라고 청했다."], ["“Give me some broth.", "\"수프를 좀 다오."], ["Take away the broth.", "수프를 치워라."], ["Talk of something: why are you silent?”", "무슨 이야기든 해 봐. 왜들 잠자코 있는 거야?\""], ["And directly they began to talk he would close his eyes, and would show weariness, indifference, and loathing.", "그래서 사람들이 이야기를 시작하면, 그는 곧 눈을 감고 피로와 무관심과 혐오를 드러냈다."]], [["On the tenth day from their arrival at the town, Kitty was unwell.", "그들이 그 도시에 도착한 지 열흘째 되던 날, 키티가 몸이 좋지 않았다."], ["She suffered from headache and sickness, and she could not get up all the morning.", "그녀는 두통과 메스꺼움에 시달렸고, 아침 내내 일어나지 못했다."]], [["The doctor opined that the indisposition arose from fatigue and excitement, and prescribed rest.", "의사는 그 부진이 피로와 흥분에서 비롯된 것이라는 소견을 내고 안정을 처방했다."]], [["After dinner, however, Kitty got up and went as usual with her work to the sick man.", "그러나 점심 식사 후 키티는 일어나, 여느 때처럼 일감을 들고 병자에게 갔다."], ["He looked at her sternly when she came in, and smiled contemptuously when she said she had been unwell.", "그녀가 들어오자 그는 그녀를 준엄하게 바라보았고, 그녀가 몸이 좋지 않았다고 말하자 경멸하듯 미소를 지었다."], ["That day he was continually blowing his nose, and groaning piteously.", "그날 그는 줄곧 코를 풀어 댔고, 가엾게 신음했다."]], [["“How do you feel?”", "\"좀 어때요?\""], ["she asked him.", "그녀가 그에게 물었다."]], [["“Worse,”", "\"더 나빠.\""], ["he articulated with difficulty.", "그가 힘겹게 또렷이 말했다."], ["“In pain!”", "\"아파!\""]], [["“In pain, where?”", "\"아파요, 어디가요?\""]], [["“Everywhere.”", "\"온통.\""]], [["“It will be over today, you will see,”", "\"오늘 끝날 거예요, 두고 보세요.\""], ["said Marya Nikolaevna.", "마리야 니콜라예브나가 말했다."], ["Though it was said in a whisper, the sick man, whose hearing Levin had noticed was very keen, must have heard.", "속삭이듯 말했지만, 레빈이 보기에 청력이 무척 예민해진 병자는 분명 들었을 것이었다."], ["Levin said hush to her, and looked round at the sick man.", "레빈은 그녀에게 쉿 하고는, 병자를 돌아보았다."], ["Nikolay had heard; but these words produced no effect on him.", "니콜라이는 들었으나, 그 말은 그에게 아무런 영향도 주지 않았다."], ["His eyes had still the same intense, reproachful look.", "그의 눈에는 여전히 그 강렬하고 비난에 찬 눈빛이 어려 있었다."]], [["“Why do you think so?”", "\"왜 그렇게 생각해요?\""], ["Levin asked her, when she had followed him into the corridor.", "그녀가 그를 따라 복도로 나왔을 때 레빈이 물었다."]], [["“He has begun picking at himself,”", "\"자기 몸을 쥐어뜯기 시작했어요.\""], ["said Marya Nikolaevna.", "마리야 니콜라예브나가 말했다."]], [["“How do you mean?”", "\"그게 무슨 말이에요?\""]], [["“Like this,”", "\"이렇게요.\""], ["she said, tugging at the folds of her woolen skirt.", "그녀는 자기 모직 치마의 주름을 잡아당기며 말했다."], ["Levin noticed, indeed, that all that day the patient pulled at himself, as it were, trying to snatch something away.", "과연 레빈은 그날 하루 종일 환자가 무언가를 낚아채려는 듯 자기 몸을 잡아당기고 있었음을 알아차렸다."]], [["Marya Nikolaevna’s prediction came true.", "마리야 니콜라예브나의 예언은 들어맞았다."], ["Towards night the sick man was not able to lift his hands, and could only gaze before him with the same intensely concentrated expression in his eyes.", "밤이 가까워지자 병자는 손을 들어 올릴 수도 없게 되었고, 그저 한결같이 강렬하게 집중된 눈빛으로 앞만 응시할 수 있을 뿐이었다."], ["Even when his brother or Kitty bent over him, so that he could see them, he looked just the same.", "동생이나 키티가 그가 볼 수 있도록 그의 위로 몸을 굽혀도 그는 똑같은 눈빛이었다."], ["Kitty sent for the priest to read the prayer for the dying.", "키티는 임종 기도를 읽어 달라고 신부를 부르러 보냈다."]], [["While the priest was reading it, the dying man did not show any sign of life; his eyes were closed.", "신부가 기도를 읽는 동안, 죽어 가는 사람은 아무런 생기의 기미도 보이지 않았다. 그의 눈은 감겨 있었다."], ["Levin, Kitty, and Marya Nikolaevna stood at the bedside.", "레빈과 키티와 마리야 니콜라예브나가 침대 곁에 서 있었다."], ["The priest had not quite finished reading the prayer when the dying man stretched, sighed, and opened his eyes.", "신부가 기도를 채 끝맺기도 전에 죽어 가는 사람이 기지개를 켜고 한숨을 쉬더니 눈을 떴다."], ["The priest, on finishing the prayer, put the cross to the cold forehead, then slowly returned it to the stand, and after standing for two minutes more in silence, he touched the huge, bloodless hand that was turning cold.", "신부는 기도를 마치자 차가운 이마에 십자가를 갖다 댔다가 천천히 받침대에 도로 올려놓고는, 이 분쯤 더 잠자코 서 있다가, 차갑게 식어 가는 거대하고 핏기 없는 손을 만졌다."]], [["“He is gone,”", "\"운명하셨습니다.\""], ["said the priest, and would have moved away; but suddenly there was a faint stir in the mustaches of the dead man that seemed glued together, and quite distinctly in the hush they heard from the bottom of the chest the sharply defined sounds:", "신부가 말하고 자리를 뜨려 했다. 그런데 별안간, 서로 들러붙은 듯하던 죽은 사람의 콧수염이 희미하게 움찔하더니, 그 정적 속에서 가슴 밑바닥으로부터 또렷이 새어 나오는 분명한 소리가 들렸다."]], [["“Not quite ...", "\"아직……"], ["soon.”", "곧.\""]], [["And a minute later the face brightened, a smile came out under the mustaches, and the women who had gathered round began carefully laying out the corpse.", "그리고 일 분 뒤 얼굴이 환해지더니 콧수염 아래로 미소가 떠올랐고, 둘러서 있던 여자들이 조심스레 시신을 거두기 시작했다."]], [["The sight of his brother, and the nearness of death, revived in Levin that sense of horror in face of the insoluble enigma, together with the nearness and inevitability of death, that had come upon him that autumn evening when his brother had come to him.", "형의 모습과 죽음의 가까움은, 그해 가을 저녁 형이 그를 찾아왔을 때 그를 덮쳤던 그 감정—풀리지 않는 수수께끼 앞에서의 공포, 그리고 죽음의 가까움과 불가피함에 대한 감정—을 레빈의 안에 다시 일깨웠다."], ["This feeling was now even stronger than before; even less than before did he feel capable of apprehending the meaning of death, and its inevitability rose up before him more terrible than ever.", "이 감정은 이제 전보다 한층 더 강했다. 죽음의 의미를 헤아릴 능력은 전보다도 더 없게 느껴졌고, 죽음의 불가피함은 그 어느 때보다도 더 끔찍하게 그의 앞에 솟아올랐다."], ["But now, thanks to his wife’s presence, that feeling did not reduce him to despair.", "그러나 이제는 아내가 곁에 있는 덕분에, 그 감정이 그를 절망으로 몰아넣지는 않았다."], ["In spite of death, he felt the need of life and love.", "죽음에도 불구하고 그는 삶과 사랑의 필요를 느꼈다."], ["He felt that love saved him from despair, and that this love, under the menace of despair, had become still stronger and purer.", "그는 사랑이 자신을 절망에서 구해 주고 있음을, 그리고 이 사랑이 절망의 위협 아래에서 한층 더 강해지고 순수해졌음을 느꼈다."], ["The one mystery of death, still unsolved, had scarcely passed before his eyes, when another mystery had arisen, as insoluble, urging him to love and to life.", "여전히 풀리지 않은 죽음이라는 하나의 신비가 그의 눈앞을 채 지나가기도 전에, 또 다른 신비가, 그만큼이나 풀 수 없는 신비가 솟아올라, 그에게 사랑과 삶을 재촉했다."]], [["The doctor confirmed his suppositions in regard to Kitty.", "의사는 키티에 관한 그의 추측을 확인해 주었다."], ["Her indisposition was a symptom that she was with child.", "그녀의 부진은 아이를 가졌다는 징후였다."]], [["# Chapter 21", "# Chapter 21"]], [["From the moment when Alexey Alexandrovitch understood from his interviews with Betsy and with Stepan Arkadyevitch that all that was expected of him was to leave his wife in peace, without burdening her with his presence, and that his wife herself desired this, he felt so distraught that he could come to no decision of himself; he did not know himself what he wanted now, and putting himself in the hands of those who were so pleased to interest themselves in his affairs, he met everything with unqualified assent.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 베시 그리고 스테판 아르카지치와 나눈 대화를 통해, 자기에게 기대되는 것이라곤 아내를 자기 존재로 짓누르지 말고 평온히 내버려 두는 일뿐이며, 아내 자신도 그것을 바라고 있음을 깨달은 순간부터, 그는 너무도 심란해져서 스스로는 아무런 결정도 내릴 수가 없었다. 그는 지금 자기가 무엇을 원하는지조차 알지 못했고, 자기 일에 그토록 기꺼이 관심을 보이는 사람들의 손에 자신을 맡긴 채 무엇이든 무조건 동의로 받아들였다."], ["It was only when Anna had left his house, and the English governess sent to ask him whether she should dine with him or separately, that for the first time he clearly comprehended his position, and was appalled by it.", "안나가 집을 떠나고, 영국인 가정교사가 사람을 보내 그와 함께 식사할지 아니면 따로 식사할지를 물어 왔을 때에야 비로소, 그는 처음으로 자기 처지를 또렷이 깨닫고 그것에 경악했다."], ["Most difficult of all in this position was the fact that he could not in any way connect and reconcile his past with what was now.", "이 처지에서 무엇보다도 가장 힘든 것은, 자신의 과거를 지금의 상황과 도무지 연결 짓고 화해시킬 수가 없다는 사실이었다."], ["It was not the past when he had lived happily with his wife that troubled him.", "그를 괴롭히는 것은 아내와 행복하게 살았던 그 과거가 아니었다."], ["The transition from that past to a knowledge of his wife’s unfaithfulness he had lived through miserably already; that state was painful, but he could understand it.", "그 과거에서 아내의 부정을 알게 되기까지의 과정은 이미 비참하게 겪어 냈다. 그 상태는 고통스러웠지만, 그래도 이해할 수는 있었다."], ["If his wife had then, on declaring to him her unfaithfulness, left him, he would have been wounded, unhappy, but he would not have been in the hopeless position—incomprehensible to himself—in which he felt himself now.", "만약 그때 아내가 자기 부정을 그에게 털어놓은 뒤 그를 떠났더라면, 그는 상처받고 불행했겠지만, 지금 자기가 처한 듯한 이 희망 없는 처지—스스로도 이해할 수 없는 처지에는 빠지지 않았을 것이다."], ["He could not now reconcile his immediate past, his tenderness, his love for his sick wife, and for the other man’s child with what was now the case, that is with the fact that, as it were, in return for all this he now found himself alone, put to shame, a laughing-stock, needed by no one, and despised by everyone.", "그는 지금 자신의 가장 가까운 과거, 즉 병든 아내와 다른 남자의 아이에게 쏟았던 자신의 다정함과 사랑을, 지금의 상황과 도무지 화해시킬 수가 없었다. 다시 말해 이 모든 것의 대가라도 되는 양, 지금 자기가 외톨이가 되어 수치를 당하고, 웃음거리가 되었으며, 누구에게도 필요치 않고, 모든 사람에게 멸시받는 신세가 되어 버린 사실과 말이다."]], [["For the first two days after his wife’s departure Alexey Alexandrovitch received applicants for assistance and his chief secretary, drove to the committee, and went down to dinner in the dining-room as usual.", "아내가 떠난 뒤 처음 이틀 동안 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 도움을 청하러 온 사람들과 수석 비서를 맞이했고, 마차를 타고 위원회로 갔으며, 평소처럼 식당으로 내려가 식사를 했다."], ["Without giving himself a reason for what he was doing, he strained every nerve of his being for those two days, simply to preserve an appearance of composure, and even of indifference.", "자기가 무엇을 하고 있는지 스스로에게 이유를 대지도 않은 채, 그는 그 이틀 동안 오로지 침착한, 아니 무심한 겉모습마저 지켜 내려고 자기 존재의 온 신경을 곤두세웠다."], ["Answering inquiries about the disposition of Anna Arkadyevna’s rooms and belongings, he had exercised immense self-control to appear like a man in whose eyes what had occurred was not unforeseen nor out of the ordinary course of events, and he attained his aim: no one could have detected in him signs of despair.", "안나 아르카지예브나의 방과 소지품을 어떻게 처리할지 묻는 물음에 답하면서, 그는 일어난 일이 자기 눈에 예상치 못한 것도, 흔한 일의 흐름에서 벗어난 것도 아닌 사람처럼 보이려고 엄청난 자제력을 발휘했고, 그 목적을 이루어 냈다. 누구도 그에게서 절망의 기색을 알아채지는 못했을 것이다."], ["But on the second day after her departure, when Korney gave him a bill from a fashionable draper’s shop, which Anna had forgotten to pay, and announced that the clerk from the shop was waiting, Alexey Alexandrovitch told him to show the clerk up.", "그러나 그녀가 떠난 지 이틀째 되던 날, 코르네이가 안나가 미처 지불하지 않은 어느 고급 포목점의 청구서를 가져오면서, 가게의 점원이 기다리고 있다고 알리자, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 그에게 점원을 들여보내라고 했다."]], [["“Excuse me, your excellency, for venturing to trouble you.", "\"각하, 폐를 끼쳐 송구합니다."], ["But if you direct us to apply to her excellency, would you graciously oblige us with her address?”", "하지만 마님께 청구하라 분부하신다면, 황송하오나 마님의 주소를 알려 주시겠습니까?\""]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch pondered, as it seemed to the clerk, and all at once, turning round, he sat down at the table.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 점원이 보기에 곰곰이 생각에 잠긴 듯하더니, 별안간 몸을 돌려 탁자 앞에 앉았다."], ["Letting his head sink into his hands, he sat for a long while in that position, several times attempted to speak and stopped short.", "두 손에 머리를 파묻은 채 그는 한참 동안 그 자세로 앉아 있었고, 몇 번이나 말을 꺼내려다 입을 다물었다."], ["Korney, perceiving his master’s emotion, asked the clerk to call another time.", "코르네이는 주인의 동요를 알아채고 점원에게 다음에 다시 오라고 청했다."], ["Left alone, Alexey Alexandrovitch recognized that he had not the strength to keep up the line of firmness and composure any longer.", "홀로 남게 되자 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 더 이상 단호함과 침착함의 자세를 유지할 힘이 자기에게 없음을 인정했다."], ["He gave orders for the carriage that was awaiting him to be taken back, and for no one to be admitted, and he did not go down to dinner.", "그는 자기를 기다리던 마차를 돌려보내고, 누구도 들이지 말라고 명한 뒤, 식사하러 내려가지 않았다."]], [["He felt that he could not endure the weight of universal contempt and exasperation, which he had distinctly seen in the face of the clerk and of Korney, and of everyone, without exception, whom he had met during those two days.", "그는 그 이틀 동안 마주친 점원과 코르네이, 그리고 예외 없이 모든 사람의 얼굴에서 또렷이 보았던 그 보편적인 경멸과 분노의 무게를 견딜 수 없다고 느꼈다."], ["He felt that he could not turn aside from himself the hatred of men, because that hatred did not come from his being bad (in that case he could have tried to be better), but from his being shamefully and repulsively unhappy.", "그는 사람들의 증오를 자기에게서 돌려놓을 수 없다고 느꼈는데, 그 증오가 그가 나쁜 사람이라서 비롯된 것이 아니라(그렇다면 더 나은 사람이 되려 애써 볼 수도 있었으리라) 그가 수치스럽고 역겹게 불행한 사람이라서 비롯된 것이기 때문이었다."], ["He knew that for this, for the very fact that his heart was torn with grief, they would be merciless to him.", "그는 바로 이 때문에, 바로 그의 마음이 슬픔으로 찢어졌다는 그 사실 때문에 사람들이 그에게 무자비하게 굴리라는 것을 알았다."], ["He felt that men would crush him as dogs strangle a torn dog yelping with pain.", "그는 개들이 고통으로 깽깽대는 상처 입은 개를 물어 죽이듯, 사람들이 자기를 짓밟아 버리리라고 느꼈다."], ["He knew that his sole means of security against people was to hide his wounds from them, and instinctively he tried to do this for two days, but now he felt incapable of keeping up the unequal struggle.", "그는 사람들로부터 자신을 지킬 유일한 방법이 자기 상처를 그들에게 숨기는 것임을 알았고, 본능적으로 이틀 동안 그렇게 하려 애썼지만, 이제는 그 불공평한 싸움을 더 이상 견뎌 낼 수 없다고 느꼈다."]], [["His despair was even intensified by the consciousness that he was utterly alone in his sorrow.", "자기가 이 슬픔 속에서 철저히 혼자라는 의식 때문에 그의 절망은 한층 더 깊어졌다."], ["In all Petersburg there was not a human being to whom he could express what he was feeling, who would feel for him, not as a high official, not as a member of society, but simply as a suffering man; indeed he had not such a one in the whole world.", "페테르부르크를 다 뒤져도, 고위 관료로서도 아니고, 사교계의 일원으로서도 아니고, 그저 고통받는 한 인간으로서 자기가 느끼는 것을 털어놓을 수 있고 자기를 동정해 줄 사람은 단 한 명도 없었다. 아니, 이 세상 어디에도 그런 사람은 없었다."]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch grew up an orphan.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 고아로 자랐다."], ["There were two brothers.", "형제는 둘이었다."], ["They did not remember their father, and their mother died when Alexey Alexandrovitch was ten years old.", "그들은 아버지를 기억하지 못했고, 어머니는 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 열 살 때 세상을 떠났다."], ["The property was a small one.", "재산은 변변치 않았다."], ["Their uncle, Karenin, a government official of high standing, at one time a favorite of the late Tsar, had brought them up.", "한때 선왕의 총애를 받았던 고위 관료인 숙부 카레닌이 그들을 키웠다."]], [["On completing his high school and university courses with medals, Alexey Alexandrovitch had, with his uncle’s aid, immediately started in a prominent position in the service, and from that time forward he had devoted himself exclusively to political ambition.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 고등학교와 대학 과정을 우등으로 마친 뒤, 숙부의 도움으로 곧바로 관직의 요직에서 출발했고, 그때부터 줄곧 오로지 정치적 야망에만 몰두했다."], ["In the high school and the university, and afterwards in the service, Alexey Alexandrovitch had never formed a close friendship with anyone.", "고등학교와 대학에서, 그리고 그 후 관직에 있으면서도 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 그 누구와도 가까운 우정을 맺은 적이 없었다."], ["His brother had been the person nearest to his heart, but he had a post in the Ministry of Foreign Affairs, and was always abroad, where he had died shortly after Alexey Alexandrovitch’s marriage.", "형은 그의 마음에 가장 가까운 사람이었지만, 외무성에 자리를 얻어 늘 외국에 나가 있었고, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 결혼하고 얼마 지나지 않아 그곳에서 죽었다."]], [["While he was governor of a province, Anna’s aunt, a wealthy provincial lady, had thrown him—middle-aged as he was, though young for a governor—with her niece, and had succeeded in putting him in such a position that he had either to declare himself or to leave the town.", "그가 어느 지방의 지사로 있을 때, 부유한 지방 부인이던 안나의 숙모가 그를—지사치고는 젊었으나 이미 중년이었던 그를 자기 조카딸과 엮어 놓았고, 그가 청혼을 하든지 아니면 그 도시를 떠나든지 해야 할 처지에 몰아넣는 데 성공했다."], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch was not long in hesitation.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 오래 망설이지 않았다."], ["There were at the time as many reasons for the step as against it, and there was no overbalancing consideration to outweigh his invariable rule of abstaining when in doubt.", "그 일에는 찬성할 이유도 반대할 이유도 그만큼이나 많았고, 의심스러울 때는 삼간다는 그의 변함없는 원칙을 뒤엎을 만한 결정적인 사정도 없었다."], ["But Anna’s aunt had through a common acquaintance insinuated that he had already compromised the girl, and that he was in honor bound to make her an offer.", "그러나 안나의 숙모는 공통의 지인을 통해, 그가 이미 그 처녀의 명예를 손상시켰으며 명예를 지키려면 마땅히 청혼해야 한다고 넌지시 일러 왔다."], ["He made the offer, and concentrated on his betrothed and his wife all the feeling of which he was capable.", "그는 청혼을 했고, 자기가 품을 수 있는 모든 감정을 약혼녀와 아내에게 쏟아부었다."]], [["The attachment he felt to Anna precluded in his heart every need of intimate relations with others.", "안나에게 느끼는 애착은 그의 마음속에서 다른 이들과 친밀한 관계를 맺을 필요를 모조리 막아 버렸다."], ["And now among all his acquaintances he had not one friend.", "그리하여 지금 그의 지인들 가운데 친구라곤 한 명도 없었다."], ["He had plenty of so-called connections, but no friendships.", "이른바 인맥은 차고 넘쳤지만, 우정은 없었다."], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch had plenty of people whom he could invite to dinner, to whose sympathy he could appeal in any public affair he was concerned about, whose interest he could reckon upon for anyone he wished to help, with whom he could candidly discuss other people’s business and affairs of state.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에게는 만찬에 초대할 수 있는 사람, 자기가 관여하는 어떤 공적인 일에 동정을 호소할 수 있는 사람, 자기가 돕고자 하는 누군가를 위해 관심을 기대할 수 있는 사람, 다른 사람들의 일이며 국가의 일을 허심탄회하게 의논할 수 있는 사람은 얼마든지 있었다."], ["But his relations with these people were confined to one clearly defined channel, and had a certain routine from which it was impossible to depart.", "하지만 이 사람들과의 관계는 명확히 정해진 하나의 통로에 국한되어 있었고, 거기서 벗어나기란 불가능한 일정한 틀을 지니고 있었다."], ["There was one man, a comrade of his at the university, with whom he had made friends later, and with whom he could have spoken of a personal sorrow; but this friend had a post in the Department of Education in a remote part of Russia.", "대학 시절 동창으로 훗날 가까워져, 개인적인 슬픔을 털어놓을 수 있었을 사람이 한 명 있긴 했지만, 그 친구는 러시아의 외딴 지방 교육부에 자리를 얻어 가 있었다."], ["Of the people in Petersburg the most intimate and most possible were his chief secretary and his doctor.", "페테르부르크 사람들 중에서 가장 가깝고 가능성이 있는 사람은 그의 수석 비서와 주치의였다."]], [["Mihail Vassilievitch Sludin, the chief secretary, was a straightforward, intelligent, good-hearted, and conscientious man, and Alexey Alexandrovitch was aware of his personal goodwill.", "수석 비서인 미하일 바실리예비치 슬루딘은 솔직하고 영리하며 마음씨 곱고 양심적인 사람이었고, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치도 그의 개인적인 호의를 알고 있었다."], ["But their five years of official work together seemed to have put a barrier between them that cut off warmer relations.", "하지만 함께 일한 오 년의 관직 생활이 두 사람 사이에 더 따뜻한 관계를 가로막는 장벽을 세워 놓은 듯했다."]], [["After signing the papers brought him, Alexey Alexandrovitch had sat for a long while in silence, glancing at Mihail Vassilievitch, and several times he attempted to speak, but could not.", "가져온 서류에 서명을 마친 뒤, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 미하일 바실리예비치를 흘끔거리며 한참 동안 말없이 앉아 있었고, 몇 번이나 말을 꺼내려 했지만 그러지 못했다."], ["He had already prepared the phrase: “You have heard of my trouble?”", "그는 이미 \"내가 겪는 고초를 들으셨소?\" 하는 말을 준비해 두기까지 했다."], ["But he ended by saying, as usual: “So you’ll get this ready for me?”", "하지만 결국 평소처럼 \"그럼 이걸 준비해 주시겠소?\""], ["and with that dismissed him.", "하고 말하는 것으로 끝맺고는, 그를 물러가게 했다."]], [["The other person was the doctor, who had also a kindly feeling for him; but there had long existed a taciturn understanding between them that both were weighed down by work, and always in a hurry.", "또 한 사람은 주치의였는데, 그 역시 그에게 호의를 품고 있었다. 하지만 둘 다 일에 짓눌려 늘 바쁘다는 무언의 양해가 두 사람 사이에 오래전부터 자리 잡고 있었다."]], [["Of his women friends, foremost amongst them Countess Lidia Ivanovna, Alexey Alexandrovitch never thought.", "여자 지인들로 말하자면, 그 가운데 으뜸인 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인을 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 결코 떠올리지 않았다."], ["All women, simply as women, were terrible and distasteful to him.", "모든 여자는, 그저 여자라는 이유만으로, 그에게 끔찍하고 역겨운 존재였다."]], [["# Chapter 22", "# Chapter 22"]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch had forgotten the Countess Lidia Ivanovna, but she had not forgotten him.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인을 잊고 있었지만, 그녀는 그를 잊지 않고 있었다."], ["At the bitterest moment of his lonely despair she came to him, and without waiting to be announced, walked straight into his study.", "외로운 절망의 가장 쓰라린 순간에 그녀가 그를 찾아왔고, 안내를 기다리지도 않은 채 곧장 그의 서재로 걸어 들어왔다."], ["She found him as he was sitting with his head in both hands.", "그녀는 그가 두 손에 머리를 파묻고 앉아 있는 모습을 보았다."]], [["“J’ai forcé la consigne,”", "\"J'ai forcé la consigne.\""], ["she said, walking in with rapid steps and breathing hard with excitement and rapid exercise.", "그녀는 빠른 걸음으로 들어오며, 흥분과 빠른 운동으로 거칠게 숨을 몰아쉬며 말했다."], ["“I have heard all!", "\"다 들었어요!"], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch!", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치!"], ["Dear friend!”", "친애하는 벗이여!\""], ["she went on, warmly squeezing his hand in both of hers and gazing with her fine pensive eyes into his.", "그녀는 두 손으로 그의 손을 따뜻하게 꼭 쥐고, 그 아름답고 생각에 잠긴 눈으로 그의 눈을 응시하며 말을 이었다. * J'ai forcé la consigne: 프랑스어로 '면회 금지를 무릅쓰고 들어왔어요'라는 뜻."]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch, frowning, got up, and disengaging his hand, moved her a chair.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 미간을 찌푸린 채 일어나, 손을 빼며 그녀에게 의자를 권했다."]], [["“Won’t you sit down, countess?", "\"앉으시지요, 백작 부인?"], ["I’m seeing no one because I’m unwell, countess,”", "몸이 좋지 않아 아무도 만나지 않고 있습니다, 백작 부인.\""], ["he said, and his lips twitched.", "그가 말했고, 그의 입술이 씰룩거렸다."]], [["“Dear friend!”", "\"친애하는 벗이여!\""], ["repeated Countess Lidia Ivanovna, never taking her eyes off his, and suddenly her eyebrows rose at the inner corners, describing a triangle on her forehead, her ugly yellow face became still uglier, but Alexey Alexandrovitch felt that she was sorry for him and was preparing to cry.", "리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인은 그에게서 눈을 떼지 않은 채 거듭 말했고, 별안간 그녀의 눈썹 안쪽 끝이 치켜 올라가 이마에 삼각형을 그렸으며, 못생긴 누런 얼굴이 한층 더 못생겨졌지만, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 그녀가 자기를 가엾게 여기며 막 울려 한다는 것을 느꼈다."], ["And he too was softened; he snatched her plump hand and proceeded to kiss it.", "그리고 그 역시 마음이 누그러졌다. 그는 그녀의 통통한 손을 와락 붙잡아 입을 맞추기 시작했다."]], [["“Dear friend!”", "\"친애하는 벗이여!\""], ["she said in a voice breaking with emotion.", "그녀가 감정에 겨워 갈라지는 목소리로 말했다."], ["“You ought not to give way to grief.", "\"슬픔에 자신을 내맡겨서는 안 돼요."], ["Your sorrow is a great one, but you ought to find consolation.”", "당신의 슬픔은 크지만, 위안을 찾으셔야 해요.\""]], [["“I am crushed, I am annihilated, I am no longer a man!”", "\"나는 짓밟혔고, 무너졌고, 더 이상 사람이 아닙니다!\""], ["said Alexey Alexandrovitch, letting go her hand, but still gazing into her brimming eyes.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 그녀의 손을 놓으면서도 여전히 그녀의 그렁그렁한 눈을 응시하며 말했다."], ["“My position is so awful because I can find nowhere, I cannot find within me strength to support me.”", "\"내 처지가 너무도 끔찍한 것은, 어디에서도 나를 지탱해 줄 힘을 찾을 수 없기 때문입니다, 내 안에서도 찾을 수가 없어요.\""]], [["“You will find support; seek it—not in me, though I beseech you to believe in my friendship,”", "\"지탱해 줄 힘을 찾으시게 될 거예요. 그것을 구하세요—내게서가 아니라, 비록 나의 우정을 믿어 주시기를 간청하지만요.\""], ["she said, with a sigh.", "그녀가 한숨을 쉬며 말했다."], ["“Our support is love, that love that He has vouchsafed us.", "\"우리를 지탱해 주는 것은 사랑, 그분께서 우리에게 베풀어 주신 그 사랑이에요."], ["His burden is light,”", "그분의 짐은 가벼워요.\""], ["she said, with the look of ecstasy Alexey Alexandrovitch knew so well.", "그녀는 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 너무도 잘 아는 그 황홀한 표정으로 말했다."], ["“He will be your support and your succor.”", "\"그분이 당신을 지탱해 주시고 도와주실 거예요.\""]], [["Although there was in these words a flavor of that sentimental emotion at her own lofty feelings, and that new mystical fervor which had lately gained ground in Petersburg, and which seemed to Alexey Alexandrovitch disproportionate, still it was pleasant to him to hear this now.", "이 말들에는 자기 자신의 고매한 감정에 도취된 그 감상적인 감동의 기색과, 근래 페테르부르크에서 세력을 얻어 가던 그 새로운 신비주의적 열정—알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 보기에는 과도해 보이던—이 배어 있었지만, 그래도 지금 이 말을 듣는 것이 그에게는 기분 좋았다."]], [["“I am weak.", "\"나는 약합니다."], ["I am crushed.", "나는 짓밟혔어요."], ["I foresaw nothing, and now I understand nothing.”", "아무것도 예견하지 못했고, 이제는 아무것도 이해하지 못합니다.\""]], [["“Dear friend,”", "\"친애하는 벗이여.\""], ["repeated Lidia Ivanovna.", "리디아 이바노브나가 거듭 말했다."]], [["“It’s not the loss of what I have not now, it’s not that!”", "\"지금 내가 갖지 못한 것을 잃어서가 아닙니다, 그게 아니에요!\""], ["pursued Alexey Alexandrovitch.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 말을 이었다."], ["“I do not grieve for that.", "\"나는 그것 때문에 슬퍼하는 게 아닙니다."], ["But I cannot help feeling humiliated before other people for the position I am placed in.", "하지만 내가 처한 처지 때문에 다른 사람들 앞에서 굴욕감을 느끼지 않을 수가 없어요."], ["It is wrong, but I can’t help it, I can’t help it.”", "잘못된 일이지만, 어쩔 수가 없습니다, 어쩔 수가 없어요.\""]], [["“Not you it was performed that noble act of forgiveness, at which I was moved to ecstasy, and everyone else too, but He, working within your heart,”", "\"내가 황홀경에 빠질 만큼 감동받았고, 다른 모든 이들도 그랬던 그 고귀한 용서의 행위를 행하신 것은 당신이 아니라, 당신의 마음속에서 일하시는 그분이에요.\""], ["said Countess Lidia Ivanovna, raising her eyes rapturously, “and so you cannot be ashamed of your act.”", "리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인이 황홀하게 눈을 치켜뜨며 말했다. \"그러니 당신은 자신의 행위를 부끄러워할 수 없어요.\""]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch knitted his brows, and crooking his hands, he cracked his fingers.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 미간을 찌푸렸고, 손을 구부려 손가락을 우두둑거렸다."]], [["“One must know all the facts,”", "\"모든 사실을 알아야만 합니다.\""], ["he said in his thin voice.", "그가 가느다란 목소리로 말했다."], ["“A man’s strength has its limits, countess, and I have reached my limits.", "\"인간의 힘에는 한계가 있지요, 백작 부인, 그리고 나는 그 한계에 다다랐습니다."], ["The whole day I have had to be making arrangements, arrangements about household matters arising” (he emphasized the word arising) “from my new, solitary position.", "나는 온종일 내 새로운 고독한 처지에서 비롯되는\"(그는 비롯되는이라는 말에 힘을 주었다) \"집안일에 관한 여러 가지 조치를 취해야 했어요."], ["The servants, the governess, the accounts....", "하인들, 가정교사, 회계 장부……"], ["These pinpricks have stabbed me to the heart, and I have not the strength to bear it.", "이런 자질구레한 것들이 내 가슴을 찔러 댔고, 나는 그것을 견딜 힘이 없습니다."], ["At dinner ...", "식사 자리에서……"], ["yesterday, I was almost getting up from the dinner-table.", "어제는, 식탁에서 거의 일어설 뻔했어요."], ["I could not bear the way my son looked at me.", "아들 녀석이 나를 바라보는 그 눈길을 견딜 수가 없었습니다."], ["He did not ask me the meaning of it all, but he wanted to ask, and I could not bear the look in his eyes.", "녀석은 이게 다 무슨 일이냐고 묻지는 않았지만, 묻고 싶어 했고, 나는 그 눈빛을 견딜 수가 없었어요."], ["He was afraid to look at me, but that is not all....”", "녀석은 나를 보기를 두려워했지만, 그게 다가 아닙니다…….\""], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch would have referred to the bill that had been brought him, but his voice shook, and he stopped.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 자기에게 가져다 놓인 청구서 이야기를 꺼내려 했지만, 목소리가 떨려 멈추고 말았다."], ["That bill on blue paper, for a hat and ribbons, he could not recall without a rush of self-pity.", "모자와 리본 값으로 청구된 그 푸른 종이의 청구서는, 자기 연민이 복받쳐 오르지 않고서는 떠올릴 수가 없었다."]], [["“I understand, dear friend,”", "\"이해해요, 친애하는 벗이여.\""], ["said Lidia Ivanovna.", "리디아 이바노브나가 말했다."], ["“I understand it all.", "\"전부 이해해요."], ["Succor and comfort you will find not in me, though I have come only to aid you if I can.", "도움과 위안은 내게서가 아니라 다른 데서 찾으시겠지만, 그래도 나는 할 수 있다면 당신을 돕고자 온 것뿐이에요."], ["If I could take from off you all these petty, humiliating cares ...", "내가 이 모든 사소하고 굴욕적인 걱정거리들을 당신에게서 떠맡을 수만 있다면……"], ["I understand that a woman’s word, a woman’s superintendence is needed.", "여자의 말 한마디, 여자의 보살핌이 필요하다는 걸 잘 알아요."], ["You will intrust it to me?”", "그 일을 내게 맡겨 주시겠어요?\""]], [["Silently and gratefully Alexey Alexandrovitch pressed her hand.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 말없이 고마운 마음으로 그녀의 손을 꼭 쥐었다."]], [["“Together we will take care of Seryozha.", "\"우리 함께 세료자를 돌봐요."], ["Practical affairs are not my strong point.", "실무적인 일은 내 장기가 아니에요."], ["But I will set to work.", "하지만 나서서 해 볼게요."], ["I will be your housekeeper.", "당신의 가정부가 되어 드리겠어요."], ["Don’t thank me.", "고마워하지 마세요."], ["I do it not from myself....”", "내가 나 자신을 위해 하는 일이 아니니까요…….\""]], [["“I cannot help thanking you.”", "\"고마워하지 않을 수가 없습니다.\""]], [["“But, dear friend, do not give way to the feeling of which you spoke—being ashamed of what is the Christian’s highest glory: he who humbles himself shall be exalted.", "\"하지만, 친애하는 벗이여, 당신이 말씀하신 그 감정—그리스도교도의 가장 큰 영광을 부끄러워하는 마음에 자신을 내맡기지 마세요. 무릇 자기를 낮추는 자는 높아지리라 하셨어요."], ["And you cannot thank me.", "그리고 당신은 내게 고마워할 수 없어요."], ["You must thank Him, and pray to Him for succor.", "그분께 감사드리고, 그분께 도움을 청하셔야 해요."], ["In Him alone we find peace, consolation, salvation, and love,”", "오직 그분 안에서만 우리는 평화와 위안과 구원과 사랑을 찾을 수 있어요.\""], ["she said, and turning her eyes heavenwards, she began praying, as Alexey Alexandrovitch gathered from her silence.", "그녀는 그렇게 말하고 하늘을 향해 눈을 들더니, 기도를 시작했다. 그녀의 침묵으로 미루어 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 그렇게 짐작했다."]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch listened to her now, and those expressions which had seemed to him, if not distasteful, at least exaggerated, now seemed to him natural and consolatory.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 이제 그녀의 말에 귀를 기울였고, 거북하지는 않더라도 적어도 과장스럽게 여겨졌던 그 표현들이 이제는 자연스럽고 위안이 되는 것으로 느껴졌다."], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch had disliked this new enthusiastic fervor.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 이 새로운 열광적인 열정을 싫어했었다."], ["He was a believer, who was interested in religion primarily in its political aspect, and the new doctrine which ventured upon several new interpretations, just because it paved the way to discussion and analysis, was in principle disagreeable to him.", "그는 신앙인이긴 했지만 주로 종교의 정치적 측면에 관심이 있었고, 토론과 분석의 길을 터놓는다는 바로 그 이유로 몇몇 새로운 해석을 감행하는 그 새로운 교리가 원칙적으로 그에게는 못마땅했다."], ["He had hitherto taken up a cold and even antagonistic attitude to this new doctrine, and with Countess Lidia Ivanovna, who had been carried away by it, he had never argued, but by silence had assiduously parried her attempts to provoke him into argument.", "그는 지금까지 이 새로운 교리에 대해 차갑고 심지어 적대적인 태도를 취해 왔으며, 그것에 심취해 버린 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인과 한 번도 논쟁을 벌인 적이 없었지만, 그를 논쟁으로 끌어들이려는 그녀의 시도를 침묵으로 줄곧 받아넘겨 왔다."], ["Now for the first time he heard her words with pleasure, and did not inwardly oppose them.", "그런데 지금 처음으로 그는 그녀의 말을 기쁘게 들었고, 마음속으로 그것에 반대하지 않았다."]], [["“I am very, very grateful to you, both for your deeds and for your words,”", "\"당신께 정말, 정말 고맙습니다, 당신이 해 주신 일과 말씀 둘 다에 대해서요.\""], ["he said, when she had finished praying.", "그녀가 기도를 마쳤을 때 그가 말했다."]], [["Countess Lidia Ivanovna once more pressed both her friend’s hands.", "리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인은 다시 한번 자기 벗의 두 손을 꼭 쥐었다."]], [["“Now I will enter upon my duties,”", "\"이제 내 임무를 시작하겠어요.\""], ["she said with a smile after a pause, as she wiped away the traces of tears.", "잠시 사이를 두었다가 눈물 자국을 닦으며 그녀가 미소 띤 얼굴로 말했다."], ["“I am going to Seryozha.", "\"세료자에게 가 볼게요."], ["Only in the last extremity shall I apply to you.”", "정말 어쩔 수 없는 마지막 순간에만 당신에게 부탁할게요.\""], ["And she got up and went out.", "그러고는 일어나 나갔다."]], [["Countess Lidia Ivanovna went into Seryozha’s part of the house, and dropping tears on the scared child’s cheeks, she told him that his father was a saint and his mother was dead.", "리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인은 세료자가 지내는 집 한쪽으로 가서, 겁에 질린 아이의 뺨에 눈물을 떨어뜨리며, 아버지는 성자이고 어머니는 죽었다고 아이에게 말했다."]], [["Countess Lidia Ivanovna kept her promise.", "리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인은 약속을 지켰다."], ["She did actually take upon herself the care of the organization and management of Alexey Alexandrovitch’s household.", "그녀는 실제로 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치 집안의 조직과 관리를 맡았다."], ["But she had not overstated the case when saying that practical affairs were not her strong point.", "하지만 실무적인 일이 자기 장기가 아니라고 한 말은 결코 과장이 아니었다."], ["All her arrangements had to be modified because they could not be carried out, and they were modified by Korney, Alexey Alexandrovitch’s valet, who, though no one was aware of the fact, now managed Karenin’s household, and quietly and discreetly reported to his master while he was dressing all it was necessary for him to know.", "그녀의 모든 조치는 실행될 수 없어서 수정되어야 했고, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 시종인 코르네이가 그것을 수정했는데, 그는 아무도 모르는 사이에 이제 카레닌의 집안을 관리하고 있었으며, 주인이 옷을 입는 동안 그가 알아야 할 모든 것을 조용하고 신중하게 보고했다."], ["But Lidia Ivanovna’s help was none the less real; she gave Alexey Alexandrovitch moral support in the consciousness of her love and respect for him, and still more, as it was soothing to her to believe, in that she almost turned him to Christianity—that is, from an indifferent and apathetic believer she turned him into an ardent and steadfast adherent of the new interpretation of Christian doctrine, which had been gaining ground of late in Petersburg.", "그렇다고 해서 리디아 이바노브나의 도움이 실질적이지 않은 것은 아니었다. 그녀는 그에 대한 자신의 사랑과 존경을 의식하게 함으로써 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에게 도덕적 지지를 주었고, 더욱이 그녀가 그를 거의 그리스도교로 돌려세웠다고 스스로 믿는 것이 그녀에게 위안이 되었으니—다시 말해 무관심하고 무기력한 신앙인이던 그를, 근래 페테르부르크에서 세력을 넓혀 가던 그리스도교 교리의 새로운 해석을 열렬하고 한결같이 따르는 신봉자로 바꿔 놓은 것이다."], ["It was easy for Alexey Alexandrovitch to believe in this teaching.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 이 가르침을 믿기란 쉬운 일이었다."], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch, like Lidia Ivanovna indeed, and others who shared their views, was completely devoid of vividness of imagination, that spiritual faculty in virtue of which the conceptions evoked by the imagination become so vivid that they must needs be in harmony with other conceptions, and with actual fact.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는, 사실 리디아 이바노브나나 그들과 견해를 같이하는 다른 이들과 마찬가지로, 상상력의 생생함이 완전히 결여되어 있었다. 그 정신적 능력 덕분에 상상이 불러일으키는 관념들이 너무도 생생해져서 다른 관념들이나 실제 사실과 조화를 이루지 않을 수 없게 되는 것인데 말이다."], ["He saw nothing impossible and inconceivable in the idea that death, though existing for unbelievers, did not exist for him, and that, as he was possessed of the most perfect faith, of the measure of which he was himself the judge, therefore there was no sin in his soul, and he was experiencing complete salvation here on earth.", "그는 죽음이 불신자들에게는 존재하지만 자기에게는 존재하지 않는다는 생각, 그리고 자기는 가장 완전한 신앙—그 정도는 자기 스스로가 판단하는—을 지녔으므로 자기 영혼에는 죄가 없으며 여기 이 땅에서 완전한 구원을 경험하고 있다는 생각에서 아무런 불가능하거나 상상조차 할 수 없는 점을 보지 못했다."]], [["It is true that the erroneousness and shallowness of this conception of his faith was dimly perceptible to Alexey Alexandrovitch, and he knew that when, without the slightest idea that his forgiveness was the action of a higher power, he had surrendered directly to the feeling of forgiveness, he had felt more happiness than now when he was thinking every instant that Christ was in his heart, and that in signing official papers he was doing His will.", "자기 신앙에 대한 이 관념이 그릇되고 천박하다는 것을 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 어렴풋이 느끼고 있었던 것은 사실이며, 자기의 용서가 더 높은 힘의 작용이라는 생각이라곤 조금도 없이 곧장 용서의 감정에 자신을 내맡겼을 때가, 매 순간 그리스도가 자기 마음속에 계시며 공문서에 서명하는 것이 그분의 뜻을 행하는 것이라고 생각하는 지금보다 더 큰 행복을 느꼈다는 것도 그는 알고 있었다."], ["But for Alexey Alexandrovitch it was a necessity to think in that way; it was such a necessity for him in his humiliation to have some elevated standpoint, however imaginary, from which, looked down upon by all, he could look down on others, that he clung, as to his one salvation, to his delusion of salvation.", "하지만 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에게는 그렇게 생각하는 것이 하나의 필요였다. 자기의 굴욕 속에서, 비록 상상에 불과할지라도, 모든 이에게 멸시받는 처지에서 다른 이들을 내려다볼 수 있는 어떤 드높은 입장을 갖는 것이 그에게는 너무도 절실한 필요였기에, 그는 자신의 유일한 구원에 매달리듯 그 구원의 망상에 매달렸던 것이다."]], [["# Chapter 23", "# Chapter 23"]], [["The Countess Lidia Ivanovna had, as a very young and sentimental girl, been married to a wealthy man of high rank, an extremely good-natured, jovial, and extremely dissipated rake.", "리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인은 아주 어리고 감상적인 처녀 시절에 부유하고 지위 높은 남자, 더없이 선량하고 쾌활하지만 지독히 방탕한 난봉꾼과 결혼했다."], ["Two months after marriage her husband abandoned her, and her impassioned protestations of affection he met with a sarcasm and even hostility that people knowing the count’s good heart, and seeing no defects in the sentimental Lidia, were at a loss to explain.", "결혼 두 달 후 남편은 그녀를 버렸고, 그녀가 격정적으로 애정을 호소해도 그저 빈정거림으로, 심지어 적의로까지 응답했는데, 백작의 선량한 마음씨를 알고 감상적인 리디아에게서 아무런 흠도 찾지 못하는 사람들로서는 그 까닭을 도무지 설명할 수가 없었다."], ["Though they were divorced and lived apart, yet whenever the husband met the wife, he invariably behaved to her with the same malignant irony, the cause of which was incomprehensible.", "두 사람은 이혼하여 따로 살았지만, 그래도 남편은 아내와 마주칠 때마다 어김없이 똑같은 악의에 찬 비아냥거림으로 그녀를 대했으니, 그 까닭은 도무지 알 수 없는 것이었다."]], [["Countess Lidia Ivanovna had long given up being in love with her husband, but from that time she had never given up being in love with someone.", "리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인은 남편을 사랑하기를 진작에 그만두었지만, 그 후로 누군가를 사랑하기를 그만둔 적은 한 번도 없었다."], ["She was in love with several people at once, both men and women; she had been in love with almost everyone who had been particularly distinguished in any way.", "그녀는 동시에 여러 사람을 사랑했는데, 남자도 여자도 있었다. 그녀는 어떤 식으로든 유난히 두드러진 사람이라면 거의 누구든 사랑했다."], ["She was in love with all the new princes and princesses who married into the imperial family; she had been in love with a high dignitary of the Church, a vicar, and a parish priest; she had been in love with a journalist, three Slavophiles, with Komissarov, with a minister, a doctor, an English missionary and Karenin.", "그녀는 황실로 시집오거나 장가오는 새로운 공작과 공작 부인을 모두 사랑했고, 교회의 고위 성직자며 보좌 신부며 본당 사제를 사랑한 적도 있었으며, 어느 언론인과 슬라브주의자 셋과 코미사로프와 어느 장관과 의사와 영국인 선교사와 카레닌을 사랑한 적도 있었다."], ["All these passions constantly waning or growing more ardent, did not prevent her from keeping up the most extended and complicated relations with the court and fashionable society.", "이 모든 열정은 끊임없이 식었다가 더 뜨거워지곤 했지만, 그렇다고 해서 그녀가 궁정 및 사교계와 더없이 폭넓고 복잡한 관계를 유지하는 데에 방해가 되지는 않았다."], ["But from the time that after Karenin’s trouble she took him under her special protection, from the time that she set to work in Karenin’s household looking after his welfare, she felt that all her other attachments were not the real thing, and that she was now genuinely in love, and with no one but Karenin.", "그러나 카레닌이 곤경에 처한 후 그를 자신의 특별한 보호 아래 두게 된 때부터, 카레닌의 집안일에 발 벗고 나서 그의 안녕을 돌보기 시작한 때부터, 그녀는 자신의 다른 모든 애착이 진짜가 아니었으며 이제야말로 자신이 진정으로 사랑하고 있다는 것을, 그것도 카레닌 말고는 그 누구도 아니라는 것을 느꼈다."], ["The feeling she now experienced for him seemed to her stronger than any of her former feelings.", "지금 그에게 품은 감정은 이전의 그 어떤 감정보다도 강하게 느껴졌다."], ["Analyzing her feeling, and comparing it with former passions, she distinctly perceived that she would not have been in love with Komissarov if he had not saved the life of the Tsar, that she would not have been in love with Ristitch-Kudzhitsky if there had been no Slavonic question, but that she loved Karenin for himself, for his lofty, uncomprehended soul, for the sweet—to her—high notes of his voice, for his drawling intonation, his weary eyes, his character, and his soft white hands with their swollen veins.", "자신의 감정을 분석하여 예전의 열정들과 비교해 보면서, 그녀는 만일 코미사로프가 황제의 목숨을 구하지 않았더라면 그를 사랑하지 않았을 것이며, 슬라브 문제가 없었더라면 리스티치쿠지츠키를 사랑하지 않았으리라는 것을, 그러나 카레닌만큼은 그 사람 자체를, 그 드높고 아무도 헤아리지 못하는 영혼을, 그녀에게는 감미롭게 들리는 그 목소리의 높은 음을, 느릿하게 끄는 억양을, 지친 두 눈을, 그 성품을, 그리고 핏줄이 불거진 부드럽고 흰 두 손을 사랑한다는 것을 또렷이 깨달았다."], ["She was not simply overjoyed at meeting him, but she sought in his face signs of the impression she was making on him.", "그녀는 단지 그를 만나는 것이 더없이 기쁠 뿐만 아니라, 그의 얼굴에서 자신이 그에게 어떤 인상을 주고 있는지 그 흔적을 찾으려 했다."], ["She tried to please him, not by her words only, but in her whole person.", "그녀는 말로만이 아니라 자신의 온 존재로 그의 마음에 들려고 애썼다."], ["For his sake it was that she now lavished more care on her dress than before.", "그를 위해 이제 그녀는 전보다 옷차림에 더 많은 공을 들였다."], ["She caught herself in reveries on what might have been, if she had not been married and he had been free.", "그녀는 만일 자신이 결혼하지 않았고 그가 자유로운 몸이었다면 어땠을까 하는 공상에 빠져 있는 자기 자신을 문득문득 발견하곤 했다."], ["She blushed with emotion when he came into the room, she could not repress a smile of rapture when he said anything amiable to her.", "그가 방으로 들어오면 그녀는 감격에 겨워 얼굴을 붉혔고, 그가 그녀에게 무언가 다정한 말이라도 건네면 황홀한 미소를 억누를 수가 없었다."]], [["For several days now Countess Lidia Ivanovna had been in a state of intense excitement.", "벌써 며칠째 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인은 극도로 흥분된 상태에 있었다."], ["She had learned that Anna and Vronsky were in Petersburg.", "그녀는 안나와 브론스키가 페테르부르크에 와 있다는 것을 알게 되었다."], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch must be saved from seeing her, he must be saved even from the torturing knowledge that that awful woman was in the same town with him, and that he might meet her any minute.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 그녀를 보는 일은 어떻게든 막아야 했다. 그 끔찍한 여자가 자기와 같은 도시에 있고 언제라도 마주칠 수 있다는 그 괴로운 사실을 아는 것조차 막아야 했다."]], [["Lidia Ivanovna made inquiries through her friends as to what those infamous people, as she called Anna and Vronsky, intended doing, and she endeavored so to guide every movement of her friend during those days that he could not come across them.", "리디아 이바노브나는 자기가 안나와 브론스키를 일컫는 표현 그대로 그 파렴치한 인간들이 무엇을 하려는지 친구들을 통해 알아보았고, 그날들 동안 자기 친구의 모든 움직임을 그들과 마주치지 않도록 인도하려 애썼다."], ["The young adjutant, an acquaintance of Vronsky, through whom she obtained her information, and who hoped through Countess Lidia Ivanovna to obtain a concession, told her that they had finished their business and were going away next day.", "그녀가 정보를 얻는 통로였던, 브론스키와 아는 사이이며 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인을 통해 어떤 이권을 얻기를 바라던 젊은 부관이, 그들은 볼일을 다 마쳤으며 이튿날 떠날 거라고 그녀에게 일러 주었다."], ["Lidia Ivanovna had already begun to calm down, when the next morning a note was brought her, the handwriting of which she recognized with horror.", "리디아 이바노브나는 이미 마음을 가라앉히기 시작하던 참이었는데, 다음 날 아침 한 통의 쪽지가 그녀에게 전해졌고, 그 필체를 알아본 그녀는 소스라쳤다."], ["It was the handwriting of Anna Karenina.", "그것은 안나 카레니나의 필체였다."], ["The envelope was of paper as thick as bark; on the oblong yellow paper there was a huge monogram, and the letter smelt of agreeable scent.", "봉투는 나무껍질처럼 두꺼운 종이로 되어 있었고, 길쭉한 노란 종이에는 커다란 모노그램이 찍혀 있었으며, 편지에서는 기분 좋은 향내가 풍겼다."]], [["“Who brought it?”", "\"누가 가져왔지?\""]], [["“A commissionaire from the hotel.”", "\"호텔의 심부름꾼이 가져왔습니다.\""]], [["It was some time before Countess Lidia Ivanovna could sit down to read the letter.", "리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인이 자리에 앉아 편지를 읽기까지는 한참이 걸렸다."], ["Her excitement brought on an attack of asthma, to which she was subject.", "흥분한 탓에 그녀가 평소 앓던 천식 발작이 도졌던 것이다."], ["When she had recovered her composure, she read the following letter in French:", "마음을 가라앉힌 후 그녀는 프랑스어로 쓰인 다음과 같은 편지를 읽었다."]], [["“Madame la Comtesse,", "\"Madame la Comtesse,"]], [["“The Christian feelings with which your heart is filled give me the, I feel, unpardonable boldness to write to you.", "당신의 마음을 가득 채우고 있는 그리스도교적 감정이, 제게는 용서받을 수 없으리라 여겨지는 이 대담함을 무릅쓰고 당신께 글을 쓰게 합니다."], ["I am miserable at being separated from my son.", "저는 아들과 떨어져 있는 것이 너무도 괴롭습니다."], ["I entreat permission to see him once before my departure.", "떠나기 전에 단 한 번만이라도 그 아이를 보게 해 주십사 간청드립니다."], ["Forgive me for recalling myself to your memory.", "다시 제 존재를 당신의 기억에 떠올리게 하는 것을 용서하십시오."], ["I apply to you and not to Alexey Alexandrovitch, simply because I do not wish to cause that generous man to suffer in remembering me.", "제가 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 아니라 당신께 청하는 것은, 다만 그 너그러운 분이 저를 떠올리며 괴로워하시는 것을 원치 않기 때문입니다."], ["Knowing your friendship for him, I know you will understand me.", "그분을 향한 당신의 우정을 알기에, 당신께서 저를 이해해 주시리라 믿습니다."], ["Could you send Seryozha to me, or should I come to the house at some fixed hour, or will you let me know when and where I could see him away from home?", "세료자를 제게 보내 주실 수 있는지요, 아니면 정해진 시각에 제가 댁으로 찾아가야 할지요, 그것도 아니면 집 밖에서 그 아이를 언제 어디서 볼 수 있을지 제게 알려 주시겠는지요?"], ["I do not anticipate a refusal, knowing the magnanimity of him with whom it rests.", "이 일을 결정하실 분의 너그러움을 알기에, 거절은 예상하지 않습니다."], ["You cannot conceive the craving I have to see him, and so cannot conceive the gratitude your help will arouse in me.", "그 아이를 보고 싶어 하는 제 갈망이 얼마나 큰지 당신은 짐작도 못 하실 것이고, 그러니 당신의 도움이 제 안에 불러일으킬 감사가 어떠할지도 짐작하지 못하실 것입니다."]], [["“Anna.”", "안나.\""]], [["Everything in this letter exasperated Countess Lidia Ivanovna: its contents and the allusion to magnanimity, and especially its free and easy—as she considered—tone.", "이 편지의 모든 것이 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인의 화를 돋우었다. 그 내용도, 너그러움 운운하는 대목도, 무엇보다 그녀가 보기에 거리낌 없고 태평스러운 그 어조가 그러했다."]], [["“Say that there is no answer,”", "\"답장은 없다고 전해라.\""], ["said Countess Lidia Ivanovna, and immediately opening her blotting-book, she wrote to Alexey Alexandrovitch that she hoped to see him at one o’clock at the levee.", "리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인은 이렇게 말하고는 곧바로 압지첩을 펼쳐, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에게 한 시에 궁정 알현식에서 그를 보았으면 한다고 써 보냈다."]], [["“I must talk with you of a grave and painful subject.", "\"중대하고 괴로운 일에 관해 당신과 이야기를 나눠야겠습니다."], ["There we will arrange where to meet.", "어디서 만날지는 거기서 정하기로 하지요."], ["Best of all at my house, where I will order tea as you like it.", "무엇보다도 우리 집이 좋겠습니다. 당신이 좋아하시는 대로 차를 내오라 이르겠어요."], ["Urgent.", "급한 일입니다."], ["He lays the cross, but He gives the strength to bear it,”", "그분께서 십자가를 지우시지만, 또한 그것을 견딜 힘도 주신답니다.\""], ["she added, so as to give him some slight preparation.", "그녀는 그에게 마음의 준비를 조금이나마 시켜 두려고 이렇게 덧붙였다."], ["Countess Lidia Ivanovna usually wrote some two or three letters a day to Alexey Alexandrovitch.", "리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인은 보통 하루에 두세 통의 편지를 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에게 써 보내곤 했다."], ["She enjoyed that form of communication, which gave opportunity for a refinement and air of mystery not afforded by their personal interviews.", "그녀는 이런 형태의 소통을 즐겼는데, 직접 만나서 이야기할 때는 누릴 수 없는 세련됨과 신비로운 분위기를 거기서 얻을 수 있었기 때문이다."]], [["# Chapter 24", "# Chapter 24"]], [["The levee was drawing to a close.", "접견회가 막바지에 이르고 있었다."], ["People met as they were going away, and gossiped of the latest news, of the newly bestowed honors and the changes in the positions of the higher functionaries.", "사람들은 돌아가는 길에 마주치면 최근의 소식들, 새로 내려진 훈장들, 고위 관료들의 인사 변동에 대해 수다를 떨었다."]], [["“If only Countess Marya Borissovna were Minister of War, and Princess Vatkovskaya were Commander-in-Chief,”", "\"마리야 보리소브나 백작 부인이 육군 장관이 되고, 바트콥스카야 공작 영애가 총사령관이 되기만 한다면 좋을 텐데요.\""], ["said a gray-headed, little old man in a gold-embroidered uniform, addressing a tall, handsome maid of honor who had questioned him about the new appointments.", "금실로 수놓은 제복을 입은 머리가 희끗희끗한 작은 노인이, 새 인사 발령에 대해 묻던 키 크고 아름다운 시녀를 향해 말했다."]], [["“And me among the adjutants,”", "\"그리고 저는 부관들 가운데 끼고요.\""], ["said the maid of honor, smiling.", "시녀가 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["“You have an appointment already.", "\"당신은 이미 한 자리 차지하고 있지 않습니까."], ["You’re over the ecclesiastical department.", "종무국을 맡고 계시지요."], ["And your assistant’s Karenin.”", "그리고 보좌관이 카레닌이고요.\""]], [["“Good-day, prince!”", "\"안녕하십니까, 공작!\""], ["said the little old man to a man who came up to him.", "작은 노인이 자기 쪽으로 다가오는 한 남자에게 말했다."]], [["“What were you saying of Karenin?”", "\"카레닌에 대해 무슨 말씀을 하고 계셨습니까?\""], ["said the prince.", "공작이 물었다."]], [["“He and Putyatov have received the Alexander Nevsky.”", "\"그와 푸탸토프가 알렉산드르 넵스키 훈장을 받았답니다.\""]], [["“I thought he had it already.”", "\"이미 받은 줄 알았는데요.\""]], [["“No.", "\"아닙니다."], ["Just look at him,”", "저기 좀 보십시오.\""], ["said the little old man, pointing with his embroidered hat to Karenin in a court uniform with the new red ribbon across his shoulders, standing in the doorway of the hall with an influential member of the Imperial Council.", "작은 노인이 수놓은 모자로 카레닌을 가리키며 말했다. 카레닌은 새로 받은 붉은 리본을 어깨에 비스듬히 두른 궁정 제복 차림으로, 황실 자문회의의 영향력 있는 인사 한 명과 함께 홀 문간에 서 있었다."], ["“Pleased and happy as a brass farthing,”", "\"동전 한 닢처럼 흐뭇하고 행복해 보이는군요.\""], ["he added, stopping to shake hands with a handsome gentleman of the bedchamber of colossal proportions.", "그는 이렇게 덧붙이며, 거구의 잘생긴 시종과 악수하기 위해 걸음을 멈췄다."]], [["“No; he’s looking older,”", "\"아니요, 늙어 보이는데요.\""], ["said the gentleman of the bedchamber.", "시종이 말했다."]], [["“From overwork.", "\"일을 너무 많이 해서지요."], ["He’s always drawing up projects nowadays.", "요즘 그는 늘 무슨 계획안을 작성하고 있답니다."], ["He won’t let a poor devil go nowadays till he’s explained it all to him under heads.”", "요즘은 가엾은 상대를 붙잡으면 모든 걸 항목별로 설명해 줄 때까지 놓아주질 않아요.\""]], [["“Looking older, did you say?", "\"늙어 보인다고 하셨습니까?"], ["Il fait des passions.", "Il fait des passions."], ["I believe Countess Lidia Ivanovna’s jealous now of his wife.”", "제 생각엔 요즘 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인이 그의 아내를 질투하는 모양입니다.\""]], [["“Oh, come now, please don’t say any harm of Countess Lidia Ivanovna.”", "\"오, 그러지 마십시오, 제발 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인에 대해 나쁜 말은 하지 마세요.\""]], [["“Why, is there any harm in her being in love with Karenin?”", "\"아니, 그녀가 카레닌을 사랑한다고 해서 나쁠 게 뭐 있습니까?\""]], [["“But is it true Madame Karenina’s here?”", "\"그런데 카레닌 부인이 여기 와 있다는 게 사실입니까?\""]], [["“Well, not here in the palace, but in Petersburg.", "\"음, 여기 궁정에 있는 건 아니고, 페테르부르크에 있지요."], ["I met her yesterday with Alexey Vronsky, bras dessous, bras dessous, in the Morsky.”", "어제 모르스카야 거리에서 알렉세이 브론스키와 함께 있는 걸 봤어요, bras dessous, bras dessous로요.\""]], [["“C’est un homme qui n’a pas,...”", "\"C'est un homme qui n'a pas……\""], ["the gentleman of the bedchamber was beginning, but he stopped to make room, bowing, for a member of the Imperial family to pass.", "시종이 말을 꺼내다가, 황실 일가의 한 사람이 지나가도록 허리를 굽혀 자리를 비키느라 말을 멈췄다."]], [["Thus people talked incessantly of Alexey Alexandrovitch, finding fault with him and laughing at him, while he, blocking up the way of the member of the Imperial Council he had captured, was explaining to him point by point his new financial project, never interrupting his discourse for an instant for fear he should escape.", "* Il fait des passions: 프랑스어로 '그가 열정을 불러일으킨다'는 뜻. bras dessous: 프랑스어로 '팔짱을 끼고'라는 뜻. C'est un homme qui n'a pas: 프랑스어로 '그는 ……이 없는 사람이다'라는 뜻."]], [["Almost at the same time that his wife left Alexey Alexandrovitch there had come to him that bitterest moment in the life of an official—the moment when his upward career comes to a full stop.", "이렇게 사람들은 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에 대해 흠을 잡고 비웃으며 끊임없이 이야기를 나누었고, 그동안 그는 붙잡아 둔 황실 자문회의 인사의 앞을 가로막은 채, 자신의 새 재정 계획안을 조목조목 설명하면서, 상대가 달아날까 봐 한순간도 자신의 이야기를 멈추지 않았다."], ["This full stop had arrived and everyone perceived it, but Alexey Alexandrovitch himself was not yet aware that his career was over.", "아내가 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치를 떠난 것과 거의 동시에, 관료의 인생에서 가장 쓰라린 순간이—상승 일로를 걷던 출셋길이 완전히 멈춰 서는 순간이—그에게 찾아왔다."], ["Whether it was due to his feud with Stremov, or his misfortune with his wife, or simply that Alexey Alexandrovitch had reached his destined limits, it had become evident to everyone in the course of that year that his career was at an end.", "그 정지의 순간이 도래했고 모두가 그것을 알아차렸지만, 정작 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치 자신은 자기 출셋길이 끝났다는 것을 아직 깨닫지 못하고 있었다. 그것이 스트레모프와의 불화 탓이든, 아내로 인한 불운 탓이든, 아니면 단순히 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 정해진 한계에 도달한 것이든, 그해가 흐르는 동안 그의 출셋길이 끝났다는 것은 모두에게 분명해졌다."], ["He still filled a position of consequence, he sat on many commissions and committees, but he was a man whose day was over, and from whom nothing was expected.", "그는 여전히 비중 있는 자리를 차지하고 있었고, 여러 위원회와 분과에 이름을 올리고 있었지만, 그는 이미 전성기가 지난 사람, 더 이상 아무것도 기대할 것이 없는 사람이었다."], ["Whatever he said, whatever he proposed, was heard as though it were something long familiar, and the very thing that was not needed.", "그가 무슨 말을 하든, 무엇을 제안하든, 그것은 오래전부터 익히 알려진 것이자 도무지 필요하지 않은 것으로 여겨졌다."], ["But Alexey Alexandrovitch was not aware of this, and, on the contrary, being cut off from direct participation in governmental activity, he saw more clearly than ever the errors and defects in the action of others, and thought it his duty to point out means for their correction.", "그러나 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 이것을 깨닫지 못했고, 오히려 정부 활동에 직접 참여하는 데서 차단되자, 다른 이들의 행동에서 드러나는 오류와 결함을 그 어느 때보다 또렷이 보았으며, 그것을 바로잡을 방안을 지적하는 것이 자기 의무라고 여겼다."], ["Shortly after his separation from his wife, he began writing his first note on the new judicial procedure, the first of the endless series of notes he was destined to write in the future.", "아내와 헤어진 직후, 그는 새로운 사법 절차에 관한 첫 보고서를 쓰기 시작했는데, 그것은 앞으로 그가 끝없이 쓰게 될 보고서들의 첫 번째였다."]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch did not merely fail to observe his hopeless position in the official world, he was not merely free from anxiety on this head, he was positively more satisfied than ever with his own activity.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 관료 사회에서 자기 처지가 가망 없다는 것을 알아차리지 못했을 뿐 아니라, 이 점에 대해 아무런 불안도 느끼지 않았으며, 오히려 자기 활동에 그 어느 때보다 만족하고 있었다."]], [["“He that is unmarried careth for the things that belong to the Lord, how he may please the Lord: but he that is married careth for the things that are of the world, how he may please his wife,”", "\"장가들지 않은 자는 주의 일을 염려하여 어찌하여야 주를 기쁘게 할까 하되, 장가든 자는 세상일을 염려하여 어찌하여야 아내를 기쁘게 할까 하느니라.\""], ["says the Apostle Paul, and Alexey Alexandrovitch, who was now guided in every action by Scripture, often recalled this text.", "사도 바울은 이렇게 말했고, 이제 모든 행동을 성경에 따라 인도받는 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 이 구절을 자주 떠올렸다."], ["It seemed to him that ever since he had been left without a wife, he had in these very projects of reform been serving the Lord more zealously than before.", "아내 없이 홀로 남겨진 이래, 자기가 바로 이 개혁 계획들 속에서 전보다 더 열성적으로 주를 섬겨 왔다고 그는 여겼다."]], [["The unmistakable impatience of the member of the Council trying to get away from him did not trouble Alexey Alexandrovitch; he gave up his exposition only when the member of the Council, seizing his chance when one of the Imperial family was passing, slipped away from him.", "자문회의 인사가 그에게서 벗어나려고 안달하는 기색이 역력했지만, 그것은 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치를 괴롭히지 못했다. 그가 설명을 그만둔 것은, 자문회의 인사가 황실 일가의 한 사람이 지나가는 틈을 놓치지 않고 그에게서 빠져나가 버렸을 때뿐이었다."]], [["Left alone, Alexey Alexandrovitch looked down, collecting his thoughts, then looked casually about him and walked towards the door, where he hoped to meet Countess Lidia Ivanovna.", "홀로 남겨진 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 시선을 떨군 채 생각을 가다듬다가, 무심히 주위를 둘러보고는 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인을 만나게 되리라고 기대하던 문 쪽으로 걸어갔다."]], [["“And how strong they all are, how sound physically,”", "\"저들은 모두 얼마나 건장한가, 얼마나 육체적으로 멀쩡한가.\""], ["thought Alexey Alexandrovitch, looking at the powerfully built gentleman of the bedchamber with his well-combed, perfumed whiskers, and at the red neck of the prince, pinched by his tight uniform.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 잘 빗어 넘긴 향수 뿌린 구레나룻을 한 건장한 시종과, 꽉 끼는 제복에 짓눌린 공작의 붉은 목덜미를 바라보며 생각했다."], ["He had to pass them on his way.", "그는 그들 곁을 지나쳐 가야만 했다."], ["“Truly is it said that all the world is evil,”", "\"온 세상이 악하다는 말은 참으로 옳도다.\""], ["he thought, with another sidelong glance at the calves of the gentleman of the bedchamber.", "그는 시종의 종아리를 또 한 번 곁눈질하며 생각했다."]], [["Moving forward deliberately, Alexey Alexandrovitch bowed with his customary air of weariness and dignity to the gentleman who had been talking about him, and looking towards the door, his eyes sought Countess Lidia Ivanovna.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 신중하게 앞으로 나아가며, 자기에 대해 떠들고 있던 신사에게 평소의 그 지친 듯하면서도 위엄 있는 태도로 인사를 하고는, 문 쪽을 바라보며 눈으로 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인을 찾았다."]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch!”", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치!\""], ["said the little old man, with a malicious light in his eyes, at the moment when Karenin was on a level with them, and was nodding with a frigid gesture, “I haven’t congratulated you yet,”", "카레닌이 그들과 나란히 서서 쌀쌀맞은 몸짓으로 고개를 끄덕이는 순간, 작은 노인이 눈에 짓궂은 빛을 띠며 말했다. \"아직 축하 인사를 못 드렸군요.\""], ["said the old man, pointing to his newly received ribbon.", "노인은 그가 새로 받은 리본을 가리키며 말했다."]], [["“Thank you,”", "\"고맙습니다.\""], ["answered Alexey Alexandrovitch.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 대답했다."], ["“What an exquisite day today,”", "\"오늘은 참으로 절묘한 날이군요.\""], ["he added, laying emphasis in his peculiar way on the word exquisite.", "그는 절묘한이라는 단어에 자기 특유의 방식으로 힘을 주며 덧붙였다."]], [["That they laughed at him he was well aware, but he did not expect anything but hostility from them; he was used to that by now.", "저들이 자기를 비웃고 있다는 것을 그는 잘 알고 있었지만, 그는 저들에게서 적의 말고는 아무것도 기대하지 않았다. 그는 이제 그것에 익숙해져 있었다."]], [["Catching sight of the yellow shoulders of Lidia Ivanovna jutting out above her corset, and her fine pensive eyes bidding him to her, Alexey Alexandrovitch smiled, revealing untarnished white teeth, and went towards her.", "코르셋 위로 불룩 솟은 리디아 이바노브나의 누런 어깨와, 자기를 부르는 그녀의 곱고 생각에 잠긴 듯한 눈이 시야에 들어오자, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 빛바래지 않은 흰 이를 드러내며 미소 짓고는 그녀 쪽으로 걸어갔다."]], [["Lidia Ivanovna’s dress had cost her great pains, as indeed all her dresses had done of late.", "리디아 이바노브나의 드레스는, 사실 요즘 그녀의 모든 드레스가 그러했듯, 그녀에게 무던히도 애를 먹였다."], ["Her aim in dress was now quite the reverse of that she had pursued thirty years before.", "옷차림에서 그녀가 지향하는 바는 이제 삼십 년 전에 추구하던 것과 정반대였다."], ["Then her desire had been to adorn herself with something, and the more adorned the better.", "그때 그녀의 바람은 무언가로 몸을 치장하는 것이었고, 더 많이 치장할수록 좋았다."], ["Now, on the contrary, she was perforce decked out in a way so inconsistent with her age and her figure, that her one anxiety was to contrive that the contrast between these adornments and her own exterior should not be too appalling.", "그런데 지금은 그와 반대로, 자기 나이와 몸매에 너무도 어울리지 않게 어쩔 수 없이 잔뜩 꾸며야만 했기에, 그녀의 유일한 근심거리는 이 치장과 자신의 외모 사이의 대비가 너무 끔찍해 보이지 않도록 궁리하는 것이었다."], ["And as far as Alexey Alexandrovitch was concerned she succeeded, and was in his eyes attractive.", "그리고 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에 관한 한 그녀는 성공을 거두었고, 그의 눈에 매력적으로 비쳤다."], ["For him she was the one island not only of goodwill to him, but of love in the midst of the sea of hostility and jeering that surrounded him.", "그를 둘러싼 적의와 조롱의 바다 한가운데서, 그녀는 그에게 호의뿐 아니라 사랑까지 베풀어 주는 유일한 섬이었다."]], [["Passing through rows of ironical eyes, he was drawn as naturally to her loving glance as a plant to the sun.", "조롱 어린 눈길의 행렬 사이를 지나, 그는 식물이 태양을 향하듯 자연스럽게 그녀의 사랑스러운 눈길로 이끌려 갔다."]], [["“I congratulate you,”", "\"축하드려요.\""], ["she said to him, her eyes on his ribbon.", "그녀가 그의 리본에 눈길을 두며 말했다."]], [["Suppressing a smile of pleasure, he shrugged his shoulders, closing his eyes, as though to say that that could not be a source of joy to him.", "기쁜 미소를 억누르며 그는 어깨를 으쓱하고는, 그것이 자기에게 기쁨의 원천이 될 수 없다는 듯이 눈을 감았다."], ["Countess Lidia Ivanovna was very well aware that it was one of his chief sources of satisfaction, though he never admitted it.", "리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인은, 비록 그가 결코 인정하지 않았지만, 그것이 그의 가장 큰 만족거리 가운데 하나라는 것을 아주 잘 알고 있었다."]], [["“How is our angel?”", "\"우리 천사는 어떻게 지내나요?\""], ["said Countess Lidia Ivanovna, meaning Seryozha.", "리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인이 세료자를 두고 말했다."]], [["“I can’t say I was quite pleased with him,”", "\"그 애가 아주 만족스럽다고는 말 못 하겠습니다.\""], ["said Alexey Alexandrovitch, raising his eyebrows and opening his eyes.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 눈썹을 치켜올리며 눈을 떴다."], ["“And Sitnikov is not satisfied with him.”", "\"그리고 시트니코프도 그 애에게 만족하지 못하고 있어요.\""], ["(Sitnikov was the tutor to whom Seryozha’s secular education had been intrusted.) “As I have mentioned to you, there’s a sort of coldness in him towards the most important questions which ought to touch the heart of every man and every child....”", "(시트니코프는 세료자의 세속 교육을 맡은 가정교사였다.) \"전에도 말씀드렸듯이, 그 애는 모든 사람과 모든 아이의 마음을 움직여야 할 가장 중요한 문제들에 대해 일종의 냉담함을 보입니다…….\""], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch began expounding his views on the sole question that interested him besides the service—the education of his son.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 관직 외에 자기가 관심을 두는 유일한 문제, 곧 아들의 교육에 대한 자신의 견해를 펼치기 시작했다."]], [["When Alexey Alexandrovitch with Lidia Ivanovna’s help had been brought back anew to life and activity, he felt it his duty to undertake the education of the son left on his hands.", "리디아 이바노브나의 도움으로 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 새로이 삶과 활동으로 되돌아왔을 때, 그는 자기 손에 남겨진 아들의 교육을 떠맡는 것이 자기 의무라고 느꼈다."], ["Having never before taken any interest in educational questions, Alexey Alexandrovitch devoted some time to the theoretical study of the subject.", "전에는 교육 문제에 아무런 관심도 가져 본 적이 없었던 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 이 주제를 이론적으로 연구하는 데 얼마간 시간을 바쳤다."], ["After reading several books on anthropology, education, and didactics, Alexey Alexandrovitch drew up a plan of education, and engaging the best tutor in Petersburg to superintend it, he set to work, and the subject continually absorbed him.", "인류학, 교육학, 교수법에 관한 책 몇 권을 읽은 뒤, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 교육 계획을 세웠고, 그것을 지도할 페테르부르크 최고의 가정교사를 고용하여 일에 착수했다. 그리고 이 주제는 끊임없이 그를 사로잡았다."]], [["“Yes, but the heart.", "\"네, 하지만 마음이지요."], ["I see in him his father’s heart, and with such a heart a child cannot go far wrong,”", "저는 그 애에게서 그 아버지의 마음을 봅니다. 그런 마음을 가진 아이라면 크게 잘못될 리 없어요.\""], ["said Lidia Ivanovna with enthusiasm.", "리디아 이바노브나가 열성적으로 말했다."]], [["“Yes, perhaps....", "\"네, 어쩌면 그럴지도요……"], ["As for me, I do my duty.", "저로 말하자면, 저는 제 의무를 다할 뿐입니다."], ["It’s all I can do.”", "제가 할 수 있는 건 그게 전부예요.\""]], [["“You’re coming to me,”", "\"우리 집에 오시는 거죠?\""], ["said Countess Lidia Ivanovna, after a pause; “we have to speak of a subject painful for you.", "리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인이 잠시 사이를 두고 말했다. \"당신에게 괴로운 일에 대해 이야기를 나눠야 해요."], ["I would give anything to have spared you certain memories, but others are not of the same mind.", "어떤 기억들로부터 당신을 지켜 드릴 수만 있다면 무엇이든 다 내놓겠지만, 다른 이들은 그렇게 생각지 않는군요."], ["I have received a letter from her.", "저는 그 여자에게서 편지를 받았어요."], ["She is here in Petersburg.”", "그 여자가 여기 페테르부르크에 와 있어요.\""]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch shuddered at the allusion to his wife, but immediately his face assumed the deathlike rigidity which expressed utter helplessness in the matter.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 아내에 대한 암시에 몸을 떨었지만, 곧 그의 얼굴은 이 문제에 대해 완전한 무력감을 드러내는 죽은 사람 같은 굳은 표정을 띠었다."]], [["“I was expecting it,”", "\"예상하고 있었습니다.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["Countess Lidia Ivanovna looked at him ecstatically, and tears of rapture at the greatness of his soul came into her eyes.", "리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인은 황홀해하며 그를 바라보았고, 그의 영혼의 위대함에 감격한 눈물이 그녀의 눈에 차올랐다."]], [["# Chapter 25", "# Chapter 25"]], [["When Alexey Alexandrovitch came into the Countess Lidia Ivanovna’s snug little boudoir, decorated with old china and hung with portraits, the lady herself had not yet made her appearance.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인의 아늑하고 작은 규방으로 들어섰을 때, 오래된 도자기로 꾸며지고 초상화들이 걸린 그 방에는 정작 부인의 모습이 아직 보이지 않았다."]], [["She was changing her dress.", "그녀는 옷을 갈아입는 중이었다."]], [["A cloth was laid on a round table, and on it stood a china tea service and a silver spirit-lamp and tea kettle.", "둥근 탁자 위에는 식탁보가 깔려 있었고, 그 위에 도자기 찻잔 세트와 은제 알코올램프, 그리고 찻주전자가 놓여 있었다."], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch looked idly about at the endless familiar portraits which adorned the room, and sitting down to the table, he opened a New Testament lying upon it.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 방을 장식한, 익숙하기 그지없는 끝없는 초상화들을 멍하니 둘러보다가, 탁자에 앉아 그 위에 놓인 신약성서를 펼쳤다."], ["The rustle of the countess’s silk skirt drew his attention off.", "백작 부인의 비단 치마가 사각거리는 소리에 그의 주의가 흩어졌다."]], [["“Well now, we can sit quietly,”", "\"자, 이제 우리 조용히 앉아서,\""], ["said Countess Lidia Ivanovna, slipping hurriedly with an agitated smile between the table and the sofa, “and talk over our tea.”", "리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인은 들뜬 미소를 지으며 탁자와 소파 사이로 황급히 미끄러지듯 들어와 앉았다. \"차를 마시며 이야기를 나눌 수 있겠네요.\""]], [["After some words of preparation, Countess Lidia Ivanovna, breathing hard and flushing crimson, gave into Alexey Alexandrovitch’s hands the letter she had received.", "미리 준비해 둔 몇 마디를 건넨 뒤, 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인은 숨을 가쁘게 몰아쉬고 얼굴이 새빨갛게 달아오른 채로, 자기가 받은 편지를 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 손에 넘겨주었다."]], [["After reading the letter, he sat a long while in silence.", "편지를 다 읽고 나서, 그는 오랫동안 말없이 앉아 있었다."]], [["“I don’t think I have the right to refuse her,”", "\"나에게는 그녀의 청을 거절할 권리가 없는 것 같습니다.\""], ["he said, timidly lifting his eyes.", "그가 머뭇거리며 눈을 들어 말했다."]], [["“Dear friend, you never see evil in anyone!”", "\"친애하는 벗이여, 당신은 누구에게서도 악을 보지 못하시는군요!\""]], [["“On the contrary, I see that all is evil.", "\"오히려 그 반대입니다. 나는 모든 것이 악이라는 걸 봅니다."], ["But whether it is just....”", "하지만 그것이 과연 옳은 일인지는…….\""]], [["His face showed irresolution, and a seeking for counsel, support, and guidance in a matter he did not understand.", "그의 얼굴에는 망설임과, 자기로서는 이해할 수 없는 문제에 대해 조언과 지지와 인도를 구하는 빛이 떠올라 있었다."]], [["“No,”", "\"아니에요.\""], ["Countess Lidia Ivanovna interrupted him; “there are limits to everything.", "리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인이 그의 말을 가로막았다. \"모든 일에는 한계가 있는 법이에요."], ["I can understand immorality,”", "부도덕은 이해할 수 있어요.\" 그녀는 이렇게 말했지만 이는 그다지 진실하지 못했다."], ["she said, not quite truthfully, since she never could understand that which leads women to immorality; “but I don’t understand cruelty: to whom? to you!", "여자를 부도덕으로 이끄는 것이 무엇인지 그녀는 결코 이해할 수 없었기 때문이다."], ["How can she stay in the town where you are?", "\"하지만 잔인함은 이해할 수가 없어요. 대체 누구한테 그러는 거죠?"], ["No, the longer one lives the more one learns.", "당신한테요! 어떻게 그 여자가 당신이 있는 도시에 머물 수 있단 말인가요?"], ["And I’m learning to understand your loftiness and her baseness.”", "아니에요, 사람은 오래 살수록 더 많이 배우는 법이죠. 그리고 나는 당신의 고결함과 그 여자의 비열함을 이해하는 법을 배우고 있어요.\""]], [["“Who is to throw a stone?”", "\"누가 돌을 던질 수 있겠습니까?\""], ["said Alexey Alexandrovitch, unmistakably pleased with the part he had to play.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 자기가 맡은 역할에 분명히 만족스러워하며 말했다."], ["“I have forgiven all, and so I cannot deprive her of what is exacted by love in her—by her love for her son....”", "\"나는 모든 것을 용서했습니다. 그러니 그녀 안에 있는 사랑이—아들에 대한 그녀의 사랑이 요구하는 것을 빼앗을 수는 없지요…….\""]], [["“But is that love, my friend?", "\"하지만 그것이 사랑일까요, 벗이여?"], ["Is it sincere?", "그것이 진실한 것일까요?"], ["Admitting that you have forgiven—that you forgive—have we the right to work on the feelings of that angel?", "당신이 용서했다고—용서한다고 칩시다. 그렇다고 우리에게 그 천사 같은 아이의 감정을 흔들 권리가 있을까요?"], ["He looks on her as dead.", "그 아이는 어머니를 죽은 사람으로 여기고 있어요."], ["He prays for her, and beseeches God to have mercy on her sins.", "그 아이는 어머니를 위해 기도하고, 그녀의 죄를 자비롭게 여겨 달라고 하느님께 간구하고 있다고요."], ["And it is better so.", "그러니 이대로가 더 나아요."], ["But now what will he think?”", "그런데 이제 와서 그 아이가 무슨 생각을 하겠어요?\""]], [["“I had not thought of that,”", "\"거기까지는 미처 생각하지 못했습니다.\""], ["said Alexey Alexandrovitch, evidently agreeing.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 분명히 동의하며 말했다."]], [["Countess Lidia Ivanovna hid her face in her hands and was silent.", "리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인은 두 손에 얼굴을 묻고 말이 없었다."], ["She was praying.", "그녀는 기도를 드리고 있었다."]], [["“If you ask my advice,”", "\"제 조언을 구하신다면,\""], ["she said, having finished her prayer and uncovered her face, “I do not advise you to do this.", "기도를 마치고 얼굴에서 손을 떼면서 그녀가 말했다. \"저는 이 일을 하지 마시라고 권하겠어요."], ["Do you suppose I don’t see how you are suffering, how this has torn open your wounds?", "당신이 얼마나 괴로워하시는지, 이 일이 당신의 상처를 얼마나 헤집어 놓았는지 제가 모를 것 같으세요?"], ["But supposing that, as always, you don’t think of yourself, what can it lead to?—to fresh suffering for you, to torture for the child.", "하지만 당신이 늘 그러시듯 자신은 생각하지 않으신다 쳐도, 이 일이 어떤 결과를 가져오겠어요? 당신에게는 새로운 고통을, 그 아이에게는 시련을 안기겠지요."], ["If there were a trace of humanity left in her, she ought not to wish for it herself.", "그 여자에게 인간적인 면이 조금이라도 남아 있다면, 자기 입으로 이런 걸 바라서는 안 되는 거예요. 아니에요, 저는 망설임 없이 말씀드리겠어요."], ["No, I have no hesitation in saying I advise not, and if you will intrust it to me, I will write to her.”", "하지 마시라고요. 그리고 이 일을 제게 맡겨 주신다면, 제가 그녀에게 편지를 쓰겠어요.\""]], [["And Alexey Alexandrovitch consented, and Countess Lidia Ivanovna sent the following letter in French:", "그리하여 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 동의했고, 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인은 프랑스어로 다음과 같은 편지를 보냈다."]], [["“Dear Madame,", "\"친애하는 부인,"]], [["“To be reminded of you might have results for your son in leading to questions on his part which could not be answered without implanting in the child’s soul a spirit of censure towards what should be for him sacred, and therefore I beg you to interpret your husband’s refusal in the spirit of Christian love.", "당신에 관한 기억을 떠올리는 것은 당신의 아들에게 어떤 결과를 가져올 수 있습니다. 곧 아이의 마음속에 그가 신성하게 여겨야 할 것에 대한 비난의 정신을 심지 않고서는 답할 수 없는 의문을 그 아이가 품게 만들 수 있다는 것입니다."], ["I pray to Almighty God to have mercy on you.", "그러니 부디 당신 남편의 거절을 그리스도교적 사랑의 정신으로 받아들여 주시기를 청합니다. 전능하신 하느님께서 당신에게 자비를 베풀어 주시기를 기도합니다."]], [["“Countess Lidia.”", "리디아 백작 부인.\""]], [["This letter attained the secret object which Countess Lidia Ivanovna had concealed from herself.", "이 편지는 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인이 스스로에게도 숨겨 온 은밀한 목적을 이루었다."], ["It wounded Anna to the quick.", "그것은 안나에게 깊은 상처를 입혔다."]], [["For his part, Alexey Alexandrovitch, on returning home from Lidia Ivanovna’s, could not all that day concentrate himself on his usual pursuits, and find that spiritual peace of one saved and believing which he had felt of late.", "한편 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 리디아 이바노브나의 집에서 돌아온 뒤, 그날 하루 종일 평소 하던 일에 마음을 집중할 수도 없었고, 근래에 느껴 오던, 구원받고 믿음을 가진 자의 그 영적 평온도 찾을 수 없었다."]], [["The thought of his wife, who had so greatly sinned against him, and towards whom he had been so saintly, as Countess Lidia Ivanovna had so justly told him, ought not to have troubled him; but he was not easy; he could not understand the book he was reading; he could not drive away harassing recollections of his relations with her, of the mistake which, as it now seemed, he had made in regard to her.", "자기에게 그토록 큰 죄를 지은 아내, 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인이 그토록 옳게 말해 주었듯이 자기가 그토록 성스럽게 대해 준 아내에 대한 생각은, 그를 괴롭히지 말았어야 했다. 그런데도 그는 마음이 편치 않았다."], ["The memory of how he had received her confession of infidelity on their way home from the races (especially that he had insisted only on the observance of external decorum, and had not sent a challenge) tortured him like a remorse.", "읽고 있는 책을 이해할 수도 없었고, 그녀와의 관계에 대한, 그리고 이제 와서 생각하니 그녀에 대해 저질러 버린 것 같은 그 실수에 대한 괴로운 기억들을 떨쳐 버릴 수도 없었다. 경마장에서 집으로 돌아오던 길에 그녀가 부정을 고백했을 때 자기가 어떻게 받아들였는지(특히 겉으로 드러나는 체면을 지키라고만 했을 뿐 결투를 신청하지 않았다는 것)에 대한 기억은 회한처럼 그를 괴롭혔다."], ["He was tortured too by the thought of the letter he had written her; and most of all, his forgiveness, which nobody wanted, and his care of the other man’s child made his heart burn with shame and remorse.", "그녀에게 써 보냈던 편지에 대한 생각도 그를 괴롭혔다. 그리고 무엇보다, 아무도 원하지 않은 그의 용서와, 다른 남자의 자식을 돌본다는 일이 수치심과 회한으로 그의 가슴을 타오르게 했다."]], [["And just the same feeling of shame and regret he felt now, as he reviewed all his past with her, recalling the awkward words in which, after long wavering, he had made her an offer.", "그리고 지금도 그는 똑같은 수치심과 후회를 느꼈다. 그녀와 함께한 자기의 모든 과거를 되짚어 보면서, 오랜 망설임 끝에 그녀에게 청혼하던 그 어색했던 말들을 떠올렸던 것이다."]], [["“But how have I been to blame?”", "\"하지만 내가 무슨 잘못을 했단 말인가?\""], ["he said to himself.", "그는 혼잣말로 중얼거렸다."], ["And this question always excited another question in him—whether they felt differently, did their loving and marrying differently, these Vronskys and Oblonskys ...", "그리고 이 물음은 그의 안에서 언제나 또 다른 물음을 불러일으켰다."], ["these gentlemen of the bedchamber, with their fine calves.", "저들, 저 브론스키며 오블론스키 같은 자들은……"], ["And there passed before his mind a whole series of these mettlesome, vigorous, self-confident men, who always and everywhere drew his inquisitive attention in spite of himself.", "그 늘씬한 종아리를 가진 시종(侍從)들은, 사랑하고 결혼하는 일을 혹시 다르게 느끼고 다르게 하는 것은 아닐까 하는 물음이었다. 그러자 그 혈기 왕성하고 정력적이며 자신만만한 사내들이 줄지어 그의 머릿속을 스쳐 지나갔다."], ["He tried to dispel these thoughts, he tried to persuade himself that he was not living for this transient life, but for the life of eternity, and that there was peace and love in his heart.", "그들은 그가 원치 않는데도 언제나 어디서나 그의 호기심 어린 주의를 끄는 자들이었다. 그는 이런 생각들을 떨쳐 내려 애썼고, 자기는 이 덧없는 삶을 위해서가 아니라 영원한 삶을 위해 살고 있으며, 자기 마음속에는 평화와 사랑이 깃들어 있다고 스스로를 설득하려 애썼다."]], [["But the fact that he had in this transient, trivial life made, as it seemed to him, a few trivial mistakes tortured him as though the eternal salvation in which he believed had no existence.", "그러나 이 덧없고 하찮은 삶에서 자기가, 보기에는 몇 가지 하찮은 실수를 저질렀다는 사실이, 마치 자기가 믿는 영원한 구원이라는 것이 존재하지 않기라도 한 듯 그를 괴롭혔다."], ["But this temptation did not last long, and soon there was reestablished once more in Alexey Alexandrovitch’s soul the peace and the elevation by virtue of which he could forget what he did not want to remember.", "하지만 이 유혹은 오래가지 않았고, 이내 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 영혼 속에는 다시금 평화와 고양이 자리 잡았다. 그것의 힘으로 그는 기억하고 싶지 않은 것을 잊을 수 있었다."]], [["# Chapter 26", "# Chapter 26"]], [["“Well, Kapitonitch?”", "\"카피토니치, 어땠어?\""], ["said Seryozha, coming back rosy and good-humored from his walk the day before his birthday, and giving his overcoat to the tall old hall-porter, who smiled down at the little person from the height of his long figure.", "세료자는 생일 전날 산책에서 발그레하고 기분 좋은 얼굴로 돌아와, 키 큰 늙은 현관지기에게 외투를 건네며 물었다. 현관지기는 자기 긴 키의 높이에서 그 조그만 사람을 내려다보며 빙긋이 웃었다."], ["“Well, has the bandaged clerk been here today?", "\"그래, 붕대 감은 그 관리가 오늘 왔었어?"], ["Did papa see him?”", "아빠가 그 사람 만났어?\""]], [["“He saw him.", "\"만나셨지."], ["The minute the chief secretary came out, I announced him,”", "비서관이 나오자마자 제가 들여보냈답니다.\""], ["said the hall-porter with a good-humored wink.", "현관지기가 기분 좋게 눈을 찡긋하며 말했다."], ["“Here, I’ll take it off.”", "\"자, 제가 벗겨 드리지요.\""]], [["“Seryozha!”", "\"세료자!\""], ["said the tutor, stopping in the doorway leading to the inner rooms.", "가정교사가 안쪽 방으로 통하는 문간에서 멈춰 서서 말했다."], ["“Take it off yourself.”", "\"혼자서 벗어.\""], ["But Seryozha, though he heard his tutor’s feeble voice, did not pay attention to it.", "그러나 세료자는 가정교사의 가냘픈 목소리를 듣고도 거기에 주의를 기울이지 않았다."], ["He stood keeping hold of the hall-porter’s belt, and gazing into his face.", "그는 현관지기의 허리띠를 붙잡은 채 그의 얼굴을 빤히 올려다보며 서 있었다."]], [["“Well, and did papa do what he wanted for him?”", "\"그래서, 아빠가 그 사람을 위해 원하던 걸 해 줬어?\""]], [["The hall-porter nodded his head affirmatively.", "현관지기가 그렇다는 듯 고개를 끄덕였다."], ["The clerk with his face tied up, who had already been seven times to ask some favor of Alexey Alexandrovitch, interested both Seryozha and the hall-porter.", "얼굴을 싸맨 그 관리는 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에게 무슨 부탁을 하러 벌써 일곱 번이나 찾아온 사람이었는데, 세료자도 현관지기도 그에게 관심을 두고 있었다."], ["Seryozha had come upon him in the hall, and had heard him plaintively beg the hall-porter to announce him, saying that he and his children had death staring them in the face.", "세료자는 현관에서 그와 마주친 적이 있었고, 그가 현관지기에게 자기와 자기 아이들이 죽음을 코앞에 두고 있다고 말하며 처량하게 들여보내 달라고 애원하는 것을 들었다."]], [["Since then Seryozha, having met him a second time in the hall, took great interest in him.", "그 뒤로 세료자는 현관에서 그를 두 번째로 만나고는 그에게 큰 흥미를 가지게 되었다."]], [["“Well, was he very glad?”", "\"그래서, 그 사람 많이 기뻐했어?\""], ["he asked.", "세료자가 물었다."]], [["“Glad? I should think so!", "\"기뻐했냐고요?"], ["Almost dancing as he walked away.”", "말씀이라고요! 돌아가면서 거의 춤을 추다시피 했답니다.\""]], [["“And has anything been left?”", "\"그리고 뭐 남겨 둔 거 있어?\""], ["asked Seryozha, after a pause.", "잠시 뜸을 들인 뒤 세료자가 물었다."]], [["“Come, sir,”", "\"이리 오세요, 도련님.\""], ["said the hall-porter; then with a shake of his head he whispered, “Something from the countess.”", "현관지기가 말하고는 고개를 한 번 흔들며 속삭였다. \"백작 부인한테서 온 게 있답니다.\""]], [["Seryozha understood at once that what the hall-porter was speaking of was a present from Countess Lidia Ivanovna for his birthday.", "세료자는 현관지기가 말하는 것이 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인이 자기 생일을 위해 보낸 선물이라는 걸 단번에 알아차렸다."]], [["“What do you say?", "\"뭐라고?"], ["Where?”", "어디 있는데?\""]], [["“Korney took it to your papa.", "\"코르네이가 도련님 아빠한테 가져갔지요."], ["A fine plaything it must be too!”", "분명 근사한 장난감일 거예요!\""]], [["“How big?", "\"얼마나 커?"], ["Like this?”", "이만큼?\""]], [["“Rather small, but a fine thing.”", "\"좀 작긴 한데, 근사한 물건이에요.\""]], [["“A book.”", "\"책이구나.\""]], [["“No, a thing.", "\"아니요, 물건이라니까요."], ["Run along, run along, Vassily Lukitch is calling you,”", "어서요, 어서 가세요, 바실리 루키치가 부르네요.\""], ["said the porter, hearing the tutor’s steps approaching, and carefully taking away from his belt the little hand in the glove half pulled off, he signed with his head towards the tutor.", "가정교사의 발소리가 다가오는 것을 듣고 현관지기가 말하며, 반쯤 벗겨진 장갑을 낀 작은 손을 자기 허리띠에서 조심스레 떼어 내고는 고갯짓으로 가정교사 쪽을 가리켰다."]], [["“Vassily Lukitch, in a tiny minute!”", "\"바실리 루키치, 잠깐만요, 곧 갈게요!\""], ["answered Seryozha with that gay and loving smile which always won over the conscientious Vassily Lukitch.", "세료자는 늘 그 성실한 바실리 루키치의 마음을 사로잡곤 하던, 그 명랑하고 사랑스러운 미소를 지으며 대답했다."]], [["Seryozha was too happy, everything was too delightful for him to be able to help sharing with his friend the porter the family good fortune of which he had heard during his walk in the public gardens from Lidia Ivanovna’s niece.", "세료자는 너무나 행복했고, 모든 것이 너무나 즐거웠던 터라, 공원에서 산책하는 동안 리디아 이바노브나의 조카에게서 들은 집안의 경사를 친구인 현관지기와 나누지 않고는 견딜 수가 없었다."], ["This piece of good news seemed to him particularly important from its coming at the same time with the gladness of the bandaged clerk and his own gladness at toys having come for him.", "이 좋은 소식은, 붕대 감은 관리의 기쁨, 그리고 자기 앞으로 장난감이 왔다는 자기 자신의 기쁨과 때를 같이하여 찾아왔다는 점에서 그에게 특히 중요해 보였다."], ["It seemed to Seryozha that this was a day on which everyone ought to be glad and happy.", "세료자에게는 이날이 모두가 기뻐하고 행복해야 하는 날인 것만 같았다."]], [["“You know papa’s received the Alexander Nevsky today?”", "\"아빠가 오늘 알렉산드르 넵스키 훈장 받은 거 알아?\""]], [["“To be sure I do!", "\"그럼 알다마다요!"], ["People have been already to congratulate him.”", "벌써 축하하러 온 사람들이 있는걸요.\""]], [["“And is he glad?”", "\"그래서 아빠가 기뻐해?\""]], [["“Glad at the Tsar’s gracious favor!", "\"황제 폐하의 자애로운 은총을 받았으니 기뻐하시지요!"], ["I should think so!", "말씀이라고요!"], ["It’s a proof he’s deserved it,”", "그건 그만한 자격이 있으셨다는 증거랍니다.\""], ["said the porter severely and seriously.", "현관지기가 엄숙하고 진지하게 말했다."]], [["Seryozha fell to dreaming, gazing up at the face of the porter, which he had thoroughly studied in every detail, especially the chin that hung down between the gray whiskers, never seen by anyone but Seryozha, who saw him only from below.", "세료자는 자기가 구석구석 속속들이 살펴본 적 있는 현관지기의 얼굴을, 특히 회색 구레나룻 사이로 늘어진 턱을 올려다보며 몽상에 잠겼다. 그 턱은 늘 아래에서만 그를 올려다보는 세료자 말고는 누구도 본 적 없는 것이었다."]], [["“Well, and has your daughter been to see you lately?”", "\"그래, 따님은 요즘 보러 왔었어?\""]], [["The porter’s daughter was a ballet dancer.", "현관지기의 딸은 발레 무용수였다."]], [["“When is she to come on week-days?", "\"평일에 어떻게 오겠어요?"], ["They’ve their lessons to learn too.", "그 애들도 배워야 할 게 있거든요."], ["And you’ve your lesson, sir; run along.”", "도련님도 공부할 게 있으시잖아요, 어서 가세요.\""]], [["On coming into the room, Seryozha, instead of sitting down to his lessons, told his tutor of his supposition that what had been brought him must be a machine.", "방에 들어온 세료자는 공부 자리에 앉는 대신, 자기에게 온 것이 분명 기계일 거라는 자기 짐작을 가정교사에게 이야기했다."], ["“What do you think?”", "\"선생님은 어떻게 생각하세요?\""], ["he inquired.", "그가 물었다."]], [["But Vassily Lukitch was thinking of nothing but the necessity of learning the grammar lesson for the teacher, who was coming at two.", "그러나 바실리 루키치는 두 시에 오기로 한 선생님을 위해 문법 수업을 익혀 둬야 한다는 생각밖에는 하지 않고 있었다."]], [["“No, do just tell me, Vassily Lukitch,”", "\"아니, 그냥 말씀해 주세요, 바실리 루키치.\""], ["he asked suddenly, when he was seated at their work table with the book in his hands, “what is greater than the Alexander Nevsky?", "그가 손에 책을 든 채 공부 책상 앞에 앉아 별안간 물었다. \"알렉산드르 넵스키보다 더 높은 게 뭐예요?"], ["You know papa’s received the Alexander Nevsky?”", "아빠가 알렉산드르 넵스키 받은 거 아시죠?\""]], [["Vassily Lukitch replied that the Vladimir was greater than the Alexander Nevsky.", "바실리 루키치는 블라디미르 훈장이 알렉산드르 넵스키보다 더 높다고 대답했다."]], [["“And higher still?”", "\"그것보다 더 높은 건요?\""]], [["“Well, highest of all is the Andrey Pervozvanny.”", "\"음, 제일 높은 건 안드레이 페르보즈반니 훈장이지요.\""]], [["“And higher than the Andrey?”", "\"안드레이보다 더 높은 건요?\""]], [["“I don’t know.”", "\"모르겠는데요.\""]], [["“What, you don’t know?”", "\"뭐예요, 모르신다고요?\""], ["and Seryozha, leaning on his elbows, sank into deep meditation.", "세료자는 팔꿈치를 괴고 깊은 명상에 빠져들었다."]], [["His meditations were of the most complex and diverse character.", "그의 명상은 더없이 복잡하고 갖가지로 뻗어 나갔다."], ["He imagined his father’s having suddenly been presented with both the Vladimir and the Andrey today, and in consequence being much better tempered at his lesson, and dreamed how, when he was grown up, he would himself receive all the orders, and what they might invent higher than the Andrey.", "그는 아빠가 오늘 별안간 블라디미르 훈장과 안드레이 훈장을 한꺼번에 받게 되어, 그 덕분에 수업 시간에 기분이 훨씬 좋아질 것을 상상해 보았고, 자기가 어른이 되면 직접 모든 훈장을 받게 될 것이며, 안드레이보다 더 높은 무엇을 만들어 낼지를 꿈꿔 보았다."], ["Directly any higher order were invented, he would win it.", "더 높은 훈장이 만들어지는 즉시 그는 그것을 따낼 것이다."], ["They would make a higher one still, and he would immediately win that too.", "사람들이 그보다 더 높은 것을 만들어 내면, 그는 곧장 그것도 따낼 것이다."]], [["The time passed in such meditations, and when the teacher came, the lesson about the adverbs of place and time and manner of action was not ready, and the teacher was not only displeased, but hurt.", "이런 명상 속에 시간이 흘렀고, 선생님이 왔을 때 장소와 시간과 동작의 양태를 나타내는 부사에 관한 수업 준비가 되어 있지 않았다. 선생님은 불쾌해했을 뿐 아니라 마음이 상했다."], ["This touched Seryozha.", "세료자는 이것이 마음에 걸렸다."], ["He felt he was not to blame for not having learned the lesson; however much he tried, he was utterly unable to do that.", "그는 수업을 익히지 못한 것이 자기 잘못은 아니라고 느꼈다. 아무리 애써도 그는 도저히 그것을 할 수가 없었다."], ["As long as the teacher was explaining to him, he believed him and seemed to comprehend, but as soon as he was left alone, he was positively unable to recollect and to understand that the short and familiar word “suddenly” is an adverb of manner of action.", "선생님이 그에게 설명해 주는 동안에는 그 말을 믿었고 이해하는 듯도 했지만, 혼자 남겨지기만 하면 그 짧고 익숙한 단어 \"갑자기\"가 동작의 양태를 나타내는 부사라는 것을 도무지 떠올리지도, 이해하지도 못했다."], ["Still he was sorry that he had disappointed the teacher.", "그래도 선생님을 실망시킨 것이 미안했다."]], [["He chose a moment when the teacher was looking in silence at the book.", "그는 선생님이 말없이 책을 들여다보는 순간을 골랐다."]], [["“Mihail Ivanitch, when is your birthday?”", "\"미하일 이바니치, 선생님 생일은 언제예요?\""], ["he asked all, of a sudden.", "그가 느닷없이 물었다."]], [["“You’d much better be thinking about your work.", "\"공부 생각이나 하는 게 훨씬 낫겠다."], ["Birthdays are of no importance to a rational being.", "생일이란 이성을 가진 존재에게는 아무 의미도 없는 거야."], ["It’s a day like any other on which one has to do one’s work.”", "자기 할 일을 해야 하는, 다른 날과 똑같은 하루일 뿐이지.\""]], [["Seryozha looked intently at the teacher, at his scanty beard, at his spectacles, which had slipped down below the ridge on his nose, and fell into so deep a reverie that he heard nothing of what the teacher was explaining to him.", "세료자는 선생님을, 그 듬성듬성한 수염을, 콧대 아래로 흘러내린 안경을 뚫어지게 바라보며 깊은 몽상에 빠져들어, 선생님이 자기에게 설명하는 말을 하나도 듣지 못했다."], ["He knew that the teacher did not think what he said; he felt it from the tone in which it was said.", "그는 선생님이 자기가 한 말을 진심으로 생각하는 게 아니라는 것을 알았다. 그 말투에서 그것을 느꼈던 것이다."], ["“But why have they all agreed to speak just in the same manner always the dreariest and most useless stuff?", "\"그런데 왜 다들 늘 이렇게 가장 따분하고 가장 쓸데없는 말만 똑같은 식으로 하기로 한 걸까?"], ["Why does he keep me off; why doesn’t he love me?”", "왜 선생님은 나를 멀리하는 걸까, 왜 나를 사랑하지 않는 걸까?\""], ["he asked himself mournfully, and could not think of an answer.", "그는 서글프게 자문해 보았지만 답을 찾을 수 없었다."]], [["# Chapter 27", "# Chapter 27"]], [["After the lesson with the grammar teacher came his father’s lesson.", "문법 선생과의 수업이 끝나자 아버지와의 수업이 이어졌다."], ["While waiting for his father, Seryozha sat at the table playing with a penknife, and fell to dreaming.", "아버지를 기다리는 동안 세료자는 책상 앞에 앉아 주머니칼을 만지작거리며 공상에 잠겨 들었다."], ["Among Seryozha’s favorite occupations was searching for his mother during his walks.", "세료자가 좋아하는 일 가운데 하나는 산책할 때 어머니를 찾는 것이었다."], ["He did not believe in death generally, and in her death in particular, in spite of what Lidia Ivanovna had told him and his father had confirmed, and it was just because of that, and after he had been told she was dead, that he had begun looking for her when out for a walk.", "그는 죽음이라는 것을 도무지 믿지 않았고, 특히 어머니의 죽음은 더더욱 믿지 않았는데, 리디아 이바노브나가 그렇게 말해 주었고 아버지도 그것을 확인해 주었음에도 그랬다. 그리고 바로 그 때문에, 어머니가 죽었다는 말을 들은 이후로 그는 산책을 나갈 때면 어머니를 찾기 시작한 것이었다."], ["Every woman of full, graceful figure with dark hair was his mother.", "풍만하고 우아한 몸매에 검은 머리를 한 여자라면 누구나 그에게는 어머니였다."], ["At the sight of such a woman such a feeling of tenderness was stirred within him that his breath failed him, and tears came into his eyes.", "그런 여자를 볼 때마다 그의 마음속에는 어찌나 다정한 감정이 일었던지 숨이 막혀 왔고 눈에는 눈물이 고였다."], ["And he was on the tiptoe of expectation that she would come up to him, would lift her veil.", "그리고 그는 그 여자가 자기에게 다가와 베일을 들어 올리리라는 기대로 까치발을 들었다."], ["All her face would be visible, she would smile, she would hug him, he would sniff her fragrance, feel the softness of her arms, and cry with happiness, just as he had one evening lain on her lap while she tickled him, and he laughed and bit her white, ring-covered fingers.", "그러면 얼굴 전체가 드러나고, 어머니가 미소를 짓고, 그를 끌어안을 것이며, 그는 어머니의 향기를 들이마시고 두 팔의 부드러움을 느끼며 행복에 겨워 울 터였다. 언젠가 저녁에 그가 어머니 무릎에 누워 있고 어머니가 그를 간질여서, 그가 웃으며 반지 낀 어머니의 하얀 손가락을 깨물었던 그날처럼 말이다."], ["Later, when he accidentally learned from his old nurse that his mother was not dead, and his father and Lidia Ivanovna had explained to him that she was dead to him because she was wicked (which he could not possibly believe, because he loved her), he went on seeking her and expecting her in the same way.", "그 후 그는 늙은 유모에게서 우연히 어머니가 죽지 않았다는 사실을 알게 되었고, 아버지와 리디아 이바노브나는 그에게 어머니는 사악한 사람이기에 그에게는 죽은 사람이나 마찬가지라고 설명해 주었지만(그는 어머니를 사랑했기에 도저히 그 말을 믿을 수 없었다), 그래도 그는 여전히 같은 방식으로 어머니를 찾고 기다렸다."], ["That day in the public gardens there had been a lady in a lilac veil, whom he had watched with a throbbing heart, believing it to be she as she came towards them along the path.", "그날 공원에는 라일락빛 베일을 쓴 한 부인이 있었는데, 그 부인이 오솔길을 따라 자기들 쪽으로 다가오자 그는 두근거리는 가슴으로 그녀가 바로 어머니라고 믿으며 지켜보았다."], ["The lady had not come up to them, but had disappeared somewhere.", "그 부인은 그들에게 다가오지 않고 어디론가 사라져 버렸다."], ["That day, more intensely than ever, Seryozha felt a rush of love for her, and now, waiting for his father, he forgot everything, and cut all round the edge of the table with his penknife, staring straight before him with sparkling eyes and dreaming of her.", "그날 세료자는 그 어느 때보다도 강하게 어머니를 향한 사랑이 솟구치는 것을 느꼈고, 지금은 아버지를 기다리며 모든 것을 잊은 채 주머니칼로 책상 가장자리를 빙 둘러 깎아 내면서, 반짝이는 눈으로 정면을 똑바로 응시하며 어머니를 꿈꾸고 있었다."]], [["“Here is your papa!”", "\"아버님께서 오셨다!\""], ["said Vassily Lukitch, rousing him.", "바실리 루키치가 그를 일깨우며 말했다."]], [["Seryozha jumped up and went up to his father, and kissing his hand, looked at him intently, trying to discover signs of his joy at receiving the Alexander Nevsky.", "세료자는 벌떡 일어나 아버지에게 다가가 손에 입을 맞추고는, 알렉산드르 네프스키 훈장을 받은 기쁨의 기색을 찾아내려고 아버지를 뚫어지게 바라보았다."]], [["“Did you have a nice walk?”", "\"산책은 즐거웠느냐?\""], ["said Alexey Alexandrovitch, sitting down in his easy chair, pulling the volume of the Old Testament to him and opening it.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 안락의자에 앉아 구약성서 한 권을 끌어당겨 펼치며 물었다."], ["Although Alexey Alexandrovitch had more than once told Seryozha that every Christian ought to know Scripture history thoroughly, he often referred to the Bible himself during the lesson, and Seryozha observed this.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 모든 그리스도교인은 성서의 역사를 철저히 알아야 한다고 세료자에게 여러 번 일러 왔으면서도, 정작 수업 중에 자신이 종종 성서를 들춰 보곤 했고, 세료자는 이를 눈여겨보았다."]], [["“Yes, it was very nice indeed, papa,”", "\"네, 정말 즐거웠어요, 아버지.\""], ["said Seryozha, sitting sideways on his chair and rocking it, which was forbidden.", "세료자가 의자에 비스듬히 앉아 의자를 흔들며 대답했는데, 그것은 금지된 행동이었다."], ["“I saw Nadinka”", "\"나딘카를 봤어요.\""], ["(Nadinka was a niece of Lidia Ivanovna’s who was being brought up in her house). “She told me you’d been given a new star.", "(나딘카는 리디아 이바노브나의 조카로 그녀의 집에서 자라고 있었다.) \"나딘카가 그러는데 아버지가 새 훈장을 받으셨대요."], ["Are you glad, papa?”", "기쁘세요, 아버지?\""]], [["“First of all, don’t rock your chair, please,”", "\"우선 의자를 흔들지 말거라.\""], ["said Alexey Alexandrovitch.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 말했다."], ["“And secondly, it’s not the reward that’s precious, but the work itself.", "\"그리고 둘째로, 소중한 것은 보상이 아니라 일 자체란다."], ["And I could have wished you understood that.", "네가 그것을 이해해 주었으면 한다."], ["If you now are going to work, to study in order to win a reward, then the work will seem hard to you; but when you work”", "만약 네가 지금 보상을 얻기 위해 일하고 공부한다면 일이 너에게 힘들게 느껴질 게다."], ["(Alexey Alexandrovitch, as he spoke, thought of how he had been sustained by a sense of duty through the wearisome labor of the morning, consisting of signing one hundred and eighty papers), “loving your work, you will find your reward in it.”", "하지만 일을 사랑하며 일한다면\" (알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 이렇게 말하면서, 백여든 장의 서류에 서명하는 오전의 따분한 노동을 의무감으로 버텨 냈던 일을 떠올렸다) \"그 일 속에서 보상을 찾게 될 게다.\""]], [["Seryozha’s eyes, that had been shining with gaiety and tenderness, grew dull and dropped before his father’s gaze.", "쾌활함과 다정함으로 빛나던 세료자의 눈이 흐릿해지더니 아버지의 시선 앞에서 떨구어졌다."], ["This was the same long-familiar tone his father always took with him, and Seryozha had learned by now to fall in with it.", "이것은 아버지가 늘 그에게 쓰던, 오래전부터 익숙한 바로 그 어조였고, 세료자는 이제 거기에 장단을 맞추는 법을 터득해 두고 있었다."], ["His father always talked to him—so Seryozha felt—as though he were addressing some boy of his own imagination, one of those boys that exist in books, utterly unlike himself.", "아버지는 늘 그에게—세료자는 그렇게 느꼈다—마치 자기가 상상해 낸 어떤 소년, 책 속에나 존재하는, 자기와는 전혀 다른 그런 소년들 가운데 하나에게 말을 거는 듯이 이야기했다."], ["And Seryozha always tried with his father to act being the story-book boy.", "그리고 세료자는 아버지 앞에서는 늘 그 이야기책 속 소년인 척 연기하려고 애썼다."]], [["“You understand that, I hope?”", "\"이해하겠지, 그렇지?\""], ["said his father.", "아버지가 말했다."]], [["“Yes, papa,”", "\"네, 아버지.\""], ["answered Seryozha, acting the part of the imaginary boy.", "세료자는 상상 속 소년의 역할을 연기하며 대답했다."]], [["The lesson consisted of learning by heart several verses out of the Gospel and the repetition of the beginning of the Old Testament.", "수업은 복음서에서 몇 구절을 외우고 구약성서의 첫머리를 암송하는 것이었다."], ["The verses from the Gospel Seryozha knew fairly well, but at the moment when he was saying them he became so absorbed in watching the sharply protruding, bony knobbiness of his father’s forehead, that he lost the thread, and he transposed the end of one verse and the beginning of another.", "복음서 구절은 세료자도 제법 잘 알고 있었지만, 막 그것을 외우려는 순간 아버지의 이마에 날카롭게 튀어나온 뼈마디의 울퉁불퉁함을 바라보는 데 그만 정신이 팔려 버려서 실마리를 놓치고 말았고, 한 구절의 끝과 다른 구절의 첫머리를 뒤바꿔 버렸다."], ["So it was evident to Alexey Alexandrovitch that he did not understand what he was saying, and that irritated him.", "그래서 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에게는 아들이 자기가 외우는 말의 뜻을 이해하지 못하고 있는 것이 분명해 보였고, 그것이 그를 짜증 나게 했다."]], [["He frowned, and began explaining what Seryozha had heard many times before and never could remember, because he understood it too well, just as that “suddenly”", "그는 미간을 찌푸리고는, 세료자가 이미 여러 번 들었으면서도 끝내 외우지 못하는 것을 설명하기 시작했다. 너무도 잘 이해하고 있었기 때문에 외우지 못한 것이었으니, 마치 그 \"갑자기\"가 행동의 양태를 나타내는 부사라는 것을 외우지 못하는 것과 같았다."], ["is an adverb of manner of action. Seryozha looked with scared eyes at his father, and could think of nothing but whether his father would make him repeat what he had said, as he sometimes did.", "세료자는 겁에 질린 눈으로 아버지를 바라보면서, 아버지가 가끔 그러듯 자기가 한 말을 다시 외워 보라고 시키지는 않을까 하는 생각밖에 할 수 없었다."], ["And this thought so alarmed Seryozha that he now understood nothing.", "그리고 그 생각이 세료자를 얼마나 불안하게 했던지 이제 그는 아무것도 이해하지 못했다."], ["But his father did not make him repeat it, and passed on to the lesson out of the Old Testament.", "하지만 아버지는 그에게 다시 외워 보라고 시키지 않고 구약성서 부분으로 넘어갔다."], ["Seryozha recounted the events themselves well enough, but when he had to answer questions as to what certain events prefigured, he knew nothing, though he had already been punished over this lesson.", "세료자는 사건들 자체는 곧잘 늘어놓았지만, 어떤 사건이 무엇을 예표하는가 하는 질문에 답해야 할 때면 아무것도 알지 못했다. 이미 이 대목 때문에 벌을 받은 적이 있었는데도 그랬다."], ["The passage at which he was utterly unable to say anything, and began fidgeting and cutting the table and swinging his chair, was where he had to repeat the patriarchs before the Flood.", "그가 도무지 한마디도 하지 못하고 안절부절못하며 책상을 깎고 의자를 흔들기 시작한 대목은 대홍수 이전의 족장들을 외워야 하는 부분이었다."], ["He did not know one of them, except Enoch, who had been taken up alive to heaven.", "그는 산 채로 하늘로 들어 올려진 에녹 외에는 한 사람도 알지 못했다."], ["Last time he had remembered their names, but now he had forgotten them utterly, chiefly because Enoch was the personage he liked best in the whole of the Old Testament, and Enoch’s translation to heaven was connected in his mind with a whole long train of thought, in which he became absorbed now while he gazed with fascinated eyes at his father’s watch-chain and a half-unbuttoned button on his waistcoat.", "지난번에는 그 이름들을 기억했지만 이제는 완전히 잊어버렸는데, 무엇보다도 에녹이 구약성서 전체에서 그가 가장 좋아하는 인물이었고, 에녹이 하늘로 옮겨진 일이 그의 머릿속에서 길게 이어지는 일련의 생각과 얽혀 있었기 때문이었다. 그는 아버지의 회중시계 줄과 조끼의 반쯤 풀린 단추 하나를 매혹된 눈으로 바라보면서 그 생각에 빠져들었다."]], [["In death, of which they talked to him so often, Seryozha disbelieved entirely.", "사람들이 그에게 그토록 자주 이야기하는 죽음이라는 것을 세료자는 전혀 믿지 않았다."], ["He did not believe that those he loved could die, above all that he himself would die.", "그는 자기가 사랑하는 사람들이 죽을 수 있다는 것을, 무엇보다도 자기 자신이 죽으리라는 것을 믿지 않았다."], ["That was to him something utterly inconceivable and impossible.", "그것은 그에게 도무지 상상할 수도 있을 법하지도 않은 일이었다."], ["But he had been told that all men die; he had asked people, indeed, whom he trusted, and they too, had confirmed it; his old nurse, too, said the same, though reluctantly.", "하지만 그는 모든 사람은 죽는다는 말을 들었고, 실제로 자기가 믿는 사람들에게 물어보기도 했는데, 그들 역시 그것을 확인해 주었으며, 늙은 유모도 마지못해서이긴 했지만 같은 말을 했다."], ["But Enoch had not died, and so it followed that everyone did not die.", "그렇지만 에녹은 죽지 않았으니, 따라서 모든 사람이 죽는 것은 아니라는 결론이 나오는 것이다."], ["“And why cannot anyone else so serve God and be taken alive to heaven?”", "\"그렇다면 어째서 다른 사람은 그렇게 하느님을 섬기고 산 채로 하늘로 들어 올려질 수 없는 거지?\""], ["thought Seryozha.", "세료자는 생각했다."], ["Bad people, that is those Seryozha did not like, they might die, but the good might all be like Enoch.", "나쁜 사람들, 그러니까 세료자가 좋아하지 않는 사람들은 죽을지도 모르지만, 착한 사람들은 모두 에녹처럼 될 수 있을 터였다."]], [["“Well, what are the names of the patriarchs?”", "\"자, 족장들의 이름이 무엇이냐?\""]], [["“Enoch, Enos—”", "\"에녹, 에노스……\""]], [["“But you have said that already.", "\"그건 벌써 말하지 않았느냐."], ["This is bad, Seryozha, very bad.", "이건 나쁘구나, 세료자, 아주 나쁜 일이야."], ["If you don’t try to learn what is more necessary than anything for a Christian,”", "그리스도교인에게 무엇보다 가장 필요한 것을 배우려 들지 않는다면,\""], ["said his father, getting up, “whatever can interest you?", "아버지가 일어서며 말했다. \"도대체 무엇에 흥미를 느낄 수 있겠느냐?"], ["I am displeased with you, and Piotr Ignatitch”", "나는 너에게 실망했고, 표트르 이그나티치도\""], ["(this was the most important of his teachers) “is displeased with you....", "(이 사람은 그의 교사들 중 가장 중요한 사람이었다) \"너에게 실망했단다……"], ["I shall have to punish you.”", "너에게 벌을 주어야겠다.\""]], [["His father and his teacher were both displeased with Seryozha, and he certainly did learn his lessons very badly.", "아버지와 교사는 둘 다 세료자에게 실망했고, 그가 수업을 정말 형편없이 배운 것도 사실이었다."], ["But still it could not be said he was a stupid boy.", "하지만 그래도 그가 멍청한 아이라고는 할 수 없었다."], ["On the contrary, he was far cleverer than the boys his teacher held up as examples to Seryozha.", "오히려 그는 교사가 그에게 모범으로 내세우는 소년들보다 훨씬 영리했다."], ["In his father’s opinion, he did not want to learn what he was taught.", "아버지의 생각으로는, 그가 가르치는 것을 배우려 들지 않는 것이었다."], ["In reality he could not learn that.", "실은 그것을 배울 수가 없었던 것이다."], ["He could not, because the claims of his own soul were more binding on him than those claims his father and his teacher made upon him.", "그가 배울 수 없었던 까닭은, 자기 영혼이 요구하는 것이 아버지와 교사가 그에게 들이대는 요구보다 그를 더 강하게 옭아맸기 때문이다."], ["Those claims were in opposition, and he was in direct conflict with his education.", "그 요구들은 서로 대립했고, 그는 자기 교육과 정면으로 충돌하고 있었다."], ["He was nine years old; he was a child; but he knew his own soul, it was precious to him, he guarded it as the eyelid guards the eye, and without the key of love he let no one into his soul.", "그는 아홉 살이었고, 아이였지만, 자기 영혼을 알고 있었고, 그 영혼을 소중히 여겼으며, 눈꺼풀이 눈을 지키듯 그것을 지켰고, 사랑이라는 열쇠가 없이는 누구도 자기 영혼 안으로 들이지 않았다."], ["His teachers complained that he would not learn, while his soul was brimming over with thirst for knowledge.", "교사들은 그가 배우려 들지 않는다고 불평했지만, 정작 그의 영혼은 앎에 대한 갈증으로 흘러넘치고 있었다."], ["And he learned from Kapitonitch, from his nurse, from Nadinka, from Vassily Lukitch, but not from his teachers.", "그리고 그는 카피토니치에게서, 유모에게서, 나딘카에게서, 바실리 루키치에게서 배웠지만, 교사들에게서는 배우지 않았다."], ["The spring his father and his teachers reckoned upon to turn their mill-wheels had long dried up at the source, but its waters did their work in another channel.", "아버지와 교사들이 자기네 물레방아 바퀴를 돌리려고 기대했던 샘물은 진작에 그 원천에서 말라 버렸지만, 그 물은 다른 물길에서 제 일을 해내고 있었다."]], [["His father punished Seryozha by not letting him go to see Nadinka, Lidia Ivanovna’s niece; but this punishment turned out happily for Seryozha.", "아버지는 세료자에게 리디아 이바노브나의 조카 나딘카를 보러 가지 못하게 하는 벌을 내렸다. 하지만 이 벌은 세료자에게는 다행스러운 결과가 되었다."], ["Vassily Lukitch was in a good humor, and showed him how to make windmills.", "바실리 루키치가 기분이 좋아서, 그에게 풍차 만드는 법을 가르쳐 주었던 것이다."], ["The whole evening passed over this work and in dreaming how to make a windmill on which he could turn himself—clutching at the sails or tying himself on and whirling round.", "저녁 내내 이 일에 매달리고, 어떻게 하면 자기가 직접 올라타 돌 수 있는 풍차를—날개를 붙잡거나 몸을 묶고서 빙글빙글 도는 풍차를—만들 수 있을까 궁리하며 시간을 보냈다."], ["Of his mother Seryozha did not think all the evening, but when he had gone to bed, he suddenly remembered her, and prayed in his own words that his mother tomorrow for his birthday might leave off hiding herself and come to him.", "세료자는 저녁 내내 어머니를 생각하지 않았지만, 잠자리에 들고 나자 문득 어머니가 떠올라, 자기 나름의 말로, 내일 자기 생일에 어머니가 더 이상 모습을 숨기지 말고 자기에게 와 주기를 기도했다."]], [["“Vassily Lukitch, do you know what I prayed for tonight extra besides the regular things?”", "\"바실리 루키치, 제가 오늘 밤에 늘 하던 기도 말고 따로 또 무엇을 기도했는지 아세요?\""]], [["“That you might learn your lessons better?”", "\"수업을 더 잘 배우게 해 달라고 했느냐?\""]], [["“No.” “Toys?”", "\"아니에요.\""]], [["“No.", "\"장난감?\""], ["You’ll never guess.", "\"아니에요."], ["A splendid thing; but it’s a secret!", "절대 못 맞히실 거예요. 아주 멋진 거예요."], ["When it comes to pass I’ll tell you.", "그렇지만 그건 비밀이에요! 그게 이루어지면 말씀드릴게요."], ["Can’t you guess!”", "못 맞히시겠죠!\""]], [["“No, I can’t guess.", "\"그래, 못 맞히겠구나."], ["You tell me,”", "네가 말해 보렴.\""], ["said Vassily Lukitch with a smile, which was rare with him.", "바실리 루키치가, 그에게는 드문 일인 미소를 띠며 말했다."], ["“Come, lie down, I’m putting out the candle.”", "\"자, 누우렴, 촛불을 끌 테니.\""]], [["“Without the candle I can see better what I see and what I prayed for.", "\"촛불이 없으면 제가 보는 것과 제가 기도한 것이 더 잘 보인단 말이에요."], ["There!", "어머나!"], ["I was almost telling the secret!”", "하마터면 비밀을 말할 뻔했네!\""], ["said Seryozha, laughing gaily.", "세료자가 즐겁게 웃으며 말했다."]], [["When the candle was taken away, Seryozha heard and felt his mother.", "촛불이 치워지자 세료자는 어머니를 듣고 느꼈다."], ["She stood over him, and with loving eyes caressed him.", "어머니가 그의 위에 서서 사랑이 담긴 눈으로 그를 어루만졌다."], ["But then came windmills, a knife, everything began to be mixed up, and he fell asleep.", "하지만 이내 풍차와 칼, 모든 것이 뒤섞이기 시작했고, 그는 잠이 들었다."]], [["# Chapter 28", "# Chapter 28"]], [["On arriving in Petersburg, Vronsky and Anna stayed at one of the best hotels; Vronsky apart in a lower story, Anna above with her child, its nurse, and her maid, in a large suite of four rooms.", "페테르부르크에 도착한 브론스키와 안나는 최고급 호텔 가운데 한 곳에 묵었다. 브론스키는 아래층에 따로 들었고, 안나는 위층에 아이와 유모, 하녀와 함께 방 네 개짜리 큰 스위트룸을 잡았다."]], [["On the day of his arrival Vronsky went to his brother’s.", "도착한 날 브론스키는 형의 집으로 갔다."], ["There he found his mother, who had come from Moscow on business.", "그곳에서 그는 볼일이 있어 모스크바에서 온 어머니를 만났다."], ["His mother and sister-in-law greeted him as usual: they asked him about his stay abroad, and talked of their common acquaintances, but did not let drop a single word in allusion to his connection with Anna.", "어머니와 형수는 여느 때처럼 그를 맞이했다. 그들은 그에게 외국 체류에 대해 물었고, 서로 아는 사람들에 관한 이야기를 나누었지만, 안나와의 관계를 넌지시라도 비추는 말은 단 한마디도 흘리지 않았다."], ["His brother came the next morning to see Vronsky, and of his own accord asked him about her, and Alexey Vronsky told him directly that he looked upon his connection with Madame Karenina as marriage; that he hoped to arrange a divorce, and then to marry her, and until then he considered her as much a wife as any other wife, and he begged him to tell their mother and his wife so.", "형은 이튿날 아침 브론스키를 보러 왔고, 먼저 나서서 그녀에 대해 물었다. 그러자 알렉세이 브론스키는 솔직하게, 자기는 카레니나 부인과의 관계를 결혼으로 여기고 있으며, 이혼을 성사시킨 뒤 그녀와 결혼할 작정이고, 그때까지는 그녀를 여느 아내와 다름없는 아내로 여긴다고, 그러니 어머니와 형수에게도 그렇게 전해 달라고 부탁했다."]], [["“If the world disapproves, I don’t care,”", "\"세상이 못마땅하게 여긴다 해도 나는 상관없습니다.\""], ["said Vronsky; “but if my relations want to be on terms of relationship with me, they will have to be on the same terms with my wife.”", "브론스키가 말했다. \"하지만 내 친척들이 나와 친척으로서 관계를 이어 가길 바란다면, 내 아내와도 똑같은 관계를 맺어야 할 겁니다.\""]], [["The elder brother, who had always a respect for his younger brother’s judgment, could not well tell whether he was right or not till the world had decided the question; for his part he had nothing against it, and with Alexey he went up to see Anna.", "언제나 동생의 판단을 존중해 온 형은, 세상이 이 문제를 결정 내리기 전에는 동생이 옳은지 그른지 딱히 말할 수가 없었다. 자기로서는 반대할 것이 없었으므로, 그는 알렉세이와 함께 안나를 만나러 위층으로 올라갔다."]], [["Before his brother, as before everyone, Vronsky addressed Anna with a certain formality, treating her as he might a very intimate friend, but it was understood that his brother knew their real relations, and they talked about Anna’s going to Vronsky’s estate.", "형 앞에서도, 다른 누구 앞에서나 마찬가지로, 브론스키는 안나를 어떤 격식을 차려 대했고, 아주 가까운 친구를 대하듯 행동했다. 하지만 형이 두 사람의 진짜 관계를 알고 있다는 것은 이해된 일이었고, 그들은 안나가 브론스키의 영지로 가는 것에 대해 이야기를 나누었다."]], [["In spite of all his social experience Vronsky was, in consequence of the new position in which he was placed, laboring under a strange misapprehension.", "온갖 사교계 경험에도 불구하고, 브론스키는 자기가 놓인 새로운 처지 탓에 이상한 착각에 빠져 있었다. 사교계가 자기와 안나에게 닫혀 버렸다는 것을 그가 당연히 이해했으리라고 누구나 생각했을 것이다."], ["One would have thought he must have understood that society was closed for him and Anna; but now some vague ideas had sprung up in his brain that this was only the case in old-fashioned days, and that now with the rapidity of modern progress (he had unconsciously become by now a partisan of every sort of progress) the views of society had changed, and that the question whether they would be received in society was not a foregone conclusion.", "그러나 지금 그의 머릿속에는 어떤 막연한 생각들이 솟아났다. 그런 일은 케케묵은 옛 시절에나 그랬을 뿐, 이제는 근대적 진보가 빠르게 이루어지면서(그는 어느새 무의식적으로 온갖 진보의 신봉자가 되어 있었다) 사교계의 견해도 바뀌었으며, 자기들이 사교계에 받아들여질지 어떨지는 이미 정해진 결론이 아니라는 것이었다."], ["“Of course,”", "'물론' 그는 생각했다."], ["he thought, “she would not be received at court, but intimate friends can and must look at it in the proper light.”", "'그녀가 궁정에 받아들여지지는 않겠지. 하지만 가까운 친구들이라면 이 일을 제대로 된 시각으로 봐 줄 수 있고, 또 봐 줘야 마땅하지.'"], ["One may sit for several hours at a stretch with one’s legs crossed in the same position, if one knows that there’s nothing to prevent one’s changing one’s position; but if a man knows that he must remain sitting so with crossed legs, then cramps come on, the legs begin to twitch and to strain towards the spot to which one would like to draw them.", "자세를 바꾸는 데 아무 거리낌이 없다는 것을 알고 있으면, 사람은 다리를 꼰 똑같은 자세로 몇 시간이고 내리 앉아 있을 수 있다. 그러나 다리를 꼰 채 그대로 앉아 있어야만 한다는 것을 알게 되면, 쥐가 나기 시작하고, 다리가 씰룩거리며 끌어당기고 싶은 쪽으로 잡아당겨지기 시작한다."], ["This was what Vronsky was experiencing in regard to the world.", "브론스키가 세상에 대해 겪고 있던 것이 바로 이것이었다."], ["Though at the bottom of his heart he knew that the world was shut on them, he put it to the test whether the world had not changed by now and would not receive them.", "마음 깊은 곳에서는 세상이 자기들에게 닫혀 버렸음을 알면서도, 그는 이제 세상이 바뀌어 자기들을 받아들여 주지 않을까 시험해 보았다."], ["But he very quickly perceived that though the world was open for him personally, it was closed for Anna.", "그러나 그는 곧, 세상이 자기 개인에게는 열려 있지만 안나에게는 닫혀 있다는 것을 깨달았다."], ["Just as in the game of cat and mouse, the hands raised for him were dropped to bar the way for Anna.", "고양이와 쥐 놀이에서처럼, 그를 위해 들어 올려졌던 손들이 안나의 길을 막으려고 내려와 버린 것이다."]], [["One of the first ladies of Petersburg society whom Vronsky saw was his cousin Betsy.", "브론스키가 만난 페테르부르크 사교계의 일급 부인들 가운데 한 사람이 그의 사촌 누이 벳시였다."]], [["“At last!”", "\"드디어 왔군요!\""], ["she greeted him joyfully.", "그녀가 반갑게 그를 맞이했다."], ["“And Anna?", "\"안나는요?"], ["How glad I am!", "어찌나 기쁜지!"], ["Where are you stopping?", "어디 묵고 계세요?"], ["I can fancy after your delightful travels you must find our poor Petersburg horrid.", "그 즐거운 여행을 하고 나니 우리 가엾은 페테르부르크가 끔찍하게 느껴질 게 뻔하죠."], ["I can fancy your honeymoon in Rome.", "로마에서 신혼여행을 보냈으리라 짐작이 가는걸요."], ["How about the divorce?", "이혼은 어떻게 됐어요?"], ["Is that all over?”", "다 끝난 건가요?\""]], [["Vronsky noticed that Betsy’s enthusiasm waned when she learned that no divorce had as yet taken place.", "브론스키는 아직 이혼이 이루어지지 않았다는 것을 알게 되자 벳시의 열기가 식어 버리는 것을 알아챘다."]], [["“People will throw stones at me, I know,”", "\"사람들이 내게 돌을 던질 테죠, 알아요.\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["she said, “but I shall come and see Anna; yes, I shall certainly come.", "\"하지만 나는 안나를 보러 갈 거예요. 그럼요, 꼭 갈 거예요."], ["You won’t be here long, I suppose?”", "여기 오래 머무르시지는 않겠지요?\""]], [["And she did certainly come to see Anna the same day, but her tone was not at all the same as in former days.", "그리고 그녀는 정말로 그날 안나를 보러 왔지만, 그 말투는 예전과는 전혀 같지 않았다."], ["She unmistakably prided herself on her courage, and wished Anna to appreciate the fidelity of her friendship.", "그녀는 분명 자기 용기를 자랑스러워했고, 안나가 자기 우정의 진실함을 알아주기를 바랐다."], ["She only stayed ten minutes, talking of society gossip, and on leaving she said:", "그녀는 사교계의 잡담을 늘어놓으며 겨우 십 분쯤 머물렀고, 떠나면서 이렇게 말했다."]], [["“You’ve never told me when the divorce is to be?", "\"이혼이 언제 끝나는지는 끝내 말씀을 안 해 주셨네요."], ["Supposing I’m ready to fling my cap over the mill, other starchy people will give you the cold shoulder until you’re married.", "설령 내가 체면 따위 내던질 각오가 되어 있다 해도, 다른 깐깐한 사람들은 당신들이 결혼할 때까지 쌀쌀맞게 대할 거예요."], ["And that’s so simple nowadays.", "요즘은 그게 아주 간단한 일인데 말이죠."], ["Ça se fait.", "Ça se fait."], ["So you’re going on Friday?", "그래서 금요일에 떠나시나요?"], ["Sorry we shan’t see each other again.”", "다시 못 보게 되어 아쉽네요.\""]], [["From Betsy’s tone Vronsky might have grasped what he had to expect from the world; but he made another effort in his own family.", "* Ça se fait: 프랑스어로 '그건 흔히 있는 일이다, 그렇게들 한다'. 벳시의 말투에서 브론스키는 세상으로부터 무엇을 기대해야 할지 충분히 헤아릴 수 있었다."], ["His mother he did not reckon upon.", "하지만 그는 자기 가족 안에서 한 번 더 시도해 보았다."], ["He knew that his mother, who had been so enthusiastic over Anna at their first acquaintance, would have no mercy on her now for having ruined her son’s career.", "어머니는 기대하지 않았다. 처음 알게 되었을 때만 해도 안나에게 그토록 열광했던 어머니가, 이제는 아들의 앞길을 망쳐 놓았다는 이유로 그녀에게 조금도 자비를 베풀지 않으리라는 것을 그는 알고 있었다."], ["But he had more hope of Varya, his brother’s wife.", "그러나 형수 바랴에게는 더 큰 희망을 걸었다."], ["He fancied she would not throw stones, and would go simply and directly to see Anna, and would receive her in her own house.", "그녀라면 돌을 던지지 않을 것이고, 소박하고 거리낌 없이 안나를 보러 가서 자기 집으로 그녀를 맞아들이리라고 그는 짐작했다."]], [["The day after his arrival Vronsky went to her, and finding her alone, expressed his wishes directly.", "도착한 다음 날 브론스키는 그녀를 찾아갔고, 마침 혼자 있는 그녀를 보고 자기 바람을 솔직하게 털어놓았다."]], [["“You know, Alexey,”", "\"알렉세이, 너도 알다시피,\" 그의 말을 다 듣고 나서 그녀가 말했다."], ["she said after hearing him, “how fond I am of you, and how ready I am to do anything for you; but I have not spoken, because I knew I could be of no use to you and to Anna Arkadyevna,”", "\"내가 너를 얼마나 아끼는지, 또 너를 위해서라면 무엇이든 할 마음이 얼마나 큰지 말이야. 하지만 내가 입을 다물고 있었던 건, 너와 안나 아르카지예브나에게 아무 도움도 될 수 없다는 걸 알았기 때문이야.\""], ["she said, articulating the name “Anna Arkadyevna”", "그녀는 '안나 아르카지예브나'라는 이름을 유난히 조심스럽게 또박또박 발음했다."], ["with particular care.", "\"제발 내가 그녀를 비난한다고는 생각하지 말아 줘."], ["“Don’t suppose, please, that I judge her.", "절대 아니야. 어쩌면 내가 그녀 처지였더라도 똑같이 했을지 몰라."], ["Never; perhaps in her place I should have done the same.", "나는 그런 데 끼어들지 않고, 끼어들 수도 없어.\" 그녀는 그의 침울한 얼굴을 겁먹은 눈으로 흘끗 보며 말했다."], ["I don’t and can’t enter into that,”", "\"하지만 모든 일은 그 본래 이름대로 불러야 하는 법이지."], ["she said, glancing timidly at his gloomy face. “But one must call things by their names.", "너는 내가 가서 그녀를 만나고, 이 집으로 그녀를 초대하고, 그녀를 사교계에서 다시 일으켜 세워 주길 바라는 거야."], ["You want me to go and see her, to ask her here, and to rehabilitate her in society; but do understand that I cannot do so.", "하지만 내가 그렇게 할 수 없다는 걸 좀 알아줘. 나는 다 커 가는 딸들이 있고, 남편을 위해서라도 사교계에서 살아가야 해."], ["I have daughters growing up, and I must live in the world for my husband’s sake.", "그래, 안나 아르카지예브나를 보러 갈 마음은 있어. 그녀도 이해할 거야."], ["Well, I’m ready to come and see Anna Arkadyevna: she will understand that I can’t ask her here, or I should have to do so in such a way that she would not meet people who look at things differently; that would offend her.", "내가 그녀를 이 집으로 초대할 수는 없다는 걸 말이야. 만일 초대한다 해도, 일을 다르게 보는 사람들과 그녀가 마주치지 않도록 해야 할 텐데, 그건 그녀를 모욕하는 셈이 될 거야."], ["I can’t raise her....”", "나는 그녀를 끌어올려 줄 수가 없어……\""]], [["“Oh, I don’t regard her as fallen more than hundreds of women you do receive!”", "\"오, 나는 형수님이 집에 들이는 수백 명의 여자들보다 그녀가 더 타락했다고는 생각하지 않습니다!\""], ["Vronsky interrupted her still more gloomily, and he got up in silence, understanding that his sister-in-law’s decision was not to be shaken.", "브론스키가 한층 더 침울하게 그녀의 말을 끊었다. 그러고는 형수의 결심이 흔들리지 않으리라는 것을 알아채고는 말없이 자리에서 일어났다."]], [["“Alexey!", "\"알렉세이!"], ["don’t be angry with me.", "나한테 화내지 마."], ["Please understand that I’m not to blame,”", "제발 이게 내 잘못이 아니라는 걸 알아줘.\""], ["began Varya, looking at him with a timid smile.", "바랴가 겁먹은 미소를 띠고 그를 바라보며 말을 꺼냈다."]], [["“I’m not angry with you,”", "\"형수님께 화난 게 아닙니다.\""], ["he said still as gloomily; “but I’m sorry in two ways.", "그는 여전히 침울하게 말했다. \"다만 두 가지로 마음이 아플 뿐입니다."], ["I’m sorry, too, that this means breaking up our friendship—if not breaking up, at least weakening it.", "이 일로 우리 우정이 깨지게 되었다는 것도 마음이 아프고요. 깨지지는 않더라도, 적어도 약해지긴 하겠지요."], ["You will understand that for me, too, it cannot be otherwise.”", "나로서도 달리 어쩔 수 없다는 걸 형수님은 이해하실 겁니다.\""]], [["And with that he left her.", "그리고 그 말을 끝으로 그는 그녀의 곁을 떠났다."]], [["Vronsky knew that further efforts were useless, and that he had to spend these few days in Petersburg as though in a strange town, avoiding every sort of relation with his own old circle in order not to be exposed to the annoyances and humiliations which were so intolerable to him.", "브론스키는 더 이상의 노력이 헛되다는 것을, 그리고 이 며칠을 마치 낯선 도시에 와 있는 것처럼 페테르부르크에서 보내야 한다는 것을 알았다. 그는 자기에게 그토록 견디기 힘든 성가신 일과 굴욕에 노출되지 않으려고, 자기가 속했던 옛 무리와의 온갖 관계를 피했다."], ["One of the most unpleasant features of his position in Petersburg was that Alexey Alexandrovitch and his name seemed to meet him everywhere.", "페테르부르크에서 그가 처한 처지의 가장 불쾌한 점 가운데 하나는, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치와 그의 이름이 가는 곳마다 그를 마주치는 것 같다는 것이었다."], ["He could not begin to talk of anything without the conversation turning on Alexey Alexandrovitch; he could not go anywhere without risk of meeting him.", "무슨 이야기를 꺼내려 해도 대화는 어김없이 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치 쪽으로 흘러갔고, 어디를 가든 그와 마주칠 위험이 있었다."], ["So at least it seemed to Vronsky, just as it seems to a man with a sore finger that he is continually, as though on purpose, grazing his sore finger on everything.", "적어도 브론스키에게는 그렇게 느껴졌는데, 그것은 마치 손가락에 상처가 난 사람이 무엇에든, 마치 일부러 그러기라도 하듯, 끊임없이 그 상처 난 손가락을 부딪치는 것처럼 여겨지는 것과 같았다."]], [["Their stay in Petersburg was the more painful to Vronsky that he perceived all the time a sort of new mood that he could not understand in Anna.", "페테르부르크에서의 체류가 브론스키에게 더욱 괴로웠던 것은, 그가 안나에게서 도무지 이해할 수 없는 어떤 새로운 기분 같은 것을 줄곧 느꼈기 때문이다."], ["At one time she would seem in love with him, and then she would become cold, irritable, and impenetrable.", "어느 때는 그녀가 그를 사랑하는 듯 보이다가도, 곧 차갑고 신경질적이며 속을 알 수 없게 변해 버렸다."], ["She was worrying over something, and keeping something back from him, and did not seem to notice the humiliations which poisoned his existence, and for her, with her delicate intuition, must have been still more unbearable.", "그녀는 무언가를 두고 애를 태우며 그에게 무언가를 숨기고 있었고, 그의 삶을 좀먹는 굴욕들을 알아채지 못하는 듯했다. 섬세한 직관을 지닌 그녀에게는 그 굴욕들이 분명 더욱더 견디기 힘들었을 텐데도 말이다."]], [["# Chapter 29", "# Chapter 29"]], [["One of Anna’s objects in coming back to Russia had been to see her son.", "안나가 러시아로 돌아온 목적 가운데 하나는 아들을 보는 것이었다."], ["From the day she left Italy the thought of it had never ceased to agitate her.", "이탈리아를 떠나던 날부터 그 생각이 한순간도 그녀를 흔들어 놓기를 그치지 않았다."], ["And as she got nearer to Petersburg, the delight and importance of this meeting grew ever greater in her imagination.", "그리고 페테르부르크에 가까워질수록, 이 만남의 기쁨과 중대함은 그녀의 상상 속에서 점점 더 커져만 갔다."], ["She did not even put to herself the question how to arrange it.", "그녀는 그것을 어떻게 마련해야 할지조차 스스로에게 물어보지 않았다."], ["It seemed to her natural and simple to see her son when she should be in the same town with him.", "아들과 같은 도시에 있게 되면 아들을 보는 것이 그녀에게는 자연스럽고 단순한 일로 여겨졌다."], ["But on her arrival in Petersburg she was suddenly made distinctly aware of her present position in society, and she grasped the fact that to arrange this meeting was no easy matter.", "하지만 페테르부르크에 도착하자 그녀는 별안간 자기가 지금 사교계에서 처한 위치를 또렷이 깨닫게 되었고, 이 만남을 마련하는 일이 결코 쉬운 문제가 아니라는 사실을 알아챘다."]], [["She had now been two days in Petersburg.", "그녀는 이제 페테르부르크에 온 지 이틀이 되었다."], ["The thought of her son never left her for a single instant, but she had not yet seen him.", "아들 생각이 단 한순간도 그녀를 떠나지 않았건만, 그녀는 아직 아들을 보지 못했다."], ["To go straight to the house, where she might meet Alexey Alexandrovitch, that she felt she had no right to do.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치와 마주칠지도 모를 그 집으로 곧장 가는 것은, 자기에게 그럴 권리가 없다고 그녀는 느꼈다."], ["She might be refused admittance and insulted.", "입장을 거절당하고 모욕을 당할 수도 있었다."], ["To write and so enter into relations with her husband—that it made her miserable to think of doing; she could only be at peace when she did not think of her husband.", "편지를 써서 남편과 관계를 맺는 일은—그것을 생각하는 것만으로도 그녀는 비참해졌다. 그녀는 남편을 생각하지 않을 때에만 마음의 평안을 얻을 수 있었다."], ["To get a glimpse of her son out walking, finding out where and when he went out, was not enough for her; she had so looked forward to this meeting, she had so much she must say to him, she so longed to embrace him, to kiss him.", "아들이 산책 나가는 장소와 시간을 알아내어 그 모습을 잠깐 엿보는 것으로는 그녀에게 충분하지 않았다. 그녀는 이 만남을 그토록 고대해 왔고, 아들에게 해 줄 말이 그토록 많았으며, 아들을 끌어안고 입맞추기를 그토록 갈망했다."], ["Seryozha’s old nurse might be a help to her and show her what to do.", "세료자의 늙은 유모라면 그녀에게 도움을 주고 어떻게 해야 할지 알려 줄 수 있을지도 몰랐다."], ["But the nurse was not now living in Alexey Alexandrovitch’s house.", "하지만 유모는 지금 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 집에서 살고 있지 않았다."], ["In this uncertainty, and in efforts to find the nurse, two days had slipped by.", "이런 막막함 속에서, 유모를 찾으려는 노력 속에서 이틀이 흘러가 버렸다."]], [["Hearing of the close intimacy between Alexey Alexandrovitch and Countess Lidia Ivanovna, Anna decided on the third day to write to her a letter, which cost her great pains, and in which she intentionally said that permission to see her son must depend on her husband’s generosity.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치와 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인 사이가 매우 가깝다는 이야기를 들은 안나는 사흘째 되던 날 그녀에게 편지를 쓰기로 마음먹었고, 그 편지는 그녀에게 큰 고통을 안겨 주었으며, 거기에 그녀는 아들을 볼 수 있는 허락이 남편의 너그러움에 달려 있어야 한다고 일부러 적어 넣었다."], ["She knew that if the letter were shown to her husband, he would keep up his character of magnanimity, and would not refuse her request.", "만약 그 편지를 남편에게 보여 준다면, 남편은 자기의 관대한 인품을 지키려 할 것이고, 그녀의 청을 거절하지 않으리라는 것을 그녀는 알고 있었다."]], [["The commissionaire who took the letter had brought her back the most cruel and unexpected answer, that there was no answer.", "편지를 가져갔던 심부름꾼이 그녀에게 가져온 것은 더없이 잔인하고 뜻밖의 답변, 곧 답이 없다는 것이었다."], ["She had never felt so humiliated as at the moment when, sending for the commissionaire, she heard from him the exact account of how he had waited, and how afterwards he had been told there was no answer.", "그녀는 심부름꾼을 불러, 그가 어떻게 기다렸으며 그 뒤에 답이 없다는 말을 어떻게 들었는지 정확한 경위를 들었던 그 순간만큼 굴욕감을 느낀 적이 없었다."], ["Anna felt humiliated, insulted, but she saw that from her point of view Countess Lidia Ivanovna was right.", "안나는 굴욕과 모욕을 느꼈지만, 자기 입장에서 보더라도 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인이 옳다는 것을 알았다."], ["Her suffering was the more poignant that she had to bear it in solitude.", "그녀의 고통은 그것을 홀로 견뎌야 한다는 데에서 한층 더 사무쳤다."], ["She could not and would not share it with Vronsky.", "그녀는 그것을 브론스키와 나눌 수도 없었고 나누고 싶지도 않았다."], ["She knew that to him, although he was the primary cause of her distress, the question of her seeing her son would seem a matter of very little consequence.", "비록 그가 그녀의 괴로움의 일차적 원인이었음에도, 아들을 보는 문제가 그에게는 별로 대수롭지 않은 일로 여겨지리라는 것을 그녀는 알았다."], ["She knew that he would never be capable of understanding all the depth of her suffering, that for his cool tone at any allusion to it she would begin to hate him.", "그가 자기 고통의 깊이를 결코 온전히 이해하지 못하리라는 것을, 그리고 그것에 관해 무슨 말이라도 나올 때마다 그가 보이는 냉담한 어조 때문에 자기가 그를 미워하기 시작하리라는 것을 그녀는 알고 있었다."], ["And she dreaded that more than anything in the world, and so she hid from him everything that related to her son.", "그리고 그녀는 그것을 세상 무엇보다도 두려워했고, 그래서 아들에 관련된 모든 것을 그에게 숨겼다."], ["Spending the whole day at home she considered ways of seeing her son, and had reached a decision to write to her husband.", "온종일 집에서 지내면서 그녀는 아들을 볼 방법을 궁리했고, 남편에게 편지를 쓰기로 결심하기에 이르렀다."], ["She was just composing this letter when she was handed the letter from Lidia Ivanovna.", "그녀가 막 이 편지를 작성하고 있을 때 리디아 이바노브나에게서 온 편지가 그녀에게 건네졌다."], ["The countess’s silence had subdued and depressed her, but the letter, all that she read between the lines in it, so exasperated her, this malice was so revolting beside her passionate, legitimate tenderness for her son, that she turned against other people and left off blaming herself.", "백작 부인의 침묵은 그녀를 위축시키고 의기소침하게 만들었지만, 그 편지가, 그녀가 그 행간에서 읽어 낸 모든 것이 그녀를 어찌나 격분시켰는지, 아들을 향한 그녀의 열렬하고 정당한 애정 곁에서 이 악의가 어찌나 역겨웠는지, 그녀는 다른 사람들에게 등을 돌리고 자기 자신을 탓하기를 그만두었다."]], [["“This coldness—this pretense of feeling!”", "\"이 냉담함—이 감정의 가식이라니!\""], ["she said to herself.", "그녀는 혼자 중얼거렸다."], ["“They must needs insult me and torture the child, and I am to submit to it!", "\"저들은 굳이 나를 모욕하고 아이를 괴롭혀야겠고, 나는 거기에 굴복해야 한단 말이지!"], ["Not on any consideration!", "무슨 일이 있어도 안 돼!"], ["She is worse than I am.", "저 여자가 나보다 더 못된 인간이야."], ["I don’t lie, anyway.”", "적어도 나는 거짓말은 하지 않잖아.\""], ["And she decided on the spot that next day, Seryozha’s birthday, she would go straight to her husband’s house, bribe or deceive the servants, but at any cost see her son and overturn the hideous deception with which they were encompassing the unhappy child.", "그리고 그녀는 그 자리에서 결심했다. 다음 날, 세료자의 생일에, 곧장 남편의 집으로 가서, 하인들을 매수하든 속이든, 어떤 대가를 치르더라도 아들을 만나, 사람들이 그 불행한 아이를 옭아매고 있는 그 끔찍한 기만을 뒤엎어 버리겠노라고."]], [["She went to a toy shop, bought toys and thought over a plan of action.", "그녀는 장난감 가게로 가서 장난감을 사고 행동 계획을 곰곰이 짰다."], ["She would go early in the morning at eight o’clock, when Alexey Alexandrovitch would be certain not to be up.", "그녀는 아침 일찍 여덟 시에 갈 작정이었다. 그 시각이면 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 틀림없이 일어나지 않았을 테니까."], ["She would have money in her hand to give the hall-porter and the footman, so that they should let her in, and not raising her veil, she would say that she had come from Seryozha’s godfather to congratulate him, and that she had been charged to leave the toys at his bedside.", "그녀는 손에 돈을 쥐고 현관지기와 하인에게 쥐여 주어 자기를 들여보내게 할 것이고, 베일을 올리지 않은 채, 자기는 세료자의 대부가 보내서 축하 인사를 전하러 왔으며, 장난감을 그의 머리맡에 두고 가도록 부탁받았다고 말할 작정이었다."], ["She had prepared everything but the words she should say to her son.", "그녀는 아들에게 해야 할 말만 빼고 모든 것을 준비해 두었다."], ["Often as she had dreamed of it, she could never think of anything.", "그토록 자주 그것을 꿈꿔 왔건만, 그녀는 아무 말도 떠올릴 수가 없었다."]], [["The next day, at eight o’clock in the morning, Anna got out of a hired sledge and rang at the front entrance of her former home.", "다음 날 아침 여덟 시, 안나는 삯썰매에서 내려 옛집의 정문 초인종을 눌렀다."]], [["“Run and see what’s wanted. Some lady,”", "\"가서 무슨 일인지 보고 와."], ["said Kapitonitch, who, not yet dressed, in his overcoat and galoshes, had peeped out of the window and seen a lady in a veil standing close up to the door.", "어떤 부인이야.\" 아직 옷도 다 입지 않고 외투에 덧신을 신은 채 창밖을 내다보다 문에 바짝 붙어 선 베일 쓴 부인을 본 카피토니치가 말했다."], ["His assistant, a lad Anna did not know, had no sooner opened the door to her than she came in, and pulling a three-rouble note out of her muff put it hurriedly into his hand.", "그의 조수, 안나가 모르는 한 소년이 그녀에게 문을 열어 주기가 무섭게 그녀는 안으로 들어와, 토시에서 삼 루블짜리 지폐 한 장을 꺼내 그의 손에 급히 쥐여 주었다."]], [["“Seryozha—Sergey Alexeitch,”", "\"세료자—세르게이 알렉세이치.\""], ["she said, and was going on.", "그녀가 말하고 계속 걸어가려 했다."], ["Scrutinizing the note, the porter’s assistant stopped her at the second glass door.", "지폐를 찬찬히 들여다보던 현관지기 조수가 두 번째 유리문 앞에서 그녀를 멈춰 세웠다."]], [["“Whom do you want?”", "\"누굴 찾으십니까?\""], ["he asked.", "그가 물었다."]], [["She did not hear his words and made no answer.", "그녀는 그의 말을 듣지 못했고 아무 대답도 하지 않았다."]], [["Noticing the embarrassment of the unknown lady, Kapitonitch went out to her, opened the second door for her, and asked her what she was pleased to want.", "낯선 부인의 당혹스러운 기색을 알아챈 카피토니치가 그녀에게 다가가 두 번째 문을 열어 주고는 무슨 용무인지 물었다."]], [["“From Prince Skorodumov for Sergey Alexeitch,”", "\"스코로두모프 공작이 보내서 세르게이 알렉세이치에게 왔어요.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“His honor’s not up yet,”", "\"도련님께서는 아직 안 일어나셨습니다.\""], ["said the porter, looking at her attentively.", "현관지기가 그녀를 유심히 바라보며 말했다."]], [["Anna had not anticipated that the absolutely unchanged hall of the house where she had lived for nine years would so greatly affect her.", "안나는 자기가 구 년을 살았던 이 집의 조금도 변하지 않은 현관이 자기를 이토록 크게 흔들어 놓으리라고는 예상하지 못했다."], ["Memories sweet and painful rose one after another in her heart, and for a moment she forgot what she was here for.", "달콤하면서도 아픈 기억들이 그녀의 가슴속에서 잇따라 솟아올랐고, 한순간 그녀는 자기가 여기에 무엇 때문에 왔는지조차 잊어버렸다."]], [["“Would you kindly wait?”", "\"잠시 기다려 주시겠습니까?\""], ["said Kapitonitch, taking off her fur cloak.", "카피토니치가 그녀의 모피 외투를 벗겨 주며 말했다."]], [["As he took off the cloak, Kapitonitch glanced at her face, recognized her, and made her a low bow in silence.", "외투를 벗겨 주면서 카피토니치는 그녀의 얼굴을 흘끗 보고 그녀를 알아보았으며, 말없이 그녀에게 깊이 허리를 굽혀 인사했다."]], [["“Please walk in, your excellency,”", "\"안으로 드시지요, 마님.\""], ["he said to her.", "그가 그녀에게 말했다."]], [["She tried to say something, but her voice refused to utter any sound; with a guilty and imploring glance at the old man she went with light, swift steps up the stairs.", "그녀는 뭔가 말하려 했지만, 목소리가 어떤 소리도 내기를 거부했다. 죄지은 듯 애원하는 눈길로 노인을 바라보며 그녀는 가볍고 빠른 걸음으로 계단을 올라갔다."], ["Bent double, and his galoshes catching in the steps, Kapitonitch ran after her, trying to overtake her.", "몸을 한껏 굽힌 채, 덧신이 계단에 걸리는 것을 무릅쓰고 카피토니치가 그녀를 따라잡으려 애쓰며 그녀 뒤를 달려 올라갔다."]], [["“The tutor’s there; maybe he’s not dressed.", "\"가정교사가 와 있습니다. 아직 옷을 안 입었을지도 모르고요."], ["I’ll let him know.”", "제가 알려 드리겠습니다.\""]], [["Anna still mounted the familiar staircase, not understanding what the old man was saying.", "안나는 노인이 무슨 말을 하는지 알아듣지도 못한 채 여전히 낯익은 계단을 올라갔다."]], [["“This way, to the left, if you please.", "\"이쪽으로, 왼쪽으로 가시지요."], ["Excuse its not being tidy.", "어수선해서 송구합니다."], ["His honor’s in the old parlor now,”", "도련님께서는 지금 옛날 응접실에 계십니다.\""], ["the hall-porter said, panting.", "현관지기가 숨을 헐떡이며 말했다."], ["“Excuse me, wait a little, your excellency; I’ll just see,”", "\"송구합니다만, 잠시만 기다려 주십시오, 마님. 제가 얼른 보고 오겠습니다.\""], ["he said, and overtaking her, he opened the high door and disappeared behind it.", "그가 말하고는 그녀를 앞질러 가서 높다란 문을 열고 그 뒤로 사라졌다."], ["Anna stood still waiting.", "안나는 가만히 서서 기다렸다."], ["“He’s only just awake,”", "\"이제 막 깨셨습니다.\""], ["said the hall-porter, coming out.", "현관지기가 나오며 말했다."], ["And at the very instant the porter said this, Anna caught the sound of a childish yawn.", "그리고 현관지기가 그렇게 말하는 바로 그 순간, 안나는 어린아이가 하품하는 소리를 들었다."], ["From the sound of this yawn alone she knew her son and seemed to see him living before her eyes.", "그 하품 소리 하나만으로도 그녀는 아들을 알아보았고, 마치 눈앞에 살아 있는 아들을 보는 것 같았다."]], [["“Let me in; go away!”", "\"들여보내 줘요. 어서 비켜요!\""], ["she said, and went in through the high doorway.", "그녀가 말하고는 높다란 문을 통해 안으로 들어갔다."], ["On the right of the door stood a bed, and sitting up in the bed was the boy.", "문 오른편에는 침대가 놓여 있었고, 그 침대에 일어나 앉아 있는 것은 아이였다."], ["His little body bent forward with his nightshirt unbuttoned, he was stretching and still yawning.", "잠옷 단추를 풀어 헤친 채 작은 몸을 앞으로 굽히고서 그 애는 기지개를 켜며 여전히 하품을 하고 있었다."], ["The instant his lips came together they curved into a blissfully sleepy smile, and with that smile he slowly and deliciously rolled back again.", "입술이 다물리는 순간 그 입술은 더없이 행복한 졸음에 겨운 미소로 휘어졌고, 그 미소를 띤 채 그 애는 느릿느릿 흐뭇하게 다시 뒤로 뒹굴어 누웠다."]], [["“Seryozha!”", "\"세료자!\""], ["she whispered, going noiselessly up to him.", "그녀는 소리 없이 그 애에게 다가가며 속삭였다."]], [["When she was parted from him, and all this latter time when she had been feeling a fresh rush of love for him, she had pictured him as he was at four years old, when she had loved him most of all.", "그 애와 떨어져 지내는 동안, 그리고 그 애를 향한 새로운 사랑의 물결을 느껴 온 요 근래 내내, 그녀는 그 애를 자기가 가장 사랑했던 네 살 때의 모습으로 그려 왔다."], ["Now he was not even the same as when she had left him; he was still further from the four-year-old baby, more grown and thinner.", "이제 그 애는 그녀가 떠날 때의 모습조차 아니었다. 네 살배기 아기로부터 더 멀어졌고, 더 자라고 더 야위었다."], ["How thin his face was, how short his hair was!", "얼굴은 어찌나 야위었는지, 머리카락은 어찌나 짧은지!"], ["What long hands!", "손은 어찌나 긴지!"], ["How he had changed since she left him!", "자기가 떠난 뒤로 그 애는 어찌나 변했는지!"], ["But it was he with his head, his lips, his soft neck and broad little shoulders.", "하지만 그 애는 그 머리, 그 입술, 그 보드라운 목, 작고 넓적한 어깨를 한 바로 그 애였다."]], [["“Seryozha!”", "\"세료자!\""], ["she repeated just in the child’s ear.", "그녀는 아이의 귓가에 대고 거듭 불렀다."]], [["He raised himself again on his elbow, turned his tangled head from side to side as though looking for something, and opened his eyes.", "그 애는 다시 팔꿈치로 몸을 일으키고는 뭔가를 찾기라도 하듯 헝클어진 머리를 이리저리 돌리다가 눈을 떴다."], ["Slowly and inquiringly he looked for several seconds at his mother standing motionless before him, then all at once he smiled a blissful smile, and shutting his eyes, rolled not backwards but towards her into her arms.", "느릿느릿 의아한 눈빛으로 그 애는 자기 앞에 꼼짝 않고 서 있는 어머니를 몇 초 동안 바라보다가, 별안간 더없이 행복한 미소를 짓더니, 눈을 감으며 뒤로가 아니라 그녀 쪽으로, 그녀의 품 안으로 뒹굴어 들었다."]], [["“Seryozha!", "\"세료자!"], ["my darling boy!”", "내 사랑하는 아가!\""], ["she said, breathing hard and putting her arms round his plump little body.", "그녀가 가쁜 숨을 몰아쉬며 그 애의 통통한 작은 몸을 두 팔로 끌어안고 말했다."], ["“Mother!”", "\"엄마!\""], ["he said, wriggling about in her arms so as to touch her hands with different parts of him.", "그 애가 그녀의 품 안에서 꼼지락거리며, 자기 몸 여기저기를 그녀의 두 손에 닿게 하면서 말했다."]], [["Smiling sleepily still with closed eyes, he flung fat little arms round her shoulders, rolled towards her, with the delicious sleepy warmth and fragrance that is only found in children, and began rubbing his face against her neck and shoulders.", "여전히 눈을 감은 채 졸음에 겨운 미소를 지으며, 그 애는 통통한 작은 두 팔로 그녀의 어깨를 감싸 안고, 오직 아이에게서만 풍기는 달콤하고 졸음에 겨운 온기와 향내와 더불어 그녀 쪽으로 뒹굴어 와서는, 그녀의 목과 어깨에 얼굴을 비비기 시작했다."]], [["“I know,”", "\"나 알아요.\""], ["he said, opening his eyes; “it’s my birthday today.", "그 애가 눈을 뜨며 말했다. \"오늘은 내 생일이에요."], ["I knew you’d come.", "엄마가 올 줄 알았어요."], ["I’ll get up directly.”", "나 금방 일어날게요.\""]], [["And saying that he dropped asleep.", "그러고는 그렇게 말하면서 다시 잠이 들었다."]], [["Anna looked at him hungrily; she saw how he had grown and changed in her absence.", "안나는 갈망에 차서 그 애를 바라보았다. 그녀는 자기가 없는 사이에 그 애가 어떻게 자라고 변했는지를 보았다."], ["She knew, and did not know, the bare legs so long now, that were thrust out below the quilt, those short-cropped curls on his neck in which she had so often kissed him.", "이불 밑으로 쑥 나온, 이제는 그토록 긴 맨다리, 자기가 그토록 자주 입맞추던 그 목덜미의 짧게 깎은 곱슬머리를 그녀는 알면서도 알 수 없었다."], ["She touched all this and could say nothing; tears choked her.", "그녀는 이 모든 것을 어루만졌지만 아무 말도 할 수 없었다. 눈물이 그녀의 목을 메이게 했다."]], [["“What are you crying for, mother?”", "\"엄마, 왜 울어요?\""], ["he said, waking completely up.", "그 애가 완전히 잠에서 깨며 말했다."], ["“Mother, what are you crying for?”", "\"엄마, 왜 우는 거예요?\""], ["he cried in a tearful voice.", "그 애가 울먹이는 목소리로 외쳤다."]], [["“I won’t cry ...", "\"안 울게……."], ["I’m crying for joy.", "기뻐서 우는 거야."], ["It’s so long since I’ve seen you.", "너를 본 지가 너무 오래돼서."], ["I won’t, I won’t,”", "안 울게, 안 울게.\""], ["she said, gulping down her tears and turning away.", "그녀가 눈물을 꿀꺽 삼키며 고개를 돌리고 말했다."], ["“Come, it’s time for you to dress now,”", "\"자, 이제 옷 입을 시간이구나.\""], ["she added, after a pause, and, never letting go his hands, she sat down by his bedside on the chair, where his clothes were put ready for him.", "그녀가 잠시 뒤 덧붙이고는, 그 애의 두 손을 결코 놓지 않은 채, 그 애의 옷이 채비되어 놓인 침대맡 의자에 앉았다."]], [["“How do you dress without me?", "\"넌 나 없이 어떻게 옷을 입니?"], ["How....”", "어떻게…….\""], ["she tried to begin talking simply and cheerfully, but she could not, and again she turned away.", "그녀는 단순하고 명랑하게 이야기를 시작하려 했지만 그럴 수가 없었고, 또다시 고개를 돌렸다."]], [["“I don’t have a cold bath, papa didn’t order it.", "\"나 찬물 목욕은 안 해요, 아빠가 시키지 않았거든요."], ["And you’ve not seen Vassily Lukitch?", "그런데 엄마, 바실리 루키치 못 봤어요?"], ["He’ll come in soon.", "곧 올 거예요."], ["Why, you’re sitting on my clothes!”", "어, 엄마 내 옷 깔고 앉았어요!\""]], [["And Seryozha went off into a peal of laughter.", "그러고는 세료자가 까르르 웃음을 터뜨렸다."], ["She looked at him and smiled.", "그녀는 그 애를 바라보며 미소 지었다."]], [["“Mother, darling, sweet one!”", "\"엄마, 사랑하는 엄마, 다정한 엄마!\""], ["he shouted, flinging himself on her again and hugging her.", "그 애가 다시 그녀에게 몸을 던지며 끌어안고 소리쳤다."], ["It was as though only now, on seeing her smile, he fully grasped what had happened.", "마치 이제야, 그녀가 미소 짓는 것을 보고서야, 무슨 일이 일어났는지를 온전히 깨달은 것 같았다."]], [["“I don’t want that on,”", "\"이건 쓰기 싫어요.\""], ["he said, taking off her hat.", "그 애가 그녀의 모자를 벗기며 말했다."], ["And as it were, seeing her afresh without her hat, he fell to kissing her again.", "그러고는 모자를 벗은 그녀를 새삼 다시 보기라도 하듯, 다시 그녀에게 입을 맞추기 시작했다."]], [["“But what did you think about me?", "\"그런데 엄마는 나에 대해 무슨 생각을 했어요?"], ["You didn’t think I was dead?”", "내가 죽었다고는 생각 안 했죠?\""]], [["“I never believed it.”", "\"난 절대로 그렇게 믿지 않았어요.\""]], [["“You didn’t believe it, my sweet?”", "\"안 믿었구나, 내 사랑?\""]], [["“I knew, I knew!”", "\"난 알았어요, 알았다고요!\""], ["he repeated his favorite phrase, and snatching the hand that was stroking his hair, he pressed the open palm to his mouth and kissed it.", "그 애가 자기가 좋아하는 말을 되풀이하며, 자기 머리카락을 쓰다듬던 손을 와락 붙잡아 펼친 손바닥을 자기 입에 갖다 대고 입을 맞추었다."]], [["# Chapter 30", "# Chapter 30"]], [["Meanwhile Vassily Lukitch had not at first understood who this lady was, and had learned from their conversation that it was no other person than the mother who had left her husband, and whom he had not seen, as he had entered the house after her departure.", "그러는 동안 바실리 루키치는 처음에는 이 부인이 누구인지 몰랐다가, 두 사람의 대화를 듣고서 그녀가 다름 아닌, 남편을 버리고 떠난 어머니라는 것을 알게 되었다. 그녀가 떠난 뒤에 이 집에 들어온 그는 한 번도 그녀를 본 적이 없었다."], ["He was in doubt whether to go in or not, or whether to communicate with Alexey Alexandrovitch.", "그는 안으로 들어가야 할지 말아야 할지, 아니면 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에게 알려야 할지 망설였다."], ["Reflecting finally that his duty was to get Seryozha up at the hour fixed, and that it was therefore not his business to consider who was there, the mother or anyone else, but simply to do his duty, he finished dressing, went to the door and opened it.", "마침내 자신의 임무는 정해진 시각에 세료자를 깨우는 것이며, 따라서 거기 있는 사람이 어머니인지 다른 누구인지를 따지는 것은 자기 일이 아니고, 그저 자기 직분을 다하면 된다고 생각한 그는 옷차림을 마치고 문으로 가서 문을 열었다."]], [["But the embraces of the mother and child, the sound of their voices, and what they were saying, made him change his mind.", "그러나 어머니와 아이가 끌어안는 모습이며, 그들의 목소리며, 그들이 나누는 말은 그의 마음을 돌려놓았다."]], [["He shook his head, and with a sigh he closed the door.", "그는 고개를 가로젓고는 한숨을 내쉬며 문을 닫았다."], ["“I’ll wait another ten minutes,”", "\"십 분만 더 기다리자.\""], ["he said to himself, clearing his throat and wiping away tears.", "그는 헛기침을 하고 눈물을 닦으며 혼잣말을 했다."]], [["Among the servants of the household there was intense excitement all this time.", "그동안 집 안의 하인들 사이에서는 격렬한 동요가 일고 있었다."], ["All had heard that their mistress had come, and that Kapitonitch had let her in, and that she was even now in the nursery, and that their master always went in person to the nursery at nine o’clock, and everyone fully comprehended that it was impossible for the husband and wife to meet, and that they must prevent it.", "모두들 마님이 오셨고, 카피토니치가 그분을 들여보냈으며, 지금 그분이 육아실에 계시다는 것을 알고 있었다. 그리고 주인은 늘 아홉 시에 직접 육아실로 들어간다는 것, 따라서 부부가 마주치는 것은 있을 수 없는 일이며 그것을 반드시 막아야 한다는 것을 모두 똑똑히 알고 있었다."], ["Korney, the valet, going down to the hall-porter’s room, asked who had let her in, and how it was he had done so, and ascertaining that Kapitonitch had admitted her and shown her up, he gave the old man a talking-to.", "시종인 코르네이는 문지기 방으로 내려가, 누가 마님을 들여보냈는지, 어쩌다 그런 일을 했는지 캐물었고, 카피토니치가 그녀를 들이고 위층으로 안내했다는 것을 알아내고는 노인을 호되게 나무랐다."], ["The hall-porter was doggedly silent, but when Korney told him he ought to be sent away, Kapitonitch darted up to him, and waving his hands in Korney’s face, began:", "문지기는 고집스레 입을 다물고 있었지만, 코르네이가 너 같은 놈은 쫓겨나야 마땅하다고 말하자 카피토니치가 그에게 달려들어 코르네이의 얼굴에다 손을 휘저으며 떠들기 시작했다."]], [["“Oh yes, to be sure you’d not have let her in!", "\"오, 그래, 너 같으면 그분을 안 들여보냈겠지!"], ["After ten years’ service, and never a word but of kindness, and there you’d up and say, ‘Be off, go along, get away with you!’ Oh yes, you’re a shrewd one at politics, I dare say!", "십 년을 모셨는데, 그동안 따뜻한 말씀밖에 들은 적 없는 그분께, 너라면 떡하니 나서서 '꺼져, 가, 저리 비켜!' 하고 말했겠지!"], ["You don’t need to be taught how to swindle the master, and to filch fur coats!”", "오, 그래, 너야말로 처세에 약삭빠른 놈이지! 주인을 등쳐 먹고 모피 외투를 슬쩍하는 법쯤은 누가 가르쳐 줄 것도 없는 놈이고!\""]], [["“Soldier!”", "\"군바리 같으니!\""], ["said Korney contemptuously, and he turned to the nurse who was coming in.", "코르네이가 경멸하듯 내뱉고는, 마침 들어오던 유모를 돌아보았다."], ["“Here, what do you think, Marya Efimovna: he let her in without a word to anyone,”", "\"마리야 예피모브나, 어떻게 생각하시오, 이자가 아무에게도 한마디 없이 그분을 들여보냈단 말이오.\""], ["Korney said addressing her.", "코르네이가 그녀에게 말했다."], ["“Alexey Alexandrovitch will be down immediately—and go into the nursery!”", "\"알렉세이 알렉산드로비치께서 곧 내려오셔서 육아실로 들어가실 텐데!\""]], [["“A pretty business, a pretty business!”", "\"이거 큰일이네, 큰일이야!\""], ["said the nurse.", "유모가 말했다."], ["“You, Korney Vassilievitch, you’d best keep him some way or other, the master, while I’ll run and get her away somehow.", "\"코르네이 바실리예비치, 당신은 어떻게든 주인 나리를 좀 붙들어 두구려. 그동안 나는 달려가서 그분을 어떻게든 빼내야겠소."], ["A pretty business!”", "이거 정말 큰일이네!\""]], [["When the nurse went into the nursery, Seryozha was telling his mother how he and Nadinka had had a fall in sledging downhill, and had turned over three times.", "유모가 육아실로 들어갔을 때, 세료자는 어머니에게 자기와 나딘카가 썰매를 타고 비탈을 내려오다가 넘어져서 세 번이나 굴렀다는 이야기를 하고 있었다."], ["She was listening to the sound of his voice, watching his face and the play of expression on it, touching his hand, but she did not follow what he was saying.", "그녀는 아들의 목소리에 귀를 기울이고, 그 얼굴과 거기 떠오르는 표정의 움직임을 바라보며, 아들의 손을 만지고 있었지만, 아이가 무슨 말을 하는지는 따라가지 못했다."], ["She must go, she must leave him,—this was the only thing she was thinking and feeling.", "가야 한다, 아이를 두고 떠나야 한다—그녀가 생각하고 느끼는 것은 오직 이것뿐이었다."], ["She heard the steps of Vassily Lukitch coming up to the door and coughing; she heard, too, the steps of the nurse as she came near; but she sat like one turned to stone, incapable of beginning to speak or to get up.", "그녀는 바실리 루키치가 문 앞으로 다가와 기침하는 발소리를 들었고, 가까이 다가오는 유모의 발소리도 들었지만, 돌처럼 굳어 앉은 채 말을 꺼내지도, 일어서지도 못했다."]], [["“Mistress, darling!”", "\"마님, 우리 마님!\""], ["began the nurse, going up to Anna and kissing her hands and shoulders.", "유모가 안나에게 다가가 그녀의 두 손과 어깨에 입을 맞추며 입을 열었다."], ["“God has brought joy indeed to our boy on his birthday.", "\"하느님께서 우리 도련님 생신에 정말로 기쁨을 보내 주셨네요."], ["You aren’t changed one bit.”", "마님은 조금도 변하지 않으셨어요.\""]], [["“Oh, nurse dear, I didn’t know you were in the house,”", "\"아, 유모, 이 집에 와 있는 줄 몰랐어요.\""], ["said Anna, rousing herself for a moment.", "안나가 잠시 정신을 차리며 말했다."]], [["“I’m not living here, I’m living with my daughter.", "\"저는 여기 살지 않아요, 딸과 함께 지내요."], ["I came for the birthday, Anna Arkadyevna, darling!”", "생신이라 온 거랍니다, 안나 아르카지예브나, 우리 마님!\""]], [["The nurse suddenly burst into tears, and began kissing her hand again.", "유모는 갑자기 울음을 터뜨리며 다시 그녀의 손에 입을 맞추기 시작했다."]], [["Seryozha, with radiant eyes and smiles, holding his mother by one hand and his nurse by the other, pattered on the rug with his fat little bare feet.", "세료자는 눈을 반짝이며 환하게 웃었고, 한 손으로는 어머니를, 다른 한 손으로는 유모를 붙잡은 채 통통한 작은 맨발로 양탄자를 동동 굴렀다."], ["The tenderness shown by his beloved nurse to his mother threw him into an ecstasy.", "사랑하는 유모가 어머니에게 보이는 다정함에 아이는 황홀경에 빠졌다."]], [["“Mother!", "\"엄마!"], ["She often comes to see me, and when she comes....”", "유모는 자주 나를 보러 와요, 그리고 올 때마다……\""], ["he was beginning, but he stopped, noticing that the nurse was saying something in a whisper to his mother, and that in his mother’s face there was a look of dread and something like shame, which was so strangely unbecoming to her.", "아이는 말을 꺼내다가, 유모가 어머니에게 무언가 속삭이고 있고, 어머니의 얼굴에 그녀에게는 이상하리만치 어울리지 않는, 두려움과 부끄러움 비슷한 빛이 떠오른 것을 알아채고는 입을 다물었다."]], [["She went up to him.", "그녀는 아이에게 다가갔다."]], [["“My sweet!”", "\"내 사랑!\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["She could not say good-bye, but the expression on her face said it, and he understood.", "그녀는 작별 인사를 할 수 없었지만, 그녀의 얼굴 표정이 그것을 말하고 있었고, 아이는 알아챘다."], ["“Darling, darling Kootik!”", "\"사랑하는, 사랑하는 쿠치크!\""], ["she used the name by which she had called him when he was little, “you won’t forget me?", "그녀는 아이가 어렸을 때 부르던 이름으로 불렀다. \"엄마를 잊지 않을 거지?"], ["You....”", "너는……\""], ["but she could not say more.", "그러나 그녀는 더 말을 잇지 못했다."]], [["How often afterwards she thought of words she might have said.", "나중에 그녀는 자기가 했을 수도 있었던 말들을 얼마나 자주 떠올렸던가."], ["But now she did not know how to say it, and could say nothing.", "그러나 지금은 어떻게 말해야 할지 몰랐고, 아무 말도 할 수 없었다."], ["But Seryozha knew all she wanted to say to him. He understood that she was unhappy and loved him.", "하지만 세료자는 그녀가 자기에게 하고 싶어 하는 말을 모두 알고 있었다."], ["He understood even what the nurse had whispered.", "그는 어머니가 불행하다는 것, 그리고 자기를 사랑한다는 것을 알았다."], ["He had caught the words “always at nine o’clock,”", "그는 유모가 속삭인 말까지도 알아들었다."], ["and he knew that this was said of his father, and that his father and mother could not meet.", "\"늘 아홉 시에\"라는 말을 들었고, 그것이 아버지에 관한 이야기이며, 아버지와 어머니가 마주칠 수 없다는 뜻임을 알았다."], ["That he understood, but one thing he could not understand—why there should be a look of dread and shame in her face?...", "그것은 이해했지만, 한 가지만은 이해할 수 없었다—어째서 어머니의 얼굴에 두려움과 부끄러움의 빛이 떠올라야 하는 걸까?……"], ["She was not in fault, but she was afraid of him and ashamed of something.", "어머니는 잘못한 게 없는데, 그를 두려워하고 무언가를 부끄러워하고 있었다."], ["He would have liked to put a question that would have set at rest this doubt, but he did not dare; he saw that she was miserable, and he felt for her.", "그는 이 의문을 풀어 줄 질문을 던지고 싶었지만, 차마 그러지 못했다. 어머니가 괴로워하는 것을 본 그는 어머니가 가여웠다."], ["Silently he pressed close to her and whispered, “Don’t go yet.", "그는 말없이 어머니에게 바싹 붙어 속삭였다. \"아직 가지 마요."], ["He won’t come just yet.”", "아버지는 아직 안 오실 거예요.\""]], [["The mother held him away from her to see what he was thinking, what to say to him, and in his frightened face she read not only that he was speaking of his father, but, as it were, asking her what he ought to think about his father.", "어머니는 아이가 무슨 생각을 하는지, 아이에게 무슨 말을 해야 할지 살피려고 아이를 떼어 놓고 바라보았다. 그리고 겁에 질린 아이의 얼굴에서, 아이가 아버지 이야기를 하고 있다는 것뿐만 아니라, 말하자면 아버지에 대해 어떻게 생각해야 하느냐고 자기에게 묻고 있다는 것을 읽어 냈다."]], [["“Seryozha, my darling,”", "\"세료자, 내 사랑.\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["she said, “love him; he’s better and kinder than I am, and I have done him wrong.", "\"아버지를 사랑하렴. 아버지는 나보다 더 훌륭하고 더 다정한 분이야, 그리고 내가 아버지에게 잘못을 저질렀어."], ["When you grow up you will judge.”", "네가 자라면 판단하게 될 거야.\""]], [["“There’s no one better than you!...”", "\"엄마보다 더 나은 사람은 없어요!……\""], ["he cried in despair through his tears, and, clutching her by the shoulders, he began squeezing her with all his force to him, his arms trembling with the strain.", "아이가 눈물을 흘리며 절망에 차 소리쳤고, 어머니의 어깨를 움켜쥐고 온 힘을 다해 자기 쪽으로 끌어안기 시작했다. 그 안간힘에 아이의 두 팔이 떨렸다."]], [["“My sweet, my little one!”", "\"내 사랑, 내 어린 것!\""], ["said Anna, and she cried as weakly and childishly as he.", "안나가 말했다. 그리고 그녀는 아이만큼이나 가냘프고 어린아이처럼 울었다."]], [["At that moment the door opened.", "그 순간 문이 열렸다."], ["Vassily Lukitch came in.", "바실리 루키치가 들어왔다."]], [["At the other door there was the sound of steps, and the nurse in a scared whisper said, “He’s coming,”", "다른 쪽 문에서도 발소리가 들렸고, 유모가 겁에 질린 목소리로 속삭였다. \"나리가 오세요.\""], ["and gave Anna her hat.", "그러고는 안나에게 모자를 건넸다."]], [["Seryozha sank onto the bed and sobbed, hiding his face in his hands.", "세료자는 침대 위에 쓰러져 두 손에 얼굴을 묻고 흐느꼈다."], ["Anna removed his hands, once more kissed his wet face, and with rapid steps went to the door.", "안나는 아이의 손을 떼어 내고, 눈물에 젖은 그 얼굴에 다시 한번 입을 맞춘 뒤, 빠른 걸음으로 문으로 향했다."], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch walked in, meeting her.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 그녀와 마주치며 걸어 들어왔다."], ["Seeing her, he stopped short and bowed his head.", "그녀를 보자 그는 우뚝 멈춰 서서 고개를 숙였다."]], [["Although she had just said he was better and kinder than she, in the rapid glance she flung at him, taking in his whole figure in all its details, feelings of repulsion and hatred for him and jealousy over her son took possession of her.", "조금 전에 그가 자기보다 더 훌륭하고 더 다정한 사람이라고 말했음에도, 그에게 빠르게 던진 시선으로 그의 모습 전체를 구석구석 훑어보는 사이, 그에 대한 혐오와 증오의 감정, 그리고 아들을 두고 느끼는 질투가 그녀를 사로잡았다."], ["With a swift gesture she put down her veil, and, quickening her pace, almost ran out of the room.", "그녀는 재빠른 손짓으로 베일을 내리고는 걸음을 재촉하여 거의 뛰다시피 방을 나섰다."]], [["She had not time to undo, and so carried back with her, the parcel of toys she had chosen the day before in a toy shop with such love and sorrow.", "그녀는 미처 풀어 보지도 못한 채, 전날 장난감 가게에서 그토록 큰 사랑과 슬픔으로 골랐던 장난감 꾸러미를 그대로 도로 들고 나왔다."]], [["# Chapter 31", "# Chapter 31"]], [["As intensely as Anna had longed to see her son, and long as she had been thinking of it and preparing herself for it, she had not in the least expected that seeing him would affect her so deeply.", "아들을 보고 싶다고 그토록 간절히 바라 왔고, 그토록 오랫동안 그 일을 생각하며 마음의 준비를 해 왔건만, 안나는 아들을 보는 일이 자신에게 그토록 깊은 영향을 끼치리라고는 조금도 예상하지 못했다."], ["On getting back to her lonely rooms in the hotel she could not for a long while understand why she was there.", "호텔의 적막한 방으로 돌아온 그녀는 자기가 왜 거기에 있는지 한참 동안 알 수가 없었다."], ["“Yes, it’s all over, and I am again alone,”", "\"그래, 다 끝났어. 나는 또다시 혼자야.\""], ["she said to herself, and without taking off her hat she sat down in a low chair by the hearth.", "그녀는 혼잣말을 하고는 모자도 벗지 않은 채 벽난로 옆의 낮은 안락의자에 앉았다."], ["Fixing her eyes on a bronze clock standing on a table between the windows, she tried to think.", "창문 사이의 탁자 위에 놓인 청동 시계에 눈길을 고정한 채, 그녀는 생각을 가다듬으려 애썼다."]], [["The French maid brought from abroad came in to suggest she should dress.", "외국에서 데려온 프랑스인 하녀가 옷을 갈아입으시라고 권하러 들어왔다."], ["She gazed at her wonderingly and said, “Presently.”", "그녀는 하녀를 이상하다는 듯 바라보며 \"이따가.\"라고 말했다."], ["A footman offered her coffee.", "하인이 커피를 내왔다."], ["“Later on,”", "\"나중에.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["The Italian nurse, after having taken the baby out in her best, came in with her, and brought her to Anna.", "이탈리아인 유모는 아기에게 제일 좋은 옷을 입혀 데리고 나갔다가, 다시 안고 들어와 안나에게 데려왔다."], ["The plump, well-fed little baby, on seeing her mother, as she always did, held out her fat little hands, and with a smile on her toothless mouth, began, like a fish with a float, bobbing her fingers up and down the starched folds of her embroidered skirt, making them rustle.", "토실토실하고 잘 먹어 통통한 어린 아기는 늘 그러듯 엄마를 보자 살진 작은 손을 내밀고는, 이가 나지 않은 입에 미소를 띤 채, 마치 찌가 달린 물고기처럼 자수 놓인 치마의 풀 먹인 주름을 따라 손가락을 위아래로 까딱이며 사락사락 소리를 냈다."], ["It was impossible not to smile, not to kiss the baby, impossible not to hold out a finger for her to clutch, crowing and prancing all over; impossible not to offer her a lip which she sucked into her little mouth by way of a kiss.", "미소 짓지 않을 수 없었고, 아기에게 입 맞추지 않을 수 없었으며, 아기가 까르륵거리며 온몸을 들썩이게 손가락을 내밀어 쥐게 하지 않을 수 없었다. 또 입맞춤이랍시고 아기가 작은 입으로 쪽쪽 빠는 입술을 내밀지 않을 수 없었다."], ["And all this Anna did, and took her in her arms and made her dance, and kissed her fresh little cheek and bare little elbows; but at the sight of this child it was plainer than ever to her that the feeling she had for her could not be called love in comparison with what she felt for Seryozha.", "안나는 이 모든 것을 다 해 주었고, 아기를 품에 안아 어르며 춤추게 했고, 싱싱한 작은 뺨과 드러난 작은 팔꿈치에 입을 맞췄다. 그러나 이 아이를 보고 있으려니, 자기가 이 아이에게 느끼는 감정이 세료자에게 느끼는 감정에 견주면 사랑이라 부를 수 없는 것임이 그 어느 때보다 분명해졌다."], ["Everything in this baby was charming, but for some reason all this did not go deep to her heart.", "이 아기는 모든 것이 사랑스러웠지만, 어쩐 일인지 그 모든 것이 그녀의 마음 깊은 곳까지 닿지는 않았다."], ["On her first child, though the child of an unloved father, had been concentrated all the love that had never found satisfaction.", "비록 사랑하지 않는 남자의 아이였지만, 그녀의 첫아이에게는 만족을 찾지 못했던 모든 사랑이 집중되어 있었다."], ["Her baby girl had been born in the most painful circumstances and had not had a hundredth part of the care and thought which had been concentrated on her first child.", "어린 딸은 더없이 고통스러운 정황 속에서 태어났고, 첫아이에게 쏟았던 보살핌과 마음의 백분의 일도 받지 못했다."], ["Besides, in the little girl everything was still in the future, while Seryozha was by now almost a personality, and a personality dearly loved.", "게다가 이 어린 딸에게는 모든 것이 아직 미래의 일이었지만, 세료자는 이제 거의 하나의 인격체였고, 더없이 사랑하는 인격체였다."], ["In him there was a conflict of thought and feeling; he understood her, he loved her, he judged her, she thought, recalling his words and his eyes.", "그 아이 안에는 생각과 감정의 갈등이 있었다. 그 아이는 그녀를 이해했고, 그녀를 사랑했고, 그녀를 판단했다고, 그녀는 아이의 말과 눈빛을 떠올리며 생각했다."], ["And she was forever—not physically only but spiritually—divided from him, and it was impossible to set this right.", "그런데 그녀는 그 아이와 영영—육체적으로뿐 아니라 정신적으로도—갈라져 버렸고, 이것은 바로잡을 길이 없었다."]], [["She gave the baby back to the nurse, let her go, and opened the locket in which there was Seryozha’s portrait when he was almost of the same age as the girl.", "그녀는 아기를 유모에게 돌려주고 내보낸 다음, 세료자가 지금 그 어린 딸과 거의 같은 나이였을 때의 초상이 들어 있는 로켓을 열었다."], ["She got up, and, taking off her hat, took up from a little table an album in which there were photographs of her son at different ages.", "그녀는 일어나 모자를 벗고, 작은 탁자에서 아들의 여러 시절 사진이 담긴 앨범을 집어 들었다."], ["She wanted to compare them, and began taking them out of the album.", "그것들을 비교해 보고 싶어서 그녀는 앨범에서 사진들을 빼내기 시작했다."], ["She took them all out except one, the latest and best photograph.", "그녀는 단 한 장, 가장 최근이자 가장 잘 나온 사진만 빼고 모두 빼냈다."], ["In it he was in a white smock, sitting astride a chair, with frowning eyes and smiling lips. It was his best, most characteristic expression.", "그 사진 속에서 아이는 하얀 작업복을 입고 의자에 걸터앉아, 미간을 찌푸린 눈에 입가에는 미소를 띠고 있었다."], ["With her little supple hands, her white, delicate fingers, that moved with a peculiar intensity today, she pulled at a corner of the photograph, but the photograph had caught somewhere, and she could not get it out.", "그것은 아이의 가장 잘 나온, 가장 그 아이다운 표정이었다. 오늘따라 유난히 강렬하게 움직이는 작고 부드러운 손, 희고 가녀린 손가락으로 그녀는 사진 한쪽 귀퉁이를 잡아당겼지만, 사진이 어딘가에 걸려 빠지지 않았다."], ["There was no paper-knife on the table, and so, pulling out the photograph that was next to her son’s (it was a photograph of Vronsky taken at Rome in a round hat and with long hair), she used it to push out her son’s photograph.", "탁자 위에는 종이칼이 없었으므로, 그녀는 아들 사진 옆에 있던 사진을(둥근 모자를 쓰고 긴 머리를 한 채 로마에서 찍은 브론스키의 사진이었다) 빼내어 그것으로 아들 사진을 밀어냈다."], ["“Oh, here is he!”", "\"어머, 여기 이이가 있네!\""], ["she said, glancing at the portrait of Vronsky, and she suddenly recalled that he was the cause of her present misery.", "그녀는 브론스키의 초상을 흘끗 보며 말했다. 그러자 문득 그가 지금 자기 불행의 원인이라는 사실이 떠올랐다."], ["She had not once thought of him all the morning.", "그녀는 아침 내내 그를 단 한 번도 생각하지 않았던 것이다."], ["But now, coming all at once upon that manly, noble face, so familiar and so dear to her, she felt a sudden rush of love for him.", "그러나 이제, 그토록 낯익고 그토록 소중한 그 남자답고 고귀한 얼굴과 별안간 마주치자, 그녀는 그를 향한 사랑이 갑자기 솟구치는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["“But where is he?", "\"그런데 그이는 어디 있지?"], ["How is it he leaves me alone in my misery?”", "어떻게 내 불행 속에 나를 혼자 내버려 둘 수 있단 말이야?\""], ["she thought all at once with a feeling of reproach, forgetting she had herself kept from him everything concerning her son.", "그녀는 자기가 아들에 관한 모든 것을 그에게 숨겨 왔다는 사실은 잊은 채, 별안간 원망스러운 마음으로 생각했다."], ["She sent to ask him to come to her immediately; with a throbbing heart she awaited him, rehearsing to herself the words in which she would tell him all, and the expressions of love with which he would console her.", "그녀는 즉시 자기에게 와 달라고 사람을 보냈다. 두근거리는 가슴으로 그를 기다리며, 그에게 모든 것을 털어놓을 말과 그가 자기를 위로해 줄 사랑의 표현들을 속으로 되뇌었다."], ["The messenger returned with the answer that he had a visitor with him, but that he would come immediately, and that he asked whether she would let him bring with him Prince Yashvin, who had just arrived in Petersburg.", "심부름꾼은 그에게 손님이 와 있지만 곧 가겠다는 대답과 함께, 방금 페테르부르크에 도착한 야시빈 공작을 데려가도 되겠느냐고 묻는다는 전갈을 가지고 돌아왔다. \"혼자 오지 않는구나."], ["“He’s not coming alone, and since dinner yesterday he has not seen me,”", "어제 저녁 식사 이후로 나를 보지 못했는데도,\" 그녀는 생각했다."], ["she thought; “he’s not coming so that I could tell him everything, but coming with Yashvin.”", "\"내가 모든 걸 털어놓을 수 있게 오는 게 아니라 야시빈을 데리고 오는 거야.\""], ["And all at once a strange idea came to her: what if he had ceased to love her?", "그러자 별안간 이상한 생각이 떠올랐다. 만약 그가 나를 더 이상 사랑하지 않게 되었다면?"]], [["And going over the events of the last few days, it seemed to her that she saw in everything a confirmation of this terrible idea.", "지난 며칠간의 일들을 되짚어 보니, 모든 것에서 이 끔찍한 생각을 뒷받침하는 증거가 보이는 듯했다."], ["The fact that he had not dined at home yesterday, and the fact that he had insisted on their taking separate sets of rooms in Petersburg, and that even now he was not coming to her alone, as though he were trying to avoid meeting her face to face.", "그가 어제 집에서 저녁을 먹지 않은 사실, 페테르부르크에서 따로 방을 나누어 쓰자고 고집한 사실, 그리고 지금조차 마치 얼굴을 맞대고 만나기를 피하려는 듯 혼자 오지 않는 사실까지도."]], [["“But he ought to tell me so.", "\"하지만 그이는 내게 그렇다고 말해 줘야 해."], ["I must know that it is so.", "나는 그것이 사실인지 알아야 해."], ["If I knew it, then I know what I should do,”", "그걸 안다면, 그땐 내가 어떻게 해야 할지 알 텐데.\""], ["she said to herself, utterly unable to picture to herself the position she would be in if she were convinced of his not caring for her.", "그녀는 혼잣말을 했지만, 그가 자기를 마음에 두고 있지 않다는 것을 확신하게 된다면 자기가 어떤 처지에 놓일지는 도무지 상상조차 할 수 없었다."], ["She thought he had ceased to love her, she felt close upon despair, and consequently she felt exceptionally alert.", "그녀는 그가 자기를 더 이상 사랑하지 않게 되었다고 생각했고, 절망에 거의 다다른 기분이었으며, 그래서 더욱 유난히 신경이 곤두서 있었다."], ["She rang for her maid and went to her dressing-room.", "그녀는 하녀를 부르러 종을 울리고 화장실로 갔다."], ["As she dressed, she took more care over her appearance than she had done all those days, as though he might, if he had grown cold to her, fall in love with her again because she had dressed and arranged her hair in the way most becoming to her.", "옷을 입으면서 그녀는 요 며칠 동안보다 더 정성스레 매무새를 매만졌다. 마치 그가 자기에게 식어 버렸더라도, 자기가 가장 어울리게 옷을 입고 머리를 매만진 덕분에 다시 자기를 사랑하게 되기라도 할 것처럼."]], [["She heard the bell ring before she was ready.", "채 준비를 끝내기도 전에 그녀는 초인종 소리를 들었다."], ["When she went into the drawing-room it was not he, but Yashvin, who met her eyes.", "응접실로 들어갔을 때 그녀의 눈에 들어온 것은 그가 아니라 야시빈이었다."], ["Vronsky was looking through the photographs of her son, which she had forgotten on the table, and he made no haste to look round at her.", "브론스키는 그녀가 탁자 위에 놓아둔 채 잊고 있던 아들의 사진들을 들여다보고 있었고, 서둘러 그녀 쪽을 돌아보지 않았다."]], [["“We have met already,”", "\"우리는 이미 만난 적이 있죠.\""], ["she said, putting her little hand into the huge hand of Yashvin, whose bashfulness was so queerly out of keeping with his immense frame and coarse face.", "그녀는 야시빈의 거대한 손에 자기 작은 손을 얹으며 말했다. 그의 수줍음은 거대한 체구와 거친 얼굴과는 묘하게 어울리지 않았다."], ["“We met last year at the races.", "\"작년 경마장에서 만났어요."], ["Give them to me,”", "그것들 이리 주세요.\""], ["she said, with a rapid movement snatching from Vronsky the photographs of her son, and glancing significantly at him with flashing eyes.", "그녀는 잽싼 동작으로 브론스키에게서 아들의 사진들을 낚아채며, 번뜩이는 눈으로 의미심장하게 그를 흘끗 보았다."], ["“Were the races good this year?", "\"올해 경마는 볼만했나요?"], ["Instead of them I saw the races in the Corso in Rome.", "저는 그 대신 로마의 코르소에서 경마를 봤어요."], ["But you don’t care for life abroad,”", "하지만 당신은 외국 생활을 좋아하지 않으시죠.\""], ["she said with a cordial smile.", "그녀는 다정한 미소를 지으며 말했다."], ["“I know you and all your tastes, though I have seen so little of you.”", "\"당신을 거의 뵌 적도 없지만, 저는 당신과 당신 취향을 모두 알고 있답니다.\""]], [["“I’m awfully sorry for that, for my tastes are mostly bad,”", "\"그건 참으로 유감입니다. 제 취향이라는 게 대개 형편없거든요.\""], ["said Yashvin, gnawing at his left mustache.", "야시빈이 왼쪽 콧수염을 깨물며 말했다."]], [["Having talked a little while, and noticing that Vronsky glanced at the clock, Yashvin asked her whether she would be staying much longer in Petersburg, and unbending his huge figure reached after his cap.", "잠시 이야기를 나누다가 브론스키가 시계를 흘끗 보는 것을 알아챈 야시빈은, 그녀에게 페테르부르크에 오래 머물 것이냐고 묻고는, 거대한 몸을 펴며 모자를 집으려 손을 뻗었다."]], [["“Not long, I think,”", "\"오래 있지는 않을 것 같아요.\""], ["she said hesitatingly, glancing at Vronsky.", "그녀는 브론스키를 흘끗 보며 망설이듯 말했다."]], [["“So then we shan’t meet again?”", "\"그럼 우리는 다시 만나지 못하겠군요?\""]], [["“Come and dine with me,”", "\"저와 식사하러 오세요.\""], ["said Anna resolutely, angry it seemed with herself for her embarrassment, but flushing as she always did when she defined her position before a fresh person.", "안나는 단호하게 말했다. 자기가 당황한 것에 화가 난 듯했지만, 처음 보는 사람 앞에서 자기 처지를 규정해야 할 때면 늘 그러듯 얼굴을 붉혔다."], ["“The dinner here is not good, but at least you will see him.", "\"여기 음식은 별로지만, 적어도 그이는 만나 보실 수 있을 거예요."], ["There is no one of his old friends in the regiment Alexey cares for as he does for you.”", "알렉세이가 연대의 옛 친구들 중에서 당신만큼 아끼는 사람은 없답니다.\""]], [["“Delighted,”", "\"기꺼이 가겠습니다.\""], ["said Yashvin with a smile, from which Vronsky could see that he liked Anna very much.", "야시빈이 미소를 지으며 말했고, 그 미소에서 브론스키는 그가 안나를 무척 마음에 들어 한다는 것을 알 수 있었다."]], [["Yashvin said good-bye and went away; Vronsky stayed behind.", "야시빈은 작별 인사를 하고 떠났다. 브론스키는 남았다."]], [["“Are you going too?”", "\"당신도 가시나요?\""], ["she said to him.", "그녀가 그에게 말했다."]], [["“I’m late already,”", "\"이미 늦었소.\""], ["he answered. “Run along!", "그가 대답했다."], ["I’ll catch you up in a moment,”", "\"먼저 가게! 곧 따라가겠네.\""], ["he called to Yashvin.", "그가 야시빈에게 소리쳤다."]], [["She took him by the hand, and without taking her eyes off him, gazed at him while she ransacked her mind for the words to say that would keep him.", "그녀는 그의 손을 잡았고, 그를 붙잡아 둘 말을 찾느라 머릿속을 뒤지는 동안 그에게서 눈을 떼지 않은 채 그를 바라보았다."]], [["“Wait a minute, there’s something I want to say to you,”", "\"잠깐만요, 당신에게 하고 싶은 말이 있어요.\""], ["and taking his broad hand she pressed it on her neck.", "그러고는 그의 넓은 손을 잡아 자기 목에 가져다 댔다."], ["“Oh, was it right my asking him to dinner?”", "\"아, 그이를 식사에 초대한 게 잘한 일이었나요?\""]], [["“You did quite right,”", "\"아주 잘했소.\""], ["he said with a serene smile that showed his even teeth, and he kissed her hand.", "그가 고른 이를 드러내는 평온한 미소를 지으며 말하고는 그녀의 손에 입을 맞췄다."]], [["“Alexey, you have not changed to me?”", "\"알렉세이, 당신은 내게 변하지 않았죠?\""], ["she said, pressing his hand in both of hers.", "그녀가 두 손으로 그의 손을 꼭 쥐며 말했다."], ["“Alexey, I am miserable here.", "\"알렉세이, 나는 여기서 비참해요."], ["When are we going away?”", "우리는 언제 떠나죠?\""]], [["“Soon, soon.", "\"곧, 곧 떠나겠소."], ["You wouldn’t believe how disagreeable our way of living here is to me too,”", "여기서 우리가 사는 방식이 내게도 얼마나 불쾌한지 당신은 믿지 못할 거요.\""], ["he said, and he drew away his hand.", "그가 말하고는 손을 빼냈다."]], [["“Well, go, go!”", "\"그럼, 가요, 가!\""], ["she said in a tone of offense, and she walked quickly away from him.", "그녀가 모욕당한 듯한 어조로 말하고는 그에게서 빠르게 멀어져 갔다."]], [["# Chapter 32", "# Chapter 32"]], [["When Vronsky returned home, Anna was not yet home.", "브론스키가 집에 돌아왔을 때, 안나는 아직 돌아오지 않았다."], ["Soon after he had left, some lady, so they told him, had come to see her, and she had gone out with her.", "그가 나간 직후에 어떤 부인이 안나를 찾아왔고, 안나는 그 부인과 함께 외출했다고 하인들이 일러 주었다."], ["That she had gone out without leaving word where she was going, that she had not yet come back, and that all the morning she had been going about somewhere without a word to him—all this, together with the strange look of excitement in her face in the morning, and the recollection of the hostile tone with which she had before Yashvin almost snatched her son’s photographs out of his hands, made him serious.", "어디로 가는지 한마디도 남기지 않고 나가 버린 것, 아직도 돌아오지 않은 것, 그리고 아침 내내 자기에게 아무 말 없이 어딘가를 쏘다닌 것—이 모든 것이, 아침에 그녀의 얼굴에 떠올랐던 묘하게 흥분한 기색이며, 야시빈 앞에서 아들의 사진을 그의 손에서 거의 낚아채다시피 했던 그 적대적인 어조에 대한 기억과 더불어 그를 심각하게 만들었다."], ["He decided he absolutely must speak openly with her.", "그는 반드시 그녀와 터놓고 이야기해야겠다고 마음먹었다."], ["And he waited for her in her drawing-room.", "그래서 그는 그녀의 응접실에서 그녀를 기다렸다."], ["But Anna did not return alone, but brought with her her old unmarried aunt, Princess Oblonskaya.", "그러나 안나는 혼자 돌아오지 않고, 결혼하지 않은 늙은 이모인 오블론스카야 공작 부인을 데리고 왔다."], ["This was the lady who had come in the morning, and with whom Anna had gone out shopping.", "이 부인이 바로 아침에 찾아왔던 그 부인이었고, 안나는 이 부인과 함께 물건을 사러 나갔던 것이다."], ["Anna appeared not to notice Vronsky’s worried and inquiring expression, and began a lively account of her morning’s shopping.", "안나는 브론스키의 근심스럽고 캐묻는 듯한 표정을 알아채지 못한 듯, 아침에 물건을 사러 다닌 일을 활기차게 늘어놓기 시작했다."], ["He saw that there was something working within her; in her flashing eyes, when they rested for a moment on him, there was an intense concentration, and in her words and movements there was that nervous rapidity and grace which, during the early period of their intimacy, had so fascinated him, but which now so disturbed and alarmed him.", "그는 그녀 안에서 무언가가 작동하고 있음을 알아차렸다. 잠깐씩 그에게 머무는 그녀의 반짝이는 눈에는 강렬한 집중이 어려 있었고, 그녀의 말과 동작에는 두 사람이 막 가까워지던 무렵 그를 그토록 매혹시켰으나 이제는 그를 그토록 불안하게 하고 놀라게 하는 그 신경질적인 민첩함과 우아함이 깃들어 있었다."]], [["The dinner was laid for four.", "식탁은 네 사람 몫으로 차려졌다."], ["All were gathered together and about to go into the little dining-room when Tushkevitch made his appearance with a message from Princess Betsy.", "모두 한데 모여 막 작은 식당으로 들어가려는데, 투시케비치가 베시 공작 부인의 전갈을 들고 나타났다."], ["Princess Betsy begged her to excuse her not having come to say good-bye; she had been indisposed, but begged Anna to come to her between half-past six and nine o’clock.", "베시 공작 부인은 작별 인사를 하러 오지 못한 것을 용서해 달라고, 몸이 편치 않았다고 전하면서, 안나에게 여섯 시 반에서 아홉 시 사이에 자기에게 와 달라고 청했다."], ["Vronsky glanced at Anna at the precise limit of time, so suggestive of steps having been taken that she should meet no one; but Anna appeared not to notice it.", "브론스키는 아무도 마주치지 않게끔 조치가 취해졌음을 암시하는 그 정확한 시간 제한을 듣고 안나를 흘끗 보았다. 그러나 안나는 그것을 알아채지 못한 듯했다."]], [["“Very sorry that I can’t come just between half-past six and nine,”", "\"하필 여섯 시 반에서 아홉 시 사이라니 갈 수 없어서 정말 유감이에요.\""], ["she said with a faint smile.", "그녀가 희미하게 웃으며 말했다."]], [["“The princess will be very sorry.”", "\"공작 부인께서 무척 서운해하실 겁니다.\""]], [["“And so am I.”", "\"저도 그래요.\""]], [["“You’re going, no doubt, to hear Patti?”", "\"틀림없이 파티를 들으러 가시는 거겠죠?\""], ["said Tushkevitch.", "투시케비치가 말했다."]], [["“Patti?", "\"파티요?"], ["You suggest the idea to me.", "덕분에 그 생각이 났네요."], ["I would go if it were possible to get a box.”", "특별석을 구할 수만 있다면 가고 싶어요.\""]], [["“I can get one,”", "\"제가 구해 드릴 수 있습니다.\""], ["Tushkevitch offered his services.", "투시케비치가 자기 봉사를 자청했다."]], [["“I should be very, very grateful to you,”", "\"정말, 정말 감사하겠어요.\" 안나가 말했다."], ["said Anna. “But won’t you dine with us?”", "\"그런데 우리와 함께 식사하지 않으시겠어요?\""]], [["Vronsky gave a hardly perceptible shrug.", "브론스키는 거의 알아챌 수 없을 만큼 어깨를 으쓱했다."], ["He was at a complete loss to understand what Anna was about.", "그는 안나가 무엇을 하려는 것인지 도무지 알 수 없었다."], ["What had she brought the old Princess Oblonskaya home for, what had she made Tushkevitch stay to dinner for, and, most amazing of all, why was she sending him for a box?", "무엇 때문에 늙은 오블론스카야 공작 부인을 집으로 데려왔으며, 무엇 때문에 투시케비치를 만찬에 붙들어 두었으며, 무엇보다도 놀라운 것은 어째서 그에게 특별석을 구해 오라고 보내는 것일까?"], ["Could she possibly think in her position of going to Patti’s benefit, where all the circle of her acquaintances would be?", "지금 그녀의 처지에 자기가 아는 사람들이 죄다 모일 파티의 자선 공연에 가려는 생각을 정말로 할 수 있단 말인가?"], ["He looked at her with serious eyes, but she responded with that defiant, half-mirthful, half-desperate look, the meaning of which he could not comprehend.", "그는 심각한 눈으로 그녀를 바라보았으나, 그녀는 그가 그 뜻을 헤아릴 수 없는, 도전적이면서도 반쯤은 즐거워하고 반쯤은 절망적인 그 시선으로 응수했다."], ["At dinner Anna was in aggressively high spirits—she almost flirted both with Tushkevitch and with Yashvin.", "식사하는 동안 안나는 공격적일 만큼 들떠 있었다. 그녀는 투시케비치에게도 야시빈에게도 거의 추파를 던지다시피 했다."], ["When they got up from dinner and Tushkevitch had gone to get a box at the opera, Yashvin went to smoke, and Vronsky went down with him to his own rooms.", "식탁에서 일어나 투시케비치가 오페라 극장으로 특별석을 구하러 가고, 야시빈은 담배를 피우러 가자, 브론스키는 그와 함께 자기 방으로 내려갔다."], ["After sitting there for some time he ran upstairs.", "그곳에 한참 앉아 있던 그는 위층으로 뛰어 올라갔다."], ["Anna was already dressed in a low-necked gown of light silk and velvet that she had had made in Paris, and with costly white lace on her head, framing her face, and particularly becoming, showing up her dazzling beauty.", "안나는 이미 파리에서 맞춘, 목둘레가 깊이 파인 가벼운 비단과 벨벳 드레스를 차려입고, 머리에는 값비싼 흰 레이스를 얹어 얼굴을 감싸도록 했는데, 그것이 유난히 잘 어울려 그녀의 눈부신 아름다움을 한껏 돋보이게 했다."]], [["“Are you really going to the theater?”", "\"정말 극장에 갈 거요?\""], ["he said, trying not to look at her.", "그는 그녀를 보지 않으려 애쓰며 말했다."]], [["“Why do you ask with such alarm?”", "\"왜 그렇게 놀란 듯이 묻는 거예요?\""], ["she said, wounded again at his not looking at her.", "그가 자기를 보지 않는 것에 또다시 상처를 입은 그녀가 말했다."], ["“Why shouldn’t I go?”", "\"내가 왜 가면 안 되나요?\""]], [["She appeared not to understand the motive of his words.", "그녀는 그의 말에 담긴 의도를 이해하지 못하는 듯했다."]], [["“Oh, of course, there’s no reason whatever,”", "\"아, 물론, 가면 안 될 이유야 전혀 없지.\""], ["he said, frowning.", "그가 미간을 찌푸리며 말했다."]], [["“That’s just what I say,”", "\"그게 바로 내가 하는 말이잖아요.\""], ["she said, willfully refusing to see the irony of his tone, and quietly turning back her long, perfumed glove.", "그녀는 그의 어조에 담긴 빈정거림을 짐짓 못 본 체하며, 길고 향내 나는 장갑을 조용히 뒤집어 끼면서 말했다."]], [["“Anna, for God’s sake!", "\"안나, 제발!"], ["what is the matter with you?”", "도대체 당신 왜 이러는 거요?\""], ["he said, appealing to her exactly as once her husband had done.", "그는 언젠가 그녀의 남편이 했던 것과 똑같이 그녀에게 호소했다."]], [["“I don’t understand what you are asking.”", "\"무엇을 묻는 건지 모르겠어요.\""]], [["“You know that it’s out of the question to go.”", "\"극장에 가는 건 말도 안 된다는 걸 당신도 알지 않소.\""]], [["“Why so?", "\"왜요?"], ["I’m not going alone.", "혼자 가는 것도 아닌데."], ["Princess Varvara has gone to dress, she is going with me.”", "바르바라 공작 부인이 옷을 갈아입으러 가셨어요, 나랑 같이 갈 거예요.\""]], [["He shrugged his shoulders with an air of perplexity and despair.", "그는 당혹스럽고 절망스러운 기색으로 어깨를 으쓱했다."]], [["“But do you mean to say you don’t know?...”", "\"하지만 정말 당신은 모른단 말이오?……\""], ["he began.", "그가 입을 열었다."]], [["“But I don’t care to know!”", "\"하지만 난 알고 싶지도 않아요!\""], ["she almost shrieked.", "그녀가 거의 비명을 지르듯 말했다."], ["“I don’t care to.", "\"알고 싶지 않다고요."], ["Do I regret what I have done? No, no, no!", "내가 한 일을 후회하느냐고요?"], ["If it were all to do again from the beginning, it would be the same.", "아니요, 아니요, 아니요! 처음부터 다시 모든 걸 되풀이한다 해도 똑같을 거예요."], ["For us, for you and for me, there is only one thing that matters, whether we love each other.", "우리에게, 당신과 나에게 중요한 건 단 하나, 우리가 서로를 사랑하느냐 하는 것뿐이에요."], ["Other people we need not consider.", "다른 사람들은 신경 쓸 필요도 없어요."], ["Why are we living here apart and not seeing each other?", "우리가 왜 이렇게 떨어져서, 서로 만나지도 못하고 살아야 하죠?"], ["Why can’t I go?", "내가 왜 가면 안 되나요?"], ["I love you, and I don’t care for anything,”", "난 당신을 사랑해요, 그러니 다른 건 아무래도 좋아요.\""], ["she said in Russian, glancing at him with a peculiar gleam in her eyes that he could not understand.", "그녀는 러시아어로 말하며, 그가 이해할 수 없는 묘한 빛을 띤 눈으로 그를 흘끗 바라보았다."], ["“If you have not changed to me, why don’t you look at me?”", "\"당신이 나에 대한 마음이 변하지 않았다면, 왜 나를 보지 않는 거죠?\""]], [["He looked at her.", "그는 그녀를 바라보았다."], ["He saw all the beauty of her face and full dress, always so becoming to her.", "늘 그녀에게 그토록 잘 어울리는 그 얼굴과 차림새의 아름다움을 그는 빠짐없이 보았다."], ["But now her beauty and elegance were just what irritated him.", "그러나 지금은 바로 그 아름다움과 우아함이 그를 짜증나게 했다."]], [["“My feeling cannot change, you know, but I beg you, I entreat you,”", "\"내 감정은 변할 수 없소, 당신도 알잖소, 하지만 부탁이오, 간청하오.\""], ["he said again in French, with a note of tender supplication in his voice, but with coldness in his eyes.", "그는 다시 프랑스어로, 목소리에는 부드러운 애원의 음색을 담았으나 눈에는 차가움을 띤 채 말했다."]], [["She did not hear his words, but she saw the coldness of his eyes, and answered with irritation:", "그녀는 그의 말을 듣지 않았지만, 그의 눈에 어린 차가움은 보았고, 짜증스럽게 대답했다."]], [["“And I beg you to explain why I should not go.”", "\"그럼 나도 부탁하는데, 내가 왜 가면 안 되는지 설명해 줘요.\""]], [["“Because it might cause you....”", "\"그게 당신에게…… 화를 부를 수도 있으니까.\""], ["he hesitated.", "그가 머뭇거렸다."]], [["“I don’t understand.", "\"무슨 말인지 모르겠어요."], ["Yashvin n’est pas compromettant, and Princess Varvara is no worse than others.", "야시빈은 n'est pas compromettant이고, 바르바라 공작 부인도 다른 사람들보다 나쁠 게 없잖아요. 아, 마침 저기 오시네요!\""], ["Oh, here she is!”", "* n'est pas compromettant: 프랑스어로 '체면을 손상시킬 만한 사람이 아니다'라는 뜻."]], [["# Chapter 33", "# Chapter 33"]], [["Vronsky for the first time experienced a feeling of anger against Anna, almost a hatred for her willfully refusing to understand her own position.", "브론스키는 난생처음 안나를 향한 분노의 감정을, 자기 처지를 한사코 이해하려 들지 않는 그녀에 대한 거의 증오에 가까운 감정을 느꼈다."], ["This feeling was aggravated by his being unable to tell her plainly the cause of his anger.", "이 감정은 분노의 까닭을 그녀에게 솔직히 말할 수 없다는 사실 때문에 한층 더 격해졌다."], ["If he had told her directly what he was thinking, he would have said:", "만일 그가 자기 생각을 곧이곧대로 말했더라면, 그는 이렇게 말했을 것이다."]], [["“In that dress, with a princess only too well known to everyone, to show yourself at the theater is equivalent not merely to acknowledging your position as a fallen woman, but is flinging down a challenge to society, that is to say, cutting yourself off from it forever.”", "\"그런 옷차림에, 누구나 너무도 잘 아는 공작 부인과 함께 극장에 모습을 드러내는 것은 타락한 여자라는 당신의 처지를 인정하는 것일 뿐만 아니라, 사교계에 도전장을 던지는 것이고, 다시 말해 사교계와 영영 인연을 끊어 버리는 것이야.\""]], [["He could not say that to her.", "그는 그 말을 그녀에게 할 수 없었다."], ["“But how can she fail to see it, and what is going on in her?”", "\"하지만 어떻게 그걸 못 본단 말인가, 대체 그녀 안에서 무슨 일이 일어나고 있는 거지?\""], ["he said to himself.", "그는 혼잣말을 했다."], ["He felt at the same time that his respect for her was diminished while his sense of her beauty was intensified.", "그러면서 그는 그녀에 대한 존경심은 줄어드는 반면, 그녀의 아름다움에 대한 느낌은 더욱 강렬해지고 있음을 느꼈다."]], [["He went back scowling to his rooms, and sitting down beside Yashvin, who, with his long legs stretched out on a chair, was drinking brandy and seltzer water, he ordered a glass of the same for himself.", "그는 잔뜩 인상을 쓴 채 자기 방으로 돌아왔고, 긴 다리를 의자 위에 쭉 뻗고 브랜디에 셀처를 타 마시고 있던 야시빈 옆에 앉아, 자기에게도 똑같은 것을 한 잔 가져오라고 시켰다."]], [["“You were talking of Lankovsky’s Powerful.", "\"란콥스키의 모구치 얘길 했지."], ["That’s a fine horse, and I would advise you to buy him,”", "그거 좋은 말이야, 그러니 자네한테 사라고 권하고 싶네.\""], ["said Yashvin, glancing at his comrade’s gloomy face.", "야시빈이 친구의 침울한 얼굴을 흘끗 보며 말했다."], ["“His hind-quarters aren’t quite first-rate, but the legs and head—one couldn’t wish for anything better.”", "\"엉덩이 쪽은 그리 일류급은 아니지만, 다리와 머리는—더 바랄 게 없을 정도라네.\""]], [["“I think I will take him,”", "\"그놈을 사 둘까 하네.\""], ["answered Vronsky.", "브론스키가 대답했다."]], [["Their conversation about horses interested him, but he did not for an instant forget Anna, and could not help listening to the sound of steps in the corridor and looking at the clock on the chimney piece.", "말에 관한 그들의 대화가 그의 흥미를 끌었지만, 그는 한순간도 안나를 잊지 못했고, 복도에서 들려오는 발소리에 귀를 기울이고 벽난로 위의 시계를 바라보지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["“Anna Arkadyevna gave orders to announce that she has gone to the theater.”", "\"안나 아르카지예브나께서 극장에 가셨다고 전하라 분부하셨습니다.\""]], [["Yashvin, tipping another glass of brandy into the bubbling water, drank it and got up, buttoning his coat.", "야시빈은 거품이 이는 물에 브랜디를 한 잔 더 따라 들이켠 뒤 일어서서 외투의 단추를 채웠다."]], [["“Well, let’s go,”", "\"자, 가세.\""], ["he said, faintly smiling under his mustache, and showing by this smile that he knew the cause of Vronsky’s gloominess, and did not attach any significance to it.", "그가 콧수염 아래로 희미하게 웃으며 말했는데, 그 미소로 그는 브론스키의 침울함의 까닭을 알고 있으며 그것에 별다른 의미를 두지 않는다는 것을 내비쳤다."]], [["“I’m not going,”", "\"난 안 가겠네.\""], ["Vronsky answered gloomily.", "브론스키가 침울하게 대답했다."]], [["“Well, I must, I promised to.", "\"음, 나는 가야겠네, 약속을 했으니까."], ["Good-bye, then.", "그럼 잘 있게."], ["If you do, come to the stalls; you can take Kruzin’s stall,”", "만약 자네가 오거든 일층 좌석으로 오게. 크루진의 자리를 써도 되니까.\""], ["added Yashvin as he went out.", "야시빈은 나가면서 덧붙였다."]], [["“No, I’m busy.”", "\"아니, 난 바쁘네.\""]], [["“A wife is a care, but it’s worse when she’s not a wife,”", "'아내는 골칫거리지만, 아내가 아닐 땐 더 골치 아픈 법이지.'"], ["thought Yashvin, as he walked out of the hotel.", "야시빈은 호텔을 나서면서 생각했다."]], [["Vronsky, left alone, got up from his chair and began pacing up and down the room.", "홀로 남은 브론스키는 의자에서 일어나 방 안을 이리저리 거닐기 시작했다."]], [["“And what’s today?", "'그래, 오늘이 며칠째지?"], ["The fourth night....", "나흘째 밤이군……."], ["Yegor and his wife are there, and my mother, most likely.", "예고르와 그 아내가 와 있을 테고, 어머니도 십중팔구 와 계실 테지."], ["Of course all Petersburg’s there.", "물론 페테르부르크 사람들이 죄다 와 있을 테고."], ["Now she’s gone in, taken off her cloak and come into the light.", "지금쯤 그녀는 안으로 들어가 외투를 벗고 불빛 속에 모습을 드러냈겠지."], ["Tushkevitch, Yashvin, Princess Varvara,”", "투시케비치, 야시빈, 바르바라 공작 부인.'"], ["he pictured them to himself....", "그는 그 모습을 머릿속에 그려 보았다……."], ["“What about me?", "'그럼 나는?"], ["Either that I’m frightened or have given up to Tushkevitch the right to protect her?", "내가 겁을 먹었거나, 아니면 그녀를 보호할 권리를 투시케비치에게 넘겨 버렸다는 건가?"], ["From every point of view—stupid, stupid!...", "어느 모로 보나—멍청하다, 멍청해!……"], ["And why is she putting me in such a position?”", "대체 왜 그녀는 나를 이런 처지로 몰아넣는 거지?'"], ["he said with a gesture of despair.", "그는 절망적인 몸짓을 하며 말했다."]], [["With that gesture he knocked against the table, on which there was standing the seltzer water and the decanter of brandy, and almost upset it.", "그 몸짓을 하다가 그는 셀처와 브랜디 술병이 놓여 있는 탁자를 쳐서 거의 엎을 뻔했다."], ["He tried to catch it, let it slip, and angrily kicked the table over and rang.", "그는 그것을 붙잡으려다 놓쳐 버렸고, 화가 나서 탁자를 발로 차 넘어뜨리고는 벨을 울렸다."]], [["“If you care to be in my service,”", "\"내 밑에서 일할 마음이 있거든,\""], ["he said to the valet who came in, “you had better remember your duties.", "그는 들어온 시종에게 말했다, \"자네 할 일이나 똑똑히 기억하는 게 좋을 거야."], ["This shouldn’t be here.", "이게 여기 있어서는 안 되지."], ["You ought to have cleared away.”", "진작 치웠어야 했어.\""]], [["The valet, conscious of his own innocence, would have defended himself, but glancing at his master, he saw from his face that the only thing to do was to be silent, and hurriedly threading his way in and out, dropped down on the carpet and began gathering up the whole and broken glasses and bottles.", "시종은 자기에게 잘못이 없음을 알기에 변명을 하려 했지만, 주인을 흘끗 보고는 그 얼굴에서 이럴 땐 입을 다무는 수밖에 없음을 알아채고, 부랴부랴 이리저리 몸을 놀려 양탄자에 주저앉아 깨진 잔과 멀쩡한 잔이며 병들을 주워 모으기 시작했다."]], [["“That’s not your duty; send the waiter to clear away, and get my dress coat out.”", "\"그건 자네 일이 아니네. 웨이터를 불러 치우게 하고, 내 연미복이나 꺼내 와.\""]], [["Vronsky went into the theater at half-past eight.", "브론스키는 여덟 시 반에 극장으로 들어섰다."], ["The performance was in full swing.", "공연은 한창 무르익고 있었다."], ["The little old box-keeper, recognizing Vronsky as he helped him off with his fur coat, called him “Your Excellency,” and suggested he should not take a number but should simply call Fyodor.", "자그마한 노인 안내원은 브론스키가 모피 외투 벗는 것을 거들면서 그를 알아보고는 \"각하\"라고 부르며, 표를 받지 말고 그냥 표도르를 부르시라고 권했다."], ["In the brightly lighted corridor there was no one but the box-opener and two attendants with fur cloaks on their arms listening at the doors.", "환하게 밝혀진 복도에는 안내원과, 모피 외투를 팔에 걸친 채 문에 귀를 대고 있는 두 명의 종업원 말고는 아무도 없었다."], ["Through the closed doors came the sounds of the discreet staccato accompaniment of the orchestra, and a single female voice rendering distinctly a musical phrase.", "닫힌 문 너머로 절제된 스타카토의 오케스트라 반주 소리가 들려왔고, 여자 목소리 하나가 어떤 악구를 또렷하게 부르고 있었다."], ["The door opened to let the box-opener slip through, and the phrase drawing to the end reached Vronsky’s hearing clearly.", "안내원이 빠져나가도록 문이 열렸고, 끝을 향해 가던 악구가 브론스키의 귀에 또렷이 들려왔다."], ["But the doors were closed again at once, and Vronsky did not hear the end of the phrase and the cadence of the accompaniment, though he knew from the thunder of applause that it was over.", "그러나 문은 곧바로 다시 닫혔고, 브론스키는 악구의 끝과 반주의 종지부를 듣지 못했지만, 우레 같은 박수 소리로 그것이 끝났음을 알았다."], ["When he entered the hall, brilliantly lighted with chandeliers and gas jets, the noise was still going on.", "그가 샹들리에와 가스등으로 눈부시게 밝혀진 홀로 들어섰을 때도 소음은 여전히 이어지고 있었다."], ["On the stage the singer, bowing and smiling, with bare shoulders flashing with diamonds, was, with the help of the tenor who had given her his arm, gathering up the bouquets that were flying awkwardly over the footlights.", "무대 위에서는 여가수가 드러난 어깨에 다이아몬드를 반짝이며 고개 숙여 인사하고 미소를 지으면서, 자기에게 팔을 내민 테너의 도움을 받아 각광 너머로 어색하게 날아드는 꽃다발들을 주워 모으고 있었다."], ["Then she went up to a gentleman with glossy pomaded hair parted down the center, who was stretching across the footlights holding out something to her, and all the public in the stalls as well as in the boxes was in excitement, craning forward, shouting and clapping.", "그러더니 그녀는 가운데로 가르마를 탄 반들반들 포마드 칠한 머리의 한 신사에게로 다가갔는데, 그 신사는 각광 너머로 몸을 뻗어 그녀에게 무언가를 내밀고 있었고, 일층 관람석과 박스석의 모든 관객이 흥분하여 몸을 앞으로 내밀고 소리치며 박수를 쳐 댔다."], ["The conductor in his high chair assisted in passing the offering, and straightened his white tie.", "높은 의자에 앉은 지휘자는 그 헌정품을 건네주는 것을 도우면서 자신의 흰 넥타이를 바로잡았다."], ["Vronsky walked into the middle of the stalls, and, standing still, began looking about him.", "브론스키는 일층 좌석 한가운데로 걸어가 멈춰 서서 주위를 둘러보기 시작했다."], ["That day less than ever was his attention turned upon the familiar, habitual surroundings, the stage, the noise, all the familiar, uninteresting, particolored herd of spectators in the packed theater.", "그날은 그 어느 때보다도 그의 주의가 낯익고 습관적인 환경, 그러니까 무대며 소음이며, 꽉 들어찬 극장 안의 그 낯익고 시시하며 가지각색인 관객 무리에게로 쏠리지 않았다."]], [["There were, as always, the same ladies of some sort with officers of some sort in the back of the boxes; the same gaily dressed women—God knows who—and uniforms and black coats; the same dirty crowd in the upper gallery; and among the crowd, in the boxes and in the front rows, were some forty of the real people.", "언제나처럼, 박스석 안쪽에는 어떤 부류의 부인들이 어떤 부류의 장교들과 함께 있었고, 화려하게 차려입은 여자들이—누군지는 신만이 알겠지만—제복들과 검은 연미복들 사이에 있었으며, 위층 관람석에는 변함없이 지저분한 군중이 있었다. 그리고 그 군중 가운데, 박스석과 앞줄에는 마흔 명쯤 되는 진짜 사람들이 있었다."], ["And to those oases Vronsky at once directed his attention, and with them he entered at once into relation.", "브론스키는 곧장 그 오아시스들에게로 주의를 돌렸고, 곧바로 그들과 관계를 맺었다."]], [["The act was over when he went in, and so he did not go straight to his brother’s box, but going up to the first row of stalls stopped at the footlights with Serpuhovskoy, who, standing with one knee raised and his heel on the footlights, caught sight of him in the distance and beckoned to him, smiling.", "그가 들어섰을 때는 막이 끝나 있었기에, 그는 곧장 형의 박스석으로 가지 않고 일층 앞줄로 올라가 세르푸호프스코이와 함께 각광 옆에 멈춰 섰는데, 한쪽 무릎을 세우고 발뒤꿈치를 각광에 올려놓고 서 있던 세르푸호프스코이는 멀리서 그를 발견하고 미소 지으며 손짓해 불렀다."]], [["Vronsky had not yet seen Anna.", "브론스키는 아직 안나를 보지 못했다."], ["He purposely avoided looking in her direction.", "그는 일부러 그녀가 있는 쪽을 보지 않으려 했다."], ["But he knew by the direction of people’s eyes where she was.", "그러나 사람들의 시선이 향하는 방향으로 그녀가 어디 있는지 알 수 있었다."], ["He looked round discreetly, but he was not seeking her; expecting the worst, his eyes sought for Alexey Alexandrovitch.", "그는 조심스레 주위를 둘러보았지만, 그녀를 찾는 것은 아니었다. 최악의 사태를 예상하며, 그의 눈은 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치를 찾고 있었다."], ["To his relief Alexey Alexandrovitch was not in the theater that evening.", "다행히도 그날 저녁 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 극장에 없었다."]], [["“How little of the military man there is left in you!”", "\"자네한테는 군인다운 데가 어쩜 이렇게도 안 남았나!\""], ["Serpuhovskoy was saying to him.", "세르푸호프스코이가 그에게 말했다."], ["“A diplomat, an artist, something of that sort, one would say.”", "\"외교관이라든가 예술가라든가, 뭐 그런 부류라고나 할까.\""]], [["“Yes, it was like going back home when I put on a black coat,”", "\"그래, 검은 연미복을 입으니 마치 집으로 돌아온 것 같더군.\""], ["answered Vronsky, smiling and slowly taking out his opera-glass.", "브론스키가 미소를 지으며 천천히 오페라글라스를 꺼내며 대답했다."]], [["“Well, I’ll own I envy you there.", "\"음, 솔직히 그 점에선 자네가 부럽네."], ["When I come back from abroad and put on this,”", "외국에서 돌아와 이걸 걸칠 때면,\""], ["he touched his epaulets, “I regret my freedom.”", "그는 자기 견장을 매만졌다, \"자유가 아쉬워진단 말이야.\""]], [["Serpuhovskoy had long given up all hope of Vronsky’s career, but he liked him as before, and was now particularly cordial to him.", "세르푸호프스코이는 브론스키의 출세에 대한 희망은 오래전에 모두 접었지만, 예전과 다름없이 그를 좋아했고, 지금은 유난히 그에게 다정했다."]], [["“What a pity you were not in time for the first act!”", "\"첫 막에 늦은 게 정말 안타깝군!\""]], [["Vronsky, listening with one ear, moved his opera-glass from the stalls and scanned the boxes.", "브론스키는 한쪽 귀로만 들으면서 오페라글라스를 일층 좌석에서 옮겨 박스석들을 훑어보았다."], ["Near a lady in a turban and a bald old man, who seemed to wave angrily in the moving opera-glass, Vronsky suddenly caught sight of Anna’s head, proud, strikingly beautiful, and smiling in the frame of lace.", "터번을 두른 한 부인과, 움직이는 오페라글라스 속에서 성난 듯 손을 내젓는 것처럼 보이는 대머리 노인 곁에서, 브론스키는 문득 안나의 머리를 발견했다. 도도하고, 눈부시게 아름다우며, 레이스의 테두리 안에서 미소 짓고 있는 머리였다."], ["She was in the fifth box, twenty paces from him.", "그녀는 그에게서 스무 걸음쯤 떨어진 다섯 번째 박스석에 있었다."], ["She was sitting in front, and slightly turning, was saying something to Yashvin.", "앞쪽에 앉아 몸을 약간 돌린 채 야시빈에게 무언가를 말하고 있었다."], ["The setting of her head on her handsome, broad shoulders, and the restrained excitement and brilliance of her eyes and her whole face reminded him of her just as he had seen her at the ball in Moscow.", "아름답고 넓은 어깨 위에 얹힌 머리의 자태며, 절제된 흥분이며, 그녀의 두 눈과 얼굴 전체의 광채는, 모스크바의 무도회에서 그가 그녀를 보았던 바로 그 모습을 떠올리게 했다."], ["But he felt utterly different towards her beauty now.", "그러나 지금 그는 그녀의 아름다움에 대해 전혀 다른 감정을 느꼈다."], ["In his feeling for her now there was no element of mystery, and so her beauty, though it attracted him even more intensely than before, gave him now a sense of injury.", "지금 그녀를 향한 그의 감정에는 신비로운 요소가 전혀 없었고, 그래서 그녀의 아름다움은, 전보다 한층 더 강렬하게 그를 끌어당기면서도, 이제는 그에게 모욕감을 안겨 주었다."], ["She was not looking in his direction, but Vronsky felt that she had seen him already.", "그녀는 그가 있는 쪽을 보고 있지 않았지만, 브론스키는 그녀가 이미 자기를 보았음을 느꼈다."]], [["When Vronsky turned the opera-glass again in that direction, he noticed that Princess Varvara was particularly red, and kept laughing unnaturally and looking round at the next box.", "브론스키가 다시 그쪽으로 오페라글라스를 돌렸을 때, 그는 바르바라 공작 부인이 유난히 얼굴이 붉어진 채 부자연스럽게 자꾸 웃으며 옆 박스석을 돌아보고 있다는 것을 알아챘다."], ["Anna, folding her fan and tapping it on the red velvet, was gazing away and did not see, and obviously did not wish to see, what was taking place in the next box.", "안나는 부채를 접어 붉은 벨벳을 톡톡 두드리며 먼 곳을 바라보고 있었고, 옆 박스석에서 벌어지고 있는 일을 보지 못했으며, 분명히 보고 싶어 하지도 않았다."], ["Yashvin’s face wore the expression which was common when he was losing at cards.", "야시빈의 얼굴에는 그가 카드 노름에서 잃을 때면 흔히 짓던 표정이 어려 있었다."], ["Scowling, he sucked the left end of his mustache further and further into his mouth, and cast sidelong glances at the next box.", "그는 인상을 쓰며 콧수염의 왼쪽 끝을 점점 더 입 안으로 빨아들이며 옆 박스석을 곁눈질했다."]], [["In that box on the left were the Kartasovs.", "왼쪽 그 박스석에는 카르타소프 부부가 있었다."], ["Vronsky knew them, and knew that Anna was acquainted with them.", "브론스키는 그들을 알고 있었고, 안나도 그들과 아는 사이라는 것을 알고 있었다."], ["Madame Kartasova, a thin little woman, was standing up in her box, and, her back turned upon Anna, she was putting on a mantle that her husband was holding for her.", "카르타소프 부인은 마르고 자그마한 여자였는데, 자기 박스석에서 일어선 채 안나에게 등을 돌리고서, 남편이 들어 주고 있는 망토를 걸치고 있었다."], ["Her face was pale and angry, and she was talking excitedly.", "그녀의 얼굴은 창백하고 성나 있었으며, 흥분해서 무언가 떠들고 있었다."], ["Kartasov, a fat, bald man, was continually looking round at Anna, while he attempted to soothe his wife.", "뚱뚱하고 대머리인 카르타소프는 아내를 달래려 애쓰면서 연신 안나 쪽을 돌아보았다."], ["When the wife had gone out, the husband lingered a long while, and tried to catch Anna’s eye, obviously anxious to bow to her.", "아내가 나가 버린 뒤에도 남편은 한참을 머뭇거리며 안나의 시선을 붙잡으려 했는데, 분명 그녀에게 인사를 하고 싶어 안달이 난 것이었다."], ["But Anna, with unmistakable intention, avoided noticing him, and talked to Yashvin, whose cropped head was bent down to her.", "그러나 안나는 명백한 의도를 가지고 그를 알아보지 않으려 했고, 짧게 깎은 머리를 그녀 쪽으로 숙이고 있는 야시빈과 이야기를 나누었다."], ["Kartasov went out without making his salutation, and the box was left empty.", "카르타소프는 인사도 못 하고 나가 버렸고, 박스석은 텅 비어 버렸다."]], [["Vronsky could not understand exactly what had passed between the Kartasovs and Anna, but he saw that something humiliating for Anna had happened.", "브론스키는 카르타소프 부부와 안나 사이에 정확히 무슨 일이 오갔는지 알 수 없었지만, 안나에게 무언가 모욕적인 일이 벌어졌음을 알아차렸다."], ["He knew this both from what he had seen, and most of all from the face of Anna, who, he could see, was taxing every nerve to carry through the part she had taken up.", "그는 자기가 본 것을 통해, 그리고 무엇보다도 안나의 얼굴을 통해 그것을 알았는데, 그가 보기에 그녀는 자기가 떠맡은 배역을 끝까지 해내기 위해 온 신경을 곤두세우고 있었다."], ["And in maintaining this attitude of external composure she was completely successful.", "그리고 겉으로의 침착함이라는 이 태도를 유지하는 데 그녀는 완벽하게 성공하고 있었다."], ["Anyone who did not know her and her circle, who had not heard all the utterances of the women expressive of commiseration, indignation, and amazement, that she should show herself in society, and show herself so conspicuously with her lace and her beauty, would have admired the serenity and loveliness of this woman without a suspicion that she was undergoing the sensations of a man in the stocks.", "그녀와 그녀의 세계를 모르는 사람, 그녀가 사교계에 모습을 드러내다니, 그것도 레이스와 아름다움으로 그렇게 눈에 띄게 모습을 드러내다니 하며 측은해하고 분개하고 놀라워하는 여자들의 온갖 말을 듣지 못한 사람이라면, 이 여자가 형틀에 매인 사람의 심정을 겪고 있으리라고는 추호도 의심하지 못한 채, 그저 이 여인의 평온함과 사랑스러움에 감탄했을 것이다."]], [["Knowing that something had happened, but not knowing precisely what, Vronsky felt a thrill of agonizing anxiety, and hoping to find out something, he went towards his brother’s box.", "무언가 일이 벌어졌다는 것은 알지만 그것이 정확히 무엇인지는 모른 채, 브론스키는 괴로운 불안의 전율을 느꼈고, 무언가 알아낼 수 있을까 하는 마음으로 형의 박스석을 향해 갔다."], ["Purposely choosing the way round furthest from Anna’s box, he jostled as he came out against the colonel of his old regiment talking to two acquaintances.", "일부러 안나의 박스석에서 가장 먼 길로 빙 돌아 나가다가, 그는 두 명의 지인과 이야기하고 있던 옛 연대의 대령과 부딪쳤다."], ["Vronsky heard the name of Madame Karenina, and noticed how the colonel hastened to address Vronsky loudly by name, with a meaning glance at his companions.", "브론스키는 카레니나 부인의 이름을 들었고, 대령이 자기 동행들에게 의미심장한 눈길을 던지며 서둘러 브론스키의 이름을 큰 소리로 부르는 것을 알아챘다."]], [["“Ah, Vronsky!", "\"아, 브론스키!"], ["When are you coming to the regiment?", "언제 연대에 한번 오겠나?"], ["We can’t let you off without a supper.", "저녁 한 끼 대접도 없이 자넬 그냥 보낼 순 없지."], ["You’re one of the old set,”", "자네는 우리 옛 동료니까 말이야.\""], ["said the colonel of his regiment.", "그의 연대 대령이 말했다."]], [["“I can’t stop, awfully sorry, another time,”", "\"지금은 안 됩니다, 정말 죄송합니다, 다음 기회에.\""], ["said Vronsky, and he ran upstairs towards his brother’s box.", "브론스키는 이렇게 말하고는 형의 박스석을 향해 위층으로 뛰어 올라갔다."]], [["The old countess, Vronsky’s mother, with her steel-gray curls, was in his brother’s box.", "브론스키의 어머니인 늙은 백작 부인이, 강철빛 잿빛 곱슬머리를 하고 형의 박스석에 와 있었다."], ["Varya with the young Princess Sorokina met him in the corridor.", "바랴는 젊은 소로키나 공작 영애와 함께 복도에서 그를 맞았다."]], [["Leaving the Princess Sorokina with her mother, Varya held out her hand to her brother-in-law, and began immediately to speak of what interested him.", "바랴는 소로키나 공작 영애를 그녀의 어머니에게 맡겨 두고, 시동생에게 손을 내밀더니, 곧바로 그의 관심사에 대해 말하기 시작했다."], ["She was more excited than he had ever seen her.", "그녀는 그가 지금껏 본 적 없을 만큼 흥분해 있었다."]], [["“I think it’s mean and hateful, and Madame Kartasova had no right to do it.", "\"내 생각엔 비열하고 가증스러운 짓이에요, 카르타소바 부인은 그럴 권리가 없었어요."], ["Madame Karenina....”", "카레니나 부인은…….\""], ["she began.", "그녀가 말문을 열었다."]], [["“But what is it?", "\"하지만 대체 무슨 일이오?"], ["I don’t know.”", "난 모르오.\""]], [["“What?", "\"뭐라고요?"], ["you’ve not heard?”", "못 들으셨어요?\""]], [["“You know I should be the last person to hear of it.”", "\"내가 그걸 제일 늦게 듣는 사람이라는 거, 누님도 알잖소.\""]], [["“There isn’t a more spiteful creature than that Madame Kartasova!”", "\"그 카르타소바 부인보다 더 심술궂은 인간은 없을 거예요!\""]], [["“But what did she do?”", "\"하지만 그 여자가 무슨 짓을 했단 말이오?\""]], [["“My husband told me....", "\"우리 그이가 말해 줬는데……."], ["She has insulted Madame Karenina.", "그 여자가 카레니나 부인을 모욕했어요."], ["Her husband began talking to her across the box, and Madame Kartasova made a scene.", "그 여자 남편이 박스석 너머로 카레니나 부인에게 말을 걸기 시작했는데, 카르타소바 부인이 한바탕 소동을 벌인 거죠."], ["She said something aloud, he says, something insulting, and went away.”", "그이 말로는, 그 여자가 무언가 모욕적인 말을 큰 소리로 내뱉고는 가 버렸다는 거예요.\""]], [["“Count, your maman is asking for you,”", "\"백작님, 어머님께서 찾으세요.\""], ["said the young Princess Sorokina, peeping out of the door of the box.", "젊은 소로키나 공작 영애가 박스석 문밖으로 얼굴을 내밀며 말했다."]], [["“I’ve been expecting you all the while,”", "\"내내 너를 기다리고 있었다.\""], ["said his mother, smiling sarcastically.", "그의 어머니가 빈정대듯 미소 지으며 말했다."], ["“You were nowhere to be seen.”", "\"어디에도 보이질 않더구나.\""]], [["Her son saw that she could not suppress a smile of delight.", "아들은 어머니가 기쁨에 찬 미소를 억누르지 못하고 있음을 알아챘다."]], [["“Good evening, maman.", "\"안녕하세요, 어머니."], ["I have come to you,”", "어머니께 오는 길이었어요.\""], ["he said coldly.", "그가 쌀쌀맞게 말했다."]], [["“Why aren’t you going to faire la cour à Madame Karenina?”", "\"왜 너는 카레니나 부인의 시중을 들러 가지 않는 게냐?\""], ["she went on, when Princess Sorokina had moved away.", "소로키나 공작 영애가 자리를 뜨자 어머니가 말을 이었다."], ["“Elle fait sensation.", "\"그 여자가 화제의 중심이더구나."], ["On oublie la Patti pour elle.”", "사람들이 그 여자 때문에 파티를 잊을 정도니 말이다.\""]], [["“Maman, I have asked you not to say anything to me of that,”", "\"어머니, 그 일에 대해서는 아무 말씀도 마시라고 부탁드렸잖아요.\""], ["he answered, scowling.", "그가 인상을 쓰며 대답했다."]], [["“I’m only saying what everyone’s saying.”", "\"난 그저 다들 하는 말을 할 뿐이다.\""]], [["Vronsky made no reply, and saying a few words to Princess Sorokina, he went away.", "브론스키는 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았고, 소로키나 공작 영애에게 몇 마디 건넨 뒤 자리를 떴다."], ["At the door he met his brother.", "문에서 그는 형과 마주쳤다."]], [["“Ah, Alexey!”", "\"아, 알렉세이!\""], ["said his brother.", "형이 말했다."], ["“How disgusting!", "\"정말 역겨운 일이야!"], ["Idiot of a woman, nothing else....", "멍청한 여편네, 그 이상도 이하도 아니지……."], ["I wanted to go straight to her.", "나는 곧장 그녀에게 가려던 참이었어."], ["Let’s go together.”", "같이 가세.\""]], [["Vronsky did not hear him.", "브론스키는 형의 말을 듣지 않았다."], ["With rapid steps he went downstairs; he felt that he must do something, but he did not know what.", "그는 빠른 걸음으로 아래층으로 내려갔다. 무언가 해야만 한다고 느꼈지만, 무엇을 해야 할지는 알 수 없었다."], ["Anger with her for having put herself and him in such a false position, together with pity for her suffering, filled his heart.", "그녀가 자기 자신과 그를 이토록 그릇된 처지에 몰아넣은 데 대한 분노가, 그녀의 고통에 대한 연민과 더불어 그의 가슴을 가득 채웠다."], ["He went down, and made straight for Anna’s box.", "그는 아래로 내려가 곧장 안나의 박스석으로 향했다."], ["At her box stood Stremov, talking to her.", "그녀의 박스석에는 스트레모프가 서서 그녀와 이야기하고 있었다."]], [["“There are no more tenors.", "\"이제 테너는 더 이상 없어요."], ["Le moule en est brisé!”", "그 거푸집은 깨져 버렸답니다!\""]], [["Vronsky bowed to her and stopped to greet Stremov.", "브론스키는 그녀에게 고개 숙여 인사하고 멈춰 서서 스트레모프와 인사를 나누었다."]], [["“You came in late, I think, and have missed the best song,”", "\"당신은 늦게 들어오셔서 가장 좋은 노래를 놓치신 것 같네요.\""], ["Anna said to Vronsky, glancing ironically, he thought, at him.", "안나가 브론스키에게 말하며, 그가 보기엔 빈정대듯 그를 흘끗 쳐다보았다."]], [["“I am a poor judge of music,”", "\"저는 음악을 잘 모릅니다.\""], ["he said, looking sternly at her.", "그가 그녀를 매섭게 바라보며 말했다."]], [["“Like Prince Yashvin,”", "\"야시빈 공작처럼요.\""], ["she said smiling, “who considers that Patti sings too loud.”", "그녀가 미소 지으며 말했다, \"파티가 너무 큰 소리로 노래한다고 여기는 사람 말이에요.\""]], [["“Thank you,”", "\"고마워요.\""], ["she said, her little hand in its long glove taking the playbill Vronsky picked up, and suddenly at that instant her lovely face quivered.", "그녀가 긴 장갑을 낀 작은 손으로, 브론스키가 주워 든 프로그램을 받으며 말했는데, 바로 그 순간 갑자기 그녀의 사랑스러운 얼굴이 떨렸다."], ["She got up and went into the interior of the box.", "그녀는 일어나 박스석 안쪽으로 들어갔다."]], [["Noticing in the next act that her box was empty, Vronsky, rousing indignant “hushes”", "다음 막에서 그녀의 박스석이 텅 빈 것을 알아챈 브론스키는, 조용한 관객들 사이에서 분개한 \"쉿\""], ["in the silent audience, went out in the middle of a solo and drove home.", "소리를 일으키며 독창이 한창인 와중에 밖으로 나가 집으로 마차를 몰았다."]], [["Anna was already at home.", "안나는 이미 집에 와 있었다."], ["When Vronsky went up to her, she was in the same dress as she had worn at the theater.", "브론스키가 그녀에게 다가갔을 때, 그녀는 극장에서 입었던 그대로의 옷차림이었다."], ["She was sitting in the first armchair against the wall, looking straight before her.", "그녀는 벽에 붙은 첫 번째 안락의자에 앉아 똑바로 앞을 바라보고 있었다."], ["She looked at him, and at once resumed her former position.", "그녀는 그를 쳐다보더니, 곧바로 아까의 자세로 되돌아갔다."]], [["“Anna,”", "\"안나.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“You, you are to blame for everything!”", "\"당신, 당신이 모든 일의 원흉이에요!\""], ["she cried, with tears of despair and hatred in her voice, getting up.", "그녀가 절망과 증오의 눈물을 목소리에 담아 외치며 일어섰다."]], [["“I begged, I implored you not to go, I knew it would be unpleasant....”", "\"내가 가지 말라고 빌고 또 애원했잖소, 불쾌한 일이 생길 줄 알고 있었으니까…….\""]], [["“Unpleasant!”", "\"불쾌하다고요!\""], ["she cried—“hideous!", "그녀가 외쳤다—\"끔찍했어요!"], ["As long as I live I shall never forget it.", "살아 있는 한 결코 잊지 못할 거예요."], ["She said it was a disgrace to sit beside me.”", "그 여자가 내 옆에 앉는 건 수치라고 했단 말이에요.\""]], [["“A silly woman’s chatter,”", "\"어리석은 여자의 수다일 뿐이오.\""], ["he said: “but why risk it, why provoke?...”", "그가 말했다. \"하지만 왜 위험을 무릅쓰고, 왜 도발을 한단 말이오?……\""]], [["“I hate your calm.", "\"당신의 그 침착함이 싫어요."], ["You ought not to have brought me to this.", "당신은 나를 이런 지경으로 몰아넣지 말았어야 했어요."], ["If you had loved me....”", "당신이 나를 사랑했더라면…….\""]], [["“Anna!", "\"안나!"], ["How does the question of my love come in?”", "내 사랑이 이 일과 무슨 상관이란 말이오?\""]], [["“Oh, if you loved me, as I love, if you were tortured as I am!...”", "\"오, 당신이 나처럼 사랑했더라면, 당신이 나처럼 괴로워했더라면!…….\""], ["she said, looking at him with an expression of terror.", "그녀가 공포의 표정으로 그를 바라보며 말했다."]], [["He was sorry for her, and angry notwithstanding.", "그는 그녀가 가여웠지만, 그럼에도 화가 났다."], ["He assured her of his love because he saw that this was the only means of soothing her, and he did not reproach her in words, but in his heart he reproached her.", "그는 그녀에게 자기 사랑을 확언했는데, 그것만이 그녀를 달래는 유일한 방법임을 알았기 때문이다. 그리고 말로는 그녀를 책망하지 않았지만, 마음속으로는 그녀를 책망했다."]], [["And the asseverations of his love, which seemed to him so vulgar that he was ashamed to utter them, she drank in eagerly, and gradually became calmer.", "그리고 그가 입에 담기 부끄러울 만큼 천박하게 느껴지던 그 사랑의 맹세를, 그녀는 게걸스럽게 들이마셨고, 차츰 마음을 가라앉혔다."], ["The next day, completely reconciled, they left for the country.", "이튿날 완전히 화해한 두 사람은 시골로 떠났다."]], [["# PART SIX Chapter 1", "# PART SIX Chapter 1"]], [["Darya Alexandrovna spent the summer with her children at Pokrovskoe, at her sister Kitty Levin’s.", "다리야 알렉산드로브나는 아이들과 함께 여름을 포크로프스코예에서, 여동생인 키티 레빈의 집에서 보냈다."], ["The house on her own estate was quite in ruins, and Levin and his wife had persuaded her to spend the summer with them.", "자기 영지에 있는 집은 거의 폐허나 다름없었고, 그래서 레빈 부부가 그녀에게 여름을 자기들과 함께 보내자고 권했던 것이다."], ["Stepan Arkadyevitch greatly approved of the arrangement.", "스테판 아르카지치는 이런 방식을 크게 반겼다."], ["He said he was very sorry his official duties prevented him from spending the summer in the country with his family, which would have been the greatest happiness for him; and remaining in Moscow, he came down to the country from time to time for a day or two.", "그는 공무 때문에 가족과 함께 시골에서 여름을 보내지 못하는 것이 무척 안타깝다고, 그렇게 할 수만 있다면 자기에게는 더없는 행복일 거라고 말했다. 그러고는 모스크바에 머물면서도 가끔씩 하루 이틀 시골로 내려오곤 했다."], ["Besides the Oblonskys, with all their children and their governess, the old princess too came to stay that summer with the Levins, as she considered it her duty to watch over her inexperienced daughter in her interesting condition.", "오블론스키 집안 사람들 말고도, 아이들과 그들의 가정교사 외에 노공작 부인까지 그해 여름 레빈 부부의 집에 머무르러 왔는데, 경험이 부족한 딸이 흥미로운 상태에 있는 만큼 곁에서 돌보아 주는 것이 자기 의무라고 여겼기 때문이다."], ["Moreover, Varenka, Kitty’s friend abroad, kept her promise to come to Kitty when she was married, and stayed with her friend.", "게다가 외국에서 키티와 친구가 된 바렌카도, 키티가 결혼하면 찾아오겠다던 약속을 지켜 친구 곁에 머물렀다."], ["All of these were friends or relations of Levin’s wife.", "이들은 모두 레빈의 아내 쪽 친구이거나 친척이었다."], ["And though he liked them all, he rather regretted his own Levin world and ways, which was smothered by this influx of the “Shtcherbatsky element,” as he called it to himself.", "그리고 그는 그들 모두를 좋아하기는 했지만, 그가 속으로 이름 붙인 이른바 \"셰르바츠키 분자\"의 밀려듦에 짓눌려 버린 자기만의 레빈 세계와 레빈식 방식이 다소 아쉬웠다."], ["Of his own relations there stayed with him only Sergey Ivanovitch, but he too was a man of the Koznishev and not the Levin stamp, so that the Levin spirit was utterly obliterated.", "그의 친척 중에서는 세르게이 이바노비치만 함께 머물렀는데, 그 역시 레빈 기질이 아니라 코즈니셰프 기질의 인물이었던지라, 레빈의 정신은 완전히 지워지고 말았다."]], [["In the Levins’ house, so long deserted, there were now so many people that almost all the rooms were occupied, and almost every day it happened that the old princess, sitting down to table, counted them all over, and put the thirteenth grandson or granddaughter at a separate table.", "* 흥미로운 상태: 임신을 에둘러 이르는 당시의 완곡 표현. 오랫동안 비어 있던 레빈 부부의 집에는 이제 사람이 너무 많아 거의 모든 방이 들어찼고, 노공작 부인은 거의 매일같이 식탁에 앉으면 사람 수를 모두 헤아려 보고는, 열세 번째에 해당하는 손자나 손녀를 따로 떨어진 식탁에 앉히곤 했다."], ["And Kitty, with her careful housekeeping, had no little trouble to get all the chickens, turkeys, and geese, of which so many were needed to satisfy the summer appetites of the visitors and children.", "그리고 살림을 꼼꼼히 챙기던 키티는, 손님들과 아이들의 여름 식욕을 채우려면 그토록 많이 필요한 닭과 칠면조와 거위를 모두 마련하느라 적잖이 애를 먹었다."]], [["The whole family were sitting at dinner.", "온 식구가 식탁에 둘러앉아 식사를 하고 있었다."], ["Dolly’s children, with their governess and Varenka, were making plans for going to look for mushrooms.", "돌리의 아이들은 가정교사와 바렌카와 함께 버섯을 따러 갈 계획을 세우고 있었다."], ["Sergey Ivanovitch, who was looked up to by all the party for his intellect and learning, with a respect that almost amounted to awe, surprised everyone by joining in the conversation about mushrooms.", "그 지성과 학식 덕분에 거의 경외에 가까운 존경을 한 몸에 받던 세르게이 이바노비치가 버섯에 관한 이야기에 끼어들어 모두를 놀라게 했다."]], [["“Take me with you.", "\"나도 데려가 주시오."], ["I am very fond of picking mushrooms,”", "나는 버섯 따는 걸 무척 좋아한답니다.\""], ["he said, looking at Varenka; “I think it’s a very nice occupation.”", "그는 바렌카를 바라보며 말했다. \"참 좋은 소일거리라고 생각해요.\""]], [["“Oh, we shall be delighted,”", "\"어머, 저희야 기쁘죠.\""], ["answered Varenka, coloring a little.", "바렌카가 살짝 얼굴을 붉히며 대답했다."], ["Kitty exchanged meaningful glances with Dolly.", "키티는 돌리와 의미심장한 눈길을 주고받았다."], ["The proposal of the learned and intellectual Sergey Ivanovitch to go looking for mushrooms with Varenka confirmed certain theories of Kitty’s with which her mind had been very busy of late.", "학식 있고 지적인 세르게이 이바노비치가 바렌카와 함께 버섯을 따러 가겠다고 한 그 제안은, 요즘 들어 키티의 머릿속을 분주하게 채우던 어떤 추측을 확인시켜 주었다."], ["She made haste to address some remark to her mother, so that her look should not be noticed.", "그녀는 자기 눈길이 눈에 띄지 않도록 서둘러 어머니에게 무언가 말을 건넸다."], ["After dinner Sergey Ivanovitch sat with his cup of coffee at the drawing-room window, and while he took part in a conversation he had begun with his brother, he watched the door through which the children would start on the mushroom-picking expedition.", "식사가 끝난 뒤 세르게이 이바노비치는 응접실 창가에 앉아 커피를 마시면서, 동생과 시작한 대화에 끼어 있으면서도, 아이들이 버섯 따기 원정을 떠날 문 쪽을 지켜보고 있었다."], ["Levin was sitting in the window near his brother.", "레빈은 형 가까이 창가에 앉아 있었다."]], [["Kitty stood beside her husband, evidently awaiting the end of a conversation that had no interest for her, in order to tell him something.", "키티는 남편 곁에 서서, 그에게 무언가 할 말이 있는 듯, 자기에게는 아무 흥미도 없는 대화가 끝나기를 분명히 기다리고 있었다."]], [["“You have changed in many respects since your marriage, and for the better,”", "\"넌 결혼한 뒤로 여러 면에서 변했어, 그것도 더 나은 쪽으로 말이야.\""], ["said Sergey Ivanovitch, smiling to Kitty, and obviously little interested in the conversation, “but you have remained true to your passion for defending the most paradoxical theories.”", "세르게이 이바노비치가 키티에게 미소를 지으며, 대화에는 별 흥미가 없는 기색으로 말했다. \"하지만 더없이 역설적인 이론들을 옹호하려는 그 열정만큼은 여전하구나.\""]], [["“Katya, it’s not good for you to stand,”", "\"카탸, 서 있는 건 네 몸에 좋지 않아.\""], ["her husband said to her, putting a chair for her and looking significantly at her.", "남편이 그녀에게 의자를 가져다 놓아 주며, 의미심장한 눈길로 그녀를 바라보았다."]], [["“Oh, and there’s no time either,”", "\"아, 그리고 시간도 없겠는데.\""], ["added Sergey Ivanovitch, seeing the children running out.", "아이들이 뛰쳐나오는 것을 보고 세르게이 이바노비치가 덧붙였다."]], [["At the head of them all Tanya galloped sideways, in her tightly-drawn stockings, and waving a basket and Sergey Ivanovitch’s hat, she ran straight up to him.", "그 모두의 맨 앞에서 타냐가 팽팽하게 당겨 신은 양말 차림으로 비스듬히 달려 나오더니, 바구니와 세르게이 이바노비치의 모자를 흔들며 곧장 그에게로 달려왔다."]], [["Boldly running up to Sergey Ivanovitch with shining eyes, so like her father’s fine eyes, she handed him his hat and made as though she would put it on for him, softening her freedom by a shy and friendly smile.", "아버지의 고운 눈을 꼭 빼닮은 반짝이는 눈으로 세르게이 이바노비치에게 거침없이 달려온 그 아이는, 그에게 모자를 건네고는 직접 씌워 주려는 시늉을 하면서, 그 스스럼없는 행동을 수줍고 다정한 미소로 누그러뜨렸다."]], [["“Varenka’s waiting,”", "\"바렌카가 기다리고 있어요.\""], ["she said, carefully putting his hat on, seeing from Sergey Ivanovitch’s smile that she might do so.", "그 아이는 세르게이 이바노비치의 미소에서 그래도 된다는 것을 알아채고는, 그의 모자를 조심스럽게 씌워 주며 말했다."]], [["Varenka was standing at the door, dressed in a yellow print gown, with a white kerchief on her head.", "바렌카는 노란 무명 드레스를 입고 머리에 흰 두건을 두른 채 문가에 서 있었다."]], [["“I’m coming, I’m coming, Varvara Andreevna,”", "\"가요, 가, 바르바라 안드레예브나.\""], ["said Sergey Ivanovitch, finishing his cup of coffee, and putting into their separate pockets his handkerchief and cigar-case.", "세르게이 이바노비치가 커피를 마저 마시고는, 손수건과 담배 케이스를 각각 다른 주머니에 넣으며 말했다."]], [["“And how sweet my Varenka is!", "\"우리 바렌카, 정말 사랑스럽지 않아요?"], ["eh?”", "응?\""], ["said Kitty to her husband, as soon as Sergey Ivanovitch rose.", "세르게이 이바노비치가 일어서자마자 키티가 남편에게 말했다."], ["She spoke so that Sergey Ivanovitch could hear, and it was clear that she meant him to do so.", "그녀는 세르게이 이바노비치가 들을 수 있게끔 말했고, 그가 듣기를 바라는 것이 분명했다."], ["“And how good-looking she is—such a refined beauty!", "\"게다가 얼마나 예뻐요. 어찌나 기품 있는 미인인지!"], ["Varenka!”", "바렌카!\""], ["Kitty shouted.", "키티가 큰 소리로 불렀다."], ["“Shall you be in the mill copse?", "\"물방앗간 숲에 있을 거지요?"], ["We’ll come out to you.”", "우리도 그리로 갈게요.\""]], [["“You certainly forget your condition, Kitty,”", "\"넌 네 몸 상태를 자꾸 잊는구나, 키티.\""], ["said the old princess, hurriedly coming out at the door.", "노공작 부인이 황급히 문가로 나오며 말했다."], ["“You mustn’t shout like that.”", "\"그렇게 소리치면 안 돼.\""]], [["Varenka, hearing Kitty’s voice and her mother’s reprimand, went with light, rapid steps up to Kitty.", "키티의 목소리와 어머니의 꾸지람을 들은 바렌카는 가볍고 빠른 걸음으로 키티에게 다가왔다."], ["The rapidity of her movement, her flushed and eager face, everything betrayed that something out of the common was going on in her.", "그 동작의 빠름이며, 상기되어 들뜬 얼굴이며, 모든 것이 그녀 안에서 무언가 예사롭지 않은 일이 일어나고 있음을 드러내고 있었다."], ["Kitty knew what this was, and had been watching her intently.", "키티는 그것이 무엇인지 알았고, 그녀를 유심히 지켜보고 있던 참이었다."], ["She called Varenka at that moment merely in order mentally to give her a blessing for the important event which, as Kitty fancied, was bound to come to pass that day after dinner in the wood.", "키티가 마침 그 순간 바렌카를 부른 것은, 오로지 마음속으로, 키티가 짐작하기로는 그날 식사 뒤 숲에서 틀림없이 일어나고야 말 그 중대한 일을 위해 그녀에게 축복을 빌어 주기 위해서였다."]], [["“Varenka, I should be very happy if a certain something were to happen,”", "\"바렌카, 어떤 일이 일어난다면 난 정말 기쁠 거예요.\""], ["she whispered as she kissed her.", "그녀가 바렌카에게 입을 맞추며 속삭였다."]], [["“And are you coming with us?”", "\"그런데 당신도 우리랑 같이 가시나요?\""], ["Varenka said to Levin in confusion, pretending not to have heard what had been said.", "바렌카가 방금 한 말을 듣지 못한 척하며, 당황한 기색으로 레빈에게 물었다."]], [["“I am coming, but only as far as the threshing-floor, and there I shall stop.”", "\"가요, 다만 탈곡장까지만, 거기서 나는 멈출 거예요.\""]], [["“Why, what do you want there?”", "\"아니, 거기서 뭘 하시려고요?\""], ["said Kitty.", "키티가 물었다."]], [["“I must go to have a look at the new wagons, and to check the invoice,”", "\"새 짐마차들을 좀 살펴보고 송장을 확인해야 해요.\" 레빈이 말했다."], ["said Levin; “and where will you be?”", "\"그런데 당신은 어디 있을 거요?\""]], [["“On the terrace.”", "\"테라스에요.\""]], [["# Chapter 2", "# Chapter 2"]], [["On the terrace were assembled all the ladies of the party.", "테라스에는 일행 가운데 부인들이 모두 모여 있었다."], ["They always liked sitting there after dinner, and that day they had work to do there too.", "그들은 식사 후에 늘 거기에 앉아 있기를 좋아했고, 그날은 거기서 할 일도 있었다."], ["Besides the sewing and knitting of baby clothes, with which all of them were busy, that afternoon jam was being made on the terrace by a method new to Agafea Mihalovna, without the addition of water.", "다들 매달려 있던 갓난아기 옷 바느질과 뜨개질 말고도, 그날 오후에는 아가피야 미하일로브나에게는 새로운 방식으로, 즉 물을 넣지 않고 잼을 만드는 일이 테라스에서 벌어지고 있었다."], ["Kitty had introduced this new method, which had been in use in her home.", "키티가 친정에서 쓰던 이 새로운 방식을 들여왔던 것이다."], ["Agafea Mihalovna, to whom the task of jam-making had always been intrusted, considering that what had been done in the Levin household could not be amiss, had nevertheless put water with the strawberries, maintaining that the jam could not be made without it.", "잼 만드는 일을 늘 맡아 오던 아가피야 미하일로브나는 레빈 집안에서 해 오던 방식이 잘못일 리 없다고 여기면서도, 딸기에 물을 넣고는 물 없이는 잼을 만들 수 없다고 우겼다."], ["She had been caught in the act, and was now making jam before everyone, and it was to be proved to her conclusively that jam could be very well made without water.", "그러다 현장에서 들켜, 지금은 모두가 보는 앞에서 잼을 만들고 있었고, 물 없이도 잼을 아주 잘 만들 수 있다는 것을 그녀에게 확실히 증명해 보일 참이었다."]], [["Agafea Mihalovna, her face heated and angry, her hair untidy, and her thin arms bare to the elbows, was turning the preserving-pan over the charcoal stove, looking darkly at the raspberries and devoutly hoping they would stick and not cook properly.", "아가피야 미하일로브나는 얼굴이 벌겋게 달아오르고 화가 나서, 머리는 헝클어지고, 가는 팔은 팔꿈치까지 드러낸 채 숯 화로 위에서 졸이는 냄비를 휘젓고 있었는데, 라즈베리를 어둡게 바라보면서 그것이 눌어붙어 제대로 익지 않기를 간절히 바랐다."], ["The princess, conscious that Agafea Mihalovna’s wrath must be chiefly directed against her, as the person responsible for the raspberry jam-making, tried to appear to be absorbed in other things and not interested in the jam, talked of other matters, but cast stealthy glances in the direction of the stove.", "공작 부인은 아가피야 미하일로브나의 분노가 라즈베리 잼을 만들게 한 책임자인 자신을 향한 것임을 알아채고는, 다른 일에 정신이 팔려 잼 따위에는 관심이 없는 척하려고 다른 이야기를 하면서도 슬그머니 화로 쪽으로 눈길을 던졌다."]], [["“I always buy my maids’ dresses myself, of some cheap material,”", "\"나는 늘 하녀들 옷을 직접 사 줘요, 값싼 천으로 말이지.\""], ["the princess said, continuing the previous conversation.", "공작 부인이 앞서 하던 대화를 이어 가며 말했다."], ["“Isn’t it time to skim it, my dear?”", "\"이제 거품을 걷어 낼 때가 되지 않았나, 자네?\""], ["she added, addressing Agafea Mihalovna.", "그녀가 아가피야 미하일로브나에게 말을 건네며 덧붙였다."], ["“There’s not the slightest need for you to do it, and it’s hot for you,”", "\"네가 할 필요는 조금도 없단다, 너는 더위를 타니까.\""], ["she said, stopping Kitty.", "그녀가 키티를 막으며 말했다."]], [["“I’ll do it,”", "\"제가 할게요.\""], ["said Dolly, and getting up, she carefully passed the spoon over the frothing sugar, and from time to time shook off the clinging jam from the spoon by knocking it on a plate that was covered with yellow-red scum and blood-colored syrup.", "돌리가 말하고는 일어나, 거품이 이는 설탕물 위로 숟가락을 조심스레 휘젓더니, 이따금 노르스름하고 불그스름한 찌꺼기와 핏빛 시럽으로 덮인 접시에 숟가락을 두드려 들러붙은 잼을 떨어냈다."], ["“How they’ll enjoy this at tea-time!”", "'차 마실 때 이걸 얼마나 좋아할까!'"], ["she thought of her children, remembering how she herself as a child had wondered how it was the grown-up people did not eat what was best of all—the scum of the jam.", "그녀는 아이들을 떠올리며, 자기도 어릴 적에 어째서 어른들은 잼에서 제일 맛있는 것—잼 거품—을 먹지 않을까 궁금해했던 일을 기억해 냈다."]], [["“Stiva says it’s much better to give money.”", "\"스티바는 돈을 주는 편이 훨씬 낫다고 해요.\""], ["Dolly took up meanwhile the weighty subject under discussion, what presents should be made to servants.", "그러는 사이 돌리는 화제로 오른 무거운 주제, 즉 하인들에게 어떤 선물을 해야 하느냐 하는 문제를 꺼냈다."], ["“But....”", "\"하지만…….\""]], [["“Money’s out of the question!”", "\"돈은 말도 안 되지!\""], ["the princess and Kitty exclaimed with one voice.", "공작 부인과 키티가 한목소리로 외쳤다."], ["“They appreciate a present....”", "\"그들은 선물을 고마워한단다…….\""]], [["“Well, last year, for instance, I bought our Matrona Semyenovna, not a poplin, but something of that sort,”", "\"글쎄, 작년만 해도 나는 우리 마트료나 세묘노브나에게 포플린은 아니지만 그런 종류의 천을 사 줬지.\""], ["said the princess.", "공작 부인이 말했다."]], [["“I remember she was wearing it on your nameday.”", "\"어머니 명명일에 그걸 입고 있던 게 기억나요.\""]], [["“A charming pattern—so simple and refined,—I should have liked it myself, if she hadn’t had it.", "\"무늬가 아주 예뻤어—소박하면서도 고상했지. 그 애가 갖지 않았더라면 내가 갖고 싶었을 정도였단다."], ["Something like Varenka’s.", "바렌카가 입던 것 비슷한 거였어."], ["So pretty and inexpensive.”", "그렇게 예쁘면서도 값이 싸지.\""]], [["“Well, now I think it’s done,”", "\"자, 이제 다 된 것 같아요.\""], ["said Dolly, dropping the syrup from the spoon.", "돌리가 숟가락에서 시럽을 떨어뜨리며 말했다."]], [["“When it sets as it drops, it’s ready.", "\"떨어뜨릴 때 엉기면 다 된 거야."], ["Cook it a little longer, Agafea Mihalovna.”", "조금만 더 졸이게, 아가피야 미하일로브나.\""]], [["“The flies!”", "\"파리들이 꼬여요!\""], ["said Agafea Mihalovna angrily.", "아가피야 미하일로브나가 화난 목소리로 말했다."], ["“It’ll be just the same,”", "\"어차피 마찬가지일 거예요.\""], ["she added.", "그녀가 덧붙였다."]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["how sweet it is! don’t frighten it!”", "어쩜 저렇게 귀여울까!"], ["Kitty said suddenly, looking at a sparrow that had settled on the step and was pecking at the center of a raspberry.", "놀라게 하지 마세요!\" 키티가 별안간 계단에 내려앉아 라즈베리 한가운데를 쪼고 있는 참새를 바라보며 말했다."]], [["“Yes, but you keep a little further from the stove,”", "\"그래, 하지만 너는 화로에서 좀 더 떨어져 있으렴.\""], ["said her mother.", "어머니가 말했다."]], [["“À propos de Varenka,”", "\"À propos de Varenka.\""], ["said Kitty, speaking in French, as they had been doing all the while, so that Agafea Mihalovna should not understand them, “you know, mamma, I somehow expect things to be settled today.", "키티는 아가피야 미하일로브나가 알아듣지 못하도록 그들이 줄곧 그래 왔듯 프랑스어로 말했다. \"있잖아요, 엄마, 저는 어쩐지 오늘 일이 마무리될 것 같아요."], ["You know what I mean.", "무슨 말인지 아시죠. 그렇게 되면 정말 멋질 텐데!\""], ["How splendid it would be!”", "* À propos de Varenka: 프랑스어로 '바렌카 이야기가 나왔으니 말인데'."]], [["“But what a famous matchmaker she is!”", "\"하지만 그 애야말로 대단한 중매쟁이지!\""], ["said Dolly.", "돌리가 말했다."], ["“How carefully and cleverly she throws them together!...”", "\"얼마나 조심스럽고 영리하게 둘을 엮어 주는지!……\""]], [["“No; tell me, mamma, what do you think?”", "\"아뇨, 말씀해 보세요, 엄마, 어떻게 생각하세요?\""]], [["“Why, what is one to think? He”", "\"글쎄, 무슨 생각을 하라는 거니?"], ["(he meant Sergey Ivanovitch) “might at any time have been a match for anyone in Russia; now, of course, he’s not quite a young man, still I know ever so many girls would be glad to marry him even now....", "그 사람은\"(그 사람이란 세르게이 이바노비치를 뜻했다) \"러시아에서 누구하고든 언제라도 짝이 될 수 있었지. 물론 지금은 그리 젊은 나이는 아니다만, 그래도 지금이라도 그 사람과 기꺼이 결혼하려는 처녀가 얼마나 많은지 나는 알아……"], ["She’s a very nice girl, but he might....”", "그 애는 아주 좋은 처녀지만, 그 사람은…….\""]], [["“Oh, no, mamma, do understand why, for him and for her too, nothing better could be imagined.", "\"오, 아뇨, 엄마, 왜 그 사람한테도 그 애한테도 이보다 더 좋은 일은 상상할 수 없는지 좀 이해해 주세요."], ["In the first place, she’s charming!”", "우선, 그 애는 사랑스럽잖아요!\""], ["said Kitty, crooking one of her fingers.", "키티가 손가락 하나를 꼽으며 말했다."]], [["“He thinks her very attractive, that’s certain,”", "\"그 사람이 그 애를 아주 매력적으로 여기는 건 확실해.\""], ["assented Dolly.", "돌리가 맞장구쳤다."]], [["“Then he occupies such a position in society that he has no need to look for either fortune or position in his wife.", "\"게다가 그 사람은 사교계에서 그런 지위에 있으니까 아내한테서 재산이나 지위를 바랄 필요가 없어요."], ["All he needs is a good, sweet wife—a restful one.”", "그 사람한테 필요한 건 그저 착하고 다정한 아내—마음을 편하게 해 주는 아내뿐이에요.\""]], [["“Well, with her he would certainly be restful,”", "\"글쎄, 그 애라면 그 사람을 분명 편하게 해 주겠지.\""], ["Dolly assented.", "돌리가 동의했다."]], [["“Thirdly, that she should love him.", "\"셋째로, 그 애가 그 사람을 사랑해야 한다는 거예요."], ["And so it is ...", "그리고 정말 그래요……"], ["that is, it would be so splendid!...", "그러니까, 정말 멋진 일이 될 거예요!……"], ["I look forward to seeing them coming out of the forest—and everything settled.", "두 사람이 숲에서 나오는 걸 보면—그리고 모든 게 결정되어 있겠죠."], ["I shall see at once by their eyes.", "저는 두 사람 눈을 보면 단번에 알 거예요."], ["I should be so delighted!", "정말 기쁠 거예요!"], ["What do you think, Dolly?”", "어떻게 생각해요, 돌리?\""]], [["“But don’t excite yourself.", "\"하지만 너무 들뜨지는 말렴."], ["It’s not at all the thing for you to be excited,”", "네가 흥분하는 건 전혀 좋지 않으니까.\""], ["said her mother.", "어머니가 말했다."]], [["“Oh, I’m not excited, mamma.", "\"오, 저 안 흥분했어요, 엄마."], ["I fancy he will make her an offer today.”", "그 사람이 오늘 그 애한테 청혼할 것 같아요.\""]], [["“Ah, that’s so strange, how and when a man makes an offer!...", "\"아, 참 이상한 일이지, 남자가 어떻게, 언제 청혼하는지 말이야!……"], ["There is a sort of barrier, and all at once it’s broken down,”", "무슨 장벽 같은 게 있다가, 별안간 그게 무너져 버리거든.\""], ["said Dolly, smiling pensively and recalling her past with Stepan Arkadyevitch.", "돌리가 스테판 아르카지치와의 지난날을 떠올리며 생각에 잠긴 듯 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["“Mamma, how did papa make you an offer?”", "\"엄마, 아빠는 엄마한테 어떻게 청혼했어요?\""], ["Kitty asked suddenly.", "키티가 불쑥 물었다."]], [["“There was nothing out of the way, it was very simple,”", "\"별다른 일은 없었단다, 아주 단순했지.\""], ["answered the princess, but her face beamed all over at the recollection.", "공작 부인이 대답했지만, 그 회상에 그녀의 얼굴은 온통 환하게 빛났다."]], [["“Oh, but how was it?", "\"오, 그래도 어땠는데요?"], ["You loved him, anyway, before you were allowed to speak?”", "어쨌든 말을 꺼내도 좋다는 허락이 떨어지기 전에 이미 아빠를 사랑했죠?\""]], [["Kitty felt a peculiar pleasure in being able now to talk to her mother on equal terms about those questions of such paramount interest in a woman’s life.", "키티는 이제 여자의 인생에서 그토록 더없이 중요한 그런 문제들을 어머니와 동등한 입장에서 이야기할 수 있다는 데서 묘한 즐거움을 느꼈다."]], [["“Of course I did; he had come to stay with us in the country.”", "\"물론 그랬지. 그이가 시골 우리 집에 와서 묵었거든.\""]], [["“But how was it settled between you, mamma?”", "\"그런데 두 분 사이는 어떻게 정해졌어요, 엄마?\""]], [["“You imagine, I dare say, that you invented something quite new?", "\"아마 네가 뭔가 아주 새로운 걸 만들어 냈다고 생각하는 모양이지?"], ["It’s always just the same: it was settled by the eyes, by smiles....”", "늘 똑같단다. 눈으로, 미소로 정해지는 거야…….\""]], [["“How nicely you said that, mamma!", "\"엄마, 정말 멋지게 말씀하시네요!"], ["It’s just by the eyes, by smiles that it’s done,”", "바로 눈으로, 미소로 이루어지는 거죠.\""], ["Dolly assented.", "돌리가 맞장구쳤다."]], [["“But what words did he say?”", "\"그런데 아빠가 뭐라고 말했어요?\""]], [["“What did Kostya say to you?”", "\"코스챠는 너한테 뭐라고 했니?\""]], [["“He wrote it in chalk.", "\"그이는 분필로 썼어요."], ["It was wonderful....", "정말 멋졌죠……"], ["How long ago it seems!”", "얼마나 오래전 일인지!\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["And the three women all fell to musing on the same thing.", "그리고 세 여자 모두 똑같은 일을 떠올리며 생각에 잠겼다."], ["Kitty was the first to break the silence.", "침묵을 가장 먼저 깬 것은 키티였다."], ["She remembered all that last winter before her marriage, and her passion for Vronsky.", "그녀는 결혼 전 그 마지막 겨울과 브론스키에 대한 자신의 열정을 모두 기억해 냈다."]], [["“There’s one thing ...", "\"한 가지가 있어요……"], ["that old love affair of Varenka’s,”", "바렌카의 그 옛 연애 말이에요.\""], ["she said, a natural chain of ideas bringing her to this point.", "자연스러운 생각의 흐름이 그녀를 이 지점으로 데려와 그녀가 말했다."], ["“I should have liked to say something to Sergey Ivanovitch, to prepare him.", "\"세르게이 이바노비치한테 뭔가 말해 두고 싶었어요, 마음의 준비를 시켜 주려고요."], ["They’re all—all men, I mean,”", "다들—그러니까 남자들은 다.\""], ["she added, “awfully jealous over our past.”", "그녀가 덧붙였다. \"우리 과거에 대해 끔찍하게 질투하잖아요.\""]], [["“Not all,” said Dolly.", "\"다 그런 건 아니야.\""], ["“You judge by your own husband.", "돌리가 말했다. \"너는 네 남편을 기준으로 판단하는구나."], ["It makes him miserable even now to remember Vronsky.", "그이는 지금도 브론스키를 떠올리면 괴로워하잖니."], ["Eh?", "응?"], ["that’s true, isn’t it?”", "정말 그렇지 않아?\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["Kitty answered, a pensive smile in her eyes.", "키티가 눈에 생각에 잠긴 미소를 띠며 대답했다."]], [["“But I really don’t know,”", "\"하지만 나는 정말 모르겠구나.\""], ["the mother put in in defense of her motherly care of her daughter, “what there was in your past that could worry him?", "어머니가 딸을 향한 모성의 보살핌을 변호하듯 끼어들었다. \"네 과거에 그이를 걱정시킬 만한 게 뭐가 있었다는 거니?"], ["That Vronsky paid you attentions—that happens to every girl.”", "브론스키가 너한테 관심을 보였다지만—그런 일은 어느 처녀에게나 일어나는 거야.\""]], [["“Oh, yes, but we didn’t mean that,”", "\"오, 네, 하지만 우리가 말하는 건 그게 아니에요.\""], ["Kitty said, flushing a little.", "키티가 살짝 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다."]], [["“No, let me speak,”", "\"아니, 내 말 좀 들어 보렴.\""], ["her mother went on, “why, you yourself would not let me have a talk to Vronsky.", "어머니가 말을 이었다. \"네가 직접 내가 브론스키와 이야기하는 걸 막았잖니."], ["Don’t you remember?”", "기억 안 나니?\""]], [["“Oh, mamma!”", "\"오, 엄마!\""], ["said Kitty, with an expression of suffering.", "키티가 괴로운 표정으로 말했다."]], [["“There’s no keeping you young people in check nowadays....", "\"요즘 너희 젊은 사람들은 도무지 단속할 수가 없어……"], ["Your friendship could not have gone beyond what was suitable.", "너희 사이가 적절한 선을 넘어설 수는 없었겠지."], ["I should myself have called upon him to explain himself.", "나라면 직접 그 사람을 불러다 입장을 분명히 밝히게 했을 거야."], ["But, my darling, it’s not right for you to be agitated.", "하지만 얘야, 네가 동요하는 건 좋지 않아."], ["Please remember that, and calm yourself.”", "부디 그걸 명심하고 마음을 가라앉히렴.\""]], [["“I’m perfectly calm, maman.”", "\"저는 아주 차분해요, 엄마.\""]], [["“How happy it was for Kitty that Anna came then,”", "\"그때 안나가 와 준 게 키티한테는 얼마나 다행이었는지.\""], ["said Dolly, “and how unhappy for her.", "돌리가 말했다. \"그리고 또 얼마나 불행한 일이었는지."], ["It turned out quite the opposite,”", "결과가 정반대로 나왔잖아요.\""], ["she said, struck by her own ideas.", "그녀가 자기 생각에 문득 놀라며 말했다."], ["“Then Anna was so happy, and Kitty thought herself unhappy.", "\"그땐 안나가 그렇게 행복했고, 키티는 자기가 불행하다고 여겼지."], ["Now it is just the opposite.", "그런데 지금은 정반대예요."], ["I often think of her.”", "나는 그 사람을 자주 떠올려요.\""]], [["“A nice person to think about!", "\"떠올릴 만한 사람도 참 많다!"], ["Horrid, repulsive woman—no heart,”", "끔찍하고 역겨운 여자—마음이라곤 없는 여자야.\""], ["said her mother, who could not forget that Kitty had married not Vronsky, but Levin.", "키티가 브론스키가 아니라 레빈과 결혼했다는 사실을 잊지 못하는 어머니가 말했다."]], [["“What do you want to talk of it for?”", "\"왜 그 얘기를 하려고 하세요?\""], ["Kitty said with annoyance.", "키티가 짜증스럽게 말했다."], ["“I never think about it, and I don’t want to think of it....", "\"저는 그 일은 전혀 생각하지 않고, 생각하고 싶지도 않아요……"], ["And I don’t want to think of it,”", "그래요, 생각하고 싶지 않아요.\""], ["she said, catching the sound of her husband’s well-known step on the steps of the terrace.", "그녀가 테라스 계단에 울리는 남편의 낯익은 발소리를 알아채고는 말했다."]], [["“What’s that you don’t want to think about?”", "\"무엇을 생각하고 싶지 않다는 거요?\""], ["inquired Levin, coming onto the terrace.", "레빈이 테라스로 올라서며 물었다."]], [["But no one answered him, and he did not repeat the question.", "하지만 아무도 그에게 대답하지 않았고, 그도 질문을 되풀이하지 않았다."]], [["“I’m sorry I’ve broken in on your feminine parliament,”", "\"당신들의 여성 의회를 방해해서 미안하군요.\""], ["he said, looking round on everyone discontentedly, and perceiving that they had been talking of something which they would not talk about before him.", "그는 못마땅한 표정으로 모두를 둘러보며, 그들이 자기 앞에서는 말하지 않을 무언가를 이야기하고 있었음을 알아채고 말했다."]], [["For a second he felt that he was sharing the feeling of Agafea Mihalovna, vexation at their making jam without water, and altogether at the outside Shtcherbatsky element.", "순간 그는 아가피야 미하일로브나의 감정, 즉 물 없이 잼을 만드는 데 대한, 그리고 통틀어 외부에서 들어온 셰르바츠키 집안 요소 전반에 대한 못마땅함을 자기도 함께 느꼈다."], ["He smiled, however, and went up to Kitty.", "그래도 그는 미소를 지으며 키티에게 다가갔다."]], [["“Well, how are you?”", "\"자, 어떻소?\""], ["he asked her, looking at her with the expression with which everyone looked at her now.", "그가 요즘 모두가 그녀를 바라보는 그 표정으로 그녀를 바라보며 물었다."]], [["“Oh, very well,”", "\"오, 아주 좋아요.\""], ["said Kitty, smiling, “and how have things gone with you?”", "키티가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"당신 일은 어떻게 됐어요?\""]], [["“The wagons held three times as much as the old carts did.", "\"새 짐마차들이 옛날 수레보다 세 배는 더 실었소."], ["Well, are we going for the children?", "자, 아이들 데리러 갈까?"], ["I’ve ordered the horses to be put in.”", "말을 매어 두라고 일러뒀소.\""]], [["“What!", "\"뭐라고요!"], ["you want to take Kitty in the wagonette?”", "키티를 짐마차에 태우겠다는 거예요?\""], ["her mother said reproachfully.", "어머니가 나무라듯 말했다."]], [["“Yes, at a walking pace, princess.”", "\"네, 천천히 걷는 정도로요, 공작 부인.\""]], [["Levin never called the princess “maman” as men often do call their mothers-in-law, and the princess disliked his not doing so.", "레빈은 남자들이 흔히 장모를 부르듯 공작 부인을 \"엄마\"라고 부른 적이 한 번도 없었고, 공작 부인은 그가 그렇게 부르지 않는 것을 못마땅해했다."], ["But though he liked and respected the princess, Levin could not call her so without a sense of profaning his feeling for his dead mother.", "하지만 레빈은 공작 부인을 좋아하고 존경했음에도, 돌아가신 자기 어머니에 대한 감정을 더럽힌다는 느낌 없이는 그녀를 그렇게 부를 수 없었다."]], [["“Come with us, maman,”", "\"같이 가요, 엄마.\""], ["said Kitty.", "키티가 말했다."]], [["“I don’t like to see such imprudence.”", "\"그런 무모한 짓은 보고 싶지 않구나.\""]], [["“Well, I’ll walk then, I’m so well.”", "\"그럼 저는 걸어갈게요, 몸도 아주 좋으니까.\""], ["Kitty got up and went to her husband and took his hand.", "키티가 일어나 남편에게 다가가 그의 손을 잡았다."]], [["“You may be well, but everything in moderation,”", "\"몸이 좋더라도 무엇이든 적당히 해야지.\""], ["said the princess.", "공작 부인이 말했다."]], [["“Well, Agafea Mihalovna, is the jam done?”", "\"자, 아가피야 미하일로브나, 잼은 다 됐소?\""], ["said Levin, smiling to Agafea Mihalovna, and trying to cheer her up.", "레빈이 아가피야 미하일로브나에게 미소 지으며, 그녀를 기운 나게 해 주려고 말했다."], ["“Is it all right in the new way?”", "\"새 방식으로 해도 괜찮소?\""]], [["“I suppose it’s all right.", "\"괜찮은가 봅니다."], ["For our notions it’s boiled too long.”", "우리 식으로 보자면 너무 오래 졸였지만요.\""]], [["“It’ll be all the better, Agafea Mihalovna, it won’t mildew, even though our ice has begun to thaw already, so that we’ve no cool cellar to store it,”", "\"그게 오히려 더 나을 거요, 아가피야 미하일로브나, 곰팡이가 안 슬 테니까. 우리 얼음이 벌써 녹기 시작해서 보관해 둘 서늘한 광도 없지 않소.\""], ["said Kitty, at once divining her husband’s motive, and addressing the old housekeeper with the same feeling; “but your pickle’s so good, that mamma says she never tasted any like it,”", "키티가 남편의 의도를 곧바로 알아채고, 같은 마음으로 늙은 가정부에게 말을 건넸다. \"그런데 할멈이 만든 절임은 어찌나 맛있는지, 엄마가 그런 건 한 번도 못 먹어 봤다고 하신다니까요.\""], ["she added, smiling, and putting her kerchief straight.", "그녀가 미소 지으며, 머릿수건을 바로잡으며 덧붙였다."]], [["Agafea Mihalovna looked angrily at Kitty.", "아가피야 미하일로브나가 화난 얼굴로 키티를 바라보았다."]], [["“You needn’t try to console me, mistress.", "\"저를 위로하실 것 없습니다, 아씨."], ["I need only to look at you with him, and I feel happy,”", "저는 그저 그분과 함께 있는 아씨를 보기만 해도 행복합니다.\""], ["she said, and something in the rough familiarity of that with him touched Kitty.", "그녀가 말했고, 그 '그분과 함께'라는 투박하고 정겨운 말투에 담긴 무언가가 키티의 마음을 건드렸다."]], [["“Come along with us to look for mushrooms, you will show us the best places.”", "\"우리랑 같이 버섯 따러 가요, 좋은 자리를 알려 줘요.\""], ["Agafea Mihalovna smiled and shook her head, as though to say: “I should like to be angry with you too, but I can’t.”", "아가피야 미하일로브나는 미소 지으며 고개를 저었는데, 마치 \"저도 아씨한테 화를 내고 싶지만, 그럴 수가 없네요\" 하고 말하는 듯했다."]], [["“Do it, please, by my receipt,”", "\"부디 내 방식대로 하게.\""], ["said the princess; “put some paper over the jam, and moisten it with a little rum, and without even ice, it will never go mildewy.”", "공작 부인이 말했다. \"잼 위에 종이를 덮고 럼주를 조금 적셔 두면, 얼음이 없어도 절대 곰팡이가 슬지 않는단다.\""]], [["# Chapter 3", "# Chapter 3"]], [["Kitty was particularly glad of a chance of being alone with her husband, for she had noticed the shade of mortification that had passed over his face—always so quick to reflect every feeling—at the moment when he had come onto the terrace and asked what they were talking of, and had got no answer.", "키티는 남편과 단둘이 있을 기회가 생긴 것이 무엇보다도 기뻤다. 남편이 테라스로 나와 무슨 이야기를 하고 있었느냐고 물었다가 아무 대답도 듣지 못했던 그 순간, 그의 얼굴—언제나 온갖 감정을 그렇게도 빠르게 비추어 내는 그 얼굴—을 스쳐 지나간 모욕감의 그늘을 그녀는 알아챘던 것이다."]], [["When they had set off on foot ahead of the others, and had come out of sight of the house onto the beaten dusty road, marked with rusty wheels and sprinkled with grains of corn, she clung faster to his arm and pressed it closer to her.", "다른 사람들보다 앞서 걸어 나가 집이 보이지 않는 곳까지 이르렀을 때, 그들은 녹슨 바큇자국이 패고 낟알이 흩뿌려진 단단히 다져진 흙길로 들어섰다. 그녀는 그의 팔에 더 바짝 매달리며 그것을 자기 쪽으로 끌어당겼다."], ["He had quite forgotten the momentary unpleasant impression, and alone with her he felt, now that the thought of her approaching motherhood was never for a moment absent from his mind, a new and delicious bliss, quite pure from all alloy of sense, in the being near to the woman he loved.", "그는 잠시 스쳐 갔던 불쾌한 인상을 까맣게 잊고 있었다. 그녀가 곧 어머니가 되리라는 생각이 한순간도 머릿속을 떠나지 않는 지금, 사랑하는 여인 곁에 있다는 사실 하나만으로 그는 관능의 불순물이라곤 조금도 섞이지 않은, 새롭고도 감미로운 행복을 느꼈다."], ["There was no need of speech, yet he longed to hear the sound of her voice, which like her eyes had changed since she had been with child.", "말은 필요 없었지만, 그래도 그는 그녀의 목소리를 듣고 싶었다. 그 목소리는 그녀의 눈빛처럼 임신한 뒤로 달라져 있었다."], ["In her voice, as in her eyes, there was that softness and gravity which is found in people continually concentrated on some cherished pursuit.", "그녀의 목소리에는, 눈빛에서와 마찬가지로, 무언가 소중한 일에 끊임없이 마음을 쏟는 사람들에게서 발견되는 그 부드러움과 진중함이 깃들어 있었다."]], [["“So you’re not tired?", "\"그래, 힘들지 않아?"], ["Lean more on me,”", "내게 더 기대.\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“No, I’m so glad of a chance of being alone with you, and I must own, though I’m happy with them, I do regret our winter evenings alone.”", "\"아니에요, 당신과 단둘이 있을 기회가 생겨서 정말 기뻐요. 그리고 솔직히 말하면, 모두와 함께 있어도 행복하지만, 우리 둘만 보내던 겨울 저녁이 그립긴 해요.\""]], [["“That was good, but this is even better.", "\"그때도 좋았지, 하지만 지금은 더 좋아."], ["Both are better,”", "둘 다 좋은 거야.\""], ["he said, squeezing her hand.", "그는 그녀의 손을 꼭 쥐며 말했다."]], [["“Do you know what we were talking about when you came in?”", "\"당신이 들어왔을 때 우리가 무슨 얘기를 하고 있었는지 알아요?\""]], [["“About jam?”", "\"잼 얘기?\""]], [["“Oh, yes, about jam too; but afterwards, about how men make offers.”", "\"아, 그래요, 잼 얘기도 했죠. 하지만 그다음에는, 남자들이 어떻게 청혼을 하는가에 대해서요.\""]], [["“Ah!”", "\"아!\" 레빈은 그녀가 하는 말보다 그녀의 목소리에 더 귀를 기울이며 말했다."], ["said Levin, listening more to the sound of her voice than to the words she was saying, and all the while paying attention to the road, which passed now through the forest, and avoiding places where she might make a false step.", "그러면서도 내내 길에 신경을 쏟고 있었다. 길은 이제 숲을 지나고 있었고, 그는 그녀가 발을 헛디딜 만한 곳을 피해 갔다."]], [["“And about Sergey Ivanovitch and Varenka.", "\"그리고 세르게이 이바노비치하고 바렌카 얘기도요."], ["You’ve noticed?...", "당신도 눈치챘죠?……"], ["I’m very anxious for it,”", "난 그게 정말 간절해요.\""], ["she went on.", "그녀가 말을 이었다."], ["“What do you think about it?”", "\"당신은 어떻게 생각해요?\""], ["And she peeped into his face.", "그러고는 그의 얼굴을 들여다보았다."]], [["“I don’t know what to think,”", "\"어떻게 생각해야 할지 모르겠어.\""], ["Levin answered, smiling.", "레빈이 미소 지으며 대답했다."], ["“Sergey seems very strange to me in that way.", "\"세르게이는 그런 면에선 내겐 아주 이상해 보여."], ["I told you, you know....”", "전에 당신한테 말했잖아, 알지…….\""]], [["“Yes, that he was in love with that girl who died....”", "\"네, 형님이 죽은 그 아가씨를 사랑했었다는 거요…….\""]], [["“That was when I was a child; I know about it from hearsay and tradition.", "\"그건 내가 어렸을 때 일이야. 나도 그저 전해 들은 얘기로 알 뿐이지."], ["I remember him then.", "그때 형의 모습은 기억나."], ["He was wonderfully sweet.", "정말 다정한 사람이었어."], ["But I’ve watched him since with women; he is friendly, some of them he likes, but one feels that to him they’re simply people, not women.”", "하지만 그 후로 여자들을 대하는 형을 지켜봤는데, 형은 친절하고, 그중 몇몇은 좋아하기도 하지만, 형에게 그들은 그저 사람일 뿐 여자가 아니라는 느낌이 들어.\""]], [["“Yes, but now with Varenka ...", "\"그래요, 하지만 지금 바렌카하고는……"], ["I fancy there’s something....”", "뭔가 있는 것 같아요…….\""]], [["“Perhaps there is....", "\"어쩌면 있을지도 모르지……"], ["But one has to know him....", "하지만 형을 알아야 해……"], ["He’s a peculiar, wonderful person.", "형은 별난, 대단한 사람이야."], ["He lives a spiritual life only.", "형은 오직 정신적인 삶만 살지."], ["He’s too pure, too exalted a nature.”", "너무 순수하고, 너무 고결한 천성이야.\""]], [["“Why?", "\"왜요?"], ["Would this lower him, then?”", "그러면 이 일이 형님을 깎아내리기라도 한다는 거예요?\""]], [["“No, but he’s so used to a spiritual life that he can’t reconcile himself with actual fact, and Varenka is after all fact.”", "\"아니, 그게 아니라 형은 정신적인 삶에 너무 익숙해져서 실제 현실하고 화해할 수가 없는 거야. 그런데 바렌카는 어쨌든 현실이거든.\""]], [["Levin had grown used by now to uttering his thought boldly, without taking the trouble of clothing it in exact language.", "레빈은 이제 자기 생각을 정확한 말로 차려입히는 수고를 들이지 않고 대담하게 내뱉는 데 익숙해져 있었다."], ["He knew that his wife, in such moments of loving tenderness as now, would understand what he meant to say from a hint, and she did understand him.", "그는 지금처럼 사랑과 다정함이 넘치는 순간이면 아내가 그저 한마디 암시만으로도 자기가 하려는 말을 알아들으리라는 것을 알았고, 실제로 그녀는 그를 이해했다."]], [["“Yes, but there’s not so much of that actual fact about her as about me.", "\"그래요, 하지만 바렌카에겐 나만큼 그 현실적인 면이 많지 않아요."], ["I can see that he would never have cared for me.", "형님은 결코 날 마음에 들어 하지 않았을 거예요."], ["She is altogether spiritual.”", "바렌카는 온통 정신적인 사람이거든요.\""]], [["“Oh, no, he is so fond of you, and I am always so glad when my people like you....”", "\"오, 아니야, 형은 당신을 무척 좋아해. 그리고 우리 식구들이 당신을 좋아할 때면 난 늘 정말 기뻐…….\""]], [["“Yes, he’s very nice to me; but....”", "\"네, 형님은 내게 아주 잘해 줘요. 하지만…….\""]], [["“It’s not as it was with poor Nikolay ...", "\"가엾은 니콜라이 형하고는 다르지……"], ["you really cared for each other,”", "당신들은 정말로 서로 아꼈으니까.\""], ["Levin finished.", "레빈이 말을 맺었다."], ["“Why not speak of him?”", "\"그 형 얘긴 왜 안 하는 거지?\""], ["he added.", "그가 덧붙였다."], ["“I sometimes blame myself for not; it ends in one’s forgetting.", "\"난 가끔 그러지 않는 나를 탓하곤 해. 결국엔 잊어버리게 되니까."], ["Ah, how terrible and dear he was!...", "아, 형은 얼마나 끔찍하고 또 사랑스러운 사람이었던지!……"], ["Yes, what were we talking about?”", "그래, 우리가 무슨 얘길 하고 있었지?\""], ["Levin said, after a pause.", "레빈은 잠시 말을 멈췄다가 다시 입을 열었다."]], [["“You think he can’t fall in love,”", "\"형님은 사랑에 빠질 수 없는 사람이라고 생각하는 거죠?\""], ["said Kitty, translating into her own language.", "키티가 자기 식으로 옮겨 말했다."]], [["“It’s not so much that he can’t fall in love,”", "\"형이 사랑에 빠질 수 없다기보다는,\""], ["Levin said, smiling, “but he has not the weakness necessary....", "레빈이 미소 지으며 말했다. \"거기에 필요한 약점이 형에겐 없는 거야……"], ["I’ve always envied him, and even now, when I’m so happy, I still envy him.”", "난 늘 형을 부러워했어. 이렇게 행복한 지금조차도 여전히 형이 부러워.\""]], [["“You envy him for not being able to fall in love?”", "\"사랑에 빠질 수 없다는 걸 부러워한다고요?\""]], [["“I envy him for being better than I,”", "\"형이 나보다 나은 사람이라서 부러운 거야.\""], ["said Levin.", "레빈이 말했다."], ["“He does not live for himself.", "\"형은 자기 자신을 위해 살지 않아."], ["His whole life is subordinated to his duty.", "형의 삶 전체가 의무에 종속되어 있지."], ["And that’s why he can be calm and contented.”", "그래서 형은 평온하고 만족스러울 수 있는 거야.\""]], [["“And you?”", "\"그럼 당신은요?\""], ["Kitty asked, with an ironical and loving smile.", "키티가 비꼬는 듯하면서도 사랑이 담긴 미소를 지으며 물었다."]], [["She could never have explained the chain of thought that made her smile; but the last link in it was that her husband, in exalting his brother and abasing himself, was not quite sincere.", "그녀로서는 자기를 웃게 만든 그 생각의 사슬을 결코 설명할 수 없었을 것이다. 다만 그 마지막 고리는, 남편이 형을 치켜세우고 자기 자신을 낮추는 것이 그리 진심에서 우러난 것만은 아니라는 점이었다."], ["Kitty knew that this insincerity came from his love for his brother, from his sense of shame at being too happy, and above all from his unflagging craving to be better—she loved it in him, and so she smiled.", "키티는 이 진심 아닌 태도가 형을 향한 그의 사랑에서, 너무 행복한 것에 대한 부끄러움에서, 그리고 무엇보다도 더 나은 사람이 되고자 하는 그의 식을 줄 모르는 갈망에서 비롯된다는 것을 알았다. 그녀는 그에게서 바로 그 점을 사랑했고, 그래서 미소 지었던 것이다."]], [["“And you?", "\"그럼 당신은요?"], ["What are you dissatisfied with?”", "무엇이 그렇게 불만이에요?\""], ["she asked, with the same smile.", "그녀가 똑같은 미소를 지으며 물었다."]], [["Her disbelief in his self-dissatisfaction delighted him, and unconsciously he tried to draw her into giving utterance to the grounds of her disbelief.", "자기가 스스로에게 만족하지 못한다는 말을 그녀가 믿지 않는 것이 그는 기뻤다. 그래서 그는 자기도 모르게 그녀가 그 불신의 근거를 입 밖으로 꺼내도록 이끌어 내려 했다."]], [["“I am happy, but dissatisfied with myself....”", "\"난 행복해, 하지만 나 자신엔 불만이야…….\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“Why, how can you be dissatisfied with yourself if you are happy?”", "\"아니, 행복한데 어떻게 자기 자신에게 불만일 수가 있어요?\""]], [["“Well, how shall I say?...", "\"글쎄, 어떻게 말해야 할까?……"], ["In my heart I really care for nothing whatever but that you should not stumble—see?", "사실 내 마음속엔 오로지 당신이 발을 헛디디지 않게 하는 것 말고는 다른 어떤 것에도 관심이 없어—알겠어?"], ["Oh, but really you mustn’t skip about like that!”", "아니, 정말 그렇게 깡충깡충 뛰면 안 돼!\""], ["he cried, breaking off to scold her for too agile a movement in stepping over a branch that lay in the path.", "그는 길에 떨어진 나뭇가지를 너무 날렵하게 넘어가는 그녀를 나무라느라 말을 끊고 소리쳤다."], ["“But when I think about myself, and compare myself with others, especially with my brother, I feel I’m a poor creature.”", "\"하지만 나 자신을 생각하고, 다른 사람들과, 특히 형하고 비교해 보면, 내가 한심한 인간이라는 느낌이 들어.\""]], [["“But in what way?”", "\"하지만 어떤 점에서요?\""], ["Kitty pursued with the same smile.", "키티가 똑같은 미소를 지으며 캐물었다."], ["“Don’t you too work for others?", "\"당신도 다른 사람들을 위해 일하잖아요?"], ["What about your co-operative settlement, and your work on the estate, and your book?...”", "당신의 협동 농장이며, 영지 일이며, 책은 또 어떻고요?……\""]], [["“Oh, but I feel, and particularly just now—it’s your fault,”", "\"오, 하지만 난 느껴, 특히 바로 지금—이건 다 당신 탓이야.\""], ["he said, pressing her hand—“that all that doesn’t count.", "그는 그녀의 손을 꼭 쥐며 말했다. \"그게 다 아무 의미도 없다는 걸 말이야."], ["I do it in a way halfheartedly.", "난 그 일을 어딘가 건성으로 하고 있어."], ["If I could care for all that as I care for you!...", "내가 당신을 아끼는 만큼 그 모든 일을 아낄 수만 있다면!……"], ["Instead of that, I do it in these days like a task that is set me.”", "그런데 그러기는커녕, 요즘 난 그저 누가 떠맡긴 숙제처럼 그 일을 하고 있어.\""]], [["“Well, what would you say about papa?”", "\"그럼 아버지에 대해선 뭐라고 하시겠어요?\""], ["asked Kitty.", "키티가 물었다."], ["“Is he a poor creature then, as he does nothing for the public good?”", "\"아버지는 공공의 이익을 위해 아무것도 하지 않으니, 그럼 한심한 인간인가요?\""]], [["“He?—no!", "\"장인어른?—아니지!"], ["But then one must have the simplicity, the straightforwardness, the goodness of your father: and I haven’t got that.", "하지만 그러려면 당신 아버지 같은 순박함, 솔직함, 선량함이 있어야 해. 그런데 난 그게 없어."], ["I do nothing, and I fret about it.", "난 아무것도 하지 않으면서 그걸로 안달복달하지. 이게 다 당신 탓이야."], ["It’s all your doing.", "당신이 생기기 전에는—그리고 이 아이도 말이야.\""], ["Before there was you—and this too,”", "그는 그녀가 알아챈 그녀의 허리께를 흘끗 보며 덧붙였다. \"난 내 모든 정력을 일에 쏟았어."], ["he added with a glance towards her waist that she understood—“I put all my energies into work; now I can’t, and I’m ashamed; I do it just as though it were a task set me, I’m pretending....”", "그런데 지금은 그럴 수가 없고, 그게 부끄러워. 난 그저 누가 떠맡긴 숙제라도 되는 양 일하면서, 흉내만 내고 있는 거야…….\""]], [["“Well, but would you like to change this minute with Sergey Ivanovitch?”", "\"그럼, 당신 지금 당장 세르게이 이바노비치하고 바꾸고 싶어요?\""], ["said Kitty.", "키티가 말했다."], ["“Would you like to do this work for the general good, and to love the task set you, as he does, and nothing else?”", "\"형님처럼 공공의 이익을 위해 이 일을 하고, 떠맡겨진 그 일을 사랑하고, 그 밖엔 아무것도 없는 그런 삶을 살고 싶어요?\""]], [["“Of course not,”", "\"물론 아니지.\""], ["said Levin.", "레빈이 말했다."], ["“But I’m so happy that I don’t understand anything.", "\"하지만 난 너무 행복해서 아무것도 이해할 수가 없어."], ["So you think he’ll make her an offer today?”", "그래, 당신은 형이 오늘 그녀에게 청혼할 거라고 생각해?\""], ["he added after a brief silence.", "그는 잠시 침묵한 뒤 덧붙였다."]], [["“I think so, and I don’t think so.", "\"그럴 것도 같고, 아닐 것도 같아요."], ["Only, I’m awfully anxious for it.", "다만 난 그게 정말 간절해요."], ["Here, wait a minute.”", "자, 잠깐만요.\""], ["She stooped down and picked a wild camomile at the edge of the path.", "그녀는 몸을 굽혀 길가에 핀 들국화 한 송이를 꺾었다."], ["“Come, count: he does propose, he doesn’t,”", "\"자, 세어 봐요. 청혼한다, 안 한다.\""], ["she said, giving him the flower.", "그녀가 그에게 꽃을 건네며 말했다."]], [["“He does, he doesn’t,”", "\"한다, 안 한다.\""], ["said Levin, tearing off the white petals.", "레빈이 흰 꽃잎을 하나씩 뜯어내며 말했다."]], [["“No, no!”", "\"안 돼요, 안 돼!\""], ["Kitty, snatching at his hand, stopped him.", "키티가 그의 손을 낚아채며 멈춰 세웠다."], ["She had been watching his fingers with interest.", "그녀는 흥미롭게 그의 손가락을 지켜보고 있었던 것이다."], ["“You picked off two.”", "\"두 장을 한꺼번에 뜯었잖아요.\""]], [["“Oh, but see, this little one shan’t count to make up,”", "\"오, 하지만 봐, 이 작은 건 수를 맞추는 데 안 치는 걸로 하자.\""], ["said Levin, tearing off a little half-grown petal.", "레빈이 덜 자란 작은 꽃잎 하나를 뜯어내며 말했다."], ["“Here’s the wagonette overtaking us.”", "\"여기 마차가 우릴 따라잡고 있어.\""]], [["“Aren’t you tired, Kitty?”", "\"키티, 힘들지 않니?\""], ["called the princess.", "공작 부인이 소리쳐 물었다."]], [["“Not in the least.”", "\"하나도 안 힘들어요.\""]], [["“If you are you can get in, as the horses are quiet and walking.”", "\"힘들면 타려무나. 말들도 얌전히 걷고 있으니까.\""]], [["But it was not worth while to get in, they were quite near the place, and all walked on together.", "하지만 굳이 탈 필요는 없었다. 목적지가 바로 가까이에 있었고, 모두 함께 걸어갔다."]], [["# Chapter 4", "# Chapter 4"]], [["Varenka, with her white kerchief on her black hair, surrounded by the children, gaily and good-humoredly looking after them, and at the same time visibly excited at the possibility of receiving a declaration from the man she cared for, was very attractive.", "바렌카는 검은 머리에 흰 머릿수건을 두르고 아이들에게 둘러싸여 명랑하고 다정하게 그들을 돌보면서도, 마음에 둔 남자에게서 고백을 받을지도 모른다는 가능성에 눈에 띄게 들떠 있어 무척 매력적이었다."], ["Sergey Ivanovitch walked beside her, and never left off admiring her.", "세르게이 이바노비치는 그녀 곁을 걸으며 줄곧 그녀에게 탄복하기를 멈추지 않았다."], ["Looking at her, he recalled all the delightful things he had heard from her lips, all the good he knew about her, and became more and more conscious that the feeling he had for her was something special that he had felt long, long ago, and only once, in his early youth.", "그는 그녀를 바라보면서, 그녀의 입에서 들었던 온갖 즐거운 이야기들이며, 그녀에 관해 알고 있는 온갖 좋은 점들을 떠올렸고, 자기가 그녀에게 품은 감정이 아주 오래전, 그것도 딱 한 번, 젊디젊은 시절에 느껴 본 적 있는 특별한 무엇임을 점점 더 또렷하게 의식하게 되었다."], ["The feeling of happiness in being near her continually grew, and at last reached such a point that, as he put a huge, slender-stalked agaric fungus in her basket, he looked straight into her face, and noticing the flush of glad and alarmed excitement that overspread her face, he was confused himself, and smiled to her in silence a smile that said too much.", "그녀 곁에 있다는 행복감은 끊임없이 커져만 갔고, 마침내 어떤 지경에까지 이르러, 줄기가 가느다란 커다란 광대버섯 하나를 그녀의 바구니에 넣어 주다가 그는 그녀의 얼굴을 똑바로 바라보았는데, 그녀의 얼굴에 번지는 기쁘면서도 놀란 듯한 흥분의 홍조를 알아채고는 자기 자신마저 당황하여, 말없이 너무 많은 것을 담은 미소를 그녀에게 지어 보였다."]], [["“If so,”", "\"이러다가는,\""], ["he said to himself, “I ought to think it over and make up my mind, and not give way like a boy to the impulse of a moment.”", "그는 속으로 중얼거렸다. \"이건 곰곰이 따져 보고 마음을 정해야 할 일이지, 한순간의 충동에 소년처럼 굴복할 일이 아니야.\""]], [["“I’m going to pick by myself apart from all the rest, or else my efforts will make no show,”", "\"나는 따로 떨어져서 나 혼자 딸 겁니다. 안 그러면 내가 딴 게 표가 안 날 테니까요.\""], ["he said, and he left the edge of the forest where they were walking on low silky grass between old birch trees standing far apart, and went more into the heart of the wood, where between the white birch trunks there were gray trunks of aspen and dark bushes of hazel.", "그는 이렇게 말하고는, 멀찍이 떨어져 선 늙은 자작나무들 사이로 비단처럼 부드러운 낮은 풀이 깔린 숲 가장자리를 벗어나, 흰 자작나무 줄기들 사이로 잿빛 사시나무 줄기와 거뭇한 개암나무 덤불이 우거진 숲 속 더 깊은 곳으로 들어갔다."], ["Walking some forty paces away, Sergey Ivanovitch, knowing he was out of sight, stood still behind a bushy spindle-tree in full flower with its rosy red catkins.", "마흔 걸음쯤 떨어져 걸어가, 이제 남들 눈에 띄지 않는다는 것을 안 세르게이 이바노비치는 발갛게 물든 꽃차례를 잔뜩 피운 화살나무 덤불 뒤에 멈춰 섰다."], ["It was perfectly still all round him.", "사방이 완전히 고요했다."], ["Only overhead in the birches under which he stood, the flies, like a swarm of bees, buzzed unceasingly, and from time to time the children’s voices were floated across to him.", "다만 그가 서 있는 자작나무들 머리 위에서, 마치 벌떼처럼 날파리들이 쉴 새 없이 윙윙거렸고, 이따금 아이들의 목소리가 그에게로 실려 올 뿐이었다."], ["All at once he heard, not far from the edge of the wood, the sound of Varenka’s contralto voice, calling Grisha, and a smile of delight passed over Sergey Ivanovitch’s face.", "그때 별안간 숲 가장자리에서 그리 멀지 않은 곳에서 그리샤를 부르는 바렌카의 콘트랄토 음성이 들려왔고, 세르게이 이바노비치의 얼굴에 기쁜 미소가 스쳐 지나갔다."], ["Conscious of this smile, he shook his head disapprovingly at his own condition, and taking out a cigar, he began lighting it.", "그 미소를 의식한 그는 자기 처지가 못마땅한 듯 고개를 가로젓고는, 시가를 꺼내 불을 붙이기 시작했다."], ["For a long while he could not get a match to light against the trunk of a birch tree.", "한참 동안이나 그는 자작나무 줄기에 성냥을 그어도 불을 붙일 수가 없었다."], ["The soft scales of the white bark rubbed off the phosphorus, and the light went out.", "흰 나무껍질의 부드러운 비늘이 인을 문질러 닦아 내는 바람에 불이 꺼져 버린 것이다."], ["At last one of the matches burned, and the fragrant cigar smoke, hovering uncertainly in flat, wide coils, stretched away forwards and upwards over a bush under the overhanging branches of a birch tree.", "마침내 성냥 하나가 타올랐고, 향긋한 시가 연기가 납작하고 넓게 너울지며 어렴풋이 떠다니다가, 자작나무 가지가 늘어진 아래 덤불 위로 앞으로 또 위로 길게 뻗어 나갔다."], ["Watching the streak of smoke, Sergey Ivanovitch walked gently on, deliberating on his position.", "연기 줄기를 바라보며 세르게이 이바노비치는 자기 처지를 곰곰이 헤아리며 천천히 걸음을 옮겼다."]], [["“Why not?”", "\"안 될 게 뭐 있어?\""], ["he thought.", "그는 생각했다."], ["“If it were only a passing fancy or a passion, if it were only this attraction—this mutual attraction (I can call it a mutual attraction), but if I felt that it was in contradiction with the whole bent of my life—if I felt that in giving way to this attraction I should be false to my vocation and my duty ...", "\"이게 한낱 스쳐 가는 변덕이나 격정에 지나지 않는다면, 그저 이 끌림—서로의 끌림(이건 서로의 끌림이라고 불러도 좋겠지)에 불과한 거라면 모르겠어. 하지만 만약 이게 내 삶의 모든 방향과 어긋난다고 느껴진다면—이 끌림에 굴복하는 것이 내 소명과 내 의무에 어긋나는 짓이라고 느껴진다면 말이야……."], ["but it’s not so.", "그런데 그렇지가 않단 말이지."], ["The only thing I can say against it is that, when I lost Marie, I said to myself that I would remain faithful to her memory.", "이걸 반대해서 내가 할 수 있는 말이라곤, 마리를 잃었을 때 그녀를 추억하는 마음에 끝까지 충실하겠노라고 스스로 다짐했다는 것뿐이야."], ["That’s the only thing I can say against my feeling....", "내 감정을 반대해서 할 수 있는 말은 그것 하나밖에 없어……."], ["That’s a great thing,”", "그건 대단한 거지.\""], ["Sergey Ivanovitch said to himself, feeling at the same time that this consideration had not the slightest importance for him personally, but would only perhaps detract from his romantic character in the eyes of others.", "세르게이 이바노비치는 속으로 이렇게 말하면서도, 동시에 이런 고려가 자기 개인에게는 조금도 중요하지 않으며, 다만 남들 눈에 자기의 낭만적 면모를 떨어뜨리는 데에나 작용할 뿐임을 느꼈다."], ["“But apart from that, however much I searched, I should never find anything to say against my feeling.", "\"하지만 그것만 빼면, 아무리 뒤져 봐도 내 감정에 반대할 만한 거리를 결코 찾아내지 못할 거야."], ["If I were choosing by considerations of suitability alone, I could not have found anything better.”", "만약 내가 오로지 적합성만 따져서 고른다 해도, 이보다 더 나은 상대는 찾을 수 없을걸.\""]], [["However many women and girls he thought of whom he knew, he could not think of a girl who united to such a degree all, positively all, the qualities he would wish to see in his wife.", "자기가 아는 여자며 처녀들을 아무리 많이 떠올려 봐도, 그는 자기가 아내에게서 보고 싶어 할 자질들을 그 정도로 모조리, 정말 하나도 빠짐없이 갖춘 처녀를 떠올릴 수가 없었다."], ["She had all the charm and freshness of youth, but she was not a child; and if she loved him, she loved him consciously as a woman ought to love; that was one thing.", "그녀는 젊음의 온갖 매력과 싱그러움을 지녔으면서도 어린아이는 아니었다. 그리고 만약 그녀가 그를 사랑한다면, 그것은 여자라면 마땅히 사랑해야 하는 방식으로 의식하며 사랑하는 것이리라."], ["Another point: she was not only far from being worldly, but had an unmistakable distaste for worldly society, and at the same time she knew the world, and had all the ways of a woman of the best society, which were absolutely essential to Sergey Ivanovitch’s conception of the woman who was to share his life.", "이것이 한 가지였다. 또 한 가지는, 그녀는 속물적인 것과 거리가 멀 뿐 아니라 사교계에 대해 분명한 거부감을 지녔으면서도, 동시에 세상을 알았고, 최고 사교계 여인다운 온갖 몸가짐을 갖추고 있었는데, 이것은 자기 삶을 함께할 여자에 대한 세르게이 이바노비치의 관념에 절대적으로 필요한 점이었다."], ["Thirdly: she was religious, and not like a child, unconsciously religious and good, as Kitty, for example, was, but her life was founded on religious principles.", "셋째로, 그녀는 신앙심이 깊었는데, 이를테면 키티처럼 어린아이같이 무의식적으로 신앙심이 깊고 착한 것이 아니라, 그녀의 삶은 종교적 원리 위에 세워져 있었다."], ["Even in trifling matters, Sergey Ivanovitch found in her all that he wanted in his wife: she was poor and alone in the world, so she would not bring with her a mass of relations and their influence into her husband’s house, as he saw now in Kitty’s case.", "사소한 일에서까지도 세르게이 이바노비치는 그녀에게서 아내에게 바라는 모든 것을 찾아냈다. 그녀는 가난했고 이 세상에 홀몸이었으니, 지금 키티의 경우에서 보듯이 남편의 집안에 잔뜩 딸린 친척들과 그들의 영향력을 끌어들이지 않을 터였다."], ["She would owe everything to her husband, which was what he had always desired too for his future family life.", "그녀는 모든 것을 남편 덕으로 누리게 될 텐데, 이것이야말로 그가 자기 미래의 가정생활을 위해 늘 바라 왔던 바였다."], ["And this girl, who united all these qualities, loved him.", "그리고 이 모든 자질을 한데 갖춘 이 처녀가 그를 사랑했다."], ["He was a modest man, but he could not help seeing it.", "그는 겸손한 사람이었지만, 그것만은 알아보지 않을 수 없었다."], ["And he loved her.", "그리고 그도 그녀를 사랑했다."], ["There was one consideration against it—his age.", "이를 반대할 만한 고려가 하나 있었으니—바로 그의 나이였다."], ["But he came of a long-lived family, he had not a single gray hair, no one would have taken him for forty, and he remembered Varenka’s saying that it was only in Russia that men of fifty thought themselves old, and that in France a man of fifty considers himself dans la force de l’âge, while a man of forty is un jeune homme.", "하지만 그는 장수하는 집안 출신이었고, 흰 머리카락 한 올 없었으며, 누구도 그를 마흔으로는 보지 않았을 것이다. 게다가 쉰 먹은 사내가 스스로를 늙었다고 여기는 것은 오직 러시아에서뿐이고, 프랑스에서는 쉰 살 사내가 자기를 dans la force de l’âge라고 여기는 반면 마흔 살 사내는 un jeune homme라고 여긴다던 바렌카의 말이 떠올랐다."], ["But what did the mere reckoning of years matter when he felt as young in heart as he had been twenty years ago?", "하지만 마음이 스무 해 전과 다름없이 젊게 느껴지는데 햇수를 헤아리는 것이 무슨 대수란 말인가?"], ["Was it not youth to feel as he felt now, when coming from the other side to the edge of the wood he saw in the glowing light of the slanting sunbeams the gracious figure of Varenka in her yellow gown with her basket, walking lightly by the trunk of an old birch tree, and when this impression of the sight of Varenka blended so harmoniously with the beauty of the view, of the yellow oatfield lying bathed in the slanting sunshine, and beyond it the distant ancient forest flecked with yellow and melting into the blue of the distance?", "지금 그가 느끼는 것을 느끼는 것이야말로 젊음이 아니겠는가? 숲 가장자리 반대편에서 다가오던 그가, 비스듬히 쏟아지는 햇살의 환한 빛 속에서, 노란 드레스 차림에 바구니를 든 바렌카의 우아한 자태가 늙은 자작나무 줄기 곁을 가벼이 거니는 모습을 보았을 때, 그리고 이 바렌카의 모습이 자아내는 인상이 풍경의 아름다움과—비스듬한 햇살에 흠뻑 잠긴 노란 귀리밭과, 그 너머로 노랗게 얼룩진 채 먼 데의 푸른빛 속으로 녹아드는 아득한 옛 숲의 아름다움과—그토록 조화롭게 어우러졌을 때 느끼는 그 감정 말이다."], ["His heart throbbed joyously.", "그의 심장이 기쁘게 두근거렸다."], ["A softened feeling came over him.", "부드러운 감정이 그를 사로잡았다."], ["He felt that he had made up his mind.", "그는 자기가 마음을 정했음을 느꼈다."], ["Varenka, who had just crouched down to pick a mushroom, rose with a supple movement and looked round.", "막 버섯을 따려고 쪼그려 앉았던 바렌카가 나긋한 동작으로 몸을 일으키며 주위를 둘러보았다. 시가를 내던지고 세르게이 이바노비치는 단호한 걸음으로 그녀를 향해 다가갔다."], ["Flinging away the cigar, Sergey Ivanovitch advanced with resolute steps towards her.", "* dans la force de l’âge: 프랑스어로 '한창때의'. un jeune homme: 프랑스어로 '젊은이'."]], [["# Chapter 5", "# Chapter 5"]], [["“Varvara Andreevna, when I was very young, I set before myself the ideal of the woman I loved and should be happy to call my wife.", "\"바르바라 안드레예브나, 저는 아주 젊었을 때 제가 사랑하고 아내라 부르며 행복을 느낄 여인의 이상을 마음속에 그려 두었습니다."], ["I have lived through a long life, and now for the first time I have met what I sought—in you.", "긴 세월을 살아오는 동안 이제야 처음으로 제가 찾던 그것을 만났습니다—바로 당신에게서요. 당신을 사랑합니다."], ["I love you, and offer you my hand.”", "그리고 당신에게 제 손을 내밉니다.\""]], [["Sergey Ivanovitch was saying this to himself while he was ten paces from Varvara.", "세르게이 이바노비치는 바렌카에게서 열 걸음쯤 떨어진 곳에서 속으로 이 말을 되뇌고 있었다."], ["Kneeling down, with her hands over the mushrooms to guard them from Grisha, she was calling little Masha.", "그녀는 그리샤가 따 가지 못하도록 버섯 위로 두 손을 펼친 채 무릎을 꿇고 앉아, 어린 마샤를 부르고 있었다."]], [["“Come here, little ones!", "\"이리 와, 얘들아!"], ["There are so many!”", "여기 정말 많아!\""], ["she was saying in her sweet, deep voice.", "그녀는 달콤하고 깊은 목소리로 말했다."]], [["Seeing Sergey Ivanovitch approaching, she did not get up and did not change her position, but everything told him that she felt his presence and was glad of it.", "세르게이 이바노비치가 다가오는 것을 보고도 그녀는 일어나지도, 자세를 바꾸지도 않았다. 그러나 그녀가 그의 존재를 느끼고 또 그것을 기뻐하고 있다는 사실을 모든 것이 그에게 말해 주고 있었다."]], [["“Well, did you find some?”", "\"그래, 좀 따셨어요?\""], ["she asked from under the white kerchief, turning her handsome, gently smiling face to him.", "그녀가 하얀 머릿수건 아래에서, 아름답고 부드럽게 미소 짓는 얼굴을 그에게로 돌리며 물었다."]], [["“Not one,”", "\"하나도요.\""], ["said Sergey Ivanovitch.", "세르게이 이바노비치가 말했다."], ["“Did you?”", "\"당신은요?\""]], [["She did not answer, busy with the children who thronged about her.", "그녀는 자기 주위로 몰려든 아이들 때문에 분주해서 대답하지 못했다."]], [["“That one too, near the twig,”", "\"저것도, 저 잔가지 옆에 있는 거 말이야.\""], ["she pointed out to little Masha a little fungus, split in half across its rosy cap by the dry grass from under which it thrust itself.", "그녀는 어린 마샤에게 작은 버섯 하나를 가리켜 보였다. 그것은 마른 풀을 비집고 솟아오르느라 장밋빛 갓이 절반으로 갈라져 있었다."], ["Varenka got up while Masha picked the fungus, breaking it into two white halves.", "마샤가 버섯을 따서 두 쪽의 하얀 조각으로 쪼개는 동안 바렌카는 일어섰다."], ["“This brings back my childhood,”", "\"이러고 있으니 제 어린 시절이 떠오르네요.\""], ["she added, moving apart from the children beside Sergey Ivanovitch.", "그녀는 세르게이 이바노비치 옆으로 다가서며 아이들 곁에서 떨어져 나와 이렇게 덧붙였다."]], [["They walked on for some steps in silence.", "두 사람은 말없이 몇 걸음을 걸었다."], ["Varenka saw that he wanted to speak; she guessed of what, and felt faint with joy and panic.", "바렌카는 그가 무언가 말하고 싶어 한다는 것을 알아챘다. 무슨 말인지도 짐작했고, 기쁨과 두려움으로 정신이 아득해졌다."], ["They had walked so far away that no one could hear them now, but still he did not begin to speak.", "두 사람은 이제 아무도 그들의 말을 들을 수 없을 만큼 멀리 걸어왔는데도 그는 여전히 입을 떼지 못했다."], ["It would have been better for Varenka to be silent.", "바렌카로서는 차라리 잠자코 있는 편이 나았을 것이다."], ["After a silence it would have been easier for them to say what they wanted to say than after talking about mushrooms.", "침묵 뒤라면 버섯 이야기를 한 뒤보다 하고 싶은 말을 꺼내기가 그들에게 더 쉬웠을 테니까."], ["But against her own will, as it were accidentally, Varenka said:", "그런데 바렌카는 자기 뜻과는 달리, 말하자면 얼떨결에 이렇게 말해 버렸다."]], [["“So you found nothing?", "\"그래서 아무것도 못 따셨다고요?"], ["In the middle of the wood there are always fewer, though.”", "그래도 숲 한가운데에는 늘 버섯이 더 적은 법이긴 해요.\""], ["Sergey Ivanovitch sighed and made no answer.", "세르게이 이바노비치는 한숨을 쉬며 아무 대답도 하지 않았다."], ["He was annoyed that she had spoken about the mushrooms.", "그는 그녀가 버섯 이야기를 꺼낸 것이 못마땅했다."], ["He wanted to bring her back to the first words she had uttered about her childhood; but after a pause of some length, as though against his own will, he made an observation in response to her last words.", "그녀가 처음에 했던 어린 시절 이야기로 그녀를 되돌려 놓고 싶었다. 그러나 한참 침묵이 흐른 뒤, 마치 자기 뜻과는 어긋나게, 그는 그녀의 마지막 말에 대꾸하는 한마디를 던지고 말았다."]], [["“I have heard that the white edible funguses are found principally at the edge of the wood, though I can’t tell them apart.”", "\"하얀 식용 버섯은 주로 숲 가장자리에서 난다고 들었습니다. 하긴 저는 그것들을 구별할 줄도 모릅니다만.\""]], [["Some minutes more passed, they moved still further away from the children, and were quite alone.", "다시 몇 분이 흘렀고, 두 사람은 아이들에게서 더 멀어져 완전히 둘만 남았다."], ["Varenka’s heart throbbed so that she heard it beating, and felt that she was turning red and pale and red again.", "바렌카는 자기 심장 뛰는 소리가 들릴 만큼 가슴이 두근거렸고, 얼굴이 붉어졌다 하얘졌다 다시 붉어지는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["To be the wife of a man like Koznishev, after her position with Madame Stahl, was to her imagination the height of happiness.", "슈탈 부인 곁에서 지내던 처지에서 벗어나 코즈니셰프 같은 사람의 아내가 된다는 것은, 그녀의 상상 속에서 더없는 행복이었다."], ["Besides, she was almost certain that she was in love with him.", "게다가 그녀는 자기가 그를 사랑하고 있다는 것을 거의 확신하고 있었다."], ["And this moment it would have to be decided.", "그리고 바로 지금 이 순간 그것이 결판날 참이었다."], ["She felt frightened.", "그녀는 겁이 났다."], ["She dreaded both his speaking and his not speaking.", "그가 말하는 것도, 말하지 않는 것도 다 두려웠다."]], [["Now or never it must be said—that Sergey Ivanovitch felt too.", "지금이 아니면 영영 말할 수 없으리라는 것을—세르게이 이바노비치 또한 느끼고 있었다."], ["Everything in the expression, the flushed cheeks and the downcast eyes of Varenka betrayed a painful suspense.", "바렌카의 표정에서, 발갛게 달아오른 뺨과 내리깐 눈에서, 모든 것이 괴로운 기다림을 드러내고 있었다."], ["Sergey Ivanovitch saw it and felt sorry for her.", "세르게이 이바노비치는 그것을 보고 그녀가 가엾어졌다."], ["He felt even that to say nothing now would be a slight to her.", "지금 아무 말도 하지 않는 것은 그녀에 대한 모욕이리라는 생각마저 들었다."], ["Rapidly in his own mind he ran over all the arguments in support of his decision.", "그는 자기 결심을 뒷받침하는 모든 근거를 마음속으로 재빨리 훑어보았다."], ["He even said over to himself the words in which he meant to put his offer, but instead of those words, some utterly unexpected reflection that occurred to him made him ask:", "청혼을 담아 전할 말까지 속으로 되뇌어 보았다. 그런데 그 말 대신, 전혀 뜻밖의 생각이 문득 떠올라 그는 이렇게 묻고 말았다."]], [["“What is the difference between the ‘birch’ mushroom and the ‘white’ mushroom?”", "\"'자작나무' 버섯과 '흰' 버섯은 어떻게 다른가요?\""]], [["Varenka’s lips quivered with emotion as she answered:", "바렌카의 입술이 감정으로 떨렸고, 그녀는 대답했다."]], [["“In the top part there is scarcely any difference, it’s in the stalk.”", "\"윗부분은 거의 차이가 없어요. 차이는 대궁에 있죠.\""]], [["And as soon as these words were uttered, both he and she felt that it was over, that what was to have been said would not be said; and their emotion, which had up to then been continually growing more intense, began to subside.", "그리고 이 말이 입 밖으로 나온 순간, 그도 그녀도 모든 게 끝났음을, 했어야 할 말은 끝내 나오지 않으리라는 것을 느꼈다. 그리고 그때까지 끊임없이 더 격렬해지기만 하던 그들의 감정은 가라앉기 시작했다."]], [["“The birch mushroom’s stalk suggests a dark man’s chin after two days without shaving,”", "\"자작나무 버섯 대궁을 보면 이틀쯤 면도를 안 한 가무잡잡한 사내의 턱이 떠오르는군요.\""], ["said Sergey Ivanovitch, speaking quite calmly now.", "세르게이 이바노비치는 이제 아주 차분하게 말했다."]], [["“Yes, that’s true,”", "\"맞아요, 정말 그래요.\""], ["answered Varenka smiling, and unconsciously the direction of their walk changed.", "바렌카가 미소 지으며 대답했다. 그러자 두 사람이 걷는 방향이 저도 모르게 바뀌었다."], ["They began to turn towards the children.", "그들은 아이들 쪽으로 발길을 돌리기 시작했다."], ["Varenka felt both sore and ashamed; at the same time she had a sense of relief.", "바렌카는 마음이 쓰리고 부끄러웠지만, 동시에 안도감을 느꼈다."]], [["When he had got home again and went over the whole subject, Sergey Ivanovitch thought his previous decision had been a mistaken one.", "세르게이 이바노비치는 집에 돌아와 이 모든 일을 다시 곱씹어 본 뒤, 앞서 내린 자기 결심이 잘못된 것이었다고 생각했다."], ["He could not be false to the memory of Marie.", "그는 마리의 추억을 저버릴 수 없었다."]], [["“Gently, children, gently!”", "\"살살, 얘들아, 살살!\""], ["Levin shouted quite angrily to the children, standing before his wife to protect her when the crowd of children flew with shrieks of delight to meet them.", "환호성을 지르며 달려드는 아이들 무리를 막으려 아내 앞을 가로막고 서서, 레빈은 자못 화난 듯이 아이들에게 소리쳤다."]], [["Behind the children Sergey Ivanovitch and Varenka walked out of the wood.", "아이들 뒤로 세르게이 이바노비치와 바렌카가 숲에서 걸어 나왔다."], ["Kitty had no need to ask Varenka; she saw from the calm and somewhat crestfallen faces of both that her plans had not come off.", "키티는 바렌카에게 물어볼 필요도 없었다. 두 사람의 차분하고 어딘가 풀이 죽은 얼굴을 보고, 자기 계획이 어긋났음을 알아차렸다."]], [["“Well?”", "\"어떻게 됐어?\""], ["her husband questioned her as they were going home again.", "다시 집으로 돌아가는 길에 남편이 그녀에게 물었다."]], [["“It doesn’t bite,”", "\"입질을 안 해.\""], ["said Kitty, her smile and manner of speaking recalling her father, a likeness Levin often noticed with pleasure.", "키티가 말했다. 그녀의 미소와 말투가 친정아버지를 떠올리게 했는데, 레빈은 그 닮은 모습을 보며 종종 흐뭇해하곤 했다."]], [["“How doesn’t bite?”", "\"입질을 안 하다니, 어떻게?\""]], [["“I’ll show you,”", "\"보여 줄게.\""], ["she said, taking her husband’s hand, lifting it to her mouth, and just faintly brushing it with closed lips.", "그녀는 남편의 손을 잡아 입가로 들어 올리고는, 다문 입술로 살며시 스치듯 댔다."], ["“Like a kiss on a priest’s hand.”", "\"신부님 손에 입맞춤하듯이 말이야.\""]], [["“Which didn’t it bite with?”", "\"어느 쪽이 입질을 안 했다는 거야?\""], ["he said, laughing.", "그가 웃으며 물었다."]], [["“Both.", "\"둘 다."], ["But it should have been like this....”", "하지만 이렇게 했어야 했는데……\""]], [["“There are some peasants coming....”", "\"저기 농부들이 오고 있어……\""]], [["“Oh, they didn’t see.”", "\"어머, 그 사람들은 못 봤어.\""]], [["# Chapter 6", "# Chapter 6"]], [["During the time of the children’s tea the grown-up people sat in the balcony and talked as though nothing had happened, though they all, especially Sergey Ivanovitch and Varenka, were very well aware that there had happened an event which, though negative, was of very great importance.", "아이들이 차를 마시는 동안 어른들은 발코니에 앉아 아무 일도 없었다는 듯이 이야기를 나누었지만, 사실 그들 모두, 특히 세르게이 이바노비치와 바렌카는 어떤 사건이, 비록 부정적인 것이긴 해도 대단히 중대한 사건이 일어났음을 잘 알고 있었다."], ["They both had the same feeling, rather like that of a schoolboy after an examination, which has left him in the same class or shut him out of the school forever.", "두 사람은 똑같은 감정을 느꼈는데, 그것은 시험을 치른 뒤 같은 학년에 그대로 남게 되었거나 학교에서 영영 쫓겨나게 된 학생의 심정과 비슷한 것이었다."], ["Everyone present, feeling too that something had happened, talked eagerly about extraneous subjects.", "그 자리에 있던 사람들도 무언가 일이 있었음을 느끼고는 부러 엉뚱한 화제를 들먹이며 열심히 떠들어 댔다."], ["Levin and Kitty were particularly happy and conscious of their love that evening.", "레빈과 키티는 그날 저녁 유난히 행복했고, 자기들의 사랑을 또렷이 의식했다."], ["And their happiness in their love seemed to imply a disagreeable slur on those who would have liked to feel the same and could not—and they felt a prick of conscience.", "그런데 그들의 그 행복한 사랑은 마치 똑같은 것을 느끼고 싶어도 느끼지 못하는 사람들에게 어떤 불쾌한 모욕을 가하는 것 같았고—그래서 그들은 양심에 찔리는 듯한 느낌을 받았다."]], [["“Mark my words, Alexander will not come,”", "\"내 말 명심해라, 알렉산드르는 안 올 거야.\""], ["said the old princess.", "노공작 부인이 말했다."]], [["That evening they were expecting Stepan Arkadyevitch to come down by train, and the old prince had written that possibly he might come too.", "그날 저녁 그들은 스테판 아르카지치가 기차로 내려오기를 기다리고 있었고, 노공작도 어쩌면 함께 올지 모른다는 편지를 보내온 터였다."]], [["“And I know why,”", "\"그리고 나는 그 까닭을 알지.\""], ["the princess went on; “he says that young people ought to be left alone for a while at first.”", "공작 부인이 말을 이었다. \"그이 말이, 젊은 사람들은 처음 얼마간은 그냥 둘이서만 지내게 해 줘야 한다는 거야.\""]], [["“But papa has left us alone.", "\"하지만 아빠는 우리를 그냥 두고 계셨잖아요."], ["We’ve never seen him,”", "한 번도 뵌 적이 없는걸요.\""], ["said Kitty.", "키티가 말했다."], ["“Besides, we’re not young people!—we’re old, married people by now.”", "\"게다가 우리는 젊은 사람들도 아니에요!—이젠 결혼한 늙은이들인걸요.\""]], [["“Only if he doesn’t come, I shall say good-bye to you children,”", "\"다만 그이가 안 오면, 너희 아이들에게 작별을 고해야겠구나.\""], ["said the princess, sighing mournfully.", "공작 부인이 서글프게 한숨지으며 말했다."]], [["“What nonsense, mamma!”", "\"무슨 말씀이세요, 엄마!\""], ["both the daughters fell upon her at once.", "두 딸이 동시에 그녀에게 달려들었다."]], [["“How do you suppose he is feeling?", "\"그이가 지금 어떤 심정일지 너희가 어떻게 알겠니?"], ["Why, now....”", "왜, 지금쯤…….\""]], [["And suddenly there was an unexpected quiver in the princess’s voice.", "그리고 별안간 공작 부인의 목소리가 뜻밖에 떨렸다."], ["Her daughters were silent, and looked at one another.", "두 딸은 입을 다물고 서로를 바라보았다."], ["“Maman always finds something to be miserable about,”", "'엄마는 늘 마음 아파할 거리를 찾아내신다니까.'"], ["they said in that glance.", "그 눈길은 그렇게 말하고 있었다."], ["They did not know that happy as the princess was in her daughter’s house, and useful as she felt herself to be there, she had been extremely miserable, both on her own account and her husband’s, ever since they had married their last and favorite daughter, and the old home had been left empty.", "그들은 알지 못했다. 공작 부인이 딸의 집에서 아무리 행복하고, 또 거기서 자신이 쓸모 있는 존재라고 아무리 느낀다 해도, 그들이 막내이자 사랑하는 딸을 시집보내고 옛집이 텅 비게 된 뒤로 그녀는 자기 자신을 위해서나 남편을 위해서나 몹시도 비참한 심정이었다는 것을."]], [["“What is it, Agafea Mihalovna?”", "\"무슨 일이에요, 아가피아 미하일로브나?\""], ["Kitty asked suddenly of Agafea Mihalovna, who was standing with a mysterious air, and a face full of meaning.", "키티가 무언가 의미심장한 표정으로, 사연이 가득한 얼굴을 한 채 서 있는 아가피아 미하일로브나에게 불쑥 물었다."]], [["“About supper.”", "\"저녁 식사 일로요.\""]], [["“Well, that’s right,”", "\"그래, 그게 좋겠네.\" 돌리가 말했다."], ["said Dolly; “you go and arrange about it, and I’ll go and hear Grisha repeat his lesson, or else he will have nothing done all day.”", "\"당신은 가서 그 일을 챙겨요, 나는 가서 그리샤가 숙제 외우는 걸 들어 봐야겠어요. 안 그러면 그 애가 온종일 아무것도 안 할 테니까.\""]], [["“That’s my lesson!", "\"그건 내 일이야!"], ["No, Dolly, I’m going,”", "아니야, 돌리, 내가 갈게.\""], ["said Levin, jumping up.", "레빈이 벌떡 일어서며 말했다."]], [["Grisha, who was by now at a high school, had to go over the lessons of the term in the summer holidays.", "이제 김나지움에 다니게 된 그리샤는 여름 방학 동안 학기 중에 배운 것들을 복습해야 했다."], ["Darya Alexandrovna, who had been studying Latin with her son in Moscow before, had made it a rule on coming to the Levins’ to go over with him, at least once a day, the most difficult lessons of Latin and arithmetic.", "전에 모스크바에서 아들과 라틴어를 공부했던 다리야 알렉산드로브나는 레빈 집안에 와서부터, 하루에 적어도 한 번씩 라틴어와 산수 중 가장 어려운 과목을 아들과 함께 복습하기로 정해 두었다."], ["Levin had offered to take her place, but the mother, having once overheard Levin’s lesson, and noticing that it was not given exactly as the teacher in Moscow had given it, said resolutely, though with much embarrassment and anxiety not to mortify Levin, that they must keep strictly to the book as the teacher had done, and that she had better undertake it again herself.", "레빈이 그녀 대신 해 주겠다고 나섰지만, 어머니는 한번 레빈의 수업을 우연히 엿듣고는 그것이 모스크바의 선생이 가르치던 방식과 정확히 같지 않다는 것을 알아채고서, 레빈의 자존심을 상하게 할까 봐 무척 당황하고 마음 졸이면서도 단호하게 말했다. 선생이 했던 대로 책에 엄격하게 맞추어 가야 하니, 자기가 다시 그 일을 맡는 편이 낫겠다고."], ["Levin was amazed both at Stepan Arkadyevitch, who, by neglecting his duty, threw upon the mother the supervision of studies of which she had no comprehension, and at the teachers for teaching the children so badly.", "레빈은 자기 의무를 소홀히 하여 그 공부를 도무지 이해도 못하는 어머니에게 떠넘긴 스테판 아르카지치에게도 놀랐고, 아이들을 그토록 형편없이 가르치는 선생들에게도 놀랐다."], ["But he promised his sister-in-law to give the lessons exactly as she wished.", "하지만 그는 처형에게 그녀가 원하는 대로 정확히 가르치겠다고 약속했다."], ["And he went on teaching Grisha, not in his own way, but by the book, and so took little interest in it, and often forgot the hour of the lesson.", "그래서 그는 자기 방식이 아니라 책에 맞추어 그리샤를 가르쳐 나갔고, 그 때문에 흥미가 거의 일지 않아 수업 시간을 자주 잊어버리곤 했다."], ["So it had been today.", "오늘도 그랬다."]], [["“No, I’m going, Dolly, you sit still,” he said.", "\"아니야, 내가 갈게, 돌리, 당신은 가만히 앉아 있어.\""], ["“We’ll do it all properly, like the book.", "그가 말했다. \"다 제대로, 책대로 할 거야."], ["Only when Stiva comes, and we go out shooting, then we shall have to miss it.”", "다만 스티바가 와서 우리가 사냥을 나가게 되면, 그땐 빼먹을 수밖에 없겠지만.\""]], [["And Levin went to Grisha.", "그리고 레빈은 그리샤에게로 갔다."]], [["Varenka was saying the same thing to Kitty.", "바렌카도 키티에게 똑같은 말을 하고 있었다."], ["Even in the happy, well-ordered household of the Levins Varenka had succeeded in making herself useful.", "행복하고 잘 정돈된 레빈 집안에서조차 바렌카는 자신을 쓸모 있게 만드는 데 성공한 터였다."]], [["“I’ll see to the supper, you sit still,”", "\"저녁 식사는 제가 챙길게요, 그냥 앉아 계세요.\""], ["she said, and got up to go to Agafea Mihalovna.", "그녀가 말하고는 아가피아 미하일로브나에게 가려고 일어섰다."]], [["“Yes, yes, most likely they’ve not been able to get chickens.", "\"네, 네, 아마 닭을 구하지 못했을 거예요."], ["If so, ours....”", "그렇다면 우리 집 것을…….\""]], [["“Agafea Mihalovna and I will see about it,”", "\"아가피아 미하일로브나하고 제가 알아볼게요.\""], ["and Varenka vanished with her.", "그러더니 바렌카는 그녀와 함께 사라졌다."]], [["“What a nice girl!”", "\"참 좋은 아가씨야!\""], ["said the princess.", "공작 부인이 말했다."]], [["“Not nice, maman; she’s an exquisite girl; there’s no one else like her.”", "\"좋은 정도가 아니에요, 엄마. 더없이 훌륭한 아가씨예요. 저런 사람은 또 없을 거예요.\""]], [["“So you are expecting Stepan Arkadyevitch today?”", "\"그래, 오늘 스테판 아르카지치를 기다리고 있는 거니?\""], ["said Sergey Ivanovitch, evidently not disposed to pursue the conversation about Varenka.", "세르게이 이바노비치가, 바렌카에 관한 이야기를 더 끌고 가고 싶지 않은 기색으로 말했다."], ["“It would be difficult to find two sons-in-law more unlike than yours,”", "\"너희 두 사위만큼 서로 닮지 않은 사람도 찾기 어려울 거다.\""], ["he said with a subtle smile.", "그가 묘한 미소를 지으며 말했다."], ["“One all movement, only living in society, like a fish in water; the other our Kostya, lively, alert, quick in everything, but as soon as he is in society, he either sinks into apathy, or struggles helplessly like a fish on land.”", "\"한 사람은 온통 활동적이라 물속의 물고기처럼 오로지 사교계에서만 살아가지. 다른 한 사람, 우리 코스챠는 활기차고 기민하고 무슨 일에든 재빠르지만, 막상 사교계에만 들어서면 무기력에 빠져 버리거나 뭍에 오른 물고기처럼 어쩔 줄 몰라 허우적댄단 말이야.\""]], [["“Yes, he’s very heedless,”", "\"그래요, 그 애는 정말 무심해요.\""], ["said the princess, addressing Sergey Ivanovitch.", "공작 부인이 세르게이 이바노비치를 향해 말했다."], ["“I’ve been meaning, indeed, to ask you to tell him that it’s out of the question for her”", "\"정말이지 나는 당신에게 그 애한테 좀 일러 달라고 부탁할 참이었어요."], ["(she indicated Kitty) “to stay here; that she positively must come to Moscow.", "저 애가\"(그녀는 키티를 가리켰다) \"여기 머무는 건 말도 안 되는 일이라고, 반드시 모스크바로 와야 한다고요."], ["He talks of getting a doctor down....”", "그 애는 의사를 이리로 불러오겠다는 말이나 하고 있으니…….\""]], [["“Maman, he’ll do everything; he has agreed to everything,”", "\"엄마, 그이는 뭐든 다 할 거예요. 다 동의했다고요.\""], ["Kitty said, angry with her mother for appealing to Sergey Ivanovitch to judge in such a matter.", "키티가 그런 문제에서 세르게이 이바노비치에게 판단을 구하는 어머니에게 화가 나서 말했다."]], [["In the middle of their conversation they heard the snorting of horses and the sound of wheels on the gravel.", "이야기를 나누던 도중 그들은 말이 콧김을 내뿜는 소리와 자갈길 위를 구르는 바퀴 소리를 들었다."], ["Dolly had not time to get up to go and meet her husband, when from the window of the room below, where Grisha was having his lesson, Levin leaped out and helped Grisha out after him.", "돌리가 미처 일어나 남편을 맞으러 나가기도 전에, 그리샤가 수업을 받고 있던 아래층 방의 창문으로 레빈이 뛰어나오더니 뒤따라 그리샤도 끄집어내려 주었다."]], [["“It’s Stiva!”", "\"스티바야!\""], ["Levin shouted from under the balcony.", "레빈이 발코니 아래에서 외쳤다."], ["“We’ve finished, Dolly, don’t be afraid!”", "\"우리는 다 끝냈어, 돌리, 걱정 마!\""], ["he added, and started running like a boy to meet the carriage.", "그는 이렇게 덧붙이고는 마차를 맞으러 소년처럼 달려가기 시작했다."]], [["“Is ea id, ejus, ejus, ejus!”", "\"Is ea id, ejus, ejus, ejus!\""], ["shouted Grisha, skipping along the avenue.", "그리샤가 가로수길을 따라 깡충깡충 뛰어가며 소리쳤다."]], [["“And someone else too!", "* 라틴어 격변화 연습."], ["Papa, of course!”", "'그것이다, 그것의, 그것의, 그것의'라는 뜻."], ["cried Levin, stopping at the entrance of the avenue.", "\"누가 또 있어! 물론 아빠겠지!\""], ["“Kitty, don’t come down the steep staircase, go round.”", "레빈이 가로수길 어귀에 멈춰 서서 외쳤다. \"키티, 가파른 계단으로 내려오지 말고, 돌아서 와.\""]], [["But Levin had been mistaken in taking the person sitting in the carriage for the old prince.", "하지만 마차에 앉아 있는 사람을 노공작으로 본 것은 레빈의 착각이었다."], ["As he got nearer to the carriage he saw beside Stepan Arkadyevitch not the prince but a handsome, stout young man in a Scotch cap, with long ends of ribbon behind.", "마차에 가까이 다가서자 그는 스테판 아르카지치 곁에 노공작이 아니라, 뒤로 긴 리본 자락을 늘어뜨린 스코틀랜드 모자를 쓴 잘생기고 살집 좋은 젊은이가 앉아 있는 것을 보았다."], ["This was Vassenka Veslovsky, a distant cousin of the Shtcherbatskys, a brilliant young gentleman in Petersburg and Moscow society.", "이 사람은 셰르바츠키 집안의 먼 친척으로, 페테르부르크와 모스크바 사교계에서 잘나가는 멋진 젊은 신사인 바센카 베슬로프스키였다."], ["“A capital fellow, and a keen sportsman,”", "\"썩 괜찮은 친구이고, 열성적인 사냥꾼이지.\""], ["as Stepan Arkadyevitch said, introducing him.", "스테판 아르카지치가 그를 소개하며 그렇게 말했다."]], [["Not a whit abashed by the disappointment caused by his having come in place of the old prince, Veslovsky greeted Levin gaily, claiming acquaintance with him in the past, and snatching up Grisha into the carriage, lifted him over the pointer that Stepan Arkadyevitch had brought with him.", "노공작 대신 자기가 온 탓에 빚어진 실망에는 조금도 주눅 들지 않은 채, 베슬로프스키는 명랑하게 레빈에게 인사하며 예전부터 알던 사이라고 주장하고는, 그리샤를 와락 안아 마차 안으로 끌어올리더니 스테판 아르카지치가 데려온 포인터 너머로 그 애를 들어 옮겨 주었다."]], [["Levin did not get into the carriage, but walked behind.", "레빈은 마차에 타지 않고 뒤를 따라 걸었다."], ["He was rather vexed at the non-arrival of the old prince, whom he liked more and more the more he saw of him, and also at the arrival of this Vassenka Veslovsky, a quite uncongenial and superfluous person.", "그는 보면 볼수록 점점 더 좋아지던 노공작이 오지 않은 것이 못내 언짢았고, 또 이 바센카 베슬로프스키라는, 도무지 마음에 들지 않고 군더더기 같은 인물이 온 것도 언짢았다."], ["He seemed to him still more uncongenial and superfluous when, on approaching the steps where the whole party, children and grown-up, were gathered together in much excitement, Levin saw Vassenka Veslovsky, with a particularly warm and gallant air, kissing Kitty’s hand.", "그는, 아이들과 어른들 할 것 없이 온 식구가 잔뜩 들떠서 모여 있는 계단 가까이 다가갔을 때, 바센카 베슬로프스키가 유난히 다정하고 정중한 태도로 키티의 손에 입을 맞추는 것을 보고는 그가 한층 더 마음에 들지 않고 더욱 군더더기처럼 느껴졌다."]], [["“Your wife and I are cousins and very old friends,”", "\"댁의 부인과 저는 사촌간이고 아주 오래된 친구랍니다.\""], ["said Vassenka Veslovsky, once more shaking Levin’s hand with great warmth.", "바센카 베슬로프스키가 다시 한번 레빈의 손을 무척 따뜻하게 흔들며 말했다."]], [["“Well, are there plenty of birds?”", "\"그래, 새가 많은가?\""], ["Stepan Arkadyevitch said to Levin, hardly leaving time for everyone to utter their greetings.", "스테판 아르카지치가, 모두가 인사를 채 나눌 틈도 거의 주지 않은 채 레빈에게 말했다."], ["“We’ve come with the most savage intentions.", "\"우리는 더없이 사나운 마음을 먹고 왔다네."], ["Why, maman, they’ve not been in Moscow since!", "아니, 어머니, 저들은 그 후로 모스크바에 와 본 적도 없다니까요!"], ["Look, Tanya, here’s something for you!", "봐라, 타냐, 이건 네 거야!"], ["Get it, please, it’s in the carriage, behind!”", "자, 어서, 마차 안에, 뒤쪽에 있어!\""], ["he talked in all directions.", "그는 사방을 향해 떠들어 댔다."], ["“How pretty you’ve grown, Dolly,”", "\"참 예뻐졌네, 돌리.\""], ["he said to his wife, once more kissing her hand, holding it in one of his, and patting it with the other.", "그가 아내에게 말하며, 다시 한번 그녀의 손에 입을 맞추고는 그것을 한 손으로 쥔 채 다른 손으로 토닥였다."]], [["Levin, who a minute before had been in the happiest frame of mind, now looked darkly at everyone, and everything displeased him.", "조금 전까지만 해도 더없이 행복한 마음이던 레빈은, 이제 모든 사람을 어둡게 바라보았고, 모든 것이 못마땅했다."]], [["“Who was it he kissed yesterday with those lips?”", "'저 입술로 어제는 누구한테 입을 맞췄으려나?'"], ["he thought, looking at Stepan Arkadyevitch’s tender demonstrations to his wife.", "그는 아내에게 다정하게 구는 스테판 아르카지치를 바라보며 생각했다."], ["He looked at Dolly, and he did not like her either.", "그는 돌리를 바라보았고, 그녀 또한 마음에 들지 않았다."]], [["“She doesn’t believe in his love.", "'저 여자는 남편의 사랑을 믿지도 않잖아."], ["So what is she so pleased about?", "그런데 뭐가 저렇게 좋다는 거야?"], ["Revolting!”", "역겨워!'"], ["thought Levin.", "레빈은 생각했다."]], [["He looked at the princess, who had been so dear to him a minute before, and he did not like the manner in which she welcomed this Vassenka, with his ribbons, just as though she were in her own house.", "그는 조금 전까지만 해도 그토록 사랑스럽게 여겨지던 공작 부인을 바라보았는데, 마치 자기 집에 있기라도 한 듯 리본을 늘어뜨린 이 바센카를 맞아들이는 그녀의 태도가 마음에 들지 않았다."]], [["Even Sergey Ivanovitch, who had come out too onto the steps, seemed to him unpleasant with the show of cordiality with which he met Stepan Arkadyevitch, though Levin knew that his brother neither liked nor respected Oblonsky.", "심지어 역시 계단으로 나와 있던 세르게이 이바노비치마저도, 스테판 아르카지치를 맞이하며 보이는 다정한 태도가 그에게는 불쾌하게 느껴졌다. 형이 오블론스키를 좋아하지도 존경하지도 않는다는 것을 레빈은 알고 있었는데도 말이다."]], [["And Varenka, even she seemed hateful, with her air sainte nitouche making the acquaintance of this gentleman, while all the while she was thinking of nothing but getting married.", "그리고 바렌카, 그녀조차 미워 보였다. 늘 결혼할 생각밖에 없으면서도, 천연덕스럽게 새침을 떠는 태도로 이 신사와 처음 인사를 나누고 있었으니까."]], [["And more hateful than anyone was Kitty for falling in with the tone of gaiety with which this gentleman regarded his visit in the country, as though it were a holiday for himself and everyone else.", "* sainte nitouche: 프랑스어로 '내숭 떠는 여자, 새침데기'. 그러나 누구보다도 미운 사람은 키티였다."], ["And, above all, unpleasant was that particular smile with which she responded to his smile.", "이 신사가 시골 방문을 마치 자기와 모든 사람에게 휴일이라도 되는 양 명랑한 어조로 대하는데, 거기에 키티가 장단을 맞추고 있었기 때문이다. 그리고 무엇보다도, 그의 미소에 응답하는 그녀의 그 특유의 미소가 불쾌했다."]], [["Noisily talking, they all went into the house; but as soon as they were all seated, Levin turned and went out.", "다들 시끌벅적 떠들며 집 안으로 들어갔다. 하지만 모두가 자리에 앉자마자 레빈은 돌아서서 밖으로 나가 버렸다."]], [["Kitty saw something was wrong with her husband.", "키티는 남편에게 무언가 잘못된 게 있음을 알아챘다."], ["She tried to seize a moment to speak to him alone, but he made haste to get away from her, saying he was wanted at the counting-house.", "그녀는 그와 단둘이 이야기할 틈을 잡으려 했지만, 그는 사무실에서 자기를 찾는다는 핑계로 서둘러 그녀에게서 멀어졌다."], ["It was long since his own work on the estate had seemed to him so important as at that moment.", "그에게 자기 영지의 일이 지금 이 순간만큼 중요하게 여겨진 적은 오래도록 없었다."], ["“It’s all holiday for them,”", "'저들에게야 다 휴일이지.' 그는 생각했다."], ["he thought; “but these are no holiday matters, they won’t wait, and there’s no living without them.”", "'하지만 이것들은 휴일과는 상관없는 일이야. 기다려 주지도 않을뿐더러, 이게 없으면 살아갈 수가 없단 말이야.'"]], [["# Chapter 7", "# Chapter 7"]], [["Levin came back to the house only when they sent to summon him to supper.", "레빈은 저녁 식사에 부르러 사람을 보내고 나서야 집으로 돌아왔다."], ["On the stairs were standing Kitty and Agafea Mihalovna, consulting about wines for supper.", "계단 위에는 키티와 아가피아 미하일로브나가 서서 저녁 식사에 낼 포도주에 대해 의논하고 있었다."]], [["“But why are you making all this fuss?", "\"그런데 왜 이렇게 야단법석을 떨고 있는 거요?"], ["Have what we usually do.”", "평소대로 하면 되지.\""]], [["“No, Stiva doesn’t drink ... Kostya, stop, what’s the matter?”", "\"안 돼요, 스티바는 술을 안 마셔요......"], ["Kitty began, hurrying after him, but he strode ruthlessly away to the dining-room without waiting for her, and at once joined in the lively general conversation which was being maintained there by Vassenka Veslovsky and Stepan Arkadyevitch.", "코스챠, 잠깐만, 무슨 일이에요?\" 키티가 그를 뒤따라 서두르며 말을 꺼냈지만, 그는 그녀를 기다리지도 않고 무자비하게 성큼성큼 식당으로 가 버렸고, 거기서 바센카 베슬로프스키와 스테판 아르카지치가 활기차게 이어 가던 대화에 곧장 끼어들었다."]], [["“Well, what do you say, are we going shooting tomorrow?”", "\"그래, 어떻게 생각해, 내일 사냥을 갈까?\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch.", "스테판 아르카지치가 말했다."]], [["“Please, do let’s go,”", "\"제발, 갑시다.\""], ["said Veslovsky, moving to another chair, where he sat down sideways, with one fat leg crossed under him.", "베슬로프스키가 다른 의자로 옮겨 앉으며 말했다. 그는 통통한 다리 한쪽을 아래로 접어 깔고 옆으로 비스듬히 앉았다."]], [["“I shall be delighted, we will go.", "\"기꺼이 가지요, 갑시다."], ["And have you had any shooting yet this year?”", "그런데 올해 사냥은 좀 해 봤습니까?\""], ["said Levin to Veslovsky, looking intently at his leg, but speaking with that forced amiability that Kitty knew so well in him, and that was so out of keeping with him.", "레빈이 베슬로프스키에게 말했다. 그의 다리를 뚫어지게 바라보면서, 키티가 그에게서 너무도 잘 알고 있는, 그리고 그에게는 너무도 어울리지 않는 그 억지스러운 상냥함을 담아 말했다."], ["“I can’t answer for our finding grouse, but there are plenty of snipe.", "\"들꿩을 찾을 수 있을지는 장담 못 하지만, 도요새는 잔뜩 있어요."], ["Only we ought to start early.", "다만 일찍 나서야 합니다."], ["You’re not tired?", "피곤하지 않으십니까?"], ["Aren’t you tired, Stiva?”", "스티바, 자넨 피곤하지 않나?\""]], [["“Me tired?", "\"내가 피곤하다고?"], ["I’ve never been tired yet.", "난 아직 한 번도 피곤했던 적이 없어."], ["Suppose we stay up all night.", "밤을 꼬박 새워도 좋아."], ["Let’s go for a walk!”", "산책이나 가자고!\""]], [["“Yes, really, let’s not go to bed at all!", "\"네, 정말, 아예 잠을 자지 맙시다!"], ["Capital!”", "좋군요!\""], ["Veslovsky chimed in.", "베슬로프스키가 맞장구쳤다."]], [["“Oh, we all know you can do without sleep, and keep other people up too,”", "\"오, 당신이 잠 안 자고 버틸 수 있다는 것, 그리고 다른 사람들까지 못 자게 한다는 것은 우리 모두가 잘 알지요.\""], ["Dolly said to her husband, with that faint note of irony in her voice which she almost always had now with her husband.", "돌리가 남편에게 말했다. 요즘 남편을 대할 때면 거의 늘 묻어나던 그 희미한 비꼼의 어조였다."], ["“But to my thinking, it’s time for bed now....", "\"하지만 내 생각엔 이제 잘 시간이에요......"], ["I’m going, I don’t want supper.”", "난 가겠어요, 저녁 식사는 안 할래요.\""]], [["“No, do stay a little, Dolly,”", "\"아니, 좀 더 있어요, 돌리.\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, going round to her side behind the table where they were having supper.", "스테판 아르카지치가 그들이 저녁 식사를 하고 있던 식탁 뒤로 돌아 그녀 곁으로 가며 말했다."], ["“I’ve so much still to tell you.”", "\"아직 당신한테 할 얘기가 잔뜩 있단 말이오.\""]], [["“Nothing really, I suppose.”", "\"별것도 아닐 텐데요.\""]], [["“Do you know Veslovsky has been at Anna’s, and he’s going to them again?", "\"베슬로프스키가 안나한테 다녀왔고, 또 그쪽으로 갈 거라는 거 알아요?"], ["You know they’re hardly fifty miles from you, and I too must certainly go over there.", "당신도 알다시피 그 사람들이 당신네서 오십 베르스타도 안 되는 곳에 있어서, 나도 꼭 한번 다녀와야 해."], ["Veslovsky, come here!”", "베슬로프스키, 이리 와요!\""]], [["Vassenka crossed over to the ladies, and sat down beside Kitty.", "바센카는 부인들 쪽으로 건너가 키티 옆에 앉았다."]], [["“Ah, do tell me, please; you have stayed with her?", "\"아, 제발 좀 얘기해 주세요. 그분과 함께 지내셨다고요?"], ["How was she?”", "그분은 어떻던가요?\""], ["Darya Alexandrovna appealed to him.", "다리아 알렉산드로브나가 그에게 청했다."]], [["Levin was left at the other end of the table, and though never pausing in his conversation with the princess and Varenka, he saw that there was an eager and mysterious conversation going on between Stepan Arkadyevitch, Dolly, Kitty, and Veslovsky.", "레빈은 식탁의 다른 쪽 끝에 남겨졌고, 공작 부인 및 바렌카와의 대화를 한순간도 멈추지 않으면서도, 스테판 아르카지치와 돌리와 키티와 베슬로프스키 사이에 무언가 열띤, 그리고 은밀한 대화가 오가고 있음을 알아챘다."], ["And that was not all.", "그게 다가 아니었다."], ["He saw on his wife’s face an expression of real feeling as she gazed with fixed eyes on the handsome face of Vassenka, who was telling them something with great animation.", "그는 아내의 얼굴에서 진정한 감정의 표정을 보았다. 아내는 무언가를 신이 나서 떠들어 대는 바센카의 잘생긴 얼굴을 한 점에 시선을 고정한 채 응시하고 있었다."]], [["“It’s exceedingly nice at their place,”", "\"그분들 댁은 더없이 좋습니다.\""], ["Veslovsky was telling them about Vronsky and Anna.", "베슬로프스키가 브론스키와 안나에 대해 이야기하고 있었다."], ["“I can’t, of course, take it upon myself to judge, but in their house you feel the real feeling of home.”", "\"물론 제가 함부로 판단할 처지는 아니지만, 그 댁에서는 진짜 가정의 정취가 느껴지더군요.\""]], [["“What do they intend doing?”", "\"그분들은 어쩔 작정인가요?\""]], [["“I believe they think of going to Moscow.”", "\"제 생각엔 모스크바로 갈 마음인 것 같습니다.\""]], [["“How jolly it would be for us all to go over to them together!", "\"우리 다 함께 그쪽으로 가면 얼마나 즐거울까!"], ["When are you going there?”", "자넨 언제 거기 가나?\""], ["Stepan Arkadyevitch asked Vassenka.", "스테판 아르카지치가 바센카에게 물었다."]], [["“I’m spending July there.”", "\"전 칠월을 거기서 보낼 겁니다.\""]], [["“Will you go?”", "\"당신은 가겠소?\""], ["Stepan Arkadyevitch said to his wife.", "스테판 아르카지치가 아내에게 말했다."]], [["“I’ve been wanting to a long while; I shall certainly go,”", "\"오래전부터 가고 싶었어요. 꼭 가겠어요.\""], ["said Dolly.", "돌리가 말했다."], ["“I am sorry for her, and I know her.", "\"난 그 사람이 가엾고, 또 그 사람을 잘 알아요."], ["She’s a splendid woman.", "멋진 여인이죠."], ["I will go alone, when you go back, and then I shall be in no one’s way.", "당신이 돌아가면 나 혼자 갈 테니, 그러면 아무한테도 폐가 되지 않겠죠."], ["And it will be better indeed without you.”", "사실 당신이 없는 편이 더 나을 거예요.\""]], [["“To be sure,”", "\"그야 물론이지.\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch.", "스테판 아르카지치가 말했다."], ["“And you, Kitty?”", "\"그럼 자넨, 키티?\""]], [["“I? Why should I go?”", "\"제가요?"], ["Kitty said, flushing all over, and she glanced round at her husband.", "제가 왜 가요?\" 키티가 온통 얼굴을 붉히며 말하더니, 남편을 돌아보았다."]], [["“Do you know Anna Arkadyevna, then?”", "\"그럼 안나 아르카지예브나를 아십니까?\""], ["Veslovsky asked her.", "베슬로프스키가 그녀에게 물었다."], ["“She’s a very fascinating woman.”", "\"정말 매혹적인 여인이지요.\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["she answered Veslovsky, crimsoning still more.", "그녀가 더욱더 새빨개진 얼굴로 베슬로프스키에게 대답했다."], ["She got up and walked across to her husband.", "그녀는 일어나 남편 쪽으로 걸어갔다."]], [["“Are you going shooting, then, tomorrow?”", "\"그럼 내일 사냥을 가시나요?\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["His jealousy had in these few moments, especially at the flush that had overspread her cheeks while she was talking to Veslovsky, gone far indeed.", "이 몇 분 사이에, 특히 그녀가 베슬로프스키와 이야기하는 동안 두 뺨에 번지던 그 홍조를 보면서, 그의 질투는 정말로 멀리까지 치달아 있었다."], ["Now as he heard her words, he construed them in his own fashion.", "이제 그녀의 말을 듣자, 그는 그것을 제 나름대로 해석했다."], ["Strange as it was to him afterwards to recall it, it seemed to him at the moment clear that in asking whether he was going shooting, all she cared to know was whether he would give that pleasure to Vassenka Veslovsky, with whom, as he fancied, she was in love.", "나중에 떠올리면 스스로도 이상했지만, 그 순간 그에게는 분명해 보였다. 그녀가 사냥을 가느냐고 묻는 것은, 오직 그가, 그녀가 사랑에 빠졌다고 그가 상상하던 그 바센카 베슬로프스키에게 그 즐거움을 안겨 줄 것인지를 알고 싶어서일 뿐이라고."]], [["“Yes, I’m going,”", "\"그래, 갈 거요.\""], ["he answered her in an unnatural voice, disagreeable to himself.", "그는 부자연스러운, 스스로에게도 불쾌한 목소리로 대답했다."]], [["“No, better spend the day here tomorrow, or Dolly won’t see anything of her husband, and set off the day after,”", "\"아니에요, 내일은 여기서 하루 보내는 게 더 좋아요. 안 그러면 돌리가 남편 얼굴도 못 볼 거고요."], ["said Kitty.", "그러고 나서 모레 떠나세요.\" 키티가 말했다."]], [["The motive of Kitty’s words was interpreted by Levin thus: “Don’t separate me from him.", "키티의 말의 동기를 레빈은 이렇게 해석했다. '나를 그이한테서 떼어 놓지 마요."], ["I don’t care about your going, but do let me enjoy the society of this delightful young man.”", "당신이 가는 거야 상관없지만, 이 즐거운 청년과 함께 어울리는 건 좀 즐기게 해 줘요.'"]], [["“Oh, if you wish, we’ll stay here tomorrow,”", "\"오, 당신이 원한다면 내일은 여기 머물지.\""], ["Levin answered, with peculiar amiability.", "레빈이 유난히 상냥하게 대답했다."]], [["Vassenka meanwhile, utterly unsuspecting the misery his presence had occasioned, got up from the table after Kitty, and watching her with smiling and admiring eyes, he followed her.", "한편 바센카는 자기 존재가 빚어낸 괴로움 따위는 전혀 눈치채지 못한 채, 키티를 뒤따라 식탁에서 일어나, 미소 띤 감탄 어린 눈으로 그녀를 바라보며 그 뒤를 따라갔다."]], [["Levin saw that look.", "레빈은 그 눈길을 보았다."], ["He turned white, and for a minute he could hardly breathe.", "그는 얼굴이 하얗게 질렸고, 한순간 숨조차 제대로 쉴 수 없었다."], ["“How dare he look at my wife like that!”", "'저자가 감히 어떻게 내 아내를 저런 눈으로 본단 말인가!'"], ["was the feeling that boiled within him.", "이것이 그의 안에서 끓어오르던 감정이었다."]], [["“Tomorrow, then?", "\"그럼 내일이죠?"], ["Do, please, let us go,”", "제발, 갑시다.\""], ["said Vassenka, sitting down on a chair, and again crossing his leg as his habit was.", "바센카가 의자에 앉으며, 또다시 버릇대로 다리를 꼬며 말했다."]], [["Levin’s jealousy went further still.", "레빈의 질투는 한층 더 멀리 나아갔다."], ["Already he saw himself a deceived husband, looked upon by his wife and her lover as simply necessary to provide them with the conveniences and pleasures of life....", "그는 벌써 자신을, 아내와 그 정부(情夫)에게는 그저 삶의 편의와 즐거움을 마련해 주는 데에나 필요한 존재로 여겨지는, 기만당한 남편으로 그려 보고 있었다......"], ["But in spite of that he made polite and hospitable inquiries of Vassenka about his shooting, his gun, and his boots, and agreed to go shooting next day.", "그러나 그럼에도 그는 바센카에게 사냥과 그의 총과 그의 장화에 대해 정중하고 정성스럽게 물었고, 다음 날 사냥을 가기로 동의했다."]], [["Happily for Levin, the old princess cut short his agonies by getting up herself and advising Kitty to go to bed.", "레빈에게는 다행스럽게도, 노공작 부인이 손수 일어나 키티에게 잘 시간이라고 권함으로써 그의 고통을 끊어 주었다."], ["But even at this point Levin could not escape another agony.", "하지만 이 대목에서도 레빈은 또 한 번의 고통을 피할 수 없었다."], ["As he said good-night to his hostess, Vassenka would again have kissed her hand, but Kitty, reddening, drew back her hand and said with a naïve bluntness, for which the old princess scolded her afterwards:", "그가 안주인에게 작별 인사를 할 때, 바센카가 또다시 그녀의 손에 입을 맞추려 했는데, 키티는 얼굴을 붉히며 손을 거두고는, 나중에 노공작 부인이 그 때문에 그녀를 나무라게 될 만큼 천진한 무뚝뚝함으로 말했다."]], [["“We don’t like that fashion.”", "\"우리는 그런 풍습을 좋아하지 않아요.\""]], [["In Levin’s eyes she was to blame for having allowed such relations to arise, and still more to blame for showing so awkwardly that she did not like them.", "레빈의 눈에는, 그런 관계가 생겨나도록 허락한 것부터가 그녀의 잘못이었고, 더구나 그것을 싫어한다는 티를 그토록 어색하게 낸 것은 더 큰 잘못이었다."]], [["“Why, how can one want to go to bed!”", "\"아니, 어떻게 잘 생각이 든단 말이오!\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, who, after drinking several glasses of wine at supper, was now in his most charming and sentimental humor.", "스테판 아르카지치가 말했다. 그는 저녁 식사 자리에서 포도주를 몇 잔 마신 뒤로, 지금은 더없이 매력적이고 감상적인 기분에 젖어 있었다."], ["“Look, Kitty,”", "\"이봐, 키티.\""], ["he said, pointing to the moon, which had just risen behind the lime trees—“how exquisite!", "그가 막 보리수나무 뒤로 떠오른 달을 가리키며 말했다. \"얼마나 황홀한가!"], ["Veslovsky, this is the time for a serenade.", "베슬로프스키, 지금이 세레나데를 부를 때야."], ["You know, he has a splendid voice; we practiced songs together along the road.", "알다시피 이 친구는 목소리가 기막히게 좋다네. 우리 오는 길에 함께 노래를 연습했지."], ["He has brought some lovely songs with him, two new ones.", "이 친구가 멋진 노래들을 가져왔어, 새 노래로 두 곡이나."], ["Varvara Andreevna and he must sing some duets.”", "바르바라 안드레예브나와 이 친구가 꼭 이중창을 불러야 해.\""]], [["When the party had broken up, Stepan Arkadyevitch walked a long while about the avenue with Veslovsky; their voices could be heard singing one of the new songs.", "자리가 파한 뒤에도 스테판 아르카지치는 베슬로프스키와 함께 한참을 가로수 길을 거닐었고, 그 새 노래들 중 한 곡을 부르는 두 사람의 목소리가 들려왔다."]], [["Levin hearing these voices sat scowling in an easy-chair in his wife’s bedroom, and maintained an obstinate silence when she asked him what was wrong.", "이 목소리를 들으며 레빈은 아내의 침실 안락의자에 앉아 인상을 찌푸린 채, 무슨 일이냐고 그녀가 물어도 고집스레 침묵을 지켰다."], ["But when at last with a timid glance she hazarded the question: “Was there perhaps something you disliked about Veslovsky?”—it all burst out, and he told her all.", "그러나 마침내 그녀가 머뭇거리는 눈길로 \"혹시 베슬로프스키한테 뭔가 마음에 안 드는 게 있었던 거예요?\" 하고 조심스레 물어보자, 모든 것이 터져 나왔고, 그는 그녀에게 다 털어놓았다."], ["He was humiliated himself at what he was saying, and that exasperated him all the more.", "그는 자기가 하는 말이 스스로도 굴욕스러웠고, 그 점이 그를 한층 더 격앙시켰다."]], [["He stood facing her with his eyes glittering menacingly under his scowling brows, and he squeezed his strong arms across his chest, as though he were straining every nerve to hold himself in.", "그는 찌푸린 눈썹 아래로 위협하듯 번뜩이는 눈으로 그녀를 마주한 채 서서, 마치 온 신경을 곤두세워 자신을 억누르기라도 하는 듯 억센 두 팔을 가슴에 엇갈려 꽉 조였다."], ["The expression of his face would have been grim, and even cruel, if it had not at the same time had a look of suffering which touched her.", "그의 얼굴 표정은 험상궂었을 것이고 잔인하기까지 했을 테지만, 동시에 그녀의 마음을 울리는 고통의 빛이 어려 있지 않았다면 그랬을 것이다."], ["His jaws were twitching, and his voice kept breaking.", "그의 턱은 씰룩거렸고, 목소리는 자꾸만 갈라졌다."]], [["“You must understand that I’m not jealous, that’s a nasty word.", "\"당신은 알아줘야 해, 나는 질투하는 게 아니야, 그건 추잡한 말이지."], ["I can’t be jealous, and believe that....", "나는 질투할 수가 없어, 그걸 믿어 줘......"], ["I can’t say what I feel, but this is awful....", "내가 느끼는 걸 말로는 못 하겠지만, 이건 끔찍해......"], ["I’m not jealous, but I’m wounded, humiliated that anybody dare think, that anybody dare look at you with eyes like that.”", "나는 질투하는 게 아니라, 누군가가 감히 그런 생각을 하고, 누군가가 감히 그런 눈으로 당신을 본다는 게 상처가 되고 굴욕스러운 거야.\""]], [["“Eyes like what?”", "\"그런 눈이라니, 어떤 눈요?\""], ["said Kitty, trying as conscientiously as possible to recall every word and gesture of that evening and every shade implied in them.", "키티가 그날 저녁의 말 한마디 한마디와 몸짓 하나하나, 거기에 담겼던 온갖 미묘한 기색까지 가능한 한 양심껏 떠올려 보려 애쓰며 말했다."]], [["At the very bottom of her heart she did think there had been something precisely at the moment when he had crossed over after her to the other end of the table; but she dared not own it even to herself, and would have been even more unable to bring herself to say so to him, and so increase his suffering.", "마음속 가장 깊은 바닥에서는, 그가 그녀를 뒤따라 식탁의 다른 쪽 끝으로 건너왔던 바로 그 순간에 정말 무언가가 있었다고 그녀도 생각했다. 하지만 그녀는 자신에게조차 그것을 인정할 엄두를 내지 못했고, 더구나 그것을 그에게 말해 그의 고통을 키우는 일은 도무지 입에 올릴 수도 없었다."]], [["“And what can there possibly be attractive about me as I am now?...”", "\"게다가 지금의 내 모습에 도대체 무슨 매력적인 데가 있을 수 있겠어요?.....\""]], [["“Ah!”", "\"아!\""], ["he cried, clutching at his head, “you shouldn’t say that!...", "그가 두 손으로 머리를 움켜쥐며 외쳤다. \"그런 말 하면 안 돼!......"], ["If you had been attractive then....”", "만약 그때 당신이 매력적이었다면......\""]], [["“Oh, no, Kostya, oh, wait a minute, oh, do listen!”", "\"오, 아니에요, 코스챠, 오, 잠깐만요, 오, 좀 들어 봐요!\""], ["she said, looking at him with an expression of pained commiseration.", "그녀가 아파하는 연민의 표정으로 그를 바라보며 말했다."], ["“Why, what can you be thinking about!", "\"아니, 도대체 무슨 생각을 하는 거예요!"], ["When for me there’s no one in the world, no one, no one!...", "나한테는 이 세상에 아무도 없는데, 아무도, 아무도 없는데!......"], ["Would you like me never to see anyone?”", "내가 아무도 만나지 않았으면 좋겠어요?\""]], [["For the first minute she had been offended at his jealousy; she was angry that the slightest amusement, even the most innocent, should be forbidden her; but now she would readily have sacrificed, not merely such trifles, but everything, for his peace of mind, to save him from the agony he was suffering.", "처음 한순간 그녀는 그의 질투에 기분이 상했다. 아무리 사소한, 더없이 순수한 즐거움까지 자기에게 금지된다는 게 화가 났다. 하지만 지금은 그의 마음의 평안을 위해서라면, 그가 겪고 있는 고통에서 그를 구해 내기 위해서라면, 그런 사소한 것들뿐 아니라 모든 것이라도 기꺼이 희생할 수 있었다."]], [["“You must understand the horror and comedy of my position,”", "\"내 처지의 끔찍함과 우스꽝스러움을 당신은 알아줘야 해.\""], ["he went on in a desperate whisper; “that he’s in my house, that he’s done nothing improper positively except his free and easy airs and the way he sits on his legs.", "그가 절망적인 속삭임으로 말을 이었다. \"그자가 내 집에 있다는 것, 그런데 그자가 격식 없이 굴고 다리를 꼬고 앉는 것 말고는 딱히 부적절한 짓이라곤 한 게 없다는 것 말이야."], ["He thinks it’s the best possible form, and so I’m obliged to be civil to him.”", "그자는 그게 더없이 세련된 태도라고 여기니까, 나로서는 그자한테 정중하게 굴 수밖에 없는 거지.\""]], [["“But, Kostya, you’re exaggerating,”", "\"하지만 코스챠, 당신은 과장하고 있어요.\""], ["said Kitty, at the bottom of her heart rejoicing at the depth of his love for her, shown now in his jealousy.", "키티가 말했다. 마음속 깊은 곳에서는 이제 그의 질투를 통해 드러난 자기에 대한 그 사랑의 깊이에 기뻐하면서."]], [["“The most awful part of it all is that you’re just as you always are, and especially now when to me you’re something sacred, and we’re so happy, so particularly happy—and all of a sudden a little wretch....", "\"이 모든 일에서 가장 끔찍한 부분은, 당신은 늘 그대로의 당신인데, 더구나 지금처럼 나한테 당신이 무언가 성스러운 존재이고, 우리가 이토록 행복한데, 이토록 각별히 행복한데—그런데 별안간 한 하찮은 녀석이......"], ["He’s not a little wretch; why should I abuse him?", "그자는 하찮은 녀석이 아니야. 내가 왜 그자를 욕하겠어?"], ["I have nothing to do with him.", "난 그자와 아무 상관도 없어."], ["But why should my, and your, happiness....”", "하지만 왜 나의, 그리고 당신의 행복이......\""]], [["“Do you know, I understand now what it’s all come from,”", "\"있잖아요, 난 이제 이게 다 어디서 비롯된 건지 알겠어요.\""], ["Kitty was beginning.", "키티가 말문을 열었다."]], [["“Well, what?", "\"그래, 뭔데?"], ["what?”", "뭔데?\""]], [["“I saw how you looked while we were talking at supper.”", "\"우리가 저녁 식사 때 이야기하는 동안 당신이 어떤 표정이었는지 봤어요.\""]], [["“Well, well!”", "\"그래, 그래서!\""], ["Levin said in dismay.", "레빈이 당혹스러워하며 말했다."]], [["She told him what they had been talking about.", "그녀는 자기들이 무슨 이야기를 나누고 있었는지 그에게 말해 주었다."], ["And as she told him, she was breathless with emotion.", "그러면서 그녀는 감정에 북받쳐 숨이 가빴다."], ["Levin was silent for a space, then he scanned her pale and distressed face, and suddenly he clutched at his head.", "레빈은 한동안 잠자코 있다가, 창백하고 괴로워하는 그녀의 얼굴을 살피더니, 별안간 두 손으로 머리를 움켜쥐었다."]], [["“Katya, I’ve been worrying you!", "\"카챠, 내가 당신을 괴롭혔구려!"], ["Darling, forgive me!", "여보, 용서해 줘!"], ["It’s madness!", "이건 미친 짓이야!"], ["Katya, I’m a criminal.", "카챠, 난 죄인이야."], ["And how could you be so distressed at such idiocy?”", "그런데 어떻게 그런 바보 같은 일로 당신이 그토록 괴로워했단 말이오?\""]], [["“Oh, I was sorry for you.”", "\"오, 난 당신이 가여웠어요.\""]], [["“For me?", "\"내가? 나를?"], ["for me? How mad I am!...", "내가 얼마나 미친놈인지!......"], ["But why make you miserable?", "그런데 왜 당신을 비참하게 만든단 말이오?"], ["It’s awful to think that any outsider can shatter our happiness.”", "어떤 외부 사람이 우리의 행복을 깨뜨릴 수 있다고 생각하는 것만으로도 끔찍해.\""]], [["“It’s humiliating too, of course.”", "\"물론 굴욕스럽기도 하고요.\""]], [["“Oh, then I’ll keep him here all the summer, and will overwhelm him with civility,”", "\"오, 그렇다면 여름 내내 그자를 여기 붙들어 두고, 정중함으로 그자를 흠뻑 젖게 해 주겠어.\""], ["said Levin, kissing her hands.", "레빈이 그녀의 두 손에 입을 맞추며 말했다."], ["“You shall see.", "\"두고 봐."], ["Tomorrow....", "내일은......"], ["Oh, yes, we are going tomorrow.”", "오, 그래, 우리 내일 가는 거야.\""]], [["# Chapter 8", "# Chapter 8"]], [["Next day, before the ladies were up, the wagonette and a trap for the shooting party were at the door, and Laska, aware since early morning that they were going shooting, after much whining and darting to and fro, had sat herself down in the wagonette beside the coachman, and, disapproving of the delay, was excitedly watching the door from which the sportsmen still did not come out.", "이튿날, 부인들이 일어나기도 전에 사냥 갈 사람들을 태울 마차와 짐수레가 문 앞에 대기하고 있었다. 라스카는 이른 아침부터 사냥을 나간다는 걸 눈치채고는, 한참 동안 낑낑대며 이리저리 뛰어다니다가 마차의 마부 옆에 자리를 잡고 앉아, 늑장 부리는 꼴이 못마땅하다는 듯, 사냥꾼들이 아직도 나오지 않는 문을 흥분한 눈빛으로 지켜보고 있었다."], ["The first to come out was Vassenka Veslovsky, in new high boots that reached half-way up his thick thighs, in a green blouse, with a new Russian leather cartridge-belt, and in his Scotch cap with ribbons, with a brand-new English gun without a sling.", "맨 먼저 나온 것은 바센카 베슬로프스키였는데, 그는 굵은 허벅지의 절반까지 올라오는 새 장화에 초록색 블라우스를 걸치고, 새 러시아가죽 탄띠를 두르고, 리본이 달린 스코틀랜드식 모자를 쓰고, 멜빵끈도 없는 아주 새 영국제 총을 들고 있었다."], ["Laska flew up to him, welcomed him, and jumping up, asked him in her own way whether the others were coming soon, but getting no answer from him, she returned to her post of observation and sank into repose again, her head on one side, and one ear pricked up to listen.", "라스카는 그에게 날듯이 달려가 반기며, 펄쩍펄쩍 뛰어 제 나름의 방식으로 다른 사람들은 곧 나오느냐고 물었지만, 그에게서 아무 대답도 얻지 못하자 다시 제 감시 자리로 돌아가, 고개를 한쪽으로 갸웃이 기울이고 한쪽 귀를 쫑긋 세운 채 가만히 자리를 잡았다."], ["At last the door opened with a creak, and Stepan Arkadyevitch’s spot-and-tan pointer Krak flew out, running round and round and turning over in the air.", "마침내 문이 삐걱 소리를 내며 열리더니, 스테판 아르카지치의 얼룩무늬에 황갈색이 섞인 포인터 크라크가 빙글빙글 돌며 공중에서 몸을 뒤집으며 튀어나왔다."], ["Stepan Arkadyevitch himself followed with a gun in his hand and a cigar in his mouth.", "그 뒤로 스테판 아르카지치 본인이 손에 총을 들고 입에 시가를 문 채 따라 나왔다."]], [["“Good dog, good dog, Krak!”", "\"착하지, 착해, 크라크!\""], ["he cried encouragingly to the dog, who put his paws up on his chest, catching at his game bag.", "그는 두 앞발을 자기 가슴에 올리며 사냥 자루를 물려고 드는 개에게 다독이듯 외쳤다."], ["Stepan Arkadyevitch was dressed in rough leggings and spats, in torn trousers and a short coat.", "스테판 아르카지치는 투박한 각반과 덧신을 차고, 해진 바지에 짧은 외투를 걸치고 있었다."], ["On his head there was a wreck of a hat of indefinite form, but his gun of a new patent was a perfect gem, and his game bag and cartridge belt, though worn, were of the very best quality.", "머리에는 형체를 가늠하기 어려운, 거의 망가진 모자가 얹혀 있었지만, 새로 특허받은 그의 총은 더없는 일품이었고, 사냥 자루와 탄띠도 비록 낡긴 했어도 최상급이었다."]], [["Vassenka Veslovsky had had no notion before that it was truly chic for a sportsman to be in tatters, but to have his shooting outfit of the best quality.", "바센카 베슬로프스키는 사냥꾼이 누더기 차림을 하되 사냥 장비만큼은 최고급으로 갖추는 것이야말로 진정한 chic라는 것을 그전까지 미처 알지 못했다."], ["He saw it now as he looked at Stepan Arkadyevitch, radiant in his rags, graceful, well-fed, and joyous, a typical Russian nobleman.", "누더기 차림에도 빛나는, 우아하고 잘 먹어 윤기가 흐르고 쾌활한, 전형적인 러시아 귀족 스테판 아르카지치를 바라보면서 그는 이제 그것을 깨달았다."], ["And he made up his mind that next time he went shooting he would certainly adopt the same get-up.", "그래서 다음번에 사냥을 나올 때는 반드시 똑같은 차림을 하리라고 마음먹었다. * chic: 프랑스어로 '멋', '세련됨'."]], [["“Well, and what about our host?”", "\"그런데 우리 주인장은 어떻게 된 거요?\""], ["he asked.", "그가 물었다."]], [["“A young wife,”", "\"젊은 아내가 있잖소.\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, smiling.", "스테판 아르카지치가 미소를 지으며 말했다."]], [["“Yes, and such a charming one!”", "\"그렇지, 게다가 그토록 매력적인 아내가!\""]], [["“He came down dressed.", "\"옷은 다 갖춰 입고 내려왔었소."], ["No doubt he’s run up to her again.”", "분명 또 아내한테 올라간 거겠지.\""]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch guessed right.", "스테판 아르카지치의 짐작이 맞았다."], ["Levin had run up again to his wife to ask her once more if she forgave him for his idiocy yesterday, and, moreover, to beg her for Christ’s sake to be more careful.", "레빈은 다시 아내에게 올라가, 어제 자기가 저지른 어리석은 짓을 용서해 주었는지 한 번 더 물었고, 더구나 제발 좀 더 조심해 달라고 간청했던 것이다."], ["The great thing was for her to keep away from the children—they might any minute push against her.", "무엇보다 중요한 건 아내가 아이들 곁에서 떨어져 있는 것이었다. 아이들이 언제 그녀에게 부딪칠지 모를 일이었다."], ["Then he had once more to hear her declare that she was not angry with him for going away for two days, and to beg her to be sure to send him a note next morning by a servant on horseback, to write him, if it were but two words only, to let him know that all was well with her.", "그러고 나서 그는 이틀 동안 집을 비우는 데 대해 화가 난 게 아니라는 그녀의 말을 다시 한번 들어야 했고, 내일 아침 반드시 하인을 말에 태워 쪽지를 보내 달라고, 단 두 마디만이라도 좋으니 자기에게 잘 지낸다는 걸 알려 달라고 부탁했다."]], [["Kitty was distressed, as she always was, at parting for a couple of days from her husband, but when she saw his eager figure, looking big and strong in his shooting-boots and his white blouse, and a sort of sportsman elation and excitement incomprehensible to her, she forgot her own chagrin for the sake of his pleasure, and said good-bye to him cheerfully.", "키티는 며칠이나마 남편과 헤어지는 것이 늘 그렇듯 마음이 아팠지만, 사냥 장화를 신고 흰 블라우스를 걸친 그의 모습이 듬직하고 강건해 보였고, 그녀로서는 이해할 수 없는 사냥꾼 특유의 들뜸과 흥분이 어려 있는 것을 보자, 그의 즐거움을 위해 자기 서운함을 잊고 명랑하게 작별 인사를 했다."]], [["“Pardon, gentlemen!”", "\"미안하오, 여러분!\""], ["he said, running out onto the steps.", "그는 현관 계단으로 뛰어나오며 말했다."], ["“Have you put the lunch in?", "\"점심은 실었소?"], ["Why is the chestnut on the right?", "왜 밤색 말이 오른쪽에 있지?"], ["Well, it doesn’t matter.", "뭐, 상관없군."], ["Laska, down; go and lie down!”", "라스카, 엎드려, 가서 누워 있어!\""]], [["“Put it with the herd of oxen,”", "\"황소 떼한테 갖다 넣어 두게.\""], ["he said to the herdsman, who was waiting for him at the steps with some question.", "그는 무슨 일로 물어볼 게 있어 계단에서 그를 기다리고 있던 목동에게 말했다."], ["“Excuse me, here comes another villain.”", "\"실례하오, 또 다른 골칫거리가 오는군.\""]], [["Levin jumped out of the wagonette, in which he had already taken his seat, to meet the carpenter, who came towards the steps with a rule in his hand.", "레빈은 이미 자리를 잡고 앉았던 마차에서 뛰어내려, 손에 자를 들고 계단 쪽으로 다가오는 목수를 맞이했다."]], [["“You didn’t come to the counting house yesterday, and now you’re detaining me.", "\"어제 사무소에 오지 않더니, 이제 와서 날 붙잡는군."], ["Well, what is it?”", "그래, 무슨 일인가?\""]], [["“Would your honor let me make another turning?", "\"나리, 한 번 더 꺾어 돌게 해 주시면 안 되겠습니까요?"], ["It’s only three steps to add.", "계단 세 칸만 더 붙이면 됩니다요."], ["And we make it just fit at the same time.", "그러면 동시에 딱 맞게 됩니다요."], ["It will be much more convenient.”", "훨씬 더 편할 겁니다요.\""]], [["“You should have listened to me,”", "\"내 말을 들었어야지.\""], ["Levin answered with annoyance.", "레빈이 짜증스럽게 대답했다."], ["“I said: Put the lines and then fit in the steps.", "\"내가 말했지 않나. 먼저 선을 긋고 나서 계단을 맞추라고."], ["Now there’s no setting it right.", "이제는 바로잡을 수가 없어."], ["Do as I told you, and make a new staircase.”", "내가 시킨 대로 새 계단을 만들게.\""]], [["The point was that in the lodge that was being built the carpenter had spoiled the staircase, fitting it together without calculating the space it was to fill, so that the steps were all sloping when it was put in place.", "문제는 짓고 있던 별채에서 목수가 계단을 망쳐 놓은 데 있었다. 들어갈 공간을 계산하지도 않고 짜 맞추는 바람에, 제자리에 놓고 보니 계단이 죄다 기울어져 있었던 것이다."], ["Now the carpenter wanted, keeping the same staircase, to add three steps.", "이제 목수는 같은 계단을 그대로 두고 거기에 세 칸을 덧붙이고 싶어 했다."]], [["“It will be much better.”", "\"훨씬 나아질 겁니다요.\""]], [["“But where’s your staircase coming out with its three steps?”", "\"그래, 그럼 그 세 칸을 덧붙인 자네 계단은 어디로 나오나?\""]], [["“Why, upon my word, sir,”", "\"아니, 나리, 정말이지.\""], ["the carpenter said with a contemptuous smile.", "목수가 깔보는 듯한 미소를 지으며 말했다."], ["“It comes out right at the very spot.", "\"딱 제자리로 나옵니다요."], ["It starts, so to speak,”", "말하자면 여기서 시작해서요.\""], ["he said, with a persuasive gesture; “it comes down, and comes down, and comes out.”", "그는 설득하려는 듯 손짓을 하며 말을 이었다. \"내려오고, 또 내려와서, 이렇게 나오는 겁니다요.\""]], [["“But three steps will add to the length too ...", "\"하지만 세 칸이면 길이도 더 늘어날 텐데……"], ["where is it to come out?”", "그게 어디로 나온단 말인가?\""]], [["“Why, to be sure, it’ll start from the bottom and go up and go up, and come out so,”", "\"아니, 그러니까요, 밑에서 시작해서 올라가고, 또 올라가서, 이렇게 나옵니다요.\""], ["the carpenter said obstinately and convincingly.", "목수가 고집스럽게, 그러면서도 확신에 차서 말했다."]], [["“It’ll reach the ceiling and the wall.”", "\"그러면 천장이랑 벽에 닿을 걸세.\""]], [["“Upon my word!", "\"천만에요!"], ["Why, it’ll go up, and up, and come out like this.”", "아니, 올라가고, 또 올라가서, 이렇게 나온다니까요.\""]], [["Levin took out a ramrod and began sketching him the staircase in the dust.", "레빈은 꽂을대를 꺼내 흙바닥에 계단을 그려 보였다."]], [["“There, do you see?”", "\"자, 보이나?\""]], [["“As your honor likes,”", "\"나리 뜻대로 하십쇼.\""], ["said the carpenter, with a sudden gleam in his eyes, obviously understanding the thing at last.", "목수가 갑자기 눈을 반짝이며 말했는데, 마침내 그 이치를 깨달은 것이 분명했다."], ["“It seems it’ll be best to make a new one.”", "\"아무래도 새로 만드는 게 제일 낫겠습니다요.\""]], [["“Well, then, do it as you’re told,”", "\"그래, 그럼 시킨 대로 하게.\""], ["Levin shouted, seating himself in the wagonette.", "레빈이 마차에 올라앉으며 소리쳤다. \"엎드려!"], ["“Down! Hold the dogs, Philip!”", "개들을 붙잡아, 필립!\""]], [["Levin felt now at leaving behind all his family and household cares such an eager sense of joy in life and expectation that he was not disposed to talk.", "레빈은 이제 가족과 집안의 온갖 근심거리를 죄다 뒤로하고 떠나니, 삶에 대한 열렬한 기쁨과 기대가 솟아올라 도무지 말을 하고 싶지 않았다."], ["Besides that, he had that feeling of concentrated excitement that every sportsman experiences as he approaches the scene of action.", "게다가 사냥꾼이라면 누구나 작전 지점에 다가갈 때 느끼는, 그 응축된 흥분의 감정에 사로잡혀 있었다."], ["If he had anything on his mind at that moment, it was only the doubt whether they would start anything in the Kolpensky marsh, whether Laska would show to advantage in comparison with Krak, and whether he would shoot well that day himself.", "그 순간 그의 마음에 무언가 걸리는 것이 있었다면, 그저 콜펜스키 늪에서 사냥감을 몰아낼 수 있을지, 라스카가 크라크와 견주어 솜씨를 발휘할 수 있을지, 그리고 오늘 자기가 총을 잘 쏠 수 있을지 하는 의구심뿐이었다."], ["Not to disgrace himself before a new spectator—not to be outdone by Oblonsky—that too was a thought that crossed his brain.", "새로운 구경꾼 앞에서 망신을 당하지 않는 것, 오블론스키에게 뒤지지 않는 것, 그것도 그의 머릿속을 스치는 생각이었다."]], [["Oblonsky was feeling the same, and he too was not talkative.", "오블론스키도 같은 심정이라 역시 말이 없었다."], ["Vassenka Veslovsky kept up alone a ceaseless flow of cheerful chatter.", "바센카 베슬로프스키 혼자만 쾌활한 수다를 끊임없이 늘어놓았다."], ["As he listened to him now, Levin felt ashamed to think how unfair he had been to him the day before.", "이제 그의 말을 듣고 있자니, 레빈은 어제 자기가 그에게 얼마나 부당하게 굴었던가 싶어 부끄러웠다."], ["Vassenka was really a nice fellow, simple, good-hearted, and very good-humored.", "바센카는 정말이지 좋은 친구였다. 소박하고, 마음씨가 곱고, 더없이 유쾌했다."], ["If Levin had met him before he was married, he would have made friends with him.", "결혼하기 전에 만났더라면 레빈은 그와 친구가 되었을 것이다."], ["Levin rather disliked his holiday attitude to life and a sort of free and easy assumption of elegance.", "레빈은 그가 삶을 마치 휴일처럼 대하는 태도와, 우아함을 거리낌 없이 자처하는 태도가 다소 못마땅했다."], ["It was as though he assumed a high degree of importance in himself that could not be disputed, because he had long nails and a stylish cap, and everything else to correspond; but this could be forgiven for the sake of his good nature and good breeding.", "손톱을 길게 기르고 멋들어진 모자를 쓰고 그 밖의 모든 것을 거기에 맞춰 갖췄다는 이유만으로, 마치 자기 자신에게 누구도 따질 수 없는 높은 가치가 있다고 여기는 듯했다. 하지만 그의 선한 천성과 좋은 교양을 봐서 그 정도는 너그러이 넘길 수 있었다."], ["Levin liked him for his good education, for speaking French and English with such an excellent accent, and for being a man of his world.", "레빈은 그가 훌륭한 교육을 받았다는 점, 프랑스어와 영어를 그토록 빼어난 발음으로 구사한다는 점, 그리고 자기 세계에 속한 사람이라는 점이 마음에 들었다."]], [["Vassenka was extremely delighted with the left horse, a horse of the Don Steppes.", "바센카는 왼쪽 말, 돈 초원에서 온 말을 무척이나 마음에 들어 했다."], ["He kept praising him enthusiastically.", "그는 연신 열광적으로 그 말을 칭찬했다."], ["“How fine it must be galloping over the steppes on a steppe horse!", "\"초원의 말을 타고 초원을 내달리면 얼마나 멋질까!"], ["Eh?", "응?"], ["isn’t it?” he said.", "안 그렇소?\""], ["He had imagined riding on a steppe horse as something wild and romantic, and it turned out nothing of the sort.", "그가 말했다. 그는 초원의 말을 타는 것을 뭔가 야성적이고 낭만적인 일로 상상해 왔는데, 막상 알고 보니 전혀 그런 게 아니었다."], ["But his simplicity, particularly in conjunction with his good looks, his amiable smile, and the grace of his movements, was very attractive.", "하지만 그의 소박함은, 특히 잘생긴 외모와 상냥한 미소와 우아한 몸놀림이 어우러지니, 무척 매력적이었다."], ["Either because his nature was sympathetic to Levin, or because Levin was trying to atone for his sins of the previous evening by seeing nothing but what was good in him, anyway he liked his society.", "그의 천성이 레빈과 잘 통했기 때문인지, 아니면 레빈이 그에게서 좋은 점만 보려고 애쓰며 전날 저녁의 죄를 갚으려 했기 때문인지, 어쨌든 레빈은 그와 함께 있는 게 즐거웠다."]], [["After they had driven over two miles from home, Veslovsky all at once felt for a cigar and his pocketbook, and did not know whether he had lost them or left them on the table.", "집에서 이 마일쯤 달려왔을 때, 베슬로프스키가 갑자기 시가와 지갑을 찾았는데, 그것을 잃어버렸는지 탁자에 두고 왔는지 알 수가 없었다."], ["In the pocketbook there were thirty-seven pounds, and so the matter could not be left in uncertainty.", "지갑 안에는 삼십칠 파운드가 들어 있었으니, 그 일을 어정쩡한 채로 둘 수는 없었다."]], [["“Do you know what, Levin, I’ll gallop home on that left trace-horse.", "\"이봐요, 레빈, 내가 저 왼쪽 곁말을 타고 집으로 달려갔다 오리다."], ["That will be splendid.", "그거 아주 멋지겠는걸."], ["Eh?”", "응?\""], ["he said, preparing to get out.", "그가 내릴 채비를 하며 말했다."]], [["“No, why should you?”", "\"아니, 그럴 필요 있겠소?\""], ["answered Levin, calculating that Vassenka could hardly weigh less than seventeen stone.", "바센카의 몸무게가 십칠 스톤은 족히 나가겠다고 가늠하며 레빈이 대답했다."], ["“I’ll send the coachman.”", "\"내가 마부를 보내리다.\""]], [["The coachman rode back on the trace-horse, and Levin himself drove the remaining pair.", "마부가 곁말을 타고 되돌아갔고, 레빈은 남은 두 마리를 직접 몰았다."]], [["# Chapter 9", "# Chapter 9"]], [["“Well, now what’s our plan of campaign?", "\"자, 그럼 우리 작전 계획은 어떻게 되나?"], ["Tell us all about it,”", "다 들려주게.\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch.", "스테판 아르카지치가 말했다."]], [["“Our plan is this.", "\"우리 계획은 이렇네."], ["Now we’re driving to Gvozdyov.", "지금 우리는 그보즈됴프로 가는 길이야."], ["In Gvozdyov there’s a grouse marsh on this side, and beyond Gvozdyov come some magnificent snipe marshes where there are grouse too.", "그보즈됴프에는 이쪽 편에 멧닭 늪이 하나 있고, 그보즈됴프 너머에는 멋진 도요새 늪이 몇 군데 있는데 거기에도 멧닭이 있지."], ["It’s hot now, and we’ll get there—it’s fifteen miles or so—towards evening and have some evening shooting; we’ll spend the night there and go on tomorrow to the bigger moors.”", "지금은 더우니까, 거기 도착하면—한 십오 마일쯤 되는데—저녁 무렵이 될 테고, 그때 저녁 사냥을 좀 하는 거야. 거기서 하룻밤 묵고 내일 더 넓은 들판으로 가는 거지.\""]], [["“And is there nothing on the way?”", "\"가는 길에는 아무것도 없나?\""]], [["“Yes; but we’ll reserve ourselves; besides it’s hot.", "\"있긴 하지. 하지만 힘을 아껴 둬야 해."], ["There are two nice little places, but I doubt there being anything to shoot.”", "게다가 덥기도 하고. 괜찮은 곳이 두 군데 있긴 한데, 거기에 잡을 게 있을지는 모르겠어.\""]], [["Levin would himself have liked to go into these little places, but they were near home; he could shoot them over any time, and they were only little places—there would hardly be room for three to shoot.", "레빈도 그 작은 곳들에 들러 보고 싶긴 했지만, 그곳은 집에서 가까웠다. 언제든 사냥할 수 있는 데였고, 게다가 작은 곳이라 세 사람이 사냥할 자리도 마땅찮을 터였다."], ["And so, with some insincerity, he said that he doubted there being anything to shoot.", "그래서 그는 다소 마음에 없는 말로, 거기에 잡을 게 있을지 모르겠다고 했다."], ["When they reached a little marsh Levin would have driven by, but Stepan Arkadyevitch, with the experienced eye of a sportsman, at once detected reeds visible from the road.", "어느 작은 늪에 다다랐을 때 레빈은 그냥 지나치려 했지만, 사냥꾼 특유의 노련한 눈을 가진 스테판 아르카지치가 길에서도 보이는 갈대를 단번에 알아챘다."]], [["“Shan’t we try that?”", "\"저기 한번 해 볼까?\""], ["he said, pointing to the little marsh.", "그가 작은 늪을 가리키며 말했다."]], [["“Levin, do, please!", "\"레빈, 제발 그러죠!"], ["how delightful!”", "정말 신나겠는데요!\""], ["Vassenka Veslovsky began begging, and Levin could but consent.", "바센카 베슬로프스키가 졸라 대기 시작했고, 레빈은 응할 수밖에 없었다."]], [["Before they had time to stop, the dogs had flown one before the other into the marsh.", "미처 멈춰 서기도 전에 개들이 앞서거니 뒤서거니 늪으로 뛰어들었다."]], [["“Krak!", "\"크라크!"], ["Laska!...”", "라스카!……\""]], [["The dogs came back.", "개들이 돌아왔다."]], [["“There won’t be room for three.", "\"세 사람 자리는 없을 거야."], ["I’ll stay here,”", "난 여기 있겠네.\""], ["said Levin, hoping they would find nothing but peewits, who had been startled by the dogs, and turning over in their flight, were plaintively wailing over the marsh.", "레빈은 개들에게 놀라 후드득 날아올라서는 공중에서 몸을 뒤집으며 늪 위에서 구슬프게 울어 대는 댕기물떼새밖에는 아무것도 없기를 바라며 말했다."]], [["“No!", "\"아니에요!"], ["Come along, Levin, let’s go together!”", "가요, 레빈, 같이 갑시다!\""], ["Veslovsky called.", "베슬로프스키가 불렀다."]], [["“Really, there’s not room.", "\"정말 자리가 없어요."], ["Laska, back, Laska!", "라스카, 돌아와, 라스카!"], ["You won’t want another dog, will you?”", "개가 또 필요하진 않잖아요?\""]], [["Levin remained with the wagonette, and looked enviously at the sportsmen.", "레빈은 마차 곁에 남아 부러운 눈으로 사냥꾼들을 바라보았다."], ["They walked right across the marsh.", "그들은 늪을 가로질러 갔다."], ["Except little birds and peewits, of which Vassenka killed one, there was nothing in the marsh.", "작은 새들과 댕기물떼새 말고는—그중 한 마리를 바센카가 잡았다—늪에는 아무것도 없었다."]], [["“Come, you see now that it was not that I grudged the marsh,”", "\"자, 이제 알겠죠, 내가 늪을 아까워한 게 아니라는 걸.\""], ["said Levin, “only it’s wasting time.”", "레빈이 말했다. \"그저 시간 낭비라는 거였어요.\""]], [["“Oh, no, it was jolly all the same.", "\"오, 아니에요, 그래도 즐거웠는데요."], ["Did you see us?”", "보셨어요?\""], ["said Vassenka Veslovsky, clambering awkwardly into the wagonette with his gun and his peewit in his hands.", "바센카 베슬로프스키가 총과 댕기물떼새를 손에 든 채 어설프게 마차로 기어오르며 말했다."], ["“How splendidly I shot this bird!", "\"내가 이 새를 얼마나 멋지게 쐈는지!"], ["Didn’t I?", "안 그래요?"], ["Well, shall we soon be getting to the real place?”", "자, 이제 곧 진짜 사냥터에 도착하나요?\""]], [["The horses started off suddenly, Levin knocked his head against the stock of someone’s gun, and there was the report of a shot.", "말들이 갑자기 출발하는 바람에 레빈은 누군가의 총 개머리판에 머리를 부딪쳤고, 총소리가 울렸다."], ["The gun did actually go off first, but that was how it seemed to Levin.", "사실은 총이 먼저 발사된 것이었지만, 레빈에게는 그렇게 보였다."], ["It appeared that Vassenka Veslovsky had pulled only one trigger, and had left the other hammer still cocked.", "알고 보니 바센카 베슬로프스키가 방아쇠 하나만 당기고 다른 쪽 공이치기는 그대로 세워 두었던 것이다."], ["The charge flew into the ground without doing harm to anyone.", "탄환은 아무에게도 해를 끼치지 않고 땅속으로 날아갔다."], ["Stepan Arkadyevitch shook his head and laughed reprovingly at Veslovsky.", "스테판 아르카지치는 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 나무라듯 베슬로프스키를 보고 웃었다."], ["But Levin had not the heart to reprove him.", "그러나 레빈은 차마 그를 나무랄 마음이 들지 않았다."], ["In the first place, any reproach would have seemed to be called forth by the danger he had incurred and the bump that had come up on Levin’s forehead.", "우선, 어떤 책망이든 그것은 그가 처한 위험과 레빈의 이마에 솟아오른 혹 때문에 나온 것처럼 보일 터였다."], ["And besides, Veslovsky was at first so naïvely distressed, and then laughed so good-humoredly and infectiously at their general dismay, that one could not but laugh with him.", "게다가 베슬로프스키는 처음에는 어찌나 천진하게 괴로워하다가, 이내 모두의 당혹스러움을 두고 어찌나 사람 좋게, 그리고 옮아오게 웃어 대는지, 함께 웃지 않을 수가 없었다."]], [["When they reached the second marsh, which was fairly large, and would inevitably take some time to shoot over, Levin tried to persuade them to pass it by.", "제법 넓어서 사냥하는 데 시간이 꽤 걸릴 게 분명한 두 번째 늪에 다다랐을 때, 레빈은 그곳을 지나치자고 그들을 설득해 보았다."], ["But Veslovsky again overpersuaded him.", "그러나 베슬로프스키가 또다시 그를 설복했다."], ["Again, as the marsh was narrow, Levin, like a good host, remained with the carriage.", "이번에도 늪이 좁아서, 레빈은 점잖은 주인답게 마차 곁에 남았다."]], [["Krak made straight for some clumps of sedge.", "크라크가 사초 더미 쪽으로 곧장 달려갔다."], ["Vassenka Veslovsky was the first to run after the dog.", "바센카 베슬로프스키가 제일 먼저 개를 뒤쫓아 달렸다."], ["Before Stepan Arkadyevitch had time to come up, a grouse flew out.", "스테판 아르카지치가 미처 다가오기도 전에 멧닭 한 마리가 날아올랐다."], ["Veslovsky missed it and it flew into an unmown meadow.", "베슬로프스키가 빗맞히는 바람에 멧닭은 풀을 베지 않은 들판으로 날아갔다."], ["This grouse was left for Veslovsky to follow up.", "이 멧닭은 베슬로프스키가 쫓아가도록 남겨졌다."], ["Krak found it again and pointed, and Veslovsky shot it and went back to the carriage.", "크라크가 다시 그것을 찾아내어 멈춰 서서 가리키자, 베슬로프스키가 쏘아 잡고는 마차로 돌아왔다."], ["“Now you go and I’ll stay with the horses,”", "\"자, 이번엔 당신이 가요, 난 말들과 남아 있을 테니.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["Levin had begun to feel the pangs of a sportsman’s envy.", "레빈은 사냥꾼의 부러움이 빚어내는 통증을 느끼기 시작했다."], ["He handed the reins to Veslovsky and walked into the marsh.", "그는 베슬로프스키에게 고삐를 넘기고 늪으로 들어갔다."]], [["Laska, who had been plaintively whining and fretting against the injustice of her treatment, flew straight ahead to a hopeful place that Levin knew well, and that Krak had not yet come upon.", "부당한 대접에 구슬프게 낑낑대며 안달하던 라스카가, 레빈이 잘 아는, 그리고 크라크가 아직 들르지 않은 가망 있는 자리로 곧장 날듯이 달려갔다."]], [["“Why don’t you stop her?”", "\"왜 저놈을 멈추지 않나?\""], ["shouted Stepan Arkadyevitch.", "스테판 아르카지치가 외쳤다."]], [["“She won’t scare them,”", "\"저 녀석이 새들을 놀라게 하진 않을 거야.\""], ["answered Levin, sympathizing with his bitch’s pleasure and hurrying after her.", "레빈은 제 암캐의 기쁨에 공감하며 그 뒤를 서둘러 쫓아가며 대답했다."]], [["As she came nearer and nearer to the familiar breeding places there was more and more earnestness in Laska’s exploration.", "낯익은 둥지 자리에 점점 가까워질수록 라스카의 탐색은 점점 더 진지해졌다."], ["A little marsh bird did not divert her attention for more than an instant.", "작은 늪새 한 마리도 잠깐밖에는 그녀의 주의를 흐트러뜨리지 못했다."], ["She made one circuit round the clump of reeds, was beginning a second, and suddenly quivered with excitement and became motionless.", "그녀는 갈대 더미를 한 바퀴 돌고 두 번째 바퀴를 막 시작하다가, 별안간 흥분으로 몸을 떨더니 꼼짝 않고 멈춰 섰다."]], [["“Come, come, Stiva!”", "\"자, 어서, 스티바!\""], ["shouted Levin, feeling his heart beginning to beat more violently; and all of a sudden, as though some sort of shutter had been drawn back from his straining ears, all sounds, confused but loud, began to beat on his hearing, losing all sense of distance.", "심장이 더욱 거세게 뛰기 시작하는 것을 느끼며 레빈이 외쳤다. 그러자 별안간, 마치 팽팽히 긴장한 귀에서 무슨 덧문 같은 것이 열어젖혀지기라도 한 듯, 온갖 소리들이, 뒤엉키긴 했어도 큼직하게, 거리 감각을 모조리 잃은 채 그의 청각을 두드리기 시작했다."], ["He heard the steps of Stepan Arkadyevitch, mistaking them for the tramp of the horses in the distance; he heard the brittle sound of the twigs on which he had trodden, taking this sound for the flying of a grouse.", "그는 스테판 아르카지치의 발소리를 멀리서 들려오는 말발굽 소리로 착각했고, 자기가 밟은 잔가지가 부서지는 소리를 멧닭이 날아오르는 소리로 들었다."], ["He heard too, not far behind him, a splashing in the water, which he could not explain to himself.", "또 자기 등 뒤 멀지 않은 곳에서 물을 첨벙대는 소리가 들렸는데, 스스로도 그것을 설명할 수가 없었다."]], [["Picking his steps, he moved up to the dog.", "발걸음을 가려 디디며 그는 개에게 다가갔다."]], [["“Fetch it!”", "\"물어 와!\""]], [["Not a grouse but a snipe flew up from beside the dog.", "멧닭이 아니라 도요새 한 마리가 개 곁에서 날아올랐다."], ["Levin had lifted his gun, but at the very instant when he was taking aim, the sound of splashing grew louder, came closer, and was joined with the sound of Veslovsky’s voice, shouting something with strange loudness.", "레빈은 총을 들어 올렸지만, 막 겨냥하려던 바로 그 순간 첨벙대는 소리가 더 커지고 더 가까워지더니, 무언가를 이상하리만치 큰 소리로 외쳐 대는 베슬로프스키의 목소리와 뒤섞였다."], ["Levin saw he had his gun pointed behind the snipe, but still he fired.", "레빈은 총구가 도요새 뒤쪽을 향한 것을 보았지만, 그래도 방아쇠를 당겼다."]], [["When he had made sure he had missed, Levin looked round and saw the horses and the wagonette not on the road but in the marsh.", "빗맞혔다는 것을 확인하고 나서 레빈이 둘러보니, 말들과 마차가 길이 아니라 늪 가운데 들어와 있었다."]], [["Veslovsky, eager to see the shooting, had driven into the marsh, and got the horses stuck in the mud.", "사냥을 보고 싶어 안달이 난 베슬로프스키가 늪 속으로 마차를 몰고 들어가, 말들을 진창에 빠뜨려 놓은 것이었다."]], [["“Damn the fellow!”", "\"빌어먹을 놈 같으니!\""], ["Levin said to himself, as he went back to the carriage that had sunk in the mire.", "레빈은 진창에 빠진 마차로 돌아가며 혼잣말을 했다."], ["“What did you drive in for?”", "\"도대체 왜 몰고 들어왔어요?\""], ["he said to him dryly, and calling the coachman, he began pulling the horses out.", "그는 그에게 무뚝뚝하게 말하고는, 마부를 불러 말들을 끌어내기 시작했다."]], [["Levin was vexed both at being hindered from shooting and at his horses getting stuck in the mud, and still more at the fact that neither Stepan Arkadyevitch nor Veslovsky helped him and the coachman to unharness the horses and get them out, since neither of them had the slightest notion of harnessing.", "레빈은 사냥을 방해받은 데다 자기 말들이 진창에 빠진 것 때문에, 그리고 무엇보다 스테판 아르카지치도 베슬로프스키도 자기와 마부가 말의 마구를 풀고 끌어내는 것을 돕지 않은 데에 화가 났다. 두 사람 다 마구를 채우는 법을 도무지 알지 못했기 때문이다."], ["Without vouchsafing a syllable in reply to Vassenka’s protestations that it had been quite dry there, Levin worked in silence with the coachman at extricating the horses.", "거기는 아주 말라 있었다는 바센카의 항변에 한마디 대꾸할 가치도 두지 않은 채, 레빈은 말없이 마부와 함께 말들을 끌어내는 일에 매달렸다."], ["But then, as he got warm at the work and saw how assiduously Veslovsky was tugging at the wagonette by one of the mud-guards, so that he broke it indeed, Levin blamed himself for having under the influence of yesterday’s feelings been too cold to Veslovsky, and tried to be particularly genial so as to smooth over his chilliness.", "그러나 이내 일에 몸이 데워지고, 베슬로프스키가 흙받이 하나를 붙들고 어찌나 열심히 마차를 끌어당기는지 그러다 정말로 그것을 부러뜨리는 것을 보자, 레빈은 어제의 감정에 휘둘려 베슬로프스키에게 너무 쌀쌀맞게 군 자신을 책망하며, 그 냉랭함을 무마하려고 유난히 살갑게 굴려고 애썼다."], ["When everything had been put right, and the carriage had been brought back to the road, Levin had the lunch served.", "모든 것을 제자리로 돌려놓고 마차를 다시 길로 끌어 올린 다음, 레빈은 점심을 차리게 했다."]], [["“Bon appétit—bonne conscience! Ce poulet va tomber jusqu’au fond de mes bottes,”", "\"Bon appétit—bonne conscience!"], ["Vassenka, who had recovered his spirits, quoted the French saying as he finished his second chicken.", "Ce poulet va tomber jusqu’au fond de mes bottes.\""], ["“Well, now our troubles are over, now everything’s going to go well.", "기운을 되찾은 바센카가 두 번째 닭고기를 다 먹으며 그 프랑스 속담을 읊었다."], ["Only, to atone for my sins, I’m bound to sit on the box.", "\"자, 이제 우리 고생은 끝났고, 다 잘될 거예요. 다만 내 죄를 갚는 뜻에서, 난 마부석에 앉아야겠어요."], ["That’s so?", "그렇죠? 응?"], ["eh? No, no!", "아니, 아니!"], ["I’ll be your Automedon.", "내가 당신의 아우토메돈이 되어 드리죠."], ["You shall see how I’ll get you along,”", "내가 당신들을 얼마나 잘 모셔 가는지 두고 보세요.\""], ["he answered, not letting go the rein, when Levin begged him to let the coachman drive.", "그는 레빈이 마부에게 몰게 하라고 청했는데도 고삐를 놓지 않은 채 대답했다."], ["“No, I must atone for my sins, and I’m very comfortable on the box.”", "\"아니에요, 내 죄를 갚아야 해요, 게다가 난 마부석이 아주 편하답니다.\""], ["And he drove.", "그러고는 그가 마차를 몰았다."]], [["Levin was a little afraid he would exhaust the horses, especially the chestnut, whom he did not know how to hold in; but unconsciously he fell under the influence of his gaiety and listened to the songs he sang all the way on the box, or the descriptions and representations he gave of driving in the English fashion, four-in-hand; and it was in the very best of spirits that after lunch they drove to the Gvozdyov marsh.", "레빈은 그가 말들을, 특히 다룰 줄 모르는 밤색 말을 지치게 할까 봐 조금 걱정스러웠다. 그러나 자기도 모르게 그 명랑함에 물들어, 그가 마부석에서 가는 내내 부르는 노래며, 영국식으로 네 마리를 한데 몰던 마차 운전을 설명하고 흉내 내는 것에 귀를 기울였다. 그렇게 더없이 좋은 기분으로, 점심 식사를 마친 그들은 그보즈됴프 늪으로 마차를 몰아갔다. * Bon appétit—bonne conscience! Ce poulet va tomber jusqu’au fond de mes bottes: 프랑스어로 \"맛있게 먹으니 양심도 편하구나! 이 닭고기가 내 장화 밑바닥까지 떨어지겠는걸\"이라는 뜻."]], [["# Chapter 10", "# Chapter 10"]], [["Vassenka drove the horses so smartly that they reached the marsh too early, while it was still hot.", "바센카가 어찌나 말을 능숙하게 몰았던지, 그들은 너무 일찍, 아직 더위가 가시지 않았을 때 늪에 도착하고 말았다."]], [["As they drew near this more important marsh, the chief aim of their expedition, Levin could not help considering how he could get rid of Vassenka and be free in his movements.", "이번 사냥에서 가장 중요한, 이 더 큰 늪에 가까워지자 레빈은 어떻게 하면 바센카를 떼어 놓고 자기 행동을 자유롭게 할 수 있을까 하는 생각을 떨칠 수 없었다."], ["Stepan Arkadyevitch evidently had the same desire, and on his face Levin saw the look of anxiety always present in a true sportsman when beginning shooting, together with a certain good-humored slyness peculiar to him.", "스테판 아르카지치도 분명히 같은 바람을 품고 있었으니, 레빈은 그의 얼굴에서 진짜 사냥꾼이라면 사냥을 시작할 때 으레 떠올리는 그 긴장한 표정과, 그 특유의 사람 좋은 능청스러움이 함께 어린 것을 보았다."]], [["“How shall we go?", "\"어떻게 가지?"], ["It’s a splendid marsh, I see, and there are hawks,”", "보아하니 멋진 늪인데, 매까지 있구먼.\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, pointing to two great birds hovering over the reeds.", "스테판 아르카지치가 갈대밭 위를 맴도는 커다란 새 두 마리를 가리키며 말했다."], ["“Where there are hawks, there is sure to be game.”", "\"매가 있는 곳에는 틀림없이 사냥감이 있는 법이지.\""]], [["“Now, gentlemen,”", "\"자, 두 분.\""], ["said Levin, pulling up his boots and examining the lock of his gun with rather a gloomy expression, “do you see those reeds?”", "레빈이 장화를 추어올리고 다소 침울한 표정으로 총의 노리쇠를 살피며 말했다. \"저 갈대밭 보이세요?\""], ["He pointed to an oasis of blackish green in the huge half-mown wet meadow that stretched along the right bank of the river.", "그는 강의 오른쪽 기슭을 따라 펼쳐진, 절반쯤 풀을 벤 드넓고 축축한 풀밭 가운데 거무스름한 초록빛으로 우거진 한 자리를 가리켰다."], ["“The marsh begins here, straight in front of us, do you see—where it is greener?", "\"늪은 여기, 바로 우리 앞에서 시작됩니다. 보이시죠, 저기 더 푸른 데요."], ["From here it runs to the right where the horses are; there are breeding places there, and grouse, and all round those reeds as far as that alder, and right up to the mill.", "거기서부터 말들이 있는 오른쪽으로 늪이 이어집니다. 거긴 새들이 알을 까는 곳이라 멧닭도 있고, 저 갈대밭을 빙 둘러 저 오리나무까지, 곧장 물방앗간까지 죽 이어지죠."], ["Over there, do you see, where the pools are?", "저기 보이세요, 물웅덩이가 있는 데요?"], ["That’s the best place.", "거기가 제일 좋은 자리입니다."], ["There I once shot seventeen snipe.", "거기서 한번은 도요새를 열일곱 마리나 잡았죠."], ["We’ll separate with the dogs and go in different directions, and then meet over there at the mill.”", "우리 개를 데리고 흩어져서 각자 다른 방향으로 가다가, 저기 물방앗간에서 다시 만나도록 하죠.\""]], [["“Well, which shall go to left and which to right?”", "\"그럼, 누가 왼쪽으로 가고 누가 오른쪽으로 가지?\""], ["asked Stepan Arkadyevitch.", "스테판 아르카지치가 물었다."], ["“It’s wider to the right; you two go that way and I’ll take the left,”", "\"오른쪽이 더 넓으니 자네 둘이 그쪽으로 가게, 난 왼쪽으로 갈 테니.\""], ["he said with apparent carelessness.", "그는 짐짓 무심한 척하며 말했다."]], [["“Capital!", "\"좋습니다!"], ["we’ll make the bigger bag!", "그래야 더 많이 잡죠!"], ["Yes, come along, come along!”", "그래요, 갑시다, 가요!\""], ["Vassenka exclaimed.", "바센카가 외쳤다."]], [["Levin could do nothing but agree, and they divided.", "레빈은 동의할 수밖에 없었고, 그들은 갈라섰다."]], [["As soon as they entered the marsh, the two dogs began hunting about together and made towards the green, slime-covered pool.", "늪에 들어서자마자 두 마리 개는 함께 사냥감을 찾아 헤매기 시작하더니 초록빛 진창에 덮인 웅덩이 쪽으로 향했다."], ["Levin knew Laska’s method, wary and indefinite; he knew the place too and expected a whole covey of snipe.", "레빈은 라스카의 방식을, 그 신중하고 종잡을 수 없는 방식을 잘 알았다. 그 자리도 잘 알았기에 도요새 한 무리가 통째로 있으리라 기대했다."]], [["“Veslovsky, beside me, walk beside me!”", "\"베슬로프스키, 내 옆에서, 내 옆에서 걸어요!\""], ["he said in a faint voice to his companion splashing in the water behind him.", "그는 뒤에서 물을 첨벙거리며 따라오는 일행에게 나직한 목소리로 말했다."], ["Levin could not help feeling an interest in the direction his gun was pointed, after that casual shot near the Kolpensky marsh.", "콜펜스키 늪 근처에서 그 무심한 발포가 있은 뒤로, 레빈은 그의 총구가 어디를 향하는지에 신경이 쓰이는 것을 어쩔 수 없었다."]], [["“Oh, I won’t get in your way, don’t trouble about me.”", "\"오, 방해는 안 할 테니, 나는 신경 쓰지 마요.\""]], [["But Levin could not help troubling, and recalled Kitty’s words at parting: “Mind you don’t shoot one another.”", "그러나 레빈은 신경을 쓰지 않을 수 없었고, 헤어질 때 키티가 한 말을 떠올렸다. \"서로 쏘지 않도록 조심하세요.\""], ["The dogs came nearer and nearer, passed each other, each pursuing its own scent.", "개들은 점점 더 가까이 다가오며 서로 엇갈렸고, 저마다 자기 냄새를 좇았다."], ["The expectation of snipe was so intense that to Levin the squelching sound of his own heel, as he drew it up out of the mire, seemed to be the call of a snipe, and he clutched and pressed the lock of his gun.", "도요새에 대한 기대가 어찌나 강렬했던지, 레빈에게는 진창에서 발뒤꿈치를 빼낼 때 나는 철벅 소리마저 도요새 우는 소리처럼 들렸고, 그는 총의 노리쇠를 움켜쥐고 힘껏 눌렀다."]], [["“Bang!", "\"탕!"], ["bang!”", "탕!\""], ["sounded almost in his ear.", "소리가 거의 귓전에서 울렸다."], ["Vassenka had fired at a flock of ducks which was hovering over the marsh and flying at that moment towards the sportsmen, far out of range.", "바센카가 늪 위를 맴돌다 마침 그 순간 사냥꾼들 쪽으로, 사정거리에서 한참 벗어나 날아오던 오리 떼를 향해 쏜 것이었다."], ["Before Levin had time to look round, there was the whir of one snipe, another, a third, and some eight more rose one after another.", "레빈이 미처 돌아볼 새도 없이 도요새 한 마리가 푸드덕 날아올랐고, 또 한 마리, 세 마리, 이어 여덟 마리쯤이 잇따라 날아올랐다."]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch hit one at the very moment when it was beginning its zigzag movements, and the snipe fell in a heap into the mud.", "스테판 아르카지치는 도요새 한 마리가 지그재그로 날기 시작하는 바로 그 순간에 명중시켰고, 도요새는 진창 속으로 풀썩 떨어졌다."], ["Oblonsky aimed deliberately at another, still flying low in the reeds, and together with the report of the shot, that snipe too fell, and it could be seen fluttering out where the sedge had been cut, its unhurt wing showing white beneath.", "오블론스키는 아직 갈대밭에서 낮게 날고 있는 또 한 마리를 침착하게 겨누었고, 총성과 함께 그 도요새 또한 떨어져, 사초가 베인 자리로 다친 데 없는 날개의 흰빛을 드러내며 파닥거리는 것이 보였다."]], [["Levin was not so lucky: he aimed at his first bird too low, and missed; he aimed at it again, just as it was rising, but at that instant another snipe flew up at his very feet, distracting him so that he missed again.", "레빈은 그만큼 운이 좋지 못했다. 첫 새를 너무 낮게 겨눠 빗맞혔다. 새가 막 날아오를 때 다시 겨눴지만, 바로 그 순간 또 다른 도요새가 그의 발밑에서 날아올라 그의 정신을 흩뜨려 놓는 바람에 또다시 빗맞혔다."]], [["While they were loading their guns, another snipe rose, and Veslovsky, who had had time to load again, sent two charges of small-shot into the water.", "그들이 총을 장전하는 동안 또 한 마리의 도요새가 날아올랐고, 미리 장전을 끝냈던 베슬로프스키는 산탄 두 발을 물속에 쏘아 박았다."], ["Stepan Arkadyevitch picked up his snipe, and with sparkling eyes looked at Levin.", "스테판 아르카지치는 자기가 잡은 도요새를 주워 들고는 반짝이는 눈으로 레빈을 바라보았다."]], [["“Well, now let us separate,”", "\"자, 이제 갈라지세.\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, and limping on his left foot, holding his gun in readiness and whistling to his dog, he walked off in one direction.", "스테판 아르카지치가 말했고, 왼발을 절며, 총을 쏠 준비를 한 채 들고 개에게 휘파람을 불며 한쪽 방향으로 걸어갔다."], ["Levin and Veslovsky walked in the other.", "레빈과 베슬로프스키는 다른 쪽으로 걸어갔다."]], [["It always happened with Levin that when his first shots were a failure he got hot and out of temper, and shot badly the whole day.", "레빈은 늘 첫 발들을 놓치면 안달이 나고 신경질이 솟구쳐 하루 종일 사냥을 망치곤 했다."], ["So it was that day.", "그날도 그랬다."], ["The snipe showed themselves in numbers.", "도요새는 무수히 모습을 드러냈다."], ["They kept flying up from just under the dogs, from under the sportsmen’s legs, and Levin might have retrieved his ill luck.", "녀석들은 개들 바로 밑에서, 사냥꾼들 다리 밑에서 끊임없이 날아올랐고, 레빈은 운 나쁜 첫 출발을 만회할 수도 있었다."], ["But the more he shot, the more he felt disgraced in the eyes of Veslovsky, who kept popping away merrily and indiscriminately, killing nothing, and not in the slightest abashed by his ill success.", "그러나 쏘면 쏠수록, 그는 베슬로프스키 앞에서 더 망신스러워지는 기분이었다. 베슬로프스키는 흥겹게 아무 데나 마구 총을 쏘아 대며 아무것도 잡지 못했지만, 자신의 부진을 조금도 부끄러워하지 않았다."], ["Levin, in feverish haste, could not restrain himself, got more and more out of temper, and ended by shooting almost without a hope of hitting.", "레빈은 안달이 난 나머지 마음을 다잡지 못했고, 점점 더 신경질이 솟구쳐 나중에는 맞히리라는 기대조차 거의 없이 쏘는 지경에 이르렀다."], ["Laska, indeed, seemed to understand this.", "정말이지 라스카도 이것을 알아챈 듯했다."], ["She began looking more languidly, and gazed back at the sportsmen, as it were, with perplexity or reproach in her eyes.", "녀석은 점점 더 맥없이 사냥감을 찾기 시작했고, 어리둥절함인지 책망인지 모를 눈빛으로 사냥꾼들을 돌아보았다."], ["Shots followed shots in rapid succession.", "총성이 잇따라 빠르게 울려 퍼졌다."], ["The smoke of the powder hung about the sportsmen, while in the great roomy net of the game bag there were only three light little snipe.", "화약 연기가 사냥꾼들 주위에 자욱하게 깔렸지만, 사냥 자루의 그 널찍한 그물 속에는 작고 가벼운 도요새 세 마리뿐이었다."], ["And of these one had been killed by Veslovsky alone, and one by both of them together.", "그나마 그중 한 마리는 베슬로프스키 혼자 잡은 것이었고, 한 마리는 둘이 함께 잡은 것이었다."], ["Meanwhile from the other side of the marsh came the sound of Stepan Arkadyevitch’s shots, not frequent, but, as Levin fancied, well-directed, for almost after each they heard “Krak, Krak, apporte!”", "그러는 사이 늪 저편에서는 스테판 아르카지치의 총성이 들려왔는데, 잦지는 않았지만 레빈이 보기에는 잘 겨눈 것이었으니, 거의 매번 총성 뒤로 \"크라크, 크라크, apporte!\" 하는 소리가 들려왔던 것이다."]], [["This excited Levin still more.", "* apporte: 프랑스어로 '물어 와'."], ["The snipe were floating continually in the air over the reeds.", "사냥개에게 잡은 새를 가져오라고 내리는 명령. 이것이 레빈을 더욱 안달 나게 했다."], ["Their whirring wings close to the earth, and their harsh cries high in the air, could be heard on all sides; the snipe that had risen first and flown up into the air, settled again before the sportsmen.", "도요새는 갈대밭 위 허공을 끊임없이 떠다녔다. 땅 가까이서 푸드덕거리는 날갯소리며, 높은 공중에서 거칠게 우짖는 소리가 사방에서 들려왔다."], ["Instead of two hawks there were now dozens of them hovering with shrill cries over the marsh.", "먼저 날아올라 공중으로 솟구쳤던 도요새는 다시 사냥꾼들 앞에 내려앉았다. 매도 이제 두 마리가 아니라 수십 마리가 날카로운 울음소리를 내며 늪 위를 맴돌고 있었다."]], [["After walking through the larger half of the marsh, Levin and Veslovsky reached the place where the peasants’ mowing-grass was divided into long strips reaching to the reeds, marked off in one place by the trampled grass, in another by a path mown through it.", "늪의 절반 넘는 곳을 지나, 레빈과 베슬로프스키는 농부들의 풀밭이 갈대밭까지 길게 이어진 띠 모양으로 나뉜 곳에 이르렀는데, 한쪽은 밟혀 다져진 풀로, 다른 쪽은 그 사이를 베어 낸 오솔길로 경계가 표시되어 있었다."], ["Half of these strips had already been mown.", "이 띠들의 절반은 이미 풀을 벤 상태였다."]], [["Though there was not so much hope of finding birds in the uncut part as the cut part, Levin had promised Stepan Arkadyevitch to meet him, and so he walked on with his companion through the cut and uncut patches.", "풀을 베지 않은 쪽이 벤 쪽만큼 새를 찾을 가망이 있는 것은 아니었지만, 레빈은 스테판 아르카지치와 만나기로 약속했던 터라, 일행과 함께 풀을 벤 자리와 베지 않은 자리를 가로질러 걸었다."]], [["“Hi, sportsmen!”", "\"어이, 사냥꾼 양반들!\""], ["shouted one of a group of peasants, sitting on an unharnessed cart; “come and have some lunch with us!", "마구를 벗긴 짐수레에 앉아 있던 한 무리 농부 가운데 하나가 외쳤다. \"이리 와서 우리랑 점심이나 좀 듭시다!"], ["Have a drop of wine!”", "술 한잔하라고!\""]], [["Levin looked round.", "레빈이 둘러보았다."]], [["“Come along, it’s all right!”", "\"이리 와요, 괜찮으니까!\""], ["shouted a good-humored-looking bearded peasant with a red face, showing his white teeth in a grin, and holding up a greenish bottle that flashed in the sunlight.", "사람 좋아 보이는, 붉은 얼굴에 수염을 기른 농부가 하얀 이를 드러내며 씩 웃고는, 햇빛에 번쩍이는 푸르스름한 병을 들어 올리며 외쳤다."]], [["“Qu’est-ce qu’ils disent?”", "\"Qu'est-ce qu'ils disent?\""], ["asked Veslovsky.", "베슬로프스키가 물었다."]], [["“They invite you to have some vodka.", "* Qu'est-ce qu'ils disent?: 프랑스어로 '저들이 뭐라는 거예요?'."], ["Most likely they’ve been dividing the meadow into lots.", "\"보드카를 마시라고 권하는 거예요. 아마 풀밭을 몫몫이 나누던 참이겠죠."], ["I should have some,”", "나라면 좀 마시겠는데.\""], ["said Levin, not without some guile, hoping Veslovsky would be tempted by the vodka, and would go away to them.", "레빈은 베슬로프스키가 보드카에 마음이 동해 그들에게 가 버리기를 바라는 약간의 속셈을 품고 말했다."]], [["“Why do they offer it?”", "\"왜 권하는 거죠?\""]], [["“Oh, they’re merry-making.", "\"오, 흥겹게 놀고들 있는 거예요."], ["Really, you should join them.", "정말이지, 한번 어울려 보시지요."], ["You would be interested.”", "재미있을 겁니다.\""]], [["“Allons, c’est curieux.”", "\"Allons, c'est curieux.\""]], [["“You go, you go, you’ll find the way to the mill!”", "* Allons, c'est curieux: 프랑스어로 '갑시다, 흥미롭군요'."], ["cried Levin, and looking round he perceived with satisfaction that Veslovsky, bent and stumbling with weariness, holding his gun out at arm’s length, was making his way out of the marsh towards the peasants.", "\"가세요, 가요, 물방앗간 가는 길은 찾을 수 있을 테니!\" 레빈이 외쳤고, 둘러보다가 베슬로프스키가 피곤에 못 이겨 몸을 구부린 채 비틀거리며 총을 팔 길이만큼 앞으로 내밀고 농부들 쪽으로 늪을 빠져나가는 것을 보고 흡족해했다."]], [["“You come too!”", "\"당신도 오시구려!\""], ["the peasants shouted to Levin.", "농부들이 레빈에게 외쳤다."], ["“Never fear!", "\"겁내지 말고!"], ["You taste our cake!”", "우리 빵맛 좀 봐요!\""]], [["Levin felt a strong inclination to drink a little vodka and to eat some bread.", "레빈은 보드카를 조금 마시고 빵도 좀 먹고 싶은 마음이 간절히 일었다."], ["He was exhausted, and felt it a great effort to drag his staggering legs out of the mire, and for a minute he hesitated.", "그는 기진맥진했고, 비틀거리는 다리를 진창에서 끌어 빼는 것조차 큰일로 여겨져, 잠시 망설였다. 그러나 라스카가 사냥감을 향해 자세를 잡고 있었다."], ["But Laska was setting. And immediately all his weariness vanished, and he walked lightly through the swamp towards the dog.", "그러자 곧바로 온갖 피로가 싹 가시고, 그는 늪을 가벼이 헤치며 개 쪽으로 걸어갔다."], ["A snipe flew up at his feet; he fired and killed it.", "도요새 한 마리가 그의 발밑에서 날아올랐다. 그는 쏘았고 명중시켰다."], ["Laska still pointed.—“Fetch it!”", "라스카는 여전히 자세를 잡고 있었다."], ["Another bird flew up close to the dog.", "\"물어 와!\" 또 한 마리 새가 개 가까이서 날아올랐다."], ["Levin fired.", "레빈이 쏘았다."], ["But it was an unlucky day for him; he missed it, and when he went to look for the one he had shot, he could not find that either.", "그러나 그날은 그에게 운 없는 날이었다. 빗맞혔고, 게다가 아까 쏘아 잡은 새를 찾으러 갔으나 그것마저 찾을 수가 없었다."], ["He wandered all about the reeds, but Laska did not believe he had shot it, and when he sent her to find it, she pretended to hunt for it, but did not really.", "그는 갈대밭을 온통 헤매고 다녔지만, 라스카는 그가 새를 맞혔다고 믿지 않았고, 그가 찾아오라고 보내자 찾는 척만 할 뿐 정말로 찾지는 않았다."], ["And in the absence of Vassenka, on whom Levin threw the blame of his failure, things went no better.", "그리고 레빈이 자신의 실패를 뒤집어씌우던 바센카가 곁에 없는데도 사정은 조금도 나아지지 않았다."], ["There were plenty of snipe still, but Levin made one miss after another.", "도요새는 여전히 수두룩했지만, 레빈은 잇따라 빗맞히기만 했다."]], [["The slanting rays of the sun were still hot; his clothes, soaked through with perspiration, stuck to his body; his left boot full of water weighed heavily on his leg and squeaked at every step; the sweat ran in drops down his powder-grimed face, his mouth was full of the bitter taste, his nose of the smell of powder and stagnant water, his ears were ringing with the incessant whir of the snipe; he could not touch the stock of his gun, it was so hot; his heart beat with short, rapid throbs; his hands shook with excitement, and his weary legs stumbled and staggered over the hillocks and in the swamp, but still he walked on and still he shot. At last, after a disgraceful miss, he flung his gun and his hat on the ground.", "비스듬히 비치는 햇살은 여전히 뜨거웠다. 땀에 흠뻑 젖은 옷이 몸에 들러붙었고, 물이 가득 찬 왼쪽 장화가 다리를 무겁게 짓누르며 걸음마다 찍찍 소리를 냈다. 땀이 화약에 그을린 얼굴을 따라 방울방울 흘러내렸고, 입안은 쓴맛으로 가득했으며, 코에는 화약과 고인 물의 냄새가 배었고, 귀에는 도요새의 끊임없는 날갯소리가 울렸다. 총대는 너무 뜨거워 만질 수가 없었다. 심장은 짧고 빠르게 두근거렸고, 손은 흥분으로 떨렸으며, 지친 다리는 둔덕과 늪 위에서 휘청거리며 비틀댔지만, 그래도 그는 계속 걸었고 계속 쏘았다. 마침내 망신스럽게 또 한 발을 놓치고 나서, 그는 총과 모자를 땅바닥에 내동댕이쳤다."]], [["“No, I must control myself,”", "\"안 되겠어, 마음을 다잡아야 해.\""], ["he said to himself.", "그는 혼잣말을 했다."], ["Picking up his gun and his hat, he called Laska, and went out of the swamp.", "총과 모자를 집어 들고 라스카를 부른 뒤 늪에서 빠져나왔다."], ["When he got on to dry ground he sat down, pulled off his boot and emptied it, then walked to the marsh, drank some stagnant-tasting water, moistened his burning hot gun, and washed his face and hands.", "마른 땅에 올라선 그는 주저앉아 장화를 벗어 물을 비웠다. 그러고는 늪으로 걸어가 고인 듯한 맛이 나는 물을 좀 마시고, 뜨겁게 달궈진 총을 적시고, 얼굴과 손을 씻었다."], ["Feeling refreshed, he went back to the spot where a snipe had settled, firmly resolved to keep cool.", "한결 개운해진 그는 도요새가 내려앉았던 자리로 돌아가며 침착함을 지키리라 굳게 마음먹었다."]], [["He tried to be calm, but it was the same again.", "그는 차분해지려고 애썼지만, 또다시 같은 일이 벌어졌다."], ["His finger pressed the cock before he had taken a good aim at the bird.", "그의 손가락은 새를 제대로 겨누기도 전에 방아쇠를 눌러 버렸다."], ["It got worse and worse.", "사정은 점점 더 나빠졌다."]], [["He had only five birds in his game-bag when he walked out of the marsh towards the alders where he was to rejoin Stepan Arkadyevitch.", "스테판 아르카지치와 다시 만나기로 한 오리나무 쪽으로 늪을 빠져나올 때, 그의 사냥 자루에는 새가 다섯 마리뿐이었다."]], [["Before he caught sight of Stepan Arkadyevitch he saw his dog.", "스테판 아르카지치를 발견하기에 앞서 그는 그의 개를 보았다."], ["Krak darted out from behind the twisted root of an alder, black all over with the stinking mire of the marsh, and with the air of a conqueror sniffed at Laska.", "크라크가 오리나무의 비틀린 뿌리 뒤에서 튀어나왔는데, 온몸이 늪의 고약한 냄새가 나는 진창으로 새카맸고, 정복자 같은 기세로 라스카의 냄새를 맡았다."], ["Behind Krak there came into view in the shade of the alder tree the shapely figure of Stepan Arkadyevitch.", "크라크 뒤로 오리나무 그늘 속에서 스테판 아르카지치의 늘씬한 모습이 나타났다."], ["He came to meet him, red and perspiring, with unbuttoned neckband, still limping in the same way.", "그는 붉게 상기되어 땀을 흘리며, 옷깃을 풀어 헤친 채, 여전히 같은 모양으로 다리를 절며 그를 맞으러 다가왔다."]], [["“Well?", "\"어때?"], ["You have been popping away!”", "자네 펑펑 쏘아 대던데!\""], ["he said, smiling good-humoredly.", "그가 사람 좋게 웃으며 말했다."]], [["“How have you got on?”", "\"형님은 어떻게 됐어요?\""], ["queried Levin.", "레빈이 물었다."], ["But there was no need to ask, for he had already seen the full game bag.", "그러나 물어볼 필요도 없었으니, 이미 가득 찬 사냥 자루를 보았기 때문이다."]], [["“Oh, pretty fair.”", "\"오, 그럭저럭 괜찮았네.\""]], [["He had fourteen birds.", "그는 새를 열네 마리나 잡았다."]], [["“A splendid marsh!", "\"멋진 늪이야!"], ["I’ve no doubt Veslovsky got in your way.", "베슬로프스키가 자네한테 방해가 됐을 게 틀림없어."], ["It’s awkward too, shooting with one dog,”", "개 한 마리로 사냥하는 것도 거북한 노릇이고.\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, to soften his triumph.", "스테판 아르카지치는 자신의 승리를 누그러뜨리려는 듯 말했다."]], [["# Chapter 11", "# Chapter 11"]], [["When Levin and Stepan Arkadyevitch reached the peasant’s hut where Levin always used to stay, Veslovsky was already there.", "레빈과 스테판 아르카지치가 레빈이 늘 묵곤 하던 농가에 다다랐을 때, 베슬로프스키는 이미 그곳에 와 있었다."], ["He was sitting in the middle of the hut, clinging with both hands to the bench from which he was being pulled by a soldier, the brother of the peasant’s wife, who was helping him off with his miry boots.", "그는 농가 한가운데 의자에 앉아 두 손으로 의자를 꽉 붙들고 있었는데, 농부 아내의 오빠인 병사가 그의 진흙투성이 장화를 벗겨 주느라 그를 잡아당기고 있었기 때문이었다."], ["Veslovsky was laughing his infectious, good-humored laugh.", "베슬로프스키는 사람을 절로 따라 웃게 만드는 그 특유의 쾌활한 웃음을 터뜨리고 있었다."]], [["“I’ve only just come.", "\"이제 막 도착했어요."], ["Ils ont été charmants.", "Ils ont été charmants."], ["Just fancy, they gave me drink, fed me!", "글쎄, 술도 주고 먹을 것도 주지 뭐예요!"], ["Such bread, it was exquisite!", "그 빵이라니, 정말 일품이었어요!"], ["Délicieux!", "Délicieux!"], ["And the vodka, I never tasted any better.", "보드카도, 그렇게 좋은 건 마셔 본 적이 없어요."], ["And they would not take a penny for anything.", "그런데 무엇 하나에도 한 푼도 안 받으려 하더군요."], ["And they kept saying: ‘Excuse our homely ways.’”", "그저 이렇게만 말하는 거예요. '저희 시골 살림이라 누추하지만 양해해 주십시오.'\""]], [["“What should they take anything for?", "* Ils ont été charmants: 프랑스어로 '그들은 매력적이었다'."], ["They were entertaining you, to be sure.", "Délicieux: '맛있다'. \"받긴 뭘 받겠소?"], ["Do you suppose they keep vodka for sale?”", "손님 대접을 한 거지요, 당연히."], ["said the soldier, succeeding at last in pulling the soaked boot off the blackened stocking.", "보드카를 팔려고 두는 줄 아쇼?\" 마침내 그을음투성이 양말에서 흠뻑 젖은 장화를 벗겨 내는 데 성공한 병사가 말했다."]], [["In spite of the dirtiness of the hut, which was all muddied by their boots and the filthy dogs licking themselves clean, and the smell of marsh mud and powder that filled the room, and the absence of knives and forks, the party drank their tea and ate their supper with a relish only known to sportsmen.", "장화 때문에 온통 진흙이 묻고, 더러운 개들이 제 몸을 핥아 대고, 늪의 진흙 냄새와 화약 냄새가 방 안에 가득하고, 칼이며 포크도 없는 누추한 농가였지만, 일행은 사냥꾼들만이 아는 그 맛으로 차를 마시고 저녁을 먹었다."], ["Washed and clean, they went into a hay-barn swept ready for them, where the coachman had been making up beds for the gentlemen.", "몸을 씻고 깨끗해진 그들은 마부가 신사들을 위해 잠자리를 마련해 둔, 깨끗이 쓸어 놓은 건초 헛간으로 들어갔다."]], [["Though it was dusk, not one of them wanted to go to sleep.", "어스름이 내렸는데도 누구 하나 자려는 사람이 없었다."]], [["After wavering among reminiscences and anecdotes of guns, of dogs, and of former shooting parties, the conversation rested on a topic that interested all of them.", "총이며 개며 지난날의 사냥 모임에 대한 회상과 일화 사이를 오가던 끝에, 대화는 일행 모두의 관심을 끄는 주제로 옮겨 갔다."], ["After Vassenka had several times over expressed his appreciation of this delightful sleeping place among the fragrant hay, this delightful broken cart (he supposed it to be broken because the shafts had been taken out), of the good nature of the peasants that had treated him to vodka, of the dogs who lay at the feet of their respective masters, Oblonsky began telling them of a delightful shooting party at Malthus’s, where he had stayed the previous summer.", "바센카가 향기로운 건초 사이의 이 즐거운 잠자리며, 이 즐거운 부서진 짐수레(끌채가 빠져 있어 부서진 것이라고 그는 짐작했다)며, 보드카를 대접해 준 농부들의 인심이며, 저마다 제 주인 발치에 누운 개들에 대해 여러 차례 만족을 표한 뒤, 오블론스키가 지난여름 묵었던 말투스의 집에서 열린 멋진 사냥 모임 이야기를 꺼냈다."]], [["Malthus was a well-known capitalist, who had made his money by speculation in railway shares.", "말투스는 철도 주식 투기로 돈을 번, 널리 알려진 자본가였다."], ["Stepan Arkadyevitch described what grouse moors this Malthus had bought in the Tver province, and how they were preserved, and of the carriages and dogcarts in which the shooting party had been driven, and the luncheon pavilion that had been rigged up at the marsh.", "스테판 아르카지치는 이 말투스가 트베리 지방에 사들인 멧닭 사냥터며, 그것을 어떻게 관리하는지며, 사냥 모임이 타고 다닌 마차와 이륜마차며, 늪가에 차려 놓은 점심 식사용 천막에 대해 늘어놓았다."]], [["“I don’t understand you,”", "\"난 자네를 이해할 수가 없네.\""], ["said Levin, sitting up in the hay; “how is it such people don’t disgust you?", "레빈이 건초 위에 몸을 일으키며 말했다. \"어떻게 그런 사람들이 역겹지 않을 수가 있나?"], ["I can understand a lunch with Lafitte is all very pleasant, but don’t you dislike just that very sumptuousness?", "라피트와 함께하는 점심이 더없이 즐겁다는 건 이해하겠어. 하지만 바로 그 호사스러움이 싫지 않나?"], ["All these people, just like our spirit monopolists in old days, get their money in a way that gains them the contempt of everyone.", "그런 자들은 다, 옛날 우리 주류 독점업자들이 그랬듯이, 누구나 경멸할 만한 방식으로 돈을 벌어들이지."], ["They don’t care for their contempt, and then they use their dishonest gains to buy off the contempt they have deserved.”", "그러고는 그 경멸 따위는 아랑곳하지 않다가, 부정하게 번 돈으로 자기가 마땅히 받아야 할 그 경멸을 사서 무마하는 거야.\" * 라피트: 프랑스 보르도산 고급 적포도주."]], [["“Perfectly true!”", "\"전적으로 옳은 말이에요!\""], ["chimed in Vassenka Veslovsky.", "바센카 베슬로프스키가 맞장구쳤다."], ["“Perfectly!", "\"전적으로요!"], ["Oblonsky, of course, goes out of bonhomie, but other people say: ‘Well, Oblonsky stays with them.’...”", "오블론스키야 물론 bonhomie로 가는 거지만, 다른 사람들은 이렇게 말하거든요. '그래, 오블론스키도 그자들 집에 묵잖아.'……\""]], [["“Not a bit of it.”", "* bonhomie: 프랑스어로 '서글서글한 호의'."], ["Levin could hear that Oblonsky was smiling as he spoke.", "\"천만에.\" 레빈은 오블론스키가 미소를 지으며 말하고 있다는 것을 알아챌 수 있었다."], ["“I simply don’t consider him more dishonest than any other wealthy merchant or nobleman.", "\"나는 그를 다른 어떤 부유한 상인이나 귀족보다 더 부정직하다고 여기지 않을 뿐이야."], ["They’ve all made their money alike—by their work and their intelligence.”", "그들은 모두 똑같이 돈을 벌었어. 자기 노동과 지력으로 말이지.\""]], [["“Oh, by what work?", "\"오, 무슨 노동으로?"], ["Do you call it work to get hold of concessions and speculate with them?”", "이권을 손에 넣고 그걸로 투기하는 걸 노동이라 부르나?\""]], [["“Of course it’s work.", "\"물론 노동이지."], ["Work in this sense, that if it were not for him and others like him, there would have been no railways.”", "이런 의미에서 노동이야. 그와 그 같은 사람들이 없었다면 철도도 없었을 테니까.\""]], [["“But that’s not work, like the work of a peasant or a learned profession.”", "\"하지만 그건 농부의 노동이나 학식 있는 직업의 노동 같은 게 아니잖아.\""]], [["“Granted, but it’s work in the sense that his activity produces a result—the railways.", "\"그렇다 치지. 하지만 그의 활동이 어떤 결과를—철도를 만들어 낸다는 의미에서는 노동이야."], ["But of course you think the railways useless.”", "물론 자네는 철도가 쓸모없다고 여기겠지만.\""]], [["“No, that’s another question; I am prepared to admit that they’re useful.", "\"아니, 그건 다른 문제야. 철도가 유용하다는 건 인정할 용의가 있어."], ["But all profit that is out of proportion to the labor expended is dishonest.”", "하지만 들인 노동에 비해 지나친 모든 이윤은 부정직한 거야.\""]], [["“But who is to define what is proportionate?”", "\"그럼 무엇이 적당한 비례인지 누가 정하나?\""]], [["“Making profit by dishonest means, by trickery,”", "\"부정한 수단으로, 속임수로 이윤을 얻는 것 말이야.\""], ["said Levin, conscious that he could not draw a distinct line between honesty and dishonesty.", "레빈은 자기가 정직과 부정직 사이에 뚜렷한 선을 그을 수 없다는 것을 의식하면서 말했다."], ["“Such as banking, for instance,”", "\"예컨대 은행업 같은 거지.\""], ["he went on.", "그가 말을 이었다."], ["“It’s an evil—the amassing of huge fortunes without labor, just the same thing as with the spirit monopolies, it’s only the form that’s changed.", "\"그건 악이야—노동 없이 막대한 부를 쌓아 올리는 것, 주류 독점과 똑같은 거지. 다만 형태만 바뀌었을 뿐이야."], ["Le roi est mort, vive le roi.", "Le roi est mort, vive le roi."], ["No sooner were the spirit monopolies abolished than the railways came up, and banking companies; that, too, is profit without work.”", "주류 독점이 폐지되자마자 철도와 은행 회사들이 생겨났어. 그것도 노동 없는 이윤이지.\""]], [["“Yes, that may all be very true and clever.... Lie down, Krak!”", "* Le roi est mort, vive le roi: 프랑스어로 '왕은 죽었다, 왕이여 만세'."], ["Stepan Arkadyevitch called to his dog, who was scratching and turning over all the hay.", "\"그래, 다 옳고 영리한 말일지도 모르지…… 엎드려, 크라크!\""], ["He was obviously convinced of the correctness of his position, and so talked serenely and without haste.", "스테판 아르카지치가 건초를 온통 긁어 헤집고 뒤집어 놓고 있는 자기 개를 향해 소리쳤다."], ["“But you have not drawn the line between honest and dishonest work.", "그는 자기 입장이 옳다고 확신하는 듯, 차분하게 서두르지 않고 이야기했다."], ["That I receive a bigger salary than my chief clerk, though he knows more about the work than I do—that’s dishonest, I suppose?”", "\"하지만 자네는 정직한 노동과 부정직한 노동 사이에 선을 긋지 못했어. 내가 일에 대해 나보다 더 잘 아는 수석 서기보다 더 많은 봉급을 받는다—그건 부정직한 건가, 그렇지?\""]], [["“I can’t say.”", "\"뭐라 말 못 하겠네.\""]], [["“Well, but I can tell you: your receiving some five thousand, let’s say, for your work on the land, while our host, the peasant here, however hard he works, can never get more than fifty roubles, is just as dishonest as my earning more than my chief clerk, and Malthus getting more than a station-master.", "\"좋아, 그렇다면 내가 말해 주지. 자네가 토지에서 일한 대가로, 말하자면 오천쯤 받는 것과, 여기 우리 집주인인 농부가 아무리 죽도록 일해도 오십 루블 넘게는 결코 못 받는 것—그건 내가 수석 서기보다 더 버는 것이나, 말투스가 역장보다 더 버는 것과 똑같이 부정직한 거야."], ["No, quite the contrary; I see that society takes up a sort of antagonistic attitude to these people, which is utterly baseless, and I fancy there’s envy at the bottom of it....”", "아니, 오히려 정반대지. 내가 보기엔 사회가 이런 사람들에게 일종의 적대적인 태도를 취하는데, 그건 전혀 근거가 없는 거고, 그 밑바닥에는 시샘이 깔려 있는 것 같단 말이야……\""]], [["“No, that’s unfair,”", "\"아니, 그건 부당해요.\""], ["said Veslovsky; “how could envy come in?", "베슬로프스키가 말했다. \"어떻게 시샘이 끼어들 수 있겠어요?"], ["There is something not nice about that sort of business.”", "그런 종류의 사업에는 뭔가 마뜩잖은 데가 있어요.\""]], [["“You say,”", "\"자네 말은,\" 레빈이 말을 이었다."], ["Levin went on, “that it’s unjust for me to receive five thousand, while the peasant has fifty; that’s true.", "\"내가 오천을 받는데 농부는 오십을 받는 게 부당하다는 거지. 그건 맞아."], ["It is unfair, and I feel it, but....”", "부당하고, 나도 그걸 느껴. 하지만……\""]], [["“It really is.", "\"정말 그래요."], ["Why is it we spend our time riding, drinking, shooting, doing nothing, while they are forever at work?”", "어째서 우리는 말을 타고, 술을 마시고, 사냥을 하고, 아무것도 안 하면서 시간을 보내는데, 저 사람들은 늘 일만 하는 걸까요?\""], ["said Vassenka Veslovsky, obviously for the first time in his life reflecting on the question, and consequently considering it with perfect sincerity.", "바센카 베슬로프스키가 말했는데, 그는 분명 난생처음 이 문제를 곰곰이 생각해 보는 것이었고, 그래서 더없이 진지하게 그것을 따지고 있었다."]], [["“Yes, you feel it, but you don’t give him your property,”", "\"그래, 자네는 그걸 느끼면서도 자네 재산을 그에게 주지는 않잖아.\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, intentionally, as it seemed, provoking Levin.", "스테판 아르카지치가, 보아하니 일부러 레빈을 부추기려는 듯 말했다."]], [["There had arisen of late something like a secret antagonism between the two brothers-in-law; as though, since they had married sisters, a kind of rivalry had sprung up between them as to which was ordering his life best, and now this hostility showed itself in the conversation, as it began to take a personal note.", "근래 두 동서 사이에는 일종의 은밀한 적대감 같은 것이 생겨나 있었다. 마치 자매와 결혼한 뒤로 누가 자기 삶을 더 잘 꾸리고 있느냐를 두고 일종의 경쟁심이 그들 사이에 싹튼 듯했고, 이제 그 적대감이 개인적인 어조를 띠기 시작하면서 대화 속에 드러났다."]], [["“I don’t give it away, because no one demands that from me, and if I wanted to, I could not give it away,”", "\"내가 재산을 내주지 않는 건, 아무도 내게 그걸 요구하지 않기 때문이고, 또 내가 주고 싶다 한들 줄 수도 없기 때문이야.\""], ["answered Levin, “and have no one to give it to.”", "레빈이 대답했다. \"줄 사람도 없고.\""]], [["“Give it to this peasant, he would not refuse it.”", "\"이 농부에게 주게. 거절하지 않을 테니.\""]], [["“Yes, but how am I to give it up?", "\"그래, 하지만 내가 어떻게 내준단 말인가?"], ["Am I to go to him and make a deed of conveyance?”", "그에게 가서 양도 증서라도 써 줄까?\""]], [["“I don’t know; but if you are convinced that you have no right....”", "\"난 모르겠네. 하지만 자네가 권리가 없다고 확신한다면……\""]], [["“I’m not at all convinced.", "\"난 전혀 확신하지 않아."], ["On the contrary, I feel I have no right to give it up, that I have duties both to the land and to my family.”", "오히려 나는 그걸 내줄 권리가 없다고 느껴. 토지에 대해서도, 내 가족에 대해서도 의무가 있으니까.\""]], [["“No, excuse me, but if you consider this inequality is unjust, why is it you don’t act accordingly?...”", "\"아니, 미안하지만, 자네가 이 불평등이 부당하다고 여긴다면, 어째서 그에 맞게 행동하지 않는 건가?……\""]], [["“Well, I do act negatively on that idea, so far as not trying to increase the difference of position existing between him and me.”", "\"음, 나는 그 생각에 따라 소극적으로는 행동하고 있어. 그와 나 사이에 존재하는 처지의 격차를 더 벌리지 않으려 한다는 점에서는 말이야.\""]], [["“No, excuse me, that’s a paradox.”", "\"아니, 미안하지만, 그건 궤변이야.\""]], [["“Yes, there’s something of a sophistry about that,”", "\"그래요, 거기엔 뭔가 궤변 같은 데가 있어요.\""], ["Veslovsky agreed.", "베슬로프스키가 동의했다."], ["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["our host; so you’re not asleep yet?”", "집주인 양반, 그래 아직 안 잤소?\""], ["he said to the peasant who came into the barn, opening the creaking door.", "그는 삐걱거리는 문을 열고 헛간으로 들어온 농부에게 말했다."], ["“How is it you’re not asleep?”", "\"어째서 안 자는 거요?\""]], [["“No, how’s one to sleep!", "\"아니요, 어떻게 자겠습니까!"], ["I thought our gentlemen would be asleep, but I heard them chattering.", "나리들이 주무시는 줄 알았는데, 두런두런 말소리가 들리더군요."], ["I want to get a hook from here.", "여기서 갈고리를 하나 가져가려고요."], ["She won’t bite?”", "이놈이 물진 않겠지요?\""], ["he added, stepping cautiously with his bare feet.", "그는 맨발로 조심스럽게 발을 디디며 덧붙였다."]], [["“And where are you going to sleep?”", "\"그런데 자네는 어디서 잘 건가?\""]], [["“We are going out for the night with the beasts.”", "\"우리는 가축들하고 밤을 새우러 나갑니다.\""]], [["“Ah, what a night!”", "\"아, 정말 멋진 밤이군!\""], ["said Veslovsky, looking out at the edge of the hut and the unharnessed wagonette that could be seen in the faint light of the evening glow in the great frame of the open doors.", "베슬로프스키가, 활짝 열린 문의 커다란 틀 안으로 저녁놀의 희미한 빛을 받아 보이는 헛간 가장자리와 멍에 풀린 짐마차를 내다보며 말했다."], ["“But listen, there are women’s voices singing, and, on my word, not badly too.", "\"그런데 들어 봐요, 여자들 노랫소리가 들리네요. 게다가 정말이지 솜씨도 나쁘지 않은데."], ["Who’s that singing, my friend?”", "저거 누가 노래하는 거요, 친구?\""]], [["“That’s the maids from hard by here.”", "\"바로 이 근처 처녀들입니다.\""]], [["“Let’s go, let’s have a walk!", "\"가요, 산책이나 합시다!"], ["We shan’t go to sleep, you know.", "어차피 잠도 안 올 텐데."], ["Oblonsky, come along!”", "오블론스키, 같이 갑시다!\""]], [["“If one could only do both, lie here and go,”", "\"여기 누워 있는 것과 가는 것, 둘 다 할 수만 있다면 좋으련만.\""], ["answered Oblonsky, stretching.", "오블론스키가 기지개를 켜며 대답했다."], ["“It’s capital lying here.”", "\"여기 누워 있는 게 기막히게 좋단 말이야.\""]], [["“Well, I shall go by myself,”", "\"좋아요, 그럼 나 혼자 가지요.\""], ["said Veslovsky, getting up eagerly, and putting on his shoes and stockings.", "베슬로프스키가 벌떡 일어나 양말과 신발을 신으며 말했다."], ["“Good-bye, gentlemen.", "\"안녕히들 계세요, 신사 여러분."], ["If it’s fun, I’ll fetch you.", "재미있으면 모시러 올게요."], ["You’ve treated me to some good sport, and I won’t forget you.”", "좋은 사냥거리를 베풀어 주셨으니, 그 은혜는 잊지 않겠어요.\""]], [["“He really is a capital fellow, isn’t he?”", "\"저 친구 정말 기막힌 사람이지, 안 그래?\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, when Veslovsky had gone out and the peasant had closed the door after him.", "베슬로프스키가 나가고 농부가 그의 뒤로 문을 닫자, 스테판 아르카지치가 말했다."]], [["“Yes, capital,”", "\"그래, 기막힌 사람이야.\""], ["answered Levin, still thinking of the subject of their conversation just before.", "레빈이, 여전히 방금 전 대화의 주제를 생각하며 대답했다."], ["It seemed to him that he had clearly expressed his thoughts and feelings to the best of his capacity, and yet both of them, straightforward men and not fools, had said with one voice that he was comforting himself with sophistries.", "그가 보기에 자기는 능력껏 최선을 다해 자기 생각과 감정을 분명하게 표현한 것 같은데, 정직하고 어리석지도 않은 두 사람이 한목소리로 그가 궤변으로 스스로를 위로하고 있다고 말한 것이었다."], ["This disconcerted him.", "이 일이 그의 마음을 어지럽혔다."]], [["“It’s just this, my dear boy.", "\"바로 이거라네, 이 친구야."], ["One must do one of two things: either admit that the existing order of society is just, and then stick up for one’s rights in it; or acknowledge that you are enjoying unjust privileges, as I do, and then enjoy them and be satisfied.”", "둘 중 하나는 해야 해. 현존하는 사회 질서가 정당하다고 인정하고 그 안에서 자기 권리를 지키든가, 아니면 자네가 나처럼 부당한 특권을 누리고 있음을 인정하고 그것을 즐기며 만족하든가.\""]], [["“No, if it were unjust, you could not enjoy these advantages and be satisfied—at least I could not.", "\"아니야, 만약 그게 부당하다면 자네는 그 이점을 누리면서 만족할 수 없을 거야—적어도 나는 못 해."], ["The great thing for me is to feel that I’m not to blame.”", "나에게 가장 중요한 건 내가 비난받을 처지가 아니라고 느끼는 거야.\""]], [["“What do you say, why not go after all?”", "\"그건 그렇고, 어때, 끝내 안 갈 텐가?\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, evidently weary of the strain of thought.", "스테판 아르카지치가, 보아하니 생각의 긴장에 지친 듯 말했다."], ["“We shan’t go to sleep, you know.", "\"어차피 잠도 안 올 텐데."], ["Come, let’s go!”", "자, 가세!\""]], [["Levin did not answer.", "레빈은 대답하지 않았다."], ["What they had said in the conversation, that he acted justly only in a negative sense, absorbed his thoughts.", "대화 중에 그들이 한 말, 즉 자기가 오직 소극적인 의미에서만 정당하게 행동한다는 그 말이 그의 생각을 사로잡았다."], ["“Can it be that it’s only possible to be just negatively?”", "'정당해질 수 있는 게 오직 소극적으로만 가능하다는 게 정말일까?'"], ["he was asking himself.", "그는 자신에게 묻고 있었다."]], [["“How strong the smell of the fresh hay is, though,”", "\"그런데 갓 벤 건초 냄새가 어찌나 강한지.\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, getting up.", "스테판 아르카지치가 일어서며 말했다."], ["“There’s not a chance of sleeping.", "\"도무지 잘 가망이 없군."], ["Vassenka has been getting up some fun there.", "바센카가 저기서 뭔가 재미난 일을 벌이고 있어."], ["Do you hear the laughing and his voice?", "웃음소리며 저 친구 목소리가 들리지?"], ["Hadn’t we better go?", "우리도 가는 게 낫지 않겠나?"], ["Come along!”", "자, 같이 가세!\""]], [["“No, I’m not coming,”", "\"아니, 난 안 가.\""], ["answered Levin.", "레빈이 대답했다."]], [["“Surely that’s not a matter of principle too,”", "\"설마 그것도 원칙의 문제는 아니겠지.\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, smiling, as he felt about in the dark for his cap.", "스테판 아르카지치가 어둠 속에서 모자를 더듬어 찾으며 미소 띤 채 말했다."]], [["“It’s not a matter of principle, but why should I go?”", "\"원칙의 문제는 아니야. 하지만 내가 왜 가야 하나?\""]], [["“But do you know you are preparing trouble for yourself,”", "\"하지만 말이지, 자네는 스스로 골칫거리를 만들고 있는 거야.\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, finding his cap and getting up.", "스테판 아르카지치가 모자를 찾아 들고 일어서며 말했다."]], [["“How so?”", "\"어째서?\""]], [["“Do you suppose I don’t see the line you’ve taken up with your wife?", "\"내가 자네가 아내한테 어떤 태도를 취하고 있는지 못 본 줄 아나?"], ["I heard how it’s a question of the greatest consequence, whether or not you’re to be away for a couple of days’ shooting.", "자네가 이틀쯤 사냥하러 떠나도 되느냐 안 되느냐가 무슨 막중한 문제라도 되는 양 하더군."], ["That’s all very well as an idyllic episode, but for your whole life that won’t answer.", "전원시 같은 한 장면으로야 다 좋지. 하지만 평생을 그렇게 살 수는 없는 거야."], ["A man must be independent; he has his masculine interests.", "남자란 독립적이어야 해. 남자에겐 남자의 관심사가 있는 거니까."], ["A man has to be manly,”", "남자는 사내다워야 하는 거야.\""], ["said Oblonsky, opening the door.", "오블론스키가 문을 열며 말했다."]], [["“In what way?", "\"어떤 식으로?"], ["To go running after servant girls?”", "하녀 처녀들이나 쫓아다니면서?\""], ["said Levin.", "레빈이 말했다."]], [["“Why not, if it amuses him?", "\"재미있다는데 못 할 거 뭐 있나?"], ["Ça ne tire pas à conséquence.", "Ça ne tire pas à conséquence."], ["It won’t do my wife any harm, and it’ll amuse me.", "내 아내에게 해가 될 것도 없고, 나야 즐거우니까."], ["The great thing is to respect the sanctity of the home.", "중요한 건 가정의 신성함을 존중하는 거야."], ["There should be nothing in the home.", "가정 안에서는 아무 일도 없어야 하고."], ["But don’t tie your own hands.”", "하지만 자기 손발을 스스로 묶진 말라고.\""]], [["“Perhaps so,” said Levin dryly, and he turned on his side. “Tomorrow, early, I want to go shooting, and I won’t wake anyone, and shall set off at daybreak.”", "* Ça ne tire pas à conséquence: 프랑스어로 '그건 별일 아니다, 뒤탈 없는 일이다'."]], [["“Messieurs, venez vite!”", "\"그럴지도 모르지.\" 레빈이 무뚝뚝하게 말하고는 모로 돌아누웠다."], ["they heard the voice of Veslovsky coming back.", "\"내일은 아침 일찍 사냥하러 나갈 거야. 아무도 깨우지 않고, 동틀 무렵에 떠날 거고.\""], ["“Charmante!", "\"Messieurs, venez vite!\""], ["I’ve made such a discovery.", "베슬로프스키가 돌아오며 외치는 소리가 들렸다."], ["Charmante!", "\"Charmante!"], ["a perfect Gretchen, and I’ve already made friends with her.", "굉장한 걸 발견했어요. Charmante!"], ["Really, exceedingly pretty,”", "완벽한 그레트헨이에요. 벌써 친해졌다니까요."], ["he declared in a tone of approval, as though she had been made pretty entirely on his account, and he was expressing his satisfaction with the entertainment that had been provided for him.", "정말이지 더없이 예뻐요.\" 그는, 마치 그 처녀가 오로지 자기를 위해 예쁘게 만들어지기라도 한 양, 자기에게 베풀어진 여흥에 대한 만족을 표하는 듯한 흡족한 어조로 단언했다."]], [["Levin pretended to be asleep, while Oblonsky, putting on his slippers, and lighting a cigar, walked out of the barn, and soon their voices were lost.", "* Messieurs, venez vite!: 프랑스어로 '여러분, 어서 와요!'. Charmante: '매력적이다'. 그레트헨: 괴테의 「파우스트」에 나오는 순박한 처녀."]], [["For a long while Levin could not get to sleep.", "레빈은 자는 척했고, 오블론스키는 슬리퍼를 신고 시가에 불을 붙이고는 헛간을 나갔으며, 이윽고 그들의 목소리는 멀어져 사라졌다."], ["He heard the horses munching hay, then he heard the peasant and his elder boy getting ready for the night, and going off for the night watch with the beasts, then he heard the soldier arranging his bed on the other side of the barn, with his nephew, the younger son of their peasant host.", "레빈은 한참 동안 잠을 이루지 못했다. 말들이 건초를 우적우적 씹는 소리가 들렸고, 이어 농부와 그의 큰아들이 밤을 새울 채비를 하고 가축들과 함께 야간 망을 보러 나가는 소리가 들렸으며, 그다음에는 병사가 헛간 저편에서, 집주인 농부의 작은아들인 조카와 함께 잠자리를 펴는 소리가 들렸다."], ["He heard the boy in his shrill little voice telling his uncle what he thought about the dogs, who seemed to him huge and terrible creatures, and asking what the dogs were going to hunt next day, and the soldier in a husky, sleepy voice, telling him the sportsmen were going in the morning to the marsh, and would shoot with their guns; and then, to check the boy’s questions, he said, “Go to sleep, Vaska; go to sleep, or you’ll catch it,”", "사내아이가 가느다란 목소리로 삼촌에게 개들에 대한 자기 생각을—그에게 개들은 거대하고 무시무시한 짐승으로 보이는 모양이었다—이야기하며 내일 개들이 무엇을 사냥하느냐고 묻는 소리가 들렸고, 병사가 잠에 겨운 쉰 목소리로 나리들이 아침에 늪으로 가서 총으로 사냥을 할 거라고 일러 주는 소리가 들렸다. 그러고는 아이의 질문을 막으려고 \"자거라, 바스카."], ["and soon after he began snoring himself, and everything was still.", "자, 안 그러면 혼날 줄 알아.\" 하더니, 곧 코를 골기 시작했고, 모든 것이 고요해졌다."], ["He could only hear the snort of the horses, and the guttural cry of a snipe.", "들리는 것이라곤 말들이 코를 푸는 소리와 도요새의 목쉰 울음소리뿐이었다."]], [["“Is it really only negative?”", "'정말로 그저 소극적인 것뿐일까?'"], ["he repeated to himself.", "그는 속으로 되뇌었다."], ["“Well, what of it?", "'뭐, 그렇다 한들?"], ["It’s not my fault.”", "내 잘못은 아니잖아.'"], ["And he began thinking about the next day.", "그러고는 다음 날에 대해 생각하기 시작했다."]], [["“Tomorrow I’ll go out early, and I’ll make a point of keeping cool.", "'내일은 일찍 나가서, 꼭 침착함을 잃지 말아야지. 도요새도 많고, 멧닭도 있어."], ["There are lots of snipe; and there are grouse too.", "돌아오면 키티의 쪽지가 와 있겠지."], ["When I come back there’ll be the note from Kitty.", "그래, 스티바 말이 맞을지도 몰라."], ["Yes, Stiva may be right, I’m not manly with her, I’m tied to her apron-strings....", "난 그녀에게 사내답지 못해. 치맛자락에 매여 있는 거지……"], ["Well, it can’t be helped!", "뭐, 어쩔 수 없지!"], ["Negative again....”", "또 소극적이군……'"]], [["Half asleep, he heard the laughter and mirthful talk of Veslovsky and Stepan Arkadyevitch.", "비몽사몽간에 그는 베슬로프스키와 스테판 아르카지치의 웃음소리며 흥겨운 이야기 소리를 들었다."], ["For an instant he opened his eyes: the moon was up, and in the open doorway, brightly lighted up by the moonlight, they were standing talking.", "잠깐 눈을 떴다. 달이 떠 있었고, 달빛에 환히 비친 활짝 열린 문간에 그들이 서서 이야기하고 있었다."], ["Stepan Arkadyevitch was saying something of the freshness of one girl, comparing her to a freshly peeled nut, and Veslovsky with his infectious laugh was repeating some words, probably said to him by a peasant: “Ah, you do your best to get round her!”", "스테판 아르카지치가 한 처녀의 싱그러움에 대해 뭐라고 하며 그녀를 갓 깐 호두에 비유하고 있었고, 베슬로프스키는 그 특유의 사람을 따라 웃게 만드는 웃음을 터뜨리며, 아마도 어느 농부가 그에게 한 말인 듯한 어떤 구절을 되뇌고 있었다. \"아, 자네 그 아가씨 꼬여 보려고 별짓을 다 하는구먼!\""], ["Levin, half asleep, said:", "레빈은 비몽사몽간에 말했다."]], [["“Gentlemen, tomorrow before daylight!”", "\"여러분, 내일 동트기 전에!\""], ["and fell asleep.", "그러고는 잠이 들었다."]], [["# Chapter 12", "# Chapter 12"]], [["Waking up at earliest dawn, Levin tried to wake his companions.", "이른 새벽에 잠에서 깬 레빈은 일행을 깨우려고 애썼다."], ["Vassenka, lying on his stomach, with one leg in a stocking thrust out, was sleeping so soundly that he could elicit no response.", "바센카는 배를 깔고 누운 채 양말 신은 한쪽 다리를 쑥 내밀고는 어찌나 깊이 잠들었는지 아무리 해도 반응을 끌어낼 수 없었다."], ["Oblonsky, half asleep, declined to get up so early.", "오블론스키는 비몽사몽간에 이렇게 일찍 일어나기를 마다했다."], ["Even Laska, who was asleep, curled up in the hay, got up unwillingly, and lazily stretched out and straightened her hind legs one after the other.", "건초 속에 몸을 둥글게 말고 자던 라스카조차 마지못해 일어나, 굼뜨게 뒷다리를 하나씩 차례로 쭉 뻗어 펴는 것이었다."], ["Getting on his boots and stockings, taking his gun, and carefully opening the creaking door of the barn, Levin went out into the road.", "레빈은 장화와 양말을 신고 총을 챙긴 뒤, 삐걱거리는 헛간 문을 조심스레 열고 길로 나섰다."], ["The coachmen were sleeping in their carriages, the horses were dozing.", "마부들은 마차 안에서 자고 있었고 말들은 졸고 있었다."], ["Only one was lazily eating oats, dipping its nose into the manger.", "오직 한 마리만이 구유에 코를 박고 굼뜨게 귀리를 씹고 있었다."], ["It was still gray out-of-doors.", "바깥은 아직 잿빛이었다."]], [["“Why are you up so early, my dear?”", "\"왜 이리 일찍 일어났수, 양반?\""], ["the old woman, their hostess, said, coming out of the hut and addressing him affectionately as an old friend.", "그들의 안주인인 노파가 오두막에서 나오며, 오랜 벗을 대하듯 정답게 그에게 말을 건넸다."]], [["“Going shooting, granny.", "\"사냥하러 가는 길이오, 할멈."], ["Do I go this way to the marsh?”", "늪으로 가려면 이쪽으로 가면 되오?\""]], [["“Straight out at the back; by our threshing floor, my dear, and hemp patches; there’s a little footpath.”", "\"뒤로 곧장 나가시구려. 우리 탈곡장하고 삼밭을 지나면 작은 오솔길이 하나 있다우.\""], ["Stepping carefully with her sunburnt, bare feet, the old woman conducted Levin, and moved back the fence for him by the threshing floor.", "노파는 햇볕에 그을린 맨발로 조심스레 걸음을 옮기며 레빈을 안내했고, 탈곡장 옆의 울타리를 옆으로 치워 그가 지나가게 해 주었다."]], [["“Straight on and you’ll come to the marsh.", "\"곧장 가면 늪이 나온다우."], ["Our lads drove the cattle there yesterday evening.”", "어제저녁에 우리 집 애들이 거기로 소를 몰고 갔지.\""]], [["Laska ran eagerly forward along the little path.", "라스카가 오솔길을 따라 신이 나서 앞서 달려 나갔다."], ["Levin followed her with a light, rapid step, continually looking at the sky.", "레빈은 가볍고 빠른 걸음으로 그 뒤를 따르며 끊임없이 하늘을 올려다보았다."], ["He hoped the sun would not be up before he reached the marsh.", "그는 늪에 닿기 전에 해가 떠오르지 않기를 바랐다."], ["But the sun did not delay.", "그러나 해는 머뭇거리지 않았다."], ["The moon, which had been bright when he went out, by now shone only like a crescent of quicksilver.", "그가 나설 때만 해도 환했던 달은 이제 수은빛 초승달처럼 희미하게 빛날 뿐이었다."], ["The pink flush of dawn, which one could not help seeing before, now had to be sought to be discerned at all.", "조금 전까지만 해도 절로 눈에 들어오던 새벽의 분홍빛 노을은, 이제 일부러 찾지 않으면 분간조차 할 수 없게 되었다."], ["What were before undefined, vague blurs in the distant countryside could now be distinctly seen.", "멀리 들판에서 윤곽이 잡히지 않던 어슴푸레한 얼룩들이 이제는 또렷이 보였다."], ["They were sheaves of rye.", "그것은 호밀 단이었다."], ["The dew, not visible till the sun was up, wetted Levin’s legs and his blouse above his belt in the high growing, fragrant hemp patch, from which the pollen had already fallen out.", "해가 떠오르기 전까지는 보이지 않던 이슬이, 이미 꽃가루가 다 떨어진 키 큰 향기로운 삼밭에서 레빈의 다리와 허리띠 위쪽 블라우스를 적셨다."], ["In the transparent stillness of morning the smallest sounds were audible.", "투명한 아침의 정적 속에서는 아주 작은 소리까지 들렸다."], ["A bee flew by Levin’s ear with the whizzing sound of a bullet. He looked carefully, and saw a second and a third.", "벌 한 마리가 총알 같은 윙 소리를 내며 레빈의 귓가를 스쳐 날아갔다."], ["They were all flying from the beehives behind the hedge, and they disappeared over the hemp patch in the direction of the marsh.", "그가 가만히 살펴보니 두 번째, 세 번째 벌이 눈에 들어왔다. 모두 울타리 뒤의 벌통에서 날아와, 삼밭 위로 늪 방향으로 사라졌다."], ["The path led straight to the marsh.", "오솔길은 곧장 늪으로 이어졌다."], ["The marsh could be recognized by the mist which rose from it, thicker in one place and thinner in another, so that the reeds and willow bushes swayed like islands in this mist.", "늪은 거기서 피어오르는 안개로 알아볼 수 있었는데, 안개는 한쪽은 짙고 다른 쪽은 옅어서, 갈대와 버드나무 덤불이 그 안개 속에서 섬처럼 일렁였다."], ["At the edge of the marsh and the road, peasant boys and men, who had been herding for the night, were lying, and in the dawn all were asleep under their coats.", "늪과 길의 가장자리에는 밤새 가축을 친 농부 아이들과 사내들이 누워 있었는데, 새벽녘에 모두 외투를 덮고 잠들어 있었다. 그들에게서 멀지 않은 곳에 발이 묶인 말 세 마리가 있었다."], ["Not far from them were three hobbled horses.", "그중 한 마리가 쇠사슬을 절그럭거렸다."], ["One of them clanked a chain. Laska walked beside her master, pressing a little forward and looking round.", "라스카는 주인 곁에서 조금 앞으로 나서며 주위를 둘러보며 걸었다."], ["Passing the sleeping peasants and reaching the first reeds, Levin examined his pistols and let his dog off.", "잠든 농부들을 지나 첫 갈대밭에 이른 레빈은 권총을 살펴본 뒤 개를 풀어놓았다."], ["One of the horses, a sleek, dark-brown three-year-old, seeing the dog, started away, switched its tail and snorted.", "윤기 흐르는 짙은 갈색의 세 살배기 말 한 마리가 개를 보고는 놀라 뒷걸음치며 꼬리를 휘젓고 콧김을 뿜었다."], ["The other horses too were frightened, and splashing through the water with their hobbled legs, and drawing their hoofs out of the thick mud with a squelching sound, they bounded out of the marsh.", "다른 말들도 놀라 묶인 다리로 물을 첨벙거리며, 진창에서 발굽을 철벅철벅 빼내며 늪 밖으로 뛰쳐나갔다."], ["Laska stopped, looking ironically at the horses and inquiringly at Levin.", "라스카는 말들을 비웃듯 바라보고 레빈을 향해 묻는 듯한 눈길을 보내며 멈춰 섰다."], ["Levin patted Laska, and whistled as a sign that she might begin.", "레빈은 라스카를 토닥이고는 이제 시작해도 좋다는 신호로 휘파람을 불었다."]], [["Laska ran joyfully and anxiously through the slush that swayed under her.", "라스카는 발밑에서 출렁이는 진창을 가로질러 기쁘게, 그러나 조바심을 내며 달렸다."]], [["Running into the marsh among the familiar scents of roots, marsh plants, and slime, and the extraneous smell of horse dung, Laska detected at once a smell that pervaded the whole marsh, the scent of that strong-smelling bird that always excited her more than any other.", "뿌리며 늪의 풀이며 진흙의 익숙한 냄새, 그리고 말똥의 낯선 냄새 사이로 늪에 뛰어든 라스카는, 늪 전체에 퍼져 있는 어떤 냄새를, 그러니까 늘 그 어떤 새보다도 자기를 흥분시키는 그 독한 냄새를 가진 새의 냄새를 단번에 알아챘다."], ["Here and there among the moss and marsh plants this scent was very strong, but it was impossible to determine in which direction it grew stronger or fainter.", "이끼며 늪풀 사이 여기저기에 그 냄새가 무척 강했지만, 어느 방향으로 갈수록 짙어지고 옅어지는지는 가늠할 수 없었다."], ["To find the direction, she had to go farther away from the wind.", "방향을 잡으려면 바람에서 더 멀어져야 했다."], ["Not feeling the motion of her legs, Laska bounded with a stiff gallop, so that at each bound she could stop short, to the right, away from the wind that blew from the east before sunrise, and turned facing the wind.", "다리의 움직임도 느끼지 못한 채, 라스카는 한 번 뛸 때마다 단번에 멈출 수 있도록 뻣뻣한 질주로 껑충껑충 뛰어, 해 뜨기 전 동쪽에서 불어오는 바람을 피해 오른쪽으로 갔다가, 바람을 마주 보고 돌아섰다."], ["Sniffing in the air with dilated nostrils, she felt at once that not their tracks only but they themselves were here before her, and not one, but many.", "벌어진 콧구멍으로 공기를 들이켜며, 라스카는 단번에 그들의 발자취뿐 아니라 그들 자신이, 그것도 한 마리가 아니라 여러 마리가 자기 앞에 있다는 것을 알아챘다."], ["Laska slackened her speed.", "라스카는 속도를 늦췄다."], ["They were here, but where precisely she could not yet determine.", "그들이 여기 있긴 한데, 정확히 어디인지는 아직 알 수 없었다."], ["To find the very spot, she began to make a circle, when suddenly her master’s voice drew her off.", "바로 그 자리를 찾으려고 그녀가 원을 그리기 시작했을 때, 갑자기 주인의 목소리가 그녀를 끌어냈다."], ["“Laska! here?”", "\"라스카!"], ["he asked, pointing her to a different direction.", "여기?\" 그가 다른 방향을 가리키며 물었다."], ["She stopped, asking him if she had better not go on doing as she had begun.", "그녀는 멈춰 서서, 자기가 시작한 대로 계속하는 게 낫지 않겠느냐고 그에게 물었다."], ["But he repeated his command in an angry voice, pointing to a spot covered with water, where there could not be anything.", "그러나 그는 화난 목소리로 명령을 되풀이하며, 아무것도 있을 리 없는 물로 덮인 곳을 가리켰다."], ["She obeyed him, pretending she was looking, so as to please him, went round it, and went back to her former position, and was at once aware of the scent again.", "그녀는 그를 기쁘게 해 주려고 찾는 척하며 복종하여 그곳을 한 바퀴 돌고는, 본래의 자리로 돌아왔고, 곧 다시 그 냄새를 느꼈다."], ["Now when he was not hindering her, she knew what to do, and without looking at what was under her feet, and to her vexation stumbling over a high stump into the water, but righting herself with her strong, supple legs, she began making the circle which was to make all clear to her.", "이제 그가 방해하지 않으니 그녀는 무엇을 해야 할지 알았고, 발밑에 무엇이 있는지 보지도 않은 채—분하게도 높은 그루터기에 걸려 물에 빠지긴 했지만, 튼튼하고 유연한 다리로 몸을 바로 세우며—모든 것을 분명히 밝혀 줄 원을 그리기 시작했다."], ["The scent of them reached her, stronger and stronger, and more and more defined, and all at once it became perfectly clear to her that one of them was here, behind this tuft of reeds, five paces in front of her; she stopped, and her whole body was still and rigid.", "그들의 냄새가 점점 더 강하게, 점점 더 또렷하게 그녀에게 다가왔고, 별안간 그녀에게 한 마리가 여기, 이 갈대 덤불 뒤, 다섯 걸음 앞에 있다는 것이 더없이 분명해졌다. 그녀는 멈춰 섰고, 온몸이 고요하게 굳었다."], ["On her short legs she could see nothing in front of her, but by the scent she knew it was sitting not more than five paces off.", "짧은 다리로는 앞에 아무것도 보이지 않았지만, 냄새로 그것이 다섯 걸음도 안 되는 곳에 앉아 있음을 알았다."], ["She stood still, feeling more and more conscious of it, and enjoying it in anticipation.", "그녀는 그것을 점점 더 또렷이 의식하며, 미리부터 그 즐거움을 맛보며 가만히 서 있었다."], ["Her tail was stretched straight and tense, and only wagging at the extreme end.", "꼬리는 곧고 팽팽하게 뻗은 채 맨 끝만 흔들렸다."], ["Her mouth was slightly open, her ears raised.", "입은 조금 벌어졌고 귀는 쫑긋 섰다."], ["One ear had been turned wrong side out as she ran up, and she breathed heavily but warily, and still more warily looked round, but more with her eyes than her head, to her master.", "한쪽 귀는 달려오느라 안팎이 뒤집혀 있었고, 그녀는 무겁게, 그러나 조심스럽게 숨을 쉬었고, 더더욱 조심스럽게 주위를 둘러보았으나, 고개보다는 눈으로 주인을 향해 돌아보았다."], ["He was coming along with the face she knew so well, though the eyes were always terrible to her.", "그는 그녀에게 너무도 익숙한 얼굴로 다가오고 있었는데, 그 눈만은 그녀에게 늘 무서웠다."], ["He stumbled over the stump as he came, and moved, as she thought, extraordinarily slowly.", "그는 다가오면서 그루터기에 걸려 비틀거렸고, 그녀가 보기에는 유난히 느릿느릿 움직였다."], ["She thought he came slowly, but he was running.", "그녀는 그가 느리게 온다고 여겼지만, 사실 그는 달리고 있었다."]], [["Noticing Laska’s special attitude as she crouched on the ground, as it were, scratching big prints with her hind paws, and with her mouth slightly open, Levin knew she was pointing at grouse, and with an inward prayer for luck, especially with the first bird, he ran up to her.", "뒷발로 큼직한 자국을 긁어 파듯이 몸을 잔뜩 웅크리고 입을 조금 벌린 라스카의 그 특이한 자세를 알아챈 레빈은, 그녀가 들꿩을 가리키고 있음을 알았고, 행운을, 특히 첫 새에 행운이 따르기를 마음속으로 빌며 그녀에게 달려갔다."], ["Coming quite close up to her, he could from his height look beyond her, and he saw with his eyes what she was seeing with her nose.", "그녀 곁에 바짝 다가서자, 키가 큰 그는 그녀 너머를 볼 수 있었고, 그녀가 코로 보고 있던 것을 자기 눈으로 보았다."], ["In a space between two little thickets, at a couple of yards’ distance, he could see a grouse.", "작은 덤불 두 개 사이의 빈 공간, 두어 야드 거리에 들꿩 한 마리가 보였다."], ["Turning its head, it was listening.", "그것은 고개를 돌린 채 귀를 기울이고 있었다."], ["Then lightly preening and folding its wings, it disappeared round a corner with a clumsy wag of its tail.", "그러더니 가볍게 깃을 다듬고 날개를 접고는, 꼬리를 어색하게 한 번 흔들며 모퉁이 뒤로 사라졌다."]], [["“Fetch it, fetch it!”", "\"물어 와, 물어 와!\""], ["shouted Levin, giving Laska a shove from behind.", "레빈이 라스카를 뒤에서 떠밀며 외쳤다."]], [["“But I can’t go,”", "'하지만 갈 수가 없어.'"], ["thought Laska.", "라스카는 생각했다."], ["“Where am I to go?", "'어디로 가라는 거지?"], ["From here I feel them, but if I move forward I shall know nothing of where they are or who they are.”", "여기서는 냄새가 느껴지는데, 앞으로 나가면 그들이 어디 있는지, 누구인지 아무것도 모르게 될 텐데.'"], ["But then he shoved her with his knee, and in an excited whisper said, “Fetch it, Laska.”", "그러나 그때 그가 무릎으로 그녀를 떠밀며 흥분한 속삭임으로 말했다. \"물어 와, 라스카.\""]], [["“Well, if that’s what he wishes, I’ll do it, but I can’t answer for myself now,”", "'좋아, 그게 그가 바라는 거라면 하겠어, 하지만 이제 나도 나를 책임질 수 없어.'"], ["she thought, and darted forward as fast as her legs would carry her between the thick bushes.", "그녀는 그렇게 생각하며 다리가 닿는 데까지 빠르게 빽빽한 덤불 사이로 쏜살같이 달려 나갔다."], ["She scented nothing now; she could only see and hear, without understanding anything.", "이제 그녀는 아무 냄새도 맡지 못했다. 다만 아무것도 이해하지 못한 채 보고 들을 수 있을 뿐이었다."]], [["Ten paces from her former place a grouse rose with a guttural cry and the peculiar round sound of its wings.", "전에 있던 자리에서 열 걸음 떨어진 곳에서 들꿩 한 마리가 목쉰 울음소리와 날개의 독특한 둥근 소리를 내며 날아올랐다."], ["And immediately after the shot it splashed heavily with its white breast on the wet mire.", "그리고 총성이 울리자마자 그것은 하얀 가슴팍으로 젖은 진창에 무겁게 첨벙 떨어졌다."], ["Another bird did not linger, but rose behind Levin without the dog.", "또 한 마리는 머뭇거리지 않고, 개도 없이 레빈의 등 뒤에서 날아올랐다."], ["When Levin turned towards it, it was already some way off.", "레빈이 그쪽으로 몸을 돌렸을 때는 이미 제법 멀리 가 있었다."], ["But his shot caught it.", "그러나 그의 총알이 그것을 맞혔다."], ["Flying twenty paces further, the second grouse rose upwards, and whirling round like a ball, dropped heavily on a dry place.", "스무 걸음을 더 날아간 두 번째 들꿩은 위로 솟구쳤다가, 공처럼 빙글빙글 돌며 마른 땅에 무겁게 떨어졌다."]], [["“Come, this is going to be some good!”", "'이거, 제법 잘되겠는걸!'"], ["thought Levin, packing the warm and fat grouse into his game bag.", "레빈은 따뜻하고 통통한 들꿩을 사냥 자루에 챙겨 넣으며 생각했다."], ["“Eh, Laska, will it be good?”", "'어때, 라스카, 잘되겠지?'"]], [["When Levin, after loading his gun, moved on, the sun had fully risen, though unseen behind the storm-clouds.", "레빈이 총을 장전한 뒤 다시 나아갈 무렵에는, 비록 먹구름에 가려 보이지는 않았지만 해가 완전히 떠올라 있었다."], ["The moon had lost all of its luster, and was like a white cloud in the sky.", "달은 광채를 다 잃고 하늘에 뜬 흰 구름 같았다."], ["Not a single star could be seen.", "별은 단 하나도 보이지 않았다."], ["The sedge, silvery with dew before, now shone like gold.", "조금 전까지 이슬에 은빛으로 반짝이던 사초는 이제 금빛으로 빛났다."], ["The stagnant pools were all like amber.", "고인 웅덩이들은 모두 호박빛이었다."], ["The blue of the grass had changed to yellow-green.", "풀의 푸른빛은 누르스름한 초록으로 바뀌어 있었다."], ["The marsh birds twittered and swarmed about the brook and upon the bushes that glittered with dew and cast long shadows.", "늪의 새들이 지저귀며, 이슬에 반짝이고 긴 그림자를 드리운 개울가와 덤불 위로 떼 지어 모여들었다."], ["A hawk woke up and settled on a haycock, turning its head from side to side and looking discontentedly at the marsh.", "매 한 마리가 잠에서 깨어 건초 더미 위에 내려앉아, 고개를 이리저리 돌리며 불만스럽게 늪을 내려다보았다."], ["Crows were flying about the field, and a bare-legged boy was driving the horses to an old man, who had got up from under his long coat and was combing his hair.", "까마귀들이 들판 위를 날아다녔고, 맨다리의 소년 하나가 말들을 한 노인에게 몰고 가고 있었는데, 노인은 긴 외투 밑에서 일어나 머리를 빗고 있었다."], ["The smoke from the gun was white as milk over the green of the grass.", "총에서 나는 연기는 풀의 초록 위로 우윳빛처럼 하였다."]], [["One of the boys ran up to Levin.", "소년들 가운데 하나가 레빈에게로 달려왔다."]], [["“Uncle, there were ducks here yesterday!”", "\"아저씨, 어제 여기 오리들이 있었어요!\""], ["he shouted to him, and he walked a little way off behind him.", "그가 외치고는 그의 뒤를 조금 떨어져 따라왔다."]], [["And Levin was doubly pleased, in sight of the boy, who expressed his approval, at killing three snipe, one after another, straight off.", "그리고 레빈은, 자신을 흐뭇해하는 그 소년이 보는 앞에서 도요새 세 마리를 잇따라 단번에 잡은 것이 갑절로 기뻤다."]], [["# Chapter 13", "# Chapter 13"]], [["The sportsman’s saying, that if the first beast or the first bird is not missed, the day will be lucky, turned out correct.", "첫 짐승이나 첫 새를 놓치지 않으면 그날은 운수가 좋다는 사냥꾼들의 속설은 옳은 것으로 드러났다."]], [["At ten o’clock Levin, weary, hungry, and happy after a tramp of twenty miles, returned to his night’s lodging with nineteen head of fine game and one duck, which he tied to his belt, as it would not go into the game bag.", "스무 베르스타나 걸은 끝에 지치고 배고프고 행복해진 레빈은 열 시에 좋은 사냥감 열아홉 마리와 오리 한 마리를 들고 잠자리로 돌아왔다. 오리는 사냥 자루에 들어가지 않아 허리띠에 매달았다."], ["His companions had long been awake, and had had time to get hungry and have breakfast.", "그의 일행은 이미 오래전에 깨어 배가 고파져 아침까지 먹은 뒤였다."]], [["“Wait a bit, wait a bit, I know there are nineteen,”", "\"잠깐, 잠깐만, 열아홉 마리인 거 다 알아.\""], ["said Levin, counting a second time over the grouse and snipe, that looked so much less important now, bent and dry and bloodstained, with heads crooked aside, than they did when they were flying.", "레빈은 멧닭과 도요새를 다시 한 번 세며 말했다. 그것들은 날아다닐 때와 달리 지금은 구부정하고 바싹 마른 채 피로 얼룩지고 고개가 한쪽으로 꺾여, 훨씬 보잘것없어 보였다."]], [["The number was verified, and Stepan Arkadyevitch’s envy pleased Levin.", "수가 맞았고, 스테판 아르카지치가 부러워하는 모습에 레빈은 흐뭇해졌다."], ["He was pleased too on returning to find the man sent by Kitty with a note was already there.", "돌아와 보니 키티가 쪽지를 들려 보낸 심부름꾼이 이미 와 있는 것을 알고 그는 또 한 번 기뻤다."]], [["“I am perfectly well and happy.", "\"저는 아주 잘 지내고 행복해요."], ["If you were uneasy about me, you can feel easier than ever.", "제 걱정을 하셨다면, 이제 어느 때보다 마음을 놓으셔도 돼요."], ["I’ve a new bodyguard, Marya Vlasyevna,”—this was the midwife, a new and important personage in Levin’s domestic life.", "새로 호위병이 한 명 생겼답니다, 마리야 블라시예브나라고요.\" 이는 산파로, 레빈의 가정생활에서 새롭고도 중요한 인물이었다."], ["“She has come to have a look at me.", "\"그분이 저를 보러 오셨어요."], ["She found me perfectly well, and we have kept her till you are back.", "제가 아주 건강하다고 하셨고, 당신이 돌아오실 때까지 붙잡아 두었어요."], ["All are happy and well, and please, don’t be in a hurry to come back, but, if the sport is good, stay another day.”", "다들 행복하고 무탈하니, 부디 서둘러 돌아오려 하지 마세요. 사냥이 잘되거든 하루 더 머무르셔도 좋아요.\""]], [["These two pleasures, his lucky shooting and the letter from his wife, were so great that two slightly disagreeable incidents passed lightly over Levin.", "운 좋은 사냥과 아내의 편지라는 이 두 가지 기쁨이 너무나 컸던 터라, 살짝 언짢은 두 가지 사건도 레빈을 가볍게 스쳐 지나갔다."], ["One was that the chestnut trace horse, who had been unmistakably overworked on the previous day, was off his feed and out of sorts.", "하나는 전날 분명히 과하게 부려 먹은 밤색 곁말이 여물도 마다하고 시들시들해진 것이었다. 마부는 말했다."], ["The coachman said he was “Overdriven yesterday, Konstantin Dmitrievitch.", "\"어제 너무 부려 먹은 겁니다, 콘스탄틴 드미트리치. 정말이라니까요!"], ["Yes, indeed! driven ten miles with no sense!”", "십 베르스타를 무작정 내달렸으니!\""]], [["The other unpleasant incident, which for the first minute destroyed his good humor, though later he laughed at it a great deal, was to find that of all the provisions Kitty had provided in such abundance that one would have thought there was enough for a week, nothing was left.", "다른 불쾌한 사건은 처음 잠깐은 그의 좋은 기분을 망쳐 놓았으나, 나중에는 한참을 두고 웃었던 일로, 키티가 일주일은 너끈히 버틸 만큼 넉넉히 챙겨 준 그 많은 식량 가운데 남은 것이라곤 하나도 없다는 사실을 알게 된 것이었다."], ["On his way back, tired and hungry from shooting, Levin had so distinct a vision of meat-pies that as he approached the hut he seemed to smell and taste them, as Laska had smelt the game, and he immediately told Philip to give him some.", "돌아오는 길에 사냥으로 지치고 배가 고팠던 레빈은 고기 파이를 어찌나 또렷이 떠올렸던지, 라스카가 사냥감 냄새를 맡듯 오두막에 다가가면서 그 냄새와 맛이 느껴지는 것만 같았고, 곧장 필리프에게 좀 가져오라고 일렀다."], ["It appeared that there were no pies left, nor even any chicken.", "그런데 파이는 한 조각도 남지 않았고, 닭고기조차 없는 모양이었다."]], [["“Well, this fellow’s appetite!”", "\"이런, 저 친구 식욕 좀 보게!\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, laughing and pointing at Vassenka Veslovsky.", "스테판 아르카지치가 바센카 베슬롭스키를 가리키며 웃으며 말했다."], ["“I never suffer from loss of appetite, but he’s really marvelous!...”", "\"나도 식욕이 떨어지는 법이 없는 사람이지만, 저 친구는 정말 대단해!……\""]], [["“Well, it can’t be helped,”", "\"뭐, 어쩔 수 없지.\""], ["said Levin, looking gloomily at Veslovsky.", "레빈은 베슬롭스키를 침울하게 바라보며 말했다."], ["“Well, Philip, give me some beef, then.”", "\"그럼 필리프, 쇠고기라도 좀 주게.\""]], [["“The beef’s been eaten, and the bones given to the dogs,”", "\"쇠고기는 다 먹었고, 뼈다귀는 개들에게 주었습니다.\""], ["answered Philip.", "필리프가 대답했다."]], [["Levin was so hurt that he said, in a tone of vexation, “You might have left me something!”", "레빈은 어찌나 서운했던지 분이 난 어조로 말했다. \"내 몫으로 좀 남겨 두었으면 좋았잖아!\""], ["and he felt ready to cry.", "그는 울고 싶은 심정이었다."]], [["“Then put away the game,”", "\"그럼 사냥감을 치워 두게.\""], ["he said in a shaking voice to Philip, trying not to look at Vassenka, “and cover them with some nettles.", "그는 바센카를 보지 않으려 애쓰며 떨리는 목소리로 필리프에게 말했다. \"쐐기풀로 덮어 두고."], ["And you might at least ask for some milk for me.”", "그리고 적어도 우유라도 좀 청해 다 주게.\""]], [["But when he had drunk some milk, he felt ashamed immediately at having shown his annoyance to a stranger, and he began to laugh at his hungry mortification.", "그러나 우유를 좀 마시고 나자, 그는 낯선 사람 앞에서 짜증을 드러낸 것이 곧바로 부끄러워졌고, 배고픈 분풀이를 했던 자신을 두고 웃기 시작했다."]], [["In the evening they went shooting again, and Veslovsky had several successful shots, and in the night they drove home.", "저녁이 되자 그들은 다시 사냥을 나갔고, 베슬롭스키는 몇 차례 멋지게 맞혔으며, 밤에는 마차를 몰아 집으로 돌아왔다."]], [["Their homeward journey was as lively as their drive out had been.", "집으로 돌아오는 길은 떠나올 때만큼이나 활기찼다."], ["Veslovsky sang songs and related with enjoyment his adventures with the peasants, who had regaled him with vodka, and said to him, “Excuse our homely ways,”", "베슬롭스키는 노래를 부르고, 농부들과 겪은 일을 신나게 늘어놓았다."], ["and his night’s adventures with kiss-in-the-ring and the servant-girl and the peasant, who had asked him was he married, and on learning that he was not, said to him, “Well, mind you don’t run after other men’s wives—you’d better get one of your own.”", "농부들은 그에게 보드카를 대접하며 \"촌스러운 살림이라 죄송합니다요\"라고 했다는 둥, 밤에는 술래잡기 놀이며 하녀와 농부와 얽힌 일이며, 한 농부가 그에게 결혼했느냐고 물었다가 안 했다는 걸 알고는 \"그럼 남의 마누라 뒤꽁무니나 쫓아다니지 말고, 자네 마누라부터 하나 얻는 게 낫겠구먼\"이라고 했다는 둥 떠들어 댔다."], ["These words had particularly amused Veslovsky.", "이 말이 베슬롭스키에게는 유난히 우스웠던 모양이었다."]], [["“Altogether, I’ve enjoyed our outing awfully.", "\"통틀어 이번 나들이가 무척 즐거웠습니다."], ["And you, Levin?”", "당신은요, 레빈?\""]], [["“I have, very much,” Levin said quite sincerely.", "\"나도, 아주 즐거웠습니다.\""], ["It was particularly delightful to him to have got rid of the hostility he had been feeling towards Vassenka Veslovsky at home, and to feel instead the most friendly disposition to him.", "레빈은 진심으로 말했다. 집에서 바센카 베슬롭스키에게 품었던 적의를 떨쳐 내고, 그 대신 더없이 다정한 마음을 느끼게 된 것이 그에게는 특히나 기뻤다."]], [["# Chapter 14", "# Chapter 14"]], [["Next day at ten o’clock Levin, who had already gone his rounds, knocked at the room where Vassenka had been put for the night.", "이튿날 열 시, 이미 농장을 한 바퀴 둘러보고 온 레빈은 바센카가 하룻밤 묵게 된 방의 문을 두드렸다."]], [["“Entrez!”", "\"Entrez!\""], ["Veslovsky called to him.", "베슬롭스키가 그를 불렀다."], ["“Excuse me, I’ve only just finished my ablutions,”", "\"미안합니다, 막 세수를 끝냈거든요.\""], ["he said, smiling, standing before him in his underclothes only.", "그는 속옷 차림으로 레빈 앞에 선 채 웃으며 말했다. * Entrez: 프랑스어로 '들어오세요'."]], [["“Don’t mind me, please.”", "\"저는 신경 쓰지 마십시오.\""], ["Levin sat down in the window.", "레빈은 창가에 앉았다."], ["“Have you slept well?”", "\"잘 주무셨습니까?\""]], [["“Like the dead.", "\"죽은 듯이 잤습니다."], ["What sort of day is it for shooting?”", "사냥하기에는 어떤 날씨인가요?\""]], [["“What will you take, tea or coffee?”", "\"뭘 드시겠습니까, 차, 아니면 커피?\""]], [["“Neither.", "\"둘 다 됐습니다."], ["I’ll wait till lunch.", "점심까지 기다리지요."], ["I’m really ashamed.", "정말 부끄럽군요."], ["I suppose the ladies are down?", "부인들은 벌써 내려오셨겠죠?"], ["A walk now would be capital.", "지금 산책하면 딱 좋겠는데요."], ["You show me your horses.”", "말을 좀 보여 주십시오.\""]], [["After walking about the garden, visiting the stable, and even doing some gymnastic exercises together on the parallel bars, Levin returned to the house with his guest, and went with him into the drawing-room.", "정원을 거닐고, 마구간에 들르고, 심지어 평행봉에서 함께 체조까지 좀 한 뒤에, 레빈은 손님과 함께 집으로 돌아와 그를 데리고 응접실로 들어갔다."]], [["“We had splendid shooting, and so many delightful experiences!”", "\"사냥이 정말 근사했어요, 즐거운 일이 어찌나 많던지!\""], ["said Veslovsky, going up to Kitty, who was sitting at the samovar.", "베슬롭스키가 사모바르 앞에 앉아 있는 키티에게 다가가며 말했다."], ["“What a pity ladies are cut off from these delights!”", "\"부인들이 이런 즐거움을 누리지 못한다니 안타까운 일입니다!\""]], [["“Well, I suppose he must say something to the lady of the house,”", "'뭐, 안주인에게 무슨 말이든 해야겠지.'"], ["Levin said to himself.", "레빈은 속으로 생각했다."], ["Again he fancied something in the smile, in the all-conquering air with which their guest addressed Kitty....", "그런데 또 그 미소에서, 손님이 키티에게 말을 거는 그 거침없는 태도에서 뭔가가 느껴지는 것만 같았다……."]], [["The princess, sitting on the other side of the table with Marya Vlasyevna and Stepan Arkadyevitch, called Levin to her side, and began to talk to him about moving to Moscow for Kitty’s confinement, and getting ready rooms for them.", "공작 부인은 식탁 반대편에 마리야 블라시예브나, 스테판 아르카지치와 함께 앉아 있다가 레빈을 자기 곁으로 불러, 키티의 해산을 위해 모스크바로 옮기는 일이며 그들을 위해 방을 준비하는 일에 관해 이야기하기 시작했다."], ["Just as Levin had disliked all the trivial preparations for his wedding, as derogatory to the grandeur of the event, now he felt still more offensive the preparations for the approaching birth, the date of which they reckoned, it seemed, on their fingers.", "레빈이 결혼식 준비의 온갖 자질구레한 일들을 그 행사의 장엄함을 깎아내리는 것으로 여겨 싫어했던 것처럼, 이제 그는 다가오는 출산을 위한 준비가 한층 더 거슬렸다. 그들은 출산 날짜를 손가락으로 꼽아 가며 헤아리는 것만 같았다."], ["He tried to turn a deaf ear to these discussions of the best patterns of long clothes for the coming baby; tried to turn away and avoid seeing the mysterious, endless strips of knitting, the triangles of linen, and so on, to which Dolly attached special importance.", "그는 곧 태어날 아기를 위한 배냇저고리의 본을 어떤 게 가장 좋은지 따지는 이런 이야기들에 귀를 닫으려고 애썼고, 돌리가 각별히 중요하게 여기는 그 신비로운, 끝도 없는 뜨개질 천이며 삼각형으로 접은 아마포 따위를 보지 않으려고 고개를 돌렸다."], ["The birth of a son (he was certain it would be a son) which was promised him, but which he still could not believe in—so marvelous it seemed—presented itself to his mind, on one hand, as a happiness so immense, and therefore so incredible; on the other, as an event so mysterious, that this assumption of a definite knowledge of what would be, and consequent preparation for it, as for something ordinary that did happen to people, jarred on him as confusing and humiliating.", "그에게 약속된, 그러나 너무도 경이로워 아직도 믿어지지 않는 아들의 탄생은(그는 틀림없이 아들이리라고 확신했다) 한편으로는 너무도 엄청나서 도무지 믿을 수 없는 행복으로, 다른 한편으로는 너무도 신비로운 사건으로 그의 마음속에 떠올랐기에, 무슨 일이 일어날지를 확실히 안다는 듯이 단정하고 사람들에게 으레 일어나는 평범한 일이라도 되는 양 그에 대비하는 이 태도가 그에게는 혼란스럽고 모욕적으로 느껴져 거슬렸다."]], [["But the princess did not understand his feelings, and put down his reluctance to think and talk about it to carelessness and indifference, and so she gave him no peace.", "그러나 공작 부인은 그의 심정을 이해하지 못했고, 그가 그 일에 관해 생각하고 말하기를 꺼리는 것을 무심함과 무관심 탓으로 돌리고는 그를 가만히 내버려 두지 않았다."], ["She had commissioned Stepan Arkadyevitch to look at a flat, and now she called Levin up.", "부인은 스테판 아르카지치에게 셋집을 알아보라고 일러 둔 터라, 이제 레빈을 불렀다."]], [["“I know nothing about it, princess.", "\"저는 그런 건 아무것도 모릅니다, 어머님."], ["Do as you think fit,”", "알아서 좋으실 대로 하시지요.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“You must decide when you will move.”", "\"언제 옮길지는 자네가 정해야지.\""]], [["“I really don’t know.", "\"정말 모르겠습니다."], ["I know millions of children are born away from Moscow, and doctors ...", "모스크바를 떠난 곳에서도 수백만의 아이가 태어나고, 의사들도…… 왜…….\""], ["why....”", "\"하지만 그렇다 해도…….\""]], [["“But if so....” “Oh, no, as Kitty wishes.”", "\"아, 아닙니다, 키티가 원하는 대로 하지요.\""]], [["“We can’t talk to Kitty about it!", "\"이 일을 어떻게 키티와 의논하겠나!"], ["Do you want me to frighten her?", "그 애를 겁주라는 건가?"], ["Why, this spring Natalia Golitzina died from having an ignorant doctor.”", "글쎄, 이번 봄에 나탈리야 골리치나가 무지한 의사를 만나는 바람에 죽었다네.\""]], [["“I will do just what you say,”", "\"말씀하시는 대로 하겠습니다.\""], ["he said gloomily.", "그가 침울하게 말했다."]], [["The princess began talking to him, but he did not hear her.", "공작 부인이 그에게 말을 이어 갔지만, 그는 듣고 있지 않았다."], ["Though the conversation with the princess had indeed jarred upon him, he was gloomy, not on account of that conversation, but from what he saw at the samovar.", "공작 부인과의 대화가 그를 거슬리게 한 것은 사실이었으나, 그가 침울한 것은 그 대화 때문이 아니라 사모바르 앞에서 본 광경 때문이었다."]], [["“No, it’s impossible,”", "'아냐, 있을 수 없는 일이야.'"], ["he thought, glancing now and then at Vassenka bending over Kitty, telling her something with his charming smile, and at her, flushed and disturbed.", "그는 키티에게 몸을 숙인 채 매력적인 미소를 띠고 무언가 이야기하는 바센카를, 그리고 얼굴을 붉히며 안절부절못하는 키티를 이따금 흘끔거리며 생각했다."]], [["There was something not nice in Vassenka’s attitude, in his eyes, in his smile.", "바센카의 자세에도, 그 눈빛에도, 그 미소에도 뭔가 마뜩잖은 데가 있었다."], ["Levin even saw something not nice in Kitty’s attitude and look.", "레빈은 심지어 키티의 자세와 눈빛에서도 뭔가 마뜩잖은 것을 보았다."], ["And again the light died away in his eyes.", "그러자 다시 그의 눈에서 빛이 꺼졌다."], ["Again, as before, all of a sudden, without the slightest transition, he felt cast down from a pinnacle of happiness, peace, and dignity, into an abyss of despair, rage, and humiliation.", "또다시, 전처럼, 별안간, 어떠한 단계도 거치지 않고, 그는 행복과 평화와 존엄의 절정에서 절망과 분노와 모멸의 나락으로 곤두박질치는 기분이었다."], ["Again everything and everyone had become hateful to him.", "다시 모든 것과 모든 사람이 그에게 가증스럽게 느껴졌다."]], [["“You do just as you think best, princess,”", "\"좋으실 대로 하십시오, 어머님.\""], ["he said again, looking round.", "그는 다시 한번 주위를 둘러보며 말했다."]], [["“Heavy is the cap of Monomach,” Stepan Arkadyevitch said playfully, hinting, evidently, not simply at the princess’s conversation, but at the cause of Levin’s agitation, which he had noticed.", "\"모노마흐의 관은 무거운 법이지.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 장난스럽게 말했다. 분명 공작 부인과의 대화만이 아니라, 자기가 눈치챈 레빈의 동요의 원인까지 넌지시 빗댄 말이었다. * 모노마흐의 관: 러시아 차르의 대관식에 쓰이던 왕관. 푸시킨의 희곡 「보리스 고두노프」의 대사로, 권력에 따르는 무거운 책임을 뜻한다."]], [["“How late you are today, Dolly!”", "\"오늘은 어쩜 이렇게 늦었니, 돌리!\""]], [["Everyone got up to greet Darya Alexandrovna.", "모두 다리야 알렉산드로브나를 맞으려고 일어섰다."], ["Vassenka only rose for an instant, and with the lack of courtesy to ladies characteristic of the modern young man, he scarcely bowed, and resumed his conversation again, laughing at something.", "바센카만은 잠깐 몸을 일으켰을 뿐, 요즘 젊은이 특유의 부인들에 대한 무례함으로 거의 고개를 숙이지도 않은 채 무언가에 웃으며 다시 이야기를 이어 갔다."]], [["“I’ve been worried about Masha.", "\"마샤 때문에 마음을 졸였어."], ["She did not sleep well, and is dreadfully tiresome today,”", "잠을 푹 자지 못해서 오늘 영 보채는구나.\""], ["said Dolly.", "돌리가 말했다."]], [["The conversation Vassenka had started with Kitty was running on the same lines as on the previous evening, discussing Anna, and whether love is to be put higher than worldly considerations.", "바센카가 키티와 시작한 대화는 전날 저녁과 똑같은 흐름으로, 안나에 대해, 그리고 사랑을 세속적인 고려보다 위에 두어야 하는가에 대해 떠들고 있었다."], ["Kitty disliked the conversation, and she was disturbed both by the subject and the tone in which it was conducted, and also by the knowledge of the effect it would have on her husband.", "키티는 그 대화가 싫었고, 화제 자체와 그것이 다루어지는 어조 때문에, 또 그것이 남편에게 미칠 영향을 알기에 마음이 불편했다."], ["But she was too simple and innocent to know how to cut short this conversation, or even to conceal the superficial pleasure afforded her by the young man’s very obvious admiration.", "그러나 그녀는 너무 순박하고 천진해서 이 대화를 어떻게 끊어야 할지 몰랐고, 그 젊은이의 너무도 노골적인 찬사가 그녀에게 안겨 주는 피상적인 즐거움을 감출 줄조차 몰랐다."], ["She wanted to stop it, but she did not know what to do.", "그녀는 그것을 멈추고 싶었지만, 어찌해야 할지 알 수 없었다."], ["Whatever she did she knew would be observed by her husband, and the worst interpretation put on it.", "자기가 무엇을 하든 남편이 지켜볼 것이고, 거기에 가장 나쁜 해석을 붙이리라는 것을 그녀는 알았다."], ["And, in fact, when she asked Dolly what was wrong with Masha, and Vassenka, waiting till this uninteresting conversation was over, began to gaze indifferently at Dolly, the question struck Levin as an unnatural and disgusting piece of hypocrisy.", "그리고 실제로, 그녀가 마샤에게 무슨 일이 있느냐고 돌리에게 물었을 때, 바센카가 이 시시한 대화가 끝나기를 기다렸다가 무심히 돌리를 바라보기 시작했을 때, 그 질문은 레빈에게 부자연스럽고 역겨운 위선으로 비쳤다."]], [["“What do you say, shall we go and look for mushrooms today?”", "\"어때, 오늘 버섯 따러 갈까?\""], ["said Dolly.", "돌리가 말했다."]], [["“By all means, please, and I shall come too,”", "\"좋아요, 꼭 가요, 저도 갈게요.\""], ["said Kitty, and she blushed.", "키티는 이렇게 말하고는 얼굴을 붉혔다."], ["She wanted from politeness to ask Vassenka whether he would come, and she did not ask him.", "그녀는 예의상 바센카에게 같이 가겠느냐고 묻고 싶었지만 묻지 않았다."], ["“Where are you going, Kostya?”", "\"어디 가요, 코스챠?\""], ["she asked her husband with a guilty face, as he passed by her with a resolute step.", "단호한 걸음으로 자기 곁을 지나치는 남편에게 그녀가 죄지은 듯한 얼굴로 물었다."], ["This guilty air confirmed all his suspicions.", "이 죄스러운 기색이 그의 온갖 의심을 굳혀 주었다."]], [["“The mechanician came when I was away; I haven’t seen him yet,”", "\"내가 없는 동안 기계공이 왔어. 아직 못 만나 봤거든.\""], ["he said, not looking at her.", "그는 그녀를 보지 않은 채 말했다."]], [["He went downstairs, but before he had time to leave his study he heard his wife’s familiar footsteps running with reckless speed to him.", "그는 아래층으로 내려갔는데, 미처 서재를 떠나기도 전에 아내가 무모한 속도로 그에게 달려오는 낯익은 발소리를 들었다."]], [["“What do you want?”", "\"무슨 일이야?\""], ["he said to her shortly.", "그가 그녀에게 짧게 말했다."], ["“We are busy.”", "\"우리 바쁜데.\""]], [["“I beg your pardon,”", "\"실례합니다.\""], ["she said to the German mechanician; “I want a few words with my husband.”", "그녀가 독일인 기계공에게 말했다. \"남편과 잠깐 할 이야기가 있어서요.\""]], [["The German would have left the room, but Levin said to him:", "독일인은 방에서 나가려 했으나, 레빈이 그에게 말했다."]], [["“Don’t disturb yourself.”", "\"신경 쓰지 마십시오.\""]], [["“The train is at three?”", "\"기차가 세 시지요?\""], ["queried the German.", "독일인이 물었다."], ["“I mustn’t be late.”", "\"늦으면 안 되니까요.\""]], [["Levin did not answer him, but walked out himself with his wife.", "레빈은 그에게 대답하지 않고, 직접 아내와 함께 밖으로 나갔다."]], [["“Well, what have you to say to me?”", "\"자, 무슨 말을 하고 싶은 거야?\""], ["he said to her in French.", "그가 프랑스어로 그녀에게 말했다."]], [["He did not look her in the face, and did not care to see that she in her condition was trembling all over, and had a piteous, crushed look.", "그는 그녀의 얼굴을 보지 않았고, 그런 몸으로 그녀가 온몸을 떨며 가엾고 짓눌린 표정을 짓고 있다는 것을 알려 하지도 않았다."]], [["“I ...", "\"전……"], ["I want to say that we can’t go on like this; that this is misery....”", "전 이렇게는 더 못 살겠다는 걸 말하고 싶어요. 이건 너무 비참해요…….\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“The servants are here at the sideboard,”", "\"하인들이 여기 찬장 옆에 있잖아.\""], ["he said angrily; “don’t make a scene.”", "그가 화를 내며 말했다. \"소란 피우지 마.\""]], [["“Well, let’s go in here!”", "\"그럼, 이리로 들어가요!\""]], [["They were standing in the passage.", "그들은 복도에 서 있었다."], ["Kitty would have gone into the next room, but there the English governess was giving Tanya a lesson.", "키티는 옆방으로 들어가려 했으나, 거기서는 영국인 가정교사가 타냐에게 수업을 하고 있었다."]], [["“Well, come into the garden.”", "\"그럼, 정원으로 나가.\""]], [["In the garden they came upon a peasant weeding the path.", "정원에서 그들은 길의 풀을 뽑고 있는 농부와 마주쳤다."], ["And no longer considering that the peasant could see her tear-stained and his agitated face, that they looked like people fleeing from some disaster, they went on with rapid steps, feeling that they must speak out and clear up misunderstandings, must be alone together, and so get rid of the misery they were both feeling.", "그러나 이제 그들은 그 농부가 눈물범벅이 된 그녀의 얼굴과 격앙된 자기 얼굴을 볼 수 있다는 것도, 자기들이 무슨 재앙에서 도망치는 사람들처럼 보인다는 것도 개의치 않은 채, 속내를 털어놓고 오해를 풀어야 한다고, 단둘이 있어야 한다고, 그래서 둘 다 느끼고 있는 이 비참함에서 벗어나야 한다고 느끼며 빠른 걸음으로 걸어갔다."]], [["“We can’t go on like this!", "\"우린 이렇게는 더 못 살아요!"], ["It’s misery!", "이건 비참해요!"], ["I am wretched; you are wretched.", "난 비참하고, 당신도 비참해요."], ["What for?”", "무엇 때문에요?\""], ["she said, when they had at last reached a solitary garden seat at a turn in the lime tree avenue.", "보리수 가로수 길의 굽이진 곳에 있는 외딴 벤치에 마침내 다다랐을 때 그녀가 말했다."]], [["“But tell me one thing: was there in his tone anything unseemly, not nice, humiliatingly horrible?”", "\"하지만 한 가지만 말해 줘. 그자의 말투에 뭔가 점잖지 못한, 마뜩잖은, 모멸스러우리만치 끔찍한 게 있었어?\""], ["he said, standing before her again in the same position with his clenched fists on his chest, as he had stood before her that night.", "그가 그날 밤 그녀 앞에 섰던 것과 똑같은 자세로, 주먹을 쥐어 가슴에 댄 채 다시 그녀 앞에 서서 말했다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["she said in a shaking voice; “but, Kostya, surely you see I’m not to blame?", "그녀가 떨리는 목소리로 말했다. \"하지만, 코스챠, 내 잘못이 아니라는 거 알잖아요?"], ["All the morning I’ve been trying to take a tone ...", "아침 내내 어떤 태도를 취하려고 애썼는데……"], ["but such people....", "그런데 그런 사람들은……."], ["Why did he come?", "그 사람은 왜 온 걸까요?"], ["How happy we were!”", "우린 정말 행복했는데!\""], ["she said, breathless with the sobs that shook her.", "그녀는 자신을 뒤흔드는 흐느낌에 숨을 헐떡이며 말했다."]], [["Although nothing had been pursuing them, and there was nothing to run away from, and they could not possibly have found anything very delightful on that garden seat, the gardener saw with astonishment that they passed him on their way home with comforted and radiant faces.", "비록 아무것도 그들을 쫓아오지 않았고, 도망칠 것도 없었으며, 그 벤치에서 그리 즐거운 것이라곤 도무지 찾을 수 없었을 텐데도, 정원사는 그들이 집으로 돌아가는 길에 위안을 얻은 환한 얼굴로 자기 곁을 지나가는 것을 놀라움에 차서 바라보았다."]], [["# Chapter 15", "# Chapter 15"]], [["After escorting his wife upstairs, Levin went to Dolly’s part of the house.", "아내를 이층까지 바래다준 뒤, 레빈은 돌리가 머무는 쪽으로 갔다."], ["Darya Alexandrovna, for her part, was in great distress too that day.", "한편 다리야 알렉산드로브나도 그날 몹시 속이 상해 있었다."], ["She was walking about the room, talking angrily to a little girl, who stood in the corner roaring.", "그녀는 방 안을 이리저리 거닐면서, 구석에 서서 악을 쓰며 우는 어린 계집아이에게 화가 난 목소리로 말하고 있었다."]], [["“And you shall stand all day in the corner, and have your dinner all alone, and not see one of your dolls, and I won’t make you a new frock,”", "\"너는 하루 종일 구석에 서 있고, 밥도 혼자 먹어야 하고, 인형도 하나 못 보고, 새 옷도 안 만들어 줄 거야.\""], ["she said, not knowing how to punish her.", "그녀는 아이를 어떻게 벌해야 할지 몰라 그렇게 말했다."]], [["“Oh, she is a disgusting child!”", "\"아유, 정말 못된 아이라니까!\""], ["she turned to Levin.", "그녀는 레빈을 돌아보며 말했다."], ["“Where does she get such wicked propensities?”", "\"대체 어디서 이런 고약한 버릇을 배워 오는지 모르겠어요.\""]], [["“Why, what has she done?”", "\"왜요, 무슨 짓을 했길래요?\""], ["Levin said without much interest, for he had wanted to ask her advice, and so was annoyed that he had come at an unlucky moment.", "레빈은 그다지 관심 없이 말했다. 그는 그녀에게 의논할 일이 있어 온 터라, 마침 좋지 않은 때에 와 버린 게 짜증스러웠다."]], [["“Grisha and she went into the raspberries, and there ...", "\"그리샤랑 같이 산딸기 밭에 들어가서, 거기서……"], ["I can’t tell you really what she did.", "무슨 짓을 했는지 정말 입에 담을 수도 없어요."], ["It’s a thousand pities Miss Elliot’s not with us.", "엘리엇 양이 우리랑 같이 있지 않은 게 천만 번 아쉽네요."], ["This one sees to nothing—she’s a machine....", "이 사람은 도통 아무것도 챙기질 않아요—그냥 기계 같다니까요."], ["Figurez-vous que la petite?...”", "Figurez-vous que la petite…?\""]], [["And Darya Alexandrovna described Masha’s crime.", "그리고 다리야 알렉산드로브나는 마샤가 저지른 죄를 묘사했다."]], [["“That proves nothing; it’s not a question of evil propensities at all, it’s simply mischief,” Levin assured her.", "* Figurez-vous que la petite: 프랑스어로 \"글쎄, 그 어린 게 말이에요\"라는 뜻."]], [["“But you are upset about something?", "\"그건 아무것도 증명하지 못해요."], ["What have you come for?” asked Dolly.", "못된 버릇 같은 문제가 결코 아니라, 그냥 장난일 뿐이죠.\""], ["“What’s going on there?”", "레빈이 그녀를 안심시켰다."]], [["And in the tone of her question Levin heard that it would be easy for him to say what he had meant to say.", "\"하지만 당신, 뭔가 속상한 일이 있나 봐요? 무슨 일로 왔어요?\" 돌리가 물었다. \"거기서는 무슨 일이 있는 거예요?\""]], [["“I’ve not been in there, I’ve been alone in the garden with Kitty.", "그리고 그 물음의 어조에서 레빈은 자기가 하려던 말을 꺼내기가 쉬우리라는 것을 느꼈다. \"거기엔 안 들어갔어요."], ["We’ve had a quarrel for the second time since ...", "키티랑 둘이서 정원에 있었죠. 우리는……"], ["Stiva came.”", "스티바가 온 뒤로 벌써 두 번째 다퉜어요.\""]], [["Dolly looked at him with her shrewd, comprehending eyes.", "돌리는 영민하고 모든 걸 꿰뚫어 보는 눈으로 그를 바라보았다."]], [["“Come, tell me, honor bright, has there been ...", "\"자, 솔직하게 말해 봐요. 양심에 손을 얹고요."], ["not in Kitty, but in that gentleman’s behavior, a tone which might be unpleasant—not unpleasant, but horrible, offensive to a husband?”", "그러니까…… 키티가 아니라, 그 신사의 행동에 뭔가 불쾌할 만한—아니, 불쾌한 정도가 아니라 끔찍하고, 남편에게 모욕적일 만한 그런 기색이 있었나요?\""]], [["“You mean, how shall I say....", "\"무슨 말이냐면, 그러니까 뭐라고 해야 할까……"], ["Stay, stay in the corner!”", "구석에 가만히, 가만히 서 있어!\""], ["she said to Masha, who, detecting a faint smile in her mother’s face, had been turning round.", "그녀는 엄마 얼굴에서 희미한 미소를 알아채고 몸을 돌리려던 마샤에게 말했다. \"세상 사람들이라면 그가 젊은 남자들이 흔히 하는 대로 행동할 뿐이라고 하겠죠."], ["“The opinion of the world would be that he is behaving as young men do behave.", "Il fait la cour à une jeune et jolie femme. 그러니 세상 물정을 아는 남편이라면 오히려 우쭐해해야 할 일이라고요.\""], ["Il fait la cour à une jeune et jolie femme, and a husband who’s a man of the world should only be flattered by it.”", "* Il fait la cour à une jeune et jolie femme: 프랑스어로 \"그는 젊고 예쁜 여인에게 구애하고 있다\"는 뜻."]], [["“Yes, yes,”", "\"그래요, 그래요.\""], ["said Levin gloomily; “but you noticed it?”", "레빈이 침울하게 말했다. \"하지만 당신도 그걸 눈치챘다는 거죠?\""]], [["“Not only I, but Stiva noticed it.", "\"나뿐만 아니라 스티바도 눈치챘어요. 아침 식사가 끝나자마자 나한테 대놓고 그러더라고요."], ["Just after breakfast he said to me in so many words, Je crois que Veslovsky fait un petit brin de cour à Kitty.”", "Je crois que Veslovsky fait un petit brin de cour à Kitty.\" * Je crois que Veslovsky fait un petit brin de cour à Kitty: 프랑스어로 \"베슬롭스키가 키티에게 약간 수작을 부리고 있는 것 같아\"라는 뜻."]], [["“Well, that’s all right then; now I’m satisfied.", "\"그럼, 됐어요. 이제 마음이 놓이네요."], ["I’ll send him away,”", "그를 내보내겠어요.\""], ["said Levin.", "레빈이 말했다."]], [["“What do you mean!", "\"무슨 소리예요!"], ["Are you crazy?”", "당신 미쳤어요?\""], ["Dolly cried in horror; “nonsense, Kostya, only think!”", "돌리가 질겁하며 외쳤다. \"말도 안 돼요, 코스챠, 생각 좀 해 봐요!\""], ["she said, laughing.", "그녀가 웃으며 말했다."], ["“You can go now to Fanny,”", "\"이제 패니한테 가도 돼.\""], ["she said to Masha.", "그녀가 마샤에게 말했다."], ["“No, if you wish it, I’ll speak to Stiva.", "\"아니, 정 그렇다면 내가 스티바한테 말할게요."], ["He’ll take him away.", "그가 데려가게 하면 되잖아요."], ["He can say you’re expecting visitors.", "손님이 오기로 했다고 둘러대면 되고요."], ["Altogether he doesn’t fit into the house.”", "어쨌든 그 사람은 이 집과 어울리지 않으니까요.\""]], [["“No, no, I’ll do it myself.”", "\"아니요, 아니에요. 내가 직접 하겠어요.\""]], [["“But you’ll quarrel with him?”", "\"하지만 그와 다투게 될 텐데요?\""]], [["“Not a bit.", "\"전혀요."], ["I shall so enjoy it,”", "오히려 즐거울 것 같은데요.\""], ["Levin said, his eyes flashing with real enjoyment.", "레빈이 말했다. 그의 눈이 진짜 즐거움으로 반짝였다."], ["“Come, forgive her, Dolly, she won’t do it again,”", "\"자, 이 아이를 용서해 줘요, 돌리. 다시는 안 그럴 거예요.\""], ["he said of the little sinner, who had not gone to Fanny, but was standing irresolutely before her mother, waiting and looking up from under her brows to catch her mother’s eye.", "그는 그 어린 죄인을 두고 말했다. 아이는 패니에게 가지 않고, 머뭇거리며 엄마 앞에 선 채, 엄마와 눈을 맞추려고 눈썹 아래로 올려다보며 기다리고 있었다."]], [["The mother glanced at her.", "엄마가 아이를 흘끗 보았다."], ["The child broke into sobs, hid her face on her mother’s lap, and Dolly laid her thin, tender hand on her head.", "아이는 흐느껴 울며 엄마 무릎에 얼굴을 파묻었고, 돌리는 가늘고 부드러운 손을 아이의 머리에 얹었다."]], [["“And what is there in common between us and him?”", "\"대체 우리와 저 사람 사이에 무슨 공통점이 있단 말인가?\""], ["thought Levin, and he went off to look for Veslovsky.", "레빈은 생각하며, 베슬롭스키를 찾으러 나갔다."]], [["As he passed through the passage he gave orders for the carriage to be got ready to drive to the station.", "복도를 지나면서 그는 마차를 정거장으로 갈 채비를 해 두라고 일렀다."]], [["“The spring was broken yesterday,”", "\"어제 용수철이 부러졌습니다.\""], ["said the footman.", "하인이 말했다."]], [["“Well, the covered trap, then, and make haste.", "\"그럼 포장마차로 하지. 어서 서둘러."], ["Where’s the visitor?”", "손님은 어디 계신가?\""]], [["“The gentleman’s gone to his room.”", "\"손님께서는 방으로 가셨습니다.\""]], [["Levin came upon Veslovsky at the moment when the latter, having unpacked his things from his trunk, and laid out some new songs, was putting on his gaiters to go out riding.", "레빈이 베슬롭스키를 마주친 것은, 그가 트렁크에서 짐을 다 꺼내고 새로 가져온 악보 몇 장을 펼쳐 놓은 다음, 말을 타러 나가려고 각반을 두르고 있던 바로 그 순간이었다."]], [["Whether there was something exceptional in Levin’s face, or that Vassenka was himself conscious that ce petit brin de cour he was making was out of place in this family, but he was somewhat (as much as a young man in society can be) disconcerted at Levin’s entrance.", "레빈의 얼굴에 뭔가 심상치 않은 것이 있어서였는지, 아니면 바센카 스스로 자기가 부리던 ce petit brin de cour가 이 가족에게는 어울리지 않는다는 것을 의식해서였는지, 그는 레빈이 들어서자 (사교계의 젊은이로서 그럴 수 있는 정도껏) 다소 당황한 기색을 보였다. * ce petit brin de cour: 프랑스어로 \"그 약간의 수작\"이라는 뜻."]], [["“You ride in gaiters?”", "\"각반을 차고 말을 타시오?\""]], [["“Yes, it’s much cleaner,”", "\"네, 이게 훨씬 깔끔하거든요.\""], ["said Vassenka, putting his fat leg on a chair, fastening the bottom hook, and smiling with simple-hearted good humor.", "바센카는 살집 있는 다리를 의자에 올리고, 아래쪽 고리를 채우면서 천진하고 사람 좋은 웃음을 지으며 말했다."]], [["He was undoubtedly a good-natured fellow, and Levin felt sorry for him and ashamed of himself, as his host, when he saw the shy look on Vassenka’s face.", "그는 분명 마음씨 좋은 친구였고, 바센카의 얼굴에 떠오른 수줍은 표정을 보자 레빈은 그가 가엾기도 하고, 주인 된 처지에 자기 자신이 부끄럽기도 했다."]], [["On the table lay a piece of stick which they had broken together that morning, trying their strength.", "탁자 위에는 그날 아침 둘이서 힘을 겨루어 보겠다고 함께 부러뜨린 막대기 토막이 놓여 있었다."], ["Levin took the fragment in his hands and began smashing it up, breaking bits off the stick, not knowing how to begin.", "레빈은 그 조각을 손에 쥐고, 어떻게 말을 꺼내야 할지 몰라, 막대기에서 작은 부스러기를 떼어 내며 으스러뜨리기 시작했다."]], [["“I wanted....” He paused, but suddenly, remembering Kitty and everything that had happened, he said, looking him resolutely in the face: “I have ordered the horses to be put-to for you.”", "\"내가…… 말하고 싶었던 건……\" 그는 말을 멈추었다. 그러나 문득 키티며 그동안 일어난 모든 일이 떠올라, 그의 얼굴을 단호하게 똑바로 바라보며 말했다. \"당신을 위해 말을 매어 두라고 일러 놓았소.\""]], [["“How so?”", "\"어째서요?\""], ["Vassenka began in surprise.", "바센카가 놀라서 말을 꺼냈다."], ["“To drive where?”", "\"어디로 가라는 말입니까?\""]], [["“For you to drive to the station,”", "\"당신이 정거장으로 가도록 말이오.\""], ["Levin said gloomily.", "레빈이 침울하게 말했다."]], [["“Are you going away, or has something happened?”", "\"당신이 어디 가는 겁니까, 아니면 무슨 일이라도 생겼나요?\""]], [["“It happens that I expect visitors,”", "\"마침 손님이 올 일이 생겼소.\""], ["said Levin, his strong fingers more and more rapidly breaking off the ends of the split stick.", "레빈이 말했다. 그의 억센 손가락은 쪼개진 막대기의 끝을 점점 더 빠르게 부러뜨려 떼어 냈다."], ["“And I’m not expecting visitors, and nothing has happened, but I beg you to go away.", "\"아니, 손님이 올 일도 없고, 아무 일도 일어나지 않았소만, 그저 떠나 주기를 부탁하는 거요."], ["You can explain my rudeness as you like.”", "내 무례함이야 마음대로 해석하면 되오.\""]], [["Vassenka drew himself up.", "바센카는 몸을 곧추세웠다."]], [["“I beg you to explain....”", "\"이유를 설명해 주시기를 부탁드립니다……\""], ["he said with dignity, understanding at last.", "그는 마침내 사정을 알아채고는 위엄을 갖추어 말했다."]], [["“I can’t explain,”", "\"설명할 수 없소.\""], ["Levin said softly and deliberately, trying to control the trembling of his jaw; “and you’d better not ask.”", "레빈은 떨리는 턱을 억누르려 애쓰며 나직하고 또박또박 말했다. \"그리고 묻지 않는 편이 좋을 거요.\""]], [["And as the split ends were all broken off, Levin clutched the thick ends in his finger, broke the stick in two, and carefully caught the end as it fell.", "쪼개진 끝이 다 떨어져 나가자, 레빈은 굵은 쪽 끝을 손가락으로 움켜쥐고 막대기를 둘로 부러뜨린 다음, 떨어지는 끝을 조심스레 받아 쥐었다."]], [["Probably the sight of those nervous fingers, of the muscles he had proved that morning at gymnastics, of the glittering eyes, the soft voice, and quivering jaws, convinced Vassenka better than any words.", "그날 아침 체조에서 그 위력을 입증해 보인 근육이며, 그 신경질적인 손가락, 번득이는 눈, 나직한 목소리, 부들부들 떨리는 턱을 본 것이, 어떤 말보다도 바센카를 더 확실하게 납득시켰을 것이다."], ["He bowed, shrugging his shoulders, and smiling contemptuously.", "그는 어깨를 으쓱하고 경멸하듯 웃으며 고개를 숙였다."]], [["“Can I not see Oblonsky?”", "\"오블론스키를 만날 수는 있겠지요?\""]], [["The shrug and the smile did not irritate Levin.", "그 으쓱거림과 웃음은 레빈을 자극하지 못했다."]], [["“What else was there for him to do?”", "\"저 사람으로서야 달리 어쩌겠는가?\""], ["he thought.", "그는 생각했다."]], [["“I’ll send him to you at once.”", "\"곧 그를 당신에게 보내겠소.\""]], [["“What madness is this?”", "\"이게 대체 무슨 미친 짓인가?\""], ["Stepan Arkadyevitch said when, after hearing from his friend that he was being turned out of the house, he found Levin in the garden, where he was walking about waiting for his guest’s departure.", "스테판 아르카지치는, 친구로부터 집에서 쫓겨나는 신세가 되었다는 말을 들은 뒤, 손님이 떠나기를 기다리며 거닐고 있던 레빈을 정원에서 찾아내 말했다. \"Mais c'est ridicule!"], ["“Mais c’est ridicule!", "자네 무슨 파리한테라도 쏘인 건가?"], ["What fly has stung you?", "Mais c'est du dernier ridicule!"], ["Mais c’est du dernier ridicule!", "자네 생각엔, 만약 젊은 남자가……\" * Mais c'est ridicule!: 프랑스어로 \"정말 우스꽝스럽군!\"이라는 뜻."], ["What did you think, if a young man....”", "* Mais c'est du dernier ridicule!: 프랑스어로 \"이건 정말 더없이 우스꽝스러운 짓이야!\"라는 뜻."]], [["But the place where Levin had been stung was evidently still sore, for he turned pale again, when Stepan Arkadyevitch would have enlarged on the reason, and he himself cut him short.", "그러나 레빈이 쏘인 자리는 분명 아직도 쓰라렸던 모양이다. 스테판 아르카지치가 그 이유를 길게 늘어놓으려 하자 레빈은 또다시 얼굴이 창백해지더니, 스스로 그의 말을 끊어 버렸다."]], [["“Please don’t go into it!", "\"제발 그 얘긴 들추지 말게!"], ["I can’t help it.", "나도 어쩔 수가 없어."], ["I feel ashamed of how I’m treating you and him.", "자네와 그 사람을 이렇게 대하는 게 부끄럽긴 해."], ["But it won’t be, I imagine, a great grief to him to go, and his presence was distasteful to me and to my wife.”", "하지만 그가 떠나는 게 그에게 그리 큰 슬픔은 아닐 거라고 생각하네. 그리고 그가 여기 있는 게 나와 내 아내에겐 견디기 힘든 일이었어.\""]], [["“But it’s insulting to him!", "\"하지만 그건 그에게 모욕이야!"], ["Et puis c’est ridicule.”", "Et puis c'est ridicule.\""]], [["“And to me it’s both insulting and distressing!", "그리고 나로서는 모욕적이기도 하고 괴롭기도 해요!"], ["And I’m not at fault in any way, and there’s no need for me to suffer.”", "나는 어떤 잘못도 하지 않았는데, 내가 고통받을 이유가 없잖아요."]], [["“Well, this I didn’t expect of you!", "\"나에게는 모욕이자 괴로움이라고!"], ["On peut être jaloux, mais à ce point, c’est du dernier ridicule!”", "게다가 나는 아무 잘못도 없는데, 내가 고통받을 이유가 없잖나.\""]], [["Levin turned quickly, and walked away from him into the depths of the avenue, and he went on walking up and down alone.", "\"이런, 자네한테서 이런 모습을 보게 될 줄은 몰랐네! On peut être jaloux, mais à ce point, c'est du dernier ridicule!\""], ["Soon he heard the rumble of the trap, and saw from behind the trees how Vassenka, sitting in the hay (unluckily there was no seat in the trap) in his Scotch cap, was driven along the avenue, jolting up and down over the ruts.", "* On peut être jaloux, mais à ce point, c'est du dernier ridicule!: 프랑스어로 \"질투야 할 수 있다지만, 이 정도까지 가면 그건 더없이 우스꽝스러운 짓이야!\"라는 뜻."]], [["“What’s this?”", "“이건 또 뭐지?”"], ["Levin thought, when a footman ran out of the house and stopped the trap.", "하인이 집에서 달려나와 마차를 세우자 레빈은 생각했다."], ["It was the mechanician, whom Levin had totally forgotten.", "그는 레빈이 까맣게 잊고 있던 기계공이었다."], ["The mechanician, bowing low, said something to Veslovsky, then clambered into the trap, and they drove off together.", "기계공은 허리를 깊이 숙여 인사하고 베슬롭스키에게 뭔가 말을 하더니 마차에 기어올랐고, 둘은 함께 마차를 몰고 떠났다."]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch and the princess were much upset by Levin’s action.", "스테판 아르카지이치와 공작 부인은 레빈의 행동에 몹시 언짢아했다."], ["And he himself felt not only in the highest degree ridicule, but also utterly guilty and disgraced.", "레빈 자신도 더없이 우스꽝스러울 뿐만 아니라 완전히 잘못했고 수치스럽다고 느꼈다."], ["But remembering what sufferings he and his wife had been through, when he asked himself how he should act another time, he answered that he should do just the same again.", "하지만 자신과 아내가 어떤 고통을 겪었는지 떠올리며 다음번에는 어떻게 행동해야 할지 스스로에게 물었을 때, 그는 똑같이 다시 행동하리라고 답했다."]], [["In spite of all this, towards the end of that day, everyone except the princess, who could not pardon Levin’s action, became extraordinarily lively and good-humored, like children after a punishment or grown-up people after a dreary, ceremonious reception, so that by the evening Vassenka’s dismissal was spoken of, in the absence of the princess, as though it were some remote event. And Dolly, who had inherited her father’s gift of humorous storytelling, made Varenka helpless with laughter as she related for the third and fourth time, always with fresh humorous additions, how she had only just put on her new shoes for the benefit of the visitor, and on going into the drawing-room, heard suddenly the rumble of the trap. And who should be in the trap but Vassenka himself, with his Scotch cap, and his songs and his gaiters, and all, sitting in the hay.", "이 모든 일에도 불구하고, 그날이 저물어 갈 무렵에는, 레빈의 행동을 용서하지 못하는 공작 부인을 빼고 모두가 유난히 활기차고 기분이 좋아졌다. 마치 벌을 받고 난 아이들처럼, 혹은 지루하고 격식 차린 연회를 끝낸 어른들처럼 말이다. 그래서 저녁 무렵에는, 공작 부인이 없는 자리에서 바센카를 쫓아낸 일이 마치 까마득히 먼 옛일이라도 되는 양 이야깃거리가 되었다. 아버지에게서 우스갯소리를 풀어내는 재주를 물려받은 돌리는, 세 번째, 네 번째로 거듭 이야기를 들려주면서—매번 새로운 우스운 살을 덧붙여 가며—바레니카가 웃다 못해 정신을 못 차리게 만들었다. 손님을 위해 일부러 막 새 구두를 신고 응접실로 들어서는데, 난데없이 마차 굴러가는 소리가 들리더라는 것이었다. 그런데 그 마차에 타고 있는 게 다름 아닌 바센카 본인이지 뭔가. 스코틀랜드식 모자에, 그 악보에, 그 각반에, 죄다 챙겨서 건초더미 위에 떡하니 앉아 있더라는 것이다."]], [["“If only you’d ordered out the carriage!", "\"그저 마차를 내오라고 하시기만 했어도! 그런데 그게 아니었지 뭐예요!"], ["But no!", "그러다 '멈춰!'"], ["and then I hear: ‘Stop!’ Oh, I thought they’ve relented.", "하는 소리가 들리잖아요. 아, 마음이 누그러졌나 보다 했죠."], ["I look out, and behold a fat German being sat down by him and driving away....", "내다봤더니, 글쎄 웬 뚱뚱한 독일 사람을 그 옆에 앉히더니 그대로 가 버리지 않겠어요?……"], ["And my new shoes all for nothing!...”", "내 새 구두만 다 헛것이 되고 말았다니까요!……\""]], [["# Chapter 16", "# Chapter 16"]], [["Darya Alexandrovna carried out her intention and went to see Anna.", "다리야 알렉산드로브나는 마음먹은 대로 안나를 만나러 갔다."], ["She was sorry to annoy her sister and to do anything Levin disliked.", "동생을 언짢게 하고 레빈이 싫어하는 일을 하는 것이 그녀로서는 마음에 걸렸다."], ["She quite understood how right the Levins were in not wishing to have anything to do with Vronsky.", "레빈 부부가 브론스키와 어떤 식으로든 엮이고 싶어 하지 않는 것이 얼마나 옳은지 그녀는 충분히 이해하고 있었다."], ["But she felt she must go and see Anna, and show her that her feelings could not be changed, in spite of the change in her position.", "그러나 자기는 안나를 만나러 가서, 그녀의 처지가 달라졌어도 자기 마음만은 변할 수 없음을 그녀에게 보여 주어야 한다고 느꼈다."], ["That she might be independent of the Levins in this expedition, Darya Alexandrovna sent to the village to hire horses for the drive; but Levin learning of it went to her to protest.", "이번 행차에서 레빈 부부에게 신세를 지지 않으려고 다리야 알렉산드로브나는 마을로 사람을 보내 마차를 끌 말을 빌리려 했다. 그러나 이를 알게 된 레빈이 그녀에게 항의하러 왔다."]], [["“What makes you suppose that I dislike your going?", "\"형수님이 가시는 걸 제가 싫어한다니, 무슨 근거로 그렇게 생각하세요?"], ["But, even if I did dislike it, I should still more dislike your not taking my horses,”", "설령 제가 싫어한다 해도, 형수님이 제 말을 쓰지 않으시는 게 저로서는 더 싫습니다.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“You never told me that you were going for certain.", "\"확실히 가신다는 말씀도 안 하셨잖아요."], ["Hiring horses in the village is disagreeable to me, and, what’s of more importance, they’ll undertake the job and never get you there.", "마을에서 말을 빌리는 건 제 기분이 언짢기도 하거니와, 더 중요한 건, 그자들이 일은 맡아 놓고 형수님을 제대로 모셔다드리지 못한다는 겁니다."], ["I have horses.", "저한테 말이 있어요."], ["And if you don’t want to wound me, you’ll take mine.”", "그러니 저를 서운하게 만들고 싶지 않으시거든 제 말을 쓰세요.\""]], [["Darya Alexandrovna had to consent, and on the day fixed Levin had ready for his sister-in-law a set of four horses and relays, getting them together from the farm and saddle-horses—not at all a smart-looking set, but capable of taking Darya Alexandrovna the whole distance in a single day.", "다리야 알렉산드로브나는 승낙할 수밖에 없었고, 정해진 날에 레빈은 형수를 위해 농장 말과 안장 말을 그러모아 네 마리에 갈아탈 말까지 마련해 두었다. 그다지 번듯해 보이는 한 벌은 아니었지만, 다리야 알렉산드로브나를 하루 만에 그 거리 전부를 데려다줄 수 있는 말들이었다."], ["At that moment, when horses were wanted for the princess, who was going, and for the midwife, it was a difficult matter for Levin to make up the number, but the duties of hospitality would not let him allow Darya Alexandrovna to hire horses when staying in his house.", "마침 그 무렵에는 떠나려는 공작 부인을 위한 말과 산파를 위한 말도 필요했던 터라, 레빈으로서는 그 수를 맞추기가 쉽지 않은 일이었다. 그러나 손님을 대접하는 도리상, 자기 집에 머무는 다리야 알렉산드로브나가 말을 빌리도록 내버려 둘 수는 없었다."], ["Moreover, he was well aware that the twenty roubles that would be asked for the journey were a serious matter for her; Darya Alexandrovna’s pecuniary affairs, which were in a very unsatisfactory state, were taken to heart by the Levins as if they were their own.", "더구나 그는 이번 여정에 부르는 값으로 이십 루블이 청구되리라는 것을, 그것이 그녀에게는 만만찮은 문제임을 잘 알고 있었다. 매우 좋지 못한 상태에 놓인 다리야 알렉산드로브나의 금전 사정을 레빈 부부는 자기들 일처럼 마음에 두고 있었다."]], [["Darya Alexandrovna, by Levin’s advice, started before daybreak.", "레빈의 권유에 따라 다리야 알렉산드로브나는 동트기 전에 길을 나섰다."], ["The road was good, the carriage comfortable, the horses trotted along merrily, and on the box, besides the coachman, sat the counting-house clerk, whom Levin was sending instead of a groom for greater security.", "길은 좋았고, 마차는 편안했으며, 말들은 신나게 잰걸음을 놓았다. 마부석에는 마부 외에 회계 사무소 서기가 앉아 있었는데, 레빈이 더 든든하라고 마부 옆에 딸려 보낸 사람이었다."], ["Darya Alexandrovna dozed and waked up only on reaching the inn where the horses were to be changed.", "다리야 알렉산드로브나는 꾸벅꾸벅 졸다가, 말을 갈아탈 여인숙에 이르러서야 잠에서 깼다."]], [["After drinking tea at the same well-to-do peasant’s with whom Levin had stayed on the way to Sviazhsky’s, and chatting with the women about their children, and with the old man about Count Vronsky, whom the latter praised very highly, Darya Alexandrovna, at ten o’clock, went on again.", "레빈이 스비야시스키네로 가던 길에 묵었던 그 부유한 농부의 집에서 차를 마시고, 아낙네들과는 그 집 아이들 이야기를, 노인과는 브론스키 백작 이야기를 나눈 뒤—노인은 백작을 입에 침이 마르도록 칭찬했다—다리야 알렉산드로브나는 열 시에 다시 길을 떠났다."], ["At home, looking after her children, she had no time to think.", "집에 있을 때는 아이들을 돌보느라 생각에 잠길 겨를이 없었다."], ["So now, after this journey of four hours, all the thoughts she had suppressed before rushed swarming into her brain, and she thought over all her life as she never had before, and from the most different points of view.", "그래서 지금, 네 시간이나 이어진 이 여정 끝에, 전에는 억눌러 두었던 온갖 상념이 떼 지어 머릿속으로 밀려들었고, 그녀는 일찍이 한 번도 그래 본 적 없을 만큼, 그것도 더없이 다양한 각도에서 자기 삶 전체를 곱씹어 보았다."], ["Her thoughts seemed strange even to herself.", "그 생각들은 그녀 자신에게도 낯설게 느껴졌다."], ["At first she thought about the children, about whom she was uneasy, although the princess and Kitty (she reckoned more upon her) had promised to look after them.", "처음에 그녀는 아이들 생각을 했는데, 공작 부인과 키티가(키티 쪽을 더 믿기는 했다) 돌봐 주겠다고 약속했는데도 그 애들이 마음 놓이지 않았다."], ["“If only Masha does not begin her naughty tricks, if Grisha isn’t kicked by a horse, and Lily’s stomach isn’t upset again!”", "'마샤가 못된 장난만 시작하지 않으면, 그리샤가 말에 차이지만 않으면, 릴리가 또 배탈이 나지만 않으면!'"], ["she thought.", "그녀는 생각했다."], ["But these questions of the present were succeeded by questions of the immediate future.", "그러나 이런 당장의 문제들에 뒤이어 가까운 미래의 문제들이 떠올랐다."], ["She began thinking how she had to get a new flat in Moscow for the coming winter, to renew the drawing-room furniture, and to make her elder girl a cloak.", "그녀는 다가오는 겨울을 나려면 모스크바에 새 셋집을 구하고, 응접실 가구를 새로 들이고, 큰딸에게 망토를 지어 줘야 한다는 생각을 하기 시작했다."], ["Then questions of the more remote future occurred to her: how she was to place her children in the world.", "그러다 더 먼 미래의 문제들이 떠올랐다. 아이들을 어떻게 세상에 내보낼 것인가 하는 문제였다."], ["“The girls are all right,”", "'딸들은 괜찮아.'"], ["she thought; “but the boys?”", "그녀는 생각했다. '하지만 아들들은?'"]], [["“It’s very well that I’m teaching Grisha, but of course that’s only because I am free myself now, I’m not with child.", "'내가 그리샤를 가르치는 건 좋은 일이지만, 물론 그건 지금 내 몸이 자유롭기 때문이야, 아이를 배지 않았으니까."], ["Stiva, of course, there’s no counting on.", "스티바는 물론 기대할 게 못 되지."], ["And with the help of good-natured friends I can bring them up; but if there’s another baby coming?...”", "마음씨 좋은 친구들의 도움을 받으면 애들을 키울 수야 있겠지. 하지만 또 아이가 들어선다면?……'"], ["And the thought struck her how untruly it was said that the curse laid on woman was that in sorrow she should bring forth children.", "그러자 여자에게 내려진 저주가 슬픔 속에서 아이를 낳는 것이라는 말이 얼마나 진실과 거리가 먼지가 문득 그녀의 마음을 쳤다."]], [["“The birth itself, that’s nothing; but the months of carrying the child—that’s what’s so intolerable,”", "'낳는 것 그 자체야 아무것도 아니지. 그렇지만 아이를 배고 지내는 그 몇 달—그게 정말 견딜 수 없는 거야.'"], ["she thought, picturing to herself her last pregnancy, and the death of the last baby.", "그녀는 마지막 임신과 마지막 아이의 죽음을 떠올리며 생각했다."], ["And she recalled the conversation she had just had with the young woman at the inn.", "그리고 방금 여인숙에서 젊은 아낙과 나눈 대화가 생각났다."], ["On being asked whether she had any children, the handsome young woman had answered cheerfully:", "아이가 있느냐는 물음에 그 잘생긴 젊은 아낙은 명랑하게 대답했다."]], [["“I had a girl baby, but God set me free; I buried her last Lent.”", "\"계집애가 하나 있었는데, 하느님이 데려가셨지요. 지난 사순절에 묻었답니다.\""]], [["“Well, did you grieve very much for her?”", "\"아니, 그래서 많이 슬펐겠네요?\""], ["asked Darya Alexandrovna.", "다리야 알렉산드로브나가 물었다."]], [["“Why grieve?", "\"슬프긴요?"], ["The old man has grandchildren enough as it is.", "시아버지한테는 이미 손주가 차고 넘쳐요."], ["It was only a trouble.", "그저 짐만 됐는걸요."], ["No working, nor nothing.", "일도 못 하고, 아무것도 못 하고."], ["Only a tie.”", "그저 발목만 잡혔지요.\""]], [["This answer had struck Darya Alexandrovna as revolting in spite of the good-natured and pleasing face of the young woman; but now she could not help recalling these words.", "이 대답은, 그 젊은 아낙의 선량하고 호감 가는 얼굴에도 불구하고 다리야 알렉산드로브나에게는 역겹게 느껴졌었다. 그런데 지금은 그 말이 자꾸만 떠오르는 것을 어쩔 수 없었다."], ["In those cynical words there was indeed a grain of truth.", "그 냉소적인 말 속에는 정말이지 한 톨의 진실이 들어 있었다."]], [["“Yes, altogether,”", "'그래, 따지고 보면.'"], ["thought Darya Alexandrovna, looking back over her whole existence during those fifteen years of her married life, “pregnancy, sickness, mental incapacity, indifference to everything, and most of all—hideousness.", "다리야 알렉산드로브나는 결혼 생활 십오 년 동안의 자기 삶 전체를 돌아보며 생각했다. '임신, 입덧, 멍해지는 정신, 만사에 대한 무관심, 그리고 무엇보다도—추해지는 것."], ["Kitty, young and pretty as she is, even Kitty has lost her looks; and I when I’m with child become hideous, I know it.", "키티는 저렇게 젊고 예쁜데도, 키티마저 미모를 잃었어. 그리고 나는, 아이를 배면 흉해져, 나도 알아."], ["The birth, the agony, the hideous agonies, that last moment ...", "출산, 그 고통, 그 끔찍한 고통, 그 마지막 순간……"], ["then the nursing, the sleepless nights, the fearful pains....”", "그러고 나면 젖먹이기, 잠 못 이루는 밤들, 그 무서운 통증들……'"]], [["Darya Alexandrovna shuddered at the mere recollection of the pain from sore breasts which she had suffered with almost every child.", "다리야 알렉산드로브나는 아이를 낳을 때마다 거의 매번 겪었던, 헐어 버린 젖가슴의 통증을 떠올리는 것만으로도 몸서리를 쳤다."], ["“Then the children’s illnesses, that everlasting apprehension; then bringing them up; evil propensities”", "'그다음엔 아이들의 병치레, 그 끝없는 조마조마함."], ["(she thought of little Masha’s crime among the raspberries), “education, Latin—it’s all so incomprehensible and difficult.", "그다음엔 키워 내는 일, 못된 버릇'(그녀는 산딸기밭에서 어린 마샤가 저지른 잘못을 떠올렸다), '교육, 라틴어—전부 너무나 알 수 없고 어려운 일이야."], ["And on the top of it all, the death of these children.”", "게다가 이 모든 것 위에, 이 아이들의 죽음이 있지.'"], ["And there rose again before her imagination the cruel memory, that always tore her mother’s heart, of the death of her last little baby, who had died of croup; his funeral, the callous indifference of all at the little pink coffin, and her own torn heart, and her lonely anguish at the sight of the pale little brow with its projecting temples, and the open, wondering little mouth seen in the coffin at the moment when it was being covered with the little pink lid with a cross braided on it.", "그러자 어머니의 가슴을 늘 갈가리 찢어 놓던 그 잔인한 기억이 다시금 그녀의 상상 앞에 떠올랐다. 후두염으로 죽은 마지막 어린 아기의 죽음, 그 아이의 장례식, 작은 분홍빛 관 앞에서 모두가 보이던 매정한 무관심, 그리고 갈가리 찢긴 자기 가슴, 관 뚜껑—십자가가 수놓인 작은 분홍빛 뚜껑—이 덮이려는 순간 관 속으로 보이던, 관자놀이가 불거진 창백한 작은 이마와 무언가 의아해하듯 벌어진 작은 입을 바라보며 느꼈던 자신의 외로운 비통함이."]], [["“And all this, what’s it for?", "'그런데 이 모든 게 다 무엇을 위한 거지?"], ["What is to come of it all?", "이 모든 데서 무엇이 나온단 말이야?"], ["That I’m wasting my life, never having a moment’s peace, either with child, or nursing a child, forever irritable, peevish, wretched myself and worrying others, repulsive to my husband, while the children are growing up unhappy, badly educated, and penniless.", "나는 한순간의 평온도 누리지 못한 채, 아이를 배거나 아이에게 젖을 먹이거나 하면서, 늘 신경이 곤두서고, 신경질을 부리고, 나 자신도 비참하면서 남들까지 들볶고, 남편에게는 정나미 떨어지는 존재가 되어 내 삶을 허비하고 있어. 그러는 사이 아이들은 불행하게, 제대로 교육도 못 받고, 돈 한 푼 없이 자라고 있고."], ["Even now, if it weren’t for spending the summer at the Levins’, I don’t know how we should be managing to live.", "지금만 해도 레빈네에서 여름을 나지 않았더라면, 우리가 어떻게 살아갔을지 모르겠어."], ["Of course Kostya and Kitty have so much tact that we don’t feel it; but it can’t go on.", "물론 코스챠와 키티는 워낙 배려가 깊어서 우리가 그걸 느끼지 않게 해 주지. 하지만 이대로 계속될 수는 없어."], ["They’ll have children, they won’t be able to keep us; it’s a drag on them as it is.", "저들도 아이를 낳을 테고, 그러면 우리를 거둬 줄 수 없을 거야. 지금도 저들에게는 짐인걸."], ["How is papa, who has hardly anything left for himself, to help us?", "자기 앞가림할 것조차 거의 남지 않은 아버지가 우리를 어떻게 돕겠어?"], ["So that I can’t even bring the children up by myself, and may find it hard with the help of other people, at the cost of humiliation.", "그러니 나 혼자서는 아이들을 키울 수조차 없고, 남의 도움을 받아 가며 굴욕을 무릅쓰고 키운다 해도 힘에 부칠 거야."], ["Why, even if we suppose the greatest good luck, that the children don’t die, and I bring them up somehow.", "그래, 설령 가장 큰 행운을 가정해서, 아이들이 죽지 않고 내가 그럭저럭 키워 낸다 쳐."], ["At the very best they’ll simply be decent people.", "아무리 잘돼 봤자 그저 점잖은 사람들이 될 뿐이야."], ["That’s all I can hope for.", "내가 바랄 수 있는 건 그게 다야."], ["And to gain simply that—what agonies, what toil!...", "그런데 고작 그것을 얻으려고—이 무슨 고통이며, 이 무슨 고생이람!……"], ["One’s whole life ruined!”", "한 사람의 인생이 통째로 망가지는 거지!'"], ["Again she recalled what the young peasant woman had said, and again she was revolted at the thought; but she could not help admitting that there was a grain of brutal truth in the words.", "그녀는 다시 그 젊은 농가 아낙이 한 말을 떠올렸고, 다시금 그 생각에 역겨움을 느꼈다. 그러나 그 말 속에 잔인한 진실 한 톨이 들어 있음을 인정하지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["“Is it far now, Mihail?”", "\"이제 얼마나 더 가야 하나, 미하일?\""], ["Darya Alexandrovna asked the counting-house clerk, to turn her mind from thoughts that were frightening her.", "다리야 알렉산드로브나는 자기를 두렵게 하는 생각들에서 마음을 돌리려고 회계 사무소 서기에게 물었다."]], [["“From this village, they say, it’s five miles.”", "\"이 마을에서부터 오 베르스타라고들 합니다.\""], ["The carriage drove along the village street and onto a bridge.", "마차는 마을 길을 따라 달리다 다리 위로 올라섰다."], ["On the bridge was a crowd of peasant women with coils of ties for the sheaves on their shoulders, gaily and noisily chattering.", "다리 위에는 어깨에 곡식 단을 묶을 새끼줄 타래를 멘 농가 아낙들이 떼 지어 명랑하고 시끌벅적하게 수다를 떨고 있었다."], ["They stood still on the bridge, staring inquisitively at the carriage.", "그들은 다리 위에 멈춰 서서 호기심 어린 눈으로 마차를 빤히 쳐다보았다."], ["All the faces turned to Darya Alexandrovna looked to her healthy and happy, making her envious of their enjoyment of life.", "다리야 알렉산드로브나 쪽으로 돌린 그 얼굴들은 하나같이 건강하고 행복해 보였고, 그래서 삶을 즐기는 그들이 부러웠다."], ["“They’re all living, they’re all enjoying life,”", "'저들은 다들 살아가고 있어, 다들 삶을 즐기고 있어.'"], ["Darya Alexandrovna still mused when she had passed the peasant women and was driving uphill again at a trot, seated comfortably on the soft springs of the old carriage, “while I, let out, as it were from prison, from the world of worries that fret me to death, am only looking about me now for an instant.", "다리야 알렉산드로브나는 농가 아낙들을 지나쳐 낡은 마차의 부드러운 용수철에 편안히 몸을 묻은 채 다시 잰걸음으로 언덕을 오르면서도 생각에 잠겨 있었다. '그런데 나는, 말하자면 감옥에서, 나를 죽도록 들볶는 근심의 세계에서 풀려난 지금에야 비로소 잠깐 주위를 둘러보고 있는 거야."], ["They all live; those peasant women and my sister Natalia and Varenka and Anna, whom I am going to see—all, but not I.", "저들은 다들 살아가고 있어. 저 농가 아낙들도, 내 동생 나탈리야도, 바렌카도, 내가 만나러 가는 안나도—다들 그래, 그런데 나만 아니야."]], [["“And they attack Anna. What for?", "'그런데도 사람들은 안나를 공격하지."], ["am I any better?", "무엇 때문에?"], ["I have, anyway, a husband I love—not as I should like to love him, still I do love him, while Anna never loved hers.", "내가 그녀보다 나은 게 뭐가 있어? 나는 그래도 사랑하는 남편이 있어—내가 바라는 만큼 사랑하지는 못해도, 그래도 사랑은 하지."], ["How is she to blame? She wants to live.", "하지만 안나는 한 번도 자기 남편을 사랑한 적이 없잖아."], ["God has put that in our hearts.", "그러니 그녀에게 무슨 잘못이 있어?"], ["Very likely I should have done the same.", "그녀는 살고 싶은 거야."], ["Even to this day I don’t feel sure I did right in listening to her at that terrible time when she came to me in Moscow.", "하느님이 우리 가슴속에 그런 마음을 넣어 두셨어. 어쩌면 나라도 똑같이 했을지 몰라."], ["I ought then to have cast off my husband and have begun my life fresh.", "그녀가 그 끔찍한 시절에 모스크바로 나를 찾아왔을 때 그녀 말을 들어준 게 잘한 일이었는지, 오늘까지도 나는 확신이 서지 않아."], ["I might have loved and have been loved in reality.", "그때 나는 남편을 버리고 내 삶을 새로 시작했어야 했어."], ["And is it any better as it is?", "진짜로 사랑하고 사랑받을 수도 있었을 텐데."], ["I don’t respect him. He’s necessary to me,”", "그런데 지금 이대로가 조금이라도 더 나은가?"], ["she thought about her husband, “and I put up with him.", "나는 그를 존경하지 않아. 그저 그가 내게 필요할 뿐이지.'"], ["Is that any better?", "그녀는 남편에 대해 생각했다."], ["At that time I could still have been admired, I had beauty left me still,”", "'그래서 그를 참고 견디는 거야. 이게 조금이라도 더 나은 건가?"], ["Darya Alexandrovna pursued her thoughts, and she would have liked to look at herself in the looking-glass.", "그때만 해도 나는 아직 사람들의 찬사를 받을 수 있었어, 아직 미모가 남아 있었으니까.'"], ["She had a traveling looking-glass in her handbag, and she wanted to take it out; but looking at the backs of the coachman and the swaying counting-house clerk, she felt that she would be ashamed if either of them were to look round, and she did not take out the glass.", "다리야 알렉산드로브나는 생각을 이어 갔고, 거울에 자기 모습을 비춰 보고 싶어졌다. 손가방 속에 여행용 손거울이 있어서 그것을 꺼내려 했지만, 마부와 흔들거리는 회계 사무소 서기의 등을 보고는, 둘 중 누구라도 뒤를 돌아보면 부끄러우리라는 생각이 들어 거울을 꺼내지 않았다."]], [["But without looking in the glass, she thought that even now it was not too late; and she thought of Sergey Ivanovitch, who was always particularly attentive to her, of Stiva’s good-hearted friend, Turovtsin, who had helped her nurse her children through the scarlatina, and was in love with her.", "그러나 거울을 들여다보지 않고도 그녀는 지금이라도 아직 늦지 않았다고 생각했다. 그러고는 늘 자기에게 유독 살뜰하던 세르게이 이바노비치를, 또 성홍열을 앓던 자기 아이들을 간호하는 일을 도와주었고 자기를 사랑하던 스티바의 마음씨 좋은 친구 투롭친을 떠올렸다."], ["And there was someone else, a quite young man, who—her husband had told her it as a joke—thought her more beautiful than either of her sisters.", "그리고 또 한 사람, 아주 젊은 남자가 있었는데, 남편이 농담조로 일러 주기로는 그 사람이 그녀를 두 자매 어느 쪽보다도 더 아름답다고 여긴다는 것이었다."], ["And the most passionate and impossible romances rose before Darya Alexandrovna’s imagination.", "그리하여 더없이 열정적이고 더없이 가당찮은 연애담이 다리야 알렉산드로브나의 상상 앞에 떠올랐다."], ["“Anna did quite right, and certainly I shall never reproach her for it.", "'안나는 정말 잘한 거야, 나는 절대로 그녀를 나무라지 않을 거야."], ["She is happy, she makes another person happy, and she’s not broken down as I am, but most likely just as she always was, bright, clever, open to every impression,”", "그녀는 행복하고, 또 다른 사람을 행복하게 해 주고 있어. 게다가 그녀는 나처럼 무너져 버린 게 아니라, 십중팔구 여전히 예전 그대로일 거야, 환하고, 영리하고, 온갖 인상에 활짝 열려 있는 모습으로.'"], ["thought Darya Alexandrovna,—and a sly smile curved her lips, for, as she pondered on Anna’s love affair, Darya Alexandrovna constructed on parallel lines an almost identical love affair for herself, with an imaginary composite figure, the ideal man who was in love with her.", "다리야 알렉산드로브나는 그렇게 생각했고, 입가에 짓궂은 미소가 번졌다. 안나의 연애에 대해 곰곰이 생각하다가, 다리야 알렉산드로브나는 그것과 나란히, 자기를 사랑하는 이상적인 남자라는 상상 속의 합성 인물을 상대로, 거의 똑같은 연애담을 자기 몫으로 지어내고 있었던 것이다."], ["She, like Anna, confessed the whole affair to her husband.", "그녀는 안나처럼 그 일 전부를 남편에게 고백했다."], ["And the amazement and perplexity of Stepan Arkadyevitch at this avowal made her smile.", "그러자 이 고백에 어쩔 줄 몰라 하며 얼떨떨해하는 스테판 아르카지치의 모습이 그녀를 웃음 짓게 했다."]], [["In such daydreams she reached the turning of the highroad that led to Vozdvizhenskoe.", "이런 백일몽에 잠긴 채 그녀는 보즈드비젠스코예로 이어지는 큰길의 모퉁이에 다다랐다."]], [["# Chapter 17", "# Chapter 17"]], [["The coachman pulled up his four horses and looked round to the right, to a field of rye, where some peasants were sitting on a cart.", "마부가 네 마리 말의 고삐를 당겨 세우고는 오른쪽으로, 농부 몇몇이 짐수레에 걸터앉아 있는 호밀밭 쪽을 둘러보았다."], ["The counting-house clerk was just going to jump down, but on second thoughts he shouted peremptorily to the peasants instead, and beckoned to them to come up.", "사무소 서기는 막 뛰어내리려다가 다시 생각을 고쳐먹고는, 대신 농부들에게 명령조로 소리치며 이리 오라고 손짓을 했다."], ["The wind, that seemed to blow as they drove, dropped when the carriage stood still; gadflies settled on the steaming horses that angrily shook them off.", "마차가 달릴 때만 부는 듯하던 바람도 마차가 멈춰 서자 잦아들었다. 김이 모락모락 나는 말들 위로 등에가 달라붙었고, 말들은 성이 나서 그것을 흔들어 떨쳐 냈다."], ["The metallic clank of a whetstone against a scythe, that came to them from the cart, ceased.", "짐수레 쪽에서 들려오던, 큰 낫에 숫돌을 가는 쇳소리도 멎었다."], ["One of the peasants got up and came towards the carriage.", "농부 하나가 일어나 마차 쪽으로 다가왔다."]], [["“Well, you are slow!”", "\"이런, 어쩌면 그리 굼떠!\""], ["the counting-house clerk shouted angrily to the peasant who was stepping slowly with his bare feet over the ruts of the rough dry road.", "사무소 서기가, 거칠고 메마른 길의 바퀴 자국 위를 맨발로 천천히 디뎌 오는 농부에게 성난 목소리로 외쳤다."], ["“Come along, do!”", "\"어서 좀 와요!\""]], [["A curly-headed old man with a bit of bast tied round his hair, and his bent back dark with perspiration, came towards the carriage, quickening his steps, and took hold of the mud-guard with his sunburnt hand.", "머리카락을 둘둘 만 곱슬머리 노인이, 머리에 나무껍질 끈을 동여매고 땀에 젖어 거뭇해진 굽은 등을 한 채로, 걸음을 재촉하며 마차 쪽으로 다가와 햇볕에 그을린 손으로 흙받기를 붙잡았다."]], [["“Vozdvizhenskoe, the manor house?", "\"보즈드비젠스코예, 영주 저택 말이오?"], ["the count’s?”", "백작님 댁?\""], ["he repeated; “go on to the end of this track.", "그가 되물었다. \"이 길을 끝까지 가시오."], ["Then turn to the left.", "그러고 나서 왼쪽으로 도시오."], ["Straight along the avenue and you’ll come right upon it.", "가로수 길을 따라 곧장 가면 바로 거기 나옵니다."], ["But whom do you want?", "그런데 누구를 찾으시오?"], ["The count himself?”", "백작님 본인이오?\""]], [["“Well, are they at home, my good man?”", "\"그래, 다들 집에 있을까요, 영감님?\""], ["Darya Alexandrovna said vaguely, not knowing how to ask about Anna, even of this peasant.", "다리아 알렉산드로브나가 막연하게 물었다. 이런 농부에게조차 안나에 대해 어떻게 물어야 할지 몰랐던 것이다."]], [["“At home for sure,”", "\"틀림없이 집에 계시지요.\""], ["said the peasant, shifting from one bare foot to the other, and leaving a distinct print of five toes and a heel in the dust.", "농부가 한쪽 맨발에서 다른 쪽 맨발로 무게를 옮기며, 흙먼지 위에 발가락 다섯 개와 발뒤꿈치 자국을 또렷이 남기며 말했다."], ["“Sure to be at home,”", "\"분명 집에 계실 겁니다.\""], ["he repeated, evidently eager to talk.", "그는 이야기를 하고 싶어 안달이 난 듯 거듭 말했다."], ["“Only yesterday visitors arrived.", "\"바로 어제도 손님들이 오셨거든요."], ["There’s a sight of visitors come.", "손님이 한가득 오셨다니까요."], ["What do you want?”", "무슨 일로 오셨소?\""], ["He turned round and called to a lad, who was shouting something to him from the cart.", "그는 몸을 돌려, 짐수레에서 그에게 뭐라고 외치는 젊은이를 향해 소리쳤다."], ["“Oh!", "\"아!"], ["They all rode by here not long since, to look at a reaping machine.", "다들 얼마 전에 여기를 지나 수확 기계를 보러들 가셨소."], ["They’ll be home by now.", "지금쯤은 댁에 돌아오셨을 거요."], ["And who will you be belonging to?...”", "그런데 댁은 뉘 댁 사람이시오?……\""]], [["“We’ve come a long way,”", "\"우리는 멀리서 왔소.\""], ["said the coachman, climbing onto the box.", "마부가 마부석으로 기어오르며 말했다."], ["“So it’s not far?”", "\"그래, 이제 멀지 않은 거요?\""]], [["“I tell you, it’s just here.", "\"글쎄, 바로 여기라니까요."], ["As soon as you get out....”", "빠져나가시기만 하면……\""], ["he said, keeping hold all the while of the carriage.", "그는 그러는 내내 마차를 붙잡은 채로 말했다."]], [["A healthy-looking, broad-shouldered young fellow came up too.", "건강해 보이는, 어깨가 떡 벌어진 젊은이도 다가왔다."]], [["“What, is it laborers they want for the harvest?”", "\"뭐예요, 수확 일꾼을 구하는 거예요?\""], ["he asked.", "그가 물었다."]], [["“I don’t know, my boy.”", "\"나도 모르네, 이 사람아.\""]], [["“So you keep to the left, and you’ll come right on it,”", "\"그러니까 왼쪽으로 가시오, 그러면 바로 거기 나옵니다.\""], ["said the peasant, unmistakably loth to let the travelers go, and eager to converse.", "농부가 말했는데, 분명히 길손들을 놓아주기 싫어하며 더 이야기를 나누고 싶은 기색이었다."]], [["The coachman started the horses, but they were only just turning off when the peasant shouted: “Stop!", "마부가 말을 출발시켰지만, 이제 막 모퉁이를 돌려는 참에 농부가 외쳤다. \"서요!"], ["Hi, friend!", "어이, 양반!"], ["Stop!”", "서요!\""], ["called the two voices.", "두 목소리가 외쳤다."], ["The coachman stopped.", "마부가 멈춰 섰다."]], [["“They’re coming!", "\"저기 오십니다!"], ["They’re yonder!”", "저기 계세요!\""], ["shouted the peasant.", "농부가 외쳤다."], ["“See what a turn-out!”", "\"저 행차 좀 보시오!\""], ["he said, pointing to four persons on horseback, and two in a char-à-banc, coming along the road.", "그는 말을 타고 길을 따라 오는 네 사람과, 샤라방을 탄 두 사람을 가리키며 말했다."]], [["They were Vronsky with a jockey, Veslovsky and Anna on horseback, and Princess Varvara and Sviazhsky in the char-à-banc.", "그들은 기수를 거느린 브론스키와, 말을 탄 베슬롭스키와 안나, 그리고 샤라방에 탄 바르바라 공작 영애와 스비아시스키였다."], ["They had gone out to look at the working of a new reaping machine.", "그들은 새로 들여온 수확 기계가 어떻게 돌아가는지 보러 나갔던 것이다."]], [["When the carriage stopped, the party on horseback were coming at a walking pace.", "* 샤라방: 여러 사람이 나란히 앉을 수 있도록 좌석을 가로로 배치한 마차. 마차가 멈춰 섰을 때, 말을 탄 일행은 천천히 걸어오고 있었다."], ["Anna was in front beside Veslovsky.", "안나가 베슬롭스키 옆에서 앞장서 있었다."], ["Anna, quietly walking her horse, a sturdy English cob with cropped mane and short tail, her beautiful head with her black hair straying loose under her high hat, her full shoulders, her slender waist in her black riding habit, and all the ease and grace of her deportment, impressed Dolly.", "갈기를 짧게 깎고 꼬리를 짧게 자른, 다부진 영국산 코브를 조용히 걸리고 있는 안나, 높다란 모자 아래로 검은 머리카락을 풀어 흘러내린 아름다운 머리며, 풍만한 어깨며, 검은 승마복에 감싸인 가느다란 허리며, 그 거동의 온갖 여유로움과 우아함이 돌리에게 강한 인상을 남겼다."]], [["For the first minute it seemed to her unsuitable for Anna to be on horseback.", "처음 잠깐 동안은 안나가 말을 타고 있다는 것이 어울리지 않게 여겨졌다."], ["The conception of riding on horseback for a lady was, in Darya Alexandrovna’s mind, associated with ideas of youthful flirtation and frivolity, which, in her opinion, was unbecoming in Anna’s position.", "다리아 알렉산드로브나의 머릿속에서 부인이 말을 탄다는 관념은 젊은이다운 교태나 경박함에 대한 생각과 결부되어 있었는데, 그녀가 보기에 그런 것은 안나의 처지에는 어울리지 않았다."], ["But when she had scrutinized her, seeing her closer, she was at once reconciled to her riding.", "하지만 가까이서 그녀를 자세히 뜯어보고 나자, 그녀는 곧바로 안나의 승마를 받아들이게 되었다."], ["In spite of her elegance, everything was so simple, quiet, and dignified in the attitude, the dress and the movements of Anna, that nothing could have been more natural.", "그 우아함에도 불구하고, 안나의 자태며 옷차림이며 동작은 모두가 너무도 단순하고 차분하고 기품이 있어서, 그보다 더 자연스러울 수가 없었다."]], [["Beside Anna, on a hot-looking gray cavalry horse, was Vassenka Veslovsky in his Scotch cap with floating ribbons, his stout legs stretched out in front, obviously pleased with his own appearance.", "안나 옆에는, 더워 보이는 잿빛 군마를 탄 바센카 베슬롭스키가, 리본을 나부끼는 스코틀랜드식 모자를 쓰고, 통통한 두 다리를 앞으로 쭉 뻗은 채로, 누가 봐도 제 모습에 흡족해하며 있었다."], ["Darya Alexandrovna could not suppress a good-humored smile as she recognized him.", "다리아 알렉산드로브나는 그를 알아보고는 흐뭇한 미소를 억누를 수가 없었다."], ["Behind rode Vronsky on a dark bay mare, obviously heated from galloping.", "그 뒤로는 브론스키가, 분명 질주로 달아오른 짙은 적갈색 암말을 타고 오고 있었다."], ["He was holding her in, pulling at the reins.", "그는 고삐를 당기며 말을 제어하고 있었다."]], [["After him rode a little man in the dress of a jockey.", "그의 뒤로는 기수 차림을 한 자그마한 사내가 따라왔다."], ["Sviazhsky and Princess Varvara in a new char-à-banc with a big, raven-black trotting horse, overtook the party on horseback.", "스비아시스키와 바르바라 공작 영애는 큼직한 칠흑같이 검은 속보마가 끄는 새 샤라방을 타고서 말을 탄 일행을 앞질렀다."]], [["Anna’s face suddenly beamed with a joyful smile at the instant when, in the little figure huddled in a corner of the old carriage, she recognized Dolly.", "낡은 마차 한구석에 잔뜩 웅크린 자그마한 형체에서 돌리를 알아본 순간, 안나의 얼굴이 갑자기 기쁨에 찬 미소로 환하게 빛났다."], ["She uttered a cry, started in the saddle, and set her horse into a gallop.", "그녀는 소리를 지르며 안장 위에서 움찔하더니 말을 질주시켰다."], ["On reaching the carriage she jumped off without assistance, and holding up her riding habit, she ran up to greet Dolly.", "마차에 다다르자 그녀는 누구의 부축도 없이 뛰어내려, 승마복 자락을 추켜올린 채 돌리를 맞으러 달려갔다."]], [["“I thought it was you and dared not think it.", "\"너인 줄 알았으면서도 차마 그렇게 믿지를 못했어."], ["How delightful!", "정말 기뻐!"], ["You can’t fancy how glad I am!”", "내가 얼마나 기쁜지 너는 짐작도 못 할 거야!\""], ["she said, at one moment pressing her face against Dolly and kissing her, and at the next holding her off and examining her with a smile.", "그녀는 한순간 돌리에게 얼굴을 비비며 입을 맞추다가, 다음 순간에는 그녀를 떼어 놓고 미소를 지으며 찬찬히 들여다보면서 말했다."]], [["“Here’s a delightful surprise, Alexey!”", "\"여보, 이 얼마나 반가운 뜻밖의 손님이에요!\""], ["she said, looking round at Vronsky, who had dismounted, and was walking towards them.", "그녀는 말에서 내려 그들 쪽으로 걸어오는 브론스키를 돌아보며 말했다."]], [["Vronsky, taking off his tall gray hat, went up to Dolly.", "브론스키는 높다란 잿빛 모자를 벗으며 돌리에게 다가갔다."]], [["“You wouldn’t believe how glad we are to see you,”", "\"저희가 부인을 뵙게 되어 얼마나 기쁜지 모르실 겁니다.\""], ["he said, giving peculiar significance to the words, and showing his strong white teeth in a smile.", "그가 그 말에 각별한 의미를 실으며, 미소 속에 튼튼하고 새하얀 이를 드러내며 말했다."]], [["Vassenka Veslovsky, without getting off his horse, took off his cap and greeted the visitor by gleefully waving the ribbons over his head.", "바센카 베슬롭스키는 말에서 내리지도 않은 채 모자를 벗어, 머리 위로 신이 나서 리본을 흔들며 손님에게 인사를 했다."]], [["“That’s Princess Varvara,”", "\"저분은 바르바라 공작 영애예요.\""], ["Anna said in reply to a glance of inquiry from Dolly as the char-à-banc drove up.", "샤라방이 다가오자, 묻는 듯한 돌리의 눈길에 답하여 안나가 말했다."]], [["“Ah!”", "\"아!\""], ["said Darya Alexandrovna, and unconsciously her face betrayed her dissatisfaction.", "다리아 알렉산드로브나가 말했고, 자기도 모르게 그녀의 얼굴에 못마땅한 기색이 드러났다."]], [["Princess Varvara was her husband’s aunt, and she had long known her, and did not respect her.", "바르바라 공작 영애는 그녀의 남편의 고모였고, 그녀는 오래전부터 그녀를 알고 있었지만 그녀를 존경하지는 않았다."], ["She knew that Princess Varvara had passed her whole life toadying on her rich relations, but that she should now be sponging on Vronsky, a man who was nothing to her, mortified Dolly on account of her kinship with her husband.", "다리아 알렉산드로브나는 바르바라 공작 영애가 평생을 부유한 친척들에게 아첨하며 살아왔다는 것을 알고 있었는데, 그런 그녀가 이제 자기와는 아무 사이도 아닌 브론스키에게 빌붙어 산다는 사실은 자기 남편과의 친척 관계 때문에 돌리에게 굴욕감을 안겨 주었다."], ["Anna noticed Dolly’s expression, and was disconcerted by it.", "안나는 돌리의 표정을 알아채고 당황했다."], ["She blushed, dropped her riding habit, and stumbled over it.", "그녀는 얼굴을 붉히며 승마복 자락을 떨어뜨렸고, 거기에 발이 걸려 비틀거렸다."]], [["Darya Alexandrovna went up to the char-à-banc and coldly greeted Princess Varvara.", "다리아 알렉산드로브나는 샤라방으로 다가가 바르바라 공작 영애에게 쌀쌀하게 인사를 했다."], ["Sviazhsky too she knew.", "스비아시스키도 그녀가 아는 사람이었다."], ["He inquired how his queer friend with the young wife was, and running his eyes over the ill-matched horses and the carriage with its patched mud-guards, proposed to the ladies that they should get into the char-à-banc.", "그는 젊은 아내를 둔 그 별난 친구는 어떻게 지내느냐고 안부를 물었고, 어울리지 않는 말들과 흙받기를 기운 마차로 눈길을 휙 훑고 나서, 부인들에게 샤라방에 옮겨 타시는 게 어떻겠느냐고 권했다."]], [["“And I’ll get into this vehicle,”", "\"저는 이 마차에 타지요.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“The horse is quiet, and the princess drives capitally.”", "\"말이 순하고, 공작 영애께서는 솜씨가 아주 훌륭하시니까요.\""]], [["“No, stay as you were,”", "\"아니에요, 그냥 그대로 계세요.\""], ["said Anna, coming up, “and we’ll go in the carriage,”", "안나가 다가오며 말했다. \"우리는 이 마차로 가겠어요.\""], ["and taking Dolly’s arm, she drew her away.", "그러고는 돌리의 팔을 잡고 그녀를 끌고 갔다."]], [["Darya Alexandrovna’s eyes were fairly dazzled by the elegant carriage of a pattern she had never seen before, the splendid horses, and the elegant and gorgeous people surrounding her.", "다리아 알렉산드로브나의 눈은 일찍이 본 적 없는 형태의 우아한 마차며, 훌륭한 말들이며, 자기를 둘러싼 우아하고 화려한 사람들에 그만 어찔할 지경이었다."], ["But what struck her most of all was the change that had taken place in Anna, whom she knew so well and loved.", "하지만 무엇보다도 그녀를 놀라게 한 것은, 그토록 잘 알고 또 사랑했던 안나에게 일어난 변화였다."], ["Any other woman, a less close observer, not knowing Anna before, or not having thought as Darya Alexandrovna had been thinking on the road, would not have noticed anything special in Anna.", "다른 어떤 여자였더라면, 그처럼 가까이서 살피지 않는 사람이었더라면, 전부터 안나를 알지 못했거나 다리아 알렉산드로브나가 오는 길에 했던 것 같은 생각을 하지 않았더라면, 안나에게서 특별한 무엇도 알아채지 못했을 것이다."], ["But now Dolly was struck by that temporary beauty, which is only found in women during the moments of love, and which she saw now in Anna’s face.", "하지만 지금 돌리는, 오로지 사랑의 순간에만 여인들에게서 발견되는, 그리고 지금 안나의 얼굴에서 보이는 그 일시적인 아름다움에 마음을 사로잡혔다."], ["Everything in her face, the clearly marked dimples in her cheeks and chin, the line of her lips, the smile which, as it were, fluttered about her face, the brilliance of her eyes, the grace and rapidity of her movements, the fulness of the notes of her voice, even the manner in which, with a sort of angry friendliness, she answered Veslovsky when he asked permission to get on her cob, so as to teach it to gallop with the right leg foremost—it was all peculiarly fascinating, and it seemed as if she were herself aware of it, and rejoicing in it.", "그녀의 얼굴에 깃든 모든 것이—두 뺨과 턱에 또렷이 팬 보조개며, 입술의 윤곽이며, 그 얼굴 위로 흡사 나풀거리는 듯한 미소며, 두 눈의 광채며, 동작의 우아함과 민첩함이며, 목소리의 충만한 울림이며, 심지어 베슬롭스키가 그녀의 코브를 오른발부터 내딛어 질주하도록 길들이게 한번 타게 해 달라고 청했을 때 일종의 성난 다정함으로 대답하던 그 태도까지—그 모든 것이 유난히 매혹적이었고, 그녀 자신도 그것을 알고서 기뻐하고 있는 듯이 보였다."]], [["When both the women were seated in the carriage, a sudden embarrassment came over both of them.", "두 여자가 마차에 나란히 앉자, 갑자기 둘 다 어색한 기분에 휩싸였다."], ["Anna was disconcerted by the intent look of inquiry Dolly fixed upon her.", "안나는 돌리가 자기에게 던지는 골똘하게 묻는 듯한 눈길에 당황했다."], ["Dolly was embarrassed because after Sviazhsky’s phrase about “this vehicle,” she could not help feeling ashamed of the dirty old carriage in which Anna was sitting with her.", "돌리는, 스비아시스키가 \"이 마차\"라고 한 말을 듣고 나니, 안나가 자기와 함께 타고 앉아 있는 이 낡고 더러운 마차가 부끄럽게 느껴지는 것을 어쩔 수 없어 멋쩍었다."], ["The coachman Philip and the counting-house clerk were experiencing the same sensation.", "마부 필립과 사무소 서기도 똑같은 기분을 맛보고 있었다."], ["The counting-house clerk, to conceal his confusion, busied himself settling the ladies, but Philip the coachman became sullen, and was bracing himself not to be overawed in future by this external superiority.", "사무소 서기는 자기 당혹감을 감추려고 부인들을 자리에 앉히는 일에 분주하게 굴었지만, 마부 필립은 시무룩해져서는, 앞으로는 이런 겉치레의 우월함에 기죽지 않으리라 마음을 다잡고 있었다."], ["He smiled ironically, looking at the raven horse, and was already deciding in his own mind that this smart trotter in the char-à-banc was only good for promenage, and wouldn’t do thirty miles straight off in the heat.", "그는 칠흑 같은 말을 바라보며 비꼬는 듯한 미소를 지었고, 샤라방을 끄는 저 말쑥한 속보마는 그저 산책용으로나 쓸모가 있지, 더위 속에서 삼십 베르스타를 단숨에 달리지는 못할 거라고 벌써 속으로 단정 짓고 있었다."]], [["The peasants had all got up from the cart and were inquisitively and mirthfully staring at the meeting of the friends, making their comments on it.", "농부들은 모두 짐수레에서 일어나, 호기심 어린 즐거운 눈빛으로 두 벗의 만남을 빤히 바라보며 저마다 한마디씩 평을 늘어놓고 있었다."]], [["“They’re pleased, too; haven’t seen each other for a long while,”", "\"저분들도 좋으신 게지, 오랫동안 못 만났던 게야.\""], ["said the curly-headed old man with the bast round his hair.", "머리에 나무껍질 끈을 동여맨 곱슬머리 노인이 말했다."]], [["“I say, Uncle Gerasim, if we could take that raven horse now, to cart the corn, that ’ud be quick work!”", "\"이봐요, 게라심 아저씨, 저 칠흑 같은 말을 지금 끌어다 곡식을 실어 나르면 일이 후딱 끝나겠는걸요!\""]], [["“Look-ee!", "\"저것 좀 봐!"], ["Is that a woman in breeches?”", "저게 바지 입은 여자인가?\""], ["said one of them, pointing to Vassenka Veslovsky sitting in a side saddle.", "그들 중 하나가, 여성용 안장에 앉은 바센카 베슬롭스키를 가리키며 말했다."]], [["“Nay, a man!", "\"아니, 사내잖아!"], ["See how smartly he’s going it!”", "어찌나 멀쑥하게 달리는지 좀 보게!\""]], [["“Eh, lads!", "\"어이, 자네들!"], ["seems we’re not going to sleep, then?”", "그래, 오늘은 잠 못 자게 생겼나 보군?\""]], [["“What chance of sleep today!”", "\"오늘 같은 날 잠은 무슨 잠!\""], ["said the old man, with a sidelong look at the sun.", "노인이 해를 곁눈질하며 말했다."], ["“Midday’s past, look-ee!", "\"한낮이 지났어, 저것 좀 봐!"], ["Get your hooks, and come along!”", "갈고리들 챙겨서 어서들 가세!\""]], [["# Chapter 18", "# Chapter 18"]], [["Anna looked at Dolly’s thin, care-worn face, with its wrinkles filled with dust from the road, and she was on the point of saying what she was thinking, that is, that Dolly had got thinner.", "안나는 길에서 묻은 먼지가 주름마다 끼어 있는 돌리의 야위고 근심에 지친 얼굴을 바라보았고, 자기가 생각하는 바를, 그러니까 돌리가 더 야위었다는 말을 막 하려던 참이었다."], ["But, conscious that she herself had grown handsomer, and that Dolly’s eyes were telling her so, she sighed and began to speak about herself.", "하지만 자기 자신은 더 아름다워졌으며, 돌리의 눈이 그것을 말해 주고 있다는 것을 의식한 그녀는 한숨을 쉬고는 자기 이야기를 꺼내기 시작했다."]], [["“You are looking at me,”", "\"언니는 나를 보면서,\""], ["she said, “and wondering how I can be happy in my position?", "그녀가 말했다, \"이런 처지에서 어떻게 내가 행복할 수 있을까 하고 의아해하고 있죠?"], ["Well!", "그래요!"], ["it’s shameful to confess, but I ...", "이런 말을 하기는 부끄럽지만, 나는……"], ["I’m inexcusably happy.", "나는 변명의 여지없이 행복해요."], ["Something magical has happened to me, like a dream, when you’re frightened, panic-stricken, and all of a sudden you wake up and all the horrors are no more.", "뭔가 마법 같은 일이 나한테 일어났어요, 꿈처럼요. 무섭고 공포에 사로잡혀 있다가 별안간 잠에서 깨어나니 그 온갖 끔찍한 것들이 다 사라져 버린 그런 꿈 말이에요."], ["I have waked up.", "나는 깨어났어요."], ["I have lived through the misery, the dread, and now for a long while past, especially since we’ve been here, I’ve been so happy!...”", "그 비참함과 두려움을 지나 살아남았고, 이제는 오래전부터, 특히 이곳에 온 뒤로는, 너무나 행복해요!……\""], ["she said, with a timid smile of inquiry looking at Dolly.", "그녀는 돌리를 바라보며 무언가를 묻는 듯한 수줍은 미소를 지으며 말했다."]], [["“How glad I am!”", "\"나도 정말 기뻐!\""], ["said Dolly smiling, involuntarily speaking more coldly than she wanted to.", "돌리가 미소 지으며 말했지만, 자기도 모르게 마음먹은 것보다 더 차갑게 말이 나왔다."], ["“I’m very glad for you.", "\"너를 위해 정말 기뻐."], ["Why haven’t you written to me?”", "그런데 왜 나한테 편지를 쓰지 않았니?\""]], [["“Why?...", "\"왜냐고요?……"], ["Because I hadn’t the courage....", "용기가 없었으니까요……"], ["You forget my position....”", "언니는 내 처지를 잊고 있어요……\""]], [["“To me?", "\"나한테?"], ["Hadn’t the courage?", "용기가 없었다고? 내가 어떻게……"], ["If you knew how I ... I look at....”", "어떻게 생각하는지 안다면……\""]], [["Darya Alexandrovna wanted to express her thoughts of the morning, but for some reason it seemed to her now out of place to do so.", "다리야 알렉산드로브나는 아침에 했던 생각을 표현하고 싶었지만, 어쩐 일인지 지금은 그러는 게 어울리지 않는 것처럼 여겨졌다."]], [["“But of that we’ll talk later.", "\"하지만 그 얘긴 나중에 하자."], ["What’s this, what are all these buildings?”", "이게 뭐니, 이 건물들은 다 뭐야?\""], ["she asked, wanting to change the conversation and pointing to the red and green roofs that came into view behind the green hedges of acacia and lilac.", "그녀는 화제를 바꾸고 싶어, 아카시아와 라일락의 초록빛 울타리 너머로 모습을 드러낸 붉고 푸른 지붕들을 가리키며 물었다."], ["“Quite a little town.”", "\"아주 작은 도시 같구나.\""]], [["But Anna did not answer.", "하지만 안나는 대답하지 않았다."]], [["“No, no!", "\"아니에요, 아니에요!"], ["How do you look at my position, what do you think of it?”", "언니는 내 처지를 어떻게 보세요, 그걸 어떻게 생각하세요?\""], ["she asked.", "그녀가 물었다."]], [["“I consider....”", "\"내 생각엔……\""], ["Darya Alexandrovna was beginning, but at that instant Vassenka Veslovsky, having brought the cob to gallop with the right leg foremost, galloped past them, bumping heavily up and down in his short jacket on the chamois leather of the side saddle.", "다리야 알렉산드로브나가 입을 열려는 바로 그 순간, 바센카 베슬롭스키가 조랑말을 오른쪽 다리부터 내딛게 하여 질주시키며, 짧은 윗도리 차림으로 가죽 안장의 섀미가죽 위에서 묵직하게 위아래로 들썩이면서 그녀들 곁을 지나쳐 달려갔다."], ["“He’s doing it, Anna Arkadyevna!”", "\"되네요, 안나 아르카지예브나!\""], ["he shouted.", "그가 소리쳤다."]], [["Anna did not even glance at him; but again it seemed to Darya Alexandrovna out of place to enter upon such a long conversation in the carriage, and so she cut short her thought.", "안나는 그를 거들떠보지도 않았다. 하지만 다리야 알렉산드로브나는 또다시 마차 안에서 그런 긴 이야기를 시작하는 게 어울리지 않는 것처럼 여겨졌고, 그래서 자기 생각을 짧게 잘랐다."]], [["“I don’t think anything,”", "\"난 아무 생각도 안 해,\""], ["she said, “but I always loved you, and if one loves anyone, one loves the whole person, just as they are and not as one would like them to be....”", "그녀가 말했다. \"하지만 난 늘 너를 사랑했고, 누군가를 사랑한다면 그 사람을 통째로, 있는 그대로 사랑하는 거지, 자기가 바라는 모습대로 사랑하는 게 아니거든……\""]], [["Anna, taking her eyes off her friend’s face and dropping her eyelids (this was a new habit Dolly had not seen in her before), pondered, trying to penetrate the full significance of the words.", "안나는 친구의 얼굴에서 눈을 떼어 눈꺼풀을 내리깔고는(이것은 돌리가 전에 본 적 없는 새 버릇이었다) 그 말의 온전한 의미를 꿰뚫어 보려는 듯 곰곰이 생각에 잠겼다."], ["And obviously interpreting them as she would have wished, she glanced at Dolly.", "그러더니 분명 자기가 바라던 대로 그 말을 해석하고는 돌리를 흘끗 바라보았다."]], [["“If you had any sins,”", "\"언니한테 무슨 죄가 있다 해도,\""], ["she said, “they would all be forgiven you for your coming to see me and these words.”", "그녀가 말했다, \"나를 보러 와 준 것과 그 말 한마디로 모두 용서받을 거예요.\""]], [["And Dolly saw that tears stood in her eyes.", "그리고 돌리는 그녀의 눈에 눈물이 고인 것을 보았다."], ["She pressed Anna’s hand in silence.", "그녀는 말없이 안나의 손을 꼭 잡았다."]], [["“Well, what are these buildings?", "\"그래, 이 건물들은 다 뭐니?"], ["How many there are of them!”", "어쩜 이렇게 많아!\""], ["After a moment’s silence she repeated her question.", "잠시 침묵한 뒤 그녀는 다시 물었다."]], [["“These are the servants’ houses, barns, and stables,”", "\"이건 하인들 집이랑 곳간, 마구간이에요.\""], ["answered Anna.", "안나가 대답했다."], ["“And there the park begins.", "\"그리고 저기서부터 공원이 시작돼요."], ["It had all gone to ruin, but Alexey had everything renewed.", "다 폐허가 돼 있던 걸 알렉세이가 전부 새로 손봤어요."], ["He is very fond of this place, and, what I never expected, he has become intensely interested in looking after it.", "그이는 이곳을 무척 좋아하는데, 내가 전혀 기대하지 않았던 건, 그이가 이곳을 돌보는 데 푹 빠져 버렸다는 거예요."], ["But his is such a rich nature!", "그이는 워낙 풍요로운 천성을 타고났거든요!"], ["Whatever he takes up, he does splendidly.", "무슨 일을 하든 멋지게 해내요."], ["So far from being bored by it, he works with passionate interest.", "지루해하기는커녕 열정적인 관심을 쏟으며 일해요."], ["He—with his temperament as I know it—he has become careful and businesslike, a first-rate manager, he positively reckons every penny in his management of the land.", "내가 아는 그이의 기질로 보자면—그런 사람이 신중하고 빈틈없는 일류 경영자가 돼서, 땅을 관리하는 데 한 푼까지 꼼꼼히 따진다니까요. 하지만 그건 그 일에서만 그래요."], ["But only in that. When it’s a question of tens of thousands, he doesn’t think of money.”", "수만 단위가 걸린 문제라면 돈 따위는 생각도 안 하거든요.\""], ["She spoke with that gleefully sly smile with which women often talk of the secret characteristics only known to them—of those they love.", "그녀는 여자들이 흔히 자기들만 아는 사랑하는 사람의 은밀한 특성을 이야기할 때 짓는 그런 즐겁고 짓궂은 미소를 띠며 말했다."], ["“Do you see that big building?", "\"저 큰 건물 보여요?"], ["that’s the new hospital.", "저게 새 병원이에요."], ["I believe it will cost over a hundred thousand; that’s his hobby just now.", "십만 루블도 넘게 들 것 같아요. 그게 요즘 그이가 빠져 있는 일이죠."], ["And do you know how it all came about?", "그런데 그게 다 어떻게 시작됐는지 알아요?"], ["The peasants asked him for some meadowland, I think it was, at a cheaper rate, and he refused, and I accused him of being miserly.", "농부들이 풀밭인가를 좀 더 싼값에 달라고 부탁했는데 그이가 거절했고, 그래서 내가 그이더러 인색하다고 나무랐죠."], ["Of course it was not really because of that, but everything together, he began this hospital to prove, do you see, that he was not miserly about money.", "물론 정말 그것 때문은 아니었지만, 이런저런 게 다 합쳐져서, 그이가 자기는 돈에 인색한 게 아니라는 걸 증명하려고, 알겠어요, 이 병원을 짓기 시작한 거예요."], ["C’est une petitesse, if you like, but I love him all the more for it.", "C'est une petitesse, 굳이 말하자면 그렇지만, 난 그래서 그이를 더 사랑해요. 자, 이제 곧 집이 보일 거예요."], ["And now you’ll see the house in a moment.", "그이 할아버지가 살던 집인데, 바깥은 하나도 바꾸지 않았어요.\""], ["It was his grandfather’s house, and he has had nothing changed outside.”", "* C'est une petitesse: 프랑스어로 '그건 옹졸한 짓이죠'."]], [["“How beautiful!”", "\"어쩜 이렇게 아름답니!\""], ["said Dolly, looking with involuntary admiration at the handsome house with columns, standing out among the different-colored greens of the old trees in the garden.", "돌리가 정원의 오래된 나무들의 가지각색 초록빛 사이로 우뚝 서 있는, 기둥이 늘어선 멋진 집을 자기도 모르게 감탄하며 바라보고 말했다."]], [["“Isn’t it fine?", "\"근사하죠?"], ["And from the house, from the top, the view is wonderful.”", "그리고 저 집 꼭대기에서 내려다보는 경치가 또 기막혀요.\""]], [["They drove into a courtyard strewn with gravel and bright with flowers, in which two laborers were at work putting an edging of stones round the light mould of a flower bed, and drew up in a covered entry.", "그들은 자갈이 깔리고 꽃들로 화사한 안뜸으로 들어섰는데, 거기서는 일꾼 둘이 화단의 부드러운 흙 둘레에 돌로 가장자리를 두르는 작업을 하고 있었고, 마차는 지붕이 덮인 현관에 멈춰 섰다."]], [["“Ah, they’re here already!”", "\"아, 다들 벌써 와 있네!\""], ["said Anna, looking at the saddle horses, which were just being led away from the steps.", "안나가 막 계단에서 끌려 나가고 있는 안장 말들을 보며 말했다. \"예쁜 말이죠, 그렇죠?"], ["“It is a nice horse, isn’t it? It’s my cob; my favorite.", "내 조랑말인데, 내가 제일 좋아하는 거예요."], ["Lead him here and bring me some sugar.", "이리 데려와서 설탕을 좀 가져와요."], ["Where is the count?”", "백작은 어디 있죠?\""], ["she inquired of two smart footmen who darted out.", "그녀가 쪼르르 달려 나온 말쑥한 하인 둘에게 물었다."], ["“Ah, there he is!”", "\"아, 저기 오네!\""], ["she said, seeing Vronsky coming to meet her with Veslovsky.", "그녀는 브론스키가 베슬롭스키와 함께 자기를 맞으러 오는 것을 보며 말했다."]], [["“Where are you going to put the princess?”", "\"공작부인을 어느 방에 모실까요?\""], ["said Vronsky in French, addressing Anna, and without waiting for a reply, he once more greeted Darya Alexandrovna, and this time he kissed her hand.", "브론스키가 프랑스어로 안나에게 물었고, 대답도 기다리지 않은 채 다리야 알렉산드로브나에게 다시 한 번 인사하며 이번에는 그녀의 손에 입을 맞추었다."], ["“I think the big balcony room.”", "\"큰 발코니 방이 좋을 것 같은데요.\""]], [["“Oh, no, that’s too far off!", "\"오, 안 돼요, 거긴 너무 멀어요!"], ["Better in the corner room, we shall see each other more.", "모퉁이 방이 낫겠어요, 그래야 우리가 더 자주 볼 수 있죠."], ["Come, let’s go up,”", "자, 올라가요.\""], ["said Anna, as she gave her favorite horse the sugar the footman had brought her.", "안나는 하인이 가져다준 설탕을 자기가 좋아하는 말에게 주며 말했다."]], [["“Et vous oubliez votre devoir,”", "\"Pardon, j'en ai tout plein les poches.\""], ["she said to Veslovsky, who came out too on the steps.", "그는 미소를 지으며 조끼 주머니에 손가락을 넣고 대답했다."]], [["“Pardon, j’en ai tout plein les poches,” he answered, smiling, putting his fingers in his waistcoat pocket.", "* Pardon, j'en ai tout plein les poches: 프랑스어로 '미안합니다, 주머니에 가득 들어 있는걸요'."]], [["“Mais vous venez trop tard,”", "\"Mais vous venez trop tard.\""], ["she said, rubbing her handkerchief on her hand, which the horse had made wet in taking the sugar.", "그녀는 말이 설탕을 받아 먹으면서 침을 묻혀 놓은 자기 손을 손수건으로 문지르며 말했다."]], [["Anna turned to Dolly. “You can stay some time? For one day only? That’s impossible!”", "* Mais vous venez trop tard: 프랑스어로 '하지만 당신은 너무 늦게 왔어요'."]], [["“I promised to be back, and the children....” said Dolly, feeling embarrassed both because she had to get her bag out of the carriage, and because she knew her face must be covered with dust.", "안나가 돌리를 돌아보았다. \"좀 머물 수 있죠? 단 하루만요? 그건 안 돼요!\" \"돌아가겠다고 약속했고, 또 아이들도……\" 돌리는 마차에서 가방을 꺼내야 하는 데다, 자기 얼굴이 분명 먼지투성이일 거라는 걸 알기에 거북함을 느끼며 말했다."]], [["“No, Dolly, darling!...", "\"안 돼요, 돌리, 언니!……"], ["Well, we’ll see.", "뭐, 두고 보죠."], ["Come along, come along!”", "어서 와요, 어서요!\""], ["and Anna led Dolly to her room.", "그러고는 안나가 돌리를 자기 방으로 이끌었다."]], [["That room was not the smart guest chamber Vronsky had suggested, but the one of which Anna had said that Dolly would excuse it.", "그 방은 브론스키가 권했던 말쑥한 손님방이 아니라, 안나가 돌리더러 양해해 달라고 했던 바로 그 방이었다."], ["And this room, for which excuse was needed, was more full of luxury than any in which Dolly had ever stayed, a luxury that reminded her of the best hotels abroad.", "그런데 양해가 필요하다던 이 방은, 돌리가 그때껏 머물러 본 어느 방보다도 사치스러움으로 가득 차 있었으며, 그 사치스러움은 그녀에게 외국의 최고급 호텔들을 떠올리게 했다."]], [["“Well, darling, how happy I am!”", "\"자, 언니, 내가 얼마나 행복한지 몰라요!\""], ["Anna said, sitting down in her riding habit for a moment beside Dolly.", "안나가 승마복 차림 그대로 잠시 돌리 옆에 앉으며 말했다."], ["“Tell me about all of you.", "\"다들 어떻게 지내는지 들려줘요."], ["Stiva I had only a glimpse of, and he cannot tell one about the children.", "스티바는 잠깐 얼굴만 봤는데, 그이는 아이들 얘기를 제대로 들려주지 못해요."], ["How is my favorite, Tanya?", "내가 제일 좋아하는 타냐는 어때요?"], ["Quite a big girl, I expect?”", "이제 제법 큰 아가씨가 됐겠죠?\""]], [["“Yes, she’s very tall,”", "\"응, 키가 아주 크단다.\""], ["Darya Alexandrovna answered shortly, surprised herself that she should respond so coolly about her children.", "다리야 알렉산드로브나는 짤막하게 대답하면서, 자기가 아이들 얘기를 이토록 무덤덤하게 한다는 데에 스스로도 놀랐다."], ["“We are having a delightful stay at the Levins’,”", "\"우리는 레빈네 집에서 즐겁게 지내고 있어.\""], ["she added.", "그녀가 덧붙였다."]], [["“Oh, if I had known,”", "\"아, 진작 알았더라면,\""], ["said Anna, “that you do not despise me!...", "안나가 말했다, \"언니가 나를 경멸하지 않는다는 걸요!……"], ["You might have all come to us.", "다들 우리한테 왔으면 좋았을 텐데."], ["Stiva’s an old friend and a great friend of Alexey’s, you know,”", "알다시피 스티바는 알렉세이의 오랜 친구이자 둘도 없는 벗이거든요.\""], ["she added, and suddenly she blushed.", "그녀는 이렇게 덧붙이고는 갑자기 얼굴을 붉혔다."]], [["“Yes, but we are all....”", "\"그래, 하지만 우리는 다들……\""], ["Dolly answered in confusion.", "돌리가 당황하여 대답했다."]], [["“But in my delight I’m talking nonsense.", "\"하지만 내가 너무 기쁜 나머지 쓸데없는 소리를 하고 있네요."], ["The one thing, darling, is that I am so glad to have you!”", "언니, 한 가지 분명한 건, 언니가 와 줘서 내가 너무나 기쁘다는 거예요!\""], ["said Anna, kissing her again.", "안나가 그녀에게 다시 입을 맞추며 말했다."], ["“You haven’t told me yet how and what you think about me, and I keep wanting to know.", "\"언니는 아직 나를 어떻게 또 어떻게 생각하는지 말해 주지 않았는데, 난 자꾸만 그게 알고 싶어요."], ["But I’m glad you will see me as I am.", "하지만 언니가 나를 있는 그대로 봐 줄 거라 기뻐요."], ["The chief thing I shouldn’t like would be for people to imagine I want to prove anything.", "내가 제일 싫은 건 사람들이 내가 뭔가를 증명하고 싶어 한다고 상상하는 거예요. 난 아무것도 증명하고 싶지 않아요."], ["I don’t want to prove anything; I merely want to live, to do no one harm but myself.", "난 그저 살고 싶을 뿐이에요, 나 자신 말고는 누구에게도 해를 끼치지 않으면서요."], ["I have the right to do that, haven’t I?", "나한테 그럴 권리는 있는 거죠, 그렇지 않나요?"], ["But it is a big subject, and we’ll talk over everything properly later.", "하지만 그건 큰 이야기니까, 나중에 모든 걸 차근차근 얘기해요."], ["Now I’ll go and dress and send a maid to you.”", "이제 난 가서 옷을 갈아입고 언니한테 하녀를 보낼게요.\""]], [["# Chapter 19", "# Chapter 19"]], [["Left alone, Darya Alexandrovna, with a good housewife’s eye, scanned her room.", "혼자 남게 되자 다리아 알렉산드로브나는 살림 잘하는 안주인의 눈으로 자기 방을 찬찬히 둘러보았다."], ["All she had seen in entering the house and walking through it, and all she saw now in her room, gave her an impression of wealth and sumptuousness and of that modern European luxury of which she had only read in English novels, but had never seen in Russia and in the country.", "이 집에 들어서면서 본 것이며 집 안을 걸어 지나오며 본 모든 것, 그리고 지금 자기 방에서 보고 있는 모든 것이, 그녀에게 부유함과 호사스러움, 그리고 영국 소설에서나 읽었을 뿐 러시아에서는, 더구나 시골에서는 한 번도 본 적 없는 그 현대 유럽풍 사치의 인상을 안겨 주었다."], ["Everything was new from the new French hangings on the walls to the carpet which covered the whole floor.", "벽에 걸린 새 프랑스제 벽포에서부터 바닥 전체를 덮은 양탄자에 이르기까지 모든 것이 새것이었다."], ["The bed had a spring mattress, and a special sort of bolster and silk pillowcases on the little pillows.", "침대에는 스프링 매트리스가 깔려 있었고, 특별한 형태의 긴 베개와 작은 베개들에는 비단 베갯잇이 씌워져 있었다."], ["The marble washstand, the dressing table, the little sofa, the tables, the bronze clock on the chimney piece, the window curtains, and the portières were all new and expensive.", "대리석 세면대, 화장대, 작은 소파, 탁자들, 벽난로 위의 청동 시계, 창문 커튼, 그리고 portières까지 모두 새것이고 값비싼 것들이었다."]], [["The smart maid, who came in to offer her services, with her hair done up high, and a gown more fashionable than Dolly’s, was as new and expensive as the whole room.", "머리를 높이 틀어 올리고 돌리의 것보다 더 멋스러운 가운을 입은 채 시중을 들겠다며 들어온 맵시 있는 하녀도, 방 전체와 마찬가지로 새것이고 값비싼 존재였다."], ["Darya Alexandrovna liked her neatness, her deferential and obliging manners, but she felt ill at ease with her.", "다리아 알렉산드로브나는 그녀의 단정함과 공손하고 사근사근한 태도가 마음에 들었지만, 그녀 앞에서는 어쩐지 마음이 편치 않았다."], ["She felt ashamed of her seeing the patched dressing jacket that had unluckily been packed by mistake for her.", "그녀는 하녀가, 그만 실수로 잘못 챙겨 넣은 기운 화장 재킷을 보는 것이 부끄러웠다."], ["She was ashamed of the very patches and darned places of which she had been so proud at home.", "집에서는 그토록 자랑스러워하던 바로 그 기운 자리며 꿰맨 데가 부끄러웠다."], ["At home it had been so clear that for six dressing jackets there would be needed twenty-four yards of nainsook at sixteen pence the yard, which was a matter of thirty shillings besides the cutting-out and making, and these thirty shillings had been saved.", "집에서는 화장 재킷 여섯 벌에 야드당 십육 펜스짜리 면포 스물네 야드가 필요하고, 그것이 재단과 바느질 값을 빼고도 삼십 실링이나 된다는 것이 너무도 분명했으며, 그 삼십 실링을 아껴 두었던 것이다."], ["But before the maid she felt, if not exactly ashamed, at least uncomfortable.", "그러나 하녀 앞에서 그녀는 부끄럽다고까지는 할 수 없어도 적어도 거북함을 느꼈다."]], [["Darya Alexandrovna had a great sense of relief when Annushka, whom she had known for years, walked in.", "다리아 알렉산드로브나는 여러 해 전부터 알고 지내던 안누슈카가 들어오자 크게 안도했다."], ["The smart maid was sent for to go to her mistress, and Annushka remained with Darya Alexandrovna.", "맵시 있는 하녀는 주인마님에게 가라고 불려 갔고, 안누슈카가 다리아 알렉산드로브나 곁에 남았다."]], [["Annushka was obviously much pleased at that lady’s arrival, and began to chatter away without a pause.", "안누슈카는 그 부인이 온 것이 무척이나 기쁜 모양이었고, 쉬지 않고 재잘거리기 시작했다."], ["Dolly observed that she was longing to express her opinion in regard to her mistress’s position, especially as to the love and devotion of the count to Anna Arkadyevna, but Dolly carefully interrupted her whenever she began to speak about this.", "돌리는 그녀가 자기 주인마님의 처지에 대해, 특히 백작이 안나 아르카지예브나에게 보이는 사랑과 헌신에 대해 의견을 토로하고 싶어 안달이라는 것을 알아챘지만, 그녀가 그 이야기를 꺼내려 들 때마다 조심스럽게 말을 가로막았다."]], [["“I grew up with Anna Arkadyevna; my lady’s dearer to me than anything.", "\"전 안나 아르카지예브나 마님과 함께 자랐답니다. 제겐 마님이 무엇보다 소중한 분이지요."], ["Well, it’s not for us to judge.", "뭐, 저희가 이러쿵저러쿵 판단할 일은 아니지만요."], ["And, to be sure, there seems so much love....”", "그래도 정말이지, 그토록 사랑이 깊어 보이는 게…….\""]], [["“Kindly pour out the water for me to wash now, please,”", "\"수고스럽지만 이제 씻게 물 좀 부어 줘요.\""], ["Darya Alexandrovna cut her short.", "다리아 알렉산드로브나가 그녀의 말을 잘랐다."]], [["“Certainly.", "\"그럼요."], ["We’ve two women kept specially for washing small things, but most of the linen’s done by machinery.", "자잘한 것들 빠는 일만 따로 맡은 여자가 둘이나 있답니다만, 빨래는 거의 다 기계로 해요."], ["The count goes into everything himself.", "백작님은 무슨 일이든 직접 손수 챙기시죠."], ["Ah, what a husband!...”", "아, 정말 대단한 남편이세요!…….\""]], [["Dolly was glad when Anna came in, and by her entrance put a stop to Annushka’s gossip.", "돌리는 안나가 들어와 그 등장으로 안누슈카의 수다를 멈추게 해 주자 다행스러웠다."]], [["Anna had put on a very simple batiste gown.", "안나는 아주 수수한 옥양목 가운을 입고 있었다."], ["Dolly scrutinized that simple gown attentively.", "돌리는 그 수수한 가운을 유심히 뜯어보았다."], ["She knew what it meant, and the price at which such simplicity was obtained.", "그녀는 그것이 무엇을 의미하는지, 그런 수수함이 어떤 값을 치르고 얻어지는지 알고 있었다."]], [["“An old friend,”", "\"오랜 친구예요.\""], ["said Anna of Annushka.", "안나가 안누슈카를 두고 말했다."]], [["Anna was not embarrassed now.", "이제 안나는 당황하지 않았다."], ["She was perfectly composed and at ease.", "그녀는 완벽하게 차분하고 편안했다."], ["Dolly saw that she had now completely recovered from the impression her arrival had made on her, and had assumed that superficial, careless tone which, as it were, closed the door on that compartment in which her deeper feelings and ideas were kept.", "돌리는 그녀가 자기가 도착했을 때 받았던 인상에서 이제 완전히 벗어났으며, 마치 자기의 더 깊은 감정과 생각이 담긴 칸막이의 문을 닫아 버리기라도 하듯, 그 피상적이고 무심한 어조를 취했다는 것을 알아챘다."]], [["“Well, Anna, and how is your little girl?”", "\"그래, 안나, 어린 딸은 어때요?\""], ["asked Dolly.", "돌리가 물었다."]], [["“Annie?”", "\"애니 말이에요?\""], ["(This was what she called her little daughter Anna.) “Very well.", "(그녀는 어린 딸 안나를 이렇게 불렀다.) \"아주 잘 있어요."], ["She has got on wonderfully.", "놀랄 만큼 잘 자랐답니다."], ["Would you like to see her?", "보고 싶어요? 자, 보여 줄게요."], ["Come, I’ll show her to you. We had a terrible bother,”", "유모들 때문에 어찌나 골치가 아팠는지 몰라요.\""], ["she began telling her, “over nurses.", "그녀가 이야기를 시작했다."], ["We had an Italian wet-nurse.", "\"이탈리아 유모를 두었었죠."], ["A good creature, but so stupid!", "사람은 착한데, 어찌나 멍청하던지!"], ["We wanted to get rid of her, but the baby is so used to her that we’ve gone on keeping her still.”", "내보내려고 했지만, 애가 워낙 그 여자한테 익숙해져서 여태 데리고 있어요.\""]], [["“But how have you managed?...”", "\"하지만 어떻게 한 거예요?……\""], ["Dolly was beginning a question as to what name the little girl would have; but noticing a sudden frown on Anna’s face, she changed the drift of her question.", "돌리는 그 작은 여자아이가 어떤 성을 갖게 될지 물어보려고 했다. 그러나 안나의 얼굴에 별안간 떠오른 찌푸림을 알아차리고는, 질문의 방향을 바꾸었다."]], [["“How did you manage?", "\"어떻게 한 거예요?"], ["have you weaned her yet?”", "벌써 젖을 뗐나요?\""]], [["But Anna had understood.", "하지만 안나는 이미 알아들었다."]], [["“You didn’t mean to ask that?", "\"그걸 물어보려던 건 아니죠?"], ["You meant to ask about her surname.", "아이의 성을 물어보려던 거였죠. 그렇죠?"], ["Yes? That worries Alexey.", "그게 알렉세이를 괴롭히고 있어요."], ["She has no name—that is, she’s a Karenina,”", "아이에겐 성이 없어요—그러니까, 카레니나죠.\""], ["said Anna, dropping her eyelids till nothing could be seen but the eyelashes meeting.", "안나는 눈꺼풀을 내리깔아 마주 닿은 속눈썹밖에 보이지 않을 정도가 되도록 했다."], ["“But we’ll talk about all that later,”", "\"하지만 그건 나중에 다 이야기해요.\""], ["her face suddenly brightening.", "그녀의 얼굴이 갑자기 환해졌다."], ["“Come, I’ll show you her.", "\"자, 보여 줄게요."], ["Elle est très gentille.", "Elle est très gentille."], ["She crawls now.”", "이제 기어 다닌답니다.\""]], [["In the nursery the luxury which had impressed Dolly in the whole house struck her still more.", "* Elle est très gentille: 프랑스어로 '아이가 무척 사랑스러워요'."], ["There were little go-carts ordered from England, and appliances for learning to walk, and a sofa after the fashion of a billiard table, purposely constructed for crawling, and swings and baths, all of special pattern, and modern.", "아이 방에서는 온 집 안에서 돌리를 압도했던 호화로움이 그녀를 한층 더 강하게 사로잡았다. 영국에서 주문한 작은 유모차들이며, 걸음마를 배우는 기구들이며, 일부러 기어 다니기 좋게 만든 당구대 모양의 소파며, 그네며 욕조며, 모두가 특별한 양식의 현대식 물건들이었다."], ["They were all English, solid, and of good make, and obviously very expensive.", "죄다 영국제에다 튼튼하고 솜씨 좋게 만든 것이어서, 누가 봐도 무척 값비싼 것들이었다."], ["The room was large, and very light and lofty.", "방은 널찍하고, 아주 환하고 천장이 높았다."]], [["When they went in, the baby, with nothing on but her little smock, was sitting in a little elbow chair at the table, having her dinner of broth, which she was spilling all over her little chest.", "그들이 들어갔을 때, 아기는 조그만 속옷 하나만 걸친 채 식탁 앞 작은 팔걸이의자에 앉아, 죽을 먹고 있었는데 그것을 자기 작은 가슴팍에 온통 흘리고 있었다."], ["The baby was being fed, and the Russian nursery maid was evidently sharing her meal.", "아기에게 음식을 먹이는 중이었고, 러시아인 보모는 누가 봐도 아기의 식사를 같이 나눠 먹고 있었다."], ["Neither the wet-nurse nor the head-nurse were there; they were in the next room, from which came the sound of their conversation in the queer French which was their only means of communication.", "유모도 우두머리 보모도 거기에는 없었다. 그들은 옆방에 있었고, 거기서 그들이 서로 소통할 유일한 수단인 그 기묘한 프랑스어로 나누는 대화 소리가 들려왔다."]], [["Hearing Anna’s voice, a smart, tall, English nurse with a disagreeable face and a dissolute expression walked in at the door, hurriedly shaking her fair curls, and immediately began to defend herself though Anna had not found fault with her.", "안나의 목소리를 들은 키 크고 말쑥한 영국인 보모가, 불쾌한 얼굴에 방탕한 표정을 짓고는 금발 곱슬머리를 황급히 흔들며 문으로 걸어 들어왔고, 안나가 나무라지도 않았는데 곧장 자기변명을 늘어놓기 시작했다."], ["At every word Anna said, the English nurse said hurriedly several times, “Yes, my lady.”", "안나가 한마디 할 때마다, 그 영국인 보모는 \"예, 마님\" 하고 황급히 몇 번이고 되풀이했다."]], [["The rosy baby with her black eyebrows and hair, her sturdy red little body with tight goose-flesh skin, delighted Darya Alexandrovna in spite of the cross expression with which she stared at the stranger.", "검은 눈썹에 검은 머리카락을 가진 발그레한 아기는, 통통하고 불그스레한 작은 몸뚱이에 살갗이 닭살처럼 팽팽하게 돋아 있었는데, 낯선 사람을 노려보는 그 뾰로통한 표정에도 불구하고 다리야 알렉산드로브나의 마음을 사로잡았다."], ["She positively envied the baby’s healthy appearance.", "그녀는 아기의 건강한 모습이 정말로 부러웠다."], ["She was delighted, too, at the baby’s crawling.", "아기가 기어 다니는 모습도 마음에 쏙 들었다."], ["Not one of her own children had crawled like that.", "그녀 자신의 아이들 가운데는 누구도 저렇게 기어 다닌 적이 없었다."], ["When the baby was put on the carpet and its little dress tucked up behind, it was wonderfully charming.", "아기를 양탄자 위에 내려놓고 작은 치맛자락을 뒤로 걷어 올려 주자, 그 모습이 놀랄 만큼 사랑스러웠다."], ["Looking round like some little wild animal at the grown-up big people with her bright black eyes, she smiled, unmistakably pleased at their admiring her, and holding her legs sideways, she pressed vigorously on her arms, and rapidly drew her whole back up after, and then made another step forward with her little arms.", "어떤 작은 들짐승처럼 반짝이는 검은 눈으로 어른인 큰 사람들을 둘러보던 아기는, 그들이 자기를 보고 감탄하는 것이 기쁜 게 틀림없이 방긋 웃고는, 다리를 옆으로 짚고 두 팔에 힘껏 힘을 주어 등 전체를 잽싸게 끌어당긴 다음, 작은 팔로 또 한 걸음을 앞으로 내디뎠다."]], [["But the whole atmosphere of the nursery, and especially the English nurse, Darya Alexandrovna did not like at all.", "하지만 아이 방의 전반적인 분위기, 특히 그 영국인 보모가 다리야 알렉산드로브나는 전혀 마음에 들지 않았다."], ["It was only on the supposition that no good nurse would have entered so irregular a household as Anna’s that Darya Alexandrovna could explain to herself how Anna with her insight into people could take such an unprepossessing, disreputable-looking woman as nurse to her child.", "사람을 꿰뚫어 보는 안나가 어떻게 저토록 호감 안 가고 평판 나빠 보이는 여자를 제 아이의 보모로 둘 수 있었는지, 다리야 알렉산드로브나는 안나처럼 어수선한 가정에는 변변한 보모라면 들어오려 하지 않았으리라는 추측으로만 스스로에게 설명할 수 있었다."]], [["Besides, from a few words that were dropped, Darya Alexandrovna saw at once that Anna, the two nurses, and the child had no common existence, and that the mother’s visit was something exceptional.", "게다가 몇 마디 흘러나온 말로 미루어, 다리야 알렉산드로브나는 안나와 두 보모와 아이 사이에 공통된 삶이라는 것이 없고, 어머니의 방문이 무언가 예외적인 일이라는 것을 단번에 알아챘다."], ["Anna wanted to get the baby her plaything, and could not find it.", "안나는 아기에게 장난감을 가져다주려 했으나, 그것을 찾지 못했다."]], [["Most amazing of all was the fact that on being asked how many teeth the baby had, Anna answered wrong, and knew nothing about the two last teeth.", "무엇보다 놀라운 것은, 아기가 이가 몇 개 났느냐는 물음에 안나가 틀리게 대답했고, 마지막에 난 두 개에 대해서는 아무것도 모르고 있었다는 사실이었다."]], [["“I sometimes feel sorry I’m so superfluous here,”", "\"가끔은 내가 여기서 이렇게 군더더기 같은 존재라는 게 미안해져요.\""], ["said Anna, going out of the nursery and holding up her skirt so as to escape the plaything standing in the doorway.", "안나는 아이 방을 나서면서, 문간에 놓인 장난감을 피하느라 치맛자락을 들어 올린 채 말했다."], ["“It was very different with my first child.”", "\"첫째 아이 때는 전혀 달랐는데.\""]], [["“I expected it to be the other way,”", "\"저는 반대일 거라고 생각했어요.\""], ["said Darya Alexandrovna shyly.", "다리야 알렉산드로브나가 수줍게 말했다."]], [["“Oh, no!", "\"오, 아니에요!"], ["By the way, do you know I saw Seryozha?”", "그런데 말이에요, 내가 세료자를 봤다는 거 알아요?\""], ["said Anna, screwing up her eyes, as though looking at something far away.", "안나는 마치 멀리 있는 무언가를 바라보듯 눈을 가늘게 뜨며 말했다."], ["“But we’ll talk about that later.", "\"하지만 그건 나중에 이야기해요."], ["You wouldn’t believe it, I’m like a hungry beggar woman when a full dinner is set before her, and she does not know what to begin on first.", "믿기지 않겠지만, 나는 마치 굶주린 거지 여자 앞에 푸짐한 만찬이 차려졌는데 무엇부터 손대야 할지 모르는 꼴이에요. 그 만찬이 바로 당신이고, 또 당신과 나눌 이야기들이에요."], ["The dinner is you, and the talks I have before me with you, which I could never have with anyone else; and I don’t know which subject to begin upon first.", "다른 누구와도 결코 나눌 수 없는 그런 이야기들 말이에요. 그러니 어떤 화제부터 먼저 꺼내야 할지 모르겠어요."], ["Mais je ne vous ferai grâce de rien.", "Mais je ne vous ferai grâce de rien."], ["I must have everything out with you.”", "당신에게 전부 다 털어놓아야겠어요.\" * Mais je ne vous ferai grâce de rien: 프랑스어로 '하지만 무엇 하나 빼놓지 않을 거예요'."]], [["“Oh, I ought to give you a sketch of the company you will meet with us,”", "\"아, 우리 집에서 만나게 될 사람들을 대충 그려 줘야겠네요.\""], ["she went on.", "그녀가 말을 이었다."], ["“I’ll begin with the ladies.", "\"여자들부터 시작할게요."], ["Princess Varvara—you know her, and I know your opinion and Stiva’s about her.", "바르바라 공작 영애—그 사람은 당신도 알고, 그 사람에 대한 당신과 스티바의 생각도 내가 알아요."], ["Stiva says the whole aim of her existence is to prove her superiority over Auntie Katerina Pavlovna: that’s all true; but she’s a good-natured woman, and I am so grateful to her.", "스티바는 그 사람이 사는 목적 전부가 카체리나 파블로브나 이모보다 자기가 우월하다는 걸 증명하는 데 있다고 말하죠. 그건 다 사실이에요."], ["In Petersburg there was a moment when a chaperon was absolutely essential for me.", "하지만 사람은 마음씨가 좋고, 나는 그 사람에게 무척 고마워하고 있어요. 페테르부르크에서는 나에게 보호자가 절대적으로 필요한 순간이 있었어요."], ["Then she turned up.", "그때 그 사람이 나타났죠."], ["But really she is good-natured.", "하지만 정말로 마음씨가 좋은 사람이에요."], ["She did a great deal to alleviate my position.", "내 처지를 누그러뜨리는 데 큰 도움을 줬어요."], ["I see you don’t understand all the difficulty of my position ...", "당신은 내 처지의 그 모든 어려움을 다 이해하지 못하는 것 같네요……"], ["there in Petersburg,”", "거기 페테르부르크에서요.\""], ["she added.", "그녀가 덧붙였다."], ["“Here I’m perfectly at ease and happy.", "\"여기서 나는 더없이 편안하고 행복해요."], ["Well, of that later on, though.", "뭐, 그것도 나중에 이야기해요."], ["Then Sviazhsky—he’s the marshal of the district, and he’s a very good sort of a man, but he wants to get something out of Alexey.", "그리고 스비아시스키—그 사람은 군의 귀족회장이고, 아주 좋은 사람인데, 알렉세이에게서 뭔가 얻어 내고 싶어 해요."], ["You understand, with his property, now that we are settled in the country, Alexey can exercise great influence.", "알다시피, 우리가 이제 시골에 자리를 잡았으니, 그 사람의 영지를 생각하면 알렉세이가 큰 영향력을 행사할 수 있거든요."], ["Then there’s Tushkevitch—you have seen him, you know—Betsy’s admirer.", "그리고 투시케비치—당신도 그 사람을 본 적 있죠, 알잖아요—베치를 흠모하던 사람이에요."], ["Now he’s been thrown over and he’s come to see us.", "이제는 차였고, 그래서 우리를 찾아왔어요."], ["As Alexey says, he’s one of those people who are very pleasant if one accepts them for what they try to appear to be, et puis il est comme il faut, as Princess Varvara says.", "알렉세이 말로는, 그는 보이려고 애쓰는 그대로 받아들여 주면 아주 유쾌한 그런 부류의 사람이에요. 그리고 et puis il est comme il faut, 바르바라 공작 영애가 하는 말이죠."], ["Then Veslovsky ...", "그리고 베슬로프스키……"], ["you know him.", "당신도 그 사람을 알아요."], ["A very nice boy,”", "아주 괜찮은 청년이죠.\""], ["she said, and a sly smile curved her lips.", "그녀가 말하자, 짓궂은 미소가 그녀의 입가를 휘게 했다."], ["“What’s this wild story about him and the Levins?", "\"그 사람과 레빈 부부 사이의 그 황당한 이야기는 도대체 뭐예요?"], ["Veslovsky told Alexey about it, and we don’t believe it.", "베슬로프스키가 알렉세이에게 그 이야기를 했는데, 우리는 믿지 않아요."], ["Il est très gentil et naïf,”", "Il est très gentil et naïf.\""], ["she said again with the same smile.", "그녀가 똑같은 미소를 지으며 다시 말했다."], ["“Men need occupation, and Alexey needs a circle, so I value all these people.", "\"남자들에게는 할 일이 필요하고, 알렉세이에게는 어울릴 사람들이 필요해요. 그래서 나는 이 사람들을 다 소중히 여기죠."], ["We have to have the house lively and gay, so that Alexey may not long for any novelty.", "집을 활기차고 즐겁게 만들어야, 알렉세이가 무슨 색다른 것을 갈망하지 않을 테니까요. 그리고 집사도 보게 될 거예요—독일 사람인데, 아주 좋은 친구이고, 자기 일을 잘 알아요."], ["Then you’ll see the steward—a German, a very good fellow, and he understands his work.", "알렉세이가 그 사람을 아주 높이 평가하죠. 그리고 의사도 있는데, 젊은 사람이고, 아마 완전한 니힐리스트까지는 아니겠지만, 알다시피, 칼로 음식을 찍어 먹는 그런 부류죠……"], ["Alexey has a very high opinion of him.", "그래도 아주 좋은 의사예요. 그리고 건축가도……"], ["Then the doctor, a young man, not quite a Nihilist perhaps, but you know, eats with his knife ...", "Une petite cour!\" * et puis il est comme il faut: 프랑스어로 '게다가 그 사람은 점잖아요'."], ["but a very good doctor.", "* Il est très gentil et naïf: 프랑스어로 '그 사람은 무척 상냥하고 순진해요'."], ["Then the architect.... Une petite cour!”", "* Une petite cour!: 프랑스어로 '하나의 작은 궁정이죠!'."]], [["# Chapter 20", "# Chapter 20"]], [["“Here’s Dolly for you, princess, you were so anxious to see her,”", "바르바라 공작 부인은 그늘 속에서 자수틀 앞에 앉아 알렉세이 키릴로비치 백작의 안락의자에 씌울 덮개를 만들고 있었는데, 안나가 다리아 알렉산드로브나를 데리고 그 돌 테라스로 나오며 말했다."], ["said Anna, coming out with Darya Alexandrovna onto the stone terrace where Princess Varvara was sitting in the shade at an embroidery frame, working at a cover for Count Alexey Kirillovitch’s easy chair.", "\"공작 부인, 그토록 보고 싶어 하시던 돌리를 모시고 왔어요."], ["“She says she doesn’t want anything before dinner, but please order some lunch for her, and I’ll go and look for Alexey and bring them all in.”", "식사 전에는 아무것도 필요 없다고 하지만, 그래도 점심을 좀 차리라고 일러 주세요. 저는 알렉세이를 찾으러 가서 다들 데려올게요.\""]], [["Princess Varvara gave Dolly a cordial and rather patronizing reception, and began at once explaining to her that she was living with Anna because she had always cared more for her than her sister Katerina Pavlovna, the aunt that had brought Anna up, and that now, when everyone had abandoned Anna, she thought it her duty to help her in this most difficult period of transition.", "바르바라 공작 부인은 돌리를 다정하면서도 다소 생색을 내는 태도로 맞이하더니, 곧바로 자기가 안나와 함께 사는 까닭을 설명하기 시작했다. 안나를 키워 준 이모 카체리나 파블로브나보다 늘 안나를 더 아껴 왔으며, 모두가 안나를 저버린 지금 이 가장 힘든 과도기에 안나를 돕는 것이 자신의 의무라고 여긴다는 것이었다."]], [["“Her husband will give her a divorce, and then I shall go back to my solitude; but now I can be of use, and I am doing my duty, however difficult it may be for me—not like some other people.", "\"남편이 이혼을 해 줄 테고, 그러면 나는 다시 내 고적한 생활로 돌아갈 거예요. 하지만 지금은 내가 쓸모가 있고, 또 나는 내 의무를 다하고 있죠, 그게 아무리 힘들지라도 말이에요—다른 어떤 사람들과는 다르게요."], ["And how sweet it is of you, how right of you to have come!", "그리고 와 주다니 얼마나 고마운지, 얼마나 잘한 일인지!"], ["They live like the best of married couples; it’s for God to judge them, not for us.", "저 두 사람은 더할 나위 없이 훌륭한 부부처럼 살고 있어요. 저들을 심판하는 건 하느님의 몫이지 우리 몫이 아니에요."], ["And didn’t Biryuzovsky and Madame Avenieva ...", "비류조프스키와 아베니예바 부인도 그랬잖아요……"], ["and Sam Nikandrov, and Vassiliev and Madame Mamonova, and Liza Neptunova....", "그리고 삼 니칸드로프와, 바실리예프와 마모노바 부인, 또 리자 넵투노바도…… 그들에 대해서는 아무도 뭐라 하지 않았던가요?"], ["Did no one say anything about them?", "결국엔 모두에게 받아들여지는 걸로 끝났잖아요."], ["And it has ended by their being received by everyone.", "게다가 c’est un intérieur si joli, si comme il faut."], ["And then, c’est un intérieur si joli, si comme il faut.", "Tout-à-fait à l’anglaise."], ["Tout-à-fait à l’anglaise. On se réunit le matin au breakfast, et puis on se sépare.", "On se réunit le matin au breakfast, et puis on se sépare."], ["Everyone does as he pleases till dinner time.", "식사 시간 전까지는 다들 제멋대로 지내요."], ["Dinner at seven o’clock.", "만찬은 일곱 시고요."], ["Stiva did very rightly to send you.", "스티바가 당신을 보낸 건 아주 옳은 일이었어요."], ["He needs their support.", "그이는 저 두 사람의 지지가 필요하니까요."], ["You know that through his mother and brother he can do anything.", "알다시피 그이는 어머니와 형을 통해 무슨 일이든 할 수 있잖아요."], ["And then they do so much good.", "게다가 저 두 사람은 좋은 일도 참 많이 해요."], ["He didn’t tell you about his hospital?", "그이가 병원 얘기는 하지 않던가요?"], ["Ce sera admirable—everything from Paris.”", "Ce sera admirable—전부 파리에서 들여온 거예요.\""]], [["Their conversation was interrupted by Anna, who had found the men of the party in the billiard room, and returned with them to the terrace.", "두 사람의 대화는 안나에 의해 중단되었다. 안나는 당구실에서 일행의 남자들을 찾아내 그들을 데리고 테라스로 돌아온 것이었다."], ["There was still a long time before the dinner-hour, it was exquisite weather, and so several different methods of spending the next two hours were proposed.", "만찬 시간까지는 아직 한참 남아 있었고, 날씨도 더없이 좋아서 다음 두 시간을 보낼 여러 가지 방법이 제안되었다."], ["There were very many methods of passing the time at Vozdvizhenskoe, and these were all unlike those in use at Pokrovskoe.", "보즈드비젠스코예에는 시간을 보내는 방법이 매우 많았는데, 그것들은 모두 포크로프스코예에서 쓰던 방법과는 달랐다."]], [["“Une partie de lawn-tennis,”", "\"Une partie de lawn-tennis,\""], ["Veslovsky proposed, with his handsome smile.", "베슬로프스키가 그 잘생긴 미소를 지으며 제안했다."], ["“We’ll be partners again, Anna Arkadyevna.”", "\"안나 아르카지예브나, 우리 또 짝을 합시다.\""]], [["“No, it’s too hot; better stroll about the garden and have a row in the boat, show Darya Alexandrovna the river banks.”", "\"안 돼요, 너무 더워요. 차라리 정원을 거닐다가 보트를 타는 게 낫겠어요."], ["Vronsky proposed.", "다리아 알렉산드로브나에게 강기슭을 보여 드리자고요.\" 브론스키가 제안했다."]], [["“I agree to anything,”", "\"저는 뭐든 좋습니다.\""], ["said Sviazhsky.", "스비야시스키가 말했다."]], [["“I imagine that what Dolly would like best would be a stroll—wouldn’t you?", "\"돌리가 제일 좋아할 만한 건 산책일 것 같은데—그렇지 않아?"], ["And then the boat, perhaps,”", "그러고 나서 보트를 타는 거지, 어쩌면.\""], ["said Anna.", "안나가 말했다."]], [["So it was decided.", "그렇게 정해졌다."], ["Veslovsky and Tushkevitch went off to the bathing place, promising to get the boat ready and to wait there for them.", "베슬로프스키와 투시케비치는 보트를 준비해 놓고 거기서 기다리겠다고 약속하며 수영장으로 떠났다."]], [["They walked along the path in two couples, Anna with Sviazhsky, and Dolly with Vronsky.", "그들은 두 쌍으로 나뉘어 오솔길을 따라 걸었는데, 안나는 스비야시스키와, 돌리는 브론스키와 함께였다."], ["Dolly was a little embarrassed and anxious in the new surroundings in which she found herself.", "돌리는 자신이 놓인 이 새로운 환경 속에서 조금 거북하고 불안했다."], ["Abstractly, theoretically, she did not merely justify, she positively approved of Anna’s conduct.", "추상적으로, 이론적으로 그녀는 안나의 처신을 정당하다고 여길 뿐 아니라 진정으로 찬성하고 있었다."], ["As is indeed not unfrequent with women of unimpeachable virtue, weary of the monotony of respectable existence, at a distance she not only excused illicit love, she positively envied it.", "흠잡을 데 없이 정숙한 여인들, 점잖은 생활의 단조로움에 지친 여인들에게 흔히 그러하듯이, 그녀도 멀찍이 떨어진 자리에서는 불륜의 사랑을 너그러이 봐줄 뿐 아니라 오히려 진심으로 부러워했다."], ["Besides, she loved Anna with all her heart.", "게다가 그녀는 안나를 진심으로 사랑했다."], ["But seeing Anna in actual life among these strangers, with this fashionable tone that was so new to Darya Alexandrovna, she felt ill at ease.", "하지만 이 낯선 사람들 틈에서, 다리아 알렉산드로브나에게는 너무도 생소한 이 세련된 분위기 속에서 실제로 살아가는 안나를 보고 있자니 마음이 편치 않았다."], ["What she disliked particularly was seeing Princess Varvara ready to overlook everything for the sake of the comforts she enjoyed.", "그녀가 특히 못마땅했던 것은, 바르바라 공작 부인이 자기가 누리는 편안함을 위해 모든 것을 못 본 척할 태세라는 점이었다."]], [["As a general principle, abstractly, Dolly approved of Anna’s action; but to see the man for whose sake her action had been taken was disagreeable to her.", "일반적인 원칙으로서, 추상적으로는 돌리도 안나의 행동을 찬성했다. 그러나 안나가 그런 행동을 한 상대인 그 남자를 직접 보는 것은 그녀로서는 거북한 일이었다."], ["Moreover, she had never liked Vronsky.", "더구나 그녀는 한 번도 브론스키를 좋아한 적이 없었다."], ["She thought him very proud, and saw nothing in him of which he could be proud except his wealth.", "그녀는 그가 무척 거만하다고 여겼고, 그에게서 자랑할 만한 것이라곤 재산 말고는 아무것도 찾아볼 수 없다고 생각했다."], ["But against her own will, here in his own house, he overawed her more than ever, and she could not be at ease with him.", "그런데 본인의 뜻과는 달리, 그의 집인 이곳에서 그는 그 어느 때보다도 그녀를 주눅 들게 했고, 그녀는 그와 함께 있는 것이 편하지 않았다."], ["She felt with him the same feeling she had had with the maid about her dressing jacket.", "그녀가 그와 함께 있으면서 느낀 감정은 자기 화장 가운을 두고 하녀에게 느꼈던 것과 똑같았다."], ["Just as with the maid she had felt not exactly ashamed, but embarrassed at her darns, so she felt with him not exactly ashamed, but embarrassed at herself.", "하녀 앞에서 정확히 부끄럽다기보다 기운 자국 때문에 거북했던 것처럼, 그녀는 그 앞에서 정확히 부끄럽다기보다 자기 자신이 거북했다."]], [["Dolly was ill at ease, and tried to find a subject of conversation.", "돌리는 마음이 편치 않아 대화의 화제를 찾으려 애썼다."], ["Even though she supposed that, through his pride, praise of his house and garden would be sure to be disagreeable to him, she did all the same tell him how much she liked his house.", "그의 거만함을 생각하면 집과 정원에 대한 칭찬이 그에게 분명 거북하게 들리리라고 짐작하면서도, 그녀는 그래도 자기가 그의 집을 얼마나 마음에 들어 하는지 말해 주었다."]], [["“Yes, it’s a very fine building, and in the good old-fashioned style,”", "\"네, 아주 훌륭한 건물이죠. 멋스러운 옛날식이고요.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“I like so much the court in front of the steps.", "\"계단 앞의 안마당이 정말 마음에 들어요."], ["Was that always so?”", "늘 저랬나요?\""]], [["“Oh, no!”", "\"오, 아니에요!\""], ["he said, and his face beamed with pleasure.", "그가 말했고, 그의 얼굴은 기쁨으로 환하게 빛났다."], ["“If you could only have seen that court last spring!”", "\"지난봄에 저 안마당을 보셨더라면!\""]], [["And he began, at first rather diffidently, but more and more carried away by the subject as he went on, to draw her attention to the various details of the decoration of his house and garden.", "그러고는 처음에는 다소 머뭇거리며, 그러나 이야기를 이어 갈수록 점점 더 화제에 빠져들면서, 그는 그녀에게 집과 정원의 장식에 관한 갖가지 세부를 일러 주기 시작했다."], ["It was evident that, having devoted a great deal of trouble to improve and beautify his home, Vronsky felt a need to show off the improvements to a new person, and was genuinely delighted at Darya Alexandrovna’s praise.", "자기 집을 개량하고 아름답게 꾸미는 데 많은 공을 들인 만큼, 브론스키는 그 성과를 새로운 사람에게 자랑하고 싶은 욕구를 느꼈으며, 다리아 알렉산드로브나의 칭찬에 진심으로 기뻐하는 것이 분명했다."]], [["“If you would care to look at the hospital, and are not tired, indeed, it’s not far.", "\"병원을 한번 보고 싶으시면, 그리고 피곤하지 않으시면, 사실 멀지도 않으니까요."], ["Shall we go?”", "가 보실까요?\""], ["he said, glancing into her face to convince himself that she was not bored.", "그는 그녀가 지루해하지 않는지 확인하려고 그녀의 얼굴을 들여다보며 말했다."], ["“Are you coming, Anna?”", "\"안나, 당신도 갈래?\""], ["he turned to her.", "그가 그녀를 돌아보았다."]], [["“We will come, won’t we?”", "\"우리도 가야죠, 그렇죠?\""], ["she said, addressing Sviazhsky.", "그녀가 스비야시스키를 향해 말했다."], ["“Mais il ne faut pas laisser le pauvre Veslovsky et Tushkevitch se morfondre là dans le bateau.", "\"Mais il ne faut pas laisser le pauvre Veslovsky et Tushkevitch se morfondre là dans le bateau."], ["We must send and tell them.”", "사람을 보내서 알려 줘야겠어요.\""]], [["“Yes, this is a monument he is setting up here,”", "\"네, 이게 그이가 여기에 세우고 있는 기념비예요.\""], ["said Anna, turning to Dolly with that sly smile of comprehension with which she had previously talked about the hospital.", "안나가 앞서 병원 이야기를 할 때 지었던, 모든 걸 다 안다는 듯한 그 짓궂은 미소를 띤 채 돌리를 향해 말했다."]], [["“Oh, it’s a work of real importance!”", "\"오, 정말로 중요한 일이죠!\""], ["said Sviazhsky.", "스비야시스키가 말했다."], ["But to show he was not trying to ingratiate himself with Vronsky, he promptly added some slightly critical remarks.", "하지만 자기가 브론스키에게 비위를 맞추려는 게 아님을 보이려고 그는 곧바로 다소 비판적인 말을 덧붙였다."]], [["“I wonder, though, count,”", "\"그런데 말입니다, 백작.\""], ["he said, “that while you do so much for the health of the peasants, you take so little interest in the schools.”", "그가 말했다. \"농민들의 건강을 위해서는 그렇게 많은 일을 하시면서, 학교에는 그토록 관심이 적으시다니 의아하군요.\""]], [["“C’est devenu tellement commun les écoles,”", "\"C’est devenu tellement commun les écoles,\""], ["said Vronsky.", "브론스키가 말했다."], ["“You understand it’s not on that account, but it just happens so, my interest has been diverted elsewhere.", "\"아시다시피 그 때문은 아니고, 그저 어쩌다 보니 그렇게 됐을 뿐이에요. 제 관심이 다른 데로 쏠려 버린 거죠."], ["This way then to the hospital,”", "그럼 병원은 이쪽입니다.\""], ["he said to Darya Alexandrovna, pointing to a turning out of the avenue.", "그가 가로수길에서 갈라지는 길을 가리키며 다리아 알렉산드로브나에게 말했다."]], [["The ladies put up their parasols and turned into the side path.", "부인들은 양산을 펼쳐 들고 옆길로 접어들었다."], ["After going down several turnings, and going through a little gate, Darya Alexandrovna saw standing on rising ground before her a large pretentious-looking red building, almost finished.", "몇 굽이를 돌고 작은 문 하나를 지나자, 다리아 알렉산드로브나는 자기 앞 언덕배기에 거의 완성된, 거창해 보이는 커다란 붉은 건물이 서 있는 것을 보았다."], ["The iron roof, which was not yet painted, shone with dazzling brightness in the sunshine.", "아직 칠하지 않은 철제 지붕이 햇빛을 받아 눈부시게 반짝였다."], ["Beside the finished building another had been begun, surrounded by scaffolding.", "완성된 건물 옆에는 또 다른 건물이 비계에 둘러싸인 채 막 짓기 시작한 상태였다."], ["Workmen in aprons, standing on scaffolds, were laying bricks, pouring mortar out of vats, and smoothing it with trowels.", "앞치마를 두른 일꾼들이 비계 위에 서서 벽돌을 쌓고, 통에서 모르타르를 부어 흙손으로 매끈하게 펴 바르고 있었다."]], [["“How quickly work gets done with you!”", "\"댁에서는 일이 정말 빨리 진행되는군요!\""], ["said Sviazhsky.", "스비야시스키가 말했다."], ["“When I was here last time the roof was not on.”", "\"제가 지난번에 왔을 때만 해도 지붕이 없었는데요.\""]], [["“By the autumn it will all be ready.", "\"가을이면 다 준비될 거예요."], ["Inside almost everything is done,”", "안쪽은 거의 다 됐어요.\""], ["said Anna.", "안나가 말했다."]], [["“And what’s this new building?”", "\"그런데 이 새 건물은 뭔가요?\""]], [["“That’s the house for the doctor and the dispensary,”", "\"저건 의사가 묵을 집과 조제실이에요.\""], ["answered Vronsky, seeing the architect in a short jacket coming towards him; and excusing himself to the ladies, he went to meet him.", "짧은 윗옷 차림의 건축가가 자기 쪽으로 다가오는 것을 본 브론스키가 대답했다. 그러고는 부인들에게 양해를 구하고 그를 맞으러 갔다."]], [["Going round a hole where the workmen were slaking lime, he stood still with the architect and began talking rather warmly.", "일꾼들이 석회를 개고 있는 구덩이를 돌아, 그는 건축가와 함께 멈춰 서서 다소 열을 올리며 이야기하기 시작했다."]], [["“The front is still too low,”", "\"정면이 아직 너무 낮아요.\""], ["he said to Anna, who had asked what was the matter.", "무슨 일이냐고 묻는 안나에게 그가 말했다."]], [["“I said the foundation ought to be raised,”", "\"제가 기초를 더 높여야 한다고 말했잖아요.\""], ["said Anna.", "안나가 말했다."]], [["“Yes, of course it would have been much better, Anna Arkadyevna,”", "\"네, 물론 그렇게 했더라면 훨씬 나았을 겁니다, 안나 아르카지예브나.\""], ["said the architect, “but now it’s too late.”", "건축가가 말했다. \"하지만 이제는 너무 늦었어요.\""]], [["“Yes, I take a great interest in it,”", "\"네, 저는 이 일에 무척 관심이 많아요.\""], ["Anna answered Sviazhsky, who was expressing his surprise at her knowledge of architecture.", "안나가 그녀의 건축 지식에 놀라움을 표하는 스비야시스키에게 대답했다."], ["“This new building ought to have been in harmony with the hospital.", "\"이 새 건물은 병원과 조화를 이루었어야 했어요."], ["It was an afterthought, and was begun without a plan.”", "그런데 나중에야 생각해 낸 거라, 설계도 없이 짓기 시작한 거죠.\""]], [["Vronsky, having finished his talk with the architect, joined the ladies, and led them inside the hospital.", "브론스키는 건축가와의 이야기를 마치고 부인들에게 합류해, 그들을 병원 안으로 안내했다."]], [["Although they were still at work on the cornices outside and were painting on the ground floor, upstairs almost all the rooms were finished.", "바깥쪽 처마 돌림띠 작업은 아직 진행 중이었고 일층은 칠을 하고 있었지만, 위층은 거의 모든 방이 완성되어 있었다."], ["Going up the broad cast-iron staircase to the landing, they walked into the first large room.", "폭이 넓은 주철 계단을 올라 층계참에 이른 그들은 첫 번째 큰 방으로 들어섰다."], ["The walls were stuccoed to look like marble, the huge plate-glass windows were already in, only the parquet floor was not yet finished, and the carpenters, who were planing a block of it, left their work, taking off the bands that fastened their hair, to greet the gentry.", "벽은 대리석처럼 보이도록 회칠이 되어 있었고, 거대한 판유리 창은 이미 끼워져 있었으며, 다만 쪽모이 마룻바닥만 아직 마무리되지 않은 상태였다. 마룻장 한 토막을 대패질하던 목수들이 일손을 멈추고는 머리를 묶고 있던 끈을 풀어 내며 양반들에게 인사를 했다."]], [["“This is the reception room,”", "\"여기가 접수실입니다.\""], ["said Vronsky.", "브론스키가 말했다."], ["“Here there will be a desk, tables, and benches, and nothing more.”", "\"여기에는 책상 하나와 탁자들, 의자들이 놓일 거고, 그 밖에는 아무것도 없어요.\""]], [["“This way; let us go in here.", "\"이쪽이에요. 여기로 들어가요."], ["Don’t go near the window,”", "창문 가까이는 가지 마세요.\""], ["said Anna, trying the paint to see if it were dry.", "페인트가 말랐는지 만져 보며 안나가 말했다."], ["“Alexey, the paint’s dry already,”", "\"알렉세이, 페인트가 벌써 다 말랐어요.\""], ["she added.", "그녀가 덧붙였다."]], [["From the reception room they went into the corridor.", "접수실에서 그들은 복도로 나왔다."], ["Here Vronsky showed them the mechanism for ventilation on a novel system.", "여기서 브론스키는 새로운 방식의 환기 장치를 그들에게 보여 주었다."], ["Then he showed them marble baths, and beds with extraordinary springs.", "그다음 대리석 욕조와 비범한 용수철이 달린 침대들을 보여 주었다."], ["Then he showed them the wards one after another, the storeroom, the linen room, then the heating stove of a new pattern, then the trolleys, which would make no noise as they carried everything needed along the corridors, and many other things.", "이어서 병실들을 차례차례 보여 주었고, 창고와 침구실을, 그다음에는 새로운 양식의 난방용 스토브를, 그다음에는 필요한 물건들을 복도로 나르면서도 아무 소리를 내지 않는 손수레들을, 그 밖에도 많은 것들을 보여 주었다."], ["Sviazhsky, as a connoisseur in the latest mechanical improvements, appreciated everything fully.", "최신 기계 설비에 정통한 스비야시스키는 모든 것을 빠짐없이 높이 평가했다."], ["Dolly simply wondered at all she had not seen before, and, anxious to understand it all, made minute inquiries about everything, which gave Vronsky great satisfaction.", "돌리는 그저 전에 본 적 없는 것들에 놀라워하며, 그 모든 것을 이해하고 싶은 마음에 하나하나 꼬치꼬치 물었고, 그것은 브론스키에게 큰 만족을 안겨 주었다."]], [["“Yes, I imagine that this will be the solitary example of a properly fitted hospital in Russia,”", "\"네, 제 생각엔 이게 러시아에서 제대로 설비를 갖춘 유일한 병원 사례가 될 것 같습니다.\""], ["said Sviazhsky.", "스비야시스키가 말했다."]], [["“And won’t you have a lying-in ward?”", "\"그런데 산실은 안 두실 건가요?\""], ["asked Dolly.", "돌리가 물었다."], ["“That’s so much needed in the country.", "\"시골에서는 그게 그토록 절실히 필요한데 말이에요."], ["I have often....”", "저도 자주……\""]], [["In spite of his usual courtesy, Vronsky interrupted her.", "평소의 정중함에도 불구하고 브론스키는 그녀의 말을 가로막았다."]], [["“This is not a lying-in home, but a hospital for the sick, and is intended for all diseases, except infectious complaints,”", "\"여기는 산실이 아니라 병자들을 위한 병원이고, 전염성 질환을 빼고는 모든 병을 위한 곳입니다.\" 그가 말했다."], ["he said.", "\"아!"], ["“Ah! look at this,”", "이것 좀 보세요.\""], ["and he rolled up to Darya Alexandrovna an invalid chair that had just been ordered for the convalescents.", "그러고는 회복기 환자들을 위해 막 주문해 들여온 환자용 의자 하나를 다리아 알렉산드로브나 쪽으로 굴려 왔다."], ["“Look.”", "\"보세요.\""], ["He sat down in the chair and began moving it.", "그는 그 의자에 앉아 그것을 움직이기 시작했다."], ["“The patient can’t walk—still too weak, perhaps, or something wrong with his legs, but he must have air, and he moves, rolls himself along....”", "\"환자가 걷질 못해요—아직 너무 약하거나, 다리에 무슨 문제가 있거나 해서요. 그래도 바람을 쐬어야 하니, 이렇게 움직여서 스스로 굴려 가는 거죠……\""]], [["Darya Alexandrovna was interested by everything.", "다리아 알렉산드로브나는 모든 것이 흥미로웠다."], ["She liked everything very much, but most of all she liked Vronsky himself with his natural, simple-hearted eagerness.", "그녀는 모든 것이 무척 마음에 들었지만, 무엇보다도 마음에 든 것은 그 꾸밈없고 순박한 열정을 지닌 브론스키 그 자신이었다."], ["“Yes, he’s a very nice, good man,”", "'그래, 정말 좋은 사람, 선량한 사람이야.'"], ["she thought several times, not hearing what he said, but looking at him and penetrating into his expression, while she mentally put herself in Anna’s place.", "그녀는 그가 하는 말은 듣지 못한 채 그를 바라보며 그의 표정 속으로 파고들면서, 마음속으로 자기를 안나의 자리에 놓아 보았다."], ["She liked him so much just now with his eager interest that she saw how Anna could be in love with him.", "지금 그 열렬한 관심을 지닌 그가 너무도 마음에 들어서, 그녀는 안나가 어떻게 그를 사랑할 수 있었는지 알 것 같았다."]], [["# Chapter 21", "# Chapter 21"]], [["“No, I think the princess is tired, and horses don’t interest her,”", "\"아니, 공작 부인께서 피곤하실 것 같고, 말에는 흥미가 없으실 테니까요.\""], ["Vronsky said to Anna, who wanted to go on to the stables, where Sviazhsky wished to see the new stallion.", "브론스키는 마차 우리로 더 가 보고 싶어 하는 안나에게 말했다. 그곳에서는 스비아시스키가 새로 들여온 종마를 보고 싶어 했다."], ["“You go on, while I escort the princess home, and we’ll have a little talk,”", "\"당신은 가 봐요. 나는 공작 부인을 댁까지 모셔다드리면서 잠깐 이야기를 나누고 올 테니.\""], ["he said, “if you would like that?”", "그가 말했다. \"괜찮으시다면 말입니다.\""], ["he added, turning to her.", "그는 그녀를 돌아보며 덧붙였다."]], [["“I know nothing about horses, and I shall be delighted,”", "\"저는 말에 대해서는 아무것도 모르는걸요, 그러니 기꺼이요.\""], ["answered Darya Alexandrovna, rather astonished.", "다리아 알렉산드로브나가 다소 놀라며 대답했다."]], [["She saw by Vronsky’s face that he wanted something from her. She was not mistaken.", "그녀는 브론스키의 얼굴에서 그가 자기에게 무언가를 바라고 있다는 것을 알아챘다."], ["As soon as they had passed through the little gate back into the garden, he looked in the direction Anna had taken, and having made sure that she could neither hear nor see them, he began:", "그녀의 짐작은 틀리지 않았다. 작은 쪽문을 지나 다시 정원으로 들어서자마자, 그는 안나가 간 방향을 살펴보았고, 그녀가 자기들의 말을 듣지도 보지도 못한다는 것을 확인한 뒤 입을 열었다."]], [["“You guess that I have something I want to say to you,”", "\"제가 부인께 드리고 싶은 말씀이 있다는 걸 짐작하고 계시지요.\""], ["he said, looking at her with laughing eyes.", "그가 웃음 띤 눈으로 그녀를 바라보며 말했다."], ["“I am not wrong in believing you to be a friend of Anna’s.”", "\"부인을 안나의 벗으로 여기는 제 생각이 틀리지 않았겠지요.\""], ["He took off his hat, and taking out his handkerchief, wiped his head, which was growing bald.", "그는 모자를 벗고 손수건을 꺼내 머리를 닦았는데, 그 머리는 점점 벗어지고 있었다."]], [["Darya Alexandrovna made no answer, and merely stared at him with dismay.", "다리아 알렉산드로브나는 아무 대답도 하지 않고 그저 당황한 채 그를 바라보았다."], ["When she was left alone with him, she suddenly felt afraid; his laughing eyes and stern expression scared her.", "그와 단둘이 남게 되자, 그녀는 문득 두려움을 느꼈다. 그의 웃음 띤 눈과 엄숙한 표정이 그녀를 겁먹게 했다."]], [["The most diverse suppositions as to what he was about to speak of to her flashed into her brain.", "그가 자기에게 무슨 말을 하려는 것일까, 온갖 갖가지 추측이 머릿속을 스쳐 갔다."], ["“He is going to beg me to come to stay with them with the children, and I shall have to refuse; or to create a set that will receive Anna in Moscow....", "\"아이들을 데리고 자기들 집에 와서 지내라고 청하려는 거겠지, 그럼 나는 거절해야 할 테고. 아니면 모스크바에서 안나를 받아 줄 모임을 만들어 달라고……."], ["Or isn’t it Vassenka Veslovsky and his relations with Anna?", "아니면 바센카 베슬로프스키와 안나의 관계 때문일까?"], ["Or perhaps about Kitty, that he feels he was to blame?”", "아니면 어쩌면 키티 일로, 자기가 잘못했다고 느끼는 걸까?\""], ["All her conjectures were unpleasant, but she did not guess what he really wanted to talk about to her.", "그녀의 짐작은 하나같이 불쾌했지만, 그녀는 그가 정작 무슨 이야기를 하고 싶어 하는지는 알아맞히지 못했다."]], [["“You have so much influence with Anna, she is so fond of you,”", "\"부인은 안나에게 큰 영향력을 가지고 계시고, 안나도 부인을 무척 좋아합니다.\" 그가 말했다."], ["he said; “do help me.”", "\"제발 저를 좀 도와주세요.\""]], [["Darya Alexandrovna looked with timid inquiry into his energetic face, which under the lime-trees was continually being lighted up in patches by the sunshine, and then passing into complete shadow again.", "다리아 알렉산드로브나는 보리수 아래에서 햇살을 받아 군데군데 환하게 밝아졌다가 다시 온통 그늘 속으로 잠기곤 하는 그의 활기찬 얼굴을, 조심스러운 물음을 담아 바라보았다."], ["She waited for him to say more, but he walked in silence beside her, scratching with his cane in the gravel.", "그녀는 그가 더 말하기를 기다렸지만, 그는 지팡이로 자갈을 긁적이며 말없이 그녀 곁을 걸을 뿐이었다."]], [["“You have come to see us, you, the only woman of Anna’s former friends—I don’t count Princess Varvara—but I know that you have done this not because you regard our position as normal, but because, understanding all the difficulty of the position, you still love her and want to be a help to her.", "\"부인은 우리를 만나러 와 주셨습니다. 안나의 옛 벗들 중에서 유일하게—바르바라 공작 영애는 치지 않습니다—말이지요."], ["Have I understood you rightly?”", "하지만 저는 압니다, 부인이 우리 처지를 정상이라 여겨서가 아니라, 이 처지의 온갖 어려움을 다 헤아리면서도 여전히 그녀를 사랑하고 그녀에게 힘이 되고자 하셨기에 그리하셨다는 것을요."], ["he asked, looking round at her.", "제가 부인을 옳게 이해한 겁니까?\" 그가 그녀를 돌아보며 물었다."]], [["“Oh, yes,”", "\"아, 네.\""], ["answered Darya Alexandrovna, putting down her sunshade, “but....”", "다리아 알렉산드로브나가 양산을 내리며 대답했다. \"하지만…….\""]], [["“No,”", "\"아닙니다.\""], ["he broke in, and unconsciously, oblivious of the awkward position into which he was putting his companion, he stopped abruptly, so that she had to stop short too.", "그가 말을 가로막았고, 자기 일행을 곤란한 처지에 몰아넣고 있다는 것도 깨닫지 못한 채 무심코 갑자기 걸음을 멈춰 서는 바람에, 그녀도 따라 멈춰 설 수밖에 없었다."], ["“No one feels more deeply and intensely than I do all the difficulty of Anna’s position; and that you may well understand, if you do me the honor of supposing I have any heart.", "\"안나의 처지가 안고 있는 온갖 어려움을 저보다 더 깊고 절실하게 느끼는 사람은 없습니다. 그 점은 부인께서도 잘 이해하실 겁니다, 제게 마음이라는 게 조금이라도 있다고 여겨 주신다면 말이지요."], ["I am to blame for that position, and that is why I feel it.”", "그 처지는 제 잘못이고, 그래서 제가 그것을 느끼는 겁니다.\""]], [["“I understand,”", "\"이해해요.\""], ["said Darya Alexandrovna, involuntarily admiring the sincerity and firmness with which he said this.", "다리아 알렉산드로브나는 그가 이 말을 하는 진실함과 단호함에 자기도 모르게 감탄하며 말했다."], ["“But just because you feel yourself responsible, you exaggerate it, I am afraid,”", "\"하지만 바로 당신이 스스로 책임을 느끼기 때문에, 그것을 너무 과장하시는 게 아닌가 싶어요.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“Her position in the world is difficult, I can well understand.”", "\"그녀가 사교계에서 처한 입장이 어렵다는 건 저도 충분히 이해할 수 있어요.\""]], [["“In the world it is hell!”", "\"사교계에서 그것은 지옥입니다!\""], ["he brought out quickly, frowning darkly.", "그가 어둡게 미간을 찌푸리며 재빨리 내뱉었다."], ["“You can’t imagine moral sufferings greater than what she went through in Petersburg in that fortnight ...", "\"그녀가 페테르부르크에서 그 보름 동안 겪은 것보다 더 큰 정신적 고통은 부인께서는 상상도 못 하실 겁니다……."], ["and I beg you to believe it.”", "부디 제 말을 믿어 주십시오.\""]], [["“Yes, but here, so long as neither Anna ...", "\"네, 하지만 여기서는, 안나도……"], ["nor you miss society....”", "당신도 사교계를 아쉬워하지 않는 한…….\""]], [["“Society!”", "\"사교계요!\""], ["he said contemptuously, “how could I miss society?”", "그가 경멸하듯 말했다. \"제가 어떻게 사교계를 아쉬워하겠습니까?\""]], [["“So far—and it may be so always—you are happy and at peace.", "\"지금까지는—어쩌면 늘 그럴지도 모르지만—두 분은 행복하고 평온하시잖아요."], ["I see in Anna that she is happy, perfectly happy, she has had time to tell me so much already,”", "안나를 보면 그녀가 행복하다는 게, 더없이 행복하다는 게 보여요. 그녀는 벌써 제게 그토록 많은 이야기를 해 줄 짬이 있었답니다.\""], ["said Darya Alexandrovna, smiling; and involuntarily, as she said this, at the same moment a doubt entered her mind whether Anna really were happy.", "다리아 알렉산드로브나는 미소를 지으며 말했다. 그러면서도 무심코, 이 말을 하는 바로 그 순간, 안나가 정말로 행복한 것일까 하는 의심이 그녀의 마음속으로 파고들었다."]], [["But Vronsky, it appeared, had no doubts on that score.", "그러나 브론스키는 그 점에 대해서는 아무런 의심도 없는 듯했다."]], [["“Yes, yes,”", "\"그래요, 그래요.\""], ["he said, “I know that she has revived after all her sufferings; she is happy.", "그가 말했다. \"그녀가 그 모든 고통을 겪은 뒤 되살아났다는 걸 압니다."], ["She is happy in the present.", "그녀는 행복합니다. 그녀는 지금 이 순간 행복하지요."], ["But I?...", "하지만 저는요?……"], ["I am afraid of what is before us ...", "저는 우리 앞에 놓인 것이 두렵습니다……."], ["I beg your pardon, you would like to walk on?”", "죄송합니다, 더 걸으시겠습니까?\""]], [["“No, I don’t mind.”", "\"아니요, 괜찮아요.\""]], [["“Well, then, let us sit here.”", "\"그럼, 여기 좀 앉으시지요.\""]], [["Darya Alexandrovna sat down on a garden seat in a corner of the avenue.", "다리아 알렉산드로브나는 가로수 길 한쪽 구석의 정원 의자에 앉았다."], ["He stood up facing her.", "그는 그녀를 마주 보고 섰다."]], [["“I see that she is happy,”", "\"저는 그녀가 행복하다는 걸 압니다.\""], ["he repeated, and the doubt whether she were happy sank more deeply into Darya Alexandrovna’s mind.", "그가 거듭 말했고, 그녀가 정말 행복한 것일까 하는 의심은 다리아 알렉산드로브나의 마음속으로 더욱 깊이 가라앉았다."], ["“But can it last?", "\"하지만 그게 지속될 수 있을까요?"], ["Whether we have acted rightly or wrongly is another question, but the die is cast,”", "우리가 옳게 행동했는지 그르게 행동했는지는 별개의 문제이지만, 주사위는 이미 던져졌습니다.\""], ["he said, passing from Russian to French, “and we are bound together for life.", "그가 러시아어에서 프랑스어로 옮겨 가며 말했다. \"그리고 우리는 평생 한데 묶여 있습니다."], ["We are united by all the ties of love that we hold most sacred.", "우리는 우리가 가장 신성하게 여기는 사랑의 온갖 끈으로 맺어져 있어요."], ["We have a child, we may have other children.", "우리에게는 아이가 있고, 또 다른 아이들도 생길 수 있습니다."], ["But the law and all the conditions of our position are such that thousands of complications arise which she does not see and does not want to see.", "하지만 법이며 우리 처지의 온갖 조건들이, 그녀는 보지 못하고 또 보고 싶어 하지도 않는 수천 가지 골치 아픈 문제들을 일으킵니다."], ["And that one can well understand.", "그것도 충분히 이해할 만한 일이지요."], ["But I can’t help seeing them.", "하지만 저는 그것들을 보지 않을 수가 없습니다."], ["My daughter is by law not my daughter, but Karenin’s.", "제 딸은 법적으로 제 딸이 아니라 카레닌의 딸입니다."], ["I cannot bear this falsity!”", "저는 이 거짓을 견딜 수가 없어요!\""], ["he said, with a vigorous gesture of refusal, and he looked with gloomy inquiry towards Darya Alexandrovna.", "그가 거부하듯 힘찬 몸짓을 하며 말하고는, 침울한 물음을 담아 다리아 알렉산드로브나를 바라보았다."]], [["She made no answer, but simply gazed at him.", "그녀는 아무 대답도 하지 않고 그저 그를 응시했다."], ["He went on:", "그가 말을 이었다."]], [["“One day a son may be born, my son, and he will be legally a Karenin; he will not be the heir of my name nor of my property, and however happy we may be in our home life and however many children we may have, there will be no real tie between us.", "\"언젠가 아들이 태어날 수도 있습니다, 제 아들이요. 그런데 그 아이는 법적으로 카레닌이 될 겁니다."], ["They will be Karenins.", "그 아이는 제 이름의 상속자도, 제 재산의 상속자도 되지 못합니다."], ["You can understand the bitterness and horror of this position!", "우리 가정생활이 아무리 행복하고 아무리 많은 아이가 생긴다 해도, 우리 사이에 진정한 끈은 없을 겁니다. 그 아이들은 카레닌이 될 테니까요."], ["I have tried to speak of this to Anna.", "부인은 이 처지의 쓰라림과 끔찍함을 이해하실 수 있을 겁니다! 저는 이 일을 안나에게 이야기하려고 해 봤습니다."], ["It irritates her.", "그것은 그녀를 짜증 나게 합니다."], ["She does not understand, and to her I cannot speak plainly of all this.", "그녀는 이해하지 못하고, 또 그녀에게 이 모든 것을 솔직하게 말할 수도 없습니다."], ["Now look at another side.", "자, 이제 다른 면을 봐 주십시오."], ["I am happy, happy in her love, but I must have occupation.", "저는 행복합니다, 그녀의 사랑 속에서 행복하지요."], ["I have found occupation, and am proud of what I am doing and consider it nobler than the pursuits of my former companions at court and in the army.", "하지만 저는 할 일이 있어야 합니다. 저는 할 일을 찾아냈고, 제가 하는 일을 자랑스러워하며, 그것이 궁정과 군대에서 함께했던 옛 동료들의 일거리보다 더 고귀하다고 여깁니다."], ["And most certainly I would not change the work I am doing for theirs.", "그리고 단연코 저는 제가 하고 있는 일을 그들의 일과 바꾸지 않을 겁니다."], ["I am working here, settled in my own place, and I am happy and contented, and we need nothing more to make us happy.", "저는 이곳에서 일하며, 제 자리에 정착했고, 행복하고 만족스럽습니다. 우리가 행복하기 위해서는 그 이상 아무것도 필요하지 않아요."], ["I love my work here.", "저는 이곳에서 하는 제 일을 사랑합니다."], ["Ce n’est pas un pis-aller, on the contrary....”", "Ce n’est pas un pis-aller, 오히려 그 반대로…….\""]], [["Darya Alexandrovna noticed that at this point in his explanation he grew confused, and she did not quite understand this digression, but she felt that having once begun to speak of matters near his heart, of which he could not speak to Anna, he was now making a clean breast of everything, and that the question of his pursuits in the country fell into the same category of matters near his heart, as the question of his relations with Anna.", "* Ce n’est pas un pis-aller: 프랑스어로 '이것은 차선책이 아니다'. 다리아 알렉산드로브나는 그가 이 대목에 이르러 말을 더듬는 것을 알아챘고, 이 곁가지 이야기를 잘 이해하지 못했다. 하지만 그녀는, 그가 일단 가슴속 깊이 자리한 일들, 안나에게는 말할 수 없는 일들에 대해 이야기를 꺼낸 이상, 이제 모든 것을 거리낌 없이 털어놓고 있으며, 시골에서 그가 하는 일에 관한 문제도 안나와의 관계에 관한 문제와 마찬가지로 그의 가슴 가까이에 놓인 일들과 같은 범주에 속한다는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["“Well, I will go on,”", "\"자, 계속하겠습니다.\""], ["he said, collecting himself.", "그가 마음을 가다듬으며 말했다."], ["“The great thing is that as I work I want to have a conviction that what I am doing will not die with me, that I shall have heirs to come after me,—and this I have not.", "\"중요한 것은, 제가 일을 하면서, 제가 하는 일이 저와 함께 죽지 않으리라는, 제 뒤를 이을 상속자들이 있으리라는 확신을 갖고 싶다는 겁니다. 그런데 제게는 그것이 없습니다."], ["Conceive the position of a man who knows that his children, the children of the woman he loves, will not be his, but will belong to someone who hates them and cares nothing about them!", "자기 아이들이, 자기가 사랑하는 여자의 아이들이 자기 아이가 되지 못하고, 그 아이들을 미워하며 조금도 마음 쓰지 않는 누군가의 것이 되리라는 걸 아는 사람의 처지를 헤아려 보십시오!"], ["It is awful!”", "끔찍한 일입니다!\""]], [["He paused, evidently much moved.", "그는 말을 멈췄는데, 분명 몹시 동요한 듯했다."]], [["“Yes, indeed, I see that.", "\"네, 정말 그래요, 저도 알겠어요."], ["But what can Anna do?”", "하지만 안나가 무엇을 할 수 있을까요?\""], ["queried Darya Alexandrovna.", "다리아 알렉산드로브나가 물었다."]], [["“Yes, that brings me to the object of my conversation,”", "\"네, 바로 그것이 제가 이 대화에서 말하려던 것입니다.\""], ["he said, calming himself with an effort.", "그가 애써 마음을 진정시키며 말했다."], ["“Anna can, it depends on her....", "\"안나는 할 수 있습니다, 그건 그녀에게 달려 있어요……."], ["Even to petition the Tsar for legitimization, a divorce is essential.", "황제께 적출 인정을 청원하려고만 해도, 이혼은 반드시 필요합니다. 그리고 그것은 안나에게 달려 있어요."], ["And that depends on Anna.", "그녀의 남편은 이혼에 동의했었습니다."], ["Her husband agreed to a divorce—at that time your husband had arranged it completely.", "그때 부인의 남편께서 그것을 완전히 매듭지어 놓으셨지요. 그리고 지금도, 제가 알기로, 그는 그것을 거절하지 않을 겁니다."], ["And now, I know, he would not refuse it.", "그에게 편지를 쓰는 일만 남았습니다."], ["It is only a matter of writing to him.", "그는 그때 분명히 말했습니다, 그녀가 그 뜻을 밝히기만 하면 거절하지 않겠다고요."], ["He said plainly at that time that if she expressed the desire, he would not refuse.", "물론,\" 그가 침울하게 말했다, \"그것은 그처럼 냉혹한 인간들만이 부릴 수 있는 바리새인적인 잔인함 가운데 하나입니다."], ["Of course,” he said gloomily, “it is one of those Pharisaical cruelties of which only such heartless men are capable.", "그는 자기에 대한 어떤 기억이든 그녀에게 얼마나 큰 고통을 주는지 알면서도, 그녀를 알면서도, 그녀에게서 편지를 받아야겠다는 겁니다."], ["He knows what agony any recollection of him must give her, and knowing her, he must have a letter from her.", "그것이 그녀에게 고통이라는 걸 저는 이해할 수 있습니다. 하지만 이 일은 너무나 중대해서, passer par-dessus toutes ces finesses de sentiment."], ["I can understand that it is agony to her.", "Il y va du bonheur et de l’existence d’Anne et de ses enfants."], ["But the matter is of such importance, that one must passer par-dessus toutes ces finesses de sentiment.", "제 이야기는 하지 않겠습니다, 제게도 힘든 일이긴 하지만요, 무척 힘든 일이지만.\" 그가 그것이 자기에게 힘들다는 이유로 누군가를 위협하기라도 하는 듯한 표정으로 말했다."], ["Il y va du bonheur et de l’existence d’Anne et de ses enfants.", "\"그러니, 공작 부인, 저는 부인을 구원의 닻처럼 염치없이 붙들고 있는 겁니다."], ["I won’t speak of myself, though it’s hard for me, very hard,”", "그녀가 그에게 편지를 써서 이혼을 청하도록, 부디 저를 도와 설득해 주십시오.\""], ["he said, with an expression as though he were threatening someone for its being hard for him.", "* passer par-dessus toutes ces finesses de sentiment."], ["“And so it is, princess, that I am shamelessly clutching at you as an anchor of salvation.", "Il y va du bonheur et de l’existence d’Anne et de ses enfants: 프랑스어로 '이 모든 감정의 섬세함을 넘어서야 한다."], ["Help me to persuade her to write to him and ask for a divorce.”", "안나와 그 아이들의 행복과 존립이 걸린 문제다'."]], [["“Yes, of course,”", "\"네, 물론이지요.\""], ["Darya Alexandrovna said dreamily, as she vividly recalled her last interview with Alexey Alexandrovitch.", "다리아 알렉산드로브나는 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치와의 마지막 만남을 생생하게 떠올리며 꿈결처럼 말했다."], ["“Yes, of course,”", "\"네, 물론이에요.\""], ["she repeated with decision, thinking of Anna.", "그녀는 안나를 생각하며 단호하게 거듭 말했다."]], [["“Use your influence with her, make her write.", "\"그녀에게 부인의 영향력을 행사하셔서, 편지를 쓰게 해 주십시오."], ["I don’t like—I’m almost unable to speak about this to her.”", "저는 이 일을 그녀에게 이야기하는 게 싫습니다—거의 할 수가 없어요.\""]], [["“Very well, I will talk to her.", "\"좋아요, 제가 그녀와 이야기해 볼게요."], ["But how is it she does not think of it herself?”", "그런데 어째서 그녀는 스스로 그 생각을 못 하는 걸까요?\""], ["said Darya Alexandrovna, and for some reason she suddenly at that point recalled Anna’s strange new habit of half-closing her eyes.", "다리아 알렉산드로브나가 말했다. 그러다 무슨 까닭에선지 그 순간 문득, 눈을 반쯤 감는 안나의 이상한 새 버릇이 떠올랐다."], ["And she remembered that Anna drooped her eyelids just when the deeper questions of life were touched upon.", "그리고 안나가 인생의 더 깊은 문제들이 건드려질 때면 꼭 눈꺼풀을 내리깔던 것이 생각났다."], ["“Just as though she half-shut her eyes to her own life, so as not to see everything,”", "\"마치 모든 것을 다 보지 않으려고, 자기 삶에 대해 눈을 반쯤 감아 버리는 것 같아.\""], ["thought Dolly.", "돌리는 생각했다."], ["“Yes, indeed, for my own sake and for hers I will talk to her,”", "\"그래, 정말이지, 나 자신을 위해서도 그녀를 위해서도 그녀와 이야기해 볼 거야.\""], ["Dolly said in reply to his look of gratitude.", "돌리는 그의 감사 어린 눈빛에 답하여 말했다."]], [["They got up and walked to the house.", "그들은 일어나 집을 향해 걸어갔다."]], [["# Chapter 22", "# Chapter 22"]], [["When Anna found Dolly at home before her, she looked intently in her eyes, as though questioning her about the talk she had had with Vronsky, but she made no inquiry in words.", "안나는 자기보다 먼저 돌리가 집에 와 있는 것을 보고, 그녀의 눈을 가만히 들여다보았다. 마치 브론스키와 나눈 이야기에 대해 묻기라도 하는 듯했지만, 입으로는 아무것도 묻지 않았다."]], [["“I believe it’s dinner time,”", "\"이제 곧 만찬 시간일 거예요.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“We’ve not seen each other at all yet.", "\"우린 아직 제대로 얼굴도 못 봤네요."], ["I am reckoning on the evening.", "저녁 시간을 기대하고 있어요."], ["Now I want to go and dress.", "이제 가서 옷을 갈아입어야겠어요."], ["I expect you do too; we all got splashed at the buildings.”", "형님도 그러시겠지요. 우리 모두 공사장에서 흙탕물을 뒤집어썼으니까요.\""]], [["Dolly went to her room and she felt amused.", "돌리는 자기 방으로 가면서 우습다는 생각이 들었다."], ["To change her dress was impossible, for she had already put on her best dress.", "옷을 갈아입는 것은 불가능했다. 이미 제일 좋은 옷을 입고 있었기 때문이다."], ["But in order to signify in some way her preparation for dinner, she asked the maid to brush her dress, changed her cuffs and tie, and put some lace on her head.", "그래도 만찬을 위해 무언가 준비를 한다는 표시는 하고 싶어서, 하녀에게 옷을 솔질해 달라고 부탁하고, 커프스와 넥타이를 바꾸고, 머리에 레이스를 얹었다."]], [["“This is all I can do,”", "\"제가 할 수 있는 건 이게 다예요.\""], ["she said with a smile to Anna, who came in to her in a third dress, again of extreme simplicity.", "그녀는 다시 한번 더없이 수수한 세 번째 옷을 입고 들어온 안나에게 미소를 지으며 말했다."]], [["“Yes, we are too formal here,”", "\"네, 우린 여기서 너무 격식을 차린답니다.\""], ["she said, as it were apologizing for her magnificence.", "안나가 자신의 화려함을 변명하듯 말했다. \"알렉세이는 형님이 와 주신 걸 무척 기뻐하고 있어요."], ["“Alexey is delighted at your visit, as he rarely is at anything.", "그 사람은 좀처럼 무슨 일에 그렇게 기뻐하는 법이 없는데."], ["He has completely lost his heart to you,”", "형님한테 완전히 마음을 빼앗긴 거예요.\""], ["she added.", "그녀가 덧붙였다."], ["“You’re not tired?”", "\"피곤하진 않으세요?\""]], [["There was no time for talking about anything before dinner.", "만찬 전까지는 무언가 이야기를 나눌 틈이 없었다."], ["Going into the drawing-room they found Princess Varvara already there, and the gentlemen of the party in black frock-coats.", "응접실로 들어가니 이미 바르바라 공작 영애가 와 있었고, 검은 프록코트 차림의 신사들도 있었다."], ["The architect wore a swallow-tail coat.", "건축가는 연미복을 입고 있었다."], ["Vronsky presented the doctor and the steward to his guest.", "브론스키는 의사와 집사를 손님에게 소개했다."], ["The architect he had already introduced to her at the hospital.", "건축가는 이미 병원에서 그녀에게 인사를 시킨 터였다."]], [["A stout butler, resplendent with a smoothly shaven round chin and a starched white cravat, announced that dinner was ready, and the ladies got up.", "매끈하게 면도한 둥근 턱과 풀 먹인 흰 크라바트로 번드르르하게 차려입은 뚱뚱한 집사가 만찬 준비가 되었다고 알리자, 부인들이 자리에서 일어났다."], ["Vronsky asked Sviazhsky to take in Anna Arkadyevna, and himself offered his arm to Dolly.", "브론스키는 스비야시스키에게 안나 아르카지예브나를 안내해 달라고 청하고, 자기는 돌리에게 팔을 내밀었다."], ["Veslovsky was before Tushkevitch in offering his arm to Princess Varvara, so that Tushkevitch with the steward and the doctor walked in alone.", "베슬롭스키가 투시케비치보다 먼저 바르바라 공작 영애에게 팔을 내미는 바람에, 투시케비치는 집사와 의사와 함께 따로 걸어가야 했다."]], [["The dinner, the dining-room, the service, the waiting at table, the wine, and the food, were not simply in keeping with the general tone of modern luxury throughout all the house, but seemed even more sumptuous and modern.", "만찬도, 식당도, 식기도, 시중드는 모양새도, 포도주도, 음식도, 그저 온 집안에 흐르는 현대적 사치의 전반적인 분위기에 어울리는 정도가 아니라, 오히려 그보다 더 호화롭고 더 현대적으로 보였다."], ["Darya Alexandrovna watched this luxury which was novel to her, and as a good housekeeper used to managing a household—although she never dreamed of adapting anything she saw to her own household, as it was all in a style of luxury far above her own manner of living—she could not help scrutinizing every detail, and wondering how and by whom it was all done.", "다리야 알렉산드로브나는 자기에게는 낯선 이 사치를 지켜보았고, 살림을 도맡아 꾸려 온 알뜰한 안주인으로서—비록 본 것을 자기 살림에 끌어다 쓸 생각은 꿈에도 하지 않았는데, 이 모든 것이 자기 생활 방식을 훨씬 뛰어넘는 호사스러운 양식이었기 때문이다—구석구석을 뜯어보며 이 모든 것이 어떻게, 또 누구의 손으로 이루어진 것인지 궁금해하지 않을 수 없었다."], ["Vassenka Veslovsky, her husband, and even Sviazhsky, and many other people she knew, would never have considered this question, and would have readily believed what every well-bred host tries to make his guests feel, that is, that all that is well-ordered in his house has cost him, the host, no trouble whatever, but comes of itself.", "바센카 베슬롭스키도, 그녀의 남편도, 심지어 스비야시스키도, 그녀가 아는 다른 많은 사람도, 이런 의문 따위는 결코 품지 않을 것이며, 교양 있는 주인이라면 누구나 손님에게 느끼게 하려고 애쓰는 그것, 곧 자기 집의 잘 정돈된 모든 것이 주인인 자기에게는 아무런 수고도 들이지 않은 채 저절로 그렇게 된 것이라는 말을 선뜻 믿었을 것이다."], ["Darya Alexandrovna was well aware that even porridge for the children’s breakfast does not come of itself, and that therefore, where so complicated and magnificent a style of luxury was maintained, someone must give earnest attention to its organization.", "다리야 알렉산드로브나는 아이들 아침으로 먹일 죽 한 그릇조차 저절로 생기지 않는다는 것을, 따라서 이토록 복잡하고 웅장한 사치의 양식이 유지되는 곳이라면 누군가 그 운영에 진지하게 마음을 쏟고 있으리라는 것을 잘 알고 있었다."], ["And from the glance with which Alexey Kirillovitch scanned the table, from the way he nodded to the butler, and offered Darya Alexandrovna her choice between cold soup and hot soup, she saw that it was all organized and maintained by the care of the master of the house himself.", "그리고 알렉세이 키릴로비치가 식탁을 훑어보는 눈길에서, 집사에게 고갯짓을 하는 모습에서, 다리야 알렉산드로브나에게 차가운 수프와 뜨거운 수프 중 하나를 고르라고 권하는 태도에서, 그녀는 이 모든 것이 이 집 주인 본인의 손길로 조직되고 유지되고 있음을 알아차렸다."], ["It was evident that it all rested no more upon Anna than upon Veslovsky.", "이 모든 것이 베슬롭스키에게 기대고 있지 않은 것과 마찬가지로 안나에게 기대고 있지 않다는 것은 분명했다."], ["She, Sviazhsky, the princess, and Veslovsky, were equally guests, with light hearts enjoying what had been arranged for them.", "그녀도, 스비야시스키도, 공작 영애도, 베슬롭스키도, 모두 똑같이 손님으로서, 가벼운 마음으로 누군가가 마련해 준 것을 누리고 있을 뿐이었다."]], [["Anna was the hostess only in conducting the conversation.", "안나는 대화를 이끌어 가는 데에서만 안주인이었다."], ["The conversation was a difficult one for the lady of the house at a small table with persons present, like the steward and the architect, belonging to a completely different world, struggling not to be overawed by an elegance to which they were unaccustomed, and unable to sustain a large share in the general conversation.", "그것은 작은 식탁에 둘러앉은 안주인에게는 쉽지 않은 대화였다. 집사며 건축가처럼 전혀 다른 세계에 속한 사람들이 함께 있었는데, 그들은 익숙하지 않은 우아함에 압도되지 않으려 애쓰면서도 좌중의 대화에 큰 몫을 떠맡을 수가 없었던 것이다."], ["But this difficult conversation Anna directed with her usual tact and naturalness, and indeed she did so with actual enjoyment, as Darya Alexandrovna observed.", "하지만 안나는 이 어려운 대화를 늘 그렇듯 능숙하고 자연스럽게 이끌어 갔고, 다리야 알렉산드로브나가 보기에는 실제로 그것을 즐기면서 그렇게 하고 있었다."], ["The conversation began about the row Tushkevitch and Veslovsky had taken alone together in the boat, and Tushkevitch began describing the last boat races in Petersburg at the Yacht Club.", "대화는 투시케비치와 베슬롭스키가 둘이서만 보트를 탔던 일로 시작되었고, 투시케비치는 페테르부르크의 요트 클럽에서 열린 지난번 보트 경주를 묘사하기 시작했다."], ["But Anna, seizing the first pause, at once turned to the architect to draw him out of his silence.", "그러나 안나는 첫 번째 틈을 놓치지 않고 곧장 건축가에게 말을 돌려 그를 침묵에서 끌어냈다."]], [["“Nikolay Ivanitch was struck,”", "\"니콜라이 이바니치도 놀라워하셨어요.\" 그녀는 스비야시스키를 가리키며 말했다."], ["she said, meaning Sviazhsky, “at the progress the new building had made since he was here last; but I am there every day, and every day I wonder at the rate at which it grows.”", "\"새 건물이 지난번에 오셨을 때보다 얼마나 진척됐는지 보고요. 그런데 저는 매일 그곳에 가는데도, 날마다 그 자라는 속도에 놀란답니다.\""]], [["“It’s first-rate working with his excellency,”", "\"각하와 함께 일하는 건 더없이 좋습니다.\""], ["said the architect with a smile (he was respectful and composed, though with a sense of his own dignity).", "건축가가 미소를 지으며 말했다(그는 공손하고 차분했지만 나름의 자긍심도 지니고 있었다)."], ["“It’s a very different matter to have to do with the district authorities.", "\"지방 관청을 상대하는 것과는 전혀 다른 일이지요."], ["Where one would have to write out sheaves of papers, here I call upon the count, and in three words we settle the business.”", "다른 데서라면 서류 뭉치를 잔뜩 써내야 할 일도, 여기서는 백작님께 말씀드리면 세 마디로 일이 끝나니까요.\""]], [["“The American way of doing business,”", "\"미국식 사업 방식이로군요.\""], ["said Sviazhsky, with a smile.", "스비야시스키가 미소를 지으며 말했다."]], [["“Yes, there they build in a rational fashion....”", "\"네, 거기서는 합리적인 방식으로 짓지요…….\""]], [["The conversation passed to the misuse of political power in the United States, but Anna quickly brought it round to another topic, so as to draw the steward into talk.", "대화는 미국에서 정치권력이 오용되는 문제로 옮겨 갔지만, 안나는 집사를 대화로 끌어들이려고 재빨리 다른 화제로 돌렸다."]], [["“Have you ever seen a reaping machine?”", "\"형님은 수확기를 본 적 있으세요?\""], ["she said, addressing Darya Alexandrovna.", "그녀가 다리야 알렉산드로브나를 향해 말했다."], ["“We had just ridden over to look at one when we met.", "\"우리가 마주쳤을 때 마침 그걸 보러 갔다 오는 길이었어요."], ["It’s the first time I ever saw one.”", "저도 그날 처음 봤답니다.\""]], [["“How do they work?”", "\"그게 어떻게 작동하는데?\""], ["asked Dolly.", "돌리가 물었다."]], [["“Exactly like little scissors.", "\"꼭 작은 가위 같아요."], ["A plank and a lot of little scissors.", "널빤지 하나에 작은 가위가 잔뜩 달려 있죠."], ["Like this.”", "이렇게요.\""]], [["Anna took a knife and fork in her beautiful white hands covered with rings, and began showing how the machine worked.", "안나는 반지로 뒤덮인 아름답고 흰 손으로 나이프와 포크를 집어 들고, 기계가 어떻게 작동하는지 보여 주기 시작했다."], ["It was clear that she saw nothing would be understood from her explanation; but aware that her talk was pleasant and her hands beautiful she went on explaining.", "자기 설명으로는 아무것도 이해시킬 수 없다는 것을 분명히 알고 있었지만, 자기 이야기가 듣기 좋고 손이 아름답다는 것을 알기에 그녀는 계속 설명을 이어 갔다."]], [["“More like little penknives,”", "\"작은 주머니칼에 더 가깝지요.\""], ["Veslovsky said playfully, never taking his eyes off her.", "베슬롭스키가 그녀에게서 눈을 떼지 않은 채 장난스럽게 말했다."]], [["Anna gave a just perceptible smile, but made no answer.", "안나는 알아챌 듯 말 듯 미소를 지었지만 아무 대답도 하지 않았다."], ["“Isn’t it true, Karl Fedoritch, that it’s just like little scissors?”", "\"카를 표도리치, 정말 작은 가위 같지 않나요?\""], ["she said to the steward.", "그녀가 집사에게 말했다."]], [["“Oh, ja,”", "\"오, 야.\""], ["answered the German.", "독일인이 대답했다."], ["“Es ist ein ganz einfaches Ding,”", "\"에스 이스트 아인 간츠 아인파헤스 딩.\""], ["and he began to explain the construction of the machine.", "그러고는 기계의 구조를 설명하기 시작했다."]], [["“It’s a pity it doesn’t bind too.", "\"묶기까지 못하는 게 아쉽군요."], ["I saw one at the Vienna exhibition, which binds with a wire,”", "빈 박람회에서 철사로 묶는 기계를 봤는데 말이죠.\""], ["said Sviazhsky.", "스비야시스키가 말했다."], ["“They would be more profitable in use.”", "\"그런 게 쓰기에 더 이득일 텐데.\""]], [["“Es kommt drauf an....", "\"에스 콤트 드라우프 안……"], ["Der Preis vom Draht muss ausgerechnet werden.”", "데어 프라이스 폼 드라트 무스 아우스게레히네트 베르덴.\""], ["And the German, roused from his taciturnity, turned to Vronsky.", "그러더니 과묵함에서 깨어난 독일인이 브론스키를 향해 돌아섰다."], ["“Das lässt sich ausrechnen, Erlaucht.”", "\"다스 레스트 지히 아우스레히넨, 에를라우흐트.\""], ["The German was just feeling in the pocket where were his pencil and the notebook he always wrote in, but recollecting that he was at a dinner, and observing Vronsky’s chilly glance, he checked himself.", "독일인은 늘 무언가를 적어 넣는 연필과 수첩이 들어 있는 주머니를 막 더듬으려다가, 자기가 만찬 자리에 있다는 것을 떠올리고 브론스키의 싸늘한 눈길을 알아챘는지 손을 멈추었다."], ["“Zu compliziert, macht zu viel Klopot,”", "\"추 콤플리치르트, 마흐트 추 필 클로포트.\""], ["he concluded.", "그가 결론지었다."]], [["“Wünscht man Dochots, so hat man auch Klopots,”", "\"뷘슈트 만 도호츠, 조 하트 만 아우흐 클로포츠.\""], ["said Vassenka Veslovsky, mimicking the German.", "바센카 베슬롭스키가 독일인을 흉내 내며 말했다."], ["“J’adore l’allemand,”", "\"자도르 랄망.\""], ["he addressed Anna again with the same smile.", "그는 다시 같은 미소를 지으며 안나에게 말을 건넸다."]], [["“Cessez,”", "\"세세.\""], ["she said with playful severity.", "그녀가 장난스러운 엄격함으로 말했다."]], [["“We expected to find you in the fields, Vassily Semyonitch,”", "\"우린 당신이 들판에 있을 줄 알았어요, 바실리 세묘니치.\""], ["she said to the doctor, a sickly-looking man; “have you been there?”", "그녀가 병약해 보이는 의사에게 말했다. \"거기 다녀오셨나요?\""]], [["“I went there, but I had taken flight,”", "\"갔었는데, 그만 줄행랑을 쳤지요.\""], ["the doctor answered with gloomy jocoseness.", "의사가 침울한 농담조로 대답했다."]], [["“Then you’ve taken a good constitutional?”", "\"그럼 산책 한번 잘하셨겠네요?\" \"아주 훌륭했지요!\""]], [["“Splendid!” “Well, and how was the old woman?", "\"그래, 그 할머니는 어떠셨어요?"], ["I hope it’s not typhus?”", "발진티푸스는 아니었으면 좋겠는데요?\""]], [["“Typhus it is not, but it’s taking a bad turn.”", "\"발진티푸스는 아닙니다만, 상태가 나빠지고 있어요.\""]], [["“What a pity!”", "\"저런, 안됐네요!\""], ["said Anna, and having thus paid the dues of civility to her domestic circle, she turned to her own friends.", "안나가 말했다. 그리고 이렇게 집안 식구들에게 예의의 도리를 다하고는, 자기 친구들 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸다."]], [["“It would be a hard task, though, to construct a machine from your description, Anna Arkadyevna,”", "\"그래도 당신 설명만 듣고 기계를 만들어 내라면 꽤 힘든 일이겠는데요, 안나 아르카지예브나.\""], ["Sviazhsky said jestingly.", "스비야시스키가 농담조로 말했다."]], [["“Oh, no, why so?”", "\"어머, 아니에요, 왜요?\""], ["said Anna with a smile that betrayed that she knew there was something charming in her disquisitions upon the machine that had been noticed by Sviazhsky.", "안나가 미소를 지으며 말했다. 그 미소는 자기가 기계에 대해 늘어놓은 강론에 무언가 매력적인 데가 있고 스비야시스키가 그것을 알아챘다는 것을 자기도 알고 있음을 드러내는 것이었다."], ["This new trait of girlish coquettishness made an unpleasant impression on Dolly.", "이 새로운 소녀 같은 교태가 돌리에게는 불쾌한 인상을 주었다."]], [["“But Anna Arkadyevna’s knowledge of architecture is marvelous,”", "\"그렇지만 안나 아르카지예브나의 건축 지식은 정말 놀라워요.\""], ["said Tushkevitch.", "투시케비치가 말했다."]], [["“To be sure, I heard Anna Arkadyevna talking yesterday about plinths and damp-courses,”", "\"정말이지, 어제 안나 아르카지예브나가 초석이며 방습층 이야기를 하시는 걸 들었어요.\""], ["said Veslovsky.", "베슬롭스키가 말했다."], ["“Have I got it right?”", "\"제가 제대로 말한 건가요?\""]], [["“There’s nothing marvelous about it, when one sees and hears so much of it,”", "\"그렇게 많이 보고 듣다 보면 놀라울 것도 없어요.\" 안나가 말했다."], ["said Anna. “But, I dare say, you don’t even know what houses are made of?”", "\"하지만 당신은 집이 무엇으로 지어지는지조차 모르실 거예요, 아마.\""]], [["Darya Alexandrovna saw that Anna disliked the tone of raillery that existed between her and Veslovsky, but fell in with it against her will.", "다리야 알렉산드로브나는 안나가 자기와 베슬롭스키 사이에 오가는 그 야유 섞인 어조를 싫어하면서도, 본의 아니게 거기에 끌려들고 있음을 알아챘다."]], [["Vronsky acted in this matter quite differently from Levin.", "이 일에서 브론스키는 레빈과는 전혀 다르게 행동했다."], ["He obviously attached no significance to Veslovsky’s chattering; on the contrary, he encouraged his jests.", "그는 베슬롭스키의 시시한 수다에 분명 아무런 의미도 두지 않았다. 오히려 그의 농담을 부추겼다."]], [["“Come now, tell us, Veslovsky, how are the stones held together?”", "\"자, 어디 말해 보시지, 베슬롭스키. 돌들은 어떻게 서로 붙어 있는 거요?\""]], [["“By cement, of course.”", "\"물론 시멘트로 붙어 있지요.\""]], [["“Bravo!", "\"훌륭해!"], ["And what is cement?”", "그럼 시멘트는 뭐요?\""]], [["“Oh, some sort of paste ...", "\"아, 무슨 풀 같은 거……"], ["no, putty,”", "아니, 퍼티지요.\""], ["said Veslovsky, raising a general laugh.", "베슬롭스키가 말하자 좌중에 웃음이 터졌다."]], [["The company at dinner, with the exception of the doctor, the architect, and the steward, who remained plunged in gloomy silence, kept up a conversation that never paused, glancing off one subject, fastening on another, and at times stinging one or the other to the quick.", "만찬에 모인 사람들은, 침울한 침묵에 잠겨 있는 의사와 건축가와 집사를 빼고는, 한순간도 멈추지 않는 대화를 이어 갔다. 한 화제를 슬쩍 건드리고 다른 화제를 붙잡으며, 때로는 이 사람 저 사람의 아픈 데를 콕콕 찔러 댔다."], ["Once Darya Alexandrovna felt wounded to the quick, and got so hot that she positively flushed and wondered afterwards whether she had said anything extreme or unpleasant.", "한번은 다리야 알렉산드로브나가 정곡을 찔려 마음에 상처를 입고는, 어찌나 달아올랐던지 얼굴까지 붉어졌고, 나중에 자기가 무언가 지나치거나 불쾌한 말을 하지 않았나 곱씹어 보았다."], ["Sviazhsky began talking of Levin, describing his strange view that machinery is simply pernicious in its effects on Russian agriculture.", "스비야시스키가 레빈에 대한 이야기를 꺼내며, 기계라는 것이 러시아 농업에 그저 해롭기만 하다는 그의 기이한 견해를 늘어놓기 시작했다."]], [["“I have not the pleasure of knowing this M.", "\"그 레빈 씨를 알게 되는 기쁨은 누리지 못했습니다만.\""], ["Levin,”", "브론스키가 미소를 지으며 말했다."], ["Vronsky said, smiling, “but most likely he has never seen the machines he condemns; or if he has seen and tried any, it must have been after a queer fashion, some Russian imitation, not a machine from abroad.", "\"아마 그분은 자기가 비난하는 그 기계들을 한 번도 본 적이 없을 겁니다. 설령 보고 써 봤다 해도, 분명 이상한 방식이었겠지요."], ["What sort of views can anyone have on such a subject?”", "외국산 기계가 아니라 러시아제 모조품 같은 걸 말입니다. 그런 문제에 대해 무슨 견해라는 걸 가질 수가 있겠습니까?\""]], [["“Turkish views, in general,”", "\"대체로 터키식 견해지요.\""], ["Veslovsky said, turning to Anna with a smile.", "베슬롭스키가 미소를 지으며 안나를 향해 돌아서서 말했다."]], [["“I can’t defend his opinions,” Darya Alexandrovna said, firing up; “but I can say that he’s a highly cultivated man, and if he were here he would know very well how to answer you, though I am not capable of doing so.”", "\"저는 그분의 의견을 변호할 수는 없어요.\" 다리야 알렉산드로브나가 발끈하여 말했다. \"하지만 그분이 대단히 교양 있는 분이라는 건 말할 수 있어요. 만약 그분이 여기 계셨다면 여러분께 어떻게 대답해야 할지 잘 아셨을 거예요. 저는 그렇게 못하지만요.\""]], [["“I like him extremely, and we are great friends,”", "\"저도 그분을 무척 좋아하고, 우린 아주 막역한 사이입니다.\""], ["Sviazhsky said, smiling good-naturedly.", "스비야시스키가 사람 좋은 미소를 지으며 말했다."], ["“Mais pardon, il est un petit peu toqué; he maintains, for instance, that district councils and arbitration boards are all of no use, and he is unwilling to take part in anything.”", "\"메 파르동, 일 레 텡 프티 푀 토케. 이를테면 그는 군 자치회나 중재 위원회 같은 게 죄다 쓸모없다고 주장하면서, 무슨 일에든 참여하려 들지 않으니까요.\""]], [["“It’s our Russian apathy,”", "\"그게 우리 러시아 사람들의 무기력이지요.\" 브론스키가 얼음을 채운 유리병의 물을 가늘고 높은 줄기가 달린 섬세한 잔에 따르며 말했다."], ["said Vronsky, pouring water from an iced decanter into a delicate glass on a high stem; “we’ve no sense of the duties our privileges impose upon us, and so we refuse to recognize these duties.”", "\"우리는 특권이 우리에게 지우는 의무에 대한 감각이 없어요. 그래서 그런 의무를 인정하기를 거부하는 거지요.\""]], [["“I know no man more strict in the performance of his duties,”", "\"저는 그분만큼 자기 의무 수행에 엄격한 사람을 알지 못해요.\""], ["said Darya Alexandrovna, irritated by Vronsky’s tone of superiority.", "브론스키의 우월한 어조에 화가 난 다리야 알렉산드로브나가 말했다."]], [["“For my part,”", "\"제 경우를 말씀드리면,\""], ["pursued Vronsky, who was evidently for some reason or other keenly affected by this conversation, “such as I am, I am, on the contrary, extremely grateful for the honor they have done me, thanks to Nikolay Ivanitch”", "브론스키가 말을 이었다."], ["(he indicated Sviazhsky), “in electing me a justice of the peace.", "그는 무슨 까닭에서인지 이 대화에 분명 깊이 자극받은 듯했다."], ["I consider that for me the duty of being present at the session, of judging some peasants’ quarrel about a horse, is as important as anything I can do.", "\"이런 저 같은 사람도, 오히려 반대로, 니콜라이 이바니치 덕분에\"(그는 스비야시스키를 가리켰다) \"치안 판사로 뽑히는 영예를 누리게 된 데 대해 무척 감사하고 있습니다. 회기에 출석하고, 말 한 마리를 둘러싼 농민들의 다툼을 판결하는 의무가, 제가 할 수 있는 그 무엇 못지않게 중요한 일이라고 생각합니다."], ["And I shall regard it as an honor if they elect me for the district council.", "그리고 만일 저를 군 자치회 의원으로 뽑아 준다면 영예로 여기겠습니다."], ["It’s only in that way I can pay for the advantages I enjoy as a landowner.", "그것만이 제가 지주로서 누리는 혜택에 대한 값을 치를 수 있는 길이니까요."], ["Unluckily they don’t understand the weight that the big landowners ought to have in the state.”", "불행히도 사람들은 대지주가 국가에서 마땅히 가져야 할 비중을 이해하지 못합니다.\""]], [["It was strange to Darya Alexandrovna to hear how serenely confident he was of being right at his own table.", "다리야 알렉산드로브나는 그가 자기 식탁에서 자기가 옳다고 그토록 태연하게 확신하는 것을 듣고 묘한 기분이 들었다."], ["She thought how Levin, who believed the opposite, was just as positive in his opinions at his own table.", "그녀는 정반대를 믿는 레빈도 자기 식탁에서 똑같이 단호하게 자기 의견을 펼치던 것을 떠올렸다."], ["But she loved Levin, and so she was on his side.", "하지만 그녀는 레빈을 사랑했기에 그의 편이었다."]], [["“So we can reckon upon you, count, for the coming elections?”", "\"그럼 백작, 이번 선거에서 당신을 믿어도 되겠지요?\""], ["said Sviazhsky.", "스비야시스키가 말했다."], ["“But you must come a little beforehand, so as to be on the spot by the eighth.", "\"하지만 좀 미리 오셔야 합니다. 8일에는 현장에 계실 수 있도록 말이지요."], ["If you would do me the honor to stop with me.”", "저희 집에 묵으시는 영광을 주신다면 좋겠는데요.\""]], [["“I rather agree with your beau-frère,”", "\"저는 형부 의견에 좀 동감이에요.\""], ["said Anna, “though not quite on the same ground as he,”", "안나가 말했다. \"그분과 똑같은 근거에서는 아니지만요.\""], ["she added with a smile.", "그녀가 미소를 지으며 덧붙였다."], ["“I’m afraid that we have too many of these public duties in these latter days.", "\"요즘 들어 우리한테 이런 공적 의무가 너무 많아진 게 아닌가 싶어요."], ["Just as in old days there were so many government functionaries that one had to call in a functionary for every single thing, so now everyone’s doing some sort of public duty.", "옛날엔 관리가 워낙 많아서 무슨 일이든 일일이 관리를 불러야 했던 것처럼, 이젠 누구나 무슨 공적 의무인지를 떠맡고 있잖아요."], ["Alexey has been here now six months, and he’s a member, I do believe, of five or six different public bodies.", "알렉세이는 여기 온 지 이제 여섯 달인데, 벌써 대여섯 군데 공공 단체의 회원이라니까요, 정말이지."], ["Du train que cela va, the whole time will be wasted on it.", "뒤 트랭 크 셀라 바, 이러다간 시간을 죄다 거기에 빼앗기고 말 거예요."], ["And I’m afraid that with such a multiplicity of these bodies, they’ll end in being a mere form.", "게다가 그런 단체가 그렇게 많아지면, 결국엔 한낱 형식으로 끝나 버리고 말까 봐 걱정이에요."], ["How many are you a member of, Nikolay Ivanitch?”", "당신은 몇 군데 회원이세요, 니콜라이 이바니치?\""], ["she turned to Sviazhsky—“over twenty, I fancy.”", "그녀가 스비야시스키를 향해 돌아섰다. \"스무 군데도 넘지요, 아마.\""]], [["Anna spoke lightly, but irritation could be discerned in her tone.", "안나는 가볍게 말했지만, 그 어조에서 짜증을 알아챌 수 있었다."], ["Darya Alexandrovna, watching Anna and Vronsky attentively, detected it instantly.", "안나와 브론스키를 주의 깊게 지켜보던 다리야 알렉산드로브나는 그것을 곧바로 간파했다."], ["She noticed, too, that as she spoke Vronsky’s face had immediately taken a serious and obstinate expression.", "그녀는 또한 안나가 그렇게 말하는 동안 브론스키의 얼굴이 이내 진지하고 완고한 표정을 띠는 것을 알아챘다."], ["Noticing this, and that Princess Varvara at once made haste to change the conversation by talking of Petersburg acquaintances, and remembering what Vronsky had without apparent connection said in the garden of his work in the country, Dolly surmised that this question of public activity was connected with some deep private disagreement between Anna and Vronsky.", "이것을 알아챈 데다, 바르바라 공작 영애가 곧장 서둘러 페테르부르크의 지인들 이야기를 꺼내 대화를 바꾸려 한 것, 그리고 브론스키가 정원에서 별다른 맥락도 없이 시골에서의 자기 일에 대해 했던 말을 떠올리며, 돌리는 이 공적 활동의 문제가 안나와 브론스키 사이의 깊은 사적 불화와 얽혀 있음을 짐작했다."]], [["The dinner, the wine, the decoration of the table were all very good; but it was all like what Darya Alexandrovna had seen at formal dinners and balls which of late years had become quite unfamiliar to her; it all had the same impersonal and constrained character, and so on an ordinary day and in a little circle of friends it made a disagreeable impression on her.", "만찬도, 포도주도, 식탁 장식도 모두 매우 훌륭했다. 하지만 그 모든 것이 최근 몇 년간 그녀에게는 아주 낯설어진 격식 차린 만찬과 무도회에서 다리야 알렉산드로브나가 보아 온 것과 똑같았다. 모두 똑같이 비인격적이고 거북한 성격을 띠고 있어서, 평범한 날 친한 사람들끼리의 자그마한 모임에서 그것은 그녀에게 불쾌한 인상을 주었다."]], [["After dinner they sat on the terrace, then they proceeded to play lawn tennis.", "만찬이 끝난 뒤 그들은 테라스에 앉아 있다가, 이어 론 테니스를 치러 나갔다."], ["The players, divided into two parties, stood on opposite sides of a tightly drawn net with gilt poles on the carefully leveled and rolled croquet-ground.", "선수들은 두 패로 나뉘어, 정성껏 고르고 다진 크로케 경기장 위에 도금한 기둥으로 팽팽하게 친 그물을 사이에 두고 양편으로 갈라섰다."], ["Darya Alexandrovna made an attempt to play, but it was a long time before she could understand the game, and by the time she did understand it, she was so tired that she sat down with Princess Varvara and simply looked on at the players.", "다리야 알렉산드로브나도 한번 쳐 보려 했지만, 한참 동안 경기 방식을 이해하지 못했고, 마침내 이해했을 무렵에는 너무 지쳐서 바르바라 공작 영애 곁에 앉아 그저 선수들을 구경했다."], ["Her partner, Tushkevitch, gave up playing too, but the others kept the game up for a long time.", "그녀의 짝인 투시케비치도 경기를 그만두었지만, 나머지 사람들은 오랫동안 경기를 이어 갔다."], ["Sviazhsky and Vronsky both played very well and seriously.", "스비야시스키와 브론스키는 둘 다 매우 잘 쳤고 진지했다."], ["They kept a sharp lookout on the balls served to them, and without haste or getting in each other’s way, they ran adroitly up to them, waited for the rebound, and neatly and accurately returned them over the net.", "그들은 자기 쪽으로 넘어오는 공을 날카롭게 주시하다가, 서두르거나 서로 방해하는 일 없이, 날렵하게 공으로 달려가 튀어 오르기를 기다렸다가 깔끔하고 정확하게 그물 너머로 되받아쳤다."], ["Veslovsky played worse than the others.", "베슬롭스키는 다른 사람들보다 못 쳤다."], ["He was too eager, but he kept the players lively with his high spirits.", "너무 안달했지만, 흥겨운 기분으로 선수들의 흥을 돋우었다."], ["His laughter and outcries never paused.", "그의 웃음소리와 외침이 끊이지 않았다."], ["Like the other men of the party, with the ladies’ permission, he took off his coat, and his solid, comely figure in his white shirt-sleeves, with his red perspiring face and his impulsive movements, made a picture that imprinted itself vividly on the memory.", "그는 다른 남자들처럼 부인들의 허락을 얻어 웃옷을 벗었는데, 흰 셔츠 소매 차림의 단단하고 잘생긴 몸매에, 땀에 젖어 벌건 얼굴과 충동적인 동작이 어우러져, 기억에 또렷이 새겨지는 한 폭의 그림을 이루었다."]], [["When Darya Alexandrovna lay in bed that night, as soon as she closed her eyes, she saw Vassenka Veslovsky flying about the croquet ground.", "그날 밤 다리야 알렉산드로브나는 침대에 누워 눈을 감자마자, 크로케 경기장을 이리저리 날아다니는 바센카 베슬롭스키의 모습이 떠올랐다."]], [["During the game Darya Alexandrovna was not enjoying herself.", "경기를 하는 동안 다리야 알렉산드로브나는 즐겁지 않았다."], ["She did not like the light tone of raillery that was kept up all the time between Vassenka Veslovsky and Anna, and the unnaturalness altogether of grown-up people, all alone without children, playing at a child’s game.", "바센카 베슬롭스키와 안나 사이에 줄곧 오가던 그 야유 섞인 가벼운 어조도 마음에 들지 않았고, 아이들도 없이 어른들끼리만 모여 아이들 놀이를 하는 것이 통 자연스럽지 않게 느껴졌다."], ["But to avoid breaking up the party and to get through the time somehow, after a rest she joined the game again, and pretended to be enjoying it.", "하지만 모임을 깨지 않으려고, 또 어떻게든 시간을 보내려고, 그녀는 잠시 쉰 뒤 다시 경기에 끼어들어 즐기는 척했다."], ["All that day it seemed to her as though she were acting in a theater with actors cleverer than she, and that her bad acting was spoiling the whole performance.", "그날 하루 종일 그녀는 자기보다 솜씨 좋은 배우들과 함께 극장에서 연기를 하고 있는데, 자기의 서툰 연기가 공연 전체를 망치고 있는 것만 같았다."], ["She had come with the intention of staying two days, if all went well.", "그녀는 일이 다 잘 풀리면 이틀쯤 머물 작정으로 왔었다."], ["But in the evening, during the game, she made up her mind that she would go home next day.", "그러나 저녁에 경기를 하는 동안, 이튿날 집으로 돌아가기로 마음을 굳혔다."], ["The maternal cares and worries, which she had so hated on the way, now, after a day spent without them, struck her in quite another light, and tempted her back to them.", "오는 길에 그토록 진저리를 쳤던 어머니로서의 근심과 걱정거리가, 그것 없이 하루를 보내고 나니 이제는 전혀 다른 빛깔로 다가와, 그녀를 다시 그리로 불러들이는 것이었다."]], [["When, after evening tea and a row by night in the boat, Darya Alexandrovna went alone to her room, took off her dress, and began arranging her thin hair for the night, she had a great sense of relief.", "저녁 차를 마시고 밤에 보트를 탄 뒤, 다리야 알렉산드로브나가 혼자 자기 방으로 가서 옷을 벗고 잠자리를 위해 가느다란 머리카락을 매만지기 시작했을 때, 그녀는 커다란 안도감을 느꼈다."]], [["It was positively disagreeable to her to think that Anna was coming to see her immediately.", "안나가 곧 자기를 보러 온다는 생각이 그녀에게는 정말이지 거북했다."], ["She longed to be alone with her own thoughts.", "그녀는 자기만의 생각에 잠겨 홀로 있고 싶었다."]], [["# Chapter 23", "# Chapter 23"]], [["Dolly was wanting to go to bed when Anna came in to see her, attired for the night.", "돌리가 막 잠자리에 들려던 참에 안나가 잠옷 차림으로 그녀를 보러 들어왔다."], ["In the course of the day Anna had several times begun to speak of matters near her heart, and every time after a few words she had stopped: “Afterwards, by ourselves, we’ll talk about everything.", "그날 하루 동안 안나는 마음속에 담아 둔 일들을 몇 번이나 이야기하려 했지만, 그때마다 몇 마디 하다가 멈추곤 했다. \"나중에, 우리 둘만 있을 때 모든 걸 다 이야기해요."], ["I’ve got so much I want to tell you,”", "당신한테 하고 싶은 말이 너무 많아요.\""], ["she said.", "그녀는 말했다."]], [["Now they were by themselves, and Anna did not know what to talk about.", "이제 둘만 있게 되었는데, 안나는 무슨 이야기를 해야 할지 알 수 없었다."], ["She sat in the window looking at Dolly, and going over in her own mind all the stores of intimate talk which had seemed so inexhaustible beforehand, and she found nothing.", "그녀는 창가에 앉아 돌리를 바라보며, 미리 그렇게도 끝이 없을 것 같던 내밀한 이야깃거리들을 마음속으로 하나하나 더듬어 보았지만, 아무것도 찾을 수 없었다."], ["At that moment it seemed to her that everything had been said already.", "그 순간 그녀에게는 모든 것이 이미 다 말해진 듯이 느껴졌다."]], [["“Well, what of Kitty?”", "\"그래, 키티는 어때요?\""], ["she said with a heavy sigh, looking penitently at Dolly.", "그녀는 무거운 한숨을 내쉬며, 뉘우치는 듯한 눈길로 돌리를 바라보며 말했다."], ["“Tell me the truth, Dolly: isn’t she angry with me?”", "\"사실대로 말해 줘요, 돌리. 그 애가 나한테 화가 나 있는 거 아니에요?\""]], [["“Angry? Oh, no!”", "\"화가 나 있다니요?"], ["said Darya Alexandrovna, smiling.", "아, 아니에요!\" 다리야 알렉산드로브나가 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["“But she hates me, despises me?”", "\"하지만 나를 미워하고, 경멸하겠죠?\""]], [["“Oh, no!", "\"아, 아니에요!"], ["But you know that sort of thing isn’t forgiven.”", "하지만 그런 일은 좀처럼 용서가 안 되는 거잖아요.\""]], [["“Yes, yes,”", "\"그래요, 그래.\""], ["said Anna, turning away and looking out of the open window.", "안나는 고개를 돌려 열린 창밖을 바라보며 말했다."], ["“But I was not to blame.", "\"하지만 내 잘못은 아니었어요."], ["And who is to blame?", "그럼 누구 잘못이죠?"], ["What’s the meaning of being to blame?", "잘못이라는 게 대체 뭘 뜻하는 걸까요?"], ["Could it have been otherwise?", "달리 어떻게 될 수도 있었을까요?"], ["What do you think?", "당신은 어떻게 생각해요?"], ["Could it possibly have happened that you didn’t become the wife of Stiva?”", "당신이 스티바의 아내가 되지 않는 일이 과연 일어날 수도 있었을까요?\""]], [["“Really, I don’t know.", "\"글쎄, 잘 모르겠어요."], ["But this is what I want you to tell me....”", "그런데 내가 당신한테 듣고 싶은 건 바로 이거예요…….\""]], [["“Yes, yes, but we’ve not finished about Kitty.", "\"그래요, 그래, 하지만 키티 얘기를 아직 다 못 끝냈잖아요."], ["Is she happy?", "그 애는 행복해요?"], ["He’s a very nice man, they say.”", "그 사람이 아주 좋은 사람이라던데.\""]], [["“He’s much more than very nice.", "\"아주 좋은 정도가 아니에요."], ["I don’t know a better man.”", "그보다 더 나은 사람을 본 적이 없어요.\""]], [["“Ah, how glad I am!", "\"아, 정말 기뻐요!"], ["I’m so glad!", "너무 기뻐요!"], ["Much more than very nice,”", "아주 좋은 정도가 아니라니.\""], ["she repeated.", "그녀는 되뇌었다."]], [["Dolly smiled.", "돌리가 미소 지었다."]], [["“But tell me about yourself.", "\"하지만 당신 얘기를 들려줘요."], ["We’ve a great deal to talk about.", "우리 할 이야기가 정말 많잖아요."], ["And I’ve had a talk with....”", "그리고 나는 이미……와 이야기를 나눴어요.\""], ["Dolly did not know what to call him.", "돌리는 그를 무어라 불러야 할지 몰랐다."], ["She felt it awkward to call him either the count or Alexey Kirillovitch.", "백작이라 부르기도, 알렉세이 키릴로비치라 부르기도 어색했다."]], [["“With Alexey,”", "\"알렉세이와요.\""], ["said Anna, “I know what you talked about.", "안나가 말했다. \"무슨 이야기를 나눴는지 알아요."], ["But I wanted to ask you directly what you think of me, of my life?”", "하지만 나는 당신한테 직접 묻고 싶어요. 나에 대해, 내 삶에 대해 어떻게 생각하는지.\""]], [["“How am I to say like that straight off?", "\"어떻게 그걸 대뜸 말하겠어요?"], ["I really don’t know.”", "나도 정말 잘 모르겠어요.\""]], [["“No, tell me all the same....", "\"아니에요, 그래도 다 말해 줘요……"], ["You see my life.", "당신은 내 삶을 보고 있잖아요."], ["But you mustn’t forget that you’re seeing us in the summer, when you have come to us and we are not alone....", "하지만 한 가지 잊지 말아요. 당신이 우리를 보고 있는 건 여름이고, 당신이 우리한테 와 있어서 우리가 둘만 있는 게 아니라는 걸……"], ["But we came here early in the spring, lived quite alone, and shall be alone again, and I desire nothing better.", "우리는 이른 봄에 이리로 와서 아주 단둘이 지냈고, 또다시 단둘이 지내게 될 거예요. 나는 그보다 더 좋은 걸 바라지 않아요."], ["But imagine me living alone without him, alone, and that will be ...", "하지만 그 사람 없이 나 혼자 사는 모습을 상상해 봐요, 혼자서, 그렇게 되면……"], ["I see by everything that it will often be repeated, that he will be half the time away from home,”", "모든 걸로 미루어 보아 그런 일이 자주 되풀이될 거예요. 그 사람이 절반은 집을 비울 테니까요.\""], ["she said, getting up and sitting down close by Dolly.", "그녀는 자리에서 일어나 돌리 곁으로 바짝 다가와 앉으며 말했다."]], [["“Of course,”", "\"물론.\""], ["she interrupted Dolly, who would have answered, “of course I won’t try to keep him by force.", "그녀는 무언가 대답하려는 돌리의 말을 가로막았다. \"물론 나는 그 사람을 억지로 붙들어 두려고 하지 않을 거예요."], ["I don’t keep him indeed.", "사실 붙들어 두지도 않고요."], ["The races are just coming, his horses are running, he will go.", "곧 경마가 열려서, 그 사람의 말들이 출전하니까, 그 사람은 갈 거예요."], ["I’m very glad.", "나는 아주 기뻐요."], ["But think of me, fancy my position....", "하지만 내 입장을, 내 처지를 한번 생각해 봐요……"], ["But what’s the use of talking about it?”", "하지만 그런 이야기를 한들 무슨 소용이 있겠어요?\""], ["She smiled.", "그녀는 미소 지었다."], ["“Well, what did he talk about with you?”", "\"그래, 그 사람이 당신한테 무슨 이야기를 했어요?\""]], [["“He spoke of what I want to speak about of myself, and it’s easy for me to be his advocate; of whether there is not a possibility ...", "\"그분은 내가 나 자신에 대해 말하고 싶은 바로 그것을 이야기했어요. 그래서 그분의 대변자가 되어 주기가 나로서는 쉬워요."], ["whether you could not....”", "그러니까 어떤 가능성이 있지 않겠느냐는 거……"], ["(Darya Alexandrovna hesitated) “correct, improve your position....", "당신이 혹시…….\" (다리야 알렉산드로브나는 머뭇거렸다) \"당신의 처지를 바로잡고, 나아지게 할 수 없겠느냐는 거예요……"], ["You know how I look at it....", "내가 이 일을 어떻게 보는지 당신도 알잖아요……"], ["But all the same, if possible, you should get married....”", "하지만 그래도, 가능하다면, 당신은 결혼을 해야 해요…….\""]], [["“Divorce, you mean?”", "\"이혼 말이죠?\" 안나가 말했다."], ["said Anna.", "\"당신 알아요?"], ["“Do you know, the only woman who came to see me in Petersburg was Betsy Tverskaya?", "페테르부르크에서 나를 보러 온 단 한 사람의 여자가 베치 트베르스카야였어요."], ["You know her, of course?", "당신도 그 여자를 알죠?"], ["Au fond, c’est la femme la plus depravée qui existe.", "Au fond, c’est la femme la plus dépravée qui existe."], ["She had an intrigue with Tushkevitch, deceiving her husband in the basest way.", "그 여자는 투시케비치와 정을 통하면서 자기 남편을 가장 비열한 방식으로 속였어요."], ["And she told me that she did not care to know me so long as my position was irregular.", "그런데 그 여자가 나한테 그러더군요, 내 처지가 떳떳하지 못한 동안에는 나와 알고 지낼 마음이 없다고요. 내가 비교하려는 거라고는 생각하지 말아요……"], ["Don’t imagine I would compare ...", "나는 당신을 알아요, 자기."], ["I know you, darling.", "하지만 그 일이 떠오르는 걸 어쩔 수 없었어요……"], ["But I could not help remembering....", "그래, 그 사람이 당신한테 뭐라고 했어요?\" 그녀는 다시 물었다."], ["Well, so what did he say to you?” she repeated.", "* Au fond, c’est la femme la plus dépravée qui existe: 프랑스어로 '사실 그 여자는 세상에서 가장 타락한 여자예요.'"]], [["“He said that he was unhappy on your account and his own.", "\"그분은 당신 때문에, 또 자기 자신 때문에 불행하다고 했어요."], ["Perhaps you will say that it’s egoism, but what a legitimate and noble egoism.", "어쩌면 당신은 그게 이기심이라고 말할지도 모르지만, 정당하고 고귀한 이기심이잖아요."], ["He wants first of all to legitimize his daughter, and to be your husband, to have a legal right to you.”", "그분은 무엇보다 자기 딸을 적자로 인정받게 하고, 당신의 남편이 되어, 당신에 대한 법적인 권리를 갖고 싶어 해요.\""]], [["“What wife, what slave can be so utterly a slave as I, in my position?”", "\"내 처지에 놓인 나만큼 철저하게 노예일 수 있는 아내가, 노예가 어디 있겠어요?\""], ["she put in gloomily.", "그녀는 침울하게 끼어들었다."]], [["“The chief thing he desires ...", "\"그분이 가장 바라는 건……"], ["he desires that you should not suffer.”", "당신이 괴로워하지 않는 거예요.\""]], [["“That’s impossible.", "\"그건 불가능해요."], ["Well?”", "그래서요?\""]], [["“Well, and the most legitimate desire—he wishes that your children should have a name.”", "\"그리고, 가장 정당한 바람인데, 그분은 당신의 아이들이 떳떳한 성을 갖기를 바라요.\""]], [["“What children?”", "\"무슨 아이들이요?\""], ["Anna said, not looking at Dolly, and half closing her eyes.", "안나는 돌리를 보지 않고, 눈을 반쯤 감은 채 말했다."]], [["“Annie and those to come....”", "\"애니와, 또 앞으로 태어날 아이들 말이에요…….\""]], [["“He need not trouble on that score; I shall have no more children.”", "\"그 일이라면 그 사람이 걱정할 필요 없어요. 나는 더 이상 아이를 갖지 않을 테니까.\""]], [["“How can you tell that you won’t?”", "\"당신이 갖지 않으리라는 걸 어떻게 알아요?\""]], [["“I shall not, because I don’t wish it.”", "\"갖지 않을 거예요, 내가 원하지 않으니까.\""], ["And, in spite of all her emotion, Anna smiled, as she caught the naïve expression of curiosity, wonder, and horror on Dolly’s face.", "그리고 온갖 격한 감정에도 불구하고, 안나는 돌리의 얼굴에 떠오른 그 순진한 호기심과 놀라움, 경악의 표정을 알아채고는 미소 지었다."]], [["“The doctor told me after my illness....”", "\"병을 앓고 난 뒤에 의사가 나한테 말해 줬어요…….\""]], [["“Impossible!”", "\"설마요!\""], ["said Dolly, opening her eyes wide.", "돌리가 눈을 휘둥그레 뜨며 말했다."]], [["For her this was one of those discoveries the consequences and deductions from which are so immense that all that one feels for the first instant is that it is impossible to take it all in, and that one will have to reflect a great, great deal upon it.", "그녀에게 이것은, 그로부터 비롯되는 결과와 추론이 너무도 엄청나서, 첫 순간에 느낄 수 있는 거라고는 그 모든 것을 도무지 다 받아들일 수 없다는 것과 그것에 대해 아주, 아주 많이 곱씹어 보아야 하리라는 것뿐인, 그런 발견들 가운데 하나였다."]], [["This discovery, suddenly throwing light on all those families of one or two children, which had hitherto been so incomprehensible to her, aroused so many ideas, reflections, and contradictory emotions, that she had nothing to say, and simply gazed with wide-open eyes of wonder at Anna.", "이 발견은, 지금까지 그녀로서는 도무지 이해할 수 없었던 그 아이 하나둘짜리 가정들에 갑자기 빛을 비추어, 너무도 많은 생각과 성찰, 그리고 서로 모순되는 감정을 불러일으킨 탓에, 그녀는 아무 말도 하지 못한 채 그저 놀라움에 눈을 크게 뜨고 안나를 바라보기만 했다. 이것이야말로 그녀가 꿈꿔 오던 바로 그것이었지만, 막상 그것이 가능하다는 사실을 알게 되자 그녀는 소름이 끼쳤다."], ["This was the very thing she had been dreaming of, but now learning that it was possible, she was horrified.", "너무도 복잡한 문제에 대한 너무도 간단한 해결책이라는 느낌이 들었던 것이다."], ["She felt that it was too simple a solution of too complicated a problem.", "\"N’est-ce pas immoral?\" 그녀는 잠시 사이를 두었다가 겨우 그 말만 했다."]], [["“N’est-ce pas immoral?” was all she said, after a brief pause.", "* N’est-ce pas immoral?: 프랑스어로 '그건 부도덕한 일 아니에요?'"]], [["“Why so?", "\"왜 그렇죠?"], ["Think, I have a choice between two alternatives: either to be with child, that is an invalid, or to be the friend and companion of my husband—practically my husband,”", "생각해 봐요, 나에게는 두 가지 갈림길밖에 없어요. 아이를 배든가, 다시 말해 환자가 되든가, 아니면 내 남편의, 사실상 내 남편인 그 사람의 벗이자 반려가 되든가.\""], ["Anna said in a tone intentionally superficial and frivolous.", "안나는 일부러 가볍고 경박한 어조로 말했다."]], [["“Yes, yes,”", "\"그래요, 그래.\""], ["said Darya Alexandrovna, hearing the very arguments she had used to herself, and not finding the same force in them as before.", "다리야 알렉산드로브나는 자기가 스스로에게 써먹던 바로 그 논리를 들으면서, 그 안에서 예전만큼의 힘을 느끼지 못한 채 말했다."]], [["“For you, for other people,”", "\"당신에게는, 다른 사람들에게는.\" 안나는 마치 그녀의 생각을 꿰뚫어 보기라도 한 듯이 말했다."], ["said Anna, as though divining her thoughts, “there may be reason to hesitate; but for me....", "\"망설일 까닭이 있을지도 몰라요. 하지만 나에게는……"], ["You must consider, I am not his wife; he loves me as long as he loves me.", "한번 생각해 봐요, 나는 그 사람의 아내가 아니에요. 그 사람은 자기가 나를 사랑하는 동안에만 나를 사랑해요."], ["And how am I to keep his love?", "그러니 내가 어떻게 그 사람의 사랑을 붙들어 두겠어요?"], ["Not like this!”", "이런 모습으로는 안 돼요!\""]], [["She moved her white hands in a curve before her waist with extraordinary rapidity, as happens during moments of excitement; ideas and memories rushed into Darya Alexandrovna’s head.", "그녀는 흥분의 순간에 흔히 그러하듯 더없이 빠른 동작으로, 자기 허리 앞에서 하얀 두 손을 곡선을 그리며 움직였다. 다리야 알렉산드로브나의 머릿속으로 온갖 생각과 기억이 밀려들었다."], ["“I,”", "'나는,'"], ["she thought, “did not keep my attraction for Stiva; he left me for others, and the first woman for whom he betrayed me did not keep him by being always pretty and lively.", "그녀는 생각했다. '나는 스티바를 끌어당기던 매력을 지키지 못했어."], ["He deserted her and took another.", "그이는 다른 여자들을 찾아 나를 떠났지."], ["And can Anna attract and keep Count Vronsky in that way?", "그리고 그이가 나를 배신하며 처음 택했던 여자도, 늘 예쁘고 활기차게 군다고 해서 그이를 붙들어 두지는 못했어. 그이는 그 여자를 버리고 또 다른 여자를 택했지."], ["If that is what he looks for, he will find dresses and manners still more attractive and charming.", "그런데 안나가 과연 그런 식으로 브론스키 백작을 끌어당기고 붙들어 둘 수 있을까? 만약 그이가 찾는 게 그런 거라면, 더 매력적이고 사랑스러운 옷차림과 몸가짐을 얼마든지 찾아낼 거야."], ["And however white and beautiful her bare arms are, however beautiful her full figure and her eager face under her black curls, he will find something better still, just as my disgusting, pitiful, and charming husband does.”", "그리고 저 여자의 드러난 두 팔이 아무리 희고 아름답다 해도, 검은 곱슬머리 아래 풍만한 몸매와 생기 넘치는 얼굴이 아무리 아름답다 해도, 그이는 더 나은 무언가를 또 찾아내고 말 거야. 꼭 그 역겹고 가엾고 사랑스러운 내 남편이 그러는 것처럼.'"]], [["Dolly made no answer, she merely sighed.", "돌리는 아무 대답도 하지 않고, 그저 한숨을 내쉬었다."], ["Anna noticed this sigh, indicating dissent, and she went on.", "안나는 동의하지 않음을 드러내는 그 한숨을 알아채고 말을 이어 갔다."], ["In her armory she had other arguments so strong that no answer could be made to them.", "그녀의 무기고에는 어떤 반박도 할 수 없을 만큼 강력한 다른 논거들이 있었다."]], [["“Do you say that it’s not right?", "\"그게 옳지 않다고 말하는 거예요?"], ["But you must consider,”", "하지만 한번 생각해 봐요.\""], ["she went on; “you forget my position.", "그녀는 말을 이었다. \"당신은 내 처지를 잊고 있어요."], ["How can I desire children?", "내가 어떻게 아이들을 바랄 수 있겠어요?"], ["I’m not speaking of the suffering, I’m not afraid of that.", "나는 고통을 두고 하는 말이 아니에요, 그건 두렵지 않아요."], ["Think only, what are my children to be?", "다만 생각해 봐요, 내 아이들이 어떤 존재가 될지를."], ["Ill-fated children, who will have to bear a stranger’s name.", "남의 성을 짊어지고 살아가야 할, 불운한 아이들이에요."], ["For the very fact of their birth they will be forced to be ashamed of their mother, their father, their birth.”", "태어났다는 그 사실 하나만으로, 그 아이들은 자기 어머니를, 자기 아버지를, 자기 출생을 부끄러워하지 않을 수 없게 될 거예요.\""]], [["“But that is just why a divorce is necessary.”", "\"하지만 바로 그래서 이혼이 꼭 필요한 거예요.\""], ["But Anna did not hear her.", "그러나 안나는 그 말을 듣지 못했다."], ["She longed to give utterance to all the arguments with which she had so many times convinced herself.", "그녀는 그토록 여러 번 스스로를 납득시켰던 그 모든 논거를 한꺼번에 쏟아 내고 싶었다."]], [["“What is reason given me for, if I am not to use it to avoid bringing unhappy beings into the world!”", "\"내가 이성을 받았는데, 그 이성을 불행한 존재들을 세상에 데려오는 일을 막는 데 쓰지 않는다면, 그게 다 무슨 소용이겠어요!\""], ["She looked at Dolly, but without waiting for a reply she went on:", "그녀는 돌리를 바라보았지만, 대답을 기다리지 않고 말을 이었다."]], [["“I should always feel I had wronged these unhappy children,”", "\"나는 언제나 그 불행한 아이들에게 못할 짓을 했다고 느낄 거예요.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“If they are not, at any rate they are not unhappy; while if they are unhappy, I alone should be to blame for it.”", "\"그 아이들이 존재하지 않는다면, 적어도 불행하지는 않을 테니까요. 그런데 그 아이들이 불행하다면, 그건 오로지 나 혼자만의 잘못이 되는 거예요.\""]], [["These were the very arguments Darya Alexandrovna had used in her own reflections; but she heard them without understanding them.", "이것은 다리야 알렉산드로브나가 스스로 곱씹던 바로 그 논거였다. 하지만 그녀는 그 말을 들으면서도 그것을 이해하지 못했다."], ["“How can one wrong creatures that don’t exist?”", "'어떻게 존재하지도 않는 생명에게 못할 짓을 할 수 있단 말이지?'"], ["she thought.", "그녀는 생각했다."], ["And all at once the idea struck her: could it possibly, under any circumstances, have been better for her favorite Grisha if he had never existed?", "그러다 문득 한 가지 생각이 떠올랐다. 과연 어떤 경우에든, 사랑하는 그리샤가 아예 태어나지 않았더라면 그 애에게 더 나았을까?"], ["And this seemed to her so wild, so strange, that she shook her head to drive away this tangle of whirling, mad ideas.", "그리고 이것이 그녀에게는 너무도 터무니없고 기이하게 느껴져서, 그녀는 빙빙 도는 미친 생각들의 이 엉킨 실타래를 떨쳐 내려고 고개를 가로저었다."]], [["“No, I don’t know; it’s not right,”", "\"아니에요, 모르겠어요. 그건 옳지 않아요.\""], ["was all she said, with an expression of disgust on her face.", "그녀는 얼굴에 혐오의 표정을 띤 채 그 말만 했다."]], [["“Yes, but you mustn’t forget that you and I....", "\"그래요, 하지만 당신과 나는…… 그걸 잊으면 안 돼요."], ["And besides that,”", "게다가,\""], ["added Anna, in spite of the wealth of her arguments and the poverty of Dolly’s objections, seeming still to admit that it was not right, “don’t forget the chief point, that I am not now in the same position as you.", "안나는 자기 논거의 풍성함과 돌리가 내놓는 반박의 빈약함에도 불구하고, 여전히 그것이 옳지 않다고 인정하는 듯한 태도로 덧붙였다. \"가장 중요한 점을 잊지 말아요, 지금 나는 당신과 같은 처지에 있지 않다는 걸요."], ["For you the question is: do you desire not to have any more children; while for me it is: do I desire to have them?", "당신에게 문제는 이거예요. 당신이 더 이상 아이를 갖지 않기를 바라느냐는 것."], ["And that’s a great difference.", "그런데 나에게 문제는 이거죠. 내가 아이를 갖기를 바라느냐는 것."], ["You must see that I can’t desire it in my position.”", "그건 엄청난 차이예요. 내 처지에서는 내가 그걸 바랄 수 없다는 걸 당신도 알아야 해요.\""]], [["Darya Alexandrovna made no reply.", "다리야 알렉산드로브나는 아무 대답도 하지 않았다."], ["She suddenly felt that she had got far away from Anna; that there lay between them a barrier of questions on which they could never agree, and about which it was better not to speak.", "그녀는 문득 자기가 안나에게서 멀찍이 떨어져 버렸다는 것을, 두 사람 사이에는 결코 의견이 일치할 수 없는, 그래서 차라리 입에 올리지 않는 편이 나은 문제들의 장벽이 가로놓여 있다는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["# Chapter 24", "# Chapter 24"]], [["“Then there is all the more reason for you to legalize your position, if possible,”", "\"그럼 가능하다면 당신의 처지를 합법적인 것으로 만들어야 할 이유가 더더욱 분명해지는 거예요.\""], ["said Dolly.", "돌리가 말했다."]], [["“Yes, if possible,”", "\"그래, 가능하다면 말이지.\""], ["said Anna, speaking all at once in an utterly different tone, subdued and mournful.", "안나가 별안간 전혀 다른 어조로, 가라앉은 슬픈 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“Surely you don’t mean a divorce is impossible?", "\"설마 이혼이 불가능하다는 건 아니겠죠?"], ["I was told your husband had consented to it.”", "당신 남편이 이혼에 동의했다고 들었는데요.\""]], [["“Dolly, I don’t want to talk about that.”", "\"돌리, 나 그 얘기는 하고 싶지 않아.\""]], [["“Oh, we won’t then,” Darya Alexandrovna hastened to say, noticing the expression of suffering on Anna’s face. “All I see is that you take too gloomy a view of things.”", "\"오, 그럼 그만두죠.\" 다리아 알렉산드로브나는 안나의 얼굴에 떠오른 고통스러운 표정을 알아채고는 서둘러 말했다. \"다만 내 눈에는 당신이 모든 걸 너무 어둡게만 보는 것 같아서요.\" \"내가? 천만에! 난 늘 밝고 행복한걸. 봐, je fais des passions. 베슬로프스키만 해도……\""]], [["“I? Not at all! I’m always bright and happy. You see, je fais des passions. Veslovsky....”", "* je fais des passions: 프랑스어로 '나는 사람들의 마음을 사로잡지'라는 뜻."]], [["“Yes, to tell the truth, I don’t like Veslovsky’s tone,”", "\"그래, 솔직히 말하면 난 베슬로프스키의 그 말투가 마음에 안 들어.\""], ["said Darya Alexandrovna, anxious to change the subject.", "다리아 알렉산드로브나가 화제를 바꾸고 싶어 하며 말했다."]], [["“Oh, that’s nonsense!", "\"오, 그건 말도 안 되는 소리야!"], ["It amuses Alexey, and that’s all; but he’s a boy, and quite under my control.", "그저 알렉세이를 즐겁게 해 줄 뿐, 그게 전부야. 게다가 그는 어린애 같아서 내 손바닥 안에 있는걸."], ["You know, I turn him as I please.", "알겠지만, 난 그를 내 마음대로 다룬다고."], ["It’s just as it might be with your Grisha....", "당신네 그리샤하고 똑같은 셈이지…… 돌리!\""], ["Dolly!”—she suddenly changed the subject—“you say I take too gloomy a view of things.", "그녀는 불쑥 화제를 바꾸었다. \"당신은 내가 모든 걸 너무 어둡게 본다고 하지."], ["You can’t understand.", "당신은 이해할 수 없어."], ["It’s too awful!", "이건 너무나 끔찍해!"], ["I try not to take any view of it at all.”", "난 차라리 아무 시각도 갖지 않으려고 애쓰는 거야.\""]], [["“But I think you ought to.", "\"하지만 내 생각엔 그래선 안 돼."], ["You ought to do all you can.”", "할 수 있는 건 다 해 봐야 해.\""]], [["“But what can I do? Nothing.", "\"하지만 내가 뭘 할 수 있겠어?"], ["You tell me to marry Alexey, and say I don’t think about it.", "아무것도 못 해. 당신은 나더러 알렉세이와 결혼하라고 하면서, 내가 그걸 생각하지 않는다고 하지."], ["I don’t think about it!”", "내가 그걸 생각하지 않는다고!\""], ["she repeated, and a flush rose into her face.", "그녀는 거듭 말했고, 얼굴에 홍조가 번졌다."], ["She got up, straightening her chest, and sighed heavily.", "그녀는 가슴을 곧게 펴며 일어나 무겁게 한숨을 쉬었다."], ["With her light step she began pacing up and down the room, stopping now and then.", "그녀는 가벼운 발걸음으로 방 안을 이리저리 거닐며 이따금 멈춰 섰다."], ["“I don’t think of it?", "\"내가 그걸 생각하지 않는다고?"], ["Not a day, not an hour passes that I don’t think of it, and blame myself for thinking of it ...", "그걸 생각하지 않고, 또 그걸 생각한다는 이유로 나 자신을 책망하지 않는 날이, 단 한 시간도 없는데……"], ["because thinking of that may drive me mad.", "그걸 생각하면 미쳐 버릴 것 같으니까."], ["Drive me mad!” she repeated.", "미쳐 버릴 것 같단 말이야!\""], ["“When I think of it, I can’t sleep without morphine.", "그녀는 되풀이했다. \"그걸 생각하면 모르핀 없이는 잠을 잘 수가 없어."], ["But never mind.", "하지만 됐어."], ["Let us talk quietly.", "차분하게 얘기하자고."], ["They tell me, divorce.", "사람들은 나더러 이혼하라고 하지."], ["In the first place, he won’t give me a divorce.", "우선, 그이는 내게 이혼을 해 주지 않을 거야."], ["He’s under the influence of Countess Lidia Ivanovna now.”", "지금 그이는 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인의 영향력 아래에 있거든.\""]], [["Darya Alexandrovna, sitting erect on a chair, turned her head, following Anna with a face of sympathetic suffering.", "다리아 알렉산드로브나는 의자에 꼿꼿이 앉은 채로 고개를 돌려, 동정 어린 괴로운 얼굴로 안나의 뒤를 좇았다."]], [["“You ought to make the attempt,”", "\"그래도 시도는 해 봐야 해.\""], ["she said softly.", "그녀가 부드럽게 말했다."]], [["“Suppose I make the attempt.", "\"내가 시도를 한다고 치자."], ["What does it mean?”", "그게 무슨 뜻일까?\""], ["she said, evidently giving utterance to a thought, a thousand times thought over and learned by heart.", "그녀는 천 번도 더 곱씹어 외운 어떤 생각을 입 밖으로 내뱉는 게 분명해 보였다."], ["“It means that I, hating him, but still recognizing that I have wronged him—and I consider him magnanimous—that I humiliate myself to write to him....", "\"그건 내가, 그이를 미워하면서도 여전히 내가 그이에게 잘못을 저질렀다는 걸 인정하면서—나는 그이가 너그러운 사람이라고 여기거든—그러니까 내가 나 자신을 낮추어 그이에게 편지를 쓴다는 뜻이야……"], ["Well, suppose I make the effort; I do it.", "좋아, 그렇게 애써 본다고 치자. 내가 그렇게 한다고 치자고."], ["Either I receive a humiliating refusal or consent....", "그럼 나는 모욕적인 거절을 당하거나 동의를 받겠지……"], ["Well, I have received his consent, say....”", "좋아, 그이의 동의를 받았다고 치자……\""], ["Anna was at that moment at the furthest end of the room, and she stopped there, doing something to the curtain at the window.", "안나는 그 순간 방의 가장 먼 끝에 가 있었고, 거기서 멈춰 서서 창가의 커튼을 만지작거리며 무언가를 하고 있었다."], ["“I receive his consent, but my ...", "\"그이의 동의를 받았다고 쳐. 하지만 내……"], ["my son?", "내 아들은?"], ["They won’t give him up to me.", "그들은 그 애를 내게 내주지 않을 거야."], ["He will grow up despising me, with his father, whom I’ve abandoned.", "그 애는 내가 버린 아버지 곁에서 나를 경멸하며 자라겠지."], ["Do you see, I love ...", "알겠어, 난 사랑해……"], ["equally, I think, but both more than myself—two creatures, Seryozha and Alexey.”", "똑같이, 그래 똑같이 사랑하는 것 같아, 하지만 둘 다 나 자신보다 더 사랑해—그 두 존재, 세료자와 알렉세이를.\""]], [["She came out into the middle of the room and stood facing Dolly, with her arms pressed tightly across her chest.", "그녀는 방 한가운데로 나와 돌리를 마주 보고 섰고, 두 팔을 가슴에 단단히 포갰다."], ["In her white dressing gown her figure seemed more than usually grand and broad.", "하얀 실내복을 입은 그녀의 자태는 평소보다 더 위풍당당하고 풍만해 보였다."], ["She bent her head, and with shining, wet eyes looked from under her brows at Dolly, a thin little pitiful figure in her patched dressing jacket and nightcap, shaking all over with emotion.", "그녀는 고개를 숙인 채, 빛나는 젖은 눈으로 눈썹 아래에서 돌리를 바라보았다. 기운 실내복 윗도리에 잠자리 모자를 쓴, 가냘프고 가엾은 작은 모습으로 온몸을 떨며 격정에 사로잡힌 돌리를."]], [["“It is only those two creatures that I love, and one excludes the other.", "\"내가 사랑하는 건 오직 그 두 존재뿐인데, 한쪽이 다른 한쪽을 밀어내."], ["I can’t have them together, and that’s the only thing I want.", "난 그 둘을 함께 둘 수가 없어, 그런데 그게 내가 원하는 단 하나뿐인 거야."], ["And since I can’t have that, I don’t care about the rest.", "그리고 그걸 가질 수 없으니, 나머지는 아무래도 상관없어."], ["I don’t care about anything, anything.", "아무것도, 아무것도 상관없다고."], ["And it will end one way or another, and so I can’t, I don’t like to talk of it.", "그러니 이건 어떻게든 한쪽으로 끝나 버릴 테고, 그래서 난 이 얘기를 할 수가 없어, 하고 싶지가 않아."], ["So don’t blame me, don’t judge me for anything.", "그러니 나를 책망하지 마, 무슨 일이든 나를 판단하지 마."], ["You can’t with your pure heart understand all that I’m suffering.”", "당신의 그 순결한 마음으로는 내가 겪는 이 모든 고통을 이해할 수 없을 테니까.\""], ["She went up, sat down beside Dolly, and with a guilty look, peeped into her face and took her hand.", "그녀는 다가와 돌리 곁에 앉았고, 죄지은 듯한 표정으로 돌리의 얼굴을 들여다보며 그 손을 잡았다."]], [["“What are you thinking?", "\"무슨 생각을 해?"], ["What are you thinking about me?", "나에 대해 무슨 생각을 하는 거야?"], ["Don’t despise me.", "나를 경멸하지 마."], ["I don’t deserve contempt.", "난 경멸받을 만한 사람이 아니야."], ["I’m simply unhappy.", "난 그저 불행할 뿐이야."], ["If anyone is unhappy, I am,”", "누군가 불행한 사람이 있다면, 그게 바로 나야.\""], ["she articulated, and turning away, she burst into tears.", "그녀는 또박또박 말하더니, 고개를 돌리며 울음을 터뜨렸다."]], [["Left alone, Darya Alexandrovna said her prayers and went to bed.", "홀로 남은 다리아 알렉산드로브나는 기도를 올리고 잠자리에 들었다."], ["She had felt for Anna with all her heart while she was speaking to her, but now she could not force herself to think of her.", "안나와 이야기를 나누는 동안에는 온 마음으로 그녀를 가엾게 여겼지만, 이제는 그녀를 떠올리려 해도 도무지 마음이 가지 않았다."], ["The memories of home and of her children rose up in her imagination with a peculiar charm quite new to her, with a sort of new brilliance.", "집과 아이들에 대한 기억이 그녀의 상상 속에 떠올랐는데, 그것은 그녀에게 전혀 새로운, 어떤 독특한 매력과 함께, 일종의 새로운 광채를 띠고 있었다."], ["That world of her own seemed to her now so sweet and precious that she would not on any account spend an extra day outside it, and she made up her mind that she would certainly go back next day.", "그녀 자신의 그 세계가 이제는 너무나 달콤하고 소중하게 느껴져서, 무슨 일이 있어도 그 바깥에서 하루도 더 보내고 싶지 않았고, 그래서 그녀는 반드시 다음 날 돌아가리라 마음을 굳혔다."]], [["Anna meantime went back to her boudoir, took a wine-glass and dropped into it several drops of a medicine, of which the principal ingredient was morphine.", "그사이 안나는 자기 내실로 돌아가 포도주잔을 집어 들고는 거기에 약을 몇 방울 떨어뜨렸는데, 그 주된 성분은 모르핀이었다."], ["After drinking it off and sitting still a little while, she went into her bedroom in a soothed and more cheerful frame of mind.", "그것을 단숨에 들이켜고 잠시 가만히 앉아 있던 그녀는, 마음이 진정되고 한결 밝아진 기분으로 침실로 들어갔다."]], [["When she went into the bedroom, Vronsky looked intently at her.", "그녀가 침실로 들어서자 브론스키는 그녀를 유심히 바라보았다."], ["He was looking for traces of the conversation which he knew that, staying so long in Dolly’s room, she must have had with her.", "그는 그녀가 돌리의 방에 그토록 오래 머물렀으니 분명 나누었을 그 대화의 흔적을 찾고 있었다."], ["But in her expression of restrained excitement, and of a sort of reserve, he could find nothing but the beauty that always bewitched him afresh though he was used to it, the consciousness of it, and the desire that it should affect him.", "그러나 그녀의 절제된 흥분과 어떤 조심스러움이 깃든 표정에서 그가 찾아낼 수 있는 것이라곤, 익숙해졌으면서도 늘 새롭게 그를 매혹하는 그 아름다움과, 그 아름다움에 대한 자각과, 그것이 그에게 영향을 미치기를 바라는 욕망뿐이었다."], ["He did not want to ask her what they had been talking of, but he hoped that she would tell him something of her own accord.", "그는 그녀에게 무슨 이야기를 나누었느냐고 묻고 싶지 않았지만, 그녀가 자기 입으로 뭔가 말해 주기를 바랐다."], ["But she only said:", "하지만 그녀는 이렇게만 말했다."]], [["“I am so glad you like Dolly.", "\"당신이 돌리를 마음에 들어 해서 정말 기뻐."], ["You do, don’t you?”", "마음에 들지, 그렇지?\""]], [["“Oh, I’ve known her a long while, you know.", "\"오, 알다시피 그녀를 안 지가 오래됐잖아."], ["She’s very good-hearted, I suppose, mais excessivement terre-à-terre.", "무척 마음씨가 고운 사람인 것 같아, mais excessivement terre-à-terre. 그래도 그녀를 보게 되어서 정말 기뻐.\""], ["Still, I’m very glad to see her.”", "* mais excessivement terre-à-terre: 프랑스어로 '하지만 지나치게 현실적이고 평범하지'라는 뜻."]], [["He took Anna’s hand and looked inquiringly into her eyes.", "그는 안나의 손을 잡고 묻는 듯한 눈길로 그녀의 눈을 들여다보았다."]], [["Misinterpreting the look, she smiled to him.", "그 눈길을 잘못 해석한 그녀는 그를 향해 미소 지었다."], ["Next morning, in spite of the protests of her hosts, Darya Alexandrovna prepared for her homeward journey.", "이튿날 아침, 다리아 알렉산드로브나는 집주인들의 만류에도 불구하고 집으로 돌아갈 채비를 했다."], ["Levin’s coachman, in his by no means new coat and shabby hat, with his ill-matched horses and his coach with the patched mud-guards, drove with gloomy determination into the covered gravel approach.", "레빈의 마부가, 결코 새것이라 할 수 없는 외투에 낡은 모자를 쓰고, 짝이 안 맞는 말들과 흙받기를 기운 마차를 끌고, 자갈을 깐 지붕 덮인 현관 진입로로 침울한 결의를 띤 채 들어섰다."]], [["Darya Alexandrovna disliked taking leave of Princess Varvara and the gentlemen of the party.", "다리아 알렉산드로브나는 바르바라 공작 영애며 손님들과 작별 인사를 나누는 것이 내키지 않았다."], ["After a day spent together, both she and her hosts were distinctly aware that they did not get on together, and that it was better for them not to meet.", "하루를 함께 보내고 나자, 그녀나 집주인들이나 서로 잘 맞지 않으며, 만나지 않는 편이 낫다는 것을 분명히 깨달았던 것이다."], ["Only Anna was sad.", "오직 안나만이 서글퍼했다."], ["She knew that now, from Dolly’s departure, no one again would stir up within her soul the feelings that had been roused by their conversation.", "그녀는 이제 돌리가 떠나고 나면, 두 사람의 대화로 일깨워졌던 그 감정들을 자기 영혼 속에서 다시 흔들어 깨워 줄 사람이 아무도 없으리라는 것을 알았다."], ["It hurt her to stir up these feelings, but yet she knew that that was the best part of her soul, and that that part of her soul would quickly be smothered in the life she was leading.", "그 감정들을 흔들어 깨우는 일은 그녀에게 괴로웠지만, 그래도 그녀는 그것이 자기 영혼의 가장 좋은 부분이며, 자기가 살아가는 이 삶 속에서 그 부분이 머잖아 질식해 버리리라는 것을 알고 있었다."]], [["As she drove out into the open country, Darya Alexandrovna had a delightful sense of relief, and she felt tempted to ask the two men how they had liked being at Vronsky’s, when suddenly the coachman, Philip, expressed himself unasked:", "탁 트인 들판으로 마차를 몰아 나오자, 다리아 알렉산드로브나는 기분 좋은 해방감을 느꼈고, 두 사내에게 브론스키의 저택에 머문 것이 어땠는지 물어보고 싶은 마음이 들었다. 그때 마부 필립이 묻지도 않았는데 불쑥 말을 꺼냈다."]], [["“Rolling in wealth they may be, but three pots of oats was all they gave us.", "\"재산이야 넘쳐 나는지 몰라도, 우리한테 준 거라곤 귀리 세 됫박이 전부였습죠."], ["Everything cleared up till there wasn’t a grain left by cockcrow.", "닭이 울 무렵엔 낟알 한 톨 안 남고 죄다 동나 버렸어요. 세 됫박이 다 뭡니까?"], ["What are three pots?", "입에 풀칠도 안 되는걸요!"], ["A mere mouthful! And oats now down to forty-five kopecks.", "게다가 요즘은 귀리가 사십오 코페이카까지 떨어졌는데 말이죠."], ["At our place, no fear, all comers may have as much as they can eat.”", "우리 집에선 어림없는 소리, 누가 오든 배불리 먹을 만큼은 얼마든지 준다고요.\""]], [["“The master’s a screw,”", "\"주인이 구두쇠로군요.\""], ["put in the counting-house clerk.", "회계 사무소 서기가 한마디 거들었다."]], [["“Well, did you like their horses?”", "\"그래, 그 집 말들은 마음에 들었나?\""], ["asked Dolly.", "돌리가 물었다."]], [["“The horses!—there’s no two opinions about them.", "\"말들이라! 그건 두말할 것도 없습죠."], ["And the food was good.", "먹을 것도 좋았고요."], ["But it seemed to me sort of dreary there, Darya Alexandrovna.", "하지만 제겐 어쩐지 그곳이 좀 음울하게 느껴졌습니다요, 다리아 알렉산드로브나."], ["I don’t know what you thought,”", "마님은 어떻게 생각하셨는지 모르겠지만요.\""], ["he said, turning his handsome, good-natured face to her.", "그가 잘생기고 선량한 얼굴을 그녀 쪽으로 돌리며 말했다."]], [["“I thought so too.", "\"나도 그렇게 생각했어."], ["Well, shall we get home by evening?”", "그래, 저녁까지는 집에 닿을 수 있을까?\""]], [["“Eh, we must!”", "\"아, 닿아야 합죠!\""]], [["On reaching home and finding everyone entirely satisfactory and particularly charming, Darya Alexandrovna began with great liveliness telling them how she had arrived, how warmly they had received her, of the luxury and good taste in which the Vronskys lived, and of their recreations, and she would not allow a word to be said against them.", "집에 도착해 모든 것이 더할 나위 없이 만족스럽고 유난히 정겹다는 걸 알게 된 다리아 알렉산드로브나는, 더없이 활기찬 어조로 식구들에게 자기가 어떻게 도착했는지, 그들이 자기를 얼마나 따뜻하게 맞아 주었는지, 브론스키 부부가 얼마나 호화롭고 고상한 취향 속에서 살고 있는지, 그들이 어떤 여흥을 즐기는지 이야기하기 시작했고, 그들에 대해 험담하는 말은 한마디도 허락하지 않았다."]], [["“One has to know Anna and Vronsky—I have got to know him better now—to see how nice they are, and how touching,”", "\"안나와 브론스키를 알아야 해요—난 이제 그이를 더 잘 알게 됐는데—그래야 그들이 얼마나 멋지고 또 얼마나 가슴을 울리는 사람들인지 알 수 있죠.\""], ["she said, speaking now with perfect sincerity, and forgetting the vague feeling of dissatisfaction and awkwardness she had experienced there.", "그녀는 이제 더없이 진심 어린 어조로 말했고, 그곳에서 겪었던 막연한 불만과 어색함의 감정은 까맣게 잊어버렸다."]], [["# Chapter 25", "# Chapter 25"]], [["Vronsky and Anna spent the whole summer and part of the winter in the country, living in just the same condition, and still taking no steps to obtain a divorce.", "브론스키와 안나는 여름 내내, 그리고 겨울의 일부를 시골에서 보냈다. 여전히 똑같은 형편으로 지냈고, 이혼을 얻어 내기 위한 어떤 조치도 취하지 않았다."], ["It was an understood thing between them that they should not go away anywhere; but both felt, the longer they lived alone, especially in the autumn, without guests in the house, that they could not stand this existence, and that they would have to alter it.", "어디로든 떠나지 않는다는 것이 두 사람 사이에 암묵적으로 정해진 일이었다. 그러나 둘만 지내는 시간이 길어질수록, 특히 가을에 집에 손님 하나 없이 보낼 때면, 두 사람 모두 이런 생활을 견딜 수 없으며 무언가 바꾸어야만 한다는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["Their life was apparently such that nothing better could be desired.", "겉으로 보기에 그들의 생활은 더 바랄 것이 없을 만큼 좋았다."], ["They had the fullest abundance of everything; they had a child, and both had occupation.", "무엇 하나 모자람 없이 풍족하게 갖추고 있었고, 아이도 있었으며, 둘 다 할 일이 있었다."], ["Anna devoted just as much care to her appearance when they had no visitors, and she did a great deal of reading, both of novels and of what serious literature was in fashion.", "안나는 손님이 없을 때에도 자기 외모에 똑같이 정성을 쏟았고, 소설이든 유행하는 진지한 문학이든 무척 많이 읽었다."], ["She ordered all the books that were praised in the foreign papers and reviews she received, and read them with that concentrated attention which is only given to what is read in seclusion.", "그녀는 자기가 받아 보는 외국 신문이나 비평지에서 칭찬받는 책이라면 모조리 주문했고, 칩거 중에 읽는 것에만 쏟을 수 있는 그런 집중된 주의력으로 그것들을 읽었다."], ["Moreover, every subject that was of interest to Vronsky, she studied in books and special journals, so that he often went straight to her with questions relating to agriculture or architecture, sometimes even with questions relating to horse-breeding or sport.", "게다가 브론스키가 흥미를 두는 주제라면 무엇이든 책과 전문지를 통해 공부했기 때문에, 그는 농업이나 건축에 관한 질문, 때로는 말 사육이나 스포츠에 관한 질문조차 곧장 그녀에게 들고 가곤 했다."], ["He was amazed at her knowledge, her memory, and at first was disposed to doubt it, to ask for confirmation of her facts; and she would find what he asked for in some book, and show it to him.", "그는 그녀의 지식과 기억력에 놀랐고, 처음에는 그것을 의심하여 사실을 확인해 달라고 청하곤 했다. 그러면 그녀는 그가 묻는 것을 어느 책에선가 찾아내어 그에게 보여 주었다."]], [["The building of the hospital, too, interested her.", "병원 건축 역시 그녀의 관심을 끌었다."], ["She did not merely assist, but planned and suggested a great deal herself.", "그녀는 단순히 돕기만 한 것이 아니라 직접 많은 것을 계획하고 제안했다."], ["But her chief thought was still of herself—how far she was dear to Vronsky, how far she could make up to him for all he had given up.", "그러나 그녀의 가장 큰 생각은 여전히 자기 자신에 관한 것이었다. 자기가 브론스키에게 얼마나 소중한 존재인지, 그가 포기한 모든 것을 자기가 얼마나 보상해 줄 수 있는지 하는 것이었다."], ["Vronsky appreciated this desire not only to please, but to serve him, which had become the sole aim of her existence, but at the same time he wearied of the loving snares in which she tried to hold him fast.", "브론스키는 단지 그를 기쁘게 하려는 것만이 아니라 그를 위해 헌신하려는 이 욕망을, 그것이 그녀 존재의 유일한 목적이 되어 버린 그 욕망을 고맙게 여겼다. 그러나 동시에 그녀가 그를 단단히 붙들어 두려고 쳐 놓은 사랑의 올가미에 지쳐 갔다."], ["As time went on, and he saw himself more and more often held fast in these snares, he had an ever growing desire, not so much to escape from them, as to try whether they hindered his freedom.", "시간이 흐를수록, 그리고 자기가 점점 더 자주 그 올가미에 단단히 걸려 있는 것을 볼수록, 그에게는 거기서 벗어나려는 마음이라기보다 그것들이 자기 자유를 가로막는지 시험해 보고 싶은 마음이 점점 커져 갔다."], ["Had it not been for this growing desire to be free, not to have scenes every time he wanted to go to the town to a meeting or a race, Vronsky would have been perfectly satisfied with his life.", "이렇게 자유로워지고 싶은 욕망, 회의나 경마를 보러 시내에 가려 할 때마다 소동을 겪지 않았으면 하는 욕망만 없었더라면, 브론스키는 자기 생활에 더할 나위 없이 만족했을 것이다."], ["The rôle he had taken up, the rôle of a wealthy landowner, one of that class which ought to be the very heart of the Russian aristocracy, was entirely to his taste; and now, after spending six months in that character, he derived even greater satisfaction from it.", "그가 떠맡은 역할, 즉 마땅히 러시아 귀족 계급의 한복판이 되어야 할 부류의 한 사람인 부유한 지주라는 역할은 전적으로 그의 취향에 맞았다. 그리고 이제 그 역할로 반년을 보내고 나니 거기서 더 큰 만족을 얻었다."], ["And his management of his estate, which occupied and absorbed him more and more, was most successful.", "점점 더 그를 사로잡고 빨아들이던 영지 경영도 대단히 성공적이었다."], ["In spite of the immense sums cost him by the hospital, by machinery, by cows ordered from Switzerland, and many other things, he was convinced that he was not wasting, but increasing his substance.", "병원, 기계, 스위스에서 주문한 소들, 그 밖의 여러 가지에 막대한 비용이 들었음에도, 그는 자기가 재산을 낭비하는 것이 아니라 불려 가고 있다고 확신했다."], ["In all matters affecting income, the sales of timber, wheat, and wool, the letting of lands, Vronsky was hard as a rock, and knew well how to keep up prices.", "수입과 관계되는 모든 일, 목재와 밀과 양모의 판매, 토지의 임대에 있어서 브론스키는 바위처럼 단단했고 가격을 유지하는 법을 잘 알고 있었다."], ["In all operations on a large scale on this and his other estates, he kept to the simplest methods involving no risk, and in trifling details he was careful and exacting to an extreme degree.", "이 영지와 다른 영지들에서 벌이는 큰 규모의 모든 사업에서 그는 위험이 따르지 않는 가장 단순한 방법을 고수했고, 자질구레한 세부 사항에서는 극도로 신중하고 까다로웠다."], ["In spite of all the cunning and ingenuity of the German steward, who would try to tempt him into purchases by making his original estimate always far larger than really required, and then representing to Vronsky that he might get the thing cheaper, and so make a profit, Vronsky did not give in.", "독일인 집사는 늘 처음 견적을 실제 필요한 것보다 훨씬 부풀려 잡은 다음, 그것을 더 싸게 살 수 있으니 그러면 이득을 보는 셈이라고 브론스키를 구슬려 사들이도록 꾀려고 온갖 교활한 술수와 잔꾀를 부렸지만, 브론스키는 넘어가지 않았다."], ["He listened to his steward, cross-examined him, and only agreed to his suggestions when the implement to be ordered or constructed was the very newest, not yet known in Russia, and likely to excite wonder.", "그는 집사의 말을 듣고 따져 물었으며, 주문하거나 제작할 기구가 아주 최신식이어서 아직 러시아에 알려지지 않았고 사람들을 놀라게 할 만한 것일 때에만 그 제안에 동의했다."], ["Apart from such exceptions, he resolved upon an increased outlay only where there was a surplus, and in making such an outlay he went into the minutest details, and insisted on getting the very best for his money; so that by the method on which he managed his affairs, it was clear that he was not wasting, but increasing his substance.", "그런 예외를 빼면, 그는 여유가 있을 때에만 지출을 늘리기로 결심했고, 그런 지출을 할 때에는 아주 사소한 세부까지 따져 가며 자기 돈에 합당한 최상의 것을 얻어 내기를 고집했다. 그래서 그가 일을 처리하는 방식만 보아도 그가 재산을 낭비하는 것이 아니라 불려 가고 있음이 분명했다."]], [["In October there were the provincial elections in the Kashinsky province, where were the estates of Vronsky, Sviazhsky, Koznishev, Oblonsky, and a small part of Levin’s land.", "10월에는 카신스키 현에서 지방 선거가 있었는데, 그곳에는 브론스키, 스비아시스키, 코즈니셰프, 오블론스키의 영지가 있었고 레빈 땅도 일부 있었다."]], [["These elections were attracting public attention from several circumstances connected with them, and also from the people taking part in them.", "이 선거는 그와 관련된 몇 가지 사정으로, 또 거기에 참가하는 사람들 때문에 세간의 이목을 끌고 있었다."], ["There had been a great deal of talk about them, and great preparations were being made for them.", "그것을 두고 말이 무성했고 큰 준비가 진행되고 있었다."], ["Persons who never attended the elections were coming from Moscow, from Petersburg, and from abroad to attend these.", "평소 선거에 한 번도 나가 본 적 없는 사람들까지 모스크바에서, 페테르부르크에서, 그리고 외국에서 이 선거에 참석하러 오고 있었다."], ["Vronsky had long before promised Sviazhsky to go to them.", "브론스키는 오래전에 스비아시스키에게 함께 가겠다고 약속해 두었다."], ["Before the elections Sviazhsky, who often visited Vozdvizhenskoe, drove over to fetch Vronsky.", "선거를 앞두고, 보즈드비젠스코예에 자주 들르던 스비아시스키가 브론스키를 데리러 마차를 몰고 왔다."], ["On the day before there had been almost a quarrel between Vronsky and Anna over this proposed expedition.", "그 전날, 이 예정된 나들이를 두고 브론스키와 안나 사이에는 거의 다툼이 일 뻔했다."], ["It was the very dullest autumn weather, which is so dreary in the country, and so, preparing himself for a struggle, Vronsky, with a hard and cold expression, informed Anna of his departure as he had never spoken to her before.", "시골에서는 더없이 음울하게 느껴지는, 가장 따분한 가을 날씨였다. 그래서 브론스키는 한판 싸움을 각오하고, 일찍이 그녀에게 그래 본 적 없는 딱딱하고 차가운 표정으로 떠난다는 것을 안나에게 알렸다."], ["But, to his surprise, Anna accepted the information with great composure, and merely asked when he would be back.", "그러나 뜻밖에도 안나는 그 소식을 아주 침착하게 받아들이며, 언제 돌아오느냐고 묻기만 했다."], ["He looked intently at her, at a loss to explain this composure.", "그는 이 침착함을 어떻게 설명해야 할지 몰라 그녀를 뚫어지게 바라보았다."], ["She smiled at his look.", "그녀는 그의 시선에 미소를 지었다."], ["He knew that way she had of withdrawing into herself, and knew that it only happened when she had determined upon something without letting him know her plans.", "그는 그녀가 자기 안으로 움츠러드는 그 방식을 알고 있었고, 그것이 그녀가 자기에게 계획을 알리지 않은 채 무언가를 마음먹었을 때에만 일어난다는 것도 알고 있었다."], ["He was afraid of this; but he was so anxious to avoid a scene that he kept up appearances, and half sincerely believed in what he longed to believe in—her reasonableness.", "그는 이것이 두려웠다. 그러나 소동만큼은 피하고 싶은 마음이 어찌나 간절했던지, 그는 겉모양을 꾸미면서 자기가 믿고 싶은 것을, 즉 그녀의 분별 있음을 반쯤은 진심으로 믿었다."]], [["“I hope you won’t be dull?”", "\"심심하지는 않겠지?\""]], [["“I hope not,”", "\"안 그럴 거예요.\""], ["said Anna.", "안나가 말했다."], ["“I got a box of books yesterday from Gautier’s.", "\"어제 고티에서 책 한 상자를 받았거든요."], ["No, I shan’t be dull.”", "아니요, 심심하지 않을 거예요.\""]], [["“She’s trying to take that tone, and so much the better,”", "'저런 투를 취하려는 거군, 그러는 편이 차라리 낫지.'"], ["he thought, “or else it would be the same thing over and over again.”", "그는 생각했다. '안 그러면 또 똑같은 일이 끝없이 되풀이될 테니까.'"]], [["And he set off for the elections without appealing to her for a candid explanation.", "그리고 그는 그녀에게 솔직한 해명을 청하지 않은 채 선거를 향해 떠났다."], ["It was the first time since the beginning of their intimacy that he had parted from her without a full explanation.", "두 사람이 가까워진 이래 그가 충분한 해명 없이 그녀와 헤어진 것은 이번이 처음이었다."], ["From one point of view this troubled him, but on the other side he felt that it was better so.", "한편으로 이것이 그를 불안하게 했지만, 다른 한편으로는 이렇게 하는 편이 낫다고 느꼈다."], ["“At first there will be, as this time, something undefined kept back, and then she will get used to it.", "'처음에는 이번처럼 무언가 분명치 않은 것을 감추어 두겠지만, 그러다 보면 익숙해질 거야."], ["In any case I can give up anything for her, but not my masculine independence,”", "어쨌든 나는 그녀를 위해 무엇이든 포기할 수 있어. 하지만 남자로서의 독립만큼은 안 돼.'"], ["he thought.", "그는 생각했다."]], [["# Chapter 26", "# Chapter 26"]], [["In September Levin moved to Moscow for Kitty’s confinement.", "9월에 레빈은 키티의 출산을 위해 모스크바로 거처를 옮겼다."], ["He had spent a whole month in Moscow with nothing to do, when Sergey Ivanovitch, who had property in the Kashinsky province, and took great interest in the question of the approaching elections, made ready to set off to the elections.", "모스크바에서 할 일 없이 꼬박 한 달을 보냈을 무렵, 카신 현에 영지를 가지고 있고 다가오는 선거 문제에 큰 관심을 두고 있던 세르게이 이바노비치가 선거에 참석하러 떠날 채비를 했다."], ["He invited his brother, who had a vote in the Seleznevsky district, to come with him.", "그는 셀레즈뇨프스키 군에서 투표권을 가진 동생에게 함께 가자고 청했다."], ["Levin had, moreover, to transact in Kashin some extremely important business relating to the wardship of land and to the receiving of certain redemption money for his sister, who was abroad.", "게다가 레빈은 카신에서 토지의 후견에 관련된 일과, 외국에 나가 있는 누이를 위해 모종의 상환금을 수령하는, 대단히 중대한 용무를 처리해야 했다."]], [["Levin still hesitated, but Kitty, who saw that he was bored in Moscow, and urged him to go, on her own authority ordered him the proper nobleman’s uniform, costing seven pounds.", "레빈은 여전히 망설였지만, 그가 모스크바에서 지루해하는 것을 본 키티가 가라고 부추겼고, 제 권한으로 칠 파운드짜리 귀족용 정복을 그에게 맞춰 주었다."], ["And that seven pounds paid for the uniform was the chief cause that finally decided Levin to go.", "그리고 정복 값으로 치른 그 칠 파운드가, 결국 레빈으로 하여금 떠나기로 마음먹게 한 가장 큰 이유였다."], ["He went to Kashin....", "그는 카신으로 떠났다……."]], [["Levin had been six days in Kashin, visiting the assembly each day, and busily engaged about his sister’s business, which still dragged on.", "레빈은 카신에 엿새째 머물면서 날마다 의회에 들렀고, 여전히 질질 끌고 있는 누이의 용무로 분주히 매달려 있었다."], ["The district marshals of nobility were all occupied with the elections, and it was impossible to get the simplest thing done that depended upon the court of wardship.", "군의 귀족회 의장들은 모두 선거에 매여 있었고, 후견 재판소에 달린 가장 단순한 일조차 처리할 도리가 없었다."], ["The other matter, the payment of the sums due, was met too by difficulties.", "다른 일, 즉 받아야 할 돈의 지급 문제 역시 난관에 부딪혔다."], ["After long negotiations over the legal details, the money was at last ready to be paid; but the notary, a most obliging person, could not hand over the order, because it must have the signature of the president, and the president, though he had not given over his duties to a deputy, was at the elections.", "법적 세부 사항을 둘러싸고 오래 협의한 끝에 마침내 돈을 지급할 채비가 갖춰졌지만, 더없이 친절한 사람인 공증인은 그 지급 명령서를 넘겨줄 수가 없었다. 거기에는 의장의 서명이 있어야 했는데, 의장은 직무를 대리인에게 넘기지도 않은 채 선거에 가 있었기 때문이다."], ["All these worrying negotiations, this endless going from place to place, and talking with pleasant and excellent people, who quite saw the unpleasantness of the petitioner’s position, but were powerless to assist him—all these efforts that yielded no result, led to a feeling of misery in Levin akin to the mortifying helplessness one experiences in dreams when one tries to use physical force.", "이 모든 성가신 협의들, 이곳저곳 끝없이 돌아다니는 일, 청원자의 처지가 곤란하다는 것을 충분히 알면서도 그를 도울 힘은 없는 친절하고 훌륭한 사람들과 이야기를 나누는 일—아무런 결실도 맺지 못하는 이 모든 노력은, 꿈속에서 물리적인 힘을 쓰려 할 때 느끼는, 그 굴욕스러운 무력감과 비슷한 비참한 감정을 레빈에게 안겨 주었다."], ["He felt this frequently as he talked to his most good-natured solicitor.", "더없이 친절한 변호사와 이야기를 나눌 때면 그는 이 감정을 자주 느꼈다."], ["This solicitor did, it seemed, everything possible, and strained every nerve to get him out of his difficulties.", "이 변호사는 보아하니 가능한 모든 일을 다 했고, 그를 곤경에서 빼내려고 온 신경을 곤두세웠다."], ["“I tell you what you might try,”", "\"이렇게 한번 해 보시지요.\""], ["he said more than once; “go to so-and-so and so-and-so,”", "그는 한두 번이 아니게 말했다. \"아무개와 아무개를 찾아가 보십시오.\""], ["and the solicitor drew up a regular plan for getting round the fatal point that hindered everything.", "그러고는 모든 일을 가로막는 그 치명적인 지점을 우회할 만한 정식 계획을 세워 주었다."], ["But he would add immediately, “It’ll mean some delay, anyway, but you might try it.”", "하지만 그는 곧바로 이렇게 덧붙였다. \"어쨌든 시간은 좀 걸릴 테지만, 한번 해 보시는 게 좋겠지요.\""], ["And Levin did try, and did go.", "그래서 레빈은 시도했고, 또 찾아갔다."], ["Everyone was kind and civil, but the point evaded seemed to crop up again in the end, and again to bar the way.", "모두가 친절하고 정중했지만, 피해 간 듯했던 그 지점은 결국 다시 불쑥 튀어나와 또다시 길을 가로막았다."], ["What was particularly trying, was that Levin could not make out with whom he was struggling, to whose interest it was that his business should not be done.", "특히 견디기 힘든 것은, 자기가 누구와 씨름하고 있는지, 자기 일이 처리되지 않는 것이 누구에게 이득이 되는지를 레빈으로서는 도무지 알 수 없다는 점이었다."], ["That no one seemed to know; the solicitor certainly did not know.", "아무도 그것을 모르는 듯했고, 변호사 역시 분명 몰랐다."], ["If Levin could have understood why, just as he saw why one can only approach the booking office of a railway station in single file, it would not have been so vexatious and tiresome to him.", "마치 철도역 매표소에는 한 줄로만 다가갈 수 있다는 것을 그가 이해하듯, 만약 그 까닭을 이해할 수만 있었다면, 그에게 이토록 짜증스럽고 진저리 나는 일은 아니었을 것이다."], ["But with the hindrances that confronted him in his business, no one could explain why they existed.", "그러나 그의 용무 앞에 가로놓인 장애물들에 대해서는, 어째서 그것들이 존재하는지 아무도 설명해 줄 수 없었다."]], [["But Levin had changed a good deal since his marriage; he was patient, and if he could not see why it was all arranged like this, he told himself that he could not judge without knowing all about it, and that most likely it must be so, and he tried not to fret.", "그러나 결혼한 이후 레빈은 꽤나 달라져 있었다. 그는 인내심이 있었고, 어째서 모든 일이 이렇게 짜여 있는지 알 수 없을 때면, 전후 사정을 다 알지 못하고서는 판단할 수 없으며 십중팔구 그럴 수밖에 없는 노릇이리라고 스스로에게 일렀고, 안달하지 않으려 애썼다."]], [["In attending the elections, too, and taking part in them, he tried now not to judge, not to fall foul of them, but to comprehend as fully as he could the question which was so earnestly and ardently absorbing honest and excellent men whom he respected.", "선거에 참석하고 거기에 한몫 끼면서도, 그는 이제 함부로 판단하거나 트집 잡지 않으려 했고, 자기가 존경하는 정직하고 훌륭한 사람들이 그토록 진지하고 열렬하게 몰두하는 그 문제를 되도록 온전히 이해하려고 애썼다."], ["Since his marriage there had been revealed to Levin so many new and serious aspects of life that had previously, through his frivolous attitude to them, seemed of no importance, that in the question of the elections too he assumed and tried to find some serious significance.", "결혼한 이후 레빈에게는 삶의 새롭고 진지한 면면들이 숱하게 드러났는데, 예전에는 그것들을 경박하게 대했던 탓에 아무 중요성도 없어 보였던 것들이었다. 그래서 선거 문제에서도 그는 어떤 진지한 의미를 가정하고 그것을 찾아내려 애썼다."]], [["Sergey Ivanovitch explained to him the meaning and object of the proposed revolution at the elections.", "세르게이 이바노비치는 선거에서 꾀하는 변혁의 의미와 목적을 그에게 설명해 주었다."], ["The marshal of the province in whose hands the law had placed the control of so many important public functions—the guardianship of wards (the very department which was giving Levin so much trouble just now), the disposal of large sums subscribed by the nobility of the province, the high schools, female, male, and military, and popular instruction on the new model, and finally, the district council—the marshal of the province, Snetkov, was a nobleman of the old school,—dissipating an immense fortune, a good-hearted man, honest after his own fashion, but utterly without any comprehension of the needs of modern days.", "법률이 그토록 많은 중요한 공적 직무의 통제권을 손에 쥐여 준 사람, 즉 현 귀족회 의장—피후견인의 후견(바로 지금 레빈을 그토록 애먹이고 있는 그 부서), 현 귀족들이 갹출한 거액의 운용, 여자·남자·군사 고등학교, 그리고 새로운 방식의 국민 교육, 끝으로 군 의회까지—그 현 귀족회 의장인 스네트코프는 구식 귀족이었다. 막대한 재산을 흥청망청 탕진하는 사람, 마음씨는 착하고 제 나름으로는 정직하지만, 현대의 요구에 대해서는 전혀 이해가 없는 사람이었다."], ["He always took, in every question, the side of the nobility; he was positively antagonistic to the spread of popular education, and he succeeded in giving a purely party character to the district council which ought by rights to be of such an immense importance.", "그는 어떤 문제에서든 늘 귀족 편을 들었다. 국민 교육의 보급에 대놓고 적대적이었고, 마땅히 막대한 중요성을 지녀야 할 군 의회에 순전히 당파적인 성격을 입히는 데 성공했다."], ["What was needed was to put in his place a fresh, capable, perfectly modern man, of contemporary ideas, and to frame their policy so as from the rights conferred upon the nobles, not as the nobility, but as an element of the district council, to extract all the powers of self-government that could possibly be derived from them.", "필요한 것은 그의 자리에 참신하고 유능하며 완전히 현대적이고 동시대적인 사상을 지닌 인물을 앉히는 일이었고, 또한 귀족에게 주어진 권리로부터—귀족으로서가 아니라 군 의회의 한 요소로서—거기서 끌어낼 수 있는 자치의 권능을 죄다 끄집어내도록 정책을 짜는 일이었다."], ["In the wealthy Kashinsky province, which always took the lead of other provinces in everything, there was now such a preponderance of forces that this policy, once carried through properly there, might serve as a model for other provinces for all Russia.", "모든 면에서 늘 다른 현들을 앞서 이끌어 온 부유한 카신 현에는, 이제 일단 그곳에서 이 정책이 제대로 관철되기만 하면 다른 현들, 나아가 러시아 전체의 본보기가 될 만큼 막강한 세력의 우위가 형성되어 있었다."], ["And hence the whole question was of the greatest importance.", "그러므로 이 문제 전체가 더없이 중대한 것이었다."], ["It was proposed to elect as marshal in place of Snetkov either Sviazhsky, or, better still, Nevyedovsky, a former university professor, a man of remarkable intelligence and a great friend of Sergey Ivanovitch.", "스네트코프 대신 의장으로 스비아시스키를, 아니 그보다는 더 나은 인물로서 전직 대학교수이자 비범한 지성을 갖춘, 세르게이 이바노비치의 절친한 벗인 네뱌돕스키를 선출하자는 안이 제기되었다."]], [["The meeting was opened by the governor, who made a speech to the nobles, urging them to elect the public functionaries, not from regard for persons, but for the service and welfare of their fatherland, and hoping that the honorable nobility of the Kashinsky province would, as at all former elections, hold their duty as sacred, and vindicate the exalted confidence of the monarch.", "회의는 지사가 개회했는데, 그는 귀족들에게 연설을 하면서 인물에 대한 사사로운 정 때문이 아니라 조국에 대한 봉사와 복리를 위해 공직자들을 선출하라고 촉구했고, 카신 현의 명예로운 귀족들이 지난 모든 선거에서처럼 그들의 의무를 신성하게 여기며 군주의 드높은 신임에 보답하기를 바란다고 했다."]], [["When he had finished with his speech, the governor walked out of the hall, and the noblemen noisily and eagerly—some even enthusiastically—followed him and thronged round him while he put on his fur coat and conversed amicably with the marshal of the province.", "연설을 마치고 나자 지사는 회장을 걸어 나갔고, 귀족들은 떠들썩하고 열렬하게—더러는 흥분하기까지 하며—그를 뒤따라 우르르 몰려갔으며, 그가 모피 외투를 입고 현 귀족회 의장과 다정하게 이야기를 나누는 동안 그를 에워쌌다."], ["Levin, anxious to see into everything and not to miss anything, stood there too in the crowd, and heard the governor say: “Please tell Marya Ivanovna my wife is very sorry she couldn’t come to the Home.”", "모든 것을 들여다보고 무엇 하나 놓치지 않으려는 레빈도 그 무리 속에 끼여 서 있었는데, 지사가 이렇게 말하는 소리를 들었다. \"마리야 이바노브나에게 제 아내가 보육원에 오지 못해 무척 아쉬워한다고 전해 주십시오.\""], ["And thereupon the nobles in high good-humor sorted out their fur coats and all drove off to the cathedral.", "그러고 나서 귀족들은 한껏 들뜬 기분으로 저마다 모피 외투를 골라 입고는 모두 대성당으로 향했다."]], [["In the cathedral Levin, lifting his hand like the rest and repeating the words of the archdeacon, swore with most terrible oaths to do all the governor had hoped they would do.", "대성당에서 레빈은 다른 사람들처럼 손을 들고 부제의 말을 따라 외며, 지사가 그들이 해 주기를 바란 모든 일을 하겠노라고 무시무시한 맹세를 했다."], ["Church services always affected Levin, and as he uttered the words “I kiss the cross,” and glanced round at the crowd of young and old men repeating the same, he felt touched.", "교회의 예배는 늘 레빈의 마음을 움직였고, 그가 \"십자가에 입을 맞춥니다\"라는 말을 내뱉으며 같은 말을 따라 외는 젊고 늙은 사내들의 무리를 둘러보았을 때, 그는 가슴이 뭉클해지는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["On the second and third days there was business relating to the finances of the nobility and the female high school, of no importance whatever, as Sergey Ivanovitch explained, and Levin, busy seeing after his own affairs, did not attend the meetings.", "이튿날과 사흗날에는 세르게이 이바노비치의 설명에 따르면 아무 중요성도 없는, 귀족의 재정과 여자 고등학교에 관한 안건이 다뤄졌고, 자기 일을 돌보느라 분주한 레빈은 회의에 참석하지 않았다."], ["On the fourth day the auditing of the marshal’s accounts took place at the high table of the marshal of the province.", "나흗날에는 현 귀족회 의장의 높은 자리에서 의장의 회계 감사가 이루어졌다."], ["And then there occurred the first skirmish between the new party and the old.", "그리고 바로 그때 신파(新派)와 구파(舊派) 사이에 첫 충돌이 일어났다."], ["The committee who had been deputed to verify the accounts reported to the meeting that all was in order.", "회계를 검증하도록 위임받은 위원회가 모든 것이 제대로 되어 있다고 회의에 보고했다."], ["The marshal of the province got up, thanked the nobility for their confidence, and shed tears.", "현 귀족회 의장은 자리에서 일어나 귀족들의 신임에 감사하며 눈물을 흘렸다."], ["The nobles gave him a loud welcome, and shook hands with him.", "귀족들은 그에게 우렁찬 갈채를 보내며 악수를 나누었다."], ["But at that instant a nobleman of Sergey Ivanovitch’s party said that he had heard that the committee had not verified the accounts, considering such a verification an insult to the marshal of the province.", "그러나 그 순간 세르게이 이바노비치 편의 한 귀족이, 위원회가 그런 검증을 현 귀족회 의장에 대한 모욕이라고 여겨 회계를 검증하지 않았다는 말을 들었다고 했다."], ["One of the members of the committee incautiously admitted this.", "위원회 위원 가운데 한 사람이 경솔하게도 그것을 시인했다."], ["Then a small gentleman, very young-looking but very malignant, began to say that it would probably be agreeable to the marshal of the province to give an account of his expenditures of the public moneys, and that the misplaced delicacy of the members of the committee was depriving him of this moral satisfaction.", "그러자 매우 젊어 보이지만 매우 악의에 찬 자그마한 신사가, 현 귀족회 의장으로서는 아마 공금 지출에 대해 해명하는 편이 흡족할 터인데, 위원회 위원들의 엉뚱한 배려가 그에게서 이 도덕적 만족을 앗아 가고 있는 것이라고 말하기 시작했다."], ["Then the members of the committee tried to withdraw their admission, and Sergey Ivanovitch began to prove that they must logically admit either that they had verified the accounts or that they had not, and he developed this dilemma in detail.", "그러자 위원회 위원들은 자기들의 시인을 철회하려 했고, 세르게이 이바노비치는 논리적으로 따져 회계를 검증했든가 하지 않았든가 둘 중 하나를 인정할 수밖에 없다는 것을 증명하기 시작하더니, 이 딜레마를 세세하게 펼쳐 보였다."], ["Sergey Ivanovitch was answered by the spokesman of the opposite party.", "세르게이 이바노비치에게 반대편의 대변인이 응수했다."], ["Then Sviazhsky spoke, and then the malignant gentleman again.", "그다음에는 스비아시스키가 발언했고, 그다음에는 그 악의에 찬 신사가 또다시 발언했다."], ["The discussion lasted a long time and ended in nothing.", "토론은 오래도록 이어졌으나 아무 결론도 없이 끝났다."], ["Levin was surprised that they should dispute upon this subject so long, especially as, when he asked Sergey Ivanovitch whether he supposed that money had been misappropriated, Sergey Ivanovitch answered:", "레빈은 그들이 이 주제를 두고 이토록 오래 다투는 것이 의아했는데, 더욱이 세르게이 이바노비치에게 돈이 횡령되었다고 생각하느냐고 물었을 때 세르게이 이바노비치가 이렇게 대답했기에 더 그러했다."]], [["“Oh, no! He’s an honest man.", "\"오, 아니야!"], ["But those old-fashioned methods of paternal family arrangements in the management of provincial affairs must be broken down.”", "그는 정직한 사람이지. 하지만 현의 일을 운영하면서 가부장적인 가족 운영 방식의 그 구태의연한 수법은 깨부숴야 해.\""]], [["On the fifth day came the elections of the district marshals.", "닷새째 되던 날에는 군 귀족회 의장 선거가 있었다."], ["It was rather a stormy day in several districts.", "몇몇 군에서는 꽤나 험악한 날이었다."], ["In the Seleznevsky district Sviazhsky was elected unanimously without a ballot, and he gave a dinner that evening.", "셀레즈뇨프스키 군에서는 스비아시스키가 투표도 없이 만장일치로 선출되었고, 그는 그날 저녁 만찬을 베풀었다."]], [["# Chapter 27", "# Chapter 27"]], [["The sixth day was fixed for the election of the marshal of the province.", "엿새째 되는 날이 도(道) 귀족회장 선거일로 정해졌다."]], [["The rooms, large and small, were full of noblemen in all sorts of uniforms.", "크고 작은 방마다 온갖 제복을 입은 귀족들로 가득 찼다."], ["Many had come only for that day.", "많은 이들이 오로지 그날을 위해서만 모여들었다."], ["Men who had not seen each other for years, some from the Crimea, some from Petersburg, some from abroad, met in the rooms of the Hall of Nobility.", "몇 년 동안 서로 만나지 못한 사람들이, 더러는 크림 반도에서, 더러는 페테르부르크에서, 더러는 외국에서 와 귀족회관의 방들에서 마주쳤다."], ["There was much discussion around the governor’s table under the portrait of the Tsar.", "차르의 초상 아래 놓인 지사(知事)의 탁자 주위에서는 많은 논의가 오갔다."]], [["The nobles, both in the larger and the smaller rooms, grouped themselves in camps, and from their hostile and suspicious glances, from the silence that fell upon them when outsiders approached a group, and from the way that some, whispering together, retreated to the farther corridor, it was evident that each side had secrets from the other.", "귀족들은 큰 방에서나 작은 방에서나 진영을 이루어 패를 갈랐고, 서로를 향한 적대적이고 의심에 찬 눈길에서, 외부인이 한 무리에 다가오면 일제히 입을 다무는 침묵에서, 또 몇몇이 서로 귓속말을 주고받으며 저 안쪽 복도로 물러나는 모양새에서, 양편이 서로에게 숨기는 비밀을 품고 있음이 뚜렷이 드러났다."], ["In appearance the noblemen were sharply divided into two classes: the old and the new.", "외관상으로 귀족들은 두 부류로 분명하게 나뉘었다. 구파와 신파였다."], ["The old were for the most part either in old uniforms of the nobility, buttoned up closely, with spurs and hats, or in their own special naval, cavalry, infantry, or official uniforms.", "구파는 대개 단추를 목까지 꼭 채운 구식 귀족 제복에 박차와 모자를 갖추었거나, 아니면 저마다의 해군, 기병, 보병, 혹은 관직 제복을 입고 있었다."], ["The uniforms of the older men were embroidered in the old-fashioned way with epaulets on their shoulders; they were unmistakably tight and short in the waist, as though their wearers had grown out of them.", "나이 든 이들의 제복은 어깨에 견장을 단 구식 자수로 장식되어 있었는데, 마치 입은 사람이 그 옷을 벗어나도록 자라 버린 듯, 영락없이 허리께가 끼고 짧았다."], ["The younger men wore the uniform of the nobility with long waists and broad shoulders, unbuttoned over white waistcoats, or uniforms with black collars and with the embroidered badges of justices of the peace.", "젊은 축들은 허리가 길고 어깨가 넓은 귀족 제복을 흰 조끼 위에 단추를 풀어 헤쳐 입거나, 검은 깃에 치안판사의 자수 휘장을 단 제복을 입고 있었다."], ["To the younger men belonged the court uniforms that here and there brightened up the crowd.", "군중 속을 여기저기서 환하게 밝히던 궁정 제복도 젊은 축의 것이었다."]], [["But the division into young and old did not correspond with the division of parties.", "그러나 노소(老少)의 구분이 당파의 구분과 일치하지는 않았다."], ["Some of the young men, as Levin observed, belonged to the old party; and some of the very oldest noblemen, on the contrary, were whispering with Sviazhsky, and were evidently ardent partisans of the new party.", "레빈이 보니 젊은이들 가운데 더러는 구파에 속했고, 반대로 가장 나이 든 귀족들 가운데 더러는 스비아시스키와 귓속말을 나누며 명백히 신파의 열성 지지자 노릇을 하고 있었다."]], [["Levin stood in the smaller room, where they were smoking and taking light refreshments, close to his own friends, and listening to what they were saying, he conscientiously exerted all his intelligence trying to understand what was said.", "레빈은 사람들이 담배를 피우고 가벼운 음식을 들고 있는 작은 방에서, 제 친구들 가까이에 서서 그들이 하는 말을 귀담아들으며, 무슨 이야기인지 알아내려고 양심껏 자기 머리를 다 짜냈다."], ["Sergey Ivanovitch was the center round which the others grouped themselves.", "세르게이 이바노비치가 다른 이들이 둘러서는 중심이었다."], ["He was listening at that moment to Sviazhsky and Hliustov, the marshal of another district, who belonged to their party.", "그는 그때 스비아시스키와, 같은 당파에 속한 다른 군(郡)의 귀족회장 흘류스토프의 말에 귀를 기울이고 있었다."], ["Hliustov would not agree to go with his district to ask Snetkov to stand, while Sviazhsky was persuading him to do so, and Sergey Ivanovitch was approving of the plan.", "흘류스토프는 자기 군을 이끌고 스네트코프에게 출마를 청하러 가는 데 동의하려 들지 않았고, 스비아시스키는 그렇게 하라고 그를 설득하고 있었으며, 세르게이 이바노비치는 그 계획에 찬성하고 있었다."], ["Levin could not make out why the opposition was to ask the marshal to stand whom they wanted to supersede.", "레빈은 자기들이 끌어내리려는 바로 그 회장에게 어째서 반대파가 출마를 청해야 한다는 것인지 도무지 알 수가 없었다."]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch, who had just been drinking and taking some lunch, came up to them in his uniform of a gentleman of the bedchamber, wiping his lips with a perfumed handkerchief of bordered batiste.", "마침 술을 마시고 간단히 점심을 들고 온 스테판 아르카지치가 시종(侍從) 제복 차림으로, 가장자리에 무늬를 두른 얇은 무명 손수건에 향수를 뿌려 입술을 닦으며 그들에게 다가왔다."]], [["“We are placing our forces,”", "\"우리는 지금 병력을 배치하는 중이지.\""], ["he said, pulling out his whiskers, “Sergey Ivanovitch!”", "그가 구레나룻을 잡아당기며 말했다. \"세르게이 이바노비치!\""]], [["And listening to the conversation, he supported Sviazhsky’s contention.", "그러고는 대화에 귀를 기울이다가 스비아시스키의 주장을 거들었다."]], [["“One district’s enough, and Sviazhsky’s obviously of the opposition,”", "\"한 군이면 충분하고말고. 스비아시스키가 반대파라는 건 누가 봐도 뻔하니까.\""], ["he said, words evidently intelligible to all except Levin.", "그가 말했는데, 레빈을 제외한 모두에게는 명백히 알아들을 만한 말이었다."]], [["“Why, Kostya, you here too!", "\"아니, 코스챠, 너도 여기 있었구나!"], ["I suppose you’re converted, eh?”", "너도 넘어온 모양이지, 응?\""], ["he added, turning to Levin and drawing his arm through his.", "그는 레빈에게 돌아서서 그의 팔에 자기 팔을 끼며 덧붙였다."], ["Levin would have been glad indeed to be converted, but could not make out what the point was, and retreating a few steps from the speakers, he explained to Stepan Arkadyevitch his inability to understand why the marshal of the province should be asked to stand.", "레빈은 정말이지 기꺼이 넘어가고 싶었지만, 요점이 무엇인지 도무지 알 수가 없어, 이야기하는 이들에게서 몇 걸음 물러서서 스테판 아르카지치에게 어째서 도 귀족회장에게 출마를 청해야 하는지 자기로서는 이해할 수 없노라고 털어놓았다."]], [["“O sancta simplicitas!”", "\"O sancta simplicitas!\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, and briefly and clearly he explained it to Levin.", "스테판 아르카지치가 말하고는 레빈에게 그것을 짤막하고 분명하게 설명해 주었다."], ["If, as at previous elections, all the districts asked the marshal of the province to stand, then he would be elected without a ballot.", "만약 지난 선거들에서처럼 모든 군이 도 귀족회장에게 출마를 청한다면, 그는 투표 없이 선출될 것이다."], ["That must not be.", "그래서는 안 된다."], ["Now eight districts had agreed to call upon him: if two refused to do so, Snetkov might decline to stand at all; and then the old party might choose another of their party, which would throw them completely out in their reckoning.", "지금 여덟 개 군이 그를 추대하기로 합의했는데, 만일 두 개 군이 거부한다면 스네트코프가 아예 출마를 사양해 버릴지도 모르고, 그러면 구파가 자기 당파에서 다른 사람을 뽑을 수 있어, 그들의 계산이 송두리째 어그러질 것이다."], ["But if only one district, Sviazhsky’s, did not call upon him to stand, Snetkov would let himself be balloted for.", "그러나 단 한 군, 곧 스비아시스키의 군만 그를 추대하지 않는다면, 스네트코프는 투표에 부쳐지도록 자신을 내맡길 것이다."], ["They were even, some of them, going to vote for him, and purposely to let him get a good many votes, so that the enemy might be thrown off the scent, and when a candidate of the other side was put up, they too might give him some votes.", "그들 가운데 몇몇은 일부러 그에게 표를 던져 그가 제법 많은 표를 얻게 만들 작정까지 하고 있었는데, 이는 적이 낌새를 채지 못하게 하기 위해서였고, 그래야 상대편 후보가 나설 때 자기들도 그에게 얼마간 표를 줄 수 있기 때문이었다."], ["Levin understood to some extent, but not fully, and would have put a few more questions, when suddenly everyone began talking and making a noise and they moved towards the big room.", "레빈은 어느 정도는 알아들었으나 온전히 다는 아니어서 몇 가지를 더 물어보려던 참인데, 별안간 모두가 떠들고 소란을 피우기 시작하더니 큰 방 쪽으로 우르르 몰려갔다."]], [["“What is it?", "\"무슨 일이야?"], ["eh?", "응?"], ["whom?”", "누구를?\""], ["“No guarantee?", "\"자격이 없다고?"], ["whose?", "누가?"], ["what?”", "뭐가?\""], ["“They won’t pass him?”", "\"그를 통과시키지 않겠다고?\""], ["“No guarantee?”", "\"자격이 없어?\""], ["“They won’t let Flerov in?”", "\"플레로프를 들이지 않겠다는 거야?\""], ["“Eh, because of the charge against him?”", "\"어, 그자에게 걸린 혐의 때문에?\""], ["“Why, at this rate, they won’t admit anyone.", "\"아니, 그런 식이라면 아무도 들이지 못하겠군."], ["It’s a swindle!”", "이건 사기야!\""], ["“The law!”", "\"법이라고!\""], ["Levin heard exclamations on all sides, and he moved into the big room together with the others, all hurrying somewhere and afraid of missing something.", "레빈은 사방에서 터져 나오는 외침을 들으며, 저마다 어딘가로 서두르고 무언가를 놓칠까 봐 두려워하는 다른 이들과 함께 큰 방으로 옮겨 갔다. 밀려드는 귀족들 틈에 끼여, 그는 도 귀족회장과 스비아시스키와 다른 지도자들이 무언가를 두고 격렬하게 다투고 있는 높은 탁자 가까이로 다가갔다."], ["Squeezed by the crowding noblemen, he drew near the high table where the marshal of the province, Sviazhsky, and the other leaders were hotly disputing about something.", "* O sancta simplicitas!: 라틴어로 '오, 거룩한 단순함이여!'. 순진함을 가벼이 비꼬는 표현."]], [["# Chapter 28", "# Chapter 28"]], [["Levin was standing rather far off.", "레빈은 꽤 멀찍이 서 있었다."], ["A nobleman breathing heavily and hoarsely at his side, and another whose thick boots were creaking, prevented him from hearing distinctly.", "옆에서 거칠고 쉰 숨을 몰아쉬는 귀족 한 사람과, 두꺼운 장화를 삐걱대는 또 다른 한 사람 때문에 그는 또렷하게 들을 수가 없었다."], ["He could only hear the soft voice of the marshal faintly, then the shrill voice of the malignant gentleman, and then the voice of Sviazhsky.", "그저 귀족 회장의 부드러운 목소리가 희미하게 들리고, 그다음에는 그 심술궂은 신사의 새된 목소리가, 또 그다음에는 스비야시스키의 목소리가 들릴 뿐이었다."], ["They were disputing, as far as he could make out, as to the interpretation to be put on the act and the exact meaning of the words: “liable to be called up for trial.”", "그가 알아들은 바로는, 그들은 법령을 어떻게 해석할 것인가, 그리고 \"재판에 회부될 수 있는\"이라는 말의 정확한 의미가 무엇인가를 두고 다투고 있었다."]], [["The crowd parted to make way for Sergey Ivanovitch approaching the table.", "세르게이 이바노비치가 탁자로 다가가자 군중이 길을 터 주었다."], ["Sergey Ivanovitch, waiting till the malignant gentleman had finished speaking, said that he thought the best solution would be to refer to the act itself, and asked the secretary to find the act.", "세르게이 이바노비치는 그 심술궂은 신사가 말을 끝마치기를 기다렸다가, 가장 좋은 해결책은 법령 자체를 참조하는 것이라고 본다고 말하며 서기에게 법령을 찾아 달라고 청했다."], ["The act said that in case of difference of opinion, there must be a ballot.", "법령에는, 의견 차이가 있을 경우 반드시 투표를 해야 한다고 적혀 있었다."]], [["Sergey Ivanovitch read the act and began to explain its meaning, but at that point a tall, stout, round-shouldered landowner, with dyed whiskers, in a tight uniform that cut the back of his neck, interrupted him.", "세르게이 이바노비치가 법령을 읽고 그 의미를 설명하기 시작했는데, 바로 그때 키가 크고 뚱뚱하며 등이 굽은, 염색한 구레나룻을 기른 한 지주가, 목덜미를 옥죄는 꽉 끼는 제복을 입은 채 그의 말을 가로막았다."], ["He went up to the table, and striking it with his finger ring, he shouted loudly: “A ballot!", "그는 탁자로 다가가 손가락의 반지로 탁자를 두드리며 큰 소리로 외쳤다. \"투표합시다!"], ["Put it to the vote!", "표결에 부칩시다!"], ["No need for more talking!”", "더 이상 말할 것 없소!\""], ["Then several voices began to talk all at once, and the tall nobleman with the ring, getting more and more exasperated, shouted more and more loudly.", "그러자 여러 목소리가 한꺼번에 떠들기 시작했고, 반지를 낀 그 키 큰 귀족은 점점 더 격앙되어 점점 더 큰 소리로 외쳤다."], ["But it was impossible to make out what he said.", "하지만 그가 무슨 말을 하는지는 도무지 알아들을 수 없었다."]], [["He was shouting for the very course Sergey Ivanovitch had proposed; but it was evident that he hated him and all his party, and this feeling of hatred spread through the whole party and roused in opposition to it the same vindictiveness, though in a more seemly form, on the other side.", "그는 세르게이 이바노비치가 제안한 바로 그 방안을 외치고 있었지만, 그가 세르게이 이바노비치와 그 일파를 모두 미워한다는 것은 분명했다. 그리고 이 증오의 감정은 무리 전체로 번져 나가, 반대편에서도 좀 더 점잖은 형태이긴 하나 똑같은 앙심을 불러일으켰다."], ["Shouts were raised, and for a moment all was confusion, so that the marshal of the province had to call for order.", "고함이 터져 나오고, 한순간 온통 아수라장이 되어, 도(道) 귀족 회장이 조용히 하라고 외쳐야 할 정도였다."]], [["“A ballot!", "\"투표!"], ["A ballot!", "투표!"], ["Every nobleman sees it!", "모든 귀족이 다 알고 있소!"], ["We shed our blood for our country!...", "우리는 조국을 위해 피를 흘렸소!……"], ["The confidence of the monarch....", "군주의 신임을……"], ["No checking the accounts of the marshal; he’s not a cashier....", "회장의 회계 장부는 검사할 수 없소. 그는 출납계가 아니란 말이오……"], ["But that’s not the point....", "하지만 그게 요점이 아니오……"], ["Votes, please!", "투표 좀 합시다!"], ["Beastly!...”", "추잡하기는!……\""], ["shouted furious and violent voices on all sides.", "사방에서 격분하고 거친 목소리들이 외쳤다."], ["Looks and faces were even more violent and furious than their words.", "눈빛과 얼굴은 그들의 말보다도 더 거칠고 격렬했다."], ["They expressed the most implacable hatred.", "그것은 더없이 화해할 수 없는 증오를 드러내고 있었다."], ["Levin did not in the least understand what was the matter, and he marveled at the passion with which it was disputed whether or not the decision about Flerov should be put to the vote.", "레빈은 도대체 무엇이 문제인지 조금도 이해하지 못한 채, 플레로프에 관한 결정을 표결에 부칠 것이냐 말 것이냐를 두고 저토록 격정적으로 다투는 모습에 놀라워했다."], ["He forgot, as Sergey Ivanovitch explained to him afterwards, this syllogism: that it was necessary for the public good to get rid of the marshal of the province; that to get rid of the marshal it was necessary to have a majority of votes; that to get a majority of votes it was necessary to secure Flerov’s right to vote; that to secure the recognition of Flerov’s right to vote they must decide on the interpretation to be put on the act.", "그는 세르게이 이바노비치가 나중에 설명해 준 그 삼단논법을 잊고 있었던 것이다. 즉, 공공의 이익을 위해서는 도 귀족 회장을 몰아내야 하고, 회장을 몰아내려면 다수표를 얻어야 하며, 다수표를 얻으려면 플레로프의 투표권을 확보해야 하고, 플레로프의 투표권을 인정받으려면 법령을 어떻게 해석할지 결정해야 한다는 것이었다."]], [["“And one vote may decide the whole question, and one must be serious and consecutive, if one wants to be of use in public life,”", "\"한 표가 문제 전체를 결정할 수도 있어. 그리고 공적인 일에 쓸모 있는 사람이 되고 싶다면 진지하고 일관성이 있어야 하는 거야.\""], ["concluded Sergey Ivanovitch.", "세르게이 이바노비치가 결론지어 말했다."], ["But Levin forgot all that, and it was painful to him to see all these excellent persons, for whom he had a respect, in such an unpleasant and vicious state of excitement.", "하지만 레빈은 그 모든 것을 잊고 있었고, 자기가 존경하는 이 훌륭한 사람들이 저토록 불쾌하고 악의에 찬 흥분 상태에 빠진 모습을 보는 것이 괴로웠다."], ["To escape from this painful feeling he went away into the other room where there was nobody except the waiters at the refreshment bar.", "이 괴로운 감정에서 벗어나려고 그는 옆방으로 가 버렸는데, 그곳에는 간이식당의 급사들 말고는 아무도 없었다."], ["Seeing the waiters busy over washing up the crockery and setting in order their plates and wine-glasses, seeing their calm and cheerful faces, Levin felt an unexpected sense of relief as though he had come out of a stuffy room into the fresh air.", "그릇을 씻고 접시며 포도주잔을 정리하느라 분주한 급사들을 보고, 그 차분하고 명랑한 얼굴들을 보면서, 레빈은 마치 답답한 방에서 신선한 공기 속으로 나온 듯 뜻밖의 안도감을 느꼈다."], ["He began walking up and down, looking with pleasure at the waiters.", "그는 흐뭇하게 급사들을 바라보며 이리저리 거닐기 시작했다."], ["He particularly liked the way one gray-whiskered waiter, who showed his scorn for the other younger ones and was jeered at by them, was teaching them how to fold up napkins properly.", "특히 마음에 든 것은, 다른 젊은 급사들을 깔보다가 도리어 그들에게 놀림을 받는 회색 구레나룻의 급사 하나가 그들에게 냅킨을 제대로 접는 법을 가르치는 모습이었다."], ["Levin was just about to enter into conversation with the old waiter, when the secretary of the court of wardship, a little old man whose specialty it was to know all the noblemen of the province by name and patronymic, drew him away.", "레빈이 막 그 늙은 급사와 이야기를 나누려던 참에, 도의 모든 귀족을 이름과 부칭으로 알아맞히는 것이 전문인 후견 재판소의 서기, 그 자그마한 노인이 그를 끌고 갔다."]], [["“Please come, Konstantin Dmitrievitch,”", "\"이리 좀 오십시오, 콘스탄틴 드미트리예비치.\""], ["he said, “your brother’s looking for you.", "그가 말했다. \"형님께서 찾고 계십니다."], ["They are voting on the legal point.”", "법적 쟁점에 대해 투표하는 중입니다.\""]], [["Levin walked into the room, received a white ball, and followed his brother, Sergey Ivanovitch, to the table where Sviazhsky was standing with a significant and ironical face, holding his beard in his fist and sniffing at it.", "레빈은 방으로 들어가 흰 공 하나를 받아 들고, 형 세르게이 이바노비치를 따라 탁자로 갔다. 그곳에는 스비야시스키가 의미심장하고도 비꼬는 듯한 얼굴로, 주먹에 수염을 쥔 채 그것을 킁킁대며 서 있었다."], ["Sergey Ivanovitch put his hand into the box, put the ball somewhere, and making room for Levin, stopped.", "세르게이 이바노비치는 상자 안에 손을 넣어 어딘가에 공을 넣고는, 레빈에게 자리를 내주며 멈춰 섰다."], ["Levin advanced, but utterly forgetting what he was to do, and much embarrassed, he turned to Sergey Ivanovitch with the question, “Where am I to put it?”", "레빈은 앞으로 나아갔으나, 자기가 무엇을 해야 하는지 까맣게 잊은 채 몹시 당황하여 세르게이 이바노비치에게 물었다. \"이걸 어디에 넣어야 하죠?\""], ["He asked this softly, at a moment when there was talking going on near, so that he had hoped his question would not be overheard.", "그는 옆에서 한창 이야기가 오가는 틈을 타 작은 소리로 물었기에, 자기 질문이 들리지 않으리라 기대했다."], ["But the persons speaking paused, and his improper question was overheard.", "하지만 이야기하던 사람들이 말을 멈춘 탓에, 그의 부적절한 질문이 그만 들리고 말았다."], ["Sergey Ivanovitch frowned.", "세르게이 이바노비치는 미간을 찌푸렸다."]], [["“That is a matter for each man’s own decision,”", "\"그건 각자가 스스로 결정할 문제야.\""], ["he said severely.", "그가 엄하게 말했다."]], [["Several people smiled.", "몇몇 사람이 미소를 지었다."], ["Levin crimsoned, hurriedly thrust his hand under the cloth, and put the ball to the right as it was in his right hand.", "레빈은 얼굴이 새빨개진 채 황급히 천 밑으로 손을 밀어 넣고, 오른손에 공이 쥐여 있던 대로 그것을 오른쪽에 넣었다."], ["Having put it in, he recollected that he ought to have thrust his left hand too, and so he thrust it in though too late, and, still more overcome with confusion, he beat a hasty retreat into the background.", "다 넣고 나서야 그는 왼손도 마저 밀어 넣었어야 했다는 것을 떠올리고, 너무 늦었지만 그래도 손을 밀어 넣었다. 그러고는 더욱더 당혹감에 사로잡혀 황급히 뒤쪽으로 물러나 버렸다."]], [["“A hundred and twenty-six for admission!", "\"가입 찬성 백스물여섯 표!"], ["Ninety-eight against!”", "반대 아흔여덟 표!\""], ["sang out the voice of the secretary, who could not pronounce the letter r.", "서기의 목소리가 노래하듯 울려 퍼졌는데, 그는 'ㄹ' 발음을 제대로 하지 못했다."], ["Then there was a laugh; a button and two nuts were found in the box.", "그러자 웃음이 터졌다. 상자 안에서 단추 하나와 호두 두 알이 나온 것이다."], ["The nobleman was allowed the right to vote, and the new party had conquered.", "그 귀족에게는 투표권이 인정되었고, 새로운 일파가 승리를 거두었다."]], [["But the old party did not consider themselves conquered.", "하지만 옛 일파는 자기들이 졌다고 여기지 않았다."], ["Levin heard that they were asking Snetkov to stand, and he saw that a crowd of noblemen was surrounding the marshal, who was saying something.", "레빈은 그들이 스네트코프에게 출마를 청하는 소리를 들었고, 한 무리의 귀족들이 무언가 말하고 있는 회장을 에워싸고 있는 것을 보았다."], ["Levin went nearer.", "레빈은 가까이 다가갔다."], ["In reply Snetkov spoke of the trust the noblemen of the province had placed in him, the affection they had shown him, which he did not deserve, as his only merit had been his attachment to the nobility, to whom he had devoted twelve years of service.", "거기에 대답하며 스네트코프는, 도의 귀족들이 자기에게 보내 준 신뢰며 자기로서는 받을 자격이 없는 애정에 관해 이야기했다. 자기의 유일한 공이라면 십이 년을 봉직해 온 귀족 사회에 대한 헌신뿐이었노라고 말했다."], ["Several times he repeated the words: “I have served to the best of my powers with truth and good faith, I value your goodness and thank you,”", "그는 몇 번이고 같은 말을 되풀이했다. \"저는 진실과 성심을 다해 제 힘이 닿는 한 봉직해 왔습니다."], ["and suddenly he stopped short from the tears that choked him, and went out of the room.", "여러분의 후의를 소중히 여기며 감사드립니다.\" 그러다 별안간 복받쳐 오르는 눈물에 말문이 막혀 방을 나가 버렸다."], ["Whether these tears came from a sense of the injustice being done him, from his love for the nobility, or from the strain of the position he was placed in, feeling himself surrounded by enemies, his emotion infected the assembly, the majority were touched, and Levin felt a tenderness for Snetkov.", "그 눈물이 자기에게 가해진 부당함에 대한 느낌에서 나온 것인지, 귀족 사회에 대한 사랑에서 나온 것인지, 아니면 사방이 적들에게 둘러싸였다고 느끼는 자신의 처지가 주는 긴장에서 나온 것인지는 알 수 없었으나, 그의 감정은 회의장 전체로 번져 갔고, 대다수가 마음이 움직였으며, 레빈도 스네트코프에게 애틋한 정을 느꼈다."]], [["In the doorway the marshal of the province jostled against Levin.", "문간에서 도 귀족 회장이 레빈과 부딪쳤다."]], [["“Beg pardon, excuse me, please,”", "\"이거 죄송합니다, 실례했습니다.\""], ["he said as to a stranger, but recognizing Levin, he smiled timidly.", "그는 모르는 사람을 대하듯 말했으나, 레빈을 알아보고는 수줍게 미소 지었다."], ["It seemed to Levin that he would have liked to say something, but could not speak for emotion.", "레빈에게는 그가 무언가 말하고 싶어 하지만 감정에 북받쳐 말을 못 하는 것처럼 보였다."], ["His face and his whole figure in his uniform with the crosses, and white trousers striped with braid, as he moved hurriedly along, reminded Levin of some hunted beast who sees that he is in evil case.", "십자 훈장을 단 제복에 가두리 장식이 달린 흰 바지 차림으로 황급히 걸어가는 그의 얼굴과 그 모습 전체가, 레빈에게는 자기가 궁지에 몰린 것을 아는 어떤 쫓기는 짐승을 떠올리게 했다."], ["This expression in the marshal’s face was particularly touching to Levin, because, only the day before, he had been at his house about his trustee business and had seen him in all his grandeur, a kind-hearted, fatherly man.", "회장의 얼굴에 떠오른 이 표정이 레빈에게 유난히 가슴 아프게 다가온 것은, 바로 그 전날 후견 업무 때문에 그의 집을 찾아갔다가 위풍당당한 그의 모습, 마음씨 곱고 아버지 같은 그 사람을 보았기 때문이었다."], ["The big house with the old family furniture; the rather dirty, far from stylish, but respectful footmen, unmistakably old house serfs who had stuck to their master; the stout, good-natured wife in a cap with lace and a Turkish shawl, petting her pretty grandchild, her daughter’s daughter; the young son, a sixth form high school boy, coming home from school, and greeting his father, kissing his big hand; the genuine, cordial words and gestures of the old man—all this had the day before roused an instinctive feeling of respect and sympathy in Levin.", "오래된 가구가 놓인 그 큰 저택, 좀 지저분하고 멋스럽다고는 할 수 없으나 공손한 하인들—틀림없이 주인에게 끝까지 붙어 있던 늙은 집안 농노들이었다—레이스 달린 모자에 터키식 숄을 두르고, 딸이 낳은 어여쁜 손녀를 어르고 있던 뚱뚱하고 사람 좋은 아내, 학교에서 돌아와 아버지에게 인사하며 그 큼직한 손에 입을 맞추던 어린 아들, 고등학교 육학년생, 그리고 노인의 진실하고 다정한 말과 몸짓—이 모든 것이 그 전날 레빈에게 본능적인 존경과 동정의 감정을 불러일으켰던 것이다."], ["This old man was a touching and pathetic figure to Levin now, and he longed to say something pleasant to him.", "이 노인은 지금 레빈에게 가슴 아프고 애처로운 존재였고, 그는 그에게 무언가 기분 좋은 말을 해 주고 싶었다."]], [["“So you’re sure to be our marshal again,”", "\"그러니까 어르신은 틀림없이 다시 우리 회장이 되실 겁니다.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“It’s not likely,”", "\"그럴 것 같진 않소.\""], ["said the marshal, looking round with a scared expression.", "회장이 겁먹은 표정으로 주위를 둘러보며 말했다."], ["“I’m worn out, I’m old.", "\"나는 지쳤소, 늙었지."], ["If there are men younger and more deserving than I, let them serve.”", "나보다 더 젊고 더 자격 있는 사람들이 있다면, 그들이 봉직하게 합시다.\""]], [["And the marshal disappeared through a side door.", "그러고는 회장은 옆문으로 사라졌다."]], [["The most solemn moment was at hand.", "가장 엄숙한 순간이 다가오고 있었다."], ["They were to proceed immediately to the election.", "곧바로 선거에 들어갈 참이었다."], ["The leaders of both parties were reckoning white and black on their fingers.", "양쪽 일파의 우두머리들은 흰 표와 검은 표를 손가락으로 헤아리고 있었다."]], [["The discussion upon Flerov had given the new party not only Flerov’s vote, but had also gained time for them, so that they could send to fetch three noblemen who had been rendered unable to take part in the elections by the wiles of the other party.", "플레로프를 둘러싼 논쟁은 새 일파에게 플레로프의 표를 안겨 주었을 뿐 아니라 시간까지 벌어 주어, 그들은 상대편의 술책 때문에 선거에 참여하지 못하게 된 귀족 셋을 데리러 사람을 보낼 수 있었다."], ["Two noble gentlemen, who had a weakness for strong drink, had been made drunk by the partisans of Snetkov, and a third had been robbed of his uniform.", "독한 술에 약점이 있는 귀족 둘이 스네트코프 일파에게 술에 취하게 되었고, 또 한 사람은 제복을 빼앗긴 터였다."]], [["On learning this, the new party had made haste, during the dispute about Flerov, to send some of their men in a sledge to clothe the stripped gentleman, and to bring along one of the intoxicated to the meeting.", "이를 알아챈 새 일파는 플레로프에 관한 논쟁이 벌어지는 동안 서둘러 자기들 사람 몇을 썰매에 태워 보내, 옷을 빼앗긴 신사에게 옷을 입히고, 술 취한 사람 하나를 회의장으로 데려오게 했다."]], [["“I’ve brought one, drenched him with water,”", "\"한 사람 데려왔습니다, 물을 끼얹어 놨고요.\""], ["said the landowner, who had gone on this errand, to Sviazhsky.", "그 심부름을 다녀온 지주가 스비야시스키에게 말했다. \"괜찮아요."], ["“He’s all right? he’ll do.”", "쓸 만할 겁니다.\""]], [["“Not too drunk, he won’t fall down?”", "\"너무 취한 건 아니겠지, 쓰러지지 않겠어?\""], ["said Sviazhsky, shaking his head.", "스비야시스키가 고개를 저으며 말했다."]], [["“No, he’s first-rate.", "\"아닙니다, 아주 멀쩡합니다."], ["If only they don’t give him any more here....", "여기서 술만 더 안 주면 됩니다……"], ["I’ve told the waiter not to give him anything on any account.”", "무슨 일이 있어도 그 사람한테 아무것도 주지 말라고 급사한테 일러뒀습니다.\""]], [["# Chapter 29", "# Chapter 29"]], [["The narrow room, in which they were smoking and taking refreshments, was full of noblemen.", "사람들이 담배를 피우고 간식을 들고 있는 좁은 방은 귀족들로 가득 차 있었다."], ["The excitement grew more intense, and every face betrayed some uneasiness.", "흥분은 점점 더 격렬해졌고, 모든 얼굴에 어떤 불안이 드러났다."], ["The excitement was specially keen for the leaders of each party, who knew every detail, and had reckoned up every vote.", "흥분이 특히 격렬했던 것은 세세한 사정을 죄다 꿰뚫고 있으며 표 하나하나까지 셈해 둔 각 파벌의 지도자들이었다."], ["They were the generals organizing the approaching battle.", "그들은 다가올 전투를 짜는 장군들이었다."], ["The rest, like the rank and file before an engagement, though they were getting ready for the fight, sought for other distractions in the interval.", "나머지 사람들은 교전을 앞둔 졸병들처럼 싸울 채비를 갖추면서도 그 사이의 시간을 다른 데로 돌려 보내려 했다."], ["Some were lunching, standing at the bar, or sitting at the table; others were walking up and down the long room, smoking cigarettes, and talking with friends whom they had not seen for a long while.", "어떤 이들은 술집 앞에 선 채로, 또는 식탁에 앉아 간식을 들었고, 다른 이들은 긴 방을 오락가락하며 담배를 피우고 오랫동안 보지 못한 친구들과 이야기를 나누었다."]], [["Levin did not care to eat, and he was not smoking; he did not want to join his own friends, that is Sergey Ivanovitch, Stepan Arkadyevitch, Sviazhsky and the rest, because Vronsky in his equerry’s uniform was standing with them in eager conversation.", "레빈은 먹고 싶지도 않았고 담배도 피우지 않았다. 자기 친구들, 그러니까 세르게이 이바노비치며 스테판 아르카지치, 스비야시스키 같은 이들과 어울리고 싶지도 않았는데, 그것은 시종무관 제복을 입은 브론스키가 그들과 함께 열심히 이야기를 나누며 서 있었기 때문이었다."], ["Levin had seen him already at the meeting on the previous day, and he had studiously avoided him, not caring to greet him.", "레빈은 전날 회의에서 이미 그를 보았고, 인사를 나누고 싶지 않아 일부러 그를 피해 왔다."], ["He went to the window and sat down, scanning the groups, and listening to what was being said around him.", "그는 창가로 가서 앉아 무리들을 둘러보며 주위에서 오가는 말을 들었다."], ["He felt depressed, especially because everyone else was, as he saw, eager, anxious, and interested, and he alone, with an old, toothless little man with mumbling lips wearing a naval uniform, sitting beside him, had no interest in it and nothing to do.", "그는 우울했는데, 무엇보다도 보아하니 다른 사람들은 다들 열의에 차 있고 긴장해 있으며 관심이 깊은데, 옆에는 해군 제복을 입고 입술을 우물거리는, 이도 빠진 늙은이 하나가 앉아 있을 뿐, 자기 혼자만 거기에 아무 관심도 없고 할 일도 없었기 때문이다."]], [["“He’s such a blackguard!", "\"그놈은 정말 악당이야!"], ["I have told him so, but it makes no difference.", "내가 그렇게 말했는데도 도무지 소용이 없단 말이야."], ["Only think of it!", "생각해 보라고!"], ["He couldn’t collect it in three years!”", "삼 년이 지나도록 그걸 거둬들이질 못했다니까!\""], ["he heard vigorously uttered by a round-shouldered, short, country gentleman, who had pomaded hair hanging on his embroidered collar, and new boots obviously put on for the occasion, with heels that tapped energetically as he spoke.", "어깨가 둥글게 굽은, 키가 작은 시골 양반이 힘차게 내뱉는 소리가 들렸다. 그는 수놓은 깃 위로 포마드를 바른 머리카락을 늘어뜨리고, 이런 자리를 위해 일부러 신은 게 분명한 새 부츠를 신고 있었는데, 말을 할 때마다 굽이 기운차게 또각거렸다."], ["Casting a displeased glance at Levin, this gentleman sharply turned his back.", "이 양반은 레빈에게 못마땅한 눈길을 던지더니 홱 등을 돌렸다."]], [["“Yes, it’s a dirty business, there’s no denying,”", "\"그래, 더러운 일이지, 그건 부정할 수 없어.\""], ["a small gentleman assented in a high voice.", "키 작은 양반 하나가 새된 목소리로 맞장구를 쳤다."]], [["Next, a whole crowd of country gentlemen, surrounding a stout general, hurriedly came near Levin.", "이어서 한 무리의 시골 양반들이 뚱뚱한 장군을 둘러싼 채 황급히 레빈 가까이로 다가왔다."], ["These persons were unmistakably seeking a place where they could talk without being overheard.", "이 사람들은 누가 엿듣지 못하는 데서 이야기할 자리를 찾고 있는 것이 분명했다."]], [["“How dare he say I had his breeches stolen!", "\"감히 내가 제 바지를 훔쳐 가게 했다고 지껄이다니!"], ["Pawned them for drink, I expect.", "제가 술값으로 잡혔겠지, 분명히."], ["Damn the fellow, prince indeed!", "빌어먹을 놈, 공작이라니 가당치도 않아!"], ["He’d better not say it, the beast!”", "그딴 말은 하지 않는 게 좋을 거야, 그 짐승 같은 놈!\""]], [["“But excuse me!", "\"하지만 실례지만!"], ["They take their stand on the act,”", "그자들은 법령을 내세우거든.\""], ["was being said in another group; “the wife must be registered as noble.”", "다른 무리에서 이런 말이 오갔다. \"아내가 귀족으로 등록되어 있어야 한다는 거지.\""]], [["“Oh, damn your acts!", "\"오, 자네 법령 따위 엿이나 먹으라지!"], ["I speak from my heart.", "나는 진심으로 말하는 거야."], ["We’re all gentlemen, aren’t we?", "우리는 다 양반들 아닌가?"], ["Above suspicion.”", "의심받을 게 없는 사람들이라고.\""]], [["“Shall we go on, your excellency, fine champagne?”", "\"가실까요, 각하, fine champagne 한잔 어떠십니까?\""]], [["Another group was following a nobleman, who was shouting something in a loud voice; it was one of the three intoxicated gentlemen.", "또 다른 무리는 큰 소리로 뭐라고 외쳐 대는 귀족 하나를 따라가고 있었다. 그는 취한 양반 셋 중 하나였다."]], [["“I always advised Marya Semyonovna to let for a fair rent, for she can never save a profit,”", "\"내가 마리야 세묘노브나에게는 늘 적당한 임대료로 빌려주라고 충고했지."], ["he heard a pleasant voice say.", "그러지 않고는 결코 이문을 남길 수 없으니까.\""], ["The speaker was a country gentleman with gray whiskers, wearing the regimental uniform of an old general staff-officer.", "듣기 좋은 목소리로 누군가 말하는 소리가 들렸다. 말하는 이는 회색 구레나룻을 기른 시골 양반으로, 옛 참모 장교의 연대 제복을 입고 있었다."], ["It was the very landowner Levin had met at Sviazhsky’s.", "바로 레빈이 스비야시스키의 집에서 만났던 그 지주였다."], ["He knew him at once.", "레빈은 그를 단번에 알아보았다."], ["The landowner too stared at Levin, and they exchanged greetings.", "지주 역시 레빈을 빤히 바라보았고, 둘은 인사를 나누었다."]], [["“Very glad to see you!", "\"이거 만나 뵙게 되어 정말 반갑소이다!"], ["To be sure!", "그렇고말고요!"], ["I remember you very well.", "당신을 아주 잘 기억하고 있지요."], ["Last year at our district marshal, Nikolay Ivanovitch’s.”", "작년에 우리 군 귀족회장이신 니콜라이 이바노비치 댁에서 뵈었지요.\""]], [["“Well, and how is your land doing?”", "\"그래, 당신 땅은 어떻게 돼 가고 있습니까?\""], ["asked Levin.", "레빈이 물었다."]], [["“Oh, still just the same, always at a loss,”", "\"오, 여전히 그저 똑같습죠, 늘 밑지고 있지요.\""], ["the landowner answered with a resigned smile, but with an expression of serenity and conviction that so it must be.", "지주는 체념한 미소를 띠며 대답했지만, 그 표정에는 으레 그래야 마땅하다는 듯한 평온함과 확신이 어려 있었다."], ["“And how do you come to be in our province?”", "\"그런데 어쩐 일로 우리 도(道)까지 오셨소?\""], ["he asked.", "그가 물었다."], ["“Come to take part in our coup d’état?”", "\"우리의 coup d'état에 한몫 거들러 오셨소?\""], ["he said, confidently pronouncing the French words with a bad accent.", "그는 자신만만하게, 형편없는 발음으로 그 프랑스어를 발음하며 말했다."], ["“All Russia’s here—gentlemen of the bedchamber, and everything short of the ministry.”", "\"온 러시아가 다 여기 모였지요. 시종(侍從)들에서부터, 장관 빼고는 죄다 와 있다니까요.\""], ["He pointed to the imposing figure of Stepan Arkadyevitch in white trousers and his court uniform, walking by with a general.", "그는 흰 바지에 궁정 제복을 입고 장군 한 사람과 나란히 걸어가는 스테판 아르카지치의 당당한 모습을 가리켰다."]], [["“I ought to own that I don’t very well understand the drift of the provincial elections,”", "\"솔직히 고백하자면, 저는 이 지방 선거의 취지를 잘 이해하지 못하겠습니다.\""], ["said Levin.", "레빈이 말했다."]], [["The landowner looked at him.", "지주가 그를 바라보았다."]], [["“Why, what is there to understand?", "\"아니, 이해하고 자시고 할 게 뭐 있겠소?"], ["There’s no meaning in it at all.", "거기엔 아무 의미도 없어요."], ["It’s a decaying institution that goes on running only by the force of inertia.", "그저 관성의 힘으로만 굴러가는, 다 쓰러져 가는 제도지요."], ["Just look, the very uniforms tell you that it’s an assembly of justices of the peace, permanent members of the court, and so on, but not of noblemen.”", "보시구려, 제복만 봐도 이게 치안판사며 법원 상임위원 따위의 모임이지, 귀족들의 모임이 아니라는 걸 알 수 있잖소.\""]], [["“Then why do you come?”", "\"그렇다면 왜 오시는 겁니까?\""], ["asked Levin.", "레빈이 물었다."]], [["“From habit, nothing else.", "\"습관이지요, 그뿐이오."], ["Then, too, one must keep up connections.", "게다가 인맥도 유지해야 하고 말이오."], ["It’s a moral obligation of a sort.", "일종의 도덕적 의무인 셈이지요."], ["And then, to tell the truth, there’s one’s own interests.", "그리고 솔직히 말하자면, 제 잇속도 있고요."], ["My son-in-law wants to stand as a permanent member; they’re not rich people, and he must be brought forward.", "사위가 상임위원으로 나서고 싶어 하는데, 넉넉한 사람들이 아니라서 밀어 줘야 하거든요."], ["These gentlemen, now, what do they come for?”", "그건 그렇고, 저런 양반들은 대체 무엇 하러 오는 걸까요?\""], ["he said, pointing to the malignant gentleman, who was talking at the high table.", "그는 높은 식탁에서 떠들어 대고 있는, 그 심술궂은 양반을 가리키며 말했다."]], [["“That’s the new generation of nobility.”", "\"저들은 새로운 세대의 귀족이지요.\""]], [["“New it may be, but nobility it isn’t.", "\"새롭긴 새롭겠지만, 귀족은 아니지요."], ["They’re proprietors of a sort, but we’re the landowners.", "일종의 자산가들이긴 해도, 우리야말로 지주(地主)란 말이오."], ["As noblemen, they’re cutting their own throats.”", "귀족으로서 보자면, 저들은 제 목을 제 손으로 베는 짓을 하고 있는 거요.\""]], [["“But you say it’s an institution that’s served its time.”", "\"하지만 당신은 이게 제 수명을 다한 제도라고 하시지 않습니까.\""]], [["“That it may be, but still it ought to be treated a little more respectfully.", "\"그럴지도 모르지요. 하지만 그래도 좀 더 존중하며 다뤄야 마땅한 거요."], ["Snetkov, now....", "가령 스네트코프만 봐도……"], ["We may be of use, or we may not, but we’re the growth of a thousand years.", "우리가 쓸모가 있을지 없을지는 모르겠지만, 우리는 천 년에 걸쳐 자라난 산물이란 말이오."], ["If we’re laying out a garden, planning one before the house, you know, and there you’ve a tree that’s stood for centuries in the very spot....", "정원을 가꾼다 칩시다, 집 앞에 하나 설계한다 칩시다, 그런데 바로 그 자리에 수백 년을 버텨 온 나무가 한 그루 서 있단 말이지요……"], ["Old and gnarled it may be, and yet you don’t cut down the old fellow to make room for the flowerbeds, but lay out your beds so as to take advantage of the tree.", "늙고 옹이가 졌을지언정, 화단 자리를 내겠다고 그 늙은 친구를 베어 버리지는 않잖소. 도리어 그 나무를 살리도록 화단을 배치하지요."], ["You won’t grow him again in a year,”", "그런 나무는 일 년 만에 다시 길러 낼 수 없으니까.\""], ["he said cautiously, and he immediately changed the conversation.", "그는 조심스럽게 말하고는 곧장 화제를 바꾸었다."], ["“Well, and how is your land doing?”", "\"그래, 당신 땅은 어떻게 돼 가고 있소?\""]], [["“Oh, not very well.", "\"오, 그리 잘되지는 않습니다."], ["I make five per cent.”", "오 푼 정도 남기지요.\""]], [["“Yes, but you don’t reckon your own work.", "\"그래요, 하지만 당신은 제 노동은 셈에 넣지 않는 거요."], ["Aren’t you worth something too?", "당신 자신도 무언가 값어치가 있지 않겠소?"], ["I’ll tell you my own case.", "내 경우를 얘기해 드리리다."], ["Before I took to seeing after the land, I had a salary of three hundred pounds from the service.", "내가 땅을 직접 돌보기 시작하기 전에는 관직에서 삼백 파운드의 급료를 받았소."], ["Now I do more work than I did in the service, and like you I get five per cent. on the land, and thank God for that.", "지금은 관직에 있을 때보다 더 많은 일을 하는데, 당신처럼 땅에서 오 푼을 얻고, 그것만으로도 하느님께 감사하지요."], ["But one’s work is thrown in for nothing.”", "하지만 제 노동은 거저 던져 넣는 셈이오.\""]], [["“Then why do you do it, if it’s a clear loss?”", "\"그렇다면 그게 분명한 손해인데도 왜 하시는 겁니까?\""]], [["“Oh, well, one does it!", "\"오, 글쎄, 그냥 하는 거지요!"], ["What would you have?", "그럼 어쩌겠소?"], ["It’s habit, and one knows it’s how it should be.", "습관이고, 또 마땅히 그래야 하는 거라는 걸 아니까."], ["And what’s more,”", "게다가 말이오,\""], ["the landowner went on, leaning his elbows on the window and chatting on, “my son, I must tell you, has no taste for it.", "지주는 창틀에 팔꿈치를 괴고 이야기를 이어 갔다. \"내 아들놈은, 이 말은 해 둬야겠는데, 그런 일에 도무지 취미가 없소."], ["There’s no doubt he’ll be a scientific man.", "그놈은 영락없이 학자가 될 거요."], ["So there’ll be no one to keep it up.", "그러니 이걸 이어 갈 사람이 없는 셈이지요."], ["And yet one does it.", "그런데도 하는 거요."], ["Here this year I’ve planted an orchard.”", "올해만 해도 여기에 과수원을 하나 심었다오.\""]], [["“Yes, yes,”", "\"그래요, 그래요.\""], ["said Levin, “that’s perfectly true.", "레빈이 말했다. \"그건 정말 옳은 말씀입니다."], ["I always feel there’s no real balance of gain in my work on the land, and yet one does it....", "저도 땅에서 하는 제 일에 진정한 이득의 균형 같은 건 없다고 늘 느끼는데, 그런데도 하게 되지요……"], ["It’s a sort of duty one feels to the land.”", "땅에 대해 느끼는 일종의 의무 같은 거랄까요.\""]], [["“But I tell you what,”", "\"하지만 내 한마디 하리다.\""], ["the landowner pursued; “a neighbor of mine, a merchant, was at my place.", "지주가 말을 이었다. \"우리 이웃 중에 장사꾼이 하나 있는데, 그자가 우리 집에 왔었소."], ["We walked about the fields and the garden.", "우리는 들판이며 정원을 둘러보았지요. '아니요,'"], ["‘No,’ said he, ‘Stepan Vassilievitch, everything’s well looked after, but your garden’s neglected.’ But, as a fact, it’s well kept up.", "그자가 그러더군. '스테판 바실리예비치, 다른 건 죄다 잘 건사해 두셨는데, 정원은 방치돼 있네요.'"], ["‘To my thinking, I’d cut down that lime-tree.", "하지만 사실 정원도 잘 관리돼 있었소."], ["Here you’ve thousands of limes, and each would make two good bundles of bark.", "'제 생각에는, 저 보리수를 베어 버리겠어요. 여기 보리수가 수천 그루나 있는데, 한 그루당 좋은 껍질 두 다발은 나오겠어요."], ["And nowadays that bark’s worth something.", "게다가 요즘 그 껍질이 값이 좀 나가거든요."], ["I’d cut down the lot.’”", "저라면 죄다 베어 버리겠습니다.'\""]], [["“And with what he made he’d increase his stock, or buy some land for a trifle, and let it out in lots to the peasants,”", "\"그러고는 그걸로 번 돈으로 가축을 늘리거나, 헐값에 땅을 좀 사들여서, 그걸 잘게 쪼개 농부들에게 빌려주겠지요.\""], ["Levin added, smiling.", "레빈이 미소 지으며 덧붙였다."], ["He had evidently more than once come across those commercial calculations.", "그는 그런 상업적 계산을 한두 번 접해 본 게 아닌 듯했다."], ["“And he’d make his fortune.", "\"그래서 한밑천 잡는 거지요."], ["But you and I must thank God if we keep what we’ve got and leave it to our children.”", "하지만 당신이나 나나 우리가 가진 걸 지켜서 자식들에게 물려줄 수만 있어도 하느님께 감사해야 할 처지란 말입니다.\""]], [["“You’re married, I’ve heard?”", "\"결혼하셨다고 들었는데요?\""], ["said the landowner.", "지주가 말했다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["Levin answered, with proud satisfaction.", "레빈이 자랑스러운 만족감을 담아 대답했다."], ["“Yes, it’s rather strange,”", "\"네, 좀 이상하지요.\""], ["he went on.", "그가 말을 이었다."], ["“So we live without making anything, as though we were ancient vestals set to keep in a fire.”", "\"그러니까 우리는 아무것도 벌어들이지 못하면서 사는 셈이오, 마치 불씨를 지키도록 세워진 옛 베스타 여사제들처럼 말입니다.\""]], [["The landowner chuckled under his white mustaches.", "지주는 흰 콧수염 아래로 빙긋 웃었다."]], [["“There are some among us, too, like our friend Nikolay Ivanovitch, or Count Vronsky, that’s settled here lately, who try to carry on their husbandry as though it were a factory; but so far it leads to nothing but making away with capital on it.”", "\"우리 중에도 그런 사람들이 더러 있지요. 우리 친구 니콜라이 이바노비치라든가, 근래에 이곳에 정착한 브론스키 백작 같은 이들 말이오. 그자들은 마치 공장이라도 운영하듯 농사를 꾸려 가려 하지만, 지금까지는 거기에 자본을 탕진하는 것 말고는 아무것도 이루지 못했다오.\""]], [["“But why is it we don’t do like the merchants?", "\"그런데 어째서 우리는 장사꾼들처럼 하지 않는 걸까요?"], ["Why don’t we cut down our parks for timber?”", "어째서 우리는 목재로 쓰려고 우리 정원을 베어 버리지 않는 걸까요?\""], ["said Levin, returning to a thought that had struck him.", "자기 머릿속을 스쳤던 생각으로 되돌아가며 레빈이 말했다."]], [["“Why, as you said, to keep the fire in.", "\"아니, 당신이 말했듯이, 불씨를 지키기 위해서지요."], ["Besides that’s not work for a nobleman.", "게다가 그건 귀족이 할 일이 아니오."], ["And our work as noblemen isn’t done here at the elections, but yonder, each in our corner.", "그리고 귀족으로서 우리가 할 일은 여기 선거에서 하는 게 아니라, 저마다 제 구석에서 하는 거란 말이오."], ["There’s a class instinct, too, of what one ought and oughtn’t to do.", "무엇을 해야 하고 무엇을 해서는 안 되는지에 대한 일종의 계급적 본능 같은 것도 있고요."], ["There’s the peasants, too, I wonder at them sometimes; any good peasant tries to take all the land he can.", "농부들도 그렇소, 나는 가끔 그들을 보면 놀라워요. 변변한 농부라면 누구나 가질 수 있는 땅은 죄다 가지려 하지요."], ["However bad the land is, he’ll work it.", "땅이 아무리 나빠도 그걸 부쳐 먹는단 말이오."], ["Without a return too.", "게다가 아무 소득도 없이."], ["At a simple loss.”", "그저 손해를 보면서도 말이지요.\""]], [["“Just as we do,”", "\"우리가 하는 것과 꼭 같군요.\""], ["said Levin.", "레빈이 말했다."], ["“Very, very glad to have met you,”", "\"정말, 정말 만나 뵈어서 반가웠습니다.\""], ["he added, seeing Sviazhsky approaching him.", "그는 스비야시스키가 자기 쪽으로 다가오는 것을 보고 이렇게 덧붙였다."]], [["“And here we’ve met for the first time since we met at your place,”", "\"우리는 당신 댁에서 만난 이후로 여기서 처음 만난 거라오.\""], ["said the landowner to Sviazhsky, “and we’ve had a good talk too.”", "지주가 스비야시스키에게 말했다. \"그래서 이야기도 실컷 나눴소이다.\""]], [["“Well, have you been attacking the new order of things?”", "\"그래, 새로운 질서를 한바탕 공격하고 계셨던 겁니까?\""], ["said Sviazhsky with a smile.", "스비야시스키가 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["“That we’re bound to do.”", "\"그럴 수밖에요.\""]], [["“You’ve relieved your feelings?”", "\"속이 좀 후련해지셨나요?\""]], [["# Chapter 30", "# Chapter 30"]], [["Sviazhsky took Levin’s arm, and went with him to his own friends.", "스비아시스키는 레빈의 팔을 잡고 자기 친구들이 있는 쪽으로 함께 갔다."]], [["This time there was no avoiding Vronsky.", "이번에는 브론스키를 피할 도리가 없었다."], ["He was standing with Stepan Arkadyevitch and Sergey Ivanovitch, and looking straight at Levin as he drew near.", "그는 스테판 아르카지치와 세르게이 이바노비치와 함께 서 있었고, 레빈이 다가가자 그를 똑바로 바라보았다."]], [["“Delighted!", "\"반갑습니다!"], ["I believe I’ve had the pleasure of meeting you ...", "전에 뵌 적이 있는 것 같군요……"], ["at Princess Shtcherbatskaya’s,”", "셰르바츠카야 공작 영애 댁에서요.\""], ["he said, giving Levin his hand.", "그가 레빈에게 손을 내밀며 말했다."]], [["“Yes, I quite remember our meeting,”", "\"네, 그때 만난 걸 잘 기억합니다.\""], ["said Levin, and blushing crimson, he turned away immediately, and began talking to his brother.", "레빈은 이렇게 말하더니, 얼굴이 새빨개진 채 곧바로 돌아서서 형과 이야기를 나누기 시작했다."]], [["With a slight smile Vronsky went on talking to Sviazhsky, obviously without the slightest inclination to enter into conversation with Levin.", "브론스키는 엷은 미소를 지으며 스비아시스키와 계속 이야기를 나누었는데, 레빈과 대화를 트고 싶은 마음이 조금도 없는 게 분명했다."], ["But Levin, as he talked to his brother, was continually looking round at Vronsky, trying to think of something to say to him to gloss over his rudeness.", "하지만 레빈은 형과 이야기하면서도 자기의 무례함을 무마할 만한 말을 찾으려고 끊임없이 브론스키 쪽을 돌아보았다."]], [["“What are we waiting for now?”", "\"지금 우리는 뭘 기다리는 겁니까?\""], ["asked Levin, looking at Sviazhsky and Vronsky.", "레빈이 스비아시스키와 브론스키를 보며 물었다."]], [["“For Snetkov.", "\"스네트코프를요."], ["He has to refuse or to consent to stand,”", "그가 출마를 거부할지 수락할지 결정해야 하거든요.\""], ["answered Sviazhsky.", "스비아시스키가 대답했다."]], [["“Well, and what has he done, consented or not?”", "\"그래서, 어떻게 됐답니까, 수락했답니까, 아닙니까?\""]], [["“That’s the point, that he’s done neither,”", "\"바로 그게 문제죠, 이쪽도 저쪽도 아니거든요.\""], ["said Vronsky.", "브론스키가 말했다."]], [["“And if he refuses, who will stand then?”", "\"그가 거부하면 그땐 누가 출마하는 겁니까?\""], ["asked Levin, looking at Vronsky.", "레빈이 브론스키를 보며 물었다."]], [["“Whoever chooses to,”", "\"하겠다는 사람이 하는 거죠.\""], ["said Sviazhsky.", "스비아시스키가 말했다."]], [["“Shall you?”", "\"당신이 하시는 겁니까?\""], ["asked Levin.", "레빈이 물었다."]], [["“Certainly not I,”", "\"절대 제가 아닙니다.\""], ["said Sviazhsky, looking confused, and turning an alarmed glance at the malignant gentleman, who was standing beside Sergey Ivanovitch.", "스비아시스키는 당황한 기색으로, 세르게이 이바노비치 옆에 서 있는 심술궂은 신사에게 놀란 시선을 던지며 말했다."]], [["“Who then?", "\"그럼 누구죠?"], ["Nevyedovsky?”", "네뱌도프스키입니까?\""], ["said Levin, feeling he was putting his foot into it.", "레빈은 자기가 실수를 저지르고 있다는 걸 느끼며 말했다."]], [["But this was worse still.", "하지만 이건 더 나빴다."], ["Nevyedovsky and Sviazhsky were the two candidates.", "네뱌도프스키와 스비아시스키가 두 후보였던 것이다."]], [["“I certainly shall not, under any circumstances,”", "\"저는 어떤 경우에도 절대 하지 않습니다.\""], ["answered the malignant gentleman.", "심술궂은 신사가 대답했다."]], [["This was Nevyedovsky himself.", "이 사람이 바로 네뱌도프스키였다."], ["Sviazhsky introduced him to Levin.", "스비아시스키가 그를 레빈에게 소개해 주었다."]], [["“Well, you find it exciting too?”", "\"자, 당신도 이게 흥미진진하지요?\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, winking at Vronsky.", "스테판 아르카지치가 브론스키에게 눈을 찡긋하며 말했다."], ["“It’s something like a race.", "\"꼭 경마 같단 말이죠."], ["One might bet on it.”", "내기를 걸어도 좋을 정도예요.\""]], [["“Yes, it is keenly exciting,”", "\"그래, 무척 흥미진진해.\""], ["said Vronsky.", "브론스키가 말했다."], ["“And once taking the thing up, one’s eager to see it through.", "\"일단 일을 시작하면 끝까지 보고 싶어지는 법이지."], ["It’s a fight!”", "이건 싸움이야!\""], ["he said, scowling and setting his powerful jaws.", "그는 눈살을 찌푸리며 억센 턱을 굳게 다물고 말했다."]], [["“What a capable fellow Sviazhsky is!", "\"스비아시스키는 정말 유능한 친구야!"], ["Sees it all so clearly.”", "모든 걸 그렇게 명확히 꿰뚫어 보니 말이지.\""]], [["“Oh, yes!”", "\"오, 그렇지!\""], ["Vronsky assented indifferently.", "브론스키가 무심하게 맞장구를 쳤다."]], [["A silence followed, during which Vronsky—since he had to look at something—looked at Levin, at his feet, at his uniform, then at his face, and noticing his gloomy eyes fixed upon him, he said, in order to say something:", "침묵이 이어졌고, 그 사이에 브론스키는—무언가를 보긴 봐야 했기에—레빈을, 그의 발을, 그의 제복을 바라보다가 그의 얼굴을 보았고, 자기에게 꽂힌 그 침울한 눈길을 알아채고는 무슨 말이든 하려고 입을 열었다."]], [["“How is it that you, living constantly in the country, are not a justice of the peace?", "\"시골에서 줄곧 사시는데 어째서 치안판사가 아니십니까?"], ["You are not in the uniform of one.”", "치안판사 제복을 입고 계시지 않군요.\""]], [["“It’s because I consider that the justice of the peace is a silly institution,”", "\"치안판사란 어리석은 제도라고 생각하기 때문입니다.\""], ["Levin answered gloomily.", "레빈이 침울하게 대답했다."], ["He had been all the time looking for an opportunity to enter into conversation with Vronsky, so as to smooth over his rudeness at their first meeting.", "그는 첫 대면 때의 무례함을 만회하려고 줄곧 브론스키와 대화를 틀 기회를 노리던 참이었다."]], [["“I don’t think so, quite the contrary,”", "\"저는 그렇게 생각하지 않습니다, 오히려 정반대지요.\""], ["Vronsky said, with quiet surprise.", "브론스키가 조용히 놀라며 말했다."]], [["“It’s a plaything,”", "\"그건 장난감 같은 거예요.\""], ["Levin cut him short.", "레빈이 그의 말을 잘랐다."], ["“We don’t want justices of the peace.", "\"우리에겐 치안판사가 필요 없어요."], ["I’ve never had a single thing to do with them during eight years.", "저는 팔 년 동안 그들과 단 한 번도 엮인 적이 없습니다."], ["And what I have had was decided wrongly by them.", "그리고 어쩌다 엮인 일은 그들이 잘못 판결해 버렸지요."], ["The justice of the peace is over thirty miles from me.", "치안판사가 우리 집에서 삼십 베르스타도 넘게 떨어져 있어요."], ["For some matter of two roubles I should have to send a lawyer, who costs me fifteen.”", "이 루블짜리 사건 하나 때문에 변호사를 보내야 하는데, 그 변호사 비용이 십오 루블이나 든단 말입니다.\""]], [["And he related how a peasant had stolen some flour from the miller, and when the miller told him of it, had lodged a complaint for slander.", "그러고는 어느 농부가 방앗간 주인에게서 밀가루를 훔쳤는데, 방앗간 주인이 그 일을 따지자 명예훼손으로 고소를 했다는 이야기를 늘어놓았다."], ["All this was utterly uncalled for and stupid, and Levin felt it himself as he said it.", "이 모든 게 전혀 쓸데없고 어리석은 이야기였고, 레빈 자신도 말하면서 그것을 느꼈다."]], [["“Oh, this is such an original fellow!”", "\"오, 이 친구 정말 별난 사람이에요!\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch with his most soothing, almond-oil smile.", "스테판 아르카지치가 더없이 부드러운, 편도유처럼 매끄러운 미소를 지으며 말했다."], ["“But come along; I think they’re voting....”", "\"자, 가 봅시다. 투표를 하는 것 같으니…….\""]], [["And they separated.", "그리고 그들은 흩어졌다."]], [["“I can’t understand,”", "\"난 이해가 안 가.\""], ["said Sergey Ivanovitch, who had observed his brother’s clumsiness, “I can’t understand how anyone can be so absolutely devoid of political tact.", "동생의 서툰 처신을 지켜본 세르게이 이바노비치가 말했다. \"어떻게 사람이 그렇게 정치적 요령이라곤 완전히 없을 수 있는지 이해가 안 돼."], ["That’s where we Russians are so deficient.", "바로 그 점이 우리 러시아 사람들의 결점이지."], ["The marshal of the province is our opponent, and with him you’re ami cochon, and you beg him to stand.", "도의 귀족 단장은 우리의 적인데, 너는 그와 ami cochon이면서 그더러 출마하라고 청하지 않나. 브론스키 백작만 해도……"], ["Count Vronsky, now ...", "난 그를 친구로 삼는 건 아니야."], ["I’m not making a friend of him; he’s asked me to dinner, and I’m not going; but he’s one of our side—why make an enemy of him?", "그가 만찬에 초대했지만 나는 가지 않을 거야. 하지만 그는 우리 편이거든—그런데 뭐 하러 그를 적으로 만드나?"], ["Then you ask Nevyedovsky if he’s going to stand.", "게다가 너는 네뱌도프스키한테 출마할 거냐고 묻질 않나. 그건 할 짓이 아니야.\""], ["That’s not a thing to do.”", "* ami cochon: 프랑스어로 '허물없는 친구', 막역한 사이."]], [["“Oh, I don’t understand it at all!", "\"오, 전 그런 게 도무지 이해가 안 됩니다!"], ["And it’s all such nonsense,”", "그리고 이게 다 그렇게 부질없는 짓이잖아요.\""], ["Levin answered gloomily.", "레빈이 침울하게 대답했다."]], [["“You say it’s all such nonsense, but as soon as you have anything to do with it, you make a muddle.”", "\"너는 이게 다 부질없는 짓이라고 말하면서도, 막상 그 일에 끼어들기만 하면 엉망으로 만들어 버리잖니.\""]], [["Levin did not answer, and they walked together into the big room.", "레빈은 대답하지 않았고, 그들은 함께 큰 방으로 걸어 들어갔다."]], [["The marshal of the province, though he was vaguely conscious in the air of some trap being prepared for him, and though he had not been called upon by all to stand, had still made up his mind to stand.", "도의 귀족 단장은 막연하게나마 자기를 노리는 어떤 함정이 준비되고 있다는 것을 분위기로 감지하고 있었고, 모두가 그에게 출마를 권한 것도 아니었지만, 그래도 출마하기로 마음을 굳힌 터였다."], ["All was silence in the room.", "방 안은 온통 고요했다."], ["The secretary announced in a loud voice that the captain of the guards, Mihail Stepanovitch Snetkov, would now be balloted for as marshal of the province.", "서기가 큰 소리로, 친위대 대위 미하일 스테파노비치 스네트코프에 대해 이제 도의 귀족 단장 선출 투표를 실시하겠다고 알렸다."]], [["The district marshals walked carrying plates, on which were balls, from their tables to the high table, and the election began.", "군의 귀족 단장들이 구슬이 담긴 접시를 들고 자기들 탁자에서 높은 탁자로 걸어갔고, 선거가 시작되었다."]], [["“Put it in the right side,”", "\"오른쪽에 넣어.\""], ["whispered Stepan Arkadyevitch, as with his brother Levin followed the marshal of his district to the table.", "레빈이 형과 함께 자기 군의 귀족 단장을 따라 탁자로 갔을 때, 스테판 아르카지치가 속삭였다."], ["But Levin had forgotten by now the calculations that had been explained to him, and was afraid Stepan Arkadyevitch might be mistaken in saying “the right side.”", "하지만 레빈은 이미 그에게 설명해 준 셈법을 잊어버린 상태였고, 스테판 아르카지치가 \"오른쪽\"이라고 말한 게 틀린 게 아닐까 두려웠다."], ["Surely Snetkov was the enemy.", "분명 스네트코프는 적이지 않은가."], ["As he went up, he held the ball in his right hand, but thinking he was wrong, just at the box he changed to the left hand, and undoubtedly put the ball to the left.", "그는 다가가면서 구슬을 오른손에 쥐고 있었지만, 자기가 틀렸다고 생각하고는 바로 투표함 앞에서 왼손으로 바꿔 쥐었고, 틀림없이 구슬을 왼쪽에 넣었다."], ["An adept in the business, standing at the box and seeing by the mere action of the elbow where each put his ball, scowled with annoyance.", "투표함 옆에 서서 팔꿈치의 움직임만으로 각자가 구슬을 어디에 넣는지 알아내던 그 방면의 전문가는 못마땅한 듯 눈살을 찌푸렸다."], ["It was no good for him to use his insight.", "이번에는 그의 통찰력이 소용없었다."]], [["Everything was still, and the counting of the balls was heard.", "모든 것이 고요했고, 구슬을 세는 소리가 들렸다."], ["Then a single voice rose and proclaimed the numbers for and against.", "그러고 나서 한 사람의 목소리가 일어나 찬성과 반대의 숫자를 선포했다."], ["The marshal had been voted for by a considerable majority.", "단장은 상당한 다수표로 선출되었다."], ["All was noise and eager movement towards the doors.", "사방이 떠들썩해졌고, 사람들이 다투어 문 쪽으로 몰려갔다."], ["Snetkov came in, and the nobles thronged round him, congratulating him.", "스네트코프가 들어오자 귀족들이 그를 둘러싸고 모여들어 축하의 말을 건넸다."]], [["“Well, now is it over?”", "\"자, 이제 끝난 겁니까?\""], ["Levin asked Sergey Ivanovitch.", "레빈이 세르게이 이바노비치에게 물었다."]], [["“It’s only just beginning,”", "\"이제 막 시작된 거죠.\""], ["Sviazhsky said, replying for Sergey Ivanovitch with a smile.", "스비아시스키가 미소를 지으며 세르게이 이바노비치를 대신해 대답했다."], ["“Some other candidate may receive more votes than the marshal.”", "\"다른 후보가 단장보다 더 많은 표를 얻을 수도 있으니까요.\""]], [["Levin had quite forgotten about that.", "레빈은 그 점을 까맣게 잊고 있었다."], ["Now he could only remember that there was some sort of trickery in it, but he was too bored to think what it was exactly.", "이제 그는 여기에 어떤 술책 같은 것이 있다는 것만 떠올릴 수 있을 뿐, 그게 정확히 무엇인지 생각하기에는 너무 지루했다."], ["He felt depressed, and longed to get out of the crowd.", "그는 의기소침해졌고, 이 군중 속을 벗어나고 싶었다."]], [["As no one was paying any attention to him, and no one apparently needed him, he quietly slipped away into the little room where the refreshments were, and again had a great sense of comfort when he saw the waiters.", "아무도 그에게 관심을 두지 않았고, 아무도 그를 필요로 하지 않는 듯 보였기에, 그는 슬그머니 다과가 차려진 작은 방으로 빠져나갔고, 급사들을 보자 또다시 큰 안도감을 느꼈다."], ["The little old waiter pressed him to have something, and Levin agreed.", "자그마한 노급사가 뭔가 좀 들라고 권했고, 레빈은 그러기로 했다."], ["After eating a cutlet with beans and talking to the waiters of their former masters, Levin, not wishing to go back to the hall, where it was all so distasteful to him, proceeded to walk through the galleries.", "콩을 곁들인 커틀릿을 먹고 급사들과 그들의 옛 주인들에 대해 이야기를 나눈 뒤, 레빈은 모든 것이 그렇게도 거북한 그 홀로 돌아가고 싶지 않아 회랑들을 거닐기 시작했다."], ["The galleries were full of fashionably dressed ladies, leaning over the balustrade and trying not to lose a single word of what was being said below.", "회랑에는 멋지게 차려입은 부인들이 가득했는데, 그들은 난간 너머로 몸을 내밀고 아래에서 오가는 말을 한 마디도 놓치지 않으려 애쓰고 있었다."], ["With the ladies were sitting and standing smart lawyers, high school teachers in spectacles, and officers.", "부인들과 함께 멋쟁이 변호사들과 안경을 쓴 김나지움 교사들과 장교들이 앉거나 서 있었다."], ["Everywhere they were talking of the election, and of how worried the marshal was, and how splendid the discussions had been.", "어디서나 사람들은 선거 이야기를, 그리고 단장이 얼마나 애를 태웠는지, 또 토론이 얼마나 훌륭했는지를 이야기하고 있었다."], ["In one group Levin heard his brother’s praises.", "한 무리에서 레빈은 형을 칭송하는 말을 들었다."], ["One lady was telling a lawyer:", "어떤 부인이 한 변호사에게 이렇게 말하고 있었다."]], [["“How glad I am I heard Koznishev!", "\"코즈니셰프의 연설을 들어서 어찌나 기쁜지요!"], ["It’s worth losing one’s dinner.", "저녁 식사를 거를 만한 가치가 있어요."], ["He’s exquisite!", "그분은 정말 탁월해요!"], ["So clear and distinct all of it!", "그 모든 게 어찌나 명료하고 또렷한지!"], ["There’s not one of you in the law courts that speaks like that.", "법정에 계신 여러분 중에는 그렇게 말하는 분이 없어요."], ["The only one is Meidel, and he’s not so eloquent by a long way.”", "유일하게 마이델이 있긴 하지만, 그분도 한참 못 미친답니다.\""]], [["Finding a free place, Levin leaned over the balustrade and began looking and listening.", "빈자리를 찾은 레빈은 난간 너머로 몸을 기대고 보고 듣기 시작했다."]], [["All the noblemen were sitting railed off behind barriers according to their districts.", "모든 귀족이 자기 군에 따라 칸막이 뒤에 구획을 나누어 앉아 있었다."], ["In the middle of the room stood a man in a uniform, who shouted in a loud, high voice:", "방 한가운데에는 제복을 입은 한 남자가 서서, 크고 높은 목소리로 외쳤다."]], [["“As a candidate for the marshalship of the nobility of the province we call upon staff-captain Yevgeney Ivanovitch Apuhtin!”", "\"도의 귀족 단장 후보로 참모 대위 예브게니 이바노비치 아푸흐친을 추천합니다!\""], ["A dead silence followed, and then a weak old voice was heard: “Declined!”", "죽은 듯한 침묵이 이어졌고, 그러더니 힘없는 노인의 목소리가 들렸다. \"사양합니다!\""]], [["“We call upon the privy councilor Pyotr Petrovitch Bol,”", "\"추밀 고문관 표트르 페트로비치 볼을 추천합니다.\""], ["the voice began again.", "목소리가 다시 시작되었다."]], [["“Declined!”", "\"사양합니다!\""], ["a high boyish voice replied.", "높은 소년 같은 목소리가 대답했다."]], [["Again it began, and again “Declined.”", "다시 시작되었고, 또다시 \"사양합니다.\""], ["And so it went on for about an hour.", "그렇게 한 시간쯤 이어졌다."], ["Levin, with his elbows on the balustrade, looked and listened.", "레빈은 난간에 팔꿈치를 괸 채 보고 들었다."], ["At first he wondered and wanted to know what it meant; then feeling sure that he could not make it out he began to be bored.", "처음에는 의아해하며 그게 무슨 뜻인지 알고 싶었다. 그러다 도저히 알아낼 수 없다는 확신이 들자 지루해지기 시작했다."], ["Then recalling all the excitement and vindictiveness he had seen on all the faces, he felt sad; he made up his mind to go, and went downstairs.", "그러고는 모든 얼굴에서 보았던 그 온갖 흥분과 적개심을 떠올리며 서글퍼졌다. 그는 가기로 마음먹고 아래층으로 내려갔다."], ["As he passed through the entry to the galleries he met a dejected high school boy walking up and down with tired-looking eyes.", "회랑 입구를 지나면서 그는 지쳐 보이는 눈으로 서성거리는 풀이 죽은 김나지움 학생 하나와 마주쳤다."], ["On the stairs he met a couple—a lady running quickly on her high heels and the jaunty deputy prosecutor.", "계단에서는 한 쌍과 마주쳤는데—높은 굽으로 빠르게 달려 내려가는 부인과 경쾌한 검사보였다."]], [["“I told you you weren’t late,”", "\"늦지 않았다고 했잖습니까.\""], ["the deputy prosecutor was saying at the moment when Levin moved aside to let the lady pass.", "레빈이 부인을 지나가게 하려고 옆으로 비켜섰을 때, 검사보가 마침 이렇게 말하고 있었다."]], [["Levin was on the stairs to the way out, and was just feeling in his waistcoat pocket for the number of his overcoat, when the secretary overtook him.", "레빈은 출구로 가는 계단에 있었고, 외투 번호표를 찾으려고 막 조끼 주머니를 더듬던 참인데, 서기가 그를 따라잡았다."]], [["“This way, please, Konstantin Dmitrievitch; they are voting.”", "\"이쪽으로 오시지요, 콘스탄틴 드미트리치. 투표가 진행 중입니다.\""]], [["The candidate who was being voted on was Nevyedovsky, who had so stoutly denied all idea of standing.", "투표에 부쳐진 후보는, 출마 의사를 그렇게도 한사코 부인하던 그 네뱌도프스키였다."], ["Levin went up to the door of the room; it was locked.", "레빈은 방문으로 다가갔다. 문은 잠겨 있었다."], ["The secretary knocked, the door opened, and Levin was met by two red-faced gentlemen, who darted out.", "서기가 노크하자 문이 열렸고, 레빈은 뛰쳐나오는 얼굴이 벌건 두 신사와 마주쳤다."]], [["“I can’t stand any more of it,”", "\"더는 이걸 견딜 수가 없네.\""], ["said one red-faced gentleman.", "얼굴이 벌건 한 신사가 말했다."]], [["After them the face of the marshal of the province was poked out.", "그들 뒤로 도의 귀족 단장의 얼굴이 쑥 내밀어졌다."], ["His face was dreadful-looking from exhaustion and dismay.", "그의 얼굴은 탈진과 낙담으로 끔찍해 보였다."]], [["“I told you not to let anyone out!”", "\"아무도 내보내지 말라고 했잖나!\""], ["he cried to the doorkeeper.", "그가 문지기에게 소리쳤다."]], [["“I let someone in, your excellency!”", "\"한 사람을 들여보낸 겁니다, 각하!\""]], [["“Mercy on us!”", "\"맙소사!\""], ["and with a heavy sigh the marshal of the province walked with downcast head to the high table in the middle of the room, his legs staggering in his white trousers.", "도의 귀족 단장은 무거운 한숨을 내쉬며 고개를 떨군 채, 흰 바지를 입은 두 다리를 비틀거리며 방 한가운데의 높은 탁자로 걸어갔다."]], [["Nevyedovsky had scored a higher majority, as they had planned, and he was the new marshal of the province.", "네뱌도프스키는 그들이 계획한 대로 더 많은 다수표를 얻었고, 새로운 도의 귀족 단장이 되었다."], ["Many people were amused, many were pleased and happy, many were in ecstasies, many were disgusted and unhappy.", "많은 사람이 즐거워했고, 많은 사람이 기뻐하고 행복해했으며, 많은 사람이 황홀경에 빠졌고, 많은 사람이 역겨워하며 불행해했다."], ["The former marshal of the province was in a state of despair, which he could not conceal.", "전임 도의 귀족 단장은 감추지 못할 절망에 빠져 있었다."], ["When Nevyedovsky went out of the room, the crowd thronged round him and followed him enthusiastically, just as they had followed the governor who had opened the meetings, and just as they had followed Snetkov when he was elected.", "네뱌도프스키가 방에서 나가자, 군중이 그를 둘러싸고 열광적으로 뒤따랐는데, 그것은 회의를 개회한 지사를 뒤따랐을 때나, 스네트코프가 선출되었을 때 그를 뒤따랐을 때와 꼭 같았다."]], [["# Chapter 31", "# Chapter 31"]], [["The newly elected marshal and many of the successful party dined that day with Vronsky.", "새로 선출된 귀족 회장과 승리한 당파의 많은 사람들이 그날 브론스키와 함께 만찬을 했다."]], [["Vronsky had come to the elections partly because he was bored in the country and wanted to show Anna his right to independence, and also to repay Sviazhsky by his support at the election for all the trouble he had taken for Vronsky at the district council election, but chiefly in order strictly to perform all those duties of a nobleman and landowner which he had taken upon himself.", "브론스키가 선거에 온 것은 한편으로는 시골에서 따분했던 데다 안나에게 자신의 독립적인 권리를 보여 주고 싶었기 때문이고, 또한 군 의회 선거에서 자기를 위해 애써 준 스비야시스키에게 이번 선거에서 지지로 보답하기 위해서이기도 했지만, 무엇보다도 자기가 떠맡은 귀족이자 지주로서의 온갖 의무를 빈틈없이 수행하기 위해서였다."], ["But he had not in the least expected that the election would so interest him, so keenly excite him, and that he would be so good at this kind of thing.", "그러나 그는 선거가 그토록 자기 흥미를 끌고, 그토록 격렬하게 자기를 들뜨게 하며, 자기가 이런 일에 그토록 능숙하리라고는 조금도 예상하지 못했다."], ["He was quite a new man in the circle of the nobility of the province, but his success was unmistakable, and he was not wrong in supposing that he had already obtained a certain influence.", "그는 도(道) 귀족 사회에서는 완전히 새로운 인물이었지만 그의 성공은 의심할 여지가 없었고, 자기가 이미 어느 정도 영향력을 얻었다는 그의 짐작은 틀리지 않았다."], ["This influence was due to his wealth and reputation, the capital house in the town lent him by his old friend Shirkov, who had a post in the department of finances and was director of a flourishing bank in Kashin; the excellent cook Vronsky had brought from the country, and his friendship with the governor, who was a schoolfellow of Vronsky’s—a schoolfellow he had patronized and protected indeed.", "이 영향력은 그의 재산과 명성에서, 재무국에 자리를 두고 카신의 번창하는 은행 이사를 맡고 있는 옛 친구 시르코프가 빌려준 시내의 멋진 저택에서, 브론스키가 시골에서 데려온 훌륭한 요리사에서, 그리고 페이지 군단 시절 학우였던—실은 그가 후원하고 보호해 주던 학우였던—지사와의 우정에서 비롯된 것이었다."], ["But what contributed more than all to his success was his direct, equable manner with everyone, which very quickly made the majority of the noblemen reverse the current opinion of his supposed haughtiness.", "그러나 무엇보다도 그의 성공에 가장 크게 기여한 것은 누구를 대하든 솔직하고 한결같은 그의 태도였으니, 그 덕분에 대다수 귀족들은 그가 거만하리라던 기존의 평판을 아주 빠르게 뒤집었다."], ["He was himself conscious that, except that whimsical gentleman married to Kitty Shtcherbatskaya, who had à propos de bottes poured out a stream of irrelevant absurdities with such spiteful fury, every nobleman with whom he had made acquaintance had become his adherent.", "그 자신도 의식하고 있었는데, à propos de bottes 그토록 악의에 찬 격분으로 엉뚱한 헛소리를 늘어놓던, 키티 시체르바츠카야와 결혼한 그 변덕스러운 신사를 빼고는, 그가 알게 된 귀족 한 사람 한 사람이 모두 그의 지지자가 되었던 것이다."], ["He saw clearly, and other people recognized it, too, that he had done a great deal to secure the success of Nevyedovsky.", "그는 자기가 네비예돕스키의 성공을 확보하기 위해 큰일을 해냈다는 것을 똑똑히 알았고, 다른 사람들도 그것을 인정했다."], ["And now at his own table, celebrating Nevyedovsky’s election, he was experiencing an agreeable sense of triumph over the success of his candidate.", "그리고 이제 자기 식탁에서 네비예돕스키의 당선을 축하하며 그는 자기 후보의 성공을 거둔 흐뭇한 승리감을 맛보고 있었다."], ["The election itself had so fascinated him that, if he could succeed in getting married during the next three years, he began to think of standing himself—much as after winning a race ridden by a jockey, he had longed to ride a race himself.", "선거 자체가 그를 어찌나 매료했던지, 만약 앞으로 삼 년 안에 결혼하는 데 성공한다면 자기도 출마해 볼까 생각하기 시작했는데, 그것은 마치 기수가 모는 말을 응원해 경주에서 이긴 뒤에 자기가 직접 말을 타고 경주에 나가고 싶어졌던 것과 마찬가지였다. * à propos de bottes: 프랑스어로 '뜬금없이, 까닭 없이'라는 뜻."]], [["Today he was celebrating the success of his jockey.", "오늘 그는 자기 기수의 성공을 축하하고 있었다."], ["Vronsky sat at the head of the table, on his right hand sat the young governor, a general of high rank.", "브론스키는 식탁 상석에 앉았고, 그의 오른편에는 고위 장군인 젊은 지사가 앉았다."], ["To all the rest he was the chief man in the province, who had solemnly opened the elections with his speech, and aroused a feeling of respect and even of awe in many people, as Vronsky saw; to Vronsky he was little Katka Maslov—that had been his nickname in the Pages’ Corps—whom he felt to be shy and tried to mettre à son aise.", "다른 모든 사람에게 그는 연설로 선거를 엄숙하게 개회한 도의 최고 인물이었으며, 브론스키가 보기에 많은 사람들에게 존경심과 심지어 외경심까지 불러일으키는 사람이었다. 하지만 브론스키에게 그는 어린 카치카 마슬로프였으니—그것이 페이지 군단 시절 그의 별명이었다—그가 수줍어한다고 느낀 브론스키는 그를 mettre à son aise 하려고 애썼다."], ["On the left hand sat Nevyedovsky with his youthful, stubborn, and malignant face.", "왼편에는 젊고 고집스럽고 표독스러운 얼굴을 한 네비예돕스키가 앉았다. 그를 대하는 브론스키의 태도는 소탈하고 정중했다."], ["With him Vronsky was simple and deferential.", "* mettre à son aise: 프랑스어로 '편안하게 해 주다'라는 뜻."]], [["Sviazhsky took his failure very light-heartedly.", "스비야시스키는 자기의 낙선을 아주 가볍게 받아넘겼다."], ["It was indeed no failure in his eyes, as he said himself, turning, glass in hand, to Nevyedovsky; they could not have found a better representative of the new movement, which the nobility ought to follow.", "사실 그것은 그의 눈에는 낙선이 아니었으니, 그가 잔을 손에 들고 네비예돕스키를 향해 몸을 돌리며 직접 말했듯이, 귀족이 마땅히 따라야 할 새로운 흐름의 대표로 이보다 더 나은 사람을 찾을 수는 없었던 것이다."], ["And so every honest person, as he said, was on the side of today’s success and was rejoicing over it.", "그러므로 그가 말했듯이, 정직한 사람이라면 누구나 오늘의 성공 편에 서서 그것을 기뻐하고 있었다."]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch was glad, too, that he was having a good time, and that everyone was pleased.", "스테판 아르카지치 역시 자기가 즐거운 시간을 보내고 모두가 만족한다는 것이 기뻤다."], ["The episode of the elections served as a good occasion for a capital dinner.", "이 선거 사건은 멋진 만찬을 위한 좋은 구실이 되었다."], ["Sviazhsky comically imitated the tearful discourse of the marshal, and observed, addressing Nevyedovsky, that his excellency would have to select another more complicated method of auditing the accounts than tears.", "스비야시스키는 회장의 눈물 어린 연설을 익살스럽게 흉내 내면서 네비예돕스키를 향해, 각하께서는 회계를 감사하는 데 눈물 말고 더 복잡한 방법을 골라야 할 것이라고 말했다."], ["Another nobleman jocosely described how footmen in stockings had been ordered for the marshal’s ball, and how now they would have to be sent back unless the new marshal would give a ball with footmen in stockings.", "또 다른 귀족은, 회장의 무도회를 위해 양말 신은 하인들을 주문해 두었는데 새 회장이 양말 신은 하인들을 두고 무도회를 열지 않는 한 이제 그들을 도로 돌려보내야 할 것이라고 우스꽝스럽게 늘어놓았다."]], [["Continually during dinner they said of Nevyedovsky: “our marshal,” and “your excellency.”", "만찬 내내 그들은 네비예돕스키를 두고 \"우리 회장\"이라느니 \"각하\"라느니 했다."]], [["This was said with the same pleasure with which a bride is called “Madame” and her husband’s name.", "이것은 신부를 \"마담\"이라고, 남편의 성을 붙여 부를 때와 똑같은 즐거움으로 하는 말이었다."], ["Nevyedovsky affected to be not merely indifferent but scornful of this appellation, but it was obvious that he was highly delighted, and had to keep a curb on himself not to betray the triumph which was unsuitable to their new liberal tone.", "네비예돕스키는 이 호칭에 무관심한 척하는 정도가 아니라 경멸하는 척까지 했지만, 그가 더없이 기뻐하고 있으며 그들의 새로운 자유주의적 어조에 어울리지 않는 그 승리감을 드러내지 않으려고 자제하지 않으면 안 된다는 것은 누가 봐도 뻔했다."]], [["After dinner several telegrams were sent to people interested in the result of the election.", "만찬이 끝난 뒤 선거 결과에 관심을 가진 사람들에게 몇 통의 전보가 발송되었다."], ["And Stepan Arkadyevitch, who was in high good humor, sent Darya Alexandrovna a telegram: “Nevyedovsky elected by twenty votes.", "그리고 한껏 들뜬 기분이던 스테판 아르카지치는 다리아 알렉산드로브나에게 전보를 쳤다. \"네비예돕스키 스무 표 차로 당선."], ["Congratulations.", "축하 바람."], ["Tell people.”", "사람들에게 알릴 것.\""], ["He dictated it aloud, saying: “We must let them share our rejoicing.”", "그는 \"우리의 기쁨을 그들과 나눠야지.\" 하면서 소리 내어 받아쓰게 했다."], ["Darya Alexandrovna, getting the message, simply sighed over the rouble wasted on it, and understood that it was an after-dinner affair.", "전보를 받은 다리아 알렉산드로브나는 그저 거기에 낭비된 1루블을 두고 한숨지을 뿐, 그것이 만찬 뒤의 일이라는 것을 알아챘다."], ["She knew Stiva had a weakness after dining for faire jouer le télégraphe.", "그녀는 스티바가 식사 후에 faire jouer le télégraphe 하는 버릇이 있다는 것을 알고 있었다."]], [["Everything, together with the excellent dinner and the wine, not from Russian merchants, but imported direct from abroad, was extremely dignified, simple, and enjoyable.", "* faire jouer le télégraphe: 프랑스어로 '전보를 놀려 대다, 전보를 쳐 대다'라는 뜻. 훌륭한 만찬과, 러시아 상인에게서가 아니라 외국에서 직접 들여온 포도주를 비롯한 모든 것이 더없이 품위 있고 소탈하며 유쾌했다."], ["The party—some twenty—had been selected by Sviazhsky from among the more active new liberals, all of the same way of thinking, who were at the same time clever and well bred.", "스무 명쯤 되는 일행은 스비야시스키가 좀 더 적극적인 신진 자유주의자들 가운데서 골라낸 사람들로, 다들 생각이 같으면서도 동시에 똑똑하고 교양 있는 자들이었다."], ["They drank, also half in jest, to the health of the new marshal of the province, of the governor, of the bank director, and of “our amiable host.”", "그들은 또 반쯤 농담조로 새 도 귀족 회장과 지사와 은행 이사와 \"우리의 상냥한 주인\"의 건강을 위해 잔을 들었다."]], [["Vronsky was satisfied.", "브론스키는 만족스러웠다."], ["He had never expected to find so pleasant a tone in the provinces.", "그는 지방에서 이렇게 유쾌한 분위기를 만나게 되리라고는 결코 예상하지 못했다."]], [["Towards the end of dinner it was still more lively.", "만찬이 끝나갈 무렵 분위기는 더욱 활기를 띠었다."], ["The governor asked Vronsky to come to a concert for the benefit of the Servians which his wife, who was anxious to make his acquaintance, had been getting up.", "지사는 브론스키에게, 그와 알고 지내고 싶어 안달하는 자기 아내가 준비해 온 세르비아인을 위한 자선 음악회에 와 달라고 청했다."]], [["“There’ll be a ball, and you’ll see the belle of the province.", "\"무도회도 있을 거고, 우리 도의 미인도 보실 수 있을 거요."], ["Worth seeing, really.”", "정말이지 볼 만하지요.\""]], [["“Not in my line,”", "\"제 취향은 아닙니다.\""], ["Vronsky answered.", "브론스키가 대답했다."], ["He liked that English phrase.", "그는 그 영어 표현이 마음에 들었다."], ["But he smiled, and promised to come.", "하지만 그는 미소 지으며 가겠다고 약속했다."]], [["Before they rose from the table, when all of them were smoking, Vronsky’s valet went up to him with a letter on a tray.", "그들이 식탁에서 일어나기 전, 모두가 담배를 피우고 있을 때, 브론스키의 시종이 쟁반에 편지 한 통을 받쳐 들고 그에게 다가왔다."]], [["“From Vozdvizhenskoe by special messenger,”", "\"보즈드비젠스코예에서 특별 심부름꾼 편으로 왔습니다.\""], ["he said with a significant expression.", "그는 의미심장한 표정으로 말했다."]], [["“Astonishing!", "\"놀랍군!"], ["how like he is to the deputy prosecutor Sventitsky,”", "차장검사 스벤티츠키와 어찌나 닮았는지.\""], ["said one of the guests in French of the valet, while Vronsky, frowning, read the letter.", "손님 가운데 한 사람이 시종을 두고 프랑스어로 말했고, 그러는 동안 브론스키는 얼굴을 찌푸린 채 편지를 읽었다."]], [["The letter was from Anna.", "편지는 안나에게서 온 것이었다."], ["Before he read the letter, he knew its contents.", "그는 편지를 읽기 전부터 그 내용을 알고 있었다."], ["Expecting the elections to be over in five days, he had promised to be back on Friday.", "선거가 닷새 안에 끝나리라 예상했던 그는 금요일에 돌아가겠다고 약속했었다."], ["Today was Saturday, and he knew that the letter contained reproaches for not being back at the time fixed.", "오늘이 토요일이니, 그는 그 편지에 정해진 날에 돌아오지 않은 것을 책망하는 말이 담겨 있으리라는 것을 알았다."], ["The letter he had sent the previous evening had probably not reached her yet.", "그가 전날 저녁에 보낸 편지는 아마 아직 그녀에게 닿지 않았을 터였다."]], [["The letter was what he had expected, but the form of it was unexpected, and particularly disagreeable to him.", "편지는 그가 예상하던 그대로였지만, 그 형식은 뜻밖이었고 특히 그의 마음에 들지 않았다."], ["“Annie is very ill, the doctor says it may be inflammation.", "\"아니가 몹시 아파요. 의사는 폐렴일지도 모른다고 해요."], ["I am losing my head all alone.", "나 혼자서는 정신을 못 차리겠어요."], ["Princess Varvara is no help, but a hindrance.", "바르바라 공작 영애는 도움이 되기는커녕 방해만 돼요."], ["I expected you the day before yesterday, and yesterday, and now I am sending to find out where you are and what you are doing.", "그저께도, 어제도 당신을 기다렸는데, 이제 당신이 어디서 무엇을 하고 있는지 알아보려고 사람을 보냅니다."], ["I wanted to come myself, but thought better of it, knowing you would dislike it.", "직접 가고 싶었지만, 당신이 싫어할 것을 알기에 그만두었어요."], ["Send some answer, that I may know what to do.”", "어떻게 해야 할지 알 수 있도록 답이라도 좀 보내 줘요.\""]], [["The child ill, yet she had thought of coming herself.", "아이가 아픈데도 그녀는 직접 올 생각을 했었다."], ["Their daughter ill, and this hostile tone.", "딸이 아픈데, 이 적대적인 어조라니."]], [["The innocent festivities over the election, and this gloomy, burdensome love to which he had to return struck Vronsky by their contrast.", "선거를 둘러싼 천진한 즐거움과, 그가 돌아가야만 하는 이 음울하고 짐스러운 사랑이 그 대조로 브론스키를 후려쳤다."], ["But he had to go, and by the first train that night he set off home.", "그러나 그는 가야만 했고, 그날 밤 첫 기차로 집을 향해 떠났다."]], [["# Chapter 32", "# Chapter 32"]], [["Before Vronsky’s departure for the elections, Anna had reflected that the scenes constantly repeated between them each time he left home, might only make him cold to her instead of attaching him to her, and resolved to do all she could to control herself so as to bear the parting with composure.", "브론스키가 선거에 떠나기 전, 안나는 그가 집을 떠날 때마다 두 사람 사이에서 끊임없이 되풀이되는 장면이 그를 자기에게 묶어 두기는커녕 오히려 자기에게 냉담하게 만들 뿐이라고 생각했다. 그래서 마음을 다잡아 이별을 의연하게 견디기 위해 할 수 있는 모든 것을 다 하겠다고 결심했다."], ["But the cold, severe glance with which he had looked at her when he came to tell her he was going had wounded her, and before he had started her peace of mind was destroyed.", "그러나 자기가 떠난다는 말을 하러 왔을 때 그가 그녀를 바라본 그 차갑고 매서운 시선이 그녀에게 상처를 입혔고, 그가 출발하기도 전에 그녀의 마음의 평정은 무너지고 말았다."]], [["In solitude afterwards, thinking over that glance which had expressed his right to freedom, she came, as she always did, to the same point—the sense of her own humiliation.", "그가 떠난 뒤 홀로 남아, 자유에 대한 그의 권리를 드러낸 그 시선을 곱씹는 동안, 그녀는 언제나처럼 똑같은 지점에 다다랐다. 바로 자기 자신이 모욕당했다는 느낌이었다."], ["“He has the right to go away when and where he chooses.", "\"그는 자기가 원할 때 원하는 곳으로 떠날 권리가 있어."], ["Not simply to go away, but to leave me.", "그냥 떠나는 게 아니라 나를 버려둘 권리까지."], ["He has every right, and I have none.", "그에게는 모든 권리가 있고, 나에게는 아무 권리도 없지."], ["But knowing that, he ought not to do it.", "하지만 그걸 알면서도 그렇게 해서는 안 되는 거야."], ["What has he done, though?...", "그런데 그가 뭘 했단 말이야?……"], ["He looked at me with a cold, severe expression.", "그는 차갑고 매서운 표정으로 나를 바라봤어."], ["Of course that is something indefinable, impalpable, but it has never been so before, and that glance means a great deal,”", "물론 그건 뭐라고 규정할 수도, 손에 잡을 수도 없는 거지. 하지만 전에는 한 번도 그런 적이 없었고, 그 시선은 많은 걸 뜻해.\""], ["she thought.", "그녀는 생각했다."], ["“That glance shows the beginning of indifference.”", "\"그 시선은 무관심의 시작을 보여 주는 거야.\""]], [["And though she felt sure that a coldness was beginning, there was nothing she could do, she could not in any way alter her relations to him.", "그리고 비록 냉담함이 시작되고 있음을 확신하면서도, 그녀가 할 수 있는 일은 아무것도 없었고, 그와의 관계를 어떤 식으로도 바꿀 수가 없었다."], ["Just as before, only by love and by charm could she keep him.", "예전과 마찬가지로, 오직 사랑과 매력으로만 그를 붙잡을 수 있을 뿐이었다."], ["And so, just as before, only by occupation in the day, by morphine at night, could she stifle the fearful thought of what would be if he ceased to love her.", "그래서 예전과 마찬가지로, 낮에는 분주한 일거리로, 밤에는 모르핀으로, 그가 자기를 더 이상 사랑하지 않게 된다면 어떻게 될까 하는 무서운 생각을 억누를 수밖에 없었다. 사실 한 가지 수단이 더 남아 있긴 했다."], ["It is true there was still one means; not to keep him—for that she wanted nothing more than his love—but to be nearer to him, to be in such a position that he would not leave her.", "그를 붙잡아 두기 위한 것은 아니었다. 그것을 위해서라면 그녀는 그의 사랑 외에는 아무것도 바라지 않았으니까."], ["That means was divorce and marriage.", "그것은 그에게 더 가까이 다가가, 그가 그녀를 떠날 수 없는 그런 처지에 놓이는 것이었다."], ["And she began to long for that, and made up her mind to agree to it the first time he or Stiva approached her on the subject.", "그 수단이란 이혼과 결혼이었다. 그래서 그녀는 그것을 갈망하기 시작했고, 그나 스티바가 그 문제를 처음 꺼내는 순간 거기에 동의하기로 마음을 굳혔다."]], [["Absorbed in such thoughts, she passed five days without him, the five days that he was to be at the elections.", "그런 생각에 몰두한 채, 그녀는 그가 선거에 가 있어야 할 닷새 동안 그 없이 닷새를 보냈다."]], [["Walks, conversation with Princess Varvara, visits to the hospital, and, most of all, reading—reading of one book after another—filled up her time.", "산책, 바르바라 공작 영애와의 대화, 병원 방문, 그리고 무엇보다도 독서, 그러니까 책을 한 권씩 차례차례 읽어 나가는 것이 그녀의 시간을 채웠다."], ["But on the sixth day, when the coachman came back without him, she felt that now she was utterly incapable of stifling the thought of him and of what he was doing there, just at that time her little girl was taken ill.", "그러나 엿새째 되던 날, 마부가 그 없이 돌아오자, 그녀는 이제 그에 대한 생각과 그가 그곳에서 무엇을 하고 있을까 하는 생각을 도무지 억누를 수 없음을 느꼈다. 바로 그때 어린 딸이 병이 났다."], ["Anna began to look after her, but even that did not distract her mind, especially as the illness was not serious.", "안나는 아이를 돌보기 시작했지만, 그것조차 그녀의 마음을 다른 데로 돌려놓지 못했다. 더욱이 병이 심각하지도 않았다."], ["However hard she tried, she could not love this little child, and to feign love was beyond her powers.", "아무리 애를 써도 그녀는 이 어린아이를 사랑할 수가 없었고, 사랑하는 척하는 것은 그녀의 힘으로는 할 수 없는 일이었다."], ["Towards the evening of that day, still alone, Anna was in such a panic about him that she decided to start for the town, but on second thoughts wrote him the contradictory letter that Vronsky received, and without reading it through, sent it off by a special messenger.", "그날 저녁 무렵, 여전히 혼자였던 안나는 그에 대한 걱정으로 어찌나 안절부절못했던지 도시로 떠나기로 결심했다. 그러나 다시 생각을 고쳐먹고는, 브론스키가 받게 된 그 모순된 편지를 써서 끝까지 읽어 보지도 않은 채 특별한 심부름꾼을 시켜 부쳐 버렸다."], ["The next morning she received his letter and regretted her own.", "이튿날 아침 그녀는 그의 편지를 받았고 자기가 보낸 편지를 후회했다."], ["She dreaded a repetition of the severe look he had flung at her at parting, especially when he knew that the baby was not dangerously ill.", "그녀는 헤어질 때 그가 그녀에게 던진 그 매서운 눈길이 되풀이될까 봐, 특히 아이가 위중한 병이 아니라는 걸 그가 알았을 때 그럴까 봐 두려웠다."], ["But still she was glad she had written to him.", "그래도 그녀는 그에게 편지를 쓴 것이 기뻤다."], ["At this moment Anna was positively admitting to herself that she was a burden to him, that he would relinquish his freedom regretfully to return to her, and in spite of that she was glad he was coming.", "이 순간 안나는 자기가 그에게 짐스러운 존재라는 것을, 그가 그녀에게 돌아오기 위해 마지못해 자기 자유를 내려놓으리라는 것을 분명히 인정하면서도, 그럼에도 그가 오고 있다는 사실이 기뻤다."], ["Let him weary of her, but he would be here with her, so that she would see him, would know of every action he took.", "그가 그녀에게 싫증을 내든 말든, 그는 여기 그녀 곁에 있을 것이고, 그러면 그녀는 그를 볼 수 있고, 그가 하는 행동 하나하나를 알 수 있을 테니까."]], [["She was sitting in the drawing-room near a lamp, with a new volume of Taine, and as she read, listening to the sound of the wind outside, and every minute expecting the carriage to arrive.", "그녀는 응접실 램프 옆에 앉아 텐의 새 책을 펼쳐 들고 있었다. 책을 읽으면서도 바깥에서 부는 바람 소리에 귀를 기울이며, 매 순간 마차가 도착하기를 기다렸다."], ["Several times she had fancied she heard the sound of wheels, but she had been mistaken.", "몇 번이나 바퀴 소리가 들리는 것 같았지만, 그때마다 착각이었다."], ["At last she heard not the sound of wheels, but the coachman’s shout and the dull rumble in the covered entry.", "마침내 그녀는 바퀴 소리가 아니라 마부의 고함 소리와 지붕 덮인 현관 진입로에서 울리는 둔중한 소리를 들었다."], ["Even Princess Varvara, playing patience, confirmed this, and Anna, flushing hotly, got up; but instead of going down, as she had done twice before, she stood still.", "페이션스 카드놀이를 하고 있던 바르바라 공작 영애조차 그것을 확인해 주었고, 안나는 얼굴이 화끈 달아오른 채 일어섰다. 그러나 앞서 두 번 그랬던 것처럼 아래로 내려가는 대신, 그녀는 그 자리에 멈춰 섰다."], ["She suddenly felt ashamed of her duplicity, but even more she dreaded how he might meet her.", "그녀는 갑자기 자기가 속임수를 쓴 것이 부끄러워졌지만, 그보다 더, 그가 자기를 어떻게 맞이할까가 두려웠다."], ["All feeling of wounded pride had passed now; she was only afraid of the expression of his displeasure.", "상처받은 자존심의 감정은 이제 모두 사라져 버렸다. 다만 그의 불쾌한 표정이 두려울 뿐이었다."], ["She remembered that her child had been perfectly well again for the last two days.", "그녀는 아이가 지난 이틀 동안 완전히 다시 건강해졌다는 것을 떠올렸다."], ["She felt positively vexed with her for getting better from the very moment her letter was sent off.", "자기가 편지를 부친 바로 그 순간부터 아이가 좋아진 것에 대해 그녀는 정말로 짜증이 났다. 그러다 그가 떠올랐다."], ["Then she thought of him, that he was here, all of him, with his hands, his eyes.", "그가 여기 있다는 것, 그의 손도, 그의 눈도, 그의 모든 것이 여기 있다는 것이."], ["She heard his voice.", "그녀는 그의 목소리를 들었다."], ["And forgetting everything, she ran joyfully to meet him.", "그리고 모든 것을 잊은 채, 그녀는 기쁨에 차서 그를 맞으러 달려갔다."]], [["“Well, how is Annie?”", "\"그래, 아니는 좀 어때?\""], ["he said timidly from below, looking up to Anna as she ran down to him.", "그가 아래에서 머뭇거리며, 자기에게 달려 내려오는 안나를 올려다보며 말했다."]], [["He was sitting on a chair, and a footman was pulling off his warm over-boot.", "그는 의자에 앉아 있었고, 하인 하나가 그의 따뜻한 덧신을 벗기고 있었다."]], [["“Oh, she is better.”", "\"아, 그 애는 좋아졌어요.\""]], [["“And you?”", "\"당신은?\""], ["he said, shaking himself.", "그가 몸을 한 번 부르르 떨며 말했다."]], [["She took his hand in both of hers, and drew it to her waist, never taking her eyes off him.", "그녀는 두 손으로 그의 손을 잡아 자기 허리께로 끌어당기며, 그에게서 한순간도 눈을 떼지 않았다."]], [["“Well, I’m glad,”", "\"음, 다행이군.\""], ["he said, coldly scanning her, her hair, her dress, which he knew she had put on for him.", "그가 그녀를, 그녀의 머리를, 그를 위해 차려입은 줄 그가 아는 그녀의 옷차림을 차갑게 훑어보며 말했다."], ["All was charming, but how many times it had charmed him!", "모든 것이 매혹적이었지만, 그를 매혹시킨 게 어디 한두 번이었던가!"], ["And the stern, stony expression that she so dreaded settled upon his face.", "그리고 그녀가 그토록 두려워하던 그 엄하고 돌처럼 굳은 표정이 그의 얼굴에 자리 잡았다."]], [["“Well, I’m glad.", "\"음, 다행이야."], ["And are you well?”", "그래, 당신은 잘 지냈고?\""], ["he said, wiping his damp beard with his handkerchief and kissing her hand.", "그가 손수건으로 축축한 턱수염을 닦고 그녀의 손에 입을 맞추며 말했다."]], [["“Never mind,”", "\"상관없어.\" 그녀는 생각했다."], ["she thought, “only let him be here, and so long as he’s here he cannot, he dare not, cease to love me.”", "\"그저 그가 여기 있게만 해 줘. 그가 여기 있는 한 그는 나를 사랑하지 않을 수가 없어, 감히 그러지 못해.\""]], [["The evening was spent happily and gaily in the presence of Princess Varvara, who complained to him that Anna had been taking morphine in his absence.", "저녁은 바르바라 공작 영애가 함께한 가운데 행복하고 즐겁게 흘러갔다. 공작 영애는 그가 없는 동안 안나가 모르핀을 복용해 왔다고 그에게 일러바쳤다."]], [["“What am I to do?", "\"어쩌겠어요?"], ["I couldn’t sleep....", "잠을 잘 수가 없었는걸요……"], ["My thoughts prevented me.", "온갖 생각이 잠을 방해했어요."], ["When he’s here I never take it—hardly ever.”", "그가 여기 있을 때는 절대 먹지 않아요, 거의 한 번도요.\""]], [["He told her about the election, and Anna knew how by adroit questions to bring him to what gave him most pleasure—his own success.", "그는 그녀에게 선거 이야기를 들려주었고, 안나는 능숙한 질문으로 그를 그가 가장 기뻐하는 화제, 곧 그 자신의 성공으로 이끌어 가는 법을 알았다."], ["She told him of everything that interested him at home; and all that she told him was of the most cheerful description.", "그녀는 집에서 그가 관심을 가질 만한 모든 것을 그에게 이야기해 주었다. 그리고 그녀가 들려준 이야기는 하나같이 더없이 명랑한 내용이었다."]], [["But late in the evening, when they were alone, Anna, seeing that she had regained complete possession of him, wanted to erase the painful impression of the glance he had given her for her letter.", "그러나 저녁이 깊어 두 사람만 남게 되었을 때, 안나는 자기가 그를 완전히 다시 손에 넣었음을 깨닫고는, 편지 때문에 그가 자기에게 던진 그 시선의 괴로운 인상을 지워 버리고 싶었다."], ["She said:", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“Tell me frankly, you were vexed at getting my letter, and you didn’t believe me?”", "\"솔직히 말해 줘요. 당신은 내 편지를 받고 짜증이 났고, 나를 믿지 않았죠?\""]], [["As soon as she had said it, she felt that however warm his feelings were to her, he had not forgiven her for that.", "그 말을 내뱉자마자, 그녀는 그의 감정이 아무리 따뜻하다 해도 그가 그 일에 대해서만큼은 자기를 용서하지 않았음을 느꼈다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그래.\""], ["he said, “the letter was so strange.", "그가 말했다. \"편지가 너무 이상했거든."], ["First, Annie ill, and then you thought of coming yourself.”", "처음엔 아니가 아프다더니, 그다음엔 당신이 직접 오려고 했다니.\""]], [["“It was all the truth.”", "\"전부 사실이었어요.\""]], [["“Oh, I don’t doubt it.”", "\"아, 그걸 의심하는 건 아니야.\""]], [["“Yes, you do doubt it.", "\"아니, 의심하잖아요."], ["You are vexed, I see.”", "짜증이 났군요, 보여요.\""]], [["“Not for one moment.", "\"단 한순간도 그렇지 않아."], ["I’m only vexed, that’s true, that you seem somehow unwilling to admit that there are duties....”", "다만 짜증이 나는 건, 그건 사실이야, 당신이 어쩐지 의무라는 게 있다는 걸 인정하려 들지 않는 것 같아서……\""]], [["“The duty of going to a concert....”", "\"음악회에 갈 의무 말이죠……\""]], [["“But we won’t talk about it,”", "\"하지만 그 얘긴 하지 말자고.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“Why not talk about it?”", "\"왜 얘기하면 안 되죠?\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“I only meant to say that matters of real importance may turn up.", "\"난 그저 정말로 중요한 일이 생길 수도 있다는 걸 말하려던 거야."], ["Now, for instance, I shall have to go to Moscow to arrange about the house....", "가령 지금만 해도, 난 집 문제를 처리하러 모스크바에 가야 해……"], ["Oh, Anna, why are you so irritable?", "아, 안나, 왜 이렇게 신경이 곤두서 있어?"], ["Don’t you know that I can’t live without you?”", "내가 당신 없이는 살 수 없다는 걸 몰라?\""]], [["“If so,”", "\"그렇다면,\""], ["said Anna, her voice suddenly changing, “it means that you are sick of this life....", "안나가 별안간 목소리를 바꾸며 말했다. \"그건 당신이 이 생활에 진저리가 났다는 뜻이에요……"], ["Yes, you will come for a day and go away, as men do....”", "그래요, 당신은 하루쯤 왔다가 떠나 버리겠죠, 남자들이 흔히 그러듯이……\""]], [["“Anna, that’s cruel.", "\"안나, 그건 잔인한 말이야."], ["I am ready to give up my whole life.”", "난 내 인생 전부를 내던질 각오가 되어 있어.\""]], [["But she did not hear him.", "그러나 그녀는 그의 말을 듣고 있지 않았다."]], [["“If you go to Moscow, I will go too.", "\"당신이 모스크바에 간다면, 나도 갈 거예요."], ["I will not stay here.", "난 여기 남아 있지 않겠어요."], ["Either we must separate or else live together.”", "우린 헤어지거나, 아니면 함께 살아야 해요.\""]], [["“Why, you know, that’s my one desire.", "\"아니, 알다시피 그게 내 유일한 바람이잖아."], ["But for that....”", "하지만 그러려면……\""]], [["“We must get a divorce.", "\"우린 이혼을 해야 해요."], ["I will write to him.", "내가 그에게 편지를 쓰겠어요."], ["I see I cannot go on like this....", "이대로는 계속 살아갈 수 없다는 걸 알겠어요……"], ["But I will come with you to Moscow.”", "하지만 난 당신과 함께 모스크바로 가겠어요.\""]], [["“You talk as if you were threatening me.", "\"당신은 마치 나를 협박하듯 말하는군."], ["But I desire nothing so much as never to be parted from you,”", "하지만 난 당신과 결코 떨어지지 않는 것만큼 간절히 바라는 게 없어.\""], ["said Vronsky, smiling.", "브론스키가 미소를 지으며 말했다."]], [["But as he said these words there gleamed in his eyes not merely a cold look, but the vindictive look of a man persecuted and made cruel.", "그러나 이 말을 하는 동안, 그의 눈에서는 단순히 차가운 빛만이 아니라, 박해받아 잔인해진 사람의 앙심에 찬 빛이 번뜩였다."]], [["She saw the look and correctly divined its meaning.", "그녀는 그 눈빛을 보았고, 그 의미를 정확히 알아차렸다."]], [["“If so, it’s a calamity!”", "\"그렇다면 그건 재앙이야!\""], ["that glance told her.", "그 시선은 그녀에게 그렇게 말하고 있었다."], ["It was a moment’s impression, but she never forgot it.", "그것은 한순간의 인상이었지만, 그녀는 그것을 결코 잊지 못했다."]], [["Anna wrote to her husband asking him about a divorce, and towards the end of November, taking leave of Princess Varvara, who wanted to go to Petersburg, she went with Vronsky to Moscow.", "안나는 남편에게 이혼에 관해 묻는 편지를 썼고, 11월 말 무렵 페테르부르크로 가고 싶어 하던 바르바라 공작 영애와 작별한 뒤, 브론스키와 함께 모스크바로 갔다."], ["Expecting every day an answer from Alexey Alexandrovitch, and after that the divorce, they now established themselves together like married people.", "날마다 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 답장을, 그리고 그 뒤에 이어질 이혼을 기다리며, 두 사람은 이제 결혼한 부부처럼 함께 살림을 차렸다."]], [["# PART SEVEN Chapter 1", "# PART SEVEN Chapter 1"]], [["The Levins had been three months in Moscow.", "레빈 부부는 모스크바에 온 지 석 달째였다."], ["The date had long passed on which, according to the most trustworthy calculations of people learned in such matters, Kitty should have been confined.", "이런 일에 정통한 사람들의 가장 믿을 만한 계산에 따르면 키티가 해산했어야 할 날짜는 이미 한참 지나 있었다."], ["But she was still about, and there was nothing to show that her time was any nearer than two months ago.", "그러나 그녀는 여전히 멀쩡히 돌아다녔고, 두 달 전보다 그 시기가 조금이라도 가까워졌다는 기미는 어디에도 없었다."], ["The doctor, the monthly nurse, and Dolly and her mother, and most of all Levin, who could not think of the approaching event without terror, began to be impatient and uneasy.", "의사도, 산파도, 돌리와 그녀의 어머니도, 그리고 무엇보다도 다가오는 그 일을 두려움 없이는 생각조차 할 수 없는 레빈도 점점 초조해하고 불안해하기 시작했다."], ["Kitty was the only person who felt perfectly calm and happy.", "완전히 침착하고 행복한 사람은 오직 키티 하나뿐이었다."]], [["She was distinctly conscious now of the birth of a new feeling of love for the future child, for her to some extent actually existing already, and she brooded blissfully over this feeling.", "이제 그녀는 장차 태어날 아이에 대한 사랑이라는 새로운 감정이 자기 안에서 싹튼 것을 또렷이 의식하고 있었다. 그 아이는 어느 정도는 이미 실제로 존재하는 것이나 다름없었고, 그녀는 더없이 행복한 마음으로 이 감정에 잠겨 있었다."], ["He was not by now altogether a part of herself, but sometimes lived his own life independently of her.", "이제 아이는 그녀의 일부만은 아니어서, 때로는 그녀와 상관없이 제 나름의 삶을 살았다."], ["Often this separate being gave her pain, but at the same time she wanted to laugh with a strange new joy.", "종종 이 별개의 존재가 그녀에게 고통을 주었지만, 동시에 그녀는 낯설고도 새로운 기쁨에 웃음을 터뜨리고 싶어졌다."]], [["All the people she loved were with her, and all were so good to her, so attentively caring for her, so entirely pleasant was everything presented to her, that if she had not known and felt that it must all soon be over, she could not have wished for a better and pleasanter life.", "그녀가 사랑하는 사람들이 모두 그녀 곁에 있었고, 다들 그녀에게 더없이 다정하고, 더없이 세심하게 보살펴 주었으며, 그녀에게 주어지는 모든 것이 너무도 흐뭇해서, 이 모든 것이 곧 끝나리라는 것을 알지도 느끼지도 못했더라면 이보다 더 낫고 더 즐거운 삶은 바라지도 못했을 정도였다."], ["The only thing that spoiled the charm of this manner of life was that her husband was not here as she loved him to be, and as he was in the country.", "이런 생활의 매력을 망치는 것이 딱 하나 있었으니, 그것은 그녀가 사랑하는 모습 그대로의, 그러니까 시골에서의 그 모습 그대로의 남편이 여기에는 없다는 점이었다."]], [["She liked his serene, friendly, and hospitable manner in the country.", "그녀는 시골에서의 그의 평온하고 다정하며 손님을 반기는 태도가 좋았다."], ["In the town he seemed continually uneasy and on his guard, as though he were afraid someone would be rude to him, and still more to her.", "도시에서 그는 끊임없이 불안해하고 경계하는 듯했는데, 누군가가 자기에게, 더더욱 그녀에게 무례하게 굴까 봐 두려워하는 사람 같았다."], ["At home in the country, knowing himself distinctly to be in his right place, he was never in haste to be off elsewhere.", "시골 집에서는 자기가 분명히 제자리에 있다는 것을 알기에 그는 결코 어디론가 서둘러 떠나려 하지 않았다. 그는 한순간도 빈둥거리는 법이 없었다."], ["He was never unoccupied. Here in town he was in a continual hurry, as though afraid of missing something, and yet he had nothing to do.", "그런데 이 도시에서는 마치 무언가를 놓칠까 봐 겁이라도 내는 사람처럼 끊임없이 부산을 떨었지만, 정작 할 일이라곤 아무것도 없었다."], ["And she felt sorry for him.", "그래서 그녀는 그가 가여웠다."], ["To others, she knew, he did not appear an object of pity.", "그녀는 다른 사람들 눈에는 그가 동정의 대상으로 보이지 않는다는 것을 알았다."], ["On the contrary, when Kitty looked at him in society, as one sometimes looks at those one loves, trying to see him as if he were a stranger, so as to catch the impression he must make on others, she saw with a panic even of jealous fear that he was far indeed from being a pitiable figure, that he was very attractive with his fine breeding, his rather old-fashioned, reserved courtesy with women, his powerful figure, and striking, as she thought, and expressive face.", "오히려 키티가 사교계에서, 사랑하는 사람을 이따금 그렇게 바라보듯이, 그를 낯선 사람처럼 보려 애쓰며—그가 남들에게 줄 인상을 포착하려고—바라보면, 그가 결코 가여운 모습과는 거리가 멀다는 것을, 훌륭한 교양과 여성을 대하는 다소 구식의 절제된 정중함, 다부진 체격, 그리고 그녀가 보기에 인상적이고 표정이 풍부한 얼굴로 무척 매력적이라는 것을, 질투 어린 두려움마저 느낄 만큼 깜짝 놀라며 깨닫곤 했다. 그러나 그녀는 그를 바깥에서가 아니라 안에서 보았다."], ["But she saw him not from without, but from within; she saw that here he was not himself; that was the only way she could define his condition to herself.", "그녀는 여기에서 그가 그 자신이 아니라는 것을 보았고, 그것이 그의 상태를 스스로에게 설명할 수 있는 유일한 방식이었다."], ["Sometimes she inwardly reproached him for his inability to live in the town; sometimes she recognized that it was really hard for him to order his life here so that he could be satisfied with it.", "때로 그녀는 그가 도시에서 살지 못하는 것을 마음속으로 나무랐고, 때로는 이곳에서 그가 만족할 만하게 자기 삶을 꾸려 나가기란 정말로 어려운 일이라는 것을 인정했다."]], [["What had he to do, indeed?", "대체 그가 여기서 무엇을 할 수 있었겠는가?"], ["He did not care for cards; he did not go to a club.", "그는 카드놀이를 좋아하지 않았고, 클럽에도 가지 않았다."], ["Spending the time with jovial gentlemen of Oblonsky’s type—she knew now what that meant ...", "오블론스키 같은 부류의 유쾌한 신사들과 시간을 보낸다는 것이—이제 그녀는 그게 무슨 뜻인지 알았다……"], ["it meant drinking and going somewhere after drinking.", "그것은 술을 마시고, 마신 뒤에 또 어디론가 가는 것을 뜻했다."], ["She could not think without horror of where men went on such occasions.", "그런 자리에서 남자들이 어디로 가는지를 그녀는 끔찍한 마음 없이는 생각할 수도 없었다."], ["Was he to go into society?", "그렇다면 사교계에 나가야 할까?"], ["But she knew he could only find satisfaction in that if he took pleasure in the society of young women, and that she could not wish for.", "그러나 그녀는 그가 거기서 만족을 찾을 수 있는 길이라곤 젊은 여자들과 어울리는 것을 즐기는 경우뿐이라는 것을 알았고, 그것은 그녀가 바랄 수 없는 일이었다."], ["Should he stay at home with her, her mother and her sisters?", "그렇다면 집에서 그녀와 그녀의 어머니, 자매들과 함께 있어야 할까?"], ["But much as she liked and enjoyed their conversations forever on the same subjects—“Aline-Nadine,” as the old prince called the sisters’ talks—she knew it must bore him.", "그러나 늘 똑같은 화제를 두고 끝없이 이어지는 그들의 대화를—노공작이 자매들의 수다를 두고 \"알린나딘\"이라 부르던 그것을—그녀 자신은 아무리 좋아하고 즐긴다 해도, 그것이 그를 지루하게 하리라는 것을 알았다."], ["What was there left for him to do?", "그렇다면 그에게 남은 일이 무엇이었겠는가?"], ["To go on writing at his book he had indeed attempted, and at first he used to go to the library and make extracts and look up references for his book.", "자기 책을 계속 써 나가는 것은 실제로 시도해 본 일이었고, 처음에는 도서관에 가서 발췌를 하고 책에 필요한 참고 자료를 찾아보곤 했다."], ["But, as he told her, the more he did nothing, the less time he had to do anything.", "그러나 그가 그녀에게 말했듯이, 아무것도 하지 않으면 않을수록 무언가를 할 시간이 더 없어졌다."], ["And besides, he complained that he had talked too much about his book here, and that consequently all his ideas about it were muddled and had lost their interest for him.", "게다가 그는 여기서 자기 책에 대해 너무 많이 떠들어 댄 탓에, 그것에 관한 자기 생각이 죄다 뒤죽박죽이 되고 흥미마저 잃어버렸다고 푸념했다."]], [["One advantage in this town life was that quarrels hardly ever happened between them here in town.", "이 도시 생활의 한 가지 이점이라면, 여기 도시에서는 두 사람 사이에 다툼이 좀처럼 일어나지 않는다는 것이었다."], ["Whether it was that their conditions were different, or that they had both become more careful and sensible in that respect, they had no quarrels in Moscow from jealousy, which they had so dreaded when they moved from the country.", "처지가 달라진 탓인지, 아니면 그 점에서 둘 다 더 조심스럽고 분별 있게 된 탓인지, 그들은 시골에서 이사 올 때 그토록 두려워했던 질투로 인한 다툼을 모스크바에서는 한 번도 벌이지 않았다."]], [["One event, an event of great importance to both from that point of view, did indeed happen—that was Kitty’s meeting with Vronsky.", "다만 그런 관점에서 두 사람에게 무척 중요한 사건이 하나 실제로 일어나기는 했다. 그것은 키티가 브론스키와 마주친 일이었다."]], [["The old Princess Marya Borissovna, Kitty’s godmother, who had always been very fond of her, had insisted on seeing her.", "키티의 대모인 노공작 부인 마리야 보리소브나는 늘 그녀를 무척 아꼈는데, 한사코 그녀를 보아야겠다고 우겼다."], ["Kitty, though she did not go into society at all on account of her condition, went with her father to see the venerable old lady, and there met Vronsky.", "키티는 몸 상태 때문에 사교계에는 전혀 나가지 않았지만, 아버지와 함께 그 존경스러운 노부인을 찾아갔고, 거기서 브론스키를 만났다."]], [["The only thing Kitty could reproach herself for at this meeting was that at the instant when she recognized in his civilian dress the features once so familiar to her, her breath failed her, the blood rushed to her heart, and a vivid blush—she felt it—overspread her face.", "이 만남에서 키티가 자책할 만한 것이 딱 하나 있었다면, 사복 차림 속에서 한때 그토록 익숙했던 그의 이목구비를 알아본 그 순간 숨이 막히고, 피가 심장으로 쏠리며, 선명한 홍조가—그녀 자신도 느꼈다—얼굴에 번졌다는 점이었다."], ["But this lasted only a few seconds.", "그러나 그것은 단 몇 초간 이어졌을 뿐이었다."], ["Before her father, who purposely began talking in a loud voice to Vronsky, had finished, she was perfectly ready to look at Vronsky, to speak to him, if necessary, exactly as she spoke to Princess Marya Borissovna, and more than that, to do so in such a way that everything to the faintest intonation and smile would have been approved by her husband, whose unseen presence she seemed to feel about her at that instant.", "일부러 큰 소리로 브론스키에게 말을 걸기 시작한 아버지의 말이 끝나기도 전에, 그녀는 마리야 보리소브나 공작 부인에게 말하는 그대로 브론스키를 바라보고, 필요하다면 말을 건넬 만반의 준비가 완전히 되어 있었으며, 그뿐 아니라 더없이 미세한 억양과 미소까지도, 그 순간 자기 곁에 보이지 않게 함께 있는 듯 느껴지는 남편의 인정을 받을 만한 방식으로 그렇게 할 수 있었다."]], [["She said a few words to him, even smiled serenely at his joke about the elections, which he called “our parliament.”", "그녀는 그에게 몇 마디를 건넸고, 그가 선거를 두고 \"우리 의회\"라고 부르며 한 농담에도 평온하게 미소까지 지어 보였다."], ["(She had to smile to show she saw the joke.) But she turned away immediately to Princess Marya Borissovna, and did not once glance at him till he got up to go; then she looked at him, but evidently only because it would be uncivil not to look at a man when he is saying good-bye.", "(그 농담을 알아들었다는 것을 보이려면 미소를 지어야 했다.) 그러나 그녀는 곧바로 마리야 보리소브나 공작 부인 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸고, 그가 일어나 가려 할 때까지 단 한 번도 그를 쳐다보지 않았다. 그제야 그를 바라보았지만, 그것은 분명 작별 인사를 하는 사람을 쳐다보지 않는 것이 무례한 일이기 때문일 뿐이었다."]], [["She was grateful to her father for saying nothing to her about their meeting Vronsky, but she saw by his special warmth to her after the visit during their usual walk that he was pleased with her.", "그녀는 아버지가 브론스키와 마주친 일에 대해 자기에게 아무 말도 하지 않은 것이 고마웠다. 그러나 방문을 마친 뒤 평소처럼 산책하는 동안 그녀를 대하는 아버지의 각별한 다정함을 보고, 아버지가 그녀를 흐뭇해한다는 것을 알았다."], ["She was pleased with herself.", "그녀는 스스로가 만족스러웠다."], ["She had not expected she would have had the power, while keeping somewhere in the bottom of her heart all the memories of her old feeling for Vronsky, not only to seem but to be perfectly indifferent and composed with him.", "브론스키에 대한 옛 감정의 온갖 기억을 마음 밑바닥 어딘가에 고스란히 간직한 채로도, 그저 겉으로만 그런 척하는 것이 아니라 정말로 그에게 완전히 무심하고 침착할 수 있는 힘이 자기에게 있으리라고는 미처 기대하지 못했던 것이다."]], [["Levin flushed a great deal more than she when she told him she had met Vronsky at Princess Marya Borissovna’s.", "마리야 보리소브나 공작 부인 댁에서 브론스키를 만났다는 이야기를 그녀가 했을 때, 레빈은 그녀보다 훨씬 더 새빨개졌다."], ["It was very hard for her to tell him this, but still harder to go on speaking of the details of the meeting, as he did not question her, but simply gazed at her with a frown.", "이 이야기를 그에게 꺼내는 것 자체가 그녀에게는 무척 힘들었지만, 만남의 세세한 정황을 계속 이야기하기란 더더욱 힘들었다. 그가 그녀에게 물어보지도 않고 그저 미간을 찌푸린 채 그녀를 빤히 바라보기만 했기 때문이다."]], [["“I am very sorry you weren’t there,”", "\"당신이 그 자리에 없었던 게 정말 아쉬워요.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“Not that you weren’t in the room ...", "\"당신이 그 방에 없었다는 게 아니라……"], ["I couldn’t have been so natural in your presence ...", "당신이 있었으면 난 그렇게 자연스럽게 굴지 못했을 거예요……"], ["I am blushing now much more, much, much more,”", "지금 난 그때보다 훨씬, 정말 훨씬 더 새빨개지고 있는걸요.\""], ["she said, blushing till the tears came into her eyes.", "그녀는 눈에 눈물이 맺힐 만큼 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다."], ["“But that you couldn’t see through a crack.”", "\"그게 아니라, 당신이 문틈으로라도 그 모습을 볼 수 없었던 게 아쉽다는 거예요.\""]], [["The truthful eyes told Levin that she was satisfied with herself, and in spite of her blushing he was quickly reassured and began questioning her, which was all she wanted.", "그 진실한 눈빛은 그녀가 스스로 만족하고 있다는 것을 레빈에게 말해 주었고, 그녀가 얼굴을 붉히는데도 그는 금세 안심하여 그녀에게 이것저것 묻기 시작했다."], ["When he had heard everything, even to the detail that for the first second she could not help flushing, but that afterwards she was just as direct and as much at her ease as with any chance acquaintance, Levin was quite happy again and said he was glad of it, and would not now behave as stupidly as he had done at the election, but would try the first time he met Vronsky to be as friendly as possible.", "그것이 바로 그녀가 바라던 전부였다. 첫 순간에는 그녀도 얼굴을 붉히지 않을 수 없었지만 그 뒤로는 어쩌다 알게 된 그 누구를 대할 때와 마찬가지로 솔직하고 편안했다는 세세한 대목까지 모두 듣고 나자, 레빈은 다시 무척 행복해져서 그것이 기쁘다고, 이제는 선거 때처럼 어리석게 굴지 않고 다음번에 브론스키를 만나면 되도록 다정하게 대하도록 애쓰겠다고 말했다."]], [["“It’s so wretched to feel that there’s a man almost an enemy whom it’s painful to meet,”", "\"만나기 거북한, 거의 적이나 다름없는 사람이 있다고 느끼는 건 정말 비참한 일이지.\""], ["said Levin.", "레빈이 말했다."], ["“I’m very, very glad.”", "\"정말, 정말 기뻐.\""]], [["# Chapter 2", "# Chapter 2"]], [["“Go, please, go then and call on the Bols,”", "\"가요, 제발, 가서 볼 집안에 다녀와요.\""], ["Kitty said to her husband, when he came in to see her at eleven o’clock before going out.", "키티가 남편에게 말했다. 그가 외출하기 전, 열한 시에 그녀를 보러 들어왔을 때였다."], ["“I know you are dining at the club; papa put down your name.", "\"당신이 클럽에서 식사하는 거 알아요. 아빠가 당신 이름을 적어 두셨거든요."], ["But what are you going to do in the morning?”", "그런데 오전에는 뭘 할 거예요?\""]], [["“I am only going to Katavasov,”", "\"카타바소프한테나 가려고.\""], ["answered Levin.", "레빈이 대답했다."]], [["“Why so early?”", "\"왜 그렇게 일찍요?\""]], [["“He promised to introduce me to Metrov.", "\"그가 나를 메트로프한테 소개해 주기로 했거든."], ["I wanted to talk to him about my work.", "내 연구에 대해 그와 이야기하고 싶어서."], ["He’s a distinguished scientific man from Petersburg,”", "페테르부르크에서 온 저명한 학자야.\""], ["said Levin.", "레빈이 말했다."]], [["“Yes; wasn’t it his article you were praising so?", "\"맞다, 당신이 그렇게 칭찬하던 게 그 사람 논문 아니었어요?"], ["Well, and after that?”", "그래, 그러고 나서는요?\""], ["said Kitty.", "키티가 말했다."]], [["“I shall go to the court, perhaps, about my sister’s business.”", "\"법원에 가야 할지도 몰라, 누이 일 때문에.\""]], [["“And the concert?”", "\"그럼 음악회는요?\""], ["she queried.", "그녀가 물었다."]], [["“I shan’t go there all alone.”", "\"나 혼자서는 거기 안 갈 거야.\""]], [["“No?", "\"안 가요?"], ["do go; there are going to be some new things....", "가요, 새로운 곡들이 연주된대요……"], ["That interested you so.", "당신이 그렇게 관심 있어 하던 것들이잖아요."], ["I should certainly go.”", "나라면 꼭 가겠어요.\""]], [["“Well, anyway, I shall come home before dinner,”", "\"뭐, 어쨌든 식사 전에는 집에 올게.\""], ["he said, looking at his watch.", "그가 시계를 보며 말했다."]], [["“Put on your frock coat, so that you can go straight to call on Countess Bola.”", "\"프록코트를 입어요. 곧장 볼라 백작 부인 댁에 다녀올 수 있게요.\""]], [["“But is it absolutely necessary?”", "\"그게 꼭 그렇게 해야 하는 일이야?\""]], [["“Oh, absolutely!", "\"아, 그럼요, 꼭 해야죠!"], ["He has been to see us.", "그분이 우리를 보러 오셨잖아요."], ["Come, what is it?", "자, 별거 아니에요."], ["You go in, sit down, talk for five minutes of the weather, get up and go away.”", "들어가서 앉고, 오 분쯤 날씨 이야기 하다가 일어나서 나오면 돼요.\""]], [["“Oh, you wouldn’t believe it!", "\"아, 당신은 믿지 못할 거야!"], ["I’ve got so out of the way of all this that it makes me feel positively ashamed.", "내가 이런 일에서 너무 멀어져서 정말이지 부끄러워질 정도라니까."], ["It’s such a horrible thing to do!", "이건 정말 끔찍한 일이야!"], ["A complete outsider walks in, sits down, stays on with nothing to do, wastes their time and worries himself, and walks away!”", "완전히 남남인 사람이 걸어 들어가서, 앉아서, 할 일도 없이 머물며 남의 시간을 축내고 자기도 안절부절못하다가 걸어 나오는 거잖아!\""]], [["Kitty laughed.", "키티가 웃었다."]], [["“Why, I suppose you used to pay calls before you were married, didn’t you?”", "\"아니, 결혼하기 전에는 방문 인사를 다니곤 했을 거 아니에요?\""]], [["“Yes, I did, but I always felt ashamed, and now I’m so out of the way of it that, by Jove!", "\"그래, 다녔지. 하지만 늘 부끄러웠어."], ["I’d sooner go two days running without my dinner than pay this call!", "그리고 지금은 그런 일에서 너무 멀어져서, 맙소사! 이런 방문을 하느니 차라리 이틀 내리 저녁을 굶는 게 낫겠어!"], ["One’s so ashamed!", "정말 부끄럽단 말이야!"], ["I feel all the while that they’re annoyed, that they’re saying, ‘What has he come for?’”", "나는 줄곧 그들이 짜증스러워하면서 '저 사람은 뭐 하러 온 거야?' 하고 말하는 것만 같다고.\""]], [["“No, they won’t. I’ll answer for that,”", "\"아니에요, 그렇지 않을 거예요."], ["said Kitty, looking into his face with a laugh.", "내가 장담해요.\" 키티가 웃으며 그의 얼굴을 들여다보며 말했다."], ["She took his hand.", "그녀는 그의 손을 잡았다."], ["“Well, good-bye....", "\"자, 잘 다녀와요……"], ["Do go, please.”", "제발, 가요.\""]], [["He was just going out after kissing his wife’s hand, when she stopped him.", "그는 아내의 손에 입을 맞추고 막 나가려던 참이었는데, 그녀가 그를 멈춰 세웠다."]], [["“Kostya, do you know I’ve only fifty roubles left?”", "\"코스챠, 나한테 이제 오십 루블밖에 안 남은 거 알아요?\""]], [["“Oh, all right, I’ll go to the bank and get some.", "\"아, 알았어, 은행에 가서 좀 찾아올게."], ["How much?”", "얼마나?\""], ["he said, with the expression of dissatisfaction she knew so well.", "그가 그녀가 너무도 잘 아는 그 불만스러운 표정을 지으며 말했다."]], [["“No, wait a minute.”", "\"아니, 잠깐만요.\""], ["She held his hand.", "그녀가 그의 손을 잡았다."], ["“Let’s talk about it, it worries me.", "\"이 얘기 좀 해요, 나 신경 쓰여서요."], ["I seem to spend nothing unnecessary, but money seems to fly away simply.", "쓸데없는 데는 아무것도 안 쓰는 것 같은데, 돈은 그냥 날아가 버리는 것 같아요."], ["We don’t manage well, somehow.”", "우리, 어쩐지 살림을 잘 못하나 봐요.\""]], [["“Oh, it’s all right,”", "\"아니, 괜찮아.\""], ["he said with a little cough, looking at her from under his brows.", "그가 살짝 헛기침을 하며 눈썹 아래로 그녀를 바라보며 말했다."]], [["That cough she knew well.", "그 헛기침을 그녀는 잘 알았다."], ["It was a sign of intense dissatisfaction, not with her, but with himself.", "그것은 그녀가 아니라 자기 자신에 대한 극심한 불만의 표시였다."], ["He certainly was displeased not at so much money being spent, but at being reminded of what he, knowing something was unsatisfactory, wanted to forget.", "그가 못마땅해한 것은 분명 돈을 그렇게 많이 쓴 게 아니라, 뭔가 잘못되고 있다는 걸 알면서도 잊고 싶었던 그 일을 새삼 떠올리게 되었다는 점이었다."]], [["“I have told Sokolov to sell the wheat, and to borrow an advance on the mill.", "\"소콜로프한테 밀을 팔고, 방앗간을 담보로 가불을 받으라고 일러 뒀어."], ["We shall have money enough in any case.”", "어쨌든 우리한테 돈은 충분할 거야.\""]], [["“Yes, but I’m afraid that altogether....”", "\"네, 하지만 전체적으로 봐서 걱정이 돼서요……\""]], [["“Oh, it’s all right, all right,”", "\"아, 괜찮아, 괜찮다니까.\""], ["he repeated.", "그가 되풀이했다."], ["“Well, good-bye, darling.”", "\"자, 잘 있어, 여보.\""]], [["“No, I’m really sorry sometimes that I listened to mamma.", "\"아니에요, 나는 가끔 엄마 말을 들은 게 정말 후회돼요."], ["How nice it would have been in the country!", "시골에 있었으면 얼마나 좋았을까요!"], ["As it is, I’m worrying you all, and we’re wasting our money.”", "지금은 내가 모두를 괴롭히고 있고, 우리 돈만 낭비하고 있잖아요.\""]], [["“Not at all, not at all.", "\"전혀 그렇지 않아, 전혀."], ["Not once since I’ve been married have I said that things could have been better than they are....”", "결혼한 뒤로 단 한 번도 지금보다 형편이 더 나을 수 있었을 거라고 생각한 적 없어……\""]], [["“Truly?”", "\"정말요?\""], ["she said, looking into his eyes.", "그녀가 그의 눈을 들여다보며 말했다."]], [["He had said it without thinking, simply to console her.", "그는 아무 생각 없이, 그저 그녀를 위로하려고 그렇게 말했던 것이다."], ["But when he glanced at her and saw those sweet truthful eyes fastened questioningly on him, he repeated it with his whole heart.", "그러나 그녀를 흘긋 보고, 그 사랑스럽고 진실한 눈이 미심쩍은 듯 자기를 향해 고정되어 있는 것을 보았을 때, 그는 온 마음을 담아 같은 말을 되풀이했다."], ["“I was positively forgetting her,”", "'내가 정말이지 이 사람을 잊고 있었구나.'"], ["he thought.", "그는 생각했다."], ["And he remembered what was before them, so soon to come.", "그리고 그는 자기들 앞에 놓인 것, 머지않아 닥쳐올 그 일을 떠올렸다."]], [["“Will it be soon?", "\"곧일까?"], ["How do you feel?”", "몸은 어때?\""], ["he whispered, taking her two hands.", "그가 그녀의 두 손을 잡으며 속삭였다."]], [["“I have so often thought so, that now I don’t think about it or know anything about it.”", "\"너무 자주 곧이려니 생각해서, 이제는 그 생각도 안 하고 아무것도 모르겠어요.\""]], [["“And you’re not frightened?”", "\"무섭지는 않고?\""]], [["She smiled contemptuously.", "그녀가 경멸하듯 미소 지었다."]], [["“Not the least little bit,”", "\"조금도 안 무서워요.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“Well, if anything happens, I shall be at Katavasov’s.”", "\"음, 무슨 일이 생기면 나는 카타바소프 집에 있을게.\""]], [["“No, nothing will happen, and don’t think about it.", "\"아니에요, 아무 일도 안 생길 거예요. 그런 생각은 하지 마요."], ["I’m going for a walk on the boulevard with papa.", "나는 아빠랑 가로수 길로 산책 갈 거예요."], ["We’re going to see Dolly.", "돌리를 만나러 갈 거고요."], ["I shall expect you before dinner.", "식사 전에 당신을 기다릴게요."], ["Oh, yes!", "아, 맞다!"], ["Do you know that Dolly’s position is becoming utterly impossible?", "돌리 형편이 완전히 감당이 안 되게 돼 가는 거 알아요?"], ["She’s in debt all round; she hasn’t a penny.", "온통 빚투성이에다 한 푼도 없대요."], ["We were talking yesterday with mamma and Arseny” (this was her sister’s husband Lvov), “and we determined to send you with him to talk to Stiva.", "어제 엄마랑 아르세니랑\"—이는 그녀의 언니 남편 리보프였다—\"이야기를 나눴는데, 당신을 그이와 함께 보내서 스티바한테 얘기하게 하기로 정했어요."], ["It’s really unbearable.", "정말 견딜 수가 없어요."], ["One can’t speak to papa about it....", "아빠한테는 이 얘기를 할 수가 없고요……"], ["But if you and he....”", "하지만 당신이랑 그이가……\""]], [["“Why, what can we do?”", "\"아니, 우리가 뭘 할 수 있겠어?\""], ["said Levin.", "레빈이 말했다."]], [["“You’ll be at Arseny’s, anyway; talk to him, he will tell what we decided.”", "\"어차피 아르세니 집에 갈 거잖아요. 그이랑 얘기해 봐요, 우리가 어떻게 정했는지 말해 줄 거예요.\""]], [["“Oh, I agree to everything Arseny thinks beforehand.", "\"아, 아르세니가 미리 생각해 둔 거라면 뭐든 다 찬성이야."], ["I’ll go and see him.", "가서 그를 만나 볼게."], ["By the way, if I do go to the concert, I’ll go with Natalia.", "그건 그렇고, 음악회에 가게 되면 나탈리아랑 같이 갈게."], ["Well, good-bye.”", "자, 잘 있어.\""]], [["On the steps Levin was stopped by his old servant Kouzma, who had been with him before his marriage, and now looked after their household in town.", "현관 계단에서 레빈은 그의 늙은 하인 쿠즈마에게 붙들렸다. 쿠즈마는 결혼 전부터 그의 곁에 있었고, 지금은 그들의 시내 살림을 돌보고 있었다."]], [["“Beauty” (that was the left shaft-horse brought up from the country) “has been badly shod and is quite lame,”", "\"'미녀'가\"—이는 시골에서 데려온 왼쪽 끌채 말이었다—\"편자를 잘못 박아서 다리를 심하게 절뚝거립니다."], ["he said.", "나리께서는 어떻게 하면 좋겠습니까?\""], ["“What does your honor wish to be done?”", "그가 말했다."]], [["During the first part of their stay in Moscow, Levin had used his own horses brought up from the country.", "모스크바 체류 초기에 레빈은 시골에서 데려온 자기 말들을 부렸다."], ["He had tried to arrange this part of their expenses in the best and cheapest way possible; but it appeared that their own horses came dearer than hired horses, and they still hired too.", "그는 이 부분의 비용을 가능한 한 가장 좋고 가장 싸게 처리해 보려고 애썼다. 하지만 자기 말들이 빌린 말보다 오히려 더 비싸게 먹혔고, 그래서 결국 말도 빌려 쓰게 되었다."]], [["“Send for the veterinary, there may be a bruise.”", "\"수의사를 부르게. 멍이 들었는지도 모르니까.\""]], [["“And for Katerina Alexandrovna?”", "\"카체리나 알렉산드로브나 마님께는 어떡할까요?\""], ["asked Kouzma.", "쿠즈마가 물었다."]], [["Levin was not by now struck as he had been at first by the fact that to get from one end of Moscow to the other he had to have two powerful horses put into a heavy carriage, to take the carriage three miles through the snowy slush and to keep it standing there four hours, paying five roubles every time.", "모스크바 이쪽 끝에서 저쪽 끝으로 가려면 묵직한 마차에 힘센 말 두 필을 매고, 눈 녹은 진창길을 삼 마일이나 달려 거기에 네 시간이나 세워 두면서 매번 오 루블씩 치러야 한다는 사실이, 이제 레빈에게는 처음만큼 충격적으로 다가오지 않았다."]], [["Now it seemed quite natural.", "이제는 그것이 아주 당연하게 여겨졌다."]], [["“Hire a pair for our carriage from the jobmaster,”", "\"마차에 매도록 삯마차업자한테서 한 쌍을 빌려 오게.\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“Yes, sir.”", "\"네, 나리.\""]], [["And so, simply and easily, thanks to the facilities of town life, Levin settled a question which, in the country, would have called for so much personal trouble and exertion, and going out onto the steps, he called a sledge, sat down, and drove to Nikitsky.", "이렇게 도시 생활의 편리함 덕분에 레빈은 시골이라면 그토록 많은 수고와 노력을 요했을 문제를 간단하고 손쉽게 처리하고는, 계단으로 나가 썰매를 불러 올라타고 니키츠키로 향했다."], ["On the way he thought no more of money, but mused on the introduction that awaited him to the Petersburg savant, a writer on sociology, and what he would say to him about his book.", "가는 길에 그는 더 이상 돈 생각을 하지 않고, 자기를 기다리는 페테르부르크 학자—사회학에 관한 글을 쓰는 사람—와의 소개 자리를, 그리고 자기 책에 대해 그에게 무슨 말을 할지를 곰곰이 생각했다."]], [["Only during the first days of his stay in Moscow Levin had been struck by the expenditure, strange to one living in the country, unproductive but inevitable, that was expected of him on every side.", "모스크바 체류 첫 며칠 동안에만 레빈은 시골에 사는 사람에게는 낯선, 비생산적이면서도 어쩔 수 없는, 사방에서 그에게 기대되는 그 지출에 충격을 받았었다."], ["But by now he had grown used to it.", "하지만 이제는 그것에 익숙해져 있었다."], ["That had happened to him in this matter which is said to happen to drunkards—the first glass sticks in the throat, the second flies down like a hawk, but after the third they’re like tiny little birds.", "술꾼들에게 일어난다는 그 일이 이 문제에서 그에게 일어난 것이다. 첫 잔은 목구멍에 걸리고, 둘째 잔은 매처럼 날아 내려가고, 셋째 잔부터는 작은 새들처럼 술술 넘어간다는 것 말이다."], ["When Levin had changed his first hundred-rouble note to pay for liveries for his footmen and hall-porter he could not help reflecting that these liveries were of no use to anyone—but they were indubitably necessary, to judge by the amazement of the princess and Kitty when he suggested that they might do without liveries,—that these liveries would cost the wages of two laborers for the summer, that is, would pay for about three hundred working days from Easter to Ash Wednesday, and each a day of hard work from early morning to late evening—and that hundred-rouble note did stick in his throat.", "하인들과 현관지기의 제복 값을 치르려고 첫 백 루블짜리 지폐를 헐었을 때, 레빈은 이 제복이 누구에게도 쓸모없는 것이라는 생각을 하지 않을 수 없었다—하지만 제복 없이 지내도 되지 않겠냐고 그가 제안했을 때 공작 부인과 키티가 보인 그 경악을 보면 그것은 의심할 여지 없이 필요한 것이었다—그리고 이 제복 값이 일꾼 둘의 여름 한철 품삯, 그러니까 부활절부터 재의 수요일까지 약 삼백 일치 노동, 그것도 매일 이른 아침부터 늦은 저녁까지 이어지는 고된 하루 노동의 대가에 맞먹는다는 생각도 들었고—그 백 루블짜리 지폐는 정말로 목구멍에 걸렸다."], ["But the next note, changed to pay for providing a dinner for their relations, that cost twenty-eight roubles, though it did excite in Levin the reflection that twenty-eight roubles meant nine measures of oats, which men would with groans and sweat have reaped and bound and thrashed and winnowed and sifted and sown,—this next one he parted with more easily.", "하지만 친척들에게 식사를 대접하느라 헐어 쓴 다음 지폐는, 그것이 이십팔 루블이었고, 이십팔 루블이면 사람들이 끙끙 신음하고 땀 흘리며 베고 묶고 타작하고 까부르고 체질하고 뿌렸을 귀리 아홉 말에 해당한다는 생각을 레빈에게 불러일으키긴 했어도—이 지폐는 더 수월하게 떠나보냈다."], ["And now the notes he changed no longer aroused such reflections, and they flew off like little birds.", "그리고 이제 그가 헐어 쓰는 지폐들은 더 이상 그런 생각을 불러일으키지 않았고, 작은 새들처럼 날아가 버렸다."], ["Whether the labor devoted to obtaining the money corresponded to the pleasure given by what was bought with it, was a consideration he had long ago dismissed.", "돈을 마련하는 데 들인 노동이 그 돈으로 산 것이 주는 즐거움에 걸맞은지 어떤지는, 그가 이미 오래전에 떨쳐 버린 고려 사항이었다."], ["His business calculation that there was a certain price below which he could not sell certain grain was forgotten too.", "어떤 곡물을 어느 가격 아래로는 팔 수 없다는 그의 사업적 계산도 잊혀 버렸다."], ["The rye, for the price of which he had so long held out, had been sold for fifty kopecks a measure cheaper than it had been fetching a month ago.", "그가 그토록 오래 버티며 값을 받으려 했던 호밀은, 한 달 전에 받던 값보다 한 말에 오십 코페이카나 싸게 팔려 나갔다."], ["Even the consideration that with such an expenditure he could not go on living for a year without debt, that even had no force.", "이런 식으로 지출하면 일 년도 빚 없이 살아갈 수 없으리라는 생각조차, 그것조차 아무런 힘이 없었다."], ["Only one thing was essential: to have money in the bank, without inquiring where it came from, so as to know that one had the wherewithal to buy meat for tomorrow.", "단 한 가지만이 필수적이었다. 그것이 어디서 나왔는지 캐묻지 않고 은행에 돈이 있어서, 내일 고기를 살 밑천이 있다는 것을 아는 것 말이다."], ["And this condition had hitherto been fulfilled; he had always had the money in the bank.", "그리고 이 조건은 지금까지 충족되어 왔다. 그는 언제나 은행에 돈이 있었다."], ["But now the money in the bank had gone, and he could not quite tell where to get the next installment.", "하지만 이제 은행의 돈이 바닥났고, 그는 다음 몫을 어디서 구해야 할지 도무지 알 수가 없었다."], ["And this it was which, at the moment when Kitty had mentioned money, had disturbed him; but he had no time to think about it.", "그리고 바로 이것이, 키티가 돈 이야기를 꺼냈던 그 순간에 그의 마음을 어지럽혔던 것이다. 하지만 그는 그것을 생각할 겨를이 없었다."], ["He drove off, thinking of Katavasov and the meeting with Metrov that was before him.", "그는 카타바소프와, 자기 앞에 놓인 메트로프와의 만남을 생각하며 마차를 몰고 떠났다."]], [["# Chapter 3", "# Chapter 3"]], [["Levin had on this visit to town seen a great deal of his old friend at the university, Professor Katavasov, whom he had not seen since his marriage.", "레빈은 이번 도시 방문 중에 대학 시절의 옛 친구인 카타바소프 교수를 자주 만났는데, 그는 결혼한 이후로 그를 보지 못했었다."], ["He liked in Katavasov the clearness and simplicity of his conception of life.", "레빈은 카타바소프의 인생관이 지닌 명료함과 단순함을 좋아했다."], ["Levin thought that the clearness of Katavasov’s conception of life was due to the poverty of his nature; Katavasov thought that the disconnectedness of Levin’s ideas was due to his lack of intellectual discipline; but Levin enjoyed Katavasov’s clearness, and Katavasov enjoyed the abundance of Levin’s untrained ideas, and they liked to meet and to discuss.", "레빈은 카타바소프의 인생관이 그토록 명료한 것은 그의 천성이 빈약하기 때문이라고 생각했고, 카타바소프는 레빈의 사상이 두서없는 것은 그가 지적 단련을 게을리한 탓이라고 생각했다. 그렇지만 레빈은 카타바소프의 명료함을 즐겼고, 카타바소프는 레빈의 다듬어지지 않은 사상의 풍부함을 즐겼으며, 두 사람은 만나서 토론하기를 좋아했다."]], [["Levin had read Katavasov some parts of his book, and he had liked them.", "레빈은 카타바소프에게 자기 책의 몇 부분을 읽어 준 적이 있었는데, 그는 그것을 마음에 들어 했다."], ["On the previous day Katavasov had met Levin at a public lecture and told him that the celebrated Metrov, whose article Levin had so much liked, was in Moscow, that he had been much interested by what Katavasov had told him about Levin’s work, and that he was coming to see him tomorrow at eleven, and would be very glad to make Levin’s acquaintance.", "전날 카타바소프는 공개 강연에서 레빈을 만나, 레빈이 그토록 좋아하던 논문을 쓴 그 유명한 메트로프가 모스크바에 와 있으며, 카타바소프가 레빈의 연구에 대해 들려준 이야기에 그가 큰 흥미를 느꼈고, 내일 열한 시에 만나러 올 것이며, 레빈과 알고 지내게 되어 무척 기쁘리라고 전했다."]], [["“You’re positively a reformed character, I’m glad to see,”", "\"자네 정말 사람이 달라졌군, 보기 좋아.\""], ["said Katavasov, meeting Levin in the little drawing-room.", "카타바소프가 작은 응접실에서 레빈을 맞으며 말했다."], ["“I heard the bell and thought: Impossible that it can be he at the exact time!...", "\"초인종 소리를 듣고 생각했지. 정확한 시각에 올 리가 없는데 설마 그 친구일까!……"], ["Well, what do you say to the Montenegrins now?", "자, 그래 이제 몬테네그로 사람들을 어떻게 생각하나?"], ["They’re a race of warriors.”", "타고난 전사들이지.\""]], [["“Why, what’s happened?”", "\"왜, 무슨 일이 있었나?\""], ["asked Levin.", "레빈이 물었다."]], [["Katavasov in a few words told him the last piece of news from the war, and going into his study, introduced Levin to a short, thick-set man of pleasant appearance. This was Metrov.", "카타바소프는 몇 마디로 전쟁의 가장 최근 소식을 들려주고는, 서재로 들어가 레빈에게 키가 작고 다부진 체격에 호감 가는 인상의 남자를 소개했다."], ["The conversation touched for a brief space on politics and on how recent events were looked at in the higher spheres in Petersburg.", "그가 바로 메트로프였다. 대화는 잠시 정치와, 페테르부르크의 상층부에서 최근 사건들을 어떻게 바라보는가 하는 문제를 건드렸다."], ["Metrov repeated a saying that had reached him through a most trustworthy source, reported as having been uttered on this subject by the Tsar and one of the ministers.", "메트로프는 더없이 믿을 만한 출처를 통해 자기에게 전해진 이야기, 곧 이 문제에 대해 차르와 한 장관이 했다는 말을 되풀이했다."], ["Katavasov had heard also on excellent authority that the Tsar had said something quite different.", "카타바소프 또한 아주 확실한 출처를 통해 차르가 전혀 다른 말을 했다고 들었다."], ["Levin tried to imagine circumstances in which both sayings might have been uttered, and the conversation on that topic dropped.", "레빈은 두 가지 말이 모두 나올 수 있었을 법한 정황을 상상해 보려 했고, 그 화제에 대한 대화는 흐지부지되었다."]], [["“Yes, here he’s written almost a book on the natural conditions of the laborer in relation to the land,”", "\"그래, 이 친구가 토지와 관련한 노동자의 자연적 조건에 대해 거의 책 한 권을 써 냈다네.\" 카타바소프가 말했다."], ["said Katavasov; “I’m not a specialist, but I, as a natural science man, was pleased at his not taking mankind as something outside biological laws; but, on the contrary, seeing his dependence on his surroundings, and in that dependence seeking the laws of his development.”", "\"나야 전문가는 아니지만, 자연과학을 하는 사람으로서 이 친구가 인류를 생물학적 법칙 바깥에 있는 무언가로 다루지 않았다는 점이 마음에 들었지. 오히려 인간이 환경에 의존하고 있음을 보고, 그 의존 속에서 인간 발전의 법칙을 찾고 있더란 말일세.\""]], [["“That’s very interesting,”", "\"그거 아주 흥미롭군요.\""], ["said Metrov.", "메트로프가 말했다."]], [["“What I began precisely was to write a book on agriculture; but studying the chief instrument of agriculture, the laborer,”", "\"제가 정확히 쓰기 시작한 건 농업에 관한 책이었습니다. 그런데 농업의 주된 도구인 노동자를 연구하다 보니,\""], ["said Levin, reddening, “I could not help coming to quite unexpected results.”", "레빈이 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다. \"전혀 예상치 못한 결론에 이르지 않을 수 없었지요.\""]], [["And Levin began carefully, as it were, feeling his ground, to expound his views.", "그리고 레빈은 마치 발밑을 더듬어 살피듯 조심스럽게 자기 견해를 펼치기 시작했다."], ["He knew Metrov had written an article against the generally accepted theory of political economy, but to what extent he could reckon on his sympathy with his own new views he did not know and could not guess from the clever and serene face of the learned man.", "그는 메트로프가 일반적으로 받아들여지는 정치경제학 이론에 반대하는 논문을 썼다는 것을 알고 있었지만, 자기의 새로운 견해에 대한 그의 공감을 어느 정도까지 기대할 수 있을지는 알 수 없었고, 그 학자의 영리하고 평온한 얼굴에서도 짐작할 수가 없었다."]], [["“But in what do you see the special characteristics of the Russian laborer?”", "\"하지만 당신은 러시아 노동자의 특수한 성격을 어디에서 보십니까?\""], ["said Metrov; “in his biological characteristics, so to speak, or in the condition in which he is placed?”", "메트로프가 말했다. \"이른바 생물학적 특성에서입니까, 아니면 그가 놓인 조건에서입니까?\""]], [["Levin saw that there was an idea underlying this question with which he did not agree.", "레빈은 이 질문 밑에 자기가 동의하지 않는 어떤 생각이 깔려 있음을 알아차렸다."], ["But he went on explaining his own idea that the Russian laborer has a quite special view of the land, different from that of other people; and to support this proposition he made haste to add that in his opinion this attitude of the Russian peasant was due to the consciousness of his vocation to people vast unoccupied expanses in the East.", "하지만 그는 러시아 노동자가 다른 사람들과는 다른, 토지에 대한 아주 특수한 관점을 지니고 있다는 자기 생각을 계속 설명해 나갔다. 그리고 이 주장을 뒷받침하기 위해, 러시아 농민의 이러한 태도는 동방의 광대한 미개척지에 사람들을 정착시키는 것이 자기 사명이라는 의식에서 비롯된 것이라는 게 자기 의견이라고 서둘러 덧붙였다."]], [["“One may easily be led into error in basing any conclusion on the general vocation of a people,”", "\"한 민족의 일반적인 사명을 근거로 어떤 결론을 끌어내다 보면 쉽게 오류에 빠질 수 있습니다.\""], ["said Metrov, interrupting Levin.", "메트로프가 레빈의 말을 가로막으며 말했다."], ["“The condition of the laborer will always depend on his relation to the land and to capital.”", "\"노동자의 조건은 언제나 그가 토지와 자본에 대해 맺는 관계에 달려 있는 법이지요.\""]], [["And without letting Levin finish explaining his idea, Metrov began expounding to him the special point of his own theory.", "그리고 메트로프는 레빈이 자기 생각을 다 설명하도록 두지 않고, 자기 이론의 특수한 요점을 그에게 펼쳐 보이기 시작했다."]], [["In what the point of his theory lay, Levin did not understand, because he did not take the trouble to understand.", "자기 이론의 요점이 어디에 있는지 레빈은 이해하지 못했는데, 굳이 이해하려고 애쓰지 않았기 때문이다."], ["He saw that Metrov, like other people, in spite of his own article, in which he had attacked the current theory of political economy, looked at the position of the Russian peasant simply from the point of view of capital, wages, and rent.", "그는 메트로프가 현행 정치경제학 이론을 공격한 자신의 논문에도 불구하고, 다른 사람들과 마찬가지로 러시아 농민의 처지를 그저 자본, 임금, 지대의 관점에서만 바라보고 있음을 알아차렸다."], ["He would indeed have been obliged to admit that in the eastern—much the larger—part of Russia rent was as yet nil, that for nine-tenths of the eighty millions of the Russian peasants wages took the form simply of food provided for themselves, and that capital does not so far exist except in the form of the most primitive tools.", "사실 메트로프는, 러시아의 동부 지역, 그것도 훨씬 더 넓은 그 지역에서는 지대가 아직 전무하며, 팔천만 러시아 농민의 십분의 구에게 임금이란 그저 자신들을 위해 마련하는 식량의 형태를 띨 뿐이고, 자본이란 것도 아직은 가장 원시적인 도구의 형태로밖에 존재하지 않는다는 사실을 인정하지 않을 수 없었을 것이다."], ["Yet it was only from that point of view that he considered every laborer, though in many points he differed from the economists and had his own theory of the wage-fund, which he expounded to Levin.", "그럼에도 그는 오로지 그 관점에서만 모든 노동자를 고찰했으니, 비록 여러 점에서 경제학자들과 견해를 달리하고 임금 기금에 대한 자기 나름의 이론을 가지고 있어 그것을 레빈에게 펼쳐 보이기는 했지만 말이다."]], [["Levin listened reluctantly, and at first made objections.", "레빈은 마지못해 들었고, 처음에는 이의를 제기했다."], ["He would have liked to interrupt Metrov, to explain his own thought, which in his opinion would have rendered further exposition of Metrov’s theories superfluous.", "그는 메트로프의 말을 끊고 자기 생각을 설명하고 싶었는데, 그의 생각으로는 그렇게 하면 메트로프의 이론을 더 펼쳐 보일 필요가 없어질 것 같았다."], ["But later on, feeling convinced that they looked at the matter so differently, that they could never understand one another, he did not even oppose his statements, but simply listened.", "그러나 나중에는 두 사람이 이 문제를 너무도 다르게 바라보고 있어 서로를 결코 이해할 수 없으리라는 확신이 들자, 그의 주장에 반대조차 하지 않고 그저 듣기만 했다."], ["Although what Metrov was saying was by now utterly devoid of interest for him, he yet experienced a certain satisfaction in listening to him.", "메트로프가 하는 말이 이제 그에게는 도무지 흥미라곤 없었지만, 그래도 그의 말을 듣는 데서 어떤 만족을 느꼈다."], ["It flattered his vanity that such a learned man should explain his ideas to him so eagerly, with such intensity and confidence in Levin’s understanding of the subject, sometimes with a mere hint referring him to a whole aspect of the subject.", "이런 학식 있는 사람이 자기에게 그토록 열성적으로, 그토록 강렬하게, 또 레빈이 이 주제를 이해하고 있다고 굳게 믿으며, 때로는 그저 넌지시 한마디 던지는 것만으로 주제의 한 측면 전체를 떠올리게 하면서 자기 생각을 설명해 준다는 사실이 그의 허영심을 우쭐하게 했다."], ["He put this down to his own credit, unaware that Metrov, who had already discussed his theory over and over again with all his intimate friends, talked of it with special eagerness to every new person, and in general was eager to talk to anyone of any subject that interested him, even if still obscure to himself.", "그는 이것을 자기 공으로 돌렸지만, 메트로프가 이미 자기 이론을 가까운 친구들 모두와 거듭거듭 논해 왔으면서도 새로 만나는 사람마다 그것을 유난히 열심히 이야기하며, 대체로 자기가 흥미를 느끼는 주제라면, 설령 아직 자기 자신에게도 분명치 않은 것일지라도, 누구에게든 기꺼이 이야기하고 싶어 한다는 것은 알지 못했다."]], [["“We are late though,”", "\"그런데 우리가 늦겠는데.\""], ["said Katavasov, looking at his watch directly Metrov had finished his discourse.", "메트로프가 강론을 마치자마자 카타바소프가 시계를 보며 말했다."]], [["“Yes, there’s a meeting of the Society of Amateurs today in commemoration of the jubilee of Svintitch,”", "\"그래, 오늘 스빈티치 기념제를 맞아 애호가 협회 모임이 있거든.\""], ["said Katavasov in answer to Levin’s inquiry.", "카타바소프가 레빈의 물음에 답하며 말했다."], ["“Pyotr Ivanovitch and I were going.", "\"표트르 이바노비치와 나도 가려던 참이지."], ["I’ve promised to deliver an address on his labors in zoology.", "내가 그의 동물학 업적에 대해 연설을 하기로 약속했어."], ["Come along with us, it’s very interesting.”", "자네도 같이 가세, 아주 흥미로울 거야.\""]], [["“Yes, and indeed it’s time to start,”", "\"그래요, 정말 이제 출발할 시간이군요.\""], ["said Metrov.", "메트로프가 말했다."], ["“Come with us, and from there, if you care to, come to my place.", "\"우리와 함께 가시지요. 그리고 거기서, 원하신다면 우리 집으로 오십시오."], ["I should very much like to hear your work.”", "당신 연구를 꼭 듣고 싶습니다.\""]], [["“Oh, no!", "\"오, 아닙니다!"], ["It’s no good yet, it’s unfinished.", "아직 변변치 못합니다, 완성되지도 않았고요."], ["But I shall be very glad to go to the meeting.”", "하지만 모임에는 기꺼이 가겠습니다.\""]], [["“I say, friends, have you heard?", "\"여보게들, 들었나?"], ["He has handed in the separate report,”", "그가 별도 보고서를 제출했다네.\""], ["Katavasov called from the other room, where he was putting on his frock coat.", "다른 방에서 프록코트를 입고 있던 카타바소프가 소리쳤다."]], [["And a conversation sprang up upon the university question, which was a very important event that winter in Moscow.", "그러자 대학 문제를 둘러싼 대화가 일었는데, 이는 그해 겨울 모스크바에서 매우 중대한 사건이었다."], ["Three old professors in the council had not accepted the opinion of the younger professors.", "평의회의 노교수 셋이 젊은 교수들의 의견을 받아들이지 않았던 것이다."], ["The young ones had registered a separate resolution.", "젊은 쪽에서는 별도의 결의안을 등록했다."], ["This, in the judgment of some people, was monstrous, in the judgment of others it was the simplest and most just thing to do, and the professors were split up into two parties.", "이것이 어떤 사람들의 판단으로는 괴이한 짓이었고, 또 어떤 사람들의 판단으로는 더할 나위 없이 단순하고 정당한 처사였으며, 교수들은 두 파로 갈라졌다."]], [["One party, to which Katavasov belonged, saw in the opposite party a scoundrelly betrayal and treachery, while the opposite party saw in them childishness and lack of respect for the authorities.", "카타바소프가 속한 한쪽 파는 반대편 파에게서 비열한 배신과 변절을 보았고, 반대편 파는 그들에게서 유치함과 권위에 대한 존중의 결여를 보았다."], ["Levin, though he did not belong to the university, had several times already during his stay in Moscow heard and talked about this matter, and had his own opinion on the subject.", "레빈은 대학에 몸담고 있지는 않았지만, 모스크바에 머무는 동안 이미 여러 번 이 문제에 대해 듣기도 하고 이야기도 했으며, 이 주제에 대해 나름의 의견을 가지고 있었다."], ["He took part in the conversation that was continued in the street, as they all three walked to the buildings of the old university.", "그는 세 사람이 함께 옛 대학 건물로 걸어가는 동안 거리에서 이어진 대화에 끼어들었다."]], [["The meeting had already begun.", "모임은 이미 시작되어 있었다."], ["Round the cloth-covered table, at which Katavasov and Metrov seated themselves, there were some half-dozen persons, and one of these was bending close over a manuscript, reading something aloud.", "천을 덮은 탁자 둘레에, 카타바소프와 메트로프가 자리를 잡았는데, 거기에는 대여섯 명이 있었고, 그중 한 사람이 원고 위로 바짝 몸을 굽힌 채 무언가를 소리 내어 읽고 있었다."], ["Levin sat down in one of the empty chairs that were standing round the table, and in a whisper asked a student sitting near what was being read.", "레빈은 탁자 둘레에 놓여 있던 빈 의자 중 하나에 앉아, 가까이 앉은 학생에게 무엇을 읽고 있는 거냐고 속삭이듯 물었다."], ["The student, eyeing Levin with displeasure, said:", "그 학생은 못마땅한 눈초리로 레빈을 흘끔 보며 말했다."]], [["“Biography.”", "\"전기요.\""]], [["Though Levin was not interested in the biography, he could not help listening, and learned some new and interesting facts about the life of the distinguished man of science.", "레빈은 그 전기에 흥미가 없었지만, 듣지 않을 수 없었고, 그 저명한 과학자의 생애에 대한 새롭고 흥미로운 사실 몇 가지를 알게 되었다."]], [["When the reader had finished, the chairman thanked him and read some verses of the poet Ment sent him on the jubilee, and said a few words by way of thanks to the poet.", "낭독자가 끝마치자, 의장은 그에게 감사를 표한 뒤 시인 멘트가 기념제에 부쳐 보내온 시 몇 구절을 읽고, 그 시인에게 감사하는 의미로 몇 마디 말을 했다."], ["Then Katavasov in his loud, ringing voice read his address on the scientific labors of the man whose jubilee was being kept.", "그러고 나서 카타바소프가 우렁차게 울리는 목소리로, 기념제의 주인공인 그 사람의 학문적 업적에 대한 연설문을 읽었다."]], [["When Katavasov had finished, Levin looked at his watch, saw it was past one, and thought that there would not be time before the concert to read Metrov his book, and indeed, he did not now care to do so.", "카타바소프가 끝마치자, 레빈은 시계를 보았고, 한 시가 지났음을 알고는, 음악회 전에 메트로프에게 자기 책을 읽어 줄 시간이 없겠다고 생각했으며, 사실 이제는 그러고 싶지도 않았다."], ["During the reading he had thought over their conversation.", "낭독이 이어지는 동안 그는 둘이 나눈 대화를 곰곰이 되짚어 보았다."], ["He saw distinctly now that though Metrov’s ideas might perhaps have value, his own ideas had a value too, and their ideas could only be made clear and lead to something if each worked separately in his chosen path, and that nothing would be gained by putting their ideas together.", "그는 이제 분명히 알 수 있었으니, 메트로프의 생각에도 어쩌면 가치가 있을지 모르나 자기 생각에도 가치가 있으며, 두 사람의 생각은 각자가 택한 길에서 따로따로 작업할 때라야 비로소 명료해지고 어떤 결실에 이를 수 있을 뿐, 두 사람의 생각을 한데 합친다고 해서 얻을 것은 아무것도 없으리라는 것이었다."], ["And having made up his mind to refuse Metrov’s invitation, Levin went up to him at the end of the meeting.", "그래서 메트로프의 초대를 거절하기로 마음먹은 레빈은, 모임이 끝날 무렵 그에게 다가갔다."], ["Metrov introduced Levin to the chairman, with whom he was talking of the political news.", "메트로프는 마침 정치 소식을 이야기하고 있던 의장에게 레빈을 소개했다."], ["Metrov told the chairman what he had already told Levin, and Levin made the same remarks on his news that he had already made that morning, but for the sake of variety he expressed also a new opinion which had only just struck him.", "메트로프는 의장에게 이미 레빈에게 들려준 이야기를 했고, 레빈은 그날 아침에 이미 했던 것과 똑같은 소감을 그 소식에 대해 말했지만, 변화를 주려는 마음에 방금 막 떠오른 새로운 의견 하나도 함께 내놓았다."], ["After that the conversation turned again on the university question.", "그 후 대화는 다시 대학 문제로 돌아갔다."], ["As Levin had already heard it all, he made haste to tell Metrov that he was sorry he could not take advantage of his invitation, took leave, and drove to Lvov’s.", "레빈은 이미 그것을 다 들은 터라, 메트로프에게 그의 초대에 응할 수 없어 유감이라고 서둘러 말하고는 작별을 고한 뒤, 마차를 타고 리보프의 집으로 향했다."]], [["# Chapter 4", "# Chapter 4"]], [["Lvov, the husband of Natalia, Kitty’s sister, had spent all his life in foreign capitals, where he had been educated, and had been in the diplomatic service.", "키티의 언니인 나탈리아의 남편 리보프는 평생을 외국 수도들에서 지냈는데, 그곳에서 교육을 받았고 외교관으로 일했다."]], [["During the previous year he had left the diplomatic service, not owing to any “unpleasantness”", "지난 한 해 동안 그는 외교관 일을 그만두었는데, 무슨 \"불미스러운 일\""], ["(he never had any “unpleasantness” with anyone), and was transferred to the department of the court of the palace in Moscow, in order to give his two boys the best education possible.", "때문은 아니었고(그는 누구와도 \"불미스러운 일\"을 겪어 본 적이 없었다), 두 아들에게 가능한 한 최고의 교육을 시키기 위해 모스크바의 궁내부로 자리를 옮겼다."]], [["In spite of the striking contrast in their habits and views and the fact that Lvov was older than Levin, they had seen a great deal of one another that winter, and had taken a great liking to each other.", "두 사람의 습관이며 견해가 두드러지게 대조적이었고 리보프가 레빈보다 나이가 많다는 사실에도 불구하고, 그들은 그해 겨울 서로를 자주 만났고 서로에게 깊은 호감을 가지게 되었다."]], [["Lvov was at home, and Levin went in to him unannounced.", "리보프는 집에 있었고, 레빈은 기별 없이 그에게 들어갔다."]], [["Lvov, in a house coat with a belt and in chamois leather shoes, was sitting in an armchair, and with a pince-nez with blue glasses he was reading a book that stood on a reading desk, while in his beautiful hand he held a half-burned cigarette daintily away from him.", "리보프는 허리띠를 맨 실내복을 입고 섀미 가죽 구두를 신은 채 안락의자에 앉아, 푸른 알이 끼워진 코안경을 쓰고 독서대 위에 놓인 책을 읽고 있었으며, 아름다운 손에는 반쯤 타들어 간 담배를 우아하게 몸에서 멀찍이 떼어 들고 있었다."]], [["His handsome, delicate, and still youthful-looking face, to which his curly, glistening silvery hair gave a still more aristocratic air, lighted up with a smile when he saw Levin.", "곱슬곱슬하게 윤이 나는 은빛 머리칼이 한결 더 귀족적인 분위기를 더해 주는 그의 잘생기고 섬세하며 아직도 젊어 보이는 얼굴이, 레빈을 보자 미소로 환하게 밝아졌다."]], [["“Capital!", "\"잘됐군!"], ["I was meaning to send to you.", "안 그래도 자네한테 사람을 보내려던 참이었네."], ["How’s Kitty?", "키티는 좀 어떤가?"], ["Sit here, it’s more comfortable.”", "여기 앉게, 이쪽이 더 편하니까.\""], ["He got up and pushed up a rocking chair.", "그는 일어나서 흔들의자를 끌어다 놓았다."], ["“Have you read the last circular in the Journal de St.", "\"Journal de St. Pétersbourg에 실린 최근 회람은 읽어 봤나?"], ["Pétersbourg? I think it’s excellent,”", "내 보기엔 아주 훌륭하던데.\""], ["he said, with a slight French accent.", "그는 가벼운 프랑스어 억양으로 말했다."]], [["Levin told him what he had heard from Katavasov was being said in Petersburg, and after talking a little about politics, he told him of his interview with Metrov, and the learned society’s meeting.", "* Journal de St. Pétersbourg: 페테르부르크에서 발행되던 프랑스어 신문."], ["To Lvov it was very interesting.", "레빈은 카타바소프에게 들은, 페테르부르크에서 화제가 되고 있다는 이야기를 그에게 전했고, 정치에 관해 잠깐 이야기를 나눈 뒤 메트로프와의 면담과 학회 모임에 관해 말했다. 리보프에게는 그것이 무척 흥미로웠다."]], [["“That’s what I envy you, that you are able to mix in these interesting scientific circles,”", "\"바로 그게 내가 자네를 부러워하는 점이라네. 그런 흥미로운 학술 모임에 어울릴 수 있다는 것 말일세.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["And as he talked, he passed as usual into French, which was easier to him.", "그리고 이야기를 하면서 그는 늘 그렇듯이 자기에게 더 편한 프랑스어로 넘어갔다."], ["“It’s true I haven’t the time for it.", "\"사실 나는 그럴 시간이 없어."], ["My official work and the children leave me no time; and then I’m not ashamed to own that my education has been too defective.”", "관청 일에다 아이들 때문에 도무지 짬이 안 나거든. 게다가 내가 받은 교육이 너무 부실했다는 걸 인정하는 것도 부끄럽지 않네.\""]], [["“That I don’t believe,”", "\"그건 못 믿겠는데요.\""], ["said Levin with a smile, feeling, as he always did, touched at Lvov’s low opinion of himself, which was not in the least put on from a desire to seem or to be modest, but was absolutely sincere.", "레빈은 미소를 지으며 말했다. 그는 언제나 그렇듯이 리보프가 자기 자신을 낮게 평가하는 데에 마음이 움직였는데, 그것은 겸손해 보이거나 겸손하려는 마음에서 꾸민 것이 조금도 아니고 완전히 진심이었던 것이다."]], [["“Oh, yes, indeed!", "\"오, 정말 그렇다네!"], ["I feel now how badly educated I am.", "지금 와서야 내가 얼마나 교육을 못 받았는지 느낀다네."], ["To educate my children I positively have to look up a great deal, and in fact simply to study myself.", "아이들을 가르치려니 정말이지 이것저것 찾아봐야 하고, 사실은 그냥 내가 직접 공부를 해야 할 지경이야. 선생을 두는 것만으로는 충분치 않고, 그들을 감독할 사람이 있어야 하거든."], ["For it’s not enough to have teachers, there must be someone to look after them, just as on your land you want laborers and an overseer.", "마치 자네 영지에 일꾼과 함께 감독이 필요한 것처럼 말이지. 내가 뭘 읽고 있는지 보게.\""], ["See what I’m reading”—he pointed to Buslaev’s Grammar on the desk—“it’s expected of Misha, and it’s so difficult....", "그는 책상 위에 놓인 부슬라예프의 「문법」을 가리켰다. \"이게 미샤한테 요구되는 건데, 어찌나 어려운지……"], ["Come, explain to me....", "자, 나한테 좀 설명해 주게……"], ["Here he says....”", "여기서 이렇게 말하는데……\""]], [["Levin tried to explain to him that it couldn’t be understood, but that it had to be taught; but Lvov would not agree with him.", "레빈은 그것이 이해할 수 있는 게 아니라 그냥 가르쳐야 하는 거라고 설명하려 했지만, 리보프는 그의 말에 동의하려 하지 않았다."]], [["“Oh, you’re laughing at it!”", "\"오, 자넨 그걸 비웃는군!\""]], [["“On the contrary, you can’t imagine how, when I look at you, I’m always learning the task that lies before me, that is the education of one’s children.”", "\"천만에요. 제가 형님을 보고 있으면 늘 제 앞에 놓인 과제, 그러니까 자식 교육이라는 걸 배운다니까요. 형님은 상상도 못 하실 거예요.\""]], [["“Well, there’s nothing for you to learn,”", "\"뭐, 자네가 배울 게 어디 있겠나.\""], ["said Lvov.", "리보프가 말했다."]], [["“All I know,”", "\"제가 아는 건,\""], ["said Levin, “is that I have never seen better brought-up children than yours, and I wouldn’t wish for children better than yours.”", "레빈이 말했다. \"형님네 아이들보다 더 잘 자란 아이들을 본 적이 없다는 것, 그리고 형님네 아이들보다 더 나은 아이들을 바라지도 않겠다는 것이지요.\""]], [["Lvov visibly tried to restrain the expression of his delight, but he was positively radiant with smiles.", "리보프는 기쁜 빛을 드러내지 않으려고 애쓰는 게 눈에 보였지만, 그의 얼굴은 분명히 미소로 환하게 빛나고 있었다."]], [["“If only they’re better than I!", "\"그저 그 애들이 나보다 나은 사람이 되기만 하면!"], ["That’s all I desire.", "그게 내가 바라는 전부라네."], ["You don’t know yet all the work,”", "자넨 아직 모를 걸세.\" 그가 말했다."], ["he said, “with boys who’ve been left like mine to run wild abroad.”", "\"나처럼 아이들을 외국에서 제멋대로 굴게 내버려 두었을 때 따르는 그 온갖 수고를 말이야.\""]], [["“You’ll catch all that up.", "\"그건 다 따라잡으실 거예요."], ["They’re such clever children.", "그렇게 영리한 아이들인데요."], ["The great thing is the education of character.", "중요한 건 인격 교육이지요."], ["That’s what I learn when I look at your children.”", "그게 바로 제가 형님네 아이들을 보면서 배우는 점이고요.\""]], [["“You talk of the education of character.", "\"인격 교육이라고 했나."], ["You can’t imagine how difficult that is!", "자넨 그게 얼마나 어려운지 상상도 못 할 걸세!"], ["You have hardly succeeded in combating one tendency when others crop up, and the struggle begins again.", "한 가지 성향을 겨우 다스려 놓으면 다른 것들이 불쑥불쑥 튀어나오고, 그러면 싸움이 다시 시작되거든."], ["If one had not a support in religion—you remember we talked about that—no father could bring children up relying on his own strength alone without that help.”", "종교에 의지하지 않는다면—우리가 그 얘길 나눈 거 기억하지—그 도움 없이는 어떤 아버지도 자기 힘만으로 아이들을 키울 수 없을 거야.\""]], [["This subject, which always interested Levin, was cut short by the entrance of the beauty Natalia Alexandrovna, dressed to go out.", "레빈이 늘 흥미를 느끼던 이 화제는, 외출 채비를 마친 미인 나탈리아 알렉산드로브나가 들어오는 바람에 끊기고 말았다."]], [["“I didn’t know you were here,”", "\"여기 계신 줄 몰랐네요.\""], ["she said, unmistakably feeling no regret, but a positive pleasure, in interrupting this conversation on a topic she had heard so much of that she was by now weary of it.", "그녀가 말했는데, 하도 많이 들어서 이제는 지겨워진 화제에 관한 이 대화를 끊는 데에 아쉬움이라고는 조금도 없이 오히려 뚜렷한 즐거움을 느끼고 있는 게 분명했다."], ["“Well, how is Kitty?", "\"그래, 카체리나는 좀 어때요?"], ["I am dining with you today.", "오늘은 댁에서 저녁을 먹을 거예요."], ["I tell you what, Arseny,”", "그러니까 말이죠, 아르세니,\""], ["she turned to her husband, “you take the carriage.”", "그녀가 남편 쪽을 돌아보았다. \"당신이 마차를 쓰세요.\""]], [["And the husband and wife began to discuss their arrangements for the day.", "그러고 나서 부부는 그날의 일정을 의논하기 시작했다."], ["As the husband had to drive to meet someone on official business, while the wife had to go to the concert and some public meeting of a committee on the Eastern Question, there was a great deal to consider and settle.", "남편은 공무로 누군가를 만나러 가야 했고, 아내는 음악회와 동방 문제를 다루는 어느 위원회의 공개 모임에 가야 했으므로, 따져 보고 정리해야 할 것이 많았다."], ["Levin had to take part in their plans as one of themselves.", "레빈은 자기 자신도 그들 중 한 사람인 양 그들의 계획에 참여해야 했다."], ["It was settled that Levin should go with Natalia to the concert and the meeting, and that from there they should send the carriage to the office for Arseny, and he should call for her and take her to Kitty’s; or that, if he had not finished his work, he should send the carriage back and Levin would go with her.", "레빈이 나탈리아와 함께 음악회와 모임에 가고, 거기서 마차를 관청으로 보내 아르세니를 데려오게 하고, 그가 그녀를 데리러 와서 카체리나의 집으로 데려가기로, 혹시 그가 일을 다 마치지 못했으면 마차를 돌려보내고 레빈이 그녀와 함께 가기로 정해졌다."]], [["“He’s spoiling me,”", "\"이 사람이 날 응석받이로 만든다니까.\""], ["Lvov said to his wife; “he assures me that our children are splendid, when I know how much that’s bad there is in them.”", "리보프가 아내에게 말했다. \"우리 애들한테 얼마나 나쁜 데가 많은지 내가 다 아는데, 우리 애들이 훌륭하다고 자꾸 장담을 한단 말이야.\""]], [["“Arseny goes to extremes, I always say,”", "\"아르세니는 매사에 극단으로 치닫는다니까요. 내가 늘 하는 말이지만.\""], ["said his wife.", "그의 아내가 말했다. \"완벽함을 찾으려 들면 결코 만족하지 못하는 법이에요."], ["“If you look for perfection, you will never be satisfied.", "그리고 아빠 말씀이 맞아요. 우리가 자랄 때는 한쪽 극단이었죠."], ["And it’s true, as papa says,—that when we were brought up there was one extreme—we were kept in the basement, while our parents lived in the best rooms; now it’s just the other way—the parents are in the wash house, while the children are in the best rooms.", "부모님은 제일 좋은 방에서 지내시고 우리는 지하실에 처박혀 있었으니까요. 그런데 지금은 정반대예요."], ["Parents now are not expected to live at all, but to exist altogether for their children.”", "부모는 세탁실에 있고 아이들이 제일 좋은 방을 차지하죠. 요즘 부모는 자기 인생을 살아서는 안 되고, 오로지 자식을 위해서만 존재해야 하나 봐요.\""]], [["“Well, what if they like it better?”", "\"뭐, 아이들이 그걸 더 좋아한다면 어떻겠소?\""], ["Lvov said, with his beautiful smile, touching her hand.", "리보프가 그 아름다운 미소를 지으며 아내의 손을 만지고 말했다."], ["“Anyone who didn’t know you would think you were a stepmother, not a true mother.”", "\"당신을 모르는 사람이 보면 친엄마가 아니라 계모인 줄 알겠어.\""]], [["“No, extremes are not good in anything,”", "\"아니에요, 무엇이든 극단은 좋지 않아요.\""], ["Natalia said serenely, putting his paper-knife straight in its proper place on the table.", "나탈리아가 차분하게 말하며 식탁 위의 종이칼을 제자리에 똑바로 놓았다."]], [["“Well, come here, you perfect children,”", "\"자, 이리 오너라, 우리 완벽한 아이들.\""], ["Lvov said to the two handsome boys who came in, and after bowing to Levin, went up to their father, obviously wishing to ask him about something.", "리보프가 방으로 들어온 두 잘생긴 사내아이에게 말했고, 아이들은 레빈에게 인사를 한 뒤, 무언가를 아버지에게 물어보고 싶은 게 분명한 얼굴로 아버지에게 다가갔다."]], [["Levin would have liked to talk to them, to hear what they would say to their father, but Natalia began talking to him, and then Lvov’s colleague in the service, Mahotin, walked in, wearing his court uniform, to go with him to meet someone, and a conversation was kept up without a break upon Herzegovina, Princess Korzinskaya, the town council, and the sudden death of Madame Apraksina.", "레빈은 아이들과 이야기를 나누고 싶었고, 그들이 아버지에게 무슨 말을 하는지 듣고 싶었지만, 나탈리아가 그에게 말을 걸기 시작했고, 그러는 사이에 리보프의 동료 관리인 마호틴이 그와 함께 누군가를 만나러 가려고 궁정 제복 차림으로 들어왔으며, 헤르체고비나며, 코르진스카야 공작 부인이며, 시 의회며, 아프락시나 부인의 갑작스러운 죽음에 관한 대화가 끊임없이 이어졌다. 레빈은 자기에게 맡겨진 부탁까지 잊어버리고 있었다. 그는 현관으로 나가다가 그것을 떠올렸다."]], [["Levin even forgot the commission intrusted to him.", "\"아, 카체리나가 형님께 오블론스키 얘기를 좀 해 보라더군요.\""], ["He recollected it as he was going into the hall.", "리보프가 계단에 서서 아내와 레빈을 배웅하고 있을 때 그가 말했다."]], [["“Oh, Kitty told me to talk to you about Oblonsky,”", "\"그래, 그래, maman은 우리 동서들끼리 그 사람을 다그쳐 줬으면 하시지.\""], ["he said, as Lvov was standing on the stairs, seeing his wife and Levin off.", "그가 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다. \"하지만 내가 왜 그래야 하나?\""]], [["“Yes, yes, maman wants us, les beaux-frères, to attack him,” he said, blushing. “But why should I?”", "* les beaux-frères: 프랑스어로 '처남·매부 사이의 동서'를 뜻함."]], [["“Well, then, I will attack him,”", "\"그럼 좋아요, 내가 다그칠게요.\""], ["said Madame Lvova, with a smile, standing in her white sheepskin cape, waiting till they had finished speaking.", "리보프 부인이 하얀 양가죽 망토 차림으로 두 사람이 이야기를 마칠 때까지 기다리고 서 있다가 미소를 지으며 말했다."], ["“Come, let us go.”", "\"자, 이제 가요.\""]], [["# Chapter 5", "# Chapter 5"]], [["At the concert in the afternoon two very interesting things were performed.", "오후 음악회에서는 무척 흥미로운 곡 두 개가 연주되었다."], ["One was a fantasia, King Lear; the other was a quartette dedicated to the memory of Bach.", "하나는 환상곡 「리어 왕」이었고, 다른 하나는 바흐를 추모하여 바친 사중주였다."], ["Both were new and in the new style, and Levin was eager to form an opinion of them.", "둘 다 새 작품이었고 새로운 양식을 따른 것이어서, 레빈은 그것들에 대한 자기 나름의 견해를 세우고 싶었다."], ["After escorting his sister-in-law to her stall, he stood against a column and tried to listen as attentively and conscientiously as possible.", "처제를 그녀의 자리로 안내한 뒤, 그는 기둥에 기대선 채 할 수 있는 한 주의 깊고 성실하게 귀를 기울이려 애썼다."], ["He tried not to let his attention be distracted, and not to spoil his impression by looking at the conductor in a white tie, waving his arms, which always disturbed his enjoyment of music so much, or the ladies in bonnets, with strings carefully tied over their ears, and all these people either thinking of nothing at all or thinking of all sorts of things except the music.", "그는 주의가 흐트러지지 않도록, 그리고 흰 넥타이를 매고 팔을 휘젓는 지휘자—언제나 그의 음악 감상을 무던히도 방해하던—나, 끈을 귀 위로 야무지게 묶은 보닛 차림의 부인들, 그리고 음악 말고는 아무 생각도 하지 않거나 온갖 잡다한 것을 생각하고 있는 그 모든 사람들을 바라봄으로써 자기 인상을 망치지 않으려 했다."], ["He tried to avoid meeting musical connoisseurs or talkative acquaintances, and stood looking at the floor straight before him, listening.", "그는 음악 애호가나 말 많은 지인과 마주치지 않으려 애쓰며, 똑바로 앞쪽 바닥을 바라본 채 서서 귀를 기울였다."]], [["But the more he listened to the fantasia of King Lear the further he felt from forming any definite opinion of it.", "그러나 「리어 왕」 환상곡을 들으면 들을수록, 그는 그것에 대한 어떤 분명한 견해를 세우는 일로부터 점점 더 멀어지는 것 같았다."], ["There was, as it were, a continual beginning, a preparation of the musical expression of some feeling, but it fell to pieces again directly, breaking into new musical motives, or simply nothing but the whims of the composer, exceedingly complex but disconnected sounds.", "무언가 어떤 감정의 음악적 표현이 끊임없이 시작되고 준비되는 듯하다가도, 그것은 곧바로 산산조각 나 버리고 새로운 음악적 동기로 부서지거나, 아니면 그저 작곡가의 변덕에 지나지 않는, 지극히 복잡하면서도 서로 단절된 소리들에 불과했다."], ["And these fragmentary musical expressions, though sometimes beautiful, were disagreeable, because they were utterly unexpected and not led up to by anything.", "그리고 이 단편적인 음악적 표현들은, 때로 아름답기는 했지만 불쾌했다. 그것들이 전혀 예기치 못한 것이었고, 어떤 것에 의해서도 이끌려 나오지 않았기 때문이다."], ["Gaiety and grief and despair and tenderness and triumph followed one another without any connection, like the emotions of a madman.", "환희와 비탄과 절망과 애틋함과 환호가 마치 미치광이의 감정처럼 아무런 연관도 없이 잇따랐다."], ["And those emotions, like a madman’s, sprang up quite unexpectedly.", "그리고 그 감정들은, 미치광이의 그것처럼 아주 느닷없이 솟아올랐다."]], [["During the whole of the performance Levin felt like a deaf man watching people dancing, and was in a state of complete bewilderment when the fantasia was over, and felt a great weariness from the fruitless strain on his attention.", "연주가 진행되는 내내 레빈은 사람들이 춤추는 것을 바라보는 귀머거리 같은 기분이었고, 환상곡이 끝났을 때는 완전한 어리둥절함에 빠진 상태였으며, 보람도 없이 주의를 쥐어짠 탓에 큰 피로를 느꼈다."], ["Loud applause resounded on all sides.", "사방에서 우레 같은 박수가 울려 퍼졌다."], ["Everyone got up, moved about, and began talking.", "모두가 일어나 이리저리 움직이며 이야기를 나누기 시작했다."], ["Anxious to throw some light on his own perplexity from the impressions of others, Levin began to walk about, looking for connoisseurs, and was glad to see a well-known musical amateur in conversation with Pestsov, whom he knew.", "자신의 당혹감에 다른 사람들의 인상으로 어떤 빛을 비춰 보고 싶었던 레빈은, 음악 전문가를 찾아 이리저리 거닐기 시작했고, 자기가 아는 한 유명한 음악 애호가가 역시 그가 아는 페스초프와 이야기를 나누고 있는 것을 보고 반가워했다."]], [["“Marvelous!”", "\"훌륭해!\""], ["Pestsov was saying in his mellow bass.", "페스초프가 부드러운 저음으로 말하고 있었다."], ["“How are you, Konstantin Dmitrievitch?", "\"안녕하시오, 콘스탄틴 드미트리치?"], ["Particularly sculpturesque and plastic, so to say, and richly colored is that passage where you feel Cordelia’s approach, where woman, das ewig Weibliche, enters into conflict with fate.", "특히나, 말하자면 조각적이고 입체적이며, 색채가 풍부한 대목은 코델리아의 다가옴이 느껴지는 부분이오. 여인이, das ewig Weibliche가 운명과 갈등을 빚는 그 대목 말이오."], ["Isn’t it?”", "그렇지 않소?\""]], [["“You mean ... what has Cordelia to do with it?”", "* das ewig Weibliche: 독일어로 '영원히 여성적인 것'."], ["Levin asked timidly, forgetting that the fantasia was supposed to represent King Lear.", "괴테 「파우스트」의 마지막 구절에서 따온 표현."]], [["“Cordelia comes in ... see here!”", "\"그러니까……"], ["said Pestsov, tapping his finger on the satiny surface of the program he held in his hand and passing it to Levin.", "코델리아가 거기에 무슨 상관이 있다는 거요?\" 환상곡이 리어 왕을 표현하기로 되어 있다는 것을 잊은 채, 레빈이 머뭇거리며 물었다."]], [["Only then Levin recollected the title of the fantasia, and made haste to read in the Russian translation the lines from Shakespeare that were printed on the back of the program.", "\"코델리아가 등장하잖소…… 여기 보시오!\" 페스초프가 손에 든 프로그램의 매끄러운 표면을 손가락으로 톡톡 두드리며 말하고는, 그것을 레빈에게 건넸다."]], [["“You can’t follow it without that,” said Pestsov, addressing Levin, as the person he had been speaking to had gone away, and he had no one to talk to.", "그제야 레빈은 환상곡의 제목을 떠올렸고, 프로그램 뒷면에 인쇄된, 셰익스피어에서 따온 구절의 러시아어 번역을 서둘러 읽었다."]], [["In the entr’acte Levin and Pestsov fell into an argument upon the merits and defects of music of the Wagner school.", "\"그게 없으면 따라갈 수가 없소.\" 페스초프가 레빈에게 말을 걸며 말했다."], ["Levin maintained that the mistake of Wagner and all his followers lay in their trying to take music into the sphere of another art, just as poetry goes wrong when it tries to paint a face as the art of painting ought to do, and as an instance of this mistake he cited the sculptor who carved in marble certain poetic phantasms flitting round the figure of the poet on the pedestal.", "그가 이야기를 나누던 사람이 가 버려서, 더 이상 말을 나눌 상대가 없었던 것이다. 막간에 레빈과 페스초프는 바그너 악파의 음악이 지닌 장점과 결점을 두고 논쟁에 빠져들었다."], ["“These phantoms were so far from being phantoms that they were positively clinging on the ladder,”", "레빈은 바그너와 그의 모든 추종자들의 잘못이 음악을 다른 예술의 영역으로 끌어들이려 한 데 있다고 주장했다. 마치 시가 회화의 예술이 마땅히 해야 할 일인 얼굴 묘사를 시도할 때 잘못되는 것처럼 말이다."], ["said Levin.", "그리고 이런 잘못의 한 예로, 그는 받침대 위에 선 시인의 형상 둘레를 떠도는 어떤 시적 환영들을 대리석에 새긴 조각가를 들었다. \"그 환영들은 환영이기는커녕, 그야말로 사다리에 매달려 있었지 뭐요.\""], ["The comparison pleased him, but he could not remember whether he had not used the same phrase before, and to Pestsov, too, and as he said it he felt confused.", "레빈이 말했다. 그 비유가 마음에 들었지만, 그는 전에 같은 표현을 쓴 적이 있는지, 그것도 페스초프에게 쓴 적이 있는지 도무지 기억이 나지 않았고, 그 말을 하면서 당황스러움을 느꼈다."]], [["Pestsov maintained that art is one, and that it can attain its highest manifestations only by conjunction with all kinds of art.", "페스초프는 예술은 하나이며, 온갖 종류의 예술과 결합함으로써만 그 최고의 발현에 다다를 수 있다고 주장했다."]], [["The second piece that was performed Levin could not hear.", "두 번째로 연주된 곡은 레빈이 들을 수가 없었다."], ["Pestsov, who was standing beside him, was talking to him almost all the time, condemning the music for its excessive affected assumption of simplicity, and comparing it with the simplicity of the Pre-Raphaelites in painting.", "옆에 서 있던 페스초프가 거의 내내 그에게 말을 걸며, 그 음악이 단순함을 지나치게 꾸며 내어 가장한다고 비난하고, 그것을 회화에서의 라파엘 전파의 단순함에 견주었던 것이다."], ["As he went out Levin met many more acquaintances, with whom he talked of politics, of music, and of common acquaintances.", "밖으로 나가면서 레빈은 더 많은 지인을 만났고, 그들과 정치며 음악이며 공통의 지인들에 관해 이야기를 나누었다."], ["Among others he met Count Bol, whom he had utterly forgotten to call upon.", "그중에는 그가 방문하기로 해 놓고는 까맣게 잊고 있던 볼 백작도 있었다."]], [["“Well, go at once then,”", "\"그럼 어서 다녀와요.\""], ["Madame Lvova said, when he told her; “perhaps they’ll not be at home, and then you can come to the meeting to fetch me.", "레빈이 그 이야기를 하자 르보바 부인이 말했다. \"어쩌면 댁에 없을지도 모르니, 그러면 모임으로 나를 데리러 오면 되겠네요."], ["You’ll find me still there.”", "그때까지도 거기 있을 테니까요.\""]], [["# Chapter 6", "# Chapter 6"]], [["“Perhaps they’re not at home?”", "\"어쩌면 집에 없을지도 몰라.\""], ["said Levin, as he went into the hall of Countess Bola’s house.", "레빈은 볼라 백작 부인 댁의 현관으로 들어서며 말했다."]], [["“At home; please walk in,”", "\"계십니다, 들어가시지요.\""], ["said the porter, resolutely removing his overcoat.", "문지기가 단호하게 그의 외투를 벗기며 말했다."]], [["“How annoying!”", "'아, 성가셔라!'"], ["thought Levin with a sigh, taking off one glove and stroking his hat.", "레빈은 한숨을 쉬며 생각했다. 그는 장갑 한 짝을 벗고 모자를 쓰다듬었다."], ["“What did I come for?", "'내가 뭐 하러 왔지?"], ["What have I to say to them?”", "이 사람들한테 무슨 할 말이 있다고?'"]], [["As he passed through the first drawing-room Levin met in the doorway Countess Bola, giving some order to a servant with a care-worn and severe face.", "첫 번째 응접실을 지나갈 때 레빈은 출입구에서 볼라 백작 부인과 마주쳤다. 부인은 근심스럽고 엄한 얼굴로 하인에게 무언가 지시를 내리고 있었다."], ["On seeing Levin she smiled, and asked him to come into the little drawing-room, where he heard voices.", "레빈을 보자 부인은 미소를 지으며 목소리가 들려오는 작은 응접실로 들어오라고 청했다."], ["In this room there were sitting in armchairs the two daughters of the countess, and a Moscow colonel, whom Levin knew.", "그 방에는 백작 부인의 두 딸과, 레빈도 아는 모스크바 출신 대령이 안락의자에 앉아 있었다."], ["Levin went up, greeted them, and sat down beside the sofa with his hat on his knees.", "레빈은 다가가 그들에게 인사를 하고는 모자를 무릎에 얹은 채 소파 옆에 앉았다."]], [["“How is your wife?", "\"부인은 어떻게 지내세요?"], ["Have you been at the concert?", "음악회에는 다녀오셨나요?"], ["We couldn’t go.", "저희는 못 갔답니다."], ["Mamma had to be at the funeral service.”", "어머니가 장례식에 참석하셔야 했거든요.\""]], [["“Yes, I heard....", "\"네, 들었습니다……"], ["What a sudden death!”", "정말 갑작스러운 죽음이었지요!\""], ["said Levin.", "레빈이 말했다."]], [["The countess came in, sat down on the sofa, and she too asked after his wife and inquired about the concert.", "백작 부인이 들어와 소파에 앉더니, 부인도 그의 아내의 안부를 묻고 음악회에 대해 물었다."]], [["Levin answered, and repeated an inquiry about Madame Apraksina’s sudden death.", "레빈은 대답을 한 뒤 아프락시나 부인의 갑작스러운 죽음에 대해 다시 한번 물었다."]], [["“But she was always in weak health.”", "\"하지만 그분은 늘 몸이 약하셨잖아요.\""]], [["“Were you at the opera yesterday?”", "\"어제 오페라에는 가셨어요?\""]], [["“Yes, I was.”", "\"네, 갔습니다.\""]], [["“Lucca was very good.”", "\"루카가 아주 훌륭했지요.\""]], [["“Yes, very good,”", "\"네, 아주 훌륭했어요.\""], ["he said, and as it was utterly of no consequence to him what they thought of him, he began repeating what they had heard a hundred times about the characteristics of the singer’s talent.", "그가 말했다. 그리고 그들이 자기를 어떻게 생각하든 자기로서는 아무래도 상관없었으므로, 그는 그 가수의 재능이 지닌 특징에 대해 사람들이 이미 백 번은 들었을 이야기를 되풀이하기 시작했다."], ["Countess Bola pretended to be listening.", "볼라 백작 부인은 듣는 척했다."], ["Then, when he had said enough and paused, the colonel, who had been silent till then, began to talk.", "그러다 그가 할 말을 충분히 하고 말을 멈추자, 그때까지 잠자코 있던 대령이 입을 열기 시작했다."], ["The colonel too talked of the opera, and about culture.", "대령도 오페라에 대해, 그리고 문화에 대해 이야기했다."], ["At last, after speaking of the proposed folle journée at Turin’s, the colonel laughed, got up noisily, and went away.", "마침내 대령은 투린 댁에서 열 예정인 folle journée에 대해 이야기하고는 웃음을 터뜨리며 요란하게 일어나 자리를 떴다. 레빈도 일어섰지만, 백작 부인의 얼굴을 보고 아직 떠날 때가 아니라는 것을 알아챘다."], ["Levin too rose, but he saw by the face of the countess that it was not yet time for him to go.", "이 분쯤은 더 머물러야 했다. 그는 다시 앉았다."], ["He must stay two minutes longer. He sat down.", "* folle journée: 프랑스어로 '미친 듯한 하루', 곧 떠들썩한 잔치를 뜻한다."]], [["But as he was thinking all the while how stupid it was, he could not find a subject for conversation, and sat silent.", "그러나 이게 얼마나 어리석은 짓인가 하는 생각만 줄곧 하고 있었으므로, 그는 대화의 화젯거리를 찾지 못한 채 잠자코 앉아 있었다."]], [["“You are not going to the public meeting?", "\"공개 회의에는 안 가세요?"], ["They say it will be very interesting,”", "무척 흥미로울 거라고들 하던데요.\""], ["began the countess.", "백작 부인이 말을 꺼냈다."]], [["“No, I promised my belle-sœur to fetch her from it,”", "\"아니요, 처형을 거기서 데려오겠다고 약속을 했답니다.\""], ["said Levin.", "레빈이 말했다."]], [["A silence followed.", "침묵이 이어졌다."], ["The mother once more exchanged glances with a daughter.", "어머니는 다시 한번 딸과 눈길을 주고받았다."]], [["“Well, now I think the time has come,”", "'자, 이제 때가 된 것 같군.'"], ["thought Levin, and he got up.", "레빈은 생각하며 일어났다."], ["The ladies shook hands with him, and begged him to say mille choses to his wife for them.", "부인들은 그와 악수를 나누고, 부디 자기들 대신 부인에게 mille choses를 전해 달라고 부탁했다."]], [["The porter asked him, as he gave him his coat, “Where is your honor staying?”", "* mille choses: 프랑스어로 '천 가지 안부', 곧 '안부 인사를 듬뿍'이라는 뜻. 문지기는 그에게 외투를 건네주며 물었다."], ["and immediately wrote down his address in a big handsomely bound book.", "\"나리께서는 어디에 묵고 계십니까?\" 그러고는 곧바로 크고 멋지게 장정된 책에 그의 주소를 적어 넣었다."]], [["“Of course I don’t care, but still I feel ashamed and awfully stupid,”", "'물론 난 상관없지만, 그래도 부끄럽고 형편없이 어리석은 짓 같단 말이야.'"], ["thought Levin, consoling himself with the reflection that everyone does it.", "레빈은 생각하며, 누구나 다 그렇게 하는 거라는 생각으로 스스로를 위로했다."], ["He drove to the public meeting, where he was to find his sister-in-law, so as to drive home with her.", "그는 처형을 만나 함께 집으로 돌아가기 위해 공개 회의로 마차를 몰았다."]], [["At the public meeting of the committee there were a great many people, and almost all the highest society.", "위원회의 공개 회의에는 아주 많은 사람들이, 그것도 거의 최상류 사교계 인사들이 모여 있었다."], ["Levin was in time for the report which, as everyone said, was very interesting.", "레빈은 모두가 입을 모아 무척 흥미롭다고 한 보고가 시작될 때 맞춰 도착했다."], ["When the reading of the report was over, people moved about, and Levin met Sviazhsky, who invited him very pressingly to come that evening to a meeting of the Society of Agriculture, where a celebrated lecture was to be delivered, and Stepan Arkadyevitch, who had only just come from the races, and many other acquaintances; and Levin heard and uttered various criticisms on the meeting, on the new fantasia, and on a public trial.", "보고 낭독이 끝나자 사람들이 이리저리 움직였고, 레빈은 스비야시스키와 마주쳤다. 그는 그날 저녁 농업협회 모임에 오라고 간곡히 권했는데, 거기서 유명한 강연이 있을 거라고 했다."], ["But, probably from the mental fatigue he was beginning to feel, he made a blunder in speaking of the trial, and this blunder he recalled several times with vexation.", "또 방금 경마장에서 온 스테판 아르카지치와도, 그 밖의 여러 지인들과도 마주쳤다. 레빈은 회의에 대해, 새로운 환상곡에 대해, 그리고 어느 공판에 대해 갖가지 비평을 듣기도 하고 늘어놓기도 했다."], ["Speaking of the sentence upon a foreigner who had been condemned in Russia, and of how unfair it would be to punish him by exile abroad, Levin repeated what he had heard the day before in conversation from an acquaintance.", "그러나 슬슬 느껴지기 시작한 정신적 피로 탓이었는지, 그는 공판 이야기를 하다가 실수를 저질렀고, 이 실수를 그는 여러 번 분하게 떠올렸다. 러시아에서 유죄 판결을 받은 한 외국인에 대한 선고를 이야기하면서, 그를 국외 추방으로 처벌하는 것이 얼마나 부당한 일인가에 대해 레빈은 전날 어느 지인과의 대화에서 들은 이야기를 그대로 되풀이했다."]], [["“I think sending him abroad is much the same as punishing a carp by putting it into the water,”", "\"제 생각엔 그를 외국으로 보내는 것은 잉어를 물속에 넣어 벌하는 거나 다름없는 것 같습니다.\""], ["said Levin.", "레빈은 말했다."], ["Then he recollected that this idea, which he had heard from an acquaintance and uttered as his own, came from a fable of Krilov’s, and that the acquaintance had picked it up from a newspaper article.", "그러다 문득, 어느 지인에게서 듣고는 마치 자기 생각인 양 말한 이 발상이 사실은 크릴로프의 우화에서 나온 것이며, 그 지인도 어느 신문 기사에서 주워들은 것임을 떠올렸다."]], [["After driving home with his sister-in-law, and finding Kitty in good spirits and quite well, Levin drove to the club.", "처형과 함께 집으로 돌아와 키티가 기분도 좋고 아주 건강한 것을 확인한 뒤, 레빈은 클럽으로 마차를 몰았다."]], [["# Chapter 7", "# Chapter 7"]], [["Levin reached the club just at the right time.", "레빈은 마침 알맞은 때에 클럽에 닿았다."], ["Members and visitors were driving up as he arrived.", "그가 도착할 무렵 회원들과 손님들이 마차를 타고 속속 들어오고 있었다."], ["Levin had not been at the club for a very long while—not since he lived in Moscow, when he was leaving the university and going into society.", "레빈은 아주 오랫동안 클럽에 와 본 적이 없었다. 모스크바에 살며 대학을 졸업하고 사교계에 발을 들이던 시절 이후로는 처음이었다."], ["He remembered the club, the external details of its arrangement, but he had completely forgotten the impression it had made on him in old days.", "그는 클럽을, 그 구조의 외형적인 세부들을 기억하고 있었지만, 옛 시절 그것이 자기에게 주었던 인상만은 까맣게 잊고 있었다."], ["But as soon as, driving into the wide semicircular court and getting out of the sledge, he mounted the steps, and the hall-porter, adorned with a crossway scarf, noiselessly opened the door to him with a bow; as soon as he saw in the porter’s room the cloaks and galoshes of members who thought it less trouble to take them off downstairs; as soon as he heard the mysterious ringing bell that preceded him as he ascended the easy, carpeted staircase, and saw the statue on the landing, and the third porter at the top doors, a familiar figure grown older, in the club livery, opening the door without haste or delay, and scanning the visitors as they passed in—Levin felt the old impression of the club come back in a rush, an impression of repose, comfort, and propriety.", "그러나 넓고 반원형인 마당으로 마차를 몰아 들어가 썰매에서 내려 계단을 오르고, 어깨에 가로로 띠를 두른 수위가 소리 없이 고개를 숙이며 문을 열어 주자마자, 수위실에서 아래층에서 벗어 두는 편이 덜 번거롭다고 여긴 회원들의 외투며 덧신을 보자마자, 양탄자가 깔린 편안한 계단을 오를 때 그를 앞서 울리는 신비로운 종소리를 듣고, 층계참의 조각상을 보고, 맨 위 문 앞의 세 번째 수위—클럽 제복을 입은, 낯익으면서도 나이 든 모습으로 서두름도 지체도 없이 문을 열며 지나가는 손님들을 훑어보던—를 보자마자, 레빈은 클럽의 옛 인상이, 안온함과 편안함과 단정함의 인상이 한꺼번에 되살아나는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["“Your hat, please,”", "\"모자를 주십시오.\""], ["the porter said to Levin, who forgot the club rule to leave his hat in the porter’s room.", "모자를 수위실에 맡기는 클럽 규칙을 잊은 레빈에게 수위가 말했다."], ["“Long time since you’ve been.", "\"오랜만에 오셨습니다."], ["The prince put your name down yesterday.", "공작님께서 어제 성함을 적어 두셨습니다."], ["Prince Stepan Arkadyevitch is not here yet.”", "스테판 아르카지치 공작님은 아직 안 오셨습니다.\""]], [["The porter did not only know Levin, but also all his ties and relationships, and so immediately mentioned his intimate friends.", "수위는 레빈을 알 뿐만 아니라 그의 모든 친분과 관계까지 알고 있어서, 곧바로 그의 가까운 친구들을 입에 올렸다."]], [["Passing through the outer hall, divided up by screens, and the room partitioned on the right, where a man sits at the fruit buffet, Levin overtook an old man walking slowly in, and entered the dining-room full of noise and people.", "칸막이로 나뉜 바깥 홀과, 한 남자가 과일 진열대 앞에 앉아 있는 오른쪽의 칸막이 친 방을 지나면서, 레빈은 천천히 걸어 들어가던 한 노인을 앞질러, 사람들로 가득하고 떠들썩한 식당으로 들어섰다."]], [["He walked along the tables, almost all full, and looked at the visitors.", "그는 거의 다 들어찬 식탁들을 따라 걸으며 손님들을 둘러보았다."], ["He saw people of all sorts, old and young; some he knew a little, some intimate friends.", "늙은이도 젊은이도, 온갖 부류의 사람들이 보였다. 어떤 이들은 조금 아는 사이였고, 어떤 이들은 가까운 친구였다."], ["There was not a single cross or worried-looking face.", "화나거나 근심스러워 보이는 얼굴은 단 하나도 없었다."], ["All seemed to have left their cares and anxieties in the porter’s room with their hats, and were all deliberately getting ready to enjoy the material blessings of life.", "모두가 근심과 걱정을 모자와 함께 수위실에 맡겨 두고, 인생의 물질적 축복을 한껏 누릴 채비를 느긋하게 차리고 있는 듯했다."], ["Sviazhsky was here and Shtcherbatsky, Nevyedovsky and the old prince, and Vronsky and Sergey Ivanovitch.", "이곳에는 스비아시스키와 시체르바츠키, 네베돕스키와 늙은 공작, 그리고 브론스키와 세르게이 이바노비치가 있었다."]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["why are you late?”", "왜 이리 늦었나?\""], ["the prince said smiling, and giving him his hand over his own shoulder. “How’s Kitty?”", "공작이 미소 지으며 자기 어깨 너머로 손을 내밀어 그에게 악수를 청했다."], ["he added, smoothing out the napkin he had tucked in at his waistcoat buttons.", "\"키티는 어떤가?\" 그는 조끼 단추에 끼워 넣은 냅킨을 매만지며 덧붙였다."]], [["“All right; they are dining at home, all the three of them.”", "\"잘 있습니다. 셋이서 집에서 식사 중이지요.\""]], [["“Ah, ‘Aline-Nadine,’ to be sure!", "\"아, '알린-나딘'이로군!"], ["There’s no room with us.", "우리 자리엔 빈 데가 없네."], ["Go to that table, and make haste and take a seat,”", "저 식탁으로 가서 얼른 자리를 잡게나.\""], ["said the prince, and turning away he carefully took a plate of eel soup.", "공작은 그렇게 말하고는 몸을 돌려 조심스레 뱀장어 수프 접시를 받아 들었다."]], [["“Levin, this way!”", "\"레빈, 이쪽이오!\""], ["a good-natured voice shouted a little farther on.", "조금 떨어진 곳에서 사람 좋은 목소리가 외쳤다."], ["It was Turovtsin.", "투롭친이었다."], ["He was sitting with a young officer, and beside them were two chairs turned upside down.", "그는 젊은 장교와 함께 앉아 있었고, 그들 곁에는 뒤집어 놓은 의자 두 개가 있었다. 레빈은 반가운 마음으로 그들에게 다가갔다."], ["Levin gladly went up to them.", "그는 늘 그 마음씨 고운 난봉꾼 투롭친을 좋아했다."], ["He had always liked the good-hearted rake, Turovtsin—he was associated in his mind with memories of his courtship—and at that moment, after the strain of intellectual conversation, the sight of Turovtsin’s good-natured face was particularly welcome.", "그는 레빈의 마음속에서 구애 시절의 추억과 이어져 있었다. 게다가 지금 이 순간, 지적인 대화의 긴장이 지난 뒤라, 투롭친의 사람 좋은 얼굴은 유독 반가웠다."]], [["“For you and Oblonsky.", "\"당신과 오블론스키 자리요."], ["He’ll be here directly.”", "그가 곧 올 거요.\""]], [["The young man, holding himself very erect, with eyes forever twinkling with enjoyment, was an officer from Petersburg, Gagin.", "몸을 아주 꼿꼿이 세우고 두 눈이 끊임없이 즐거움으로 반짝이던 그 젊은이는 페테르부르크에서 온 장교 가긴이었다."], ["Turovtsin introduced them.", "투롭친이 두 사람을 소개해 주었다."]], [["“Oblonsky’s always late.”", "\"오블론스키는 늘 늦지요.\""]], [["“Ah, here he is!”", "\"아, 저기 오는군!\""]], [["“Have you only just come?”", "\"이제 막 왔나?\""], ["said Oblonsky, coming quickly towards them.", "오블론스키가 그들에게 빠른 걸음으로 다가오며 말했다."], ["“Good day.", "\"안녕들 하신가."], ["Had some vodka?", "보드카는 좀 했나?"], ["Well, come along then.”", "자, 그럼 이리 오게.\""]], [["Levin got up and went with him to the big table spread with spirits and appetizers of the most various kinds.", "레빈은 일어나 그와 함께 온갖 종류의 술과 안주가 차려진 큰 식탁으로 갔다."], ["One would have thought that out of two dozen delicacies one might find something to one’s taste, but Stepan Arkadyevitch asked for something special, and one of the liveried waiters standing by immediately brought what was required.", "스물네 가지나 되는 진미 가운데 입맛에 맞는 것 하나쯤은 찾을 법도 했건만, 스테판 아르카지치는 뭔가 특별한 것을 청했고, 곁에 서 있던 제복 입은 웨이터 하나가 즉시 원하는 것을 가져왔다."], ["They drank a wine-glassful and returned to their table.", "두 사람은 포도주를 한 잔씩 마시고 자기들 식탁으로 돌아왔다."]], [["At once, while they were still at the soup, Gagin was served with champagne, and told the waiter to fill four glasses.", "수프를 먹는 사이에 벌써 가긴에게 샴페인이 나왔고, 그는 웨이터에게 네 잔을 채우라고 일렀다."], ["Levin did not refuse the wine, and asked for a second bottle.", "레빈은 포도주를 마다하지 않았고 두 번째 병을 청했다."], ["He was very hungry, and ate and drank with great enjoyment, and with still greater enjoyment took part in the lively and simple conversation of his companions.", "그는 몹시 배가 고팠으므로 큰 즐거움으로 먹고 마셨으며, 일행의 활기차고 소탈한 대화에 끼는 것은 그보다 더 큰 즐거움이었다."], ["Gagin, dropping his voice, told the last good story from Petersburg, and the story, though improper and stupid, was so ludicrous that Levin broke into roars of laughter so loud that those near looked round.", "가긴이 목소리를 낮추어 페테르부르크에서 나온 최신 우스갯소리를 들려주었는데, 그 이야기는 점잖지 못하고 어리석은 것이었지만 어찌나 우스꽝스러웠던지 레빈이 어찌나 큰 소리로 폭소를 터뜨렸던지 곁에 있던 사람들이 돌아볼 정도였다."]], [["“That’s in the same style as, ‘that’s a thing I can’t endure!’ You know the story?”", "\"그건 '내가 도저히 못 견디는 게 바로 그거야!' 하는 거랑 똑같은 식이군."], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch.", "그 이야기 아나?\" 스테판 아르카지치가 말했다."], ["“Ah, that’s exquisite!", "\"아, 그건 정말 일품이지!"], ["Another bottle,”", "한 병 더.\""], ["he said to the waiter, and he began to relate his good story.", "그는 웨이터에게 일러 놓고는 자기 우스갯소리를 풀어놓기 시작했다."]], [["“Pyotr Illyitch Vinovsky invites you to drink with him,”", "\"표트르 일리치 비놉스키 님이 함께 한잔하시자고 청하십니다.\""], ["a little old waiter interrupted Stepan Arkadyevitch, bringing two delicate glasses of sparkling champagne, and addressing Stepan Arkadyevitch and Levin.", "자그마한 늙은 웨이터가 스테판 아르카지치의 말을 끊으며, 거품이 이는 샴페인이 담긴 가느다란 잔 두 개를 가져와 스테판 아르카지치와 레빈에게 건넸다."], ["Stepan Arkadyevitch took the glass, and looking towards a bald man with red mustaches at the other end of the table, he nodded to him, smiling.", "스테판 아르카지치는 잔을 받아 들고는, 식탁 저쪽 끝의 붉은 콧수염을 기른 대머리 사내를 향해 미소 지으며 고개를 끄덕였다."]], [["“Who’s that?”", "\"저 사람은 누굽니까?\""], ["asked Levin.", "레빈이 물었다."]], [["“You met him once at my place, don’t you remember?", "\"언젠가 우리 집에서 만났잖나, 기억 안 나?"], ["A good-natured fellow.”", "사람 좋은 친구지.\""]], [["Levin did the same as Stepan Arkadyevitch and took the glass.", "레빈도 스테판 아르카지치처럼 잔을 받아 들었다."]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch’s anecdote too was very amusing.", "스테판 아르카지치의 일화도 무척 재미있었다."], ["Levin told his story, and that too was successful.", "레빈도 자기 이야기를 했고, 그것 역시 호응을 얻었다."], ["Then they talked of horses, of the races, of what they had been doing that day, and of how smartly Vronsky’s Atlas had won the first prize.", "그러고 나서 그들은 말 이야기며 경마 이야기, 그날 무엇을 했는지, 브론스키의 아틀라스가 얼마나 멋지게 일등상을 거머쥐었는지를 화제 삼았다."], ["Levin did not notice how the time passed at dinner.", "레빈은 만찬 자리에서 시간이 어떻게 흘러가는지조차 알아채지 못했다."]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["and here they are!”", "저기들 오는군!\""], ["Stepan Arkadyevitch said towards the end of dinner, leaning over the back of his chair and holding out his hand to Vronsky, who came up with a tall officer of the Guards.", "만찬이 끝나 갈 무렵 스테판 아르카지치가 의자 등받이에 몸을 기대고 키 큰 근위 장교와 함께 다가오는 브론스키에게 손을 내밀며 말했다."], ["Vronsky’s face too beamed with the look of good-humored enjoyment that was general in the club.", "브론스키의 얼굴 역시 클럽 전체에 감도는 그 기분 좋은 즐거움의 표정으로 환하게 빛났다."], ["He propped his elbow playfully on Stepan Arkadyevitch’s shoulder, whispering something to him, and he held out his hand to Levin with the same good-humored smile.", "그는 장난스럽게 스테판 아르카지치의 어깨에 팔꿈치를 괴고는 그에게 무어라 속삭이고는, 똑같이 기분 좋은 미소를 머금은 채 레빈에게 손을 내밀었다."]], [["“Very glad to meet you,” he said.", "\"만나서 정말 반갑소.\""], ["“I looked out for you at the election, but I was told you had gone away.”", "그가 말했다. \"선거 때 당신을 찾았는데, 이미 떠났다고들 하더군요.\""]], [["“Yes, I left the same day.", "\"네, 바로 그날 떠났습니다."], ["We’ve just been talking of your horse.", "마침 당신의 말 이야기를 하던 참이었지요."], ["I congratulate you,”", "축하드립니다.\""], ["said Levin.", "레빈이 말했다."], ["“It was very rapidly run.”", "\"정말 빠르게 달리더군요.\""]], [["“Yes; you’ve race horses too, haven’t you?”", "\"그렇소. 당신도 경주마를 가지고 있지 않소?\""]], [["“No, my father had; but I remember and know something about it.”", "\"아니요, 제 아버지가 가지고 계셨죠. 하지만 기억도 하고 그쪽으로 좀 아는 바도 있습니다.\""]], [["“Where have you dined?”", "\"어디서 식사했나?\""], ["asked Stepan Arkadyevitch.", "스테판 아르카지치가 물었다."]], [["“We were at the second table, behind the columns.”", "\"우린 기둥 뒤편 둘째 식탁에 있었네.\""]], [["“We’ve been celebrating his success,”", "\"우리는 이 친구의 성공을 축하하던 참이오.\""], ["said the tall colonel.", "키 큰 대령이 말했다."], ["“It’s his second Imperial prize.", "\"벌써 두 번째 황실 상이지요."], ["I wish I might have the luck at cards he has with horses.", "나도 이 친구가 말에서 누리는 행운만큼만 카드에서 운이 따르면 좋으련만."], ["Well, why waste the precious time?", "자, 귀한 시간을 허비할 게 뭐 있겠소?"], ["I’m going to the ‘infernal regions,’”", "나는 '지옥의 영역'으로 가 봐야겠소.\""], ["added the colonel, and he walked away.", "대령은 그렇게 덧붙이고는 자리를 떴다."]], [["“That’s Yashvin,”", "\"저 친구가 야시빈이오.\""], ["Vronsky said in answer to Turovtsin, and he sat down in the vacated seat beside them.", "브론스키가 투롭친의 물음에 답하며, 그들 곁의 빈자리에 앉았다."], ["He drank the glass offered him, and ordered a bottle of wine.", "그는 건네받은 잔을 비우고 포도주 한 병을 청했다."], ["Under the influence of the club atmosphere or the wine he had drunk, Levin chatted away to Vronsky of the best breeds of cattle, and was very glad not to feel the slightest hostility to this man.", "클럽의 분위기 탓인지 마신 술 탓인지, 레빈은 브론스키와 가장 좋은 소 품종에 관해 한참 이야기를 나누었고, 이 사내에게 털끝만 한 적대감도 느끼지 않는 것이 무척 기뻤다."], ["He even told him, among other things, that he had heard from his wife that she had met him at Princess Marya Borissovna’s.", "그는 다른 이야기 끝에, 아내에게서 그녀가 마리야 보리소브나 공작 부인 댁에서 그를 만났다는 말을 들었다는 것까지 말해 주었다."]], [["“Ah, Princess Marya Borissovna, she’s exquisite!”", "\"아, 마리야 보리소브나 공작 부인, 그분은 참 멋진 분이지!\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, and he told an anecdote about her which set them all laughing.", "스테판 아르카지치가 말하고는 그 부인에 관한 일화를 들려주어 모두를 웃겼다."], ["Vronsky particularly laughed with such simplehearted amusement that Levin felt quite reconciled to him.", "특히 브론스키가 어찌나 천진한 즐거움으로 웃었던지, 레빈은 그에게 완전히 마음이 누그러지는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["“Well, have we finished?”", "\"자, 우린 다 끝났나?\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, getting up with a smile.", "스테판 아르카지치가 미소 지으며 일어났다."], ["“Let us go.”", "\"갑시다.\""]], [["# Chapter 8", "# Chapter 8"]], [["Getting up from the table, Levin walked with Gagin through the lofty room to the billiard room, feeling his arms swing as he walked with a peculiar lightness and ease.", "식탁에서 일어선 레빈은 가긴과 함께 천장이 높은 방을 지나 당구실로 걸어갔다. 걸음을 옮길 때마다 두 팔이 묘하게 가볍고 편안하게 흔들리는 것이 느껴졌다."], ["As he crossed the big room, he came upon his father-in-law.", "큰 방을 가로지르다가 그는 장인과 마주쳤다."]], [["“Well, how do you like our Temple of Indolence?”", "\"어떤가, 우리의 게으름의 전당이 마음에 드는가?\""], ["said the prince, taking his arm.", "공작이 그의 팔을 잡으며 말했다."], ["“Come along, come along!”", "\"가세, 가자고!\""]], [["“Yes, I wanted to walk about and look at everything.", "\"네, 여기저기 좀 거닐면서 모든 걸 둘러보고 싶었습니다."], ["It’s interesting.”", "흥미롭군요.\""]], [["“Yes, it’s interesting for you.", "\"그래, 자네한테는 흥미롭겠지."], ["But its interest for me is quite different.", "하지만 나한테는 그 흥미가 사뭇 달라."], ["You look at those little old men now,”", "저기 저 작은 노인네들을 좀 보게나.\""], ["he said, pointing to a club member with bent back and projecting lip, shuffling towards them in his soft boots, “and imagine that they were shlupiks like that from their birth up.”", "그는 등이 굽고 입술을 쑥 내민 채 부드러운 장화를 신고 발을 끌며 다가오는 한 클럽 회원을 가리키며 말했다. \"그러고는 저들이 태어날 때부터 저런 슐루피크였다고 한번 상상해 보게.\""]], [["“How shlupiks?”", "\"슐루피크라니요?\""]], [["“I see you don’t know that name.", "\"자네는 그 말을 모르는 모양이군."], ["That’s our club designation.", "그게 우리 클럽에서 쓰는 명칭이야."], ["You know the game of rolling eggs: when one’s rolled a long while it becomes a shlupik.", "달걀 굴리기 놀이 알지? 한참을 굴리다 보면 달걀이 슐루피크가 되어 버리거든."], ["So it is with us; one goes on coming and coming to the club, and ends by becoming a shlupik.", "우리도 그래. 클럽에 자꾸자꾸 나오다 보면 결국엔 슐루피크가 되어 버리는 거지."], ["Ah, you laugh!", "아, 자네 웃는구먼!"], ["but we look out, for fear of dropping into it ourselves.", "하지만 우리도 자칫 그 꼴이 될까 봐 늘 조심한다네."], ["You know Prince Tchetchensky?”", "자네 체첸스키 공작 아나?\""], ["inquired the prince; and Levin saw by his face that he was just going to relate something funny.", "공작이 물었다. 레빈은 그의 얼굴을 보고 그가 막 무슨 우스운 이야기를 늘어놓으려 한다는 것을 알아차렸다."]], [["“No, I don’t know him.”", "\"아니요, 모릅니다.\""]], [["“You don’t say so!", "\"저런!"], ["Well, Prince Tchetchensky is a well-known figure.", "그래, 체첸스키 공작은 꽤 알려진 인물이야."], ["No matter, though.", "하지만 그건 아무래도 좋아."], ["He’s always playing billiards here.", "그는 늘 여기서 당구를 친다네."], ["Only three years ago he was not a shlupik and kept up his spirits and even used to call other people shlupiks.", "불과 삼 년 전만 해도 그는 슐루피크가 아니었고, 기운도 펄펄해서 오히려 남들을 슐루피크라고 부르고 다녔지. 그런데 어느 날 그가 나타났는데, 우리 문지기가..."], ["But one day he turns up, and our porter ...", "자네 바실리 알지? 왜, 그 뚱뚱한 친구 말이야."], ["you know Vassily?", "그는 재담으로 유명하지."], ["Why, that fat one; he’s famous for his bon mots.", "그래서 체첸스키 공작이 그에게 묻기를, '이보게, 바실리, 누가 와 있나?"], ["And so Prince Tchetchensky asks him, ‘Come, Vassily, who’s here?", "슐루피크들 좀 와 있나?' 하니까, 그가 '나리께서 세 번째이십니다.'"], ["Any shlupiks here yet?’ And he says, ‘You’re the third.’ Yes, my dear boy, that he did!”", "하더라네. 그래, 이보게, 정말 그랬다니까!\""]], [["Talking and greeting the friends they met, Levin and the prince walked through all the rooms: the great room where tables had already been set, and the usual partners were playing for small stakes; the divan room, where they were playing chess, and Sergey Ivanovitch was sitting talking to somebody; the billiard room, where, about a sofa in a recess, there was a lively party drinking champagne—Gagin was one of them.", "이야기를 나누고 마주치는 친구들과 인사를 주고받으며 레빈과 공작은 모든 방을 두루 걸어 다녔다. 이미 테이블들이 차려지고 늘 어울리던 짝패들이 적은 판돈을 걸고 노름을 하고 있는 큰 방, 사람들이 장기를 두고 세르게이 이바노비치가 누군가와 이야기를 나누며 앉아 있던 소파 방, 움푹 들어간 구석의 소파를 둘러싸고 한 무리가 신나게 샴페인을 마시고 있던 당구실—가긴도 그중 하나였다—을 지났다."], ["They peeped into the “infernal regions,” where a good many men were crowding round one table, at which Yashvin was sitting.", "그들은 야시빈이 앉아 있는 테이블 하나를 빙 둘러싸고 꽤 많은 사람들이 몰려 있는 \"지옥의 영역\"을 들여다보았다."], ["Trying not to make a noise, they walked into the dark reading room, where under the shaded lamps there sat a young man with a wrathful countenance, turning over one journal after another, and a bald general buried in a book.", "소리를 내지 않으려 애쓰며, 갓을 씌운 등불 아래 성난 얼굴을 한 젊은이가 잡지를 하나씩 차례로 넘기고 있고 대머리 장군 하나가 책에 파묻혀 있는 어두운 독서실로 들어갔다."], ["They went, too, into what the prince called the intellectual room, where three gentlemen were engaged in a heated discussion of the latest political news.", "또한 공작이 지성의 방이라 부르는 곳에도 들어갔는데, 거기서는 세 신사가 최근의 정치 소식을 두고 열띤 토론을 벌이고 있었다."]], [["“Prince, please come, we’re ready,”", "\"공작님, 어서 오십시오, 다 준비됐습니다.\""], ["said one of his card party, who had come to look for him, and the prince went off.", "그를 찾으러 온 카드 짝패 중 하나가 말하자, 공작은 그쪽으로 가 버렸다."], ["Levin sat down and listened, but recalling all the conversation of the morning he felt all of a sudden fearfully bored.", "레빈은 자리에 앉아 귀를 기울였지만, 아침에 나누었던 온갖 대화를 떠올리자 별안간 끔찍하게 지루해졌다."], ["He got up hurriedly, and went to look for Oblonsky and Turovtsin, with whom it had been so pleasant.", "그는 부랴부랴 일어나 그토록 즐거웠던 오블론스키와 투롭친을 찾으러 갔다."]], [["Turovtsin was one of the circle drinking in the billiard room, and Stepan Arkadyevitch was talking with Vronsky near the door at the farther corner of the room.", "투롭친은 당구실에서 술을 마시는 무리 가운데 하나였고, 스테판 아르카지치는 방 저쪽 구석의 문 가까이에서 브론스키와 이야기를 나누고 있었다."]], [["“It’s not that she’s dull; but this undefined, this unsettled position,”", "\"그 사람이 따분해서가 아니라, 이 모호하고 불안정한 처지가 문제야.\""], ["Levin caught, and he was hurrying away, but Stepan Arkadyevitch called to him.", "레빈은 이런 말을 얼핏 들었고, 서둘러 자리를 뜨려 했지만 스테판 아르카지치가 그를 불러 세웠다."]], [["“Levin,”", "\"레빈.\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, and Levin noticed that his eyes were not full of tears exactly, but moist, which always happened when he had been drinking, or when he was touched.", "스테판 아르카지치가 말했다. 레빈은 그의 눈이 정확히 눈물로 가득 찼다기보다는 촉촉이 젖어 있다는 것을 알아챘는데, 그것은 그가 술을 마셨거나 감동했을 때면 늘 일어나는 일이었다."], ["Just now it was due to both causes.", "지금은 그 두 가지 까닭 모두 때문이었다."], ["“Levin, don’t go,”", "\"레빈, 가지 마.\""], ["he said, and he warmly squeezed his arm above the elbow, obviously not at all wishing to let him go.", "그는 그의 팔꿈치 위쪽을 다정하게 꽉 움켜쥐며 말했다. 그를 도무지 보내고 싶지 않은 기색이 역력했다."]], [["“This is a true friend of mine—almost my greatest friend,”", "\"이 사람은 나의 진정한 벗이야—거의 가장 가까운 벗이지.\""], ["he said to Vronsky.", "그는 브론스키에게 말했다."], ["“You have become even closer and dearer to me.", "\"자네는 나한테 더욱 가깝고 소중한 사람이 되었고. 그래서 난 자네 둘이 친구가, 그것도 막역한 친구가 되었으면 해."], ["And I want you, and I know you ought, to be friends, and great friends, because you’re both splendid fellows.”", "또 마땅히 그래야 한다고 생각하고. 자네들 둘 다 훌륭한 사람들이니까.\""]], [["“Well, there’s nothing for us now but to kiss and be friends,”", "\"뭐, 이제 우리한테 남은 거라곤 입맞춤하고 친구가 되는 일밖에 없겠군요.\""], ["Vronsky said, with good-natured playfulness, holding out his hand.", "브론스키가 사람 좋게 농을 던지며 손을 내밀었다."]], [["Levin quickly took the offered hand, and pressed it warmly.", "레빈은 내민 손을 얼른 잡고 따뜻하게 마주 쥐었다."]], [["“I’m very, very glad,”", "\"정말, 정말 기쁩니다.\""], ["said Levin.", "레빈이 말했다."]], [["“Waiter, a bottle of champagne,”", "\"여보게, 샴페인 한 병.\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch.", "스테판 아르카지치가 말했다."]], [["“And I’m very glad,”", "\"저도 무척 기쁩니다.\""], ["said Vronsky.", "브론스키가 말했다."]], [["But in spite of Stepan Arkadyevitch’s desire, and their own desire, they had nothing to talk about, and both felt it.", "그러나 스테판 아르카지치의 바람에도, 또 그들 자신의 바람에도 불구하고, 그들에게는 나눌 이야깃거리가 없었고, 둘 다 그것을 느꼈다."]], [["“Do you know, he has never met Anna?”", "\"있잖아, 이 친구가 안나를 한 번도 못 만나 봤다네.\""], ["Stepan Arkadyevitch said to Vronsky.", "스테판 아르카지치가 브론스키에게 말했다."], ["“And I want above everything to take him to see her.", "\"그래서 난 무엇보다 이 친구를 데리고 가서 그 사람을 만나게 해 주고 싶어."], ["Let us go, Levin!”", "가세, 레빈!\""]], [["“Really?”", "\"정말입니까?\""], ["said Vronsky.", "브론스키가 말했다."], ["“She will be very glad to see you.", "\"그녀도 당신을 만나면 무척 반가워할 겁니다."], ["I should be going home at once,”", "저야 당장 집에 갔으면 합니다만,\""], ["he added, “but I’m worried about Yashvin, and I want to stay on till he finishes.”", "그가 덧붙였다. \"야시빈이 걱정돼서, 그가 끝낼 때까지 남아 있고 싶군요.\""]], [["“Why, is he losing?”", "\"왜, 그가 잃고 있나?\""]], [["“He keeps losing, and I’m the only friend that can restrain him.”", "\"계속 잃고 있어서요, 그를 말릴 수 있는 친구는 저밖에 없거든요.\""]], [["“Well, what do you say to pyramids?", "\"그럼, 피라미드 한 판 어떤가?"], ["Levin, will you play?", "레빈, 자네 칠 텐가?"], ["Capital!”", "좋아!\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch.", "스테판 아르카지치가 말했다."], ["“Get the table ready,”", "\"테이블 준비하게.\""], ["he said to the marker.", "그가 점수원에게 말했다."]], [["“It has been ready a long while,”", "\"진작에 준비됐습니다.\""], ["answered the marker, who had already set the balls in a triangle, and was knocking the red one about for his own diversion.", "점수원이 대답했다. 그는 벌써 공들을 삼각형으로 모아 놓고는 심심풀이로 빨간 공을 이리저리 굴리고 있었다."]], [["“Well, let us begin.”", "\"자, 그럼 시작하지.\""]], [["After the game Vronsky and Levin sat down at Gagin’s table, and at Stepan Arkadyevitch’s suggestion Levin took a hand in the game.", "게임이 끝난 뒤 브론스키와 레빈은 가긴의 테이블에 앉았고, 스테판 아르카지치의 권유로 레빈도 게임에 끼어들었다."]], [["Vronsky sat down at the table, surrounded by friends, who were incessantly coming up to him.", "브론스키는 테이블에 앉았고, 끊임없이 그에게 다가오는 친구들에 둘러싸였다."], ["Every now and then he went to the “infernal” to keep an eye on Yashvin.", "이따금씩 그는 야시빈을 살피러 \"지옥\"으로 갔다."], ["Levin was enjoying a delightful sense of repose after the mental fatigue of the morning.", "레빈은 아침의 정신적 피로 끝에 찾아온 기분 좋은 휴식의 감각을 만끽하고 있었다."], ["He was glad that all hostility was at an end with Vronsky, and the sense of peace, decorum, and comfort never left him.", "그는 브론스키와의 모든 적대감이 끝난 것이 기뻤고, 평온과 예의와 안락의 감각이 그를 좀처럼 떠나지 않았다."]], [["When the game was over, Stepan Arkadyevitch took Levin’s arm.", "게임이 끝나자 스테판 아르카지치가 레빈의 팔을 잡았다."]], [["“Well, let us go to Anna’s, then.", "\"자, 그럼 안나한테 가세."], ["At once?", "당장? 응?"], ["Eh? She is at home.", "그 사람 집에 있어."], ["I promised her long ago to bring you.", "진작에 자넬 데려가겠다고 약속했거든."], ["Where were you meaning to spend the evening?”", "자넨 오늘 저녁을 어디서 보낼 작정이었나?\""]], [["“Oh, nowhere specially.", "\"아, 딱히 정해 둔 데는 없습니다."], ["I promised Sviazhsky to go to the Society of Agriculture.", "스비야시스키한테 농업 협회에 가겠다고 약속하긴 했지만요."], ["By all means, let us go,”", "어떻게든 가시지요.\""], ["said Levin.", "레빈이 말했다."]], [["“Very good; come along.", "\"아주 좋아. 가세."], ["Find out if my carriage is here,”", "내 마차가 와 있는지 알아봐 주게.\""], ["Stepan Arkadyevitch said to the waiter.", "스테판 아르카지치가 종업원에게 말했다."]], [["Levin went up to the table, paid the forty roubles he had lost; paid his bill, the amount of which was in some mysterious way ascertained by the little old waiter who stood at the counter, and swinging his arms he walked through all the rooms to the way out.", "레빈은 테이블로 다가가 자기가 잃은 사십 루블을 치렀다. 계산대 앞에 서 있던 작은 노인 종업원이 무슨 영문인지 어떻게든 알아낸 그의 계산서도 치르고는, 두 팔을 흔들며 모든 방을 지나 출구로 걸어 나갔다."]], [["# Chapter 9", "# Chapter 9"]], [["“Oblonsky’s carriage!”", "\"오블론스키 댁 마차!\""], ["the porter shouted in an angry bass.", "문지기가 화난 듯한 굵은 저음으로 외쳤다."], ["The carriage drove up and both got in.", "마차가 다가왔고 둘은 올라탔다."], ["It was only for the first few moments, while the carriage was driving out of the clubhouse gates, that Levin was still under the influence of the club atmosphere of repose, comfort, and unimpeachable good form.", "마차가 클럽 정문을 빠져나가는 처음 몇 순간 동안만, 레빈은 여전히 클럽 특유의 휴식과 안락과 나무랄 데 없는 품격의 분위기에 젖어 있었다."], ["But as soon as the carriage drove out into the street, and he felt it jolting over the uneven road, heard the angry shout of a sledge driver coming towards them, saw in the uncertain light the red blind of a tavern and the shops, this impression was dissipated, and he began to think over his actions, and to wonder whether he was doing right in going to see Anna.", "하지만 마차가 거리로 나서자, 울퉁불퉁한 길 위에서 덜컹거리는 것을 느끼고, 맞은편에서 다가오는 썰매꾼의 성난 고함 소리를 듣고, 흐릿한 불빛 속에서 어느 선술집과 가게들의 붉은 차양을 보게 되자, 그 인상은 흩어져 버렸고, 그는 자기 행동을 곰곰이 따져 보며 안나를 만나러 가는 것이 옳은 일인지 스스로 묻기 시작했다."], ["What would Kitty say?", "키티가 뭐라고 할까?"], ["But Stepan Arkadyevitch gave him no time for reflection, and, as though divining his doubts, he scattered them.", "하지만 스테판 아르카지치는 그에게 생각할 틈을 주지 않았고, 마치 그의 의심을 꿰뚫어 보기라도 한 듯 그것을 흩어 버렸다."]], [["“How glad I am,” he said, “that you should know her!", "\"자네가 그 사람을 알게 되다니, 내가 얼마나 기쁜지 모르겠네.\""], ["You know Dolly has long wished for it.", "그가 말했다. \"돌리도 진작부터 바라던 일이라네."], ["And Lvov’s been to see her, and often goes.", "르보프도 그녀를 보러 갔고, 자주 들른다네."], ["Though she is my sister,”", "내 누이이긴 하네만,\""], ["Stepan Arkadyevitch pursued, “I don’t hesitate to say that she’s a remarkable woman.", "스테판 아르카지치가 말을 이었다. \"그래도 주저 없이 말하겠는데, 정말 비범한 여자라네."], ["But you will see.", "자네도 곧 알게 될 거야."], ["Her position is very painful, especially now.”", "그녀의 처지는 매우 괴로워, 특히 요즘은 더하지.\""]], [["“Why especially now?”", "\"왜 특히 요즘이지?\""]], [["“We are carrying on negotiations with her husband about a divorce.", "\"그녀의 남편과 이혼 문제를 두고 협상을 진행하고 있다네."], ["And he’s agreed; but there are difficulties in regard to the son, and the business, which ought to have been arranged long ago, has been dragging on for three months past.", "그 사람도 동의는 했지만, 아들 문제로 어려움이 좀 있어서, 진작에 정리되었어야 할 일이 벌써 석 달째 질질 끌리고 있다네."], ["As soon as the divorce is over, she will marry Vronsky.", "이혼만 끝나면 그녀는 브론스키와 결혼할 걸세."], ["How stupid these old ceremonies are, that no one believes in, and which only prevent people being comfortable!”", "아무도 믿지 않는, 그저 사람들이 편안하게 사는 것만 가로막는 그런 케케묵은 절차들이 얼마나 어리석은지!\""], ["Stepan Arkadyevitch put in.", "스테판 아르카지치가 끼어들었다."], ["“Well, then their position will be as regular as mine, as yours.”", "\"뭐, 그렇게 되면 그들의 처지도 내 처지나 자네 처지처럼 떳떳해지겠지.\""]], [["“What is the difficulty?”", "\"무엇이 그리 어려운 건가?\""], ["said Levin.", "레빈이 말했다."]], [["“Oh, it’s a long and tedious story!", "\"아, 길고 따분한 이야기일세!"], ["The whole business is in such an anomalous position with us.", "우리네 모든 일이라는 게 다 그렇게 비정상적인 상태에 놓여 있다네. 어쨌든 요점은 이거야."], ["But the point is she has been for three months in Moscow, where everyone knows her, waiting for the divorce; she goes out nowhere, sees no woman except Dolly, because, do you understand, she doesn’t care to have people come as a favor.", "그녀는 다들 그녀를 아는 모스크바에서 석 달째 이혼을 기다리고 있다네. 어디에도 나다니지 않고, 돌리 말고는 어떤 여자도 만나지 않아."], ["That fool Princess Varvara, even she has left her, considering this a breach of propriety.", "왜냐하면, 자네 알겠나, 사람들이 마지못해 인심 쓰듯 찾아오는 걸 그녀가 원치 않기 때문이지."], ["Well, you see, in such a position any other woman would not have found resources in herself.", "그 바보 같은 바르바라 공작 부인마저, 이걸 예의에 어긋나는 일로 여기고 그녀를 떠나 버렸다네. 자, 보게나, 이런 처지에 놓이면 다른 여자라면 어떤 여자도 제 안에서 헤쳐 나갈 방도를 찾지 못했을 거야."], ["But you’ll see how she has arranged her life—how calm, how dignified she is.", "하지만 자네는 그녀가 자기 삶을 어떻게 꾸려 왔는지, 얼마나 차분하고 얼마나 기품 있는지 보게 될 걸세."], ["To the left, in the crescent opposite the church!”", "왼쪽으로, 교회 맞은편 반달 모양 거리로!\""], ["shouted Stepan Arkadyevitch, leaning out of the window.", "스테판 아르카지치가 창밖으로 몸을 내밀며 외쳤다."], ["“Phew!", "\"휴!"], ["how hot it is!”", "어찌나 덥던지!\""], ["he said, in spite of twelve degrees of frost, flinging his open overcoat still wider open.", "영하 십이 도인데도 그는 이렇게 말하며 풀어 헤친 외투를 더 활짝 젖혔다."]], [["“But she has a daughter: no doubt she’s busy looking after her?”", "\"하지만 그녀에게는 딸이 있지 않은가. 분명 그 아이를 돌보느라 바쁠 텐데?\""], ["said Levin.", "레빈이 말했다."]], [["“I believe you picture every woman simply as a female, une couveuse,”", "\"자네는 모든 여자를 그저 암컷으로만, une couveuse로만 그려 보는 모양이군.\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch.", "스테판 아르카지치가 말했다."], ["“If she’s occupied, it must be with her children.", "\"여자가 바쁘다면 그건 분명 아이들 때문일 거라고 말이지."], ["No, she brings her up capitally, I believe, but one doesn’t hear about her.", "아닐세, 내가 알기로 그녀는 딸을 더없이 훌륭하게 기르고 있지만, 그것에 대해서는 입에 올리지 않는다네."], ["She’s busy, in the first place, with what she writes.", "그녀가 바쁜 건, 무엇보다 자기가 쓰는 글 때문이야."], ["I see you’re smiling ironically, but you’re wrong.", "자네가 비웃듯 미소 짓는 게 보이는데, 그건 잘못된 생각일세."], ["She’s writing a children’s book, and doesn’t talk about it to anyone, but she read it to me and I gave the manuscript to Vorkuev ...", "그녀는 동화책을 쓰고 있는데, 그것에 대해 누구에게도 말하지 않는다네. 하지만 내게는 읽어 주었고, 나는 그 원고를 보르쿠예프에게 건넸지..."], ["you know the publisher ...", "자네도 알지, 그 출판업자 말이야..."], ["and he’s an author himself too, I fancy.", "그 사람도 내 생각엔 작가일 거야."], ["He understands those things, and he says it’s a remarkable piece of work.", "그런 일을 잘 아는 사람인데, 그 사람 말로는 비범한 작품이라더군."], ["But are you fancying she’s an authoress?—not a bit of it.", "그렇다고 자네는 그녀가 여류 작가라고 생각하나?—전혀 그렇지 않아."], ["She’s a woman with a heart, before everything, but you’ll see.", "그녀는 무엇보다도 마음이 따뜻한 여자라네, 하지만 자네도 곧 알게 될 거야."], ["Now she has a little English girl with her, and a whole family she’s looking after.”", "지금은 어린 영국 여자아이 하나를 데리고 있고, 가족 전체를 돌보고 있다네.\" * une couveuse: 프랑스어로 '알을 품는 암탉'."]], [["“Oh, something in a philanthropic way?”", "\"오, 무슨 자선 사업 같은 건가?\""]], [["“Why, you will look at everything in the worst light.", "\"이런, 자네는 매사를 가장 나쁜 쪽으로만 보려 드는군."], ["It’s not from philanthropy, it’s from the heart.", "자선에서 나온 게 아니라, 마음에서 우러나온 일이라네."], ["They—that is, Vronsky—had a trainer, an Englishman, first-rate in his own line, but a drunkard.", "그들에게는—그러니까 브론스키에게는—조련사가 하나 있었는데, 영국인이고 자기 분야에서는 일류였지만 술꾼이었지."], ["He’s completely given up to drink—delirium tremens—and the family were cast on the world.", "그 사람은 완전히 술에 빠져 버려서—진전섬망에 걸려—그 가족이 세상에 내팽개쳐지고 말았다네."], ["She saw them, helped them, got more and more interested in them, and now the whole family is on her hands.", "그녀가 그들을 보았고, 도왔고, 점점 더 그들에게 마음을 쓰게 되었지. 그래서 이제는 가족 전체가 그녀의 손에 달려 있다네."], ["But not by way of patronage, you know, helping with money; she’s herself preparing the boys in Russian for the high school, and she’s taken the little girl to live with her.", "하지만 자네도 알겠지만, 돈으로 돕는 식의 시혜는 아니야. 그녀는 직접 그 집 사내아이들에게 김나지움 진학을 위한 러시아어를 가르치고, 어린 여자아이는 데려와 함께 살고 있다네."], ["But you’ll see her for yourself.”", "하지만 자네가 직접 그녀를 보게 될 걸세.\""]], [["The carriage drove into the courtyard, and Stepan Arkadyevitch rang loudly at the entrance where sledges were standing.", "마차가 안마당으로 들어섰고, 스테판 아르카지치는 썰매들이 늘어선 현관에서 요란하게 초인종을 울렸다."]], [["And without asking the servant who opened the door whether the lady were at home, Stepan Arkadyevitch walked into the hall.", "그리고 문을 열어 준 하인에게 마님이 댁에 계시느냐고 묻지도 않고, 스테판 아르카지치는 곧장 현관 안으로 들어섰다."], ["Levin followed him, more and more doubtful whether he was doing right or wrong.", "레빈은 자기가 옳은 일을 하는 것인지 그른 일을 하는 것인지 점점 더 의심하며 그의 뒤를 따랐다."]], [["Looking at himself in the glass, Levin noticed that he was red in the face, but he felt certain he was not drunk, and he followed Stepan Arkadyevitch up the carpeted stairs.", "거울에 자기 모습을 비춰 본 레빈은 얼굴이 벌겋게 달아오른 것을 알아챘지만, 자기가 취하지 않았다는 것만은 확신하며 양탄자가 깔린 계단을 올라 스테판 아르카지치를 뒤따랐다."], ["At the top Stepan Arkadyevitch inquired of the footman, who bowed to him as to an intimate friend, who was with Anna Arkadyevna, and received the answer that it was M. Vorkuev.", "계단 꼭대기에서 스테판 아르카지치는 마치 친한 친구를 대하듯 자기에게 절하는 하인에게 안나 아르카지예브나와 누가 함께 있느냐고 물었고, 보르쿠예프 씨라는 대답을 들었다."]], [["“Where are they?”", "\"어디 있나?\""]], [["“In the study.”", "\"서재에 계십니다.\""]], [["Passing through the dining-room, a room not very large, with dark, paneled walls, Stepan Arkadyevitch and Levin walked over the soft carpet to the half-dark study, lighted up by a single lamp with a big dark shade.", "그리 크지 않고 어두운 색의 판자를 댄 벽으로 둘러싸인 식당을 지나, 스테판 아르카지치와 레빈은 푹신한 양탄자를 밟으며 커다랗고 짙은 갓을 씌운 등불 하나만이 밝히고 있는 어둑한 서재로 걸어 들어갔다."], ["Another lamp with a reflector was hanging on the wall, lighting up a big full-length portrait of a woman, which Levin could not help looking at.", "벽에는 반사판이 달린 또 다른 등이 걸려 한 여인의 커다란 전신 초상화를 비추고 있었는데, 레빈은 그것을 보지 않을 수 없었다."], ["It was the portrait of Anna, painted in Italy by Mihailov.", "그것은 안나의 초상화로, 이탈리아에서 미하일로프가 그린 것이었다."], ["While Stepan Arkadyevitch went behind the treillage, and the man’s voice which had been speaking paused, Levin gazed at the portrait, which stood out from the frame in the brilliant light thrown on it, and he could not tear himself away from it.", "스테판 아르카지치가 격자 칸막이 뒤로 들어가고, 줄곧 이야기하던 남자의 목소리가 멈춘 사이, 레빈은 그 초상화를 응시했다. 환한 빛을 받은 초상화는 액자에서 도드라져 나와 있었고, 그는 도무지 거기서 눈을 뗄 수가 없었다."], ["He positively forgot where he was, and not even hearing what was said, he could not take his eyes off the marvelous portrait.", "그는 자기가 어디에 있는지조차 까맣게 잊었고, 무슨 말이 오가는지도 듣지 못한 채, 그 경이로운 초상화에서 시선을 떼지 못했다. 그것은 한 폭의 그림이 아니라, 살아 있는 매혹적인 여인이었다."], ["It was not a picture, but a living, charming woman, with black curling hair, with bare arms and shoulders, with a pensive smile on the lips, covered with soft down; triumphantly and softly she looked at him with eyes that baffled him.", "검고 곱슬거리는 머리에, 드러낸 팔과 어깨, 부드러운 솜털로 덮인 입술에는 사색에 잠긴 미소가 어려 있었다. 그녀는 그를 당혹스럽게 하는 눈으로, 의기양양하면서도 부드럽게 그를 바라보고 있었다."], ["She was not living only because she was more beautiful than a living woman can be.", "그녀가 살아 있지 않은 것은 오직 살아 있는 여인이 도달할 수 있는 것보다 더 아름다웠기 때문이었다."]], [["“I am delighted!”", "\"정말 반가워요!\""], ["He heard suddenly near him a voice, unmistakably addressing him, the voice of the very woman he had been admiring in the portrait.", "그는 문득 가까이에서, 틀림없이 자기에게 건네는 목소리를, 바로 그 초상화 속에서 그가 감탄하던 여인의 목소리를 들었다. 안나가 격자 칸막이 뒤에서 나와 그를 맞으러 온 것이었다."], ["Anna had come from behind the treillage to meet him, and Levin saw in the dim light of the study the very woman of the portrait, in a dark blue shot gown, not in the same position nor with the same expression, but with the same perfection of beauty which the artist had caught in the portrait.", "레빈은 서재의 어슴푸레한 불빛 속에서 짙은 청색의 광택 나는 드레스를 입은, 바로 그 초상화 속 여인을 보았다. 같은 자세도, 같은 표정도 아니었지만, 화가가 초상화에 담아낸 그 완벽한 아름다움만은 그대로였다."], ["She was less dazzling in reality, but, on the other hand, there was something fresh and seductive in the living woman which was not in the portrait.", "실제 모습은 덜 눈부셨으나, 그 대신 살아 있는 여인에게는 초상화에 없는 어떤 싱그럽고 매혹적인 무언가가 있었다."]], [["# Chapter 10", "# Chapter 10"]], [["She had risen to meet him, not concealing her pleasure at seeing him; and in the quiet ease with which she held out her little vigorous hand, introduced him to Vorkuev and indicated a red-haired, pretty little girl who was sitting at work, calling her her pupil, Levin recognized and liked the manners of a woman of the great world, always self-possessed and natural.", "그녀는 그를 맞으려고 자리에서 일어났는데, 그를 보는 기쁨을 감추지 않았다. 그녀가 작고 다부진 손을 내밀어 그를 보르쿠예프에게 소개하고, 일감을 든 채 앉아 있던 빨간 머리의 예쁘장한 소녀를 자기 제자라고 가리켜 보이던 그 조용하고 편안한 태도에서, 레빈은 늘 침착하고 자연스러운, 상류 사교계 여인의 몸가짐을 알아보았고 또 그것이 마음에 들었다."]], [["“I am delighted, delighted,”", "\"정말 기뻐요, 너무나 기뻐요.\""], ["she repeated, and on her lips these simple words took for Levin’s ears a special significance.", "그녀가 거듭 말했는데, 그녀의 입술에서 흘러나온 이 평범한 말들이 레빈의 귀에는 각별한 의미를 띠고 들렸다."], ["“I have known you and liked you for a long while, both from your friendship with Stiva and for your wife’s sake....", "\"오래전부터 당신을 알았고 또 좋아하고 있었답니다. 스티바와의 우정 때문이기도 하고, 또 당신 부인 때문이기도 하지요……"], ["I knew her for a very short time, but she left on me the impression of an exquisite flower, simply a flower.", "그분과는 아주 잠깐 알고 지냈을 뿐이지만, 제게는 더없이 아름다운 꽃 같은, 그야말로 한 송이 꽃 같은 인상을 남겼어요."], ["And to think she will soon be a mother!”", "그런데 그분이 곧 어머니가 된다니!\""]], [["She spoke easily and without haste, looking now and then from Levin to her brother, and Levin felt that the impression he was making was good, and he felt immediately at home, simple and happy with her, as though he had known her from childhood.", "그녀는 이따금 레빈에게서 오빠에게로 시선을 옮겨 가며 서두르지 않고 편안하게 이야기했고, 레빈은 자기가 그녀에게 좋은 인상을 주고 있음을 느꼈으며, 마치 어릴 적부터 그녀를 알아 온 것처럼 곧바로 마음이 놓이고 편안하고 행복해졌다."]], [["“Ivan Petrovitch and I settled in Alexey’s study,”", "\"이반 페트로비치와 저는 알렉세이의 서재에 자리를 잡았답니다.\" 담배를 피워도 되겠느냐는 스테판 아르카지치의 물음에 그녀가 답했다."], ["she said in answer to Stepan Arkadyevitch’s question whether he might smoke, “just so as to be able to smoke”—and glancing at Levin, instead of asking whether he would smoke, she pulled closer a tortoise-shell cigar-case and took a cigarette.", "\"그저 담배를 피울 수 있게 말이에요.\" 그러고는 레빈에게 담배를 피우겠느냐고 묻는 대신 그를 흘긋 보며 자라 등딱지로 만든 담뱃갑을 끌어당겨 궐련 한 개비를 집었다."]], [["“How are you feeling today?”", "\"오늘은 좀 어때?\""], ["her brother asked her.", "오빠가 그녀에게 물었다."]], [["“Oh, nothing.", "\"아, 아무렇지도 않아."], ["Nerves, as usual.”", "늘 그렇듯 신경이 좀.\""]], [["“Yes, isn’t it extraordinarily fine?”", "\"그래, 정말 빼어나게 잘 그렸지?\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, noticing that Levin was scrutinizing the picture.", "레빈이 그림을 유심히 들여다보는 것을 알아챈 스테판 아르카지치가 말했다."]], [["“I have never seen a better portrait.”", "\"이보다 더 훌륭한 초상화는 본 적이 없습니다.\""]], [["“And extraordinarily like, isn’t it?”", "\"게다가 놀랄 만큼 닮지 않았습니까?\""], ["said Vorkuev.", "보르쿠예프가 말했다."]], [["Levin looked from the portrait to the original.", "레빈은 초상화에서 실물로 시선을 옮겼다."], ["A peculiar brilliance lighted up Anna’s face when she felt his eyes on her.", "자기에게 쏠린 그의 눈길을 느끼자 안나의 얼굴이 묘한 광채로 빛났다."], ["Levin flushed, and to cover his confusion would have asked whether she had seen Darya Alexandrovna lately; but at that moment Anna spoke.", "레빈은 얼굴을 붉혔고, 당혹감을 감추려고 요즘 다리아 알렉산드로브나를 만났느냐고 물어보려 했다. 그런데 바로 그 순간 안나가 입을 열었다."], ["“We were just talking, Ivan Petrovitch and I, of Vashtchenkov’s last pictures.", "\"마침 이반 페트로비치와 바슈첸코프의 근작들에 관해 이야기하던 참이었어요."], ["Have you seen them?”", "그 그림들 보셨나요?\""]], [["“Yes, I have seen them,”", "\"예, 봤습니다.\""], ["answered Levin.", "레빈이 대답했다."]], [["“But, I beg your pardon, I interrupted you ...", "\"아, 죄송해요, 제가 말을 가로막았네요……"], ["you were saying?...”", "무슨 말씀을 하시려던 거였죠?……\""]], [["Levin asked if she had seen Dolly lately.", "레빈은 요즘 돌리를 만났느냐고 물었다."]], [["“She was here yesterday.", "\"어제 여기 왔었어요."], ["She was very indignant with the high school people on Grisha’s account.", "그리샤 일로 김나지움 사람들에게 잔뜩 화가 나 있더군요."], ["The Latin teacher, it seems, had been unfair to him.”", "라틴어 선생이 그 아이에게 부당하게 굴었던 모양이에요.\""]], [["“Yes, I have seen his pictures.", "\"예, 그 사람 그림은 봤습니다."], ["I didn’t care for them very much,”", "별로 마음에 들지 않았어요.\""], ["Levin went back to the subject she had started.", "레빈은 그녀가 꺼냈던 화제로 되돌아갔다."]], [["Levin talked now not at all with that purely businesslike attitude to the subject with which he had been talking all the morning.", "레빈은 이제 그날 아침 내내 그래 왔던 그 순전히 사무적인 태도와는 전혀 다르게 이야기하고 있었다."], ["Every word in his conversation with her had a special significance.", "그녀와 나누는 대화에서는 한 마디 한 마디가 각별한 의미를 띠었다."], ["And talking to her was pleasant; still pleasanter it was to listen to her.", "그녀와 이야기하는 것은 즐거웠고, 그녀의 말을 듣는 것은 더더욱 즐거웠다."]], [["Anna talked not merely naturally and cleverly, but cleverly and carelessly, attaching no value to her own ideas and giving great weight to the ideas of the person she was talking to.", "안나는 그저 자연스럽고 영리하게 이야기할 뿐만 아니라, 영리하면서도 무심하게, 자기 생각에는 아무런 가치도 두지 않으면서 상대방의 생각에는 큰 무게를 실어 주며 이야기했다."]], [["The conversation turned on the new movement in art, on the new illustrations of the Bible by a French artist.", "대화는 예술의 새로운 흐름으로, 어느 프랑스 화가가 그린 새로운 성서 삽화로 옮겨 갔다."], ["Vorkuev attacked the artist for a realism carried to the point of coarseness.", "보르쿠예프는 그 화가가 조잡할 정도로 극단까지 밀어붙인 사실주의를 두고 공격했다."]], [["Levin said that the French had carried conventionality further than anyone, and that consequently they see a great merit in the return to realism.", "레빈은 프랑스인들이 누구보다도 인습을 멀리까지 밀고 나갔으며, 그래서 그들이 사실주의로의 회귀에서 커다란 가치를 발견하는 거라고 말했다."], ["In the fact of not lying they see poetry.", "거짓말하지 않는다는 사실 그 자체에서 그들은 시를 본다는 것이었다."]], [["Never had anything clever said by Levin given him so much pleasure as this remark.", "레빈은 자기가 한 어떤 영리한 말에서도 이 한마디만큼 큰 기쁨을 느껴 본 적이 없었다."], ["Anna’s face lighted up at once, as at once she appreciated the thought.", "안나의 얼굴이 단번에 환해졌으니, 그 생각을 곧바로 알아챈 것이었다."], ["She laughed.", "그녀가 웃었다."]], [["“I laugh,”", "\"제가 웃는 건,\""], ["she said, “as one laughs when one sees a very true portrait.", "그녀가 말했다, \"아주 똑 닮은 초상화를 봤을 때처럼 웃는 거예요."], ["What you said so perfectly hits off French art now, painting and literature too, indeed—Zola, Daudet.", "당신이 하신 말씀은 지금의 프랑스 예술을, 회화도 문학도, 정말이지—졸라며 도데며, 완벽하게 짚어 냈어요."], ["But perhaps it is always so, that men form their conceptions from fictitious, conventional types, and then—all the combinaisons made—they are tired of the fictitious figures and begin to invent more natural, true figures.”", "하지만 어쩌면 늘 그런 건지도 모르지요. 사람들이 가공의, 인습적인 전형들로 자기 개념을 빚어내고, 그러다가—온갖 조합combinaisons을 다 만들어 보고 나면—그 가공의 인물들에 싫증이 나서 더 자연스럽고 참된 인물들을 만들어 내기 시작하는 거예요.\""]], [["“That’s perfectly true,”", "\"정말이지 옳은 말씀입니다.\""], ["said Vorknev.", "보르쿠예프가 말했다."]], [["“So you’ve been at the club?”", "\"그래서 클럽에 다녀온 거예요?\""], ["she said to her brother.", "그녀가 오빠에게 물었다."]], [["“Yes, yes, this is a woman!”", "\"그래, 그래, 이런 게 바로 여자야!\""], ["Levin thought, forgetting himself and staring persistently at her lovely, mobile face, which at that moment was all at once completely transformed.", "레빈은 자기 자신도 잊은 채, 그 순간 별안간 완전히 딴판으로 변해 버린 그녀의 사랑스럽고 생기 넘치는 얼굴을 뚫어지게 바라보며 생각했다."], ["Levin did not hear what she was talking of as she leaned over to her brother, but he was struck by the change of her expression.", "그녀가 오빠에게로 몸을 기울이며 무슨 이야기를 하는지 레빈은 듣지 못했지만, 그녀의 표정 변화에 충격을 받았다."], ["Her face—so handsome a moment before in its repose—suddenly wore a look of strange curiosity, anger, and pride.", "조금 전 차분함 속에서 그토록 아름다웠던 그녀의 얼굴이, 별안간 묘한 호기심과 분노와 자존심이 어린 표정을 띤 것이다."], ["But this lasted only an instant.", "하지만 그것은 한순간뿐이었다."], ["She dropped her eyelids, as though recollecting something.", "그녀는 무언가를 떠올리기라도 한 듯 눈꺼풀을 내리깔았다."]], [["“Oh, well, but that’s of no interest to anyone,”", "\"아, 뭐, 그런 건 누구한테도 흥미로운 일이 아니죠.\""], ["she said, and she turned to the English girl.", "그녀가 말하고는 영국 소녀 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸다."]], [["“Please order the tea in the drawing-room,”", "\"응접실에 차를 내오라고 일러 주렴.\""], ["she said in English.", "그녀가 영어로 말했다."]], [["The girl got up and went out.", "소녀가 일어나 밖으로 나갔다."]], [["“Well, how did she get through her examination?”", "\"그래, 그 애 시험은 어떻게 됐어?\""], ["asked Stepan Arkadyevitch.", "스테판 아르카지치가 물었다."]], [["“Splendidly!", "\"훌륭했어!"], ["She’s a very gifted child and a sweet character.”", "정말 재능 있는 아이인 데다 마음씨도 곱지.\""]], [["“It will end in your loving her more than your own.”", "\"그러다가 결국엔 네 친자식보다 그 애를 더 사랑하게 되겠구나.\""]], [["“There a man speaks.", "\"꼭 남자다운 말이네."], ["In love there’s no more nor less.", "사랑에는 더하고 덜하고가 없어."], ["I love my daughter with one love, and her with another.”", "내 딸은 한 가지 사랑으로 사랑하고, 저 아이는 또 다른 사랑으로 사랑하는 거지.\""]], [["“I was just telling Anna Arkadyevna,”", "\"제가 안나 아르카지예브나께 말씀드렸지요.\""], ["said Vorkuev, “that if she were to put a hundredth part of the energy she devotes to this English girl to the public question of the education of Russian children, she would be doing a great and useful work.”", "보르쿠예프가 말했다. \"저 영국 아이에게 쏟는 정성의 백분의 일이라도 러시아 어린이 교육이라는 공적인 문제에 기울이신다면, 위대하고 유익한 일을 하시게 될 거라고요.\""]], [["“Yes, but I can’t help it; I couldn’t do it.", "\"맞아요, 하지만 어쩔 수가 없어요. 저는 그렇게 못 해요."], ["Count Alexey Kirillovitch urged me very much”", "알렉세이 키릴로비치 백작이 무척 권했답니다.\""], ["(as she uttered the words Count Alexey Kirillovitch she glanced with appealing timidity at Levin, and he unconsciously responded with a respectful and reassuring look); “he urged me to take up the school in the village.", "(그녀는 알렉세이 키릴로비치 백작이라는 말을 입에 올리며 애원하듯 머뭇거리는 눈빛으로 레빈을 흘긋 보았고, 그는 자기도 모르게 정중하고 안심시키는 눈길로 답했다.) \"그분이 마을 학교를 맡아 보라고 권하셨어요."], ["I visited it several times.", "저도 몇 번이나 가 봤지요."], ["The children were very nice, but I could not feel drawn to the work.", "아이들은 무척 사랑스러웠지만, 그 일에 마음이 끌리질 않더군요. 정성을 말씀하시는데, 정성은 사랑에 기대는 거예요."], ["You speak of energy. Energy rests upon love; and come as it will, there’s no forcing it.", "그리고 사랑이란 제멋대로 오는 거라서, 억지로 짜낼 수가 없어요."], ["I took to this child—I could not myself say why.”", "저는 이 아이가 좋아진 거고요—왜 그런지는 저도 말로 못 하겠어요.\""]], [["And she glanced again at Levin.", "그러고는 그녀가 다시 레빈을 흘긋 보았다."], ["And her smile and her glance—all told him that it was to him only she was addressing her words, valuing his good opinion, and at the same time sure beforehand that they understood each other.", "그녀의 미소와 눈길은—이 모든 것이 그에게, 오직 그에게만 자기 말을 건네고 있으며, 그의 좋은 평가를 소중히 여기고 있고, 또한 두 사람이 서로를 이해하고 있음을 미리부터 확신하고 있다고 말해 주고 있었다."]], [["“I quite understand that,”", "\"충분히 이해합니다.\""], ["Levin answered.", "레빈이 대답했다."], ["“It’s impossible to give one’s heart to a school or such institutions in general, and I believe that’s just why philanthropic institutions always give such poor results.”", "\"학교나 그런 류의 기관 일반에 마음을 쏟기란 불가능한 일이고, 바로 그래서 자선 기관들이 늘 그렇게 형편없는 결과를 내는 거라고 생각합니다.\""]], [["She was silent for a while, then she smiled.", "그녀는 잠시 입을 다물고 있다가 미소를 지었다."]], [["“Yes, yes,”", "\"맞아요, 맞아요.\""], ["she agreed; “I never could.", "그녀가 동의했다. \"저는 도무지 그럴 수가 없었어요."], ["Je n’ai pas le cœur assez large to love a whole asylum of horrid little girls. Cela ne m’a jamais réussi.", "끔찍한 계집애들로 가득 찬 보육원 하나를 통째로 사랑할 만큼 제 마음이 넉넉하질Je n'ai pas le cœur assez 못해요."], ["There are so many women who have made themselves une position sociale in that way.", "제겐 한 번도 그게 안 됐어요Cela ne m'a jamais réussi. 그런 식으로 사회적 지위une position sociale를 만들어 낸 여자들이 어찌나 많은지요."], ["And now more than ever,”", "그리고 지금은 그 어느 때보다도 더,\""], ["she said with a mournful, confiding expression, ostensibly addressing her brother, but unmistakably intending her words only for Levin, “now when I have such need of some occupation, I cannot.”", "그녀는 겉으로는 오빠를 향하는 척했지만 그 말이 오직 레빈만을 겨냥한 것임이 분명한, 슬프고 속내를 털어놓는 듯한 표정으로 말했다, \"지금처럼 무언가 할 일이 절실히 필요한 때조차, 저는 그럴 수가 없어요.\""], ["And suddenly frowning (Levin saw that she was frowning at herself for talking about herself) she changed the subject.", "그러고는 별안간 이맛살을 찌푸리며(레빈은 그녀가 자기 이야기를 한 자신을 향해 찌푸리고 있음을 알아챘다) 화제를 바꾸었다. \"당신에 대해 알고 있어요.\""], ["“I know about you,”", "그녀가 레빈에게 말했다."], ["she said to Levin; “that you’re not a public-spirited citizen, and I have defended you to the best of my ability.”", "\"당신이 공공심이 있는 시민은 아니라는 거 말이에요. 그래서 제가 힘닿는 데까지 당신을 변호해 드렸답니다.\""]], [["“How have you defended me?”", "\"어떻게 저를 변호하셨는데요?\""]], [["“Oh, according to the attacks made on you.", "\"아, 당신에게 쏟아지는 공격에 맞춰서요."], ["But won’t you have some tea?”", "그건 그렇고, 차 좀 드시지 않겠어요?\""], ["She rose and took up a book bound in morocco.", "그녀는 자리에서 일어나 모로코가죽으로 장정된 책 한 권을 집어 들었다."]], [["“Give it to me, Anna Arkadyevna,”", "\"그건 제게 주십시오, 안나 아르카지예브나.\""], ["said Vorkuev, indicating the book.", "보르쿠예프가 그 책을 가리키며 말했다."], ["“It’s well worth taking up.”", "\"충분히 다뤄 볼 만한 가치가 있는 작품입니다.\""]], [["“Oh, no, it’s all so sketchy.”", "\"아, 아니에요, 죄다 어설픈걸요.\""]], [["“I told him about it,”", "\"내가 그 사람한테 그 책 얘기를 했어.\""], ["Stepan Arkadyevitch said to his sister, nodding at Levin.", "스테판 아르카지치가 레빈 쪽으로 고갯짓하며 누이에게 말했다."]], [["“You shouldn’t have.", "\"그러지 말았어야지."], ["My writing is something after the fashion of those little baskets and carving which Liza Mertsalova used to sell me from the prisons.", "내 글 같은 건 리자 메르찰로바가 감옥에서 가져다 나한테 팔던 그 자그마한 바구니며 조각 세공 같은 거야."], ["She had the direction of the prison department in that society,”", "그 사람이 그 협회에서 감옥 담당 일을 맡고 있었거든.\""], ["she turned to Levin; “and they were miracles of patience, the work of those poor wretches.”", "그녀가 레빈을 향해 몸을 돌렸다. \"그 가엾은 사람들이 만든 그 물건들은 인내심의 기적이었지요.\""]], [["And Levin saw a new trait in this woman, who attracted him so extraordinarily.", "그리하여 레빈은 자기를 그토록 유별나게 끌어당기는 이 여인에게서 새로운 면모를 발견했다."], ["Besides wit, grace, and beauty, she had truth.", "재치며 우아함이며 아름다움 외에도, 그녀에게는 진실함이 있었다."], ["She had no wish to hide from him all the bitterness of her position.", "그녀는 자기 처지의 온갖 쓰라림을 그에게 숨기려 하지 않았다."], ["As she said that she sighed, and her face suddenly taking a hard expression, looked as it were turned to stone.", "그렇게 말하면서 그녀는 한숨을 내쉬었고, 그녀의 얼굴은 별안간 굳은 표정을 띠며 마치 돌로 변한 것처럼 보였다."], ["With that expression on her face she was more beautiful than ever; but the expression was new; it was utterly unlike that expression, radiant with happiness and creating happiness, which had been caught by the painter in her portrait.", "그런 표정을 지을 때 그녀는 어느 때보다도 아름다웠지만, 그 표정은 새로운 것이었다. 화가가 초상화에 담아낸, 행복으로 빛나며 또 행복을 자아내던 그 표정과는 전혀 딴판이었다."], ["Levin looked more than once at the portrait and at her figure, as taking her brother’s arm she walked with him to the high doors and he felt for her a tenderness and pity at which he wondered himself.", "그녀가 오빠의 팔을 잡고 높다란 문 쪽으로 함께 걸어갈 때, 레빈은 초상화와 그녀의 모습을 몇 번이고 번갈아 보았고, 자기 자신도 놀랄 만큼 그녀에 대한 애틋함과 연민을 느꼈다."]], [["She asked Levin and Vorkuev to go into the drawing-room, while she stayed behind to say a few words to her brother.", "그녀는 레빈과 보르쿠예프에게 응접실로 가 있으라 청하고는, 자기는 남아 오빠에게 몇 마디 할 말이 있다고 했다."], ["“About her divorce, about Vronsky, and what he’s doing at the club, about me?”", "\"자기 이혼 얘기일까, 브론스키 얘기일까, 아니면 그가 클럽에서 무얼 하고 있는지, 혹은 내 얘기일까?\""], ["wondered Levin.", "레빈은 궁금해했다."], ["And he was so keenly interested by the question of what she was saying to Stepan Arkadyevitch, that he scarcely heard what Vorkuev was telling him of the qualities of the story for children Anna Arkadyevna had written.", "그리고 그녀가 스테판 아르카지치에게 무슨 이야기를 하고 있을까 하는 물음에 너무나 골똘히 빠진 나머지, 안나 아르카지예브나가 쓴 어린이용 이야기의 장점에 대해 보르쿠예프가 들려주는 말을 거의 듣지 못했다."]], [["At tea the same pleasant sort of talk, full of interesting matter, continued.", "차를 마시는 동안에도 흥미로운 화제로 가득한, 그 똑같이 유쾌한 종류의 대화가 이어졌다."], ["There was not a single instant when a subject for conversation was to seek; on the contrary, it was felt that one had hardly time to say what one had to say, and eagerly held back to hear what the others were saying.", "무슨 이야기를 꺼낼지 찾아 헤매는 순간이라곤 단 한 번도 없었다. 오히려 하고 싶은 말을 다 할 시간이 모자랄 지경이어서, 다른 사람들이 무슨 말을 하는지 듣고 싶은 마음에 기꺼이 자기 말을 삼키곤 했다."], ["And all that was said, not only by her, but by Vorkuev and Stepan Arkadyevitch—all, so it seemed to Levin, gained peculiar significance from her appreciation and her criticism.", "그리고 거기서 오간 모든 말은, 그녀뿐 아니라 보르쿠예프며 스테판 아르카지치가 한 말까지도—레빈에게는 그렇게 느껴졌다—그녀의 공감과 비평을 거치며 각별한 의미를 얻었다."], ["While he followed this interesting conversation, Levin was all the time admiring her—her beauty, her intelligence, her culture, and at the same time her directness and genuine depth of feeling.", "이 흥미로운 대화를 좇으면서도 레빈은 줄곧 그녀에게 감탄하고 있었다—그녀의 아름다움, 지성, 교양, 그리고 동시에 그녀의 솔직함과 진실로 깊은 감정에."], ["He listened and talked, and all the while he was thinking of her inner life, trying to divine her feelings.", "그는 듣고 또 말하면서도, 내내 그녀의 내면 생활을 생각하며 그녀의 감정을 헤아려 보려 애썼다."], ["And though he had judged her so severely hitherto, now by some strange chain of reasoning he was justifying her and was also sorry for her, and afraid that Vronsky did not fully understand her.", "지금껏 그토록 가혹하게 그녀를 판단해 왔으면서도, 이제는 어떤 묘한 사고의 사슬을 따라 그녀를 두둔하고 있었고, 또 그녀를 안쓰러워하며, 브론스키가 그녀를 온전히 이해하지 못하는 것은 아닐까 두려워했다."], ["At eleven o’clock, when Stepan Arkadyevitch got up to go (Vorkuev had left earlier), it seemed to Levin that he had only just come.", "열한 시가 되어 스테판 아르카지치가 가려고 일어섰을 때(보르쿠예프는 더 일찍 떠난 뒤였다), 레빈은 자기가 이제 막 도착한 것만 같았다."], ["Regretfully Levin too rose.", "레빈도 아쉬운 마음으로 일어섰다."]], [["“Good-bye,”", "\"안녕히 가세요.\""], ["she said, holding his hand and glancing into his face with a winning look.", "그녀가 그의 손을 잡고 마음을 사로잡는 눈길로 그의 얼굴을 들여다보며 말했다."], ["“I am very glad que la glace est rompue.”", "\"얼음이 깨져서que la glace est rompue 정말 기뻐요.\""]], [["She dropped his hand, and half closed her eyes.", "그녀는 그의 손을 놓고 눈을 반쯤 감았다."]], [["“Tell your wife that I love her as before, and that if she cannot pardon me my position, then my wish for her is that she may never pardon it.", "\"부인께 전해 주세요, 제가 예전과 다름없이 그분을 사랑한다고요. 그리고 만약 그분이 제 처지를 용서하지 못하신다면, 저는 차라리 그분이 영영 그것을 용서하지 않기를 바란다고요."], ["To pardon it, one must go through what I have gone through, and may God spare her that.”", "그걸 용서하려면 제가 겪어 온 일을 똑같이 겪어야 하니, 부디 하느님께서 그분에게는 그것을 면하게 해 주시기를.\""]], [["“Certainly, yes, I will tell her....”", "\"물론입니다, 예, 전하겠습니다……\""], ["Levin said, blushing.", "레빈이 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다."]], [["# Chapter 11", "# Chapter 11"]], [["“What a marvelous, sweet and unhappy woman!”", "\"정말 놀랍고, 다정하고, 불행한 여자야!\""], ["he was thinking, as he stepped out into the frosty air with Stepan Arkadyevitch.", "스테판 아르카지치와 함께 살을 에는 바깥 공기 속으로 나서며 그는 생각했다."]], [["“Well, didn’t I tell you?”", "\"그래, 내가 뭐라고 했나?\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, seeing that Levin had been completely won over.", "레빈이 완전히 마음을 빼앗긴 것을 보고 스테판 아르카지치가 말했다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"맞아.\""], ["said Levin dreamily, “an extraordinary woman!", "레빈이 꿈꾸듯 말했다. \"비범한 여자야!"], ["It’s not her cleverness, but she has such wonderful depth of feeling.", "머리가 좋다는 게 아니라, 감정의 깊이가 정말 놀랍더군."], ["I’m awfully sorry for her!”", "그녀가 너무 안됐어!\""]], [["“Now, please God, everything will soon be settled.", "\"자, 이제 신의 가호로 모든 게 곧 해결될 걸세."], ["Well, well, don’t be hard on people in future,”", "그래, 그래, 앞으로는 사람을 너무 모질게 판단하지 말게.\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, opening the carriage door.", "스테판 아르카지치가 마차 문을 열며 말했다."], ["“Good-bye; we don’t go the same way.”", "\"잘 가게. 우리는 가는 길이 다르니.\""]], [["Still thinking of Anna, of everything, even the simplest phrase in their conversation with her, and recalling the minutest changes in her expression, entering more and more into her position, and feeling sympathy for her, Levin reached home.", "여전히 안나를, 모든 것을, 그녀와 나눈 대화 속의 가장 단순한 말 한마디까지 떠올리고, 그녀의 표정에 일어난 아주 미세한 변화들까지 되새기며, 점점 더 그녀의 처지 속으로 들어가 그녀에게 연민을 느끼면서 레빈은 집에 다다랐다."]], [["At home Kouzma told Levin that Katerina Alexandrovna was quite well, and that her sisters had not long been gone, and he handed him two letters.", "집에서 쿠즈마는 레빈에게 카체리나 알렉산드로브나는 아주 잘 지내고 있으며 그녀의 자매들이 떠난 지 얼마 되지 않았다고 알리고는 편지 두 통을 건넸다."], ["Levin read them at once in the hall, that he might not overlook them later.", "레빈은 나중에 깜빡할까 봐 현관에서 곧장 그것들을 읽었다."], ["One was from Sokolov, his bailiff.", "하나는 그의 집사인 소콜로프에게서 온 것이었다."], ["Sokolov wrote that the corn could not be sold, that it was fetching only five and a half roubles, and that more than that could not be got for it.", "소콜로프는 곡물을 팔 수 없는 형편이며, 고작 5루블 반밖에 받지 못하는데 그 이상은 도무지 받아낼 수 없다고 썼다."], ["The other letter was from his sister.", "다른 편지는 누이에게서 온 것이었다."], ["She scolded him for her business being still unsettled.", "그녀는 자기 일이 아직도 해결되지 않았다고 그를 나무랐다."]], [["“Well, we must sell it at five and a half if we can’t get more,”", "\"음, 더 받을 수 없다면 5루블 반에라도 팔아야지.\""], ["Levin decided the first question, which had always before seemed such a weighty one, with extraordinary facility on the spot.", "레빈은 전에는 늘 그토록 무겁게만 여겨지던 첫 번째 문제를 그 자리에서 놀라우리만치 쉽게 결정해 버렸다."], ["“It’s extraordinary how all one’s time is taken up here,”", "\"여기서는 어쩌면 이렇게 시간이 통째로 잡아먹히는지 놀랍군.\""], ["he thought, considering the second letter.", "두 번째 편지를 생각하며 그는 마음속으로 중얼거렸다."], ["He felt himself to blame for not having got done what his sister had asked him to do for her.", "그는 누이가 부탁한 일을 처리하지 못한 것에 자책감을 느꼈다."], ["“Today, again, I’ve not been to the court, but today I’ve certainly not had time.”", "\"오늘도 또 법원에 가지 못했어. 하지만 오늘은 정말 시간이 없었지.\""], ["And resolving that he would not fail to do it next day, he went up to his wife.", "그리고 내일은 반드시 처리하리라 다짐하며 그는 아내에게로 올라갔다."], ["As he went in, Levin rapidly ran through mentally the day he had spent.", "방으로 들어가면서 레빈은 그날 하루를 마음속으로 빠르게 되짚어 보았다."], ["All the events of the day were conversations, conversations he had heard and taken part in.", "그날의 사건이라는 것은 죄다 대화였다. 그가 듣고 또 끼어들었던 대화들."], ["All the conversations were upon subjects which, if he had been alone at home, he would never have taken up, but here they were very interesting.", "그 모든 대화는 만약 그가 집에 혼자 있었다면 결코 입에 올리지 않았을 화제들이었는데, 거기서는 무척 흥미로웠다."], ["And all these conversations were right enough, only in two places there was something not quite right.", "그리고 그 모든 대화는 충분히 옳았으나, 다만 두 군데가 어딘가 옳지 않았다."], ["One was what he had said about the carp, the other was something not “quite the thing”", "하나는 잉어에 관해 그가 했던 말이었고, 다른 하나는 안나에게 느낀 애틋한 연민 속에 무언가 \"온당치 못한\""], ["in the tender sympathy he was feeling for Anna.", "데가 있다는 것이었다."]], [["Levin found his wife low-spirited and dull.", "레빈은 아내가 풀이 죽고 시무룩해진 것을 보았다."], ["The dinner of the three sisters had gone off very well, but then they had waited and waited for him, all of them had felt dull, the sisters had departed, and she had been left alone.", "세 자매의 만찬은 아주 잘 치러졌지만, 그러고 나서 그들은 그를 기다리고 또 기다렸으며, 다들 따분해졌고, 자매들은 떠났으며, 그녀는 혼자 남겨졌던 것이다."]], [["“Well, and what have you been doing?”", "\"그래서, 당신은 뭘 하고 있었어요?\""], ["she asked him, looking straight into his eyes, which shone with rather a suspicious brightness.", "그녀는 의심스러운 빛으로 다소 반짝이는 그의 눈을 똑바로 들여다보며 물었다."], ["But that she might not prevent his telling her everything, she concealed her close scrutiny of him, and with an approving smile listened to his account of how he had spent the evening.", "그러나 그가 모든 것을 털어놓는 것을 방해하지 않으려고, 그녀는 그를 면밀히 살피는 기색을 감추고는 그가 저녁을 어떻게 보냈는지 이야기하는 것을 호의적인 미소를 띤 채 들어주었다."]], [["“Well, I’m very glad I met Vronsky.", "\"음, 브론스키를 만나서 정말 기뻤어."], ["I felt quite at ease and natural with him.", "그 사람 앞에서 아주 편안하고 자연스러웠거든."], ["You understand, I shall try not to see him, but I’m glad that this awkwardness is all over,”", "알겠지만, 앞으로 그를 만나지 않으려고 애쓰긴 할 거야. 하지만 이 어색함이 다 끝나서 다행이야.\""], ["he said, and remembering that by way of trying not to see him, he had immediately gone to call on Anna, he blushed.", "그는 말했고, 그를 만나지 않으려고 애쓴다면서 곧장 안나를 찾아갔던 것을 떠올리며 얼굴을 붉혔다. \"우리는 농민들이 술 마시는 이야기를 하지."], ["“We talk about the peasants drinking; I don’t know which drinks most, the peasantry or our own class; the peasants do on holidays, but....”", "농민이 더 많이 마시는지, 우리 계급이 더 많이 마시는지 나는 모르겠어. 농민들은 명절에 마시지만…….\""]], [["But Kitty took not the slightest interest in discussing the drinking habits of the peasants.", "그러나 키티는 농민들의 음주 습관을 논하는 데는 조금의 관심도 없었다."], ["She saw that he blushed, and she wanted to know why.", "그녀는 그가 얼굴을 붉히는 것을 보았고, 그 까닭을 알고 싶었다."]], [["“Well, and then where did you go?”", "\"그래서, 그다음엔 어디 갔어요?\""]], [["“Stiva urged me awfully to go and see Anna Arkadyevna.”", "\"스티바가 안나 아르카지예브나를 보러 가자고 엄청나게 졸라 댔어.\""]], [["And as he said this, Levin blushed even more, and his doubts as to whether he had done right in going to see Anna were settled once for all.", "이 말을 하면서 레빈은 더욱 얼굴을 붉혔고, 안나를 보러 간 것이 옳은 일이었는지에 대한 그의 의혹은 단번에 결판이 났다."], ["He knew now that he ought not to have done so.", "그는 이제 그래서는 안 되었다는 것을 알았다."]], [["Kitty’s eyes opened in a curious way and gleamed at Anna’s name, but controlling herself with an effort, she concealed her emotion and deceived him.", "키티의 눈이 묘하게 커지더니 안나의 이름에 번뜩였다. 그러나 그녀는 안간힘을 다해 자제하며 감정을 숨기고 그를 속였다."]], [["“Oh!”", "\"어머!\""], ["was all she said.", "그녀가 한 말은 그것뿐이었다."]], [["“I’m sure you won’t be angry at my going.", "\"내가 간 것에 당신이 화내지 않을 거라고 믿어."], ["Stiva begged me to, and Dolly wished it,”", "스티바가 부탁했고, 돌리도 바랐던 일이거든.\""], ["Levin went on.", "레빈이 말을 이었다."]], [["“Oh, no!”", "\"아, 아니에요!\""], ["she said, but he saw in her eyes a constraint that boded him no good.", "그녀가 말했지만, 그는 그녀의 눈 속에서 자기에게 좋을 게 없는 어떤 긴장을 보았다."]], [["“She is a very sweet, very, very unhappy, good woman,”", "\"그녀는 아주 다정하고, 아주, 아주 불행하지만 좋은 여자야.\""], ["he said, telling her about Anna, her occupations, and what she had told him to say to her.", "그는 안나에 관해, 그녀가 하는 일들에 관해, 그리고 그녀가 키티에게 전해 달라고 부탁한 말에 관해 이야기하며 말했다."]], [["“Yes, of course, she is very much to be pitied,”", "\"네, 물론, 그분은 정말 가엾은 분이에요.\""], ["said Kitty, when he had finished.", "그가 이야기를 마치자 키티가 말했다."], ["“Whom was your letter from?”", "\"당신 편지는 누구한테서 온 거예요?\""]], [["He told her, and believing in her calm tone, he went to change his coat.", "그는 말해 주었고, 그녀의 차분한 어조를 믿고는 외투를 갈아입으러 갔다."]], [["Coming back, he found Kitty in the same easy chair.", "돌아와 보니 키티는 여전히 그 안락의자에 앉아 있었다."], ["When he went up to her, she glanced at him and broke into sobs.", "그가 다가가자 그녀는 그를 흘끗 보더니 흐느껴 울기 시작했다."]], [["“What?", "\"왜?"], ["what is it?”", "무슨 일이야?\""], ["he asked, knowing beforehand what.", "그는 무슨 일인지 미리 알면서도 물었다."]], [["“You’re in love with that hateful woman; she has bewitched you!", "\"당신은 그 가증스러운 여자를 사랑하고 있어요. 그 여자가 당신한테 마법을 걸었어요!"], ["I saw it in your eyes.", "당신 눈에서 봤어요."], ["Yes, yes!", "그래요, 그래!"], ["What can it all lead to?", "이게 다 어떻게 되겠어요?"], ["You were drinking at the club, drinking and gambling, and then you went ...", "당신은 클럽에서 술을 마시고, 마시고 도박을 하더니, 그러고는 갔잖아요……"], ["to her of all people!", "하필이면 그 여자한테로!"], ["No, we must go away....", "안 돼요, 우리는 떠나야 해요……"], ["I shall go away tomorrow.”", "나는 내일 떠나겠어요.\""]], [["It was a long while before Levin could soothe his wife.", "레빈이 아내를 달래기까지는 오랜 시간이 걸렸다."], ["At last he succeeded in calming her, only by confessing that a feeling of pity, in conjunction with the wine he had drunk, had been too much for him, that he had succumbed to Anna’s artful influence, and that he would avoid her.", "마침내 그가 그녀를 진정시킬 수 있었던 것은, 연민의 감정이 마신 술과 겹쳐 자기로서는 도저히 감당이 안 됐고, 안나의 교묘한 영향력에 굴복하고 말았으며, 앞으로는 그녀를 피하겠다고 고백하고 나서였다."], ["One thing he did with more sincerity confess to was that living so long in Moscow, a life of nothing but conversation, eating and drinking, he was degenerating.", "좀 더 진심으로 인정한 것 하나는, 모스크바에서 오로지 대화와 먹고 마시는 일뿐인 생활을 그토록 오래 하다 보니 자기가 타락해 가고 있다는 점이었다."], ["They talked till three o’clock in the morning.", "그들은 새벽 세 시까지 이야기를 나누었다."], ["Only at three o’clock were they sufficiently reconciled to be able to go to sleep.", "세 시가 되어서야 그들은 잠자리에 들 수 있을 만큼 충분히 화해했다."]], [["# Chapter 12", "# Chapter 12"]], [["After taking leave of her guests, Anna did not sit down, but began walking up and down the room.", "손님들을 배웅한 뒤에도 안나는 자리에 앉지 않고 방 안을 이리저리 거닐기 시작했다."], ["She had unconsciously the whole evening done her utmost to arouse in Levin a feeling of love—as of late she had fallen into doing with all young men—and she knew she had attained her aim, as far as was possible in one evening, with a married and conscientious man.", "그녀는 저녁 내내 무의식적으로 레빈의 마음속에 사랑의 감정을 불러일으키려고 온 힘을 다했다—요즈음 들어 그녀는 모든 젊은 남자들에게 그렇게 하곤 했다—그리고 결혼한 데다 양심적인 남자를 상대로 하룻저녁에 가능한 한도 안에서 자기 목적을 이루었음을 알고 있었다."], ["She liked him indeed extremely, and, in spite of the striking difference, from the masculine point of view, between Vronsky and Levin, as a woman she saw something they had in common, which had made Kitty able to love both.", "사실 그녀는 그가 무척 마음에 들었고, 남자의 관점에서 볼 때 브론스키와 레빈 사이에 두드러진 차이가 있음에도, 한 여자로서 그녀는 두 사람에게 공통된 무언가를 보았다. 바로 그것이 키티로 하여금 두 사람을 다 사랑할 수 있게 했던 것이다."], ["Yet as soon as he was out of the room, she ceased to think of him.", "그러나 그가 방을 나서자마자 그녀는 그에 대한 생각을 멈추었다."]], [["One thought, and one only, pursued her in different forms, and refused to be shaken off.", "한 가지 생각, 오직 한 가지 생각만이 갖가지 형태로 그녀를 쫓아다니며 떨쳐 내기를 거부했다."], ["“If I have so much effect on others, on this man, who loves his home and his wife, why is it he is so cold to me?...", "'내가 다른 사람들에게, 제 집과 아내를 사랑하는 이 남자에게 이토록 영향을 미칠 수 있다면, 어째서 그이는 나에게 이토록 차가운 걸까?……"], ["not cold exactly, he loves me, I know that!", "차갑다는 건 정확한 말이 아니지, 그이는 나를 사랑해, 그건 내가 알아!"], ["But something new is drawing us apart now.", "하지만 이제 뭔가 새로운 것이 우리를 갈라놓고 있어."], ["Why wasn’t he here all the evening?", "왜 그이는 저녁 내내 여기 없었을까?"], ["He told Stiva to say he could not leave Yashvin, and must watch over his play.", "스티바를 시켜 야시빈을 두고 떠날 수 없으니 그의 노름을 지켜봐야 한다고 전하라고 했지."], ["Is Yashvin a child?", "야시빈이 어린애야?"], ["But supposing it’s true.", "하지만 그게 사실이라고 치자."], ["He never tells a lie.", "그이는 결코 거짓말을 하지 않으니까."], ["But there’s something else in it if it’s true.", "하지만 그게 사실이라면 거기엔 또 다른 무언가가 있어."], ["He is glad of an opportunity of showing me that he has other duties; I know that, I submit to that.", "그이는 자기에게 다른 의무가 있다는 걸 나에게 보여 줄 기회를 반긴 거야. 그건 나도 알고, 그것에 따르겠어."], ["But why prove that to me?", "하지만 왜 그걸 나에게 증명하려는 걸까?"], ["He wants to show me that his love for me is not to interfere with his freedom.", "그이는 나에 대한 사랑이 자기 자유를 방해해선 안 된다는 걸 보여 주고 싶은 거야."], ["But I need no proofs, I need love.", "하지만 나는 증명 따위는 필요 없어, 나는 사랑이 필요해."], ["He ought to understand all the bitterness of this life for me here in Moscow.", "그이는 이곳 모스크바에서 내가 겪는 이 삶의 온갖 쓰라림을 이해해야 마땅해."], ["Is this life?", "이게 사는 건가?"], ["I am not living, but waiting for an event, which is continually put off and put off.", "나는 살고 있는 게 아니야, 끊임없이 미루어지고 또 미루어지는 어떤 사건을 기다리고 있을 뿐이지."], ["No answer again!", "또다시 답이 없어!"], ["And Stiva says he cannot go to Alexey Alexandrovitch.", "그리고 스티바는 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에게 갈 수 없다고 하고."], ["And I can’t write again.", "나는 다시 편지를 쓸 수도 없어."], ["I can do nothing, can begin nothing, can alter nothing; I hold myself in, I wait, inventing amusements for myself—the English family, writing, reading—but it’s all nothing but a sham, it’s all the same as morphine.", "나는 아무것도 할 수 없고, 아무것도 시작할 수 없고, 아무것도 바꿀 수 없어. 나는 나를 억누르고, 기다리며, 나 자신을 위한 소일거리를 만들어 내지—영국인 가족이며, 글쓰기며, 독서며—하지만 그건 전부 거짓에 지나지 않아, 전부 모르핀이나 마찬가지야."], ["He ought to feel for me,”", "그이는 나를 가엾게 여겨야 마땅해.'"], ["she said, feeling tears of self-pity coming into her eyes.", "그녀는 자기 연민의 눈물이 두 눈에 차오르는 것을 느끼며 그렇게 생각했다."]], [["She heard Vronsky’s abrupt ring and hurriedly dried her tears—not only dried her tears, but sat down by a lamp and opened a book, affecting composure.", "그녀는 브론스키가 거칠게 초인종을 울리는 소리를 듣고 황급히 눈물을 닦았다—눈물을 닦았을 뿐 아니라 등불 곁에 앉아 책을 펼치고는 침착한 척했다."], ["She wanted to show him that she was displeased that he had not come home as he had promised—displeased only, and not on any account to let him see her distress, and least of all, her self-pity.", "그녀는 그가 약속한 대로 집에 돌아오지 않은 데 대해 자기가 언짢아하고 있음을 그에게 보여 주고 싶었다—언짢아하는 것뿐이지, 무슨 일이 있어도 자신의 괴로움을, 그중에서도 특히 자기 연민을 그에게 들켜서는 안 되었다."], ["She might pity herself, but he must not pity her.", "자기 자신은 스스로를 가엾게 여길 수 있어도, 그가 그녀를 가엾게 여겨서는 안 되었다."], ["She did not want strife, she blamed him for wanting to quarrel, but unconsciously put herself into an attitude of antagonism.", "그녀는 다툼을 원하지 않았고, 다투려 한다며 그를 탓했지만, 무의식적으로 자신을 적대의 태세로 몰아넣었다."]], [["“Well, you’ve not been dull?”", "\"음, 지루하지는 않았소?\""], ["he said, eagerly and good-humoredly, going up to her.", "그가 그녀에게 다가오며 들뜨고 기분 좋은 목소리로 말했다."], ["“What a terrible passion it is—gambling!”", "\"노름이라는 게 얼마나 무서운 정념인지!\""]], [["“No, I’ve not been dull; I’ve learned long ago not to be dull.", "\"아뇨, 지루하지 않았어요. 지루해하지 않는 법은 오래전에 배웠으니까요."], ["Stiva has been here and Levin.”", "스티바가 왔다 갔고 레빈도요.\""]], [["“Yes, they meant to come and see you.", "\"그래, 두 사람이 당신을 보러 오겠다고 했지."], ["Well, how did you like Levin?”", "그래, 레빈은 마음에 들었소?\""], ["he said, sitting down beside her.", "그가 그녀 곁에 앉으며 말했다."]], [["“Very much.", "\"아주 마음에 들었어요."], ["They have not long been gone.", "떠난 지 얼마 안 됐어요."], ["What was Yashvin doing?”", "야시빈은 뭘 하고 있었나요?\""]], [["“He was winning—seventeen thousand.", "\"따고 있었지—만 칠천 루블을."], ["I got him away.", "내가 그를 끌어냈소."], ["He had really started home, but he went back again, and now he’s losing.”", "그가 정말로 집으로 향하던 참이었는데, 다시 돌아갔고, 지금은 잃고 있어.\""]], [["“Then what did you stay for?”", "\"그럼 당신은 뭐 하러 남아 있었던 거예요?\""], ["she asked, suddenly lifting her eyes to him.", "그녀가 갑자기 두 눈을 들어 그를 바라보며 물었다."], ["The expression of her face was cold and ungracious.", "그녀의 얼굴 표정은 차갑고 매정했다."], ["“You told Stiva you were staying on to get Yashvin away.", "\"당신은 스티바한테 야시빈을 끌어내려고 남아 있는 거라고 했잖아요."], ["And you have left him there.”", "그런데 그를 거기 두고 왔고요.\""]], [["The same expression of cold readiness for the conflict appeared on his face too.", "차갑게 다툼에 응할 준비가 되었다는 똑같은 표정이 그의 얼굴에도 떠올랐다."]], [["“In the first place, I did not ask him to give you any message; and secondly, I never tell lies.", "\"우선, 나는 그에게 당신한테 어떤 말을 전해 달라고 부탁한 적이 없소. 그리고 둘째로, 나는 결코 거짓말을 하지 않아."], ["But what’s the chief point, I wanted to stay, and I stayed,”", "하지만 무엇보다 핵심은, 내가 남아 있고 싶었고 그래서 남아 있었다는 거요.\""], ["he said, frowning.", "그가 미간을 찌푸리며 말했다."], ["“Anna, what is it for, why will you?”", "\"안나, 대체 왜 이러는 거요, 왜 자꾸 이러느냔 말이오?\""], ["he said after a moment’s silence, bending over towards her, and he opened his hand, hoping she would lay hers in it.", "그가 잠시 침묵하다 그녀 쪽으로 몸을 굽히며 말했고, 그녀가 자기 손에 손을 얹어 주기를 바라며 손바닥을 폈다."]], [["She was glad of this appeal for tenderness.", "그녀는 다정함을 청하는 이 몸짓이 반가웠다."], ["But some strange force of evil would not let her give herself up to her feelings, as though the rules of warfare would not permit her to surrender.", "그러나 어떤 기이한 악의 힘이 그녀로 하여금 자기 감정에 자신을 내맡기지 못하게 했다. 마치 전쟁의 규칙이 그녀에게 항복을 허락하지 않는 것 같았다."]], [["“Of course you wanted to stay, and you stayed.", "\"물론 당신은 남아 있고 싶었고, 그래서 남아 있었겠죠."], ["You do everything you want to.", "당신은 하고 싶은 건 다 하니까."], ["But what do you tell me that for?", "하지만 그걸 나한테 왜 말하는 거예요?"], ["With what object?”", "무슨 목적으로요?\""], ["she said, getting more and more excited.", "그녀가 점점 더 흥분하며 말했다."], ["“Does anyone contest your rights?", "\"누가 당신 권리에 시비를 거나요?"], ["But you want to be right, and you’re welcome to be right.”", "하지만 당신은 옳은 사람이 되고 싶은 거죠, 그럼 마음껏 옳은 사람이 되시든가요.\""]], [["His hand closed, he turned away, and his face wore a still more obstinate expression.", "그의 손이 오므라들었고, 그는 고개를 돌렸으며, 그의 얼굴에는 한층 더 고집스러운 표정이 떠올랐다."]], [["“For you it’s a matter of obstinacy,”", "\"당신에게 이건 고집의 문제예요.\""], ["she said, watching him intently and suddenly finding the right word for that expression that irritated her, “simply obstinacy.", "그녀가 그를 뚫어지게 바라보다가 자기를 화나게 한 그 표정을 가리킬 알맞은 말을 문득 찾아내고는 말했다."], ["For you it’s a question of whether you keep the upper hand of me, while for me....”", "\"그저 고집이죠. 당신에게 이건 나에 대해 우위를 지키느냐 마느냐의 문제지만, 나에게는…….\""], ["Again she felt sorry for herself, and she almost burst into tears.", "다시금 그녀는 자기 자신이 가엾어져 거의 울음을 터뜨릴 뻔했다."], ["“If you knew what it is for me!", "\"당신이 이게 나에게 어떤 건지 안다면!"], ["When I feel as I do now that you are hostile, yes, hostile to me, if you knew what this means for me!", "지금 내가 느끼는 것처럼 당신이 나에게 적대적이라고, 그래요, 적대적이라고 느낄 때, 이게 나에게 무슨 의미인지 당신이 안다면!"], ["If you knew how I feel on the brink of calamity at this instant, how afraid I am of myself!”", "바로 이 순간 내가 얼마나 재앙의 벼랑 끝에 서 있다고 느끼는지, 내가 나 자신을 얼마나 두려워하는지 당신이 안다면!\""], ["And she turned away, hiding her sobs.", "그러고는 흐느낌을 감추며 고개를 돌렸다."]], [["“But what are you talking about?”", "\"하지만 대체 무슨 말을 하는 거요?\""], ["he said, horrified at her expression of despair, and again bending over her, he took her hand and kissed it.", "절망에 찬 그녀의 표정에 질겁한 그가 말하고는, 다시 그녀에게로 몸을 굽혀 그녀의 손을 잡고 입을 맞추었다."], ["“What is it for?", "\"왜 이러는 거요?"], ["Do I seek amusements outside our home?", "내가 우리 집 밖에서 즐길 거리를 찾기라도 한단 말이오?"], ["Don’t I avoid the society of women?”", "내가 여자들과 어울리는 걸 피하지 않소?\""]], [["“Well, yes!", "\"그래요, 맞아요!"], ["If that were all!”", "그게 전부라면 얼마나 좋겠어요!\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“Come, tell me what I ought to do to give you peace of mind?", "\"자, 당신 마음을 편안하게 해 주려면 내가 어떻게 해야 하는지 말해 보오."], ["I am ready to do anything to make you happy,”", "당신을 행복하게 해 줄 수 있다면 나는 무엇이든 할 준비가 되어 있소.\""], ["he said, touched by her expression of despair; “what wouldn’t I do to save you from distress of any sort, as now, Anna!”", "그녀의 절망에 찬 표정에 마음이 움직인 그가 말했다. \"지금처럼 어떤 종류의 괴로움에서든 당신을 구할 수만 있다면 내가 못 할 일이 뭐가 있겠소, 안나!\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“It’s nothing, nothing!” she said.", "\"아무것도 아니에요, 아무것도!\""], ["“I don’t know myself whether it’s the solitary life, my nerves....", "그녀가 말했다. \"혼자 지내는 생활 탓인지, 신경 탓인지 나도 잘 모르겠어요……"], ["Come, don’t let us talk of it.", "자, 이 얘기는 그만해요."], ["What about the race?", "경마는 어떻게 됐어요?"], ["You haven’t told me!”", "아직 얘기 안 해 줬잖아요!\""], ["she inquired, trying to conceal her triumph at the victory, which had anyway been on her side.", "그녀는 어쨌든 자기 쪽으로 기운 승리에 대한 의기양양함을 감추려 애쓰며 물었다."]], [["He asked for supper, and began telling her about the races; but in his tone, in his eyes, which became more and more cold, she saw that he did not forgive her for her victory, that the feeling of obstinacy with which she had been struggling had asserted itself again in him.", "그는 야식을 청하고는 경마 이야기를 들려주기 시작했다. 그러나 그녀는 그의 어조에서, 점점 더 차가워지는 그의 눈에서, 그가 자기의 승리를 용서하지 않았음을, 그녀가 맞서 싸워 온 그 고집의 감정이 그의 안에서 다시 고개를 들었음을 알아챘다."], ["He was colder to her than before, as though he were regretting his surrender.", "그는 마치 자기가 항복한 것을 후회하기라도 하듯 전보다 더 그녀에게 차가웠다."], ["And she, remembering the words that had given her the victory, “how I feel on the brink of calamity, how afraid I am of myself,” saw that this weapon was a dangerous one, and that it could not be used a second time.", "그리고 그녀는 자기에게 승리를 안겨 준 그 말, \"내가 얼마나 재앙의 벼랑 끝에 서 있다고 느끼는지, 내가 나 자신을 얼마나 두려워하는지\"라는 말을 떠올리며, 이 무기가 위험한 것이며, 두 번째로는 쓸 수 없는 것임을 깨달았다."], ["And she felt that beside the love that bound them together there had grown up between them some evil spirit of strife, which she could not exorcise from his, and still less from her own heart.", "그리고 그녀는 두 사람을 묶어 주는 사랑 곁에, 그의 마음에서도 몰아낼 수 없고 자기 자신의 마음에서는 더더욱 몰아낼 수 없는 어떤 악한 다툼의 영(靈)이 두 사람 사이에 자라났음을 느꼈다."]], [["# Chapter 13", "# Chapter 13"]], [["There are no conditions to which a man cannot become used, especially if he sees that all around him are living in the same way.", "인간이 익숙해지지 못할 처지란 없다. 더욱이 주위 사람들이 모두 그와 똑같이 살아가는 것을 본다면 더욱 그렇다."], ["Levin could not have believed three months before that he could have gone quietly to sleep in the condition in which he was that day, that leading an aimless, irrational life, living too beyond his means, after drinking to excess (he could not call what happened at the club anything else), forming inappropriately friendly relations with a man with whom his wife had once been in love, and a still more inappropriate call upon a woman who could only be called a lost woman, after being fascinated by that woman and causing his wife distress—he could still go quietly to sleep.", "석 달 전이었다면 레빈은 그날 자기가 처한 상황 속에서 태평하게 잠들 수 있으리라고는 믿지 못했을 것이다. 목표도 없고 분별도 없는 삶을 살고, 분수에 넘치게 돈을 쓰고, 과음을 하고(클럽에서 있었던 일을 달리 표현할 길이 없었다), 한때 아내가 사랑했던 남자와 어울리지 않게 가까운 관계를 맺고, 그보다 더 어울리지 않게 타락한 여자라고밖에 부를 수 없는 여인을 찾아가고, 그 여자에게 마음을 빼앗겨 아내를 괴롭게 하고서도—여전히 태평하게 잠들 수 있으리라고는."], ["But under the influence of fatigue, a sleepless night, and the wine he had drunk, his sleep was sound and untroubled.", "하지만 피로와 잠 못 이룬 밤과 마신 술의 영향으로 그의 잠은 깊고 평온했다."]], [["At five o’clock the creak of a door opening waked him.", "다섯 시에 문 열리는 삐걱 소리가 그를 깨웠다."], ["He jumped up and looked round.", "그는 벌떡 일어나 주위를 둘러보았다."], ["Kitty was not in bed beside him.", "키티가 옆자리에 없었다."], ["But there was a light moving behind the screen, and he heard her steps.", "그러나 칸막이 뒤에서 불빛이 움직이고 있었고, 그녀의 발소리가 들렸다."]], [["“What is it?...", "\"무슨 일이야?……"], ["what is it?”", "무슨 일이야?\""], ["he said, half-asleep.", "그가 비몽사몽 중에 말했다."], ["“Kitty!", "\"키티!"], ["What is it?”", "무슨 일이야?\""]], [["“Nothing,”", "\"아무것도 아니에요.\""], ["she said, coming from behind the screen with a candle in her hand.", "그녀가 손에 촛불을 들고 칸막이 뒤에서 나오며 말했다."], ["“I felt unwell,”", "\"몸이 좀 안 좋았어요.\""], ["she said, smiling a particularly sweet and meaning smile.", "그녀는 유난히 달콤하고 의미심장한 미소를 지으며 말했다."]], [["“What?", "\"뭐?"], ["has it begun?”", "시작된 거야?\""], ["he said in terror.", "그가 겁에 질려 말했다."], ["“We ought to send....”", "\"사람을 보내야…….\""], ["and hurriedly he reached after his clothes.", "그러고는 황급히 옷을 더듬어 찾았다."]], [["“No, no,”", "\"아니, 아니에요.\""], ["she said, smiling and holding his hand.", "그녀가 미소를 지으며 그의 손을 잡았다."], ["“It’s sure to be nothing.", "\"분명 아무것도 아닐 거예요."], ["I was rather unwell, only a little.", "좀 안 좋았을 뿐이에요, 아주 조금."], ["It’s all over now.”", "이제 다 괜찮아요.\""]], [["And getting into bed, she blew out the candle, lay down and was still.", "그러고는 침대로 들어와 촛불을 끄고 누워 가만히 있었다."], ["Though he thought her stillness suspicious, as though she were holding her breath, and still more suspicious the expression of peculiar tenderness and excitement with which, as she came from behind the screen, she said “nothing,” he was so sleepy that he fell asleep at once.", "그는 마치 그녀가 숨을 죽이고 있기라도 한 듯 그 고요함이 수상쩍게 느껴졌고, 칸막이 뒤에서 나오며 \"아무것도 아니에요\"라고 말할 때의 그 유난히 다정하고 들뜬 표정은 더욱 수상쩍게 느껴졌지만, 너무도 졸린 나머지 곧바로 잠들어 버렸다."], ["Only later he remembered the stillness of her breathing, and understood all that must have been passing in her sweet, precious heart while she lay beside him, not stirring, in anticipation of the greatest event in a woman’s life.", "나중에야 그는 그녀의 고른 숨소리를 떠올렸고, 그녀가 미동도 없이 그의 곁에 누워, 한 여인의 삶에서 가장 위대한 사건을 기다리는 동안, 그 사랑스럽고 소중한 가슴속에서 무슨 일이 일어나고 있었을지를 깨달았다."], ["At seven o’clock he was waked by the touch of her hand on his shoulder, and a gentle whisper.", "일곱 시에 그는 그의 어깨에 닿는 그녀의 손길과 부드러운 속삭임에 잠을 깼다."], ["She seemed struggling between regret at waking him, and the desire to talk to him.", "그녀는 그를 깨운 것을 미안해하는 마음과 그에게 말을 하고 싶은 마음 사이에서 갈등하는 듯했다."]], [["“Kostya, don’t be frightened.", "\"코스챠, 놀라지 마세요. 괜찮아요."], ["It’s all right.", "그런데 아무래도……"], ["But I fancy.... We ought to send for Lizaveta Petrovna.”", "리자베타 페트로브나를 부르러 보내야 할 것 같아요.\""]], [["The candle was lighted again.", "촛불이 다시 켜졌다."], ["She was sitting up in bed, holding some knitting, which she had been busy upon during the last few days.", "그녀는 침대에 일어나 앉아, 지난 며칠 동안 부지런히 뜨고 있던 뜨개질감을 손에 들고 있었다."]], [["“Please, don’t be frightened, it’s all right.", "\"제발, 놀라지 마세요, 괜찮아요."], ["I’m not a bit afraid,”", "저는 조금도 무섭지 않아요.\""], ["she said, seeing his scared face, and she pressed his hand to her bosom and then to her lips.", "그녀가 그의 겁먹은 얼굴을 보며 말했고, 그의 손을 자기 가슴에 댔다가 입술로 가져갔다."]], [["He hurriedly jumped up, hardly awake, and kept his eyes fixed on her, as he put on his dressing gown; then he stopped, still looking at her.", "그는 잠에서 채 깨지도 못한 채 황급히 벌떡 일어났고, 실내복을 걸치면서 그녀에게서 눈을 떼지 못했다. 그러다 멈춰 서서 여전히 그녀를 바라보았다."], ["He had to go, but he could not tear himself from her eyes.", "가야만 했지만 그는 그녀의 눈에서 자신을 떼어 낼 수가 없었다."], ["He thought he loved her face, knew her expression, her eyes, but never had he seen it like this.", "그는 자기가 그녀의 얼굴을 사랑하고, 그녀의 표정을, 그녀의 눈을 안다고 여겼지만, 이런 모습은 한 번도 본 적이 없었다."], ["How hateful and horrible he seemed to himself, thinking of the distress he had caused her yesterday.", "어제 그가 그녀에게 안겨 준 괴로움을 떠올리니, 자기 자신이 얼마나 가증스럽고 끔찍하게 느껴지던지."], ["Her flushed face, fringed with soft curling hair under her night cap, was radiant with joy and courage.", "나이트캡 아래로 부드럽게 곱슬거리는 머리카락이 드리워진 그녀의 발갛게 상기된 얼굴은 기쁨과 용기로 빛나고 있었다."]], [["Though there was so little that was complex or artificial in Kitty’s character in general, Levin was struck by what was revealed now, when suddenly all disguises were thrown off and the very kernel of her soul shone in her eyes.", "키티의 성격에는 본래 복잡하거나 꾸민 데가 거의 없었지만, 별안간 모든 가식이 벗겨지고 그녀 영혼의 바로 그 알맹이가 두 눈에 빛나는 지금, 드러난 그 모습에 레빈은 충격을 받았다."], ["And in this simplicity and nakedness of her soul, she, the very woman he loved in her, was more manifest than ever.", "그리고 이 단순함과 영혼의 적나라함 속에서, 그가 그녀에게서 사랑하던 바로 그 여인이 그 어느 때보다 또렷하게 드러났다."], ["She looked at him, smiling; but all at once her brows twitched, she threw up her head, and going quickly up to him, clutched his hand and pressed close up to him, breathing her hot breath upon him.", "그녀는 미소를 지으며 그를 바라보았다. 그런데 별안간 그녀의 눈썹이 씰룩거렸고, 그녀는 고개를 젖히더니 재빨리 그에게 다가와 그의 손을 움켜쥐고 그에게 바짝 붙어 뜨거운 숨을 그에게 내쉬었다."], ["She was in pain and was, as it were, complaining to him of her suffering.", "그녀는 고통스러워하며, 말하자면 자기 괴로움을 그에게 하소연하고 있었다."], ["And for the first minute, from habit, it seemed to him that he was to blame.", "그리고 첫 순간에는 습관처럼, 그에게는 자기가 잘못한 것만 같았다."], ["But in her eyes there was a tenderness that told him that she was far from reproaching him, that she loved him for her sufferings.", "그러나 그녀의 눈에는 그녀가 결코 그를 탓하지 않으며, 자기 괴로움 때문에 오히려 그를 사랑한다는 것을 말해 주는 다정함이 어려 있었다."], ["“If not I, who is to blame for it?”", "'내가 아니라면, 대체 누가 이 일에 책임이 있단 말인가?'"], ["he thought unconsciously, seeking someone responsible for this suffering for him to punish; but there was no one responsible.", "그는 무의식중에, 벌하기 위해 이 괴로움에 책임 있는 누군가를 찾으며 생각했다. 그러나 책임 있는 자는 아무도 없었다."], ["She was suffering, complaining, and triumphing in her sufferings, and rejoicing in them, and loving them.", "그녀는 괴로워하고, 하소연하고, 자기 괴로움 속에서 의기양양해하고, 그것을 기뻐하고, 그것을 사랑하고 있었다."], ["He saw that something sublime was being accomplished in her soul, but what?", "그는 그녀의 영혼 속에서 무언가 숭고한 일이 이루어지고 있음을 보았다. 그러나 무엇일까?"], ["He could not make it out.", "그는 도무지 알 수 없었다."], ["It was beyond his understanding.", "그것은 그의 이해를 넘어서는 일이었다."]], [["“I have sent to mamma.", "\"엄마한테는 사람을 보냈어요."], ["You go quickly to fetch Lizaveta Petrovna ...", "당신은 어서 리자베타 페트로브나를 데리러 가세요……"], ["Kostya!...", "코스챠!……"], ["Nothing, it’s over.”", "아무것도 아니에요, 다 지나갔어요.\""]], [["She moved away from him and rang the bell.", "그녀는 그에게서 떨어져 벨을 울렸다."]], [["“Well, go now; Pasha’s coming.", "\"자, 이제 가세요. 파샤가 오고 있어요."], ["I am all right.”", "저는 괜찮아요.\""]], [["And Levin saw with astonishment that she had taken up the knitting she had brought in in the night and begun working at it again.", "그리고 레빈은 그녀가 밤중에 가지고 들어왔던 뜨개질감을 다시 집어 들고 또다시 뜨기 시작하는 것을 보고는 깜짝 놀랐다."]], [["As Levin was going out of one door, he heard the maid-servant come in at the other.", "레빈이 한쪽 문으로 나가는데, 다른 쪽 문으로 하녀가 들어오는 소리가 들렸다."], ["He stood at the door and heard Kitty giving exact directions to the maid, and beginning to help her move the bedstead.", "그는 문가에 서서, 키티가 하녀에게 정확하게 지시를 내리고 침대 옮기는 일을 거들기 시작하는 소리를 들었다."]], [["He dressed, and while they were putting in his horses, as a hired sledge was not to be seen yet, he ran again up to the bedroom, not on tiptoe, it seemed to him, but on wings.", "그는 옷을 입었고, 말을 마차에 매는 동안—아직 삯마차 썰매는 보이지 않았다—다시 침실로 뛰어 올라갔는데, 발끝으로 가는 것이 아니라 날아가는 것 같은 기분이었다."], ["Two maid-servants were carefully moving something in the bedroom.", "하녀 둘이 침실에서 무언가를 조심스럽게 옮기고 있었다."]], [["Kitty was walking about knitting rapidly and giving directions.", "키티는 빠르게 뜨개질을 하며 이리저리 돌아다니며 지시를 내리고 있었다."]], [["“I’m going for the doctor.", "\"나는 의사를 부르러 갈게."], ["They have sent for Lizaveta Petrovna, but I’ll go on there too.", "리자베타 페트로브나한테는 사람을 보냈지만, 나도 거기 들렀다 올게."], ["Isn’t there anything wanted?", "필요한 거 없어?"], ["Yes, shall I go to Dolly’s?”", "그래, 돌리한테 갈까?\""]], [["She looked at him, obviously not hearing what he was saying.", "그녀는 그를 보았지만, 분명 그가 하는 말을 듣고 있지 않았다."]], [["“Yes, yes.", "\"네, 네."], ["Do go,”", "다녀오세요.\""], ["she said quickly, frowning and waving her hand to him.", "그녀가 미간을 찌푸리고 그에게 손을 흔들며 재빨리 말했다."]], [["He had just gone into the drawing-room, when suddenly a plaintive moan sounded from the bedroom, smothered instantly.", "그가 막 응접실로 들어섰을 때, 침실에서 별안간 애처로운 신음 소리가 들려왔다가 이내 잦아들었다."], ["He stood still, and for a long while he could not understand.", "그는 우뚝 멈춰 섰고, 한참 동안 영문을 알 수 없었다."]], [["“Yes, that is she,”", "'그래, 저건 그녀야.'"], ["he said to himself, and clutching at his head he ran downstairs.", "그는 혼잣말을 하고는 머리를 움켜쥐고 아래층으로 뛰어 내려갔다."]], [["“Lord have mercy on us!", "\"주여, 우리에게 자비를 베푸소서!"], ["pardon us! aid us!”", "우리를 용서하소서!"], ["he repeated the words that for some reason came suddenly to his lips.", "우리를 도우소서!\" 그는 무슨 까닭인지 별안간 입술에 떠오른 말을 되뇌었다."], ["And he, an unbeliever, repeated these words not with his lips only.", "그리고 신을 믿지 않는 그가 이 말을 입술로만 되뇌는 것이 아니었다."], ["At that instant he knew that all his doubts, even the impossibility of believing with his reason, of which he was aware in himself, did not in the least hinder his turning to God.", "그 순간 그는, 자기 안에서 스스로 의식하고 있던 온갖 의심도, 심지어 이성으로는 믿을 수 없다는 사실조차도, 그가 신에게 의지하는 것을 조금도 가로막지 못한다는 것을 알았다."], ["All of that now floated out of his soul like dust.", "그 모든 것이 이제 먼지처럼 그의 영혼에서 떠나가 버렸다."], ["To whom was he to turn if not to Him in whose hands he felt himself, his soul, and his love?", "자기 자신과 자기 영혼과 자기 사랑이 그분의 손안에 있음을 느끼는 지금, 그분이 아니라면 누구에게 의지한단 말인가?"]], [["The horse was not yet ready, but feeling a peculiar concentration of his physical forces and his intellect on what he had to do, he started off on foot without waiting for the horse, and told Kouzma to overtake him.", "말은 아직 준비되지 않았지만, 그는 해야 할 일에 자기 육체의 힘과 지력이 유달리 집중되는 것을 느끼며, 말을 기다리지 않고 걸어서 나섰고, 쿠즈마에게 뒤따라오라고 일렀다."]], [["At the corner he met a night cabman driving hurriedly.", "길모퉁이에서 그는 황급히 마차를 모는 야간 마부와 마주쳤다."], ["In the little sledge, wrapped in a velvet cloak, sat Lizaveta Petrovna with a kerchief round her head.", "작은 썰매 안에는 벨벳 외투를 두른 리자베타 페트로브나가 머리에 두건을 감고 앉아 있었다."], ["“Thank God!", "\"고맙기도 하지!"], ["thank God!”", "고맙기도 해!\""], ["he said, overjoyed to recognize her little fair face which wore a peculiarly serious, even stern expression.", "그는 유난히 진지하다 못해 엄숙하기까지 한 표정을 띤 그녀의 작고 희끄무레한 얼굴을 알아보고는 기쁨에 차 말했다."], ["Telling the driver not to stop, he ran along beside her.", "마부에게 멈추지 말라고 이르고는, 그녀의 곁을 따라 달렸다."]], [["“For two hours, then?", "\"그럼 두 시간이라고요?"], ["Not more?” she inquired.", "그보다 길지는 않고요?\""], ["“You should let Pyotr Dmitrievitch know, but don’t hurry him.", "그녀가 물었다. \"표트르 드미트리예비치한테 알리시되, 서두르게 하지는 마세요."], ["And get some opium at the chemist’s.”", "그리고 약국에 가서 아편을 좀 사 오세요.\""]], [["“So you think that it may go on well?", "\"그럼 잘될 거라고 보시는 거죠?"], ["Lord have mercy on us and help us!”", "주여, 우리에게 자비를 베푸시고 우리를 도우소서!\""], ["Levin said, seeing his own horse driving out of the gate.", "레빈은 자기 말이 대문을 나서는 것을 보며 말했다."], ["Jumping into the sledge beside Kouzma, he told him to drive to the doctor’s.", "쿠즈마 곁의 썰매에 뛰어올라, 그는 의사의 집으로 가자고 일렀다."]], [["# Chapter 14", "# Chapter 14"]], [["The doctor was not yet up, and the footman said that “he had been up late, and had given orders not to be waked, but would get up soon.”", "의사는 아직 일어나지 않았고, 하인은 \"선생님이 늦게 주무셨고 깨우지 말라고 분부하셨지만 곧 일어나실 겁니다.\"라고 말했다."], ["The footman was cleaning the lamp-chimneys, and seemed very busy about them.", "하인은 램프 등피를 닦고 있었는데 그 일에 무척 몰두해 있는 듯했다."], ["This concentration of the footman upon his lamps, and his indifference to what was passing in Levin, at first astounded him, but immediately on considering the question he realized that no one knew or was bound to know his feelings, and that it was all the more necessary to act calmly, sensibly, and resolutely to get through this wall of indifference and attain his aim.", "하인이 자기 램프에만 이토록 집중해 있고 레빈에게 일어난 일에는 무관심하다는 사실이 처음에는 그를 어리둥절하게 했지만, 곧 그 문제를 헤아려 보고는, 아무도 자기 심정을 알지 못하고 또 알아야 할 의무도 없으며, 그렇기에 이 무관심의 벽을 뚫고 목적을 이루려면 더더욱 침착하고 분별 있고 단호하게 행동할 필요가 있다는 것을 깨달았다."]], [["“Don’t be in a hurry or let anything slip,”", "\"서두르지 말고, 어떤 것도 놓치지 말자.\""], ["Levin said to himself, feeling a greater and greater flow of physical energy and attention to all that lay before him to do.", "레빈은 자기 앞에 놓인 해야 할 모든 일에 대해 점점 더 강해지는 육체적 활력과 주의력을 느끼며 혼잣말을 했다."]], [["Having ascertained that the doctor was not getting up, Levin considered various plans, and decided on the following one: that Kouzma should go for another doctor, while he himself should go to the chemist’s for opium, and if when he came back the doctor had not yet begun to get up, he would either by tipping the footman, or by force, wake the doctor at all hazards.", "의사가 일어나지 않으리라는 것을 확인한 레빈은 여러 가지 방안을 궁리한 끝에 다음과 같이 정했다. 쿠지마는 다른 의사를 부르러 가고, 자기는 약국에 아편을 사러 가며, 돌아왔을 때도 의사가 아직 일어나지 않았으면 하인에게 돈을 찔러주든 힘을 쓰든 무슨 수를 써서라도 의사를 깨우기로 했다."]], [["At the chemist’s the lank shopman sealed up a packet of powders for a coachman who stood waiting, and refused him opium with the same callousness with which the doctor’s footman had cleaned his lamp chimneys.", "약국에서는 키만 멀쑥한 점원이 기다리고 서 있는 한 마부에게 줄 가루약 봉지를 봉하고 있었는데, 의사의 하인이 램프 등피를 닦던 것과 똑같은 무신경함으로 그에게 아편을 내주기를 거절했다."], ["Trying not to get flurried or out of temper, Levin mentioned the names of the doctor and midwife, and explaining what the opium was needed for, tried to persuade him.", "레빈은 허둥대거나 화내지 않으려 애쓰며 의사와 산파의 이름을 대고, 아편이 무엇에 필요한지 설명하며 그를 설득하려 했다."], ["The assistant inquired in German whether he should give it, and receiving an affirmative reply from behind the partition, he took out a bottle and a funnel, deliberately poured the opium from a bigger bottle into a little one, stuck on a label, sealed it up, in spite of Levin’s request that he would not do so, and was about to wrap it up too.", "점원은 독일어로 내주어도 되겠느냐고 물었고, 칸막이 너머에서 그렇게 하라는 대답을 듣자 병 하나와 깔때기를 꺼내 큰 병에서 작은 병으로 천천히 아편을 따르고는 상표를 붙이고, 그러지 말아 달라는 레빈의 부탁에도 아랑곳없이 봉인까지 한 다음, 그것을 포장하려고까지 했다."], ["This was more than Levin could stand; he took the bottle firmly out of his hands, and ran to the big glass doors.", "이것만은 레빈도 더 참을 수가 없었다. 그는 점원의 손에서 병을 단호히 빼앗아 큰 유리문 쪽으로 달려갔다."], ["The doctor was not even now getting up, and the footman, busy now in putting down the rugs, refused to wake him.", "의사는 여전히 일어나지 않았고, 이제 깔개를 깔며 분주한 하인은 그를 깨우려 하지 않았다."], ["Levin deliberately took out a ten rouble note, and, careful to speak slowly, though losing no time over the business, he handed him the note, and explained that Pyotr Dmitrievitch (what a great and important personage he seemed to Levin now, this Pyotr Dmitrievitch, who had been of so little consequence in his eyes before!) had promised to come at any time; that he would certainly not be angry! and that he must therefore wake him at once.", "레빈은 천천히 십 루블짜리 지폐를 꺼내, 일 처리에 시간을 끌지 않으면서도 또박또박 말하려고 신경 쓰며, 그에게 지폐를 건네고는 표트르 드미트리치(전에는 그의 눈에 그토록 하찮게 보였던 이 표트르 드미트리치가 지금은 얼마나 위대하고 중요한 인물로 여겨지는지!)가 언제든 오겠다고 약속했으며, 절대 화내지 않을 것이니 지금 당장 깨워야 한다고 설명했다."]], [["The footman agreed, and went upstairs, taking Levin into the waiting room.", "하인은 동의했고, 레빈을 대기실로 안내한 뒤 위층으로 올라갔다."]], [["Levin could hear through the door the doctor coughing, moving about, washing, and saying something.", "레빈은 문 너머로 의사가 기침을 하고 이리저리 움직이며 세수를 하고 무언가 말하는 소리를 들을 수 있었다."], ["Three minutes passed; it seemed to Levin that more than an hour had gone by.", "삼 분이 지났다. 레빈에게는 한 시간도 더 흐른 것만 같았다."], ["He could not wait any longer.", "그는 더 이상 기다릴 수 없었다."]], [["“Pyotr Dmitrievitch, Pyotr Dmitrievitch!”", "\"표트르 드미트리치, 표트르 드미트리치!\""], ["he said in an imploring voice at the open door.", "그는 열린 문가에서 애원하는 목소리로 말했다."], ["“For God’s sake, forgive me!", "\"제발 용서하십시오!"], ["See me as you are.", "그냥 그대로 좀 나와 주십시오."], ["It’s been going on more than two hours already.”", "벌써 두 시간도 넘게 이러고 있단 말입니다.\""]], [["“In a minute; in a minute!”", "\"금방, 금방 갑니다!\""], ["answered a voice, and to his amazement Levin heard that the doctor was smiling as he spoke.", "한 목소리가 대답했고, 놀랍게도 레빈은 의사가 말하면서 미소 짓고 있다는 것을 알아챘다."]], [["“For one instant.”", "\"잠깐만이라도요.\""]], [["“In a minute.”", "\"금방 갑니다.\""]], [["Two minutes more passed while the doctor was putting on his boots, and two minutes more while the doctor put on his coat and combed his hair.", "의사가 부츠를 신는 동안 다시 이 분이 지났고, 외투를 걸치고 머리를 빗는 동안 또 이 분이 지났다."]], [["“Pyotr Dmitrievitch!”", "\"표트르 드미트리치!\""], ["Levin was beginning again in a plaintive voice, just as the doctor came in dressed and ready.", "레빈이 다시 하소연하는 목소리로 입을 떼려는데, 마침 의사가 옷을 차려입고 준비를 마친 채 들어왔다."], ["“These people have no conscience,”", "\"이 사람들은 양심이라곤 없어.\""], ["thought Levin.", "레빈은 생각했다."], ["“Combing his hair, while we’re dying!”", "\"우리는 죽어 가는데 머리나 빗고 있다니!\""]], [["“Good morning!”", "\"안녕하십니까!\""], ["the doctor said to him, shaking hands, and, as it were, teasing him with his composure.", "의사가 그에게 악수를 청하며, 마치 그 침착함으로 그를 약 올리기라도 하듯 말했다."], ["“There’s no hurry.", "\"서두를 것 없습니다."], ["Well now?”", "자, 어떻습니까?\""]], [["Trying to be as accurate as possible, Levin began to tell him every unnecessary detail of his wife’s condition, interrupting his account repeatedly with entreaties that the doctor would come with him at once.", "레빈은 되도록 정확하게 말하려 애쓰며 아내의 상태를 시시콜콜한 데까지 죄다 늘어놓기 시작했고, 이야기를 하다 말고 거듭 당장 같이 가 달라고 애원했다."]], [["“Oh, you needn’t be in any hurry.", "\"아, 서두르실 것 없습니다."], ["You don’t understand, you know.", "모르셔서 그러는데요, 분명 제가 필요하지도 않을 겁니다."], ["I’m certain I’m not wanted, still I’ve promised, and if you like, I’ll come.", "그래도 약속을 했으니, 원하신다면 가지요."], ["But there’s no hurry.", "하지만 급할 것 없어요."], ["Please sit down; won’t you have some coffee?”", "자, 앉으세요. 커피 한잔 드시겠습니까?\""]], [["Levin stared at him with eyes that asked whether he was laughing at him; but the doctor had no notion of making fun of him.", "레빈은 자기를 놀리는 것이냐고 묻는 듯한 눈으로 그를 빤히 바라보았지만, 의사는 그를 놀릴 생각 따위는 전혀 없었다."]], [["“I know, I know,”", "\"압니다, 알아요.\""], ["the doctor said, smiling; “I’m a married man myself; and at these moments we husbands are very much to be pitied.", "의사가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"저도 결혼한 몸이지요."], ["I’ve a patient whose husband always takes refuge in the stables on such occasions.”", "이런 순간이면 우리 남편들은 참 가엾은 신세랍니다. 제 환자 중에는 이럴 때면 남편이 늘 마구간으로 피신해 버리는 집도 있지요.\""]], [["“But what do you think, Pyotr Dmitrievitch?", "\"하지만 어떻게 보십니까, 표트르 드미트리치?"], ["Do you suppose it may go all right?”", "다 잘되리라고 생각하십니까?\""]], [["“Everything points to a favorable issue.”", "\"모든 정황이 좋은 결과를 가리키고 있습니다.\""]], [["“So you’ll come immediately?”", "\"그럼 곧장 가시는 거죠?\""], ["said Levin, looking wrathfully at the servant who was bringing in the coffee.", "레빈이 커피를 들고 들어오는 하인을 노한 눈으로 쏘아보며 말했다."]], [["“In an hour’s time.”", "\"한 시간 안에요.\""]], [["“Oh, for mercy’s sake!”", "\"오, 제발 좀!\""]], [["“Well, let me drink my coffee, anyway.”", "\"아무튼 커피나 좀 마시게 해 주십시오.\""]], [["The doctor started upon his coffee.", "의사는 커피를 마시기 시작했다."], ["Both were silent.", "두 사람 다 말이 없었다."]], [["“The Turks are really getting beaten, though.", "\"그런데 터키군이 정말로 지고 있더군요."], ["Did you read yesterday’s telegrams?”", "어제 전보 보셨습니까?\""], ["said the doctor, munching some roll.", "의사가 빵을 우물거리며 말했다."]], [["“No, I can’t stand it!”", "\"아니요, 더는 못 참겠습니다!\""], ["said Levin, jumping up.", "레빈이 벌떡 일어나며 말했다."], ["“So you’ll be with us in a quarter of an hour.”", "\"그럼 십오 분 안에 우리 집으로 와 주시는 거죠.\""]], [["“In half an hour.”", "\"삼십 분 안에요.\""]], [["“On your honor?”", "\"명예를 걸고요?\""]], [["When Levin got home, he drove up at the same time as the princess, and they went up to the bedroom door together.", "레빈이 집에 도착했을 때 마침 공작 부인도 같은 시각에 마차를 타고 왔고, 두 사람은 함께 침실 문으로 올라갔다."], ["The princess had tears in her eyes, and her hands were shaking.", "공작 부인의 눈에는 눈물이 고여 있었고 손은 떨리고 있었다."], ["Seeing Levin, she embraced him, and burst into tears.", "레빈을 보자 그녀는 그를 끌어안고 울음을 터뜨렸다."]], [["“Well, my dear Lizaveta Petrovna?”", "\"자, 어떤가요, 리자베타 페트로브나?\""], ["she queried, clasping the hand of the midwife, who came out to meet them with a beaming and anxious face.", "그녀가 환하면서도 근심 어린 얼굴로 그들을 맞으러 나온 산파의 손을 잡으며 물었다."]], [["“She’s going on well,”", "\"잘 진행되고 있어요.\""], ["she said; “persuade her to lie down.", "산파가 말했다. \"아씨께 좀 누우시라고 권해 주세요."], ["She will be easier so.”", "그게 한결 편하실 거예요.\""]], [["From the moment when he had waked up and understood what was going on, Levin had prepared his mind to bear resolutely what was before him, and without considering or anticipating anything, to avoid upsetting his wife, and on the contrary to soothe her and keep up her courage.", "잠에서 깨어 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지 알게 된 순간부터, 레빈은 앞에 닥친 일을 단호히 견뎌 내고, 이런저런 생각이나 예측을 하지 않으며, 아내를 동요시키지 않고 오히려 그녀를 달래며 용기를 북돋워 주리라 마음을 다잡았다."], ["Without allowing himself even to think of what was to come, of how it would end, judging from his inquiries as to the usual duration of these ordeals, Levin had in his imagination braced himself to bear up and to keep a tight rein on his feelings for five hours, and it had seemed to him he could do this.", "앞으로 어떻게 될지, 어떻게 끝날지조차 생각하지 않으려 애쓰며, 이런 산고가 보통 얼마나 오래 가는지 물어본 바에 비추어, 레빈은 다섯 시간 동안 굳세게 버티고 감정을 단단히 틀어쥐겠다고 마음속으로 각오를 다졌고, 그것은 자기로서도 해낼 수 있을 것만 같았다."], ["But when he came back from the doctor’s and saw her sufferings again, he fell to repeating more and more frequently: “Lord, have mercy on us, and succor us!”", "그러나 의사에게서 돌아와 다시 그녀의 고통을 보자, 그는 점점 더 자주 되뇌기 시작했다. \"주여, 우리를 불쌍히 여기시고, 우리를 도우소서!\""], ["He sighed, and flung his head up, and began to feel afraid he could not bear it, that he would burst into tears or run away.", "그는 한숨을 쉬며 고개를 번쩍 쳐들었고, 자기가 이것을 견뎌 내지 못할 것 같다는, 그만 울음을 터뜨리거나 달아나 버릴 것 같다는 두려움에 휩싸이기 시작했다. 그에게는 그것이 그토록 큰 고통이었다."], ["Such agony it was to him. And only one hour had passed.", "그런데 이제 겨우 한 시간이 지났을 뿐이었다."]], [["But after that hour there passed another hour, two hours, three, the full five hours he had fixed as the furthest limit of his sufferings, and the position was still unchanged; and he was still bearing it because there was nothing to be done but bear it; every instant feeling that he had reached the utmost limits of his endurance, and that his heart would break with sympathy and pain.", "그러나 그 한 시간이 지나고 다시 한 시간이, 두 시간이, 세 시간이 지나고, 그가 자기 고통의 마지막 한계로 정해 둔 다섯 시간이 꼬박 다 지나도록 상황은 여전히 그대로였다. 그리고 그는 견디는 것 말고는 달리 할 수 있는 일이 없었기에 여전히 견디고 있었으며, 매 순간 자기 인내의 한계에 다다랐다고, 동정과 고통으로 심장이 터져 버릴 것만 같다고 느꼈다."]], [["But still the minutes passed by and the hours, and still hours more, and his misery and horror grew and were more and more intense.", "그러나 여전히 분이 지나고 시간이 흐르고, 또 시간이 더 흘러갔으며, 그의 비참함과 공포는 점점 더 커져 더욱더 격렬해졌다."]], [["All the ordinary conditions of life, without which one can form no conception of anything, had ceased to exist for Levin.", "그 무엇에 대해서도 도무지 아무런 관념을 가질 수 없게 만드는, 삶의 온갖 일상적인 조건이 레빈에게는 더 이상 존재하지 않게 되었다."], ["He lost all sense of time.", "그는 시간 감각을 완전히 잃어버렸다."], ["Minutes—those minutes when she sent for him and he held her moist hand, that would squeeze his hand with extraordinary violence and then push it away—seemed to him hours, and hours seemed to him minutes.", "몇 분—그녀가 그를 부르고 그가 그녀의 축축한 손을 잡으면 그 손이 엄청난 힘으로 그의 손을 움켜쥐었다가 다시 밀쳐 내던 그 몇 분—은 그에게 몇 시간처럼 여겨졌고, 몇 시간은 또 몇 분처럼 여겨졌다."], ["He was surprised when Lizaveta Petrovna asked him to light a candle behind a screen, and he found that it was five o’clock in the afternoon.", "리자베타 페트로브나가 그에게 칸막이 뒤에서 촛불을 켜 달라고 부탁했을 때, 그는 오후 다섯 시라는 것을 알고는 놀랐다."], ["If he had been told it was only ten o’clock in the morning, he would not have been more surprised.", "아직 아침 열 시밖에 안 되었다고 했더라도 더 놀라지는 않았을 것이다."], ["Where he was all this time, he knew as little as the time of anything.", "그동안 자기가 어디에 있었는지, 그는 무슨 일이 언제 일어났는지만큼이나 알지 못했다."], ["He saw her swollen face, sometimes bewildered and in agony, sometimes smiling and trying to reassure him.", "그는 그녀의 부어오른 얼굴을 보았는데, 어떤 때는 어리둥절하고 고통에 차 있었고 어떤 때는 미소 지으며 그를 안심시키려 애썼다."], ["He saw the old princess too, flushed and overwrought, with her gray curls in disorder, forcing herself to gulp down her tears, biting her lips; he saw Dolly too and the doctor, smoking fat cigarettes, and Lizaveta Petrovna with a firm, resolute, reassuring face, and the old prince walking up and down the hall with a frowning face.", "그는 늙은 공작 부인도 보았는데, 얼굴이 벌게져 잔뜩 흥분한 채 잿빛 곱슬머리는 헝클어졌고 입술을 깨물며 억지로 눈물을 삼키고 있었다. 그는 돌리와 살찐 담배를 피우는 의사, 그리고 굳고 단호하며 안심시키는 듯한 얼굴을 한 리자베타 페트로브나, 그리고 찌푸린 얼굴로 홀을 오락가락 거니는 늙은 공작도 보았다."], ["But why they came in and went out, where they were, he did not know.", "그러나 그들이 왜 들어왔다 나가는지, 그들이 어디에 있는지 그는 알지 못했다."], ["The princess was with the doctor in the bedroom, then in the study, where a table set for dinner suddenly appeared; then she was not there, but Dolly was.", "공작 부인은 의사와 함께 침실에 있다가, 그다음에는 어느새 저녁 식탁이 차려진 서재에 있었고, 그러다 보면 그녀는 거기에 없고 돌리가 있었다."], ["Then Levin remembered he had been sent somewhere.", "그러다 레빈은 자기가 어딘가로 심부름을 보내졌던 것을 떠올렸다."], ["Once he had been sent to move a table and sofa.", "한번은 탁자와 소파를 옮기라는 심부름을 받았다."], ["He had done this eagerly, thinking it had to be done for her sake, and only later on he found it was his own bed he had been getting ready.", "그는 그것이 그녀를 위해 해야 할 일이라고 여기며 열심히 했는데, 나중에야 그것이 자기가 잘 자리를 준비하던 것이었음을 알았다."], ["Then he had been sent to the study to ask the doctor something.", "그다음에는 의사에게 무언가를 물어보러 서재로 보내졌다."], ["The doctor had answered and then had said something about the irregularities in the municipal council.", "의사는 대답을 하고는 시의회의 부정에 관해 무슨 이야기를 했다."], ["Then he had been sent to the bedroom to help the old princess to move the holy picture in its silver and gold setting, and with the princess’s old waiting maid he had clambered on a shelf to reach it and had broken the little lamp, and the old servant had tried to reassure him about the lamp and about his wife, and he carried the holy picture and set it at Kitty’s head, carefully tucking it in behind the pillow.", "그다음에는 늙은 공작 부인이 은과 금으로 장식된 성화를 옮기는 것을 도우러 침실로 보내졌고, 공작 부인의 늙은 시녀와 함께 그것에 손이 닿으려고 선반 위로 기어올랐다가 작은 등을 깨뜨렸으며, 늙은 하녀는 등에 대해서도 그의 아내에 대해서도 그를 안심시키려 애썼고, 그는 성화를 들어 키티의 머리맡에 가져다 놓으며 베개 뒤에 조심스럽게 끼워 넣었다."], ["But where, when, and why all this had happened, he could not tell.", "그러나 이 모든 일이 어디서, 언제, 왜 일어났는지 그는 도무지 알 수 없었다."], ["He did not understand why the old princess took his hand, and looking compassionately at him, begged him not to worry himself, and Dolly persuaded him to eat something and led him out of the room, and even the doctor looked seriously and with commiseration at him and offered him a drop of something.", "그는 늙은 공작 부인이 왜 자기 손을 잡고 가엾다는 듯 그를 바라보며 너무 걱정하지 말라고 당부하는지, 돌리가 왜 무언가 좀 먹으라고 권하며 그를 방 밖으로 데리고 나가는지, 심지어 의사마저 왜 진지하고 측은한 눈길로 그를 바라보며 무슨 물약 같은 것을 한 방울 권하는지 이해하지 못했다."]], [["All he knew and felt was that what was happening was what had happened nearly a year before in the hotel of the country town at the deathbed of his brother Nikolay.", "그가 알고 느끼는 것이라곤, 지금 벌어지고 있는 이 일이 거의 일 년 전 어느 지방 소도시의 여관에서 형 니콜라이의 임종 자리에서 일어났던 일과 같다는 것뿐이었다."], ["But that had been grief—this was joy.", "그러나 그것은 슬픔이었고 이것은 기쁨이었다."], ["Yet that grief and this joy were alike outside all the ordinary conditions of life; they were loop-holes, as it were, in that ordinary life through which there came glimpses of something sublime.", "하지만 그 슬픔도 이 기쁨도 똑같이 삶의 온갖 일상적인 조건 바깥에 있었다. 그것들은 말하자면 그 일상적인 삶에 난 틈새 같은 것이어서, 그 틈새를 통해 무언가 숭고한 것이 언뜻언뜻 비쳐 들었다."], ["And in the contemplation of this sublime something the soul was exalted to inconceivable heights of which it had before had no conception, while reason lagged behind, unable to keep up with it.", "그리고 이 숭고한 무언가를 응시하는 가운데 영혼은 이전에는 한 번도 헤아려 본 적 없는, 상상할 수도 없는 높이로 고양되었고, 이성은 그 뒤에 처져 도무지 따라잡지 못했다."]], [["“Lord, have mercy on us, and succor us!”", "\"주여, 우리를 불쌍히 여기시고, 우리를 도우소서!\""], ["he repeated to himself incessantly, feeling, in spite of his long and, as it seemed, complete alienation from religion, that he turned to God just as trustfully and simply as he had in his childhood and first youth.", "그는 끊임없이 혼자 되뇌었고, 종교로부터 오래, 그리고 보기에는 완전히 멀어졌음에도, 자기가 어린 시절과 첫 청년기에 그랬던 것과 똑같이 미덥고 단순하게 하느님께 돌아서고 있음을 느꼈다."]], [["All this time he had two distinct spiritual conditions.", "이 시간 내내 그에게는 뚜렷이 구분되는 두 가지 정신 상태가 있었다."], ["One was away from her, with the doctor, who kept smoking one fat cigarette after another and extinguishing them on the edge of a full ashtray, with Dolly, and with the old prince, where there was talk about dinner, about politics, about Marya Petrovna’s illness, and where Levin suddenly forgot for a minute what was happening, and felt as though he had waked up from sleep; the other was in her presence, at her pillow, where his heart seemed breaking and still did not break from sympathetic suffering, and he prayed to God without ceasing.", "하나는 그녀에게서 떨어져, 살찐 담배를 하나 또 하나 끊임없이 피워 대고는 가득 찬 재떨이 가장자리에 비벼 끄는 의사와, 돌리와, 늙은 공작과 함께 있을 때였는데, 거기서는 저녁 식사며 정치며 마리야 페트로브나의 병에 관한 이야기가 오갔고, 레빈은 문득 일 분쯤 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지 잊은 채 마치 잠에서 깨어난 것처럼 느꼈다. 다른 하나는 그녀 곁, 그녀의 머리맡에 있을 때였는데, 거기서는 그의 심장이 동정의 고통으로 터질 듯하면서도 여전히 터지지 않았고, 그는 끊임없이 하느님께 기도했다."], ["And every time he was brought back from a moment of oblivion by a scream reaching him from the bedroom, he fell into the same strange terror that had come upon him the first minute.", "그리고 망각의 순간에서 침실로부터 들려오는 비명에 끌려 나올 때마다, 그는 첫 순간에 자기를 덮쳤던 바로 그 기이한 공포에 빠져들었다."], ["Every time he heard a shriek, he jumped up, ran to justify himself, remembered on the way that he was not to blame, and he longed to defend her, to help her.", "비명 소리를 들을 때마다 그는 벌떡 일어나 변명하러 달려갔고, 가는 도중에 자기 잘못이 아니라는 것을 떠올렸으며, 그녀를 지켜 주고 도와주고 싶어 못 견뎌 했다."], ["But as he looked at her, he saw again that help was impossible, and he was filled with terror and prayed: “Lord, have mercy on us, and help us!”", "그러나 그녀를 바라보면, 그는 도울 길이 없다는 것을 다시금 깨달았고, 공포에 사로잡혀 기도했다. \"주여, 우리를 불쌍히 여기시고, 우리를 도우소서!\""], ["And as time went on, both these conditions became more intense; the calmer he became away from her, completely forgetting her, the more agonizing became both her sufferings and his feeling of helplessness before them.", "그리고 시간이 흐를수록 이 두 가지 상태는 점점 더 격렬해졌다. 그녀에게서 떨어져 그녀를 까맣게 잊은 채 마음이 차분해질수록, 그녀의 고통과 그 고통 앞에서 느끼는 자신의 무력감은 그만큼 더 견딜 수 없는 것이 되었다."], ["He jumped up, would have liked to run away, but ran to her.", "그는 벌떡 일어나 달아나고 싶었지만, 그녀에게로 달려갔다."]], [["Sometimes, when again and again she called upon him, he blamed her; but seeing her patient, smiling face, and hearing the words, “I am worrying you,”", "때로 그녀가 거듭거듭 그를 부를 때면 그는 그녀를 탓했다. 그러나 참고 견디며 미소 짓는 그녀의 얼굴을 보고, \"당신을 힘들게 하네요.\"라는 말을 들으면, 그는 그 탓을 하느님께 돌렸다."], ["he threw the blame on God; but thinking of God, at once he fell to beseeching God to forgive him and have mercy.", "그러다 하느님을 생각하면, 곧바로 자기를 용서하시고 불쌍히 여겨 주십사 하느님께 빌기 시작했다."]], [["# Chapter 15", "# Chapter 15"]], [["He did not know whether it was late or early.", "그는 시간이 늦은 것인지 이른 것인지 알 수 없었다."], ["The candles had all burned out.", "초는 모두 다 타 버렸다."], ["Dolly had just been in the study and had suggested to the doctor that he should lie down.", "돌리는 방금 서재에 들렀다가 의사에게 잠시 누워 쉬라고 권한 참이었다."], ["Levin sat listening to the doctor’s stories of a quack mesmerizer and looking at the ashes of his cigarette.", "레빈은 어느 돌팔이 최면술사 이야기를 늘어놓는 의사의 말을 들으며 담배의 재를 바라보고 앉아 있었다."], ["There had been a period of repose, and he had sunk into oblivion.", "잠시 잠잠한 시간이 있었고, 그는 망각 속으로 빠져들었다."], ["He had completely forgotten what was going on now.", "그는 지금 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지를 완전히 잊고 있었다."], ["He heard the doctor’s chat and understood it.", "그는 의사의 잡담을 듣고 그것을 알아들었다."], ["Suddenly there came an unearthly shriek.", "그때 갑자기 이 세상의 것이 아닌 듯한 비명이 들려왔다."], ["The shriek was so awful that Levin did not even jump up, but holding his breath, gazed in terrified inquiry at the doctor.", "그 비명이 어찌나 끔찍했던지 레빈은 벌떡 일어나지도 못한 채 숨을 죽이고 겁에 질린 채 의사를 향해 묻는 눈길을 던졌다."], ["The doctor put his head on one side, listened, and smiled approvingly.", "의사는 고개를 한쪽으로 기울이고 귀를 기울이더니 만족스럽다는 듯 미소를 지었다."], ["Everything was so extraordinary that nothing could strike Levin as strange.", "모든 것이 너무도 비상해서 레빈에게는 그 무엇도 이상하게 여겨질 수가 없었다."], ["“I suppose it must be so,”", "'아마 이래야 하는 모양이지.'"], ["he thought, and still sat where he was.", "그는 생각하며 여전히 자리에 그대로 앉아 있었다."], ["Whose scream was this?", "이건 누구의 비명일까?"], ["He jumped up, ran on tiptoe to the bedroom, edged round Lizaveta Petrovna and the princess, and took up his position at Kitty’s pillow.", "그는 벌떡 일어나 발끝으로 침실까지 달려가, 리자베타 페트로브나와 공작 부인 곁을 비집고 지나 키티의 베개맡에 자리를 잡았다."], ["The scream had subsided, but there was some change now.", "비명은 잦아들었지만, 이제 무언가 달라져 있었다."], ["What it was he did not see and did not comprehend, and he had no wish to see or comprehend.", "그것이 무엇인지 그는 보지 못했고 이해하지도 못했으며, 또 보거나 이해하고 싶지도 않았다."], ["But he saw it by the face of Lizaveta Petrovna.", "그러나 그는 리자베타 페트로브나의 얼굴을 보고 그것을 알아차렸다."], ["Lizaveta Petrovna’s face was stern and pale, and still as resolute, though her jaws were twitching, and her eyes were fixed intently on Kitty.", "리자베타 페트로브나의 얼굴은 굳어 있고 창백했으며, 턱이 실룩거리고 두 눈이 키티에게 못 박힌 듯 고정되어 있었음에도 여전히 단호했다."], ["Kitty’s swollen and agonized face, a tress of hair clinging to her moist brow, was turned to him and sought his eyes.", "부어오르고 고통으로 일그러진 키티의 얼굴이, 축축한 이마에 머리카락 한 가닥이 들러붙은 채 그를 향해 돌려지더니 그의 눈을 찾았다."], ["Her lifted hands asked for his hands.", "들어 올린 두 손이 그의 손을 청했다."], ["Clutching his chill hands in her moist ones, she began squeezing them to her face.", "차가운 그의 손을 축축한 자신의 손으로 움켜잡고는 그것을 제 얼굴로 끌어다 누르기 시작했다."]], [["“Don’t go, don’t go!", "\"가지 마요, 가지 마요!"], ["I’m not afraid, I’m not afraid!”", "무섭지 않아요, 무섭지 않아요!\""], ["she said rapidly.", "그녀가 빠르게 말했다."], ["“Mamma, take my earrings.", "\"엄마, 귀고리를 빼 줘요."], ["They bother me.", "거추장스러워요."], ["You’re not afraid?", "안 무서우세요?"], ["Quick, quick, Lizaveta Petrovna....”", "빨리, 빨리요, 리자베타 페트로브나……\""]], [["She spoke quickly, very quickly, and tried to smile.", "그녀는 빠르게, 아주 빠르게 말하며 미소를 지으려고 애썼다."], ["But suddenly her face was drawn, she pushed him away.", "그러나 별안간 그녀의 얼굴이 일그러지더니 그를 밀쳐 냈다."]], [["“Oh, this is awful!", "\"아, 끔찍해요!"], ["I’m dying, I’m dying!", "죽을 것 같아, 죽을 것 같아!"], ["Go away!”", "저리 가요!\""], ["she shrieked, and again he heard that unearthly scream.", "그녀가 비명을 질렀고, 그는 다시 이 세상의 것이 아닌 듯한 그 비명을 들었다."]], [["Levin clutched at his head and ran out of the room.", "레빈은 머리를 움켜쥐고 방에서 뛰쳐나갔다."]], [["“It’s nothing, it’s nothing, it’s all right,”", "\"아무것도 아니에요, 아무것도 아니야, 다 괜찮아요.\""], ["Dolly called after him.", "돌리가 그의 등 뒤로 외쳤다."]], [["But they might say what they liked, he knew now that all was over.", "그러나 사람들이 뭐라고 하든, 그는 이제 모든 것이 끝났다는 것을 알았다."], ["He stood in the next room, his head leaning against the door post, and heard shrieks, howls such as he had never heard before, and he knew that what had been Kitty was uttering these shrieks.", "그는 옆방에 서서 문기둥에 머리를 기댄 채, 전에는 한 번도 들어 본 적 없는 비명을, 울부짖음을 들었고, 한때 키티였던 존재가 이런 비명을 지르고 있다는 것을 알았다."], ["He had long ago ceased to wish for the child.", "그는 이미 오래전부터 아이를 바라는 마음을 그쳤다."], ["By now he loathed this child.", "이제 그는 이 아이가 혐오스러웠다."], ["He did not even wish for her life now, all he longed for was the end of this awful anguish.", "그는 이제 그녀의 목숨조차 바라지 않았고, 그가 바라는 것은 오직 이 끔찍한 고통의 끝뿐이었다."]], [["“Doctor!", "\"선생님!"], ["What is it?", "어떻게 된 겁니까?"], ["What is it? By God!”", "어떻게 된 거예요?"], ["he said, snatching at the doctor’s hand as he came up.", "맙소사!\" 그는 다가오는 의사의 손을 붙들며 말했다."]], [["“It’s the end,”", "\"끝입니다.\""], ["said the doctor.", "의사가 말했다."], ["And the doctor’s face was so grave as he said it that Levin took the end as meaning her death.", "그 말을 하는 의사의 얼굴이 너무도 침통해서, 레빈은 그 끝이라는 말을 그녀의 죽음을 뜻하는 것으로 받아들였다."]], [["Beside himself, he ran into the bedroom.", "제정신이 아닌 채로 그는 침실로 달려 들어갔다."], ["The first thing he saw was the face of Lizaveta Petrovna.", "그가 가장 먼저 본 것은 리자베타 페트로브나의 얼굴이었다."], ["It was even more frowning and stern.", "그 얼굴은 한층 더 찌푸려져 있고 굳어 있었다."], ["Kitty’s face he did not know.", "키티의 얼굴은 그가 알아볼 수 없었다."], ["In the place where it had been was something that was fearful in its strained distortion and in the sounds that came from it.", "그 얼굴이 있던 자리에는, 팽팽하게 일그러진 모습과 거기에서 흘러나오는 소리로 보아 무언가 무서운 것이 있을 뿐이었다."], ["He fell down with his head on the wooden framework of the bed, feeling that his heart was bursting.", "그는 심장이 터질 것만 같은 기분으로 침대의 나무 틀에 머리를 박고 쓰러졌다."], ["The awful scream never paused, it became still more awful, and as though it had reached the utmost limit of terror, suddenly it ceased.", "끔찍한 비명은 한순간도 그치지 않았고, 점점 더 끔찍해지더니, 마치 공포의 극한에까지 다다르기라도 한 듯 별안간 멎었다."], ["Levin could not believe his ears, but there could be no doubt; the scream had ceased and he heard a subdued stir and bustle, and hurried breathing, and her voice, gasping, alive, tender, and blissful, uttered softly, “It’s over!”", "레빈은 제 귀를 믿을 수 없었지만, 의심의 여지가 없었다. 비명이 멎었고, 그는 나직한 움직임과 부산함을, 가쁜 숨소리를, 그리고 헐떡이면서도 살아 있고 부드러우며 더없이 행복에 찬 그녀의 목소리가 나직이 \"끝났어요!\"라고 말하는 것을 들었다."]], [["He lifted his head.", "그는 고개를 들었다."], ["With her hands hanging exhausted on the quilt, looking extraordinarily lovely and serene, she looked at him in silence and tried to smile, and could not.", "두 손을 기진맥진한 듯 이불 위에 떨군 채, 더없이 사랑스럽고 평온해 보이는 그녀가 말없이 그를 바라보며 미소 지으려 했지만, 그러지 못했다."]], [["And suddenly, from the mysterious and awful far-away world in which he had been living for the last twenty-two hours, Levin felt himself all in an instant borne back to the old every-day world, glorified though now, by such a radiance of happiness that he could not bear it.", "그러자 갑자기, 지난 스물두 시간 동안 그가 머물러 있던 그 신비롭고 끔찍한 머나먼 세계로부터, 레빈은 순식간에 옛 일상의 세계로 도로 옮겨진 듯한 느낌을 받았다. 다만 그 세계는 이제 그가 견딜 수 없을 만큼 찬란한 행복의 빛으로 환히 빛나고 있었다."], ["The strained chords snapped, sobs and tears of joy which he had never foreseen rose up with such violence that his whole body shook, that for long they prevented him from speaking.", "팽팽하게 당겨졌던 현이 끊어지고, 그가 한 번도 예감하지 못했던 기쁨의 흐느낌과 눈물이 어찌나 격렬하게 솟구쳤던지 온몸이 떨렸고, 한참 동안 그는 말을 할 수가 없었다."]], [["Falling on his knees before the bed, he held his wife’s hand before his lips and kissed it, and the hand, with a weak movement of the fingers, responded to his kiss.", "침대 앞에 무릎을 꿇은 채 그는 아내의 손을 입술에 가져다 입을 맞추었고, 그 손은 손가락의 희미한 움직임으로 그의 입맞춤에 응답했다."], ["And meanwhile, there at the foot of the bed, in the deft hands of Lizaveta Petrovna, like a flickering light in a lamp, lay the life of a human creature, which had never existed before, and which would now with the same right, with the same importance to itself, live and create in its own image.", "그러는 사이, 저기 침대 발치에서는 리자베타 페트로브나의 능숙한 손안에, 등잔불의 가물거리는 빛처럼, 전에는 한 번도 존재한 적 없었으나 이제 똑같은 권리와 똑같은 자기 자신에 대한 소중함을 지니고 제 모습대로 살아가며 창조해 나갈 한 인간 존재의 생명이 놓여 있었다."]], [["“Alive! alive!", "\"살아 있어요!"], ["And a boy too!", "살아 있어!"], ["Set your mind at rest!”", "게다가 사내아이예요!"], ["Levin heard Lizaveta Petrovna saying, as she slapped the baby’s back with a shaking hand.", "마음 푹 놓으세요!\" 떨리는 손으로 갓난아기의 등을 두드리며 리자베타 페트로브나가 말하는 소리를 레빈은 들었다."]], [["“Mamma, is it true?”", "\"엄마, 정말이에요?\""], ["said Kitty’s voice.", "키티의 목소리가 말했다."]], [["The princess’s sobs were all the answers she could make.", "공작 부인의 흐느낌이 그녀가 해 줄 수 있는 답의 전부였다."], ["And in the midst of the silence there came in unmistakable reply to the mother’s question, a voice quite unlike the subdued voices speaking in the room.", "그리고 그 침묵 한가운데로, 어머니의 물음에 대한 틀림없는 대답인 듯, 방 안에서 오가던 나직한 목소리들과는 전혀 다른 목소리가 들려왔다."], ["It was the bold, clamorous, self-assertive squall of the new human being, who had so incomprehensibly appeared.", "그토록 불가해하게 나타난 새로운 인간 존재의 대담하고 떠들썩하며 자기를 주장하는 듯한 울음소리였다."]], [["If Levin had been told before that Kitty was dead, and that he had died with her, and that their children were angels, and that God was standing before him, he would have been surprised at nothing.", "만약 레빈이 키티가 죽었다는, 그리고 그도 그녀와 함께 죽었다는, 그들의 아이들이 천사들이라는, 그리고 하느님이 그의 앞에 서 계시다는 말을 미리 들었더라도, 그는 그 무엇에도 놀라지 않았을 것이다."], ["But now, coming back to the world of reality, he had to make great mental efforts to take in that she was alive and well, and that the creature squalling so desperately was his son.", "그러나 이제 현실의 세계로 돌아온 그는, 그녀가 살아 있고 무사하다는 것을, 그리고 저토록 필사적으로 울어 대는 존재가 자기 아들이라는 것을 받아들이기 위해 엄청난 정신적 노력을 기울여야만 했다. 키티는 살아 있었고, 그녀의 고통은 끝났다."], ["Kitty was alive, her agony was over.", "그리고 그는 이루 말할 수 없이 행복했다."], ["And he was unutterably happy.", "그것만은 그도 이해할 수 있었다."], ["That he understood; he was completely happy in it.", "그는 그 점에서 더없이 행복했다. 그런데 이 아기는?"], ["But the baby? Whence, why, who was he?...", "어디서, 무엇 때문에, 누구란 말인가?……"], ["He could not get used to the idea.", "그는 도무지 그 생각에 익숙해질 수가 없었다."], ["It seemed to him something extraneous, superfluous, to which he could not accustom himself.", "그것은 그에게 무언가 이질적이고 군더더기 같은, 도저히 익숙해질 수 없는 것으로만 여겨졌다."]], [["# Chapter 16", "# Chapter 16"]], [["At ten o’clock the old prince, Sergey Ivanovitch, and Stepan Arkadyevitch were sitting at Levin’s.", "열 시에 노공작과 세르게이 이바노비치와 스테판 아르카지치가 레빈의 집에 앉아 있었다."], ["Having inquired after Kitty, they had dropped into conversation upon other subjects.", "키티의 안부를 묻고 난 다음, 그들은 다른 화제로 넘어가 이야기를 나누고 있었다."], ["Levin heard them, and unconsciously, as they talked, going over the past, over what had been up to that morning, he thought of himself as he had been yesterday till that point.", "레빈은 그들의 말을 듣고 있었는데, 그들이 이야기하는 동안 자기도 모르게 지난 일들을, 그날 아침까지 있었던 일들을 되짚으며, 바로 그 순간까지 어제의 자기가 어떠했던가를 떠올렸다."], ["It was as though a hundred years had passed since then.", "그때 이후로 마치 백 년이라도 흐른 것 같았다."], ["He felt himself exalted to unattainable heights, from which he studiously lowered himself so as not to wound the people he was talking to.", "그는 자신이 다다를 수 없는 높이로 올라가 있는 듯 느꼈고, 함께 이야기하는 사람들에게 상처를 주지 않으려고 일부러 그 높이에서 자신을 끌어내렸다."], ["He talked, and was all the time thinking of his wife, of her condition now, of his son, in whose existence he tried to school himself into believing.", "그는 이야기를 하면서도 줄곧 아내를, 지금 아내의 상태를, 그리고 그 존재를 믿어 보려고 스스로를 다잡고 있는 아들을 생각했다."], ["The whole world of woman, which had taken for him since his marriage a new value he had never suspected before, was now so exalted that he could not take it in in his imagination.", "결혼 이후 그에게 전에는 짐작조차 못했던 새로운 가치를 띠게 된 여성이라는 온 세계가, 이제는 너무도 높이 올라가 있어서 그는 그것을 상상으로조차 받아들일 수 없었다."], ["He heard them talk of yesterday’s dinner at the club, and thought: “What is happening with her now?", "그는 어제 클럽에서 있었던 만찬 이야기를 들으면서도 생각했다. '지금 그녀에게는 무슨 일이 일어나고 있을까?"], ["Is she asleep?", "자고 있을까?"], ["How is she? What is she thinking of?", "어떻게 지내고 있을까?"], ["Is he crying, my son Dmitri?”", "무슨 생각을 하고 있을까?"], ["And in the middle of the conversation, in the middle of a sentence, he jumped up and went out of the room.", "울고 있을까, 내 아들 드미트리는?' 그러다 대화 도중에, 한 문장 중간에, 그는 벌떡 일어나 방을 나가 버렸다."]], [["“Send me word if I can see her,”", "\"그녀를 볼 수 있겠으면 내게 전갈을 주게.\""], ["said the prince.", "공작이 말했다."]], [["“Very well, in a minute,”", "\"네, 곧 알려 드리겠습니다.\""], ["answered Levin, and without stopping, he went to her room.", "레빈은 대답하고는 멈추지 않고 아내의 방으로 갔다."]], [["She was not asleep, she was talking gently with her mother, making plans about the christening.", "그녀는 자고 있지 않았다. 어머니와 부드럽게 이야기를 나누며 세례식 계획을 세우고 있었다."]], [["Carefully set to rights, with hair well-brushed, in a smart little cap with some blue in it, her arms out on the quilt, she was lying on her back.", "머리를 곱게 빗고, 파란빛이 섞인 맵시 있는 작은 모자를 쓰고, 두 팔을 누비이불 위에 내놓은 채 그녀는 단정하게 매무새를 가다듬고 반듯이 누워 있었다."], ["Meeting his eyes, her eyes drew him to her.", "그의 눈과 마주치자 그녀의 눈이 그를 자기 쪽으로 끌어당겼다."], ["Her face, bright before, brightened still more as he drew near her.", "전부터 환하던 그녀의 얼굴은 그가 다가가자 더욱 환해졌다."], ["There was the same change in it from earthly to unearthly that is seen in the face of the dead.", "거기에는 죽은 사람의 얼굴에서 보이는, 이승에서 저승으로 옮겨 가는 듯한 그 변화가 깃들어 있었다."], ["But then it means farewell, here it meant welcome.", "다만 죽은 이의 얼굴에서 그것이 작별을 뜻한다면, 여기서는 맞이함을 뜻했다."], ["Again a rush of emotion, such as he had felt at the moment of the child’s birth, flooded his heart.", "아이가 태어나던 순간에 느꼈던 것과 같은 감정의 물결이 다시금 그의 가슴으로 밀려들었다."], ["She took his hand and asked him if he had slept.", "그녀는 그의 손을 잡고 잠을 좀 잤느냐고 물었다."], ["He could not answer, and turned away, struggling with his weakness.", "그는 대답할 수 없어, 약해지려는 마음과 싸우며 고개를 돌렸다."]], [["“I have had a nap, Kostya!”", "\"나는 한숨 잤어요, 코스챠!\""], ["she said to him; “and I am so comfortable now.”", "그녀가 그에게 말했다. \"이제 아주 편안해요.\""]], [["She looked at him, but suddenly her expression changed.", "그녀는 그를 바라보다가, 별안간 표정이 바뀌었다."]], [["“Give him to me,”", "\"그 애를 내게 줘요.\""], ["she said, hearing the baby’s cry.", "그녀가 아기의 울음소리를 듣고 말했다."], ["“Give him to me, Lizaveta Petrovna, and he shall look at him.”", "\"그 애를 내게 줘요, 리자베타 페트로브나, 아빠가 그 애를 봐야지요.\""]], [["“To be sure, his papa shall look at him,”", "\"그럼요, 아빠가 봐야지요.\""], ["said Lizaveta Petrovna, getting up and bringing something red, and queer, and wriggling.", "리자베타 페트로브나는 일어나서 빨갛고 묘하게 생긴, 꿈틀거리는 무언가를 안고 오며 말했다."], ["“Wait a minute, we’ll make him tidy first,”", "\"잠깐만요, 우선 깨끗이 해 드려야지요.\""], ["and Lizaveta Petrovna laid the red wobbling thing on the bed, began untrussing and trussing up the baby, lifting it up and turning it over with one finger and powdering it with something.", "리자베타 페트로브나는 빨갛게 흔들거리는 것을 침대에 내려놓고는 아기를 풀었다 다시 싸기 시작하면서, 손가락 하나로 들어 올렸다 뒤집었다 하고 무언가를 톡톡 발라 주었다."]], [["Levin, looking at the tiny, pitiful creature, made strenuous efforts to discover in his heart some traces of fatherly feeling for it.", "레빈은 그 자그맣고 가련한 생명체를 바라보며 자기 마음속에서 그것을 향한 부성애의 흔적이라도 찾아보려고 안간힘을 썼다."], ["He felt nothing towards it but disgust.", "그는 그것에 대해 혐오감 외에는 아무것도 느끼지 못했다."], ["But when it was undressed and he caught a glimpse of wee, wee, little hands, little feet, saffron-colored, with little toes, too, and positively with a little big toe different from the rest, and when he saw Lizaveta Petrovna closing the wide-open little hands, as though they were soft springs, and putting them into linen garments, such pity for the little creature came upon him, and such terror that she would hurt it, that he held her hand back.", "그런데 아기의 옷이 벗겨지고 아주아주 조그만 손과 발이 언뜻 눈에 들어왔을 때, 샤프란 빛깔에 작은 발가락들까지 달려 있고, 게다가 다른 발가락들과는 다른 작은 엄지발가락까지 있는 것이 보였을 때, 그리고 리자베타 페트로브나가 부드러운 용수철이라도 다루듯 활짝 벌어진 그 조그만 두 손을 오므려 무명옷 속으로 넣는 것을 보았을 때, 그 작은 생명체에 대한 연민이, 그리고 그녀가 그것을 다치게 할까 봐 두려운 마음이 어찌나 사무치게 밀려왔던지, 그는 그녀의 손을 붙들어 막았다."]], [["Lizaveta Petrovna laughed.", "리자베타 페트로브나가 웃음을 터뜨렸다."]], [["“Don’t be frightened, don’t be frightened!”", "\"무서워 마세요, 무서워 마세요!\""]], [["When the baby had been put to rights and transformed into a firm doll, Lizaveta Petrovna dandled it as though proud of her handiwork, and stood a little away so that Levin might see his son in all his glory.", "아기가 단정하게 매무새가 다듬어지고 단단한 인형처럼 변하자, 리자베타 페트로브나는 자기 솜씨가 자랑스럽기라도 한 듯 아기를 어르며 살짝 비켜섰다. 레빈이 아들의 온전한 위용을 볼 수 있도록 말이다."]], [["Kitty looked sideways in the same direction, never taking her eyes off the baby.", "키티는 아기에게서 눈을 떼지 않은 채 같은 쪽으로 곁눈질을 했다."], ["“Give him to me!", "\"그 애를 내게 줘요!"], ["give him to me!”", "내게 줘요!\""], ["she said, and even made as though she would sit up.", "그녀는 이렇게 말하며 일어나 앉으려는 시늉까지 했다."]], [["“What are you thinking of, Katerina Alexandrovna, you mustn’t move like that!", "\"무슨 생각을 하시는 거예요, 카체리나 알렉산드로브나, 그렇게 움직이시면 안 돼요! 잠깐만요."], ["Wait a minute.", "제가 드릴게요."], ["I’ll give him to you. Here we’re showing papa what a fine fellow we are!”", "자, 우리가 얼마나 잘생긴 사내인지 아빠한테 보여 드리는 거예요!\""]], [["And Lizaveta Petrovna, with one hand supporting the wobbling head, lifted up on the other arm the strange, limp, red creature, whose head was lost in its swaddling clothes.", "그러고서 리자베타 페트로브나는 한 손으로 흔들거리는 머리를 받치고, 다른 팔로 그 이상하고 흐물흐물한 빨간 생명체를 들어 올렸다. 그 머리는 강보 속에 파묻혀 보이지 않았다."], ["But it had a nose, too, and slanting eyes and smacking lips.", "그래도 코도 있고, 비스듬한 눈도 있고, 쩝쩝거리는 입술도 있었다."]], [["“A splendid baby!”", "\"훌륭한 아기예요!\""], ["said Lizaveta Petrovna.", "리자베타 페트로브나가 말했다."]], [["Levin sighed with mortification.", "레빈은 분한 마음에 한숨을 내쉬었다."], ["This splendid baby excited in him no feeling but disgust and compassion.", "이 훌륭한 아기는 그의 마음속에 혐오와 연민 외에는 아무런 감정도 불러일으키지 못했다."], ["It was not at all the feeling he had looked forward to.", "그가 기대했던 것과는 전혀 다른 감정이었다."]], [["He turned away while Lizaveta Petrovna put the baby to the unaccustomed breast.", "리자베타 페트로브나가 아기를 아직 익숙지 않은 젖가슴에 안겨 주는 동안 그는 고개를 돌렸다."]], [["Suddenly laughter made him look round.", "별안간 웃음소리에 그는 돌아보았다."], ["The baby had taken the breast.", "아기가 젖을 물고 있었다."]], [["“Come, that’s enough, that’s enough!”", "\"자, 이제 됐어요, 그만하면 됐어요!\""], ["said Lizaveta Petrovna, but Kitty would not let the baby go.", "리자베타 페트로브나가 말했지만, 키티는 아기를 놓아주려 하지 않았다."], ["He fell asleep in her arms.", "아기는 그녀의 품에서 잠이 들었다."]], [["“Look, now,”", "\"자, 봐요.\""], ["said Kitty, turning the baby so that he could see it.", "키티는 그가 볼 수 있도록 아기를 돌려 보이며 말했다."], ["The aged-looking little face suddenly puckered up still more and the baby sneezed.", "늙은이 같던 그 작은 얼굴이 갑자기 더욱더 찡그려지더니 아기가 재채기를 했다."]], [["Smiling, hardly able to restrain his tears, Levin kissed his wife and went out of the dark room.", "레빈은 미소를 지으며, 눈물을 가까스로 참은 채 아내에게 입을 맞추고 어두운 방을 나섰다."], ["What he felt towards this little creature was utterly unlike what he had expected.", "그가 이 작은 생명체에게 느낀 것은 그가 기대했던 것과는 전혀 달랐다."], ["There was nothing cheerful and joyous in the feeling; on the contrary, it was a new torture of apprehension.", "그 감정 속에는 명랑하고 기쁜 데라곤 없었다. 오히려 그것은 새로운 불안의 고통이었다."], ["It was the consciousness of a new sphere of liability to pain.", "그것은 고통에 노출되는 새로운 영역이 생겨났다는 의식이었다."], ["And this sense was so painful at first, the apprehension lest this helpless creature should suffer was so intense, that it prevented him from noticing the strange thrill of senseless joy and even pride that he had felt when the baby sneezed.", "그리고 처음에 이 감각은 어찌나 괴로웠던지, 이 무력한 생명체가 고통받지나 않을까 하는 염려가 어찌나 강렬했던지, 아기가 재채기했을 때 자기가 느꼈던 그 까닭 모를 기쁨의, 심지어 자랑스러움의 묘한 전율조차 알아채지 못할 지경이었다."]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch’s affairs were in a very bad way.", "스테판 아르카지치의 형편은 매우 좋지 않았다."]], [["The money for two-thirds of the forest had all been spent already, and he had borrowed from the merchant in advance at ten per cent discount, almost all the remaining third.", "숲의 삼분의 이에 대한 대금은 이미 다 써 버렸고, 나머지 삼분의 일에 해당하는 돈도 거의 전부, 상인에게서 십 퍼센트 할인을 무릅쓰고 미리 당겨 받은 상태였다."], ["The merchant would not give more, especially as Darya Alexandrovna, for the first time that winter insisting on her right to her own property, had refused to sign the receipt for the payment of the last third of the forest.", "상인은 더 이상 돈을 내주려 하지 않았는데, 특히 다리야 알렉산드로브나가 그해 겨울 처음으로 자기 재산에 대한 권리를 주장하며, 숲의 마지막 삼분의 일에 대한 대금 수령증에 서명하기를 거부했기 때문이었다."], ["All his salary went on household expenses and in payment of petty debts that could not be put off.", "그의 급료는 죄다 살림 비용과, 미룰 수 없는 자질구레한 빚을 갚는 데 들어갔다."], ["There was positively no money.", "그야말로 돈이라곤 없었다."]], [["This was unpleasant and awkward, and in Stepan Arkadyevitch’s opinion things could not go on like this.", "이는 불쾌하고 거북한 일이었고, 스테판 아르카지치가 보기에 이런 식으로 계속 갈 수는 없었다."], ["The explanation of the position was, in his view, to be found in the fact that his salary was too small.", "그의 관점에서 이 처지의 원인은, 자기 급료가 너무 적다는 사실에 있었다."], ["The post he filled had been unmistakably very good five years ago, but it was so no longer.", "그가 맡은 자리는 오 년 전만 해도 의심할 여지 없이 아주 좋은 자리였지만, 지금은 더 이상 그렇지 않았다."]], [["Petrov, the bank director, had twelve thousand; Sventitsky, a company director, had seventeen thousand; Mitin, who had founded a bank, received fifty thousand.", "은행장인 페트로프는 만 이천을 받았고, 어느 회사의 이사인 스벤티츠키는 만 칠천을 받았으며, 은행을 세운 미틴은 오만을 받았다."]], [["“Clearly I’ve been napping, and they’ve overlooked me,”", "\"내가 졸고 있는 사이에 저들이 나를 빼놓고 가 버린 게 분명해.\""], ["Stepan Arkadyevitch thought about himself.", "스테판 아르카지치는 자신에 대해 그렇게 생각했다."], ["And he began keeping his eyes and ears open, and towards the end of the winter he had discovered a very good berth and had formed a plan of attack upon it, at first from Moscow through aunts, uncles, and friends, and then, when the matter was well advanced, in the spring, he went himself to Petersburg.", "그래서 그는 눈과 귀를 활짝 열어 두기 시작했고, 겨울이 끝나 갈 무렵에는 아주 괜찮은 자리를 하나 발견하여 그것을 공략할 계획을 세웠다. 처음에는 모스크바에서 이모며 삼촌이며 친구들을 통해 손을 쓰다가, 일이 어느 정도 무르익은 봄이 되자 직접 페테르부르크로 갔다."], ["It was one of those snug, lucrative berths of which there are so many more nowadays than there used to be, with incomes ranging from one thousand to fifty thousand roubles.", "그것은 오늘날 예전보다 훨씬 많아진, 수입이 일천 루블에서 오만 루블에 이르는 안락하고 짭짤한 자리들 가운데 하나였다."], ["It was the post of secretary of the committee of the amalgamated agency of the southern railways, and of certain banking companies.", "남부 철도 통합 대리점과 몇몇 은행 회사의 합동 위원회 서기 자리였다."], ["This position, like all such appointments, called for such immense energy and such varied qualifications, that it was difficult for them to be found united in any one man.", "이런 직책은 모든 그런 임명이 그렇듯, 어마어마한 정력과 갖가지 자질을 요구했기에, 그것들을 한 사람 안에서 모두 갖춘 이를 찾기란 어려웠다."], ["And since a man combining all the qualifications was not to be found, it was at least better that the post be filled by an honest than by a dishonest man.", "그리고 모든 자질을 두루 갖춘 사람을 찾을 수 없는 이상, 적어도 그 자리는 부정직한 사람보다는 정직한 사람으로 채워지는 편이 나았다."], ["And Stepan Arkadyevitch was not merely an honest man—unemphatically—in the common acceptation of the words, he was an honest man—emphatically—in that special sense which the word has in Moscow, when they talk of an “honest”", "그런데 스테판 아르카지치는 그저 흔히 말하는 의미에서의—강조 없이—정직한 사람이었을 뿐 아니라, 모스크바에서 그 단어가 지니는 특별한 의미에서—강조해서 말하는—정직한 사람이었다. 모스크바에서 '정직한'"], ["politician, an “honest”", "정치가, '정직한'"], ["writer, an “honest”", "작가, '정직한'"], ["newspaper, an “honest”", "신문, '정직한'"], ["institution, an “honest”", "기관, '정직한'"], ["tendency, meaning not simply that the man or the institution is not dishonest, but that they are capable on occasion of taking a line of their own in opposition to the authorities.", "노선이라고 말할 때, 그것은 단지 그 사람이나 기관이 부정직하지 않다는 뜻이 아니라, 경우에 따라 당국에 맞서 자기 나름의 입장을 취할 능력이 있다는 뜻이었다."]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch moved in those circles in Moscow in which that expression had come into use, was regarded there as an honest man, and so had more right to this appointment than others.", "스테판 아르카지치는 모스크바에서 그런 표현이 통용되는 사교계에 드나들었고, 그곳에서 정직한 사람으로 통했으니, 다른 이들보다 이 자리에 대한 권리가 더 있는 셈이었다."]], [["The appointment yielded an income of from seven to ten thousand a year, and Oblonsky could fill it without giving up his government position.", "그 자리는 연간 칠천에서 일만 루블의 수입을 가져다주었고, 오블론스키는 자기 관직을 그대로 둔 채 그것을 맡을 수 있었다."], ["It was in the hands of two ministers, one lady, and two Jews, and all these people, though the way had been paved already with them, Stepan Arkadyevitch had to see in Petersburg.", "그 자리는 두 장관과 한 부인, 그리고 두 유대인의 손에 달려 있었는데, 이 사람들과는 이미 길을 닦아 두었음에도 불구하고 스테판 아르카지치는 페테르부르크에서 그들을 직접 만나야 했다."], ["Besides this business, Stepan Arkadyevitch had promised his sister Anna to obtain from Karenin a definite answer on the question of divorce.", "이 일 말고도 스테판 아르카지치는 누이 안나에게, 카레닌으로부터 이혼 문제에 대한 확실한 대답을 받아 내겠다고 약속한 터였다."], ["And begging fifty roubles from Dolly, he set off for Petersburg.", "그래서 돌리에게 오십 루블을 꾸어, 페테르부르크로 떠났다."]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch sat in Karenin’s study listening to his report on the causes of the unsatisfactory position of Russian finance, and only waiting for the moment when he would finish to speak about his own business or about Anna.", "스테판 아르카지치는 카레닌의 서재에 앉아, 러시아 재정이 만족스럽지 못한 상태에 놓인 원인에 관한 그의 보고를 들으면서, 그가 말을 끝내는 순간 자기 일이나 안나에 관한 이야기를 꺼낼 수 있기만을 기다리고 있었다."]], [["“Yes, that’s very true,”", "\"그래, 그건 아주 옳은 말씀이야.\" 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 이제는 그것 없이는 글을 읽지 못하는 코안경을 벗고서 전 처남을 캐묻듯 바라보았을 때, 그가 말했다."], ["he said, when Alexey Alexandrovitch took off the pince-nez, without which he could not read now, and looked inquiringly at his former brother-in-law, “that’s very true in particular cases, but still the principle of our day is freedom.”", "\"특정한 경우에는 그게 아주 옳지. 하지만 그래도 우리 시대의 원칙은 자유잖나.\""]], [["“Yes, but I lay down another principle, embracing the principle of freedom,”", "\"그래, 하지만 나는 자유의 원칙을 포괄하는 또 다른 원칙을 내세우네.\""], ["said Alexey Alexandrovitch, with emphasis on the word “embracing,” and he put on his pince-nez again, so as to read the passage in which this statement was made.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 '포괄하는'이라는 말에 힘을 주어 말하고는, 그 주장이 담긴 대목을 읽으려고 다시 코안경을 썼다."], ["And turning over the beautifully written, wide-margined manuscript, Alexey Alexandrovitch read aloud over again the conclusive passage.", "그리고 아름답게 쓰인, 여백이 넓은 원고를 넘기며, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 그 결론 부분을 다시 소리 내어 읽었다."]], [["“I don’t advocate protection for the sake of private interests, but for the public weal, and for the lower and upper classes equally,”", "\"나는 사적 이익을 위해서가 아니라 공공의 복리를 위해, 그리고 하층 계급과 상층 계급을 똑같이 위해 보호 정책을 옹호하는 거네.\""], ["he said, looking over his pince-nez at Oblonsky.", "그는 코안경 너머로 오블론스키를 바라보며 말했다."], ["“But they cannot grasp that, they are taken up now with personal interests, and carried away by phrases.”", "\"하지만 그자들은 그걸 이해하지 못해. 그자들은 지금 개인적 이해관계에만 골몰하고, 듣기 좋은 말에 휩쓸려 다닌단 말이야.\""]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch knew that when Karenin began to talk of what they were doing and thinking, the persons who would not accept his report and were the cause of everything wrong in Russia, that it was coming near the end.", "스테판 아르카지치는 카레닌이 그자들이 무엇을 하고 무엇을 생각하는지, 즉 자기 보고를 받아들이지 않으며 러시아의 모든 잘못된 일의 원인인 그 사람들에 대해 이야기를 시작하면, 이야기가 끝나갈 무렵이라는 것을 알았다."], ["And so now he eagerly abandoned the principle of free-trade, and fully agreed.", "그래서 이번에도 그는 자유무역의 원칙을 선뜻 버리고 전적으로 동의했다."], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch paused, thoughtfully turning over the pages of his manuscript.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 생각에 잠긴 채 원고 장을 넘기며 말을 멈췄다."]], [["“Oh, by the way,”", "\"아, 그건 그렇고,\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, “I wanted to ask you, some time when you see Pomorsky, to drop him a hint that I should be very glad to get that new appointment of secretary of the committee of the amalgamated agency of the southern railways and banking companies.”", "스테판 아르카지치가 말했다. \"부탁이 있는데, 언제 포모르스키를 만나거든 내가 남부 철도와 은행 회사 통합 대리점 합동 위원회 서기라는 그 새 자리를 얻으면 정말 기쁘겠다고 슬쩍 귀띔해 주었으면 하네.\""], ["Stepan Arkadyevitch was familiar by now with the title of the post he coveted, and he brought it out rapidly without mistake.", "스테판 아르카지치는 이제 자기가 탐내는 그 자리의 명칭에 익숙해져서, 막힘없이 술술 읊어 댔다."]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch questioned him as to the duties of this new committee, and pondered.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 그 새 위원회의 임무에 관해 그에게 물어보고는 곰곰이 생각했다."], ["He was considering whether the new committee would not be acting in some way contrary to the views he had been advocating.", "그 새 위원회가 자기가 옹호해 온 견해에 어떤 식으로든 어긋나게 움직이지는 않을지 따져 보고 있었다."], ["But as the influence of the new committee was of a very complex nature, and his views were of very wide application, he could not decide this straight off, and taking off his pince-nez, he said:", "하지만 그 새 위원회의 영향력이라는 것이 매우 복잡한 성격을 띠고 있었고, 자기 견해는 적용 범위가 매우 넓었으므로, 그는 그 자리에서 곧바로 결론을 내릴 수 없었다. 그래서 코안경을 벗으며 말했다."]], [["“Of course, I can mention it to him; but what is your reason precisely for wishing to obtain the appointment?”", "\"물론 그자에게 말은 해 줄 수 있네. 그런데 정확히 무슨 이유로 그 자리를 얻으려는 건가?\""]], [["“It’s a good salary, rising to nine thousand, and my means....”", "\"급료가 좋아서, 구천까지 오르거든. 게다가 내 형편이……\""]], [["“Nine thousand!”", "\"구천이라!\""], ["repeated Alexey Alexandrovitch, and he frowned.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 되뇌며 미간을 찌푸렸다."], ["The high figure of the salary made him reflect that on that side Stepan Arkadyevitch’s proposed position ran counter to the main tendency of his own projects of reform, which always leaned towards economy.", "그 높은 급료 액수는, 그쪽 방면에서 스테판 아르카지치가 노리는 자리가 늘 절약 쪽으로 기우는 자기 개혁안의 주된 방향과 어긋난다는 사실을 곱씹게 만들었다."]], [["“I consider, and I have embodied my views in a note on the subject, that in our day these immense salaries are evidence of the unsound economic assiette of our finances.”", "\"내 생각으로는, 그리고 이 문제에 관한 견해를 글로도 정리해 두었네만, 오늘날 이런 어마어마한 급료는 우리 재정의 건전하지 못한 경제적 assiette를 보여 주는 증거일세.\""]], [["“But what’s to be done?”", "* assiette: 프랑스어로 '기반, 토대'."], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch.", "\"하지만 어쩌겠나?\" 스테판 아르카지치가 말했다."], ["“Suppose a bank director gets ten thousand—well, he’s worth it; or an engineer gets twenty thousand—after all, it’s a growing thing, you know!”", "\"은행장이 만을 받는다고 치세. 글쎄, 그만한 값어치가 있는 거고, 기사가 이만을 받는다 해도, 어쨌든 그건 자라나는 분야 아닌가!\""]], [["“I assume that a salary is the price paid for a commodity, and it ought to conform with the law of supply and demand.", "\"나는 급료란 어떤 상품에 대해 지불되는 값이라고 보네. 그러니 그것은 수요와 공급의 법칙에 부합해야 마땅하지."], ["If the salary is fixed without any regard for that law, as, for instance, when I see two engineers leaving college together, both equally well trained and efficient, and one getting forty thousand while the other is satisfied with two; or when I see lawyers and hussars, having no special qualifications, appointed directors of banking companies with immense salaries, I conclude that the salary is not fixed in accordance with the law of supply and demand, but simply through personal interest.", "만일 그 법칙을 전혀 고려하지 않고 급료가 정해진다면, 가령 똑같이 잘 훈련받고 유능한 두 기사가 함께 학교를 나왔는데 한 사람은 사만을 받고 다른 한 사람은 이천으로 만족하는 경우, 또는 아무런 특별한 자질도 없는 법률가나 경기병이 어마어마한 급료를 받으며 은행 회사 이사로 임명되는 경우를 내가 본다면, 나는 그 급료가 수요와 공급의 법칙에 따라 정해진 것이 아니라 그저 개인적 이해관계를 통해 정해진 것이라고 결론짓네."], ["And this is an abuse of great gravity in itself, and one that reacts injuriously on the government service.", "그리고 이것은 그 자체로 매우 중대한 폐단이며, 관청의 업무에도 해로운 영향을 미치는 일이지."], ["I consider....”", "내 생각으로는……\""]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch made haste to interrupt his brother-in-law.", "스테판 아르카지치는 서둘러 매형의 말을 끊었다."]], [["“Yes; but you must agree that it’s a new institution of undoubted utility that’s being started.", "\"그래, 하지만 이건 의심할 여지 없이 유익한 새 기관이 출범하는 거라는 점은 자네도 인정해야 하네."], ["After all, you know, it’s a growing thing!", "어쨌든 자라나는 분야 아닌가!"], ["What they lay particular stress on is the thing being carried on honestly,”", "그들이 특히 강조하는 건 일이 정직하게 운영되어야 한다는 점이야.\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch with emphasis.", "스테판 아르카지치가 힘주어 말했다."]], [["But the Moscow significance of the word “honest” was lost on Alexey Alexandrovitch.", "그러나 '정직한'이라는 단어의 모스크바식 의미는 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에게 통하지 않았다."]], [["“Honesty is only a negative qualification,”", "\"정직성이란 그저 소극적인 자질일 뿐이지.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“Well, you’ll do me a great service, anyway,”", "\"뭐, 어쨌든 자네가 포모르스키에게 한마디만 거들어 준다면 내게는 큰 도움이 될 걸세.\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, “by putting in a word to Pomorsky—just in the way of conversation....”", "스테판 아르카지치가 말했다. \"그냥 이야기를 나누는 김에 말이지……\""]], [["“But I fancy it’s more in Volgarinov’s hands,”", "\"하지만 그 일은 볼가리노프의 손에 더 많이 달려 있는 것 같네만.\""], ["said Alexey Alexandrovitch.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 말했다."]], [["“Volgarinov has fully assented, as far as he’s concerned,”", "\"볼가리노프는 자기 쪽에서는 전적으로 동의했네.\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, turning red.", "스테판 아르카지치가 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다."], ["Stepan Arkadyevitch reddened at the mention of that name, because he had been that morning at the Jew Volgarinov’s, and the visit had left an unpleasant recollection.", "스테판 아르카지치가 그 이름이 나오자 낯을 붉힌 것은, 그날 아침 유대인 볼가리노프의 집에 다녀왔는데 그 방문이 불쾌한 기억을 남겼기 때문이었다."]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch believed most positively that the committee in which he was trying to get an appointment was a new, genuine, and honest public body, but that morning when Volgarinov had—intentionally, beyond a doubt—kept him two hours waiting with other petitioners in his waiting room, he had suddenly felt uneasy.", "스테판 아르카지치는 자기가 자리를 얻으려는 그 위원회가 새롭고 진정하며 정직한 공공 기관이라고 더없이 확신했지만, 그날 아침 볼가리노프가—틀림없이 의도적으로—다른 청원자들과 함께 그를 두 시간이나 대기실에서 기다리게 했을 때, 그는 별안간 마음이 거북해졌다."]], [["Whether he was uncomfortable that he, a descendant of Rurik, Prince Oblonsky, had been kept for two hours waiting to see a Jew, or that for the first time in his life he was not following the example of his ancestors in serving the government, but was turning off into a new career, anyway he was very uncomfortable.", "류리크의 후손이자 오블론스키 공작인 자기가 유대인을 만나려고 두 시간이나 기다렸다는 사실 때문에 불편했는지, 아니면 평생 처음으로 관청에 봉직하던 조상들의 본을 따르지 않고 새로운 길로 들어서고 있다는 사실 때문이었는지는 몰라도, 어쨌든 그는 몹시 거북했다."], ["During those two hours in Volgarinov’s waiting room Stepan Arkadyevitch, stepping jauntily about the room, pulling his whiskers, entering into conversation with the other petitioners, and inventing an epigram on his position, assiduously concealed from others, and even from himself, the feeling he was experiencing.", "볼가리노프의 대기실에서 보낸 그 두 시간 동안 스테판 아르카지치는 방 안을 경쾌하게 거닐고, 구레나룻을 매만지고, 다른 청원자들과 이야기를 나누고, 자기 처지에 관한 경구를 지어내면서, 자기가 느끼고 있던 감정을 남들에게는 물론 자기 자신에게까지도 부지런히 감추었다. * 류리크: 9세기 러시아 최초의 왕조를 세운 인물."]], [["But all the time he was uncomfortable and angry, he could not have said why—whether because he could not get his epigram just right, or from some other reason.", "하지만 그동안 내내 그는 거북하고 화가 났는데, 자기도 그 까닭을 알 수 없었다. 경구를 딱 들어맞게 다듬어 내지 못해서인지, 아니면 다른 무슨 이유에서인지."], ["When at last Volgarinov had received him with exaggerated politeness and unmistakable triumph at his humiliation, and had all but refused the favor asked of him, Stepan Arkadyevitch had made haste to forget it all as soon as possible.", "마침내 볼가리노프가 그를 지나치게 정중하게, 그러면서도 그의 굴욕에 대한 분명한 승리감을 드러내며 맞이하고는, 그가 청한 부탁을 거의 거절하다시피 했을 때, 스테판 아르카지치는 그 모든 일을 가능한 한 빨리 잊으려고 서둘렀다."], ["And now, at the mere recollection, he blushed.", "그런데 지금, 그저 그 기억을 떠올리는 것만으로도 그는 얼굴이 붉어졌다."]], [["# Chapter 18", "# Chapter 18"]], [["“Now there is something I want to talk about, and you know what it is. About Anna,”", "\"이제 한 가지 의논하고 싶은 게 있는데, 자네도 그게 뭔지 알 거야."], ["Stepan Arkadyevitch said, pausing for a brief space, and shaking off the unpleasant impression.", "안나에 관한 일이지.\" 스테판 아르카지치는 잠시 말을 멈추고 그 불쾌한 느낌을 떨쳐 내며 말했다."]], [["As soon as Oblonsky uttered Anna’s name, the face of Alexey Alexandrovitch was completely transformed; all the life was gone out of it, and it looked weary and dead.", "오블론스키가 안나의 이름을 입에 올리자마자, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 얼굴은 완전히 딴판으로 변했다. 거기서 모든 생기가 사라지고, 지치고 죽은 듯이 보였다."]], [["“What is it exactly that you want from me?”", "\"나한테서 정확히 뭘 원하는 건가?\""], ["he said, moving in his chair and snapping his pince-nez.", "그는 의자에서 몸을 움직이며 코안경을 탁 접고 말했다."]], [["“A definite settlement, Alexey Alexandrovitch, some settlement of the position.", "\"확실한 결말을 보자는 거야, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치."], ["I’m appealing to you”", "이 상황을 어떻게든 매듭짓자는 거지."], ["(“not as an injured husband,”", "내가 자네에게 호소하는 건,\""], ["Stepan Arkadyevitch was going to say, but afraid of wrecking his negotiation by this, he changed the words) “not as a statesman”", "(스테판 아르카지치는 \"상처받은 남편으로서가 아니라\"라고 말하려다가, 이 말로 협상을 그르칠까 두려워 표현을 바꾸었다) \"정치가로서가 아니라\""], ["(which did not sound à propos), “but simply as a man, and a good-hearted man and a Christian.", "(이것도 적절치 않게 들렸다) \"그저 한 사람으로서, 마음씨 착한 한 인간으로서, 그리고 한 기독교인으로서일세."], ["You must have pity on her,”", "자네는 그녀를 가엾게 여겨야 해.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“That is, in what way precisely?”", "\"그러니까, 정확히 어떤 식으로 말인가?\""], ["Karenin said softly.", "카레닌이 나직이 말했다."]], [["“Yes, pity on her.", "\"그래, 그녀를 가엾게 여겨 달라는 거야."], ["If you had seen her as I have!—I have been spending all the winter with her—you would have pity on her.", "자네가 나처럼 그녀를 봤더라면!—나는 겨우내 그녀와 함께 지냈거든—자네도 그녀를 가엾게 여겼을 걸세."], ["Her position is awful, simply awful!”", "그녀의 처지는 끔찍해, 그저 끔찍할 뿐이야!\""]], [["“I had imagined,”", "\"내 생각엔,\""], ["answered Alexey Alexandrovitch in a higher, almost shrill voice, “that Anna Arkadyevna had everything she had desired for herself.”", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 한결 높은, 거의 째지는 목소리로 대꾸했다. \"안나 아르카지예브나는 자기가 바라던 걸 다 가졌을 텐데.\""]], [["“Oh, Alexey Alexandrovitch, for heaven’s sake, don’t let us indulge in recriminations!", "\"오, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치, 제발 부탁이니 서로 헐뜯는 짓은 그만두세! 지나간 건 지나간 거야."], ["What is past is past, and you know what she wants and is waiting for—divorce.”", "그리고 자네도 그녀가 뭘 원하는지, 뭘 기다리는지 알잖나—이혼 말이야.\""]], [["“But I believe Anna Arkadyevna refuses a divorce, if I make it a condition to leave me my son.", "\"하지만 내가 알기로 안나 아르카지예브나는, 내가 아들을 내게 남겨 둘 것을 조건으로 내건다면 이혼을 거부하지."], ["I replied in that sense, and supposed that the matter was ended.", "나는 그런 취지로 답했고, 그 문제는 끝난 걸로 알았네."], ["I consider it at an end,”", "나는 그 일을 끝난 걸로 여기고 있어.\""], ["shrieked Alexey Alexandrovitch.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 째지는 소리로 말했다."]], [["“But, for heaven’s sake, don’t get hot!”", "\"하지만 제발, 흥분하지 말게!\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, touching his brother-in-law’s knee.", "스테판 아르카지치가 매형의 무릎을 만지며 말했다. \"그 문제는 끝나지 않았어."], ["“The matter is not ended.", "내가 다시 한번 정리하게 허락해 준다면, 일은 이랬어."], ["If you will allow me to recapitulate, it was like this: when you parted, you were as magnanimous as could possibly be; you were ready to give her everything—freedom, divorce even.", "자네들이 갈라설 때, 자네는 더없이 너그러웠어. 그녀에게 뭐든 다 줄 작정이었지—자유든, 이혼이든 말이야."], ["She appreciated that.", "그녀는 그걸 고맙게 여겼어."], ["No, don’t think that.", "아니, 그렇게 생각하지 말게."], ["She did appreciate it—to such a degree that at the first moment, feeling how she had wronged you, she did not consider and could not consider everything.", "그녀는 정말로 고맙게 여겼어—얼마나 고마워했는지, 첫 순간에는 자기가 자네에게 얼마나 못할 짓을 했는지를 느끼느라, 모든 걸 헤아리지도 못했고 헤아릴 수도 없었지."], ["She gave up everything.", "그녀는 모든 걸 포기했어."], ["But experience, time, have shown that her position is unbearable, impossible.”", "하지만 경험과 시간이 보여 준 거야, 그녀의 처지가 견딜 수 없고 불가능하다는 걸 말이야.\""]], [["“The life of Anna Arkadyevna can have no interest for me,”", "\"안나 아르카지예브나의 삶은 내게 아무런 흥미가 없네.\""], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch put in, lifting his eyebrows.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 눈썹을 치켜올리며 끼어들었다."]], [["“Allow me to disbelieve that,”", "\"그 말은 믿지 않겠네.\""], ["Stepan Arkadyevitch replied gently.", "스테판 아르카지치가 부드럽게 대꾸했다."], ["“Her position is intolerable for her, and of no benefit to anyone whatever.", "\"그녀의 처지는 그녀에게 견딜 수 없는 것이고, 누구에게도 아무런 득이 안 돼."], ["She has deserved it, you will say.", "그녀가 자초한 일이라고 자넨 말하겠지."], ["She knows that and asks you for nothing; she says plainly that she dare not ask you.", "그녀도 그걸 알고 있고, 자네에게 아무것도 요구하지 않아. 자네에게 감히 요구할 수 없다고 분명히 말해."], ["But I, all of us, her relatives, all who love her, beg you, entreat you.", "하지만 나는, 우리 모두는, 그녀의 친척들은, 그녀를 사랑하는 모든 이들은 자네에게 청하고, 간청하는 거야."], ["Why should she suffer?", "어째서 그녀가 고통받아야 하지?"], ["Who is any the better for it?”", "그걸로 누가 조금이라도 나아지는가?\""]], [["“Excuse me, you seem to put me in the position of the guilty party,”", "\"미안하지만, 자넨 나를 죄지은 쪽에 세우려는 것 같군.\""], ["observed Alexey Alexandrovitch.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 말했다."]], [["“Oh, no, oh, no, not at all!", "\"오, 아니야, 오, 아니야, 전혀 그렇지 않네!"], ["please understand me,”", "제발 내 말을 이해해 주게.\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, touching his hand again, as though feeling sure this physical contact would soften his brother-in-law.", "스테판 아르카지치가 다시 그의 손을 만지며 말했다. 이 신체 접촉이 처남의 마음을 누그러뜨려 주리라고 확신하기라도 하는 듯이."], ["“All I say is this: her position is intolerable, and it might be alleviated by you, and you will lose nothing by it.", "\"내가 말하는 건 이것뿐이야. 그녀의 처지는 견딜 수 없는 것이고, 자네가 그걸 덜어 줄 수 있으며, 그렇게 해도 자넨 잃을 게 아무것도 없다는 거지."], ["I will arrange it all for you, so that you’ll not notice it.", "내가 자네를 위해 다 처리해 줄 테니, 자네는 신경 쓸 일조차 없을 거야."], ["You did promise it, you know.”", "자네도 약속했잖나, 그러지 않았나.\""]], [["“The promise was given before.", "\"그 약속은 이전에 한 거지."], ["And I had supposed that the question of my son had settled the matter.", "그리고 나는 아들 문제로 그 일이 매듭지어진 줄 알았네."], ["Besides, I had hoped that Anna Arkadyevna had enough generosity....”", "게다가 안나 아르카지예브나에게도 충분한 아량이 있으리라고 기대했고…….\""], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch articulated with difficulty, his lips twitching and his face white.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 입술을 씰룩이고 얼굴이 하얗게 질린 채 가까스로 또박또박 말했다."]], [["“She leaves it all to your generosity.", "\"그녀는 모든 걸 자네의 아량에 맡기고 있어."], ["She begs, she implores one thing of you—to extricate her from the impossible position in which she is placed.", "그녀가 자네에게 빌고, 애원하는 건 단 한 가지야—자기가 처한 이 불가능한 상황에서 자기를 빼내 달라는 거지."], ["She does not ask for her son now.", "그녀는 이제 아들을 요구하지 않아."], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch, you are a good man.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치, 자넨 선한 사람이야."], ["Put yourself in her position for a minute.", "잠깐만이라도 그녀의 처지가 되어 보게."], ["The question of divorce for her in her position is a question of life and death.", "그녀에게 이혼 문제는, 그녀가 처한 상황에서는 죽느냐 사느냐의 문제야."], ["If you had not promised it once, she would have reconciled herself to her position, she would have gone on living in the country.", "자네가 한때 그걸 약속하지 않았더라면, 그녀는 자기 처지를 받아들이고 시골에서 계속 살았을 거야."], ["But you promised it, and she wrote to you, and moved to Moscow.", "하지만 자네가 약속했고, 그래서 그녀는 자네에게 편지를 쓰고 모스크바로 옮겨 온 거야."], ["And here she’s been for six months in Moscow, where every chance meeting cuts her to the heart, every day expecting an answer.", "그러고는 여기 모스크바에서 여섯 달째 지내고 있는데, 어쩌다 마주치는 사람마다 그녀의 가슴을 후벼 파고, 날마다 답을 기다리고 있어."], ["Why, it’s like keeping a condemned criminal for six months with the rope round his neck, promising him perhaps death, perhaps mercy.", "이건 사형수를 여섯 달 동안 목에 밧줄을 건 채로 두면서, 어쩌면 죽음일지, 어쩌면 사면일지 약속하는 것과 다름없어."], ["Have pity on her, and I will undertake to arrange everything.", "그녀를 가엾게 여겨 주게, 그러면 내가 모든 걸 처리하겠네."], ["Vos scrupules....”", "Vos scrupules…….\""]], [["“I am not talking about that, about that....”", "\"그 얘기를 하는 게 아니야, 그게 아니라…….\""], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch interrupted with disgust.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 넌더리를 내며 말을 가로막았다."], ["“But, perhaps, I promised what I had no right to promise.”", "\"하지만, 어쩌면 나는 약속할 권리도 없는 걸 약속했는지도 모르지.\""]], [["“So you go back from your promise?”", "\"그래서 자네 약속을 무를 셈인가?\""]], [["“I have never refused to do all that is possible, but I want time to consider how much of what I promised is possible.”", "\"나는 가능한 모든 걸 하기를 거부한 적이 없어. 다만 내가 약속한 것 가운데 얼마나 많은 부분이 가능한지를 생각해 볼 시간이 필요한 거지.\""]], [["“No, Alexey Alexandrovitch!”", "\"아니야, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치!\""], ["cried Oblonsky, jumping up, “I won’t believe that!", "오블론스키가 벌떡 일어나며 외쳤다. \"그 말은 믿지 않겠네!"], ["She’s unhappy as only an unhappy woman can be, and you cannot refuse in such....”", "그녀는 불행한 여자가 불행할 수 있는 만큼 불행한데, 자네가 그런 상황에서 거절할 수는…….\""]], [["“As much of what I promised as is possible.", "\"내가 약속한 것 가운데 가능한 만큼은 하겠어."], ["Vous professez d’être libre penseur.", "Vous professez d'être libre penseur."], ["But I as a believer cannot, in a matter of such gravity, act in opposition to the Christian law.”", "하지만 나는 신앙인으로서, 이토록 중대한 문제에서 기독교 율법에 거슬러 행동할 수는 없네.\""]], [["“But in Christian societies and among us, as far as I’m aware, divorce is allowed,”", "\"하지만 기독교 사회에서도, 우리들 사이에서도, 내가 알기로는 이혼이 허용되잖나.\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch.", "스테판 아르카지치가 말했다."], ["“Divorce is sanctioned even by our church.", "\"이혼은 우리 교회에서도 인정하고 있어."], ["And we see....”", "그리고 우리는 보고 있지…….\""]], [["“It is allowed, but not in the sense....”", "\"허용은 되지만, 그런 의미에서는 아니야…….\""]], [["“Alexey Alexandrovitch, you are not like yourself,”", "\"알렉세이 알렉산드로비치, 자네답지 않군.\""], ["said Oblonsky, after a brief pause.", "오블론스키가 잠시 사이를 두었다가 말했다."], ["“Wasn’t it you (and didn’t we all appreciate it in you?) who forgave everything, and moved simply by Christian feeling was ready to make any sacrifice?", "\"모든 걸 용서했던 게, (그리고 우리 모두 그 점을 자네에게서 높이 샀던 게) 바로 자네 아니었나? 순전히 기독교적 감정에 이끌려 어떤 희생이라도 치를 각오가 되어 있던 게 말이야."], ["You said yourself: if a man take thy coat, give him thy cloak also, and now....”", "자네가 직접 말했잖아. 누가 네 속옷을 가지려 하거든 겉옷까지도 주라고, 그런데 지금은…….\""]], [["“I beg,”", "\"부탁하네.\""], ["said Alexey Alexandrovitch shrilly, getting suddenly onto his feet, his face white and his jaws twitching, “I beg you to drop this ...", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 째지는 소리로 말하며, 얼굴이 하얗게 질리고 턱을 씰룩이며 별안간 벌떡 일어섰다."], ["to drop ... this subject!”", "\"부탁이니 이걸 그만두게……이 얘기를……그만둬!\""]], [["“Oh, no!", "\"오, 아니야!"], ["Oh, forgive me, forgive me if I have wounded you,”", "오, 미안하네, 내가 자네에게 상처를 줬다면 용서해 주게.\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, holding out his hand with a smile of embarrassment; “but like a messenger I have simply performed the commission given me.”", "스테판 아르카지치가 멋쩍은 미소를 지으며 손을 내밀었다. \"하지만 나는 그저 심부름꾼처럼, 내게 맡겨진 일을 수행했을 뿐일세.\""]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch gave him his hand, pondered a little, and said:", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 그에게 손을 내밀고는 잠시 생각에 잠겼다가 말했다."]], [["“I must think it over and seek for guidance.", "\"곰곰이 생각해 보고 길을 찾아봐야겠네."], ["The day after tomorrow I will give you a final answer,”", "모레 자네에게 최종적인 답을 주겠어.\""], ["he said, after considering a moment.", "그는 잠시 헤아려 본 뒤에 말했다."]], [["# Chapter 19", "# Chapter 19"]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch was about to go away when Korney came in to announce:", "스테판 아르카지치가 막 떠나려는데 코르네이가 들어와 알렸다."]], [["“Sergey Alexyevitch!”", "\"세르게이 알렉세이치 도련님이십니다!\""]], [["“Who’s Sergey Alexyevitch?”", "\"세르게이 알렉세이치가 누구지?\""], ["Stepan Arkadyevitch was beginning, but he remembered immediately.", "스테판 아르카지치는 입을 떼려다가 곧바로 떠올렸다."]], [["“Ah, Seryozha!”", "\"아, 세료자!\""], ["he said aloud.", "그는 소리 내어 말했다."], ["“Sergey Alexyevitch!", "\"세르게이 알렉세이치라니!"], ["I thought it was the director of a department.", "나는 무슨 관청 국장이라도 되는 줄 알았네."], ["Anna asked me to see him too,”", "안나도 그 애를 만나 달라고 했었지.\""], ["he thought.", "그는 생각했다."]], [["And he recalled the timid, piteous expression with which Anna had said to him at parting: “Anyway, you will see him.", "그러자 헤어질 때 안나가 겁먹고 애처로운 표정으로 그에게 했던 말이 떠올랐다. \"어쨌든 그 애를 보게 될 거예요."], ["Find out exactly where he is, who is looking after him.", "그 애가 정확히 어디 있는지, 누가 돌보고 있는지 알아봐 주세요."], ["And Stiva ...", "그리고 스티바……"], ["if it were possible!", "혹시 가능하다면!"], ["Could it be possible?”", "그게 가능할까요?\""], ["Stepan Arkadyevitch knew what was meant by that “if it were possible,”—if it were possible to arrange the divorce so as to let her have her son....", "스테판 아르카지치는 그 \"혹시 가능하다면\"이 무슨 뜻인지 알고 있었다—이혼을 그녀가 아들을 데리고 갈 수 있도록 성사시키는 게 가능하다면……"], ["Stepan Arkadyevitch saw now that it was no good to dream of that, but still he was glad to see his nephew.", "스테판 아르카지치는 이제 그런 것을 꿈꿔 봐야 소용없다는 것을 알았지만, 그래도 조카를 보게 되어 반가웠다."]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch reminded his brother-in-law that they never spoke to the boy of his mother, and he begged him not to mention a single word about her.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 처남에게 아이 앞에서는 결코 그 어머니 이야기를 하지 않는다는 점을 일러두며, 그녀에 관해서는 단 한마디도 꺼내지 말아 달라고 당부했다."]], [["“He was very ill after that interview with his mother, which we had not foreseen,”", "\"어머니와 그 면회를 한 뒤에 그 애는 몹시 앓았소."], ["said Alexey Alexandrovitch.", "우리도 예상하지 못한 일이었지.\""], ["“Indeed, we feared for his life.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 말했다."], ["But with rational treatment, and sea-bathing in the summer, he regained his strength, and now, by the doctor’s advice, I have let him go to school.", "\"정말이지 그 애의 목숨까지 걱정했었소. 하지만 합리적인 치료에다 여름에 바닷물 목욕을 하고 나서 기력을 되찾았고, 이제는 의사의 권유에 따라 학교에 보냈소."], ["And certainly the companionship of school has had a good effect on him, and he is perfectly well, and making good progress.”", "그리고 확실히 학교 친구들과 어울리는 것이 그 애에게 좋은 영향을 미쳐서, 지금은 아주 건강하고 공부도 잘하고 있소.\""]], [["“What a fine fellow he’s grown!", "\"참 늠름하게 자랐군!"], ["He’s not Seryozha now, but quite full-fledged Sergey Alexyevitch!”", "이제는 세료자가 아니라 어엿한 세르게이 알렉세이치인걸!\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, smiling, as he looked at the handsome, broad-shouldered lad in blue coat and long trousers, who walked in alertly and confidently.", "스테판 아르카지치는 미소를 지으며 말했는데, 파란 윗도리에 긴 바지를 입고 또랑또랑하고 자신감 있는 걸음으로 들어오는, 잘생기고 어깨가 떡 벌어진 소년을 바라보면서였다."], ["The boy looked healthy and good-humored.", "아이는 건강하고 명랑해 보였다."], ["He bowed to his uncle as to a stranger, but recognizing him, he blushed and turned hurriedly away from him, as though offended and irritated at something.", "아이는 낯선 사람을 대하듯 외삼촌에게 절을 했지만, 그를 알아보고는 얼굴을 붉히며 무언가에 기분이 상하고 짜증이 난 듯 황급히 그에게서 돌아섰다."], ["The boy went up to his father and handed him a note of the marks he had gained in school.", "아이는 아버지에게 다가가 학교에서 받은 성적표를 건넸다."]], [["“Well, that’s very fair,”", "\"음, 아주 괜찮구나.\""], ["said his father, “you can go.”", "아버지가 말했다. \"이제 가 보아라.\""]], [["“He’s thinner and taller, and has grown out of being a child into a boy; I like that,”", "\"전보다 호리호리해지고 키도 컸어. 아이 티를 벗고 소년이 다 됐군."], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch.", "그게 마음에 드는걸.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 말했다."], ["“Do you remember me?”", "\"나를 기억하니?\""]], [["The boy looked back quickly at his uncle.", "아이는 재빨리 외삼촌을 돌아보았다."]], [["“Yes, mon oncle,”", "\"네, mon oncle.\""], ["he answered, glancing at his father, and again he looked downcast.", "아이는 아버지를 흘끗 쳐다보며 대답하더니, 다시 풀 죽은 표정을 지었다."]], [["His uncle called him to him, and took his hand.", "* mon oncle: 프랑스어로 '외삼촌'."]], [["“Well, and how are you getting on?”", "\"그래, 요즘은 어떻게 지내니?\""], ["he said, wanting to talk to him, and not knowing what to say.", "그는 아이와 이야기를 나누고 싶었지만 무슨 말을 해야 할지 몰라 그렇게 물었다."]], [["The boy, blushing and making no answer, cautiously drew his hand away.", "아이는 얼굴을 붉히며 아무 대답도 하지 않고 슬그머니 손을 빼냈다."], ["As soon as Stepan Arkadyevitch let go his hand, he glanced doubtfully at his father, and like a bird set free, he darted out of the room.", "스테판 아르카지치가 손을 놓아 주기가 무섭게, 아이는 미심쩍은 눈으로 아버지를 흘끗 쳐다보더니, 풀려난 새처럼 방에서 쏜살같이 나가 버렸다."]], [["A year had passed since the last time Seryozha had seen his mother.", "세료자가 어머니를 마지막으로 본 지 일 년이 지났다."], ["Since then he had heard nothing more of her.", "그 뒤로 아이는 어머니에 대해 아무 소식도 듣지 못했다."], ["And in the course of that year he had gone to school, and made friends among his schoolfellows.", "그리고 그 한 해 동안 아이는 학교에 들어갔고, 학교 친구들과 사귀게 되었다."], ["The dreams and memories of his mother, which had made him ill after seeing her, did not occupy his thoughts now.", "어머니를 본 뒤 자신을 앓아눕게 만들었던 그 꿈과 기억은 이제 아이의 생각을 차지하지 않았다."], ["When they came back to him, he studiously drove them away, regarding them as shameful and girlish, below the dignity of a boy and a schoolboy.", "그것들이 되살아날 때면 아이는 그것을 부끄럽고 계집애 같은 것, 소년이자 학생인 자기의 체면에 어울리지 않는 것으로 여기며 애써 몰아냈다."], ["He knew that his father and mother were separated by some quarrel, he knew that he had to remain with his father, and he tried to get used to that idea.", "아이는 아버지와 어머니가 무슨 다툼으로 갈라섰다는 것을 알았고, 자기는 아버지와 함께 남아야 한다는 것을 알았으며, 그 생각에 익숙해지려고 애썼다."]], [["He disliked seeing his uncle, so like his mother, for it called up those memories of which he was ashamed.", "아이는 어머니를 그토록 닮은 외삼촌을 보는 것이 싫었다. 그것이 부끄러워하던 그 기억들을 불러일으켰기 때문이다."], ["He disliked it all the more as from some words he had caught as he waited at the study door, and still more from the faces of his father and uncle, he guessed that they must have been talking of his mother.", "게다가 서재 문 앞에서 기다리는 동안 주워들은 몇 마디 말에서, 그리고 무엇보다 아버지와 외삼촌의 표정에서, 두 사람이 어머니 이야기를 나누고 있었음을 짐작했기에 더더욱 싫었다."], ["And to avoid condemning the father with whom he lived and on whom he was dependent, and, above all, to avoid giving way to sentimentality, which he considered so degrading, Seryozha tried not to look at his uncle who had come to disturb his peace of mind, and not to think of what he recalled to him.", "그래서 자기가 함께 살고 의지하고 있는 아버지를 비난하지 않기 위해, 그리고 무엇보다 그토록 치욕스럽게 여기는 감상에 빠지지 않기 위해, 세료자는 자기 마음의 평화를 어지럽히러 온 외삼촌을 쳐다보지 않으려, 그리고 그가 떠올리게 하는 것을 생각하지 않으려 애썼다."]], [["But when Stepan Arkadyevitch, going out after him, saw him on the stairs, and calling to him, asked him how he spent his playtime at school, Seryozha talked more freely to him away from his father’s presence.", "그러나 스테판 아르카지치가 아이를 뒤따라 나오다가 계단에서 그를 보고 불러 세워, 학교에서 쉬는 시간을 어떻게 보내느냐고 묻자, 아버지가 없는 자리에서 세료자는 한결 거리낌 없이 그에게 이야기했다."]], [["“We have a railway now,”", "\"이제 우리한테는 철도가 있어요.\""], ["he said in answer to his uncle’s question.", "아이는 외삼촌의 물음에 답하며 말했다. \"그러니까 이런 거예요."], ["“It’s like this, do you see: two sit on a bench—they’re the passengers; and one stands up straight on the bench.", "보세요. 둘이 의자에 앉아요—그 애들이 승객이에요."], ["And all are harnessed to it by their arms or by their belts, and they run through all the rooms—the doors are left open beforehand.", "그리고 한 명은 의자 위에 똑바로 서요. 그러면 다들 팔이나 허리띠로 그 애한테 묶여서 모든 방을 가로질러 달리는 거예요—문은 미리 다 열어 두고요."], ["Well, and it’s pretty hard work being the conductor!”", "그런데 차장 노릇 하기가 꽤 힘들어요!\""]], [["“That’s the one that stands?”", "\"그게 서 있는 애란 말이지?\""], ["Stepan Arkadyevitch inquired, smiling.", "스테판 아르카지치가 미소를 지으며 물었다."]], [["“Yes, you want pluck for it, and cleverness too, especially when they stop all of a sudden, or someone falls down.”", "\"네, 그러려면 배짱도 있어야 하고 머리도 좋아야 해요. 특히 다들 갑자기 멈추거나 누가 넘어질 때는요.\""]], [["“Yes, that must be a serious matter,”", "\"그래, 그건 정말 중요한 일이겠구나.\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, watching with mournful interest the eager eyes, like his mother’s; not childish now—no longer fully innocent.", "스테판 아르카지치는 어머니를 닮은 그 초롱초롱한 눈을 서글픈 흥미로 바라보며 말했다. 이제는 어린애 같지도 않고, 더 이상 온전히 천진하지도 않은 눈이었다."], ["And though he had promised Alexey Alexandrovitch not to speak of Anna, he could not restrain himself.", "그리고 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에게 안나 이야기를 하지 않겠다고 약속했건만, 그는 자신을 억누를 수 없었다."]], [["“Do you remember your mother?”", "\"너, 어머니를 기억하니?\""], ["he asked suddenly.", "그는 불쑥 물었다."]], [["“No, I don’t,”", "\"아뇨, 기억 안 나요.\""], ["Seryozha said quickly.", "세료자가 재빨리 말했다."], ["He blushed crimson, and his face clouded over.", "아이는 새빨갛게 얼굴을 붉혔고, 표정이 어두워졌다."], ["And his uncle could get nothing more out of him.", "외삼촌은 그에게서 더 이상 아무 말도 끌어낼 수 없었다."], ["His tutor found his pupil on the staircase half an hour later, and for a long while he could not make out whether he was ill-tempered or crying.", "가정교사는 삼십 분쯤 뒤에 계단에서 제자를 발견했는데, 한참 동안 아이가 화가 난 것인지 울고 있는 것인지 분간할 수 없었다."]], [["“What is it?", "\"무슨 일이냐?"], ["I expect you hurt yourself when you fell down?”", "넘어지면서 다친 모양이구나?\""], ["said the tutor.", "가정교사가 말했다."], ["“I told you it was a dangerous game.", "\"내가 그건 위험한 놀이라고 했잖니."], ["And we shall have to speak to the director.”", "교장 선생님께 말씀드려야겠다.\""]], [["“If I had hurt myself, nobody should have found it out, that’s certain.”", "\"제가 다쳤더라도 아무한테도 들키지 않았을 거예요. 그건 확실해요.\""]], [["“Well, what is it, then?”", "\"그럼 대체 무슨 일이냐?\""]], [["“Leave me alone!", "\"날 좀 내버려 둬요!"], ["If I remember, or if I don’t remember?...", "내가 기억하든 안 하든……"], ["what business is it of his?", "그게 그분이랑 무슨 상관이에요?"], ["Why should I remember?", "내가 왜 기억해야 해요?"], ["Leave me in peace!”", "날 좀 가만히 내버려 두라고요!\""], ["he said, addressing not his tutor, but the whole world.", "아이는 가정교사가 아니라 온 세상을 향해 말했다."]], [["# Chapter 20", "# Chapter 20"]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch, as usual, did not waste his time in Petersburg.", "스테판 아르카지치는 여느 때처럼 페테르부르크에서 시간을 헛되이 보내지 않았다."], ["In Petersburg, besides business, his sister’s divorce, and his coveted appointment, he wanted, as he always did, to freshen himself up, as he said, after the mustiness of Moscow.", "페테르부르크에서는 볼일, 즉 누이의 이혼 문제와 그토록 갈망하던 자리 외에도, 그는 늘 그러하듯 자기 표현대로 모스크바의 곰팡내를 씻어 내고 새로워지고 싶었다."]], [["In spite of its cafés chantants and its omnibuses, Moscow was yet a stagnant bog.", "카페 샹탕과 합승마차가 있다 해도 모스크바는 여전히 고인 늪이었다."], ["Stepan Arkadyevitch always felt it.", "스테판 아르카지치는 늘 그렇게 느꼈다."], ["After living for some time in Moscow, especially in close relations with his family, he was conscious of a depression of spirits.", "모스크바에서 얼마간 살다 보면, 특히 가족과 가까이 지내다 보면, 그는 마음이 가라앉는 것을 의식했다."], ["After being a long time in Moscow without a change, he reached a point when he positively began to be worrying himself over his wife’s ill-humor and reproaches, over his children’s health and education, and the petty details of his official work; even the fact of being in debt worried him.", "변화 없이 모스크바에서 오래 지내다 보면, 그는 아내의 언짢은 기분과 잔소리며, 아이들의 건강과 교육이며, 직무의 자질구레한 세부사항에 대해 어느새 진지하게 안달복달하기 시작하는 지경에 이르렀다. 심지어 빚을 지고 있다는 사실조차 그를 괴롭혔다."], ["But he had only to go and stay a little while in Petersburg, in the circle there in which he moved, where people lived—really lived—instead of vegetating as in Moscow, and all such ideas vanished and melted away at once, like wax before the fire.", "그러나 그저 페테르부르크에 가서 그가 드나드는 그 사교계 모임에 잠시 머무르기만 하면, 그곳에서는 사람들이 모스크바에서처럼 그저 풀처럼 시들시들 연명하는 것이 아니라 진짜로, 정말로 살아가고 있었기에, 그런 온갖 생각들은 불 앞의 밀랍처럼 단숨에 사라져 녹아 버렸다."], ["His wife?...", "아내라고?……"], ["Only that day he had been talking to Prince Tchetchensky.", "바로 그날 그는 체첸스키 공작과 이야기를 나누었다."], ["Prince Tchetchensky had a wife and family, grown-up pages in the corps, ...", "체첸스키 공작에게는 아내와 가족이 있었고, 사관학교에 다 큰 시동들도 있었으며……"], ["and he had another illegitimate family of children also.", "게다가 또 하나의 사생아 자식들로 이루어진 가족도 있었다."], ["Though the first family was very nice too, Prince Tchetchensky felt happier in his second family; and he used to take his eldest son with him to his second family, and told Stepan Arkadyevitch that he thought it good for his son, enlarging his ideas.", "첫 번째 가족도 무척 좋았지만, 체첸스키 공작은 두 번째 가족에서 더 행복하다고 느꼈다. 그는 큰아들을 두 번째 가족에게 데려가곤 했고, 그것이 아들의 견문을 넓혀 주어 아들에게 좋다고 생각한다고 스테판 아르카지치에게 말했다."], ["What would have been said to that in Moscow?", "모스크바에서 그런 말을 하면 뭐라고들 했을까?"]], [["His children?", "아이들은?"], ["In Petersburg children did not prevent their parents from enjoying life.", "페테르부르크에서는 아이들이 부모가 인생을 즐기는 것을 가로막지 않았다."], ["The children were brought up in schools, and there was no trace of the wild idea that prevailed in Moscow, in Lvov’s household, for instance, that all the luxuries of life were for the children, while the parents have nothing but work and anxiety.", "아이들은 학교에서 길러졌고, 모스크바에서, 이를테면 리보프 집안 같은 데서 횡행하던 그 터무니없는 생각—인생의 온갖 호사는 모두 아이들 몫이고 부모에게는 오직 일과 근심만 있을 뿐이라는—같은 것은 흔적도 없었다."], ["Here people understood that a man is in duty bound to live for himself, as every man of culture should live.", "이곳 사람들은 교양 있는 사람이라면 누구나 그래야 하듯 인간은 자기 자신을 위해 살아야 할 의무가 있다는 것을 이해하고 있었다."]], [["His official duties?", "직무는?"], ["Official work here was not the stiff, hopeless drudgery that it was in Moscow.", "이곳의 공직 일은 모스크바에서처럼 뻣뻣하고 가망 없는 노역이 아니었다."], ["Here there was some interest in official life.", "이곳에서는 관직 생활에 흥미로운 데가 있었다."], ["A chance meeting, a service rendered, a happy phrase, a knack of facetious mimicry, and a man’s career might be made in a trice.", "우연한 만남, 베푼 호의, 적절한 한마디, 익살스럽게 흉내 내는 재주만 있으면, 한 사람의 출셋길이 순식간에 열릴 수도 있었다."], ["So it had been with Bryantsev, whom Stepan Arkadyevitch had met the previous day, and who was one of the highest functionaries in government now.", "스테판 아르카지치가 전날 만난 브랸체프가 바로 그랬는데, 그는 이제 정부의 최고위 관료 가운데 하나였다."], ["There was some interest in official work like that.", "그런 식의 공직 일에는 흥미로운 데가 있었다."]], [["The Petersburg attitude on pecuniary matters had an especially soothing effect on Stepan Arkadyevitch.", "금전 문제를 대하는 페테르부르크의 태도는 특히 스테판 아르카지치의 마음을 달래 주는 효과가 있었다."], ["Bartnyansky, who must spend at least fifty thousand to judge by the style he lived in, had made an interesting comment the day before on that subject.", "사는 꼴을 보아 적어도 오만 루블은 써야 할 바르트냔스키가 그 문제에 관해 전날 흥미로운 말을 한 적이 있었다."]], [["As they were talking before dinner, Stepan Arkadyevitch said to Bartnyansky:", "만찬 전에 이야기를 나누다가 스테판 아르카지치는 바르트냔스키에게 말했다."]], [["“You’re friendly, I fancy, with Mordvinsky; you might do me a favor: say a word to him, please, for me.", "\"자네는 모르드빈스키와 친한 것 같던데, 부탁 하나 들어주게나. 나를 위해 그 사람한테 한마디 해 주게."], ["There’s an appointment I should like to get—secretary of the agency....”", "내가 얻고 싶은 자리가 하나 있어—그 대리국의 서기관이라네……\""]], [["“Oh, I shan’t remember all that, if you tell it to me....", "\"아, 그렇게 말해 봤자 난 그런 거 다 기억 못 해……"], ["But what possesses you to have to do with railways and Jews?...", "한데 도대체 무슨 마음으로 철도며 유대인들이랑 엮이려는 건가?……"], ["Take it as you will, it’s a low business.”", "어떻게 받아들이든, 그건 천한 일이야.\""]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch did not say to Bartnyansky that it was a “growing thing”—Bartnyansky would not have understood that.", "스테판 아르카지치는 그것이 \"전망 있는 사업\"이라고 바르트냔스키에게 말하지 않았다—바르트냔스키는 그 말을 이해하지 못했을 테니까."]], [["“I want the money, I’ve nothing to live on.”", "\"돈이 필요하다네. 먹고살 게 없어서.\""]], [["“You’re living, aren’t you?”", "\"먹고살고 있잖나, 안 그래?\""]], [["“Yes, but in debt.”", "\"그렇지, 하지만 빚을 지고 있다네.\""]], [["“Are you, though?", "\"정말인가? 많이?\""], ["Heavily?” said Bartnyansky sympathetically.", "바르트냔스키가 동정 어린 어조로 물었다."]], [["“Very heavily: twenty thousand.”", "\"아주 많지. 이만 루블이야.\""]], [["Bartnyansky broke into good-humored laughter.", "바르트냔스키가 너털웃음을 터뜨렸다."]], [["“Oh, lucky fellow!”", "\"오, 복 받은 사람 같으니!\""], ["said he.", "그가 말했다."], ["“My debts mount up to a million and a half, and I’ve nothing, and still I can live, as you see!”", "\"내 빚은 백오십만 루블에 이르고, 가진 건 아무것도 없는데, 그래도 자네가 보다시피 멀쩡히 살고 있잖나!\""]], [["And Stepan Arkadyevitch saw the correctness of this view not in words only but in actual fact.", "그리고 스테판 아르카지치는 이 견해가 옳다는 것을 말로만이 아니라 실제 사실로도 확인했다."], ["Zhivahov owed three hundred thousand, and hadn’t a farthing to bless himself with, and he lived, and in style too!", "지바호프는 삼십만 루블의 빚을 지고 한 푼도 가진 게 없었지만 그래도 살았고, 그것도 호사스럽게 살았다!"], ["Count Krivtsov was considered a hopeless case by everyone, and yet he kept two mistresses.", "크리프초프 백작은 모두에게 가망 없는 인간으로 여겨졌지만, 그래도 정부를 둘이나 거느리고 있었다."], ["Petrovsky had run through five millions, and still lived in just the same style, and was even a manager in the financial department with a salary of twenty thousand.", "페트로프스키는 오백만 루블을 탕진했지만, 여전히 똑같은 방식으로 살았고, 게다가 재무부에서 이만 루블의 봉급을 받는 관리자였다."], ["But besides this, Petersburg had physically an agreeable effect on Stepan Arkadyevitch.", "그러나 이것 말고도 페테르부르크는 스테판 아르카지치에게 육체적으로도 기분 좋은 영향을 미쳤다."], ["It made him younger.", "그것이 그를 젊게 만들었다."], ["In Moscow he sometimes found a gray hair in his head, dropped asleep after dinner, stretched, walked slowly upstairs, breathing heavily, was bored by the society of young women, and did not dance at balls.", "모스크바에서는 이따금 머리에서 흰머리를 발견하고, 만찬 후에는 잠이 들고, 기지개를 켜고, 가쁜 숨을 몰아쉬며 천천히 계단을 오르고, 젊은 여자들과 어울리는 것을 지루해하고, 무도회에서 춤도 추지 않았다."], ["In Petersburg he always felt ten years younger.", "페테르부르크에서는 늘 열 살은 젊어진 기분이었다."]], [["His experience in Petersburg was exactly what had been described to him on the previous day by Prince Pyotr Oblonsky, a man of sixty, who had just come back from abroad:", "페테르부르크에서 그가 겪은 일은 전날 막 외국에서 돌아온 예순 살의 표트르 오블론스키 공작이 그에게 들려준 것과 꼭 같았다."]], [["“We don’t know the way to live here,”", "\"우리는 여기서 사는 법을 모른단 말일세.\""], ["said Pyotr Oblonsky.", "표트르 오블론스키가 말했다."], ["“I spent the summer in Baden, and you wouldn’t believe it, I felt quite a young man.", "\"여름을 바덴에서 보냈는데, 자네는 믿지 못하겠지만, 내가 아주 젊은이가 된 기분이었어."], ["At a glimpse of a pretty woman, my thoughts....", "예쁜 여자가 언뜻 보이면 내 생각이란 게……"], ["One dines and drinks a glass of wine, and feels strong and ready for anything.", "식사를 하고 포도주를 한 잔 마시면 힘이 넘치고 뭐든 할 수 있을 것 같았지."], ["I came home to Russia—had to see my wife, and, what’s more, go to my country place; and there, you’d hardly believe it, in a fortnight I’d got into a dressing gown and given up dressing for dinner.", "러시아 집으로 돌아왔는데—아내도 봐야 했고, 게다가 시골 영지에도 가야 했는데, 거기서, 자네는 도무지 믿지 못할 거야, 보름 만에 실내복을 걸치고 만찬 때 정장 차려입는 일도 그만뒀다네."], ["Needn’t say I had no thoughts left for pretty women.", "예쁜 여자들 생각 따위 남지 않았다는 건 말할 것도 없고."], ["I became quite an old gentleman.", "나는 아주 늙은이가 돼 버렸어."], ["There was nothing left for me but to think of my eternal salvation.", "영혼의 구원이나 생각하는 것 말고는 할 일이 없었지."], ["I went off to Paris—I was as right as could be at once.”", "그러다 파리로 떠났더니—당장에 더없이 멀쩡해지더군.\""]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch felt exactly the difference that Pyotr Oblonsky described.", "스테판 아르카지치는 표트르 오블론스키가 말한 그 차이를 똑같이 느꼈다."], ["In Moscow he degenerated so much that if he had had to be there for long together, he might in good earnest have come to considering his salvation; in Petersburg he felt himself a man of the world again.", "모스크바에서는 어찌나 퇴락하는지, 거기서 오래 붙박여 지내야 했다면 정말로 자기 구원을 진지하게 생각하는 지경에 이르렀을지도 몰랐다. 페테르부르크에서는 다시 사교계의 한 인물이 된 기분이었다."]], [["Between Princess Betsy Tverskaya and Stepan Arkadyevitch there had long existed rather curious relations.", "베시 트베르스카야 공작 부인과 스테판 아르카지치 사이에는 오래전부터 다소 묘한 관계가 있었다."], ["Stepan Arkadyevitch always flirted with her in jest, and used to say to her, also in jest, the most unseemly things, knowing that nothing delighted her so much.", "스테판 아르카지치는 늘 농담조로 그녀에게 추파를 던졌고, 역시 농담조로 더없이 점잖지 못한 말을 그녀에게 하곤 했는데, 그녀가 그런 것을 무엇보다 좋아한다는 것을 알았기 때문이다."], ["The day after his conversation with Karenin, Stepan Arkadyevitch went to see her, and felt so youthful that in this jesting flirtation and nonsense he recklessly went so far that he did not know how to extricate himself, as unluckily he was so far from being attracted by her that he thought her positively disagreeable.", "카레닌과 이야기를 나눈 다음 날, 스테판 아르카지치는 그녀를 보러 갔고, 어찌나 젊어진 기분이었던지 이 농담 섞인 추파와 객쩍은 수작에서 분별없이 너무 멀리 나가 버려, 어떻게 빠져나와야 할지 알 수 없게 되었다. 공교롭게도 그는 그녀에게 끌리기는커녕 오히려 그녀가 영 마음에 들지 않는다고 여기고 있었기 때문이다."], ["What made it hard to change the conversation was the fact that he was very attractive to her.", "화제를 바꾸기 어렵게 만든 것은 그가 그녀에게 무척 매력적인 존재였다는 사실이었다."], ["So that he was considerably relieved at the arrival of Princess Myakaya, which cut short their tête-à-tête.", "그래서 그는 먀흐카야 공작 부인이 도착해 둘만의 자리가 중단되자 상당히 안도했다."]], [["“Ah, so you’re here!”", "\"아, 여기 와 계셨군요!\""], ["said she when she saw him.", "그녀가 그를 보고 말했다."], ["“Well, and what news of your poor sister?", "\"그래, 그 가엾은 누이동생은 어떻게 지내나요?"], ["You needn’t look at me like that,”", "그렇게 쳐다보실 것 없어요.\""], ["she added.", "그녀가 덧붙였다."], ["“Ever since they’ve all turned against her, all those who’re a thousand times worse than she, I’ve thought she did a very fine thing.", "\"그 여자보다 천 배는 더 못된 사람들이 죄다 그녀에게 등을 돌린 뒤로, 나는 그녀가 아주 훌륭한 일을 했다고 생각해 왔어요."], ["I can’t forgive Vronsky for not letting me know when she was in Petersburg.", "브론스키가 그녀가 페테르부르크에 있을 때 내게 알려 주지 않은 건 도저히 용서할 수 없어요."], ["I’d have gone to see her and gone about with her everywhere.", "알았더라면 보러 갔을 테고, 어디든 함께 다녔을 텐데."], ["Please give her my love.", "부디 그녀에게 내 안부를 전해 주세요."], ["Come, tell me about her.”", "자, 그녀 이야기 좀 들려주세요.\""]], [["“Yes, her position is very difficult; she....”", "\"네, 그 애 처지가 무척 힘들지요. 그 애는……\""], ["began Stepan Arkadyevitch, in the simplicity of his heart accepting as sterling coin Princess Myakaya’s words “tell me about her.”", "스테판 아르카지치는 먀흐카야 공작 부인의 \"그녀 이야기 좀 들려주세요\"라는 말을 순진하게도 액면 그대로 받아들이고 말문을 열었다."], ["Princess Myakaya interrupted him immediately, as she always did, and began talking herself.", "먀흐카야 공작 부인은 늘 그러듯 곧바로 그의 말을 가로막고 자기가 이야기하기 시작했다."]], [["“She’s done what they all do, except me—only they hide it.", "\"그녀는 나만 빼고 다들 하는 짓을 한 거예요—다만 다른 사람들은 그걸 숨기죠."], ["But she wouldn’t be deceitful, and she did a fine thing.", "한데 그녀는 거짓말을 하려 들지 않았고, 그래서 훌륭한 일을 한 거예요."], ["And she did better still in throwing up that crazy brother-in-law of yours.", "게다가 당신의 그 정신 나간 매제를 차 버린 건 더 잘한 일이고요."], ["You must excuse me.", "양해해 주세요."], ["Everybody used to say he was so clever, so very clever; I was the only one that said he was a fool.", "다들 그 사람이 아주 영리하다고, 정말 대단히 영리하다고 했지만, 그를 바보라고 한 건 나 혼자뿐이었어요."], ["Now that he’s so thick with Lidia Ivanovna and Landau, they all say he’s crazy, and I should prefer not to agree with everybody, but this time I can’t help it.”", "이제 그가 리디아 이바노브나며 란다우와 그렇게 가까워지고 나니, 다들 그가 미쳤다고들 하네요. 나야 모두와 의견을 같이하기 싫지만, 이번만은 어쩔 수가 없군요.\""]], [["“Oh, do please explain,”", "\"오, 제발 설명 좀 해 주세요.\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch; “what does it mean?", "스테판 아르카지치가 말했다."], ["Yesterday I was seeing him on my sister’s behalf, and I asked him to give me a final answer.", "\"그게 무슨 뜻인가요? 어제 누이동생 일로 그를 만나서 최종 답변을 달라고 했지요."], ["He gave me no answer, and said he would think it over.", "그는 아무 답도 주지 않고, 생각해 보겠다고 했어요."], ["But this morning, instead of an answer, I received an invitation from Countess Lidia Ivanovna for this evening.”", "그런데 오늘 아침 답변 대신 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인에게서 오늘 저녁에 와 달라는 초대를 받았답니다.\""]], [["“Ah, so that’s it, that’s it!”", "\"아, 그거였군요, 그거예요!\""], ["said Princess Myakaya gleefully, “they’re going to ask Landau what he’s to say.”", "먀흐카야 공작 부인이 신이 나서 말했다. \"그들은 란다우에게 무슨 말을 해야 할지 물어보려는 거예요.\""]], [["“Ask Landau?", "\"란다우에게 물어본다고요?"], ["What for?", "무엇 때문에요?"], ["Who or what’s Landau?”", "도대체 란다우가 누구입니까, 뭐 하는 사람이에요?\""]], [["“What!", "\"아니!"], ["you don’t know Jules Landau, le fameux Jules Landau, le clairvoyant?", "쥘 란다우를 모르세요, le fameux Jules Landau, le clairvoyant 말이에요?"], ["He’s crazy too, but on him your sister’s fate depends.", "그 사람도 미쳤지만, 당신 누이동생의 운명이 그 사람한테 달려 있어요."], ["See what comes of living in the provinces—you know nothing about anything.", "시골에서 사니까 이렇게 되는 거예요—세상일을 아무것도 모르잖아요."], ["Landau, do you see, was a commis in a shop in Paris, and he went to a doctor’s; and in the doctor’s waiting room he fell asleep, and in his sleep he began giving advice to all the patients.", "란다우는 말이죠, 파리의 어느 가게 점원이었는데, 의사한테 갔다가 그 의사의 대기실에서 잠이 들었어요. 그런데 잠든 채로 환자들 모두에게 충고를 하기 시작한 거예요."], ["And wonderful advice it was!", "그것도 놀라운 충고였죠!"], ["Then the wife of Yury Meledinsky—you know, the invalid?—heard of this Landau, and had him to see her husband.", "그러다 유리 멜레딘스키—아시죠, 그 병약한 사람?—그 사람의 아내가 이 란다우 이야기를 듣고, 남편을 보게 했어요. 그러고는 그가 그녀의 남편을 고쳤는데, 내가 보기엔 별로 효험이 있었던 것 같진 않아요."], ["And he cured her husband, though I can’t say that I see he did him much good, for he’s just as feeble a creature as ever he was, but they believed in him, and took him along with them and brought him to Russia.", "그 사람은 여전히 예전처럼 비실비실한 인간이니까요. 어쨌든 사람들이 그를 믿고, 데리고 다니다가 러시아까지 데려온 거예요."], ["Here there’s been a general rush to him, and he’s begun doctoring everyone.", "여기서는 다들 그에게 우르르 몰려들었고, 그는 모든 사람을 치료하기 시작했죠. 베주보바 백작 부인을 고쳐 주었더니, 부인이 어찌나 그를 마음에 들어 했던지 그를 양자로 삼았답니다.\""], ["He cured Countess Bezzubova, and she took such a fancy to him that she adopted him.”", "* le fameux Jules Landau, le clairvoyant: 프랑스어로 '저 유명한 쥘 란다우, 그 천리안'이라는 뜻."]], [["“Adopted him?”", "\"양자로 삼았다고요?\""]], [["“Yes, as her son.", "\"그래요, 아들로요."], ["He’s not Landau any more now, but Count Bezzubov.", "그는 이제 더는 란다우가 아니라 베주보프 백작이에요. 하긴 그건 아무래도 상관없는 일이지만요."], ["That’s neither here nor there, though; but Lidia—I’m very fond of her, but she has a screw loose somewhere—has lost her heart to this Landau now, and nothing is settled now in her house or Alexey Alexandrovitch’s without him, and so your sister’s fate is now in the hands of Landau, alias Count Bezzubov.”", "다만 리디아가—난 그녀를 무척 좋아하지만, 어딘가 나사가 하나 빠진 사람이에요—이제 이 란다우에게 홀딱 마음을 빼앗겨서, 그녀의 집에서든 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 집에서든 그 사람 없이는 아무것도 결정되지 않아요. 그러니 당신 누이동생의 운명이 이제 란다우, 다시 말해 베주보프 백작의 손에 달려 있다는 거죠.\""]], [["# Chapter 21", "# Chapter 21"]], [["After a capital dinner and a great deal of cognac drunk at Bartnyansky’s, Stepan Arkadyevitch, only a little later than the appointed time, went in to Countess Lidia Ivanovna’s.", "바르트냔스키 집에서 훌륭한 만찬을 들고 코냑을 잔뜩 마신 뒤, 스테판 아르카지치는 약속된 시각보다 아주 조금 늦게 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인의 집으로 들어섰다."]], [["“Who else is with the countess?—a Frenchman?”", "\"백작 부인과 함께 또 누가 있나—프랑스인인가?\""], ["Stepan Arkadyevitch asked the hall-porter, as he glanced at the familiar overcoat of Alexey Alexandrovitch and a queer, rather artless-looking overcoat with clasps.", "스테판 아르카지치는 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 낯익은 외투와, 어딘지 기묘하고 꽤나 소박해 보이는 죔쇠가 달린 외투를 흘끗 바라보며 현관지기에게 물었다."]], [["“Alexey Alexandrovitch Karenin and Count Bezzubov,”", "\"알렉세이 알렉산드로비치 카레닌 님과 베주보프 백작 님이십니다.\""], ["the porter answered severely.", "현관지기가 근엄하게 대답했다."]], [["“Princess Myakaya guessed right,”", "'먀흐카야 공작 부인이 제대로 알아맞혔군.'"], ["thought Stepan Arkadyevitch, as he went upstairs.", "스테판 아르카지치는 계단을 오르며 생각했다."], ["“Curious!", "'신기한걸!"], ["It would be quite as well, though, to get on friendly terms with her.", "그래도 저 부인과 좋게 지내 두면 나쁘지 않겠어."], ["She has immense influence.", "영향력이 어마어마하니까."], ["If she would say a word to Pomorsky, the thing would be a certainty.”", "저 부인이 포모르스키에게 한마디만 해 준다면 그 일은 확실해질 거야.'"]], [["It was still quite light out-of-doors, but in Countess Lidia Ivanovna’s little drawing-room the blinds were drawn and the lamps lighted.", "바깥은 아직 환했지만, 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인의 작은 응접실에는 블라인드가 내려져 있었고 등불이 켜져 있었다."], ["At a round table under a lamp sat the countess and Alexey Alexandrovitch, talking softly.", "등불 아래 둥근 탁자에 앉아 백작 부인과 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 나직이 이야기를 나누고 있었다."], ["A short, thinnish man, very pale and handsome, with feminine hips and knock-kneed legs, with fine brilliant eyes and long hair lying on the collar of his coat, was standing at the end of the room gazing at the portraits on the wall.", "키가 작고 다소 마른, 몹시 창백하고 잘생긴 한 남자가, 여자 같은 엉덩이에 안짱다리를 하고, 가늘게 빛나는 눈에 외투 깃 위로 긴 머리카락을 늘어뜨린 채 방 한쪽 끝에 서서 벽에 걸린 초상화들을 바라보고 있었다."], ["After greeting the lady of the house and Alexey Alexandrovitch, Stepan Arkadyevitch could not resist glancing once more at the unknown man.", "안주인과 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에게 인사를 건넨 뒤에도, 스테판 아르카지치는 그 낯선 남자를 다시 한 번 흘끗 바라보지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["“Monsieur Landau!”", "\"무슈 랑도!\""], ["the countess addressed him with a softness and caution that impressed Oblonsky.", "백작 부인이 오블론스키에게 깊은 인상을 줄 만큼 부드럽고 조심스럽게 그를 불렀다."], ["And she introduced them.", "그러고는 두 사람을 서로에게 소개했다."]], [["Landau looked round hurriedly, came up, and smiling, laid his moist, lifeless hand in Stepan Arkadyevitch’s outstretched hand and immediately walked away and fell to gazing at the portraits again.", "랑도는 황급히 돌아보더니 다가와, 미소를 지으며 축축하고 생기 없는 손을 스테판 아르카지치가 내민 손에 얹었다가는 곧바로 물러나 다시 초상화들을 바라보기 시작했다."], ["The countess and Alexey Alexandrovitch looked at each other significantly.", "백작 부인과 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 의미심장하게 서로를 마주 보았다."]], [["“I am very glad to see you, particularly today,”", "\"이렇게 와 주셔서 정말 기뻐요, 더구나 오늘 같은 날에.\""], ["said Countess Lidia Ivanovna, pointing Stepan Arkadyevitch to a seat beside Karenin.", "리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인은 스테판 아르카지치에게 카레닌 옆자리를 가리키며 말했다."]], [["“I introduced you to him as Landau,”", "\"제가 당신께 저분을 랑도라고 소개했지만,\""], ["she said in a soft voice, glancing at the Frenchman and again immediately after at Alexey Alexandrovitch, “but he is really Count Bezzubov, as you’re probably aware.", "그녀는 프랑스인을 흘끗 보았다가 곧바로 다시 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치를 보며 부드러운 목소리로 말했다. \"사실은 베주보프 백작이세요, 아마 알고 계실 테지만."], ["Only he does not like the title.”", "다만 저분이 그 칭호를 좋아하지 않으세요.\""]], [["“Yes, I heard so,”", "\"네, 그렇게 들었습니다.\""], ["answered Stepan Arkadyevitch; “they say he completely cured Countess Bezzubova.”", "스테판 아르카지치가 대답했다. \"저분이 베주보바 백작 부인을 완전히 낫게 했다고들 하더군요.\""]], [["“She was here today, poor thing!”", "\"오늘 그분이 여기 오셨었어요, 가엾기도 하지!\""], ["the countess said, turning to Alexey Alexandrovitch.", "백작 부인이 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치를 돌아보며 말했다."], ["“This separation is awful for her.", "\"이 이별이 그분에게는 끔찍한 일이에요."], ["It’s such a blow to her!”", "그분에게는 정말 큰 타격이지요!\""]], [["“And he positively is going?”", "\"그래서 저분은 정말로 떠나는 겁니까?\""], ["queried Alexey Alexandrovitch.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 물었다."]], [["“Yes, he’s going to Paris.", "\"네, 파리로 가신답니다."], ["He heard a voice yesterday,”", "어제 어떤 목소리를 들으셨대요.\""], ["said Countess Lidia Ivanovna, looking at Stepan Arkadyevitch.", "리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인이 스테판 아르카지치를 바라보며 말했다."]], [["“Ah, a voice!”", "\"아, 목소리를요!\""], ["repeated Oblonsky, feeling that he must be as circumspect as he possibly could in this society, where something peculiar was going on, or was to go on, to which he had not the key.", "오블론스키는, 자기가 열쇠를 쥐고 있지 못한 무언가 기묘한 일이 벌어지고 있거나 벌어질 참인 이 사교 모임에서 될 수 있는 한 신중을 기해야겠다고 느끼며 따라 말했다."]], [["A moment’s silence followed, after which Countess Lidia Ivanovna, as though approaching the main topic of conversation, said with a fine smile to Oblonsky:", "잠시 침묵이 흘렀고, 그런 뒤 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인은 대화의 본론으로 다가가려는 듯, 우아한 미소를 띠며 오블론스키에게 말했다."]], [["“I’ve known you for a long while, and am very glad to make a closer acquaintance with you.", "\"오래전부터 당신을 알고 있었고, 이렇게 더 가까이 알게 되어 무척 기뻐요."], ["Les amis de nos amis sont nos amis.", "Les amis de nos amis sont nos amis."], ["But to be a true friend, one must enter into the spiritual state of one’s friend, and I fear that you are not doing so in the case of Alexey Alexandrovitch.", "하지만 진정한 친구가 되려면 친구의 영적인 상태 속으로 들어가야 하는 법인데, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치에 관해서는 당신이 그렇게 하고 있지 못한 것 같아 걱정이에요. 제 말이 무슨 뜻인지 아시겠어요?\""], ["You understand what I mean?”", "그녀는 그 우아하고 사색에 잠긴 눈을 들어 올리며 말했다."], ["she said, lifting her fine pensive eyes.", "* Les amis de nos amis sont nos amis: 프랑스어로 '우리 친구의 친구는 우리의 친구다'."]], [["“In part, countess, I understand the position of Alexey Alexandrovitch....”", "\"부분적으로는요, 백작 부인, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 처지를 이해합니다…….\""], ["said Oblonsky.", "오블론스키가 말했다."], ["Having no clear idea what they were talking about, he wanted to confine himself to generalities.", "두 사람이 무슨 이야기를 하는지 명확히 알지 못했던 그는 두루뭉술한 말로 그치고 싶었다."]], [["“The change is not in his external position,”", "\"달라진 것은 그분의 외적인 처지가 아니에요.\""], ["Countess Lidia Ivanovna said sternly, following with eyes of love the figure of Alexey Alexandrovitch as he got up and crossed over to Landau; “his heart is changed, a new heart has been vouchsafed him, and I fear you don’t fully apprehend the change that has taken place in him.”", "리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인은, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 일어나 랑도에게로 건너가는 모습을 사랑이 담긴 눈으로 좇으며 엄숙하게 말했다. \"그분의 마음이 달라졌어요, 새 마음을 받으셨지요, 그래서 저는 당신이 그분에게 일어난 그 변화를 온전히 헤아리지 못하고 있는 게 아닌가 걱정이에요.\""]], [["“Oh, well, in general outlines I can conceive the change.", "\"오, 글쎄요, 대략적인 윤곽은 그 변화를 짐작할 수 있습니다."], ["We have always been friendly, and now....”", "저희는 늘 친하게 지내 왔고, 이제는…….\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, responding with a sympathetic glance to the expression of the countess, and mentally balancing the question with which of the two ministers she was most intimate, so as to know about which to ask her to speak for him.", "스테판 아르카지치는 백작 부인의 표정에 공감 어린 눈빛으로 화답하면서도, 속으로는 그녀가 두 장관 가운데 어느 쪽과 더 가까운지, 그래서 누구를 위해 자기 말을 넣어 달라고 부탁해야 할지를 가늠하며 말했다."]], [["“The change that has taken place in him cannot lessen his love for his neighbors; on the contrary, that change can only intensify love in his heart.", "\"그분에게 일어난 변화는 이웃에 대한 사랑을 줄어들게 할 수 없어요. 오히려 그 변화는 그분 마음속의 사랑을 더 강하게 할 뿐이지요."], ["But I am afraid you do not understand me.", "하지만 당신이 제 말을 이해하지 못하시는 것 같아 걱정이에요."], ["Won’t you have some tea?”", "차라도 좀 드시겠어요?\""], ["she said, with her eyes indicating the footman, who was handing round tea on a tray.", "그녀는 쟁반에 차를 받쳐 들고 돌아다니는 하인을 눈짓으로 가리키며 말했다."]], [["“Not quite, countess.", "\"잘 모르겠습니다, 백작 부인."], ["Of course, his misfortune....”", "물론 그분의 불행은…….\""]], [["“Yes, a misfortune which has proved the highest happiness, when his heart was made new, was filled full of it,”", "\"네, 불행이었지요, 하지만 그분의 마음이 새로워지고 그것으로 가득 찼을 때, 그것은 더없이 높은 행복임이 드러난 불행이었어요.\""], ["she said, gazing with eyes full of love at Stepan Arkadyevitch.", "그녀는 사랑이 가득 담긴 눈으로 스테판 아르카지치를 바라보며 말했다."]], [["“I do believe I might ask her to speak to both of them,”", "'두 사람 모두에게 말을 넣어 달라고 부탁해도 되겠는걸.'"], ["thought Stepan Arkadyevitch.", "스테판 아르카지치는 생각했다."]], [["“Oh, of course, countess,”", "\"오, 물론입니다, 백작 부인.\""], ["he said; “but I imagine such changes are a matter so private that no one, even the most intimate friend, would care to speak of them.”", "그가 말했다. \"다만 그런 변화는 너무나 사적인 일이라 누구도, 아무리 가까운 친구라도, 입에 올리고 싶어 하지 않을 것 같습니다만.\""]], [["“On the contrary!", "\"천만에요!"], ["We ought to speak freely and help one another.”", "우리는 거리낌 없이 이야기하며 서로 도와야 해요.\""]], [["“Yes, undoubtedly so, but there is such a difference of convictions, and besides....”", "\"네, 그야 분명 그렇지요, 하지만 신념의 차이라는 게 워낙 크고, 게다가…….\""], ["said Oblonsky with a soft smile.", "오블론스키가 부드러운 미소를 띠며 말했다."]], [["“There can be no difference where it is a question of holy truth.”", "\"거룩한 진리가 문제일 때는 차이라는 게 있을 수 없어요.\""]], [["“Oh, no, of course; but....”", "\"오, 그럼요, 물론이지요."], ["and Stepan Arkadyevitch paused in confusion.", "하지만…….\" 스테판 아르카지치는 당황하여 말을 멈추었다."], ["He understood at last that they were talking of religion.", "그제야 그는 두 사람이 종교 이야기를 하고 있다는 것을 깨달았다."]], [["“I fancy he will fall asleep immediately,”", "\"저분이 곧 잠들 것 같습니다.\""], ["said Alexey Alexandrovitch in a whisper full of meaning, going up to Lidia Ivanovna.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 리디아 이바노브나에게 다가가며 의미심장하게 속삭였다."]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch looked round.", "스테판 아르카지치는 돌아보았다."], ["Landau was sitting at the window, leaning on his elbow and the back of his chair, his head drooping.", "랑도는 창가에 앉아 팔꿈치와 의자 등받이에 몸을 기댄 채 고개를 떨구고 있었다."], ["Noticing that all eyes were turned on him he raised his head and smiled a smile of childlike artlessness.", "모든 시선이 자기에게 쏠린 것을 알아채자 그는 고개를 들고 어린아이처럼 천진한 미소를 지었다."]], [["“Don’t take any notice,”", "\"신경 쓰지 마세요.\""], ["said Lidia Ivanovna, and she lightly moved a chair up for Alexey Alexandrovitch.", "리디아 이바노브나가 말하며 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치를 위해 의자를 살짝 가져다 놓았다."], ["“I have observed....”", "\"제가 보기에는…….\""], ["she was beginning, when a footman came into the room with a letter.", "그녀가 막 입을 떼는데, 하인이 편지를 들고 방으로 들어왔다."], ["Lidia Ivanovna rapidly ran her eyes over the note, and excusing herself, wrote an answer with extraordinary rapidity, handed it to the man, and came back to the table.", "리디아 이바노브나는 쪽지를 재빨리 훑어보고는 양해를 구한 뒤, 놀라울 만큼 빠르게 답장을 써서 하인에게 건네고 다시 탁자로 돌아왔다."], ["“I have observed,”", "\"제가 보기에는,\""], ["she went on, “that Moscow people, especially the men, are more indifferent to religion than anyone.”", "그녀가 말을 이었다. \"모스크바 사람들은, 특히 남자들은 누구보다도 종교에 무관심한 것 같아요.\""]], [["“Oh, no, countess, I thought Moscow people had the reputation of being the firmest in the faith,”", "\"오, 그렇지 않습니다, 백작 부인. 저는 모스크바 사람들이 신앙심이 가장 굳건하다는 평판을 얻고 있다고 생각했는데요.\""], ["answered Stepan Arkadyevitch.", "스테판 아르카지치가 대답했다."]], [["“But as far as I can make out, you are unfortunately one of the indifferent ones,”", "\"하지만 제가 알아본 바로는, 당신은 안타깝게도 그 무관심한 부류 가운데 한 사람이군요.\""], ["said Alexey Alexandrovitch, turning to him with a weary smile.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 지친 미소를 띠며 그를 향해 말했다."]], [["“How anyone can be indifferent!”", "\"어떻게 사람이 무관심할 수가 있어요!\""], ["said Lidia Ivanovna.", "리디아 이바노브나가 말했다."]], [["“I am not so much indifferent on that subject as I am waiting in suspense,”", "\"그 문제에 관해 제가 무관심하다기보다는, 마음을 정하지 못한 채 기다리고 있는 거지요.\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, with his most deprecating smile.", "스테판 아르카지치가 더없이 미안한 듯한 미소를 띠며 말했다."], ["“I hardly think that the time for such questions has come yet for me.”", "\"저로서는 아직 그런 문제를 따질 때가 오지 않은 것 같습니다.\""]], [["Alexey Alexandrovitch and Lidia Ivanovna looked at each other.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치와 리디아 이바노브나가 서로를 마주 보았다."]], [["“We can never tell whether the time has come for us or not,”", "\"우리에게 그때가 왔는지 아닌지는 결코 알 수 없는 법입니다.\""], ["said Alexey Alexandrovitch severely.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 엄숙하게 말했다."], ["“We ought not to think whether we are ready or not ready.", "\"우리가 준비됐는지 안 됐는지를 따져서는 안 됩니다."], ["God’s grace is not guided by human considerations: sometimes it comes not to those that strive for it, and comes to those that are unprepared, like Saul.”", "하느님의 은총은 인간의 헤아림에 따라 움직이지 않으니까요. 때로 그것은 그것을 얻으려 애쓰는 자에게는 오지 않고, 사울처럼 준비되지 않은 자에게 오기도 합니다.\""]], [["“No, I believe it won’t be just yet,”", "\"아니에요, 제 생각엔 그게 아직은 아닐 것 같아요.\""], ["said Lidia Ivanovna, who had been meanwhile watching the movements of the Frenchman.", "그동안 프랑스인의 움직임을 지켜보고 있던 리디아 이바노브나가 말했다."], ["Landau got up and came to them.", "랑도가 일어나 그들에게 다가왔다."]], [["“Do you allow me to listen?”", "\"제가 들어도 될까요?\""], ["he asked.", "그가 물었다."]], [["“Oh, yes; I did not want to disturb you,”", "\"오, 그럼요. 방해하고 싶지 않았을 뿐이에요.\""], ["said Lidia Ivanovna, gazing tenderly at him; “sit here with us.”", "리디아 이바노브나가 다정하게 그를 바라보며 말했다. \"여기 우리와 함께 앉으세요.\""]], [["“One has only not to close one’s eyes to shut out the light,”", "\"빛을 가리려면 눈을 감기만 하면 그뿐이지요.\""], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch went on.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 말을 이었다."]], [["“Ah, if you knew the happiness we know, feeling His presence ever in our hearts!”", "\"아, 우리가 아는 그 행복을, 우리 마음속에 늘 그분의 임재를 느끼는 그 행복을 당신이 안다면!\""], ["said Countess Lidia Ivanovna with a rapturous smile.", "리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인이 황홀한 미소를 띠며 말했다."]], [["“But a man may feel himself unworthy sometimes to rise to that height,”", "\"하지만 사람은 때로 그런 높은 경지에 오르기에 자기가 미천하다고 느낄 수도 있지 않습니까.\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, conscious of hypocrisy in admitting this religious height, but at the same time unable to bring himself to acknowledge his free-thinking views before a person who, by a single word to Pomorsky, might procure him the coveted appointment.", "스테판 아르카지치는 이런 종교적 경지를 인정하는 데 위선이 있음을 의식하면서도, 동시에 포모르스키에게 한마디만 하면 자기가 그토록 탐내는 자리를 마련해 줄 수 있는 사람 앞에서 차마 자기의 자유사상을 털어놓을 수 없어 이렇게 말했다."]], [["“That is, you mean that sin keeps him back?”", "\"그러니까, 죄가 그분을 가로막는다는 말씀인가요?\""], ["said Lidia Ivanovna.", "리디아 이바노브나가 말했다."], ["“But that is a false idea.", "\"하지만 그것은 잘못된 생각이에요."], ["There is no sin for believers, their sin has been atoned for. Pardon,”", "믿는 자에게는 죄가 없어요, 그들의 죄는 이미 속죄되었으니까요."], ["she added, looking at the footman, who came in again with another letter.", "Pardon.\" 그녀는 또다시 다른 편지를 들고 들어온 하인을 바라보며 덧붙였다."], ["She read it and gave a verbal answer: “Tomorrow at the Grand Duchess’s, say.”", "그녀는 그것을 읽고 구두로 답했다. \"내일 대공비 전하 댁에서, 라고 전해요.\""], ["“For the believer sin is not,”", "\"믿는 자에게 죄란 없는 거예요.\""], ["she went on.", "그녀가 말을 이었다."]], [["“Yes, but faith without works is dead,”", "* Pardon: 프랑스어로 '실례합니다'."], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, recalling the phrase from the catechism, and only by his smile clinging to his independence.", "\"네, 하지만 행위가 없는 믿음은 죽은 것이지요.\" 스테판 아르카지치가 교리문답에서 본 구절을 떠올리며, 오직 그 미소로써만 자기 독자성에 매달리며 말했다."]], [["“There you have it—from the epistle of St.", "\"바로 그거예요—야고보서의 한 구절이지요.\""], ["James,” said Alexey Alexandrovitch, addressing Lidia Ivanovna, with a certain reproachfulness in his tone.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 어딘지 책망하는 어조로 리디아 이바노브나를 향해 말했다."], ["It was unmistakably a subject they had discussed more than once before.", "그것은 두 사람이 전에도 한두 번 이야기를 나눈 적이 있는 화제임이 분명했다."], ["“What harm has been done by the false interpretation of that passage!", "\"그 구절을 잘못 해석한 탓에 얼마나 많은 해악이 빚어졌는지!"], ["Nothing holds men back from belief like that misinterpretation.", "그 그릇된 해석만큼 사람들을 믿음에서 가로막는 것은 없습니다."], ["‘I have not works, so I cannot believe,’ though all the while that is not said.", "'나에게는 행위가 없으니 믿을 수가 없다'는 식이지요, 정작 거기엔 그런 말이 적혀 있지도 않은데 말입니다."], ["But the very opposite is said.”", "오히려 정반대의 말이 적혀 있어요.\""]], [["“Striving for God, saving the soul by fasting,”", "\"하느님을 향해 애쓰고, 단식으로 영혼을 구하려 한다는 것,\""], ["said Countess Lidia Ivanovna, with disgusted contempt, “those are the crude ideas of our monks....", "리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인이 진저리 나는 듯한 경멸을 담아 말했다. \"그건 우리 수도사들의 투박한 생각이에요……"], ["Yet that is nowhere said.", "하지만 어디에도 그런 말은 없어요."], ["It is far simpler and easier,”", "훨씬 더 단순하고 쉬운 일이지요.\""], ["she added, looking at Oblonsky with the same encouraging smile with which at court she encouraged youthful maids of honor, disconcerted by the new surroundings of the court.", "그녀는 궁정에서 새로운 분위기에 어쩔 줄 몰라 하는 젊은 시녀들을 격려할 때 짓던 바로 그 격려의 미소를 띠며 오블론스키를 바라보고 덧붙였다."]], [["“We are saved by Christ who suffered for us.", "\"우리는 우리를 위해 고난받으신 그리스도로 말미암아 구원받습니다."], ["We are saved by faith,”", "우리는 믿음으로 구원받지요.\""], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch chimed in, with a glance of approval at her words.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 그녀의 말에 동의하는 눈빛을 보내며 거들었다."]], [["“Vous comprenez l’anglais?”", "\"Vous comprenez l'anglais?\""], ["asked Lidia Ivanovna, and receiving a reply in the affirmative, she got up and began looking through a shelf of books.", "리디아 이바노브나가 물었고, 그렇다는 대답을 듣자 자리에서 일어나 책꽂이를 살펴보기 시작했다."]], [["“I want to read him ‘Safe and Happy,’ or ‘Under the Wing,’”", "* Vous comprenez l'anglais?: 프랑스어로 '영어를 이해하세요?'."], ["she said, looking inquiringly at Karenin.", "\"이분께 「안전과 행복」이나 「날개 아래서」를 읽어 드리고 싶어요.\""], ["And finding the book, and sitting down again in her place, she opened it.", "그녀는 카레닌을 미심쩍게 바라보며 말했다. 그러고는 책을 찾아 다시 제자리에 앉아 그것을 펼쳤다."], ["“It’s very short.", "\"아주 짧아요."], ["In it is described the way by which faith can be reached, and the happiness, above all earthly bliss, with which it fills the soul.", "여기엔 믿음에 이르는 길과, 모든 지상의 기쁨을 넘어서서 영혼을 가득 채우는 그 행복이 그려져 있어요."], ["The believer cannot be unhappy because he is not alone.", "믿는 자는 혼자가 아니므로 불행할 수 없는 법이지요."], ["But you will see.”", "직접 보시게 될 거예요.\""], ["She was just settling herself to read when the footman came in again.", "그녀가 막 자세를 고쳐 앉아 읽으려는데 하인이 또 들어왔다."], ["“Madame Borozdina?", "\"보로즈디나 부인?"], ["Tell her, tomorrow at two o’clock.", "내일 두 시에 오시라고 전해요."], ["Yes,”", "그래요.\""], ["she said, putting her finger in the place in the book, and gazing before her with her fine pensive eyes, “that is how true faith acts.", "그녀는 책의 그 자리에 손가락을 끼운 채 우아하고 사색에 잠긴 눈으로 앞을 바라보며 말했다. \"참된 믿음이란 그렇게 작용하는 거예요."], ["You know Marie Sanina?", "마리 사니나를 아세요?"], ["You know about her trouble?", "그분의 불행에 대해 들으셨나요?"], ["She lost her only child.", "그분은 외동아이를 잃었어요."], ["She was in despair.", "절망에 빠져 있었지요."], ["And what happened?", "그런데 어떻게 됐을까요?"], ["She found this comforter, and she thanks God now for the death of her child.", "그분은 이 위안자를 찾았고, 지금은 자식의 죽음에 대해 하느님께 감사드린답니다."], ["Such is the happiness faith brings!”", "믿음이 가져다주는 행복이란 바로 그런 거예요!\""]], [["“Oh, yes, that is most....”", "\"오, 그렇군요, 그건 정말이지…….\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, glad they were going to read, and let him have a chance to collect his faculties.", "스테판 아르카지치는 이제 책을 읽게 되어 정신을 추스를 기회가 생긴 것을 다행으로 여기며 말했다."], ["“No, I see I’d better not ask her about anything today,”", "'안 되겠어, 오늘은 부인에게 아무것도 부탁하지 않는 게 좋겠어.'"], ["he thought.", "그는 생각했다."], ["“If only I can get out of this without putting my foot in it!”", "'괜한 실수만 하지 않고 이 자리를 빠져나갈 수 있기만 하면!'"]], [["“It will be dull for you,”", "\"당신께는 따분할 거예요.\" 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인이 랑도를 향해 말했다."], ["said Countess Lidia Ivanovna, addressing Landau; “you don’t know English, but it’s short.”", "\"영어를 모르시니까요. 하지만 짧답니다.\""]], [["“Oh, I shall understand,”", "\"오, 저는 이해할 겁니다.\""], ["said Landau, with the same smile, and he closed his eyes.", "랑도는 여전한 그 미소를 띠며 말하고는 눈을 감았다."], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch and Lidia Ivanovna exchanged meaningful glances, and the reading began.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치와 리디아 이바노브나가 의미심장한 눈빛을 주고받았고, 낭독이 시작되었다."]], [["# Chapter 22", "# Chapter 22"]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch felt completely nonplussed by the strange talk which he was hearing for the first time.", "스테판 아르카지치는 난생처음 듣는 이 기묘한 이야기 앞에서 완전히 어쩔 줄을 몰랐다."], ["The complexity of Petersburg, as a rule, had a stimulating effect on him, rousing him out of his Moscow stagnation.", "페테르부르크의 그 복잡함은 대개 그를 자극하여 모스크바에서의 침체로부터 그를 일깨워 주곤 했다."], ["But he liked these complications, and understood them only in the circles he knew and was at home in.", "하지만 그가 그런 복잡함을 좋아하고 또 이해할 수 있는 것은 자기가 잘 아는, 내 집처럼 편안한 사교 모임에서뿐이었다."], ["In these unfamiliar surroundings he was puzzled and disconcerted, and could not get his bearings.", "이 낯선 환경 속에서 그는 어리둥절하고 당황스러워 갈피를 잡지 못했다."], ["As he listened to Countess Lidia Ivanovna, aware of the beautiful, artless—or perhaps artful, he could not decide which—eyes of Landau fixed upon him, Stepan Arkadyevitch began to be conscious of a peculiar heaviness in his head.", "리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인의 말에 귀를 기울이면서, 그리고 자기에게 고정된 란다우의 아름답고 천진한—아니, 어쩌면 교활한, 어느 쪽인지 단정할 수 없는—눈을 의식하면서, 스테판 아르카지치는 머릿속에서 묘한 무거움을 느끼기 시작했다."]], [["The most incongruous ideas were in confusion in his head.", "머릿속에서는 도무지 어울리지 않는 생각들이 뒤죽박죽이었다."], ["“Marie Sanina is glad her child’s dead....", "'마리 사니나는 제 아이가 죽어서 기뻐한다는군……"], ["How good a smoke would be now!...", "지금 담배 한 대 피우면 얼마나 좋을까!……"], ["To be saved, one need only believe, and the monks don’t know how the thing’s to be done, but Countess Lidia Ivanovna does know....", "구원을 받으려면 그저 믿기만 하면 되는데, 수도사들은 그게 어떻게 이뤄지는지도 모르면서, 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인은 안다는 거지……"], ["And why is my head so heavy?", "그런데 왜 내 머리는 이렇게 무거울까?"], ["Is it the cognac, or all this being so queer?", "코냑 탓인가, 아니면 이 모든 게 너무 기묘해서 그런 건가?"], ["Anyway, I fancy I’ve done nothing unsuitable so far.", "어쨌든 지금까지는 부적절한 짓은 한 게 없는 것 같은데."], ["But anyway, it won’t do to ask her now.", "하지만 어쨌든 지금 그 여자에게 부탁을 꺼낼 수는 없겠어."], ["They say they make one say one’s prayers.", "사람들 말로는 여기선 기도를 시킨다던데."], ["I only hope they won’t make me!", "제발 나한테는 시키지 말아야 할 텐데!"], ["That’ll be too imbecile.", "그건 너무 멍청한 짓이 될 거야."], ["And what stuff it is she’s reading!", "그리고 저 여자가 읽고 있는 건 또 무슨 헛소리람!"], ["but she has a good accent.", "하지만 발음 하나는 좋군."], ["Landau—Bezzubov—what’s he Bezzubov for?”", "란다우—베주보프—이 사람이 왜 베주보프인 거지?'"], ["All at once Stepan Arkadyevitch became aware that his lower jaw was uncontrollably forming a yawn.", "그 순간 스테판 아르카지치는 자기 아래턱이 도무지 어쩔 수 없이 하품을 만들어 내고 있음을 알아차렸다."], ["He pulled his whiskers to cover the yawn, and shook himself together.", "그는 하품을 가리려 구레나룻을 잡아당기며 몸을 추슬렀다."], ["But soon after he became aware that he was dropping asleep and on the very point of snoring.", "하지만 곧이어 자기가 잠에 빠져들고 있으며 막 코를 골 지경에 이르렀음을 깨달았다."], ["He recovered himself at the very moment when the voice of Countess Lidia Ivanovna was saying “he’s asleep.”", "그는 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인의 목소리가 \"잠드셨네요\" 하고 말하던 바로 그 순간에 정신을 차렸다."], ["Stepan Arkadyevitch started with dismay, feeling guilty and caught.", "스테판 아르카지치는 죄를 짓다 들킨 듯한 기분으로 깜짝 놀라며 움찔했다."], ["But he was reassured at once by seeing that the words “he’s asleep” referred not to him, but to Landau.", "하지만 그 \"잠드셨네요\"라는 말이 자기가 아니라 란다우를 가리킨 것임을 알고는 곧 안심했다."], ["The Frenchman was asleep as well as Stepan Arkadyevitch.", "그 프랑스인이 스테판 아르카지치와 마찬가지로 잠들어 있었던 것이다."], ["But Stepan Arkadyevitch’s being asleep would have offended them, as he thought (though even this, he thought, might not be so, as everything seemed so queer), while Landau’s being asleep delighted them extremely, especially Countess Lidia Ivanovna.", "그러나 스테판 아르카지치가 잠든 것은 그들의 기분을 상하게 했을 테지만(하긴 이것도 그렇지 않을지 모른다고, 모든 게 너무도 기묘해 보였으므로 그는 생각했다), 란다우가 잠든 것은 그들을, 특히 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인을 더없이 기쁘게 했다."]], [["“Mon ami,”", "\"Mon ami,\""], ["said Lidia Ivanovna, carefully holding the folds of her silk gown so as not to rustle, and in her excitement calling Karenin not Alexey Alexandrovitch, but “mon ami,”", "리디아 이바노브나가 비단 가운 자락이 바스락거리지 않도록 조심스레 붙잡고, 흥분한 나머지 카레닌을 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 아니라 \"mon ami\"라고 부르며 말했다. \"donnez-lui la main."], ["“donnez-lui la main.", "Vous voyez? 쉿!\""], ["Vous voyez?", "그녀는 다시 들어오는 하인을 향해 쉿 소리를 냈다."], ["Sh!”", "\"안 계신다고 해.\""], ["she hissed at the footman as he came in again.", "* Mon ami: 프랑스어로 '내 친구'. donnez-lui la main."], ["“Not at home.”", "Vous voyez?: '그에게 손을 건네세요. 보이시죠?'"]], [["The Frenchman was asleep, or pretending to be asleep, with his head on the back of his chair, and his moist hand, as it lay on his knee, made faint movements, as though trying to catch something.", "그 프랑스인은 머리를 의자 등받이에 기댄 채 잠들어 있거나 잠든 척하고 있었고, 무릎 위에 놓인 그의 축축한 손은 마치 무언가를 잡으려는 듯 희미하게 움직였다. 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 일어나 조심스레 움직이려 했으나 탁자에 부딪치고는, 다가가 자기 손을 프랑스인의 손에 얹었다."], ["Alexey Alexandrovitch got up, tried to move carefully, but stumbled against the table, went up and laid his hand in the Frenchman’s hand.", "스테판 아르카지치도 일어나, 자기가 잠들어 있는 것이라면 깨어 보려는 듯 두 눈을 크게 뜨고 먼저 한 사람을, 그다음에 다른 사람을 바라보았다. 모든 게 실제였다."], ["Stepan Arkadyevitch got up too, and opening his eyes wide, trying to wake himself up if he were asleep, he looked first at one and then at the other.", "스테판 아르카지치는 자기 머리가 점점 더 나빠지고 있음을 느꼈다. \"Que la personne qui est arrivée la dernière, celle qui demande, qu’elle sorte!"], ["It was all real.", "Qu’elle sorte!\""], ["Stepan Arkadyevitch felt that his head was getting worse and worse.", "프랑스인이 눈도 뜨지 않은 채 또렷이 말했다."]], [["“Que la personne qui est arrivée la dernière, celle qui demande, qu’elle sorte!", "* '가장 늦게 도착한 사람, 청을 하려는 그 사람을 나가게 하시오!"], ["Qu’elle sorte!”", "나가게 하시오!'"], ["articulated the Frenchman, without opening his eyes.", "\"Vous m’excuserez, mais vous voyez…… Revenez vers dix heures, encore mieux demain.\""]], [["“Vous m’excuserez, mais vous voyez....", "* '용서하시구려, 하지만 보시다시피……"], ["Revenez vers dix heures, encore mieux demain.”", "열 시쯤 다시 오시오, 아니면 내일이 더 좋겠소.'"]], [["“Qu’elle sorte!”", "\"Qu’elle sorte!\""], ["repeated the Frenchman impatiently.", "프랑스인이 초조하게 되풀이했다."]], [["“C’est moi, n’est-ce pas?”", "* '그 사람을 나가게 하시오!'"], ["And receiving an answer in the affirmative, Stepan Arkadyevitch, forgetting the favor he had meant to ask of Lidia Ivanovna, and forgetting his sister’s affairs, caring for nothing, but filled with the sole desire to get away as soon as possible, went out on tiptoe and ran out into the street as though from a plague-stricken house.", "\"C’est moi, n’est-ce pas?\" 그리고 그렇다는 대답을 듣고서, 스테판 아르카지치는 리디아 이바노브나에게 청하려던 부탁도 잊고, 누이의 일도 잊고, 아무것도 안중에 없이 오로지 한시바삐 이 자리를 벗어나고 싶은 마음만으로 가득 차, 발끝으로 살금살금 빠져나가서는 페스트가 도는 집에서 도망치듯 거리로 뛰쳐나왔다."], ["For a long while he chatted and joked with his cab-driver, trying to recover his spirits.", "그는 한참 동안 마부와 잡담을 나누고 농담을 던지며 기분을 추스르려 애썼다."]], [["At the French theater where he arrived for the last act, and afterwards at the Tatar restaurant after his champagne, Stepan Arkadyevitch felt a little refreshed in the atmosphere he was used to.", "* '저 말이군요, 그렇죠?' 마지막 막에 맞춰 도착한 프랑스 극장에서, 그리고 그다음 타타르 음식점에서 샴페인을 마신 뒤, 스테판 아르카지치는 늘 익숙하던 분위기 속에서 조금쯤 기운을 되찾았다."], ["But still he felt quite unlike himself all that evening.", "하지만 그래도 그날 저녁 내내 그는 영 자기답지 못한 기분이었다."]], [["On getting home to Pyotr Oblonsky’s, where he was staying, Stepan Arkadyevitch found a note from Betsy.", "자기가 묵고 있던 표트르 오블론스키의 집에 돌아온 스테판 아르카지치는 벳시가 보낸 쪽지를 발견했다."], ["She wrote to him that she was very anxious to finish their interrupted conversation, and begged him to come next day.", "그녀는 중단되었던 둘의 대화를 무척 마무리하고 싶다며, 다음 날 와 달라고 부탁하는 편지를 써 두었다."], ["He had scarcely read this note, and frowned at its contents, when he heard below the ponderous tramp of the servants, carrying something heavy.", "그가 이 쪽지를 채 다 읽고 그 내용에 미간을 찌푸리기도 전에, 아래쪽에서 하인들이 무언가 무거운 것을 나르며 무겁게 쿵쿵거리는 발소리가 들려왔다."]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch went out to look.", "스테판 아르카지치는 보려고 나갔다."], ["It was the rejuvenated Pyotr Oblonsky.", "그것은 회춘한 표트르 오블론스키였다."], ["He was so drunk that he could not walk upstairs; but he told them to set him on his legs when he saw Stepan Arkadyevitch, and clinging to him, walked with him into his room and there began telling him how he had spent the evening, and fell asleep doing so.", "그는 너무 취해서 계단을 올라오지도 못할 지경이었지만, 스테판 아르카지치를 보자 사람들에게 자기를 두 발로 세워 달라고 하고는, 그에게 매달려 함께 방으로 들어가, 거기서 저녁을 어떻게 보냈는지 늘어놓기 시작하더니 그러다가 잠들어 버렸다."]], [["Stepan Arkadyevitch was in very low spirits, which happened rarely with him, and for a long while he could not go to sleep.", "스테판 아르카지치는 좀처럼 드문 일이지만 몹시 기분이 가라앉아 있었고, 한참 동안 잠을 이루지 못했다."], ["Everything he could recall to his mind, everything was disgusting; but most disgusting of all, as if it were something shameful, was the memory of the evening he had spent at Countess Lidia Ivanovna’s.", "머릿속에 떠올릴 수 있는 것은 무엇이든 다 역겨웠다. 하지만 그중에서도 가장 역겨운 것은, 마치 무언가 수치스러운 일이라도 되는 양, 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인의 집에서 보낸 그 저녁의 기억이었다."]], [["Next day he received from Alexey Alexandrovitch a final answer, refusing to grant Anna’s divorce, and he understood that this decision was based on what the Frenchman had said in his real or pretended trance.", "다음 날 그는 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치로부터 안나의 이혼을 허락할 수 없다는 최종 답변을 받았고, 그 결정이 그 프랑스인이 진짜인지 가짜인지 모를 무아지경 속에서 한 말에 근거하고 있다는 것을 알아차렸다."]], [["# Chapter 23", "# Chapter 23"]], [["In order to carry through any undertaking in family life, there must necessarily be either complete division between the husband and wife, or loving agreement.", "가정생활에서 어떤 일이든 끝까지 해내려면, 남편과 아내 사이에 완전한 결별이 있거나 아니면 사랑에 찬 화합이 있거나, 둘 중 하나여야 한다."], ["When the relations of a couple are vacillating and neither one thing nor the other, no sort of enterprise can be undertaken.", "부부의 관계가 흔들리고 이것도 저것도 아닐 때는 그 어떤 일도 도모할 수가 없다."]], [["Many families remain for years in the same place, though both husband and wife are sick of it, simply because there is neither complete division nor agreement between them.", "많은 가정이 남편과 아내가 둘 다 진절머리를 내면서도 여러 해 동안 같은 자리에 머무는데, 그것은 그저 그들 사이에 완전한 결별도 화합도 없기 때문이다."]], [["Both Vronsky and Anna felt life in Moscow insupportable in the heat and dust, when the spring sunshine was followed by the glare of summer, and all the trees in the boulevards had long since been in full leaf, and the leaves were covered with dust.", "브론스키도 안나도 모스크바의 삶이 더위와 먼지 속에서 견딜 수 없다고 느꼈다. 봄 햇살이 지나가고 여름의 작열하는 빛이 뒤따랐으며, 가로수 길의 나무들은 진작부터 잎이 무성했고, 그 잎사귀들은 먼지로 뒤덮여 있었다."], ["But they did not go back to Vozdvizhenskoe, as they had arranged to do long before; they went on staying in Moscow, though they both loathed it, because of late there had been no agreement between them.", "하지만 두 사람은 오래전부터 그러기로 약속했던 보즈드비젠스코예로 돌아가지 않았다. 둘 다 모스크바를 몹시 싫어하면서도 거기 계속 머물렀는데, 요즘 들어 둘 사이에 화합이 없었던 까닭이다."]], [["The irritability that kept them apart had no external cause, and all efforts to come to an understanding intensified it, instead of removing it.", "두 사람을 갈라놓는 그 짜증에는 외적인 원인이 없었고, 화해해 보려는 온갖 노력은 그것을 없애기는커녕 오히려 더 키웠다."], ["It was an inner irritation, grounded in her mind on the conviction that his love had grown less; in his, on regret that he had put himself for her sake in a difficult position, which she, instead of lightening, made still more difficult.", "그것은 내면의 짜증이었으니, 그녀의 마음속에서는 그의 사랑이 식었다는 확신에 뿌리를 두었고, 그의 마음속에서는 그녀를 위해 곤란한 처지에 스스로를 빠뜨렸는데도 그녀가 그것을 덜어 주기는커녕 더 곤란하게 만든다는 후회에 뿌리를 두었다."], ["Neither of them gave full utterance to their sense of grievance, but they considered each other in the wrong, and tried on every pretext to prove this to one another.", "둘 중 누구도 자기 불만을 온전히 입 밖에 내지는 않았지만, 서로를 잘못했다고 여겼고, 무슨 핑계든 잡아 그것을 상대에게 증명하려 들었다."]], [["In her eyes the whole of him, with all his habits, ideas, desires, with all his spiritual and physical temperament, was one thing—love for women, and that love, she felt, ought to be entirely concentrated on her alone.", "그녀의 눈에는 그의 모든 것이, 그의 온갖 습관, 생각, 욕망, 그의 정신적·육체적 기질 전부가 한 가지로 귀결되었다. 여자에 대한 사랑이었다."], ["That love was less; consequently, as she reasoned, he must have transferred part of his love to other women or to another woman—and she was jealous.", "그리고 그 사랑은, 그녀가 느끼기에, 마땅히 자기 한 사람에게만 온전히 집중되어야 하는 것이었다. 그 사랑이 줄어들었다."], ["She was jealous not of any particular woman but of the decrease of his love.", "따라서, 그녀의 추론대로라면, 그는 자기 사랑의 일부를 다른 여자들에게, 혹은 또 다른 한 여자에게 옮긴 것이 분명했다. 그래서 그녀는 질투했다."], ["Not having got an object for her jealousy, she was on the lookout for it.", "그녀는 어떤 특정한 여자를 질투한 것이 아니라 그의 사랑이 줄어든 것을 질투했다."], ["At the slightest hint she transferred her jealousy from one object to another.", "질투의 대상을 아직 찾지 못한 채, 그녀는 그것을 찾아 두리번거렸다."], ["At one time she was jealous of those low women with whom he might so easily renew his old bachelor ties; then she was jealous of the society women he might meet; then she was jealous of the imaginary girl whom he might want to marry, for whose sake he would break with her.", "아주 사소한 낌새만 보여도 그녀는 질투를 이 대상에서 저 대상으로 옮겼다. 어느 때는 그가 너무도 쉽게 옛 독신 시절의 관계를 다시 맺을 수 있을 천한 여자들을 질투했고, 그다음에는 그가 만날지도 모를 사교계 여자들을 질투했고, 또 그다음에는 그가 결혼하고 싶어 할지도 모를, 그녀를 위해 자기를 버릴지도 모를 상상 속의 처녀를 질투했다."], ["And this last form of jealousy tortured her most of all, especially as he had unwarily told her, in a moment of frankness, that his mother knew him so little that she had had the audacity to try and persuade him to marry the young Princess Sorokina.", "그리고 이 마지막 형태의 질투가 그녀를 가장 괴롭혔는데, 특히 그가 솔직해진 어느 순간 경솔하게도, 자기 어머니가 자기를 너무 몰라서 뻔뻔하게도 젊은 소로키나 공작 영애와 결혼하라고 설득하려 들었다고 말해 버린 탓이었다."]], [["And being jealous of him, Anna was indignant against him and found grounds for indignation in everything.", "그리고 그를 질투하는 동안 안나는 그에게 분개했고, 모든 것에서 분개할 거리를 찾아냈다."], ["For everything that was difficult in her position she blamed him.", "자기 처지에서 힘든 일은 무엇이든 그의 탓으로 돌렸다."], ["The agonizing condition of suspense she had passed in Moscow, the tardiness and indecision of Alexey Alexandrovitch, her solitude—she put it all down to him.", "모스크바에서 겪은 그 괴로운 불안의 상태도, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치의 굼뜸과 우유부단함도, 자기의 고독도, 그녀는 모조리 그의 탓으로 여겼다."], ["If he had loved her he would have seen all the bitterness of her position, and would have rescued her from it.", "그가 그녀를 사랑했다면 자기 처지의 온갖 쓰라림을 알아보았을 테고, 그녀를 거기서 구해 냈을 것이다."], ["For her being in Moscow and not in the country, he was to blame too.", "그녀가 시골이 아니라 모스크바에 있는 것도 그의 탓이었다."], ["He could not live buried in the country as she would have liked to do.", "그는 그녀가 원하는 대로 시골에 파묻혀 살 수 없는 사람이었다."], ["He must have society, and he had put her in this awful position, the bitterness of which he would not see.", "그에게는 사교계가 있어야 했고, 그래서 그는 그녀를 이 끔찍한 처지에 빠뜨려 놓고도 그 쓰라림을 보려 하지 않았다."], ["And again, it was his fault that she was forever separated from her son.", "게다가 그녀가 아들과 영영 떨어지게 된 것도 그의 잘못이었다."]], [["Even the rare moments of tenderness that came from time to time did not soothe her; in his tenderness now she saw a shade of complacency, of self-confidence, which had not been of old, and which exasperated her.", "이따금 찾아오는 드문 다정함의 순간조차 그녀를 달래 주지 못했다. 이제 그의 다정함 속에서 그녀는 예전에는 없던 흡족함과 자신만만함의 그늘을 보았고, 그것이 그녀를 격분하게 했다."]], [["It was dusk.", "해 질 녘이었다."], ["Anna was alone, and waiting for him to come back from a bachelor dinner.", "안나는 혼자였고, 그가 독신 남자들의 만찬에서 돌아오기를 기다리고 있었다."], ["She walked up and down in his study (the room where the noise from the street was least heard), and thought over every detail of their yesterday’s quarrel.", "그녀는 그의 서재(거리의 소음이 가장 덜 들리는 방이었다)를 이리저리 거닐며 어제 다툰 일을 낱낱이 곱씹었다."], ["Going back from the well-remembered, offensive words of the quarrel to what had been the ground of it, she arrived at last at its origin.", "다툼에서 똑똑히 기억나는 모진 말들로부터 그 발단이 되었던 것으로 거슬러 올라가, 그녀는 마침내 그 시초에 다다랐다."], ["For a long while she could hardly believe that their dissension had arisen from a conversation so inoffensive, of so little moment to either.", "한참 동안 그녀는 그토록 악의 없고, 두 사람 모두에게 하찮기 그지없던 대화에서 자기들의 불화가 비롯되었다는 것을 좀처럼 믿을 수가 없었다."], ["But so it actually had been.", "하지만 실제로 그러했다."], ["It all arose from his laughing at the girls’ high schools, declaring they were useless, while she defended them.", "모든 것은 그가 여자 고등학교를 비웃으며 그런 것은 쓸데없다고 단언하고, 그녀는 그것을 옹호한 데서 비롯되었다."], ["He had spoken slightingly of women’s education in general, and had said that Hannah, Anna’s English protégée, had not the slightest need to know anything of physics.", "그는 여성 교육 일반을 깔보듯 말했고, 안나의 영국인 양녀 한나가 물리학을 조금이라도 알 필요는 전혀 없다고 했다."]], [["This irritated Anna.", "이 말에 안나는 짜증이 났다."], ["She saw in this a contemptuous reference to her occupations.", "그녀는 그 안에서 자기가 하는 일에 대한 멸시의 암시를 보았다."], ["And she bethought her of a phrase to pay him back for the pain he had given her.", "그래서 그녀는 그가 안긴 아픔을 되갚아 줄 한마디를 떠올렸다."], ["“I don’t expect you to understand me, my feelings, as anyone who loved me might, but simple delicacy I did expect,”", "\"당신이 나를, 내 감정을, 나를 사랑하는 사람이라면 누구나 그러듯 이해해 주기를 바라는 건 아니에요. 하지만 그저 약간의 배려는 기대했어요.\""], ["she said.", "그녀는 말했다."]], [["And he had actually flushed with vexation, and had said something unpleasant.", "그러자 그는 정말로 분에 못 이겨 얼굴을 붉혔고, 무언가 불쾌한 말을 했다."], ["She could not recall her answer, but at that point, with an unmistakable desire to wound her too, he had said:", "그녀는 자기가 무어라 대꾸했는지는 기억나지 않았지만, 바로 그 대목에서 그가 자기 또한 그녀에게 상처를 주고 싶다는 분명한 욕망을 품은 채 이렇게 말했던 것은 떠올랐다."]], [["“I feel no interest in your infatuation over this girl, that’s true, because I see it’s unnatural.”", "\"당신이 그 여자애한테 그렇게 빠져 있는 것에 나는 아무 흥미도 못 느껴, 그건 사실이야. 자연스럽지 못한 일로 보이니까.\""]], [["The cruelty with which he shattered the world she had built up for herself so laboriously to enable her to endure her hard life, the injustice with which he had accused her of affectation, of artificiality, aroused her.", "그녀가 그토록 고된 삶을 견뎌 내려고 그렇게 애써서 쌓아 올린 세계를 그가 산산이 부수어 버린 그 잔인함, 그녀를 꾸밈이라고, 가식이라고 몰아붙인 그 부당함이 그녀를 들끓게 했다."]], [["“I am very sorry that nothing but what’s coarse and material is comprehensible and natural to you,”", "\"당신에게는 거칠고 물질적인 것 말고는 아무것도 이해되지도, 자연스럽지도 않다니 참 안됐네요.\""], ["she said and walked out of the room.", "그녀는 이렇게 말하고 방을 나가 버렸다."]], [["When he had come in to her yesterday evening, they had not referred to the quarrel, but both felt that the quarrel had been smoothed over, but was not at an end.", "어제저녁 그가 그녀에게 들어왔을 때, 두 사람은 다툼을 입에 올리지 않았지만, 둘 다 다툼이 무마되긴 했어도 끝나지는 않았다고 느꼈다."]], [["Today he had not been at home all day, and she felt so lonely and wretched in being on bad terms with him that she wanted to forget it all, to forgive him, and be reconciled with him; she wanted to throw the blame on herself and to justify him.", "오늘 그는 하루 종일 집에 없었고, 그와 사이가 틀어진 채로 지내는 것이 너무도 외롭고 비참하게 느껴진 나머지 그녀는 모든 것을 잊고, 그를 용서하고, 그와 화해하고 싶었다. 그녀는 모든 잘못을 자기에게 돌리고 그를 두둔하고 싶었다."]], [["“I am myself to blame.", "\"잘못한 건 나야."], ["I’m irritable, I’m insanely jealous.", "난 신경질적이고, 미친 듯이 질투하지."], ["I will make it up with him, and we’ll go away to the country; there I shall be more at peace.”", "그이와 화해하고, 우리 시골로 떠나자. 거기서라면 좀 더 마음이 편안해질 거야.\""]], [["“Unnatural!”", "\"자연스럽지 못하다니!\""], ["She suddenly recalled the word that had stung her most of all, not so much the word itself as the intent to wound her with which it was said.", "그녀는 무엇보다 자기를 가장 아프게 찔렀던 그 말을 문득 떠올렸다. 그 말 자체보다도 그것을 내뱉을 때 담겼던, 자기에게 상처를 주려는 의도가 더 아팠다."], ["“I know what he meant; he meant—unnatural, not loving my own daughter, to love another person’s child.", "\"그이가 무슨 뜻으로 한 말인지 알아. 그이 말은—내 친딸도 사랑하지 않으면서 남의 자식을 사랑하는 게 자연스럽지 못하다는 거지."], ["What does he know of love for children, of my love for Seryozha, whom I’ve sacrificed for him?", "그이가 아이들에 대한 사랑을, 그이를 위해 내가 희생한 세료자에 대한 내 사랑을 뭘 알겠어?"], ["But that wish to wound me!", "하지만 나를 상처 주려는 그 마음이라니!"], ["No, he loves another woman, it must be so.”", "아니, 그이는 다른 여자를 사랑하는 거야, 틀림없이 그래.\""]], [["And perceiving that, while trying to regain her peace of mind, she had gone round the same circle that she had been round so often before, and had come back to her former state of exasperation, she was horrified at herself.", "그리고 마음의 평온을 되찾으려 애쓰는 동안 자기가 예전에도 그토록 자주 돌고 돌았던 바로 그 원을 또다시 돌아, 이전의 격분 상태로 되돌아왔음을 깨닫자, 그녀는 스스로에게 소름이 끼쳤다. \"이게 정말 안 되는 일일까?"], ["“Can it be impossible? Can it be beyond me to control myself?”", "내가 나 자신을 다스리는 게 정말 내 힘으로 안 되는 일일까?\""], ["she said to herself, and began again from the beginning.", "그녀는 혼잣말을 하고는 다시 처음부터 시작했다."], ["“He’s truthful, he’s honest, he loves me.", "\"그이는 진실하고, 정직하고, 나를 사랑해."], ["I love him, and in a few days the divorce will come.", "나도 그이를 사랑하고, 며칠 뒤면 이혼이 이루어질 거야."], ["What more do I want?", "더 바랄 게 뭐가 있어?"], ["I want peace of mind and trust, and I will take the blame on myself.", "나는 마음의 평온과 신뢰를 원하니, 잘못은 내가 떠안겠어."], ["Yes, now when he comes in, I will tell him I was wrong, though I was not wrong, and we will go away tomorrow.”", "그래, 이제 그이가 들어오면, 사실은 잘못이 없었지만 내가 잘못했다고 말하고, 우리는 내일 떠나는 거야.\""]], [["And to escape thinking any more, and being overcome by irritability, she rang, and ordered the boxes to be brought up for packing their things for the country.", "그리고 더는 생각하지 않으려고, 또 짜증에 휩싸이지 않으려고, 그녀는 종을 울려 시골로 가져갈 짐을 꾸리게 트렁크를 올려 오라고 일렀다."]], [["At ten o’clock Vronsky came in.", "열 시에 브론스키가 들어왔다."]], [["# Chapter 24", "# Chapter 24"]], [["“Well, was it nice?”", "\"그래, 즐거웠어요?\""], ["she asked, coming out to meet him with a penitent and meek expression.", "그녀는 뉘우치는 듯한 온순한 표정으로 그를 맞으러 나오며 물었다."]], [["“Just as usual,”", "\"여느 때와 같았지.\""], ["he answered, seeing at a glance that she was in one of her good moods.", "그녀가 기분 좋은 상태라는 것을 한눈에 알아채고 그가 대답했다."], ["He was used by now to these transitions, and he was particularly glad to see it today, as he was in a specially good humor himself.", "그는 이제 이런 변화에 익숙해져 있었고, 오늘은 자기 자신도 유난히 기분이 좋았던 터라 그것을 보는 것이 특히 반가웠다."]], [["“What do I see?", "\"이게 다 뭐지?"], ["Come, that’s good!”", "이거 잘됐군!\""], ["he said, pointing to the boxes in the passage.", "그가 복도에 놓인 짐 상자들을 가리키며 말했다."]], [["“Yes, we must go.", "\"네, 우리 떠나야죠."], ["I went out for a drive, and it was so fine I longed to be in the country.", "마차를 타고 나갔다가 날이 어찌나 좋던지 시골에 가고 싶어졌어요."], ["There’s nothing to keep you, is there?”", "당신을 붙잡아 둘 일은 없죠, 그렇죠?\""]], [["“It’s the one thing I desire.", "\"내가 바라는 게 바로 그거야."], ["I’ll be back directly, and we’ll talk it over; I only want to change my coat.", "곧 돌아와서 의논합시다. 옷만 갈아입으면 돼."], ["Order some tea.”", "차나 좀 내오라고 해요.\""]], [["And he went into his room.", "그러고는 자기 방으로 들어갔다."]], [["There was something mortifying in the way he had said “Come, that’s good,”", "그가 \"이거 잘됐군\"이라고 말한 투에는 어딘가 모욕적인 데가 있었다. 마치 아이가 떼쓰기를 그쳤을 때 어른이 건네는 말투 같았다."], ["as one says to a child when it leaves off being naughty, and still more mortifying was the contrast between her penitent and his self-confident tone; and for one instant she felt the lust of strife rising up in her again, but making an effort she conquered it, and met Vronsky as good-humoredly as before.", "그리고 그보다 더 모욕적인 것은 뉘우치는 그녀의 어조와 자신만만한 그의 어조 사이의 대조였다. 한순간 그녀 안에서 다시 다투고 싶은 욕망이 솟구쳤지만, 그녀는 안간힘을 써서 그것을 억눌렀고, 전과 다름없이 기분 좋게 브론스키를 맞이했다."]], [["When he came in she told him, partly repeating phrases she had prepared beforehand, how she had spent the day, and her plans for going away.", "그가 들어오자 그녀는 미리 준비해 둔 말들을 군데군데 되풀이하면서, 하루를 어떻게 보냈는지, 그리고 떠날 계획을 그에게 이야기했다."]], [["“You know it came to me almost like an inspiration,”", "\"있잖아요, 거의 영감처럼 떠올랐어요.\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["she said. “Why wait here for the divorce?", "\"여기서 이혼을 기다릴 게 뭐 있어요?"], ["Won’t it be just the same in the country?", "시골에서나 여기서나 마찬가지 아닌가요?"], ["I can’t wait any longer!", "난 더는 못 기다리겠어요!"], ["I don’t want to go on hoping, I don’t want to hear anything about the divorce.", "더 이상 희망을 품고 싶지도 않고, 이혼에 관해 아무 말도 듣고 싶지 않아요."], ["I have made up my mind it shall not have any more influence on my life.", "그 일이 내 삶에 더는 아무 영향도 끼치지 못하도록 마음을 정했어요."], ["Do you agree?”", "당신도 동의하죠?\""]], [["“Oh, yes!”", "\"오, 그럼!\""], ["he said, glancing uneasily at her excited face.", "그가 흥분한 그녀의 얼굴을 불안하게 흘끔 보며 말했다."]], [["“What did you do?", "\"무얼 했어요?"], ["Who was there?”", "누가 왔던가요?\""], ["she said, after a pause.", "잠시 침묵하다가 그녀가 물었다."]], [["Vronsky mentioned the names of the guests. “The dinner was first rate, and the boat race, and it was all pleasant enough, but in Moscow they can never do anything without something ridicule. A lady of a sort appeared on the scene, teacher of swimming to the Queen of Sweden, and gave us an exhibition of her skill.”", "브론스키는 손님들의 이름을 댔다. \"만찬은 일류였고, 보트 경주도 있었지. 다 그런대로 유쾌했어. 하지만 모스크바에서는 뭔가 ridicule한 것 없이는 무슨 일이든 도무지 해내질 못한다니까. 어떤 부류의 여자가 나타났는데, 스웨덴 여왕에게 수영을 가르치는 교사라더군. 우리한테 자기 솜씨를 한바탕 선보였지.\" * ridicule: 프랑스어로 '우스꽝스러운'."]], [["“How?", "\"어떻게요?"], ["did she swim?”", "헤엄을 쳤단 말이에요?\""], ["asked Anna, frowning.", "안나가 미간을 찌푸리며 물었다."]], [["“In an absurd red costume de natation; she was old and hideous too.", "\"우스꽝스러운 빨간 costume de natation 차림으로 말이야. 게다가 늙고 흉측했지."], ["So when shall we go?”", "그래, 우리는 언제 떠날까?\""]], [["“What an absurd fancy!", "\"참 별난 취향이네요!"], ["Why, did she swim in some special way, then?”", "그래서, 무슨 특별한 방식으로 헤엄을 쳤다는 거예요?\""], ["said Anna, not answering.", "안나는 그의 물음에는 대답하지 않고 말했다."]], [["“There was absolutely nothing in it.", "* costume de natation: 프랑스어로 '수영복'."], ["That’s just what I say, it was awfully stupid.", "\"전혀 별것 없었어. 그러니까 내가 하는 말이, 정말 어처구니없이 시시했다는 거지."], ["Well, then, when do you think of going?”", "자, 그래서 언제 떠날 생각이야?\""]], [["Anna shook her head as though trying to drive away some unpleasant idea.", "안나는 어떤 불쾌한 생각을 떨쳐 내려는 듯 고개를 저었다."]], [["“When?", "\"언제냐고요?"], ["Why, the sooner the better!", "빠르면 빠를수록 좋죠!"], ["By tomorrow we shan’t be ready.", "내일은 준비가 안 될 거예요."], ["The day after tomorrow.”", "모레로 해요.\""]], [["“Yes ...", "\"그래……"], ["oh, no, wait a minute!", "아, 아니, 잠깐만!"], ["The day after tomorrow’s Sunday, I have to be at maman’s,”", "모레가 일요일이로군. 그날은 maman을 뵈러 가야 해.\""], ["said Vronsky, embarrassed, because as soon as he uttered his mother’s name he was aware of her intent, suspicious eyes.", "브론스키가 당황하며 말했다. 어머니의 이름을 입에 올리자마자 자기를 응시하는 그녀의 의심스러운 눈길을 의식했기 때문이다."], ["His embarrassment confirmed her suspicion.", "그의 당황은 그녀의 의심을 굳혀 놓았다."], ["She flushed hotly and drew away from him.", "그녀는 확 달아오르며 그에게서 몸을 뺐다."], ["It was now not the Queen of Sweden’s swimming-mistress who filled Anna’s imagination, but the young Princess Sorokina.", "이제 안나의 상상을 채운 것은 스웨덴 여왕의 수영 교사가 아니라 젊은 소로키나 공작 영애였다. 그녀는 모스크바 근교의 마을에서 브론스카야 백작 부인과 함께 머무르고 있었다."], ["She was staying in a village near Moscow with Countess Vronskaya.", "* maman: 프랑스어로 '엄마'."]], [["“Can’t you go tomorrow?”", "\"내일 못 가나요?\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“Well, no!", "\"글쎄, 안 돼!"], ["The deeds and the money for the business I’m going there for I can’t get by tomorrow,”", "내가 거기 가려는 그 일에 필요한 서류와 돈을 내일까지는 마련할 수가 없어.\""], ["he answered.", "그가 대답했다."]], [["“If so, we won’t go at all.”", "\"그렇다면 아예 가지 말죠.\""]], [["“But why so?”", "\"아니, 왜 그래?\""]], [["“I shall not go later.", "\"난 더 나중에는 안 가요."], ["Monday or never!”", "월요일 아니면 영영 안 가요!\""]], [["“What for?”", "\"왜 그러는데?\""], ["said Vronsky, as though in amazement.", "브론스키가 어이없다는 듯이 말했다."], ["“Why, there’s no meaning in it!”", "\"그건 아무 의미도 없잖아!\""]], [["“There’s no meaning in it to you, because you care nothing for me.", "\"당신한테야 아무 의미가 없겠죠. 당신은 나한테 조금도 관심이 없으니까."], ["You don’t care to understand my life.", "내 삶을 이해할 마음조차 없으니까."], ["The one thing that I cared for here was Hannah.", "여기서 내가 마음 쓴 단 한 가지가 한나였어요."], ["You say it’s affectation.", "당신은 그게 가식이라고 하죠."], ["Why, you said yesterday that I don’t love my daughter, that I love this English girl, that it’s unnatural.", "어제도 내가 내 딸은 사랑하지 않으면서 이 영국 여자애를 사랑한다고, 그건 자연스럽지 못한 일이라고 했잖아요."], ["I should like to know what life there is for me that could be natural!”", "나한테 자연스러울 수 있는 삶이라는 게 도대체 어떤 건지 알고 싶군요!\""]], [["For an instant she had a clear vision of what she was doing, and was horrified at how she had fallen away from her resolution.", "한순간 그녀는 자기가 무슨 짓을 하고 있는지 똑똑히 보았고, 자기가 결심에서 얼마나 멀어졌는지에 소스라쳤다."], ["But even though she knew it was her own ruin, she could not restrain herself, could not keep herself from proving to him that he was wrong, could not give way to him.", "하지만 그것이 자기 자신의 파멸이라는 걸 알면서도, 그녀는 스스로를 억누를 수 없었고, 그가 틀렸다는 것을 그에게 증명하지 않을 수 없었으며, 그에게 굽힐 수 없었다."]], [["“I never said that; I said I did not sympathize with this sudden passion.”", "\"난 그런 말 한 적 없어. 다만 이 갑작스러운 열정에 공감하지 않는다고 말했을 뿐이야.\""]], [["“How is it, though you boast of your straightforwardness, you don’t tell the truth?”", "\"그런데 어쩌면, 그렇게 자신의 솔직함을 자랑하면서도 진실을 말하지 않는 거죠?\""]], [["“I never boast, and I never tell lies,”", "\"난 자랑한 적도 없고 거짓말한 적도 없어.\""], ["he said slowly, restraining his rising anger.", "그는 치미는 분노를 억누르며 천천히 말했다."], ["“It’s a great pity if you can’t respect....”", "\"당신이 존중할 줄 모른다면 그건 참으로 유감스러운 일이지…….\""]], [["“Respect was invented to cover the empty place where love should be.", "\"존중이란 사랑이 있어야 할 빈자리를 가리려고 만들어 낸 말이에요."], ["And if you don’t love me any more, it would be better and more honest to say so.”", "그리고 당신이 더 이상 날 사랑하지 않는다면, 차라리 그렇다고 말하는 편이 더 낫고 더 정직할 거예요.\""]], [["“No, this is becoming unbearable!”", "\"아니, 이건 정말 견딜 수가 없군!\""], ["cried Vronsky, getting up from his chair; and stopping short, facing her, he said, speaking deliberately: “What do you try my patience for?”", "브론스키가 의자에서 벌떡 일어나며 소리쳤다. 그러고는 우뚝 멈춰 서서 그녀를 마주 보며 또박또박 힘주어 말했다."], ["looking as though he might have said much more, but was restraining himself.", "\"당신은 무엇 때문에 내 인내심을 시험하는 거지?\" 그는 훨씬 더 많은 말을 할 수도 있었으나 참고 있다는 표정이었다."], ["“It has limits.”", "\"거기에도 한계라는 게 있어.\""]], [["“What do you mean by that?”", "\"그게 무슨 뜻이죠?\""], ["she cried, looking with terror at the undisguised hatred in his whole face, and especially in his cruel, menacing eyes.", "그녀가 외쳤다. 그의 얼굴 전체에, 특히 잔인하고 위협적인 그의 두 눈에 가득한 노골적인 증오를 공포에 질려 바라보면서."]], [["“I mean to say....”", "\"내 말은…….\""], ["he was beginning, but he checked himself.", "그가 입을 떼다가 자제했다."], ["“I must ask what it is you want of me?”", "\"당신이 나한테 원하는 게 뭔지 묻고 싶을 뿐이야.\""]], [["“What can I want?", "\"내가 뭘 원할 수 있겠어요?"], ["All I can want is that you should not desert me, as you think of doing,”", "내가 원할 수 있는 건 오직 당신이 지금 생각하고 있는 것처럼 날 버리지 않는 것뿐이에요.\""], ["she said, understanding all he had not uttered.", "그녀는 그가 입 밖에 내지 않은 모든 것을 알아채고 말했다."], ["“But that I don’t want; that’s secondary.", "\"하지만 그건 내가 원하는 게 아니에요. 그건 부차적인 거죠."], ["I want love, and there is none.", "난 사랑을 원해요. 그런데 사랑이 없어요."], ["So then all is over.”", "그러니 모든 게 끝난 거예요.\""]], [["She turned towards the door.", "그녀가 문 쪽으로 돌아섰다."]], [["“Stop!", "\"멈춰!"], ["sto-op!”", "머, 멈추라고!\""], ["said Vronsky, with no change in the gloomy lines of his brows, though he held her by the hand.", "브론스키가 음울한 눈썹의 주름을 조금도 바꾸지 않은 채 말했다. 그러면서도 그는 그녀의 손을 붙잡았다."], ["“What is it all about?", "\"도대체 무슨 일로 이러는 거야?"], ["I said that we must put off going for three days, and on that you told me I was lying, that I was not an honorable man.”", "난 떠나는 걸 사흘만 미루자고 했을 뿐인데, 그 말에 당신은 내가 거짓말을 한다느니, 내가 명예롭지 못한 사람이라느니 했잖아.\""]], [["“Yes, and I repeat that the man who reproaches me with having sacrificed everything for me,”", "\"그래요, 그리고 다시 말하지만, 나를 위해 모든 걸 희생했다며 나를 나무라는 남자는,\" 그녀가 더 오래전 다툼 때의 말을 떠올리며 말했다."], ["she said, recalling the words of a still earlier quarrel, “that he’s worse than a dishonorable man—he’s a heartless man.”", "\"그런 남자는 명예롭지 못한 사람보다 더 나빠요. 그는 무정한 사람이에요.\""]], [["“Oh, there are limits to endurance!”", "\"오, 인내에도 한계가 있어!\""], ["he cried, and hastily let go her hand.", "그가 외치며 황급히 그녀의 손을 놓았다."]], [["“He hates me, that’s clear,”", "'그는 날 미워해, 그건 분명해.'"], ["she thought, and in silence, without looking round, she walked with faltering steps out of the room.", "그녀는 생각했고, 아무 말 없이 뒤도 돌아보지 않은 채 휘청거리는 걸음으로 방을 나섰다."], ["“He loves another woman, that’s even clearer,”", "'그는 다른 여자를 사랑해, 그건 더 분명하지.'"], ["she said to herself as she went into her own room.", "자기 방으로 들어가면서 그녀는 혼잣말을 했다."], ["“I want love, and there is none.", "'난 사랑을 원하는데, 사랑이 없어."], ["So, then, all is over.”", "그러니 모든 게 끝난 거야.'"], ["She repeated the words she had said, “and it must be ended.”", "그녀는 자기가 했던 말을 되풀이했다. '그래서 끝을 내야만 해.'"]], [["“But how?”", "'하지만 어떻게?'"], ["she asked herself, and she sat down in a low chair before the looking-glass.", "그녀는 스스로에게 물으며 거울 앞 낮은 의자에 앉았다."]], [["Thoughts of where she would go now, whether to the aunt who had brought her up, to Dolly, or simply alone abroad, and of what he was doing now alone in his study; whether this was the final quarrel, or whether reconciliation were still possible; and of what all her old friends at Petersburg would say of her now; and of how Alexey Alexandrovitch would look at it, and many other ideas of what would happen now after this rupture, came into her head; but she did not give herself up to them with all her heart.", "이제 어디로 갈까, 자기를 키워 준 이모에게로 갈까, 돌리에게로 갈까, 아니면 그저 혼자 외국으로 떠날까 하는 생각, 그가 지금 서재에 혼자서 무얼 하고 있을까 하는 생각, 이것이 마지막 다툼일까 아니면 아직 화해가 가능할까 하는 생각, 페테르부르크의 옛 친구들이 이제 자기를 두고 뭐라고 할까 하는 생각, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치는 이 일을 어떻게 받아들일까 하는 생각, 그리고 이 파국 뒤에 이제 무슨 일이 벌어질까 하는 온갖 생각이 그녀의 머릿속에 떠올랐다. 하지만 그녀는 그 생각들에 온 마음을 다해 빠져들지 않았다."], ["At the bottom of her heart was some obscure idea that alone interested her, but she could not get clear sight of it.", "그녀의 마음 밑바닥에는 그녀의 관심을 끄는 어떤 어렴풋한 생각 하나가 있었는데, 그녀는 그것을 또렷이 들여다볼 수 없었다."], ["Thinking once more of Alexey Alexandrovitch, she recalled the time of her illness after her confinement, and the feeling which never left her at that time.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치를 다시 떠올리던 그녀는 출산 후 앓았던 때를, 그리고 그 무렵 한시도 자기를 떠나지 않던 그 감정을 떠올렸다."], ["“Why didn’t I die?”", "'나는 왜 죽지 않았을까?'"], ["and the words and the feeling of that time came back to her.", "그때의 말과 그때의 감정이 그녀에게 되돌아왔다."], ["And all at once she knew what was in her soul.", "그리고 별안간 그녀는 자기 영혼 속에 무엇이 있는지 알았다."], ["Yes, it was that idea which alone solved all.", "그래, 모든 것을 풀어 줄 단 하나의 생각은 바로 그것이었다."], ["“Yes, to die!...", "'그래, 죽는 거야!……"], ["And the shame and disgrace of Alexey Alexandrovitch and of Seryozha, and my awful shame, it will all be saved by death.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치와 세료자의 부끄러움과 치욕도, 나의 끔찍한 수치도, 죽음으로 모두 구원받을 거야. 죽는 거야!"], ["To die!", "그러면 그는 자책하겠지."], ["and he will feel remorse; will be sorry; will love me; he will suffer on my account.”", "미안해하겠지. 나를 사랑하겠지."], ["With the trace of a smile of commiseration for herself she sat down in the armchair, taking off and putting on the rings on her left hand, vividly picturing from different sides his feelings after her death.", "나 때문에 괴로워할 거야.' 자기 자신에 대한 연민의 미소를 어렴풋이 띤 채 그녀는 안락의자에 앉아, 왼손의 반지들을 뺐다 끼웠다 하면서, 자기가 죽은 뒤 그가 느낄 감정들을 여러 각도에서 생생하게 그려 보았다."]], [["Approaching footsteps—his steps—distracted her attention.", "가까이 다가오는 발소리—그의 발소리—가 그녀의 주의를 흩뜨렸다."], ["As though absorbed in the arrangement of her rings, she did not even turn to him.", "그녀는 반지를 매만지는 데 몰두한 척, 그를 향해 돌아보지도 않았다."]], [["He went up to her, and taking her by the hand, said softly:", "그가 그녀에게 다가와 손을 잡으며 부드럽게 말했다."]], [["“Anna, we’ll go the day after tomorrow, if you like.", "\"안나, 당신이 원한다면 우리 모레 떠납시다."], ["I agree to everything.”", "난 무엇이든 다 동의할게.\""]], [["She did not speak.", "그녀는 말이 없었다."]], [["“What is it?”", "\"왜 그래?\""], ["he urged.", "그가 채근했다."]], [["“You know,”", "\"당신도 알잖아요.\""], ["she said, and at the same instant, unable to restrain herself any longer, she burst into sobs.", "그녀가 말했고, 바로 그 순간 더는 자제하지 못하고 흐느껴 울음을 터뜨렸다."]], [["“Cast me off!”", "\"날 내쳐 버려요!\""], ["she articulated between her sobs.", "그녀가 흐느낌 사이로 또박또박 말했다."], ["“I’ll go away tomorrow ...", "\"난 내일 떠날게요……"], ["I’ll do more.", "더한 일도 할 거예요."], ["What am I?", "내가 뭐죠?"], ["An immoral woman!", "부정한 여자예요!"], ["A stone round your neck.", "당신 목에 매달린 돌덩이라고요."], ["I don’t want to make you wretched, I don’t want to!", "난 당신을 비참하게 만들고 싶지 않아요, 그러고 싶지 않아요!"], ["I’ll set you free.", "당신을 자유롭게 해 줄게요."], ["You don’t love me; you love someone else!”", "당신은 날 사랑하지 않아요. 당신은 다른 사람을 사랑해요!\""]], [["Vronsky besought her to be calm, and declared that there was no trace of foundation for her jealousy; that he had never ceased, and never would cease, to love her; that he loved her more than ever.", "브론스키는 진정하라고 그녀에게 애원했고, 그녀의 질투에는 근거가 될 만한 구석이 조금도 없다고, 자기는 결코 그녀를 사랑하기를 멈춘 적이 없으며 앞으로도 결코 멈추지 않을 거라고, 그 어느 때보다 더 그녀를 사랑한다고 단언했다."]], [["“Anna, why distress yourself and me so?”", "\"안나, 어쩌자고 당신 자신과 나를 이렇게 괴롭히는 거야?\""], ["he said to her, kissing her hands.", "그가 그녀의 두 손에 입 맞추며 말했다."], ["There was tenderness now in his face, and she fancied she caught the sound of tears in his voice, and she felt them wet on her hand.", "이제 그의 얼굴에는 다정함이 어려 있었고, 그녀는 그의 목소리에서 눈물의 기척을 느낀 듯했으며, 자기 손에 그 눈물이 젖어 드는 것을 느꼈다."], ["And instantly Anna’s despairing jealousy changed to a despairing passion of tenderness.", "그러자 순식간에 안나의 절망적인 질투는 절망적인 다정함의 격정으로 바뀌었다."], ["She put her arms round him, and covered with kisses his head, his neck, his hands.", "그녀는 두 팔로 그를 끌어안고 그의 머리에, 목에, 두 손에 입맞춤을 퍼부었다."]], [["# Chapter 25", "# Chapter 25"]], [["Feeling that the reconciliation was complete, Anna set eagerly to work in the morning preparing for their departure.", "화해가 완전히 이루어졌다고 느낀 안나는 아침부터 출발 준비에 열심히 매달렸다."], ["Though it was not settled whether they should go on Monday or Tuesday, as they had each given way to the other, Anna packed busily, feeling absolutely indifferent whether they went a day earlier or later.", "두 사람이 서로 양보하느라 월요일에 떠날지 화요일에 떠날지가 아직 정해지지 않았지만, 안나는 하루 일찍 가든 늦게 가든 전혀 개의치 않는다는 듯이 부지런히 짐을 꾸렸다."], ["She was standing in her room over an open box, taking things out of it, when he came in to see her earlier than usual, dressed to go out.", "그녀가 자기 방에서 뚜껑이 열린 트렁크 위로 몸을 구부린 채 그 안의 물건들을 꺼내고 있을 때, 외출 차림을 한 그가 평소보다 일찍 그녀를 보러 들어왔다."]], [["“I’m going off at once to see maman; she can send me the money by Yegorov.", "\"지금 곧 maman한테 다녀오려고. 어머니가 예고로프 편에 돈을 보내 줄 수 있을 거야."], ["And I shall be ready to go tomorrow,”", "나는 내일 떠날 채비가 될 거고.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["Though she was in such a good mood, the thought of his visit to his mother’s gave her a pang.", "이렇게 기분이 좋은데도, 그가 어머니를 찾아간다는 생각이 그녀의 가슴을 쿡 찔렀다."]], [["“No, I shan’t be ready by then myself,”", "\"아니요, 나는 그때까지 준비가 안 될 거예요.\""], ["she said; and at once reflected, “so then it was possible to arrange to do as I wished.”", "그녀는 말하고는 곧바로 생각했다. '그러니까 내가 바라는 대로 일을 맞출 수도 있었던 거잖아.'"], ["“No, do as you meant to do.", "\"아니, 당신이 하려던 대로 해요."], ["Go into the dining-room, I’m coming directly.", "식당으로 가세요, 내가 곧 갈 테니까."], ["It’s only to turn out those things that aren’t wanted,”", "그냥 필요 없는 물건들을 추려 내는 것뿐이에요.\""], ["she said, putting something more on the heap of frippery that lay in Annushka’s arms.", "그녀는 이렇게 말하며 안누슈카의 팔에 안긴 잡동사니 더미 위에 무언가를 더 얹었다."]], [["Vronsky was eating his beefsteak when she came into the dining-room.", "안나가 식당으로 들어갔을 때 브론스키는 비프스테이크를 먹고 있었다."]], [["“You wouldn’t believe how distasteful these rooms have become to me,”", "\"이 방들이 내게 얼마나 정나미가 떨어졌는지 당신은 믿지 못할 거예요.\""], ["she said, sitting down beside him to her coffee.", "그녀는 그의 옆에 앉아 커피를 들며 말했다."], ["“There’s nothing more awful than these chambres garnies.", "\"이런 chambres garnies보다 더 끔찍한 건 없어요."], ["There’s no individuality in them, no soul.", "여긴 개성이 없어요, 영혼도 없고."], ["These clocks, and curtains, and, worst of all, the wallpapers—they’re a nightmare.", "이 시계들이며, 커튼이며, 무엇보다 이 벽지들—악몽 같아요. 나는 보즈드비젠스코예를 약속의 땅처럼 떠올린다니까요."], ["I think of Vozdvizhenskoe as the promised land.", "말들은 아직 안 보내는 거죠?\""], ["You’re not sending the horses off yet?”", "* chambres garnies: 프랑스어로 '가구가 갖추어진 셋방'."]], [["“No, they will come after us.", "\"안 보내, 우리 뒤를 따라올 거야."], ["Where are you going to?”", "당신은 어디 가는데?\""]], [["“I wanted to go to Wilson’s to take some dresses to her.", "\"윌슨네에 드레스를 몇 벌 가져다주려고요."], ["So it’s really to be tomorrow?”", "그럼 정말로 내일인 거예요?\""], ["she said in a cheerful voice; but suddenly her face changed.", "그녀는 명랑한 목소리로 말했다. 그러나 별안간 그녀의 얼굴이 변했다."]], [["Vronsky’s valet came in to ask him to sign a receipt for a telegram from Petersburg.", "브론스키의 시종이 페테르부르크에서 온 전보의 수령증에 서명해 달라고 청하러 들어왔다."], ["There was nothing out of the way in Vronsky’s getting a telegram, but he said, as though anxious to conceal something from her, that the receipt was in his study, and he turned hurriedly to her.", "브론스키가 전보 한 통 받는 일에 별다른 데는 없었지만, 그는 무언가를 그녀에게 숨기고 싶어 안달하는 듯이, 수령증은 서재에 있다고 말하고는 황급히 그녀를 향해 돌아섰다."]], [["“By tomorrow, without fail, I will finish it all.”", "\"내일까지는 틀림없이, 다 끝내 놓을게.\""]], [["“From whom is the telegram?”", "\"전보가 누구한테서 온 거예요?\""], ["she asked, not hearing him.", "그녀가 그의 말을 듣지도 않고 물었다."]], [["“From Stiva,”", "\"스티바한테서.\""], ["he answered reluctantly.", "그가 마지못해 대답했다."]], [["“Why didn’t you show it to me?", "\"왜 나한테 안 보여 줬어요?"], ["What secret can there be between Stiva and me?”", "스티바와 나 사이에 무슨 비밀이 있을 수 있다고?\""]], [["Vronsky called the valet back, and told him to bring the telegram.", "브론스키는 시종을 다시 불러 전보를 가져오라고 일렀다."]], [["“I didn’t want to show it to you, because Stiva has such a passion for telegraphing: why telegraph when nothing is settled?”", "\"보여 주고 싶지 않았어, 스티바가 전보 치는 데 어찌나 열을 올리는지 말이야. 아직 아무것도 정해지지 않았는데 전보는 왜 친담?\""]], [["“About the divorce?”", "\"이혼에 관한 거예요?\""]], [["“Yes; but he says he has not been able to come at anything yet.", "\"응. 그런데 아직 아무것도 얻어 내지 못했다는군."], ["He has promised a decisive answer in a day or two.", "하루 이틀 안에 확실한 답을 주겠다고 약속했어."], ["But here it is; read it.”", "자, 여기 있어, 읽어 봐.\""]], [["With trembling hands Anna took the telegram, and read what Vronsky had told her.", "안나는 떨리는 손으로 전보를 받아 브론스키가 일러 준 내용을 읽었다. 끝에는 이렇게 덧붙여져 있었다."], ["At the end was added: “Little hope; but I will do everything possible and impossible.”", "\"희망은 적음. 그러나 가능한 일이든 불가능한 일이든 다 해 보겠음.\""]], [["“I said yesterday that it’s absolutely nothing to me when I get, or whether I never get, a divorce,”", "\"내가 어제 말했잖아요, 이혼을 언제 받든, 아니면 영영 못 받든 나한테는 전혀 아무 의미가 없다고.\""], ["she said, flushing crimson.", "그녀는 새빨갛게 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다."], ["“There was not the slightest necessity to hide it from me.”", "\"그걸 나한테 숨길 이유는 눈곱만큼도 없었어요.\""], ["“So he may hide and does hide his correspondence with women from me,”", "'그러니까 저 사람은 여자들과 주고받은 편지도 나한테 숨길 수 있고, 또 숨기고 있는 거야.'"], ["she thought.", "그녀는 생각했다."]], [["“Yashvin meant to come this morning with Voytov,”", "\"야시빈이 오늘 아침에 보이토프와 함께 오기로 했어.\""], ["said Vronsky; “I believe he’s won from Pyevtsov all and more than he can pay, about sixty thousand.”", "브론스키가 말했다. \"퍄프초프한테서 그가 갚을 수 있는 것 이상을 다 땄다더군, 한 육만 정도.\""]], [["“No,”", "\"아니에요.\""], ["she said, irritated by his so obviously showing by this change of subject that he was irritated, “why did you suppose that this news would affect me so, that you must even try to hide it?", "그녀는 그가 이렇게 화제를 돌리는 것으로 자기가 짜증이 났음을 너무도 빤히 드러내는 데에 신경이 곤두서서 말했다. \"당신은 어째서 이 소식이 나한테 그토록 영향을 미칠 거라고, 그래서 그걸 숨기려고까지 해야 한다고 생각한 거예요?"], ["I said I don’t want to consider it, and I should have liked you to care as little about it as I do.”", "나는 이 일을 따지고 싶지 않다고 말했고, 당신도 나만큼이나 이 일을 대수롭지 않게 여겨 줬으면 했어요.\""]], [["“I care about it because I like definiteness,”", "\"나는 명확한 게 좋으니까 이 일에 신경을 쓰는 거야.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“Definiteness is not in the form but the love,”", "\"명확함은 형식에 있는 게 아니라 사랑에 있어요.\""], ["she said, more and more irritated, not by his words, but by the tone of cool composure in which he spoke.", "그녀는 점점 더 신경이 곤두서서 말했는데, 그것은 그의 말 때문이 아니라 그가 말하는 그 차분하고 냉정한 어조 때문이었다."], ["“What do you want it for?”", "\"당신은 그게 뭣 때문에 필요한 거예요?\""]], [["“My God!", "'맙소사!"], ["love again,”", "또 사랑 타령이군.'"], ["he thought, frowning.", "그는 미간을 찌푸리며 생각했다."]], [["“Oh, you know what for; for your sake and your children’s in the future.”", "\"아, 당신도 뭣 때문인지 알잖아. 당신을 위해서, 그리고 앞으로 우리 아이들을 위해서지.\""]], [["“There won’t be children in the future.”", "\"앞으로 아이들은 없을 거예요.\""]], [["“That’s a great pity,”", "\"그거 참 안타까운 일이군.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“You want it for the children’s sake, but you don’t think of me?”", "\"당신은 아이들을 위해서 그걸 원하는 거지, 나는 생각하지 않는 거잖아요?\""], ["she said, quite forgetting or not having heard that he had said, “For your sake and the children’s.”", "그녀는 그가 \"당신을 위해서, 그리고 아이들을 위해서\"라고 말한 것을 까맣게 잊었거나, 아니면 아예 듣지 못한 채 말했다."]], [["The question of the possibility of having children had long been a subject of dispute and irritation to her.", "아이를 가질 수 있느냐 없느냐 하는 문제는 이미 오래전부터 그녀에게 다툼과 짜증의 거리였다."], ["His desire to have children she interpreted as a proof he did not prize her beauty.", "아이를 갖고 싶어 하는 그의 바람을, 그녀는 그가 자기 미모를 소중히 여기지 않는다는 증거로 해석했다."]], [["“Oh, I said: for your sake.", "\"아, 내가 말했잖아, 당신을 위해서라고."], ["Above all for your sake,”", "무엇보다도 당신을 위해서라고.\""], ["he repeated, frowning as though in pain, “because I am certain that the greater part of your irritability comes from the indefiniteness of the position.”", "그가 고통스러운 듯 미간을 찌푸리며 거듭 말했다. \"왜냐하면 당신이 짜증을 내는 것은 대부분 이 처지가 불명확한 데서 비롯된다고 나는 확신하니까.\""]], [["“Yes, now he has laid aside all pretense, and all his cold hatred for me is apparent,”", "'그래, 이제 저 사람은 온갖 가식을 다 벗어 버렸고, 나를 향한 차가운 증오를 죄다 드러내고 있어.'"], ["she thought, not hearing his words, but watching with terror the cold, cruel judge who looked mocking her out of his eyes.", "그녀는 그의 말은 듣지도 않고, 그의 눈 속에서 자기를 비웃듯 바라보는 그 차갑고 잔인한 재판관을 공포에 질려 지켜보며 생각했다."]], [["“The cause is not that,”", "\"그 원인은 그게 아니에요.\""], ["she said, “and, indeed, I don’t see how the cause of my irritability, as you call it, can be that I am completely in your power.", "그녀가 말했다. \"게다가 정말이지 내가, 당신 말마따나 짜증을 내는 그 원인이 내가 완전히 당신의 손아귀에 들어 있다는 데에 있다니, 도무지 모르겠네요."], ["What indefiniteness is there in the position?", "이 처지에 무슨 불명확함이 있다는 거예요?"], ["on the contrary....”", "오히려 그 반대지…….\""]], [["“I am very sorry that you don’t care to understand,”", "\"당신이 이해하려 들지 않는다니 참으로 유감이군.\""], ["he interrupted, obstinately anxious to give utterance to his thought.", "그가 자기 생각을 끝내 입 밖으로 내고야 말겠다는 듯 고집스럽게 그녀의 말을 가로막았다."], ["“The indefiniteness consists in your imagining that I am free.”", "\"그 불명확함이란, 당신이 나를 자유로운 몸이라고 상상하는 데 있는 거야.\""]], [["“On that score you can set your mind quite at rest,”", "\"그 점이라면 마음을 아주 푹 놓아도 돼요.\""], ["she said, and turning away from him, she began drinking her coffee.", "그녀는 이렇게 말하고는 그에게서 몸을 돌려 커피를 마시기 시작했다."]], [["She lifted her cup, with her little finger held apart, and put it to her lips.", "그녀는 새끼손가락을 따로 세운 채 잔을 들어 입술로 가져갔다."], ["After drinking a few sips she glanced at him, and by his expression, she saw clearly that he was repelled by her hand, and her gesture, and the sound made by her lips.", "몇 모금 마시고는 그를 흘끗 보았는데, 그 표정으로 그녀는 그가 자기 손과 자기 몸짓과 자기 입술이 내는 소리에 진저리를 치고 있다는 것을 똑똑히 알아챘다."]], [["“I don’t care in the least what your mother thinks, and what match she wants to make for you,”", "\"당신 어머니가 뭐라고 생각하든, 당신을 어떤 사람과 짝지어 주고 싶어 하든, 나는 조금도 관심 없어요.\""], ["she said, putting the cup down with a shaking hand.", "그녀는 떨리는 손으로 잔을 내려놓으며 말했다."]], [["“But we are not talking about that.”", "\"하지만 지금 우리가 그 얘길 하고 있는 게 아니잖아.\""]], [["“Yes, that’s just what we are talking about.", "\"아니에요, 우리는 바로 그 얘길 하고 있는 거예요."], ["And let me tell you that a heartless woman, whether she’s old or not old, your mother or anyone else, is of no consequence to me, and I would not consent to know her.”", "그리고 분명히 말해 두겠는데, 매정한 여자는, 그게 늙었든 안 늙었든, 당신 어머니든 그 누구든, 나한테는 아무런 의미도 없고, 나는 그런 여자와 알고 지내는 데 절대 동의할 수 없어요.\""]], [["“Anna, I beg you not to speak disrespectfully of my mother.”", "\"안나, 부탁이니 우리 어머니를 함부로 말하지 말아 줘.\""]], [["“A woman whose heart does not tell her where her son’s happiness and honor lie has no heart.”", "\"제 아들의 행복과 명예가 어디에 있는지 마음으로 일러 주지 못하는 여자라면, 그런 여자한테는 마음이라는 게 없는 거예요.\""]], [["“I repeat my request that you will not speak disrespectfully of my mother, whom I respect,”", "\"내가 존경하는 우리 어머니를 함부로 말하지 말아 달라고, 다시 한번 부탁하지.\""], ["he said, raising his voice and looking sternly at her.", "그는 목소리를 높이며 그녀를 매섭게 노려보며 말했다."]], [["She did not answer.", "그녀는 대답하지 않았다."], ["Looking intently at him, at his face, his hands, she recalled all the details of their reconciliation the previous day, and his passionate caresses.", "그를, 그의 얼굴을, 그의 손을 뚫어지게 바라보며, 그녀는 어제 화해할 때의 온갖 정황과 그의 정열적인 애무를 모조리 떠올렸다."], ["“There, just such caresses he has lavished, and will lavish, and longs to lavish on other women!”", "'저런, 바로 저런 애무를 저 사람은 다른 여자들한테도 퍼부었고, 또 퍼부을 테고, 퍼붓고 싶어 안달하겠지!'"], ["she thought.", "그녀는 생각했다."]], [["“You don’t love your mother.", "\"당신은 어머니를 사랑하지 않아요."], ["That’s all talk, and talk, and talk!”", "그건 다 말뿐이고, 말뿐이고, 또 말뿐이에요!\""], ["she said, looking at him with hatred in her eyes.", "그녀는 증오에 찬 눈으로 그를 바라보며 말했다."]], [["“Even if so, you must....”", "\"설령 그렇다 해도, 당신은…….\""]], [["“Must decide, and I have decided,”", "\"결정을 내려야 한다는 거겠죠, 나는 이미 결정했어요.\""], ["she said, and she would have gone away, but at that moment Yashvin walked into the room.", "그녀는 이렇게 말하고는 나가 버리려 했지만, 바로 그 순간 야시빈이 방으로 들어왔다."], ["Anna greeted him and remained.", "안나는 그에게 인사하고 그 자리에 남았다."]], [["Why, when there was a tempest in her soul, and she felt she was standing at a turning point in her life, which might have fearful consequences—why, at that minute, she had to keep up appearances before an outsider, who sooner or later must know it all—she did not know.", "영혼 속에 폭풍이 몰아치고, 자기 인생에서 어쩌면 무시무시한 결과를 빚을지도 모르는 전환점에 서 있다고 느끼는 마당에, 어째서 그 순간에 머지않아 어차피 모든 것을 다 알게 될 외부 사람 앞에서 겉치레를 유지해야만 했는지—그녀 자신도 알 수 없었다."], ["But at once quelling the storm within her, she sat down and began talking to their guest.", "그러나 그녀는 곧 자기 안의 폭풍을 잠재우고 자리에 앉아 손님과 이야기를 나누기 시작했다."]], [["“Well, how are you getting on?", "\"그래, 어떻게 지내세요?"], ["Has your debt been paid you?”", "빚은 받으셨나요?\""], ["she asked Yashvin.", "그녀가 야시빈에게 물었다."]], [["“Oh, pretty fair; I fancy I shan’t get it all, but I shall get a good half.", "\"아, 그럭저럭이요. 전부 다는 못 받을 것 같습니다만, 절반은 톡톡히 받을 겁니다."], ["And when are you off?”", "그런데 두 분은 언제 떠나십니까?\""], ["said Yashvin, looking at Vronsky, and unmistakably guessing at a quarrel.", "야시빈은 브론스키를 바라보며, 둘 사이에 다툼이 있었음을 영락없이 알아채고 말했다."]], [["“The day after tomorrow, I think,”", "\"모레쯤일 것 같소.\""], ["said Vronsky.", "브론스키가 말했다."]], [["“You’ve been meaning to go so long, though.”", "\"하지만 떠나신다고 하신 지 한참 됐잖습니까.\""]], [["“But now it’s quite decided,”", "\"하지만 이제는 완전히 정해졌어요.\""], ["said Anna, looking Vronsky straight in the face with a look which told him not to dream of the possibility of reconciliation.", "안나는 브론스키의 얼굴을 똑바로 들여다보며 말했는데, 그 눈빛은 그에게 화해 따위의 가능성은 꿈도 꾸지 말라고 일러 주고 있었다."]], [["“Don’t you feel sorry for that unlucky Pyevtsov?”", "\"그 운 없는 퍄프초프가 가엾지 않으세요?\""], ["she went on, talking to Yashvin.", "그녀가 야시빈에게 말을 이었다."]], [["“I’ve never asked myself the question, Anna Arkadyevna, whether I’m sorry for him or not.", "\"제가 그를 가엾어하는지 아닌지, 한 번도 스스로 물어본 적이 없습니다, 안나 아르카지예브나."], ["You see, all my fortune’s here”—he touched his breast pocket—“and just now I’m a wealthy man.", "보십시오, 제 전 재산은 여기 들어 있고요\"—그는 가슴 안주머니를 가볍게 두드렸다—\"지금 이 순간 저는 부자입니다."], ["But today I’m going to the club, and I may come out a beggar.", "하지만 오늘 클럽에 갔다가 거지꼴이 되어 나올 수도 있지요."], ["You see, whoever sits down to play with me—he wants to leave me without a shirt to my back, and so do I him.", "보십시오, 누구든 저와 마주 앉아 노름을 하는 자는—저를 등에 걸칠 셔츠 한 장 없이 발가벗겨 놓으려 들고, 저도 그자한테 그렇게 하려 들죠."], ["And so we fight it out, and that’s the pleasure of it.”", "그렇게 우리는 한판 붙는 거고, 거기에 묘미가 있는 겁니다.\""]], [["“Well, but suppose you were married,”", "\"그래도, 만약 당신이 결혼한 몸이라고 쳐 봐요.\""], ["said Anna, “how would it be for your wife?”", "안나가 말했다. \"당신 부인은 어떻게 되겠어요?\""]], [["Yashvin laughed.", "야시빈이 껄껄 웃었다."]], [["“That’s why I’m not married, and never mean to be.”", "\"그래서 저는 결혼을 안 했고, 앞으로도 할 생각이 없는 겁니다.\""]], [["“And Helsingfors?”", "\"그럼 헬싱포르스는요?\""], ["said Vronsky, entering into the conversation and glancing at Anna’s smiling face.", "브론스키가 대화에 끼어들며 미소 짓는 안나의 얼굴을 흘끗 보고 말했다."], ["Meeting his eyes, Anna’s face instantly took a coldly severe expression as though she were saying to him: “It’s not forgotten.", "그의 눈과 마주치자, 안나의 얼굴은 즉각 차갑고 매서운 표정으로 굳어졌는데, 마치 그에게 이렇게 말하는 듯했다. '잊지 않았어."], ["It’s all the same.”", "다 마찬가지야.'"]], [["“Were you really in love?”", "\"정말로 사랑에 빠지셨던 거예요?\""], ["she said to Yashvin.", "그녀가 야시빈에게 말했다."]], [["“Oh heavens!", "\"오, 맙소사!"], ["ever so many times!", "그것도 수도 없이요!"], ["But you see, some men can play but only so that they can always lay down their cards when the hour of a rendezvous comes, while I can take up love, but only so as not to be late for my cards in the evening.", "하지만 보십시오, 어떤 남자들은 노름을 하긴 하되 rendezvous 시간이 오면 언제든 카드를 내려놓을 수 있게끔만 하지요. 반면에 저는 사랑을 하긴 하되, 저녁 카드 시간에 늦지 않도록만 합니다."], ["That’s how I manage things.”", "저는 그런 식으로 일을 처리하지요.\" * rendezvous: 프랑스어로 '밀회, 만남의 약속'."]], [["“No, I didn’t mean that, but the real thing.”", "\"아니요, 그런 게 아니라, 진짜 사랑 말이에요.\""], ["She would have said Helsingfors, but would not repeat the word used by Vronsky.", "그녀는 '헬싱포르스'라고 말하려다가, 브론스키가 쓴 그 단어를 되풀이하고 싶지 않았다."]], [["Voytov, who was buying the horse, came in.", "말을 사기로 한 보이토프가 들어왔다."], ["Anna got up and went out of the room.", "안나는 자리에서 일어나 방을 나갔다."]], [["Before leaving the house, Vronsky went into her room.", "집을 나서기 전, 브론스키는 그녀의 방으로 들어갔다."], ["She would have pretended to be looking for something on the table, but ashamed of making a pretense, she looked straight in his face with cold eyes.", "그녀는 탁자 위에서 무언가를 찾는 척하려다가, 그렇게 가장하는 것이 부끄러워 차가운 눈으로 그의 얼굴을 똑바로 쳐다보았다."]], [["“What do you want?”", "\"무슨 일이에요?\""], ["she asked in French.", "그녀가 프랑스어로 물었다."]], [["“To get the guarantee for Gambetta, I’ve sold him,”", "\"감베타의 보증서를 가지러, 그놈을 팔았거든.\""], ["he said, in a tone which said more clearly than words, “I’ve no time for discussing things, and it would lead to nothing.”", "그는 말로 하는 것보다 더 또렷하게 이렇게 말하는 어조로 대답했다. \"이런 일들을 두고 얘기할 시간이 없고, 얘기해 봤자 아무 소용도 없어.\""]], [["“I’m not to blame in any way,”", "'나는 어떤 식으로도 잘못한 게 없어.'"], ["he thought.", "그는 생각했다."], ["“If she will punish herself, tant pis pour elle.", "'저 여자가 스스로를 벌하겠다면, tant pis pour elle.'"], ["But as he was going he fancied that she said something, and his heart suddenly ached with pity for her.", "그러나 막 나가려는데 그녀가 무언가를 말한 것 같았고, 별안간 그의 가슴이 그녀에 대한 연민으로 욱신거렸다. * tant pis pour elle: 프랑스어로 '그녀에게 안된 일이지만 할 수 없지'."]], [["“Eh, Anna?”", "\"응, 안나?\""], ["he queried.", "그가 물었다."]], [["“I said nothing,”", "\"아무 말도 안 했어요.\""], ["she answered just as coldly and calmly.", "그녀는 여전히 차갑고 침착하게 대답했다."]], [["“Oh, nothing, tant pis then,”", "'오, 아무것도 아니라면, tant pis로군.'"], ["he thought, feeling cold again, and he turned and went out.", "그는 다시 마음이 싸늘해지는 것을 느끼며 생각하고는, 돌아서서 나갔다."], ["As he was going out he caught a glimpse in the looking-glass of her face, white, with quivering lips.", "나가는 길에 그는 거울 속에서 그녀의 얼굴을, 입술을 파르르 떠는 새하얀 얼굴을 언뜻 보았다."], ["He even wanted to stop and to say some comforting word to her, but his legs carried him out of the room before he could think what to say.", "그는 걸음을 멈추고 그녀에게 무언가 위로의 말을 건네고 싶기까지 했지만, 무슨 말을 해야 할지 미처 생각해 내기도 전에 두 다리가 그를 방 밖으로 데리고 나갔다."], ["The whole of that day he spent away from home, and when he came in late in the evening the maid told him that Anna Arkadyevna had a headache and begged him not to go in to her.", "그날 하루 종일 그는 집을 떠나 있었고, 밤늦게 돌아왔을 때 하녀는 안나 아르카지예브나가 두통이 있어서 자기 방에 들어오지 말아 달라고 청했다고 그에게 전했다. * tant pis: 프랑스어로 '할 수 없지'."]], [["# Chapter 26", "# Chapter 26"]], [["Never before had a day been passed in quarrel.", "이제껏 단 하루도 다툼으로 보낸 적은 없었다."], ["Today was the first time.", "오늘이 처음이었다."], ["And this was not a quarrel.", "그리고 이것은 다툼도 아니었다."], ["It was the open acknowledgment of complete coldness.", "이것은 완전한 냉담을 공공연히 인정하는 것이었다."], ["Was it possible to glance at her as he had glanced when he came into the room for the guarantee?—to look at her, see her heart was breaking with despair, and go out without a word with that face of callous composure?", "보증서 때문에 방에 들어왔을 때 그가 자기를 쳐다보았던 것처럼 그렇게 쳐다볼 수가 있단 말인가?—자기를 바라보고, 자기 마음이 절망으로 찢어지고 있는 것을 보면서도, 그 무감각하게 침착한 얼굴로 한마디 말도 없이 나가 버리다니?"], ["He was not merely cold to her, he hated her because he loved another woman—that was clear.", "그는 단지 자기에게 냉담한 게 아니라, 자기를 미워하고 있었다, 다른 여자를 사랑하기 때문에—그것은 분명했다."]], [["And remembering all the cruel words he had said, Anna supplied, too, the words that he had unmistakably wished to say and could have said to her, and she grew more and more exasperated.", "그리고 그가 했던 잔인한 말들을 모조리 떠올리면서, 안나는 그가 분명히 하고 싶어 했고 자기에게 할 수도 있었을 말들까지 덧붙였고, 점점 더 격분해 갔다."]], [["“I won’t prevent you,”", "\"당신을 막지 않겠소.\""], ["he might say. “You can go where you like.", "그는 이렇게 말할 수도 있었다."], ["You were unwilling to be divorced from your husband, no doubt so that you might go back to him.", "\"당신이 원하는 곳 어디든 갈 수 있소. 당신은 남편과 이혼하기를 원치 않았지, 틀림없이 그에게 돌아가려는 거겠지."], ["Go back to him.", "그에게 돌아가시오."], ["If you want money, I’ll give it to you.", "돈이 필요하면 내가 주겠소."], ["How many roubles do you want?”", "몇 루블이나 원하오?\""]], [["All the most cruel words that a brutal man could say, he said to her in her imagination, and she could not forgive him for them, as though he had actually said them.", "잔혹한 남자라면 할 법한 가장 잔인한 말들을 그는 그녀의 상상 속에서 그녀에게 했고, 그녀는 그가 실제로 그 말들을 한 것처럼 그를 용서할 수 없었다."]], [["“But didn’t he only yesterday swear he loved me, he, a truthful and sincere man?", "\"하지만 그는 바로 어제 나를 사랑한다고 맹세하지 않았나, 진실하고 성실한 사람인 그가?"], ["Haven’t I despaired for nothing many times already?”", "나는 벌써 여러 번 공연히 절망하지 않았던가?\""], ["she said to herself afterwards.", "그녀는 그 후에 혼잣말을 했다."]], [["All that day, except for the visit to Wilson’s, which occupied two hours, Anna spent in doubts whether everything were over or whether there were still hope of reconciliation, whether she should go away at once or see him once more.", "그날 하루 종일, 두 시간을 차지한 윌슨의 가게 방문을 빼고는, 안나는 모든 게 끝났는지 아니면 아직 화해할 희망이 있는지, 당장 떠나야 할지 아니면 그를 한 번 더 봐야 할지 망설이며 보냈다."], ["She was expecting him the whole day, and in the evening, as she went to her own room, leaving a message for him that her head ached, she said to herself, “If he comes in spite of what the maid says, it means that he loves me still.", "그녀는 하루 종일 그를 기다렸고, 저녁이 되어 자기 방으로 들어가면서 머리가 아프다는 전갈을 그에게 남긴 채, 혼잣말을 했다. \"하녀가 뭐라고 하든 그가 들어온다면, 그건 그가 아직 나를 사랑한다는 뜻이야."], ["If not, it means that all is over, and then I will decide what I’m to do!...”", "그렇지 않으면, 그건 모든 게 끝났다는 뜻이고, 그러면 내가 어떻게 할지 결정하겠어!……\""]], [["In the evening she heard the rumbling of his carriage stop at the entrance, his ring, his steps and his conversation with the servant; he believed what was told him, did not care to find out more, and went to his own room.", "저녁에 그녀는 그의 마차가 덜커덩거리며 현관 앞에 멈추는 소리, 그가 초인종을 울리는 소리, 그의 발소리, 그리고 그가 하인과 나누는 대화를 들었다. 그는 자기에게 전해진 말을 믿었고, 더 알아보려 하지도 않았으며, 자기 방으로 들어가 버렸다."], ["So then everything was over.", "그러니 이제 모든 게 끝난 것이었다."]], [["And death rose clearly and vividly before her mind as the sole means of bringing back love for her in his heart, of punishing him and of gaining the victory in that strife which the evil spirit in possession of her heart was waging with him.", "그러자 죽음이, 그의 마음속에 자기에 대한 사랑을 되살리는 유일한 수단으로서, 그를 벌하고 그녀의 마음을 사로잡은 악령이 그를 상대로 벌이고 있는 그 싸움에서 승리를 거두는 유일한 수단으로서, 그녀의 머릿속에 또렷하고 생생하게 떠올랐다."]], [["Now nothing mattered: going or not going to Vozdvizhenskoe, getting or not getting a divorce from her husband—all that did not matter.", "이제 아무것도 중요하지 않았다. 보즈드비젠스코예로 가든 안 가든, 남편과 이혼을 하든 못 하든—그 모든 것은 중요하지 않았다."], ["The one thing that mattered was punishing him.", "중요한 단 한 가지는 그를 벌하는 것이었다."], ["When she poured herself out her usual dose of opium, and thought that she had only to drink off the whole bottle to die, it seemed to her so simple and easy, that she began musing with enjoyment on how he would suffer, and repent and love her memory when it would be too late.", "그녀가 평소대로 아편을 한 모금 따르면서, 죽으려면 병째로 다 마시기만 하면 된다고 생각하자, 그것이 너무나 간단하고 쉬워 보여서, 그녀는 그가 어떻게 괴로워할지, 그리고 너무 늦어 버렸을 때 어떻게 후회하고 자기를 그리워할지를 즐거이 상상하기 시작했다."], ["She lay in bed with open eyes, by the light of a single burned-down candle, gazing at the carved cornice of the ceiling and at the shadow of the screen that covered part of it, while she vividly pictured to herself how he would feel when she would be no more, when she would be only a memory to him.", "그녀는 다 타들어 가는 촛불 하나의 빛에 의지해 눈을 뜬 채 침대에 누워, 천장의 조각된 처마 장식과 그 일부를 가린 칸막이의 그림자를 바라보면서, 자기가 더 이상 없을 때, 자기가 그에게 한낱 기억일 뿐일 때 그가 어떤 심정일지를 생생하게 그려 보았다. \"어떻게 내가 그녀에게 그토록 잔인한 말을 할 수 있었을까?\""], ["“How could I say such cruel things to her?”", "그는 이렇게 말할 것이다."], ["he would say. “How could I go out of the room without saying anything to her?", "\"어떻게 내가 그녀에게 아무 말도 없이 방을 나갈 수 있었을까?"], ["But now she is no more.", "그러나 이제 그녀는 없다."], ["She has gone away from us forever.", "그녀는 우리 곁을 영원히 떠나 버렸다."], ["She is....”", "그녀는……\""], ["Suddenly the shadow of the screen wavered, pounced on the whole cornice, the whole ceiling; other shadows from the other side swooped to meet it, for an instant the shadows flitted back, but then with fresh swiftness they darted forward, wavered, commingled, and all was darkness.", "갑자기 칸막이의 그림자가 흔들리더니, 처마 장식 전체로, 천장 전체로 덮쳐 왔고, 다른 쪽에서 다른 그림자들이 그것을 맞이하러 달려들었으며, 한순간 그림자들은 뒤로 물러났다가, 다시 새로운 빠르기로 앞으로 돌진해 흔들리고 뒤엉키더니, 모든 것이 어둠이 되었다."], ["“Death!”", "\"죽음!\""], ["she thought.", "그녀는 생각했다."], ["And such horror came upon her that for a long while she could not realize where she was, and for a long while her trembling hands could not find the matches and light another candle, instead of the one that had burned down and gone out.", "그러자 너무나 큰 공포가 밀려와서, 그녀는 한참 동안 자기가 어디에 있는지 알 수 없었고, 한참 동안 떨리는 손으로 성냥을 찾지 못해, 다 타서 꺼져 버린 초 대신 다른 초에 불을 붙이지 못했다. \"아니, 무엇이든—그저 살고만 싶어!"], ["“No, anything—only to live!", "아니, 나는 그를 사랑해!"], ["Why, I love him! Why, he loves me!", "아니, 그는 나를 사랑해!"], ["This has been before and will pass,”", "이런 일은 전에도 있었고 지나갈 거야.\""], ["she said, feeling that tears of joy at the return to life were trickling down her cheeks.", "그녀는 삶으로 돌아온 기쁨의 눈물이 뺨을 타고 흘러내리는 것을 느끼며 말했다."], ["And to escape from her panic she went hurriedly to his room.", "그리고 공황에서 벗어나려고 그녀는 황급히 그의 방으로 갔다."]], [["He was asleep there, and sleeping soundly.", "그는 거기서 잠들어 있었고, 깊이 자고 있었다."], ["She went up to him, and holding the light above his face, she gazed a long while at him.", "그녀는 그에게 다가가, 촛불을 그의 얼굴 위로 들어 올린 채 한참 동안 그를 바라보았다."], ["Now when he was asleep, she loved him so that at the sight of him she could not keep back tears of tenderness.", "잠들어 있는 지금, 그녀는 그를 너무나 사랑해서 그를 보고 있노라니 다정한 눈물을 참을 수 없었다."], ["But she knew that if he waked up he would look at her with cold eyes, convinced that he was right, and that before telling him of her love, she would have to prove to him that he had been wrong in his treatment of her.", "그러나 그녀는 알고 있었다, 그가 깨어나면 자기가 옳다고 확신한 채 차가운 눈으로 자기를 볼 것이며, 자기 사랑을 그에게 말하기 전에 먼저 그가 자기를 대한 방식이 잘못되었음을 그에게 증명해야 한다는 것을."], ["Without waking him, she went back, and after a second dose of opium she fell towards morning into a heavy, incomplete sleep, during which she never quite lost consciousness.", "그녀는 그를 깨우지 않고 돌아왔고, 아편을 두 번째로 마신 뒤 새벽녘에야 무겁고 어설픈 잠에 빠져들었는데, 그동안 의식을 완전히 잃은 적은 한 번도 없었다."]], [["In the morning she was waked by a horrible nightmare, which had recurred several times in her dreams, even before her connection with Vronsky.", "아침에 그녀는 끔찍한 악몽에 깨어났는데, 그것은 브론스키와 관계를 맺기 전에도 여러 번 꿈에서 되풀이되던 것이었다."], ["A little old man with unkempt beard was doing something bent down over some iron, muttering meaningless French words, and she, as she always did in this nightmare (it was what made the horror of it), felt that this peasant was taking no notice of her, but was doing something horrible with the iron—over her.", "헝클어진 수염을 한 자그마한 노인이 무슨 쇳덩이 위로 몸을 구부린 채 무언가를 하면서 뜻 모를 프랑스 말을 중얼거리고 있었고, 그녀는, 이 악몽에서 늘 그랬듯이(바로 그것이 악몽의 공포를 만들어 내는 것이었다), 이 농부가 자기에게는 아무 관심도 두지 않으면서 그 쇳덩이로 무언가 끔찍한 짓을 하고 있다고—자기 위에서 하고 있다고 느꼈다."], ["And she waked up in a cold sweat.", "그리고 그녀는 식은땀을 흘리며 깨어났다."]], [["When she got up, the previous day came back to her as though veiled in mist.", "그녀가 일어났을 때, 어제 일이 마치 안개에 가려진 듯 떠올랐다."]], [["“There was a quarrel.", "\"다툼이 있었지."], ["Just what has happened several times.", "여러 번 있었던 바로 그런 일이야."], ["I said I had a headache, and he did not come in to see me.", "나는 머리가 아프다고 했고, 그는 나를 보러 들어오지 않았어."], ["Tomorrow we’re going away; I must see him and get ready for the journey,”", "내일 우리는 떠나니까, 나는 그를 만나서 여행 준비를 해야 해.\""], ["she said to herself.", "그녀는 혼잣말을 했다."], ["And learning that he was in his study, she went down to him.", "그리고 그가 서재에 있다는 것을 알고는, 그에게로 내려갔다."], ["As she passed through the drawing-room she heard a carriage stop at the entrance, and looking out of the window she saw the carriage, from which a young girl in a lilac hat was leaning out giving some direction to the footman ringing the bell.", "응접실을 지나가는데 마차 한 대가 현관 앞에 멈추는 소리가 들렸고, 창밖을 내다보니 라일락빛 모자를 쓴 젊은 처녀가 마차에서 몸을 내밀고 초인종을 울리는 하인에게 무언가 지시를 하고 있었다."], ["After a parley in the hall, someone came upstairs, and Vronsky’s steps could be heard passing the drawing-room.", "현관에서 몇 마디 이야기가 오간 뒤, 누군가 위층으로 올라왔고, 응접실을 지나는 브론스키의 발소리가 들렸다."], ["He went rapidly downstairs.", "그는 빠르게 아래층으로 내려갔다."], ["Anna went again to the window.", "안나는 다시 창가로 갔다."], ["She saw him come out onto the steps without his hat and go up to the carriage.", "그녀는 그가 모자도 쓰지 않은 채 계단으로 나와 마차로 다가가는 것을 보았다."], ["The young girl in the lilac hat handed him a parcel.", "라일락빛 모자를 쓴 젊은 처녀가 그에게 꾸러미 하나를 건넸다."], ["Vronsky, smiling, said something to her.", "브론스키는 미소를 지으며 그녀에게 무언가 말했다."], ["The carriage drove away, he ran rapidly upstairs again.", "마차가 떠나갔고, 그는 다시 빠르게 위층으로 뛰어 올라왔다."]], [["The mists that had shrouded everything in her soul parted suddenly.", "그녀의 마음속 모든 것을 뒤덮고 있던 안개가 갑자기 걷혔다."], ["The feelings of yesterday pierced the sick heart with a fresh pang.", "어제의 감정들이 새로운 격통으로 병든 가슴을 찔렀다."], ["She could not understand now how she could have lowered herself by spending a whole day with him in his house.", "그녀는 어떻게 자기가 그의 집에서 그와 함께 하루를 통째로 보내며 그렇게까지 스스로를 낮출 수 있었는지 이제 이해할 수 없었다."], ["She went into his room to announce her determination.", "그녀는 자기 결심을 알리려고 그의 방으로 들어갔다."]], [["“That was Madame Sorokina and her daughter.", "\"소로키나 부인하고 그 딸이었소."], ["They came and brought me the money and the deeds from maman.", "와서 maman한테서 온 돈과 증서를 가져왔지."], ["I couldn’t get them yesterday.", "어제는 받을 수가 없었거든."], ["How is your head, better?”", "머리는 어떻소, 좀 나아졌소?\""], ["he said quietly, not wishing to see and to understand the gloomy and solemn expression of her face.", "그는 그녀의 얼굴에 어린 침울하고 엄숙한 표정을 보지도 이해하지도 않으려 하면서 조용히 말했다. * maman: 프랑스어로 '어머니'."]], [["She looked silently, intently at him, standing in the middle of the room.", "그녀는 방 한가운데 선 채, 말없이 그를 뚫어지게 바라보았다."], ["He glanced at her, frowned for a moment, and went on reading a letter.", "그는 그녀를 흘끗 보고는 한순간 미간을 찌푸렸다가, 다시 편지를 읽어 나갔다."], ["She turned, and went deliberately out of the room.", "그녀는 몸을 돌려 천천히 방을 나갔다."], ["He still might have turned her back, but she had reached the door, he was still silent, and the only sound audible was the rustling of the note paper as he turned it.", "그는 아직 그녀를 불러 세울 수도 있었지만, 그녀가 문에 이르렀는데도 그는 여전히 말이 없었고, 들리는 소리라고는 그가 편지지를 넘기며 내는 바스락거리는 소리뿐이었다."]], [["“Oh, by the way,”", "\"아, 그런데,\""], ["he said at the very moment she was in the doorway, “we’re going tomorrow for certain, aren’t we?”", "그녀가 막 문간에 섰을 때 그가 말했다. \"우리 내일 틀림없이 떠나는 거지, 그렇지?\""]], [["“You, but not I,”", "\"당신은 가지만, 나는 안 가요.\""], ["she said, turning round to him.", "그녀가 그에게로 돌아서며 말했다."]], [["“Anna, we can’t go on like this....”", "\"안나, 우리 이렇게 계속할 수는 없잖소……\""]], [["“You, but not I,”", "\"당신은 가지만, 나는 안 가요.\""], ["she repeated.", "그녀가 되풀이했다."]], [["“This is getting unbearable!”", "\"이건 정말 견딜 수가 없군!\""]], [["“You ...", "\"당신은……"], ["you will be sorry for this,”", "당신은 이 일을 후회하게 될 거예요.\""], ["she said, and went out.", "그녀는 이렇게 말하고 나가 버렸다."]], [["Frightened by the desperate expression with which these words were uttered, he jumped up and would have run after her, but on second thoughts he sat down and scowled, setting his teeth.", "이 말이 내뱉어진 그 절망적인 표정에 겁이 난 그는 벌떡 일어나 그녀를 뒤쫓아 가려 했지만, 다시 생각해 보고는 도로 앉아 이를 악물고 얼굴을 찌푸렸다."], ["This vulgar—as he thought it—threat of something vague exasperated him.", "무언가 막연한 것에 대한, 그가 보기에는 천박한 이 위협이 그를 격분시켰다."], ["“I’ve tried everything,”", "\"나는 모든 걸 다 해 봤어.\""], ["he thought; “the only thing left is not to pay attention,”", "그는 생각했다. \"남은 건 신경 쓰지 않는 것뿐이야.\""], ["and he began to get ready to drive into town, and again to his mother’s to get her signature to the deeds.", "그러고는 시내로, 다시 어머니에게로 가서 증서에 서명을 받을 채비를 하기 시작했다."]], [["She heard the sound of his steps about the study and the dining-room.", "그녀는 서재와 식당 쪽에서 그의 발소리를 들었다."], ["At the drawing-room he stood still.", "응접실에서 그는 멈춰 섰다."], ["But he did not turn in to see her, he merely gave an order that the horse should be given to Voytov if he came while he was away.", "그러나 그는 그녀를 보러 들르지 않고, 다만 자기가 없는 동안 보이토프가 오면 말을 내주라는 지시만 했다."], ["Then she heard the carriage brought round, the door opened, and he came out again.", "그러고 나서 그녀는 마차가 마련되는 소리를, 문이 열리는 소리를 들었고, 그가 다시 밖으로 나갔다."], ["But he went back into the porch again, and someone was running upstairs.", "그러나 그는 다시 현관으로 들어갔고, 누군가 위층으로 뛰어 올라가고 있었다."], ["It was the valet running up for his gloves that had been forgotten.", "잊고 간 그의 장갑을 가지러 뛰어 올라간 하인이었다."], ["She went to the window and saw him take the gloves without looking, and touching the coachman on the back he said something to him.", "그녀가 창가로 가 보니, 그는 쳐다보지도 않고 장갑을 받아 들고서, 마부의 등을 툭 치며 그에게 무언가 말하고 있었다."], ["Then without looking up at the window he settled himself in his usual attitude in the carriage, with his legs crossed, and drawing on his gloves he vanished round the corner.", "그러고는 창을 올려다보지도 않은 채 다리를 꼰 평소의 자세로 마차에 자리를 잡고 앉아, 장갑을 끼면서 모퉁이를 돌아 사라져 버렸다."]], [["# Chapter 27", "# Chapter 27"]], [["“He has gone!", "\"그가 가 버렸어!"], ["It is over!”", "끝난 거야!\""], ["Anna said to herself, standing at the window; and in answer to this statement the impression of the darkness when the candle had flickered out, and of her fearful dream mingling into one, filled her heart with cold terror.", "안나는 창가에 선 채 혼잣말을 했다. 그러자 이 말에 화답이라도 하듯, 촛불이 꺼졌을 때의 어둠과 그 무서운 꿈에 대한 인상이 하나로 뒤섞여 그녀의 가슴을 차디찬 공포로 가득 채웠다."]], [["“No, that cannot be!”", "\"아니야, 그럴 리 없어!\""], ["she cried, and crossing the room she rang the bell.", "그녀는 소리치며 방을 가로질러 가 종을 울렸다."], ["She was so afraid now of being alone, that without waiting for the servant to come in, she went out to meet him.", "이제 그녀는 혼자 있는 것이 너무도 두려워, 하인이 들어오기를 기다리지도 않고 그를 맞으러 나갔다."]], [["“Inquire where the count has gone,” she said.", "\"백작이 어디로 가셨는지 알아보게.\""], ["The servant answered that the count had gone to the stable.", "그녀가 말했다. 하인은 백작이 마구간으로 갔다고 대답했다."]], [["“His honor left word that if you cared to drive out, the carriage would be back immediately.”", "\"백작 나리께서 마님이 외출하고 싶어 하시면 마차를 곧 돌려보내겠다는 말씀을 남기셨습니다.\""]], [["“Very good.", "\"좋아."], ["Wait a minute.", "잠깐만 기다려."], ["I’ll write a note at once.", "지금 당장 쪽지를 쓸 테니까."], ["Send Mihail with the note to the stables.", "미하일에게 그 쪽지를 들려서 마구간으로 보내게."], ["Make haste.”", "어서 서둘러.\""]], [["She sat down and wrote:", "그녀는 자리에 앉아 글을 썼다."]], [["“I was wrong.", "\"제가 잘못했어요."], ["Come back home; I must explain.", "집으로 돌아와요. 제가 설명할게요."], ["For God’s sake come!", "제발 와요!"], ["I’m afraid.”", "무서워요.\""]], [["She sealed it up and gave it to the servant.", "그녀는 쪽지를 봉해 하인에게 건넸다."]], [["She was afraid of being left alone now; she followed the servant out of the room, and went to the nursery.", "이제 그녀는 혼자 남는 것이 두려워, 하인을 따라 방을 나와 아이 방으로 갔다."]], [["“Why, this isn’t it, this isn’t he!", "\"아니, 이게 아니야, 이 아이가 아니야!"], ["Where are his blue eyes, his sweet, shy smile?”", "그 애의 파란 눈은, 그 다정하고 수줍은 미소는 어디 있지?\""], ["was her first thought when she saw her chubby, rosy little girl with her black, curly hair instead of Seryozha, whom in the tangle of her ideas she had expected to see in the nursery.", "통통하고 발그레한 어린 딸아이가 검은 곱슬머리를 하고 있는 것을 보았을 때, 그녀의 머릿속에 가장 먼저 떠오른 생각이었다. 뒤죽박죽 엉킨 생각 속에서 그녀는 아이 방에서 세료자를 보리라고 기대했던 것이다."], ["The little girl sitting at the table was obstinately and violently battering on it with a cork, and staring aimlessly at her mother with her pitch-black eyes.", "식탁에 앉은 어린 딸아이는 고집스럽고 격렬하게 코르크 마개로 식탁을 두드려 대며, 칠흑같이 검은 눈으로 멍하니 어머니를 쳐다보고 있었다."], ["Answering the English nurse that she was quite well, and that she was going to the country tomorrow, Anna sat down by the little girl and began spinning the cork to show her.", "영국인 보모에게 자기는 아주 잘 지내며 내일 시골로 떠날 거라고 대답한 뒤, 안나는 어린 딸아이 곁에 앉아 코르크 마개를 돌려 아이에게 보여 주기 시작했다."], ["But the child’s loud, ringing laugh, and the motion of her eyebrows, recalled Vronsky so vividly that she got up hurriedly, restraining her sobs, and went away.", "그러나 아이의 크고 낭랑한 웃음소리와 눈썹을 움직이는 모습이 브론스키를 너무도 생생하게 떠오르게 해서, 그녀는 흐느낌을 억누르며 황급히 일어나 그 자리를 떠났다."], ["“Can it be all over?", "\"정말 모든 게 끝난 걸까?"], ["No, it cannot be!”", "아니야, 그럴 리 없어!\""], ["she thought.", "그녀는 생각했다."], ["“He will come back.", "\"그는 돌아올 거야."], ["But how can he explain that smile, that excitement after he had been talking to her?", "하지만 그가 그 여자와 이야기를 나눈 뒤에 지은 그 미소, 그 흥분을 어떻게 설명할 수 있을까?"], ["But even if he doesn’t explain, I will believe.", "하지만 그가 설명하지 못한다 해도, 나는 믿겠어."], ["If I don’t believe, there’s only one thing left for me, and I can’t.”", "내가 믿지 않는다면 내게 남은 건 단 한 가지뿐인데, 나는 그렇게 할 수 없어.\""]], [["She looked at her watch.", "그녀는 시계를 들여다보았다."], ["Twenty minutes had passed.", "이십 분이 지나 있었다."], ["“By now he has received the note and is coming back.", "\"지금쯤 그는 쪽지를 받고 돌아오는 길일 거야."], ["Not long, ten minutes more....", "오래 걸리지 않아, 십 분만 더 있으면 돼……"], ["But what if he doesn’t come?", "하지만 그가 오지 않는다면?"], ["No, that cannot be.", "아니야, 그럴 리 없어."], ["He mustn’t see me with tear-stained eyes.", "그가 눈물범벅이 된 내 얼굴을 봐서는 안 돼."], ["I’ll go and wash.", "가서 씻어야지."], ["Yes, yes; did I do my hair or not?”", "그래, 그래. 내가 머리를 매만졌던가, 안 매만졌던가?\""], ["she asked herself.", "그녀는 자문했다."], ["And she could not remember.", "도무지 기억이 나지 않았다."], ["She felt her head with her hand.", "그녀는 손으로 머리를 만져 보았다."], ["“Yes, my hair has been done, but when I did it I can’t in the least remember.”", "\"그래, 머리는 매만졌어, 그런데 언제 했는지는 도무지 떠오르지 않아.\""], ["She could not believe the evidence of her hand, and went up to the pier-glass to see whether she really had done her hair.", "그녀는 자기 손이 알려 주는 증거를 믿을 수 없어, 정말로 머리를 매만졌는지 확인하려고 체경 앞으로 다가갔다."], ["She certainly had, but she could not think when she had done it.", "분명 머리는 매만져져 있었지만, 언제 했는지는 도통 생각이 나지 않았다."], ["“Who’s that?”", "\"저게 누구지?\""], ["she thought, looking in the looking-glass at the swollen face with strangely glittering eyes, that looked in a scared way at her.", "그녀는 거울에 비친, 이상하게 번득이는 눈으로 겁에 질린 듯 자기를 바라보는 부어오른 얼굴을 들여다보며 생각했다."], ["“Why, it’s I!”", "\"아니, 나잖아!\""], ["she suddenly understood, and looking round, she seemed all at once to feel his kisses on her, and twitched her shoulders, shuddering.", "그녀는 문득 깨달았고, 주위를 둘러보다가 별안간 그의 입맞춤이 온몸에 느껴지는 듯해, 몸서리를 치며 어깨를 떨었다."], ["Then she lifted her hand to her lips and kissed it.", "그러다 손을 들어 입술에 대고 입을 맞추었다."]], [["“What is it?", "\"이게 뭐람?"], ["Why, I’m going out of my mind!”", "이런, 내가 미쳐 가고 있어!\""], ["and she went into her bedroom, where Annushka was tidying the room.", "그녀는 안누슈카가 방을 정돈하고 있는 침실로 들어갔다."]], [["“Annushka,”", "\"안누슈카.\""], ["she said, coming to a standstill before her, and she stared at the maid, not knowing what to say to her.", "그녀는 그녀 앞에 우뚝 멈춰 서서 말했다. 그러고는 무슨 말을 해야 할지 몰라 하녀를 빤히 바라보았다."]], [["“You meant to go and see Darya Alexandrovna,”", "\"다리아 알렉산드로브나를 뵈러 가실 생각이셨잖아요.\""], ["said the girl, as though she understood.", "하녀가 마치 그 마음을 알아채기라도 한 듯 말했다."]], [["“Darya Alexandrovna?", "\"다리아 알렉산드로브나?"], ["Yes, I’ll go.”", "그래, 갈 거야.\""]], [["“Fifteen minutes there, fifteen minutes back.", "\"가는 데 십오 분, 오는 데 십오 분."], ["He’s coming, he’ll be here soon.”", "그는 오고 있어, 곧 여기 올 거야.\""], ["She took out her watch and looked at it.", "그녀는 시계를 꺼내 들여다보았다."], ["“But how could he go away, leaving me in such a state?", "\"그런데 어떻게 나를 이런 상태로 내버려 둔 채 가 버릴 수가 있지?"], ["How can he live, without making it up with me?”", "나와 화해하지도 않고 어떻게 살아갈 수 있단 말이야?\""], ["She went to the window and began looking into the street.", "그녀는 창가로 가서 거리를 내다보기 시작했다."], ["Judging by the time, he might be back now.", "시간으로 보아 이제 그가 돌아왔을 법도 했다."], ["But her calculations might be wrong, and she began once more to recall when he had started and to count the minutes.", "그러나 자기 계산이 틀렸을지도 모른다는 생각에, 그녀는 다시금 그가 언제 출발했는지를 떠올리며 분 단위로 시간을 헤아리기 시작했다."]], [["At the moment when she had moved away to the big clock to compare it with her watch, someone drove up.", "그녀가 회중시계와 맞춰 보려고 큰 시계 쪽으로 자리를 옮긴 바로 그 순간, 누군가 마차를 타고 다가왔다."], ["Glancing out of the window, she saw his carriage.", "창밖을 흘끗 내다본 그녀는 그의 마차를 보았다."], ["But no one came upstairs, and voices could be heard below.", "그러나 아무도 위층으로 올라오지 않았고, 아래쪽에서 사람들 목소리가 들렸다."], ["It was the messenger who had come back in the carriage.", "마차를 타고 돌아온 것은 심부름꾼이었다."], ["She went down to him.", "그녀는 그에게로 내려갔다."]], [["“We didn’t catch the count.", "\"백작님을 만나지 못했습니다."], ["The count had driven off on the lower city road.”", "백작님께서는 아래쪽 시내 길로 떠나셨더군요.\""]], [["“What do you say?", "\"뭐라고?"], ["What!...”", "뭐라고!……\""], ["she said to the rosy, good-humored Mihail, as he handed her back her note.", "그녀는 발그레하고 사람 좋아 보이는 미하일이 쪽지를 돌려주자 그에게 말했다."]], [["“Why, then, he has never received it!”", "\"그렇다면 그는 이걸 받아 보지도 못한 거잖아!\""], ["she thought.", "그녀는 생각했다."]], [["“Go with this note to Countess Vronskaya’s place, you know?", "\"이 쪽지를 가지고 브론스카야 백작 부인 댁으로 가게, 알겠지?"], ["and bring an answer back immediately,”", "그리고 곧장 답장을 받아 오게나.\""], ["she said to the messenger.", "그녀가 심부름꾼에게 말했다."]], [["“And I, what am I going to do?”", "\"그럼 나는, 나는 어떻게 하지?\""], ["she thought.", "그녀는 생각했다."], ["“Yes, I’m going to Dolly’s, that’s true or else I shall go out of my mind.", "\"그래, 돌리네 집으로 가는 거야, 정말이지 그러지 않으면 미쳐 버릴 거야."], ["Yes, and I can telegraph, too.”", "그래, 전보를 칠 수도 있고.\""], ["And she wrote a telegram.", "그러고 그녀는 전보를 썼다."], ["“I absolutely must talk to you; come at once.”", "\"당신과 꼭 이야기해야 해요. 당장 와요.\""], ["After sending off the telegram, she went to dress.", "전보를 부친 뒤 그녀는 옷을 갈아입으러 갔다."], ["When she was dressed and in her hat, she glanced again into the eyes of the plump, comfortable-looking Annushka.", "옷을 다 차려입고 모자까지 쓴 다음, 그녀는 통통하고 편안해 보이는 안누슈카의 눈을 다시 한번 들여다보았다."], ["There was unmistakable sympathy in those good-natured little gray eyes.", "그 선량한 작은 잿빛 눈에는 틀림없는 연민이 어려 있었다."]], [["“Annushka, dear, what am I to do?”", "\"안누슈카, 나는 어쩌면 좋지?\""], ["said Anna, sobbing and sinking helplessly into a chair.", "안나는 흐느끼며 맥없이 의자에 주저앉았다."]], [["“Why fret yourself so, Anna Arkadyevna?", "\"왜 그렇게 마음을 졸이세요, 안나 아르카지예브나?"], ["Why, there’s nothing out of the way.", "무슨 별난 일이 일어난 것도 아니잖아요."], ["You drive out a little, and it’ll cheer you up,”", "바람이라도 좀 쐬고 오시면 기분이 나아지실 거예요.\""], ["said the maid.", "하녀가 말했다."]], [["“Yes, I’m going,”", "\"그래, 가야지.\""], ["said Anna, rousing herself and getting up.", "안나는 정신을 차리고 일어서며 말했다."], ["“And if there’s a telegram while I’m away, send it on to Darya Alexandrovna’s ...", "\"그리고 내가 없는 동안 전보가 오거든, 다리아 알렉산드로브나 댁으로 보내 줘……"], ["but no, I shall be back myself.”", "아니, 내가 직접 돌아올 테니까.\""]], [["“Yes, I mustn’t think, I must do something, drive somewhere, and most of all, get out of this house,”", "\"그래, 생각을 하면 안 돼, 무언가를 해야 해, 어디로든 마차를 타고 나가야 해, 그리고 무엇보다도 이 집에서 벗어나야 해.\""], ["she said, feeling with terror the strange turmoil going on in her own heart, and she made haste to go out and get into the carriage.", "그녀는 자기 가슴속에서 일어나는 기이한 동요를 공포에 질려 느끼며 말했고, 서둘러 밖으로 나가 마차에 올라탔다."]], [["“Where to?”", "\"어디로 모실까요?\""], ["asked Pyotr before getting onto the box.", "마부석에 오르기 전 표트르가 물었다."]], [["“To Znamenka, the Oblonskys’.”", "\"즈나멘카의 오블론스키 댁으로.\""]], [["# Chapter 28", "# Chapter 28"]], [["It was bright and sunny.", "햇살이 밝게 빛나는 화창한 날이었다."], ["A fine rain had been falling all the morning, and now it had not long cleared up.", "아침 내내 가랑비가 내렸는데, 막 갠 지 얼마 되지 않았다."], ["The iron roofs, the flags of the roads, the flints of the pavements, the wheels and leather, the brass and the tinplate of the carriages—all glistened brightly in the May sunshine.", "철제 지붕이며, 길에 깔린 포석이며, 보도의 부싯돌이며, 마차의 바퀴와 가죽이며, 놋쇠와 양철이며—이 모두가 5월의 햇살 속에서 환하게 반짝였다."], ["It was three o’clock, and the very liveliest time in the streets.", "오후 세 시, 거리가 가장 활기를 띠는 시각이었다."]], [["As she sat in a corner of the comfortable carriage, that hardly swayed on its supple springs, while the grays trotted swiftly, in the midst of the unceasing rattle of wheels and the changing impressions in the pure air, Anna ran over the events of the last days, and she saw her position quite differently from how it had seemed at home.", "탄력 있는 용수철 위에서 거의 흔들리지도 않는 안락한 마차 한구석에 앉아, 잿빛 말들이 경쾌하게 달리는 가운데 끊임없이 덜컹거리는 바퀴 소리와 맑은 공기 속에서 시시각각 바뀌는 인상에 둘러싸인 채, 안나는 지난 며칠의 일들을 되짚어 보았고, 집에서 보였던 것과는 사뭇 다르게 자기 처지를 바라보았다."], ["Now the thought of death seemed no longer so terrible and so clear to her, and death itself no longer seemed so inevitable.", "이제 죽음에 대한 생각은 더 이상 그토록 끔찍하고 또렷하게 다가오지 않았고, 죽음 자체도 더 이상 그토록 피할 수 없는 것으로 여겨지지 않았다."], ["Now she blamed herself for the humiliation to which she had lowered herself.", "이제 그녀는 자기 자신을 그토록 비참하게 낮춘 굴욕에 대해 스스로를 책망했다."], ["“I entreat him to forgive me.", "'내가 그이에게 용서해 달라고 애원했어."], ["I have given in to him.", "내가 그이에게 굴복했어."], ["I have owned myself in fault.", "내가 잘못했다고 인정했어. 무엇 때문에?"], ["What for? Can’t I live without him?”", "내가 그이 없이는 살 수 없단 말인가?'"], ["And leaving unanswered the question how she was going to live without him, she fell to reading the signs on the shops.", "그러면서 그이 없이 어떻게 살아갈 것인가 하는 물음에는 답을 내리지 못한 채, 그녀는 상점들의 간판을 읽기 시작했다."], ["“Office and warehouse.", "'사무소 겸 창고."], ["Dental surgeon.", "치과 의사."], ["Yes, I’ll tell Dolly all about it.", "그래, 돌리한테 다 털어놓을 거야."], ["She doesn’t like Vronsky.", "그 애는 브론스키를 싫어하지."], ["I shall be sick and ashamed, but I’ll tell her.", "괴롭고 부끄럽겠지만, 그래도 말할 거야."], ["She loves me, and I’ll follow her advice.", "그 애는 날 사랑하니까, 그 애 충고를 따를 거야."], ["I won’t give in to him; I won’t let him train me as he pleases.", "그이한테 굴복하지 않을 거야. 자기 멋대로 날 길들이게 두지 않겠어."], ["Filippov, bun shop.", "필리포프, 빵집."], ["They say they send their dough to Petersburg.", "사람들 말로는 저 집 반죽을 페테르부르크로 보낸다지."], ["The Moscow water is so good for it.", "모스크바 물이 빵에 그렇게 좋다는군."], ["Ah, the springs at Mitishtchen, and the pancakes!”", "아, 미티시첸의 샘물, 그리고 그 팬케이크!'"]], [["And she remembered how, long, long ago, when she was a girl of seventeen, she had gone with her aunt to Troitsa.", "그러다 그녀는 아주 오래전, 자기가 열일곱 살 처녀였을 때 고모와 함께 트로이차에 갔던 일을 떠올렸다."], ["“Riding, too.", "'그땐 말도 탔지."], ["Was that really me, with red hands?", "손이 빨갰던 게 정말 나였나?"], ["How much that seemed to me then splendid and out of reach has become worthless, while what I had then has gone out of my reach forever!", "그때는 그렇게 근사하고 손 닿지 않을 것처럼 보였던 게 이제는 하찮아져 버렸는데, 그때 내가 가졌던 것들은 영영 내 손이 닿지 않는 곳으로 가 버렸어!"], ["Could I ever have believed then that I could come to such humiliation?", "그때 내가 이런 굴욕을 겪게 되리라고 상상이나 할 수 있었을까?"], ["How conceited and self-satisfied he will be when he gets my note!", "내 쪽지를 받으면 그이가 얼마나 우쭐대고 흡족해할까!"], ["But I will show him....", "하지만 본때를 보여 주겠어……"], ["How horrid that paint smells!", "저 페인트 냄새는 또 왜 저렇게 지독한지!"], ["Why is it they’re always painting and building?", "어째서 사람들은 늘 칠을 하고 짓고 하는 걸까?'"], ["Modes et robes, she read.", "Modes et robes, 그녀가 읽었다."], ["A man bowed to her.", "한 남자가 그녀에게 인사를 했다."], ["It was Annushka’s husband.", "안누슈카의 남편이었다."], ["“Our parasites”; she remembered how Vronsky had said that. “Our?", "'우리 식객들', 그녀는 브론스키가 그렇게 말했던 것을 떠올렸다."], ["Why our?", "'우리?"], ["What’s so awful is that one can’t tear up the past by its roots.", "어째서 우리지? 정말 끔찍한 건 과거를 뿌리째 뽑아 버릴 수 없다는 거야."], ["One can’t tear it out, but one can hide one’s memory of it.", "뽑아낼 수는 없어도, 그것에 대한 기억은 감출 수 있지."], ["And I’ll hide it.”", "그래, 감춰 버릴 거야.'"], ["And then she thought of her past with Alexey Alexandrovitch, of how she had blotted the memory of it out of her life.", "그러고는 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치와의 과거를, 자기가 어떻게 그 기억을 인생에서 지워 버렸는지를 떠올렸다."], ["“Dolly will think I’m leaving my second husband, and so I certainly must be in the wrong.", "'돌리는 내가 두 번째 남편을 떠나는 거라고 생각하겠지, 그러니 분명 내가 잘못한 거라고 여길 거야."], ["As if I cared to be right!", "마치 내가 옳고 싶어 안달이라도 난 것처럼!"], ["I can’t help it!”", "어쩔 수 없는걸!'"], ["she said, and she wanted to cry.", "그녀는 그렇게 중얼거렸고, 울고 싶어졌다."], ["But at once she fell to wondering what those two girls could be smiling about.", "하지만 곧 저 두 처녀가 무엇 때문에 저렇게 웃고 있을까 궁금해졌다."], ["“Love, most likely.", "'아마 사랑이겠지."], ["They don’t know how dreary it is, how low....", "저 애들은 그게 얼마나 따분하고, 얼마나 천한 것인지 몰라……"], ["The boulevard and the children.", "가로수 길과 아이들."], ["Three boys running, playing at horses.", "사내아이 셋이 말놀이를 하며 뛰어다니네."], ["Seryozha!", "세료자!"], ["And I’m losing everything and not getting him back.", "나는 모든 것을 잃어 가는데, 그 아이는 되찾지 못하는구나."], ["Yes, I’m losing everything, if he doesn’t return.", "그래, 그이가 돌아오지 않으면 나는 모든 걸 잃는 거야."], ["Perhaps he was late for the train and has come back by now.", "어쩌면 기차를 놓치는 바람에 지금쯤 돌아와 있을지도 몰라."], ["Longing for humiliation again!”", "또 굴욕을 갈망하고 있구나!'"], ["she said to herself.", "그녀는 혼잣말을 했다."], ["“No, I’ll go to Dolly, and say straight out to her, I’m unhappy, I deserve this, I’m to blame, but still I’m unhappy, help me.", "'아니, 돌리한테 가서 바로 말해야지. 나는 불행해, 이런 꼴을 당해도 싸, 내 잘못이야, 그래도 나는 불행하니까, 도와줘."], ["These horses, this carriage—how loathsome I am to myself in this carriage—all his; but I won’t see them again.”", "이 말들, 이 마차—이 마차 안에 앉아 있는 내가 나 자신에게 얼마나 역겨운지—전부 그이 거야. 하지만 다시는 이것들을 보지 않을 거야.'"]], [["Thinking over the words in which she would tell Dolly, and mentally working her heart up to great bitterness, Anna went upstairs.", "돌리에게 어떻게 말할 것인지 그 말들을 곱씹으며, 마음속으로 자기 가슴을 크나큰 비통함으로 몰아세우면서, 안나는 계단을 올라갔다."]], [["“Is there anyone with her?”", "\"부인께 손님이 와 계신가요?\""], ["she asked in the hall.", "그녀가 현관에서 물었다."]], [["“Katerina Alexandrovna Levin,”", "\"카체리나 알렉산드로브나 레비나 마님이 와 계십니다.\""], ["answered the footman.", "하인이 대답했다."]], [["“Kitty!", "'키티!"], ["Kitty, whom Vronsky was in love with!”", "브론스키가 사랑했던 그 키티!'"], ["thought Anna, “the girl he thinks of with love.", "안나는 생각했다. '그이가 사랑을 품고 떠올리는 처녀."], ["He’s sorry he didn’t marry her.", "그이는 그 애와 결혼하지 않은 걸 후회하지."], ["But me he thinks of with hatred, and is sorry he had anything to do with me.”", "그런데 나는 증오를 품고 떠올리고, 나와 엮인 것을 후회하고 있어.'"]], [["The sisters were having a consultation about nursing when Anna called.", "안나가 찾아왔을 때 자매는 육아에 관해 상의하고 있었다."], ["Dolly went down alone to see the visitor who had interrupted their conversation.", "돌리는 둘의 대화를 끊은 손님을 맞으려고 혼자 아래층으로 내려왔다."]], [["“Well, so you’ve not gone away yet?", "\"어머, 그래 아직 안 떠났구나?"], ["I meant to have come to you,”", "내가 너를 찾아가려고 했는데.\""], ["she said; “I had a letter from Stiva today.”", "그녀가 말했다. \"오늘 스티바한테서 편지가 왔어.\""]], [["“We had a telegram too,”", "\"우리도 전보를 받았어요.\""], ["answered Anna, looking round for Kitty.", "안나는 키티를 찾아 주위를 둘러보며 대답했다."]], [["“He writes that he can’t make out quite what Alexey Alexandrovitch wants, but he won’t go away without a decisive answer.”", "\"알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 정확히 뭘 원하는지 도무지 모르겠다고 쓰긴 했는데, 확실한 답을 듣기 전엔 떠나지 않겠대.\""]], [["“I thought you had someone with you.", "\"누군가 와 있는 것 같던데요."], ["Can I see the letter?”", "편지를 봐도 될까요?\""]], [["“Yes; Kitty,”", "\"그래, 키티야.\""], ["said Dolly, embarrassed.", "돌리가 당황하며 말했다."], ["“She stayed in the nursery.", "\"그 애는 아이 방에 있어."], ["She has been very ill.”", "한동안 많이 아팠거든.\""]], [["“So I heard.", "\"그렇다고 들었어요."], ["May I see the letter?”", "편지를 봐도 될까요?\""]], [["“I’ll get it directly.", "\"금방 가져올게."], ["But he doesn’t refuse; on the contrary, Stiva has hopes,”", "그래도 그이가 거절하는 건 아니야. 오히려 스티바는 희망을 품고 있어.\""], ["said Dolly, stopping in the doorway.", "돌리가 문간에 멈춰 서서 말했다."]], [["“I haven’t, and indeed I don’t wish it,”", "\"저는 희망이 없어요. 사실 바라지도 않고요.\""], ["said Anna.", "안나가 말했다."]], [["“What’s this?", "'이게 뭐람?"], ["Does Kitty consider it degrading to meet me?”", "키티가 나를 만나는 걸 굴욕으로 여기는 걸까?'"], ["thought Anna when she was alone.", "혼자 남게 되자 안나는 생각했다."], ["“Perhaps she’s right, too.", "'어쩌면 그 애 말도 맞아."], ["But it’s not for her, the girl who was in love with Vronsky, it’s not for her to show me that, even if it is true.", "하지만 그 애가, 브론스키를 사랑했던 그 처녀가 나한테 그런 티를 낼 자격은 없지, 설령 그게 사실이라 해도."], ["I know that in my position I can’t be received by any decent woman.", "내 처지에서는 점잖은 여자라면 누구도 나를 받아들이지 않으리란 걸 나도 알아."], ["I knew that from the first moment I sacrificed everything to him.", "그이에게 모든 걸 바친 그 첫 순간부터 알고 있었어."], ["And this is my reward! Oh, how I hate him!", "그런데 이게 나한테 돌아온 보답이라니!"], ["And what did I come here for?", "아, 내가 그이를 얼마나 미워하는지!"], ["I’m worse here, more miserable.”", "게다가 내가 여긴 뭐하러 온 걸까?"], ["She heard from the next room the sisters’ voices in consultation.", "여기 오니 더 비참하고, 더 불행해.' 그녀는 옆방에서 상의하는 자매의 목소리를 들었다."], ["“And what am I going to say to Dolly now?", "'그래서 이제 내가 돌리한테 무슨 말을 한단 말이야?"], ["Amuse Kitty by the sight of my wretchedness, submit to her patronizing?", "내 비참한 꼴을 보여 줘서 키티를 즐겁게 해 주고, 그 애의 동정 어린 시혜를 받으라고?"], ["No; and besides, Dolly wouldn’t understand.", "안 돼. 게다가 돌리는 이해하지도 못할 거야."], ["And it would be no good my telling her.", "말해 봐야 아무 소용없어."], ["It would only be interesting to see Kitty, to show her how I despise everyone and everything, how nothing matters to me now.”", "그저 키티를 보는 건 흥미롭겠지, 내가 모든 사람과 모든 것을 얼마나 경멸하는지, 이제 나한테는 아무것도 중요하지 않다는 걸 그 애한테 보여 주는 건.'"]], [["Dolly came in with the letter.", "돌리가 편지를 들고 들어왔다."], ["Anna read it and handed it back in silence.", "안나는 그것을 읽고는 말없이 돌려주었다."]], [["“I knew all that,”", "\"다 알고 있던 내용이에요.\""], ["she said, “and it doesn’t interest me in the least.”", "그녀가 말했다. \"조금도 관심 없어요.\""]], [["“Oh, why so?", "\"어머, 왜 그래?"], ["On the contrary, I have hopes,”", "오히려 난 희망이 있는데.\""], ["said Dolly, looking inquisitively at Anna.", "돌리가 안나를 캐묻듯 바라보며 말했다."], ["She had never seen her in such a strangely irritable condition.", "그녀는 안나가 이토록 묘하게 신경이 곤두선 모습을 본 적이 없었다."], ["“When are you going away?”", "\"언제 떠나니?\""], ["she asked.", "그녀가 물었다."]], [["Anna, half-closing her eyes, looked straight before her and did not answer.", "안나는 눈을 반쯤 감은 채 앞만 똑바로 바라볼 뿐 대답하지 않았다."]], [["“Why does Kitty shrink from me?”", "\"키티는 왜 나를 피하는 거예요?\""], ["she said, looking at the door and flushing red.", "그녀는 문 쪽을 바라보며 얼굴을 붉히고 말했다."]], [["“Oh, what nonsense!", "\"어머, 무슨 그런 소릴!"], ["She’s nursing, and things aren’t going right with her, and I’ve been advising her....", "그 애는 젖을 먹이고 있는데 일이 잘 안 풀려서, 내가 조언을 해 주던 참이야……"], ["She’s delighted.", "그 애도 무척 반가워해."], ["She’ll be here in a minute,”", "곧 내려올 거야.\""], ["said Dolly awkwardly, not clever at lying.", "거짓말에 서툰 돌리가 어색하게 말했다."], ["“Yes, here she is.”", "\"그래, 저기 오는구나.\""]], [["Hearing that Anna had called, Kitty had wanted not to appear, but Dolly persuaded her.", "안나가 찾아왔다는 말을 듣고 키티는 모습을 보이고 싶지 않았지만, 돌리가 설득했다."], ["Rallying her forces, Kitty went in, walked up to her, blushing, and shook hands.", "키티는 마음을 다잡고 들어와, 얼굴을 붉히며 안나에게 다가가 악수를 했다."]], [["“I am so glad to see you,”", "\"이렇게 뵙게 되어 정말 기뻐요.\""], ["she said with a trembling voice.", "그녀가 떨리는 목소리로 말했다."]], [["Kitty had been thrown into confusion by the inward conflict between her antagonism to this bad woman and her desire to be nice to her.", "키티는 이 나쁜 여자에 대한 반감과 그녀에게 다정하게 대하고 싶은 마음 사이의 내적 갈등으로 혼란에 빠져 있었다."], ["But as soon as she saw Anna’s lovely and attractive face, all feeling of antagonism disappeared.", "하지만 안나의 사랑스럽고 매혹적인 얼굴을 보는 순간, 반감의 감정이 모두 사라져 버렸다."]], [["“I should not have been surprised if you had not cared to meet me.", "\"나를 만나고 싶지 않았다 해도 놀라지 않았을 거예요."], ["I’m used to everything.", "나는 어떤 일에도 익숙하니까."], ["You have been ill?", "그동안 아팠다고요?"], ["Yes, you are changed,”", "그래요, 좀 변했네요.\""], ["said Anna.", "안나가 말했다."]], [["Kitty felt that Anna was looking at her with hostile eyes.", "키티는 안나가 적의 어린 눈으로 자기를 바라보고 있다고 느꼈다."], ["She ascribed this hostility to the awkward position in which Anna, who had once patronized her, must feel with her now, and she felt sorry for her.", "그녀는 그 적의를, 한때 자기를 시혜하듯 대하던 안나가 이제 자기 앞에서 느낄 수밖에 없는 어색한 처지 탓으로 여겼고, 그녀가 가엾게 느껴졌다."]], [["They talked of Kitty’s illness, of the baby, of Stiva, but it was obvious that nothing interested Anna.", "그들은 키티의 병에 대해, 아기에 대해, 스티바에 대해 이야기했지만, 안나는 어느 것에도 관심이 없다는 게 분명했다."]], [["“I came to say good-bye to you,”", "\"작별 인사를 하러 왔어요.\""], ["she said, getting up.", "그녀가 자리에서 일어서며 말했다."]], [["“Oh, when are you going?”", "\"어머, 언제 떠나는데?\""]], [["But again not answering, Anna turned to Kitty.", "하지만 이번에도 대답하지 않고, 안나는 키티에게로 몸을 돌렸다."]], [["“Yes, I am very glad to have seen you,”", "\"그래요, 당신을 만나게 되어 정말 기뻐요.\""], ["she said with a smile.", "그녀가 미소를 지으며 말했다."], ["“I have heard so much of you from everyone, even from your husband.", "\"여러 사람한테, 당신 남편한테까지 당신 이야기를 참 많이 들었어요."], ["He came to see me, and I liked him exceedingly,”", "그분이 나를 찾아왔었는데, 나는 그분이 무척 마음에 들었어요.\""], ["she said, unmistakably with malicious intent.", "그녀는 분명 악의를 품고 말했다."], ["“Where is he?”", "\"그분은 어디 계신가요?\""]], [["“He has gone back to the country,”", "\"시골로 돌아갔어요.\""], ["said Kitty, blushing.", "키티가 얼굴을 붉히며 말했다."]], [["“Remember me to him, be sure you do.”", "\"그분께 안부 전해 줘요, 꼭이요.\""]], [["“I’ll be sure to!”", "\"꼭 전할게요!\""], ["Kitty said naïvely, looking compassionately into her eyes.", "키티는 동정 어린 눈으로 안나의 눈을 들여다보며 순진하게 말했다."]], [["“So good-bye, Dolly.”", "\"그럼 잘 있어, 돌리.\""], ["And kissing Dolly and shaking hands with Kitty, Anna went out hurriedly.", "그리고 돌리에게 입을 맞추고 키티와 악수를 한 뒤, 안나는 서둘러 나가 버렸다."]], [["“She’s just the same and just as charming!", "\"여전하시네, 여전히 매력적이셔!"], ["She’s very lovely!”", "정말 아름다운 분이야!\""], ["said Kitty, when she was alone with her sister.", "언니와 단둘이 남게 되자 키티가 말했다."], ["“But there’s something piteous about her.", "\"그런데 어딘가 가여운 데가 있어."], ["Awfully piteous!”", "너무나 가여워!\""]], [["“Yes, there’s something unusual about her today,”", "\"그래, 오늘 저 애한테 뭔가 심상치 않은 데가 있어.\""], ["said Dolly.", "돌리가 말했다."], ["“When I went with her into the hall, I fancied she was almost crying.”", "\"내가 같이 현관까지 나갔을 때, 거의 울고 있는 것 같았거든.\""]], [["# Chapter 29", "# Chapter 29"]], [["Anna got into the carriage again in an even worse frame of mind than when she set out from home.", "안나는 집에서 나설 때보다 한층 더 나쁜 심정으로 다시 마차에 올랐다."], ["To her previous tortures was added now that sense of mortification and of being an outcast which she had felt so distinctly on meeting Kitty.", "이전의 고통에 더해, 키티를 만났을 때 그토록 또렷하게 느꼈던, 모욕당하고 버림받은 듯한 그 감각까지 더해진 것이다."]], [["“Where to?", "\"어디로 모실까요?"], ["Home?”", "댁으로 가나요?\""], ["asked Pyotr.", "표트르가 물었다."]], [["“Yes, home,”", "\"그래, 집으로.\""], ["she said, not even thinking now where she was going.", "그녀는 자기가 어디로 가는지조차 생각하지 않고 대답했다."]], [["“How they looked at me as something dreadful, incomprehensible, and curious!", "'그 사람들은 나를 마치 무섭고 알 수 없고 신기한 것이라도 되는 양 쳐다봤어."], ["What can he be telling the other with such warmth?”", "저 사람은 무슨 얘기를 저렇게 열을 올려 떠들어 대는 걸까?'"], ["she thought, staring at two men who walked by.", "그녀는 곁을 지나가는 두 남자를 응시하며 생각했다."], ["“Can one ever tell anyone what one is feeling?", "'자기가 느끼는 걸 누군가에게 말할 수 있기나 한 걸까?"], ["I meant to tell Dolly, and it’s a good thing I didn’t tell her.", "돌리한테 말하려고 했는데, 말하지 않은 게 잘한 일이야."], ["How pleased she would have been at my misery!", "그 여자는 내 불행을 얼마나 고소해했을까!"], ["She would have concealed it, but her chief feeling would have been delight at my being punished for the happiness she envied me for.", "겉으로는 감췄겠지만, 마음속 깊은 데서는, 그녀가 나에게 부러워하던 그 행복 때문에 내가 벌을 받는다는 사실에 기뻐했을 거야."], ["Kitty, she would have been even more pleased.", "키티는, 그 애는 더 고소해했겠지."], ["How I can see through her!", "나는 그 애 속을 훤히 들여다볼 수 있어!"], ["She knows I was more than usually sweet to her husband.", "그 애는 내가 자기 남편한테 평소보다 더 상냥하게 굴었다는 걸 알아."], ["And she’s jealous and hates me.", "그래서 질투하고 나를 미워하는 거야."], ["And she despises me.", "게다가 나를 경멸하지."], ["In her eyes I’m an immoral woman.", "그 애 눈에 나는 부도덕한 여자야."], ["If I were an immoral woman I could have made her husband fall in love with me ... if I’d cared to.", "내가 정말 부도덕한 여자였다면, 그 애 남편이 나한테 반하게 만들 수도 있었어……마음만 먹었다면 말이야."], ["And, indeed, I did care to.", "사실 마음먹기도 했고.'"], ["There’s someone who’s pleased with himself,” she thought, as she saw a fat, rubicund gentleman coming towards her.", "저기 자기 자신에게 만족스러워하는 사람이 하나 있군, 하고 그녀는 뚱뚱하고 혈색 좋은 신사가 자기 쪽으로 걸어오는 것을 보며 생각했다."], ["He took her for an acquaintance, and lifted his glossy hat above his bald, glossy head, and then perceived his mistake.", "그는 그녀를 아는 사람으로 착각하고는 번들거리는 대머리 위로 반들반들한 모자를 들어 올렸다가, 곧 자기 실수를 깨달았다."], ["“He thought he knew me.", "'저 사람은 나를 안다고 생각했지."], ["Well, he knows me as well as anyone in the world knows me.", "글쎄, 저 사람도 이 세상 누구만큼이나 나를 알아."], ["I don’t know myself.", "나 자신도 나를 모르는데."], ["I know my appetites, as the French say.", "프랑스 사람들 말마따나 나는 내 욕망만 알 뿐이지."], ["They want that dirty ice cream, that they do know for certain,”", "저 애들은 저 더러운 아이스크림이 먹고 싶은 거고, 그것만큼은 확실히 아는 거야.'"], ["she thought, looking at two boys stopping an ice cream seller, who took a barrel off his head and began wiping his perspiring face with a towel.", "그녀는 아이스크림 장수를 붙잡아 세운 두 사내아이를 바라보며 생각했다. 장수는 머리에서 통을 내려놓고 수건으로 땀에 젖은 얼굴을 닦기 시작했다."], ["“We all want what is sweet and nice.", "'우리는 모두 달콤하고 기분 좋은 걸 원해."], ["If not sweetmeats, then a dirty ice.", "사탕이 아니면 더러운 아이스크림이라도."], ["And Kitty’s the same—if not Vronsky, then Levin.", "키티도 마찬가지야. 브론스키가 아니면 레빈이지."], ["And she envies me, and hates me.", "그 애는 나를 부러워하고, 또 미워해."], ["And we all hate each other.", "우리는 모두 서로 미워해."], ["I Kitty, Kitty me.", "나는 키티를, 키티는 나를."], ["Yes, that’s the truth.", "그래, 그게 진실이야."], ["‘Tiutkin, coiffeur.’ Je me fais coiffer par Tiutkin....", "Tiutkin, coiffeur. Je me fais coiffer par Tiutkin……"], ["I’ll tell him that when he comes,”", "그 사람이 오면 이 얘기를 해 줘야지.'"], ["she thought and smiled.", "그녀는 그렇게 생각하며 미소 지었다."], ["But the same instant she remembered that she had no one now to tell anything amusing to.", "하지만 바로 그 순간 그녀는 이제 자기에게는 재미있는 이야기를 들려줄 상대가 아무도 없다는 사실을 떠올렸다."], ["“And there’s nothing amusing, nothing mirthful, really.", "'게다가 재미있는 것도, 즐거운 것도 아무것도 없어, 사실은."], ["It’s all hateful.", "모든 게 다 가증스러워."], ["They’re singing for vespers, and how carefully that merchant crosses himself!", "저녁 기도를 위해 노래를 부르고 있고, 저 상인은 또 얼마나 정성스레 성호를 긋는지!"], ["as if he were afraid of missing something.", "마치 뭔가 놓칠까 봐 두려워하기라도 하는 것처럼."], ["Why these churches and this singing and this humbug?", "이 교회들이며 이 노래며 이 가식은 다 뭐 때문이지?"], ["Simply to conceal that we all hate each other like these cab drivers who are abusing each other so angrily.", "그저 우리가 모두 서로 미워한다는 걸 감추기 위해서일 뿐이야, 저기서 저렇게 험악하게 욕설을 퍼붓는 마부들처럼 말이야. 야시빈은 말하지."], ["Yashvin says, ‘He wants to strip me of my shirt, and I him of his.’ Yes, that’s the truth!”", "\"저 사람은 내 셔츠까지 벗겨 가려 하고, 나는 그자의 셔츠를 벗겨 가려 하지.\" 그래, 그게 진실이야!'"]], [["She was plunged in these thoughts, which so engrossed her that she left off thinking of her own position, when the carriage drew up at the steps of her house.", "* coiffeur(쿠아푀르): 프랑스어로 '미용사'. Je me fais coiffer par Tiutkin: '나는 튜트킨에게 머리를 맡긴다'라는 뜻."], ["It was only when she saw the porter running out to meet her that she remembered she had sent the note and the telegram.", "그녀는 이런 생각들에 너무 깊이 빠져들어 자기 처지에 대한 생각마저 잊어버렸는데, 그때 마차가 그녀의 집 현관 계단 앞에 멈춰 섰다. 자기를 맞으러 달려 나오는 문지기를 보고서야 그녀는 자기가 쪽지와 전보를 보냈다는 사실을 떠올렸다."]], [["“Is there an answer?”", "\"답장이 왔나?\""], ["she inquired.", "그녀가 물었다."]], [["“I’ll see this minute,”", "\"지금 바로 보고 오겠습니다.\""], ["answered the porter, and glancing into his room, he took out and gave her the thin square envelope of a telegram.", "문지기는 그렇게 대답하고 자기 방을 들여다보더니, 얇은 정사각형 전보 봉투를 꺼내 그녀에게 건넸다."], ["“I can’t come before ten o’clock.—Vronsky,”", "\"열 시 전에는 갈 수 없소.—브론스키.\""], ["she read.", "그녀는 읽었다."]], [["“And hasn’t the messenger come back?”", "\"심부름꾼은 아직 안 돌아왔고?\""]], [["“No,”", "\"네.\""], ["answered the porter.", "문지기가 대답했다."]], [["“Then, since it’s so, I know what I must do,”", "\"그렇다면, 사정이 그러하다면, 내가 어떻게 해야 할지 알겠어.\""], ["she said, and feeling a vague fury and craving for revenge rising up within her, she ran upstairs.", "그녀는 이렇게 말하며, 자기 안에서 막연한 분노와 복수에 대한 갈망이 솟구쳐 오르는 것을 느끼면서 위층으로 뛰어올라 갔다."], ["“I’ll go to him myself.", "'내가 직접 그이한테 가야지."], ["Before going away forever, I’ll tell him all.", "영영 떠나기 전에, 그이한테 모든 걸 말해 줄 거야."], ["Never have I hated anyone as I hate that man!”", "그 사람을 미워하는 만큼 누군가를 미워해 본 적이 없어!'"], ["she thought.", "그녀는 생각했다."], ["Seeing his hat on the rack, she shuddered with aversion.", "옷걸이에 걸린 그의 모자를 보자 그녀는 혐오감에 몸서리쳤다."], ["She did not consider that his telegram was an answer to her telegram and that he had not yet received her note.", "그녀는 그의 전보가 자기 전보에 대한 답장이며, 그가 아직 자기 쪽지를 받지 못했다는 것을 헤아리지 못했다."], ["She pictured him to herself as talking calmly to his mother and Princess Sorokina and rejoicing at her sufferings.", "그녀는 그가 자기 어머니와 소로키나 공작 영애에게 태연히 이야기를 늘어놓으며 자기의 괴로움을 고소해하는 모습을 머릿속에 그려 보았다."], ["“Yes, I must go quickly,”", "'그래, 어서 가야 해.'"], ["she said, not knowing yet where she was going.", "그녀는 아직 자기가 어디로 가는지도 모른 채 그렇게 말했다."], ["She longed to get away as quickly as possible from the feelings she had gone through in that awful house.", "그녀는 그 끔찍한 집에서 겪은 감정들로부터 한시바삐 벗어나고 싶었다."], ["The servants, the walls, the things in that house—all aroused repulsion and hatred in her and lay like a weight upon her.", "하인들, 벽, 그 집 안의 물건들—모든 것이 그녀 안에 반감과 증오를 불러일으키며 무거운 짐처럼 그녀를 짓눌렀다."]], [["“Yes, I must go to the railway station, and if he’s not there, then go there and catch him.”", "'그래, 역으로 가야지. 그이가 거기 없으면, 거기로 가서 그이를 붙잡아야겠어.'"], ["Anna looked at the railway timetable in the newspapers.", "안나는 신문에 실린 기차 시간표를 들여다보았다."], ["An evening train went at two minutes past eight.", "저녁 기차는 여덟 시 이 분에 떠났다."], ["“Yes, I shall be in time.”", "'그래, 시간 안에 닿을 수 있어.'"], ["She gave orders for the other horses to be put in the carriage, and packed in a traveling-bag the things needed for a few days.", "그녀는 마차에 다른 말들을 매라고 지시하고, 며칠 동안 필요한 물건들을 여행 가방에 꾸렸다."], ["She knew she would never come back here again.", "그녀는 다시는 이곳으로 돌아오지 않으리라는 것을 알고 있었다."]], [["Among the plans that came into her head she vaguely determined that after what would happen at the station or at the countess’s house, she would go as far as the first town on the Nizhni road and stop there.", "머릿속에 떠오른 여러 계획 가운데, 그녀는 역에서나 백작 부인 집에서 어떤 일이 벌어진 뒤에는, 니즈니로 가는 길의 첫 번째 도시까지 가서 거기 머물기로 어렴풋이 마음먹었다."]], [["Dinner was on the table; she went up, but the smell of the bread and cheese was enough to make her feel that all food was disgusting.", "저녁 식사가 식탁에 차려져 있었다. 그녀는 가까이 다가갔지만, 빵과 치즈 냄새만으로도 모든 음식이 역겹게 느껴졌다."], ["She ordered the carriage and went out.", "그녀는 마차를 대령하라 이르고 밖으로 나갔다."], ["The house threw a shadow now right across the street, but it was a bright evening and still warm in the sunshine.", "이제 집은 길 건너편까지 그림자를 드리우고 있었으나, 환한 저녁이었고 햇살 속은 여전히 따뜻했다."], ["Annushka, who came down with her things, and Pyotr, who put the things in the carriage, and the coachman, evidently out of humor, were all hateful to her, and irritated her by their words and actions.", "그녀의 짐을 들고 따라 내려온 안누시카도, 짐을 마차에 싣는 표트르도, 누가 봐도 심기가 불편한 마부도, 모두 그녀에게는 가증스러웠고, 그들의 말과 행동이 그녀를 짜증나게 했다."]], [["“I don’t want you, Pyotr.”", "\"넌 필요 없어, 표트르.\""]], [["“But how about the ticket?”", "\"그렇지만 표는 어떻게 합니까?\""]], [["“Well, as you like, it doesn’t matter,”", "\"뭐, 네 마음대로 해, 그건 아무래도 좋아.\""], ["she said crossly.", "그녀가 짜증스레 말했다."]], [["Pyotr jumped on the box, and putting his arms akimbo, told the coachman to drive to the booking-office.", "표트르는 마부석으로 뛰어올라, 두 손을 허리에 척 짚고는 마부에게 매표소로 가자고 일렀다."]], [["# Chapter 30", "# Chapter 30"]], [["“Here it is again!", "\"또 시작이군!"], ["Again I understand it all!”", "또 모든 게 이해가 돼!\""], ["Anna said to herself, as soon as the carriage had started and swaying lightly, rumbled over the tiny cobbles of the paved road, and again one impression followed rapidly upon another.", "마차가 출발해 가볍게 흔들리며 포장도로의 작은 자갈 위를 덜컹덜컹 달리기 시작하자마자, 그리고 다시 하나의 인상이 다른 인상에 빠르게 잇따르자마자, 안나는 혼잣말을 했다."]], [["“Yes; what was the last thing I thought of so clearly?”", "\"그래, 내가 그렇게 또렷이 떠올렸던 마지막 게 뭐였지?\""], ["she tried to recall it.", "그녀는 그것을 떠올리려 애썼다."], ["“‘Tiutkin, coiffeur?’—no, not that.", "\"‘Tiutkin, coiffeur?’—아니, 그게 아니야."], ["Yes, of what Yashvin says, the struggle for existence and hatred is the one thing that holds men together.", "그래, 야시빈이 말한 거였지, 생존을 위한 투쟁과 증오야말로 사람들을 한데 묶어 주는 유일한 거라고."], ["No, it’s a useless journey you’re making,”", "아니, 너희가 가는 그 여행은 부질없는 거야.\""], ["she said, mentally addressing a party in a coach and four, evidently going for an excursion into the country.", "그녀는 분명 시골로 소풍을 가는 듯한, 사두마차에 탄 일행에게 마음속으로 말을 걸었다."], ["“And the dog you’re taking with you will be no help to you.", "\"그리고 데려가는 그 개도 너희에게 아무 도움이 안 될 거고."], ["You can’t get away from yourselves.”", "너희 자신에게서는 달아날 수 없으니까.\""], ["Turning her eyes in the direction Pyotr had turned to look, she saw a factory-hand almost dead-drunk, with hanging head, being led away by a policeman.", "표트르가 돌아보던 방향으로 눈길을 돌리자, 거의 곤드레만드레 취해 고개를 떨군 공장 직공 하나가 경관에게 끌려가는 모습이 보였다."], ["“Come, he’s found a quicker way,”", "\"흥, 저 사람은 더 빠른 길을 찾았군.\""], ["she thought.", "그녀는 생각했다."], ["“Count Vronsky and I did not find that happiness either, though we expected so much from it.”", "\"브론스키 백작과 나도 그 행복을 찾지 못했어, 그것에서 그렇게 많은 걸 기대했는데도.\""], ["And now for the first time Anna turned that glaring light in which she was seeing everything on to her relations with him, which she had hitherto avoided thinking about.", "그리고 이제 처음으로 안나는, 모든 것을 비추던 그 눈부신 빛을, 지금껏 생각하기를 피해 왔던 그와의 관계로 돌렸다."], ["“What was it he sought in me?", "\"그이가 내게서 찾으려 한 게 뭐였지?"], ["Not love so much as the satisfaction of vanity.”", "사랑이라기보다는 허영심의 만족이었어.\""], ["She remembered his words, the expression of his face, that recalled an abject setter-dog, in the early days of their connection.", "그녀는 그의 말, 그의 표정을 떠올렸다, 그것은 그들 관계 초기의, 비굴한 세터 사냥개를 떠올리게 했다."], ["And everything now confirmed this.", "그리고 이제 모든 것이 이를 확인해 주었다."], ["“Yes, there was the triumph of success in him.", "\"그래, 그이 안에는 성공의 승리감이 있었어."], ["Of course there was love too, but the chief element was the pride of success.", "물론 사랑도 있었지만, 주된 요소는 성공의 자부심이었지. 그이는 나를 자랑거리로 삼았어."], ["He boasted of me.", "이제 그건 끝났어."], ["Now that’s over. There’s nothing to be proud of.", "자랑할 게 아무것도 없는 거야."], ["Not to be proud of, but to be ashamed of.", "자랑할 게 아니라 부끄러워할 거지."], ["He has taken from me all he could, and now I am no use to him.", "그이는 내게서 가질 수 있는 걸 다 가져갔고, 이제 나는 그이에게 쓸모가 없어."], ["He is weary of me and is trying not to be dishonorable in his behavior to me.", "그이는 나한테 싫증이 났으면서도 내게 비열하게 굴지 않으려고 애쓰는 거야."], ["He let that out yesterday—he wants divorce and marriage so as to burn his ships.", "어제 그걸 흘렸어—배수의 진을 치려고 이혼과 결혼을 원한다고. 그이는 나를 사랑해, 하지만 어떻게?"], ["He loves me, but how? The zest is gone, as the English say.", "영국 사람들 말마따나, 흥취가 가신 거지."], ["That fellow wants everyone to admire him and is very much pleased with himself,”", "저 사내는 모두가 자기를 우러러봐 주길 바라고 자기 자신에게 아주 만족해하는군.\""], ["she thought, looking at a red-faced clerk, riding on a riding-school horse.", "그녀는 승마학교 말을 타고 가는, 얼굴이 벌건 서기를 바라보며 생각했다."], ["“Yes, there’s not the same flavor about me for him now.", "\"그래, 이제 내겐 그이를 위한 그런 풍미가 없어."], ["If I go away from him, at the bottom of his heart he will be glad.”", "내가 그이를 떠나면, 마음 깊은 곳에서 그이는 기뻐할 거야.\""]], [["This was not mere supposition, she saw it distinctly in the piercing light, which revealed to her now the meaning of life and human relations.", "이것은 한낱 추측이 아니었다, 그녀는 그것을 꿰뚫는 빛 속에서 또렷이 보았다, 그 빛은 이제 그녀에게 삶과 인간관계의 의미를 드러내 주었다."]], [["“My love keeps growing more passionate and egoistic, while his is waning and waning, and that’s why we’re drifting apart.”", "\"내 사랑은 점점 더 격정적이고 이기적으로 자라 가는데, 그이의 사랑은 점점 더 시들고 또 시들어 가, 그래서 우리가 서로 멀어지는 거지.\""], ["She went on musing.", "그녀는 계속 생각에 잠겼다."], ["“And there’s no help for it.", "\"그리고 이건 어쩔 도리가 없어."], ["He is everything for me, and I want him more and more to give himself up to me entirely.", "그이는 내게 전부인데, 나는 점점 더 그이가 온전히 나에게만 자신을 바쳐 주길 바라."], ["And he wants more and more to get away from me.", "그런데 그이는 점점 더 나에게서 벗어나려 하지."], ["We walked to meet each other up to the time of our love, and then we have been irresistibly drifting in different directions.", "우리는 사랑이 무르익을 때까지는 서로를 향해 걸어왔고, 그 후로는 거역할 수 없이 서로 다른 방향으로 흘러가고 있어."], ["And there’s no altering that.", "그리고 그건 바꿀 수가 없어."], ["He tells me I’m insanely jealous, and I have told myself that I am insanely jealous; but it’s not true.", "그이는 내가 미치도록 질투한다고 말하고, 나도 스스로 미치도록 질투한다고 말해 왔지, 하지만 그건 사실이 아니야."], ["I’m not jealous, but I’m unsatisfied. But....”", "나는 질투하는 게 아니라 만족하지 못하는 거야."], ["she opened her lips, and shifted her place in the carriage in the excitement, aroused by the thought that suddenly struck her.", "하지만…….\" 그녀는 입술을 벌리고, 문득 떠오른 생각에 흥분해 마차 안에서 자리를 고쳐 앉았다."], ["“If I could be anything but a mistress, passionately caring for nothing but his caresses; but I can’t and I don’t care to be anything else.", "\"내가 정부(情婦) 말고, 그이의 애무 말고는 아무것에도 격정적으로 마음 쓰지 않는 여자 말고 다른 무언가가 될 수만 있다면. 하지만 나는 다른 무언가가 될 수 없고, 되고 싶지도 않아."], ["And by that desire I rouse aversion in him, and he rouses fury in me, and it cannot be different.", "그리고 그 욕망으로 나는 그이 안에 혐오를 불러일으키고, 그이는 내 안에 분노를 불러일으키고, 그건 달라질 수가 없어."], ["Don’t I know that he wouldn’t deceive me, that he has no schemes about Princess Sorokina, that he’s not in love with Kitty, that he won’t desert me!", "그이가 나를 속이지 않으리라는 걸, 소로키나 공작 영애에 대해 아무 꿍꿍이도 없다는 걸, 키티를 사랑하지 않는다는 걸, 나를 버리지 않으리라는 걸 내가 모르겠어!"], ["I know all that, but it makes it no better for me.", "그걸 다 알지만, 그렇다고 내게 나아지는 건 하나도 없어."], ["If without loving me, from duty he’ll be good and kind to me, without what I want, that’s a thousand times worse than unkindness!", "만약 나를 사랑하지도 않으면서, 의무에서 내게 다정하고 친절하게 군다면, 내가 원하는 것은 없이 말이야, 그건 불친절보다 천 배는 더 끔찍해!"], ["That’s—hell!", "그건—지옥이야!"], ["And that’s just how it is.", "그리고 바로 그런 거야."], ["For a long while now he hasn’t loved me.", "이미 오래전부터 그이는 나를 사랑하지 않아."], ["And where love ends, hate begins.", "그리고 사랑이 끝나는 곳에서 증오가 시작되지."], ["I don’t know these streets at all.", "이 거리들은 통 모르겠네."], ["Hills it seems, and still houses, and houses....", "언덕인 것 같고, 또 집들이, 집들이……."], ["And in the houses always people and people....", "그리고 집들 안에는 늘 사람들이, 사람들이……."], ["How many of them, no end, and all hating each other!", "얼마나 많은지, 끝이 없어, 게다가 모두가 서로를 미워하지!"], ["Come, let me try and think what I want, to make me happy.", "자, 나를 행복하게 해 주려면 내가 뭘 원하는지 한번 생각해 보자."], ["Well?", "어떨까?"], ["Suppose I am divorced, and Alexey Alexandrovitch lets me have Seryozha, and I marry Vronsky.”", "내가 이혼하고, 알렉세이 알렉산드로비치가 세료자를 내게 주고, 내가 브론스키와 결혼한다고 치자.\""], ["Thinking of Alexey Alexandrovitch, she at once pictured him with extraordinary vividness as though he were alive before her, with his mild, lifeless, dull eyes, the blue veins in his white hands, his intonations and the cracking of his fingers, and remembering the feeling which had existed between them, and which was also called love, she shuddered with loathing.", "알렉세이 알렉산드로비치를 떠올리자, 그녀는 곧바로 그가 살아서 눈앞에 있는 듯이 비상하게 생생하게 그려 보았다, 그 온순하고 생기 없고 흐리멍덩한 눈, 흰 손등의 푸른 핏줄, 그의 억양과 손가락 마디를 꺾는 소리를, 그리고 그들 사이에 존재했던, 역시 사랑이라 불리던 그 감정을 떠올리며, 그녀는 혐오로 몸서리쳤다."], ["“Well, I’m divorced, and become Vronsky’s wife.", "\"좋아, 나는 이혼하고, 브론스키의 아내가 돼."], ["Well, will Kitty cease looking at me as she looked at me today? No.", "그래서, 키티가 오늘 나를 바라보던 것처럼 더는 나를 바라보지 않게 될까?"], ["And will Seryozha leave off asking and wondering about my two husbands?", "아니야. 그리고 세료자가 내 두 남편에 대해 묻고 의아해하는 걸 그만둘까?"], ["And is there any new feeling I can awaken between Vronsky and me?", "그리고 브론스키와 나 사이에 내가 일깨울 수 있는 새로운 감정이 있을까?"], ["Is there possible, if not happiness, some sort of ease from misery?", "행복은 아니더라도, 비참함에서 벗어난 어떤 편안함이라도 가능할까? 아니, 아니야!\""], ["No, no!” she answered now without the slightest hesitation.", "그녀는 이제 조금의 망설임도 없이 스스로 대답했다."], ["“Impossible!", "\"불가능해!"], ["We are drawn apart by life, and I make his unhappiness, and he mine, and there’s no altering him or me.", "우리는 삶에 의해 갈라졌고, 나는 그이를 불행하게 하고, 그이는 나를 불행하게 하고, 그이도 나도 바꿀 수 없어."], ["Every attempt has been made, the screw has come unscrewed.", "온갖 시도를 다 해 봤지만, 나사가 헐거워져 빠져 버렸어."], ["Oh, a beggar woman with a baby.", "아, 아기를 안은 거지 여자네."], ["She thinks I’m sorry for her.", "저 여자는 내가 자기를 가엾어한다고 여기지."], ["Aren’t we all flung into the world only to hate each other, and so to torture ourselves and each other?", "우리 모두는 서로를 미워하고, 그렇게 자기 자신과 서로를 괴롭히려고 세상에 내던져진 게 아닐까?"], ["Schoolboys coming—laughing Seryozha?”", "학생들이 오네—웃고 있는 세료자?\""], ["she thought.", "그녀는 생각했다."], ["“I thought, too, that I loved him, and used to be touched by my own tenderness.", "\"나도 그 애를 사랑한다고 생각했고, 내 자신의 다정함에 곧잘 가슴이 뭉클하곤 했지."], ["But I have lived without him, I gave him up for another love, and did not regret the exchange till that love was satisfied.”", "하지만 나는 그 애 없이 살았고, 다른 사랑을 위해 그 애를 포기했고, 그 사랑이 충족될 때까지는 그 맞바꿈을 후회하지 않았어.\""], ["And with loathing she thought of what she meant by that love.", "그리고 그녀는 그 사랑이라는 게 무얼 뜻하는지 혐오스럽게 생각했다."], ["And the clearness with which she saw life now, her own and all men’s, was a pleasure to her.", "그리고 이제 자신과 모든 사람의 삶을 보는 그 명료함이 그녀에게는 하나의 쾌감이었다."], ["“It’s so with me and Pyotr, and the coachman, Fyodor, and that merchant, and all the people living along the Volga, where those placards invite one to go, and everywhere and always,”", "\"나와 표트르도, 마부 표도르도, 저 상인도, 저 광고판들이 가 보라고 권하는 볼가 강을 따라 사는 모든 사람들도, 어디서나 언제나 다 그런 거야.\""], ["she thought when she had driven under the low-pitched roof of the Nizhigorod station, and the porters ran to meet her.", "나지고로드 역의 나지막한 지붕 아래로 마차가 들어서고 짐꾼들이 그녀를 맞으러 달려왔을 때, 그녀는 생각했다."]], [["“A ticket to Obiralovka?”", "\"오비랄로프카행 표 한 장이오?\""], ["said Pyotr.", "표트르가 말했다."]], [["She had utterly forgotten where and why she was going, and only by a great effort she understood the question.", "그녀는 어디로 무엇 때문에 가는지를 완전히 잊고 있었고, 큰 노력을 기울여서야 겨우 그 질문을 알아들었다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그래.\""], ["she said, handing him her purse, and taking a little red bag in her hand, she got out of the carriage.", "그녀는 그에게 지갑을 건네며 말하고는, 손에 작은 빨간 가방을 들고 마차에서 내렸다."]], [["Making her way through the crowd to the first-class waiting-room, she gradually recollected all the details of her position, and the plans between which she was hesitating.", "군중을 헤치고 일등 대합실로 향하면서, 그녀는 차츰 자기 처지의 온갖 세세한 정황과, 자기가 망설이고 있는 여러 계획들을 떠올렸다."], ["And again at the old sore places, hope and then despair poisoned the wounds of her tortured, fearfully throbbing heart.", "그리고 다시금 그 오래된 상처 자리마다, 희망이, 그다음에는 절망이 그녀의 괴로워하는, 무섭게 두근거리는 심장의 상처를 독으로 적셨다."], ["As she sat on the star-shaped sofa waiting for the train, she gazed with aversion at the people coming and going (they were all hateful to her), and thought how she would arrive at the station, would write him a note, and what she would write to him, and how he was at this moment complaining to his mother of his position, not understanding her sufferings, and how she would go into the room, and what she would say to him.", "기차를 기다리며 별 모양 소파에 앉아 있는 동안, 그녀는 오가는 사람들을 (그들 모두가 그녀에게는 가증스러웠다) 혐오스럽게 바라보았고, 자기가 역에 어떻게 도착할지, 그에게 어떻게 쪽지를 쓸지, 그에게 무어라고 쓸지, 그리고 그가 지금 이 순간 자기 어머니에게 자기 처지를 어떻게 하소연하고 있을지, 그녀의 고통은 이해하지도 못한 채로, 그리고 자기가 어떻게 방으로 들어갈지, 그에게 무어라고 말할지를 생각했다."], ["Then she thought that life might still be happy, and how miserably she loved and hated him, and how fearfully her heart was beating.", "그러다 삶이 아직 행복할 수도 있으리라는 생각이, 자기가 얼마나 비참하게 그를 사랑하고 또 미워하는지가, 그리고 자기 심장이 얼마나 무섭게 뛰고 있는지가 떠올랐다."]], [["# Chapter 31", "# Chapter 31"]], [["A bell rang, some young men, ugly and impudent, and at the same time careful of the impression they were making, hurried by.", "벨이 울리자, 못생기고 뻔뻔하면서도 동시에 자기가 주는 인상에 신경을 쓰는 젊은 남자 몇이 서둘러 지나갔다."], ["Pyotr, too, crossed the room in his livery and top-boots, with his dull, animal face, and came up to her to take her to the train.", "표트르 역시 제복과 긴 장화 차림으로, 둔하고 짐승 같은 얼굴을 한 채 방을 가로질러 그녀에게 다가와 기차로 데려가려 했다."], ["Some noisy men were quiet as she passed them on the platform, and one whispered something about her to another—something vile, no doubt.", "시끄럽게 떠들던 남자들이 그녀가 플랫폼에서 곁을 지나가자 입을 다물었고, 그중 하나가 그녀에 대해 다른 남자에게 뭔가를 속삭였다."], ["She stepped up on the high step, and sat down in a carriage by herself on a dirty seat that had been white.", "분명 천박한 말이었으리라. 그녀는 높은 발판을 딛고 올라가, 한때 흰색이었던 더러운 좌석에 혼자 객실에 앉았다."], ["Her bag lay beside her, shaken up and down by the springiness of the seat.", "그녀의 가방은 옆에 놓여 좌석의 탄력에 따라 위아래로 흔들렸다."], ["With a foolish smile Pyotr raised his hat, with its colored band, at the window, in token of farewell; an impudent conductor slammed the door and the latch.", "표트르는 멍청한 미소를 지으며 작별의 표시로 창가에서 색띠가 둘린 모자를 들어 올렸고, 뻔뻔한 차장이 문과 빗장을 쾅 닫았다."], ["A grotesque-looking lady wearing a bustle (Anna mentally undressed the woman, and was appalled at her hideousness), and a little girl laughing affectedly ran down the platform.", "버슬을 댄 기괴한 모습의 부인(안나는 마음속으로 그 여자의 옷을 벗겨 보고는 그 흉측함에 소름이 끼쳤다)과 꾸민 듯이 웃어 대는 어린 여자아이가 플랫폼을 뛰어 내려갔다."]], [["“Katerina Andreevna, she’s got them all, ma tante!”", "\"카체리나 안드레예브나, 그 애가 다 가졌어요, ma tante!\""], ["cried the girl.", "여자아이가 소리쳤다."]], [["“Even the child’s hideous and affected,”", "* ma tante: 프랑스어로 '이모(고모)'. \"애조차 흉측하고 가식적이군.\""], ["thought Anna.", "안나는 생각했다."], ["To avoid seeing anyone, she got up quickly and seated herself at the opposite window of the empty carriage.", "누구의 눈에도 띄지 않으려고 그녀는 얼른 일어나 텅 빈 객실의 맞은편 창가에 자리를 잡았다."], ["A misshapen-looking peasant covered with dirt, in a cap from which his tangled hair stuck out all round, passed by that window, stooping down to the carriage wheels.", "더러운 것을 뒤집어쓴 기형적인 모습의 농부 하나가, 헝클어진 머리카락이 사방으로 삐져나온 모자를 쓰고서 객차 바퀴 쪽으로 몸을 숙이며 그 창 옆을 지나갔다."], ["“There’s something familiar about that hideous peasant,”", "\"저 흉측한 농부에게 뭔가 낯익은 데가 있어.\""], ["thought Anna.", "안나는 생각했다."], ["And remembering her dream, she moved away to the opposite door, shaking with terror.", "그리고 자기 꿈을 떠올리며 공포에 떨면서 맞은편 문 쪽으로 옮겨 갔다."], ["The conductor opened the door and let in a man and his wife.", "차장이 문을 열고 한 남자와 그의 아내를 들여보냈다."]], [["“Do you wish to get out?”", "\"내리시겠습니까?\""]], [["Anna made no answer.", "안나는 아무 대답도 하지 않았다."], ["The conductor and her two fellow-passengers did not notice under her veil her panic-stricken face.", "차장과 두 동승객은 그녀의 베일 아래 공포에 질린 얼굴을 알아채지 못했다."], ["She went back to her corner and sat down.", "그녀는 다시 자기 구석 자리로 돌아가 앉았다."], ["The couple seated themselves on the opposite side, and intently but surreptitiously scrutinized her clothes.", "부부는 맞은편에 자리를 잡고, 유심히 그러나 몰래 그녀의 옷차림을 살폈다."], ["Both husband and wife seemed repulsive to Anna.", "남편도 아내도 안나에게는 역겹게 느껴졌다."], ["The husband asked, would she allow him to smoke, obviously not with a view to smoking but to getting into conversation with her.", "남편은 그녀에게 담배를 피워도 되겠느냐고 물었는데, 분명 담배를 피우려는 것이 아니라 그녀와 대화를 트려는 속셈이었다."], ["Receiving her assent, he said to his wife in French something about caring less to smoke than to talk.", "그녀의 승낙을 얻자 그는 아내에게 프랑스어로 담배를 피우기보다 이야기를 나누고 싶다는 식의 말을 했다."], ["They made inane and affected remarks to one another, entirely for her benefit.", "그들은 오로지 그녀에게 들으라는 듯이 서로에게 시시하고 가식적인 말을 주고받았다."], ["Anna saw clearly that they were sick of each other, and hated each other.", "안나는 그들이 서로에게 진력이 났고 서로를 증오하고 있음을 똑똑히 알아챘다."], ["And no one could have helped hating such miserable monstrosities.", "그리고 이토록 가련하고 기괴한 인간들이라면 누구라도 증오하지 않을 수 없었으리라."]], [["A second bell sounded, and was followed by moving of luggage, noise, shouting and laughter.", "두 번째 벨이 울렸고, 뒤이어 짐 옮기는 소리, 소음, 고함과 웃음이 따랐다."], ["It was so clear to Anna that there was nothing for anyone to be glad of, that this laughter irritated her agonizingly, and she would have liked to stop up her ears not to hear it.", "누구에게도 기뻐할 거리라곤 전혀 없다는 것이 안나에게는 너무도 분명했기에, 이 웃음소리는 그녀를 괴로울 만큼 짜증나게 했고, 그녀는 그 소리를 듣지 않으려고 귀를 막아 버리고 싶었다."], ["At last the third bell rang, there was a whistle and a hiss of steam, and a clank of chains, and the man in her carriage crossed himself.", "마침내 세 번째 벨이 울리고, 기적 소리와 증기 새는 소리, 쇠사슬 부딪치는 소리가 났고, 그녀의 객실에 있던 남자가 성호를 그었다."], ["“It would be interesting to ask him what meaning he attaches to that,”", "\"저 사람에게 그게 무슨 의미인지 물어보면 재미있겠군.\""], ["thought Anna, looking angrily at him.", "안나는 그를 화난 눈으로 바라보며 생각했다."], ["She looked past the lady out of the window at the people who seemed whirling by as they ran beside the train or stood on the platform.", "그녀는 부인 너머로 창밖을 내다보았는데, 기차 옆을 따라 달리거나 플랫폼에 서 있는 사람들이 빙빙 돌며 스쳐 지나가는 것 같았다."], ["The train, jerking at regular intervals at the junctions of the rails, rolled by the platform, past a stone wall, a signal-box, past other trains; the wheels, moving more smoothly and evenly, resounded with a slight clang on the rails.", "기차는 레일 이음매에서 일정한 간격으로 덜컹거리며 플랫폼을 지나, 돌담과 신호소를 지나, 다른 기차들을 지나쳐 굴러갔고, 바퀴는 더 부드럽고 고르게 움직이며 레일 위에서 가벼운 쇳소리를 냈다."], ["The window was lighted up by the bright evening sun, and a slight breeze fluttered the curtain.", "창문은 밝은 저녁 햇살로 환해졌고, 가벼운 산들바람이 커튼을 나부끼게 했다."], ["Anna forgot her fellow passengers, and to the light swaying of the train she fell to thinking again, as she breathed the fresh air.", "안나는 동승객들을 잊은 채, 기차의 가벼운 흔들림 속에서 신선한 공기를 들이마시며 다시 생각에 잠겼다."]], [["“Yes, what did I stop at?", "\"그래, 내가 어디서 멈췄더라?"], ["That I couldn’t conceive a position in which life would not be a misery, that we are all created to be miserable, and that we all know it, and all invent means of deceiving each other.", "삶이 비참하지 않을 상황이란 도무지 상상할 수 없다는 것, 우리는 모두 비참해지도록 만들어졌다는 것, 그리고 우리 모두 그 사실을 알면서도 서로를 속이는 수단을 만들어 낸다는 것."], ["And when one sees the truth, what is one to do?”", "그러면 진실을 보았을 때, 사람은 어떻게 해야 하지?\""]], [["“That’s what reason is given man for, to escape from what worries him,”", "\"그게 바로 인간에게 이성이 주어진 이유잖아요. 자기를 괴롭히는 것에서 벗어나기 위해서요.\""], ["said the lady in French, lisping affectedly, and obviously pleased with her phrase.", "부인이 프랑스어로 일부러 혀 짧은 소리를 내며 말했는데, 자기가 한 말에 흡족해하는 기색이 역력했다."]], [["The words seemed an answer to Anna’s thoughts.", "그 말은 마치 안나의 생각에 대한 대답인 것 같았다."]], [["“To escape from what worries him,”", "\"자기를 괴롭히는 것에서 벗어나기 위해.\""], ["repeated Anna.", "안나는 되뇌었다."], ["And glancing at the red-cheeked husband and the thin wife, she saw that the sickly wife considered herself misunderstood, and the husband deceived her and encouraged her in that idea of herself.", "그리고 두 볼이 불그스레한 남편과 마른 아내를 흘끗 보고는, 병약한 아내는 자기가 이해받지 못한다고 여기고, 남편은 그녀를 속이면서 그녀가 스스로에 대해 품은 그런 생각을 부추기고 있음을 알아차렸다."], ["Anna seemed to see all their history and all the crannies of their souls, as it were turning a light upon them.", "안나는 마치 그들 위에 빛을 비추기라도 하듯, 그들의 모든 내력과 영혼의 온갖 구석구석을 다 들여다보는 것 같았다."], ["But there was nothing interesting in them, and she pursued her thought.", "하지만 거기에는 흥미로운 것이라곤 아무것도 없었고, 그녀는 자기 생각을 이어 나갔다."]], [["“Yes, I’m very much worried, and that’s what reason was given me for, to escape; so then one must escape: why not put out the light when there’s nothing more to look at, when it’s sickening to look at it all?", "\"그래, 나는 무척 괴로워하고 있어. 바로 그것 때문에 내게 이성이 주어진 거지, 벗어나라고."], ["But how?", "그러니 벗어나야 해."], ["Why did the conductor run along the footboard, why are they shrieking, those young men in that train?", "더 이상 볼 것이 없을 때, 그 모든 걸 보는 게 역겨울 때, 어째서 불을 꺼 버리면 안 되는 거지? 그런데 어떻게?"], ["why are they talking, why are they laughing?", "어째서 차장은 발판을 따라 달려갔을까, 어째서 저들은, 저 기차 안의 젊은 남자들은 비명을 질러 대고 있을까?"], ["It’s all falsehood, all lying, all humbug, all cruelty!...”", "어째서 저들은 이야기를 나누고, 어째서 웃어 대는 거지? 모두 거짓이야, 모두 허위고, 모두 속임수고, 모두 잔인함이야!……\""]], [["When the train came into the station, Anna got out into the crowd of passengers, and moving apart from them as if they were lepers, she stood on the platform, trying to think what she had come here for, and what she meant to do.", "기차가 역에 들어서자 안나는 승객들 무리 속으로 내려서서, 그들이 나병 환자라도 되는 양 그들에게서 떨어져 플랫폼에 서서, 자기가 무엇 하러 여기 왔는지, 무엇을 하려 했는지 떠올리려 애썼다."], ["Everything that had seemed to her possible before was now so difficult to consider, especially in this noisy crowd of hideous people who would not leave her alone.", "전에는 가능해 보였던 모든 일이 이제 와서는 도무지 생각하기 어려웠는데, 특히 그녀를 가만히 내버려 두지 않는 이 흉측한 사람들의 시끄러운 무리 속에서는 더욱 그랬다."], ["One moment porters ran up to her proffering their services, then young men, clacking their heels on the planks of the platform and talking loudly, stared at her; people meeting her dodged past on the wrong side.", "어느 순간 짐꾼들이 그녀에게 달려와 시중을 자청했고, 그다음에는 젊은 남자들이 플랫폼 널빤지에 구두 뒤축을 따각거리고 큰 소리로 떠들며 그녀를 빤히 쳐다보았으며, 그녀와 마주친 사람들은 엉뚱한 쪽으로 비켜 지나갔다."], ["Remembering that she had meant to go on further if there were no answer, she stopped a porter and asked if her coachman were not here with a note from Count Vronsky.", "답장이 없으면 더 멀리 가려 했다는 것을 떠올리고, 그녀는 짐꾼 하나를 붙잡고 자기 마부가 브론스키 백작의 쪽지를 가지고 여기 와 있지 않느냐고 물었다."]], [["“Count Vronsky?", "\"브론스키 백작이요?"], ["They sent up here from the Vronskys just this minute, to meet Princess Sorokina and her daughter.", "방금 막 브론스키 댁에서 여기로 사람을 보냈습니다, 소로키나 공작 부인과 따님을 마중하러요."], ["And what is the coachman like?”", "그런데 마부가 어떻게 생겼습니까?\""]], [["Just as she was talking to the porter, the coachman Mihail, red and cheerful in his smart blue coat and chain, evidently proud of having so successfully performed his commission, came up to her and gave her a letter.", "그녀가 짐꾼과 이야기를 나누고 있을 바로 그때, 마부 미하일이 멋진 푸른 외투와 사슬 장식을 두르고 얼굴이 불그레하니 명랑한 모습으로, 자기 임무를 그토록 성공적으로 완수한 것을 분명 자랑스러워하며 그녀에게 다가와 편지를 건넸다."], ["She broke it open, and her heart ached before she had read it.", "그녀는 편지를 뜯었고, 미처 읽기도 전에 가슴이 아려 왔다."]], [["“I am very sorry your note did not reach me.", "\"당신의 쪽지가 내게 닿지 못해서 정말 유감이오."], ["I will be home at ten,”", "열 시에 집에 돌아가겠소.\""], ["Vronsky had written carelessly....", "브론스키는 무심하게 휘갈겨 놓았다……"]], [["“Yes, that’s what I expected!”", "\"그래, 내가 예상했던 그대로야!\""], ["she said to herself with an evil smile.", "그녀는 사악한 미소를 지으며 혼잣말을 했다."]], [["“Very good, you can go home then,”", "\"좋아, 그럼 자네는 집으로 돌아가게.\""], ["she said softly, addressing Mihail.", "그녀는 미하일을 향해 나직이 말했다."], ["She spoke softly because the rapidity of her heart’s beating hindered her breathing.", "심장이 너무 빠르게 뛰어 숨쉬기가 힘들었기에 나직이 말한 것이었다."], ["“No, I won’t let you make me miserable,”", "\"안 돼, 네가 나를 비참하게 만들도록 내버려 두지 않을 거야.\""], ["she thought menacingly, addressing not him, not herself, but the power that made her suffer, and she walked along the platform.", "그녀는 그를 향해서도, 자기 자신을 향해서도 아닌, 자기를 괴롭히는 그 힘을 향해 위협하듯 생각하며 플랫폼을 따라 걸었다."]], [["Two maid-servants walking along the platform turned their heads, staring at her and making some remarks about her dress.", "플랫폼을 따라 걷던 두 하녀가 고개를 돌려 그녀를 빤히 보며 그녀의 옷차림에 대해 뭐라고 한마디씩 했다. \"진짜네.\""], ["“Real,” they said of the lace she was wearing.", "그들은 그녀가 두른 레이스를 두고 말했다."], ["The young men would not leave her in peace.", "젊은 남자들은 그녀를 가만히 내버려 두지 않았다."], ["Again they passed by, peering into her face, and with a laugh shouting something in an unnatural voice.", "그들은 다시 그녀의 얼굴을 들여다보며 곁을 지나갔고, 웃으면서 부자연스러운 목소리로 뭐라고 소리쳤다."], ["The station-master coming up asked her whether she was going by train.", "다가온 역장은 그녀에게 기차로 가시느냐고 물었다."], ["A boy selling kvas never took his eyes off her.", "크바스를 파는 소년은 그녀에게서 한순간도 눈을 떼지 않았다."], ["“My God!", "\"맙소사!"], ["where am I to go?”", "나는 어디로 가야 하지?\""], ["she thought, going farther and farther along the platform.", "그녀는 플랫폼을 따라 점점 더 멀리 가며 생각했다."], ["At the end she stopped.", "끝에 이르러 그녀는 멈춰 섰다."], ["Some ladies and children, who had come to meet a gentleman in spectacles, paused in their loud laughter and talking, and stared at her as she reached them.", "안경 쓴 신사를 마중 나온 몇몇 부인과 아이들이 큰 소리로 웃고 떠들다가 멈추고는, 그녀가 다가가자 빤히 쳐다보았다."], ["She quickened her pace and walked away from them to the edge of the platform.", "그녀는 걸음을 재촉해 그들에게서 멀어져 플랫폼 가장자리로 걸어갔다."], ["A luggage train was coming in.", "화물 열차가 들어오고 있었다."], ["The platform began to sway, and she fancied she was in the train again.", "플랫폼이 흔들리기 시작했고, 그녀는 자기가 다시 기차 안에 있는 것 같은 느낌이 들었다."]], [["And all at once she thought of the man crushed by the train the day she had first met Vronsky, and she knew what she had to do.", "그러다 문득 그녀는 브론스키를 처음 만난 날 기차에 깔려 죽은 남자를 떠올렸고, 자기가 무엇을 해야 하는지 깨달았다."], ["With a rapid, light step she went down the steps that led from the tank to the rails and stopped quite near the approaching train.", "빠르고 가벼운 걸음으로 그녀는 급수탑에서 레일로 이어지는 계단을 내려가, 다가오는 기차 바로 가까이에 멈춰 섰다."]], [["She looked at the lower part of the carriages, at the screws and chains and the tall cast-iron wheel of the first carriage slowly moving up, and trying to measure the middle between the front and back wheels, and the very minute when that middle point would be opposite her.", "그녀는 객차의 아랫부분, 나사와 쇠사슬, 천천히 굴러오는 첫 번째 객차의 높다란 주철 바퀴를 바라보며, 앞바퀴와 뒷바퀴 사이의 한가운데를, 그리고 그 한가운데 지점이 정확히 자기 맞은편에 오는 바로 그 순간을 가늠하려 애썼다."]], [["“There,”", "\"저기.\""], ["she said to herself, looking into the shadow of the carriage, at the sand and coal dust which covered the sleepers—“there, in the very middle, and I will punish him and escape from everyone and from myself.”", "그녀는 객차의 그림자 속, 침목을 덮은 모래와 석탄 가루를 들여다보며 혼잣말을 했다. \"저기, 바로 한가운데에, 그러면 나는 그를 벌하고 모든 사람에게서, 그리고 나 자신에게서 벗어날 거야.\""]], [["She tried to fling herself below the wheels of the first carriage as it reached her; but the red bag which she tried to drop out of her hand delayed her, and she was too late; she missed the moment.", "그녀는 첫 번째 객차가 자기에게 닿는 순간 바퀴 아래로 몸을 던지려 했지만, 손에서 떨어뜨리려던 붉은 가방이 그녀를 지체시켜 너무 늦어 버렸고, 그 순간을 놓치고 말았다."], ["She had to wait for the next carriage.", "그녀는 다음 객차를 기다려야 했다."], ["A feeling such as she had known when about to take the first plunge in bathing came upon her, and she crossed herself.", "물에 들어가 처음으로 몸을 담그려 할 때 느끼던 것과 같은 감각이 그녀에게 밀려왔고, 그녀는 성호를 그었다."], ["That familiar gesture brought back into her soul a whole series of girlish and childish memories, and suddenly the darkness that had covered everything for her was torn apart, and life rose up before her for an instant with all its bright past joys.", "그 익숙한 동작은 그녀의 영혼에 처녀 시절과 어린 시절의 온갖 기억을 한꺼번에 되살려 놓았고, 갑자기 그녀의 모든 것을 덮고 있던 어둠이 찢겨 나가면서, 삶이 그 모든 밝고 지난 기쁨과 더불어 한순간 그녀 앞에 솟아올랐다."], ["But she did not take her eyes from the wheels of the second carriage.", "하지만 그녀는 두 번째 객차의 바퀴에서 눈을 떼지 않았다."], ["And exactly at the moment when the space between the wheels came opposite her, she dropped the red bag, and drawing her head back into her shoulders, fell on her hands under the carriage, and lightly, as though she would rise again at once, dropped on to her knees.", "그리고 바퀴 사이의 공간이 자기 맞은편에 온 바로 그 순간, 그녀는 붉은 가방을 떨어뜨리고, 어깨 사이로 고개를 움츠리고는, 객차 아래로 두 손을 짚으며 쓰러졌고, 마치 곧바로 다시 일어서기라도 할 것처럼 가볍게 무릎을 꿇었다."], ["And at the same instant she was terror-stricken at what she was doing.", "그리고 바로 그 순간 그녀는 자기가 하고 있는 일에 공포에 사로잡혔다. \"내가 어디 있는 거지?"], ["“Where am I? What am I doing?", "내가 무얼 하고 있는 거야?"], ["What for?”", "무엇 때문에?\""], ["She tried to get up, to drop backwards; but something huge and merciless struck her on the head and rolled her on her back.", "그녀는 일어서려고, 뒤로 몸을 빼려고 애썼다. 하지만 무언가 거대하고 무자비한 것이 그녀의 머리를 내리쳤고 그녀의 등을 짓이기며 굴러갔다."], ["“Lord, forgive me all!” she said, feeling it impossible to struggle.", "\"주여, 저의 모든 죄를 용서하소서!\""], ["A peasant muttering something was working at the iron above her.", "그녀는 더 이상 발버둥 치는 것이 불가능함을 느끼며 말했다."], ["And the light by which she had read the book filled with troubles, falsehoods, sorrow, and evil, flared up more brightly than ever before, lighted up for her all that had been in darkness, flickered, began to grow dim, and was quenched forever.", "무어라 중얼거리는 농부 하나가 그녀의 머리 위쪽에서 쇠를 다루고 있었다. 그리고 근심과 거짓과 슬픔과 악으로 가득 찬 그 책을 읽게 해 주던 빛이, 그 어느 때보다 환하게 타올라, 어둠 속에 있던 모든 것을 그녀에게 밝혀 주고는, 깜박이다가 어두워지기 시작하더니, 영영 꺼져 버렸다."]], [["# PART EIGHT Chapter 1", "# PART EIGHT Chapter 1"]], [["Almost two months had passed.", "거의 두 달이 흘렀다."], ["The hot summer was half over, but Sergey Ivanovitch was only just preparing to leave Moscow.", "무더운 여름이 절반쯤 지났지만, 세르게이 이바노비치는 이제야 비로소 모스크바를 떠날 채비를 하고 있었다."]], [["Sergey Ivanovitch’s life had not been uneventful during this time.", "이 기간 동안 세르게이 이바노비치의 삶에 별다른 사건이 없었던 것은 아니다."], ["A year ago he had finished his book, the fruit of six years’ labor, “Sketch of a Survey of the Principles and Forms of Government in Europe and Russia.”", "일 년 전, 그는 육 년에 걸친 노고의 결실인 자신의 책 『유럽과 러시아의 통치 원리 및 형태에 관한 개관 시론』을 완성했다."], ["Several sections of this book and its introduction had appeared in periodical publications, and other parts had been read by Sergey Ivanovitch to persons of his circle, so that the leading ideas of the work could not be completely novel to the public.", "이 책의 몇몇 부분과 서문은 정기 간행물에 발표되었고, 다른 부분들은 세르게이 이바노비치가 자기 모임의 사람들에게 읽어 준 바 있었으므로, 이 저작의 핵심 사상이 대중에게 완전히 새로운 것일 수는 없었다."], ["But still Sergey Ivanovitch had expected that on its appearance his book would be sure to make a serious impression on society, and if it did not cause a revolution in social science it would, at any rate, make a great stir in the scientific world.", "그러나 그래도 세르게이 이바노비치는 자기 책이 세상에 나오면 틀림없이 사회에 진지한 인상을 줄 것이며, 설령 사회과학에 혁명을 일으키지는 못하더라도, 적어도 학계에 큰 파란을 불러일으키리라 기대하고 있었다."]], [["After the most conscientious revision the book had last year been published, and had been distributed among the booksellers.", "지극히 양심적인 교정을 거친 끝에 그 책은 작년에 출간되어 서점들에 배포되었다."]], [["Though he asked no one about it, reluctantly and with feigned indifference answered his friends’ inquiries as to how the book was going, and did not even inquire of the booksellers how the book was selling, Sergey Ivanovitch was all on the alert, with strained attention, watching for the first impression his book would make in the world and in literature.", "비록 그는 누구에게도 그것에 대해 묻지 않았고, 책이 어떻게 되어 가느냐는 친구들의 물음에는 마지못해 무관심을 가장하며 대답했으며, 책이 얼마나 팔리는지조차 서점에 물어보지 않았지만, 세르게이 이바노비치는 자기 책이 세상과 문단에 일으킬 첫 반응을 긴장된 주의를 곤두세운 채 온통 신경을 곤두세우고 지켜보고 있었다."]], [["But a week passed, a second, a third, and in society no impression whatever could be detected.", "그러나 한 주가 지나고, 또 한 주가 지나고, 세 번째 주가 지나도, 사교계에서는 아무런 반응도 감지할 수 없었다."], ["His friends who were specialists and savants, occasionally—unmistakably from politeness—alluded to it.", "전문가나 학자인 그의 친구들은 가끔씩—틀림없이 예의상—그 책을 슬쩍 언급했다."], ["The rest of his acquaintances, not interested in a book on a learned subject, did not talk of it at all.", "학문적인 주제를 다룬 책에 관심이 없는 나머지 지인들은 그것에 대해 전혀 입에 올리지 않았다."], ["And society generally—just now especially absorbed in other things—was absolutely indifferent.", "그리고 사교계는 일반적으로—특히 지금은 다른 일에 정신이 팔려—철저히 무관심했다."], ["In the press, too, for a whole month there was not a word about his book.", "언론에서도 한 달이 다 가도록 그의 책에 관해서는 한마디도 없었다."]], [["Sergey Ivanovitch had calculated to a nicety the time necessary for writing a review, but a month passed, and a second, and still there was silence.", "세르게이 이바노비치는 서평을 쓰는 데 필요한 시간을 정확하게 계산해 두었지만, 한 달이 지나고, 또 한 달이 지나도 여전히 침묵뿐이었다."]], [["Only in the Northern Beetle, in a comic article on the singer Drabanti, who had lost his voice, there was a contemptuous allusion to Koznishev’s book, suggesting that the book had been long ago seen through by everyone, and was a subject of general ridicule.", "다만 『북방의 딱정벌레』에 목소리를 잃은 가수 드라반티에 관한 익살스러운 글이 실렸는데, 거기서 코즈니셰프의 책을 경멸하듯 언급하며, 그 책이 이미 오래전에 모두에게 간파되어 일반적인 조롱거리가 되었다고 빈정거렸다."]], [["At last in the third month a critical article appeared in a serious review.", "마침내 세 번째 달에 한 진지한 평론지에 비평 기사가 실렸다."], ["Sergey Ivanovitch knew the author of the article.", "세르게이 이바노비치는 그 글의 필자를 알고 있었다."], ["He had met him once at Golubtsov’s.", "골루프초프의 집에서 한 번 만난 적이 있었다."]], [["The author of the article was a young man, an invalid, very bold as a writer, but extremely deficient in breeding and shy in personal relations.", "그 글의 필자는 젊은이로, 병약한 몸이었고, 작가로서는 무척 대담했지만, 교양이 몹시 부족하고 개인적인 교제에서는 수줍음을 탔다."]], [["In spite of his absolute contempt for the author, it was with complete respect that Sergey Ivanovitch set about reading the article.", "필자에 대한 절대적인 경멸에도 불구하고, 세르게이 이바노비치는 더없이 정중한 태도로 그 글을 읽기 시작했다."], ["The article was awful.", "그 글은 끔찍했다."]], [["The critic had undoubtedly put an interpretation upon the book which could not possibly be put on it.", "비평가는 분명 그 책에 도저히 부여할 수 없는 해석을 부여해 놓았다."], ["But he had selected quotations so adroitly that for people who had not read the book (and obviously scarcely anyone had read it) it seemed absolutely clear that the whole book was nothing but a medley of high-flown phrases, not even—as suggested by marks of interrogation—used appropriately, and that the author of the book was a person absolutely without knowledge of the subject.", "그러나 그는 인용문을 어찌나 교묘하게 골라냈던지, 책을 읽지 않은 사람들에게는(그리고 분명 그 책을 읽은 사람은 거의 없었다) 이 책 전체가 거창한 문구들의 잡탕에 지나지 않으며, 그나마—물음표로 암시되었듯이—적절하게 쓰이지도 않았고, 책의 저자가 그 주제에 대해 아무런 지식도 없는 사람이라는 것이 너무도 명백해 보였다."], ["And all this was so wittily done that Sergey Ivanovitch would not have disowned such wit himself.", "그리고 이 모든 것이 어찌나 재치 있게 이루어졌던지, 세르게이 이바노비치 자신도 그런 재치라면 마다하지 않았을 정도였다."], ["But that was just what was so awful.", "그러나 바로 그 점이 그토록 끔찍했다."]], [["In spite of the scrupulous conscientiousness with which Sergey Ivanovitch verified the correctness of the critic’s arguments, he did not for a minute stop to ponder over the faults and mistakes which were ridiculed; but unconsciously he began immediately trying to recall every detail of his meeting and conversation with the author of the article.", "세르게이 이바노비치는 비평가의 논거가 옳은지 세심한 양심을 다해 검증했음에도, 조롱당한 결함과 오류를 곱씹어 보느라 한순간도 멈추지 않았다. 그 대신 자기도 모르게 곧바로 그 글의 필자와 만났던 일이며 나누었던 대화의 세세한 부분들을 하나하나 떠올리기 시작했다."]], [["“Didn’t I offend him in some way?”", "\"내가 어떤 식으로든 그를 모욕하지 않았던가?\""], ["Sergey Ivanovitch wondered.", "세르게이 이바노비치는 생각했다."]], [["And remembering that when they met he had corrected the young man about something he had said that betrayed ignorance, Sergey Ivanovitch found the clue to explain the article.", "그리고 두 사람이 만났을 때 자기가 그 젊은이의 무지를 드러내는 어떤 말을 바로잡아 준 일을 떠올리고는, 세르게이 이바노비치는 그 글을 설명할 실마리를 찾아냈다."]], [["This article was followed by a deadly silence about the book both in the press and in conversation, and Sergey Ivanovitch saw that his six years’ task, toiled at with such love and labor, had gone, leaving no trace.", "이 글이 나온 뒤로 언론에서도 대화에서도 그 책에 관해서는 죽음 같은 침묵이 이어졌고, 세르게이 이바노비치는 그토록 사랑과 노고를 기울여 매달려 온 육 년의 작업이 아무런 흔적도 남기지 못하고 사라져 버렸음을 깨달았다."]], [["Sergey Ivanovitch’s position was still more difficult from the fact that, since he had finished his book, he had had no more literary work to do, such as had hitherto occupied the greater part of his time.", "세르게이 이바노비치의 처지는 책을 끝낸 이후로, 지금껏 그의 시간 대부분을 차지해 왔던 그런 문필 작업이 더 이상 없다는 사실 때문에 한층 더 곤란해졌다."]], [["Sergey Ivanovitch was clever, cultivated, healthy, and energetic, and he did not know what use to make of his energy.", "세르게이 이바노비치는 영리하고, 교양 있고, 건강하고, 정력적이었으나, 자기 정력을 어디에 써야 할지 알지 못했다."], ["Conversations in drawing-rooms, in meetings, assemblies, and committees—everywhere where talk was possible—took up part of his time.", "응접실에서, 회합에서, 집회에서, 위원회에서—이야기가 가능한 곳이라면 어디서든 나누는 대화가 그의 시간 일부를 차지했다."], ["But being used for years to town life, he did not waste all his energies in talk, as his less experienced younger brother did, when he was in Moscow.", "그러나 여러 해 도시 생활에 익숙해진 터라, 그는 경험이 부족한 동생이 모스크바에 와 있을 때 그러듯이 자기 정력을 모조리 이야기에 허비하지는 않았다."], ["He had a great deal of leisure and intellectual energy still to dispose of.", "아직도 마음껏 쓸 수 있는 여가와 지적 정력이 그에게는 무척 많았다."]], [["Fortunately for him, at this period so difficult for him from the failure of his book, the various public questions of the dissenting sects, of the American alliance, of the Samara famine, of exhibitions, and of spiritualism, were definitely replaced in public interest by the Slavonic question, which had hitherto rather languidly interested society, and Sergey Ivanovitch, who had been one of the first to raise this subject, threw himself into it heart and soul.", "그에게는 다행스럽게도, 책의 실패로 그토록 힘겨웠던 이 시기에, 분리파 종파 문제며, 미국과의 동맹 문제며, 사마라의 기근 문제며, 전시회며, 강신술 같은 갖가지 공적 화제들이 대중의 관심 속에서 슬라브 문제로 확연히 대체되었다. 그것은 그때까지만 해도 사교계의 관심을 다소 미적지근하게 끌던 문제였는데, 일찍이 이 주제를 처음 제기한 사람 가운데 하나였던 세르게이 이바노비치는 거기에 온 마음과 영혼을 던져 넣었다."]], [["In the circle to which Sergey Ivanovitch belonged, nothing was talked of or written about just now but the Servian War.", "세르게이 이바노비치가 속한 모임에서는 지금 세르비아 전쟁 외에 달리 화제에 오르거나 글로 쓰이는 것이 없었다."], ["Everything that the idle crowd usually does to kill time was done now for the benefit of the Slavonic States.", "한가한 무리가 보통 시간을 죽이려고 하던 온갖 일들이 이제는 슬라브 국가들을 위해 행해졌다."], ["Balls, concerts, dinners, matchboxes, ladies’ dresses, beer, restaurants—everything testified to sympathy with the Slavonic peoples.", "무도회, 음악회, 만찬, 성냥갑, 부인들의 의상, 맥주, 식당—모든 것이 슬라브 민족에 대한 공감을 증언하고 있었다."]], [["From much of what was spoken and written on the subject, Sergey Ivanovitch differed on various points.", "이 주제를 두고 사람들이 말하고 쓰는 많은 것들에 대해, 세르게이 이바노비치는 여러 점에서 견해를 달리했다."], ["He saw that the Slavonic question had become one of those fashionable distractions which succeed one another in providing society with an object and an occupation.", "그는 슬라브 문제가 사교계에 대상과 소일거리를 제공하며 잇따라 나타나는 그런 유행하는 심심풀이 가운데 하나가 되어 버렸음을 알아보았다."], ["He saw, too, that a great many people were taking up the subject from motives of self-interest and self-advertisement.", "또한 그는 수많은 사람들이 사리사욕과 자기 과시의 동기에서 이 주제에 매달리고 있음도 알아보았다."], ["He recognized that the newspapers published a great deal that was superfluous and exaggerated, with the sole aim of attracting attention and outbidding one another.", "그는 신문들이 오로지 관심을 끌고 서로를 능가하려는 목적 하나로, 군더더기에다 과장된 것들을 잔뜩 게재하고 있음을 알아챘다."], ["He saw that in this general movement those who thrust themselves most forward and shouted the loudest were men who had failed and were smarting under a sense of injury—generals without armies, ministers not in the ministry, journalists not on any paper, party leaders without followers.", "그는 이 전반적인 움직임 속에서 가장 앞으로 나서고 가장 큰 소리로 외쳐 대는 자들이, 실패하여 모욕감에 쓰라려하는 사람들—군대 없는 장군들, 부처에 들지 못한 장관들, 어느 신문에도 소속되지 못한 언론인들, 추종자 없는 당수들—임을 알아보았다."], ["He saw that there was a great deal in it that was frivolous and absurd.", "그는 거기에 경박하고 터무니없는 면이 많다는 것도 알아보았다."], ["But he saw and recognized an unmistakable growing enthusiasm, uniting all classes, with which it was impossible not to sympathize.", "그러나 그는 모든 계층을 하나로 묶는, 공감하지 않을 수 없는 의심할 바 없이 자라나는 열정 또한 보았고 또 인정했다."], ["The massacre of men who were fellow Christians, and of the same Slavonic race, excited sympathy for the sufferers and indignation against the oppressors.", "같은 그리스도교도이자 같은 슬라브 민족인 사람들이 학살당했다는 사실은 그 수난자들에 대한 공감과 억압자들에 대한 분노를 불러일으켰다."], ["And the heroism of the Servians and Montenegrins struggling for a great cause begot in the whole people a longing to help their brothers not in word but in deed.", "그리고 위대한 대의를 위해 싸우는 세르비아인들과 몬테네그로인들의 영웅적 행위는 온 국민의 마음속에 말이 아니라 행동으로 자기 형제들을 돕고자 하는 갈망을 낳았다."]], [["But in this there was another aspect that rejoiced Sergey Ivanovitch.", "그러나 여기에는 세르게이 이바노비치를 기쁘게 하는 또 다른 측면이 있었다."], ["That was the manifestation of public opinion.", "그것은 바로 여론의 발현이었다."], ["The public had definitely expressed its desire.", "대중이 자신의 바람을 확연히 표명한 것이다."], ["The soul of the people had, as Sergey Ivanovitch said, found expression.", "세르게이 이바노비치의 말마따나, 민중의 영혼이 표현을 찾아낸 것이다."], ["And the more he worked in this cause, the more incontestable it seemed to him that it was a cause destined to assume vast dimensions, to create an epoch.", "그리고 이 대의를 위해 일하면 일할수록, 그것이 거대한 규모를 띠고 한 시대를 열도록 운명 지어진 대의라는 사실이 그에게는 더욱 반박할 수 없는 것으로 여겨졌다."]], [["He threw himself heart and soul into the service of this great cause, and forgot to think about his book.", "그는 이 위대한 대의에 봉사하는 일에 온 마음과 영혼을 던졌고, 자기 책에 대해 생각하는 것조차 잊어버렸다."], ["His whole time now was engrossed by it, so that he could scarcely manage to answer all the letters and appeals addressed to him.", "이제 그의 시간 전부가 그것에 사로잡혀, 자기에게 오는 온갖 편지와 호소에 일일이 답하는 것조차 간신히 해낼 정도였다."], ["He worked the whole spring and part of the summer, and it was only in July that he prepared to go away to his brother’s in the country.", "그는 봄 내내, 그리고 여름의 일부까지 그 일에 매달렸고, 시골에 있는 동생의 집으로 떠날 채비를 한 것은 칠월에 이르러서였다."]], [["He was going both to rest for a fortnight, and in the very heart of the people, in the farthest wilds of the country, to enjoy the sight of that uplifting of the spirit of the people, of which, like all residents in the capital and big towns, he was fully persuaded.", "그는 보름 동안 쉬려는 것이기도 했고, 또한 민중의 한복판에서, 시골의 가장 외진 벽지에서, 자신이 수도와 대도시의 모든 주민들과 마찬가지로 굳게 믿고 있던 그 민중의 정신적 고양을 직접 보고 즐기려는 것이기도 했다."], ["Katavasov had long been meaning to carry out his promise to stay with Levin, and so he was going with him.", "카타바소프는 레빈의 집에 머물겠다던 약속을 진작부터 지키려 하고 있었으므로, 그와 함께 떠나기로 했다."]], [["# Chapter 2", "# Chapter 2"]], [["Sergey Ivanovitch and Katavasov had only just reached the station of the Kursk line, which was particularly busy and full of people that day, when, looking round for the groom who was following with their things, they saw a party of volunteers driving up in four cabs.", "세르게이 이바노비치와 카타바소프는 마침 그날따라 유난히 붐비고 사람들로 가득한 쿠르스크 선 정거장에 막 도착한 참이었는데, 짐을 들고 뒤따라오는 마부를 찾으려고 주위를 둘러보다가 의용병 일행이 마차 네 대에 나누어 타고 다가오는 것을 보았다."], ["Ladies met them with bouquets of flowers, and followed by the rushing crowd they went into the station.", "여인들이 꽃다발을 들고 그들을 맞이했고, 밀려드는 군중을 뒤에 거느린 채 의용병들은 정거장 안으로 들어갔다."]], [["One of the ladies, who had met the volunteers, came out of the hall and addressed Sergey Ivanovitch.", "의용병들을 맞이한 여인들 가운데 한 사람이 홀에서 나와 세르게이 이바노비치에게 말을 걸었다."]], [["“You too come to see them off?”", "\"당신도 이분들을 배웅하러 오셨나요?\""], ["she asked in French.", "그녀가 프랑스어로 물었다."]], [["“No, I’m going away myself, princess.", "\"아닙니다, 공작 부인. 저는 떠나는 길입니다."], ["To my brother’s for a holiday.", "동생 집으로 휴가를 가려고요."], ["Do you always see them off?”", "부인은 늘 이분들을 배웅하시나요?\""], ["said Sergey Ivanovitch with a hardly perceptible smile.", "세르게이 이바노비치가 알아챌 듯 말 듯한 미소를 띠며 말했다."]], [["“Oh, that would be impossible!”", "\"오, 그건 도저히 무리예요!\""], ["answered the princess.", "공작 부인이 대답했다."], ["“Is it true that eight hundred have been sent from us already?", "\"우리 쪽에서 벌써 팔백 명이나 보냈다는 게 사실인가요?"], ["Malvinsky wouldn’t believe me.”", "말빈스키는 제 말을 믿으려 하질 않더군요.\""]], [["“More than eight hundred.", "\"팔백 명이 넘습니다."], ["If you reckon those who have been sent not directly from Moscow, over a thousand,”", "모스크바에서 직접 보내지 않은 사람들까지 헤아리면 천 명이 넘지요.\""], ["answered Sergey Ivanovitch.", "세르게이 이바노비치가 대답했다."]], [["“There!", "\"거 보세요!"], ["That’s just what I said!”", "제가 한 말이 바로 그거예요!\""], ["exclaimed the lady.", "부인이 소리쳤다."], ["“And it’s true too, I suppose, that more than a million has been subscribed?”", "\"그리고 백만 루블 넘게 모금되었다는 것도 사실이겠죠?\""]], [["“Yes, princess.”", "\"그렇습니다, 공작 부인.\""]], [["“What do you say to today’s telegram?", "\"오늘 온 전보는 어떻게 보세요?"], ["Beaten the Turks again.”", "또다시 터키군을 무찔렀다더군요.\""]], [["“Yes, so I saw,” answered Sergey Ivanovitch.", "\"네, 저도 봤습니다.\""], ["They were speaking of the last telegram stating that the Turks had been for three days in succession beaten at all points and put to flight, and that tomorrow a decisive engagement was expected.", "세르게이 이바노비치가 대답했다. 두 사람은 터키군이 사흘 연속으로 모든 지점에서 격파되어 패주했으며, 내일은 결정적인 교전이 예상된다는 마지막 전보 이야기를 하고 있었다."]], [["“Ah, by the way, a splendid young fellow has asked leave to go, and they’ve made some difficulty, I don’t know why.", "\"아, 그런데 말이죠, 아주 훌륭한 젊은이 하나가 가게 해 달라고 청을 넣었는데, 무슨 까닭인지 일을 까다롭게 만들어 놓았더군요. 당신께 부탁드리려 했어요."], ["I meant to ask you; I know him; please write a note about his case.", "저도 아는 청년이거든요. 부디 그 사람 일에 대해 쪽지를 한 장 써 주세요."], ["He’s being sent by Countess Lidia Ivanovna.”", "리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인이 보내는 사람이에요.\""]], [["Sergey Ivanovitch asked for all the details the princess knew about the young man, and going into the first-class waiting-room, wrote a note to the person on whom the granting of leave of absence depended, and handed it to the princess.", "세르게이 이바노비치는 공작 부인이 그 젊은이에 대해 아는 것을 죄다 물어본 뒤, 일등 대합실로 들어가 휴가 허가를 좌우하는 인물에게 쪽지를 써서 공작 부인에게 건넸다."]], [["“You know Count Vronsky, the notorious one ...", "\"당신도 그 악명 높은 브론스키 백작이……"], ["is going by this train?”", "이 기차로 떠난다는 거 아세요?\""], ["said the princess with a smile full of triumph and meaning, when he found her again and gave her the letter.", "그가 다시 부인을 찾아 편지를 건네주자, 부인은 승리감과 의미가 가득 담긴 미소를 지으며 말했다."]], [["“I had heard he was going, but I did not know when.", "\"떠난다는 얘기는 들었지만, 언제인지는 몰랐습니다."], ["By this train?”", "이 기차로요?\""]], [["“I’ve seen him.", "\"제가 봤어요."], ["He’s here: there’s only his mother seeing him off.", "여기 와 있어요. 어머니만 배웅하러 나왔더군요."], ["It’s the best thing, anyway, that he could do.”", "어쨌거나 그로서는 할 수 있는 가장 잘한 일이죠.\""]], [["“Oh, yes, of course.”", "\"오, 그럼요, 물론입니다.\""]], [["While they were talking the crowd streamed by them into the dining-room.", "두 사람이 이야기를 나누는 동안 군중이 그들 곁을 지나 식당으로 흘러 들어갔다."], ["They went forward too, and heard a gentleman with a glass in his hand delivering a loud discourse to the volunteers.", "두 사람도 앞으로 나아갔는데, 손에 잔을 든 한 신사가 의용병들을 향해 큰 소리로 연설을 늘어놓는 소리가 들렸다."], ["“In the service of religion, humanity, and our brothers,”", "\"신앙과 인류와 우리 형제들을 위한 봉사에 나서는……\""], ["the gentleman said, his voice growing louder and louder; “to this great cause mother Moscow dedicates you with her blessing.", "신사는 점점 더 목청을 높여 가며 말했다. \"이 위대한 대의에 어머니 모스크바가 축복으로 그대들을 바치노라."], ["Jivio!”", "지비오!\""], ["he concluded, loudly and tearfully.", "그는 큰 소리로, 눈물을 글썽이며 끝맺었다. * 지비오(Jivio): 세르비아어로 '만세'라는 뜻의 환호."]], [["Everyone shouted Jivio!", "모두가 \"지비오!\""], ["and a fresh crowd dashed into the hall, almost carrying the princess off her legs.", "하고 외쳤고, 새로운 군중이 홀 안으로 우르르 밀려들어 공작 부인을 하마터면 넘어뜨릴 뻔했다."]], [["“Ah, princess!", "\"아, 공작 부인!"], ["that was something like!”", "그것참 대단했지요!\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch, suddenly appearing in the middle of the crowd and beaming upon them with a delighted smile.", "군중 한가운데 불쑥 나타난 스테판 아르카지치가 기쁨에 넘치는 미소로 두 사람을 향해 환하게 웃으며 말했다."], ["“Capitally, warmly said, wasn’t it?", "\"근사하고 뜨겁게 말하지 않았습니까?"], ["Bravo!", "브라보!"], ["And Sergey Ivanovitch!", "그리고 세르게이 이바노비치!"], ["Why, you ought to have said something—just a few words, you know, to encourage them; you do that so well,”", "형님이야말로 뭔가 한마디 하셨어야지요. 그저 몇 마디로 저들을 격려하는 말 말입니다."], ["he added with a soft, respectful, and discreet smile, moving Sergey Ivanovitch forward a little by the arm.", "그런 건 형님이 아주 잘하시잖습니까.\" 그는 부드럽고 공손하면서도 조심스러운 미소를 지으며 덧붙이고는, 세르게이 이바노비치의 팔을 잡아 앞으로 조금 끌었다."]], [["“No, I’m just off.”", "\"아니, 난 막 떠나는 참이야.\""]], [["“Where to?”", "\"어디로요?\""]], [["“To the country, to my brother’s,”", "\"시골로, 동생 집으로 가는 길이야.\""], ["answered Sergey Ivanovitch.", "세르게이 이바노비치가 대답했다."]], [["“Then you’ll see my wife.", "\"그럼 제 아내를 보시겠군요."], ["I’ve written to her, but you’ll see her first.", "편지를 써 두긴 했지만, 형님이 먼저 만나실 테죠."], ["Please tell her that they’ve seen me and that it’s ‘all right,’ as the English say.", "부디 아내에게 사람들이 저를 만났고 영국인들 말마따나 '올 라이트'라고 전해 주세요."], ["She’ll understand.", "그러면 알아들을 겁니다."], ["Oh, and be so good as to tell her I’m appointed secretary of the committee....", "아, 그리고 제가 위원회 간사로 임명되었다고 전해 주시면 고맙겠습니다……"], ["But she’ll understand!", "하지만 아내는 알아들을 거예요!"], ["You know, les petites misères de la vie humaine,”", "아시잖습니까, les petites misères de la vie humaine 말입니다.\" 그는 마치 공작 부인에게 변명이라도 하듯 말했다."], ["he said, as it were apologizing to the princess.", "\"그리고 먀흐카야 공작 부인은요, 리자가 아니라 비비시 말입니다, 소총 천 정과 간호사 열두 명을 보내고 있어요. 제가 말씀드렸던가요?\""], ["“And Princess Myakaya—not Liza, but Bibish—is sending a thousand guns and twelve nurses. Did I tell you?”", "* les petites misères de la vie humaine: 프랑스어로 '인간 삶의 자질구레한 고충들'."]], [["“Yes, I heard so,”", "\"네, 들었습니다.\""], ["answered Koznishev indifferently.", "코즈니셰프가 무심하게 대답했다."]], [["“It’s a pity you’re going away,”", "\"형님이 떠나신다니 아쉽군요.\""], ["said Stepan Arkadyevitch.", "스테판 아르카지치가 말했다."], ["“Tomorrow we’re giving a dinner to two who’re setting off—Dimer-Bartnyansky from Petersburg and our Veslovsky, Grisha.", "\"내일 우리는 출발하는 두 사람에게 만찬을 베풀 겁니다. 페테르부르크에서 온 디메르바르트냔스키와 우리 베슬롭스키, 그리샤 말입니다."], ["They’re both going.", "둘 다 가거든요."], ["Veslovsky’s only lately married.", "베슬롭스키는 최근에야 결혼했지요."], ["There’s a fine fellow for you!", "정말 멋진 친구 아닙니까!"], ["Eh, princess?”", "그렇죠, 공작 부인?\""], ["he turned to the lady.", "그가 부인을 향해 돌아서며 말했다."]], [["The princess looked at Koznishev without replying.", "공작 부인은 아무 대답도 없이 코즈니셰프를 바라보았다."], ["But the fact that Sergey Ivanovitch and the princess seemed anxious to get rid of him did not in the least disconcert Stepan Arkadyevitch.", "하지만 세르게이 이바노비치와 공작 부인이 자기를 떼어 놓고 싶어 하는 기색을 보였는데도 스테판 아르카지치는 조금도 당황하지 않았다."], ["Smiling, he stared at the feather in the princess’s hat, and then about him as though he were going to pick something up.", "그는 미소를 지으며 공작 부인의 모자에 달린 깃털을 빤히 바라보다가, 무언가를 주워 들기라도 할 듯 주위를 둘러보았다."], ["Seeing a lady approaching with a collecting box, he beckoned her up and put in a five-rouble note.", "모금함을 든 부인 하나가 다가오는 것을 보고는 그녀를 손짓해 불러 5루블짜리 지폐를 넣었다."]], [["“I can never see these collecting boxes unmoved while I’ve money in my pocket,”", "\"호주머니에 돈이 있는 한 이런 모금함을 보고 마음이 동하지 않을 수가 없단 말이에요.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“And how about today’s telegram?", "\"그건 그렇고 오늘 전보는 어떻습니까?"], ["Fine chaps those Montenegrins!”", "그 몬테네그로 사람들, 대단한 친구들이지요!\""]], [["“You don’t say so!”", "\"설마요!\""], ["he cried, when the princess told him that Vronsky was going by this train.", "그는 브론스키가 이 기차로 떠난다는 공작 부인의 말에 이렇게 외쳤다."], ["For an instant Stepan Arkadyevitch’s face looked sad, but a minute later, when, stroking his mustaches and swinging as he walked, he went into the hall where Vronsky was, he had completely forgotten his own despairing sobs over his sister’s corpse, and he saw in Vronsky only a hero and an old friend.", "한순간 스테판 아르카지치의 얼굴이 슬퍼 보였지만, 잠시 후 콧수염을 쓰다듬으며 몸을 흔들흔들 걸어 브론스키가 있는 홀로 들어갈 때쯤에는, 누이의 주검 앞에서 절망에 빠져 흐느꼈던 일을 까맣게 잊은 채 브론스키에게서 오직 영웅이자 옛 친구의 모습만을 보고 있었다."]], [["“With all his faults one can’t refuse to do him justice,”", "\"여러 결점이 있긴 해도 저 사람의 공정함만은 인정하지 않을 수 없죠.\""], ["said the princess to Sergey Ivanovitch as soon as Stepan Arkadyevitch had left them.", "스테판 아르카지치가 자리를 뜨자마자 공작 부인이 세르게이 이바노비치에게 말했다."], ["“What a typically Russian, Slav nature!", "\"얼마나 전형적인 러시아적, 슬라브적 천성인지요!"], ["Only, I’m afraid it won’t be pleasant for Vronsky to see him.", "다만 브론스키로서는 저 사람을 보는 게 달갑지 않을 듯해 걱정이에요."], ["Say what you will, I’m touched by that man’s fate.", "뭐라고 하시든, 저는 저 사람의 운명에 마음이 짠해요."], ["Do talk to him a little on the way,”", "가시는 길에 저 사람과 얘기 좀 나눠 보세요.\""], ["said the princess.", "공작 부인이 말했다."]], [["“Yes, perhaps, if it happens so.”", "\"네, 그렇게 될지도 모르겠습니다. 그리되면요.\""]], [["“I never liked him.", "\"저는 그 사람을 한 번도 좋아한 적이 없어요."], ["But this atones for a great deal.", "하지만 이번 일로 많은 게 용서되는군요."], ["He’s not merely going himself, he’s taking a squadron at his own expense.” “Yes, so I heard.”", "그저 자기 혼자 가는 게 아니라, 자기 비용으로 기병 중대까지 데려가니까요.\""]], [["A bell sounded.", "\"네, 저도 그렇게 들었습니다.\""], ["Everyone crowded to the doors.", "종이 울렸다. 모두가 출입구로 몰려들었다."], ["“Here he is!”", "\"저기 저 사람이에요!\""], ["said the princess, indicating Vronsky, who with his mother on his arm walked by, wearing a long overcoat and wide-brimmed black hat.", "공작 부인이 브론스키를 가리키며 말했다. 그는 긴 외투를 걸치고 챙 넓은 검은 모자를 쓴 채 어머니를 팔로 부축하며 지나가고 있었다."], ["Oblonsky was walking beside him, talking eagerly of something.", "오블론스키가 그 곁에서 무언가를 열심히 떠들며 걷고 있었다."]], [["Vronsky was frowning and looking straight before him, as though he did not hear what Stepan Arkadyevitch was saying.", "브론스키는 미간을 찌푸린 채, 스테판 아르카지치의 말이 들리지도 않는다는 듯 똑바로 앞만 바라보고 있었다."]], [["Probably on Oblonsky’s pointing them out, he looked round in the direction where the princess and Sergey Ivanovitch were standing, and without speaking lifted his hat.", "아마 오블론스키가 그들을 가리켜 보인 모양인지, 그는 공작 부인과 세르게이 이바노비치가 서 있는 쪽으로 고개를 돌리더니, 아무 말 없이 모자를 들어 올렸다."], ["His face, aged and worn by suffering, looked stony.", "고통으로 늙고 수척해진 그의 얼굴은 돌처럼 굳어 보였다."]], [["Going onto the platform, Vronsky left his mother and disappeared into a compartment.", "플랫폼으로 나서며 브론스키는 어머니를 두고 어느 객실 안으로 사라졌다."]], [["On the platform there rang out “God save the Tsar,”", "플랫폼에서 \"하느님, 차르를 구하소서\"라는 노래가 울려 퍼지더니, 이어 \"우라!\"와 \"지비오!\""], ["then shouts of “hurrah!”", "하는 외침이 터져 나왔다."], ["and “jivio!” One of the volunteers, a tall, very young man with a hollow chest, was particularly conspicuous, bowing and waving his felt hat and a nosegay over his head.", "의용병 가운데 한 사람, 가슴이 움푹 꺼진 키 큰 새파란 젊은이가 특히 눈에 띄었는데, 그는 머리 위로 펠트 모자와 꽃다발을 흔들며 연신 고개를 숙이고 있었다."], ["Then two officers emerged, bowing too, and a stout man with a big beard, wearing a greasy forage cap.", "이어서 장교 둘이 역시 고개를 숙이며 나왔고, 기름때 묻은 군모를 쓴 커다란 턱수염의 뚱뚱한 사내도 모습을 드러냈다."]], [["# Chapter 3", "# Chapter 3"]], [["Saying good-bye to the princess, Sergey Ivanovitch was joined by Katavasov; together they got into a carriage full to overflowing, and the train started.", "공작 부인과 작별 인사를 나눈 세르게이 이바노비치는 카타바소프와 합류했다. 두 사람은 발 디딜 틈 없이 가득 찬 객차에 함께 올라탔고, 기차가 출발했다."]], [["At Tsaritsino station the train was met by a chorus of young men singing “Hail to Thee!”", "차리치노 역에서 기차는 \"그대에게 영광을!\"을 부르는 청년 합창단의 환영을 받았다."], ["Again the volunteers bowed and poked their heads out, but Sergey Ivanovitch paid no attention to them.", "의용병들은 또다시 고개를 숙이고 창밖으로 머리를 내밀었지만, 세르게이 이바노비치는 그들에게 아무런 관심도 두지 않았다."], ["He had had so much to do with the volunteers that the type was familiar to him and did not interest him.", "그는 의용병들과 워낙 많이 얽혀 본 터라 그 유형이 이미 익숙했고, 흥미를 느끼지 못했다."], ["Katavasov, whose scientific work had prevented his having a chance of observing them hitherto, was very much interested in them and questioned Sergey Ivanovitch.", "반면 학문 연구 탓에 그들을 관찰할 기회를 미처 갖지 못했던 카타바소프는 그들에게 큰 흥미를 느꼈고, 세르게이 이바노비치에게 이것저것 물었다."]], [["Sergey Ivanovitch advised him to go into the second-class and talk to them himself.", "세르게이 이바노비치는 그에게 직접 이등칸으로 가서 그들과 이야기해 보라고 권했다."], ["At the next station Katavasov acted on this suggestion.", "다음 정거장에서 카타바소프는 그 제안대로 했다."]], [["At the first stop he moved into the second-class and made the acquaintance of the volunteers.", "첫 정차역에서 그는 이등칸으로 옮겨가 의용병들과 인사를 텄다."], ["They were sitting in a corner of the carriage, talking loudly and obviously aware that the attention of the passengers and Katavasov as he got in was concentrated upon them.", "그들은 객차 한구석에 앉아 큰 소리로 떠들고 있었고, 승객들과 자기가 들어서는 카타바소프의 시선이 자기들에게 쏠려 있다는 것을 분명히 의식하고 있었다."], ["More loudly than all talked the tall, hollow-chested young man.", "누구보다도 크게 떠드는 사람은 키가 크고 가슴이 움푹 꺼진 청년이었다."], ["He was unmistakably tipsy, and was relating some story that had occurred at his school.", "그는 영락없이 술에 취해, 자기 학교에서 있었던 무슨 이야기를 늘어놓고 있었다."], ["Facing him sat a middle-aged officer in the Austrian military jacket of the Guards uniform.", "그 맞은편에는 근위대 제복인 오스트리아식 군용 재킷을 입은 중년 장교가 앉아 있었다."], ["He was listening with a smile to the hollow-chested youth, and occasionally pulling him up.", "그는 가슴 꺼진 청년의 이야기를 미소를 띤 채 듣다가 이따금 말을 끊었다."], ["The third, in an artillery uniform, was sitting on a box beside them.", "세 번째 사람은 포병 제복을 입고 그들 옆 상자 위에 앉아 있었다."], ["A fourth was asleep.", "네 번째 사람은 잠들어 있었다."]], [["Entering into conversation with the youth, Katavasov learned that he was a wealthy Moscow merchant who had run through a large fortune before he was two-and-twenty.", "청년과 이야기를 나누면서 카타바소프는 그가 스물두 살도 되기 전에 막대한 재산을 탕진해 버린 부유한 모스크바 상인이라는 것을 알게 되었다."], ["Katavasov did not like him, because he was unmanly and effeminate and sickly.", "카타바소프는 그가 마음에 들지 않았다. 사내답지 못하고 계집애 같으며 병약했기 때문이다."], ["He was obviously convinced, especially now after drinking, that he was performing a heroic action, and he bragged of it in the most unpleasant way.", "그는 분명, 특히 술을 마신 지금은 더더욱, 자기가 영웅적인 행동을 하고 있다고 확신하며 그것을 더없이 불쾌한 방식으로 떠벌리고 있었다."]], [["The second, the retired officer, made an unpleasant impression too upon Katavasov.", "두 번째 사람, 그 퇴역 장교 역시 카타바소프에게 불쾌한 인상을 주었다."], ["He was, it seemed, a man who had tried everything.", "그는 무슨 일이든 다 해 본 사람인 듯했다."], ["He had been on a railway, had been a land-steward, and had started factories, and he talked, quite without necessity, of all he had done, and used learned expressions quite inappropriately.", "철도에서 일했고, 영지 관리인 노릇도 했고, 공장을 차린 적도 있었는데, 그는 굳이 그럴 필요도 없는데 자기가 했던 일들을 죄다 늘어놓았고 학술적인 표현을 아주 엉뚱한 자리에 갖다 붙였다."]], [["The third, the artilleryman, on the contrary, struck Katavasov very favorably.", "반면 세 번째 사람, 그 포병은 카타바소프에게 무척 좋은 인상을 남겼다."], ["He was a quiet, modest fellow, unmistakably impressed by the knowledge of the officer and the heroic self-sacrifice of the merchant and saying nothing about himself.", "그는 조용하고 겸손한 사내로, 장교의 박식함과 상인의 영웅적인 자기희생에 영락없이 감명받은 듯했고, 자기 자신에 대해서는 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["When Katavasov asked him what had impelled him to go to Servia, he answered modestly:", "카타바소프가 그에게 무엇 때문에 세르비아로 가게 되었느냐고 물었을 때, 그는 겸손하게 대답했다."]], [["“Oh, well, everyone’s going.", "\"아, 뭐, 다들 가니까요."], ["The Servians want help, too.", "세르비아 사람들도 도움이 필요하고요."], ["I’m sorry for them.”", "그들이 딱해서요.\""]], [["“Yes, you artillerymen especially are scarce there,”", "\"그렇지요, 당신네 포병이 특히 거긴 부족하니까요.\""], ["said Katavasov.", "카타바소프가 말했다."]], [["“Oh, I wasn’t long in the artillery, maybe they’ll put me into the infantry or the cavalry.”", "\"아, 저는 포병에 오래 있지 않았어요. 어쩌면 보병이나 기병으로 보낼지도 모르죠.\""]], [["“Into the infantry when they need artillery more than anything?”", "\"포병이 무엇보다도 필요한 마당에 보병으로요?\" 카타바소프가 말했다."], ["said Katavasov, fancying from the artilleryman’s apparent age that he must have reached a fairly high grade.", "그는 포병의 겉보기 나이로 미루어 그가 꽤 높은 계급에 올라 있으리라 짐작했던 것이다."]], [["“I wasn’t long in the artillery; I’m a cadet retired,”", "\"포병에 오래 있지 않았어요. 사관후보생으로 퇴역했죠.\""], ["he said, and he began to explain how he had failed in his examination.", "그가 말하더니, 자기가 어떻게 시험에 떨어졌는지 설명하기 시작했다."]], [["All of this together made a disagreeable impression on Katavasov, and when the volunteers got out at a station for a drink, Katavasov would have liked to compare his unfavorable impression in conversation with someone.", "이 모든 것이 한데 어우러져 카타바소프에게 못마땅한 인상을 남겼다. 의용병들이 한잔하러 어느 역에 내렸을 때, 카타바소프는 누군가와 이야기를 나누며 자기의 좋지 못한 인상을 견주어 보고 싶었다."], ["There was an old man in the carriage, wearing a military overcoat, who had been listening all the while to Katavasov’s conversation with the volunteers.", "객차 안에는 군용 외투를 걸친 노인이 하나 있었는데, 그는 그동안 내내 카타바소프와 의용병들의 대화를 듣고 있었다."], ["When they were left alone, Katavasov addressed him.", "둘만 남게 되자 카타바소프는 그에게 말을 걸었다."]], [["“What different positions they come from, all those fellows who are going off there,”", "\"저기로 떠나는 저 친구들, 참 다양한 신분에서 왔군요.\""], ["Katavasov said vaguely, not wishing to express his own opinion, and at the same time anxious to find out the old man’s views.", "카타바소프는 자기 의견을 드러내고 싶지 않으면서도, 동시에 노인의 생각을 알아내고 싶어서 모호하게 말했다."]], [["The old man was an officer who had served on two campaigns.", "노인은 두 차례 출정에 참가한 적이 있는 장교였다."], ["He knew what makes a soldier, and judging by the appearance and the talk of those persons, by the swagger with which they had recourse to the bottle on the journey, he considered them poor soldiers.", "그는 무엇이 군인을 만드는지 알았고, 그 사람들의 겉모습과 말투로 보아, 또 여행길에 술병에 손을 대는 그 허세로 보아, 그들을 형편없는 군인으로 여겼다."], ["Moreover, he lived in a district town, and he was longing to tell how one soldier had volunteered from his town, a drunkard and a thief whom no one would employ as a laborer.", "게다가 그는 어느 군청 소재지에 살고 있었는데, 자기 고장에서 의용병으로 자원한 어떤 병사 이야기를 들려주고 싶어 입이 근질거렸다. 그자는 술꾼에 도둑이라 아무도 일꾼으로 쓰려 하지 않던 자였다."], ["But knowing by experience that in the present condition of the public temper it was dangerous to express an opinion opposed to the general one, and especially to criticize the volunteers unfavorably, he too watched Katavasov without committing himself.", "하지만 지금 같은 여론의 분위기에서는 일반적인 의견에 반하는 견해를 입에 올리는 것이, 특히 의용병들을 나쁘게 비판하는 것이 위험하다는 것을 경험으로 알고 있었던 터라, 그 역시 자기 속내를 드러내지 않은 채 카타바소프를 살폈다."]], [["“Well, men are wanted there,”", "\"뭐, 저기엔 사람이 필요하니까요.\""], ["he said, laughing with his eyes.", "그가 눈으로 웃으며 말했다."], ["And they fell to talking of the last war news, and each concealed from the other his perplexity as to the engagement expected next day, since the Turks had been beaten, according to the latest news, at all points.", "그러고서 둘은 최근의 전쟁 소식 이야기로 넘어갔는데, 최근 소식에 따르면 터키군이 모든 전선에서 패한 까닭에, 이튿날 예정된 교전을 두고 품은 저마다의 의혹을 서로에게 감추었다."], ["And so they parted, neither giving expression to his opinion.", "그렇게 두 사람은 어느 쪽도 제 의견을 드러내지 않은 채 헤어졌다."]], [["Katavasov went back to his own carriage, and with reluctant hypocrisy reported to Sergey Ivanovitch his observations of the volunteers, from which it would appear that they were capital fellows.", "카타바소프는 자기 객차로 돌아와, 마지못해 위선을 부리며 의용병들을 관찰한 바를 세르게이 이바노비치에게 보고했는데, 그 말대로라면 그들은 훌륭한 친구들인 듯했다."]], [["At a big station at a town the volunteers were again greeted with shouts and singing, again men and women with collecting boxes appeared, and provincial ladies brought bouquets to the volunteers and followed them into the refreshment room; but all this was on a much smaller and feebler scale than in Moscow.", "어느 도시의 큰 역에서 의용병들은 또다시 함성과 노래로 환영받았고, 또다시 모금함을 든 남녀가 나타났으며, 지방 부인들이 의용병들에게 꽃다발을 건네고 그들을 따라 식당칸까지 따라 들어왔다. 하지만 이 모든 것은 모스크바에서보다는 훨씬 작고 미약한 규모였다."]], [["# Chapter 4", "# Chapter 4"]], [["While the train was stopping at the provincial town, Sergey Ivanovitch did not go to the refreshment room, but walked up and down the platform.", "기차가 그 지방 도시에 정차해 있는 동안, 세르게이 이바노비치는 식당칸으로 가지 않고 플랫폼을 이리저리 거닐었다."]], [["The first time he passed Vronsky’s compartment he noticed that the curtain was drawn over the window; but as he passed it the second time he saw the old countess at the window.", "브론스키의 객실 앞을 처음 지나칠 때 그는 창문에 커튼이 드리워져 있는 것을 알아챘다. 하지만 두 번째로 그 앞을 지날 때, 그는 창가에 앉은 노백작 부인을 보았다."], ["She beckoned to Koznishev.", "부인이 코즈니셰프에게 손짓했다."]], [["“I’m going, you see, taking him as far as Kursk,”", "\"보다시피 나도 가는 길이에요, 쿠르스크까지 저 애를 바래다주려고요.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“Yes, so I heard,”", "\"네, 그렇게 들었습니다.\""], ["said Sergey Ivanovitch, standing at her window and peeping in.", "세르게이 이바노비치가 그녀의 창가에 서서 안을 들여다보며 말했다."], ["“What a noble act on his part!”", "\"참으로 고결한 일이지요!\""], ["he added, noticing that Vronsky was not in the compartment.", "그는 객실 안에 브론스키가 없는 것을 알아채고는 덧붙였다."]], [["“Yes, after his misfortune, what was there for him to do?”", "\"그래요, 그런 불행을 겪었으니, 저 애가 달리 뭘 할 수 있었겠어요?\""]], [["“What a terrible thing it was!”", "\"정말 끔찍한 일이었습니다!\""], ["said Sergey Ivanovitch.", "세르게이 이바노비치가 말했다."]], [["“Ah, what I have been through!", "\"아, 내가 무슨 일을 겪었는지!"], ["But do get in....", "그러지 말고 들어와요……"], ["Ah, what I have been through!”", "아, 내가 무슨 일을 겪었는지!\""], ["she repeated, when Sergey Ivanovitch had got in and sat down beside her.", "세르게이 이바노비치가 객실 안으로 들어와 그녀 곁에 앉자 그녀가 거듭 말했다."], ["“You can’t conceive it!", "\"당신은 상상도 못 할 거예요!"], ["For six weeks he did not speak to anyone, and would not touch food except when I implored him.", "그 애는 여섯 주 동안 누구와도 말을 하지 않았고, 내가 사정사정할 때를 빼고는 음식에 손도 대지 않았어요."], ["And not for one minute could we leave him alone.", "단 한 순간도 그 애를 혼자 둘 수가 없었지요."], ["We took away everything he could have used against himself.", "우리는 그 애가 제 몸에 해를 입힐 만한 것들은 모조리 치워 버렸어요."], ["We lived on the ground floor, but there was no reckoning on anything.", "우리는 일 층에서 지냈는데, 그래도 아무것도 장담할 수가 없었어요."], ["You know, of course, that he had shot himself once already on her account,”", "물론 당신도 알겠지만, 그 애는 그 여자 때문에 이미 한 번 스스로 총을 쏜 적이 있었어요.\""], ["she said, and the old lady’s eyelashes twitched at the recollection.", "그녀가 말했고, 그 일을 떠올리자 노부인의 속눈썹이 파르르 떨렸다."], ["“Yes, hers was the fitting end for such a woman.", "\"그래요, 그런 여자에게는 그게 어울리는 최후였어요."], ["Even the death she chose was low and vulgar.”", "그 여자가 택한 죽음마저도 천하고 비속했지요.\""]], [["“It’s not for us to judge, countess,”", "\"우리가 판단할 일은 아니지요, 백작 부인.\""], ["said Sergey Ivanovitch; “but I can understand that it has been very hard for you.”", "세르게이 이바노비치가 말했다. \"하지만 부인께서 무척 힘드셨으리라는 건 이해할 수 있습니다.\""]], [["“Ah, don’t speak of it!", "\"아, 그 얘긴 하지 말아요!"], ["I was staying on my estate, and he was with me.", "나는 영지에 머물고 있었고, 그 애도 나와 함께 있었어요."], ["A note was brought him.", "그 애한테 쪽지 하나가 전해졌지요."], ["He wrote an answer and sent it off.", "그 애는 답장을 써서 보냈어요."], ["We hadn’t an idea that she was close by at the station.", "우리는 그 여자가 바로 근처 역에 와 있는 줄은 꿈에도 몰랐어요."], ["In the evening I had only just gone to my room, when my Mary told me a lady had thrown herself under the train.", "저녁 무렵, 막 내 방으로 돌아갔을 때, 우리 마리가 어떤 부인이 기차에 몸을 던졌다고 알려 주더군요."], ["Something seemed to strike me at once.", "순간 무언가가 나를 내리치는 것 같았어요."], ["I knew it was she.", "나는 그게 그 여자라는 걸 알았지요."], ["The first thing I said was, he was not to be told.", "내가 맨 처음 한 말은, 저 애한테는 알리지 말라는 거였어요."], ["But they’d told him already.", "하지만 사람들은 이미 그 애한테 말해 버린 뒤였어요."], ["His coachman was there and saw it all.", "그 애의 마부가 거기 있었고, 모든 걸 보았던 거예요."], ["When I ran into his room, he was beside himself—it was fearful to see him.", "내가 그 애 방으로 뛰어들었을 때, 그 애는 제정신이 아니었어요. 보기에도 무서울 지경이었지요."], ["He didn’t say a word, but galloped off there.", "그 애는 한마디도 하지 않은 채 그곳으로 말을 몰아 달려가 버렸어요."], ["I don’t know to this day what happened there, but he was brought back at death’s door.", "거기서 무슨 일이 있었는지는 지금까지도 모르지만, 그 애는 사경을 헤매는 상태로 실려 왔어요."], ["I shouldn’t have known him.", "나는 그 애를 알아보지도 못했을 거예요."], ["Prostration complète, the doctor said.", "Prostration complète, 라고 의사가 말했어요."], ["And that was followed almost by madness.", "그러고는 거의 광기에 가까운 상태가 뒤따랐지요."], ["Oh, why talk of it!”", "아, 그 얘긴 해서 뭘 하겠어요!\""], ["said the countess with a wave of her hand.", "백작 부인이 손을 내저으며 말했다."], ["“It was an awful time!", "\"끔찍한 시간이었어요!"], ["No, say what you will, she was a bad woman.", "아니, 당신이 뭐라 하든, 그 여자는 나쁜 여자였어요."], ["Why, what is the meaning of such desperate passions?", "대체 그런 절망적인 격정이 무슨 의미가 있단 말인가요?"], ["It was all to show herself something out of the way.", "모두 자기가 무언가 남다른 사람이라는 걸 과시하려던 거였어요."], ["Well, and that she did do.", "그래요, 그것만은 해냈지요."], ["She brought herself to ruin and two good men—her husband and my unhappy son.”", "그 여자는 자기 자신을 파멸로 몰아넣었고, 두 명의 선량한 남자, 그러니까 제 남편과 내 불행한 아들까지도 파멸시켰어요.\" * Prostration complète: 프랑스어로 '완전한 쇠약(허탈)'."]], [["“And what did her husband do?”", "\"그러면 그 여자의 남편은 어떻게 했습니까?\""], ["asked Sergey Ivanovitch.", "세르게이 이바노비치가 물었다."]], [["“He has taken her daughter.", "\"그 사람이 딸을 데려갔어요."], ["Alexey was ready to agree to anything at first.", "알렉세이는 처음엔 무엇이든 받아들일 마음이었지요."], ["Now it worries him terribly that he should have given his own child away to another man.", "지금은 제 자식을 남에게 넘겨 버렸다는 게 그 애를 몹시 괴롭히고 있어요."], ["But he can’t take back his word.", "하지만 한번 한 말을 거둘 수는 없는 노릇이지요."], ["Karenin came to the funeral.", "카레닌은 장례식에 왔어요."], ["But we tried to prevent his meeting Alexey.", "하지만 우리는 그가 알렉세이와 마주치지 않도록 막으려 애썼어요."], ["For him, for her husband, it was easier, anyway.", "그에게는, 그 여자의 남편에게는, 어쨌든 그게 더 견디기 쉬운 일이었지요."], ["She had set him free.", "그 여자가 그를 자유롭게 풀어 준 셈이니까요."], ["But my poor son was utterly given up to her.", "하지만 내 가엾은 아들은 그 여자에게 완전히 자신을 내맡겼어요."], ["He had thrown up everything, his career, me, and even then she had no mercy on him, but of set purpose she made his ruin complete.", "그 애는 모든 걸 내던졌지요, 경력도, 나도. 그런데도 그 여자는 그 애를 가엾게 여기기는커녕, 작정한 듯 그 애를 완전히 파멸시켜 버렸어요."], ["No, say what you will, her very death was the death of a vile woman, of no religious feeling.", "아니, 당신이 뭐라 하든, 그 여자의 죽음 자체가 비열한 여자의, 신앙심이라곤 없는 여자의 죽음이었어요. 하느님, 용서하소서."], ["God forgive me, but I can’t help hating the memory of her, when I look at my son’s misery!”", "하지만 내 아들의 비참한 꼴을 보고 있으면, 나는 그 여자에 대한 기억을 미워하지 않을 수가 없어요!\""]], [["“But how is he now?”", "\"그런데 지금 그 애는 어떻습니까?\""]], [["“It was a blessing from Providence for us—this Servian war.", "\"우리에게는 하늘이 내려 준 축복이었어요, 이 세르비아 전쟁 말이에요."], ["I’m old, and I don’t understand the rights and wrongs of it, but it’s come as a providential blessing to him.", "나는 늙어서 그게 옳은지 그른지는 모르지만, 그 애한테는 신의 섭리가 내린 축복처럼 찾아왔어요. 물론 그 애의 어미인 나로서는 끔찍한 일이지요."], ["Of course for me, as his mother, it’s terrible; and what’s worse, they say, ce n’est pas très bien vu à Pétersbourg.", "게다가 더 나쁜 건, 사람들 말로는, ce n'est pas très bien vu à Pétersbourg 하다는 거예요."], ["But it can’t be helped!", "하지만 어쩔 도리가 없어요!"], ["It was the one thing that could rouse him.", "그 애를 다시 일으켜 세울 수 있는 유일한 길이었으니까요."], ["Yashvin—a friend of his—he had lost all he had at cards and he was going to Servia.", "야시빈이라고, 그 애 친구가 있는데, 카드로 가진 걸 모조리 잃고 세르비아로 가려던 참이었어요. 그가 그 애를 찾아와 함께 가자고 설득했지요."], ["He came to see him and persuaded him to go.", "이제 그 애한테는 그것이 하나의 관심거리가 되었어요."], ["Now it’s an interest for him.", "제발 그 애와 잠깐 이야기를 나눠 줘요."], ["Do please talk to him a little.", "나는 그 애의 마음을 다른 데로 돌려 주고 싶어요."], ["I want to distract his mind.", "그 애는 너무나 의기소침해 있어요."], ["He’s so low-spirited.", "게다가 운 나쁘게도 치통까지 앓고 있지요."], ["And as bad luck would have it, he has toothache too.", "하지만 당신을 보면 기뻐할 거예요. 부디 그 애와 이야기를 나눠 줘요."], ["But he’ll be delighted to see you.", "그 애는 저쪽에서 이리저리 거닐고 있어요.\""], ["Please do talk to him; he’s walking up and down on that side.”", "* ce n'est pas très bien vu à Pétersbourg: 프랑스어로 '페테르부르크에서는 그다지 좋게 보지 않는다'."]], [["Sergey Ivanovitch said he would be very glad to, and crossed over to the other side of the station.", "세르게이 이바노비치는 기꺼이 그러겠노라고 말하고는 역의 반대편으로 건너갔다."]], [["# Chapter 5", "# Chapter 5"]], [["In the slanting evening shadows cast by the baggage piled up on the platform, Vronsky in his long overcoat and slouch hat, with his hands in his pockets, strode up and down, like a wild beast in a cage, turning sharply after twenty paces.", "플랫폼에 쌓아 올린 짐이 저녁 비스듬한 그림자를 드리운 가운데, 긴 외투에 챙 늘어진 모자를 쓴 브론스키가 두 손을 주머니에 찌른 채, 우리에 갇힌 야수처럼 스무 걸음마다 홱 돌아서며 오락가락 거닐고 있었다."], ["Sergey Ivanovitch fancied, as he approached him, that Vronsky saw him but was pretending not to see.", "세르게이 이바노비치는 그에게 다가가면서, 브론스키가 자기를 보았으면서도 못 본 척하고 있다고 느꼈다."], ["This did not affect Sergey Ivanovitch in the slightest.", "하지만 그런 것은 세르게이 이바노비치에게 조금도 영향을 주지 않았다."], ["He was above all personal considerations with Vronsky.", "브론스키와 관련해서 그는 그 모든 개인적인 고려를 초월해 있었다."]], [["At that moment Sergey Ivanovitch looked upon Vronsky as a man taking an important part in a great cause, and Koznishev thought it his duty to encourage him and express his approval.", "그 순간 세르게이 이바노비치는 브론스키를 위대한 대의에서 중요한 역할을 맡은 인물로 바라보았고, 코즈니셰프는 그를 격려하고 자기의 찬동을 표하는 것을 자기 의무라고 여겼다."], ["He went up to him.", "그는 브론스키에게 다가갔다."]], [["Vronsky stood still, looked intently at him, recognized him, and going a few steps forward to meet him, shook hands with him very warmly.", "브론스키는 걸음을 멈추고 그를 뚫어지게 바라보다가, 그를 알아보고는 몇 걸음 앞으로 나아가 그를 맞이하며 아주 따뜻하게 악수를 나누었다."]], [["“Possibly you didn’t wish to see me,”", "\"아마 저를 만나고 싶지 않으셨겠지요.\""], ["said Sergey Ivanovitch, “but couldn’t I be of use to you?”", "세르게이 이바노비치가 말했다. \"하지만 제가 뭔가 도움이 될 수는 없을까요?\""]], [["“There’s no one I should less dislike seeing than you,”", "\"제가 만나기를 가장 덜 꺼리는 사람이 있다면 바로 당신입니다.\""], ["said Vronsky.", "브론스키가 말했다."], ["“Excuse me; and there’s nothing in life for me to like.”", "\"용서하십시오. 그리고 제게는 인생에서 좋아할 만한 게 아무것도 없습니다.\""]], [["“I quite understand, and I merely meant to offer you my services,”", "\"잘 알겠습니다. 저는 그저 제 도움을 드리고 싶었을 뿐입니다.\""], ["said Sergey Ivanovitch, scanning Vronsky’s face, full of unmistakable suffering.", "세르게이 이바노비치가, 틀림없는 고통으로 가득 찬 브론스키의 얼굴을 살피며 말했다."], ["“Wouldn’t it be of use to you to have a letter to Ristitch—to Milan?”", "\"리스티치에게—밀란에게 보내는 소개장이 있으면 도움이 되지 않겠습니까?\""]], [["“Oh, no!”", "\"아, 아닙니다!\""], ["Vronsky said, seeming to understand him with difficulty.", "브론스키가 그의 말을 가까스로 이해하는 듯한 표정으로 말했다."], ["“If you don’t mind, let’s walk on.", "\"괜찮으시다면, 계속 걸읍시다."], ["It’s so stuffy among the carriages.", "객차 사이는 너무 답답하군요."], ["A letter?", "소개장이라니요?"], ["No, thank you; to meet death one needs no letters of introduction.", "아니요, 고맙습니다. 죽음을 맞이하는 데에는 소개장 따위 필요 없습니다."], ["Nor for the Turks....”", "터키군에게도 마찬가지고요…….\""], ["he said, with a smile that was merely of the lips.", "그가 입술만으로 짓는 미소를 띠며 말했다."], ["His eyes still kept their look of angry suffering.", "그의 눈은 여전히 성난 고통의 빛을 간직하고 있었다."]], [["“Yes; but you might find it easier to get into relations, which are after all essential, with anyone prepared to see you.", "\"그렇지요. 하지만 어쨌든 꼭 필요한 관계를, 당신을 만나려 하는 누군가와 맺는 데에 좀 더 수월할 수도 있습니다."], ["But that’s as you like.", "하지만 그건 당신 뜻대로 하시지요."], ["I was very glad to hear of your intention.", "저는 당신의 의도를 듣고 무척 기뻤습니다."], ["There have been so many attacks made on the volunteers, and a man like you raises them in public estimation.”", "의용병들에 대한 비난이 그토록 많았는데, 당신 같은 사람이 그들의 평판을 세상의 평가에서 끌어올려 주니까요.\""]], [["“My use as a man,”", "\"한 인간으로서 제 쓸모는,\""], ["said Vronsky, “is that life’s worth nothing to me.", "브론스키가 말했다. \"제게 삶이 아무런 가치도 없다는 데에 있지요."], ["And that I’ve enough bodily energy to cut my way into their ranks, and to trample on them or fall—I know that.", "그리고 제게는 그들의 대열을 헤치고 들어가 그들을 짓밟거나 아니면 쓰러질 만큼의 육체적 기력이 있다는 것—그건 저도 압니다."], ["I’m glad there’s something to give my life for, for it’s not simply useless but loathsome to me.", "제 목숨을 바칠 무언가가 있다는 게 기쁩니다. 제 목숨은 그저 쓸모없을 뿐 아니라 혐오스러우니까요."], ["Anyone’s welcome to it.”", "누구든 가져가도 좋습니다.\""], ["And his jaw twitched impatiently from the incessant gnawing toothache, that prevented him from even speaking with a natural expression.", "그러고는 끊임없이 갉아 대는 치통 탓에, 그것이 자연스러운 표정으로 말하는 것조차 가로막아, 그의 턱이 안달하듯 씰룩거렸다."]], [["“You will become another man, I predict,”", "\"당신은 다른 사람이 될 겁니다, 제가 장담하지요.\""], ["said Sergey Ivanovitch, feeling touched.", "세르게이 이바노비치가 감동을 느끼며 말했다."], ["“To deliver one’s brother-men from bondage is an aim worth death and life.", "\"형제들을 속박에서 해방시키는 것은 죽음도 삶도 바칠 만한 목적입니다."], ["God grant you success outwardly—and inwardly peace,”", "신께서 당신에게 겉으로는 성공을, 안으로는 평화를 내려 주시기를.\""], ["he added, and he held out his hand.", "그가 이렇게 덧붙이고는 손을 내밀었다."], ["Vronsky warmly pressed his outstretched hand.", "브론스키는 그가 내민 손을 따뜻하게 잡았다."]], [["“Yes, as a weapon I may be of some use.", "\"그렇지요, 하나의 무기로서라면 저도 얼마간 쓸모가 있을지 모릅니다."], ["But as a man, I’m a wreck,”", "하지만 한 인간으로서 저는 폐인입니다.\""], ["he jerked out.", "그가 툭 내뱉었다."]], [["He could hardly speak for the throbbing ache in his strong teeth, that were like rows of ivory in his mouth.", "입 안의 상아 줄 같은 튼튼한 이가 욱신거리는 통증 때문에 그는 거의 말을 잇지 못했다."], ["He was silent, and his eyes rested on the wheels of the tender, slowly and smoothly rolling along the rails.", "그는 입을 다물었고, 그의 눈은 레일 위를 느리고 매끄럽게 굴러가는 탄수차의 바퀴에 머물렀다."]], [["And all at once a different pain, not an ache, but an inner trouble, that set his whole being in anguish, made him for an instant forget his toothache.", "그런데 별안간 다른 종류의 아픔이, 욱신거리는 통증이 아니라 그의 온 존재를 고뇌에 빠뜨리는 내적인 괴로움이, 한순간 그로 하여금 치통을 잊게 만들었다."], ["As he glanced at the tender and the rails, under the influence of the conversation with a friend he had not met since his misfortune, he suddenly recalled her—that is, what was left of her when he had run like one distraught into the cloak room of the railway station—on the table, shamelessly sprawling out among strangers, the bloodstained body so lately full of life; the head unhurt dropping back with its weight of hair, and the curling tresses about the temples, and the exquisite face, with red, half-opened mouth, the strange, fixed expression, piteous on the lips and awful in the still open eyes, that seemed to utter that fearful phrase—that he would be sorry for it—that she had said when they were quarreling.", "탄수차와 레일을 바라보면서, 불행을 겪은 이후 처음 만난 친구와 나눈 대화의 영향 아래, 그는 문득 그녀를 떠올렸다—그러니까 그가 넋이 나간 사람처럼 철도역의 화물 보관실로 뛰어들었을 때 그녀에게 남아 있던 것을—탁자 위에, 낯선 사람들 틈에서 부끄러운 줄도 모르고 아무렇게나 널브러진 채, 그토록 얼마 전까지만 해도 생명으로 가득했던 피투성이 몸뚱이를. 상하지 않은 머리는 풍성한 머리채의 무게에 짓눌려 뒤로 떨어져 있었고, 관자놀이 주위에는 곱슬곱슬한 머리 타래가 늘어져 있었으며, 아름다운 얼굴에는 붉은, 반쯤 벌어진 입과, 입술에는 애처롭고 여전히 뜬 두 눈에는 무시무시한, 그 기묘하고 굳어 버린 표정이 있었는데, 그 표정은 그들이 다투던 때 그녀가 했던 그 끔찍한 말—그가 이것을 후회하게 될 거라던 그 말을 내뱉는 듯했다."]], [["And he tried to think of her as she was when he met her the first time, at a railway station too, mysterious, exquisite, loving, seeking and giving happiness, and not cruelly revengeful as he remembered her on that last moment.", "그리고 그는 그녀가 처음 만났을 때의 모습, 그때도 철도역에서였던, 신비롭고 아름답고 사랑스러우며, 행복을 찾고 또 주려 했던 모습으로, 그가 마지막 순간에 기억하던 잔혹하게 복수심에 불타던 모습이 아닌 그런 모습으로 그녀를 떠올리려 애썼다."], ["He tried to recall his best moments with her, but those moments were poisoned forever.", "그는 그녀와 함께한 가장 좋았던 순간들을 떠올리려 했지만, 그 순간들은 영원히 더럽혀져 버렸다."], ["He could only think of her as triumphant, successful in her menace of a wholly useless remorse never to be effaced.", "그는 오로지 그녀가 의기양양하게, 결코 지워질 수 없을 전혀 쓸모없는 회한이라는 그 협박을 성공시킨 모습으로밖에 떠올릴 수 없었다."], ["He lost all consciousness of toothache, and his face worked with sobs.", "그는 치통의 의식을 완전히 잃었고, 그의 얼굴은 흐느낌으로 일그러졌다."]], [["Passing twice up and down beside the baggage in silence and regaining his self-possession, he addressed Sergey Ivanovitch calmly:", "말없이 짐 옆을 두 번 오르락내리락한 뒤 그는 평정을 되찾고, 세르게이 이바노비치에게 침착하게 말을 건넸다."]], [["“You have had no telegrams since yesterday’s?", "\"어제 이후로 전보는 받지 못하셨습니까?"], ["Yes, driven back for a third time, but a decisive engagement expected for tomorrow.”", "그렇지요, 세 번째로 격퇴당했지만, 내일은 결정적인 교전이 예상된다고 하더군요.\""]], [["And after talking a little more of King Milan’s proclamation, and the immense effect it might have, they parted, going to their carriages on hearing the second bell.", "그리고 밀란 왕의 포고문과 그것이 미칠 수도 있는 엄청난 영향에 대해 좀 더 이야기를 나눈 뒤, 두 번째 종소리를 듣고 두 사람은 각자의 객차로 향하며 헤어졌다."]], [["# Chapter 6", "# Chapter 6"]], [["Sergey Ivanovitch had not telegraphed to his brother to send to meet him, as he did not know when he should be able to leave Moscow.", "세르게이 이바노비치는 자기가 언제 모스크바를 떠날 수 있을지 알 수 없었기에, 마중을 나오라고 동생에게 전보를 치지 않았다."], ["Levin was not at home when Katavasov and Sergey Ivanovitch in a fly hired at the station drove up to the steps of the Pokrovskoe house, as black as Moors from the dust of the road.", "카타바소프와 세르게이 이바노비치가 역에서 빌린 마차를 타고 길 먼지를 뒤집어써 무어인처럼 까매진 채 포크로프스코예 저택의 현관 앞에 다다랐을 때, 레빈은 집에 없었다."], ["Kitty, sitting on the balcony with her father and sister, recognized her brother-in-law, and ran down to meet him.", "발코니에서 아버지와 언니와 함께 앉아 있던 키티는 형부를 알아보고 마중하러 뛰어 내려갔다."]], [["“What a shame not to have let us know,”", "\"기별도 안 주시다니, 너무하세요.\""], ["she said, giving her hand to Sergey Ivanovitch, and putting her forehead up for him to kiss.", "그녀가 세르게이 이바노비치에게 손을 내밀고 입을 맞추도록 이마를 들이밀며 말했다."]], [["“We drove here capitally, and have not put you out,”", "\"썩 잘 달려와서, 자네들을 번거롭게 하지도 않았는걸.\""], ["answered Sergey Ivanovitch.", "세르게이 이바노비치가 대답했다."], ["“I’m so dirty. I’m afraid to touch you.", "\"이렇게 지저분해서 손도 못 대겠구나."], ["I’ve been so busy, I didn’t know when I should be able to tear myself away.", "너무 바빠서 언제 빠져나올 수 있을지 가늠이 안 됐지."], ["And so you’re still as ever enjoying your peaceful, quiet happiness,”", "그래, 너는 여전히 그 평온하고 조용한 행복을 누리고 있구나.\""], ["he said, smiling, “out of the reach of the current in your peaceful backwater.", "그가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"이 잔잔한 후미에서 세상의 물살이 닿지 않는 곳에 말이야."], ["Here’s our friend Fyodor Vassilievitch who has succeeded in getting here at last.”", "여기 우리 친구 표도르 바실리예비치도 마침내 여기까지 오는 데 성공했단다.\""]], [["“But I’m not a negro, I shall look like a human being when I wash,”", "\"하지만 저는 흑인이 아니라서, 씻고 나면 사람 꼴이 될 겁니다.\""], ["said Katavasov in his jesting fashion, and he shook hands and smiled, his teeth flashing white in his black face.", "카타바소프가 농을 던지듯 말하며 악수를 청하고 미소를 지었는데, 까만 얼굴 속에서 이가 하얗게 반짝였다."]], [["“Kostya will be delighted.", "\"코스챠가 아주 기뻐할 거예요."], ["He has gone to his settlement. It’s time he should be home.”", "농장에 가 있는데, 이제 돌아올 때가 됐어요.\""]], [["“Busy as ever with his farming.", "\"여전히 농사일로 바쁘시군요."], ["It really is a peaceful backwater,”", "정말이지 잔잔한 후미야.\""], ["said Katavasov; “while we in town think of nothing but the Servian war.", "카타바소프가 말했다. \"우리는 도시에서 세르비아 전쟁 말고는 아무 생각도 없는데."], ["Well, how does our friend look at it?", "그래, 우리 친구는 그 일을 어떻게 보고 있습니까?"], ["He’s sure not to think like other people.”", "분명 남들과는 다르게 생각하겠지요.\""]], [["“Oh, I don’t know, like everybody else,”", "\"아, 글쎄요, 다들 생각하는 거랑 비슷하지 않을까요.\""], ["Kitty answered, a little embarrassed, looking round at Sergey Ivanovitch.", "키티가 세르게이 이바노비치를 흘끔 돌아보며 약간 당황한 듯 대답했다."], ["“I’ll send to fetch him.", "\"사람을 보내서 데려오라고 할게요."], ["Papa’s staying with us.", "아빠도 우리와 함께 계세요."], ["He’s only just come home from abroad.”", "외국에서 막 돌아오셨거든요.\""]], [["And making arrangements to send for Levin and for the guests to wash, one in his room and the other in what had been Dolly’s, and giving orders for their luncheon, Kitty ran out onto the balcony, enjoying the freedom, and rapidity of movement, of which she had been deprived during the months of her pregnancy.", "그러고 나서 키티는 레빈을 부르러 사람을 보내고, 손님 한 사람은 자기 방에서, 다른 한 사람은 돌리가 쓰던 방에서 씻도록 채비를 시키고, 점심 식사를 차리라고 지시한 뒤, 임신 기간 내내 빼앗겼던 자유로움과 민첩한 움직임을 만끽하며 발코니로 뛰어나갔다."]], [["“It’s Sergey Ivanovitch and Katavasov, a professor,”", "\"세르게이 이바노비치하고, 교수인 카타바소프 씨예요.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“Oh, that’s a bore in this heat,”", "\"아, 이 더위에 손님이라니 성가시구나.\""], ["said the prince.", "공작이 말했다."]], [["“No, papa, he’s very nice, and Kostya’s very fond of him,”", "\"아니에요, 아빠, 그분은 정말 좋은 분이고, 코스챠도 무척 좋아해요.\""], ["Kitty said, with a deprecating smile, noticing the irony on her father’s face.", "키티가 아버지 얼굴에 떠오른 빈정거림을 알아채고는 달래는 듯한 미소를 지으며 말했다."]], [["“Oh, I didn’t say anything.”", "\"아니, 난 아무 말도 안 했다.\""]], [["“You go to them, darling,”", "\"언니가 가서 좀 상대해 줘요.\""], ["said Kitty to her sister, “and entertain them.", "키티가 언니에게 말했다."], ["They saw Stiva at the station; he was quite well.", "\"두 분이 역에서 스티바를 봤대요. 아주 건강하더래요."], ["And I must run to Mitya.", "난 미챠한테 가 봐야겠어요."], ["As ill-luck would have it, I haven’t fed him since tea.", "하필이면 차 마신 뒤로 아직 젖을 못 먹였거든요."], ["He’s awake now, and sure to be screaming.”", "지금쯤 깨어서 분명 울고 있을 거예요.\""], ["And feeling a rush of milk, she hurried to the nursery.", "그러면서 젖이 도는 것을 느끼고는 서둘러 아기방으로 향했다."]], [["This was not a mere guess; her connection with the child was still so close, that she could gauge by the flow of her milk his need of food, and knew for certain he was hungry.", "이것은 그저 짐작이 아니었다. 아이와의 연결이 아직 너무도 긴밀해서, 그녀는 젖이 도는 것으로 아이의 식욕을 가늠할 수 있었고, 아이가 배고프다는 것을 확실히 알았다."]], [["She knew he was crying before she reached the nursery.", "그녀는 아기방에 닿기도 전에 아이가 울고 있다는 것을 알았다."], ["And he was indeed crying.", "그리고 정말로 아이는 울고 있었다."], ["She heard him and hastened.", "울음소리를 듣고 그녀는 걸음을 재촉했다."], ["But the faster she went, the louder he screamed.", "하지만 빨리 갈수록 아이는 더 크게 울어 댔다."], ["It was a fine healthy scream, hungry and impatient.", "배고프고 보채는, 우렁차고 건강한 울음소리였다."]], [["“Has he been screaming long, nurse, very long?”", "\"오래 울었어요, 유모, 아주 오래?\""], ["said Kitty hurriedly, seating herself on a chair, and preparing to give the baby the breast.", "키티가 의자에 앉아 아기에게 젖을 물릴 채비를 하며 다급하게 말했다."], ["“But give me him quickly.", "\"어서 이리 줘요."], ["Oh, nurse, how tiresome you are!", "아, 유모, 어쩜 이렇게 굼떠요!"], ["There, tie the cap afterwards, do!”", "자, 모자는 나중에 매 줘요, 어서!\""]], [["The baby’s greedy scream was passing into sobs.", "아기의 게걸스러운 울음소리가 흐느낌으로 잦아들고 있었다."]], [["“But you can’t manage so, ma’am,”", "\"하지만 그렇게 하시면 안 돼요, 마님.\""], ["said Agafea Mihalovna, who was almost always to be found in the nursery.", "거의 언제나 아기방에 붙어 있는 아가피야 미하일로브나가 말했다."], ["“He must be put straight.", "\"똑바로 안아 줘야지요."], ["A-oo! a-oo!”", "아우, 아우!\""], ["she chanted over him, paying no attention to the mother.", "그녀는 어미는 안중에도 없이 아기를 어르며 노래하듯 흥얼거렸다."]], [["The nurse brought the baby to his mother.", "유모가 아기를 어미에게 데려왔다."], ["Agafea Mihalovna followed him with a face dissolving with tenderness.", "아가피야 미하일로브나가 다정함에 녹아내리는 얼굴로 그 뒤를 따랐다."]], [["“He knows me, he knows me.", "\"이 애가 저를 알아봐요, 알아본다니까요."], ["In God’s faith, Katerina Alexandrovna, ma’am, he knew me!”", "하느님께 맹세코, 카체리나 알렉산드로브나 마님, 저를 알아봤어요!\""], ["Agafea Mihalovna cried above the baby’s screams.", "아가피야 미하일로브나가 아기의 울음소리 위로 외쳤다."]], [["But Kitty did not hear her words.", "하지만 키티는 그 말을 듣지 못했다."], ["Her impatience kept growing, like the baby’s.", "아기와 마찬가지로 그녀의 조바심도 점점 커져만 갔다."]], [["Their impatience hindered things for a while.", "두 사람의 조바심이 한동안 일을 더디게 했다."], ["The baby could not get hold of the breast right, and was furious.", "아기는 젖을 제대로 물지 못해 잔뜩 약이 올랐다."]], [["At last, after despairing, breathless screaming, and vain sucking, things went right, and mother and child felt simultaneously soothed, and both subsided into calm.", "마침내 절망에 빠진 듯 숨넘어가게 울고 헛되이 빨아 대고 한 끝에, 일이 제대로 풀렸고, 어미와 아이는 동시에 안도감을 느끼며 둘 다 평온 속으로 가라앉았다."]], [["“But poor darling, he’s all in perspiration!”", "\"아유, 가엾은 것, 땀범벅이 됐네!\""], ["said Kitty in a whisper, touching the baby.", "키티가 아기를 만지며 속삭이듯 말했다."]], [["“What makes you think he knows you?”", "\"무얼 보고 이 애가 자기를 알아본다고 하는 거예요?\""], ["she added, with a sidelong glance at the baby’s eyes, that peered roguishly, as she fancied, from under his cap, at his rhythmically puffing cheeks, and the little red-palmed hand he was waving.", "그녀는 모자 밑에서, 자기 생각에는, 장난스레 빠끔 내다보는 아기의 눈과 율동적으로 부풀어 오르는 볼, 그리고 손바닥이 발그레한 작은 손을 흔들어 대는 모습을 곁눈질하며 덧붙였다."]], [["“Impossible!", "\"말도 안 돼요!"], ["If he knew anyone, he would have known me,”", "누구든 알아본다면, 나를 알아봤겠지요.\""], ["said Kitty, in response to Agafea Mihalovna’s statement, and she smiled.", "키티가 아가피야 미하일로브나의 말에 대꾸하며 미소를 지었다."]], [["She smiled because, though she said he could not know her, in her heart she was sure that he knew not merely Agafea Mihalovna, but that he knew and understood everything, and knew and understood a great deal too that no one else knew, and that she, his mother, had learned and come to understand only through him.", "그녀가 미소 지은 것은, 아이가 자기를 알아볼 리 없다고 말은 했지만, 마음속으로는 아이가 아가피야 미하일로브나뿐 아니라 모든 것을 알고 이해하고 있으며, 다른 누구도 모르는 많은 것까지 알고 이해하고 있다고, 그리고 어미인 자기 자신이 오직 이 아이를 통해서만 배우고 깨닫게 된 그 많은 것까지도 안다고 확신하고 있었기 때문이다."], ["To Agafea Mihalovna, to the nurse, to his grandfather, to his father even, Mitya was a living being, requiring only material care, but for his mother he had long been a moral being, with whom there had been a whole series of spiritual relations already.", "아가피야 미하일로브나에게도, 유모에게도, 외할아버지에게도, 심지어 아버지에게조차 미챠는 그저 물질적인 보살핌만 필요로 하는 살아 있는 존재였지만, 어미에게 미챠는 이미 오래전부터 도덕적 존재였고, 둘 사이에는 이미 영적인 관계가 한 묶음이나 자리 잡고 있었다."]], [["“When he wakes up, please God, you shall see for yourself.", "\"하느님 덕분에 깨어나면, 직접 보시게 될 거예요."], ["Then when I do like this, he simply beams on me, the darling!", "그때 제가 이렇게 해 주면, 글쎄, 그냥 저를 보고 환하게 웃는다니까요, 우리 귀염둥이가!"], ["Simply beams like a sunny day!”", "그저 화창한 날처럼 환하게 빛나지요!\""], ["said Agafea Mihalovna.", "아가피야 미하일로브나가 말했다."]], [["“Well, well; then we shall see,”", "\"그래요, 그래요, 그럼 두고 보지요.\""], ["whispered Kitty.", "키티가 속삭였다."], ["“But now go away, he’s going to sleep.”", "\"하지만 이제 그만 나가 봐요, 이 애가 잠들려고 하니까.\""]], [["# Chapter 7", "# Chapter 7"]], [["Agafea Mihalovna went out on tiptoe; the nurse let down the blind, chased a fly out from under the muslin canopy of the crib, and a bumblebee struggling on the window-frame, and sat down waving a faded branch of birch over the mother and the baby.", "아가피야 미하일로브나는 발끝으로 살금살금 걸어 나갔다. 유모는 블라인드를 내리고, 요람의 모슬린 천장 밑에서 파리 한 마리를 쫓아내고, 창틀에서 버둥대는 호박벌 한 마리를 내쫓고는, 시든 자작나무 가지 하나를 어머니와 아기 위로 흔들며 앉았다."]], [["“How hot it is!", "\"어쩌면 이렇게 덥담!"], ["if God would send a drop of rain,”", "하느님이 비라도 한 줄기 내려 주시면 좋으련만.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“Yes, yes, sh—sh—sh——”", "\"네, 네, 쉿—쉿—쉿——\""], ["was all Kitty answered, rocking a little, and tenderly squeezing the plump little arm, with rolls of fat at the wrist, which Mitya still waved feebly as he opened and shut his eyes.", "키티는 그렇게만 대답하며 살짝 몸을 흔들었고, 미챠가 눈을 떴다 감았다 하며 여전히 힘없이 흔들어 대는, 손목에 살이 접혀 통통한 그 작은 팔을 다정하게 꼭 쥐었다."], ["That hand worried Kitty; she longed to kiss the little hand, but was afraid to for fear of waking the baby.", "그 손이 키티의 마음을 흔들었다. 그 작은 손에 입을 맞추고 싶었지만, 아기가 깰까 봐 두려워 차마 그러지 못했다."], ["At last the little hand ceased waving, and the eyes closed.", "마침내 작은 손이 흔들기를 멈추었고, 눈이 감겼다."], ["Only from time to time, as he went on sucking, the baby raised his long, curly eyelashes and peeped at his mother with wet eyes, that looked black in the twilight.", "다만 이따금 젖을 빨면서 아기는 길고 곱슬곱슬한 속눈썹을 들어, 어스름 속에서 까맣게 보이는 젖은 눈으로 어머니를 빠끔히 올려다보았다."], ["The nurse had left off fanning, and was dozing.", "유모는 부채질을 멈추고 꾸벅꾸벅 졸고 있었다."], ["From above came the peals of the old prince’s voice, and the chuckle of Katavasov.", "위층에서는 늙은 공작의 쩌렁쩌렁한 목소리와 카타바소프의 킬킬대는 웃음소리가 들려왔다."]], [["“They have got into talk without me,”", "'나를 빼놓고 이야기에 빠졌구나.'"], ["thought Kitty, “but still it’s vexing that Kostya’s out.", "키티는 생각했다. '하지만 코스챠가 자리에 없는 건 그래도 속상해."], ["He’s sure to have gone to the bee-house again.", "분명 또 양봉장에 갔을 거야."], ["Though it’s a pity he’s there so often, still I’m glad. It distracts his mind.", "거기에 너무 자주 가는 게 안타깝긴 해도, 그래도 난 기뻐."], ["He’s become altogether happier and better now than in the spring.", "그이의 마음을 다른 데로 돌려 주니까. 봄에 비하면 이제 훨씬 행복해지고 좋아졌어."], ["He used to be so gloomy and worried that I felt frightened for him.", "예전엔 어찌나 침울하고 안절부절못하던지 내가 다 겁이 날 정도였지."], ["And how absurd he is!”", "그런데 어쩜 그렇게 우스운 사람일까!'"], ["she whispered, smiling.", "그녀는 미소를 지으며 속삭였다."]], [["She knew what worried her husband.", "그녀는 남편을 괴롭히는 게 무엇인지 알고 있었다."], ["It was his unbelief.", "그것은 그의 불신앙이었다."], ["Although, if she had been asked whether she supposed that in the future life, if he did not believe, he would be damned, she would have had to admit that he would be damned, his unbelief did not cause her unhappiness.", "만약 누군가 그녀에게, 남편이 믿지 않으니 내세에서 그가 저주받으리라고 생각하느냐고 물었다면, 그녀는 그가 저주받을 거라고 인정할 수밖에 없었을 테지만, 그래도 그의 불신앙이 그녀를 불행하게 만들지는 않았다."], ["And she, confessing that for an unbeliever there can be no salvation, and loving her husband’s soul more than anything in the world, thought with a smile of his unbelief, and told herself that he was absurd.", "그리고 그녀는, 믿지 않는 자에게는 구원이 있을 수 없다고 고백하면서도, 세상 그 무엇보다 남편의 영혼을 사랑하면서도, 그의 불신앙을 떠올리면 미소가 지어졌고, 그이는 참 우스운 사람이라고 혼자 되뇌었다."]], [["“What does he keep reading philosophy of some sort for all this year?”", "'대체 무엇 때문에 그이는 올 한 해 내내 무슨 철학 같은 걸 그렇게 읽어 대는 걸까?'"], ["she wondered.", "그녀는 궁금했다."], ["“If it’s all written in those books, he can understand them.", "'만약 그게 다 그 책들에 쓰여 있다면, 그이는 그걸 이해할 수 있을 거야."], ["If it’s all wrong, why does he read them?", "만약 그게 다 틀린 거라면, 왜 읽는 걸까?"], ["He says himself that he would like to believe.", "자기 입으로도 믿고 싶다고 하잖아."], ["Then why is it he doesn’t believe?", "그런데 왜 믿지 못하는 걸까?"], ["Surely from his thinking so much?", "분명 너무 많이 생각해서 그런 거야."], ["And he thinks so much from being solitary.", "그리고 너무 외로워서 그렇게 많이 생각하는 거지."], ["He’s always alone, alone.", "늘 혼자, 혼자야."], ["He can’t talk about it all to us.", "그런 걸 우리한테 다 털어놓을 수도 없고."], ["I fancy he’ll be glad of these visitors, especially Katavasov.", "이번 손님들, 특히 카타바소프가 와서 그이도 반가워할 것 같아."], ["He likes discussions with them,”", "그 사람들과 토론하는 걸 좋아하니까.'"], ["she thought, and passed instantly to the consideration of where it would be more convenient to put Katavasov, to sleep alone or to share Sergey Ivanovitch’s room.", "그녀는 그렇게 생각하다가, 곧바로 카타바소프를 어디에 묵게 하는 게 더 편할지, 혼자 자게 할지 아니면 세르게이 이바노비치의 방을 같이 쓰게 할지를 궁리하기 시작했다."], ["And then an idea suddenly struck her, which made her shudder and even disturb Mitya, who glanced severely at her.", "그러다 문득 한 가지 생각이 떠올라 그녀는 몸서리쳤고, 그 바람에 미챠까지 움찔하게 만들어 아기가 그녀를 엄하게 흘끗 쳐다보았다."], ["“I do believe the laundress hasn’t sent the washing yet, and all the best sheets are in use.", "'세탁부가 아직 빨래를 보내지 않은 게 분명한데, 제일 좋은 시트는 죄다 쓰고 있잖아."], ["If I don’t see to it, Agafea Mihalovna will give Sergey Ivanovitch the wrong sheets,”", "내가 챙기지 않으면 아가피야 미하일로브나가 세르게이 이바노비치한테 엉뚱한 시트를 내줄 거야.'"], ["and at the very idea of this the blood rushed to Kitty’s face.", "이 생각만으로도 키티의 얼굴로 피가 확 몰렸다."]], [["“Yes, I will arrange it,”", "'그래, 내가 처리해야지.'"], ["she decided, and going back to her former thoughts, she remembered that some spiritual question of importance had been interrupted, and she began to recall what.", "그녀는 마음을 정하고는, 다시 아까의 생각으로 돌아가, 무언가 중요한 영적인 문제가 도중에 끊겼다는 것을 떠올리며, 그게 무엇이었는지 기억해 내려 애썼다."], ["“Yes, Kostya, an unbeliever,”", "'그래, 코스챠, 믿지 않는 사람.'"], ["she thought again with a smile.", "그녀는 다시 미소 지으며 생각했다."]], [["“Well, an unbeliever then!", "'뭐, 믿지 않는 사람이면 또 어때!"], ["Better let him always be one than like Madame Stahl, or what I tried to be in those days abroad.", "슈탈 부인처럼 되거나, 그 시절 외국에서 내가 되어 보려고 했던 그런 모습이 되느니, 차라리 늘 그런 사람으로 있는 게 나아."], ["No, he won’t ever sham anything.”", "아니, 그이는 무엇 하나 거짓으로 꾸미는 법이 없을 거야.'"]], [["And a recent instance of his goodness rose vividly to her mind.", "그러자 최근에 있었던 그의 선량함의 한 사례가 그녀의 마음속에 생생히 떠올랐다."], ["A fortnight ago a penitent letter had come from Stepan Arkadyevitch to Dolly.", "두 주 전에 스테판 아르카지치에게서 돌리에게 뉘우치는 편지가 한 통 왔다."], ["He besought her to save his honor, to sell her estate to pay his debts.", "그는 자기 명예를 지켜 달라고, 빚을 갚을 수 있도록 그녀의 영지를 팔아 달라고 간청했다."], ["Dolly was in despair, she detested her husband, despised him, pitied him, resolved on a separation, resolved to refuse, but ended by agreeing to sell part of her property.", "돌리는 절망에 빠져, 남편을 미워하고, 경멸하고, 가엾게 여기고, 헤어지기로 결심했다가, 거절하기로 마음먹었다가, 결국에는 재산의 일부를 팔기로 동의하고 말았다."], ["After that, with an irrepressible smile of tenderness, Kitty recalled her husband’s shamefaced embarrassment, his repeated awkward efforts to approach the subject, and how at last, having thought of the one means of helping Dolly without wounding her pride, he had suggested to Kitty—what had not occurred to her before—that she should give up her share of the property.", "그 일이 있은 뒤, 키티는 억누를 수 없는 다정한 미소를 지으며, 남편의 부끄러운 듯 쩔쩔매던 모습을, 그 이야기를 꺼내려고 거듭 어색하게 애쓰던 모습을, 그리고 마침내 돌리의 자존심을 다치게 하지 않으면서 그녀를 도울 유일한 방법을 생각해 낸 끝에, 그녀가—전에는 미처 생각지도 못했던—자기 몫의 재산을 포기하면 어떻겠느냐고 제안하던 그 모습을 떠올렸다."]], [["“He an unbeliever indeed!", "'그런 사람이 믿지 않는 사람이라고!"], ["With his heart, his dread of offending anyone, even a child!", "그 마음씨로, 누구 하나, 심지어 아이 하나라도 상하게 할까 봐 그토록 두려워하는 사람이!"], ["Everything for others, nothing for himself.", "모든 게 남을 위한 것이고, 자기를 위한 건 하나도 없어."], ["Sergey Ivanovitch simply considers it as Kostya’s duty to be his steward.", "세르게이 이바노비치는 그저 코스챠가 자기 살림을 관리해 주는 게 당연한 의무라고 여기지."], ["And it’s the same with his sister.", "누이도 마찬가지고."], ["Now Dolly and her children are under his guardianship; all these peasants who come to him every day, as though he were bound to be at their service.”", "이제 돌리와 그 아이들도 그이의 보살핌 아래 있어. 날마다 그이를 찾아오는 이 모든 농부들도, 마치 그이가 자기들 시중을 들어야 마땅하다는 듯이 굴잖아.'"]], [["“Yes, only be like your father, only like him,”", "\"그래, 그저 네 아빠처럼만, 꼭 그이처럼만 되어 다오.\""], ["she said, handing Mitya over to the nurse, and putting her lips to his cheek.", "그녀는 미챠를 유모에게 건네주며, 아기의 뺨에 입술을 갖다 대며 말했다."]], [["# Chapter 8", "# Chapter 8"]], [["Ever since, by his beloved brother’s deathbed, Levin had first glanced into the questions of life and death in the light of these new convictions, as he called them, which had during the period from his twentieth to his thirty-fourth year imperceptibly replaced his childish and youthful beliefs—he had been stricken with horror, not so much of death, as of life, without any knowledge of whence, and why, and how, and what it was.", "사랑하는 형의 임종 자리에서, 그가 새로운 신념이라고 부르는 것에 비추어 처음으로 삶과 죽음의 문제를 들여다본 이래로—스무 살에서 서른네 살에 이르는 동안 어느새 그의 어린 시절과 젊은 날의 믿음을 대신해 버린 그 신념에 비추어—레빈은 공포에 사로잡혀 있었다. 그것은 죽음에 대한 공포라기보다는, 그것이 어디서 와서 무엇 때문에 어떻게 존재하며 또 무엇인지 전혀 알지 못한 채로 살아가는 삶 자체에 대한 공포였다."], ["The physical organization, its decay, the indestructibility of matter, the law of the conservation of energy, evolution, were the words which usurped the place of his old belief.", "육체의 조직, 그 부패, 물질의 불멸성, 에너지 보존의 법칙, 진화—이런 말들이 그의 옛 믿음의 자리를 빼앗아 버렸다."], ["These words and the ideas associated with them were very well for intellectual purposes.", "이런 말들과 거기에 결부된 관념들은 지적인 목적에는 더없이 훌륭했다."], ["But for life they yielded nothing, and Levin felt suddenly like a man who has changed his warm fur cloak for a muslin garment, and going for the first time into the frost is immediately convinced, not by reason, but by his whole nature that he is as good as naked, and that he must infallibly perish miserably.", "그러나 삶에는 아무것도 가져다주지 못했고, 레빈은 문득 따뜻한 모피 외투를 모슬린 옷으로 바꿔 입고서 처음으로 추위 속에 나선 사람처럼 느꼈다. 그런 사람은 이성이 아니라 자기 존재 전체로, 자기가 벌거벗은 거나 다름없으며 비참하게 죽고 말 수밖에 없다는 것을 곧장 확신하게 되는 것이다."]], [["From that moment, though he did not distinctly face it, and still went on living as before, Levin had never lost this sense of terror at his lack of knowledge.", "그 순간 이후로, 비록 또렷하게 직시하지는 않았고 여전히 예전처럼 살아가기는 했지만, 레빈은 자기가 아무것도 알지 못한다는 데서 오는 이 공포의 감각을 단 한 번도 잃은 적이 없었다."]], [["He vaguely felt, too, that what he called his new convictions were not merely lack of knowledge, but that they were part of a whole order of ideas, in which no knowledge of what he needed was possible.", "그는 또한 자기가 새로운 신념이라고 부르는 것이 단순히 무지에 그치는 것이 아니라, 자기에게 필요한 그 어떤 앎도 가능하지 않은 어떤 관념의 전체 질서의 일부라는 것을 어렴풋이 느꼈다."]], [["At first, marriage, with the new joys and duties bound up with it, had completely crowded out these thoughts.", "처음에는 결혼과, 거기에 따라붙은 새로운 기쁨과 의무가 이런 생각들을 완전히 밀어냈다."], ["But of late, while he was staying in Moscow after his wife’s confinement, with nothing to do, the question that clamored for solution had more and more often, more and more insistently, haunted Levin’s mind.", "그러나 최근 들어, 아내의 출산 후 모스크바에 머물며 할 일 없이 지내는 동안, 해결을 부르짖는 그 물음이 점점 더 자주, 점점 더 집요하게 레빈의 마음을 사로잡았다."]], [["The question was summed up for him thus: “If I do not accept the answers Christianity gives to the problems of my life, what answers do I accept?”", "그 물음은 그에게 이렇게 요약되었다. \"내가 그리스도교가 내 삶의 문제들에 내놓는 답들을 받아들이지 않는다면, 나는 어떤 답을 받아들이는 것인가?\""], ["And in the whole arsenal of his convictions, so far from finding any satisfactory answers, he was utterly unable to find anything at all like an answer.", "그리고 그의 신념이라는 무기고 전체를 통틀어, 만족스러운 답을 찾기는커녕 답 비슷한 것조차 도무지 찾아낼 수가 없었다."]], [["He was in the position of a man seeking food in toy shops and tool shops.", "그는 마치 장난감 가게와 연장 가게에서 먹을 것을 찾는 사람과 같은 처지였다."]], [["Instinctively, unconsciously, with every book, with every conversation, with every man he met, he was on the lookout for light on these questions and their solution.", "본능적으로, 무의식적으로, 모든 책에서, 모든 대화에서, 마주치는 모든 사람에게서, 그는 이 물음들과 그 해답에 빛을 던져 줄 무언가를 찾고 있었다."]], [["What puzzled and distracted him above everything was that the majority of men of his age and circle had, like him, exchanged their old beliefs for the same new convictions, and yet saw nothing to lament in this, and were perfectly satisfied and serene.", "무엇보다도 그를 당혹스럽게 하고 어지럽게 한 것은, 자기 또래에 자기와 같은 부류에 속한 사람들 대다수가 자기와 마찬가지로 옛 믿음을 똑같은 새 신념으로 바꿔 버렸으면서도, 거기서 한탄할 만한 것을 전혀 보지 못한 채 더없이 만족스럽고 평온하게 지낸다는 사실이었다."], ["So that, apart from the principal question, Levin was tortured by other questions too.", "그래서 그 근본적인 물음과는 별개로, 레빈은 다른 물음들로도 괴로워했다. 이 사람들은 진실한 것일까?"], ["Were these people sincere?", "그는 스스로에게 물었다."], ["he asked himself, or were they playing a part?", "아니면 그저 연기를 하고 있는 것일까?"], ["or was it that they understood the answers science gave to these problems in some different, clearer sense than he did?", "아니면 그들은 과학이 이 문제들에 내놓는 답을 그와는 다르게, 더 명료한 의미로 이해하고 있는 것일까?"], ["And he assiduously studied both these men’s opinions and the books which treated of these scientific explanations.", "그래서 그는 이 사람들의 견해도, 이런 과학적 설명을 다룬 책들도 부지런히 연구했다."]], [["One fact he had found out since these questions had engrossed his mind, was that he had been quite wrong in supposing from the recollections of the circle of his young days at college, that religion had outlived its day, and that it was now practically non-existent.", "이 물음들이 그의 마음을 사로잡은 이래로 그가 알아낸 한 가지 사실은, 대학 시절 젊은 날의 친구들에 대한 기억으로 미루어 종교는 이미 그 수명을 다했으며 이제 사실상 존재하지 않는다고 짐작한 것이 완전히 잘못이었다는 점이다."], ["All the people nearest to him who were good in their lives were believers.", "자기와 가장 가까우면서 삶에서 선량한 사람들은 모두 신자였다."], ["The old prince, and Lvov, whom he liked so much, and Sergey Ivanovitch, and all the women believed, and his wife believed as simply as he had believed in his earliest childhood, and ninety-nine hundredths of the Russian people, all the working people for whose life he felt the deepest respect, believed.", "늙은 공작도, 그가 무척 좋아하는 리보프도, 세르게이 이바노비치도, 모든 여자들도 믿었고, 그의 아내도 그가 아주 어린 시절에 믿었던 것만큼이나 단순하게 믿었으며, 러시아 민중의 백 가운데 아흔아홉이, 그가 그 삶에 가장 깊은 존경을 느끼는 모든 노동하는 사람들이 믿었다."]], [["Another fact of which he became convinced, after reading many scientific books, was that the men who shared his views had no other construction to put on them, and that they gave no explanation of the questions which he felt he could not live without answering, but simply ignored their existence and attempted to explain other questions of no possible interest to him, such as the evolution of organisms, the materialistic theory of consciousness, and so forth.", "많은 과학 책을 읽은 뒤 그가 확신하게 된 또 하나의 사실은, 자기와 같은 견해를 가진 사람들이 그 견해 위에 다른 어떤 구조도 세우지 못하며, 답 없이는 살아갈 수 없다고 그가 느끼는 물음들에 대해 아무런 설명도 내놓지 못하고, 그저 그 물음들의 존재를 무시한 채 그에게는 도무지 흥미로울 수 없는 다른 물음들, 이를테면 유기체의 진화라든가 의식에 관한 유물론적 이론 따위를 설명하려 든다는 점이었다."]], [["Moreover, during his wife’s confinement, something had happened that seemed extraordinary to him.", "게다가 아내의 출산 동안 그에게는 무언가 예사롭지 않게 여겨지는 일이 일어났다."], ["He, an unbeliever, had fallen into praying, and at the moment he prayed, he believed.", "그는, 믿지 않는 그가, 어느새 기도를 하게 되었고, 기도하는 그 순간에는 믿었던 것이다."], ["But that moment had passed, and he could not make his state of mind at that moment fit into the rest of his life.", "그러나 그 순간은 지나가 버렸고, 그는 그 순간의 자기 마음 상태를 나머지 삶에 들어맞게 할 수가 없었다."]], [["He could not admit that at that moment he knew the truth, and that now he was wrong; for as soon as he began thinking calmly about it, it all fell to pieces.", "그 순간 자기가 진리를 알았고 지금은 틀렸다고 인정할 수는 없었다. 그것에 대해 차분히 생각하기 시작하자마자 모든 것이 산산조각 나 버렸기 때문이다."], ["He could not admit that he was mistaken then, for his spiritual condition then was precious to him, and to admit that it was a proof of weakness would have been to desecrate those moments.", "그렇다고 그때 자기가 잘못 생각한 것이라고 인정할 수도 없었다. 그때의 영적 상태가 그에게는 소중했고, 그것이 나약함의 증거라고 인정하는 것은 그 순간들을 모독하는 일이 될 터였기 때문이다."], ["He was miserably divided against himself, and strained all his spiritual forces to the utmost to escape from this condition.", "그는 비참하게 자기 자신과 분열되었고, 이 상태에서 벗어나기 위해 온갖 영적인 힘을 한껏 쥐어짰다."]], [["# Chapter 9", "# Chapter 9"]], [["These doubts fretted and harassed him, growing weaker or stronger from time to time, but never leaving him.", "이런 의심들이 때로는 약해지고 때로는 강해지면서 그를 들볶고 괴롭혔으나, 결코 그를 떠나지는 않았다."], ["He read and thought, and the more he read and the more he thought, the further he felt from the aim he was pursuing.", "그는 읽고 생각했고, 더 많이 읽고 더 많이 생각할수록 자기가 좇고 있는 목표에서 점점 더 멀어지는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["Of late in Moscow and in the country, since he had become convinced that he would find no solution in the materialists, he had read and re-read thoroughly Plato, Spinoza, Kant, Schelling, Hegel, and Schopenhauer, the philosophers who gave a non-materialistic explanation of life.", "근래 모스크바에서, 그리고 시골에서, 유물론자들에게서는 아무런 해답도 찾을 수 없으리라고 확신하게 된 뒤로, 그는 플라톤, 스피노자, 칸트, 셸링, 헤겔, 쇼펜하우어, 그러니까 삶에 대해 비유물론적 설명을 내놓은 철학자들을 꼼꼼히 읽고 또 읽었다."]], [["Their ideas seemed to him fruitful when he was reading or was himself seeking arguments to refute other theories, especially those of the materialists; but as soon as he began to read or sought for himself a solution of problems, the same thing always happened.", "이들의 사상은 그가 그것을 읽고 있을 때나, 다른 이론들, 특히 유물론자들의 이론을 반박할 논거를 스스로 찾고 있을 때는 풍요롭게 느껴졌다. 그러나 그가 문제들의 해답을 읽기 시작하거나 스스로 찾기 시작하면, 언제나 똑같은 일이 벌어졌다."], ["As long as he followed the fixed definition of obscure words such as spirit, will, freedom, essence, purposely letting himself go into the snare of words the philosophers set for him, he seemed to comprehend something.", "정신, 의지, 자유, 본질 같은 모호한 낱말들의 고정된 정의를 따라가면서, 철학자들이 그를 위해 놓아둔 낱말의 덫 속으로 일부러 자기 자신을 빠져들게 하는 동안에는 무언가를 이해하는 것만 같았다."], ["But he had only to forget the artificial train of reasoning, and to turn from life itself to what had satisfied him while thinking in accordance with the fixed definitions, and all this artificial edifice fell to pieces at once like a house of cards, and it became clear that the edifice had been built up out of those transposed words, apart from anything in life more important than reason.", "그러나 이 인위적인 추론의 흐름을 잊어버리고, 삶 그 자체로부터 고정된 정의에 따라 사색하는 동안 자기를 만족시켜 주었던 것으로 돌아서기만 하면, 이 인위적인 구조물 전체가 카드로 지은 집처럼 한순간에 무너져 내렸고, 그 구조물이 이성보다 더 중요한 삶 속의 어떤 것과는 동떨어진 채, 이리저리 자리를 바꾼 낱말들로 지어진 것이었음이 분명해졌다."]], [["At one time, reading Schopenhauer, he put in place of his will the word love, and for a couple of days this new philosophy charmed him, till he removed a little away from it.", "한번은 쇼펜하우어를 읽으면서 그의 의지라는 말 자리에 사랑이라는 말을 넣어 보았고, 이틀쯤 이 새로운 철학이 그를 매혹했으나, 거기서 조금 물러서자 그뿐이었다."], ["But then, when he turned from life itself to glance at it again, it fell away too, and proved to be the same muslin garment with no warmth in it.", "그러다 삶 그 자체로부터 그것을 다시 흘끗 바라보려고 돌아서자, 그것 또한 떨어져 나가 버렸고, 따스함이라고는 없는 똑같은 모슬린 옷자락임이 드러났다."]], [["His brother Sergey Ivanovitch advised him to read the theological works of Homiakov.", "형 세르게이 이바노비치는 그에게 호미아코프의 신학 저작들을 읽어 보라고 권했다."], ["Levin read the second volume of Homiakov’s works, and in spite of the elegant, epigrammatic, argumentative style which at first repelled him, he was impressed by the doctrine of the church he found in them.", "레빈은 호미아코프 저작의 둘째 권을 읽었는데, 처음에는 그를 거부감 들게 했던 그 우아하고 경구적이며 논쟁적인 문체에도 불구하고, 그 안에서 발견한 교회의 교리에 깊은 인상을 받았다."], ["He was struck at first by the idea that the apprehension of divine truths had not been vouchsafed to man, but to a corporation of men bound together by love—to the church.", "그는 우선 신의 진리에 대한 깨달음이 한 인간에게 주어진 것이 아니라, 사랑으로 한데 묶인 사람들의 공동체, 곧 교회에 주어졌다는 생각에 놀랐다."], ["What delighted him was the thought how much easier it was to believe in a still existing living church, embracing all the beliefs of men, and having God at its head, and therefore holy and infallible, and from it to accept the faith in God, in the creation, the fall, the redemption, than to begin with God, a mysterious, far-away God, the creation, etc.", "그를 기쁘게 한 것은, 신비롭고 아득히 먼 신으로, 그리고 창조 따위로 시작하기보다는, 인간의 온갖 믿음을 품고 신을 그 머리에 두었기에 거룩하고 무류(無謬)한, 지금도 살아 존재하는 교회를 믿고, 거기서부터 신에 대한 믿음과 창조와 타락과 구원에 대한 믿음을 받아들이는 편이 얼마나 더 쉬운가 하는 생각이었다."], ["But afterwards, on reading a Catholic writer’s history of the church, and then a Greek orthodox writer’s history of the church, and seeing that the two churches, in their very conception infallible, each deny the authority of the other, Homiakov’s doctrine of the church lost all its charm for him, and this edifice crumbled into dust like the philosophers’ edifices.", "그러나 그 뒤에 한 가톨릭 저술가가 쓴 교회사를 읽고, 이어서 그리스 정교 저술가가 쓴 교회사를 읽으면서, 그 두 교회가 그 개념 자체로는 무류하면서도 저마다 상대편의 권위를 부정한다는 것을 보게 되자, 호미아코프의 교회 교리는 그에게서 모든 매력을 잃었고, 이 구조물도 철학자들의 구조물처럼 먼지가 되어 부스러졌다."]], [["All that spring he was not himself, and went through fearful moments of horror.", "그해 봄 내내 그는 제정신이 아니었고, 끔찍한 공포의 순간들을 겪었다."]], [["“Without knowing what I am and why I am here, life’s impossible; and that I can’t know, and so I can’t live,”", "\"내가 무엇이며 왜 여기에 있는지를 모른다면, 산다는 건 불가능해. 그런데 그걸 알 수가 없으니, 결국 살아갈 수가 없는 거지.\""], ["Levin said to himself.", "레빈은 혼잣말을 했다."]], [["“In infinite time, in infinite matter, in infinite space, is formed a bubble-organism, and that bubble lasts a while and bursts, and that bubble is Me.”", "\"무한한 시간, 무한한 물질, 무한한 공간 속에서 거품 같은 유기체 하나가 생겨나고, 그 거품은 잠시 머물다가 터져 버려. 그리고 그 거품이 바로 나야.\""]], [["It was an agonizing error, but it was the sole logical result of ages of human thought in that direction.", "그것은 괴로운 오류였으나, 그 방향으로 흘러온 수백 년에 걸친 인간 사유의 유일한 논리적 귀결이었다."]], [["This was the ultimate belief on which all the systems elaborated by human thought in almost all their ramifications rested.", "이것이야말로 인간의 사유가 거의 모든 갈래에 걸쳐 정교하게 다듬어 낸 모든 체계가 그 위에 의지하고 있는 궁극의 믿음이었다."], ["It was the prevalent conviction, and of all other explanations Levin had unconsciously, not knowing when or how, chosen it, as anyway the clearest, and made it his own.", "그것은 널리 퍼진 확신이었고, 레빈은 다른 모든 설명 가운데서 언제 어떻게인지도 모른 채 무의식적으로 그것을, 어쨌든 가장 명료한 것이라 여겨 골라잡고는 자기 것으로 삼아 버렸다."]], [["But it was not merely a falsehood, it was the cruel jeer of some wicked power, some evil, hateful power, to whom one could not submit.", "그러나 그것은 단지 거짓에 그치지 않았다. 그것은 어떤 사악한 힘, 어떤 악하고 가증스러운 힘, 도저히 굴복할 수 없는 그런 힘의 잔인한 비웃음이었다."]], [["He must escape from this power.", "그는 이 힘에서 벗어나야 했다."], ["And the means of escape every man had in his own hands.", "그리고 벗어날 수단은 누구나 제 손 안에 쥐고 있었다."], ["He had but to cut short this dependence on evil.", "악에 대한 이 종속을 끊어 버리기만 하면 되었다."], ["And there was one means—death.", "그리고 그 수단은 하나, 죽음이었다."]], [["And Levin, a happy father and husband, in perfect health, was several times so near suicide that he hid the cord that he might not be tempted to hang himself, and was afraid to go out with his gun for fear of shooting himself.", "그리하여 행복한 아버지요 남편이며 더없이 건강한 레빈은, 목을 매고 싶은 유혹에 빠질까 봐 끈을 감추고, 자기 자신을 쏠까 두려워 총을 들고 나가는 것마저 겁낼 만큼, 몇 번이고 자살에 그토록 가까이 다가갔다."]], [["But Levin did not shoot himself, and did not hang himself; he went on living.", "그러나 레빈은 자기를 쏘지도 않았고, 목을 매지도 않았다. 그는 계속 살아갔다."]], [["# Chapter 10", "# Chapter 10"]], [["When Levin thought what he was and what he was living for, he could find no answer to the questions and was reduced to despair, but he left off questioning himself about it.", "레빈은 자기가 무엇이며 무엇을 위해 살고 있는지 생각해 보아도 그 물음들에 아무런 답을 찾을 수 없었고, 그래서 절망에 빠지곤 했다. 하지만 그는 그것에 관해 스스로 캐묻기를 그만두었다."], ["It seemed as though he knew both what he was and for what he was living, for he acted and lived resolutely and without hesitation.", "마치 그는 자기가 무엇이며 무엇을 위해 살고 있는지를 다 아는 것 같았는데, 왜냐하면 그는 단호하게, 망설임 없이 행동하고 살아갔기 때문이다."], ["Indeed, in these latter days he was far more decided and unhesitating in life than he had ever been.", "정말이지 요즘 들어 그는 그 어느 때보다도 훨씬 결단력 있게, 흔들림 없이 살아가고 있었다."]], [["When he went back to the country at the beginning of June, he went back also to his usual pursuits.", "유월 초에 시골로 돌아온 그는 평소의 일상으로도 돌아갔다."], ["The management of the estate, his relations with the peasants and the neighbors, the care of his household, the management of his sister’s and brother’s property, of which he had the direction, his relations with his wife and kindred, the care of his child, and the new bee-keeping hobby he had taken up that spring, filled all his time.", "영지 경영, 농부들과 이웃들과의 관계, 살림을 돌보는 일, 그가 관리를 맡고 있던 누이와 형의 재산을 돌보는 일, 아내와 친척들과의 관계, 아이를 돌보는 일, 그리고 그해 봄에 새로 시작한 양봉 취미가 그의 시간을 온통 채웠다."]], [["These things occupied him now, not because he justified them to himself by any sort of general principles, as he had done in former days; on the contrary, disappointed by the failure of his former efforts for the general welfare, and too much occupied with his own thought and the mass of business with which he was burdened from all sides, he had completely given up thinking of the general good, and he busied himself with all this work simply because it seemed to him that he must do what he was doing—that he could not do otherwise.", "이런 일들이 지금 그를 사로잡고 있는 것은, 예전에 그랬듯이 어떤 일반적인 원칙으로 그것들을 스스로에게 정당화했기 때문이 아니었다. 오히려 그 반대였다."], ["In former days—almost from childhood, and increasingly up to full manhood—when he had tried to do anything that would be good for all, for humanity, for Russia, for the whole village, he had noticed that the idea of it had been pleasant, but the work itself had always been incoherent, that then he had never had a full conviction of its absolute necessity, and that the work that had begun by seeming so great, had grown less and less, till it vanished into nothing.", "공공의 복리를 위한 예전의 노력이 실패로 끝난 것에 실망하고, 자기 자신의 생각과 사방에서 그를 짓누르는 산더미 같은 일거리에 너무 깊이 매여 있던 터라, 그는 공공의 선에 대해 생각하기를 완전히 그만두었고, 그저 자기가 하고 있는 일을 해야만 한다고—달리 어쩔 수 없다고—여겨졌기 때문에 그 모든 일에 매달렸다. 예전에는—거의 어린 시절부터, 그리고 완전한 어른이 될 때까지 점점 더—모두를 위해, 인류를 위해, 러시아를 위해, 마을 전체를 위해 무엇이든 이로운 일을 해 보려 할 때면, 그는 그런 일에 대한 생각 자체는 즐거웠지만 일 자체는 늘 갈피를 잡을 수 없었고, 그때마다 그 일이 절대적으로 필요하다는 온전한 확신을 한 번도 가져 본 적이 없었으며, 처음에는 그토록 위대해 보이던 일이 점점 작아지다가 끝내 아무것도 아닌 것으로 사라져 버리는 것을 늘 느껴 왔다."], ["But now, since his marriage, when he had begun to confine himself more and more to living for himself, though he experienced no delight at all at the thought of the work he was doing, he felt a complete conviction of its necessity, saw that it succeeded far better than in old days, and that it kept on growing more and more.", "그런데 이제, 결혼한 뒤로 점점 더 자기 자신을 위해 사는 쪽으로 자신을 옭아매기 시작하자, 비록 자기가 하고 있는 일에 대한 생각에서 아무런 기쁨도 느끼지 못하면서도, 그 일이 필요하다는 완전한 확신을 느꼈고, 그 일이 예전보다 훨씬 잘 풀려 나가는 것을, 그리고 점점 더 커져 가는 것을 보았다."]], [["Now, involuntarily it seemed, he cut more and more deeply into the soil like a plough, so that he could not be drawn out without turning aside the furrow.", "이제 그는 자기 의지와 상관없이, 쟁기처럼 점점 더 깊이 흙 속을 파고드는 것 같았고, 그래서 이랑을 비스듬히 틀지 않고서는 빠져나올 수 없게 되어 버렸다."]], [["To live the same family life as his father and forefathers—that is, in the same condition of culture—and to bring up his children in the same, was incontestably necessary.", "자기 아버지와 조상들이 살았던 것과 똑같은 가정생활을 하는 것—즉 똑같은 교양의 조건 속에서 사는 것—과 자식들을 똑같이 길러 내는 것은 의심할 여지 없이 필요한 일이었다."], ["It was as necessary as dining when one was hungry.", "그것은 배고플 때 식사를 하는 것만큼이나 필요한 일이었다."], ["And to do this, just as it was necessary to cook dinner, it was necessary to keep the mechanism of agriculture at Pokrovskoe going so as to yield an income.", "그리고 이를 위해서는, 마치 저녁 식사를 짓는 일이 필요한 것처럼, 포크롭스코예의 농사 기구가 수입을 내도록 계속 돌아가게 하는 것이 필요했다."], ["Just as incontestably as it was necessary to repay a debt was it necessary to keep the property in such a condition that his son, when he received it as a heritage, would say “thank you”", "빚을 갚는 것이 필요한 것만큼이나 의심할 여지 없이, 그의 아들이 그것을 유산으로 물려받을 때, 레빈이 자기 할아버지가 짓고 심어 놓은 모든 것에 대해 \"고맙습니다\""], ["to his father as Levin had said “thank you”", "하고 말했던 것처럼 그 아들도 아버지에게 \"고맙습니다\""], ["to his grandfather for all he built and planted.", "하고 말할 수 있도록 재산을 그런 상태로 지켜 두는 것이 필요했다."], ["And to do this it was necessary to look after the land himself, not to let it, and to breed cattle, manure the fields, and plant timber.", "그리고 이를 위해서는 땅을 직접 돌보아야지 남에게 빌려주어서는 안 되었고, 가축을 기르고, 밭에 거름을 주고, 나무를 심어야 했다."]], [["It was impossible not to look after the affairs of Sergey Ivanovitch, of his sister, of the peasants who came to him for advice and were accustomed to do so—as impossible as to fling down a child one is carrying in one’s arms.", "세르게이 이바노비치의 일, 누이의 일, 그에게 조언을 구하러 와서 늘 그래 오던 농부들의 일을 돌보지 않는 것은 불가능했다—마치 품에 안고 가던 아이를 내동댕이치는 것이 불가능한 것처럼."], ["It was necessary to look after the comfort of his sister-in-law and her children, and of his wife and baby, and it was impossible not to spend with them at least a short time each day.", "형수와 그 아이들의 편안함을, 그리고 아내와 갓난아이의 편안함을 돌보는 것이 필요했고, 매일 적어도 잠깐씩이라도 그들과 함께 시간을 보내지 않는 것은 불가능했다."]], [["And all this, together with shooting and his new bee-keeping, filled up the whole of Levin’s life, which had no meaning at all for him, when he began to think.", "그리고 이 모든 것이, 사냥과 새로 시작한 양봉과 더불어, 레빈의 삶 전체를 채우고 있었는데, 그 삶은 그가 곰곰이 생각하기 시작하면 그에게 아무런 의미도 없는 것이었다."]], [["But besides knowing thoroughly what he had to do, Levin knew in just the same way how he had to do it all, and what was more important than the rest.", "그러나 자기가 무엇을 해야 하는지를 속속들이 아는 것 외에도, 레빈은 똑같은 방식으로 그 모든 일을 어떻게 해야 하는지를, 그리고 무엇이 나머지보다 더 중요한지를 알고 있었다."]], [["He knew he must hire laborers as cheaply as possible; but to hire men under bond, paying them in advance at less than the current rate of wages, was what he must not do, even though it was very profitable.", "그는 일꾼들을 되도록 싸게 고용해야 한다는 것을 알았다. 하지만 사람들을 계약으로 묶어 두고 현행 임금보다 낮은 값에 미리 삯을 치르는 것은, 비록 그것이 무척 이익이 된다 해도 결코 해서는 안 될 일이었다."], ["Selling straw to the peasants in times of scarcity of provender was what he might do, even though he felt sorry for them; but the tavern and the pothouse must be put down, though they were a source of income.", "사료가 부족한 때에 농부들에게 짚을 파는 것은, 비록 그들이 가엾게 느껴진다 해도 해도 되는 일이었다. 하지만 술집과 선술집은, 비록 수입의 원천이라 해도 없애 버려야 했다."], ["Felling timber must be punished as severely as possible, but he could not exact forfeits for cattle being driven onto his fields; and though it annoyed the keeper and made the peasants not afraid to graze their cattle on his land, he could not keep their cattle as a punishment.", "나무를 베어 가는 것은 되도록 엄하게 벌해야 했지만, 가축을 그의 밭으로 몰아넣었다고 해서 벌금을 물릴 수는 없었다. 그리고 그것이 산지기를 짜증나게 하고 농부들이 그의 땅에 거리낌 없이 가축을 풀어놓게 만들었지만, 그래도 그는 벌로 그들의 가축을 붙잡아 둘 수는 없었다."]], [["To Pyotr, who was paying a money-lender ten per cent. a month, he must lend a sum of money to set him free.", "고리대금업자에게 한 달에 십 퍼센트의 이자를 물고 있는 표트르에게는, 그가 그 빚에서 벗어나도록 돈을 빌려 주어야 했다."], ["But he could not let off peasants who did not pay their rent, nor let them fall into arrears.", "하지만 소작료를 내지 않는 농부들을 그냥 봐줄 수도, 그들이 밀린 빚을 쌓아 가도록 내버려 둘 수도 없었다."], ["It was impossible to overlook the bailiff’s not having mown the meadows and letting the hay spoil; and it was equally impossible to mow those acres where a young copse had been planted.", "마름이 풀밭의 풀을 베지 않아 건초를 썩게 내버려 둔 것을 눈감아 주는 것은 불가능했고, 어린 잡목 숲을 심어 놓은 땅을 베어 내는 것도 마찬가지로 불가능했다."], ["It was impossible to excuse a laborer who had gone home in the busy season because his father was dying, however sorry he might feel for him, and he must subtract from his pay those costly months of idleness.", "한창 바쁜 철에 아버지가 죽어 간다는 이유로 집에 가 버린 일꾼을, 아무리 그가 가엾게 느껴진다 해도 너그러이 봐주는 것은 불가능했고, 그가 빈둥거린 그 값비싼 몇 달치 삯은 품삯에서 깎아야 했다."], ["But it was impossible not to allow monthly rations to the old servants who were of no use for anything.", "하지만 아무 쓸모도 없는 늙은 하인들에게 다달이 식량을 주지 않는 것은 불가능했다."]], [["Levin knew that when he got home he must first of all go to his wife, who was unwell, and that the peasants who had been waiting for three hours to see him could wait a little longer.", "레빈은 집에 돌아가면 무엇보다 먼저 몸이 편치 않은 아내에게 가야 한다는 것을, 그리고 세 시간째 그를 만나려고 기다리고 있던 농부들은 좀 더 기다려도 된다는 것을 알았다."], ["He knew too that, regardless of all the pleasure he felt in taking a swarm, he must forego that pleasure, and leave the old man to see to the bees alone, while he talked to the peasants who had come after him to the bee-house.", "그는 또한, 벌떼를 거두는 일에서 느끼는 온갖 즐거움에도 불구하고 그 즐거움을 포기하고 노인에게 혼자 벌을 돌보도록 맡겨 둔 채, 자기를 따라 양봉장까지 온 농부들과 이야기를 나누어야 한다는 것도 알았다."]], [["Whether he were acting rightly or wrongly he did not know, and far from trying to prove that he was, nowadays he avoided all thought or talk about it.", "자기가 옳게 행동하는지 그르게 행동하는지 그는 알지 못했고, 자기가 옳다는 것을 증명하려 하기는커녕 요즘 그는 그것에 관한 일체의 생각이나 이야기를 피했다."]], [["Reasoning had brought him to doubt, and prevented him from seeing what he ought to do and what he ought not.", "이치를 따지는 것은 그를 의심으로 몰아넣었고, 무엇을 해야 하고 무엇을 하지 말아야 하는지 보지 못하게 가로막았다."], ["When he did not think, but simply lived, he was continually aware of the presence of an infallible judge in his soul, determining which of two possible courses of action was the better and which was the worse, and as soon as he did not act rightly, he was at once aware of it.", "그가 생각하지 않고 그저 살아갈 때면, 그는 자기 영혼 속에 결코 그르치지 않는 재판관이 있어, 가능한 두 가지 행동 가운데 어느 것이 더 나은지 어느 것이 더 못한지를 가려 준다는 것을 끊임없이 의식했고, 옳게 행동하지 못하면 곧바로 그것을 알아챘다."]], [["So he lived, not knowing and not seeing any chance of knowing what he was and what he was living for, and harassed at this lack of knowledge to such a point that he was afraid of suicide, and yet firmly laying down his own individual definite path in life.", "그렇게 그는 자기가 무엇이며 무엇을 위해 사는지 알지 못한 채, 또 그것을 알 가망도 보이지 않는 채 살아갔으며, 이 앎의 결핍에 시달린 나머지 자살을 두려워할 지경에까지 이르렀으면서도, 자기만의 뚜렷한 인생길을 굳건히 닦아 나가고 있었다."]], [["# Chapter 11", "# Chapter 11"]], [["The day on which Sergey Ivanovitch came to Pokrovskoe was one of Levin’s most painful days.", "세르게이 이바노비치가 포크롭스코예에 온 그날은 레빈에게 가장 괴로운 날 가운데 하나였다."], ["It was the very busiest working time, when all the peasantry show an extraordinary intensity of self-sacrifice in labor, such as is never shown in any other conditions of life, and would be highly esteemed if the men who showed these qualities themselves thought highly of them, and if it were not repeated every year, and if the results of this intense labor were not so simple.", "한 해 가운데 일이 가장 바쁜 철이었으니, 이때가 되면 온 농민이 다른 어떤 삶의 조건에서도 결코 보이지 않는, 자기를 희생하는 비범한 열정을 노동에 쏟아붓는다. 만약 그런 자질을 보이는 사람들 스스로가 그것을 대단하게 여겼다면, 그리고 그것이 해마다 되풀이되지 않았다면, 또 이 격렬한 노동의 결실이 그토록 단순하지만 않았다면, 그 열정은 마땅히 높이 평가받았을 것이다."]], [["To reap and bind the rye and oats and to carry it, to mow the meadows, turn over the fallows, thrash the seed and sow the winter corn—all this seems so simple and ordinary; but to succeed in getting through it all everyone in the village, from the old man to the young child, must toil incessantly for three or four weeks, three times as hard as usual, living on rye-beer, onions, and black bread, thrashing and carrying the sheaves at night, and not giving more than two or three hours in the twenty-four to sleep.", "호밀과 귀리를 베어 단으로 묶어 나르고, 풀밭의 풀을 베고, 묵정밭을 갈아엎고, 씨앗을 타작하고, 겨울 곡식을 뿌리는 일—이 모든 것이 너무도 단순하고 평범해 보인다. 그러나 이 일들을 죄다 해내려면 마을의 노인부터 어린아이까지 모두가 서너 주 동안 쉼 없이, 평소의 세 배는 더 힘들게, 호밀로 빚은 맥주와 양파와 검은 빵으로 끼니를 때우며, 밤에는 곡식 단을 타작하고 실어 나르고, 하루 스물네 시간 가운데 두세 시간 넘게는 잠도 자지 못하며 일해야 한다."], ["And every year this is done all over Russia.", "그리고 이런 일이 해마다 러시아 곳곳에서 되풀이된다."]], [["Having lived the greater part of his life in the country and in the closest relations with the peasants, Levin always felt in this busy time that he was infected by this general quickening of energy in the people.", "생애의 대부분을 시골에서, 농민과 더없이 가까운 관계를 맺으며 살아온 레빈은 이 바쁜 철이 되면 늘 사람들 사이에 두루 번지는 이 활력의 고조에 자기도 전염되는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["In the early morning he rode over to the first sowing of the rye, and to the oats, which were being carried to the stacks, and returning home at the time his wife and sister-in-law were getting up, he drank coffee with them and walked to the farm, where a new thrashing machine was to be set working to get ready the seed-corn.", "이른 아침 그는 호밀을 처음으로 뿌리는 밭과, 낟가리로 실려 나가던 귀리밭으로 말을 타고 나갔다가, 아내와 처형이 일어날 무렵 집으로 돌아와 그들과 함께 커피를 마셨다. 그러고는 씨앗으로 쓸 곡식을 마련하려고 새 타작기를 돌리기 시작하는 농장까지 걸어갔다."]], [["He was standing in the cool granary, still fragrant with the leaves of the hazel branches interlaced on the freshly peeled aspen beams of the new thatch roof. He gazed through the open door in which the dry bitter dust of the thrashing whirled and played, at the grass of the thrashing floor in the sunlight and the fresh straw that had been brought in from the barn, then at the speckly-headed, white-breasted swallows that flew chirping in under the roof and, fluttering their wings, settled in the crevices of the doorway, then at the peasants bustling in the dark, dusty barn, and he thought strange thoughts.", "그는 서늘한 곳간에 서 있었다. 새 이엉 지붕의, 갓 껍질을 벗긴 사시나무 들보 위에 엮어 놓은 개암나무 가지의 잎 향기가 아직 감돌고 있었다. 그는 열린 문 너머를 바라보았다. 그 문에서는 타작의 메마르고 쓴 먼지가 소용돌이치며 춤추고 있었고, 햇빛 속에 드러난 타작마당의 풀과 헛간에서 들여온 싱싱한 짚이 보였다. 그러다 점점이 무늬 진 머리에 가슴이 흰 제비들이 지저귀며 지붕 밑으로 날아들어, 날개를 파닥이며 문틈에 내려앉는 것을 바라보았고, 다시 어둡고 먼지 자욱한 헛간 안에서 분주히 움직이는 농민들을 바라보았다. 그러면서 그는 이상한 생각에 잠겼다."]], [["“Why is it all being done?”", "\"이 모든 게 왜 이루어지고 있는 거지?\""], ["he thought.", "그는 생각했다."], ["“Why am I standing here, making them work?", "\"나는 왜 여기 서서 저들을 일하게 만들고 있는 거지?"], ["What are they all so busy for, trying to show their zeal before me?", "저들은 다 무엇 때문에 저토록 바쁘게, 내 앞에서 열의를 보이려고 애쓰는 걸까?"], ["What is that old Matrona, my old friend, toiling for?", "내 오랜 친구인 저 노파 마트료나는 무엇 때문에 저렇게 고생하는 걸까?"], ["(I doctored her, when the beam fell on her in the fire)”", "(불이 났을 때 들보가 그녀를 덮쳤을 때 내가 치료해 주었지)\""], ["he thought, looking at a thin old woman who was raking up the grain, moving painfully with her bare, sun-blackened feet over the uneven, rough floor.", "그는 곡식을 긁어모으는 비쩍 마른 노파를 바라보며 생각했다. 노파는 햇볕에 검게 그을린 맨발로 울퉁불퉁하고 거친 바닥 위를 힘겹게 옮겨 다니고 있었다."], ["“Then she recovered, but today or tomorrow or in ten years she won’t; they’ll bury her, and nothing will be left either of her or of that smart girl in the red jacket, who with that skillful, soft action shakes the ears out of their husks.", "\"그때는 회복했지만, 오늘이든 내일이든 십 년 뒤든 그녀는 죽을 거고, 사람들은 그녀를 묻을 테고, 그녀에게서도, 저 익숙하고 부드러운 손놀림으로 이삭에서 낟알을 털어 내는 붉은 윗옷의 저 야무진 처녀에게서도 아무것도 남지 않겠지."], ["They’ll bury her and this piebald horse, and very soon too,”", "사람들은 그녀를 묻을 거고, 저 얼룩말도 묻을 거야, 그것도 머지않아.\""], ["he thought, gazing at the heavily moving, panting horse that kept walking up the wheel that turned under him.", "그는 자기 밑에서 돌아가는 바퀴를 끝없이 밟으며 헐떡이고 무겁게 움직이는 말을 바라보며 생각했다."], ["“And they will bury her and Fyodor the thrasher with his curly beard full of chaff and his shirt torn on his white shoulders—they will bury him.", "\"그리고 사람들은 그녀도, 곱슬곱슬한 수염에 검불이 가득하고 흰 어깨 위로 셔츠가 찢어진 타작꾼 표도르도 묻을 거야—사람들은 그를 묻겠지."], ["He’s untying the sheaves, and giving orders, and shouting to the women, and quickly setting straight the strap on the moving wheel.", "저자는 곡식 단을 풀고, 지시를 내리고, 여자들에게 소리치고, 돌아가는 바퀴의 가죽끈을 잽싸게 바로잡고 있어."], ["And what’s more, it’s not them alone—me they’ll bury too, and nothing will be left.", "게다가 저들만이 아니야—사람들은 나도 묻을 거고, 아무것도 남지 않을 거야."], ["What for?”", "무엇을 위해서지?\""]], [["He thought this, and at the same time looked at his watch to reckon how much they thrashed in an hour.", "그는 이런 생각을 하면서도, 동시에 한 시간에 얼마나 타작이 되는지 가늠하려고 시계를 들여다보았다."], ["He wanted to know this so as to judge by it the task to set for the day.", "그것으로 하루 작업량을 정하려고 알고 싶었던 것이다."]], [["“It’ll soon be one, and they’re only beginning the third sheaf,”", "\"곧 한 시인데, 이제 겨우 세 번째 단을 시작하고 있군.\""], ["thought Levin.", "레빈은 생각했다."], ["He went up to the man that was feeding the machine, and shouting over the roar of the machine he told him to put it in more slowly.", "그는 기계에 곡식을 넣고 있는 사내에게 다가가, 기계의 굉음 너머로 소리쳐서 좀 더 천천히 넣으라고 일렀다."], ["“You put in too much at a time, Fyodor.", "\"한꺼번에 너무 많이 넣잖아, 표도르."], ["Do you see—it gets choked, that’s why it isn’t getting on.", "봐—막혀 버려서 일이 안 되는 거야."], ["Do it evenly.”", "고르게 넣으라고.\""]], [["Fyodor, black with the dust that clung to his moist face, shouted something in response, but still went on doing it as Levin did not want him to.", "축축한 얼굴에 들러붙은 먼지로 새까매진 표도르가 무어라 대꾸하며 소리쳤지만, 여전히 레빈이 원하지 않는 방식대로 일을 계속했다."]], [["Levin, going up to the machine, moved Fyodor aside, and began feeding the corn in himself.", "레빈은 기계로 다가가 표도르를 옆으로 비키게 하고는, 직접 곡식을 넣기 시작했다."], ["Working on till the peasants’ dinner hour, which was not long in coming, he went out of the barn with Fyodor and fell into talk with him, stopping beside a neat yellow sheaf of rye laid on the thrashing floor for seed.", "머지않아 다가온 농민들의 점심시간까지 일하고 나서, 그는 표도르와 함께 헛간을 나와, 씨앗으로 쓰려고 타작마당에 가지런히 쌓아 놓은 노란 호밀 단 곁에 멈춰 서서 그와 이야기를 나누게 되었다."]], [["Fyodor came from a village at some distance from the one in which Levin had once allotted land to his cooperative association.", "표도르는 레빈이 한때 협동조합에 땅을 떼어 주었던 마을에서 좀 떨어진 마을 출신이었다."], ["Now it had been let to a former house porter.", "지금 그 땅은 전에 문지기로 일하던 사내에게 넘어가 있었다."]], [["Levin talked to Fyodor about this land and asked whether Platon, a well-to-do peasant of good character belonging to the same village, would not take the land for the coming year.", "레빈은 표도르와 이 땅에 관해 이야기하면서, 같은 마을에 사는, 살림이 넉넉하고 성품도 좋은 농민 플라톤이 내년에 그 땅을 맡지 않겠느냐고 물었다."]], [["“It’s a high rent; it wouldn’t pay Platon, Konstantin Dmitrievitch,”", "\"소작료가 너무 비싸서요, 플라톤한테는 수지가 안 맞을 겁니다, 콘스탄친 드미트리치.\""], ["answered the peasant, picking the ears off his sweat-drenched shirt.", "농민이 땀에 흠뻑 젖은 셔츠에서 이삭을 떼어 내며 대답했다."]], [["“But how does Kirillov make it pay?”", "\"하지만 키릴로프는 어떻게 수지를 맞추지?\""]], [["“Mituh!”", "\"미투흐 말입니까!\""], ["(so the peasant called the house porter, in a tone of contempt), “you may be sure he’ll make it pay, Konstantin Dmitrievitch!", "(농민은 그 문지기를 경멸하는 어조로 그렇게 불렀다.) \"그 사람이야 틀림없이 수지를 맞출 겁니다, 콘스탄친 드미트리치!"], ["He’ll get his share, however he has to squeeze to get it!", "어떻게든 쥐어짜서라도 자기 몫은 챙길걸요!"], ["He’s no mercy on a Christian.", "그 사람은 그리스도교도한테도 인정사정없으니까요."], ["But Uncle Fokanitch”", "하지만 포카니치 영감님은\""], ["(so he called the old peasant Platon), “do you suppose he’d flay the skin off a man?", "(그는 늙은 농민 플라톤을 그렇게 불렀다.) \"그 양반이 남의 가죽을 벗겨 먹기야 하겠습니까?"], ["Where there’s debt, he’ll let anyone off.", "빚이 있는 사람이면 누구든 봐주고요."], ["And he’ll not wring the last penny out.", "마지막 한 푼까지 짜내지도 않습죠."], ["He’s a man too.”", "그 양반도 사람이니까요.\""]], [["“But why will he let anyone off?”", "\"하지만 왜 남을 봐주는 거지?\""]], [["“Oh, well, of course, folks are different.", "\"아, 뭐, 그야 당연히, 사람이 다 다르니까요."], ["One man lives for his own wants and nothing else, like Mituh, he only thinks of filling his belly, but Fokanitch is a righteous man.", "어떤 사람은 미투흐처럼 제 욕심만 챙기며 살고, 제 배 채울 생각밖에 안 하지만, 포카니치 영감님은 의로운 분이거든요."], ["He lives for his soul.", "그분은 영혼을 위해 사십니다."], ["He does not forget God.”", "하느님을 잊지 않으세요.\""]], [["“How thinks of God?", "\"하느님을 어떻게 생각한다는 거지?"], ["How does he live for his soul?”", "영혼을 위해 산다는 건 또 어떻게 사는 건데?\""], ["Levin almost shouted.", "레빈은 거의 소리치다시피 했다."]], [["“Why, to be sure, in truth, in God’s way.", "\"왜, 그야 물론, 진실되게, 하느님의 방식대로지요."], ["Folks are different.", "사람이 다 다르니까요."], ["Take you now, you wouldn’t wrong a man....”", "나리만 봐도, 나리는 남에게 해를 끼치실 분이 아니잖습니까…….\""]], [["“Yes, yes, good-bye!”", "\"그래, 그래, 잘 있게!\""], ["said Levin, breathless with excitement, and turning round he took his stick and walked quickly away towards home.", "흥분으로 숨이 차오른 레빈은 그렇게 말하고 돌아서서 지팡이를 집어 들고는 집을 향해 빠르게 걸어갔다."], ["At the peasant’s words that Fokanitch lived for his soul, in truth, in God’s way, undefined but significant ideas seemed to burst out as though they had been locked up, and all striving towards one goal, they thronged whirling through his head, blinding him with their light.", "포카니치가 영혼을 위해, 진실되게, 하느님의 방식대로 산다는 농민의 말에, 막연하지만 의미심장한 생각들이 마치 갇혀 있다가 터져 나오는 것 같았다. 그리고 그 모든 생각이 하나의 목표를 향해 치달으며, 그의 머릿속을 소용돌이치며 가득 메워 그 빛으로 그의 눈을 멀게 했다."]], [["# Chapter 12", "# Chapter 12"]], [["Levin strode along the highroad, absorbed not so much in his thoughts (he could not yet disentangle them) as in his spiritual condition, unlike anything he had experienced before.", "레빈은 큰길을 따라 성큼성큼 걸었다. 그를 사로잡은 것은 자기 생각이라기보다(아직 그는 그 생각들을 풀어낼 수 없었다)—이전에 한 번도 겪어 본 적 없는 어떤 영혼의 상태였다."]], [["The words uttered by the peasant had acted on his soul like an electric shock, suddenly transforming and combining into a single whole the whole swarm of disjointed, impotent, separate thoughts that incessantly occupied his mind.", "농부가 내뱉은 그 말이 그의 영혼에 전기 충격처럼 작용했고, 끊임없이 그의 마음을 차지하던 그 흩어지고 무력하고 따로 노는 무수한 생각의 떼를 별안간 변형시켜 하나의 전체로 결합시켰다."], ["These thoughts had unconsciously been in his mind even when he was talking about the land.", "그 생각들은 그가 토지에 관해 이야기하는 동안에도 무의식 속에서 그의 마음을 떠나지 않고 있었던 것이다."]], [["He was aware of something new in his soul, and joyfully tested this new thing, not yet knowing what it was.", "그는 자기 영혼 속에 무언가 새로운 것이 있음을 느꼈고, 그것이 무엇인지 아직 알지 못한 채 그 새로운 것을 기쁘게 음미했다."]], [["“Not living for his own wants, but for God? For what God?", "\"자기 욕구를 위해서가 아니라, 하느님을 위해 산다고?"], ["And could one say anything more senseless than what he said?", "어떤 하느님을 위해서? 그가 한 말보다 더 터무니없는 말을 할 수 있을까?"], ["He said that one must not live for one’s own wants, that is, that one must not live for what we understand, what we are attracted by, what we desire, but must live for something incomprehensible, for God, whom no one can understand nor even define.", "그는 자기 욕구를 위해 살아서는 안 된다고 했어. 즉 우리가 이해하는 것, 우리를 끌어당기는 것, 우리가 욕망하는 것을 위해 살아서는 안 되고, 무언가 이해할 수 없는 것, 아무도 이해할 수도 정의할 수도 없는 하느님을 위해 살아야 한다는 거지."], ["What of it?", "그게 어쨌단 말인가?"], ["Didn’t I understand those senseless words of Fyodor’s?", "표도르의 그 터무니없는 말을 내가 이해하지 못했던가?"], ["And understanding them, did I doubt of their truth?", "그리고 그것을 이해하면서, 내가 그 진실을 의심했던가?"], ["Did I think them stupid, obscure, inexact?", "그 말이 어리석다거나 모호하다거나 부정확하다고 생각했던가?"], ["No, I understood him, and exactly as he understands the words.", "아니, 나는 그를 이해했어, 그가 그 말을 이해하는 것과 똑같이."], ["I understood them more fully and clearly than I understand anything in life, and never in my life have I doubted nor can I doubt about it.", "나는 그 말을 내가 인생에서 그 무엇을 이해하는 것보다 더 충분하고 명료하게 이해했고, 평생 단 한 번도 그것을 의심한 적이 없으며 또 의심할 수도 없어."], ["And not only I, but everyone, the whole world understands nothing fully but this, and about this only they have no doubt and are always agreed.", "그리고 나뿐만 아니라 모두가, 온 세상이 그것 말고는 아무것도 온전히 이해하지 못하면서, 오직 그것에 대해서만은 의심이 없고 언제나 의견이 일치하지."]], [["“And I looked out for miracles, complained that I did not see a miracle which would convince me.", "그리고 나는 기적을 찾아 헤매며, 나를 납득시켜 줄 기적을 보지 못한다고 불평했어."], ["A material miracle would have persuaded me.", "물질적인 기적이라면 나를 설득했을 테지."], ["And here is a miracle, the sole miracle possible, continually existing, surrounding me on all sides, and I never noticed it!", "그런데 여기 기적이, 가능한 단 하나의 기적이, 끊임없이 존재하며 사방에서 나를 둘러싸고 있는 그 기적이 있었는데, 나는 한 번도 그것을 알아채지 못했던 거야!"]], [["“Fyodor says that Kirillov lives for his belly.", "표도르는 키릴로프가 제 배를 위해 산다고 말하지."], ["That’s comprehensible and rational.", "그건 이해할 수 있고 합리적이야."], ["All of us as rational beings can’t do anything else but live for our belly.", "우리 모두는 합리적 존재로서 제 배를 위해 사는 것 말고는 달리 아무것도 할 수 없으니까."], ["And all of a sudden the same Fyodor says that one mustn’t live for one’s belly, but must live for truth, for God, and at a hint I understand him!", "그런데 별안간 같은 표도르가, 제 배를 위해 살아서는 안 되고 진리를 위해, 하느님을 위해 살아야 한다고 말하니, 한마디 암시만으로도 나는 그를 이해해!"], ["And I and millions of men, men who lived ages ago and men living now—peasants, the poor in spirit and the learned, who have thought and written about it, in their obscure words saying the same thing—we are all agreed about this one thing: what we must live for and what is good.", "그리고 나와 수백만의 사람들이, 오래전에 살았던 사람들과 지금 살아 있는 사람들이—농부들이며, 마음이 가난한 자들이며, 그것에 관해 생각하고 글을 쓰며 그 모호한 말로 똑같은 것을 말해 온 학자들이—우리 모두는 이 한 가지에 대해서는 의견이 일치해. 즉 우리가 무엇을 위해 살아야 하는가, 무엇이 선인가에 대해서는."], ["I and all men have only one firm, incontestable, clear knowledge, and that knowledge cannot be explained by the reason—it is outside it, and has no causes and can have no effects.", "나와 모든 사람은 오직 하나의 확고하고 반박할 수 없는 명료한 앎을 가지고 있고, 그 앎은 이성으로 설명할 수 없어. 그것은 이성 밖에 있고, 원인도 없으며 결과도 가질 수 없어."]], [["“If goodness has causes, it is not goodness; if it has effects, a reward, it is not goodness either.", "선에 원인이 있다면 그것은 선이 아니야. 선에 결과가, 보상이 있다면 그 또한 선이 아니지."], ["So goodness is outside the chain of cause and effect.", "그러니 선은 원인과 결과의 사슬 밖에 있는 거야."]], [["“And yet I know it, and we all know it.", "그런데도 나는 그것을 알고 있고, 우리 모두가 그것을 알고 있어."]], [["“What could be a greater miracle than that?", "그보다 더 큰 기적이 어디 있겠는가?"]], [["“Can I have found the solution of it all?", "내가 이 모든 것의 해답을 찾은 걸까?"], ["can my sufferings be over?”", "내 고통이 끝날 수 있는 걸까?\""], ["thought Levin, striding along the dusty road, not noticing the heat nor his weariness, and experiencing a sense of relief from prolonged suffering.", "레빈은 더위도 피로도 느끼지 못한 채 먼지투성이 길을 성큼성큼 걸으며 생각했고, 오랜 고통에서 벗어난 안도감을 느꼈다."], ["This feeling was so delicious that it seemed to him incredible.", "이 감정은 어찌나 달콤한지 그에게는 믿기지 않을 정도였다."], ["He was breathless with emotion and incapable of going farther; he turned off the road into the forest and lay down in the shade of an aspen on the uncut grass.", "그는 벅찬 감격에 숨이 막혔고 더는 걸어갈 수가 없었다. 그는 길에서 벗어나 숲으로 들어가 사시나무 그늘 아래 베지 않은 풀밭 위에 누웠다."], ["He took his hat off his hot head and lay propped on his elbow in the lush, feathery, woodland grass.", "그는 달아오른 머리에서 모자를 벗고 무성하고 보드라운 숲속 풀밭에 팔꿈치를 괴고 누웠다."]], [["“Yes, I must make it clear to myself and understand,”", "\"그래, 나는 이것을 스스로에게 명확히 하고 이해해야 해.\""], ["he thought, looking intently at the untrampled grass before him, and following the movements of a green beetle, advancing along a blade of couch-grass and lifting up in its progress a leaf of goat-weed.", "그는 앞에 펼쳐진 밟히지 않은 풀밭을 골똘히 바라보며, 개밀 잎새를 따라 기어가다 길을 막는 미나리아재비 잎사귀를 들어 올리며 나아가는 푸른 딱정벌레의 움직임을 좇으며 생각했다."], ["“What have I discovered?”", "\"내가 무엇을 발견한 거지?\""], ["he asked himself, bending aside the leaf of goat-weed out of the beetle’s way and twisting another blade of grass above for the beetle to cross over onto it.", "그는 딱정벌레가 지나가도록 미나리아재비 잎사귀를 옆으로 비켜 주고, 딱정벌레가 건너갈 수 있도록 다른 풀잎 하나를 그 위로 비틀어 놓으며 스스로에게 물었다."], ["“What is it makes me glad?", "\"무엇이 나를 기쁘게 하는 걸까?"], ["What have I discovered?", "내가 무엇을 발견한 거지?"]], [["“I have discovered nothing.", "나는 아무것도 발견하지 못했어."], ["I have only found out what I knew.", "나는 다만 내가 이미 알고 있던 것을 알아냈을 뿐이야."], ["I understand the force that in the past gave me life, and now too gives me life.", "과거에 내게 생명을 주었고 지금도 내게 생명을 주는 그 힘을 이해하게 된 거지."], ["I have been set free from falsity, I have found the Master.", "나는 거짓에서 풀려났고, 주인을 찾았어."]], [["“Of old I used to say that in my body, that in the body of this grass and of this beetle (there, she didn’t care for the grass, she’s opened her wings and flown away), there was going on a transformation of matter in accordance with physical, chemical, and physiological laws.", "예전에 나는 내 몸 안에서, 이 풀과 이 딱정벌레의 몸 안에서(저런, 저것은 풀에는 관심이 없었군, 날개를 펴고 날아가 버렸어) 물리적, 화학적, 생리적 법칙에 따라 물질의 변형이 일어나고 있다고 말하곤 했지."], ["And in all of us, as well as in the aspens and the clouds and the misty patches, there was a process of evolution.", "그리고 우리 모두 안에서도, 사시나무며 구름이며 안개 자락 안에서와 마찬가지로, 진화의 과정이 진행되고 있다고 말이야."], ["Evolution from what?", "무엇으로부터의 진화인가?"], ["into what?—Eternal evolution and struggle....", "무엇으로의 진화인가? 영원한 진화와 투쟁이라……."], ["As though there could be any sort of tendency and struggle in the eternal!", "마치 영원한 것 속에 무슨 경향이나 투쟁 따위가 있을 수 있다는 듯이!"], ["And I was astonished that in spite of the utmost effort of thought along that road I could not discover the meaning of life, the meaning of my impulses and yearnings.", "그리고 나는 그 길을 따라 온 힘을 다해 사유했음에도 인생의 의미를, 내 충동과 갈망의 의미를 발견할 수 없다는 데 놀라곤 했어. 이제 나는 내 인생의 의미를 안다고 말해."], ["Now I say that I know the meaning of my life: ‘To live for God, for my soul.’ And this meaning, in spite of its clearness, is mysterious and marvelous.", "'하느님을 위해, 내 영혼을 위해 사는 것.' 그리고 이 의미는, 그 명료함에도 불구하고 신비롭고 경이로워."], ["Such, indeed, is the meaning of everything existing. Yes, pride,”", "실로 존재하는 모든 것의 의미가 그러하지."], ["he said to himself, turning over on his stomach and beginning to tie a noose of blades of grass, trying not to break them.", "그래, 교만이야.\" 그는 배를 깔고 돌아누우며 풀잎을 끊지 않으려 조심하며 풀잎으로 올가미를 매기 시작했다."]], [["“And not merely pride of intellect, but dulness of intellect.", "\"단지 지성의 교만만이 아니라, 지성의 우둔함이지."], ["And most of all, the deceitfulness; yes, the deceitfulness of intellect.", "그리고 무엇보다도, 그 기만이야. 그래, 지성의 기만."], ["The cheating knavishness of intellect, that’s it,”", "지성의 속임수와 간교함, 바로 그거야.\""], ["he said to himself.", "그는 스스로에게 말했다."]], [["And he briefly went through, mentally, the whole course of his ideas during the last two years, the beginning of which was the clear confronting of death at the sight of his dear brother hopelessly ill.", "그리고 그는 지난 이 년간 자기 생각이 거쳐 온 모든 과정을 마음속으로 간략히 되짚어 보았다. 그 시작은 사랑하는 형이 절망적으로 병들어 가는 광경 앞에서 죽음을 명료하게 마주한 일이었다."]], [["Then, for the first time, grasping that for every man, and himself too, there was nothing in store but suffering, death, and forgetfulness, he had made up his mind that life was impossible like that, and that he must either interpret life so that it would not present itself to him as the evil jest of some devil, or shoot himself.", "그때 처음으로, 모든 사람에게, 그리고 자기 자신에게도 고통과 죽음과 망각 외에는 아무것도 예비되어 있지 않다는 것을 깨닫고, 그는 그런 식으로는 삶이 불가능하다고, 그러니 삶을 어떤 악마의 사악한 농지거리로 자기에게 비치지 않도록 해석해 내든지, 아니면 스스로 총을 쏘아 버리든지 해야 한다고 마음먹었다."]], [["But he had not done either, but had gone on living, thinking, and feeling, and had even at that very time married, and had had many joys and had been happy, when he was not thinking of the meaning of his life.", "그러나 그는 둘 중 어느 것도 하지 않은 채, 계속 살아가고 생각하고 느꼈으며, 심지어 바로 그 무렵 결혼까지 했고, 많은 기쁨을 누렸으며, 자기 인생의 의미를 생각하지 않을 때면 행복하기까지 했다."]], [["What did this mean?", "이것은 무엇을 의미하는가?"], ["It meant that he had been living rightly, but thinking wrongly.", "그것은 그가 올바르게 살아왔으나, 그릇되게 생각해 왔음을 의미했다."]], [["He had lived (without being aware of it) on those spiritual truths that he had sucked in with his mother’s milk, but he had thought, not merely without recognition of these truths, but studiously ignoring them.", "그는 (자신도 알지 못한 채) 어머니의 젖과 함께 빨아들인 그 영혼의 진리들에 의지해 살아왔으면서도, 그 진리들을 인정하지 않았을 뿐 아니라 의도적으로 무시하며 생각해 왔던 것이다."]], [["Now it was clear to him that he could only live by virtue of the beliefs in which he had been brought up.", "이제 그에게는 자기가 오직 자기를 길러 낸 그 믿음들의 힘으로만 살아갈 수 있다는 것이 명백해졌다."]], [["“What should I have been, and how should I have spent my life, if I had not had these beliefs, if I had not known that I must live for God and not for my own desires?", "\"내게 이 믿음들이 없었다면, 내가 내 욕망을 위해서가 아니라 하느님을 위해 살아야 한다는 것을 알지 못했다면, 나는 무엇이 되었을 것이며 내 인생을 어떻게 보냈을까?"], ["I should have robbed and lied and killed.", "나는 도둑질하고 거짓말하고 살인을 저질렀을 거야."], ["Nothing of what makes the chief happiness of my life would have existed for me.”", "내 인생의 가장 큰 행복을 이루는 그 무엇도 내게는 존재하지 않았을 테지.\""], ["And with the utmost stretch of imagination he could not conceive the brutal creature he would have been himself, if he had not known what he was living for.", "그리고 상상력을 한껏 펼쳐 보아도, 자기가 무엇을 위해 살고 있는지를 알지 못했다면 자신이 어떤 짐승 같은 존재가 되었을지 그는 도무지 그려 낼 수 없었다."]], [["“I looked for an answer to my question.", "\"나는 내 물음에 대한 답을 찾고 있었어. 그런데 사유는 내 물음에 답을 줄 수 없었어."], ["And thought could not give an answer to my question—it is incommensurable with my question.", "그것은 내 물음과는 차원이 맞지 않으니까. 그 답은 인생 자체가 내게 주었어."], ["The answer has been given me by life itself, in my knowledge of what is right and what is wrong.", "무엇이 옳고 무엇이 그른가에 대한 나의 앎을 통해서 말이야. 그리고 그 앎에 나는 어떤 방식으로도 도달한 것이 아니야."], ["And that knowledge I did not arrive at in any way, it was given to me as to all men, given, because I could not have got it from anywhere.", "그것은 모든 사람에게 주어지듯 내게도 주어진 거야. 주어진 것이지, 왜냐하면 나는 어디서도 그것을 얻을 수 없었을 테니까."]], [["“Where could I have got it?", "내가 그것을 어디서 얻을 수 있었겠는가?"], ["By reason could I have arrived at knowing that I must love my neighbor and not oppress him?", "내가 이성으로, 이웃을 사랑하고 억압해서는 안 된다는 것을 알아낼 수 있었을까?"], ["I was told that in my childhood, and I believed it gladly, for they told me what was already in my soul.", "어린 시절에 사람들이 내게 그렇게 말해 주었고, 나는 그것을 기쁘게 믿었어. 왜냐하면 그들은 이미 내 영혼 속에 있던 것을 말해 주었으니까."], ["But who discovered it?", "그런데 누가 그것을 발견했는가?"], ["Not reason.", "이성이 아니야."], ["Reason discovered the struggle for existence, and the law that requires us to oppress all who hinder the satisfaction of our desires.", "이성은 생존을 위한 투쟁을 발견했고, 우리의 욕망을 충족시키는 데 방해가 되는 모든 것을 억압하라고 요구하는 법칙을 발견했어."], ["That is the deduction of reason.", "그것이 이성의 추론이지."], ["But loving one’s neighbor reason could never discover, because it’s irrational.”", "하지만 이웃을 사랑하는 것은 이성이 결코 발견할 수 없었을 거야. 그것은 비합리적이니까.\""]], [["# Chapter 13", "# Chapter 13"]], [["And Levin remembered a scene he had lately witnessed between Dolly and her children.", "레빈은 얼마 전 돌리와 그녀의 아이들 사이에서 목격한 한 장면을 떠올렸다."], ["The children, left to themselves, had begun cooking raspberries over the candles and squirting milk into each other’s mouths with a syringe.", "아이들은 어른들 손을 벗어나 촛불에 산딸기를 구워 먹고 주사기로 서로의 입에 우유를 뿜어 대기 시작했다."], ["Their mother, catching them at these pranks, began reminding them in Levin’s presence of the trouble their mischief gave to the grown-up people, and that this trouble was all for their sake, and that if they smashed the cups they would have nothing to drink their tea out of, and that if they wasted the milk, they would have nothing to eat, and die of hunger.", "어머니는 그 장난을 들켰을 때, 레빈이 보는 앞에서 너희들의 못된 짓이 어른들에게 얼마나 성가신 일을 만드는지, 그리고 그 수고가 모두 너희들을 위한 것이라는 점, 또 컵을 깨뜨리면 차를 따라 마실 그릇이 없어질 거라는 점, 우유를 낭비하면 먹을 것이 없어 굶어 죽게 될 거라는 점을 아이들에게 일깨우기 시작했다."]], [["And Levin had been struck by the passive, weary incredulity with which the children heard what their mother said to them.", "레빈은 어머니가 하는 말을 듣는 아이들의 그 수동적이고 지친, 믿지 않는 태도에 충격을 받았다."], ["They were simply annoyed that their amusing play had been interrupted, and did not believe a word of what their mother was saying.", "아이들은 그저 재미난 놀이가 가로막힌 것이 짜증스러울 뿐, 어머니가 하는 말은 한마디도 믿지 않았다."], ["They could not believe it indeed, for they could not take in the immensity of all they habitually enjoyed, and so could not conceive that what they were destroying was the very thing they lived by.", "사실 믿을 수가 없었던 것이, 아이들은 자기들이 늘 누리는 그 모든 것의 어마어마함을 헤아릴 수 없었고, 그래서 자기들이 망가뜨리고 있는 것이 바로 자기들을 살게 해 주는 그것이라는 사실을 떠올릴 수도 없었기 때문이다."]], [["“That all comes of itself,”", "\"이런 건 전부 저절로 생기는 거야.\""], ["they thought, “and there’s nothing interesting or important about it because it has always been so, and always will be so.", "아이들은 생각했다. \"그러니까 재미있을 것도 없고 중요할 것도 없어, 언제나 이래 왔고 앞으로도 늘 이럴 테니까."], ["And it’s all always the same.", "게다가 다 늘 똑같단 말이야."], ["We’ve no need to think about that, it’s all ready.", "그런 건 우리가 생각할 필요도 없어, 이미 다 갖춰져 있으니까."], ["But we want to invent something of our own, and new.", "하지만 우리는 우리만의 것, 새로운 걸 만들어 내고 싶어."], ["So we thought of putting raspberries in a cup, and cooking them over a candle, and squirting milk straight into each other’s mouths.", "그래서 컵에 산딸기를 담아 촛불에 구울 생각을 했고, 서로의 입에 우유를 곧장 뿜을 생각을 했지."], ["That’s fun, and something new, and not a bit worse than drinking out of cups.”", "그게 재미있고, 새롭고, 컵으로 마시는 것보다 조금도 못할 게 없거든.\""]], [["“Isn’t it just the same that we do, that I did, searching by the aid of reason for the significance of the forces of nature and the meaning of the life of man?”", "\"이성의 힘을 빌려 자연의 힘들이 지닌 의미와 인간의 삶의 의미를 찾으려는 우리가, 내가 한 일이 바로 저것과 똑같은 게 아닌가?\""], ["he thought.", "그는 생각했다."]], [["“And don’t all the theories of philosophy do the same, trying by the path of thought, which is strange and not natural to man, to bring him to a knowledge of what he has known long ago, and knows so certainly that he could not live at all without it?", "\"그리고 모든 철학 이론들도 똑같은 짓을 하고 있는 게 아닌가? 인간에게 낯설고 자연스럽지 않은 사유의 길을 통해, 그가 이미 오래전부터 알고 있던 것, 그것 없이는 도무지 살아갈 수조차 없을 만큼 확실하게 알고 있던 것에 대한 앎으로 그를 데려가려 애쓰는 것이?"], ["Isn’t it distinctly to be seen in the development of each philosopher’s theory, that he knows what is the chief significance of life beforehand, just as positively as the peasant Fyodor, and not a bit more clearly than he, and is simply trying by a dubious intellectual path to come back to what everyone knows?", "어느 철학자의 이론이 전개되는 과정을 보면 분명히 드러나지 않는가? 그가 삶의 가장 중요한 의미가 무엇인지를 이미 알고 있다는 것이, 농부 표도르와 꼭 마찬가지로 확신을 가지고, 그보다 조금도 더 명확하지 않게 알고 있으면서, 그저 미심쩍은 지적 경로를 통해 모두가 아는 것으로 되돌아오려 애쓰고 있을 뿐이라는 것이?"]], [["“Now then, leave the children to themselves to get things alone and make their crockery, get the milk from the cows, and so on.", "\"자, 그럼 아이들을 저희들끼리 내버려 두고 혼자 힘으로 그릇을 만들고, 소에서 우유를 짜고, 그 밖의 일을 하게 해 보라."], ["Would they be naughty then?", "그래도 못된 짓을 할까?"], ["Why, they’d die of hunger!", "아니, 굶어 죽고 말 거다!"], ["Well, then, leave us with our passions and thoughts, without any idea of the one God, of the Creator, or without any idea of what is right, without any idea of moral evil.", "자, 그럼 우리를 우리의 욕정과 사념과 함께, 유일한 신, 창조주에 대한 관념도 없이, 무엇이 옳은지에 대한 관념도 없이, 도덕적 악에 대한 관념도 없이 내버려 두어 보라."]], [["“Just try and build up anything without those ideas!", "\"그 관념들 없이 무엇이든 한번 세워 보라!"]], [["“We only try to destroy them, because we’re spiritually provided for.", "\"우리가 그것들을 부수려고만 드는 것은, 우리가 영적으로 이미 공급받고 있기 때문이다."], ["Exactly like the children!", "꼭 저 아이들처럼!"]], [["“Whence have I that joyful knowledge, shared with the peasant, that alone gives peace to my soul?", "\"농부와 함께 나누는 그 기쁜 앎, 오직 그것만이 내 영혼에 평화를 주는 그 앎을 나는 어디서 얻었는가?"], ["Whence did I get it?", "어디서 그것을 얻었단 말인가?"]], [["“Brought up with an idea of God, a Christian, my whole life filled with the spiritual blessings Christianity has given me, full of them, and living on those blessings, like the children I did not understand them, and destroy, that is try to destroy, what I live by.", "\"신에 대한 관념 속에서 자라난 그리스도교인으로서, 내 평생이 그리스도교가 내게 베푼 영적 축복으로 가득 차 있고, 그것으로 충만하며, 그 축복에 기대어 살아가면서도, 나는 아이들처럼 그것을 이해하지 못한 채, 내가 그것에 기대어 살아가는 바로 그것을 부수고, 아니 부수려 애쓰고 있었다."], ["And as soon as an important moment of life comes, like the children when they are cold and hungry, I turn to Him, and even less than the children when their mother scolds them for their childish mischief, do I feel that my childish efforts at wanton madness are reckoned against me.", "그러다 삶의 중대한 순간이 닥치면, 춥고 배고픈 아이들처럼 나는 그분께로 돌아서는데, 어머니가 아이들의 철없는 장난을 꾸짖을 때 아이들이 느끼는 것보다도 더 적게, 나는 함부로 부린 광기 어린 내 철없는 짓들이 내게 죄로 헤아려진다고 느끼지 못한다."]], [["“Yes, what I know, I know not by reason, but it has been given to me, revealed to me, and I know it with my heart, by faith in the chief thing taught by the church.", "\"그렇다, 내가 아는 것은 이성으로 아는 것이 아니라 내게 주어진 것, 계시된 것이며, 나는 그것을 가슴으로, 교회가 가르치는 가장 중요한 것에 대한 믿음으로 안다."]], [["“The church!", "\"교회!"], ["the church!”", "교회 말이야!\""], ["Levin repeated to himself.", "레빈은 혼자 거듭 되뇌었다."], ["He turned over on the other side, and leaning on his elbow, fell to gazing into the distance at a herd of cattle crossing over to the river.", "그는 몸을 반대편으로 돌려 팔꿈치를 괸 채, 멀리 강을 건너가는 한 무리의 가축들을 바라보기 시작했다."]], [["“But can I believe in all the church teaches?”", "\"하지만 교회가 가르치는 모든 것을 내가 믿을 수 있을까?\""], ["he thought, trying himself, and thinking of everything that could destroy his present peace of mind.", "그는 자신을 시험하며, 지금 누리는 마음의 평화를 무너뜨릴 수 있는 모든 것을 떠올리며 생각했다."], ["Intentionally he recalled all those doctrines of the church which had always seemed most strange and had always been a stumbling block to him.", "그는 늘 가장 이상하게 여겨졌고 늘 자기에게 걸림돌이 되어 왔던 교회의 모든 교리를 일부러 되짚어 보았다."]], [["“The Creation?", "\"창조?"], ["But how did I explain existence?", "하지만 내가 존재를 어떻게 설명했지?"], ["By existence?", "존재로? 무로?"], ["By nothing?", "악마와 죄."], ["The devil and sin. But how do I explain evil?...", "하지만 악을 내가 어떻게 설명하지?……"], ["The atonement?...", "속죄는?……"]], [["“But I know nothing, nothing, and I can know nothing but what has been told to me and all men.”", "\"하지만 나는 아무것도, 아무것도 알지 못하며, 나와 모든 인간에게 전해진 것 외에는 아무것도 알 수가 없다.\""]], [["And it seemed to him that there was not a single article of faith of the church which could destroy the chief thing—faith in God, in goodness, as the one goal of man’s destiny.", "그리고 그에게는 교회의 신앙 조항 가운데 단 하나도 가장 중요한 것을, 즉 신에 대한 믿음, 인간의 운명의 유일한 목표인 선에 대한 믿음을 무너뜨릴 수 있는 것은 없는 것처럼 여겨졌다."]], [["Under every article of faith of the church could be put the faith in the service of truth instead of one’s desires.", "교회의 신앙 조항 하나하나의 밑바탕에는, 자기 욕망 대신 진리를 섬기는 데 대한 믿음을 놓을 수 있었다."], ["And each doctrine did not simply leave that faith unshaken, each doctrine seemed essential to complete that great miracle, continually manifest upon earth, that made it possible for each man and millions of different sorts of men, wise men and imbeciles, old men and children—all men, peasants, Lvov, Kitty, beggars and kings to understand perfectly the same one thing, and to build up thereby that life of the soul which alone is worth living, and which alone is precious to us.", "그리고 어느 교리도 그 믿음을 흔들어 놓기는커녕, 모든 교리가 저마다 그 위대한 기적, 곧 땅 위에서 끊임없이 드러나는 그 기적을 완성하는 데 꼭 필요한 것처럼 여겨졌다. 그 기적이란, 각각의 사람이, 그리고 수백만의 온갖 부류의 사람들이, 현명한 자와 백치, 노인과 아이—모든 사람, 농부들, 르보프, 키티, 거지와 왕에 이르기까지—똑같은 단 하나의 것을 완벽하게 이해하고, 그것으로써 영혼의 삶, 오직 그것만이 살 가치가 있고 우리에게 유일하게 소중한 그 삶을 세울 수 있게 해 주는 것이었다."]], [["Lying on his back, he gazed up now into the high, cloudless sky.", "그는 등을 대고 누워, 이제 높고 구름 한 점 없는 하늘을 올려다보았다."], ["“Do I not know that that is infinite space, and that it is not a round arch?", "\"저것이 무한한 공간이며 둥근 둥근 천장이 아니라는 것을 내가 모르는가?"], ["But, however I screw up my eyes and strain my sight, I cannot see it not round and not bounded, and in spite of my knowing about infinite space, I am incontestably right when I see a solid blue dome, and more right than when I strain my eyes to see beyond it.”", "하지만 아무리 눈을 가늘게 뜨고 시선을 긴장시켜 보아도, 나는 그것이 둥글지 않고 경계가 없다고는 볼 수가 없으며, 무한한 공간에 대해 알고 있음에도, 단단한 푸른 둥근 천장을 볼 때의 내가 의심할 여지 없이 옳고, 그 너머를 보려고 눈을 긴장시킬 때보다 더 옳다.\""]], [["Levin ceased thinking, and only, as it were, listened to mysterious voices that seemed talking joyfully and earnestly within him.", "레빈은 생각을 멈추고, 그저 자기 안에서 기쁘고 진지하게 이야기하는 듯한 신비로운 목소리들에 말하자면 귀를 기울일 뿐이었다."]], [["“Can this be faith?”", "\"이것이 믿음일 수 있을까?\""], ["he thought, afraid to believe in his happiness.", "그는 자신의 행복을 믿기가 두려워 생각했다."], ["“My God, I thank Thee!”", "\"나의 하느님, 당신께 감사드립니다!\""], ["he said, gulping down his sobs, and with both hands brushing away the tears that filled his eyes.", "그는 복받쳐 오르는 흐느낌을 삼키며, 두 손으로 두 눈에 그득 고인 눈물을 훔치며 말했다."]], [["# Chapter 14", "# Chapter 14"]], [["Levin looked before him and saw a herd of cattle, then he caught sight of his trap with Raven in the shafts, and the coachman, who, driving up to the herd, said something to the herdsman.", "레빈은 앞을 바라보다 한 무리의 소 떼를 보았고, 이어 보로네이를 채에 맨 자신의 마차와, 그 소 떼 쪽으로 다가가 목동에게 무언가를 말하는 마부를 알아보았다."], ["Then he heard the rattle of the wheels and the snort of the sleek horse close by him.", "그러고는 바퀴가 덜그럭거리는 소리와 윤기 흐르는 말이 바로 곁에서 콧김을 내뿜는 소리를 들었다."], ["But he was so buried in his thoughts that he did not even wonder why the coachman had come for him.", "하지만 그는 자기 생각에 어찌나 깊이 잠겨 있었던지, 마부가 무슨 일로 자기를 데리러 왔는지조차 의아해하지 않았다."]], [["He only thought of that when the coachman had driven quite up to him and shouted to him.", "마부가 그에게 바짝 마차를 몰고 와 소리쳤을 때에야 비로소 그는 그것을 의식했다."], ["“The mistress sent me.", "\"마님께서 보내셨습니다요."], ["Your brother has come, and some gentleman with him.”", "동생분이 오셨고, 어떤 신사분도 함께 오셨습니다.\""]], [["Levin got into the trap and took the reins.", "레빈은 마차에 올라타 고삐를 잡았다."], ["As though just roused out of sleep, for a long while Levin could not collect his faculties.", "잠에서 막 깨어난 사람처럼, 레빈은 한참 동안 정신을 가다듬을 수가 없었다."], ["He stared at the sleek horse flecked with lather between his haunches and on his neck, where the harness rubbed, stared at Ivan the coachman sitting beside him, and remembered that he was expecting his brother, thought that his wife was most likely uneasy at his long absence, and tried to guess who was the visitor who had come with his brother.", "그는 마구가 쓸린 엉덩이 사이와 목덜미에 거품이 군데군데 묻어 있는 윤기 나는 말을 멍하니 바라보았고, 곁에 앉은 마부 이반을 멍하니 바라보았으며, 자기가 동생을 기다리고 있었다는 것을 떠올렸고, 아내가 자기가 오래 자리를 비워 불안해하고 있을 거라고 생각했으며, 동생과 함께 온 손님이 누구일지 짐작해 보려 애썼다."], ["And his brother and his wife and the unknown guest seemed to him now quite different from before.", "그러자 동생도, 아내도, 알지 못하는 손님도 이제 전과는 사뭇 다르게 느껴졌다."], ["He fancied that now his relations with all men would be different.", "그는 이제 모든 사람과의 관계가 달라지리라고 생각했다."]], [["“With my brother there will be none of that aloofness there always used to be between us, there will be no disputes; with Kitty there shall never be quarrels; with the visitor, whoever he may be, I will be friendly and nice; with the servants, with Ivan, it will all be different.”", "\"동생과는 늘 우리 사이에 있던 그 서먹함이 사라질 거야, 다툼도 없을 테고. 키티와는 결코 말다툼하지 않을 거야. 손님과는, 그가 누구든 간에, 다정하고 친절하게 대해야지. 하인들과도, 이반과도, 모든 게 달라질 거야.\""]], [["Pulling the stiff rein and holding in the good horse that snorted with impatience and seemed begging to be let go, Levin looked round at Ivan sitting beside him, not knowing what to do with his unoccupied hand, continually pressing down his shirt as it puffed out, and he tried to find something to start a conversation about with him.", "뻣뻣한 고삐를 당겨, 조바심에 콧김을 내뿜으며 어서 내달리게 해 달라고 조르는 듯한 그 좋은 말을 다잡으면서, 레빈은 곁에 앉은 이반을 돌아보았다. 이반은 할 일 없는 한 손을 어찌해야 할지 몰라 부풀어 오르는 셔츠 자락을 자꾸 눌러 대고 있었고, 레빈은 그와 더불어 이야기를 꺼낼 만한 무언가를 찾아보려 애썼다."], ["He would have said that Ivan had pulled the saddle-girth up too high, but that was like blame, and he longed for friendly, warm talk.", "안장 뱃대끈을 너무 높이 당겨 맸다고 말할 뻔했지만, 그건 책망처럼 들릴 듯했고, 그는 다정하고 따뜻한 대화를 갈망하고 있었다."], ["Nothing else occurred to him.", "그 밖에는 아무것도 떠오르지 않았다."]], [["“Your honor must keep to the right and mind that stump,”", "\"나리, 오른쪽으로 붙으셔야 합니다요, 저 그루터기를 조심하시고요.\""], ["said the coachman, pulling the rein Levin held.", "마부가 레빈이 쥔 고삐를 당기며 말했다."]], [["“Please don’t touch and don’t teach me!”", "\"제발 건드리지도 말고 나한테 가르치려 들지도 마!\""], ["said Levin, angered by this interference.", "이런 참견에 화가 치민 레빈이 말했다."], ["Now, as always, interference made him angry, and he felt sorrowfully at once how mistaken had been his supposition that his spiritual condition could immediately change him in contact with reality.", "지금도, 언제나 그랬듯이, 참견은 그를 화나게 했고, 그는 자신의 정신적 상태가 현실과 맞닥뜨리는 순간 곧바로 자기를 변화시킬 수 있으리라던 가정이 얼마나 잘못된 것이었는지를 이내 서글프게 느꼈다."]], [["He was not a quarter of a mile from home when he saw Grisha and Tanya running to meet him.", "집에서 채 사백 미터도 못 미친 지점에서 그는 그리샤와 타냐가 자기를 맞으러 달려오는 것을 보았다."]], [["“Uncle Kostya!", "\"코스챠 삼촌!"], ["mamma’s coming, and grandfather, and Sergey Ivanovitch, and someone else,”", "엄마가 오고 있어요, 할아버지하고, 세르게이 이바노비치하고, 또 다른 사람도요.\""], ["they said, clambering up into the trap.", "아이들은 마차로 기어오르며 말했다."]], [["“Who is he?”", "\"그 사람이 누구냐?\""]], [["“An awfully terrible person!", "\"엄청 무서운 사람이에요!"], ["And he does like this with his arms,”", "그리고 팔을 이렇게 한다니까요.\""], ["said Tanya, getting up in the trap and mimicking Katavasov.", "타냐가 마차에서 일어나 카타바소프를 흉내 내며 말했다."]], [["“Old or young?”", "\"늙은 사람이냐, 젊은 사람이냐?\""], ["asked Levin, laughing, reminded of someone, he did not know whom, by Tanya’s performance.", "레빈은 웃으며 물었다. 타냐의 시늉이 누군가를 떠올리게 했는데, 그게 누구인지는 알 수 없었다."]], [["“Oh, I hope it’s not a tiresome person!”", "\"아, 제발 따분한 사람이 아니어야 할 텐데!\""], ["thought Levin.", "레빈은 생각했다."]], [["As soon as he turned, at a bend in the road, and saw the party coming, Levin recognized Katavasov in a straw hat, walking along swinging his arms just as Tanya had shown him.", "길이 굽어지는 곳에서 몸을 돌려 그 일행이 다가오는 것을 보자마자, 레빈은 밀짚모자를 쓰고 타냐가 보여 준 그대로 팔을 휘저으며 걸어오는 카타바소프를 알아보았다."], ["Katavasov was very fond of discussing metaphysics, having derived his notions from natural science writers who had never studied metaphysics, and in Moscow Levin had had many arguments with him of late.", "카타바소프는 형이상학을 한 번도 공부한 적 없는 자연과학 저술가들에게서 자기 견해를 끌어다 쓰면서도 형이상학을 논하기를 무척 좋아했고, 모스크바에서 레빈은 근래 그와 여러 차례 논쟁을 벌인 적이 있었다."]], [["And one of these arguments, in which Katavasov had obviously considered that he came off victorious, was the first thing Levin thought of as he recognized him.", "그리고 그 논쟁들 가운데 하나, 카타바소프가 자기가 승리를 거두었다고 분명히 여겼던 그 논쟁이, 레빈이 그를 알아보면서 가장 먼저 떠올린 것이었다."]], [["“No, whatever I do, I won’t argue and give utterance to my ideas lightly,”", "\"아니, 무슨 일이 있어도, 나는 논쟁하지 않을 거고 내 생각을 함부로 입에 올리지도 않을 거야.\""], ["he thought.", "그는 생각했다."]], [["Getting out of the trap and greeting his brother and Katavasov, Levin asked about his wife.", "마차에서 내려 동생과 카타바소프에게 인사한 뒤, 레빈은 아내에 대해 물었다."]], [["“She has taken Mitya to Kolok” (a copse near the house).", "\"미챠를 데리고 콜록(집 근처의 작은 숲)에 갔어요."], ["“She meant to have him out there because it’s so hot indoors,”", "집 안이 너무 더워서 아이를 거기 데려가 있으려고요.\""], ["said Dolly.", "돌리가 말했다."], ["Levin had always advised his wife not to take the baby to the wood, thinking it unsafe, and he was not pleased to hear this.", "레빈은 늘 아내에게 아기를 숲으로 데려가지 말라고, 안전하지 않다고 충고해 왔던 터라, 이 말을 듣고 마음이 편치 않았다."]], [["“She rushes about from place to place with him,”", "\"애를 데리고 이곳저곳 헤집고 다닌다니까.\""], ["said the prince, smiling.", "공작이 미소 지으며 말했다."], ["“I advised her to try putting him in the ice cellar.”", "\"내가 아예 얼음 저장고에 넣어 두라고 권했지.\""]], [["“She meant to come to the bee-house.", "\"양봉장으로 오려고 했어요."], ["She thought you would be there.", "자네가 거기 있을 거라고 생각했거든."], ["We are going there,”", "우리도 그리로 가는 길이에요.\""], ["said Dolly.", "돌리가 말했다."]], [["“Well, and what are you doing?”", "\"그래, 너는 뭘 하고 지내냐?\""], ["said Sergey Ivanovitch, falling back from the rest and walking beside him.", "세르게이 이바노비치가 나머지 사람들에게서 뒤처져 그의 곁에서 걸으며 말했다."]], [["“Oh, nothing special.", "\"아, 특별한 건 없어요."], ["Busy as usual with the land,”", "늘 그렇듯 땅 일로 바쁘죠.\""], ["answered Levin.", "레빈이 대답했다."], ["“Well, and what about you?", "\"그런데 형은요?"], ["Come for long?", "오래 머무를 거예요?"], ["We have been expecting you for such a long time.”", "정말 오랫동안 형을 기다렸어요.\""]], [["“Only for a fortnight.", "\"보름만 있을 거야."], ["I’ve a great deal to do in Moscow.”", "모스크바에 할 일이 산더미라서.\""]], [["At these words the brothers’ eyes met, and Levin, in spite of the desire he always had, stronger than ever just now, to be on affectionate and still more open terms with his brother, felt an awkwardness in looking at him.", "이 말에 형제의 눈이 마주쳤고, 레빈은 늘 품어 온, 지금은 그 어느 때보다 강렬한, 형과 더 다정하고 더 허물없는 사이가 되고 싶다는 바람에도 불구하고, 형을 마주 보기가 어색하게 느껴졌다."], ["He dropped his eyes and did not know what to say.", "그는 눈길을 떨구었고 무슨 말을 해야 할지 몰랐다."]], [["Casting over the subjects of conversation that would be pleasant to Sergey Ivanovitch, and would keep him off the subject of the Servian war and the Slavonic question, at which he had hinted by the allusion to what he had to do in Moscow, Levin began to talk of Sergey Ivanovitch’s book.", "세르게이 이바노비치가 반가워할 만한, 그러면서도 그가 모스크바에서 할 일이 있다는 말로 넌지시 비친 세르비아 전쟁이며 슬라브 문제에서 그를 떼어 놓을 만한 화제를 이리저리 더듬던 끝에, 레빈은 세르게이 이바노비치의 책 이야기를 꺼냈다."]], [["“Well, have there been reviews of your book?”", "\"그래, 형 책에 대한 서평은 좀 나왔어요?\""], ["he asked.", "그가 물었다."]], [["Sergey Ivanovitch smiled at the intentional character of the question.", "세르게이 이바노비치는 그 질문의 의도적인 성격에 미소를 지었다."]], [["“No one is interested in that now, and I less than anyone,”", "\"이제 아무도 그런 데 관심이 없어, 나는 누구보다도 더 그렇고.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“Just look, Darya Alexandrovna, we shall have a shower,”", "\"저것 좀 보세요, 다리아 알렉산드로브나, 소나기가 오겠는데요.\""], ["he added, pointing with a sunshade at the white rain clouds that showed above the aspen tree-tops.", "그는 사시나무 우듬지 위로 보이는 하얀 비구름을 양산으로 가리키며 덧붙였다."]], [["And these words were enough to re-establish again between the brothers that tone—hardly hostile, but chilly—which Levin had been so longing to avoid.", "그리고 이 말만으로도 형제 사이에 다시 그 어조가—거의 적대적이지는 않으나 싸늘한 그 어조가—되살아나기에 충분했으니, 그것은 레빈이 그토록 피하고 싶어 하던 바로 그것이었다."]], [["Levin went up to Katavasov.", "레빈은 카타바소프에게 다가갔다."]], [["“It was jolly of you to make up your mind to come,”", "\"이렇게 와 주기로 마음먹어 주다니 정말 기쁩니다.\""], ["he said to him.", "그가 그에게 말했다."]], [["“I’ve been meaning to a long while.", "\"오래전부터 오려고 했지."], ["Now we shall have some discussion, we’ll see to that.", "이제 우리 토론 좀 해야지, 그건 두고 봅시다."], ["Have you been reading Spencer?”", "스펜서는 읽어 봤나?\""]], [["“No, I’ve not finished reading him,”", "\"아니요, 끝까지 읽지는 못했어요.\""], ["said Levin.", "레빈이 말했다."], ["“But I don’t need him now.”", "\"하지만 이제 그가 필요 없어요.\""]], [["“How’s that?", "\"그게 무슨 소린가?"], ["that’s interesting.", "그거 흥미로운데."], ["Why so?”", "어째서?\""]], [["“I mean that I’m fully convinced that the solution of the problems that interest me I shall never find in him and his like.", "\"제 말은, 저는 제가 관심을 두는 문제들의 해답을 그나 그 비슷한 사람들에게서는 결코 찾지 못하리라는 걸 완전히 확신하게 됐다는 거예요."], ["Now....”", "이제…….\""]], [["But Katavasov’s serene and good-humored expression suddenly struck him, and he felt such tenderness for his own happy mood, which he was unmistakably disturbing by this conversation, that he remembered his resolution and stopped short.", "그러나 카타바소프의 평온하고 유쾌한 표정이 문득 그의 눈에 들어왔고, 그는 이 대화로 자기가 틀림없이 흐트러뜨리고 있는 자신의 행복한 기분이 어찌나 애틋하던지, 자신의 결심을 떠올리고는 말을 뚝 그쳤다."]], [["“But we’ll talk later on,” he added.", "\"하지만 그 얘기는 나중에 하죠.\""], ["“If we’re going to the bee-house, it’s this way, along this little path,”", "그가 덧붙였다. \"양봉장으로 갈 거면, 이쪽이에요, 이 작은 오솔길을 따라서요.\""], ["he said, addressing them all.", "그가 모두에게 말했다."]], [["Going along the narrow path to a little uncut meadow covered on one side with thick clumps of brilliant heart’s-ease among which stood up here and there tall, dark green tufts of hellebore, Levin settled his guests in the dense, cool shade of the young aspens on a bench and some stumps purposely put there for visitors to the bee-house who might be afraid of the bees, and he went off himself to the hut to get bread, cucumbers, and fresh honey, to regale them with.", "좁은 오솔길을 따라, 한쪽으로는 화사한 삼색제비꽃이 빽빽이 무더기로 덮여 있고 그 사이로 군데군데 키 큰 짙은 초록빛 헬레보어 다발이 솟아 있는, 풀을 베지 않은 작은 풀밭에 이르자, 레빈은 어린 사시나무의 짙고 서늘한 그늘 아래, 벌을 무서워할지도 모를 양봉장 손님들을 위해 일부러 거기 놓아 둔 벤치와 그루터기 몇 개에 손님들을 앉혔고, 자신은 그들을 대접할 빵과 오이와 신선한 꿀을 가지러 오두막으로 갔다."]], [["Trying to make his movements as deliberate as possible, and listening to the bees that buzzed more and more frequently past him, he walked along the little path to the hut.", "되도록 동작을 느긋하게 하려고 애쓰며, 점점 더 자주 곁을 스쳐 지나가는 벌들의 윙윙거리는 소리에 귀를 기울이면서, 그는 오솔길을 따라 오두막으로 걸어갔다."], ["In the very entry one bee hummed angrily, caught in his beard, but he carefully extricated it.", "바로 입구에서 벌 한 마리가 그의 수염에 걸려 성난 듯 윙윙댔지만, 그는 조심스럽게 그것을 떼어 냈다."], ["Going into the shady outer room, he took down from the wall his veil, that hung on a peg, and putting it on, and thrusting his hands into his pockets, he went into the fenced-in bee-garden, where there stood in the midst of a closely mown space in regular rows, fastened with bast on posts, all the hives he knew so well, the old stocks, each with its own history, and along the fences the younger swarms hived that year.", "그늘진 바깥방으로 들어가 못에 걸려 있던 망사 모자를 벽에서 내려 머리에 쓰고 두 손을 주머니에 찔러 넣은 채, 그는 울타리를 두른 양봉장으로 들어갔다. 그곳에는 말끔하게 풀을 벤 공간 한복판에 가지런한 줄을 이루어, 말뚝에 인피로 동여맨 그가 너무도 잘 아는 벌통들이 죽 늘어서 있었으니, 저마다 제 사연을 지닌 오래된 벌통들이었고, 울타리를 따라서는 그해에 새로 들어앉힌 어린 벌 떼의 벌통들이 있었다."], ["In front of the openings of the hives, it made his eyes giddy to watch the bees and drones whirling round and round about the same spot, while among them the working bees flew in and out with spoils or in search of them, always in the same direction into the wood to the flowering lime trees and back to the hives.", "벌통 입구 앞에서는 벌과 수벌이 같은 자리를 빙글빙글 맴도는 것을 보고 있노라면 눈이 어지러웠고, 그 사이로 일벌들이 전리품을 안고 혹은 그것을 찾아 드나들었는데, 언제나 같은 방향으로, 꽃이 핀 보리수가 있는 숲으로 날아갔다가 다시 벌통으로 돌아왔다."]], [["His ears were filled with the incessant hum in various notes, now the busy hum of the working bee flying quickly off, then the blaring of the lazy drone, and the excited buzz of the bees on guard protecting their property from the enemy and preparing to sting.", "그의 귀는 갖가지 음조의 끊임없는 윙윙거림으로 가득 찼으니, 어떤 때는 부산하게 날아가는 일벌의 윙윙대는 소리였고, 또 어떤 때는 게으른 수벌의 시끄러운 소리였으며, 또 적으로부터 제 재산을 지키려고 침을 쏠 채비를 하는 보초 벌들의 흥분한 윙윙 소리였다."], ["On the farther side of the fence the old bee-keeper was shaving a hoop for a tub, and he did not see Levin.", "울타리 저편에서는 늙은 양봉지기가 통에 끼울 테를 깎고 있었는데, 그는 레빈을 보지 못했다."], ["Levin stood still in the midst of the beehives and did not call him.", "레빈은 벌통들 한복판에 가만히 서서 그를 부르지 않았다."]], [["He was glad of a chance to be alone to recover from the influence of ordinary actual life, which had already depressed his happy mood.", "그는 이미 자신의 행복한 기분을 짓눌러 버린 평범하고 현실적인 삶의 영향에서 벗어나 홀로 추스를 기회가 생긴 것이 기뻤다."], ["He thought that he had already had time to lose his temper with Ivan, to show coolness to his brother, and to talk flippantly with Katavasov.", "그는 자기가 벌써 이반에게 성을 내고, 형에게 냉랭함을 드러내고, 카타바소프와 경솔하게 떠들 시간을 가졌다는 것을 떠올렸다."]], [["“Can it have been only a momentary mood, and will it pass and leave no trace?”", "\"그게 그저 한순간의 기분에 지나지 않았던 걸까, 그래서 흔적도 없이 사라져 버리는 걸까?\""], ["he thought.", "그는 생각했다."], ["But the same instant, going back to his mood, he felt with delight that something new and important had happened to him.", "하지만 바로 그 순간, 그 기분으로 되돌아가면서, 그는 무언가 새롭고 중요한 일이 자기에게 일어났음을 환희에 차서 느꼈다."], ["Real life had only for a time overcast the spiritual peace he had found, but it was still untouched within him.", "현실의 삶은 그가 찾아낸 정신적 평화를 잠시 흐려 놓았을 뿐, 그 평화는 그의 내면에서 여전히 손상되지 않은 채 남아 있었다."]], [["Just as the bees, whirling round him, now menacing him and distracting his attention, prevented him from enjoying complete physical peace, forced him to restrain his movements to avoid them, so had the petty cares that had swarmed about him from the moment he got into the trap restricted his spiritual freedom; but that lasted only so long as he was among them.", "마치 그의 주위를 빙빙 돌며 때로는 위협하고 때로는 주의를 흩뜨리는 벌들이 그가 온전한 육체적 평온을 누리지 못하게 하고, 벌들을 피하려고 동작을 억누르게 만들었듯이, 마차에 올라탄 순간부터 그의 주위로 몰려든 사소한 근심거리들이 그의 정신적 자유를 옭아맸다. 하지만 그것은 그가 그것들 한가운데 있는 동안에만 그러했다."], ["Just as his bodily strength was still unaffected, in spite of the bees, so too was the spiritual strength that he had just become aware of.", "벌들이 있어도 그의 육체적 힘이 여전히 멀쩡했던 것과 똑같이, 그가 방금 깨닫게 된 정신적 힘 또한 그러했다."]], [["# Chapter 15", "# Chapter 15"]], [["“Do you know, Kostya, with whom Sergey Ivanovitch traveled on his way here?”", "“코스챠, 세르게이 이바노비치가 여기 오는 길에 누구랑 같이 왔는지 알아?”"], ["said Dolly, doling out cucumbers and honey to the children; “with Vronsky!", "돌리가 아이들에게 오이와 꿀을 나눠 주며 말했다. “브론스키랑 왔대!"], ["He’s going to Servia.”", "그가 세르비아로 간대.”"]], [["“And not alone; he’s taking a squadron out with him at his own expense,”", "“그것도 혼자가 아니라 자기 돈을 들여서 기병 중대 하나를 데리고 간다는군.”"], ["said Katavasov.", "카타바소프가 말했다."]], [["“That’s the right thing for him,”", "“그 사람한테는 딱 어울리는 일이지.”"], ["said Levin.", "레빈이 말했다."], ["“Are volunteers still going out then?”", "“그럼 의용병들이 아직도 나가고 있는 겁니까?”"], ["he added, glancing at Sergey Ivanovitch.", "그는 세르게이 이바노비치를 흘끗 바라보며 덧붙였다."]], [["Sergey Ivanovitch did not answer.", "세르게이 이바노비치는 대답하지 않았다."], ["He was carefully with a blunt knife getting a live bee covered with sticky honey out of a cup full of white honeycomb.", "그는 무딘 칼로, 끈끈한 꿀을 뒤집어쓴 살아 있는 벌 한 마리를 새하얀 벌집이 가득 든 잔에서 조심스럽게 꺼내고 있었다."]], [["“I should think so!", "“그럼요!"], ["You should have seen what was going on at the station yesterday!”", "어제 정거장에서 무슨 일이 벌어졌는지 보셨어야 합니다!”"], ["said Katavasov, biting with a juicy sound into a cucumber.", "카타바소프가 즙이 흐르는 소리를 내며 오이를 베어 물고 말했다."]], [["“Well, what is one to make of it?", "“글쎄, 대체 그걸 어떻게 이해해야 하나?"], ["For mercy’s sake, do explain to me, Sergey Ivanovitch, where are all those volunteers going, whom are they fighting with?”", "제발, 세르게이 이바노비치, 나한테 좀 설명해 주게. 그 의용병들이 다들 어디로 가는 거고, 누구랑 싸우겠다는 건가?”"], ["asked the old prince, unmistakably taking up a conversation that had sprung up in Levin’s absence.", "노공작이 물었다. 레빈이 없는 사이에 시작된 대화를 다시 이어 가려는 게 분명했다."]], [["“With the Turks,”", "“터키군과 싸우러 가는 거지요.”"], ["Sergey Ivanovitch answered, smiling serenely, as he extricated the bee, dark with honey and helplessly kicking, and put it with the knife on a stout aspen leaf.", "세르게이 이바노비치가 평온하게 미소 지으며 대답했다. 그러고는 꿀에 절어 거뭇해진 채 힘없이 발버둥치는 벌을 꺼내, 칼로 떠서 단단한 사시나무 잎 위에 올려놓았다."]], [["“But who has declared war on the Turks?—Ivan Ivanovitch Ragozov and Countess Lidia Ivanovna, assisted by Madame Stahl?”", "“하지만 누가 터키에 선전포고를 했단 말인가? 이반 이바노비치 라고조프와 리디아 이바노브나 백작 부인이, 스탈 부인의 도움을 받아서 한 건가?”"]], [["“No one has declared war, but people sympathize with their neighbors’ sufferings and are eager to help them,”", "“아무도 선전포고를 한 건 아닙니다. 다만 사람들이 이웃의 고통에 공감하고 그들을 돕고 싶어 하는 거지요.”"], ["said Sergey Ivanovitch.", "세르게이 이바노비치가 말했다."]], [["“But the prince is not speaking of help,”", "“하지만 공작님은 도움 얘기를 하시는 게 아닙니다.”"], ["said Levin, coming to the assistance of his father-in-law, “but of war.", "레빈이 장인을 거들고 나섰다. “전쟁 얘기를 하시는 거지요."], ["The prince says that private persons cannot take part in war without the permission of the government.”", "공작님 말씀은 사인(私人)이 정부의 허가 없이 전쟁에 참여할 수는 없다는 겁니다.”"]], [["“Kostya, mind, that’s a bee!", "“코스챠, 조심해요, 저거 벌이에요!"], ["Really, they’ll sting us!”", "정말, 우리를 쏘겠어요!”"], ["said Dolly, waving away a wasp.", "돌리가 말벌을 휘저어 쫓으며 말했다."]], [["“But that’s not a bee, it’s a wasp,”", "“그건 벌이 아니라 말벌이오.”"], ["said Levin.", "레빈이 말했다."]], [["“Well now, well, what’s your own theory?”", "“자, 그럼, 자네 자신의 이론은 뭔가?”"], ["Katavasov said to Levin with a smile, distinctly challenging him to a discussion.", "카타바소프가 레빈에게 미소를 지으며 말했다. 분명히 논쟁을 걸어오는 것이었다."], ["“Why have not private persons the right to do so?”", "“어째서 사인은 그럴 권리가 없다는 거지?”"]], [["“Oh, my theory’s this: war is on one side such a beastly, cruel, and awful thing, that no one man, not to speak of a Christian, can individually take upon himself the responsibility of beginning wars; that can only be done by a government, which is called upon to do this, and is driven inevitably into war. On the other hand, both political science and common sense teach us that in matters of state, and especially in the matter of war, private citizens must forego their personal individual will.”", "“오, 내 이론은 이렇습니다. 전쟁이란 한편으로는 너무나 잔인하고 끔찍하고 짐승 같은 짓이어서, 기독교인은 말할 것도 없고 어떤 사람도 개인적으로 전쟁을 시작하는 책임을 자기 어깨에 짊어질 수 없다는 거죠. 그건 오직 정부만 할 수 있는 일이고, 정부는 그렇게 하도록 부름받았으며, 불가피하게 전쟁으로 내몰리는 겁니다. 다른 한편으로, 정치학도 상식도 우리에게 가르쳐 주잖습니까. 국가의 일에서, 특히 전쟁의 일에서, 사적인 시민은 자기 개인적 의지를 포기해야 한다고 말입니다.”"]], [["Sergey Ivanovitch and Katavasov had their replies ready, and both began speaking at the same time.", "세르게이 이바노비치와 카타바소프는 반박할 말을 이미 준비하고 있었고, 둘이 동시에 입을 열었다."]], [["“But the point is, my dear fellow, that there may be cases when the government does not carry out the will of the citizens and then the public asserts its will,”", "“하지만 요점은 말일세, 이보게, 정부가 시민의 의지를 실행하지 않는 경우가 있을 수 있고, 그럴 때는 대중이 자기 의지를 주장한다는 거지.”"], ["said Katavasov.", "카타바소프가 말했다."]], [["But evidently Sergey Ivanovitch did not approve of this answer.", "그러나 세르게이 이바노비치는 분명 이 대답이 못마땅한 모양이었다."], ["His brows contracted at Katavasov’s words and he said something else.", "카타바소프의 말에 그의 눈썹이 찌푸려졌고, 그는 다른 말을 했다."]], [["“You don’t put the matter in its true light.", "“자네는 이 문제를 올바른 빛 속에서 보고 있지 않아."], ["There is no question here of a declaration of war, but simply the expression of a human Christian feeling.", "여기서 선전포고가 문제가 되는 게 아니라, 그저 인간적이고 기독교적인 감정의 표현이 문제인 거지."], ["Our brothers, one with us in religion and in race, are being massacred.", "종교와 혈통에서 우리와 하나인 우리 형제들이 학살당하고 있어."], ["Even supposing they were not our brothers nor fellow-Christians, but simply children, women, old people, feeling is aroused and Russians go eagerly to help in stopping these atrocities.", "설령 그들이 우리 형제도 아니고 같은 기독교인도 아니라 그저 아이들이고 여자들이고 노인들이라 해도, 감정이 일어나는 거고, 러시아인들은 그 잔학 행위를 멈추도록 돕겠다고 기꺼이 달려가는 거지."], ["Fancy, if you were going along the street and saw drunken men beating a woman or a child—I imagine you would not stop to inquire whether war had been declared on the men, but would throw yourself on them, and protect the victim.”", "생각해 보게, 자네가 거리를 가다가 술 취한 사내들이 여자나 아이를 두들겨 패는 걸 본다면 말이야—내 생각엔 자네는 그 사내들한테 전쟁이 선포됐는지 어떤지 따지고 있지는 않을 걸세. 그자들한테 몸을 던져서 희생자를 보호하겠지.”"]], [["“But I should not kill them,”", "“하지만 난 그들을 죽이지는 않겠지요.”"], ["said Levin.", "레빈이 말했다."]], [["“Yes, you would kill them.”", "“아니, 자네는 죽일 걸세.”"]], [["“I don’t know.", "“모르겠습니다."], ["If I saw that, I might give way to my impulse of the moment, but I can’t say beforehand.", "그런 광경을 본다면 순간적인 충동에 굴복할지도 모르지요. 하지만 미리 단정할 수는 없습니다."], ["And such a momentary impulse there is not, and there cannot be, in the case of the oppression of the Slavonic peoples.”", "그리고 슬라브 민족이 억압당하는 경우에는 그런 순간적인 충동 같은 건 없고, 있을 수도 없습니다.”"]], [["“Possibly for you there is not; but for others there is,”", "“자네한테는 없을지 몰라도, 다른 사람들한테는 있어.”"], ["said Sergey Ivanovitch, frowning with displeasure.", "세르게이 이바노비치가 못마땅한 듯 얼굴을 찌푸리며 말했다."], ["“There are traditions still extant among the people of Slavs of the true faith suffering under the yoke of the ‘unclean sons of Hagar.’ The people have heard of the sufferings of their brethren and have spoken.”", "“‘하갈의 부정한 자손들’의 멍에 아래서 고통받는 참된 신앙의 슬라브인들에 관한 전설이 민중 사이에 아직도 살아 있어. 민중은 자기 형제들의 고통에 관해 들었고, 입을 연 거지.”"]], [["“Perhaps so,”", "“그럴지도 모르죠.”"], ["said Levin evasively; “but I don’t see it.", "레빈이 얼버무리며 말했다. “하지만 저는 그게 보이지 않습니다."], ["I’m one of the people myself, and I don’t feel it.”", "저 자신이 민중의 한 사람인데, 저는 그걸 느끼지 못한단 말이에요.”"]], [["“Here am I too,”", "“나도 마찬가지일세.”"], ["said the old prince.", "노공작이 말했다."], ["“I’ve been staying abroad and reading the papers, and I must own, up to the time of the Bulgarian atrocities, I couldn’t make out why it was all the Russians were all of a sudden so fond of their Slavonic brethren, while I didn’t feel the slightest affection for them.", "“난 외국에 머물면서 신문을 읽었는데, 솔직히 말해서 불가리아 잔학 사건이 일어나기 전까지는 어째서 러시아인들이 죄다 갑자기 슬라브 형제들을 그렇게 끔찍이 사랑하게 됐는지 도무지 알 수가 없었어. 나는 그들에게 손톱만큼의 애정도 느끼지 못했으니까."], ["I was very much upset, thought I was a monster, or that it was the influence of Carlsbad on me.", "몹시 마음이 언짢았고, 내가 괴물인가, 아니면 카를스바트 물이 나한테 영향을 미친 건가 생각했지."], ["But since I have been here, my mind’s been set at rest.", "그런데 여기 온 뒤로 마음이 놓이더군."], ["I see that there are people besides me who’re only interested in Russia, and not in their Slavonic brethren.", "나 말고도 슬라브 형제들이 아니라 오직 러시아에만 관심이 있는 사람들이 있다는 걸 알게 됐거든."], ["Here’s Konstantin too.”", "여기 콘스탄친도 그렇지 않나.”"]], [["“Personal opinions mean nothing in such a case,”", "“이런 경우에 개인적인 의견은 아무 의미도 없어요.”"], ["said Sergey Ivanovitch; “it’s not a matter of personal opinions when all Russia—the whole people—has expressed its will.”", "세르게이 이바노비치가 말했다. “러시아 전체가—민중 전체가—그 의지를 표명했을 때는 개인적인 의견의 문제가 아닌 거죠.”"]], [["“But excuse me, I don’t see that.", "“하지만 미안하지만, 난 그게 보이지 않아."], ["The people don’t know anything about it, if you come to that,”", "따지고 보면 민중은 그것에 대해 아무것도 모른다고.”"], ["said the old prince.", "노공작이 말했다."]], [["“Oh, papa!...", "“어머, 아빠!……"], ["how can you say that?", "어떻게 그런 말씀을 하세요?"], ["And last Sunday in church?”", "지난 일요일 교회에서는요?”"], ["said Dolly, listening to the conversation.", "대화를 듣고 있던 돌리가 말했다."], ["“Please give me a cloth,”", "“행주 좀 주세요.”"], ["she said to the old man, who was looking at the children with a smile.", "그녀는 미소를 지으며 아이들을 바라보고 있던 노인에게 말했다."], ["“Why, it’s not possible that all....”", "“설마 다들 그게……”"]], [["“But what was it in church on Sunday?", "“하지만 일요일 교회에서 그게 뭐였느냐?"], ["The priest had been told to read that. He read it.", "사제더러 그걸 읽으라고 시킨 거고, 사제가 읽었지."], ["They didn’t understand a word of it.", "사람들은 한 마디도 못 알아들었어."], ["Then they were told that there was to be a collection for a pious object in church; well, they pulled out their halfpence and gave them, but what for they couldn’t say.”", "그러고는 교회에서 경건한 목적으로 헌금을 걷는다고 하니까, 글쎄, 다들 동전 몇 닢 꺼내서 냈지. 하지만 그게 무엇을 위한 건지는 말도 못 했단 말이야.”"]], [["“The people cannot help knowing; the sense of their own destinies is always in the people, and at such moments as the present that sense finds utterance,”", "“민중은 모를 수가 없습니다. 자신의 운명에 대한 감각은 언제나 민중 속에 있고, 지금 같은 순간에는 그 감각이 표현되는 거지요.”"], ["said Sergey Ivanovitch with conviction, glancing at the old bee-keeper.", "세르게이 이바노비치가 확신에 차서 말하며, 늙은 양봉가를 흘끗 바라보았다."]], [["The handsome old man, with black grizzled beard and thick silvery hair, stood motionless, holding a cup of honey, looking down from the height of his tall figure with friendly serenity at the gentlefolk, obviously understanding nothing of their conversation and not caring to understand it.", "검은빛이 섞인 희끗한 수염과 숱 많은 은발을 지닌 잘생긴 노인은, 꿀이 든 잔을 들고 꼼짝도 않고 서서, 큰 키의 높이에서 다정하고 평온한 눈으로 신사들을 내려다보고 있었다. 그들의 대화는 분명 한 마디도 이해하지 못하는 데다, 이해하려는 마음도 없어 보였다."]], [["“That’s so, no doubt,”", "“그렇고말고요, 틀림없습니다.”"], ["he said, with a significant shake of his head at Sergey Ivanovitch’s words.", "그는 세르게이 이바노비치의 말에 의미심장하게 고개를 끄덕이며 말했다."]], [["“Here, then, ask him.", "“자, 그럼, 이 사람한테 물어보십시오."], ["He knows nothing about it and thinks nothing,”", "이 사람은 그것에 대해 아무것도 모르고 아무 생각도 없습니다.”"], ["said Levin.", "레빈이 말했다."], ["“Have you heard about the war, Mihalitch?”", "“미할리치, 전쟁에 대해 들었소?”"], ["he said, turning to him.", "그가 노인을 돌아보며 말했다."], ["“What they read in the church?", "“교회에서 읽어 준 것 말이오."], ["What do you think about it?", "그걸 어떻게 생각하오?"], ["Ought we to fight for the Christians?”", "우리가 기독교인들을 위해 싸워야 한단 말이오?”"]], [["“What should we think?", "“우리가 뭘 생각하겠습니까?"], ["Alexander Nikolaevitch our Emperor has thought for us; he thinks for us indeed in all things.", "우리 황제 알렉산드르 니콜라예비치께서 우리를 위해 생각해 주시지요. 정말이지 모든 일에서 우리를 위해 생각해 주십니다."], ["It’s clearer for him to see.", "그분께는 더 잘 보이는 법이니까요."], ["Shall I bring a bit more bread?", "빵을 좀 더 가져올까요?"], ["Give the little lad some more?”", "도련님께 더 드릴까요?”"], ["he said addressing Darya Alexandrovna and pointing to Grisha, who had finished his crust.", "그는 다랴 알렉산드로브나를 향해 말하며, 빵 껍질을 다 먹은 그리샤를 가리켰다."]], [["“I don’t need to ask,”", "“난 물어볼 필요도 없네.”"], ["said Sergey Ivanovitch, “we have seen and are seeing hundreds and hundreds of people who give up everything to serve a just cause, come from every part of Russia, and directly and clearly express their thought and aim.", "세르게이 이바노비치가 말했다. “우리는 정의로운 대의를 위해 모든 걸 버리고, 러시아 방방곡곡에서 와서 자기 생각과 목적을 직접적이고 분명하게 표현하는 사람들을 수백, 수백 명씩 보아 왔고, 지금도 보고 있어."], ["They bring their halfpence or go themselves and say directly what for.", "그들은 동전을 가져오거나 직접 떠나면서 무엇을 위한 것인지 똑바로 말하지."], ["What does it mean?”", "그게 무슨 뜻이겠나?”"]], [["“It means, to my thinking,”", "“제 생각엔 그게 이런 뜻입니다.” 점점 흥분하기 시작한 레빈이 말했다."], ["said Levin, who was beginning to get warm, “that among eighty millions of people there can always be found not hundreds, as now, but tens of thousands of people who have lost caste, ne’er-do-wells, who are always ready to go anywhere—to Pogatchev’s bands, to Khiva, to Servia....”", "“팔천만 명의 사람들 중에는 언제든, 지금처럼 수백 명이 아니라 수만 명의, 신분을 잃고 빌빌대는 건달들이 있게 마련이라는 거죠. 그자들은 언제든 어디로든—푸가초프의 무리로든, 히바로든, 세르비아로든—기꺼이 떠날 준비가 돼 있는 자들이고요…….”"]], [["“I tell you that it’s not a case of hundreds or of ne’er-do-wells, but the best representatives of the people!”", "“수백 명이니 건달이니 하는 게 아니라, 민중의 가장 훌륭한 대표자들이라고 말하지 않나!”"], ["said Sergey Ivanovitch, with as much irritation as if he were defending the last penny of his fortune.", "세르게이 이바노비치가 마치 제 재산의 마지막 한 푼을 지키기라도 하는 양 잔뜩 짜증을 내며 말했다."], ["“And what of the subscriptions?", "“그럼 헌금은 또 어떻고?"], ["In this case it is a whole people directly expressing their will.”", "이 경우엔 한 민족 전체가 직접 자기 의지를 표현하고 있는 거지.”"]], [["“That word ‘people’ is so vague,”", "“그 ‘민중’이라는 말이 너무 막연합니다.”"], ["said Levin.", "레빈이 말했다."], ["“Parish clerks, teachers, and one in a thousand of the peasants, maybe, know what it’s all about.", "“교구 서기들, 교사들, 그리고 어쩌면 천 명에 한 명꼴인 농민들이나 이게 다 무슨 일인지 알겠지요."], ["The rest of the eighty millions, like Mihalitch, far from expressing their will, haven’t the faintest idea what there is for them to express their will about.", "나머지 팔천만 명은 미할리치처럼, 자기 의지를 표현하기는커녕 자기들이 무엇에 대해 의지를 표현해야 하는지 짐작조차 못 하는 사람들이에요."], ["What right have we to say that this is the people’s will?”", "그런데 우리한테 무슨 권리가 있어서 이게 민중의 의지라고 말한단 말입니까?”"]], [["# Chapter 16", "# Chapter 16"]], [["Sergey Ivanovitch, being practiced in argument, did not reply, but at once turned the conversation to another aspect of the subject.", "세르게이 이바노비치는 논쟁에 단련된 사람이었으므로 곧바로 반박하지 않고, 화제를 그 문제의 다른 측면으로 돌렸다."]], [["“Oh, if you want to learn the spirit of the people by arithmetical computation, of course it’s very difficult to arrive at it.", "\"오, 인민의 정신을 산술적 계산으로 알아내려 한다면야 물론 거기에 다다르기란 무척 어렵지. 게다가 투표는 우리에게 도입되지 않았고 또 도입될 수도 없어."], ["And voting has not been introduced among us and cannot be introduced, for it does not express the will of the people; but there are other ways of reaching that.", "그건 인민의 의지를 표현하지 못하니까. 하지만 거기에 이르는 다른 길들이 있단 말이야."], ["It is felt in the air, it is felt by the heart.", "그건 공기 중에서 느껴지고, 가슴으로 느껴지지."], ["I won’t speak of those deep currents which are astir in the still ocean of the people, and which are evident to every unprejudiced man; let us look at society in the narrow sense.", "편견 없는 사람이라면 누구에게나 분명히 보이는, 저 인민이라는 고요한 대양 속에서 일렁이는 깊은 물결에 대해서는 말하지 않겠어. 좁은 의미의 사회를 한번 보자고."], ["All the most diverse sections of the educated public, hostile before, are merged in one.", "전에는 서로 적대하던, 교육받은 대중의 가장 다양한 부류들이 하나로 합쳐졌어."], ["Every division is at an end, all the public organs say the same thing over and over again, all feel the mighty torrent that has overtaken them and is carrying them in one direction.”", "모든 분열은 끝났고, 모든 언론 기관이 똑같은 말을 거듭거듭 되풀이하고 있으며, 모두가 자기들을 사로잡아 한 방향으로 실어 나르는 거대한 격류를 느끼고 있어.\""]], [["“Yes, all the newspapers do say the same thing,”", "\"그래, 모든 신문이 똑같은 말을 하긴 하지.\""], ["said the prince.", "공작이 말했다."], ["“That’s true.", "\"그건 사실이야."], ["But so it is the same thing that all the frogs croak before a storm.", "하지만 폭풍 전에 개구리들이 죄다 똑같이 우는 것도 마찬가지란 말이지."], ["One can hear nothing for them.”", "그것들 때문에 아무 소리도 들리지 않거든.\""]], [["“Frogs or no frogs, I’m not the editor of a paper and I don’t want to defend them; but I am speaking of the unanimity in the intellectual world,”", "\"개구리든 아니든, 나는 신문 편집자도 아니고 그들을 옹호하고 싶지도 않습니다. 다만 나는 지식인 세계의 만장일치를 말하고 있는 거예요.\""], ["said Sergey Ivanovitch, addressing his brother.", "세르게이 이바노비치가 동생을 향해 말했다."], ["Levin would have answered, but the old prince interrupted him.", "레빈이 뭐라 대답하려 했지만 늙은 공작이 가로막았다."]], [["“Well, about that unanimity, that’s another thing, one may say,”", "\"글쎄, 그 만장일치라는 것 말인데, 그건 또 다른 얘기라고 할 수 있지.\""], ["said the prince.", "공작이 말했다."], ["“There’s my son-in-law, Stepan Arkadyevitch, you know him.", "\"내 사위 스테판 아르카지치, 자네도 알지 않나."], ["He’s got a place now on the committee of a commission and something or other, I don’t remember.", "그 친구가 지금 무슨 위원회의 어느 분과인지 뭔지에 자리를 하나 얻었는데, 나도 기억이 안 나."], ["Only there’s nothing to do in it—why, Dolly, it’s no secret!—and a salary of eight thousand.", "다만 거기서 할 일이라고는 아무것도 없단 말이지—아니 돌리, 이건 비밀도 아니잖니!—그런데 봉급은 팔천이야."], ["You try asking him whether his post is of use, he’ll prove to you that it’s most necessary.", "그 친구한테 한번 물어보게나, 자네 자리가 쓸모가 있느냐고. 그러면 그게 가장 필요한 자리라는 걸 자네한테 증명해 보일 거야."], ["And he’s a truthful man too, but there’s no refusing to believe in the utility of eight thousand roubles.”", "게다가 그 친구는 정직한 사람이거든. 하지만 팔천 루블의 유용성을 믿지 않을 도리가 없는 거지.\""]], [["“Yes, he asked me to give a message to Darya Alexandrovna about the post,”", "\"그렇잖아도 그 자리에 관해 다리아 알렉산드로브나에게 전해 달라고 부탁하더군요.\""], ["said Sergey Ivanovitch reluctantly, feeling the prince’s remark to be ill-timed.", "세르게이 이바노비치가 마지못해 말했다. 그는 공작의 말이 때에 맞지 않는다고 느꼈다."]], [["“So it is with the unanimity of the press.", "\"언론의 만장일치라는 것도 마찬가지야."], ["That’s been explained to me: as soon as there’s war their incomes are doubled.", "누가 나한테 설명해 줬는데, 전쟁이 나기만 하면 그자들 수입이 두 배가 된다는군. 그러니 어떻게 인민과 슬라브 민족의 운명을……"], ["How can they help believing in the destinies of the people and the Slavonic races ... and all that?”", "그 밖에 온갖 것들을 믿지 않을 수 있겠나?\""]], [["“I don’t care for many of the papers, but that’s unjust,”", "\"신문 중에는 내 마음에 안 드는 게 많긴 하지만, 그건 부당한 말씀입니다.\""], ["said Sergey Ivanovitch.", "세르게이 이바노비치가 말했다."]], [["“I would only make one condition,”", "\"나는 단 한 가지 조건만 달겠네.\""], ["pursued the old prince.", "늙은 공작이 말을 이었다."], ["“Alphonse Karr said a capital thing before the war with Prussia: ‘You consider war to be inevitable?", "\"알퐁스 카르가 프로이센과의 전쟁 전에 기막힌 말을 했지. '당신은 전쟁이 불가피하다고 보시오?"], ["Very good.", "아주 좋소."], ["Let everyone who advocates war be enrolled in a special regiment of advance-guards, for the front of every storm, of every attack, to lead them all!’”", "그렇다면 전쟁을 주창하는 자는 모조리 특별 선발 전위 연대에 편입시켜, 모든 폭풍의 맨 앞, 모든 공격의 선봉에 세워 그들 전부를 이끌게 합시다!'\""]], [["“A nice lot the editors would make!”", "\"편집자들이 좋은 부대를 만들겠군요!\""], ["said Katavasov, with a loud roar, as he pictured the editors he knew in this picked legion.", "카타바소프가 그 선발된 군단 속에 자기가 아는 편집자들을 그려 보며 큰 소리로 껄껄 웃었다."]], [["“But they’d run,”", "\"하지만 그자들은 도망칠 거예요.\""], ["said Dolly, “they’d only be in the way.”", "돌리가 말했다. \"방해만 될 뿐이죠.\""]], [["“Oh, if they ran away, then we’d have grape-shot or Cossacks with whips behind them,”", "\"오, 도망친다면 그 뒤에 산탄포나 채찍을 든 카자크 병사들을 세워 두면 되지.\""], ["said the prince.", "공작이 말했다."]], [["“But that’s a joke, and a poor one too, if you’ll excuse my saying so, prince,”", "\"하지만 그건 농담이고, 죄송하지만 그것도 시원찮은 농담입니다, 공작님.\""], ["said Sergey Ivanovitch.", "세르게이 이바노비치가 말했다."]], [["“I don’t see that it was a joke, that....”", "\"나는 그게 농담인지 모르겠는데, 그게…….\""], ["Levin was beginning, but Sergey Ivanovitch interrupted him.", "레빈이 말을 꺼내려 했지만 세르게이 이바노비치가 가로막았다."]], [["“Every member of society is called upon to do his own special work,” said he.", "\"사회의 모든 구성원은 저마다 자기 고유의 일을 하도록 부름을 받은 거야.\""], ["“And men of thought are doing their work when they express public opinion.", "그가 말했다. \"그리고 사상가들은 여론을 표현할 때 자기 일을 하는 거지."], ["And the single-hearted and full expression of public opinion is the service of the press and a phenomenon to rejoice us at the same time.", "여론의 순수하고 충만한 표현이야말로 언론의 봉사이며, 동시에 우리를 기쁘게 하는 현상이야. 이십 년 전이었다면 우리는 침묵하고 있었겠지만, 이제 우리는 러시아 인민의 목소리를 들었어."], ["Twenty years ago we should have been silent, but now we have heard the voice of the Russian people, which is ready to rise as one man and ready to sacrifice itself for its oppressed brethren; that is a great step and a proof of strength.”", "한 사람처럼 떨쳐 일어나 박해받는 형제들을 위해 자신을 희생할 준비가 된 그 목소리를 말이야. 그건 위대한 진보이며 힘의 증거지.\""]], [["“But it’s not only making a sacrifice, but killing Turks,”", "\"하지만 그건 단지 희생하는 게 아니라 터키인을 죽이는 일이잖아요.\""], ["said Levin timidly.", "레빈이 머뭇거리며 말했다."], ["“The people make sacrifices and are ready to make sacrifices for their soul, but not for murder,”", "\"인민은 자기들의 영혼을 위해 희생을 하고 또 희생할 준비도 되어 있지만, 살인을 위해서는 아니에요.\""], ["he added, instinctively connecting the conversation with the ideas that had been absorbing his mind.", "그는 자기 마음을 사로잡고 있던 생각들과 이 대화를 본능적으로 연결시키며 덧붙였다."]], [["“For their soul?", "\"영혼을 위해서라고?"], ["That’s a most puzzling expression for a natural science man, do you understand?", "그건 자연과학을 하는 사람에게는 참으로 알쏭달쏭한 표현인데, 알겠나?"], ["What sort of thing is the soul?”", "영혼이라는 게 도대체 어떤 거지?\""], ["said Katavasov, smiling.", "카타바소프가 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["“Oh, you know!”", "\"아, 알면서 그러시는군요!\""]], [["“No, by God, I haven’t the faintest idea!”", "\"아니, 맹세코 난 도무지 모르겠는걸!\""], ["said Katavasov with a loud roar of laughter.", "카타바소프가 큰 소리로 웃음을 터뜨리며 말했다."]], [["“‘I bring not peace, but a sword,’ says Christ,”", "\"'나는 평화가 아니라 칼을 주러 왔노라'라고 그리스도께서 말씀하셨지.\""], ["Sergey Ivanovitch rejoined for his part, quoting as simply as though it were the easiest thing to understand the very passage that had always puzzled Levin most.", "세르게이 이바노비치가 자기 입장에서 응수하며, 늘 레빈을 가장 곤혹스럽게 했던 바로 그 구절을 마치 이해하기에 더없이 쉬운 것이라도 되는 양 간단히 인용했다."]], [["“That’s so, no doubt,”", "\"그렇고말고, 의심할 여지가 없지.\""], ["the old man repeated again.", "늙은 공작이 다시 한번 되뇌었다."], ["He was standing near them and responded to a chance glance turned in his direction.", "그는 그들 가까이에 서 있다가 자기 쪽으로 우연히 던져진 시선에 응답한 것이었다."]], [["“Ah, my dear fellow, you’re defeated, utterly defeated!”", "\"아하, 이보게, 자네 졌네, 완전히 졌어!\""], ["cried Katavasov good-humoredly.", "카타바소프가 유쾌하게 외쳤다."]], [["Levin reddened with vexation, not at being defeated, but at having failed to control himself and being drawn into argument.", "레빈은 분해서 얼굴이 붉어졌는데, 진 것 때문이 아니라 자제하지 못하고 논쟁에 말려든 것 때문이었다."]], [["“No, I can’t argue with them,”", "'아냐, 난 저 사람들과 논쟁할 수 없어.'"], ["he thought; “they wear impenetrable armor, while I’m naked.”", "그는 생각했다. '저들은 뚫리지 않는 갑옷을 입고 있는데, 나는 알몸이잖아.'"]], [["He saw that it was impossible to convince his brother and Katavasov, and he saw even less possibility of himself agreeing with them.", "그는 형과 카타바소프를 설득하는 것이 불가능하다는 것을 알았고, 자기가 그들에게 동의하는 것은 그보다 더 불가능하다는 것을 알았다."], ["What they advocated was the very pride of intellect that had almost been his ruin.", "그들이 주창하는 것은 바로 그를 거의 파멸시킬 뻔했던 그 지성의 오만이었다."], ["He could not admit that some dozens of men, among them his brother, had the right, on the ground of what they were told by some hundreds of glib volunteers swarming to the capital, to say that they and the newspapers were expressing the will and feeling of the people, and a feeling which was expressed in vengeance and murder.", "그는 몇십 명의 사람들이, 그중에는 자기 형도 끼여 있지만, 수도로 몰려드는 수백 명의 말 잘하는 의용병들에게 들은 이야기를 근거로, 자기들과 신문이 인민의 의지와 감정을 표현하고 있다고, 그것도 복수와 살인으로 표출되는 감정을 표현하고 있다고 말할 권리가 있다는 것을 인정할 수 없었다."], ["He could not admit this, because he neither saw the expression of such feelings in the people among whom he was living, nor found them in himself (and he could not but consider himself one of the persons making up the Russian people), and most of all because he, like the people, did not know and could not know what is for the general good, though he knew beyond a doubt that this general good could be attained only by the strict observance of that law of right and wrong which has been revealed to every man, and therefore he could not wish for war or advocate war for any general objects whatever.", "그는 이것을 인정할 수 없었는데, 자기가 살아가는 인민들 속에서도 그런 감정의 표현을 보지 못했고, 자기 자신 안에서도 그것을 찾지 못했기 때문이며(그리고 그는 자신을 러시아 인민을 구성하는 사람들 중 하나로 여기지 않을 수 없었다), 무엇보다도 그 자신이 인민과 마찬가지로 무엇이 공공의 선인지 알지 못하고 또 알 수도 없었기 때문이다. 다만 그는 이 공공의 선이 모든 사람에게 계시된 옳고 그름의 그 법칙을 엄격히 준수함으로써만 이룰 수 있다는 것을 의심의 여지 없이 알고 있었기에, 그 어떤 공공의 목적을 위해서도 전쟁을 바랄 수도, 전쟁을 주창할 수도 없었다."], ["He said as Mihalitch did and the people, who had expressed their feeling in the traditional invitations of the Varyagi: “Be princes and rule over us.", "그는 미할리치가 한 말처럼, 그리고 바랴기를 부르는 전통적인 초청의 말로 자기 감정을 표현한 인민들처럼 말했다. \"공작이 되어 우리를 다스려 주소서."], ["Gladly we promise complete submission.", "우리는 기꺼이 완전한 복종을 약속하겠나이다."], ["All the labor, all humiliations, all sacrifices we take upon ourselves; but we will not judge and decide.”", "모든 노고, 모든 굴욕, 모든 희생을 우리 스스로 떠맡겠나이다. 다만 우리는 판단하지도 결정하지도 않겠나이다.\""], ["And now, according to Sergey Ivanovitch’s account, the people had foregone this privilege they had bought at such a costly price.", "그런데 세르게이 이바노비치의 이야기대로라면, 인민은 그토록 비싼 값을 치르고 사들인 이 특권을 이제 스스로 포기해 버린 셈이었다."]], [["He wanted to say too that if public opinion were an infallible guide, then why were not revolutions and the commune as lawful as the movement in favor of the Slavonic peoples?", "그는 이런 말도 하고 싶었다. 만약 여론이 오류 없는 길잡이라면, 그렇다면 어째서 혁명이나 코뮌은 슬라브 민족을 위한 운동만큼 정당한 것이 못 되느냐고."], ["But these were merely thoughts that could settle nothing.", "하지만 이런 것들은 아무것도 해결할 수 없는 한낱 생각에 지나지 않았다."], ["One thing could be seen beyond doubt—that was that at the actual moment the discussion was irritating Sergey Ivanovitch, and so it was wrong to continue it.", "의심의 여지 없이 분명한 것은 단 하나—바로 이 순간 이 논의가 세르게이 이바노비치를 짜증나게 하고 있으며, 따라서 그것을 계속하는 것은 잘못이라는 사실이었다."], ["And Levin ceased speaking and then called the attention of his guests to the fact that the storm clouds were gathering, and that they had better be going home before it rained.", "레빈은 말을 멈추고, 이어 손님들에게 먹구름이 몰려들고 있으니 비가 오기 전에 집으로 돌아가는 편이 낫겠다고 일러 주었다."]], [["# Chapter 17", "# Chapter 17"]], [["The old prince and Sergey Ivanovitch got into the trap and drove off; the rest of the party hastened homewards on foot.", "노공작과 세르게이 이바노비치는 트랩에 올라 떠났고, 나머지 일행은 서둘러 집으로 걸어갔다."]], [["But the storm-clouds, turning white and then black, moved down so quickly that they had to quicken their pace to get home before the rain.", "그러나 하얗게 변했다가 다시 검게 변하는 먹구름이 어찌나 빠르게 내려오는지, 그들은 비가 오기 전에 집에 닿으려고 걸음을 재촉해야 했다."], ["The foremost clouds, lowering and black as soot-laden smoke, rushed with extraordinary swiftness over the sky.", "맨 앞의 구름은 그을음을 잔뜩 머금은 연기처럼 낮게 깔리고 새카매서, 보통이 아닌 빠르기로 하늘을 가로질러 내달았다."], ["They were still two hundred paces from home and a gust of wind had already blown up, and every second the downpour might be looked for.", "집까지는 아직 이백 보쯤 남아 있었는데 이미 한바탕 돌풍이 불어 닥쳤고, 당장이라도 폭우가 쏟아질 것만 같았다."]], [["The children ran ahead with frightened and gleeful shrieks.", "아이들은 겁에 질리면서도 즐거운 비명을 지르며 앞장서 달려갔다."], ["Darya Alexandrovna, struggling painfully with her skirts that clung round her legs, was not walking, but running, her eyes fixed on the children.", "다리아 알렉산드로브나는 다리에 휘감기는 치맛자락과 괴롭게 씨름하면서, 걷는 게 아니라 뛰다시피 했고, 두 눈은 아이들에게 고정되어 있었다."], ["The men of the party, holding their hats on, strode with long steps beside her.", "일행의 남자들은 모자를 손으로 누른 채 그녀 곁에서 성큼성큼 큰 걸음으로 걸었다."], ["They were just at the steps when a big drop fell splashing on the edge of the iron guttering.", "그들이 막 현관 계단에 다다랐을 때, 큰 빗방울 하나가 철제 빗물받이 가장자리에 철퍽 떨어졌다."], ["The children and their elders after them ran into the shelter of the house, talking merrily.", "아이들이, 그리고 그 뒤를 따라 어른들이 즐겁게 떠들며 집 안의 처마 밑으로 뛰어 들어갔다."]], [["“Katerina Alexandrovna?”", "\"카체리나 알렉산드로브나는?\""], ["Levin asked of Agafea Mihalovna, who met them with kerchiefs and rugs in the hall.", "레빈이 손수건과 무릎 덮개를 들고 현관에서 그들을 맞은 아가피야 미하일로브나에게 물었다."]], [["“We thought she was with you,”", "\"우리는 나리와 함께 계신 줄 알았는데요.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“And Mitya?”", "\"미챠는?\""]], [["“In the copse, he must be, and the nurse with him.”", "\"숲 속에 계실 거예요, 유모랑 같이요.\""]], [["Levin snatched up the rugs and ran towards the copse.", "레빈은 무릎 덮개를 낚아채 숲을 향해 달려갔다."]], [["In that brief interval of time the storm clouds had moved on, covering the sun so completely that it was dark as an eclipse.", "그 짧은 사이에 먹구름은 더 멀리 이동해 해를 완전히 가려 버려, 일식이라도 일어난 듯 어두웠다."], ["Stubbornly, as though insisting on its rights, the wind stopped Levin, and tearing the leaves and flowers off the lime trees and stripping the white birch branches into strange unseemly nakedness, it twisted everything on one side—acacias, flowers, burdocks, long grass, and tall tree-tops.", "마치 제 권리를 우기기라도 하듯 고집스럽게 바람이 레빈을 가로막았고, 보리수의 잎과 꽃을 뜯어내고 흰 자작나무 가지들을 기이하고 흉하게 발가벗기며, 아카시아며 꽃이며 우엉이며 긴 풀이며 키 큰 나무 우듬지며 모든 것을 한쪽으로 비틀어 놓았다."], ["The peasant girls working in the garden ran shrieking into shelter in the servants’ quarters.", "정원에서 일하던 농가 처녀들이 비명을 지르며 하인 숙소로 피해 달려갔다."], ["The streaming rain had already flung its white veil over all the distant forest and half the fields close by, and was rapidly swooping down upon the copse.", "쏟아지는 비는 이미 저 멀리 숲 전체와 가까운 들판 절반에 하얀 장막을 드리웠고, 빠르게 숲을 향해 덮쳐 오고 있었다."], ["The wet of the rain spurting up in tiny drops could be smelt in the air.", "잘게 부서지며 튀어 오르는 빗물의 축축한 냄새가 공기 중에 풍겼다."]], [["Holding his head bent down before him, and struggling with the wind that strove to tear the wraps away from him, Levin was moving up to the copse and had just caught sight of something white behind the oak tree, when there was a sudden flash, the whole earth seemed on fire, and the vault of heaven seemed crashing overhead.", "레빈은 머리를 앞으로 숙인 채, 덮개를 자기에게서 떼어 내려고 기를 쓰는 바람과 씨름하며 숲으로 다가가고 있었고, 막 떡갈나무 뒤편의 하얀 무언가를 발견한 순간, 별안간 번쩍하는 섬광이 일더니 온 땅이 불타오르는 듯하고 하늘의 천장이 머리 위로 무너져 내리는 것 같았다."], ["Opening his blinded eyes, Levin gazed through the thick veil of rain that separated him now from the copse, and to his horror the first thing he saw was the green crest of the familiar oak-tree in the middle of the copse uncannily changing its position.", "멀어 버린 눈을 뜨고 레빈은 이제 그를 숲에서 갈라놓는 두터운 빗줄기의 장막 너머를 응시했고, 끔찍하게도 가장 먼저 눈에 들어온 것은 숲 한가운데 낯익은 떡갈나무의 푸른 우듬지가 으스스하게 위치를 바꾸는 모습이었다."], ["“Can it have been struck?”", "\"벼락을 맞은 건가?\""], ["Levin hardly had time to think when, moving more and more rapidly, the oak tree vanished behind the other trees, and he heard the crash of the great tree falling upon the others.", "레빈은 미처 생각할 겨를도 없이, 점점 더 빠르게 움직이던 떡갈나무가 다른 나무들 뒤로 사라지는 것을 보았고, 그 거대한 나무가 다른 나무들 위로 쓰러지는 굉음을 들었다."]], [["The flash of lightning, the crash of thunder, and the instantaneous chill that ran through him were all merged for Levin in one sense of terror.", "번개의 섬광, 천둥의 굉음, 그리고 순간적으로 온몸을 훑고 지나간 한기, 이 모든 것이 레빈에게는 하나의 공포감으로 뒤엉켰다."]], [["“My God!", "\"오, 하느님!"], ["my God!", "하느님!"], ["not on them!”", "그들에게는 안 됩니다!\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["And though he thought at once how senseless was his prayer that they should not have been killed by the oak which had fallen now, he repeated it, knowing that he could do nothing better than utter this senseless prayer.", "그리고 방금 쓰러진 떡갈나무에 그들이 깔려 죽지 않게 해 달라는 자기 기도가 얼마나 부질없는 것인지 곧바로 떠올렸음에도, 그는 이 부질없는 기도를 입에 올리는 것 말고는 달리 할 수 있는 일이 없음을 알면서도 그것을 거듭 되뇌었다."]], [["Running up to the place where they usually went, he did not find them there.", "그들이 늘 가던 곳까지 달려갔지만, 거기서 그들을 찾지 못했다."]], [["They were at the other end of the copse under an old lime-tree; they were calling him.", "그들은 숲의 다른 쪽 끝, 늙은 보리수 아래에 있었다. 그들이 그를 부르고 있었다."], ["Two figures in dark dresses (they had been light summer dresses when they started out) were standing bending over something.", "검은 옷차림(처음 나섰을 때는 가벼운 여름 옷차림이었다)의 두 사람이 무언가를 굽어보며 서 있었다."], ["It was Kitty with the nurse.", "키티와 유모였다."], ["The rain was already ceasing, and it was beginning to get light when Levin reached them.", "레빈이 그들에게 닿았을 때 비는 이미 그쳐 가고 있었고 사위가 밝아지기 시작했다."], ["The nurse was not wet on the lower part of her dress, but Kitty was drenched through, and her soaked clothes clung to her.", "유모는 옷의 아랫부분이 젖지 않았지만, 키티는 흠뻑 젖어 흠씬 적은 옷이 몸에 들러붙어 있었다."], ["Though the rain was over, they still stood in the same position in which they had been standing when the storm broke.", "비는 그쳤건만, 그들은 폭풍우가 닥쳤을 때 서 있던 그 자리에 그대로 서 있었다."], ["Both stood bending over a perambulator with a green umbrella.", "둘 다 초록 양산이 달린 유모차 위로 몸을 굽히고 있었다."]], [["“Alive? Unhurt?", "\"살아 있어?"], ["Thank God!”", "다친 데 없어?"], ["he said, splashing with his soaked boots through the standing water and running up to them.", "다행이다!\" 그는 흠뻑 젖은 장화로 고인 물을 철벅거리며 그들에게 달려가 말했다."]], [["Kitty’s rosy wet face was turned towards him, and she smiled timidly under her shapeless sopped hat.", "키티의 발그레하고 젖은 얼굴이 그를 향해 돌려졌고, 그녀는 형태를 잃고 흠뻑 젖은 모자 아래에서 머뭇머뭇 미소를 지었다."]], [["“Aren’t you ashamed of yourself?", "\"부끄러운 줄도 모르나?"], ["I can’t think how you can be so reckless!”", "어쩌면 이렇게 무모할 수가 있어!\""], ["he said angrily to his wife.", "그가 아내에게 화를 내며 말했다."]], [["“It wasn’t my fault, really.", "\"정말이지 내 잘못이 아니에요."], ["We were just meaning to go, when he made such a to-do that we had to change him.", "우리도 막 가려던 참이었는데, 이 애가 어찌나 칭얼대는지 기저귀를 갈아 줘야 했어요."], ["We were just....”", "우리는 그저…….\""], ["Kitty began defending herself.", "키티가 자신을 변호하기 시작했다."]], [["Mitya was unharmed, dry, and still fast asleep.", "미챠는 다친 데 없이 보송보송하게 여전히 곤히 자고 있었다."]], [["“Well, thank God!", "\"그래, 다행이군!"], ["I don’t know what I’m saying!”", "내가 무슨 소리를 하는지 모르겠어!\""]], [["They gathered up the baby’s wet belongings; the nurse picked up the baby and carried it.", "그들은 아기의 젖은 물건들을 챙겼다. 유모가 아기를 안아 들고 갔다."], ["Levin walked beside his wife, and, penitent for having been angry, he squeezed her hand when the nurse was not looking.", "레빈은 아내 곁에서 걸었고, 화를 낸 것을 뉘우치며 유모가 보지 않을 때 아내의 손을 꼭 쥐었다."]], [["# Chapter 18", "# Chapter 18"]], [["During the whole of that day, in the extremely different conversations in which he took part, only as it were with the top layer of his mind, in spite of the disappointment of not finding the change he expected in himself, Levin had been all the while joyfully conscious of the fulness of his heart.", "그날 하루 종일 레빈은, 자기 마음의 가장 윗부분으로만 끼어든 갖가지 잡다한 대화 속에서, 그리고 자기 안에서 기대했던 변화를 찾지 못한 실망에도 불구하고, 내내 가슴이 충만하다는 것을 기쁘게 의식하고 있었다."]], [["After the rain it was too wet to go for a walk; besides, the storm clouds still hung about the horizon, and gathered here and there, black and thundery, on the rim of the sky.", "비가 온 뒤라 땅이 너무 질척여서 산책을 나갈 수 없었다. 게다가 먹구름이 여전히 지평선 언저리에 걸려 있다가 하늘 가장자리 여기저기에 검고 우레가 칠 듯 모여들곤 했다."], ["The whole party spent the rest of the day in the house.", "일행은 남은 하루를 모두 집 안에서 보냈다."]], [["No more discussions sprang up; on the contrary, after dinner everyone was in the most amiable frame of mind.", "더 이상 논쟁은 일어나지 않았다. 오히려 식사가 끝나자 모두가 더없이 다정한 기분에 젖어 있었다."]], [["At first Katavasov amused the ladies by his original jokes, which always pleased people on their first acquaintance with him.", "처음에는 카타바소프가 특유의 기발한 농담으로 부인들을 즐겁게 했는데, 그것은 그를 처음 알게 된 사람들을 늘 흐뭇하게 만드는 것이었다."], ["Then Sergey Ivanovitch induced him to tell them about the very interesting observations he had made on the habits and characteristics of common houseflies, and their life.", "그러고 나서 세르게이 이바노비치가 그를 부추겨, 그가 평범한 집파리의 습성과 특징, 그 생태에 관해 관찰한 무척 흥미로운 이야기를 들려주게 했다."], ["Sergey Ivanovitch, too, was in good spirits, and at tea his brother drew him on to explain his views of the future of the Eastern question, and he spoke so simply and so well, that everyone listened eagerly.", "세르게이 이바노비치도 기분이 좋았고, 차를 마실 때 동생이 그를 이끌어 동방 문제의 장래에 대한 견해를 풀어놓게 하자, 그는 어찌나 간결하고 또 훌륭하게 말하던지 모두가 열심히 귀를 기울였다."]], [["Kitty was the only one who did not hear it all—she was summoned to give Mitya his bath.", "다만 키티만은 그 이야기를 끝까지 듣지 못했다. 미차를 목욕시키러 오라는 부름을 받았기 때문이다."]], [["A few minutes after Kitty had left the room she sent for Levin to come to the nursery.", "키티가 방을 나간 지 몇 분 뒤, 그녀는 레빈을 육아실로 오라며 사람을 보냈다."]], [["Leaving his tea, and regretfully interrupting the interesting conversation, and at the same time uneasily wondering why he had been sent for, as this only happened on important occasions, Levin went to the nursery.", "레빈은 마시던 차를 내려놓고, 흥미로운 대화를 아쉬운 마음으로 끊으면서, 동시에 왜 자기를 부르는지 불안하게 궁금해하며—이런 일은 중요한 경우에만 일어났기 때문이다—육아실로 갔다."]], [["Although he had been much interested by Sergey Ivanovitch’s views of the new epoch in history that would be created by the emancipation of forty millions of men of Slavonic race acting with Russia, a conception quite new to him, and although he was disturbed by uneasy wonder at being sent for by Kitty, as soon as he came out of the drawing-room and was alone, his mind reverted at once to the thoughts of the morning.", "러시아와 함께 행동하는 슬라브 민족 사천만 명의 해방이 빚어낼 역사의 새로운 시대에 대한 세르게이 이바노비치의 견해, 그에게는 아주 새로운 그 발상에 그는 무척 흥미를 느꼈고, 또 키티가 자기를 부른 데에 불안한 의구심을 품고 있었지만, 응접실에서 나와 혼자가 되자마자 그의 마음은 곧장 아침의 상념으로 되돌아갔다."], ["And all the theories of the significance of the Slav element in the history of the world seemed to him so trivial compared with what was passing in his own soul, that he instantly forgot it all and dropped back into the same frame of mind that he had been in that morning.", "그리고 세계 역사 속에서 슬라브 요소가 갖는 의의에 관한 온갖 이론들이, 자기 영혼 안에서 일어나고 있는 일에 비하면 너무도 하찮게 느껴져서, 그는 단숨에 그 모든 것을 잊고 그날 아침에 잠겨 있던 바로 그 심경으로 되돌아갔다."]], [["He did not, as he had done at other times, recall the whole train of thought—that he did not need.", "그는 다른 때 그랬던 것처럼 생각의 전 과정을 되짚지는 않았다. 그럴 필요가 없었다."], ["He fell back at once into the feeling which had guided him, which was connected with those thoughts, and he found that feeling in his soul even stronger and more definite than before.", "그는 곧바로, 자기를 이끌어 온, 그 상념들과 이어져 있던 그 느낌 속으로 다시 빠져들었고, 그 느낌이 자기 영혼 안에서 전보다 더 강하고 더 또렷해진 것을 발견했다."], ["He did not, as he had had to do with previous attempts to find comforting arguments, need to revive a whole chain of thought to find the feeling.", "위안이 되는 논거를 찾으려던 이전의 시도에서 그래야 했던 것과 달리, 이제는 그 느낌을 되살리기 위해 일련의 생각 전체를 되살릴 필요가 없었다."], ["Now, on the contrary, the feeling of joy and peace was keener than ever, and thought could not keep pace with feeling.", "오히려 지금은 기쁨과 평화의 느낌이 그 어느 때보다 또렷했고, 생각이 느낌을 따라잡지 못했다."]], [["He walked across the terrace and looked at two stars that had come out in the darkening sky, and suddenly he remembered.", "그는 테라스를 가로질러 걸으며 어두워지는 하늘에 막 돋아난 별 두 개를 바라보았고, 문득 떠올랐다."], ["“Yes, looking at the sky, I thought that the dome that I see is not a deception, and then I thought something, I shirked facing something,”", "\"그래, 하늘을 올려다보면서, 내가 보는 저 둥근 천장이 속임수가 아니라고 생각했지. 그러고 나서 뭔가를 생각했는데, 어떤 것과 마주하기를 회피했어.\""], ["he mused.", "그는 곰곰이 생각했다."], ["“But whatever it was, there can be no disproving it!", "\"하지만 그게 무엇이었든, 그걸 반박할 수는 없어!"], ["I have but to think, and all will come clear!”", "나는 그저 생각하기만 하면 되고, 그러면 모든 게 분명해질 거야!\""]], [["Just as he was going into the nursery he remembered what it was he had shirked facing.", "육아실로 막 들어서려는 순간 그는 자기가 마주하기를 회피했던 것이 무엇인지 떠올렸다."], ["It was that if the chief proof of the Divinity was His revelation of what is right, how is it this revelation is confined to the Christian church alone?", "그것은, 신성의 으뜸가는 증거가 무엇이 옳은지를 드러내 보인 그분의 계시라면, 어째서 이 계시가 오직 기독교 교회에만 국한되어 있는가 하는 것이었다."], ["What relation to this revelation have the beliefs of the Buddhists, Mohammedans, who preached and did good too?", "마찬가지로 설교하고 선을 행한 불교도며 이슬람교도의 신앙은 이 계시와 어떤 관계가 있는가?"]], [["It seemed to him that he had an answer to this question; but he had not time to formulate it to himself before he went into the nursery.", "그는 이 물음에 대한 답을 가지고 있는 것 같았다. 그러나 그것을 스스로에게 정리해 표현할 틈도 없이 육아실로 들어섰다."]], [["Kitty was standing with her sleeves tucked up over the baby in the bath.", "키티는 소매를 걷어 올린 채 욕조 안의 아기 위로 몸을 굽히고 서 있었다."], ["Hearing her husband’s footstep, she turned towards him, summoning him to her with her smile.", "남편의 발소리를 듣자 그녀는 그를 향해 돌아서며 미소로 그를 곁으로 불렀다."], ["With one hand she was supporting the fat baby that lay floating and sprawling on its back, while with the other she squeezed the sponge over him.", "한 손으로는 등을 대고 둥둥 떠서 사지를 버둥거리는 통통한 아기를 받치고, 다른 손으로는 아기 위로 해면을 짜고 있었다."]], [["“Come, look, look!”", "\"이리 와서 좀 봐요, 봐!\""], ["she said, when her husband came up to her.", "남편이 다가오자 그녀가 말했다. \"아가피야 미하일로브나 말이 맞아요."], ["“Agafea Mihalovna’s right. He knows us!”", "이 애가 우리를 알아본다니까요!\""]], [["Mitya had on that day given unmistakable, incontestable signs of recognizing all his friends.", "그날 미차는 자기 식구들을 모두 알아본다는 틀림없고 부정할 수 없는 신호를 보였던 것이다."]], [["As soon as Levin approached the bath, the experiment was tried, and it was completely successful.", "레빈이 욕조에 다가서자마자 실험이 행해졌고, 그것은 완전히 성공이었다."], ["The cook, sent for with this object, bent over the baby.", "이 일을 위해 불려 온 요리사가 아기 위로 몸을 굽혔다."], ["He frowned and shook his head disapprovingly.", "아기는 얼굴을 찌푸리며 못마땅한 듯 고개를 저었다."], ["Kitty bent down to him, he gave her a beaming smile, propped his little hands on the sponge and chirruped, making such a queer little contented sound with his lips, that Kitty and the nurse were not alone in their admiration.", "키티가 아기에게 몸을 굽히자, 아기는 그녀에게 환한 미소를 지어 보이고는 작은 두 손을 해면에 짚고 짹짹거렸는데, 입술로 어찌나 묘하고 만족스러운 작은 소리를 내던지, 감탄한 것은 키티와 유모만이 아니었다."], ["Levin, too, was surprised and delighted.", "레빈도 놀라고 기뻐했다."]], [["The baby was taken out of the bath, drenched with water, wrapped in towels, dried, and after a piercing scream, handed to his mother.", "아기는 물에 흠뻑 젖은 채 욕조에서 들려 나와 수건에 싸여 물기가 닦였고, 찢어질 듯한 비명을 한 차례 지른 뒤 어머니에게 건네졌다."]], [["“Well, I am glad you are beginning to love him,”", "\"자, 당신이 이 애를 사랑하기 시작했다니 정말 기뻐요.\""], ["said Kitty to her husband, when she had settled herself comfortably in her usual place, with the baby at her breast.", "키티가 아기를 가슴에 안고 늘 앉던 자리에 편안히 자리를 잡은 뒤 남편에게 말했다."], ["“I am so glad!", "\"정말 기뻐요!"], ["It had begun to distress me.", "그동안 그게 나를 괴롭히기 시작했거든요."], ["You said you had no feeling for him.”", "당신은 이 애한테 아무 정도 느껴지지 않는다고 했잖아요.\""]], [["“No; did I say that?", "\"아니, 내가 그렇게 말했나?"], ["I only said I was disappointed.”", "그저 실망했다고 했을 뿐인데.\""]], [["“What!", "\"뭐라고요!"], ["disappointed in him?”", "이 애한테 실망했다고요?\""]], [["“Not disappointed in him, but in my own feeling; I had expected more.", "\"이 애한테 실망한 게 아니라, 내 자신의 감정에 실망했다는 거야."], ["I had expected a rush of new delightful emotion to come as a surprise.", "더 많은 걸 기대했거든. 새롭고 황홀한 감정이 뜻밖의 선물처럼 와락 밀려들 거라고 기대했지."], ["And then instead of that—disgust, pity....”", "그런데 그 대신에—혐오감, 연민…….\""]], [["She listened attentively, looking at him over the baby, while she put back on her slender fingers the rings she had taken off while giving Mitya his bath.", "그녀는 미차를 목욕시키느라 빼 두었던 반지들을 가느다란 손가락에 다시 끼우면서, 아기 너머로 그를 바라보며 주의 깊게 귀를 기울였다."]], [["“And most of all, at there being far more apprehension and pity than pleasure.", "\"그리고 무엇보다도, 기쁨보다 두려움과 연민이 훨씬 더 컸다는 거야."], ["Today, after that fright during the storm, I understand how I love him.”", "오늘, 폭풍이 칠 때 그렇게 겁에 질리고 나서야, 내가 이 애를 얼마나 사랑하는지 깨달았어.\""]], [["Kitty’s smile was radiant.", "키티의 미소가 환하게 빛났다."]], [["“Were you very much frightened?”", "\"많이 무서웠어요?\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“So was I too, but I feel it more now that it’s over.", "\"나도 그랬어요. 그런데 다 지나고 나니 이제 더 실감이 나네요."], ["I’m going to look at the oak.", "나중에 그 떡갈나무를 보러 갈래요."], ["How nice Katavasov is!", "카타바소프는 참 좋은 사람이에요!"], ["And what a happy day we’ve had altogether.", "그리고 오늘 우리 모두 얼마나 즐거운 하루를 보냈는지 몰라요."], ["And you’re so nice with Sergey Ivanovitch, when you care to be....", "그리고 당신, 마음만 먹으면 세르게이 이바노비치한테 참 다정하게 굴잖아요……"], ["Well, go back to them.", "자, 이제 그분들한테 돌아가요."], ["It’s always so hot and steamy here after the bath.”", "목욕시키고 나면 여긴 늘 이렇게 덥고 김이 자욱해서요.\""]], [["# Chapter 19", "# Chapter 19"]], [["Going out of the nursery and being again alone, Levin went back at once to the thought, in which there was something not clear.", "육아실을 나와 다시 혼자가 되자, 레빈은 곧바로 무언가 분명치 않은 점이 남아 있던 그 생각으로 되돌아갔다."]], [["Instead of going into the drawing-room, where he heard voices, he stopped on the terrace, and leaning his elbows on the parapet, he gazed up at the sky.", "목소리가 들려오는 응접실로 들어가는 대신, 그는 테라스에 멈춰 서서 난간에 팔꿈치를 괴고 하늘을 올려다보았다."]], [["It was quite dark now, and in the south, where he was looking, there were no clouds.", "이제 사방이 어두웠고, 그가 바라보고 있는 남쪽에는 구름 한 점 없었다."], ["The storm had drifted on to the opposite side of the sky, and there were flashes of lightning and distant thunder from that quarter.", "폭풍우는 하늘 반대편으로 흘러갔고, 그쪽에서 번개가 번쩍이고 먼 천둥소리가 들려왔다."], ["Levin listened to the monotonous drip from the lime trees in the garden, and looked at the triangle of stars he knew so well, and the Milky Way with its branches that ran through its midst.", "레빈은 정원의 보리수에서 단조롭게 떨어지는 물방울 소리에 귀를 기울이며, 자기가 너무도 잘 아는 별들의 삼각형과, 그 한가운데를 지르며 갈래갈래 뻗어 있는 은하수를 바라보았다."], ["At each flash of lightning the Milky Way, and even the bright stars, vanished, but as soon as the lightning died away, they reappeared in their places as though some hand had flung them back with careful aim.", "번개가 번쩍일 때마다 은하수도, 심지어 밝은 별들도 사라졌지만, 번개가 잦아들기가 무섭게 별들은 마치 어떤 손이 정성껏 겨냥해 도로 던져 놓기라도 한 듯 제자리에 다시 나타났다."]], [["“Well, what is it perplexes me?”", "\"자, 무엇이 나를 이토록 혼란스럽게 하는 걸까?\""], ["Levin said to himself, feeling beforehand that the solution of his difficulties was ready in his soul, though he did not know it yet.", "레빈은 자기 어려움의 해답이 영혼 속에 이미 준비되어 있다는 것을 미처 알지 못한 채 그저 미리 느끼면서 혼잣말을 했다. \"그래, 신성이 틀림없고 다툴 여지 없이 드러나는 단 하나의 발현은 선과 악의 법칙이야."], ["“Yes, the one unmistakable, incontestable manifestation of the Divinity is the law of right and wrong, which has come into the world by revelation, and which I feel in myself, and in the recognition of which—I don’t make myself, but whether I will or not—I am made one with other men in one body of believers, which is called the church.", "그 법칙은 계시를 통해 세상에 들어왔고, 나는 그것을 내 안에서 느끼지. 그리고 그것을 인정함으로써—내가 스스로 그렇게 만드는 게 아니라, 내가 원하든 원치 않든—나는 교회라 불리는 신자들의 한 몸 안에서 다른 사람들과 하나가 되는 거야."], ["Well, but the Jews, the Mohammedans, the Confucians, the Buddhists—what of them?”", "자, 그런데 유대인, 마호메트교도, 공자교도, 불교도는—이들은 어떻게 되는 거지?\""], ["he put to himself the question he had feared to face.", "그는 직면하기를 두려워했던 물음을 스스로에게 던졌다."], ["“Can these hundreds of millions of men be deprived of that highest blessing without which life has no meaning?”", "\"이 수억의 사람들이 그것 없이는 삶이 아무 의미도 없는 그 지고의 축복을 빼앗긴 채로 살아갈 수 있단 말인가?\""], ["He pondered a moment, but immediately corrected himself.", "그는 잠시 곰곰이 생각하다가, 곧바로 자신을 바로잡았다."], ["“But what am I questioning?” he said to himself.", "\"그런데 내가 무엇을 묻고 있는 거지?\""], ["“I am questioning the relation to Divinity of all the different religions of all mankind.", "그는 스스로에게 말했다. \"나는 온 인류의 온갖 다양한 종교들이 신성과 맺는 관계를 묻고 있어."], ["I am questioning the universal manifestation of God to all the world with all those misty blurs. What am I about?", "나는 그 모든 안개 같은 흐릿함을 안고서 신이 온 세상에 보편적으로 발현하는 것을 묻고 있어."], ["To me individually, to my heart has been revealed a knowledge beyond all doubt, and unattainable by reason, and here I am obstinately trying to express that knowledge in reason and words.", "내가 지금 무얼 하고 있는 건가? 나라는 개인에게, 내 마음에게는 의심의 여지없는, 이성으로는 도달할 수 없는 앎이 계시되었는데, 나는 여기서 그 앎을 이성과 말로 표현하려고 고집스럽게 애쓰고 있는 거야."]], [["“Don’t I know that the stars don’t move?”", "\"별들이 움직이지 않는다는 걸 내가 모를 리 있나?\""], ["he asked himself, gazing at the bright planet which had shifted its position up to the topmost twig of the birch-tree.", "그는 자작나무의 가장 높은 가지 끝까지 위치를 옮겨 간 저 밝은 별을 바라보며 자문했다."], ["“But looking at the movements of the stars, I can’t picture to myself the rotation of the earth, and I’m right in saying that the stars move.", "\"하지만 별들의 움직임을 바라보면서 나는 지구의 회전을 머릿속에 그릴 수가 없어. 그러니 별들이 움직인다고 말하는 내가 옳은 거야."]], [["“And could the astronomers have understood and calculated anything, if they had taken into account all the complicated and varied motions of the earth?", "\"그리고 만약 천문학자들이 지구의 그 복잡하고 다양한 온갖 운동을 다 고려했더라면, 무언가를 이해하고 계산해 낼 수 있었을까?"], ["All the marvelous conclusions they have reached about the distances, weights, movements, and deflections of the heavenly bodies are only founded on the apparent motions of the heavenly bodies about a stationary earth, on that very motion I see before me now, which has been so for millions of men during long ages, and was and will be always alike, and can always be trusted.", "천체들의 거리며 무게며 운동이며 편차에 관해 그들이 도달한 그 모든 경이로운 결론은, 오로지 정지해 있는 지구를 중심으로 한 천체들의 겉보기 운동에, 곧 지금 내 눈앞에 펼쳐져 있는, 오랜 세월 동안 수백만 사람들에게 그러했고 또 언제나 한결같이 그러했으며 또 그러할, 그래서 언제나 믿을 수 있는 바로 그 운동에 근거를 두고 있을 뿐이야."], ["And just as the conclusions of the astronomers would have been vain and uncertain if not founded on observations of the seen heavens, in relation to a single meridian and a single horizon, so would my conclusions be vain and uncertain if not founded on that conception of right, which has been and will be always alike for all men, which has been revealed to me as a Christian, and which can always be trusted in my soul.", "그리고 천문학자들의 결론이 단 하나의 자오선과 단 하나의 지평선을 기준으로 한 눈에 보이는 하늘의 관측에 근거를 두지 않았다면 헛되고 불확실했을 것처럼, 내 결론도 모든 사람에게 한결같이 그러했고 또 그러할 선의 관념에, 곧 그리스도교도인 내게 계시되었고 또 내 영혼 속에서 언제나 믿을 수 있는 그 관념에 근거를 두지 않았다면 헛되고 불확실했을 거야."], ["The question of other religions and their relations to Divinity I have no right to decide, and no possibility of deciding.”", "다른 종교들과 그것들이 신성과 맺는 관계의 문제를 결정할 권리도, 결정할 가능성도 내게는 없어.\""]], [["“Oh, you haven’t gone in then?”", "\"어머, 아직 안 들어가셨어요?\""], ["he heard Kitty’s voice all at once, as she came by the same way to the drawing-room.", "같은 길을 지나 응접실로 가던 키티의 목소리가 별안간 들려왔다."]], [["“What is it?", "\"무슨 일이에요?"], ["you’re not worried about anything?”", "무슨 걱정이라도 있으세요?\""], ["she said, looking intently at his face in the starlight.", "그녀가 별빛 속에서 그의 얼굴을 유심히 들여다보며 물었다."]], [["But she could not have seen his face if a flash of lightning had not hidden the stars and revealed it.", "하지만 번개 한 줄기가 별들을 감추고 그의 얼굴을 드러내지 않았더라면 그녀는 그의 얼굴을 볼 수 없었을 것이다."], ["In that flash she saw his face distinctly, and seeing him calm and happy, she smiled at him.", "그 번갯불 속에서 그녀는 그의 얼굴을 또렷이 보았고, 그가 차분하고 행복해 보이자 그를 향해 미소 지었다."]], [["“She understands,”", "\"이 사람은 알고 있어.\""], ["he thought; “she knows what I’m thinking about.", "그는 생각했다. \"내가 무슨 생각을 하고 있는지 알고 있어."], ["Shall I tell her or not?", "이 사람한테 말할까 말까?"], ["Yes, I’ll tell her.”", "그래, 말해야지.\""], ["But at the moment he was about to speak, she began speaking.", "그러나 그가 막 입을 열려는 순간, 그녀가 먼저 말을 꺼냈다."]], [["“Kostya!", "\"코스챠!"], ["do something for me,”", "부탁 하나만 들어줘요.\""], ["she said; “go into the corner room and see if they’ve made it all right for Sergey Ivanovitch.", "그녀가 말했다. \"구석방에 가서 세르게이 이바노비치를 위해 다 제대로 준비됐는지 좀 봐 줘요."], ["I can’t very well.", "내가 가기는 좀 그래서요."], ["See if they’ve put the new wash stand in it.”", "새 세면대를 거기 들여놓았는지 봐 주세요.\""]], [["“Very well, I’ll go directly,”", "\"좋아, 지금 곧 가 보지.\""], ["said Levin, standing up and kissing her.", "레빈이 일어나 그녀에게 입을 맞추며 말했다."]], [["“No, I’d better not speak of it,”", "\"아니야, 이건 말하지 않는 편이 낫겠어.\""], ["he thought, when she had gone in before him.", "그녀가 그보다 먼저 안으로 들어가자 그는 생각했다."], ["“It is a secret for me alone, of vital importance for me, and not to be put into words.", "\"이건 오직 나만의 비밀이고, 내게 더없이 중대한 것이며, 말로 옮길 수 있는 게 아니야."]], [["“This new feeling has not changed me, has not made me happy and enlightened all of a sudden, as I had dreamed, just like the feeling for my child.", "\"이 새로운 감정은 나를 바꾸지도 않았고, 내가 꿈꾸었던 것처럼 나를 갑작스레 행복하고 깨우치게 만들지도 않았어. 내 아이에 대한 감정과 꼭 마찬가지로 말이야."], ["There was no surprise in this either.", "여기에도 놀라움은 없었어."], ["Faith—or not faith—I don’t know what it is—but this feeling has come just as imperceptibly through suffering, and has taken firm root in my soul.", "믿음이든—아니면 믿음이 아니든—그게 무엇인지 나는 모르지만, 이 감정은 고통을 통해 똑같이 어느새 슬며시 찾아왔고, 내 영혼 속에 단단히 뿌리를 내렸어."]], [["“I shall go on in the same way, losing my temper with Ivan the coachman, falling into angry discussions, expressing my opinions tactlessly; there will be still the same wall between the holy of holies of my soul and other people, even my wife; I shall still go on scolding her for my own terror, and being remorseful for it; I shall still be as unable to understand with my reason why I pray, and I shall still go on praying; but my life now, my whole life apart from anything that can happen to me, every minute of it is no more meaningless, as it was before, but it has the positive meaning of goodness, which I have the power to put into it.”", "\"나는 예전과 똑같은 모습으로 살아갈 거야. 마부 이반에게 성을 내고, 화를 내며 따지고 들고, 요령 없이 내 의견을 내뱉으면서. 여전히 내 영혼의 지성소와 다른 사람들 사이에는, 심지어 내 아내와의 사이에도 똑같은 벽이 가로놓여 있을 거고, 나는 여전히 내 자신의 두려움 탓에 아내를 나무라고는 그 일을 후회할 거야. 나는 여전히 내가 왜 기도하는지를 이성으로는 이해하지 못할 거고, 그러면서도 여전히 기도할 거야. 하지만 이제 내 삶은, 내게 무슨 일이 일어나든 그것과는 별개로, 내 삶 전체는, 그 한순간 한순간이, 예전처럼 더 이상 무의미하지 않고, 내가 거기에 불어넣을 힘을 지닌 선이라는 긍정적인 의미를 지니게 된 거야.\""]]];
